if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-963"]="# BOOK THE SECOND: RICHES\n\nBOOK THE FIRST: POVERTY 1. Sun and Shadow 2. Fellow Travellers 3. Home 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream 5. Family Affairs 6. The Father of the Marshalsea 7. The Child of the Marshalsea 8. The Lock 9. little Mother 10. Containing the whole Science of Government 11. Let Loose 12. Bleeding Heart Yard 13. Patriarchal 14. Little Dorrit’s Party 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream 16. Nobody’s Weakness 17. Nobody’s Rival 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations 20. Moving in Society 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint 22. A Puzzle 23. Machinery in Motion 24. Fortune-Telling 25. Conspirators and Others 26. Nobody’s State of Mind 27. Five-and-Twenty 28. Nobody’s Disappearance 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming 30. The Word of a Gentleman 31. Spirit 32. More Fortune-Telling 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint 34. A Shoal of Barnacles 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan\n1. Fellow Travellers 2. Mrs General 3. On the Road 4. A Letter from Little Dorrit 5. Something Wrong Somewhere 6. Something Right Somewhere 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’ 9. Appearance and Disappearance 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken 11. A Letter from Little Dorrit 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden 13. The Progress of an Epidemic 14. Taking Advice 15. No just Cause or Impediment why these Two Persons should not be joined together 16. Getting on 17. Missing 18. A Castle in the Air 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air 20. Introduces the next 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor 22. Who Passes by this Road so late? 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams 24. The Evening of a Long Day 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office 26. Reaping the Whirlwind 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea 30. Closing in 31. Closed 32. Going 33. Going! 34. Gone\n\nI have been occupied with this story, during many working hours of two years. I must have been very ill employed, if I could not leave its merits and demerits as a whole, to express themselves on its being read as a whole. But, as it is not unreasonable to suppose that I may have held its threads with a more continuous attention than anyone else can have given them during its desultory publication, it is not unreasonable to ask that the weaving may be looked at in its completed state, and with the pattern finished.\nIf I might offer any apology for so exaggerated a fiction as the Barnacles and the Circumlocution Office, I would seek it in the common experience of an Englishman, without presuming to mention the unimportant fact of my having done that violence to good manners, in the days of a Russian war, and of a Court of Inquiry at Chelsea. If I might make so bold as to defend that extravagant conception, Mr Merdle, I would hint that it originated after the Railroad-share epoch, in the times of a certain Irish bank, and of one or two other equally laudable enterprises. If I were to plead anything in mitigation of the preposterous fancy that a bad design will sometimes claim to be a good and an expressly religious design, it would be the curious coincidence that it has been brought to its climax in these pages, in the days of the public examination of late Directors of a Royal British Bank. But, I submit myself to suffer judgment to go by default on all these counts, if need be, and to accept the assurance (on good authority) that nothing like them was ever known in this land.\n\nSome of my readers may have an interest in being informed whether or no any portions of the Marshalsea Prison are yet standing. I did not know, myself, until the sixth of this present month, when I went to look. I found the outer front courtyard, often mentioned here, metamorphosed into a butter shop; and I then almost gave up every brick of the jail for lost. Wandering, however, down a certain adjacent ‘Angel Court, leading to Bermondsey’, I came to ‘Marshalsea Place:’ the houses in which I recognised, not only as the great block of the former prison, but as preserving the rooms that arose in my mind’s-eye when I became Little Dorrit’s biographer. The smallest boy I ever conversed with, carrying the largest baby I ever saw, offered a supernaturally intelligent explanation of the locality in its old uses, and was very nearly correct. How this young Newton (for such I judge him to be) came by his information, I don’t know; he was a quarter of a century too young to know anything about it of himself. I pointed to the window of the room where Little Dorrit was born, and where her father lived so long, and asked him what was the name of the lodger who tenanted that apartment at present? He said, ‘Tom Pythick.’ I asked him who was Tom Pythick? and he said, ‘Joe Pythick’s uncle.’\n\nA little further on, I found the older and smaller wall, which used to enclose the pent-up inner prison where nobody was put, except for ceremony. But, whosoever goes into Marshalsea Place, turning out of Angel Court, leading to Bermondsey, will find his feet on the very paving-stones of the extinct Marshalsea jail; will see its narrow yard to the right and to the left, very little altered if at all, except that the walls were lowered when the place got free; will look upon rooms in which the debtors lived; and will stand among the crowding ghosts of many miserable years.\n\nIn the Preface to Bleak House I remarked that I had never had so many readers. In the Preface to its next successor, Little Dorrit, I have still to repeat the same words. Deeply sensible of the affection and confidence that have grown up between us, I add to this Preface, as I added to that, May we meet again!\n\nLondon May 1857\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Sun and Shadow\n\nThirty years ago, Marseilles lay burning in the sun, one day.\n\nA blazing sun upon a fierce August day was no greater rarity in southern France then, than at any other time, before or since. Everything in Marseilles, and about Marseilles, had stared at the fervid sky, and been stared at in return, until a staring habit had become universal there. Strangers were stared out of countenance by staring white houses, staring white walls, staring white streets, staring tracts of arid road, staring hills from which verdure was burnt away. The only things to be seen not fixedly staring and glaring were the vines drooping under their load of grapes. These did occasionally wink a little, as the hot air barely moved their faint leaves.\n\nThere was no wind to make a ripple on the foul water within the harbour, or on the beautiful sea without. The line of demarcation between the two colours, black and blue, showed the point which the pure sea would not pass; but it lay as quiet as the abominable pool, with which it never mixed. Boats without awnings were too hot to touch; ships blistered at their moorings; the stones of the quays had not cooled, night or day, for months. Hindoos, Russians, Chinese, Spaniards, Portuguese, Englishmen, Frenchmen, Genoese, Neapolitans, Venetians, Greeks, Turks, descendants from all the builders of Babel, come to trade at Marseilles, sought the shade alike--taking refuge in any hiding-place from a sea too intensely blue to be looked at, and a sky of purple, set with one great flaming jewel of fire.\n\nThe universal stare made the eyes ache. Towards the distant line of Italian coast, indeed, it was a little relieved by light clouds of mist, slowly rising from the evaporation of the sea, but it softened nowhere else. Far away the staring roads, deep in dust, stared from the hill-side, stared from the hollow, stared from the interminable plain. Far away the dusty vines overhanging wayside cottages, and the monotonous wayside avenues of parched trees without shade, drooped beneath the stare of earth and sky. So did the horses with drowsy bells, in long files of carts, creeping slowly towards the interior; so did their recumbent drivers, when they were awake, which rarely happened; so did the exhausted labourers in the fields. Everything that lived or grew, was oppressed by the glare; except the lizard, passing swiftly over rough stone walls, and the cicala, chirping his dry hot chirp, like a rattle. The very dust was scorched brown, and something quivered in the atmosphere as if the air itself were panting.\n\nBlinds, shutters, curtains, awnings, were all closed and drawn to keep out the stare. Grant it but a chink or keyhole, and it shot in like a white-hot arrow. The churches were the freest from it. To come out of the twilight of pillars and arches--dreamily dotted with winking lamps, dreamily peopled with ugly old shadows piously dozing, spitting, and begging--was to plunge into a fiery river, and swim for life to the nearest strip of shade. So, with people lounging and lying wherever shade was, with but little hum of tongues or barking of dogs, with occasional jangling of discordant church bells and rattling of vicious drums, Marseilles, a fact to be strongly smelt and tasted, lay broiling in the sun one day.\n\nIn Marseilles that day there was a villainous prison. In one of its chambers, so repulsive a place that even the obtrusive stare blinked at it, and left it to such refuse of reflected light as it could find for itself, were two men. Besides the two men, a notched and disfigured bench, immovable from the wall, with a draught-board rudely hacked upon it with a knife, a set of draughts, made of old buttons and soup bones, a set of dominoes, two mats, and two or three wine bottles. That was all the chamber held, exclusive of rats and other unseen vermin, in addition to the seen vermin, the two men.\n\nIt received such light as it got through a grating of iron bars fashioned like a pretty large window, by means of which it could be always inspected from the gloomy staircase on which the grating gave. There was a broad strong ledge of stone to this grating where the bottom of it was let into the masonry, three or four feet above the ground. Upon it, one of the two men lolled, half sitting and half lying, with his knees drawn up, and his feet and shoulders planted against the opposite sides of the aperture. The bars were wide enough apart to admit of his thrusting his arm through to the elbow; and so he held on negligently, for his greater ease.\n\nA prison taint was on everything there. The imprisoned air, the imprisoned light, the imprisoned damps, the imprisoned men, were all deteriorated by confinement. As the captive men were faded and haggard, so the iron was rusty, the stone was slimy, the wood was rotten, the air was faint, the light was dim. Like a well, like a vault, like a tomb, the prison had no knowledge of the brightness outside, and would have kept its polluted atmosphere intact in one of the spice islands of the Indian ocean.\n\nThe man who lay on the ledge of the grating was even chilled. He jerked his great cloak more heavily upon him by an impatient movement of one shoulder, and growled, ‘To the devil with this Brigand of a Sun that never shines in here!’\n\nHe was waiting to be fed, looking sideways through the bars that he might see the further down the stairs, with much of the expression of a wild beast in similar expectation. But his eyes, too close together, were not so nobly set in his head as those of the king of beasts are in his, and they were sharp rather than bright--pointed weapons with little surface to betray them. They had no depth or change; they glittered, and they opened and shut. So far, and waiving their use to himself, a clockmaker could have made a better pair. He had a hook nose, handsome after its kind, but too high between the eyes by probably just as much as his eyes were too near to one another. For the rest, he was large and tall in frame, had thin lips, where his thick moustache showed them at all, and a quantity of dry hair, of no definable colour, in its shaggy state, but shot with red. The hand with which he held the grating (seamed all over the back with ugly scratches newly healed), was unusually small and plump; would have been unusually white but for the prison grime.\n\nThe other man was lying on the stone floor, covered with a coarse brown coat.\n\n‘Get up, pig!’ growled the first. ‘Don’t sleep when I am hungry.’\n\n‘It’s all one, master,’ said the pig, in a submissive manner, and not without cheerfulness; ‘I can wake when I will, I can sleep when I will. It’s all the same.’\n\nAs he said it, he rose, shook himself, scratched himself, tied his brown coat loosely round his neck by the sleeves (he had previously used it as a coverlet), and sat down upon the pavement yawning, with his back against the wall opposite to the grating.\n\n‘Say what the hour is,’ grumbled the first man.\n\n‘The mid-day bells will ring--in forty minutes.’ When he made the little pause, he had looked round the prison-room, as if for certain information.\n\n‘You are a clock. How is it that you always know?’\n\n‘How can I say? I always know what the hour is, and where I am. I was brought in here at night, and out of a boat, but I know where I am. See here! Marseilles harbour;’ on his knees on the pavement, mapping it all out with a swarthy forefinger; ‘Toulon (where the galleys are), Spain over there, Algiers over _there_. Creeping away to the left here, Nice. Round by the Cornice to Genoa. Genoa Mole and Harbour. Quarantine Ground. City there; terrace gardens blushing with the bella donna. Here, Porto Fino. Stand out for Leghorn. Out again for Civita Vecchia, so away to--hey! there’s no room for Naples;’ he had got to the wall by this time; ‘but it’s all one; it’s in there!’\n\nHe remained on his knees, looking up at his fellow-prisoner with a lively look for a prison. A sunburnt, quick, lithe, little man, though rather thickset. Earrings in his brown ears, white teeth lighting up his grotesque brown face, intensely black hair clustering about his brown throat, a ragged red shirt open at his brown breast. Loose, seaman-like trousers, decent shoes, a long red cap, a red sash round his waist, and a knife in it.\n\n‘Judge if I come back from Naples as I went! See here, my master! Civita Vecchia, Leghorn, Porto Fino, Genoa, Cornice, Off Nice (which is in there), Marseilles, you and me. The apartment of the jailer and his keys is where I put this thumb; and here at my wrist they keep the national razor in its case--the guillotine locked up.’\n\nThe other man spat suddenly on the pavement, and gurgled in his throat.\n\nSome lock below gurgled in _its_ throat immediately afterwards, and then a door crashed. Slow steps began ascending the stairs; the prattle of a sweet little voice mingled with the noise they made; and the prison-keeper appeared carrying his daughter, three or four years old, and a basket.\n\n‘How goes the world this forenoon, gentlemen? My little one, you see, going round with me to have a peep at her father’s birds. Fie, then! Look at the birds, my pretty, look at the birds.’\n\nHe looked sharply at the birds himself, as he held the child up at the grate, especially at the little bird, whose activity he seemed to mistrust. ‘I have brought your bread, Signor John Baptist,’ said he (they all spoke in French, but the little man was an Italian); ‘and if I might recommend you not to game--’\n\n‘You don’t recommend the master!’ said John Baptist, showing his teeth as he smiled.\n\n‘Oh! but the master wins,’ returned the jailer, with a passing look of no particular liking at the other man, ‘and you lose. It’s quite another thing. You get husky bread and sour drink by it; and he gets sausage of Lyons, veal in savoury jelly, white bread, strachino cheese, and good wine by it. Look at the birds, my pretty!’\n\n‘Poor birds!’ said the child.\n\nThe fair little face, touched with divine compassion, as it peeped shrinkingly through the grate, was like an angel’s in the prison. John Baptist rose and moved towards it, as if it had a good attraction for him. The other bird remained as before, except for an impatient glance at the basket.\n\n‘Stay!’ said the jailer, putting his little daughter on the outer ledge of the grate, ‘she shall feed the birds. This big loaf is for Signor John Baptist. We must break it to get it through into the cage. So, there’s a tame bird to kiss the little hand! This sausage in a vine leaf is for Monsieur Rigaud. Again--this veal in savoury jelly is for Monsieur Rigaud. Again--these three white little loaves are for Monsieur Rigaud. Again, this cheese--again, this wine--again, this tobacco--all for Monsieur Rigaud. Lucky bird!’\n\nThe child put all these things between the bars into the soft, Smooth, well-shaped hand, with evident dread--more than once drawing back her own and looking at the man with her fair brow roughened into an expression half of fright and half of anger. Whereas she had put the lump of coarse bread into the swart, scaled, knotted hands of John Baptist (who had scarcely as much nail on his eight fingers and two thumbs as would have made out one for Monsieur Rigaud), with ready confidence; and, when he kissed her hand, had herself passed it caressingly over his face. Monsieur Rigaud, indifferent to this distinction, propitiated the father by laughing and nodding at the daughter as often as she gave him anything; and, so soon as he had all his viands about him in convenient nooks of the ledge on which he rested, began to eat with an appetite.\n\nWhen Monsieur Rigaud laughed, a change took place in his face, that was more remarkable than prepossessing. His moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache, in a very sinister and cruel manner.\n\n‘There!’ said the jailer, turning his basket upside down to beat the crumbs out, ‘I have expended all the money I received; here is the note of it, and _that’s_ a thing accomplished. Monsieur Rigaud, as I expected yesterday, the President will look for the pleasure of your society at an hour after mid-day, to-day.’\n\n‘To try me, eh?’ said Rigaud, pausing, knife in hand and morsel in mouth.\n\n‘You have said it. To try you.’\n\n‘There is no news for me?’ asked John Baptist, who had begun, contentedly, to munch his bread.\n\nThe jailer shrugged his shoulders.\n\n‘Lady of mine! Am I to lie here all my life, my father?’\n\n‘What do I know!’ cried the jailer, turning upon him with southern quickness, and gesticulating with both his hands and all his fingers, as if he were threatening to tear him to pieces. ‘My friend, how is it possible for me to tell how long you are to lie here? What do I know, John Baptist Cavalletto? Death of my life! There are prisoners here sometimes, who are not in such a devil of a hurry to be tried.’\n\nHe seemed to glance obliquely at Monsieur Rigaud in this remark; but Monsieur Rigaud had already resumed his meal, though not with quite so quick an appetite as before.\n\n‘Adieu, my birds!’ said the keeper of the prison, taking his pretty child in his arms, and dictating the words with a kiss.\n\n‘Adieu, my birds!’ the pretty child repeated.\n\nHer innocent face looked back so brightly over his shoulder, as he walked away with her, singing her the song of the child’s game:\n\n‘Who passes by this road so late? Compagnon de la Majolaine! Who passes by this road so late? Always gay!’\n\nthat John Baptist felt it a point of honour to reply at the grate, and in good time and tune, though a little hoarsely:\n\n‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine! Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’\n\nWhich accompanied them so far down the few steep stairs, that the prison-keeper had to stop at last for his little daughter to hear the song out, and repeat the Refrain while they were yet in sight. Then the child’s head disappeared, and the prison-keeper’s head disappeared, but the little voice prolonged the strain until the door clashed.\n\nMonsieur Rigaud, finding the listening John Baptist in his way before the echoes had ceased (even the echoes were the weaker for imprisonment, and seemed to lag), reminded him with a push of his foot that he had better resume his own darker place. The little man sat down again upon the pavement with the negligent ease of one who was thoroughly accustomed to pavements; and placing three hunks of coarse bread before himself, and falling to upon a fourth, began contentedly to work his way through them as if to clear them off were a sort of game.\n\nPerhaps he glanced at the Lyons sausage, and perhaps he glanced at the veal in savoury jelly, but they were not there long, to make his mouth water; Monsieur Rigaud soon dispatched them, in spite of the president and tribunal, and proceeded to suck his fingers as clean as he could, and to wipe them on his vine leaves. Then, as he paused in his drink to contemplate his fellow-prisoner, his moustache went up, and his nose came down.\n\n‘How do you find the bread?’\n\n‘A little dry, but I have my old sauce here,’ returned John Baptist, holding up his knife.\n\n‘How sauce?’\n\n‘I can cut my bread so--like a melon. Or so--like an omelette. Or so--like a fried fish. Or so--like Lyons sausage,’ said John Baptist, demonstrating the various cuts on the bread he held, and soberly chewing what he had in his mouth.\n\n‘Here!’ cried Monsieur Rigaud. ‘You may drink. You may finish this.’\n\nIt was no great gift, for there was mighty little wine left; but Signor Cavalletto, jumping to his feet, received the bottle gratefully, turned it upside down at his mouth, and smacked his lips.\n\n‘Put the bottle by with the rest,’ said Rigaud.\n\nThe little man obeyed his orders, and stood ready to give him a lighted match; for he was now rolling his tobacco into cigarettes by the aid of little squares of paper which had been brought in with it.\n\n‘Here! You may have one.’\n\n‘A thousand thanks, my master!’ John Baptist said in his own language, and with the quick conciliatory manner of his own countrymen.\n\nMonsieur Rigaud arose, lighted a cigarette, put the rest of his stock into a breast-pocket, and stretched himself out at full length upon the bench. Cavalletto sat down on the pavement, holding one of his ankles in each hand, and smoking peacefully. There seemed to be some uncomfortable attraction of Monsieur Rigaud’s eyes to the immediate neighbourhood of that part of the pavement where the thumb had been in the plan. They were so drawn in that direction, that the Italian more than once followed them to and back from the pavement in some surprise.\n\n‘What an infernal hole this is!’ said Monsieur Rigaud, breaking a long pause. ‘Look at the light of day. Day? the light of yesterday week, the light of six months ago, the light of six years ago. So slack and dead!’\n\nIt came languishing down a square funnel that blinded a window in the staircase wall, through which the sky was never seen--nor anything else.\n\n‘Cavalletto,’ said Monsieur Rigaud, suddenly withdrawing his gaze from this funnel to which they had both involuntarily turned their eyes, ‘you know me for a gentleman?’\n\n‘Surely, surely!’\n\n‘How long have we been here?’\n\n‘I, eleven weeks, to-morrow night at midnight. You, nine weeks and three days, at five this afternoon.’\n\n‘Have I ever done anything here? Ever touched the broom, or spread the mats, or rolled them up, or found the draughts, or collected the dominoes, or put my hand to any kind of work?’\n\n‘Never!’\n\n‘Have you ever thought of looking to me to do any kind of work?’\n\nJohn Baptist answered with that peculiar back-handed shake of the right forefinger which is the most expressive negative in the Italian language.\n\n‘No! You knew from the first moment when you saw me here, that I was a gentleman?’\n\n‘ALTRO!’ returned John Baptist, closing his eyes and giving his head a most vehement toss. The word being, according to its Genoese emphasis, a confirmation, a contradiction, an assertion, a denial, a taunt, a compliment, a joke, and fifty other things, became in the present instance, with a significance beyond all power of written expression, our familiar English ‘I believe you!’\n\n‘Haha! You are right! A gentleman I am! And a gentleman I’ll live, and a gentleman I’ll die! It’s my intent to be a gentleman. It’s my game. Death of my soul, I play it out wherever I go!’\n\nHe changed his posture to a sitting one, crying with a triumphant air:\n\n‘Here I am! See me! Shaken out of destiny’s dice-box into the company of a mere smuggler;--shut up with a poor little contraband trader, whose papers are wrong, and whom the police lay hold of besides, for placing his boat (as a means of getting beyond the frontier) at the disposition of other little people whose papers are wrong; and he instinctively recognises my position, even by this light and in this place. It’s well done! By Heaven! I win, however the game goes.’\n\nAgain his moustache went up, and his nose came down.\n\n‘What’s the hour now?’ he asked, with a dry hot pallor upon him, rather difficult of association with merriment.\n\n‘A little half-hour after mid-day.’\n\n‘Good! The President will have a gentleman before him soon. Come! Shall I tell you on what accusation? It must be now, or never, for I shall not return here. Either I shall go free, or I shall go to be made ready for shaving. You know where they keep the razor.’\n\nSignor Cavalletto took his cigarette from between his parted lips, and showed more momentary discomfiture than might have been expected.\n\n‘I am a’--Monsieur Rigaud stood up to say it--‘I am a cosmopolitan gentleman. I own no particular country. My father was Swiss--Canton de Vaud. My mother was French by blood, English by birth. I myself was born in Belgium. I am a citizen of the world.’\n\nHis theatrical air, as he stood with one arm on his hip within the folds of his cloak, together with his manner of disregarding his companion and addressing the opposite wall instead, seemed to intimate that he was rehearsing for the President, whose examination he was shortly to undergo, rather than troubling himself merely to enlighten so small a person as John Baptist Cavalletto.\n\n‘Call me five-and-thirty years of age. I have seen the world. I have lived here, and lived there, and lived like a gentleman everywhere. I have been treated and respected as a gentleman universally. If you try to prejudice me by making out that I have lived by my wits--how do your lawyers live--your politicians--your intriguers--your men of the Exchange?’\n\nHe kept his small smooth hand in constant requisition, as if it were a witness to his gentility that had often done him good service before.\n\n‘Two years ago I came to Marseilles. I admit that I was poor; I had been ill. When your lawyers, your politicians, your intriguers, your men of the Exchange fall ill, and have not scraped money together, _they_ become poor. I put up at the Cross of Gold,--kept then by Monsieur Henri Barronneau--sixty-five at least, and in a failing state of health. I had lived in the house some four months when Monsieur Henri Barronneau had the misfortune to die;--at any rate, not a rare misfortune, that. It happens without any aid of mine, pretty often.’\n\nJohn Baptist having smoked his cigarette down to his fingers’ ends, Monsieur Rigaud had the magnanimity to throw him another. He lighted the second at the ashes of the first, and smoked on, looking sideways at his companion, who, preoccupied with his own case, hardly looked at him.\n\n‘Monsieur Barronneau left a widow. She was two-and-twenty. She had gained a reputation for beauty, and (which is often another thing) was beautiful. I continued to live at the Cross of Gold. I married Madame Barronneau. It is not for me to say whether there was any great disparity in such a match. Here I stand, with the contamination of a jail upon me; but it is possible that you may think me better suited to her than her former husband was.’\n\nHe had a certain air of being a handsome man--which he was not; and a certain air of being a well-bred man--which he was not. It was mere swagger and challenge; but in this particular, as in many others, blustering assertion goes for proof, half over the world.\n\n‘Be it as it may, Madame Barronneau approved of me. _That_ is not to prejudice me, I hope?’\n\nHis eye happening to light upon John Baptist with this inquiry, that little man briskly shook his head in the negative, and repeated in an argumentative tone under his breath, altro, altro, altro, altro--an infinite number of times.\n\n‘Now came the difficulties of our position. I am proud. I say nothing in defence of pride, but I am proud. It is also my character to govern. I can’t submit; I must govern. Unfortunately, the property of Madame Rigaud was settled upon herself. Such was the insane act of her late husband. More unfortunately still, she had relations. When a wife’s relations interpose against a husband who is a gentleman, who is proud, and who must govern, the consequences are inimical to peace. There was yet another source of difference between us. Madame Rigaud was unfortunately a little vulgar. I sought to improve her manners and ameliorate her general tone; she (supported in this likewise by her relations) resented my endeavours. Quarrels began to arise between us; and, propagated and exaggerated by the slanders of the relations of Madame Rigaud, to become notorious to the neighbours. It has been said that I treated Madame Rigaud with cruelty. I may have been seen to slap her face--nothing more. I have a light hand; and if I have been seen apparently to correct Madame Rigaud in that manner, I have done it almost playfully.’\n\nIf the playfulness of Monsieur Rigaud were at all expressed by his smile at this point, the relations of Madame Rigaud might have said that they would have much preferred his correcting that unfortunate woman seriously.\n\n‘I am sensitive and brave. I do not advance it as a merit to be sensitive and brave, but it is my character. If the male relations of Madame Rigaud had put themselves forward openly, I should have known how to deal with them. They knew that, and their machinations were conducted in secret; consequently, Madame Rigaud and I were brought into frequent and unfortunate collision. Even when I wanted any little sum of money for my personal expenses, I could not obtain it without collision--and I, too, a man whose character it is to govern! One night, Madame Rigaud and myself were walking amicably--I may say like lovers--on a height overhanging the sea. An evil star occasioned Madame Rigaud to advert to her relations; I reasoned with her on that subject, and remonstrated on the want of duty and devotion manifested in her allowing herself to be influenced by their jealous animosity towards her husband. Madame Rigaud retorted; I retorted; Madame Rigaud grew warm; I grew warm, and provoked her. I admit it. Frankness is a part of my character. At length, Madame Rigaud, in an access of fury that I must ever deplore, threw herself upon me with screams of passion (no doubt those that were overheard at some distance), tore my clothes, tore my hair, lacerated my hands, trampled and trod the dust, and finally leaped over, dashing herself to death upon the rocks below. Such is the train of incidents which malice has perverted into my endeavouring to force from Madame Rigaud a relinquishment of her rights; and, on her persistence in a refusal to make the concession I required, struggling with her--assassinating her!’\n\nHe stepped aside to the ledge where the vine leaves yet lay strewn about, collected two or three, and stood wiping his hands upon them, with his back to the light.\n\n‘Well,’ he demanded after a silence, ‘have you nothing to say to all that?’\n\n‘It’s ugly,’ returned the little man, who had risen, and was brightening his knife upon his shoe, as he leaned an arm against the wall.\n\n‘What do you mean?’\n\nJohn Baptist polished his knife in silence.\n\n‘Do you mean that I have not represented the case correctly?’\n\n‘Al-tro!’ returned John Baptist. The word was an apology now, and stood for ‘Oh, by no means!’\n\n‘What then?’\n\n‘Presidents and tribunals are so prejudiced.’\n\n‘Well,’ cried the other, uneasily flinging the end of his cloak over his shoulder with an oath, ‘let them do their worst!’\n\n‘Truly I think they will,’ murmured John Baptist to himself, as he bent his head to put his knife in his sash.\n\nNothing more was said on either side, though they both began walking to and fro, and necessarily crossed at every turn. Monsieur Rigaud sometimes stopped, as if he were going to put his case in a new light, or make some irate remonstrance; but Signor Cavalletto continuing to go slowly to and fro at a grotesque kind of jog-trot pace with his eyes turned downward, nothing came of these inclinings.\n\nBy-and-by the noise of the key in the lock arrested them both. The sound of voices succeeded, and the tread of feet. The door clashed, the voices and the feet came on, and the prison-keeper slowly ascended the stairs, followed by a guard of soldiers.\n\n‘Now, Monsieur Rigaud,’ said he, pausing for a moment at the grate, with his keys in his hands, ‘have the goodness to come out.’\n\n‘I am to depart in state, I see?’\n\n‘Why, unless you did,’ returned the jailer, ‘you might depart in so many pieces that it would be difficult to get you together again. There’s a crowd, Monsieur Rigaud, and it doesn’t love you.’\n\nHe passed on out of sight, and unlocked and unbarred a low door in the corner of the chamber. ‘Now,’ said he, as he opened it and appeared within, ‘come out.’\n\nThere is no sort of whiteness in all the hues under the sun at all like the whiteness of Monsieur Rigaud’s face as it was then. Neither is there any expression of the human countenance at all like that expression in every little line of which the frightened heart is seen to beat. Both are conventionally compared with death; but the difference is the whole deep gulf between the struggle done, and the fight at its most desperate extremity.\n\nHe lighted another of his paper cigars at his companion’s; put it tightly between his teeth; covered his head with a soft slouched hat; threw the end of his cloak over his shoulder again; and walked out into the side gallery on which the door opened, without taking any further notice of Signor Cavalletto. As to that little man himself, his whole attention had become absorbed in getting near the door and looking out at it. Precisely as a beast might approach the opened gate of his den and eye the freedom beyond, he passed those few moments in watching and peering, until the door was closed upon him.\n\nThere was an officer in command of the soldiers; a stout, serviceable, profoundly calm man, with his drawn sword in his hand, smoking a cigar. He very briefly directed the placing of Monsieur Rigaud in the midst of the party, put himself with consummate indifference at their head, gave the word ‘march!’ and so they all went jingling down the staircase. The door clashed--the key turned--and a ray of unusual light, and a breath of unusual air, seemed to have passed through the jail, vanishing in a tiny wreath of smoke from the cigar.\n\nStill, in his captivity, like a lower animal--like some impatient ape, or roused bear of the smaller species--the prisoner, now left solitary, had jumped upon the ledge, to lose no glimpse of this departure. As he yet stood clasping the grate with both hands, an uproar broke upon his hearing; yells, shrieks, oaths, threats, execrations, all comprehended in it, though (as in a storm) nothing but a raging swell of sound distinctly heard.\n\nExcited into a still greater resemblance to a caged wild animal by his anxiety to know more, the prisoner leaped nimbly down, ran round the chamber, leaped nimbly up again, clasped the grate and tried to shake it, leaped down and ran, leaped up and listened, and never rested until the noise, becoming more and more distant, had died away. How many better prisoners have worn their noble hearts out so; no man thinking of it; not even the beloved of their souls realising it; great kings and governors, who had made them captive, careering in the sunlight jauntily, and men cheering them on. Even the said great personages dying in bed, making exemplary ends and sounding speeches; and polite history, more servile than their instruments, embalming them!\n\nAt last, John Baptist, now able to choose his own spot within the compass of those walls for the exercise of his faculty of going to sleep when he would, lay down upon the bench, with his face turned over on his crossed arms, and slumbered. In his submission, in his lightness, in his good humour, in his short-lived passion, in his easy contentment with hard bread and hard stones, in his ready sleep, in his fits and starts, altogether a true son of the land that gave him birth.\n\nThe wide stare stared itself out for one while; the Sun went down in a red, green, golden glory; the stars came out in the heavens, and the fire-flies mimicked them in the lower air, as men may feebly imitate the goodness of a better order of beings; the long dusty roads and the interminable plains were in repose--and so deep a hush was on the sea, that it scarcely whispered of the time when it shall give up its dead.\n\n# CHAPTER 2 Fellow Travellers\n\n‘No more of yesterday’s howling over yonder to-day, Sir; is there?’\n\n‘I have heard none.’\n\n‘Then you may be sure there _is_ none. When these people howl, they howl to be heard.’\n\n‘Most people do, I suppose.’\n\n‘Ah! but these people are always howling. Never happy otherwise.’\n\n‘Do you mean the Marseilles people?’\n\n‘I mean the French people. They’re always at it. As to Marseilles, we know what Marseilles is. It sent the most insurrectionary tune into the world that was ever composed. It couldn’t exist without allonging and marshonging to something or other--victory or death, or blazes, or something.’\n\nThe speaker, with a whimsical good humour upon him all the time, looked over the parapet-wall with the greatest disparagement of Marseilles; and taking up a determined position by putting his hands in his pockets and rattling his money at it, apostrophised it with a short laugh.\n\n‘Allong and marshong, indeed. It would be more creditable to you, I think, to let other people allong and marshong about their lawful business, instead of shutting ‘em up in quarantine!’\n\n‘Tiresome enough,’ said the other. ‘But we shall be out to-day.’\n\n‘Out to-day!’ repeated the first. ‘It’s almost an aggravation of the enormity, that we shall be out to-day. Out! What have we ever been in for?’\n\n‘For no very strong reason, I must say. But as we come from the East, and as the East is the country of the plague--’\n\n‘The plague!’ repeated the other. ‘That’s my grievance. I have had the plague continually, ever since I have been here. I am like a sane man shut up in a madhouse; I can’t stand the suspicion of the thing. I came here as well as ever I was in my life; but to suspect me of the plague is to give me the plague. And I have had it--and I have got it.’\n\n‘You bear it very well, Mr Meagles,’ said the second speaker, smiling.\n\n‘No. If you knew the real state of the case, that’s the last observation you would think of making. I have been waking up night after night, and saying, _now_ I have got it, _now_ it has developed itself, _now_ I am in for it, _now_ these fellows are making out their case for their precautions. Why, I’d as soon have a spit put through me, and be stuck upon a card in a collection of beetles, as lead the life I have been leading here.’\n\n‘Well, Mr Meagles, say no more about it now it’s over,’ urged a cheerful feminine voice.\n\n‘Over!’ repeated Mr Meagles, who appeared (though without any ill-nature) to be in that peculiar state of mind in which the last word spoken by anybody else is a new injury. ‘Over! and why should I say no more about it because it’s over?’\n\nIt was Mrs Meagles who had spoken to Mr Meagles; and Mrs Meagles was, like Mr Meagles, comely and healthy, with a pleasant English face which had been looking at homely things for five-and-fifty years or more, and shone with a bright reflection of them.\n\n‘There! Never mind, Father, never mind!’ said Mrs Meagles. ‘For goodness sake content yourself with Pet.’\n\n‘With Pet?’ repeated Mr Meagles in his injured vein. Pet, however, being close behind him, touched him on the shoulder, and Mr Meagles immediately forgave Marseilles from the bottom of his heart.\n\nPet was about twenty. A fair girl with rich brown hair hanging free in natural ringlets. A lovely girl, with a frank face, and wonderful eyes; so large, so soft, so bright, set to such perfection in her kind good head. She was round and fresh and dimpled and spoilt, and there was in Pet an air of timidity and dependence which was the best weakness in the world, and gave her the only crowning charm a girl so pretty and pleasant could have been without.\n\n‘Now, I ask you,’ said Mr Meagles in the blandest confidence, falling back a step himself, and handing his daughter a step forward to illustrate his question: ‘I ask you simply, as between man and man, you know, DID you ever hear of such damned nonsense as putting Pet in quarantine?’\n\n‘It has had the result of making even quarantine enjoyable.’\n\n‘Come!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘that’s something to be sure. I am obliged to you for that remark. Now, Pet, my darling, you had better go along with Mother and get ready for the boat. The officer of health, and a variety of humbugs in cocked hats, are coming off to let us out of this at last: and all we jail-birds are to breakfast together in something approaching to a Christian style again, before we take wing for our different destinations. Tattycoram, stick you close to your young mistress.’\n\nHe spoke to a handsome girl with lustrous dark hair and eyes, and very neatly dressed, who replied with a half curtsey as she passed off in the train of Mrs Meagles and Pet. They crossed the bare scorched terrace all three together, and disappeared through a staring white archway. Mr Meagles’s companion, a grave dark man of forty, still stood looking towards this archway after they were gone; until Mr Meagles tapped him on the arm.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ said he, starting.\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Mr Meagles.\n\nThey took one silent turn backward and forward in the shade of the wall, getting, at the height on which the quarantine barracks are placed, what cool refreshment of sea breeze there was at seven in the morning. Mr Meagles’s companion resumed the conversation.\n\n‘May I ask you,’ he said, ‘what is the name of--’\n\n‘Tattycoram?’ Mr Meagles struck in. ‘I have not the least idea.’\n\n‘I thought,’ said the other, ‘that--’\n\n‘Tattycoram?’ suggested Mr Meagles again.\n\n‘Thank you--that Tattycoram was a name; and I have several times wondered at the oddity of it.’\n\n‘Why, the fact is,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘Mrs Meagles and myself are, you see, practical people.’\n\n‘That you have frequently mentioned in the course of the agreeable and interesting conversations we have had together, walking up and down on these stones,’ said the other, with a half smile breaking through the gravity of his dark face.\n\n‘Practical people. So one day, five or six years ago now, when we took Pet to church at the Foundling--you have heard of the Foundling Hospital in London? Similar to the Institution for the Found Children in Paris?’\n\n‘I have seen it.’\n\n‘Well! One day when we took Pet to church there to hear the music--because, as practical people, it is the business of our lives to show her everything that we think can please her--Mother (my usual name for Mrs Meagles) began to cry so, that it was necessary to take her out. “What’s the matter, Mother?” said I, when we had brought her a little round: “you are frightening Pet, my dear.” “Yes, I know that, Father,” says Mother, “but I think it’s through my loving her so much, that it ever came into my head.” “That ever what came into your head, Mother?” “O dear, dear!” cried Mother, breaking out again, “when I saw all those children ranged tier above tier, and appealing from the father none of them has ever known on earth, to the great Father of us all in Heaven, I thought, does any wretched mother ever come here, and look among those young faces, wondering which is the poor child she brought into this forlorn world, never through all its life to know her love, her kiss, her face, her voice, even her name!” Now that was practical in Mother, and I told her so. I said, “Mother, that’s what I call practical in you, my dear.”’\n\nThe other, not unmoved, assented.\n\n‘So I said next day: Now, Mother, I have a proposition to make that I think you’ll approve of. Let us take one of those same little children to be a little maid to Pet. We are practical people. So if we should find her temper a little defective, or any of her ways a little wide of ours, we shall know what we have to take into account. We shall know what an immense deduction must be made from all the influences and experiences that have formed us--no parents, no child-brother or sister, no individuality of home, no Glass Slipper, or Fairy Godmother. And that’s the way we came by Tattycoram.’\n\n‘And the name itself--’\n\n‘By George!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I was forgetting the name itself. Why, she was called in the Institution, Harriet Beadle--an arbitrary name, of course. Now, Harriet we changed into Hattey, and then into Tatty, because, as practical people, we thought even a playful name might be a new thing to her, and might have a softening and affectionate kind of effect, don’t you see? As to Beadle, that I needn’t say was wholly out of the question. If there is anything that is not to be tolerated on any terms, anything that is a type of Jack-in-office insolence and absurdity, anything that represents in coats, waistcoats, and big sticks our English holding on by nonsense after every one has found it out, it is a beadle. You haven’t seen a beadle lately?’\n\n‘As an Englishman who has been more than twenty years in China, no.’\n\n‘Then,’ said Mr Meagles, laying his forefinger on his companion’s breast with great animation, ‘don’t you see a beadle, now, if you can help it. Whenever I see a beadle in full fig, coming down a street on a Sunday at the head of a charity school, I am obliged to turn and run away, or I should hit him. The name of Beadle being out of the question, and the originator of the Institution for these poor foundlings having been a blessed creature of the name of Coram, we gave that name to Pet’s little maid. At one time she was Tatty, and at one time she was Coram, until we got into a way of mixing the two names together, and now she is always Tattycoram.’\n\n‘Your daughter,’ said the other, when they had taken another silent turn to and fro, and, after standing for a moment at the wall glancing down at the sea, had resumed their walk, ‘is your only child, I know, Mr Meagles. May I ask you--in no impertinent curiosity, but because I have had so much pleasure in your society, may never in this labyrinth of a world exchange a quiet word with you again, and wish to preserve an accurate remembrance of you and yours--may I ask you, if I have not gathered from your good wife that you have had other children?’\n\n‘No. No,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘Not exactly other children. One other child.’\n\n‘I am afraid I have inadvertently touched upon a tender theme.’\n\n‘Never mind,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘If I am grave about it, I am not at all sorrowful. It quiets me for a moment, but does not make me unhappy. Pet had a twin sister who died when we could just see her eyes--exactly like Pet’s--above the table, as she stood on tiptoe holding by it.’\n\n‘Ah! indeed, indeed!’\n\n‘Yes, and being practical people, a result has gradually sprung up in the minds of Mrs Meagles and myself which perhaps you may--or perhaps you may not--understand. Pet and her baby sister were so exactly alike, and so completely one, that in our thoughts we have never been able to separate them since. It would be of no use to tell us that our dead child was a mere infant. We have changed that child according to the changes in the child spared to us and always with us. As Pet has grown, that child has grown; as Pet has become more sensible and womanly, her sister has become more sensible and womanly by just the same degrees. It would be as hard to convince me that if I was to pass into the other world to-morrow, I should not, through the mercy of God, be received there by a daughter, just like Pet, as to persuade me that Pet herself is not a reality at my side.’\n\n‘I understand you,’ said the other, gently.\n\n‘As to her,’ pursued her father, ‘the sudden loss of her little picture and playfellow, and her early association with that mystery in which we all have our equal share, but which is not often so forcibly presented to a child, has necessarily had some influence on her character. Then, her mother and I were not young when we married, and Pet has always had a sort of grown-up life with us, though we have tried to adapt ourselves to her. We have been advised more than once when she has been a little ailing, to change climate and air for her as often as we could--especially at about this time of her life--and to keep her amused. So, as I have no need to stick at a bank-desk now (though I have been poor enough in my time I assure you, or I should have married Mrs Meagles long before), we go trotting about the world. This is how you found us staring at the Nile, and the Pyramids, and the Sphinxes, and the Desert, and all the rest of it; and this is how Tattycoram will be a greater traveller in course of time than Captain Cook.’\n\n‘I thank you,’ said the other, ‘very heartily for your confidence.’\n\n‘Don’t mention it,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘I am sure you are quite welcome. And now, Mr Clennam, perhaps I may ask you whether you have yet come to a decision where to go next?’\n\n‘Indeed, no. I am such a waif and stray everywhere, that I am liable to be drifted where any current may set.’\n\n‘It’s extraordinary to me--if you’ll excuse my freedom in saying so--that you don’t go straight to London,’ said Mr Meagles, in the tone of a confidential adviser.\n\n‘Perhaps I shall.’\n\n‘Ay! But I mean with a will.’\n\n‘I have no will. That is to say,’--he coloured a little,--‘next to none that I can put in action now. Trained by main force; broken, not bent; heavily ironed with an object on which I was never consulted and which was never mine; shipped away to the other end of the world before I was of age, and exiled there until my father’s death there, a year ago; always grinding in a mill I always hated; what is to be expected from me in middle life? Will, purpose, hope? All those lights were extinguished before I could sound the words.’\n\n‘Light ‘em up again!’ said Mr Meagles.\n\n‘Ah! Easily said. I am the son, Mr Meagles, of a hard father and mother. I am the only child of parents who weighed, measured, and priced everything; for whom what could not be weighed, measured, and priced, had no existence. Strict people as the phrase is, professors of a stern religion, their very religion was a gloomy sacrifice of tastes and sympathies that were never their own, offered up as a part of a bargain for the security of their possessions. Austere faces, inexorable discipline, penance in this world and terror in the next--nothing graceful or gentle anywhere, and the void in my cowed heart everywhere--this was my childhood, if I may so misuse the word as to apply it to such a beginning of life.’\n\n‘Really though?’ said Mr Meagles, made very uncomfortable by the picture offered to his imagination. ‘That was a tough commencement. But come! You must now study, and profit by, all that lies beyond it, like a practical man.’\n\n‘If the people who are usually called practical, were practical in your direction--’\n\n‘Why, so they are!’ said Mr Meagles.\n\n‘Are they indeed?’\n\n‘Well, I suppose so,’ returned Mr Meagles, thinking about it. ‘Eh? One can but _be_ practical, and Mrs Meagles and myself are nothing else.’\n\n‘My unknown course is easier and more helpful than I had expected to find it, then,’ said Clennam, shaking his head with his grave smile. ‘Enough of me. Here is the boat.’\n\nThe boat was filled with the cocked hats to which Mr Meagles entertained a national objection; and the wearers of those cocked hats landed and came up the steps, and all the impounded travellers congregated together. There was then a mighty production of papers on the part of the cocked hats, and a calling over of names, and great work of signing, sealing, stamping, inking, and sanding, with exceedingly blurred, gritty, and undecipherable results. Finally, everything was done according to rule, and the travellers were at liberty to depart whithersoever they would.\n\nThey made little account of stare and glare, in the new pleasure of recovering their freedom, but flitted across the harbour in gay boats, and reassembled at a great hotel, whence the sun was excluded by closed lattices, and where bare paved floors, lofty ceilings, and resounding corridors tempered the intense heat. There, a great table in a great room was soon profusely covered with a superb repast; and the quarantine quarters became bare indeed, remembered among dainty dishes, southern fruits, cooled wines, flowers from Genoa, snow from the mountain tops, and all the colours of the rainbow flashing in the mirrors.\n\n‘But I bear those monotonous walls no ill-will now,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘One always begins to forgive a place as soon as it’s left behind; I dare say a prisoner begins to relent towards his prison, after he is let out.’\n\nThey were about thirty in company, and all talking; but necessarily in groups. Father and Mother Meagles sat with their daughter between them, the last three on one side of the table: on the opposite side sat Mr Clennam; a tall French gentleman with raven hair and beard, of a swart and terrible, not to say genteelly diabolical aspect, but who had shown himself the mildest of men; and a handsome young Englishwoman, travelling quite alone, who had a proud observant face, and had either withdrawn herself from the rest or been avoided by the rest--nobody, herself excepted perhaps, could have quite decided which. The rest of the party were of the usual materials: travellers on business, and travellers for pleasure; officers from India on leave; merchants in the Greek and Turkey trades; a clerical English husband in a meek strait-waistcoat, on a wedding trip with his young wife; a majestic English mama and papa, of the patrician order, with a family of three growing-up daughters, who were keeping a journal for the confusion of their fellow-creatures; and a deaf old English mother, tough in travel, with a very decidedly grown-up daughter indeed, which daughter went sketching about the universe in the expectation of ultimately toning herself off into the married state.\n\nThe reserved Englishwoman took up Mr Meagles in his last remark.\n\n‘Do you mean that a prisoner forgives his prison?’ said she, slowly and with emphasis.\n\n‘That was my speculation, Miss Wade. I don’t pretend to know positively how a prisoner might feel. I never was one before.’\n\n‘Mademoiselle doubts,’ said the French gentleman in his own language, ‘it’s being so easy to forgive?’\n\n‘I do.’\n\nPet had to translate this passage to Mr Meagles, who never by any accident acquired any knowledge whatever of the language of any country into which he travelled. ‘Oh!’ said he. ‘Dear me! But that’s a pity, isn’t it?’\n\n‘That I am not credulous?’ said Miss Wade.\n\n‘Not exactly that. Put it another way. That you can’t believe it easy to forgive.’\n\n‘My experience,’ she quietly returned, ‘has been correcting my belief in many respects, for some years. It is our natural progress, I have heard.’\n\n‘Well, well! But it’s not natural to bear malice, I hope?’ said Mr Meagles, cheerily.\n\n‘If I had been shut up in any place to pine and suffer, I should always hate that place and wish to burn it down, or raze it to the ground. I know no more.’\n\n‘Strong, sir?’ said Mr Meagles to the Frenchman; it being another of his habits to address individuals of all nations in idiomatic English, with a perfect conviction that they were bound to understand it somehow. ‘Rather forcible in our fair friend, you’ll agree with me, I think?’\n\nThe French gentleman courteously replied, ‘Plait-il?’ To which Mr Meagles returned with much satisfaction, ‘You are right. My opinion.’\n\nThe breakfast beginning by-and-by to languish, Mr Meagles made the company a speech. It was short enough and sensible enough, considering that it was a speech at all, and hearty. It merely went to the effect that as they had all been thrown together by chance, and had all preserved a good understanding together, and were now about to disperse, and were not likely ever to find themselves all together again, what could they do better than bid farewell to one another, and give one another good-speed in a simultaneous glass of cool champagne all round the table? It was done, and with a general shaking of hands the assembly broke up for ever.\n\nThe solitary young lady all this time had said no more. She rose with the rest, and silently withdrew to a remote corner of the great room, where she sat herself on a couch in a window, seeming to watch the reflection of the water as it made a silver quivering on the bars of the lattice. She sat, turned away from the whole length of the apartment, as if she were lonely of her own haughty choice. And yet it would have been as difficult as ever to say, positively, whether she avoided the rest, or was avoided.\n\nThe shadow in which she sat, falling like a gloomy veil across her forehead, accorded very well with the character of her beauty. One could hardly see the face, so still and scornful, set off by the arched dark eyebrows, and the folds of dark hair, without wondering what its expression would be if a change came over it. That it could soften or relent, appeared next to impossible. That it could deepen into anger or any extreme of defiance, and that it must change in that direction when it changed at all, would have been its peculiar impression upon most observers. It was dressed and trimmed into no ceremony of expression. Although not an open face, there was no pretence in it. ‘I am self-contained and self-reliant; your opinion is nothing to me; I have no interest in you, care nothing for you, and see and hear you with indifference’--this it said plainly. It said so in the proud eyes, in the lifted nostril, in the handsome but compressed and even cruel mouth. Cover either two of those channels of expression, and the third would have said so still. Mask them all, and the mere turn of the head would have shown an unsubduable nature.\n\nPet had moved up to her (she had been the subject of remark among her family and Mr Clennam, who were now the only other occupants of the room), and was standing at her side.\n\n‘Are you’--she turned her eyes, and Pet faltered--‘expecting any one to meet you here, Miss Wade?’\n\n‘I? No.’\n‘Father is sending to the Poste Restante. Shall he have the pleasure of directing the messenger to ask if there are any letters for you?’\n\n‘I thank him, but I know there can be none.’\n\n‘We are afraid,’ said Pet, sitting down beside her, shyly and half tenderly, ‘that you will feel quite deserted when we are all gone.’\n\n‘Indeed!’\n\n‘Not,’ said Pet, apologetically and embarrassed by her eyes, ‘not, of course, that we are any company to you, or that we have been able to be so, or that we thought you wished it.’\n\n‘I have not intended to make it understood that I did wish it.’\n\n‘No. Of course. But--in short,’ said Pet, timidly touching her hand as it lay impassive on the sofa between them, ‘will you not allow Father to tender you any slight assistance or service? He will be very glad.’\n\n‘Very glad,’ said Mr Meagles, coming forward with his wife and Clennam. ‘Anything short of speaking the language, I shall be delighted to undertake, I am sure.’\n\n‘I am obliged to you,’ she returned, ‘but my arrangements are made, and I prefer to go my own way in my own manner.’\n\n‘_Do_ you?’ said Mr Meagles to himself, as he surveyed her with a puzzled look. ‘Well! There’s character in that, too.’\n\n‘I am not much used to the society of young ladies, and I am afraid I may not show my appreciation of it as others might. A pleasant journey to you. Good-bye!’\n\nShe would not have put out her hand, it seemed, but that Mr Meagles put out his so straight before her that she could not pass it. She put hers in it, and it lay there just as it had lain upon the couch.\n\n‘Good-bye!’ said Mr Meagles. ‘This is the last good-bye upon the list, for Mother and I have just said it to Mr Clennam here, and he only waits to say it to Pet. Good-bye! We may never meet again.’\n\n‘In our course through life we shall meet the people who are coming to meet _us_, from many strange places and by many strange roads,’ was the composed reply; ‘and what it is set to us to do to them, and what it is set to them to do to us, will all be done.’\n\nThere was something in the manner of these words that jarred upon Pet’s ear. It implied that what was to be done was necessarily evil, and it caused her to say in a whisper, ‘O Father!’ and to shrink childishly, in her spoilt way, a little closer to him. This was not lost on the speaker.\n\n‘Your pretty daughter,’ she said, ‘starts to think of such things. Yet,’ looking full upon her, ‘you may be sure that there are men and women already on their road, who have their business to do with _you_, and who will do it. Of a certainty they will do it. They may be coming hundreds, thousands, of miles over the sea there; they may be close at hand now; they may be coming, for anything you know or anything you can do to prevent it, from the vilest sweepings of this very town.’\n\nWith the coldest of farewells, and with a certain worn expression on her beauty that gave it, though scarcely yet in its prime, a wasted look, she left the room.\n\nNow, there were many stairs and passages that she had to traverse in passing from that part of the spacious house to the chamber she had secured for her own occupation. When she had almost completed the journey, and was passing along the gallery in which her room was, she heard an angry sound of muttering and sobbing. A door stood open, and within she saw the attendant upon the girl she had just left; the maid with the curious name.\n\nShe stood still, to look at this maid. A sullen, passionate girl! Her rich black hair was all about her face, her face was flushed and hot, and as she sobbed and raged, she plucked at her lips with an unsparing hand.\n\n‘Selfish brutes!’ said the girl, sobbing and heaving between whiles. ‘Not caring what becomes of me! Leaving me here hungry and thirsty and tired, to starve, for anything they care! Beasts! Devils! Wretches!’\n\n‘My poor girl, what is the matter?’\n\nShe looked up suddenly, with reddened eyes, and with her hands suspended, in the act of pinching her neck, freshly disfigured with great scarlet blots. ‘It’s nothing to you what’s the matter. It don’t signify to any one.’\n\n‘O yes it does; I am sorry to see you so.’\n\n‘You are not sorry,’ said the girl. ‘You are glad. You know you are glad. I never was like this but twice over in the quarantine yonder; and both times you found me. I am afraid of you.’\n\n‘Afraid of me?’\n\n‘Yes. You seem to come like my own anger, my own malice, my own--whatever it is--I don’t know what it is. But I am ill-used, I am ill-used, I am ill-used!’ Here the sobs and the tears, and the tearing hand, which had all been suspended together since the first surprise, went on together anew.\n\nThe visitor stood looking at her with a strange attentive smile. It was wonderful to see the fury of the contest in the girl, and the bodily struggle she made as if she were rent by the Demons of old.\n\n‘I am younger than she is by two or three years, and yet it’s me that looks after her, as if I was old, and it’s she that’s always petted and called Baby! I detest the name. I hate her! They make a fool of her, they spoil her. She thinks of nothing but herself, she thinks no more of me than if I was a stock and a stone!’ So the girl went on.\n\n‘You must have patience.’\n\n‘I _won’t_ have patience!’\n\n‘If they take much care of themselves, and little or none of you, you must not mind it.’\n\nI _will_ mind it.’\n\n‘Hush! Be more prudent. You forget your dependent position.’\n\n‘I don’t care for that. I’ll run away. I’ll do some mischief. I won’t bear it; I can’t bear it; I shall die if I try to bear it!’\n\nThe observer stood with her hand upon her own bosom, looking at the girl, as one afflicted with a diseased part might curiously watch the dissection and exposition of an analogous case.\n\nThe girl raged and battled with all the force of her youth and fulness of life, until by little and little her passionate exclamations trailed off into broken murmurs as if she were in pain. By corresponding degrees she sank into a chair, then upon her knees, then upon the ground beside the bed, drawing the coverlet with her, half to hide her shamed head and wet hair in it, and half, as it seemed, to embrace it, rather than have nothing to take to her repentant breast.\n\n‘Go away from me, go away from me! When my temper comes upon me, I am mad. I know I might keep it off if I only tried hard enough, and sometimes I do try hard enough, and at other times I don’t and won’t. What have I said! I knew when I said it, it was all lies. They think I am being taken care of somewhere, and have all I want. They are nothing but good to me. I love them dearly; no people could ever be kinder to a thankless creature than they always are to me. Do, do go away, for I am afraid of you. I am afraid of myself when I feel my temper coming, and I am as much afraid of you. Go away from me, and let me pray and cry myself better!’\n\nThe day passed on; and again the wide stare stared itself out; and the hot night was on Marseilles; and through it the caravan of the morning, all dispersed, went their appointed ways. And thus ever by day and night, under the sun and under the stars, climbing the dusty hills and toiling along the weary plains, journeying by land and journeying by sea, coming and going so strangely, to meet and to act and react on one another, move all we restless travellers through the pilgrimage of life.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. Home\n\nIt was a Sunday evening in London, gloomy, close, and stale. Maddening church bells of all degrees of dissonance, sharp and flat, cracked and clear, fast and slow, made the brick-and-mortar echoes hideous. Melancholy streets, in a penitential garb of soot, steeped the souls of the people who were condemned to look at them out of windows, in dire despondency. In every thoroughfare, up almost every alley, and down almost every turning, some doleful bell was throbbing, jerking, tolling, as if the Plague were in the city and the dead-carts were going round. Everything was bolted and barred that could by possibility furnish relief to an overworked people. No pictures, no unfamiliar animals, no rare plants or flowers, no natural or artificial wonders of the ancient world--all _taboo_ with that enlightened strictness, that the ugly South Sea gods in the British Museum might have supposed themselves at home again. Nothing to see but streets, streets, streets. Nothing to breathe but streets, streets, streets. Nothing to change the brooding mind, or raise it up. Nothing for the spent toiler to do, but to compare the monotony of his seventh day with the monotony of his six days, think what a weary life he led, and make the best of it--or the worst, according to the probabilities.\n\nAt such a happy time, so propitious to the interests of religion and morality, Mr Arthur Clennam, newly arrived from Marseilles by way of Dover, and by Dover coach the Blue-eyed Maid, sat in the window of a coffee-house on Ludgate Hill. Ten thousand responsible houses surrounded him, frowning as heavily on the streets they composed, as if they were every one inhabited by the ten young men of the Calender’s story, who blackened their faces and bemoaned their miseries every night. Fifty thousand lairs surrounded him where people lived so unwholesomely that fair water put into their crowded rooms on Saturday night, would be corrupt on Sunday morning; albeit my lord, their county member, was amazed that they failed to sleep in company with their butcher’s meat. Miles of close wells and pits of houses, where the inhabitants gasped for air, stretched far away towards every point of the compass. Through the heart of the town a deadly sewer ebbed and flowed, in the place of a fine fresh river. What secular want could the million or so of human beings whose daily labour, six days in the week, lay among these Arcadian objects, from the sweet sameness of which they had no escape between the cradle and the grave--what secular want could they possibly have upon their seventh day? Clearly they could want nothing but a stringent policeman.\n\nMr Arthur Clennam sat in the window of the coffee-house on Ludgate Hill, counting one of the neighbouring bells, making sentences and burdens of songs out of it in spite of himself, and wondering how many sick people it might be the death of in the course of the year. As the hour approached, its changes of measure made it more and more exasperating. At the quarter, it went off into a condition of deadly-lively importunity, urging the populace in a voluble manner to Come to church, Come to church, Come to church! At the ten minutes, it became aware that the congregation would be scanty, and slowly hammered out in low spirits, They _won’t_ come, they _won’t_ come, they _won’t_ come! At the five minutes, it abandoned hope, and shook every house in the neighbourhood for three hundred seconds, with one dismal swing per second, as a groan of despair.\n\n‘Thank Heaven!’ said Clennam, when the hour struck, and the bell stopped.\n\nBut its sound had revived a long train of miserable Sundays, and the procession would not stop with the bell, but continued to march on. ‘Heaven forgive me,’ said he, ‘and those who trained me. How I have hated this day!’\n\nThere was the dreary Sunday of his childhood, when he sat with his hands before him, scared out of his senses by a horrible tract which commenced business with the poor child by asking him in its title, why he was going to Perdition?--a piece of curiosity that he really, in a frock and drawers, was not in a condition to satisfy--and which, for the further attraction of his infant mind, had a parenthesis in every other line with some such hiccupping reference as 2 Ep. Thess. c. iii, v. 6 & 7. There was the sleepy Sunday of his boyhood, when, like a military deserter, he was marched to chapel by a picquet of teachers three times a day, morally handcuffed to another boy; and when he would willingly have bartered two meals of indigestible sermon for another ounce or two of inferior mutton at his scanty dinner in the flesh. There was the interminable Sunday of his nonage; when his mother, stern of face and unrelenting of heart, would sit all day behind a Bible--bound, like her own construction of it, in the hardest, barest, and straitest boards, with one dinted ornament on the cover like the drag of a chain, and a wrathful sprinkling of red upon the edges of the leaves--as if it, of all books! were a fortification against sweetness of temper, natural affection, and gentle intercourse. There was the resentful Sunday of a little later, when he sat down glowering and glooming through the tardy length of the day, with a sullen sense of injury in his heart, and no more real knowledge of the beneficent history of the New Testament than if he had been bred among idolaters. There was a legion of Sundays, all days of unserviceable bitterness and mortification, slowly passing before him.\n\n‘Beg pardon, sir,’ said a brisk waiter, rubbing the table. ‘Wish see bed-room?’\n\n‘Yes. I have just made up my mind to do it.’\n\n‘Chaymaid!’ cried the waiter. ‘Gelen box num seven wish see room!’\n\n‘Stay!’ said Clennam, rousing himself. ‘I was not thinking of what I said; I answered mechanically. I am not going to sleep here. I am going home.’\n\n‘Deed, sir? Chaymaid! Gelen box num seven, not go sleep here, gome.’\n\nHe sat in the same place as the day died, looking at the dull houses opposite, and thinking, if the disembodied spirits of former inhabitants were ever conscious of them, how they must pity themselves for their old places of imprisonment. Sometimes a face would appear behind the dingy glass of a window, and would fade away into the gloom as if it had seen enough of life and had vanished out of it. Presently the rain began to fall in slanting lines between him and those houses, and people began to collect under cover of the public passage opposite, and to look out hopelessly at the sky as the rain dropped thicker and faster. Then wet umbrellas began to appear, draggled skirts, and mud. What the mud had been doing with itself, or where it came from, who could say? But it seemed to collect in a moment, as a crowd will, and in five minutes to have splashed all the sons and daughters of Adam. The lamplighter was going his rounds now; and as the fiery jets sprang up under his touch, one might have fancied them astonished at being suffered to introduce any show of brightness into such a dismal scene.\n\nMr Arthur Clennam took up his hat and buttoned his coat, and walked out. In the country, the rain would have developed a thousand fresh scents, and every drop would have had its bright association with some beautiful form of growth or life. In the city, it developed only foul stale smells, and was a sickly, lukewarm, dirt-stained, wretched addition to the gutters.\n\nHe crossed by St Paul’s and went down, at a long angle, almost to the water’s edge, through some of the crooked and descending streets which lie (and lay more crookedly and closely then) between the river and Cheapside. Passing, now the mouldy hall of some obsolete Worshipful Company, now the illuminated windows of a Congregationless Church that seemed to be waiting for some adventurous Belzoni to dig it out and discover its history; passing silent warehouses and wharves, and here and there a narrow alley leading to the river, where a wretched little bill, FOUND DROWNED, was weeping on the wet wall; he came at last to the house he sought. An old brick house, so dingy as to be all but black, standing by itself within a gateway. Before it, a square court-yard where a shrub or two and a patch of grass were as rank (which is saying much) as the iron railings enclosing them were rusty; behind it, a jumble of roots. It was a double house, with long, narrow, heavily-framed windows. Many years ago, it had had it in its mind to slide down sideways; it had been propped up, however, and was leaning on some half-dozen gigantic crutches: which gymnasium for the neighbouring cats, weather-stained, smoke-blackened, and overgrown with weeds, appeared in these latter days to be no very sure reliance.\n\n‘Nothing changed,’ said the traveller, stopping to look round. ‘Dark and miserable as ever. A light in my mother’s window, which seems never to have been extinguished since I came home twice a year from school, and dragged my box over this pavement. Well, well, well!’\n\nHe went up to the door, which had a projecting canopy in carved work of festooned jack-towels and children’s heads with water on the brain, designed after a once-popular monumental pattern, and knocked. A shuffling step was soon heard on the stone floor of the hall, and the door was opened by an old man, bent and dried, but with keen eyes.\n\nHe had a candle in his hand, and he held it up for a moment to assist his keen eyes. ‘Ah, Mr Arthur?’ he said, without any emotion, ‘you are come at last? Step in.’\n\nMr Arthur stepped in and shut the door.\n\n‘Your figure is filled out, and set,’ said the old man, turning to look at him with the light raised again, and shaking his head; ‘but you don’t come up to your father in my opinion. Nor yet your mother.’\n\n‘How is my mother?’\n\n‘She is as she always is now. Keeps her room when not actually bedridden, and hasn’t been out of it fifteen times in as many years, Arthur.’ They had walked into a spare, meagre dining-room. The old man had put the candlestick upon the table, and, supporting his right elbow with his left hand, was smoothing his leathern jaws while he looked at the visitor. The visitor offered his hand. The old man took it coldly enough, and seemed to prefer his jaws, to which he returned as soon as he could.\n\n‘I doubt if your mother will approve of your coming home on the Sabbath, Arthur,’ he said, shaking his head warily.\n\n‘You wouldn’t have me go away again?’\n\n‘Oh! I? I? I am not the master. It’s not what _I_ would have. I have stood between your father and mother for a number of years. I don’t pretend to stand between your mother and you.’\n\n‘Will you tell her that I have come home?’\n\n‘Yes, Arthur, yes. Oh, to be sure! I’ll tell her that you have come home. Please to wait here. You won’t find the room changed.’ He took another candle from a cupboard, lighted it, left the first on the table, and went upon his errand. He was a short, bald old man, in a high-shouldered black coat and waistcoat, drab breeches, and long drab gaiters. He might, from his dress, have been either clerk or servant, and in fact had long been both. There was nothing about him in the way of decoration but a watch, which was lowered into the depths of its proper pocket by an old black ribbon, and had a tarnished copper key moored above it, to show where it was sunk. His head was awry, and he had a one-sided, crab-like way with him, as if his foundations had yielded at about the same time as those of the house, and he ought to have been propped up in a similar manner.\n\n‘How weak am I,’ said Arthur Clennam, when he was gone, ‘that I could shed tears at this reception! I, who have never experienced anything else; who have never expected anything else.’\n\nHe not only could, but did. It was the momentary yielding of a nature that had been disappointed from the dawn of its perceptions, but had not quite given up all its hopeful yearnings yet. He subdued it, took up the candle, and examined the room. The old articles of furniture were in their old places; the Plagues of Egypt, much the dimmer for the fly and smoke plagues of London, were framed and glazed upon the walls. There was the old cellaret with nothing in it, lined with lead, like a sort of coffin in compartments; there was the old dark closet, also with nothing in it, of which he had been many a time the sole contents, in days of punishment, when he had regarded it as the veritable entrance to that bourne to which the tract had found him galloping. There was the large, hard-featured clock on the sideboard, which he used to see bending its figured brows upon him with a savage joy when he was behind-hand with his lessons, and which, when it was wound up once a week with an iron handle, used to sound as if it were growling in ferocious anticipation of the miseries into which it would bring him. But here was the old man come back, saying, ‘Arthur, I’ll go before and light you.’\n\nArthur followed him up the staircase, which was panelled off into spaces like so many mourning tablets, into a dim bed-chamber, the floor of which had gradually so sunk and settled, that the fire-place was in a dell. On a black bier-like sofa in this hollow, propped up behind with one great angular black bolster like the block at a state execution in the good old times, sat his mother in a widow’s dress.\n\nShe and his father had been at variance from his earliest remembrance. To sit speechless himself in the midst of rigid silence, glancing in dread from the one averted face to the other, had been the peacefullest occupation of his childhood. She gave him one glassy kiss, and four stiff fingers muffled in worsted. This embrace concluded, he sat down on the opposite side of her little table. There was a fire in the grate, as there had been night and day for fifteen years. There was a kettle on the hob, as there had been night and day for fifteen years. There was a little mound of damped ashes on the top of the fire, and another little mound swept together under the grate, as there had been night and day for fifteen years. There was a smell of black dye in the airless room, which the fire had been drawing out of the crape and stuff of the widow’s dress for fifteen months, and out of the bier-like sofa for fifteen years.\n\n‘Mother, this is a change from your old active habits.’\n\n‘The world has narrowed to these dimensions, Arthur,’ she replied, glancing round the room. ‘It is well for me that I never set my heart upon its hollow vanities.’\n\nThe old influence of her presence and her stern strong voice, so gathered about her son, that he felt conscious of a renewal of the timid chill and reserve of his childhood.\n\n‘Do you never leave your room, mother?’\n\n‘What with my rheumatic affection, and what with its attendant debility or nervous weakness--names are of no matter now--I have lost the use of my limbs. I never leave my room. I have not been outside this door for--tell him for how long,’ she said, speaking over her shoulder.\n\n‘A dozen year next Christmas,’ returned a cracked voice out of the dimness behind.\n\n‘Is that Affery?’ said Arthur, looking towards it.\n\nThe cracked voice replied that it was Affery: and an old woman came forward into what doubtful light there was, and kissed her hand once; then subsided again into the dimness.\n\n‘I am able,’ said Mrs Clennam, with a slight motion of her worsted-muffled right hand toward a chair on wheels, standing before a tall writing cabinet close shut up, ‘I am able to attend to my business duties, and I am thankful for the privilege. It is a great privilege. But no more of business on this day. It is a bad night, is it not?’\n\n‘Yes, mother.’\n\n‘Does it snow?’\n\n‘Snow, mother? And we only yet in September?’\n\n‘All seasons are alike to me,’ she returned, with a grim kind of luxuriousness. ‘I know nothing of summer and winter, shut up here. The Lord has been pleased to put me beyond all that.’ With her cold grey eyes and her cold grey hair, and her immovable face, as stiff as the folds of her stony head-dress,--her being beyond the reach of the seasons seemed but a fit sequence to her being beyond the reach of all changing emotions.\n\nOn her little table lay two or three books, her handkerchief, a pair of steel spectacles newly taken off, and an old-fashioned gold watch in a heavy double case. Upon this last object her son’s eyes and her own now rested together.\n\n‘I see that you received the packet I sent you on my father’s death, safely, mother.’\n\n‘You see.’\n\n‘I never knew my father to show so much anxiety on any subject, as that his watch should be sent straight to you.’\n\n‘I keep it here as a remembrance of your father.’\n\n‘It was not until the last, that he expressed the wish; when he could only put his hand upon it, and very indistinctly say to me “your mother.” A moment before, I thought him wandering in his mind, as he had been for many hours--I think he had no consciousness of pain in his short illness--when I saw him turn himself in his bed and try to open it.’\n\n‘Was your father, then, not wandering in his mind when he tried to open it?’\n\n‘No. He was quite sensible at that time.’\n\nMrs Clennam shook her head; whether in dismissal of the deceased or opposing herself to her son’s opinion, was not clearly expressed.\n\n‘After my father’s death I opened it myself, thinking there might be, for anything I knew, some memorandum there. However, as I need not tell you, mother, there was nothing but the old silk watch-paper worked in beads, which you found (no doubt) in its place between the cases, where I found and left it.’\n\nMrs Clennam signified assent; then added, ‘No more of business on this day,’ and then added, ‘Affery, it is nine o’clock.’\n\nUpon this, the old woman cleared the little table, went out of the room, and quickly returned with a tray on which was a dish of little rusks and a small precise pat of butter, cool, symmetrical, white, and plump. The old man who had been standing by the door in one attitude during the whole interview, looking at the mother up-stairs as he had looked at the son down-stairs, went out at the same time, and, after a longer absence, returned with another tray on which was the greater part of a bottle of port wine (which, to judge by his panting, he had brought from the cellar), a lemon, a sugar-basin, and a spice box. With these materials and the aid of the kettle, he filled a tumbler with a hot and odorous mixture, measured out and compounded with as much nicety as a physician’s prescription. Into this mixture Mrs Clennam dipped certain of the rusks, and ate them; while the old woman buttered certain other of the rusks, which were to be eaten alone. When the invalid had eaten all the rusks and drunk all the mixture, the two trays were removed; and the books and the candle, watch, handkerchief, and spectacles were replaced upon the table. She then put on the spectacles and read certain passages aloud from a book--sternly, fiercely, wrathfully--praying that her enemies (she made them by her tone and manner expressly hers) might be put to the edge of the sword, consumed by fire, smitten by plagues and leprosy, that their bones might be ground to dust, and that they might be utterly exterminated. As she read on, years seemed to fall away from her son like the imaginings of a dream, and all the old dark horrors of his usual preparation for the sleep of an innocent child to overshadow him.\n\nShe shut the book and remained for a little time with her face shaded by her hand. So did the old man, otherwise still unchanged in attitude; so, probably, did the old woman in her dimmer part of the room. Then the sick woman was ready for bed.\n\n‘Good night, Arthur. Affery will see to your accommodation. Only touch me, for my hand is tender.’ He touched the worsted muffling of her hand--that was nothing; if his mother had been sheathed in brass there would have been no new barrier between them--and followed the old man and woman down-stairs.\n\nThe latter asked him, when they were alone together among the heavy shadows of the dining-room, would he have some supper?\n\n‘No, Affery, no supper.’\n\n‘You shall if you like,’ said Affery. ‘There’s her tomorrow’s partridge in the larder--her first this year; say the word and I’ll cook it.’\n\nNo, he had not long dined, and could eat nothing.\n\n‘Have something to drink, then,’ said Affery; ‘you shall have some of her bottle of port, if you like. I’ll tell Jeremiah that you ordered me to bring it you.’\n\nNo; nor would he have that, either.\n\n‘It’s no reason, Arthur,’ said the old woman, bending over him to whisper, ‘that because I am afeared of my life of ‘em, you should be. You’ve got half the property, haven’t you?’\n\n‘Yes, yes.’\n\n‘Well then, don’t you be cowed. You’re clever, Arthur, an’t you?’\n\nHe nodded, as she seemed to expect an answer in the affirmative.\n\n‘Then stand up against them! She’s awful clever, and none but a clever one durst say a word to her. _He’s_ a clever one--oh, he’s a clever one!--and he gives it her when he has a mind to’t, he does!’\n\n‘Your husband does?’\n\n‘Does? It makes me shake from head to foot, to hear him give it her. My husband, Jeremiah Flintwinch, can conquer even your mother. What can he be but a clever one to do that!’\n\nHis shuffling footstep coming towards them caused her to retreat to the other end of the room. Though a tall, hard-favoured, sinewy old woman, who in her youth might have enlisted in the Foot Guards without much fear of discovery, she collapsed before the little keen-eyed crab-like old man.\n\n‘Now, Affery,’ said he, ‘now, woman, what are you doing? Can’t you find Master Arthur something or another to pick at?’\n\nMaster Arthur repeated his recent refusal to pick at anything.\n\n‘Very well, then,’ said the old man; ‘make his bed. Stir yourself.’ His neck was so twisted that the knotted ends of his white cravat usually dangled under one ear; his natural acerbity and energy, always contending with a second nature of habitual repression, gave his features a swollen and suffused look; and altogether, he had a weird appearance of having hanged himself at one time or other, and of having gone about ever since, halter and all, exactly as some timely hand had cut him down.\n\n‘You’ll have bitter words together to-morrow, Arthur; you and your mother,’ said Jeremiah. ‘Your having given up the business on your father’s death--which she suspects, though we have left it to you to tell her--won’t go off smoothly.’\n\n‘I have given up everything in life for the business, and the time came for me to give up that.’\n\n‘Good!’ cried Jeremiah, evidently meaning Bad. ‘Very good! only don’t expect me to stand between your mother and you, Arthur. I stood between your mother and your father, fending off this, and fending off that, and getting crushed and pounded betwixt em; and I’ve done with such work.’\n\n‘You will never be asked to begin it again for me, Jeremiah.’\n\n‘Good. I’m glad to hear it; because I should have had to decline it, if I had been. That’s enough--as your mother says--and more than enough of such matters on a Sabbath night. Affery, woman, have you found what you want yet?’\n\nShe had been collecting sheets and blankets from a press, and hastened to gather them up, and to reply, ‘Yes, Jeremiah.’ Arthur Clennam helped her by carrying the load himself, wished the old man good night, and went up-stairs with her to the top of the house.\n\nThey mounted up and up, through the musty smell of an old close house, little used, to a large garret bed-room. Meagre and spare, like all the other rooms, it was even uglier and grimmer than the rest, by being the place of banishment for the worn-out furniture. Its movables were ugly old chairs with worn-out seats, and ugly old chairs without any seats; a threadbare patternless carpet, a maimed table, a crippled wardrobe, a lean set of fire-irons like the skeleton of a set deceased, a washing-stand that looked as if it had stood for ages in a hail of dirty soapsuds, and a bedstead with four bare atomies of posts, each terminating in a spike, as if for the dismal accommodation of lodgers who might prefer to impale themselves. Arthur opened the long low window, and looked out upon the old blasted and blackened forest of chimneys, and the old red glare in the sky, which had seemed to him once upon a time but a nightly reflection of the fiery environment that was presented to his childish fancy in all directions, let it look where it would.\n\nHe drew in his head again, sat down at the bedside, and looked on at Affery Flintwinch making the bed.\n\n‘Affery, you were not married when I went away.’\n\nShe screwed her mouth into the form of saying ‘No,’ shook her head, and proceeded to get a pillow into its case.\n\n‘How did it happen?’\n\n‘Why, Jeremiah, o’ course,’ said Affery, with an end of the pillow-case between her teeth.\n\n‘Of course he proposed it, but how did it all come about? I should have thought that neither of you would have married; least of all should I have thought of your marrying each other.’\n\n‘No more should I,’ said Mrs Flintwinch, tying the pillow tightly in its case.\n\n‘That’s what I mean. When did you begin to think otherwise?’\n\n‘Never begun to think otherwise at all,’ said Mrs Flintwinch.\n\nSeeing, as she patted the pillow into its place on the bolster, that he was still looking at her as if waiting for the rest of her reply, she gave it a great poke in the middle, and asked, ‘How could I help myself?’\n\n‘How could you help yourself from being married!’\n\n‘O’ course,’ said Mrs Flintwinch. ‘It was no doing o’ mine. I’d never thought of it. I’d got something to do, without thinking, indeed! She kept me to it (as well as he) when she could go about, and she could go about then.’\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘Well?’ echoed Mrs Flintwinch. ‘That’s what I said myself. Well! What’s the use of considering? If them two clever ones have made up their minds to it, what’s left for _me_ to do? Nothing.’\n\n‘Was it my mother’s project, then?’\n\n‘The Lord bless you, Arthur, and forgive me the wish!’ cried Affery, speaking always in a low tone. ‘If they hadn’t been both of a mind in it, how could it ever have been? Jeremiah never courted me; t’ant likely that he would, after living in the house with me and ordering me about for as many years as he’d done. He said to me one day, he said, “Affery,” he said, “now I am going to tell you something. What do you think of the name of Flintwinch?” “What do I think of it?” I says. “Yes,” he said, “because you’re going to take it,” he said. “Take it?” I says. “Jere-_mi_-ah?” Oh! he’s a clever one!’\n\nMrs Flintwinch went on to spread the upper sheet over the bed, and the blanket over that, and the counterpane over that, as if she had quite concluded her story.\n\n‘Well?’ said Arthur again.\n\n‘Well?’ echoed Mrs Flintwinch again. ‘How could I help myself? He said to me, “Affery, you and me must be married, and I’ll tell you why. She’s failing in health, and she’ll want pretty constant attendance up in her room, and we shall have to be much with her, and there’ll be nobody about now but ourselves when we’re away from her, and altogether it will be more convenient. She’s of my opinion,” he said, “so if you’ll put your bonnet on next Monday morning at eight, we’ll get it over.”’ Mrs Flintwinch tucked up the bed.\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘Well?’ repeated Mrs Flintwinch, ‘I think so! I sits me down and says it. Well!--Jeremiah then says to me, “As to banns, next Sunday being the third time of asking (for I’ve put ‘em up a fortnight), is my reason for naming Monday. She’ll speak to you about it herself, and now she’ll find you prepared, Affery.” That same day she spoke to me, and she said, “So, Affery, I understand that you and Jeremiah are going to be married. I am glad of it, and so are you, with reason. It is a very good thing for you, and very welcome under the circumstances to me. He is a sensible man, and a trustworthy man, and a persevering man, and a pious man.” What could I say when it had come to that? Why, if it had been--a smothering instead of a wedding,’ Mrs Flintwinch cast about in her mind with great pains for this form of expression, ‘I couldn’t have said a word upon it, against them two clever ones.’\n\n‘In good faith, I believe so.’\n\n‘And so you may, Arthur.’\n\n‘Affery, what girl was that in my mother’s room just now?’\n\n‘Girl?’ said Mrs Flintwinch in a rather sharp key.\n\n‘It was a girl, surely, whom I saw near you--almost hidden in the dark corner?’\n\n‘Oh! She? Little Dorrit? _She_‘s nothing; she’s a whim of--hers.’ It was a peculiarity of Affery Flintwinch that she never spoke of Mrs Clennam by name. ‘But there’s another sort of girls than that about. Have you forgot your old sweetheart? Long and long ago, I’ll be bound.’\n\n‘I suffered enough from my mother’s separating us, to remember her. I recollect her very well.’\n\n‘Have you got another?’\n\n‘No.’\n\n‘Here’s news for you, then. She’s well to do now, and a widow. And if you like to have her, why you can.’\n\n‘And how do you know that, Affery?’\n\n‘Them two clever ones have been speaking about it.--There’s Jeremiah on the stairs!’ She was gone in a moment.\n\nMrs Flintwinch had introduced into the web that his mind was busily weaving, in that old workshop where the loom of his youth had stood, the last thread wanting to the pattern. The airy folly of a boy’s love had found its way even into that house, and he had been as wretched under its hopelessness as if the house had been a castle of romance. Little more than a week ago at Marseilles, the face of the pretty girl from whom he had parted with regret, had had an unusual interest for him, and a tender hold upon him, because of some resemblance, real or imagined, to this first face that had soared out of his gloomy life into the bright glories of fancy. He leaned upon the sill of the long low window, and looking out upon the blackened forest of chimneys again, began to dream; for it had been the uniform tendency of this man’s life--so much was wanting in it to think about, so much that might have been better directed and happier to speculate upon--to make him a dreamer, after all.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream\n\nWhen Mrs Flintwinch dreamed, she usually dreamed, unlike the son of her old mistress, with her eyes shut. She had a curiously vivid dream that night, and before she had left the son of her old mistress many hours. In fact it was not at all like a dream; it was so very real in every respect. It happened in this wise.\n\nThe bed-chamber occupied by Mr and Mrs Flintwinch was within a few paces of that to which Mrs Clennam had been so long confined. It was not on the same floor, for it was a room at the side of the house, which was approached by a steep descent of a few odd steps, diverging from the main staircase nearly opposite to Mrs Clennam’s door. It could scarcely be said to be within call, the walls, doors, and panelling of the old place were so cumbrous; but it was within easy reach, in any undress, at any hour of the night, in any temperature. At the head of the bed and within a foot of Mrs Flintwinch’s ear, was a bell, the line of which hung ready to Mrs Clennam’s hand. Whenever this bell rang, up started Affery, and was in the sick room before she was awake.\n\nHaving got her mistress into bed, lighted her lamp, and given her good night, Mrs Flintwinch went to roost as usual, saving that her lord had not yet appeared. It was her lord himself who became--unlike the last theme in the mind, according to the observation of most philosophers--the subject of Mrs Flintwinch’s dream.\n\nIt seemed to her that she awoke after sleeping some hours, and found Jeremiah not yet abed. That she looked at the candle she had left burning, and, measuring the time like King Alfred the Great, was confirmed by its wasted state in her belief that she had been asleep for some considerable period. That she arose thereupon, muffled herself up in a wrapper, put on her shoes, and went out on the staircase, much surprised, to look for Jeremiah.\n\nThe staircase was as wooden and solid as need be, and Affery went straight down it without any of those deviations peculiar to dreams. She did not skim over it, but walked down it, and guided herself by the banisters on account of her candle having died out. In one corner of the hall, behind the house-door, there was a little waiting-room, like a well-shaft, with a long narrow window in it as if it had been ripped up. In this room, which was never used, a light was burning.\n\nMrs Flintwinch crossed the hall, feeling its pavement cold to her stockingless feet, and peeped in between the rusty hinges on the door, which stood a little open. She expected to see Jeremiah fast asleep or in a fit, but he was calmly seated in a chair, awake, and in his usual health. But what--hey?--Lord forgive us!--Mrs Flintwinch muttered some ejaculation to this effect, and turned giddy.\n\nFor, Mr Flintwinch awake, was watching Mr Flintwinch asleep. He sat on one side of the small table, looking keenly at himself on the other side with his chin sunk on his breast, snoring. The waking Flintwinch had his full front face presented to his wife; the sleeping Flintwinch was in profile. The waking Flintwinch was the old original; the sleeping Flintwinch was the double, just as she might have distinguished between a tangible object and its reflection in a glass, Affery made out this difference with her head going round and round.\n\nIf she had had any doubt which was her own Jeremiah, it would have been resolved by his impatience. He looked about him for an offensive weapon, caught up the snuffers, and, before applying them to the cabbage-headed candle, lunged at the sleeper as though he would have run him through the body.\n\n‘Who’s that? What’s the matter?’ cried the sleeper, starting.\n\nMr Flintwinch made a movement with the snuffers, as if he would have enforced silence on his companion by putting them down his throat; the companion, coming to himself, said, rubbing his eyes, ‘I forgot where I was.’\n\n‘You have been asleep,’ snarled Jeremiah, referring to his watch, ‘two hours. You said you would be rested enough if you had a short nap.’\n\n‘I have had a short nap,’ said Double.\n\n‘Half-past two o’clock in the morning,’ muttered Jeremiah. ‘Where’s your hat? Where’s your coat? Where’s the box?’\n\n‘All here,’ said Double, tying up his throat with sleepy carefulness in a shawl. ‘Stop a minute. Now give me the sleeve--not that sleeve, the other one. Ha! I’m not as young as I was.’ Mr Flintwinch had pulled him into his coat with vehement energy. ‘You promised me a second glass after I was rested.’\n\n‘Drink it!’ returned Jeremiah, ‘and--choke yourself, I was going to say--but go, I mean.’ At the same time he produced the identical port-wine bottle, and filled a wine-glass.\n\n‘Her port-wine, I believe?’ said Double, tasting it as if he were in the Docks, with hours to spare. ‘Her health.’\n\nHe took a sip.\n\n‘Your health!’\n\nHe took another sip.\n\n‘His health!’\n\nHe took another sip.\n\n‘And all friends round St Paul’s.’ He emptied and put down the wine-glass half-way through this ancient civic toast, and took up the box. It was an iron box some two feet square, which he carried under his arms pretty easily. Jeremiah watched his manner of adjusting it, with jealous eyes; tried it with his hands, to be sure that he had a firm hold of it; bade him for his life be careful what he was about; and then stole out on tiptoe to open the door for him. Affery, anticipating the last movement, was on the staircase. The sequence of things was so ordinary and natural, that, standing there, she could hear the door open, feel the night air, and see the stars outside.\n\nBut now came the most remarkable part of the dream. She felt so afraid of her husband, that being on the staircase, she had not the power to retreat to her room (which she might easily have done before he had fastened the door), but stood there staring. Consequently when he came up the staircase to bed, candle in hand, he came full upon her. He looked astonished, but said not a word. He kept his eyes upon her, and kept advancing; and she, completely under his influence, kept retiring before him. Thus, she walking backward and he walking forward, they came into their own room. They were no sooner shut in there, than Mr Flintwinch took her by the throat, and shook her until she was black in the face.\n\n‘Why, Affery, woman--Affery!’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘What have you been dreaming of? Wake up, wake up! What’s the matter?’\n\n‘The--the matter, Jeremiah?’ gasped Mrs Flintwinch, rolling her eyes.\n\n‘Why, Affery, woman--Affery! You have been getting out of bed in your sleep, my dear! I come up, after having fallen asleep myself, below, and find you in your wrapper here, with the nightmare. Affery, woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with a friendly grin on his expressive countenance, ‘if you ever have a dream of this sort again, it’ll be a sign of your being in want of physic. And I’ll give you such a dose, old woman--such a dose!’\n\nMrs Flintwinch thanked him and crept into bed.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Family Affairs\n\nAs the city clocks struck nine on Monday morning, Mrs Clennam was wheeled by Jeremiah Flintwinch of the cut-down aspect to her tall cabinet. When she had unlocked and opened it, and had settled herself at its desk, Jeremiah withdrew--as it might be, to hang himself more effectually--and her son appeared.\n\n‘Are you any better this morning, mother?’\n\nShe shook her head, with the same austere air of luxuriousness that she had shown over-night when speaking of the weather. ‘I shall never be better any more. It is well for me, Arthur, that I know it and can bear it.’\n\nSitting with her hands laid separately upon the desk, and the tall cabinet towering before her, she looked as if she were performing on a dumb church organ. Her son thought so (it was an old thought with him), while he took his seat beside it.\n\nShe opened a drawer or two, looked over some business papers, and put them back again. Her severe face had no thread of relaxation in it, by which any explorer could have been guided to the gloomy labyrinth of her thoughts.\n\n‘Shall I speak of our affairs, mother? Are you inclined to enter upon business?’\n\n‘Am I inclined, Arthur? Rather, are you? Your father has been dead a year and more. I have been at your disposal, and waiting your pleasure, ever since.’\n\n‘There was much to arrange before I could leave; and when I did leave, I travelled a little for rest and relief.’\n\nShe turned her face towards him, as not having heard or understood his last words.\n\n‘For rest and relief.’\n\nShe glanced round the sombre room, and appeared from the motion of her lips to repeat the words to herself, as calling it to witness how little of either it afforded her.\n\n‘Besides, mother, you being sole executrix, and having the direction and management of the estate, there remained little business, or I might say none, that I could transact, until you had had time to arrange matters to your satisfaction.’\n\n‘The accounts are made out,’ she returned. ‘I have them here. The vouchers have all been examined and passed. You can inspect them when you like, Arthur; now, if you please.’\n\n‘It is quite enough, mother, to know that the business is completed. Shall I proceed then?’\n\n‘Why not?’ she said, in her frozen way.\n\n‘Mother, our House has done less and less for some years past, and our dealings have been progressively on the decline. We have never shown much confidence, or invited much; we have attached no people to us; the track we have kept is not the track of the time; and we have been left far behind. I need not dwell on this to you, mother. You know it necessarily.’\n\n‘I know what you mean,’ she answered, in a qualified tone.\n\n‘Even this old house in which we speak,’ pursued her son, ‘is an instance of what I say. In my father’s earlier time, and in his uncle’s time before him, it was a place of business--really a place of business, and business resort. Now, it is a mere anomaly and incongruity here, out of date and out of purpose. All our consignments have long been made to Rovinghams’ the commission-merchants; and although, as a check upon them, and in the stewardship of my father’s resources, your judgment and watchfulness have been actively exerted, still those qualities would have influenced my father’s fortunes equally, if you had lived in any private dwelling: would they not?’\n\n‘Do you consider,’ she returned, without answering his question, ‘that a house serves no purpose, Arthur, in sheltering your infirm and afflicted--justly infirm and righteously afflicted--mother?’\n\n‘I was speaking only of business purposes.’\n\n‘With what object?’\n\n‘I am coming to it.’\n\n‘I foresee,’ she returned, fixing her eyes upon him, ‘what it is. But the Lord forbid that I should repine under any visitation. In my sinfulness I merit bitter disappointment, and I accept it.’\n\n‘Mother, I grieve to hear you speak like this, though I have had my apprehensions that you would--’\n\n‘You knew I would. You knew _me_,’ she interrupted.\n\nHer son paused for a moment. He had struck fire out of her, and was surprised. ‘Well!’ she said, relapsing into stone. ‘Go on. Let me hear.’\n\n‘You have anticipated, mother, that I decide for my part, to abandon the business. I have done with it. I will not take upon myself to advise you; you will continue it, I see. If I had any influence with you, I would simply use it to soften your judgment of me in causing you this disappointment: to represent to you that I have lived the half of a long term of life, and have never before set my own will against yours. I cannot say that I have been able to conform myself, in heart and spirit, to your rules; I cannot say that I believe my forty years have been profitable or pleasant to myself, or any one; but I have habitually submitted, and I only ask you to remember it.’\n\nWoe to the suppliant, if such a one there were or ever had been, who had any concession to look for in the inexorable face at the cabinet. Woe to the defaulter whose appeal lay to the tribunal where those severe eyes presided. Great need had the rigid woman of her mystical religion, veiled in gloom and darkness, with lightnings of cursing, vengeance, and destruction, flashing through the sable clouds. Forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors, was a prayer too poor in spirit for her. Smite Thou my debtors, Lord, wither them, crush them; do Thou as I would do, and Thou shalt have my worship: this was the impious tower of stone she built up to scale Heaven.\n\n‘Have you finished, Arthur, or have you anything more to say to me? I think there can be nothing else. You have been short, but full of matter!’\n\n‘Mother, I have yet something more to say. It has been upon my mind, night and day, this long time. It is far more difficult to say than what I have said. That concerned myself; this concerns us all.’\n‘Us all! Who are us all?’\n\n‘Yourself, myself, my dead father.’\n\nShe took her hands from the desk; folded them in her lap; and sat looking towards the fire, with the impenetrability of an old Egyptian sculpture.\n\n‘You knew my father infinitely better than I ever knew him; and his reserve with me yielded to you. You were much the stronger, mother, and directed him. As a child, I knew it as well as I know it now. I knew that your ascendancy over him was the cause of his going to China to take care of the business there, while you took care of it here (though I do not even now know whether these were really terms of separation that you agreed upon); and that it was your will that I should remain with you until I was twenty, and then go to him as I did. You will not be offended by my recalling this, after twenty years?’\n\n‘I am waiting to hear why you recall it.’\n\nHe lowered his voice, and said, with manifest reluctance, and against his will:\n\n‘I want to ask you, mother, whether it ever occurred to you to suspect--’\n\nAt the word Suspect, she turned her eyes momentarily upon her son, with a dark frown. She then suffered them to seek the fire, as before; but with the frown fixed above them, as if the sculptor of old Egypt had indented it in the hard granite face, to frown for ages.\n\n‘--that he had any secret remembrance which caused him trouble of mind--remorse? Whether you ever observed anything in his conduct suggesting that; or ever spoke to him upon it, or ever heard him hint at such a thing?’\n\n‘I do not understand what kind of secret remembrance you mean to infer that your father was a prey to,’ she returned, after a silence. ‘You speak so mysteriously.’\n\n‘Is it possible, mother,’ her son leaned forward to be the nearer to her while he whispered it, and laid his hand nervously upon her desk, ‘is it possible, mother, that he had unhappily wronged any one, and made no reparation?’\n\nLooking at him wrathfully, she bent herself back in her chair to keep him further off, but gave him no reply.\n\n‘I am deeply sensible, mother, that if this thought has never at any time flashed upon you, it must seem cruel and unnatural in me, even in this confidence, to breathe it. But I cannot shake it off. Time and change (I have tried both before breaking silence) do nothing to wear it out. Remember, I was with my father. Remember, I saw his face when he gave the watch into my keeping, and struggled to express that he sent it as a token you would understand, to you. Remember, I saw him at the last with the pencil in his failing hand, trying to write some word for you to read, but to which he could give no shape. The more remote and cruel this vague suspicion that I have, the stronger the circumstances that could give it any semblance of probability to me. For Heaven’s sake, let us examine sacredly whether there is any wrong entrusted to us to set right. No one can help towards it, mother, but you.’\n\nStill so recoiling in her chair that her overpoised weight moved it, from time to time, a little on its wheels, and gave her the appearance of a phantom of fierce aspect gliding away from him, she interposed her left arm, bent at the elbow with the back of her hand towards her face, between herself and him, and looked at him in a fixed silence.\n\n‘In grasping at money and in driving hard bargains--I have begun, and I must speak of such things now, mother--some one may have been grievously deceived, injured, ruined. You were the moving power of all this machinery before my birth; your stronger spirit has been infused into all my father’s dealings for more than two score years. You can set these doubts at rest, I think, if you will really help me to discover the truth. Will you, mother?’\n\nHe stopped in the hope that she would speak. But her grey hair was not more immovable in its two folds, than were her firm lips.\n\n‘If reparation can be made to any one, if restitution can be made to any one, let us know it and make it. Nay, mother, if within my means, let _me_ make it. I have seen so little happiness come of money; it has brought within my knowledge so little peace to this house, or to any one belonging to it, that it is worth less to me than to another. It can buy me nothing that will not be a reproach and misery to me, if I am haunted by a suspicion that it darkened my father’s last hours with remorse, and that it is not honestly and justly mine.’\n\nThere was a bell-rope hanging on the panelled wall, some two or three yards from the cabinet. By a swift and sudden action of her foot, she drove her wheeled chair rapidly back to it and pulled it violently--still holding her arm up in its shield-like posture, as if he were striking at her, and she warding off the blow.\n\nA girl came hurrying in, frightened.\n\n‘Send Flintwinch here!’\n\nIn a moment the girl had withdrawn, and the old man stood within the door. ‘What! You’re hammer and tongs, already, you two?’ he said, coolly stroking his face. ‘I thought you would be. I was pretty sure of it.’\n\n‘Flintwinch!’ said the mother, ‘look at my son. Look at him!’\n\n‘Well, I _am_ looking at him,’ said Flintwinch.\n\nShe stretched out the arm with which she had shielded herself, and as she went on, pointed at the object of her anger.\n\n‘In the very hour of his return almost--before the shoe upon his foot is dry--he asperses his father’s memory to his mother! Asks his mother to become, with him, a spy upon his father’s transactions through a lifetime! Has misgivings that the goods of this world which we have painfully got together early and late, with wear and tear and toil and self-denial, are so much plunder; and asks to whom they shall be given up, as reparation and restitution!’\n\nAlthough she said this raging, she said it in a voice so far from being beyond her control that it was even lower than her usual tone. She also spoke with great distinctness.\n\n‘Reparation!’ said she. ‘Yes, truly! It is easy for him to talk of reparation, fresh from journeying and junketing in foreign lands, and living a life of vanity and pleasure. But let him look at me, in prison, and in bonds here. I endure without murmuring, because it is appointed that I shall so make reparation for my sins. Reparation! Is there none in this room? Has there been none here this fifteen years?’\n\nThus was she always balancing her bargains with the Majesty of heaven, posting up the entries to her credit, strictly keeping her set-off, and claiming her due. She was only remarkable in this, for the force and emphasis with which she did it. Thousands upon thousands do it, according to their varying manner, every day.\n\n‘Flintwinch, give me that book!’\n\nThe old man handed it to her from the table. She put two fingers between the leaves, closed the book upon them, and held it up to her son in a threatening way.\n\n‘In the days of old, Arthur, treated of in this commentary, there were pious men, beloved of the Lord, who would have cursed their sons for less than this: who would have sent them forth, and sent whole nations forth, if such had supported them, to be avoided of God and man, and perish, down to the baby at the breast. But I only tell you that if you ever renew that theme with me, I will renounce you; I will so dismiss you through that doorway, that you had better have been motherless from your cradle. I will never see or know you more. And if, after all, you were to come into this darkened room to look upon me lying dead, my body should bleed, if I could make it, when you came near me.’\n\nIn part relieved by the intensity of this threat, and in part (monstrous as the fact is) by a general impression that it was in some sort a religious proceeding, she handed back the book to the old man, and was silent.\n\n‘Now,’ said Jeremiah; ‘premising that I’m not going to stand between you two, will you let me ask (as I _have_ been called in, and made a third) what is all this about?’\n\n‘Take your version of it,’ returned Arthur, finding it left to him to speak, ‘from my mother. Let it rest there. What I have said, was said to my mother only.’\n\n‘Oh!’ returned the old man. ‘From your mother? Take it from your mother? Well! But your mother mentioned that you had been suspecting your father. That’s not dutiful, Mr Arthur. Who will you be suspecting next?’\n\n‘Enough,’ said Mrs Clennam, turning her face so that it was addressed for the moment to the old man only. ‘Let no more be said about this.’\n\n‘Yes, but stop a bit, stop a bit,’ the old man persisted. ‘Let us see how we stand. Have you told Mr Arthur that he mustn’t lay offences at his father’s door? That he has no right to do it? That he has no ground to go upon?’\n\n‘I tell him so now.’\n\n‘Ah! Exactly,’ said the old man. ‘You tell him so now. You hadn’t told him so before, and you tell him so now. Ay, ay! That’s right! You know I stood between you and his father so long, that it seems as if death had made no difference, and I was still standing between you. So I will, and so in fairness I require to have that plainly put forward. Arthur, you please to hear that you have no right to mistrust your father, and have no ground to go upon.’\n\nHe put his hands to the back of the wheeled chair, and muttering to himself, slowly wheeled his mistress back to her cabinet. ‘Now,’ he resumed, standing behind her: ‘in case I should go away leaving things half done, and so should be wanted again when you come to the other half and get into one of your flights, has Arthur told you what he means to do about the business?’\n\n‘He has relinquished it.’\n\n‘In favour of nobody, I suppose?’\n\nMrs Clennam glanced at her son, leaning against one of the windows. He observed the look and said, ‘To my mother, of course. She does what she pleases.’\n\n‘And if any pleasure,’ she said after a short pause, ‘could arise for me out of the disappointment of my expectations that my son, in the prime of his life, would infuse new youth and strength into it, and make it of great profit and power, it would be in advancing an old and faithful servant. Jeremiah, the captain deserts the ship, but you and I will sink or float with it.’\n\nJeremiah, whose eyes glistened as if they saw money, darted a sudden look at the son, which seemed to say, ‘I owe _you_ no thanks for this; _you_ have done nothing towards it!’ and then told the mother that he thanked her, and that Affery thanked her, and that he would never desert her, and that Affery would never desert her. Finally, he hauled up his watch from its depths, and said, ‘Eleven. Time for your oysters!’ and with that change of subject, which involved no change of expression or manner, rang the bell.\n\nBut Mrs Clennam, resolved to treat herself with the greater rigour for having been supposed to be unacquainted with reparation, refused to eat her oysters when they were brought. They looked tempting; eight in number, circularly set out on a white plate on a tray covered with a white napkin, flanked by a slice of buttered French roll, and a little compact glass of cool wine and water; but she resisted all persuasions, and sent them down again--placing the act to her credit, no doubt, in her Eternal Day-Book.\n\nThis refection of oysters was not presided over by Affery, but by the girl who had appeared when the bell was rung; the same who had been in the dimly-lighted room last night. Now that he had an opportunity of observing her, Arthur found that her diminutive figure, small features, and slight spare dress, gave her the appearance of being much younger than she was. A woman, probably of not less than two-and-twenty, she might have been passed in the street for little more than half that age. Not that her face was very youthful, for in truth there was more consideration and care in it than naturally belonged to her utmost years; but she was so little and light, so noiseless and shy, and appeared so conscious of being out of place among the three hard elders, that she had all the manner and much of the appearance of a subdued child.\n\nIn a hard way, and in an uncertain way that fluctuated between patronage and putting down, the sprinkling from a watering-pot and hydraulic pressure, Mrs Clennam showed an interest in this dependent. Even in the moment of her entrance, upon the violent ringing of the bell, when the mother shielded herself with that singular action from the son, Mrs Clennam’s eyes had had some individual recognition in them, which seemed reserved for her. As there are degrees of hardness in the hardest metal, and shades of colour in black itself, so, even in the asperity of Mrs Clennam’s demeanour towards all the rest of humanity and towards Little Dorrit, there was a fine gradation.\n\nLittle Dorrit let herself out to do needlework. At so much a day--or at so little--from eight to eight, Little Dorrit was to be hired. Punctual to the moment, Little Dorrit appeared; punctual to the moment, Little Dorrit vanished. What became of Little Dorrit between the two eights was a mystery.\n\nAnother of the moral phenomena of Little Dorrit. Besides her consideration money, her daily contract included meals. She had an extraordinary repugnance to dining in company; would never do so, if it were possible to escape. Would always plead that she had this bit of work to begin first, or that bit of work to finish first; and would, of a certainty, scheme and plan--not very cunningly, it would seem, for she deceived no one--to dine alone. Successful in this, happy in carrying off her plate anywhere, to make a table of her lap, or a box, or the ground, or even as was supposed, to stand on tip-toe, dining moderately at a mantel-shelf; the great anxiety of Little Dorrit’s day was set at rest.\n\nIt was not easy to make out Little Dorrit’s face; she was so retiring, plied her needle in such removed corners, and started away so scared if encountered on the stairs. But it seemed to be a pale transparent face, quick in expression, though not beautiful in feature, its soft hazel eyes excepted. A delicately bent head, a tiny form, a quick little pair of busy hands, and a shabby dress--it must needs have been very shabby to look at all so, being so neat--were Little Dorrit as she sat at work.\n\nFor these particulars or generalities concerning Little Dorrit, Mr Arthur was indebted in the course of the day to his own eyes and to Mrs Affery’s tongue. If Mrs Affery had had any will or way of her own, it would probably have been unfavourable to Little Dorrit. But as ‘them two clever ones’--Mrs Affery’s perpetual reference, in whom her personality was swallowed up--were agreed to accept Little Dorrit as a matter of course, she had nothing for it but to follow suit. Similarly, if the two clever ones had agreed to murder Little Dorrit by candlelight, Mrs Affery, being required to hold the candle, would no doubt have done it.\n\nIn the intervals of roasting the partridge for the invalid chamber, and preparing a baking-dish of beef and pudding for the dining-room, Mrs Affery made the communications above set forth; invariably putting her head in at the door again after she had taken it out, to enforce resistance to the two clever ones. It appeared to have become a perfect passion with Mrs Flintwinch, that the only son should be pitted against them.\n\nIn the course of the day, too, Arthur looked through the whole house. Dull and dark he found it. The gaunt rooms, deserted for years upon years, seemed to have settled down into a gloomy lethargy from which nothing could rouse them again. The furniture, at once spare and lumbering, hid in the rooms rather than furnished them, and there was no colour in all the house; such colour as had ever been there, had long ago started away on lost sunbeams--got itself absorbed, perhaps, into flowers, butterflies, plumage of birds, precious stones, what not. There was not one straight floor from the foundation to the roof; the ceilings were so fantastically clouded by smoke and dust, that old women might have told fortunes in them better than in grouts of tea; the dead-cold hearths showed no traces of having ever been warmed but in heaps of soot that had tumbled down the chimneys, and eddied about in little dusky whirlwinds when the doors were opened. In what had once been a drawing-room, there were a pair of meagre mirrors, with dismal processions of black figures carrying black garlands, walking round the frames; but even these were short of heads and legs, and one undertaker-like Cupid had swung round on its own axis and got upside down, and another had fallen off altogether. The room Arthur Clennam’s deceased father had occupied for business purposes, when he first remembered him, was so unaltered that he might have been imagined still to keep it invisibly, as his visible relict kept her room up-stairs; Jeremiah Flintwinch still going between them negotiating. His picture, dark and gloomy, earnestly speechless on the wall, with the eyes intently looking at his son as they had looked when life departed from them, seemed to urge him awfully to the task he had attempted; but as to any yielding on the part of his mother, he had now no hope, and as to any other means of setting his distrust at rest, he had abandoned hope a long time. Down in the cellars, as up in the bed-chambers, old objects that he well remembered were changed by age and decay, but were still in their old places; even to empty beer-casks hoary with cobwebs, and empty wine-bottles with fur and fungus choking up their throats. There, too, among unusual bottle-racks and pale slants of light from the yard above, was the strong room stored with old ledgers, which had as musty and corrupt a smell as if they were regularly balanced, in the dead small hours, by a nightly resurrection of old book-keepers.\n\nThe baking-dish was served up in a penitential manner on a shrunken cloth at an end of the dining-table, at two o’clock, when he dined with Mr Flintwinch, the new partner. Mr Flintwinch informed him that his mother had recovered her equanimity now, and that he need not fear her again alluding to what had passed in the morning. ‘And don’t you lay offences at your father’s door, Mr Arthur,’ added Jeremiah, ‘once for all, don’t do it! Now, we have done with the subject.’\n\nMr Flintwinch had been already rearranging and dusting his own particular little office, as if to do honour to his accession to new dignity. He resumed this occupation when he was replete with beef, had sucked up all the gravy in the baking-dish with the flat of his knife, and had drawn liberally on a barrel of small beer in the scullery. Thus refreshed, he tucked up his shirt-sleeves and went to work again; and Mr Arthur, watching him as he set about it, plainly saw that his father’s picture, or his father’s grave, would be as communicative with him as this old man.\n\n‘Now, Affery, woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch, as she crossed the hall. ‘You hadn’t made Mr Arthur’s bed when I was up there last. Stir yourself. Bustle.’\n\nBut Mr Arthur found the house so blank and dreary, and was so unwilling to assist at another implacable consignment of his mother’s enemies (perhaps himself among them) to mortal disfigurement and immortal ruin, that he announced his intention of lodging at the coffee-house where he had left his luggage. Mr Flintwinch taking kindly to the idea of getting rid of him, and his mother being indifferent, beyond considerations of saving, to most domestic arrangements that were not bounded by the walls of her own chamber, he easily carried this point without new offence. Daily business hours were agreed upon, which his mother, Mr Flintwinch, and he, were to devote together to a necessary checking of books and papers; and he left the home he had so lately found, with depressed heart.\n\nBut Little Dorrit?\n\nThe business hours, allowing for intervals of invalid regimen of oysters and partridges, during which Clennam refreshed himself with a walk, were from ten to six for about a fortnight. Sometimes Little Dorrit was employed at her needle, sometimes not, sometimes appeared as a humble visitor: which must have been her character on the occasion of his arrival. His original curiosity augmented every day, as he watched for her, saw or did not see her, and speculated about her. Influenced by his predominant idea, he even fell into a habit of discussing with himself the possibility of her being in some way associated with it. At last he resolved to watch Little Dorrit and know more of her story.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. The Father of the Marshalsea\n\nThirty years ago there stood, a few doors short of the church of Saint George, in the borough of Southwark, on the left-hand side of the way going southward, the Marshalsea Prison. It had stood there many years before, and it remained there some years afterwards; but it is gone now, and the world is none the worse without it.\n\nIt was an oblong pile of barrack building, partitioned into squalid houses standing back to back, so that there were no back rooms; environed by a narrow paved yard, hemmed in by high walls duly spiked at top. Itself a close and confined prison for debtors, it contained within it a much closer and more confined jail for smugglers. Offenders against the revenue laws, and defaulters to excise or customs who had incurred fines which they were unable to pay, were supposed to be incarcerated behind an iron-plated door closing up a second prison, consisting of a strong cell or two, and a blind alley some yard and a half wide, which formed the mysterious termination of the very limited skittle-ground in which the Marshalsea debtors bowled down their troubles.\n\nSupposed to be incarcerated there, because the time had rather outgrown the strong cells and the blind alley. In practice they had come to be considered a little too bad, though in theory they were quite as good as ever; which may be observed to be the case at the present day with other cells that are not at all strong, and with other blind alleys that are stone-blind. Hence the smugglers habitually consorted with the debtors (who received them with open arms), except at certain constitutional moments when somebody came from some Office, to go through some form of overlooking something which neither he nor anybody else knew anything about. On these truly British occasions, the smugglers, if any, made a feint of walking into the strong cells and the blind alley, while this somebody pretended to do his something: and made a reality of walking out again as soon as he hadn’t done it--neatly epitomising the administration of most of the public affairs in our right little, tight little, island.\n\nThere had been taken to the Marshalsea Prison, long before the day when the sun shone on Marseilles and on the opening of this narrative, a debtor with whom this narrative has some concern.\n\nHe was, at that time, a very amiable and very helpless middle-aged gentleman, who was going out again directly. Necessarily, he was going out again directly, because the Marshalsea lock never turned upon a debtor who was not. He brought in a portmanteau with him, which he doubted its being worth while to unpack; he was so perfectly clear--like all the rest of them, the turnkey on the lock said--that he was going out again directly.\n\nHe was a shy, retiring man; well-looking, though in an effeminate style; with a mild voice, curling hair, and irresolute hands--rings upon the fingers in those days--which nervously wandered to his trembling lip a hundred times in the first half-hour of his acquaintance with the jail. His principal anxiety was about his wife.\n\n‘Do you think, sir,’ he asked the turnkey, ‘that she will be very much shocked, if she should come to the gate to-morrow morning?’\n\nThe turnkey gave it as the result of his experience that some of ‘em was and some of ‘em wasn’t. In general, more no than yes. ‘What like is she, you see?’ he philosophically asked: ‘that’s what it hinges on.’\n\n‘She is very delicate and inexperienced indeed.’\n\n‘That,’ said the turnkey, ‘is agen her.’\n\n‘She is so little used to go out alone,’ said the debtor, ‘that I am at a loss to think how she will ever make her way here, if she walks.’\n\n‘P’raps,’ quoth the turnkey, ‘she’ll take a ackney coach.’\n\n‘Perhaps.’ The irresolute fingers went to the trembling lip. ‘I hope she will. She may not think of it.’\n\n‘Or p’raps,’ said the turnkey, offering his suggestions from the the top of his well-worn wooden stool, as he might have offered them to a child for whose weakness he felt a compassion, ‘p’raps she’ll get her brother, or her sister, to come along with her.’\n\n‘She has no brother or sister.’\n\n‘Niece, nevy, cousin, serwant, young ‘ooman, greengrocer.--Dash it! One or another on ‘em,’ said the turnkey, repudiating beforehand the refusal of all his suggestions.\n\n‘I fear--I hope it is not against the rules--that she will bring the children.’\n\n‘The children?’ said the turnkey. ‘And the rules? Why, lord set you up like a corner pin, we’ve a reg’lar playground o’ children here. Children! Why we swarm with ‘em. How many a you got?’\n\n‘Two,’ said the debtor, lifting his irresolute hand to his lip again, and turning into the prison.\n\nThe turnkey followed him with his eyes. ‘And you another,’ he observed to himself, ‘which makes three on you. And your wife another, I’ll lay a crown. Which makes four on you. And another coming, I’ll lay half-a-crown. Which’ll make five on you. And I’ll go another seven and sixpence to name which is the helplessest, the unborn baby or you!’\n\nHe was right in all his particulars. She came next day with a little boy of three years old, and a little girl of two, and he stood entirely corroborated.\n\n‘Got a room now; haven’t you?’ the turnkey asked the debtor after a week or two.\n\n‘Yes, I have got a very good room.’\n\n‘Any little sticks a coming to furnish it?’ said the turnkey.\n\n‘I expect a few necessary articles of furniture to be delivered by the carrier, this afternoon.’\n\n‘Missis and little ‘uns a coming to keep you company?’ asked the turnkey.\n\n‘Why, yes, we think it better that we should not be scattered, even for a few weeks.’\n\n‘Even for a few weeks, _of_ course,’ replied the turnkey. And he followed him again with his eyes, and nodded his head seven times when he was gone.\n\nThe affairs of this debtor were perplexed by a partnership, of which he knew no more than that he had invested money in it; by legal matters of assignment and settlement, conveyance here and conveyance there, suspicion of unlawful preference of creditors in this direction, and of mysterious spiriting away of property in that; and as nobody on the face of the earth could be more incapable of explaining any single item in the heap of confusion than the debtor himself, nothing comprehensible could be made of his case. To question him in detail, and endeavour to reconcile his answers; to closet him with accountants and sharp practitioners, learned in the wiles of insolvency and bankruptcy; was only to put the case out at compound interest and incomprehensibility. The irresolute fingers fluttered more and more ineffectually about the trembling lip on every such occasion, and the sharpest practitioners gave him up as a hopeless job.\n\n‘Out?’ said the turnkey, ‘_he_‘ll never get out, unless his creditors take him by the shoulders and shove him out.’\n\nHe had been there five or six months, when he came running to this turnkey one forenoon to tell him, breathless and pale, that his wife was ill.\n\n‘As anybody might a known she would be,’ said the turnkey.\n\n‘We intended,’ he returned, ‘that she should go to a country lodging only to-morrow. What am I to do! Oh, good heaven, what am I to do!’\n\n‘Don’t waste your time in clasping your hands and biting your fingers,’ responded the practical turnkey, taking him by the elbow, ‘but come along with me.’\n\nThe turnkey conducted him--trembling from head to foot, and constantly crying under his breath, What was he to do! while his irresolute fingers bedabbled the tears upon his face--up one of the common staircases in the prison to a door on the garret story. Upon which door the turnkey knocked with the handle of his key.\n\n‘Come in!’ cried a voice inside.\n\nThe turnkey, opening the door, disclosed in a wretched, ill-smelling little room, two hoarse, puffy, red-faced personages seated at a rickety table, playing at all-fours, smoking pipes, and drinking brandy.\n\n‘Doctor,’ said the turnkey, ‘here’s a gentleman’s wife in want of you without a minute’s loss of time!’\n\nThe doctor’s friend was in the positive degree of hoarseness, puffiness, red-facedness, all-fours, tobacco, dirt, and brandy; the doctor in the comparative--hoarser, puffier, more red-faced, more all-fourey, tobaccoer, dirtier, and brandier. The doctor was amazingly shabby, in a torn and darned rough-weather sea-jacket, out at elbows and eminently short of buttons (he had been in his time the experienced surgeon carried by a passenger ship), the dirtiest white trousers conceivable by mortal man, carpet slippers, and no visible linen. ‘Childbed?’ said the doctor. ‘I’m the boy!’ With that the doctor took a comb from the chimney-piece and stuck his hair upright--which appeared to be his way of washing himself--produced a professional chest or case, of most abject appearance, from the cupboard where his cup and saucer and coals were, settled his chin in the frowsy wrapper round his neck, and became a ghastly medical scarecrow.\n\nThe doctor and the debtor ran down-stairs, leaving the turnkey to return to the lock, and made for the debtor’s room. All the ladies in the prison had got hold of the news, and were in the yard. Some of them had already taken possession of the two children, and were hospitably carrying them off; others were offering loans of little comforts from their own scanty store; others were sympathising with the greatest volubility. The gentlemen prisoners, feeling themselves at a disadvantage, had for the most part retired, not to say sneaked, to their rooms; from the open windows of which some of them now complimented the doctor with whistles as he passed below, while others, with several stories between them, interchanged sarcastic references to the prevalent excitement.\n\nIt was a hot summer day, and the prison rooms were baking between the high walls. In the debtor’s confined chamber, Mrs Bangham, charwoman and messenger, who was not a prisoner (though she had been once), but was the popular medium of communication with the outer world, had volunteered her services as fly-catcher and general attendant. The walls and ceiling were blackened with flies. Mrs Bangham, expert in sudden device, with one hand fanned the patient with a cabbage leaf, and with the other set traps of vinegar and sugar in gallipots; at the same time enunciating sentiments of an encouraging and congratulatory nature, adapted to the occasion.\n\n‘The flies trouble you, don’t they, my dear?’ said Mrs Bangham. ‘But p’raps they’ll take your mind off of it, and do you good. What between the buryin ground, the grocer’s, the waggon-stables, and the paunch trade, the Marshalsea flies gets very large. P’raps they’re sent as a consolation, if we only know’d it. How are you now, my dear? No better? No, my dear, it ain’t to be expected; you’ll be worse before you’re better, and you know it, don’t you? Yes. That’s right! And to think of a sweet little cherub being born inside the lock! Now ain’t it pretty, ain’t _that_ something to carry you through it pleasant? Why, we ain’t had such a thing happen here, my dear, not for I couldn’t name the time when. And you a crying too?’ said Mrs Bangham, to rally the patient more and more. ‘You! Making yourself so famous! With the flies a falling into the gallipots by fifties! And everything a going on so well! And here if there ain’t,’ said Mrs Bangham as the door opened, ‘if there ain’t your dear gentleman along with Dr Haggage! And now indeed we _are_ complete, I _think_!’\n\nThe doctor was scarcely the kind of apparition to inspire a patient with a sense of absolute completeness, but as he presently delivered the opinion, ‘We are as right as we can be, Mrs Bangham, and we shall come out of this like a house afire;’ and as he and Mrs Bangham took possession of the poor helpless pair, as everybody else and anybody else had always done, the means at hand were as good on the whole as better would have been. The special feature in Dr Haggage’s treatment of the case, was his determination to keep Mrs Bangham up to the mark. As thus:\n\n‘Mrs Bangham,’ said the doctor, before he had been there twenty minutes, ‘go outside and fetch a little brandy, or we shall have you giving in.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir. But none on my accounts,’ said Mrs Bangham.\n\n‘Mrs Bangham,’ returned the doctor, ‘I am in professional attendance on this lady, and don’t choose to allow any discussion on your part. Go outside and fetch a little brandy, or I foresee that you’ll break down.’\n\n‘You’re to be obeyed, sir,’ said Mrs Bangham, rising. ‘If you was to put your own lips to it, I think you wouldn’t be the worse, for you look but poorly, sir.’\n\n‘Mrs Bangham,’ returned the doctor, ‘I am not your business, thank you, but you are mine. Never you mind _me_, if you please. What you have got to do, is, to do as you are told, and to go and get what I bid you.’\n\nMrs Bangham submitted; and the doctor, having administered her potion, took his own. He repeated the treatment every hour, being very determined with Mrs Bangham. Three or four hours passed; the flies fell into the traps by hundreds; and at length one little life, hardly stronger than theirs, appeared among the multitude of lesser deaths.\n\n‘A very nice little girl indeed,’ said the doctor; ‘little, but well-formed. Halloa, Mrs Bangham! You’re looking queer! You be off, ma’am, this minute, and fetch a little more brandy, or we shall have you in hysterics.’\n\nBy this time, the rings had begun to fall from the debtor’s irresolute hands, like leaves from a wintry tree. Not one was left upon them that night, when he put something that chinked into the doctor’s greasy palm. In the meantime Mrs Bangham had been out on an errand to a neighbouring establishment decorated with three golden balls, where she was very well known.\n\n‘Thank you,’ said the doctor, ‘thank you. Your good lady is quite composed. Doing charmingly.’\n\n‘I am very happy and very thankful to know it,’ said the debtor, ‘though I little thought once, that--’\n\n‘That a child would be born to you in a place like this?’ said the doctor. ‘Bah, bah, sir, what does it signify? A little more elbow-room is all we want here. We are quiet here; we don’t get badgered here; there’s no knocker here, sir, to be hammered at by creditors and bring a man’s heart into his mouth. Nobody comes here to ask if a man’s at home, and to say he’ll stand on the door mat till he is. Nobody writes threatening letters about money to this place. It’s freedom, sir, it’s freedom! I have had to-day’s practice at home and abroad, on a march, and aboard ship, and I’ll tell you this: I don’t know that I have ever pursued it under such quiet circumstances as here this day. Elsewhere, people are restless, worried, hurried about, anxious respecting one thing, anxious respecting another. Nothing of the kind here, sir. We have done all that--we know the worst of it; we have got to the bottom, we can’t fall, and what have we found? Peace. That’s the word for it. Peace.’ With this profession of faith, the doctor, who was an old jail-bird, and was more sodden than usual, and had the additional and unusual stimulus of money in his pocket, returned to his associate and chum in hoarseness, puffiness, red-facedness, all-fours, tobacco, dirt, and brandy.\n\nNow, the debtor was a very different man from the doctor, but he had already begun to travel, by his opposite segment of the circle, to the same point. Crushed at first by his imprisonment, he had soon found a dull relief in it. He was under lock and key; but the lock and key that kept him in, kept numbers of his troubles out. If he had been a man with strength of purpose to face those troubles and fight them, he might have broken the net that held him, or broken his heart; but being what he was, he languidly slipped into this smooth descent, and never more took one step upward.\n\nWhen he was relieved of the perplexed affairs that nothing would make plain, through having them returned upon his hands by a dozen agents in succession who could make neither beginning, middle, nor end of them or him, he found his miserable place of refuge a quieter refuge than it had been before. He had unpacked the portmanteau long ago; and his elder children now played regularly about the yard, and everybody knew the baby, and claimed a kind of proprietorship in her.\n\n‘Why, I’m getting proud of you,’ said his friend the turnkey, one day. ‘You’ll be the oldest inhabitant soon. The Marshalsea wouldn’t be like the Marshalsea now, without you and your family.’\n\nThe turnkey really was proud of him. He would mention him in laudatory terms to new-comers, when his back was turned. ‘You took notice of him,’ he would say, ‘that went out of the lodge just now?’\n\nNew-comer would probably answer Yes.\n\n‘Brought up as a gentleman, he was, if ever a man was. Ed’cated at no end of expense. Went into the Marshal’s house once to try a new piano for him. Played it, I understand, like one o’clock--beautiful! As to languages--speaks anything. We’ve had a Frenchman here in his time, and it’s my opinion he knowed more French than the Frenchman did. We’ve had an Italian here in his time, and he shut _him_ up in about half a minute. You’ll find some characters behind other locks, I don’t say you won’t; but if you want the top sawyer in such respects as I’ve mentioned, you must come to the Marshalsea.’\n\nWhen his youngest child was eight years old, his wife, who had long been languishing away--of her own inherent weakness, not that she retained any greater sensitiveness as to her place of abode than he did--went upon a visit to a poor friend and old nurse in the country, and died there. He remained shut up in his room for a fortnight afterwards; and an attorney’s clerk, who was going through the Insolvent Court, engrossed an address of condolence to him, which looked like a Lease, and which all the prisoners signed. When he appeared again he was greyer (he had soon begun to turn grey); and the turnkey noticed that his hands went often to his trembling lips again, as they had used to do when he first came in. But he got pretty well over it in a month or two; and in the meantime the children played about the yard as regularly as ever, but in black.\n\nThen Mrs Bangham, long popular medium of communication with the outer world, began to be infirm, and to be found oftener than usual comatose on pavements, with her basket of purchases spilt, and the change of her clients ninepence short. His son began to supersede Mrs Bangham, and to execute commissions in a knowing manner, and to be of the prison prisonous, of the streets streety.\n\nTime went on, and the turnkey began to fail. His chest swelled, and his legs got weak, and he was short of breath. The well-worn wooden stool was ‘beyond him,’ he complained. He sat in an arm-chair with a cushion, and sometimes wheezed so, for minutes together, that he couldn’t turn the key. When he was overpowered by these fits, the debtor often turned it for him.\n\n‘You and me,’ said the turnkey, one snowy winter’s night when the lodge, with a bright fire in it, was pretty full of company, ‘is the oldest inhabitants. I wasn’t here myself above seven year before you. I shan’t last long. When I’m off the lock for good and all, you’ll be the Father of the Marshalsea.’\n\nThe turnkey went off the lock of this world next day. His words were remembered and repeated; and tradition afterwards handed down from generation to generation--a Marshalsea generation might be calculated as about three months--that the shabby old debtor with the soft manner and the white hair, was the Father of the Marshalsea.\n\nAnd he grew to be proud of the title. If any impostor had arisen to claim it, he would have shed tears in resentment of the attempt to deprive him of his rights. A disposition began to be perceived in him to exaggerate the number of years he had been there; it was generally understood that you must deduct a few from his account; he was vain, the fleeting generations of debtors said.\n\nAll new-comers were presented to him. He was punctilious in the exaction of this ceremony. The wits would perform the office of introduction with overcharged pomp and politeness, but they could not easily overstep his sense of its gravity. He received them in his poor room (he disliked an introduction in the mere yard, as informal--a thing that might happen to anybody), with a kind of bowed-down beneficence. They were welcome to the Marshalsea, he would tell them. Yes, he was the Father of the place. So the world was kind enough to call him; and so he was, if more than twenty years of residence gave him a claim to the title. It looked small at first, but there was very good company there--among a mixture--necessarily a mixture--and very good air.\n\nIt became a not unusual circumstance for letters to be put under his door at night, enclosing half-a-crown, two half-crowns, now and then at long intervals even half-a-sovereign, for the Father of the Marshalsea. ‘With the compliments of a collegian taking leave.’ He received the gifts as tributes, from admirers, to a public character. Sometimes these correspondents assumed facetious names, as the Brick, Bellows, Old Gooseberry, Wideawake, Snooks, Mops, Cutaway, the Dogs-meat Man; but he considered this in bad taste, and was always a little hurt by it.\n\nIn the fulness of time, this correspondence showing signs of wearing out, and seeming to require an effort on the part of the correspondents to which in the hurried circumstances of departure many of them might not be equal, he established the custom of attending collegians of a certain standing, to the gate, and taking leave of them there. The collegian under treatment, after shaking hands, would occasionally stop to wrap up something in a bit of paper, and would come back again calling ‘Hi!’\n\nHe would look round surprised.’Me?’ he would say, with a smile.\n\nBy this time the collegian would be up with him, and he would paternally add, VWhat have you forgotten? What can I do for you?’\n\n‘I forgot to leave this,’ the collegian would usually return, ‘for the Father of the Marshalsea.’\n\n‘My good sir,’ he would rejoin, ‘he is infinitely obliged to you.’ But, to the last, the irresolute hand of old would remain in the pocket into which he had slipped the money during two or three turns about the yard, lest the transaction should be too conspicuous to the general body of collegians.\n\nOne afternoon he had been doing the honours of the place to a rather large party of collegians, who happened to be going out, when, as he was coming back, he encountered one from the poor side who had been taken in execution for a small sum a week before, had ‘settled’ in the course of that afternoon, and was going out too. The man was a mere Plasterer in his working dress; had his wife with him, and a bundle; and was in high spirits.\n\n‘God bless you, sir,’ he said in passing.\n\n‘And you,’ benignantly returned the Father of the Marshalsea.\n\nThey were pretty far divided, going their several ways, when the Plasterer called out, ‘I say!--sir!’ and came back to him.\n\n‘It ain’t much,’ said the Plasterer, putting a little pile of halfpence in his hand, ‘but it’s well meant.’\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea had never been offered tribute in copper yet. His children often had, and with his perfect acquiescence it had gone into the common purse to buy meat that he had eaten, and drink that he had drunk; but fustian splashed with white lime, bestowing halfpence on him, front to front, was new.\n\n‘How dare you!’ he said to the man, and feebly burst into tears.\n\nThe Plasterer turned him towards the wall, that his face might not be seen; and the action was so delicate, and the man was so penetrated with repentance, and asked pardon so honestly, that he could make him no less acknowledgment than, ‘I know you meant it kindly. Say no more.’\n\n‘Bless your soul, sir,’ urged the Plasterer, ‘I did indeed. I’d do more by you than the rest of ‘em do, I fancy.’\n\n‘What would you do?’ he asked.\n\n‘I’d come back to see you, after I was let out.’\n\n‘Give me the money again,’ said the other, eagerly, ‘and I’ll keep it, and never spend it. Thank you for it, thank you! I shall see you again?’\n\n‘If I live a week you shall.’\n\nThey shook hands and parted. The collegians, assembled in Symposium in the Snuggery that night, marvelled what had happened to their Father; he walked so late in the shadows of the yard, and seemed so downcast.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. The Child of the Marshalsea\n\nThe baby whose first draught of air had been tinctured with Doctor Haggage’s brandy, was handed down among the generations of collegians, like the tradition of their common parent. In the earlier stages of her existence, she was handed down in a literal and prosaic sense; it being almost a part of the entrance footing of every new collegian to nurse the child who had been born in the college.\n\n‘By rights,’ remarked the turnkey when she was first shown to him, ‘I ought to be her godfather.’\n\nThe debtor irresolutely thought of it for a minute, and said, ‘Perhaps you wouldn’t object to really being her godfather?’\n\n‘Oh! _I_ don’t object,’ replied the turnkey, ‘if you don’t.’\n\nThus it came to pass that she was christened one Sunday afternoon, when the turnkey, being relieved, was off the lock; and that the turnkey went up to the font of Saint George’s Church, and promised and vowed and renounced on her behalf, as he himself related when he came back, ‘like a good ‘un.’\n\nThis invested the turnkey with a new proprietary share in the child, over and above his former official one. When she began to walk and talk, he became fond of her; bought a little arm-chair and stood it by the high fender of the lodge fire-place; liked to have her company when he was on the lock; and used to bribe her with cheap toys to come and talk to him. The child, for her part, soon grew so fond of the turnkey that she would come climbing up the lodge-steps of her own accord at all hours of the day. When she fell asleep in the little armchair by the high fender, the turnkey would cover her with his pocket-handkerchief; and when she sat in it dressing and undressing a doll which soon came to be unlike dolls on the other side of the lock, and to bear a horrible family resemblance to Mrs Bangham--he would contemplate her from the top of his stool with exceeding gentleness. Witnessing these things, the collegians would express an opinion that the turnkey, who was a bachelor, had been cut out by nature for a family man. But the turnkey thanked them, and said, ‘No, on the whole it was enough to see other people’s children there.’\n\nAt what period of her early life the little creature began to perceive that it was not the habit of all the world to live locked up in narrow yards surrounded by high walls with spikes at the top, would be a difficult question to settle. But she was a very, very little creature indeed, when she had somehow gained the knowledge that her clasp of her father’s hand was to be always loosened at the door which the great key opened; and that while her own light steps were free to pass beyond it, his feet must never cross that line. A pitiful and plaintive look, with which she had begun to regard him when she was still extremely young, was perhaps a part of this discovery.\n\nWith a pitiful and plaintive look for everything, indeed, but with something in it for only him that was like protection, this Child of the Marshalsea and the child of the Father of the Marshalsea, sat by her friend the turnkey in the lodge, kept the family room, or wandered about the prison-yard, for the first eight years of her life. With a pitiful and plaintive look for her wayward sister; for her idle brother; for the high blank walls; for the faded crowd they shut in; for the games of the prison children as they whooped and ran, and played at hide-and-seek, and made the iron bars of the inner gateway ‘Home.’\n\nWistful and wondering, she would sit in summer weather by the high fender in the lodge, looking up at the sky through the barred window, until, when she turned her eyes away, bars of light would arise between her and her friend, and she would see him through a grating, too.\n\n‘Thinking of the fields,’ the turnkey said once, after watching her, ‘ain’t you?’\n\n‘Where are they?’ she inquired.\n\n‘Why, they’re--over there, my dear,’ said the turnkey, with a vague flourish of his key. ‘Just about there.’\n\n‘Does anybody open them, and shut them? Are they locked?’\n\nThe turnkey was discomfited. ‘Well,’ he said. ‘Not in general.’\n\n‘Are they very pretty, Bob?’ She called him Bob, by his own particular request and instruction.\n\n‘Lovely. Full of flowers. There’s buttercups, and there’s daisies, and there’s’--the turnkey hesitated, being short of floral nomenclature--‘there’s dandelions, and all manner of games.’\n\n‘Is it very pleasant to be there, Bob?’\n\n‘Prime,’ said the turnkey.\n\n‘Was father ever there?’\n\n‘Hem!’ coughed the turnkey. ‘O yes, he was there, sometimes.’\n\n‘Is he sorry not to be there now?’\n\n‘N-not particular,’ said the turnkey.\n\n‘Nor any of the people?’ she asked, glancing at the listless crowd within. ‘O are you quite sure and certain, Bob?’\n\nAt this difficult point of the conversation Bob gave in, and changed the subject to hard-bake: always his last resource when he found his little friend getting him into a political, social, or theological corner. But this was the origin of a series of Sunday excursions that these two curious companions made together. They used to issue from the lodge on alternate Sunday afternoons with great gravity, bound for some meadows or green lanes that had been elaborately appointed by the turnkey in the course of the week; and there she picked grass and flowers to bring home, while he smoked his pipe. Afterwards, there were tea-gardens, shrimps, ale, and other delicacies; and then they would come back hand in hand, unless she was more than usually tired, and had fallen asleep on his shoulder.\n\nIn those early days, the turnkey first began profoundly to consider a question which cost him so much mental labour, that it remained undetermined on the day of his death. He decided to will and bequeath his little property of savings to his godchild, and the point arose how could it be so ‘tied up’ as that only she should have the benefit of it? His experience on the lock gave him such an acute perception of the enormous difficulty of ‘tying up’ money with any approach to tightness, and contrariwise of the remarkable ease with which it got loose, that through a series of years he regularly propounded this knotty point to every new insolvent agent and other professional gentleman who passed in and out.\n\n‘Supposing,’ he would say, stating the case with his key on the professional gentleman’s waistcoat; ‘supposing a man wanted to leave his property to a young female, and wanted to tie it up so that nobody else should ever be able to make a grab at it; how would you tie up that property?’\n\n‘Settle it strictly on herself,’ the professional gentleman would complacently answer.\n\n‘But look here,’ quoth the turnkey. ‘Supposing she had, say a brother, say a father, say a husband, who would be likely to make a grab at that property when she came into it--how about that?’\n\n‘It would be settled on herself, and they would have no more legal claim on it than you,’ would be the professional answer.\n\n‘Stop a bit,’ said the turnkey. ‘Supposing she was tender-hearted, and they came over her. Where’s your law for tying it up then?’\n\nThe deepest character whom the turnkey sounded, was unable to produce his law for tying such a knot as that. So, the turnkey thought about it all his life, and died intestate after all.\n\nBut that was long afterwards, when his god-daughter was past sixteen. The first half of that space of her life was only just accomplished, when her pitiful and plaintive look saw her father a widower. From that time the protection that her wondering eyes had expressed towards him, became embodied in action, and the Child of the Marshalsea took upon herself a new relation towards the Father.\n\nAt first, such a baby could do little more than sit with him, deserting her livelier place by the high fender, and quietly watching him. But this made her so far necessary to him that he became accustomed to her, and began to be sensible of missing her when she was not there. Through this little gate, she passed out of childhood into the care-laden world.\n\nWhat her pitiful look saw, at that early time, in her father, in her sister, in her brother, in the jail; how much, or how little of the wretched truth it pleased God to make visible to her; lies hidden with many mysteries. It is enough that she was inspired to be something which was not what the rest were, and to be that something, different and laborious, for the sake of the rest. Inspired? Yes. Shall we speak of the inspiration of a poet or a priest, and not of the heart impelled by love and self-devotion to the lowliest work in the lowliest way of life!\n\nWith no earthly friend to help her, or so much as to see her, but the one so strangely assorted; with no knowledge even of the common daily tone and habits of the common members of the free community who are not shut up in prisons; born and bred in a social condition, false even with a reference to the falsest condition outside the walls; drinking from infancy of a well whose waters had their own peculiar stain, their own unwholesome and unnatural taste; the Child of the Marshalsea began her womanly life.\n\nNo matter through what mistakes and discouragements, what ridicule (not unkindly meant, but deeply felt) of her youth and little figure, what humble consciousness of her own babyhood and want of strength, even in the matter of lifting and carrying; through how much weariness and hopelessness, and how many secret tears; she drudged on, until recognised as useful, even indispensable. That time came. She took the place of eldest of the three, in all things but precedence; was the head of the fallen family; and bore, in her own heart, its anxieties and shames.\n\nAt thirteen, she could read and keep accounts, that is, could put down in words and figures how much the bare necessaries that they wanted would cost, and how much less they had to buy them with. She had been, by snatches of a few weeks at a time, to an evening school outside, and got her sister and brother sent to day-schools by desultory starts, during three or four years. There was no instruction for any of them at home; but she knew well--no one better--that a man so broken as to be the Father of the Marshalsea, could be no father to his own children.\n\nTo these scanty means of improvement, she added another of her own contriving. Once, among the heterogeneous crowd of inmates there appeared a dancing-master. Her sister had a great desire to learn the dancing-master’s art, and seemed to have a taste that way. At thirteen years old, the Child of the Marshalsea presented herself to the dancing-master, with a little bag in her hand, and preferred her humble petition.\n\n‘If you please, I was born here, sir.’\n\n‘Oh! You are the young lady, are you?’ said the dancing-master, surveying the small figure and uplifted face.\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘And what can I do for you?’ said the dancing-master.\n\n‘Nothing for me, sir, thank you,’ anxiously undrawing the strings of the little bag; ‘but if, while you stay here, you could be so kind as to teach my sister cheap--’\n\n‘My child, I’ll teach her for nothing,’ said the dancing-master, shutting up the bag. He was as good-natured a dancing-master as ever danced to the Insolvent Court, and he kept his word. The sister was so apt a pupil, and the dancing-master had such abundant leisure to bestow upon her (for it took him a matter of ten weeks to set to his creditors, lead off, turn the Commissioners, and right and left back to his professional pursuits), that wonderful progress was made. Indeed the dancing-master was so proud of it, and so wishful to display it before he left to a few select friends among the collegians, that at six o’clock on a certain fine morning, a minuet de la cour came off in the yard--the college-rooms being of too confined proportions for the purpose--in which so much ground was covered, and the steps were so conscientiously executed, that the dancing-master, having to play the kit besides, was thoroughly blown.\n\nThe success of this beginning, which led to the dancing-master’s continuing his instruction after his release, emboldened the poor child to try again. She watched and waited months for a seamstress. In the fulness of time a milliner came in, and to her she repaired on her own behalf.\n\n‘I beg your pardon, ma’am,’ she said, looking timidly round the door of the milliner, whom she found in tears and in bed: ‘but I was born here.’\n\nEverybody seemed to hear of her as soon as they arrived; for the milliner sat up in bed, drying her eyes, and said, just as the dancing-master had said:\n\n‘Oh! _You_ are the child, are you?’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am.’\n\n‘I am sorry I haven’t got anything for you,’ said the milliner, shaking her head.\n\n‘It’s not that, ma’am. If you please I want to learn needle-work.’\n\n‘Why should you do that,’ returned the milliner, ‘with me before you? It has not done me much good.’\n\n‘Nothing--whatever it is--seems to have done anybody much good who comes here,’ she returned in all simplicity; ‘but I want to learn just the same.’\n\n‘I am afraid you are so weak, you see,’ the milliner objected.\n\n‘I don’t think I am weak, ma’am.’\n\n‘And you are so very, very little, you see,’ the milliner objected.\n\n‘Yes, I am afraid I am very little indeed,’ returned the Child of the Marshalsea; and so began to sob over that unfortunate defect of hers, which came so often in her way. The milliner--who was not morose or hard-hearted, only newly insolvent--was touched, took her in hand with goodwill, found her the most patient and earnest of pupils, and made her a cunning work-woman in course of time.\n\nIn course of time, and in the very self-same course of time, the Father of the Marshalsea gradually developed a new flower of character. The more Fatherly he grew as to the Marshalsea, and the more dependent he became on the contributions of his changing family, the greater stand he made by his forlorn gentility. With the same hand that he pocketed a collegian’s half-crown half an hour ago, he would wipe away the tears that streamed over his cheeks if any reference were made to his daughters’ earning their bread. So, over and above other daily cares, the Child of the Marshalsea had always upon her the care of preserving the genteel fiction that they were all idle beggars together.\n\nThe sister became a dancer. There was a ruined uncle in the family group--ruined by his brother, the Father of the Marshalsea, and knowing no more how than his ruiner did, but accepting the fact as an inevitable certainty--on whom her protection devolved. Naturally a retired and simple man, he had shown no particular sense of being ruined at the time when that calamity fell upon him, further than that he left off washing himself when the shock was announced, and never took to that luxury any more. He had been a very indifferent musical amateur in his better days; and when he fell with his brother, resorted for support to playing a clarionet as dirty as himself in a small Theatre Orchestra. It was the theatre in which his niece became a dancer; he had been a fixture there a long time when she took her poor station in it; and he accepted the task of serving as her escort and guardian, just as he would have accepted an illness, a legacy, a feast, starvation--anything but soap.\n\nTo enable this girl to earn her few weekly shillings, it was necessary for the Child of the Marshalsea to go through an elaborate form with the Father.\n\n‘Fanny is not going to live with us just now, father. She will be here a good deal in the day, but she is going to live outside with uncle.’\n\n‘You surprise me. Why?’\n\n‘I think uncle wants a companion, father. He should be attended to, and looked after.’\n\n‘A companion? He passes much of his time here. And you attend to him and look after him, Amy, a great deal more than ever your sister will. You all go out so much; you all go out so much.’\n\nThis was to keep up the ceremony and pretence of his having no idea that Amy herself went out by the day to work.\n\n‘But we are always glad to come home, father; now, are we not? And as to Fanny, perhaps besides keeping uncle company and taking care of him, it may be as well for her not quite to live here, always. She was not born here as I was, you know, father.’\n\n‘Well, Amy, well. I don’t quite follow you, but it’s natural I suppose that Fanny should prefer to be outside, and even that you often should, too. So, you and Fanny and your uncle, my dear, shall have your own way. Good, good. I’ll not meddle; don’t mind me.’\n\nTo get her brother out of the prison; out of the succession to Mrs Bangham in executing commissions, and out of the slang interchange with very doubtful companions consequent upon both; was her hardest task. At eighteen he would have dragged on from hand to mouth, from hour to hour, from penny to penny, until eighty. Nobody got into the prison from whom he derived anything useful or good, and she could find no patron for him but her old friend and godfather.\n\n‘Dear Bob,’ said she, ‘what is to become of poor Tip?’ His name was Edward, and Ted had been transformed into Tip, within the walls.\n\nThe turnkey had strong private opinions as to what would become of poor Tip, and had even gone so far with the view of averting their fulfilment, as to sound Tip in reference to the expediency of running away and going to serve his country. But Tip had thanked him, and said he didn’t seem to care for his country.\n\n‘Well, my dear,’ said the turnkey, ‘something ought to be done with him. Suppose I try and get him into the law?’\n\n‘That would be so good of you, Bob!’\n\nThe turnkey had now two points to put to the professional gentlemen as they passed in and out. He put this second one so perseveringly that a stool and twelve shillings a week were at last found for Tip in the office of an attorney in a great National Palladium called the Palace Court; at that time one of a considerable list of everlasting bulwarks to the dignity and safety of Albion, whose places know them no more.\n\nTip languished in Clifford’s Inns for six months, and at the expiration of that term sauntered back one evening with his hands in his pockets, and incidentally observed to his sister that he was not going back again.\n\n‘Not going back again?’ said the poor little anxious Child of the Marshalsea, always calculating and planning for Tip, in the front rank of her charges.\n\n‘I am so tired of it,’ said Tip, ‘that I have cut it.’\n\nTip tired of everything. With intervals of Marshalsea lounging, and Mrs Bangham succession, his small second mother, aided by her trusty friend, got him into a warehouse, into a market garden, into the hop trade, into the law again, into an auctioneers, into a brewery, into a stockbroker’s, into the law again, into a coach office, into a waggon office, into the law again, into a general dealer’s, into a distillery, into the law again, into a wool house, into a dry goods house, into the Billingsgate trade, into the foreign fruit trade, and into the docks. But whatever Tip went into, he came out of tired, announcing that he had cut it. Wherever he went, this foredoomed Tip appeared to take the prison walls with him, and to set them up in such trade or calling; and to prowl about within their narrow limits in the old slip-shod, purposeless, down-at-heel way; until the real immovable Marshalsea walls asserted their fascination over him, and brought him back.\n\nNevertheless, the brave little creature did so fix her heart on her brother’s rescue, that while he was ringing out these doleful changes, she pinched and scraped enough together to ship him for Canada. When he was tired of nothing to do, and disposed in its turn to cut even that, he graciously consented to go to Canada. And there was grief in her bosom over parting with him, and joy in the hope of his being put in a straight course at last.\n\n‘God bless you, dear Tip. Don’t be too proud to come and see us, when you have made your fortune.’\n\n‘All right!’ said Tip, and went.\n\nBut not all the way to Canada; in fact, not further than Liverpool. After making the voyage to that port from London, he found himself so strongly impelled to cut the vessel, that he resolved to walk back again. Carrying out which intention, he presented himself before her at the expiration of a month, in rags, without shoes, and much more tired than ever.\n\nAt length, after another interval of successorship to Mrs Bangham, he found a pursuit for himself, and announced it.\n\n‘Amy, I have got a situation.’\n\n‘Have you really and truly, Tip?’\n\n‘All right. I shall do now. You needn’t look anxious about me any more, old girl.’\n\n‘What is it, Tip?’\n\n‘Why, you know Slingo by sight?’\n\n‘Not the man they call the dealer?’\n\n‘That’s the chap. He’ll be out on Monday, and he’s going to give me a berth.’\n\n‘What is he a dealer in, Tip?’\n\n‘Horses. All right! I shall do now, Amy.’\n\nShe lost sight of him for months afterwards, and only heard from him once. A whisper passed among the elder collegians that he had been seen at a mock auction in Moorfields, pretending to buy plated articles for massive silver, and paying for them with the greatest liberality in bank notes; but it never reached her ears. One evening she was alone at work--standing up at the window, to save the twilight lingering above the wall--when he opened the door and walked in.\n\nShe kissed and welcomed him; but was afraid to ask him any questions. He saw how anxious and timid she was, and appeared sorry.\n\n‘I am afraid, Amy, you’ll be vexed this time. Upon my life I am!’\n\n‘I am very sorry to hear you say so, Tip. Have you come back?’\n\n‘Why--yes.’\n\n‘Not expecting this time that what you had found would answer very well, I am less surprised and sorry than I might have been, Tip.’\n\n‘Ah! But that’s not the worst of it.’\n\n‘Not the worst of it?’\n\n‘Don’t look so startled. No, Amy, not the worst of it. I have come back, you see; but--_don’t_ look so startled--I have come back in what I may call a new way. I am off the volunteer list altogether. I am in now, as one of the regulars.’\n\n‘Oh! Don’t say you are a prisoner, Tip! Don’t, don’t!’\n\n‘Well, I don’t want to say it,’ he returned in a reluctant tone; ‘but if you can’t understand me without my saying it, what am I to do? I am in for forty pound odd.’\n\nFor the first time in all those years, she sunk under her cares. She cried, with her clasped hands lifted above her head, that it would kill their father if he ever knew it; and fell down at Tip’s graceless feet.\n\nIt was easier for Tip to bring her to her senses than for her to bring _him_ to understand that the Father of the Marshalsea would be beside himself if he knew the truth. The thing was incomprehensible to Tip, and altogether a fanciful notion. He yielded to it in that light only, when he submitted to her entreaties, backed by those of his uncle and sister. There was no want of precedent for his return; it was accounted for to the father in the usual way; and the collegians, with a better comprehension of the pious fraud than Tip, supported it loyally.\n\nThis was the life, and this the history, of the child of the Marshalsea at twenty-two. With a still surviving attachment to the one miserable yard and block of houses as her birthplace and home, she passed to and fro in it shrinkingly now, with a womanly consciousness that she was pointed out to every one. Since she had begun to work beyond the walls, she had found it necessary to conceal where she lived, and to come and go as secretly as she could, between the free city and the iron gates, outside of which she had never slept in her life. Her original timidity had grown with this concealment, and her light step and her little figure shunned the thronged streets while they passed along them.\n\nWorldly wise in hard and poor necessities, she was innocent in all things else. Innocent, in the mist through which she saw her father, and the prison, and the turbid living river that flowed through it and flowed on.\n\nThis was the life, and this the history, of Little Dorrit; now going home upon a dull September evening, observed at a distance by Arthur Clennam. This was the life, and this the history, of Little Dorrit; turning at the end of London Bridge, recrossing it, going back again, passing on to Saint George’s Church, turning back suddenly once more, and flitting in at the open outer gate and little court-yard of the Marshalsea.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Lock\n\nArthur Clennam stood in the street, waiting to ask some passer-by what place that was. He suffered a few people to pass him in whose face there was no encouragement to make the inquiry, and still stood pausing in the street, when an old man came up and turned into the courtyard.\n\nHe stooped a good deal, and plodded along in a slow pre-occupied manner, which made the bustling London thoroughfares no very safe resort for him. He was dirtily and meanly dressed, in a threadbare coat, once blue, reaching to his ankles and buttoned to his chin, where it vanished in the pale ghost of a velvet collar. A piece of red cloth with which that phantom had been stiffened in its lifetime was now laid bare, and poked itself up, at the back of the old man’s neck, into a confusion of grey hair and rusty stock and buckle which altogether nearly poked his hat off. A greasy hat it was, and a napless; impending over his eyes, cracked and crumpled at the brim, and with a wisp of pocket-handkerchief dangling out below it. His trousers were so long and loose, and his shoes so clumsy and large, that he shuffled like an elephant; though how much of this was gait, and how much trailing cloth and leather, no one could have told. Under one arm he carried a limp and worn-out case, containing some wind instrument; in the same hand he had a pennyworth of snuff in a little packet of whitey-brown paper, from which he slowly comforted his poor blue old nose with a lengthened-out pinch, as Arthur Clennam looked at him.\n\nTo this old man crossing the court-yard, he preferred his inquiry, touching him on the shoulder. The old man stopped and looked round, with the expression in his weak grey eyes of one whose thoughts had been far off, and who was a little dull of hearing also.\n\n‘Pray, sir,’ said Arthur, repeating his question, ‘what is this place?’\n\n‘Ay! This place?’ returned the old man, staying his pinch of snuff on its road, and pointing at the place without looking at it. ‘This is the Marshalsea, sir.’\n\n‘The debtors’ prison?’\n\n‘Sir,’ said the old man, with the air of deeming it not quite necessary to insist upon that designation, ‘the debtors’ prison.’\n\nHe turned himself about, and went on.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ said Arthur, stopping him once more, ‘but will you allow me to ask you another question? Can any one go in here?’\n\n‘Any one can _go in_,’ replied the old man; plainly adding by the significance of his emphasis, ‘but it is not every one who can go out.’\n\n‘Pardon me once more. Are you familiar with the place?’\n\n‘Sir,’ returned the old man, squeezing his little packet of snuff in his hand, and turning upon his interrogator as if such questions hurt him. ‘I am.’\n\n‘I beg you to excuse me. I am not impertinently curious, but have a good object. Do you know the name of Dorrit here?’\n\n‘My name, sir,’ replied the old man most unexpectedly, ‘is Dorrit.’\n\nArthur pulled off his hat to him. ‘Grant me the favour of half-a-dozen words. I was wholly unprepared for your announcement, and hope that assurance is my sufficient apology for having taken the liberty of addressing you. I have recently come home to England after a long absence. I have seen at my mother’s--Mrs Clennam in the city--a young woman working at her needle, whom I have only heard addressed or spoken of as Little Dorrit. I have felt sincerely interested in her, and have had a great desire to know something more about her. I saw her, not a minute before you came up, pass in at that door.’\n\nThe old man looked at him attentively. ‘Are you a sailor, sir?’ he asked. He seemed a little disappointed by the shake of the head that replied to him. ‘Not a sailor? I judged from your sunburnt face that you might be. Are you in earnest, sir?’\n\n‘I do assure you that I am, and do entreat you to believe that I am, in plain earnest.’\n\n‘I know very little of the world, sir,’ returned the other, who had a weak and quavering voice. ‘I am merely passing on, like the shadow over the sun-dial. It would be worth no man’s while to mislead me; it would really be too easy--too poor a success, to yield any satisfaction. The young woman whom you saw go in here is my brother’s child. My brother is William Dorrit; I am Frederick. You say you have seen her at your mother’s (I know your mother befriends her), you have felt an interest in her, and you wish to know what she does here. Come and see.’\n\nHe went on again, and Arthur accompanied him.\n\n‘My brother,’ said the old man, pausing on the step and slowly facing round again, ‘has been here many years; and much that happens even among ourselves, out of doors, is kept from him for reasons that I needn’t enter upon now. Be so good as to say nothing of my niece’s working at her needle. Be so good as to say nothing that goes beyond what is said among us. If you keep within our bounds, you cannot well be wrong. Now! Come and see.’\n\nArthur followed him down a narrow entry, at the end of which a key was turned, and a strong door was opened from within. It admitted them into a lodge or lobby, across which they passed, and so through another door and a grating into the prison. The old man always plodding on before, turned round, in his slow, stiff, stooping manner, when they came to the turnkey on duty, as if to present his companion. The turnkey nodded; and the companion passed in without being asked whom he wanted.\n\nThe night was dark; and the prison lamps in the yard, and the candles in the prison windows faintly shining behind many sorts of wry old curtain and blind, had not the air of making it lighter. A few people loitered about, but the greater part of the population was within doors. The old man, taking the right-hand side of the yard, turned in at the third or fourth doorway, and began to ascend the stairs. ‘They are rather dark, sir, but you will not find anything in the way.’\n\nHe paused for a moment before opening a door on the second story. He had no sooner turned the handle than the visitor saw Little Dorrit, and saw the reason of her setting so much store by dining alone.\n\nShe had brought the meat home that she should have eaten herself, and was already warming it on a gridiron over the fire for her father, clad in an old grey gown and a black cap, awaiting his supper at the table. A clean cloth was spread before him, with knife, fork, and spoon, salt-cellar, pepper-box, glass, and pewter ale-pot. Such zests as his particular little phial of cayenne pepper and his pennyworth of pickles in a saucer, were not wanting.\n\nShe started, coloured deeply, and turned white. The visitor, more with his eyes than by the slight impulsive motion of his hand, entreated her to be reassured and to trust him.\n\n‘I found this gentleman,’ said the uncle--‘Mr Clennam, William, son of Amy’s friend--at the outer gate, wishful, as he was going by, of paying his respects, but hesitating whether to come in or not. This is my brother William, sir.’\n\n‘I hope,’ said Arthur, very doubtful what to say, ‘that my respect for your daughter may explain and justify my desire to be presented to you, sir.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ returned the other, rising, taking his cap off in the flat of his hand, and so holding it, ready to put on again, ‘you do me honour. You are welcome, sir;’ with a low bow. ‘Frederick, a chair. Pray sit down, Mr Clennam.’\n\nHe put his black cap on again as he had taken it off, and resumed his own seat. There was a wonderful air of benignity and patronage in his manner. These were the ceremonies with which he received the collegians.\n\n‘You are welcome to the Marshalsea, sir. I have welcomed many gentlemen to these walls. Perhaps you are aware--my daughter Amy may have mentioned that I am the Father of this place.’\n\n‘I--so I have understood,’ said Arthur, dashing at the assertion.\n\n‘You know, I dare say, that my daughter Amy was born here. A good girl, sir, a dear girl, and long a comfort and support to me. Amy, my dear, put this dish on; Mr Clennam will excuse the primitive customs to which we are reduced here. Is it a compliment to ask you if you would do me the honour, sir, to--’\n\n‘Thank you,’ returned Arthur. ‘Not a morsel.’\n\nHe felt himself quite lost in wonder at the manner of the man, and that the probability of his daughter’s having had a reserve as to her family history, should be so far out of his mind.\n\nShe filled his glass, put all the little matters on the table ready to his hand, and then sat beside him while he ate his supper. Evidently in observance of their nightly custom, she put some bread before herself, and touched his glass with her lips; but Arthur saw she was troubled and took nothing. Her look at her father, half admiring him and proud of him, half ashamed for him, all devoted and loving, went to his inmost heart.\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea condescended towards his brother as an amiable, well-meaning man; a private character, who had not arrived at distinction. ‘Frederick,’ said he, ‘you and Fanny sup at your lodgings to-night, I know. What have you done with Fanny, Frederick?’\n\n‘She is walking with Tip.’\n\n‘Tip--as you may know--is my son, Mr Clennam. He has been a little wild, and difficult to settle, but his introduction to the world was rather’--he shrugged his shoulders with a faint sigh, and looked round the room--‘a little adverse. Your first visit here, sir?’\n\n‘My first.’\n\n‘You could hardly have been here since your boyhood without my knowledge. It very seldom happens that anybody--of any pretensions--any pretensions--comes here without being presented to me.’\n\n‘As many as forty or fifty in a day have been introduced to my brother,’ said Frederick, faintly lighting up with a ray of pride.\n\n‘Yes!’ the Father of the Marshalsea assented. ‘We have even exceeded that number. On a fine Sunday in term time, it is quite a Levee--quite a Levee. Amy, my dear, I have been trying half the day to remember the name of the gentleman from Camberwell who was introduced to me last Christmas week by that agreeable coal-merchant who was remanded for six months.’\n\n‘I don’t remember his name, father.’\n\n‘Frederick, do _you_ remember his name?’\n\nFrederick doubted if he had ever heard it. No one could doubt that Frederick was the last person upon earth to put such a question to, with any hope of information.\n\n‘I mean,’ said his brother, ‘the gentleman who did that handsome action with so much delicacy. Ha! Tush! The name has quite escaped me. Mr Clennam, as I have happened to mention handsome and delicate action, you may like, perhaps, to know what it was.’\n\n‘Very much,’ said Arthur, withdrawing his eyes from the delicate head beginning to droop and the pale face with a new solicitude stealing over it.\n\n‘It is so generous, and shows so much fine feeling, that it is almost a duty to mention it. I said at the time that I always would mention it on every suitable occasion, without regard to personal sensitiveness. A--well--a--it’s of no use to disguise the fact--you must know, Mr Clennam, that it does sometimes occur that people who come here desire to offer some little--Testimonial--to the Father of the place.’\n\nTo see her hand upon his arm in mute entreaty half-repressed, and her timid little shrinking figure turning away, was to see a sad, sad sight.\n\n‘Sometimes,’ he went on in a low, soft voice, agitated, and clearing his throat every now and then; ‘sometimes--hem--it takes one shape and sometimes another; but it is generally--ha--Money. And it is, I cannot but confess it, it is too often--hem--acceptable. This gentleman that I refer to, was presented to me, Mr Clennam, in a manner highly gratifying to my feelings, and conversed not only with great politeness, but with great--ahem--information.’ All this time, though he had finished his supper, he was nervously going about his plate with his knife and fork, as if some of it were still before him. ‘It appeared from his conversation that he had a garden, though he was delicate of mentioning it at first, as gardens are--hem--are not accessible to me. But it came out, through my admiring a very fine cluster of geranium--beautiful cluster of geranium to be sure--which he had brought from his conservatory. On my taking notice of its rich colour, he showed me a piece of paper round it, on which was written, “For the Father of the Marshalsea,” and presented it to me. But this was--hem--not all. He made a particular request, on taking leave, that I would remove the paper in half an hour. I--ha--I did so; and I found that it contained--ahem--two guineas. I assure you, Mr Clennam, I have received--hem--Testimonials in many ways, and of many degrees of value, and they have always been--ha--unfortunately acceptable; but I never was more pleased than with this--ahem--this particular Testimonial.’\n\nArthur was in the act of saying the little he could say on such a theme, when a bell began to ring, and footsteps approached the door. A pretty girl of a far better figure and much more developed than Little Dorrit, though looking much younger in the face when the two were observed together, stopped in the doorway on seeing a stranger; and a young man who was with her, stopped too.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, Fanny. My eldest daughter and my son, Mr Clennam. The bell is a signal for visitors to retire, and so they have come to say good night; but there is plenty of time, plenty of time. Girls, Mr Clennam will excuse any household business you may have together. He knows, I dare say, that I have but one room here.’\n\n‘I only want my clean dress from Amy, father,’ said the second girl.\n\n‘And I my clothes,’ said Tip.\n\nAmy opened a drawer in an old piece of furniture that was a chest of drawers above and a bedstead below, and produced two little bundles, which she handed to her brother and sister. ‘Mended and made up?’ Clennam heard the sister ask in a whisper. To which Amy answered ‘Yes.’ He had risen now, and took the opportunity of glancing round the room. The bare walls had been coloured green, evidently by an unskilled hand, and were poorly decorated with a few prints. The window was curtained, and the floor carpeted; and there were shelves and pegs, and other such conveniences, that had accumulated in the course of years. It was a close, confined room, poorly furnished; and the chimney smoked to boot, or the tin screen at the top of the fireplace was superfluous; but constant pains and care had made it neat, and even, after its kind, comfortable.\n\nAll the while the bell was ringing, and the uncle was anxious to go. ‘Come, Fanny, come, Fanny,’ he said, with his ragged clarionet case under his arm; ‘the lock, child, the lock!’\n\nFanny bade her father good night, and whisked off airily. Tip had already clattered down-stairs. ‘Now, Mr Clennam,’ said the uncle, looking back as he shuffled out after them, ‘the lock, sir, the lock.’\n\nMr Clennam had two things to do before he followed; one, to offer his testimonial to the Father of the Marshalsea, without giving pain to his child; the other to say something to that child, though it were but a word, in explanation of his having come there.\n\n‘Allow me,’ said the Father, ‘to see you down-stairs.’\n\nShe had slipped out after the rest, and they were alone. ‘Not on any account,’ said the visitor, hurriedly. ‘Pray allow me to--’ chink, chink, chink.\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ said the Father, ‘I am deeply, deeply--’ But his visitor had shut up his hand to stop the clinking, and had gone down-stairs with great speed.\n\nHe saw no Little Dorrit on his way down, or in the yard. The last two or three stragglers were hurrying to the lodge, and he was following, when he caught sight of her in the doorway of the first house from the entrance. He turned back hastily.\n\n‘Pray forgive me,’ he said, ‘for speaking to you here; pray forgive me for coming here at all! I followed you to-night. I did so, that I might endeavour to render you and your family some service. You know the terms on which I and my mother are, and may not be surprised that I have preserved our distant relations at her house, lest I should unintentionally make her jealous, or resentful, or do you any injury in her estimation. What I have seen here, in this short time, has greatly increased my heartfelt wish to be a friend to you. It would recompense me for much disappointment if I could hope to gain your confidence.’\n\nShe was scared at first, but seemed to take courage while he spoke to her.\n\n‘You are very good, sir. You speak very earnestly to me. But I--but I wish you had not watched me.’\n\nHe understood the emotion with which she said it, to arise in her father’s behalf; and he respected it, and was silent.\n\n‘Mrs Clennam has been of great service to me; I don’t know what we should have done without the employment she has given me; I am afraid it may not be a good return to become secret with her; I can say no more to-night, sir. I am sure you mean to be kind to us. Thank you, thank you.’\n\n‘Let me ask you one question before I leave. Have you known my mother long?’\n\n‘I think two years, sir,--The bell has stopped.’\n\n‘How did you know her first? Did she send here for you?’\n\n‘No. She does not even know that I live here. We have a friend, father and I--a poor labouring man, but the best of friends--and I wrote out that I wished to do needlework, and gave his address. And he got what I wrote out displayed at a few places where it cost nothing, and Mrs Clennam found me that way, and sent for me. The gate will be locked, sir!’\n\nShe was so tremulous and agitated, and he was so moved by compassion for her, and by deep interest in her story as it dawned upon him, that he could scarcely tear himself away. But the stoppage of the bell, and the quiet in the prison, were a warning to depart; and with a few hurried words of kindness he left her gliding back to her father.\n\nBut he remained too late. The inner gate was locked, and the lodge closed. After a little fruitless knocking with his hand, he was standing there with the disagreeable conviction upon him that he had got to get through the night, when a voice accosted him from behind.\n\n‘Caught, eh?’ said the voice. ‘You won’t go home till morning. Oh! It’s you, is it, Mr Clennam?’\n\nThe voice was Tip’s; and they stood looking at one another in the prison-yard, as it began to rain.\n\n‘You’ve done it,’ observed Tip; ‘you must be sharper than that next time.’\n\n‘But you are locked in too,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘I believe I am!’ said Tip, sarcastically. ‘About! But not in your way. I belong to the shop, only my sister has a theory that our governor must never know it. I don’t see why, myself.’\n\n‘Can I get any shelter?’ asked Arthur. ‘What had I better do?’\n\n‘We had better get hold of Amy first of all,’ said Tip, referring any difficulty to her as a matter of course.\n\n‘I would rather walk about all night--it’s not much to do--than give that trouble.’\n\n‘You needn’t do that, if you don’t mind paying for a bed. If you don’t mind paying, they’ll make you up one on the Snuggery table, under the circumstances. If you’ll come along, I’ll introduce you there.’\n\nAs they passed down the yard, Arthur looked up at the window of the room he had lately left, where the light was still burning. ‘Yes, sir,’ said Tip, following his glance. ‘That’s the governor’s. She’ll sit with him for another hour reading yesterday’s paper to him, or something of that sort; and then she’ll come out like a little ghost, and vanish away without a sound.’\n\n‘I don’t understand you.’\n\n‘The governor sleeps up in the room, and she has a lodging at the turnkey’s. First house there,’ said Tip, pointing out the doorway into which she had retired. ‘First house, sky parlour. She pays twice as much for it as she would for one twice as good outside. But she stands by the governor, poor dear girl, day and night.’\n\nThis brought them to the tavern-establishment at the upper end of the prison, where the collegians had just vacated their social evening club. The apartment on the ground-floor in which it was held, was the Snuggery in question; the presidential tribune of the chairman, the pewter-pots, glasses, pipes, tobacco-ashes, and general flavour of members, were still as that convivial institution had left them on its adjournment. The Snuggery had two of the qualities popularly held to be essential to grog for ladies, in respect that it was hot and strong; but in the third point of analogy, requiring plenty of it, the Snuggery was defective; being but a cooped-up apartment.\n\nThe unaccustomed visitor from outside, naturally assumed everybody here to be prisoners--landlord, waiter, barmaid, potboy, and all. Whether they were or not, did not appear; but they all had a weedy look. The keeper of a chandler’s shop in a front parlour, who took in gentlemen boarders, lent his assistance in making the bed. He had been a tailor in his time, and had kept a phaeton, he said. He boasted that he stood up litigiously for the interests of the college; and he had undefined and undefinable ideas that the marshal intercepted a ‘Fund,’ which ought to come to the collegians. He liked to believe this, and always impressed the shadowy grievance on new-comers and strangers; though he could not, for his life, have explained what Fund he meant, or how the notion had got rooted in his soul. He had fully convinced himself, notwithstanding, that his own proper share of the Fund was three and ninepence a week; and that in this amount he, as an individual collegian, was swindled by the marshal, regularly every Monday. Apparently, he helped to make the bed, that he might not lose an opportunity of stating this case; after which unloading of his mind, and after announcing (as it seemed he always did, without anything coming of it) that he was going to write a letter to the papers and show the marshal up, he fell into miscellaneous conversation with the rest. It was evident from the general tone of the whole party, that they had come to regard insolvency as the normal state of mankind, and the payment of debts as a disease that occasionally broke out.\n\nIn this strange scene, and with these strange spectres flitting about him, Arthur Clennam looked on at the preparations as if they were part of a dream. Pending which, the long-initiated Tip, with an awful enjoyment of the Snuggery’s resources, pointed out the common kitchen fire maintained by subscription of collegians, the boiler for hot water supported in like manner, and other premises generally tending to the deduction that the way to be healthy, wealthy, and wise, was to come to the Marshalsea.\n\nThe two tables put together in a corner, were, at length, converted into a very fair bed; and the stranger was left to the Windsor chairs, the presidential tribune, the beery atmosphere, sawdust, pipe-lights, spittoons and repose. But the last item was long, long, long, in linking itself to the rest. The novelty of the place, the coming upon it without preparation, the sense of being locked up, the remembrance of that room up-stairs, of the two brothers, and above all of the retiring childish form, and the face in which he now saw years of insufficient food, if not of want, kept him waking and unhappy.\n\nSpeculations, too, bearing the strangest relations towards the prison, but always concerning the prison, ran like nightmares through his mind while he lay awake. Whether coffins were kept ready for people who might die there, where they were kept, how they were kept, where people who died in the prison were buried, how they were taken out, what forms were observed, whether an implacable creditor could arrest the dead? As to escaping, what chances there were of escape? Whether a prisoner could scale the walls with a cord and grapple, how he would descend upon the other side? whether he could alight on a housetop, steal down a staircase, let himself out at a door, and get lost in the crowd? As to Fire in the prison, if one were to break out while he lay there?\n\nAnd these involuntary starts of fancy were, after all, but the setting of a picture in which three people kept before him. His father, with the steadfast look with which he had died, prophetically darkened forth in the portrait; his mother, with her arm up, warding off his suspicion; Little Dorrit, with her hand on the degraded arm, and her drooping head turned away.\n\nWhat if his mother had an old reason she well knew for softening to this poor girl! What if the prisoner now sleeping quietly--Heaven grant it!--by the light of the great Day of judgment should trace back his fall to her. What if any act of hers and of his father’s, should have even remotely brought the grey heads of those two brothers so low!\n\nA swift thought shot into his mind. In that long imprisonment here, and in her own long confinement to her room, did his mother find a balance to be struck? ‘I admit that I was accessory to that man’s captivity. I have suffered for it in kind. He has decayed in his prison: I in mine. I have paid the penalty.’\n\nWhen all the other thoughts had faded out, this one held possession of him. When he fell asleep, she came before him in her wheeled chair, warding him off with this justification. When he awoke, and sprang up causelessly frightened, the words were in his ears, as if her voice had slowly spoken them at his pillow, to break his rest: ‘He withers away in his prison; I wither away in mine; inexorable justice is done; what do I owe on this score!’\n\n# CHAPTER 9. Little Mother\n\nThe morning light was in no hurry to climb the prison wall and look in at the Snuggery windows; and when it did come, it would have been more welcome if it had come alone, instead of bringing a rush of rain with it. But the equinoctial gales were blowing out at sea, and the impartial south-west wind, in its flight, would not neglect even the narrow Marshalsea. While it roared through the steeple of St George’s Church, and twirled all the cowls in the neighbourhood, it made a swoop to beat the Southwark smoke into the jail; and, plunging down the chimneys of the few early collegians who were yet lighting their fires, half suffocated them.\n\nArthur Clennam would have been little disposed to linger in bed, though his bed had been in a more private situation, and less affected by the raking out of yesterday’s fire, the kindling of to-day’s under the collegiate boiler, the filling of that Spartan vessel at the pump, the sweeping and sawdusting of the common room, and other such preparations. Heartily glad to see the morning, though little rested by the night, he turned out as soon as he could distinguish objects about him, and paced the yard for two heavy hours before the gate was opened.\n\nThe walls were so near to one another, and the wild clouds hurried over them so fast, that it gave him a sensation like the beginning of sea-sickness to look up at the gusty sky. The rain, carried aslant by flaws of wind, blackened that side of the central building which he had visited last night, but left a narrow dry trough under the lee of the wall, where he walked up and down among the waits of straw and dust and paper, the waste droppings of the pump, and the stray leaves of yesterday’s greens. It was as haggard a view of life as a man need look upon.\n\nNor was it relieved by any glimpse of the little creature who had brought him there. Perhaps she glided out of her doorway and in at that where her father lived, while his face was turned from both; but he saw nothing of her. It was too early for her brother; to have seen him once, was to have seen enough of him to know that he would be sluggish to leave whatever frowsy bed he occupied at night; so, as Arthur Clennam walked up and down, waiting for the gate to open, he cast about in his mind for future rather than for present means of pursuing his discoveries.\n\nAt last the lodge-gate turned, and the turnkey, standing on the step, taking an early comb at his hair, was ready to let him out. With a joyful sense of release he passed through the lodge, and found himself again in the little outer court-yard where he had spoken to the brother last night.\n\nThere was a string of people already straggling in, whom it was not difficult to identify as the nondescript messengers, go-betweens, and errand-bearers of the place. Some of them had been lounging in the rain until the gate should open; others, who had timed their arrival with greater nicety, were coming up now, and passing in with damp whitey-brown paper bags from the grocers, loaves of bread, lumps of butter, eggs, milk, and the like. The shabbiness of these attendants upon shabbiness, the poverty of these insolvent waiters upon insolvency, was a sight to see. Such threadbare coats and trousers, such fusty gowns and shawls, such squashed hats and bonnets, such boots and shoes, such umbrellas and walking-sticks, never were seen in Rag Fair. All of them wore the cast-off clothes of other men and women, were made up of patches and pieces of other people’s individuality, and had no sartorial existence of their own proper. Their walk was the walk of a race apart. They had a peculiar way of doggedly slinking round the corner, as if they were eternally going to the pawnbroker’s. When they coughed, they coughed like people accustomed to be forgotten on doorsteps and in draughty passages, waiting for answers to letters in faded ink, which gave the recipients of those manuscripts great mental disturbance and no satisfaction. As they eyed the stranger in passing, they eyed him with borrowing eyes--hungry, sharp, speculative as to his softness if they were accredited to him, and the likelihood of his standing something handsome. Mendicity on commission stooped in their high shoulders, shambled in their unsteady legs, buttoned and pinned and darned and dragged their clothes, frayed their button-holes, leaked out of their figures in dirty little ends of tape, and issued from their mouths in alcoholic breathings.\n\nAs these people passed him standing still in the court-yard, and one of them turned back to inquire if he could assist him with his services, it came into Arthur Clennam’s mind that he would speak to Little Dorrit again before he went away. She would have recovered her first surprise, and might feel easier with him. He asked this member of the fraternity (who had two red herrings in his hand, and a loaf and a blacking brush under his arm), where was the nearest place to get a cup of coffee at. The nondescript replied in encouraging terms, and brought him to a coffee-shop in the street within a stone’s throw.\n\n‘Do you know Miss Dorrit?’ asked the new client.\n\nThe nondescript knew two Miss Dorrits; one who was born inside--That was the one! That was the one? The nondescript had known her many years. In regard of the other Miss Dorrit, the nondescript lodged in the same house with herself and uncle.\n\nThis changed the client’s half-formed design of remaining at the coffee-shop until the nondescript should bring him word that Dorrit had issued forth into the street. He entrusted the nondescript with a confidential message to her, importing that the visitor who had waited on her father last night, begged the favour of a few words with her at her uncle’s lodging; he obtained from the same source full directions to the house, which was very near; dismissed the nondescript gratified with half-a-crown; and having hastily refreshed himself at the coffee-shop, repaired with all speed to the clarionet-player’s dwelling.\n\nThere were so many lodgers in this house that the doorpost seemed to be as full of bell-handles as a cathedral organ is of stops. Doubtful which might be the clarionet-stop, he was considering the point, when a shuttlecock flew out of the parlour window, and alighted on his hat. He then observed that in the parlour window was a blind with the inscription, MR CRIPPLES’s ACADEMY; also in another line, EVENING TUITION; and behind the blind was a little white-faced boy, with a slice of bread-and-butter and a battledore. The window being accessible from the footway, he looked in over the blind, returned the shuttlecock, and put his question.\n\n‘Dorrit?’ said the little white-faced boy (Master Cripples in fact). ‘_Mr_ Dorrit? Third bell and one knock.’\n\nThe pupils of Mr Cripples appeared to have been making a copy-book of the street-door, it was so extensively scribbled over in pencil. The frequency of the inscriptions, ‘Old Dorrit,’ and ‘Dirty Dick,’ in combination, suggested intentions of personality on the part Of Mr Cripples’s pupils. There was ample time to make these observations before the door was opened by the poor old man himself.\n\n‘Ha!’ said he, very slowly remembering Arthur, ‘you were shut in last night?’\n\n‘Yes, Mr Dorrit. I hope to meet your niece here presently.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said he, pondering. ‘Out of my brother’s way? True. Would you come up-stairs and wait for her?’\n\n‘Thank you.’\n\nTurning himself as slowly as he turned in his mind whatever he heard or said, he led the way up the narrow stairs. The house was very close, and had an unwholesome smell. The little staircase windows looked in at the back windows of other houses as unwholesome as itself, with poles and lines thrust out of them, on which unsightly linen hung; as if the inhabitants were angling for clothes, and had had some wretched bites not worth attending to. In the back garret--a sickly room, with a turn-up bedstead in it, so hastily and recently turned up that the blankets were boiling over, as it were, and keeping the lid open--a half-finished breakfast of coffee and toast for two persons was jumbled down anyhow on a rickety table.\n\nThere was no one there. The old man mumbling to himself, after some consideration, that Fanny had run away, went to the next room to fetch her back. The visitor, observing that she held the door on the inside, and that, when the uncle tried to open it, there was a sharp adjuration of ‘Don’t, stupid!’ and an appearance of loose stocking and flannel, concluded that the young lady was in an undress. The uncle, without appearing to come to any conclusion, shuffled in again, sat down in his chair, and began warming his hands at the fire; not that it was cold, or that he had any waking idea whether it was or not.\n\n‘What did you think of my brother, sir?’ he asked, when he by-and-by discovered what he was doing, left off, reached over to the chimney-piece, and took his clarionet case down.\n\n‘I was glad,’ said Arthur, very much at a loss, for his thoughts were on the brother before him; ‘to find him so well and cheerful.’\n\n‘Ha!’ muttered the old man, ‘yes, yes, yes, yes, yes!’\n\nArthur wondered what he could possibly want with the clarionet case. He did not want it at all. He discovered, in due time, that it was not the little paper of snuff (which was also on the chimney-piece), put it back again, took down the snuff instead, and solaced himself with a pinch. He was as feeble, spare, and slow in his pinches as in everything else, but a certain little trickling of enjoyment of them played in the poor worn nerves about the corners of his eyes and mouth.\n\n‘Amy, Mr Clennam. What do you think of her?’\n\n‘I am much impressed, Mr Dorrit, by all that I have seen of her and thought of her.’\n\n‘My brother would have been quite lost without Amy,’ he returned. ‘We should all have been lost without Amy. She is a very good girl, Amy. She does her duty.’\n\nArthur fancied that he heard in these praises a certain tone of custom, which he had heard from the father last night with an inward protest and feeling of antagonism. It was not that they stinted her praises, or were insensible to what she did for them; but that they were lazily habituated to her, as they were to all the rest of their condition. He fancied that although they had before them, every day, the means of comparison between her and one another and themselves, they regarded her as being in her necessary place; as holding a position towards them all which belonged to her, like her name or her age. He fancied that they viewed her, not as having risen away from the prison atmosphere, but as appertaining to it; as being vaguely what they had a right to expect, and nothing more.\n\nHer uncle resumed his breakfast, and was munching toast sopped in coffee, oblivious of his guest, when the third bell rang. That was Amy, he said, and went down to let her in; leaving the visitor with as vivid a picture on his mind of his begrimed hands, dirt-worn face, and decayed figure, as if he were still drooping in his chair.\n\nShe came up after him, in the usual plain dress, and with the usual timid manner. Her lips were a little parted, as if her heart beat faster than usual.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, Amy,’ said her uncle, ‘has been expecting you some time.’\n\n‘I took the liberty of sending you a message.’\n\n‘I received the message, sir.’\n\n‘Are you going to my mother’s this morning? I think not, for it is past your usual hour.’\n\n‘Not to-day, sir. I am not wanted to-day.’\n\n‘Will you allow Me to walk a little way in whatever direction you may be going? I can then speak to you as we walk, both without detaining you here, and without intruding longer here myself.’\n\nShe looked embarrassed, but said, if he pleased. He made a pretence of having mislaid his walking-stick, to give her time to set the bedstead right, to answer her sister’s impatient knock at the wall, and to say a word softly to her uncle. Then he found it, and they went down-stairs; she first, he following; the uncle standing at the stair-head, and probably forgetting them before they had reached the ground floor.\n\nMr Cripples’s pupils, who were by this time coming to school, desisted from their morning recreation of cuffing one another with bags and books, to stare with all the eyes they had at a stranger who had been to see Dirty Dick. They bore the trying spectacle in silence, until the mysterious visitor was at a safe distance; when they burst into pebbles and yells, and likewise into reviling dances, and in all respects buried the pipe of peace with so many savage ceremonies, that, if Mr Cripples had been the chief of the Cripplewayboo tribe with his war-paint on, they could scarcely have done greater justice to their education.\n\nIn the midst of this homage, Mr Arthur Clennam offered his arm to Little Dorrit, and Little Dorrit took it. ‘Will you go by the Iron Bridge,’ said he, ‘where there is an escape from the noise of the street?’ Little Dorrit answered, if he pleased, and presently ventured to hope that he would ‘not mind’ Mr Cripples’s boys, for she had herself received her education, such as it was, in Mr Cripples’s evening academy. He returned, with the best will in the world, that Mr Cripples’s boys were forgiven out of the bottom of his soul. Thus did Cripples unconsciously become a master of the ceremonies between them, and bring them more naturally together than Beau Nash might have done if they had lived in his golden days, and he had alighted from his coach and six for the purpose.\n\nThe morning remained squally, and the streets were miserably muddy, but no rain fell as they walked towards the Iron Bridge. The little creature seemed so young in his eyes, that there were moments when he found himself thinking of her, if not speaking to her, as if she were a child. Perhaps he seemed as old in her eyes as she seemed young in his.\n\n‘I am sorry to hear you were so inconvenienced last night, sir, as to be locked in. It was very unfortunate.’\n\nIt was nothing, he returned. He had had a very good bed.\n\n‘Oh yes!’ she said quickly; ‘she believed there were excellent beds at the coffee-house.’ He noticed that the coffee-house was quite a majestic hotel to her, and that she treasured its reputation.\n\n‘I believe it is very expensive,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘but my father has told me that quite beautiful dinners may be got there. And wine,’ she added timidly.\n\n‘Were you ever there?’\n\n‘Oh no! Only into the kitchen to fetch hot water.’\n\nTo think of growing up with a kind of awe upon one as to the luxuries of that superb establishment, the Marshalsea Hotel!\n\n‘I asked you last night,’ said Clennam, ‘how you had become acquainted with my mother. Did you ever hear her name before she sent for you?’\n\n‘No, sir.’\n‘Do you think your father ever did?’\n\n‘No, sir.’\n\nHe met her eyes raised to his with so much wonder in them (she was scared when the encounter took place, and shrunk away again), that he felt it necessary to say:\n\n‘I have a reason for asking, which I cannot very well explain; but you must, on no account, suppose it to be of a nature to cause you the least alarm or anxiety. Quite the reverse. And you think that at no time of your father’s life was my name of Clennam ever familiar to him?’\n\n‘No, sir.’\n\nHe felt, from the tone in which she spoke, that she was glancing up at him with those parted lips; therefore he looked before him, rather than make her heart beat quicker still by embarrassing her afresh.\n\nThus they emerged upon the Iron Bridge, which was as quiet after the roaring streets as though it had been open country. The wind blew roughly, the wet squalls came rattling past them, skimming the pools on the road and pavement, and raining them down into the river. The clouds raced on furiously in the lead-coloured sky, the smoke and mist raced after them, the dark tide ran fierce and strong in the same direction. Little Dorrit seemed the least, the quietest, and weakest of Heaven’s creatures.\n\n‘Let me put you in a coach,’ said Clennam, very nearly adding ‘my poor child.’\n\nShe hurriedly declined, saying that wet or dry made little difference to her; she was used to go about in all weathers. He knew it to be so, and was touched with more pity; thinking of the slight figure at his side, making its nightly way through the damp dark boisterous streets to such a place of rest.\n\n‘You spoke so feelingly to me last night, sir, and I found afterwards that you had been so generous to my father, that I could not resist your message, if it was only to thank you; especially as I wished very much to say to you--’ she hesitated and trembled, and tears rose in her eyes, but did not fall.\n\n‘To say to me--?’\n\n‘That I hope you will not misunderstand my father. Don’t judge him, sir, as you would judge others outside the gates. He has been there so long! I never saw him outside, but I can understand that he must have grown different in some things since.’\n\n‘My thoughts will never be unjust or harsh towards him, believe me.’\n\n‘Not,’ she said, with a prouder air, as the misgiving evidently crept upon her that she might seem to be abandoning him, ‘not that he has anything to be ashamed of for himself, or that I have anything to be ashamed of for him. He only requires to be understood. I only ask for him that his life may be fairly remembered. All that he said was quite true. It all happened just as he related it. He is very much respected. Everybody who comes in, is glad to know him. He is more courted than anyone else. He is far more thought of than the Marshal is.’\n\nIf ever pride were innocent, it was innocent in Little Dorrit when she grew boastful of her father.\n\n‘It is often said that his manners are a true gentleman’s, and quite a study. I see none like them in that place, but he is admitted to be superior to all the rest. This is quite as much why they make him presents, as because they know him to be needy. He is not to be blamed for being in need, poor love. Who could be in prison a quarter of a century, and be prosperous!’\n\nWhat affection in her words, what compassion in her repressed tears, what a great soul of fidelity within her, how true the light that shed false brightness round him!\n\n‘If I have found it best to conceal where my home is, it is not because I am ashamed of him. God forbid! Nor am I so much ashamed of the place itself as might be supposed. People are not bad because they come there. I have known numbers of good, persevering, honest people come there through misfortune. They are almost all kind-hearted to one another. And it would be ungrateful indeed in me, to forget that I have had many quiet, comfortable hours there; that I had an excellent friend there when I was quite a baby, who was very very fond of me; that I have been taught there, and have worked there, and have slept soundly there. I think it would be almost cowardly and cruel not to have some little attachment for it, after all this.’\n\nShe had relieved the faithful fulness of her heart, and modestly said, raising her eyes appealingly to her new friend’s, ‘I did not mean to say so much, nor have I ever but once spoken about this before. But it seems to set it more right than it was last night. I said I wished you had not followed me, sir. I don’t wish it so much now, unless you should think--indeed I don’t wish it at all, unless I should have spoken so confusedly, that--that you can scarcely understand me, which I am afraid may be the case.’\n\nHe told her with perfect truth that it was not the case; and putting himself between her and the sharp wind and rain, sheltered her as well as he could.\n\n‘I feel permitted now,’ he said, ‘to ask you a little more concerning your father. Has he many creditors?’\n\n‘Oh! a great number.’\n\n‘I mean detaining creditors, who keep him where he is?’\n\n‘Oh yes! a great number.’\n\n‘Can you tell me--I can get the information, no doubt, elsewhere, if you cannot--who is the most influential of them?’\n\nLittle Dorrit said, after considering a little, that she used to hear long ago of Mr Tite Barnacle as a man of great power. He was a commissioner, or a board, or a trustee, ‘or something.’ He lived in Grosvenor Square, she thought, or very near it. He was under Government--high in the Circumlocution Office. She appeared to have acquired, in her infancy, some awful impression of the might of this formidable Mr Tite Barnacle of Grosvenor Square, or very near it, and the Circumlocution Office, which quite crushed her when she mentioned him.\n\n‘It can do no harm,’ thought Arthur, ‘if I see this Mr Tite Barnacle.’\n\nThe thought did not present itself so quietly but that her quickness intercepted it. ‘Ah!’ said Little Dorrit, shaking her head with the mild despair of a lifetime. ‘Many people used to think once of getting my poor father out, but you don’t know how hopeless it is.’\n\nShe forgot to be shy at the moment, in honestly warning him away from the sunken wreck he had a dream of raising; and looked at him with eyes which assuredly, in association with her patient face, her fragile figure, her spare dress, and the wind and rain, did not turn him from his purpose of helping her.\n\n‘Even if it could be done,’ said she--‘and it never can be done now--where could father live, or how could he live? I have often thought that if such a change could come, it might be anything but a service to him now. People might not think so well of him outside as they do there. He might not be so gently dealt with outside as he is there. He might not be so fit himself for the life outside as he is for that.’\n\nHere for the first time she could not restrain her tears from falling; and the little thin hands he had watched when they were so busy, trembled as they clasped each other.\n\n‘It would be a new distress to him even to know that I earn a little money, and that Fanny earns a little money. He is so anxious about us, you see, feeling helplessly shut up there. Such a good, good father!’\n\nHe let the little burst of feeling go by before he spoke. It was soon gone. She was not accustomed to think of herself, or to trouble any one with her emotions. He had but glanced away at the piles of city roofs and chimneys among which the smoke was rolling heavily, and at the wilderness of masts on the river, and the wilderness of steeples on the shore, indistinctly mixed together in the stormy haze, when she was again as quiet as if she had been plying her needle in his mother’s room.\n\n‘You would be glad to have your brother set at liberty?’\n\n‘Oh very, very glad, sir!’\n\n‘Well, we will hope for him at least. You told me last night of a friend you had?’\n\nHis name was Plornish, Little Dorrit said.\n\nAnd where did Plornish live? Plornish lived in Bleeding Heart Yard. He was ‘only a plasterer,’ Little Dorrit said, as a caution to him not to form high social expectations of Plornish. He lived at the last house in Bleeding Heart Yard, and his name was over a little gateway.\n\nArthur took down the address and gave her his. He had now done all he sought to do for the present, except that he wished to leave her with a reliance upon him, and to have something like a promise from her that she would cherish it.\n\n‘There is one friend!’ he said, putting up his pocketbook. ‘As I take you back--you are going back?’\n\n‘Oh yes! going straight home.’\n\n‘--As I take you back,’ the word home jarred upon him, ‘let me ask you to persuade yourself that you have another friend. I make no professions, and say no more.’\n\n‘You are truly kind to me, sir. I am sure I need no more.’\n\nThey walked back through the miserable muddy streets, and among the poor, mean shops, and were jostled by the crowds of dirty hucksters usual to a poor neighbourhood. There was nothing, by the short way, that was pleasant to any of the five senses. Yet it was not a common passage through common rain, and mire, and noise, to Clennam, having this little, slender, careful creature on his arm. How young she seemed to him, or how old he to her; or what a secret either to the other, in that beginning of the destined interweaving of their stories, matters not here. He thought of her having been born and bred among these scenes, and shrinking through them now, familiar yet misplaced; he thought of her long acquaintance with the squalid needs of life, and of her innocence; of her solicitude for others, and her few years, and her childish aspect.\n\nThey were come into the High Street, where the prison stood, when a voice cried, ‘Little mother, little mother!’ Little Dorrit stopping and looking back, an excited figure of a strange kind bounced against them (still crying ‘little mother’), fell down, and scattered the contents of a large basket, filled with potatoes, in the mud.\n\n‘Oh, Maggy,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘what a clumsy child you are!’\n\nMaggy was not hurt, but picked herself up immediately, and then began to pick up the potatoes, in which both Little Dorrit and Arthur Clennam helped. Maggy picked up very few potatoes and a great quantity of mud; but they were all recovered, and deposited in the basket. Maggy then smeared her muddy face with her shawl, and presenting it to Mr Clennam as a type of purity, enabled him to see what she was like.\n\nShe was about eight-and-twenty, with large bones, large features, large feet and hands, large eyes and no hair. Her large eyes were limpid and almost colourless; they seemed to be very little affected by light, and to stand unnaturally still. There was also that attentive listening expression in her face, which is seen in the faces of the blind; but she was not blind, having one tolerably serviceable eye. Her face was not exceedingly ugly, though it was only redeemed from being so by a smile; a good-humoured smile, and pleasant in itself, but rendered pitiable by being constantly there. A great white cap, with a quantity of opaque frilling that was always flapping about, apologised for Maggy’s baldness, and made it so very difficult for her old black bonnet to retain its place upon her head, that it held on round her neck like a gipsy’s baby. A commission of haberdashers could alone have reported what the rest of her poor dress was made of, but it had a strong general resemblance to seaweed, with here and there a gigantic tea-leaf. Her shawl looked particularly like a tea-leaf after long infusion.\n\nArthur Clennam looked at Little Dorrit with the expression of one saying, ‘May I ask who this is?’ Little Dorrit, whose hand this Maggy, still calling her little mother, had begun to fondle, answered in words (they were under a gateway into which the majority of the potatoes had rolled).\n\n‘This is Maggy, sir.’\n\n‘Maggy, sir,’ echoed the personage presented. ‘Little mother!’\n\n‘She is the grand-daughter--’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Grand-daughter,’ echoed Maggy.\n\n‘Of my old nurse, who has been dead a long time. Maggy, how old are you?’\n\n‘Ten, mother,’ said Maggy.\n\n‘You can’t think how good she is, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, with infinite tenderness.\n\n‘Good _she_ is,’ echoed Maggy, transferring the pronoun in a most expressive way from herself to her little mother.\n\n‘Or how clever,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘She goes on errands as well as any one.’ Maggy laughed. ‘And is as trustworthy as the Bank of England.’ Maggy laughed. ‘She earns her own living entirely. Entirely, sir!’ said Little Dorrit, in a lower and triumphant tone. ‘Really does!’\n\n‘What is her history?’ asked Clennam.\n\n‘Think of that, Maggy?’ said Little Dorrit, taking her two large hands and clapping them together. ‘A gentleman from thousands of miles away, wanting to know your history!’\n\n‘_My_ history?’ cried Maggy. ‘Little mother.’\n\n‘She means me,’ said Little Dorrit, rather confused; ‘she is very much attached to me. Her old grandmother was not so kind to her as she should have been; was she, Maggy?’\n\nMaggy shook her head, made a drinking vessel of her clenched left hand, drank out of it, and said, ‘Gin.’ Then beat an imaginary child, and said, ‘Broom-handles and pokers.’\n\n‘When Maggy was ten years old,’ said Little Dorrit, watching her face while she spoke, ‘she had a bad fever, sir, and she has never grown any older ever since.’\n\n‘Ten years old,’ said Maggy, nodding her head. ‘But what a nice hospital! So comfortable, wasn’t it? Oh so nice it was. Such a Ev’nly place!’\n\n‘She had never been at peace before, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, turning towards Arthur for an instant and speaking low, ‘and she always runs off upon that.’\n\n‘Such beds there is there!’ cried Maggy. ‘Such lemonades! Such oranges! Such d’licious broth and wine! Such Chicking! Oh, AIN’T it a delightful place to go and stop at!’\n\n‘So Maggy stopped there as long as she could,’ said Little Dorrit, in her former tone of telling a child’s story; the tone designed for Maggy’s ear, ‘and at last, when she could stop there no longer, she came out. Then, because she was never to be more than ten years old, however long she lived--’\n\n‘However long she lived,’ echoed Maggy.\n\n‘--And because she was very weak; indeed was so weak that when she began to laugh she couldn’t stop herself--which was a great pity--’\n\n(Maggy mighty grave of a sudden.)\n\n‘--Her grandmother did not know what to do with her, and for some years was very unkind to her indeed. At length, in course of time, Maggy began to take pains to improve herself, and to be very attentive and very industrious; and by degrees was allowed to come in and out as often as she liked, and got enough to do to support herself, and does support herself. And that,’ said Little Dorrit, clapping the two great hands together again, ‘is Maggy’s history, as Maggy knows!’\n\nAh! But Arthur would have known what was wanting to its completeness, though he had never heard of the words Little mother; though he had never seen the fondling of the small spare hand; though he had had no sight for the tears now standing in the colourless eyes; though he had had no hearing for the sob that checked the clumsy laugh. The dirty gateway with the wind and rain whistling through it, and the basket of muddy potatoes waiting to be spilt again or taken up, never seemed the common hole it really was, when he looked back to it by these lights. Never, never!\n\nThey were very near the end of their walk, and they now came out of the gateway to finish it. Nothing would serve Maggy but that they must stop at a grocer’s window, short of their destination, for her to show her learning. She could read after a sort; and picked out the fat figures in the tickets of prices, for the most part correctly. She also stumbled, with a large balance of success against her failures, through various philanthropic recommendations to Try our Mixture, Try our Family Black, Try our Orange-flavoured Pekoe, challenging competition at the head of Flowery Teas; and various cautions to the public against spurious establishments and adulterated articles. When he saw how pleasure brought a rosy tint into Little Dorrit’s face when Maggy made a hit, he felt that he could have stood there making a library of the grocer’s window until the rain and wind were tired.\n\nThe court-yard received them at last, and there he said goodbye to Little Dorrit. Little as she had always looked, she looked less than ever when he saw her going into the Marshalsea lodge passage, the little mother attended by her big child.\n\nThe cage door opened, and when the small bird, reared in captivity, had tamely fluttered in, he saw it shut again; and then he came away.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. Containing the whole Science of Government\n\nThe Circumlocution Office was (as everybody knows without being told) the most important Department under Government. No public business of any kind could possibly be done at any time without the acquiescence of the Circumlocution Office. Its finger was in the largest public pie, and in the smallest public tart. It was equally impossible to do the plainest right and to undo the plainest wrong without the express authority of the Circumlocution Office. If another Gunpowder Plot had been discovered half an hour before the lighting of the match, nobody would have been justified in saving the parliament until there had been half a score of boards, half a bushel of minutes, several sacks of official memoranda, and a family-vault full of ungrammatical correspondence, on the part of the Circumlocution Office.\n\nThis glorious establishment had been early in the field, when the one sublime principle involving the difficult art of governing a country, was first distinctly revealed to statesmen. It had been foremost to study that bright revelation and to carry its shining influence through the whole of the official proceedings. Whatever was required to be done, the Circumlocution Office was beforehand with all the public departments in the art of perceiving--HOW NOT TO DO IT.\n\nThrough this delicate perception, through the tact with which it invariably seized it, and through the genius with which it always acted on it, the Circumlocution Office had risen to overtop all the public departments; and the public condition had risen to be--what it was.\n\nIt is true that How not to do it was the great study and object of all public departments and professional politicians all round the Circumlocution Office. It is true that every new premier and every new government, coming in because they had upheld a certain thing as necessary to be done, were no sooner come in than they applied their utmost faculties to discovering How not to do it. It is true that from the moment when a general election was over, every returned man who had been raving on hustings because it hadn’t been done, and who had been asking the friends of the honourable gentleman in the opposite interest on pain of impeachment to tell him why it hadn’t been done, and who had been asserting that it must be done, and who had been pledging himself that it should be done, began to devise, How it was not to be done. It is true that the debates of both Houses of Parliament the whole session through, uniformly tended to the protracted deliberation, How not to do it. It is true that the royal speech at the opening of such session virtually said, My lords and gentlemen, you have a considerable stroke of work to do, and you will please to retire to your respective chambers, and discuss, How not to do it. It is true that the royal speech, at the close of such session, virtually said, My lords and gentlemen, you have through several laborious months been considering with great loyalty and patriotism, How not to do it, and you have found out; and with the blessing of Providence upon the harvest (natural, not political), I now dismiss you. All this is true, but the Circumlocution Office went beyond it.\n\nBecause the Circumlocution Office went on mechanically, every day, keeping this wonderful, all-sufficient wheel of statesmanship, How not to do it, in motion. Because the Circumlocution Office was down upon any ill-advised public servant who was going to do it, or who appeared to be by any surprising accident in remote danger of doing it, with a minute, and a memorandum, and a letter of instructions that extinguished him. It was this spirit of national efficiency in the Circumlocution Office that had gradually led to its having something to do with everything. Mechanicians, natural philosophers, soldiers, sailors, petitioners, memorialists, people with grievances, people who wanted to prevent grievances, people who wanted to redress grievances, jobbing people, jobbed people, people who couldn’t get rewarded for merit, and people who couldn’t get punished for demerit, were all indiscriminately tucked up under the foolscap paper of the Circumlocution Office.\n\nNumbers of people were lost in the Circumlocution Office. Unfortunates with wrongs, or with projects for the general welfare (and they had better have had wrongs at first, than have taken that bitter English recipe for certainly getting them), who in slow lapse of time and agony had passed safely through other public departments; who, according to rule, had been bullied in this, over-reached by that, and evaded by the other; got referred at last to the Circumlocution Office, and never reappeared in the light of day. Boards sat upon them, secretaries minuted upon them, commissioners gabbled about them, clerks registered, entered, checked, and ticked them off, and they melted away. In short, all the business of the country went through the Circumlocution Office, except the business that never came out of it; and _its_ name was Legion.\n\nSometimes, angry spirits attacked the Circumlocution Office. Sometimes, parliamentary questions were asked about it, and even parliamentary motions made or threatened about it by demagogues so low and ignorant as to hold that the real recipe of government was, How to do it. Then would the noble lord, or right honourable gentleman, in whose department it was to defend the Circumlocution Office, put an orange in his pocket, and make a regular field-day of the occasion. Then would he come down to that house with a slap upon the table, and meet the honourable gentleman foot to foot. Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that the Circumlocution Office not only was blameless in this matter, but was commendable in this matter, was extollable to the skies in this matter. Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that, although the Circumlocution Office was invariably right and wholly right, it never was so right as in this matter. Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that it would have been more to his honour, more to his credit, more to his good taste, more to his good sense, more to half the dictionary of commonplaces, if he had left the Circumlocution Office alone, and never approached this matter. Then would he keep one eye upon a coach or crammer from the Circumlocution Office sitting below the bar, and smash the honourable gentleman with the Circumlocution Office account of this matter. And although one of two things always happened; namely, either that the Circumlocution Office had nothing to say and said it, or that it had something to say of which the noble lord, or right honourable gentleman, blundered one half and forgot the other; the Circumlocution Office was always voted immaculate by an accommodating majority.\n\nSuch a nursery of statesmen had the Department become in virtue of a long career of this nature, that several solemn lords had attained the reputation of being quite unearthly prodigies of business, solely from having practised, How not to do it, as the head of the Circumlocution Office. As to the minor priests and acolytes of that temple, the result of all this was that they stood divided into two classes, and, down to the junior messenger, either believed in the Circumlocution Office as a heaven-born institution that had an absolute right to do whatever it liked; or took refuge in total infidelity, and considered it a flagrant nuisance.\n\nThe Barnacle family had for some time helped to administer the Circumlocution Office. The Tite Barnacle Branch, indeed, considered themselves in a general way as having vested rights in that direction, and took it ill if any other family had much to say to it. The Barnacles were a very high family, and a very large family. They were dispersed all over the public offices, and held all sorts of public places. Either the nation was under a load of obligation to the Barnacles, or the Barnacles were under a load of obligation to the nation. It was not quite unanimously settled which; the Barnacles having their opinion, the nation theirs.\n\nThe Mr Tite Barnacle who at the period now in question usually coached or crammed the statesman at the head of the Circumlocution Office, when that noble or right honourable individual sat a little uneasily in his saddle by reason of some vagabond making a tilt at him in a newspaper, was more flush of blood than money. As a Barnacle he had his place, which was a snug thing enough; and as a Barnacle he had of course put in his son Barnacle Junior in the office. But he had intermarried with a branch of the Stiltstalkings, who were also better endowed in a sanguineous point of view than with real or personal property, and of this marriage there had been issue, Barnacle junior and three young ladies. What with the patrician requirements of Barnacle junior, the three young ladies, Mrs Tite Barnacle nee Stiltstalking, and himself, Mr Tite Barnacle found the intervals between quarter day and quarter day rather longer than he could have desired; a circumstance which he always attributed to the country’s parsimony.\n\nFor Mr Tite Barnacle, Mr Arthur Clennam made his fifth inquiry one day at the Circumlocution Office; having on previous occasions awaited that gentleman successively in a hall, a glass case, a waiting room, and a fire-proof passage where the Department seemed to keep its wind. On this occasion Mr Barnacle was not engaged, as he had been before, with the noble prodigy at the head of the Department; but was absent. Barnacle Junior, however, was announced as a lesser star, yet visible above the office horizon.\n\nWith Barnacle junior, he signified his desire to confer; and found that young gentleman singeing the calves of his legs at the parental fire, and supporting his spine against the mantel-shelf. It was a comfortable room, handsomely furnished in the higher official manner; and presenting stately suggestions of the absent Barnacle, in the thick carpet, the leather-covered desk to sit at, the leather-covered desk to stand at, the formidable easy-chair and hearth-rug, the interposed screen, the torn-up papers, the dispatch-boxes with little labels sticking out of them, like medicine bottles or dead game, the pervading smell of leather and mahogany, and a general bamboozling air of How not to do it.\n\nThe present Barnacle, holding Mr Clennam’s card in his hand, had a youthful aspect, and the fluffiest little whisker, perhaps, that ever was seen. Such a downy tip was on his callow chin, that he seemed half fledged like a young bird; and a compassionate observer might have urged that, if he had not singed the calves of his legs, he would have died of cold. He had a superior eye-glass dangling round his neck, but unfortunately had such flat orbits to his eyes and such limp little eyelids that it wouldn’t stick in when he put it up, but kept tumbling out against his waistcoat buttons with a click that discomposed him very much.\n\n‘Oh, I say. Look here! My father’s not in the way, and won’t be in the way to-day,’ said Barnacle Junior. ‘Is this anything that I can do?’\n\n(Click! Eye-glass down. Barnacle Junior quite frightened and feeling all round himself, but not able to find it.)\n\n‘You are very good,’ said Arthur Clennam. ‘I wish however to see Mr Barnacle.’\n\n‘But I say. Look here! You haven’t got any appointment, you know,’ said Barnacle Junior.\n\n(By this time he had found the eye-glass, and put it up again.)\n\n‘No,’ said Arthur Clennam. ‘That is what I wish to have.’\n\n‘But I say. Look here! Is this public business?’ asked Barnacle junior.\n\n(Click! Eye-glass down again. Barnacle Junior in that state of search after it that Mr Clennam felt it useless to reply at present.)\n\n‘Is it,’ said Barnacle junior, taking heed of his visitor’s brown face, ‘anything about--Tonnage--or that sort of thing?’\n\n(Pausing for a reply, he opened his right eye with his hand, and stuck his glass in it, in that inflammatory manner that his eye began watering dreadfully.)\n\n‘No,’ said Arthur, ‘it is nothing about tonnage.’\n\n‘Then look here. Is it private business?’\n\n‘I really am not sure. It relates to a Mr Dorrit.’\n\n‘Look here, I tell you what! You had better call at our house, if you are going that way. Twenty-four, Mews Street, Grosvenor Square. My father’s got a slight touch of the gout, and is kept at home by it.’\n\n(The misguided young Barnacle evidently going blind on his eye-glass side, but ashamed to make any further alteration in his painful arrangements.)\n\n‘Thank you. I will call there now. Good morning.’ Young Barnacle seemed discomfited at this, as not having at all expected him to go.\n\n‘You are quite sure,’ said Barnacle junior, calling after him when he got to the door, unwilling wholly to relinquish the bright business idea he had conceived; ‘that it’s nothing about Tonnage?’\n\n‘Quite sure.’\n\nWith such assurance, and rather wondering what might have taken place if it _had_ been anything about tonnage, Mr Clennam withdrew to pursue his inquiries.\n\nMews Street, Grosvenor Square, was not absolutely Grosvenor Square itself, but it was very near it. It was a hideous little street of dead wall, stables, and dunghills, with lofts over coach-houses inhabited by coachmen’s families, who had a passion for drying clothes and decorating their window-sills with miniature turnpike-gates. The principal chimney-sweep of that fashionable quarter lived at the blind end of Mews Street; and the same corner contained an establishment much frequented about early morning and twilight for the purchase of wine-bottles and kitchen-stuff. Punch’s shows used to lean against the dead wall in Mews Street, while their proprietors were dining elsewhere; and the dogs of the neighbourhood made appointments to meet in the same locality. Yet there were two or three small airless houses at the entrance end of Mews Street, which went at enormous rents on account of their being abject hangers-on to a fashionable situation; and whenever one of these fearful little coops was to be let (which seldom happened, for they were in great request), the house agent advertised it as a gentlemanly residence in the most aristocratic part of town, inhabited solely by the elite of the beau monde.\n\nIf a gentlemanly residence coming strictly within this narrow margin had not been essential to the blood of the Barnacles, this particular branch would have had a pretty wide selection among, let us say, ten thousand houses, offering fifty times the accommodation for a third of the money. As it was, Mr Barnacle, finding his gentlemanly residence extremely inconvenient and extremely dear, always laid it, as a public servant, at the door of the country, and adduced it as another instance of the country’s parsimony.\n\nArthur Clennam came to a squeezed house, with a ramshackle bowed front, little dingy windows, and a little dark area like a damp waistcoat-pocket, which he found to be number twenty-four, Mews Street, Grosvenor Square. To the sense of smell the house was like a sort of bottle filled with a strong distillation of Mews; and when the footman opened the door, he seemed to take the stopper out.\n\nThe footman was to the Grosvenor Square footmen, what the house was to the Grosvenor Square houses. Admirable in his way, his way was a back and a bye way. His gorgeousness was not unmixed with dirt; and both in complexion and consistency he had suffered from the closeness of his pantry. A sallow flabbiness was upon him when he took the stopper out, and presented the bottle to Mr Clennam’s nose.\n\n‘Be so good as to give that card to Mr Tite Barnacle, and to say that I have just now seen the younger Mr Barnacle, who recommended me to call here.’\n\nThe footman (who had as many large buttons with the Barnacle crest upon them on the flaps of his pockets, as if he were the family strong box, and carried the plate and jewels about with him buttoned up) pondered over the card a little; then said, ‘Walk in.’ It required some judgment to do it without butting the inner hall-door open, and in the consequent mental confusion and physical darkness slipping down the kitchen stairs. The visitor, however, brought himself up safely on the door-mat.\n\nStill the footman said ‘Walk in,’ so the visitor followed him. At the inner hall-door, another bottle seemed to be presented and another stopper taken out. This second vial appeared to be filled with concentrated provisions and extract of Sink from the pantry. After a skirmish in the narrow passage, occasioned by the footman’s opening the door of the dismal dining-room with confidence, finding some one there with consternation, and backing on the visitor with disorder, the visitor was shut up, pending his announcement, in a close back parlour. There he had an opportunity of refreshing himself with both the bottles at once, looking out at a low blinding wall three feet off, and speculating on the number of Barnacle families within the bills of mortality who lived in such hutches of their own free flunkey choice.\n\nMr Barnacle would see him. Would he walk up-stairs? He would, and he did; and in the drawing-room, with his leg on a rest, he found Mr Barnacle himself, the express image and presentment of How not to do it.\n\nMr Barnacle dated from a better time, when the country was not so parsimonious and the Circumlocution Office was not so badgered. He wound and wound folds of white cravat round his neck, as he wound and wound folds of tape and paper round the neck of the country. His wristbands and collar were oppressive; his voice and manner were oppressive. He had a large watch-chain and bunch of seals, a coat buttoned up to inconvenience, a waistcoat buttoned up to inconvenience, an unwrinkled pair of trousers, a stiff pair of boots. He was altogether splendid, massive, overpowering, and impracticable. He seemed to have been sitting for his portrait to Sir Thomas Lawrence all the days of his life.\n\n‘Mr Clennam?’ said Mr Barnacle. ‘Be seated.’\n\nMr Clennam became seated.\n\n‘You have called on me, I believe,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘at the Circumlocution--’ giving it the air of a word of about five-and-twenty syllables--‘Office.’\n\n‘I have taken that liberty.’\n\nMr Barnacle solemnly bent his head as who should say, ‘I do not deny that it is a liberty; proceed to take another liberty, and let me know your business.’\n\n‘Allow me to observe that I have been for some years in China, am quite a stranger at home, and have no personal motive or interest in the inquiry I am about to make.’\n\nMr Barnacle tapped his fingers on the table, and, as if he were now sitting for his portrait to a new and strange artist, appeared to say to his visitor, ‘If you will be good enough to take me with my present lofty expression, I shall feel obliged.’\n\n‘I have found a debtor in the Marshalsea Prison of the name of Dorrit, who has been there many years. I wish to investigate his confused affairs so far as to ascertain whether it may not be possible, after this lapse of time, to ameliorate his unhappy condition. The name of Mr Tite Barnacle has been mentioned to me as representing some highly influential interest among his creditors. Am I correctly informed?’\n\nIt being one of the principles of the Circumlocution Office never, on any account whatever, to give a straightforward answer, Mr Barnacle said, ‘Possibly.’\n\n‘On behalf of the Crown, may I ask, or as private individual?’\n\n‘The Circumlocution Department, sir,’ Mr Barnacle replied, ‘may have possibly recommended--possibly--I cannot say--that some public claim against the insolvent estate of a firm or copartnership to which this person may have belonged, should be enforced. The question may have been, in the course of official business, referred to the Circumlocution Department for its consideration. The Department may have either originated, or confirmed, a Minute making that recommendation.’\n\n‘I assume this to be the case, then.’\n\n‘The Circumlocution Department,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘is not responsible for any gentleman’s assumptions.’\n\n‘May I inquire how I can obtain official information as to the real state of the case?’\n\n‘It is competent,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘to any member of the--Public,’ mentioning that obscure body with reluctance, as his natural enemy, ‘to memorialise the Circumlocution Department. Such formalities as are required to be observed in so doing, may be known on application to the proper branch of that Department.’\n\n‘Which is the proper branch?’\n\n‘I must refer you,’ returned Mr Barnacle, ringing the bell, ‘to the Department itself for a formal answer to that inquiry.’\n\n‘Excuse my mentioning--’\n\n‘The Department is accessible to the--Public,’ Mr Barnacle was always checked a little by that word of impertinent signification, ‘if the--Public approaches it according to the official forms; if the--Public does not approach it according to the official forms, the--Public has itself to blame.’\n\nMr Barnacle made him a severe bow, as a wounded man of family, a wounded man of place, and a wounded man of a gentlemanly residence, all rolled into one; and he made Mr Barnacle a bow, and was shut out into Mews Street by the flabby footman.\n\nHaving got to this pass, he resolved as an exercise in perseverance, to betake himself again to the Circumlocution Office, and try what satisfaction he could get there. So he went back to the Circumlocution Office, and once more sent up his card to Barnacle junior by a messenger who took it very ill indeed that he should come back again, and who was eating mashed potatoes and gravy behind a partition by the hall fire.\n\nHe was readmitted to the presence of Barnacle junior, and found that young gentleman singeing his knees now, and gaping his weary way on to four o’clock.\n\n‘I say. Look here. You stick to us in a devil of a manner,’ Said Barnacle junior, looking over his shoulder.\n\n‘I want to know--’\n\n‘Look here. Upon my soul you mustn’t come into the place saying you want to know, you know,’ remonstrated Barnacle junior, turning about and putting up the eye-glass.\n\n‘I want to know,’ said Arthur Clennam, who had made up his mind to persistence in one short form of words, ‘the precise nature of the claim of the Crown against a prisoner for debt, named Dorrit.’\n\n‘I say. Look here. You really are going it at a great pace, you know. Egad, you haven’t got an appointment,’ said Barnacle junior, as if the thing were growing serious.\n\n‘I want to know,’ said Arthur, and repeated his case.\n\nBarnacle junior stared at him until his eye-glass fell out, and then put it in again and stared at him until it fell out again. ‘You have no right to come this sort of move,’ he then observed with the greatest weakness. ‘Look here. What do you mean? You told me you didn’t know whether it was public business or not.’\n\n‘I have now ascertained that it is public business,’ returned the suitor, ‘and I want to know’--and again repeated his monotonous inquiry.\n\nIts effect upon young Barnacle was to make him repeat in a defenceless way, ‘Look here! Upon my SOUL you mustn’t come into the place saying you want to know, you know!’ The effect of that upon Arthur Clennam was to make him repeat his inquiry in exactly the same words and tone as before. The effect of that upon young Barnacle was to make him a wonderful spectacle of failure and helplessness.\n\n‘Well, I tell you what. Look here. You had better try the Secretarial Department,’ he said at last, sidling to the bell and ringing it. ‘Jenkinson,’ to the mashed potatoes messenger, ‘Mr Wobbler!’\n\nArthur Clennam, who now felt that he had devoted himself to the storming of the Circumlocution Office, and must go through with it, accompanied the messenger to another floor of the building, where that functionary pointed out Mr Wobbler’s room. He entered that apartment, and found two gentlemen sitting face to face at a large and easy desk, one of whom was polishing a gun-barrel on his pocket-handkerchief, while the other was spreading marmalade on bread with a paper-knife.\n\n‘Mr Wobbler?’ inquired the suitor.\n\nBoth gentlemen glanced at him, and seemed surprised at his assurance.\n\n‘So he went,’ said the gentleman with the gun-barrel, who was an extremely deliberate speaker, ‘down to his cousin’s place, and took the Dog with him by rail. Inestimable Dog. Flew at the porter fellow when he was put into the dog-box, and flew at the guard when he was taken out. He got half-a-dozen fellows into a Barn, and a good supply of Rats, and timed the Dog. Finding the Dog able to do it immensely, made the match, and heavily backed the Dog. When the match came off, some devil of a fellow was bought over, Sir, Dog was made drunk, Dog’s master was cleaned out.’\n\n‘Mr Wobbler?’ inquired the suitor.\n\nThe gentleman who was spreading the marmalade returned, without looking up from that occupation, ‘What did he call the Dog?’\n\n‘Called him Lovely,’ said the other gentleman. ‘Said the Dog was the perfect picture of the old aunt from whom he had expectations. Found him particularly like her when hocussed.’\n\n‘Mr Wobbler?’ said the suitor.\n\nBoth gentlemen laughed for some time. The gentleman with the gun-barrel, considering it, on inspection, in a satisfactory state, referred it to the other; receiving confirmation of his views, he fitted it into its place in the case before him, and took out the stock and polished that, softly whistling.\n\n‘Mr Wobbler?’ said the suitor.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ then said Mr Wobbler, with his mouth full.\n\n‘I want to know--’ and Arthur Clennam again mechanically set forth what he wanted to know.\n\n‘Can’t inform you,’ observed Mr Wobbler, apparently to his lunch. ‘Never heard of it. Nothing at all to do with it. Better try Mr Clive, second door on the left in the next passage.’\n\n‘Perhaps he will give me the same answer.’\n\n‘Very likely. Don’t know anything about it,’ said Mr Wobbler.\n\nThe suitor turned away and had left the room, when the gentleman with the gun called out ‘Mister! Hallo!’\n\nHe looked in again.\n\n‘Shut the door after you. You’re letting in a devil of a draught here!’\n\nA few steps brought him to the second door on the left in the next passage. In that room he found three gentlemen; number one doing nothing particular, number two doing nothing particular, number three doing nothing particular. They seemed, however, to be more directly concerned than the others had been in the effective execution of the great principle of the office, as there was an awful inner apartment with a double door, in which the Circumlocution Sages appeared to be assembled in council, and out of which there was an imposing coming of papers, and into which there was an imposing going of papers, almost constantly; wherein another gentleman, number four, was the active instrument.\n\n‘I want to know,’ said Arthur Clennam,--and again stated his case in the same barrel-organ way. As number one referred him to number two, and as number two referred him to number three, he had occasion to state it three times before they all referred him to number four, to whom he stated it again.\n\nNumber four was a vivacious, well-looking, well-dressed, agreeable young fellow--he was a Barnacle, but on the more sprightly side of the family--and he said in an easy way, ‘Oh! you had better not bother yourself about it, I think.’\n\n‘Not bother myself about it?’\n\n‘No! I recommend you not to bother yourself about it.’\n\nThis was such a new point of view that Arthur Clennam found himself at a loss how to receive it.\n\n‘You can if you like. I can give you plenty of forms to fill up. Lots of ‘em here. You can have a dozen if you like. But you’ll never go on with it,’ said number four.\n\n‘Would it be such hopeless work? Excuse me; I am a stranger in England.’\n\n‘_I_ don’t say it would be hopeless,’ returned number four, with a frank smile. ‘I don’t express an opinion about that; I only express an opinion about you. _I_ don’t think you’d go on with it. However, of course, you can do as you like. I suppose there was a failure in the performance of a contract, or something of that kind, was there?’\n\n‘I really don’t know.’\n\n‘Well! That you can find out. Then you’ll find out what Department the contract was in, and then you’ll find out all about it there.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon. How shall I find out?’\n\n‘Why, you’ll--you’ll ask till they tell you. Then you’ll memorialise that Department (according to regular forms which you’ll find out) for leave to memorialise this Department. If you get it (which you may after a time), that memorial must be entered in that Department, sent to be registered in this Department, sent back to be signed by that Department, sent back to be countersigned by this Department, and then it will begin to be regularly before that Department. You’ll find out when the business passes through each of these stages by asking at both Departments till they tell you.’\n\n‘But surely this is not the way to do the business,’ Arthur Clennam could not help saying.\n\nThis airy young Barnacle was quite entertained by his simplicity in supposing for a moment that it was. This light in hand young Barnacle knew perfectly that it was not. This touch and go young Barnacle had ‘got up’ the Department in a private secretaryship, that he might be ready for any little bit of fat that came to hand; and he fully understood the Department to be a politico-diplomatic hocus pocus piece of machinery for the assistance of the nobs in keeping off the snobs. This dashing young Barnacle, in a word, was likely to become a statesman, and to make a figure.\n\n‘When the business is regularly before that Department, whatever it is,’ pursued this bright young Barnacle, ‘then you can watch it from time to time through that Department. When it comes regularly before this Department, then you must watch it from time to time through this Department. We shall have to refer it right and left; and when we refer it anywhere, then you’ll have to look it up. When it comes back to us at any time, then you had better look _us_ up. When it sticks anywhere, you’ll have to try to give it a jog. When you write to another Department about it, and then to this Department about it, and don’t hear anything satisfactory about it, why then you had better--keep on writing.’\n\nArthur Clennam looked very doubtful indeed. ‘But I am obliged to you at any rate,’ said he, ‘for your politeness.’\n\n‘Not at all,’ replied this engaging young Barnacle. ‘Try the thing, and see how you like it. It will be in your power to give it up at any time, if you don’t like it. You had better take a lot of forms away with you. Give him a lot of forms!’ With which instruction to number two, this sparkling young Barnacle took a fresh handful of papers from numbers one and three, and carried them into the sanctuary to offer to the presiding Idol of the Circumlocution Office.\n\nArthur Clennam put his forms in his pocket gloomily enough, and went his way down the long stone passage and the long stone staircase. He had come to the swing doors leading into the street, and was waiting, not over patiently, for two people who were between him and them to pass out and let him follow, when the voice of one of them struck familiarly on his ear. He looked at the speaker and recognised Mr Meagles. Mr Meagles was very red in the face--redder than travel could have made him--and collaring a short man who was with him, said, ‘come out, you rascal, come Out!’\n\nIt was such an unexpected hearing, and it was also such an unexpected sight to see Mr Meagles burst the swing doors open, and emerge into the street with the short man, who was of an unoffending appearance, that Clennam stood still for the moment exchanging looks of surprise with the porter. He followed, however, quickly; and saw Mr Meagles going down the street with his enemy at his side. He soon came up with his old travelling companion, and touched him on the back. The choleric face which Mr Meagles turned upon him smoothed when he saw who it was, and he put out his friendly hand.\n\n‘How are you?’ said Mr Meagles. ‘How d’ye _do?_ I have only just come over from abroad. I am glad to see you.’\n\n‘And I am rejoiced to see you.’\n\n‘Thank’ee. Thank’ee!’\n\n‘Mrs Meagles and your daughter--?’\n\n‘Are as well as possible,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘I only wish you had come upon me in a more prepossessing condition as to coolness.’\n\nThough it was anything but a hot day, Mr Meagles was in a heated state that attracted the attention of the passersby; more particularly as he leaned his back against a railing, took off his hat and cravat, and heartily rubbed his steaming head and face, and his reddened ears and neck, without the least regard for public opinion.\n\n‘Whew!’ said Mr Meagles, dressing again. ‘That’s comfortable. Now I am cooler.’\n\n‘You have been ruffled, Mr Meagles. What is the matter?’\n\n‘Wait a bit, and I’ll tell you. Have you leisure for a turn in the Park?’\n\n‘As much as you please.’\n\n‘Come along then. Ah! you may well look at him.’ He happened to have turned his eyes towards the offender whom Mr Meagles had so angrily collared. ‘He’s something to look at, that fellow is.’\n\nHe was not much to look at, either in point of size or in point of dress; being merely a short, square, practical looking man, whose hair had turned grey, and in whose face and forehead there were deep lines of cogitation, which looked as though they were carved in hard wood. He was dressed in decent black, a little rusty, and had the appearance of a sagacious master in some handicraft. He had a spectacle-case in his hand, which he turned over and over while he was thus in question, with a certain free use of the thumb that is never seen but in a hand accustomed to tools.\n\n‘You keep with us,’ said Mr Meagles, in a threatening kind of Way, ‘and I’ll introduce you presently. Now then!’\n\nClennam wondered within himself, as they took the nearest way to the Park, what this unknown (who complied in the gentlest manner) could have been doing. His appearance did not at all justify the suspicion that he had been detected in designs on Mr Meagles’s pocket-handkerchief; nor had he any appearance of being quarrelsome or violent. He was a quiet, plain, steady man; made no attempt to escape; and seemed a little depressed, but neither ashamed nor repentant. If he were a criminal offender, he must surely be an incorrigible hypocrite; and if he were no offender, why should Mr Meagles have collared him in the Circumlocution Office? He perceived that the man was not a difficulty in his own mind alone, but in Mr Meagles’s too; for such conversation as they had together on the short way to the Park was by no means well sustained, and Mr Meagles’s eye always wandered back to the man, even when he spoke of something very different.\n\nAt length they being among the trees, Mr Meagles stopped short, and said:\n\n‘Mr Clennam, will you do me the favour to look at this man? His name is Doyce, Daniel Doyce. You wouldn’t suppose this man to be a notorious rascal; would you?’\n\n‘I certainly should not.’ It was really a disconcerting question, with the man there.\n\n‘No. You would not. I know you would not. You wouldn’t suppose him to be a public offender; would you?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘No. But he is. He is a public offender. What has he been guilty of? Murder, manslaughter, arson, forgery, swindling, house-breaking, highway robbery, larceny, conspiracy, fraud? Which should you say, now?’\n\n‘I should say,’ returned Arthur Clennam, observing a faint smile in Daniel Doyce’s face, ‘not one of them.’\n\n‘You are right,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘But he has been ingenious, and he has been trying to turn his ingenuity to his country’s service. That makes him a public offender directly, sir.’\n\nArthur looked at the man himself, who only shook his head.\n\n‘This Doyce,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘is a smith and engineer. He is not in a large way, but he is well known as a very ingenious man. A dozen years ago, he perfects an invention (involving a very curious secret process) of great importance to his country and his fellow-creatures. I won’t say how much money it cost him, or how many years of his life he had been about it, but he brought it to perfection a dozen years ago. Wasn’t it a dozen?’ said Mr Meagles, addressing Doyce. ‘He is the most exasperating man in the world; he never complains!’\n\n‘Yes. Rather better than twelve years ago.’\n\n‘Rather better?’ said Mr Meagles, ‘you mean rather worse. Well, Mr Clennam, he addresses himself to the Government. The moment he addresses himself to the Government, he becomes a public offender! Sir,’ said Mr Meagles, in danger of making himself excessively hot again, ‘he ceases to be an innocent citizen, and becomes a culprit. He is treated from that instant as a man who has done some infernal action. He is a man to be shirked, put off, brow-beaten, sneered at, handed over by this highly-connected young or old gentleman, to that highly-connected young or old gentleman, and dodged back again; he is a man with no rights in his own time, or his own property; a mere outlaw, whom it is justifiable to get rid of anyhow; a man to be worn out by all possible means.’\n\nIt was not so difficult to believe, after the morning’s experience, as Mr Meagles supposed.\n\n‘Don’t stand there, Doyce, turning your spectacle-case over and over,’ cried Mr Meagles, ‘but tell Mr Clennam what you confessed to me.’\n\n‘I undoubtedly was made to feel,’ said the inventor, ‘as if I had committed an offence. In dancing attendance at the various offices, I was always treated, more or less, as if it was a very bad offence. I have frequently found it necessary to reflect, for my own self-support, that I really had not done anything to bring myself into the Newgate Calendar, but only wanted to effect a great saving and a great improvement.’\n\n‘There!’ said Mr Meagles. ‘Judge whether I exaggerate. Now you’ll be able to believe me when I tell you the rest of the case.’\n\nWith this prelude, Mr Meagles went through the narrative; the established narrative, which has become tiresome; the matter-of-course narrative which we all know by heart. How, after interminable attendance and correspondence, after infinite impertinences, ignorances, and insults, my lords made a Minute, number three thousand four hundred and seventy-two, allowing the culprit to make certain trials of his invention at his own expense. How the trials were made in the presence of a board of six, of whom two ancient members were too blind to see it, two other ancient members were too deaf to hear it, one other ancient member was too lame to get near it, and the final ancient member was too pig-headed to look at it. How there were more years; more impertinences, ignorances, and insults. How my lords then made a Minute, number five thousand one hundred and three, whereby they resigned the business to the Circumlocution Office. How the Circumlocution Office, in course of time, took up the business as if it were a bran new thing of yesterday, which had never been heard of before; muddled the business, addled the business, tossed the business in a wet blanket. How the impertinences, ignorances, and insults went through the multiplication table. How there was a reference of the invention to three Barnacles and a Stiltstalking, who knew nothing about it; into whose heads nothing could be hammered about it; who got bored about it, and reported physical impossibilities about it. How the Circumlocution Office, in a Minute, number eight thousand seven hundred and forty, ‘saw no reason to reverse the decision at which my lords had arrived.’ How the Circumlocution Office, being reminded that my lords had arrived at no decision, shelved the business. How there had been a final interview with the head of the Circumlocution Office that very morning, and how the Brazen Head had spoken, and had been, upon the whole, and under all the circumstances, and looking at it from the various points of view, of opinion that one of two courses was to be pursued in respect of the business: that was to say, either to leave it alone for evermore, or to begin it all over again.\n\n‘Upon which,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘as a practical man, I then and there, in that presence, took Doyce by the collar, and told him it was plain to me that he was an infamous rascal and treasonable disturber of the government peace, and took him away. I brought him out of the office door by the collar, that the very porter might know I was a practical man who appreciated the official estimate of such characters; and here we are!’\n\nIf that airy young Barnacle had been there, he would have frankly told them perhaps that the Circumlocution Office had achieved its function. That what the Barnacles had to do, was to stick on to the national ship as long as they could. That to trim the ship, lighten the ship, clean the ship, would be to knock them off; that they could but be knocked off once; and that if the ship went down with them yet sticking to it, that was the ship’s look out, and not theirs.\n\n‘There!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘now you know all about Doyce. Except, which I own does not improve my state of mind, that even now you don’t hear him complain.’\n\n‘You must have great patience,’ said Arthur Clennam, looking at him with some wonder, ‘great forbearance.’\n\n‘No,’ he returned, ‘I don’t know that I have more than another man.’\n\n‘By the Lord, you have more than I have, though!’ cried Mr Meagles.\n\nDoyce smiled, as he said to Clennam, ‘You see, my experience of these things does not begin with myself. It has been in my way to know a little about them from time to time. Mine is not a particular case. I am not worse used than a hundred others who have put themselves in the same position--than all the others, I was going to say.’\n\n‘I don’t know that I should find that a consolation, if it were my case; but I am very glad that you do.’\n\n‘Understand me! I don’t say,’ he replied in his steady, planning way, and looking into the distance before him as if his grey eye were measuring it, ‘that it’s recompense for a man’s toil and hope; but it’s a certain sort of relief to know that I might have counted on this.’\n\nHe spoke in that quiet deliberate manner, and in that undertone, which is often observable in mechanics who consider and adjust with great nicety. It belonged to him like his suppleness of thumb, or his peculiar way of tilting up his hat at the back every now and then, as if he were contemplating some half-finished work of his hand and thinking about it.\n\n‘Disappointed?’ he went on, as he walked between them under the trees. ‘Yes. No doubt I am disappointed. Hurt? Yes. No doubt I am hurt. That’s only natural. But what I mean when I say that people who put themselves in the same position are mostly used in the same way--’\n\n‘In England,’ said Mr Meagles.\n\n‘Oh! of course I mean in England. When they take their inventions into foreign countries, that’s quite different. And that’s the reason why so many go there.’\n\nMr Meagles very hot indeed again.\n\n‘What I mean is, that however this comes to be the regular way of our government, it is its regular way. Have you ever heard of any projector or inventor who failed to find it all but inaccessible, and whom it did not discourage and ill-treat?’\n\n‘I cannot say that I ever have.’\n\n‘Have you ever known it to be beforehand in the adoption of any useful thing? Ever known it to set an example of any useful kind?’\n\n‘I am a good deal older than my friend here,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘and I’ll answer that. Never.’\n\n‘But we all three have known, I expect,’ said the inventor, ‘a pretty many cases of its fixed determination to be miles upon miles, and years upon years, behind the rest of us; and of its being found out persisting in the use of things long superseded, even after the better things were well known and generally taken up?’\n\nThey all agreed upon that.\n\n‘Well then,’ said Doyce, with a sigh, ‘as I know what such a metal will do at such a temperature, and such a body under such a pressure, so I may know (if I will only consider), how these great lords and gentlemen will certainly deal with such a matter as mine. I have no right to be surprised, with a head upon my shoulders, and memory in it, that I fall into the ranks with all who came before me. I ought to have let it alone. I have had warning enough, I am sure.’\n\nWith that he put up his spectacle-case, and said to Arthur, ‘If I don’t complain, Mr Clennam, I can feel gratitude; and I assure you that I feel it towards our mutual friend. Many’s the day, and many’s the way in which he has backed me.’\n\n‘Stuff and nonsense,’ said Mr Meagles.\n\nArthur could not but glance at Daniel Doyce in the ensuing silence. Though it was evidently in the grain of his character, and of his respect for his own case, that he should abstain from idle murmuring, it was evident that he had grown the older, the sterner, and the poorer, for his long endeavour. He could not but think what a blessed thing it would have been for this man, if he had taken a lesson from the gentlemen who were so kind as to take a nation’s affairs in charge, and had learnt How not to do it.\n\nMr Meagles was hot and despondent for about five minutes, and then began to cool and clear up.\n\n‘Come, come!’ said he. ‘We shall not make this the better by being grim. Where do you think of going, Dan?’\n\n‘I shall go back to the factory,’ said Dan.\n\n‘Why then, we’ll all go back to the factory, or walk in that direction,’ returned Mr Meagles cheerfully. ‘Mr Clennam won’t be deterred by its being in Bleeding Heart Yard.’\n\n‘Bleeding Heart Yard?’ said Clennam. ‘I want to go there.’\n\n‘So much the better,’ cried Mr Meagles. ‘Come along!’\n\nAs they went along, certainly one of the party, and probably more than one, thought that Bleeding Heart Yard was no inappropriate destination for a man who had been in official correspondence with my lords and the Barnacles--and perhaps had a misgiving also that Britannia herself might come to look for lodgings in Bleeding Heart Yard some ugly day or other, if she over-did the Circumlocution Office.\n\n# CHAPTER 11. Let Loose\n\nA late, dull autumn night was closing in upon the river Saone. The stream, like a sullied looking-glass in a gloomy place, reflected the clouds heavily; and the low banks leaned over here and there, as if they were half curious, and half afraid, to see their darkening pictures in the water. The flat expanse of country about Chalons lay a long heavy streak, occasionally made a little ragged by a row of poplar trees against the wrathful sunset. On the banks of the river Saone it was wet, depressing, solitary; and the night deepened fast.\n\nOne man slowly moving on towards Chalons was the only visible figure in the landscape. Cain might have looked as lonely and avoided. With an old sheepskin knapsack at his back, and a rough, unbarked stick cut out of some wood in his hand; miry, footsore, his shoes and gaiters trodden out, his hair and beard untrimmed; the cloak he carried over his shoulder, and the clothes he wore, sodden with wet; limping along in pain and difficulty; he looked as if the clouds were hurrying from him, as if the wail of the wind and the shuddering of the grass were directed against him, as if the low mysterious plashing of the water murmured at him, as if the fitful autumn night were disturbed by him.\n\nHe glanced here, and he glanced there, sullenly but shrinkingly; and sometimes stopped and turned about, and looked all round him. Then he limped on again, toiling and muttering.\n\n‘To the devil with this plain that has no end! To the devil with these stones that cut like knives! To the devil with this dismal darkness, wrapping itself about one with a chill! I hate you!’\n\nAnd he would have visited his hatred upon it all with the scowl he threw about him, if he could. He trudged a little further; and looking into the distance before him, stopped again.\n\n‘I, hungry, thirsty, weary. You, imbeciles, where the lights are yonder, eating and drinking, and warming yourselves at fires! I wish I had the sacking of your town; I would repay you, my children!’\n\nBut the teeth he set at the town, and the hand he shook at the town, brought the town no nearer; and the man was yet hungrier, and thirstier, and wearier, when his feet were on its jagged pavement, and he stood looking about him.\n\nThere was the hotel with its gateway, and its savoury smell of cooking; there was the cafe with its bright windows, and its rattling of dominoes; there was the dyer’s with its strips of red cloth on the doorposts; there was the silversmith’s with its earrings, and its offerings for altars; there was the tobacco dealer’s with its lively group of soldier customers coming out pipe in mouth; there were the bad odours of the town, and the rain and the refuse in the kennels, and the faint lamps slung across the road, and the huge Diligence, and its mountain of luggage, and its six grey horses with their tails tied up, getting under weigh at the coach office. But no small cabaret for a straitened traveller being within sight, he had to seek one round the dark corner, where the cabbage leaves lay thickest, trodden about the public cistern at which women had not yet left off drawing water. There, in the back street he found one, the Break of Day. The curtained windows clouded the Break of Day, but it seemed light and warm, and it announced in legible inscriptions with appropriate pictorial embellishment of billiard cue and ball, that at the Break of Day one could play billiards; that there one could find meat, drink, and lodgings, whether one came on horseback, or came on foot; and that it kept good wines, liqueurs, and brandy. The man turned the handle of the Break of Day door, and limped in.\n\nHe touched his discoloured slouched hat, as he came in at the door, to a few men who occupied the room. Two were playing dominoes at one of the little tables; three or four were seated round the stove, conversing as they smoked; the billiard-table in the centre was left alone for the time; the landlady of the Daybreak sat behind her little counter among her cloudy bottles of syrups, baskets of cakes, and leaden drainage for glasses, working at her needle.\n\nMaking his way to an empty little table in a corner of the room behind the stove, he put down his knapsack and his cloak upon the ground. As he raised his head from stooping to do so, he found the landlady beside him.\n\n‘One can lodge here to-night, madame?’\n\n‘Perfectly!’ said the landlady in a high, sing-song, cheery voice.\n\n‘Good. One can dine--sup--what you please to call it?’\n\n‘Ah, perfectly!’ cried the landlady as before.\n\n‘Dispatch then, madame, if you please. Something to eat, as quickly as you can; and some wine at once. I am exhausted.’\n\n‘It is very bad weather, monsieur,’ said the landlady.\n\n‘Cursed weather.’\n\n‘And a very long road.’\n\n‘A cursed road.’\n\nHis hoarse voice failed him, and he rested his head upon his hands until a bottle of wine was brought from the counter. Having filled and emptied his little tumbler twice, and having broken off an end from the great loaf that was set before him with his cloth and napkin, soup-plate, salt, pepper, and oil, he rested his back against the corner of the wall, made a couch of the bench on which he sat, and began to chew crust, until such time as his repast should be ready.\n\nThere had been that momentary interruption of the talk about the stove, and that temporary inattention to and distraction from one another, which is usually inseparable in such a company from the arrival of a stranger. It had passed over by this time; and the men had done glancing at him, and were talking again.\n\n‘That’s the true reason,’ said one of them, bringing a story he had been telling, to a close, ‘that’s the true reason why they said that the devil was let loose.’ The speaker was the tall Swiss belonging to the church, and he brought something of the authority of the church into the discussion--especially as the devil was in question.\n\nThe landlady having given her directions for the new guest’s entertainment to her husband, who acted as cook to the Break of Day, had resumed her needlework behind her counter. She was a smart, neat, bright little woman, with a good deal of cap and a good deal of stocking, and she struck into the conversation with several laughing nods of her head, but without looking up from her work.\n\n‘Ah Heaven, then,’ said she. ‘When the boat came up from Lyons, and brought the news that the devil was actually let loose at Marseilles, some fly-catchers swallowed it. But I? No, not I.’\n\n‘Madame, you are always right,’ returned the tall Swiss. ‘Doubtless you were enraged against that man, madame?’\n\n‘Ay, yes, then!’ cried the landlady, raising her eyes from her work, opening them very wide, and tossing her head on one side. ‘Naturally, yes.’\n\n‘He was a bad subject.’\n\n‘He was a wicked wretch,’ said the landlady, ‘and well merited what he had the good fortune to escape. So much the worse.’\n\n‘Stay, madame! Let us see,’ returned the Swiss, argumentatively turning his cigar between his lips. ‘It may have been his unfortunate destiny. He may have been the child of circumstances. It is always possible that he had, and has, good in him if one did but know how to find it out. Philosophical philanthropy teaches--’\n\nThe rest of the little knot about the stove murmured an objection to the introduction of that threatening expression. Even the two players at dominoes glanced up from their game, as if to protest against philosophical philanthropy being brought by name into the Break of Day.\n\n‘Hold there, you and your philanthropy,’ cried the smiling landlady, nodding her head more than ever. ‘Listen then. I am a woman, I. I know nothing of philosophical philanthropy. But I know what I have seen, and what I have looked in the face in this world here, where I find myself. And I tell you this, my friend, that there are people (men and women both, unfortunately) who have no good in them--none. That there are people whom it is necessary to detest without compromise. That there are people who must be dealt with as enemies of the human race. That there are people who have no human heart, and who must be crushed like savage beasts and cleared out of the way. They are but few, I hope; but I have seen (in this world here where I find myself, and even at the little Break of Day) that there are such people. And I do not doubt that this man--whatever they call him, I forget his name--is one of them.’\n\nThe landlady’s lively speech was received with greater favour at the Break of Day, than it would have elicited from certain amiable whitewashers of the class she so unreasonably objected to, nearer Great Britain.\n\n‘My faith! If your philosophical philanthropy,’ said the landlady, putting down her work, and rising to take the stranger’s soup from her husband, who appeared with it at a side door, ‘puts anybody at the mercy of such people by holding terms with them at all, in words or deeds, or both, take it away from the Break of Day, for it isn’t worth a sou.’\n\nAs she placed the soup before the guest, who changed his attitude to a sitting one, he looked her full in the face, and his moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache.\n\n‘Well!’ said the previous speaker, ‘let us come back to our subject. Leaving all that aside, gentlemen, it was because the man was acquitted on his trial that people said at Marseilles that the devil was let loose. That was how the phrase began to circulate, and what it meant; nothing more.’\n\n‘How do they call him?’ said the landlady. ‘Biraud, is it not?’\n\n‘Rigaud, madame,’ returned the tall Swiss.\n\n‘Rigaud! To be sure.’\n\nThe traveller’s soup was succeeded by a dish of meat, and that by a dish of vegetables. He ate all that was placed before him, emptied his bottle of wine, called for a glass of rum, and smoked his cigarette with his cup of coffee. As he became refreshed, he became overbearing; and patronised the company at the Daybreak in certain small talk at which he assisted, as if his condition were far above his appearance.\n\nThe company might have had other engagements, or they might have felt their inferiority, but in any case they dispersed by degrees, and not being replaced by other company, left their new patron in possession of the Break of Day. The landlord was clinking about in his kitchen; the landlady was quiet at her work; and the refreshed traveller sat smoking by the stove, warming his ragged feet.\n\n‘Pardon me, madame--that Biraud.’\n\n‘Rigaud, monsieur.’\n\n‘Rigaud. Pardon me again--has contracted your displeasure, how?’\n\nThe landlady, who had been at one moment thinking within herself that this was a handsome man, at another moment that this was an ill-looking man, observed the nose coming down and the moustache going up, and strongly inclined to the latter decision. Rigaud was a criminal, she said, who had killed his wife.\n\n‘Ay, ay? Death of my life, that’s a criminal indeed. But how do you know it?’\n\n‘All the world knows it.’\n\n‘Hah! And yet he escaped justice?’\n\n‘Monsieur, the law could not prove it against him to its satisfaction. So the law says. Nevertheless, all the world knows he did it. The people knew it so well, that they tried to tear him to pieces.’\n\n‘Being all in perfect accord with their own wives?’ said the guest. ‘Haha!’\n\nThe landlady of the Break of Day looked at him again, and felt almost confirmed in her last decision. He had a fine hand, though, and he turned it with a great show. She began once more to think that he was not ill-looking after all.\n\n‘Did you mention, madame--or was it mentioned among the gentlemen--what became of him?’\n\nThe landlady shook her head; it being the first conversational stage at which her vivacious earnestness had ceased to nod it, keeping time to what she said. It had been mentioned at the Daybreak, she remarked, on the authority of the journals, that he had been kept in prison for his own safety. However that might be, he had escaped his deserts; so much the worse.\n\nThe guest sat looking at her as he smoked out his final cigarette, and as she sat with her head bent over her work, with an expression that might have resolved her doubts, and brought her to a lasting conclusion on the subject of his good or bad looks if she had seen it. When she did look up, the expression was not there. The hand was smoothing his shaggy moustache.\n\n‘May one ask to be shown to bed, madame?’\n\nVery willingly, monsieur. Hola, my husband! My husband would conduct him up-stairs. There was one traveller there, asleep, who had gone to bed very early indeed, being overpowered by fatigue; but it was a large chamber with two beds in it, and space enough for twenty. This the landlady of the Break of Day chirpingly explained, calling between whiles, ‘Hola, my husband!’ out at the side door.\n\nMy husband answered at length, ‘It is I, my wife!’ and presenting himself in his cook’s cap, lighted the traveller up a steep and narrow staircase; the traveller carrying his own cloak and knapsack, and bidding the landlady good night with a complimentary reference to the pleasure of seeing her again to-morrow. It was a large room, with a rough splintery floor, unplastered rafters overhead, and two bedsteads on opposite sides. Here ‘my husband’ put down the candle he carried, and with a sidelong look at his guest stooping over his knapsack, gruffly gave him the instruction, ‘The bed to the right!’ and left him to his repose. The landlord, whether he was a good or a bad physiognomist, had fully made up his mind that the guest was an ill-looking fellow.\n\nThe guest looked contemptuously at the clean coarse bedding prepared for him, and, sitting down on the rush chair at the bedside, drew his money out of his pocket, and told it over in his hand. ‘One must eat,’ he muttered to himself, ‘but by Heaven I must eat at the cost of some other man to-morrow!’\n\nAs he sat pondering, and mechanically weighing his money in his palm, the deep breathing of the traveller in the other bed fell so regularly upon his hearing that it attracted his eyes in that direction. The man was covered up warm, and had drawn the white curtain at his head, so that he could be only heard, not seen. But the deep regular breathing, still going on while the other was taking off his worn shoes and gaiters, and still continuing when he had laid aside his coat and cravat, became at length a strong provocative to curiosity, and incentive to get a glimpse of the sleeper’s face.\n\nThe waking traveller, therefore, stole a little nearer, and yet a little nearer, and a little nearer to the sleeping traveller’s bed, until he stood close beside it. Even then he could not see his face, for he had drawn the sheet over it. The regular breathing still continuing, he put his smooth white hand (such a treacherous hand it looked, as it went creeping from him!) to the sheet, and gently lifted it away.\n\n‘Death of my soul!’ he whispered, falling back, ‘here’s Cavalletto!’\n\nThe little Italian, previously influenced in his sleep, perhaps, by the stealthy presence at his bedside, stopped in his regular breathing, and with a long deep respiration opened his eyes. At first they were not awake, though open. He lay for some seconds looking placidly at his old prison companion, and then, all at once, with a cry of surprise and alarm, sprang out of bed.\n\n‘Hush! What’s the matter? Keep quiet! It’s I. You know me?’ cried the other, in a suppressed voice.\n\nBut John Baptist, widely staring, muttering a number of invocations and ejaculations, tremblingly backing into a corner, slipping on his trousers, and tying his coat by the two sleeves round his neck, manifested an unmistakable desire to escape by the door rather than renew the acquaintance. Seeing this, his old prison comrade fell back upon the door, and set his shoulders against it.\n\n‘Cavalletto! Wake, boy! Rub your eyes and look at me. Not the name you used to call me--don’t use that--Lagnier, say Lagnier!’\n\nJohn Baptist, staring at him with eyes opened to their utmost width, made a number of those national, backhanded shakes of the right forefinger in the air, as if he were resolved on negativing beforehand everything that the other could possibly advance during the whole term of his life.\n\n‘Cavalletto! Give me your hand. You know Lagnier, the gentleman. Touch the hand of a gentleman!’\n\nSubmitting himself to the old tone of condescending authority, John Baptist, not at all steady on his legs as yet, advanced and put his hand in his patron’s. Monsieur Lagnier laughed; and having given it a squeeze, tossed it up and let it go.\n\n‘Then you were--’ faltered John Baptist.\n\n‘Not shaved? No. See here!’ cried Lagnier, giving his head a twirl; ‘as tight on as your own.’\n\nJohn Baptist, with a slight shiver, looked all round the room as if to recall where he was. His patron took that opportunity of turning the key in the door, and then sat down upon his bed.\n\n‘Look!’ he said, holding up his shoes and gaiters. ‘That’s a poor trim for a gentleman, you’ll say. No matter, you shall see how soon I’ll mend it. Come and sit down. Take your old place!’\n\nJohn Baptist, looking anything but reassured, sat down on the floor at the bedside, keeping his eyes upon his patron all the time.\n\n‘That’s well!’ cried Lagnier. ‘Now we might be in the old infernal hole again, hey? How long have you been out?’\n\n‘Two days after you, my master.’\n\n‘How do you come here?’\n\n‘I was cautioned not to stay there, and so I left the town at once, and since then I have changed about. I have been doing odds and ends at Avignon, at Pont Esprit, at Lyons; upon the Rhone, upon the Saone.’ As he spoke, he rapidly mapped the places out with his sunburnt hand upon the floor.\n\n‘And where are you going?’\n\n‘Going, my master?’\n\n‘Ay!’\n\nJohn Baptist seemed to desire to evade the question without knowing how. ‘By Bacchus!’ he said at last, as if he were forced to the admission, ‘I have sometimes had a thought of going to Paris, and perhaps to England.’\n\n‘Cavalletto. This is in confidence. I also am going to Paris and perhaps to England. We’ll go together.’\n\nThe little man nodded his head, and showed his teeth; and yet seemed not quite convinced that it was a surpassingly desirable arrangement.\n\n‘We’ll go together,’ repeated Lagnier. ‘You shall see how soon I will force myself to be recognised as a gentleman, and you shall profit by it. It is agreed? Are we one?’\n\n‘Oh, surely, surely!’ said the little man.\n\n‘Then you shall hear before I sleep--and in six words, for I want sleep--how I appear before you, I, Lagnier. Remember that. Not the other.’\n\n‘Altro, altro! Not Ri----’ Before John Baptist could finish the name, his comrade had got his hand under his chin and fiercely shut up his mouth.\n\n‘Death! what are you doing? Do you want me to be trampled upon and stoned? Do _you_ want to be trampled upon and stoned? You would be. You don’t imagine that they would set upon me, and let my prison chum go? Don’t think it!’\n\nThere was an expression in his face as he released his grip of his friend’s jaw, from which his friend inferred that if the course of events really came to any stoning and trampling, Monsieur Lagnier would so distinguish him with his notice as to ensure his having his full share of it. He remembered what a cosmopolitan gentleman Monsieur Lagnier was, and how few weak distinctions he made.\n\n‘I am a man,’ said Monsieur Lagnier, ‘whom society has deeply wronged since you last saw me. You know that I am sensitive and brave, and that it is my character to govern. How has society respected those qualities in me? I have been shrieked at through the streets. I have been guarded through the streets against men, and especially women, running at me armed with any weapons they could lay their hands on. I have lain in prison for security, with the place of my confinement kept a secret, lest I should be torn out of it and felled by a hundred blows. I have been carted out of Marseilles in the dead of night, and carried leagues away from it packed in straw. It has not been safe for me to go near my house; and, with a beggar’s pittance in my pocket, I have walked through vile mud and weather ever since, until my feet are crippled--look at them! Such are the humiliations that society has inflicted upon me, possessing the qualities I have mentioned, and which you know me to possess. But society shall pay for it.’\n\nAll this he said in his companion’s ear, and with his hand before his lips.\n\n‘Even here,’ he went on in the same way, ‘even in this mean drinking-shop, society pursues me. Madame defames me, and her guests defame me. I, too, a gentleman with manners and accomplishments to strike them dead! But the wrongs society has heaped upon me are treasured in this breast.’\n\nTo all of which John Baptist, listening attentively to the suppressed hoarse voice, said from time to time, ‘Surely, surely!’ tossing his head and shutting his eyes, as if there were the clearest case against society that perfect candour could make out.\n\n‘Put my shoes there,’ continued Lagnier. ‘Hang my cloak to dry there by the door. Take my hat.’ He obeyed each instruction, as it was given. ‘And this is the bed to which society consigns me, is it? Hah. _Very_ well!’\n\nAs he stretched out his length upon it, with a ragged handkerchief bound round his wicked head, and only his wicked head showing above the bedclothes, John Baptist was rather strongly reminded of what had so very nearly happened to prevent the moustache from any more going up as it did, and the nose from any more coming down as it did.\n\n‘Shaken out of destiny’s dice-box again into your company, eh? By Heaven! So much the better for you. You’ll profit by it. I shall need a long rest. Let me sleep in the morning.’\n\nJohn Baptist replied that he should sleep as long as he would, and wishing him a happy night, put out the candle. One might have supposed that the next proceeding of the Italian would have been to undress; but he did exactly the reverse, and dressed himself from head to foot, saving his shoes. When he had so done, he lay down upon his bed with some of its coverings over him, and his coat still tied round his neck, to get through the night.\n\nWhen he started up, the Godfather Break of Day was peeping at its namesake. He rose, took his shoes in his hand, turned the key in the door with great caution, and crept downstairs. Nothing was astir there but the smell of coffee, wine, tobacco, and syrups; and madame’s little counter looked ghastly enough. But he had paid madame his little note at it over night, and wanted to see nobody--wanted nothing but to get on his shoes and his knapsack, open the door, and run away.\n\nHe prospered in his object. No movement or voice was heard when he opened the door; no wicked head tied up in a ragged handkerchief looked out of the upper window. When the sun had raised his full disc above the flat line of the horizon, and was striking fire out of the long muddy vista of paved road with its weary avenue of little trees, a black speck moved along the road and splashed among the flaming pools of rain-water, which black speck was John Baptist Cavalletto running away from his patron.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. Bleeding Heart Yard\n\nIn London itself, though in the old rustic road towards a suburb of note where in the days of William Shakespeare, author and stage-player, there were Royal hunting-seats--howbeit no sport is left there now but for hunters of men--Bleeding Heart Yard was to be found; a place much changed in feature and in fortune, yet with some relish of ancient greatness about it. Two or three mighty stacks of chimneys, and a few large dark rooms which had escaped being walled and subdivided out of the recognition of their old proportions, gave the Yard a character. It was inhabited by poor people, who set up their rest among its faded glories, as Arabs of the desert pitch their tents among the fallen stones of the Pyramids; but there was a family sentimental feeling prevalent in the Yard, that it had a character.\n\nAs if the aspiring city had become puffed up in the very ground on which it stood, the ground had so risen about Bleeding Heart Yard that you got into it down a flight of steps which formed no part of the original approach, and got out of it by a low gateway into a maze of shabby streets, which went about and about, tortuously ascending to the level again. At this end of the Yard and over the gateway, was the factory of Daniel Doyce, often heavily beating like a bleeding heart of iron, with the clink of metal upon metal.\n\nThe opinion of the Yard was divided respecting the derivation of its name. The more practical of its inmates abided by the tradition of a murder; the gentler and more imaginative inhabitants, including the whole of the tender sex, were loyal to the legend of a young lady of former times closely imprisoned in her chamber by a cruel father for remaining true to her own true love, and refusing to marry the suitor he chose for her. The legend related how that the young lady used to be seen up at her window behind the bars, murmuring a love-lorn song of which the burden was, ‘Bleeding Heart, Bleeding Heart, bleeding away,’ until she died. It was objected by the murderous party that this Refrain was notoriously the invention of a tambour-worker, a spinster and romantic, still lodging in the Yard. But, forasmuch as all favourite legends must be associated with the affections, and as many more people fall in love than commit murder--which it may be hoped, howsoever bad we are, will continue until the end of the world to be the dispensation under which we shall live--the Bleeding Heart, Bleeding Heart, bleeding away story, carried the day by a great majority. Neither party would listen to the antiquaries who delivered learned lectures in the neighbourhood, showing the Bleeding Heart to have been the heraldic cognisance of the old family to whom the property had once belonged. And, considering that the hour-glass they turned from year to year was filled with the earthiest and coarsest sand, the Bleeding Heart Yarders had reason enough for objecting to be despoiled of the one little golden grain of poetry that sparkled in it.\n\nDown in to the Yard, by way of the steps, came Daniel Doyce, Mr Meagles, and Clennam. Passing along the Yard, and between the open doors on either hand, all abundantly garnished with light children nursing heavy ones, they arrived at its opposite boundary, the gateway. Here Arthur Clennam stopped to look about him for the domicile of Plornish, plasterer, whose name, according to the custom of Londoners, Daniel Doyce had never seen or heard of to that hour.\n\nIt was plain enough, nevertheless, as Little Dorrit had said; over a lime-splashed gateway in the corner, within which Plornish kept a ladder and a barrel or two. The last house in Bleeding Heart Yard which she had described as his place of habitation, was a large house, let off to various tenants; but Plornish ingeniously hinted that he lived in the parlour, by means of a painted hand under his name, the forefinger of which hand (on which the artist had depicted a ring and a most elaborate nail of the genteelest form) referred all inquirers to that apartment.\n\nParting from his companions, after arranging another meeting with Mr Meagles, Clennam went alone into the entry, and knocked with his knuckles at the parlour-door. It was opened presently by a woman with a child in her arms, whose unoccupied hand was hastily rearranging the upper part of her dress. This was Mrs Plornish, and this maternal action was the action of Mrs Plornish during a large part of her waking existence.\n\nWas Mr Plornish at home? ‘Well, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish, a civil woman, ‘not to deceive you, he’s gone to look for a job.’\n\n‘Not to deceive you’ was a method of speech with Mrs Plornish. She would deceive you, under any circumstances, as little as might be; but she had a trick of answering in this provisional form.\n\n‘Do you think he will be back soon, if I wait for him?’\n\n‘I have been expecting him,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘this half an hour, at any minute of time. Walk in, sir.’\n\nArthur entered the rather dark and close parlour (though it was lofty too), and sat down in the chair she placed for him.\n\n‘Not to deceive you, sir, I notice it,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘and I take it kind of you.’\n\nHe was at a loss to understand what she meant; and by expressing as much in his looks, elicited her explanation.\n\n‘It ain’t many that comes into a poor place, that deems it worth their while to move their hats,’ said Mrs Plornish. ‘But people think more of it than people think.’\n\nClennam returned, with an uncomfortable feeling in so very slight a courtesy being unusual, Was that all! And stooping down to pinch the cheek of another young child who was sitting on the floor, staring at him, asked Mrs Plornish how old that fine boy was?\n\n‘Four year just turned, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish. ‘He _is_ a fine little fellow, ain’t he, sir? But this one is rather sickly.’ She tenderly hushed the baby in her arms, as she said it. ‘You wouldn’t mind my asking if it happened to be a job as you was come about, sir, would you?’ asked Mrs Plornish wistfully.\n\nShe asked it so anxiously, that if he had been in possession of any kind of tenement, he would have had it plastered a foot deep rather than answer No. But he was obliged to answer No; and he saw a shade of disappointment on her face, as she checked a sigh, and looked at the low fire. Then he saw, also, that Mrs Plornish was a young woman, made somewhat slatternly in herself and her belongings by poverty; and so dragged at by poverty and the children together, that their united forces had already dragged her face into wrinkles.\n\n‘All such things as jobs,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘seems to me to have gone underground, they do indeed.’ (Herein Mrs Plornish limited her remark to the plastering trade, and spoke without reference to the Circumlocution Office and the Barnacle Family.)\n\n‘Is it so difficult to get work?’ asked Arthur Clennam.\n\n‘Plornish finds it so,’ she returned. ‘He is quite unfortunate. Really he is.’\n\nReally he was. He was one of those many wayfarers on the road of life, who seem to be afflicted with supernatural corns, rendering it impossible for them to keep up even with their lame competitors. A willing, working, soft hearted, not hard-headed fellow, Plornish took his fortune as smoothly as could be expected; but it was a rough one. It so rarely happened that anybody seemed to want him, it was such an exceptional case when his powers were in any request, that his misty mind could not make out how it happened. He took it as it came, therefore; he tumbled into all kinds of difficulties, and tumbled out of them; and, by tumbling through life, got himself considerably bruised.\n\n‘It’s not for want of looking after jobs, I am sure,’ said Mrs Plornish, lifting up her eyebrows, and searching for a solution of the problem between the bars of the grate; ‘nor yet for want of working at them when they are to be got. No one ever heard my husband complain of work.’\n\nSomehow or other, this was the general misfortune of Bleeding Heart Yard. From time to time there were public complaints, pathetically going about, of labour being scarce--which certain people seemed to take extraordinarily ill, as though they had an absolute right to it on their own terms--but Bleeding Heart Yard, though as willing a Yard as any in Britain, was never the better for the demand. That high old family, the Barnacles, had long been too busy with their great principle to look into the matter; and indeed the matter had nothing to do with their watchfulness in out-generalling all other high old families except the Stiltstalkings.\n\nWhile Mrs Plornish spoke in these words of her absent lord, her lord returned. A smooth-cheeked, fresh-coloured, sandy-whiskered man of thirty. Long in the legs, yielding at the knees, foolish in the face, flannel-jacketed, lime-whitened.\n\n‘This is Plornish, sir.’\n\n‘I came,’ said Clennam, rising, ‘to beg the favour of a little conversation with you on the subject of the Dorrit family.’\n\nPlornish became suspicious. Seemed to scent a creditor. Said, ‘Ah, yes. Well. He didn’t know what satisfaction _he_ could give any gentleman, respecting that family. What might it be about, now?’\n\n‘I know you better,’ said Clennam, smiling, ‘than you suppose.’\n\nPlornish observed, not smiling in return, And yet he hadn’t the pleasure of being acquainted with the gentleman, neither.\n\n‘No,’ said Arthur, ‘I know your kind offices at second hand, but on the best authority; through Little Dorrit.--I mean,’ he explained, ‘Miss Dorrit.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam, is it? Oh! I’ve heard of you, Sir.’\n\n‘And I of you,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Please to sit down again, Sir, and consider yourself welcome.--Why, yes,’ said Plornish, taking a chair, and lifting the elder child upon his knee, that he might have the moral support of speaking to a stranger over his head, ‘I have been on the wrong side of the Lock myself, and in that way we come to know Miss Dorrit. Me and my wife, we are well acquainted with Miss Dorrit.’\n\n‘Intimate!’ cried Mrs Plornish. Indeed, she was so proud of the acquaintance, that she had awakened some bitterness of spirit in the Yard by magnifying to an enormous amount the sum for which Miss Dorrit’s father had become insolvent. The Bleeding Hearts resented her claiming to know people of such distinction.\n\n‘It was her father that I got acquainted with first. And through getting acquainted with him, you see--why--I got acquainted with her,’ said Plornish tautologically.\n\n‘I see.’\n\n‘Ah! And there’s manners! There’s polish! There’s a gentleman to have run to seed in the Marshalsea jail! Why, perhaps you are not aware,’ said Plornish, lowering his voice, and speaking with a perverse admiration of what he ought to have pitied or despised, ‘not aware that Miss Dorrit and her sister dursn’t let him know that they work for a living. No!’ said Plornish, looking with a ridiculous triumph first at his wife, and then all round the room. ‘Dursn’t let him know it, they dursn’t!’\n\n‘Without admiring him for that,’ Clennam quietly observed, ‘I am very sorry for him.’ The remark appeared to suggest to Plornish, for the first time, that it might not be a very fine trait of character after all. He pondered about it for a moment, and gave it up.\n\n‘As to me,’ he resumed, ‘certainly Mr Dorrit is as affable with me, I am sure, as I can possibly expect. Considering the differences and distances betwixt us, more so. But it’s Miss Dorrit that we were speaking of.’\n\n‘True. Pray how did you introduce her at my mother’s!’\n\nMr Plornish picked a bit of lime out of his whisker, put it between his lips, turned it with his tongue like a sugar-plum, considered, found himself unequal to the task of lucid explanation, and appealing to his wife, said, ‘Sally, _you_ may as well mention how it was, old woman.’\n\n‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Sally, hushing the baby from side to side, and laying her chin upon the little hand as it tried to disarrange the gown again, ‘came here one afternoon with a bit of writing, telling that how she wished for needlework, and asked if it would be considered any ill-conwenience in case she was to give her address here.’ (Plornish repeated, her address here, in a low voice, as if he were making responses at church.) ‘Me and Plornish says, No, Miss Dorrit, no ill-conwenience,’ (Plornish repeated, no ill-conwenience,) ‘and she wrote it in, according. Which then me and Plornish says, Ho Miss Dorrit!’ (Plornish repeated, Ho Miss Dorrit.) ‘Have you thought of copying it three or four times, as the way to make it known in more places than one? No, says Miss Dorrit, I have not, but I will. She copied it out according, on this table, in a sweet writing, and Plornish, he took it where he worked, having a job just then,’ (Plornish repeated job just then,) ‘and likewise to the landlord of the Yard; through which it was that Mrs Clennam first happened to employ Miss Dorrit.’ Plornish repeated, employ Miss Dorrit; and Mrs Plornish having come to an end, feigned to bite the fingers of the little hand as she kissed it.\n\n‘The landlord of the Yard,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘is--’\n\n‘He is Mr Casby, by name, he is,’ said Plornish, ‘and Pancks, he collects the rents. That,’ added Mr Plornish, dwelling on the subject with a slow thoughtfulness that appeared to have no connection with any specific object, and to lead him nowhere, ‘that is about what _they_ are, you may believe me or not, as you think proper.’\n\n‘Ay?’ returned Clennam, thoughtful in his turn. ‘Mr Casby, too! An old acquaintance of mine, long ago!’\n\nMr Plornish did not see his road to any comment on this fact, and made none. As there truly was no reason why he should have the least interest in it, Arthur Clennam went on to the present purport of his visit; namely, to make Plornish the instrument of effecting Tip’s release, with as little detriment as possible to the self-reliance and self-helpfulness of the young man, supposing him to possess any remnant of those qualities: without doubt a very wide stretch of supposition. Plornish, having been made acquainted with the cause of action from the Defendant’s own mouth, gave Arthur to understand that the Plaintiff was a ‘Chaunter’--meaning, not a singer of anthems, but a seller of horses--and that he (Plornish) considered that ten shillings in the pound ‘would settle handsome,’ and that more would be a waste of money. The Principal and instrument soon drove off together to a stable-yard in High Holborn, where a remarkably fine grey gelding, worth, at the lowest figure, seventy-five guineas (not taking into account the value of the shot he had been made to swallow for the improvement of his form), was to be parted with for a twenty-pound note, in consequence of his having run away last week with Mrs Captain Barbary of Cheltenham, who wasn’t up to a horse of his courage, and who, in mere spite, insisted on selling him for that ridiculous sum: or, in other words, on giving him away. Plornish, going up this yard alone and leaving his Principal outside, found a gentleman with tight drab legs, a rather old hat, a little hooked stick, and a blue neckerchief (Captain Maroon of Gloucestershire, a private friend of Captain Barbary); who happened to be there, in a friendly way, to mention these little circumstances concerning the remarkably fine grey gelding to any real judge of a horse and quick snapper-up of a good thing, who might look in at that address as per advertisement. This gentleman, happening also to be the Plaintiff in the Tip case, referred Mr Plornish to his solicitor, and declined to treat with Mr Plornish, or even to endure his presence in the yard, unless he appeared there with a twenty-pound note: in which case only, the gentleman would augur from appearances that he meant business, and might be induced to talk to him. On this hint, Mr Plornish retired to communicate with his Principal, and presently came back with the required credentials. Then said Captain Maroon, ‘Now, how much time do you want to make the other twenty in? Now, I’ll give you a month.’ Then said Captain Maroon, when that wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, I’ll tell what I’ll do with you. You shall get me a good bill at four months, made payable at a banking-house, for the other twenty!’ Then said Captain Maroon, when _that_ wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, come; Here’s the last I’ve got to say to you. You shall give me another ten down, and I’ll run my pen clean through it.’ Then said Captain Maroon when _that_ wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, I’ll tell you what it is, and this shuts it up; he has used me bad, but I’ll let him off for another five down and a bottle of wine; and if you mean done, say done, and if you don’t like it, leave it.’ Finally said Captain Maroon, when _that_ wouldn’t suit either, ‘Hand over, then!’--And in consideration of the first offer, gave a receipt in full and discharged the prisoner.\n\n‘Mr Plornish,’ said Arthur, ‘I trust to you, if you please, to keep my secret. If you will undertake to let the young man know that he is free, and to tell him that you were employed to compound for the debt by some one whom you are not at liberty to name, you will not only do me a service, but may do him one, and his sister also.’\n\n‘The last reason, sir,’ said Plornish, ‘would be quite sufficient. Your wishes shall be attended to.’\n\n‘A Friend has obtained his discharge, you can say if you please. A Friend who hopes that for his sister’s sake, if for no one else’s, he will make good use of his liberty.’\n\n‘Your wishes, sir, shall be attended to.’\n\n‘And if you will be so good, in your better knowledge of the family, as to communicate freely with me, and to point out to me any means by which you think I may be delicately and really useful to Little Dorrit, I shall feel under an obligation to you.’\n\n‘Don’t name it, sir,’ returned Plornish, ‘it’ll be ekally a pleasure an a--it’l be ekally a pleasure and a--’ Finding himself unable to balance his sentence after two efforts, Mr Plornish wisely dropped it. He took Clennam’s card and appropriate pecuniary compliment.\n\nHe was earnest to finish his commission at once, and his Principal was in the same mind. So his Principal offered to set him down at the Marshalsea Gate, and they drove in that direction over Blackfriars Bridge. On the way, Arthur elicited from his new friend a confused summary of the interior life of Bleeding Heart Yard. They was all hard up there, Mr Plornish said, uncommon hard up, to be sure. Well, he couldn’t say how it was; he didn’t know as anybody _could_ say how it was; all he know’d was, that so it was. When a man felt, on his own back and in his own belly, that poor he was, that man (Mr Plornish gave it as his decided belief) know’d well that he was poor somehow or another, and you couldn’t talk it out of him, no more than you could talk Beef into him. Then you see, some people as was better off said, and a good many such people lived pretty close up to the mark themselves if not beyond it so he’d heerd, that they was ‘improvident’ (that was the favourite word) down the Yard. For instance, if they see a man with his wife and children going to Hampton Court in a Wan, perhaps once in a year, they says, ‘Hallo! I thought you was poor, my improvident friend!’ Why, Lord, how hard it was upon a man! What was a man to do? He couldn’t go mollancholy mad, and even if he did, you wouldn’t be the better for it. In Mr Plornish’s judgment you would be the worse for it. Yet you seemed to want to make a man mollancholy mad. You was always at it--if not with your right hand, with your left. What was they a doing in the Yard? Why, take a look at ‘em and see. There was the girls and their mothers a working at their sewing, or their shoe-binding, or their trimming, or their waistcoat making, day and night and night and day, and not more than able to keep body and soul together after all--often not so much. There was people of pretty well all sorts of trades you could name, all wanting to work, and yet not able to get it. There was old people, after working all their lives, going and being shut up in the workhouse, much worse fed and lodged and treated altogether, than--Mr Plornish said manufacturers, but appeared to mean malefactors. Why, a man didn’t know where to turn himself for a crumb of comfort. As to who was to blame for it, Mr Plornish didn’t know who was to blame for it. He could tell you who suffered, but he couldn’t tell you whose fault it was. It wasn’t _his_ place to find out, and who’d mind what he said, if he did find out? He only know’d that it wasn’t put right by them what undertook that line of business, and that it didn’t come right of itself. And, in brief, his illogical opinion was, that if you couldn’t do nothing for him, you had better take nothing from him for doing of it; so far as he could make out, that was about what it come to. Thus, in a prolix, gently-growling, foolish way, did Plornish turn the tangled skein of his estate about and about, like a blind man who was trying to find some beginning or end to it; until they reached the prison gate. There, he left his Principal alone; to wonder, as he rode away, how many thousand Plornishes there might be within a day or two’s journey of the Circumlocution Office, playing sundry curious variations on the same tune, which were not known by ear in that glorious institution.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. Patriarchal\n\nThe mention of Mr Casby again revived in Clennam’s memory the smouldering embers of curiosity and interest which Mrs Flintwinch had fanned on the night of his arrival. Flora Casby had been the beloved of his boyhood; and Flora was the daughter and only child of wooden-headed old Christopher (so he was still occasionally spoken of by some irreverent spirits who had had dealings with him, and in whom familiarity had bred its proverbial result perhaps), who was reputed to be rich in weekly tenants, and to get a good quantity of blood out of the stones of several unpromising courts and alleys.\n\nAfter some days of inquiry and research, Arthur Clennam became convinced that the case of the Father of the Marshalsea was indeed a hopeless one, and sorrowfully resigned the idea of helping him to freedom again. He had no hopeful inquiry to make at present, concerning Little Dorrit either; but he argued with himself that it might--for anything he knew--it might be serviceable to the poor child, if he renewed this acquaintance. It is hardly necessary to add that beyond all doubt he would have presented himself at Mr Casby’s door, if there had been no Little Dorrit in existence; for we all know how we all deceive ourselves--that is to say, how people in general, our profounder selves excepted, deceive themselves--as to motives of action.\n\nWith a comfortable impression upon him, and quite an honest one in its way, that he was still patronising Little Dorrit in doing what had no reference to her, he found himself one afternoon at the corner of Mr Casby’s street. Mr Casby lived in a street in the Gray’s Inn Road, which had set off from that thoroughfare with the intention of running at one heat down into the valley, and up again to the top of Pentonville Hill; but which had run itself out of breath in twenty yards, and had stood still ever since. There is no such place in that part now; but it remained there for many years, looking with a baulked countenance at the wilderness patched with unfruitful gardens and pimpled with eruptive summerhouses, that it had meant to run over in no time.\n\n‘The house,’ thought Clennam, as he crossed to the door, ‘is as little changed as my mother’s, and looks almost as gloomy. But the likeness ends outside. I know its staid repose within. The smell of its jars of old rose-leaves and lavender seems to come upon me even here.’\n\nWhen his knock at the bright brass knocker of obsolete shape brought a woman-servant to the door, those faded scents in truth saluted him like wintry breath that had a faint remembrance in it of the bygone spring. He stepped into the sober, silent, air-tight house--one might have fancied it to have been stifled by Mutes in the Eastern manner--and the door, closing again, seemed to shut out sound and motion. The furniture was formal, grave, and quaker-like, but well-kept; and had as prepossessing an aspect as anything, from a human creature to a wooden stool, that is meant for much use and is preserved for little, can ever wear. There was a grave clock, ticking somewhere up the staircase; and there was a songless bird in the same direction, pecking at his cage, as if he were ticking too. The parlour-fire ticked in the grate. There was only one person on the parlour-hearth, and the loud watch in his pocket ticked audibly.\n\nThe servant-maid had ticked the two words ‘Mr Clennam’ so softly that she had not been heard; and he consequently stood, within the door she had closed, unnoticed. The figure of a man advanced in life, whose smooth grey eyebrows seemed to move to the ticking as the fire-light flickered on them, sat in an arm-chair, with his list shoes on the rug, and his thumbs slowly revolving over one another. This was old Christopher Casby--recognisable at a glance--as unchanged in twenty years and upward as his own solid furniture--as little touched by the influence of the varying seasons as the old rose-leaves and old lavender in his porcelain jars.\n\nPerhaps there never was a man, in this troublesome world, so troublesome for the imagination to picture as a boy. And yet he had changed very little in his progress through life. Confronting him, in the room in which he sat, was a boy’s portrait, which anybody seeing him would have identified as Master Christopher Casby, aged ten: though disguised with a haymaking rake, for which he had had, at any time, as much taste or use as for a diving-bell; and sitting (on one of his own legs) upon a bank of violets, moved to precocious contemplation by the spire of a village church. There was the same smooth face and forehead, the same calm blue eye, the same placid air. The shining bald head, which looked so very large because it shone so much; and the long grey hair at its sides and back, like floss silk or spun glass, which looked so very benevolent because it was never cut; were not, of course, to be seen in the boy as in the old man. Nevertheless, in the Seraphic creature with the haymaking rake, were clearly to be discerned the rudiments of the Patriarch with the list shoes.\n\nPatriarch was the name which many people delighted to give him. Various old ladies in the neighbourhood spoke of him as The Last of the Patriarchs. So grey, so slow, so quiet, so impassionate, so very bumpy in the head, Patriarch was the word for him. He had been accosted in the streets, and respectfully solicited to become a Patriarch for painters and for sculptors; with so much importunity, in sooth, that it would appear to be beyond the Fine Arts to remember the points of a Patriarch, or to invent one. Philanthropists of both sexes had asked who he was, and on being informed, ‘Old Christopher Casby, formerly Town-agent to Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle,’ had cried in a rapture of disappointment, ‘Oh! why, with that head, is he not a benefactor to his species! Oh! why, with that head, is he not a father to the orphan and a friend to the friendless!’ With that head, however, he remained old Christopher Casby, proclaimed by common report rich in house property; and with that head, he now sat in his silent parlour. Indeed it would be the height of unreason to expect him to be sitting there without that head.\n\nArthur Clennam moved to attract his attention, and the grey eyebrows turned towards him.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ said Clennam, ‘I fear you did not hear me announced?’\n\n‘No, sir, I did not. Did you wish to see me, sir?’\n\n‘I wished to pay my respects.’\n\nMr Casby seemed a feather’s weight disappointed by the last words, having perhaps prepared himself for the visitor’s wishing to pay something else. ‘Have I the pleasure, sir,’ he proceeded--‘take a chair, if you please--have I the pleasure of knowing--? Ah! truly, yes, I think I have! I believe I am not mistaken in supposing that I am acquainted with those features? I think I address a gentleman of whose return to this country I was informed by Mr Flintwinch?’\n\n‘That is your present visitor.’\n\n‘Really! Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘No other, Mr Casby.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam, I am glad to see you. How have you been since we met?’\n\nWithout thinking it worth while to explain that in the course of some quarter of a century he had experienced occasional slight fluctuations in his health and spirits, Clennam answered generally that he had never been better, or something equally to the purpose; and shook hands with the possessor of ‘that head’ as it shed its patriarchal light upon him.\n\n‘We are older, Mr Clennam,’ said Christopher Casby.\n\n‘We are--not younger,’ said Clennam. After this wise remark he felt that he was scarcely shining with brilliancy, and became aware that he was nervous.\n\n‘And your respected father,’ said Mr Casby, ‘is no more! I was grieved to hear it, Mr Clennam, I was grieved.’\n\nArthur replied in the usual way that he felt infinitely obliged to him.\n\n‘There was a time,’ said Mr Casby, ‘when your parents and myself were not on friendly terms. There was a little family misunderstanding among us. Your respected mother was rather jealous of her son, maybe; when I say her son, I mean your worthy self, your worthy self.’\n\nHis smooth face had a bloom upon it like ripe wall-fruit. What with his blooming face, and that head, and his blue eyes, he seemed to be delivering sentiments of rare wisdom and virtue. In like manner, his physiognomical expression seemed to teem with benignity. Nobody could have said where the wisdom was, or where the virtue was, or where the benignity was; but they all seemed to be somewhere about him.\n\n‘Those times, however,’ pursued Mr Casby, ‘are past and gone, past and gone. I do myself the pleasure of making a visit to your respected mother occasionally, and of admiring the fortitude and strength of mind with which she bears her trials, bears her trials.’\n\nWhen he made one of these little repetitions, sitting with his hands crossed before him, he did it with his head on one side, and a gentle smile, as if he had something in his thoughts too sweetly profound to be put into words. As if he denied himself the pleasure of uttering it, lest he should soar too high; and his meekness therefore preferred to be unmeaning.\n\n‘I have heard that you were kind enough on one of those occasions,’ said Arthur, catching at the opportunity as it drifted past him, ‘to mention Little Dorrit to my mother.’\n\n‘Little--? Dorrit? That’s the seamstress who was mentioned to me by a small tenant of mine? Yes, yes. Dorrit? That’s the name. Ah, yes, yes! You call her Little Dorrit?’\n\nNo road in that direction. Nothing came of the cross-cut. It led no further.\n\n‘My daughter Flora,’ said Mr Casby, ‘as you may have heard probably, Mr Clennam, was married and established in life, several years ago. She had the misfortune to lose her husband when she had been married a few months. She resides with me again. She will be glad to see you, if you will permit me to let her know that you are here.’\n\n‘By all means,’ returned Clennam. ‘I should have preferred the request, if your kindness had not anticipated me.’\n\nUpon this Mr Casby rose up in his list shoes, and with a slow, heavy step (he was of an elephantine build), made for the door. He had a long wide-skirted bottle-green coat on, and a bottle-green pair of trousers, and a bottle-green waistcoat. The Patriarchs were not dressed in bottle-green broadcloth, and yet his clothes looked patriarchal.\n\nHe had scarcely left the room, and allowed the ticking to become audible again, when a quick hand turned a latchkey in the house-door, opened it, and shut it. Immediately afterwards, a quick and eager short dark man came into the room with so much way upon him that he was within a foot of Clennam before he could stop.\n\n‘Halloa!’ he said.\n\nClennam saw no reason why he should not say ‘Halloa!’ too.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ said the short dark man.\n\n‘I have not heard that anything is the matter,’ returned Clennam.\n\n‘Where’s Mr Casby?’ asked the short dark man, looking about.\n\n‘He will be here directly, if you want him.’\n\n‘_I_ want him?’ said the short dark man. ‘Don’t you?’\n\nThis elicited a word or two of explanation from Clennam, during the delivery of which the short dark man held his breath and looked at him. He was dressed in black and rusty iron grey; had jet black beads of eyes; a scrubby little black chin; wiry black hair striking out from his head in prongs, like forks or hair-pins; and a complexion that was very dingy by nature, or very dirty by art, or a compound of nature and art. He had dirty hands and dirty broken nails, and looked as if he had been in the coals; he was in a perspiration, and snorted and sniffed and puffed and blew, like a little labouring steam-engine.\n\n‘Oh!’ said he, when Arthur told him how he came to be there. ‘Very well. That’s right. If he should ask for Pancks, will you be so good as to say that Pancks is come in?’ And so, with a snort and a puff, he worked out by another door.\n\nNow, in the old days at home, certain audacious doubts respecting the last of the Patriarchs, which were afloat in the air, had, by some forgotten means, come in contact with Arthur’s sensorium. He was aware of motes and specks of suspicion in the atmosphere of that time; seen through which medium, Christopher Casby was a mere Inn signpost, without any Inn--an invitation to rest and be thankful, when there was no place to put up at, and nothing whatever to be thankful for. He knew that some of these specks even represented Christopher as capable of harbouring designs in ‘that head,’ and as being a crafty impostor. Other motes there were which showed him as a heavy, selfish, drifting Booby, who, having stumbled, in the course of his unwieldy jostlings against other men, on the discovery that to get through life with ease and credit, he had but to hold his tongue, keep the bald part of his head well polished, and leave his hair alone, had had just cunning enough to seize the idea and stick to it. It was said that his being town-agent to Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle was referable, not to his having the least business capacity, but to his looking so supremely benignant that nobody could suppose the property screwed or jobbed under such a man; also, that for similar reasons he now got more money out of his own wretched lettings, unquestioned, than anybody with a less nobby and less shining crown could possibly have done. In a word, it was represented (Clennam called to mind, alone in the ticking parlour) that many people select their models, much as the painters, just now mentioned, select theirs; and that, whereas in the Royal Academy some evil old ruffian of a Dog-stealer will annually be found embodying all the cardinal virtues, on account of his eyelashes, or his chin, or his legs (thereby planting thorns of confusion in the breasts of the more observant students of nature), so, in the great social Exhibition, accessories are often accepted in lieu of the internal character.\n\nCalling these things to mind, and ranging Mr Pancks in a row with them, Arthur Clennam leaned this day to the opinion, without quite deciding on it, that the last of the Patriarchs was the drifting Booby aforesaid, with the one idea of keeping the bald part of his head highly polished: and that, much as an unwieldy ship in the Thames river may sometimes be seen heavily driving with the tide, broadside on, stern first, in its own way and in the way of everything else, though making a great show of navigation, when all of a sudden, a little coaly steam-tug will bear down upon it, take it in tow, and bustle off with it; similarly the cumbrous Patriarch had been taken in tow by the snorting Pancks, and was now following in the wake of that dingy little craft.\n\nThe return of Mr Casby with his daughter Flora, put an end to these meditations. Clennam’s eyes no sooner fell upon the subject of his old passion than it shivered and broke to pieces.\n\nMost men will be found sufficiently true to themselves to be true to an old idea. It is no proof of an inconstant mind, but exactly the opposite, when the idea will not bear close comparison with the reality, and the contrast is a fatal shock to it. Such was Clennam’s case. In his youth he had ardently loved this woman, and had heaped upon her all the locked-up wealth of his affection and imagination. That wealth had been, in his desert home, like Robinson Crusoe’s money; exchangeable with no one, lying idle in the dark to rust, until he poured it out for her. Ever since that memorable time, though he had, until the night of his arrival, as completely dismissed her from any association with his Present or Future as if she had been dead (which she might easily have been for anything he knew), he had kept the old fancy of the Past unchanged, in its old sacred place. And now, after all, the last of the Patriarchs coolly walked into the parlour, saying in effect, ‘Be good enough to throw it down and dance upon it. This is Flora.’\n\nFlora, always tall, had grown to be very broad too, and short of breath; but that was not much. Flora, whom he had left a lily, had become a peony; but that was not much. Flora, who had seemed enchanting in all she said and thought, was diffuse and silly. That was much. Flora, who had been spoiled and artless long ago, was determined to be spoiled and artless now. That was a fatal blow.\n\nThis is Flora!\n\n‘I am sure,’ giggled Flora, tossing her head with a caricature of her girlish manner, such as a mummer might have presented at her own funeral, if she had lived and died in classical antiquity, ‘I am ashamed to see Mr Clennam, I am a mere fright, I know he’ll find me fearfully changed, I am actually an old woman, it’s shocking to be found out, it’s really shocking!’\n\nHe assured her that she was just what he had expected and that time had not stood still with himself.\n\n‘Oh! But with a gentleman it’s so different and really you look so amazingly well that you have no right to say anything of the kind, while, as to me, you know--oh!’ cried Flora with a little scream, ‘I am dreadful!’\n\nThe Patriarch, apparently not yet understanding his own part in the drama under representation, glowed with vacant serenity.\n\n‘But if we talk of not having changed,’ said Flora, who, whatever she said, never once came to a full stop, ‘look at Papa, is not Papa precisely what he was when you went away, isn’t it cruel and unnatural of Papa to be such a reproach to his own child, if we go on in this way much longer people who don’t know us will begin to suppose that I am Papa’s Mama!’\n\nThat must be a long time hence, Arthur considered.\n\n‘Oh Mr Clennam you insincerest of creatures,’ said Flora, ‘I perceive already you have not lost your old way of paying compliments, your old way when you used to pretend to be so sentimentally struck you know--at least I don’t mean that, I--oh I don’t know what I mean!’ Here Flora tittered confusedly, and gave him one of her old glances.\n\nThe Patriarch, as if he now began to perceive that his part in the piece was to get off the stage as soon as might be, rose, and went to the door by which Pancks had worked out, hailing that Tug by name. He received an answer from some little Dock beyond, and was towed out of sight directly.\n\n‘You mustn’t think of going yet,’ said Flora--Arthur had looked at his hat, being in a ludicrous dismay, and not knowing what to do: ‘you could never be so unkind as to think of going, Arthur--I mean Mr Arthur--or I suppose Mr Clennam would be far more proper--but I am sure I don’t know what I am saying--without a word about the dear old days gone for ever, when I come to think of it I dare say it would be much better not to speak of them and it’s highly probable that you have some much more agreeable engagement and pray let Me be the last person in the world to interfere with it though there _was_ a time, but I am running into nonsense again.’\n\nWas it possible that Flora could have been such a chatterer in the days she referred to? Could there have been anything like her present disjointed volubility in the fascinations that had captivated him?\n\n‘Indeed I have little doubt,’ said Flora, running on with astonishing speed, and pointing her conversation with nothing but commas, and very few of them, ‘that you are married to some Chinese lady, being in China so long and being in business and naturally desirous to settle and extend your connection nothing was more likely than that you should propose to a Chinese lady and nothing was more natural I am sure than that the Chinese lady should accept you and think herself very well off too, I only hope she’s not a Pagodian dissenter.’\n\n‘I am not,’ returned Arthur, smiling in spite of himself, ‘married to any lady, Flora.’\n\n‘Oh good gracious me I hope you never kept yourself a bachelor so long on my account!’ tittered Flora; ‘but of course you never did why should you, pray don’t answer, I don’t know where I’m running to, oh do tell me something about the Chinese ladies whether their eyes are really so long and narrow always putting me in mind of mother-of-pearl fish at cards and do they really wear tails down their back and plaited too or is it only the men, and when they pull their hair so very tight off their foreheads don’t they hurt themselves, and why do they stick little bells all over their bridges and temples and hats and things or don’t they really do it?’ Flora gave him another of her old glances. Instantly she went on again, as if he had spoken in reply for some time.\n\n‘Then it’s all true and they really do! good gracious Arthur!--pray excuse me--old habit--Mr Clennam far more proper--what a country to live in for so long a time, and with so many lanterns and umbrellas too how very dark and wet the climate ought to be and no doubt actually is, and the sums of money that must be made by those two trades where everybody carries them and hangs them everywhere, the little shoes too and the feet screwed back in infancy is quite surprising, what a traveller you are!’\n\nIn his ridiculous distress, Clennam received another of the old glances without in the least knowing what to do with it.\n\n‘Dear dear,’ said Flora, ‘only to think of the changes at home Arthur--cannot overcome it, and seems so natural, Mr Clennam far more proper--since you became familiar with the Chinese customs and language which I am persuaded you speak like a Native if not better for you were always quick and clever though immensely difficult no doubt, I am sure the tea chests alone would kill me if I tried, such changes Arthur--I am doing it again, seems so natural, most improper--as no one could have believed, who could have ever imagined Mrs Finching when I can’t imagine it myself!’\n\n‘Is that your married name?’ asked Arthur, struck, in the midst of all this, by a certain warmth of heart that expressed itself in her tone when she referred, however oddly, to the youthful relation in which they had stood to one another. ‘Finching?’\n\n‘Finching oh yes isn’t it a dreadful name, but as Mr F. said when he proposed to me which he did seven times and handsomely consented I must say to be what he used to call on liking twelve months, after all, he wasn’t answerable for it and couldn’t help it could he, Excellent man, not at all like you but excellent man!’\n\nFlora had at last talked herself out of breath for one moment. One moment; for she recovered breath in the act of raising a minute corner of her pocket-handkerchief to her eye, as a tribute to the ghost of the departed Mr F., and began again.\n\n‘No one could dispute, Arthur--Mr Clennam--that it’s quite right you should be formally friendly to me under the altered circumstances and indeed you couldn’t be anything else, at least I suppose not you ought to know, but I can’t help recalling that there _was_ a time when things were very different.’\n\n‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ Arthur began, struck by the good tone again.\n\n‘Oh not that nasty ugly name, say Flora!’\n\n‘Flora. I assure you, Flora, I am happy in seeing you once more, and in finding that, like me, you have not forgotten the old foolish dreams, when we saw all before us in the light of our youth and hope.’\n\n‘You don’t seem so,’ pouted Flora, ‘you take it very coolly, but however I know you are disappointed in me, I suppose the Chinese ladies--Mandarinesses if you call them so--are the cause or perhaps I am the cause myself, it’s just as likely.’\n\n‘No, no,’ Clennam entreated, ‘don’t say that.’\n\n‘Oh I must you know,’ said Flora, in a positive tone, ‘what nonsense not to, I know I am not what you expected, I know that very well.’\n\nIn the midst of her rapidity, she had found that out with the quick perception of a cleverer woman. The inconsistent and profoundly unreasonable way in which she instantly went on, nevertheless, to interweave their long-abandoned boy and girl relations with their present interview, made Clennam feel as if he were light-headed.\n\n‘One remark,’ said Flora, giving their conversation, without the slightest notice and to the great terror of Clennam, the tone of a love-quarrel, ‘I wish to make, one explanation I wish to offer, when your Mama came and made a scene of it with my Papa and when I was called down into the little breakfast-room where they were looking at one another with your Mama’s parasol between them seated on two chairs like mad bulls what was I to do?’\n\n‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ urged Clennam--‘all so long ago and so long concluded, is it worth while seriously to--’\n\n‘I can’t Arthur,’ returned Flora, ‘be denounced as heartless by the whole society of China without setting myself right when I have the opportunity of doing so, and you must be very well aware that there was Paul and Virginia which had to be returned and which was returned without note or comment, not that I mean to say you could have written to me watched as I was but if it had only come back with a red wafer on the cover I should have known that it meant Come to Pekin Nankeen and What’s the third place, barefoot.’\n\n‘My dear Mrs Finching, you were not to blame, and I never blamed you. We were both too young, too dependent and helpless, to do anything but accept our separation.--Pray think how long ago,’ gently remonstrated Arthur.\n\n‘One more remark,’ proceeded Flora with unslackened volubility, ‘I wish to make, one more explanation I wish to offer, for five days I had a cold in the head from crying which I passed entirely in the back drawing-room--there is the back drawing-room still on the first floor and still at the back of the house to confirm my words--when that dreary period had passed a lull succeeded years rolled on and Mr F. became acquainted with us at a mutual friend’s, he was all attention he called next day he soon began to call three evenings a week and to send in little things for supper it was not love on Mr F.’s part it was adoration, Mr F. proposed with the full approval of Papa and what could I do?’\n\n‘Nothing whatever,’ said Arthur, with the cheerfulest readiness, ‘but what you did. Let an old friend assure you of his full conviction that you did quite right.’\n\n‘One last remark,’ proceeded Flora, rejecting commonplace life with a wave of her hand, ‘I wish to make, one last explanation I wish to offer, there _was_ a time ere Mr F. first paid attentions incapable of being mistaken, but that is past and was not to be, dear Mr Clennam you no longer wear a golden chain you are free I trust you may be happy, here is Papa who is always tiresome and putting in his nose everywhere where he is not wanted.’\n\nWith these words, and with a hasty gesture fraught with timid caution--such a gesture had Clennam’s eyes been familiar with in the old time--poor Flora left herself at eighteen years of age, a long long way behind again; and came to a full stop at last.\n\nOr rather, she left about half of herself at eighteen years of age behind, and grafted the rest on to the relict of the late Mr F.; thus making a moral mermaid of herself, which her once boy-lover contemplated with feelings wherein his sense of the sorrowful and his sense of the comical were curiously blended.\n\nFor example. As if there were a secret understanding between herself and Clennam of the most thrilling nature; as if the first of a train of post-chaises and four, extending all the way to Scotland, were at that moment round the corner; and as if she couldn’t (and wouldn’t) have walked into the Parish Church with him, under the shade of the family umbrella, with the Patriarchal blessing on her head, and the perfect concurrence of all mankind; Flora comforted her soul with agonies of mysterious signalling, expressing dread of discovery. With the sensation of becoming more and more light-headed every minute, Clennam saw the relict of the late Mr F. enjoying herself in the most wonderful manner, by putting herself and him in their old places, and going through all the old performances--now, when the stage was dusty, when the scenery was faded, when the youthful actors were dead, when the orchestra was empty, when the lights were out. And still, through all this grotesque revival of what he remembered as having once been prettily natural to her, he could not but feel that it revived at sight of him, and that there was a tender memory in it.\n\nThe Patriarch insisted on his staying to dinner, and Flora signalled ‘Yes!’ Clennam so wished he could have done more than stay to dinner--so heartily wished he could have found the Flora that had been, or that never had been--that he thought the least atonement he could make for the disappointment he almost felt ashamed of, was to give himself up to the family desire. Therefore, he stayed to dinner.\n\nPancks dined with them. Pancks steamed out of his little dock at a quarter before six, and bore straight down for the Patriarch, who happened to be then driving, in an inane manner, through a stagnant account of Bleeding Heart Yard. Pancks instantly made fast to him and hauled him out.\n\n‘Bleeding Heart Yard?’ said Pancks, with a puff and a snort. ‘It’s a troublesome property. Don’t pay you badly, but rents are very hard to get there. You have more trouble with that one place than with all the places belonging to you.’\n\nJust as the big ship in tow gets the credit, with most spectators, of being the powerful object, so the Patriarch usually seemed to have said himself whatever Pancks said for him.\n\n‘Indeed?’ returned Clennam, upon whom this impression was so efficiently made by a mere gleam of the polished head that he spoke the ship instead of the Tug. ‘The people are so poor there?’\n\n‘_You_ can’t say, you know,’ snorted Pancks, taking one of his dirty hands out of his rusty iron-grey pockets to bite his nails, if he could find any, and turning his beads of eyes upon his employer, ‘whether they’re poor or not. They say they are, but they all say that. When a man says he’s rich, you’re generally sure he isn’t. Besides, if they _are_ poor, you can’t help it. You’d be poor yourself if you didn’t get your rents.’\n\n‘True enough,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘You’re not going to keep open house for all the poor of London,’ pursued Pancks. ‘You’re not going to lodge ‘em for nothing. You’re not going to open your gates wide and let ‘em come free. Not if you know it, you ain’t.’\n\nMr Casby shook his head, in Placid and benignant generality.\n\n‘If a man takes a room of you at half-a-crown a week, and when the week comes round hasn’t got the half-crown, you say to that man, Why have you got the room, then? If you haven’t got the one thing, why have you got the other? What have you been and done with your money? What do you mean by it? What are you up to? That’s what _you_ say to a man of that sort; and if you didn’t say it, more shame for you!’ Mr Pancks here made a singular and startling noise, produced by a strong blowing effort in the region of the nose, unattended by any result but that acoustic one.\n\n‘You have some extent of such property about the east and north-east here, I believe?’ said Clennam, doubtful which of the two to address.\n\n‘Oh, pretty well,’ said Pancks. ‘You’re not particular to east or north-east, any point of the compass will do for you. What you want is a good investment and a quick return. You take it where you can find it. You ain’t nice as to situation--not you.’\n\nThere was a fourth and most original figure in the Patriarchal tent, who also appeared before dinner. This was an amazing little old woman, with a face like a staring wooden doll too cheap for expression, and a stiff yellow wig perched unevenly on the top of her head, as if the child who owned the doll had driven a tack through it anywhere, so that it only got fastened on. Another remarkable thing in this little old woman was, that the same child seemed to have damaged her face in two or three places with some blunt instrument in the nature of a spoon; her countenance, and particularly the tip of her nose, presenting the phenomena of several dints, generally answering to the bowl of that article. A further remarkable thing in this little old woman was, that she had no name but Mr F.’s Aunt.\n\nShe broke upon the visitor’s view under the following circumstances: Flora said when the first dish was being put on the table, perhaps Mr Clennam might not have heard that Mr F. had left her a legacy? Clennam in return implied his hope that Mr F. had endowed the wife whom he adored, with the greater part of his worldly substance, if not with all. Flora said, oh yes, she didn’t mean that, Mr F. had made a beautiful will, but he had left her as a separate legacy, his Aunt. She then went out of the room to fetch the legacy, and, on her return, rather triumphantly presented ‘Mr F.’s Aunt.’\n\nThe major characteristics discoverable by the stranger in Mr F.’s Aunt, were extreme severity and grim taciturnity; sometimes interrupted by a propensity to offer remarks in a deep warning voice, which, being totally uncalled for by anything said by anybody, and traceable to no association of ideas, confounded and terrified the Mind. Mr F.’s Aunt may have thrown in these observations on some system of her own, and it may have been ingenious, or even subtle: but the key to it was wanted.\n\nThe neatly-served and well-cooked dinner (for everything about the Patriarchal household promoted quiet digestion) began with some soup, some fried soles, a butter-boat of shrimp sauce, and a dish of potatoes. The conversation still turned on the receipt of rents. Mr F.’s Aunt, after regarding the company for ten minutes with a malevolent gaze, delivered the following fearful remark:\n\n‘When we lived at Henley, Barnes’s gander was stole by tinkers.’\n\nMr Pancks courageously nodded his head and said, ‘All right, ma’am.’ But the effect of this mysterious communication upon Clennam was absolutely to frighten him. And another circumstance invested this old lady with peculiar terrors. Though she was always staring, she never acknowledged that she saw any individual. The polite and attentive stranger would desire, say, to consult her inclinations on the subject of potatoes. His expressive action would be hopelessly lost upon her, and what could he do? No man could say, ‘Mr F.’s Aunt, will you permit me?’ Every man retired from the spoon, as Clennam did, cowed and baffled.\n\nThere was mutton, a steak, and an apple-pie--nothing in the remotest way connected with ganders--and the dinner went on like a disenchanted feast, as it truly was. Once upon a time Clennam had sat at that table taking no heed of anything but Flora; now the principal heed he took of Flora was to observe, against his will, that she was very fond of porter, that she combined a great deal of sherry with sentiment, and that if she were a little overgrown, it was upon substantial grounds. The last of the Patriarchs had always been a mighty eater, and he disposed of an immense quantity of solid food with the benignity of a good soul who was feeding some one else. Mr Pancks, who was always in a hurry, and who referred at intervals to a little dirty notebook which he kept beside him (perhaps containing the names of the defaulters he meant to look up by way of dessert), took in his victuals much as if he were coaling; with a good deal of noise, a good deal of dropping about, and a puff and a snort occasionally, as if he were nearly ready to steam away.\n\nAll through dinner, Flora combined her present appetite for eating and drinking with her past appetite for romantic love, in a way that made Clennam afraid to lift his eyes from his plate; since he could not look towards her without receiving some glance of mysterious meaning or warning, as if they were engaged in a plot. Mr F.’s Aunt sat silently defying him with an aspect of the greatest bitterness, until the removal of the cloth and the appearance of the decanters, when she originated another observation--struck into the conversation like a clock, without consulting anybody.\n\nFlora had just said, ‘Mr Clennam, will you give me a glass of port for Mr F.’s Aunt?’\n\n‘The Monument near London Bridge,’ that lady instantly proclaimed, ‘was put up arter the Great Fire of London; and the Great Fire of London was not the fire in which your uncle George’s workshops was burned down.’\n\nMr Pancks, with his former courage, said, ‘Indeed, ma’am? All right!’ But appearing to be incensed by imaginary contradiction, or other ill-usage, Mr F.’s Aunt, instead of relapsing into silence, made the following additional proclamation:\n\n‘I hate a fool!’\n\nShe imparted to this sentiment, in itself almost Solomonic, so extremely injurious and personal a character by levelling it straight at the visitor’s head, that it became necessary to lead Mr F.’s Aunt from the room. This was quietly done by Flora; Mr F.’s Aunt offering no resistance, but inquiring on her way out, ‘What he come there for, then?’ with implacable animosity.\n\nWhen Flora returned, she explained that her legacy was a clever old lady, but was sometimes a little singular, and ‘took dislikes’--peculiarities of which Flora seemed to be proud rather than otherwise. As Flora’s good nature shone in the case, Clennam had no fault to find with the old lady for eliciting it, now that he was relieved from the terrors of her presence; and they took a glass or two of wine in peace. Foreseeing then that the Pancks would shortly get under weigh, and that the Patriarch would go to sleep, he pleaded the necessity of visiting his mother, and asked Mr Pancks in which direction he was going?\n\n‘Citywards, sir,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘Shall we walk together?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Quite agreeable,’ said Pancks.\n\nMeanwhile Flora was murmuring in rapid snatches for his ear, that there was a time and that the past was a yawning gulf however and that a golden chain no longer bound him and that she revered the memory of the late Mr F. and that she should be at home to-morrow at half-past one and that the decrees of Fate were beyond recall and that she considered nothing so improbable as that he ever walked on the north-west side of Gray’s-Inn Gardens at exactly four o’clock in the afternoon. He tried at parting to give his hand in frankness to the existing Flora--not the vanished Flora, or the mermaid--but Flora wouldn’t have it, couldn’t have it, was wholly destitute of the power of separating herself and him from their bygone characters. He left the house miserably enough; and so much more light-headed than ever, that if it had not been his good fortune to be towed away, he might, for the first quarter of an hour, have drifted anywhere.\n\nWhen he began to come to himself, in the cooler air and the absence of Flora, he found Pancks at full speed, cropping such scanty pasturage of nails as he could find, and snorting at intervals. These, in conjunction with one hand in his pocket and his roughened hat hind side before, were evidently the conditions under which he reflected.\n\n‘A fresh night!’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Yes, it’s pretty fresh,’ assented Pancks. ‘As a stranger you feel the climate more than I do, I dare say. Indeed I haven’t got time to feel it.’\n\n‘You lead such a busy life?’\n\n‘Yes, I have always some of ‘em to look up, or something to look after. But I like business,’ said Pancks, getting on a little faster. ‘What’s a man made for?’\n\n‘For nothing else?’ said Clennam.\n\nPancks put the counter question, ‘What else?’ It packed up, in the smallest compass, a weight that had rested on Clennam’s life; and he made no answer.\n\n‘That’s what I ask our weekly tenants,’ said Pancks. ‘Some of ‘em will pull long faces to me, and say, Poor as you see us, master, we’re always grinding, drudging, toiling, every minute we’re awake. I say to them, What else are you made for? It shuts them up. They haven’t a word to answer. What else are you made for? That clinches it.’\n\n‘Ah dear, dear, dear!’ sighed Clennam.\n\n‘Here am I,’ said Pancks, pursuing his argument with the weekly tenant. ‘What else do you suppose I think I am made for? Nothing. Rattle me out of bed early, set me going, give me as short a time as you like to bolt my meals in, and keep me at it. Keep me always at it, and I’ll keep you always at it, you keep somebody else always at it. There you are with the Whole Duty of Man in a commercial country.’\n\nWhen they had walked a little further in silence, Clennam said: ‘Have you no taste for anything, Mr Pancks?’\n\n‘What’s taste?’ drily retorted Pancks.\n\n‘Let us say inclination.’\n\n‘I have an inclination to get money, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘if you will show me how.’ He blew off that sound again, and it occurred to his companion for the first time that it was his way of laughing. He was a singular man in all respects; he might not have been quite in earnest, but that the short, hard, rapid manner in which he shot out these cinders of principles, as if it were done by mechanical revolvency, seemed irreconcilable with banter.\n\n‘You are no great reader, I suppose?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Never read anything but letters and accounts. Never collect anything but advertisements relative to next of kin. If _that’s_ a taste, I have got that. You’re not of the Clennams of Cornwall, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘Not that I ever heard of.’\n\n‘I know you’re not. I asked your mother, sir. She has too much character to let a chance escape her.’\n\n‘Supposing I had been of the Clennams of Cornwall?’\n\n‘You’d have heard of something to your advantage.’\n\n‘Indeed! I have heard of little enough to my advantage for some time.’\n\n‘There’s a Cornish property going a begging, sir, and not a Cornish Clennam to have it for the asking,’ said Pancks, taking his note-book from his breast pocket and putting it in again. ‘I turn off here. I wish you good night.’\n\n‘Good night!’ said Clennam. But the Tug, suddenly lightened, and untrammelled by having any weight in tow, was already puffing away into the distance.\n\nThey had crossed Smithfield together, and Clennam was left alone at the corner of Barbican. He had no intention of presenting himself in his mother’s dismal room that night, and could not have felt more depressed and cast away if he had been in a wilderness. He turned slowly down Aldersgate Street, and was pondering his way along towards Saint Paul’s, purposing to come into one of the great thoroughfares for the sake of their light and life, when a crowd of people flocked towards him on the same pavement, and he stood aside against a shop to let them pass. As they came up, he made out that they were gathered around a something that was carried on men’s shoulders. He soon saw that it was a litter, hastily made of a shutter or some such thing; and a recumbent figure upon it, and the scraps of conversation in the crowd, and a muddy bundle carried by one man, and a muddy hat carried by another, informed him that an accident had occurred. The litter stopped under a lamp before it had passed him half-a-dozen paces, for some readjustment of the burden; and, the crowd stopping too, he found himself in the midst of the array.\n\n‘An accident going to the Hospital?’ he asked an old man beside him, who stood shaking his head, inviting conversation.\n\n‘Yes,’ said the man, ‘along of them Mails. They ought to be prosecuted and fined, them Mails. They come a racing out of Lad Lane and Wood Street at twelve or fourteen mile a hour, them Mails do. The only wonder is, that people ain’t killed oftener by them Mails.’\n\n‘This person is not killed, I hope?’\n\n‘I don’t know!’ said the man, ‘it an’t for the want of a will in them Mails, if he an’t.’ The speaker having folded his arms, and set in comfortably to address his depreciation of them Mails to any of the bystanders who would listen, several voices, out of pure sympathy with the sufferer, confirmed him; one voice saying to Clennam, ‘They’re a public nuisance, them Mails, sir;’ another, ‘_I_ see one on ‘em pull up within half a inch of a boy, last night;’ another, ‘_I_ see one on ‘em go over a cat, sir--and it might have been your own mother;’ and all representing, by implication, that if he happened to possess any public influence, he could not use it better than against them Mails.\n\n‘Why, a native Englishman is put to it every night of his life, to save his life from them Mails,’ argued the first old man; ‘and _he_ knows when they’re a coming round the corner, to tear him limb from limb. What can you expect from a poor foreigner who don’t know nothing about ‘em!’\n\n‘Is this a foreigner?’ said Clennam, leaning forward to look.\n\nIn the midst of such replies as ‘Frenchman, sir,’ ‘Porteghee, sir,’ ‘Dutchman, sir,’ ‘Prooshan, sir,’ and other conflicting testimony, he now heard a feeble voice asking, both in Italian and in French, for water. A general remark going round, in reply, of ‘Ah, poor fellow, he says he’ll never get over it; and no wonder!’ Clennam begged to be allowed to pass, as he understood the poor creature. He was immediately handed to the front, to speak to him.\n\n‘First, he wants some water,’ said he, looking round. (A dozen good fellows dispersed to get it.) ‘Are you badly hurt, my friend?’ he asked the man on the litter, in Italian.\n\n‘Yes, sir; yes, yes, yes. It’s my leg, it’s my leg. But it pleases me to hear the old music, though I am very bad.’\n\n‘You are a traveller! Stay! See, the water! Let me give you some.’\n\nThey had rested the litter on a pile of paving stones. It was at a convenient height from the ground, and by stooping he could lightly raise the head with one hand and hold the glass to his lips with the other. A little, muscular, brown man, with black hair and white teeth. A lively face, apparently. Earrings in his ears.\n\n‘That’s well. You are a traveller?’\n\n‘Surely, sir.’\n\n‘A stranger in this city?’\n\n‘Surely, surely, altogether. I am arrived this unhappy evening.’\n\n‘From what country?’\n\n‘Marseilles.’\n\n‘Why, see there! I also! Almost as much a stranger here as you, though born here, I came from Marseilles a little while ago. Don’t be cast down.’ The face looked up at him imploringly, as he rose from wiping it, and gently replaced the coat that covered the writhing figure. ‘I won’t leave you till you shall be well taken care of. Courage! You will be very much better half an hour hence.’\n\n‘Ah! Altro, Altro!’ cried the poor little man, in a faintly incredulous tone; and as they took him up, hung out his right hand to give the forefinger a back-handed shake in the air.\n\nArthur Clennam turned; and walking beside the litter, and saying an encouraging word now and then, accompanied it to the neighbouring hospital of Saint Bartholomew. None of the crowd but the bearers and he being admitted, the disabled man was soon laid on a table in a cool, methodical way, and carefully examined by a surgeon who was as near at hand, and as ready to appear as Calamity herself. ‘He hardly knows an English word,’ said Clennam; ‘is he badly hurt?’\n\n‘Let us know all about it first,’ said the surgeon, continuing his examination with a businesslike delight in it, ‘before we pronounce.’\n\nAfter trying the leg with a finger, and two fingers, and one hand and two hands, and over and under, and up and down, and in this direction and in that, and approvingly remarking on the points of interest to another gentleman who joined him, the surgeon at last clapped the patient on the shoulder, and said, ‘He won’t hurt. He’ll do very well. It’s difficult enough, but we shall not want him to part with his leg this time.’ Which Clennam interpreted to the patient, who was full of gratitude, and, in his demonstrative way, kissed both the interpreter’s hand and the surgeon’s several times.\n\n‘It’s a serious injury, I suppose?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Ye-es,’ replied the surgeon, with the thoughtful pleasure of an artist contemplating the work upon his easel. ‘Yes, it’s enough. There’s a compound fracture above the knee, and a dislocation below. They are both of a beautiful kind.’ He gave the patient a friendly clap on the shoulder again, as if he really felt that he was a very good fellow indeed, and worthy of all commendation for having broken his leg in a manner interesting to science.\n\n‘He speaks French?’ said the surgeon.\n\n‘Oh yes, he speaks French.’\n\n‘He’ll be at no loss here, then.--You have only to bear a little pain like a brave fellow, my friend, and to be thankful that all goes as well as it does,’ he added, in that tongue, ‘and you’ll walk again to a marvel. Now, let us see whether there’s anything else the matter, and how our ribs are?’\n\nThere was nothing else the matter, and our ribs were sound. Clennam remained until everything possible to be done had been skilfully and promptly done--the poor belated wanderer in a strange land movingly besought that favour of him--and lingered by the bed to which he was in due time removed, until he had fallen into a doze. Even then he wrote a few words for him on his card, with a promise to return to-morrow, and left it to be given to him when he should awake.\n\nAll these proceedings occupied so long that it struck eleven o’clock at night as he came out at the Hospital Gate. He had hired a lodging for the present in Covent Garden, and he took the nearest way to that quarter, by Snow Hill and Holborn.\n\nLeft to himself again, after the solicitude and compassion of his last adventure, he was naturally in a thoughtful mood. As naturally, he could not walk on thinking for ten minutes without recalling Flora. She necessarily recalled to him his life, with all its misdirection and little happiness.\n\nWhen he got to his lodging, he sat down before the dying fire, as he had stood at the window of his old room looking out upon the blackened forest of chimneys, and turned his gaze back upon the gloomy vista by which he had come to that stage in his existence. So long, so bare, so blank. No childhood; no youth, except for one remembrance; that one remembrance proved, only that day, to be a piece of folly.\n\nIt was a misfortune to him, trifle as it might have been to another. For, while all that was hard and stern in his recollection, remained Reality on being proved--was obdurate to the sight and touch, and relaxed nothing of its old indomitable grimness--the one tender recollection of his experience would not bear the same test, and melted away. He had foreseen this, on the former night, when he had dreamed with waking eyes, but he had not felt it then; and he had now.\n\nHe was a dreamer in such wise, because he was a man who had, deep-rooted in his nature, a belief in all the gentle and good things his life had been without. Bred in meanness and hard dealing, this had rescued him to be a man of honourable mind and open hand. Bred in coldness and severity, this had rescued him to have a warm and sympathetic heart. Bred in a creed too darkly audacious to pursue, through its process of reserving the making of man in the image of his Creator to the making of his Creator in the image of an erring man, this had rescued him to judge not, and in humility to be merciful, and have hope and charity.\n\nAnd this saved him still from the whimpering weakness and cruel selfishness of holding that because such a happiness or such a virtue had not come into his little path, or worked well for him, therefore it was not in the great scheme, but was reducible, when found in appearance, to the basest elements. A disappointed mind he had, but a mind too firm and healthy for such unwholesome air. Leaving himself in the dark, it could rise into the light, seeing it shine on others and hailing it.\n\nTherefore, he sat before his dying fire, sorrowful to think upon the way by which he had come to that night, yet not strewing poison on the way by which other men had come to it. That he should have missed so much, and at his time of life should look so far about him for any staff to bear him company upon his downward journey and cheer it, was a just regret. He looked at the fire from which the blaze departed, from which the afterglow subsided, in which the ashes turned grey, from which they dropped to dust, and thought, ‘How soon I too shall pass through such changes, and be gone!’\n\nTo review his life was like descending a green tree in fruit and flower, and seeing all the branches wither and drop off, one by one, as he came down towards them.\n\n‘From the unhappy suppression of my youngest days, through the rigid and unloving home that followed them, through my departure, my long exile, my return, my mother’s welcome, my intercourse with her since, down to the afternoon of this day with poor Flora,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘what have I found!’\n\nHis door was softly opened, and these spoken words startled him, and came as if they were an answer:\n\n‘Little Dorrit.’\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Little Dorrit’s Party\n\nArthur Clennam rose hastily, and saw her standing at the door. This history must sometimes see with Little Dorrit’s eyes, and shall begin that course by seeing him.\n\nLittle Dorrit looked into a dim room, which seemed a spacious one to her, and grandly furnished. Courtly ideas of Covent Garden, as a place with famous coffee-houses, where gentlemen wearing gold-laced coats and swords had quarrelled and fought duels; costly ideas of Covent Garden, as a place where there were flowers in winter at guineas a-piece, pine-apples at guineas a pound, and peas at guineas a pint; picturesque ideas of Covent Garden, as a place where there was a mighty theatre, showing wonderful and beautiful sights to richly-dressed ladies and gentlemen, and which was for ever far beyond the reach of poor Fanny or poor uncle; desolate ideas of Covent Garden, as having all those arches in it, where the miserable children in rags among whom she had just now passed, like young rats, slunk and hid, fed on offal, huddled together for warmth, and were hunted about (look to the rats young and old, all ye Barnacles, for before God they are eating away our foundations, and will bring the roofs on our heads!); teeming ideas of Covent Garden, as a place of past and present mystery, romance, abundance, want, beauty, ugliness, fair country gardens, and foul street gutters; all confused together,--made the room dimmer than it was in Little Dorrit’s eyes, as they timidly saw it from the door.\n\nAt first in the chair before the gone-out fire, and then turned round wondering to see her, was the gentleman whom she sought. The brown, grave gentleman, who smiled so pleasantly, who was so frank and considerate in his manner, and yet in whose earnestness there was something that reminded her of his mother, with the great difference that she was earnest in asperity and he in gentleness. Now he regarded her with that attentive and inquiring look before which Little Dorrit’s eyes had always fallen, and before which they fell still.\n\n‘My poor child! Here at midnight?’\n\n‘I said Little Dorrit, sir, on purpose to prepare you. I knew you must be very much surprised.’\n\n‘Are you alone?’\n\n‘No sir, I have got Maggy with me.’\n\nConsidering her entrance sufficiently prepared for by this mention of her name, Maggy appeared from the landing outside, on the broad grin. She instantly suppressed that manifestation, however, and became fixedly solemn.\n\n‘And I have no fire,’ said Clennam. ‘And you are--’ He was going to say so lightly clad, but stopped himself in what would have been a reference to her poverty, saying instead, ‘And it is so cold.’\n\nPutting the chair from which he had risen nearer to the grate, he made her sit down in it; and hurriedly bringing wood and coal, heaped them together and got a blaze.\n\n‘Your foot is like marble, my child;’ he had happened to touch it, while stooping on one knee at his work of kindling the fire; ‘put it nearer the warmth.’ Little Dorrit thanked him hastily. It was quite warm, it was very warm! It smote upon his heart to feel that she hid her thin, worn shoe.\n\nLittle Dorrit was not ashamed of her poor shoes. He knew her story, and it was not that. Little Dorrit had a misgiving that he might blame her father, if he saw them; that he might think, ‘why did he dine to-day, and leave this little creature to the mercy of the cold stones!’ She had no belief that it would have been a just reflection; she simply knew, by experience, that such delusions did sometimes present themselves to people. It was a part of her father’s misfortunes that they did.\n\n‘Before I say anything else,’ Little Dorrit began, sitting before the pale fire, and raising her eyes again to the face which in its harmonious look of interest, and pity, and protection, she felt to be a mystery far above her in degree, and almost removed beyond her guessing at; ‘may I tell you something, sir?’\n\n‘Yes, my child.’\n\nA slight shade of distress fell upon her, at his so often calling her a child. She was surprised that he should see it, or think of such a slight thing; but he said directly:\n\n‘I wanted a tender word, and could think of no other. As you just now gave yourself the name they give you at my mother’s, and as that is the name by which I always think of you, let me call you Little Dorrit.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, I should like it better than any name.’\n\n‘Little Dorrit.’\n\n‘Little mother,’ Maggy (who had been falling asleep) put in, as a correction.\n\n‘It’s all the same, Maggy,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘all the same.’\n\n‘Is it all the same, mother?’\n\n‘Just the same.’\n\nMaggy laughed, and immediately snored. In Little Dorrit’s eyes and ears, the uncouth figure and the uncouth sound were as pleasant as could be. There was a glow of pride in her big child, overspreading her face, when it again met the eyes of the grave brown gentleman. She wondered what he was thinking of, as he looked at Maggy and her. She thought what a good father he would be. How, with some such look, he would counsel and cherish his daughter.\n\n‘What I was going to tell you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘is, that my brother is at large.’\n\nArthur was rejoiced to hear it, and hoped he would do well.\n\n‘And what I was going to tell you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, trembling in all her little figure and in her voice, ‘is, that I am not to know whose generosity released him--am never to ask, and am never to be told, and am never to thank that gentleman with all my grateful heart!’\n\nHe would probably need no thanks, Clennam said. Very likely he would be thankful himself (and with reason), that he had had the means and chance of doing a little service to her, who well deserved a great one.\n\n‘And what I was going to say, sir, is,’ said Little Dorrit, trembling more and more, ‘that if I knew him, and I might, I would tell him that he can never, never know how I feel his goodness, and how my good father would feel it. And what I was going to say, sir, is, that if I knew him, and I might--but I don’t know him and I must not--I know that!--I would tell him that I shall never any more lie down to sleep without having prayed to Heaven to bless him and reward him. And if I knew him, and I might, I would go down on my knees to him, and take his hand and kiss it and ask him not to draw it away, but to leave it--O to leave it for a moment--and let my thankful tears fall on it; for I have no other thanks to give him!’\n\nLittle Dorrit had put his hand to her lips, and would have kneeled to him, but he gently prevented her, and replaced her in her chair. Her eyes, and the tones of her voice, had thanked him far better than she thought. He was not able to say, quite as composedly as usual, ‘There, Little Dorrit, there, there, there! We will suppose that you did know this person, and that you might do all this, and that it was all done. And now tell me, Who am quite another person--who am nothing more than the friend who begged you to trust him--why you are out at midnight, and what it is that brings you so far through the streets at this late hour, my slight, delicate,’ child was on his lips again, ‘Little Dorrit!’\n\n‘Maggy and I have been to-night,’ she answered, subduing herself with the quiet effort that had long been natural to her, ‘to the theatre where my sister is engaged.’\n\n‘And oh ain’t it a Ev’nly place,’ suddenly interrupted Maggy, who seemed to have the power of going to sleep and waking up whenever she chose. ‘Almost as good as a hospital. Only there ain’t no Chicking in it.’\n\nHere she shook herself, and fell asleep again.\n\n‘We went there,’ said Little Dorrit, glancing at her charge, ‘because I like sometimes to know, of my own knowledge, that my sister is doing well; and like to see her there, with my own eyes, when neither she nor Uncle is aware. It is very seldom indeed that I can do that, because when I am not out at work, I am with my father, and even when I am out at work, I hurry home to him. But I pretend to-night that I am at a party.’\n\nAs she made the confession, timidly hesitating, she raised her eyes to the face, and read its expression so plainly that she answered it.\n\n‘Oh no, certainly! I never was at a party in my life.’\n\nShe paused a little under his attentive look, and then said, ‘I hope there is no harm in it. I could never have been of any use, if I had not pretended a little.’\n\nShe feared that he was blaming her in his mind for so devising to contrive for them, think for them, and watch over them, without their knowledge or gratitude; perhaps even with their reproaches for supposed neglect. But what was really in his mind, was the weak figure with its strong purpose, the thin worn shoes, the insufficient dress, and the pretence of recreation and enjoyment. He asked where the suppositious party was? At a place where she worked, answered Little Dorrit, blushing. She had said very little about it; only a few words to make her father easy. Her father did not believe it to be a grand party--indeed he might suppose that. And she glanced for an instant at the shawl she wore.\n\n‘It is the first night,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that I have ever been away from home. And London looks so large, so barren, and so wild.’ In Little Dorrit’s eyes, its vastness under the black sky was awful; a tremor passed over her as she said the words.\n\n‘But this is not,’ she added, with the quiet effort again, ‘what I have come to trouble you with, sir. My sister’s having found a friend, a lady she has told me of and made me rather anxious about, was the first cause of my coming away from home. And being away, and coming (on purpose) round by where you lived and seeing a light in the window--’\n\nNot for the first time. No, not for the first time. In Little Dorrit’s eyes, the outside of that window had been a distant star on other nights than this. She had toiled out of her way, tired and troubled, to look up at it, and wonder about the grave, brown gentleman from so far off, who had spoken to her as a friend and protector.\n\n‘There were three things,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that I thought I would like to say, if you were alone and I might come up-stairs. First, what I have tried to say, but never can--never shall--’\n\n‘Hush, hush! That is done with, and disposed of. Let us pass to the second,’ said Clennam, smiling her agitation away, making the blaze shine upon her, and putting wine and cake and fruit towards her on the table.\n\n‘I think,’ said Little Dorrit--‘this is the second thing, sir--I think Mrs Clennam must have found out my secret, and must know where I come from and where I go to. Where I live, I mean.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ returned Clennam quickly. He asked her, after short consideration, why she supposed so.\n\n‘I think,’ replied Little Dorrit, ‘that Mr Flintwinch must have watched me.’\n\nAnd why, Clennam asked, as he turned his eyes upon the fire, bent his brows, and considered again; why did she suppose that?\n\n‘I have met him twice. Both times near home. Both times at night, when I was going back. Both times I thought (though that may easily be my mistake), that he hardly looked as if he had met me by accident.’\n\n‘Did he say anything?’\n\n‘No; he only nodded and put his head on one side.’\n\n‘The devil take his head!’ mused Clennam, still looking at the fire; ‘it’s always on one side.’\n\nHe roused himself to persuade her to put some wine to her lips, and to touch something to eat--it was very difficult, she was so timid and shy--and then said, musing again:\n\n‘Is my mother at all changed to you?’\n\n‘Oh, not at all. She is just the same. I wondered whether I had better tell her my history. I wondered whether I might--I mean, whether you would like me to tell her. I wondered,’ said Little Dorrit, looking at him in a suppliant way, and gradually withdrawing her eyes as he looked at her, ‘whether you would advise me what I ought to do.’\n\n‘Little Dorrit,’ said Clennam; and the phrase had already begun, between these two, to stand for a hundred gentle phrases, according to the varying tone and connection in which it was used; ‘do nothing. I will have some talk with my old friend, Mrs Affery. Do nothing, Little Dorrit--except refresh yourself with such means as there are here. I entreat you to do that.’\n\n‘Thank you, I am not hungry. Nor,’ said Little Dorrit, as he softly put her glass towards her, ‘nor thirsty.--I think Maggy might like something, perhaps.’\n\n‘We will make her find pockets presently for all there is here,’ said Clennam: ‘but before we awake her, there was a third thing to say.’\n\n‘Yes. You will not be offended, sir?’\n\n‘I promise that, unreservedly.’\n\n‘It will sound strange. I hardly know how to say it. Don’t think it unreasonable or ungrateful in me,’ said Little Dorrit, with returning and increasing agitation.\n\n‘No, no, no. I am sure it will be natural and right. I am not afraid that I shall put a wrong construction on it, whatever it is.’\n\n‘Thank you. You are coming back to see my father again?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘You have been so good and thoughtful as to write him a note, saying that you are coming to-morrow?’\n\n‘Oh, that was nothing! Yes.’\n\n‘Can you guess,’ said Little Dorrit, folding her small hands tight in one another, and looking at him with all the earnestness of her soul looking steadily out of her eyes, ‘what I am going to ask you not to do?’\n\n‘I think I can. But I may be wrong.’\n\n‘No, you are not wrong,’ said Little Dorrit, shaking her head. ‘If we should want it so very, very badly that we cannot do without it, let me ask you for it.’\n\n‘I Will,--I Will.’\n\n‘Don’t encourage him to ask. Don’t understand him if he does ask. Don’t give it to him. Save him and spare him that, and you will be able to think better of him!’\n\nClennam said--not very plainly, seeing those tears glistening in her anxious eyes--that her wish should be sacred with him.\n\n‘You don’t know what he is,’ she said; ‘you don’t know what he really is. How can you, seeing him there all at once, dear love, and not gradually, as I have done! You have been so good to us, so delicately and truly good, that I want him to be better in your eyes than in anybody’s. And I cannot bear to think,’ cried Little Dorrit, covering her tears with her hands, ‘I cannot bear to think that you of all the world should see him in his only moments of degradation.’\n\n‘Pray,’ said Clennam, ‘do not be so distressed. Pray, pray, Little Dorrit! This is quite understood now.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir. Thank you! I have tried very much to keep myself from saying this; I have thought about it, days and nights; but when I knew for certain you were coming again, I made up my mind to speak to you. Not because I am ashamed of him,’ she dried her tears quickly, ‘but because I know him better than any one does, and love him, and am proud of him.’\n\nRelieved of this weight, Little Dorrit was nervously anxious to be gone. Maggy being broad awake, and in the act of distantly gloating over the fruit and cakes with chuckles of anticipation, Clennam made the best diversion in his power by pouring her out a glass of wine, which she drank in a series of loud smacks; putting her hand upon her windpipe after every one, and saying, breathless, with her eyes in a prominent state, ‘Oh, ain’t it d’licious! Ain’t it hospitally!’ When she had finished the wine and these encomiums, he charged her to load her basket (she was never without her basket) with every eatable thing upon the table, and to take especial care to leave no scrap behind. Maggy’s pleasure in doing this and her little mother’s pleasure in seeing Maggy pleased, was as good a turn as circumstances could have given to the late conversation.\n\n‘But the gates will have been locked long ago,’ said Clennam, suddenly remembering it. ‘Where are you going?’\n\n‘I am going to Maggy’s lodging,’ answered Little Dorrit. ‘I shall be quite safe, quite well taken care of.’\n\n‘I must accompany you there,’ said Clennam, ‘I cannot let you go alone.’\n\n‘Yes, pray leave us to go there by ourselves. Pray do!’ begged Little Dorrit.\n\nShe was so earnest in the petition, that Clennam felt a delicacy in obtruding himself upon her: the rather, because he could well understand that Maggy’s lodging was of the obscurest sort. ‘Come, Maggy,’ said Little Dorrit cheerily, ‘we shall do very well; we know the way by this time, Maggy?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, little mother; we know the way,’ chuckled Maggy. And away they went. Little Dorrit turned at the door to say, ‘God bless you!’ She said it very softly, but perhaps she may have been as audible above--who knows!--as a whole cathedral choir.\n\nArthur Clennam suffered them to pass the corner of the street before he followed at a distance; not with any idea of encroaching a second time on Little Dorrit’s privacy, but to satisfy his mind by seeing her secure in the neighbourhood to which she was accustomed. So diminutive she looked, so fragile and defenceless against the bleak damp weather, flitting along in the shuffling shadow of her charge, that he felt, in his compassion, and in his habit of considering her a child apart from the rest of the rough world, as if he would have been glad to take her up in his arms and carry her to her journey’s end.\n\nIn course of time she came into the leading thoroughfare where the Marshalsea was, and then he saw them slacken their pace, and soon turn down a by-street. He stopped, felt that he had no right to go further, and slowly left them. He had no suspicion that they ran any risk of being houseless until morning; had no idea of the truth until long, long afterwards.\n\nBut, said Little Dorrit, when they stopped at a poor dwelling all in darkness, and heard no sound on listening at the door, ‘Now, this is a good lodging for you, Maggy, and we must not give offence. Consequently, we will only knock twice, and not very loud; and if we cannot wake them so, we must walk about till day.’\n\nOnce, Little Dorrit knocked with a careful hand, and listened. Twice, Little Dorrit knocked with a careful hand, and listened. All was close and still. ‘Maggy, we must do the best we can, my dear. We must be patient, and wait for day.’\n\nIt was a chill dark night, with a damp wind blowing, when they came out into the leading street again, and heard the clocks strike half-past one. ‘In only five hours and a half,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘we shall be able to go home.’ To speak of home, and to go and look at it, it being so near, was a natural sequence. They went to the closed gate, and peeped through into the court-yard. ‘I hope he is sound asleep,’ said Little Dorrit, kissing one of the bars, ‘and does not miss me.’\n\nThe gate was so familiar, and so like a companion, that they put down Maggy’s basket in a corner to serve for a seat, and keeping close together, rested there for some time. While the street was empty and silent, Little Dorrit was not afraid; but when she heard a footstep at a distance, or saw a moving shadow among the street lamps, she was startled, and whispered, ‘Maggy, I see some one. Come away!’ Maggy would then wake up more or less fretfully, and they would wander about a little, and come back again.\n\nAs long as eating was a novelty and an amusement, Maggy kept up pretty well. But that period going by, she became querulous about the cold, and shivered and whimpered. ‘It will soon be over, dear,’ said Little Dorrit patiently. ‘Oh it’s all very fine for you, little mother,’ returned Maggy, ‘but I’m a poor thing, only ten years old.’ At last, in the dead of the night, when the street was very still indeed, Little Dorrit laid the heavy head upon her bosom, and soothed her to sleep. And thus she sat at the gate, as it were alone; looking up at the stars, and seeing the clouds pass over them in their wild flight--which was the dance at Little Dorrit’s party.\n\n‘If it really was a party!’ she thought once, as she sat there. ‘If it was light and warm and beautiful, and it was our house, and my poor dear was its master, and had never been inside these walls. And if Mr Clennam was one of our visitors, and we were dancing to delightful music, and were all as gay and light-hearted as ever we could be! I wonder--’ Such a vista of wonder opened out before her, that she sat looking up at the stars, quite lost, until Maggy was querulous again, and wanted to get up and walk.\n\nThree o’clock, and half-past three, and they had passed over London Bridge. They had heard the rush of the tide against obstacles; and looked down, awed, through the dark vapour on the river; had seen little spots of lighted water where the bridge lamps were reflected, shining like demon eyes, with a terrible fascination in them for guilt and misery. They had shrunk past homeless people, lying coiled up in nooks. They had run from drunkards. They had started from slinking men, whistling and signing to one another at bye corners, or running away at full speed. Though everywhere the leader and the guide, Little Dorrit, happy for once in her youthful appearance, feigned to cling to and rely upon Maggy. And more than once some voice, from among a knot of brawling or prowling figures in their path, had called out to the rest to ‘let the woman and the child go by!’\n\nSo, the woman and the child had gone by, and gone on, and five had sounded from the steeples. They were walking slowly towards the east, already looking for the first pale streak of day, when a woman came after them.\n\n‘What are you doing with the child?’ she said to Maggy.\n\nShe was young--far too young to be there, Heaven knows!--and neither ugly nor wicked-looking. She spoke coarsely, but with no naturally coarse voice; there was even something musical in its sound.\n\n‘What are you doing with yourself?’ retorted Maggy, for want of a better answer.\n\n‘Can’t you see, without my telling you?’\n\n‘I don’t know as I can,’ said Maggy.\n\n‘Killing myself! Now I have answered you, answer me. What are you doing with the child?’\n\nThe supposed child kept her head drooped down, and kept her form close at Maggy’s side.\n\n‘Poor thing!’ said the woman. ‘Have you no feeling, that you keep her out in the cruel streets at such a time as this? Have you no eyes, that you don’t see how delicate and slender she is? Have you no sense (you don’t look as if you had much) that you don’t take more pity on this cold and trembling little hand?’\n\nShe had stepped across to that side, and held the hand between her own two, chafing it. ‘Kiss a poor lost creature, dear,’ she said, bending her face, ‘and tell me where’s she taking you.’\n\nLittle Dorrit turned towards her.\n\n‘Why, my God!’ she said, recoiling, ‘you’re a woman!’\n\n‘Don’t mind that!’ said Little Dorrit, clasping one of her hands that had suddenly released hers. ‘I am not afraid of you.’\n\n‘Then you had better be,’ she answered. ‘Have you no mother?’\n\n‘No.’\n\n‘No father?’\n\n‘Yes, a very dear one.’\n\n‘Go home to him, and be afraid of me. Let me go. Good night!’\n\n‘I must thank you first; let me speak to you as if I really were a child.’\n\n‘You can’t do it,’ said the woman. ‘You are kind and innocent; but you can’t look at me out of a child’s eyes. I never should have touched you, but I thought that you were a child.’ And with a strange, wild cry, she went away.\n\nNo day yet in the sky, but there was day in the resounding stones of the streets; in the waggons, carts, and coaches; in the workers going to various occupations; in the opening of early shops; in the traffic at markets; in the stir of the riverside. There was coming day in the flaring lights, with a feebler colour in them than they would have had at another time; coming day in the increased sharpness of the air, and the ghastly dying of the night.\n\nThey went back again to the gate, intending to wait there now until it should be opened; but the air was so raw and cold that Little Dorrit, leading Maggy about in her sleep, kept in motion. Going round by the Church, she saw lights there, and the door open; and went up the steps and looked in.\n\n‘Who’s that?’ cried a stout old man, who was putting on a nightcap as if he were going to bed in a vault.\n\n‘It’s no one particular, sir,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Stop!’ cried the man. ‘Let’s have a look at you!’\n\nThis caused her to turn back again in the act of going out, and to present herself and her charge before him.\n\n‘I thought so!’ said he. ‘I know _you_.’\n\n‘We have often seen each other,’ said Little Dorrit, recognising the sexton, or the beadle, or the verger, or whatever he was, ‘when I have been at church here.’\n\n‘More than that, we’ve got your birth in our Register, you know; you’re one of our curiosities.’\n\n‘Indeed!’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘To be sure. As the child of the--by-the-bye, how did you get out so early?’\n\n‘We were shut out last night, and are waiting to get in.’\n\n‘You don’t mean it? And there’s another hour good yet! Come into the vestry. You’ll find a fire in the vestry, on account of the painters. I’m waiting for the painters, or I shouldn’t be here, you may depend upon it. One of our curiosities mustn’t be cold when we have it in our power to warm her up comfortable. Come along.’\n\nHe was a very good old fellow, in his familiar way; and having stirred the vestry fire, he looked round the shelves of registers for a particular volume. ‘Here you are, you see,’ he said, taking it down and turning the leaves. ‘Here you’ll find yourself, as large as life. Amy, daughter of William and Fanny Dorrit. Born, Marshalsea Prison, Parish of St George. And we tell people that you have lived there, without so much as a day’s or a night’s absence, ever since. Is it true?’\n\n‘Quite true, till last night.’\n\n‘Lord!’ But his surveying her with an admiring gaze suggested Something else to him, to wit: ‘I am sorry to see, though, that you are faint and tired. Stay a bit. I’ll get some cushions out of the church, and you and your friend shall lie down before the fire. Don’t be afraid of not going in to join your father when the gate opens. _I’ll_ call you.’\n\nHe soon brought in the cushions, and strewed them on the ground.\n\n‘There you are, you see. Again as large as life. Oh, never mind thanking. I’ve daughters of my own. And though they weren’t born in the Marshalsea Prison, they might have been, if I had been, in my ways of carrying on, of your father’s breed. Stop a bit. I must put something under the cushion for your head. Here’s a burial volume, just the thing! We have got Mrs Bangham in this book. But what makes these books interesting to most people is--not who’s in ‘em, but who isn’t--who’s coming, you know, and when. That’s the interesting question.’\n\nCommendingly looking back at the pillow he had improvised, he left them to their hour’s repose. Maggy was snoring already, and Little Dorrit was soon fast asleep with her head resting on that sealed book of Fate, untroubled by its mysterious blank leaves.\n\nThis was Little Dorrit’s party. The shame, desertion, wretchedness, and exposure of the great capital; the wet, the cold, the slow hours, and the swift clouds of the dismal night. This was the party from which Little Dorrit went home, jaded, in the first grey mist of a rainy morning.\n\n# CHAPTER 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream\n\nThe debilitated old house in the city, wrapped in its mantle of soot, and leaning heavily on the crutches that had partaken of its decay and worn out with it, never knew a healthy or a cheerful interval, let what would betide. If the sun ever touched it, it was but with a ray, and that was gone in half an hour; if the moonlight ever fell upon it, it was only to put a few patches on its doleful cloak, and make it look more wretched. The stars, to be sure, coldly watched it when the nights and the smoke were clear enough; and all bad weather stood by it with a rare fidelity. You should alike find rain, hail, frost, and thaw lingering in that dismal enclosure when they had vanished from other places; and as to snow, you should see it there for weeks, long after it had changed from yellow to black, slowly weeping away its grimy life. The place had no other adherents. As to street noises, the rumbling of wheels in the lane merely rushed in at the gateway in going past, and rushed out again: making the listening Mistress Affery feel as if she were deaf, and recovered the sense of hearing by instantaneous flashes. So with whistling, singing, talking, laughing, and all pleasant human sounds. They leaped the gap in a moment, and went upon their way.\n\nThe varying light of fire and candle in Mrs Clennam’s room made the greatest change that ever broke the dead monotony of the spot. In her two long narrow windows, the fire shone sullenly all day, and sullenly all night. On rare occasions it flashed up passionately, as she did; but for the most part it was suppressed, like her, and preyed upon itself evenly and slowly. During many hours of the short winter days, however, when it was dusk there early in the afternoon, changing distortions of herself in her wheeled chair, of Mr Flintwinch with his wry neck, of Mistress Affery coming and going, would be thrown upon the house wall that was over the gateway, and would hover there like shadows from a great magic lantern. As the room-ridden invalid settled for the night, these would gradually disappear: Mistress Affery’s magnified shadow always flitting about, last, until it finally glided away into the air, as though she were off upon a witch excursion. Then the solitary light would burn unchangingly, until it burned pale before the dawn, and at last died under the breath of Mrs Affery, as her shadow descended on it from the witch-region of sleep.\n\nStrange, if the little sick-room fire were in effect a beacon fire, summoning some one, and that the most unlikely some one in the world, to the spot that _must_ be come to. Strange, if the little sick-room light were in effect a watch-light, burning in that place every night until an appointed event should be watched out! Which of the vast multitude of travellers, under the sun and the stars, climbing the dusty hills and toiling along the weary plains, journeying by land and journeying by sea, coming and going so strangely, to meet and to act and react on one another; which of the host may, with no suspicion of the journey’s end, be travelling surely hither?\n\nTime shall show us. The post of honour and the post of shame, the general’s station and the drummer’s, a peer’s statue in Westminster Abbey and a seaman’s hammock in the bosom of the deep, the mitre and the workhouse, the woolsack and the gallows, the throne and the guillotine--the travellers to all are on the great high road, but it has wonderful divergencies, and only Time shall show us whither each traveller is bound.\n\nOn a wintry afternoon at twilight, Mrs Flintwinch, having been heavy all day, dreamed this dream:\n\nShe thought she was in the kitchen getting the kettle ready for tea, and was warming herself with her feet upon the fender and the skirt of her gown tucked up, before the collapsed fire in the middle of the grate, bordered on either hand by a deep cold black ravine. She thought that as she sat thus, musing upon the question whether life was not for some people a rather dull invention, she was frightened by a sudden noise behind her. She thought that she had been similarly frightened once last week, and that the noise was of a mysterious kind--a sound of rustling and of three or four quick beats like a rapid step; while a shock or tremble was communicated to her heart, as if the step had shaken the floor, or even as if she had been touched by some awful hand. She thought that this revived within her certain old fears of hers that the house was haunted; and that she flew up the kitchen stairs without knowing how she got up, to be nearer company.\n\nMistress Affery thought that on reaching the hall, she saw the door of her liege lord’s office standing open, and the room empty. That she went to the ripped-up window in the little room by the street door to connect her palpitating heart, through the glass, with living things beyond and outside the haunted house. That she then saw, on the wall over the gateway, the shadows of the two clever ones in conversation above. That she then went upstairs with her shoes in her hand, partly to be near the clever ones as a match for most ghosts, and partly to hear what they were talking about.\n\n‘None of your nonsense with me,’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘I won’t take it from you.’\n\nMrs Flintwinch dreamed that she stood behind the door, which was just ajar, and most distinctly heard her husband say these bold words.\n\n‘Flintwinch,’ returned Mrs Clennam, in her usual strong low voice, ‘there is a demon of anger in you. Guard against it.’\n\n‘I don’t care whether there’s one or a dozen,’ said Mr Flintwinch, forcibly suggesting in his tone that the higher number was nearer the mark. ‘If there was fifty, they should all say, None of your nonsense with me, I won’t take it from you--I’d make ‘em say it, whether they liked it or not.’\n\n‘What have I done, you wrathful man?’ her strong voice asked.\n\n‘Done?’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘Dropped down upon me.’\n\n‘If you mean, remonstrated with you--’\n\n‘Don’t put words into my mouth that I don’t mean,’ said Jeremiah, sticking to his figurative expression with tenacious and impenetrable obstinacy: ‘I mean dropped down upon me.’\n\n‘I remonstrated with you,’ she began again, ‘because--’\n\n‘I won’t have it!’ cried Jeremiah. ‘You dropped down upon me.’\n\n‘I dropped down upon you, then, you ill-conditioned man,’ (Jeremiah chuckled at having forced her to adopt his phrase,) ‘for having been needlessly significant to Arthur that morning. I have a right to complain of it as almost a breach of confidence. You did not mean it--’\n\n‘I won’t have it!’ interposed the contradictory Jeremiah, flinging back the concession. ‘I did mean it.’\n\n‘I suppose I must leave you to speak in soliloquy if you choose,’ she replied, after a pause that seemed an angry one. ‘It is useless my addressing myself to a rash and headstrong old man who has a set purpose not to hear me.’\n\n‘Now, I won’t take that from you either,’ said Jeremiah. ‘I have no such purpose. I have told you I did mean it. Do you wish to know why I meant it, you rash and headstrong old woman?’\n\n‘After all, you only restore me my own words,’ she said, struggling with her indignation. ‘Yes.’\n\n‘This is why, then. Because you hadn’t cleared his father to him, and you ought to have done it. Because, before you went into any tantrum about yourself, who are--’\n\n‘Hold there, Flintwinch!’ she cried out in a changed voice: ‘you may go a word too far.’\n\nThe old man seemed to think so. There was another pause, and he had altered his position in the room, when he spoke again more mildly:\n\n‘I was going to tell you why it was. Because, before you took your own part, I thought you ought to have taken the part of Arthur’s father. Arthur’s father! I had no particular love for Arthur’s father. I served Arthur’s father’s uncle, in this house, when Arthur’s father was not much above me--was poorer as far as his pocket went--and when his uncle might as soon have left me his heir as have left him. He starved in the parlour, and I starved in the kitchen; that was the principal difference in our positions; there was not much more than a flight of breakneck stairs between us. I never took to him in those times; I don’t know that I ever took to him greatly at any time. He was an undecided, irresolute chap, who had everything but his orphan life scared out of him when he was young. And when he brought you home here, the wife his uncle had named for him, I didn’t need to look at you twice (you were a good-looking woman at that time) to know who’d be master. You have stood of your own strength ever since. Stand of your own strength now. Don’t lean against the dead.’\n\n‘I do _not_--as you call it--lean against the dead.’\n\n‘But you had a mind to do it, if I had submitted,’ growled Jeremiah, ‘and that’s why you drop down upon me. You can’t forget that I didn’t submit. I suppose you are astonished that I should consider it worth my while to have justice done to Arthur’s father? Hey? It doesn’t matter whether you answer or not, because I know you are, and you know you are. Come, then, I’ll tell you how it is. I may be a bit of an oddity in point of temper, but this is my temper--I can’t let anybody have entirely their own way. You are a determined woman, and a clever woman; and when you see your purpose before you, nothing will turn you from it. Who knows that better than I do?’\n\n‘Nothing will turn me from it, Flintwinch, when I have justified it to myself. Add that.’\n\n‘Justified it to yourself? I said you were the most determined woman on the face of the earth (or I meant to say so), and if you are determined to justify any object you entertain, of course you’ll do it.’\n\n‘Man! I justify myself by the authority of these Books,’ she cried, with stern emphasis, and appearing from the sound that followed to strike the dead-weight of her arm upon the table.\n\n‘Never mind that,’ returned Jeremiah calmly, ‘we won’t enter into that question at present. However that may be, you carry out your purposes, and you make everything go down before them. Now, I won’t go down before them. I have been faithful to you, and useful to you, and I am attached to you. But I can’t consent, and I won’t consent, and I never did consent, and I never will consent to be lost in you. Swallow up everybody else, and welcome. The peculiarity of my temper is, ma’am, that I won’t be swallowed up alive.’\n\nPerhaps this had originally been the mainspring of the understanding between them. Descrying thus much of force of character in Mr Flintwinch, perhaps Mrs Clennam had deemed alliance with him worth her while.\n\n‘Enough and more than enough of the subject,’ said she gloomily.\n\n‘Unless you drop down upon me again,’ returned the persistent Flintwinch, ‘and then you must expect to hear of it again.’\n\nMistress Affery dreamed that the figure of her lord here began walking up and down the room, as if to cool his spleen, and that she ran away; but that, as he did not issue forth when she had stood listening and trembling in the shadowy hall a little time, she crept up-stairs again, impelled as before by ghosts and curiosity, and once more cowered outside the door.\n\n‘Please to light the candle, Flintwinch,’ Mrs Clennam was saying, apparently wishing to draw him back into their usual tone. ‘It is nearly time for tea. Little Dorrit is coming, and will find me in the dark.’\n\nMr Flintwinch lighted the candle briskly, and said as he put it down upon the table:\n\n‘What are you going to do with Little Dorrit? Is she to come to work here for ever? To come to tea here for ever? To come backwards and forwards here, in the same way, for ever?’\n\n‘How can you talk about “for ever” to a maimed creature like me? Are we not all cut down like the grass of the field, and was not I shorn by the scythe many years ago: since when I have been lying here, waiting to be gathered into the barn?’\n\n‘Ay, ay! But since you have been lying here--not near dead--nothing like it--numbers of children and young people, blooming women, strong men, and what not, have been cut down and carried; and still here are you, you see, not much changed after all. Your time and mine may be a long one yet. When I say for ever, I mean (though I am not poetical) through all our time.’ Mr Flintwinch gave this explanation with great calmness, and calmly waited for an answer.\n\n‘So long as Little Dorrit is quiet and industrious, and stands in need of the slight help I can give her, and deserves it; so long, I suppose, unless she withdraws of her own act, she will continue to come here, I being spared.’\n\n‘Nothing more than that?’ said Flintwinch, stroking his mouth and chin.\n\n‘What should there be more than that! What could there be more than that!’ she ejaculated in her sternly wondering way.\n\nMrs Flintwinch dreamed, that, for the space of a minute or two, they remained looking at each other with the candle between them, and that she somehow derived an impression that they looked at each other fixedly.\n\n‘Do you happen to know, Mrs Clennam,’ Affery’s liege lord then demanded in a much lower voice, and with an amount of expression that seemed quite out of proportion to the simple purpose of his words, ‘where she lives?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘Would you--now, would you like to know?’ said Jeremiah with a pounce as if he had sprung upon her.\n\n‘If I cared to know, I should know already. Could I not have asked her any day?’\n\n‘Then you don’t care to know?’\n\n‘I do not.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, having expelled a long significant breath said, with his former emphasis, ‘For I have accidentally--mind!--found out.’\n\n‘Wherever she lives,’ said Mrs Clennam, speaking in one unmodulated hard voice, and separating her words as distinctly as if she were reading them off from separate bits of metal that she took up one by one, ‘she has made a secret of it, and she shall always keep her secret from me.’\n\n‘After all, perhaps you would rather not have known the fact, any how?’ said Jeremiah; and he said it with a twist, as if his words had come out of him in his own wry shape.\n\n‘Flintwinch,’ said his mistress and partner, flashing into a sudden energy that made Affery start, ‘why do you goad me? Look round this room. If it is any compensation for my long confinement within these narrow limits--not that I complain of being afflicted; you know I never complain of that--if it is any compensation to me for long confinement to this room, that while I am shut up from all pleasant change I am also shut up from the knowledge of some things that I may prefer to avoid knowing, why should you, of all men, grudge me that belief?’\n\n‘I don’t grudge it to you,’ returned Jeremiah.\n\n‘Then say no more. Say no more. Let Little Dorrit keep her secret from me, and do you keep it from me also. Let her come and go, unobserved and unquestioned. Let me suffer, and let me have what alleviation belongs to my condition. Is it so much, that you torment me like an evil spirit?’\n\n‘I asked you a question. That’s all.’\n\n‘I have answered it. So, say no more. Say no more.’ Here the sound of the wheeled chair was heard upon the floor, and Affery’s bell rang with a hasty jerk.\n\nMore afraid of her husband at the moment than of the mysterious sound in the kitchen, Affery crept away as lightly and as quickly as she could, descended the kitchen stairs almost as rapidly as she had ascended them, resumed her seat before the fire, tucked up her skirt again, and finally threw her apron over her head. Then the bell rang once more, and then once more, and then kept on ringing; in despite of which importunate summons, Affery still sat behind her apron, recovering her breath.\n\nAt last Mr Flintwinch came shuffling down the staircase into the hall, muttering and calling ‘Affery woman!’ all the way. Affery still remaining behind her apron, he came stumbling down the kitchen stairs, candle in hand, sidled up to her, twitched her apron off, and roused her.\n\n‘Oh Jeremiah!’ cried Affery, waking. ‘What a start you gave me!’\n\n‘What have you been doing, woman?’ inquired Jeremiah. ‘You’ve been rung for fifty times.’\n\n‘Oh Jeremiah,’ said Mistress Affery, ‘I have been a-dreaming!’\n\nReminded of her former achievement in that way, Mr Flintwinch held the candle to her head, as if he had some idea of lighting her up for the illumination of the kitchen.\n\n‘Don’t you know it’s her tea-time?’ he demanded with a vicious grin, and giving one of the legs of Mistress Affery’s chair a kick.\n\n‘Jeremiah? Tea-time? I don’t know what’s come to me. But I got such a dreadful turn, Jeremiah, before I went--off a-dreaming, that I think it must be that.’\n\n‘Yoogh! Sleepy-Head!’ said Mr Flintwinch, ‘what are you talking about?’\n\n‘Such a strange noise, Jeremiah, and such a curious movement. In the kitchen here--just here.’\n\nJeremiah held up his light and looked at the blackened ceiling, held down his light and looked at the damp stone floor, turned round with his light and looked about at the spotted and blotched walls.\n\n‘Rats, cats, water, drains,’ said Jeremiah.\n\nMistress Affery negatived each with a shake of her head. ‘No, Jeremiah; I have felt it before. I have felt it up-stairs, and once on the staircase as I was going from her room to ours in the night--a rustle and a sort of trembling touch behind me.’\n\n‘Affery, my woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch grimly, after advancing his nose to that lady’s lips as a test for the detection of spirituous liquors, ‘if you don’t get tea pretty quick, old woman, you’ll become sensible of a rustle and a touch that’ll send you flying to the other end of the kitchen.’\n\nThis prediction stimulated Mrs Flintwinch to bestir herself, and to hasten up-stairs to Mrs Clennam’s chamber. But, for all that, she now began to entertain a settled conviction that there was something wrong in the gloomy house. Henceforth, she was never at peace in it after daylight departed; and never went up or down stairs in the dark without having her apron over her head, lest she should see something.\n\nWhat with these ghostly apprehensions and her singular dreams, Mrs Flintwinch fell that evening into a haunted state of mind, from which it may be long before this present narrative descries any trace of her recovery. In the vagueness and indistinctness of all her new experiences and perceptions, as everything about her was mysterious to herself she began to be mysterious to others: and became as difficult to be made out to anybody’s satisfaction as she found the house and everything in it difficult to make out to her own.\n\nShe had not yet finished preparing Mrs Clennam’s tea, when the soft knock came to the door which always announced Little Dorrit. Mistress Affery looked on at Little Dorrit taking off her homely bonnet in the hall, and at Mr Flintwinch scraping his jaws and contemplating her in silence, as expecting some wonderful consequence to ensue which would frighten her out of her five wits or blow them all three to pieces.\n\nAfter tea there came another knock at the door, announcing Arthur. Mistress Affery went down to let him in, and he said on entering, ‘Affery, I am glad it’s you. I want to ask you a question.’ Affery immediately replied, ‘For goodness sake don’t ask me nothing, Arthur! I am frightened out of one half of my life, and dreamed out of the other. Don’t ask me nothing! I don’t know which is which, or what is what!’--and immediately started away from him, and came near him no more.\n\nMistress Affery having no taste for reading, and no sufficient light for needlework in the subdued room, supposing her to have the inclination, now sat every night in the dimness from which she had momentarily emerged on the evening of Arthur Clennam’s return, occupied with crowds of wild speculations and suspicions respecting her mistress and her husband and the noises in the house. When the ferocious devotional exercises were engaged in, these speculations would distract Mistress Affery’s eyes towards the door, as if she expected some dark form to appear at those propitious moments, and make the party one too many.\n\nOtherwise, Affery never said or did anything to attract the attention of the two clever ones towards her in any marked degree, except on certain occasions, generally at about the quiet hour towards bed-time, when she would suddenly dart out of her dim corner, and whisper with a face of terror to Mr Flintwinch, reading the paper near Mrs Clennam’s little table:\n\n‘There, Jeremiah! Now! What’s that noise?’\n\nThen the noise, if there were any, would have ceased, and Mr Flintwinch would snarl, turning upon her as if she had cut him down that moment against his will, ‘Affery, old woman, you shall have a dose, old woman, such a dose! You have been dreaming again!’\n\n# CHAPTER 16. Nobody’s Weakness\n\nThe time being come for the renewal of his acquaintance with the Meagles family, Clennam, pursuant to contract made between himself and Mr Meagles within the precincts of Bleeding Heart Yard, turned his face on a certain Saturday towards Twickenham, where Mr Meagles had a cottage-residence of his own. The weather being fine and dry, and any English road abounding in interest for him who had been so long away, he sent his valise on by the coach, and set out to walk. A walk was in itself a new enjoyment to him, and one that had rarely diversified his life afar off.\n\nHe went by Fulham and Putney, for the pleasure of strolling over the heath. It was bright and shining there; and when he found himself so far on his road to Twickenham, he found himself a long way on his road to a number of airier and less substantial destinations. They had risen before him fast, in the healthful exercise and the pleasant road. It is not easy to walk alone in the country without musing upon something. And he had plenty of unsettled subjects to meditate upon, though he had been walking to the Land’s End.\n\nFirst, there was the subject seldom absent from his mind, the question, what he was to do henceforth in life; to what occupation he should devote himself, and in what direction he had best seek it. He was far from rich, and every day of indecision and inaction made his inheritance a source of greater anxiety to him. As often as he began to consider how to increase this inheritance, or to lay it by, so often his misgiving that there was some one with an unsatisfied claim upon his justice, returned; and that alone was a subject to outlast the longest walk. Again, there was the subject of his relations with his mother, which were now upon an equable and peaceful but never confidential footing, and whom he saw several times a week. Little Dorrit was a leading and a constant subject: for the circumstances of his life, united to those of her own story, presented the little creature to him as the only person between whom and himself there were ties of innocent reliance on one hand, and affectionate protection on the other; ties of compassion, respect, unselfish interest, gratitude, and pity. Thinking of her, and of the possibility of her father’s release from prison by the unbarring hand of death--the only change of circumstance he could foresee that might enable him to be such a friend to her as he wished to be, by altering her whole manner of life, smoothing her rough road, and giving her a home--he regarded her, in that perspective, as his adopted daughter, his poor child of the Marshalsea hushed to rest. If there were a last subject in his thoughts, and it lay towards Twickenham, its form was so indefinite that it was little more than the pervading atmosphere in which these other subjects floated before him.\n\nHe had crossed the heath and was leaving it behind when he gained upon a figure which had been in advance of him for some time, and which, as he gained upon it, he thought he knew. He derived this impression from something in the turn of the head, and in the figure’s action of consideration, as it went on at a sufficiently sturdy walk. But when the man--for it was a man’s figure--pushed his hat up at the back of his head, and stopped to consider some object before him, he knew it to be Daniel Doyce.\n\n‘How do you do, Mr Doyce?’ said Clennam, overtaking him. ‘I am glad to see you again, and in a healthier place than the Circumlocution Office.’\n\n‘Ha! Mr Meagles’s friend!’ exclaimed that public criminal, coming out of some mental combinations he had been making, and offering his hand. ‘I am glad to see you, sir. Will you excuse me if I forget your name?’\n\n‘Readily. It’s not a celebrated name. It’s not Barnacle.’\n\n‘No, no,’ said Daniel, laughing. ‘And now I know what it is. It’s Clennam. How do you do, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘I have some hope,’ said Arthur, as they walked on together, ‘that we may be going to the same place, Mr Doyce.’\n\n‘Meaning Twickenham?’ returned Daniel. ‘I am glad to hear it.’\n\nThey were soon quite intimate, and lightened the way with a variety of conversation. The ingenious culprit was a man of great modesty and good sense; and, though a plain man, had been too much accustomed to combine what was original and daring in conception with what was patient and minute in execution, to be by any means an ordinary man. It was at first difficult to lead him to speak about himself, and he put off Arthur’s advances in that direction by admitting slightly, oh yes, he had done this, and he had done that, and such a thing was of his making, and such another thing was his discovery, but it was his trade, you see, his trade; until, as he gradually became assured that his companion had a real interest in his account of himself, he frankly yielded to it. Then it appeared that he was the son of a north-country blacksmith, and had originally been apprenticed by his widowed mother to a lock-maker; that he had ‘struck out a few little things’ at the lock-maker’s, which had led to his being released from his indentures with a present, which present had enabled him to gratify his ardent wish to bind himself to a working engineer, under whom he had laboured hard, learned hard, and lived hard, seven years. His time being out, he had ‘worked in the shop’ at weekly wages seven or eight years more; and had then betaken himself to the banks of the Clyde, where he had studied, and filed, and hammered, and improved his knowledge, theoretical and practical, for six or seven years more. There he had had an offer to go to Lyons, which he had accepted; and from Lyons had been engaged to go to Germany, and in Germany had had an offer to go to St Petersburg, and there had done very well indeed--never better. However, he had naturally felt a preference for his own country, and a wish to gain distinction there, and to do whatever service he could do, there rather than elsewhere. And so he had come home. And so at home he had established himself in business, and had invented and executed, and worked his way on, until, after a dozen years of constant suit and service, he had been enrolled in the Great British Legion of Honour, the Legion of the Rebuffed of the Circumlocution Office, and had been decorated with the Great British Order of Merit, the Order of the Disorder of the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings.\n\n‘It is much to be regretted,’ said Clennam, ‘that you ever turned your thoughts that way, Mr Doyce.’\n\n‘True, sir, true to a certain extent. But what is a man to do? if he has the misfortune to strike out something serviceable to the nation, he must follow where it leads him.’\n\n‘Hadn’t he better let it go?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘He can’t do it,’ said Doyce, shaking his head with a thoughtful smile. ‘It’s not put into his head to be buried. It’s put into his head to be made useful. You hold your life on the condition that to the last you shall struggle hard for it. Every man holds a discovery on the same terms.’\n\n‘That is to say,’ said Arthur, with a growing admiration of his quiet companion, ‘you are not finally discouraged even now?’\n\n‘I have no right to be, if I am,’ returned the other. ‘The thing is as true as it ever was.’\n\nWhen they had walked a little way in silence, Clennam, at once to change the direct point of their conversation and not to change it too abruptly, asked Mr Doyce if he had any partner in his business to relieve him of a portion of its anxieties?\n\n‘No,’ he returned, ‘not at present. I had when I first entered on it, and a good man he was. But he has been dead some years; and as I could not easily take to the notion of another when I lost him, I bought his share for myself and have gone on by myself ever since. And here’s another thing,’ he said, stopping for a moment with a good-humoured laugh in his eyes, and laying his closed right hand, with its peculiar suppleness of thumb, on Clennam’s arm, ‘no inventor can be a man of business, you know.’\n\n‘No?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Why, so the men of business say,’ he answered, resuming the walk and laughing outright. ‘I don’t know why we unfortunate creatures should be supposed to want common sense, but it is generally taken for granted that we do. Even the best friend I have in the world, our excellent friend over yonder,’ said Doyce, nodding towards Twickenham, ‘extends a sort of protection to me, don’t you know, as a man not quite able to take care of himself?’\n\nArthur Clennam could not help joining in the good-humoured laugh, for he recognised the truth of the description.\n\n‘So I find that I must have a partner who is a man of business and not guilty of any inventions,’ said Daniel Doyce, taking off his hat to pass his hand over his forehead, ‘if it’s only in deference to the current opinion, and to uphold the credit of the Works. I don’t think he’ll find that I have been very remiss or confused in my way of conducting them; but that’s for him to say--whoever he is--not for me.’\n\n‘You have not chosen him yet, then?’\n\n‘No, sir, no. I have only just come to a decision to take one. The fact is, there’s more to do than there used to be, and the Works are enough for me as I grow older. What with the books and correspondence, and foreign journeys for which a Principal is necessary, I can’t do all. I am going to talk over the best way of negotiating the matter, if I find a spare half-hour between this and Monday morning, with my--my Nurse and protector,’ said Doyce, with laughing eyes again. ‘He is a sagacious man in business, and has had a good apprenticeship to it.’\n\nAfter this, they conversed on different subjects until they arrived at their journey’s end. A composed and unobtrusive self-sustainment was noticeable in Daniel Doyce--a calm knowledge that what was true must remain true, in spite of all the Barnacles in the family ocean, and would be just the truth, and neither more nor less when even that sea had run dry--which had a kind of greatness in it, though not of the official quality.\n\nAs he knew the house well, he conducted Arthur to it by the way that showed it to the best advantage. It was a charming place (none the worse for being a little eccentric), on the road by the river, and just what the residence of the Meagles family ought to be. It stood in a garden, no doubt as fresh and beautiful in the May of the Year as Pet now was in the May of her life; and it was defended by a goodly show of handsome trees and spreading evergreens, as Pet was by Mr and Mrs Meagles. It was made out of an old brick house, of which a part had been altogether pulled down, and another part had been changed into the present cottage; so there was a hale elderly portion, to represent Mr and Mrs Meagles, and a young picturesque, very pretty portion to represent Pet. There was even the later addition of a conservatory sheltering itself against it, uncertain of hue in its deep-stained glass, and in its more transparent portions flashing to the sun’s rays, now like fire and now like harmless water drops; which might have stood for Tattycoram. Within view was the peaceful river and the ferry-boat, to moralise to all the inmates saying: Young or old, passionate or tranquil, chafing or content, you, thus runs the current always. Let the heart swell into what discord it will, thus plays the rippling water on the prow of the ferry-boat ever the same tune. Year after year, so much allowance for the drifting of the boat, so many miles an hour the flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet, upon this road that steadily runs away; while you, upon your flowing road of time, are so capricious and distracted.\n\nThe bell at the gate had scarcely sounded when Mr Meagles came out to receive them. Mr Meagles had scarcely come out, when Mrs Meagles came out. Mrs Meagles had scarcely come out, when Pet came out. Pet scarcely had come out, when Tattycoram came out. Never had visitors a more hospitable reception.\n\n‘Here we are, you see,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘boxed up, Mr Clennam, within our own home-limits, as if we were never going to expand--that is, travel--again. Not like Marseilles, eh? No allonging and marshonging here!’\n\n‘A different kind of beauty, indeed!’ said Clennam, looking about him.\n\n‘But, Lord bless me!’ cried Mr Meagles, rubbing his hands with a relish, ‘it was an uncommonly pleasant thing being in quarantine, wasn’t it? Do you know, I have often wished myself back again? We were a capital party.’\n\nThis was Mr Meagles’s invariable habit. Always to object to everything while he was travelling, and always to want to get back to it when he was not travelling.\n\n‘If it was summer-time,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘which I wish it was on your account, and in order that you might see the place at its best, you would hardly be able to hear yourself speak for birds. Being practical people, we never allow anybody to scare the birds; and the birds, being practical people too, come about us in myriads. We are delighted to see you, Clennam (if you’ll allow me, I shall drop the Mister); I heartily assure you, we are delighted.’\n\n‘I have not had so pleasant a greeting,’ said Clennam--then he recalled what Little Dorrit had said to him in his own room, and faithfully added ‘except once--since we last walked to and fro, looking down at the Mediterranean.’\n\n‘Ah!’ returned Mr Meagles. ‘Something like a look out, _that_ was, wasn’t it? I don’t want a military government, but I shouldn’t mind a little allonging and marshonging--just a dash of it--in this neighbourhood sometimes. It’s Devilish still.’\n\nBestowing this eulogium on the retired character of his retreat with a dubious shake of the head, Mr Meagles led the way into the house. It was just large enough, and no more; was as pretty within as it was without, and was perfectly well-arranged and comfortable. Some traces of the migratory habits of the family were to be observed in the covered frames and furniture, and wrapped-up hangings; but it was easy to see that it was one of Mr Meagles’s whims to have the cottage always kept, in their absence, as if they were always coming back the day after to-morrow. Of articles collected on his various expeditions, there was such a vast miscellany that it was like the dwelling of an amiable Corsair. There were antiquities from Central Italy, made by the best modern houses in that department of industry; bits of mummy from Egypt (and perhaps Birmingham); model gondolas from Venice; model villages from Switzerland; morsels of tesselated pavement from Herculaneum and Pompeii, like petrified minced veal; ashes out of tombs, and lava out of Vesuvius; Spanish fans, Spezzian straw hats, Moorish slippers, Tuscan hairpins, Carrara sculpture, Trastaverini scarves, Genoese velvets and filigree, Neapolitan coral, Roman cameos, Geneva jewellery, Arab lanterns, rosaries blest all round by the Pope himself, and an infinite variety of lumber. There were views, like and unlike, of a multitude of places; and there was one little picture-room devoted to a few of the regular sticky old Saints, with sinews like whipcord, hair like Neptune’s, wrinkles like tattooing, and such coats of varnish that every holy personage served for a fly-trap, and became what is now called in the vulgar tongue a Catch-em-alive O. Of these pictorial acquisitions Mr Meagles spoke in the usual manner. He was no judge, he said, except of what pleased himself; he had picked them up, dirt-cheap, and people _had_ considered them rather fine. One man, who at any rate ought to know something of the subject, had declared that ‘Sage, Reading’ (a specially oily old gentleman in a blanket, with a swan’s-down tippet for a beard, and a web of cracks all over him like rich pie-crust), to be a fine Guercino. As for Sebastian del Piombo there, you would judge for yourself; if it were not his later manner, the question was, Who was it? Titian, that might or might not be--perhaps he had only touched it. Daniel Doyce said perhaps he hadn’t touched it, but Mr Meagles rather declined to overhear the remark.\n\nWhen he had shown all his spoils, Mr Meagles took them into his own snug room overlooking the lawn, which was fitted up in part like a dressing-room and in part like an office, and in which, upon a kind of counter-desk, were a pair of brass scales for weighing gold, and a scoop for shovelling out money.\n\n‘Here they are, you see,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘I stood behind these two articles five-and-thirty years running, when I no more thought of gadding about than I now think of--staying at home. When I left the Bank for good, I asked for them, and brought them away with me. I mention it at once, or you might suppose that I sit in my counting-house (as Pet says I do), like the king in the poem of the four-and-twenty blackbirds, counting out my money.’\n\nClennam’s eyes had strayed to a natural picture on the wall, of two pretty little girls with their arms entwined. ‘Yes, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, in a lower voice. ‘There they both are. It was taken some seventeen years ago. As I often say to Mother, they were babies then.’\n\n‘Their names?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Ah, to be sure! You have never heard any name but Pet. Pet’s name is Minnie; her sister’s Lillie.’\n\n‘Should you have known, Mr Clennam, that one of them was meant for me?’ asked Pet herself, now standing in the doorway.\n\n‘I might have thought that both of them were meant for you, both are still so like you. Indeed,’ said Clennam, glancing from the fair original to the picture and back, ‘I cannot even now say which is not your portrait.’\n\n‘D’ye hear that, Mother?’ cried Mr Meagles to his wife, who had followed her daughter. ‘It’s always the same, Clennam; nobody can decide. The child to your left is Pet.’\n\nThe picture happened to be near a looking-glass. As Arthur looked at it again, he saw, by the reflection of the mirror, Tattycoram stop in passing outside the door, listen to what was going on, and pass away with an angry and contemptuous frown upon her face, that changed its beauty into ugliness.\n\n‘But come!’ said Mr Meagles. ‘You have had a long walk, and will be glad to get your boots off. As to Daniel here, I suppose he’d never think of taking _his_ boots off, unless we showed him a boot-jack.’\n\n‘Why not?’ asked Daniel, with a significant smile at Clennam.\n\n‘Oh! You have so many things to think about,’ returned Mr Meagles, clapping him on the shoulder, as if his weakness must not be left to itself on any account. ‘Figures, and wheels, and cogs, and levers, and screws, and cylinders, and a thousand things.’\n\n‘In my calling,’ said Daniel, amused, ‘the greater usually includes the less. But never mind, never mind! Whatever pleases you, pleases me.’\n\nClennam could not help speculating, as he seated himself in his room by the fire, whether there might be in the breast of this honest, affectionate, and cordial Mr Meagles, any microscopic portion of the mustard-seed that had sprung up into the great tree of the Circumlocution Office. His curious sense of a general superiority to Daniel Doyce, which seemed to be founded, not so much on anything in Doyce’s personal character as on the mere fact of his being an originator and a man out of the beaten track of other men, suggested the idea. It might have occupied him until he went down to dinner an hour afterwards, if he had not had another question to consider, which had been in his mind so long ago as before he was in quarantine at Marseilles, and which had now returned to it, and was very urgent with it. No less a question than this: Whether he should allow himself to fall in love with Pet?\n\nHe was twice her age. (He changed the leg he had crossed over the other, and tried the calculation again, but could not bring out the total at less.) He was twice her age. Well! He was young in appearance, young in health and strength, young in heart. A man was certainly not old at forty; and many men were not in circumstances to marry, or did not marry, until they had attained that time of life. On the other hand, the question was, not what he thought of the point, but what she thought of it.\n\nHe believed that Mr Meagles was disposed to entertain a ripe regard for him, and he knew that he had a sincere regard for Mr Meagles and his good wife. He could foresee that to relinquish this beautiful only child, of whom they were so fond, to any husband, would be a trial of their love which perhaps they never yet had had the fortitude to contemplate. But the more beautiful and winning and charming she, the nearer they must always be to the necessity of approaching it. And why not in his favour, as well as in another’s?\n\nWhen he had got so far, it came again into his head that the question was, not what they thought of it, but what she thought of it.\n\nArthur Clennam was a retiring man, with a sense of many deficiencies; and he so exalted the merits of the beautiful Minnie in his mind, and depressed his own, that when he pinned himself to this point, his hopes began to fail him. He came to the final resolution, as he made himself ready for dinner, that he would not allow himself to fall in love with Pet.\n\nThere were only five, at a round table, and it was very pleasant indeed. They had so many places and people to recall, and they were all so easy and cheerful together (Daniel Doyce either sitting out like an amused spectator at cards, or coming in with some shrewd little experiences of his own, when it happened to be to the purpose), that they might have been together twenty times, and not have known so much of one another.\n\n‘And Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, after they had recalled a number of fellow-travellers. ‘Has anybody seen Miss Wade?’\n\n‘I have,’ said Tattycoram.\n\nShe had brought a little mantle which her young mistress had sent for, and was bending over her, putting it on, when she lifted up her dark eyes and made this unexpected answer.\n\n‘Tatty!’ her young mistress exclaimed. ‘You seen Miss Wade?--where?’\n\n‘Here, miss,’ said Tattycoram.\n‘How?’\n\nAn impatient glance from Tattycoram seemed, as Clennam saw it, to answer ‘With my eyes!’ But her only answer in words was: ‘I met her near the church.’\n\n‘What was she doing there I wonder!’ said Mr Meagles. ‘Not going to it, I should think.’\n\n‘She had written to me first,’ said Tattycoram.\n\n‘Oh, Tatty!’ murmured her mistress, ‘take your hands away. I feel as if some one else was touching me!’\n\nShe said it in a quick involuntary way, but half playfully, and not more petulantly or disagreeably than a favourite child might have done, who laughed next moment. Tattycoram set her full red lips together, and crossed her arms upon her bosom.\n\n‘Did you wish to know, sir,’ she said, looking at Mr Meagles, ‘what Miss Wade wrote to me about?’\n\n‘Well, Tattycoram,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘since you ask the question, and we are all friends here, perhaps you may as well mention it, if you are so inclined.’\n\n‘She knew, when we were travelling, where you lived,’ said Tattycoram, ‘and she had seen me not quite--not quite--’\n\n‘Not quite in a good temper, Tattycoram?’ suggested Mr Meagles, shaking his head at the dark eyes with a quiet caution. ‘Take a little time--count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’\n\nShe pressed her lips together again, and took a long deep breath.\n\n‘So she wrote to me to say that if I ever felt myself hurt,’ she looked down at her young mistress, ‘or found myself worried,’ she looked down at her again, ‘I might go to her, and be considerately treated. I was to think of it, and could speak to her by the church. So I went there to thank her.’\n\n‘Tatty,’ said her young mistress, putting her hand up over her shoulder that the other might take it, ‘Miss Wade almost frightened me when we parted, and I scarcely like to think of her just now as having been so near me without my knowing it. Tatty dear!’\n\nTatty stood for a moment, immovable.\n\n‘Hey?’ cried Mr Meagles. ‘Count another five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’\n\nShe might have counted a dozen, when she bent and put her lips to the caressing hand. It patted her cheek, as it touched the owner’s beautiful curls, and Tattycoram went away.\n\n‘Now there,’ said Mr Meagles softly, as he gave a turn to the dumb-waiter on his right hand to twirl the sugar towards himself. ‘There’s a girl who might be lost and ruined, if she wasn’t among practical people. Mother and I know, solely from being practical, that there are times when that girl’s whole nature seems to roughen itself against seeing us so bound up in Pet. No father and mother were bound up in her, poor soul. I don’t like to think of the way in which that unfortunate child, with all that passion and protest in her, feels when she hears the Fifth Commandment on a Sunday. I am always inclined to call out, Church, Count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’\n\nBesides his dumb-waiter, Mr Meagles had two other not dumb waiters in the persons of two parlour-maids with rosy faces and bright eyes, who were a highly ornamental part of the table decoration. ‘And why not, you see?’ said Mr Meagles on this head. ‘As I always say to Mother, why not have something pretty to look at, if you have anything at all?’\n\nA certain Mrs Tickit, who was Cook and Housekeeper when the family were at home, and Housekeeper only when the family were away, completed the establishment. Mr Meagles regretted that the nature of the duties in which she was engaged, rendered Mrs Tickit unpresentable at present, but hoped to introduce her to the new visitor to-morrow. She was an important part of the Cottage, he said, and all his friends knew her. That was her picture up in the corner. When they went away, she always put on the silk-gown and the jet-black row of curls represented in that portrait (her hair was reddish-grey in the kitchen), established herself in the breakfast-room, put her spectacles between two particular leaves of Doctor Buchan’s Domestic Medicine, and sat looking over the blind all day until they came back again. It was supposed that no persuasion could be invented which would induce Mrs Tickit to abandon her post at the blind, however long their absence, or to dispense with the attendance of Dr Buchan; the lucubrations of which learned practitioner, Mr Meagles implicitly believed she had never yet consulted to the extent of one word in her life.\n\nIn the evening they played an old-fashioned rubber; and Pet sat looking over her father’s hand, or singing to herself by fits and starts at the piano. She was a spoilt child; but how could she be otherwise? Who could be much with so pliable and beautiful a creature, and not yield to her endearing influence? Who could pass an evening in the house, and not love her for the grace and charm of her very presence in the room? This was Clennam’s reflection, notwithstanding the final conclusion at which he had arrived up-stairs.\n\nIn making it, he revoked. ‘Why, what are you thinking of, my good sir?’ asked the astonished Mr Meagles, who was his partner. ‘I beg your pardon. Nothing,’ returned Clennam. ‘Think of something, next time; that’s a dear fellow,’ said Mr Meagles. Pet laughingly believed he had been thinking of Miss Wade. ‘Why of Miss Wade, Pet?’ asked her father. ‘Why, indeed!’ said Arthur Clennam. Pet coloured a little, and went to the piano again.\n\nAs they broke up for the night, Arthur overheard Doyce ask his host if he could give him half an hour’s conversation before breakfast in the morning? The host replying willingly, Arthur lingered behind a moment, having his own word to add to that topic.\n\n‘Mr Meagles,’ he said, on their being left alone, ‘do you remember when you advised me to go straight to London?’\n\n‘Perfectly well.’\n\n‘And when you gave me some other good advice which I needed at that time?’\n\n‘I won’t say what it was worth,’ answered Mr Meagles: ‘but of course I remember our being very pleasant and confidential together.’\n\n‘I have acted on your advice; and having disembarrassed myself of an occupation that was painful to me for many reasons, wish to devote myself and what means I have, to another pursuit.’\n\n‘Right! You can’t do it too soon,’ said Mr Meagles.\n\n‘Now, as I came down to-day, I found that your friend, Mr Doyce, is looking for a partner in his business--not a partner in his mechanical knowledge, but in the ways and means of turning the business arising from it to the best account.’\n\n‘Just so,’ said Mr Meagles, with his hands in his pockets, and with the old business expression of face that had belonged to the scales and scoop.\n\n‘Mr Doyce mentioned incidentally, in the course of our conversation, that he was going to take your valuable advice on the subject of finding such a partner. If you should think our views and opportunities at all likely to coincide, perhaps you will let him know my available position. I speak, of course, in ignorance of the details, and they may be unsuitable on both sides.’\n\n‘No doubt, no doubt,’ said Mr Meagles, with the caution belonging to the scales and scoop.\n\n‘But they will be a question of figures and accounts--’\n\n‘Just so, just so,’ said Mr Meagles, with arithmetical solidity belonging to the scales and scoop.\n\n‘--And I shall be glad to enter into the subject, provided Mr Doyce responds, and you think well of it. If you will at present, therefore, allow me to place it in your hands, you will much oblige me.’\n\n‘Clennam, I accept the trust with readiness,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘And without anticipating any of the points which you, as a man of business, have of course reserved, I am free to say to you that I think something may come of this. Of one thing you may be perfectly certain. Daniel is an honest man.’\n\n‘I am so sure of it that I have promptly made up my mind to speak to you.’\n\n‘You must guide him, you know; you must steer him; you must direct him; he is one of a crotchety sort,’ said Mr Meagles, evidently meaning nothing more than that he did new things and went new ways; ‘but he is as honest as the sun, and so good night!’\n\nClennam went back to his room, sat down again before his fire, and made up his mind that he was glad he had resolved not to fall in love with Pet. She was so beautiful, so amiable, so apt to receive any true impression given to her gentle nature and her innocent heart, and make the man who should be so happy as to communicate it, the most fortunate and enviable of all men, that he was very glad indeed he had come to that conclusion.\n\nBut, as this might have been a reason for coming to the opposite conclusion, he followed out the theme again a little way in his mind; to justify himself, perhaps.\n\n‘Suppose that a man,’ so his thoughts ran, ‘who had been of age some twenty years or so; who was a diffident man, from the circumstances of his youth; who was rather a grave man, from the tenor of his life; who knew himself to be deficient in many little engaging qualities which he admired in others, from having been long in a distant region, with nothing softening near him; who had no kind sisters to present to her; who had no congenial home to make her known in; who was a stranger in the land; who had not a fortune to compensate, in any measure, for these defects; who had nothing in his favour but his honest love and his general wish to do right--suppose such a man were to come to this house, and were to yield to the captivation of this charming girl, and were to persuade himself that he could hope to win her; what a weakness it would be!’\n\nHe softly opened his window, and looked out upon the serene river. Year after year so much allowance for the drifting of the ferry-boat, so many miles an hour the flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet.\n\nWhy should he be vexed or sore at heart? It was not his weakness that he had imagined. It was nobody’s, nobody’s within his knowledge; why should it trouble him? And yet it did trouble him. And he thought--who has not thought for a moment, sometimes?--that it might be better to flow away monotonously, like the river, and to compound for its insensibility to happiness with its insensibility to pain.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. Nobody’s Rival\n\nBefore breakfast in the morning, Arthur walked out to look about him. As the morning was fine and he had an hour on his hands, he crossed the river by the ferry, and strolled along a footpath through some meadows. When he came back to the towing-path, he found the ferry-boat on the opposite side, and a gentleman hailing it and waiting to be taken over.\n\nThis gentleman looked barely thirty. He was well dressed, of a sprightly and gay appearance, a well-knit figure, and a rich dark complexion. As Arthur came over the stile and down to the water’s edge, the lounger glanced at him for a moment, and then resumed his occupation of idly tossing stones into the water with his foot. There was something in his way of spurning them out of their places with his heel, and getting them into the required position, that Clennam thought had an air of cruelty in it. Most of us have more or less frequently derived a similar impression from a man’s manner of doing some very little thing: plucking a flower, clearing away an obstacle, or even destroying an insentient object.\n\nThe gentleman’s thoughts were preoccupied, as his face showed, and he took no notice of a fine Newfoundland dog, who watched him attentively, and watched every stone too, in its turn, eager to spring into the river on receiving his master’s sign. The ferry-boat came over, however, without his receiving any sign, and when it grounded his master took him by the collar and walked him into it.\n\n‘Not this morning,’ he said to the dog. ‘You won’t do for ladies’ company, dripping wet. Lie down.’\n\nClennam followed the man and the dog into the boat, and took his seat. The dog did as he was ordered. The man remained standing, with his hands in his pockets, and towered between Clennam and the prospect. Man and dog both jumped lightly out as soon as they touched the other side, and went away. Clennam was glad to be rid of them.\n\nThe church clock struck the breakfast hour as he walked up the little lane by which the garden-gate was approached. The moment he pulled the bell a deep loud barking assailed him from within the wall.\n\n‘I heard no dog last night,’ thought Clennam. The gate was opened by one of the rosy maids, and on the lawn were the Newfoundland dog and the man.\n\n‘Miss Minnie is not down yet, gentlemen,’ said the blushing portress, as they all came together in the garden. Then she said to the master of the dog, ‘Mr Clennam, sir,’ and tripped away.\n\n‘Odd enough, Mr Clennam, that we should have met just now,’ said the man. Upon which the dog became mute. ‘Allow me to introduce myself--Henry Gowan. A pretty place this, and looks wonderfully well this morning!’\n\nThe manner was easy, and the voice agreeable; but still Clennam thought, that if he had not made that decided resolution to avoid falling in love with Pet, he would have taken a dislike to this Henry Gowan.\n\n‘It’s new to you, I believe?’ said this Gowan, when Arthur had extolled the place.\n\n‘Quite new. I made acquaintance with it only yesterday afternoon.’\n\n‘Ah! Of course this is not its best aspect. It used to look charming in the spring, before they went away last time. I should like you to have seen it then.’\n\nBut for that resolution so often recalled, Clennam might have wished him in the crater of Mount Etna, in return for this civility.\n\n‘I have had the pleasure of seeing it under many circumstances during the last three years, and it’s--a Paradise.’\n\nIt was (at least it might have been, always excepting for that wise resolution) like his dexterous impudence to call it a Paradise. He only called it a Paradise because he first saw her coming, and so made her out within her hearing to be an angel, Confusion to him!\n\nAnd ah! how beaming she looked, and how glad! How she caressed the dog, and how the dog knew her! How expressive that heightened colour in her face, that fluttered manner, her downcast eyes, her irresolute happiness! When had Clennam seen her look like this? Not that there was any reason why he might, could, would, or should have ever seen her look like this, or that he had ever hoped for himself to see her look like this; but still--when had he ever known her do it!\n\nHe stood at a little distance from them. This Gowan when he had talked about a Paradise, had gone up to her and taken her hand. The dog had put his great paws on her arm and laid his head against her dear bosom. She had laughed and welcomed them, and made far too much of the dog, far, far, too much--that is to say, supposing there had been any third person looking on who loved her.\n\nShe disengaged herself now, and came to Clennam, and put her hand in his and wished him good morning, and gracefully made as if she would take his arm and be escorted into the house. To this Gowan had no objection. No, he knew he was too safe.\n\nThere was a passing cloud on Mr Meagles’s good-humoured face when they all three (four, counting the dog, and he was the most objectionable but one of the party) came in to breakfast. Neither it, nor the touch of uneasiness on Mrs Meagles as she directed her eyes towards it, was unobserved by Clennam.\n\n‘Well, Gowan,’ said Mr Meagles, even suppressing a sigh; ‘how goes the world with you this morning?’\n\n‘Much as usual, sir. Lion and I being determined not to waste anything of our weekly visit, turned out early, and came over from Kingston, my present headquarters, where I am making a sketch or two.’ Then he told how he had met Mr Clennam at the ferry, and they had come over together.\n\n‘Mrs Gowan is well, Henry?’ said Mrs Meagles. (Clennam became attentive.)\n\n‘My mother is quite well, thank you.’ (Clennam became inattentive.) ‘I have taken the liberty of making an addition to your family dinner-party to-day, which I hope will not be inconvenient to you or to Mr Meagles. I couldn’t very well get out of it,’ he explained, turning to the latter. ‘The young fellow wrote to propose himself to me; and as he is well connected, I thought you would not object to my transferring him here.’\n\n‘Who _is_ the young fellow?’ asked Mr Meagles with peculiar complacency.\n\n‘He is one of the Barnacles. Tite Barnacle’s son, Clarence Barnacle, who is in his father’s Department. I can at least guarantee that the river shall not suffer from his visit. He won’t set it on fire.’\n\n‘Aye, aye?’ said Meagles. ‘A Barnacle is he? _We_ know something of that family, eh, Dan? By George, they are at the top of the tree, though! Let me see. What relation will this young fellow be to Lord Decimus now? His Lordship married, in seventeen ninety-seven, Lady Jemima Bilberry, who was the second daughter by the third marriage--no! There I am wrong! That was Lady Seraphina--Lady Jemima was the first daughter by the second marriage of the fifteenth Earl of Stiltstalking with the Honourable Clementina Toozellem. Very well. Now this young fellow’s father married a Stiltstalking and _his_ father married his cousin who was a Barnacle. The father of that father who married a Barnacle, married a Joddleby.--I am getting a little too far back, Gowan; I want to make out what relation this young fellow is to Lord Decimus.’\n\n‘That’s easily stated. His father is nephew to Lord Decimus.’\n\n‘Nephew--to--Lord--Decimus,’ Mr Meagles luxuriously repeated with his eyes shut, that he might have nothing to distract him from the full flavour of the genealogical tree. ‘By George, you are right, Gowan. So he is.’\n\n‘Consequently, Lord Decimus is his great uncle.’\n\n‘But stop a bit!’ said Mr Meagles, opening his eyes with a fresh discovery. ‘Then on the mother’s side, Lady Stiltstalking is his great aunt.’\n\n‘Of course she is.’\n\n‘Aye, aye, aye?’ said Mr Meagles with much interest. ‘Indeed, indeed? We shall be glad to see him. We’ll entertain him as well as we can, in our humble way; and we shall not starve him, I hope, at all events.’\n\nIn the beginning of this dialogue, Clennam had expected some great harmless outburst from Mr Meagles, like that which had made him burst out of the Circumlocution Office, holding Doyce by the collar. But his good friend had a weakness which none of us need go into the next street to find, and which no amount of Circumlocution experience could long subdue in him. Clennam looked at Doyce; but Doyce knew all about it beforehand, and looked at his plate, and made no sign, and said no word.\n\n‘I am much obliged to you,’ said Gowan, to conclude the subject. ‘Clarence is a great ass, but he is one of the dearest and best fellows that ever lived!’\n\nIt appeared, before the breakfast was over, that everybody whom this Gowan knew was either more or less of an ass, or more or less of a knave; but was, notwithstanding, the most lovable, the most engaging, the simplest, truest, kindest, dearest, best fellow that ever lived. The process by which this unvarying result was attained, whatever the premises, might have been stated by Mr Henry Gowan thus: ‘I claim to be always book-keeping, with a peculiar nicety, in every man’s case, and posting up a careful little account of Good and Evil with him. I do this so conscientiously, that I am happy to tell you I find the most worthless of men to be the dearest old fellow too: and am in a condition to make the gratifying report, that there is much less difference than you are inclined to suppose between an honest man and a scoundrel.’ The effect of this cheering discovery happened to be, that while he seemed to be scrupulously finding good in most men, he did in reality lower it where it was, and set it up where it was not; but that was its only disagreeable or dangerous feature.\n\nIt scarcely seemed, however, to afford Mr Meagles as much satisfaction as the Barnacle genealogy had done. The cloud that Clennam had never seen upon his face before that morning, frequently overcast it again; and there was the same shadow of uneasy observation of him on the comely face of his wife. More than once or twice when Pet caressed the dog, it appeared to Clennam that her father was unhappy in seeing her do it; and, in one particular instance when Gowan stood on the other side of the dog, and bent his head at the same time, Arthur fancied that he saw tears rise to Mr Meagles’s eyes as he hurried out of the room. It was either the fact too, or he fancied further, that Pet herself was not insensible to these little incidents; that she tried, with a more delicate affection than usual, to express to her good father how much she loved him; that it was on this account that she fell behind the rest, both as they went to church and as they returned from it, and took his arm. He could not have sworn but that as he walked alone in the garden afterwards, he had an instantaneous glimpse of her in her father’s room, clinging to both her parents with the greatest tenderness, and weeping on her father’s shoulder.\n\nThe latter part of the day turning out wet, they were fain to keep the house, look over Mr Meagles’s collection, and beguile the time with conversation. This Gowan had plenty to say for himself, and said it in an off-hand and amusing manner. He appeared to be an artist by profession, and to have been at Rome some time; yet he had a slight, careless, amateur way with him--a perceptible limp, both in his devotion to art and his attainments--which Clennam could scarcely understand.\n\nHe applied to Daniel Doyce for help, as they stood together, looking out of window.\n\n‘You know Mr Gowan?’ he said in a low voice.\n\n‘I have seen him here. Comes here every Sunday when they are at home.’\n\n‘An artist, I infer from what he says?’\n\n‘A sort of a one,’ said Daniel Doyce, in a surly tone.\n\n‘What sort of a one?’ asked Clennam, with a smile.\n\n‘Why, he has sauntered into the Arts at a leisurely Pall-Mall pace,’ said Doyce, ‘and I doubt if they care to be taken quite so coolly.’\n\nPursuing his inquiries, Clennam found that the Gowan family were a very distant ramification of the Barnacles; and that the paternal Gowan, originally attached to a legation abroad, had been pensioned off as a Commissioner of nothing particular somewhere or other, and had died at his post with his drawn salary in his hand, nobly defending it to the last extremity. In consideration of this eminent public service, the Barnacle then in power had recommended the Crown to bestow a pension of two or three hundred a-year on his widow; to which the next Barnacle in power had added certain shady and sedate apartments in the Palaces at Hampton Court, where the old lady still lived, deploring the degeneracy of the times in company with several other old ladies of both sexes. Her son, Mr Henry Gowan, inheriting from his father, the Commissioner, that very questionable help in life, a very small independence, had been difficult to settle; the rather, as public appointments chanced to be scarce, and his genius, during his earlier manhood, was of that exclusively agricultural character which applies itself to the cultivation of wild oats. At last he had declared that he would become a Painter; partly because he had always had an idle knack that way, and partly to grieve the souls of the Barnacles-in-chief who had not provided for him. So it had come to pass successively, first, that several distinguished ladies had been frightfully shocked; then, that portfolios of his performances had been handed about o’ nights, and declared with ecstasy to be perfect Claudes, perfect Cuyps, perfect phaenomena; then, that Lord Decimus had bought his picture, and had asked the President and Council to dinner at a blow, and had said, with his own magnificent gravity, ‘Do you know, there appears to me to be really immense merit in that work?’ and, in short, that people of condition had absolutely taken pains to bring him into fashion. But, somehow, it had all failed. The prejudiced public had stood out against it obstinately. They had determined not to admire Lord Decimus’s picture. They had determined to believe that in every service, except their own, a man must qualify himself, by striving early and late, and by working heart and soul, might and main. So now Mr Gowan, like that worn-out old coffin which never was Mahomet’s nor anybody else’s, hung midway between two points: jaundiced and jealous as to the one he had left: jaundiced and jealous as to the other that he couldn’t reach.\n\nSuch was the substance of Clennam’s discoveries concerning him, made that rainy Sunday afternoon and afterwards.\n\nAbout an hour or so after dinner time, Young Barnacle appeared, attended by his eye-glass; in honour of whose family connections, Mr Meagles had cashiered the pretty parlour-maids for the day, and had placed on duty in their stead two dingy men. Young Barnacle was in the last degree amazed and disconcerted at sight of Arthur, and had murmured involuntarily, ‘Look here! upon my soul, you know!’ before his presence of mind returned.\n\nEven then, he was obliged to embrace the earliest opportunity of taking his friend into a window, and saying, in a nasal way that was a part of his general debility:\n\n‘I want to speak to you, Gowan. I say. Look here. Who is that fellow?’\n\n‘A friend of our host’s. None of mine.’\n\n‘He’s a most ferocious Radical, you know,’ said Young Barnacle.\n\n‘Is he? How do you know?’\n\n‘Ecod, sir, he was Pitching into our people the other day in the most tremendous manner. Went up to our place and Pitched into my father to that extent that it was necessary to order him out. Came back to our Department, and Pitched into me. Look here. You never saw such a fellow.’\n\n‘What did he want?’\n\n‘Ecod, sir,’ returned Young Barnacle, ‘he said he wanted to know, you know! Pervaded our Department--without an appointment--and said he wanted to know!’\n\nThe stare of indignant wonder with which Young Barnacle accompanied this disclosure, would have strained his eyes injuriously but for the opportune relief of dinner. Mr Meagles (who had been extremely solicitous to know how his uncle and aunt were) begged him to conduct Mrs Meagles to the dining-room. And when he sat on Mrs Meagles’s right hand, Mr Meagles looked as gratified as if his whole family were there.\n\nAll the natural charm of the previous day was gone. The eaters of the dinner, like the dinner itself, were lukewarm, insipid, overdone--and all owing to this poor little dull Young Barnacle. Conversationless at any time, he was now the victim of a weakness special to the occasion, and solely referable to Clennam. He was under a pressing and continual necessity of looking at that gentleman, which occasioned his eye-glass to get into his soup, into his wine-glass, into Mrs Meagles’s plate, to hang down his back like a bell-rope, and be several times disgracefully restored to his bosom by one of the dingy men. Weakened in mind by his frequent losses of this instrument, and its determination not to stick in his eye, and more and more enfeebled in intellect every time he looked at the mysterious Clennam, he applied spoons to his eyes, forks, and other foreign matters connected with the furniture of the dinner-table. His discovery of these mistakes greatly increased his difficulties, but never released him from the necessity of looking at Clennam. And whenever Clennam spoke, this ill-starred young man was clearly seized with a dread that he was coming, by some artful device, round to that point of wanting to know, you know.\n\nIt may be questioned, therefore, whether any one but Mr Meagles had much enjoyment of the time. Mr Meagles, however, thoroughly enjoyed Young Barnacle. As a mere flask of the golden water in the tale became a full fountain when it was poured out, so Mr Meagles seemed to feel that this small spice of Barnacle imparted to his table the flavour of the whole family-tree. In its presence, his frank, fine, genuine qualities paled; he was not so easy, he was not so natural, he was striving after something that did not belong to him, he was not himself. What a strange peculiarity on the part of Mr Meagles, and where should we find another such case!\n\nAt last the wet Sunday wore itself out in a wet night; and Young Barnacle went home in a cab, feebly smoking; and the objectionable Gowan went away on foot, accompanied by the objectionable dog. Pet had taken the most amiable pains all day to be friendly with Clennam, but Clennam had been a little reserved since breakfast--that is to say, would have been, if he had loved her.\n\nWhen he had gone to his own room, and had again thrown himself into the chair by the fire, Mr Doyce knocked at the door, candle in hand, to ask him how and at what hour he proposed returning on the morrow? After settling this question, he said a word to Mr Doyce about this Gowan--who would have run in his head a good deal, if he had been his rival.\n\n‘Those are not good prospects for a painter,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘No,’ returned Doyce.\n\nMr Doyce stood, chamber-candlestick in hand, the other hand in his pocket, looking hard at the flame of his candle, with a certain quiet perception in his face that they were going to say something more.\n\n‘I thought our good friend a little changed, and out of spirits, after he came this morning?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Doyce.\n\n‘But not his daughter?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘No,’ said Doyce.\n\nThere was a pause on both sides. Mr Doyce, still looking at the flame of his candle, slowly resumed:\n\n‘The truth is, he has twice taken his daughter abroad in the hope of separating her from Mr Gowan. He rather thinks she is disposed to like him, and he has painful doubts (I quite agree with him, as I dare say you do) of the hopefulness of such a marriage.’\n\n‘There--’ Clennam choked, and coughed, and stopped.\n\n‘Yes, you have taken cold,’ said Daniel Doyce. But without looking at him.\n\n‘--There is an engagement between them, of course?’ said Clennam airily.\n\n‘No. As I am told, certainly not. It has been solicited on the gentleman’s part, but none has been made. Since their recent return, our friend has yielded to a weekly visit, but that is the utmost. Minnie would not deceive her father and mother. You have travelled with them, and I believe you know what a bond there is among them, extending even beyond this present life. All that there is between Miss Minnie and Mr Gowan, I have no doubt we see.’\n\n‘Ah! We see enough!’ cried Arthur.\n\nMr Doyce wished him Good Night in the tone of a man who had heard a mournful, not to say despairing, exclamation, and who sought to infuse some encouragement and hope into the mind of the person by whom it had been uttered. Such tone was probably a part of his oddity, as one of a crotchety band; for how could he have heard anything of that kind, without Clennam’s hearing it too?\n\nThe rain fell heavily on the roof, and pattered on the ground, and dripped among the evergreens and the leafless branches of the trees. The rain fell heavily, drearily. It was a night of tears.\n\nIf Clennam had not decided against falling in love with Pet; if he had had the weakness to do it; if he had, little by little, persuaded himself to set all the earnestness of his nature, all the might of his hope, and all the wealth of his matured character, on that cast; if he had done this and found that all was lost; he would have been, that night, unutterably miserable. As it was--\n\nAs it was, the rain fell heavily, drearily.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover\n\nLittle Dorrit had not attained her twenty-second birthday without finding a lover. Even in the shallow Marshalsea, the ever young Archer shot off a few featherless arrows now and then from a mouldy bow, and winged a Collegian or two.\n\nLittle Dorrit’s lover, however, was not a Collegian. He was the sentimental son of a turnkey. His father hoped, in the fulness of time, to leave him the inheritance of an unstained key; and had from his early youth familiarised him with the duties of his office, and with an ambition to retain the prison-lock in the family. While the succession was yet in abeyance, he assisted his mother in the conduct of a snug tobacco business round the corner of Horsemonger Lane (his father being a non-resident turnkey), which could usually command a neat connection within the College walls.\n\nYears agone, when the object of his affections was wont to sit in her little arm-chair by the high Lodge-fender, Young John (family name, Chivery), a year older than herself, had eyed her with admiring wonder. When he had played with her in the yard, his favourite game had been to counterfeit locking her up in corners, and to counterfeit letting her out for real kisses. When he grew tall enough to peep through the keyhole of the great lock of the main door, he had divers times set down his father’s dinner, or supper, to get on as it might on the outer side thereof, while he stood taking cold in one eye by dint of peeping at her through that airy perspective.\n\nIf Young John had ever slackened in his truth in the less penetrable days of his boyhood, when youth is prone to wear its boots unlaced and is happily unconscious of digestive organs, he had soon strung it up again and screwed it tight. At nineteen, his hand had inscribed in chalk on that part of the wall which fronted her lodgings, on the occasion of her birthday, ‘Welcome sweet nursling of the Fairies!’ At twenty-three, the same hand falteringly presented cigars on Sundays to the Father of the Marshalsea, and Father of the queen of his soul.\n\nYoung John was small of stature, with rather weak legs and very weak light hair. One of his eyes (perhaps the eye that used to peep through the keyhole) was also weak, and looked larger than the other, as if it couldn’t collect itself. Young John was gentle likewise. But he was great of soul. Poetical, expansive, faithful.\n\nThough too humble before the ruler of his heart to be sanguine, Young John had considered the object of his attachment in all its lights and shades. Following it out to blissful results, he had descried, without self-commendation, a fitness in it. Say things prospered, and they were united. She, the child of the Marshalsea; he, the lock-keeper. There was a fitness in that. Say he became a resident turnkey. She would officially succeed to the chamber she had rented so long. There was a beautiful propriety in that. It looked over the wall, if you stood on tip-toe; and, with a trellis-work of scarlet beans and a canary or so, would become a very Arbour. There was a charming idea in that. Then, being all in all to one another, there was even an appropriate grace in the lock. With the world shut out (except that part of it which would be shut in); with its troubles and disturbances only known to them by hearsay, as they would be described by the pilgrims tarrying with them on their way to the Insolvent Shrine; with the Arbour above, and the Lodge below; they would glide down the stream of time, in pastoral domestic happiness. Young John drew tears from his eyes by finishing the picture with a tombstone in the adjoining churchyard, close against the prison wall, bearing the following touching inscription: ‘Sacred to the Memory Of JOHN CHIVERY, Sixty years Turnkey, and fifty years Head Turnkey, Of the neighbouring Marshalsea, Who departed this life, universally respected, on the thirty-first of December, One thousand eight hundred and eighty-six, Aged eighty-three years. Also of his truly beloved and truly loving wife, AMY, whose maiden name was DORRIT, Who survived his loss not quite forty-eight hours, And who breathed her last in the Marshalsea aforesaid. There she was born, There she lived, There she died.’\n\nThe Chivery parents were not ignorant of their son’s attachment--indeed it had, on some exceptional occasions, thrown him into a state of mind that had impelled him to conduct himself with irascibility towards the customers, and damage the business--but they, in their turns, had worked it out to desirable conclusions. Mrs Chivery, a prudent woman, had desired her husband to take notice that their John’s prospects of the Lock would certainly be strengthened by an alliance with Miss Dorrit, who had herself a kind of claim upon the College and was much respected there. Mrs Chivery had desired her husband to take notice that if, on the one hand, their John had means and a post of trust, on the other hand, Miss Dorrit had family; and that her (Mrs Chivery’s) sentiment was, that two halves made a whole. Mrs Chivery, speaking as a mother and not as a diplomatist, had then, from a different point of view, desired her husband to recollect that their John had never been strong, and that his love had fretted and worrited him enough as it was, without his being driven to do himself a mischief, as nobody couldn’t say he wouldn’t be if he was crossed. These arguments had so powerfully influenced the mind of Mr Chivery, who was a man of few words, that he had on sundry Sunday mornings, given his boy what he termed ‘a lucky touch,’ signifying that he considered such commendation of him to Good Fortune, preparatory to his that day declaring his passion and becoming triumphant. But Young John had never taken courage to make the declaration; and it was principally on these occasions that he had returned excited to the tobacco shop, and flown at the customers.\n\nIn this affair, as in every other, Little Dorrit herself was the last person considered. Her brother and sister were aware of it, and attained a sort of station by making a peg of it on which to air the miserably ragged old fiction of the family gentility. Her sister asserted the family gentility by flouting the poor swain as he loitered about the prison for glimpses of his dear. Tip asserted the family gentility, and his own, by coming out in the character of the aristocratic brother, and loftily swaggering in the little skittle ground respecting seizures by the scruff of the neck, which there were looming probabilities of some gentleman unknown executing on some little puppy not mentioned. These were not the only members of the Dorrit family who turned it to account. No, no. The Father of the Marshalsea was supposed to know nothing about the matter, of course: his poor dignity could not see so low. But he took the cigars, on Sundays, and was glad to get them; and sometimes even condescended to walk up and down the yard with the donor (who was proud and hopeful then), and benignantly to smoke one in his society. With no less readiness and condescension did he receive attentions from Chivery Senior, who always relinquished his arm-chair and newspaper to him, when he came into the Lodge during one of his spells of duty; and who had even mentioned to him, that, if he would like at any time after dusk quietly to step out into the fore-court and take a look at the street, there was not much to prevent him. If he did not avail himself of this latter civility, it was only because he had lost the relish for it; inasmuch as he took everything else he could get, and would say at times, ‘Extremely civil person, Chivery; very attentive man and very respectful. Young Chivery, too; really almost with a delicate perception of one’s position here. A very well conducted family indeed, the Chiveries. Their behaviour gratifies me.’\n\nThe devoted Young John all this time regarded the family with reverence. He never dreamed of disputing their pretensions, but did homage to the miserable Mumbo jumbo they paraded. As to resenting any affront from _her_ brother, he would have felt, even if he had not naturally been of a most pacific disposition, that to wag his tongue or lift his hand against that sacred gentleman would be an unhallowed act. He was sorry that his noble mind should take offence; still, he felt the fact to be not incompatible with its nobility, and sought to propitiate and conciliate that gallant soul. Her father, a gentleman in misfortune--a gentleman of a fine spirit and courtly manners, who always bore with him--he deeply honoured. Her sister he considered somewhat vain and proud, but a young lady of infinite accomplishments, who could not forget the past. It was an instinctive testimony to Little Dorrit’s worth and difference from all the rest, that the poor young fellow honoured and loved her for being simply what she was.\n\nThe tobacco business round the corner of Horsemonger Lane was carried out in a rural establishment one story high, which had the benefit of the air from the yards of Horsemonger Lane jail, and the advantage of a retired walk under the wall of that pleasant establishment. The business was of too modest a character to support a life-size Highlander, but it maintained a little one on a bracket on the door-post, who looked like a fallen Cherub that had found it necessary to take to a kilt.\n\nFrom the portal thus decorated, one Sunday after an early dinner of baked viands, Young John issued forth on his usual Sunday errand; not empty-handed, but with his offering of cigars. He was neatly attired in a plum-coloured coat, with as large a collar of black velvet as his figure could carry; a silken waistcoat, bedecked with golden sprigs; a chaste neckerchief much in vogue at that day, representing a preserve of lilac pheasants on a buff ground; pantaloons so highly decorated with side-stripes that each leg was a three-stringed lute; and a hat of state very high and hard. When the prudent Mrs Chivery perceived that in addition to these adornments her John carried a pair of white kid gloves, and a cane like a little finger-post, surmounted by an ivory hand marshalling him the way that he should go; and when she saw him, in this heavy marching order, turn the corner to the right; she remarked to Mr Chivery, who was at home at the time, that she thought she knew which way the wind blew.\n\nThe Collegians were entertaining a considerable number of visitors that Sunday afternoon, and their Father kept his room for the purpose of receiving presentations. After making the tour of the yard, Little Dorrit’s lover with a hurried heart went up-stairs, and knocked with his knuckles at the Father’s door.\n\n‘Come in, come in!’ said a gracious voice. The Father’s voice, her father’s, the Marshalsea’s father’s. He was seated in his black velvet cap, with his newspaper, three-and-sixpence accidentally left on the table, and two chairs arranged. Everything prepared for holding his Court.\n\n‘Ah, Young John! How do you do, how do you do!’\n\n‘Pretty well, I thank you, sir. I hope you are the same.’\n\n‘Yes, John Chivery; yes. Nothing to complain of.’\n\n‘I have taken the liberty, sir, of--’\n\n‘Eh?’ The Father of the Marshalsea always lifted up his eyebrows at this point, and became amiably distraught and smilingly absent in mind.\n\n‘--A few cigars, sir.’\n\n‘Oh!’ (For the moment, excessively surprised.) ‘Thank you, Young John, thank you. But really, I am afraid I am too--No? Well then, I will say no more about it. Put them on the mantelshelf, if you please, Young John. And sit down, sit down. You are not a stranger, John.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, I am sure--Miss;’ here Young John turned the great hat round and round upon his left-hand, like a slowly twirling mouse-cage; ‘Miss Amy quite well, sir?’\n\n‘Yes, John, yes; very well. She is out.’\n‘Indeed, sir?’\n\n‘Yes, John. Miss Amy is gone for an airing. My young people all go out a good deal. But at their time of life, it’s natural, John.’\n\n‘Very much so, I am sure, sir.’\n\n‘An airing. An airing. Yes.’ He was blandly tapping his fingers on the table, and casting his eyes up at the window. ‘Amy has gone for an airing on the Iron Bridge. She has become quite partial to the Iron Bridge of late, and seems to like to walk there better than anywhere.’ He returned to conversation. ‘Your father is not on duty at present, I think, John?’\n\n‘No, sir, he comes on later in the afternoon.’ Another twirl of the great hat, and then Young John said, rising, ‘I am afraid I must wish you good day, sir.’\n\n‘So soon? Good day, Young John. Nay, nay,’ with the utmost condescension, ‘never mind your glove, John. Shake hands with it on. You are no stranger here, you know.’\n\nHighly gratified by the kindness of his reception, Young John descended the staircase. On his way down he met some Collegians bringing up visitors to be presented, and at that moment Mr Dorrit happened to call over the banisters with particular distinctness, ‘Much obliged to you for your little testimonial, John!’\n\nLittle Dorrit’s lover very soon laid down his penny on the tollplate of the Iron Bridge, and came upon it looking about him for the well-known and well-beloved figure. At first he feared she was not there; but as he walked on towards the Middlesex side, he saw her standing still, looking at the water. She was absorbed in thought, and he wondered what she might be thinking about. There were the piles of city roofs and chimneys, more free from smoke than on week-days; and there were the distant masts and steeples. Perhaps she was thinking about them.\n\nLittle Dorrit mused so long, and was so entirely preoccupied, that although her lover stood quiet for what he thought was a long time, and twice or thrice retired and came back again to the former spot, still she did not move. So, in the end, he made up his mind to go on, and seem to come upon her casually in passing, and speak to her. The place was quiet, and now or never was the time to speak to her.\n\nHe walked on, and she did not appear to hear his steps until he was close upon her. When he said ‘Miss Dorrit!’ she started and fell back from him, with an expression in her face of fright and something like dislike that caused him unutterable dismay. She had often avoided him before--always, indeed, for a long, long while. She had turned away and glided off so often when she had seen him coming toward her, that the unfortunate Young John could not think it accidental. But he had hoped that it might be shyness, her retiring character, her foreknowledge of the state of his heart, anything short of aversion. Now, that momentary look had said, ‘You, of all people! I would rather have seen any one on earth than you!’\n\nIt was but a momentary look, inasmuch as she checked it, and said in her soft little voice, ‘Oh, Mr John! Is it you?’ But she felt what it had been, as he felt what it had been; and they stood looking at one another equally confused.\n\n‘Miss Amy, I am afraid I disturbed you by speaking to you.’\n\n‘Yes, rather. I--I came here to be alone, and I thought I was.’\n\n‘Miss Amy, I took the liberty of walking this way, because Mr Dorrit chanced to mention, when I called upon him just now, that you--’\n\nShe caused him more dismay than before by suddenly murmuring, ‘O father, father!’ in a heartrending tone, and turning her face away.\n\n‘Miss Amy, I hope I don’t give you any uneasiness by naming Mr Dorrit. I assure you I found him very well and in the best of Spirits, and he showed me even more than his usual kindness; being so very kind as to say that I was not a stranger there, and in all ways gratifying me very much.’\n\nTo the inexpressible consternation of her lover, Little Dorrit, with her hands to her averted face, and rocking herself where she stood as if she were in pain, murmured, ‘O father, how can you! O dear, dear father, how can you, can you, do it!’\n\nThe poor fellow stood gazing at her, overflowing with sympathy, but not knowing what to make of this, until, having taken out her handkerchief and put it to her still averted face, she hurried away. At first he remained stock still; then hurried after her.\n\n‘Miss Amy, pray! Will you have the goodness to stop a moment? Miss Amy, if it comes to that, let _me_ go. I shall go out of my senses, if I have to think that I have driven you away like this.’\n\nHis trembling voice and unfeigned earnestness brought Little Dorrit to a stop. ‘Oh, I don’t know what to do,’ she cried, ‘I don’t know what to do!’\n\nTo Young John, who had never seen her bereft of her quiet self-command, who had seen her from her infancy ever so reliable and self-suppressed, there was a shock in her distress, and in having to associate himself with it as its cause, that shook him from his great hat to the pavement. He felt it necessary to explain himself. He might be misunderstood--supposed to mean something, or to have done something, that had never entered into his imagination. He begged her to hear him explain himself, as the greatest favour she could show him.\n\n‘Miss Amy, I know very well that your family is far above mine. It were vain to conceal it. There never was a Chivery a gentleman that ever I heard of, and I will not commit the meanness of making a false representation on a subject so momentous. Miss Amy, I know very well that your high-souled brother, and likewise your spirited sister, spurn me from a height. What I have to do is to respect them, to wish to be admitted to their friendship, to look up at the eminence on which they are placed from my lowlier station--for, whether viewed as tobacco or viewed as the lock, I well know it is lowly--and ever wish them well and happy.’\n\nThere really was a genuineness in the poor fellow, and a contrast between the hardness of his hat and the softness of his heart (albeit, perhaps, of his head, too), that was moving. Little Dorrit entreated him to disparage neither himself nor his station, and, above all things, to divest himself of any idea that she supposed hers to be superior. This gave him a little comfort.\n\n‘Miss Amy,’ he then stammered, ‘I have had for a long time--ages they seem to me--Revolving ages--a heart-cherished wish to say something to you. May I say it?’\n\nLittle Dorrit involuntarily started from his side again, with the faintest shadow of her former look; conquering that, she went on at great speed half across the Bridge without replying!\n\n‘May I--Miss Amy, I but ask the question humbly--may I say it? I have been so unlucky already in giving you pain without having any such intentions, before the holy Heavens! that there is no fear of my saying it unless I have your leave. I can be miserable alone, I can be cut up by myself, why should I also make miserable and cut up one that I would fling myself off that parapet to give half a moment’s joy to! Not that that’s much to do, for I’d do it for twopence.’\n\nThe mournfulness of his spirits, and the gorgeousness of his appearance, might have made him ridiculous, but that his delicacy made him respectable. Little Dorrit learnt from it what to do.\n\n‘If you please, John Chivery,’ she returned, trembling, but in a quiet way, ‘since you are so considerate as to ask me whether you shall say any more--if you please, no.’\n\n‘Never, Miss Amy?’\n\n‘No, if you please. Never.’\n‘O Lord!’ gasped Young John.\n\n‘But perhaps you will let me, instead, say something to you. I want to say it earnestly, and with as plain a meaning as it is possible to express. When you think of us, John--I mean my brother, and sister, and me--don’t think of us as being any different from the rest; for, whatever we once were (which I hardly know) we ceased to be long ago, and never can be any more. It will be much better for you, and much better for others, if you will do that instead of what you are doing now.’\n\nYoung John dolefully protested that he would try to bear it in mind, and would be heartily glad to do anything she wished.\n\n‘As to me,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘think as little of me as you can; the less, the better. When you think of me at all, John, let it only be as the child you have seen grow up in the prison with one set of duties always occupying her; as a weak, retired, contented, unprotected girl. I particularly want you to remember, that when I come outside the gate, I am unprotected and solitary.’\n\nHe would try to do anything she wished. But why did Miss Amy so much want him to remember that?\n\n‘Because,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘I know I can then quite trust you not to forget to-day, and not to say any more to me. You are so generous that I know I can trust to you for that; and I do and I always will. I am going to show you, at once, that I fully trust you. I like this place where we are speaking better than any place I know;’ her slight colour had faded, but her lover thought he saw it coming back just then; ‘and I may be often here. I know it is only necessary for me to tell you so, to be quite sure that you will never come here again in search of me. And I am--quite sure!’\n\nShe might rely upon it, said Young John. He was a miserable wretch, but her word was more than a law for him.\n\n‘And good-bye, John,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘And I hope you will have a good wife one day, and be a happy man. I am sure you will deserve to be happy, and you will be, John.’\n\nAs she held out her hand to him with these words, the heart that was under the waistcoat of sprigs--mere slop-work, if the truth must be known--swelled to the size of the heart of a gentleman; and the poor common little fellow, having no room to hold it, burst into tears.\n\n‘Oh, don’t cry,’ said Little Dorrit piteously. ‘Don’t, don’t! Good-bye, John. God bless you!’\n\n‘Good-bye, Miss Amy. Good-bye!’\n\nAnd so he left her: first observing that she sat down on the corner of a seat, and not only rested her little hand upon the rough wall, but laid her face against it too, as if her head were heavy, and her mind were sad.\n\nIt was an affecting illustration of the fallacy of human projects, to behold her lover, with the great hat pulled over his eyes, the velvet collar turned up as if it rained, the plum-coloured coat buttoned to conceal the silken waistcoat of golden sprigs, and the little direction-post pointing inexorably home, creeping along by the worst back-streets, and composing, as he went, the following new inscription for a tombstone in St George’s Churchyard:\n\n‘Here lie the mortal remains Of JOHN CHIVERY, Never anything worth mentioning, Who died about the end of the year one thousand eight hundred and twenty-six, Of a broken heart, Requesting with his last breath that the word AMY might be inscribed over his ashes, which was accordingly directed to be done, By his afflicted Parents.’\n\n# CHAPTER 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations\n\nThe brothers William and Frederick Dorrit, walking up and down the College-yard--of course on the aristocratic or Pump side, for the Father made it a point of his state to be chary of going among his children on the Poor side, except on Sunday mornings, Christmas Days, and other occasions of ceremony, in the observance whereof he was very punctual, and at which times he laid his hand upon the heads of their infants, and blessed those young insolvents with a benignity that was highly edifying--the brothers, walking up and down the College-yard together, were a memorable sight. Frederick the free, was so humbled, bowed, withered, and faded; William the bond, was so courtly, condescending, and benevolently conscious of a position; that in this regard only, if in no other, the brothers were a spectacle to wonder at.\n\nThey walked up and down the yard on the evening of Little Dorrit’s Sunday interview with her lover on the Iron Bridge. The cares of state were over for that day, the Drawing Room had been well attended, several new presentations had taken place, the three-and-sixpence accidentally left on the table had accidentally increased to twelve shillings, and the Father of the Marshalsea refreshed himself with a whiff of cigar. As he walked up and down, affably accommodating his step to the shuffle of his brother, not proud in his superiority, but considerate of that poor creature, bearing with him, and breathing toleration of his infirmities in every little puff of smoke that issued from his lips and aspired to get over the spiked wall, he was a sight to wonder at.\n\nHis brother Frederick of the dim eye, palsied hand, bent form, and groping mind, submissively shuffled at his side, accepting his patronage as he accepted every incident of the labyrinthian world in which he had got lost. He held the usual screwed bit of whitey-brown paper in his hand, from which he ever and again unscrewed a spare pinch of snuff. That falteringly taken, he would glance at his brother not unadmiringly, put his hands behind him, and shuffle on so at his side until he took another pinch, or stood still to look about him--perchance suddenly missing his clarionet.\n\nThe College visitors were melting away as the shades of night drew on, but the yard was still pretty full, the Collegians being mostly out, seeing their friends to the Lodge. As the brothers paced the yard, William the bond looked about him to receive salutes, returned them by graciously lifting off his hat, and, with an engaging air, prevented Frederick the free from running against the company, or being jostled against the wall. The Collegians as a body were not easily impressible, but even they, according to their various ways of wondering, appeared to find in the two brothers a sight to wonder at.\n\n‘You are a little low this evening, Frederick,’ said the Father of the Marshalsea. ‘Anything the matter?’\n\n‘The matter?’ He stared for a moment, and then dropped his head and eyes again. ‘No, William, no. Nothing is the matter.’\n\n‘If you could be persuaded to smarten yourself up a little, Frederick--’\n\n‘Aye, aye!’ said the old man hurriedly. ‘But I can’t be. I can’t be. Don’t talk so. That’s all over.’\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea glanced at a passing Collegian with whom he was on friendly terms, as who should say, ‘An enfeebled old man, this; but he is my brother, sir, my brother, and the voice of Nature is potent!’ and steered his brother clear of the handle of the pump by the threadbare sleeve. Nothing would have been wanting to the perfection of his character as a fraternal guide, philosopher and friend, if he had only steered his brother clear of ruin, instead of bringing it upon him.\n\n‘I think, William,’ said the object of his affectionate consideration, ‘that I am tired, and will go home to bed.’\n\n‘My dear Frederick,’ returned the other, ‘don’t let me detain you; don’t sacrifice your inclination to me.’\n\n‘Late hours, and a heated atmosphere, and years, I suppose,’ said Frederick, ‘weaken me.’\n\n‘My dear Frederick,’ returned the Father of the Marshalsea, ‘do you think you are sufficiently careful of yourself? Do you think your habits are as precise and methodical as--shall I say as mine are? Not to revert again to that little eccentricity which I mentioned just now, I doubt if you take air and exercise enough, Frederick. Here is the parade, always at your service. Why not use it more regularly than you do?’\n\n‘Hah!’ sighed the other. ‘Yes, yes, yes, yes.’\n\n‘But it is of no use saying yes, yes, my dear Frederick,’ the Father of the Marshalsea in his mild wisdom persisted, ‘unless you act on that assent. Consider my case, Frederick. I am a kind of example. Necessity and time have taught me what to do. At certain stated hours of the day, you will find me on the parade, in my room, in the Lodge, reading the paper, receiving company, eating and drinking. I have impressed upon Amy during many years, that I must have my meals (for instance) punctually. Amy has grown up in a sense of the importance of these arrangements, and you know what a good girl she is.’\n\nThe brother only sighed again, as he plodded dreamily along, ‘Hah! Yes, yes, yes, yes.’\n\n‘My dear fellow,’ said the Father of the Marshalsea, laying his hand upon his shoulder, and mildly rallying him--mildly, because of his weakness, poor dear soul; ‘you said that before, and it does not express much, Frederick, even if it means much. I wish I could rouse you, my good Frederick; you want to be roused.’\n\n‘Yes, William, yes. No doubt,’ returned the other, lifting his dim eyes to his face. ‘But I am not like you.’\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea said, with a shrug of modest self-depreciation, ‘Oh! You might be like me, my dear Frederick; you might be, if you chose!’ and forbore, in the magnanimity of his strength, to press his fallen brother further.\n\nThere was a great deal of leave-taking going on in corners, as was usual on Sunday nights; and here and there in the dark, some poor woman, wife or mother, was weeping with a new Collegian. The time had been when the Father himself had wept, in the shades of that yard, as his own poor wife had wept. But it was many years ago; and now he was like a passenger aboard ship in a long voyage, who has recovered from sea-sickness, and is impatient of that weakness in the fresher passengers taken aboard at the last port. He was inclined to remonstrate, and to express his opinion that people who couldn’t get on without crying, had no business there. In manner, if not in words, he always testified his displeasure at these interruptions of the general harmony; and it was so well understood, that delinquents usually withdrew if they were aware of him.\n\nOn this Sunday evening, he accompanied his brother to the gate with an air of endurance and clemency; being in a bland temper and graciously disposed to overlook the tears. In the flaring gaslight of the Lodge, several Collegians were basking; some taking leave of visitors, and some who had no visitors, watching the frequent turning of the key, and conversing with one another and with Mr Chivery. The paternal entrance made a sensation of course; and Mr Chivery, touching his hat (in a short manner though) with his key, hoped he found himself tolerable.\n\n‘Thank you, Chivery, quite well. And you?’\n\nMr Chivery said in a low growl, ‘Oh! _he_ was all right.’ Which was his general way of acknowledging inquiries after his health when a little sullen.\n\n‘I had a visit from Young John to-day, Chivery. And very smart he looked, I assure you.’\n\nSo Mr Chivery had heard. Mr Chivery must confess, however, that his wish was that the boy didn’t lay out so much money upon it. For what did it bring him in? It only brought him in wexation. And he could get that anywhere for nothing.\n\n‘How vexation, Chivery?’ asked the benignant father.\n\n‘No odds,’ returned Mr Chivery. ‘Never mind. Mr Frederick going out?’\n\n‘Yes, Chivery, my brother is going home to bed. He is tired, and not quite well. Take care, Frederick, take care. Good night, my dear Frederick!’\n\nShaking hands with his brother, and touching his greasy hat to the company in the Lodge, Frederick slowly shuffled out of the door which Mr Chivery unlocked for him. The Father of the Marshalsea showed the amiable solicitude of a superior being that he should come to no harm.\n\n‘Be so kind as to keep the door open a moment, Chivery, that I may see him go along the passage and down the steps. Take care, Frederick! (He is very infirm.) Mind the steps! (He is so very absent.) Be careful how you cross, Frederick. (I really don’t like the notion of his going wandering at large, he is so extremely liable to be run over.)’\n\nWith these words, and with a face expressive of many uneasy doubts and much anxious guardianship, he turned his regards upon the assembled company in the Lodge: so plainly indicating that his brother was to be pitied for not being under lock and key, that an opinion to that effect went round among the Collegians assembled.\n\nBut he did not receive it with unqualified assent; on the contrary, he said, No, gentlemen, no; let them not misunderstand him. His brother Frederick was much broken, no doubt, and it might be more comfortable to himself (the Father of the Marshalsea) to know that he was safe within the walls. Still, it must be remembered that to support an existence there during many years, required a certain combination of qualities--he did not say high qualities, but qualities--moral qualities. Now, had his brother Frederick that peculiar union of qualities? Gentlemen, he was a most excellent man, a most gentle, tender, and estimable man, with the simplicity of a child; but would he, though unsuited for most other places, do for that place? No; he said confidently, no! And, he said, Heaven forbid that Frederick should be there in any other character than in his present voluntary character! Gentlemen, whoever came to that College, to remain there a length of time, must have strength of character to go through a good deal and to come out of a good deal. Was his beloved brother Frederick that man? No. They saw him, even as it was, crushed. Misfortune crushed him. He had not power of recoil enough, not elasticity enough, to be a long time in such a place, and yet preserve his self-respect and feel conscious that he was a gentleman. Frederick had not (if he might use the expression) Power enough to see in any delicate little attentions and--and--Testimonials that he might under such circumstances receive, the goodness of human nature, the fine spirit animating the Collegians as a community, and at the same time no degradation to himself, and no depreciation of his claims as a gentleman. Gentlemen, God bless you!\n\nSuch was the homily with which he improved and pointed the occasion to the company in the Lodge before turning into the sallow yard again, and going with his own poor shabby dignity past the Collegian in the dressing-gown who had no coat, and past the Collegian in the sea-side slippers who had no shoes, and past the stout greengrocer Collegian in the corduroy knee-breeches who had no cares, and past the lean clerk Collegian in buttonless black who had no hopes, up his own poor shabby staircase to his own poor shabby room.\n\nThere, the table was laid for his supper, and his old grey gown was ready for him on his chair-back at the fire. His daughter put her little prayer-book in her pocket--had she been praying for pity on all prisoners and captives!--and rose to welcome him.\n\nUncle had gone home, then? she asked him, as she changed his coat and gave him his black velvet cap. Yes, uncle had gone home. Had her father enjoyed his walk? Why, not much, Amy; not much. No! Did he not feel quite well?\n\nAs she stood behind him, leaning over his chair so lovingly, he looked with downcast eyes at the fire. An uneasiness stole over him that was like a touch of shame; and when he spoke, as he presently did, it was in an unconnected and embarrassed manner.\n\n‘Something, I--hem!--I don’t know what, has gone wrong with Chivery. He is not--ha!--not nearly so obliging and attentive as usual to-night. It--hem!--it’s a little thing, but it puts me out, my love. It’s impossible to forget,’ turning his hands over and over and looking closely at them, ‘that--hem!--that in such a life as mine, I am unfortunately dependent on these men for something every hour in the day.’\n\nHer arm was on his shoulder, but she did not look in his face while he spoke. Bending her head she looked another way.\n\n‘I--hem!--I can’t think, Amy, what has given Chivery offence. He is generally so--so very attentive and respectful. And to-night he was quite--quite short with me. Other people there too! Why, good Heaven! if I was to lose the support and recognition of Chivery and his brother officers, I might starve to death here.’ While he spoke, he was opening and shutting his hands like valves; so conscious all the time of that touch of shame, that he shrunk before his own knowledge of his meaning.\n\n‘I--ha!--I can’t think what it’s owing to. I am sure I cannot imagine what the cause of it is. There was a certain Jackson here once, a turnkey of the name of Jackson (I don’t think you can remember him, my dear, you were very young), and--hem!--and he had a--brother, and this--young brother paid his addresses to--at least, did not go so far as to pay his addresses to--but admired--respectfully admired--the--not daughter, the sister--of one of us; a rather distinguished Collegian; I may say, very much so. His name was Captain Martin; and he consulted me on the question whether it was necessary that his daughter--sister--should hazard offending the turnkey brother by being too--ha!--too plain with the other brother. Captain Martin was a gentleman and a man of honour, and I put it to him first to give me his--his own opinion. Captain Martin (highly respected in the army) then unhesitatingly said that it appeared to him that his--hem!--sister was not called upon to understand the young man too distinctly, and that she might lead him on--I am doubtful whether “lead him on” was Captain Martin’s exact expression: indeed I think he said tolerate him--on her father’s--I should say, brother’s--account. I hardly know how I have strayed into this story. I suppose it has been through being unable to account for Chivery; but as to the connection between the two, I don’t see--’\n\nHis voice died away, as if she could not bear the pain of hearing him, and her hand had gradually crept to his lips. For a little while there was a dead silence and stillness; and he remained shrunk in his chair, and she remained with her arm round his neck and her head bowed down upon his shoulder.\n\nHis supper was cooking in a saucepan on the fire, and, when she moved, it was to make it ready for him on the table. He took his usual seat, she took hers, and he began his meal. They did not, as yet, look at one another. By little and little he began; laying down his knife and fork with a noise, taking things up sharply, biting at his bread as if he were offended with it, and in other similar ways showing that he was out of sorts. At length he pushed his plate from him, and spoke aloud; with the strangest inconsistency.\n\n‘What does it matter whether I eat or starve? What does it matter whether such a blighted life as mine comes to an end, now, next week, or next year? What am I worth to anyone? A poor prisoner, fed on alms and broken victuals; a squalid, disgraced wretch!’\n\n‘Father, father!’ As he rose she went on her knees to him, and held up her hands to him.\n\n‘Amy,’ he went on in a suppressed voice, trembling violently, and looking at her as wildly as if he had gone mad. ‘I tell you, if you could see me as your mother saw me, you wouldn’t believe it to be the creature you have only looked at through the bars of this cage. I was young, I was accomplished, I was good-looking, I was independent--by God I was, child!--and people sought me out, and envied me. Envied me!’\n\n‘Dear father!’ She tried to take down the shaking arm that he flourished in the air, but he resisted, and put her hand away.\n\n‘If I had but a picture of myself in those days, though it was ever so ill done, you would be proud of it, you would be proud of it. But I have no such thing. Now, let me be a warning! Let no man,’ he cried, looking haggardly about, ‘fail to preserve at least that little of the times of his prosperity and respect. Let his children have that clue to what he was. Unless my face, when I am dead, subsides into the long departed look--they say such things happen, I don’t know--my children will have never seen me.’\n\n‘Father, father!’\n\n‘O despise me, despise me! Look away from me, don’t listen to me, stop me, blush for me, cry for me--even you, Amy! Do it, do it! I do it to myself! I am hardened now, I have sunk too low to care long even for that.’\n\n‘Dear father, loved father, darling of my heart!’ She was clinging to him with her arms, and she got him to drop into his chair again, and caught at the raised arm, and tried to put it round her neck.\n\n‘Let it lie there, father. Look at me, father, kiss me, father! Only think of me, father, for one little moment!’\n\nStill he went on in the same wild way, though it was gradually breaking down into a miserable whining.\n\n‘And yet I have some respect here. I have made some stand against it. I am not quite trodden down. Go out and ask who is the chief person in the place. They’ll tell you it’s your father. Go out and ask who is never trifled with, and who is always treated with some delicacy. They’ll say, your father. Go out and ask what funeral here (it must be here, I know it can be nowhere else) will make more talk, and perhaps more grief, than any that has ever gone out at the gate. They’ll say your father’s. Well then. Amy! Amy! Is your father so universally despised? Is there nothing to redeem him? Will you have nothing to remember him by but his ruin and decay? Will you be able to have no affection for him when he is gone, poor castaway, gone?’\n\nHe burst into tears of maudlin pity for himself, and at length suffering her to embrace him and take charge of him, let his grey head rest against her cheek, and bewailed his wretchedness. Presently he changed the subject of his lamentations, and clasping his hands about her as she embraced him, cried, O Amy, his motherless, forlorn child! O the days that he had seen her careful and laborious for him! Then he reverted to himself, and weakly told her how much better she would have loved him if she had known him in his vanished character, and how he would have married her to a gentleman who should have been proud of her as his daughter, and how (at which he cried again) she should first have ridden at his fatherly side on her own horse, and how the crowd (by which he meant in effect the people who had given him the twelve shillings he then had in his pocket) should have trudged the dusty roads respectfully.\n\nThus, now boasting, now despairing, in either fit a captive with the jail-rot upon him, and the impurity of his prison worn into the grain of his soul, he revealed his degenerate state to his affectionate child. No one else ever beheld him in the details of his humiliation. Little recked the Collegians who were laughing in their rooms over his late address in the Lodge, what a serious picture they had in their obscure gallery of the Marshalsea that Sunday night.\n\nThere was a classical daughter once--perhaps--who ministered to her father in his prison as her mother had ministered to her. Little Dorrit, though of the unheroic modern stock and mere English, did much more, in comforting her father’s wasted heart upon her innocent breast, and turning to it a fountain of love and fidelity that never ran dry or waned through all his years of famine.\n\nShe soothed him; asked him for his forgiveness if she had been, or seemed to have been, undutiful; told him, Heaven knows truly, that she could not honour him more if he were the favourite of Fortune and the whole world acknowledged him. When his tears were dried, and he sobbed in his weakness no longer, and was free from that touch of shame, and had recovered his usual bearing, she prepared the remains of his supper afresh, and, sitting by his side, rejoiced to see him eat and drink. For now he sat in his black velvet cap and old grey gown, magnanimous again; and would have comported himself towards any Collegian who might have looked in to ask his advice, like a great moral Lord Chesterfield, or Master of the ethical ceremonies of the Marshalsea.\n\nTo keep his attention engaged, she talked with him about his wardrobe; when he was pleased to say, that Yes, indeed, those shirts she proposed would be exceedingly acceptable, for those he had were worn out, and, being ready-made, had never fitted him. Being conversational, and in a reasonable flow of spirits, he then invited her attention to his coat as it hung behind the door: remarking that the Father of the place would set an indifferent example to his children, already disposed to be slovenly, if he went among them out at elbows. He was jocular, too, as to the heeling of his shoes; but became grave on the subject of his cravat, and promised her that, when she could afford it, she should buy him a new one.\n\nWhile he smoked out his cigar in peace, she made his bed, and put the small room in order for his repose. Being weary then, owing to the advanced hour and his emotions, he came out of his chair to bless her and wish her Good night. All this time he had never once thought of _her_ dress, her shoes, her need of anything. No other person upon earth, save herself, could have been so unmindful of her wants.\n\nHe kissed her many times with ‘Bless you, my love. Good night, my dear!’\n\nBut her gentle breast had been so deeply wounded by what she had seen of him that she was unwilling to leave him alone, lest he should lament and despair again. ‘Father, dear, I am not tired; let me come back presently, when you are in bed, and sit by you.’\n\nHe asked her, with an air of protection, if she felt solitary?\n\n‘Yes, father.’\n\n‘Then come back by all means, my love.’\n\n‘I shall be very quiet, father.’\n\n‘Don’t think of me, my dear,’ he said, giving her his kind permission fully. ‘Come back by all means.’\n\nHe seemed to be dozing when she returned, and she put the low fire together very softly lest she should awake him. But he overheard her, and called out who was that?\n\n‘Only Amy, father.’\n\n‘Amy, my child, come here. I want to say a word to you.’\n\nHe raised himself a little in his low bed, as she kneeled beside it to bring her face near him; and put his hand between hers. O! Both the private father and the Father of the Marshalsea were strong within him then.\n\n‘My love, you have had a life of hardship here. No companions, no recreations, many cares I am afraid?’\n\n‘Don’t think of that, dear. I never do.’\n\n‘You know my position, Amy. I have not been able to do much for you; but all I have been able to do, I have done.’\n\n‘Yes, my dear father,’ she rejoined, kissing him. ‘I know, I know.’\n\n‘I am in the twenty-third year of my life here,’ he said, with a catch in his breath that was not so much a sob as an irrepressible sound of self-approval, the momentary outburst of a noble consciousness. ‘It is all I could do for my children--I have done it. Amy, my love, you are by far the best loved of the three; I have had you principally in my mind--whatever I have done for your sake, my dear child, I have done freely and without murmuring.’\n\nOnly the wisdom that holds the clue to all hearts and all mysteries, can surely know to what extent a man, especially a man brought down as this man had been, can impose upon himself. Enough, for the present place, that he lay down with wet eyelashes, serene, in a manner majestic, after bestowing his life of degradation as a sort of portion on the devoted child upon whom its miseries had fallen so heavily, and whose love alone had saved him to be even what he was.\n\nThat child had no doubts, asked herself no question, for she was but too content to see him with a lustre round his head. Poor dear, good dear, truest, kindest, dearest, were the only words she had for him, as she hushed him to rest.\n\nShe never left him all that night. As if she had done him a wrong which her tenderness could hardly repair, she sat by him in his sleep, at times softly kissing him with suspended breath, and calling him in a whisper by some endearing name. At times she stood aside so as not to intercept the low fire-light, and, watching him when it fell upon his sleeping face, wondered did he look now at all as he had looked when he was prosperous and happy; as he had so touched her by imagining that he might look once more in that awful time. At the thought of that time, she kneeled beside his bed again, and prayed, ‘O spare his life! O save him to me! O look down upon my dear, long-suffering, unfortunate, much-changed, dear dear father!’\n\nNot until the morning came to protect him and encourage him, did she give him a last kiss and leave the small room. When she had stolen down-stairs, and along the empty yard, and had crept up to her own high garret, the smokeless housetops and the distant country hills were discernible over the wall in the clear morning. As she gently opened the window, and looked eastward down the prison yard, the spikes upon the wall were tipped with red, then made a sullen purple pattern on the sun as it came flaming up into the heavens. The spikes had never looked so sharp and cruel, nor the bars so heavy, nor the prison space so gloomy and contracted. She thought of the sunrise on rolling rivers, of the sunrise on wide seas, of the sunrise on rich landscapes, of the sunrise on great forests where the birds were waking and the trees were rustling; and she looked down into the living grave on which the sun had risen, with her father in it three-and-twenty years, and said, in a burst of sorrow and compassion, ‘No, no, I have never seen him in my life!’\n\n# CHAPTER 20. Moving in Society\n\nIf Young John Chivery had had the inclination and the power to write a satire on family pride, he would have had no need to go for an avenging illustration out of the family of his beloved. He would have found it amply in that gallant brother and that dainty sister, so steeped in mean experiences, and so loftily conscious of the family name; so ready to beg or borrow from the poorest, to eat of anybody’s bread, spend anybody’s money, drink from anybody’s cup and break it afterwards. To have painted the sordid facts of their lives, and they throughout invoking the death’s head apparition of the family gentility to come and scare their benefactors, would have made Young John a satirist of the first water.\n\nTip had turned his liberty to hopeful account by becoming a billiard-marker. He had troubled himself so little as to the means of his release, that Clennam scarcely needed to have been at the pains of impressing the mind of Mr Plornish on that subject. Whoever had paid him the compliment, he very readily accepted the compliment with _his_ compliments, and there was an end of it. Issuing forth from the gate on these easy terms, he became a billiard-marker; and now occasionally looked in at the little skittle-ground in a green Newmarket coat (second-hand), with a shining collar and bright buttons (new), and drank the beer of the Collegians.\n\nOne solid stationary point in the looseness of this gentleman’s character was, that he respected and admired his sister Amy. The feeling had never induced him to spare her a moment’s uneasiness, or to put himself to any restraint or inconvenience on her account; but with that Marshalsea taint upon his love, he loved her. The same rank Marshalsea flavour was to be recognised in his distinctly perceiving that she sacrificed her life to her father, and in his having no idea that she had done anything for himself.\n\nWhen this spirited young man and his sister had begun systematically to produce the family skeleton for the overawing of the College, this narrative cannot precisely state. Probably at about the period when they began to dine on the College charity. It is certain that the more reduced and necessitous they were, the more pompously the skeleton emerged from its tomb; and that when there was anything particularly shabby in the wind, the skeleton always came out with the ghastliest flourish.\n\nLittle Dorrit was late on the Monday morning, for her father slept late, and afterwards there was his breakfast to prepare and his room to arrange. She had no engagement to go out to work, however, and therefore stayed with him until, with Maggy’s help, she had put everything right about him, and had seen him off upon his morning walk (of twenty yards or so) to the coffee-house to read the paper. She then got on her bonnet and went out, having been anxious to get out much sooner. There was, as usual, a cessation of the small-talk in the Lodge as she passed through it; and a Collegian who had come in on Saturday night, received the intimation from the elbow of a more seasoned Collegian, ‘Look out. Here she is!’\n\nShe wanted to see her sister, but when she got round to Mr Cripples’s, she found that both her sister and her uncle had gone to the theatre where they were engaged. Having taken thought of this probability by the way, and having settled that in such case she would follow them, she set off afresh for the theatre, which was on that side of the river, and not very far away.\n\nLittle Dorrit was almost as ignorant of the ways of theatres as of the ways of gold mines, and when she was directed to a furtive sort of door, with a curious up-all-night air about it, that appeared to be ashamed of itself and to be hiding in an alley, she hesitated to approach it; being further deterred by the sight of some half-dozen close-shaved gentlemen with their hats very strangely on, who were lounging about the door, looking not at all unlike Collegians. On her applying to them, reassured by this resemblance, for a direction to Miss Dorrit, they made way for her to enter a dark hall--it was more like a great grim lamp gone out than anything else--where she could hear the distant playing of music and the sound of dancing feet. A man so much in want of airing that he had a blue mould upon him, sat watching this dark place from a hole in a corner, like a spider; and he told her that he would send a message up to Miss Dorrit by the first lady or gentleman who went through. The first lady who went through had a roll of music, half in her muff and half out of it, and was in such a tumbled condition altogether, that it seemed as if it would be an act of kindness to iron her. But as she was very good-natured, and said, ‘Come with me; I’ll soon find Miss Dorrit for you,’ Miss Dorrit’s sister went with her, drawing nearer and nearer at every step she took in the darkness to the sound of music and the sound of dancing feet.\n\nAt last they came into a maze of dust, where a quantity of people were tumbling over one another, and where there was such a confusion of unaccountable shapes of beams, bulkheads, brick walls, ropes, and rollers, and such a mixing of gaslight and daylight, that they seemed to have got on the wrong side of the pattern of the universe. Little Dorrit, left to herself, and knocked against by somebody every moment, was quite bewildered, when she heard her sister’s voice.\n\n‘Why, good gracious, Amy, what ever brought you here?’\n\n‘I wanted to see you, Fanny dear; and as I am going out all day to-morrow, and knew you might be engaged all day to-day, I thought--’\n\n‘But the idea, Amy, of _you_ coming behind! I never did!’ As her sister said this in no very cordial tone of welcome, she conducted her to a more open part of the maze, where various golden chairs and tables were heaped together, and where a number of young ladies were sitting on anything they could find, chattering. All these young ladies wanted ironing, and all had a curious way of looking everywhere while they chattered.\n\nJust as the sisters arrived here, a monotonous boy in a Scotch cap put his head round a beam on the left, and said, ‘Less noise there, ladies!’ and disappeared. Immediately after which, a sprightly gentleman with a quantity of long black hair looked round a beam on the right, and said, ‘Less noise there, darlings!’ and also disappeared.\n\n‘The notion of you among professionals, Amy, is really the last thing I could have conceived!’ said her sister. ‘Why, how did you ever get here?’\n\n‘I don’t know. The lady who told you I was here, was so good as to bring me in.’\n\n‘Like you quiet little things! You can make your way anywhere, I believe. _I_ couldn’t have managed it, Amy, though I know so much more of the world.’\n\nIt was the family custom to lay it down as family law, that she was a plain domestic little creature, without the great and sage experience of the rest. This family fiction was the family assertion of itself against her services. Not to make too much of them.\n\n‘Well! And what have you got on your mind, Amy? Of course you have got something on your mind about me?’ said Fanny. She spoke as if her sister, between two and three years her junior, were her prejudiced grandmother.\n\n‘It is not much; but since you told me of the lady who gave you the bracelet, Fanny--’\n\nThe monotonous boy put his head round the beam on the left, and said, ‘Look out there, ladies!’ and disappeared. The sprightly gentleman with the black hair as suddenly put his head round the beam on the right, and said, ‘Look out there, darlings!’ and also disappeared. Thereupon all the young ladies rose and began shaking their skirts out behind.\n\n‘Well, Amy?’ said Fanny, doing as the rest did; ‘what were you going to say?’\n\n‘Since you told me a lady had given you the bracelet you showed me, Fanny, I have not been quite easy on your account, and indeed want to know a little more if you will confide more to me.’\n\n‘Now, ladies!’ said the boy in the Scotch cap. ‘Now, darlings!’ said the gentleman with the black hair. They were every one gone in a moment, and the music and the dancing feet were heard again.\n\nLittle Dorrit sat down in a golden chair, made quite giddy by these rapid interruptions. Her sister and the rest were a long time gone; and during their absence a voice (it appeared to be that of the gentleman with the black hair) was continually calling out through the music, ‘One, two, three, four, five, six--go! One, two, three, four, five, six--go! Steady, darlings! One, two, three, four, five, six--go!’ Ultimately the voice stopped, and they all came back again, more or less out of breath, folding themselves in their shawls, and making ready for the streets. ‘Stop a moment, Amy, and let them get away before us,’ whispered Fanny. They were soon left alone; nothing more important happening, in the meantime, than the boy looking round his old beam, and saying, ‘Everybody at eleven to-morrow, ladies!’ and the gentleman with the black hair looking round his old beam, and saying, ‘Everybody at eleven to-morrow, darlings!’ each in his own accustomed manner.\n\nWhen they were alone, something was rolled up or by other means got out of the way, and there was a great empty well before them, looking down into the depths of which Fanny said, ‘Now, uncle!’ Little Dorrit, as her eyes became used to the darkness, faintly made him out at the bottom of the well, in an obscure corner by himself, with his instrument in its ragged case under his arm.\n\nThe old man looked as if the remote high gallery windows, with their little strip of sky, might have been the point of his better fortunes, from which he had descended, until he had gradually sunk down below there to the bottom. He had been in that place six nights a week for many years, but had never been observed to raise his eyes above his music-book, and was confidently believed to have never seen a play. There were legends in the place that he did not so much as know the popular heroes and heroines by sight, and that the low comedian had ‘mugged’ at him in his richest manner fifty nights for a wager, and he had shown no trace of consciousness. The carpenters had a joke to the effect that he was dead without being aware of it; and the frequenters of the pit supposed him to pass his whole life, night and day, and Sunday and all, in the orchestra. They had tried him a few times with pinches of snuff offered over the rails, and he had always responded to this attention with a momentary waking up of manner that had the pale phantom of a gentleman in it: beyond this he never, on any occasion, had any other part in what was going on than the part written out for the clarionet; in private life, where there was no part for the clarionet, he had no part at all. Some said he was poor, some said he was a wealthy miser; but he said nothing, never lifted up his bowed head, never varied his shuffling gait by getting his springless foot from the ground. Though expecting now to be summoned by his niece, he did not hear her until she had spoken to him three or four times; nor was he at all surprised by the presence of two nieces instead of one, but merely said in his tremulous voice, ‘I am coming, I am coming!’ and crept forth by some underground way which emitted a cellarous smell.\n\n‘And so, Amy,’ said her sister, when the three together passed out at the door that had such a shame-faced consciousness of being different from other doors: the uncle instinctively taking Amy’s arm as the arm to be relied on: ‘so, Amy, you are curious about me?’\n\nShe was pretty, and conscious, and rather flaunting; and the condescension with which she put aside the superiority of her charms, and of her worldly experience, and addressed her sister on almost equal terms, had a vast deal of the family in it.\n\n‘I am interested, Fanny, and concerned in anything that concerns you.’\n\n‘So you are, so you are, and you are the best of Amys. If I am ever a little provoking, I am sure you’ll consider what a thing it is to occupy my position and feel a consciousness of being superior to it. I shouldn’t care,’ said the Daughter of the Father of the Marshalsea, ‘if the others were not so common. None of them have come down in the world as we have. They are all on their own level. Common.’\n\nLittle Dorrit mildly looked at the speaker, but did not interrupt her. Fanny took out her handkerchief, and rather angrily wiped her eyes. ‘I was not born where you were, you know, Amy, and perhaps that makes a difference. My dear child, when we get rid of Uncle, you shall know all about it. We’ll drop him at the cook’s shop where he is going to dine.’\n\nThey walked on with him until they came to a dirty shop window in a dirty street, which was made almost opaque by the steam of hot meats, vegetables, and puddings. But glimpses were to be caught of a roast leg of pork bursting into tears of sage and onion in a metal reservoir full of gravy, of an unctuous piece of roast beef and blisterous Yorkshire pudding, bubbling hot in a similar receptacle, of a stuffed fillet of veal in rapid cut, of a ham in a perspiration with the pace it was going at, of a shallow tank of baked potatoes glued together by their own richness, of a truss or two of boiled greens, and other substantial delicacies. Within, were a few wooden partitions, behind which such customers as found it more convenient to take away their dinners in stomachs than in their hands, Packed their purchases in solitude. Fanny opening her reticule, as they surveyed these things, produced from that repository a shilling and handed it to Uncle. Uncle, after not looking at it a little while, divined its object, and muttering ‘Dinner? Ha! Yes, yes, yes!’ slowly vanished from them into the mist.\n\n‘Now, Amy,’ said her sister, ‘come with me, if you are not too tired to walk to Harley Street, Cavendish Square.’\n\nThe air with which she threw off this distinguished address and the toss she gave to her new bonnet (which was more gauzy than serviceable), made her sister wonder; however, she expressed her readiness to go to Harley Street, and thither they directed their steps. Arrived at that grand destination, Fanny singled out the handsomest house, and knocking at the door, inquired for Mrs Merdle. The footman who opened the door, although he had powder on his head and was backed up by two other footmen likewise powdered, not only admitted Mrs Merdle to be at home, but asked Fanny to walk in. Fanny walked in, taking her sister with her; and they went up-stairs with powder going before and powder stopping behind, and were left in a spacious semicircular drawing-room, one of several drawing-rooms, where there was a parrot on the outside of a golden cage holding on by its beak, with its scaly legs in the air, and putting itself into many strange upside-down postures. This peculiarity has been observed in birds of quite another feather, climbing upon golden wires.\n\nThe room was far more splendid than anything Little Dorrit had ever imagined, and would have been splendid and costly in any eyes. She looked in amazement at her sister and would have asked a question, but that Fanny with a warning frown pointed to a curtained doorway of communication with another room. The curtain shook next moment, and a lady, raising it with a heavily ringed hand, dropped it behind her again as she entered.\n\nThe lady was not young and fresh from the hand of Nature, but was young and fresh from the hand of her maid. She had large unfeeling handsome eyes, and dark unfeeling handsome hair, and a broad unfeeling handsome bosom, and was made the most of in every particular. Either because she had a cold, or because it suited her face, she wore a rich white fillet tied over her head and under her chin. And if ever there were an unfeeling handsome chin that looked as if, for certain, it had never been, in familiar parlance, ‘chucked’ by the hand of man, it was the chin curbed up so tight and close by that laced bridle.\n\n‘Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny. ‘My sister, ma’am.’\n\n‘I am glad to see your sister, Miss Dorrit. I did not remember that you had a sister.’\n\n‘I did not mention that I had,’ said Fanny.\n\n‘Ah!’ Mrs Merdle curled the little finger of her left hand as who should say, ‘I have caught you. I know you didn’t!’ All her action was usually with her left hand because her hands were not a pair; and left being much the whiter and plumper of the two. Then she added: ‘Sit down,’ and composed herself voluptuously, in a nest of crimson and gold cushions, on an ottoman near the parrot.\n\n‘Also professional?’ said Mrs Merdle, looking at Little Dorrit through an eye-glass.\n\nFanny answered No. ‘No,’ said Mrs Merdle, dropping her glass. ‘Has not a professional air. Very pleasant; but not professional.’\n\n‘My sister, ma’am,’ said Fanny, in whom there was a singular mixture of deference and hardihood, ‘has been asking me to tell her, as between sisters, how I came to have the honour of knowing you. And as I had engaged to call upon you once more, I thought I might take the liberty of bringing her with me, when perhaps you would tell her. I wish her to know, and perhaps you will tell her?’\n\n‘Do you think, at your sister’s age--’ hinted Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘She is much older than she looks,’ said Fanny; ‘almost as old as I am.’\n\n‘Society,’ said Mrs Merdle, with another curve of her little finger, ‘is so difficult to explain to young persons (indeed is so difficult to explain to most persons), that I am glad to hear that. I wish Society was not so arbitrary, I wish it was not so exacting--Bird, be quiet!’\n\nThe parrot had given a most piercing shriek, as if its name were Society and it asserted its right to its exactions.\n\n‘But,’ resumed Mrs Merdle, ‘we must take it as we find it. We know it is hollow and conventional and worldly and very shocking, but unless we are Savages in the Tropical seas (I should have been charmed to be one myself--most delightful life and perfect climate, I am told), we must consult it. It is the common lot. Mr Merdle is a most extensive merchant, his transactions are on the vastest scale, his wealth and influence are very great, but even he--Bird, be quiet!’\n\nThe parrot had shrieked another shriek; and it filled up the sentence so expressively that Mrs Merdle was under no necessity to end it.\n\n‘Since your sister begs that I would terminate our personal acquaintance,’ she began again, addressing Little Dorrit, ‘by relating the circumstances that are much to her credit, I cannot object to comply with her request, I am sure. I have a son (I was first married extremely young) of two or three-and-twenty.’\n\nFanny set her lips, and her eyes looked half triumphantly at her sister.\n\n‘A son of two or three-and-twenty. He is a little gay, a thing Society is accustomed to in young men, and he is very impressible. Perhaps he inherits that misfortune. I am very impressible myself, by nature. The weakest of creatures--my feelings are touched in a moment.’\n\nShe said all this, and everything else, as coldly as a woman of snow; quite forgetting the sisters except at odd times, and apparently addressing some abstraction of Society; for whose behoof, too, she occasionally arranged her dress, or the composition of her figure upon the ottoman.\n\n‘So he is very impressible. Not a misfortune in our natural state I dare say, but we are not in a natural state. Much to be lamented, no doubt, particularly by myself, who am a child of nature if I could but show it; but so it is. Society suppresses us and dominates us--Bird, be quiet!’\n\nThe parrot had broken into a violent fit of laughter, after twisting divers bars of his cage with his crooked bill, and licking them with his black tongue.\n\n‘It is quite unnecessary to say to a person of your good sense, wide range of experience, and cultivated feeling,’ said Mrs Merdle from her nest of crimson and gold--and there put up her glass to refresh her memory as to whom she was addressing,--‘that the stage sometimes has a fascination for young men of that class of character. In saying the stage, I mean the people on it of the female sex. Therefore, when I heard that my son was supposed to be fascinated by a dancer, I knew what that usually meant in Society, and confided in her being a dancer at the Opera, where young men moving in Society are usually fascinated.’\n\nShe passed her white hands over one another, observant of the sisters now; and the rings upon her fingers grated against each other with a hard sound.\n\n‘As your sister will tell you, when I found what the theatre was I was much surprised and much distressed. But when I found that your sister, by rejecting my son’s advances (I must add, in an unexpected manner), had brought him to the point of proposing marriage, my feelings were of the profoundest anguish--acute.’\n\nShe traced the outline of her left eyebrow, and put it right.\n\n‘In a distracted condition, which only a mother--moving in Society--can be susceptible of, I determined to go myself to the theatre, and represent my state of mind to the dancer. I made myself known to your sister. I found her, to my surprise, in many respects different from my expectations; and certainly in none more so, than in meeting me with--what shall I say--a sort of family assertion on her own part?’ Mrs Merdle smiled.\n\n‘I told you, ma’am,’ said Fanny, with a heightening colour, ‘that although you found me in that situation, I was so far above the rest, that I considered my family as good as your son’s; and that I had a brother who, knowing the circumstances, would be of the same opinion, and would not consider such a connection any honour.’\n\n‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Mrs Merdle, after frostily looking at her through her glass, ‘precisely what I was on the point of telling your sister, in pursuance of your request. Much obliged to you for recalling it so accurately and anticipating me. I immediately,’ addressing Little Dorrit, ‘(for I am the creature of impulse), took a bracelet from my arm, and begged your sister to let me clasp it on hers, in token of the delight I had in our being able to approach the subject so far on a common footing.’ (This was perfectly true, the lady having bought a cheap and showy article on her way to the interview, with a general eye to bribery.)\n\n‘And I told you, Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny, ‘that we might be unfortunate, but we are not common.’\n\n‘I think, the very words, Miss Dorrit,’ assented Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘And I told you, Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny, ‘that if you spoke to me of the superiority of your son’s standing in Society, it was barely possible that you rather deceived yourself in your suppositions about my origin; and that my father’s standing, even in the Society in which he now moved (what that was, was best known to myself), was eminently superior, and was acknowledged by every one.’\n\n‘Quite accurate,’ rejoined Mrs Merdle. ‘A most admirable memory.’\n\n‘Thank you, ma’am. Perhaps you will be so kind as to tell my sister the rest.’\n\n‘There is very little to tell,’ said Mrs Merdle, reviewing the breadth of bosom which seemed essential to her having room enough to be unfeeling in, ‘but it is to your sister’s credit. I pointed out to your sister the plain state of the case; the impossibility of the Society in which we moved recognising the Society in which she moved--though charming, I have no doubt; the immense disadvantage at which she would consequently place the family she had so high an opinion of, upon which we should find ourselves compelled to look down with contempt, and from which (socially speaking) we should feel obliged to recoil with abhorrence. In short, I made an appeal to that laudable pride in your sister.’\n\n‘Let my sister know, if you please, Mrs Merdle,’ Fanny pouted, with a toss of her gauzy bonnet, ‘that I had already had the honour of telling your son that I wished to have nothing whatever to say to him.’\n\n‘Well, Miss Dorrit,’ assented Mrs Merdle, ‘perhaps I might have mentioned that before. If I did not think of it, perhaps it was because my mind reverted to the apprehensions I had at the time that he might persevere and you might have something to say to him. I also mentioned to your sister--I again address the non-professional Miss Dorrit--that my son would have nothing in the event of such a marriage, and would be an absolute beggar. (I mention that merely as a fact which is part of the narrative, and not as supposing it to have influenced your sister, except in the prudent and legitimate way in which, constituted as our artificial system is, we must all be influenced by such considerations.) Finally, after some high words and high spirit on the part of your sister, we came to the complete understanding that there was no danger; and your sister was so obliging as to allow me to present her with a mark or two of my appreciation at my dressmaker’s.’\n\nLittle Dorrit looked sorry, and glanced at Fanny with a troubled face.\n\n‘Also,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘as to promise to give me the present pleasure of a closing interview, and of parting with her on the best of terms. On which occasion,’ added Mrs Merdle, quitting her nest, and putting something in Fanny’s hand, ‘Miss Dorrit will permit me to say Farewell with best wishes in my own dull manner.’\n\nThe sisters rose at the same time, and they all stood near the cage of the parrot, as he tore at a claw-full of biscuit and spat it out, seemed to mock them with a pompous dance of his body without moving his feet, and suddenly turned himself upside down and trailed himself all over the outside of his golden cage, with the aid of his cruel beak and black tongue.\n\n‘Adieu, Miss Dorrit, with best wishes,’ said Mrs Merdle. ‘If we could only come to a Millennium, or something of that sort, I for one might have the pleasure of knowing a number of charming and talented persons from whom I am at present excluded. A more primitive state of society would be delicious to me. There used to be a poem when I learnt lessons, something about Lo the poor Indians whose something mind! If a few thousand persons moving in Society, could only go and be Indians, I would put my name down directly; but as, moving in Society, we can’t be Indians, unfortunately--Good morning!’\n\nThey came down-stairs with powder before them and powder behind, the elder sister haughty and the younger sister humbled, and were shut out into unpowdered Harley Street, Cavendish Square.\n\n‘Well?’ said Fanny, when they had gone a little way without speaking. ‘Have you nothing to say, Amy?’\n\n‘Oh, I don’t know what to say!’ she answered, distressed. ‘You didn’t like this young man, Fanny?’\n\n‘Like him? He is almost an idiot.’\n\n‘I am so sorry--don’t be hurt--but, since you ask me what I have to say, I am so very sorry, Fanny, that you suffered this lady to give you anything.’\n\n‘You little Fool!’ returned her sister, shaking her with the sharp pull she gave her arm. ‘Have you no spirit at all? But that’s just the way! You have no self-respect, you have no becoming pride, just as you allow yourself to be followed about by a contemptible little Chivery of a thing,’ with the scornfullest emphasis, ‘you would let your family be trodden on, and never turn.’\n\n‘Don’t say that, dear Fanny. I do what I can for them.’\n\n‘You do what you can for them!’ repeated Fanny, walking her on very fast. ‘Would you let a woman like this, whom you could see, if you had any experience of anything, to be as false and insolent as a woman can be--would you let her put her foot upon your family, and thank her for it?’\n\n‘No, Fanny, I am sure.’\n\n‘Then make her pay for it, you mean little thing. What else can you make her do? Make her pay for it, you stupid child; and do your family some credit with the money!’\n\nThey spoke no more all the way back to the lodging where Fanny and her uncle lived. When they arrived there, they found the old man practising his clarionet in the dolefullest manner in a corner of the room. Fanny had a composite meal to make, of chops, and porter, and tea; and indignantly pretended to prepare it for herself, though her sister did all that in quiet reality. When at last Fanny sat down to eat and drink, she threw the table implements about and was angry with her bread, much as her father had been last night.\n\n‘If you despise me,’ she said, bursting into vehement tears, ‘because I am a dancer, why did you put me in the way of being one? It was your doing. You would have me stoop as low as the ground before this Mrs Merdle, and let her say what she liked and do what she liked, and hold us all in contempt, and tell me so to my face. Because I am a dancer!’\n\n‘O Fanny!’\n\n‘And Tip, too, poor fellow. She is to disparage him just as much as she likes, without any check--I suppose because he has been in the law, and the docks, and different things. Why, it was your doing, Amy. You might at least approve of his being defended.’\n\nAll this time the uncle was dolefully blowing his clarionet in the corner, sometimes taking it an inch or so from his mouth for a moment while he stopped to gaze at them, with a vague impression that somebody had said something.\n\n‘And your father, your poor father, Amy. Because he is not free to show himself and to speak for himself, you would let such people insult him with impunity. If you don’t feel for yourself because you go out to work, you might at least feel for him, I should think, knowing what he has undergone so long.’\n\nPoor Little Dorrit felt the injustice of this taunt rather sharply. The remembrance of last night added a barbed point to it. She said nothing in reply, but turned her chair from the table towards the fire. Uncle, after making one more pause, blew a dismal wail and went on again.\n\nFanny was passionate with the tea-cups and the bread as long as her passion lasted, and then protested that she was the wretchedest girl in the world, and she wished she was dead. After that, her crying became remorseful, and she got up and put her arms round her sister. Little Dorrit tried to stop her from saying anything, but she answered that she would, she must! Thereupon she said again, and again, ‘I beg your pardon, Amy,’ and ‘Forgive me, Amy,’ almost as passionately as she had said what she regretted.\n\n‘But indeed, indeed, Amy,’ she resumed when they were seated in sisterly accord side by side, ‘I hope and I think you would have seen this differently, if you had known a little more of Society.’\n\n‘Perhaps I might, Fanny,’ said the mild Little Dorrit.\n\n‘You see, while you have been domestic and resignedly shut up there, Amy,’ pursued her sister, gradually beginning to patronise, ‘I have been out, moving more in Society, and may have been getting proud and spirited--more than I ought to be, perhaps?’\n\nLittle Dorrit answered ‘Yes. O yes!’\n\n‘And while you have been thinking of the dinner or the clothes, I may have been thinking, you know, of the family. Now, may it not be so, Amy?’\n\nLittle Dorrit again nodded ‘Yes,’ with a more cheerful face than heart.\n\n‘Especially as we know,’ said Fanny, ‘that there certainly is a tone in the place to which you have been so true, which does belong to it, and which does make it different from other aspects of Society. So kiss me once again, Amy dear, and we will agree that we may both be right, and that you are a tranquil, domestic, home-loving, good girl.’\n\nThe clarionet had been lamenting most pathetically during this dialogue, but was cut short now by Fanny’s announcement that it was time to go; which she conveyed to her uncle by shutting up his scrap of music, and taking the clarionet out of his mouth.\n\nLittle Dorrit parted from them at the door, and hastened back to the Marshalsea. It fell dark there sooner than elsewhere, and going into it that evening was like going into a deep trench. The shadow of the wall was on every object. Not least upon the figure in the old grey gown and the black velvet cap, as it turned towards her when she opened the door of the dim room.\n\n‘Why not upon me too!’ thought Little Dorrit, with the door yet in her hand. ‘It was not unreasonable in Fanny.’\n\n# CHAPTER 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint\n\nUpon that establishment of state, the Merdle establishment in Harley Street, Cavendish Square, there was the shadow of no more common wall than the fronts of other establishments of state on the opposite side of the street. Like unexceptionable Society, the opposing rows of houses in Harley Street were very grim with one another. Indeed, the mansions and their inhabitants were so much alike in that respect, that the people were often to be found drawn up on opposite sides of dinner-tables, in the shade of their own loftiness, staring at the other side of the way with the dullness of the houses.\n\nEverybody knows how like the street the two dinner-rows of people who take their stand by the street will be. The expressionless uniform twenty houses, all to be knocked at and rung at in the same form, all approachable by the same dull steps, all fended off by the same pattern of railing, all with the same impracticable fire-escapes, the same inconvenient fixtures in their heads, and everything without exception to be taken at a high valuation--who has not dined with these? The house so drearily out of repair, the occasional bow-window, the stuccoed house, the newly-fronted house, the corner house with nothing but angular rooms, the house with the blinds always down, the house with the hatchment always up, the house where the collector has called for one quarter of an Idea, and found nobody at home--who has not dined with these? The house that nobody will take, and is to be had a bargain--who does not know her? The showy house that was taken for life by the disappointed gentleman, and which does not suit him at all--who is unacquainted with that haunted habitation?\n\nHarley Street, Cavendish Square, was more than aware of Mr and Mrs Merdle. Intruders there were in Harley Street, of whom it was not aware; but Mr and Mrs Merdle it delighted to honour. Society was aware of Mr and Mrs Merdle. Society had said ‘Let us license them; let us know them.’\n\nMr Merdle was immensely rich; a man of prodigious enterprise; a Midas without the ears, who turned all he touched to gold. He was in everything good, from banking to building. He was in Parliament, of course. He was in the City, necessarily. He was Chairman of this, Trustee of that, President of the other. The weightiest of men had said to projectors, ‘Now, what name have you got? Have you got Merdle?’ And, the reply being in the negative, had said, ‘Then I won’t look at you.’\n\nThis great and fortunate man had provided that extensive bosom which required so much room to be unfeeling enough in, with a nest of crimson and gold some fifteen years before. It was not a bosom to repose upon, but it was a capital bosom to hang jewels upon. Mr Merdle wanted something to hang jewels upon, and he bought it for the purpose. Storr and Mortimer might have married on the same speculation.\n\nLike all his other speculations, it was sound and successful. The jewels showed to the richest advantage. The bosom moving in Society with the jewels displayed upon it, attracted general admiration. Society approving, Mr Merdle was satisfied. He was the most disinterested of men,--did everything for Society, and got as little for himself out of all his gain and care, as a man might.\n\nThat is to say, it may be supposed that he got all he wanted, otherwise with unlimited wealth he would have got it. But his desire was to the utmost to satisfy Society (whatever that was), and take up all its drafts upon him for tribute. He did not shine in company; he had not very much to say for himself; he was a reserved man, with a broad, overhanging, watchful head, that particular kind of dull red colour in his cheeks which is rather stale than fresh, and a somewhat uneasy expression about his coat-cuffs, as if they were in his confidence, and had reasons for being anxious to hide his hands. In the little he said, he was a pleasant man enough; plain, emphatic about public and private confidence, and tenacious of the utmost deference being shown by every one, in all things, to Society. In this same Society (if that were it which came to his dinners, and to Mrs Merdle’s receptions and concerts), he hardly seemed to enjoy himself much, and was mostly to be found against walls and behind doors. Also when he went out to it, instead of its coming home to him, he seemed a little fatigued, and upon the whole rather more disposed for bed; but he was always cultivating it nevertheless, and always moving in it--and always laying out money on it with the greatest liberality.\n\nMrs Merdle’s first husband had been a colonel, under whose auspices the bosom had entered into competition with the snows of North America, and had come off at little disadvantage in point of whiteness, and at none in point of coldness. The colonel’s son was Mrs Merdle’s only child. He was of a chuckle-headed, high-shouldered make, with a general appearance of being, not so much a young man as a swelled boy. He had given so few signs of reason, that a by-word went among his companions that his brain had been frozen up in a mighty frost which prevailed at St John’s, New Brunswick, at the period of his birth there, and had never thawed from that hour. Another by-word represented him as having in his infancy, through the negligence of a nurse, fallen out of a high window on his head, which had been heard by responsible witnesses to crack. It is probable that both these representations were of ex post facto origin; the young gentleman (whose expressive name was Sparkler) being monomaniacal in offering marriage to all manner of undesirable young ladies, and in remarking of every successive young lady to whom he tendered a matrimonial proposal that she was ‘a doosed fine gal--well educated too--with no biggodd nonsense about her.’\n\nA son-in-law with these limited talents, might have been a clog upon another man; but Mr Merdle did not want a son-in-law for himself; he wanted a son-in-law for Society. Mr Sparkler having been in the Guards, and being in the habit of frequenting all the races, and all the lounges, and all the parties, and being well known, Society was satisfied with its son-in-law. This happy result Mr Merdle would have considered well attained, though Mr Sparkler had been a more expensive article. And he did not get Mr Sparkler by any means cheap for Society, even as it was.\n\nThere was a dinner giving in the Harley Street establishment, while Little Dorrit was stitching at her father’s new shirts by his side that night; and there were magnates from the Court and magnates from the City, magnates from the Commons and magnates from the Lords, magnates from the bench and magnates from the bar, Bishop magnates, Treasury magnates, Horse Guard magnates, Admiralty magnates,--all the magnates that keep us going, and sometimes trip us up.\n\n‘I am told,’ said Bishop magnate to Horse Guards, ‘that Mr Merdle has made another enormous hit. They say a hundred thousand pounds.’\n\nHorse Guards had heard two.\n\nTreasury had heard three.\n\nBar, handling his persuasive double eye-glass, was by no means clear but that it might be four. It was one of those happy strokes of calculation and combination, the result of which it was difficult to estimate. It was one of those instances of a comprehensive grasp, associated with habitual luck and characteristic boldness, of which an age presented us but few. But here was Brother Bellows, who had been in the great Bank case, and who could probably tell us more. What did Brother Bellows put this new success at?\n\nBrother Bellows was on his way to make his bow to the bosom, and could only tell them in passing that he had heard it stated, with great appearance of truth, as being worth, from first to last, half-a-million of money.\n\nAdmiralty said Mr Merdle was a wonderful man, Treasury said he was a new power in the country, and would be able to buy up the whole House of Commons. Bishop said he was glad to think that this wealth flowed into the coffers of a gentleman who was always disposed to maintain the best interests of Society.\n\nMr Merdle himself was usually late on these occasions, as a man still detained in the clutch of giant enterprises when other men had shaken off their dwarfs for the day. On this occasion, he was the last arrival. Treasury said Merdle’s work punished him a little. Bishop said he was glad to think that this wealth flowed into the coffers of a gentleman who accepted it with meekness.\n\nPowder! There was so much Powder in waiting, that it flavoured the dinner. Pulverous particles got into the dishes, and Society’s meats had a seasoning of first-rate footmen. Mr Merdle took down a countess who was secluded somewhere in the core of an immense dress, to which she was in the proportion of the heart to the overgrown cabbage. If so low a simile may be admitted, the dress went down the staircase like a richly brocaded Jack in the Green, and nobody knew what sort of small person carried it.\n\nSociety had everything it could want, and could not want, for dinner. It had everything to look at, and everything to eat, and everything to drink. It is to be hoped it enjoyed itself; for Mr Merdle’s own share of the repast might have been paid for with eighteenpence. Mrs Merdle was magnificent. The chief butler was the next magnificent institution of the day. He was the stateliest man in the company. He did nothing, but he looked on as few other men could have done. He was Mr Merdle’s last gift to Society. Mr Merdle didn’t want him, and was put out of countenance when the great creature looked at him; but inappeasable Society would have him--and had got him.\n\nThe invisible countess carried out the Green at the usual stage of the entertainment, and the file of beauty was closed up by the bosom. Treasury said, Juno. Bishop said, Judith.\n\nBar fell into discussion with Horse Guards concerning courts-martial. Brothers Bellows and Bench struck in. Other magnates paired off. Mr Merdle sat silent, and looked at the table-cloth. Sometimes a magnate addressed him, to turn the stream of his own particular discussion towards him; but Mr Merdle seldom gave much attention to it, or did more than rouse himself from his calculations and pass the wine.\n\nWhen they rose, so many of the magnates had something to say to Mr Merdle individually that he held little levees by the sideboard, and checked them off as they went out at the door.\n\nTreasury hoped he might venture to congratulate one of England’s world-famed capitalists and merchant-princes (he had turned that original sentiment in the house a few times, and it came easy to him) on a new achievement. To extend the triumphs of such men was to extend the triumphs and resources of the nation; and Treasury felt--he gave Mr Merdle to understand--patriotic on the subject.\n\n‘Thank you, my lord,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘thank you. I accept your congratulations with pride, and I am glad you approve.’\n\n‘Why, I don’t unreservedly approve, my dear Mr Merdle. Because,’ smiling Treasury turned him by the arm towards the sideboard and spoke banteringly, ‘it never can be worth your while to come among us and help us.’\n\nMr Merdle felt honoured by the--\n\n‘No, no,’ said Treasury, ‘that is not the light in which one so distinguished for practical knowledge and great foresight, can be expected to regard it. If we should ever be happily enabled, by accidentally possessing the control over circumstances, to propose to one so eminent to--to come among us, and give us the weight of his influence, knowledge, and character, we could only propose it to him as a duty. In fact, as a duty that he owed to Society.’\n\nMr Merdle intimated that Society was the apple of his eye, and that its claims were paramount to every other consideration. Treasury moved on, and Bar came up.\n\nBar, with his little insinuating jury droop, and fingering his persuasive double eye-glass, hoped he might be excused if he mentioned to one of the greatest converters of the root of all evil into the root of all good, who had for a long time reflected a shining lustre on the annals even of our commercial country--if he mentioned, disinterestedly, and as, what we lawyers called in our pedantic way, amicus curiae, a fact that had come by accident within his knowledge. He had been required to look over the title of a very considerable estate in one of the eastern counties--lying, in fact, for Mr Merdle knew we lawyers loved to be particular, on the borders of two of the eastern counties. Now, the title was perfectly sound, and the estate was to be purchased by one who had the command of--Money (jury droop and persuasive eye-glass), on remarkably advantageous terms. This had come to Bar’s knowledge only that day, and it had occurred to him, ‘I shall have the honour of dining with my esteemed friend Mr Merdle this evening, and, strictly between ourselves, I will mention the opportunity.’ Such a purchase would involve not only a great legitimate political influence, but some half-dozen church presentations of considerable annual value. Now, that Mr Merdle was already at no loss to discover means of occupying even his capital, and of fully employing even his active and vigorous intellect, Bar well knew: but he would venture to suggest that the question arose in his mind, whether one who had deservedly gained so high a position and so European a reputation did not owe it--we would not say to himself, but we would say to Society, to possess himself of such influences as these; and to exercise them--we would not say for his own, or for his party’s, but we would say for Society’s--benefit.\n\nMr Merdle again expressed himself as wholly devoted to that object of his constant consideration, and Bar took his persuasive eye-glass up the grand staircase. Bishop then came undesignedly sidling in the direction of the sideboard.\n\nSurely the goods of this world, it occurred in an accidental way to Bishop to remark, could scarcely be directed into happier channels than when they accumulated under the magic touch of the wise and sagacious, who, while they knew the just value of riches (Bishop tried here to look as if he were rather poor himself), were aware of their importance, judiciously governed and rightly distributed, to the welfare of our brethren at large.\n\nMr Merdle with humility expressed his conviction that Bishop couldn’t mean him, and with inconsistency expressed his high gratification in Bishop’s good opinion.\n\nBishop then--jauntily stepping out a little with his well-shaped right leg, as though he said to Mr Merdle ‘don’t mind the apron; a mere form!’ put this case to his good friend:\n\nWhether it had occurred to his good friend, that Society might not unreasonably hope that one so blest in his undertakings, and whose example on his pedestal was so influential with it, would shed a little money in the direction of a mission or so to Africa?\n\nMr Merdle signifying that the idea should have his best attention, Bishop put another case:\n\nWhether his good friend had at all interested himself in the proceedings of our Combined Additional Endowed Dignitaries Committee, and whether it had occurred to him that to shed a little money in _that_ direction might be a great conception finely executed?\n\nMr Merdle made a similar reply, and Bishop explained his reason for inquiring.\n\nSociety looked to such men as his good friend to do such things. It was not that _he_ looked to them, but that Society looked to them. Just as it was not Our Committee who wanted the Additional Endowed Dignitaries, but it was Society that was in a state of the most agonising uneasiness of mind until it got them. He begged to assure his good friend that he was extremely sensible of his good friend’s regard on all occasions for the best interests of Society; and he considered that he was at once consulting those interests and expressing the feeling of Society, when he wished him continued prosperity, continued increase of riches, and continued things in general.\n\nBishop then betook himself up-stairs, and the other magnates gradually floated up after him until there was no one left below but Mr Merdle. That gentleman, after looking at the table-cloth until the soul of the chief butler glowed with a noble resentment, went slowly up after the rest, and became of no account in the stream of people on the grand staircase. Mrs Merdle was at home, the best of the jewels were hung out to be seen, Society got what it came for, Mr Merdle drank twopennyworth of tea in a corner and got more than he wanted.\n\nAmong the evening magnates was a famous physician, who knew everybody, and whom everybody knew. On entering at the door, he came upon Mr Merdle drinking his tea in a corner, and touched him on the arm.\n\nMr Merdle started. ‘Oh! It’s you!’\n\n‘Any better to-day?’\n\n‘No,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I am no better.’\n\n‘A pity I didn’t see you this morning. Pray come to me to-morrow, or let me come to you.’\n\n‘Well!’ he replied. ‘I will come to-morrow as I drive by.’\n\nBar and Bishop had both been bystanders during this short dialogue, and as Mr Merdle was swept away by the crowd, they made their remarks upon it to the Physician. Bar said, there was a certain point of mental strain beyond which no man could go; that the point varied with various textures of brain and peculiarities of constitution, as he had had occasion to notice in several of his learned brothers; but the point of endurance passed by a line’s breadth, depression and dyspepsia ensued. Not to intrude on the sacred mysteries of medicine, he took it, now (with the jury droop and persuasive eye-glass), that this was Merdle’s case? Bishop said that when he was a young man, and had fallen for a brief space into the habit of writing sermons on Saturdays, a habit which all young sons of the church should sedulously avoid, he had frequently been sensible of a depression, arising as he supposed from an over-taxed intellect, upon which the yolk of a new-laid egg, beaten up by the good woman in whose house he at that time lodged, with a glass of sound sherry, nutmeg, and powdered sugar acted like a charm. Without presuming to offer so simple a remedy to the consideration of so profound a professor of the great healing art, he would venture to inquire whether the strain, being by way of intricate calculations, the spirits might not (humanly speaking) be restored to their tone by a gentle and yet generous stimulant?\n\n‘Yes,’ said the physician, ‘yes, you are both right. But I may as well tell you that I can find nothing the matter with Mr Merdle. He has the constitution of a rhinoceros, the digestion of an ostrich, and the concentration of an oyster. As to nerves, Mr Merdle is of a cool temperament, and not a sensitive man: is about as invulnerable, I should say, as Achilles. How such a man should suppose himself unwell without reason, you may think strange. But I have found nothing the matter with him. He may have some deep-seated recondite complaint. I can’t say. I only say, that at present I have not found it out.’\n\nThere was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on the bosom now displaying precious stones in rivalry with many similar superb jewel-stands; there was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on young Sparkler hovering about the rooms, monomaniacally seeking any sufficiently ineligible young lady with no nonsense about her; there was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings, of whom whole colonies were present; or on any of the company. Even on himself, its shadow was faint enough as he moved about among the throng, receiving homage.\n\nMr Merdle’s complaint. Society and he had so much to do with one another in all things else, that it is hard to imagine his complaint, if he had one, being solely his own affair. Had he that deep-seated recondite complaint, and did any doctor find it out? Patience, in the meantime, the shadow of the Marshalsea wall was a real darkening influence, and could be seen on the Dorrit Family at any stage of the sun’s course.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. A Puzzle\n\nMr Clennam did not increase in favour with the Father of the Marshalsea in the ratio of his increasing visits. His obtuseness on the great Testimonial question was not calculated to awaken admiration in the paternal breast, but had rather a tendency to give offence in that sensitive quarter, and to be regarded as a positive shortcoming in point of gentlemanly feeling. An impression of disappointment, occasioned by the discovery that Mr Clennam scarcely possessed that delicacy for which, in the confidence of his nature, he had been inclined to give him credit, began to darken the fatherly mind in connection with that gentleman. The father went so far as to say, in his private family circle, that he feared Mr Clennam was not a man of high instincts. He was happy, he observed, in his public capacity as leader and representative of the College, to receive Mr Clennam when he called to pay his respects; but he didn’t find that he got on with him personally. There appeared to be something (he didn’t know what it was) wanting in him. Howbeit, the father did not fail in any outward show of politeness, but, on the contrary, honoured him with much attention; perhaps cherishing the hope that, although not a man of a sufficiently brilliant and spontaneous turn of mind to repeat his former testimonial unsolicited, it might still be within the compass of his nature to bear the part of a responsive gentleman, in any correspondence that way tending.\n\nIn the threefold capacity, of the gentleman from outside who had been accidentally locked in on the night of his first appearance, of the gentleman from outside who had inquired into the affairs of the Father of the Marshalsea with the stupendous idea of getting him out, and of the gentleman from outside who took an interest in the child of the Marshalsea, Clennam soon became a visitor of mark. He was not surprised by the attentions he received from Mr Chivery when that officer was on the lock, for he made little distinction between Mr Chivery’s politeness and that of the other turnkeys. It was on one particular afternoon that Mr Chivery surprised him all at once, and stood forth from his companions in bold relief.\n\nMr Chivery, by some artful exercise of his power of clearing the Lodge, had contrived to rid it of all sauntering Collegians; so that Clennam, coming out of the prison, should find him on duty alone.\n\n‘(Private) I ask your pardon, sir,’ said Mr Chivery in a secret manner; ‘but which way might you be going?’\n\n‘I am going over the Bridge.’ He saw in Mr Chivery, with some astonishment, quite an Allegory of Silence, as he stood with his key on his lips.\n\n‘(Private) I ask your pardon again,’ said Mr Chivery, ‘but could you go round by Horsemonger Lane? Could you by any means find time to look in at that address?’ handing him a little card, printed for circulation among the connection of Chivery and Co., Tobacconists, Importers of pure Havannah Cigars, Bengal Cheroots, and fine-flavoured Cubas, Dealers in Fancy Snuffs, &c. &c.\n\n‘(Private) It an’t tobacco business,’ said Mr Chivery. ‘The truth is, it’s my wife. She’s wishful to say a word to you, sir, upon a point respecting--yes,’ said Mr Chivery, answering Clennam’s look of apprehension with a nod, ‘respecting _her_.’\n\n‘I will make a point of seeing your wife directly.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir. Much obliged. It an’t above ten minutes out of your way. Please to ask for _Mrs_ Chivery!’ These instructions, Mr Chivery, who had already let him out, cautiously called through a little slide in the outer door, which he could draw back from within for the inspection of visitors when it pleased him.\n\nArthur Clennam, with the card in his hand, betook himself to the address set forth upon it, and speedily arrived there. It was a very small establishment, wherein a decent woman sat behind the counter working at her needle. Little jars of tobacco, little boxes of cigars, a little assortment of pipes, a little jar or two of snuff, and a little instrument like a shoeing horn for serving it out, composed the retail stock in trade.\n\nArthur mentioned his name, and his having promised to call, on the solicitation of Mr Chivery. About something relating to Miss Dorrit, he believed. Mrs Chivery at once laid aside her work, rose up from her seat behind the counter, and deploringly shook her head.\n\n‘You may see him now,’ said she, ‘if you’ll condescend to take a peep.’\n\nWith these mysterious words, she preceded the visitor into a little parlour behind the shop, with a little window in it commanding a very little dull back-yard. In this yard a wash of sheets and table-cloths tried (in vain, for want of air) to get itself dried on a line or two; and among those flapping articles was sitting in a chair, like the last mariner left alive on the deck of a damp ship without the power of furling the sails, a little woe-begone young man.\n\n‘Our John,’ said Mrs Chivery.\n\nNot to be deficient in interest, Clennam asked what he might be doing there?\n\n‘It’s the only change he takes,’ said Mrs Chivery, shaking her head afresh. ‘He won’t go out, even in the back-yard, when there’s no linen; but when there’s linen to keep the neighbours’ eyes off, he’ll sit there, hours. Hours he will. Says he feels as if it was groves!’ Mrs Chivery shook her head again, put her apron in a motherly way to her eyes, and reconducted her visitor into the regions of the business.\n\n‘Please to take a seat, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery. ‘Miss Dorrit is the matter with Our John, sir; he’s a breaking his heart for her, and I would wish to take the liberty to ask how it’s to be made good to his parents when bust?’\n\nMrs Chivery, who was a comfortable-looking woman much respected about Horsemonger Lane for her feelings and her conversation, uttered this speech with fell composure, and immediately afterwards began again to shake her head and dry her eyes.\n\n‘Sir,’ said she in continuation, ‘you are acquainted with the family, and have interested yourself with the family, and are influential with the family. If you can promote views calculated to make two young people happy, let me, for Our John’s sake, and for both their sakes, implore you so to do!’\n\n‘I have been so habituated,’ returned Arthur, at a loss, ‘during the short time I have known her, to consider Little--I have been so habituated to consider Miss Dorrit in a light altogether removed from that in which you present her to me, that you quite take me by surprise. Does she know your son?’\n\n‘Brought up together, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery. ‘Played together.’\n\n‘Does she know your son as her admirer?’\n\n‘Oh! bless you, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, with a sort of triumphant shiver, ‘she never could have seen him on a Sunday without knowing he was that. His cane alone would have told it long ago, if nothing else had. Young men like John don’t take to ivory hands a pinting, for nothing. How did I first know it myself? Similarly.’\n\n‘Perhaps Miss Dorrit may not be so ready as you, you see.’\n\n‘Then she knows it, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘by word of mouth.’\n\n‘Are you sure?’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘sure and certain as in this house I am. I see my son go out with my own eyes when in this house I was, and I see my son come in with my own eyes when in this house I was, and I know he done it!’ Mrs Chivery derived a surprising force of emphasis from the foregoing circumstantiality and repetition.\n\n‘May I ask you how he came to fall into the desponding state which causes you so much uneasiness?’\n\n‘That,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘took place on that same day when to this house I see that John with these eyes return. Never been himself in this house since. Never was like what he has been since, not from the hour when to this house seven year ago me and his father, as tenants by the quarter, came!’ An effect in the nature of an affidavit was gained from this speech by Mrs Chivery’s peculiar power of construction.\n\n‘May I venture to inquire what is your version of the matter?’\n\n‘You may,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘and I will give it to you in honour and in word as true as in this shop I stand. Our John has every one’s good word and every one’s good wish. He played with her as a child when in that yard a child she played. He has known her ever since. He went out upon the Sunday afternoon when in this very parlour he had dined, and met her, with appointment or without appointment; which, I do not pretend to say. He made his offer to her. Her brother and sister is high in their views, and against Our John. Her father is all for himself in his views and against sharing her with any one. Under which circumstances she has answered Our John, “No, John, I cannot have you, I cannot have any husband, it is not my intentions ever to become a wife, it is my intentions to be always a sacrifice, farewell, find another worthy of you, and forget me!” This is the way in which she is doomed to be a constant slave to them that are not worthy that a constant slave she unto them should be. This is the way in which Our John has come to find no pleasure but in taking cold among the linen, and in showing in that yard, as in that yard I have myself shown you, a broken-down ruin that goes home to his mother’s heart!’ Here the good woman pointed to the little window, whence her son might be seen sitting disconsolate in the tuneless groves; and again shook her head and wiped her eyes, and besought him, for the united sakes of both the young people, to exercise his influence towards the bright reversal of these dismal events.\n\nShe was so confident in her exposition of the case, and it was so undeniably founded on correct premises in so far as the relative positions of Little Dorrit and her family were concerned, that Clennam could not feel positive on the other side. He had come to attach to Little Dorrit an interest so peculiar--an interest that removed her from, while it grew out of, the common and coarse things surrounding her--that he found it disappointing, disagreeable, almost painful, to suppose her in love with young Mr Chivery in the back-yard, or any such person. On the other hand, he reasoned with himself that she was just as good and just as true in love with him, as not in love with him; and that to make a kind of domesticated fairy of her, on the penalty of isolation at heart from the only people she knew, would be but a weakness of his own fancy, and not a kind one. Still, her youthful and ethereal appearance, her timid manner, the charm of her sensitive voice and eyes, the very many respects in which she had interested him out of her own individuality, and the strong difference between herself and those about her, were not in unison, and were determined not to be in unison, with this newly presented idea.\n\nHe told the worthy Mrs Chivery, after turning these things over in his mind--he did that, indeed, while she was yet speaking--that he might be relied upon to do his utmost at all times to promote the happiness of Miss Dorrit, and to further the wishes of her heart if it were in his power to do so, and if he could discover what they were. At the same time he cautioned her against assumptions and appearances; enjoined strict silence and secrecy, lest Miss Dorrit should be made unhappy; and particularly advised her to endeavour to win her son’s confidence and so to make quite sure of the state of the case. Mrs Chivery considered the latter precaution superfluous, but said she would try. She shook her head as if she had not derived all the comfort she had fondly expected from this interview, but thanked him nevertheless for the trouble he had kindly taken. They then parted good friends, and Arthur walked away.\n\nThe crowd in the street jostling the crowd in his mind, and the two crowds making a confusion, he avoided London Bridge, and turned off in the quieter direction of the Iron Bridge. He had scarcely set foot upon it, when he saw Little Dorrit walking on before him. It was a pleasant day, with a light breeze blowing, and she seemed to have that minute come there for air. He had left her in her father’s room within an hour.\n\nIt was a timely chance, favourable to his wish of observing her face and manner when no one else was by. He quickened his pace; but before he reached her, she turned her head.\n\n‘Have I startled you?’ he asked.\n\n‘I thought I knew the step,’ she answered, hesitating.\n\n‘And did you know it, Little Dorrit? You could hardly have expected mine.’\n\n‘I did not expect any. But when I heard a step, I thought it--sounded like yours.’\n\n‘Are you going further?’\n\n‘No, sir, I am only walking here for a little change.’\n\nThey walked together, and she recovered her confiding manner with him, and looked up in his face as she said, after glancing around:\n\n‘It is so strange. Perhaps you can hardly understand it. I sometimes have a sensation as if it was almost unfeeling to walk here.’\n\n‘Unfeeling?’\n\n‘To see the river, and so much sky, and so many objects, and such change and motion. Then to go back, you know, and find him in the same cramped place.’\n\n‘Ah yes! But going back, you must remember that you take with you the spirit and influence of such things to cheer him.’\n\n‘Do I? I hope I may! I am afraid you fancy too much, sir, and make me out too powerful. If you were in prison, could I bring such comfort to you?’\n\n‘Yes, Little Dorrit, I am sure of it.’\n\nHe gathered from a tremor on her lip, and a passing shadow of great agitation on her face, that her mind was with her father. He remained silent for a few moments, that she might regain her composure. The Little Dorrit, trembling on his arm, was less in unison than ever with Mrs Chivery’s theory, and yet was not irreconcilable with a new fancy which sprung up within him, that there might be some one else in the hopeless--newer fancy still--in the hopeless unattainable distance.\n\nThey turned, and Clennam said, Here was Maggy coming! Little Dorrit looked up, surprised, and they confronted Maggy, who brought herself at sight of them to a dead stop. She had been trotting along, so preoccupied and busy that she had not recognised them until they turned upon her. She was now in a moment so conscience-stricken that her very basket partook of the change.\n\n‘Maggy, you promised me to stop near father.’\n\n‘So I would, Little Mother, only he wouldn’t let me. If he takes and sends me out I must go. If he takes and says, “Maggy, you hurry away and back with that letter, and you shall have a sixpence if the answer’s a good ‘un,” I must take it. Lor, Little Mother, what’s a poor thing of ten year old to do? And if Mr Tip--if he happens to be a coming in as I come out, and if he says “Where are you going, Maggy?” and if I says, “I’m a going So and So,” and if he says, “I’ll have a Try too,” and if he goes into the George and writes a letter and if he gives it me and says, “Take that one to the same place, and if the answer’s a good ‘un I’ll give you a shilling,” it ain’t my fault, mother!’\n\nArthur read, in Little Dorrit’s downcast eyes, to whom she foresaw that the letters were addressed.\n\n‘I’m a going So and So. There! That’s where I am a going to,’ said Maggy. ‘I’m a going So and So. It ain’t you, Little Mother, that’s got anything to do with it--it’s you, you know,’ said Maggy, addressing Arthur. ‘You’d better come, So and So, and let me take and give ‘em to you.’\n\n‘We will not be so particular as that, Maggy. Give them me here,’ said Clennam in a low voice.\n\n‘Well, then, come across the road,’ answered Maggy in a very loud whisper. ‘Little Mother wasn’t to know nothing of it, and she would never have known nothing of it if you had only gone So and So, instead of bothering and loitering about. It ain’t my fault. I must do what I am told. They ought to be ashamed of themselves for telling me.’\n\nClennam crossed to the other side, and hurriedly opened the letters. That from the father mentioned that most unexpectedly finding himself in the novel position of having been disappointed of a remittance from the City on which he had confidently counted, he took up his pen, being restrained by the unhappy circumstance of his incarceration during three-and-twenty years (doubly underlined), from coming himself, as he would otherwise certainly have done--took up his pen to entreat Mr Clennam to advance him the sum of Three Pounds Ten Shillings upon his I.O.U., which he begged to enclose. That from the son set forth that Mr Clennam would, he knew, be gratified to hear that he had at length obtained permanent employment of a highly satisfactory nature, accompanied with every prospect of complete success in life; but that the temporary inability of his employer to pay him his arrears of salary to that date (in which condition said employer had appealed to that generous forbearance in which he trusted he should never be wanting towards a fellow-creature), combined with the fraudulent conduct of a false friend and the present high price of provisions, had reduced him to the verge of ruin, unless he could by a quarter before six that evening raise the sum of eight pounds. This sum, Mr Clennam would be happy to learn, he had, through the promptitude of several friends who had a lively confidence in his probity, already raised, with the exception of a trifling balance of one pound seventeen and fourpence; the loan of which balance, for the period of one month, would be fraught with the usual beneficent consequences.\n\nThese letters Clennam answered with the aid of his pencil and pocket-book, on the spot; sending the father what he asked for, and excusing himself from compliance with the demand of the son. He then commissioned Maggy to return with his replies, and gave her the shilling of which the failure of her supplemental enterprise would have disappointed her otherwise.\n\nWhen he rejoined Little Dorrit, and they had begun walking as before, she said all at once:\n\n‘I think I had better go. I had better go home.’\n\n‘Don’t be distressed,’ said Clennam, ‘I have answered the letters. They were nothing. You know what they were. They were nothing.’\n\n‘But I am afraid,’ she returned, ‘to leave him, I am afraid to leave any of them. When I am gone, they pervert--but they don’t mean it--even Maggy.’\n\n‘It was a very innocent commission that she undertook, poor thing. And in keeping it secret from you, she supposed, no doubt, that she was only saving you uneasiness.’\n\n‘Yes, I hope so, I hope so. But I had better go home! It was but the other day that my sister told me I had become so used to the prison that I had its tone and character. It must be so. I am sure it must be when I see these things. My place is there. I am better there, it is unfeeling in me to be here, when I can do the least thing there. Good-bye. I had far better stay at home!’\n\nThe agonised way in which she poured this out, as if it burst of itself from her suppressed heart, made it difficult for Clennam to keep the tears from his eyes as he saw and heard her.\n\n‘Don’t call it home, my child!’ he entreated. ‘It is always painful to me to hear you call it home.’\n\n‘But it is home! What else can I call home? Why should I ever forget it for a single moment?’\n\n‘You never do, dear Little Dorrit, in any good and true service.’\n\n‘I hope not, O I hope not! But it is better for me to stay there; much better, much more dutiful, much happier. Please don’t go with me, let me go by myself. Good-bye, God bless you. Thank you, thank you.’\n\nHe felt that it was better to respect her entreaty, and did not move while her slight form went quickly away from him. When it had fluttered out of sight, he turned his face towards the water and stood thinking.\n\nShe would have been distressed at any time by this discovery of the letters; but so much so, and in that unrestrainable way?\n\nNo.\nWhen she had seen her father begging with his threadbare disguise on, when she had entreated him not to give her father money, she had been distressed, but not like this. Something had made her keenly and additionally sensitive just now. Now, was there some one in the hopeless unattainable distance? Or had the suspicion been brought into his mind, by his own associations of the troubled river running beneath the bridge with the same river higher up, its changeless tune upon the prow of the ferry-boat, so many miles an hour the peaceful flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet?\n\nHe thought of his poor child, Little Dorrit, for a long time there; he thought of her going home; he thought of her in the night; he thought of her when the day came round again. And the poor child Little Dorrit thought of him--too faithfully, ah, too faithfully!--in the shadow of the Marshalsea wall.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. Machinery in Motion\n\nMr Meagles bestirred himself with such prompt activity in the matter of the negotiation with Daniel Doyce which Clennam had entrusted to him, that he soon brought it into business train, and called on Clennam at nine o’clock one morning to make his report.\n\n‘Doyce is highly gratified by your good opinion,’ he opened the business by saying, ‘and desires nothing so much as that you should examine the affairs of the Works for yourself, and entirely understand them. He has handed me the keys of all his books and papers--here they are jingling in this pocket--and the only charge he has given me is “Let Mr Clennam have the means of putting himself on a perfect equality with me as to knowing whatever I know. If it should come to nothing after all, he will respect my confidence. Unless I was sure of that to begin with, I should have nothing to do with him.” And there, you see,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘you have Daniel Doyce all over.’\n\n‘A very honourable character.’\n\n‘Oh, yes, to be sure. Not a doubt of it. Odd, but very honourable. Very odd though. Now, would you believe, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, with a hearty enjoyment of his friend’s eccentricity, ‘that I had a whole morning in What’s-his-name Yard--’\n\n‘Bleeding Heart?’\n\n‘A whole morning in Bleeding Heart Yard, before I could induce him to pursue the subject at all?’\n\n‘How was that?’\n\n‘How was that, my friend? I no sooner mentioned your name in connection with it than he declared off.’\n\n‘Declared off on my account?’\n\n‘I no sooner mentioned your name, Clennam, than he said, “That will never do!” What did he mean by that? I asked him. No matter, Meagles; that would never do. Why would it never do? You’ll hardly believe it, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, laughing within himself, ‘but it came out that it would never do, because you and he, walking down to Twickenham together, had glided into a friendly conversation in the course of which he had referred to his intention of taking a partner, supposing at the time that you were as firmly and finally settled as St Paul’s Cathedral. “Whereas,” says he, “Mr Clennam might now believe, if I entertained his proposition, that I had a sinister and designing motive in what was open free speech. Which I can’t bear,” says he, “which I really am too proud to bear.”’\n\n‘I should as soon suspect--’\n\n‘Of course you would,’ interrupted Mr Meagles, ‘and so I told him. But it took a morning to scale that wall; and I doubt if any other man than myself (he likes me of old) could have got his leg over it. Well, Clennam. This business-like obstacle surmounted, he then stipulated that before resuming with you I should look over the books and form my own opinion. I looked over the books, and formed my own opinion. “Is it, on the whole, for, or against?” says he. “For,” says I. “Then,” says he, “you may now, my good friend, give Mr Clennam the means of forming his opinion. To enable him to do which, without bias and with perfect freedom, I shall go out of town for a week.” And he’s gone,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘that’s the rich conclusion of the thing.’\n\n‘Leaving me,’ said Clennam, ‘with a high sense, I must say, of his candour and his--’\n\n‘Oddity,’ Mr Meagles struck in. ‘I should think so!’\n\nIt was not exactly the word on Clennam’s lips, but he forbore to interrupt his good-humoured friend.\n\n‘And now,’ added Mr Meagles, ‘you can begin to look into matters as soon as you think proper. I have undertaken to explain where you may want explanation, but to be strictly impartial, and to do nothing more.’\n\nThey began their perquisitions in Bleeding Heart Yard that same forenoon. Little peculiarities were easily to be detected by experienced eyes in Mr Doyce’s way of managing his affairs, but they almost always involved some ingenious simplification of a difficulty, and some plain road to the desired end. That his papers were in arrear, and that he stood in need of assistance to develop the capacity of his business, was clear enough; but all the results of his undertakings during many years were distinctly set forth, and were ascertainable with ease. Nothing had been done for the purposes of the pending investigation; everything was in its genuine working dress, and in a certain honest rugged order. The calculations and entries, in his own hand, of which there were many, were bluntly written, and with no very neat precision; but were always plain and directed straight to the purpose. It occurred to Arthur that a far more elaborate and taking show of business--such as the records of the Circumlocution Office made perhaps--might be far less serviceable, as being meant to be far less intelligible.\n\nThree or four days of steady application tendered him master of all the facts it was essential to become acquainted with. Mr Meagles was at hand the whole time, always ready to illuminate any dim place with the bright little safety-lamp belonging to the scales and scoop. Between them they agreed upon the sum it would be fair to offer for the purchase of a half-share in the business, and then Mr Meagles unsealed a paper in which Daniel Doyce had noted the amount at which he valued it; which was even something less. Thus, when Daniel came back, he found the affair as good as concluded.\n\n‘And I may now avow, Mr Clennam,’ said he, with a cordial shake of the hand, ‘that if I had looked high and low for a partner, I believe I could not have found one more to my mind.’\n\n‘I say the same,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘And I say of both of you,’ added Mr Meagles, ‘that you are well matched. You keep him in check, Clennam, with your common sense, and you stick to the Works, Dan, with your--’\n\n‘Uncommon sense?’ suggested Daniel, with his quiet smile.\n\n‘You may call it so, if you like--and each of you will be a right hand to the other. Here’s my own right hand upon it, as a practical man, to both of you.’\n\nThe purchase was completed within a month. It left Arthur in possession of private personal means not exceeding a few hundred pounds; but it opened to him an active and promising career. The three friends dined together on the auspicious occasion; the factory and the factory wives and children made holiday and dined too; even Bleeding Heart Yard dined and was full of meat. Two months had barely gone by in all, when Bleeding Heart Yard had become so familiar with short-commons again, that the treat was forgotten there; when nothing seemed new in the partnership but the paint of the inscription on the door-posts, DOYCE AND CLENNAM; when it appeared even to Clennam himself, that he had had the affairs of the firm in his mind for years.\n\nThe little counting-house reserved for his own occupation, was a room of wood and glass at the end of a long low workshop, filled with benches, and vices, and tools, and straps, and wheels; which, when they were in gear with the steam-engine, went tearing round as though they had a suicidal mission to grind the business to dust and tear the factory to pieces. A communication of great trap-doors in the floor and roof with the workshop above and the workshop below, made a shaft of light in this perspective, which brought to Clennam’s mind the child’s old picture-book, where similar rays were the witnesses of Abel’s murder. The noises were sufficiently removed and shut out from the counting-house to blend into a busy hum, interspersed with periodical clinks and thumps. The patient figures at work were swarthy with the filings of iron and steel that danced on every bench and bubbled up through every chink in the planking. The workshop was arrived at by a step-ladder from the outer yard below, where it served as a shelter for the large grindstone where tools were sharpened. The whole had at once a fanciful and practical air in Clennam’s eyes, which was a welcome change; and, as often as he raised them from his first work of getting the array of business documents into perfect order, he glanced at these things with a feeling of pleasure in his pursuit that was new to him.\n\nRaising his eyes thus one day, he was surprised to see a bonnet labouring up the step-ladder. The unusual apparition was followed by another bonnet. He then perceived that the first bonnet was on the head of Mr F.’s Aunt, and that the second bonnet was on the head of Flora, who seemed to have propelled her legacy up the steep ascent with considerable difficulty.\n\nThough not altogether enraptured at the sight of these visitors, Clennam lost no time in opening the counting-house door, and extricating them from the workshop; a rescue which was rendered the more necessary by Mr F.’s Aunt already stumbling over some impediment, and menacing steam power as an Institution with a stony reticule she carried.\n\n‘Good gracious, Arthur,--I should say Mr Clennam, far more proper--the climb we have had to get up here and how ever to get down again without a fire-escape and Mr F.’s Aunt slipping through the steps and bruised all over and you in the machinery and foundry way too only think, and never told us!’\n\nThus, Flora, out of breath. Meanwhile, Mr F.’s Aunt rubbed her esteemed insteps with her umbrella, and vindictively glared.\n\n‘Most unkind never to have come back to see us since that day, though naturally it was not to be expected that there should be any attraction at _our_ house and you were much more pleasantly engaged, that’s pretty certain, and is she fair or dark blue eyes or black I wonder, not that I expect that she should be anything but a perfect contrast to me in all particulars for I am a disappointment as I very well know and you are quite right to be devoted no doubt though what I am saying Arthur never mind I hardly know myself Good gracious!’\n\nBy this time he had placed chairs for them in the counting-house. As Flora dropped into hers, she bestowed the old look upon him.\n\n‘And to think of Doyce and Clennam, and who Doyce can be,’ said Flora; ‘delightful man no doubt and married perhaps or perhaps a daughter, now has he really? then one understands the partnership and sees it all, don’t tell me anything about it for I know I have no claim to ask the question the golden chain that once was forged being snapped and very proper.’\n\nFlora put her hand tenderly on his, and gave him another of the youthful glances.\n\n‘Dear Arthur--force of habit, Mr Clennam every way more delicate and adapted to existing circumstances--I must beg to be excused for taking the liberty of this intrusion but I thought I might so far presume upon old times for ever faded never more to bloom as to call with Mr F.’s Aunt to congratulate and offer best wishes, A great deal superior to China not to be denied and much nearer though higher up!’\n\n‘I am very happy to see you,’ said Clennam, ‘and I thank you, Flora, very much for your kind remembrance.’\n\n‘More than I can say myself at any rate,’ returned Flora, ‘for I might have been dead and buried twenty distinct times over and no doubt whatever should have been before you had genuinely remembered Me or anything like it in spite of which one last remark I wish to make, one last explanation I wish to offer--’\n\n‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ Arthur remonstrated in alarm.\n\n‘Oh not that disagreeable name, say Flora!’\n\n‘Flora, is it worth troubling yourself afresh to enter into explanations? I assure you none are needed. I am satisfied--I am perfectly satisfied.’\n\nA diversion was occasioned here, by Mr F.’s Aunt making the following inexorable and awful statement:\n\n‘There’s mile-stones on the Dover road!’\n\nWith such mortal hostility towards the human race did she discharge this missile, that Clennam was quite at a loss how to defend himself; the rather as he had been already perplexed in his mind by the honour of a visit from this venerable lady, when it was plain she held him in the utmost abhorrence. He could not but look at her with disconcertment, as she sat breathing bitterness and scorn, and staring leagues away. Flora, however, received the remark as if it had been of a most apposite and agreeable nature; approvingly observing aloud that Mr F.’s Aunt had a great deal of spirit. Stimulated either by this compliment, or by her burning indignation, that illustrious woman then added, ‘Let him meet it if he can!’ And, with a rigid movement of her stony reticule (an appendage of great size and of a fossil appearance), indicated that Clennam was the unfortunate person at whom the challenge was hurled.\n\n‘One last remark,’ resumed Flora, ‘I was going to say I wish to make one last explanation I wish to offer, Mr F.’s Aunt and myself would not have intruded on business hours Mr F. having been in business and though the wine trade still business is equally business call it what you will and business habits are just the same as witness Mr F. himself who had his slippers always on the mat at ten minutes before six in the afternoon and his boots inside the fender at ten minutes before eight in the morning to the moment in all weathers light or dark--would not therefore have intruded without a motive which being kindly meant it may be hoped will be kindly taken Arthur, Mr Clennam far more proper, even Doyce and Clennam probably more business-like.’\n\n‘Pray say nothing in the way of apology,’ Arthur entreated. ‘You are always welcome.’\n\n‘Very polite of you to say so Arthur--cannot remember Mr Clennam until the word is out, such is the habit of times for ever fled, and so true it is that oft in the stilly night ere slumber’s chain has bound people, fond memory brings the light of other days around people--very polite but more polite than true I am afraid, for to go into the machinery business without so much as sending a line or a card to papa--I don’t say me though there was a time but that is past and stern reality has now my gracious never mind--does not look like it you must confess.’\n\nEven Flora’s commas seemed to have fled on this occasion; she was so much more disjointed and voluble than in the preceding interview.\n\n‘Though indeed,’ she hurried on, ‘nothing else is to be expected and why should it be expected and if it’s not to be expected why should it be, and I am far from blaming you or any one, When your mama and my papa worried us to death and severed the golden bowl--I mean bond but I dare say you know what I mean and if you don’t you don’t lose much and care just as little I will venture to add--when they severed the golden bond that bound us and threw us into fits of crying on the sofa nearly choked at least myself everything was changed and in giving my hand to Mr F. I know I did so with my eyes open but he was so very unsettled and in such low spirits that he had distractedly alluded to the river if not oil of something from the chemist’s and I did it for the best.’\n\n‘My good Flora, we settled that before. It was all quite right.’\n\n‘It’s perfectly clear you think so,’ returned Flora, ‘for you take it very coolly, if I hadn’t known it to be China I should have guessed myself the Polar regions, dear Mr Clennam you are right however and I cannot blame you but as to Doyce and Clennam papa’s property being about here we heard it from Pancks and but for him we never should have heard one word about it I am satisfied.’\n\n‘No, no, don’t say that.’\n\n‘What nonsense not to say it Arthur--Doyce and Clennam--easier and less trying to me than Mr Clennam--when I know it and you know it too and can’t deny it.’\n\n‘But I do deny it, Flora. I should soon have made you a friendly visit.’\n\n‘Ah!’ said Flora, tossing her head. ‘I dare say!’ and she gave him another of the old looks. ‘However when Pancks told us I made up my mind that Mr F.’s Aunt and I would come and call because when papa--which was before that--happened to mention her name to me and to say that you were interested in her I said at the moment Good gracious why not have her here then when there’s anything to do instead of putting it out.’\n\n‘When you say Her,’ observed Clennam, by this time pretty well bewildered, ‘do you mean Mr F.’s--’\n\n‘My goodness, Arthur--Doyce and Clennam really easier to me with old remembrances--who ever heard of Mr F.’s Aunt doing needlework and going out by the day?’\n\n‘Going out by the day! Do you speak of Little Dorrit?’\n\n‘Why yes of course,’ returned Flora; ‘and of all the strangest names I ever heard the strangest, like a place down in the country with a turnpike, or a favourite pony or a puppy or a bird or something from a seed-shop to be put in a garden or a flower-pot and come up speckled.’\n\n‘Then, Flora,’ said Arthur, with a sudden interest in the conversation, ‘Mr Casby was so kind as to mention Little Dorrit to you, was he? What did he say?’\n\n‘Oh you know what papa is,’ rejoined Flora, ‘and how aggravatingly he sits looking beautiful and turning his thumbs over and over one another till he makes one giddy if one keeps one’s eyes upon him, he said when we were talking of you--I don’t know who began the subject Arthur (Doyce and Clennam) but I am sure it wasn’t me, at least I hope not but you really must excuse my confessing more on that point.’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Arthur. ‘By all means.’\n\n‘You are very ready,’ pouted Flora, coming to a sudden stop in a captivating bashfulness, ‘that I must admit, Papa said you had spoken of her in an earnest way and I said what I have told you and that’s all.’\n\n‘That’s all?’ said Arthur, a little disappointed.\n\n‘Except that when Pancks told us of your having embarked in this business and with difficulty persuaded us that it was really you I said to Mr F.’s Aunt then we would come and ask you if it would be agreeable to all parties that she should be engaged at our house when required for I know she often goes to your mama’s and I know that your mama has a very touchy temper Arthur--Doyce and Clennam--or I never might have married Mr F. and might have been at this hour but I am running into nonsense.’\n\n‘It was very kind of you, Flora, to think of this.’\n\nPoor Flora rejoined with a plain sincerity which became her better than her youngest glances, that she was glad he thought so. She said it with so much heart that Clennam would have given a great deal to buy his old character of her on the spot, and throw it and the mermaid away for ever.\n\n‘I think, Flora,’ he said, ‘that the employment you can give Little Dorrit, and the kindness you can show her--’\n\n‘Yes and I will,’ said Flora, quickly.\n\n‘I am sure of it--will be a great assistance and support to her. I do not feel that I have the right to tell you what I know of her, for I acquired the knowledge confidentially, and under circumstances that bind me to silence. But I have an interest in the little creature, and a respect for her that I cannot express to you. Her life has been one of such trial and devotion, and such quiet goodness, as you can scarcely imagine. I can hardly think of her, far less speak of her, without feeling moved. Let that feeling represent what I could tell you, and commit her to your friendliness with my thanks.’\n\nOnce more he put out his hand frankly to poor Flora; once more poor Flora couldn’t accept it frankly, found it worth nothing openly, must make the old intrigue and mystery of it. As much to her own enjoyment as to his dismay, she covered it with a corner of her shawl as she took it. Then, looking towards the glass front of the counting-house, and seeing two figures approaching, she cried with infinite relish, ‘Papa! Hush, Arthur, for Mercy’s sake!’ and tottered back to her chair with an amazing imitation of being in danger of swooning, in the dread surprise and maidenly flutter of her spirits.\n\nThe Patriarch, meanwhile, came inanely beaming towards the counting-house in the wake of Pancks. Pancks opened the door for him, towed him in, and retired to his own moorings in a corner.\n\n‘I heard from Flora,’ said the Patriarch with his benevolent smile, ‘that she was coming to call, coming to call. And being out, I thought I’d come also, thought I’d come also.’\n\nThe benign wisdom he infused into this declaration (not of itself profound), by means of his blue eyes, his shining head, and his long white hair, was most impressive. It seemed worth putting down among the noblest sentiments enunciated by the best of men. Also, when he said to Clennam, seating himself in the proffered chair, ‘And you are in a new business, Mr Clennam? I wish you well, sir, I wish you well!’ he seemed to have done benevolent wonders.\n\n‘Mrs Finching has been telling me, sir,’ said Arthur, after making his acknowledgments; the relict of the late Mr F. meanwhile protesting, with a gesture, against his use of that respectable name; ‘that she hopes occasionally to employ the young needlewoman you recommended to my mother. For which I have been thanking her.’\n\nThe Patriarch turning his head in a lumbering way towards Pancks, that assistant put up the note-book in which he had been absorbed, and took him in tow.\n\n‘You didn’t recommend her, you know,’ said Pancks; ‘how could you? You knew nothing about her, you didn’t. The name was mentioned to you, and you passed it on. That’s what _you_ did.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Clennam. ‘As she justifies any recommendation, it is much the same thing.’\n\n‘You are glad she turns out well,’ said Pancks, ‘but it wouldn’t have been your fault if she had turned out ill. The credit’s not yours as it is, and the blame wouldn’t have been yours as it might have been. You gave no guarantee. You knew nothing about her.’\n\n‘You are not acquainted, then,’ said Arthur, hazarding a random question, ‘with any of her family?’\n\n‘Acquainted with any of her family?’ returned Pancks. ‘How should you be acquainted with any of her family? You never heard of ‘em. You can’t be acquainted with people you never heard of, can you? You should think not!’\n\nAll this time the Patriarch sat serenely smiling; nodding or shaking his head benevolently, as the case required.\n\n‘As to being a reference,’ said Pancks, ‘you know, in a general way, what being a reference means. It’s all your eye, that is! Look at your tenants down the Yard here. They’d all be references for one another, if you’d let ‘em. What would be the good of letting ‘em? It’s no satisfaction to be done by two men instead of one. One’s enough. A person who can’t pay, gets another person who can’t pay, to guarantee that he can pay. Like a person with two wooden legs getting another person with two wooden legs, to guarantee that he has got two natural legs. It don’t make either of them able to do a walking match. And four wooden legs are more troublesome to you than two, when you don’t want any.’ Mr Pancks concluded by blowing off that steam of his.\n\nA momentary silence that ensued was broken by Mr F.’s Aunt, who had been sitting upright in a cataleptic state since her last public remark. She now underwent a violent twitch, calculated to produce a startling effect on the nerves of the uninitiated, and with the deadliest animosity observed:\n\n‘You can’t make a head and brains out of a brass knob with nothing in it. You couldn’t do it when your Uncle George was living; much less when he’s dead.’\n\nMr Pancks was not slow to reply, with his usual calmness, ‘Indeed, ma’am! Bless my soul! I’m surprised to hear it.’ Despite his presence of mind, however, the speech of Mr F.’s Aunt produced a depressing effect on the little assembly; firstly, because it was impossible to disguise that Clennam’s unoffending head was the particular temple of reason depreciated; and secondly, because nobody ever knew on these occasions whose Uncle George was referred to, or what spectral presence might be invoked under that appellation.\n\nTherefore Flora said, though still not without a certain boastfulness and triumph in her legacy, that Mr F.’s Aunt was ‘very lively to-day, and she thought they had better go.’ But Mr F.’s Aunt proved so lively as to take the suggestion in unexpected dudgeon and declare that she would not go; adding, with several injurious expressions, that if ‘He’--too evidently meaning Clennam--wanted to get rid of her, ‘let him chuck her out of winder;’ and urgently expressing her desire to see ‘Him’ perform that ceremony.\n\nIn this dilemma, Mr Pancks, whose resources appeared equal to any emergency in the Patriarchal waters, slipped on his hat, slipped out at the counting-house door, and slipped in again a moment afterwards with an artificial freshness upon him, as if he had been in the country for some weeks. ‘Why, bless my heart, ma’am!’ said Mr Pancks, rubbing up his hair in great astonishment, ‘is that you? How do you _do_, ma’am? You are looking charming to-day! I am delighted to see you. Favour me with your arm, ma’am; we’ll have a little walk together, you and me, if you’ll honour me with your company.’ And so escorted Mr F.’s Aunt down the private staircase of the counting-house with great gallantry and success. The patriarchal Mr Casby then rose with the air of having done it himself, and blandly followed: leaving his daughter, as she followed in her turn, to remark to her former lover in a distracted whisper (which she very much enjoyed), that they had drained the cup of life to the dregs; and further to hint mysteriously that the late Mr F. was at the bottom of it.\n\nAlone again, Clennam became a prey to his old doubts in reference to his mother and Little Dorrit, and revolved the old thoughts and suspicions. They were all in his mind, blending themselves with the duties he was mechanically discharging, when a shadow on his papers caused him to look up for the cause. The cause was Mr Pancks. With his hat thrown back upon his ears as if his wiry prongs of hair had darted up like springs and cast it off, with his jet-black beads of eyes inquisitively sharp, with the fingers of his right hand in his mouth that he might bite the nails, and with the fingers of his left hand in reserve in his pocket for another course, Mr Pancks cast his shadow through the glass upon the books and papers.\n\nMr Pancks asked, with a little inquiring twist of his head, if he might come in again? Clennam replied with a nod of his head in the affirmative. Mr Pancks worked his way in, came alongside the desk, made himself fast by leaning his arms upon it, and started conversation with a puff and a snort.\n\n‘Mr F.’s Aunt is appeased, I hope?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘All right, sir,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘I am so unfortunate as to have awakened a strong animosity in the breast of that lady,’ said Clennam. ‘Do you know why?’\n\n‘Does _she_ know why?’ said Pancks.\n\n‘I suppose not.’\n\n‘_I_ suppose not,’ said Pancks.\n\nHe took out his note-book, opened it, shut it, dropped it into his hat, which was beside him on the desk, and looked in at it as it lay at the bottom of the hat: all with a great appearance of consideration.\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ he then began, ‘I am in want of information, sir.’\n\n‘Connected with this firm?’ asked Clennam.\n\n‘No,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘With what then, Mr Pancks? That is to say, assuming that you want it of me.’\n\n‘Yes, sir; yes, I want it of you,’ said Pancks, ‘if I can persuade you to furnish it. A, B, C, D. DA, DE, DI, DO. Dictionary order. Dorrit. That’s the name, sir?’\n\nMr Pancks blew off his peculiar noise again, and fell to at his right-hand nails. Arthur looked searchingly at him; he returned the look.\n\n‘I don’t understand you, Mr Pancks.’\n\n‘That’s the name that I want to know about.’\n\n‘And what do you want to know?’\n\n‘Whatever you can and will tell me.’ This comprehensive summary of his desires was not discharged without some heavy labouring on the part of Mr Pancks’s machinery.\n\n‘This is a singular visit, Mr Pancks. It strikes me as rather extraordinary that you should come, with such an object, to me.’\n\n‘It may be all extraordinary together,’ returned Pancks. ‘It may be out of the ordinary course, and yet be business. In short, it is business. I am a man of business. What business have I in this present world, except to stick to business? No business.’\n\nWith his former doubt whether this dry hard personage were quite in earnest, Clennam again turned his eyes attentively upon his face. It was as scrubby and dingy as ever, and as eager and quick as ever, and he could see nothing lurking in it that was at all expressive of a latent mockery that had seemed to strike upon his ear in the voice.\n\n‘Now,’ said Pancks, ‘to put this business on its own footing, it’s not my proprietor’s.’\n\n‘Do you refer to Mr Casby as your proprietor?’\n\nPancks nodded. ‘My proprietor. Put a case. Say, at my proprietor’s I hear name--name of young person Mr Clennam wants to serve. Say, name first mentioned to my proprietor by Plornish in the Yard. Say, I go to Plornish. Say, I ask Plornish as a matter of business for information. Say, Plornish, though six weeks in arrear to my proprietor, declines. Say, Mrs Plornish declines. Say, both refer to Mr Clennam. Put the case.’\n\n‘Well?’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ returned Pancks, ‘say, I come to him. Say, here I am.’\n\nWith those prongs of hair sticking up all over his head, and his breath coming and going very hard and short, the busy Pancks fell back a step (in Tug metaphor, took half a turn astern) as if to show his dingy hull complete, then forged a-head again, and directed his quick glance by turns into his hat where his note-book was, and into Clennam’s face.\n\n‘Mr Pancks, not to trespass on your grounds of mystery, I will be as plain with you as I can. Let me ask two questions. First--’\n\n‘All right!’ said Pancks, holding up his dirty forefinger with his broken nail. ‘I see! “What’s your motive?”’\n\n‘Exactly.’\n\n‘Motive,’ said Pancks, ‘good. Nothing to do with my proprietor; not stateable at present, ridiculous to state at present; but good. Desiring to serve young person, name of Dorrit,’ said Pancks, with his forefinger still up as a caution. ‘Better admit motive to be good.’\n\n‘Secondly, and lastly, what do you want to know?’\n\nMr Pancks fished up his note-book before the question was put, and buttoning it with care in an inner breast-pocket, and looking straight at Clennam all the time, replied with a pause and a puff, ‘I want supplementary information of any sort.’\n\nClennam could not withhold a smile, as the panting little steam-tug, so useful to that unwieldy ship, the Casby, waited on and watched him as if it were seeking an opportunity of running in and rifling him of all he wanted before he could resist its manoeuvres; though there was that in Mr Pancks’s eagerness, too, which awakened many wondering speculations in his mind. After a little consideration, he resolved to supply Mr Pancks with such leading information as it was in his power to impart him; well knowing that Mr Pancks, if he failed in his present research, was pretty sure to find other means of getting it.\n\nHe, therefore, first requesting Mr Pancks to remember his voluntary declaration that his proprietor had no part in the disclosure, and that his own intentions were good (two declarations which that coaly little gentleman with the greatest ardour repeated), openly told him that as to the Dorrit lineage or former place of habitation, he had no information to communicate, and that his knowledge of the family did not extend beyond the fact that it appeared to be now reduced to five members; namely, to two brothers, of whom one was single, and one a widower with three children. The ages of the whole family he made known to Mr Pancks, as nearly as he could guess at them; and finally he described to him the position of the Father of the Marshalsea, and the course of time and events through which he had become invested with that character. To all this, Mr Pancks, snorting and blowing in a more and more portentous manner as he became more interested, listened with great attention; appearing to derive the most agreeable sensations from the painfullest parts of the narrative, and particularly to be quite charmed by the account of William Dorrit’s long imprisonment.\n\n‘In conclusion, Mr Pancks,’ said Arthur, ‘I have but to say this. I have reasons beyond a personal regard for speaking as little as I can of the Dorrit family, particularly at my mother’s house’ (Mr Pancks nodded), ‘and for knowing as much as I can. So devoted a man of business as you are--eh?’\n\nFor Mr Pancks had suddenly made that blowing effort with unusual force.\n\n‘It’s nothing,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘So devoted a man of business as yourself has a perfect understanding of a fair bargain. I wish to make a fair bargain with you, that you shall enlighten me concerning the Dorrit family when you have it in your power, as I have enlightened you. It may not give you a very flattering idea of my business habits, that I failed to make my terms beforehand,’ continued Clennam; ‘but I prefer to make them a point of honour. I have seen so much business done on sharp principles that, to tell you the truth, Mr Pancks, I am tired of them.’\n\nMr Pancks laughed. ‘It’s a bargain, sir,’ said he. ‘You shall find me stick to it.’\n\nAfter that, he stood a little while looking at Clennam, and biting his ten nails all round; evidently while he fixed in his mind what he had been told, and went over it carefully, before the means of supplying a gap in his memory should be no longer at hand. ‘It’s all right,’ he said at last, ‘and now I’ll wish you good day, as it’s collecting day in the Yard. By-the-bye, though. A lame foreigner with a stick.’\n\n‘Ay, ay. You do take a reference sometimes, I see?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘When he can pay, sir,’ replied Pancks. ‘Take all you can get, and keep back all you can’t be forced to give up. That’s business. The lame foreigner with the stick wants a top room down the Yard. Is he good for it?’\n\n‘I am,’ said Clennam, ‘and I will answer for him.’\n\n‘That’s enough. What I must have of Bleeding Heart Yard,’ said Pancks, making a note of the case in his book, ‘is my bond. I want my bond, you see. Pay up, or produce your property! That’s the watchword down the Yard. The lame foreigner with the stick represented that you sent him; but he could represent (as far as that goes) that the Great Mogul sent him. He has been in the hospital, I believe?’\n\n‘Yes. Through having met with an accident. He is only just now discharged.’\n\n‘It’s pauperising a man, sir, I have been shown, to let him into a hospital?’ said Pancks. And again blew off that remarkable sound.\n\n‘I have been shown so too,’ said Clennam, coldly.\n\nMr Pancks, being by that time quite ready for a start, got under steam in a moment, and, without any other signal or ceremony, was snorting down the step-ladder and working into Bleeding Heart Yard, before he seemed to be well out of the counting-house.\n\nThroughout the remainder of the day, Bleeding Heart Yard was in consternation, as the grim Pancks cruised in it; haranguing the inhabitants on their backslidings in respect of payment, demanding his bond, breathing notices to quit and executions, running down defaulters, sending a swell of terror on before him, and leaving it in his wake. Knots of people, impelled by a fatal attraction, lurked outside any house in which he was known to be, listening for fragments of his discourses to the inmates; and, when he was rumoured to be coming down the stairs, often could not disperse so quickly but that he would be prematurely in among them, demanding their own arrears, and rooting them to the spot. Throughout the remainder of the day, Mr Pancks’s What were they up to? and What did they mean by it? sounded all over the Yard. Mr Pancks wouldn’t hear of excuses, wouldn’t hear of complaints, wouldn’t hear of repairs, wouldn’t hear of anything but unconditional money down. Perspiring and puffing and darting about in eccentric directions, and becoming hotter and dingier every moment, he lashed the tide of the yard into a most agitated and turbid state. It had not settled down into calm water again full two hours after he had been seen fuming away on the horizon at the top of the steps.\n\nThere were several small assemblages of the Bleeding Hearts at the popular points of meeting in the Yard that night, among whom it was universally agreed that Mr Pancks was a hard man to have to do with; and that it was much to be regretted, so it was, that a gentleman like Mr Casby should put his rents in his hands, and never know him in his true light. For (said the Bleeding Hearts), if a gentleman with that head of hair and them eyes took his rents into his own hands, ma’am, there would be none of this worriting and wearing, and things would be very different.\n\nAt which identical evening hour and minute, the Patriarch--who had floated serenely through the Yard in the forenoon before the harrying began, with the express design of getting up this trustfulness in his shining bumps and silken locks--at which identical hour and minute, that first-rate humbug of a thousand guns was heavily floundering in the little Dock of his exhausted Tug at home, and was saying, as he turned his thumbs:\n\n‘A very bad day’s work, Pancks, very bad day’s work. It seems to me, sir, and I must insist on making this observation forcibly in justice to myself, that you ought to have got much more money, much more money.’\n\n# CHAPTER 24. Fortune-Telling\n\nLittle Dorrit received a call that same evening from Mr Plornish, who, having intimated that he wished to speak to her privately, in a series of coughs so very noticeable as to favour the idea that her father, as regarded her seamstress occupation, was an illustration of the axiom that there are no such stone-blind men as those who will not see, obtained an audience with her on the common staircase outside the door.\n\n‘There’s been a lady at our place to-day, Miss Dorrit,’ Plornish growled, ‘and another one along with her as is a old wixen if ever I met with such. The way she snapped a person’s head off, dear me!’\n\nThe mild Plornish was at first quite unable to get his mind away from Mr F.’s Aunt. ‘For,’ said he, to excuse himself, ‘she is, I do assure you, the winegariest party.’\n\nAt length, by a great effort, he detached himself from the subject sufficiently to observe:\n\n‘But she’s neither here nor there just at present. The other lady, she’s Mr Casby’s daughter; and if Mr Casby an’t well off, none better, it an’t through any fault of Pancks. For, as to Pancks, he does, he really does, he does indeed!’\n\nMr Plornish, after his usual manner, was a little obscure, but conscientiously emphatic.\n\n‘And what she come to our place for,’ he pursued, ‘was to leave word that if Miss Dorrit would step up to that card--which it’s Mr Casby’s house that is, and Pancks he has a office at the back, where he really does, beyond belief--she would be glad for to engage her. She was a old and a dear friend, she said particular, of Mr Clennam, and hoped for to prove herself a useful friend to _his_ friend. Them was her words. Wishing to know whether Miss Dorrit could come to-morrow morning, I said I would see you, Miss, and inquire, and look round there to-night, to say yes, or, if you was engaged to-morrow, when?’\n\n‘I can go to-morrow, thank you,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘This is very kind of you, but you are always kind.’\n\nMr Plornish, with a modest disavowal of his merits, opened the room door for her readmission, and followed her in with such an exceedingly bald pretence of not having been out at all, that her father might have observed it without being very suspicious. In his affable unconsciousness, however, he took no heed. Plornish, after a little conversation, in which he blended his former duty as a Collegian with his present privilege as a humble outside friend, qualified again by his low estate as a plasterer, took his leave; making the tour of the prison before he left, and looking on at a game of skittles with the mixed feelings of an old inhabitant who had his private reasons for believing that it might be his destiny to come back again.\n\nEarly in the morning, Little Dorrit, leaving Maggy in high domestic trust, set off for the Patriarchal tent. She went by the Iron Bridge, though it cost her a penny, and walked more slowly in that part of her journey than in any other. At five minutes before eight her hand was on the Patriarchal knocker, which was quite as high as she could reach.\n\nShe gave Mrs Finching’s card to the young woman who opened the door, and the young woman told her that ‘Miss Flora’--Flora having, on her return to the parental roof, reinvested herself with the title under which she had lived there--was not yet out of her bedroom, but she was to please to walk up into Miss Flora’s sitting-room. She walked up into Miss Flora’s sitting-room, as in duty bound, and there found a breakfast-table comfortably laid for two, with a supplementary tray upon it laid for one. The young woman, disappearing for a few moments, returned to say that she was to please to take a chair by the fire, and to take off her bonnet and make herself at home. But Little Dorrit, being bashful, and not used to make herself at home on such occasions, felt at a loss how to do it; so she was still sitting near the door with her bonnet on, when Flora came in in a hurry half an hour afterwards.\n\nFlora was so sorry to have kept her waiting, and good gracious why did she sit out there in the cold when she had expected to find her by the fire reading the paper, and hadn’t that heedless girl given her the message then, and had she really been in her bonnet all this time, and pray for goodness sake let Flora take it off! Flora taking it off in the best-natured manner in the world, was so struck with the face disclosed, that she said, ‘Why, what a good little thing you are, my dear!’ and pressed her face between her hands like the gentlest of women.\n\nIt was the word and the action of a moment. Little Dorrit had hardly time to think how kind it was, when Flora dashed at the breakfast-table full of business, and plunged over head and ears into loquacity.\n\n‘Really so sorry that I should happen to be late on this morning of all mornings because my intention and my wish was to be ready to meet you when you came in and to say that any one that interested Arthur Clennam half so much must interest me and that I gave you the heartiest welcome and was so glad, instead of which they never called me and there I still am snoring I dare say if the truth was known and if you don’t like either cold fowl or hot boiled ham which many people don’t I dare say besides Jews and theirs are scruples of conscience which we must all respect though I must say I wish they had them equally strong when they sell us false articles for real that certainly ain’t worth the money I shall be quite vexed,’ said Flora.\n\nLittle Dorrit thanked her, and said, shyly, bread-and-butter and tea was all she usually--\n\n‘Oh nonsense my dear child I can never hear of that,’ said Flora, turning on the urn in the most reckless manner, and making herself wink by splashing hot water into her eyes as she bent down to look into the teapot. ‘You are coming here on the footing of a friend and companion you know if you will let me take that liberty and I should be ashamed of myself indeed if you could come here upon any other, besides which Arthur Clennam spoke in such terms--you are tired my dear.’\n\n‘No, ma’am.’\n\n‘You turn so pale you have walked too far before breakfast and I dare say live a great way off and ought to have had a ride,’ said Flora, ‘dear dear is there anything that would do you good?’\n\n‘Indeed I am quite well, ma’am. I thank you again and again, but I am quite well.’\n\n‘Then take your tea at once I beg,’ said Flora, ‘and this wing of fowl and bit of ham, don’t mind me or wait for me, because I always carry in this tray myself to Mr F.’s Aunt who breakfasts in bed and a charming old lady too and very clever, Portrait of Mr F. behind the door and very like though too much forehead and as to a pillar with a marble pavement and balustrades and a mountain, I never saw him near it nor not likely in the wine trade, excellent man but not at all in that way.’\n\nLittle Dorrit glanced at the portrait, very imperfectly following the references to that work of art.\n\n‘Mr F. was so devoted to me that he never could bear me out of his sight,’ said Flora, ‘though of course I am unable to say how long that might have lasted if he hadn’t been cut short while I was a new broom, worthy man but not poetical manly prose but not romance.’\n\nLittle Dorrit glanced at the portrait again. The artist had given it a head that would have been, in an intellectual point of view, top-heavy for Shakespeare.\n\n‘Romance, however,’ Flora went on, busily arranging Mr F.’s Aunt’s toast, ‘as I openly said to Mr F. when he proposed to me and you will be surprised to hear that he proposed seven times once in a hackney-coach once in a boat once in a pew once on a donkey at Tunbridge Wells and the rest on his knees, Romance was fled with the early days of Arthur Clennam, our parents tore us asunder we became marble and stern reality usurped the throne, Mr F. said very much to his credit that he was perfectly aware of it and even preferred that state of things accordingly the word was spoken the fiat went forth and such is life you see my dear and yet we do not break but bend, pray make a good breakfast while I go in with the tray.’\n\nShe disappeared, leaving Little Dorrit to ponder over the meaning of her scattered words. She soon came back again; and at last began to take her own breakfast, talking all the while.\n\n‘You see, my dear,’ said Flora, measuring out a spoonful or two of some brown liquid that smelt like brandy, and putting it into her tea, ‘I am obliged to be careful to follow the directions of my medical man though the flavour is anything but agreeable being a poor creature and it may be have never recovered the shock received in youth from too much giving way to crying in the next room when separated from Arthur, have you known him long?’\n\nAs soon as Little Dorrit comprehended that she had been asked this question--for which time was necessary, the galloping pace of her new patroness having left her far behind--she answered that she had known Mr Clennam ever since his return.\n\n‘To be sure you couldn’t have known him before unless you had been in China or had corresponded neither of which is likely,’ returned Flora, ‘for travelling-people usually get more or less mahogany and you are not at all so and as to corresponding what about? that’s very true unless tea, so it was at his mother’s was it really that you knew him first, highly sensible and firm but dreadfully severe--ought to be the mother of the man in the iron mask.’\n\n‘Mrs Clennam has been kind to me,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Really? I am sure I am glad to hear it because as Arthur’s mother it’s naturally pleasant to my feelings to have a better opinion of her than I had before, though what she thinks of me when I run on as I am certain to do and she sits glowering at me like Fate in a go-cart--shocking comparison really--invalid and not her fault--I never know or can imagine.’\n\n‘Shall I find my work anywhere, ma’am?’ asked Little Dorrit, looking timidly about; ‘can I get it?’\n\n‘You industrious little fairy,’ returned Flora, taking, in another cup of tea, another of the doses prescribed by her medical man, ‘there’s not the slightest hurry and it’s better that we should begin by being confidential about our mutual friend--too cold a word for me at least I don’t mean that, very proper expression mutual friend--than become through mere formalities not you but me like the Spartan boy with the fox biting him, which I hope you’ll excuse my bringing up for of all the tiresome boys that will go tumbling into every sort of company that boy’s the tiresomest.’\n\nLittle Dorrit, her face very pale, sat down again to listen. ‘Hadn’t I better work the while?’ she asked. ‘I can work and attend too. I would rather, if I may.’\n\nHer earnestness was so expressive of her being uneasy without her work, that Flora answered, ‘Well my dear whatever you like best,’ and produced a basket of white handkerchiefs. Little Dorrit gladly put it by her side, took out her little pocket-housewife, threaded the needle, and began to hem.\n\n‘What nimble fingers you have,’ said Flora, ‘but are you sure you are well?’\n\n‘Oh yes, indeed!’\n\nFlora put her feet upon the fender, and settled herself for a thorough good romantic disclosure. She started off at score, tossing her head, sighing in the most demonstrative manner, making a great deal of use of her eyebrows, and occasionally, but not often, glancing at the quiet face that bent over the work.\n\n‘You must know my dear,’ said Flora, ‘but that I have no doubt you know already not only because I have already thrown it out in a general way but because I feel I carry it stamped in burning what’s his names upon my brow that before I was introduced to the late Mr F. I had been engaged to Arthur Clennam--Mr Clennam in public where reserve is necessary Arthur here--we were all in all to one another it was the morning of life it was bliss it was frenzy it was everything else of that sort in the highest degree, when rent asunder we turned to stone in which capacity Arthur went to China and I became the statue bride of the late Mr F.’\n\nFlora, uttering these words in a deep voice, enjoyed herself immensely.\n\n‘To paint,’ said she, ‘the emotions of that morning when all was marble within and Mr F.’s Aunt followed in a glass-coach which it stands to reason must have been in shameful repair or it never could have broken down two streets from the house and Mr F.’s Aunt brought home like the fifth of November in a rush-bottomed chair I will not attempt, suffice it to say that the hollow form of breakfast took place in the dining-room downstairs that papa partaking too freely of pickled salmon was ill for weeks and that Mr F. and myself went upon a continental tour to Calais where the people fought for us on the pier until they separated us though not for ever that was not yet to be.’\n\nThe statue bride, hardly pausing for breath, went on, with the greatest complacency, in a rambling manner sometimes incidental to flesh and blood.\n\n‘I will draw a veil over that dreamy life, Mr F. was in good spirits his appetite was good he liked the cookery he considered the wine weak but palatable and all was well, we returned to the immediate neighbourhood of Number Thirty Little Gosling Street London Docks and settled down, ere we had yet fully detected the housemaid in selling the feathers out of the spare bed Gout flying upwards soared with Mr F. to another sphere.’\n\nHis relict, with a glance at his portrait, shook her head and wiped her eyes.\n\n‘I revere the memory of Mr F. as an estimable man and most indulgent husband, only necessary to mention Asparagus and it appeared or to hint at any little delicate thing to drink and it came like magic in a pint bottle it was not ecstasy but it was comfort, I returned to papa’s roof and lived secluded if not happy during some years until one day papa came smoothly blundering in and said that Arthur Clennam awaited me below, I went below and found him ask me not what I found him except that he was still unmarried still unchanged!’\n\nThe dark mystery with which Flora now enshrouded herself might have stopped other fingers than the nimble fingers that worked near her. They worked on without pause, and the busy head bent over them watching the stitches.\n\n‘Ask me not,’ said Flora, ‘if I love him still or if he still loves me or what the end is to be or when, we are surrounded by watchful eyes and it may be that we are destined to pine asunder it may be never more to be reunited not a word not a breath not a look to betray us all must be secret as the tomb wonder not therefore that even if I should seem comparatively cold to Arthur or Arthur should seem comparatively cold to me we have fatal reasons it is enough if we understand them hush!’\n\nAll of which Flora said with so much headlong vehemence as if she really believed it. There is not much doubt that when she worked herself into full mermaid condition, she did actually believe whatever she said in it.\n\n‘Hush!’ repeated Flora, ‘I have now told you all, confidence is established between us hush, for Arthur’s sake I will always be a friend to you my dear girl and in Arthur’s name you may always rely upon me.’\n\nThe nimble fingers laid aside the work, and the little figure rose and kissed her hand. ‘You are very cold,’ said Flora, changing to her own natural kind-hearted manner, and gaining greatly by the change. ‘Don’t work to-day. I am sure you are not well I am sure you are not strong.’\n\n‘It is only that I feel a little overcome by your kindness, and by Mr Clennam’s kindness in confiding me to one he has known and loved so long.’\n\n‘Well really my dear,’ said Flora, who had a decided tendency to be always honest when she gave herself time to think about it, ‘it’s as well to leave that alone now, for I couldn’t undertake to say after all, but it doesn’t signify lie down a little!’\n\n‘I have always been strong enough to do what I want to do, and I shall be quite well directly,’ returned Little Dorrit, with a faint smile. ‘You have overpowered me with gratitude, that’s all. If I keep near the window for a moment I shall be quite myself.’\n\nFlora opened a window, sat her in a chair by it, and considerately retired to her former place. It was a windy day, and the air stirring on Little Dorrit’s face soon brightened it. In a very few minutes she returned to her basket of work, and her nimble fingers were as nimble as ever.\n\nQuietly pursuing her task, she asked Flora if Mr Clennam had told her where she lived? When Flora replied in the negative, Little Dorrit said that she understood why he had been so delicate, but that she felt sure he would approve of her confiding her secret to Flora, and that she would therefore do so now with Flora’s permission. Receiving an encouraging answer, she condensed the narrative of her life into a few scanty words about herself and a glowing eulogy upon her father; and Flora took it all in with a natural tenderness that quite understood it, and in which there was no incoherence.\n\nWhen dinner-time came, Flora drew the arm of her new charge through hers, and led her down-stairs, and presented her to the Patriarch and Mr Pancks, who were already in the dining-room waiting to begin. (Mr F.’s Aunt was, for the time, laid up in ordinary in her chamber.) By those gentlemen she was received according to their characters; the Patriarch appearing to do her some inestimable service in saying that he was glad to see her, glad to see her; and Mr Pancks blowing off his favourite sound as a salute.\n\nIn that new presence she would have been bashful enough under any circumstances, and particularly under Flora’s insisting on her drinking a glass of wine and eating of the best that was there; but her constraint was greatly increased by Mr Pancks. The demeanour of that gentleman at first suggested to her mind that he might be a taker of likenesses, so intently did he look at her, and so frequently did he glance at the little note-book by his side. Observing that he made no sketch, however, and that he talked about business only, she began to have suspicions that he represented some creditor of her father’s, the balance due to whom was noted in that pocket volume. Regarded from this point of view Mr Pancks’s puffings expressed injury and impatience, and each of his louder snorts became a demand for payment.\n\nBut here again she was undeceived by anomalous and incongruous conduct on the part of Mr Pancks himself. She had left the table half an hour, and was at work alone. Flora had ‘gone to lie down’ in the next room, concurrently with which retirement a smell of something to drink had broken out in the house. The Patriarch was fast asleep, with his philanthropic mouth open under a yellow pocket-handkerchief in the dining-room. At this quiet time, Mr Pancks softly appeared before her, urbanely nodding.\n\n‘Find it a little dull, Miss Dorrit?’ inquired Pancks in a low voice.\n\n‘No, thank you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Busy, I see,’ observed Mr Pancks, stealing into the room by inches. ‘What are those now, Miss Dorrit?’\n\n‘Handkerchiefs.’\n\n‘Are they, though!’ said Pancks. ‘I shouldn’t have thought it.’ Not in the least looking at them, but looking at Little Dorrit. ‘Perhaps you wonder who I am. Shall I tell you? I am a fortune-teller.’\n\nLittle Dorrit now began to think he was mad.\n\n‘I belong body and soul to my proprietor,’ said Pancks; ‘you saw my proprietor having his dinner below. But I do a little in the other way, sometimes; privately, very privately, Miss Dorrit.’\n\nLittle Dorrit looked at him doubtfully, and not without alarm. ‘I wish you’d show me the palm of your hand,’ said Pancks. ‘I should like to have a look at it. Don’t let me be troublesome.’\n\nHe was so far troublesome that he was not at all wanted there, but she laid her work in her lap for a moment, and held out her left hand with her thimble on it.\n\n‘Years of toil, eh?’ said Pancks, softly, touching it with his blunt forefinger. ‘But what else are we made for? Nothing. Hallo!’ looking into the lines. ‘What’s this with bars? It’s a College! And what’s this with a grey gown and a black velvet cap? it’s a father! And what’s this with a clarionet? It’s an uncle! And what’s this in dancing-shoes? It’s a sister! And what’s this straggling about in an idle sort of a way? It’s a brother! And what’s this thinking for ‘em all? Why, this is you, Miss Dorrit!’\n\nHer eyes met his as she looked up wonderingly into his face, and she thought that although his were sharp eyes, he was a brighter and gentler-looking man than she had supposed at dinner. His eyes were on her hand again directly, and her opportunity of confirming or correcting the impression was gone.\n\n‘Now, the deuce is in it,’ muttered Pancks, tracing out a line in her hand with his clumsy finger, ‘if this isn’t me in the corner here! What do I want here? What’s behind me?’\n\nHe carried his finger slowly down to the wrist, and round the wrist, and affected to look at the back of the hand for what was behind him.\n\n‘Is it any harm?’ asked Little Dorrit, smiling.\n\n‘Deuce a bit!’ said Pancks. ‘What do you think it’s worth?’\n\n‘I ought to ask you that. I am not the fortune-teller.’\n\n‘True,’ said Pancks. ‘What’s it worth? You shall live to see, Miss Dorrit.’\n\nReleasing the hand by slow degrees, he drew all his fingers through his prongs of hair, so that they stood up in their most portentous manner; and repeated slowly, ‘Remember what I say, Miss Dorrit. You shall live to see.’\n\nShe could not help showing that she was much surprised, if it were only by his knowing so much about her.\n\n‘Ah! That’s it!’ said Pancks, pointing at her. ‘Miss Dorrit, not that, ever!’\n\nMore surprised than before, and a little more frightened, she looked to him for an explanation of his last words.\n\n‘Not that,’ said Pancks, making, with great seriousness, an imitation of a surprised look and manner that appeared to be unintentionally grotesque. ‘Don’t do that. Never on seeing me, no matter when, no matter where. I am nobody. Don’t take on to mind me. Don’t mention me. Take no notice. Will you agree, Miss Dorrit?’\n\n‘I hardly know what to say,’ returned Little Dorrit, quite astounded. ‘Why?’\n\n‘Because I am a fortune-teller. Pancks the gipsy. I haven’t told you so much of your fortune yet, Miss Dorrit, as to tell you what’s behind me on that little hand. I have told you you shall live to see. Is it agreed, Miss Dorrit?’\n\n‘Agreed that I--am--to--’\n\n‘To take no notice of me away from here, unless I take on first. Not to mind me when I come and go. It’s very easy. I am no loss, I am not handsome, I am not good company, I am only my proprietors grubber. You need do no more than think, “Ah! Pancks the gipsy at his fortune-telling--he’ll tell the rest of my fortune one day--I shall live to know it.” Is it agreed, Miss Dorrit?’\n\n‘Ye-es,’ faltered Little Dorrit, whom he greatly confused, ‘I suppose so, while you do no harm.’\n\n‘Good!’ Mr Pancks glanced at the wall of the adjoining room, and stooped forward. ‘Honest creature, woman of capital points, but heedless and a loose talker, Miss Dorrit.’ With that he rubbed his hands as if the interview had been very satisfactory to him, panted away to the door, and urbanely nodded himself out again.\n\nIf Little Dorrit were beyond measure perplexed by this curious conduct on the part of her new acquaintance, and by finding herself involved in this singular treaty, her perplexity was not diminished by ensuing circumstances. Besides that Mr Pancks took every opportunity afforded him in Mr Casby’s house of significantly glancing at her and snorting at her--which was not much, after what he had done already--he began to pervade her daily life. She saw him in the street, constantly. When she went to Mr Casby’s, he was always there. When she went to Mrs Clennam’s, he came there on any pretence, as if to keep her in his sight. A week had not gone by, when she found him to her astonishment in the Lodge one night, conversing with the turnkey on duty, and to all appearance one of his familiar companions. Her next surprise was to find him equally at his ease within the prison; to hear of his presenting himself among the visitors at her father’s Sunday levee; to see him arm in arm with a Collegiate friend about the yard; to learn, from Fame, that he had greatly distinguished himself one evening at the social club that held its meetings in the Snuggery, by addressing a speech to the members of the institution, singing a song, and treating the company to five gallons of ale--report madly added a bushel of shrimps. The effect on Mr Plornish of such of these phenomena as he became an eye-witness of in his faithful visits, made an impression on Little Dorrit only second to that produced by the phenomena themselves. They seemed to gag and bind him. He could only stare, and sometimes weakly mutter that it wouldn’t be believed down Bleeding Heart Yard that this was Pancks; but he never said a word more, or made a sign more, even to Little Dorrit. Mr Pancks crowned his mysteries by making himself acquainted with Tip in some unknown manner, and taking a Sunday saunter into the College on that gentleman’s arm. Throughout he never took any notice of Little Dorrit, save once or twice when he happened to come close to her and there was no one very near; on which occasions, he said in passing, with a friendly look and a puff of encouragement, ‘Pancks the gipsy--fortune-telling.’\n\nLittle Dorrit worked and strove as usual, wondering at all this, but keeping her wonder, as she had from her earliest years kept many heavier loads, in her own breast. A change had stolen, and was stealing yet, over the patient heart. Every day found her something more retiring than the day before. To pass in and out of the prison unnoticed, and elsewhere to be overlooked and forgotten, were, for herself, her chief desires.\n\nTo her own room too, strangely assorted room for her delicate youth and character, she was glad to retreat as often as she could without desertion of any duty. There were afternoon times when she was unemployed, when visitors dropped in to play a hand at cards with her father, when she could be spared and was better away. Then she would flit along the yard, climb the scores of stairs that led to her room, and take her seat at the window. Many combinations did those spikes upon the wall assume, many light shapes did the strong iron weave itself into, many golden touches fell upon the rust, while Little Dorrit sat there musing. New zig-zags sprung into the cruel pattern sometimes, when she saw it through a burst of tears; but beautified or hardened still, always over it and under it and through it, she was fain to look in her solitude, seeing everything with that ineffaceable brand.\n\nA garret, and a Marshalsea garret without compromise, was Little Dorrit’s room. Beautifully kept, it was ugly in itself, and had little but cleanliness and air to set it off; for what embellishment she had ever been able to buy, had gone to her father’s room. Howbeit, for this poor place she showed an increasing love; and to sit in it alone became her favourite rest.\n\nInsomuch, that on a certain afternoon during the Pancks mysteries, when she was seated at her window, and heard Maggy’s well-known step coming up the stairs, she was very much disturbed by the apprehension of being summoned away. As Maggy’s step came higher up and nearer, she trembled and faltered; and it was as much as she could do to speak, when Maggy at length appeared.\n\n‘Please, Little Mother,’ said Maggy, panting for breath, ‘you must come down and see him. He’s here.’\n\n‘Who, Maggy?’\n\n‘Who, o’ course Mr Clennam. He’s in your father’s room, and he says to me, Maggy, will you be so kind and go and say it’s only me.’\n\n‘I am not very well, Maggy. I had better not go. I am going to lie down. See! I lie down now, to ease my head. Say, with my grateful regard, that you left me so, or I would have come.’\n\n‘Well, it an’t very polite though, Little Mother,’ said the staring Maggy, ‘to turn your face away, neither!’\n\nMaggy was very susceptible to personal slights, and very ingenious in inventing them. ‘Putting both your hands afore your face too!’ she went on. ‘If you can’t bear the looks of a poor thing, it would be better to tell her so at once, and not go and shut her out like that, hurting her feelings and breaking her heart at ten year old, poor thing!’\n\n‘It’s to ease my head, Maggy.’\n\n‘Well, and if you cry to ease your head, Little Mother, let me cry too. Don’t go and have all the crying to yourself,’ expostulated Maggy, ‘that an’t not being greedy.’ And immediately began to blubber.\n\nIt was with some difficulty that she could be induced to go back with the excuse; but the promise of being told a story--of old her great delight--on condition that she concentrated her faculties upon the errand and left her little mistress to herself for an hour longer, combined with a misgiving on Maggy’s part that she had left her good temper at the bottom of the staircase, prevailed. So away she went, muttering her message all the way to keep it in her mind, and, at the appointed time, came back.\n\n‘He was very sorry, I can tell you,’ she announced, ‘and wanted to send a doctor. And he’s coming again to-morrow he is and I don’t think he’ll have a good sleep to-night along o’ hearing about your head, Little Mother. Oh my! Ain’t you been a-crying!’\n\n‘I think I have, a little, Maggy.’\n\n‘A little! Oh!’\n\n‘But it’s all over now--all over for good, Maggy. And my head is much better and cooler, and I am quite comfortable. I am very glad I did not go down.’\n\nHer great staring child tenderly embraced her; and having smoothed her hair, and bathed her forehead and eyes with cold water (offices in which her awkward hands became skilful), hugged her again, exulted in her brighter looks, and stationed her in her chair by the window. Over against this chair, Maggy, with apoplectic exertions that were not at all required, dragged the box which was her seat on story-telling occasions, sat down upon it, hugged her own knees, and said, with a voracious appetite for stories, and with widely-opened eyes:\n\n‘Now, Little Mother, let’s have a good ‘un!’\n\n‘What shall it be about, Maggy?’\n\n‘Oh, let’s have a princess,’ said Maggy, ‘and let her be a reg’lar one. Beyond all belief, you know!’\n\nLittle Dorrit considered for a moment; and with a rather sad smile upon her face, which was flushed by the sunset, began:\n\n‘Maggy, there was once upon a time a fine King, and he had everything he could wish for, and a great deal more. He had gold and silver, diamonds and rubies, riches of every kind. He had palaces, and he had--’\n\n‘Hospitals,’ interposed Maggy, still nursing her knees. ‘Let him have hospitals, because they’re so comfortable. Hospitals with lots of Chicking.’\n\n‘Yes, he had plenty of them, and he had plenty of everything.’\n\n‘Plenty of baked potatoes, for instance?’ said Maggy.\n\n‘Plenty of everything.’\n\n‘Lor!’ chuckled Maggy, giving her knees a hug. ‘Wasn’t it prime!’\n\n‘This King had a daughter, who was the wisest and most beautiful Princess that ever was seen. When she was a child she understood all her lessons before her masters taught them to her; and when she was grown up, she was the wonder of the world. Now, near the Palace where this Princess lived, there was a cottage in which there was a poor little tiny woman, who lived all alone by herself.’\n\n‘An old woman,’ said Maggy, with an unctuous smack of her lips.\n\n‘No, not an old woman. Quite a young one.’\n\n‘I wonder she warn’t afraid,’ said Maggy. ‘Go on, please.’\n\n‘The Princess passed the cottage nearly every day, and whenever she went by in her beautiful carriage, she saw the poor tiny woman spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the tiny woman, and the tiny woman looked at her. So, one day she stopped the coachman a little way from the cottage, and got out and walked on and peeped in at the door, and there, as usual, was the tiny woman spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the Princess, and the Princess looked at her.’\n\n‘Like trying to stare one another out,’ said Maggy. ‘Please go on, Little Mother.’\n\n‘The Princess was such a wonderful Princess that she had the power of knowing secrets, and she said to the tiny woman, Why do you keep it there? This showed her directly that the Princess knew why she lived all alone by herself spinning at her wheel, and she kneeled down at the Princess’s feet, and asked her never to betray her. So the Princess said, I never will betray you. Let me see it. So the tiny woman closed the shutter of the cottage window and fastened the door, and trembling from head to foot for fear that any one should suspect her, opened a very secret place and showed the Princess a shadow.’\n\n‘Lor!’ said Maggy.\n\n‘It was the shadow of Some one who had gone by long before: of Some one who had gone on far away quite out of reach, never, never to come back. It was bright to look at; and when the tiny woman showed it to the Princess, she was proud of it with all her heart, as a great, great treasure. When the Princess had considered it a little while, she said to the tiny woman, And you keep watch over this every day? And she cast down her eyes, and whispered, Yes. Then the Princess said, Remind me why. To which the other replied, that no one so good and kind had ever passed that way, and that was why in the beginning. She said, too, that nobody missed it, that nobody was the worse for it, that Some one had gone on, to those who were expecting him--’\n\n‘Some one was a man then?’ interposed Maggy.\n\nLittle Dorrit timidly said Yes, she believed so; and resumed:\n\n‘--Had gone on to those who were expecting him, and that this remembrance was stolen or kept back from nobody. The Princess made answer, Ah! But when the cottager died it would be discovered there. The tiny woman told her No; when that time came, it would sink quietly into her own grave, and would never be found.’\n\n‘Well, to be sure!’ said Maggy. ‘Go on, please.’\n\n‘The Princess was very much astonished to hear this, as you may suppose, Maggy.’\n\n[‘And well she might be,’ said Maggy.)\n\n‘So she resolved to watch the tiny woman, and see what came of it. Every day she drove in her beautiful carriage by the cottage-door, and there she saw the tiny woman always alone by herself spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the tiny woman, and the tiny woman looked at her. At last one day the wheel was still, and the tiny woman was not to be seen. When the Princess made inquiries why the wheel had stopped, and where the tiny woman was, she was informed that the wheel had stopped because there was nobody to turn it, the tiny woman being dead.’\n\n[‘They ought to have took her to the Hospital,’ said Maggy, and then she’d have got over it.’)\n\n‘The Princess, after crying a very little for the loss of the tiny woman, dried her eyes and got out of her carriage at the place where she had stopped it before, and went to the cottage and peeped in at the door. There was nobody to look at her now, and nobody for her to look at, so she went in at once to search for the treasured shadow. But there was no sign of it to be found anywhere; and then she knew that the tiny woman had told her the truth, and that it would never give anybody any trouble, and that it had sunk quietly into her own grave, and that she and it were at rest together.\n\n‘That’s all, Maggy.’\n\nThe sunset flush was so bright on Little Dorrit’s face when she came thus to the end of her story, that she interposed her hand to shade it.\n\n‘Had she got to be old?’ Maggy asked.\n\n‘The tiny woman?’\n\n‘Ah!’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘But it would have been just the same if she had been ever so old.’\n\n‘Would it raly!’ said Maggy. ‘Well, I suppose it would though.’ And sat staring and ruminating.\n\nShe sat so long with her eyes wide open, that at length Little Dorrit, to entice her from her box, rose and looked out of window. As she glanced down into the yard, she saw Pancks come in and leer up with the corner of his eye as he went by.\n\n‘Who’s he, Little Mother?’ said Maggy. She had joined her at the window and was leaning on her shoulder. ‘I see him come in and out often.’\n\n‘I have heard him called a fortune-teller,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘But I doubt if he could tell many people even their past or present fortunes.’\n\n‘Couldn’t have told the Princess hers?’ said Maggy.\n\nLittle Dorrit, looking musingly down into the dark valley of the prison, shook her head.\n\n‘Nor the tiny woman hers?’ said Maggy.\n\n‘No,’ said Little Dorrit, with the sunset very bright upon her. ‘But let us come away from the window.’\n\n# CHAPTER 25. Conspirators and Others\n\nThe private residence of Mr Pancks was in Pentonville, where he lodged on the second-floor of a professional gentleman in an extremely small way, who had an inner-door within the street door, poised on a spring and starting open with a click like a trap; and who wrote up in the fan-light, RUGG, GENERAL AGENT, ACCOUNTANT, DEBTS RECOVERED.\n\nThis scroll, majestic in its severe simplicity, illuminated a little slip of front garden abutting on the thirsty high-road, where a few of the dustiest of leaves hung their dismal heads and led a life of choking. A professor of writing occupied the first-floor, and enlivened the garden railings with glass-cases containing choice examples of what his pupils had been before six lessons and while the whole of his young family shook the table, and what they had become after six lessons when the young family was under restraint. The tenancy of Mr Pancks was limited to one airy bedroom; he covenanting and agreeing with Mr Rugg his landlord, that in consideration of a certain scale of payments accurately defined, and on certain verbal notice duly given, he should be at liberty to elect to share the Sunday breakfast, dinner, tea, or supper, or each or any or all of those repasts or meals of Mr and Miss Rugg (his daughter) in the back-parlour.\n\nMiss Rugg was a lady of a little property which she had acquired, together with much distinction in the neighbourhood, by having her heart severely lacerated and her feelings mangled by a middle-aged baker resident in the vicinity, against whom she had, by the agency of Mr Rugg, found it necessary to proceed at law to recover damages for a breach of promise of marriage. The baker having been, by the counsel for Miss Rugg, witheringly denounced on that occasion up to the full amount of twenty guineas, at the rate of about eighteen-pence an epithet, and having been cast in corresponding damages, still suffered occasional persecution from the youth of Pentonville. But Miss Rugg, environed by the majesty of the law, and having her damages invested in the public securities, was regarded with consideration.\n\nIn the society of Mr Rugg, who had a round white visage, as if all his blushes had been drawn out of him long ago, and who had a ragged yellow head like a worn-out hearth broom; and in the society of Miss Rugg, who had little nankeen spots, like shirt buttons, all over her face, and whose own yellow tresses were rather scrubby than luxuriant; Mr Pancks had usually dined on Sundays for some few years, and had twice a week, or so, enjoyed an evening collation of bread, Dutch cheese, and porter. Mr Pancks was one of the very few marriageable men for whom Miss Rugg had no terrors, the argument with which he reassured himself being twofold; that is to say, firstly, ‘that it wouldn’t do twice,’ and secondly, ‘that he wasn’t worth it.’ Fortified within this double armour, Mr Pancks snorted at Miss Rugg on easy terms.\n\nUp to this time, Mr Pancks had transacted little or no business at his quarters in Pentonville, except in the sleeping line; but now that he had become a fortune-teller, he was often closeted after midnight with Mr Rugg in his little front-parlour office, and even after those untimely hours, burnt tallow in his bed-room. Though his duties as his proprietor’s grubber were in no wise lessened; and though that service bore no greater resemblance to a bed of roses than was to be discovered in its many thorns; some new branch of industry made a constant demand upon him. When he cast off the Patriarch at night, it was only to take an anonymous craft in tow, and labour away afresh in other waters.\n\nThe advance from a personal acquaintance with the elder Mr Chivery to an introduction to his amiable wife and disconsolate son, may have been easy; but easy or not, Mr Pancks soon made it. He nestled in the bosom of the tobacco business within a week or two after his first appearance in the College, and particularly addressed himself to the cultivation of a good understanding with Young John. In this endeavour he so prospered as to lure that pining shepherd forth from the groves, and tempt him to undertake mysterious missions; on which he began to disappear at uncertain intervals for as long a space as two or three days together. The prudent Mrs Chivery, who wondered greatly at this change, would have protested against it as detrimental to the Highland typification on the doorpost but for two forcible reasons; one, that her John was roused to take strong interest in the business which these starts were supposed to advance--and this she held to be good for his drooping spirits; the other, that Mr Pancks confidentially agreed to pay her, for the occupation of her son’s time, at the handsome rate of seven and sixpence per day. The proposal originated with himself, and was couched in the pithy terms, ‘If your John is weak enough, ma’am, not to take it, that is no reason why you should be, don’t you see? So, quite between ourselves, ma’am, business being business, here it is!’\n\nWhat Mr Chivery thought of these things, or how much or how little he knew about them, was never gathered from himself. It has been already remarked that he was a man of few words; and it may be here observed that he had imbibed a professional habit of locking everything up. He locked himself up as carefully as he locked up the Marshalsea debtors. Even his custom of bolting his meals may have been a part of an uniform whole; but there is no question, that, as to all other purposes, he kept his mouth as he kept the Marshalsea door. He never opened it without occasion. When it was necessary to let anything out, he opened it a little way, held it open just as long as sufficed for the purpose, and locked it again. Even as he would be sparing of his trouble at the Marshalsea door, and would keep a visitor who wanted to go out, waiting for a few moments if he saw another visitor coming down the yard, so that one turn of the key should suffice for both, similarly he would often reserve a remark if he perceived another on its way to his lips, and would deliver himself of the two together. As to any key to his inner knowledge being to be found in his face, the Marshalsea key was as legible as an index to the individual characters and histories upon which it was turned.\n\nThat Mr Pancks should be moved to invite any one to dinner at Pentonville, was an unprecedented fact in his calendar. But he invited Young John to dinner, and even brought him within range of the dangerous (because expensive) fascinations of Miss Rugg. The banquet was appointed for a Sunday, and Miss Rugg with her own hands stuffed a leg of mutton with oysters on the occasion, and sent it to the baker’s--not _the_ baker’s but an opposition establishment. Provision of oranges, apples, and nuts was also made. And rum was brought home by Mr Pancks on Saturday night, to gladden the visitor’s heart.\n\nThe store of creature comforts was not the chief part of the visitor’s reception. Its special feature was a foregone family confidence and sympathy. When Young John appeared at half-past one without the ivory hand and waistcoat of golden sprigs, the sun shorn of his beams by disastrous clouds, Mr Pancks presented him to the yellow-haired Ruggs as the young man he had so often mentioned who loved Miss Dorrit.\n\n‘I am glad,’ said Mr Rugg, challenging him specially in that character, ‘to have the distinguished gratification of making your acquaintance, sir. Your feelings do you honour. You are young; may you never outlive your feelings! If I was to outlive my own feelings, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, who was a man of many words, and was considered to possess a remarkably good address; ‘if I was to outlive my own feelings, I’d leave fifty pound in my will to the man who would put me out of existence.’\n\nMiss Rugg heaved a sigh.\n\n‘My daughter, sir,’ said Mr Rugg. ‘Anastatia, you are no stranger to the state of this young man’s affections. My daughter has had her trials, sir’--Mr Rugg might have used the word more pointedly in the singular number--‘and she can feel for you.’\n\nYoung John, almost overwhelmed by the touching nature of this greeting, professed himself to that effect.\n\n‘What I envy you, sir, is,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘allow me to take your hat--we are rather short of pegs--I’ll put it in the corner, nobody will tread on it there--What I envy you, sir, is the luxury of your own feelings. I belong to a profession in which that luxury is sometimes denied us.’\n\nYoung John replied, with acknowledgments, that he only hoped he did what was right, and what showed how entirely he was devoted to Miss Dorrit. He wished to be unselfish; and he hoped he was. He wished to do anything as laid in his power to serve Miss Dorrit, altogether putting himself out of sight; and he hoped he did. It was but little that he could do, but he hoped he did it.\n\n‘Sir,’ said Mr Rugg, taking him by the hand, ‘you are a young man that it does one good to come across. You are a young man that I should like to put in the witness-box, to humanise the minds of the legal profession. I hope you have brought your appetite with you, and intend to play a good knife and fork?’\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ returned Young John, ‘I don’t eat much at present.’\n\nMr Rugg drew him a little apart. ‘My daughter’s case, sir,’ said he, ‘at the time when, in vindication of her outraged feelings and her sex, she became the plaintiff in Rugg and Bawkins. I suppose I could have put it in evidence, Mr Chivery, if I had thought it worth my while, that the amount of solid sustenance my daughter consumed at that period did not exceed ten ounces per week.’\n\n‘I think I go a little beyond that, sir,’ returned the other, hesitating, as if he confessed it with some shame.\n\n‘But in your case there’s no fiend in human form,’ said Mr Rugg, with argumentative smile and action of hand. ‘Observe, Mr Chivery! No fiend in human form!’\n\n‘No, sir, certainly,’ Young John added with simplicity, ‘I should be very sorry if there was.’\n\n‘The sentiment,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘is what I should have expected from your known principles. It would affect my daughter greatly, sir, if she heard it. As I perceive the mutton, I am glad she didn’t hear it. Mr Pancks, on this occasion, pray face me. My dear, face Mr Chivery. For what we are going to receive, may we (and Miss Dorrit) be truly thankful!’\n\nBut for a grave waggishness in Mr Rugg’s manner of delivering this introduction to the feast, it might have appeared that Miss Dorrit was expected to be one of the company. Pancks recognised the sally in his usual way, and took in his provender in his usual way. Miss Rugg, perhaps making up some of her arrears, likewise took very kindly to the mutton, and it rapidly diminished to the bone. A bread-and-butter pudding entirely disappeared, and a considerable amount of cheese and radishes vanished by the same means. Then came the dessert.\n\nThen also, and before the broaching of the rum and water, came Mr Pancks’s note-book. The ensuing business proceedings were brief but curious, and rather in the nature of a conspiracy. Mr Pancks looked over his note-book, which was now getting full, studiously; and picked out little extracts, which he wrote on separate slips of paper on the table; Mr Rugg, in the meanwhile, looking at him with close attention, and Young John losing his uncollected eye in mists of meditation. When Mr Pancks, who supported the character of chief conspirator, had completed his extracts, he looked them over, corrected them, put up his note-book, and held them like a hand at cards.\n\n‘Now, there’s a churchyard in Bedfordshire,’ said Pancks. ‘Who takes it?’\n\n‘I’ll take it, sir,’ returned Mr Rugg, ‘if no one bids.’\n\nMr Pancks dealt him his card, and looked at his hand again.\n\n‘Now, there’s an Enquiry in York,’ said Pancks. ‘Who takes it?’\n\n‘I’m not good for York,’ said Mr Rugg.\n\n‘Then perhaps,’ pursued Pancks, ‘you’ll be so obliging, John Chivery?’\n\nYoung John assenting, Pancks dealt him his card, and consulted his hand again.\n\n‘There’s a Church in London; I may as well take that. And a Family Bible; I may as well take that, too. That’s two to me. Two to me,’ repeated Pancks, breathing hard over his cards. ‘Here’s a Clerk at Durham for you, John, and an old seafaring gentleman at Dunstable for you, Mr Rugg. Two to me, was it? Yes, two to me. Here’s a Stone; three to me. And a Still-born Baby; four to me. And all, for the present, told.’\n\nWhen he had thus disposed of his cards, all being done very quietly and in a suppressed tone, Mr Pancks puffed his way into his own breast-pocket and tugged out a canvas bag; from which, with a sparing hand, he told forth money for travelling expenses in two little portions. ‘Cash goes out fast,’ he said anxiously, as he pushed a portion to each of his male companions, ‘very fast.’\n\n‘I can only assure you, Mr Pancks,’ said Young John, ‘that I deeply regret my circumstances being such that I can’t afford to pay my own charges, or that it’s not advisable to allow me the time necessary for my doing the distances on foot; because nothing would give me greater satisfaction than to walk myself off my legs without fee or reward.’\n\nThis young man’s disinterestedness appeared so very ludicrous in the eyes of Miss Rugg, that she was obliged to effect a precipitate retirement from the company, and to sit upon the stairs until she had had her laugh out. Meanwhile Mr Pancks, looking, not without some pity, at Young John, slowly and thoughtfully twisted up his canvas bag as if he were wringing its neck. The lady, returning as he restored it to his pocket, mixed rum and water for the party, not forgetting her fair self, and handed to every one his glass. When all were supplied, Mr Rugg rose, and silently holding out his glass at arm’s length above the centre of the table, by that gesture invited the other three to add theirs, and to unite in a general conspiratorial clink. The ceremony was effective up to a certain point, and would have been wholly so throughout, if Miss Rugg, as she raised her glass to her lips in completion of it, had not happened to look at Young John; when she was again so overcome by the contemptible comicality of his disinterestedness as to splutter some ambrosial drops of rum and water around, and withdraw in confusion.\n\nSuch was the dinner without precedent, given by Pancks at Pentonville; and such was the busy and strange life Pancks led. The only waking moments at which he appeared to relax from his cares, and to recreate himself by going anywhere or saying anything without a pervading object, were when he showed a dawning interest in the lame foreigner with the stick, down Bleeding Heart Yard.\n\nThe foreigner, by name John Baptist Cavalletto--they called him Mr Baptist in the Yard--was such a chirping, easy, hopeful little fellow, that his attraction for Pancks was probably in the force of contrast. Solitary, weak, and scantily acquainted with the most necessary words of the only language in which he could communicate with the people about him, he went with the stream of his fortunes, in a brisk way that was new in those parts. With little to eat, and less to drink, and nothing to wear but what he wore upon him, or had brought tied up in one of the smallest bundles that ever were seen, he put as bright a face upon it as if he were in the most flourishing circumstances when he first hobbled up and down the Yard, humbly propitiating the general good-will with his white teeth.\n\nIt was uphill work for a foreigner, lame or sound, to make his way with the Bleeding Hearts. In the first place, they were vaguely persuaded that every foreigner had a knife about him; in the second, they held it to be a sound constitutional national axiom that he ought to go home to his own country. They never thought of inquiring how many of their own countrymen would be returned upon their hands from divers parts of the world, if the principle were generally recognised; they considered it particularly and peculiarly British. In the third place, they had a notion that it was a sort of Divine visitation upon a foreigner that he was not an Englishman, and that all kinds of calamities happened to his country because it did things that England did not, and did not do things that England did. In this belief, to be sure, they had long been carefully trained by the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings, who were always proclaiming to them, officially, that no country which failed to submit itself to those two large families could possibly hope to be under the protection of Providence; and who, when they believed it, disparaged them in private as the most prejudiced people under the sun.\n\nThis, therefore, might be called a political position of the Bleeding Hearts; but they entertained other objections to having foreigners in the Yard. They believed that foreigners were always badly off; and though they were as ill off themselves as they could desire to be, that did not diminish the force of the objection. They believed that foreigners were dragooned and bayoneted; and though they certainly got their own skulls promptly fractured if they showed any ill-humour, still it was with a blunt instrument, and that didn’t count. They believed that foreigners were always immoral; and though they had an occasional assize at home, and now and then a divorce case or so, that had nothing to do with it. They believed that foreigners had no independent spirit, as never being escorted to the poll in droves by Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle, with colours flying and the tune of Rule Britannia playing. Not to be tedious, they had many other beliefs of a similar kind.\n\nAgainst these obstacles, the lame foreigner with the stick had to make head as well as he could; not absolutely single-handed, because Mr Arthur Clennam had recommended him to the Plornishes (he lived at the top of the same house), but still at heavy odds. However, the Bleeding Hearts were kind hearts; and when they saw the little fellow cheerily limping about with a good-humoured face, doing no harm, drawing no knives, committing no outrageous immoralities, living chiefly on farinaceous and milk diet, and playing with Mrs Plornish’s children of an evening, they began to think that although he could never hope to be an Englishman, still it would be hard to visit that affliction on his head. They began to accommodate themselves to his level, calling him ‘Mr Baptist,’ but treating him like a baby, and laughing immoderately at his lively gestures and his childish English--more, because he didn’t mind it, and laughed too. They spoke to him in very loud voices as if he were stone deaf. They constructed sentences, by way of teaching him the language in its purity, such as were addressed by the savages to Captain Cook, or by Friday to Robinson Crusoe. Mrs Plornish was particularly ingenious in this art; and attained so much celebrity for saying ‘Me ope you leg well soon,’ that it was considered in the Yard but a very short remove indeed from speaking Italian. Even Mrs Plornish herself began to think that she had a natural call towards that language. As he became more popular, household objects were brought into requisition for his instruction in a copious vocabulary; and whenever he appeared in the Yard ladies would fly out at their doors crying ‘Mr Baptist--tea-pot!’ ‘Mr Baptist--dust-pan!’ ‘Mr Baptist--flour-dredger!’ ‘Mr Baptist--coffee-biggin!’ At the same time exhibiting those articles, and penetrating him with a sense of the appalling difficulties of the Anglo-Saxon tongue.\n\nIt was in this stage of his progress, and in about the third week of his occupation, that Mr Pancks’s fancy became attracted by the little man. Mounting to his attic, attended by Mrs Plornish as interpreter, he found Mr Baptist with no furniture but his bed on the ground, a table, and a chair, carving with the aid of a few simple tools, in the blithest way possible.\n\n‘Now, old chap,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘pay up!’\n\nHe had his money ready, folded in a scrap of paper, and laughingly handed it in; then with a free action, threw out as many fingers of his right hand as there were shillings, and made a cut crosswise in the air for an odd sixpence.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Mr Pancks, watching him, wonderingly. ‘That’s it, is it? You’re a quick customer. It’s all right. I didn’t expect to receive it, though.’\n\nMrs Plornish here interposed with great condescension, and explained to Mr Baptist. ‘E please. E glad get money.’\n\nThe little man smiled and nodded. His bright face seemed uncommonly attractive to Mr Pancks. ‘How’s he getting on in his limb?’ he asked Mrs Plornish.\n\n‘Oh, he’s a deal better, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish. ‘We expect next week he’ll be able to leave off his stick entirely.’ (The opportunity being too favourable to be lost, Mrs Plornish displayed her great accomplishment by explaining with pardonable pride to Mr Baptist, ‘E ope you leg well soon.’)\n\n‘He’s a merry fellow, too,’ said Mr Pancks, admiring him as if he were a mechanical toy. ‘How does he live?’\n\n‘Why, sir,’ rejoined Mrs Plornish, ‘he turns out to have quite a power of carving them flowers that you see him at now.’ (Mr Baptist, watching their faces as they spoke, held up his work. Mrs Plornish interpreted in her Italian manner, on behalf of Mr Pancks, ‘E please. Double good!’)\n\n‘Can he live by that?’ asked Mr Pancks.\n\n‘He can live on very little, sir, and it is expected as he will be able, in time, to make a very good living. Mr Clennam got it him to do, and gives him odd jobs besides in at the Works next door--makes ‘em for him, in short, when he knows he wants ‘em.’\n\n‘And what does he do with himself, now, when he ain’t hard at it?’ said Mr Pancks.\n\n‘Why, not much as yet, sir, on accounts I suppose of not being able to walk much; but he goes about the Yard, and he chats without particular understanding or being understood, and he plays with the children, and he sits in the sun--he’ll sit down anywhere, as if it was an arm-chair--and he’ll sing, and he’ll laugh!’\n\n‘Laugh!’ echoed Mr Pancks. ‘He looks to me as if every tooth in his head was always laughing.’\n\n‘But whenever he gets to the top of the steps at t’other end of the Yard,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘he’ll peep out in the curiousest way! So that some of us thinks he’s peeping out towards where his own country is, and some of us thinks he’s looking for somebody he don’t want to see, and some of us don’t know what to think.’\n\nMr Baptist seemed to have a general understanding of what she said; or perhaps his quickness caught and applied her slight action of peeping. In any case he closed his eyes and tossed his head with the air of a man who had sufficient reasons for what he did, and said in his own tongue, it didn’t matter. Altro!\n\n‘What’s Altro?’ said Pancks.\n\n‘Hem! It’s a sort of a general kind of expression, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish.\n\n‘Is it?’ said Pancks. ‘Why, then Altro to you, old chap. Good afternoon. Altro!’\n\nMr Baptist in his vivacious way repeating the word several times, Mr Pancks in his duller way gave it him back once. From that time it became a frequent custom with Pancks the gipsy, as he went home jaded at night, to pass round by Bleeding Heart Yard, go quietly up the stairs, look in at Mr Baptist’s door, and, finding him in his room, to say, ‘Hallo, old chap! Altro!’ To which Mr Baptist would reply with innumerable bright nods and smiles, ‘Altro, signore, altro, altro, altro!’ After this highly condensed conversation, Mr Pancks would go his way with an appearance of being lightened and refreshed.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Nobody’s State of Mind\n\nIf Arthur Clennam had not arrived at that wise decision firmly to restrain himself from loving Pet, he would have lived on in a state of much perplexity, involving difficult struggles with his own heart. Not the least of these would have been a contention, always waging within it, between a tendency to dislike Mr Henry Gowan, if not to regard him with positive repugnance, and a whisper that the inclination was unworthy. A generous nature is not prone to strong aversions, and is slow to admit them even dispassionately; but when it finds ill-will gaining upon it, and can discern between-whiles that its origin is not dispassionate, such a nature becomes distressed.\n\nTherefore Mr Henry Gowan would have clouded Clennam’s mind, and would have been far oftener present to it than more agreeable persons and subjects but for the great prudence of his decision aforesaid. As it was, Mr Gowan seemed transferred to Daniel Doyce’s mind; at all events, it so happened that it usually fell to Mr Doyce’s turn, rather than to Clennam’s, to speak of him in the friendly conversations they held together. These were of frequent occurrence now; as the two partners shared a portion of a roomy house in one of the grave old-fashioned City streets, lying not far from the Bank of England, by London Wall.\n\nMr Doyce had been to Twickenham to pass the day. Clennam had excused himself. Mr Doyce was just come home. He put in his head at the door of Clennam’s sitting-room to say Good night.\n\n‘Come in, come in!’ said Clennam.\n\n‘I saw you were reading,’ returned Doyce, as he entered, ‘and thought you might not care to be disturbed.’\n\nBut for the notable resolution he had made, Clennam really might not have known what he had been reading; really might not have had his eyes upon the book for an hour past, though it lay open before him. He shut it up, rather quickly.\n\n‘Are they well?’ he asked.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Doyce; ‘they are well. They are all well.’\n\nDaniel had an old workmanlike habit of carrying his pocket-handkerchief in his hat. He took it out and wiped his forehead with it, slowly repeating, ‘They are all well. Miss Minnie looking particularly well, I thought.’\n\n‘Any company at the cottage?’\n\n‘No, no company.’\n\n‘And how did you get on, you four?’ asked Clennam gaily.\n\n‘There were five of us,’ returned his partner. ‘There was What’s-his-name. He was there.’\n\n‘Who is he?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Mr Henry Gowan.’\n\n‘Ah, to be sure!’ cried Clennam with unusual vivacity, ‘Yes!--I forgot him.’\n\n‘As I mentioned, you may remember,’ said Daniel Doyce, ‘he is always there on Sunday.’\n\n‘Yes, yes,’ returned Clennam; ‘I remember now.’\n\nDaniel Doyce, still wiping his forehead, ploddingly repeated. ‘Yes. He was there, he was there. Oh yes, he was there. And his dog. _He_ was there too.’\n\n‘Miss Meagles is quite attached to--the--dog,’ observed Clennam.\n\n‘Quite so,’ assented his partner. ‘More attached to the dog than I am to the man.’\n\n‘You mean Mr--?’\n\n‘I mean Mr Gowan, most decidedly,’ said Daniel Doyce.\n\nThere was a gap in the conversation, which Clennam devoted to winding up his watch.\n\n‘Perhaps you are a little hasty in your judgment,’ he said. ‘Our judgments--I am supposing a general case--’\n\n‘Of course,’ said Doyce.\n\n‘Are so liable to be influenced by many considerations, which, almost without our knowing it, are unfair, that it is necessary to keep a guard upon them. For instance, Mr--’\n\n‘Gowan,’ quietly said Doyce, upon whom the utterance of the name almost always devolved.\n\n‘Is young and handsome, easy and quick, has talent, and has seen a good deal of various kinds of life. It might be difficult to give an unselfish reason for being prepossessed against him.’\n\n‘Not difficult for me, I think, Clennam,’ returned his partner. ‘I see him bringing present anxiety, and, I fear, future sorrow, into my old friend’s house. I see him wearing deeper lines into my old friend’s face, the nearer he draws to, and the oftener he looks at, the face of his daughter. In short, I see him with a net about the pretty and affectionate creature whom he will never make happy.’\n\n‘We don’t know,’ said Clennam, almost in the tone of a man in pain, ‘that he will not make her happy.’\n\n‘We don’t know,’ returned his partner, ‘that the earth will last another hundred years, but we think it highly probable.’\n\n‘Well, well!’ said Clennam, ‘we must be hopeful, and we must at least try to be, if not generous (which, in this case, we have no opportunity of being), just. We will not disparage this gentleman, because he is successful in his addresses to the beautiful object of his ambition; and we will not question her natural right to bestow her love on one whom she finds worthy of it.’\n\n‘Maybe, my friend,’ said Doyce. ‘Maybe also, that she is too young and petted, too confiding and inexperienced, to discriminate well.’\n\n‘That,’ said Clennam, ‘would be far beyond our power of correction.’\n\nDaniel Doyce shook his head gravely, and rejoined, ‘I fear so.’\n\n‘Therefore, in a word,’ said Clennam, ‘we should make up our minds that it is not worthy of us to say any ill of Mr Gowan. It would be a poor thing to gratify a prejudice against him. And I resolve, for my part, not to depreciate him.’\n\n‘I am not quite so sure of myself, and therefore I reserve my privilege of objecting to him,’ returned the other. ‘But, if I am not sure of myself, I am sure of you, Clennam, and I know what an upright man you are, and how much to be respected. Good night, _my_ friend and partner!’ He shook his hand in saying this, as if there had been something serious at the bottom of their conversation; and they separated.\n\nBy this time they had visited the family on several occasions, and had always observed that even a passing allusion to Mr Henry Gowan when he was not among them, brought back the cloud which had obscured Mr Meagles’s sunshine on the morning of the chance encounter at the Ferry. If Clennam had ever admitted the forbidden passion into his breast, this period might have been a period of real trial; under the actual circumstances, doubtless it was nothing--nothing.\n\nEqually, if his heart had given entertainment to that prohibited guest, his silent fighting of his way through the mental condition of this period might have been a little meritorious. In the constant effort not to be betrayed into a new phase of the besetting sin of his experience, the pursuit of selfish objects by low and small means, and to hold instead to some high principle of honour and generosity, there might have been a little merit. In the resolution not even to avoid Mr Meagles’s house, lest, in the selfish sparing of himself, he should bring any slight distress upon the daughter through making her the cause of an estrangement which he believed the father would regret, there might have been a little merit. In the modest truthfulness of always keeping in view the greater equality of Mr Gowan’s years and the greater attractions of his person and manner, there might have been a little merit. In doing all this and much more, in a perfectly unaffected way and with a manful and composed constancy, while the pain within him (peculiar as his life and history) was very sharp, there might have been some quiet strength of character. But, after the resolution he had made, of course he could have no such merits as these; and such a state of mind was nobody’s--nobody’s.\n\nMr Gowan made it no concern of his whether it was nobody’s or somebody’s. He preserved his perfect serenity of manner on all occasions, as if the possibility of Clennam’s presuming to have debated the great question were too distant and ridiculous to be imagined. He had always an affability to bestow on Clennam and an ease to treat him with, which might of itself (in the supposititious case of his not having taken that sagacious course) have been a very uncomfortable element in his state of mind.\n\n‘I quite regret you were not with us yesterday,’ said Mr Henry Gowan, calling on Clennam the next morning. ‘We had an agreeable day up the river there.’\n\nSo he had heard, Arthur said.\n\n‘From your partner?’ returned Henry Gowan. ‘What a dear old fellow he is!’\n\n‘I have a great regard for him.’\n\n‘By Jove, he is the finest creature!’ said Gowan. ‘So fresh, so green, trusts in such wonderful things!’\n\nHere was one of the many little rough points that had a tendency to grate on Clennam’s hearing. He put it aside by merely repeating that he had a high regard for Mr Doyce.\n\n‘He is charming! To see him mooning along to that time of life, laying down nothing by the way and picking up nothing by the way, is delightful. It warms a man. So unspoilt, so simple, such a good soul! Upon my life Mr Clennam, one feels desperately worldly and wicked in comparison with such an innocent creature. I speak for myself, let me add, without including you. You are genuine also.’\n\n‘Thank you for the compliment,’ said Clennam, ill at ease; ‘you are too, I hope?’\n\n‘So so,’ rejoined the other. ‘To be candid with you, tolerably. I am not a great impostor. Buy one of my pictures, and I assure you, in confidence, it will not be worth the money. Buy one of another man’s--any great professor who beats me hollow--and the chances are that the more you give him, the more he’ll impose upon you. They all do it.’\n\n‘All painters?’\n\n‘Painters, writers, patriots, all the rest who have stands in the market. Give almost any man I know ten pounds, and he will impose upon you to a corresponding extent; a thousand pounds--to a corresponding extent; ten thousand pounds--to a corresponding extent. So great the success, so great the imposition. But what a capital world it is!’ cried Gowan with warm enthusiasm. ‘What a jolly, excellent, lovable world it is!’\n\n‘I had rather thought,’ said Clennam, ‘that the principle you mention was chiefly acted on by--’\n\n‘By the Barnacles?’ interrupted Gowan, laughing.\n\n‘By the political gentlemen who condescend to keep the Circumlocution Office.’\n\n‘Ah! Don’t be hard upon the Barnacles,’ said Gowan, laughing afresh, ‘they are darling fellows! Even poor little Clarence, the born idiot of the family, is the most agreeable and most endearing blockhead! And by Jupiter, with a kind of cleverness in him too that would astonish you!’\n\n‘It would. Very much,’ said Clennam, drily.\n\n‘And after all,’ cried Gowan, with that characteristic balancing of his which reduced everything in the wide world to the same light weight, ‘though I can’t deny that the Circumlocution Office may ultimately shipwreck everybody and everything, still, that will probably not be in our time--and it’s a school for gentlemen.’\n\n‘It’s a very dangerous, unsatisfactory, and expensive school to the people who pay to keep the pupils there, I am afraid,’ said Clennam, shaking his head.\n\n‘Ah! You are a terrible fellow,’ returned Gowan, airily. ‘I can understand how you have frightened that little donkey, Clarence, the most estimable of moon-calves (I really love him) nearly out of his wits. But enough of him, and of all the rest of them. I want to present you to my mother, Mr Clennam. Pray do me the favour to give me the opportunity.’\n\nIn nobody’s state of mind, there was nothing Clennam would have desired less, or would have been more at a loss how to avoid.\n\n‘My mother lives in a most primitive manner down in that dreary red-brick dungeon at Hampton Court,’ said Gowan. ‘If you would make your own appointment, suggest your own day for permitting me to take you there to dinner, you would be bored and she would be charmed. Really that’s the state of the case.’\n\nWhat could Clennam say after this? His retiring character included a great deal that was simple in the best sense, because unpractised and unused; and in his simplicity and modesty, he could only say that he was happy to place himself at Mr Gowan’s disposal. Accordingly he said it, and the day was fixed. And a dreaded day it was on his part, and a very unwelcome day when it came and they went down to Hampton Court together.\n\nThe venerable inhabitants of that venerable pile seemed, in those times, to be encamped there like a sort of civilised gipsies. There was a temporary air about their establishments, as if they were going away the moment they could get anything better; there was also a dissatisfied air about themselves, as if they took it very ill that they had not already got something much better. Genteel blinds and makeshifts were more or less observable as soon as their doors were opened; screens not half high enough, which made dining-rooms out of arched passages, and warded off obscure corners where footboys slept at nights with their heads among the knives and forks; curtains which called upon you to believe that they didn’t hide anything; panes of glass which requested you not to see them; many objects of various forms, feigning to have no connection with their guilty secret, a bed; disguised traps in walls, which were clearly coal-cellars; affectations of no thoroughfares, which were evidently doors to little kitchens. Mental reservations and artful mysteries grew out of these things. Callers looking steadily into the eyes of their receivers, pretended not to smell cooking three feet off; people, confronting closets accidentally left open, pretended not to see bottles; visitors with their heads against a partition of thin canvas, and a page and a young female at high words on the other side, made believe to be sitting in a primeval silence. There was no end to the small social accommodation-bills of this nature which the gipsies of gentility were constantly drawing upon, and accepting for, one another.\n\nSome of these Bohemians were of an irritable temperament, as constantly soured and vexed by two mental trials: the first, the consciousness that they had never got enough out of the public; the second, the consciousness that the public were admitted into the building. Under the latter great wrong, a few suffered dreadfully--particularly on Sundays, when they had for some time expected the earth to open and swallow the public up; but which desirable event had not yet occurred, in consequence of some reprehensible laxity in the arrangements of the Universe.\n\nMrs Gowan’s door was attended by a family servant of several years’ standing, who had his own crow to pluck with the public concerning a situation in the Post-Office which he had been for some time expecting, and to which he was not yet appointed. He perfectly knew that the public could never have got him in, but he grimly gratified himself with the idea that the public kept him out. Under the influence of this injury (and perhaps of some little straitness and irregularity in the matter of wages), he had grown neglectful of his person and morose in mind; and now beholding in Clennam one of the degraded body of his oppressors, received him with ignominy.\n\nMrs Gowan, however, received him with condescension. He found her a courtly old lady, formerly a Beauty, and still sufficiently well-favoured to have dispensed with the powder on her nose and a certain impossible bloom under each eye. She was a little lofty with him; so was another old lady, dark-browed and high-nosed, and who must have had something real about her or she could not have existed, but it was certainly not her hair or her teeth or her figure or her complexion; so was a grey old gentleman of dignified and sullen appearance; both of whom had come to dinner. But, as they had all been in the British Embassy way in sundry parts of the earth, and as a British Embassy cannot better establish a character with the Circumlocution Office than by treating its compatriots with illimitable contempt (else it would become like the Embassies of other countries), Clennam felt that on the whole they let him off lightly.\n\nThe dignified old gentleman turned out to be Lord Lancaster Stiltstalking, who had been maintained by the Circumlocution Office for many years as a representative of the Britannic Majesty abroad. This noble Refrigerator had iced several European courts in his time, and had done it with such complete success that the very name of Englishman yet struck cold to the stomachs of foreigners who had the distinguished honour of remembering him at a distance of a quarter of a century.\n\nHe was now in retirement, and hence (in a ponderous white cravat, like a stiff snow-drift) was so obliging as to shade the dinner. There was a whisper of the pervading Bohemian character in the nomadic nature of the service and its curious races of plates and dishes; but the noble Refrigerator, infinitely better than plate or porcelain, made it superb. He shaded the dinner, cooled the wines, chilled the gravy, and blighted the vegetables.\n\nThere was only one other person in the room: a microscopically small footboy, who waited on the malevolent man who hadn’t got into the Post-Office. Even this youth, if his jacket could have been unbuttoned and his heart laid bare, would have been seen, as a distant adherent of the Barnacle family, already to aspire to a situation under Government.\n\nMrs Gowan with a gentle melancholy upon her, occasioned by her son’s being reduced to court the swinish public as a follower of the low Arts, instead of asserting his birthright and putting a ring through its nose as an acknowledged Barnacle, headed the conversation at dinner on the evil days. It was then that Clennam learned for the first time what little pivots this great world goes round upon.\n\n‘If John Barnacle,’ said Mrs Gowan, after the degeneracy of the times had been fully ascertained, ‘if John Barnacle had but abandoned his most unfortunate idea of conciliating the mob, all would have been well, and I think the country would have been preserved.’\n\nThe old lady with the high nose assented; but added that if Augustus Stiltstalking had in a general way ordered the cavalry out with instructions to charge, she thought the country would have been preserved.\n\nThe noble Refrigerator assented; but added that if William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, when they came over to one another and formed their ever-memorable coalition, had boldly muzzled the newspapers, and rendered it penal for any Editor-person to presume to discuss the conduct of any appointed authority abroad or at home, he thought the country would have been preserved.\n\nIt was agreed that the country (another word for the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings) wanted preserving, but how it came to want preserving was not so clear. It was only clear that the question was all about John Barnacle, Augustus Stiltstalking, William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, Tom, Dick, or Harry Barnacle or Stiltstalking, because there was nobody else but mob. And this was the feature of the conversation which impressed Clennam, as a man not used to it, very disagreeably: making him doubt if it were quite right to sit there, silently hearing a great nation narrowed to such little bounds. Remembering, however, that in the Parliamentary debates, whether on the life of that nation’s body or the life of its soul, the question was usually all about and between John Barnacle, Augustus Stiltstalking, William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, Tom, Dick, or Harry Barnacle or Stiltstalking, and nobody else; he said nothing on the part of mob, bethinking himself that mob was used to it.\n\nMr Henry Gowan seemed to have a malicious pleasure in playing off the three talkers against each other, and in seeing Clennam startled by what they said. Having as supreme a contempt for the class that had thrown him off as for the class that had not taken him on, he had no personal disquiet in anything that passed. His healthy state of mind appeared even to derive a gratification from Clennam’s position of embarrassment and isolation among the good company; and if Clennam had been in that condition with which Nobody was incessantly contending, he would have suspected it, and would have struggled with the suspicion as a meanness, even while he sat at the table.\n\nIn the course of a couple of hours the noble Refrigerator, at no time less than a hundred years behind the period, got about five centuries in arrears, and delivered solemn political oracles appropriate to that epoch. He finished by freezing a cup of tea for his own drinking, and retiring at his lowest temperature.\n\nThen Mrs Gowan, who had been accustomed in her days of a vacant arm-chair beside her to which to summon state to retain her devoted slaves, one by one, for short audiences as marks of her especial favour, invited Clennam with a turn of her fan to approach the presence. He obeyed, and took the tripod recently vacated by Lord Lancaster Stiltstalking.\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘apart from the happiness I have in becoming known to you, though in this odiously inconvenient place--a mere barrack--there is a subject on which I am dying to speak to you. It is the subject in connection with which my son first had, I believe, the pleasure of cultivating your acquaintance.’\n\nClennam inclined his head, as a generally suitable reply to what he did not yet quite understand.\n\n‘First,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘now, is she really pretty?’\n\nIn nobody’s difficulties, he would have found it very difficult to answer; very difficult indeed to smile, and say ‘Who?’\n\n‘Oh! You know!’ she returned. ‘This flame of Henry’s. This unfortunate fancy. There! If it is a point of honour that I should originate the name--Miss Mickles--Miggles.’\n\n‘Miss Meagles,’ said Clennam, ‘is very beautiful.’\n\n‘Men are so often mistaken on those points,’ returned Mrs Gowan, shaking her head, ‘that I candidly confess to you I feel anything but sure of it, even now; though it is something to have Henry corroborated with so much gravity and emphasis. He picked the people up at Rome, I think?’\n\nThe phrase would have given nobody mortal offence. Clennam replied, ‘Excuse me, I doubt if I understand your expression.’\n\n‘Picked the people up,’ said Mrs Gowan, tapping the sticks of her closed fan (a large green one, which she used as a hand-screen) on her little table. ‘Came upon them. Found them out. Stumbled against them.’\n\n‘The people?’\n\n‘Yes. The Miggles people.’\n\n‘I really cannot say,’ said Clennam, ‘where my friend Mr Meagles first presented Mr Henry Gowan to his daughter.’\n\n‘I am pretty sure he picked her up at Rome; but never mind where--somewhere. Now (this is entirely between ourselves), is she very plebeian?’\n\n‘Really, ma’am,’ returned Clennam, ‘I am so undoubtedly plebeian myself, that I do not feel qualified to judge.’\n\n‘Very neat!’ said Mrs Gowan, coolly unfurling her screen. ‘Very happy! From which I infer that you secretly think her manner equal to her looks?’\n\nClennam, after a moment’s stiffness, bowed.\n\n‘That’s comforting, and I hope you may be right. Did Henry tell me you had travelled with them?’\n\n‘I travelled with my friend Mr Meagles, and his wife and daughter, during some months.’ (Nobody’s heart might have been wrung by the remembrance.)\n\n‘Really comforting, because you must have had a large experience of them. You see, Mr Clennam, this thing has been going on for a long time, and I find no improvement in it. Therefore to have the opportunity of speaking to one so well informed about it as yourself, is an immense relief to me. Quite a boon. Quite a blessing, I am sure.’\n\n‘Pardon me,’ returned Clennam, ‘but I am not in Mr Henry Gowan’s confidence. I am far from being so well informed as you suppose me to be. Your mistake makes my position a very delicate one. No word on this topic has ever passed between Mr Henry Gowan and myself.’\n\nMrs Gowan glanced at the other end of the room, where her son was playing ecarte on a sofa, with the old lady who was for a charge of cavalry.\n\n‘Not in his confidence? No,’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘No word has passed between you? No. That I can imagine. But there are unexpressed confidences, Mr Clennam; and as you have been together intimately among these people, I cannot doubt that a confidence of that sort exists in the present case. Perhaps you have heard that I have suffered the keenest distress of mind from Henry’s having taken to a pursuit which--well!’ shrugging her shoulders, ‘a very respectable pursuit, I dare say, and some artists are, as artists, quite superior persons; still, we never yet in our family have gone beyond an Amateur, and it is a pardonable weakness to feel a little--’\n\nAs Mrs Gowan broke off to heave a sigh, Clennam, however resolute to be magnanimous, could not keep down the thought that there was mighty little danger of the family’s ever going beyond an Amateur, even as it was.\n\n‘Henry,’ the mother resumed, ‘is self-willed and resolute; and as these people naturally strain every nerve to catch him, I can entertain very little hope, Mr Clennam, that the thing will be broken off. I apprehend the girl’s fortune will be very small; Henry might have done much better; there is scarcely anything to compensate for the connection: still, he acts for himself; and if I find no improvement within a short time, I see no other course than to resign myself and make the best of these people. I am infinitely obliged to you for what you have told me.’\n\nAs she shrugged her shoulders, Clennam stiffly bowed again. With an uneasy flush upon his face, and hesitation in his manner, he then said in a still lower tone than he had adopted yet:\n\n‘Mrs Gowan, I scarcely know how to acquit myself of what I feel to be a duty, and yet I must ask you for your kind consideration in attempting to discharge it. A misconception on your part, a very great misconception if I may venture to call it so, seems to require setting right. You have supposed Mr Meagles and his family to strain every nerve, I think you said--’\n\n‘Every nerve,’ repeated Mrs Gowan, looking at him in calm obstinacy, with her green fan between her face and the fire.\n\n‘To secure Mr Henry Gowan?’\n\nThe lady placidly assented.\n\n‘Now that is so far,’ said Arthur, ‘from being the case, that I know Mr Meagles to be unhappy in this matter; and to have interposed all reasonable obstacles with the hope of putting an end to it.’\n\nMrs Gowan shut up her great green fan, tapped him on the arm with it, and tapped her smiling lips. ‘Why, of course,’ said she. ‘Just what I mean.’\n\nArthur watched her face for some explanation of what she did mean.\n\n‘Are you really serious, Mr Clennam? Don’t you see?’\n\nArthur did not see; and said so.\n\n‘Why, don’t I know my son, and don’t I know that this is exactly the way to hold him?’ said Mrs Gowan, contemptuously; ‘and do not these Miggles people know it, at least as well as I? Oh, shrewd people, Mr Clennam: evidently people of business! I believe Miggles belonged to a Bank. It ought to have been a very profitable Bank, if he had much to do with its management. This is very well done, indeed.’\n\n‘I beg and entreat you, ma’am--’ Arthur interposed.\n\n‘Oh, Mr Clennam, can you really be so credulous?’\n\nIt made such a painful impression upon him to hear her talking in this haughty tone, and to see her patting her contemptuous lips with her fan, that he said very earnestly, ‘Believe me, ma’am, this is unjust, a perfectly groundless suspicion.’\n\n‘Suspicion?’ repeated Mrs Gowan. ‘Not suspicion, Mr Clennam, Certainty. It is very knowingly done indeed, and seems to have taken _you_ in completely.’ She laughed; and again sat tapping her lips with her fan, and tossing her head, as if she added, ‘Don’t tell me. I know such people will do anything for the honour of such an alliance.’\n\nAt this opportune moment, the cards were thrown up, and Mr Henry Gowan came across the room saying, ‘Mother, if you can spare Mr Clennam for this time, we have a long way to go, and it’s getting late.’ Mr Clennam thereupon rose, as he had no choice but to do; and Mrs Gowan showed him, to the last, the same look and the same tapped contemptuous lips.\n\n‘You have had a portentously long audience of my mother,’ said Gowan, as the door closed upon them. ‘I fervently hope she has not bored you?’\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Clennam.\n\nThey had a little open phaeton for the journey, and were soon in it on the road home. Gowan, driving, lighted a cigar; Clennam declined one. Do what he would, he fell into such a mood of abstraction that Gowan said again, ‘I am very much afraid my mother has bored you?’ To which he roused himself to answer, ‘Not at all!’ and soon relapsed again.\n\nIn that state of mind which rendered nobody uneasy, his thoughtfulness would have turned principally on the man at his side. He would have thought of the morning when he first saw him rooting out the stones with his heel, and would have asked himself, ‘Does he jerk me out of the path in the same careless, cruel way?’ He would have thought, had this introduction to his mother been brought about by him because he knew what she would say, and that he could thus place his position before a rival and loftily warn him off, without himself reposing a word of confidence in him? He would have thought, even if there were no such design as that, had he brought him there to play with his repressed emotions, and torment him? The current of these meditations would have been stayed sometimes by a rush of shame, bearing a remonstrance to himself from his own open nature, representing that to shelter such suspicions, even for the passing moment, was not to hold the high, unenvious course he had resolved to keep. At those times, the striving within him would have been hardest; and looking up and catching Gowan’s eyes, he would have started as if he had done him an injury.\n\nThen, looking at the dark road and its uncertain objects, he would have gradually trailed off again into thinking, ‘Where are we driving, he and I, I wonder, on the darker road of life? How will it be with us, and with her, in the obscure distance?’ Thinking of her, he would have been troubled anew with a reproachful misgiving that it was not even loyal to her to dislike him, and that in being so easily prejudiced against him he was less deserving of her than at first.\n\n‘You are evidently out of spirits,’ said Gowan; ‘I am very much afraid my mother must have bored you dreadfully.’\n\n‘Believe me, not at all,’ said Clennam. ‘It’s nothing--nothing!’\n\n# CHAPTER 27. Five-and-Twenty\n\nA frequently recurring doubt, whether Mr Pancks’s desire to collect information relative to the Dorrit family could have any possible bearing on the misgivings he had imparted to his mother on his return from his long exile, caused Arthur Clennam much uneasiness at this period. What Mr Pancks already knew about the Dorrit family, what more he really wanted to find out, and why he should trouble his busy head about them at all, were questions that often perplexed him. Mr Pancks was not a man to waste his time and trouble in researches prompted by idle curiosity. That he had a specific object Clennam could not doubt. And whether the attainment of that object by Mr Pancks’s industry might bring to light, in some untimely way, secret reasons which had induced his mother to take Little Dorrit by the hand, was a serious speculation.\n\nNot that he ever wavered either in his desire or his determination to repair a wrong that had been done in his father’s time, should a wrong come to light, and be reparable. The shadow of a supposed act of injustice, which had hung over him since his father’s death, was so vague and formless that it might be the result of a reality widely remote from his idea of it. But, if his apprehensions should prove to be well founded, he was ready at any moment to lay down all he had, and begin the world anew. As the fierce dark teaching of his childhood had never sunk into his heart, so that first article in his code of morals was, that he must begin, in practical humility, with looking well to his feet on Earth, and that he could never mount on wings of words to Heaven. Duty on earth, restitution on earth, action on earth; these first, as the first steep steps upward. Strait was the gate and narrow was the way; far straiter and narrower than the broad high road paved with vain professions and vain repetitions, motes from other men’s eyes and liberal delivery of others to the judgment--all cheap materials costing absolutely nothing.\n\nNo. It was not a selfish fear or hesitation that rendered him uneasy, but a mistrust lest Pancks might not observe his part of the understanding between them, and, making any discovery, might take some course upon it without imparting it to him. On the other hand, when he recalled his conversation with Pancks, and the little reason he had to suppose that there was any likelihood of that strange personage being on that track at all, there were times when he wondered that he made so much of it. Labouring in this sea, as all barks labour in cross seas, he tossed about and came to no haven.\n\nThe removal of Little Dorrit herself from their customary association, did not mend the matter. She was so much out, and so much in her own room, that he began to miss her and to find a blank in her place. He had written to her to inquire if she were better, and she had written back, very gratefully and earnestly telling him not to be uneasy on her behalf, for she was quite well; but he had not seen her, for what, in their intercourse, was a long time.\n\nHe returned home one evening from an interview with her father, who had mentioned that she was out visiting--which was what he always said when she was hard at work to buy his supper--and found Mr Meagles in an excited state walking up and down his room. On his opening the door, Mr Meagles stopped, faced round, and said:\n\n‘Clennam!--Tattycoram!’\n\n‘What’s the matter?’\n\n‘Lost!’\n\n‘Why, bless my heart alive!’ cried Clennam in amazement. ‘What do you mean?’\n\n‘Wouldn’t count five-and-twenty, sir; couldn’t be got to do it; stopped at eight, and took herself off.’\n\n‘Left your house?’\n\n‘Never to come back,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head. ‘You don’t know that girl’s passionate and proud character. A team of horses couldn’t draw her back now; the bolts and bars of the old Bastille couldn’t keep her.’\n\n‘How did it happen? Pray sit down and tell me.’\n\n‘As to how it happened, it’s not so easy to relate: because you must have the unfortunate temperament of the poor impetuous girl herself, before you can fully understand it. But it came about in this way. Pet and Mother and I have been having a good deal of talk together of late. I’ll not disguise from you, Clennam, that those conversations have not been of as bright a kind as I could wish; they have referred to our going away again. In proposing to do which, I have had, in fact, an object.’\n\nNobody’s heart beat quickly.\n\n‘An object,’ said Mr Meagles, after a moment’s pause, ‘that I will not disguise from you, either, Clennam. There’s an inclination on the part of my dear child which I am sorry for. Perhaps you guess the person. Henry Gowan.’\n\n‘I was not unprepared to hear it.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr Meagles, with a heavy sigh, ‘I wish to God you had never had to hear it. However, so it is. Mother and I have done all we could to get the better of it, Clennam. We have tried tender advice, we have tried time, we have tried absence. As yet, of no use. Our late conversations have been upon the subject of going away for another year at least, in order that there might be an entire separation and breaking off for that term. Upon that question, Pet has been unhappy, and therefore Mother and I have been unhappy.’\n\nClennam said that he could easily believe it.\n\n‘Well!’ continued Mr Meagles in an apologetic way, ‘I admit as a practical man, and I am sure Mother would admit as a practical woman, that we do, in families, magnify our troubles and make mountains of our molehills in a way that is calculated to be rather trying to people who look on--to mere outsiders, you know, Clennam. Still, Pet’s happiness or unhappiness is quite a life or death question with us; and we may be excused, I hope, for making much of it. At all events, it might have been borne by Tattycoram. Now, don’t you think so?’\n\n‘I do indeed think so,’ returned Clennam, in most emphatic recognition of this very moderate expectation.\n\n‘No, sir,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head ruefully. ‘She couldn’t stand it. The chafing and firing of that girl, the wearing and tearing of that girl within her own breast, has been such that I have softly said to her again and again in passing her, “Five-and-twenty, Tattycoram, five-and-twenty!” I heartily wish she could have gone on counting five-and-twenty day and night, and then it wouldn’t have happened.’\n\nMr Meagles with a despondent countenance in which the goodness of his heart was even more expressed than in his times of cheerfulness and gaiety, stroked his face down from his forehead to his chin, and shook his head again.\n\n‘I said to Mother (not that it was necessary, for she would have thought it all for herself), we are practical people, my dear, and we know her story; we see in this unhappy girl some reflection of what was raging in her mother’s heart before ever such a creature as this poor thing was in the world; we’ll gloss her temper over, Mother, we won’t notice it at present, my dear, we’ll take advantage of some better disposition in her another time. So we said nothing. But, do what we would, it seems as if it was to be; she broke out violently one night.’\n\n‘How, and why?’\n\n‘If you ask me Why,’ said Mr Meagles, a little disturbed by the question, for he was far more intent on softening her case than the family’s, ‘I can only refer you to what I have just repeated as having been pretty near my words to Mother. As to How, we had said Good night to Pet in her presence (very affectionately, I must allow), and she had attended Pet up-stairs--you remember she was her maid. Perhaps Pet, having been out of sorts, may have been a little more inconsiderate than usual in requiring services of her: but I don’t know that I have any right to say so; she was always thoughtful and gentle.’\n\n‘The gentlest mistress in the world.’\n\n‘Thank you, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking him by the hand; ‘you have often seen them together. Well! We presently heard this unfortunate Tattycoram loud and angry, and before we could ask what was the matter, Pet came back in a tremble, saying she was frightened of her. Close after her came Tattycoram in a flaming rage. “I hate you all three,” says she, stamping her foot at us. “I am bursting with hate of the whole house.”’\n\n‘Upon which you--?’\n\n‘I?’ said Mr Meagles, with a plain good faith that might have commanded the belief of Mrs Gowan herself. ‘I said, count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’\n\nMr Meagles again stroked his face and shook his head, with an air of profound regret.\n\n‘She was so used to do it, Clennam, that even then, such a picture of passion as you never saw, she stopped short, looked me full in the face, and counted (as I made out) to eight. But she couldn’t control herself to go any further. There she broke down, poor thing, and gave the other seventeen to the four winds. Then it all burst out. She detested us, she was miserable with us, she couldn’t bear it, she wouldn’t bear it, she was determined to go away. She was younger than her young mistress, and would she remain to see her always held up as the only creature who was young and interesting, and to be cherished and loved? No. She wouldn’t, she wouldn’t, she wouldn’t! What did we think she, Tattycoram, might have been if she had been caressed and cared for in her childhood, like her young mistress? As good as her? Ah! Perhaps fifty times as good. When we pretended to be so fond of one another, we exulted over her; that was what we did; we exulted over her and shamed her. And all in the house did the same. They talked about their fathers and mothers, and brothers and sisters; they liked to drag them up before her face. There was Mrs Tickit, only yesterday, when her little grandchild was with her, had been amused by the child’s trying to call her (Tattycoram) by the wretched name we gave her; and had laughed at the name. Why, who didn’t; and who were we that we should have a right to name her like a dog or a cat? But she didn’t care. She would take no more benefits from us; she would fling us her name back again, and she would go. She would leave us that minute, nobody should stop her, and we should never hear of her again.’\n\nMr Meagles had recited all this with such a vivid remembrance of his original, that he was almost as flushed and hot by this time as he described her to have been.\n\n‘Ah, well!’ he said, wiping his face. ‘It was of no use trying reason then, with that vehement panting creature (Heaven knows what her mother’s story must have been); so I quietly told her that she should not go at that late hour of night, and I gave her my hand and took her to her room, and locked the house doors. But she was gone this morning.’\n\n‘And you know no more of her?’\n\n‘No more,’ returned Mr Meagles. ‘I have been hunting about all day. She must have gone very early and very silently. I have found no trace of her down about us.’\n\n‘Stay! You want,’ said Clennam, after a moment’s reflection, ‘to see her? I assume that?’\n\n‘Yes, assuredly; I want to give her another chance; Mother and Pet want to give her another chance; come! You yourself,’ said Mr Meagles, persuasively, as if the provocation to be angry were not his own at all, ‘want to give the poor passionate girl another chance, I know, Clennam.’\n\n‘It would be strange and hard indeed if I did not,’ said Clennam, ‘when you are all so forgiving. What I was going to ask you was, have you thought of that Miss Wade?’\n\n‘I have. I did not think of her until I had pervaded the whole of our neighbourhood, and I don’t know that I should have done so then but for finding Mother and Pet, when I went home, full of the idea that Tattycoram must have gone to her. Then, of course, I recalled what she said that day at dinner when you were first with us.’\n\n‘Have you any idea where Miss Wade is to be found?’\n\n‘To tell you the truth,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘it’s because I have an addled jumble of a notion on that subject that you found me waiting here. There is one of those odd impressions in my house, which do mysteriously get into houses sometimes, which nobody seems to have picked up in a distinct form from anybody, and yet which everybody seems to have got hold of loosely from somebody and let go again, that she lives, or was living, thereabouts.’ Mr Meagles handed him a slip of paper, on which was written the name of one of the dull by-streets in the Grosvenor region, near Park Lane.\n\n‘Here is no number,’ said Arthur looking over it.\n\n‘No number, my dear Clennam?’ returned his friend. ‘No anything! The very name of the street may have been floating in the air; for, as I tell you, none of my people can say where they got it from. However, it’s worth an inquiry; and as I would rather make it in company than alone, and as you too were a fellow-traveller of that immovable woman’s, I thought perhaps--’ Clennam finished the sentence for him by taking up his hat again, and saying he was ready.\n\nIt was now summer-time; a grey, hot, dusty evening. They rode to the top of Oxford Street, and there alighting, dived in among the great streets of melancholy stateliness, and the little streets that try to be as stately and succeed in being more melancholy, of which there is a labyrinth near Park Lane. Wildernesses of corner houses, with barbarous old porticoes and appurtenances; horrors that came into existence under some wrong-headed person in some wrong-headed time, still demanding the blind admiration of all ensuing generations and determined to do so until they tumbled down; frowned upon the twilight. Parasite little tenements, with the cramp in their whole frame, from the dwarf hall-door on the giant model of His Grace’s in the Square to the squeezed window of the boudoir commanding the dunghills in the Mews, made the evening doleful. Rickety dwellings of undoubted fashion, but of a capacity to hold nothing comfortably except a dismal smell, looked like the last result of the great mansions’ breeding in-and-in; and, where their little supplementary bows and balconies were supported on thin iron columns, seemed to be scrofulously resting upon crutches. Here and there a Hatchment, with the whole science of Heraldry in it, loomed down upon the street, like an Archbishop discoursing on Vanity. The shops, few in number, made no show; for popular opinion was as nothing to them. The pastrycook knew who was on his books, and in that knowledge could be calm, with a few glass cylinders of dowager peppermint-drops in his window, and half-a-dozen ancient specimens of currant-jelly. A few oranges formed the greengrocer’s whole concession to the vulgar mind. A single basket made of moss, once containing plovers’ eggs, held all that the poulterer had to say to the rabble. Everybody in those streets seemed (which is always the case at that hour and season) to be gone out to dinner, and nobody seemed to be giving the dinners they had gone to. On the doorsteps there were lounging footmen with bright parti-coloured plumage and white polls, like an extinct race of monstrous birds; and butlers, solitary men of recluse demeanour, each of whom appeared distrustful of all other butlers. The roll of carriages in the Park was done for the day; the street lamps were lighting; and wicked little grooms in the tightest fitting garments, with twists in their legs answering to the twists in their minds, hung about in pairs, chewing straws and exchanging fraudulent secrets. The spotted dogs who went out with the carriages, and who were so associated with splendid equipages that it looked like a condescension in those animals to come out without them, accompanied helpers to and fro on messages. Here and there was a retiring public-house which did not require to be supported on the shoulders of the people, and where gentlemen out of livery were not much wanted.\n\nThis last discovery was made by the two friends in pursuing their inquiries. Nothing was there, or anywhere, known of such a person as Miss Wade, in connection with the street they sought. It was one of the parasite streets; long, regular, narrow, dull and gloomy; like a brick and mortar funeral. They inquired at several little area gates, where a dejected youth stood spiking his chin on the summit of a precipitous little shoot of wooden steps, but could gain no information. They walked up the street on one side of the way, and down it on the other, what time two vociferous news-sellers, announcing an extraordinary event that had never happened and never would happen, pitched their hoarse voices into the secret chambers; but nothing came of it. At length they stood at the corner from which they had begun, and it had fallen quite dark, and they were no wiser.\n\nIt happened that in the street they had several times passed a dingy house, apparently empty, with bills in the windows, announcing that it was to let. The bills, as a variety in the funeral procession, almost amounted to a decoration. Perhaps because they kept the house separated in his mind, or perhaps because Mr Meagles and himself had twice agreed in passing, ‘It is clear she don’t live there,’ Clennam now proposed that they should go back and try that house before finally going away. Mr Meagles agreed, and back they went.\n\nThey knocked once, and they rang once, without any response. ‘Empty,’ said Mr Meagles, listening. ‘Once more,’ said Clennam, and knocked again. After that knock they heard a movement below, and somebody shuffling up towards the door.\n\nThe confined entrance was so dark that it was impossible to make out distinctly what kind of person opened the door; but it appeared to be an old woman. ‘Excuse our troubling you,’ said Clennam. ‘Pray can you tell us where Miss Wade lives?’ The voice in the darkness unexpectedly replied, ‘Lives here.’\n\n‘Is she at home?’\n\nNo answer coming, Mr Meagles asked again. ‘Pray is she at home?’\n\nAfter another delay, ‘I suppose she is,’ said the voice abruptly; ‘you had better come in, and I’ll ask.’\n\nThey were summarily shut into the close black house; and the figure rustling away, and speaking from a higher level, said, ‘Come up, if you please; you can’t tumble over anything.’ They groped their way up-stairs towards a faint light, which proved to be the light of the street shining through a window; and the figure left them shut in an airless room.\n\n‘This is odd, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, softly.\n\n‘Odd enough,’ assented Clennam in the same tone, ‘but we have succeeded; that’s the main point. Here’s a light coming!’\n\nThe light was a lamp, and the bearer was an old woman: very dirty, very wrinkled and dry. ‘She’s at home,’ she said (and the voice was the same that had spoken before); ‘she’ll come directly.’ Having set the lamp down on the table, the old woman dusted her hands on her apron, which she might have done for ever without cleaning them, looked at the visitors with a dim pair of eyes, and backed out.\n\nThe lady whom they had come to see, if she were the present occupant of the house, appeared to have taken up her quarters there as she might have established herself in an Eastern caravanserai. A small square of carpet in the middle of the room, a few articles of furniture that evidently did not belong to the room, and a disorder of trunks and travelling articles, formed the whole of her surroundings. Under some former regular inhabitant, the stifling little apartment had broken out into a pier-glass and a gilt table; but the gilding was as faded as last year’s flowers, and the glass was so clouded that it seemed to hold in magic preservation all the fogs and bad weather it had ever reflected. The visitors had had a minute or two to look about them, when the door opened and Miss Wade came in.\n\nShe was exactly the same as when they had parted, just as handsome, just as scornful, just as repressed. She manifested no surprise in seeing them, nor any other emotion. She requested them to be seated; and declining to take a seat herself, at once anticipated any introduction of their business.\n\n‘I apprehend,’ she said, ‘that I know the cause of your favouring me with this visit. We may come to it at once.’\n\n‘The cause then, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘is Tattycoram.’\n\n‘So I supposed.’\n\n‘Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘will you be so kind as to say whether you know anything of her?’\n\n‘Surely. I know she is here with me.’\n\n‘Then, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘allow me to make known to you that I shall be happy to have her back, and that my wife and daughter will be happy to have her back. She has been with us a long time: we don’t forget her claims upon us, and I hope we know how to make allowances.’\n\n‘You hope to know how to make allowances?’ she returned, in a level, measured voice. ‘For what?’\n\n‘I think my friend would say, Miss Wade,’ Arthur Clennam interposed, seeing Mr Meagles rather at a loss, ‘for the passionate sense that sometimes comes upon the poor girl, of being at a disadvantage. Which occasionally gets the better of better remembrances.’\n\nThe lady broke into a smile as she turned her eyes upon him. ‘Indeed?’ was all she answered.\n\nShe stood by the table so perfectly composed and still after this acknowledgment of his remark that Mr Meagles stared at her under a sort of fascination, and could not even look to Clennam to make another move. After waiting, awkwardly enough, for some moments, Arthur said:\n\n‘Perhaps it would be well if Mr Meagles could see her, Miss Wade?’\n\n‘That is easily done,’ said she. ‘Come here, child.’ She had opened a door while saying this, and now led the girl in by the hand. It was very curious to see them standing together: the girl with her disengaged fingers plaiting the bosom of her dress, half irresolutely, half passionately; Miss Wade with her composed face attentively regarding her, and suggesting to an observer, with extraordinary force, in her composure itself (as a veil will suggest the form it covers), the unquenchable passion of her own nature.\n\n‘See here,’ she said, in the same level way as before. ‘Here is your patron, your master. He is willing to take you back, my dear, if you are sensible of the favour and choose to go. You can be, again, a foil to his pretty daughter, a slave to her pleasant wilfulness, and a toy in the house showing the goodness of the family. You can have your droll name again, playfully pointing you out and setting you apart, as it is right that you should be pointed out and set apart. (Your birth, you know; you must not forget your birth.) You can again be shown to this gentleman’s daughter, Harriet, and kept before her, as a living reminder of her own superiority and her gracious condescension. You can recover all these advantages and many more of the same kind which I dare say start up in your memory while I speak, and which you lose in taking refuge with me--you can recover them all by telling these gentlemen how humbled and penitent you are, and by going back to them to be forgiven. What do you say, Harriet? Will you go?’\n\nThe girl who, under the influence of these words, had gradually risen in anger and heightened in colour, answered, raising her lustrous black eyes for the moment, and clenching her hand upon the folds it had been puckering up, ‘I’d die sooner!’\n\nMiss Wade, still standing at her side holding her hand, looked quietly round and said with a smile, ‘Gentlemen! What do you do upon that?’\n\nPoor Mr Meagles’s inexpressible consternation in hearing his motives and actions so perverted, had prevented him from interposing any word until now; but now he regained the power of speech.\n\n‘Tattycoram,’ said he, ‘for I’ll call you by that name still, my good girl, conscious that I meant nothing but kindness when I gave it to you, and conscious that you know it--’\n\n‘I don’t!’ said she, looking up again, and almost rending herself with the same busy hand.\n\n‘No, not now, perhaps,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘not with that lady’s eyes so intent upon you, Tattycoram,’ she glanced at them for a moment, ‘and that power over you, which we see she exercises; not now, perhaps, but at another time. Tattycoram, I’ll not ask that lady whether she believes what she has said, even in the anger and ill blood in which I and my friend here equally know she has spoken, though she subdues herself, with a determination that any one who has once seen her is not likely to forget. I’ll not ask you, with your remembrance of my house and all belonging to it, whether you believe it. I’ll only say that you have no profession to make to me or mine, and no forgiveness to entreat; and that all in the world that I ask you to do, is, to count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’\n\nShe looked at him for an instant, and then said frowningly, ‘I won’t. Miss Wade, take me away, please.’\n\nThe contention that raged within her had no softening in it now; it was wholly between passionate defiance and stubborn defiance. Her rich colour, her quick blood, her rapid breath, were all setting themselves against the opportunity of retracing their steps. ‘I won’t. I won’t. I won’t!’ she repeated in a low, thick voice. ‘I’d be torn to pieces first. I’d tear myself to pieces first!’\n\nMiss Wade, who had released her hold, laid her hand protectingly on the girl’s neck for a moment, and then said, looking round with her former smile and speaking exactly in her former tone, ‘Gentlemen! What do you do upon that?’\n\n‘Oh, Tattycoram, Tattycoram!’ cried Mr Meagles, adjuring her besides with an earnest hand. ‘Hear that lady’s voice, look at that lady’s face, consider what is in that lady’s heart, and think what a future lies before you. My child, whatever you may think, that lady’s influence over you--astonishing to us, and I should hardly go too far in saying terrible to us to see--is founded in passion fiercer than yours, and temper more violent than yours. What can you two be together? What can come of it?’\n\n‘I am alone here, gentlemen,’ observed Miss Wade, with no change of voice or manner. ‘Say anything you will.’\n\n‘Politeness must yield to this misguided girl, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘at her present pass; though I hope not altogether to dismiss it, even with the injury you do her so strongly before me. Excuse me for reminding you in her hearing--I must say it--that you were a mystery to all of us, and had nothing in common with any of us when she unfortunately fell in your way. I don’t know what you are, but you don’t hide, can’t hide, what a dark spirit you have within you. If it should happen that you are a woman, who, from whatever cause, has a perverted delight in making a sister-woman as wretched as she is (I am old enough to have heard of such), I warn her against you, and I warn you against yourself.’\n\n‘Gentlemen!’ said Miss Wade, calmly. ‘When you have concluded--Mr Clennam, perhaps you will induce your friend--’\n\n‘Not without another effort,’ said Mr Meagles, stoutly. ‘Tattycoram, my poor dear girl, count five-and-twenty.’\n\n‘Do not reject the hope, the certainty, this kind man offers you,’ said Clennam in a low emphatic voice. ‘Turn to the friends you have not forgotten. Think once more!’\n\n‘I won’t! Miss Wade,’ said the girl, with her bosom swelling high, and speaking with her hand held to her throat, ‘take me away!’\n\n‘Tattycoram,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘Once more yet! The only thing I ask of you in the world, my child! Count five-and-twenty!’\n\nShe put her hands tightly over her ears, confusedly tumbling down her bright black hair in the vehemence of the action, and turned her face resolutely to the wall. Miss Wade, who had watched her under this final appeal with that strange attentive smile, and that repressing hand upon her own bosom with which she had watched her in her struggle at Marseilles, then put her arm about her waist as if she took possession of her for evermore.\n\nAnd there was a visible triumph in her face when she turned it to dismiss the visitors.\n\n‘As it is the last time I shall have the honour,’ she said, ‘and as you have spoken of not knowing what I am, and also of the foundation of my influence here, you may now know that it is founded in a common cause. What your broken plaything is as to birth, I am. She has no name, I have no name. Her wrong is my wrong. I have nothing more to say to you.’\n\nThis was addressed to Mr Meagles, who sorrowfully went out. As Clennam followed, she said to him, with the same external composure and in the same level voice, but with a smile that is only seen on cruel faces: a very faint smile, lifting the nostril, scarcely touching the lips, and not breaking away gradually, but instantly dismissed when done with:\n\n‘I hope the wife of your dear friend Mr Gowan, may be happy in the contrast of her extraction to this girl’s and mine, and in the high good fortune that awaits her.’\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Nobody’s Disappearance\n\nNot resting satisfied with the endeavours he had made to recover his lost charge, Mr Meagles addressed a letter of remonstrance, breathing nothing but goodwill, not only to her, but to Miss Wade too. No answer coming to these epistles, or to another written to the stubborn girl by the hand of her late young mistress, which might have melted her if anything could (all three letters were returned weeks afterwards as having been refused at the house-door), he deputed Mrs Meagles to make the experiment of a personal interview. That worthy lady being unable to obtain one, and being steadfastly denied admission, Mr Meagles besought Arthur to essay once more what he could do. All that came of his compliance was, his discovery that the empty house was left in charge of the old woman, that Miss Wade was gone, that the waifs and strays of furniture were gone, and that the old woman would accept any number of half-crowns and thank the donor kindly, but had no information whatever to exchange for those coins, beyond constantly offering for perusal a memorandum relative to fixtures, which the house-agent’s young man had left in the hall.\n\nUnwilling, even under this discomfiture, to resign the ingrate and leave her hopeless, in case of her better dispositions obtaining the mastery over the darker side of her character, Mr Meagles, for six successive days, published a discreetly covert advertisement in the morning papers, to the effect that if a certain young person who had lately left home without reflection, would at any time apply to his address at Twickenham, everything would be as it had been before, and no reproaches need be apprehended. The unexpected consequences of this notification suggested to the dismayed Mr Meagles for the first time that some hundreds of young persons must be leaving their homes without reflection every day; for shoals of wrong young people came down to Twickenham, who, not finding themselves received with enthusiasm, generally demanded compensation by way of damages, in addition to coach-hire there and back. Nor were these the only uninvited clients whom the advertisement produced. The swarm of begging-letter writers, who would seem to be always watching eagerly for any hook, however small, to hang a letter upon, wrote to say that having seen the advertisement, they were induced to apply with confidence for various sums, ranging from ten shillings to fifty pounds: not because they knew anything about the young person, but because they felt that to part with those donations would greatly relieve the advertiser’s mind. Several projectors, likewise, availed themselves of the same opportunity to correspond with Mr Meagles; as, for example, to apprise him that their attention having been called to the advertisement by a friend, they begged to state that if they should ever hear anything of the young person, they would not fail to make it known to him immediately, and that in the meantime if he would oblige them with the funds necessary for bringing to perfection a certain entirely novel description of Pump, the happiest results would ensue to mankind.\n\nMr Meagles and his family, under these combined discouragements, had begun reluctantly to give up Tattycoram as irrecoverable, when the new and active firm of Doyce and Clennam, in their private capacities, went down on a Saturday to stay at the cottage until Monday. The senior partner took the coach, and the junior partner took his walking-stick.\n\nA tranquil summer sunset shone upon him as he approached the end of his walk, and passed through the meadows by the river side. He had that sense of peace, and of being lightened of a weight of care, which country quiet awakens in the breasts of dwellers in towns. Everything within his view was lovely and placid. The rich foliage of the trees, the luxuriant grass diversified with wild flowers, the little green islands in the river, the beds of rushes, the water-lilies floating on the surface of the stream, the distant voices in boats borne musically towards him on the ripple of the water and the evening air, were all expressive of rest. In the occasional leap of a fish, or dip of an oar, or twittering of a bird not yet at roost, or distant barking of a dog, or lowing of a cow--in all such sounds, there was the prevailing breath of rest, which seemed to encompass him in every scent that sweetened the fragrant air. The long lines of red and gold in the sky, and the glorious track of the descending sun, were all divinely calm. Upon the purple tree-tops far away, and on the green height near at hand up which the shades were slowly creeping, there was an equal hush. Between the real landscape and its shadow in the water, there was no division; both were so untroubled and clear, and, while so fraught with solemn mystery of life and death, so hopefully reassuring to the gazer’s soothed heart, because so tenderly and mercifully beautiful.\n\nClennam had stopped, not for the first time by many times, to look about him and suffer what he saw to sink into his soul, as the shadows, looked at, seemed to sink deeper and deeper into the water. He was slowly resuming his way, when he saw a figure in the path before him which he had, perhaps, already associated with the evening and its impressions.\n\nMinnie was there, alone. She had some roses in her hand, and seemed to have stood still on seeing him, waiting for him. Her face was towards him, and she appeared to have been coming from the opposite direction. There was a flutter in her manner, which Clennam had never seen in it before; and as he came near her, it entered his mind all at once that she was there of a set purpose to speak to him.\n\nShe gave him her hand, and said, ‘You wonder to see me here by myself? But the evening is so lovely, I have strolled further than I meant at first. I thought it likely I might meet you, and that made me more confident. You always come this way, do you not?’\n\nAs Clennam said that it was his favourite way, he felt her hand falter on his arm, and saw the roses shake.\n\n‘Will you let me give you one, Mr Clennam? I gathered them as I came out of the garden. Indeed, I almost gathered them for you, thinking it so likely I might meet you. Mr Doyce arrived more than an hour ago, and told us you were walking down.’\n\nHis own hand shook, as he accepted a rose or two from hers and thanked her. They were now by an avenue of trees. Whether they turned into it on his movement or on hers matters little. He never knew how that was.\n\n‘It is very grave here,’ said Clennam, ‘but very pleasant at this hour. Passing along this deep shade, and out at that arch of light at the other end, we come upon the ferry and the cottage by the best approach, I think.’\n\nIn her simple garden-hat and her light summer dress, with her rich brown hair naturally clustering about her, and her wonderful eyes raised to his for a moment with a look in which regard for him and trustfulness in him were strikingly blended with a kind of timid sorrow for him, she was so beautiful that it was well for his peace--or ill for his peace, he did not quite know which--that he had made that vigorous resolution he had so often thought about.\n\nShe broke a momentary silence by inquiring if he knew that papa had been thinking of another tour abroad? He said he had heard it mentioned. She broke another momentary silence by adding, with some hesitation, that papa had abandoned the idea.\n\nAt this, he thought directly, ‘they are to be married.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ she said, hesitating more timidly yet, and speaking so low that he bent his head to hear her. ‘I should very much like to give you my confidence, if you would not mind having the goodness to receive it. I should have very much liked to have given it to you long ago, because--I felt that you were becoming so much our friend.’\n\n‘How can I be otherwise than proud of it at any time! Pray give it to me. Pray trust me.’\n\n‘I could never have been afraid of trusting you,’ she returned, raising her eyes frankly to his face. ‘I think I would have done so some time ago, if I had known how. But I scarcely know how, even now.’\n\n‘Mr Gowan,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘has reason to be very happy. God bless his wife and him!’\n\nShe wept, as she tried to thank him. He reassured her, took her hand as it lay with the trembling roses in it on his arm, took the remaining roses from it, and put it to his lips. At that time, it seemed to him, he first finally resigned the dying hope that had flickered in nobody’s heart so much to its pain and trouble; and from that time he became in his own eyes, as to any similar hope or prospect, a very much older man who had done with that part of life.\n\nHe put the roses in his breast and they walked on for a little while, slowly and silently, under the umbrageous trees. Then he asked her, in a voice of cheerful kindness, was there anything else that she would say to him as her friend and her father’s friend, many years older than herself; was there any trust she would repose in him, any service she would ask of him, any little aid to her happiness that she could give him the lasting gratification of believing it was in his power to render?\n\nShe was going to answer, when she was so touched by some little hidden sorrow or sympathy--what could it have been?--that she said, bursting into tears again: ‘O Mr Clennam! Good, generous, Mr Clennam, pray tell me you do not blame me.’\n\n‘I blame you?’ said Clennam. ‘My dearest girl! I blame you? No!’\n\nAfter clasping both her hands upon his arm, and looking confidentially up into his face, with some hurried words to the effect that she thanked him from her heart (as she did, if it be the source of earnestness), she gradually composed herself, with now and then a word of encouragement from him, as they walked on slowly and almost silently under the darkening trees.\n\n‘And, now, Minnie Gowan,’ at length said Clennam, smiling; ‘will you ask me nothing?’\n\n‘Oh! I have very much to ask of you.’\n\n‘That’s well! I hope so; I am not disappointed.’\n\n‘You know how I am loved at home, and how I love home. You can hardly think it perhaps, dear Mr Clennam,’ she spoke with great agitation, ‘seeing me going from it of my own free will and choice, but I do so dearly love it!’\n\n‘I am sure of that,’ said Clennam. ‘Can you suppose I doubt it?’\n\n‘No, no. But it is strange, even to me, that loving it so much and being so much beloved in it, I can bear to cast it away. It seems so neglectful of it, so unthankful.’\n\n‘My dear girl,’ said Clennam, ‘it is in the natural progress and change of time. All homes are left so.’\n\n‘Yes, I know; but all homes are not left with such a blank in them as there will be in mine when I am gone. Not that there is any scarcity of far better and more endearing and more accomplished girls than I am; not that I am much, but that they have made so much of me!’\n\nPet’s affectionate heart was overcharged, and she sobbed while she pictured what would happen.\n\n‘I know what a change papa will feel at first, and I know that at first I cannot be to him anything like what I have been these many years. And it is then, Mr Clennam, then more than at any time, that I beg and entreat you to remember him, and sometimes to keep him company when you can spare a little while; and to tell him that you know I was fonder of him when I left him, than I ever was in all my life. For there is nobody--he told me so himself when he talked to me this very day--there is nobody he likes so well as you, or trusts so much.’\n\nA clue to what had passed between the father and daughter dropped like a heavy stone into the well of Clennam’s heart, and swelled the water to his eyes. He said, cheerily, but not quite so cheerily as he tried to say, that it should be done--that he gave her his faithful promise.\n\n‘If I do not speak of mama,’ said Pet, more moved by, and more pretty in, her innocent grief, than Clennam could trust himself even to consider--for which reason he counted the trees between them and the fading light as they slowly diminished in number--‘it is because mama will understand me better in this action, and will feel my loss in a different way, and will look forward in a different manner. But you know what a dear, devoted mother she is, and you will remember her too; will you not?’\n\nLet Minnie trust him, Clennam said, let Minnie trust him to do all she wished.\n\n‘And, dear Mr Clennam,’ said Minnie, ‘because papa and one whom I need not name, do not fully appreciate and understand one another yet, as they will by-and-by; and because it will be the duty, and the pride, and pleasure of my new life, to draw them to a better knowledge of one another, and to be a happiness to one another, and to be proud of one another, and to love one another, both loving me so dearly; oh, as you are a kind, true man! when I am first separated from home (I am going a long distance away), try to reconcile papa to him a little more, and use your great influence to keep him before papa’s mind free from prejudice and in his real form. Will you do this for me, as you are a noble-hearted friend?’\n\nPoor Pet! Self-deceived, mistaken child! When were such changes ever made in men’s natural relations to one another: when was such reconcilement of ingrain differences ever effected! It has been tried many times by other daughters, Minnie; it has never succeeded; nothing has ever come of it but failure.\n\nSo Clennam thought. So he did not say; it was too late. He bound himself to do all she asked, and she knew full well that he would do it.\n\nThey were now at the last tree in the avenue. She stopped, and withdrew her arm. Speaking to him with her eyes lifted up to his, and with the hand that had lately rested on his sleeve trembling by touching one of the roses in his breast as an additional appeal to him, she said:\n\n‘Dear Mr Clennam, in my happiness--for I am happy, though you have seen me crying--I cannot bear to leave any cloud between us. If you have anything to forgive me (not anything that I have wilfully done, but any trouble I may have caused you without meaning it, or having it in my power to help it), forgive me to-night out of your noble heart!’\n\nHe stooped to meet the guileless face that met his without shrinking. He kissed it, and answered, Heaven knew that he had nothing to forgive. As he stooped to meet the innocent face once again, she whispered, ‘Good-bye!’ and he repeated it. It was taking leave of all his old hopes--all nobody’s old restless doubts. They came out of the avenue next moment, arm-in-arm as they had entered it: and the trees seemed to close up behind them in the darkness, like their own perspective of the past.\n\nThe voices of Mr and Mrs Meagles and Doyce were audible directly, speaking near the garden gate. Hearing Pet’s name among them, Clennam called out, ‘She is here, with me.’ There was some little wondering and laughing until they came up; but as soon as they had all come together, it ceased, and Pet glided away.\n\nMr Meagles, Doyce, and Clennam, without speaking, walked up and down on the brink of the river, in the light of the rising moon, for a few minutes; and then Doyce lingered behind, and went into the house. Mr Meagles and Clennam walked up and down together for a few minutes more without speaking, until at length the former broke silence.\n\n‘Arthur,’ said he, using that familiar address for the first time in their communication, ‘do you remember my telling you, as we walked up and down one hot morning, looking over the harbour at Marseilles, that Pet’s baby sister who was dead seemed to Mother and me to have grown as she had grown, and changed as she had changed?’\n\n‘Very well.’\n\n‘You remember my saying that our thoughts had never been able to separate those twin sisters, and that, in our fancy, whatever Pet was, the other was?’\n\n‘Yes, very well.’\n\n‘Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, much subdued, ‘I carry that fancy further to-night. I feel to-night, my dear fellow, as if you had loved my dead child very tenderly, and had lost her when she was like what Pet is now.’\n\n‘Thank you!’ murmured Clennam, ‘thank you!’ And pressed his hand.\n\n‘Will you come in?’ said Mr Meagles, presently.\n\n‘In a little while.’\n\nMr Meagles fell away, and he was left alone. When he had walked on the river’s brink in the peaceful moonlight for some half an hour, he put his hand in his breast and tenderly took out the handful of roses. Perhaps he put them to his heart, perhaps he put them to his lips, but certainly he bent down on the shore and gently launched them on the flowing river. Pale and unreal in the moonlight, the river floated them away.\n\nThe lights were bright within doors when he entered, and the faces on which they shone, his own face not excepted, were soon quietly cheerful. They talked of many subjects (his partner never had had such a ready store to draw upon for the beguiling of the time), and so to bed, and to sleep. While the flowers, pale and unreal in the moonlight, floated away upon the river; and thus do greater things that once were in our breasts, and near our hearts, flow from us to the eternal seas.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming\n\nThe house in the city preserved its heavy dulness through all these transactions, and the invalid within it turned the same unvarying round of life. Morning, noon, and night, morning, noon, and night, each recurring with its accompanying monotony, always the same reluctant return of the same sequences of machinery, like a dragging piece of clockwork.\n\nThe wheeled chair had its associated remembrances and reveries, one may suppose, as every place that is made the station of a human being has. Pictures of demolished streets and altered houses, as they formerly were when the occupant of the chair was familiar with them, images of people as they too used to be, with little or no allowance made for the lapse of time since they were seen; of these, there must have been many in the long routine of gloomy days. To stop the clock of busy existence at the hour when we were personally sequestered from it, to suppose mankind stricken motionless when we were brought to a stand-still, to be unable to measure the changes beyond our view by any larger standard than the shrunken one of our own uniform and contracted existence, is the infirmity of many invalids, and the mental unhealthiness of almost all recluses.\n\nWhat scenes and actors the stern woman most reviewed, as she sat from season to season in her one dark room, none knew but herself. Mr Flintwinch, with his wry presence brought to bear upon her daily like some eccentric mechanical force, would perhaps have screwed it out of her, if there had been less resistance in her; but she was too strong for him. So far as Mistress Affery was concerned, to regard her liege-lord and her disabled mistress with a face of blank wonder, to go about the house after dark with her apron over her head, always to listen for the strange noises and sometimes to hear them, and never to emerge from her ghostly, dreamy, sleep-waking state, was occupation enough for her.\n\nThere was a fair stroke of business doing, as Mistress Affery made out, for her husband had abundant occupation in his little office, and saw more people than had been used to come there for some years. This might easily be, the house having been long deserted; but he did receive letters, and comers, and keep books, and correspond. Moreover, he went about to other counting-houses, and to wharves, and docks, and to the Custom House, and to Garraway’s Coffee House, and the Jerusalem Coffee House, and on ‘Change; so that he was much in and out. He began, too, sometimes of an evening, when Mrs Clennam expressed no particular wish for his society, to resort to a tavern in the neighbourhood to look at the shipping news and closing prices in the evening paper, and even to exchange small socialities with mercantile Sea Captains who frequented that establishment. At some period of every day, he and Mrs Clennam held a council on matters of business; and it appeared to Affery, who was always groping about, listening and watching, that the two clever ones were making money.\n\nThe state of mind into which Mr Flintwinch’s dazed lady had fallen, had now begun to be so expressed in all her looks and actions that she was held in very low account by the two clever ones, as a person, never of strong intellect, who was becoming foolish. Perhaps because her appearance was not of a commercial cast, or perhaps because it occurred to him that his having taken her to wife might expose his judgment to doubt in the minds of customers, Mr Flintwinch laid his commands upon her that she should hold her peace on the subject of her conjugal relations, and should no longer call him Jeremiah out of the domestic trio. Her frequent forgetfulness of this admonition intensified her startled manner, since Mr Flintwinch’s habit of avenging himself on her remissness by making springs after her on the staircase, and shaking her, occasioned her to be always nervously uncertain when she might be thus waylaid next.\n\nLittle Dorrit had finished a long day’s work in Mrs Clennam’s room, and was neatly gathering up her shreds and odds and ends before going home. Mr Pancks, whom Affery had just shown in, was addressing an inquiry to Mrs Clennam on the subject of her health, coupled with the remark that, ‘happening to find himself in that direction,’ he had looked in to inquire, on behalf of his proprietor, how she found herself. Mrs Clennam, with a deep contraction of her brows, was looking at him.\n\n‘Mr Casby knows,’ said she, ‘that I am not subject to changes. The change that I await here is the great change.’\n\n‘Indeed, ma’am?’ returned Mr Pancks, with a wandering eye towards the figure of the little seamstress on her knee picking threads and fraying of her work from the carpet. ‘You look nicely, ma’am.’\n\n‘I bear what I have to bear,’ she answered. ‘Do you what you have to do.’\n\n‘Thank you, ma’am,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘such is my endeavour.’\n\n‘You are often in this direction, are you not?’ asked Mrs Clennam.\n\n‘Why, yes, ma’am,’ said Pancks, ‘rather so lately; I have lately been round this way a good deal, owing to one thing and another.’\n\n‘Beg Mr Casby and his daughter not to trouble themselves, by deputy, about me. When they wish to see me, they know I am here to see them. They have no need to trouble themselves to send. You have no need to trouble yourself to come.’\n\n‘Not the least trouble, ma’am,’ said Mr Pancks. ‘You really are looking uncommonly nicely, ma’am.’\n\n‘Thank you. Good evening.’\n\nThe dismissal, and its accompanying finger pointed straight at the door, was so curt and direct that Mr Pancks did not see his way to prolong his visit. He stirred up his hair with his sprightliest expression, glanced at the little figure again, said ‘Good evening, ma ‘am; don’t come down, Mrs Affery, I know the road to the door,’ and steamed out. Mrs Clennam, her chin resting on her hand, followed him with attentive and darkly distrustful eyes; and Affery stood looking at her as if she were spell-bound.\n\nSlowly and thoughtfully, Mrs Clennam’s eyes turned from the door by which Pancks had gone out, to Little Dorrit, rising from the carpet. With her chin drooping more heavily on her hand, and her eyes vigilant and lowering, the sick woman sat looking at her until she attracted her attention. Little Dorrit coloured under such a gaze, and looked down. Mrs Clennam still sat intent.\n\n‘Little Dorrit,’ she said, when she at last broke silence, ‘what do you know of that man?’\n\n‘I don’t know anything of him, ma’am, except that I have seen him about, and that he has spoken to me.’\n\n‘What has he said to you?’\n\n‘I don’t understand what he has said, he is so strange. But nothing rough or disagreeable.’\n\n‘Why does he come here to see you?’\n\n‘I don’t know, ma’am,’ said Little Dorrit, with perfect frankness.\n\n‘You know that he does come here to see you?’\n\n‘I have fancied so,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘But why he should come here or anywhere for that, ma’am, I can’t think.’\n\nMrs Clennam cast her eyes towards the ground, and with her strong, set face, as intent upon a subject in her mind as it had lately been upon the form that seemed to pass out of her view, sat absorbed. Some minutes elapsed before she came out of this thoughtfulness, and resumed her hard composure.\n\nLittle Dorrit in the meanwhile had been waiting to go, but afraid to disturb her by moving. She now ventured to leave the spot where she had been standing since she had risen, and to pass gently round by the wheeled chair. She stopped at its side to say ‘Good night, ma’am.’\n\nMrs Clennam put out her hand, and laid it on her arm. Little Dorrit, confused under the touch, stood faltering. Perhaps some momentary recollection of the story of the Princess may have been in her mind.\n\n‘Tell me, Little Dorrit,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘have you many friends now?’\n\n‘Very few, ma’am. Besides you, only Miss Flora and--one more.’\n\n‘Meaning,’ said Mrs Clennam, with her unbent finger again pointing to the door, ‘that man?’\n\n‘Oh no, ma’am!’\n\n‘Some friend of his, perhaps?’\n\n‘No ma’am.’ Little Dorrit earnestly shook her head. ‘Oh no! No one at all like him, or belonging to him.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Mrs Clennam, almost smiling. ‘It is no affair of mine. I ask, because I take an interest in you; and because I believe I was your friend when you had no other who could serve you. Is that so?’\n\n‘Yes, ma’am; indeed it is. I have been here many a time when, but for you and the work you gave me, we should have wanted everything.’\n\n‘We,’ repeated Mrs Clennam, looking towards the watch, once her dead husband’s, which always lay upon her table. ‘Are there many of you?’\n\n‘Only father and I, now. I mean, only father and I to keep regularly out of what we get.’\n\n‘Have you undergone many privations? You and your father and who else there may be of you?’ asked Mrs Clennam, speaking deliberately, and meditatively turning the watch over and over.\n\n‘Sometimes it has been rather hard to live,’ said Little Dorrit, in her soft voice, and timid uncomplaining way; ‘but I think not harder--as to that--than many people find it.’\n\n‘That’s well said!’ Mrs Clennam quickly returned. ‘That’s the truth! You are a good, thoughtful girl. You are a grateful girl too, or I much mistake you.’\n\n‘It is only natural to be that. There is no merit in being that,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘I am indeed.’\n\nMrs Clennam, with a gentleness of which the dreaming Affery had never dreamed her to be capable, drew down the face of her little seamstress, and kissed her on the forehead.\n\n‘Now go, Little Dorrit,’ said she, ‘or you will be late, poor child!’\n\nIn all the dreams Mistress Affery had been piling up since she first became devoted to the pursuit, she had dreamed nothing more astonishing than this. Her head ached with the idea that she would find the other clever one kissing Little Dorrit next, and then the two clever ones embracing each other and dissolving into tears of tenderness for all mankind. The idea quite stunned her, as she attended the light footsteps down the stairs, that the house door might be safely shut.\n\nOn opening it to let Little Dorrit out, she found Mr Pancks, instead of having gone his way, as in any less wonderful place and among less wonderful phenomena he might have been reasonably expected to do, fluttering up and down the court outside the house. The moment he saw Little Dorrit, he passed her briskly, said with his finger to his nose (as Mrs Affery distinctly heard), ‘Pancks the gipsy, fortune-telling,’ and went away. ‘Lord save us, here’s a gipsy and a fortune-teller in it now!’ cried Mistress Affery. ‘What next!’\n\nShe stood at the open door, staggering herself with this enigma, on a rainy, thundery evening. The clouds were flying fast, and the wind was coming up in gusts, banging some neighbouring shutters that had broken loose, twirling the rusty chimney-cowls and weather-cocks, and rushing round and round a confined adjacent churchyard as if it had a mind to blow the dead citizens out of their graves. The low thunder, muttering in all quarters of the sky at once, seemed to threaten vengeance for this attempted desecration, and to mutter, ‘Let them rest! Let them rest!’\n\nMistress Affery, whose fear of thunder and lightning was only to be equalled by her dread of the haunted house with a premature and preternatural darkness in it, stood undecided whether to go in or not, until the question was settled for her by the door blowing upon her in a violent gust of wind and shutting her out. ‘What’s to be done now, what’s to be done now!’ cried Mistress Affery, wringing her hands in this last uneasy dream of all; ‘when she’s all alone by herself inside, and can no more come down to open it than the churchyard dead themselves!’\n\nIn this dilemma, Mistress Affery, with her apron as a hood to keep the rain off, ran crying up and down the solitary paved enclosure several times. Why she should then stoop down and look in at the keyhole of the door as if an eye would open it, it would be difficult to say; but it is none the less what most people would have done in the same situation, and it is what she did.\n\nFrom this posture she started up suddenly, with a half scream, feeling something on her shoulder. It was the touch of a hand; of a man’s hand.\n\nThe man was dressed like a traveller, in a foraging cap with fur about it, and a heap of cloak. He looked like a foreigner. He had a quantity of hair and moustache--jet black, except at the shaggy ends, where it had a tinge of red--and a high hook nose. He laughed at Mistress Affery’s start and cry; and as he laughed, his moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache.\n\n‘What’s the matter?’ he asked in plain English. ‘What are you frightened at?’\n\n‘At you,’ panted Affery.\n‘Me, madam?’\n\n‘And the dismal evening, and--and everything,’ said Affery. ‘And here! The wind has been and blown the door to, and I can’t get in.’\n\n‘Hah!’ said the gentleman, who took that very coolly. ‘Indeed! Do you know such a name as Clennam about here?’\n\n‘Lord bless us, I should think I did, I should think I did!’ cried Affery, exasperated into a new wringing of hands by the inquiry.\n\n‘Where about here?’\n\n‘Where!’ cried Affery, goaded into another inspection of the keyhole. ‘Where but here in this house? And she’s all alone in her room, and lost the use of her limbs and can’t stir to help herself or me, and t’other clever one’s out, and Lord forgive me!’ cried Affery, driven into a frantic dance by these accumulated considerations, ‘if I ain’t a-going headlong out of my mind!’\n\nTaking a warmer view of the matter now that it concerned himself, the gentleman stepped back to glance at the house, and his eye soon rested on the long narrow window of the little room near the hall-door.\n\n‘Where may the lady be who has lost the use of her limbs, madam?’ he inquired, with that peculiar smile which Mistress Affery could not choose but keep her eyes upon.\n\n‘Up there!’ said Affery. ‘Them two windows.’\n\n‘Hah! I am of a fair size, but could not have the honour of presenting myself in that room without a ladder. Now, madam, frankly--frankness is a part of my character--shall I open the door for you?’\n\n‘Yes, bless you, sir, for a dear creetur, and do it at once,’ cried Affery, ‘for she may be a-calling to me at this very present minute, or may be setting herself a fire and burning herself to death, or there’s no knowing what may be happening to her, and me a-going out of my mind at thinking of it!’\n\n‘Stay, my good madam!’ He restrained her impatience with a smooth white hand. ‘Business-hours, I apprehend, are over for the day?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, yes,’ cried Affery. ‘Long ago.’\n\n‘Let me make, then, a fair proposal. Fairness is a part of my character. I am just landed from the packet-boat, as you may see.’ He showed her that his cloak was very wet, and that his boots were saturated with water; she had previously observed that he was dishevelled and sallow, as if from a rough voyage, and so chilled that he could not keep his teeth from chattering. ‘I am just landed from the packet-boat, madam, and have been delayed by the weather: the infernal weather! In consequence of this, madam, some necessary business that I should otherwise have transacted here within the regular hours (necessary business because money-business), still remains to be done. Now, if you will fetch any authorised neighbouring somebody to do it in return for my opening the door, I’ll open the door. If this arrangement should be objectionable, I’ll--’ and with the same smile he made a significant feint of backing away.\n\nMistress Affery, heartily glad to effect the proposed compromise, gave in her willing adhesion to it. The gentleman at once requested her to do him the favour of holding his cloak, took a short run at the narrow window, made a leap at the sill, clung his way up the bricks, and in a moment had his hand at the sash, raising it. His eyes looked so very sinister, as he put his leg into the room and glanced round at Mistress Affery, that she thought with a sudden coldness, if he were to go straight up-stairs to murder the invalid, what could she do to prevent him?\n\nHappily he had no such purpose; for he reappeared, in a moment, at the house door. ‘Now, my dear madam,’ he said, as he took back his cloak and threw it on, ‘if you have the goodness to--what the Devil’s that!’\n\nThe strangest of sounds. Evidently close at hand from the peculiar shock it communicated to the air, yet subdued as if it were far off. A tremble, a rumble, and a fall of some light dry matter.\n\n‘What the Devil is it?’\n\n‘I don’t know what it is, but I’ve heard the like of it over and over again,’ said Affery, who had caught his arm.\n\nHe could hardly be a very brave man, even she thought in her dreamy start and fright, for his trembling lips had turned colourless. After listening a few moments, he made light of it.\n\n‘Bah! Nothing! Now, my dear madam, I think you spoke of some clever personage. Will you be so good as to confront me with that genius?’ He held the door in his hand, as though he were quite ready to shut her out again if she failed.\n\n‘Don’t you say anything about the door and me, then,’ whispered Affery.\n\n‘Not a word.’\n\n‘And don’t you stir from here, or speak if she calls, while I run round the corner.’\n\n‘Madam, I am a statue.’\n\nAffery had so vivid a fear of his going stealthily up-stairs the moment her back was turned, that after hurrying out of sight, she returned to the gateway to peep at him. Seeing him still on the threshold, more out of the house than in it, as if he had no love for darkness and no desire to probe its mysteries, she flew into the next street, and sent a message into the tavern to Mr Flintwinch, who came out directly. The two returning together--the lady in advance, and Mr Flintwinch coming up briskly behind, animated with the hope of shaking her before she could get housed--saw the gentleman standing in the same place in the dark, and heard the strong voice of Mrs Clennam calling from her room, ‘Who is it? What is it? Why does no one answer? Who _is_ that, down there?’\n\n# CHAPTER 30. The Word of a Gentleman\n\nWhen Mr and Mrs Flintwinch panted up to the door of the old house in the twilight, Jeremiah within a second of Affery, the stranger started back. ‘Death of my soul!’ he exclaimed. ‘Why, how did you get here?’\n\nMr Flintwinch, to whom these words were spoken, repaid the stranger’s wonder in full. He gazed at him with blank astonishment; he looked over his own shoulder, as expecting to see some one he had not been aware of standing behind him; he gazed at the stranger again, speechlessly, at a loss to know what he meant; he looked to his wife for explanation; receiving none, he pounced upon her, and shook her with such heartiness that he shook her cap off her head, saying between his teeth, with grim raillery, as he did it, ‘Affery, my woman, you must have a dose, my woman! This is some of your tricks! You have been dreaming again, mistress. What’s it about? Who is it? What does it mean! Speak out or be choked! It’s the only choice I’ll give you.’\n\nSupposing Mistress Affery to have any power of election at the moment, her choice was decidedly to be choked; for she answered not a syllable to this adjuration, but, with her bare head wagging violently backwards and forwards, resigned herself to her punishment. The stranger, however, picking up her cap with an air of gallantry, interposed.\n\n‘Permit me,’ said he, laying his hand on the shoulder of Jeremiah, who stopped and released his victim. ‘Thank you. Excuse me. Husband and wife I know, from this playfulness. Haha! Always agreeable to see that relation playfully maintained. Listen! May I suggest that somebody up-stairs, in the dark, is becoming energetically curious to know what is going on here?’\n\nThis reference to Mrs Clennam’s voice reminded Mr Flintwinch to step into the hall and call up the staircase. ‘It’s all right, I am here, Affery is coming with your light.’ Then he said to the latter flustered woman, who was putting her cap on, ‘Get out with you, and get up-stairs!’ and then turned to the stranger and said to him, ‘Now, sir, what might you please to want?’\n\n‘I am afraid,’ said the stranger, ‘I must be so troublesome as to propose a candle.’\n\n‘True,’ assented Jeremiah. ‘I was going to do so. Please to stand where you are while I get one.’\n\nThe visitor was standing in the doorway, but turned a little into the gloom of the house as Mr Flintwinch turned, and pursued him with his eyes into the little room, where he groped about for a phosphorus box. When he found it, it was damp, or otherwise out of order; and match after match that he struck into it lighted sufficiently to throw a dull glare about his groping face, and to sprinkle his hands with pale little spots of fire, but not sufficiently to light the candle. The stranger, taking advantage of this fitful illumination of his visage, looked intently and wonderingly at him. Jeremiah, when he at last lighted the candle, knew he had been doing this, by seeing the last shade of a lowering watchfulness clear away from his face, as it broke into the doubtful smile that was a large ingredient in its expression.\n\n‘Be so good,’ said Jeremiah, closing the house door, and taking a pretty sharp survey of the smiling visitor in his turn, ‘as to step into my counting-house.--It’s all right, I tell you!’ petulantly breaking off to answer the voice up-stairs, still unsatisfied, though Affery was there, speaking in persuasive tones. ‘Don’t I tell you it’s all right? Preserve the woman, has she no reason at all in her!’\n\n‘Timorous,’ remarked the stranger.\n\n‘Timorous?’ said Mr Flintwinch, turning his head to retort, as he went before with the candle. ‘More courageous than ninety men in a hundred, sir, let me tell you.’\n\n‘Though an invalid?’\n\n‘Many years an invalid. Mrs Clennam. The only one of that name left in the House now. My partner.’\n\nSaying something apologetically as he crossed the hall, to the effect that at that time of night they were not in the habit of receiving any one, and were always shut up, Mr Flintwinch led the way into his own office, which presented a sufficiently business-like appearance. Here he put the light on his desk, and said to the stranger, with his wryest twist upon him, ‘Your commands.’\n\n‘My name is Blandois.’\n\n‘Blandois. I don’t know it,’ said Jeremiah.\n\n‘I thought it possible,’ resumed the other, ‘that you might have been advised from Paris--’\n\n‘We have had no advice from Paris respecting anybody of the name of Blandois,’ said Jeremiah.\n\n‘No?’\n‘No.’\n\nJeremiah stood in his favourite attitude. The smiling Mr Blandois, opening his cloak to get his hand to a breast-pocket, paused to say, with a laugh in his glittering eyes, which it occurred to Mr Flintwinch were too near together:\n\n‘You are so like a friend of mine! Not so identically the same as I supposed when I really did for the moment take you to be the same in the dusk--for which I ought to apologise; permit me to do so; a readiness to confess my errors is, I hope, a part of the frankness of my character--still, however, uncommonly like.’\n\n‘Indeed?’ said Jeremiah, perversely. ‘But I have not received any letter of advice from anywhere respecting anybody of the name of Blandois.’\n\n‘Just so,’ said the stranger.\n\n‘_Just_ so,’ said Jeremiah.\n\nMr Blandois, not at all put out by this omission on the part of the correspondents of the house of Clennam and Co., took his pocket-book from his breast-pocket, selected a letter from that receptacle, and handed it to Mr Flintwinch. ‘No doubt you are well acquainted with the writing. Perhaps the letter speaks for itself, and requires no advice. You are a far more competent judge of such affairs than I am. It is my misfortune to be, not so much a man of business, as what the world calls (arbitrarily) a gentleman.’\n\nMr Flintwinch took the letter, and read, under date of Paris, ‘We have to present to you, on behalf of a highly esteemed correspondent of our Firm, M. Blandois, of this city,’ &c. &c. ‘Such facilities as he may require and such attentions as may lie in your power,’ &c. &c. ‘Also have to add that if you will honour M. Blandois’ drafts at sight to the extent of, say Fifty Pounds sterling (50_l_.),’ &c. &c.\n\n‘Very good, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘Take a chair. To the extent of anything that our House can do--we are in a retired, old-fashioned, steady way of business, sir--we shall be happy to render you our best assistance. I observe, from the date of this, that we could not yet be advised of it. Probably you came over with the delayed mail that brings the advice.’\n\n‘That I came over with the delayed mail, sir,’ returned Mr Blandois, passing his white hand down his high-hooked nose, ‘I know to the cost of my head and stomach: the detestable and intolerable weather having racked them both. You see me in the plight in which I came out of the packet within this half-hour. I ought to have been here hours ago, and then I should not have to apologise--permit me to apologise--for presenting myself so unreasonably, and frightening--no, by-the-bye, you said not frightening; permit me to apologise again--the esteemed lady, Mrs Clennam, in her invalid chamber above stairs.’\n\nSwagger and an air of authorised condescension do so much, that Mr Flintwinch had already begun to think this a highly gentlemanly personage. Not the less unyielding with him on that account, he scraped his chin and said, what could he have the honour of doing for Mr Blandois to-night, out of business hours?\n\n‘Faith!’ returned that gentleman, shrugging his cloaked shoulders, ‘I must change, and eat and drink, and be lodged somewhere. Have the kindness to advise me, a total stranger, where, and money is a matter of perfect indifference until to-morrow. The nearer the place, the better. Next door, if that’s all.’\n\nMr Flintwinch was slowly beginning, ‘For a gentleman of your habits, there is not in this immediate neighbourhood any hotel--’ when Mr Blandois took him up.\n\n‘So much for my habits! my dear sir,’ snapping his fingers. ‘A citizen of the world has no habits. That I am, in my poor way, a gentleman, by Heaven! I will not deny, but I have no unaccommodating prejudiced habits. A clean room, a hot dish for dinner, and a bottle of not absolutely poisonous wine, are all I want tonight. But I want that much without the trouble of going one unnecessary inch to get it.’\n\n‘There is,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with more than his usual deliberation, as he met, for a moment, Mr Blandois’ shining eyes, which were restless; ‘there is a coffee-house and tavern close here, which, so far, I can recommend; but there’s no style about it.’\n\n‘I dispense with style!’ said Mr Blandois, waving his hand. ‘Do me the honour to show me the house, and introduce me there (if I am not too troublesome), and I shall be infinitely obliged.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, upon this, looked up his hat, and lighted Mr Blandois across the hall again. As he put the candle on a bracket, where the dark old panelling almost served as an extinguisher for it, he bethought himself of going up to tell the invalid that he would not be absent five minutes.\n\n‘Oblige me,’ said the visitor, on his saying so, ‘by presenting my card of visit. Do me the favour to add that I shall be happy to wait on Mrs Clennam, to offer my personal compliments, and to apologise for having occasioned any agitation in this tranquil corner, if it should suit her convenience to endure the presence of a stranger for a few minutes, after he shall have changed his wet clothes and fortified himself with something to eat and drink.’\n\nJeremiah made all despatch, and said, on his return, ‘She’ll be glad to see you, sir; but, being conscious that her sick room has no attractions, wishes me to say that she won’t hold you to your offer, in case you should think better of it.’\n\n‘To think better of it,’ returned the gallant Blandois, ‘would be to slight a lady; to slight a lady would be to be deficient in chivalry towards the sex; and chivalry towards the sex is a part of my character!’ Thus expressing himself, he threw the draggled skirt of his cloak over his shoulder, and accompanied Mr Flintwinch to the tavern; taking up on the road a porter who was waiting with his portmanteau on the outer side of the gateway.\n\nThe house was kept in a homely manner, and the condescension of Mr Blandois was infinite. It seemed to fill to inconvenience the little bar in which the widow landlady and her two daughters received him; it was much too big for the narrow wainscoted room with a bagatelle-board in it, that was first proposed for his reception; it perfectly swamped the little private holiday sitting-room of the family, which was finally given up to him. Here, in dry clothes and scented linen, with sleeked hair, a great ring on each forefinger and a massive show of watch-chain, Mr Blandois waiting for his dinner, lolling on a window-seat with his knees drawn up, looked (for all the difference in the setting of the jewel) fearfully and wonderfully like a certain Monsieur Rigaud who had once so waited for his breakfast, lying on the stone ledge of the iron grating of a cell in a villainous dungeon at Marseilles.\n\nHis greed at dinner, too, was closely in keeping with the greed of Monsieur Rigaud at breakfast. His avaricious manner of collecting all the eatables about him, and devouring some with his eyes while devouring others with his jaws, was the same manner. His utter disregard of other people, as shown in his way of tossing the little womanly toys of furniture about, flinging favourite cushions under his boots for a softer rest, and crushing delicate coverings with his big body and his great black head, had the same brute selfishness at the bottom of it. The softly moving hands that were so busy among the dishes had the old wicked facility of the hands that had clung to the bars. And when he could eat no more, and sat sucking his delicate fingers one by one and wiping them on a cloth, there wanted nothing but the substitution of vine-leaves to finish the picture.\n\nOn this man, with his moustache going up and his nose coming down in that most evil of smiles, and with his surface eyes looking as if they belonged to his dyed hair, and had had their natural power of reflecting light stopped by some similar process, Nature, always true, and never working in vain, had set the mark, Beware! It was not her fault, if the warning were fruitless. She is never to blame in any such instance.\n\nMr Blandois, having finished his repast and cleaned his fingers, took a cigar from his pocket, and, lying on the window-seat again, smoked it out at his leisure, occasionally apostrophising the smoke as it parted from his thin lips in a thin stream:\n\n‘Blandois, you shall turn the tables on society, my little child. Haha! Holy blue, you have begun well, Blandois! At a pinch, an excellent master in English or French; a man for the bosom of families! You have a quick perception, you have humour, you have ease, you have insinuating manners, you have a good appearance; in effect, you are a gentleman! A gentleman you shall live, my small boy, and a gentleman you shall die. You shall win, however the game goes. They shall all confess your merit, Blandois. You shall subdue the society which has grievously wronged you, to your own high spirit. Death of my soul! You are high spirited by right and by nature, my Blandois!’\n\nTo such soothing murmurs did this gentleman smoke out his cigar and drink out his bottle of wine. Both being finished, he shook himself into a sitting attitude; and with the concluding serious apostrophe, ‘Hold, then! Blandois, you ingenious one, have all your wits about you!’ arose and went back to the house of Clennam and Co.\n\nHe was received at the door by Mistress Affery, who, under instructions from her lord, had lighted up two candles in the hall and a third on the staircase, and who conducted him to Mrs Clennam’s room. Tea was prepared there, and such little company arrangements had been made as usually attended the reception of expected visitors. They were slight on the greatest occasion, never extending beyond the production of the China tea-service, and the covering of the bed with a sober and sad drapery. For the rest, there was the bier-like sofa with the block upon it, and the figure in the widow’s dress, as if attired for execution; the fire topped by the mound of damped ashes; the grate with its second little mound of ashes; the kettle and the smell of black dye; all as they had been for fifteen years.\n\nMr Flintwinch presented the gentleman commended to the consideration of Clennam and Co. Mrs Clennam, who had the letter lying before her, bent her head and requested him to sit. They looked very closely at one another. That was but natural curiosity.\n\n‘I thank you, sir, for thinking of a disabled woman like me. Few who come here on business have any remembrance to bestow on one so removed from observation. It would be idle to expect that they should have. Out of sight, out of mind. While I am grateful for the exception, I don’t complain of the rule.’\n\nMr Blandois, in his most gentlemanly manner, was afraid he had disturbed her by unhappily presenting himself at such an unconscionable time. For which he had already offered his best apologies to Mr--he begged pardon--but by name had not the distinguished honour--\n\n‘Mr Flintwinch has been connected with the House many years.’\n\nMr Blandois was Mr Flintwinch’s most obedient humble servant. He entreated Mr Flintwinch to receive the assurance of his profoundest consideration.\n\n‘My husband being dead,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘and my son preferring another pursuit, our old House has no other representative in these days than Mr Flintwinch.’\n\n‘What do you call yourself?’ was the surly demand of that gentleman. ‘You have the head of two men.’\n\n‘My sex disqualifies me,’ she proceeded with merely a slight turn of her eyes in Jeremiah’s direction, ‘from taking a responsible part in the business, even if I had the ability; and therefore Mr Flintwinch combines my interest with his own, and conducts it. It is not what it used to be; but some of our old friends (principally the writers of this letter) have the kindness not to forget us, and we retain the power of doing what they entrust to us as efficiently as we ever did. This however is not interesting to you. You are English, sir?’\n\n‘Faith, madam, no; I am neither born nor bred in England. In effect, I am of no country,’ said Mr Blandois, stretching out his leg and smiting it: ‘I descend from half-a-dozen countries.’\n\n‘You have been much about the world?’\n\n‘It is true. By Heaven, madam, I have been here and there and everywhere!’\n\n‘You have no ties, probably. Are not married?’\n\n‘Madam,’ said Mr Blandois, with an ugly fall of his eyebrows, ‘I adore your sex, but I am not married--never was.’\n\nMistress Affery, who stood at the table near him, pouring out the tea, happened in her dreamy state to look at him as he said these words, and to fancy that she caught an expression in his eyes which attracted her own eyes so that she could not get them away. The effect of this fancy was to keep her staring at him with the tea-pot in her hand, not only to her own great uneasiness, but manifestly to his, too; and, through them both, to Mrs Clennam’s and Mr Flintwinch’s. Thus a few ghostly moments supervened, when they were all confusedly staring without knowing why.\n\n‘Affery,’ her mistress was the first to say, ‘what is the matter with you?’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Mistress Affery, with her disengaged left hand extended towards the visitor. ‘It ain’t me. It’s him!’\n\n‘What does this good woman mean?’ cried Mr Blandois, turning white, hot, and slowly rising with a look of such deadly wrath that it contrasted surprisingly with the slight force of his words. ‘How is it possible to understand this good creature?’\n\n‘It’s _not_ possible,’ said Mr Flintwinch, screwing himself rapidly in that direction. ‘She don’t know what she means. She’s an idiot, a wanderer in her mind. She shall have a dose, she shall have such a dose! Get along with you, my woman,’ he added in her ear, ‘get along with you, while you know you’re Affery, and before you’re shaken to yeast.’\n\nMistress Affery, sensible of the danger in which her identity stood, relinquished the tea-pot as her husband seized it, put her apron over her head, and in a twinkling vanished. The visitor gradually broke into a smile, and sat down again.\n\n‘You’ll excuse her, Mr Blandois,’ said Jeremiah, pouring out the tea himself, ‘she’s failing and breaking up; that’s what she’s about. Do you take sugar, sir?’\n\n‘Thank you, no tea for me.--Pardon my observing it, but that’s a very remarkable watch!’\n\nThe tea-table was drawn up near the sofa, with a small interval between it and Mrs Clennam’s own particular table. Mr Blandois in his gallantry had risen to hand that lady her tea (her dish of toast was already there), and it was in placing the cup conveniently within her reach that the watch, lying before her as it always did, attracted his attention. Mrs Clennam looked suddenly up at him.\n\n‘May I be permitted? Thank you. A fine old-fashioned watch,’ he said, taking it in his hand. ‘Heavy for use, but massive and genuine. I have a partiality for everything genuine. Such as I am, I am genuine myself. Hah! A gentleman’s watch with two cases in the old fashion. May I remove it from the outer case? Thank you. Aye? An old silk watch-lining, worked with beads! I have often seen these among old Dutch people and Belgians. Quaint things!’\n\n‘They are old-fashioned, too,’ said Mrs Clennam.\n\n‘Very. But this is not so old as the watch, I think?’\n\n‘I think not.’\n\n‘Extraordinary how they used to complicate these cyphers!’ remarked Mr Blandois, glancing up with his own smile again. ‘Now is this D. N. F.? It might be almost anything.’\n\n‘Those are the letters.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, who had been observantly pausing all this time with a cup of tea in his hand, and his mouth open ready to swallow the contents, began to do so: always entirely filling his mouth before he emptied it at a gulp; and always deliberating again before he refilled it.\n\n‘D. N. F. was some tender, lovely, fascinating fair-creature, I make no doubt,’ observed Mr Blandois, as he snapped on the case again. ‘I adore her memory on the assumption. Unfortunately for my peace of mind, I adore but too readily. It may be a vice, it may be a virtue, but adoration of female beauty and merit constitutes three parts of my character, madam.’\n\nMr Flintwinch had by this time poured himself out another cup of tea, which he was swallowing in gulps as before, with his eyes directed to the invalid.\n\n‘You may be heart-free here, sir,’ she returned to Mr Blandois. ‘Those letters are not intended, I believe, for the initials of any name.’\n\n‘Of a motto, perhaps,’ said Mr Blandois, casually.\n\n‘Of a sentence. They have always stood, I believe, for Do Not Forget!’\n\n‘And naturally,’ said Mr Blandois, replacing the watch and stepping backward to his former chair, ‘you do _not_ forget.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, finishing his tea, not only took a longer gulp than he had taken yet, but made his succeeding pause under new circumstances: that is to say, with his head thrown back and his cup held still at his lips, while his eyes were still directed at the invalid. She had that force of face, and that concentrated air of collecting her firmness or obstinacy, which represented in her case what would have been gesture and action in another, as she replied with her deliberate strength of speech:\n\n‘No, sir, I do not forget. To lead a life as monotonous as mine has been during many years, is not the way to forget. To lead a life of self-correction is not the way to forget. To be sensible of having (as we all have, every one of us, all the children of Adam!) offences to expiate and peace to make, does not justify the desire to forget. Therefore I have long dismissed it, and I neither forget nor wish to forget.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, who had latterly been shaking the sediment at the bottom of his tea-cup, round and round, here gulped it down, and putting the cup in the tea-tray, as done with, turned his eyes upon Mr Blandois as if to ask him what he thought of that?\n\n‘All expressed, madam,’ said Mr Blandois, with his smoothest bow and his white hand on his breast, ‘by the word “naturally,” which I am proud to have had sufficient apprehension and appreciation (but without appreciation I could not be Blandois) to employ.’\n\n‘Pardon me, sir,’ she returned, ‘if I doubt the likelihood of a gentleman of pleasure, and change, and politeness, accustomed to court and to be courted--’\n\n‘Oh madam! By Heaven!’\n\n‘--If I doubt the likelihood of such a character quite comprehending what belongs to mine in my circumstances. Not to obtrude doctrine upon you,’ she looked at the rigid pile of hard pale books before her, ‘(for you go your own way, and the consequences are on your own head), I will say this much: that I shape my course by pilots, strictly by proved and tried pilots, under whom I cannot be shipwrecked--can not be--and that if I were unmindful of the admonition conveyed in those three letters, I should not be half as chastened as I am.’\n\nIt was curious how she seized the occasion to argue with some invisible opponent. Perhaps with her own better sense, always turning upon herself and her own deception.\n\n‘If I forgot my ignorances in my life of health and freedom, I might complain of the life to which I am now condemned. I never do; I never have done. If I forgot that this scene, the Earth, is expressly meant to be a scene of gloom, and hardship, and dark trial, for the creatures who are made out of its dust, I might have some tenderness for its vanities. But I have no such tenderness. If I did not know that we are, every one, the subject (most justly the subject) of a wrath that must be satisfied, and against which mere actions are nothing, I might repine at the difference between me, imprisoned here, and the people who pass that gateway yonder. But I take it as a grace and favour to be elected to make the satisfaction I am making here, to know what I know for certain here, and to work out what I have worked out here. My affliction might otherwise have had no meaning to me. Hence I would forget, and I do forget, nothing. Hence I am contented, and say it is better with me than with millions.’\n\nAs she spoke these words, she put her hand upon the watch, and restored it to the precise spot on her little table which it always occupied. With her touch lingering upon it, she sat for some moments afterwards, looking at it steadily and half-defiantly.\n\nMr Blandois, during this exposition, had been strictly attentive, keeping his eyes fastened on the lady, and thoughtfully stroking his moustache with his two hands. Mr Flintwinch had been a little fidgety, and now struck in.\n\n‘There, there, there!’ said he. ‘That is quite understood, Mrs Clennam, and you have spoken piously and well. Mr Blandois, I suspect, is not of a pious cast.’\n\n‘On the contrary, sir!’ that gentleman protested, snapping his fingers. ‘Your pardon! It’s a part of my character. I am sensitive, ardent, conscientious, and imaginative. A sensitive, ardent, conscientious, and imaginative man, Mr Flintwinch, must be that, or nothing!’\n\nThere was an inkling of suspicion in Mr Flintwinch’s face that he might be nothing, as he swaggered out of his chair (it was characteristic of this man, as it is of all men similarly marked, that whatever he did, he overdid, though it were sometimes by only a hairsbreadth), and approached to take his leave of Mrs Clennam.\n\n‘With what will appear to you the egotism of a sick old woman, sir,’ she then said, ‘though really through your accidental allusion, I have been led away into the subject of myself and my infirmities. Being so considerate as to visit me, I hope you will be likewise so considerate as to overlook that. Don’t compliment me, if you please.’ For he was evidently going to do it. ‘Mr Flintwinch will be happy to render you any service, and I hope your stay in this city may prove agreeable.’\n\nMr Blandois thanked her, and kissed his hand several times. ‘This is an old room,’ he remarked, with a sudden sprightliness of manner, looking round when he got near the door, ‘I have been so interested that I have not observed it. But it’s a genuine old room.’\n\n‘It is a genuine old house,’ said Mrs Clennam, with her frozen smile. ‘A place of no pretensions, but a piece of antiquity.’\n\n‘Faith!’ cried the visitor. ‘If Mr Flintwinch would do me the favour to take me through the rooms on my way out, he could hardly oblige me more. An old house is a weakness with me. I have many weaknesses, but none greater. I love and study the picturesque in all its varieties. I have been called picturesque myself. It is no merit to be picturesque--I have greater merits, perhaps--but I may be, by an accident. Sympathy, sympathy!’\n\n‘I tell you beforehand, Mr Blandois, that you’ll find it very dingy and very bare,’ said Jeremiah, taking up the candle. ‘It’s not worth your looking at.’But Mr Blandois, smiting him in a friendly manner on the back, only laughed; so the said Blandois kissed his hand again to Mrs Clennam, and they went out of the room together.\n\n‘You don’t care to go up-stairs?’ said Jeremiah, on the landing.\n\n‘On the contrary, Mr Flintwinch; if not tiresome to you, I shall be ravished!’\n\nMr Flintwinch, therefore, wormed himself up the staircase, and Mr Blandois followed close. They ascended to the great garret bed-room which Arthur had occupied on the night of his return. ‘There, Mr Blandois!’ said Jeremiah, showing it, ‘I hope you may think that worth coming so high to see. I confess I don’t.’\n\nMr Blandois being enraptured, they walked through other garrets and passages, and came down the staircase again. By this time Mr Flintwinch had remarked that he never found the visitor looking at any room, after throwing one quick glance around, but always found the visitor looking at him, Mr Flintwinch. With this discovery in his thoughts, he turned about on the staircase for another experiment. He met his eyes directly; and on the instant of their fixing one another, the visitor, with that ugly play of nose and moustache, laughed (as he had done at every similar moment since they left Mrs Clennam’s chamber) a diabolically silent laugh.\n\nAs a much shorter man than the visitor, Mr Flintwinch was at the physical disadvantage of being thus disagreeably leered at from a height; and as he went first down the staircase, and was usually a step or two lower than the other, this disadvantage was at the time increased. He postponed looking at Mr Blandois again until this accidental inequality was removed by their having entered the late Mr Clennam’s room. But, then twisting himself suddenly round upon him, he found his look unchanged.\n\n‘A most admirable old house,’ smiled Mr Blandois. ‘So mysterious. Do you never hear any haunted noises here?’\n\n‘Noises,’ returned Mr Flintwinch. ‘No.’\n\n‘Nor see any devils?’\n\n‘Not,’ said Mr Flintwinch, grimly screwing himself at his questioner, ‘not any that introduce themselves under that name and in that capacity.’\n\n‘Haha! A portrait here, I see.’\n\n(Still looking at Mr Flintwinch, as if he were the portrait.)\n\n‘It’s a portrait, sir, as you observe.’\n\n‘May I ask the subject, Mr Flintwinch?’\n\n‘Mr Clennam, deceased. Her husband.’\n\n‘Former owner of the remarkable watch, perhaps?’ said the visitor.\n\nMr Flintwinch, who had cast his eyes towards the portrait, twisted himself about again, and again found himself the subject of the same look and smile. ‘Yes, Mr Blandois,’ he replied tartly. ‘It was his, and his uncle’s before him, and Lord knows who before him; and that’s all I can tell you of its pedigree.’\n\n‘That’s a strongly marked character, Mr Flintwinch, our friend up-stairs.’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ said Jeremiah, twisting himself at the visitor again, as he did during the whole of this dialogue, like some screw-machine that fell short of its grip; for the other never changed, and he always felt obliged to retreat a little. ‘She is a remarkable woman. Great fortitude--great strength of mind.’\n\n‘They must have been very happy,’ said Blandois.\n\n‘Who?’ demanded Mr Flintwinch, with another screw at him.\n\nMr Blandois shook his right forefinger towards the sick room, and his left forefinger towards the portrait, and then, putting his arms akimbo and striding his legs wide apart, stood smiling down at Mr Flintwinch with the advancing nose and the retreating moustache.\n\n‘As happy as most other married people, I suppose,’ returned Mr Flintwinch. ‘I can’t say. I don’t know. There are secrets in all families.’\n\n‘Secrets!’ cried Mr Blandois, quickly. ‘Say it again, my son.’\n\n‘I say,’ replied Mr Flintwinch, upon whom he had swelled himself so suddenly that Mr Flintwinch found his face almost brushed by the dilated chest. ‘I say there are secrets in all families.’\n\n‘So there are,’ cried the other, clapping him on both shoulders, and rolling him backwards and forwards. ‘Haha! you are right. So there are! Secrets! Holy Blue! There are the devil’s own secrets in some families, Mr Flintwinch!’ With that, after clapping Mr Flintwinch on both shoulders several times, as if in a friendly and humorous way he were rallying him on a joke he had made, he threw up his arms, threw back his head, hooked his hands together behind it, and burst into a roar of laughter. It was in vain for Mr Flintwinch to try another screw at him. He had his laugh out.\n\n‘But, favour me with the candle a moment,’ he said, when he had done. ‘Let us have a look at the husband of the remarkable lady. Hah!’ holding up the light at arm’s length. ‘A decided expression of face here too, though not of the same character. Looks as if he were saying, what is it--Do Not Forget--does he not, Mr Flintwinch? By Heaven, sir, he does!’\n\nAs he returned the candle, he looked at him once more; and then, leisurely strolling out with him into the hall, declared it to be a charming old house indeed, and one which had so greatly pleased him that he would not have missed inspecting it for a hundred pounds.\n\nThroughout these singular freedoms on the part of Mr Blandois, which involved a general alteration in his demeanour, making it much coarser and rougher, much more violent and audacious than before, Mr Flintwinch, whose leathern face was not liable to many changes, preserved its immobility intact. Beyond now appearing perhaps, to have been left hanging a trifle too long before that friendly operation of cutting down, he outwardly maintained an equable composure. They had brought their survey to a close in the little room at the side of the hall, and he stood there, eyeing Mr Blandois.\n\n‘I am glad you are so well satisfied, sir,’ was his calm remark. ‘I didn’t expect it. You seem to be quite in good spirits.’\n\n‘In admirable spirits,’ returned Blandois. ‘Word of honour! never more refreshed in spirits. Do you ever have presentiments, Mr Flintwinch?’\n\n‘I am not sure that I know what you mean by the term, sir,’ replied that gentleman.\n\n‘Say, in this case, Mr Flintwinch, undefined anticipations of pleasure to come.’\n\n‘I can’t say I’m sensible of such a sensation at present,’ returned Mr Flintwinch with the utmost gravity. ‘If I should find it coming on, I’ll mention it.’\n\n‘Now I,’ said Blandois, ‘I, my son, have a presentiment to-night that we shall be well acquainted. Do you find it coming on?’\n\n‘N-no,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, deliberately inquiring of himself. ‘I can’t say I do.’\n\n‘I have a strong presentiment that we shall become intimately acquainted.--You have no feeling of that sort yet?’\n\n‘Not yet,’ said Mr Flintwinch.\n\nMr Blandois, taking him by both shoulders again, rolled him about a little in his former merry way, then drew his arm through his own, and invited him to come off and drink a bottle of wine like a dear deep old dog as he was.\n\nWithout a moment’s indecision, Mr Flintwinch accepted the invitation, and they went out to the quarters where the traveller was lodged, through a heavy rain which had rattled on the windows, roofs, and pavements, ever since nightfall. The thunder and lightning had long ago passed over, but the rain was furious. On their arrival at Mr Blandois’ room, a bottle of port wine was ordered by that gallant gentleman; who (crushing every pretty thing he could collect, in the soft disposition of his dainty figure) coiled himself upon the window-seat, while Mr Flintwinch took a chair opposite to him, with the table between them. Mr Blandois proposed having the largest glasses in the house, to which Mr Flintwinch assented. The bumpers filled, Mr Blandois, with a roystering gaiety, clinked the top of his glass against the bottom of Mr Flintwinch’s, and the bottom of his glass against the top of Mr Flintwinch’s, and drank to the intimate acquaintance he foresaw. Mr Flintwinch gravely pledged him, and drank all the wine he could get, and said nothing. As often as Mr Blandois clinked glasses (which was at every replenishment), Mr Flintwinch stolidly did his part of the clinking, and would have stolidly done his companion’s part of the wine as well as his own: being, except in the article of palate, a mere cask.\n\nIn short, Mr Blandois found that to pour port wine into the reticent Flintwinch was, not to open him but to shut him up. Moreover, he had the appearance of a perfect ability to go on all night; or, if occasion were, all next day and all next night; whereas Mr Blandois soon grew indistinctly conscious of swaggering too fiercely and boastfully. He therefore terminated the entertainment at the end of the third bottle.\n\n‘You will draw upon us to-morrow, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with a business-like face at parting.\n\n‘My Cabbage,’ returned the other, taking him by the collar with both hands, ‘I’ll draw upon you; have no fear. Adieu, my Flintwinch. Receive at parting;’ here he gave him a southern embrace, and kissed him soundly on both cheeks; ‘the word of a gentleman! By a thousand Thunders, you shall see me again!’\n\nHe did not present himself next day, though the letter of advice came duly to hand. Inquiring after him at night, Mr Flintwinch found, with surprise, that he had paid his bill and gone back to the Continent by way of Calais. Nevertheless, Jeremiah scraped out of his cogitating face a lively conviction that Mr Blandois would keep his word on this occasion, and would be seen again.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. Spirit\n\nAnybody may pass, any day, in the thronged thoroughfares of the metropolis, some meagre, wrinkled, yellow old man (who might be supposed to have dropped from the stars, if there were any star in the Heavens dull enough to be suspected of casting off so feeble a spark), creeping along with a scared air, as though bewildered and a little frightened by the noise and bustle. This old man is always a little old man. If he were ever a big old man, he has shrunk into a little old man; if he were always a little old man, he has dwindled into a less old man. His coat is a colour, and cut, that never was the mode anywhere, at any period. Clearly, it was not made for him, or for any individual mortal. Some wholesale contractor measured Fate for five thousand coats of such quality, and Fate has lent this old coat to this old man, as one of a long unfinished line of many old men. It has always large dull metal buttons, similar to no other buttons. This old man wears a hat, a thumbed and napless and yet an obdurate hat, which has never adapted itself to the shape of his poor head. His coarse shirt and his coarse neckcloth have no more individuality than his coat and hat; they have the same character of not being his--of not being anybody’s. Yet this old man wears these clothes with a certain unaccustomed air of being dressed and elaborated for the public ways; as though he passed the greater part of his time in a nightcap and gown. And so, like the country mouse in the second year of a famine, come to see the town mouse, and timidly threading his way to the town-mouse’s lodging through a city of cats, this old man passes in the streets.\n\nSometimes, on holidays towards evening, he will be seen to walk with a slightly increased infirmity, and his old eyes will glimmer with a moist and marshy light. Then the little old man is drunk. A very small measure will overset him; he may be bowled off his unsteady legs with a half-pint pot. Some pitying acquaintance--chance acquaintance very often--has warmed up his weakness with a treat of beer, and the consequence will be the lapse of a longer time than usual before he shall pass again. For the little old man is going home to the Workhouse; and on his good behaviour they do not let him out often (though methinks they might, considering the few years he has before him to go out in, under the sun); and on his bad behaviour they shut him up closer than ever in a grove of two score and nineteen more old men, every one of whom smells of all the others.\n\nMrs Plornish’s father,--a poor little reedy piping old gentleman, like a worn-out bird; who had been in what he called the music-binding business, and met with great misfortunes, and who had seldom been able to make his way, or to see it or to pay it, or to do anything at all with it but find it no thoroughfare,--had retired of his own accord to the Workhouse which was appointed by law to be the Good Samaritan of his district (without the twopence, which was bad political economy), on the settlement of that execution which had carried Mr Plornish to the Marshalsea College. Previous to his son-in-law’s difficulties coming to that head, Old Nandy (he was always so called in his legal Retreat, but he was Old Mr Nandy among the Bleeding Hearts) had sat in a corner of the Plornish fireside, and taken his bite and sup out of the Plornish cupboard. He still hoped to resume that domestic position when Fortune should smile upon his son-in-law; in the meantime, while she preserved an immovable countenance, he was, and resolved to remain, one of these little old men in a grove of little old men with a community of flavour.\n\nBut no poverty in him, and no coat on him that never was the mode, and no Old Men’s Ward for his dwelling-place, could quench his daughter’s admiration. Mrs Plornish was as proud of her father’s talents as she could possibly have been if they had made him Lord Chancellor. She had as firm a belief in the sweetness and propriety of his manners as she could possibly have had if he had been Lord Chamberlain. The poor little old man knew some pale and vapid little songs, long out of date, about Chloe, and Phyllis, and Strephon being wounded by the son of Venus; and for Mrs Plornish there was no such music at the Opera as the small internal flutterings and chirpings wherein he would discharge himself of these ditties, like a weak, little, broken barrel-organ, ground by a baby. On his ‘days out,’ those flecks of light in his flat vista of pollard old men,’ it was at once Mrs Plornish’s delight and sorrow, when he was strong with meat, and had taken his full halfpenny-worth of porter, to say, ‘Sing us a song, Father.’ Then he would give them Chloe, and if he were in pretty good spirits, Phyllis also--Strephon he had hardly been up to since he went into retirement--and then would Mrs Plornish declare she did believe there never was such a singer as Father, and wipe her eyes.\n\nIf he had come from Court on these occasions, nay, if he had been the noble Refrigerator come home triumphantly from a foreign court to be presented and promoted on his last tremendous failure, Mrs Plornish could not have handed him with greater elevation about Bleeding Heart Yard. ‘Here’s Father,’ she would say, presenting him to a neighbour. ‘Father will soon be home with us for good, now. Ain’t Father looking well? Father’s a sweeter singer than ever; you’d never have forgotten it, if you’d aheard him just now.’ As to Mr Plornish, he had married these articles of belief in marrying Mr Nandy’s daughter, and only wondered how it was that so gifted an old gentleman had not made a fortune. This he attributed, after much reflection, to his musical genius not having been scientifically developed in his youth. ‘For why,’ argued Mr Plornish, ‘why go a-binding music when you’ve got it in yourself? That’s where it is, I consider.’\n\nOld Nandy had a patron: one patron. He had a patron who in a certain sumptuous way--an apologetic way, as if he constantly took an admiring audience to witness that he really could not help being more free with this old fellow than they might have expected, on account of his simplicity and poverty--was mightily good to him. Old Nandy had been several times to the Marshalsea College, communicating with his son-in-law during his short durance there; and had happily acquired to himself, and had by degrees and in course of time much improved, the patronage of the Father of that national institution.\n\nMr Dorrit was in the habit of receiving this old man as if the old man held of him in vassalage under some feudal tenure. He made little treats and teas for him, as if he came in with his homage from some outlying district where the tenantry were in a primitive state. It seemed as if there were moments when he could by no means have sworn but that the old man was an ancient retainer of his, who had been meritoriously faithful. When he mentioned him, he spoke of him casually as his old pensioner. He had a wonderful satisfaction in seeing him, and in commenting on his decayed condition after he was gone. It appeared to him amazing that he could hold up his head at all, poor creature. ‘In the Workhouse, sir, the Union; no privacy, no visitors, no station, no respect, no speciality. Most deplorable!’\n\nIt was Old Nandy’s birthday, and they let him out. He said nothing about its being his birthday, or they might have kept him in; for such old men should not be born. He passed along the streets as usual to Bleeding Heart Yard, and had his dinner with his daughter and son-in-law, and gave them Phyllis. He had hardly concluded, when Little Dorrit looked in to see how they all were.\n\n‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘here’s Father! Ain’t he looking nice? And such voice he’s in!’\n\nLittle Dorrit gave him her hand, and smilingly said she had not seen him this long time.\n\n‘No, they’re rather hard on poor Father,’ said Mrs Plornish with a lengthening face, ‘and don’t let him have half as much change and fresh air as would benefit him. But he’ll soon be home for good, now. Won’t you, Father?’\n\n‘Yes, my dear, I hope so. In good time, please God.’\n\nHere Mr Plornish delivered himself of an oration which he invariably made, word for word the same, on all such opportunities. It was couched in the following terms:\n\n‘John Edward Nandy. Sir. While there’s a ounce of wittles or drink of any sort in this present roof, you’re fully welcome to your share on it. While there’s a handful of fire or a mouthful of bed in this present roof, you’re fully welcome to your share on it. If so be as there should be nothing in this present roof, you should be as welcome to your share on it as if it was something, much or little. And this is what I mean and so I don’t deceive you, and consequently which is to stand out is to entreat of you, and therefore why not do it?’\n\nTo this lucid address, which Mr Plornish always delivered as if he had composed it (as no doubt he had) with enormous labour, Mrs Plornish’s father pipingly replied:\n\n‘I thank you kindly, Thomas, and I know your intentions well, which is the same I thank you kindly for. But no, Thomas. Until such times as it’s not to take it out of your children’s mouths, which take it is, and call it by what name you will it do remain and equally deprive, though may they come, and too soon they can not come, no Thomas, no!’\n\nMrs Plornish, who had been turning her face a little away with a corner of her apron in her hand, brought herself back to the conversation again by telling Miss Dorrit that Father was going over the water to pay his respects, unless she knew of any reason why it might not be agreeable.\n\nHer answer was, ‘I am going straight home, and if he will come with me I shall be so glad to take care of him--so glad,’ said Little Dorrit, always thoughtful of the feelings of the weak, ‘of his company.’\n\n‘There, Father!’ cried Mrs Plornish. ‘Ain’t you a gay young man to be going for a walk along with Miss Dorrit! Let me tie your neck-handkerchief into a regular good bow, for you’re a regular beau yourself, Father, if ever there was one.’\n\nWith this filial joke his daughter smartened him up, and gave him a loving hug, and stood at the door with her weak child in her arms, and her strong child tumbling down the steps, looking after her little old father as he toddled away with his arm under Little Dorrit’s.\n\nThey walked at a slow pace, and Little Dorrit took him by the Iron Bridge and sat him down there for a rest, and they looked over at the water and talked about the shipping, and the old man mentioned what he would do if he had a ship full of gold coming home to him (his plan was to take a noble lodging for the Plornishes and himself at a Tea Gardens, and live there all the rest of their lives, attended on by the waiter), and it was a special birthday of the old man. They were within five minutes of their destination, when, at the corner of her own street, they came upon Fanny in her new bonnet bound for the same port.\n\n‘Why, good gracious me, Amy!’ cried that young lady starting. ‘You never mean it!’\n\n‘Mean what, Fanny dear?’\n\n‘Well! I could have believed a great deal of you,’ returned the young lady with burning indignation, ‘but I don’t think even I could have believed this, of even you!’\n\n‘Fanny!’ cried Little Dorrit, wounded and astonished.\n\n‘Oh! Don’t Fanny me, you mean little thing, don’t! The idea of coming along the open streets, in the broad light of day, with a Pauper!’ (firing off the last word as if it were a ball from an air-gun).\n\n‘O Fanny!’\n‘I tell you not to Fanny me, for I’ll not submit to it! I never knew such a thing. The way in which you are resolved and determined to disgrace us on all occasions, is really infamous. You bad little thing!’\n\n‘Does it disgrace anybody,’ said Little Dorrit, very gently, ‘to take care of this poor old man?’\n\n‘Yes, miss,’ returned her sister, ‘and you ought to know it does. And you do know it does, and you do it because you know it does. The principal pleasure of your life is to remind your family of their misfortunes. And the next great pleasure of your existence is to keep low company. But, however, if you have no sense of decency, I have. You’ll please to allow me to go on the other side of the way, unmolested.’\n\nWith this, she bounced across to the opposite pavement. The old disgrace, who had been deferentially bowing a pace or two off (for Little Dorrit had let his arm go in her wonder, when Fanny began), and who had been hustled and cursed by impatient passengers for stopping the way, rejoined his companion, rather giddy, and said, ‘I hope nothing’s wrong with your honoured father, Miss? I hope there’s nothing the matter in the honoured family?’\n\n‘No, no,’ returned Little Dorrit. ‘No, thank you. Give me your arm again, Mr Nandy. We shall soon be there now.’\n\nSo she talked to him as she had talked before, and they came to the Lodge and found Mr Chivery on the lock, and went in. Now, it happened that the Father of the Marshalsea was sauntering towards the Lodge at the moment when they were coming out of it, entering the prison arm in arm. As the spectacle of their approach met his view, he displayed the utmost agitation and despondency of mind; and--altogether regardless of Old Nandy, who, making his reverence, stood with his hat in his hand, as he always did in that gracious presence--turned about, and hurried in at his own doorway and up the staircase.\n\nLeaving the old unfortunate, whom in an evil hour she had taken under her protection, with a hurried promise to return to him directly, Little Dorrit hastened after her father, and, on the staircase, found Fanny following her, and flouncing up with offended dignity. The three came into the room almost together; and the Father sat down in his chair, buried his face in his hands, and uttered a groan.\n\n‘Of course,’ said Fanny. ‘Very proper. Poor, afflicted Pa! Now, I hope you believe me, Miss?’\n\n‘What is it, father?’ cried Little Dorrit, bending over him. ‘Have I made you unhappy, father? Not I, I hope!’\n\n‘You hope, indeed! I dare say! Oh, you’--Fanny paused for a sufficiently strong expression--‘you Common-minded little Amy! You complete prison-child!’\n\nHe stopped these angry reproaches with a wave of his hand, and sobbed out, raising his face and shaking his melancholy head at his younger daughter, ‘Amy, I know that you are innocent in intention. But you have cut me to the soul.’\n\n‘Innocent in intention!’ the implacable Fanny struck in. ‘Stuff in intention! Low in intention! Lowering of the family in intention!’\n\n‘Father!’ cried Little Dorrit, pale and trembling. ‘I am very sorry. Pray forgive me. Tell me how it is, that I may not do it again!’\n\n‘How it is, you prevaricating little piece of goods!’ cried Fanny. ‘You know how it is. I have told you already, so don’t fly in the face of Providence by attempting to deny it!’\n\n‘Hush! Amy,’ said the father, passing his pocket-handkerchief several times across his face, and then grasping it convulsively in the hand that dropped across his knee, ‘I have done what I could to keep you select here; I have done what I could to retain you a position here. I may have succeeded; I may not. You may know it; you may not. I give no opinion. I have endured everything here but humiliation. That I have happily been spared--until this day.’\n\nHere his convulsive grasp unclosed itself, and he put his pocket-handkerchief to his eyes again. Little Dorrit, on the ground beside him, with her imploring hand upon his arm, watched him remorsefully. Coming out of his fit of grief, he clenched his pocket-handkerchief once more.\n\n‘Humiliation I have happily been spared until this day. Through all my troubles there has been that--Spirit in myself, and that--that submission to it, if I may use the term, in those about me, which has spared me--ha--humiliation. But this day, this minute, I have keenly felt it.’\n\n‘Of course! How could it be otherwise?’ exclaimed the irrepressible Fanny. ‘Careering and prancing about with a Pauper!’ (air-gun again).\n\n‘But, dear father,’ cried Little Dorrit, ‘I don’t justify myself for having wounded your dear heart--no! Heaven knows I don’t!’ She clasped her hands in quite an agony of distress. ‘I do nothing but beg and pray you to be comforted and overlook it. But if I had not known that you were kind to the old man yourself, and took much notice of him, and were always glad to see him, I would not have come here with him, father, I would not, indeed. What I have been so unhappy as to do, I have done in mistake. I would not wilfully bring a tear to your eyes, dear love!’ said Little Dorrit, her heart well-nigh broken, ‘for anything the world could give me, or anything it could take away.’\n\nFanny, with a partly angry and partly repentant sob, began to cry herself, and to say--as this young lady always said when she was half in passion and half out of it, half spiteful with herself and half spiteful with everybody else--that she wished she were dead.\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea in the meantime took his younger daughter to his breast, and patted her head.\n\n‘There, there! Say no more, Amy, say no more, my child. I will forget it as soon as I can. I,’ with hysterical cheerfulness, ‘I--shall soon be able to dismiss it. It is perfectly true, my dear, that I am always glad to see my old pensioner--as such, as such--and that I do--ha--extend as much protection and kindness to the--hum--the bruised reed--I trust I may so call him without impropriety--as in my circumstances, I can. It is quite true that this is the case, my dear child. At the same time, I preserve in doing this, if I may--ha--if I may use the expression--Spirit. Becoming Spirit. And there are some things which are,’ he stopped to sob, ‘irreconcilable with that, and wound that--wound it deeply. It is not that I have seen my good Amy attentive, and--ha--condescending to my old pensioner--it is not _that_ that hurts me. It is, if I am to close the painful subject by being explicit, that I have seen my child, my own child, my own daughter, coming into this College out of the public streets--smiling! smiling!--arm in arm with--O my God, a livery!’\n\nThis reference to the coat of no cut and no time, the unfortunate gentleman gasped forth, in a scarcely audible voice, and with his clenched pocket-handkerchief raised in the air. His excited feelings might have found some further painful utterance, but for a knock at the door, which had been already twice repeated, and to which Fanny (still wishing herself dead, and indeed now going so far as to add, buried) cried ‘Come in!’\n\n‘Ah, Young John!’ said the Father, in an altered and calmed voice. ‘What is it, Young John?’\n\n‘A letter for you, sir, being left in the Lodge just this minute, and a message with it, I thought, happening to be there myself, sir, I would bring it to your room.’ The speaker’s attention was much distracted by the piteous spectacle of Little Dorrit at her father’s feet, with her head turned away.\n\n‘Indeed, John? Thank you.’\n\n‘The letter is from Mr Clennam, sir--it’s the answer--and the message was, sir, that Mr Clennam also sent his compliments, and word that he would do himself the pleasure of calling this afternoon, hoping to see you, and likewise,’ attention more distracted than before, ‘Miss Amy.’\n\n‘Oh!’ As the Father glanced into the letter (there was a bank-note in it), he reddened a little, and patted Amy on the head afresh. ‘Thank you, Young John. Quite right. Much obliged to you for your attention. No one waiting?’\n\n‘No, sir, no one waiting.’\n\n‘Thank you, John. How is your mother, Young John?’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, she’s not quite as well as we could wish--in fact, we none of us are, except father--but she’s pretty well, sir.’\n\n‘Say we sent our remembrances, will you? Say kind remembrances, if you please, Young John.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, I will.’ And Mr Chivery junior went his way, having spontaneously composed on the spot an entirely new epitaph for himself, to the effect that Here lay the body of John Chivery, Who, Having at such a date, Beheld the idol of his life, In grief and tears, And feeling unable to bear the harrowing spectacle, Immediately repaired to the abode of his inconsolable parents, And terminated his existence by his own rash act.\n\n‘There, there, Amy!’ said the Father, when Young John had closed the door, ‘let us say no more about it.’ The last few minutes had improved his spirits remarkably, and he was quite lightsome. ‘Where is my old pensioner all this while? We must not leave him by himself any longer, or he will begin to suppose he is not welcome, and that would pain me. Will you fetch him, my child, or shall I?’\n\n‘If you wouldn’t mind, father,’ said Little Dorrit, trying to bring her sobbing to a close.\n\n‘Certainly I will go, my dear. I forgot; your eyes are rather red. There! Cheer up, Amy. Don’t be uneasy about me. I am quite myself again, my love, quite myself. Go to your room, Amy, and make yourself look comfortable and pleasant to receive Mr Clennam.’\n\n‘I would rather stay in my own room, Father,’ returned Little Dorrit, finding it more difficult than before to regain her composure. ‘I would far rather not see Mr Clennam.’\n\n‘Oh, fie, fie, my dear, that’s folly. Mr Clennam is a very gentlemanly man--very gentlemanly. A little reserved at times; but I will say extremely gentlemanly. I couldn’t think of your not being here to receive Mr Clennam, my dear, especially this afternoon. So go and freshen yourself up, Amy; go and freshen yourself up, like a good girl.’\n\nThus directed, Little Dorrit dutifully rose and obeyed: only pausing for a moment as she went out of the room, to give her sister a kiss of reconciliation. Upon which, that young lady, feeling much harassed in her mind, and having for the time worn out the wish with which she generally relieved it, conceived and executed the brilliant idea of wishing Old Nandy dead, rather than that he should come bothering there like a disgusting, tiresome, wicked wretch, and making mischief between two sisters.\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea, even humming a tune, and wearing his black velvet cap a little on one side, so much improved were his spirits, went down into the yard, and found his old pensioner standing there hat in hand just within the gate, as he had stood all this time. ‘Come, Nandy!’ said he, with great suavity. ‘Come up-stairs, Nandy; you know the way; why don’t you come up-stairs?’ He went the length, on this occasion, of giving him his hand and saying, ‘How are you, Nandy? Are you pretty well?’ To which that vocalist returned, ‘I thank you, honoured sir, I am all the better for seeing your honour.’ As they went along the yard, the Father of the Marshalsea presented him to a Collegian of recent date. ‘An old acquaintance of mine, sir, an old pensioner.’ And then said, ‘Be covered, my good Nandy; put your hat on,’ with great consideration.\n\nHis patronage did not stop here; for he charged Maggy to get the tea ready, and instructed her to buy certain tea-cakes, fresh butter, eggs, cold ham, and shrimps: to purchase which collation he gave her a bank-note for ten pounds, laying strict injunctions on her to be careful of the change. These preparations were in an advanced stage of progress, and his daughter Amy had come back with her work, when Clennam presented himself; whom he most graciously received, and besought to join their meal.\n\n‘Amy, my love, you know Mr Clennam even better than I have the happiness of doing. Fanny, my dear, you are acquainted with Mr Clennam.’ Fanny acknowledged him haughtily; the position she tacitly took up in all such cases being that there was a vast conspiracy to insult the family by not understanding it, or sufficiently deferring to it, and here was one of the conspirators. ‘This, Mr Clennam, you must know, is an old pensioner of mine, Old Nandy, a very faithful old man.’ (He always spoke of him as an object of great antiquity, but he was two or three years younger than himself.) ‘Let me see. You know Plornish, I think? I think my daughter Amy has mentioned to me that you know poor Plornish?’\n\n‘O yes!’ said Arthur Clennam.\n\n‘Well, sir, this is Mrs Plornish’s father.’\n\n‘Indeed? I am glad to see him.’\n\n‘You would be more glad if you knew his many good qualities, Mr Clennam.’\n\n‘I hope I shall come to know them through knowing him,’ said Arthur, secretly pitying the bowed and submissive figure.\n\n‘It is a holiday with him, and he comes to see his old friends, who are always glad to see him,’ observed the Father of the Marshalsea. Then he added behind his hand, [‘Union, poor old fellow. Out for the day.’)\n\nBy this time Maggy, quietly assisted by her Little Mother, had spread the board, and the repast was ready. It being hot weather and the prison very close, the window was as wide open as it could be pushed. ‘If Maggy will spread that newspaper on the window-sill, my dear,’ remarked the Father complacently and in a half whisper to Little Dorrit, ‘my old pensioner can have his tea there, while we are having ours.’\n\nSo, with a gulf between him and the good company of about a foot in width, standard measure, Mrs Plornish’s father was handsomely regaled. Clennam had never seen anything like his magnanimous protection by that other Father, he of the Marshalsea; and was lost in the contemplation of its many wonders.\n\nThe most striking of these was perhaps the relishing manner in which he remarked on the pensioner’s infirmities and failings, as if he were a gracious Keeper making a running commentary on the decline of the harmless animal he exhibited.\n\n‘Not ready for more ham yet, Nandy? Why, how slow you are! (His last teeth,’ he explained to the company, ‘are going, poor old boy.’)\n\nAt another time, he said, ‘No shrimps, Nandy?’ and on his not instantly replying, observed, [‘His hearing is becoming very defective. He’ll be deaf directly.’)\n\nAt another time he asked him, ‘Do you walk much, Nandy, about the yard within the walls of that place of yours?’\n\n‘No, sir; no. I haven’t any great liking for that.’\n\n‘No, to be sure,’ he assented. ‘Very natural.’ Then he privately informed the circle [‘Legs going.’)\n\nOnce he asked the pensioner, in that general clemency which asked him anything to keep him afloat, how old his younger grandchild was?\n\n‘John Edward,’ said the pensioner, slowly laying down his knife and fork to consider. ‘How old, sir? Let me think now.’\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea tapped his forehead [‘Memory weak.’)\n\n‘John Edward, sir? Well, I really forget. I couldn’t say at this minute, sir, whether it’s two and two months, or whether it’s two and five months. It’s one or the other.’\n\n‘Don’t distress yourself by worrying your mind about it,’ he returned, with infinite forbearance. [‘Faculties evidently decaying--old man rusts in the life he leads!’)\n\nThe more of these discoveries that he persuaded himself he made in the pensioner, the better he appeared to like him; and when he got out of his chair after tea to bid the pensioner good-bye, on his intimating that he feared, honoured sir, his time was running out, he made himself look as erect and strong as possible.\n\n‘We don’t call this a shilling, Nandy, you know,’ he said, putting one in his hand. ‘We call it tobacco.’\n\n‘Honoured sir, I thank you. It shall buy tobacco. My thanks and duty to Miss Amy and Miss Fanny. I wish you good night, Mr Clennam.’\n\n‘And mind you don’t forget us, you know, Nandy,’ said the Father. ‘You must come again, mind, whenever you have an afternoon. You must not come out without seeing us, or we shall be jealous. Good night, Nandy. Be very careful how you descend the stairs, Nandy; they are rather uneven and worn.’ With that he stood on the landing, watching the old man down: and when he came into the room again, said, with a solemn satisfaction on him, ‘A melancholy sight that, Mr Clennam, though one has the consolation of knowing that he doesn’t feel it himself. The poor old fellow is a dismal wreck. Spirit broken and gone--pulverised--crushed out of him, sir, completely!’\n\nAs Clennam had a purpose in remaining, he said what he could responsive to these sentiments, and stood at the window with their enunciator, while Maggy and her Little Mother washed the tea-service and cleared it away. He noticed that his companion stood at the window with the air of an affable and accessible Sovereign, and that, when any of his people in the yard below looked up, his recognition of their salutes just stopped short of a blessing.\n\nWhen Little Dorrit had her work on the table, and Maggy hers on the bedstead, Fanny fell to tying her bonnet as a preliminary to her departure. Arthur, still having his purpose, still remained. At this time the door opened, without any notice, and Mr Tip came in. He kissed Amy as she started up to meet him, nodded to Fanny, nodded to his father, gloomed on the visitor without further recognition, and sat down.\n\n‘Tip, dear,’ said Little Dorrit, mildly, shocked by this, ‘don’t you see--’\n\n‘Yes, I see, Amy. If you refer to the presence of any visitor you have here--I say, if you refer to that,’ answered Tip, jerking his head with emphasis towards his shoulder nearest Clennam, ‘I see!’\n\n‘Is that all you say?’\n\n‘That’s all I say. And I suppose,’ added the lofty young man, after a moment’s pause, ‘that visitor will understand me, when I say that’s all I say. In short, I suppose the visitor will understand that he hasn’t used me like a gentleman.’\n\n‘I do not understand that,’ observed the obnoxious personage referred to with tranquillity.\n\n‘No? Why, then, to make it clearer to you, sir, I beg to let you know that when I address what I call a properly-worded appeal, and an urgent appeal, and a delicate appeal, to an individual, for a small temporary accommodation, easily within his power--easily within his power, mind!--and when that individual writes back word to me that he begs to be excused, I consider that he doesn’t treat me like a gentleman.’\n\nThe Father of the Marshalsea, who had surveyed his son in silence, no sooner heard this sentiment, than he began in angry voice:--\n\n‘How dare you--’ But his son stopped him.\n\n‘Now, don’t ask me how I dare, father, because that’s bosh. As to the fact of the line of conduct I choose to adopt towards the individual present, you ought to be proud of my showing a proper spirit.’\n\n‘I should think so!’ cried Fanny.\n\n‘A proper spirit?’ said the Father. ‘Yes, a proper spirit; a becoming spirit. Is it come to this that my son teaches me--_me_--spirit!’\n\n‘Now, don’t let us bother about it, father, or have any row on the subject. I have fully made up my mind that the individual present has not treated me like a gentleman. And there’s an end of it.’\n\n‘But there is not an end of it, sir,’ returned the Father. ‘But there shall not be an end of it. You have made up your mind? You have made up your mind?’\n\n‘Yes, _I_ have. What’s the good of keeping on like that?’\n\n‘Because,’ returned the Father, in a great heat, ‘you had no right to make up your mind to what is monstrous, to what is--ha--immoral, to what is--hum--parricidal. No, Mr Clennam, I beg, sir. Don’t ask me to desist; there is a--hum--a general principle involved here, which rises even above considerations of--ha--hospitality. I object to the assertion made by my son. I--ha--I personally repel it.’\n\n‘Why, what is it to you, father?’ returned the son, over his shoulder.\n\n‘What is it to me, sir? I have a--hum--a spirit, sir, that will not endure it. I,’ he took out his pocket-handkerchief again and dabbed his face. ‘I am outraged and insulted by it. Let me suppose the case that I myself may at a certain time--ha--or times, have made a--hum--an appeal, and a properly-worded appeal, and a delicate appeal, and an urgent appeal to some individual for a small temporary accommodation. Let me suppose that that accommodation could have been easily extended, and was not extended, and that that individual informed me that he begged to be excused. Am I to be told by my own son, that I therefore received treatment not due to a gentleman, and that I--ha--I submitted to it?’\n\nHis daughter Amy gently tried to calm him, but he would not on any account be calmed. He said his spirit was up, and wouldn’t endure this.\n\nWas he to be told that, he wished to know again, by his own son on his own hearth, to his own face? Was that humiliation to be put upon him by his own blood?\n\n‘You are putting it on yourself, father, and getting into all this injury of your own accord!’ said the young gentleman morosely. ‘What I have made up my mind about has nothing to do with you. What I said had nothing to do with you. Why need you go trying on other people’s hats?’\n\n‘I reply it has everything to do with me,’ returned the Father. ‘I point out to you, sir, with indignation, that--hum--the--ha--delicacy and peculiarity of your father’s position should strike you dumb, sir, if nothing else should, in laying down such--ha--such unnatural principles. Besides; if you are not filial, sir, if you discard that duty, you are at least--hum--not a Christian? Are you--ha--an Atheist? And is it Christian, let me ask you, to stigmatise and denounce an individual for begging to be excused this time, when the same individual may--ha--respond with the required accommodation next time? Is it the part of a Christian not to--hum--not to try him again?’ He had worked himself into quite a religious glow and fervour.\n\n‘I see precious well,’ said Mr Tip, rising, ‘that I shall get no sensible or fair argument here to-night, and so the best thing I can do is to cut. Good night, Amy. Don’t be vexed. I am very sorry it happens here, and you here, upon my soul I am; but I can’t altogether part with my spirit, even for your sake, old girl.’\n\nWith those words he put on his hat and went out, accompanied by Miss Fanny; who did not consider it spirited on her part to take leave of Clennam with any less opposing demonstration than a stare, importing that she had always known him for one of the large body of conspirators.\n\nWhen they were gone, the Father of the Marshalsea was at first inclined to sink into despondency again, and would have done so, but that a gentleman opportunely came up within a minute or two to attend him to the Snuggery. It was the gentleman Clennam had seen on the night of his own accidental detention there, who had that impalpable grievance about the misappropriated Fund on which the Marshal was supposed to batten. He presented himself as deputation to escort the Father to the Chair, it being an occasion on which he had promised to preside over the assembled Collegians in the enjoyment of a little Harmony.\n\n‘Such, you see, Mr Clennam,’ said the Father, ‘are the incongruities of my position here. But a public duty! No man, I am sure, would more readily recognise a public duty than yourself.’\n\nClennam besought him not to delay a moment.\n\n‘Amy, my dear, if you can persuade Mr Clennam to stay longer, I can leave the honours of our poor apology for an establishment with confidence in your hands, and perhaps you may do something towards erasing from Mr Clennam’s mind the--ha--untoward and unpleasant circumstance which has occurred since tea-time.’\n\nClennam assured him that it had made no impression on his mind, and therefore required no erasure.\n\n‘My dear sir,’ said the Father, with a removal of his black cap and a grasp of Clennam’s hand, combining to express the safe receipt of his note and enclosure that afternoon, ‘Heaven ever bless you!’\n\nSo, at last, Clennam’s purpose in remaining was attained, and he could speak to Little Dorrit with nobody by. Maggy counted as nobody, and she was by.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. More Fortune-Telling\n\nMaggy sat at her work in her great white cap with its quantity of opaque frilling hiding what profile she had (she had none to spare), and her serviceable eye brought to bear upon her occupation, on the window side of the room. What with her flapping cap, and what with her unserviceable eye, she was quite partitioned off from her Little Mother, whose seat was opposite the window. The tread and shuffle of feet on the pavement of the yard had much diminished since the taking of the Chair, the tide of Collegians having set strongly in the direction of Harmony. Some few who had no music in their souls, or no money in their pockets, dawdled about; and the old spectacle of the visitor-wife and the depressed unseasoned prisoner still lingered in corners, as broken cobwebs and such unsightly discomforts draggle in corners of other places. It was the quietest time the College knew, saving the night hours when the Collegians took the benefit of the act of sleep. The occasional rattle of applause upon the tables of the Snuggery, denoted the successful termination of a morsel of Harmony; or the responsive acceptance, by the united children, of some toast or sentiment offered to them by their Father. Occasionally, a vocal strain more sonorous than the generality informed the listener that some boastful bass was in blue water, or in the hunting field, or with the reindeer, or on the mountain, or among the heather; but the Marshal of the Marshalsea knew better, and had got him hard and fast.\n\nAs Arthur Clennam moved to sit down by the side of Little Dorrit, she trembled so that she had much ado to hold her needle. Clennam gently put his hand upon her work, and said, ‘Dear Little Dorrit, let me lay it down.’\n\nShe yielded it to him, and he put it aside. Her hands were then nervously clasping together, but he took one of them.\n\n‘How seldom I have seen you lately, Little Dorrit!’\n\n‘I have been busy, sir.’\n\n‘But I heard only to-day,’ said Clennam, ‘by mere accident, of your having been with those good people close by me. Why not come to me, then?’\n\n‘I--I don’t know. Or rather, I thought you might be busy too. You generally are now, are you not?’\n\nHe saw her trembling little form and her downcast face, and the eyes that drooped the moment they were raised to his--he saw them almost with as much concern as tenderness.\n\n‘My child, your manner is so changed!’\n\nThe trembling was now quite beyond her control. Softly withdrawing her hand, and laying it in her other hand, she sat before him with her head bent and her whole form trembling.\n\n‘My own Little Dorrit,’ said Clennam, compassionately.\n\nShe burst into tears. Maggy looked round of a sudden, and stared for at least a minute; but did not interpose. Clennam waited some little while before he spoke again.\n\n‘I cannot bear,’ he said then, ‘to see you weep; but I hope this is a relief to an overcharged heart.’\n\n‘Yes it is, sir. Nothing but that.’\n\n‘Well, well! I feared you would think too much of what passed here just now. It is of no moment; not the least. I am only unfortunate to have come in the way. Let it go by with these tears. It is not worth one of them. One of them? Such an idle thing should be repeated, with my glad consent, fifty times a day, to save you a moment’s heart-ache, Little Dorrit.’\n\nShe had taken courage now, and answered, far more in her usual manner, ‘You are so good! But even if there was nothing else in it to be sorry for and ashamed of, it is such a bad return to you--’\n\n‘Hush!’ said Clennam, smiling and touching her lips with his hand. ‘Forgetfulness in you who remember so many and so much, would be new indeed. Shall I remind you that I am not, and that I never was, anything but the friend whom you agreed to trust? No. You remember it, don’t you?’\n\n‘I try to do so, or I should have broken the promise just now, when my mistaken brother was here. You will consider his bringing-up in this place, and will not judge him hardly, poor fellow, I know!’ In raising her eyes with these words, she observed his face more nearly than she had done yet, and said, with a quick change of tone, ‘You have not been ill, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘Nor tried? Nor hurt?’ she asked him, anxiously.\n\nIt fell to Clennam now, to be not quite certain how to answer. He said in reply:\n\n‘To speak the truth, I have been a little troubled, but it is over. Do I show it so plainly? I ought to have more fortitude and self-command than that. I thought I had. I must learn them of you. Who could teach me better!’\n\nHe never thought that she saw in him what no one else could see. He never thought that in the whole world there were no other eyes that looked upon him with the same light and strength as hers.\n\n‘But it brings me to something that I wish to say,’ he continued, ‘and therefore I will not quarrel even with my own face for telling tales and being unfaithful to me. Besides, it is a privilege and pleasure to confide in my Little Dorrit. Let me confess then, that, forgetting how grave I was, and how old I was, and how the time for such things had gone by me with the many years of sameness and little happiness that made up my long life far away, without marking it--that, forgetting all this, I fancied I loved some one.’\n\n‘Do I know her, sir?’ asked Little Dorrit.\n\n‘No, my child.’\n\n‘Not the lady who has been kind to me for your sake?’\n\n‘Flora. No, no. Do you think--’\n\n‘I never quite thought so,’ said Little Dorrit, more to herself than him. ‘I did wonder at it a little.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Clennam, abiding by the feeling that had fallen on him in the avenue on the night of the roses, the feeling that he was an older man, who had done with that tender part of life, ‘I found out my mistake, and I thought about it a little--in short, a good deal--and got wiser. Being wiser, I counted up my years and considered what I am, and looked back, and looked forward, and found that I should soon be grey. I found that I had climbed the hill, and passed the level ground upon the top, and was descending quickly.’\n\nIf he had known the sharpness of the pain he caused the patient heart, in speaking thus! While doing it, too, with the purpose of easing and serving her.\n\n‘I found that the day when any such thing would have been graceful in me, or good in me, or hopeful or happy for me or any one in connection with me, was gone, and would never shine again.’\n\nO! If he had known, if he had known! If he could have seen the dagger in his hand, and the cruel wounds it struck in the faithful bleeding breast of his Little Dorrit!\n\n‘All that is over, and I have turned my face from it. Why do I speak of this to Little Dorrit? Why do I show you, my child, the space of years that there is between us, and recall to you that I have passed, by the amount of your whole life, the time that is present to you?’\n\n‘Because you trust me, I hope. Because you know that nothing can touch you without touching me; that nothing can make you happy or unhappy, but it must make me, who am so grateful to you, the same.’\n\nHe heard the thrill in her voice, he saw her earnest face, he saw her clear true eyes, he saw the quickened bosom that would have joyfully thrown itself before him to receive a mortal wound directed at his breast, with the dying cry, ‘I love him!’ and the remotest suspicion of the truth never dawned upon his mind. No. He saw the devoted little creature with her worn shoes, in her common dress, in her jail-home; a slender child in body, a strong heroine in soul; and the light of her domestic story made all else dark to him.\n\n‘For those reasons assuredly, Little Dorrit, but for another too. So far removed, so different, and so much older, I am the better fitted for your friend and adviser. I mean, I am the more easily to be trusted; and any little constraint that you might feel with another, may vanish before me. Why have you kept so retired from me? Tell me.’\n\n‘I am better here. My place and use are here. I am much better here,’ said Little Dorrit, faintly.\n\n‘So you said that day upon the bridge. I thought of it much afterwards. Have you no secret you could entrust to me, with hope and comfort, if you would!’\n\n‘Secret? No, I have no secret,’ said Little Dorrit in some trouble.\n\nThey had been speaking in low voices; more because it was natural to what they said to adopt that tone, than with any care to reserve it from Maggy at her work. All of a sudden Maggy stared again, and this time spoke:\n\n‘I say! Little Mother!’\n\n‘Yes, Maggy.’\n\n‘If you an’t got no secret of your own to tell him, tell him that about the Princess. _She_ had a secret, you know.’\n\n‘The Princess had a secret?’ said Clennam, in some surprise. ‘What Princess was that, Maggy?’\n\n‘Lor! How you do go and bother a gal of ten,’ said Maggy, ‘catching the poor thing up in that way. Whoever said the Princess had a secret? _I_ never said so.’\n\n‘I beg your pardon. I thought you did.’\n\n‘No, I didn’t. How could I, when it was her as wanted to find it out? It was the little woman as had the secret, and she was always a spinning at her wheel. And so she says to her, why do you keep it there? And so the t’other one says to her, no I don’t; and so the t’other one says to her, yes you do; and then they both goes to the cupboard, and there it is. And she wouldn’t go into the Hospital, and so she died. _You_ know, Little Mother; tell him that. For it was a reg’lar good secret, that was!’ cried Maggy, hugging herself.\n\nArthur looked at Little Dorrit for help to comprehend this, and was struck by seeing her so timid and red. But, when she told him that it was only a Fairy Tale she had one day made up for Maggy, and that there was nothing in it which she wouldn’t be ashamed to tell again to anybody else, even if she could remember it, he left the subject where it was.\n\nHowever, he returned to his own subject by first entreating her to see him oftener, and to remember that it was impossible to have a stronger interest in her welfare than he had, or to be more set upon promoting it than he was. When she answered fervently, she well knew that, she never forgot it, he touched upon his second and more delicate point--the suspicion he had formed.\n\n‘Little Dorrit,’ he said, taking her hand again, and speaking lower than he had spoken yet, so that even Maggy in the small room could not hear him, ‘another word. I have wanted very much to say this to you; I have tried for opportunities. Don’t mind me, who, for the matter of years, might be your father or your uncle. Always think of me as quite an old man. I know that all your devotion centres in this room, and that nothing to the last will ever tempt you away from the duties you discharge here. If I were not sure of it, I should, before now, have implored you, and implored your father, to let me make some provision for you in a more suitable place. But you may have an interest--I will not say, now, though even that might be--may have, at another time, an interest in some one else; an interest not incompatible with your affection here.’\n\nShe was very, very pale, and silently shook her head.\n\n‘It may be, dear Little Dorrit.’\n\n‘No. No. No.’ She shook her head, after each slow repetition of the word, with an air of quiet desolation that he remembered long afterwards. The time came when he remembered it well, long afterwards, within those prison walls; within that very room.\n\n‘But, if it ever should be, tell me so, my dear child. Entrust the truth to me, point out the object of such an interest to me, and I will try with all the zeal, and honour, and friendship and respect that I feel for you, good Little Dorrit of my heart, to do you a lasting service.’\n\n‘O thank you, thank you! But, O no, O no, O no!’ She said this, looking at him with her work-worn hands folded together, and in the same resigned accents as before.\n\n‘I press for no confidence now. I only ask you to repose unhesitating trust in me.’\n\n‘Can I do less than that, when you are so good!’\n\n‘Then you will trust me fully? Will have no secret unhappiness, or anxiety, concealed from me?’\n\n‘Almost none.’\n\n‘And you have none now?’\n\nShe shook her head. But she was very pale.\n\n‘When I lie down to-night, and my thoughts come back--as they will, for they do every night, even when I have not seen you--to this sad place, I may believe that there is no grief beyond this room, now, and its usual occupants, which preys on Little Dorrit’s mind?’\n\nShe seemed to catch at these words--that he remembered, too, long afterwards--and said, more brightly, ‘Yes, Mr Clennam; yes, you may!’\n\nThe crazy staircase, usually not slow to give notice when any one was coming up or down, here creaked under a quick tread, and a further sound was heard upon it, as if a little steam-engine with more steam than it knew what to do with, were working towards the room. As it approached, which it did very rapidly, it laboured with increased energy; and, after knocking at the door, it sounded as if it were stooping down and snorting in at the keyhole.\n\nBefore Maggy could open the door, Mr Pancks, opening it from without, stood without a hat and with his bare head in the wildest condition, looking at Clennam and Little Dorrit, over her shoulder. He had a lighted cigar in his hand, and brought with him airs of ale and tobacco smoke.\n\n‘Pancks the gipsy,’ he observed out of breath, ‘fortune-telling.’\n\nHe stood dingily smiling, and breathing hard at them, with a most curious air; as if, instead of being his proprietor’s grubber, he were the triumphant proprietor of the Marshalsea, the Marshal, all the turnkeys, and all the Collegians. In his great self-satisfaction he put his cigar to his lips (being evidently no smoker), and took such a pull at it, with his right eye shut up tight for the purpose, that he underwent a convulsion of shuddering and choking. But even in the midst of that paroxysm, he still essayed to repeat his favourite introduction of himself, ‘Pa-ancks the gi-ipsy, fortune-telling.’\n\n‘I am spending the evening with the rest of ‘em,’ said Pancks. ‘I’ve been singing. I’ve been taking a part in White sand and grey sand. _I_ don’t know anything about it. Never mind. I’ll take any part in anything. It’s all the same, if you’re loud enough.’\n\nAt first Clennam supposed him to be intoxicated. But he soon perceived that though he might be a little the worse (or better) for ale, the staple of his excitement was not brewed from malt, or distilled from any grain or berry.\n\n‘How d’ye do, Miss Dorrit?’ said Pancks. ‘I thought you wouldn’t mind my running round, and looking in for a moment. Mr Clennam I heard was here, from Mr Dorrit. How are you, Sir?’\n\nClennam thanked him, and said he was glad to see him so gay.\n\n‘Gay!’ said Pancks. ‘I’m in wonderful feather, sir. I can’t stop a minute, or I shall be missed, and I don’t want ‘em to miss me.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nHe seemed to have an insatiate delight in appealing to her and looking at her; excitedly sticking his hair up at the same moment, like a dark species of cockatoo.\n\n‘I haven’t been here half an hour. I knew Mr Dorrit was in the chair, and I said, “I’ll go and support him!” I ought to be down in Bleeding Heart Yard by rights; but I can worry them to-morrow.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nHis little black eyes sparkled electrically. His very hair seemed to sparkle as he roughened it. He was in that highly-charged state that one might have expected to draw sparks and snaps from him by presenting a knuckle to any part of his figure.\n\n‘Capital company here,’ said Pancks.--‘Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nShe was half afraid of him, and irresolute what to say. He laughed, with a nod towards Clennam.\n\n‘Don’t mind him, Miss Dorrit. He’s one of us. We agreed that you shouldn’t take on to mind me before people, but we didn’t mean Mr Clennam. He’s one of us. He’s in it. An’t you, Mr Clennam?--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nThe excitement of this strange creature was fast communicating itself to Clennam. Little Dorrit with amazement, saw this, and observed that they exchanged quick looks.\n\n‘I was making a remark,’ said Pancks, ‘but I declare I forget what it was. Oh, I know! Capital company here. I’ve been treating ‘em all round.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\n‘Very generous of you,’ she returned, noticing another of the quick looks between the two.\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Pancks. ‘Don’t mention it. I’m coming into my property, that’s the fact. I can afford to be liberal. I think I’ll give ‘em a treat here. Tables laid in the yard. Bread in stacks. Pipes in faggots. Tobacco in hayloads. Roast beef and plum-pudding for every one. Quart of double stout a head. Pint of wine too, if they like it, and the authorities give permission.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nShe was thrown into such a confusion by his manner, or rather by Clennam’s growing understanding of his manner (for she looked to him after every fresh appeal and cockatoo demonstration on the part of Mr Pancks), that she only moved her lips in answer, without forming any word.\n\n‘And oh, by-the-bye!’ said Pancks, ‘you were to live to know what was behind us on that little hand of yours. And so you shall, you shall, my darling.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’\n\nHe had suddenly checked himself. Where he got all the additional black prongs from, that now flew up all over his head like the myriads of points that break out in the large change of a great firework, was a wonderful mystery.\n\n‘But I shall be missed;’ he came back to that; ‘and I don’t want ‘em to miss me. Mr Clennam, you and I made a bargain. I said you should find me stick to it. You shall find me stick to it now, sir, if you’ll step out of the room a moment. Miss Dorrit, I wish you good night. Miss Dorrit, I wish you good fortune.’\n\nHe rapidly shook her by both hands, and puffed down stairs. Arthur followed him with such a hurried step, that he had very nearly tumbled over him on the last landing, and rolled him down into the yard.\n\n‘What is it, for Heaven’s sake!’ Arthur demanded, when they burst out there both together.\n\n‘Stop a moment, sir. Mr Rugg. Let me introduce him.’\n\nWith those words he presented another man without a hat, and also with a cigar, and also surrounded with a halo of ale and tobacco smoke, which man, though not so excited as himself, was in a state which would have been akin to lunacy but for its fading into sober method when compared with the rampancy of Mr Pancks.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, Mr Rugg,’ said Pancks. ‘Stop a moment. Come to the pump.’\n\nThey adjourned to the pump. Mr Pancks, instantly putting his head under the spout, requested Mr Rugg to take a good strong turn at the handle. Mr Rugg complying to the letter, Mr Pancks came forth snorting and blowing to some purpose, and dried himself on his handkerchief.\n\n‘I am the clearer for that,’ he gasped to Clennam standing astonished. ‘But upon my soul, to hear her father making speeches in that chair, knowing what we know, and to see her up in that room in that dress, knowing what we know, is enough to--give me a back, Mr Rugg--a little higher, sir,--that’ll do!’\n\nThen and there, on that Marshalsea pavement, in the shades of evening, did Mr Pancks, of all mankind, fly over the head and shoulders of Mr Rugg of Pentonville, General Agent, Accountant, and Recoverer of Debts. Alighting on his feet, he took Clennam by the button-hole, led him behind the pump, and pantingly produced from his pocket a bundle of papers.\n\nMr Rugg, also, pantingly produced from his pocket a bundle of papers.\n\n‘Stay!’ said Clennam in a whisper.’You have made a discovery.’\n\nMr Pancks answered, with an unction which there is no language to convey, ‘We rather think so.’\n\n‘Does it implicate any one?’\n\n‘How implicate, sir?’\n\n‘In any suppression or wrong dealing of any kind?’\n\n‘Not a bit of it.’\n\n‘Thank God!’ said Clennam to himself. ‘Now show me.’\n\n‘You are to understand’--snorted Pancks, feverishly unfolding papers, and speaking in short high-pressure blasts of sentences, ‘Where’s the Pedigree? Where’s Schedule number four, Mr Rugg? Oh! all right! Here we are.--You are to understand that we are this very day virtually complete. We shan’t be legally for a day or two. Call it at the outside a week. We’ve been at it night and day for I don’t know how long. Mr Rugg, you know how long? Never mind. Don’t say. You’ll only confuse me. You shall tell her, Mr Clennam. Not till we give you leave. Where’s that rough total, Mr Rugg? Oh! Here we are! There sir! That’s what you’ll have to break to her. That man’s your Father of the Marshalsea!’\n\n# CHAPTER 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint\n\nResigning herself to inevitable fate by making the best of those people, the Miggleses, and submitting her philosophy to the draught upon it, of which she had foreseen the likelihood in her interview with Arthur, Mrs Gowan handsomely resolved not to oppose her son’s marriage. In her progress to, and happy arrival at, this resolution, she was possibly influenced, not only by her maternal affections but by three politic considerations.\n\nOf these, the first may have been that her son had never signified the smallest intention to ask her consent, or any mistrust of his ability to dispense with it; the second, that the pension bestowed upon her by a grateful country (and a Barnacle) would be freed from any little filial inroads, when her Henry should be married to the darling only child of a man in very easy circumstances; the third, that Henry’s debts must clearly be paid down upon the altar-railing by his father-in-law. When, to these three-fold points of prudence there is added the fact that Mrs Gowan yielded her consent the moment she knew of Mr Meagles having yielded his, and that Mr Meagles’s objection to the marriage had been the sole obstacle in its way all along, it becomes the height of probability that the relict of the deceased Commissioner of nothing particular, turned these ideas in her sagacious mind.\n\nAmong her connections and acquaintances, however, she maintained her individual dignity and the dignity of the blood of the Barnacles, by diligently nursing the pretence that it was a most unfortunate business; that she was sadly cut up by it; that this was a perfect fascination under which Henry laboured; that she had opposed it for a long time, but what could a mother do; and the like. She had already called Arthur Clennam to bear witness to this fable, as a friend of the Meagles family; and she followed up the move by now impounding the family itself for the same purpose. In the first interview she accorded to Mr Meagles, she slided herself into the position of disconsolately but gracefully yielding to irresistible pressure. With the utmost politeness and good-breeding, she feigned that it was she--not he--who had made the difficulty, and who at length gave way; and that the sacrifice was hers--not his. The same feint, with the same polite dexterity, she foisted on Mrs Meagles, as a conjuror might have forced a card on that innocent lady; and, when her future daughter-in-law was presented to her by her son, she said on embracing her, ‘My dear, what have you done to Henry that has bewitched him so!’ at the same time allowing a few tears to carry before them, in little pills, the cosmetic powder on her nose; as a delicate but touching signal that she suffered much inwardly for the show of composure with which she bore her misfortune.\n\nAmong the friends of Mrs Gowan (who piqued herself at once on being Society, and on maintaining intimate and easy relations with that Power), Mrs Merdle occupied a front row. True, the Hampton Court Bohemians, without exception, turned up their noses at Merdle as an upstart; but they turned them down again, by falling flat on their faces to worship his wealth. In which compensating adjustment of their noses, they were pretty much like Treasury, Bar, and Bishop, and all the rest of them.\n\nTo Mrs Merdle, Mrs Gowan repaired on a visit of self-condolence, after having given the gracious consent aforesaid. She drove into town for the purpose in a one-horse carriage irreverently called at that period of English history, a pill-box. It belonged to a job-master in a small way, who drove it himself, and who jobbed it by the day, or hour, to most of the old ladies in Hampton Court Palace; but it was a point of ceremony, in that encampment, that the whole equipage should be tacitly regarded as the private property of the jobber for the time being, and that the job-master should betray personal knowledge of nobody but the jobber in possession. So the Circumlocution Barnacles, who were the largest job-masters in the universe, always pretended to know of no other job but the job immediately in hand.\n\nMrs Merdle was at home, and was in her nest of crimson and gold, with the parrot on a neighbouring stem watching her with his head on one side, as if he took her for another splendid parrot of a larger species. To whom entered Mrs Gowan, with her favourite green fan, which softened the light on the spots of bloom.\n\n‘My dear soul,’ said Mrs Gowan, tapping the back of her friend’s hand with this fan after a little indifferent conversation, ‘you are my only comfort. That affair of Henry’s that I told you of, is to take place. Now, how does it strike you? I am dying to know, because you represent and express Society so well.’\n\nMrs Merdle reviewed the bosom which Society was accustomed to review; and having ascertained that show-window of Mr Merdle’s and the London jewellers’ to be in good order, replied:\n\n‘As to marriage on the part of a man, my dear, Society requires that he should retrieve his fortunes by marriage. Society requires that he should gain by marriage. Society requires that he should found a handsome establishment by marriage. Society does not see, otherwise, what he has to do with marriage. Bird, be quiet!’\n\nFor the parrot on his cage above them, presiding over the conference as if he were a judge (and indeed he looked rather like one), had wound up the exposition with a shriek.\n\n‘Cases there are,’ said Mrs Merdle, delicately crooking the little finger of her favourite hand, and making her remarks neater by that neat action; ‘cases there are where a man is not young or elegant, and is rich, and has a handsome establishment already. Those are of a different kind. In such cases--’\n\nMrs Merdle shrugged her snowy shoulders and put her hand upon the jewel-stand, checking a little cough, as though to add, ‘why, a man looks out for this sort of thing, my dear.’ Then the parrot shrieked again, and she put up her glass to look at him, and said, ‘Bird! Do be quiet!’\n\n‘But, young men,’ resumed Mrs Merdle, ‘and by young men you know what I mean, my love--I mean people’s sons who have the world before them--they must place themselves in a better position towards Society by marriage, or Society really will not have any patience with their making fools of themselves. Dreadfully worldly all this sounds,’ said Mrs Merdle, leaning back in her nest and putting up her glass again, ‘does it not?’\n\n‘But it is true,’ said Mrs Gowan, with a highly moral air.\n\n‘My dear, it is not to be disputed for a moment,’ returned Mrs Merdle; ‘because Society has made up its mind on the subject, and there is nothing more to be said. If we were in a more primitive state, if we lived under roofs of leaves, and kept cows and sheep and creatures instead of banker’s accounts (which would be delicious; my dear, I am pastoral to a degree, by nature), well and good. But we don’t live under leaves, and keep cows and sheep and creatures. I perfectly exhaust myself sometimes, in pointing out the distinction to Edmund Sparkler.’\n\nMrs Gowan, looking over her green fan when this young gentleman’s name was mentioned, replied as follows:\n\n‘My love, you know the wretched state of the country--those unfortunate concessions of John Barnacle’s!--and you therefore know the reasons for my being as poor as Thingummy.’\n\n‘A church mouse?’ Mrs Merdle suggested with a smile.\n\n‘I was thinking of the other proverbial church person--Job,’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘Either will do. It would be idle to disguise, consequently, that there is a wide difference between the position of your son and mine. I may add, too, that Henry has talent--’\n\n‘Which Edmund certainly has not,’ said Mrs Merdle, with the greatest suavity.\n\n‘--and that his talent, combined with disappointment,’ Mrs Gowan went on, ‘has led him into a pursuit which--ah dear me! You know, my dear. Such being Henry’s different position, the question is what is the most inferior class of marriage to which I can reconcile myself.’\n\nMrs Merdle was so much engaged with the contemplation of her arms (beautiful-formed arms, and the very thing for bracelets), that she omitted to reply for a while. Roused at length by the silence, she folded the arms, and with admirable presence of mind looked her friend full in the face, and said interrogatively, ‘Ye-es? And then?’\n\n‘And then, my dear,’ said Mrs Gowan not quite so sweetly as before, ‘I should be glad to hear what you have to say to it.’\n\nHere the parrot, who had been standing on one leg since he screamed last, burst into a fit of laughter, bobbed himself derisively up and down on both legs, and finished by standing on one leg again, and pausing for a reply, with his head as much awry as he could possibly twist it.\n\n‘Sounds mercenary to ask what the gentleman is to get with the lady,’ said Mrs Merdle; ‘but Society is perhaps a little mercenary, you know, my dear.’\n\n‘From what I can make out,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘I believe I may say that Henry will be relieved from debt--’\n\n‘Much in debt?’ asked Mrs Merdle through her eyeglass.\n\n‘Why tolerably, I should think,’ said Mrs Gowan.\n\n‘Meaning the usual thing; I understand; just so,’ Mrs Merdle observed in a comfortable sort of way.\n\n‘And that the father will make them an allowance of three hundred a-year, or perhaps altogether something more, which, in Italy-’\n\n‘Oh! Going to Italy?’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘For Henry to study. You need be at no loss to guess why, my dear. That dreadful Art--’\n\nTrue. Mrs Merdle hastened to spare the feelings of her afflicted friend. She understood. Say no more!\n\n‘And that,’ said Mrs Gowan, shaking her despondent head, ‘that’s all. That,’ repeated Mrs Gowan, furling her green fan for the moment, and tapping her chin with it (it was on the way to being a double chin; might be called a chin and a half at present), ‘that’s all! On the death of the old people, I suppose there will be more to come; but how it may be restricted or locked up, I don’t know. And as to that, they may live for ever. My dear, they are just the kind of people to do it.’\n\nNow, Mrs Merdle, who really knew her friend Society pretty well, and who knew what Society’s mothers were, and what Society’s daughters were, and what Society’s matrimonial market was, and how prices ruled in it, and what scheming and counter-scheming took place for the high buyers, and what bargaining and huckstering went on, thought in the depths of her capacious bosom that this was a sufficiently good catch. Knowing, however, what was expected of her, and perceiving the exact nature of the fiction to be nursed, she took it delicately in her arms, and put her required contribution of gloss upon it.\n\n‘And that is all, my dear?’ said she, heaving a friendly sigh. ‘Well, well! The fault is not yours. You have nothing to reproach yourself with. You must exercise the strength of mind for which you are renowned, and make the best of it.’\n\n‘The girl’s family have made,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘of course, the most strenuous endeavours to--as the lawyers say--to have and to hold Henry.’\n\n‘Of course they have, my dear,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘I have persisted in every possible objection, and have worried myself morning, noon, and night, for means to detach Henry from the connection.’\n\n‘No doubt you have, my dear,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘And all of no use. All has broken down beneath me. Now tell me, my love. Am I justified in at last yielding my most reluctant consent to Henry’s marrying among people not in Society; or, have I acted with inexcusable weakness?’\n\nIn answer to this direct appeal, Mrs Merdle assured Mrs Gowan (speaking as a Priestess of Society) that she was highly to be commended, that she was much to be sympathised with, that she had taken the highest of parts, and had come out of the furnace refined. And Mrs Gowan, who of course saw through her own threadbare blind perfectly, and who knew that Mrs Merdle saw through it perfectly, and who knew that Society would see through it perfectly, came out of this form, notwithstanding, as she had gone into it, with immense complacency and gravity.\n\nThe conference was held at four or five o’clock in the afternoon, when all the region of Harley Street, Cavendish Square, was resonant of carriage-wheels and double-knocks. It had reached this point when Mr Merdle came home from his daily occupation of causing the British name to be more and more respected in all parts of the civilised globe capable of the appreciation of world-wide commercial enterprise and gigantic combinations of skill and capital. For, though nobody knew with the least precision what Mr Merdle’s business was, except that it was to coin money, these were the terms in which everybody defined it on all ceremonious occasions, and which it was the last new polite reading of the parable of the camel and the needle’s eye to accept without inquiry.\n\nFor a gentleman who had this splendid work cut out for him, Mr Merdle looked a little common, and rather as if, in the course of his vast transactions, he had accidentally made an interchange of heads with some inferior spirit. He presented himself before the two ladies in the course of a dismal stroll through his mansion, which had no apparent object but escape from the presence of the chief butler.\n\n‘I beg your pardon,’ he said, stopping short in confusion; ‘I didn’t know there was anybody here but the parrot.’\n\nHowever, as Mrs Merdle said, ‘You can come in!’ and as Mrs Gowan said she was just going, and had already risen to take her leave, he came in, and stood looking out at a distant window, with his hands crossed under his uneasy coat-cuffs, clasping his wrists as if he were taking himself into custody. In this attitude he fell directly into a reverie from which he was only aroused by his wife’s calling to him from her ottoman, when they had been for some quarter of an hour alone.\n\n‘Eh? Yes?’ said Mr Merdle, turning towards her. ‘What is it?’\n\n‘What is it?’ repeated Mrs Merdle. ‘It is, I suppose, that you have not heard a word of my complaint.’\n\n‘Your complaint, Mrs Merdle?’ said Mr Merdle. ‘I didn’t know that you were suffering from a complaint. What complaint?’\n\n‘A complaint of you,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘Oh! A complaint of me,’ said Mr Merdle. ‘What is the--what have I--what may you have to complain of in me, Mrs Merdle?’\n\nIn his withdrawing, abstracted, pondering way, it took him some time to shape this question. As a kind of faint attempt to convince himself that he was the master of the house, he concluded by presenting his forefinger to the parrot, who expressed his opinion on that subject by instantly driving his bill into it.\n\n‘You were saying, Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, with his wounded finger in his mouth, ‘that you had a complaint against me?’\n\n‘A complaint which I could scarcely show the justice of more emphatically, than by having to repeat it,’ said Mrs Merdle. ‘I might as well have stated it to the wall. I had far better have stated it to the bird. He would at least have screamed.’\n\n‘You don’t want me to scream, Mrs Merdle, I suppose,’ said Mr Merdle, taking a chair.\n\n‘Indeed I don’t know,’ retorted Mrs Merdle, ‘but that you had better do that, than be so moody and distraught. One would at least know that you were sensible of what was going on around you.’\n\n‘A man might scream, and yet not be that, Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, heavily.\n\n‘And might be dogged, as you are at present, without screaming,’ returned Mrs Merdle. ‘That’s very true. If you wish to know the complaint I make against you, it is, in so many plain words, that you really ought not to go into Society unless you can accommodate yourself to Society.’\n\nMr Merdle, so twisting his hands into what hair he had upon his head that he seemed to lift himself up by it as he started out of his chair, cried:\n\n‘Why, in the name of all the infernal powers, Mrs Merdle, who does more for Society than I do? Do you see these premises, Mrs Merdle? Do you see this furniture, Mrs Merdle? Do you look in the glass and see yourself, Mrs Merdle? Do you know the cost of all this, and who it’s all provided for? And yet will you tell me that I oughtn’t to go into Society? I, who shower money upon it in this way? I, who might always be said--to--to--to harness myself to a watering-cart full of money, and go about saturating Society every day of my life.’\n\n‘Pray, don’t be violent, Mr Merdle,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘Violent?’ said Mr Merdle. ‘You are enough to make me desperate. You don’t know half of what I do to accommodate Society. You don’t know anything of the sacrifices I make for it.’\n\n‘I know,’ returned Mrs Merdle, ‘that you receive the best in the land. I know that you move in the whole Society of the country. And I believe I know (indeed, not to make any ridiculous pretence about it, I know I know) who sustains you in it, Mr Merdle.’\n\n‘Mrs Merdle,’ retorted that gentleman, wiping his dull red and yellow face, ‘I know that as well as you do. If you were not an ornament to Society, and if I was not a benefactor to Society, you and I would never have come together. When I say a benefactor to it, I mean a person who provides it with all sorts of expensive things to eat and drink and look at. But, to tell me that I am not fit for it after all I have done for it--after all I have done for it,’ repeated Mr Merdle, with a wild emphasis that made his wife lift up her eyelids, ‘after all--all!--to tell me I have no right to mix with it after all, is a pretty reward.’\n\n‘I say,’ answered Mrs Merdle composedly, ‘that you ought to make yourself fit for it by being more degage, and less preoccupied. There is a positive vulgarity in carrying your business affairs about with you as you do.’\n\n‘How do I carry them about, Mrs Merdle?’ asked Mr Merdle.\n\n‘How do you carry them about?’ said Mrs Merdle. ‘Look at yourself in the glass.’\n\nMr Merdle involuntarily turned his eyes in the direction of the nearest mirror, and asked, with a slow determination of his turbid blood to his temples, whether a man was to be called to account for his digestion?\n\n‘You have a physician,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘He does me no good,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nMrs Merdle changed her ground.\n\n‘Besides,’ said she, ‘your digestion is nonsense. I don’t speak of your digestion. I speak of your manner.’\n\n‘Mrs Merdle,’ returned her husband, ‘I look to you for that. You supply manner, and I supply money.’\n\n‘I don’t expect you,’ said Mrs Merdle, reposing easily among her cushions, ‘to captivate people. I don’t want you to take any trouble upon yourself, or to try to be fascinating. I simply request you to care about nothing--or seem to care about nothing--as everybody else does.’\n\n‘Do I ever say I care about anything?’ asked Mr Merdle.\n\n‘Say? No! Nobody would attend to you if you did. But you show it.’\n\n‘Show what? What do I show?’ demanded Mr Merdle hurriedly.\n\n‘I have already told you. You show that you carry your business cares an projects about, instead of leaving them in the City, or wherever else they belong to,’ said Mrs Merdle. ‘Or seeming to. Seeming would be quite enough: I ask no more. Whereas you couldn’t be more occupied with your day’s calculations and combinations than you habitually show yourself to be, if you were a carpenter.’\n\n‘A carpenter!’ repeated Mr Merdle, checking something like a groan. ‘I shouldn’t so much mind being a carpenter, Mrs Merdle.’\n\n‘And my complaint is,’ pursued the lady, disregarding the low remark, ‘that it is not the tone of Society, and that you ought to correct it, Mr Merdle. If you have any doubt of my judgment, ask even Edmund Sparkler.’ The door of the room had opened, and Mrs Merdle now surveyed the head of her son through her glass. ‘Edmund; we want you here.’\n\nMr Sparkler, who had merely put in his head and looked round the room without entering (as if he were searching the house for that young lady with no nonsense about her), upon this followed up his head with his body, and stood before them. To whom, in a few easy words adapted to his capacity, Mrs Merdle stated the question at issue.\n\nThe young gentleman, after anxiously feeling his shirt-collar as if it were his pulse and he were hypochondriacal, observed, ‘That he had heard it noticed by fellers.’\n\n‘Edmund Sparkler has heard it noticed,’ said Mrs Merdle, with languid triumph. ‘Why, no doubt everybody has heard it noticed!’ Which in truth was no unreasonable inference; seeing that Mr Sparkler would probably be the last person, in any assemblage of the human species, to receive an impression from anything that passed in his presence.\n\n‘And Edmund Sparkler will tell you, I dare say,’ said Mrs Merdle, waving her favourite hand towards her husband, ‘how he has heard it noticed.’\n\n‘I couldn’t,’ said Mr Sparkler, after feeling his pulse as before, ‘couldn’t undertake to say what led to it--‘cause memory desperate loose. But being in company with the brother of a doosed fine gal--well educated too--with no biggodd nonsense about her--at the period alluded to--’\n\n‘There! Never mind the sister,’ remarked Mrs Merdle, a little impatiently. ‘What did the brother say?’\n\n‘Didn’t say a word, ma’am,’ answered Mr Sparkler. ‘As silent a feller as myself. Equally hard up for a remark.’\n\n‘Somebody said something,’ returned Mrs Merdle. ‘Never mind who it was.’\n\n[‘Assure you I don’t in the least,’ said Mr Sparkler.)\n\n‘But tell us what it was.’\n\nMr Sparkler referred to his pulse again, and put himself through some severe mental discipline before he replied:\n\n‘Fellers referring to my Governor--expression not my own--occasionally compliment my Governor in a very handsome way on being immensely rich and knowing--perfect phenomenon of Buyer and Banker and that--but say the Shop sits heavily on him. Say he carried the Shop about, on his back rather--like Jew clothesmen with too much business.’\n\n‘Which,’ said Mrs Merdle, rising, with her floating drapery about her, ‘is exactly my complaint. Edmund, give me your arm up-stairs.’\n\nMr Merdle, left alone to meditate on a better conformation of himself to Society, looked out of nine windows in succession, and appeared to see nine wastes of space. When he had thus entertained himself he went down-stairs, and looked intently at all the carpets on the ground-floor; and then came up-stairs again, and looked intently at all the carpets on the first-floor; as if they were gloomy depths, in unison with his oppressed soul. Through all the rooms he wandered, as he always did, like the last person on earth who had any business to approach them. Let Mrs Merdle announce, with all her might, that she was at Home ever so many nights in a season, she could not announce more widely and unmistakably than Mr Merdle did that he was never at home.\n\nAt last he met the chief butler, the sight of which splendid retainer always finished him. Extinguished by this great creature, he sneaked to his dressing-room, and there remained shut up until he rode out to dinner, with Mrs Merdle, in her own handsome chariot. At dinner, he was envied and flattered as a being of might, was Treasuried, Barred, and Bishoped, as much as he would; and an hour after midnight came home alone, and being instantly put out again in his own hall, like a rushlight, by the chief butler, went sighing to bed.\n\n# CHAPTER 34. A Shoal of Barnacles\n\nMr Henry Gowan and the dog were established frequenters of the cottage, and the day was fixed for the wedding. There was to be a convocation of Barnacles on the occasion, in order that that very high and very large family might shed as much lustre on the marriage as so dim an event was capable of receiving.\n\nTo have got the whole Barnacle family together would have been impossible for two reasons. Firstly, because no building could have held all the members and connections of that illustrious house. Secondly, because wherever there was a square yard of ground in British occupation under the sun or moon, with a public post upon it, sticking to that post was a Barnacle. No intrepid navigator could plant a flag-staff upon any spot of earth, and take possession of it in the British name, but to that spot of earth, so soon as the discovery was known, the Circumlocution Office sent out a Barnacle and a despatch-box. Thus the Barnacles were all over the world, in every direction--despatch-boxing the compass.\n\nBut, while the so-potent art of Prospero himself would have failed in summoning the Barnacles from every speck of ocean and dry land on which there was nothing (except mischief) to be done and anything to be pocketed, it was perfectly feasible to assemble a good many Barnacles. This Mrs Gowan applied herself to do; calling on Mr Meagles frequently with new additions to the list, and holding conferences with that gentleman when he was not engaged (as he generally was at this period) in examining and paying the debts of his future son-in-law, in the apartment of scales and scoop.\n\nOne marriage guest there was, in reference to whose presence Mr Meagles felt a nearer interest and concern than in the attendance of the most elevated Barnacle expected; though he was far from insensible of the honour of having such company. This guest was Clennam. But Clennam had made a promise he held sacred, among the trees that summer night, and, in the chivalry of his heart, regarded it as binding him to many implied obligations. In forgetfulness of himself, and delicate service to her on all occasions, he was never to fail; to begin it, he answered Mr Meagles cheerfully, ‘I shall come, of course.’\n\nHis partner, Daniel Doyce, was something of a stumbling-block in Mr Meagles’s way, the worthy gentleman being not at all clear in his own anxious mind but that the mingling of Daniel with official Barnacleism might produce some explosive combination, even at a marriage breakfast. The national offender, however, lightened him of his uneasiness by coming down to Twickenham to represent that he begged, with the freedom of an old friend, and as a favour to one, that he might not be invited. ‘For,’ said he, ‘as my business with this set of gentlemen was to do a public duty and a public service, and as their business with me was to prevent it by wearing my soul out, I think we had better not eat and drink together with a show of being of one mind.’ Mr Meagles was much amused by his friend’s oddity; and patronised him with a more protecting air of allowance than usual, when he rejoined: ‘Well, well, Dan, you shall have your own crotchety way.’\n\nTo Mr Henry Gowan, as the time approached, Clennam tried to convey by all quiet and unpretending means, that he was frankly and disinterestedly desirous of tendering him any friendship he would accept. Mr Gowan treated him in return with his usual ease, and with his usual show of confidence, which was no confidence at all.\n\n‘You see, Clennam,’ he happened to remark in the course of conversation one day, when they were walking near the Cottage within a week of the marriage, ‘I am a disappointed man. That you know already.’\n\n‘Upon my word,’ said Clennam, a little embarrassed, ‘I scarcely know how.’\n\n‘Why,’ returned Gowan, ‘I belong to a clan, or a clique, or a family, or a connection, or whatever you like to call it, that might have provided for me in any one of fifty ways, and that took it into its head not to do it at all. So here I am, a poor devil of an artist.’\n\nClennam was beginning, ‘But on the other hand--’ when Gowan took him up.\n\n‘Yes, yes, I know. I have the good fortune of being beloved by a beautiful and charming girl whom I love with all my heart.’\n\n[‘Is there much of it?’ Clennam thought. And as he thought it, felt ashamed of himself.)\n\n‘And of finding a father-in-law who is a capital fellow and a liberal good old boy. Still, I had other prospects washed and combed into my childish head when it was washed and combed for me, and I took them to a public school when I washed and combed it for myself, and I am here without them, and thus I am a disappointed man.’\n\nClennam thought (and as he thought it, again felt ashamed of himself), was this notion of being disappointed in life, an assertion of station which the bridegroom brought into the family as his property, having already carried it detrimentally into his pursuit? And was it a hopeful or a promising thing anywhere?\n\n‘Not bitterly disappointed, I think,’ he said aloud.\n\n‘Hang it, no; not bitterly,’ laughed Gowan. ‘My people are not worth that--though they are charming fellows, and I have the greatest affection for them. Besides, it’s pleasant to show them that I can do without them, and that they may all go to the Devil. And besides, again, most men are disappointed in life, somehow or other, and influenced by their disappointment. But it’s a dear good world, and I love it!’\n\n‘It lies fair before you now,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Fair as this summer river,’ cried the other, with enthusiasm, ‘and by Jove I glow with admiration of it, and with ardour to run a race in it. It’s the best of old worlds! And my calling! The best of old callings, isn’t it?’\n\n‘Full of interest and ambition, I conceive,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘And imposition,’ added Gowan, laughing; ‘we won’t leave out the imposition. I hope I may not break down in that; but there, my being a disappointed man may show itself. I may not be able to face it out gravely enough. Between you and me, I think there is some danger of my being just enough soured not to be able to do that.’\n\n‘To do what?’ asked Clennam.\n\n‘To keep it up. To help myself in my turn, as the man before me helps himself in his, and pass the bottle of smoke. To keep up the pretence as to labour, and study, and patience, and being devoted to my art, and giving up many solitary days to it, and abandoning many pleasures for it, and living in it, and all the rest of it--in short, to pass the bottle of smoke according to rule.’\n\n‘But it is well for a man to respect his own vocation, whatever it is; and to think himself bound to uphold it, and to claim for it the respect it deserves; is it not?’ Arthur reasoned. ‘And your vocation, Gowan, may really demand this suit and service. I confess I should have thought that all Art did.’\n\n‘What a good fellow you are, Clennam!’ exclaimed the other, stopping to look at him, as if with irrepressible admiration. ‘What a capital fellow! _You_ have never been disappointed. That’s easy to see.’\n\nIt would have been so cruel if he had meant it, that Clennam firmly resolved to believe he did not mean it. Gowan, without pausing, laid his hand upon his shoulder, and laughingly and lightly went on:\n\n‘Clennam, I don’t like to dispel your generous visions, and I would give any money (if I had any), to live in such a rose-coloured mist. But what I do in my trade, I do to sell. What all we fellows do, we do to sell. If we didn’t want to sell it for the most we can get for it, we shouldn’t do it. Being work, it has to be done; but it’s easily enough done. All the rest is hocus-pocus. Now here’s one of the advantages, or disadvantages, of knowing a disappointed man. You hear the truth.’\n\nWhatever he had heard, and whether it deserved that name or another, it sank into Clennam’s mind. It so took root there, that he began to fear Henry Gowan would always be a trouble to him, and that so far he had gained little or nothing from the dismissal of Nobody, with all his inconsistencies, anxieties, and contradictions. He found a contest still always going on in his breast between his promise to keep Gowan in none but good aspects before the mind of Mr Meagles, and his enforced observation of Gowan in aspects that had no good in them. Nor could he quite support his own conscientious nature against misgivings that he distorted and discoloured himself, by reminding himself that he never sought those discoveries, and that he would have avoided them with willingness and great relief. For he never could forget what he had been; and he knew that he had once disliked Gowan for no better reason than that he had come in his way.\n\nHarassed by these thoughts, he now began to wish the marriage over, Gowan and his young wife gone, and himself left to fulfil his promise, and discharge the generous function he had accepted. This last week was, in truth, an uneasy interval for the whole house. Before Pet, or before Gowan, Mr Meagles was radiant; but Clennam had more than once found him alone, with his view of the scales and scoop much blurred, and had often seen him look after the lovers, in the garden or elsewhere when he was not seen by them, with the old clouded face on which Gowan had fallen like a shadow. In the arrangement of the house for the great occasion, many little reminders of the old travels of the father and mother and daughter had to be disturbed and passed from hand to hand; and sometimes, in the midst of these mute witnesses, to the life they had had together, even Pet herself would yield to lamenting and weeping. Mrs Meagles, the blithest and busiest of mothers, went about singing and cheering everybody; but she, honest soul, had her flights into store rooms, where she would cry until her eyes were red, and would then come out, attributing that appearance to pickled onions and pepper, and singing clearer than ever. Mrs Tickit, finding no balsam for a wounded mind in Buchan’s Domestic Medicine, suffered greatly from low spirits, and from moving recollections of Minnie’s infancy. When the latter was powerful with her, she usually sent up secret messages importing that she was not in parlour condition as to her attire, and that she solicited a sight of ‘her child’ in the kitchen; there, she would bless her child’s face, and bless her child’s heart, and hug her child, in a medley of tears and congratulations, chopping-boards, rolling-pins, and pie-crust, with the tenderness of an old attached servant, which is a very pretty tenderness indeed.\n\nBut all days come that are to be; and the marriage-day was to be, and it came; and with it came all the Barnacles who were bidden to the feast.\n\nThere was Mr Tite Barnacle, from the Circumlocution Office, and Mews Street, Grosvenor Square, with the expensive Mrs Tite Barnacle _nee_ Stiltstalking, who made the Quarter Days so long in coming, and the three expensive Miss Tite Barnacles, double-loaded with accomplishments and ready to go off, and yet not going off with the sharpness of flash and bang that might have been expected, but rather hanging fire. There was Barnacle junior, also from the Circumlocution Office, leaving the Tonnage of the country, which he was somehow supposed to take under his protection, to look after itself, and, sooth to say, not at all impairing the efficiency of its protection by leaving it alone. There was the engaging Young Barnacle, deriving from the sprightly side of the family, also from the Circumlocution Office, gaily and agreeably helping the occasion along, and treating it, in his sparkling way, as one of the official forms and fees of the Church Department of How not to do it. There were three other Young Barnacles from three other offices, insipid to all the senses, and terribly in want of seasoning, doing the marriage as they would have ‘done’ the Nile, Old Rome, the new singer, or Jerusalem.\n\nBut there was greater game than this. There was Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle himself, in the odour of Circumlocution--with the very smell of Despatch-Boxes upon him. Yes, there was Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle, who had risen to official heights on the wings of one indignant idea, and that was, My Lords, that I am yet to be told that it behoves a Minister of this free country to set bounds to the philanthropy, to cramp the charity, to fetter the public spirit, to contract the enterprise, to damp the independent self-reliance, of its people. That was, in other words, that this great statesman was always yet to be told that it behoved the Pilot of the ship to do anything but prosper in the private loaf and fish trade ashore, the crew being able, by dint of hard pumping, to keep the ship above water without him. On this sublime discovery in the great art How not to do it, Lord Decimus had long sustained the highest glory of the Barnacle family; and let any ill-advised member of either House but try How to do it by bringing in a Bill to do it, that Bill was as good as dead and buried when Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle rose up in his place and solemnly said, soaring into indignant majesty as the Circumlocution cheering soared around him, that he was yet to be told, My Lords, that it behoved him as the Minister of this free country, to set bounds to the philanthropy, to cramp the charity, to fetter the public spirit, to contract the enterprise, to damp the independent self-reliance, of its people. The discovery of this Behoving Machine was the discovery of the political perpetual motion. It never wore out, though it was always going round and round in all the State Departments.\n\nAnd there, with his noble friend and relative Lord Decimus, was William Barnacle, who had made the ever-famous coalition with Tudor Stiltstalking, and who always kept ready his own particular recipe for How not to do it; sometimes tapping the Speaker, and drawing it fresh out of him, with a ‘First, I will beg you, sir, to inform the House what Precedent we have for the course into which the honourable gentleman would precipitate us;’ sometimes asking the honourable gentleman to favour him with his own version of the Precedent; sometimes telling the honourable gentleman that he (William Barnacle) would search for a Precedent; and oftentimes crushing the honourable gentleman flat on the spot by telling him there was no Precedent. But Precedent and Precipitate were, under all circumstances, the well-matched pair of battle-horses of this able Circumlocutionist. No matter that the unhappy honourable gentleman had been trying in vain, for twenty-five years, to precipitate William Barnacle into this--William Barnacle still put it to the House, and (at second-hand or so) to the country, whether he was to be precipitated into this. No matter that it was utterly irreconcilable with the nature of things and course of events that the wretched honourable gentleman could possibly produce a Precedent for this--William Barnacle would nevertheless thank the honourable gentleman for that ironical cheer, and would close with him upon that issue, and would tell him to his teeth that there Was NO Precedent for this. It might perhaps have been objected that the William Barnacle wisdom was not high wisdom or the earth it bamboozled would never have been made, or, if made in a rash mistake, would have remained blank mud. But Precedent and Precipitate together frightened all objection out of most people.\n\nAnd there, too, was another Barnacle, a lively one, who had leaped through twenty places in quick succession, and was always in two or three at once, and who was the much-respected inventor of an art which he practised with great success and admiration in all Barnacle Governments. This was, when he was asked a Parliamentary question on any one topic, to return an answer on any other. It had done immense service, and brought him into high esteem with the Circumlocution Office.\n\nAnd there, too, was a sprinkling of less distinguished Parliamentary Barnacles, who had not as yet got anything snug, and were going through their probation to prove their worthiness. These Barnacles perched upon staircases and hid in passages, waiting their orders to make houses or not to make houses; and they did all their hearing, and ohing, and cheering, and barking, under directions from the heads of the family; and they put dummy motions on the paper in the way of other men’s motions; and they stalled disagreeable subjects off until late in the night and late in the session, and then with virtuous patriotism cried out that it was too late; and they went down into the country, whenever they were sent, and swore that Lord Decimus had revived trade from a swoon, and commerce from a fit, and had doubled the harvest of corn, quadrupled the harvest of hay, and prevented no end of gold from flying out of the Bank. Also these Barnacles were dealt, by the heads of the family, like so many cards below the court-cards, to public meetings and dinners; where they bore testimony to all sorts of services on the part of their noble and honourable relatives, and buttered the Barnacles on all sorts of toasts. And they stood, under similar orders, at all sorts of elections; and they turned out of their own seats, on the shortest notice and the most unreasonable terms, to let in other men; and they fetched and carried, and toadied and jobbed, and corrupted, and ate heaps of dirt, and were indefatigable in the public service. And there was not a list, in all the Circumlocution Office, of places that might fall vacant anywhere within half a century, from a lord of the Treasury to a Chinese consul, and up again to a governor-general of India, but as applicants for such places, the names of some or of every one of these hungry and adhesive Barnacles were down.\n\nIt was necessarily but a sprinkling of any class of Barnacles that attended the marriage, for there were not two score in all, and what is that subtracted from Legion! But the sprinkling was a swarm in the Twickenham cottage, and filled it. A Barnacle (assisted by a Barnacle) married the happy pair, and it behoved Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle himself to conduct Mrs Meagles to breakfast.\n\nThe entertainment was not as agreeable and natural as it might have been. Mr Meagles, hove down by his good company while he highly appreciated it, was not himself. Mrs Gowan was herself, and that did not improve him. The fiction that it was not Mr Meagles who had stood in the way, but that it was the Family greatness, and that the Family greatness had made a concession, and there was now a soothing unanimity, pervaded the affair, though it was never openly expressed. Then the Barnacles felt that they for their parts would have done with the Meagleses when the present patronising occasion was over; and the Meagleses felt the same for their parts. Then Gowan asserting his rights as a disappointed man who had his grudge against the family, and who, perhaps, had allowed his mother to have them there, as much in the hope it might give them some annoyance as with any other benevolent object, aired his pencil and his poverty ostentatiously before them, and told them he hoped in time to settle a crust of bread and cheese on his wife, and that he begged such of them as (more fortunate than himself) came in for any good thing, and could buy a picture, to please to remember the poor painter. Then Lord Decimus, who was a wonder on his own Parliamentary pedestal, turned out to be the windiest creature here: proposing happiness to the bride and bridegroom in a series of platitudes that would have made the hair of any sincere disciple and believer stand on end; and trotting, with the complacency of an idiotic elephant, among howling labyrinths of sentences which he seemed to take for high roads, and never so much as wanted to get out of. Then Mr Tite Barnacle could not but feel that there was a person in company, who would have disturbed his life-long sitting to Sir Thomas Lawrence in full official character, if such disturbance had been possible: while Barnacle junior did, with indignation, communicate to two vapid gentlemen, his relatives, that there was a feller here, look here, who had come to our Department without an appointment and said he wanted to know, you know; and that, look here, if he was to break out now, as he might you know (for you never could tell what an ungentlemanly Radical of that sort would be up to next), and was to say, look here, that he wanted to know this moment, you know, that would be jolly; wouldn’t it?\n\nThe pleasantest part of the occasion by far, to Clennam, was the painfullest. When Mr and Mrs Meagles at last hung about Pet in the room with the two pictures (where the company were not), before going with her to the threshold which she could never recross to be the old Pet and the old delight, nothing could be more natural and simple than the three were. Gowan himself was touched, and answered Mr Meagles’s ‘O Gowan, take care of her, take care of her!’ with an earnest ‘Don’t be so broken-hearted, sir. By Heaven I will!’\n\nAnd so, with the last sobs and last loving words, and a last look to Clennam of confidence in his promise, Pet fell back in the carriage, and her husband waved his hand, and they were away for Dover; though not until the faithful Mrs Tickit, in her silk gown and jet black curls, had rushed out from some hiding-place, and thrown both her shoes after the carriage: an apparition which occasioned great surprise to the distinguished company at the windows.\n\nThe said company being now relieved from further attendance, and the chief Barnacles being rather hurried (for they had it in hand just then to send a mail or two which was in danger of going straight to its destination, beating about the seas like the Flying Dutchman, and to arrange with complexity for the stoppage of a good deal of important business otherwise in peril of being done), went their several ways; with all affability conveying to Mr and Mrs Meagles that general assurance that what they had been doing there, they had been doing at a sacrifice for Mr and Mrs Meagles’s good, which they always conveyed to Mr John Bull in their official condescension to that most unfortunate creature.\n\nA miserable blank remained in the house and in the hearts of the father and mother and Clennam. Mr Meagles called only one remembrance to his aid, that really did him good.\n\n‘It’s very gratifying, Arthur,’ he said, ‘after all, to look back upon.’\n\n‘The past?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Yes--but I mean the company.’\n\nIt had made him much more low and unhappy at the time, but now it really did him good. ‘It’s very gratifying,’ he said, often repeating the remark in the course of the evening. ‘Such high company!’\n\n# CHAPTER 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand\n\nIt was at this time that Mr Pancks, in discharge of his compact with Clennam, revealed to him the whole of his gipsy story, and told him Little Dorrit’s fortune. Her father was heir-at-law to a great estate that had long lain unknown of, unclaimed, and accumulating. His right was now clear, nothing interposed in his way, the Marshalsea gates stood open, the Marshalsea walls were down, a few flourishes of his pen, and he was extremely rich.\n\nIn his tracking out of the claim to its complete establishment, Mr Pancks had shown a sagacity that nothing could baffle, and a patience and secrecy that nothing could tire. ‘I little thought, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘when you and I crossed Smithfield that night, and I told you what sort of a Collector I was, that this would come of it. I little thought, sir, when I told you you were not of the Clennams of Cornwall, that I was ever going to tell you who were of the Dorrits of Dorsetshire.’ He then went on to detail. How, having that name recorded in his note-book, he was first attracted by the name alone. How, having often found two exactly similar names, even belonging to the same place, to involve no traceable consanguinity, near or distant, he did not at first give much heed to this, except in the way of speculation as to what a surprising change would be made in the condition of a little seamstress, if she could be shown to have any interest in so large a property. How he rather supposed himself to have pursued the idea into its next degree, because there was something uncommon in the quiet little seamstress, which pleased him and provoked his curiosity. How he had felt his way inch by inch, and ‘Moled it out, sir’ (that was Mr Pancks’s expression), grain by grain. How, in the beginning of the labour described by this new verb, and to render which the more expressive Mr Pancks shut his eyes in pronouncing it and shook his hair over them, he had alternated from sudden lights and hopes to sudden darkness and no hopes, and back again, and back again. How he had made acquaintances in the Prison, expressly that he might come and go there as all other comers and goers did; and how his first ray of light was unconsciously given him by Mr Dorrit himself and by his son; to both of whom he easily became known; with both of whom he talked much, casually [‘but always Moleing you’ll observe,’ said Mr Pancks): and from whom he derived, without being at all suspected, two or three little points of family history which, as he began to hold clues of his own, suggested others. How it had at length become plain to Mr Pancks that he had made a real discovery of the heir-at-law to a great fortune, and that his discovery had but to be ripened to legal fulness and perfection. How he had, thereupon, sworn his landlord, Mr Rugg, to secrecy in a solemn manner, and taken him into Moleing partnership. How they had employed John Chivery as their sole clerk and agent, seeing to whom he was devoted. And how, until the present hour, when authorities mighty in the Bank and learned in the law declared their successful labours ended, they had confided in no other human being.\n\n‘So if the whole thing had broken down, sir,’ concluded Pancks, ‘at the very last, say the day before the other day when I showed you our papers in the Prison yard, or say that very day, nobody but ourselves would have been cruelly disappointed, or a penny the worse.’\n\nClennam, who had been almost incessantly shaking hands with him throughout the narrative, was reminded by this to say, in an amazement which even the preparation he had had for the main disclosure smoothed down, ‘My dear Mr Pancks, this must have cost you a great sum of money.’\n\n‘Pretty well, sir,’ said the triumphant Pancks. ‘No trifle, though we did it as cheap as it could be done. And the outlay was a difficulty, let me tell you.’\n\n‘A difficulty!’ repeated Clennam. ‘But the difficulties you have so wonderfully conquered in the whole business!’ shaking his hand again.\n\n‘I’ll tell you how I did it,’ said the delighted Pancks, putting his hair into a condition as elevated as himself. ‘First, I spent all I had of my own. That wasn’t much.’\n\n‘I am sorry for it,’ said Clennam: ‘not that it matters now, though. Then, what did you do?’\n\n‘Then,’ answered Pancks, ‘I borrowed a sum of my proprietor.’\n\n‘Of Mr Casby?’ said Clennam. ‘He’s a fine old fellow.’\n\n‘Noble old boy; an’t he?’ said Mr Pancks, entering on a series of the dryest snorts. ‘Generous old buck. Confiding old boy. Philanthropic old buck. Benevolent old boy! Twenty per cent. I engaged to pay him, sir. But we never do business for less at our shop.’\n\nArthur felt an awkward consciousness of having, in his exultant condition, been a little premature.\n\n‘I said to that boiling-over old Christian,’ Mr Pancks pursued, appearing greatly to relish this descriptive epithet, ‘that I had got a little project on hand; a hopeful one; I told him a hopeful one; which wanted a certain small capital. I proposed to him to lend me the money on my note. Which he did, at twenty; sticking the twenty on in a business-like way, and putting it into the note, to look like a part of the principal. If I had broken down after that, I should have been his grubber for the next seven years at half wages and double grind. But he’s a perfect Patriarch; and it would do a man good to serve him on such terms--on any terms.’\n\nArthur for his life could not have said with confidence whether Pancks really thought so or not.\n\n‘When that was gone, sir,’ resumed Pancks, ‘and it did go, though I dribbled it out like so much blood, I had taken Mr Rugg into the secret. I proposed to borrow of Mr Rugg (or of Miss Rugg; it’s the same thing; she made a little money by a speculation in the Common Pleas once). He lent it at ten, and thought that pretty high. But Mr Rugg’s a red-haired man, sir, and gets his hair cut. And as to the crown of his hat, it’s high. And as to the brim of his hat, it’s narrow. And there’s no more benevolence bubbling out of him, than out of a ninepin.’\n\n‘Your own recompense for all this, Mr Pancks,’ said Clennam, ‘ought to be a large one.’\n\n‘I don’t mistrust getting it, sir,’ said Pancks. ‘I have made no bargain. I owed you one on that score; now I have paid it. Money out of pocket made good, time fairly allowed for, and Mr Rugg’s bill settled, a thousand pounds would be a fortune to me. That matter I place in your hands. I authorize you now to break all this to the family in any way you think best. Miss Amy Dorrit will be with Mrs Finching this morning. The sooner done the better. Can’t be done too soon.’\n\nThis conversation took place in Clennam’s bed-room, while he was yet in bed. For Mr Pancks had knocked up the house and made his way in, very early in the morning; and, without once sitting down or standing still, had delivered himself of the whole of his details (illustrated with a variety of documents) at the bedside. He now said he would ‘go and look up Mr Rugg’, from whom his excited state of mind appeared to require another back; and bundling up his papers, and exchanging one more hearty shake of the hand with Clennam, he went at full speed down-stairs, and steamed off.\n\nClennam, of course, resolved to go direct to Mr Casby’s. He dressed and got out so quickly that he found himself at the corner of the patriarchal street nearly an hour before her time; but he was not sorry to have the opportunity of calming himself with a leisurely walk.\n\nWhen he returned to the street, and had knocked at the bright brass knocker, he was informed that she had come, and was shown up-stairs to Flora’s breakfast-room. Little Dorrit was not there herself, but Flora was, and testified the greatest amazement at seeing him.\n\n‘Good gracious, Arthur--Doyce and Clennam!’ cried that lady, ‘who would have ever thought of seeing such a sight as this and pray excuse a wrapper for upon my word I really never and a faded check too which is worse but our little friend is making me, not that I need mind mentioning it to you for you must know that there are such things a skirt, and having arranged that a trying on should take place after breakfast is the reason though I wish not so badly starched.’\n\n‘I ought to make an apology,’ said Arthur, ‘for so early and abrupt a visit; but you will excuse it when I tell you the cause.’\n\n‘In times for ever fled Arthur,’ returned Mrs Finching, ‘pray excuse me Doyce and Clennam infinitely more correct and though unquestionably distant still ‘tis distance lends enchantment to the view, at least I don’t mean that and if I did I suppose it would depend considerably on the nature of the view, but I’m running on again and you put it all out of my head.’\n\nShe glanced at him tenderly, and resumed:\n\n‘In times for ever fled I was going to say it would have sounded strange indeed for Arthur Clennam--Doyce and Clennam naturally quite different--to make apologies for coming here at any time, but that is past and what is past can never be recalled except in his own case as poor Mr F. said when he was in spirits Cucumber and therefore never ate it.’\n\nShe was making the tea when Arthur came in, and now hastily finished that operation.\n\n‘Papa,’ she said, all mystery and whisper, as she shut down the tea-pot lid, ‘is sitting prosingly breaking his new laid egg in the back parlour over the City article exactly like the Woodpecker Tapping and need never know that you are here, and our little friend you are well aware may be fully trusted when she comes down from cutting out on the large table overhead.’\n\nArthur then told her, in the fewest words, that it was their little friend he came to see; and what he had to announce to their little friend. At which astounding intelligence, Flora clasped her hands, fell into a tremble, and shed tears of sympathy and pleasure, like the good-natured creature she really was.\n\n‘For gracious sake let me get out of the way first,’ said Flora, putting her hands to her ears and moving towards the door, ‘or I know I shall go off dead and screaming and make everybody worse, and the dear little thing only this morning looking so nice and neat and good and yet so poor and now a fortune is she really and deserves it too! and might I mention it to Mr F.’s Aunt Arthur not Doyce and Clennam for this once or if objectionable not on any account.’\n\nArthur nodded his free permission, since Flora shut out all verbal communication. Flora nodded in return to thank him, and hurried out of the room.\n\nLittle Dorrit’s step was already on the stairs, and in another moment she was at the door. Do what he could to compose his face, he could not convey so much of an ordinary expression into it, but that the moment she saw it she dropped her work, and cried, ‘Mr Clennam! What’s the matter?’\n\n‘Nothing, nothing. That is, no misfortune has happened. I have come to tell you something, but it is a piece of great good-fortune.’\n\n‘Good-fortune?’\n\n‘Wonderful fortune!’\n\nThey stood in a window, and her eyes, full of light, were fixed upon his face. He put an arm about her, seeing her likely to sink down. She put a hand upon that arm, partly to rest upon it, and partly so to preserve their relative positions as that her intent look at him should be shaken by no change of attitude in either of them. Her lips seemed to repeat ‘Wonderful fortune?’ He repeated it again, aloud.\n\n‘Dear Little Dorrit! Your father.’\n\nThe ice of the pale face broke at the word, and little lights and shoots of expression passed all over it. They were all expressions of pain. Her breath was faint and hurried. Her heart beat fast. He would have clasped the little figure closer, but he saw that the eyes appealed to him not to be moved.\n\n‘Your father can be free within this week. He does not know it; we must go to him from here, to tell him of it. Your father will be free within a few days. Your father will be free within a few hours. Remember we must go to him from here, to tell him of it!’\n\nThat brought her back. Her eyes were closing, but they opened again.\n\n‘This is not all the good-fortune. This is not all the wonderful good-fortune, my dear Little Dorrit. Shall I tell you more?’\n\nHer lips shaped ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Your father will be no beggar when he is free. He will want for nothing. Shall I tell you more? Remember! He knows nothing of it; we must go to him, from here, to tell him of it!’\n\nShe seemed to entreat him for a little time. He held her in his arm, and, after a pause, bent down his ear to listen.\n\n‘Did you ask me to go on?’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘He will be a rich man. He is a rich man. A great sum of money is waiting to be paid over to him as his inheritance; you are all henceforth very wealthy. Bravest and best of children, I thank Heaven that you are rewarded!’\n\nAs he kissed her, she turned her head towards his shoulder, and raised her arm towards his neck; cried out ‘Father! Father! Father!’ and swooned away.\n\nUpon which Flora returned to take care of her, and hovered about her on a sofa, intermingling kind offices and incoherent scraps of conversation in a manner so confounding, that whether she pressed the Marshalsea to take a spoonful of unclaimed dividends, for it would do her good; or whether she congratulated Little Dorrit’s father on coming into possession of a hundred thousand smelling-bottles; or whether she explained that she put seventy-five thousand drops of spirits of lavender on fifty thousand pounds of lump sugar, and that she entreated Little Dorrit to take that gentle restorative; or whether she bathed the foreheads of Doyce and Clennam in vinegar, and gave the late Mr F. more air; no one with any sense of responsibility could have undertaken to decide. A tributary stream of confusion, moreover, poured in from an adjoining bedroom, where Mr F.’s Aunt appeared, from the sound of her voice, to be in a horizontal posture, awaiting her breakfast; and from which bower that inexorable lady snapped off short taunts, whenever she could get a hearing, as, ‘Don’t believe it’s his doing!’ and ‘He needn’t take no credit to himself for it!’ and ‘It’ll be long enough, I expect, afore he’ll give up any of his own money!’ all designed to disparage Clennam’s share in the discovery, and to relieve those inveterate feelings with which Mr F.’s Aunt regarded him.\n\nBut Little Dorrit’s solicitude to get to her father, and to carry the joyful tidings to him, and not to leave him in his jail a moment with this happiness in store for him and still unknown to him, did more for her speedy restoration than all the skill and attention on earth could have done. ‘Come with me to my dear father. Pray come and tell my dear father!’ were the first words she said. Her father, her father. She spoke of nothing but him, thought of nothing but him. Kneeling down and pouring out her thankfulness with uplifted hands, her thanks were for her father.\n\nFlora’s tenderness was quite overcome by this, and she launched out among the cups and saucers into a wonderful flow of tears and speech.\n\n‘I declare,’ she sobbed, ‘I never was so cut up since your mama and my papa not Doyce and Clennam for this once but give the precious little thing a cup of tea and make her put it to her lips at least pray Arthur do, not even Mr F.’s last illness for that was of another kind and gout is not a child’s affection though very painful for all parties and Mr F. a martyr with his leg upon a rest and the wine trade in itself inflammatory for they will do it more or less among themselves and who can wonder, it seems like a dream I am sure to think of nothing at all this morning and now Mines of money is it really, but you must know my darling love because you never will be strong enough to tell him all about it upon teaspoons, mightn’t it be even best to try the directions of my own medical man for though the flavour is anything but agreeable still I force myself to do it as a prescription and find the benefit, you’d rather not why no my dear I’d rather not but still I do it as a duty, everybody will congratulate you some in earnest and some not and many will congratulate you with all their hearts but none more so I do assure you from the bottom of my own I do myself though sensible of blundering and being stupid, and will be judged by Arthur not Doyce and Clennam for this once so good-bye darling and God bless you and may you be very happy and excuse the liberty, vowing that the dress shall never be finished by anybody else but shall be laid by for a keepsake just as it is and called Little Dorrit though why that strangest of denominations at any time I never did myself and now I never shall!’\n\nThus Flora, in taking leave of her favourite. Little Dorrit thanked her, and embraced her, over and over again; and finally came out of the house with Clennam, and took coach for the Marshalsea.\n\nIt was a strangely unreal ride through the old squalid streets, with a sensation of being raised out of them into an airy world of wealth and grandeur. When Arthur told her that she would soon ride in her own carriage through very different scenes, when all the familiar experiences would have vanished away, she looked frightened. But when he substituted her father for herself, and told her how he would ride in his carriage, and how great and grand he would be, her tears of joy and innocent pride fell fast. Seeing that the happiness her mind could realise was all shining upon him, Arthur kept that single figure before her; and so they rode brightly through the poor streets in the prison neighbourhood to carry him the great news.\n\nWhen Mr Chivery, who was on duty, admitted them into the Lodge, he saw something in their faces which filled him with astonishment. He stood looking after them, when they hurried into the prison, as though he perceived that they had come back accompanied by a ghost a-piece. Two or three Collegians whom they passed, looked after them too, and presently joining Mr Chivery, formed a little group on the Lodge steps, in the midst of which there spontaneously originated a whisper that the Father was going to get his discharge. Within a few minutes, it was heard in the remotest room in the College.\n\nLittle Dorrit opened the door from without, and they both entered. He was sitting in his old grey gown and his old black cap, in the sunlight by the window, reading his newspaper. His glasses were in his hand, and he had just looked round; surprised at first, no doubt, by her step upon the stairs, not expecting her until night; surprised again, by seeing Arthur Clennam in her company. As they came in, the same unwonted look in both of them which had already caught attention in the yard below, struck him. He did not rise or speak, but laid down his glasses and his newspaper on the table beside him, and looked at them with his mouth a little open and his lips trembling. When Arthur put out his hand, he touched it, but not with his usual state; and then he turned to his daughter, who had sat down close beside him with her hands upon his shoulder, and looked attentively in her face.\n\n‘Father! I have been made so happy this morning!’\n\n‘You have been made so happy, my dear?’\n\n‘By Mr Clennam, father. He brought me such joyful and wonderful intelligence about you! If he had not with his great kindness and gentleness, prepared me for it, father--prepared me for it, father--I think I could not have borne it.’\n\nHer agitation was exceedingly great, and the tears rolled down her face. He put his hand suddenly to his heart, and looked at Clennam.\n\n‘Compose yourself, sir,’ said Clennam, ‘and take a little time to think. To think of the brightest and most fortunate accidents of life. We have all heard of great surprises of joy. They are not at an end, sir. They are rare, but not at an end.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam? Not at an end? Not at an end for--’ He touched himself upon the breast, instead of saying ‘me.’\n\n‘No,’ returned Clennam.\n\n‘What surprise,’ he asked, keeping his left hand over his heart, and there stopping in his speech, while with his right hand he put his glasses exactly level on the table: ‘what such surprise can be in store for me?’\n\n‘Let me answer with another question. Tell me, Mr Dorrit, what surprise would be the most unlooked for and the most acceptable to you. Do not be afraid to imagine it, or to say what it would be.’\n\nHe looked steadfastly at Clennam, and, so looking at him, seemed to change into a very old haggard man. The sun was bright upon the wall beyond the window, and on the spikes at top. He slowly stretched out the hand that had been upon his heart, and pointed at the wall.\n\n‘It is down,’ said Clennam. ‘Gone!’\n\nHe remained in the same attitude, looking steadfastly at him.\n\n‘And in its place,’ said Clennam, slowly and distinctly, ‘are the means to possess and enjoy the utmost that they have so long shut out. Mr Dorrit, there is not the smallest doubt that within a few days you will be free, and highly prosperous. I congratulate you with all my soul on this change of fortune, and on the happy future into which you are soon to carry the treasure you have been blest with here--the best of all the riches you can have elsewhere--the treasure at your side.’\n\nWith those words, he pressed his hand and released it; and his daughter, laying her face against his, encircled him in the hour of his prosperity with her arms, as she had in the long years of his adversity encircled him with her love and toil and truth; and poured out her full heart in gratitude, hope, joy, blissful ecstasy, and all for him.\n\n‘I shall see him as I never saw him yet. I shall see my dear love, with the dark cloud cleared away. I shall see him, as my poor mother saw him long ago. O my dear, my dear! O father, father! O thank God, thank God!’\n\nHe yielded himself to her kisses and caresses, but did not return them, except that he put an arm about her. Neither did he say one word. His steadfast look was now divided between her and Clennam, and he began to shake as if he were very cold. Explaining to Little Dorrit that he would run to the coffee-house for a bottle of wine, Arthur fetched it with all the haste he could use. While it was being brought from the cellar to the bar, a number of excited people asked him what had happened; when he hurriedly informed them that Mr Dorrit had succeeded to a fortune.\n\nOn coming back with the wine in his hand, he found that she had placed her father in his easy chair, and had loosened his shirt and neckcloth. They filled a tumbler with wine, and held it to his lips. When he had swallowed a little, he took the glass himself and emptied it. Soon after that, he leaned back in his chair and cried, with his handkerchief before his face.\n\nAfter this had lasted a while Clennam thought it a good season for diverting his attention from the main surprise, by relating its details. Slowly, therefore, and in a quiet tone of voice, he explained them as best he could, and enlarged on the nature of Pancks’s service.\n\n‘He shall be--ha--he shall be handsomely recompensed, sir,’ said the Father, starting up and moving hurriedly about the room. ‘Assure yourself, Mr Clennam, that everybody concerned shall be--ha--shall be nobly rewarded. No one, my dear sir, shall say that he has an unsatisfied claim against me. I shall repay the--hum--the advances I have had from you, sir, with peculiar pleasure. I beg to be informed at your earliest convenience, what advances you have made my son.’\n\nHe had no purpose in going about the room, but he was not still a moment.\n\n‘Everybody,’ he said, ‘shall be remembered. I will not go away from here in anybody’s debt. All the people who have been--ha--well behaved towards myself and my family, shall be rewarded. Chivery shall be rewarded. Young John shall be rewarded. I particularly wish, and intend, to act munificently, Mr Clennam.’\n\n‘Will you allow me,’ said Arthur, laying his purse on the table, ‘to supply any present contingencies, Mr Dorrit? I thought it best to bring a sum of money for the purpose.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, thank you. I accept with readiness, at the present moment, what I could not an hour ago have conscientiously taken. I am obliged to you for the temporary accommodation. Exceedingly temporary, but well timed--well timed.’ His hand had closed upon the money, and he carried it about with him. ‘Be so kind, sir, as to add the amount to those former advances to which I have already referred; being careful, if you please, not to omit advances made to my son. A mere verbal statement of the gross amount is all I shall--ha--all I shall require.’\n\nHis eye fell upon his daughter at this point, and he stopped for a moment to kiss her, and to pat her head.\n\n‘It will be necessary to find a milliner, my love, and to make a speedy and complete change in your very plain dress. Something must be done with Maggy too, who at present is--ha--barely respectable, barely respectable. And your sister, Amy, and your brother. And _my_ brother, your uncle--poor soul, I trust this will rouse him--messengers must be despatched to fetch them. They must be informed of this. We must break it to them cautiously, but they must be informed directly. We owe it as a duty to them and to ourselves, from this moment, not to let them--hum--not to let them do anything.’\n\nThis was the first intimation he had ever given, that he was privy to the fact that they did something for a livelihood.\n\nHe was still jogging about the room, with the purse clutched in his hand, when a great cheering arose in the yard. ‘The news has spread already,’ said Clennam, looking down from the window. ‘Will you show yourself to them, Mr Dorrit? They are very earnest, and they evidently wish it.’\n\n‘I--hum--ha--I confess I could have desired, Amy my dear,’ he said, jogging about in a more feverish flutter than before, ‘to have made some change in my dress first, and to have bought a--hum--a watch and chain. But if it must be done as it is, it--ha--it must be done. Fasten the collar of my shirt, my dear. Mr Clennam, would you oblige me--hum--with a blue neckcloth you will find in that drawer at your elbow. Button my coat across at the chest, my love. It looks--ha--it looks broader, buttoned.’\n\nWith his trembling hand he pushed his grey hair up, and then, taking Clennam and his daughter for supporters, appeared at the window leaning on an arm of each. The Collegians cheered him very heartily, and he kissed his hand to them with great urbanity and protection. When he withdrew into the room again, he said ‘Poor creatures!’ in a tone of much pity for their miserable condition.\n\nLittle Dorrit was deeply anxious that he should lie down to compose himself. On Arthur’s speaking to her of his going to inform Pancks that he might now appear as soon as he would, and pursue the joyful business to its close, she entreated him in a whisper to stay with her until her father should be quite calm and at rest. He needed no second entreaty; and she prepared her father’s bed, and begged him to lie down. For another half-hour or more he would be persuaded to do nothing but go about the room, discussing with himself the probabilities for and against the Marshal’s allowing the whole of the prisoners to go to the windows of the official residence which commanded the street, to see himself and family depart for ever in a carriage--which, he said, he thought would be a Sight for them. But gradually he began to droop and tire, and at last stretched himself upon the bed.\n\nShe took her faithful place beside him, fanning him and cooling his forehead; and he seemed to be falling asleep (always with the money in his hand), when he unexpectedly sat up and said:\n\n‘Mr Clennam, I beg your pardon. Am I to understand, my dear sir, that I could--ha--could pass through the Lodge at this moment, and--hum--take a walk?’\n\n‘I think not, Mr Dorrit,’ was the unwilling reply. ‘There are certain forms to be completed; and although your detention here is now in itself a form, I fear it is one that for a little longer has to be observed too.’\n\nAt this he shed tears again.\n\n‘It is but a few hours, sir,’ Clennam cheerfully urged upon him.\n\n‘A few hours, sir,’ he returned in a sudden passion. ‘You talk very easily of hours, sir! How long do you suppose, sir, that an hour is to a man who is choking for want of air?’\n\nIt was his last demonstration for that time; as, after shedding some more tears and querulously complaining that he couldn’t breathe, he slowly fell into a slumber. Clennam had abundant occupation for his thoughts, as he sat in the quiet room watching the father on his bed, and the daughter fanning his face.\n\nLittle Dorrit had been thinking too. After softly putting his grey hair aside, and touching his forehead with her lips, she looked towards Arthur, who came nearer to her, and pursued in a low whisper the subject of her thoughts.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, will he pay all his debts before he leaves here?’\n\n‘No doubt. All.’\n\n‘All the debts for which he had been imprisoned here, all my life and longer?’\n\n‘No doubt.’\n\nThere was something of uncertainty and remonstrance in her look; something that was not all satisfaction. He wondered to detect it, and said:\n\n‘You are glad that he should do so?’\n\n‘Are you?’ asked Little Dorrit, wistfully.\n\n‘Am I? Most heartily glad!’\n\n‘Then I know I ought to be.’\n\n‘And are you not?’\n\n‘It seems to me hard,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that he should have lost so many years and suffered so much, and at last pay all the debts as well. It seems to me hard that he should pay in life and money both.’\n\n‘My dear child--’ Clennam was beginning.\n\n‘Yes, I know I am wrong,’ she pleaded timidly, ‘don’t think any worse of me; it has grown up with me here.’\n\nThe prison, which could spoil so many things, had tainted Little Dorrit’s mind no more than this. Engendered as the confusion was, in compassion for the poor prisoner, her father, it was the first speck Clennam had ever seen, it was the last speck Clennam ever saw, of the prison atmosphere upon her.\n\nHe thought this, and forbore to say another word. With the thought, her purity and goodness came before him in their brightest light. The little spot made them the more beautiful.\n\nWorn out with her own emotions, and yielding to the silence of the room, her hand slowly slackened and failed in its fanning movement, and her head dropped down on the pillow at her father’s side. Clennam rose softly, opened and closed the door without a sound, and passed from the prison, carrying the quiet with him into the turbulent streets.\n\n# CHAPTER 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan\n\nAnd now the day arrived when Mr Dorrit and his family were to leave the prison for ever, and the stones of its much-trodden pavement were to know them no more.\n\nThe interval had been short, but he had greatly complained of its length, and had been imperious with Mr Rugg touching the delay. He had been high with Mr Rugg, and had threatened to employ some one else. He had requested Mr Rugg not to presume upon the place in which he found him, but to do his duty, sir, and to do it with promptitude. He had told Mr Rugg that he knew what lawyers and agents were, and that he would not submit to imposition. On that gentleman’s humbly representing that he exerted himself to the utmost, Miss Fanny was very short with him; desiring to know what less he could do, when he had been told a dozen times that money was no object, and expressing her suspicion that he forgot whom he talked to.\n\nTowards the Marshal, who was a Marshal of many years’ standing, and with whom he had never had any previous difference, Mr Dorrit comported himself with severity. That officer, on personally tendering his congratulations, offered the free use of two rooms in his house for Mr Dorrit’s occupation until his departure. Mr Dorrit thanked him at the moment, and replied that he would think of it; but the Marshal was no sooner gone than he sat down and wrote him a cutting note, in which he remarked that he had never on any former occasion had the honour of receiving his congratulations (which was true, though indeed there had not been anything particular to congratulate him upon), and that he begged, on behalf of himself and family, to repudiate the Marshal’s offer, with all those thanks which its disinterested character and its perfect independence of all worldly considerations demanded.\n\nAlthough his brother showed so dim a glimmering of interest in their altered fortunes that it was very doubtful whether he understood them, Mr Dorrit caused him to be measured for new raiment by the hosiers, tailors, hatters, and bootmakers whom he called in for himself; and ordered that his old clothes should be taken from him and burned. Miss Fanny and Mr Tip required no direction in making an appearance of great fashion and elegance; and the three passed this interval together at the best hotel in the neighbourhood--though truly, as Miss Fanny said, the best was very indifferent. In connection with that establishment, Mr Tip hired a cabriolet, horse, and groom, a very neat turn out, which was usually to be observed for two or three hours at a time gracing the Borough High Street, outside the Marshalsea court-yard. A modest little hired chariot and pair was also frequently to be seen there; in alighting from and entering which vehicle, Miss Fanny fluttered the Marshal’s daughters by the display of inaccessible bonnets.\n\nA great deal of business was transacted in this short period. Among other items, Messrs Peddle and Pool, solicitors, of Monument Yard, were instructed by their client Edward Dorrit, Esquire, to address a letter to Mr Arthur Clennam, enclosing the sum of twenty-four pounds nine shillings and eightpence, being the amount of principal and interest computed at the rate of five per cent. per annum, in which their client believed himself to be indebted to Mr Clennam. In making this communication and remittance, Messrs Peddle and Pool were further instructed by their client to remind Mr Clennam that the favour of the advance now repaid (including gate-fees) had not been asked of him, and to inform him that it would not have been accepted if it had been openly proffered in his name. With which they requested a stamped receipt, and remained his obedient servants. A great deal of business had likewise to be done, within the so-soon-to-be-orphaned Marshalsea, by Mr Dorrit so long its Father, chiefly arising out of applications made to him by Collegians for small sums of money. To these he responded with the greatest liberality, and with no lack of formality; always first writing to appoint a time at which the applicant might wait upon him in his room, and then receiving him in the midst of a vast accumulation of documents, and accompanying his donation (for he said in every such case, ‘it is a donation, not a loan’) with a great deal of good counsel: to the effect that he, the expiring Father of the Marshalsea, hoped to be long remembered, as an example that a man might preserve his own and the general respect even there.\n\nThe Collegians were not envious. Besides that they had a personal and traditional regard for a Collegian of so many years’ standing, the event was creditable to the College, and made it famous in the newspapers. Perhaps more of them thought, too, than were quite aware of it, that the thing might in the lottery of chances have happened to themselves, or that something of the sort might yet happen to themselves some day or other. They took it very well. A few were low at the thought of being left behind, and being left poor; but even these did not grudge the family their brilliant reverse. There might have been much more envy in politer places. It seems probable that mediocrity of fortune would have been disposed to be less magnanimous than the Collegians, who lived from hand to mouth--from the pawnbroker’s hand to the day’s dinner.\n\nThey got up an address to him, which they presented in a neat frame and glass (though it was not afterwards displayed in the family mansion or preserved among the family papers); and to which he returned a gracious answer. In that document he assured them, in a Royal manner, that he received the profession of their attachment with a full conviction of its sincerity; and again generally exhorted them to follow his example--which, at least in so far as coming into a great property was concerned, there is no doubt they would have gladly imitated. He took the same occasion of inviting them to a comprehensive entertainment, to be given to the whole College in the yard, and at which he signified he would have the honour of taking a parting glass to the health and happiness of all those whom he was about to leave behind.\n\nHe did not in person dine at this public repast (it took place at two in the afternoon, and his dinners now came in from the hotel at six), but his son was so good as to take the head of the principal table, and to be very free and engaging. He himself went about among the company, and took notice of individuals, and saw that the viands were of the quality he had ordered, and that all were served. On the whole, he was like a baron of the olden time in a rare good humour. At the conclusion of the repast, he pledged his guests in a bumper of old Madeira; and told them that he hoped they had enjoyed themselves, and what was more, that they would enjoy themselves for the rest of the evening; that he wished them well; and that he bade them welcome. His health being drunk with acclamations, he was not so baronial after all but that in trying to return thanks he broke down, in the manner of a mere serf with a heart in his breast, and wept before them all. After this great success, which he supposed to be a failure, he gave them ‘Mr Chivery and his brother officers;’ whom he had beforehand presented with ten pounds each, and who were all in attendance. Mr Chivery spoke to the toast, saying, What you undertake to lock up, lock up; but remember that you are, in the words of the fettered African, a man and a brother ever. The list of toasts disposed of, Mr Dorrit urbanely went through the motions of playing a game of skittles with the Collegian who was the next oldest inhabitant to himself; and left the tenantry to their diversions.\n\nBut all these occurrences preceded the final day. And now the day arrived when he and his family were to leave the prison for ever, and when the stones of its much-trodden pavement were to know them no more.\n\nNoon was the hour appointed for the departure. As it approached, there was not a Collegian within doors, nor a turnkey absent. The latter class of gentlemen appeared in their Sunday clothes, and the greater part of the Collegians were brightened up as much as circumstances allowed. Two or three flags were even displayed, and the children put on odds and ends of ribbon. Mr Dorrit himself, at this trying time, preserved a serious but graceful dignity. Much of his great attention was given to his brother, as to whose bearing on the great occasion he felt anxious.\n\n‘My dear Frederick,’ said he, ‘if you will give me your arm we will pass among our friends together. I think it is right that we should go out arm in arm, my dear Frederick.’\n\n‘Hah!’ said Frederick. ‘Yes, yes, yes, yes.’\n\n‘And if, my dear Frederick--if you could, without putting any great constraint upon yourself, throw a little (pray excuse me, Frederick), a little polish into your usual demeanour--’\n\n‘William, William,’ said the other, shaking his head, ‘it’s for you to do all that. I don’t know how. All forgotten, forgotten!’\n\n‘But, my dear fellow,’ returned William, ‘for that very reason, if for no other, you must positively try to rouse yourself. What you have forgotten you must now begin to recall, my dear Frederick. Your position--’\n\n‘Eh?’ said Frederick.\n\n‘Your position, my dear Frederick.’\n\n‘Mine?’ He looked first at his own figure, and then at his brother’s, and then, drawing a long breath, cried, ‘Hah, to be sure! Yes, yes, yes.’\n\n‘Your position, my dear Frederick, is now a fine one. Your position, as my brother, is a very fine one. And I know that it belongs to your conscientious nature to try to become worthy of it, my dear Frederick, and to try to adorn it. To be no discredit to it, but to adorn it.’\n\n‘William,’ said the other weakly, and with a sigh, ‘I will do anything you wish, my brother, provided it lies in my power. Pray be so kind as to recollect what a limited power mine is. What would you wish me to do to-day, brother? Say what it is, only say what it is.’\n\n‘My dearest Frederick, nothing. It is not worth troubling so good a heart as yours with.’\n\n‘Pray trouble it,’ returned the other. ‘It finds it no trouble, William, to do anything it can for you.’\n\nWilliam passed his hand across his eyes, and murmured with august satisfaction, ‘Blessings on your attachment, my poor dear fellow!’ Then he said aloud, ‘Well, my dear Frederick, if you will only try, as we walk out, to show that you are alive to the occasion--that you think about it--’\n\n‘What would you advise me to think about it?’ returned his submissive brother.\n\n‘Oh! my dear Frederick, how can I answer you? I can only say what, in leaving these good people, I think myself.’\n\n‘That’s it!’ cried his brother. ‘That will help me.’\n\n‘I find that I think, my dear Frederick, and with mixed emotions in which a softened compassion predominates, What will they do without me!’\n\n‘True,’ returned his brother. ‘Yes, yes, yes, yes. I’ll think that as we go, What will they do without my brother! Poor things! What will they do without him!’\n\nTwelve o’clock having just struck, and the carriage being reported ready in the outer court-yard, the brothers proceeded down-stairs arm-in-arm. Edward Dorrit, Esquire (once Tip), and his sister Fanny followed, also arm-in-arm; Mr Plornish and Maggy, to whom had been entrusted the removal of such of the family effects as were considered worth removing, followed, bearing bundles and burdens to be packed in a cart.\n\nIn the yard, were the Collegians and turnkeys. In the yard, were Mr Pancks and Mr Rugg, come to see the last touch given to their work. In the yard, was Young John making a new epitaph for himself, on the occasion of his dying of a broken heart. In the yard, was the Patriarchal Casby, looking so tremendously benevolent that many enthusiastic Collegians grasped him fervently by the hand, and the wives and female relatives of many more Collegians kissed his hand, nothing doubting that he had done it all. In the yard, was the man with the shadowy grievance respecting the Fund which the Marshal embezzled, who had got up at five in the morning to complete the copying of a perfectly unintelligible history of that transaction, which he had committed to Mr Dorrit’s care, as a document of the last importance, calculated to stun the Government and effect the Marshal’s downfall. In the yard, was the insolvent whose utmost energies were always set on getting into debt, who broke into prison with as much pains as other men have broken out of it, and who was always being cleared and complimented; while the insolvent at his elbow--a mere little, snivelling, striving tradesman, half dead of anxious efforts to keep out of debt--found it a hard matter, indeed, to get a Commissioner to release him with much reproof and reproach. In the yard, was the man of many children and many burdens, whose failure astonished everybody; in the yard, was the man of no children and large resources, whose failure astonished nobody. There, were the people who were always going out to-morrow, and always putting it off; there, were the people who had come in yesterday, and who were much more jealous and resentful of this freak of fortune than the seasoned birds. There, were some who, in pure meanness of spirit, cringed and bowed before the enriched Collegian and his family; there, were others who did so really because their eyes, accustomed to the gloom of their imprisonment and poverty, could not support the light of such bright sunshine. There, were many whose shillings had gone into his pocket to buy him meat and drink; but none who were now obtrusively Hail fellow well met! with him, on the strength of that assistance. It was rather to be remarked of the caged birds, that they were a little shy of the bird about to be so grandly free, and that they had a tendency to withdraw themselves towards the bars, and seem a little fluttered as he passed.\n\nThrough these spectators the little procession, headed by the two brothers, moved slowly to the gate. Mr Dorrit, yielding to the vast speculation how the poor creatures were to get on without him, was great, and sad, but not absorbed. He patted children on the head like Sir Roger de Coverley going to church, he spoke to people in the background by their Christian names, he condescended to all present, and seemed for their consolation to walk encircled by the legend in golden characters, ‘Be comforted, my people! Bear it!’\n\nAt last three honest cheers announced that he had passed the gate, and that the Marshalsea was an orphan. Before they had ceased to ring in the echoes of the prison walls, the family had got into their carriage, and the attendant had the steps in his hand.\n\nThen, and not before, ‘Good Gracious!’ cried Miss Fanny all at once, ‘Where’s Amy!’\n\nHer father had thought she was with her sister. Her sister had thought she was ‘somewhere or other.’ They had all trusted to finding her, as they had always done, quietly in the right place at the right moment. This going away was perhaps the very first action of their joint lives that they had got through without her.\n\nA minute might have been consumed in the ascertaining of these points, when Miss Fanny, who, from her seat in the carriage, commanded the long narrow passage leading to the Lodge, flushed indignantly.\n\n‘Now I do say, Pa,’ cried she, ‘that this is disgraceful!’\n\n‘What is disgraceful, Fanny?’\n\n‘I do say,’ she repeated, ‘this is perfectly infamous! Really almost enough, even at such a time as this, to make one wish one was dead! Here is that child Amy, in her ugly old shabby dress, which she was so obstinate about, Pa, which I over and over again begged and prayed her to change, and which she over and over again objected to, and promised to change to-day, saying she wished to wear it as long as ever she remained in there with you--which was absolutely romantic nonsense of the lowest kind--here is that child Amy disgracing us to the last moment and at the last moment, by being carried out in that dress after all. And by that Mr Clennam too!’\n\nThe offence was proved, as she delivered the indictment. Clennam appeared at the carriage-door, bearing the little insensible figure in his arms.\n\n‘She has been forgotten,’ he said, in a tone of pity not free from reproach. ‘I ran up to her room (which Mr Chivery showed me) and found the door open, and that she had fainted on the floor, dear child. She appeared to have gone to change her dress, and to have sunk down overpowered. It may have been the cheering, or it may have happened sooner. Take care of this poor cold hand, Miss Dorrit. Don’t let it fall.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ returned Miss Dorrit, bursting into tears. ‘I believe I know what to do, if you will give me leave. Dear Amy, open your eyes, that’s a love! Oh, Amy, Amy, I really am so vexed and ashamed! Do rouse yourself, darling! Oh, why are they not driving on! Pray, Pa, do drive on!’\n\nThe attendant, getting between Clennam and the carriage-door, with a sharp ‘By your leave, sir!’ bundled up the steps, and they drove away.\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Fellow Travellers\n\nIn the autumn of the year, Darkness and Night were creeping up to the highest ridges of the Alps.\n\nIt was vintage time in the valleys on the Swiss side of the Pass of the Great Saint Bernard, and along the banks of the Lake of Geneva. The air there was charged with the scent of gathered grapes. Baskets, troughs, and tubs of grapes stood in the dim village doorways, stopped the steep and narrow village streets, and had been carrying all day along the roads and lanes. Grapes, split and crushed under foot, lay about everywhere. The child carried in a sling by the laden peasant woman toiling home, was quieted with picked-up grapes; the idiot sunning his big goitre under the leaves of the wooden chalet by the way to the Waterfall, sat munching grapes; the breath of the cows and goats was redolent of leaves and stalks of grapes; the company in every little cabaret were eating, drinking, talking grapes. A pity that no ripe touch of this generous abundance could be given to the thin, hard, stony wine, which after all was made from the grapes!\n\nThe air had been warm and transparent through the whole of the bright day. Shining metal spires and church-roofs, distant and rarely seen, had sparkled in the view; and the snowy mountain-tops had been so clear that unaccustomed eyes, cancelling the intervening country, and slighting their rugged heights for something fabulous, would have measured them as within a few hours easy reach. Mountain-peaks of great celebrity in the valleys, whence no trace of their existence was visible sometimes for months together, had been since morning plain and near in the blue sky. And now, when it was dark below, though they seemed solemnly to recede, like spectres who were going to vanish, as the red dye of the sunset faded out of them and left them coldly white, they were yet distinctly defined in their loneliness above the mists and shadows.\n\nSeen from these solitudes, and from the Pass of the Great Saint Bernard, which was one of them, the ascending Night came up the mountain like a rising water. When it at last rose to the walls of the convent of the Great Saint Bernard, it was as if that weather-beaten structure were another Ark, and floated on the shadowy waves.\n\nDarkness, outstripping some visitors on mules, had risen thus to the rough convent walls, when those travellers were yet climbing the mountain. As the heat of the glowing day when they had stopped to drink at the streams of melted ice and snow, was changed to the searching cold of the frosty rarefied night air at a great height, so the fresh beauty of the lower journey had yielded to barrenness and desolation. A craggy track, up which the mules in single file scrambled and turned from block to block, as though they were ascending the broken staircase of a gigantic ruin, was their way now. No trees were to be seen, nor any vegetable growth save a poor brown scrubby moss, freezing in the chinks of rock. Blackened skeleton arms of wood by the wayside pointed upward to the convent as if the ghosts of former travellers overwhelmed by the snow haunted the scene of their distress. Icicle-hung caves and cellars built for refuges from sudden storms, were like so many whispers of the perils of the place; never-resting wreaths and mazes of mist wandered about, hunted by a moaning wind; and snow, the besetting danger of the mountain, against which all its defences were taken, drifted sharply down.\n\nThe file of mules, jaded by their day’s work, turned and wound slowly up the deep ascent; the foremost led by a guide on foot, in his broad-brimmed hat and round jacket, carrying a mountain staff or two upon his shoulder, with whom another guide conversed. There was no speaking among the string of riders. The sharp cold, the fatigue of the journey, and a new sensation of a catching in the breath, partly as if they had just emerged from very clear crisp water, and partly as if they had been sobbing, kept them silent.\n\nAt length, a light on the summit of the rocky staircase gleamed through the snow and mist. The guides called to the mules, the mules pricked up their drooping heads, the travellers’ tongues were loosened, and in a sudden burst of slipping, climbing, jingling, clinking, and talking, they arrived at the convent door.\n\nOther mules had arrived not long before, some with peasant riders and some with goods, and had trodden the snow about the door into a pool of mud. Riding-saddles and bridles, pack-saddles and strings of bells, mules and men, lanterns, torches, sacks, provender, barrels, cheeses, kegs of honey and butter, straw bundles and packages of many shapes, were crowded confusedly together in this thawed quagmire and about the steps. Up here in the clouds, everything was seen through cloud, and seemed dissolving into cloud. The breath of the men was cloud, the breath of the mules was cloud, the lights were encircled by cloud, speakers close at hand were not seen for cloud, though their voices and all other sounds were surprisingly clear. Of the cloudy line of mules hastily tied to rings in the wall, one would bite another, or kick another, and then the whole mist would be disturbed: with men diving into it, and cries of men and beasts coming out of it, and no bystander discerning what was wrong. In the midst of this, the great stable of the convent, occupying the basement story and entered by the basement door, outside which all the disorder was, poured forth its contribution of cloud, as if the whole rugged edifice were filled with nothing else, and would collapse as soon as it had emptied itself, leaving the snow to fall upon the bare mountain summit.\n\nWhile all this noise and hurry were rife among the living travellers, there, too, silently assembled in a grated house half-a-dozen paces removed, with the same cloud enfolding them and the same snow flakes drifting in upon them, were the dead travellers found upon the mountain. The mother, storm-belated many winters ago, still standing in the corner with her baby at her breast; the man who had frozen with his arm raised to his mouth in fear or hunger, still pressing it with his dry lips after years and years. An awful company, mysteriously come together! A wild destiny for that mother to have foreseen! ‘Surrounded by so many and such companions upon whom I never looked, and never shall look, I and my child will dwell together inseparable, on the Great Saint Bernard, outlasting generations who will come to see us, and will never know our name, or one word of our story but the end.’\n\nThe living travellers thought little or nothing of the dead just then. They thought much more of alighting at the convent door, and warming themselves at the convent fire. Disengaged from the turmoil, which was already calming down as the crowd of mules began to be bestowed in the stable, they hurried shivering up the steps and into the building. There was a smell within, coming up from the floor, of tethered beasts, like the smell of a menagerie of wild animals. There were strong arched galleries within, huge stone piers, great staircases, and thick walls pierced with small sunken windows--fortifications against the mountain storms, as if they had been human enemies. There were gloomy vaulted sleeping-rooms within, intensely cold, but clean and hospitably prepared for guests. Finally, there was a parlour for guests to sit in and sup in, where a table was already laid, and where a blazing fire shone red and high.\n\nIn this room, after having had their quarters for the night allotted to them by two young Fathers, the travellers presently drew round the hearth. They were in three parties; of whom the first, as the most numerous and important, was the slowest, and had been overtaken by one of the others on the way up. It consisted of an elderly lady, two grey-haired gentlemen, two young ladies, and their brother. These were attended (not to mention four guides), by a courier, two footmen, and two waiting-maids: which strong body of inconvenience was accommodated elsewhere under the same roof. The party that had overtaken them, and followed in their train, consisted of only three members: one lady and two gentlemen. The third party, which had ascended from the valley on the Italian side of the Pass, and had arrived first, were four in number: a plethoric, hungry, and silent German tutor in spectacles, on a tour with three young men, his pupils, all plethoric, hungry, and silent, and all in spectacles.\n\nThese three groups sat round the fire eyeing each other drily, and waiting for supper. Only one among them, one of the gentlemen belonging to the party of three, made advances towards conversation. Throwing out his lines for the Chief of the important tribe, while addressing himself to his own companions, he remarked, in a tone of voice which included all the company if they chose to be included, that it had been a long day, and that he felt for the ladies. That he feared one of the young ladies was not a strong or accustomed traveller, and had been over-fatigued two or three hours ago. That he had observed, from his station in the rear, that she sat her mule as if she were exhausted. That he had, twice or thrice afterwards, done himself the honour of inquiring of one of the guides, when he fell behind, how the lady did. That he had been enchanted to learn that she had recovered her spirits, and that it had been but a passing discomfort. That he trusted (by this time he had secured the eyes of the Chief, and addressed him) he might be permitted to express his hope that she was now none the worse, and that she would not regret having made the journey.\n\n‘My daughter, I am obliged to you, sir,’ returned the Chief, ‘is quite restored, and has been greatly interested.’\n\n‘New to mountains, perhaps?’ said the insinuating traveller.\n\n‘New to--ha--to mountains,’ said the Chief.\n\n‘But you are familiar with them, sir?’ the insinuating traveller assumed.\n\n‘I am--hum--tolerably familiar. Not of late years. Not of late years,’ replied the Chief, with a flourish of his hand.\n\nThe insinuating traveller, acknowledging the flourish with an inclination of his head, passed from the Chief to the second young lady, who had not yet been referred to otherwise than as one of the ladies in whose behalf he felt so sensitive an interest.\n\nHe hoped she was not incommoded by the fatigues of the day.\n\n‘Incommoded, certainly,’ returned the young lady, ‘but not tired.’\n\nThe insinuating traveller complimented her on the justice of the distinction. It was what he had meant to say. Every lady must doubtless be incommoded by having to do with that proverbially unaccommodating animal, the mule.\n\n‘We have had, of course,’ said the young lady, who was rather reserved and haughty, ‘to leave the carriages and fourgon at Martigny. And the impossibility of bringing anything that one wants to this inaccessible place, and the necessity of leaving every comfort behind, is not convenient.’\n\n‘A savage place indeed,’ said the insinuating traveller.\n\nThe elderly lady, who was a model of accurate dressing, and whose manner was perfect, considered as a piece of machinery, here interposed a remark in a low soft voice.\n\n‘But, like other inconvenient places,’ she observed, ‘it must be seen. As a place much spoken of, it is necessary to see it.’\n\n‘O! I have not the least objection to seeing it, I assure you, Mrs General,’ returned the other, carelessly.\n\n‘You, madam,’ said the insinuating traveller, ‘have visited this spot before?’\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Mrs General. ‘I have been here before. Let me commend you, my dear,’ to the former young lady, ‘to shade your face from the hot wood, after exposure to the mountain air and snow. You, too, my dear,’ to the other and younger lady, who immediately did so; while the former merely said, ‘Thank you, Mrs General, I am Perfectly comfortable, and prefer remaining as I am.’\n\nThe brother, who had left his chair to open a piano that stood in the room, and who had whistled into it and shut it up again, now came strolling back to the fire with his glass in his eye. He was dressed in the very fullest and completest travelling trim. The world seemed hardly large enough to yield him an amount of travel proportionate to his equipment.\n\n‘These fellows are an immense time with supper,’ he drawled. ‘I wonder what they’ll give us! Has anybody any idea?’\n\n‘Not roast man, I believe,’ replied the voice of the second gentleman of the party of three.\n\n‘I suppose not. What d’ye mean?’ he inquired.\n\n‘That, as you are not to be served for the general supper, perhaps you will do us the favour of not cooking yourself at the general fire,’ returned the other.\n\nThe young gentleman who was standing in an easy attitude on the hearth, cocking his glass at the company, with his back to the blaze and his coat tucked under his arms, something as if he were Of the Poultry species and were trussed for roasting, lost countenance at this reply; he seemed about to demand further explanation, when it was discovered--through all eyes turning on the speaker--that the lady with him, who was young and beautiful, had not heard what had passed through having fainted with her head upon his shoulder.\n\n‘I think,’ said the gentleman in a subdued tone, ‘I had best carry her straight to her room. Will you call to some one to bring a light?’ addressing his companion, ‘and to show the way? In this strange rambling place I don’t know that I could find it.’\n\n‘Pray, let me call my maid,’ cried the taller of the young ladies.\n\n‘Pray, let me put this water to her lips,’ said the shorter, who had not spoken yet.\n\nEach doing what she suggested, there was no want of assistance. Indeed, when the two maids came in (escorted by the courier, lest any one should strike them dumb by addressing a foreign language to them on the road), there was a prospect of too much assistance. Seeing this, and saying as much in a few words to the slighter and younger of the two ladies, the gentleman put his wife’s arm over his shoulder, lifted her up, and carried her away.\n\nHis friend, being left alone with the other visitors, walked slowly up and down the room without coming to the fire again, pulling his black moustache in a contemplative manner, as if he felt himself committed to the late retort. While the subject of it was breathing injury in a corner, the Chief loftily addressed this gentleman.\n\n‘Your friend, sir,’ said he, ‘is--ha--is a little impatient; and, in his impatience, is not perhaps fully sensible of what he owes to--hum--to--but we will waive that, we will waive that. Your friend is a little impatient, sir.’\n\n‘It may be so, sir,’ returned the other. ‘But having had the honour of making that gentleman’s acquaintance at the hotel at Geneva, where we and much good company met some time ago, and having had the honour of exchanging company and conversation with that gentleman on several subsequent excursions, I can hear nothing--no, not even from one of your appearance and station, sir--detrimental to that gentleman.’\n\n‘You are in no danger, sir, of hearing any such thing from me. In remarking that your friend has shown impatience, I say no such thing. I make that remark, because it is not to be doubted that my son, being by birth and by--ha--by education a--hum--a gentleman, would have readily adapted himself to any obligingly expressed wish on the subject of the fire being equally accessible to the whole of the present circle. Which, in principle, I--ha--for all are--hum--equal on these occasions--I consider right.’\n\n‘Good,’ was the reply. ‘And there it ends! I am your son’s obedient servant. I beg your son to receive the assurance of my profound consideration. And now, sir, I may admit, freely admit, that my friend is sometimes of a sarcastic temper.’\n\n‘The lady is your friend’s wife, sir?’\n\n‘The lady is my friend’s wife, sir.’\n\n‘She is very handsome.’\n\n‘Sir, she is peerless. They are still in the first year of their marriage. They are still partly on a marriage, and partly on an artistic, tour.’\n\n‘Your friend is an artist, sir?’\n\nThe gentleman replied by kissing the fingers of his right hand, and wafting the kiss the length of his arm towards Heaven. As who should say, I devote him to the celestial Powers as an immortal artist!\n\n‘But he is a man of family,’ he added. ‘His connections are of the best. He is more than an artist: he is highly connected. He may, in effect, have repudiated his connections, proudly, impatiently, sarcastically (I make the concession of both words); but he has them. Sparks that have been struck out during our intercourse have shown me this.’\n\n‘Well! I hope,’ said the lofty gentleman, with the air of finally disposing of the subject, ‘that the lady’s indisposition may be only temporary.’\n\n‘Mere fatigue, I dare say.’\n\n‘Not altogether mere fatigue, sir, for her mule stumbled to-day, and she fell from the saddle. She fell lightly, and was up again without assistance, and rode from us laughing; but she complained towards evening of a slight bruise in the side. She spoke of it more than once, as we followed your party up the mountain.’\n\nThe head of the large retinue, who was gracious but not familiar, appeared by this time to think that he had condescended more than enough. He said no more, and there was silence for some quarter of an hour until supper appeared.\n\nWith the supper came one of the young Fathers (there seemed to be no old Fathers) to take the head of the table. It was like the supper of an ordinary Swiss hotel, and good red wine grown by the convent in more genial air was not wanting. The artist traveller calmly came and took his place at table when the rest sat down, with no apparent sense upon him of his late skirmish with the completely dressed traveller.\n\n‘Pray,’ he inquired of the host, over his soup, ‘has your convent many of its famous dogs now?’\n\n‘Monsieur, it has three.’\n\n‘I saw three in the gallery below. Doubtless the three in question.’\n\nThe host, a slender, bright-eyed, dark young man of polite manners, whose garment was a black gown with strips of white crossed over it like braces, and who no more resembled the conventional breed of Saint Bernard monks than he resembled the conventional breed of Saint Bernard dogs, replied, doubtless those were the three in question.\n\n‘And I think,’ said the artist traveller, ‘I have seen one of them before.’\n\nIt was possible. He was a dog sufficiently well known. Monsieur might have easily seen him in the valley or somewhere on the lake, when he (the dog) had gone down with one of the order to solicit aid for the convent.\n\n‘Which is done in its regular season of the year, I think?’\n\nMonsieur was right.\n\n‘And never without a dog. The dog is very important.’\n\nAgain Monsieur was right. The dog was very important. People were justly interested in the dog. As one of the dogs celebrated everywhere, Ma’amselle would observe.\n\nMa’amselle was a little slow to observe it, as though she were not yet well accustomed to the French tongue. Mrs General, however, observed it for her.\n\n‘Ask him if he has saved many lives?’ said, in his native English, the young man who had been put out of countenance.\n\nThe host needed no translation of the question. He promptly replied in French, ‘No. Not this one.’\n\n‘Why not?’ the same gentleman asked.\n\n‘Pardon,’ returned the host composedly, ‘give him the opportunity and he will do it without doubt. For example, I am well convinced,’ smiling sedately, as he cut up the dish of veal to be handed round, on the young man who had been put out of countenance, ‘that if you, Monsieur, would give him the opportunity, he would hasten with great ardour to fulfil his duty.’\n\nThe artist traveller laughed. The insinuating traveller (who evinced a provident anxiety to get his full share of the supper), wiping some drops of wine from his moustache with a piece of bread, joined the conversation.\n\n‘It is becoming late in the year, my Father,’ said he, ‘for tourist-travellers, is it not?’\n\n‘Yes, it is late. Yet two or three weeks, at most, and we shall be left to the winter snows.’\n\n‘And then,’ said the insinuating traveller, ‘for the scratching dogs and the buried children, according to the pictures!’\n\n‘Pardon,’ said the host, not quite understanding the allusion. ‘How, then the scratching dogs and the buried children according to the pictures?’\n\nThe artist traveller struck in again before an answer could be given.\n\n‘Don’t you know,’ he coldly inquired across the table of his companion, ‘that none but smugglers come this way in the winter or can have any possible business this way?’\n\n‘Holy blue! No; never heard of it.’\n\n‘So it is, I believe. And as they know the signs of the weather tolerably well, they don’t give much employment to the dogs--who have consequently died out rather--though this house of entertainment is conveniently situated for themselves. Their young families, I am told, they usually leave at home. But it’s a grand idea!’ cried the artist traveller, unexpectedly rising into a tone of enthusiasm. ‘It’s a sublime idea. It’s the finest idea in the world, and brings tears into a man’s eyes, by Jupiter!’ He then went on eating his veal with great composure.\n\nThere was enough of mocking inconsistency at the bottom of this speech to make it rather discordant, though the manner was refined and the person well-favoured, and though the depreciatory part of it was so skilfully thrown off as to be very difficult for one not perfectly acquainted with the English language to understand, or, even understanding, to take offence at: so simple and dispassionate was its tone. After finishing his veal in the midst of silence, the speaker again addressed his friend.\n\n‘Look,’ said he, in his former tone, ‘at this gentleman our host, not yet in the prime of life, who in so graceful a way and with such courtly urbanity and modesty presides over us! Manners fit for a crown! Dine with the Lord Mayor of London (if you can get an invitation) and observe the contrast. This dear fellow, with the finest cut face I ever saw, a face in perfect drawing, leaves some laborious life and comes up here I don’t know how many feet above the level of the sea, for no other purpose on earth (except enjoying himself, I hope, in a capital refectory) than to keep an hotel for idle poor devils like you and me, and leave the bill to our consciences! Why, isn’t it a beautiful sacrifice? What do we want more to touch us? Because rescued people of interesting appearance are not, for eight or nine months out of every twelve, holding on here round the necks of the most sagacious of dogs carrying wooden bottles, shall we disparage the place? No! Bless the place. It’s a great place, a glorious place!’\n\nThe chest of the grey-haired gentleman who was the Chief of the important party, had swelled as if with a protest against his being numbered among poor devils. No sooner had the artist traveller ceased speaking than he himself spoke with great dignity, as having it incumbent on him to take the lead in most places, and having deserted that duty for a little while.\n\nHe weightily communicated his opinion to their host, that his life must be a very dreary life here in the winter.\n\nThe host allowed to Monsieur that it was a little monotonous. The air was difficult to breathe for a length of time consecutively. The cold was very severe. One needed youth and strength to bear it. However, having them and the blessing of Heaven--\n\nYes, that was very good. ‘But the confinement,’ said the grey-haired gentleman.\n\nThere were many days, even in bad weather, when it was possible to walk about outside. It was the custom to beat a little track, and take exercise there.\n\n‘But the space,’ urged the grey-haired gentleman. ‘So small. So--ha--very limited.’\n\nMonsieur would recall to himself that there were the refuges to visit, and that tracks had to be made to them also.\n\nMonsieur still urged, on the other hand, that the space was so--ha--hum--so very contracted. More than that, it was always the same, always the same.\n\nWith a deprecating smile, the host gently raised and gently lowered his shoulders. That was true, he remarked, but permit him to say that almost all objects had their various points of view. Monsieur and he did not see this poor life of his from the same point of view. Monsieur was not used to confinement.\n\n‘I--ha--yes, very true,’ said the grey-haired gentleman. He seemed to receive quite a shock from the force of the argument.\n\nMonsieur, as an English traveller, surrounded by all means of travelling pleasantly; doubtless possessing fortune, carriages, and servants--\n\n‘Perfectly, perfectly. Without doubt,’ said the gentleman.\n\nMonsieur could not easily place himself in the position of a person who had not the power to choose, I will go here to-morrow, or there next day; I will pass these barriers, I will enlarge those bounds. Monsieur could not realise, perhaps, how the mind accommodated itself in such things to the force of necessity.\n\n‘It is true,’ said Monsieur. ‘We will--ha--not pursue the subject. You are--hum--quite accurate, I have no doubt. We will say no more.’\n\nThe supper having come to a close, he drew his chair away as he spoke, and moved back to his former place by the fire. As it was very cold at the greater part of the table, the other guests also resumed their former seats by the fire, designing to toast themselves well before going to bed. The host, when they rose from the table, bowed to all present, wished them good night, and withdrew. But first the insinuating traveller had asked him if they could have some wine made hot; and as he had answered Yes, and had presently afterwards sent it in, that traveller, seated in the centre of the group, and in the full heat of the fire, was soon engaged in serving it out to the rest.\n\nAt this time, the younger of the two young ladies, who had been silently attentive in her dark corner (the fire-light was the chief light in the sombre room, the lamp being smoky and dull) to what had been said of the absent lady, glided out. She was at a loss which way to turn when she had softly closed the door; but, after a little hesitation among the sounding passages and the many ways, came to a room in a corner of the main gallery, where the servants were at their supper. From these she obtained a lamp, and a direction to the lady’s room.\n\nIt was up the great staircase on the story above. Here and there, the bare white walls were broken by an iron grate, and she thought as she went along that the place was something like a prison. The arched door of the lady’s room, or cell, was not quite shut. After knocking at it two or three times without receiving an answer, she pushed it gently open, and looked in.\n\nThe lady lay with closed eyes on the outside of the bed, protected from the cold by the blankets and wrappers with which she had been covered when she revived from her fainting fit. A dull light placed in the deep recess of the window, made little impression on the arched room. The visitor timidly stepped to the bed, and said, in a soft whisper, ‘Are you better?’\n\nThe lady had fallen into a slumber, and the whisper was too low to awake her. Her visitor, standing quite still, looked at her attentively.\n\n‘She is very pretty,’ she said to herself. ‘I never saw so beautiful a face. O how unlike me!’\n\nIt was a curious thing to say, but it had some hidden meaning, for it filled her eyes with tears.\n\n‘I know I must be right. I know he spoke of her that evening. I could very easily be wrong on any other subject, but not on this, not on this!’\n\nWith a quiet and tender hand she put aside a straying fold of the sleeper’s hair, and then touched the hand that lay outside the covering.\n\n‘I like to look at her,’ she breathed to herself. ‘I like to see what has affected him so much.’\n\nShe had not withdrawn her hand, when the sleeper opened her eyes and started.\n\n‘Pray don’t be alarmed. I am only one of the travellers from down-stairs. I came to ask if you were better, and if I could do anything for you.’\n\n‘I think you have already been so kind as to send your servants to my assistance?’\n\n‘No, not I; that was my sister. Are you better?’\n\n‘Much better. It is only a slight bruise, and has been well looked to, and is almost easy now. It made me giddy and faint in a moment. It had hurt me before; but at last it overpowered me all at once.’\n\n‘May I stay with you until some one comes? Would you like it?’\n\n‘I should like it, for it is lonely here; but I am afraid you will feel the cold too much.’\n\n‘I don’t mind cold. I am not delicate, if I look so.’ She quickly moved one of the two rough chairs to the bedside, and sat down. The other as quickly moved a part of some travelling wrapper from herself, and drew it over her, so that her arm, in keeping it about her, rested on her shoulder.\n\n‘You have so much the air of a kind nurse,’ said the lady, smiling on her, ‘that you seem as if you had come to me from home.’\n\n‘I am very glad of it.’\n\n‘I was dreaming of home when I woke just now. Of my old home, I mean, before I was married.’\n\n‘And before you were so far away from it.’\n\n‘I have been much farther away from it than this; but then I took the best part of it with me, and missed nothing. I felt solitary as I dropped asleep here, and, missing it a little, wandered back to it.’\n\nThere was a sorrowfully affectionate and regretful sound in her voice, which made her visitor refrain from looking at her for the moment.\n\n‘It is a curious chance which at last brings us together, under this covering in which you have wrapped me,’ said the visitor after a pause; ‘for do you know, I think I have been looking for you some time.’\n\n‘Looking for me?’\n\n‘I believe I have a little note here, which I was to give to you whenever I found you. This is it. Unless I greatly mistake, it is addressed to you? Is it not?’\n\nThe lady took it, and said yes, and read it. Her visitor watched her as she did so. It was very short. She flushed a little as she put her lips to her visitor’s cheek, and pressed her hand.\n\n‘The dear young friend to whom he presents me, may be a comfort to me at some time, he says. She is truly a comfort to me the first time I see her.’\n\n‘Perhaps you don’t,’ said the visitor, hesitating--‘perhaps you don’t know my story? Perhaps he never told you my story?’\n\n‘No.’\n\n‘Oh no, why should he! I have scarcely the right to tell it myself at present, because I have been entreated not to do so. There is not much in it, but it might account to you for my asking you not to say anything about the letter here. You saw my family with me, perhaps? Some of them--I only say this to you--are a little proud, a little prejudiced.’\n\n‘You shall take it back again,’ said the other; ‘and then my husband is sure not to see it. He might see it and speak of it, otherwise, by some accident. Will you put it in your bosom again, to be certain?’\n\nShe did so with great care. Her small, slight hand was still upon the letter, when they heard some one in the gallery outside.\n\n‘I promised,’ said the visitor, rising, ‘that I would write to him after seeing you (I could hardly fail to see you sooner or later), and tell him if you were well and happy. I had better say you were well and happy.’\n\n‘Yes, yes, yes! Say I was very well and very happy. And that I thanked him affectionately, and would never forget him.’\n\n‘I shall see you in the morning. After that we are sure to meet again before very long. Good night!’\n\n‘Good night. Thank you, thank you. Good night, my dear!’\n\nBoth of them were hurried and fluttered as they exchanged this parting, and as the visitor came out of the door. She had expected to meet the lady’s husband approaching it; but the person in the gallery was not he: it was the traveller who had wiped the wine-drops from his moustache with the piece of bread. When he heard the step behind him, he turned round--for he was walking away in the dark.\n\nHis politeness, which was extreme, would not allow of the young lady’s lighting herself down-stairs, or going down alone. He took her lamp, held it so as to throw the best light on the stone steps, and followed her all the way to the supper-room. She went down, not easily hiding how much she was inclined to shrink and tremble; for the appearance of this traveller was particularly disagreeable to her. She had sat in her quiet corner before supper imagining what he would have been in the scenes and places within her experience, until he inspired her with an aversion that made him little less than terrific.\n\nHe followed her down with his smiling politeness, followed her in, and resumed his seat in the best place in the hearth. There with the wood-fire, which was beginning to burn low, rising and falling upon him in the dark room, he sat with his legs thrust out to warm, drinking the hot wine down to the lees, with a monstrous shadow imitating him on the wall and ceiling.\n\nThe tired company had broken up, and all the rest were gone to bed except the young lady’s father, who dozed in his chair by the fire. The traveller had been at the pains of going a long way up-stairs to his sleeping-room to fetch his pocket-flask of brandy. He told them so, as he poured its contents into what was left of the wine, and drank with a new relish.\n\n‘May I ask, sir, if you are on your way to Italy?’\n\nThe grey-haired gentleman had roused himself, and was preparing to withdraw. He answered in the affirmative.\n\n‘I also!’ said the traveller. ‘I shall hope to have the honour of offering my compliments in fairer scenes, and under softer circumstances, than on this dismal mountain.’\n\nThe gentleman bowed, distantly enough, and said he was obliged to him.\n\n‘We poor gentlemen, sir,’ said the traveller, pulling his moustache dry with his hand, for he had dipped it in the wine and brandy; ‘we poor gentlemen do not travel like princes, but the courtesies and graces of life are precious to us. To your health, sir!’\n\n‘Sir, I thank you.’\n\n‘To the health of your distinguished family--of the fair ladies, your daughters!’\n\n‘Sir, I thank you again, I wish you good night. My dear, are our--ha--our people in attendance?’\n\n‘They are close by, father.’\n\n‘Permit me!’ said the traveller, rising and holding the door open, as the gentleman crossed the room towards it with his arm drawn through his daughter’s. ‘Good repose! To the pleasure of seeing you once more! To to-morrow!’\n\nAs he kissed his hand, with his best manner and his daintiest smile, the young lady drew a little nearer to her father, and passed him with a dread of touching him.\n\n‘Humph!’ said the insinuating traveller, whose manner shrunk, and whose voice dropped when he was left alone. ‘If they all go to bed, why I must go. They are in a devil of a hurry. One would think the night would be long enough, in this freezing silence and solitude, if one went to bed two hours hence.’\n\nThrowing back his head in emptying his glass, he cast his eyes upon the travellers’ book, which lay on the piano, open, with pens and ink beside it, as if the night’s names had been registered when he was absent. Taking it in his hand, he read these entries.\n\nWilliam Dorrit, Esquire Frederick Dorrit, Esquire Edward Dorrit, Esquire Miss Dorrit Miss Amy Dorrit Mrs General and Suite. From France to Italy.\n\nMr and Mrs Henry Gowan. From France to Italy.\n\nTo which he added, in a small complicated hand, ending with a long lean flourish, not unlike a lasso thrown at all the rest of the names:\n\nBlandois. Paris. From France to Italy.\n\nAnd then, with his nose coming down over his moustache and his moustache going up and under his nose, repaired to his allotted cell.\n\n# CHAPTER 2. Mrs General\n\nIt is indispensable to present the accomplished lady who was of sufficient importance in the suite of the Dorrit Family to have a line to herself in the Travellers’ Book.\n\nMrs General was the daughter of a clerical dignitary in a cathedral town, where she had led the fashion until she was as near forty-five as a single lady can be. A stiff commissariat officer of sixty, famous as a martinet, had then become enamoured of the gravity with which she drove the proprieties four-in-hand through the cathedral town society, and had solicited to be taken beside her on the box of the cool coach of ceremony to which that team was harnessed. His proposal of marriage being accepted by the lady, the commissary took his seat behind the proprieties with great decorum, and Mrs General drove until the commissary died. In the course of their united journey, they ran over several people who came in the way of the proprieties; but always in a high style and with composure.\n\nThe commissary having been buried with all the decorations suitable to the service (the whole team of proprieties were harnessed to his hearse, and they all had feathers and black velvet housings with his coat of arms in the corner), Mrs General began to inquire what quantity of dust and ashes was deposited at the bankers’. It then transpired that the commissary had so far stolen a march on Mrs General as to have bought himself an annuity some years before his marriage, and to have reserved that circumstance in mentioning, at the period of his proposal, that his income was derived from the interest of his money. Mrs General consequently found her means so much diminished, that, but for the perfect regulation of her mind, she might have felt disposed to question the accuracy of that portion of the late service which had declared that the commissary could take nothing away with him.\n\nIn this state of affairs it occurred to Mrs General, that she might ‘form the mind,’ and eke the manners of some young lady of distinction. Or, that she might harness the proprieties to the carriage of some rich young heiress or widow, and become at once the driver and guard of such vehicle through the social mazes. Mrs General’s communication of this idea to her clerical and commissariat connection was so warmly applauded that, but for the lady’s undoubted merit, it might have appeared as though they wanted to get rid of her. Testimonials representing Mrs General as a prodigy of piety, learning, virtue, and gentility, were lavishly contributed from influential quarters; and one venerable archdeacon even shed tears in recording his testimony to her perfections (described to him by persons on whom he could rely), though he had never had the honour and moral gratification of setting eyes on Mrs General in all his life.\n\nThus delegated on her mission, as it were by Church and State, Mrs General, who had always occupied high ground, felt in a condition to keep it, and began by putting herself up at a very high figure. An interval of some duration elapsed, in which there was no bid for Mrs General. At length a county-widower, with a daughter of fourteen, opened negotiations with the lady; and as it was a part either of the native dignity or of the artificial policy of Mrs General (but certainly one or the other) to comport herself as if she were much more sought than seeking, the widower pursued Mrs General until he prevailed upon her to form his daughter’s mind and manners.\n\nThe execution of this trust occupied Mrs General about seven years, in the course of which time she made the tour of Europe, and saw most of that extensive miscellany of objects which it is essential that all persons of polite cultivation should see with other people’s eyes, and never with their own. When her charge was at length formed, the marriage, not only of the young lady, but likewise of her father, the widower, was resolved on. The widower then finding Mrs General both inconvenient and expensive, became of a sudden almost as much affected by her merits as the archdeacon had been, and circulated such praises of her surpassing worth, in all quarters where he thought an opportunity might arise of transferring the blessing to somebody else, that Mrs General was a name more honourable than ever.\n\nThe phoenix was to let, on this elevated perch, when Mr Dorrit, who had lately succeeded to his property, mentioned to his bankers that he wished to discover a lady, well-bred, accomplished, well connected, well accustomed to good society, who was qualified at once to complete the education of his daughters, and to be their matron or chaperon. Mr Dorrit’s bankers, as bankers of the county-widower, instantly said, ‘Mrs General.’\n\nPursuing the light so fortunately hit upon, and finding the concurrent testimony of the whole of Mrs General’s acquaintance to be of the pathetic nature already recorded, Mr Dorrit took the trouble of going down to the county of the county-widower to see Mrs General, in whom he found a lady of a quality superior to his highest expectations.\n\n‘Might I be excused,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘if I inquired--ha--what remune--’\n\n‘Why, indeed,’ returned Mrs General, stopping the word, ‘it is a subject on which I prefer to avoid entering. I have never entered on it with my friends here; and I cannot overcome the delicacy, Mr Dorrit, with which I have always regarded it. I am not, as I hope you are aware, a governess--’\n\n‘O dear no!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Pray, madam, do not imagine for a moment that I think so.’ He really blushed to be suspected of it.\n\nMrs General gravely inclined her head. ‘I cannot, therefore, put a price upon services which it is a pleasure to me to render if I can render them spontaneously, but which I could not render in mere return for any consideration. Neither do I know how, or where, to find a case parallel to my own. It is peculiar.’\n\nNo doubt. But how then (Mr Dorrit not unnaturally hinted) could the subject be approached?\n\n‘I cannot object,’ said Mrs General--‘though even that is disagreeable to me--to Mr Dorrit’s inquiring, in confidence of my friends here, what amount they have been accustomed, at quarterly intervals, to pay to my credit at my bankers’.’\n\nMr Dorrit bowed his acknowledgements.\n\n‘Permit me to add,’ said Mrs General, ‘that beyond this, I can never resume the topic. Also that I can accept no second or inferior position. If the honour were proposed to me of becoming known to Mr Dorrit’s family--I think two daughters were mentioned?--’\n\n‘Two daughters.’\n\n‘I could only accept it on terms of perfect equality, as a companion, protector, Mentor, and friend.’\n\nMr Dorrit, in spite of his sense of his importance, felt as if it would be quite a kindness in her to accept it on any conditions. He almost said as much.\n\n‘I think,’ repeated Mrs General, ‘two daughters were mentioned?’\n\n‘Two daughters,’ said Mr Dorrit again.\n\n‘It would therefore,’ said Mrs General, ‘be necessary to add a third more to the payment (whatever its amount may prove to be), which my friends here have been accustomed to make to my bankers’.’\n\nMr Dorrit lost no time in referring the delicate question to the county-widower, and finding that he had been accustomed to pay three hundred pounds a-year to the credit of Mrs General, arrived, without any severe strain on his arithmetic, at the conclusion that he himself must pay four. Mrs General being an article of that lustrous surface which suggests that it is worth any money, he made a formal proposal to be allowed to have the honour and pleasure of regarding her as a member of his family. Mrs General conceded that high privilege, and here she was.\n\nIn person, Mrs General, including her skirts which had much to do with it, was of a dignified and imposing appearance; ample, rustling, gravely voluminous; always upright behind the proprieties. She might have been taken--had been taken--to the top of the Alps and the bottom of Herculaneum, without disarranging a fold in her dress, or displacing a pin. If her countenance and hair had rather a floury appearance, as though from living in some transcendently genteel Mill, it was rather because she was a chalky creation altogether, than because she mended her complexion with violet powder, or had turned grey. If her eyes had no expression, it was probably because they had nothing to express. If she had few wrinkles, it was because her mind had never traced its name or any other inscription on her face. A cool, waxy, blown-out woman, who had never lighted well.\n\nMrs General had no opinions. Her way of forming a mind was to prevent it from forming opinions. She had a little circular set of mental grooves or rails on which she started little trains of other people’s opinions, which never overtook one another, and never got anywhere. Even her propriety could not dispute that there was impropriety in the world; but Mrs General’s way of getting rid of it was to put it out of sight, and make believe that there was no such thing. This was another of her ways of forming a mind--to cram all articles of difficulty into cupboards, lock them up, and say they had no existence. It was the easiest way, and, beyond all comparison, the properest.\n\nMrs General was not to be told of anything shocking. Accidents, miseries, and offences, were never to be mentioned before her. Passion was to go to sleep in the presence of Mrs General, and blood was to change to milk and water. The little that was left in the world, when all these deductions were made, it was Mrs General’s province to varnish. In that formation process of hers, she dipped the smallest of brushes into the largest of pots, and varnished the surface of every object that came under consideration. The more cracked it was, the more Mrs General varnished it.\n\nThere was varnish in Mrs General’s voice, varnish in Mrs General’s touch, an atmosphere of varnish round Mrs General’s figure. Mrs General’s dreams ought to have been varnished--if she had any--lying asleep in the arms of the good Saint Bernard, with the feathery snow falling on his house-top.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. On the Road\n\nThe bright morning sun dazzled the eyes, the snow had ceased, the mists had vanished, the mountain air was so clear and light that the new sensation of breathing it was like the having entered on a new existence. To help the delusion, the solid ground itself seemed gone, and the mountain, a shining waste of immense white heaps and masses, to be a region of cloud floating between the blue sky above and the earth far below.\n\nSome dark specks in the snow, like knots upon a little thread, beginning at the convent door and winding away down the descent in broken lengths which were not yet pieced together, showed where the Brethren were at work in several places clearing the track. Already the snow had begun to be foot-thawed again about the door. Mules were busily brought out, tied to the rings in the wall, and laden; strings of bells were buckled on, burdens were adjusted, the voices of drivers and riders sounded musically. Some of the earliest had even already resumed their journey; and, both on the level summit by the dark water near the convent, and on the downward way of yesterday’s ascent, little moving figures of men and mules, reduced to miniatures by the immensity around, went with a clear tinkling of bells and a pleasant harmony of tongues.\n\nIn the supper-room of last night, a new fire, piled upon the feathery ashes of the old one, shone upon a homely breakfast of loaves, butter, and milk. It also shone on the courier of the Dorrit family, making tea for his party from a supply he had brought up with him, together with several other small stores which were chiefly laid in for the use of the strong body of inconvenience. Mr Gowan and Blandois of Paris had already breakfasted, and were walking up and down by the lake, smoking their cigars.\n\n‘Gowan, eh?’ muttered Tip, otherwise Edward Dorrit, Esquire, turning over the leaves of the book, when the courier had left them to breakfast. ‘Then Gowan is the name of a puppy, that’s all I have got to say! If it was worth my while, I’d pull his nose. But it isn’t worth my while--fortunately for him. How’s his wife, Amy? I suppose you know. You generally know things of that sort.’\n\n‘She is better, Edward. But they are not going to-day.’\n\n‘Oh! They are not going to-day! Fortunately for that fellow too,’ said Tip, ‘or he and I might have come into collision.’\n\n‘It is thought better here that she should lie quiet to-day, and not be fatigued and shaken by the ride down until to-morrow.’\n\n‘With all my heart. But you talk as if you had been nursing her. You haven’t been relapsing into (Mrs General is not here) into old habits, have you, Amy?’\n\nHe asked her the question with a sly glance of observation at Miss Fanny, and at his father too.\n\n‘I have only been in to ask her if I could do anything for her, Tip,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘You needn’t call me Tip, Amy child,’ returned that young gentleman with a frown; ‘because that’s an old habit, and one you may as well lay aside.’\n\n‘I didn’t mean to say so, Edward dear. I forgot. It was so natural once, that it seemed at the moment the right word.’\n\n‘Oh yes!’ Miss Fanny struck in. ‘Natural, and right word, and once, and all the rest of it! Nonsense, you little thing! I know perfectly well why you have been taking such an interest in this Mrs Gowan. You can’t blind _me_.’\n\n‘I will not try to, Fanny. Don’t be angry.’\n\n‘Oh! angry!’ returned that young lady with a flounce. ‘I have no patience’ (which indeed was the truth).\n\n‘Pray, Fanny,’ said Mr Dorrit, raising his eyebrows, ‘what do you mean? Explain yourself.’\n\n‘Oh! Never mind, Pa,’ replied Miss Fanny, ‘it’s no great matter. Amy will understand me. She knew, or knew of, this Mrs Gowan before yesterday, and she may as well admit that she did.’\n\n‘My child,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning to his younger daughter, ‘has your sister--any--ha--authority for this curious statement?’\n\n‘However meek we are,’ Miss Fanny struck in before she could answer, ‘we don’t go creeping into people’s rooms on the tops of cold mountains, and sitting perishing in the frost with people, unless we know something about them beforehand. It’s not very hard to divine whose friend Mrs Gowan is.’\n\n‘Whose friend?’ inquired her father.\n\n‘Pa, I am sorry to say,’ returned Miss Fanny, who had by this time succeeded in goading herself into a state of much ill-usage and grievance, which she was often at great pains to do: ‘that I believe her to be a friend of that very objectionable and unpleasant person, who, with a total absence of all delicacy, which our experience might have led us to expect from him, insulted us and outraged our feelings in so public and wilful a manner on an occasion to which it is understood among us that we will not more pointedly allude.’\n\n‘Amy, my child,’ said Mr Dorrit, tempering a bland severity with a dignified affection, ‘is this the case?’\n\nLittle Dorrit mildly answered, yes it was.\n\n‘Yes it is!’ cried Miss Fanny. ‘Of course! I said so! And now, Pa, I do declare once for all’--this young lady was in the habit of declaring the same thing once for all every day of her life, and even several times in a day--‘that this is shameful! I do declare once for all that it ought to be put a stop to. Is it not enough that we have gone through what is only known to ourselves, but are we to have it thrown in our faces, perseveringly and systematically, by the very person who should spare our feelings most? Are we to be exposed to this unnatural conduct every moment of our lives? Are we never to be permitted to forget? I say again, it is absolutely infamous!’\n\n‘Well, Amy,’ observed her brother, shaking his head, ‘you know I stand by you whenever I can, and on most occasions. But I must say, that, upon my soul, I do consider it rather an unaccountable mode of showing your sisterly affection, that you should back up a man who treated me in the most ungentlemanly way in which one man can treat another. And who,’ he added convincingly, ‘must be a low-minded thief, you know, or he never could have conducted himself as he did.’\n\n‘And see,’ said Miss Fanny, ‘see what is involved in this! Can we ever hope to be respected by our servants? Never. Here are our two women, and Pa’s valet, and a footman, and a courier, and all sorts of dependents, and yet in the midst of these, we are to have one of ourselves rushing about with tumblers of cold water, like a menial! Why, a policeman,’ said Miss Fanny, ‘if a beggar had a fit in the street, could but go plunging about with tumblers, as this very Amy did in this very room before our very eyes last night!’\n\n‘I don’t so much mind that, once in a way,’ remarked Mr Edward; ‘but your Clennam, as he thinks proper to call himself, is another thing.’\n\n‘He is part of the same thing,’ returned Miss Fanny, ‘and of a piece with all the rest. He obtruded himself upon us in the first instance. We never wanted him. I always showed him, for one, that I could have dispensed with his company with the greatest pleasure. He then commits that gross outrage upon our feelings, which he never could or would have committed but for the delight he took in exposing us; and then we are to be demeaned for the service of his friends! Why, I don’t wonder at this Mr Gowan’s conduct towards you. What else was to be expected when he was enjoying our past misfortunes--gloating over them at the moment!’\n\n‘Father--Edward--no indeed!’ pleaded Little Dorrit. ‘Neither Mr nor Mrs Gowan had ever heard our name. They were, and they are, quite ignorant of our history.’\n\n‘So much the worse,’ retorted Fanny, determined not to admit anything in extenuation, ‘for then you have no excuse. If they had known about us, you might have felt yourself called upon to conciliate them. That would have been a weak and ridiculous mistake, but I can respect a mistake, whereas I can’t respect a wilful and deliberate abasing of those who should be nearest and dearest to us. No. I can’t respect that. I can do nothing but denounce that.’\n\n‘I never offend you wilfully, Fanny,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘though you are so hard with me.’\n\n‘Then you should be more careful, Amy,’ returned her sister. ‘If you do such things by accident, you should be more careful. If I happened to have been born in a peculiar place, and under peculiar circumstances that blunted my knowledge of propriety, I fancy I should think myself bound to consider at every step, “Am I going, ignorantly, to compromise any near and dear relations?” That is what I fancy _I_ should do, if it was _my_ case.’\n\nMr Dorrit now interposed, at once to stop these painful subjects by his authority, and to point their moral by his wisdom.\n\n‘My dear,’ said he to his younger daughter, ‘I beg you to--ha--to say no more. Your sister Fanny expresses herself strongly, but not without considerable reason. You have now a--hum--a great position to support. That great position is not occupied by yourself alone, but by--ha--by me, and--ha hum--by us. Us. Now, it is incumbent upon all people in an exalted position, but it is particularly so on this family, for reasons which I--ha--will not dwell upon, to make themselves respected. To be vigilant in making themselves respected. Dependants, to respect us, must be--ha--kept at a distance and--hum--kept down. Down. Therefore, your not exposing yourself to the remarks of our attendants by appearing to have at any time dispensed with their services and performed them for yourself, is--ha--highly important.’\n\n‘Why, who can doubt it?’ cried Miss Fanny. ‘It’s the essence of everything.’\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned her father, grandiloquently, ‘give me leave, my dear. We then come to--ha--to Mr Clennam. I am free to say that I do not, Amy, share your sister’s sentiments--that is to say altogether--hum-- altogether--in reference to Mr Clennam. I am content to regard that individual in the light of--ha--generally--a well-behaved person. Hum. A well-behaved person. Nor will I inquire whether Mr Clennam did, at any time, obtrude himself on--ha--my society. He knew my society to be--hum--sought, and his plea might be that he regarded me in the light of a public character. But there were circumstances attending my--ha--slight knowledge of Mr Clennam (it was very slight), which,’ here Mr Dorrit became extremely grave and impressive, ‘would render it highly indelicate in Mr Clennam to--ha--to seek to renew communication with me or with any member of my family under existing circumstances. If Mr Clennam has sufficient delicacy to perceive the impropriety of any such attempt, I am bound as a responsible gentleman to--ha--defer to that delicacy on his part. If, on the other hand, Mr Clennam has not that delicacy, I cannot for a moment--ha--hold any correspondence with so--hum--coarse a mind. In either case, it would appear that Mr Clennam is put altogether out of the question, and that we have nothing to do with him or he with us. Ha--Mrs General!’\n\nThe entrance of the lady whom he announced, to take her place at the breakfast-table, terminated the discussion. Shortly afterwards, the courier announced that the valet, and the footman, and the two maids, and the four guides, and the fourteen mules, were in readiness; so the breakfast party went out to the convent door to join the cavalcade.\n\nMr Gowan stood aloof with his cigar and pencil, but Mr Blandois was on the spot to pay his respects to the ladies. When he gallantly pulled off his slouched hat to Little Dorrit, she thought he had even a more sinister look, standing swart and cloaked in the snow, than he had in the fire-light over-night. But, as both her father and her sister received his homage with some favour, she refrained from expressing any distrust of him, lest it should prove to be a new blemish derived from her prison birth.\n\nNevertheless, as they wound down the rugged way while the convent was yet in sight, she more than once looked round, and descried Mr Blandois, backed by the convent smoke which rose straight and high from the chimneys in a golden film, always standing on one jutting point looking down after them. Long after he was a mere black stick in the snow, she felt as though she could yet see that smile of his, that high nose, and those eyes that were too near it. And even after that, when the convent was gone and some light morning clouds veiled the pass below it, the ghastly skeleton arms by the wayside seemed to be all pointing up at him.\n\nMore treacherous than snow, perhaps, colder at heart, and harder to melt, Blandois of Paris by degrees passed out of her mind, as they came down into the softer regions. Again the sun was warm, again the streams descending from glaciers and snowy caverns were refreshing to drink at, again they came among the pine-trees, the rocky rivulets, the verdant heights and dales, the wooden chalets and rough zigzag fences of Swiss country. Sometimes the way so widened that she and her father could ride abreast. And then to look at him, handsomely clothed in his fur and broadcloths, rich, free, numerously served and attended, his eyes roving far away among the glories of the landscape, no miserable screen before them to darken his sight and cast its shadow on him, was enough.\n\nHer uncle was so far rescued from that shadow of old, that he wore the clothes they gave him, and performed some ablutions as a sacrifice to the family credit, and went where he was taken, with a certain patient animal enjoyment, which seemed to express that the air and change did him good. In all other respects, save one, he shone with no light but such as was reflected from his brother. His brother’s greatness, wealth, freedom, and grandeur, pleased him without any reference to himself. Silent and retiring, he had no use for speech when he could hear his brother speak; no desire to be waited on, so that the servants devoted themselves to his brother. The only noticeable change he originated in himself, was an alteration in his manner to his younger niece. Every day it refined more and more into a marked respect, very rarely shown by age to youth, and still more rarely susceptible, one would have said, of the fitness with which he invested it. On those occasions when Miss Fanny did declare once for all, he would take the next opportunity of baring his grey head before his younger niece, and of helping her to alight, or handing her to the carriage, or showing her any other attention, with the profoundest deference. Yet it never appeared misplaced or forced, being always heartily simple, spontaneous, and genuine. Neither would he ever consent, even at his brother’s request, to be helped to any place before her, or to take precedence of her in anything. So jealous was he of her being respected, that, on this very journey down from the Great Saint Bernard, he took sudden and violent umbrage at the footman’s being remiss to hold her stirrup, though standing near when she dismounted; and unspeakably astonished the whole retinue by charging at him on a hard-headed mule, riding him into a corner, and threatening to trample him to death.\n\nThey were a goodly company, and the Innkeepers all but worshipped them. Wherever they went, their importance preceded them in the person of the courier riding before, to see that the rooms of state were ready. He was the herald of the family procession. The great travelling-carriage came next: containing, inside, Mr Dorrit, Miss Dorrit, Miss Amy Dorrit, and Mrs General; outside, some of the retainers, and (in fine weather) Edward Dorrit, Esquire, for whom the box was reserved. Then came the chariot containing Frederick Dorrit, Esquire, and an empty place occupied by Edward Dorrit, Esquire, in wet weather. Then came the fourgon with the rest of the retainers, the heavy baggage, and as much as it could carry of the mud and dust which the other vehicles left behind.\n\nThese equipages adorned the yard of the hotel at Martigny, on the return of the family from their mountain excursion. Other vehicles were there, much company being on the road, from the patched Italian Vettura--like the body of a swing from an English fair put upon a wooden tray on wheels, and having another wooden tray without wheels put atop of it--to the trim English carriage. But there was another adornment of the hotel which Mr Dorrit had not bargained for. Two strange travellers embellished one of his rooms.\n\nThe Innkeeper, hat in hand in the yard, swore to the courier that he was blighted, that he was desolated, that he was profoundly afflicted, that he was the most miserable and unfortunate of beasts, that he had the head of a wooden pig. He ought never to have made the concession, he said, but the very genteel lady had so passionately prayed him for the accommodation of that room to dine in, only for a little half-hour, that he had been vanquished. The little half-hour was expired, the lady and gentleman were taking their little dessert and half-cup of coffee, the note was paid, the horses were ordered, they would depart immediately; but, owing to an unhappy destiny and the curse of Heaven, they were not yet gone.\n\nNothing could exceed Mr Dorrit’s indignation, as he turned at the foot of the staircase on hearing these apologies. He felt that the family dignity was struck at by an assassin’s hand. He had a sense of his dignity, which was of the most exquisite nature. He could detect a design upon it when nobody else had any perception of the fact. His life was made an agony by the number of fine scalpels that he felt to be incessantly engaged in dissecting his dignity.\n\n‘Is it possible, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, reddening excessively, ‘that you have--ha--had the audacity to place one of my rooms at the disposition of any other person?’\n\nThousands of pardons! It was the host’s profound misfortune to have been overcome by that too genteel lady. He besought Monseigneur not to enrage himself. He threw himself on Monseigneur for clemency. If Monseigneur would have the distinguished goodness to occupy the other salon especially reserved for him, for but five minutes, all would go well.\n\n‘No, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘I will not occupy any salon. I will leave your house without eating or drinking, or setting foot in it. How do you dare to act like this? Who am I that you--ha--separate me from other gentlemen?’\n\nAlas! The host called all the universe to witness that Monseigneur was the most amiable of the whole body of nobility, the most important, the most estimable, the most honoured. If he separated Monseigneur from others, it was only because he was more distinguished, more cherished, more generous, more renowned.\n\n‘Don’t tell me so, sir,’ returned Mr Dorrit, in a mighty heat. ‘You have affronted me. You have heaped insults upon me. How dare you? Explain yourself.’\n\nAh, just Heaven, then, how could the host explain himself when he had nothing more to explain; when he had only to apologise, and confide himself to the so well-known magnanimity of Monseigneur!\n\n‘I tell you, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, panting with anger, ‘that you separate me--ha--from other gentlemen; that you make distinctions between me and other gentlemen of fortune and station. I demand of you, why? I wish to know on--ha--what authority, on whose authority. Reply sir. Explain. Answer why.’\n\nPermit the landlord humbly to submit to Monsieur the Courier then, that Monseigneur, ordinarily so gracious, enraged himself without cause. There was no why. Monsieur the Courier would represent to Monseigneur, that he deceived himself in suspecting that there was any why, but the why his devoted servant had already had the honour to present to him. The very genteel lady--\n\n‘Silence!’ cried Mr Dorrit. ‘Hold your tongue! I will hear no more of the very genteel lady; I will hear no more of you. Look at this family--my family--a family more genteel than any lady. You have treated this family with disrespect; you have been insolent to this family. I’ll ruin you. Ha--send for the horses, pack the carriages, I’ll not set foot in this man’s house again!’\n\nNo one had interfered in the dispute, which was beyond the French colloquial powers of Edward Dorrit, Esquire, and scarcely within the province of the ladies. Miss Fanny, however, now supported her father with great bitterness; declaring, in her native tongue, that it was quite clear there was something special in this man’s impertinence; and that she considered it important that he should be, by some means, forced to give up his authority for making distinctions between that family and other wealthy families. What the reasons of his presumption could be, she was at a loss to imagine; but reasons he must have, and they ought to be torn from him.\n\nAll the guides, mule-drivers, and idlers in the yard, had made themselves parties to the angry conference, and were much impressed by the courier’s now bestirring himself to get the carriages out. With the aid of some dozen people to each wheel, this was done at a great cost of noise; and then the loading was proceeded with, pending the arrival of the horses from the post-house.\n\nBut the very genteel lady’s English chariot being already horsed and at the inn-door, the landlord had slipped up-stairs to represent his hard case. This was notified to the yard by his now coming down the staircase in attendance on the gentleman and the lady, and by his pointing out the offended majesty of Mr Dorrit to them with a significant motion of his hand.\n\n‘Beg your pardon,’ said the gentleman, detaching himself from the lady, and coming forward. ‘I am a man of few words and a bad hand at an explanation--but lady here is extremely anxious that there should be no Row. Lady--a mother of mine, in point of fact--wishes me to say that she hopes no Row.’\n\nMr Dorrit, still panting under his injury, saluted the gentleman, and saluted the lady, in a distant, final, and invincible manner.\n\n‘No, but really--here, old feller; you!’ This was the gentleman’s way of appealing to Edward Dorrit, Esquire, on whom he pounced as a great and providential relief. ‘Let you and I try to make this all right. Lady so very much wishes no Row.’\n\nEdward Dorrit, Esquire, led a little apart by the button, assumed a diplomatic expression of countenance in replying, ‘Why you must confess, that when you bespeak a lot of rooms beforehand, and they belong to you, it’s not pleasant to find other people in ‘em.’\n\n‘No,’ said the other, ‘I know it isn’t. I admit it. Still, let you and I try to make it all right, and avoid Row. The fault is not this chap’s at all, but my mother’s. Being a remarkably fine woman with no bigodd nonsense about her--well educated, too--she was too many for this chap. Regularly pocketed him.’\n\n‘If that’s the case--’ Edward Dorrit, Esquire, began.\n\n‘Assure you ‘pon my soul ‘tis the case. Consequently,’ said the other gentleman, retiring on his main position, ‘why Row?’\n\n‘Edmund,’ said the lady from the doorway, ‘I hope you have explained, or are explaining, to the satisfaction of this gentleman and his family that the civil landlord is not to blame?’\n\n‘Assure you, ma’am,’ returned Edmund, ‘perfectly paralysing myself with trying it on.’ He then looked steadfastly at Edward Dorrit, Esquire, for some seconds, and suddenly added, in a burst of confidence, ‘Old feller! _Is_ it all right?’\n\n‘I don’t know, after all,’ said the lady, gracefully advancing a step or two towards Mr Dorrit, ‘but that I had better say myself, at once, that I assured this good man I took all the consequences on myself of occupying one of a stranger’s suite of rooms during his absence, for just as much (or as little) time as I could dine in. I had no idea the rightful owner would come back so soon, nor had I any idea that he had come back, or I should have hastened to make restoration of my ill-gotten chamber, and to have offered my explanation and apology. I trust in saying this--’\n\nFor a moment the lady, with a glass at her eye, stood transfixed and speechless before the two Miss Dorrits. At the same moment, Miss Fanny, in the foreground of a grand pictorial composition, formed by the family, the family equipages, and the family servants, held her sister tight under one arm to detain her on the spot, and with the other arm fanned herself with a distinguished air, and negligently surveyed the lady from head to foot.\n\nThe lady, recovering herself quickly--for it was Mrs Merdle and she was not easily dashed--went on to add that she trusted in saying this, she apologised for her boldness, and restored this well-behaved landlord to the favour that was so very valuable to him. Mr Dorrit, on the altar of whose dignity all this was incense, made a gracious reply; and said that his people should--ha--countermand his horses, and he would--hum--overlook what he had at first supposed to be an affront, but now regarded as an honour. Upon this the bosom bent to him; and its owner, with a wonderful command of feature, addressed a winning smile of adieu to the two sisters, as young ladies of fortune in whose favour she was much prepossessed, and whom she had never had the gratification of seeing before.\n\nNot so, however, Mr Sparkler. This gentleman, becoming transfixed at the same moment as his lady-mother, could not by any means unfix himself again, but stood stiffly staring at the whole composition with Miss Fanny in the Foreground. On his mother saying, ‘Edmund, we are quite ready; will you give me your arm?’ he seemed, by the motion of his lips, to reply with some remark comprehending the form of words in which his shining talents found the most frequent utterance, but he relaxed no muscle. So fixed was his figure, that it would have been matter of some difficulty to bend him sufficiently to get him in the carriage-door, if he had not received the timely assistance of a maternal pull from within. He was no sooner within than the pad of the little window in the back of the chariot disappeared, and his eye usurped its place. There it remained as long as so small an object was discernible, and probably much longer, staring (as though something inexpressibly surprising should happen to a codfish) like an ill-executed eye in a large locket.\n\nThis encounter was so highly agreeable to Miss Fanny, and gave her so much to think of with triumph afterwards, that it softened her asperities exceedingly. When the procession was again in motion next day, she occupied her place in it with a new gaiety; and showed such a flow of spirits indeed, that Mrs General looked rather surprised.\n\nLittle Dorrit was glad to be found no fault with, and to see that Fanny was pleased; but her part in the procession was a musing part, and a quiet one. Sitting opposite her father in the travelling-carriage, and recalling the old Marshalsea room, her present existence was a dream. All that she saw was new and wonderful, but it was not real; it seemed to her as if those visions of mountains and picturesque countries might melt away at any moment, and the carriage, turning some abrupt corner, bring up with a jolt at the old Marshalsea gate.\n\nTo have no work to do was strange, but not half so strange as having glided into a corner where she had no one to think for, nothing to plan and contrive, no cares of others to load herself with. Strange as that was, it was far stranger yet to find a space between herself and her father, where others occupied themselves in taking care of him, and where she was never expected to be. At first, this was so much more unlike her old experience than even the mountains themselves, that she had been unable to resign herself to it, and had tried to retain her old place about him. But he had spoken to her alone, and had said that people--ha--people in an exalted position, my dear, must scrupulously exact respect from their dependents; and that for her, his daughter, Miss Amy Dorrit, of the sole remaining branch of the Dorrits of Dorsetshire, to be known to--hum--to occupy herself in fulfilling the functions of--ha hum--a valet, would be incompatible with that respect. Therefore, my dear, he--ha--he laid his parental injunctions upon her, to remember that she was a lady, who had now to conduct herself with--hum--a proper pride, and to preserve the rank of a lady; and consequently he requested her to abstain from doing what would occasion--ha--unpleasant and derogatory remarks. She had obeyed without a murmur. Thus it had been brought about that she now sat in her corner of the luxurious carriage with her little patient hands folded before her, quite displaced even from the last point of the old standing ground in life on which her feet had lingered.\n\nIt was from this position that all she saw appeared unreal; the more surprising the scenes, the more they resembled the unreality of her own inner life as she went through its vacant places all day long. The gorges of the Simplon, its enormous depths and thundering waterfalls, the wonderful road, the points of danger where a loose wheel or a faltering horse would have been destruction, the descent into Italy, the opening of that beautiful land as the rugged mountain-chasm widened and let them out from a gloomy and dark imprisonment--all a dream--only the old mean Marshalsea a reality. Nay, even the old mean Marshalsea was shaken to its foundations when she pictured it without her father. She could scarcely believe that the prisoners were still lingering in the close yard, that the mean rooms were still every one tenanted, and that the turnkey still stood in the Lodge letting people in and out, all just as she well knew it to be.\n\nWith a remembrance of her father’s old life in prison hanging about her like the burden of a sorrowful tune, Little Dorrit would wake from a dream of her birth-place into a whole day’s dream. The painted room in which she awoke, often a humbled state-chamber in a dilapidated palace, would begin it; with its wild red autumnal vine-leaves overhanging the glass, its orange-trees on the cracked white terrace outside the window, a group of monks and peasants in the little street below, misery and magnificence wrestling with each other upon every rood of ground in the prospect, no matter how widely diversified, and misery throwing magnificence with the strength of fate. To this would succeed a labyrinth of bare passages and pillared galleries, with the family procession already preparing in the quadrangle below, through the carriages and luggage being brought together by the servants for the day’s journey. Then breakfast in another painted chamber, damp-stained and of desolate proportions; and then the departure, which, to her timidity and sense of not being grand enough for her place in the ceremonies, was always an uneasy thing. For then the courier (who himself would have been a foreign gentleman of high mark in the Marshalsea) would present himself to report that all was ready; and then her father’s valet would pompously induct him into his travelling-cloak; and then Fanny’s maid, and her own maid (who was a weight on Little Dorrit’s mind--absolutely made her cry at first, she knew so little what to do with her), would be in attendance; and then her brother’s man would complete his master’s equipment; and then her father would give his arm to Mrs General, and her uncle would give his to her, and, escorted by the landlord and Inn servants, they would swoop down-stairs. There, a crowd would be collected to see them enter their carriages, which, amidst much bowing, and begging, and prancing, and lashing, and clattering, they would do; and so they would be driven madly through narrow unsavoury streets, and jerked out at the town gate.\n\nAmong the day’s unrealities would be roads where the bright red vines were looped and garlanded together on trees for many miles; woods of olives; white villages and towns on hill-sides, lovely without, but frightful in their dirt and poverty within; crosses by the way; deep blue lakes with fairy islands, and clustering boats with awnings of bright colours and sails of beautiful forms; vast piles of building mouldering to dust; hanging-gardens where the weeds had grown so strong that their stems, like wedges driven home, had split the arch and rent the wall; stone-terraced lanes, with the lizards running into and out of every chink; beggars of all sorts everywhere: pitiful, picturesque, hungry, merry; children beggars and aged beggars. Often at posting-houses and other halting places, these miserable creatures would appear to her the only realities of the day; and many a time, when the money she had brought to give them was all given away, she would sit with her folded hands, thoughtfully looking after some diminutive girl leading her grey father, as if the sight reminded her of something in the days that were gone.\n\nAgain, there would be places where they stayed the week together in splendid rooms, had banquets every day, rode out among heaps of wonders, walked through miles of palaces, and rested in dark corners of great churches; where there were winking lamps of gold and silver among pillars and arches, kneeling figures dotted about at confessionals and on the pavements; where there was the mist and scent of incense; where there were pictures, fantastic images, gaudy altars, great heights and distances, all softly lighted through stained glass, and the massive curtains that hung in the doorways. From these cities they would go on again, by the roads of vines and olives, through squalid villages, where there was not a hovel without a gap in its filthy walls, not a window with a whole inch of glass or paper; where there seemed to be nothing to support life, nothing to eat, nothing to make, nothing to grow, nothing to hope, nothing to do but die.\n\nAgain they would come to whole towns of palaces, whose proper inmates were all banished, and which were all changed into barracks: troops of idle soldiers leaning out of the state windows, where their accoutrements hung drying on the marble architecture, and showing to the mind like hosts of rats who were (happily) eating away the props of the edifices that supported them, and must soon, with them, be smashed on the heads of the other swarms of soldiers and the swarms of priests, and the swarms of spies, who were all the ill-looking population left to be ruined, in the streets below.\n\nThrough such scenes, the family procession moved on to Venice. And here it dispersed for a time, as they were to live in Venice some few months in a palace (itself six times as big as the whole Marshalsea) on the Grand Canal.\n\nIn this crowning unreality, where all the streets were paved with water, and where the deathlike stillness of the days and nights was broken by no sound but the softened ringing of church-bells, the rippling of the current, and the cry of the gondoliers turning the corners of the flowing streets, Little Dorrit, quite lost by her task being done, sat down to muse. The family began a gay life, went here and there, and turned night into day; but she was timid of joining in their gaieties, and only asked leave to be left alone.\n\nSometimes she would step into one of the gondolas that were always kept in waiting, moored to painted posts at the door--when she could escape from the attendance of that oppressive maid, who was her mistress, and a very hard one--and would be taken all over the strange city. Social people in other gondolas began to ask each other who the little solitary girl was whom they passed, sitting in her boat with folded hands, looking so pensively and wonderingly about her. Never thinking that it would be worth anybody’s while to notice her or her doings, Little Dorrit, in her quiet, scared, lost manner, went about the city none the less.\n\nBut her favourite station was the balcony of her own room, overhanging the canal, with other balconies below, and none above. It was of massive stone darkened by ages, built in a wild fancy which came from the East to that collection of wild fancies; and Little Dorrit was little indeed, leaning on the broad-cushioned ledge, and looking over. As she liked no place of an evening half so well, she soon began to be watched for, and many eyes in passing gondolas were raised, and many people said, There was the little figure of the English girl who was always alone.\n\nSuch people were not realities to the little figure of the English girl; such people were all unknown to her. She would watch the sunset, in its long low lines of purple and red, and its burning flush high up into the sky: so glowing on the buildings, and so lightening their structure, that it made them look as if their strong walls were transparent, and they shone from within. She would watch those glories expire; and then, after looking at the black gondolas underneath, taking guests to music and dancing, would raise her eyes to the shining stars. Was there no party of her own, in other times, on which the stars had shone? To think of that old gate now!\n\nShe would think of that old gate, and of herself sitting at it in the dead of the night, pillowing Maggy’s head; and of other places and of other scenes associated with those different times. And then she would lean upon her balcony, and look over at the water, as though they all lay underneath it. When she got to that, she would musingly watch its running, as if, in the general vision, it might run dry, and show her the prison again, and herself, and the old room, and the old inmates, and the old visitors: all lasting realities that had never changed.\n\nDear Mr Clennam,\n\nI write to you from my own room at Venice, thinking you will be glad to hear from me. But I know you cannot be so glad to hear from me as I am to write to you; for everything about you is as you have been accustomed to see it, and you miss nothing--unless it should be me, which can only be for a very little while together and very seldom--while everything in my life is so strange, and I miss so much.\n\nWhen we were in Switzerland, which appears to have been years ago, though it was only weeks, I met young Mrs Gowan, who was on a mountain excursion like ourselves. She told me she was very well and very happy. She sent you the message, by me, that she thanked you affectionately and would never forget you. She was quite confiding with me, and I loved her almost as soon as I spoke to her. But there is nothing singular in that; who could help loving so beautiful and winning a creature! I could not wonder at any one loving her. No indeed.\n\nIt will not make you uneasy on Mrs Gowan’s account, I hope--for I remember that you said you had the interest of a true friend in her--if I tell you that I wish she could have married some one better suited to her. Mr Gowan seems fond of her, and of course she is very fond of him, but I thought he was not earnest enough--I don’t mean in that respect--I mean in anything. I could not keep it out of my mind that if I was Mrs Gowan (what a change that would be, and how I must alter to become like her!) I should feel that I was rather lonely and lost, for the want of some one who was steadfast and firm in purpose. I even thought she felt this want a little, almost without knowing it. But mind you are not made uneasy by this, for she was ‘very well and very happy.’ And she looked most beautiful.\n\nI expect to meet her again before long, and indeed have been expecting for some days past to see her here. I will ever be as good a friend to her as I can for your sake. Dear Mr Clennam, I dare say you think little of having been a friend to me when I had no other (not that I have any other now, for I have made no new friends), but I think much of it, and I never can forget it.\n\nI wish I knew--but it is best for no one to write to me--how Mr and Mrs Plornish prosper in the business which my dear father bought for them, and that old Mr Nandy lives happily with them and his two grandchildren, and sings all his songs over and over again. I cannot quite keep back the tears from my eyes when I think of my poor Maggy, and of the blank she must have felt at first, however kind they all are to her, without her Little Mother. Will you go and tell her, as a strict secret, with my love, that she never can have regretted our separation more than I have regretted it? And will you tell them all that I have thought of them every day, and that my heart is faithful to them everywhere? O, if you could know how faithful, you would almost pity me for being so far away and being so grand!\n\nYou will be glad, I am sure, to know that my dear father is very well in health, and that all these changes are highly beneficial to him, and that he is very different indeed from what he used to be when you used to see him. There is an improvement in my uncle too, I think, though he never complained of old, and never exults now. Fanny is very graceful, quick, and clever. It is natural to her to be a lady; she has adapted herself to our new fortunes with wonderful ease.\n\nThis reminds me that I have not been able to do so, and that I sometimes almost despair of ever being able to do so. I find that I cannot learn. Mrs General is always with us, and we speak French and speak Italian, and she takes pains to form us in many ways. When I say we speak French and Italian, I mean they do. As for me, I am so slow that I scarcely get on at all. As soon as I begin to plan, and think, and try, all my planning, thinking, and trying go in old directions, and I begin to feel careful again about the expenses of the day, and about my dear father, and about my work, and then I remember with a start that there are no such cares left, and that in itself is so new and improbable that it sets me wandering again. I should not have the courage to mention this to any one but you.\n\nIt is the same with all these new countries and wonderful sights. They are very beautiful, and they astonish me, but I am not collected enough--not familiar enough with myself, if you can quite understand what I mean--to have all the pleasure in them that I might have. What I knew before them, blends with them, too, so curiously. For instance, when we were among the mountains, I often felt (I hesitate to tell such an idle thing, dear Mr Clennam, even to you) as if the Marshalsea must be behind that great rock; or as if Mrs Clennam’s room where I have worked so many days, and where I first saw you, must be just beyond that snow. Do you remember one night when I came with Maggy to your lodging in Covent Garden? That room I have often and often fancied I have seen before me, travelling along for miles by the side of our carriage, when I have looked out of the carriage-window after dark. We were shut out that night, and sat at the iron gate, and walked about till morning. I often look up at the stars, even from the balcony of this room, and believe that I am in the street again, shut out with Maggy. It is the same with people that I left in England.\n\nWhen I go about here in a gondola, I surprise myself looking into other gondolas as if I hoped to see them. It would overcome me with joy to see them, but I don’t think it would surprise me much, at first. In my fanciful times, I fancy that they might be anywhere; and I almost expect to see their dear faces on the bridges or the quays.\n\nAnother difficulty that I have will seem very strange to you. It must seem very strange to any one but me, and does even to me: I often feel the old sad pity for--I need not write the word--for him. Changed as he is, and inexpressibly blest and thankful as I always am to know it, the old sorrowful feeling of compassion comes upon me sometimes with such strength that I want to put my arms round his neck, tell him how I love him, and cry a little on his breast. I should be glad after that, and proud and happy. But I know that I must not do this; that he would not like it, that Fanny would be angry, that Mrs General would be amazed; and so I quiet myself. Yet in doing so, I struggle with the feeling that I have come to be at a distance from him; and that even in the midst of all the servants and attendants, he is deserted, and in want of me.\n\nDear Mr Clennam, I have written a great deal about myself, but I must write a little more still, or what I wanted most of all to say in this weak letter would be left out of it. In all these foolish thoughts of mine, which I have been so hardy as to confess to you because I know you will understand me if anybody can, and will make more allowance for me than anybody else would if you cannot--in all these thoughts, there is one thought scarcely ever--never--out of my memory, and that is that I hope you sometimes, in a quiet moment, have a thought for me. I must tell you that as to this, I have felt, ever since I have been away, an anxiety which I am very anxious to relieve. I have been afraid that you may think of me in a new light, or a new character. Don’t do that, I could not bear that--it would make me more unhappy than you can suppose. It would break my heart to believe that you thought of me in any way that would make me stranger to you than I was when you were so good to me. What I have to pray and entreat of you is, that you will never think of me as the daughter of a rich person; that you will never think of me as dressing any better, or living any better, than when you first knew me. That you will remember me only as the little shabby girl you protected with so much tenderness, from whose threadbare dress you have kept away the rain, and whose wet feet you have dried at your fire. That you will think of me (when you think of me at all), and of my true affection and devoted gratitude, always without change, as of\n\nYour poor child,\n\n# LITTLE DORRIT.\n\nP.S.--Particularly remember that you are not to be uneasy about Mrs Gowan. Her words were, ‘Very well and very happy.’ And she looked most beautiful.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Something Wrong Somewhere\n\nThe family had been a month or two at Venice, when Mr Dorrit, who was much among Counts and Marquises, and had but scant leisure, set an hour of one day apart, beforehand, for the purpose of holding some conference with Mrs General.\n\nThe time he had reserved in his mind arriving, he sent Mr Tinkler, his valet, to Mrs General’s apartment (which would have absorbed about a third of the area of the Marshalsea), to present his compliments to that lady, and represent him as desiring the favour of an interview. It being that period of the forenoon when the various members of the family had coffee in their own chambers, some couple of hours before assembling at breakfast in a faded hall which had once been sumptuous, but was now the prey of watery vapours and a settled melancholy, Mrs General was accessible to the valet. That envoy found her on a little square of carpet, so extremely diminutive in reference to the size of her stone and marble floor that she looked as if she might have had it spread for the trying on of a ready-made pair of shoes; or as if she had come into possession of the enchanted piece of carpet, bought for forty purses by one of the three princes in the Arabian Nights, and had that moment been transported on it, at a wish, into a palatial saloon with which it had no connection.\n\nMrs General, replying to the envoy, as she set down her empty coffee-cup, that she was willing at once to proceed to Mr Dorrit’s apartment, and spare him the trouble of coming to her (which, in his gallantry, he had proposed), the envoy threw open the door, and escorted Mrs General to the presence. It was quite a walk, by mysterious staircases and corridors, from Mrs General’s apartment,--hoodwinked by a narrow side street with a low gloomy bridge in it, and dungeon-like opposite tenements, their walls besmeared with a thousand downward stains and streaks, as if every crazy aperture in them had been weeping tears of rust into the Adriatic for centuries--to Mr Dorrit’s apartment: with a whole English house-front of window, a prospect of beautiful church-domes rising into the blue sky sheer out of the water which reflected them, and a hushed murmur of the Grand Canal laving the doorways below, where his gondolas and gondoliers attended his pleasure, drowsily swinging in a little forest of piles.\n\nMr Dorrit, in a resplendent dressing-gown and cap--the dormant grub that had so long bided its time among the Collegians had burst into a rare butterfly--rose to receive Mrs General. A chair to Mrs General. An easier chair, sir; what are you doing, what are you about, what do you mean? Now, leave us!\n\n‘Mrs General,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I took the liberty--’\n\n‘By no means,’ Mrs General interposed. ‘I was quite at your disposition. I had had my coffee.’\n\n‘--I took the liberty,’ said Mr Dorrit again, with the magnificent placidity of one who was above correction, ‘to solicit the favour of a little private conversation with you, because I feel rather worried respecting my--ha--my younger daughter. You will have observed a great difference of temperament, madam, between my two daughters?’\n\nSaid Mrs General in response, crossing her gloved hands (she was never without gloves, and they never creased and always fitted), ‘There is a great difference.’\n\n‘May I ask to be favoured with your view of it?’ said Mr Dorrit, with a deference not incompatible with majestic serenity.\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned Mrs General, ‘has force of character and self-reliance. Amy, none.’\n\nNone? O Mrs General, ask the Marshalsea stones and bars. O Mrs General, ask the milliner who taught her to work, and the dancing-master who taught her sister to dance. O Mrs General, Mrs General, ask me, her father, what I owe her; and hear my testimony touching the life of this slighted little creature from her childhood up!\n\nNo such adjuration entered Mr. Dorrit’s head. He looked at Mrs General, seated in her usual erect attitude on her coach-box behind the proprieties, and he said in a thoughtful manner, ‘True, madam.’\n\n‘I would not,’ said Mrs General, ‘be understood to say, observe, that there is nothing to improve in Fanny. But there is material there--perhaps, indeed, a little too much.’\n\n‘Will you be kind enough, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘to be--ha--more explicit? I do not quite understand my elder daughter’s having--hum--too much material. What material?’\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned Mrs General, ‘at present forms too many opinions. Perfect breeding forms none, and is never demonstrative.’\n\nLest he himself should be found deficient in perfect breeding, Mr Dorrit hastened to reply, ‘Unquestionably, madam, you are right.’ Mrs General returned, in her emotionless and expressionless manner, ‘I believe so.’\n\n‘But you are aware, my dear madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘that my daughters had the misfortune to lose their lamented mother when they were very young; and that, in consequence of my not having been until lately the recognised heir to my property, they have lived with me as a comparatively poor, though always proud, gentleman, in--ha hum--retirement!’\n\n‘I do not,’ said Mrs General, ‘lose sight of the circumstance.’\n\n‘Madam,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, ‘of my daughter Fanny, under her present guidance and with such an example constantly before her--’\n\n(Mrs General shut her eyes.)\n\n--‘I have no misgivings. There is adaptability of character in Fanny. But my younger daughter, Mrs General, rather worries and vexes my thoughts. I must inform you that she has always been my favourite.’\n\n‘There is no accounting,’ said Mrs General, ‘for these partialities.’\n\n‘Ha--no,’ assented Mr Dorrit. ‘No. Now, madam, I am troubled by noticing that Amy is not, so to speak, one of ourselves. She does not care to go about with us; she is lost in the society we have here; our tastes are evidently not her tastes. Which,’ said Mr Dorrit, summing up with judicial gravity, ‘is to say, in other words, that there is something wrong in--ha--Amy.’\n\n‘May we incline to the supposition,’ said Mrs General, with a little touch of varnish, ‘that something is referable to the novelty of the position?’\n\n‘Excuse me, madam,’ observed Mr Dorrit, rather quickly. ‘The daughter of a gentleman, though--ha--himself at one time comparatively far from affluent--comparatively--and herself reared in--hum--retirement, need not of necessity find this position so very novel.’\n‘True,’ said Mrs General, ‘true.’\n\n‘Therefore, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I took the liberty’ (he laid an emphasis on the phrase and repeated it, as though he stipulated, with urbane firmness, that he must not be contradicted again), ‘I took the liberty of requesting this interview, in order that I might mention the topic to you, and inquire how you would advise me?’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, ‘I have conversed with Amy several times since we have been residing here, on the general subject of the formation of a demeanour. She has expressed herself to me as wondering exceedingly at Venice. I have mentioned to her that it is better not to wonder. I have pointed out to her that the celebrated Mr Eustace, the classical tourist, did not think much of it; and that he compared the Rialto, greatly to its disadvantage, with Westminster and Blackfriars Bridges. I need not add, after what you have said, that I have not yet found my arguments successful. You do me the honour to ask me what to advise. It always appears to me (if this should prove to be a baseless assumption, I shall be pardoned), that Mr Dorrit has been accustomed to exercise influence over the minds of others.’\n\n‘Hum--madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I have been at the head of--ha of a considerable community. You are right in supposing that I am not unaccustomed to--an influential position.’\n\n‘I am happy,’ returned Mrs General, ‘to be so corroborated. I would therefore the more confidently recommend that Mr Dorrit should speak to Amy himself, and make his observations and wishes known to her. Being his favourite, besides, and no doubt attached to him, she is all the more likely to yield to his influence.’\n\n‘I had anticipated your suggestion, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘but--ha--was not sure that I might--hum--not encroach on--’\n\n‘On my province, Mr Dorrit?’ said Mrs General, graciously. ‘Do not mention it.’\n\n‘Then, with your leave, madam,’ resumed Mr Dorrit, ringing his little bell to summon his valet, ‘I will send for her at once.’\n\n‘Does Mr Dorrit wish me to remain?’\n\n‘Perhaps, if you have no other engagement, you would not object for a minute or two--’\n\n‘Not at all.’\n\nSo, Tinkler the valet was instructed to find Miss Amy’s maid, and to request that subordinate to inform Miss Amy that Mr Dorrit wished to see her in his own room. In delivering this charge to Tinkler, Mr Dorrit looked severely at him, and also kept a jealous eye upon him until he went out at the door, mistrusting that he might have something in his mind prejudicial to the family dignity; that he might have even got wind of some Collegiate joke before he came into the service, and might be derisively reviving its remembrance at the present moment. If Tinkler had happened to smile, however faintly and innocently, nothing would have persuaded Mr Dorrit, to the hour of his death, but that this was the case. As Tinkler happened, however, very fortunately for himself, to be of a serious and composed countenance, he escaped the secret danger that threatened him. And as on his return--when Mr Dorrit eyed him again--he announced Miss Amy as if she had come to a funeral, he left a vague impression on Mr Dorrit’s mind that he was a well-conducted young fellow, who had been brought up in the study of his Catechism by a widowed mother.\n\n‘Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘you have just now been the subject of some conversation between myself and Mrs General. We agree that you scarcely seem at home here. Ha--how is this?’\n\nA pause.\n\n‘I think, father, I require a little time.’\n\n‘Papa is a preferable mode of address,’ observed Mrs General. ‘Father is rather vulgar, my dear. The word Papa, besides, gives a pretty form to the lips. Papa, potatoes, poultry, prunes, and prism are all very good words for the lips: especially prunes and prism. You will find it serviceable, in the formation of a demeanour, if you sometimes say to yourself in company--on entering a room, for instance--Papa, potatoes, poultry, prunes and prism, prunes and prism.’\n\n‘Pray, my child,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘attend to the--hum--precepts of Mrs General.’\n\nPoor Little Dorrit, with a rather forlorn glance at that eminent varnisher, promised to try.\n\n‘You say, Amy,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, ‘that you think you require time. Time for what?’\n\nAnother pause.\n\n‘To become accustomed to the novelty of my life, was all I meant,’ said Little Dorrit, with her loving eyes upon her father; whom she had very nearly addressed as poultry, if not prunes and prism too, in her desire to submit herself to Mrs General and please him.\n\nMr Dorrit frowned, and looked anything but pleased. ‘Amy,’ he returned, ‘it appears to me, I must say, that you have had abundance of time for that. Ha--you surprise me. You disappoint me. Fanny has conquered any such little difficulties, and--hum--why not you?’\n\n‘I hope I shall do better soon,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘I hope so,’ returned her father. ‘I--ha--I most devoutly hope so, Amy. I sent for you, in order that I might say--hum--impressively say, in the presence of Mrs General, to whom we are all so much indebted for obligingly being present among us, on--ha--on this or any other occasion,’ Mrs General shut her eyes, ‘that I--ha hum--am not pleased with you. You make Mrs General’s a thankless task. You--ha--embarrass me very much. You have always (as I have informed Mrs General) been my favourite child; I have always made you a--hum--a friend and companion; in return, I beg--I--ha--I _do_ beg, that you accommodate yourself better to--hum--circumstances, and dutifully do what becomes your--your station.’\n\nMr Dorrit was even a little more fragmentary than usual, being excited on the subject and anxious to make himself particularly emphatic.\n\n‘I do beg,’ he repeated, ‘that this may be attended to, and that you will seriously take pains and try to conduct yourself in a manner both becoming your position as--ha--Miss Amy Dorrit, and satisfactory to myself and Mrs General.’\n\nThat lady shut her eyes again, on being again referred to; then, slowly opening them and rising, added these words:\n\n‘If Miss Amy Dorrit will direct her own attention to, and will accept of my poor assistance in, the formation of a surface, Mr. Dorrit will have no further cause of anxiety. May I take this opportunity of remarking, as an instance in point, that it is scarcely delicate to look at vagrants with the attention which I have seen bestowed upon them by a very dear young friend of mine? They should not be looked at. Nothing disagreeable should ever be looked at. Apart from such a habit standing in the way of that graceful equanimity of surface which is so expressive of good breeding, it hardly seems compatible with refinement of mind. A truly refined mind will seem to be ignorant of the existence of anything that is not perfectly proper, placid, and pleasant.’ Having delivered this exalted sentiment, Mrs General made a sweeping obeisance, and retired with an expression of mouth indicative of Prunes and Prism.\n\nLittle Dorrit, whether speaking or silent, had preserved her quiet earnestness and her loving look. It had not been clouded, except for a passing moment, until now. But now that she was left alone with him the fingers of her lightly folded hands were agitated, and there was repressed emotion in her face.\n\nNot for herself. She might feel a little wounded, but her care was not for herself. Her thoughts still turned, as they always had turned, to him. A faint misgiving, which had hung about her since their accession to fortune, that even now she could never see him as he used to be before the prison days, had gradually begun to assume form in her mind. She felt that, in what he had just now said to her and in his whole bearing towards her, there was the well-known shadow of the Marshalsea wall. It took a new shape, but it was the old sad shadow. She began with sorrowful unwillingness to acknowledge to herself that she was not strong enough to keep off the fear that no space in the life of man could overcome that quarter of a century behind the prison bars. She had no blame to bestow upon him, therefore: nothing to reproach him with, no emotions in her faithful heart but great compassion and unbounded tenderness.\n\nThis is why it was, that, even as he sat before her on his sofa, in the brilliant light of a bright Italian day, the wonderful city without and the splendours of an old palace within, she saw him at the moment in the long-familiar gloom of his Marshalsea lodging, and wished to take her seat beside him, and comfort him, and be again full of confidence with him, and of usefulness to him. If he divined what was in her thoughts, his own were not in tune with it. After some uneasy moving in his seat, he got up and walked about, looking very much dissatisfied.\n\n‘Is there anything else you wish to say to me, dear father?’\n\n‘No, no. Nothing else.’\n\n‘I am sorry you have not been pleased with me, dear. I hope you will not think of me with displeasure now. I am going to try, more than ever, to adapt myself as you wish to what surrounds me--for indeed I have tried all along, though I have failed, I know.’\n\n‘Amy,’ he returned, turning short upon her. ‘You--ha--habitually hurt me.’\n‘Hurt you, father! I!’\n\n‘There is a--hum--a topic,’ said Mr Dorrit, looking all about the ceiling of the room, and never at the attentive, uncomplainingly shocked face, ‘a painful topic, a series of events which I wish--ha--altogether to obliterate. This is understood by your sister, who has already remonstrated with you in my presence; it is understood by your brother; it is understood by--ha hum--by every one of delicacy and sensitiveness except yourself--ha--I am sorry to say, except yourself. You, Amy--hum--you alone and only you--constantly revive the topic, though not in words.’\n\nShe laid her hand on his arm. She did nothing more. She gently touched him. The trembling hand may have said, with some expression, ‘Think of me, think how I have worked, think of my many cares!’ But she said not a syllable herself.\n\nThere was a reproach in the touch so addressed to him that she had not foreseen, or she would have withheld her hand. He began to justify himself in a heated, stumbling, angry manner, which made nothing of it.\n\n‘I was there all those years. I was--ha--universally acknowledged as the head of the place. I--hum--I caused you to be respected there, Amy. I--ha hum--I gave my family a position there. I deserve a return. I claim a return. I say, sweep it off the face of the earth and begin afresh. Is that much? I ask, is _that_ much?’\n\nHe did not once look at her, as he rambled on in this way; but gesticulated at, and appealed to, the empty air.\n\n‘I have suffered. Probably I know how much I have suffered better than any one--ha--I say than any one! If _I_ can put that aside, if _I_ can eradicate the marks of what I have endured, and can emerge before the world--a--ha--gentleman unspoiled, unspotted--is it a great deal to expect--I say again, is it a great deal to expect--that my children should--hum--do the same and sweep that accursed experience off the face of the earth?’\n\nIn spite of his flustered state, he made all these exclamations in a carefully suppressed voice, lest the valet should overhear anything.\n\n‘Accordingly, they do it. Your sister does it. Your brother does it. You alone, my favourite child, whom I made the friend and companion of my life when you were a mere--hum--Baby, do not do it. You alone say you can’t do it. I provide you with valuable assistance to do it. I attach an accomplished and highly bred lady--ha--Mrs General, to you, for the purpose of doing it. Is it surprising that I should be displeased? Is it necessary that I should defend myself for expressing my displeasure? No!’\n\nNotwithstanding which, he continued to defend himself, without any abatement of his flushed mood.\n\n‘I am careful to appeal to that lady for confirmation, before I express any displeasure at all. I--hum--I necessarily make that appeal within limited bounds, or I--ha--should render legible, by that lady, what I desire to be blotted out. Am I selfish? Do I complain for my own sake? No. No. Principally for--ha hum--your sake, Amy.’\n\nThis last consideration plainly appeared, from his manner of pursuing it, to have just that instant come into his head.\n\n‘I said I was hurt. So I am. So I--ha--am determined to be, whatever is advanced to the contrary. I am hurt that my daughter, seated in the--hum--lap of fortune, should mope and retire and proclaim herself unequal to her destiny. I am hurt that she should--ha--systematically reproduce what the rest of us blot out; and seem--hum--I had almost said positively anxious--to announce to wealthy and distinguished society that she was born and bred in--ha hum--a place that I myself decline to name. But there is no inconsistency--ha--not the least, in my feeling hurt, and yet complaining principally for your sake, Amy. I do; I say again, I do. It is for your sake that I wish you, under the auspices of Mrs General, to form a--hum--a surface. It is for your sake that I wish you to have a--ha--truly refined mind, and (in the striking words of Mrs General) to be ignorant of everything that is not perfectly proper, placid, and pleasant.’\n\nHe had been running down by jerks, during his last speech, like a sort of ill-adjusted alarum. The touch was still upon his arm. He fell silent; and after looking about the ceiling again for a little while, looked down at her. Her head drooped, and he could not see her face; but her touch was tender and quiet, and in the expression of her dejected figure there was no blame--nothing but love. He began to whimper, just as he had done that night in the prison when she afterwards sat at his bedside till morning; exclaimed that he was a poor ruin and a poor wretch in the midst of his wealth; and clasped her in his arms. ‘Hush, hush, my own dear! Kiss me!’ was all she said to him. His tears were soon dried, much sooner than on the former occasion; and he was presently afterwards very high with his valet, as a way of righting himself for having shed any.\n\nWith one remarkable exception, to be recorded in its place, this was the only time, in his life of freedom and fortune, when he spoke to his daughter Amy of the old days.\n\nBut, now, the breakfast hour arrived; and with it Miss Fanny from her apartment, and Mr Edward from his apartment. Both these young persons of distinction were something the worse for late hours. As to Miss Fanny, she had become the victim of an insatiate mania for what she called ‘going into society;’ and would have gone into it head-foremost fifty times between sunset and sunrise, if so many opportunities had been at her disposal. As to Mr Edward, he, too, had a large acquaintance, and was generally engaged (for the most part, in diceing circles, or others of a kindred nature), during the greater part of every night. For this gentleman, when his fortunes changed, had stood at the great advantage of being already prepared for the highest associates, and having little to learn: so much was he indebted to the happy accidents which had made him acquainted with horse-dealing and billiard-marking.\n\nAt breakfast, Mr Frederick Dorrit likewise appeared. As the old gentleman inhabited the highest story of the palace, where he might have practised pistol-shooting without much chance of discovery by the other inmates, his younger niece had taken courage to propose the restoration to him of his clarionet, which Mr Dorrit had ordered to be confiscated, but which she had ventured to preserve. Notwithstanding some objections from Miss Fanny, that it was a low instrument, and that she detested the sound of it, the concession had been made. But it was then discovered that he had had enough of it, and never played it, now that it was no longer his means of getting bread. He had insensibly acquired a new habit of shuffling into the picture-galleries, always with his twisted paper of snuff in his hand (much to the indignation of Miss Fanny, who had proposed the purchase of a gold box for him that the family might not be discredited, which he had absolutely refused to carry when it was bought); and of passing hours and hours before the portraits of renowned Venetians. It was never made out what his dazed eyes saw in them; whether he had an interest in them merely as pictures, or whether he confusedly identified them with a glory that was departed, like the strength of his own mind. But he paid his court to them with great exactness, and clearly derived pleasure from the pursuit. After the first few days, Little Dorrit happened one morning to assist at these attentions. It so evidently heightened his gratification that she often accompanied him afterwards, and the greatest delight of which the old man had shown himself susceptible since his ruin, arose out of these excursions, when he would carry a chair about for her from picture to picture, and stand behind it, in spite of all her remonstrances, silently presenting her to the noble Venetians.\n\nIt fell out that, at this family breakfast, he referred to their having seen in a gallery, on the previous day, the lady and gentleman whom they had encountered on the Great Saint Bernard, ‘I forget the name,’ said he. ‘I dare say you remember them, William? I dare say you do, Edward?’\n\n‘_I_ remember ‘em well enough,’ said the latter.\n\n‘I should think so,’ observed Miss Fanny, with a toss of her head and a glance at her sister. ‘But they would not have been recalled to our remembrance, I suspect, if Uncle hadn’t tumbled over the subject.’\n\n‘My dear, what a curious phrase,’ said Mrs General. ‘Would not inadvertently lighted upon, or accidentally referred to, be better?’\n\n‘Thank you very much, Mrs General,’ returned the young lady, ‘no, I think not. On the whole I prefer my own expression.’\n\nThis was always Miss Fanny’s way of receiving a suggestion from Mrs General. But she always stored it up in her mind, and adopted it at another time.\n\n‘I should have mentioned our having met Mr and Mrs Gowan, Fanny,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘even if Uncle had not. I have scarcely seen you since, you know. I meant to have spoken of it at breakfast; because I should like to pay a visit to Mrs Gowan, and to become better acquainted with her, if Papa and Mrs General do not object.’\n\n‘Well, Amy,’ said Fanny, ‘I am sure I am glad to find you at last expressing a wish to become better acquainted with anybody in Venice. Though whether Mr and Mrs Gowan are desirable acquaintances, remains to be determined.’\n\n‘Mrs Gowan I spoke of, dear.’\n\n‘No doubt,’ said Fanny. ‘But you can’t separate her from her husband, I believe, without an Act of Parliament.’\n\n‘Do you think, Papa,’ inquired Little Dorrit, with diffidence and hesitation, ‘there is any objection to my making this visit?’\n\n‘Really,’ he replied, ‘I--ha--what is Mrs General’s view?’\n\nMrs General’s view was, that not having the honour of any acquaintance with the lady and gentleman referred to, she was not in a position to varnish the present article. She could only remark, as a general principle observed in the varnishing trade, that much depended on the quarter from which the lady under consideration was accredited to a family so conspicuously niched in the social temple as the family of Dorrit.\n\nAt this remark the face of Mr Dorrit gloomed considerably. He was about (connecting the accrediting with an obtrusive person of the name of Clennam, whom he imperfectly remembered in some former state of existence) to black-ball the name of Gowan finally, when Edward Dorrit, Esquire, came into the conversation, with his glass in his eye, and the preliminary remark of ‘I say--you there! Go out, will you!’--which was addressed to a couple of men who were handing the dishes round, as a courteous intimation that their services could be temporarily dispensed with.\n\nThose menials having obeyed the mandate, Edward Dorrit, Esquire, proceeded.\n\n‘Perhaps it’s a matter of policy to let you all know that these Gowans--in whose favour, or at least the gentleman’s, I can’t be supposed to be much prepossessed myself--are known to people of importance, if that makes any difference.’\n\n‘That, I would say,’ observed the fair varnisher, ‘Makes the greatest difference. The connection in question, being really people of importance and consideration--’\n\n‘As to that,’ said Edward Dorrit, Esquire, ‘I’ll give you the means of judging for yourself. You are acquainted, perhaps, with the famous name of Merdle?’\n\n‘The great Merdle!’ exclaimed Mrs General.\n\n‘_The_ Merdle,’ said Edward Dorrit, Esquire. ‘They are known to him. Mrs Gowan--I mean the dowager, my polite friend’s mother--is intimate with Mrs Merdle, and I know these two to be on their visiting list.’\n\n‘If so, a more undeniable guarantee could not be given,’ said Mrs General to Mr Dorrit, raising her gloves and bowing her head, as if she were doing homage to some visible graven image.\n\n‘I beg to ask my son, from motives of--ah--curiosity,’ Mr Dorrit observed, with a decided change in his manner, ‘how he becomes possessed of this--hum--timely information?’\n\n‘It’s not a long story, sir,’ returned Edward Dorrit, Esquire, ‘and you shall have it out of hand. To begin with, Mrs Merdle is the lady you had the parley with at what’s-his-name place.’\n\n‘Martigny,’ interposed Miss Fanny with an air of infinite languor.\n\n‘Martigny,’ assented her brother, with a slight nod and a slight wink; in acknowledgment of which, Miss Fanny looked surprised, and laughed and reddened.\n\n‘How can that be, Edward?’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘You informed me that the name of the gentleman with whom you conferred was--ha--Sparkler. Indeed, you showed me his card. Hum. Sparkler.’\n\n‘No doubt of it, father; but it doesn’t follow that his mother’s name must be the same. Mrs Merdle was married before, and he is her son. She is in Rome now; where probably we shall know more of her, as you decide to winter there. Sparkler is just come here. I passed last evening in company with Sparkler. Sparkler is a very good fellow on the whole, though rather a bore on one subject, in consequence of being tremendously smitten with a certain young lady.’ Here Edward Dorrit, Esquire, eyed Miss Fanny through his glass across the table. ‘We happened last night to compare notes about our travels, and I had the information I have given you from Sparkler himself.’ Here he ceased; continuing to eye Miss Fanny through his glass, with a face much twisted, and not ornamentally so, in part by the action of keeping his glass in his eye, and in part by the great subtlety of his smile.\n\n‘Under these circumstances,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I believe I express the sentiments of--ha--Mrs General, no less than my own, when I say that there is no objection, but--ha hum--quite the contrary--to your gratifying your desire, Amy. I trust I may--ha--hail--this desire,’ said Mr Dorrit, in an encouraging and forgiving manner, ‘as an auspicious omen. It is quite right to know these people. It is a very proper thing. Mr Merdle’s is a name of--ha--world-wide repute. Mr Merdle’s undertakings are immense. They bring him in such vast sums of money that they are regarded as--hum--national benefits. Mr Merdle is the man of this time. The name of Merdle is the name of the age. Pray do everything on my behalf that is civil to Mr and Mrs Gowan, for we will--ha--we will certainly notice them.’\n\nThis magnificent accordance of Mr Dorrit’s recognition settled the matter. It was not observed that Uncle had pushed away his plate, and forgotten his breakfast; but he was not much observed at any time, except by Little Dorrit. The servants were recalled, and the meal proceeded to its conclusion. Mrs General rose and left the table. Little Dorrit rose and left the table. When Edward and Fanny remained whispering together across it, and when Mr Dorrit remained eating figs and reading a French newspaper, Uncle suddenly fixed the attention of all three by rising out of his chair, striking his hand upon the table, and saying, ‘Brother! I protest against it!’\n\nIf he had made a proclamation in an unknown tongue, and given up the ghost immediately afterwards, he could not have astounded his audience more. The paper fell from Mr Dorrit’s hand, and he sat petrified, with a fig half way to his mouth.\n\n‘Brother!’ said the old man, conveying a surprising energy into his trembling voice, ‘I protest against it! I love you; you know I love you dearly. In these many years I have never been untrue to you in a single thought. Weak as I am, I would at any time have struck any man who spoke ill of you. But, brother, brother, brother, I protest against it!’\n\nIt was extraordinary to see of what a burst of earnestness such a decrepit man was capable. His eyes became bright, his grey hair rose on his head, markings of purpose on his brow and face which had faded from them for five-and-twenty years, started out again, and there was an energy in his hand that made its action nervous once more.\n\n‘My dear Frederick!’ exclaimed Mr Dorrit faintly. ‘What is wrong? What is the matter?’\n\n‘How dare you,’ said the old man, turning round on Fanny, ‘how dare you do it? Have you no memory? Have you no heart?’\n\n‘Uncle?’ cried Fanny, affrighted and bursting into tears, ‘why do you attack me in this cruel manner? What have I done?’\n\n‘Done?’ returned the old man, pointing to her sister’s place, ‘where’s your affectionate invaluable friend? Where’s your devoted guardian? Where’s your more than mother? How dare you set up superiorities against all these characters combined in your sister? For shame, you false girl, for shame!’\n\n‘I love Amy,’ cried Miss Fanny, sobbing and weeping, ‘as well as I love my life--better than I love my life. I don’t deserve to be so treated. I am as grateful to Amy, and as fond of Amy, as it’s possible for any human being to be. I wish I was dead. I never was so wickedly wronged. And only because I am anxious for the family credit.’\n\n‘To the winds with the family credit!’ cried the old man, with great scorn and indignation. ‘Brother, I protest against pride. I protest against ingratitude. I protest against any one of us here who have known what we have known, and have seen what we have seen, setting up any pretension that puts Amy at a moment’s disadvantage, or to the cost of a moment’s pain. We may know that it’s a base pretension by its having that effect. It ought to bring a judgment on us. Brother, I protest against it in the sight of God!’\n\nAs his hand went up above his head and came down on the table, it might have been a blacksmith’s. After a few moments’ silence, it had relaxed into its usual weak condition. He went round to his brother with his ordinary shuffling step, put the hand on his shoulder, and said, in a softened voice, ‘William, my dear, I felt obliged to say it; forgive me, for I felt obliged to say it!’ and then went, in his bowed way, out of the palace hall, just as he might have gone out of the Marshalsea room.\n\nAll this time Fanny had been sobbing and crying, and still continued to do so. Edward, beyond opening his mouth in amazement, had not opened his lips, and had done nothing but stare. Mr Dorrit also had been utterly discomfited, and quite unable to assert himself in any way. Fanny was now the first to speak.\n\n‘I never, never, never was so used!’ she sobbed. ‘There never was anything so harsh and unjustifiable, so disgracefully violent and cruel! Dear, kind, quiet little Amy, too, what would she feel if she could know that she had been innocently the means of exposing me to such treatment! But I’ll never tell her! No, good darling, I’ll never tell her!’\n\nThis helped Mr Dorrit to break his silence.\n\n‘My dear,’ said he, ‘I--ha--approve of your resolution. It will be--ha hum--much better not to speak of this to Amy. It might--hum--it might distress her. Ha. No doubt it would distress her greatly. It is considerate and right to avoid doing so. We will--ha--keep this to ourselves.’\n\n‘But the cruelty of Uncle!’ cried Miss Fanny. ‘O, I never can forgive the wanton cruelty of Uncle!’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, recovering his tone, though he remained unusually pale, ‘I must request you not to say so. You must remember that your uncle is--ha--not what he formerly was. You must remember that your uncle’s state requires--hum--great forbearance from us, great forbearance.’\n\n‘I am sure,’ cried Fanny, piteously, ‘it is only charitable to suppose that there must be something wrong in him somewhere, or he never could have so attacked Me, of all the people in the world.’\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit in a deeply fraternal tone, ‘you know, with his innumerable good points, what a--hum--wreck your uncle is; and, I entreat you by the fondness that I have for him, and by the fidelity that you know I have always shown him, to--ha--to draw your own conclusions, and to spare my brotherly feelings.’\n\nThis ended the scene; Edward Dorrit, Esquire, saying nothing throughout, but looking, to the last, perplexed and doubtful. Miss Fanny awakened much affectionate uneasiness in her sister’s mind that day by passing the greater part of it in violent fits of embracing her, and in alternately giving her brooches, and wishing herself dead.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. Something Right Somewhere\n\nTo be in the halting state of Mr Henry Gowan; to have left one of two powers in disgust; to want the necessary qualifications for finding promotion with another, and to be loitering moodily about on neutral ground, cursing both; is to be in a situation unwholesome for the mind, which time is not likely to improve. The worst class of sum worked in the every-day world is cyphered by the diseased arithmeticians who are always in the rule of Subtraction as to the merits and successes of others, and never in Addition as to their own.\n\nThe habit, too, of seeking some sort of recompense in the discontented boast of being disappointed, is a habit fraught with degeneracy. A certain idle carelessness and recklessness of consistency soon comes of it. To bring deserving things down by setting undeserving things up is one of its perverted delights; and there is no playing fast and loose with the truth, in any game, without growing the worse for it.\n\nIn his expressed opinions of all performances in the Art of painting that were completely destitute of merit, Gowan was the most liberal fellow on earth. He would declare such a man to have more power in his little finger (provided he had none), than such another had (provided he had much) in his whole mind and body. If the objection were taken that the thing commended was trash, he would reply, on behalf of his art, ‘My good fellow, what do we all turn out but trash? _I_ turn out nothing else, and I make you a present of the confession.’\n\nTo make a vaunt of being poor was another of the incidents of his splenetic state, though this may have had the design in it of showing that he ought to be rich; just as he would publicly laud and decry the Barnacles, lest it should be forgotten that he belonged to the family. Howbeit, these two subjects were very often on his lips; and he managed them so well that he might have praised himself by the month together, and not have made himself out half so important a man as he did by his light disparagement of his claims on anybody’s consideration.\n\nOut of this same airy talk of his, it always soon came to be understood, wherever he and his wife went, that he had married against the wishes of his exalted relations, and had had much ado to prevail on them to countenance her. He never made the representation, on the contrary seemed to laugh the idea to scorn; but it did happen that, with all his pains to depreciate himself, he was always in the superior position. From the days of their honeymoon, Minnie Gowan felt sensible of being usually regarded as the wife of a man who had made a descent in marrying her, but whose chivalrous love for her had cancelled that inequality.\n\nTo Venice they had been accompanied by Monsieur Blandois of Paris, and at Venice Monsieur Blandois of Paris was very much in the society of Gowan. When they had first met this gallant gentleman at Geneva, Gowan had been undecided whether to kick him or encourage him; and had remained for about four-and-twenty hours, so troubled to settle the point to his satisfaction, that he had thought of tossing up a five-franc piece on the terms, ‘Tails, kick; heads, encourage,’ and abiding by the voice of the oracle. It chanced, however, that his wife expressed a dislike to the engaging Blandois, and that the balance of feeling in the hotel was against him. Upon it, Gowan resolved to encourage him.\n\nWhy this perversity, if it were not in a generous fit?--which it was not. Why should Gowan, very much the superior of Blandois of Paris, and very well able to pull that prepossessing gentleman to pieces and find out the stuff he was made of, take up with such a man? In the first place, he opposed the first separate wish he observed in his wife, because her father had paid his debts and it was desirable to take an early opportunity of asserting his independence. In the second place, he opposed the prevalent feeling, because with many capacities of being otherwise, he was an ill-conditioned man. He found a pleasure in declaring that a courtier with the refined manners of Blandois ought to rise to the greatest distinction in any polished country. He found a pleasure in setting up Blandois as the type of elegance, and making him a satire upon others who piqued themselves on personal graces. He seriously protested that the bow of Blandois was perfect, that the address of Blandois was irresistible, and that the picturesque ease of Blandois would be cheaply purchased (if it were not a gift, and unpurchasable) for a hundred thousand francs. That exaggeration in the manner of the man which has been noticed as appertaining to him and to every such man, whatever his original breeding, as certainly as the sun belongs to this system, was acceptable to Gowan as a caricature, which he found it a humorous resource to have at hand for the ridiculing of numbers of people who necessarily did more or less of what Blandois overdid. Thus he had taken up with him; and thus, negligently strengthening these inclinations with habit, and idly deriving some amusement from his talk, he had glided into a way of having him for a companion. This, though he supposed him to live by his wits at play-tables and the like; though he suspected him to be a coward, while he himself was daring and courageous; though he thoroughly knew him to be disliked by Minnie; and though he cared so little for him, after all, that if he had given her any tangible personal cause to regard him with aversion, he would have had no compunction whatever in flinging him out of the highest window in Venice into the deepest water of the city.\n\nLittle Dorrit would have been glad to make her visit to Mrs Gowan, alone; but as Fanny, who had not yet recovered from her Uncle’s protest, though it was four-and-twenty hours of age, pressingly offered her company, the two sisters stepped together into one of the gondolas under Mr Dorrit’s window, and, with the courier in attendance, were taken in high state to Mrs Gowan’s lodging. In truth, their state was rather too high for the lodging, which was, as Fanny complained, ‘fearfully out of the way,’ and which took them through a complexity of narrow streets of water, which the same lady disparaged as ‘mere ditches.’\n\nThe house, on a little desert island, looked as if it had broken away from somewhere else, and had floated by chance into its present anchorage in company with a vine almost as much in want of training as the poor wretches who were lying under its leaves. The features of the surrounding picture were, a church with hoarding and scaffolding about it, which had been under suppositious repair so long that the means of repair looked a hundred years old, and had themselves fallen into decay; a quantity of washed linen, spread to dry in the sun; a number of houses at odds with one another and grotesquely out of the perpendicular, like rotten pre-Adamite cheeses cut into fantastic shapes and full of mites; and a feverish bewilderment of windows, with their lattice-blinds all hanging askew, and something draggled and dirty dangling out of most of them.\n\nOn the first-floor of the house was a Bank--a surprising experience for any gentleman of commercial pursuits bringing laws for all mankind from a British city--where two spare clerks, like dried dragoons, in green velvet caps adorned with golden tassels, stood, bearded, behind a small counter in a small room, containing no other visible objects than an empty iron-safe with the door open, a jug of water, and a papering of garland of roses; but who, on lawful requisition, by merely dipping their hands out of sight, could produce exhaustless mounds of five-franc pieces. Below the Bank was a suite of three or four rooms with barred windows, which had the appearance of a jail for criminal rats. Above the Bank was Mrs Gowan’s residence.\n\nNotwithstanding that its walls were blotched, as if missionary maps were bursting out of them to impart geographical knowledge; notwithstanding that its weird furniture was forlornly faded and musty, and that the prevailing Venetian odour of bilge water and an ebb tide on a weedy shore was very strong; the place was better within, than it promised. The door was opened by a smiling man like a reformed assassin--a temporary servant--who ushered them into the room where Mrs Gowan sat, with the announcement that two beautiful English ladies were come to see the mistress.\n\nMrs Gowan, who was engaged in needlework, put her work aside in a covered basket, and rose, a little hurriedly. Miss Fanny was excessively courteous to her, and said the usual nothings with the skill of a veteran.\n\n‘Papa was extremely sorry,’ proceeded Fanny, ‘to be engaged to-day (he is so much engaged here, our acquaintance being so wretchedly large!); and particularly requested me to bring his card for Mr Gowan. That I may be sure to acquit myself of a commission which he impressed upon me at least a dozen times, allow me to relieve my conscience by placing it on the table at once.’\n\nWhich she did with veteran ease.\n\n‘We have been,’ said Fanny, ‘charmed to understand that you know the Merdles. We hope it may be another means of bringing us together.’\n\n‘They are friends,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘of Mr Gowan’s family. I have not yet had the pleasure of a personal introduction to Mrs Merdle, but I suppose I shall be presented to her at Rome.’\n\n‘Indeed?’ returned Fanny, with an appearance of amiably quenching her own superiority. ‘I think you’ll like her.’\n\n‘You know her very well?’\n\n‘Why, you see,’ said Fanny, with a frank action of her pretty shoulders, ‘in London one knows every one. We met her on our way here, and, to say the truth, papa was at first rather cross with her for taking one of the rooms that our people had ordered for us. However, of course, that soon blew over, and we were all good friends again.’\n\nAlthough the visit had as yet given Little Dorrit no opportunity of conversing with Mrs Gowan, there was a silent understanding between them, which did as well. She looked at Mrs Gowan with keen and unabated interest; the sound of her voice was thrilling to her; nothing that was near her, or about her, or at all concerned her, escaped Little Dorrit. She was quicker to perceive the slightest matter here, than in any other case--but one.\n\n‘You have been quite well,’ she now said, ‘since that night?’\n\n‘Quite, my dear. And you?’\n\n‘Oh! I am always well,’ said Little Dorrit, timidly. ‘I--yes, thank you.’\n\nThere was no reason for her faltering and breaking off, other than that Mrs Gowan had touched her hand in speaking to her, and their looks had met. Something thoughtfully apprehensive in the large, soft eyes, had checked Little Dorrit in an instant.\n\n‘You don’t know that you are a favourite of my husband’s, and that I am almost bound to be jealous of you?’ said Mrs Gowan.\n\nLittle Dorrit, blushing, shook her head.\n\n‘He will tell you, if he tells you what he tells me, that you are quieter and quicker of resource than any one he ever saw.’\n\n‘He speaks far too well of me,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘I doubt that; but I don’t at all doubt that I must tell him you are here. I should never be forgiven, if I were to let you--and Miss Dorrit--go, without doing so. May I? You can excuse the disorder and discomfort of a painter’s studio?’\n\nThe inquiries were addressed to Miss Fanny, who graciously replied that she would be beyond anything interested and enchanted. Mrs Gowan went to a door, looked in beyond it, and came back. ‘Do Henry the favour to come in,’ said she, ‘I knew he would be pleased!’\n\nThe first object that confronted Little Dorrit, entering first, was Blandois of Paris in a great cloak and a furtive slouched hat, standing on a throne platform in a corner, as he had stood on the Great Saint Bernard, when the warning arms seemed to be all pointing up at him. She recoiled from this figure, as it smiled at her.\n\n‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said Gowan, coming from his easel behind the door. ‘It’s only Blandois. He is doing duty as a model to-day. I am making a study of him. It saves me money to turn him to some use. We poor painters have none to spare.’\n\nBlandois of Paris pulled off his slouched hat, and saluted the ladies without coming out of his corner.\n\n‘A thousand pardons!’ said he. ‘But the Professore here is so inexorable with me, that I am afraid to stir.’\n\n‘Don’t stir, then,’ said Gowan coolly, as the sisters approached the easel. ‘Let the ladies at least see the original of the daub, that they may know what it’s meant for. There he stands, you see. A bravo waiting for his prey, a distinguished noble waiting to save his country, the common enemy waiting to do somebody a bad turn, an angelic messenger waiting to do somebody a good turn--whatever you think he looks most like!’\n\n‘Say, Professore Mio, a poor gentleman waiting to do homage to elegance and beauty,’ remarked Blandois.\n\n‘Or say, Cattivo Soggetto Mio,’ returned Gowan, touching the painted face with his brush in the part where the real face had moved, ‘a murderer after the fact. Show that white hand of yours, Blandois. Put it outside the cloak. Keep it still.’\n\nBlandois’ hand was unsteady; but he laughed, and that would naturally shake it.\n\n‘He was formerly in some scuffle with another murderer, or with a victim, you observe,’ said Gowan, putting in the markings of the hand with a quick, impatient, unskilful touch, ‘and these are the tokens of it. Outside the cloak, man!--Corpo di San Marco, what are you thinking of?’\n\nBlandois of Paris shook with a laugh again, so that his hand shook more; now he raised it to twist his moustache, which had a damp appearance; and now he stood in the required position, with a little new swagger.\n\nHis face was so directed in reference to the spot where Little Dorrit stood by the easel, that throughout he looked at her. Once attracted by his peculiar eyes, she could not remove her own, and they had looked at each other all the time. She trembled now; Gowan, feeling it, and supposing her to be alarmed by the large dog beside him, whose head she caressed in her hand, and who had just uttered a low growl, glanced at her to say, ‘He won’t hurt you, Miss Dorrit.’\n\n‘I am not afraid of him,’ she returned in the same breath; ‘but will you look at him?’\n\nIn a moment Gowan had thrown down his brush, and seized the dog with both hands by the collar.\n\n‘Blandois! How can you be such a fool as to provoke him! By Heaven, and the other place too, he’ll tear you to bits! Lie down! Lion! Do you hear my voice, you rebel!’\n\nThe great dog, regardless of being half-choked by his collar, was obdurately pulling with his dead weight against his master, resolved to get across the room. He had been crouching for a spring at the moment when his master caught him.\n\n‘Lion! Lion!’ He was up on his hind legs, and it was a wrestle between master and dog. ‘Get back! Down, Lion! Get out of his sight, Blandois! What devil have you conjured into the dog?’\n\n‘I have done nothing to him.’\n\n‘Get out of his sight or I can’t hold the wild beast! Get out of the room! By my soul, he’ll kill you!’\n\nThe dog, with a ferocious bark, made one other struggle as Blandois vanished; then, in the moment of the dog’s submission, the master, little less angry than the dog, felled him with a blow on the head, and standing over him, struck him many times severely with the heel of his boot, so that his mouth was presently bloody.\n\n‘Now get you into that corner and lie down,’ said Gowan, ‘or I’ll take you out and shoot you.’\n\nLion did as he was ordered, and lay down licking his mouth and chest. Lion’s master stopped for a moment to take breath, and then, recovering his usual coolness of manner, turned to speak to his frightened wife and her visitors. Probably the whole occurrence had not occupied two minutes.\n\n‘Come, come, Minnie! You know he is always good-humoured and tractable. Blandois must have irritated him,--made faces at him. The dog has his likings and dislikings, and Blandois is no great favourite of his; but I am sure you will give him a character, Minnie, for never having been like this before.’\n\nMinnie was too much disturbed to say anything connected in reply; Little Dorrit was already occupied in soothing her; Fanny, who had cried out twice or thrice, held Gowan’s arm for protection; Lion, deeply ashamed of having caused them this alarm, came trailing himself along the ground to the feet of his mistress.\n\n‘You furious brute,’ said Gowan, striking him with his foot again. ‘You shall do penance for this.’ And he struck him again, and yet again.\n\n‘O, pray don’t punish him any more,’ cried Little Dorrit. ‘Don’t hurt him. See how gentle he is!’ At her entreaty, Gowan spared him; and he deserved her intercession, for truly he was as submissive, and as sorry, and as wretched as a dog could be.\n\nIt was not easy to recover this shock and make the visit unrestrained, even though Fanny had not been, under the best of circumstances, the least trifle in the way. In such further communication as passed among them before the sisters took their departure, Little Dorrit fancied it was revealed to her that Mr Gowan treated his wife, even in his very fondness, too much like a beautiful child. He seemed so unsuspicious of the depths of feeling which she knew must lie below that surface, that she doubted if there could be any such depths in himself. She wondered whether his want of earnestness might be the natural result of his want of such qualities, and whether it was with people as with ships, that, in too shallow and rocky waters, their anchors had no hold, and they drifted anywhere.\n\nHe attended them down the staircase, jocosely apologising for the poor quarters to which such poor fellows as himself were limited, and remarking that when the high and mighty Barnacles, his relatives, who would be dreadfully ashamed of them, presented him with better, he would live in better to oblige them. At the water’s edge they were saluted by Blandois, who looked white enough after his late adventure, but who made very light of it notwithstanding,--laughing at the mention of Lion.\n\nLeaving the two together under the scrap of vine upon the causeway, Gowan idly scattering the leaves from it into the water, and Blandois lighting a cigarette, the sisters were paddled away in state as they had come. They had not glided on for many minutes, when Little Dorrit became aware that Fanny was more showy in manner than the occasion appeared to require, and, looking about for the cause through the window and through the open door, saw another gondola evidently in waiting on them.\n\nAs this gondola attended their progress in various artful ways; sometimes shooting on a-head, and stopping to let them pass; sometimes, when the way was broad enough, skimming along side by side with them; and sometimes following close astern; and as Fanny gradually made no disguise that she was playing off graces upon somebody within it, of whom she at the same time feigned to be unconscious; Little Dorrit at length asked who it was?\n\nTo which Fanny made the short answer, ‘That gaby.’\n\n‘Who?’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘My dear child,’ returned Fanny (in a tone suggesting that before her Uncle’s protest she might have said, You little fool, instead), ‘how slow you are! Young Sparkler.’\n\nShe lowered the window on her side, and, leaning back and resting her elbow on it negligently, fanned herself with a rich Spanish fan of black and gold. The attendant gondola, having skimmed forward again, with some swift trace of an eye in the window, Fanny laughed coquettishly and said, ‘Did you ever see such a fool, my love?’\n\n‘Do you think he means to follow you all the way?’ asked Little Dorrit.\n\n‘My precious child,’ returned Fanny, ‘I can’t possibly answer for what an idiot in a state of desperation may do, but I should think it highly probable. It’s not such an enormous distance. All Venice would scarcely be that, I imagine, if he’s dying for a glimpse of me.’\n\n‘And is he?’ asked Little Dorrit in perfect simplicity.\n\n‘Well, my love, that really is an awkward question for me to answer,’ said her sister. ‘I believe he is. You had better ask Edward. He tells Edward he is, I believe. I understand he makes a perfect spectacle of himself at the Casino, and that sort of places, by going on about me. But you had better ask Edward if you want to know.’\n\n‘I wonder he doesn’t call,’ said Little Dorrit after thinking a moment.\n\n‘My dear Amy, your wonder will soon cease, if I am rightly informed. I should not be at all surprised if he called to-day. The creature has only been waiting to get his courage up, I suspect.’\n\n‘Will you see him?’\n\n‘Indeed, my darling,’ said Fanny, ‘that’s just as it may happen. Here he is again. Look at him. O, you simpleton!’\n\nMr Sparkler had, undeniably, a weak appearance; with his eye in the window like a knot in the glass, and no reason on earth for stopping his bark suddenly, except the real reason.\n\n‘When you asked me if I will see him, my dear,’ said Fanny, almost as well composed in the graceful indifference of her attitude as Mrs Merdle herself, ‘what do you mean?’\n\n‘I mean,’ said Little Dorrit--‘I think I rather mean what do you mean, dear Fanny?’\n\nFanny laughed again, in a manner at once condescending, arch, and affable; and said, putting her arm round her sister in a playfully affectionate way:\n\n‘Now tell me, my little pet. When we saw that woman at Martigny, how did you think she carried it off? Did you see what she decided on in a moment?’\n‘No, Fanny.’\n\n‘Then I’ll tell you, Amy. She settled with herself, now I’ll never refer to that meeting under such different circumstances, and I’ll never pretend to have any idea that these are the same girls. That’s _her_ way out of a difficulty. What did I tell you when we came away from Harley Street that time? She is as insolent and false as any woman in the world. But in the first capacity, my love, she may find people who can match her.’\n\nA significant turn of the Spanish fan towards Fanny’s bosom, indicated with great expression where one of these people was to be found.\n\n‘Not only that,’ pursued Fanny, ‘but she gives the same charge to Young Sparkler; and doesn’t let him come after me until she has got it thoroughly into his most ridiculous of all ridiculous noddles (for one really can’t call it a head), that he is to pretend to have been first struck with me in that Inn Yard.’\n\n‘Why?’ asked Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Why? Good gracious, my love!’ (again very much in the tone of You stupid little creature) ‘how can you ask? Don’t you see that I may have become a rather desirable match for a noddle? And don’t you see that she puts the deception upon us, and makes a pretence, while she shifts it from her own shoulders (very good shoulders they are too, I must say),’ observed Miss Fanny, glancing complacently at herself, ‘of considering our feelings?’\n\n‘But we can always go back to the plain truth.’\n\n‘Yes, but if you please we won’t,’ retorted Fanny. ‘No; I am not going to have that done, Amy. The pretext is none of mine; it’s hers, and she shall have enough of it.’\n\nIn the triumphant exaltation of her feelings, Miss Fanny, using her Spanish fan with one hand, squeezed her sister’s waist with the other, as if she were crushing Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘No,’ repeated Fanny. ‘She shall find me go her way. She took it, and I’ll follow it. And, with the blessing of fate and fortune, I’ll go on improving that woman’s acquaintance until I have given her maid, before her eyes, things from my dressmaker’s ten times as handsome and expensive as she once gave me from hers!’\n\nLittle Dorrit was silent; sensible that she was not to be heard on any question affecting the family dignity, and unwilling to lose to no purpose her sister’s newly and unexpectedly restored favour. She could not concur, but she was silent. Fanny well knew what she was thinking of; so well, that she soon asked her.\n\nHer reply was, ‘Do you mean to encourage Mr Sparkler, Fanny?’\n\n‘Encourage him, my dear?’ said her sister, smiling contemptuously, ‘that depends upon what you call encourage. No, I don’t mean to encourage him. But I’ll make a slave of him.’\n\nLittle Dorrit glanced seriously and doubtfully in her face, but Fanny was not to be so brought to a check. She furled her fan of black and gold, and used it to tap her sister’s nose; with the air of a proud beauty and a great spirit, who toyed with and playfully instructed a homely companion.\n\n‘I shall make him fetch and carry, my dear, and I shall make him subject to me. And if I don’t make his mother subject to me, too, it shall not be my fault.’\n\n‘Do you think--dear Fanny, don’t be offended, we are so comfortable together now--that you can quite see the end of that course?’\n\n‘I can’t say I have so much as looked for it yet, my dear,’ answered Fanny, with supreme indifference; ‘all in good time. Such are my intentions. And really they have taken me so long to develop, that here we are at home. And Young Sparkler at the door, inquiring who is within. By the merest accident, of course!’\n\nIn effect, the swain was standing up in his gondola, card-case in hand, affecting to put the question to a servant. This conjunction of circumstances led to his immediately afterwards presenting himself before the young ladies in a posture, which in ancient times would not have been considered one of favourable augury for his suit; since the gondoliers of the young ladies, having been put to some inconvenience by the chase, so neatly brought their own boat in the gentlest collision with the bark of Mr Sparkler, as to tip that gentleman over like a larger species of ninepin, and cause him to exhibit the soles of his shoes to the object of his dearest wishes: while the nobler portions of his anatomy struggled at the bottom of his boat in the arms of one of his men.\n\nHowever, as Miss Fanny called out with much concern, Was the gentleman hurt, Mr Sparkler rose more restored than might have been expected, and stammered for himself with blushes, ‘Not at all so.’ Miss Fanny had no recollection of having ever seen him before, and was passing on, with a distant inclination of her head, when he announced himself by name. Even then she was in a difficulty from being unable to call it to mind, until he explained that he had had the honour of seeing her at Martigny. Then she remembered him, and hoped his lady-mother was well.\n\n‘Thank you,’ stammered Mr Sparkler, ‘she’s uncommonly well--at least, poorly.’\n\n‘In Venice?’ said Miss Fanny.\n\n‘In Rome,’ Mr Sparkler answered. ‘I am here by myself, myself. I came to call upon Mr Edward Dorrit myself. Indeed, upon Mr Dorrit likewise. In fact, upon the family.’\n\nTurning graciously to the attendants, Miss Fanny inquired whether her papa or brother was within? The reply being that they were both within, Mr Sparkler humbly offered his arm. Miss Fanny accepting it, was squired up the great staircase by Mr Sparkler, who, if he still believed (which there is not any reason to doubt) that she had no nonsense about her, rather deceived himself.\n\nArrived in a mouldering reception-room, where the faded hangings, of a sad sea-green, had worn and withered until they looked as if they might have claimed kindred with the waifs of seaweed drifting under the windows, or clinging to the walls and weeping for their imprisoned relations, Miss Fanny despatched emissaries for her father and brother. Pending whose appearance, she showed to great advantage on a sofa, completing Mr Sparkler’s conquest with some remarks upon Dante--known to that gentleman as an eccentric man in the nature of an Old File, who used to put leaves round his head, and sit upon a stool for some unaccountable purpose, outside the cathedral at Florence.\n\nMr Dorrit welcomed the visitor with the highest urbanity, and most courtly manners. He inquired particularly after Mrs Merdle. He inquired particularly after Mr Merdle. Mr Sparkler said, or rather twitched out of himself in small pieces by the shirt-collar, that Mrs Merdle having completely used up her place in the country, and also her house at Brighton, and being, of course, unable, don’t you see, to remain in London when there wasn’t a soul there, and not feeling herself this year quite up to visiting about at people’s places, had resolved to have a touch at Rome, where a woman like herself, with a proverbially fine appearance, and with no nonsense about her, couldn’t fail to be a great acquisition. As to Mr Merdle, he was so much wanted by the men in the City and the rest of those places, and was such a doosed extraordinary phenomenon in Buying and Banking and that, that Mr Sparkler doubted if the monetary system of the country would be able to spare him; though that his work was occasionally one too many for him, and that he would be all the better for a temporary shy at an entirely new scene and climate, Mr Sparkler did not conceal. As to himself, Mr Sparkler conveyed to the Dorrit family that he was going, on rather particular business, wherever they were going.\n\nThis immense conversational achievement required time, but was effected. Being effected, Mr Dorrit expressed his hope that Mr Sparkler would shortly dine with them. Mr Sparkler received the idea so kindly that Mr Dorrit asked what he was going to do that day, for instance? As he was going to do nothing that day (his usual occupation, and one for which he was particularly qualified), he was secured without postponement; being further bound over to accompany the ladies to the Opera in the evening.\n\nAt dinner-time Mr Sparkler rose out of the sea, like Venus’s son taking after his mother, and made a splendid appearance ascending the great staircase. If Fanny had been charming in the morning, she was now thrice charming, very becomingly dressed in her most suitable colours, and with an air of negligence upon her that doubled Mr Sparkler’s fetters, and riveted them.\n\n‘I hear you are acquainted, Mr Sparkler,’ said his host at dinner, ‘with--ha--Mr Gowan. Mr Henry Gowan?’\n\n‘Perfectly, sir,’ returned Mr Sparkler. ‘His mother and my mother are cronies in fact.’\n\n‘If I had thought of it, Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a patronage as magnificent as that of Lord Decimus himself, ‘you should have despatched a note to them, asking them to dine to-day. Some of our people could have--ha--fetched them, and taken them home. We could have spared a--hum--gondola for that purpose. I am sorry to have forgotten this. Pray remind me of them to-morrow.’\n\nLittle Dorrit was not without doubts how Mr Henry Gowan might take their patronage; but she promised not to fail in the reminder.\n\n‘Pray, does Mr Henry Gowan paint--ha--Portraits?’ inquired Mr Dorrit.\n\nMr Sparkler opined that he painted anything, if he could get the job.\n\n‘He has no particular walk?’ said Mr Dorrit.\n\nMr Sparkler, stimulated by Love to brilliancy, replied that for a particular walk a man ought to have a particular pair of shoes; as, for example, shooting, shooting-shoes; cricket, cricket-shoes. Whereas, he believed that Henry Gowan had no particular pair of shoes.\n\n‘No speciality?’ said Mr Dorrit.\n\nThis being a very long word for Mr Sparkler, and his mind being exhausted by his late effort, he replied, ‘No, thank you. I seldom take it.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘It would be very agreeable to me to present a gentleman so connected, with some--ha--Testimonial of my desire to further his interests, and develop the--hum--germs of his genius. I think I must engage Mr Gowan to paint my picture. If the result should be--ha--mutually satisfactory, I might afterwards engage him to try his hand upon my family.’\n\nThe exquisitely bold and original thought presented itself to Mr Sparkler, that there was an opening here for saying there were some of the family (emphasising ‘some’ in a marked manner) to whom no painter could render justice. But, for want of a form of words in which to express the idea, it returned to the skies.\n\nThis was the more to be regretted as Miss Fanny greatly applauded the notion of the portrait, and urged her papa to act upon it. She surmised, she said, that Mr Gowan had lost better and higher opportunities by marrying his pretty wife; and Love in a cottage, painting pictures for dinner, was so delightfully interesting, that she begged her papa to give him the commission whether he could paint a likeness or not: though indeed both she and Amy knew he could, from having seen a speaking likeness on his easel that day, and having had the opportunity of comparing it with the original. These remarks made Mr Sparkler (as perhaps they were intended to do) nearly distracted; for while on the one hand they expressed Miss Fanny’s susceptibility of the tender passion, she herself showed such an innocent unconsciousness of his admiration that his eyes goggled in his head with jealousy of an unknown rival.\n\nDescending into the sea again after dinner, and ascending out of it at the Opera staircase, preceded by one of their gondoliers, like an attendant Merman, with a great linen lantern, they entered their box, and Mr Sparkler entered on an evening of agony. The theatre being dark, and the box light, several visitors lounged in during the representation; in whom Fanny was so interested, and in conversation with whom she fell into such charming attitudes, as she had little confidences with them, and little disputes concerning the identity of people in distant boxes, that the wretched Sparkler hated all mankind. But he had two consolations at the close of the performance. She gave him her fan to hold while she adjusted her cloak, and it was his blessed privilege to give her his arm down-stairs again. These crumbs of encouragement, Mr Sparkler thought, would just keep him going; and it is not impossible that Miss Dorrit thought so too.\n\nThe Merman with his light was ready at the box-door, and other Mermen with other lights were ready at many of the doors. The Dorrit Merman held his lantern low, to show the steps, and Mr Sparkler put on another heavy set of fetters over his former set, as he watched her radiant feet twinkling down the stairs beside him. Among the loiterers here, was Blandois of Paris. He spoke, and moved forward beside Fanny.\n\nLittle Dorrit was in front with her brother and Mrs General (Mr Dorrit had remained at home), but on the brink of the quay they all came together. She started again to find Blandois close to her, handing Fanny into the boat.\n\n‘Gowan has had a loss,’ he said, ‘since he was made happy to-day by a visit from fair ladies.’\n\n‘A loss?’ repeated Fanny, relinquished by the bereaved Sparkler, and taking her seat.\n\n‘A loss,’ said Blandois. ‘His dog Lion.’\n\nLittle Dorrit’s hand was in his, as he spoke.\n\n‘He is dead,’ said Blandois.\n\n‘Dead?’ echoed Little Dorrit. ‘That noble dog?’\n\n‘Faith, dear ladies!’ said Blandois, smiling and shrugging his shoulders, ‘somebody has poisoned that noble dog. He is as dead as the Doges!’\n\n# CHAPTER 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism\n\nMrs General, always on her coach-box keeping the proprieties well together, took pains to form a surface on her very dear young friend, and Mrs General’s very dear young friend tried hard to receive it. Hard as she had tried in her laborious life to attain many ends, she had never tried harder than she did now, to be varnished by Mrs General. It made her anxious and ill at ease to be operated upon by that smoothing hand, it is true; but she submitted herself to the family want in its greatness as she had submitted herself to the family want in its littleness, and yielded to her own inclinations in this thing no more than she had yielded to her hunger itself, in the days when she had saved her dinner that her father might have his supper.\n\nOne comfort that she had under the Ordeal by General was more sustaining to her, and made her more grateful than to a less devoted and affectionate spirit, not habituated to her struggles and sacrifices, might appear quite reasonable; and, indeed, it may often be observed in life, that spirits like Little Dorrit do not appear to reason half as carefully as the folks who get the better of them. The continued kindness of her sister was this comfort to Little Dorrit. It was nothing to her that the kindness took the form of tolerant patronage; she was used to that. It was nothing to her that it kept her in a tributary position, and showed her in attendance on the flaming car in which Miss Fanny sat on an elevated seat, exacting homage; she sought no better place. Always admiring Fanny’s beauty, and grace, and readiness, and not now asking herself how much of her disposition to be strongly attached to Fanny was due to her own heart, and how much to Fanny’s, she gave her all the sisterly fondness her great heart contained.\n\nThe wholesale amount of Prunes and Prism which Mrs General infused into the family life, combined with the perpetual plunges made by Fanny into society, left but a very small residue of any natural deposit at the bottom of the mixture. This rendered confidences with Fanny doubly precious to Little Dorrit, and heightened the relief they afforded her.\n\n‘Amy,’ said Fanny to her one night when they were alone, after a day so tiring that Little Dorrit was quite worn out, though Fanny would have taken another dip into society with the greatest pleasure in life, ‘I am going to put something into your little head. You won’t guess what it is, I suspect.’\n\n‘I don’t think that’s likely, dear,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Come, I’ll give you a clue, child,’ said Fanny. ‘Mrs General.’\n\nPrunes and Prism, in a thousand combinations, having been wearily in the ascendant all day--everything having been surface and varnish and show without substance--Little Dorrit looked as if she had hoped that Mrs General was safely tucked up in bed for some hours.\n\n‘_Now_, can you guess, Amy?’ said Fanny.\n\n‘No, dear. Unless I have done anything,’ said Little Dorrit, rather alarmed, and meaning anything calculated to crack varnish and ruffle surface.\n\nFanny was so very much amused by the misgiving, that she took up her favourite fan (being then seated at her dressing-table with her armoury of cruel instruments about her, most of them reeking from the heart of Sparkler), and tapped her sister frequently on the nose with it, laughing all the time.\n\n‘Oh, our Amy, our Amy!’ said Fanny. ‘What a timid little goose our Amy is! But this is nothing to laugh at. On the contrary, I am very cross, my dear.’\n\n‘As it is not with me, Fanny, I don’t mind,’ returned her sister, smiling.\n\n‘Ah! But I do mind,’ said Fanny, ‘and so will you, Pet, when I enlighten you. Amy, has it never struck you that somebody is monstrously polite to Mrs General?’\n\n‘Everybody is polite to Mrs General,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘Because--’\n\n‘Because she freezes them into it?’ interrupted Fanny. ‘I don’t mean that; quite different from that. Come! Has it never struck you, Amy, that Pa is monstrously polite to Mrs General.’\n\nAmy, murmuring ‘No,’ looked quite confounded.\n\n‘No; I dare say not. But he is,’ said Fanny. ‘He is, Amy. And remember my words. Mrs General has designs on Pa!’\n\n‘Dear Fanny, do you think it possible that Mrs General has designs on any one?’\n\n‘Do I think it possible?’ retorted Fanny. ‘My love, I know it. I tell you she has designs on Pa. And more than that, I tell you Pa considers her such a wonder, such a paragon of accomplishment, and such an acquisition to our family, that he is ready to get himself into a state of perfect infatuation with her at any moment. And that opens a pretty picture of things, I hope? Think of me with Mrs General for a Mama!’\n\nLittle Dorrit did not reply, ‘Think of me with Mrs General for a Mama;’ but she looked anxious, and seriously inquired what had led Fanny to these conclusions.\n\n‘Lord, my darling,’ said Fanny, tartly. ‘You might as well ask me how I know when a man is struck with myself! But, of course I do know. It happens pretty often: but I always know it. I know this in much the same way, I suppose. At all events, I know it.’\n\n‘You never heard Papa say anything?’\n\n‘Say anything?’ repeated Fanny. ‘My dearest, darling child, what necessity has he had, yet awhile, to say anything?’\n\n‘And you have never heard Mrs General say anything?’\n\n‘My goodness me, Amy,’ returned Fanny, ‘is she the sort of woman to say anything? Isn’t it perfectly plain and clear that she has nothing to do at present but to hold herself upright, keep her aggravating gloves on, and go sweeping about? Say anything! If she had the ace of trumps in her hand at whist, she wouldn’t say anything, child. It would come out when she played it.’\n\n‘At least, you may be mistaken, Fanny. Now, may you not?’\n\n‘O yes, I _may_ be,’ said Fanny, ‘but I am not. However, I am glad you can contemplate such an escape, my dear, and I am glad that you can take this for the present with sufficient coolness to think of such a chance. It makes me hope that you may be able to bear the connection. I should not be able to bear it, and I should not try. I’d marry young Sparkler first.’\n\n‘O, you would never marry him, Fanny, under any circumstances.’\n\n‘Upon my word, my dear,’ rejoined that young lady with exceeding indifference, ‘I wouldn’t positively answer even for that. There’s no knowing what might happen. Especially as I should have many opportunities, afterwards, of treating that woman, his mother, in her own style. Which I most decidedly should not be slow to avail myself of, Amy.’\n\nNo more passed between the sisters then; but what had passed gave the two subjects of Mrs General and Mr Sparkler great prominence in Little Dorrit’s mind, and thenceforth she thought very much of both.\n\nMrs General, having long ago formed her own surface to such perfection that it hid whatever was below it (if anything), no observation was to be made in that quarter. Mr Dorrit was undeniably very polite to her and had a high opinion of her; but Fanny, impetuous at most times, might easily be wrong for all that. Whereas, the Sparkler question was on the different footing that any one could see what was going on there, and Little Dorrit saw it and pondered on it with many doubts and wonderings.\n\nThe devotion of Mr Sparkler was only to be equalled by the caprice and cruelty of his enslaver. Sometimes she would prefer him to such distinction of notice, that he would chuckle aloud with joy; next day, or next hour, she would overlook him so completely, and drop him into such an abyss of obscurity, that he would groan under a weak pretence of coughing. The constancy of his attendance never touched Fanny: though he was so inseparable from Edward, that, when that gentleman wished for a change of society, he was under the irksome necessity of gliding out like a conspirator in disguised boats and by secret doors and back ways; though he was so solicitous to know how Mr Dorrit was, that he called every other day to inquire, as if Mr Dorrit were the prey of an intermittent fever; though he was so constantly being paddled up and down before the principal windows, that he might have been supposed to have made a wager for a large stake to be paddled a thousand miles in a thousand hours; though whenever the gondola of his mistress left the gate, the gondola of Mr Sparkler shot out from some watery ambush and gave chase, as if she were a fair smuggler and he a custom-house officer. It was probably owing to this fortification of the natural strength of his constitution with so much exposure to the air, and the salt sea, that Mr Sparkler did not pine outwardly; but, whatever the cause, he was so far from having any prospect of moving his mistress by a languishing state of health, that he grew bluffer every day, and that peculiarity in his appearance of seeming rather a swelled boy than a young man, became developed to an extraordinary degree of ruddy puffiness.\n\nBlandois calling to pay his respects, Mr Dorrit received him with affability as the friend of Mr Gowan, and mentioned to him his idea of commissioning Mr Gowan to transmit him to posterity. Blandois highly extolling it, it occurred to Mr Dorrit that it might be agreeable to Blandois to communicate to his friend the great opportunity reserved for him. Blandois accepted the commission with his own free elegance of manner, and swore he would discharge it before he was an hour older. On his imparting the news to Gowan, that Master gave Mr Dorrit to the Devil with great liberality some round dozen of times (for he resented patronage almost as much as he resented the want of it), and was inclined to quarrel with his friend for bringing him the message.\n\n‘It may be a defect in my mental vision, Blandois,’ said he, ‘but may I die if I see what you have to do with this.’\n\n‘Death of my life,’ replied Blandois, ‘nor I neither, except that I thought I was serving my friend.’\n\n‘By putting an upstart’s hire in his pocket?’ said Gowan, frowning. ‘Do you mean that? Tell your other friend to get his head painted for the sign of some public-house, and to get it done by a sign-painter. Who am I, and who is he?’\n\n‘Professore,’ returned the ambassador, ‘and who is Blandois?’\n\nWithout appearing at all interested in the latter question, Gowan angrily whistled Mr Dorrit away. But, next day, he resumed the subject by saying in his off-hand manner and with a slighting laugh, ‘Well, Blandois, when shall we go to this Maecenas of yours? We journeymen must take jobs when we can get them. When shall we go and look after this job?’\n\n‘When you will,’ said the injured Blandois, ‘as you please. What have I to do with it? What is it to me?’\n\n‘I can tell you what it is to me,’ said Gowan. ‘Bread and cheese. One must eat! So come along, my Blandois.’\n\nMr Dorrit received them in the presence of his daughters and of Mr Sparkler, who happened, by some surprising accident, to be calling there. ‘How are you, Sparkler?’ said Gowan carelessly. ‘When you have to live by your mother wit, old boy, I hope you may get on better than I do.’\n\nMr Dorrit then mentioned his proposal. ‘Sir,’ said Gowan, laughing, after receiving it gracefully enough, ‘I am new to the trade, and not expert at its mysteries. I believe I ought to look at you in various lights, tell you you are a capital subject, and consider when I shall be sufficiently disengaged to devote myself with the necessary enthusiasm to the fine picture I mean to make of you. I assure you,’ and he laughed again, ‘I feel quite a traitor in the camp of those dear, gifted, good, noble fellows, my brother artists, by not doing the hocus-pocus better. But I have not been brought up to it, and it’s too late to learn it. Now, the fact is, I am a very bad painter, but not much worse than the generality. If you are going to throw away a hundred guineas or so, I am as poor as a poor relation of great people usually is, and I shall be very much obliged to you, if you’ll throw them away upon me. I’ll do the best I can for the money; and if the best should be bad, why even then, you may probably have a bad picture with a small name to it, instead of a bad picture with a large name to it.’\n\nThis tone, though not what he had expected, on the whole suited Mr Dorrit remarkably well. It showed that the gentleman, highly connected, and not a mere workman, would be under an obligation to him. He expressed his satisfaction in placing himself in Mr Gowan’s hands, and trusted that he would have the pleasure, in their characters of private gentlemen, of improving his acquaintance.\n\n‘You are very good,’ said Gowan. ‘I have not forsworn society since I joined the brotherhood of the brush (the most delightful fellows on the face of the earth), and am glad enough to smell the old fine gunpowder now and then, though it did blow me into mid-air and my present calling. You’ll not think, Mr Dorrit,’ and here he laughed again in the easiest way, ‘that I am lapsing into the freemasonry of the craft--for it’s not so; upon my life I can’t help betraying it wherever I go, though, by Jupiter, I love and honour the craft with all my might--if I propose a stipulation as to time and place?’\n\nHa! Mr Dorrit could erect no--hum--suspicion of that kind on Mr Gowan’s frankness.\n\n‘Again you are very good,’ said Gowan. ‘Mr Dorrit, I hear you are going to Rome. I am going to Rome, having friends there. Let me begin to do you the injustice I have conspired to do you, there--not here. We shall all be hurried during the rest of our stay here; and though there’s not a poorer man with whole elbows in Venice, than myself, I have not quite got all the Amateur out of me yet--comprising the trade again, you see!--and can’t fall on to order, in a hurry, for the mere sake of the sixpences.’\n\nThese remarks were not less favourably received by Mr Dorrit than their predecessors. They were the prelude to the first reception of Mr and Mrs Gowan at dinner, and they skilfully placed Gowan on his usual ground in the new family.\n\nHis wife, too, they placed on her usual ground. Miss Fanny understood, with particular distinctness, that Mrs Gowan’s good looks had cost her husband very dear; that there had been a great disturbance about her in the Barnacle family; and that the Dowager Mrs Gowan, nearly heart-broken, had resolutely set her face against the marriage until overpowered by her maternal feelings. Mrs General likewise clearly understood that the attachment had occasioned much family grief and dissension. Of honest Mr Meagles no mention was made; except that it was natural enough that a person of that sort should wish to raise his daughter out of his own obscurity, and that no one could blame him for trying his best to do so.\n\nLittle Dorrit’s interest in the fair subject of this easily accepted belief was too earnest and watchful to fail in accurate observation. She could see that it had its part in throwing upon Mrs Gowan the touch of a shadow under which she lived, and she even had an instinctive knowledge that there was not the least truth in it. But it had an influence in placing obstacles in the way of her association with Mrs Gowan by making the Prunes and Prism school excessively polite to her, but not very intimate with her; and Little Dorrit, as an enforced sizar of that college, was obliged to submit herself humbly to its ordinances.\n\nNevertheless, there was a sympathetic understanding already established between the two, which would have carried them over greater difficulties, and made a friendship out of a more restricted intercourse. As though accidents were determined to be favourable to it, they had a new assurance of congeniality in the aversion which each perceived that the other felt towards Blandois of Paris; an aversion amounting to the repugnance and horror of a natural antipathy towards an odious creature of the reptile kind.\n\nAnd there was a passive congeniality between them, besides this active one. To both of them, Blandois behaved in exactly the same manner; and to both of them his manner had uniformly something in it, which they both knew to be different from his bearing towards others. The difference was too minute in its expression to be perceived by others, but they knew it to be there. A mere trick of his evil eyes, a mere turn of his smooth white hand, a mere hair’s-breadth of addition to the fall of his nose and the rise of the moustache in the most frequent movement of his face, conveyed to both of them, equally, a swagger personal to themselves. It was as if he had said, ‘I have a secret power in this quarter. I know what I know.’\n\nThis had never been felt by them both in so great a degree, and never by each so perfectly to the knowledge of the other, as on a day when he came to Mr Dorrit’s to take his leave before quitting Venice. Mrs Gowan was herself there for the same purpose, and he came upon the two together; the rest of the family being out. The two had not been together five minutes, and the peculiar manner seemed to convey to them, ‘You were going to talk about me. Ha! Behold me here to prevent it!’\n\n‘Gowan is coming here?’ said Blandois, with a smile.\n\nMrs Gowan replied he was not coming.\n\n‘Not coming!’ said Blandois. ‘Permit your devoted servant, when you leave here, to escort you home.’\n\n‘Thank you: I am not going home.’\n\n‘Not going home!’ said Blandois. ‘Then I am forlorn.’\n\nThat he might be; but he was not so forlorn as to roam away and leave them together. He sat entertaining them with his finest compliments, and his choicest conversation; but he conveyed to them, all the time, ‘No, no, no, dear ladies. Behold me here expressly to prevent it!’\n\nHe conveyed it to them with so much meaning, and he had such a diabolical persistency in him, that at length, Mrs Gowan rose to depart. On his offering his hand to Mrs Gowan to lead her down the staircase, she retained Little Dorrit’s hand in hers, with a cautious pressure, and said, ‘No, thank you. But, if you will please to see if my boatman is there, I shall be obliged to you.’\n\nIt left him no choice but to go down before them. As he did so, hat in hand, Mrs Gowan whispered:\n\n‘He killed the dog.’\n\n‘Does Mr Gowan know it?’ Little Dorrit whispered.\n\n‘No one knows it. Don’t look towards me; look towards him. He will turn his face in a moment. No one knows it, but I am sure he did. You are?’\n\n‘I--I think so,’ Little Dorrit answered.\n\n‘Henry likes him, and he will not think ill of him; he is so generous and open himself. But you and I feel sure that we think of him as he deserves. He argued with Henry that the dog had been already poisoned when he changed so, and sprang at him. Henry believes it, but we do not. I see he is listening, but can’t hear. Good-bye, my love! Good-bye!’\n\nThe last words were spoken aloud, as the vigilant Blandois stopped, turned his head, and looked at them from the bottom of the staircase. Assuredly he did look then, though he looked his politest, as if any real philanthropist could have desired no better employment than to lash a great stone to his neck, and drop him into the water flowing beyond the dark arched gateway in which he stood. No such benefactor to mankind being on the spot, he handed Mrs Gowan to her boat, and stood there until it had shot out of the narrow view; when he handed himself into his own boat and followed.\n\nLittle Dorrit had sometimes thought, and now thought again as she retraced her steps up the staircase, that he had made his way too easily into her father’s house. But so many and such varieties of people did the same, through Mr Dorrit’s participation in his elder daughter’s society mania, that it was hardly an exceptional case. A perfect fury for making acquaintances on whom to impress their riches and importance, had seized the House of Dorrit.\n\nIt appeared on the whole, to Little Dorrit herself, that this same society in which they lived, greatly resembled a superior sort of Marshalsea. Numbers of people seemed to come abroad, pretty much as people had come into the prison; through debt, through idleness, relationship, curiosity, and general unfitness for getting on at home. They were brought into these foreign towns in the custody of couriers and local followers, just as the debtors had been brought into the prison. They prowled about the churches and picture-galleries, much in the old, dreary, prison-yard manner. They were usually going away again to-morrow or next week, and rarely knew their own minds, and seldom did what they said they would do, or went where they said they would go: in all this again, very like the prison debtors. They paid high for poor accommodation, and disparaged a place while they pretended to like it: which was exactly the Marshalsea custom. They were envied when they went away by people left behind, feigning not to want to go: and that again was the Marshalsea habit invariably. A certain set of words and phrases, as much belonging to tourists as the College and the Snuggery belonged to the jail, was always in their mouths. They had precisely the same incapacity for settling down to anything, as the prisoners used to have; they rather deteriorated one another, as the prisoners used to do; and they wore untidy dresses, and fell into a slouching way of life: still, always like the people in the Marshalsea.\n\nThe period of the family’s stay at Venice came, in its course, to an end, and they moved, with their retinue, to Rome. Through a repetition of the former Italian scenes, growing more dirty and more haggard as they went on, and bringing them at length to where the very air was diseased, they passed to their destination. A fine residence had been taken for them on the Corso, and there they took up their abode, in a city where everything seemed to be trying to stand still for ever on the ruins of something else--except the water, which, following eternal laws, tumbled and rolled from its glorious multitude of fountains.\n\nHere it seemed to Little Dorrit that a change came over the Marshalsea spirit of their society, and that Prunes and Prism got the upper hand. Everybody was walking about St Peter’s and the Vatican on somebody else’s cork legs, and straining every visible object through somebody else’s sieve. Nobody said what anything was, but everybody said what the Mrs Generals, Mr Eustace, or somebody else said it was. The whole body of travellers seemed to be a collection of voluntary human sacrifices, bound hand and foot, and delivered over to Mr Eustace and his attendants, to have the entrails of their intellects arranged according to the taste of that sacred priesthood. Through the rugged remains of temples and tombs and palaces and senate halls and theatres and amphitheatres of ancient days, hosts of tongue-tied and blindfolded moderns were carefully feeling their way, incessantly repeating Prunes and Prism in the endeavour to set their lips according to the received form. Mrs General was in her pure element. Nobody had an opinion. There was a formation of surface going on around her on an amazing scale, and it had not a flaw of courage or honest free speech in it.\n\nAnother modification of Prunes and Prism insinuated itself on Little Dorrit’s notice very shortly after their arrival. They received an early visit from Mrs Merdle, who led that extensive department of life in the Eternal City that winter; and the skilful manner in which she and Fanny fenced with one another on the occasion, almost made her quiet sister wink, like the glittering of small-swords.\n\n‘So delighted,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘to resume an acquaintance so inauspiciously begun at Martigny.’\n\n‘At Martigny, of course,’ said Fanny. ‘Charmed, I am sure!’\n\n‘I understand,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘from my son Edmund Sparkler, that he has already improved that chance occasion. He has returned quite transported with Venice.’\n\n‘Indeed?’ returned the careless Fanny. ‘Was he there long?’\n\n‘I might refer that question to Mr Dorrit,’ said Mrs Merdle, turning the bosom towards that gentleman; ‘Edmund having been so much indebted to him for rendering his stay agreeable.’\n\n‘Oh, pray don’t speak of it,’ returned Fanny. ‘I believe Papa had the pleasure of inviting Mr Sparkler twice or thrice,--but it was nothing. We had so many people about us, and kept such open house, that if he had that pleasure, it was less than nothing.’\n\n‘Except, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘except--ha--as it afforded me unusual gratification to--hum--show by any means, however slight and worthless, the--ha, hum--high estimation in which, in--ha--common with the rest of the world, I hold so distinguished and princely a character as Mr Merdle’s.’\n\nThe bosom received this tribute in its most engaging manner. ‘Mr Merdle,’ observed Fanny, as a means of dismissing Mr Sparkler into the background, ‘is quite a theme of Papa’s, you must know, Mrs Merdle.’\n\n‘I have been--ha--disappointed, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘to understand from Mr Sparkler that there is no great--hum--probability of Mr Merdle’s coming abroad.’\n\n‘Why, indeed,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘he is so much engaged and in such request, that I fear not. He has not been able to get abroad for years. You, Miss Dorrit, I believe have been almost continually abroad for a long time.’\n\n‘Oh dear yes,’ drawled Fanny, with the greatest hardihood. ‘An immense number of years.’\n\n‘So I should have inferred,’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘Exactly,’ said Fanny.\n\n‘I trust, however,’ resumed Mr Dorrit, ‘that if I have not the--hum--great advantage of becoming known to Mr Merdle on this side of the Alps or Mediterranean, I shall have that honour on returning to England. It is an honour I particularly desire and shall particularly esteem.’\n\n‘Mr Merdle,’ said Mrs Merdle, who had been looking admiringly at Fanny through her eye-glass, ‘will esteem it, I am sure, no less.’\n\nLittle Dorrit, still habitually thoughtful and solitary though no longer alone, at first supposed this to be mere Prunes and Prism. But as her father when they had been to a brilliant reception at Mrs Merdle’s, harped at their own family breakfast-table on his wish to know Mr Merdle, with the contingent view of benefiting by the advice of that wonderful man in the disposal of his fortune, she began to think it had a real meaning, and to entertain a curiosity on her own part to see the shining light of the time.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’\n\nWhile the waters of Venice and the ruins of Rome were sunning themselves for the pleasure of the Dorrit family, and were daily being sketched out of all earthly proportion, lineament, and likeness, by travelling pencils innumerable, the firm of Doyce and Clennam hammered away in Bleeding Heart Yard, and the vigorous clink of iron upon iron was heard there through the working hours.\n\nThe younger partner had, by this time, brought the business into sound trim; and the elder, left free to follow his own ingenious devices, had done much to enhance the character of the factory. As an ingenious man, he had necessarily to encounter every discouragement that the ruling powers for a length of time had been able by any means to put in the way of this class of culprits; but that was only reasonable self-defence in the powers, since How to do it must obviously be regarded as the natural and mortal enemy of How not to do it. In this was to be found the basis of the wise system, by tooth and nail upheld by the Circumlocution Office, of warning every ingenious British subject to be ingenious at his peril: of harassing him, obstructing him, inviting robbers (by making his remedy uncertain, and expensive) to plunder him, and at the best of confiscating his property after a short term of enjoyment, as though invention were on a par with felony. The system had uniformly found great favour with the Barnacles, and that was only reasonable, too; for one who worthily invents must be in earnest, and the Barnacles abhorred and dreaded nothing half so much. That again was very reasonable; since in a country suffering under the affliction of a great amount of earnestness, there might, in an exceeding short space of time, be not a single Barnacle left sticking to a post.\n\nDaniel Doyce faced his condition with its pains and penalties attached to it, and soberly worked on for the work’s sake. Clennam cheering him with a hearty co-operation, was a moral support to him, besides doing good service in his business relation. The concern prospered, and the partners were fast friends.\n\nBut Daniel could not forget the old design of so many years. It was not in reason to be expected that he should; if he could have lightly forgotten it, he could never have conceived it, or had the patience and perseverance to work it out. So Clennam thought, when he sometimes observed him of an evening looking over the models and drawings, and consoling himself by muttering with a sigh as he put them away again, that the thing was as true as it ever was.\n\nTo show no sympathy with so much endeavour, and so much disappointment, would have been to fail in what Clennam regarded as among the implied obligations of his partnership. A revival of the passing interest in the subject which had been by chance awakened at the door of the Circumlocution Office, originated in this feeling. He asked his partner to explain the invention to him; ‘having a lenient consideration,’ he stipulated, ‘for my being no workman, Doyce.’\n\n‘No workman?’ said Doyce. ‘You would have been a thorough workman if you had given yourself to it. You have as good a head for understanding such things as I have met with.’\n\n‘A totally uneducated one, I am sorry to add,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘I don’t know that,’ returned Doyce, ‘and I wouldn’t have you say that. No man of sense who has been generally improved, and has improved himself, can be called quite uneducated as to anything. I don’t particularly favour mysteries. I would as soon, on a fair and clear explanation, be judged by one class of man as another, provided he had the qualification I have named.’\n\n‘At all events,’ said Clennam--‘this sounds as if we were exchanging compliments, but we know we are not--I shall have the advantage of as plain an explanation as can be given.’\n\n‘Well!’ said Daniel, in his steady even way, ‘I’ll try to make it so.’\n\nHe had the power, often to be found in union with such a character, of explaining what he himself perceived, and meant, with the direct force and distinctness with which it struck his own mind. His manner of demonstration was so orderly and neat and simple, that it was not easy to mistake him. There was something almost ludicrous in the complete irreconcilability of a vague conventional notion that he must be a visionary man, with the precise, sagacious travelling of his eye and thumb over the plans, their patient stoppages at particular points, their careful returns to other points whence little channels of explanation had to be traced up, and his steady manner of making everything good and everything sound at each important stage, before taking his hearer on a line’s-breadth further. His dismissal of himself from his description, was hardly less remarkable. He never said, I discovered this adaptation or invented that combination; but showed the whole thing as if the Divine artificer had made it, and he had happened to find it; so modest he was about it, such a pleasant touch of respect was mingled with his quiet admiration of it, and so calmly convinced he was that it was established on irrefragable laws.\n\nNot only that evening, but for several succeeding evenings, Clennam was quite charmed by this investigation. The more he pursued it, and the oftener he glanced at the grey head bending over it, and the shrewd eye kindling with pleasure in it and love of it--instrument for probing his heart though it had been made for twelve long years--the less he could reconcile it to his younger energy to let it go without one effort more. At length he said:\n\n‘Doyce, it came to this at last--that the business was to be sunk with Heaven knows how many more wrecks, or begun all over again?’\n\n‘Yes,’ returned Doyce, ‘that’s what the noblemen and gentlemen made of it after a dozen years.’\n\n‘And pretty fellows too!’ said Clennam, bitterly.\n\n‘The usual thing!’ observed Doyce. ‘I must not make a martyr of myself, when I am one of so large a company.’\n\n‘Relinquish it, or begin it all over again?’ mused Clennam.\n\n‘That was exactly the long and the short of it,’ said Doyce.\n\n‘Then, my friend,’ cried Clennam, starting up and taking his work-roughened hand, ‘it shall be begun all over again!’\n\nDoyce looked alarmed, and replied in a hurry--for him, ‘No, no. Better put it by. Far better put it by. It will be heard of, one day. I can put it by. You forget, my good Clennam; I _have_ put it by. It’s all at an end.’\n\n‘Yes, Doyce,’ returned Clennam, ‘at an end as far as your efforts and rebuffs are concerned, I admit, but not as far as mine are. I am younger than you: I have only once set foot in that precious office, and I am fresh game for them. Come! I’ll try them. You shall do exactly as you have been doing since we have been together. I will add (as I easily can) to what I have been doing, the attempt to get public justice done to you; and, unless I have some success to report, you shall hear no more of it.’\n\nDaniel Doyce was still reluctant to consent, and again and again urged that they had better put it by. But it was natural that he should gradually allow himself to be over-persuaded by Clennam, and should yield. Yield he did. So Arthur resumed the long and hopeless labour of striving to make way with the Circumlocution Office.\n\nThe waiting-rooms of that Department soon began to be familiar with his presence, and he was generally ushered into them by its janitors much as a pickpocket might be shown into a police-office; the principal difference being that the object of the latter class of public business is to keep the pickpocket, while the Circumlocution object was to get rid of Clennam. However, he was resolved to stick to the Great Department; and so the work of form-filling, corresponding, minuting, memorandum-making, signing, counter-signing, counter-counter-signing, referring backwards and forwards, and referring sideways, crosswise, and zig-zag, recommenced.\n\nHere arises a feature of the Circumlocution Office, not previously mentioned in the present record. When that admirable Department got into trouble, and was, by some infuriated members of Parliament whom the smaller Barnacles almost suspected of labouring under diabolic possession, attacked on the merits of no individual case, but as an Institution wholly abominable and Bedlamite; then the noble or right honourable Barnacle who represented it in the House, would smite that member and cleave him asunder, with a statement of the quantity of business (for the prevention of business) done by the Circumlocution Office. Then would that noble or right honourable Barnacle hold in his hand a paper containing a few figures, to which, with the permission of the House, he would entreat its attention. Then would the inferior Barnacles exclaim, obeying orders, ‘Hear, Hear, Hear!’ and ‘Read!’ Then would the noble or right honourable Barnacle perceive, sir, from this little document, which he thought might carry conviction even to the perversest mind (Derisive laughter and cheering from the Barnacle fry), that within the short compass of the last financial half-year, this much-maligned Department (Cheers) had written and received fifteen thousand letters (Loud cheers), had written twenty-four thousand minutes (Louder cheers), and thirty-two thousand five hundred and seventeen memoranda (Vehement cheering). Nay, an ingenious gentleman connected with the Department, and himself a valuable public servant, had done him the favour to make a curious calculation of the amount of stationery consumed in it during the same period. It formed a part of this same short document; and he derived from it the remarkable fact that the sheets of foolscap paper it had devoted to the public service would pave the footways on both sides of Oxford Street from end to end, and leave nearly a quarter of a mile to spare for the park (Immense cheering and laughter); while of tape--red tape--it had used enough to stretch, in graceful festoons, from Hyde Park Corner to the General Post Office. Then, amidst a burst of official exultation, would the noble or right honourable Barnacle sit down, leaving the mutilated fragments of the Member on the field. No one, after that exemplary demolition of him, would have the hardihood to hint that the more the Circumlocution Office did, the less was done, and that the greatest blessing it could confer on an unhappy public would be to do nothing.\n\nWith sufficient occupation on his hands, now that he had this additional task--such a task had many and many a serviceable man died of before his day--Arthur Clennam led a life of slight variety. Regular visits to his mother’s dull sick room, and visits scarcely less regular to Mr Meagles at Twickenham, were its only changes during many months.\n\nHe sadly and sorely missed Little Dorrit. He had been prepared to miss her very much, but not so much. He knew to the full extent only through experience, what a large place in his life was left blank when her familiar little figure went out of it. He felt, too, that he must relinquish the hope of its return, understanding the family character sufficiently well to be assured that he and she were divided by a broad ground of separation. The old interest he had had in her, and her old trusting reliance on him, were tinged with melancholy in his mind: so soon had change stolen over them, and so soon had they glided into the past with other secret tendernesses.\n\nWhen he received her letter he was greatly moved, but did not the less sensibly feel that she was far divided from him by more than distance. It helped him to a clearer and keener perception of the place assigned him by the family. He saw that he was cherished in her grateful remembrance secretly, and that they resented him with the jail and the rest of its belongings.\n\nThrough all these meditations which every day of his life crowded about her, he thought of her otherwise in the old way. She was his innocent friend, his delicate child, his dear Little Dorrit. This very change of circumstances fitted curiously in with the habit, begun on the night when the roses floated away, of considering himself as a much older man than his years really made him. He regarded her from a point of view which in its remoteness, tender as it was, he little thought would have been unspeakable agony to her. He speculated about her future destiny, and about the husband she might have, with an affection for her which would have drained her heart of its dearest drop of hope, and broken it.\n\nEverything about him tended to confirm him in the custom of looking on himself as an elderly man, from whom such aspirations as he had combated in the case of Minnie Gowan (though that was not so long ago either, reckoning by months and seasons), were finally departed. His relations with her father and mother were like those on which a widower son-in-law might have stood. If the twin sister who was dead had lived to pass away in the bloom of womanhood, and he had been her husband, the nature of his intercourse with Mr and Mrs Meagles would probably have been just what it was. This imperceptibly helped to render habitual the impression within him, that he had done with, and dismissed that part of life.\n\nHe invariably heard of Minnie from them, as telling them in her letters how happy she was, and how she loved her husband; but inseparable from that subject, he invariably saw the old cloud on Mr Meagles’s face. Mr Meagles had never been quite so radiant since the marriage as before. He had never quite recovered the separation from Pet. He was the same good-humoured, open creature; but as if his face, from being much turned towards the pictures of his two children which could show him only one look, unconsciously adopted a characteristic from them, it always had now, through all its changes of expression, a look of loss in it.\n\nOne wintry Saturday when Clennam was at the cottage, the Dowager Mrs Gowan drove up, in the Hampton Court equipage which pretended to be the exclusive equipage of so many individual proprietors. She descended, in her shady ambuscade of green fan, to favour Mr and Mrs Meagles with a call.\n\n‘And how do you both do, Papa and Mama Meagles?’ said she, encouraging her humble connections. ‘And when did you last hear from or about my poor fellow?’\n\nMy poor fellow was her son; and this mode of speaking of him politely kept alive, without any offence in the world, the pretence that he had fallen a victim to the Meagles’ wiles.\n\n‘And the dear pretty one?’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘Have you later news of her than I have?’\n\nWhich also delicately implied that her son had been captured by mere beauty, and under its fascination had forgone all sorts of worldly advantages.\n\n‘I am sure,’ said Mrs Gowan, without straining her attention on the answers she received, ‘it’s an unspeakable comfort to know they continue happy. My poor fellow is of such a restless disposition, and has been so used to roving about, and to being inconstant and popular among all manner of people, that it’s the greatest comfort in life. I suppose they’re as poor as mice, Papa Meagles?’\n\nMr Meagles, fidgety under the question, replied, ‘I hope not, ma’am. I hope they will manage their little income.’\n\n‘Oh! my dearest Meagles!’ returned the lady, tapping him on the arm with the green fan and then adroitly interposing it between a yawn and the company, ‘how can you, as a man of the world and one of the most business-like of human beings--for you know you are business-like, and a great deal too much for us who are not--’\n\n(Which went to the former purpose, by making Mr Meagles out to be an artful schemer.)\n\n‘--How can you talk about their managing their little means? My poor dear fellow! The idea of his managing hundreds! And the sweet pretty creature too. The notion of her managing! Papa Meagles! Don’t!’\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, gravely, ‘I am sorry to admit, then, that Henry certainly does anticipate his means.’\n\n‘My dear good man--I use no ceremony with you, because we are a kind of relations;--positively, Mama Meagles,’ exclaimed Mrs Gowan cheerfully, as if the absurd coincidence then flashed upon her for the first time, ‘a kind of relations! My dear good man, in this world none of us can have _everything_ our own way.’\n\nThis again went to the former point, and showed Mr Meagles with all good breeding that, so far, he had been brilliantly successful in his deep designs. Mrs Gowan thought the hit so good a one, that she dwelt upon it; repeating ‘Not _everything_. No, no; in this world we must not expect _everything_, Papa Meagles.’\n\n‘And may I ask, ma’am,’ retorted Mr Meagles, a little heightened in colour, ‘who does expect everything?’\n\n‘Oh, nobody, nobody!’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘I was going to say--but you put me out. You interrupting Papa, what was I going to say?’\n\nDrooping her large green fan, she looked musingly at Mr Meagles while she thought about it; a performance not tending to the cooling of that gentleman’s rather heated spirits.\n\n‘Ah! Yes, to be sure!’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘You must remember that my poor fellow has always been accustomed to expectations. They may have been realised, or they may not have been realised--’\n\n‘Let us say, then, may not have been realised,’ observed Mr Meagles.\n\nThe Dowager for a moment gave him an angry look; but tossed it off with her head and her fan, and pursued the tenor of her way in her former manner.\n\n‘It makes no difference. My poor fellow has been accustomed to that sort of thing, and of course you knew it, and were prepared for the consequences. I myself always clearly foresaw the consequences, and am not surprised. And you must not be surprised. In fact, can’t be surprised. Must have been prepared for it.’\n\nMr Meagles looked at his wife and at Clennam; bit his lip; and coughed.\n\n‘And now here’s my poor fellow,’ Mrs Gowan pursued, ‘receiving notice that he is to hold himself in expectation of a baby, and all the expenses attendant on such an addition to his family! Poor Henry! But it can’t be helped now; it’s too late to help it now. Only don’t talk of anticipating means, Papa Meagles, as a discovery; because that would be too much.’\n\n‘Too much, ma’am?’ said Mr Meagles, as seeking an explanation.\n\n‘There, there!’ said Mrs Gowan, putting him in his inferior place with an expressive action of her hand. ‘Too much for my poor fellow’s mother to bear at this time of day. They are fast married, and can’t be unmarried. There, there! I know that! You needn’t tell me that, Papa Meagles. I know it very well. What was it I said just now? That it was a great comfort they continued happy. It is to be hoped they will still continue happy. It is to be hoped Pretty One will do everything she can to make my poor fellow happy, and keep him contented. Papa and Mama Meagles, we had better say no more about it. We never did look at this subject from the same side, and we never shall. There, there! Now I am good.’\n\nTruly, having by this time said everything she could say in maintenance of her wonderfully mythical position, and in admonition to Mr Meagles that he must not expect to bear his honours of alliance too cheaply, Mrs Gowan was disposed to forgo the rest. If Mr Meagles had submitted to a glance of entreaty from Mrs Meagles, and an expressive gesture from Clennam, he would have left her in the undisturbed enjoyment of this state of mind. But Pet was the darling and pride of his heart; and if he could ever have championed her more devotedly, or loved her better, than in the days when she was the sunlight of his house, it would have been now, when, as its daily grace and delight, she was lost to it.\n\n‘Mrs Gowan, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I have been a plain man all my life. If I was to try--no matter whether on myself, on somebody else, or both--any genteel mystifications, I should probably not succeed in them.’\n\n‘Papa Meagles,’ returned the Dowager, with an affable smile, but with the bloom on her cheeks standing out a little more vividly than usual as the neighbouring surface became paler, ‘probably not.’\n\n‘Therefore, my good madam,’ said Mr Meagles, at great pains to restrain himself, ‘I hope I may, without offence, ask to have no such mystification played off upon me.’\n\n‘Mama Meagles,’ observed Mrs Gowan, ‘your good man is incomprehensible.’\n\nHer turning to that worthy lady was an artifice to bring her into the discussion, quarrel with her, and vanquish her. Mr Meagles interposed to prevent that consummation.\n\n‘Mother,’ said he, ‘you are inexpert, my dear, and it is not a fair match. Let me beg of you to remain quiet. Come, Mrs Gowan, come! Let us try to be sensible; let us try to be good-natured; let us try to be fair. Don’t you pity Henry, and I won’t pity Pet. And don’t be one-sided, my dear madam; it’s not considerate, it’s not kind. Don’t let us say that we hope Pet will make Henry happy, or even that we hope Henry will make Pet happy,’ (Mr Meagles himself did not look happy as he spoke the words,) ‘but let us hope they will make each other happy.’\n\n‘Yes, sure, and there leave it, father,’ said Mrs Meagles the kind-hearted and comfortable.\n\n‘Why, mother, no,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘not exactly there. I can’t quite leave it there; I must say just half-a-dozen words more. Mrs Gowan, I hope I am not over-sensitive. I believe I don’t look it.’\n\n‘Indeed you do not,’ said Mrs Gowan, shaking her head and the great green fan together, for emphasis.\n\n‘Thank you, ma’am; that’s well. Notwithstanding which, I feel a little--I don’t want to use a strong word--now shall I say hurt?’ asked Mr Meagles at once with frankness and moderation, and with a conciliatory appeal in his tone.\n\n‘Say what you like,’ answered Mrs Gowan. ‘It is perfectly indifferent to me.’\n\n‘No, no, don’t say that,’ urged Mr Meagles, ‘because that’s not responding amiably. I feel a little hurt when I hear references made to consequences having been foreseen, and to its being too late now, and so forth.’\n\n‘_Do_ you, Papa Meagles?’ said Mrs Gowan. ‘I am not surprised.’\n\n‘Well, ma’am,’ reasoned Mr Meagles, ‘I was in hopes you would have been at least surprised, because to hurt me wilfully on so tender a subject is surely not generous.’\n\n‘I am not responsible,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘for your conscience, you know.’\n\nPoor Mr Meagles looked aghast with astonishment.\n\n‘If I am unluckily obliged to carry a cap about with me, which is yours and fits you,’ pursued Mrs Gowan, ‘don’t blame me for its pattern, Papa Meagles, I beg!’\n\n‘Why, good Lord, ma’am!’ Mr Meagles broke out, ‘that’s as much as to state--’\n\n‘Now, Papa Meagles, Papa Meagles,’ said Mrs Gowan, who became extremely deliberate and prepossessing in manner whenever that gentleman became at all warm, ‘perhaps to prevent confusion, I had better speak for myself than trouble your kindness to speak for me. It’s as much as to state, you begin. If you please, I will finish the sentence. It is as much as to state--not that I wish to press it or even recall it, for it is of no use now, and my only wish is to make the best of existing circumstances--that from the first to the last I always objected to this match of yours, and at a very late period yielded a most unwilling consent to it.’\n\n‘Mother!’ cried Mr Meagles. ‘Do you hear this! Arthur! Do you hear this!’\n\n‘The room being of a convenient size,’ said Mrs Gowan, looking about as she fanned herself, ‘and quite charmingly adapted in all respects to conversation, I should imagine I am audible in any part of it.’\n\nSome moments passed in silence, before Mr Meagles could hold himself in his chair with sufficient security to prevent his breaking out of it at the next word he spoke. At last he said: ‘Ma’am, I am very unwilling to revive them, but I must remind you what my opinions and my course were, all along, on that unfortunate subject.’\n\n‘O, my dear sir!’ said Mrs Gowan, smiling and shaking her head with accusatory intelligence, ‘they were well understood by me, I assure you.’\n\n‘I never, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘knew unhappiness before that time, I never knew anxiety before that time. It was a time of such distress to me that--’ That Mr Meagles could really say no more about it, in short, but passed his handkerchief before his face.\n\n‘I understood the whole affair,’ said Mrs Gowan, composedly looking over her fan. ‘As you have appealed to Mr Clennam, I may appeal to Mr Clennam, too. He knows whether I did or not.’\n\n‘I am very unwilling,’ said Clennam, looked to by all parties, ‘to take any share in this discussion, more especially because I wish to preserve the best understanding and the clearest relations with Mr Henry Gowan. I have very strong reasons indeed, for entertaining that wish. Mrs Gowan attributed certain views of furthering the marriage to my friend here, in conversation with me before it took place; and I endeavoured to undeceive her. I represented that I knew him (as I did and do) to be strenuously opposed to it, both in opinion and action.’\n\n‘You see?’ said Mrs Gowan, turning the palms of her hands towards Mr Meagles, as if she were Justice herself, representing to him that he had better confess, for he had not a leg to stand on. ‘You see? Very good! Now Papa and Mama Meagles both!’ here she rose; ‘allow me to take the liberty of putting an end to this rather formidable controversy. I will not say another word upon its merits. I will only say that it is an additional proof of what one knows from all experience; that this kind of thing never answers--as my poor fellow himself would say, that it never pays--in one word, that it never does.’\n\nMr Meagles asked, What kind of thing?\n\n‘It is in vain,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘for people to attempt to get on together who have such extremely different antecedents; who are jumbled against each other in this accidental, matrimonial sort of way; and who cannot look at the untoward circumstance which has shaken them together in the same light. It never does.’\n\nMr Meagles was beginning, ‘Permit me to say, ma’am--’\n\n‘No, don’t,’ returned Mrs Gowan. ‘Why should you! It is an ascertained fact. It never does. I will therefore, if you please, go my way, leaving you to yours. I shall at all times be happy to receive my poor fellow’s pretty wife, and I shall always make a point of being on the most affectionate terms with her. But as to these terms, semi-family and semi-stranger, semi-goring and semi-boring, they form a state of things quite amusing in its impracticability. I assure you it never does.’\n\nThe Dowager here made a smiling obeisance, rather to the room than to any one in it, and therewith took a final farewell of Papa and Mama Meagles. Clennam stepped forward to hand her to the Pill-Box which was at the service of all the Pills in Hampton Court Palace; and she got into that vehicle with distinguished serenity, and was driven away.\n\nThenceforth the Dowager, with a light and careless humour, often recounted to her particular acquaintance how, after a hard trial, she had found it impossible to know those people who belonged to Henry’s wife, and who had made that desperate set to catch him. Whether she had come to the conclusion beforehand, that to get rid of them would give her favourite pretence a better air, might save her some occasional inconvenience, and could risk no loss (the pretty creature being fast married, and her father devoted to her), was best known to herself. Though this history has its opinion on that point too, and decidedly in the affirmative.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. Appearance and Disappearance\n\n‘Arthur, my dear boy,’ said Mr Meagles, on the evening of the following day, ‘Mother and I have been talking this over, and we don’t feel comfortable in remaining as we are. That elegant connection of ours--that dear lady who was here yesterday--’\n\n‘I understand,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Even that affable and condescending ornament of society,’ pursued Mr Meagles, ‘may misrepresent us, we are afraid. We could bear a great deal, Arthur, for her sake; but we think we would rather not bear that, if it was all the same to her.’\n\n‘Good,’ said Arthur. ‘Go on.’\n\n‘You see,’ proceeded Mr Meagles ‘it might put us wrong with our son-in-law, it might even put us wrong with our daughter, and it might lead to a great deal of domestic trouble. You see, don’t you?’\n\n‘Yes, indeed,’ returned Arthur, ‘there is much reason in what you say.’ He had glanced at Mrs Meagles, who was always on the good and sensible side; and a petition had shone out of her honest face that he would support Mr Meagles in his present inclinings.\n\n‘So we are very much disposed, are Mother and I,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘to pack up bags and baggage and go among the Allongers and Marshongers once more. I mean, we are very much disposed to be off, strike right through France into Italy, and see our Pet.’\n\n‘And I don’t think,’ replied Arthur, touched by the motherly anticipation in the bright face of Mrs Meagles (she must have been very like her daughter, once), ‘that you could do better. And if you ask me for my advice, it is that you set off to-morrow.’\n\n‘Is it really, though?’ said Mr Meagles. ‘Mother, this is being backed in an idea!’\n\nMother, with a look which thanked Clennam in a manner very agreeable to him, answered that it was indeed.\n\n‘The fact is, besides, Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, the old cloud coming over his face, ‘that my son-in-law is already in debt again, and that I suppose I must clear him again. It may be as well, even on this account, that I should step over there, and look him up in a friendly way. Then again, here’s Mother foolishly anxious (and yet naturally too) about Pet’s state of health, and that she should not be left to feel lonesome at the present time. It’s undeniably a long way off, Arthur, and a strange place for the poor love under all the circumstances. Let her be as well cared for as any lady in that land, still it is a long way off. just as Home is Home though it’s never so Homely, why you see,’ said Mr Meagles, adding a new version to the proverb, ‘Rome is Rome, though it’s never so Romely.’\n\n‘All perfectly true,’ observed Arthur, ‘and all sufficient reasons for going.’\n\n‘I am glad you think so; it decides me. Mother, my dear, you may get ready. We have lost our pleasant interpreter (she spoke three foreign languages beautifully, Arthur; you have heard her many a time), and you must pull me through it, Mother, as well as you can. I require a deal of pulling through, Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head, ‘a deal of pulling through. I stick at everything beyond a noun-substantive--and I stick at him, if he’s at all a tight one.’\n\n‘Now I think of it,’ returned Clennam, ‘there’s Cavalletto. He shall go with you, if you like. I could not afford to lose him, but you will bring him safe back.’\n\n‘Well! I am much obliged to you, my boy,’ said Mr Meagles, turning it over, ‘but I think not. No, I think I’ll be pulled through by Mother. Cavallooro (I stick at his very name to start with, and it sounds like the chorus to a comic song) is so necessary to you, that I don’t like the thought of taking him away. More than that, there’s no saying when we may come home again; and it would never do to take him away for an indefinite time. The cottage is not what it was. It only holds two little people less than it ever did, Pet, and her poor unfortunate maid Tattycoram; but it seems empty now. Once out of it, there’s no knowing when we may come back to it. No, Arthur, I’ll be pulled through by Mother.’\n\nThey would do best by themselves perhaps, after all, Clennam thought; therefore did not press his proposal.\n\n‘If you would come down and stay here for a change, when it wouldn’t trouble you,’ Mr Meagles resumed, ‘I should be glad to think--and so would Mother too, I know--that you were brightening up the old place with a bit of life it was used to when it was full, and that the Babies on the wall there had a kind eye upon them sometimes. You so belong to the spot, and to them, Arthur, and we should every one of us have been so happy if it had fallen out--but, let us see--how’s the weather for travelling now?’ Mr Meagles broke off, cleared his throat, and got up to look out of the window.\n\nThey agreed that the weather was of high promise; and Clennam kept the talk in that safe direction until it had become easy again, when he gently diverted it to Henry Gowan and his quick sense and agreeable qualities when he was delicately dealt with; he likewise dwelt on the indisputable affection he entertained for his wife. Clennam did not fail of his effect upon good Mr Meagles, whom these commendations greatly cheered; and who took Mother to witness that the single and cordial desire of his heart in reference to their daughter’s husband, was harmoniously to exchange friendship for friendship, and confidence for confidence. Within a few hours the cottage furniture began to be wrapped up for preservation in the family absence--or, as Mr Meagles expressed it, the house began to put its hair in papers--and within a few days Father and Mother were gone, Mrs Tickit and Dr Buchan were posted, as of yore, behind the parlour blind, and Arthur’s solitary feet were rustling among the dry fallen leaves in the garden walks.\n\nAs he had a liking for the spot, he seldom let a week pass without paying a visit. Sometimes, he went down alone from Saturday to Monday; sometimes his partner accompanied him; sometimes, he merely strolled for an hour or two about the house and garden, saw that all was right, and returned to London again. At all times, and under all circumstances, Mrs Tickit, with her dark row of curls, and Dr Buchan, sat in the parlour window, looking out for the family return.\n\nOn one of his visits Mrs Tickit received him with the words, ‘I have something to tell you, Mr Clennam, that will surprise you.’ So surprising was the something in question, that it actually brought Mrs Tickit out of the parlour window and produced her in the garden walk, when Clennam went in at the gate on its being opened for him.\n\n‘What is it, Mrs Tickit?’ said he.\n\n‘Sir,’ returned that faithful housekeeper, having taken him into the parlour and closed the door; ‘if ever I saw the led away and deluded child in my life, I saw her identically in the dusk of yesterday evening.’\n\n‘You don’t mean Tatty--’\n\n‘Coram yes I do!’ quoth Mrs Tickit, clearing the disclosure at a leap.\n\n‘Where?’\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ returned Mrs Tickit, ‘I was a little heavy in my eyes, being that I was waiting longer than customary for my cup of tea which was then preparing by Mary Jane. I was not sleeping, nor what a person would term correctly, dozing. I was more what a person would strictly call watching with my eyes closed.’\n\nWithout entering upon an inquiry into this curious abnormal condition, Clennam said, ‘Exactly. Well?’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ proceeded Mrs Tickit, ‘I was thinking of one thing and thinking of another, just as you yourself might. Just as anybody might.’\n\n‘Precisely so,’ said Clennam. ‘Well?’\n\n‘And when I do think of one thing and do think of another,’ pursued Mrs Tickit, ‘I hardly need to tell you, Mr Clennam, that I think of the family. Because, dear me! a person’s thoughts,’ Mrs Tickit said this with an argumentative and philosophic air, ‘however they may stray, will go more or less on what is uppermost in their minds. They _will_ do it, sir, and a person can’t prevent them.’\n\nArthur subscribed to this discovery with a nod.\n\n‘You find it so yourself, sir, I’ll be bold to say,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘and we all find it so. It an’t our stations in life that changes us, Mr Clennam; thoughts is free!--As I was saying, I was thinking of one thing and thinking of another, and thinking very much of the family. Not of the family in the present times only, but in the past times too. For when a person does begin thinking of one thing and thinking of another in that manner, as it’s getting dark, what I say is, that all times seem to be present, and a person must get out of that state and consider before they can say which is which.’\n\nHe nodded again; afraid to utter a word, lest it should present any new opening to Mrs Tickit’s conversational powers.\n\n‘In consequence of which,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘when I quivered my eyes and saw her actual form and figure looking in at the gate, I let them close again without so much as starting, for that actual form and figure came so pat to the time when it belonged to the house as much as mine or your own, that I never thought at the moment of its having gone away. But, sir, when I quivered my eyes again, and saw that it wasn’t there, then it all flooded upon me with a fright, and I jumped up.’\n\n‘You ran out directly?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘I ran out,’ assented Mrs Tickit, ‘as fast as ever my feet would carry me; and if you’ll credit it, Mr Clennam, there wasn’t in the whole shining Heavens, no not so much as a finger of that young woman.’\n\nPassing over the absence from the firmament of this novel constellation, Arthur inquired of Mrs Tickit if she herself went beyond the gate?\n\n‘Went to and fro, and high and low,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘and saw no sign of her!’\n\nHe then asked Mrs Tickit how long a space of time she supposed there might have been between the two sets of ocular quiverings she had experienced? Mrs Tickit, though minutely circumstantial in her reply, had no settled opinion between five seconds and ten minutes. She was so plainly at sea on this part of the case, and had so clearly been startled out of slumber, that Clennam was much disposed to regard the appearance as a dream. Without hurting Mrs Tickit’s feelings with that infidel solution of her mystery, he took it away from the cottage with him; and probably would have retained it ever afterwards if a circumstance had not soon happened to change his opinion.\n\nHe was passing at nightfall along the Strand, and the lamp-lighter was going on before him, under whose hand the street-lamps, blurred by the foggy air, burst out one after another, like so many blazing sunflowers coming into full-blow all at once,--when a stoppage on the pavement, caused by a train of coal-waggons toiling up from the wharves at the river-side, brought him to a stand-still. He had been walking quickly, and going with some current of thought, and the sudden check given to both operations caused him to look freshly about him, as people under such circumstances usually do.\n\nImmediately, he saw in advance--a few people intervening, but still so near to him that he could have touched them by stretching out his arm--Tattycoram and a strange man of a remarkable appearance: a swaggering man, with a high nose, and a black moustache as false in its colour as his eyes were false in their expression, who wore his heavy cloak with the air of a foreigner. His dress and general appearance were those of a man on travel, and he seemed to have very recently joined the girl. In bending down (being much taller than she was), listening to whatever she said to him, he looked over his shoulder with the suspicious glance of one who was not unused to be mistrustful that his footsteps might be dogged. It was then that Clennam saw his face; as his eyes lowered on the people behind him in the aggregate, without particularly resting upon Clennam’s face or any other.\n\nHe had scarcely turned his head about again, and it was still bent down, listening to the girl, when the stoppage ceased, and the obstructed stream of people flowed on. Still bending his head and listening to the girl, he went on at her side, and Clennam followed them, resolved to play this unexpected play out, and see where they went.\n\nHe had hardly made the determination (though he was not long about it), when he was again as suddenly brought up as he had been by the stoppage. They turned short into the Adelphi,--the girl evidently leading,--and went straight on, as if they were going to the Terrace which overhangs the river.\n\nThere is always, to this day, a sudden pause in that place to the roar of the great thoroughfare. The many sounds become so deadened that the change is like putting cotton in the ears, or having the head thickly muffled. At that time the contrast was far greater; there being no small steam-boats on the river, no landing places but slippery wooden stairs and foot-causeways, no railroad on the opposite bank, no hanging bridge or fish-market near at hand, no traffic on the nearest bridge of stone, nothing moving on the stream but watermen’s wherries and coal-lighters. Long and broad black tiers of the latter, moored fast in the mud as if they were never to move again, made the shore funereal and silent after dark; and kept what little water-movement there was, far out towards mid-stream. At any hour later than sunset, and not least at that hour when most of the people who have anything to eat at home are going home to eat it, and when most of those who have nothing have hardly yet slunk out to beg or steal, it was a deserted place and looked on a deserted scene.\n\nSuch was the hour when Clennam stopped at the corner, observing the girl and the strange man as they went down the street. The man’s footsteps were so noisy on the echoing stones that he was unwilling to add the sound of his own. But when they had passed the turning and were in the darkness of the dark corner leading to the terrace, he made after them with such indifferent appearance of being a casual passenger on his way, as he could assume.\n\nWhen he rounded the dark corner, they were walking along the terrace towards a figure which was coming towards them. If he had seen it by itself, under such conditions of gas-lamp, mist, and distance, he might not have known it at first sight, but with the figure of the girl to prompt him, he at once recognised Miss Wade.\n\nHe stopped at the corner, seeming to look back expectantly up the street as if he had made an appointment with some one to meet him there; but he kept a careful eye on the three. When they came together, the man took off his hat, and made Miss Wade a bow. The girl appeared to say a few words as though she presented him, or accounted for his being late, or early, or what not; and then fell a pace or so behind, by herself. Miss Wade and the man then began to walk up and down; the man having the appearance of being extremely courteous and complimentary in manner; Miss Wade having the appearance of being extremely haughty.\n\nWhen they came down to the corner and turned, she was saying, ‘If I pinch myself for it, sir, that is my business. Confine yourself to yours, and ask me no question.’\n\n‘By Heaven, ma’am!’ he replied, making her another bow. ‘It was my profound respect for the strength of your character, and my admiration of your beauty.’\n\n‘I want neither the one nor the other from any one,’ said she, ‘and certainly not from you of all creatures. Go on with your report.’\n\n‘Am I pardoned?’ he asked, with an air of half abashed gallantry.\n\n‘You are paid,’ she said, ‘and that is all you want.’\n\nWhether the girl hung behind because she was not to hear the business, or as already knowing enough about it, Clennam could not determine. They turned and she turned. She looked away at the river, as she walked with her hands folded before her; and that was all he could make of her without showing his face. There happened, by good fortune, to be a lounger really waiting for some one; and he sometimes looked over the railing at the water, and sometimes came to the dark corner and looked up the street, rendering Arthur less conspicuous.\n\nWhen Miss Wade and the man came back again, she was saying, ‘You must wait until to-morrow.’\n\n‘A thousand pardons?’ he returned. ‘My faith! Then it’s not convenient to-night?’\n\n‘No. I tell you I must get it before I can give it to you.’\n\nShe stopped in the roadway, as if to put an end to the conference. He of course stopped too. And the girl stopped.\n\n‘It’s a little inconvenient,’ said the man. ‘A little. But, Holy Blue! that’s nothing in such a service. I am without money to-night, by chance. I have a good banker in this city, but I would not wish to draw upon the house until the time when I shall draw for a round sum.’\n\n‘Harriet,’ said Miss Wade, ‘arrange with him--this gentleman here--for sending him some money to-morrow.’ She said it with a slur of the word gentleman which was more contemptuous than any emphasis, and walked slowly on.\n\nThe man bent his head again, and the girl spoke to him as they both followed her. Clennam ventured to look at the girl as they moved away. He could note that her rich black eyes were fastened upon the man with a scrutinising expression, and that she kept at a little distance from him, as they walked side by side to the further end of the terrace.\n\nA loud and altered clank upon the pavement warned him, before he could discern what was passing there, that the man was coming back alone. Clennam lounged into the road, towards the railing; and the man passed at a quick swing, with the end of his cloak thrown over his shoulder, singing a scrap of a French song.\n\nThe whole vista had no one in it now but himself. The lounger had lounged out of view, and Miss Wade and Tattycoram were gone. More than ever bent on seeing what became of them, and on having some information to give his good friend, Mr Meagles, he went out at the further end of the terrace, looking cautiously about him. He rightly judged that, at first at all events, they would go in a contrary direction from their late companion. He soon saw them in a neighbouring bye-street, which was not a thoroughfare, evidently allowing time for the man to get well out of their way. They walked leisurely arm-in-arm down one side of the street, and returned on the opposite side. When they came back to the street-corner, they changed their pace for the pace of people with an object and a distance before them, and walked steadily away. Clennam, no less steadily, kept them in sight.\n\nThey crossed the Strand, and passed through Covent Garden (under the windows of his old lodging where dear Little Dorrit had come that night), and slanted away north-east, until they passed the great building whence Tattycoram derived her name, and turned into the Gray’s Inn Road. Clennam was quite at home here, in right of Flora, not to mention the Patriarch and Pancks, and kept them in view with ease. He was beginning to wonder where they might be going next, when that wonder was lost in the greater wonder with which he saw them turn into the Patriarchal street. That wonder was in its turn swallowed up on the greater wonder with which he saw them stop at the Patriarchal door. A low double knock at the bright brass knocker, a gleam of light into the road from the opened door, a brief pause for inquiry and answer and the door was shut, and they were housed.\n\nAfter looking at the surrounding objects for assurance that he was not in an odd dream, and after pacing a little while before the house, Arthur knocked at the door. It was opened by the usual maid-servant, and she showed him up at once, with her usual alacrity, to Flora’s sitting-room.\n\nThere was no one with Flora but Mr F.’s Aunt, which respectable gentlewoman, basking in a balmy atmosphere of tea and toast, was ensconced in an easy-chair by the fireside, with a little table at her elbow, and a clean white handkerchief spread over her lap on which two pieces of toast at that moment awaited consumption. Bending over a steaming vessel of tea, and looking through the steam, and breathing forth the steam, like a malignant Chinese enchantress engaged in the performance of unholy rites, Mr F.’s Aunt put down her great teacup and exclaimed, ‘Drat him, if he an’t come back again!’\n\nIt would seem from the foregoing exclamation that this uncompromising relative of the lamented Mr F., measuring time by the acuteness of her sensations and not by the clock, supposed Clennam to have lately gone away; whereas at least a quarter of a year had elapsed since he had had the temerity to present himself before her.\n\n‘My goodness Arthur!’ cried Flora, rising to give him a cordial reception, ‘Doyce and Clennam what a start and a surprise for though not far from the machinery and foundry business and surely might be taken sometimes if at no other time about mid-day when a glass of sherry and a humble sandwich of whatever cold meat in the larder might not come amiss nor taste the worse for being friendly for you know you buy it somewhere and wherever bought a profit must be made or they would never keep the place it stands to reason without a motive still never seen and learnt now not to be expected, for as Mr F. himself said if seeing is believing not seeing is believing too and when you don’t see you may fully believe you’re not remembered not that I expect you Arthur Doyce and Clennam to remember me why should I for the days are gone but bring another teacup here directly and tell her fresh toast and pray sit near the fire.’\n\nArthur was in the greatest anxiety to explain the object of his visit; but was put off for the moment, in spite of himself, by what he understood of the reproachful purport of these words, and by the genuine pleasure she testified in seeing him.\n\n‘And now pray tell me something all you know,’ said Flora, drawing her chair near to his, ‘about the good dear quiet little thing and all the changes of her fortunes carriage people now no doubt and horses without number most romantic, a coat of arms of course and wild beasts on their hind legs showing it as if it was a copy they had done with mouths from ear to ear good gracious, and has she her health which is the first consideration after all for what is wealth without it Mr F. himself so often saying when his twinges came that sixpence a day and find yourself and no gout so much preferable, not that he could have lived on anything like it being the last man or that the previous little thing though far too familiar an expression now had any tendency of that sort much too slight and small but looked so fragile bless her?’\n\nMr F.’s Aunt, who had eaten a piece of toast down to the crust, here solemnly handed the crust to Flora, who ate it for her as a matter of business. Mr F.’s Aunt then moistened her ten fingers in slow succession at her lips, and wiped them in exactly the same order on the white handkerchief; then took the other piece of toast, and fell to work upon it. While pursuing this routine, she looked at Clennam with an expression of such intense severity that he felt obliged to look at her in return, against his personal inclinations.\n\n‘She is in Italy, with all her family, Flora,’ he said, when the dreaded lady was occupied again.\n\n‘In Italy is she really?’ said Flora, ‘with the grapes growing everywhere and lava necklaces and bracelets too that land of poetry with burning mountains picturesque beyond belief though if the organ-boys come away from the neighbourhood not to be scorched nobody can wonder being so young and bringing their white mice with them most humane, and is she really in that favoured land with nothing but blue about her and dying gladiators and Belvederes though Mr F. himself did not believe for his objection when in spirits was that the images could not be true there being no medium between expensive quantities of linen badly got up and all in creases and none whatever, which certainly does not seem probable though perhaps in consequence of the extremes of rich and poor which may account for it.’\n\nArthur tried to edge a word in, but Flora hurried on again.\n\n‘Venice Preserved too,’ said she, ‘I think you have been there is it well or ill preserved for people differ so and Maccaroni if they really eat it like the conjurors why not cut it shorter, you are acquainted Arthur--dear Doyce and Clennam at least not dear and most assuredly not Doyce for I have not the pleasure but pray excuse me--acquainted I believe with Mantua what _has_ it got to do with Mantua-making for I never have been able to conceive?’\n\n‘I believe there is no connection, Flora, between the two,’ Arthur was beginning, when she caught him up again.\n\n‘Upon your word no isn’t there I never did but that’s like me I run away with an idea and having none to spare I keep it, alas there was a time dear Arthur that is to say decidedly not dear nor Arthur neither but you understand me when one bright idea gilded the what’s-his-name horizon of et cetera but it is darkly clouded now and all is over.’\n\nArthur’s increasing wish to speak of something very different was by this time so plainly written on his face, that Flora stopped in a tender look, and asked him what it was?\n\n‘I have the greatest desire, Flora, to speak to some one who is now in this house--with Mr Casby no doubt. Some one whom I saw come in, and who, in a misguided and deplorable way, has deserted the house of a friend of mine.’\n\n‘Papa sees so many and such odd people,’ said Flora, rising, ‘that I shouldn’t venture to go down for any one but you Arthur but for you I would willingly go down in a diving-bell much more a dining-room and will come back directly if you’ll mind and at the same time not mind Mr F.’s Aunt while I’m gone.’\n\nWith those words and a parting glance, Flora bustled out, leaving Clennam under dreadful apprehension of this terrible charge.\n\nThe first variation which manifested itself in Mr F.’s Aunt’s demeanour when she had finished her piece of toast, was a loud and prolonged sniff. Finding it impossible to avoid construing this demonstration into a defiance of himself, its gloomy significance being unmistakable, Clennam looked plaintively at the excellent though prejudiced lady from whom it emanated, in the hope that she might be disarmed by a meek submission.\n\n‘None of your eyes at me,’ said Mr F.’s Aunt, shivering with hostility. ‘Take that.’\n\n‘That’ was the crust of the piece of toast. Clennam accepted the boon with a look of gratitude, and held it in his hand under the pressure of a little embarrassment, which was not relieved when Mr F.’s Aunt, elevating her voice into a cry of considerable power, exclaimed, ‘He has a proud stomach, this chap! He’s too proud a chap to eat it!’ and, coming out of her chair, shook her venerable fist so very close to his nose as to tickle the surface. But for the timely return of Flora, to find him in this difficult situation, further consequences might have ensued. Flora, without the least discomposure or surprise, but congratulating the old lady in an approving manner on being ‘very lively to-night’, handed her back to her chair.\n\n‘He has a proud stomach, this chap,’ said Mr F.’s relation, on being reseated. ‘Give him a meal of chaff!’\n\n‘Oh! I don’t think he would like that, aunt,’ returned Flora.\n\n‘Give him a meal of chaff, I tell you,’ said Mr F.’s Aunt, glaring round Flora on her enemy. ‘It’s the only thing for a proud stomach. Let him eat up every morsel. Drat him, give him a meal of chaff!’\n\nUnder a general pretence of helping him to this refreshment, Flora got him out on the staircase; Mr F.’s Aunt even then constantly reiterating, with inexpressible bitterness, that he was ‘a chap,’ and had a ‘proud stomach,’ and over and over again insisting on that equine provision being made for him which she had already so strongly prescribed.\n\n‘Such an inconvenient staircase and so many corner-stairs Arthur,’ whispered Flora, ‘would you object to putting your arm round me under my pelerine?’\n\nWith a sense of going down-stairs in a highly-ridiculous manner, Clennam descended in the required attitude, and only released his fair burden at the dining-room door; indeed, even there she was rather difficult to be got rid of, remaining in his embrace to murmur, ‘Arthur, for mercy’s sake, don’t breathe it to papa!’\n\nShe accompanied Arthur into the room, where the Patriarch sat alone, with his list shoes on the fender, twirling his thumbs as if he had never left off. The youthful Patriarch, aged ten, looked out of his picture-frame above him with no calmer air than he. Both smooth heads were alike beaming, blundering, and bumpy.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, I am glad to see you. I hope you are well, sir, I hope you are well. Please to sit down, please to sit down.’\n\n‘I had hoped, sir,’ said Clennam, doing so, and looking round with a face of blank disappointment, ‘not to find you alone.’\n\n‘Ah, indeed?’ said the Patriarch, sweetly. ‘Ah, indeed?’\n\n‘I told you so you know papa,’ cried Flora.\n\n‘Ah, to be sure!’ returned the Patriarch. ‘Yes, just so. Ah, to be sure!’\n\n‘Pray, sir,’ demanded Clennam, anxiously, ‘is Miss Wade gone?’\n\n‘Miss--? Oh, you call her Wade,’ returned Mr Casby. ‘Highly proper.’\n\nArthur quickly returned, ‘What do you call her?’\n\n‘Wade,’ said Mr Casby. ‘Oh, always Wade.’\n\nAfter looking at the philanthropic visage and the long silky white hair for a few seconds, during which Mr Casby twirled his thumbs, and smiled at the fire as if he were benevolently wishing it to burn him that he might forgive it, Arthur began:\n\n‘I beg your pardon, Mr Casby--’\n\n‘Not so, not so,’ said the Patriarch, ‘not so.’\n\n‘--But, Miss Wade had an attendant with her--a young woman brought up by friends of mine, over whom her influence is not considered very salutary, and to whom I should be glad to have the opportunity of giving the assurance that she has not yet forfeited the interest of those protectors.’\n\n‘Really, really?’ returned the Patriarch.\n\n‘Will you therefore be so good as to give me the address of Miss Wade?’\n\n‘Dear, dear, dear!’ said the Patriarch, ‘how very unfortunate! If you had only sent in to me when they were here! I observed the young woman, Mr Clennam. A fine full-coloured young woman, Mr Clennam, with very dark hair and very dark eyes. If I mistake not, if I mistake not?’\n\nArthur assented, and said once more with new expression, ‘If you would be so good as to give me the address.’\n\n‘Dear, dear, dear!’ exclaimed the Patriarch in sweet regret. ‘Tut, tut, tut! what a pity, what a pity! I have no address, sir. Miss Wade mostly lives abroad, Mr Clennam. She has done so for some years, and she is (if I may say so of a fellow-creature and a lady) fitful and uncertain to a fault, Mr Clennam. I may not see her again for a long, long time. I may never see her again. What a pity, what a pity!’\n\nClennam saw now, that he had as much hope of getting assistance out of the Portrait as out of the Patriarch; but he said nevertheless:\n\n‘Mr Casby, could you, for the satisfaction of the friends I have mentioned, and under any obligation of secrecy that you may consider it your duty to impose, give me any information at all touching Miss Wade? I have seen her abroad, and I have seen her at home, but I know nothing of her. Could you give me any account of her whatever?’\n\n‘None,’ returned the Patriarch, shaking his big head with his utmost benevolence. ‘None at all. Dear, dear, dear! What a real pity that she stayed so short a time, and you delayed! As confidential agency business, agency business, I have occasionally paid this lady money; but what satisfaction is it to you, sir, to know that?’\n\n‘Truly, none at all,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Truly,’ assented the Patriarch, with a shining face as he philanthropically smiled at the fire, ‘none at all, sir. You hit the wise answer, Mr Clennam. Truly, none at all, sir.’\n\nHis turning of his smooth thumbs over one another as he sat there, was so typical to Clennam of the way in which he would make the subject revolve if it were pursued, never showing any new part of it nor allowing it to make the smallest advance, that it did much to help to convince him of his labour having been in vain. He might have taken any time to think about it, for Mr Casby, well accustomed to get on anywhere by leaving everything to his bumps and his white hair, knew his strength to lie in silence. So there Casby sat, twirling and twirling, and making his polished head and forehead look largely benevolent in every knob.\n\nWith this spectacle before him, Arthur had risen to go, when from the inner Dock where the good ship Pancks was hove down when out in no cruising ground, the noise was heard of that steamer labouring towards him. It struck Arthur that the noise began demonstratively far off, as though Mr Pancks sought to impress on any one who might happen to think about it, that he was working on from out of hearing.\n\nMr Pancks and he shook hands, and the former brought his employer a letter or two to sign. Mr Pancks in shaking hands merely scratched his eyebrow with his left forefinger and snorted once, but Clennam, who understood him better now than of old, comprehended that he had almost done for the evening and wished to say a word to him outside. Therefore, when he had taken his leave of Mr Casby, and (which was a more difficult process) of Flora, he sauntered in the neighbourhood on Mr Pancks’s line of road.\n\nHe had waited but a short time when Mr Pancks appeared. Mr Pancks shaking hands again with another expressive snort, and taking off his hat to put his hair up, Arthur thought he received his cue to speak to him as one who knew pretty well what had just now passed. Therefore he said, without any preface:\n\n‘I suppose they were really gone, Pancks?’\n\n‘Yes,’ replied Pancks. ‘They were really gone.’\n\n‘Does he know where to find that lady?’\n\n‘Can’t say. I should think so.’\n\nMr Pancks did not? No, Mr Pancks did not. Did Mr Pancks know anything about her?\n\n‘I expect,’ rejoined that worthy, ‘I know as much about her as she knows about herself. She is somebody’s child--anybody’s, nobody’s. Put her in a room in London here with any six people old enough to be her parents, and her parents may be there for anything she knows. They may be in any house she sees, they may be in any churchyard she passes, she may run against ‘em in any street, she may make chance acquaintance of ‘em at any time; and never know it. She knows nothing about ‘em. She knows nothing about any relative whatever. Never did. Never will.’\n\n‘Mr Casby could enlighten her, perhaps?’\n\n‘May be,’ said Pancks. ‘I expect so, but don’t know. He has long had money (not overmuch as I make out) in trust to dole out to her when she can’t do without it. Sometimes she’s proud and won’t touch it for a length of time; sometimes she’s so poor that she must have it. She writhes under her life. A woman more angry, passionate, reckless, and revengeful never lived. She came for money to-night. Said she had peculiar occasion for it.’\n\n‘I think,’ observed Clennam musing, ‘I by chance know what occasion--I mean into whose pocket the money is to go.’\n\n‘Indeed?’ said Pancks. ‘If it’s a compact, I recommend that party to be exact in it. I wouldn’t trust myself to that woman, young and handsome as she is, if I had wronged her; no, not for twice my proprietor’s money! Unless,’ Pancks added as a saving clause, ‘I had a lingering illness on me, and wanted to get it over.’\n\nArthur, hurriedly reviewing his own observation of her, found it to tally pretty nearly with Mr Pancks’s view.\n\n‘The wonder is to me,’ pursued Pancks, ‘that she has never done for my proprietor, as the only person connected with her story she can lay hold of. Mentioning that, I may tell you, between ourselves, that I am sometimes tempted to do for him myself.’\n\nArthur started and said, ‘Dear me, Pancks, don’t say that!’\n\n‘Understand me,’ said Pancks, extending five cropped coaly finger-nails on Arthur’s arm; ‘I don’t mean, cut his throat. But by all that’s precious, if he goes too far, I’ll cut his hair!’\n\nHaving exhibited himself in the new light of enunciating this tremendous threat, Mr Pancks, with a countenance of grave import, snorted several times and steamed away.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken\n\nThe shady waiting-rooms of the Circumlocution Office, where he passed a good deal of time in company with various troublesome Convicts who were under sentence to be broken alive on that wheel, had afforded Arthur Clennam ample leisure, in three or four successive days, to exhaust the subject of his late glimpse of Miss Wade and Tattycoram. He had been able to make no more of it and no less of it, and in this unsatisfactory condition he was fain to leave it.\n\nDuring this space he had not been to his mother’s dismal old house. One of his customary evenings for repairing thither now coming round, he left his dwelling and his partner at nearly nine o’clock, and slowly walked in the direction of that grim home of his youth.\n\nIt always affected his imagination as wrathful, mysterious, and sad; and his imagination was sufficiently impressible to see the whole neighbourhood under some tinge of its dark shadow. As he went along, upon a dreary night, the dim streets by which he went, seemed all depositories of oppressive secrets. The deserted counting-houses, with their secrets of books and papers locked up in chests and safes; the banking-houses, with their secrets of strong rooms and wells, the keys of which were in a very few secret pockets and a very few secret breasts; the secrets of all the dispersed grinders in the vast mill, among whom there were doubtless plunderers, forgers, and trust-betrayers of many sorts, whom the light of any day that dawned might reveal; he could have fancied that these things, in hiding, imparted a heaviness to the air. The shadow thickening and thickening as he approached its source, he thought of the secrets of the lonely church-vaults, where the people who had hoarded and secreted in iron coffers were in their turn similarly hoarded, not yet at rest from doing harm; and then of the secrets of the river, as it rolled its turbid tide between two frowning wildernesses of secrets, extending, thick and dense, for many miles, and warding off the free air and the free country swept by winds and wings of birds.\n\nThe shadow still darkening as he drew near the house, the melancholy room which his father had once occupied, haunted by the appealing face he had himself seen fade away with him when there was no other watcher by the bed, arose before his mind. Its close air was secret. The gloom, and must, and dust of the whole tenement, were secret. At the heart of it his mother presided, inflexible of face, indomitable of will, firmly holding all the secrets of her own and his father’s life, and austerely opposing herself, front to front, to the great final secret of all life.\n\nHe had turned into the narrow and steep street from which the court of enclosure wherein the house stood opened, when another footstep turned into it behind him, and so close upon his own that he was jostled to the wall. As his mind was teeming with these thoughts, the encounter took him altogether unprepared, so that the other passenger had had time to say, boisterously, ‘Pardon! Not my fault!’ and to pass on before the instant had elapsed which was requisite to his recovery of the realities about him.\n\nWhen that moment had flashed away, he saw that the man striding on before him was the man who had been so much in his mind during the last few days. It was no casual resemblance, helped out by the force of the impression the man made upon him. It was the man; the man he had followed in company with the girl, and whom he had overheard talking to Miss Wade.\n\nThe street was a sharp descent and was crooked too, and the man (who although not drunk had the air of being flushed with some strong drink) went down it so fast that Clennam lost him as he looked at him. With no defined intention of following him, but with an impulse to keep the figure in view a little longer, Clennam quickened his pace to pass the twist in the street which hid him from his sight. On turning it, he saw the man no more.\n\nStanding now, close to the gateway of his mother’s house, he looked down the street: but it was empty. There was no projecting shadow large enough to obscure the man; there was no turning near that he could have taken; nor had there been any audible sound of the opening and closing of a door. Nevertheless, he concluded that the man must have had a key in his hand, and must have opened one of the many house-doors and gone in.\n\nRuminating on this strange chance and strange glimpse, he turned into the court-yard. As he looked, by mere habit, towards the feebly lighted windows of his mother’s room, his eyes encountered the figure he had just lost, standing against the iron railings of the little waste enclosure looking up at those windows and laughing to himself. Some of the many vagrant cats who were always prowling about there by night, and who had taken fright at him, appeared to have stopped when he had stopped, and were looking at him with eyes by no means unlike his own from tops of walls and porches, and other safe points of pause. He had only halted for a moment to entertain himself thus; he immediately went forward, throwing the end of his cloak off his shoulder as he went, ascended the unevenly sunken steps, and knocked a sounding knock at the door.\n\nClennam’s surprise was not so absorbing but that he took his resolution without any incertitude. He went up to the door too, and ascended the steps too. His friend looked at him with a braggart air, and sang to himself.\n\n‘Who passes by this road so late? Compagnon de la Majolaine; Who passes by this road so late? Always gay!’\n\nAfter which he knocked again.\n\n‘You are impatient, sir,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘I am, sir. Death of my life, sir,’ returned the stranger, ‘it’s my character to be impatient!’\n\nThe sound of Mistress Affery cautiously chaining the door before she opened it, caused them both to look that way. Affery opened it a very little, with a flaring candle in her hands and asked who was that, at that time of night, with that knock! ‘Why, Arthur!’ she added with astonishment, seeing him first. ‘Not you sure? Ah, Lord save us! No,’ she cried out, seeing the other. ‘Him again!’\n\n‘It’s true! Him again, dear Mrs Flintwinch,’ cried the stranger. ‘Open the door, and let me take my dear friend Jeremiah to my arms! Open the door, and let me hasten myself to embrace my Flintwinch!’\n\n‘He’s not at home,’ cried Affery.\n\n‘Fetch him!’ cried the stranger. ‘Fetch my Flintwinch! Tell him that it is his old Blandois, who comes from arriving in England; tell him that it is his little boy who is here, his cabbage, his well-beloved! Open the door, beautiful Mrs Flintwinch, and in the meantime let me to pass upstairs, to present my compliments--homage of Blandois--to my lady! My lady lives always? It is well. Open then!’\n\nTo Arthur’s increased surprise, Mistress Affery, stretching her eyes wide at himself, as if in warning that this was not a gentleman for him to interfere with, drew back the chain, and opened the door. The stranger, without ceremony, walked into the hall, leaving Arthur to follow him.\n\n‘Despatch then! Achieve then! Bring my Flintwinch! Announce me to my lady!’ cried the stranger, clanking about the stone floor.\n\n‘Pray tell me, Affery,’ said Arthur aloud and sternly, as he surveyed him from head to foot with indignation; ‘who is this gentleman?’\n\n‘Pray tell me, Affery,’ the stranger repeated in his turn, ‘who--ha, ha, ha!--who is this gentleman?’\n\nThe voice of Mrs Clennam opportunely called from her chamber above, ‘Affery, let them both come up. Arthur, come straight to me!’\n\n‘Arthur?’ exclaimed Blandois, taking off his hat at arm’s length, and bringing his heels together from a great stride in making him a flourishing bow. ‘The son of my lady? I am the all-devoted of the son of my lady!’\n\nArthur looked at him again in no more flattering manner than before, and, turning on his heel without acknowledgment, went up-stairs. The visitor followed him up-stairs. Mistress Affery took the key from behind the door, and deftly slipped out to fetch her lord.\n\nA bystander, informed of the previous appearance of Monsieur Blandois in that room, would have observed a difference in Mrs Clennam’s present reception of him. Her face was not one to betray it; and her suppressed manner, and her set voice, were equally under her control. It wholly consisted in her never taking her eyes off his face from the moment of his entrance, and in her twice or thrice, when he was becoming noisy, swaying herself a very little forward in the chair in which she sat upright, with her hands immovable upon its elbows; as if she gave him the assurance that he should be presently heard at any length he would. Arthur did not fail to observe this; though the difference between the present occasion and the former was not within his power of observation.\n\n‘Madame,’ said Blandois, ‘do me the honour to present me to Monsieur, your son. It appears to me, madame, that Monsieur, your son, is disposed to complain of me. He is not polite.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Arthur, striking in expeditiously, ‘whoever you are, and however you come to be here, if I were the master of this house I would lose no time in placing you on the outside of it.’\n\n‘But you are not,’ said his mother, without looking at him. ‘Unfortunately for the gratification of your unreasonable temper, you are not the master, Arthur.’\n\n‘I make no claim to be, mother. If I object to this person’s manner of conducting himself here, and object to it so much, that if I had any authority here I certainly would not suffer him to remain a minute, I object on your account.’\n\n‘In the case of objection being necessary,’ she returned, ‘I could object for myself. And of course I should.’\n\nThe subject of their dispute, who had seated himself, laughed aloud, and rapped his legs with his hand.\n\n‘You have no right,’ said Mrs Clennam, always intent on Blandois, however directly she addressed her son, ‘to speak to the prejudice of any gentleman (least of all a gentleman from another country), because he does not conform to your standard, or square his behaviour by your rules. It is possible that the gentleman may, on similar grounds, object to you.’\n\n‘I hope so,’ returned Arthur.\n\n‘The gentleman,’ pursued Mrs Clennam, ‘on a former occasion brought a letter of recommendation to us from highly esteemed and responsible correspondents. I am perfectly unacquainted with the gentleman’s object in coming here at present. I am entirely ignorant of it, and cannot be supposed likely to be able to form the remotest guess at its nature;’ her habitual frown became stronger, as she very slowly and weightily emphasised those words; ‘but, when the gentleman proceeds to explain his object, as I shall beg him to have the goodness to do to myself and Flintwinch, when Flintwinch returns, it will prove, no doubt, to be one more or less in the usual way of our business, which it will be both our business and our pleasure to advance. It can be nothing else.’\n\n‘We shall see, madame!’ said the man of business.\n\n‘We shall see,’ she assented. ‘The gentleman is acquainted with Flintwinch; and when the gentleman was in London last, I remember to have heard that he and Flintwinch had some entertainment or good-fellowship together. I am not in the way of knowing much that passes outside this room, and the jingle of little worldly things beyond it does not much interest me; but I remember to have heard that.’\n\n‘Right, madame. It is true.’ He laughed again, and whistled the burden of the tune he had sung at the door.\n\n‘Therefore, Arthur,’ said his mother, ‘the gentleman comes here as an acquaintance, and no stranger; and it is much to be regretted that your unreasonable temper should have found offence in him. I regret it. I say so to the gentleman. You will not say so, I know; therefore I say it for myself and Flintwinch, since with us two the gentleman’s business lies.’\n\nThe key of the door below was now heard in the lock, and the door was heard to open and close. In due sequence Mr Flintwinch appeared; on whose entrance the visitor rose from his chair, laughing loud, and folded him in a close embrace.\n\n‘How goes it, my cherished friend!’ said he. ‘How goes the world, my Flintwinch? Rose-coloured? So much the better, so much the better! Ah, but you look charming! Ah, but you look young and fresh as the flowers of Spring! Ah, good little boy! Brave child, brave child!’\n\nWhile heaping these compliments on Mr Flintwinch, he rolled him about with a hand on each of his shoulders, until the staggerings of that gentleman, who under the circumstances was dryer and more twisted than ever, were like those of a teetotum nearly spent.\n\n‘I had a presentiment, last time, that we should be better and more intimately acquainted. Is it coming on you, Flintwinch? Is it yet coming on?’\n\n‘Why, no, sir,’ retorted Mr Flintwinch. ‘Not unusually. Hadn’t you better be seated? You have been calling for some more of that port, sir, I guess?’\n\n‘Ah, Little joker! Little pig!’ cried the visitor. ‘Ha ha ha ha!’ And throwing Mr Flintwinch away, as a closing piece of raillery, he sat down again.\n\nThe amazement, suspicion, resentment, and shame, with which Arthur looked on at all this, struck him dumb. Mr Flintwinch, who had spun backward some two or three yards under the impetus last given to him, brought himself up with a face completely unchanged in its stolidity except as it was affected by shortness of breath, and looked hard at Arthur. Not a whit less reticent and wooden was Mr Flintwinch outwardly, than in the usual course of things: the only perceptible difference in him being that the knot of cravat which was generally under his ear, had worked round to the back of his head: where it formed an ornamental appendage not unlike a bagwig, and gave him something of a courtly appearance.\n\nAs Mrs Clennam never removed her eyes from Blandois (on whom they had some effect, as a steady look has on a lower sort of dog), so Jeremiah never removed his from Arthur. It was as if they had tacitly agreed to take their different provinces. Thus, in the ensuing silence, Jeremiah stood scraping his chin and looking at Arthur as though he were trying to screw his thoughts out of him with an instrument.\n\nAfter a little, the visitor, as if he felt the silence irksome, rose, and impatiently put himself with his back to the sacred fire which had burned through so many years. Thereupon Mrs Clennam said, moving one of her hands for the first time, and moving it very slightly with an action of dismissal:\n\n‘Please to leave us to our business, Arthur.’\n\n‘Mother, I do so with reluctance.’\n\n‘Never mind with what,’ she returned, ‘or with what not. Please to leave us. Come back at any other time when you may consider it a duty to bury half an hour wearily here. Good night.’\n\nShe held up her muffled fingers that he might touch them with his, according to their usual custom, and he stood over her wheeled chair to touch her face with his lips. He thought, then, that her cheek was more strained than usual, and that it was colder. As he followed the direction of her eyes, in rising again, towards Mr Flintwinch’s good friend, Mr Blandois, Mr Blandois snapped his finger and thumb with one loud contemptuous snap.\n\n‘I leave your--your business acquaintance in my mother’s room, Mr Flintwinch,’ said Clennam, ‘with a great deal of surprise and a great deal of unwillingness.’\n\nThe person referred to snapped his finger and thumb again.\n\n‘Good night, mother.’\n\n‘Good night.’\n\n‘I had a friend once, my good comrade Flintwinch,’ said Blandois, standing astride before the fire, and so evidently saying it to arrest Clennam’s retreating steps, that he lingered near the door; ‘I had a friend once, who had heard so much of the dark side of this city and its ways, that he wouldn’t have confided himself alone by night with two people who had an interest in getting him under the ground--my faith! not even in a respectable house like this--unless he was bodily too strong for them. Bah! What a poltroon, my Flintwinch! Eh?’\n‘A cur, sir.’\n\n‘Agreed! A cur. But he wouldn’t have done it, my Flintwinch, unless he had known them to have the will to silence him, without the power. He wouldn’t have drunk from a glass of water under such circumstances--not even in a respectable house like this, my Flintwinch--unless he had seen one of them drink first, and swallow too!’\n\nDisdaining to speak, and indeed not very well able, for he was half-choking, Clennam only glanced at the visitor as he passed out. The visitor saluted him with another parting snap, and his nose came down over his moustache and his moustache went up under his nose, in an ominous and ugly smile.\n\n‘For Heaven’s sake, Affery,’ whispered Clennam, as she opened the door for him in the dark hall, and he groped his way to the sight of the night-sky, ‘what is going on here?’\n\nHer own appearance was sufficiently ghastly, standing in the dark with her apron thrown over her head, and speaking behind it in a low, deadened voice.\n\n‘Don’t ask me anything, Arthur. I’ve been in a dream for ever so long. Go away!’\n\nHe went out, and she shut the door upon him. He looked up at the windows of his mother’s room, and the dim light, deadened by the yellow blinds, seemed to say a response after Affery, and to mutter, ‘Don’t ask me anything. Go away!’\n\nDear Mr Clennam,\n\nAs I said in my last that it was best for nobody to write to me, and as my sending you another little letter can therefore give you no other trouble than the trouble of reading it (perhaps you may not find leisure for even that, though I hope you will some day), I am now going to devote an hour to writing to you again. This time, I write from Rome.\n\nWe left Venice before Mr and Mrs Gowan did, but they were not so long upon the road as we were, and did not travel by the same way, and so when we arrived we found them in a lodging here, in a place called the Via Gregoriana. I dare say you know it.\n\nNow I am going to tell you all I can about them, because I know that is what you most want to hear. Theirs is not a very comfortable lodging, but perhaps I thought it less so when I first saw it than you would have done, because you have been in many different countries and have seen many different customs. Of course it is a far, far better place--millions of times--than any I have ever been used to until lately; and I fancy I don’t look at it with my own eyes, but with hers. For it would be easy to see that she has always been brought up in a tender and happy home, even if she had not told me so with great love for it.\n\nWell, it is a rather bare lodging up a rather dark common staircase, and it is nearly all a large dull room, where Mr Gowan paints. The windows are blocked up where any one could look out, and the walls have been all drawn over with chalk and charcoal by others who have lived there before--oh,--I should think, for years! There is a curtain more dust-coloured than red, which divides it, and the part behind the curtain makes the private sitting-room. When I first saw her there she was alone, and her work had fallen out of her hand, and she was looking up at the sky shining through the tops of the windows. Pray do not be uneasy when I tell you, but it was not quite so airy, nor so bright, nor so cheerful, nor so happy and youthful altogether as I should have liked it to be.\n\nOn account of Mr Gowan’s painting Papa’s picture (which I am not quite convinced I should have known from the likeness if I had not seen him doing it), I have had more opportunities of being with her since then than I might have had without this fortunate chance. She is very much alone. Very much alone indeed.\n\nShall I tell you about the second time I saw her? I went one day, when it happened that I could run round by myself, at four or five o’clock in the afternoon. She was then dining alone, and her solitary dinner had been brought in from somewhere, over a kind of brazier with a fire in it, and she had no company or prospect of company, that I could see, but the old man who had brought it. He was telling her a long story (of robbers outside the walls being taken up by a stone statue of a Saint), to entertain her--as he said to me when I came out, ‘because he had a daughter of his own, though she was not so pretty.’\n\nI ought now to mention Mr Gowan, before I say what little more I have to say about her. He must admire her beauty, and he must be proud of her, for everybody praises it, and he must be fond of her, and I do not doubt that he is--but in his way. You know his way, and if it appears as careless and discontented in your eyes as it does in mine, I am not wrong in thinking that it might be better suited to her. If it does not seem so to you, I am quite sure I am wholly mistaken; for your unchanged poor child confides in your knowledge and goodness more than she could ever tell you if she was to try. But don’t be frightened, I am not going to try.\n\nOwing (as I think, if you think so too) to Mr Gowan’s unsettled and dissatisfied way, he applies himself to his profession very little. He does nothing steadily or patiently; but equally takes things up and throws them down, and does them, or leaves them undone, without caring about them. When I have heard him talking to Papa during the sittings for the picture, I have sat wondering whether it could be that he has no belief in anybody else, because he has no belief in himself. Is it so? I wonder what you will say when you come to this! I know how you will look, and I can almost hear the voice in which you would tell me on the Iron Bridge.\n\nMr Gowan goes out a good deal among what is considered the best company here--though he does not look as if he enjoyed it or liked it when he is with it--and she sometimes accompanies him, but lately she has gone out very little. I think I have noticed that they have an inconsistent way of speaking about her, as if she had made some great self-interested success in marrying Mr Gowan, though, at the same time, the very same people, would not have dreamed of taking him for themselves or their daughters. Then he goes into the country besides, to think about making sketches; and in all places where there are visitors, he has a large acquaintance and is very well known. Besides all this, he has a friend who is much in his society both at home and away from home, though he treats this friend very coolly and is very uncertain in his behaviour to him. I am quite sure (because she has told me so), that she does not like this friend. He is so revolting to me, too, that his being away from here, at present, is quite a relief to my mind. How much more to hers!\n\nBut what I particularly want you to know, and why I have resolved to tell you so much while I am afraid it may make you a little uncomfortable without occasion, is this. She is so true and so devoted, and knows so completely that all her love and duty are his for ever, that you may be certain she will love him, admire him, praise him, and conceal all his faults, until she dies. I believe she conceals them, and always will conceal them, even from herself. She has given him a heart that can never be taken back; and however much he may try it, he will never wear out its affection. You know the truth of this, as you know everything, far far better than I; but I cannot help telling you what a nature she shows, and that you can never think too well of her.\n\nI have not yet called her by her name in this letter, but we are such friends now that I do so when we are quietly together, and she speaks to me by my name--I mean, not my Christian name, but the name you gave me. When she began to call me Amy, I told her my short story, and that you had always called me Little Dorrit. I told her that the name was much dearer to me than any other, and so she calls me Little Dorrit too.\n\nPerhaps you have not heard from her father or mother yet, and may not know that she has a baby son. He was born only two days ago, and just a week after they came. It has made them very happy. However, I must tell you, as I am to tell you all, that I fancy they are under a constraint with Mr Gowan, and that they feel as if his mocking way with them was sometimes a slight given to their love for her. It was but yesterday, when I was there, that I saw Mr Meagles change colour, and get up and go out, as if he was afraid that he might say so, unless he prevented himself by that means. Yet I am sure they are both so considerate, good-humoured, and reasonable, that he might spare them. It is hard in him not to think of them a little more.\n\nI stopped at the last full stop to read all this over. It looked at first as if I was taking on myself to understand and explain so much, that I was half inclined not to send it. But when I thought it over a little, I felt more hopeful for your knowing at once that I had only been watchful for you, and had only noticed what I think I have noticed, because I was quickened by your interest in it. Indeed, you may be sure that is the truth.\n\nAnd now I have done with the subject in the present letter, and have little left to say.\n\nWe are all quite well, and Fanny improves every day. You can hardly think how kind she is to me, and what pains she takes with me. She has a lover, who has followed her, first all the way from Switzerland, and then all the way from Venice, and who has just confided to me that he means to follow her everywhere. I was much confused by his speaking to me about it, but he would. I did not know what to say, but at last I told him that I thought he had better not. For Fanny (but I did not tell him this) is much too spirited and clever to suit him. Still, he said he would, all the same. I have no lover, of course.\n\nIf you should ever get so far as this in this long letter, you will perhaps say, Surely Little Dorrit will not leave off without telling me something about her travels, and surely it is time she did. I think it is indeed, but I don’t know what to tell you. Since we left Venice we have been in a great many wonderful places, Genoa and Florence among them, and have seen so many wonderful sights, that I am almost giddy when I think what a crowd they make. But you can tell me so much more about them than I can tell you, that why should I tire you with my accounts and descriptions?\n\nDear Mr Clennam, as I had the courage to tell you what the familiar difficulties in my travelling mind were before, I will not be a coward now. One of my frequent thoughts is this:--Old as these cities are, their age itself is hardly so curious, to my reflections, as that they should have been in their places all through those days when I did not even know of the existence of more than two or three of them, and when I scarcely knew of anything outside our old walls. There is something melancholy in it, and I don’t know why. When we went to see the famous leaning tower at Pisa, it was a bright sunny day, and it and the buildings near it looked so old, and the earth and the sky looked so young, and its shadow on the ground was so soft and retired! I could not at first think how beautiful it was, or how curious, but I thought, ‘O how many times when the shadow of the wall was falling on our room, and when that weary tread of feet was going up and down the yard--O how many times this place was just as quiet and lovely as it is to-day!’ It quite overpowered me. My heart was so full that tears burst out of my eyes, though I did what I could to restrain them. And I have the same feeling often--often.\n\nDo you know that since the change in our fortunes, though I appear to myself to have dreamed more than before, I have always dreamed of myself as very young indeed! I am not very old, you may say. No, but that is not what I mean. I have always dreamed of myself as a child learning to do needlework. I have often dreamed of myself as back there, seeing faces in the yard little known, and which I should have thought I had quite forgotten; but, as often as not, I have been abroad here--in Switzerland, or France, or Italy--somewhere where we have been--yet always as that little child. I have dreamed of going down to Mrs General, with the patches on my clothes in which I can first remember myself. I have over and over again dreamed of taking my place at dinner at Venice when we have had a large company, in the mourning for my poor mother which I wore when I was eight years old, and wore long after it was threadbare and would mend no more. It has been a great distress to me to think how irreconcilable the company would consider it with my father’s wealth, and how I should displease and disgrace him and Fanny and Edward by so plainly disclosing what they wished to keep secret. But I have not grown out of the little child in thinking of it; and at the self-same moment I have dreamed that I have sat with the heart-ache at table, calculating the expenses of the dinner, and quite distracting myself with thinking how they were ever to be made good. I have never dreamed of the change in our fortunes itself; I have never dreamed of your coming back with me that memorable morning to break it; I have never even dreamed of you.\n\nDear Mr Clennam, it is possible that I have thought of you--and others--so much by day, that I have no thoughts left to wander round you by night. For I must now confess to you that I suffer from home-sickness--that I long so ardently and earnestly for home, as sometimes, when no one sees me, to pine for it. I cannot bear to turn my face further away from it. My heart is a little lightened when we turn towards it, even for a few miles, and with the knowledge that we are soon to turn away again. So dearly do I love the scene of my poverty and your kindness. O so dearly, O so dearly!\n\nHeaven knows when your poor child will see England again. We are all fond of the life here (except me), and there are no plans for our return. My dear father talks of a visit to London late in this next spring, on some affairs connected with the property, but I have no hope that he will bring me with him.\n\nI have tried to get on a little better under Mrs General’s instruction, and I hope I am not quite so dull as I used to be. I have begun to speak and understand, almost easily, the hard languages I told you about. I did not remember, at the moment when I wrote last, that you knew them both; but I remembered it afterwards, and it helped me on. God bless you, dear Mr Clennam. Do not forget\n\nYour ever grateful and affectionate\n\n# LITTLE DORRIT.\n\nP.S.--Particularly remember that Minnie Gowan deserves the best remembrance in which you can hold her. You cannot think too generously or too highly of her. I forgot Mr Pancks last time. Please, if you should see him, give him your Little Dorrit’s kind regard. He was very good to Little D.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden\n\nThe famous name of Merdle became, every day, more famous in the land. Nobody knew that the Merdle of such high renown had ever done any good to any one, alive or dead, or to any earthly thing; nobody knew that he had any capacity or utterance of any sort in him, which had ever thrown, for any creature, the feeblest farthing-candle ray of light on any path of duty or diversion, pain or pleasure, toil or rest, fact or fancy, among the multiplicity of paths in the labyrinth trodden by the sons of Adam; nobody had the smallest reason for supposing the clay of which this object of worship was made, to be other than the commonest clay, with as clogged a wick smouldering inside of it as ever kept an image of humanity from tumbling to pieces. All people knew (or thought they knew) that he had made himself immensely rich; and, for that reason alone, prostrated themselves before him, more degradedly and less excusably than the darkest savage creeps out of his hole in the ground to propitiate, in some log or reptile, the Deity of his benighted soul.\n\nNay, the high priests of this worship had the man before them as a protest against their meanness. The multitude worshipped on trust--though always distinctly knowing why--but the officiators at the altar had the man habitually in their view. They sat at his feasts, and he sat at theirs. There was a spectre always attendant on him, saying to these high priests, ‘Are such the signs you trust, and love to honour; this head, these eyes, this mode of speech, the tone and manner of this man? You are the levers of the Circumlocution Office, and the rulers of men. When half-a-dozen of you fall out by the ears, it seems that mother earth can give birth to no other rulers. Does your qualification lie in the superior knowledge of men which accepts, courts, and puffs this man? Or, if you are competent to judge aright the signs I never fail to show you when he appears among you, is your superior honesty your qualification?’ Two rather ugly questions these, always going about town with Mr Merdle; and there was a tacit agreement that they must be stifled.\n\nIn Mrs Merdle’s absence abroad, Mr Merdle still kept the great house open for the passage through it of a stream Of visitors. A few of these took affable possession of the establishment. Three or four ladies of distinction and liveliness used to say to one another, ‘Let us dine at our dear Merdle’s next Thursday. Whom shall we have?’ Our dear Merdle would then receive his instructions; and would sit heavily among the company at table and wander lumpishly about his drawing-rooms afterwards, only remarkable for appearing to have nothing to do with the entertainment beyond being in its way.\n\nThe Chief Butler, the Avenging Spirit of this great man’s life, relaxed nothing of his severity. He looked on at these dinners when the bosom was not there, as he looked on at other dinners when the bosom was there; and his eye was a basilisk to Mr Merdle. He was a hard man, and would never bate an ounce of plate or a bottle of wine. He would not allow a dinner to be given, unless it was up to his mark. He set forth the table for his own dignity. If the guests chose to partake of what was served, he saw no objection; but it was served for the maintenance of his rank. As he stood by the sideboard he seemed to announce, ‘I have accepted office to look at this which is now before me, and to look at nothing less than this.’ If he missed the presiding bosom, it was as a part of his own state of which he was, from unavoidable circumstances, temporarily deprived, just as he might have missed a centre-piece, or a choice wine-cooler, which had been sent to the Banker’s.\n\nMr Merdle issued invitations for a Barnacle dinner. Lord Decimus was to be there, Mr Tite Barnacle was to be there, the pleasant young Barnacle was to be there; and the Chorus of Parliamentary Barnacles who went about the provinces when the House was up, warbling the praises of their Chief, were to be represented there. It was understood to be a great occasion. Mr Merdle was going to take up the Barnacles. Some delicate little negotiations had occurred between him and the noble Decimus--the young Barnacle of engaging manners acting as negotiator--and Mr Merdle had decided to cast the weight of his great probity and great riches into the Barnacle scale. Jobbery was suspected by the malicious; perhaps because it was indisputable that if the adherence of the immortal Enemy of Mankind could have been secured by a job, the Barnacles would have jobbed him--for the good of the country, for the good of the country.\n\nMrs Merdle had written to this magnificent spouse of hers, whom it was heresy to regard as anything less than all the British Merchants since the days of Whittington rolled into one, and gilded three feet deep all over--had written to this spouse of hers, several letters from Rome, in quick succession, urging upon him with importunity that now or never was the time to provide for Edmund Sparkler. Mrs Merdle had shown him that the case of Edmund was urgent, and that infinite advantages might result from his having some good thing directly. In the grammar of Mrs Merdle’s verbs on this momentous subject, there was only one mood, the Imperative; and that Mood had only one Tense, the Present. Mrs Merdle’s verbs were so pressingly presented to Mr Merdle to conjugate, that his sluggish blood and his long coat-cuffs became quite agitated.\n\nIn which state of agitation, Mr Merdle, evasively rolling his eyes round the Chief Butler’s shoes without raising them to the index of that stupendous creature’s thoughts, had signified to him his intention of giving a special dinner: not a very large dinner, but a very special dinner. The Chief Butler had signified, in return, that he had no objection to look on at the most expensive thing in that way that could be done; and the day of the dinner was now come.\n\nMr Merdle stood in one of his drawing-rooms, with his back to the fire, waiting for the arrival of his important guests. He seldom or never took the liberty of standing with his back to the fire unless he was quite alone. In the presence of the Chief Butler, he could not have done such a deed. He would have clasped himself by the wrists in that constabulary manner of his, and have paced up and down the hearthrug, or gone creeping about among the rich objects of furniture, if his oppressive retainer had appeared in the room at that very moment. The sly shadows which seemed to dart out of hiding when the fire rose, and to dart back into it when the fire fell, were sufficient witnesses of his making himself so easy. They were even more than sufficient, if his uncomfortable glances at them might be taken to mean anything.\n\nMr Merdle’s right hand was filled with the evening paper, and the evening paper was full of Mr Merdle. His wonderful enterprise, his wonderful wealth, his wonderful Bank, were the fattening food of the evening paper that night. The wonderful Bank, of which he was the chief projector, establisher, and manager, was the latest of the many Merdle wonders. So modest was Mr Merdle withal, in the midst of these splendid achievements, that he looked far more like a man in possession of his house under a distraint, than a commercial Colossus bestriding his own hearthrug, while the little ships were sailing into dinner.\n\nBehold the vessels coming into port! The engaging young Barnacle was the first arrival; but Bar overtook him on the staircase. Bar, strengthened as usual with his double eye-glass and his little jury droop, was overjoyed to see the engaging young Barnacle; and opined that we were going to sit in Banco, as we lawyers called it, to take a special argument?\n\n‘Indeed,’ said the sprightly young Barnacle, whose name was Ferdinand; ‘how so?’\n\n‘Nay,’ smiled Bar. ‘If you don’t know, how can I know? You are in the innermost sanctuary of the temple; I am one of the admiring concourse on the plain without.’\n\nBar could be light in hand, or heavy in hand, according to the customer he had to deal with. With Ferdinand Barnacle he was gossamer. Bar was likewise always modest and self-depreciatory--in his way. Bar was a man of great variety; but one leading thread ran through the woof of all his patterns. Every man with whom he had to do was in his eyes a jury-man; and he must get that jury-man over, if he could.\n\n‘Our illustrious host and friend,’ said Bar; ‘our shining mercantile star;--going into politics?’\n\n‘Going? He has been in Parliament some time, you know,’ returned the engaging young Barnacle.\n\n‘True,’ said Bar, with his light-comedy laugh for special jury-men, which was a very different thing from his low-comedy laugh for comic tradesmen on common juries: ‘he has been in Parliament for some time. Yet hitherto our star has been a vacillating and wavering star? Humph?’\n\nAn average witness would have been seduced by the Humph? into an affirmative answer, But Ferdinand Barnacle looked knowingly at Bar as he strolled up-stairs, and gave him no answer at all.\n\n‘Just so, just so,’ said Bar, nodding his head, for he was not to be put off in that way, ‘and therefore I spoke of our sitting _in Banco_ to take a special argument--meaning this to be a high and solemn occasion, when, as Captain Macheath says, “the judges are met: a terrible show!” We lawyers are sufficiently liberal, you see, to quote the Captain, though the Captain is severe upon us. Nevertheless, I think I could put in evidence an admission of the Captain’s,’ said Bar, with a little jocose roll of his head; for, in his legal current of speech, he always assumed the air of rallying himself with the best grace in the world; ‘an admission of the Captain’s that Law, in the gross, is at least intended to be impartial. For what says the Captain, if I quote him correctly--and if not,’ with a light-comedy touch of his double eye-glass on his companion’s shoulder, ‘my learned friend will set me right:\n\n“Since laws were made for every degree, To curb vice in others as well as in me, I wonder we ha’n’t better company Upon Tyburn Tree!”’\n\nThese words brought them to the drawing-room, where Mr Merdle stood before the fire. So immensely astounded was Mr Merdle by the entrance of Bar with such a reference in his mouth, that Bar explained himself to have been quoting Gay. ‘Assuredly not one of our Westminster Hall authorities,’ said he, ‘but still no despicable one to a man possessing the largely-practical Mr Merdle’s knowledge of the world.’\n\nMr Merdle looked as if he thought he would say something, but subsequently looked as if he thought he wouldn’t. The interval afforded time for Bishop to be announced.\n\nBishop came in with meekness, and yet with a strong and rapid step as if he wanted to get his seven-league dress-shoes on, and go round the world to see that everybody was in a satisfactory state. Bishop had no idea that there was anything significant in the occasion. That was the most remarkable trait in his demeanour. He was crisp, fresh, cheerful, affable, bland; but so surprisingly innocent.\n\nBar sidled up to prefer his politest inquiries in reference to the health of Mrs Bishop. Mrs Bishop had been a little unfortunate in the article of taking cold at a Confirmation, but otherwise was well. Young Mr Bishop was also well. He was down, with his young wife and little family, at his Cure of Souls.\n\nThe representatives of the Barnacle Chorus dropped in next, and Mr Merdle’s physician dropped in next. Bar, who had a bit of one eye and a bit of his double eye-glass for every one who came in at the door, no matter with whom he was conversing or what he was talking about, got among them all by some skilful means, without being seen to get at them, and touched each individual gentleman of the jury on his own individual favourite spot. With some of the Chorus, he laughed about the sleepy member who had gone out into the lobby the other night, and voted the wrong way: with others, he deplored that innovating spirit in the time which could not even be prevented from taking an unnatural interest in the public service and the public money: with the physician he had a word to say about the general health; he had also a little information to ask him for, concerning a professional man of unquestioned erudition and polished manners--but those credentials in their highest development he believed were the possession of other professors of the healing art (jury droop)--whom he had happened to have in the witness-box the day before yesterday, and from whom he had elicited in cross-examination that he claimed to be one of the exponents of this new mode of treatment which appeared to Bar to--eh?--well, Bar thought so; Bar had thought, and hoped, Physician would tell him so. Without presuming to decide where doctors disagreed, it did appear to Bar, viewing it as a question of common sense and not of so-called legal penetration, that this new system was--might be, in the presence of so great an authority--say, Humbug? Ah! Fortified by such encouragement, he could venture to say Humbug; and now Bar’s mind was relieved.\n\nMr Tite Barnacle, who, like Dr Johnson’s celebrated acquaintance, had only one idea in his head and that was a wrong one, had appeared by this time. This eminent gentleman and Mr Merdle, seated diverse ways and with ruminating aspects on a yellow ottoman in the light of the fire, holding no verbal communication with each other, bore a strong general resemblance to the two cows in the Cuyp picture over against them.\n\nBut now, Lord Decimus arrived. The Chief Butler, who up to this time had limited himself to a branch of his usual function by looking at the company as they entered (and that, with more of defiance than favour), put himself so far out of his way as to come up-stairs with him and announce him. Lord Decimus being an overpowering peer, a bashful young member of the Lower House who was the last fish but one caught by the Barnacles, and who had been invited on this occasion to commemorate his capture, shut his eyes when his Lordship came in.\n\nLord Decimus, nevertheless, was glad to see the Member. He was also glad to see Mr Merdle, glad to see Bishop, glad to see Bar, glad to see Physician, glad to see Tite Barnacle, glad to see Chorus, glad to see Ferdinand his private secretary. Lord Decimus, though one of the greatest of the earth, was not remarkable for ingratiatory manners, and Ferdinand had coached him up to the point of noticing all the fellows he might find there, and saying he was glad to see them. When he had achieved this rush of vivacity and condescension, his Lordship composed himself into the picture after Cuyp, and made a third cow in the group.\n\nBar, who felt that he had got all the rest of the jury and must now lay hold of the Foreman, soon came sidling up, double eye-glass in hand. Bar tendered the weather, as a subject neatly aloof from official reserve, for the Foreman’s consideration. Bar said that he was told (as everybody always is told, though who tells them, and why, will ever remain a mystery), that there was to be no wall-fruit this year. Lord Decimus had not heard anything amiss of his peaches, but rather believed, if his people were correct, he was to have no apples. No apples? Bar was lost in astonishment and concern. It would have been all one to him, in reality, if there had not been a pippin on the surface of the earth, but his show of interest in this apple question was positively painful. Now, to what, Lord Decimus--for we troublesome lawyers loved to gather information, and could never tell how useful it might prove to us--to what, Lord Decimus, was this to be attributed? Lord Decimus could not undertake to propound any theory about it. This might have stopped another man; but Bar, sticking to him fresh as ever, said, ‘As to pears, now?’\n\nLong after Bar got made Attorney-General, this was told of him as a master-stroke. Lord Decimus had a reminiscence about a pear-tree formerly growing in a garden near the back of his dame’s house at Eton, upon which pear-tree the only joke of his life perennially bloomed. It was a joke of a compact and portable nature, turning on the difference between Eton pears and Parliamentary pairs; but it was a joke, a refined relish of which would seem to have appeared to Lord Decimus impossible to be had without a thorough and intimate acquaintance with the tree. Therefore, the story at first had no idea of such a tree, sir, then gradually found it in winter, carried it through the changing season, saw it bud, saw it blossom, saw it bear fruit, saw the fruit ripen; in short, cultivated the tree in that diligent and minute manner before it got out of the bed-room window to steal the fruit, that many thanks had been offered up by belated listeners for the trees having been planted and grafted prior to Lord Decimus’s time. Bar’s interest in apples was so overtopped by the wrapt suspense in which he pursued the changes of these pears, from the moment when Lord Decimus solemnly opened with ‘Your mentioning pears recalls to my remembrance a pear-tree,’ down to the rich conclusion, ‘And so we pass, through the various changes of life, from Eton pears to Parliamentary pairs,’ that he had to go down-stairs with Lord Decimus, and even then to be seated next to him at table in order that he might hear the anecdote out. By that time, Bar felt that he had secured the Foreman, and might go to dinner with a good appetite.\n\nIt was a dinner to provoke an appetite, though he had not had one. The rarest dishes, sumptuously cooked and sumptuously served; the choicest fruits; the most exquisite wines; marvels of workmanship in gold and silver, china and glass; innumerable things delicious to the senses of taste, smell, and sight, were insinuated into its composition. O, what a wonderful man this Merdle, what a great man, what a master man, how blessedly and enviably endowed--in one word, what a rich man!\n\nHe took his usual poor eighteenpennyworth of food in his usual indigestive way, and had as little to say for himself as ever a wonderful man had. Fortunately Lord Decimus was one of those sublimities who have no occasion to be talked to, for they can be at any time sufficiently occupied with the contemplation of their own greatness. This enabled the bashful young Member to keep his eyes open long enough at a time to see his dinner. But, whenever Lord Decimus spoke, he shut them again.\n\nThe agreeable young Barnacle, and Bar, were the talkers of the party. Bishop would have been exceedingly agreeable also, but that his innocence stood in his way. He was so soon left behind. When there was any little hint of anything being in the wind, he got lost directly. Worldly affairs were too much for him; he couldn’t make them out at all.\n\nThis was observable when Bar said, incidentally, that he was happy to have heard that we were soon to have the advantage of enlisting on the good side, the sound and plain sagacity--not demonstrative or ostentatious, but thoroughly sound and practical--of our friend Mr Sparkler.\n\nFerdinand Barnacle laughed, and said oh yes, he believed so. A vote was a vote, and always acceptable.\n\nBar was sorry to miss our good friend Mr Sparkler to-day, Mr Merdle.\n\n‘He is away with Mrs Merdle,’ returned that gentleman, slowly coming out of a long abstraction, in the course of which he had been fitting a tablespoon up his sleeve. ‘It is not indispensable for him to be on the spot.’\n\n‘The magic name of Merdle,’ said Bar, with the jury droop, ‘no doubt will suffice for all.’\n\n‘Why--yes--I believe so,’ assented Mr Merdle, putting the spoon aside, and clumsily hiding each of his hands in the coat-cuff of the other hand. ‘I believe the people in my interest down there will not make any difficulty.’\n\n‘Model people!’ said Bar.\n\n‘I am glad you approve of them,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\n‘And the people of those other two places, now,’ pursued Bar, with a bright twinkle in his keen eye, as it slightly turned in the direction of his magnificent neighbour; ‘we lawyers are always curious, always inquisitive, always picking up odds and ends for our patchwork minds, since there is no knowing when and where they may fit into some corner;--the people of those other two places now? Do they yield so laudably to the vast and cumulative influence of such enterprise and such renown; do those little rills become absorbed so quietly and easily, and, as it were by the influence of natural laws, so beautifully, in the swoop of the majestic stream as it flows upon its wondrous way enriching the surrounding lands; that their course is perfectly to be calculated, and distinctly to be predicated?’\n\nMr Merdle, a little troubled by Bar’s eloquence, looked fitfully about the nearest salt-cellar for some moments, and then said hesitating:\n\n‘They are perfectly aware, sir, of their duty to Society. They will return anybody I send to them for that purpose.’\n\n‘Cheering to know,’ said Bar. ‘Cheering to know.’\n\nThe three places in question were three little rotten holes in this Island, containing three little ignorant, drunken, guzzling, dirty, out-of-the-way constituencies, that had reeled into Mr Merdle’s pocket. Ferdinand Barnacle laughed in his easy way, and airily said they were a nice set of fellows. Bishop, mentally perambulating among paths of peace, was altogether swallowed up in absence of mind.\n\n‘Pray,’ asked Lord Decimus, casting his eyes around the table, ‘what is this story I have heard of a gentleman long confined in a debtors’ prison proving to be of a wealthy family, and having come into the inheritance of a large sum of money? I have met with a variety of allusions to it. Do you know anything of it, Ferdinand?’\n\n‘I only know this much,’ said Ferdinand, ‘that he has given the Department with which I have the honour to be associated;’ this sparkling young Barnacle threw off the phrase sportively, as who should say, We know all about these forms of speech, but we must keep it up, we must keep the game alive; ‘no end of trouble, and has put us into innumerable fixes.’\n\n‘Fixes?’ repeated Lord Decimus, with a majestic pausing and pondering on the word that made the bashful Member shut his eyes quite tight. ‘Fixes?’\n\n‘A very perplexing business indeed,’ observed Mr Tite Barnacle, with an air of grave resentment.\n\n‘What,’ said Lord Decimus, ‘was the character of his business; what was the nature of these--a--Fixes, Ferdinand?’\n\n‘Oh, it’s a good story, as a story,’ returned that gentleman; ‘as good a thing of its kind as need be. This Mr Dorrit (his name is Dorrit) had incurred a responsibility to us, ages before the fairy came out of the Bank and gave him his fortune, under a bond he had signed for the performance of a contract which was not at all performed. He was a partner in a house in some large way--spirits, or buttons, or wine, or blacking, or oatmeal, or woollen, or pork, or hooks and eyes, or iron, or treacle, or shoes, or something or other that was wanted for troops, or seamen, or somebody--and the house burst, and we being among the creditors, detainees were lodged on the part of the Crown in a scientific manner, and all the rest of it. When the fairy had appeared and he wanted to pay us off, Egad we had got into such an exemplary state of checking and counter-checking, signing and counter-signing, that it was six months before we knew how to take the money, or how to give a receipt for it. It was a triumph of public business,’ said this handsome young Barnacle, laughing heartily, ‘You never saw such a lot of forms in your life. “Why,” the attorney said to me one day, “if I wanted this office to give me two or three thousand pounds instead of take it, I couldn’t have more trouble about it.” “You are right, old fellow,” I told him, “and in future you’ll know that we have something to do here.”’ The pleasant young Barnacle finished by once more laughing heartily. He was a very easy, pleasant fellow indeed, and his manners were exceedingly winning.\n\nMr Tite Barnacle’s view of the business was of a less airy character. He took it ill that Mr Dorrit had troubled the Department by wanting to pay the money, and considered it a grossly informal thing to do after so many years. But Mr Tite Barnacle was a buttoned-up man, and consequently a weighty one. All buttoned-up men are weighty. All buttoned-up men are believed in. Whether or no the reserved and never-exercised power of unbuttoning, fascinates mankind; whether or no wisdom is supposed to condense and augment when buttoned up, and to evaporate when unbuttoned; it is certain that the man to whom importance is accorded is the buttoned-up man. Mr Tite Barnacle never would have passed for half his current value, unless his coat had been always buttoned-up to his white cravat.\n\n‘May I ask,’ said Lord Decimus, ‘if Mr Darrit--or Dorrit--has any family?’\n\nNobody else replying, the host said, ‘He has two daughters, my lord.’\n\n‘Oh! you are acquainted with him?’ asked Lord Decimus.\n\n‘Mrs Merdle is. Mr Sparkler is, too. In fact,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I rather believe that one of the young ladies has made an impression on Edmund Sparkler. He is susceptible, and--I--think--the conquest--’ Here Mr Merdle stopped, and looked at the table-cloth, as he usually did when he found himself observed or listened to.\n\nBar was uncommonly pleased to find that the Merdle family, and this family, had already been brought into contact. He submitted, in a low voice across the table to Bishop, that it was a kind of analogical illustration of those physical laws, in virtue of which Like flies to Like. He regarded this power of attraction in wealth to draw wealth to it, as something remarkably interesting and curious--something indefinably allied to the loadstone and gravitation. Bishop, who had ambled back to earth again when the present theme was broached, acquiesced. He said it was indeed highly important to Society that one in the trying situation of unexpectedly finding himself invested with a power for good or for evil in Society, should become, as it were, merged in the superior power of a more legitimate and more gigantic growth, the influence of which (as in the case of our friend at whose board we sat) was habitually exercised in harmony with the best interests of Society. Thus, instead of two rival and contending flames, a larger and a lesser, each burning with a lurid and uncertain glare, we had a blended and a softened light whose genial ray diffused an equable warmth throughout the land. Bishop seemed to like his own way of putting the case very much, and rather dwelt upon it; Bar, meanwhile (not to throw away a jury-man), making a show of sitting at his feet and feeding on his precepts.\n\nThe dinner and dessert being three hours long, the bashful Member cooled in the shadow of Lord Decimus faster than he warmed with food and drink, and had but a chilly time of it. Lord Decimus, like a tall tower in a flat country, seemed to project himself across the table-cloth, hide the light from the honourable Member, cool the honourable Member’s marrow, and give him a woeful idea of distance. When he asked this unfortunate traveller to take wine, he encompassed his faltering steps with the gloomiest of shades; and when he said, ‘Your health sir!’ all around him was barrenness and desolation.\n\nAt length Lord Decimus, with a coffee-cup in his hand, began to hover about among the pictures, and to cause an interesting speculation to arise in all minds as to the probabilities of his ceasing to hover, and enabling the smaller birds to flutter up-stairs; which could not be done until he had urged his noble pinions in that direction. After some delay, and several stretches of his wings which came to nothing, he soared to the drawing-rooms.\n\nAnd here a difficulty arose, which always does arise when two people are specially brought together at a dinner to confer with one another. Everybody (except Bishop, who had no suspicion of it) knew perfectly well that this dinner had been eaten and drunk, specifically to the end that Lord Decimus and Mr Merdle should have five minutes’ conversation together. The opportunity so elaborately prepared was now arrived, and it seemed from that moment that no mere human ingenuity could so much as get the two chieftains into the same room. Mr Merdle and his noble guest persisted in prowling about at opposite ends of the perspective. It was in vain for the engaging Ferdinand to bring Lord Decimus to look at the bronze horses near Mr Merdle. Then Mr Merdle evaded, and wandered away. It was in vain for him to bring Mr Merdle to Lord Decimus to tell him the history of the unique Dresden vases. Then Lord Decimus evaded and wandered away, while he was getting his man up to the mark.\n\n‘Did you ever see such a thing as this?’ said Ferdinand to Bar when he had been baffled twenty times.\n\n‘Often,’ returned Bar.\n\n‘Unless I butt one of them into an appointed corner, and you butt the other,’ said Ferdinand, ‘it will not come off after all.’\n\n‘Very good,’ said Bar. ‘I’ll butt Merdle, if you like; but not my lord.’\n\nFerdinand laughed, in the midst of his vexation. ‘Confound them both!’ said he, looking at his watch. ‘I want to get away. Why the deuce can’t they come together! They both know what they want and mean to do. Look at them!’\n\nThey were still looming at opposite ends of the perspective, each with an absurd pretence of not having the other on his mind, which could not have been more transparently ridiculous though his real mind had been chalked on his back. Bishop, who had just now made a third with Bar and Ferdinand, but whose innocence had again cut him out of the subject and washed him in sweet oil, was seen to approach Lord Decimus and glide into conversation.\n\n‘I must get Merdle’s doctor to catch and secure him, I suppose,’ said Ferdinand; ‘and then I must lay hold of my illustrious kinsman, and decoy him if I can--drag him if I can’t--to the conference.’\n\n‘Since you do me the honour,’ said Bar, with his slyest smile, to ask for my poor aid, it shall be yours with the greatest pleasure. I don’t think this is to be done by one man. But if you will undertake to pen my lord into that furthest drawing-room where he is now so profoundly engaged, I will undertake to bring our dear Merdle into the presence, without the possibility of getting away.’\n\n‘Done!’ said Ferdinand. ‘Done!’ said Bar.\n\nBar was a sight wondrous to behold, and full of matter, when, jauntily waving his double eye-glass by its ribbon, and jauntily drooping to an Universe of Jurymen, he, in the most accidental manner ever seen, found himself at Mr Merdle’s shoulder, and embraced that opportunity of mentioning a little point to him, on which he particularly wished to be guided by the light of his practical knowledge. (Here he took Mr Merdle’s arm and walked him gently away.) A banker, whom we would call A. B., advanced a considerable sum of money, which we would call fifteen thousand pounds, to a client or customer of his, whom he would call P. Q. (Here, as they were getting towards Lord Decimus, he held Mr Merdle tight.) As a security for the repayment of this advance to P. Q. whom we would call a widow lady, there were placed in A. B.’s hands the title-deeds of a freehold estate, which we would call Blinkiter Doddles. Now, the point was this. A limited right of felling and lopping in the woods of Blinkiter Doddles, lay in the son of P. Q. then past his majority, and whom we would call X. Y.--but really this was too bad! In the presence of Lord Decimus, to detain the host with chopping our dry chaff of law, was really too bad! Another time! Bar was truly repentant, and would not say another syllable. Would Bishop favour him with half-a-dozen words? (He had now set Mr Merdle down on a couch, side by side with Lord Decimus, and to it they must go, now or never.)\n\nAnd now the rest of the company, highly excited and interested, always excepting Bishop, who had not the slightest idea that anything was going on, formed in one group round the fire in the next drawing-room, and pretended to be chatting easily on the infinite variety of small topics, while everybody’s thoughts and eyes were secretly straying towards the secluded pair. The Chorus were excessively nervous, perhaps as labouring under the dreadful apprehension that some good thing was going to be diverted from them! Bishop alone talked steadily and evenly. He conversed with the great Physician on that relaxation of the throat with which young curates were too frequently afflicted, and on the means of lessening the great prevalence of that disorder in the church. Physician, as a general rule, was of opinion that the best way to avoid it was to know how to read, before you made a profession of reading. Bishop said dubiously, did he really think so? And Physician said, decidedly, yes he did.\n\nFerdinand, meanwhile, was the only one of the party who skirmished on the outside of the circle; he kept about mid-way between it and the two, as if some sort of surgical operation were being performed by Lord Decimus on Mr Merdle, or by Mr Merdle on Lord Decimus, and his services might at any moment be required as Dresser. In fact, within a quarter of an hour Lord Decimus called to him ‘Ferdinand!’ and he went, and took his place in the conference for some five minutes more. Then a half-suppressed gasp broke out among the Chorus; for Lord Decimus rose to take his leave. Again coached up by Ferdinand to the point of making himself popular, he shook hands in the most brilliant manner with the whole company, and even said to Bar, ‘I hope you were not bored by my pears?’ To which Bar retorted, ‘Eton, my lord, or Parliamentary?’ neatly showing that he had mastered the joke, and delicately insinuating that he could never forget it while his life remained.\n\nAll the grave importance that was buttoned up in Mr Tite Barnacle, took itself away next; and Ferdinand took himself away next, to the opera. Some of the rest lingered a little, marrying golden liqueur glasses to Buhl tables with sticky rings; on the desperate chance of Mr Merdle’s saying something. But Merdle, as usual, oozed sluggishly and muddily about his drawing-room, saying never a word.\n\nIn a day or two it was announced to all the town, that Edmund Sparkler, Esquire, son-in-law of the eminent Mr Merdle of worldwide renown, was made one of the Lords of the Circumlocution Office; and proclamation was issued, to all true believers, that this admirable appointment was to be hailed as a graceful and gracious mark of homage, rendered by the graceful and gracious Decimus, to that commercial interest which must ever in a great commercial country--and all the rest of it, with blast of trumpet. So, bolstered by this mark of Government homage, the wonderful Bank and all the other wonderful undertakings went on and went up; and gapers came to Harley Street, Cavendish Square, only to look at the house where the golden wonder lived.\n\nAnd when they saw the Chief Butler looking out at the hall-door in his moments of condescension, the gapers said how rich he looked, and wondered how much money he had in the wonderful Bank. But, if they had known that respectable Nemesis better, they would not have wondered about it, and might have stated the amount with the utmost precision.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. The Progress of an Epidemic\n\nThat it is at least as difficult to stay a moral infection as a physical one; that such a disease will spread with the malignity and rapidity of the Plague; that the contagion, when it has once made head, will spare no pursuit or condition, but will lay hold on people in the soundest health, and become developed in the most unlikely constitutions: is a fact as firmly established by experience as that we human creatures breathe an atmosphere. A blessing beyond appreciation would be conferred upon mankind, if the tainted, in whose weakness or wickedness these virulent disorders are bred, could be instantly seized and placed in close confinement (not to say summarily smothered) before the poison is communicable.\n\nAs a vast fire will fill the air to a great distance with its roar, so the sacred flame which the mighty Barnacles had fanned caused the air to resound more and more with the name of Merdle. It was deposited on every lip, and carried into every ear. There never was, there never had been, there never again should be, such a man as Mr Merdle. Nobody, as aforesaid, knew what he had done; but everybody knew him to be the greatest that had appeared.\n\nDown in Bleeding Heart Yard, where there was not one unappropriated halfpenny, as lively an interest was taken in this paragon of men as on the Stock Exchange. Mrs Plornish, now established in the small grocery and general trade in a snug little shop at the crack end of the Yard, at the top of the steps, with her little old father and Maggy acting as assistants, habitually held forth about him over the counter in conversation with her customers. Mr Plornish, who had a small share in a small builder’s business in the neighbourhood, said, trowel in hand, on the tops of scaffolds and on the tiles of houses, that people did tell him as Mr Merdle was _the_ one, mind you, to put us all to rights in respects of that which all on us looked to, and to bring us all safe home as much as we needed, mind you, fur toe be brought. Mr Baptist, sole lodger of Mr and Mrs Plornish was reputed in whispers to lay by the savings which were the result of his simple and moderate life, for investment in one of Mr Merdle’s certain enterprises. The female Bleeding Hearts, when they came for ounces of tea, and hundredweights of talk, gave Mrs Plornish to understand, That how, ma’am, they had heard from their cousin Mary Anne, which worked in the line, that his lady’s dresses would fill three waggons. That how she was as handsome a lady, ma’am, as lived, no matter wheres, and a busk like marble itself. That how, according to what they was told, ma’am, it was her son by a former husband as was took into the Government; and a General he had been, and armies he had marched again and victory crowned, if all you heard was to be believed. That how it was reported that Mr Merdle’s words had been, that if they could have made it worth his while to take the whole Government he would have took it without a profit, but that take it he could not and stand a loss. That how it was not to be expected, ma’am, that he should lose by it, his ways being, as you might say and utter no falsehood, paved with gold; but that how it was much to be regretted that something handsome hadn’t been got up to make it worth his while; for it was such and only such that knowed the heighth to which the bread and butchers’ meat had rose, and it was such and only such that both could and would bring that heighth down.\n\nSo rife and potent was the fever in Bleeding Heart Yard, that Mr Pancks’s rent-days caused no interval in the patients. The disease took the singular form, on those occasions, of causing the infected to find an unfathomable excuse and consolation in allusions to the magic name.\n\n‘Now, then!’ Mr Pancks would say, to a defaulting lodger. ‘Pay up! Come on!’\n\n‘I haven’t got it, Mr Pancks,’ Defaulter would reply. ‘I tell you the truth, sir, when I say I haven’t got so much as a single sixpence of it to bless myself with.’\n\n‘This won’t do, you know,’ Mr Pancks would retort. ‘You don’t expect it _will_ do; do you?’\n\nDefaulter would admit, with a low-spirited ‘No, sir,’ having no such expectation.\n\n‘My proprietor isn’t going to stand this, you know,’ Mr Pancks would proceed. ‘He don’t send me here for this. Pay up! Come!’\n\nThe Defaulter would make answer, ‘Ah, Mr Pancks. If I was the rich gentleman whose name is in everybody’s mouth--if my name was Merdle, sir--I’d soon pay up, and be glad to do it.’\n\nDialogues on the rent-question usually took place at the house-doors or in the entries, and in the presence of several deeply interested Bleeding Hearts. They always received a reference of this kind with a low murmur of response, as if it were convincing; and the Defaulter, however black and discomfited before, always cheered up a little in making it.\n\n‘If I was Mr Merdle, sir, you wouldn’t have cause to complain of me then. No, believe me!’ the Defaulter would proceed with a shake of the head. ‘I’d pay up so quick then, Mr Pancks, that you shouldn’t have to ask me.’\n\nThe response would be heard again here, implying that it was impossible to say anything fairer, and that this was the next thing to paying the money down.\n\nMr Pancks would be now reduced to saying as he booked the case, ‘Well! You’ll have the broker in, and be turned out; that’s what’ll happen to you. It’s no use talking to me about Mr Merdle. You are not Mr Merdle, any more than I am.’\n\n‘No, sir,’ the Defaulter would reply. ‘I only wish you _were_ him, sir.’\n\nThe response would take this up quickly; replying with great feeling, ‘Only wish you _were_ him, sir.’\n\n‘You’d be easier with us if you were Mr Merdle, sir,’ the Defaulter would go on with rising spirits, ‘and it would be better for all parties. Better for our sakes, and better for yours, too. You wouldn’t have to worry no one, then, sir. You wouldn’t have to worry us, and you wouldn’t have to worry yourself. You’d be easier in your own mind, sir, and you’d leave others easier, too, you would, if you were Mr Merdle.’\n\nMr Pancks, in whom these impersonal compliments produced an irresistible sheepishness, never rallied after such a charge. He could only bite his nails and puff away to the next Defaulter. The responsive Bleeding Hearts would then gather round the Defaulter whom he had just abandoned, and the most extravagant rumours would circulate among them, to their great comfort, touching the amount of Mr Merdle’s ready money.\n\nFrom one of the many such defeats of one of many rent-days, Mr Pancks, having finished his day’s collection, repaired with his note-book under his arm to Mrs Plornish’s corner. Mr Pancks’s object was not professional, but social. He had had a trying day, and wanted a little brightening. By this time he was on friendly terms with the Plornish family, having often looked in upon them at similar seasons, and borne his part in recollections of Miss Dorrit.\n\nMrs Plornish’s shop-parlour had been decorated under her own eye, and presented, on the side towards the shop, a little fiction in which Mrs Plornish unspeakably rejoiced. This poetical heightening of the parlour consisted in the wall being painted to represent the exterior of a thatched cottage; the artist having introduced (in as effective a manner as he found compatible with their highly disproportionate dimensions) the real door and window. The modest sunflower and hollyhock were depicted as flourishing with great luxuriance on this rustic dwelling, while a quantity of dense smoke issuing from the chimney indicated good cheer within, and also, perhaps, that it had not been lately swept. A faithful dog was represented as flying at the legs of the friendly visitor, from the threshold; and a circular pigeon-house, enveloped in a cloud of pigeons, arose from behind the garden-paling. On the door (when it was shut), appeared the semblance of a brass-plate, presenting the inscription, Happy Cottage, T. and M. Plornish; the partnership expressing man and wife. No Poetry and no Art ever charmed the imagination more than the union of the two in this counterfeit cottage charmed Mrs Plornish. It was nothing to her that Plornish had a habit of leaning against it as he smoked his pipe after work, when his hat blotted out the pigeon-house and all the pigeons, when his back swallowed up the dwelling, when his hands in his pockets uprooted the blooming garden and laid waste the adjacent country. To Mrs Plornish, it was still a most beautiful cottage, a most wonderful deception; and it made no difference that Mr Plornish’s eye was some inches above the level of the gable bed-room in the thatch. To come out into the shop after it was shut, and hear her father sing a song inside this cottage, was a perfect Pastoral to Mrs Plornish, the Golden Age revived. And truly if that famous period had been revived, or had ever been at all, it may be doubted whether it would have produced many more heartily admiring daughters than the poor woman.\n\nWarned of a visitor by the tinkling bell at the shop-door, Mrs Plornish came out of Happy Cottage to see who it might be. ‘I guessed it was you, Mr Pancks,’ said she, ‘for it’s quite your regular night; ain’t it? Here’s father, you see, come out to serve at the sound of the bell, like a brisk young shopman. Ain’t he looking well? Father’s more pleased to see you than if you was a customer, for he dearly loves a gossip; and when it turns upon Miss Dorrit, he loves it all the more. You never heard father in such voice as he is at present,’ said Mrs Plornish, her own voice quavering, she was so proud and pleased. ‘He gave us Strephon last night to that degree that Plornish gets up and makes him this speech across the table. “John Edward Nandy,” says Plornish to father, “I never heard you come the warbles as I have heard you come the warbles this night.” An’t it gratifying, Mr Pancks, though; really?’\n\nMr Pancks, who had snorted at the old man in his friendliest manner, replied in the affirmative, and casually asked whether that lively Altro chap had come in yet? Mrs Plornish answered no, not yet, though he had gone to the West-End with some work, and had said he should be back by tea-time. Mr Pancks was then hospitably pressed into Happy Cottage, where he encountered the elder Master Plornish just come home from school. Examining that young student, lightly, on the educational proceedings of the day, he found that the more advanced pupils who were in the large text and the letter M, had been set the copy ‘Merdle, Millions.’\n\n‘And how are _you_ getting on, Mrs Plornish,’ said Pancks, ‘since we’re mentioning millions?’\n\n‘Very steady, indeed, sir,’ returned Mrs Plornish. ‘Father, dear, would you go into the shop and tidy the window a little bit before tea, your taste being so beautiful?’\n\nJohn Edward Nandy trotted away, much gratified, to comply with his daughter’s request. Mrs Plornish, who was always in mortal terror of mentioning pecuniary affairs before the old gentleman, lest any disclosure she made might rouse his spirit and induce him to run away to the workhouse, was thus left free to be confidential with Mr Pancks.\n\n‘It’s quite true that the business is very steady indeed,’ said Mrs Plornish, lowering her voice; ‘and has a excellent connection. The only thing that stands in its way, sir, is the Credit.’\n\nThis drawback, rather severely felt by most people who engaged in commercial transactions with the inhabitants of Bleeding Heart Yard, was a large stumbling-block in Mrs Plornish’s trade. When Mr Dorrit had established her in the business, the Bleeding Hearts had shown an amount of emotion and a determination to support her in it, that did honour to human nature. Recognising her claim upon their generous feelings as one who had long been a member of their community, they pledged themselves, with great feeling, to deal with Mrs Plornish, come what would and bestow their patronage on no other establishment. Influenced by these noble sentiments, they had even gone out of their way to purchase little luxuries in the grocery and butter line to which they were unaccustomed; saying to one another, that if they did stretch a point, was it not for a neighbour and a friend, and for whom ought a point to be stretched if not for such? So stimulated, the business was extremely brisk, and the articles in stock went off with the greatest celerity. In short, if the\n\nBleeding Hearts had but paid, the undertaking would have been a complete success; whereas, by reason of their exclusively confining themselves to owing, the profits actually realised had not yet begun to appear in the books.\nMr Pancks was making a very porcupine of himself by sticking his hair up in the contemplation of this state of accounts, when old Mr Nandy, re-entering the cottage with an air of mystery, entreated them to come and look at the strange behaviour of Mr Baptist, who seemed to have met with something that had scared him. All three going into the shop, and watching through the window, then saw Mr Baptist, pale and agitated, go through the following extraordinary performances. First, he was observed hiding at the top of the steps leading down into the Yard, and peeping up and down the street with his head cautiously thrust out close to the side of the shop-door. After very anxious scrutiny, he came out of his retreat, and went briskly down the street as if he were going away altogether; then, suddenly turned about, and went, at the same pace, and with the same feint, up the street. He had gone no further up the street than he had gone down, when he crossed the road and disappeared. The object of this last manoeuvre was only apparent, when his entering the shop with a sudden twist, from the steps again, explained that he had made a wide and obscure circuit round to the other, or Doyce and Clennam, end of the Yard, and had come through the Yard and bolted in. He was out of breath by that time, as he might well be, and his heart seemed to jerk faster than the little shop-bell, as it quivered and jingled behind him with his hasty shutting of the door.\n\n‘Hallo, old chap!’ said Mr Pancks. ‘Altro, old boy! What’s the matter?’\n\nMr Baptist, or Signor Cavalletto, understood English now almost as well as Mr Pancks himself, and could speak it very well too. Nevertheless, Mrs Plornish, with a pardonable vanity in that accomplishment of hers which made her all but Italian, stepped in as interpreter.\n\n‘E ask know,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘what go wrong?’\n\n‘Come into the happy little cottage, Padrona,’ returned Mr Baptist, imparting great stealthiness to his flurried back-handed shake of his right forefinger. ‘Come there!’\n\nMrs Plornish was proud of the title Padrona, which she regarded as signifying: not so much Mistress of the house, as Mistress of the Italian tongue. She immediately complied with Mr Baptist’s request, and they all went into the cottage.\n\n‘E ope you no fright,’ said Mrs Plornish then, interpreting Mr Pancks in a new way with her usual fertility of resource. ‘What appen? Peaka Padrona!’\n\n‘I have seen some one,’ returned Baptist. ‘I have rincontrato him.’\n\n‘Im? Oo him?’ asked Mrs Plornish.\n\n‘A bad man. A baddest man. I have hoped that I should never see him again.’\n\n‘Ow you know him bad?’ asked Mrs Plornish.\n\n‘It does not matter, Padrona. I know it too well.’\n\n‘E see you?’ asked Mrs Plornish.\n\n‘No. I hope not. I believe not.’\n\n‘He says,’ Mrs Plornish then interpreted, addressing her father and Pancks with mild condescension, ‘that he has met a bad man, but he hopes the bad man didn’t see him--Why,’ inquired Mrs Plornish, reverting to the Italian language, ‘why ope bad man no see?’\n\n‘Padrona, dearest,’ returned the little foreigner whom she so considerately protected, ‘do not ask, I pray. Once again I say it matters not. I have fear of this man. I do not wish to see him, I do not wish to be known of him--never again! Enough, most beautiful. Leave it.’\n\nThe topic was so disagreeable to him, and so put his usual liveliness to the rout, that Mrs Plornish forbore to press him further: the rather as the tea had been drawing for some time on the hob. But she was not the less surprised and curious for asking no more questions; neither was Mr Pancks, whose expressive breathing had been labouring hard since the entrance of the little man, like a locomotive engine with a great load getting up a steep incline. Maggy, now better dressed than of yore, though still faithful to the monstrous character of her cap, had been in the background from the first with open mouth and eyes, which staring and gaping features were not diminished in breadth by the untimely suppression of the subject. However, no more was said about it, though much appeared to be thought on all sides: by no means excepting the two young Plornishes, who partook of the evening meal as if their eating the bread and butter were rendered almost superfluous by the painful probability of the worst of men shortly presenting himself for the purpose of eating them. Mr Baptist, by degrees began to chirp a little; but never stirred from the seat he had taken behind the door and close to the window, though it was not his usual place. As often as the little bell rang, he started and peeped out secretly, with the end of the little curtain in his hand and the rest before his face; evidently not at all satisfied but that the man he dreaded had tracked him through all his doublings and turnings, with the certainty of a terrible bloodhound.\n\nThe entrance, at various times, of two or three customers and of Mr Plornish, gave Mr Baptist just enough of this employment to keep the attention of the company fixed upon him. Tea was over, and the children were abed, and Mrs Plornish was feeling her way to the dutiful proposal that her father should favour them with Chloe, when the bell rang again, and Mr Clennam came in.\n\nClennam had been poring late over his books and letters; for the waiting-rooms of the Circumlocution Office ravaged his time sorely. Over and above that, he was depressed and made uneasy by the late occurrence at his mother’s. He looked worn and solitary. He felt so, too; but, nevertheless, was returning home from his counting-house by that end of the Yard to give them the intelligence that he had received another letter from Miss Dorrit.\n\nThe news made a sensation in the cottage which drew off the general attention from Mr Baptist. Maggy, who pushed her way into the foreground immediately, would have seemed to draw in the tidings of her Little Mother equally at her ears, nose, mouth, and eyes, but that the last were obstructed by tears. She was particularly delighted when Clennam assured her that there were hospitals, and very kindly conducted hospitals, in Rome. Mr Pancks rose into new distinction in virtue of being specially remembered in the letter. Everybody was pleased and interested, and Clennam was well repaid for his trouble.\n\n‘But you are tired, sir. Let me make you a cup of tea,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘if you’d condescend to take such a thing in the cottage; and many thanks to you, too, I am sure, for bearing us in mind so kindly.’\n\nMr Plornish deeming it incumbent on him, as host, to add his personal acknowledgments, tendered them in the form which always expressed his highest ideal of a combination of ceremony with sincerity.\n\n‘John Edward Nandy,’ said Mr Plornish, addressing the old gentleman. ‘Sir. It’s not too often that you see unpretending actions without a spark of pride, and therefore when you see them give grateful honour unto the same, being that if you don’t, and live to want ‘em, it follows serve you right.’\n\nTo which Mr Nandy replied:\n\n‘I am heartily of your opinion, Thomas, and which your opinion is the same as mine, and therefore no more words and not being backwards with that opinion, which opinion giving it as yes, Thomas, yes, is the opinion in which yourself and me must ever be unanimously jined by all, and where there is not difference of opinion there can be none but one opinion, which fully no, Thomas, Thomas, no!’\n\nArthur, with less formality, expressed himself gratified by their high appreciation of so very slight an attention on his part; and explained as to the tea that he had not yet dined, and was going straight home to refresh after a long day’s labour, or he would have readily accepted the hospitable offer. As Mr Pancks was somewhat noisily getting his steam up for departure, he concluded by asking that gentleman if he would walk with him? Mr Pancks said he desired no better engagement, and the two took leave of Happy Cottage.\n\n‘If you will come home with me, Pancks,’ said Arthur, when they got into the street, ‘and will share what dinner or supper there is, it will be next door to an act of charity; for I am weary and out of sorts to-night.’\n\n‘Ask me to do a greater thing than that,’ said Pancks, ‘when you want it done, and I’ll do it.’\n\nBetween this eccentric personage and Clennam, a tacit understanding and accord had been always improving since Mr Pancks flew over Mr Rugg’s back in the Marshalsea Yard. When the carriage drove away on the memorable day of the family’s departure, these two had looked after it together, and had walked slowly away together. When the first letter came from little Dorrit, nobody was more interested in hearing of her than Mr Pancks. The second letter, at that moment in Clennam’s breast-pocket, particularly remembered him by name. Though he had never before made any profession or protestation to Clennam, and though what he had just said was little enough as to the words in which it was expressed, Clennam had long had a growing belief that Mr Pancks, in his own odd way, was becoming attached to him. All these strings intertwining made Pancks a very cable of anchorage that night.\n\n‘I am quite alone,’ Arthur explained as they walked on. ‘My partner is away, busily engaged at a distance on his branch of our business, and you shall do just as you like.’\n\n‘Thank you. You didn’t take particular notice of little Altro just now; did you?’ said Pancks.\n\n‘No. Why?’\n‘He’s a bright fellow, and I like him,’ said Pancks. ‘Something has gone amiss with him to-day. Have you any idea of any cause that can have overset him?’\n\n‘You surprise me! None whatever.’\n\nMr Pancks gave his reasons for the inquiry. Arthur was quite unprepared for them, and quite unable to suggest an explanation of them.\n\n‘Perhaps you’ll ask him,’ said Pancks, ‘as he’s a stranger?’\n\n‘Ask him what?’ returned Clennam.\n\n‘What he has on his mind.’\n\n‘I ought first to see for myself that he has something on his mind, I think,’ said Clennam. ‘I have found him in every way so diligent, so grateful (for little enough), and so trustworthy, that it might look like suspecting him. And that would be very unjust.’\n\n‘True,’ said Pancks. ‘But, I say! You oughtn’t to be anybody’s proprietor, Mr Clennam. You’re much too delicate.’\n\n‘For the matter of that,’ returned Clennam laughing, ‘I have not a large proprietary share in Cavalletto. His carving is his livelihood. He keeps the keys of the Factory, watches it every alternate night, and acts as a sort of housekeeper to it generally; but we have little work in the way of his ingenuity, though we give him what we have. No! I am rather his adviser than his proprietor. To call me his standing counsel and his banker would be nearer the fact. Speaking of being his banker, is it not curious, Pancks, that the ventures which run just now in so many people’s heads, should run even in little Cavalletto’s?’\n\n‘Ventures?’ retorted Pancks, with a snort. ‘What ventures?’\n\n‘These Merdle enterprises.’\n\n‘Oh! Investments,’ said Pancks. ‘Ay, ay! I didn’t know you were speaking of investments.’\n\nHis quick way of replying caused Clennam to look at him, with a doubt whether he meant more than he said. As it was accompanied, however, with a quickening of his pace and a corresponding increase in the labouring of his machinery, Arthur did not pursue the matter, and they soon arrived at his house.\n\nA dinner of soup and a pigeon-pie, served on a little round table before the fire, and flavoured with a bottle of good wine, oiled Mr Pancks’s works in a highly effective manner; so that when Clennam produced his Eastern pipe, and handed Mr Pancks another Eastern pipe, the latter gentleman was perfectly comfortable.\n\nThey puffed for a while in silence, Mr Pancks like a steam-vessel with wind, tide, calm water, and all other sea-going conditions in her favour. He was the first to speak, and he spoke thus:\n\n‘Yes. Investments is the word.’\n\nClennam, with his former look, said ‘Ah!’\n\n‘I am going back to it, you see,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘Yes. I see you are going back to it,’ returned Clennam, wondering why.\n\n‘Wasn’t it a curious thing that they should run in little Altro’s head? Eh?’ said Pancks as he smoked. ‘Wasn’t that how you put it?’\n\n‘That was what I said.’\n\n‘Ay! But think of the whole Yard having got it. Think of their all meeting me with it, on my collecting days, here and there and everywhere. Whether they pay, or whether they don’t pay. Merdle, Merdle, Merdle. Always Merdle.’\n\n‘Very strange how these runs on an infatuation prevail,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘An’t it?’ returned Pancks. After smoking for a minute or so, more drily than comported with his recent oiling, he added: ‘Because you see these people don’t understand the subject.’\n\n‘Not a bit,’ assented Clennam.\n\n‘Not a bit,’ cried Pancks. ‘Know nothing of figures. Know nothing of money questions. Never made a calculation. Never worked it, sir!’\n\n‘If they had--’ Clennam was going on to say; when Mr Pancks, without change of countenance, produced a sound so far surpassing all his usual efforts, nasal or bronchial, that he stopped.\n\n‘If they had?’ repeated Pancks in an inquiring tone.\n\n‘I thought you--spoke,’ said Arthur, hesitating what name to give the interruption.\n\n‘Not at all,’ said Pancks. ‘Not yet. I may in a minute. If they had?’\n\n‘If they had,’ observed Clennam, who was a little at a loss how to take his friend, ‘why, I suppose they would have known better.’\n\n‘How so, Mr Clennam?’ Pancks asked quickly, and with an odd effect of having been from the commencement of the conversation loaded with the heavy charge he now fired off. ‘They’re right, you know. They don’t mean to be, but they’re right.’\n\n‘Right in sharing Cavalletto’s inclination to speculate with Mr Merdle?’\n\n‘Per-fectly, sir,’ said Pancks. ‘I’ve gone into it. I’ve made the calculations. I’ve worked it. They’re safe and genuine.’ Relieved by having got to this, Mr Pancks took as long a pull as his lungs would permit at his Eastern pipe, and looked sagaciously and steadily at Clennam while inhaling and exhaling too.\n\nIn those moments, Mr Pancks began to give out the dangerous infection with which he was laden. It is the manner of communicating these diseases; it is the subtle way in which they go about.\n\n‘Do you mean, my good Pancks,’ asked Clennam emphatically, ‘that you would put that thousand pounds of yours, let us say, for instance, out at this kind of interest?’\n\n‘Certainly,’ said Pancks. ‘Already done it, sir.’\n\nMr Pancks took another long inhalation, another long exhalation, another long sagacious look at Clennam.\n\n‘I tell you, Mr Clennam, I’ve gone into it,’ said Pancks. ‘He’s a man of immense resources--enormous capital--government influence. They’re the best schemes afloat. They’re safe. They’re certain.’\n\n‘Well!’ returned Clennam, looking first at him gravely and then at the fire gravely. ‘You surprise me!’\n\n‘Bah!’ Pancks retorted. ‘Don’t say that, sir. It’s what you ought to do yourself! Why don’t you do as I do?’\n\nOf whom Mr Pancks had taken the prevalent disease, he could no more have told than if he had unconsciously taken a fever. Bred at first, as many physical diseases are, in the wickedness of men, and then disseminated in their ignorance, these epidemics, after a period, get communicated to many sufferers who are neither ignorant nor wicked. Mr Pancks might, or might not, have caught the illness himself from a subject of this class; but in this category he appeared before Clennam, and the infection he threw off was all the more virulent.\n\n‘And you have really invested,’ Clennam had already passed to that word, ‘your thousand pounds, Pancks?’\n\n‘To be sure, sir!’ replied Pancks boldly, with a puff of smoke. ‘And only wish it ten!’\n\nNow, Clennam had two subjects lying heavy on his lonely mind that night; the one, his partner’s long-deferred hope; the other, what he had seen and heard at his mother’s. In the relief of having this companion, and of feeling that he could trust him, he passed on to both, and both brought him round again, with an increase and acceleration of force, to his point of departure.\n\nIt came about in the simplest manner. Quitting the investment subject, after an interval of silent looking at the fire through the smoke of his pipe, he told Pancks how and why he was occupied with the great National Department. ‘A hard case it has been, and a hard case it is on Doyce,’ he finished by saying, with all the honest feeling the topic roused in him.\n\n‘Hard indeed,’ Pancks acquiesced. ‘But you manage for him, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘How do you mean?’\n\n‘Manage the money part of the business?’\n\n‘Yes. As well as I can.’\n\n‘Manage it better, sir,’ said Pancks. ‘Recompense him for his toils and disappointments. Give him the chances of the time. He’ll never benefit himself in that way, patient and preoccupied workman. He looks to you, sir.’\n\n‘I do my best, Pancks,’ returned Clennam, uneasily. ‘As to duly weighing and considering these new enterprises of which I have had no experience, I doubt if I am fit for it, I am growing old.’\n\n‘Growing old?’ cried Pancks. ‘Ha, ha!’\n\nThere was something so indubitably genuine in the wonderful laugh, and series of snorts and puffs, engendered in Mr Pancks’s astonishment at, and utter rejection of, the idea, that his being quite in earnest could not be questioned.\n\n‘Growing old?’ cried Pancks. ‘Hear, hear, hear! Old? Hear him, hear him!’\n\nThe positive refusal expressed in Mr Pancks’s continued snorts, no less than in these exclamations, to entertain the sentiment for a single instant, drove Arthur away from it. Indeed, he was fearful of something happening to Mr Pancks in the violent conflict that took place between the breath he jerked out of himself and the smoke he jerked into himself. This abandonment of the second topic threw him on the third.\n\n‘Young, old, or middle-aged, Pancks,’ he said, when there was a favourable pause, ‘I am in a very anxious and uncertain state; a state that even leads me to doubt whether anything now seeming to belong to me, may be really mine. Shall I tell you how this is? Shall I put a great trust in you?’\n\n‘You shall, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘if you believe me worthy of it.’\n\n‘I do.’\n\n‘You may!’ Mr Pancks’s short and sharp rejoinder, confirmed by the sudden outstretching of his coaly hand, was most expressive and convincing. Arthur shook the hand warmly.\n\nHe then, softening the nature of his old apprehensions as much as was possible consistently with their being made intelligible and never alluding to his mother by name, but speaking vaguely of a relation of his, confided to Mr Pancks a broad outline of the misgivings he entertained, and of the interview he had witnessed. Mr Pancks listened with such interest that, regardless of the charms of the Eastern pipe, he put it in the grate among the fire-irons, and occupied his hands during the whole recital in so erecting the loops and hooks of hair all over his head, that he looked, when it came to a conclusion, like a journeyman Hamlet in conversation with his father’s spirit.\n\n‘Brings me back, sir,’ was his exclamation then, with a startling touch on Clennam’s knee, ‘brings me back, sir, to the Investments! I don’t say anything of your making yourself poor to repair a wrong you never committed. That’s you. A man must be himself. But I say this, fearing you may want money to save your own blood from exposure and disgrace--make as much as you can!’\n\nArthur shook his head, but looked at him thoughtfully too.\n\n‘Be as rich as you can, sir,’ Pancks adjured him with a powerful concentration of all his energies on the advice. ‘Be as rich as you honestly can. It’s your duty. Not for your sake, but for the sake of others. Take time by the forelock. Poor Mr Doyce (who really _is_ growing old) depends upon you. Your relative depends upon you. You don’t know what depends upon you.’\n\n‘Well, well, well!’ returned Arthur. ‘Enough for to-night.’\n\n‘One word more, Mr Clennam,’ retorted Pancks, ‘and then enough for to-night. Why should you leave all the gains to the gluttons, knaves, and impostors? Why should you leave all the gains that are to be got to my proprietor and the like of him? Yet you’re always doing it. When I say you, I mean such men as you. You know you are. Why, I see it every day of my life. I see nothing else. It’s my business to see it. Therefore I say,’ urged Pancks, ‘Go in and win!’\n\n‘But what of Go in and lose?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Can’t be done, sir,’ returned Pancks. ‘I have looked into it. Name up everywhere--immense resources--enormous capital--great position--high connection--government influence. Can’t be done!’\n\nGradually, after this closing exposition, Mr Pancks subsided; allowed his hair to droop as much as it ever would droop on the utmost persuasion; reclaimed the pipe from the fire-irons, filled it anew, and smoked it out. They said little more; but were company to one another in silently pursuing the same subjects, and did not part until midnight. On taking his leave, Mr Pancks, when he had shaken hands with Clennam, worked completely round him before he steamed out at the door. This, Arthur received as an assurance that he might implicitly rely on Pancks, if he ever should come to need assistance; either in any of the matters of which they had spoken that night, or any other subject that could in any way affect himself.\n\nAt intervals all next day, and even while his attention was fixed on other things, he thought of Mr Pancks’s investment of his thousand pounds, and of his having ‘looked into it.’ He thought of Mr Pancks’s being so sanguine in this matter, and of his not being usually of a sanguine character. He thought of the great National Department, and of the delight it would be to him to see Doyce better off. He thought of the darkly threatening place that went by the name of Home in his remembrance, and of the gathering shadows which made it yet more darkly threatening than of old. He observed anew that wherever he went, he saw, or heard, or touched, the celebrated name of Merdle; he found it difficult even to remain at his desk a couple of hours, without having it presented to one of his bodily senses through some agency or other. He began to think it was curious too that it should be everywhere, and that nobody but he should seem to have any mistrust of it. Though indeed he began to remember, when he got to this, even _he_ did not mistrust it; he had only happened to keep aloof from it.\n\nSuch symptoms, when a disease of the kind is rife, are usually the signs of sickening.\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Taking Advice\n\nWhen it became known to the Britons on the shore of the yellow Tiber that their intelligent compatriot, Mr Sparkler, was made one of the Lords of their Circumlocution Office, they took it as a piece of news with which they had no nearer concern than with any other piece of news--any other Accident or Offence--in the English papers. Some laughed; some said, by way of complete excuse, that the post was virtually a sinecure, and any fool who could spell his name was good enough for it; some, and these the more solemn political oracles, said that Decimus did wisely to strengthen himself, and that the sole constitutional purpose of all places within the gift of Decimus, was, that Decimus _should_ strengthen himself. A few bilious Britons there were who would not subscribe to this article of faith; but their objection was purely theoretical. In a practical point of view, they listlessly abandoned the matter, as being the business of some other Britons unknown, somewhere, or nowhere. In like manner, at home, great numbers of Britons maintained, for as long as four-and-twenty consecutive hours, that those invisible and anonymous Britons ‘ought to take it up;’ and that if they quietly acquiesced in it, they deserved it. But of what class the remiss Britons were composed, and where the unlucky creatures hid themselves, and why they hid themselves, and how it constantly happened that they neglected their interests, when so many other Britons were quite at a loss to account for their not looking after those interests, was not, either upon the shore of the yellow Tiber or the shore of the black Thames, made apparent to men.\n\nMrs Merdle circulated the news, as she received congratulations on it, with a careless grace that displayed it to advantage, as the setting displays the jewel. Yes, she said, Edmund had taken the place. Mr Merdle wished him to take it, and he had taken it. She hoped Edmund might like it, but really she didn’t know. It would keep him in town a good deal, and he preferred the country. Still, it was not a disagreeable position--and it was a position. There was no denying that the thing was a compliment to Mr Merdle, and was not a bad thing for Edmund if he liked it. It was just as well that he should have something to do, and it was just as well that he should have something for doing it. Whether it would be more agreeable to Edmund than the army, remained to be seen.\n\nThus the Bosom; accomplished in the art of seeming to make things of small account, and really enhancing them in the process. While Henry Gowan, whom Decimus had thrown away, went through the whole round of his acquaintance between the Gate of the People and the town of Albano, vowing, almost (but not quite) with tears in his eyes, that Sparkler was the sweetest-tempered, simplest-hearted, altogether most lovable jackass that ever grazed on the public common; and that only one circumstance could have delighted him (Gowan) more, than his (the beloved jackass’s) getting this post, and that would have been his (Gowan’s) getting it himself. He said it was the very thing for Sparkler. There was nothing to do, and he would do it charmingly; there was a handsome salary to draw, and he would draw it charmingly; it was a delightful, appropriate, capital appointment; and he almost forgave the donor his slight of himself, in his joy that the dear donkey for whom he had so great an affection was so admirably stabled. Nor did his benevolence stop here. He took pains, on all social occasions, to draw Mr Sparkler out, and make him conspicuous before the company; and, although the considerate action always resulted in that young gentleman’s making a dreary and forlorn mental spectacle of himself, the friendly intention was not to be doubted.\n\nUnless, indeed, it chanced to be doubted by the object of Mr Sparkler’s affections. Miss Fanny was now in the difficult situation of being universally known in that light, and of not having dismissed Mr Sparkler, however capriciously she used him. Hence, she was sufficiently identified with the gentleman to feel compromised by his being more than usually ridiculous; and hence, being by no means deficient in quickness, she sometimes came to his rescue against Gowan, and did him very good service. But, while doing this, she was ashamed of him, undetermined whether to get rid of him or more decidedly encourage him, distracted with apprehensions that she was every day becoming more and more immeshed in her uncertainties, and tortured by misgivings that Mrs Merdle triumphed in her distress. With this tumult in her mind, it is no subject for surprise that Miss Fanny came home one night in a state of agitation from a concert and ball at Mrs Merdle’s house, and on her sister affectionately trying to soothe her, pushed that sister away from the toilette-table at which she sat angrily trying to cry, and declared with a heaving bosom that she detested everybody, and she wished she was dead.\n\n‘Dear Fanny, what is the matter? Tell me.’\n\n‘Matter, you little Mole,’ said Fanny. ‘If you were not the blindest of the blind, you would have no occasion to ask me. The idea of daring to pretend to assert that you have eyes in your head, and yet ask me what’s the matter!’\n\n‘Is it Mr Sparkler, dear?’\n\n‘Mis-ter Spark-ler!’ repeated Fanny, with unbounded scorn, as if he were the last subject in the Solar system that could possibly be near her mind. ‘No, Miss Bat, it is not.’\n\nImmediately afterwards, she became remorseful for having called her sister names; declaring with sobs that she knew she made herself hateful, but that everybody drove her to it.\n\n‘I don’t think you are well to-night, dear Fanny.’\n\n‘Stuff and nonsense!’ replied the young lady, turning angry again; ‘I am as well as you are. Perhaps I might say better, and yet make no boast of it.’\n\nPoor Little Dorrit, not seeing her way to the offering of any soothing words that would escape repudiation, deemed it best to remain quiet. At first, Fanny took this ill, too; protesting to her looking-glass, that of all the trying sisters a girl could have, she did think the most trying sister was a flat sister. That she knew she was at times a wretched temper; that she knew she made herself hateful; that when she made herself hateful, nothing would do her half the good as being told so; but that, being afflicted with a flat sister, she never _was_ told so, and the consequence resulted that she was absolutely tempted and goaded into making herself disagreeable. Besides (she angrily told her looking-glass), she didn’t want to be forgiven. It was not a right example, that she should be constantly stooping to be forgiven by a younger sister. And this was the Art of it--that she was always being placed in the position of being forgiven, whether she liked it or not. Finally she burst into violent weeping, and, when her sister came and sat close at her side to comfort her, said, ‘Amy, you’re an Angel!’\n\n‘But, I tell you what, my Pet,’ said Fanny, when her sister’s gentleness had calmed her, ‘it now comes to this; that things cannot and shall not go on as they are at present going on, and that there must be an end of this, one way or another.’\n\nAs the announcement was vague, though very peremptory, Little Dorrit returned, ‘Let us talk about it.’\n\n‘Quite so, my dear,’ assented Fanny, as she dried her eyes. ‘Let us talk about it. I am rational again now, and you shall advise me. _Will_ you advise me, my sweet child?’\n\nEven Amy smiled at this notion, but she said, ‘I will, Fanny, as well as I can.’\n\n‘Thank you, dearest Amy,’ returned Fanny, kissing her. ‘You are my anchor.’\n\nHaving embraced her Anchor with great affection, Fanny took a bottle of sweet toilette water from the table, and called to her maid for a fine handkerchief. She then dismissed that attendant for the night, and went on to be advised; dabbing her eyes and forehead from time to time to cool them.\n\n‘My love,’ Fanny began, ‘our characters and points of view are sufficiently different (kiss me again, my darling), to make it very probable that I shall surprise you by what I am going to say. What I am going to say, my dear, is, that notwithstanding our property, we labour, socially speaking, under disadvantages. You don’t quite understand what I mean, Amy?’\n\n‘I have no doubt I shall,’ said Amy, mildly, ‘after a few words more.’\n\n‘Well, my dear, what I mean is, that we are, after all, newcomers into fashionable life.’\n\n‘I am sure, Fanny,’ Little Dorrit interposed in her zealous admiration, ‘no one need find that out in you.’\n\n‘Well, my dear child, perhaps not,’ said Fanny, ‘though it’s most kind and most affectionate in you, you precious girl, to say so.’ Here she dabbed her sister’s forehead, and blew upon it a little. ‘But you are,’ resumed Fanny, ‘as is well known, the dearest little thing that ever was! To resume, my child. Pa is extremely gentlemanly and extremely well informed, but he is, in some trifling respects, a little different from other gentlemen of his fortune: partly on account of what he has gone through, poor dear: partly, I fancy, on account of its often running in his mind that other people are thinking about that, while he is talking to them. Uncle, my love, is altogether unpresentable. Though a dear creature to whom I am tenderly attached, he is, socially speaking, shocking. Edward is frightfully expensive and dissipated. I don’t mean that there is anything ungenteel in that itself--far from it--but I do mean that he doesn’t do it well, and that he doesn’t, if I may so express myself, get the money’s-worth in the sort of dissipated reputation that attaches to him.’\n\n‘Poor Edward!’ sighed Little Dorrit, with the whole family history in the sigh.\n\n‘Yes. And poor you and me, too,’ returned Fanny, rather sharply. ‘Very true! Then, my dear, we have no mother, and we have a Mrs General. And I tell you again, darling, that Mrs General, if I may reverse a common proverb and adapt it to her, is a cat in gloves who _will_ catch mice. That woman, I am quite sure and confident, will be our mother-in-law.’\n\n‘I can hardly think, Fanny--’ Fanny stopped her.\n\n‘Now, don’t argue with me about it, Amy,’ said she, ‘because I know better.’ Feeling that she had been sharp again, she dabbed her sister’s forehead again, and blew upon it again. ‘To resume once more, my dear. It then becomes a question with me (I am proud and spirited, Amy, as you very well know: too much so, I dare say) whether I shall make up my mind to take it upon myself to carry the family through.’\n\n‘How?’ asked her sister, anxiously.\n\n‘I will not,’ said Fanny, without answering the question, ‘submit to be mother-in-lawed by Mrs General; and I will not submit to be, in any respect whatever, either patronised or tormented by Mrs Merdle.’\n\nLittle Dorrit laid her hand upon the hand that held the bottle of sweet water, with a still more anxious look. Fanny, quite punishing her own forehead with the vehement dabs she now began to give it, fitfully went on.\n\n‘That he has somehow or other, and how is of no consequence, attained a very good position, no one can deny. That it is a very good connection, no one can deny. And as to the question of clever or not clever, I doubt very much whether a clever husband would be suitable to me. I cannot submit. I should not be able to defer to him enough.’\n\n‘O, my dear Fanny!’ expostulated Little Dorrit, upon whom a kind of terror had been stealing as she perceived what her sister meant. ‘If you loved any one, all this feeling would change. If you loved any one, you would no more be yourself, but you would quite lose and forget yourself in your devotion to him. If you loved him, Fanny--’ Fanny had stopped the dabbing hand, and was looking at her fixedly.\n\n‘O, indeed!’ cried Fanny. ‘Really? Bless me, how much some people know of some subjects! They say every one has a subject, and I certainly seem to have hit upon yours, Amy. There, you little thing, I was only in fun,’ dabbing her sister’s forehead; ‘but don’t you be a silly puss, and don’t you think flightily and eloquently about degenerate impossibilities. There! Now, I’ll go back to myself.’\n\n‘Dear Fanny, let me say first, that I would far rather we worked for a scanty living again than I would see you rich and married to Mr Sparkler.’\n\n‘_Let_ you say, my dear?’ retorted Fanny. ‘Why, of course, I will _let_ you say anything. There is no constraint upon you, I hope. We are together to talk it over. And as to marrying Mr Sparkler, I have not the slightest intention of doing so to-night, my dear, or to-morrow morning either.’\n\n‘But at some time?’\n\n‘At no time, for anything I know at present,’ answered Fanny, with indifference. Then, suddenly changing her indifference into a burning restlessness, she added, ‘You talk about the clever men, you little thing! It’s all very fine and easy to talk about the clever men; but where are they? _I_ don’t see them anywhere near _me_!’\n\n‘My dear Fanny, so short a time--’\n\n‘Short time or long time,’ interrupted Fanny. ‘I am impatient of our situation. I don’t like our situation, and very little would induce me to change it. Other girls, differently reared and differently circumstanced altogether, might wonder at what I say or may do. Let them. They are driven by their lives and characters; I am driven by mine.’\n\n‘Fanny, my dear Fanny, you know that you have qualities to make you the wife of one very superior to Mr Sparkler.’\n\n‘Amy, my dear Amy,’ retorted Fanny, parodying her words, ‘I know that I wish to have a more defined and distinct position, in which I can assert myself with greater effect against that insolent woman.’\n\n‘Would you therefore--forgive my asking, Fanny--therefore marry her son?’\n\n‘Why, perhaps,’ said Fanny, with a triumphant smile. ‘There may be many less promising ways of arriving at an end than that, my dear. That piece of insolence may think, now, that it would be a great success to get her son off upon me, and shelve me. But, perhaps, she little thinks how I would retort upon her if I married her son. I would oppose her in everything, and compete with her. I would make it the business of my life.’\n\nFanny set down the bottle when she came to this, and walked about the room; always stopping and standing still while she spoke.\n\n‘One thing I could certainly do, my child: I could make her older. And I would!’\n\nThis was followed by another walk.\n\n‘I would talk of her as an old woman. I would pretend to know--if I didn’t, but I should from her son--all about her age. And she should hear me say, Amy: affectionately, quite dutifully and affectionately: how well she looked, considering her time of life. I could make her seem older at once, by being myself so much younger. I may not be as handsome as she is; I am not a fair judge of that question, I suppose; but I know I am handsome enough to be a thorn in her side. And I would be!’\n\n‘My dear sister, would you condemn yourself to an unhappy life for this?’\n\n‘It wouldn’t be an unhappy life, Amy. It would be the life I am fitted for. Whether by disposition, or whether by circumstances, is no matter; I am better fitted for such a life than for almost any other.’\n\nThere was something of a desolate tone in those words; but, with a short proud laugh she took another walk, and after passing a great looking-glass came to another stop.\n\n‘Figure! Figure, Amy! Well. The woman has a good figure. I will give her her due, and not deny it. But is it so far beyond all others that it is altogether unapproachable? Upon my word, I am not so sure of it. Give some much younger woman the latitude as to dress that she has, being married; and we would see about that, my dear!’\n\nSomething in the thought that was agreeable and flattering, brought her back to her seat in a gayer temper. She took her sister’s hands in hers, and clapped all four hands above her head as she looked in her sister’s face laughing:\n\n‘And the dancer, Amy, that she has quite forgotten--the dancer who bore no sort of resemblance to me, and of whom I never remind her, oh dear no!--should dance through her life, and dance in her way, to such a tune as would disturb her insolent placidity a little. Just a little, my dear Amy, just a little!’\n\nMeeting an earnest and imploring look in Amy’s face, she brought the four hands down, and laid only one on Amy’s lips.\n\n‘Now, don’t argue with me, child,’ she said in a sterner way, ‘because it is of no use. I understand these subjects much better than you do. I have not nearly made up my mind, but it may be. Now we have talked this over comfortably, and may go to bed. You best and dearest little mouse, Good night!’ With those words Fanny weighed her Anchor, and--having taken so much advice--left off being advised for that occasion.\n\nThenceforward, Amy observed Mr Sparkler’s treatment by his enslaver, with new reasons for attaching importance to all that passed between them. There were times when Fanny appeared quite unable to endure his mental feebleness, and when she became so sharply impatient of it that she would all but dismiss him for good. There were other times when she got on much better with him; when he amused her, and when her sense of superiority seemed to counterbalance that opposite side of the scale. If Mr Sparkler had been other than the faithfullest and most submissive of swains, he was sufficiently hard pressed to have fled from the scene of his trials, and have set at least the whole distance from Rome to London between himself and his enchantress. But he had no greater will of his own than a boat has when it is towed by a steam-ship; and he followed his cruel mistress through rough and smooth, on equally strong compulsion.\n\nMrs Merdle, during these passages, said little to Fanny, but said more about her. She was, as it were, forced to look at her through her eye-glass, and in general conversation to allow commendations of her beauty to be wrung from her by its irresistible demands. The defiant character it assumed when Fanny heard these extollings (as it generally happened that she did), was not expressive of concessions to the impartial bosom; but the utmost revenge the bosom took was, to say audibly, ‘A spoilt beauty--but with that face and shape, who could wonder?’\n\nIt might have been about a month or six weeks after the night of the new advice, when Little Dorrit began to think she detected some new understanding between Mr Sparkler and Fanny. Mr Sparkler, as if in attendance to some compact, scarcely ever spoke without first looking towards Fanny for leave. That young lady was too discreet ever to look back again; but, if Mr Sparkler had permission to speak, she remained silent; if he had not, she herself spoke. Moreover, it became plain whenever Henry Gowan attempted to perform the friendly office of drawing him out, that he was not to be drawn. And not only that, but Fanny would presently, without any pointed application in the world, chance to say something with such a sting in it that Gowan would draw back as if he had put his hand into a bee-hive.\n\nThere was yet another circumstance which went a long way to confirm Little Dorrit in her fears, though it was not a great circumstance in itself. Mr Sparkler’s demeanour towards herself changed. It became fraternal. Sometimes, when she was in the outer circle of assemblies--at their own residence, at Mrs Merdle’s, or elsewhere--she would find herself stealthily supported round the waist by Mr Sparkler’s arm. Mr Sparkler never offered the slightest explanation of this attention; but merely smiled with an air of blundering, contented, good-natured proprietorship, which, in so heavy a gentleman, was ominously expressive.\n\nLittle Dorrit was at home one day, thinking about Fanny with a heavy heart. They had a room at one end of their drawing-room suite, nearly all irregular bay-window, projecting over the street, and commanding all the picturesque life and variety of the Corso, both up and down. At three or four o’clock in the afternoon, English time, the view from this window was very bright and peculiar; and Little Dorrit used to sit and muse here, much as she had been used to while away the time in her balcony at Venice. Seated thus one day, she was softly touched on the shoulder, and Fanny said, ‘Well, Amy dear,’ and took her seat at her side. Their seat was a part of the window; when there was anything in the way of a procession going on, they used to have bright draperies hung out of the window, and used to kneel or sit on this seat, and look out at it, leaning on the brilliant colour. But there was no procession that day, and Little Dorrit was rather surprised by Fanny’s being at home at that hour, as she was generally out on horseback then.\n\n‘Well, Amy,’ said Fanny, ‘what are you thinking of, little one?’\n\n‘I was thinking of you, Fanny.’\n\n‘No? What a coincidence! I declare here’s some one else. You were not thinking of this some one else too; were you, Amy?’\n\nAmy _had_ been thinking of this some one else too; for it was Mr Sparkler. She did not say so, however, as she gave him her hand. Mr Sparkler came and sat down on the other side of her, and she felt the fraternal railing come behind her, and apparently stretch on to include Fanny.\n\n‘Well, my little sister,’ said Fanny with a sigh, ‘I suppose you know what this means?’\n\n‘She’s as beautiful as she’s doated on,’ stammered Mr Sparkler--‘and there’s no nonsense about her--it’s arranged--’\n\n‘You needn’t explain, Edmund,’ said Fanny.\n\n‘No, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler.\n\n‘In short, pet,’ proceeded Fanny, ‘on the whole, we are engaged. We must tell papa about it either to-night or to-morrow, according to the opportunities. Then it’s done, and very little more need be said.’\n\n‘My dear Fanny,’ said Mr Sparkler, with deference, ‘I should like to say a word to Amy.’\n\n‘Well, well! Say it for goodness’ sake,’ returned the young lady.\n\n‘I am convinced, my dear Amy,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘that if ever there was a girl, next to your highly endowed and beautiful sister, who had no nonsense about her--’\n\n‘We know all about that, Edmund,’ interposed Miss Fanny. ‘Never mind that. Pray go on to something else besides our having no nonsense about us.’\n\n‘Yes, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler. ‘And I assure you, Amy, that nothing can be a greater happiness to myself, myself--next to the happiness of being so highly honoured with the choice of a glorious girl who hasn’t an atom of--’\n\n‘Pray, Edmund, pray!’ interrupted Fanny, with a slight pat of her pretty foot upon the floor.\n\n‘My love, you’re quite right,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘and I know I have a habit of it. What I wished to declare was, that nothing can be a greater happiness to myself, myself-next to the happiness of being united to pre-eminently the most glorious of girls--than to have the happiness of cultivating the affectionate acquaintance of Amy. I may not myself,’ said Mr Sparkler manfully, ‘be up to the mark on some other subjects at a short notice, and I am aware that if you were to poll Society the general opinion would be that I am not; but on the subject of Amy I AM up to the mark!’\n\nMr Sparkler kissed her, in witness thereof.\n\n‘A knife and fork and an apartment,’ proceeded Mr Sparkler, growing, in comparison with his oratorical antecedents, quite diffuse, ‘will ever be at Amy’s disposal. My Governor, I am sure, will always be proud to entertain one whom I so much esteem. And regarding my mother,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘who is a remarkably fine woman, with--’\n\n‘Edmund, Edmund!’ cried Miss Fanny, as before.\n\n‘With submission, my soul,’ pleaded Mr Sparkler. ‘I know I have a habit of it, and I thank you very much, my adorable girl, for taking the trouble to correct it; but my mother is admitted on all sides to be a remarkably fine woman, and she really hasn’t any.’\n\n‘That may be, or may not be,’ returned Fanny, ‘but pray don’t mention it any more.’\n\n‘I will not, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler.\n\n‘Then, in fact, you have nothing more to say, Edmund; have you?’ inquired Fanny.\n\n‘So far from it, my adorable girl,’ answered Mr Sparkler, ‘I apologise for having said so much.’\n\nMr Sparkler perceived, by a kind of inspiration, that the question implied had he not better go? He therefore withdrew the fraternal railing, and neatly said that he thought he would, with submission, take his leave. He did not go without being congratulated by Amy, as well as she could discharge that office in the flutter and distress of her spirits.\n\nWhen he was gone, she said, ‘O Fanny, Fanny!’ and turned to her sister in the bright window, and fell upon her bosom and cried there. Fanny laughed at first; but soon laid her face against her sister’s and cried too--a little. It was the last time Fanny ever showed that there was any hidden, suppressed, or conquered feeling in her on the matter. From that hour the way she had chosen lay before her, and she trod it with her own imperious self-willed step.\n\nCHAPTER 15. No just Cause or Impediment why these Two Persons should not be joined together\nMr Dorrit, on being informed by his elder daughter that she had accepted matrimonial overtures from Mr Sparkler, to whom she had plighted her troth, received the communication at once with great dignity and with a large display of parental pride; his dignity dilating with the widened prospect of advantageous ground from which to make acquaintances, and his parental pride being developed by Miss Fanny’s ready sympathy with that great object of his existence. He gave her to understand that her noble ambition found harmonious echoes in his heart; and bestowed his blessing on her, as a child brimful of duty and good principle, self-devoted to the aggrandisement of the family name.\n\nTo Mr Sparkler, when Miss Fanny permitted him to appear, Mr Dorrit said, he would not disguise that the alliance Mr Sparkler did him the honour to propose was highly congenial to his feelings; both as being in unison with the spontaneous affections of his daughter Fanny, and as opening a family connection of a gratifying nature with Mr Merdle, the master spirit of the age. Mrs Merdle also, as a leading lady rich in distinction, elegance, grace, and beauty, he mentioned in very laudatory terms. He felt it his duty to remark (he was sure a gentleman of Mr Sparkler’s fine sense would interpret him with all delicacy), that he could not consider this proposal definitely determined on, until he should have had the privilege of holding some correspondence with Mr Merdle; and of ascertaining it to be so far accordant with the views of that eminent gentleman as that his (Mr Dorrit’s) daughter would be received on that footing which her station in life and her dowry and expectations warranted him in requiring that she should maintain in what he trusted he might be allowed, without the appearance of being mercenary, to call the Eye of the Great World. While saying this, which his character as a gentleman of some little station, and his character as a father, equally demanded of him, he would not be so diplomatic as to conceal that the proposal remained in hopeful abeyance and under conditional acceptance, and that he thanked Mr Sparkler for the compliment rendered to himself and to his family. He concluded with some further and more general observations on the--ha--character of an independent gentleman, and the--hum--character of a possibly too partial and admiring parent. To sum the whole up shortly, he received Mr Sparkler’s offer very much as he would have received three or four half-crowns from him in the days that were gone.\n\nMr Sparkler, finding himself stunned by the words thus heaped upon his inoffensive head, made a brief though pertinent rejoinder; the same being neither more nor less than that he had long perceived Miss Fanny to have no nonsense about her, and that he had no doubt of its being all right with his Governor. At that point the object of his affections shut him up like a box with a spring lid, and sent him away.\n\nProceeding shortly afterwards to pay his respects to the Bosom, Mr Dorrit was received by it with great consideration. Mrs Merdle had heard of this affair from Edmund. She had been surprised at first, because she had not thought Edmund a marrying man. Society had not thought Edmund a marrying man. Still, of course she had seen, as a woman (we women did instinctively see these things, Mr Dorrit!), that Edmund had been immensely captivated by Miss Dorrit, and she had openly said that Mr Dorrit had much to answer for in bringing so charming a girl abroad to turn the heads of his countrymen.\n\n‘Have I the honour to conclude, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘that the direction which Mr Sparkler’s affections have taken, is--ha-approved of by you?’\n\n‘I assure you, Mr Dorrit,’ returned the lady, ‘that, personally, I am charmed.’\n\nThat was very gratifying to Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘Personally,’ repeated Mrs Merdle, ‘charmed.’\n\nThis casual repetition of the word ‘personally,’ moved Mr Dorrit to express his hope that Mr Merdle’s approval, too, would not be wanting?\n\n‘I cannot,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘take upon myself to answer positively for Mr Merdle; gentlemen, especially gentlemen who are what Society calls capitalists, having their own ideas of these matters. But I should think--merely giving an opinion, Mr Dorrit--I should think Mr Merdle would be upon the whole,’ here she held a review of herself before adding at her leisure, ‘quite charmed.’\n\nAt the mention of gentlemen whom Society called capitalists, Mr Dorrit had coughed, as if some internal demur were breaking out of him. Mrs Merdle had observed it, and went on to take up the cue.\n\n‘Though, indeed, Mr Dorrit, it is scarcely necessary for me to make that remark, except in the mere openness of saying what is uppermost to one whom I so highly regard, and with whom I hope I may have the pleasure of being brought into still more agreeable relations. For one cannot but see the great probability of your considering such things from Mr Merdle’s own point of view, except indeed that circumstances have made it Mr Merdle’s accidental fortune, or misfortune, to be engaged in business transactions, and that they, however vast, may a little cramp his horizons. I am a very child as to having any notion of business,’ said Mrs Merdle; ‘but I am afraid, Mr Dorrit, it may have that tendency.’\n\nThis skilful see-saw of Mr Dorrit and Mrs Merdle, so that each of them sent the other up, and each of them sent the other down, and neither had the advantage, acted as a sedative on Mr Dorrit’s cough. He remarked with his utmost politeness, that he must beg to protest against its being supposed, even by Mrs Merdle, the accomplished and graceful (to which compliment she bent herself), that such enterprises as Mr Merdle’s, apart as they were from the puny undertakings of the rest of men, had any lower tendency than to enlarge and expand the genius in which they were conceived. ‘You are generosity itself,’ said Mrs Merdle in return, smiling her best smile; ‘let us hope so. But I confess I am almost superstitious in my ideas about business.’\n\nMr Dorrit threw in another compliment here, to the effect that business, like the time which was precious in it, was made for slaves; and that it was not for Mrs Merdle, who ruled all hearts at her supreme pleasure, to have anything to do with it. Mrs Merdle laughed, and conveyed to Mr Dorrit an idea that the Bosom flushed--which was one of her best effects.\n\n‘I say so much,’ she then explained, ‘merely because Mr Merdle has always taken the greatest interest in Edmund, and has always expressed the strongest desire to advance his prospects. Edmund’s public position, I think you know. His private position rests solely with Mr Merdle. In my foolish incapacity for business, I assure you I know no more.’\n\nMr Dorrit again expressed, in his own way, the sentiment that business was below the ken of enslavers and enchantresses. He then mentioned his intention, as a gentleman and a parent, of writing to Mr Merdle. Mrs Merdle concurred with all her heart--or with all her art, which was exactly the same thing--and herself despatched a preparatory letter by the next post to the eighth wonder of the world.\n\nIn his epistolary communication, as in his dialogues and discourses on the great question to which it related, Mr Dorrit surrounded the subject with flourishes, as writing-masters embellish copy-books and ciphering-books: where the titles of the elementary rules of arithmetic diverge into swans, eagles, griffins, and other calligraphic recreations, and where the capital letters go out of their minds and bodies into ecstasies of pen and ink. Nevertheless, he did render the purport of his letter sufficiently clear, to enable Mr Merdle to make a decent pretence of having learnt it from that source. Mr Merdle replied to it accordingly. Mr Dorrit replied to Mr Merdle; Mr Merdle replied to Mr Dorrit; and it was soon announced that the corresponding powers had come to a satisfactory understanding.\n\nNow, and not before, Miss Fanny burst upon the scene, completely arrayed for her new part. Now and not before, she wholly absorbed Mr Sparkler in her light, and shone for both, and twenty more. No longer feeling that want of a defined place and character which had caused her so much trouble, this fair ship began to steer steadily on a shaped course, and to swim with a weight and balance that developed her sailing qualities.\n\n‘The preliminaries being so satisfactorily arranged, I think I will now, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘announce--ha--formally, to Mrs General--’\n\n‘Papa,’ returned Fanny, taking him up short upon that name, ‘I don’t see what Mrs General has got to do with it.’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘it will be an act of courtesy to--hum--a lady, well bred and refined--’\n\n‘Oh! I am sick of Mrs General’s good breeding and refinement, papa,’ said Fanny. ‘I am tired of Mrs General.’\n\n‘Tired,’ repeated Mr Dorrit in reproachful astonishment, ‘of--ha--Mrs General.’\n\n‘Quite disgusted with her, papa,’ said Fanny. ‘I really don’t see what she has to do with my marriage. Let her keep to her own matrimonial projects--if she has any.’\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit, with a grave and weighty slowness upon him, contrasting strongly with his daughter’s levity: ‘I beg the favour of your explaining--ha--what it is you mean.’\n\n‘I mean, papa,’ said Fanny, ‘that if Mrs General should happen to have any matrimonial projects of her own, I dare say they are quite enough to occupy her spare time. And that if she has not, so much the better; but still I don’t wish to have the honour of making announcements to her.’\n\n‘Permit me to ask you, Fanny,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘why not?’\n\n‘Because she can find my engagement out for herself, papa,’ retorted Fanny. ‘She is watchful enough, I dare say. I think I have seen her so. Let her find it out for herself. If she should not find it out for herself, she will know it when I am married. And I hope you will not consider me wanting in affection for you, papa, if I say it strikes me that will be quite enough for Mrs General.’\n\n‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit, ‘I am amazed, I am displeased by this--hum--this capricious and unintelligible display of animosity towards--ha--Mrs General.’\n\n‘Do not, if you please, papa,’ urged Fanny, ‘call it animosity, because I assure you I do not consider Mrs General worth my animosity.’\n\nAt this, Mr Dorrit rose from his chair with a fixed look of severe reproof, and remained standing in his dignity before his daughter. His daughter, turning the bracelet on her arm, and now looking at him, and now looking from him, said, ‘Very well, papa. I am truly sorry if you don’t like it; but I can’t help it. I am not a child, and I am not Amy, and I must speak.’\n\n‘Fanny,’ gasped Mr Dorrit, after a majestic silence, ‘if I request you to remain here, while I formally announce to Mrs General, as an exemplary lady, who is--hum--a trusted member of this family, the--ha--the change that is contemplated among us; if I--ha--not only request it, but--hum--insist upon it--’\n\n‘Oh, papa,’ Fanny broke in with pointed significance, ‘if you make so much of it as that, I have in duty nothing to do but comply. I hope I may have my thoughts upon the subject, however, for I really cannot help it under the circumstances.’ So, Fanny sat down with a meekness which, in the junction of extremes, became defiance; and her father, either not deigning to answer, or not knowing what to answer, summoned Mr Tinkler into his presence.\n\n‘Mrs General.’\n\nMr Tinkler, unused to receive such short orders in connection with the fair varnisher, paused. Mr Dorrit, seeing the whole Marshalsea and all its testimonials in the pause, instantly flew at him with, ‘How dare you, sir? What do you mean?’\n\n‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ pleaded Mr Tinkler, ‘I was wishful to know--’\n\n‘You wished to know nothing, sir,’ cried Mr Dorrit, highly flushed. ‘Don’t tell me you did. Ha. You didn’t. You are guilty of mockery, sir.’\n\n‘I assure you, sir--’ Mr Tinkler began.\n\n‘Don’t assure me!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘I will not be assured by a domestic. You are guilty of mockery. You shall leave me--hum--the whole establishment shall leave me. What are you waiting for?’\n\n‘Only for my orders, sir.’\n\n‘It’s false,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘you have your orders. Ha--hum. My compliments to Mrs General, and I beg the favour of her coming to me, if quite convenient, for a few minutes. Those are your orders.’\n\nIn his execution of this mission, Mr Tinkler perhaps expressed that Mr Dorrit was in a raging fume. However that was, Mrs General’s skirts were very speedily heard outside, coming along--one might almost have said bouncing along--with unusual expedition. Albeit, they settled down at the door and swept into the room with their customary coolness.\n\n‘Mrs General,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘take a chair.’\n\nMrs General, with a graceful curve of acknowledgment, descended into the chair which Mr Dorrit offered.\n\n‘Madam,’ pursued that gentleman, ‘as you have had the kindness to undertake the--hum--formation of my daughters, and as I am persuaded that nothing nearly affecting them can--ha--be indifferent to you--’\n\n‘Wholly impossible,’ said Mrs General in the calmest of ways.\n\n‘--I therefore wish to announce to you, madam, that my daughter now present--’\n\nMrs General made a slight inclination of her head to Fanny, who made a very low inclination of her head to Mrs General, and came loftily upright again.\n\n‘--That my daughter Fanny is--ha--contracted to be married to Mr Sparkler, with whom you are acquainted. Hence, madam, you will be relieved of half your difficult charge--ha--difficult charge.’ Mr Dorrit repeated it with his angry eye on Fanny. ‘But not, I hope, to the--hum--diminution of any other portion, direct or indirect, of the footing you have at present the kindness to occupy in my family.’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, with her gloved hands resting on one another in exemplary repose, ‘is ever considerate, and ever but too appreciative of my friendly services.’\n\n(Miss Fanny coughed, as much as to say, ‘You are right.’)\n\n‘Miss Dorrit has no doubt exercised the soundest discretion of which the circumstances admitted, and I trust will allow me to offer her my sincere congratulations. When free from the trammels of passion,’ Mrs General closed her eyes at the word, as if she could not utter it, and see anybody; ‘when occurring with the approbation of near relatives; and when cementing the proud structure of a family edifice; these are usually auspicious events. I trust Miss Dorrit will allow me to offer her my best congratulations.’\n\nHere Mrs General stopped, and added internally, for the setting of her face, ‘Papa, potatoes, poultry, Prunes, and prism.’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ she superadded aloud, ‘is ever most obliging; and for the attention, and I will add distinction, of having this confidence imparted to me by himself and Miss Dorrit at this early time, I beg to offer the tribute of my thanks. My thanks, and my congratulations, are equally the meed of Mr Dorrit and of Miss Dorrit.’\n\n‘To me,’ observed Miss Fanny, ‘they are excessively gratifying--inexpressibly so. The relief of finding that you have no objection to make, Mrs General, quite takes a load off my mind, I am sure. I hardly know what I should have done,’ said Fanny, ‘if you had interposed any objection, Mrs General.’\n\nMrs General changed her gloves, as to the right glove being uppermost and the left undermost, with a Prunes and Prism smile.\n\n‘To preserve your approbation, Mrs General,’ said Fanny, returning the smile with one in which there was no trace of those ingredients, ‘will of course be the highest object of my married life; to lose it, would of course be perfect wretchedness. I am sure your great kindness will not object, and I hope papa will not object, to my correcting a small mistake you have made, however. The best of us are so liable to mistakes, that even you, Mrs General, have fallen into a little error. The attention and distinction you have so impressively mentioned, Mrs General, as attaching to this confidence, are, I have no doubt, of the most complimentary and gratifying description; but they don’t at all proceed from me. The merit of having consulted you on the subject would have been so great in me, that I feel I must not lay claim to it when it really is not mine. It is wholly papa’s. I am deeply obliged to you for your encouragement and patronage, but it was papa who asked for it. I have to thank you, Mrs General, for relieving my breast of a great weight by so handsomely giving your consent to my engagement, but you have really nothing to thank me for. I hope you will always approve of my proceedings after I have left home and that my sister also may long remain the favoured object of your condescension, Mrs General.’\n\nWith this address, which was delivered in her politest manner, Fanny left the room with an elegant and cheerful air--to tear up-stairs with a flushed face as soon as she was out of hearing, pounce in upon her sister, call her a little Dormouse, shake her for the better opening of her eyes, tell her what had passed below, and ask her what she thought of Pa now?\n\nTowards Mrs Merdle, the young lady comported herself with great independence and self-possession; but not as yet with any more decided opening of hostilities. Occasionally they had a slight skirmish, as when Fanny considered herself patted on the back by that lady, or as when Mrs Merdle looked particularly young and well; but Mrs Merdle always soon terminated those passages of arms by sinking among her cushions with the gracefullest indifference, and finding her attention otherwise engaged. Society (for that mysterious creature sat upon the Seven Hills too) found Miss Fanny vastly improved by her engagement. She was much more accessible, much more free and engaging, much less exacting; insomuch that she now entertained a host of followers and admirers, to the bitter indignation of ladies with daughters to marry, who were to be regarded as Having revolted from Society on the Miss Dorrit grievance, and erected a rebellious standard. Enjoying the flutter she caused. Miss Dorrit not only haughtily moved through it in her own proper person, but haughtily, even Ostentatiously, led Mr Sparkler through it too: seeming to say to them all, ‘If I think proper to march among you in triumphal procession attended by this weak captive in bonds, rather than a stronger one, that is my business. Enough that I choose to do it!’ Mr Sparkler for his part, questioned nothing; but went wherever he was taken, did whatever he was told, felt that for his bride-elect to be distinguished was for him to be distinguished on the easiest terms, and was truly grateful for being so openly acknowledged.\n\nThe winter passing on towards the spring while this condition of affairs prevailed, it became necessary for Mr Sparkler to repair to England, and take his appointed part in the expression and direction of its genius, learning, commerce, spirit, and sense. The land of Shakespeare, Milton, Bacon, Newton, Watt, the land of a host of past and present abstract philosophers, natural philosophers, and subduers of Nature and Art in their myriad forms, called to Mr Sparkler to come and take care of it, lest it should perish. Mr Sparkler, unable to resist the agonised cry from the depths of his country’s soul, declared that he must go.\n\nIt followed that the question was rendered pressing when, where, and how Mr Sparkler should be married to the foremost girl in all this world with no nonsense about her. Its solution, after some little mystery and secrecy, Miss Fanny herself announced to her sister.\n\n‘Now, my child,’ said she, seeking her out one day, ‘I am going to tell you something. It is only this moment broached; and naturally I hurry to you the moment it _is_ broached.’\n\n‘Your marriage, Fanny?’\n\n‘My precious child,’ said Fanny, ‘don’t anticipate me. Let me impart my confidence to you, you flurried little thing, in my own way. As to your guess, if I answered it literally, I should answer no. For really it is not my marriage that is in question, half as much as it is Edmund’s.’\n\nLittle Dorrit looked, and perhaps not altogether without cause, somewhat at a loss to understand this fine distinction.\n\n‘I am in no difficulty,’ exclaimed Fanny, ‘and in no hurry. I am not wanted at any public office, or to give any vote anywhere else. But Edmund is. And Edmund is deeply dejected at the idea of going away by himself, and, indeed, I don’t like that he should be trusted by himself. For, if it’s possible--and it generally is--to do a foolish thing, he is sure to do it.’\n\nAs she concluded this impartial summary of the reliance that might be safely placed upon her future husband, she took off, with an air of business, the bonnet she wore, and dangled it by its strings upon the ground.\n\n‘It is far more Edmund’s question, therefore, than mine. However, we need say no more about that. That is self-evident on the face of it. Well, my dearest Amy! The point arising, is he to go by himself, or is he not to go by himself, this other point arises, are we to be married here and shortly, or are we to be married at home months hence?’\n\n‘I see I am going to lose you, Fanny.’\n\n‘What a little thing you are,’ cried Fanny, half tolerant and half impatient, ‘for anticipating one! Pray, my darling, hear me out. That woman,’ she spoke of Mrs Merdle, of course, ‘remains here until after Easter; so, in the case of my being married here and going to London with Edmund, I should have the start of her. That is something. Further, Amy. That woman being out of the way, I don’t know that I greatly object to Mr Merdle’s proposal to Pa that Edmund and I should take up our abode in that house--_you_ know--where you once went with a dancer, my dear, until our own house can be chosen and fitted up. Further still, Amy. Papa having always intended to go to town himself, in the spring,--you see, if Edmund and I were married here, we might go off to Florence, where papa might join us, and we might all three travel home together. Mr Merdle has entreated Pa to stay with him in that same mansion I have mentioned, and I suppose he will. But he is master of his own actions; and upon that point (which is not at all material) I can’t speak positively.’\n\nThe difference between papa’s being master of his own actions and Mr Sparkler’s being nothing of the sort, was forcibly expressed by Fanny in her manner of stating the case. Not that her sister noticed it; for she was divided between regret at the coming separation, and a lingering wish that she had been included in the plans for visiting England.\n\n‘And these are the arrangements, Fanny dear?’\n\n‘Arrangements!’ repeated Fanny. ‘Now, really, child, you are a little trying. You know I particularly guarded myself against laying my words open to any such construction. What I said was, that certain questions present themselves; and these are the questions.’\n\nLittle Dorrit’s thoughtful eyes met hers, tenderly and quietly.\n\n‘Now, my own sweet girl,’ said Fanny, weighing her bonnet by the strings with considerable impatience, ‘it’s no use staring. A little owl could stare. I look to you for advice, Amy. What do you advise me to do?’\n\n‘Do you think,’ asked Little Dorrit, persuasively, after a short hesitation, ‘do you think, Fanny, that if you were to put it off for a few months, it might be, considering all things, best?’\n\n‘No, little Tortoise,’ retorted Fanny, with exceeding sharpness. ‘I don’t think anything of the kind.’\n\nHere, she threw her bonnet from her altogether, and flounced into a chair. But, becoming affectionate almost immediately, she flounced out of it again, and kneeled down on the floor to take her sister, chair and all, in her arms.\n\n‘Don’t suppose I am hasty or unkind, darling, because I really am not. But you are such a little oddity! You make one bite your head off, when one wants to be soothing beyond everything. Didn’t I tell you, you dearest baby, that Edmund can’t be trusted by himself? And don’t you know that he can’t?’\n\n‘Yes, yes, Fanny. You said so, I know.’\n\n‘And you know it, I know,’ retorted Fanny. ‘Well, my precious child! If he is not to be trusted by himself, it follows, I suppose, that I should go with him?’\n\n‘It--seems so, love,’ said Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Therefore, having heard the arrangements that are feasible to carry out that object, am I to understand, dearest Amy, that on the whole you advise me to make them?’\n\n‘It--seems so, love,’ said Little Dorrit again.\n\n‘Very well,’ cried Fanny with an air of resignation, ‘then I suppose it must be done! I came to you, my sweet, the moment I saw the doubt, and the necessity of deciding. I have now decided. So let it be.’\n\nAfter yielding herself up, in this pattern manner, to sisterly advice and the force of circumstances, Fanny became quite benignant: as one who had laid her own inclinations at the feet of her dearest friend, and felt a glow of conscience in having made the sacrifice. ‘After all, my Amy,’ she said to her sister, ‘you are the best of small creatures, and full of good sense; and I don’t know what I shall ever do without you!’\n\nWith which words she folded her in a closer embrace, and a really fond one.\n\n‘Not that I contemplate doing without You, Amy, by any means, for I hope we shall ever be next to inseparable. And now, my pet, I am going to give you a word of advice. When you are left alone here with Mrs General--’\n\n‘I am to be left alone here with Mrs General?’ said Little Dorrit, quietly.\n\n‘Why, of course, my precious, till papa comes back! Unless you call Edward company, which he certainly is not, even when he is here, and still more certainly is not when he is away at Naples or in Sicily. I was going to say--but you are such a beloved little Marplot for putting one out--when you are left alone here with Mrs General, Amy, don’t you let her slide into any sort of artful understanding with you that she is looking after Pa, or that Pa is looking after her. She will if she can. I know her sly manner of feeling her way with those gloves of hers. But don’t you comprehend her on any account. And if Pa should tell you when he comes back, that he has it in contemplation to make Mrs General your mama (which is not the less likely because I am going away), my advice to you is, that you say at once, “Papa, I beg to object most strongly. Fanny cautioned me about this, and she objected, and I object.” I don’t mean to say that any objection from you, Amy, is likely to be of the smallest effect, or that I think you likely to make it with any degree of firmness. But there is a principle involved--a filial principle--and I implore you not to submit to be mother-in-lawed by Mrs General, without asserting it in making every one about you as uncomfortable as possible. I don’t expect you to stand by it--indeed, I know you won’t, Pa being concerned--but I wish to rouse you to a sense of duty. As to any help from me, or as to any opposition that I can offer to such a match, you shall not be left in the lurch, my love. Whatever weight I may derive from my position as a married girl not wholly devoid of attractions--used, as that position always shall be, to oppose that woman--I will bring to bear, you May depend upon it, on the head and false hair (for I am confident it’s not all real, ugly as it is and unlikely as it appears that any One in their Senses would go to the expense of buying it) of Mrs General!’\n\nLittle Dorrit received this counsel without venturing to oppose it but without giving Fanny any reason to believe that she intended to act upon it. Having now, as it were, formally wound up her single life and arranged her worldly affairs, Fanny proceeded with characteristic ardour to prepare for the serious change in her condition.\n\nThe preparation consisted in the despatch of her maid to Paris under the protection of the Courier, for the purchase of that outfit for a bride on which it would be extremely low, in the present narrative, to bestow an English name, but to which (on a vulgar principle it observes of adhering to the language in which it professes to be written) it declines to give a French one. The rich and beautiful wardrobe purchased by these agents, in the course of a few weeks made its way through the intervening country, bristling with custom-houses, garrisoned by an immense army of shabby mendicants in uniform who incessantly repeated the Beggar’s Petition over it, as if every individual warrior among them were the ancient Belisarius: and of whom there were so many Legions, that unless the Courier had expended just one bushel and a half of silver money relieving their distresses, they would have worn the wardrobe out before it got to Rome, by turning it over and over. Through all such dangers, however, it was triumphantly brought, inch by inch, and arrived at its journey’s end in fine condition.\n\nThere it was exhibited to select companies of female viewers, in whose gentle bosoms it awakened implacable feelings. Concurrently, active preparations were made for the day on which some of its treasures were to be publicly displayed. Cards of breakfast-invitation were sent out to half the English in the city of Romulus; the other half made arrangements to be under arms, as criticising volunteers, at various outer points of the solemnity. The most high and illustrious English Signor Edgardo Dorrit, came post through the deep mud and ruts (from forming a surface under the improving Neapolitan nobility), to grace the occasion. The best hotel and all its culinary myrmidons, were set to work to prepare the feast. The drafts of Mr Dorrit almost constituted a run on the Torlonia Bank. The British Consul hadn’t had such a marriage in the whole of his Consularity.\n\nThe day came, and the She-Wolf in the Capitol might have snarled with envy to see how the Island Savages contrived these things now-a-days. The murderous-headed statues of the wicked Emperors of the Soldiery, whom sculptors had not been able to flatter out of their villainous hideousness, might have come off their pedestals to run away with the Bride. The choked old fountain, where erst the gladiators washed, might have leaped into life again to honour the ceremony. The Temple of Vesta might have sprung up anew from its ruins, expressly to lend its countenance to the occasion. Might have done; but did not. Like sentient things--even like the lords and ladies of creation sometimes--might have done much, but did nothing. The celebration went off with admirable pomp; monks in black robes, white robes, and russet robes stopped to look after the carriages; wandering peasants in fleeces of sheep, begged and piped under the house-windows; the English volunteers defiled; the day wore on to the hour of vespers; the festival wore away; the thousand churches rang their bells without any reference to it; and St Peter denied that he had anything to do with it.\n\nBut by that time the Bride was near the end of the first day’s journey towards Florence. It was the peculiarity of the nuptials that they were all Bride. Nobody noticed the Bridegroom. Nobody noticed the first Bridesmaid. Few could have seen Little Dorrit (who held that post) for the glare, even supposing many to have sought her. So, the Bride had mounted into her handsome chariot, incidentally accompanied by the Bridegroom; and after rolling for a few minutes smoothly over a fair pavement, had begun to jolt through a Slough of Despond, and through a long, long avenue of wrack and ruin. Other nuptial carriages are said to have gone the same road, before and since.\n\nIf Little Dorrit found herself left a little lonely and a little low that night, nothing would have done so much against her feeling of depression as the being able to sit at work by her father, as in the old time, and help him to his supper and his rest. But that was not to be thought of now, when they sat in the state-equipage with Mrs General on the coach-box. And as to supper! If Mr Dorrit had wanted supper, there was an Italian cook and there was a Swiss confectioner, who must have put on caps as high as the Pope’s Mitre, and have performed the mysteries of Alchemists in a copper-saucepaned laboratory below, before he could have got it.\n\nHe was sententious and didactic that night. If he had been simply loving, he would have done Little Dorrit more good; but she accepted him as he was--when had she not accepted him as he was!--and made the most and best of him. Mrs General at length retired. Her retirement for the night was always her frostiest ceremony, as if she felt it necessary that the human imagination should be chilled into stone to prevent its following her. When she had gone through her rigid preliminaries, amounting to a sort of genteel platoon-exercise, she withdrew. Little Dorrit then put her arm round her father’s neck, to bid him good night.\n\n‘Amy, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, taking her by the hand, ‘this is the close of a day, that has--ha--greatly impressed and gratified me.’\n\n‘A little tired you, dear, too?’\n\n‘No,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘no: I am not sensible of fatigue when it arises from an occasion so--hum--replete with gratification of the purest kind.’\n\nLittle Dorrit was glad to find him in such heart, and smiled from her own heart.\n\n‘My dear,’ he continued, ‘this is an occasion--ha--teeming with a good example. With a good example, my favourite and attached child--hum--to you.’\n\nLittle Dorrit, fluttered by his words, did not know what to say, though he stopped as if he expected her to say something.\n\n‘Amy,’ he resumed; ‘your dear sister, our Fanny, has contracted ha hum--a marriage, eminently calculated to extend the basis of our--ha--connection, and to--hum--consolidate our social relations. My love, I trust that the time is not far distant when some--ha--eligible partner may be found for you.’\n\n‘Oh no! Let me stay with you. I beg and pray that I may stay with you! I want nothing but to stay and take care of you!’\n\nShe said it like one in sudden alarm.\n\n‘Nay, Amy, Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘This is weak and foolish, weak and foolish. You have a--ha--responsibility imposed upon you by your position. It is to develop that position, and be--hum--worthy of that position. As to taking care of me; I can--ha--take care of myself. Or,’ he added after a moment, ‘if I should need to be taken care of, I--hum--can, with the--ha--blessing of Providence, be taken care of, I--ha hum--I cannot, my dear child, think of engrossing, and--ha--as it were, sacrificing you.’\n\nO what a time of day at which to begin that profession of self-denial; at which to make it, with an air of taking credit for it; at which to believe it, if such a thing could be!\n\n‘Don’t speak, Amy. I positively say I cannot do it. I--ha--must not do it. My--hum--conscience would not allow it. I therefore, my love, take the opportunity afforded by this gratifying and impressive occasion of--ha--solemnly remarking, that it is now a cherished wish and purpose of mine to see you--ha--eligibly (I repeat eligibly) married.’\n\n‘Oh no, dear! Pray!’\n\n‘Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I am well persuaded that if the topic were referred to any person of superior social knowledge, of superior delicacy and sense--let us say, for instance, to--ha--Mrs General--that there would not be two opinions as to the--hum--affectionate character and propriety of my sentiments. But, as I know your loving and dutiful nature from--hum--from experience, I am quite satisfied that it is necessary to say no more. I have--hum--no husband to propose at present, my dear: I have not even one in view. I merely wish that we should--ha--understand each other. Hum. Good night, my dear and sole remaining daughter. Good night. God bless you!’\n\nIf the thought ever entered Little Dorrit’s head that night, that he could give her up lightly now in his prosperity, and when he had it in his mind to replace her with a second wife, she drove it away. Faithful to him still, as in the worst times through which she had borne him single-handed, she drove the thought away; and entertained no harder reflection, in her tearful unrest, than that he now saw everything through their wealth, and through the care he always had upon him that they should continue rich, and grow richer.\n\nThey sat in their equipage of state, with Mrs General on the box, for three weeks longer, and then he started for Florence to join Fanny. Little Dorrit would have been glad to bear him company so far, only for the sake of her own love, and then to have turned back alone, thinking of dear England. But, though the Courier had gone on with the Bride, the Valet was next in the line; and the succession would not have come to her, as long as any one could be got for money.\n\nMrs General took life easily--as easily, that is, as she could take anything--when the Roman establishment remained in their sole occupation; and Little Dorrit would often ride out in a hired carriage that was left them, and alight alone and wander among the ruins of old Rome. The ruins of the vast old Amphitheatre, of the old Temples, of the old commemorative Arches, of the old trodden highways, of the old tombs, besides being what they were, to her were ruins of the old Marshalsea--ruins of her own old life--ruins of the faces and forms that of old peopled it--ruins of its loves, hopes, cares, and joys. Two ruined spheres of action and suffering were before the solitary girl often sitting on some broken fragment; and in the lonely places, under the blue sky, she saw them both together.\n\nUp, then, would come Mrs General; taking all the colour out of everything, as Nature and Art had taken it out of herself; writing Prunes and Prism, in Mr Eustace’s text, wherever she could lay a hand; looking everywhere for Mr Eustace and company, and seeing nothing else; scratching up the driest little bones of antiquity, and bolting them whole without any human visitings--like a Ghoule in gloves.\n\n# CHAPTER 16. Getting on\n\nThe newly married pair, on their arrival in Harley Street, Cavendish Square, London, were received by the Chief Butler. That great man was not interested in them, but on the whole endured them. People must continue to be married and given in marriage, or Chief Butlers would not be wanted. As nations are made to be taxed, so families are made to be butlered. The Chief Butler, no doubt, reflected that the course of nature required the wealthy population to be kept up, on his account.\n\nHe therefore condescended to look at the carriage from the Hall-door without frowning at it, and said, in a very handsome way, to one of his men, ‘Thomas, help with the luggage.’ He even escorted the Bride up-stairs into Mr Merdle’s presence; but this must be considered as an act of homage to the sex (of which he was an admirer, being notoriously captivated by the charms of a certain Duchess), and not as a committal of himself with the family.\n\nMr Merdle was slinking about the hearthrug, waiting to welcome Mrs Sparkler. His hand seemed to retreat up his sleeve as he advanced to do so, and he gave her such a superfluity of coat-cuff that it was like being received by the popular conception of Guy Fawkes. When he put his lips to hers, besides, he took himself into custody by the wrists, and backed himself among the ottomans and chairs and tables as if he were his own Police officer, saying to himself, ‘Now, none of that! Come! I’ve got you, you know, and you go quietly along with me!’\n\nMrs Sparkler, installed in the rooms of state--the innermost sanctuary of down, silk, chintz, and fine linen--felt that so far her triumph was good, and her way made, step by step. On the day before her marriage, she had bestowed on Mrs Merdle’s maid with an air of gracious indifference, in Mrs Merdle’s presence, a trifling little keepsake (bracelet, bonnet, and two dresses, all new) about four times as valuable as the present formerly made by Mrs Merdle to her. She was now established in Mrs Merdle’s own rooms, to which some extra touches had been given to render them more worthy of her occupation. In her mind’s eye, as she lounged there, surrounded by every luxurious accessory that wealth could obtain or invention devise, she saw the fair bosom that beat in unison with the exultation of her thoughts, competing with the bosom that had been famous so long, outshining it, and deposing it. Happy? Fanny must have been happy. No more wishing one’s self dead now.\n\nThe Courier had not approved of Mr Dorrit’s staying in the house of a friend, and had preferred to take him to an hotel in Brook Street, Grosvenor Square. Mr Merdle ordered his carriage to be ready early in the morning that he might wait upon Mr Dorrit immediately after breakfast.\n\nBright the carriage looked, sleek the horses looked, gleaming the harness looked, luscious and lasting the liveries looked. A rich, responsible turn-out. An equipage for a Merdle. Early people looked after it as it rattled along the streets, and said, with awe in their breath, ‘There he goes!’\n\nThere he went, until Brook Street stopped him. Then, forth from its magnificent case came the jewel; not lustrous in itself, but quite the contrary.\n\nCommotion in the office of the hotel. Merdle! The landlord, though a gentleman of a haughty spirit who had just driven a pair of thorough-bred horses into town, turned out to show him up-stairs. The clerks and servants cut him off by back-passages, and were found accidentally hovering in doorways and angles, that they might look upon him. Merdle! O ye sun, moon, and stars, the great man! The rich man, who had in a manner revised the New Testament, and already entered into the kingdom of Heaven. The man who could have any one he chose to dine with him, and who had made the money! As he went up the stairs, people were already posted on the lower stairs, that his shadow might fall upon them when he came down. So were the sick brought out and laid in the track of the Apostle--who had _not_ got into the good society, and had _not_ made the money.\n\nMr Dorrit, dressing-gowned and newspapered, was at his breakfast. The Courier, with agitation in his voice, announced ‘Miss Mairdale!’ Mr Dorrit’s overwrought heart bounded as he leaped up.\n\n‘Mr Merdle, this is--ha--indeed an honour. Permit me to express the--hum--sense, the high sense, I entertain of this--ha hum--highly gratifying act of attention. I am well aware, sir, of the many demands upon your time, and its--ha--enormous value,’ Mr Dorrit could not say enormous roundly enough for his own satisfaction. ‘That you should--ha--at this early hour, bestow any of your priceless time upon me, is--ha--a compliment that I acknowledge with the greatest esteem.’ Mr Dorrit positively trembled in addressing the great man.\n\nMr Merdle uttered, in his subdued, inward, hesitating voice, a few sounds that were to no purpose whatever; and finally said, ‘I am glad to see you, sir.’\n\n‘You are very kind,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Truly kind.’ By this time the visitor was seated, and was passing his great hand over his exhausted forehead. ‘You are well, I hope, Mr Merdle?’\n\n‘I am as well as I--yes, I am as well as I usually am,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\n‘Your occupations must be immense.’\n\n‘Tolerably so. But--Oh dear no, there’s not much the matter with _me_,’ said Mr Merdle, looking round the room.\n\n‘A little dyspeptic?’ Mr Dorrit hinted.\n\n‘Very likely. But I--Oh, I am well enough,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nThere were black traces on his lips where they met, as if a little train of gunpowder had been fired there; and he looked like a man who, if his natural temperament had been quicker, would have been very feverish that morning. This, and his heavy way of passing his hand over his forehead, had prompted Mr Dorrit’s solicitous inquiries.\n\n‘Mrs Merdle,’ Mr Dorrit insinuatingly pursued, ‘I left, as you will be prepared to hear, the--ha--observed of all observers, the--hum--admired of all admirers, the leading fascination and charm of Society in Rome. She was looking wonderfully well when I quitted it.’\n\n‘Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘is generally considered a very attractive woman. And she is, no doubt. I am sensible of her being so.’\n\n‘Who can be otherwise?’ responded Mr Dorrit.\n\nMr Merdle turned his tongue in his closed mouth--it seemed rather a stiff and unmanageable tongue--moistened his lips, passed his hand over his forehead again, and looked all round the room again, principally under the chairs.\n\n‘But,’ he said, looking Mr Dorrit in the face for the first time, and immediately afterwards dropping his eyes to the buttons of Mr Dorrit’s waistcoat; ‘if we speak of attractions, your daughter ought to be the subject of our conversation. She is extremely beautiful. Both in face and figure, she is quite uncommon. When the young people arrived last night, I was really surprised to see such charms.’\n\nMr Dorrit’s gratification was such that he said--ha--he could not refrain from telling Mr Merdle verbally, as he had already done by letter, what honour and happiness he felt in this union of their families. And he offered his hand. Mr Merdle looked at the hand for a little while, took it on his for a moment as if his were a yellow salver or fish-slice, and then returned it to Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘I thought I would drive round the first thing,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘to offer my services, in case I can do anything for you; and to say that I hope you will at least do me the honour of dining with me to-day, and every day when you are not better engaged during your stay in town.’\n\nMr Dorrit was enraptured by these attentions.\n\n‘Do you stay long, sir?’\n\n‘I have not at present the intention,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘of--ha--exceeding a fortnight.’\n\n‘That’s a very short stay, after so long a journey,’ returned Mr Merdle.\n\n‘Hum. Yes,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘But the truth is--ha--my dear Mr Merdle, that I find a foreign life so well suited to my health and taste, that I--hum--have but two objects in my present visit to London. First, the--ha--the distinguished happiness and--ha--privilege which I now enjoy and appreciate; secondly, the arrangement--hum--the laying out, that is to say, in the best way, of--ha, hum--my money.’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ said Mr Merdle, after turning his tongue again, ‘if I can be of any use to you in that respect, you may command me.’\n\nMr Dorrit’s speech had had more hesitation in it than usual, as he approached the ticklish topic, for he was not perfectly clear how so exalted a potentate might take it. He had doubts whether reference to any individual capital, or fortune, might not seem a wretchedly retail affair to so wholesale a dealer. Greatly relieved by Mr Merdle’s affable offer of assistance, he caught at it directly, and heaped acknowledgments upon him.\n\n‘I scarcely--ha--dared,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I assure you, to hope for so--hum--vast an advantage as your direct advice and assistance. Though of course I should, under any circumstances, like the--ha, hum--rest of the civilised world, have followed in Mr Merdle’s train.’\n\n‘You know we may almost say we are related, sir,’ said Mr Merdle, curiously interested in the pattern of the carpet, ‘and, therefore, you may consider me at your service.’\n\n‘Ha. Very handsome, indeed!’ cried Mr Dorrit. ‘Ha. Most handsome!’\n\n‘It would not,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘be at the present moment easy for what I may call a mere outsider to come into any of the good things--of course I speak of my own good things--’\n\n‘Of course, of course!’ cried Mr Dorrit, in a tone implying that there were no other good things.\n\n‘--Unless at a high price. At what we are accustomed to term a very long figure.’\n\nMr Dorrit laughed in the buoyancy of his spirit. Ha, ha, ha! Long figure. Good. Ha. Very expressive to be sure!\n\n‘However,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I do generally retain in my own hands the power of exercising some preference--people in general would be pleased to call it favour--as a sort of compliment for my care and trouble.’\n\n‘And public spirit and genius,’ Mr Dorrit suggested.\n\nMr Merdle, with a dry, swallowing action, seemed to dispose of those qualities like a bolus; then added, ‘As a sort of return for it. I will see, if you please, how I can exert this limited power (for people are jealous, and it is limited), to your advantage.’\n\n‘You are very good,’ replied Mr Dorrit. ‘You are _very_ good.’\n\n‘Of course,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘there must be the strictest integrity and uprightness in these transactions; there must be the purest faith between man and man; there must be unimpeached and unimpeachable confidence; or business could not be carried on.’\n\nMr Dorrit hailed these generous sentiments with fervour.\n\n‘Therefore,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I can only give you a preference to a certain extent.’\n\n‘I perceive. To a defined extent,’ observed Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘Defined extent. And perfectly above-board. As to my advice, however,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘that is another matter. That, such as it is--’\n\nOh! Such as it was! (Mr Dorrit could not bear the faintest appearance of its being depreciated, even by Mr Merdle himself.)\n\n‘--That, there is nothing in the bonds of spotless honour between myself and my fellow-man to prevent my parting with, if I choose. And that,’ said Mr Merdle, now deeply intent upon a dust-cart that was passing the windows, ‘shall be at your command whenever you think proper.’\n\nNew acknowledgments from Mr Dorrit. New passages of Mr Merdle’s hand over his forehead. Calm and silence. Contemplation of Mr Dorrit’s waistcoat buttons by Mr Merdle.\n\n‘My time being rather precious,’ said Mr Merdle, suddenly getting up, as if he had been waiting in the interval for his legs and they had just come, ‘I must be moving towards the City. Can I take you anywhere, sir? I shall be happy to set you down, or send you on. My carriage is at your disposal.’\n\nMr Dorrit bethought himself that he had business at his banker’s. His banker’s was in the City. That was fortunate; Mr Merdle would take him into the City. But, surely, he might not detain Mr Merdle while he assumed his coat? Yes, he might and must; Mr Merdle insisted on it. So Mr Dorrit, retiring into the next room, put himself under the hands of his valet, and in five minutes came back glorious.\n\nThen said Mr Merdle, ‘Allow me, sir. Take my arm!’ Then leaning on Mr Merdle’s arm, did Mr Dorrit descend the staircase, seeing the worshippers on the steps, and feeling that the light of Mr Merdle shone by reflection in himself. Then the carriage, and the ride into the City; and the people who looked at them; and the hats that flew off grey heads; and the general bowing and crouching before this wonderful mortal the like of which prostration of spirit was not to be seen--no, by high Heaven, no! It may be worth thinking of by Fawners of all denominations--in Westminster Abbey and Saint Paul’s Cathedral put together, on any Sunday in the year. It was a rapturous dream to Mr Dorrit to find himself set aloft in this public car of triumph, making a magnificent progress to that befitting destination, the golden Street of the Lombards.\n\nThere Mr Merdle insisted on alighting and going his way a-foot, and leaving his poor equipage at Mr Dorrit’s disposition. So the dream increased in rapture when Mr Dorrit came out of the bank alone, and people looked at _him_ in default of Mr Merdle, and when, with the ears of his mind, he heard the frequent exclamation as he rolled glibly along, ‘A wonderful man to be Mr Merdle’s friend!’\n\nAt dinner that day, although the occasion was not foreseen and provided for, a brilliant company of such as are not made of the dust of the earth, but of some superior article for the present unknown, shed their lustrous benediction upon Mr Dorrit’s daughter’s marriage. And Mr Dorrit’s daughter that day began, in earnest, her competition with that woman not present; and began it so well that Mr Dorrit could all but have taken his affidavit, if required, that Mrs Sparkler had all her life been lying at full length in the lap of luxury, and had never heard of such a rough word in the English tongue as Marshalsea.\n\nNext day, and the day after, and every day, all graced by more dinner company, cards descended on Mr Dorrit like theatrical snow. As the friend and relative by marriage of the illustrious Merdle, Bar, Bishop, Treasury, Chorus, Everybody, wanted to make or improve Mr Dorrit’s acquaintance. In Mr Merdle’s heap of offices in the City, when Mr Dorrit appeared at any of them on his business taking him Eastward (which it frequently did, for it throve amazingly), the name of Dorrit was always a passport to the great presence of Merdle. So the dream increased in rapture every hour, as Mr Dorrit felt increasingly sensible that this connection had brought him forward indeed.\n\nOnly one thing sat otherwise than auriferously, and at the same time lightly, on Mr Dorrit’s mind. It was the Chief Butler. That stupendous character looked at him, in the course of his official looking at the dinners, in a manner that Mr Dorrit considered questionable. He looked at him, as he passed through the hall and up the staircase, going to dinner, with a glazed fixedness that Mr Dorrit did not like. Seated at table in the act of drinking, Mr Dorrit still saw him through his wine-glass, regarding him with a cold and ghostly eye. It misgave him that the Chief Butler must have known a Collegian, and must have seen him in the College--perhaps had been presented to him. He looked as closely at the Chief Butler as such a man could be looked at, and yet he did not recall that he had ever seen him elsewhere. Ultimately he was inclined to think that there was no reverence in the man, no sentiment in the great creature. But he was not relieved by that; for, let him think what he would, the Chief Butler had him in his supercilious eye, even when that eye was on the plate and other table-garniture; and he never let him out of it. To hint to him that this confinement in his eye was disagreeable, or to ask him what he meant, was an act too daring to venture upon; his severity with his employers and their visitors being terrific, and he never permitting himself to be approached with the slightest liberty.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. Missing\n\nThe term of Mr Dorrit’s visit was within two days of being out, and he was about to dress for another inspection by the Chief Butler (whose victims were always dressed expressly for him), when one of the servants of the hotel presented himself bearing a card. Mr Dorrit, taking it, read:\n\n‘Mrs Finching.’\n\nThe servant waited in speechless deference.\n\n‘Man, man,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning upon him with grievous indignation, ‘explain your motive in bringing me this ridiculous name. I am wholly unacquainted with it. Finching, sir?’ said Mr Dorrit, perhaps avenging himself on the Chief Butler by Substitute. ‘Ha! What do you mean by Finching?’\n\nThe man, man, seemed to mean Flinching as much as anything else, for he backed away from Mr Dorrit’s severe regard, as he replied, ‘A lady, sir.’\n\n‘I know no such lady, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Take this card away. I know no Finching of either sex.’\n\n‘Ask your pardon, sir. The lady said she was aware she might be unknown by name. But she begged me to say, sir, that she had formerly the honour of being acquainted with Miss Dorrit. The lady said, sir, the youngest Miss Dorrit.’\n\nMr Dorrit knitted his brows and rejoined, after a moment or two, ‘Inform Mrs Finching, sir,’ emphasising the name as if the innocent man were solely responsible for it, ‘that she can come up.’\n\nHe had reflected, in his momentary pause, that unless she were admitted she might leave some message, or might say something below, having a disgraceful reference to that former state of existence. Hence the concession, and hence the appearance of Flora, piloted in by the man, man.\n\n‘I have not the pleasure,’ said Mr Dorrit, standing with the card in his hand, and with an air which imported that it would scarcely have been a first-class pleasure if he had had it, ‘of knowing either this name, or yourself, madam. Place a chair, sir.’\n\nThe responsible man, with a start, obeyed, and went out on tiptoe. Flora, putting aside her veil with a bashful tremor upon her, proceeded to introduce herself. At the same time a singular combination of perfumes was diffused through the room, as if some brandy had been put by mistake in a lavender-water bottle, or as if some lavender-water had been put by mistake in a brandy-bottle.\n\n‘I beg Mr Dorrit to offer a thousand apologies and indeed they would be far too few for such an intrusion which I know must appear extremely bold in a lady and alone too, but I thought it best upon the whole however difficult and even apparently improper though Mr F.’s Aunt would have willingly accompanied me and as a character of great force and spirit would probably have struck one possessed of such a knowledge of life as no doubt with so many changes must have been acquired, for Mr F. himself said frequently that although well educated in the neighbourhood of Blackheath at as high as eighty guineas which is a good deal for parents and the plate kept back too on going away but that is more a meanness than its value that he had learnt more in his first years as a commercial traveller with a large commission on the sale of an article that nobody would hear of much less buy which preceded the wine trade a long time than in the whole six years in that academy conducted by a college Bachelor, though why a Bachelor more clever than a married man I do not see and never did but pray excuse me that is not the point.’\n\nMr Dorrit stood rooted to the carpet, a statue of mystification.\n\n‘I must openly admit that I have no pretensions,’ said Flora, ‘but having known the dear little thing which under altered circumstances appears a liberty but is not so intended and Goodness knows there was no favour in half-a-crown a-day to such a needle as herself but quite the other way and as to anything lowering in it far from it the labourer is worthy of his hire and I am sure I only wish he got it oftener and more animal food and less rheumatism in the back and legs poor soul.’\n\n‘Madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, recovering his breath by a great effort, as the relict of the late Mr Finching stopped to take hers; ‘madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, very red in the face, ‘if I understand you to refer to--ha--to anything in the antecedents of--hum--a daughter of mine, involving--ha hum--daily compensation, madam, I beg to observe that the--ha--fact, assuming it--ha--to be fact, never was within my knowledge. Hum. I should not have permitted it. Ha. Never! Never!’\n\n‘Unnecessary to pursue the subject,’ returned Flora, ‘and would not have mentioned it on any account except as supposing it a favourable and only letter of introduction but as to being fact no doubt whatever and you may set your mind at rest for the very dress I have on now can prove it and sweetly made though there is no denying that it would tell better on a better figure for my own is much too fat though how to bring it down I know not, pray excuse me I am roving off again.’\n\nMr Dorrit backed to his chair in a stony way, and seated himself, as Flora gave him a softening look and played with her parasol.\n\n‘The dear little thing,’ said Flora, ‘having gone off perfectly limp and white and cold in my own house or at least papa’s for though not a freehold still a long lease at a peppercorn on the morning when Arthur--foolish habit of our youthful days and Mr Clennam far more adapted to existing circumstances particularly addressing a stranger and that stranger a gentleman in an elevated station--communicated the glad tidings imparted by a person of name of Pancks emboldens me.’\n\nAt the mention of these two names, Mr Dorrit frowned, stared, frowned again, hesitated with his fingers at his lips, as he had hesitated long ago, and said, ‘Do me the favour to--ha--state your pleasure, madam.’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ said Flora, ‘you are very kind in giving me permission and highly natural it seems to me that you should be kind for though more stately I perceive a likeness filled out of course but a likeness still, the object of my intruding is my own without the slightest consultation with any human being and most decidedly not with Arthur--pray excuse me Doyce and Clennam I don’t know what I am saying Mr Clennam solus--for to put that individual linked by a golden chain to a purple time when all was ethereal out of any anxiety would be worth to me the ransom of a monarch not that I have the least idea how much that would come to but using it as the total of all I have in the world and more.’\n\nMr Dorrit, without greatly regarding the earnestness of these latter words, repeated, ‘State your pleasure, madam.’\n\n‘It’s not likely I well know,’ said Flora, ‘but it’s possible and being possible when I had the gratification of reading in the papers that you had arrived from Italy and were going back I made up my mind to try it for you might come across him or hear something of him and if so what a blessing and relief to all!’\n\n‘Allow me to ask, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, with his ideas in wild confusion, ‘to whom--ha--TO WHOM,’ he repeated it with a raised voice in mere desperation, ‘you at present allude?’\n\n‘To the foreigner from Italy who disappeared in the City as no doubt you have read in the papers equally with myself,’ said Flora, ‘not referring to private sources by the name of Pancks from which one gathers what dreadfully ill-natured things some people are wicked enough to whisper most likely judging others by themselves and what the uneasiness and indignation of Arthur--quite unable to overcome it Doyce and Clennam--cannot fail to be.’\n\nIt happened, fortunately for the elucidation of any intelligible result, that Mr Dorrit had heard or read nothing about the matter. This caused Mrs Finching, with many apologies for being in great practical difficulties as to finding the way to her pocket among the stripes of her dress at length to produce a police handbill, setting forth that a foreign gentleman of the name of Blandois, last from Venice, had unaccountably disappeared on such a night in such a part of the city of London; that he was known to have entered such a house, at such an hour; that he was stated by the inmates of that house to have left it, about so many minutes before midnight; and that he had never been beheld since. This, with exact particulars of time and locality, and with a good detailed description of the foreign gentleman who had so mysteriously vanished, Mr Dorrit read at large.\n\n‘Blandois!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Venice! And this description! I know this gentleman. He has been in my house. He is intimately acquainted with a gentleman of good family (but in indifferent circumstances), of whom I am a--hum--patron.’\n\n‘Then my humble and pressing entreaty is the more,’ said Flora, ‘that in travelling back you will have the kindness to look for this foreign gentleman along all the roads and up and down all the turnings and to make inquiries for him at all the hotels and orange-trees and vineyards and volcanoes and places for he must be somewhere and why doesn’t he come forward and say he’s there and clear all parties up?’\n\n‘Pray, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, referring to the handbill again, ‘who is Clennam and Co.? Ha. I see the name mentioned here, in connection with the occupation of the house which Monsieur Blandois was seen to enter: who is Clennam and Co.? Is it the individual of whom I had formerly--hum--some--ha--slight transitory knowledge, and to whom I believe you have referred? Is it--ha--that person?’\n\n‘It’s a very different person indeed,’ replied Flora, ‘with no limbs and wheels instead and the grimmest of women though his mother.’\n\n‘Clennam and Co. a--hum--a mother!’ exclaimed Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘And an old man besides,’ said Flora.\n\nMr Dorrit looked as if he must immediately be driven out of his mind by this account. Neither was it rendered more favourable to sanity by Flora’s dashing into a rapid analysis of Mr Flintwinch’s cravat, and describing him, without the lightest boundary line of separation between his identity and Mrs Clennam’s, as a rusty screw in gaiters. Which compound of man and woman, no limbs, wheels, rusty screw, grimness, and gaiters, so completely stupefied Mr Dorrit, that he was a spectacle to be pitied.\n\n‘But I would not detain you one moment longer,’ said Flora, upon whom his condition wrought its effect, though she was quite unconscious of having produced it, ‘if you would have the goodness to give your promise as a gentleman that both in going back to Italy and in Italy too you would look for this Mr Blandois high and low and if you found or heard of him make him come forward for the clearing of all parties.’\n\nBy that time Mr Dorrit had so far recovered from his bewilderment, as to be able to say, in a tolerably connected manner, that he should consider that his duty. Flora was delighted with her success, and rose to take her leave.\n\n‘With a million thanks,’ said she, ‘and my address upon my card in case of anything to be communicated personally, I will not send my love to the dear little thing for it might not be acceptable, and indeed there is no dear little thing left in the transformation so why do it but both myself and Mr F.’s Aunt ever wish her well and lay no claim to any favour on our side you may be sure of that but quite the other way for what she undertook to do she did and that is more than a great many of us do, not to say anything of her doing it as well as it could be done and I myself am one of them for I have said ever since I began to recover the blow of Mr F’s death that I would learn the Organ of which I am extremely fond but of which I am ashamed to say I do not yet know a note, good evening!’\n\nWhen Mr Dorrit, who attended her to the room-door, had had a little time to collect his senses, he found that the interview had summoned back discarded reminiscences which jarred with the Merdle dinner-table. He wrote and sent off a brief note excusing himself for that day, and ordered dinner presently in his own rooms at the hotel. He had another reason for this. His time in London was very nearly out, and was anticipated by engagements; his plans were made for returning; and he thought it behoved his importance to pursue some direct inquiry into the Blandois disappearance, and be in a condition to carry back to Mr Henry Gowan the result of his own personal investigation. He therefore resolved that he would take advantage of that evening’s freedom to go down to Clennam and Co.’s, easily to be found by the direction set forth in the handbill; and see the place, and ask a question or two there himself.\n\nHaving dined as plainly as the establishment and the Courier would let him, and having taken a short sleep by the fire for his better recovery from Mrs Finching, he set out in a hackney-cabriolet alone. The deep bell of St Paul’s was striking nine as he passed under the shadow of Temple Bar, headless and forlorn in these degenerate days.\n\nAs he approached his destination through the by-streets and water-side ways, that part of London seemed to him an uglier spot at such an hour than he had ever supposed it to be. Many long years had passed since he had seen it; he had never known much of it; and it wore a mysterious and dismal aspect in his eyes. So powerfully was his imagination impressed by it, that when his driver stopped, after having asked the way more than once, and said to the best of his belief this was the gateway they wanted, Mr Dorrit stood hesitating, with the coach-door in his hand, half afraid of the dark look of the place.\n\nTruly, it looked as gloomy that night as even it had ever looked. Two of the handbills were posted on the entrance wall, one on either side, and as the lamp flickered in the night air, shadows passed over them, not unlike the shadows of fingers following the lines. A watch was evidently kept upon the place. As Mr Dorrit paused, a man passed in from over the way, and another man passed out from some dark corner within; and both looked at him in passing, and both remained standing about.\n\nAs there was only one house in the enclosure, there was no room for uncertainty, so he went up the steps of that house and knocked. There was a dim light in two windows on the first-floor. The door gave back a dreary, vacant sound, as though the house were empty; but it was not, for a light was visible, and a step was audible, almost directly. They both came to the door, and a chain grated, and a woman with her apron thrown over her face and head stood in the aperture.\n\n‘Who is it?’ said the woman.\n\nMr Dorrit, much amazed by this appearance, replied that he was from Italy, and that he wished to ask a question relative to the missing person, whom he knew.\n\n‘Hi!’ cried the woman, raising a cracked voice. ‘Jeremiah!’\n\nUpon this, a dry old man appeared, whom Mr Dorrit thought he identified by his gaiters, as the rusty screw. The woman was under apprehensions of the dry old man, for she whisked her apron away as he approached, and disclosed a pale affrighted face. ‘Open the door, you fool,’ said the old man; ‘and let the gentleman in.’\n\nMr Dorrit, not without a glance over his shoulder towards his driver and the cabriolet, walked into the dim hall. ‘Now, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch, ‘you can ask anything here you think proper; there are no secrets here, sir.’\n\nBefore a reply could be made, a strong stern voice, though a woman’s, called from above, ‘Who is it?’\n\n‘Who is it?’ returned Jeremiah. ‘More inquiries. A gentleman from Italy.’\n\n‘Bring him up here!’\n\nMr Flintwinch muttered, as if he deemed that unnecessary; but, turning to Mr Dorrit, said, ‘Mrs Clennam. She _will_ do as she likes. I’ll show you the way.’ He then preceded Mr Dorrit up the blackened staircase; that gentleman, not unnaturally looking behind him on the road, saw the woman following, with her apron thrown over her head again in her former ghastly manner.\n\nMrs Clennam had her books open on her little table. ‘Oh!’ said she abruptly, as she eyed her visitor with a steady look. ‘You are from Italy, sir, are you. Well?’\n\nMr Dorrit was at a loss for any more distinct rejoinder at the moment than ‘Ha--well?’\n\n‘Where is this missing man? Have you come to give us information where he is? I hope you have?’\n\n‘So far from it, I--hum--have come to seek information.’\n\n‘Unfortunately for us, there is none to be got here. Flintwinch, show the gentleman the handbill. Give him several to take away. Hold the light for him to read it.’\n\nMr Flintwinch did as he was directed, and Mr Dorrit read it through, as if he had not previously seen it; glad enough of the opportunity of collecting his presence of mind, which the air of the house and of the people in it had a little disturbed. While his eyes were on the paper, he felt that the eyes of Mr Flintwinch and of Mrs Clennam were on him. He found, when he looked up, that this sensation was not a fanciful one.\n\n‘Now you know as much,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘as we know, sir. Is Mr Blandois a friend of yours?’\n\n‘No--a--hum--an acquaintance,’ answered Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘You have no commission from him, perhaps?’\n\n‘I? Ha. Certainly not.’\n\nThe searching look turned gradually to the floor, after taking Mr Flintwinch’s face in its way. Mr Dorrit, discomfited by finding that he was the questioned instead of the questioner, applied himself to the reversal of that unexpected order of things.\n\n‘I am--ha--a gentleman of property, at present residing in Italy with my family, my servants, and--hum--my rather large establishment. Being in London for a short time on affairs connected with--ha--my estate, and hearing of this strange disappearance, I wished to make myself acquainted with the circumstances at first-hand, because there is--ha hum--an English gentleman in Italy whom I shall no doubt see on my return, who has been in habits of close and daily intimacy with Monsieur Blandois. Mr Henry Gowan. You may know the name.’\n\n‘Never heard of it.’\n\nMrs Clennam said it, and Mr Flintwinch echoed it.\n\n‘Wishing to--ha--make the narrative coherent and consecutive to him,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘may I ask--say, three questions?’\n\n‘Thirty, if you choose.’\n\n‘Have you known Monsieur Blandois long?’\n\n‘Not a twelvemonth. Mr Flintwinch here, will refer to the books and tell you when, and by whom at Paris he was introduced to us. If that,’ Mrs Clennam added, ‘should be any satisfaction to you. It is poor satisfaction to us.’\n\n‘Have you seen him often?’\n\n‘No. Twice. Once before, and--’\n\n‘That once,’ suggested Mr Flintwinch.\n\n‘And that once.’\n\n‘Pray, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a growing fancy upon him as he recovered his importance, that he was in some superior way in the Commission of the Peace; ‘pray, madam, may I inquire, for the greater satisfaction of the gentleman whom I have the honour to--ha--retain, or protect or let me say to--hum--know--to know--Was Monsieur Blandois here on business on the night indicated in this present sheet?’\n\n‘On what he called business,’ returned Mrs Clennam.\n\n‘Is--ha--excuse me--is its nature to be communicated?’\n‘No.’\n\nIt was evidently impracticable to pass the barrier of that reply.\n\n‘The question has been asked before,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘and the answer has been, No. We don’t choose to publish our transactions, however unimportant, to all the town. We say, No.’\n\n‘I mean, he took away no money with him, for example,’ said Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘He took away none of ours, sir, and got none here.’\n\n‘I suppose,’ observed Mr Dorrit, glancing from Mrs Clennam to Mr Flintwinch, and from Mr Flintwinch to Mrs Clennam, ‘you have no way of accounting to yourself for this mystery?’\n\n‘Why do you suppose so?’ rejoined Mrs Clennam.\n\nDisconcerted by the cold and hard inquiry, Mr Dorrit was unable to assign any reason for his supposing so.\n\n‘I account for it, sir,’ she pursued after an awkward silence on Mr Dorrit’s part, ‘by having no doubt that he is travelling somewhere, or hiding somewhere.’\n\n‘Do you know--ha--why he should hide anywhere?’\n‘No.’\n\nIt was exactly the same No as before, and put another barrier up.\n\n‘You asked me if I accounted for the disappearance to myself,’ Mrs Clennam sternly reminded him, ‘not if I accounted for it to you. I do not pretend to account for it to you, sir. I understand it to be no more my business to do that, than it is yours to require that.’\n\nMr Dorrit answered with an apologetic bend of his head. As he stepped back, preparatory to saying he had no more to ask, he could not but observe how gloomily and fixedly she sat with her eyes fastened on the ground, and a certain air upon her of resolute waiting; also, how exactly the self-same expression was reflected in Mr Flintwinch, standing at a little distance from her chair, with his eyes also on the ground, and his right hand softly rubbing his chin.\n\nAt that moment, Mistress Affery (of course, the woman with the apron) dropped the candlestick she held, and cried out, ‘There! O good Lord! there it is again. Hark, Jeremiah! Now!’\n\nIf there were any sound at all, it was so slight that she must have fallen into a confirmed habit of listening for sounds; but Mr Dorrit believed he did hear a something, like the falling of dry leaves. The woman’s terror, for a very short space, seemed to touch the three; and they all listened.\n\nMr Flintwinch was the first to stir. ‘Affery, my woman,’ said he, sidling at her with his fists clenched, and his elbows quivering with impatience to shake her, ‘you are at your old tricks. You’ll be walking in your sleep next, my woman, and playing the whole round of your distempered antics. You must have some physic. When I have shown this gentleman out, I’ll make you up such a comfortable dose, my woman; such a comfortable dose!’\n\nIt did not appear altogether comfortable in expectation to Mistress Affery; but Jeremiah, without further reference to his healing medicine, took another candle from Mrs Clennam’s table, and said, ‘Now, sir; shall I light you down?’\n\nMr Dorrit professed himself obliged, and went down. Mr Flintwinch shut him out, and chained him out, without a moment’s loss of time. He was again passed by the two men, one going out and the other coming in; got into the vehicle he had left waiting, and was driven away.\n\nBefore he had gone far, the driver stopped to let him know that he had given his name, number, and address to the two men, on their joint requisition; and also the address at which he had taken Mr Dorrit up, the hour at which he had been called from his stand and the way by which he had come. This did not make the night’s adventure run any less hotly in Mr Dorrit’s mind, either when he sat down by his fire again, or when he went to bed. All night he haunted the dismal house, saw the two people resolutely waiting, heard the woman with her apron over her face cry out about the noise, and found the body of the missing Blandois, now buried in the cellar, and now bricked up in a wall.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. A Castle in the Air\n\nManifold are the cares of wealth and state. Mr Dorrit’s satisfaction in remembering that it had not been necessary for him to announce himself to Clennam and Co., or to make an allusion to his having had any knowledge of the intrusive person of that name, had been damped over-night, while it was still fresh, by a debate that arose within him whether or no he should take the Marshalsea in his way back, and look at the old gate. He had decided not to do so; and had astonished the coachman by being very fierce with him for proposing to go over London Bridge and recross the river by Waterloo Bridge--a course which would have taken him almost within sight of his old quarters. Still, for all that, the question had raised a conflict in his breast; and, for some odd reason or no reason, he was vaguely dissatisfied. Even at the Merdle dinner-table next day, he was so out of sorts about it that he continued at intervals to turn it over and over, in a manner frightfully inconsistent with the good society surrounding him. It made him hot to think what the Chief Butler’s opinion of him would have been, if that illustrious personage could have plumbed with that heavy eye of his the stream of his meditations.\n\nThe farewell banquet was of a gorgeous nature, and wound up his visit in a most brilliant manner. Fanny combined with the attractions of her youth and beauty, a certain weight of self-sustainment as if she had been married twenty years. He felt that he could leave her with a quiet mind to tread the paths of distinction, and wished--but without abatement of patronage, and without prejudice to the retiring virtues of his favourite child--that he had such another daughter.\n\n‘My dear,’ he told her at parting, ‘our family looks to you to--ha--assert its dignity and--hum--maintain its importance. I know you will never disappoint it.’\n\n‘No, papa,’ said Fanny, ‘you may rely upon that, I think. My best love to dearest Amy, and I will write to her very soon.’\n\n‘Shall I convey any message to--ha--anybody else?’ asked Mr Dorrit, in an insinuating manner.\n\n‘Papa,’ said Fanny, before whom Mrs General instantly loomed, ‘no, I thank you. You are very kind, Pa, but I must beg to be excused. There is no other message to send, I thank you, dear papa, that it would be at all agreeable to you to take.’\n\nThey parted in an outer drawing-room, where only Mr Sparkler waited on his lady, and dutifully bided his time for shaking hands. When Mr Sparkler was admitted to this closing audience, Mr Merdle came creeping in with not much more appearance of arms in his sleeves than if he had been the twin brother of Miss Biffin, and insisted on escorting Mr Dorrit down-stairs. All Mr Dorrit’s protestations being in vain, he enjoyed the honour of being accompanied to the hall-door by this distinguished man, who (as Mr Dorrit told him in shaking hands on the step) had really overwhelmed him with attentions and services during this memorable visit. Thus they parted; Mr Dorrit entering his carriage with a swelling breast, not at all sorry that his Courier, who had come to take leave in the lower regions, should have an opportunity of beholding the grandeur of his departure.\n\nThe aforesaid grandeur was yet full upon Mr Dorrit when he alighted at his hotel. Helped out by the Courier and some half-dozen of the hotel servants, he was passing through the hall with a serene magnificence, when lo! a sight presented itself that struck him dumb and motionless. John Chivery, in his best clothes, with his tall hat under his arm, his ivory-handled cane genteelly embarrassing his deportment, and a bundle of cigars in his hand!\n\n‘Now, young man,’ said the porter. ‘This is the gentleman. This young man has persisted in waiting, sir, saying you would be glad to see him.’\n\nMr Dorrit glared on the young man, choked, and said, in the mildest of tones, ‘Ah! Young John! It is Young John, I think; is it not?’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ returned Young John.\n\n‘I--ha--thought it was Young John!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘The young man may come up,’ turning to the attendants, as he passed on: ‘oh yes, he may come up. Let Young John follow. I will speak to him above.’\n\nYoung John followed, smiling and much gratified. Mr Dorrit’s rooms were reached. Candles were lighted. The attendants withdrew.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning round upon him and seizing him by the collar when they were safely alone. ‘What do you mean by this?’\n\nThe amazement and horror depicted in the unfortunate John’s face--for he had rather expected to be embraced next--were of that powerfully expressive nature that Mr Dorrit withdrew his hand and merely glared at him.\n\n‘How dare you do this?’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘How do you presume to come here? How dare you insult me?’\n\n‘I insult you, sir?’ cried Young John. ‘Oh!’\n\n‘Yes, sir,’ returned Mr Dorrit. ‘Insult me. Your coming here is an affront, an impertinence, an audacity. You are not wanted here. Who sent you here? What--ha--the Devil do you do here?’\n\n‘I thought, sir,’ said Young John, with as pale and shocked a face as ever had been turned to Mr Dorrit’s in his life--even in his College life: ‘I thought, sir, you mightn’t object to have the goodness to accept a bundle--’\n\n‘Damn your bundle, sir!’ cried Mr Dorrit, in irrepressible rage. ‘I--hum--don’t smoke.’\n\n‘I humbly beg your pardon, sir. You used to.’\n\n‘Tell me that again,’ cried Mr Dorrit, quite beside himself, ‘and I’ll take the poker to you!’\n\nJohn Chivery backed to the door.\n\n‘Stop, sir!’ cried Mr Dorrit. ‘Stop! Sit down. Confound you sit down!’\n\nJohn Chivery dropped into the chair nearest the door, and Mr Dorrit walked up and down the room; rapidly at first; then, more slowly. Once, he went to the window, and stood there with his forehead against the glass. All of a sudden, he turned and said:\n\n‘What else did you come for, Sir?’\n\n‘Nothing else in the world, sir. Oh dear me! Only to say, Sir, that I hoped you was well, and only to ask if Miss Amy was Well?’\n\n‘What’s that to you, sir?’ retorted Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘It’s nothing to me, sir, by rights. I never thought of lessening the distance betwixt us, I am sure. I know it’s a liberty, sir, but I never thought you’d have taken it ill. Upon my word and honour, sir,’ said Young John, with emotion, ‘in my poor way, I am too proud to have come, I assure you, if I had thought so.’\n\nMr Dorrit was ashamed. He went back to the window, and leaned his forehead against the glass for some time. When he turned, he had his handkerchief in his hand, and he had been wiping his eyes with it, and he looked tired and ill.\n\n‘Young John, I am very sorry to have been hasty with you, but--ha--some remembrances are not happy remembrances, and--hum--you shouldn’t have come.’\n\n‘I feel that now, sir,’ returned John Chivery; ‘but I didn’t before, and Heaven knows I meant no harm, sir.’\n\n‘No. No,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘I am--hum--sure of that. Ha. Give me your hand, Young John, give me your hand.’\n\nYoung John gave it; but Mr Dorrit had driven his heart out of it, and nothing could change his face now, from its white, shocked look.\n\n‘There!’ said Mr Dorrit, slowly shaking hands with him. ‘Sit down again, Young John.’\n\n‘Thank you, sir--but I’d rather stand.’\n\nMr Dorrit sat down instead. After painfully holding his head a little while, he turned it to his visitor, and said, with an effort to be easy:\n\n‘And how is your father, Young John? How--ha--how are they all, Young John?’\n\n‘Thank you, sir, They’re all pretty well, sir. They’re not any ways complaining.’\n\n‘Hum. You are in your--ha--old business I see, John?’ said Mr Dorrit, with a glance at the offending bundle he had anathematised.\n\n‘Partly, sir. I am in my’--John hesitated a little--‘father’s business likewise.’\n\n‘Oh indeed!’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Do you--ha hum--go upon the ha--’\n\n‘Lock, sir? Yes, sir.’\n‘Much to do, John?’\n\n‘Yes, sir; we’re pretty heavy at present. I don’t know how it is, but we generally _are_ pretty heavy.’\n\n‘At this time of the year, Young John?’\n\n‘Mostly at all times of the year, sir. I don’t know the time that makes much difference to us. I wish you good night, sir.’\n\n‘Stay a moment, John--ha--stay a moment. Hum. Leave me the cigars, John, I--ha--beg.’\n\n‘Certainly, sir.’ John put them, with a trembling hand, on the table.\n\n‘Stay a moment, Young John; stay another moment. It would be a--ha--a gratification to me to send a little--hum--Testimonial, by such a trusty messenger, to be divided among--ha hum--them--_them_--according to their wants. Would you object to take it, John?’\n\n‘Not in any ways, sir. There’s many of them, I’m sure, that would be the better for it.’\n\n‘Thank you, John. I--ha--I’ll write it, John.’\n\nHis hand shook so that he was a long time writing it, and wrote it in a tremulous scrawl at last. It was a cheque for one hundred pounds. He folded it up, put it in Young John’s hand, and pressed the hand in his.\n\n‘I hope you’ll--ha--overlook--hum--what has passed, John.’\n\n‘Don’t speak of it, sir, on any accounts. I don’t in any ways bear malice, I’m sure.’\n\nBut nothing while John was there could change John’s face to its natural colour and expression, or restore John’s natural manner.\n\n‘And, John,’ said Mr Dorrit, giving his hand a final pressure, and releasing it, ‘I hope we--ha--agree that we have spoken together in confidence; and that you will abstain, in going out, from saying anything to any one that might--hum--suggest that--ha--once I--’\n\n‘Oh! I assure you, sir,’ returned John Chivery, ‘in my poor humble way, sir, I’m too proud and honourable to do it, sir.’\n\nMr Dorrit was not too proud and honourable to listen at the door that he might ascertain for himself whether John really went straight out, or lingered to have any talk with any one. There was no doubt that he went direct out at the door, and away down the street with a quick step. After remaining alone for an hour, Mr Dorrit rang for the Courier, who found him with his chair on the hearth-rug, sitting with his back towards him and his face to the fire. ‘You can take that bundle of cigars to smoke on the journey, if you like,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a careless wave of his hand. ‘Ha--brought by--hum--little offering from--ha--son of old tenant of mine.’\n\nNext morning’s sun saw Mr Dorrit’s equipage upon the Dover road, where every red-jacketed postilion was the sign of a cruel house, established for the unmerciful plundering of travellers. The whole business of the human race, between London and Dover, being spoliation, Mr Dorrit was waylaid at Dartford, pillaged at Gravesend, rifled at Rochester, fleeced at Sittingbourne, and sacked at Canterbury. However, it being the Courier’s business to get him out of the hands of the banditti, the Courier brought him off at every stage; and so the red-jackets went gleaming merrily along the spring landscape, rising and falling to a regular measure, between Mr Dorrit in his snug corner and the next chalky rise in the dusty highway.\n\nAnother day’s sun saw him at Calais. And having now got the Channel between himself and John Chivery, he began to feel safe, and to find that the foreign air was lighter to breathe than the air of England.\n\nOn again by the heavy French roads for Paris. Having now quite recovered his equanimity, Mr Dorrit, in his snug corner, fell to castle-building as he rode along. It was evident that he had a very large castle in hand. All day long he was running towers up, taking towers down, adding a wing here, putting on a battlement there, looking to the walls, strengthening the defences, giving ornamental touches to the interior, making in all respects a superb castle of it. His preoccupied face so clearly denoted the pursuit in which he was engaged, that every cripple at the post-houses, not blind, who shoved his little battered tin-box in at the carriage window for Charity in the name of Heaven, Charity in the name of our Lady, Charity in the name of all the Saints, knew as well what work he was at, as their countryman Le Brun could have known it himself, though he had made that English traveller the subject of a special physiognomical treatise.\n\nArrived at Paris, and resting there three days, Mr Dorrit strolled much about the streets alone, looking in at the shop-windows, and particularly the jewellers’ windows. Ultimately, he went into the most famous jeweller’s, and said he wanted to buy a little gift for a lady.\n\nIt was a charming little woman to whom he said it--a sprightly little woman, dressed in perfect taste, who came out of a green velvet bower to attend upon him, from posting up some dainty little books of account which one could hardly suppose to be ruled for the entry of any articles more commercial than kisses, at a dainty little shining desk which looked in itself like a sweetmeat.\n\nFor example, then, said the little woman, what species of gift did Monsieur desire? A love-gift?\n\nMr Dorrit smiled, and said, Eh, well! Perhaps. What did he know? It was always possible; the sex being so charming. Would she show him some?\n\nMost willingly, said the little woman. Flattered and enchanted to show him many. But pardon! To begin with, he would have the great goodness to observe that there were love-gifts, and there were nuptial gifts. For example, these ravishing ear-rings and this necklace so superb to correspond, were what one called a love-gift. These brooches and these rings, of a beauty so gracious and celestial, were what one called, with the permission of Monsieur, nuptial gifts.\n\nPerhaps it would be a good arrangement, Mr Dorrit hinted, smiling, to purchase both, and to present the love-gift first, and to finish with the nuptial offering?\n\nAh Heaven! said the little woman, laying the tips of the fingers of her two little hands against each other, that would be generous indeed, that would be a special gallantry! And without doubt the lady so crushed with gifts would find them irresistible.\n\nMr Dorrit was not sure of that. But, for example, the sprightly little woman was very sure of it, she said. So Mr Dorrit bought a gift of each sort, and paid handsomely for it. As he strolled back to his hotel afterwards, he carried his head high: having plainly got up his castle now to a much loftier altitude than the two square towers of Notre Dame.\n\nBuilding away with all his might, but reserving the plans of his castle exclusively for his own eye, Mr Dorrit posted away for Marseilles. Building on, building on, busily, busily, from morning to night. Falling asleep, and leaving great blocks of building materials dangling in the air; waking again, to resume work and get them into their places. What time the Courier in the rumble, smoking Young John’s best cigars, left a little thread of thin light smoke behind--perhaps as _he_ built a castle or two with stray pieces of Mr Dorrit’s money.\n\nNot a fortified town that they passed in all their journey was as strong, not a Cathedral summit was as high, as Mr Dorrit’s castle. Neither the Saone nor the Rhone sped with the swiftness of that peerless building; nor was the Mediterranean deeper than its foundations; nor were the distant landscapes on the Cornice road, nor the hills and bay of Genoa the Superb, more beautiful. Mr Dorrit and his matchless castle were disembarked among the dirty white houses and dirtier felons of Civita Vecchia, and thence scrambled on to Rome as they could, through the filth that festered on the way.\n\n# CHAPTER 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air\n\nThe sun had gone down full four hours, and it was later than most travellers would like it to be for finding themselves outside the walls of Rome, when Mr Dorrit’s carriage, still on its last wearisome stage, rattled over the solitary Campagna. The savage herdsmen and the fierce-looking peasants who had chequered the way while the light lasted, had all gone down with the sun, and left the wilderness blank. At some turns of the road, a pale flare on the horizon, like an exhalation from the ruin-sown land, showed that the city was yet far off; but this poor relief was rare and short-lived. The carriage dipped down again into a hollow of the black dry sea, and for a long time there was nothing visible save its petrified swell and the gloomy sky.\n\nMr Dorrit, though he had his castle-building to engage his mind, could not be quite easy in that desolate place. He was far more curious, in every swerve of the carriage, and every cry of the postilions, than he had been since he quitted London. The valet on the box evidently quaked. The Courier in the rumble was not altogether comfortable in his mind. As often as Mr Dorrit let down the glass and looked back at him (which was very often), he saw him smoking John Chivery out, it is true, but still generally standing up the while and looking about him, like a man who had his suspicions, and kept upon his guard. Then would Mr Dorrit, pulling up the glass again, reflect that those postilions were cut-throat looking fellows, and that he would have done better to have slept at Civita Vecchia, and have started betimes in the morning. But, for all this, he worked at his castle in the intervals.\n\nAnd now, fragments of ruinous enclosure, yawning window-gap and crazy wall, deserted houses, leaking wells, broken water-tanks, spectral cypress-trees, patches of tangled vine, and the changing of the track to a long, irregular, disordered lane where everything was crumbling away, from the unsightly buildings to the jolting road--now, these objects showed that they were nearing Rome. And now, a sudden twist and stoppage of the carriage inspired Mr Dorrit with the mistrust that the brigand moment was come for twisting him into a ditch and robbing him; until, letting down the glass again and looking out, he perceived himself assailed by nothing worse than a funeral procession, which came mechanically chaunting by, with an indistinct show of dirty vestments, lurid torches, swinging censers, and a great cross borne before a priest. He was an ugly priest by torchlight; of a lowering aspect, with an overhanging brow; and as his eyes met those of Mr Dorrit, looking bareheaded out of the carriage, his lips, moving as they chaunted, seemed to threaten that important traveller; likewise the action of his hand, which was in fact his manner of returning the traveller’s salutation, seemed to come in aid of that menace. So thought Mr Dorrit, made fanciful by the weariness of building and travelling, as the priest drifted past him, and the procession straggled away, taking its dead along with it. Upon their so-different way went Mr Dorrit’s company too; and soon, with their coach load of luxuries from the two great capitals of Europe, they were (like the Goths reversed) beating at the gates of Rome.\n\nMr Dorrit was not expected by his own people that night. He had been; but they had given him up until to-morrow, not doubting that it was later than he would care, in those parts, to be out. Thus, when his equipage stopped at his own gate, no one but the porter appeared to receive him. Was Miss Dorrit from home? he asked. No. She was within. Good, said Mr Dorrit to the assembling servants; let them keep where they were; let them help to unload the carriage; he would find Miss Dorrit for himself.\n\nSo he went up his grand staircase, slowly, and tired, and looked into various chambers which were empty, until he saw a light in a small ante-room. It was a curtained nook, like a tent, within two other rooms; and it looked warm and bright in colour, as he approached it through the dark avenue they made.\n\nThere was a draped doorway, but no door; and as he stopped here, looking in unseen, he felt a pang. Surely not like jealousy? For why like jealousy? There was only his daughter and his brother there: he, with his chair drawn to the hearth, enjoying the warmth of the evening wood fire; she seated at a little table, busied with some embroidery work. Allowing for the great difference in the still-life of the picture, the figures were much the same as of old; his brother being sufficiently like himself to represent himself, for a moment, in the composition. So had he sat many a night, over a coal fire far away; so had she sat, devoted to him. Yet surely there was nothing to be jealous of in the old miserable poverty. Whence, then, the pang in his heart?\n\n‘Do you know, uncle, I think you are growing young again?’\n\nHer uncle shook his head and said, ‘Since when, my dear; since when?’\n\n‘I think,’ returned Little Dorrit, plying her needle, ‘that you have been growing younger for weeks past. So cheerful, uncle, and so ready, and so interested.’\n\n‘My dear child--all you.’\n\n‘All me, uncle!’\n\n‘Yes, yes. You have done me a world of good. You have been so considerate of me, and so tender with me, and so delicate in trying to hide your attentions from me, that I--well, well, well! It’s treasured up, my darling, treasured up.’\n\n‘There is nothing in it but your own fresh fancy, uncle,’ said Little Dorrit, cheerfully.\n\n‘Well, well, well!’ murmured the old man. ‘Thank God!’\n\nShe paused for an instant in her work to look at him, and her look revived that former pain in her father’s breast; in his poor weak breast, so full of contradictions, vacillations, inconsistencies, the little peevish perplexities of this ignorant life, mists which the morning without a night only can clear away.\n\n‘I have been freer with you, you see, my dove,’ said the old man, ‘since we have been alone. I say, alone, for I don’t count Mrs General; I don’t care for her; she has nothing to do with me. But I know Fanny was impatient of me. And I don’t wonder at it, or complain of it, for I am sensible that I must be in the way, though I try to keep out of it as well as I can. I know I am not fit company for our company. My brother William,’ said the old man admiringly, ‘is fit company for monarchs; but not so your uncle, my dear. Frederick Dorrit is no credit to William Dorrit, and he knows it quite well. Ah! Why, here’s your father, Amy! My dear William, welcome back! My beloved brother, I am rejoiced to see you!’\n\n(Turning his head in speaking, he had caught sight of him as he stood in the doorway.)\n\nLittle Dorrit with a cry of pleasure put her arms about her father’s neck, and kissed him again and again. Her father was a little impatient, and a little querulous. ‘I am glad to find you at last, Amy,’ he said. ‘Ha. Really I am glad to find--hum--any one to receive me at last. I appear to have been--ha--so little expected, that upon my word I began--ha hum--to think it might be right to offer an apology for--ha--taking the liberty of coming back at all.’\n\n‘It was so late, my dear William,’ said his brother, ‘that we had given you up for to-night.’\n\n‘I am stronger than you, dear Frederick,’ returned his brother with an elaboration of fraternity in which there was severity; ‘and I hope I can travel without detriment at--ha--any hour I choose.’\n\n‘Surely, surely,’ returned the other, with a misgiving that he had given offence. ‘Surely, William.’\n\n‘Thank you, Amy,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, as she helped him to put off his wrappers. ‘I can do it without assistance. I--ha--need not trouble you, Amy. Could I have a morsel of bread and a glass of wine, or--hum--would it cause too much inconvenience?’\n\n‘Dear father, you shall have supper in a very few minutes.’\n\n‘Thank you, my love,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a reproachful frost upon him; ‘I--ha--am afraid I am causing inconvenience. Hum. Mrs General pretty well?’\n\n‘Mrs General complained of a headache, and of being fatigued; and so, when we gave you up, she went to bed, dear.’\n\nPerhaps Mr Dorrit thought that Mrs General had done well in being overcome by the disappointment of his not arriving. At any rate, his face relaxed, and he said with obvious satisfaction, ‘Extremely sorry to hear that Mrs General is not well.’\n\nDuring this short dialogue, his daughter had been observant of him, with something more than her usual interest. It would seem as though he had a changed or worn appearance in her eyes, and he perceived and resented it; for he said with renewed peevishness, when he had divested himself of his travelling-cloak, and had come to the fire:\n\n‘Amy, what are you looking at? What do you see in me that causes you to--ha--concentrate your solicitude on me in that--hum--very particular manner?’\n\n‘I did not know it, father; I beg your pardon. It gladdens my eyes to see you again; that’s all.’\n\n‘Don’t say that’s all, because--ha--that’s not all. You--hum--you think,’ said Mr Dorrit, with an accusatory emphasis, ‘that I am not looking well.’\n\n‘I thought you looked a little tired, love.’\n\n‘Then you are mistaken,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Ha, I am _not_ tired. Ha, hum. I am very much fresher than I was when I went away.’\n\nHe was so inclined to be angry that she said nothing more in her justification, but remained quietly beside him embracing his arm. As he stood thus, with his brother on the other side, he fell into a heavy doze, of not a minute’s duration, and awoke with a start.\n\n‘Frederick,’ he said, turning to his brother: ‘I recommend you to go to bed immediately.’\n\n‘No, William. I’ll wait and see you sup.’\n\n‘Frederick,’ he retorted, ‘I beg you to go to bed. I--ha--make it a personal request that you go to bed. You ought to have been in bed long ago. You are very feeble.’\n\n‘Hah!’ said the old man, who had no wish but to please him. ‘Well, well, well! I dare say I am.’\n\n‘My dear Frederick,’ returned Mr Dorrit, with an astonishing superiority to his brother’s failing powers, ‘there can be no doubt of it. It is painful to me to see you so weak. Ha. It distresses me. Hum. I don’t find you looking at all well. You are not fit for this sort of thing. You should be more careful, you should be very careful.’\n\n‘Shall I go to bed?’ asked Frederick.\n\n‘Dear Frederick,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘do, I adjure you! Good night, brother. I hope you will be stronger to-morrow. I am not at all pleased with your looks. Good night, dear fellow.’ After dismissing his brother in this gracious way, he fell into a doze again before the old man was well out of the room: and he would have stumbled forward upon the logs, but for his daughter’s restraining hold.\n\n‘Your uncle wanders very much, Amy,’ he said, when he was thus roused. ‘He is less--ha--coherent, and his conversation is more--hum--broken, than I have--ha, hum--ever known. Has he had any illness since I have been gone?’\n\n‘No, father.’\n\n‘You--ha--see a great change in him, Amy?’\n\n‘I have not observed it, dear.’\n\n‘Greatly broken,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘Greatly broken. My poor, affectionate, failing Frederick! Ha. Even taking into account what he was before, he is--hum--sadly broken!’\n\nHis supper, which was brought to him there, and spread upon the little table where he had seen her working, diverted his attention. She sat at his side as in the days that were gone, for the first time since those days ended. They were alone, and she helped him to his meat and poured out his drink for him, as she had been used to do in the prison. All this happened now, for the first time since their accession to wealth. She was afraid to look at him much, after the offence he had taken; but she noticed two occasions in the course of his meal, when he all of a sudden looked at her, and looked about him, as if the association were so strong that he needed assurance from his sense of sight that they were not in the old prison-room. Both times, he put his hand to his head as if he missed his old black cap--though it had been ignominiously given away in the Marshalsea, and had never got free to that hour, but still hovered about the yards on the head of his successor.\n\nHe took very little supper, but was a long time over it, and often reverted to his brother’s declining state. Though he expressed the greatest pity for him, he was almost bitter upon him. He said that poor Frederick--ha hum--drivelled. There was no other word to express it; drivelled. Poor fellow! It was melancholy to reflect what Amy must have undergone from the excessive tediousness of his Society--wandering and babbling on, poor dear estimable creature, wandering and babbling on--if it had not been for the relief she had had in Mrs General. Extremely sorry, he then repeated with his former satisfaction, that that--ha--superior woman was poorly.\n\nLittle Dorrit, in her watchful love, would have remembered the lightest thing he said or did that night, though she had had no subsequent reason to recall that night. She always remembered that, when he looked about him under the strong influence of the old association, he tried to keep it out of her mind, and perhaps out of his own too, by immediately expatiating on the great riches and great company that had encompassed him in his absence, and on the lofty position he and his family had to sustain. Nor did she fail to recall that there were two under-currents, side by side, pervading all his discourse and all his manner; one showing her how well he had got on without her, and how independent he was of her; the other, in a fitful and unintelligible way almost complaining of her, as if it had been possible that she had neglected him while he was away.\n\nHis telling her of the glorious state that Mr Merdle kept, and of the court that bowed before him, naturally brought him to Mrs Merdle. So naturally indeed, that although there was an unusual want of sequence in the greater part of his remarks, he passed to her at once, and asked how she was.\n\n‘She is very well. She is going away next week.’\n\n‘Home?’ asked Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘After a few weeks’ stay upon the road.’\n\n‘She will be a vast loss here,’ said Mr Dorrit. ‘A vast--ha--acquisition at home. To Fanny, and to--hum--the rest of the--ha--great world.’\n\nLittle Dorrit thought of the competition that was to be entered upon, and assented very softly.\n\n‘Mrs Merdle is going to have a great farewell Assembly, dear, and a dinner before it. She has been expressing her anxiety that you should return in time. She has invited both you and me to her dinner.’\n\n‘She is--ha--very kind. When is the day?’\n\n‘The day after to-morrow.’\n\n‘Write round in the morning, and say that I have returned, and shall--hum--be delighted.’\n\n‘May I walk with you up the stairs to your room, dear?’\n\n‘No!’ he answered, looking angrily round; for he was moving away, as if forgetful of leave-taking. ‘You may not, Amy. I want no help. I am your father, not your infirm uncle!’ He checked himself, as abruptly as he had broken into this reply, and said, ‘You have not kissed me, Amy. Good night, my dear! We must marry--ha--we must marry _you_, now.’ With that he went, more slowly and more tired, up the staircase to his rooms, and, almost as soon as he got there, dismissed his valet. His next care was to look about him for his Paris purchases, and, after opening their cases and carefully surveying them, to put them away under lock and key. After that, what with dozing and what with castle-building, he lost himself for a long time, so that there was a touch of morning on the eastward rim of the desolate Campagna when he crept to bed.\n\nMrs General sent up her compliments in good time next day, and hoped he had rested well after this fatiguing journey. He sent down his compliments, and begged to inform Mrs General that he had rested very well indeed, and was in high condition. Nevertheless, he did not come forth from his own rooms until late in the afternoon; and, although he then caused himself to be magnificently arrayed for a drive with Mrs General and his daughter, his appearance was scarcely up to his description of himself.\n\nAs the family had no visitors that day, its four members dined alone together. He conducted Mrs General to the seat at his right hand with immense ceremony; and Little Dorrit could not but notice as she followed with her uncle, both that he was again elaborately dressed, and that his manner towards Mrs General was very particular. The perfect formation of that accomplished lady’s surface rendered it difficult to displace an atom of its genteel glaze, but Little Dorrit thought she descried a slight thaw of triumph in a corner of her frosty eye.\n\nNotwithstanding what may be called in these pages the Pruney and Prismatic nature of the family banquet, Mr Dorrit several times fell asleep while it was in progress. His fits of dozing were as sudden as they had been overnight, and were as short and profound. When the first of these slumberings seized him, Mrs General looked almost amazed: but, on each recurrence of the symptoms, she told her polite beads, Papa, Potatoes, Poultry, Prunes, and Prism; and, by dint of going through that infallible performance very slowly, appeared to finish her rosary at about the same time as Mr Dorrit started from his sleep.\n\nHe was again painfully aware of a somnolent tendency in Frederick (which had no existence out of his own imagination), and after dinner, when Frederick had withdrawn, privately apologised to Mrs General for the poor man. ‘The most estimable and affectionate of brothers,’ he said, ‘but--ha, hum--broken up altogether. Unhappily, declining fast.’\n\n‘Mr Frederick, sir,’ quoth Mrs General, ‘is habitually absent and drooping, but let us hope it is not so bad as that.’\n\nMr Dorrit, however, was determined not to let him off. ‘Fast declining, madam. A wreck. A ruin. Mouldering away before our eyes. Hum. Good Frederick!’\n\n‘You left Mrs Sparkler quite well and happy, I trust?’ said Mrs General, after heaving a cool sigh for Frederick.\n\n‘Surrounded,’ replied Mr Dorrit, ‘by--ha--all that can charm the taste, and--hum--elevate the mind. Happy, my dear madam, in a--hum--husband.’\n\nMrs General was a little fluttered; seeming delicately to put the word away with her gloves, as if there were no knowing what it might lead to.\n\n‘Fanny,’ Mr Dorrit continued. ‘Fanny, Mrs General, has high qualities. Ha. Ambition--hum--purpose, consciousness of--ha--position, determination to support that position--ha, hum--grace, beauty, and native nobility.’\n\n‘No doubt,’ said Mrs General (with a little extra stiffness).\n\n‘Combined with these qualities, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘Fanny has--ha--manifested one blemish which has made me--hum--made me uneasy, and--ha--I must add, angry; but which I trust may now be considered at an end, even as to herself, and which is undoubtedly at an end as to--ha--others.’\n\n‘To what, Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, with her gloves again somewhat excited, ‘can you allude? I am at a loss to--’\n\n‘Do not say that, my dear madam,’ interrupted Mr Dorrit.\n\nMrs General’s voice, as it died away, pronounced the words, ‘at a loss to imagine.’\n\nAfter which Mr Dorrit was seized with a doze for about a minute, out of which he sprang with spasmodic nimbleness.\n\n‘I refer, Mrs General, to that--ha--strong spirit of opposition, or--hum--I might say--ha--jealousy in Fanny, which has occasionally risen against the--ha--sense I entertain of--hum--the claims of--ha--the lady with whom I have now the honour of communing.’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, ‘is ever but too obliging, ever but too appreciative. If there have been moments when I have imagined that Miss Dorrit has indeed resented the favourable opinion Mr Dorrit has formed of my services, I have found, in that only too high opinion, my consolation and recompense.’\n\n‘Opinion of your services, madam?’ said Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘Of,’ Mrs General repeated, in an elegantly impressive manner, ‘my services.’\n\n‘Of your services alone, dear madam?’ said Mr Dorrit.\n\n‘I presume,’ retorted Mrs General, in her former impressive manner, ‘of my services alone. For, to what else,’ said Mrs General, with a slightly interrogative action of her gloves, ‘could I impute--’\n\n‘To--ha--yourself, Mrs General. Ha, hum. To yourself and your merits,’ was Mr Dorrit’s rejoinder.\n\n‘Mr Dorrit will pardon me,’ said Mrs General, ‘if I remark that this is not a time or place for the pursuit of the present conversation. Mr Dorrit will excuse me if I remind him that Miss Dorrit is in the adjoining room, and is visible to myself while I utter her name. Mr Dorrit will forgive me if I observe that I am agitated, and that I find there are moments when weaknesses I supposed myself to have subdued, return with redoubled power. Mr Dorrit will allow me to withdraw.’\n\n‘Hum. Perhaps we may resume this--ha--interesting conversation,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘at another time; unless it should be, what I hope it is not--hum--in any way disagreeable to--ah--Mrs General.’\n\n‘Mr Dorrit,’ said Mrs General, casting down her eyes as she rose with a bend, ‘must ever claim my homage and obedience.’\n\nMrs General then took herself off in a stately way, and not with that amount of trepidation upon her which might have been expected in a less remarkable woman. Mr Dorrit, who had conducted his part of the dialogue with a certain majestic and admiring condescension--much as some people may be seen to conduct themselves in Church, and to perform their part in the service--appeared, on the whole, very well satisfied with himself and with Mrs General too. On the return of that lady to tea, she had touched herself up with a little powder and pomatum, and was not without moral enchantment likewise: the latter showing itself in much sweet patronage of manner towards Miss Dorrit, and in an air of as tender interest in Mr Dorrit as was consistent with rigid propriety. At the close of the evening, when she rose to retire, Mr Dorrit took her by the hand as if he were going to lead her out into the Piazza of the people to walk a minuet by moonlight, and with great solemnity conducted her to the room door, where he raised her knuckles to his lips. Having parted from her with what may be conjectured to have been a rather bony kiss of a cosmetic flavour, he gave his daughter his blessing, graciously. And having thus hinted that there was something remarkable in the wind, he again went to bed.\n\nHe remained in the seclusion of his own chamber next morning; but, early in the afternoon, sent down his best compliments to Mrs General, by Mr Tinkler, and begged she would accompany Miss Dorrit on an airing without him. His daughter was dressed for Mrs Merdle’s dinner before he appeared. He then presented himself in a refulgent condition as to his attire, but looking indefinably shrunken and old. However, as he was plainly determined to be angry with her if she so much as asked him how he was, she only ventured to kiss his cheek, before accompanying him to Mrs Merdle’s with an anxious heart.\n\nThe distance that they had to go was very short, but he was at his building work again before the carriage had half traversed it. Mrs Merdle received him with great distinction; the bosom was in admirable preservation, and on the best terms with itself; the dinner was very choice; and the company was very select.\n\nIt was principally English; saving that it comprised the usual French Count and the usual Italian Marchese--decorative social milestones, always to be found in certain places, and varying very little in appearance. The table was long, and the dinner was long; and Little Dorrit, overshadowed by a large pair of black whiskers and a large white cravat, lost sight of her father altogether, until a servant put a scrap of paper in her hand, with a whispered request from Mrs Merdle that she would read it directly. Mrs Merdle had written on it in pencil, ‘Pray come and speak to Mr Dorrit, I doubt if he is well.’\n\nShe was hurrying to him, unobserved, when he got up out of his chair, and leaning over the table called to her, supposing her to be still in her place:\n\n‘Amy, Amy, my child!’\n\nThe action was so unusual, to say nothing of his strange eager appearance and strange eager voice, that it instantaneously caused a profound silence.\n\n‘Amy, my dear,’ he repeated. ‘Will you go and see if Bob is on the lock?’\n\nShe was at his side, and touching him, but he still perversely supposed her to be in her seat, and called out, still leaning over the table, ‘Amy, Amy. I don’t feel quite myself. Ha. I don’t know what’s the matter with me. I particularly wish to see Bob. Ha. Of all the turnkeys, he’s as much my friend as yours. See if Bob is in the lodge, and beg him to come to me.’\n\nAll the guests were now in consternation, and everybody rose.\n\n‘Dear father, I am not there; I am here, by you.’\n\n‘Oh! You are here, Amy! Good. Hum. Good. Ha. Call Bob. If he has been relieved, and is not on the lock, tell Mrs Bangham to go and fetch him.’\n\nShe was gently trying to get him away; but he resisted, and would not go.\n\n‘I tell you, child,’ he said petulantly, ‘I can’t be got up the narrow stairs without Bob. Ha. Send for Bob. Hum. Send for Bob--best of all the turnkeys--send for Bob!’\n\nHe looked confusedly about him, and, becoming conscious of the number of faces by which he was surrounded, addressed them:\n\n‘Ladies and gentlemen, the duty--ha--devolves upon me of--hum--welcoming you to the Marshalsea! Welcome to the Marshalsea! The space is--ha--limited--limited--the parade might be wider; but you will find it apparently grow larger after a time--a time, ladies and gentlemen--and the air is, all things considered, very good. It blows over the--ha--Surrey hills. Blows over the Surrey hills. This is the Snuggery. Hum. Supported by a small subscription of the--ha--Collegiate body. In return for which--hot water--general kitchen--and little domestic advantages. Those who are habituated to the--ha--Marshalsea, are pleased to call me its father. I am accustomed to be complimented by strangers as the--ha--Father of the Marshalsea. Certainly, if years of residence may establish a claim to so--ha--honourable a title, I may accept the--hum--conferred distinction. My child, ladies and gentlemen. My daughter. Born here!’\n\nShe was not ashamed of it, or ashamed of him. She was pale and frightened; but she had no other care than to soothe him and get him away, for his own dear sake. She was between him and the wondering faces, turned round upon his breast with her own face raised to his. He held her clasped in his left arm, and between whiles her low voice was heard tenderly imploring him to go away with her.\n\n‘Born here,’ he repeated, shedding tears. ‘Bred here. Ladies and gentlemen, my daughter. Child of an unfortunate father, but--ha--always a gentleman. Poor, no doubt, but--hum--proud. Always proud. It has become a--hum--not infrequent custom for my--ha--personal admirers--personal admirers solely--to be pleased to express their desire to acknowledge my semi-official position here, by offering--ha--little tributes, which usually take the form of--ha--voluntary recognitions of my humble endeavours to--hum--to uphold a Tone here--a Tone--I beg it to be understood that I do not consider myself compromised. Ha. Not compromised. Ha. Not a beggar. No; I repudiate the title! At the same time far be it from me to--hum--to put upon the fine feelings by which my partial friends are actuated, the slight of scrupling to admit that those offerings are--hum--highly acceptable. On the contrary, they are most acceptable. In my child’s name, if not in my own, I make the admission in the fullest manner, at the same time reserving--ha--shall I say my personal dignity? Ladies and gentlemen, God bless you all!’\n\nBy this time, the exceeding mortification undergone by the Bosom had occasioned the withdrawal of the greater part of the company into other rooms. The few who had lingered thus long followed the rest, and Little Dorrit and her father were left to the servants and themselves. Dearest and most precious to her, he would come with her now, would he not? He replied to her fervid entreaties, that he would never be able to get up the narrow stairs without Bob; where was Bob, would nobody fetch Bob? Under pretence of looking for Bob, she got him out against the stream of gay company now pouring in for the evening assembly, and got him into a coach that had just set down its load, and got him home.\n\nThe broad stairs of his Roman palace were contracted in his failing sight to the narrow stairs of his London prison; and he would suffer no one but her to touch him, his brother excepted. They got him up to his room without help, and laid him down on his bed. And from that hour his poor maimed spirit, only remembering the place where it had broken its wings, cancelled the dream through which it had since groped, and knew of nothing beyond the Marshalsea. When he heard footsteps in the street, he took them for the old weary tread in the yards. When the hour came for locking up, he supposed all strangers to be excluded for the night. When the time for opening came again, he was so anxious to see Bob, that they were fain to patch up a narrative how that Bob--many a year dead then, gentle turnkey--had taken cold, but hoped to be out to-morrow, or the next day, or the next at furthest.\n\nHe fell away into a weakness so extreme that he could not raise his hand. But he still protected his brother according to his long usage; and would say with some complacency, fifty times a day, when he saw him standing by his bed, ‘My good Frederick, sit down. You are very feeble indeed.’\n\nThey tried him with Mrs General, but he had not the faintest knowledge of her. Some injurious suspicion lodged itself in his brain, that she wanted to supplant Mrs Bangham, and that she was given to drinking. He charged her with it in no measured terms; and was so urgent with his daughter to go round to the Marshal and entreat him to turn her out, that she was never reproduced after the first failure.\n\nSaving that he once asked ‘if Tip had gone outside?’ the remembrance of his two children not present seemed to have departed from him. But the child who had done so much for him and had been so poorly repaid, was never out of his mind. Not that he spared her, or was fearful of her being spent by watching and fatigue; he was not more troubled on that score than he had usually been. No; he loved her in his old way. They were in the jail again, and she tended him, and he had constant need of her, and could not turn without her; and he even told her, sometimes, that he was content to have undergone a great deal for her sake. As to her, she bent over his bed with her quiet face against his, and would have laid down her own life to restore him.\n\nWhen he had been sinking in this painless way for two or three days, she observed him to be troubled by the ticking of his watch--a pompous gold watch that made as great a to-do about its going as if nothing else went but itself and Time. She suffered it to run down; but he was still uneasy, and showed that was not what he wanted. At length he roused himself to explain that he wanted money to be raised on this watch. He was quite pleased when she pretended to take it away for the purpose, and afterwards had a relish for his little tastes of wine and jelly, that he had not had before.\n\nHe soon made it plain that this was so; for, in another day or two he sent off his sleeve-buttons and finger-rings. He had an amazing satisfaction in entrusting her with these errands, and appeared to consider it equivalent to making the most methodical and provident arrangements. After his trinkets, or such of them as he had been able to see about him, were gone, his clothes engaged his attention; and it is as likely as not that he was kept alive for some days by the satisfaction of sending them, piece by piece, to an imaginary pawnbroker’s.\n\nThus for ten days Little Dorrit bent over his pillow, laying her cheek against his. Sometimes she was so worn out that for a few minutes they would slumber together. Then she would awake; to recollect with fast-flowing silent tears what it was that touched her face, and to see, stealing over the cherished face upon the pillow, a deeper shadow than the shadow of the Marshalsea Wall.\n\nQuietly, quietly, all the lines of the plan of the great Castle melted one after another. Quietly, quietly, the ruled and cross-ruled countenance on which they were traced, became fair and blank. Quietly, quietly, the reflected marks of the prison bars and of the zig-zag iron on the wall-top, faded away. Quietly, quietly, the face subsided into a far younger likeness of her own than she had ever seen under the grey hair, and sank to rest.\n\nAt first her uncle was stark distracted. ‘O my brother! O William, William! You to go before me; you to go alone; you to go, and I to remain! You, so far superior, so distinguished, so noble; I, a poor useless creature fit for nothing, and whom no one would have missed!’\n\nIt did her, for the time, the good of having him to think of and to succour.\n\n‘Uncle, dear uncle, spare yourself, spare me!’\n\nThe old man was not deaf to the last words. When he did begin to restrain himself, it was that he might spare her. He had no care for himself; but, with all the remaining power of the honest heart, stunned so long and now awaking to be broken, he honoured and blessed her.\n\n‘O God,’ he cried, before they left the room, with his wrinkled hands clasped over her. ‘Thou seest this daughter of my dear dead brother! All that I have looked upon, with my half-blind and sinful eyes, Thou hast discerned clearly, brightly. Not a hair of her head shall be harmed before Thee. Thou wilt uphold her here to her last hour. And I know Thou wilt reward her hereafter!’\n\nThey remained in a dim room near, until it was almost midnight, quiet and sad together. At times his grief would seek relief in a burst like that in which it had found its earliest expression; but, besides that his little strength would soon have been unequal to such strains, he never failed to recall her words, and to reproach himself and calm himself. The only utterance with which he indulged his sorrow, was the frequent exclamation that his brother was gone, alone; that they had been together in the outset of their lives, that they had fallen into misfortune together, that they had kept together through their many years of poverty, that they had remained together to that day; and that his brother was gone alone, alone!\n\nThey parted, heavy and sorrowful. She would not consent to leave him anywhere but in his own room, and she saw him lie down in his clothes upon his bed, and covered him with her own hands. Then she sank upon her own bed, and fell into a deep sleep: the sleep of exhaustion and rest, though not of complete release from a pervading consciousness of affliction. Sleep, good Little Dorrit. Sleep through the night!\n\nIt was a moonlight night; but the moon rose late, being long past the full. When it was high in the peaceful firmament, it shone through half-closed lattice blinds into the solemn room where the stumblings and wanderings of a life had so lately ended. Two quiet figures were within the room; two figures, equally still and impassive, equally removed by an untraversable distance from the teeming earth and all that it contains, though soon to lie in it.\n\nOne figure reposed upon the bed. The other, kneeling on the floor, drooped over it; the arms easily and peacefully resting on the coverlet; the face bowed down, so that the lips touched the hand over which with its last breath it had bent. The two brothers were before their Father; far beyond the twilight judgment of this world; high above its mists and obscurities.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. Introduces the next\n\nThe passengers were landing from the packet on the pier at Calais. A low-lying place and a low-spirited place Calais was, with the tide ebbing out towards low water-mark. There had been no more water on the bar than had sufficed to float the packet in; and now the bar itself, with a shallow break of sea over it, looked like a lazy marine monster just risen to the surface, whose form was indistinctly shown as it lay asleep. The meagre lighthouse all in white, haunting the seaboard as if it were the ghost of an edifice that had once had colour and rotundity, dropped melancholy tears after its late buffeting by the waves. The long rows of gaunt black piles, slimy and wet and weather-worn, with funeral garlands of seaweed twisted about them by the late tide, might have represented an unsightly marine cemetery. Every wave-dashed, storm-beaten object, was so low and so little, under the broad grey sky, in the noise of the wind and sea, and before the curling lines of surf, making at it ferociously, that the wonder was there was any Calais left, and that its low gates and low wall and low roofs and low ditches and low sand-hills and low ramparts and flat streets, had not yielded long ago to the undermining and besieging sea, like the fortifications children make on the sea-shore.\n\nAfter slipping among oozy piles and planks, stumbling up wet steps and encountering many salt difficulties, the passengers entered on their comfortless peregrination along the pier; where all the French vagabonds and English outlaws in the town (half the population) attended to prevent their recovery from bewilderment. After being minutely inspected by all the English, and claimed and reclaimed and counter-claimed as prizes by all the French in a hand-to-hand scuffle three quarters of a mile long, they were at last free to enter the streets, and to make off in their various directions, hotly pursued.\n\nClennam, harassed by more anxieties than one, was among this devoted band. Having rescued the most defenceless of his compatriots from situations of great extremity, he now went his way alone, or as nearly alone as he could be, with a native gentleman in a suit of grease and a cap of the same material, giving chase at a distance of some fifty yards, and continually calling after him, ‘Hi! Ice-say! You! Seer! Ice-say! Nice Oatel!’\n\nEven this hospitable person, however, was left behind at last, and Clennam pursued his way, unmolested. There was a tranquil air in the town after the turbulence of the Channel and the beach, and its dulness in that comparison was agreeable. He met new groups of his countrymen, who had all a straggling air of having at one time overblown themselves, like certain uncomfortable kinds of flowers, and of being now mere weeds. They had all an air, too, of lounging out a limited round, day after day, which strongly reminded him of the Marshalsea. But, taking no further note of them than was sufficient to give birth to the reflection, he sought out a certain street and number which he kept in his mind.\n\n‘So Pancks said,’ he murmured to himself, as he stopped before a dull house answering to the address. ‘I suppose his information to be correct and his discovery, among Mr Casby’s loose papers, indisputable; but, without it, I should hardly have supposed this to be a likely place.’\n\nA dead sort of house, with a dead wall over the way and a dead gateway at the side, where a pendant bell-handle produced two dead tinkles, and a knocker produced a dead, flat, surface-tapping, that seemed not to have depth enough in it to penetrate even the cracked door. However, the door jarred open on a dead sort of spring; and he closed it behind him as he entered a dull yard, soon brought to a close by another dead wall, where an attempt had been made to train some creeping shrubs, which were dead; and to make a little fountain in a grotto, which was dry; and to decorate that with a little statue, which was gone.\n\nThe entry to the house was on the left, and it was garnished as the outer gateway was, with two printed bills in French and English, announcing Furnished Apartments to let, with immediate possession. A strong cheerful peasant woman, all stocking, petticoat, white cap, and ear-ring, stood here in a dark doorway, and said with a pleasant show of teeth, ‘Ice-say! Seer! Who?’\n\nClennam, replying in French, said the English lady; he wished to see the English lady. ‘Enter then and ascend, if you please,’ returned the peasant woman, in French likewise. He did both, and followed her up a dark bare staircase to a back room on the first-floor. Hence, there was a gloomy view of the yard that was dull, and of the shrubs that were dead, and of the fountain that was dry, and of the pedestal of the statue that was gone.\n\n‘Monsieur Blandois,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘With pleasure, Monsieur.’\n\nThereupon the woman withdrew and left him to look at the room. It was the pattern of room always to be found in such a house. Cool, dull, and dark. Waxed floor very slippery. A room not large enough to skate in; nor adapted to the easy pursuit of any other occupation. Red and white curtained windows, little straw mat, little round table with a tumultuous assemblage of legs underneath, clumsy rush-bottomed chairs, two great red velvet arm-chairs affording plenty of space to be uncomfortable in, bureau, chimney-glass in several pieces pretending to be in one piece, pair of gaudy vases of very artificial flowers; between them a Greek warrior with his helmet off, sacrificing a clock to the Genius of France.\n\nAfter some pause, a door of communication with another room was opened, and a lady entered. She manifested great surprise on seeing Clennam, and her glance went round the room in search of some one else.\n\n‘Pardon me, Miss Wade. I am alone.’\n\n‘It was not your name that was brought to me.’\n\n‘No; I know that. Excuse me. I have already had experience that my name does not predispose you to an interview; and I ventured to mention the name of one I am in search of.’\n\n‘Pray,’ she returned, motioning him to a chair so coldly that he remained standing, ‘what name was it that you gave?’\n\n‘I mentioned the name of Blandois.’\n\n‘Blandois?’\n\n‘A name you are acquainted with.’\n\n‘It is strange,’ she said, frowning, ‘that you should still press an undesired interest in me and my acquaintances, in me and my affairs, Mr Clennam. I don’t know what you mean.’\n\n‘Pardon me. You know the name?’\n\n‘What can you have to do with the name? What can I have to do with the name? What can you have to do with my knowing or not knowing any name? I know many names and I have forgotten many more. This may be in the one class, or it may be in the other, or I may never have heard it. I am acquainted with no reason for examining myself, or for being examined, about it.’\n\n‘If you will allow me,’ said Clennam, ‘I will tell you my reason for pressing the subject. I admit that I do press it, and I must beg you to forgive me if I do so, very earnestly. The reason is all mine, I do not insinuate that it is in any way yours.’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ she returned, repeating a little less haughtily than before her former invitation to him to be seated: to which he now deferred, as she seated herself. ‘I am at least glad to know that this is not another bondswoman of some friend of yours, who is bereft of free choice, and whom I have spirited away. I will hear your reason, if you please.’\n\n‘First, to identify the person of whom we speak,’ said Clennam, ‘let me observe that it is the person you met in London some time back. You will remember meeting him near the river--in the Adelphi!’\n\n‘You mix yourself most unaccountably with my business,’ she replied, looking full at him with stern displeasure. ‘How do you know that?’\n\n‘I entreat you not to take it ill. By mere accident.’\n\n‘What accident?’\n\n‘Solely the accident of coming upon you in the street and seeing the meeting.’\n\n‘Do you speak of yourself, or of some one else?’\n‘Of myself. I saw it.’\n\n‘To be sure it was in the open street,’ she observed, after a few moments of less and less angry reflection. ‘Fifty people might have seen it. It would have signified nothing if they had.’\n\n‘Nor do I make my having seen it of any moment, nor (otherwise than as an explanation of my coming here) do I connect my visit with it or the favour that I have to ask.’\n\n‘Oh! You have to ask a favour! It occurred to me,’ and the handsome face looked bitterly at him, ‘that your manner was softened, Mr Clennam.’\n\nHe was content to protest against this by a slight action without contesting it in words. He then referred to Blandois’ disappearance, of which it was probable she had heard? However probable it was to him, she had heard of no such thing. Let him look round him (she said) and judge for himself what general intelligence was likely to reach the ears of a woman who had been shut up there while it was rife, devouring her own heart. When she had uttered this denial, which he believed to be true, she asked him what he meant by disappearance? That led to his narrating the circumstances in detail, and expressing something of his anxiety to discover what had really become of the man, and to repel the dark suspicions that clouded about his mother’s house. She heard him with evident surprise, and with more marks of suppressed interest than he had seen in her; still they did not overcome her distant, proud, and self-secluded manner. When he had finished, she said nothing but these words:\n\n‘You have not yet told me, sir, what I have to do with it, or what the favour is? Will you be so good as come to that?’\n\n‘I assume,’ said Arthur, persevering, in his endeavour to soften her scornful demeanour, ‘that being in communication--may I say, confidential communication?--with this person--’\n\n‘You may say, of course, whatever you like,’ she remarked; ‘but I do not subscribe to your assumptions, Mr Clennam, or to any one’s.’\n\n‘--that being, at least in personal communication with him,’ said Clennam, changing the form of his position in the hope of making it unobjectionable, ‘you can tell me something of his antecedents, pursuits, habits, usual place of residence. Can give me some little clue by which to seek him out in the likeliest manner, and either produce him, or establish what has become of him. This is the favour I ask, and I ask it in a distress of mind for which I hope you will feel some consideration. If you should have any reason for imposing conditions upon me, I will respect it without asking what it is.’\n\n‘You chanced to see me in the street with the man,’ she observed, after being, to his mortification, evidently more occupied with her own reflections on the matter than with his appeal. ‘Then you knew the man before?’\n\n‘Not before; afterwards. I never saw him before, but I saw him again on this very night of his disappearance. In my mother’s room, in fact. I left him there. You will read in this paper all that is known of him.’\n\nHe handed her one of the printed bills, which she read with a steady and attentive face.\n\n‘This is more than _I_ knew of him,’ she said, giving it back. Clennam’s looks expressed his heavy disappointment, perhaps his incredulity; for she added in the same unsympathetic tone: ‘You don’t believe it. Still, it is so. As to personal communication: it seems that there was personal communication between him and your mother. And yet you say you believe _her_ declaration that she knows no more of him!’\n\nA sufficiently expressive hint of suspicion was conveyed in these words, and in the smile by which they were accompanied, to bring the blood into Clennam’s cheeks.\n\n‘Come, sir,’ she said, with a cruel pleasure in repeating the stab, ‘I will be as open with you as you can desire. I will confess that if I cared for my credit (which I do not), or had a good name to preserve (which I have not, for I am utterly indifferent to its being considered good or bad), I should regard myself as heavily compromised by having had anything to do with this fellow. Yet he never passed in at _my_ door--never sat in colloquy with _me_ until midnight.’\n\nShe took her revenge for her old grudge in thus turning his subject against him. Hers was not the nature to spare him, and she had no compunction.\n\n‘That he is a low, mercenary wretch; that I first saw him prowling about Italy (where I was, not long ago), and that I hired him there, as the suitable instrument of a purpose I happened to have; I have no objection to tell you. In short, it was worth my while, for my own pleasure--the gratification of a strong feeling--to pay a spy who would fetch and carry for money. I paid this creature. And I dare say that if I had wanted to make such a bargain, and if I could have paid him enough, and if he could have done it in the dark, free from all risk, he would have taken any life with as little scruple as he took my money. That, at least, is my opinion of him; and I see it is not very far removed from yours. Your mother’s opinion of him, I am to assume (following your example of assuming this and that), was vastly different.’\n\n‘My mother, let me remind you,’ said Clennam, ‘was first brought into communication with him in the unlucky course of business.’\n\n‘It appears to have been an unlucky course of business that last brought her into communication with him,’ returned Miss Wade; ‘and business hours on that occasion were late.’\n\n‘You imply,’ said Arthur, smarting under these cool-handed thrusts, of which he had deeply felt the force already, ‘that there was something--’\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ she composedly interrupted, ‘recollect that I do not speak by implication about the man. He is, I say again without disguise, a low mercenary wretch. I suppose such a creature goes where there is occasion for him. If I had not had occasion for him, you would not have seen him and me together.’\n\nWrung by her persistence in keeping that dark side of the case before him, of which there was a half-hidden shadow in his own breast, Clennam was silent.\n\n‘I have spoken of him as still living,’ she added, ‘but he may have been put out of the way for anything I know. For anything I care, also. I have no further occasion for him.’\n\nWith a heavy sigh and a despondent air, Arthur Clennam slowly rose. She did not rise also, but said, having looked at him in the meanwhile with a fixed look of suspicion, and lips angrily compressed:\n\n‘He was the chosen associate of your dear friend, Mr Gowan, was he not? Why don’t you ask your dear friend to help you?’\n\nThe denial that he was a dear friend rose to Arthur’s lips; but he repressed it, remembering his old struggles and resolutions, and said:\n\n‘Further than that he has never seen Blandois since Blandois set out for England, Mr Gowan knows nothing additional about him. He was a chance acquaintance, made abroad.’\n\n‘A chance acquaintance made abroad!’ she repeated. ‘Yes. Your dear friend has need to divert himself with all the acquaintances he can make, seeing what a wife he has. I hate his wife, sir.’\n\nThe anger with which she said it, the more remarkable for being so much under her restraint, fixed Clennam’s attention, and kept him on the spot. It flashed out of her dark eyes as they regarded him, quivered in her nostrils, and fired the very breath she exhaled; but her face was otherwise composed into a disdainful serenity; and her attitude was as calmly and haughtily graceful as if she had been in a mood of complete indifference.\n\n‘All I will say is, Miss Wade,’ he remarked, ‘that you can have received no provocation to a feeling in which I believe you have no sharer.’\n\n‘You may ask your dear friend, if you choose,’ she returned, ‘for his opinion upon that subject.’\n\n‘I am scarcely on those intimate terms with my dear friend,’ said Arthur, in spite of his resolutions, ‘that would render my approaching the subject very probable, Miss Wade.’\n\n‘I hate him,’ she returned. ‘Worse than his wife, because I was once dupe enough, and false enough to myself, almost to love him. You have seen me, sir, only on common-place occasions, when I dare say you have thought me a common-place woman, a little more self-willed than the generality. You don’t know what I mean by hating, if you know me no better than that; you can’t know, without knowing with what care I have studied myself and people about me. For this reason I have for some time inclined to tell you what my life has been--not to propitiate your opinion, for I set no value on it; but that you may comprehend, when you think of your dear friend and his dear wife, what I mean by hating. Shall I give you something I have written and put by for your perusal, or shall I hold my hand?’\n\nArthur begged her to give it to him. She went to the bureau, unlocked it, and took from an inner drawer a few folded sheets of paper. Without any conciliation of him, scarcely addressing him, rather speaking as if she were speaking to her own looking-glass for the justification of her own stubbornness, she said, as she gave them to him:\n\n‘Now you may know what I mean by hating! No more of that. Sir, whether you find me temporarily and cheaply lodging in an empty London house, or in a Calais apartment, you find Harriet with me. You may like to see her before you leave. Harriet, come in!’ She called Harriet again. The second call produced Harriet, once Tattycoram.\n\n‘Here is Mr Clennam,’ said Miss Wade; ‘not come for you; he has given you up,--I suppose you have, by this time?’\n\n‘Having no authority, or influence--yes,’ assented Clennam.\n\n‘Not come in search of you, you see; but still seeking some one. He wants that Blandois man.’\n\n‘With whom I saw you in the Strand in London,’ hinted Arthur.\n\n‘If you know anything of him, Harriet, except that he came from Venice--which we all know--tell it to Mr Clennam freely.’\n\n‘I know nothing more about him,’ said the girl.\n\n‘Are you satisfied?’ Miss Wade inquired of Arthur.\n\nHe had no reason to disbelieve them; the girl’s manner being so natural as to be almost convincing, if he had had any previous doubts. He replied, ‘I must seek for intelligence elsewhere.’\n\nHe was not going in the same breath; but he had risen before the girl entered, and she evidently thought he was. She looked quickly at him, and said:\n\n‘Are they well, sir?’\n‘Who?’\n\nShe stopped herself in saying what would have been ‘all of them;’ glanced at Miss Wade; and said ‘Mr and Mrs Meagles.’\n\n‘They were, when I last heard of them. They are not at home. By the way, let me ask you. Is it true that you were seen there?’\n\n‘Where? Where does any one say I was seen?’ returned the girl, sullenly casting down her eyes.\n\n‘Looking in at the garden gate of the cottage.’\n\n‘No,’ said Miss Wade. ‘She has never been near it.’\n\n‘You are wrong, then,’ said the girl. ‘I went down there the last time we were in London. I went one afternoon when you left me alone. And I did look in.’\n\n‘You poor-spirited girl,’ returned Miss Wade with infinite contempt; ‘does all our companionship, do all our conversations, do all your old complainings, tell for so little as that?’\n\n‘There was no harm in looking in at the gate for an instant,’ said the girl. ‘I saw by the windows that the family were not there.’\n\n‘Why should you go near the place?’\n\n‘Because I wanted to see it. Because I felt that I should like to look at it again.’\n\nAs each of the two handsome faces looked at the other, Clennam felt how each of the two natures must be constantly tearing the other to pieces.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Miss Wade, coldly subduing and removing her glance; ‘if you had any desire to see the place where you led the life from which I rescued you because you had found out what it was, that is another thing. But is that your truth to me? Is that your fidelity to me? Is that the common cause I make with you? You are not worth the confidence I have placed in you. You are not worth the favour I have shown you. You are no higher than a spaniel, and had better go back to the people who did worse than whip you.’\n\n‘If you speak so of them with any one else by to hear, you’ll provoke me to take their part,’ said the girl.\n\n‘Go back to them,’ Miss Wade retorted. ‘Go back to them.’\n\n‘You know very well,’ retorted Harriet in her turn, ‘that I won’t go back to them. You know very well that I have thrown them off, and never can, never shall, never will, go back to them. Let them alone, then, Miss Wade.’\n\n‘You prefer their plenty to your less fat living here,’ she rejoined. ‘You exalt them, and slight me. What else should I have expected? I ought to have known it.’\n\n‘It’s not so,’ said the girl, flushing high, ‘and you don’t say what you mean. I know what you mean. You are reproaching me, underhanded, with having nobody but you to look to. And because I have nobody but you to look to, you think you are to make me do, or not do, everything you please, and are to put any affront upon me. You are as bad as they were, every bit. But I will not be quite tamed, and made submissive. I will say again that I went to look at the house, because I had often thought that I should like to see it once more. I will ask again how they are, because I once liked them and at times thought they were kind to me.’\n\nHereupon Clennam said that he was sure they would still receive her kindly, if she should ever desire to return.\n\n‘Never!’ said the girl passionately. ‘I shall never do that. Nobody knows that better than Miss Wade, though she taunts me because she has made me her dependent. And I know I am so; and I know she is overjoyed when she can bring it to my mind.’\n\n‘A good pretence!’ said Miss Wade, with no less anger, haughtiness, and bitterness; ‘but too threadbare to cover what I plainly see in this. My poverty will not bear competition with their money. Better go back at once, better go back at once, and have done with it!’\n\nArthur Clennam looked at them, standing a little distance asunder in the dull confined room, each proudly cherishing her own anger; each, with a fixed determination, torturing her own breast, and torturing the other’s. He said a word or two of leave-taking; but Miss Wade barely inclined her head, and Harriet, with the assumed humiliation of an abject dependent and serf (but not without defiance for all that), made as if she were too low to notice or to be noticed.\n\nHe came down the dark winding stairs into the yard with an increased sense upon him of the gloom of the wall that was dead, and of the shrubs that were dead, and of the fountain that was dry, and of the statue that was gone. Pondering much on what he had seen and heard in that house, as well as on the failure of all his efforts to trace the suspicious character who was lost, he returned to London and to England by the packet that had taken him over. On the way he unfolded the sheets of paper, and read in them what is reproduced in the next chapter.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor\n\nI have the misfortune of not being a fool. From a very early age I have detected what those about me thought they hid from me. If I could have been habitually imposed upon, instead of habitually discerning the truth, I might have lived as smoothly as most fools do.\n\nMy childhood was passed with a grandmother; that is to say, with a lady who represented that relative to me, and who took that title on herself. She had no claim to it, but I--being to that extent a little fool--had no suspicion of her. She had some children of her own family in her house, and some children of other people. All girls; ten in number, including me. We all lived together and were educated together.\n\nI must have been about twelve years old when I began to see how determinedly those girls patronised me. I was told I was an orphan. There was no other orphan among us; and I perceived (here was the first disadvantage of not being a fool) that they conciliated me in an insolent pity, and in a sense of superiority. I did not set this down as a discovery, rashly. I tried them often. I could hardly make them quarrel with me. When I succeeded with any of them, they were sure to come after an hour or two, and begin a reconciliation. I tried them over and over again, and I never knew them wait for me to begin. They were always forgiving me, in their vanity and condescension. Little images of grown people!\n\nOne of them was my chosen friend. I loved that stupid mite in a passionate way that she could no more deserve than I can remember without feeling ashamed of, though I was but a child. She had what they called an amiable temper, an affectionate temper. She could distribute, and did distribute pretty looks and smiles to every one among them. I believe there was not a soul in the place, except myself, who knew that she did it purposely to wound and gall me!\n\nNevertheless, I so loved that unworthy girl that my life was made stormy by my fondness for her. I was constantly lectured and disgraced for what was called ‘trying her;’ in other words charging her with her little perfidy and throwing her into tears by showing her that I read her heart. However, I loved her faithfully; and one time I went home with her for the holidays.\n\nShe was worse at home than she had been at school. She had a crowd of cousins and acquaintances, and we had dances at her house, and went out to dances at other houses, and, both at home and out, she tormented my love beyond endurance. Her plan was, to make them all fond of her--and so drive me wild with jealousy. To be familiar and endearing with them all--and so make me mad with envying them. When we were left alone in our bedroom at night, I would reproach her with my perfect knowledge of her baseness; and then she would cry and cry and say I was cruel, and then I would hold her in my arms till morning: loving her as much as ever, and often feeling as if, rather than suffer so, I could so hold her in my arms and plunge to the bottom of a river--where I would still hold her after we were both dead.\n\nIt came to an end, and I was relieved. In the family there was an aunt who was not fond of me. I doubt if any of the family liked me much; but I never wanted them to like me, being altogether bound up in the one girl. The aunt was a young woman, and she had a serious way with her eyes of watching me. She was an audacious woman, and openly looked compassionately at me. After one of the nights that I have spoken of, I came down into a greenhouse before breakfast. Charlotte (the name of my false young friend) had gone down before me, and I heard this aunt speaking to her about me as I entered. I stopped where I was, among the leaves, and listened.\n\nThe aunt said, ‘Charlotte, Miss Wade is wearing you to death, and this must not continue.’ I repeat the very words I heard.\n\nNow, what did she answer? Did she say, ‘It is I who am wearing her to death, I who am keeping her on a rack and am the executioner, yet she tells me every night that she loves me devotedly, though she knows what I make her undergo?’ No; my first memorable experience was true to what I knew her to be, and to all my experience. She began sobbing and weeping (to secure the aunt’s sympathy to herself), and said, ‘Dear aunt, she has an unhappy temper; other girls at school, besides I, try hard to make it better; we all try hard.’\n\nUpon that the aunt fondled her, as if she had said something noble instead of despicable and false, and kept up the infamous pretence by replying, ‘But there are reasonable limits, my dear love, to everything, and I see that this poor miserable girl causes you more constant and useless distress than even so good an effort justifies.’\n\nThe poor miserable girl came out of her concealment, as you may be prepared to hear, and said, ‘Send me home.’ I never said another word to either of them, or to any of them, but ‘Send me home, or I will walk home alone, night and day!’ When I got home, I told my supposed grandmother that, unless I was sent away to finish my education somewhere else before that girl came back, or before any one of them came back, I would burn my sight away by throwing myself into the fire, rather than I would endure to look at their plotting faces.\n\nI went among young women next, and I found them no better. Fair words and fair pretences; but I penetrated below those assertions of themselves and depreciations of me, and they were no better. Before I left them, I learned that I had no grandmother and no recognised relation. I carried the light of that information both into my past and into my future. It showed me many new occasions on which people triumphed over me, when they made a pretence of treating me with consideration, or doing me a service.\n\nA man of business had a small property in trust for me. I was to be a governess; I became a governess; and went into the family of a poor nobleman, where there were two daughters--little children, but the parents wished them to grow up, if possible, under one instructress. The mother was young and pretty. From the first, she made a show of behaving to me with great delicacy. I kept my resentment to myself; but I knew very well that it was her way of petting the knowledge that she was my Mistress, and might have behaved differently to her servant if it had been her fancy.\n\nI say I did not resent it, nor did I; but I showed her, by not gratifying her, that I understood her. When she pressed me to take wine, I took water. If there happened to be anything choice at table, she always sent it to me: but I always declined it, and ate of the rejected dishes. These disappointments of her patronage were a sharp retort, and made me feel independent.\n\nI liked the children. They were timid, but on the whole disposed to attach themselves to me. There was a nurse, however, in the house, a rosy-faced woman always making an obtrusive pretence of being gay and good-humoured, who had nursed them both, and who had secured their affections before I saw them. I could almost have settled down to my fate but for this woman. Her artful devices for keeping herself before the children in constant competition with me, might have blinded many in my place; but I saw through them from the first. On the pretext of arranging my rooms and waiting on me and taking care of my wardrobe (all of which she did busily), she was never absent. The most crafty of her many subtleties was her feint of seeking to make the children fonder of me. She would lead them to me and coax them to me. ‘Come to good Miss Wade, come to dear Miss Wade, come to pretty Miss Wade. She loves you very much. Miss Wade is a clever lady, who has read heaps of books, and can tell you far better and more interesting stories than I know. Come and hear Miss Wade!’ How could I engage their attentions, when my heart was burning against these ignorant designs? How could I wonder, when I saw their innocent faces shrinking away, and their arms twining round her neck, instead of mine? Then she would look up at me, shaking their curls from her face, and say, ‘They’ll come round soon, Miss Wade; they’re very simple and loving, ma’am; don’t be at all cast down about it, ma’am’--exulting over me!\n\nThere was another thing the woman did. At times, when she saw that she had safely plunged me into a black despondent brooding by these means, she would call the attention of the children to it, and would show them the difference between herself and me. ‘Hush! Poor Miss Wade is not well. Don’t make a noise, my dears, her head aches. Come and comfort her. Come and ask her if she is better; come and ask her to lie down. I hope you have nothing on your mind, ma’am. Don’t take on, ma’am, and be sorry!’\n\nIt became intolerable. Her ladyship, my Mistress, coming in one day when I was alone, and at the height of feeling that I could support it no longer, I told her I must go. I could not bear the presence of that woman Dawes.\n\n‘Miss Wade! Poor Dawes is devoted to you; would do anything for you!’\n\nI knew beforehand she would say so; I was quite prepared for it; I only answered, it was not for me to contradict my Mistress; I must go.\n\n‘I hope, Miss Wade,’ she returned, instantly assuming the tone of superiority she had always so thinly concealed, ‘that nothing I have ever said or done since we have been together, has justified your use of that disagreeable word, “Mistress.” It must have been wholly inadvertent on my part. Pray tell me what it is.’\n\nI replied that I had no complaint to make, either of my Mistress or to my Mistress; but I must go.\n\nShe hesitated a moment, and then sat down beside me, and laid her hand on mine. As if that honour would obliterate any remembrance!\n\n‘Miss Wade, I fear you are unhappy, through causes over which I have no influence.’\n\nI smiled, thinking of the experience the word awakened, and said, ‘I have an unhappy temper, I suppose.’\n\n‘I did not say that.’\n\n‘It is an easy way of accounting for anything,’ said I.\n\n‘It may be; but I did not say so. What I wish to approach is something very different. My husband and I have exchanged some remarks upon the subject, when we have observed with pain that you have not been easy with us.’\n\n‘Easy? Oh! You are such great people, my lady,’ said I.\n\n‘I am unfortunate in using a word which may convey a meaning--and evidently does--quite opposite to my intention.’ (She had not expected my reply, and it shamed her.) ‘I only mean, not happy with us. It is a difficult topic to enter on; but, from one young woman to another, perhaps--in short, we have been apprehensive that you may allow some family circumstances of which no one can be more innocent than yourself, to prey upon your spirits. If so, let us entreat you not to make them a cause of grief. My husband himself, as is well known, formerly had a very dear sister who was not in law his sister, but who was universally beloved and respected--’\n\nI saw directly that they had taken me in for the sake of the dead woman, whoever she was, and to have that boast of me and advantage of me; I saw, in the nurse’s knowledge of it, an encouragement to goad me as she had done; and I saw, in the children’s shrinking away, a vague impression, that I was not like other people. I left that house that night.\n\nAfter one or two short and very similar experiences, which are not to the present purpose, I entered another family where I had but one pupil: a girl of fifteen, who was the only daughter. The parents here were elderly people: people of station, and rich. A nephew whom they had brought up was a frequent visitor at the house, among many other visitors; and he began to pay me attention. I was resolute in repulsing him; for I had determined when I went there, that no one should pity me or condescend to me. But he wrote me a letter. It led to our being engaged to be married.\n\nHe was a year younger than I, and young-looking even when that allowance was made. He was on absence from India, where he had a post that was soon to grow into a very good one. In six months we were to be married, and were to go to India. I was to stay in the house, and was to be married from the house. Nobody objected to any part of the plan.\n\nI cannot avoid saying he admired me; but, if I could, I would. Vanity has nothing to do with the declaration, for his admiration worried me. He took no pains to hide it; and caused me to feel among the rich people as if he had bought me for my looks, and made a show of his purchase to justify himself. They appraised me in their own minds, I saw, and were curious to ascertain what my full value was. I resolved that they should not know. I was immovable and silent before them; and would have suffered any one of them to kill me sooner than I would have laid myself out to bespeak their approval.\n\nHe told me I did not do myself justice. I told him I did, and it was because I did and meant to do so to the last, that I would not stoop to propitiate any of them. He was concerned and even shocked, when I added that I wished he would not parade his attachment before them; but he said he would sacrifice even the honest impulses of his affection to my peace.\n\nUnder that pretence he began to retort upon me. By the hour together, he would keep at a distance from me, talking to any one rather than to me. I have sat alone and unnoticed, half an evening, while he conversed with his young cousin, my pupil. I have seen all the while, in people’s eyes, that they thought the two looked nearer on an equality than he and I. I have sat, divining their thoughts, until I have felt that his young appearance made me ridiculous, and have raged against myself for ever loving him.\n\nFor I did love him once. Undeserving as he was, and little as he thought of all these agonies that it cost me--agonies which should have made him wholly and gratefully mine to his life’s end--I loved him. I bore with his cousin’s praising him to my face, and with her pretending to think that it pleased me, but full well knowing that it rankled in my breast; for his sake. While I have sat in his presence recalling all my slights and wrongs, and deliberating whether I should not fly from the house at once and never see him again--I have loved him.\n\nHis aunt (my Mistress you will please to remember) deliberately, wilfully, added to my trials and vexations. It was her delight to expatiate on the style in which we were to live in India, and on the establishment we should keep, and the company we should entertain when he got his advancement. My pride rose against this barefaced way of pointing out the contrast my married life was to present to my then dependent and inferior position. I suppressed my indignation; but I showed her that her intention was not lost upon me, and I repaid her annoyance by affecting humility. What she described would surely be a great deal too much honour for me, I would tell her. I was afraid I might not be able to support so great a change. Think of a mere governess, her daughter’s governess, coming to that high distinction! It made her uneasy, and made them all uneasy, when I answered in this way. They knew that I fully understood her.\n\nIt was at the time when my troubles were at their highest, and when I was most incensed against my lover for his ingratitude in caring as little as he did for the innumerable distresses and mortifications I underwent on his account, that your dear friend, Mr Gowan, appeared at the house. He had been intimate there for a long time, but had been abroad. He understood the state of things at a glance, and he understood me.\n\nHe was the first person I had ever seen in my life who had understood me. He was not in the house three times before I knew that he accompanied every movement of my mind. In his coldly easy way with all of them, and with me, and with the whole subject, I saw it clearly. In his light protestations of admiration of my future husband, in his enthusiasm regarding our engagement and our prospects, in his hopeful congratulations on our future wealth and his despondent references to his own poverty--all equally hollow, and jesting, and full of mockery--I saw it clearly. He made me feel more and more resentful, and more and more contemptible, by always presenting to me everything that surrounded me with some new hateful light upon it, while he pretended to exhibit it in its best aspect for my admiration and his own. He was like the dressed-up Death in the Dutch series; whatever figure he took upon his arm, whether it was youth or age, beauty or ugliness, whether he danced with it, sang with it, played with it, or prayed with it, he made it ghastly.\n\nYou will understand, then, that when your dear friend complimented me, he really condoled with me; that when he soothed me under my vexations, he laid bare every smarting wound I had; that when he declared my ‘faithful swain’ to be ‘the most loving young fellow in the world, with the tenderest heart that ever beat,’ he touched my old misgiving that I was made ridiculous. These were not great services, you may say. They were acceptable to me, because they echoed my own mind, and confirmed my own knowledge. I soon began to like the society of your dear friend better than any other.\n\nWhen I perceived (which I did, almost as soon) that jealousy was growing out of this, I liked this society still better. Had I not been subject to jealousy, and were the endurances to be all mine? No. Let him know what it was! I was delighted that he should know it; I was delighted that he should feel keenly, and I hoped he did. More than that. He was tame in comparison with Mr Gowan, who knew how to address me on equal terms, and how to anatomise the wretched people around us.\n\nThis went on, until the aunt, my Mistress, took it upon herself to speak to me. It was scarcely worth alluding to; she knew I meant nothing; but she suggested from herself, knowing it was only necessary to suggest, that it might be better if I were a little less companionable with Mr Gowan.\n\nI asked her how she could answer for what I meant? She could always answer, she replied, for my meaning nothing wrong. I thanked her, but said I would prefer to answer for myself and to myself. Her other servants would probably be grateful for good characters, but I wanted none.\n\nOther conversation followed, and induced me to ask her how she knew that it was only necessary for her to make a suggestion to me, to have it obeyed? Did she presume on my birth, or on my hire? I was not bought, body and soul. She seemed to think that her distinguished nephew had gone into a slave-market and purchased a wife.\n\nIt would probably have come, sooner or later, to the end to which it did come, but she brought it to its issue at once. She told me, with assumed commiseration, that I had an unhappy temper. On this repetition of the old wicked injury, I withheld no longer, but exposed to her all I had known of her and seen in her, and all I had undergone within myself since I had occupied the despicable position of being engaged to her nephew. I told her that Mr Gowan was the only relief I had had in my degradation; that I had borne it too long, and that I shook it off too late; but that I would see none of them more. And I never did.\n\nYour dear friend followed me to my retreat, and was very droll on the severance of the connection; though he was sorry, too, for the excellent people (in their way the best he had ever met), and deplored the necessity of breaking mere house-flies on the wheel. He protested before long, and far more truly than I then supposed, that he was not worth acceptance by a woman of such endowments, and such power of character; but--well, well--!\n\nYour dear friend amused me and amused himself as long as it suited his inclinations; and then reminded me that we were both people of the world, that we both understood mankind, that we both knew there was no such thing as romance, that we were both prepared for going different ways to seek our fortunes like people of sense, and that we both foresaw that whenever we encountered one another again we should meet as the best friends on earth. So he said, and I did not contradict him.\n\nIt was not very long before I found that he was courting his present wife, and that she had been taken away to be out of his reach. I hated her then, quite as much as I hate her now; and naturally, therefore, could desire nothing better than that she should marry him. But I was restlessly curious to look at her--so curious that I felt it to be one of the few sources of entertainment left to me. I travelled a little: travelled until I found myself in her society, and in yours. Your dear friend, I think, was not known to you then, and had not given you any of those signal marks of his friendship which he has bestowed upon you.\n\nIn that company I found a girl, in various circumstances of whose position there was a singular likeness to my own, and in whose character I was interested and pleased to see much of the rising against swollen patronage and selfishness, calling themselves kindness, protection, benevolence, and other fine names, which I have described as inherent in my nature. I often heard it said, too, that she had ‘an unhappy temper.’ Well understanding what was meant by the convenient phrase, and wanting a companion with a knowledge of what I knew, I thought I would try to release the girl from her bondage and sense of injustice. I have no occasion to relate that I succeeded.\n\nWe have been together ever since, sharing my small means.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. Who passes by this Road so late?\n\nArthur Clennam had made his unavailing expedition to Calais in the midst of a great pressure of business. A certain barbaric Power with valuable possessions on the map of the world, had occasion for the services of one or two engineers, quick in invention and determined in execution: practical men, who could make the men and means their ingenuity perceived to be wanted out of the best materials they could find at hand; and who were as bold and fertile in the adaptation of such materials to their purpose, as in the conception of their purpose itself. This Power, being a barbaric one, had no idea of stowing away a great national object in a Circumlocution Office, as strong wine is hidden from the light in a cellar until its fire and youth are gone, and the labourers who worked in the vineyard and pressed the grapes are dust. With characteristic ignorance, it acted on the most decided and energetic notions of How to do it; and never showed the least respect for, or gave any quarter to, the great political science, How not to do it. Indeed it had a barbarous way of striking the latter art and mystery dead, in the person of any enlightened subject who practised it.\n\nAccordingly, the men who were wanted were sought out and found; which was in itself a most uncivilised and irregular way of proceeding. Being found, they were treated with great confidence and honour (which again showed dense political ignorance), and were invited to come at once and do what they had to do. In short, they were regarded as men who meant to do it, engaging with other men who meant it to be done.\n\nDaniel Doyce was one of the chosen. There was no foreseeing at that time whether he would be absent months or years. The preparations for his departure, and the conscientious arrangement for him of all the details and results of their joint business, had necessitated labour within a short compass of time, which had occupied Clennam day and night. He had slipped across the water in his first leisure, and had slipped as quickly back again for his farewell interview with Doyce.\n\nHim Arthur now showed, with pains and care, the state of their gains and losses, responsibilities and prospects. Daniel went through it all in his patient manner, and admired it all exceedingly. He audited the accounts, as if they were a far more ingenious piece of mechanism than he had ever constructed, and afterwards stood looking at them, weighing his hat over his head by the brims, as if he were absorbed in the contemplation of some wonderful engine.\n\n‘It’s all beautiful, Clennam, in its regularity and order. Nothing can be plainer. Nothing can be better.’\n\n‘I am glad you approve, Doyce. Now, as to the management of your capital while you are away, and as to the conversion of so much of it as the business may need from time to time--’ His partner stopped him.\n\n‘As to that, and as to everything else of that kind, all rests with you. You will continue in all such matters to act for both of us, as you have done hitherto, and to lighten my mind of a load it is much relieved from.’\n\n‘Though, as I often tell you,’ returned Clennam, ‘you unreasonably depreciate your business qualities.’\n\n‘Perhaps so,’ said Doyce, smiling. ‘And perhaps not. Anyhow, I have a calling that I have studied more than such matters, and that I am better fitted for. I have perfect confidence in my partner, and I am satisfied that he will do what is best. If I have a prejudice connected with money and money figures,’ continued Doyce, laying that plastic workman’s thumb of his on the lapel of his partner’s coat, ‘it is against speculating. I don’t think I have any other. I dare say I entertain that prejudice, only because I have never given my mind fully to the subject.’\n\n‘But you shouldn’t call it a prejudice,’ said Clennam. ‘My dear Doyce, it is the soundest sense.’\n\n‘I am glad you think so,’ returned Doyce, with his grey eye looking kind and bright.\n\n‘It so happens,’ said Clennam, ‘that just now, not half an hour before you came down, I was saying the same thing to Pancks, who looked in here. We both agreed that to travel out of safe investments is one of the most dangerous, as it is one of the most common, of those follies which often deserve the name of vices.’\n\n‘Pancks?’ said Doyce, tilting up his hat at the back, and nodding with an air of confidence. ‘Aye, aye, aye! That’s a cautious fellow.’\n\n‘He is a very cautious fellow indeed,’ returned Arthur. ‘Quite a specimen of caution.’\n\nThey both appeared to derive a larger amount of satisfaction from the cautious character of Mr Pancks, than was quite intelligible, judged by the surface of their conversation.\n\n‘And now,’ said Daniel, looking at his watch, ‘as time and tide wait for no man, my trusty partner, and as I am ready for starting, bag and baggage, at the gate below, let me say a last word. I want you to grant a request of mine.’\n\n‘Any request you can make--Except,’ Clennam was quick with his exception, for his partner’s face was quick in suggesting it, ‘except that I will abandon your invention.’\n\n‘That’s the request, and you know it is,’ said Doyce.\n\n‘I say, No, then. I say positively, No. Now that I have begun, I will have some definite reason, some responsible statement, something in the nature of a real answer, from those people.’\n\n‘You will not,’ returned Doyce, shaking his head. ‘Take my word for it, you never will.’\n\n‘At least, I’ll try,’ said Clennam. ‘It will do me no harm to try.’\n\n‘I am not certain of that,’ rejoined Doyce, laying his hand persuasively on his shoulder. ‘It has done me harm, my friend. It has aged me, tired me, vexed me, disappointed me. It does no man any good to have his patience worn out, and to think himself ill-used. I fancy, even already, that unavailing attendance on delays and evasions has made you something less elastic than you used to be.’\n\n‘Private anxieties may have done that for the moment,’ said Clennam, ‘but not official harrying. Not yet. I am not hurt yet.’\n\n‘Then you won’t grant my request?’\n\n‘Decidedly, No,’ said Clennam. ‘I should be ashamed if I submitted to be so soon driven out of the field, where a much older and a much more sensitively interested man contended with fortitude so long.’\n\nAs there was no moving him, Daniel Doyce returned the grasp of his hand, and, casting a farewell look round the counting-house, went down-stairs with him. Doyce was to go to Southampton to join the small staff of his fellow-travellers; and a coach was at the gate, well furnished and packed, and ready to take him there. The workmen were at the gate to see him off, and were mightily proud of him. ‘Good luck to you, Mr Doyce!’ said one of the number. ‘Wherever you go, they’ll find as they’ve got a man among ‘em, a man as knows his tools and as his tools knows, a man as is willing and a man as is able, and if that’s not a man, where is a man!’ This oration from a gruff volunteer in the back-ground, not previously suspected of any powers in that way, was received with three loud cheers; and the speaker became a distinguished character for ever afterwards. In the midst of the three loud cheers, Daniel gave them all a hearty ‘Good Bye, Men!’ and the coach disappeared from sight, as if the concussion of the air had blown it out of Bleeding Heart Yard.\n\nMr Baptist, as a grateful little fellow in a position of trust, was among the workmen, and had done as much towards the cheering as a mere foreigner could. In truth, no men on earth can cheer like Englishmen, who do so rally one another’s blood and spirit when they cheer in earnest, that the stir is like the rush of their whole history, with all its standards waving at once, from Saxon Alfred’s downwards. Mr Baptist had been in a manner whirled away before the onset, and was taking his breath in quite a scared condition when Clennam beckoned him to follow up-stairs, and return the books and papers to their places.\n\nIn the lull consequent on the departure--in that first vacuity which ensues on every separation, foreshadowing the great separation that is always overhanging all mankind--Arthur stood at his desk, looking dreamily out at a gleam of sun. But his liberated attention soon reverted to the theme that was foremost in his thoughts, and began, for the hundredth time, to dwell upon every circumstance that had impressed itself upon his mind on the mysterious night when he had seen the man at his mother’s. Again the man jostled him in the crooked street, again he followed the man and lost him, again he came upon the man in the court-yard looking at the house, again he followed the man and stood beside him on the door-steps.\n\n‘Who passes by this road so late? Compagnon de la Majolaine; Who passes by this road so late? Always gay!’\n\nIt was not the first time, by many, that he had recalled the song of the child’s game, of which the fellow had hummed this verse while they stood side by side; but he was so unconscious of having repeated it audibly, that he started to hear the next verse.\n\n‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine; Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’\n\nCavalletto had deferentially suggested the words and tune, supposing him to have stopped short for want of more.\n\n‘Ah! You know the song, Cavalletto?’\n\n‘By Bacchus, yes, sir! They all know it in France. I have heard it many times, sung by the little children. The last time when it I have heard,’ said Mr Baptist, formerly Cavalletto, who usually went back to his native construction of sentences when his memory went near home, ‘is from a sweet little voice. A little voice, very pretty, very innocent. Altro!’\n\n‘The last time I heard it,’ returned Arthur, ‘was in a voice quite the reverse of pretty, and quite the reverse of innocent.’ He said it more to himself than to his companion, and added to himself, repeating the man’s next words. ‘Death of my life, sir, it’s my character to be impatient!’\n\n‘EH!’ cried Cavalletto, astounded, and with all his colour gone in a moment.\n\n‘What is the matter?’\n\n‘Sir! You know where I have heard that song the last time?’\n\nWith his rapid native action, his hands made the outline of a high hook nose, pushed his eyes near together, dishevelled his hair, puffed out his upper lip to represent a thick moustache, and threw the heavy end of an ideal cloak over his shoulder. While doing this, with a swiftness incredible to one who has not watched an Italian peasant, he indicated a very remarkable and sinister smile. The whole change passed over him like a flash of light, and he stood in the same instant, pale and astonished, before his patron.\n\n‘In the name of Fate and wonder,’ said Clennam, ‘what do you mean? Do you know a man of the name of Blandois?’\n\n‘No!’ said Mr Baptist, shaking his head.\n\n‘You have just now described a man who was by when you heard that song; have you not?’\n\n‘Yes!’ said Mr Baptist, nodding fifty times.\n\n‘And was he not called Blandois?’\n\n‘No!’ said Mr Baptist. ‘Altro, Altro, Altro, Altro!’ He could not reject the name sufficiently, with his head and his right forefinger going at once.\n\n‘Stay!’ cried Clennam, spreading out the handbill on his desk. ‘Was this the man? You can understand what I read aloud?’\n\n‘Altogether. Perfectly.’\n\n‘But look at it, too. Come here and look over me, while I read.’\n\nMr Baptist approached, followed every word with his quick eyes, saw and heard it all out with the greatest impatience, then clapped his two hands flat upon the bill as if he had fiercely caught some noxious creature, and cried, looking eagerly at Clennam, ‘It is the man! Behold him!’\n\n‘This is of far greater moment to me’ said Clennam, in great agitation, ‘than you can imagine. Tell me where you knew the man.’\n\nMr Baptist, releasing the paper very slowly and with much discomfiture, and drawing himself back two or three paces, and making as though he dusted his hands, returned, very much against his will:\n\n‘At Marsiglia--Marseilles.’\n\n‘What was he?’\n\n‘A prisoner, and--Altro! I believe yes!--an,’ Mr Baptist crept closer again to whisper it, ‘Assassin!’\n\nClennam fell back as if the word had struck him a blow: so terrible did it make his mother’s communication with the man appear. Cavalletto dropped on one knee, and implored him, with a redundancy of gesticulation, to hear what had brought himself into such foul company.\n\nHe told with perfect truth how it had come of a little contraband trading, and how he had in time been released from prison, and how he had gone away from those antecedents. How, at the house of entertainment called the Break of Day at Chalons on the Saone, he had been awakened in his bed at night by the same assassin, then assuming the name of Lagnier, though his name had formerly been Rigaud; how the assassin had proposed that they should join their fortunes together; how he held the assassin in such dread and aversion that he had fled from him at daylight, and how he had ever since been haunted by the fear of seeing the assassin again and being claimed by him as an acquaintance. When he had related this, with an emphasis and poise on the word, ‘assassin,’ peculiarly belonging to his own language, and which did not serve to render it less terrible to Clennam, he suddenly sprang to his feet, pounced upon the bill again, and with a vehemence that would have been absolute madness in any man of Northern origin, cried ‘Behold the same assassin! Here he is!’\n\nIn his passionate raptures, he at first forgot the fact that he had lately seen the assassin in London. On his remembering it, it suggested hope to Clennam that the recognition might be of later date than the night of the visit at his mother’s; but Cavalletto was too exact and clear about time and place, to leave any opening for doubt that it had preceded that occasion.\n\n‘Listen,’ said Arthur, very seriously. ‘This man, as we have read here, has wholly disappeared.’\n\n‘Of it I am well content!’ said Cavalletto, raising his eyes piously. ‘A thousand thanks to Heaven! Accursed assassin!’\n\n‘Not so,’ returned Clennam; ‘for until something more is heard of him, I can never know an hour’s peace.’\n\n‘Enough, Benefactor; that is quite another thing. A million of excuses!’\n\n‘Now, Cavalletto,’ said Clennam, gently turning him by the arm, so that they looked into each other’s eyes. ‘I am certain that for the little I have been able to do for you, you are the most sincerely grateful of men.’\n\n‘I swear it!’ cried the other.\n\n‘I know it. If you could find this man, or discover what has become of him, or gain any later intelligence whatever of him, you would render me a service above any other service I could receive in the world, and would make me (with far greater reason) as grateful to you as you are to me.’\n\n‘I know not where to look,’ cried the little man, kissing Arthur’s hand in a transport. ‘I know not where to begin. I know not where to go. But, courage! Enough! It matters not! I go, in this instant of time!’\n\n‘Not a word to any one but me, Cavalletto.’\n\n‘Al-tro!’ cried Cavalletto. And was gone with great speed.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams\n\nLeft alone, with the expressive looks and gestures of Mr Baptist, otherwise Giovanni Baptista Cavalletto, vividly before him, Clennam entered on a weary day. It was in vain that he tried to control his attention by directing it to any business occupation or train of thought; it rode at anchor by the haunting topic, and would hold to no other idea. As though a criminal should be chained in a stationary boat on a deep clear river, condemned, whatever countless leagues of water flowed past him, always to see the body of the fellow-creature he had drowned lying at the bottom, immovable, and unchangeable, except as the eddies made it broad or long, now expanding, now contracting its terrible lineaments; so Arthur, below the shifting current of transparent thoughts and fancies which were gone and succeeded by others as soon as come, saw, steady and dark, and not to be stirred from its place, the one subject that he endeavoured with all his might to rid himself of, and that he could not fly from.\n\nThe assurance he now had, that Blandois, whatever his right name, was one of the worst of characters, greatly augmented the burden of his anxieties. Though the disappearance should be accounted for to-morrow, the fact that his mother had been in communication with such a man, would remain unalterable. That the communication had been of a secret kind, and that she had been submissive to him and afraid of him, he hoped might be known to no one beyond himself; yet, knowing it, how could he separate it from his old vague fears, and how believe that there was nothing evil in such relations?\n\nHer resolution not to enter on the question with him, and his knowledge of her indomitable character, enhanced his sense of helplessness. It was like the oppression of a dream to believe that shame and exposure were impending over her and his father’s memory, and to be shut out, as by a brazen wall, from the possibility of coming to their aid. The purpose he had brought home to his native country, and had ever since kept in view, was, with her greatest determination, defeated by his mother herself, at the time of all others when he feared that it pressed most. His advice, energy, activity, money, credit, all his resources whatsoever, were all made useless. If she had been possessed of the old fabled influence, and had turned those who looked upon her into stone, she could not have rendered him more completely powerless (so it seemed to him in his distress of mind) than she did, when she turned her unyielding face to his in her gloomy room.\n\nBut the light of that day’s discovery, shining on these considerations, roused him to take a more decided course of action. Confident in the rectitude of his purpose, and impelled by a sense of overhanging danger closing in around, he resolved, if his mother would still admit of no approach, to make a desperate appeal to Affery. If she could be brought to become communicative, and to do what lay in her to break the spell of secrecy that enshrouded the house, he might shake off the paralysis of which every hour that passed over his head made him more acutely sensible. This was the result of his day’s anxiety, and this was the decision he put in practice when the day closed in.\n\nHis first disappointment, on arriving at the house, was to find the door open, and Mr Flintwinch smoking a pipe on the steps. If circumstances had been commonly favourable, Mistress Affery would have opened the door to his knock. Circumstances being uncommonly unfavourable, the door stood open, and Mr Flintwinch was smoking his pipe on the steps.\n\n‘Good evening,’ said Arthur.\n\n‘Good evening,’ said Mr Flintwinch.\n\nThe smoke came crookedly out of Mr Flintwinch’s mouth, as if it circulated through the whole of his wry figure and came back by his wry throat, before coming forth to mingle with the smoke from the crooked chimneys and the mists from the crooked river.\n\n‘Have you any news?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘We have no news,’ said Jeremiah.\n\n‘I mean of the foreign man,’ Arthur explained.\n\n‘_I_ mean of the foreign man,’ said Jeremiah.\n\nHe looked so grim, as he stood askew, with the knot of his cravat under his ear, that the thought passed into Clennam’s mind, and not for the first time by many, could Flintwinch for a purpose of his own have got rid of Blandois? Could it have been his secret, and his safety, that were at issue? He was small and bent, and perhaps not actively strong; yet he was as tough as an old yew-tree, and as crusty as an old jackdaw. Such a man, coming behind a much younger and more vigorous man, and having the will to put an end to him and no relenting, might do it pretty surely in that solitary place at a late hour.\n\nWhile, in the morbid condition of his thoughts, these thoughts drifted over the main one that was always in Clennam’s mind, Mr Flintwinch, regarding the opposite house over the gateway with his neck twisted and one eye shut up, stood smoking with a vicious expression upon him; more as if he were trying to bite off the stem of his pipe, than as if he were enjoying it. Yet he was enjoying it in his own way.\n\n‘You’ll be able to take my likeness, the next time you call, Arthur, I should think,’ said Mr Flintwinch, drily, as he stooped to knock the ashes out.\n\nRather conscious and confused, Arthur asked his pardon, if he had stared at him unpolitely. ‘But my mind runs so much upon this matter,’ he said, ‘that I lose myself.’\n\n‘Hah! Yet I don’t see,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, quite at his leisure, ‘why it should trouble _you_, Arthur.’\n‘No?’\n\n‘No,’ said Mr Flintwinch, very shortly and decidedly: much as if he were of the canine race, and snapped at Arthur’s hand.\n\n‘Is it nothing to see those placards about? Is it nothing to me to see my mother’s name and residence hawked up and down in such an association?’\n\n‘I don’t see,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, scraping his horny cheek, ‘that it need signify much to you. But I’ll tell you what I do see, Arthur,’ glancing up at the windows; ‘I see the light of fire and candle in your mother’s room!’\n\n‘And what has that to do with it?’\n\n‘Why, sir, I read by it,’ said Mr Flintwinch, screwing himself at him, ‘that if it’s advisable (as the proverb says it is) to let sleeping dogs lie, it’s just as advisable, perhaps, to let missing dogs lie. Let ‘em be. They generally turn up soon enough.’\n\nMr Flintwinch turned short round when he had made this remark, and went into the dark hall. Clennam stood there, following him with his eyes, as he dipped for a light in the phosphorus-box in the little room at the side, got one after three or four dips, and lighted the dim lamp against the wall. All the while, Clennam was pursuing the probabilities--rather as if they were being shown to him by an invisible hand than as if he himself were conjuring them up--of Mr Flintwinch’s ways and means of doing that darker deed, and removing its traces by any of the black avenues of shadow that lay around them.\n\n‘Now, sir,’ said the testy Jeremiah; ‘will it be agreeable to walk up-stairs?’\n\n‘My mother is alone, I suppose?’\n\n‘Not alone,’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘Mr Casby and his daughter are with her. They came in while I was smoking, and I stayed behind to have my smoke out.’\n\nThis was the second disappointment. Arthur made no remark upon it, and repaired to his mother’s room, where Mr Casby and Flora had been taking tea, anchovy paste, and hot buttered toast. The relics of those delicacies were not yet removed, either from the table or from the scorched countenance of Affery, who, with the kitchen toasting-fork still in her hand, looked like a sort of allegorical personage; except that she had a considerable advantage over the general run of such personages in point of significant emblematical purpose.\n\nFlora had spread her bonnet and shawl upon the bed, with a care indicative of an intention to stay some time. Mr Casby, too, was beaming near the hob, with his benevolent knobs shining as if the warm butter of the toast were exuding through the patriarchal skull, and with his face as ruddy as if the colouring matter of the anchovy paste were mantling in the patriarchal visage. Seeing this, as he exchanged the usual salutations, Clennam decided to speak to his mother without postponement.\n\nIt had long been customary, as she never changed her room, for those who had anything to say to her apart, to wheel her to her desk; where she sat, usually with the back of her chair turned towards the rest of the room, and the person who talked with her seated in a corner, on a stool which was always set in that place for that purpose. Except that it was long since the mother and son had spoken together without the intervention of a third person, it was an ordinary matter of course within the experience of visitors for Mrs Clennam to be asked, with a word of apology for the interruption, if she could be spoken with on a matter of business, and, on her replying in the affirmative, to be wheeled into the position described.\n\nTherefore, when Arthur now made such an apology, and such a request, and moved her to her desk and seated himself on the stool, Mrs Finching merely began to talk louder and faster, as a delicate hint that she could overhear nothing, and Mr Casby stroked his long white locks with sleepy calmness.\n\n‘Mother, I have heard something to-day which I feel persuaded you don’t know, and which I think you should know, of the antecedents of that man I saw here.’\n\n‘I know nothing of the antecedents of the man you saw here, Arthur.’\n\nShe spoke aloud. He had lowered his own voice; but she rejected that advance towards confidence as she rejected every other, and spoke in her usual key and in her usual stern voice.\n\n‘I have received it on no circuitous information; it has come to me direct.’\n\nShe asked him, exactly as before, if he were there to tell her what it was?\n\n‘I thought it right that you should know it.’\n\n‘And what is it?’\n\n‘He has been a prisoner in a French gaol.’\n\nShe answered with composure, ‘I should think that very likely.’\n\n‘But in a gaol for criminals, mother. On an accusation of murder.’\n\nShe started at the word, and her looks expressed her natural horror. Yet she still spoke aloud, when she demanded:--\n\n‘Who told you so?’\n\n‘A man who was his fellow-prisoner.’\n\n‘That man’s antecedents, I suppose, were not known to you, before he told you?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘Though the man himself was?’\n\n‘My case and Flintwinch’s, in respect of this other man! I dare say the resemblance is not so exact, though, as that your informant became known to you through a letter from a correspondent with whom he had deposited money? How does that part of the parallel stand?’\n\nArthur had no choice but to say that his informant had not become known to him through the agency of any such credentials, or indeed of any credentials at all. Mrs Clennam’s attentive frown expanded by degrees into a severe look of triumph, and she retorted with emphasis, ‘Take care how you judge others, then. I say to you, Arthur, for your good, take care how you judge!’\n\nHer emphasis had been derived from her eyes quite as much as from the stress she laid upon her words. She continued to look at him; and if, when he entered the house, he had had any latent hope of prevailing in the least with her, she now looked it out of his heart.\n\n‘Mother, shall I do nothing to assist you?’\n\n‘Nothing.’\n\n‘Will you entrust me with no confidence, no charge, no explanation? Will you take no counsel with me? Will you not let me come near you?’\n\n‘How can you ask me? You separated yourself from my affairs. It was not my act; it was yours. How can you consistently ask me such a question? You know that you left me to Flintwinch, and that he occupies your place.’\n\nGlancing at Jeremiah, Clennam saw in his very gaiters that his attention was closely directed to them, though he stood leaning against the wall scraping his jaw, and pretended to listen to Flora as she held forth in a most distracting manner on a chaos of subjects, in which mackerel, and Mr F.’s Aunt in a swing, had become entangled with cockchafers and the wine trade.\n\n‘A prisoner, in a French gaol, on an accusation of murder,’ repeated Mrs Clennam, steadily going over what her son had said. ‘That is all you know of him from the fellow-prisoner?’\n\n‘In substance, all.’\n\n‘And was the fellow-prisoner his accomplice and a murderer, too? But, of course, he gives a better account of himself than of his friend; it is needless to ask. This will supply the rest of them here with something new to talk about. Casby, Arthur tells me--’\n\n‘Stay, mother! Stay, stay!’ He interrupted her hastily, for it had not entered his imagination that she would openly proclaim what he had told her.\n\n‘What now?’ she said with displeasure. ‘What more?’\n\n‘I beg you to excuse me, Mr Casby--and you, too, Mrs Finching--for one other moment with my mother--’\n\nHe had laid his hand upon her chair, or she would otherwise have wheeled it round with the touch of her foot upon the ground. They were still face to face. She looked at him, as he ran over the possibilities of some result he had not intended, and could not foresee, being influenced by Cavalletto’s disclosure becoming a matter of notoriety, and hurriedly arrived at the conclusion that it had best not be talked about; though perhaps he was guided by no more distinct reason than that he had taken it for granted that his mother would reserve it to herself and her partner.\n\n‘What now?’ she said again, impatiently. ‘What is it?’\n\n‘I did not mean, mother, that you should repeat what I have communicated. I think you had better not repeat it.’\n\n‘Do you make that a condition with me?’\n\n‘Well! Yes.’\n\n‘Observe, then! It is you who make this a secret,’ said she, holding up her hand, ‘and not I. It is you, Arthur, who bring here doubts and suspicions and entreaties for explanations, and it is you, Arthur, who bring secrets here. What is it to me, do you think, where the man has been, or what he has been? What can it be to me? The whole world may know it, if they care to know it; it is nothing to me. Now, let me go.’\n\nHe yielded to her imperious but elated look, and turned her chair back to the place from which he had wheeled it. In doing so he saw elation in the face of Mr Flintwinch, which most assuredly was not inspired by Flora. This turning of his intelligence and of his whole attempt and design against himself, did even more than his mother’s fixedness and firmness to convince him that his efforts with her were idle. Nothing remained but the appeal to his old friend Affery.\n\nBut even to get the very doubtful and preliminary stage of making the appeal, seemed one of the least promising of human undertakings. She was so completely under the thrall of the two clever ones, was so systematically kept in sight by one or other of them, and was so afraid to go about the house besides, that every opportunity of speaking to her alone appeared to be forestalled. Over and above that, Mistress Affery, by some means (it was not very difficult to guess, through the sharp arguments of her liege lord), had acquired such a lively conviction of the hazard of saying anything under any circumstances, that she had remained all this time in a corner guarding herself from approach with that symbolical instrument of hers; so that, when a word or two had been addressed to her by Flora, or even by the bottle-green patriarch himself, she had warded off conversation with the toasting-fork like a dumb woman.\n\nAfter several abortive attempts to get Affery to look at him while she cleared the table and washed the tea-service, Arthur thought of an expedient which Flora might originate. To whom he therefore whispered, ‘Could you say you would like to go through the house?’\n\nNow, poor Flora, being always in fluctuating expectation of the time when Clennam would renew his boyhood and be madly in love with her again, received the whisper with the utmost delight; not only as rendered precious by its mysterious character, but as preparing the way for a tender interview in which he would declare the state of his affections. She immediately began to work out the hint.\n\n‘Ah dear me the poor old room,’ said Flora, glancing round, ‘looks just as ever Mrs Clennam I am touched to see except for being smokier which was to be expected with time and which we must all expect and reconcile ourselves to being whether we like it or not as I am sure I have had to do myself if not exactly smokier dreadfully stouter which is the same or worse, to think of the days when papa used to bring me here the least of girls a perfect mass of chilblains to be stuck upon a chair with my feet on the rails and stare at Arthur--pray excuse me--Mr Clennam--the least of boys in the frightfullest of frills and jackets ere yet Mr F. appeared a misty shadow on the horizon paying attentions like the well-known spectre of some place in Germany beginning with a B is a moral lesson inculcating that all the paths in life are similar to the paths down in the North of England where they get the coals and make the iron and things gravelled with ashes!’\n\nHaving paid the tribute of a sigh to the instability of human existence, Flora hurried on with her purpose.\n\n‘Not that at any time,’ she proceeded, ‘its worst enemy could have said it was a cheerful house for that it was never made to be but always highly impressive, fond memory recalls an occasion in youth ere yet the judgment was mature when Arthur--confirmed habit--Mr Clennam--took me down into an unused kitchen eminent for mouldiness and proposed to secrete me there for life and feed me on what he could hide from his meals when he was not at home for the holidays and on dry bread in disgrace which at that halcyon period too frequently occurred, would it be inconvenient or asking too much to beg to be permitted to revive those scenes and walk through the house?’\n\nMrs Clennam, who responded with a constrained grace to Mrs Finching’s good nature in being there at all, though her visit (before Arthur’s unexpected arrival) was undoubtedly an act of pure good nature and no self-gratification, intimated that all the house was open to her. Flora rose and looked to Arthur for his escort. ‘Certainly,’ said he, aloud; ‘and Affery will light us, I dare say.’\n\nAffery was excusing herself with ‘Don’t ask nothing of me, Arthur!’ when Mr Flintwinch stopped her with ‘Why not? Affery, what’s the matter with you, woman? Why not, jade!’ Thus expostulated with, she came unwillingly out of her corner, resigned the toasting-fork into one of her husband’s hands, and took the candlestick he offered from the other.\n\n‘Go before, you fool!’ said Jeremiah. ‘Are you going up, or down, Mrs Finching?’\n\nFlora answered, ‘Down.’\n\n‘Then go before, and down, you Affery,’ said Jeremiah. ‘And do it properly, or I’ll come rolling down the banisters, and tumbling over you!’\n\nAffery headed the exploring party; Jeremiah closed it. He had no intention of leaving them. Clennam looking back, and seeing him following three stairs behind, in the coolest and most methodical manner exclaimed in a low voice, ‘Is there no getting rid of him!’ Flora reassured his mind by replying promptly, ‘Why though not exactly proper Arthur and a thing I couldn’t think of before a younger man or a stranger still I don’t mind him if you so particularly wish it and provided you’ll have the goodness not to take me too tight.’\n\nWanting the heart to explain that this was not at all what he meant, Arthur extended his supporting arm round Flora’s figure. ‘Oh my goodness me,’ said she. ‘You are very obedient indeed really and it’s extremely honourable and gentlemanly in you I am sure but still at the same time if you would like to be a little tighter than that I shouldn’t consider it intruding.’\n\nIn this preposterous attitude, unspeakably at variance with his anxious mind, Clennam descended to the basement of the house; finding that wherever it became darker than elsewhere, Flora became heavier, and that when the house was lightest she was too. Returning from the dismal kitchen regions, which were as dreary as they could be, Mistress Affery passed with the light into his father’s old room, and then into the old dining-room; always passing on before like a phantom that was not to be overtaken, and neither turning nor answering when he whispered, ‘Affery! I want to speak to you!’\n\nIn the dining-room, a sentimental desire came over Flora to look into the dragon closet which had so often swallowed Arthur in the days of his boyhood--not improbably because, as a very dark closet, it was a likely place to be heavy in. Arthur, fast subsiding into despair, had opened it, when a knock was heard at the outer door.\n\nMistress Affery, with a suppressed cry, threw her apron over her head.\n\n‘What? You want another dose!’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘You shall have it, my woman, you shall have a good one! Oh! You shall have a sneezer, you shall have a teaser!’\n\n‘In the meantime is anybody going to the door?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘In the meantime, _I_ am going to the door, sir,’ returned the old man so savagely, as to render it clear that in a choice of difficulties he felt he must go, though he would have preferred not to go. ‘Stay here the while, all! Affery, my woman, move an inch, or speak a word in your foolishness, and I’ll treble your dose!’\n\nThe moment he was gone, Arthur released Mrs Finching: with some difficulty, by reason of that lady misunderstanding his intentions, and making arrangements with a view to tightening instead of slackening.\n\n‘Affery, speak to me now!’\n\n‘Don’t touch me, Arthur!’ she cried, shrinking from him. ‘Don’t come near me. He’ll see you. Jeremiah will. Don’t.’\n\n‘He can’t see me,’ returned Arthur, suiting the action to the word, ‘if I blow the candle out.’\n\n‘He’ll hear you,’ cried Affery.\n\n‘He can’t hear me,’ returned Arthur, suiting the action to the words again, ‘if I draw you into this black closet, and speak here. Why do you hide your face?’\n\n‘Because I am afraid of seeing something.’\n\n‘You can’t be afraid of seeing anything in this darkness, Affery.’\n\n‘Yes I am. Much more than if it was light.’\n\n‘Why are you afraid?’\n\n‘Because the house is full of mysteries and secrets; because it’s full of whisperings and counsellings; because it’s full of noises. There never was such a house for noises. I shall die of ‘em, if Jeremiah don’t strangle me first. As I expect he will.’\n\n‘I have never heard any noises here, worth speaking of.’\n\n‘Ah! But you would, though, if you lived in the house, and was obliged to go about it as I am,’ said Affery; ‘and you’d feel that they was so well worth speaking of, that you’d feel you was nigh bursting through not being allowed to speak of ‘em. Here’s Jeremiah! You’ll get me killed.’\n\n‘My good Affery, I solemnly declare to you that I can see the light of the open door on the pavement of the hall, and so could you if you would uncover your face and look.’\n\n‘I durstn’t do it,’ said Affery, ‘I durstn’t never, Arthur. I’m always blind-folded when Jeremiah an’t a looking, and sometimes even when he is.’\n\n‘He cannot shut the door without my seeing him,’ said Arthur. ‘You are as safe with me as if he was fifty miles away.’\n\n[‘I wish he was!’ cried Affery.)\n\n‘Affery, I want to know what is amiss here; I want some light thrown on the secrets of this house.’\n\n‘I tell you, Arthur,’ she interrupted, ‘noises is the secrets, rustlings and stealings about, tremblings, treads overhead and treads underneath.’\n\n‘But those are not all the secrets.’\n\n‘I don’t know,’ said Affery. ‘Don’t ask me no more. Your old sweetheart an’t far off, and she’s a blabber.’\n\nHis old sweetheart, being in fact so near at hand that she was then reclining against him in a flutter, a very substantial angle of forty-five degrees, here interposed to assure Mistress Affery with greater earnestness than directness of asseveration, that what she heard should go no further, but should be kept inviolate, ‘if on no other account on Arthur’s--sensible of intruding in being too familiar Doyce and Clennam’s.’\n\n‘I make an imploring appeal to you, Affery, to you, one of the few agreeable early remembrances I have, for my mother’s sake, for your husband’s sake, for my own, for all our sakes. I am sure you can tell me something connected with the coming here of this man, if you will.’\n\n‘Why, then I’ll tell you, Arthur,’ returned Affery--‘Jeremiah’s coming!’\n\n‘No, indeed he is not. The door is open, and he is standing outside, talking.’\n\n‘I’ll tell you then,’ said Affery, after listening, ‘that the first time he ever come he heard the noises his own self. “What’s that?” he said to me. “I don’t know what it is,” I says to him, catching hold of him, “but I have heard it over and over again.” While I says it, he stands a looking at me, all of a shake, he do.’\n\n‘Has he been here often?’\n\n‘Only that night, and the last night.’\n\n‘What did you see of him on the last night, after I was gone?’\n\n‘Them two clever ones had him all alone to themselves. Jeremiah come a dancing at me sideways, after I had let you out (he always comes a dancing at me sideways when he’s going to hurt me), and he said to me, “Now, Affery,” he said, “I am a coming behind you, my woman, and a going to run you up.” So he took and squeezed the back of my neck in his hand, till it made me open my mouth, and then he pushed me before him to bed, squeezing all the way. That’s what he calls running me up, he do. Oh, he’s a wicked one!’\n\n‘And did you hear or see no more, Affery?’\n\n‘Don’t I tell you I was sent to bed, Arthur! Here he is!’\n\n‘I assure you he is still at the door. Those whisperings and counsellings, Affery, that you have spoken of. What are they?’\n\n‘How should I know? Don’t ask me nothing about ‘em, Arthur. Get away!’\n\n‘But my dear Affery; unless I can gain some insight into these hidden things, in spite of your husband and in spite of my mother, ruin will come of it.’\n\n‘Don’t ask me nothing,’ repeated Affery. ‘I have been in a dream for ever so long. Go away, go away!’\n\n‘You said that before,’ returned Arthur. ‘You used the same expression that night, at the door, when I asked you what was going on here. What do you mean by being in a dream?’\n\n‘I an’t a going to tell you. Get away! I shouldn’t tell you, if you was by yourself; much less with your old sweetheart here.’\n\nIt was equally vain for Arthur to entreat, and for Flora to protest. Affery, who had been trembling and struggling the whole time, turned a deaf ear to all adjuration, and was bent on forcing herself out of the closet.\n\n‘I’d sooner scream to Jeremiah than say another word! I’ll call out to him, Arthur, if you don’t give over speaking to me. Now here’s the very last word I’ll say afore I call to him--If ever you begin to get the better of them two clever ones your own self (you ought to it, as I told you when you first come home, for you haven’t been a living here long years, to be made afeared of your life as I have), then do you get the better of ‘em afore my face; and then do you say to me, Affery tell your dreams! Maybe, then I’ll tell ‘em!’\n\nThe shutting of the door stopped Arthur from replying. They glided into the places where Jeremiah had left them; and Clennam, stepping forward as that old gentleman returned, informed him that he had accidentally extinguished the candle. Mr Flintwinch looked on as he re-lighted it at the lamp in the hall, and preserved a profound taciturnity respecting the person who had been holding him in conversation. Perhaps his irascibility demanded compensation for some tediousness that the visitor had expended on him; however that was, he took such umbrage at seeing his wife with her apron over her head, that he charged at her, and taking her veiled nose between his thumb and finger, appeared to throw the whole screw-power of his person into the wring he gave it.\n\nFlora, now permanently heavy, did not release Arthur from the survey of the house, until it had extended even to his old garret bedchamber. His thoughts were otherwise occupied than with the tour of inspection; yet he took particular notice at the time, as he afterwards had occasion to remember, of the airlessness and closeness of the house; that they left the track of their footsteps in the dust on the upper floors; and that there was a resistance to the opening of one room door, which occasioned Affery to cry out that somebody was hiding inside, and to continue to believe so, though somebody was sought and not discovered. When they at last returned to his mother’s room, they found her shading her face with her muffled hand, and talking in a low voice to the Patriarch as he stood before the fire, whose blue eyes, polished head, and silken locks, turning towards them as they came in, imparted an inestimable value and inexhaustible love of his species to his remark:\n\n‘So you have been seeing the premises, seeing the premises--premises-- seeing the premises!’\n\nIt was not in itself a jewel of benevolence or wisdom, yet he made it an exemplar of both that one would have liked to have a copy of.\n\n# CHAPTER 24. The Evening of a Long Day\n\nThat illustrious man and great national ornament, Mr Merdle, continued his shining course. It began to be widely understood that one who had done society the admirable service of making so much money out of it, could not be suffered to remain a commoner. A baronetcy was spoken of with confidence; a peerage was frequently mentioned. Rumour had it that Mr Merdle had set his golden face against a baronetcy; that he had plainly intimated to Lord Decimus that a baronetcy was not enough for him; that he had said, ‘No--a Peerage, or plain Merdle.’ This was reported to have plunged Lord Decimus as nigh to his noble chin in a slough of doubts as so lofty a person could be sunk. For the Barnacles, as a group of themselves in creation, had an idea that such distinctions belonged to them; and that when a soldier, sailor, or lawyer became ennobled, they let him in, as it were, by an act of condescension, at the family door, and immediately shut it again. Not only (said Rumour) had the troubled Decimus his own hereditary part in this impression, but he also knew of several Barnacle claims already on the file, which came into collision with that of the master spirit. Right or wrong, Rumour was very busy; and Lord Decimus, while he was, or was supposed to be, in stately excogitation of the difficulty, lent her some countenance by taking, on several public occasions, one of those elephantine trots of his through a jungle of overgrown sentences, waving Mr Merdle about on his trunk as Gigantic Enterprise, The Wealth of England, Elasticity, Credit, Capital, Prosperity, and all manner of blessings.\n\nSo quietly did the mowing of the old scythe go on, that fully three months had passed unnoticed since the two English brothers had been laid in one tomb in the strangers’ cemetery at Rome. Mr and Mrs Sparkler were established in their own house: a little mansion, rather of the Tite Barnacle class, quite a triumph of inconvenience, with a perpetual smell in it of the day before yesterday’s soup and coach-horses, but extremely dear, as being exactly in the centre of the habitable globe. In this enviable abode (and envied it really was by many people), Mrs Sparkler had intended to proceed at once to the demolition of the Bosom, when active hostilities had been suspended by the arrival of the Courier with his tidings of death. Mrs Sparkler, who was not unfeeling, had received them with a violent burst of grief, which had lasted twelve hours; after which, she had arisen to see about her mourning, and to take every precaution that could ensure its being as becoming as Mrs Merdle’s. A gloom was then cast over more than one distinguished family (according to the politest sources of intelligence), and the Courier went back again.\n\nMr and Mrs Sparkler had been dining alone, with their gloom cast over them, and Mrs Sparkler reclined on a drawing-room sofa. It was a hot summer Sunday evening. The residence in the centre of the habitable globe, at all times stuffed and close as if it had an incurable cold in its head, was that evening particularly stifling. The bells of the churches had done their worst in the way of clanging among the unmelodious echoes of the streets, and the lighted windows of the churches had ceased to be yellow in the grey dusk, and had died out opaque black. Mrs Sparkler, lying on her sofa, looking through an open window at the opposite side of a narrow street over boxes of mignonette and flowers, was tired of the view. Mrs Sparkler, looking at another window where her husband stood in the balcony, was tired of that view. Mrs Sparkler, looking at herself in her mourning, was even tired of that view: though, naturally, not so tired of that as of the other two.\n\n‘It’s like lying in a well,’ said Mrs Sparkler, changing her position fretfully. ‘Dear me, Edmund, if you have anything to say, why don’t you say it?’\n\nMr Sparkler might have replied with ingenuousness, ‘My life, I have nothing to say.’ But, as the repartee did not occur to him, he contented himself with coming in from the balcony and standing at the side of his wife’s couch.\n\n‘Good gracious, Edmund!’ said Mrs Sparkler more fretfully still, ‘you are absolutely putting mignonette up your nose! Pray don’t!’\n\nMr Sparkler, in absence of mind--perhaps in a more literal absence of mind than is usually understood by the phrase--had smelt so hard at a sprig in his hand as to be on the verge of the offence in question. He smiled, said, ‘I ask your pardon, my dear,’ and threw it out of window.\n\n‘You make my head ache by remaining in that position, Edmund,’ said Mrs Sparkler, raising her eyes to him after another minute; ‘you look so aggravatingly large by this light. Do sit down.’\n\n‘Certainly, my dear,’ said Mr Sparkler, and took a chair on the same spot.\n\n‘If I didn’t know that the longest day was past,’ said Fanny, yawning in a dreary manner, ‘I should have felt certain this was the longest day. I never did experience such a day.’\n\n‘Is that your fan, my love?’ asked Mr Sparkler, picking up one and presenting it.\n\n‘Edmund,’ returned his wife, more wearily yet, ‘don’t ask weak questions, I entreat you not. Whose can it be but mine?’\n\n‘Yes, I thought it was yours,’ said Mr Sparkler.\n\n‘Then you shouldn’t ask,’ retorted Fanny. After a little while she turned on her sofa and exclaimed, ‘Dear me, dear me, there never was such a long day as this!’ After another little while, she got up slowly, walked about, and came back again.\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr Sparkler, flashing with an original conception, ‘I think you must have got the fidgets.’\n\n‘Oh, Fidgets!’ repeated Mrs Sparkler. ‘Don’t.’\n\n‘My adorable girl,’ urged Mr Sparkler, ‘try your aromatic vinegar. I have often seen my mother try it, and it seemingly refreshed her.\nAnd she is, as I believe you are aware, a remarkably fine woman, with no non--’\n\n‘Good Gracious!’ exclaimed Fanny, starting up again. ‘It’s beyond all patience! This is the most wearisome day that ever did dawn upon the world, I am certain.’\n\nMr Sparkler looked meekly after her as she lounged about the room, and he appeared to be a little frightened. When she had tossed a few trifles about, and had looked down into the darkening street out of all the three windows, she returned to her sofa, and threw herself among its pillows.\n\n‘Now Edmund, come here! Come a little nearer, because I want to be able to touch you with my fan, that I may impress you very much with what I am going to say. That will do. Quite close enough. Oh, you _do_ look so big!’\n\nMr Sparkler apologised for the circumstance, pleaded that he couldn’t help it, and said that ‘our fellows,’ without more particularly indicating whose fellows, used to call him by the name of Quinbus Flestrin, Junior, or the Young Man Mountain.\n\n‘You ought to have told me so before,’ Fanny complained.\n\n‘My dear,’ returned Mr Sparkler, rather gratified, ‘I didn’t know It would interest you, or I would have made a point of telling you.’\n\n‘There! For goodness sake, don’t talk,’ said Fanny; ‘I want to talk, myself. Edmund, we must not be alone any more. I must take such precautions as will prevent my being ever again reduced to the state of dreadful depression in which I am this evening.’\n\n‘My dear,’ answered Mr Sparkler; ‘being as you are well known to be, a remarkably fine woman with no--’\n\n‘Oh, good GRACIOUS!’ cried Fanny.\n\nMr Sparkler was so discomposed by the energy of this exclamation, accompanied with a flouncing up from the sofa and a flouncing down again, that a minute or two elapsed before he felt himself equal to saying in explanation:\n\n‘I mean, my dear, that everybody knows you are calculated to shine in society.’\n\n‘Calculated to shine in society,’ retorted Fanny with great irritability; ‘yes, indeed! And then what happens? I no sooner recover, in a visiting point of view, the shock of poor dear papa’s death, and my poor uncle’s--though I do not disguise from myself that the last was a happy release, for, if you are not presentable you had much better die--’\n\n‘You are not referring to me, my love, I hope?’ Mr Sparkler humbly interrupted.\n\n‘Edmund, Edmund, you would wear out a Saint. Am I not expressly speaking of my poor uncle?’\n\n‘You looked with so much expression at myself, my dear girl,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘that I felt a little uncomfortable. Thank you, my love.’\n\n‘Now you have put me out,’ observed Fanny with a resigned toss of her fan, ‘and I had better go to bed.’\n\n‘Don’t do that, my love,’ urged Mr Sparkler. ‘Take time.’\n\nFanny took a good deal of time: lying back with her eyes shut, and her eyebrows raised with a hopeless expression as if she had utterly given up all terrestrial affairs. At length, without the slightest notice, she opened her eyes again, and recommenced in a short, sharp manner:\n\n‘What happens then, I ask! What happens? Why, I find myself at the very period when I might shine most in society, and should most like for very momentous reasons to shine in society--I find myself in a situation which to a certain extent disqualifies me for going into society. It’s too bad, really!’\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr Sparkler. ‘I don’t think it need keep you at home.’\n\n‘Edmund, you ridiculous creature,’ returned Fanny, with great indignation; ‘do you suppose that a woman in the bloom of youth and not wholly devoid of personal attractions, can put herself, at such a time, in competition as to figure with a woman in every other way her inferior? If you do suppose such a thing, your folly is boundless.’\n\nMr Sparkler submitted that he had thought ‘it might be got over.’\n\n‘Got over!’ repeated Fanny, with immeasurable scorn.\n\n‘For a time,’ Mr Sparkler submitted.\n\nHonouring the last feeble suggestion with no notice, Mrs Sparkler declared with bitterness that it really was too bad, and that positively it was enough to make one wish one was dead!\n\n‘However,’ she said, when she had in some measure recovered from her sense of personal ill-usage; ‘provoking as it is, and cruel as it seems, I suppose it must be submitted to.’\n\n‘Especially as it was to be expected,’ said Mr Sparkler.\n\n‘Edmund,’ returned his wife, ‘if you have nothing more becoming to do than to attempt to insult the woman who has honoured you with her hand, when she finds herself in adversity, I think _you_ had better go to bed!’\n\nMr Sparkler was much afflicted by the charge, and offered a most tender and earnest apology. His apology was accepted; but Mrs Sparkler requested him to go round to the other side of the sofa and sit in the window-curtain, to tone himself down.\n\n‘Now, Edmund,’ she said, stretching out her fan, and touching him with it at arm’s length, ‘what I was going to say to you when you began as usual to prose and worry, is, that I shall guard against our being alone any more, and that when circumstances prevent my going out to my own satisfaction, I must arrange to have some people or other always here; for I really cannot, and will not, have another such day as this has been.’\n\nMr Sparkler’s sentiments as to the plan were, in brief, that it had no nonsense about it. He added, ‘And besides, you know it’s likely that you’ll soon have your sister--’\n\n‘Dearest Amy, yes!’ cried Mrs Sparkler with a sigh of affection. ‘Darling little thing! Not, however, that Amy would do here alone.’\n\nMr Sparkler was going to say ‘No?’ interrogatively, but he saw his danger and said it assentingly, ‘No, Oh dear no; she wouldn’t do here alone.’\n\n‘No, Edmund. For not only are the virtues of the precious child of that still character that they require a contrast--require life and movement around them to bring them out in their right colours and make one love them of all things; but she will require to be roused, on more accounts than one.’\n\n‘That’s it,’ said Mr Sparkler. ‘Roused.’\n\n‘Pray don’t, Edmund! Your habit of interrupting without having the least thing in the world to say, distracts one. You must be broken of it. Speaking of Amy;--my poor little pet was devotedly attached to poor papa, and no doubt will have lamented his loss exceedingly, and grieved very much. I have done so myself. I have felt it dreadfully. But Amy will no doubt have felt it even more, from having been on the spot the whole time, and having been with poor dear papa at the last; which I unhappily was not.’\n\nHere Fanny stopped to weep, and to say, ‘Dear, dear, beloved papa! How truly gentlemanly he was! What a contrast to poor uncle!’\n\n‘From the effects of that trying time,’ she pursued, ‘my good little Mouse will have to be roused. Also, from the effects of this long attendance upon Edward in his illness; an attendance which is not yet over, which may even go on for some time longer, and which in the meanwhile unsettles us all by keeping poor dear papa’s affairs from being wound up. Fortunately, however, the papers with his agents here being all sealed up and locked up, as he left them when he providentially came to England, the affairs are in that state of order that they can wait until my brother Edward recovers his health in Sicily, sufficiently to come over, and administer, or execute, or whatever it may be that will have to be done.’\n\n‘He couldn’t have a better nurse to bring him round,’ Mr Sparkler made bold to opine.\n\n‘For a wonder, I can agree with you,’ returned his wife, languidly turning her eyelids a little in his direction (she held forth, in general, as if to the drawing-room furniture), ‘and can adopt your words. He couldn’t have a better nurse to bring him round. There are times when my dear child is a little wearing to an active mind; but, as a nurse, she is Perfection. Best of Amys!’\n\nMr Sparkler, growing rash on his late success, observed that Edward had had, biggodd, a long bout of it, my dear girl.\n\n‘If Bout, Edmund,’ returned Mrs Sparkler, ‘is the slang term for indisposition, he has. If it is not, I am unable to give an opinion on the barbarous language you address to Edward’s sister. That he contracted Malaria Fever somewhere, either by travelling day and night to Rome, where, after all, he arrived too late to see poor dear papa before his death--or under some other unwholesome circumstances--is indubitable, if that is what you mean. Likewise that his extremely careless life has made him a very bad subject for it indeed.’\n\nMr Sparkler considered it a parallel case to that of some of our fellows in the West Indies with Yellow Jack. Mrs Sparkler closed her eyes again, and refused to have any consciousness of our fellows of the West Indies, or of Yellow Jack.\n\n‘So, Amy,’ she pursued, when she reopened her eyelids, ‘will require to be roused from the effects of many tedious and anxious weeks. And lastly, she will require to be roused from a low tendency which I know very well to be at the bottom of her heart. Don’t ask me what it is, Edmund, because I must decline to tell you.’\n\n‘I am not going to, my dear,’ said Mr Sparkler.\n\n‘I shall thus have much improvement to effect in my sweet child,’ Mrs Sparkler continued, ‘and cannot have her near me too soon. Amiable and dear little Twoshoes! As to the settlement of poor papa’s affairs, my interest in that is not very selfish. Papa behaved very generously to me when I was married, and I have little or nothing to expect. Provided he had made no will that can come into force, leaving a legacy to Mrs General, I am contented. Dear papa, dear papa.’\n\nShe wept again, but Mrs General was the best of restoratives. The name soon stimulated her to dry her eyes and say:\n\n‘It is a highly encouraging circumstance in Edward’s illness, I am thankful to think, and gives one the greatest confidence in his sense not being impaired, or his proper spirit weakened--down to the time of poor dear papa’s death at all events--that he paid off Mrs General instantly, and sent her out of the house. I applaud him for it. I could forgive him a great deal for doing, with such promptitude, so exactly what I would have done myself!’\n\nMrs Sparkler was in the full glow of her gratification, when a double knock was heard at the door. A very odd knock. Low, as if to avoid making a noise and attracting attention. Long, as if the person knocking were preoccupied in mind, and forgot to leave off.\n\n‘Halloa!’ said Mr Sparkler. ‘Who’s this?’\n\n‘Not Amy and Edward without notice and without a carriage!’ said Mrs Sparkler. ‘Look out.’\n\nThe room was dark, but the street was lighter, because of its lamps. Mr Sparkler’s head peeping over the balcony looked so very bulky and heavy that it seemed on the point of overbalancing him and flattening the unknown below.\n\n‘It’s one fellow,’ said Mr Sparkler. ‘I can’t see who--stop though!’\n\nOn this second thought he went out into the balcony again and had another look. He came back as the door was opened, and announced that he believed he had identified ‘his governor’s tile.’ He was not mistaken, for his governor, with his tile in his hand, was introduced immediately afterwards.\n\n‘Candles!’ said Mrs Sparkler, with a word of excuse for the darkness.\n\n‘It’s light enough for me,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nWhen the candles were brought in, Mr Merdle was discovered standing behind the door, picking his lips. ‘I thought I’d give you a call,’ he said. ‘I am rather particularly occupied just now; and, as I happened to be out for a stroll, I thought I’d give you a call.’\n\nAs he was in dinner dress, Fanny asked him where he had been dining?\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I haven’t been dining anywhere, particularly.’\n\n‘Of course you have dined?’ said Fanny.\n\n‘Why--no, I haven’t exactly dined,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nHe had passed his hand over his yellow forehead and considered, as if he were not sure about it. Something to eat was proposed. ‘No, thank you,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I don’t feel inclined for it. I was to have dined out along with Mrs Merdle. But as I didn’t feel inclined for dinner, I let Mrs Merdle go by herself just as we were getting into the carriage, and thought I’d take a stroll instead.’\n\nWould he have tea or coffee? ‘No, thank you,’ said Mr Merdle. ‘I looked in at the Club, and got a bottle of wine.’\n\nAt this period of his visit, Mr Merdle took the chair which Edmund Sparkler had offered him, and which he had hitherto been pushing slowly about before him, like a dull man with a pair of skates on for the first time, who could not make up his mind to start. He now put his hat upon another chair beside him, and, looking down into it as if it were some twenty feet deep, said again: ‘You see I thought I’d give you a call.’\n\n‘Flattering to us,’ said Fanny, ‘for you are not a calling man.’\n\n‘No--no,’ returned Mr Merdle, who was by this time taking himself into custody under both coat-sleeves. ‘No, I am not a calling man.’\n\n‘You have too much to do for that,’ said Fanny. ‘Having so much to do, Mr Merdle, loss of appetite is a serious thing with you, and you must have it seen to. You must not be ill.’\n\n‘Oh! I am very well,’ replied Mr Merdle, after deliberating about it. ‘I am as well as I usually am. I am well enough. I am as well as I want to be.’\n\nThe master-mind of the age, true to its characteristic of being at all times a mind that had as little as possible to say for itself and great difficulty in saying it, became mute again. Mrs Sparkler began to wonder how long the master-mind meant to stay.\n\n‘I was speaking of poor papa when you came in, sir.’\n\n‘Aye! Quite a coincidence,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nFanny did not see that; but felt it incumbent on her to continue talking. ‘I was saying,’ she pursued, ‘that my brother’s illness has occasioned a delay in examining and arranging papa’s property.’\n\n‘Yes,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘yes. There has been a delay.’\n\n‘Not that it is of consequence,’ said Fanny.\n\n‘Not,’ assented Mr Merdle, after having examined the cornice of all that part of the room which was within his range: ‘not that it is of any consequence.’\n\n‘My only anxiety is,’ said Fanny, ‘that Mrs General should not get anything.’\n\n‘_She_ won’t get anything,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nFanny was delighted to hear him express the opinion. Mr Merdle, after taking another gaze into the depths of his hat as if he thought he saw something at the bottom, rubbed his hair and slowly appended to his last remark the confirmatory words, ‘Oh dear no. No. Not she. Not likely.’\n\nAs the topic seemed exhausted, and Mr Merdle too, Fanny inquired if he were going to take up Mrs Merdle and the carriage in his way home?\n\n‘No,’ he answered; ‘I shall go by the shortest way, and leave Mrs Merdle to--’ here he looked all over the palms of both his hands as if he were telling his own fortune--‘to take care of herself. I dare say she’ll manage to do it.’\n\n‘Probably,’ said Fanny.\n\nThere was then a long silence; during which, Mrs Sparkler, lying back on her sofa again, shut her eyes and raised her eyebrows in her former retirement from mundane affairs.\n\n‘But, however,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I am equally detaining you and myself. I thought I’d give you a call, you know.’\n\n‘Charmed, I am sure,’ said Fanny.\n\n‘So I am off,’ added Mr Merdle, getting up. ‘Could you lend me a penknife?’\n\nIt was an odd thing, Fanny smilingly observed, for her who could seldom prevail upon herself even to write a letter, to lend to a man of such vast business as Mr Merdle. ‘Isn’t it?’ Mr Merdle acquiesced; ‘but I want one; and I know you have got several little wedding keepsakes about, with scissors and tweezers and such things in them. You shall have it back to-morrow.’\n\n‘Edmund,’ said Mrs Sparkler, ‘open (now, very carefully, I beg and beseech, for you are so very awkward) the mother of pearl box on my little table there, and give Mr Merdle the mother of pearl penknife.’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘but if you have got one with a darker handle, I think I should prefer one with a darker handle.’\n\n‘Tortoise-shell?’\n\n‘Thank you,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘yes. I think I should prefer tortoise-shell.’\n\nEdmund accordingly received instructions to open the tortoise-shell box, and give Mr Merdle the tortoise-shell knife. On his doing so, his wife said to the master-spirit graciously:\n\n‘I will forgive you, if you ink it.’\n\n‘I’ll undertake not to ink it,’ said Mr Merdle.\n\nThe illustrious visitor then put out his coat-cuff, and for a moment entombed Mrs Sparkler’s hand: wrist, bracelet, and all. Where his own hand had shrunk to, was not made manifest, but it was as remote from Mrs Sparkler’s sense of touch as if he had been a highly meritorious Chelsea Veteran or Greenwich Pensioner.\n\nThoroughly convinced, as he went out of the room, that it was the longest day that ever did come to an end at last, and that there never was a woman, not wholly devoid of personal attractions, so worn out by idiotic and lumpish people, Fanny passed into the balcony for a breath of air. Waters of vexation filled her eyes; and they had the effect of making the famous Mr Merdle, in going down the street, appear to leap, and waltz, and gyrate, as if he were possessed of several Devils.\n\n# CHAPTER 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office\n\nThe dinner-party was at the great Physician’s. Bar was there, and in full force. Ferdinand Barnacle was there, and in his most engaging state. Few ways of life were hidden from Physician, and he was oftener in its darkest places than even Bishop. There were brilliant ladies about London who perfectly doted on him, my dear, as the most charming creature and the most delightful person, who would have been shocked to find themselves so close to him if they could have known on what sights those thoughtful eyes of his had rested within an hour or two, and near to whose beds, and under what roofs, his composed figure had stood. But Physician was a composed man, who performed neither on his own trumpet, nor on the trumpets of other people. Many wonderful things did he see and hear, and much irreconcilable moral contradiction did he pass his life among; yet his equality of compassion was no more disturbed than the Divine Master’s of all healing was. He went, like the rain, among the just and unjust, doing all the good he could, and neither proclaiming it in the synagogues nor at the corner of streets.\n\nAs no man of large experience of humanity, however quietly carried it may be, can fail to be invested with an interest peculiar to the possession of such knowledge, Physician was an attractive man. Even the daintier gentlemen and ladies who had no idea of his secret, and who would have been startled out of more wits than they had, by the monstrous impropriety of his proposing to them ‘Come and see what I see!’ confessed his attraction. Where he was, something real was. And half a grain of reality, like the smallest portion of some other scarce natural productions, will flavour an enormous quantity of diluent.\n\nIt came to pass, therefore, that Physician’s little dinners always presented people in their least conventional lights. The guests said to themselves, whether they were conscious of it or no, ‘Here is a man who really has an acquaintance with us as we are, who is admitted to some of us every day with our wigs and paint off, who hears the wanderings of our minds, and sees the undisguised expression of our faces, when both are past our control; we may as well make an approach to reality with him, for the man has got the better of us and is too strong for us.’ Therefore, Physician’s guests came out so surprisingly at his round table that they were almost natural.\n\nBar’s knowledge of that agglomeration of jurymen which is called humanity was as sharp as a razor; yet a razor is not a generally convenient instrument, and Physician’s plain bright scalpel, though far less keen, was adaptable to far wider purposes. Bar knew all about the gullibility and knavery of people; but Physician could have given him a better insight into their tendernesses and affections, in one week of his rounds, than Westminster Hall and all the circuits put together, in threescore years and ten. Bar always had a suspicion of this, and perhaps was glad to encourage it (for, if the world were really a great Law Court, one would think that the last day of Term could not too soon arrive); and so he liked and respected Physician quite as much as any other kind of man did.\n\nMr Merdle’s default left a Banquo’s chair at the table; but, if he had been there, he would have merely made the difference of Banquo in it, and consequently he was no loss. Bar, who picked up all sorts of odds and ends about Westminster Hall, much as a raven would have done if he had passed as much of his time there, had been picking up a great many straws lately and tossing them about, to try which way the Merdle wind blew. He now had a little talk on the subject with Mrs Merdle herself; sidling up to that lady, of course, with his double eye-glass and his jury droop.\n\n‘A certain bird,’ said Bar; and he looked as if it could have been no other bird than a magpie; ‘has been whispering among us lawyers lately, that there is to be an addition to the titled personages of this realm.’\n\n‘Really?’ said Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘Yes,’ said Bar. ‘Has not the bird been whispering in very different ears from ours--in lovely ears?’ He looked expressively at Mrs Merdle’s nearest ear-ring.\n\n‘Do you mean mine?’ asked Mrs Merdle.\n\n‘When I say lovely,’ said Bar, ‘I always mean you.’\n\n‘You never mean anything, I think,’ returned Mrs Merdle (not displeased).\n\n‘Oh, cruelly unjust!’ said Bar. ‘But, the bird.’\n\n‘I am the last person in the world to hear news,’ observed Mrs Merdle, carelessly arranging her stronghold. ‘Who is it?’\n\n‘What an admirable witness you would make!’ said Bar. ‘No jury (unless we could empanel one of blind men) could resist you, if you were ever so bad a one; but you would be such a good one!’\n\n‘Why, you ridiculous man?’ asked Mrs Merdle, laughing.\n\nBar waved his double eye-glass three or four times between himself and the Bosom, as a rallying answer, and inquired in his most insinuating accents:\n\n‘What am I to call the most elegant, accomplished and charming of women, a few weeks, or it may be a few days, hence?’\n\n‘Didn’t your bird tell you what to call her?’ answered Mrs Merdle. ‘Do ask it to-morrow, and tell me the next time you see me what it says.’\n\nThis led to further passages of similar pleasantry between the two; but Bar, with all his sharpness, got nothing out of them. Physician, on the other hand, taking Mrs Merdle down to her carriage and attending on her as she put on her cloak, inquired into the symptoms with his usual calm directness.\n\n‘May I ask,’ he said, ‘is this true about Merdle?’\n\n‘My dear doctor,’ she returned, ‘you ask me the very question that I was half disposed to ask you.’\n\n‘To ask me! Why me?’\n\n‘Upon my honour, I think Mr Merdle reposes greater confidence in you than in any one.’\n\n‘On the contrary, he tells me absolutely nothing, even professionally. You have heard the talk, of course?’\n\n‘Of course I have. But you know what Mr Merdle is; you know how taciturn and reserved he is. I assure you I have no idea what foundation for it there may be. I should like it to be true; why should I deny that to you? You would know better, if I did!’\n\n‘Just so,’ said Physician.\n\n‘But whether it is all true, or partly true, or entirely false, I am wholly unable to say. It is a most provoking situation, a most absurd situation; but you know Mr Merdle, and are not surprised.’\n\nPhysician was not surprised, handed her into her carriage, and bade her Good Night. He stood for a moment at his own hall door, looking sedately at the elegant equipage as it rattled away. On his return up-stairs, the rest of the guests soon dispersed, and he was left alone. Being a great reader of all kinds of literature (and never at all apologetic for that weakness), he sat down comfortably to read.\n\nThe clock upon his study table pointed to a few minutes short of twelve, when his attention was called to it by a ringing at the door bell. A man of plain habits, he had sent his servants to bed and must needs go down to open the door. He went down, and there found a man without hat or coat, whose shirt sleeves were rolled up tight to his shoulders. For a moment, he thought the man had been fighting: the rather, as he was much agitated and out of breath. A second look, however, showed him that the man was particularly clean, and not otherwise discomposed as to his dress than as it answered this description.\n\n‘I come from the warm-baths, sir, round in the neighbouring street.’\n\n‘And what is the matter at the warm-baths?’\n\n‘Would you please to come directly, sir. We found that, lying on the table.’\n\nHe put into the physician’s hand a scrap of paper. Physician looked at it, and read his own name and address written in pencil; nothing more. He looked closer at the writing, looked at the man, took his hat from its peg, put the key of his door in his pocket, and they hurried away together.\n\nWhen they came to the warm-baths, all the other people belonging to that establishment were looking out for them at the door, and running up and down the passages. ‘Request everybody else to keep back, if you please,’ said the physician aloud to the master; ‘and do you take me straight to the place, my friend,’ to the messenger.\n\nThe messenger hurried before him, along a grove of little rooms, and turning into one at the end of the grove, looked round the door. Physician was close upon him, and looked round the door too.\n\nThere was a bath in that corner, from which the water had been hastily drained off. Lying in it, as in a grave or sarcophagus, with a hurried drapery of sheet and blanket thrown across it, was the body of a heavily-made man, with an obtuse head, and coarse, mean, common features. A sky-light had been opened to release the steam with which the room had been filled; but it hung, condensed into water-drops, heavily upon the walls, and heavily upon the face and figure in the bath. The room was still hot, and the marble of the bath still warm; but the face and figure were clammy to the touch. The white marble at the bottom of the bath was veined with a dreadful red. On the ledge at the side, were an empty laudanum-bottle and a tortoise-shell handled penknife--soiled, but not with ink.\n\n‘Separation of jugular vein--death rapid--been dead at least half an hour.’ This echo of the physician’s words ran through the passages and little rooms, and through the house while he was yet straightening himself from having bent down to reach to the bottom of the bath, and while he was yet dabbling his hands in water; redly veining it as the marble was veined, before it mingled into one tint.\n\nHe turned his eyes to the dress upon the sofa, and to the watch, money, and pocket-book on the table. A folded note half buckled up in the pocket-book, and half protruding from it, caught his observant glance. He looked at it, touched it, pulled it a little further out from among the leaves, said quietly, ‘This is addressed to me,’ and opened and read it.\n\nThere were no directions for him to give. The people of the house knew what to do; the proper authorities were soon brought; and they took an equable business-like possession of the deceased, and of what had been his property, with no greater disturbance of manner or countenance than usually attends the winding-up of a clock. Physician was glad to walk out into the night air--was even glad, in spite of his great experience, to sit down upon a door-step for a little while: feeling sick and faint.\n\nBar was a near neighbour of his, and, when he came to the house, he saw a light in the room where he knew his friend often sat late getting up his work. As the light was never there when Bar was not, it gave him assurance that Bar was not yet in bed. In fact, this busy bee had a verdict to get to-morrow, against evidence, and was improving the shining hours in setting snares for the gentlemen of the jury.\n\nPhysician’s knock astonished Bar; but, as he immediately suspected that somebody had come to tell him that somebody else was robbing him, or otherwise trying to get the better of him, he came down promptly and softly. He had been clearing his head with a lotion of cold water, as a good preparative to providing hot water for the heads of the jury, and had been reading with the neck of his shirt thrown wide open that he might the more freely choke the opposite witnesses. In consequence, he came down, looking rather wild. Seeing Physician, the least expected of men, he looked wilder and said, ‘What’s the matter?’\n\n‘You asked me once what Merdle’s complaint was.’\n\n‘Extraordinary answer! I know I did.’\n\n‘I told you I had not found out.’\n\n‘Yes. I know you did.’\n\n‘I have found it out.’\n\n‘My God!’ said Bar, starting back, and clapping his hand upon the other’s breast. ‘And so have I! I see it in your face.’\n\nThey went into the nearest room, where Physician gave him the letter to read. He read it through half-a-dozen times. There was not much in it as to quantity; but it made a great demand on his close and continuous attention. He could not sufficiently give utterance to his regret that he had not himself found a clue to this. The smallest clue, he said, would have made him master of the case, and what a case it would have been to have got to the bottom of!\n\nPhysician had engaged to break the intelligence in Harley Street. Bar could not at once return to his inveiglements of the most enlightened and remarkable jury he had ever seen in that box, with whom, he could tell his learned friend, no shallow sophistry would go down, and no unhappily abused professional tact and skill prevail (this was the way he meant to begin with them); so he said he would go too, and would loiter to and fro near the house while his friend was inside. They walked there, the better to recover self-possession in the air; and the wings of day were fluttering the night when Physician knocked at the door.\n\nA footman of rainbow hues, in the public eye, was sitting up for his master--that is to say, was fast asleep in the kitchen over a couple of candles and a newspaper, demonstrating the great accumulation of mathematical odds against the probabilities of a house being set on fire by accident When this serving man was roused, Physician had still to await the rousing of the Chief Butler. At last that noble creature came into the dining-room in a flannel gown and list shoes; but with his cravat on, and a Chief Butler all over. It was morning now. Physician had opened the shutters of one window while waiting, that he might see the light.\n\n‘Mrs Merdle’s maid must be called, and told to get Mrs Merdle up, and prepare her as gently as she can to see me. I have dreadful news to break to her.’\n\nThus Physician to the Chief Butler. The latter, who had a candle in his hand, called his man to take it away. Then he approached the window with dignity; looking on at Physician’s news exactly as he had looked on at the dinners in that very room.\n\n‘Mr Merdle is dead.’\n\n‘I should wish,’ said the Chief Butler, ‘to give a month’s notice.’\n\n‘Mr Merdle has destroyed himself.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said the Chief Butler, ‘that is very unpleasant to the feelings of one in my position, as calculated to awaken prejudice; and I should wish to leave immediately.’\n\n‘If you are not shocked, are you not surprised, man?’ demanded the Physician, warmly.\n\nThe Chief Butler, erect and calm, replied in these memorable words. ‘Sir, Mr Merdle never was the gentleman, and no ungentlemanly act on Mr Merdle’s part would surprise me. Is there anybody else I can send to you, or any other directions I can give before I leave, respecting what you would wish to be done?’\n\nWhen Physician, after discharging himself of his trust up-stairs, rejoined Bar in the street, he said no more of his interview with Mrs Merdle than that he had not yet told her all, but that what he had told her she had borne pretty well. Bar had devoted his leisure in the street to the construction of a most ingenious man-trap for catching the whole of his jury at a blow; having got that matter settled in his mind, it was lucid on the late catastrophe, and they walked home slowly, discussing it in every bearing. Before parting at the Physician’s door, they both looked up at the sunny morning sky, into which the smoke of a few early fires and the breath and voices of a few early stirrers were peacefully rising, and then looked round upon the immense city, and said, if all those hundreds and thousands of beggared people who were yet asleep could only know, as they two spoke, the ruin that impended over them, what a fearful cry against one miserable soul would go up to Heaven!\n\nThe report that the great man was dead, got about with astonishing rapidity. At first, he was dead of all the diseases that ever were known, and of several bran-new maladies invented with the speed of Light to meet the demand of the occasion. He had concealed a dropsy from infancy, he had inherited a large estate of water on the chest from his grandfather, he had had an operation performed upon him every morning of his life for eighteen years, he had been subject to the explosion of important veins in his body after the manner of fireworks, he had had something the matter with his lungs, he had had something the matter with his heart, he had had something the matter with his brain. Five hundred people who sat down to breakfast entirely uninformed on the whole subject, believed before they had done breakfast, that they privately and personally knew Physician to have said to Mr Merdle, ‘You must expect to go out, some day, like the snuff of a candle;’ and that they knew Mr Merdle to have said to Physician, ‘A man can die but once.’ By about eleven o’clock in the forenoon, something the matter with the brain, became the favourite theory against the field; and by twelve the something had been distinctly ascertained to be ‘Pressure.’\n\nPressure was so entirely satisfactory to the public mind, and seemed to make everybody so comfortable, that it might have lasted all day but for Bar’s having taken the real state of the case into Court at half-past nine. This led to its beginning to be currently whispered all over London by about one, that Mr Merdle had killed himself. Pressure, however, so far from being overthrown by the discovery, became a greater favourite than ever. There was a general moralising upon Pressure, in every street. All the people who had tried to make money and had not been able to do it, said, There you were! You no sooner began to devote yourself to the pursuit of wealth than you got Pressure. The idle people improved the occasion in a similar manner. See, said they, what you brought yourself to by work, work, work! You persisted in working, you overdid it. Pressure came on, and you were done for! This consideration was very potent in many quarters, but nowhere more so than among the young clerks and partners who had never been in the slightest danger of overdoing it. These, one and all, declared, quite piously, that they hoped they would never forget the warning as long as they lived, and that their conduct might be so regulated as to keep off Pressure, and preserve them, a comfort to their friends, for many years.\n\nBut, at about the time of High ‘Change, Pressure began to wane, and appalling whispers to circulate, east, west, north, and south. At first they were faint, and went no further than a doubt whether Mr Merdle’s wealth would be found to be as vast as had been supposed; whether there might not be a temporary difficulty in ‘realising’ it; whether there might not even be a temporary suspension (say a month or so), on the part of the wonderful Bank. As the whispers became louder, which they did from that time every minute, they became more threatening. He had sprung from nothing, by no natural growth or process that any one could account for; he had been, after all, a low, ignorant fellow; he had been a down-looking man, and no one had ever been able to catch his eye; he had been taken up by all sorts of people in quite an unaccountable manner; he had never had any money of his own, his ventures had been utterly reckless, and his expenditure had been most enormous. In steady progression, as the day declined, the talk rose in sound and purpose. He had left a letter at the Baths addressed to his physician, and his physician had got the letter, and the letter would be produced at the Inquest on the morrow, and it would fall like a thunderbolt upon the multitude he had deluded. Numbers of men in every profession and trade would be blighted by his insolvency; old people who had been in easy circumstances all their lives would have no place of repentance for their trust in him but the workhouse; legions of women and children would have their whole future desolated by the hand of this mighty scoundrel. Every partaker of his magnificent feasts would be seen to have been a sharer in the plunder of innumerable homes; every servile worshipper of riches who had helped to set him on his pedestal, would have done better to worship the Devil point-blank. So, the talk, lashed louder and higher by confirmation on confirmation, and by edition after edition of the evening papers, swelled into such a roar when night came, as might have brought one to believe that a solitary watcher on the gallery above the Dome of St Paul’s would have perceived the night air to be laden with a heavy muttering of the name of Merdle, coupled with every form of execration.\n\nFor by that time it was known that the late Mr Merdle’s complaint had been simply Forgery and Robbery. He, the uncouth object of such wide-spread adulation, the sitter at great men’s feasts, the roc’s egg of great ladies’ assemblies, the subduer of exclusiveness, the leveller of pride, the patron of patrons, the bargain-driver with a Minister for Lordships of the Circumlocution Office, the recipient of more acknowledgment within some ten or fifteen years, at most, than had been bestowed in England upon all peaceful public benefactors, and upon all the leaders of all the Arts and Sciences, with all their works to testify for them, during two centuries at least--he, the shining wonder, the new constellation to be followed by the wise men bringing gifts, until it stopped over a certain carrion at the bottom of a bath and disappeared--was simply the greatest Forger and the greatest Thief that ever cheated the gallows.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Reaping the Whirlwind\n\nWith a precursory sound of hurried breath and hurried feet, Mr Pancks rushed into Arthur Clennam’s Counting-house. The Inquest was over, the letter was public, the Bank was broken, the other model structures of straw had taken fire and were turned to smoke. The admired piratical ship had blown up, in the midst of a vast fleet of ships of all rates, and boats of all sizes; and on the deep was nothing but ruin; nothing but burning hulls, bursting magazines, great guns self-exploded tearing friends and neighbours to pieces, drowning men clinging to unseaworthy spars and going down every minute, spent swimmers, floating dead, and sharks.\n\nThe usual diligence and order of the Counting-house at the Works were overthrown. Unopened letters and unsorted papers lay strewn about the desk. In the midst of these tokens of prostrated energy and dismissed hope, the master of the Counting-house stood idle in his usual place, with his arms crossed on the desk, and his head bowed down upon them.\n\nMr Pancks rushed in and saw him, and stood still. In another minute, Mr Pancks’s arms were on the desk, and Mr Pancks’s head was bowed down upon them; and for some time they remained in these attitudes, idle and silent, with the width of the little room between them.\n\nMr Pancks was the first to lift up his head and speak.\n\n‘I persuaded you to it, Mr Clennam. I know it. Say what you will. You can’t say more to me than I say to myself. You can’t say more than I deserve.’\n\n‘O, Pancks, Pancks!’ returned Clennam, ‘don’t speak of deserving. What do I myself deserve!’\n\n‘Better luck,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘I,’ pursued Clennam, without attending to him, ‘who have ruined my partner! Pancks, Pancks, I have ruined Doyce! The honest, self-helpful, indefatigable old man who has worked his way all through his life; the man who has contended against so much disappointment, and who has brought out of it such a good and hopeful nature; the man I have felt so much for, and meant to be so true and useful to; I have ruined him--brought him to shame and disgrace--ruined him, ruined him!’\n\nThe agony into which the reflection wrought his mind was so distressing to see, that Mr Pancks took hold of himself by the hair of his head, and tore it in desperation at the spectacle.\n\n‘Reproach me!’ cried Pancks. ‘Reproach me, sir, or I’ll do myself an injury. Say,--You fool, you villain. Say,--Ass, how could you do it; Beast, what did you mean by it! Catch hold of me somewhere. Say something abusive to me!’ All the time, Mr Pancks was tearing at his tough hair in a most pitiless and cruel manner.\n\n‘If you had never yielded to this fatal mania, Pancks,’ said Clennam, more in commiseration than retaliation, ‘it would have been how much better for you, and how much better for me!’\n\n‘At me again, sir!’ cried Pancks, grinding his teeth in remorse. ‘At me again!’\n\n‘If you had never gone into those accursed calculations, and brought out your results with such abominable clearness,’ groaned Clennam, ‘it would have been how much better for you, Pancks, and how much better for me!’\n\n‘At me again, sir!’ exclaimed Pancks, loosening his hold of his hair; ‘at me again, and again!’\n\nClennam, however, finding him already beginning to be pacified, had said all he wanted to say, and more. He wrung his hand, only adding, ‘Blind leaders of the blind, Pancks! Blind leaders of the blind! But Doyce, Doyce, Doyce; my injured partner!’ That brought his head down on the desk once more.\n\nTheir former attitudes and their former silence were once more first encroached upon by Pancks.\n\n‘Not been to bed, sir, since it began to get about. Been high and low, on the chance of finding some hope of saving any cinders from the fire. All in vain. All gone. All vanished.’\n\n‘I know it,’ returned Clennam, ‘too well.’\n\nMr Pancks filled up a pause with a groan that came out of the very depths of his soul.\n\n‘Only yesterday, Pancks,’ said Arthur; ‘only yesterday, Monday, I had the fixed intention of selling, realising, and making an end of it.’\n\n‘I can’t say as much for myself, sir,’ returned Pancks. ‘Though it’s wonderful how many people I’ve heard of, who were going to realise yesterday, of all days in the three hundred and sixty-five, if it hadn’t been too late!’\n\nHis steam-like breathings, usually droll in their effect, were more tragic than so many groans: while from head to foot, he was in that begrimed, besmeared, neglected state, that he might have been an authentic portrait of Misfortune which could scarcely be discerned through its want of cleaning.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, had you laid out--everything?’ He got over the break before the last word, and also brought out the last word itself with great difficulty.\n\n‘Everything.’\n\nMr Pancks took hold of his tough hair again, and gave it such a wrench that he pulled out several prongs of it. After looking at these with an eye of wild hatred, he put them in his pocket.\n\n‘My course,’ said Clennam, brushing away some tears that had been silently dropping down his face, ‘must be taken at once. What wretched amends I can make must be made. I must clear my unfortunate partner’s reputation. I must retain nothing for myself. I must resign to our creditors the power of management I have so much abused, and I must work out as much of my fault--or crime--as is susceptible of being worked out in the rest of my days.’\n\n‘Is it impossible, sir, to tide over the present?’\n\n‘Out of the question. Nothing can be tided over now, Pancks. The sooner the business can pass out of my hands, the better for it. There are engagements to be met, this week, which would bring the catastrophe before many days were over, even if I would postpone it for a single day by going on for that space, secretly knowing what I know. All last night I thought of what I would do; what remains is to do it.’\n\n‘Not entirely of yourself?’ said Pancks, whose face was as damp as if his steam were turning into water as fast as he dismally blew it off. ‘Have some legal help.’\n\n‘Perhaps I had better.’\n\n‘Have Rugg.’\n\n‘There is not much to do. He will do it as well as another.’\n\n‘Shall I fetch Rugg, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘If you could spare the time, I should be much obliged to you.’\n\nMr Pancks put on his hat that moment, and steamed away to Pentonville. While he was gone Arthur never raised his head from the desk, but remained in that one position.\n\nMr Pancks brought his friend and professional adviser, Mr Rugg, back with him. Mr Rugg had had such ample experience, on the road, of Mr Pancks’s being at that present in an irrational state of mind, that he opened his professional mediation by requesting that gentleman to take himself out of the way. Mr Pancks, crushed and submissive, obeyed.\n\n‘He is not unlike what my daughter was, sir, when we began the Breach of Promise action of Rugg and Bawkins, in which she was Plaintiff,’ said Mr Rugg. ‘He takes too strong and direct an interest in the case. His feelings are worked upon. There is no getting on, in our profession, with feelings worked upon, sir.’\n\nAs he pulled off his gloves and put them in his hat, he saw, in a side glance or two, that a great change had come over his client.\n\n‘I am sorry to perceive, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘that you have been allowing your own feelings to be worked upon. Now, pray don’t, pray don’t. These losses are much to be deplored, sir, but we must look ‘em in the face.’\n\n‘If the money I have sacrificed had been all my own, Mr Rugg,’ sighed Mr Clennam, ‘I should have cared far less.’\n\n‘Indeed, sir?’ said Mr Rugg, rubbing his hands with a cheerful air. ‘You surprise me. That’s singular, sir. I have generally found, in my experience, that it’s their own money people are most particular about. I have seen people get rid of a good deal of other people’s money, and bear it very well: very well indeed.’\n\nWith these comforting remarks, Mr Rugg seated himself on an office-stool at the desk and proceeded to business.\n\n‘Now, Mr Clennam, by your leave, let us go into the matter. Let us see the state of the case. The question is simple. The question is the usual plain, straightforward, common-sense question. What can we do for ourself? What can we do for ourself?’\n\n‘This is not the question with me, Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur. ‘You mistake it in the beginning. It is, what can I do for my partner, how can I best make reparation to him?’\n\n‘I am afraid, sir, do you know,’ argued Mr Rugg persuasively, ‘that you are still allowing your feeling to be worked upon. I _don’t_ like the term “reparation,” sir, except as a lever in the hands of counsel. Will you excuse my saying that I feel it my duty to offer you the caution, that you really must not allow your feelings to be worked upon?’\n\n‘Mr Rugg,’ said Clennam, nerving himself to go through with what he had resolved upon, and surprising that gentleman by appearing, in his despondency, to have a settled determination of purpose; ‘you give me the impression that you will not be much disposed to adopt the course I have made up my mind to take. If your disapproval of it should render you unwilling to discharge such business as it necessitates, I am sorry for it, and must seek other aid. But I will represent to you at once, that to argue against it with me is useless.’\n\n‘Good, sir,’ answered Mr Rugg, shrugging his shoulders. ‘Good, sir. Since the business is to be done by some hands, let it be done by mine. Such was my principle in the case of Rugg and Bawkins. Such is my principle in most cases.’\n\nClennam then proceeded to state to Mr Rugg his fixed resolution. He told Mr Rugg that his partner was a man of great simplicity and integrity, and that in all he meant to do, he was guided above all things by a knowledge of his partner’s character, and a respect for his feelings. He explained that his partner was then absent on an enterprise of importance, and that it particularly behoved himself publicly to accept the blame of what he had rashly done, and publicly to exonerate his partner from all participation in the responsibility of it, lest the successful conduct of that enterprise should be endangered by the slightest suspicion wrongly attaching to his partner’s honour and credit in another country. He told Mr Rugg that to clear his partner morally, to the fullest extent, and publicly and unreservedly to declare that he, Arthur Clennam, of that Firm, had of his own sole act, and even expressly against his partner’s caution, embarked its resources in the swindles that had lately perished, was the only real atonement within his power; was a better atonement to the particular man than it would be to many men; and was therefore the atonement he had first to make. With this view, his intention was to print a declaration to the foregoing effect, which he had already drawn up; and, besides circulating it among all who had dealings with the House, to advertise it in the public papers. Concurrently with this measure (the description of which cost Mr Rugg innumerable wry faces and great uneasiness in his limbs), he would address a letter to all the creditors, exonerating his partner in a solemn manner, informing them of the stoppage of the House until their pleasure could be known and his partner communicated with, and humbly submitting himself to their direction. If, through their consideration for his partner’s innocence, the affairs could ever be got into such train as that the business could be profitably resumed, and its present downfall overcome, then his own share in it should revert to his partner, as the only reparation he could make to him in money value for the distress and loss he had unhappily brought upon him, and he himself, at as small a salary as he could live upon, would ask to be allowed to serve the business as a faithful clerk.\n\nThough Mr Rugg saw plainly there was no preventing this from being done, still the wryness of his face and the uneasiness of his limbs so sorely required the propitiation of a Protest, that he made one. ‘I offer no objection, sir,’ said he, ‘I argue no point with you. I will carry out your views, sir; but, under protest.’ Mr Rugg then stated, not without prolixity, the heads of his protest. These were, in effect, because the whole town, or he might say the whole country, was in the first madness of the late discovery, and the resentment against the victims would be very strong: those who had not been deluded being certain to wax exceedingly wroth with them for not having been as wise as they were: and those who had been deluded being certain to find excuses and reasons for themselves, of which they were equally certain to see that other sufferers were wholly devoid: not to mention the great probability of every individual sufferer persuading himself, to his violent indignation, that but for the example of all the other sufferers he never would have put himself in the way of suffering. Because such a declaration as Clennam’s, made at such a time, would certainly draw down upon him a storm of animosity, rendering it impossible to calculate on forbearance in the creditors, or on unanimity among them; and exposing him a solitary target to a straggling cross-fire, which might bring him down from half-a-dozen quarters at once.\n\nTo all this Clennam merely replied that, granting the whole protest, nothing in it lessened the force, or could lessen the force, of the voluntary and public exoneration of his partner. He therefore, once and for all, requested Mr Rugg’s immediate aid in getting the business despatched. Upon that, Mr Rugg fell to work; and Arthur, retaining no property to himself but his clothes and books, and a little loose money, placed his small private banker’s-account with the papers of the business.\n\nThe disclosure was made, and the storm raged fearfully. Thousands of people were wildly staring about for somebody alive to heap reproaches on; and this notable case, courting publicity, set the living somebody so much wanted, on a scaffold. When people who had nothing to do with the case were so sensible of its flagrancy, people who lost money by it could scarcely be expected to deal mildly with it. Letters of reproach and invective showered in from the creditors; and Mr Rugg, who sat upon the high stool every day and read them all, informed his client within a week that he feared there were writs out.\n\n‘I must take the consequences of what I have done,’ said Clennam. ‘The writs will find me here.’\n\nOn the very next morning, as he was turning in Bleeding Heart Yard by Mrs Plornish’s corner, Mrs Plornish stood at the door waiting for him, and mysteriously besought him to step into Happy Cottage. There he found Mr Rugg.\n\n‘I thought I’d wait for you here. I wouldn’t go on to the Counting-house this morning if I was you, sir.’\n\n‘Why not, Mr Rugg?’\n\n‘There are as many as five out, to my knowledge.’\n\n‘It cannot be too soon over,’ said Clennam. ‘Let them take me at once.’\n\n‘Yes, but,’ said Mr Rugg, getting between him and the door, ‘hear reason, hear reason. They’ll take you soon enough, Mr Clennam, I don’t doubt; but, hear reason. It almost always happens, in these cases, that some insignificant matter pushes itself in front and makes much of itself. Now, I find there’s a little one out--a mere Palace Court jurisdiction--and I have reason to believe that a caption may be made upon that. I wouldn’t be taken upon that.’\n\n‘Why not?’ asked Clennam.\n\n‘I’d be taken on a full-grown one, sir,’ said Mr Rugg. ‘It’s as well to keep up appearances. As your professional adviser, I should prefer your being taken on a writ from one of the Superior Courts, if you have no objection to do me that favour. It looks better.’\n\n‘Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur, in his dejection, ‘my only wish is, that it should be over. I will go on, and take my chance.’\n\n‘Another word of reason, sir!’ cried Mr Rugg. ‘Now, this _is_ reason. The other may be taste; but this is reason. If you should be taken on a little one, sir, you would go to the Marshalsea. Now, you know what the Marshalsea is. Very close. Excessively confined. Whereas in the King’s Bench--’ Mr Rugg waved his right hand freely, as expressing abundance of space.\n\n‘I would rather,’ said Clennam, ‘be taken to the Marshalsea than to any other prison.’\n\n‘Do you say so indeed, sir?’ returned Mr Rugg. ‘Then this is taste, too, and we may be walking.’\n\nHe was a little offended at first, but he soon overlooked it. They walked through the Yard to the other end. The Bleeding Hearts were more interested in Arthur since his reverses than formerly; now regarding him as one who was true to the place and had taken up his freedom. Many of them came out to look after him, and to observe to one another, with great unctuousness, that he was ‘pulled down by it.’ Mrs Plornish and her father stood at the top of the steps at their own end, much depressed and shaking their heads.\n\nThere was nobody visibly in waiting when Arthur and Mr Rugg arrived at the Counting-house. But an elderly member of the Jewish persuasion, preserved in rum, followed them close, and looked in at the glass before Mr Rugg had opened one of the day’s letters. ‘Oh!’ said Mr Rugg, looking up. ‘How do you do? Step in--Mr Clennam, I think this is the gentleman I was mentioning.’\n\nThis gentleman explained the object of his visit to be ‘a tyfling madder ob bithznithz,’ and executed his legal function.\n\n‘Shall I accompany you, Mr Clennam?’ asked Mr Rugg politely, rubbing his hands.\n\n‘I would rather go alone, thank you. Be so good as send me my clothes.’ Mr Rugg in a light airy way replied in the affirmative, and shook hands with him. He and his attendant then went down-stairs, got into the first conveyance they found, and drove to the old gates.\n\n‘Where I little thought, Heaven forgive me,’ said Clennam to himself, ‘that I should ever enter thus!’\n\nMr Chivery was on the Lock, and Young John was in the Lodge: either newly released from it, or waiting to take his own spell of duty. Both were more astonished on seeing who the prisoner was, than one might have thought turnkeys would have been. The elder Mr Chivery shook hands with him in a shame-faced kind of way, and said, ‘I don’t call to mind, sir, as I was ever less glad to see you.’ The younger Mr Chivery, more distant, did not shake hands with him at all; he stood looking at him in a state of indecision so observable that it even came within the observation of Clennam with his heavy eyes and heavy heart. Presently afterwards, Young John disappeared into the jail.\n\nAs Clennam knew enough of the place to know that he was required to remain in the Lodge a certain time, he took a seat in a corner, and feigned to be occupied with the perusal of letters from his pocket. They did not so engross his attention, but that he saw, with gratitude, how the elder Mr Chivery kept the Lodge clear of prisoners; how he signed to some, with his keys, not to come in, how he nudged others with his elbows to go out, and how he made his misery as easy to him as he could.\n\nArthur was sitting with his eyes fixed on the floor, recalling the past, brooding over the present, and not attending to either, when he felt himself touched upon the shoulder. It was by Young John; and he said, ‘You can come now.’\n\nHe got up and followed Young John. When they had gone a step or two within the inner iron-gate, Young John turned and said to him:\n\n‘You want a room. I have got you one.’\n\n‘I thank you heartily.’\n\nYoung John turned again, and took him in at the old doorway, up the old staircase, into the old room. Arthur stretched out his hand. Young John looked at it, looked at him--sternly--swelled, choked, and said:\n\n‘I don’t know as I can. No, I find I can’t. But I thought you’d like the room, and here it is for you.’\n\nSurprise at this inconsistent behaviour yielded when he was gone (he went away directly) to the feelings which the empty room awakened in Clennam’s wounded breast, and to the crowding associations with the one good and gentle creature who had sanctified it. Her absence in his altered fortunes made it, and him in it, so very desolate and so much in need of such a face of love and truth, that he turned against the wall to weep, sobbing out, as his heart relieved itself, ‘O my Little Dorrit!’\n\n# CHAPTER 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea\n\nThe day was sunny, and the Marshalsea, with the hot noon striking upon it, was unwontedly quiet. Arthur Clennam dropped into a solitary arm-chair, itself as faded as any debtor in the jail, and yielded himself to his thoughts.\n\nIn the unnatural peace of having gone through the dreaded arrest, and got there,--the first change of feeling which the prison most commonly induced, and from which dangerous resting-place so many men had slipped down to the depths of degradation and disgrace by so many ways,--he could think of some passages in his life, almost as if he were removed from them into another state of existence. Taking into account where he was, the interest that had first brought him there when he had been free to keep away, and the gentle presence that was equally inseparable from the walls and bars about him and from the impalpable remembrances of his later life which no walls or bars could imprison, it was not remarkable that everything his memory turned upon should bring him round again to Little Dorrit. Yet it was remarkable to him; not because of the fact itself, but because of the reminder it brought with it, how much the dear little creature had influenced his better resolutions.\n\nNone of us clearly know to whom or to what we are indebted in this wise, until some marked stop in the whirling wheel of life brings the right perception with it. It comes with sickness, it comes with sorrow, it comes with the loss of the dearly loved, it is one of the most frequent uses of adversity. It came to Clennam in his adversity, strongly and tenderly. ‘When I first gathered myself together,’ he thought, ‘and set something like purpose before my jaded eyes, whom had I before me, toiling on, for a good object’s sake, without encouragement, without notice, against ignoble obstacles that would have turned an army of received heroes and heroines? One weak girl! When I tried to conquer my misplaced love, and to be generous to the man who was more fortunate than I, though he should never know it or repay me with a gracious word, in whom had I watched patience, self-denial, self-subdual, charitable construction, the noblest generosity of the affections? In the same poor girl! If I, a man, with a man’s advantages and means and energies, had slighted the whisper in my heart, that if my father had erred, it was my first duty to conceal the fault and to repair it, what youthful figure with tender feet going almost bare on the damp ground, with spare hands ever working, with its slight shape but half protected from the sharp weather, would have stood before me to put me to shame? Little Dorrit’s.’ So always as he sat alone in the faded chair, thinking. Always, Little Dorrit. Until it seemed to him as if he met the reward of having wandered away from her, and suffered anything to pass between him and his remembrance of her virtues.\n\nHis door was opened, and the head of the elder Chivery was put in a very little way, without being turned towards him.\n\n‘I am off the Lock, Mr Clennam, and going out. Can I do anything for you?’\n\n‘Many thanks. Nothing.’\n\n‘You’ll excuse me opening the door,’ said Mr Chivery; ‘but I couldn’t make you hear.’\n\n‘Did you knock?’ ‘Half-a-dozen times.’\n\nRousing himself, Clennam observed that the prison had awakened from its noontide doze, that the inmates were loitering about the shady yard, and that it was late in the afternoon. He had been thinking for hours.\n\n‘Your things is come,’ said Mr Chivery, ‘and my son is going to carry ‘em up. I should have sent ‘em up but for his wishing to carry ‘em himself. Indeed he would have ‘em himself, and so I couldn’t send ‘em up. Mr Clennam, could I say a word to you?’\n\n‘Pray come in,’ said Arthur; for Mr Chivery’s head was still put in at the door a very little way, and Mr Chivery had but one ear upon him, instead of both eyes. This was native delicacy in Mr Chivery--true politeness; though his exterior had very much of a turnkey about it, and not the least of a gentleman.\n\n‘Thank you, sir,’ said Mr Chivery, without advancing; ‘it’s no odds me coming in. Mr Clennam, don’t you take no notice of my son (if you’ll be so good) in case you find him cut up anyways difficult. My son has a ‘art, and my son’s ‘art is in the right place. Me and his mother knows where to find it, and we find it sitiwated correct.’\n\nWith this mysterious speech, Mr Chivery took his ear away and shut the door. He might have been gone ten minutes, when his son succeeded him.\n\n‘Here’s your portmanteau,’ he said to Arthur, putting it carefully down.\n\n‘It’s very kind of you. I am ashamed that you should have the trouble.’\n\nHe was gone before it came to that; but soon returned, saying exactly as before, ‘Here’s your black box:’ which he also put down with care.\n\n‘I am very sensible of this attention. I hope we may shake hands now, Mr John.’\n\nYoung John, however, drew back, turning his right wrist in a socket made of his left thumb and middle-finger and said as he had said at first, ‘I don’t know as I can. No; I find I can’t!’ He then stood regarding the prisoner sternly, though with a swelling humour in his eyes that looked like pity.\n\n‘Why are you angry with me,’ said Clennam, ‘and yet so ready to do me these kind services? There must be some mistake between us. If I have done anything to occasion it I am sorry.’\n\n‘No mistake, sir,’ returned John, turning the wrist backwards and forwards in the socket, for which it was rather tight. ‘No mistake, sir, in the feelings with which my eyes behold you at the present moment! If I was at all fairly equal to your weight, Mr Clennam--which I am not; and if you weren’t under a cloud--which you are; and if it wasn’t against all rules of the Marshalsea--which it is; those feelings are such, that they would stimulate me, more to having it out with you in a Round on the present spot than to anything else I could name.’\n\nArthur looked at him for a moment in some wonder, and some little anger. ‘Well, well!’ he said. ‘A mistake, a mistake!’ Turning away, he sat down with a heavy sigh in the faded chair again.\n\nYoung John followed him with his eyes, and, after a short pause, cried out, ‘I beg your pardon!’\n\n‘Freely granted,’ said Clennam, waving his hand without raising his sunken head. ‘Say no more. I am not worth it.’\n\n‘This furniture, sir,’ said Young John in a voice of mild and soft explanation, ‘belongs to me. I am in the habit of letting it out to parties without furniture, that have the room. It an’t much, but it’s at your service. Free, I mean. I could not think of letting you have it on any other terms. You’re welcome to it for nothing.’\n\nArthur raised his head again to thank him, and to say he could not accept the favour. John was still turning his wrist, and still contending with himself in his former divided manner.\n\n‘What is the matter between us?’ said Arthur.\n\n‘I decline to name it, sir,’ returned Young John, suddenly turning loud and sharp. ‘Nothing’s the matter.’\n\nArthur looked at him again, in vain, for an explanation of his behaviour. After a while, Arthur turned away his head again. Young John said, presently afterwards, with the utmost mildness:\n\n‘The little round table, sir, that’s nigh your elbow, was--you know whose--I needn’t mention him--he died a great gentleman. I bought it of an individual that he gave it to, and that lived here after him. But the individual wasn’t any ways equal to him. Most individuals would find it hard to come up to his level.’\n\nArthur drew the little table nearer, rested his arm upon it, and kept it there.\n\n‘Perhaps you may not be aware, sir,’ said Young John, ‘that I intruded upon him when he was over here in London. On the whole he was of opinion that it _was_ an intrusion, though he was so good as to ask me to sit down and to inquire after father and all other old friends. Leastways humblest acquaintances. He looked, to me, a good deal changed, and I said so when I came back. I asked him if Miss Amy was well--’\n‘And she was?’\n\n‘I should have thought you would have known without putting the question to such as me,’ returned Young John, after appearing to take a large invisible pill. ‘Since you do put me the question, I am sorry I can’t answer it. But the truth is, he looked upon the inquiry as a liberty, and said, “What was that to me?” It was then I became quite aware I was intruding: of which I had been fearful before. However, he spoke very handsome afterwards; very handsome.’\n\nThey were both silent for several minutes: except that Young John remarked, at about the middle of the pause, ‘He both spoke and acted very handsome.’\n\nIt was again Young John who broke the silence by inquiring:\n\n‘If it’s not a liberty, how long may it be your intentions, sir, to go without eating and drinking?’\n\n‘I have not felt the want of anything yet,’ returned Clennam. ‘I have no appetite just now.’\n\n‘The more reason why you should take some support, sir,’ urged Young John. ‘If you find yourself going on sitting here for hours and hours partaking of no refreshment because you have no appetite, why then you should and must partake of refreshment without an appetite. I’m going to have tea in my own apartment. If it’s not a liberty, please to come and take a cup. Or I can bring a tray here in two minutes.’\n\nFeeling that Young John would impose that trouble on himself if he refused, and also feeling anxious to show that he bore in mind both the elder Mr Chivery’s entreaty, and the younger Mr Chivery’s apology, Arthur rose and expressed his willingness to take a cup of tea in Mr John’s apartment. Young John locked his door for him as they went out, slided the key into his pocket with great dexterity, and led the way to his own residence.\n\nIt was at the top of the house nearest to the gateway. It was the room to which Clennam had hurried on the day when the enriched family had left the prison for ever, and where he had lifted her insensible from the floor. He foresaw where they were going as soon as their feet touched the staircase. The room was so far changed that it was papered now, and had been repainted, and was far more comfortably furnished; but he could recall it just as he had seen it in that single glance, when he raised her from the ground and carried her down to the carriage.\n\nYoung John looked hard at him, biting his fingers.\n\n‘I see you recollect the room, Mr Clennam?’\n\n‘I recollect it well, Heaven bless her!’\n\nOblivious of the tea, Young John continued to bite his fingers and to look at his visitor, as long as his visitor continued to glance about the room. Finally, he made a start at the teapot, gustily rattled a quantity of tea into it from a canister, and set off for the common kitchen to fill it with hot water.\n\nThe room was so eloquent to Clennam in the changed circumstances of his return to the miserable Marshalsea; it spoke to him so mournfully of her, and of his loss of her; that it would have gone hard with him to resist it, even though he had not been alone. Alone, he did not try. He had his hand on the insensible wall as tenderly as if it had been herself that he touched, and pronounced her name in a low voice. He stood at the window, looking over the prison-parapet with its grim spiked border, and breathed a benediction through the summer haze towards the distant land where she was rich and prosperous.\n\nYoung John was some time absent, and, when he came back, showed that he had been outside by bringing with him fresh butter in a cabbage leaf, some thin slices of boiled ham in another cabbage leaf, and a little basket of water-cresses and salad herbs. When these were arranged upon the table to his satisfaction, they sat down to tea.\n\nClennam tried to do honour to the meal, but unavailingly. The ham sickened him, the bread seemed to turn to sand in his mouth. He could force nothing upon himself but a cup of tea.\n\n‘Try a little something green,’ said Young John, handing him the basket.\n\nHe took a sprig or so of water-cress, and tried again; but the bread turned to a heavier sand than before, and the ham (though it was good enough of itself) seemed to blow a faint simoom of ham through the whole Marshalsea.\n\n‘Try a little more something green, sir,’ said Young John; and again handed the basket.\n\nIt was so like handing green meat into the cage of a dull imprisoned bird, and John had so evidently brought the little basket as a handful of fresh relief from the stale hot paving-stones and bricks of the jail, that Clennam said, with a smile, ‘It was very kind of you to think of putting this between the wires; but I cannot even get this down to-day.’\n\nAs if the difficulty were contagious, Young John soon pushed away his own plate, and fell to folding the cabbage-leaf that had contained the ham. When he had folded it into a number of layers, one over another, so that it was small in the palm of his hand, he began to flatten it between both his hands, and to eye Clennam attentively.\n\n‘I wonder,’ he at length said, compressing his green packet with some force, ‘that if it’s not worth your while to take care of yourself for your own sake, it’s not worth doing for some one else’s.’\n\n‘Truly,’ returned Arthur, with a sigh and a smile, ‘I don’t know for whose.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam,’ said John, warmly, ‘I am surprised that a gentleman who is capable of the straightforwardness that you are capable of, should be capable of the mean action of making me such an answer. Mr Clennam, I am surprised that a gentleman who is capable of having a heart of his own, should be capable of the heartlessness of treating mine in that way. I am astonished at it, sir. Really and truly I am astonished!’\n\nHaving got upon his feet to emphasise his concluding words, Young John sat down again, and fell to rolling his green packet on his right leg; never taking his eyes off Clennam, but surveying him with a fixed look of indignant reproach.\n\n‘I had got over it, sir,’ said John. ‘I had conquered it, knowing that it _must_ be conquered, and had come to the resolution to think no more about it. I shouldn’t have given my mind to it again, I hope, if to this prison you had not been brought, and in an hour unfortunate for me, this day!’ (In his agitation Young John adopted his mother’s powerful construction of sentences.) ‘When you first came upon me, sir, in the Lodge, this day, more as if a Upas tree had been made a capture of than a private defendant, such mingled streams of feelings broke loose again within me, that everything was for the first few minutes swept away before them, and I was going round and round in a vortex. I got out of it. I struggled, and got out of it. If it was the last word I had to speak, against that vortex with my utmost powers I strove, and out of it I came. I argued that if I had been rude, apologies was due, and those apologies without a question of demeaning, I did make. And now, when I’ve been so wishful to show that one thought is next to being a holy one with me and goes before all others--now, after all, you dodge me when I ever so gently hint at it, and throw me back upon myself. For, do not, sir,’ said Young John, ‘do not be so base as to deny that dodge you do, and thrown me back upon myself you have!’\n\nAll amazement, Arthur gazed at him like one lost, only saying, ‘What is it? What do you mean, John?’ But, John, being in that state of mind in which nothing would seem to be more impossible to a certain class of people than the giving of an answer, went ahead blindly.\n\n‘I hadn’t,’ John declared, ‘no, I hadn’t, and I never had the audaciousness to think, I am sure, that all was anything but lost. I hadn’t, no, why should I say I hadn’t if I ever had, any hope that it was possible to be so blest, not after the words that passed, not even if barriers insurmountable had not been raised! But is that a reason why I am to have no memory, why I am to have no thoughts, why I am to have no sacred spots, nor anything?’\n\n‘What can you mean?’ cried Arthur.\n\n‘It’s all very well to trample on it, sir,’ John went on, scouring a very prairie of wild words, ‘if a person can make up his mind to be guilty of the action. It’s all very well to trample on it, but it’s there. It may be that it couldn’t be trampled upon if it wasn’t there. But that doesn’t make it gentlemanly, that doesn’t make it honourable, that doesn’t justify throwing a person back upon himself after he has struggled and strived out of himself like a butterfly. The world may sneer at a turnkey, but he’s a man--when he isn’t a woman, which among female criminals he’s expected to be.’\n\nRidiculous as the incoherence of his talk was, there was yet a truthfulness in Young John’s simple, sentimental character, and a sense of being wounded in some very tender respect, expressed in his burning face and in the agitation of his voice and manner, which Arthur must have been cruel to disregard. He turned his thoughts back to the starting-point of this unknown injury; and in the meantime Young John, having rolled his green packet pretty round, cut it carefully into three pieces, and laid it on a plate as if it were some particular delicacy.\n\n‘It seems to me just possible,’ said Arthur, when he had retraced the conversation to the water-cresses and back again, ‘that you have made some reference to Miss Dorrit.’\n\n‘It is just possible, sir,’ returned John Chivery.\n\n‘I don’t understand it. I hope I may not be so unlucky as to make you think I mean to offend you again, for I never have meant to offend you yet, when I say I don’t understand it.’\n\n‘Sir,’ said Young John, ‘will you have the perfidy to deny that you know and long have known that I felt towards Miss Dorrit, call it not the presumption of love, but adoration and sacrifice?’\n\n‘Indeed, John, I will not have any perfidy if I know it; why you should suspect me of it I am at a loss to think. Did you ever hear from Mrs Chivery, your mother, that I went to see her once?’\n\n‘No, sir,’ returned John, shortly. ‘Never heard of such a thing.’\n\n‘But I did. Can you imagine why?’\n\n‘No, sir,’ returned John, shortly. ‘I can’t imagine why.’\n\n‘I will tell you. I was solicitous to promote Miss Dorrit’s happiness; and if I could have supposed that Miss Dorrit returned your affection--’\n\nPoor John Chivery turned crimson to the tips of his ears. ‘Miss Dorrit never did, sir. I wish to be honourable and true, so far as in my humble way I can, and I would scorn to pretend for a moment that she ever did, or that she ever led me to believe she did; no, nor even that it was ever to be expected in any cool reason that she would or could. She was far above me in all respects at all times. As likewise,’ added John, ‘similarly was her gen-teel family.’\n\nHis chivalrous feeling towards all that belonged to her made him so very respectable, in spite of his small stature and his rather weak legs, and his very weak hair, and his poetical temperament, that a Goliath might have sat in his place demanding less consideration at Arthur’s hands.\n\n‘You speak, John,’ he said, with cordial admiration, ‘like a Man.’\n\n‘Well, sir,’ returned John, brushing his hand across his eyes, ‘then I wish you’d do the same.’\n\nHe was quick with this unexpected retort, and it again made Arthur regard him with a wondering expression of face.\n\n‘Leastways,’ said John, stretching his hand across the tea-tray, ‘if too strong a remark, withdrawn! But, why not, why not? When I say to you, Mr Clennam, take care of yourself for some one else’s sake, why not be open, though a turnkey? Why did I get you the room which I knew you’d like best? Why did I carry up your things? Not that I found ‘em heavy; I don’t mention ‘em on that accounts; far from it. Why have I cultivated you in the manner I have done since the morning? On the ground of your own merits? No. They’re very great, I’ve no doubt at all; but not on the ground of them. Another’s merits have had their weight, and have had far more weight with Me. Then why not speak free?’\n\n‘Unaffectedly, John,’ said Clennam, ‘you are so good a fellow and I have so true a respect for your character, that if I have appeared to be less sensible than I really am of the fact that the kind services you have rendered me to-day are attributable to my having been trusted by Miss Dorrit as her friend--I confess it to be a fault, and I ask your forgiveness.’\n\n‘Oh! why not,’ John repeated with returning scorn, ‘why not speak free!’\n\n‘I declare to you,’ returned Arthur, ‘that I do not understand you. Look at me. Consider the trouble I have been in. Is it likely that I would wilfully add to my other self-reproaches, that of being ungrateful or treacherous to you. I do not understand you.’\n\nJohn’s incredulous face slowly softened into a face of doubt. He rose, backed into the garret-window of the room, beckoned Arthur to come there, and stood looking at him thoughtfully.\n\n‘Mr Clennam, do you mean to say that you don’t know?’\n\n‘What, John?’\n\n‘Lord,’ said Young John, appealing with a gasp to the spikes on the wall. ‘He says, What!’\n\nClennam looked at the spikes, and looked at John; and looked at the spikes, and looked at John.\n\n‘He says What! And what is more,’ exclaimed Young John, surveying him in a doleful maze, ‘he appears to mean it! Do you see this window, sir?’\n\n‘Of course I see this window.’\n\n‘See this room?’\n\n‘Why, of course I see this room.’\n\n‘That wall opposite, and that yard down below? They have all been witnesses of it, from day to day, from night to night, from week to week, from month to month. For how often have I seen Miss Dorrit here when she has not seen me!’\n\n‘Witnesses of what?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Of Miss Dorrit’s love.’\n\n‘For whom?’\n\n‘You,’ said John. And touched him with the back of his hand upon the breast, and backed to his chair, and sat down on it with a pale face, holding the arms, and shaking his head at him.\n\nIf he had dealt Clennam a heavy blow, instead of laying that light touch upon him, its effect could not have been to shake him more. He stood amazed; his eyes looking at John; his lips parted, and seeming now and then to form the word ‘Me!’ without uttering it; his hands dropped at his sides; his whole appearance that of a man who has been awakened from sleep, and stupefied by intelligence beyond his full comprehension.\n\n‘Me!’ he at length said aloud.\n\n‘Ah!’ groaned Young John. ‘You!’\n\nHe did what he could to muster a smile, and returned, ‘Your fancy. You are completely mistaken.’\n\n‘I mistaken, sir!’ said Young John. ‘_I_ completely mistaken on that subject! No, Mr Clennam, don’t tell me so. On any other, if you like, for I don’t set up to be a penetrating character, and am well aware of my own deficiencies. But, _I_ mistaken on a point that has caused me more smart in my breast than a flight of savages’ arrows could have done! _I_ mistaken on a point that almost sent me into my grave, as I sometimes wished it would, if the grave could only have been made compatible with the tobacco-business and father and mother’s feelings! I mistaken on a point that, even at the present moment, makes me take out my pocket-handkerchief like a great girl, as people say: though I am sure I don’t know why a great girl should be a term of reproach, for every rightly constituted male mind loves ‘em great and small. Don’t tell me so, don’t tell me so!’\n\nStill highly respectable at bottom, though absurd enough upon the surface, Young John took out his pocket-handkerchief with a genuine absence both of display and concealment, which is only to be seen in a man with a great deal of good in him, when he takes out his pocket-handkerchief for the purpose of wiping his eyes. Having dried them, and indulged in the harmless luxury of a sob and a sniff, he put it up again.\n\nThe touch was still in its influence so like a blow that Arthur could not get many words together to close the subject with. He assured John Chivery when he had returned his handkerchief to his pocket, that he did all honour to his disinterestedness and to the fidelity of his remembrance of Miss Dorrit. As to the impression on his mind, of which he had just relieved it--here John interposed, and said, ‘No impression! Certainty!’--as to that, they might perhaps speak of it at another time, but would say no more now. Feeling low-spirited and weary, he would go back to his room, with John’s leave, and come out no more that night. John assented, and he crept back in the shadow of the wall to his own lodging.\n\nThe feeling of the blow was still so strong upon him that, when the dirty old woman was gone whom he found sitting on the stairs outside his door, waiting to make his bed, and who gave him to understand while doing it, that she had received her instructions from Mr Chivery, ‘not the old ‘un but the young ‘un,’ he sat down in the faded arm-chair, pressing his head between his hands, as if he had been stunned. Little Dorrit love him! More bewildering to him than his misery, far.\n\nConsider the improbability. He had been accustomed to call her his child, and his dear child, and to invite her confidence by dwelling upon the difference in their respective ages, and to speak of himself as one who was turning old. Yet she might not have thought him old. Something reminded him that he had not thought himself so, until the roses had floated away upon the river.\n\nHe had her two letters among other papers in his box, and he took them out and read them. There seemed to be a sound in them like the sound of her sweet voice. It fell upon his ear with many tones of tenderness, that were not insusceptible of the new meaning. Now it was that the quiet desolation of her answer, ‘No, No, No,’ made to him that night in that very room--that night when he had been shown the dawn of her altered fortune, and when other words had passed between them which he had been destined to remember in humiliation and a prisoner, rushed into his mind.\n\nConsider the improbability.\n\nBut it had a preponderating tendency, when considered, to become fainter. There was another and a curious inquiry of his own heart’s that concurrently became stronger. In the reluctance he had felt to believe that she loved any one; in his desire to set that question at rest; in a half-formed consciousness he had had that there would be a kind of nobleness in his helping her love for any one, was there no suppressed something on his own side that he had hushed as it arose? Had he ever whispered to himself that he must not think of such a thing as her loving him, that he must not take advantage of her gratitude, that he must keep his experience in remembrance as a warning and reproof; that he must regard such youthful hopes as having passed away, as his friend’s dead daughter had passed away; that he must be steady in saying to himself that the time had gone by him, and he was too saddened and old?\n\nHe had kissed her when he raised her from the ground on the day when she had been so consistently and expressively forgotten. Quite as he might have kissed her, if she had been conscious? No difference?\n\nThe darkness found him occupied with these thoughts. The darkness also found Mr and Mrs Plornish knocking at his door. They brought with them a basket, filled with choice selections from that stock in trade which met with such a quick sale and produced such a slow return. Mrs Plornish was affected to tears. Mr Plornish amiably growled, in his philosophical but not lucid manner, that there was ups you see, and there was downs. It was in vain to ask why ups, why downs; there they was, you know. He had heerd it given for a truth that accordin’ as the world went round, which round it did rewolve undoubted, even the best of gentlemen must take his turn of standing with his ed upside down and all his air a flying the wrong way into what you might call Space. Wery well then. What Mr Plornish said was, wery well then. That gentleman’s ed would come up-ards when his turn come, that gentleman’s air would be a pleasure to look upon being all smooth again, and wery well then!\n\nIt has been already stated that Mrs Plornish, not being philosophical, wept. It further happened that Mrs Plornish, not being philosophical, was intelligible. It may have arisen out of her softened state of mind, out of her sex’s wit, out of a woman’s quick association of ideas, or out of a woman’s no association of ideas, but it further happened somehow that Mrs Plornish’s intelligibility displayed itself upon the very subject of Arthur’s meditations.\n\n‘The way father has been talking about you, Mr Clennam,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘you hardly would believe. It’s made him quite poorly. As to his voice, this misfortune has took it away. You know what a sweet singer father is; but he couldn’t get a note out for the children at tea, if you’ll credit what I tell you.’\n\nWhile speaking, Mrs Plornish shook her head, and wiped her eyes, and looked retrospectively about the room.\n\n‘As to Mr Baptist,’ pursued Mrs Plornish, ‘whatever he’ll do when he comes to know of it, I can’t conceive nor yet imagine. He’d have been here before now, you may be sure, but that he’s away on confidential business of your own. The persevering manner in which he follows up that business, and gives himself no rest from it--it really do,’ said Mrs Plornish, winding up in the Italian manner, ‘as I say to him, Mooshattonisha padrona.’\n\nThough not conceited, Mrs Plornish felt that she had turned this Tuscan sentence with peculiar elegance. Mr Plornish could not conceal his exultation in her accomplishments as a linguist.\n\n‘But what I say is, Mr Clennam,’ the good woman went on, ‘there’s always something to be thankful for, as I am sure you will yourself admit. Speaking in this room, it’s not hard to think what the present something is. It’s a thing to be thankful for, indeed, that Miss Dorrit is not here to know it.’\n\nArthur thought she looked at him with particular expression.\n\n‘It’s a thing,’ reiterated Mrs Plornish, ‘to be thankful for, indeed, that Miss Dorrit is far away. It’s to be hoped she is not likely to hear of it. If she had been here to see it, sir, it’s not to be doubted that the sight of you,’ Mrs Plornish repeated those words--‘not to be doubted, that the sight of you--in misfortune and trouble, would have been almost too much for her affectionate heart. There’s nothing I can think of, that would have touched Miss Dorrit so bad as that.’\n\nOf a certainty Mrs Plornish did look at him now, with a sort of quivering defiance in her friendly emotion.\n\n‘Yes!’ said she. ‘And it shows what notice father takes, though at his time of life, that he says to me this afternoon, which Happy Cottage knows I neither make it up nor any ways enlarge, “Mary, it’s much to be rejoiced in that Miss Dorrit is not on the spot to behold it.” Those were father’s words. Father’s own words was, “Much to be rejoiced in, Mary, that Miss Dorrit is not on the spot to behold it.” I says to father then, I says to him, “Father, you are right!” That,’ Mrs Plornish concluded, with the air of a very precise legal witness, ‘is what passed betwixt father and me. And I tell you nothing but what did pass betwixt me and father.’\n\nMr Plornish, as being of a more laconic temperament, embraced this opportunity of interposing with the suggestion that she should now leave Mr Clennam to himself. ‘For, you see,’ said Mr Plornish, gravely, ‘I know what it is, old gal;’ repeating that valuable remark several times, as if it appeared to him to include some great moral secret. Finally, the worthy couple went away arm in arm.\n\nLittle Dorrit, Little Dorrit. Again, for hours. Always Little Dorrit!\n\nHappily, if it ever had been so, it was over, and better over. Granted that she had loved him, and he had known it and had suffered himself to love her, what a road to have led her away upon--the road that would have brought her back to this miserable place! He ought to be much comforted by the reflection that she was quit of it forever; that she was, or would soon be, married (vague rumours of her father’s projects in that direction had reached Bleeding Heart Yard, with the news of her sister’s marriage); and that the Marshalsea gate had shut for ever on all those perplexed possibilities of a time that was gone.\n\nDear Little Dorrit.\n\nLooking back upon his own poor story, she was its vanishing-point. Every thing in its perspective led to her innocent figure. He had travelled thousands of miles towards it; previous unquiet hopes and doubts had worked themselves out before it; it was the centre of the interest of his life; it was the termination of everything that was good and pleasant in it; beyond, there was nothing but mere waste and darkened sky.\n\nAs ill at ease as on the first night of his lying down to sleep within those dreary walls, he wore the night out with such thoughts. What time Young John lay wrapt in peaceful slumber, after composing and arranging the following monumental inscription on his pillow--\n\nSTRANGER! RESPECT THE TOMB OF JOHN CHIVERY, JUNIOR, WHO DIED AT AN ADVANCED AGE NOT NECESSARY TO MENTION. HE ENCOUNTERED HIS RIVAL IN A DISTRESSED STATE, AND FELT INCLINED TO HAVE A ROUND WITH HIM; BUT, FOR THE SAKE OF THE LOVED ONE, CONQUERED THOSE FEELINGS OF BITTERNESS, AND BECAME MAGNANIMOUS.\n\n# CHAPTER 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea\n\nThe opinion of the community outside the prison gates bore hard on Clennam as time went on, and he made no friends among the community within. Too depressed to associate with the herd in the yard, who got together to forget their cares; too retiring and too unhappy to join in the poor socialities of the tavern; he kept his own room, and was held in distrust. Some said he was proud; some objected that he was sullen and reserved; some were contemptuous of him, for that he was a poor-spirited dog who pined under his debts. The whole population were shy of him on these various counts of indictment, but especially the last, which involved a species of domestic treason; and he soon became so confirmed in his seclusion, that his only time for walking up and down was when the evening Club were assembled at their songs and toasts and sentiments, and when the yard was nearly left to the women and children.\n\nImprisonment began to tell upon him. He knew that he idled and moped. After what he had known of the influences of imprisonment within the four small walls of the very room he occupied, this consciousness made him afraid of himself. Shrinking from the observation of other men, and shrinking from his own, he began to change very sensibly. Anybody might see that the shadow of the wall was dark upon him.\n\nOne day when he might have been some ten or twelve weeks in jail, and when he had been trying to read and had not been able to release even the imaginary people of the book from the Marshalsea, a footstep stopped at his door, and a hand tapped at it. He arose and opened it, and an agreeable voice accosted him with ‘How do you do, Mr Clennam? I hope I am not unwelcome in calling to see you.’\n\nIt was the sprightly young Barnacle, Ferdinand. He looked very good-natured and prepossessing, though overpoweringly gay and free, in contrast with the squalid prison.\n\n‘You are surprised to see me, Mr Clennam,’ he said, taking the seat which Clennam offered him.\n\n‘I must confess to being much surprised.’\n\n‘Not disagreeably, I hope?’\n\n‘By no means.’\n\n‘Thank you. Frankly,’ said the engaging young Barnacle, ‘I have been excessively sorry to hear that you were under the necessity of a temporary retirement here, and I hope (of course as between two private gentlemen) that our place has had nothing to do with it?’\n\n‘Your office?’\n\n‘Our Circumlocution place.’\n\n‘I cannot charge any part of my reverses upon that remarkable establishment.’\n\n‘Upon my life,’ said the vivacious young Barnacle, ‘I am heartily glad to know it. It is quite a relief to me to hear you say it. I should have so exceedingly regretted our place having had anything to do with your difficulties.’\n\nClennam again assured him that he absolved it of the responsibility.\n\n‘That’s right,’ said Ferdinand. ‘I am very happy to hear it. I was rather afraid in my own mind that we might have helped to floor you, because there is no doubt that it is our misfortune to do that kind of thing now and then. We don’t want to do it; but if men will be gravelled, why--we can’t help it.’\n\n‘Without giving an unqualified assent to what you say,’ returned Arthur, gloomily, ‘I am much obliged to you for your interest in me.’\n\n‘No, but really! Our place is,’ said the easy young Barnacle, ‘the most inoffensive place possible. You’ll say we are a humbug. I won’t say we are not; but all that sort of thing is intended to be, and must be. Don’t you see?’\n\n‘I do not,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘You don’t regard it from the right point of view. It is the point of view that is the essential thing. Regard our place from the point of view that we only ask you to leave us alone, and we are as capital a Department as you’ll find anywhere.’\n\n‘Is your place there to be left alone?’ asked Clennam.\n\n‘You exactly hit it,’ returned Ferdinand. ‘It is there with the express intention that everything shall be left alone. That is what it means. That is what it’s for. No doubt there’s a certain form to be kept up that it’s for something else, but it’s only a form. Why, good Heaven, we are nothing but forms! Think what a lot of our forms you have gone through. And you have never got any nearer to an end?’\n\n‘Never,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Look at it from the right point of view, and there you have us--official and effectual. It’s like a limited game of cricket. A field of outsiders are always going in to bowl at the Public Service, and we block the balls.’\n\nClennam asked what became of the bowlers? The airy young Barnacle replied that they grew tired, got dead beat, got lamed, got their backs broken, died off, gave it up, went in for other games.\n\n‘And this occasions me to congratulate myself again,’ he pursued, ‘on the circumstance that our place has had nothing to do with your temporary retirement. It very easily might have had a hand in it; because it is undeniable that we are sometimes a most unlucky place, in our effects upon people who will not leave us alone. Mr Clennam, I am quite unreserved with you. As between yourself and myself, I know I may be. I was so, when I first saw you making the mistake of not leaving us alone; because I perceived that you were inexperienced and sanguine, and had--I hope you’ll not object to my saying--some simplicity?’\n\n‘Not at all.’\n\n‘Some simplicity. Therefore I felt what a pity it was, and I went out of my way to hint to you (which really was not official, but I never am official when I can help it) something to the effect that if I were you, I wouldn’t bother myself. However, you did bother yourself, and you have since bothered yourself. Now, don’t do it any more.’\n\n‘I am not likely to have the opportunity,’ said Clennam.\n\n‘Oh yes, you are! You’ll leave here. Everybody leaves here. There are no ends of ways of leaving here. Now, don’t come back to us. That entreaty is the second object of my call. Pray, don’t come back to us. Upon my honour,’ said Ferdinand in a very friendly and confiding way, ‘I shall be greatly vexed if you don’t take warning by the past and keep away from us.’\n\n‘And the invention?’ said Clennam.\n\n‘My good fellow,’ returned Ferdinand, ‘if you’ll excuse the freedom of that form of address, nobody wants to know of the invention, and nobody cares twopence-halfpenny about it.’\n\n‘Nobody in the Office, that is to say?’\n\n‘Nor out of it. Everybody is ready to dislike and ridicule any invention. You have no idea how many people want to be left alone. You have no idea how the Genius of the country (overlook the Parliamentary nature of the phrase, and don’t be bored by it) tends to being left alone. Believe me, Mr Clennam,’ said the sprightly young Barnacle in his pleasantest manner, ‘our place is not a wicked Giant to be charged at full tilt; but only a windmill showing you, as it grinds immense quantities of chaff, which way the country wind blows.’\n\n‘If I could believe that,’ said Clennam, ‘it would be a dismal prospect for all of us.’\n\n‘Oh! Don’t say so!’ returned Ferdinand. ‘It’s all right. We must have humbug, we all like humbug, we couldn’t get on without humbug. A little humbug, and a groove, and everything goes on admirably, if you leave it alone.’\n\nWith this hopeful confession of his faith as the head of the rising Barnacles who were born of woman, to be followed under a variety of watchwords which they utterly repudiated and disbelieved, Ferdinand rose. Nothing could be more agreeable than his frank and courteous bearing, or adapted with a more gentlemanly instinct to the circumstances of his visit.\n\n‘Is it fair to ask,’ he said, as Clennam gave him his hand with a real feeling of thankfulness for his candour and good-humour, ‘whether it is true that our late lamented Merdle is the cause of this passing inconvenience?’\n\n‘I am one of the many he has ruined. Yes.’\n\n‘He must have been an exceedingly clever fellow,’ said Ferdinand Barnacle.\n\nArthur, not being in the mood to extol the memory of the deceased, was silent.\n\n‘A consummate rascal, of course,’ said Ferdinand, ‘but remarkably clever! One cannot help admiring the fellow. Must have been such a master of humbug. Knew people so well--got over them so completely--did so much with them!’\n\nIn his easy way, he was really moved to genuine admiration.\n\n‘I hope,’ said Arthur, ‘that he and his dupes may be a warning to people not to have so much done with them again.’\n\n‘My dear Mr Clennam,’ returned Ferdinand, laughing, ‘have you really such a verdant hope? The next man who has as large a capacity and as genuine a taste for swindling, will succeed as well. Pardon me, but I think you really have no idea how the human bees will swarm to the beating of any old tin kettle; in that fact lies the complete manual of governing them. When they can be got to believe that the kettle is made of the precious metals, in that fact lies the whole power of men like our late lamented. No doubt there are here and there,’ said Ferdinand politely, ‘exceptional cases, where people have been taken in for what appeared to them to be much better reasons; and I need not go far to find such a case; but they don’t invalidate the rule. Good day! I hope that when I have the pleasure of seeing you, next, this passing cloud will have given place to sunshine. Don’t come a step beyond the door. I know the way out perfectly. Good day!’\n\nWith those words, the best and brightest of the Barnacles went down-stairs, hummed his way through the Lodge, mounted his horse in the front court-yard, and rode off to keep an appointment with his noble kinsman, who wanted a little coaching before he could triumphantly answer certain infidel Snobs who were going to question the Nobs about their statesmanship.\n\nHe must have passed Mr Rugg on his way out, for, a minute or two afterwards, that ruddy-headed gentleman shone in at the door, like an elderly Phoebus.\n\n‘How do you do to-day, sir?’ said Mr Rugg. ‘Is there any little thing I can do for you to-day, sir?’\n\n‘No, I thank you.’\n\nMr Rugg’s enjoyment of embarrassed affairs was like a housekeeper’s enjoyment in pickling and preserving, or a washerwoman’s enjoyment of a heavy wash, or a dustman’s enjoyment of an overflowing dust-bin, or any other professional enjoyment of a mess in the way of business.\n\n‘I still look round, from time to time, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, cheerfully, ‘to see whether any lingering Detainers are accumulating at the gate. They have fallen in pretty thick, sir; as thick as we could have expected.’\n\nHe remarked upon the circumstance as if it were matter of congratulation: rubbing his hands briskly, and rolling his head a little.\n\n‘As thick,’ repeated Mr Rugg, ‘as we could reasonably have expected. Quite a shower-bath of ‘em. I don’t often intrude upon you now, when I look round, because I know you are not inclined for company, and that if you wished to see me, you would leave word in the Lodge. But I am here pretty well every day, sir. Would this be an unseasonable time, sir,’ asked Mr Rugg, coaxingly, ‘for me to offer an observation?’\n\n‘As seasonable a time as any other.’\n\n‘Hum! Public opinion, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘has been busy with you.’\n\n‘I don’t doubt it.’\n\n‘Might it not be advisable, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, more coaxingly yet, ‘now to make, at last and after all, a trifling concession to public opinion? We all do it in one way or another. The fact is, we must do it.’\n\n‘I cannot set myself right with it, Mr Rugg, and have no business to expect that I ever shall.’\n\n‘Don’t say that, sir, don’t say that. The cost of being moved to the Bench is almost insignificant, and if the general feeling is strong that you ought to be there, why--really--’\n\n‘I thought you had settled, Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur, ‘that my determination to remain here was a matter of taste.’\n\n‘Well, sir, well! But is it good taste, is it good taste? That’s the Question.’ Mr Rugg was so soothingly persuasive as to be quite pathetic. ‘I was almost going to say, is it good feeling? This is an extensive affair of yours; and your remaining here where a man can come for a pound or two, is remarked upon as not in keeping. It is not in keeping. I can’t tell you, sir, in how many quarters I heard it mentioned. I heard comments made upon it last night in a Parlour frequented by what I should call, if I did not look in there now and then myself, the best legal company--I heard, there, comments on it that I was sorry to hear. They hurt me on your account. Again, only this morning at breakfast. My daughter (but a woman, you’ll say: yet still with a feeling for these things, and even with some little personal experience, as the plaintiff in Rugg and Bawkins) was expressing her great surprise; her great surprise. Now under these circumstances, and considering that none of us can quite set ourselves above public opinion, wouldn’t a trifling concession to that opinion be--Come, sir,’ said Rugg, ‘I will put it on the lowest ground of argument, and say, Amiable?’\n\nArthur’s thoughts had once more wandered away to Little Dorrit, and the question remained unanswered.\n\n‘As to myself, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, hoping that his eloquence had reduced him to a state of indecision, ‘it is a principle of mine not to consider myself when a client’s inclinations are in the scale. But, knowing your considerate character and general wish to oblige, I will repeat that I should prefer your being in the Bench. Your case has made a noise; it is a creditable case to be professionally concerned in; I should feel on a better standing with my connection, if you went to the Bench. Don’t let that influence you, sir. I merely state the fact.’\n\nSo errant had the prisoner’s attention already grown in solitude and dejection, and so accustomed had it become to commune with only one silent figure within the ever-frowning walls, that Clennam had to shake off a kind of stupor before he could look at Mr Rugg, recall the thread of his talk, and hurriedly say, ‘I am unchanged, and unchangeable, in my decision. Pray, let it be; let it be!’ Mr Rugg, without concealing that he was nettled and mortified, replied:\n\n‘Oh! Beyond a doubt, sir. I have travelled out of the record, sir, I am aware, in putting the point to you. But really, when I hear it remarked in several companies, and in very good company, that however worthy of a foreigner, it is not worthy of the spirit of an Englishman to remain in the Marshalsea when the glorious liberties of his island home admit of his removal to the Bench, I thought I would depart from the narrow professional line marked out to me, and mention it. Personally,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘I have no opinion on the topic.’\n\n‘That’s well,’ returned Arthur.\n\n‘Oh! None at all, sir!’ said Mr Rugg. ‘If I had, I should have been unwilling, some minutes ago, to see a client of mine visited in this place by a gentleman of a high family riding a saddle-horse. But it was not my business. If I had, I might have wished to be now empowered to mention to another gentleman, a gentleman of military exterior at present waiting in the Lodge, that my client had never intended to remain here, and was on the eve of removal to a superior abode. But my course as a professional machine is clear; I have nothing to do with it. Is it your good pleasure to see the gentleman, sir?’\n\n‘Who is waiting to see me, did you say?’\n\n‘I did take that unprofessional liberty, sir. Hearing that I was your professional adviser, he declined to interpose before my very limited function was performed. Happily,’ said Mr Rugg, with sarcasm, ‘I did not so far travel out of the record as to ask the gentleman for his name.’\n\n‘I suppose I have no resource but to see him,’ sighed Clennam, wearily.\n\n‘Then it _is_ your good pleasure, sir?’ retorted Rugg. ‘Am I honoured by your instructions to mention as much to the gentleman, as I pass out? I am? Thank you, sir. I take my leave.’ His leave he took accordingly, in dudgeon.\n\nThe gentleman of military exterior had so imperfectly awakened Clennam’s curiosity, in the existing state of his mind, that a half-forgetfulness of such a visitor’s having been referred to, was already creeping over it as a part of the sombre veil which almost always dimmed it now, when a heavy footstep on the stairs aroused him. It appeared to ascend them, not very promptly or spontaneously, yet with a display of stride and clatter meant to be insulting. As it paused for a moment on the landing outside his door, he could not recall his association with the peculiarity of its sound, though he thought he had one. Only a moment was given him for consideration. His door was immediately swung open by a thump, and in the doorway stood the missing Blandois, the cause of many anxieties.\n\n‘Salve, fellow jail-bird!’ said he. ‘You want me, it seems. Here I am!’\n\nBefore Arthur could speak to him in his indignant wonder, Cavalletto followed him into the room. Mr Pancks followed Cavalletto. Neither of the two had been there since its present occupant had had possession of it. Mr Pancks, breathing hard, sidled near the window, put his hat on the ground, stirred his hair up with both hands, and folded his arms, like a man who had come to a pause in a hard day’s work. Mr Baptist, never taking his eyes from his dreaded chum of old, softly sat down on the floor with his back against the door and one of his ankles in each hand: resuming the attitude (except that it was now expressive of unwinking watchfulness) in which he had sat before the same man in the deeper shade of another prison, one hot morning at Marseilles.\n\n‘I have it on the witnessing of these two madmen,’ said Monsieur Blandois, otherwise Lagnier, otherwise Rigaud, ‘that you want me, brother-bird. Here I am!’\nGlancing round contemptuously at the bedstead, which was turned up by day, he leaned his back against it as a resting-place, without removing his hat from his head, and stood defiantly lounging with his hands in his pockets.\n\n‘You villain of ill-omen!’ said Arthur. ‘You have purposely cast a dreadful suspicion upon my mother’s house. Why have you done it? What prompted you to the devilish invention?’\n\nMonsieur Rigaud, after frowning at him for a moment, laughed. ‘Hear this noble gentleman! Listen, all the world, to this creature of Virtue! But take care, take care. It is possible, my friend, that your ardour is a little compromising. Holy Blue! It is possible.’\n\n‘Signore!’ interposed Cavalletto, also addressing Arthur: ‘for to commence, hear me! I received your instructions to find him, Rigaud; is it not?’\n\n‘It is the truth.’\n\n‘I go, consequentementally,’--it would have given Mrs Plornish great concern if she could have been persuaded that his occasional lengthening of an adverb in this way, was the chief fault of his English,--‘first among my countrymen. I ask them what news in Londra, of foreigners arrived. Then I go among the French. Then I go among the Germans. They all tell me. The great part of us know well the other, and they all tell me. But!--no person can tell me nothing of him, Rigaud. Fifteen times,’ said Cavalletto, thrice throwing out his left hand with all its fingers spread, and doing it so rapidly that the sense of sight could hardly follow the action, ‘I ask of him in every place where go the foreigners; and fifteen times,’ repeating the same swift performance, ‘they know nothing. But!--’\n\nAt this significant Italian rest on the word ‘But,’ his backhanded shake of his right forefinger came into play; a very little, and very cautiously.\n\n‘But!--After a long time when I have not been able to find that he is here in Londra, some one tells me of a soldier with white hair--hey?--not hair like this that he carries--white--who lives retired secrettementally, in a certain place. But!--’ with another rest upon the word, ‘who sometimes in the after-dinner, walks, and smokes. It is necessary, as they say in Italy (and as they know, poor people), to have patience. I have patience. I ask where is this certain place. One. believes it is here, one believes it is there. Eh well! It is not here, it is not there. I wait patientissamentally. At last I find it. Then I watch; then I hide, until he walks and smokes. He is a soldier with grey hair--But!--’ a very decided rest indeed, and a very vigorous play from side to side of the back-handed forefinger--‘he is also this man that you see.’\n\nIt was noticeable, that, in his old habit of submission to one who had been at the trouble of asserting superiority over him, he even then bestowed upon Rigaud a confused bend of his head, after thus pointing him out.\n\n‘Eh well, Signore!’ he cried in conclusion, addressing Arthur again. ‘I waited for a good opportunity. I writed some words to Signor Panco,’ an air of novelty came over Mr Pancks with this designation, ‘to come and help. I showed him, Rigaud, at his window, to Signor Panco, who was often the spy in the day. I slept at night near the door of the house. At last we entered, only this to-day, and now you see him! As he would not come up in presence of the illustrious Advocate,’ such was Mr Baptist’s honourable mention of Mr Rugg, ‘we waited down below there, together, and Signor Panco guarded the street.’\n\nAt the close of this recital, Arthur turned his eyes upon the impudent and wicked face. As it met his, the nose came down over the moustache and the moustache went up under the nose. When nose and moustache had settled into their places again, Monsieur Rigaud loudly snapped his fingers half-a-dozen times; bending forward to jerk the snaps at Arthur, as if they were palpable missiles which he jerked into his face.\n\n‘Now, Philosopher!’ said Rigaud. ‘What do you want with me?’\n\n‘I want to know,’ returned Arthur, without disguising his abhorrence, ‘how you dare direct a suspicion of murder against my mother’s house?’\n\n‘Dare!’ cried Rigaud. ‘Ho, ho! Hear him! Dare? Is it dare? By Heaven, my small boy, but you are a little imprudent!’\n\n‘I want that suspicion to be cleared away,’ said Arthur. ‘You shall be taken there, and be publicly seen. I want to know, moreover, what business you had there when I had a burning desire to fling you down-stairs. Don’t frown at me, man! I have seen enough of you to know that you are a bully and coward. I need no revival of my spirits from the effects of this wretched place to tell you so plain a fact, and one that you know so well.’\n\nWhite to the lips, Rigaud stroked his moustache, muttering, ‘By Heaven, my small boy, but you are a little compromising of my lady, your respectable mother’--and seemed for a minute undecided how to act. His indecision was soon gone. He sat himself down with a threatening swagger, and said:\n\n‘Give me a bottle of wine. You can buy wine here. Send one of your madmen to get me a bottle of wine. I won’t talk to you without wine. Come! Yes or no?’\n\n‘Fetch him what he wants, Cavalletto,’ said Arthur, scornfully, producing the money.\n\n‘Contraband beast,’ added Rigaud, ‘bring Port wine! I’ll drink nothing but Porto-Porto.’\n\nThe contraband beast, however, assuring all present, with his significant finger, that he peremptorily declined to leave his post at the door, Signor Panco offered his services. He soon returned with the bottle of wine: which, according to the custom of the place, originating in a scarcity of corkscrews among the Collegians (in common with a scarcity of much else), was already opened for use.\n\n‘Madman! A large glass,’ said Rigaud.\n\nSignor Panco put a tumbler before him; not without a visible conflict of feeling on the question of throwing it at his head.\n\n‘Haha!’ boasted Rigaud. ‘Once a gentleman, and always a gentleman. A gentleman from the beginning, and a gentleman to the end. What the Devil! A gentleman must be waited on, I hope? It’s a part of my character to be waited on!’\n\nHe half filled the tumbler as he said it, and drank off the contents when he had done saying it.\n\n‘Hah!’ smacking his lips. ‘Not a very old prisoner _that_! I judge by your looks, brave sir, that imprisonment will subdue your blood much sooner than it softens this hot wine. You are mellowing--losing body and colour already. I salute you!’\n\nHe tossed off another half glass: holding it up both before and afterwards, so as to display his small, white hand.\n\n‘To business,’ he then continued. ‘To conversation. You have shown yourself more free of speech than body, sir.’\n\n‘I have used the freedom of telling you what you know yourself to be. You know yourself, as we all know you, to be far worse than that.’\n\n‘Add, always a gentleman, and it’s no matter. Except in that regard, we are all alike. For example: you couldn’t for your life be a gentleman; I couldn’t for my life be otherwise. How great the difference! Let us go on. Words, sir, never influence the course of the cards, or the course of the dice. Do you know that? You do? I also play a game, and words are without power over it.’\n\nNow that he was confronted with Cavalletto, and knew that his story was known--whatever thin disguise he had worn, he dropped; and faced it out, with a bare face, as the infamous wretch he was.\n\n‘No, my son,’ he resumed, with a snap of his fingers. ‘I play my game to the end in spite of words; and Death of my Body and Death of my Soul! I’ll win it. You want to know why I played this little trick that you have interrupted? Know then that I had, and that I have--do you understand me? have--a commodity to sell to my lady your respectable mother. I described my precious commodity, and fixed my price. Touching the bargain, your admirable mother was a little too calm, too stolid, too immovable and statue-like. In fine, your admirable mother vexed me. To make variety in my position, and to amuse myself--what! a gentleman must be amused at somebody’s expense!--I conceived the happy idea of disappearing. An idea, see you, that your characteristic mother and my Flintwinch would have been well enough pleased to execute. Ah! Bah, bah, bah, don’t look as from high to low at me! I repeat it. Well enough pleased, excessively enchanted, and with all their hearts ravished. How strongly will you have it?’\n\nHe threw out the lees of his glass on the ground, so that they nearly spattered Cavalletto. This seemed to draw his attention to him anew. He set down his glass and said:\n\n‘I’ll not fill it. What! I am born to be served. Come then, you Cavalletto, and fill!’\n\nThe little man looked at Clennam, whose eyes were occupied with Rigaud, and, seeing no prohibition, got up from the ground, and poured out from the bottle into the glass. The blending, as he did so, of his old submission with a sense of something humorous; the striving of that with a certain smouldering ferocity, which might have flashed fire in an instant (as the born gentleman seemed to think, for he had a wary eye upon him); and the easy yielding of all to a good-natured, careless, predominant propensity to sit down on the ground again: formed a very remarkable combination of character.\n\n‘This happy idea, brave sir,’ Rigaud resumed after drinking, ‘was a happy idea for several reasons. It amused me, it worried your dear mama and my Flintwinch, it caused you agonies (my terms for a lesson in politeness towards a gentleman), and it suggested to all the amiable persons interested that your entirely devoted is a man to fear. By Heaven, he is a man to fear! Beyond this; it might have restored her wit to my lady your mother--might, under the pressing little suspicion your wisdom has recognised, have persuaded her at last to announce, covertly, in the journals, that the difficulties of a certain contract would be removed by the appearance of a certain important party to it. Perhaps yes, perhaps no. But that, you have interrupted. Now, what is it you say? What is it you want?’\n\nNever had Clennam felt more acutely that he was a prisoner in bonds, than when he saw this man before him, and could not accompany him to his mother’s house. All the undiscernible difficulties and dangers he had ever feared were closing in, when he could not stir hand or foot.\n\n‘Perhaps, my friend, philosopher, man of virtue, Imbecile, what you will; perhaps,’ said Rigaud, pausing in his drink to look out of his glass with his horrible smile, ‘you would have done better to leave me alone?’\n\n‘No! At least,’ said Clennam, ‘you are known to be alive and unharmed. At least you cannot escape from these two witnesses; and they can produce you before any public authorities, or before hundreds of people!’\n\n‘But will not produce me before one,’ said Rigaud, snapping his fingers again with an air of triumphant menace. ‘To the Devil with your witnesses! To the Devil with your produced! To the Devil with yourself! What! Do I know what I know, for that? Have I my commodity on sale, for that? Bah, poor debtor! You have interrupted my little project. Let it pass. How then? What remains? To you, nothing; to me, all. Produce _me_! Is that what you want? I will produce myself, only too quickly. Contrabandist! Give me pen, ink, and paper.’\n\nCavalletto got up again as before, and laid them before him in his former manner. Rigaud, after some villainous thinking and smiling, wrote, and read aloud, as follows:\n\n‘Wait answer.\n\n‘Prison of the Marshalsea. ‘At the apartment of your son.\n\n‘Dear Madam,\n\n‘I am in despair to be informed to-day by our prisoner here (who has had the goodness to employ spies to seek me, living for politic reasons in retirement), that you have had fears for my safety.\n\n‘Reassure yourself, dear madam. I am well, I am strong and constant.\n\n‘With the greatest impatience I should fly to your house, but that I foresee it to be possible, under the circumstances, that you will not yet have quite definitively arranged the little proposition I have had the honour to submit to you. I name one week from this day, for a last final visit on my part; when you will unconditionally accept it or reject it, with its train of consequences.\n\n‘I suppress my ardour to embrace you and achieve this interesting business, in order that you may have leisure to adjust its details to our perfect mutual satisfaction.\n\n‘In the meanwhile, it is not too much to propose (our prisoner having deranged my housekeeping), that my expenses of lodging and nourishment at an hotel shall be paid by you.\n\n‘Receive, dear madam, the assurance of my highest and most distinguished consideration,\n\n‘A thousand friendships to that dear Flintwinch.\n\n‘I kiss the hands of Madame F.’\n\nWhen he had finished this epistle, Rigaud folded it and tossed it with a flourish at Clennam’s feet. ‘Hola you! Apropos of producing, let somebody produce that at its address, and produce the answer here.’\n\n‘Cavalletto,’ said Arthur. ‘Will you take this fellow’s letter?’\n\nBut, Cavalletto’s significant finger again expressing that his post was at the door to keep watch over Rigaud, now he had found him with so much trouble, and that the duty of his post was to sit on the floor backed up by the door, looking at Rigaud and holding his own ankles,--Signor Panco once more volunteered. His services being accepted, Cavalletto suffered the door to open barely wide enough to admit of his squeezing himself out, and immediately shut it on him.\n\n‘Touch me with a finger, touch me with an epithet, question my superiority as I sit here drinking my wine at my pleasure,’ said Rigaud, ‘and I follow the letter and cancel my week’s grace. _You_ wanted me? You have got me! How do you like me?’\n\n‘You know,’ returned Clennam, with a bitter sense of his helplessness, ‘that when I sought you, I was not a prisoner.’\n\n‘To the Devil with you and your prison,’ retorted Rigaud, leisurely, as he took from his pocket a case containing the materials for making cigarettes, and employed his facile hands in folding a few for present use; ‘I care for neither of you. Contrabandist! A light.’\n\nAgain Cavalletto got up, and gave him what he wanted. There had been something dreadful in the noiseless skill of his cold, white hands, with the fingers lithely twisting about and twining one over another like serpents. Clennam could not prevent himself from shuddering inwardly, as if he had been looking on at a nest of those creatures.\n\n‘Hola, Pig!’ cried Rigaud, with a noisy stimulating cry, as if Cavalletto were an Italian horse or mule. ‘What! The infernal old jail was a respectable one to this. There was dignity in the bars and stones of that place. It was a prison for men. But this? Bah! A hospital for imbeciles!’\n\nHe smoked his cigarette out, with his ugly smile so fixed upon his face that he looked as though he were smoking with his drooping beak of a nose, rather than with his mouth; like a fancy in a weird picture. When he had lighted a second cigarette at the still burning end of the first, he said to Clennam:\n\n‘One must pass the time in the madman’s absence. One must talk. One can’t drink strong wine all day long, or I would have another bottle. She’s handsome, sir. Though not exactly to my taste, still, by the Thunder and the Lightning! handsome. I felicitate you on your admiration.’\n\n‘I neither know nor ask,’ said Clennam, ‘of whom you speak.’\n\n‘Della bella Gowana, sir, as they say in Italy. Of the Gowan, the fair Gowan.’\n\n‘Of whose husband you were the--follower, I think?’\n\n‘Sir? Follower? You are insolent. The friend.’\n\n‘Do you sell all your friends?’\n\nRigaud took his cigarette from his mouth, and eyed him with a momentary revelation of surprise. But he put it between his lips again, as he answered with coolness:\n\n‘I sell anything that commands a price. How do your lawyers live, your politicians, your intriguers, your men of the Exchange? How do you live? How do you come here? Have you sold no friend? Lady of mine! I rather think, yes!’\n\nClennam turned away from him towards the window, and sat looking out at the wall.\n\n‘Effectively, sir,’ said Rigaud, ‘Society sells itself and sells me: and I sell Society. I perceive you have acquaintance with another lady. Also handsome. A strong spirit. Let us see. How do they call her? Wade.’\n\nHe received no answer, but could easily discern that he had hit the mark.\n\n‘Yes,’ he went on, ‘that handsome lady and strong spirit addresses me in the street, and I am not insensible. I respond. That handsome lady and strong spirit does me the favour to remark, in full confidence, “I have my curiosity, and I have my chagrins. You are not more than ordinarily honourable, perhaps?” I announce myself, “Madame, a gentleman from the birth, and a gentleman to the death; but _not_ more than ordinarily honourable. I despise such a weak fantasy.” Thereupon she is pleased to compliment. “The difference between you and the rest is,” she answers, “that you say so.” For she knows Society. I accept her congratulations with gallantry and politeness. Politeness and little gallantries are inseparable from my character. She then makes a proposition, which is, in effect, that she has seen us much together; that it appears to her that I am for the passing time the cat of the house, the friend of the family; that her curiosity and her chagrins awaken the fancy to be acquainted with their movements, to know the manner of their life, how the fair Gowana is beloved, how the fair Gowana is cherished, and so on. She is not rich, but offers such and such little recompenses for the little cares and derangements of such services; and I graciously--to do everything graciously is a part of my character--consent to accept them. O yes! So goes the world. It is the mode.’\n\nThough Clennam’s back was turned while he spoke, and thenceforth to the end of the interview, he kept those glittering eyes of his that were too near together, upon him, and evidently saw in the very carriage of the head, as he passed with his braggart recklessness from clause to clause of what he said, that he was saying nothing which Clennam did not already know.\n\n‘Whoof! The fair Gowana!’ he said, lighting a third cigarette with a sound as if his lightest breath could blow her away. ‘Charming, but imprudent! For it was not well of the fair Gowana to make mysteries of letters from old lovers, in her bedchamber on the mountain, that her husband might not see them. No, no. That was not well. Whoof! The Gowana was mistaken there.’\n\n‘I earnestly hope,’ cried Arthur aloud, ‘that Pancks may not be long gone, for this man’s presence pollutes the room.’\n\n‘Ah! But he’ll flourish here, and everywhere,’ said Rigaud, with an exulting look and snap of his fingers. ‘He always has; he always will!’ Stretching his body out on the only three chairs in the room besides that on which Clennam sat, he sang, smiting himself on the breast as the gallant personage of the song.\n\n‘Who passes by this road so late? Compagnon de la Majolaine! Who passes by this road so late? Always gay!\n\n‘Sing the Refrain, pig! You could sing it once, in another jail. Sing it! Or, by every Saint who was stoned to death, I’ll be affronted and compromising; and then some people who are not dead yet, had better have been stoned along with them!’\n\n‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine! Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’\n\nPartly in his old habit of submission, partly because his not doing it might injure his benefactor, and partly because he would as soon do it as anything else, Cavalletto took up the Refrain this time. Rigaud laughed, and fell to smoking with his eyes shut.\n\nPossibly another quarter of an hour elapsed before Mr Pancks’s step was heard upon the stairs, but the interval seemed to Clennam insupportably long. His step was attended by another step; and when Cavalletto opened the door, he admitted Mr Pancks and Mr Flintwinch. The latter was no sooner visible, than Rigaud rushed at him and embraced him boisterously.\n\n‘How do you find yourself, sir?’ said Mr Flintwinch, as soon as he could disengage himself, which he struggled to do with very little ceremony. ‘Thank you, no; I don’t want any more.’ This was in reference to another menace of attention from his recovered friend. ‘Well, Arthur. You remember what I said to you about sleeping dogs and missing ones. It’s come true, you see.’\n\nHe was as imperturbable as ever, to all appearance, and nodded his head in a moralising way as he looked round the room.\n\n‘And this is the Marshalsea prison for debt!’ said Mr Flintwinch. ‘Hah! you have brought your pigs to a very indifferent market, Arthur.’\n\nIf Arthur had patience, Rigaud had not. He took his little Flintwinch, with fierce playfulness, by the two lapels of his coat, and cried:\n\n‘To the Devil with the Market, to the Devil with the Pigs, and to the Devil with the Pig-Driver! Now! Give me the answer to my letter.’\n\n‘If you can make it convenient to let go a moment, sir,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, ‘I’ll first hand Mr Arthur a little note that I have for him.’\n\nHe did so. It was in his mother’s maimed writing, on a slip of paper, and contained only these words:\n\n‘I hope it is enough that you have ruined yourself. Rest contented without more ruin. Jeremiah Flintwinch is my messenger and representative. Your affectionate M. C.’\n\nClennam read this twice, in silence, and then tore it to pieces. Rigaud in the meanwhile stepped into a chair, and sat himself on the back with his feet upon the seat.\n\n‘Now, Beau Flintwinch,’ he said, when he had closely watched the note to its destruction, ‘the answer to my letter?’\n\n‘Mrs Clennam did not write, Mr Blandois, her hands being cramped, and she thinking it as well to send it verbally by me.’ Mr Flintwinch screwed this out of himself, unwillingly and rustily. ‘She sends her compliments, and says she doesn’t on the whole wish to term you unreasonable, and that she agrees. But without prejudicing the appointment that stands for this day week.’\n\nMonsieur Rigaud, after indulging in a fit of laughter, descended from his throne, saying, ‘Good! I go to seek an hotel!’ But, there his eyes encountered Cavalletto, who was still at his post.\n\n‘Come, Pig,’ he added, ‘I have had you for a follower against my will; now, I’ll have you against yours. I tell you, my little reptiles, I am born to be served. I demand the service of this contrabandist as my domestic until this day week.’\n\nIn answer to Cavalletto’s look of inquiry, Clennam made him a sign to go; but he added aloud, ‘unless you are afraid of him.’ Cavalletto replied with a very emphatic finger-negative.’No, master, I am not afraid of him, when I no more keep it secrettementally that he was once my comrade.’ Rigaud took no notice of either remark until he had lighted his last cigarette and was quite ready for walking.\n\n‘Afraid of him,’ he said then, looking round upon them all. ‘Whoof! My children, my babies, my little dolls, you are all afraid of him. You give him his bottle of wine here; you give him meat, drink, and lodging there; you dare not touch him with a finger or an epithet. No. It is his character to triumph! Whoof!\n\n‘Of all the king’s knights he’s the flower, And he’s always gay!’\n\nWith this adaptation of the Refrain to himself, he stalked out of the room closely followed by Cavalletto, whom perhaps he had pressed into his service because he tolerably well knew it would not be easy to get rid of him. Mr Flintwinch, after scraping his chin, and looking about with caustic disparagement of the Pig-Market, nodded to Arthur, and followed. Mr Pancks, still penitent and depressed, followed too; after receiving with great attention a secret word or two of instructions from Arthur, and whispering back that he would see this affair out, and stand by it to the end. The prisoner, with the feeling that he was more despised, more scorned and repudiated, more helpless, altogether more miserable and fallen than before, was left alone again.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea\n\nHaggard anxiety and remorse are bad companions to be barred up with. Brooding all day, and resting very little indeed at night, will not arm a man against misery. Next morning, Clennam felt that his health was sinking, as his spirits had already sunk and that the weight under which he bent was bearing him down.\n\nNight after night he had risen from his bed of wretchedness at twelve or one o’clock, and had sat at his window watching the sickly lamps in the yard, and looking upward for the first wan trace of day, hours before it was possible that the sky could show it to him. Now when the night came, he could not even persuade himself to undress.\n\nFor a burning restlessness set in, an agonised impatience of the prison, and a conviction that he was going to break his heart and die there, which caused him indescribable suffering. His dread and hatred of the place became so intense that he felt it a labour to draw his breath in it. The sensation of being stifled sometimes so overpowered him, that he would stand at the window holding his throat and gasping. At the same time a longing for other air, and a yearning to be beyond the blind blank wall, made him feel as if he must go mad with the ardour of the desire.\n\nMany other prisoners had had experience of this condition before him, and its violence and continuity had worn themselves out in their cases, as they did in his. Two nights and a day exhausted it. It came back by fits, but those grew fainter and returned at lengthening intervals. A desolate calm succeeded; and the middle of the week found him settled down in the despondency of low, slow fever.\n\nWith Cavalletto and Pancks away, he had no visitors to fear but Mr and Mrs Plornish. His anxiety, in reference to that worthy pair, was that they should not come near him; for, in the morbid state of his nerves, he sought to be left alone, and spared the being seen so subdued and weak. He wrote a note to Mrs Plornish representing himself as occupied with his affairs, and bound by the necessity of devoting himself to them, to remain for a time even without the pleasant interruption of a sight of her kind face. As to Young John, who looked in daily at a certain hour, when the turnkeys were relieved, to ask if he could do anything for him; he always made a pretence of being engaged in writing, and to answer cheerfully in the negative. The subject of their only long conversation had never been revived between them. Through all these changes of unhappiness, however, it had never lost its hold on Clennam’s mind.\n\nThe sixth day of the appointed week was a moist, hot, misty day. It seemed as though the prison’s poverty, and shabbiness, and dirt, were growing in the sultry atmosphere. With an aching head and a weary heart, Clennam had watched the miserable night out, listening to the fall of rain on the yard pavement, thinking of its softer fall upon the country earth. A blurred circle of yellow haze had risen up in the sky in lieu of sun, and he had watched the patch it put upon his wall, like a bit of the prison’s raggedness. He had heard the gates open; and the badly shod feet that waited outside shuffle in; and the sweeping, and pumping, and moving about, begin, which commenced the prison morning. So ill and faint that he was obliged to rest many times in the process of getting himself washed, he had at length crept to his chair by the open window. In it he sat dozing, while the old woman who arranged his room went through her morning’s work.\n\nLight of head with want of sleep and want of food (his appetite, and even his sense of taste, having forsaken him), he had been two or three times conscious, in the night, of going astray. He had heard fragments of tunes and songs in the warm wind, which he knew had no existence. Now that he began to doze in exhaustion, he heard them again; and voices seemed to address him, and he answered, and started.\n\nDozing and dreaming, without the power of reckoning time, so that a minute might have been an hour and an hour a minute, some abiding impression of a garden stole over him--a garden of flowers, with a damp warm wind gently stirring their scents. It required such a painful effort to lift his head for the purpose of inquiring into this, or inquiring into anything, that the impression appeared to have become quite an old and importunate one when he looked round. Beside the tea-cup on his table he saw, then, a blooming nosegay: a wonderful handful of the choicest and most lovely flowers.\n\nNothing had ever appeared so beautiful in his sight. He took them up and inhaled their fragrance, and he lifted them to his hot head, and he put them down and opened his parched hands to them, as cold hands are opened to receive the cheering of a fire. It was not until he had delighted in them for some time, that he wondered who had sent them; and opened his door to ask the woman who must have put them there, how they had come into her hands. But she was gone, and seemed to have been long gone; for the tea she had left for him on the table was cold. He tried to drink some, but could not bear the odour of it: so he crept back to his chair by the open window, and put the flowers on the little round table of old.\n\nWhen the first faintness consequent on having moved about had left him, he subsided into his former state. One of the night-tunes was playing in the wind, when the door of his room seemed to open to a light touch, and, after a moment’s pause, a quiet figure seemed to stand there, with a black mantle on it. It seemed to draw the mantle off and drop it on the ground, and then it seemed to be his Little Dorrit in her old, worn dress. It seemed to tremble, and to clasp its hands, and to smile, and to burst into tears.\n\nHe roused himself, and cried out. And then he saw, in the loving, pitying, sorrowing, dear face, as in a mirror, how changed he was; and she came towards him; and with her hands laid on his breast to keep him in his chair, and with her knees upon the floor at his feet, and with her lips raised up to kiss him, and with her tears dropping on him as the rain from Heaven had dropped upon the flowers, Little Dorrit, a living presence, called him by his name.\n\n‘O, my best friend! Dear Mr Clennam, don’t let me see you weep! Unless you weep with pleasure to see me. I hope you do. Your own poor child come back!’\n\nSo faithful, tender, and unspoiled by Fortune. In the sound of her voice, in the light of her eyes, in the touch of her hands, so Angelically comforting and true!\n\nAs he embraced her, she said to him, ‘They never told me you were ill,’ and drawing an arm softly round his neck, laid his head upon her bosom, put a hand upon his head, and resting her cheek upon that hand, nursed him as lovingly, and GOD knows as innocently, as she had nursed her father in that room when she had been but a baby, needing all the care from others that she took of them.\n\nWhen he could speak, he said, ‘Is it possible that you have come to me? And in this dress?’\n\n‘I hoped you would like me better in this dress than any other. I have always kept it by me, to remind me: though I wanted no reminding. I am not alone, you see. I have brought an old friend with me.’\n\nLooking round, he saw Maggy in her big cap which had been long abandoned, with a basket on her arm as in the bygone days, chuckling rapturously.\n\n‘It was only yesterday evening that I came to London with my brother. I sent round to Mrs Plornish almost as soon as we arrived, that I might hear of you and let you know I had come. Then I heard that you were here. Did you happen to think of me in the night? I almost believe you must have thought of me a little. I thought of you so anxiously, and it appeared so long to morning.’\n\n‘I have thought of you--’ he hesitated what to call her. She perceived it in an instant.\n\n‘You have not spoken to me by my right name yet. You know what my right name always is with you.’\n\n‘I have thought of you, Little Dorrit, every day, every hour, every minute, since I have been here.’\n\n‘Have you? Have you?’\n\nHe saw the bright delight of her face, and the flush that kindled in it, with a feeling of shame. He, a broken, bankrupt, sick, dishonoured prisoner.\n\n‘I was here before the gates were opened, but I was afraid to come straight to you. I should have done you more harm than good, at first; for the prison was so familiar and yet so strange, and it brought back so many remembrances of my poor father, and of you too, that at first it overpowered me. But we went to Mr Chivery before we came to the gate, and he brought us in, and got John’s room for us--my poor old room, you know--and we waited there a little. I brought the flowers to the door, but you didn’t hear me.’\n\nShe looked something more womanly than when she had gone away, and the ripening touch of the Italian sun was visible upon her face. But, otherwise, she was quite unchanged. The same deep, timid earnestness that he had always seen in her, and never without emotion, he saw still. If it had a new meaning that smote him to the heart, the change was in his perception, not in her.\n\nShe took off her old bonnet, hung it in the old place, and noiselessly began, with Maggy’s help, to make his room as fresh and neat as it could be made, and to sprinkle it with a pleasant-smelling water. When that was done, the basket, which was filled with grapes and other fruit, was unpacked, and all its contents were quietly put away. When that was done, a moment’s whisper despatched Maggy to despatch somebody else to fill the basket again; which soon came back replenished with new stores, from which a present provision of cooling drink and jelly, and a prospective supply of roast chicken and wine and water, were the first extracts. These various arrangements completed, she took out her old needle-case to make him a curtain for his window; and thus, with a quiet reigning in the room, that seemed to diffuse itself through the else noisy prison, he found himself composed in his chair, with Little Dorrit working at his side.\n\nTo see the modest head again bent down over its task, and the nimble fingers busy at their old work--though she was not so absorbed in it, but that her compassionate eyes were often raised to his face, and, when they drooped again had tears in them--to be so consoled and comforted, and to believe that all the devotion of this great nature was turned to him in his adversity to pour out its inexhaustible wealth of goodness upon him, did not steady Clennam’s trembling voice or hand, or strengthen him in his weakness. Yet it inspired him with an inward fortitude, that rose with his love. And how dearly he loved her now, what words can tell!\n\nAs they sat side by side in the shadow of the wall, the shadow fell like light upon him. She would not let him speak much, and he lay back in his chair, looking at her. Now and again she would rise and give him the glass that he might drink, or would smooth the resting-place of his head; then she would gently resume her seat by him, and bend over her work again.\n\nThe shadow moved with the sun, but she never moved from his side, except to wait upon him. The sun went down and she was still there. She had done her work now, and her hand, faltering on the arm of his chair since its last tending of him, was hesitating there yet. He laid his hand upon it, and it clasped him with a trembling supplication.\n\n‘Dear Mr Clennam, I must say something to you before I go. I have put it off from hour to hour, but I must say it.’\n\n‘I too, dear Little Dorrit. I have put off what I must say.’\n\nShe nervously moved her hand towards his lips as if to stop him; then it dropped, trembling, into its former place.\n\n‘I am not going abroad again. My brother is, but I am not. He was always attached to me, and he is so grateful to me now--so much too grateful, for it is only because I happened to be with him in his illness--that he says I shall be free to stay where I like best, and to do what I like best. He only wishes me to be happy, he says.’\n\nThere was one bright star shining in the sky. She looked up at it while she spoke, as if it were the fervent purpose of her own heart shining above her.\n\n‘You will understand, I dare say, without my telling you, that my brother has come home to find my dear father’s will, and to take possession of his property. He says, if there is a will, he is sure I shall be left rich; and if there is none, that he will make me so.’\n\nHe would have spoken; but she put up her trembling hand again, and he stopped.\n\n‘I have no use for money, I have no wish for it. It would be of no value at all to me but for your sake. I could not be rich, and you here. I must always be much worse than poor, with you distressed. Will you let me lend you all I have? Will you let me give it you? Will you let me show you that I have never forgotten, that I never can forget, your protection of me when this was my home? Dear Mr Clennam, make me of all the world the happiest, by saying Yes? Make me as happy as I can be in leaving you here, by saying nothing to-night, and letting me go away with the hope that you will think of it kindly; and that for my sake--not for yours, for mine, for nobody’s but mine!--you will give me the greatest joy I can experience on earth, the joy of knowing that I have been serviceable to you, and that I have paid some little of the great debt of my affection and gratitude. I can’t say what I wish to say. I can’t visit you here where I have lived so long, I can’t think of you here where I have seen so much, and be as calm and comforting as I ought. My tears will make their way. I cannot keep them back. But pray, pray, pray, do not turn from your Little Dorrit, now, in your affliction! Pray, pray, pray, I beg you and implore you with all my grieving heart, my friend--my dear!--take all I have, and make it a Blessing to me!’\n\nThe star had shone on her face until now, when her face sank upon his hand and her own.\n\nIt had grown darker when he raised her in his encircling arm, and softly answered her.\n\n‘No, darling Little Dorrit. No, my child. I must not hear of such a sacrifice. Liberty and hope would be so dear, bought at such a price, that I could never support their weight, never bear the reproach of possessing them. But with what ardent thankfulness and love I say this, I may call Heaven to witness!’\n\n‘And yet you will not let me be faithful to you in your affliction?’\n\n‘Say, dearest Little Dorrit, and yet I will try to be faithful to you. If, in the bygone days when this was your home and when this was your dress, I had understood myself (I speak only of myself) better, and had read the secrets of my own breast more distinctly; if, through my reserve and self-mistrust, I had discerned a light that I see brightly now when it has passed far away, and my weak footsteps can never overtake it; if I had then known, and told you that I loved and honoured you, not as the poor child I used to call you, but as a woman whose true hand would raise me high above myself and make me a far happier and better man; if I had so used the opportunity there is no recalling--as I wish I had, O I wish I had!--and if something had kept us apart then, when I was moderately thriving, and when you were poor; I might have met your noble offer of your fortune, dearest girl, with other words than these, and still have blushed to touch it. But, as it is, I must never touch it, never!’\n\nShe besought him, more pathetically and earnestly, with her little supplicatory hand, than she could have done in any words.\n\n‘I am disgraced enough, my Little Dorrit. I must not descend so low as that, and carry you--so dear, so generous, so good--down with me. GOD bless you, GOD reward you! It is past.’\n\nHe took her in his arms, as if she had been his daughter.\n\n‘Always so much older, so much rougher, and so much less worthy, even what I was must be dismissed by both of us, and you must see me only as I am. I put this parting kiss upon your cheek, my child--who might have been more near to me, who never could have been more dear--a ruined man far removed from you, for ever separated from you, whose course is run while yours is but beginning. I have not the courage to ask to be forgotten by you in my humiliation; but I ask to be remembered only as I am.’\n\nThe bell began to ring, warning visitors to depart. He took her mantle from the wall, and tenderly wrapped it round her.\n\n‘One other word, my Little Dorrit. A hard one to me, but it is a necessary one. The time when you and this prison had anything in common has long gone by. Do you understand?’\n\n‘O! you will never say to me,’ she cried, weeping bitterly, and holding up her clasped hands in entreaty, ‘that I am not to come back any more! You will surely not desert me so!’\n\n‘I would say it, if I could; but I have not the courage quite to shut out this dear face, and abandon all hope of its return. But do not come soon, do not come often! This is now a tainted place, and I well know the taint of it clings to me. You belong to much brighter and better scenes. You are not to look back here, my Little Dorrit; you are to look away to very different and much happier paths. Again, GOD bless you in them! GOD reward you!’\n\nMaggy, who had fallen into very low spirits, here cried, ‘Oh get him into a hospital; do get him into a hospital, Mother! He’ll never look like hisself again, if he an’t got into a hospital. And then the little woman as was always a spinning at her wheel, she can go to the cupboard with the Princess, and say, what do you keep the Chicking there for? and then they can take it out and give it to him, and then all be happy!’\n\nThe interruption was seasonable, for the bell had nearly rung itself out. Again tenderly wrapping her mantle about her, and taking her on his arm (though, but for her visit, he was almost too weak to walk), Arthur led Little Dorrit down-stairs. She was the last visitor to pass out at the Lodge, and the gate jarred heavily and hopelessly upon her.\n\nWith the funeral clang that it sounded into Arthur’s heart, his sense of weakness returned. It was a toilsome journey up-stairs to his room, and he re-entered its dark solitary precincts in unutterable misery.\n\nWhen it was almost midnight, and the prison had long been quiet, a cautious creak came up the stairs, and a cautious tap of a key was given at his door. It was Young John. He glided in, in his stockings, and held the door closed, while he spoke in a whisper.\n\n‘It’s against all rules, but I don’t mind. I was determined to come through, and come to you.’\n\n‘What is the matter?’\n\n‘Nothing’s the matter, sir. I was waiting in the court-yard for Miss Dorrit when she came out. I thought you’d like some one to see that she was safe.’\n\n‘Thank you, thank you! You took her home, John?’\n\n‘I saw her to her hotel. The same that Mr Dorrit was at. Miss Dorrit walked all the way, and talked to me so kind, it quite knocked me over. Why do you think she walked instead of riding?’\n\n‘I don’t know, John.’\n\n‘To talk about you. She said to me, “John, you was always honourable, and if you’ll promise me that you will take care of him, and never let him want for help and comfort when I am not there, my mind will be at rest so far.” I promised her. And I’ll stand by you,’ said John Chivery, ‘for ever!’\n\nClennam, much affected, stretched out his hand to this honest spirit.\n\n‘Before I take it,’ said John, looking at it, without coming from the door, ‘guess what message Miss Dorrit gave me.’\n\nClennam shook his head.\n\n‘“Tell him,”’ repeated John, in a distinct, though quavering voice, ‘“that his Little Dorrit sent him her undying love.” Now it’s delivered. Have I been honourable, sir?’\n\n‘Very, very!’\n\n‘Will you tell Miss Dorrit I’ve been honourable, sir?’\n\n‘I will indeed.’\n\n‘There’s my hand, sir,’ said John, ‘and I’ll stand by you forever!’\n\nAfter a hearty squeeze, he disappeared with the same cautious creak upon the stair, crept shoeless over the pavement of the yard, and, locking the gates behind him, passed out into the front where he had left his shoes. If the same way had been paved with burning ploughshares, it is not at all improbable that John would have traversed it with the same devotion, for the same purpose.\n\n# CHAPTER 30. Closing in\n\nThe last day of the appointed week touched the bars of the Marshalsea gate. Black, all night, since the gate had clashed upon Little Dorrit, its iron stripes were turned by the early-glowing sun into stripes of gold. Far aslant across the city, over its jumbled roofs, and through the open tracery of its church towers, struck the long bright rays, bars of the prison of this lower world.\n\nThroughout the day the old house within the gateway remained untroubled by any visitors. But, when the sun was low, three men turned in at the gateway and made for the dilapidated house.\n\nRigaud was the first, and walked by himself smoking. Mr Baptist was the second, and jogged close after him, looking at no other object. Mr Pancks was the third, and carried his hat under his arm for the liberation of his restive hair; the weather being extremely hot. They all came together at the door-steps.\n\n‘You pair of madmen!’ said Rigaud, facing about. ‘Don’t go yet!’\n\n‘We don’t mean to,’ said Mr Pancks.\n\nGiving him a dark glance in acknowledgment of his answer, Rigaud knocked loudly. He had charged himself with drink, for the playing out of his game, and was impatient to begin. He had hardly finished one long resounding knock, when he turned to the knocker again and began another. That was not yet finished when Jeremiah Flintwinch opened the door, and they all clanked into the stone hall. Rigaud, thrusting Mr Flintwinch aside, proceeded straight up-stairs. His two attendants followed him, Mr Flintwinch followed them, and they all came trooping into Mrs Clennam’s quiet room. It was in its usual state; except that one of the windows was wide open, and Affery sat on its old-fashioned window-seat, mending a stocking. The usual articles were on the little table; the usual deadened fire was in the grate; the bed had its usual pall upon it; and the mistress of all sat on her black bier-like sofa, propped up by her black angular bolster that was like the headsman’s block.\n\nYet there was a nameless air of preparation in the room, as if it were strung up for an occasion. From what the room derived it--every one of its small variety of objects being in the fixed spot it had occupied for years--no one could have said without looking attentively at its mistress, and that, too, with a previous knowledge of her face. Although her unchanging black dress was in every plait precisely as of old, and her unchanging attitude was rigidly preserved, a very slight additional setting of her features and contraction of her gloomy forehead was so powerfully marked, that it marked everything about her.\n\n‘Who are these?’ she said, wonderingly, as the two attendants entered. ‘What do these people want here?’\n\n‘Who are these, dear madame, is it?’ returned Rigaud. ‘Faith, they are friends of your son the prisoner. And what do they want here, is it? Death, madame, I don’t know. You will do well to ask them.’\n\n‘You know you told us at the door, not to go yet,’ said Pancks.\n\n‘And you know you told me at the door, you didn’t mean to go,’ retorted Rigaud. ‘In a word, madame, permit me to present two spies of the prisoner’s--madmen, but spies. If you wish them to remain here during our little conversation, say the word. It is nothing to me.’\n\n‘Why should I wish them to remain here?’ said Mrs Clennam. ‘What have I to do with them?’\n\n‘Then, dearest madame,’ said Rigaud, throwing himself into an arm-chair so heavily that the old room trembled, ‘you will do well to dismiss them. It is your affair. They are not my spies, not my rascals.’\n\n‘Hark! You Pancks,’ said Mrs Clennam, bending her brows upon him angrily, ‘you Casby’s clerk! Attend to your employer’s business and your own. Go. And take that other man with you.’\n\n‘Thank you, ma’am,’ returned Mr Pancks, ‘I am glad to say I see no objection to our both retiring. We have done all we undertook to do for Mr Clennam. His constant anxiety has been (and it grew worse upon him when he became a prisoner), that this agreeable gentleman should be brought back here to the place from which he slipped away. Here he is--brought back. And I will say,’ added Mr Pancks, ‘to his ill-looking face, that in my opinion the world would be no worse for his slipping out of it altogether.’\n\n‘Your opinion is not asked,’ answered Mrs Clennam. ‘Go.’\n\n‘I am sorry not to leave you in better company, ma’am,’ said Pancks; ‘and sorry, too, that Mr Clennam can’t be present. It’s my fault, that is.’\n\n‘You mean his own,’ she returned.\n\n‘No, I mean mine, ma’am,’ said Pancks, ‘for it was my misfortune to lead him into a ruinous investment.’ (Mr Pancks still clung to that word, and never said speculation.) ‘Though I can prove by figures,’ added Mr Pancks, with an anxious countenance, ‘that it ought to have been a good investment. I have gone over it since it failed, every day of my life, and it comes out--regarded as a question of figures--triumphant. The present is not a time or place,’ Mr Pancks pursued, with a longing glance into his hat, where he kept his calculations, ‘for entering upon the figures; but the figures are not to be disputed. Mr Clennam ought to have been at this moment in his carriage and pair, and I ought to have been worth from three to five thousand pound.’\n\nMr Pancks put his hair erect with a general aspect of confidence that could hardly have been surpassed, if he had had the amount in his pocket. These incontrovertible figures had been the occupation of every moment of his leisure since he had lost his money, and were destined to afford him consolation to the end of his days.\n\n‘However,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘enough of that. Altro, old boy, you have seen the figures, and you know how they come out.’ Mr Baptist, who had not the slightest arithmetical power of compensating himself in this way, nodded, with a fine display of bright teeth.\n\nAt whom Mr Flintwinch had been looking, and to whom he then said:\n\n‘Oh! it’s you, is it? I thought I remembered your face, but I wasn’t certain till I saw your teeth. Ah! yes, to be sure. It was this officious refugee,’ said Jeremiah to Mrs Clennam, ‘who came knocking at the door on the night when Arthur and Chatterbox were here, and who asked me a whole Catechism of questions about Mr Blandois.’\n\n‘It is true,’ Mr Baptist cheerfully admitted. ‘And behold him, padrone! I have found him consequentementally.’\n\n‘I shouldn’t have objected,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, ‘to your having broken your neck consequentementally.’\n\n‘And now,’ said Mr Pancks, whose eye had often stealthily wandered to the window-seat and the stocking that was being mended there, ‘I’ve only one other word to say before I go. If Mr Clennam was here--but unfortunately, though he has so far got the better of this fine gentleman as to return him to this place against his will, he is ill and in prison--ill and in prison, poor fellow--if he was here,’ said Mr Pancks, taking one step aside towards the window-seat, and laying his right hand upon the stocking; ‘he would say, “Affery, tell your dreams!”’\n\nMr Pancks held up his right forefinger between his nose and the stocking with a ghostly air of warning, turned, steamed out and towed Mr Baptist after him. The house-door was heard to close upon them, their steps were heard passing over the dull pavement of the echoing court-yard, and still nobody had added a word. Mrs Clennam and Jeremiah had exchanged a look; and had then looked, and looked still, at Affery, who sat mending the stocking with great assiduity.\n\n‘Come!’ said Mr Flintwinch at length, screwing himself a curve or two in the direction of the window-seat, and rubbing the palms of his hands on his coat-tail as if he were preparing them to do something: ‘Whatever has to be said among us had better be begun to be said without more loss of time.--So, Affery, my woman, take yourself away!’\n\nIn a moment Affery had thrown the stocking down, started up, caught hold of the windowsill with her right hand, lodged herself upon the window-seat with her right knee, and was flourishing her left hand, beating expected assailants off.\n\n‘No, I won’t, Jeremiah--no, I won’t--no, I won’t! I won’t go! I’ll stay here. I’ll hear all I don’t know, and say all I know. I will, at last, if I die for it. I will, I will, I will, I will!’\n\nMr Flintwinch, stiffening with indignation and amazement, moistened the fingers of one hand at his lips, softly described a circle with them in the palm of the other hand, and continued with a menacing grin to screw himself in the direction of his wife; gasping some remark as he advanced, of which, in his choking anger, only the words, ‘Such a dose!’ were audible.\n\n‘Not a bit nearer, Jeremiah!’ cried Affery, never ceasing to beat the air. ‘Don’t come a bit nearer to me, or I’ll rouse the neighbourhood! I’ll throw myself out of window. I’ll scream Fire and Murder! I’ll wake the dead! Stop where you are, or I’ll make shrieks enough to wake the dead!’\n\nThe determined voice of Mrs Clennam echoed ‘Stop!’ Jeremiah had stopped already.\n\n‘It is closing in, Flintwinch. Let her alone. Affery, do you turn against me after these many years?’\n\n‘I do, if it’s turning against you to hear what I don’t know, and say what I know. I have broke out now, and I can’t go back. I am determined to do it. I will do it, I will, I will, I will! If that’s turning against you, yes, I turn against both of you two clever ones. I told Arthur when he first come home to stand up against you. I told him it was no reason, because I was afeard of my life of you, that he should be. All manner of things have been a-going on since then, and I won’t be run up by Jeremiah, nor yet I won’t be dazed and scared, nor made a party to I don’t know what, no more. I won’t, I won’t, I won’t! I’ll up for Arthur when he has nothing left, and is ill, and in prison, and can’t up for himself. I will, I will, I will, I will!’\n\n‘How do you know, you heap of confusion,’ asked Mrs Clennam sternly, ‘that in doing what you are doing now, you are even serving Arthur?’\n\n‘I don’t know nothing rightly about anything,’ said Affery; ‘and if ever you said a true word in your life, it’s when you call me a heap of confusion, for you two clever ones have done your most to make me such. You married me whether I liked it or not, and you’ve led me, pretty well ever since, such a life of dreaming and frightening as never was known, and what do you expect me to be but a heap of confusion? You wanted to make me such, and I am such; but I won’t submit no longer; no, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t!’ She was still beating the air against all comers.\n\nAfter gazing at her in silence, Mrs Clennam turned to Rigaud. ‘You see and hear this foolish creature. Do you object to such a piece of distraction remaining where she is?’\n\n‘I, madame,’ he replied, ‘do I? That’s a question for you.’\n\n‘I do not,’ she said, gloomily. ‘There is little left to choose now. Flintwinch, it is closing in.’\n\nMr Flintwinch replied by directing a look of red vengeance at his wife, and then, as if to pinion himself from falling upon her, screwed his crossed arms into the breast of his waistcoat, and with his chin very near one of his elbows stood in a corner, watching Rigaud in the oddest attitude. Rigaud, for his part, arose from his chair, and seated himself on the table with his legs dangling. In this easy attitude, he met Mrs Clennam’s set face, with his moustache going up and his nose coming down.\n\n‘Madame, I am a gentleman--’\n\n‘Of whom,’ she interrupted in her steady tones, ‘I have heard disparagement, in connection with a French jail and an accusation of murder.’\n\nHe kissed his hand to her with his exaggerated gallantry.\n\n‘Perfectly. Exactly. Of a lady too! What absurdity! How incredible! I had the honour of making a great success then; I hope to have the honour of making a great success now. I kiss your hands. Madame, I am a gentleman (I was going to observe), who when he says, “I will definitely finish this or that affair at the present sitting,” does definitely finish it. I announce to you that we are arrived at our last sitting on our little business. You do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’\n\nShe kept her eyes fixed upon him with a frown. ‘Yes.’\n\n‘Further, I am a gentleman to whom mere mercenary trade-bargains are unknown, but to whom money is always acceptable as the means of pursuing his pleasures. You do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’\n\n‘Scarcely necessary to ask, one would say. Yes.’\n\n‘Further, I am a gentleman of the softest and sweetest disposition, but who, if trifled with, becomes enraged. Noble natures under such circumstances become enraged. I possess a noble nature. When the lion is awakened--that is to say, when I enrage--the satisfaction of my animosity is as acceptable to me as money. You always do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’\n\n‘Yes,’ she answered, somewhat louder than before.\n\n‘Do not let me derange you; pray be tranquil. I have said we are now arrived at our last sitting. Allow me to recall the two sittings we have held.’\n\n‘It is not necessary.’\n\n‘Death, madame,’ he burst out, ‘it’s my fancy! Besides, it clears the way. The first sitting was limited. I had the honour of making your acquaintance--of presenting my letter; I am a Knight of Industry, at your service, madame, but my polished manners had won me so much of success, as a master of languages, among your compatriots who are as stiff as their own starch is to one another, but are ready to relax to a foreign gentleman of polished manners--and of observing one or two little things,’ he glanced around the room and smiled, ‘about this honourable house, to know which was necessary to assure me, and to convince me that I had the distinguished pleasure of making the acquaintance of the lady I sought. I achieved this. I gave my word of honour to our dear Flintwinch that I would return. I gracefully departed.’\n\nHer face neither acquiesced nor demurred. The same when he paused, and when he spoke, it as yet showed him always the one attentive frown, and the dark revelation before mentioned of her being nerved for the occasion.\n\n‘I say, gracefully departed, because it was graceful to retire without alarming a lady. To be morally graceful, not less than physically, is a part of the character of Rigaud Blandois. It was also politic, as leaving you with something overhanging you, to expect me again with a little anxiety on a day not named. But your slave is politic. By Heaven, madame, politic! Let us return. On the day not named, I have again the honour to render myself at your house. I intimate that I have something to sell, which, if not bought, will compromise madame whom I highly esteem. I explain myself generally. I demand--I think it was a thousand pounds. Will you correct me?’\n\nThus forced to speak, she replied with constraint, ‘You demanded as much as a thousand pounds.’\n\n‘I demand at present, Two. Such are the evils of delay. But to return once more. We are not accordant; we differ on that occasion. I am playful; playfulness is a part of my amiable character. Playfully, I become as one slain and hidden. For, it may alone be worth half the sum to madame, to be freed from the suspicions that my droll idea awakens. Accident and spies intermix themselves against my playfulness, and spoil the fruit, perhaps--who knows? only you and Flintwinch--when it is just ripe. Thus, madame, I am here for the last time. Listen! Definitely the last.’\n\nAs he struck his straggling boot-heels against the flap of the table, meeting her frown with an insolent gaze, he began to change his tone for a fierce one.\n\n‘Bah! Stop an instant! Let us advance by steps. Here is my Hotel-note to be paid, according to contract. Five minutes hence we may be at daggers’ points. I’ll not leave it till then, or you’ll cheat me. Pay it! Count me the money!’\n\n‘Take it from his hand and pay it, Flintwinch,’ said Mrs Clennam.\n\nHe spirted it into Mr Flintwinch’s face when the old man advanced to take it, and held forth his hand, repeating noisily, ‘Pay it! Count it out! Good money!’ Jeremiah picked the bill up, looked at the total with a bloodshot eye, took a small canvas bag from his pocket, and told the amount into his hand.\n\nRigaud chinked the money, weighed it in his hand, threw it up a little way and caught it, chinked it again.\n\n‘The sound of it, to the bold Rigaud Blandois, is like the taste of fresh meat to the tiger. Say, then, madame. How much?’\n\nHe turned upon her suddenly with a menacing gesture of the weighted hand that clenched the money, as if he were going to strike her with it.\n\n‘I tell you again, as I told you before, that we are not rich here, as you suppose us to be, and that your demand is excessive. I have not the present means of complying with such a demand, if I had ever so great an inclination.’\n\n‘If!’ cried Rigaud. ‘Hear this lady with her If! Will you say that you have not the inclination?’\n\n‘I will say what presents itself to me, and not what presents itself to you.’\n\n‘Say it then. As to the inclination. Quick! Come to the inclination, and I know what to do.’\n\nShe was no quicker, and no slower, in her reply. ‘It would seem that you have obtained possession of a paper--or of papers--which I assuredly have the inclination to recover.’\n\nRigaud, with a loud laugh, drummed his heels against the table, and chinked his money. ‘I think so! I believe you there!’\n\n‘The paper might be worth, to me, a sum of money. I cannot say how much, or how little.’\n\n‘What the Devil!’ he asked savagely. ‘Not after a week’s grace to consider?’\n\n‘No! I will not out of my scanty means--for I tell you again, we are poor here, and not rich--I will not offer any price for a power that I do not know the worst and the fullest extent of. This is the third time of your hinting and threatening. You must speak explicitly, or you may go where you will, and do what you will. It is better to be torn to pieces at a spring, than to be a mouse at the caprice of such a cat.’\n\nHe looked at her so hard with those eyes too near together that the sinister sight of each, crossing that of the other, seemed to make the bridge of his hooked nose crooked. After a long survey, he said, with the further setting off of his internal smile:\n\n‘You are a bold woman!’\n\n‘I am a resolved woman.’\n\n‘You always were. What? She always was; is it not so, my little Flintwinch?’\n\n‘Flintwinch, say nothing to him. It is for him to say, here and now, all he can; or to go hence, and do all he can. You know this to be our determination. Leave him to his action on it.’\n\nShe did not shrink under his evil leer, or avoid it. He turned it upon her again, but she remained steady at the point to which she had fixed herself. He got off the table, placed a chair near the sofa, sat down in it, and leaned an arm upon the sofa close to her own, which he touched with his hand. Her face was ever frowning, attentive, and settled.\n\n‘It is your pleasure then, madame, that I shall relate a morsel of family history in this little family society,’ said Rigaud, with a warning play of his lithe fingers on her arm. ‘I am something of a doctor. Let me touch your pulse.’\n\nShe suffered him to take her wrist in his hand. Holding it, he proceeded to say:\n\n‘A history of a strange marriage, and a strange mother, and a revenge, and a suppression.--Aye, aye, aye? this pulse is beating curiously! It appears to me that it doubles while I touch it. Are these the usual changes of your malady, madame?’\n\nThere was a struggle in her maimed arm as she twisted it away, but there was none in her face. On his face there was his own smile.\n\n‘I have lived an adventurous life. I am an adventurous character. I have known many adventurers; interesting spirits--amiable society! To one of them I owe my knowledge and my proofs--I repeat it, estimable lady--proofs--of the ravishing little family history I go to commence. You will be charmed with it. But, bah! I forget. One should name a history. Shall I name it the history of a house? But, bah, again. There are so many houses. Shall I name it the history of this house?’\n\nLeaning over the sofa, poised on two legs of his chair and his left elbow; that hand often tapping her arm to beat his words home; his legs crossed; his right hand sometimes arranging his hair, sometimes smoothing his moustache, sometimes striking his nose, always threatening her whatever it did; coarse, insolent, rapacious, cruel, and powerful, he pursued his narrative at his ease.\n\n‘In fine, then, I name it the history of this house. I commence it. There live here, let us suppose, an uncle and nephew. The uncle, a rigid old gentleman of strong force of character; the nephew, habitually timid, repressed, and under constraint.’\n\nMistress Affery, fixedly attentive in the window-seat, biting the rolled up end of her apron, and trembling from head to foot, here cried out, ‘Jeremiah, keep off from me! I’ve heerd, in my dreams, of Arthur’s father and his uncle. He’s a talking of them. It was before my time here; but I’ve heerd in my dreams that Arthur’s father was a poor, irresolute, frightened chap, who had had everything but his orphan life scared out of him when he was young, and that he had no voice in the choice of his wife even, but his uncle chose her. There she sits! I heerd it in my dreams, and you said it to her own self.’\n\nAs Mr Flintwinch shook his fist at her, and as Mrs Clennam gazed upon her, Rigaud kissed his hand to her.\n\n‘Perfectly right, dear Madame Flintwinch. You have a genius for dreaming.’\n\n‘I don’t want none of your praises,’ returned Affery. ‘I don’t want to have nothing at all to say to you. But Jeremiah said they was dreams, and I’ll tell ‘em as such!’ Here she put her apron in her mouth again, as if she were stopping somebody else’s mouth--perhaps Jeremiah’s, which was chattering with threats as if he were grimly cold.\n\n‘Our beloved Madame Flintwinch,’ said Rigaud, ‘developing all of a sudden a fine susceptibility and spirituality, is right to a marvel. Yes. So runs the history. Monsieur, the uncle, commands the nephew to marry. Monsieur says to him in effect, “My nephew, I introduce to you a lady of strong force of character, like myself--a resolved lady, a stern lady, a lady who has a will that can break the weak to powder: a lady without pity, without love, implacable, revengeful, cold as the stone, but raging as the fire.” Ah! what fortitude! Ah, what superiority of intellectual strength! Truly, a proud and noble character that I describe in the supposed words of Monsieur, the uncle. Ha, ha, ha! Death of my soul, I love the sweet lady!’\n\nMrs Clennam’s face had changed. There was a remarkable darkness of colour on it, and the brow was more contracted. ‘Madame, madame,’ said Rigaud, tapping her on the arm, as if his cruel hand were sounding a musical instrument, ‘I perceive I interest you. I perceive I awaken your sympathy. Let us go on.’\n\nThe drooping nose and the ascending moustache had, however, to be hidden for a moment with the white hand, before he could go on; he enjoyed the effect he made so much.\n\n‘The nephew, being, as the lucid Madame Flintwinch has remarked, a poor devil who has had everything but his orphan life frightened and famished out of him--the nephew abases his head, and makes response: “My uncle, it is to you to command. Do as you will!” Monsieur, the uncle, does as he will. It is what he always does. The auspicious nuptials take place; the newly married come home to this charming mansion; the lady is received, let us suppose, by Flintwinch. Hey, old intriguer?’\n\nJeremiah, with his eyes upon his mistress, made no reply. Rigaud looked from one to the other, struck his ugly nose, and made a clucking with his tongue.\n\n‘Soon the lady makes a singular and exciting discovery. Thereupon, full of anger, full of jealousy, full of vengeance, she forms--see you, madame!--a scheme of retribution, the weight of which she ingeniously forces her crushed husband to bear himself, as well as execute upon her enemy. What superior intelligence!’\n\n‘Keep off, Jeremiah!’ cried the palpitating Affery, taking her apron from her mouth again. ‘But it was one of my dreams, that you told her, when you quarrelled with her one winter evening at dusk--there she sits and you looking at her--that she oughtn’t to have let Arthur when he come home, suspect his father only; that she had always had the strength and the power; and that she ought to have stood up more to Arthur, for his father. It was in the same dream where you said to her that she was not--not something, but I don’t know what, for she burst out tremendous and stopped you. You know the dream as well as I do. When you come down-stairs into the kitchen with the candle in your hand, and hitched my apron off my head. When you told me I had been dreaming. When you wouldn’t believe the noises.’ After this explosion Affery put her apron into her mouth again; always keeping her hand on the window-sill and her knee on the window-seat, ready to cry out or jump out if her lord and master approached.\n\nRigaud had not lost a word of this.\n\n‘Haha!’ he cried, lifting his eyebrows, folding his arms, and leaning back in his chair. ‘Assuredly, Madame Flintwinch is an oracle! How shall we interpret the oracle, you and I and the old intriguer? He said that you were not--? And you burst out and stopped him! What was it you were not? What is it you are not? Say then, madame!’\n\nUnder this ferocious banter, she sat breathing harder, and her mouth was disturbed. Her lips quivered and opened, in spite of her utmost efforts to keep them still.\n\n‘Come then, madame! Speak, then! Our old intriguer said that you were not--and you stopped him. He was going to say that you were not--what? I know already, but I want a little confidence from you. How, then? You are not what?’\n\nShe tried again to repress herself, but broke out vehemently, ‘Not Arthur’s mother!’\n\n‘Good,’ said Rigaud. ‘You are amenable.’\n\nWith the set expression of her face all torn away by the explosion of her passion, and with a bursting, from every rent feature, of the smouldering fire so long pent up, she cried out: ‘I will tell it myself! I will not hear it from your lips, and with the taint of your wickedness upon it. Since it must be seen, I will have it seen by the light I stood in. Not another word. Hear me!’\n\n‘Unless you are a more obstinate and more persisting woman than even I know you to be,’ Mr Flintwinch interposed, ‘you had better leave Mr Rigaud, Mr Blandois, Mr Beelzebub, to tell it in his own way. What does it signify when he knows all about it?’\n\n‘He does not know all about it.’\n\n‘He knows all he cares about it,’ Mr Flintwinch testily urged.\n\n‘He does not know _me_.’\n\n‘What do you suppose he cares for you, you conceited woman?’ said Mr Flintwinch.\n\n‘I tell you, Flintwinch, I will speak. I tell you when it has come to this, I will tell it with my own lips, and will express myself throughout it. What! Have I suffered nothing in this room, no deprivation, no imprisonment, that I should condescend at last to contemplate myself in such a glass as _that_. Can you see him? Can you hear him? If your wife were a hundred times the ingrate that she is, and if I were a thousand times more hopeless than I am of inducing her to be silent if this man is silenced, I would tell it myself, before I would bear the torment of the hearing it from him.’\n\nRigaud pushed his chair a little back; pushed his legs out straight before him; and sat with his arms folded over against her.\n\n‘You do not know what it is,’ she went on addressing him, ‘to be brought up strictly and straitly. I was so brought up. Mine was no light youth of sinful gaiety and pleasure. Mine were days of wholesome repression, punishment, and fear. The corruption of our hearts, the evil of our ways, the curse that is upon us, the terrors that surround us--these were the themes of my childhood. They formed my character, and filled me with an abhorrence of evil-doers. When old Mr Gilbert Clennam proposed his orphan nephew to my father for my husband, my father impressed upon me that his bringing-up had been, like mine, one of severe restraint. He told me, that besides the discipline his spirit had undergone, he had lived in a starved house, where rioting and gaiety were unknown, and where every day was a day of toil and trial like the last. He told me that he had been a man in years long before his uncle had acknowledged him as one; and that from his school-days to that hour, his uncle’s roof has been a sanctuary to him from the contagion of the irreligious and dissolute. When, within a twelvemonth of our marriage, I found my husband, at that time when my father spoke of him, to have sinned against the Lord and outraged me by holding a guilty creature in my place, was I to doubt that it had been appointed to me to make the discovery, and that it was appointed to me to lay the hand of punishment upon that creature of perdition? Was I to dismiss in a moment--not my own wrongs--what was I! but all the rejection of sin, and all the war against it, in which I had been bred?’\n\nShe laid her wrathful hand upon the watch on the table.\n\n‘No! “Do not forget.” The initials of those words are within here now, and were within here then. I was appointed to find the old letter that referred to them, and that told me what they meant, and whose work they were, and why they were worked, lying with this watch in his secret drawer. But for that appointment there would have been no discovery. “Do not forget.” It spoke to me like a voice from an angry cloud. Do not forget the deadly sin, do not forget the appointed discovery, do not forget the appointed suffering. I did not forget. Was it my own wrong I remembered? Mine! I was but a servant and a minister. What power could I have over them, but that they were bound in the bonds of their sin, and delivered to me!’\n\nMore than forty years had passed over the grey head of this determined woman, since the time she recalled. More than forty years of strife and struggle with the whisper that, by whatever name she called her vindictive pride and rage, nothing through all eternity could change their nature. Yet, gone those more than forty years, and come this Nemesis now looking her in the face, she still abided by her old impiety--still reversed the order of Creation, and breathed her own breath into a clay image of her Creator. Verily, verily, travellers have seen many monstrous idols in many countries; but no human eyes have ever seen more daring, gross, and shocking images of the Divine nature than we creatures of the dust make in our own likenesses, of our own bad passions.\n\n‘When I forced him to give her up to me, by her name and place of abode,’ she went on in her torrent of indignation and defence; ‘when I accused her, and she fell hiding her face at my feet, was it my injury that I asserted, were they my reproaches that I poured upon her? Those who were appointed of old to go to wicked kings and accuse them--were they not ministers and servants? And had not I, unworthy and far-removed from them, sin to denounce? When she pleaded to me her youth, and his wretched and hard life (that was her phrase for the virtuous training he had belied), and the desecrated ceremony of marriage there had secretly been between them, and the terrors of want and shame that had overwhelmed them both when I was first appointed to be the instrument of their punishment, and the love (for she said the word to me, down at my feet) in which she had abandoned him and left him to me, was it _my_ enemy that became my footstool, were they the words of my wrath that made her shrink and quiver! Not unto me the strength be ascribed; not unto me the wringing of the expiation!’\n\nMany years had come and gone since she had had the free use even of her fingers; but it was noticeable that she had already more than once struck her clenched hand vigorously upon the table, and that when she said these words she raised her whole arm in the air, as though it had been a common action with her.\n\n‘And what was the repentance that was extorted from the hardness of her heart and the blackness of her depravity? I, vindictive and implacable? It may be so, to such as you who know no righteousness, and no appointment except Satan’s. Laugh; but I will be known as I know myself, and as Flintwinch knows me, though it is only to you and this half-witted woman.’\n\n‘Add, to yourself, madame,’ said Rigaud. ‘I have my little suspicions that madame is rather solicitous to be justified to herself.’\n\n‘It is false. It is not so. I have no need to be,’ she said, with great energy and anger.\n\n‘Truly?’ retorted Rigaud. ‘Hah!’\n\n‘I ask, what was the penitence, in works, that was demanded of her? “You have a child; I have none. You love that child. Give him to me. He shall believe himself to be my son, and he shall be believed by every one to be my son. To save you from exposure, his father shall swear never to see or communicate with you more; equally to save him from being stripped by his uncle, and to save your child from being a beggar, you shall swear never to see or communicate with either of them more. That done, and your present means, derived from my husband, renounced, I charge myself with your support. You may, with your place of retreat unknown, then leave, if you please, uncontradicted by me, the lie that when you passed out of all knowledge but mine, you merited a good name.” That was all. She had to sacrifice her sinful and shameful affections; no more. She was then free to bear her load of guilt in secret, and to break her heart in secret; and through such present misery (light enough for her, I think!) to purchase her redemption from endless misery, if she could. If, in this, I punished her here, did I not open to her a way hereafter? If she knew herself to be surrounded by insatiable vengeance and unquenchable fires, were they mine? If I threatened her, then and afterwards, with the terrors that encompassed her, did I hold them in my right hand?’\n\nShe turned the watch upon the table, and opened it, and, with an unsoftening face, looked at the worked letters within.\n\n‘They did _not_ forget. It is appointed against such offences that the offenders shall not be able to forget. If the presence of Arthur was a daily reproach to his father, and if the absence of Arthur was a daily agony to his mother, that was the just dispensation of Jehovah. As well might it be charged upon me, that the stings of an awakened conscience drove her mad, and that it was the will of the Disposer of all things that she should live so, many years. I devoted myself to reclaim the otherwise predestined and lost boy; to give him the reputation of an honest origin; to bring him up in fear and trembling, and in a life of practical contrition for the sins that were heavy on his head before his entrance into this condemned world. Was that a cruelty? Was I, too, not visited with consequences of the original offence in which I had no complicity? Arthur’s father and I lived no further apart, with half the globe between us, than when we were together in this house. He died, and sent this watch back to me, with its Do not forget. I do NOT forget, though I do not read it as he did. I read in it, that I was appointed to do these things. I have so read these three letters since I have had them lying on this table, and I did so read them, with equal distinctness, when they were thousands of miles away.’\n\nAs she took the watch-case in her hand, with that new freedom in the use of her hand of which she showed no consciousness whatever, bending her eyes upon it as if she were defying it to move her, Rigaud cried with a loud and contemptuous snapping of his fingers. ‘Come, madame! Time runs out. Come, lady of piety, it must be! You can tell nothing I don’t know. Come to the money stolen, or I will! Death of my soul, I have had enough of your other jargon. Come straight to the stolen money!’\n\n‘Wretch that you are,’ she answered, and now her hands clasped her head: ‘through what fatal error of Flintwinch’s, through what incompleteness on his part, who was the only other person helping in these things and trusted with them, through whose and what bringing together of the ashes of a burnt paper, you have become possessed of that codicil, I know no more than how you acquired the rest of your power here--’\n\n‘And yet,’ interrupted Rigaud, ‘it is my odd fortune to have by me, in a convenient place that I know of, that same short little addition to the will of Monsieur Gilbert Clennam, written by a lady and witnessed by the same lady and our old intriguer! Ah, bah, old intriguer, crooked little puppet! Madame, let us go on. Time presses. You or I to finish?’\n\n‘I!’ she answered, with increased determination, if it were possible. ‘I, because I will not endure to be shown myself, and have myself shown to any one, with your horrible distortion upon me. You, with your practices of infamous foreign prisons and galleys would make it the money that impelled me. It was not the money.’\n\n‘Bah, bah, bah! I repudiate, for the moment, my politeness, and say, Lies, lies, lies. You know you suppressed the deed and kept the money.’\n\n‘Not for the money’s sake, wretch!’ She made a struggle as if she were starting up; even as if, in her vehemence, she had almost risen on her disabled feet. ‘If Gilbert Clennam, reduced to imbecility, at the point of death, and labouring under the delusion of some imaginary relenting towards a girl of whom he had heard that his nephew had once had a fancy for her which he had crushed out of him, and that she afterwards drooped away into melancholy and withdrawal from all who knew her--if, in that state of weakness, he dictated to me, whose life she had darkened with her sin, and who had been appointed to know her wickedness from her own hand and her own lips, a bequest meant as a recompense to her for supposed unmerited suffering; was there no difference between my spurning that injustice, and coveting mere money--a thing which you, and your comrades in the prisons, may steal from anyone?’\n\n‘Time presses, madame. Take care!’\n\n‘If this house was blazing from the roof to the ground,’ she returned, ‘I would stay in it to justify myself against my righteous motives being classed with those of stabbers and thieves.’\n\nRigaud snapped his fingers tauntingly in her face. ‘One thousand guineas to the little beauty you slowly hunted to death. One thousand guineas to the youngest daughter her patron might have at fifty, or (if he had none) brother’s youngest daughter, on her coming of age, “as the remembrance his disinterestedness may like best, of his protection of a friendless young orphan girl.” Two thousand guineas. What! You will never come to the money?’\n\n‘That patron,’ she was vehemently proceeding, when he checked her.\n\n‘Names! Call him Mr Frederick Dorrit. No more evasions.’\n\n‘That Frederick Dorrit was the beginning of it all. If he had not been a player of music, and had not kept, in those days of his youth and prosperity, an idle house where singers, and players, and such-like children of Evil turned their backs on the Light and their faces to the Darkness, she might have remained in her lowly station, and might not have been raised out of it to be cast down. But, no. Satan entered into that Frederick Dorrit, and counselled him that he was a man of innocent and laudable tastes who did kind actions, and that here was a poor girl with a voice for singing music with. Then he is to have her taught. Then Arthur’s father, who has all along been secretly pining in the ways of virtuous ruggedness for those accursed snares which are called the Arts, becomes acquainted with her. And so, a graceless orphan, training to be a singing girl, carries it, by that Frederick Dorrit’s agency, against me, and I am humbled and deceived!--Not I, that is to say,’ she added quickly, as colour flushed into her face; ‘a greater than I. What am I?’\n\nJeremiah Flintwinch, who had been gradually screwing himself towards her, and who was now very near her elbow without her knowing it, made a specially wry face of objection when she said these words, and moreover twitched his gaiters, as if such pretensions were equivalent to little barbs in his legs.\n\n‘Lastly,’ she continued, ‘for I am at the end of these things, and I will say no more of them, and you shall say no more of them, and all that remains will be to determine whether the knowledge of them can be kept among us who are here present; lastly, when I suppressed that paper, with the knowledge of Arthur’s father--’\n\n‘But not with his consent, you know,’ said Mr Flintwinch.\n\n‘Who said with his consent?’ She started to find Jeremiah so near her, and drew back her head, looking at him with some rising distrust. ‘You were often enough between us when he would have had me produce it and I would not, to have contradicted me if I had said, with his consent. I say, when I suppressed that paper, I made no effort to destroy it, but kept it by me, here in this house, many years. The rest of the Gilbert property being left to Arthur’s father, I could at any time, without unsettling more than the two sums, have made a pretence of finding it. But, besides that I must have supported such pretence by a direct falsehood (a great responsibility), I have seen no new reason, in all the time I have been tried here, to bring it to light. It was a rewarding of sin; the wrong result of a delusion. I did what I was appointed to do, and I have undergone, within these four walls, what I was appointed to undergo. When the paper was at last destroyed--as I thought--in my presence, she had long been dead, and her patron, Frederick Dorrit, had long been deservedly ruined and imbecile. He had no daughter. I had found the niece before then; and what I did for her, was better for her far than the money of which she would have had no good.’ She added, after a moment, as though she addressed the watch: ‘She herself was innocent, and I might not have forgotten to relinquish it to her at my death:’ and sat looking at it.\n\n‘Shall I recall something to you, worthy madame?’ said Rigaud. ‘The little paper was in this house on the night when our friend the prisoner--jail-comrade of my soul--came home from foreign countries. Shall I recall yet something more to you? The little singing-bird that never was fledged, was long kept in a cage by a guardian of your appointing, well enough known to our old intriguer here. Shall we coax our old intriguer to tell us when he saw him last?’\n\n‘I’ll tell you!’ cried Affery, unstopping her mouth. ‘I dreamed it, first of all my dreams. Jeremiah, if you come a-nigh me now, I’ll scream to be heard at St Paul’s! The person as this man has spoken of, was Jeremiah’s own twin brother; and he was here in the dead of the night, on the night when Arthur come home, and Jeremiah with his own hands give him this paper, along with I don’t know what more, and he took it away in an iron box--Help! Murder! Save me from Jere-_mi_-ah!’\n\nMr Flintwinch had made a run at her, but Rigaud had caught him in his arms midway. After a moment’s wrestle with him, Flintwinch gave up, and put his hands in his pockets.\n\n‘What!’ cried Rigaud, rallying him as he poked and jerked him back with his elbows, ‘assault a lady with such a genius for dreaming! Ha, ha, ha! Why, she’ll be a fortune to you as an exhibition. All that she dreams comes true. Ha, ha, ha! You’re so like him, Little Flintwinch. So like him, as I knew him (when I first spoke English for him to the host) in the Cabaret of the Three Billiard Tables, in the little street of the high roofs, by the wharf at Antwerp! Ah, but he was a brave boy to drink. Ah, but he was a brave boy to smoke! Ah, but he lived in a sweet bachelor-apartment--furnished, on the fifth floor, above the wood and charcoal merchant’s, and the dress-maker’s, and the chair-maker’s, and the maker of tubs--where I knew him too, and wherewith his cognac and tobacco, he had twelve sleeps a day and one fit, until he had a fit too much, and ascended to the skies. Ha, ha, ha! What does it matter how I took possession of the papers in his iron box? Perhaps he confided it to my hands for you, perhaps it was locked and my curiosity was piqued, perhaps I suppressed it. Ha, ha, ha! What does it matter, so that I have it safe? We are not particular here; hey, Flintwinch? We are not particular here; is it not so, madame?’\n\nRetiring before him with vicious counter-jerks of his own elbows, Mr Flintwinch had got back into his corner, where he now stood with his hands in his pockets, taking breath, and returning Mrs Clennam’s stare. ‘Ha, ha, ha! But what’s this?’ cried Rigaud. ‘It appears as if you don’t know, one the other. Permit me, Madame Clennam who suppresses, to present Monsieur Flintwinch who intrigues.’\n\nMr Flintwinch, unpocketing one of his hands to scrape his jaw, advanced a step or so in that attitude, still returning Mrs Clennam’s look, and thus addressed her:\n\n‘Now, I know what you mean by opening your eyes so wide at me, but you needn’t take the trouble, because I don’t care for it. I’ve been telling you for how many years that you’re one of the most opinionated and obstinate of women. That’s what _you_ are. You call yourself humble and sinful, but you are the most Bumptious of your sex. That’s what _you_ are. I have told you, over and over again when we have had a tiff, that you wanted to make everything go down before you, but I wouldn’t go down before you--that you wanted to swallow up everybody alive, but I wouldn’t be swallowed up alive. Why didn’t you destroy the paper when you first laid hands upon it? I advised you to; but no, it’s not your way to take advice. You must keep it forsooth. Perhaps you may carry it out at some other time, forsooth. As if I didn’t know better than that! I think I see your pride carrying it out, with a chance of being suspected of having kept it by you. But that’s the way you cheat yourself. Just as you cheat yourself into making out that you didn’t do all this business because you were a rigorous woman, all slight, and spite, and power, and unforgiveness, but because you were a servant and a minister, and were appointed to do it. Who are you, that you should be appointed to do it? That may be your religion, but it’s my gammon. And to tell you all the truth while I am about it,’ said Mr Flintwinch, crossing his arms, and becoming the express image of irascible doggedness, ‘I have been rasped--rasped these forty years--by your taking such high ground even with me, who knows better; the effect of it being coolly to put me on low ground. I admire you very much; you are a woman of strong head and great talent; but the strongest head, and the greatest talent, can’t rasp a man for forty years without making him sore. So I don’t care for your present eyes. Now, I am coming to the paper, and mark what I say. You put it away somewhere, and you kept your own counsel where. You’re an active woman at that time, and if you want to get that paper, you can get it. But, mark. There comes a time when you are struck into what you are now, and then if you want to get that paper, you can’t get it. So it lies, long years, in its hiding-place. At last, when we are expecting Arthur home every day, and when any day may bring him home, and it’s impossible to say what rummaging he may make about the house, I recommend you five thousand times, if you can’t get at it, to let me get at it, that it may be put in the fire. But no--no one but you knows where it is, and that’s power; and, call yourself whatever humble names you will, I call you a female Lucifer in appetite for power! On a Sunday night, Arthur comes home. He has not been in this room ten minutes, when he speaks of his father’s watch. You know very well that the Do Not Forget, at the time when his father sent that watch to you, could only mean, the rest of the story being then all dead and over, Do Not Forget the suppression. Make restitution! Arthur’s ways have frightened you a bit, and the paper shall be burnt after all. So, before that jumping jade and Jezebel,’ Mr Flintwinch grinned at his wife, ‘has got you into bed, you at last tell me where you have put the paper, among the old ledgers in the cellars, where Arthur himself went prowling the very next morning. But it’s not to be burnt on a Sunday night. No; you are strict, you are; we must wait over twelve o’clock, and get into Monday. Now, all this is a swallowing of me up alive that rasps me; so, feeling a little out of temper, and not being as strict as yourself, I take a look at the document before twelve o’clock to refresh my memory as to its appearance--fold up one of the many yellow old papers in the cellars like it--and afterwards, when we have got into Monday morning, and I have, by the light of your lamp, to walk from you, lying on that bed, to this grate, make a little exchange like the conjuror, and burn accordingly. My brother Ephraim, the lunatic-keeper (I wish he had had himself to keep in a strait-waistcoat), had had many jobs since the close of the long job he got from you, but had not done well. His wife died (not that that was much; mine might have died instead, and welcome), he speculated unsuccessfully in lunatics, he got into difficulty about over-roasting a patient to bring him to reason, and he got into debt. He was going out of the way, on what he had been able to scrape up, and a trifle from me. He was here that early Monday morning, waiting for the tide; in short, he was going to Antwerp, where (I am afraid you’ll be shocked at my saying, And be damned to him!) he made the acquaintance of this gentleman. He had come a long way, and, I thought then, was only sleepy; but, I suppose now, was drunk. When Arthur’s mother had been under the care of him and his wife, she had been always writing, incessantly writing,--mostly letters of confession to you, and Prayers for forgiveness. My brother had handed, from time to time, lots of these sheets to me. I thought I might as well keep them to myself as have them swallowed up alive too; so I kept them in a box, looking over them when I felt in the humour. Convinced that it was advisable to get the paper out of the place, with Arthur coming about it, I put it into this same box, and I locked the whole up with two locks, and I trusted it to my brother to take away and keep, till I should write about it. I did write about it, and never got an answer. I didn’t know what to make of it, till this gentleman favoured us with his first visit. Of course, I began to suspect how it was, then; and I don’t want his word for it now to understand how he gets his knowledge from my papers, and your paper, and my brother’s cognac and tobacco talk (I wish he’d had to gag himself). Now, I have only one thing more to say, you hammer-headed woman, and that is, that I haven’t altogether made up my mind whether I might, or might not, have ever given you any trouble about the codicil. I think not; and that I should have been quite satisfied with knowing I had got the better of you, and that I held the power over you. In the present state of circumstances, I have no more explanation to give you till this time to-morrow night. So you may as well,’ said Mr Flintwinch, terminating his oration with a screw, ‘keep your eyes open at somebody else, for it’s no use keeping ‘em open at me.’\n\nShe slowly withdrew them when he had ceased, and dropped her forehead on her hand. Her other hand pressed hard upon the table, and again the curious stir was observable in her, as if she were going to rise.\n\n‘This box can never bring, elsewhere, the price it will bring here. This knowledge can never be of the same profit to you, sold to any other person, as sold to me. But I have not the present means of raising the sum you have demanded. I have not prospered. What will you take now, and what at another time, and how am I to be assured of your silence?’\n\n‘My angel,’ said Rigaud, ‘I have said what I will take, and time presses. Before coming here, I placed copies of the most important of these papers in another hand. Put off the time till the Marshalsea gate shall be shut for the night, and it will be too late to treat. The prisoner will have read them.’\n\nShe put her two hands to her head again, uttered a loud exclamation, and started to her feet. She staggered for a moment, as if she would have fallen; then stood firm.\n\n‘Say what you mean. Say what you mean, man!’\n\nBefore her ghostly figure, so long unused to its erect attitude, and so stiffened in it, Rigaud fell back and dropped his voice. It was, to all the three, almost as if a dead woman had risen.\n\n‘Miss Dorrit,’ answered Rigaud, ‘the little niece of Monsieur Frederick, whom I have known across the water, is attached to the prisoner. Miss Dorrit, little niece of Monsieur Frederick, watches at this moment over the prisoner, who is ill. For her I with my own hands left a packet at the prison, on my way here, with a letter of instructions, “_for his sake_”--she will do anything for his sake--to keep it without breaking the seal, in case of its being reclaimed before the hour of shutting up to-night--if it should not be reclaimed before the ringing of the prison bell, to give it to him; and it encloses a second copy for herself, which he must give to her. What! I don’t trust myself among you, now we have got so far, without giving my secret a second life. And as to its not bringing me, elsewhere, the price it will bring here, say then, madame, have you limited and settled the price the little niece will give--for his sake--to hush it up? Once more I say, time presses. The packet not reclaimed before the ringing of the bell to-night, you cannot buy. I sell, then, to the little girl!’\n\nOnce more the stir and struggle in her, and she ran to a closet, tore the door open, took down a hood or shawl, and wrapped it over her head. Affery, who had watched her in terror, darted to her in the middle of the room, caught hold of her dress, and went on her knees to her.\n\n‘Don’t, don’t, don’t! What are you doing? Where are you going? You’re a fearful woman, but I don’t bear you no ill-will. I can do poor Arthur no good now, that I see; and you needn’t be afraid of me. I’ll keep your secret. Don’t go out, you’ll fall dead in the street. Only promise me, that, if it’s the poor thing that’s kept here secretly, you’ll let me take charge of her and be her nurse. Only promise me that, and never be afraid of me.’\n\nMrs Clennam stood still for an instant, at the height of her rapid haste, saying in stern amazement:\n\n‘Kept here? She has been dead a score of years or more. Ask Flintwinch--ask _him_. They can both tell you that she died when Arthur went abroad.’\n\n‘So much the worse,’ said Affery, with a shiver, ‘for she haunts the house, then. Who else rustles about it, making signals by dropping dust so softly? Who else comes and goes, and marks the walls with long crooked touches when we are all a-bed? Who else holds the door sometimes? But don’t go out--don’t go out! Mistress, you’ll die in the street!’\n\nHer mistress only disengaged her dress from the beseeching hands, said to Rigaud, ‘Wait here till I come back!’ and ran out of the room. They saw her, from the window, run wildly through the court-yard and out at the gateway.\n\nFor a few moments they stood motionless. Affery was the first to move, and she, wringing her hands, pursued her mistress. Next, Jeremiah Flintwinch, slowly backing to the door, with one hand in a pocket, and the other rubbing his chin, twisted himself out in his reticent way, speechlessly. Rigaud, left alone, composed himself upon the window-seat of the open window, in the old Marseilles-jail attitude. He laid his cigarettes and fire-box ready to his hand, and fell to smoking.\n\n‘Whoof! Almost as dull as the infernal old jail. Warmer, but almost as dismal. Wait till she comes back? Yes, certainly; but where is she gone, and how long will she be gone? No matter! Rigaud Lagnier Blandois, my amiable subject, you will get your money. You will enrich yourself. You have lived a gentleman; you will die a gentleman. You triumph, my little boy; but it is your character to triumph. Whoof!’\n\nIn the hour of his triumph, his moustache went up and his nose came down, as he ogled a great beam over his head with particular satisfaction.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. Closed\n\nThe sun had set, and the streets were dim in the dusty twilight, when the figure so long unused to them hurried on its way. In the immediate neighbourhood of the old house it attracted little attention, for there were only a few straggling people to notice it; but, ascending from the river by the crooked ways that led to London Bridge, and passing into the great main road, it became surrounded by astonishment.\n\nResolute and wild of look, rapid of foot and yet weak and uncertain, conspicuously dressed in its black garments and with its hurried head-covering, gaunt and of an unearthly paleness, it pressed forward, taking no more heed of the throng than a sleep-walker. More remarkable by being so removed from the crowd it was among than if it had been lifted on a pedestal to be seen, the figure attracted all eyes. Saunterers pricked up their attention to observe it; busy people, crossing it, slackened their pace and turned their heads; companions pausing and standing aside, whispered one another to look at this spectral woman who was coming by; and the sweep of the figure as it passed seemed to create a vortex, drawing the most idle and most curious after it.\n\nMade giddy by the turbulent irruption of this multitude of staring faces into her cell of years, by the confusing sensation of being in the air, and the yet more confusing sensation of being afoot, by the unexpected changes in half-remembered objects, and the want of likeness between the controllable pictures her imagination had often drawn of the life from which she was secluded and the overwhelming rush of the reality, she held her way as if she were environed by distracting thoughts, rather than by external humanity and observation. But, having crossed the bridge and gone some distance straight onward, she remembered that she must ask for a direction; and it was only then, when she stopped and turned to look about her for a promising place of inquiry, that she found herself surrounded by an eager glare of faces.\n\n‘Why are you encircling me?’ she asked, trembling.\n\nNone of those who were nearest answered; but from the outer ring there arose a shrill cry of ‘’Cause you’re mad!’\n\n‘I am sure as sane as any one here. I want to find the Marshalsea prison.’\n\nThe shrill outer circle again retorted, ‘Then that ‘ud show you was mad if nothing else did, ‘cause it’s right opposite!’\n\nA short, mild, quiet-looking young man made his way through to her, as a whooping ensued on this reply, and said: ‘Was it the Marshalsea you wanted? I’m going on duty there. Come across with me.’\n\nShe laid her hand upon his arm, and he took her over the way; the crowd, rather injured by the near prospect of losing her, pressing before and behind and on either side, and recommending an adjournment to Bedlam. After a momentary whirl in the outer court-yard, the prison-door opened, and shut upon them. In the Lodge, which seemed by contrast with the outer noise a place of refuge and peace, a yellow lamp was already striving with the prison shadows.\n\n‘Why, John!’ said the turnkey who admitted them. ‘What is it?’\n\n‘Nothing, father; only this lady not knowing her way, and being badgered by the boys. Who did you want, ma’am?’\n\n‘Miss Dorrit. Is she here?’\n\nThe young man became more interested. ‘Yes, she is here. What might your name be?’\n\n‘Mrs Clennam.’\n\n‘Mr Clennam’s mother?’ asked the young man.\n\nShe pressed her lips together, and hesitated. ‘Yes. She had better be told it is his mother.’\n\n‘You see,’ said the young man, ‘the Marshal’s family living in the country at present, the Marshal has given Miss Dorrit one of the rooms in his house to use when she likes. Don’t you think you had better come up there, and let me bring Miss Dorrit?’\n\nShe signified her assent, and he unlocked a door and conducted her up a side staircase into a dwelling-house above. He showed her into a darkening room, and left her. The room looked down into the darkening prison-yard, with its inmates strolling here and there, leaning out of windows communing as much apart as they could with friends who were going away, and generally wearing out their imprisonment as they best might that summer evening. The air was heavy and hot; the closeness of the place, oppressive; and from without there arose a rush of free sounds, like the jarring memory of such things in a headache and heartache. She stood at the window, bewildered, looking down into this prison as it were out of her own different prison, when a soft word or two of surprise made her start, and Little Dorrit stood before her.\n\n‘Is it possible, Mrs Clennam, that you are so happily recovered as--’\n\nLittle Dorrit stopped, for there was neither happiness nor health in the face that turned to her.\n\n‘This is not recovery; it is not strength; I don’t know what it is.’ With an agitated wave of her hand, she put all that aside. ‘You have a packet left with you which you were to give to Arthur, if it was not reclaimed before this place closed to-night.’\n\n‘Yes.’\n\n‘I reclaim it.’\n\nLittle Dorrit took it from her bosom, and gave it into her hand, which remained stretched out after receiving it.\n\n‘Have you any idea of its contents?’\n\nFrightened by her being there with that new power Of Movement in her, which, as she said herself, was not strength, and which was unreal to look upon, as though a picture or statue had been animated, Little Dorrit answered ‘No.’\n\n‘Read them.’\n\nLittle Dorrit took the packet from the still outstretched hand, and broke the seal. Mrs Clennam then gave her the inner packet that was addressed to herself, and held the other. The shadow of the wall and of the prison buildings, which made the room sombre at noon, made it too dark to read there, with the dusk deepening apace, save in the window. In the window, where a little of the bright summer evening sky could shine upon her, Little Dorrit stood, and read. After a broken exclamation or so of wonder and of terror, she read in silence. When she had finished, she looked round, and her old mistress bowed herself before her.\n\n‘You know, now, what I have done.’\n\n‘I think so. I am afraid so; though my mind is so hurried, and so sorry, and has so much to pity that it has not been able to follow all I have read,’ said Little Dorrit tremulously.\n\n‘I will restore to you what I have withheld from you. Forgive me. Can you forgive me?’\n\n‘I can, and Heaven knows I do! Do not kiss my dress and kneel to me; you are too old to kneel to me; I forgive you freely without that.’\n\n‘I have more yet to ask.’\n\n‘Not in that posture,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘It is unnatural to see your grey hair lower than mine. Pray rise; let me help you.’ With that she raised her up, and stood rather shrinking from her, but looking at her earnestly.\n\n‘The great petition that I make to you (there is another which grows out of it), the great supplication that I address to your merciful and gentle heart, is, that you will not disclose this to Arthur until I am dead. If you think, when you have had time for consideration, that it can do him any good to know it while I am yet alive, then tell him. But you will not think that; and in such case, will you promise me to spare me until I am dead?’\n\n‘I am so sorry, and what I have read has so confused my thoughts,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘that I can scarcely give you a steady answer. If I should be quite sure that to be acquainted with it will do Mr Clennam no good--’\n\n‘I know you are attached to him, and will make him the first consideration. It is right that he should be the first consideration. I ask that. But, having regarded him, and still finding that you may spare me for the little time I shall remain on earth, will you do it?’\n\n‘I will.’\n‘GOD bless you!’\n\nShe stood in the shadow so that she was only a veiled form to Little Dorrit in the light; but the sound of her voice, in saying those three grateful words, was at once fervent and broken--broken by emotion as unfamiliar to her frozen eyes as action to her frozen limbs.\n\n‘You will wonder, perhaps,’ she said in a stronger tone, ‘that I can better bear to be known to you whom I have wronged, than to the son of my enemy who wronged me.--For she did wrong me! She not only sinned grievously against the Lord, but she wronged me. What Arthur’s father was to me, she made him. From our marriage day I was his dread, and that she made me. I was the scourge of both, and that is referable to her. You love Arthur (I can see the blush upon your face; may it be the dawn of happier days to both of you!), and you will have thought already that he is as merciful and kind as you, and why do I not trust myself to him as soon as to you. Have you not thought so?’\n\n‘No thought,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘can be quite a stranger to my heart, that springs out of the knowledge that Mr Clennam is always to be relied upon for being kind and generous and good.’\n\n‘I do not doubt it. Yet Arthur is, of the whole world, the one person from whom I would conceal this, while I am in it. I kept over him as a child, in the days of his first remembrance, my restraining and correcting hand. I was stern with him, knowing that the transgressions of the parents are visited on their offspring, and that there was an angry mark upon him at his birth. I have sat with him and his father, seeing the weakness of his father yearning to unbend to him; and forcing it back, that the child might work out his release in bondage and hardship. I have seen him, with his mother’s face, looking up at me in awe from his little books, and trying to soften me with his mother’s ways that hardened me.’\n\nThe shrinking of her auditress stopped her for a moment in her flow of words, delivered in a retrospective gloomy voice.\n\n‘For his good. Not for the satisfaction of my injury. What was I, and what was the worth of that, before the curse of Heaven! I have seen that child grow up; not to be pious in a chosen way (his mother’s influence lay too heavy on him for that), but still to be just and upright, and to be submissive to me. He never loved me, as I once half-hoped he might--so frail we are, and so do the corrupt affections of the flesh war with our trusts and tasks; but he always respected me and ordered himself dutifully to me. He does to this hour. With an empty place in his heart that he has never known the meaning of, he has turned away from me and gone his separate road; but even that he has done considerately and with deference. These have been his relations towards me. Yours have been of a much slighter kind, spread over a much shorter time. When you have sat at your needle in my room, you have been in fear of me, but you have supposed me to have been doing you a kindness; you are better informed now, and know me to have done you an injury. Your misconstruction and misunderstanding of the cause in which, and the motives with which, I have worked out this work, is lighter to endure than his would be. I would not, for any worldly recompense I can imagine, have him in a moment, however blindly, throw me down from the station I have held before him all his life, and change me altogether into something he would cast out of his respect, and think detected and exposed. Let him do it, if it must be done, when I am not here to see it. Let me never feel, while I am still alive, that I die before his face, and utterly perish away from him, like one consumed by lightning and swallowed by an earthquake.’\n\nHer pride was very strong in her, the pain of it and of her old passions was very sharp with her, when she thus expressed herself. Not less so, when she added:\n\n‘Even now, I see _you_ shrink from me, as if I had been cruel.’\n\nLittle Dorrit could not gainsay it. She tried not to show it, but she recoiled with dread from the state of mind that had burnt so fiercely and lasted so long. It presented itself to her, with no sophistry upon it, in its own plain nature.\n\n‘I have done,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘what it was given to me to do. I have set myself against evil; not against good. I have been an instrument of severity against sin. Have not mere sinners like myself been commissioned to lay it low in all time?’\n\n‘In all time?’ repeated Little Dorrit.\n\n‘Even if my own wrong had prevailed with me, and my own vengeance had moved me, could I have found no justification? None in the old days when the innocent perished with the guilty, a thousand to one? When the wrath of the hater of the unrighteous was not slaked even in blood, and yet found favour?’\n\n‘O, Mrs Clennam, Mrs Clennam,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘angry feelings and unforgiving deeds are no comfort and no guide to you and me. My life has been passed in this poor prison, and my teaching has been very defective; but let me implore you to remember later and better days. Be guided only by the healer of the sick, the raiser of the dead, the friend of all who were afflicted and forlorn, the patient Master who shed tears of compassion for our infirmities. We cannot but be right if we put all the rest away, and do everything in remembrance of Him. There is no vengeance and no infliction of suffering in His life, I am sure. There can be no confusion in following Him, and seeking for no other footsteps, I am certain.’\n\nIn the softened light of the window, looking from the scene of her early trials to the shining sky, she was not in stronger opposition to the black figure in the shade than the life and doctrine on which she rested were to that figure’s history. It bent its head low again, and said not a word. It remained thus, until the first warning bell began to ring.\n\n‘Hark!’ cried Mrs Clennam starting, ‘I said I had another petition. It is one that does not admit of delay. The man who brought you this packet and possesses these proofs, is now waiting at my house to be bought off. I can keep this from Arthur, only by buying him off. He asks a large sum; more than I can get together to pay him without having time. He refuses to make any abatement, because his threat is, that if he fails with me, he will come to you. Will you return with me and show him that you already know it? Will you return with me and try to prevail with him? Will you come and help me with him? Do not refuse what I ask in Arthur’s name, though I dare not ask it for Arthur’s sake!’\n\nLittle Dorrit yielded willingly. She glided away into the prison for a few moments, returned, and said she was ready to go. They went out by another staircase, avoiding the lodge; and coming into the front court-yard, now all quiet and deserted, gained the street.\n\nIt was one of those summer evenings when there is no greater darkness than a long twilight. The vista of street and bridge was plain to see, and the sky was serene and beautiful. People stood and sat at their doors, playing with children and enjoying the evening; numbers were walking for air; the worry of the day had almost worried itself out, and few but themselves were hurried. As they crossed the bridge, the clear steeples of the many churches looked as if they had advanced out of the murk that usually enshrouded them, and come much nearer. The smoke that rose into the sky had lost its dingy hue and taken a brightness upon it. The beauties of the sunset had not faded from the long light films of cloud that lay at peace in the horizon. From a radiant centre, over the whole length and breadth of the tranquil firmament, great shoots of light streamed among the early stars, like signs of the blessed later covenant of peace and hope that changed the crown of thorns into a glory.\n\nLess remarkable, now that she was not alone and it was darker, Mrs Clennam hurried on at Little Dorrit’s side, unmolested. They left the great thoroughfare at the turning by which she had entered it, and wound their way down among the silent, empty, cross-streets. Their feet were at the gateway, when there was a sudden noise like thunder.\n\n‘What was that! Let us make haste in,’ cried Mrs Clennam.\n\nThey were in the gateway. Little Dorrit, with a piercing cry, held her back.\n\nIn one swift instant the old house was before them, with the man lying smoking in the window; another thundering sound, and it heaved, surged outward, opened asunder in fifty places, collapsed, and fell. Deafened by the noise, stifled, choked, and blinded by the dust, they hid their faces and stood rooted to the spot. The dust storm, driving between them and the placid sky, parted for a moment and showed them the stars. As they looked up, wildly crying for help, the great pile of chimneys, which was then alone left standing like a tower in a whirlwind, rocked, broke, and hailed itself down upon the heap of ruin, as if every tumbling fragment were intent on burying the crushed wretch deeper.\n\nSo blackened by the flying particles of rubbish as to be unrecognisable, they ran back from the gateway into the street, crying and shrieking. There, Mrs Clennam dropped upon the stones; and she never from that hour moved so much as a finger again, or had the power to speak one word. For upwards of three years she reclined in a wheeled chair, looking attentively at those about her and appearing to understand what they said; but the rigid silence she had so long held was evermore enforced upon her, and except that she could move her eyes and faintly express a negative and affirmative with her head, she lived and died a statue.\n\nAffery had been looking for them at the prison, and had caught sight of them at a distance on the bridge. She came up to receive her old mistress in her arms, to help to carry her into a neighbouring house, and to be faithful to her. The mystery of the noises was out now; Affery, like greater people, had always been right in her facts, and always wrong in the theories she deduced from them.\n\nWhen the storm of dust had cleared away and the summer night was calm again, numbers of people choked up every avenue of access, and parties of diggers were formed to relieve one another in digging among the ruins. There had been a hundred people in the house at the time of its fall, there had been fifty, there had been fifteen, there had been two. Rumour finally settled the number at two; the foreigner and Mr Flintwinch.\n\nThe diggers dug all through the short night by flaring pipes of gas, and on a level with the early sun, and deeper and deeper below it as it rose into its zenith, and aslant of it as it declined, and on a level with it again as it departed. Sturdy digging, and shovelling, and carrying away, in carts, barrows, and baskets, went on without intermission, by night and by day; but it was night for the second time when they found the dirty heap of rubbish that had been the foreigner before his head had been shivered to atoms, like so much glass, by the great beam that lay upon him, crushing him.\n\nStill, they had not come upon Flintwinch yet; so the sturdy digging and shovelling and carrying away went on without intermission by night and by day. It got about that the old house had had famous cellarage (which indeed was true), and that Flintwinch had been in a cellar at the moment, or had had time to escape into one, and that he was safe under its strong arch, and even that he had been heard to cry, in hollow, subterranean, suffocated notes, ‘Here I am!’ At the opposite extremity of the town it was even known that the excavators had been able to open a communication with him through a pipe, and that he had received both soup and brandy by that channel, and that he had said with admirable fortitude that he was All right, my lads, with the exception of his collar-bone. But the digging and shovelling and carrying away went on without intermission, until the ruins were all dug out, and the cellars opened to the light; and still no Flintwinch, living or dead, all right or all wrong, had been turned up by pick or spade.\n\nIt began then to be perceived that Flintwinch had not been there at the time of the fall; and it began then to be perceived that he had been rather busy elsewhere, converting securities into as much money as could be got for them on the shortest notice, and turning to his own exclusive account his authority to act for the Firm. Affery, remembering that the clever one had said he would explain himself further in four-and-twenty hours’ time, determined for her part that his taking himself off within that period with all he could get, was the final satisfactory sum and substance of his promised explanation; but she held her peace, devoutly thankful to be quit of him. As it seemed reasonable to conclude that a man who had never been buried could not be unburied, the diggers gave him up when their task was done, and did not dig down for him into the depths of the earth.\n\nThis was taken in ill part by a great many people, who persisted in believing that Flintwinch was lying somewhere among the London geological formation. Nor was their belief much shaken by repeated intelligence which came over in course of time, that an old man who wore the tie of his neckcloth under one ear, and who was very well known to be an Englishman, consorted with the Dutchmen on the quaint banks of the canals of the Hague and in the drinking-shops of Amsterdam, under the style and designation of Mynheer von Flyntevynge.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. Going\n\nArthur continuing to lie very ill in the Marshalsea, and Mr Rugg descrying no break in the legal sky affording a hope of his enlargement, Mr Pancks suffered desperately from self-reproaches. If it had not been for those infallible figures which proved that Arthur, instead of pining in imprisonment, ought to be promenading in a carriage and pair, and that Mr Pancks, instead of being restricted to his clerkly wages, ought to have from three to five thousand pounds of his own at his immediate disposal, that unhappy arithmetician would probably have taken to his bed, and there have made one of the many obscure persons who turned their faces to the wall and died, as a last sacrifice to the late Mr Merdle’s greatness. Solely supported by his unimpugnable calculations, Mr Pancks led an unhappy and restless life; constantly carrying his figures about with him in his hat, and not only going over them himself on every possible occasion, but entreating every human being he could lay hold of to go over them with him, and observe what a clear case it was. Down in Bleeding Heart Yard there was scarcely an inhabitant of note to whom Mr Pancks had not imparted his demonstration, and, as figures are catching, a kind of cyphering measles broke out in that locality, under the influence of which the whole Yard was light-headed.\n\nThe more restless Mr Pancks grew in his mind, the more impatient he became of the Patriarch. In their later conferences his snorting assumed an irritable sound which boded the Patriarch no good; likewise, Mr Pancks had on several occasions looked harder at the Patriarchal bumps than was quite reconcilable with the fact of his not being a painter, or a peruke-maker in search of the living model.\n\nHowever, he steamed in and out of his little back Dock according as he was wanted or not wanted in the Patriarchal presence, and business had gone on in its customary course. Bleeding Heart Yard had been harrowed by Mr Pancks, and cropped by Mr Casby, at the regular seasons; Mr Pancks had taken all the drudgery and all the dirt of the business as _his_ share; Mr Casby had taken all the profits, all the ethereal vapour, and all the moonshine, as his share; and, in the form of words which that benevolent beamer generally employed on Saturday evenings, when he twirled his fat thumbs after striking the week’s balance, ‘everything had been satisfactory to all parties--all parties--satisfactory, sir, to all parties.’\n\nThe Dock of the Steam-Tug, Pancks, had a leaden roof, which, frying in the very hot sunshine, may have heated the vessel. Be that as it may, one glowing Saturday evening, on being hailed by the lumbering bottle-green ship, the Tug instantly came working out of the Dock in a highly heated condition.\n\n‘Mr Pancks,’ was the Patriarchal remark, ‘you have been remiss, you have been remiss, sir.’\n\n‘What do you mean by that?’ was the short rejoinder.\n\nThe Patriarchal state, always a state of calmness and composure, was so particularly serene that evening as to be provoking. Everybody else within the bills of mortality was hot; but the Patriarch was perfectly cool. Everybody was thirsty, and the Patriarch was drinking. There was a fragrance of limes or lemons about him; and he made a drink of golden sherry, which shone in a large tumbler as if he were drinking the evening sunshine. This was bad, but not the worst. The worst was, that with his big blue eyes, and his polished head, and his long white hair, and his bottle-green legs stretched out before him, terminating in his easy shoes easily crossed at the instep, he had a radiant appearance of having in his extensive benevolence made the drink for the human species, while he himself wanted nothing but his own milk of human kindness.\n\nWherefore, Mr Pancks said, ‘What do you mean by that?’ and put his hair up with both hands, in a highly portentous manner.\n\n‘I mean, Mr Pancks, that you must be sharper with the people, sharper with the people, much sharper with the people, sir. You don’t squeeze them. You don’t squeeze them. Your receipts are not up to the mark. You must squeeze them, sir, or our connection will not continue to be as satisfactory as I could wish it to be to all parties. All parties.’\n\n‘_Don’t_ I squeeze ‘em?’ retorted Mr Pancks. ‘What else am I made for?’\n\n‘You are made for nothing else, Mr Pancks. You are made to do your duty, but you don’t do your duty. You are paid to squeeze, and you must squeeze to pay.’ The Patriarch so much surprised himself by this brilliant turn, after Dr Johnson, which he had not in the least expected or intended, that he laughed aloud; and repeated with great satisfaction, as he twirled his thumbs and nodded at his youthful portrait, ‘Paid to squeeze, sir, and must squeeze to pay.’\n\n‘Oh,’ said Pancks. ‘Anything more?’\n\n‘Yes, sir, yes, sir. Something more. You will please, Mr Pancks, to squeeze the Yard again, the first thing on Monday morning.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Pancks. ‘Ain’t that too soon? I squeezed it dry to-day.’\n\n‘Nonsense, sir. Not near the mark, not near the mark.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Pancks, watching him as he benevolently gulped down a good draught of his mixture. ‘Anything more?’\n\n‘Yes, sir, yes, sir, something more. I am not at all pleased, Mr Pancks, with my daughter; not at all pleased. Besides calling much too often to inquire for Mrs Clennam, Mrs Clennam, who is not just now in circumstances that are by any means calculated to--to be satisfactory to all parties, she goes, Mr Pancks, unless I am much deceived, to inquire for Mr Clennam in jail. In jail.’\n\n‘He’s laid up, you know,’ said Pancks. ‘Perhaps it’s kind.’\n\n‘Pooh, pooh, Mr Pancks. She has nothing to do with that, nothing to do with that. I can’t allow it. Let him pay his debts and come out, come out; pay his debts, and come out.’\n\nAlthough Mr Pancks’s hair was standing up like strong wire, he gave it another double-handed impulse in the perpendicular direction, and smiled at his proprietor in a most hideous manner.\n\n‘You will please to mention to my daughter, Mr Pancks, that I can’t allow it, can’t allow it,’ said the Patriarch blandly.\n\n‘Oh!’ said Pancks. ‘You couldn’t mention it yourself?’\n\n‘No, sir, no; you are paid to mention it,’ the blundering old booby could not resist the temptation of trying it again, ‘and you must mention it to pay, mention it to pay.’\n\n‘Oh!’ said Pancks. ‘Anything more?’\n\n‘Yes, sir. It appears to me, Mr Pancks, that you yourself are too often and too much in that direction, that direction. I recommend you, Mr Pancks, to dismiss from your attention both your own losses and other people’s losses, and to mind your business, mind your business.’\n\nMr Pancks acknowledged this recommendation with such an extraordinarily abrupt, short, and loud utterance of the monosyllable ‘Oh!’ that even the unwieldy Patriarch moved his blue eyes in something of a hurry, to look at him. Mr Pancks, with a sniff of corresponding intensity, then added, ‘Anything more?’\n\n‘Not at present, sir, not at present. I am going,’ said the Patriarch, finishing his mixture, and rising with an amiable air, ‘to take a little stroll, a little stroll. Perhaps I shall find you here when I come back. If not, sir, duty, duty; squeeze, squeeze, squeeze, on Monday; squeeze on Monday!’\n\nMr Pancks, after another stiffening of his hair, looked on at the Patriarchal assumption of the broad-brimmed hat, with a momentary appearance of indecision contending with a sense of injury. He was also hotter than at first, and breathed harder. But he suffered Mr Casby to go out, without offering any further remark, and then took a peep at him over the little green window-blinds. ‘I thought so,’ he observed. ‘I knew where you were bound to. Good!’ He then steamed back to his Dock, put it carefully in order, took down his hat, looked round the Dock, said ‘Good-bye!’ and puffed away on his own account. He steered straight for Mrs Plornish’s end of Bleeding Heart Yard, and arrived there, at the top of the steps, hotter than ever.\n\nAt the top of the steps, resisting Mrs Plornish’s invitations to come and sit along with father in Happy Cottage--which to his relief were not so numerous as they would have been on any other night than Saturday, when the connection who so gallantly supported the business with everything but money gave their orders freely--at the top of the steps Mr Pancks remained until he beheld the Patriarch, who always entered the Yard at the other end, slowly advancing, beaming, and surrounded by suitors. Then Mr Pancks descended and bore down upon him, with his utmost pressure of steam on.\n\nThe Patriarch, approaching with his usual benignity, was surprised to see Mr Pancks, but supposed him to have been stimulated to an immediate squeeze instead of postponing that operation until Monday. The population of the Yard were astonished at the meeting, for the two powers had never been seen there together, within the memory of the oldest Bleeding Heart. But they were overcome by unutterable amazement when Mr Pancks, going close up to the most venerable of men and halting in front of the bottle-green waistcoat, made a trigger of his right thumb and forefinger, applied the same to the brim of the broad-brimmed hat, and, with singular smartness and precision, shot it off the polished head as if it had been a large marble.\n\nHaving taken this little liberty with the Patriarchal person, Mr Pancks further astounded and attracted the Bleeding Hearts by saying in an audible voice, ‘Now, you sugary swindler, I mean to have it out with you!’\n\nMr Pancks and the Patriarch were instantly the centre of a press, all eyes and ears; windows were thrown open, and door-steps were thronged.\n\n‘What do you pretend to be?’ said Mr Pancks. ‘What’s your moral game? What do you go in for? Benevolence, an’t it? You benevolent!’ Here Mr Pancks, apparently without the intention of hitting him, but merely to relieve his mind and expend his superfluous power in wholesome exercise, aimed a blow at the bumpy head, which the bumpy head ducked to avoid. This singular performance was repeated, to the ever-increasing admiration of the spectators, at the end of every succeeding article of Mr Pancks’s oration.\n\n‘I have discharged myself from your service,’ said Pancks, ‘that I may tell you what you are. You’re one of a lot of impostors that are the worst lot of all the lots to be met with. Speaking as a sufferer by both, I don’t know that I wouldn’t as soon have the Merdle lot as your lot. You’re a driver in disguise, a screwer by deputy, a wringer, and squeezer, and shaver by substitute. You’re a philanthropic sneak. You’re a shabby deceiver!’\n\n(The repetition of the performance at this point was received with a burst of laughter.)\n\n‘Ask these good people who’s the hard man here. They’ll tell you Pancks, I believe.’\n\nThis was confirmed with cries of ‘Certainly,’ and ‘Hear!’\n\n‘But I tell you, good people--Casby! This mound of meekness, this lump of love, this bottle-green smiler, this is your driver!’ said Pancks. ‘If you want to see the man who would flay you alive--here he is! Don’t look for him in me, at thirty shillings a week, but look for him in Casby, at I don’t know how much a year!’\n\n‘Good!’ cried several voices. ‘Hear Mr Pancks!’\n\n‘Hear Mr Pancks?’ cried that gentleman (after repeating the popular performance). ‘Yes, I should think so! It’s almost time to hear Mr Pancks. Mr Pancks has come down into the Yard to-night on purpose that you should hear him. Pancks is only the Works; but here’s the Winder!’\n\nThe audience would have gone over to Mr Pancks, as one man, woman, and child, but for the long, grey, silken locks, and the broad-brimmed hat.\n\n‘Here’s the Stop,’ said Pancks, ‘that sets the tune to be ground. And there is but one tune, and its name is Grind, Grind, Grind! Here’s the Proprietor, and here’s his Grubber. Why, good people, when he comes smoothly spinning through the Yard to-night, like a slow-going benevolent Humming-Top, and when you come about him with your complaints of the Grubber, you don’t know what a cheat the Proprietor is! What do you think of his showing himself to-night, that I may have all the blame on Monday? What do you think of his having had me over the coals this very evening, because I don’t squeeze you enough? What do you think of my being, at the present moment, under special orders to squeeze you dry on Monday?’\n\nThe reply was given in a murmur of ‘Shame!’ and ‘Shabby!’\n\n‘Shabby?’ snorted Pancks. ‘Yes, I should think so! The lot that your Casby belongs to, is the shabbiest of all the lots. Setting their Grubbers on, at a wretched pittance, to do what they’re ashamed and afraid to do and pretend not to do, but what they will have done, or give a man no rest! Imposing on you to give their Grubbers nothing but blame, and to give them nothing but credit! Why, the worst-looking cheat in all this town who gets the value of eighteenpence under false pretences, an’t half such a cheat as this sign-post of The Casby’s Head here!’\n\nCries of ‘That’s true!’ and ‘No more he an’t!’\n\n‘And see what you get of these fellows, besides,’ said Pancks. ‘See what more you get of these precious Humming-Tops, revolving among you with such smoothness that you’ve no idea of the pattern painted on ‘em, or the little window in ‘em. I wish to call your attention to myself for a moment. I an’t an agreeable style of chap, I know that very well.’\n\nThe auditory were divided on this point; its more uncompromising members crying, ‘No, you are not,’ and its politer materials, ‘Yes, you are.’\n\n‘I am, in general,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘a dry, uncomfortable, dreary Plodder and Grubber. That’s your humble servant. There’s his full-length portrait, painted by himself and presented to you, warranted a likeness! But what’s a man to be, with such a man as this for his Proprietor? What can be expected of him? Did anybody ever find boiled mutton and caper-sauce growing in a cocoa-nut?’\n\nNone of the Bleeding Hearts ever had, it was clear from the alacrity of their response.\n\n‘Well,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘and neither will you find in Grubbers like myself, under Proprietors like this, pleasant qualities. I’ve been a Grubber from a boy. What has my life been? Fag and grind, fag and grind, turn the wheel, turn the wheel! I haven’t been agreeable to myself, and I haven’t been likely to be agreeable to anybody else. If I was a shilling a week less useful in ten years’ time, this impostor would give me a shilling a week less; if as useful a man could be got at sixpence cheaper, he would be taken in my place at sixpence cheaper. Bargain and sale, bless you! Fixed principles! It’s a mighty fine sign-post, is The Casby’s Head,’ said Mr Pancks, surveying it with anything rather than admiration; ‘but the real name of the House is the Sham’s Arms. Its motto is, Keep the Grubber always at it. Is any gentleman present,’ said Mr Pancks, breaking off and looking round, ‘acquainted with the English Grammar?’\n\nBleeding Heart Yard was shy of claiming that acquaintance.\n\n‘It’s no matter,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘I merely wish to remark that the task this Proprietor has set me, has been never to leave off conjugating the Imperative Mood Present Tense of the verb To keep always at it. Keep thou always at it. Let him keep always at it. Keep we or do we keep always at it. Keep ye or do ye or you keep always at it. Let them keep always at it. Here is your benevolent Patriarch of a Casby, and there is his golden rule. He is uncommonly improving to look at, and I am not at all so. He is as sweet as honey, and I am as dull as ditch-water. He provides the pitch, and I handle it, and it sticks to me. Now,’ said Mr Pancks, closing upon his late Proprietor again, from whom he had withdrawn a little for the better display of him to the Yard; ‘as I am not accustomed to speak in public, and as I have made a rather lengthy speech, all circumstances considered, I shall bring my observations to a close by requesting you to get out of this.’\n\nThe Last of the Patriarchs had been so seized by assault, and required so much room to catch an idea in, an so much more room to turn it in, that he had not a word to offer in reply. He appeared to be meditating some Patriarchal way out of his delicate position, when Mr Pancks, once more suddenly applying the trigger to his hat, shot it off again with his former dexterity. On the preceding occasion, one or two of the Bleeding Heart Yarders had obsequiously picked it up and handed it to its owner; but Mr Pancks had now so far impressed his audience, that the Patriarch had to turn and stoop for it himself.\n\nQuick as lightning, Mr Pancks, who, for some moments, had had his right hand in his coat pocket, whipped out a pair of shears, swooped upon the Patriarch behind, and snipped off short the sacred locks that flowed upon his shoulders. In a paroxysm of animosity and rapidity, Mr Pancks then caught the broad-brimmed hat out of the astounded Patriarch’s hand, cut it down into a mere stewpan, and fixed it on the Patriarch’s head.\n\nBefore the frightful results of this desperate action, Mr Pancks himself recoiled in consternation. A bare-polled, goggle-eyed, big-headed lumbering personage stood staring at him, not in the least impressive, not in the least venerable, who seemed to have started out of the earth to ask what was become of Casby. After staring at this phantom in return, in silent awe, Mr Pancks threw down his shears, and fled for a place of hiding, where he might lie sheltered from the consequences of his crime. Mr Pancks deemed it prudent to use all possible despatch in making off, though he was pursued by nothing but the sound of laughter in Bleeding Heart Yard, rippling through the air and making it ring again.\n\n# CHAPTER 33. Going!\n\nThe changes of a fevered room are slow and fluctuating; but the changes of the fevered world are rapid and irrevocable.\n\nIt was Little Dorrit’s lot to wait upon both kinds of change. The Marshalsea walls, during a portion of every day, again embraced her in their shadows as their child, while she thought for Clennam, worked for him, watched him, and only left him, still to devote her utmost love and care to him. Her part in the life outside the gate urged its pressing claims upon her too, and her patience untiringly responded to them. Here was Fanny, proud, fitful, whimsical, further advanced in that disqualified state for going into society which had so much fretted her on the evening of the tortoise-shell knife, resolved always to want comfort, resolved not to be comforted, resolved to be deeply wronged, and resolved that nobody should have the audacity to think her so. Here was her brother, a weak, proud, tipsy, young old man, shaking from head to foot, talking as indistinctly as if some of the money he plumed himself upon had got into his mouth and couldn’t be got out, unable to walk alone in any act of his life, and patronising the sister whom he selfishly loved (he always had that negative merit, ill-starred and ill-launched Tip!) because he suffered her to lead him. Here was Mrs Merdle in gauzy mourning--the original cap whereof had possibly been rent to pieces in a fit of grief, but had certainly yielded to a highly becoming article from the Parisian market--warring with Fanny foot to foot, and breasting her with her desolate bosom every hour in the day. Here was poor Mr Sparkler, not knowing how to keep the peace between them, but humbly inclining to the opinion that they could do no better than agree that they were both remarkably fine women, and that there was no nonsense about either of them--for which gentle recommendation they united in falling upon him frightfully. Then, too, here was Mrs General, got home from foreign parts, sending a Prune and a Prism by post every other day, demanding a new Testimonial by way of recommendation to some vacant appointment or other. Of which remarkable gentlewoman it may be finally observed, that there surely never was a gentlewoman of whose transcendent fitness for any vacant appointment on the face of this earth, so many people were (as the warmth of her Testimonials evinced) so perfectly satisfied--or who was so very unfortunate in having a large circle of ardent and distinguished admirers, who never themselves happened to want her in any capacity.\n\nOn the first crash of the eminent Mr Merdle’s decease, many important persons had been unable to determine whether they should cut Mrs Merdle, or comfort her. As it seemed, however, essential to the strength of their own case that they should admit her to have been cruelly deceived, they graciously made the admission, and continued to know her. It followed that Mrs Merdle, as a woman of fashion and good breeding who had been sacrificed to the wiles of a vulgar barbarian (for Mr Merdle was found out from the crown of his head to the sole of his foot, the moment he was found out in his pocket), must be actively championed by her order for her order’s sake. She returned this fealty by causing it to be understood that she was even more incensed against the felonious shade of the deceased than anybody else was; thus, on the whole, she came out of her furnace like a wise woman, and did exceedingly well.\n\nMr Sparkler’s lordship was fortunately one of those shelves on which a gentleman is considered to be put away for life, unless there should be reasons for hoisting him up with the Barnacle crane to a more lucrative height. That patriotic servant accordingly stuck to his colours (the Standard of four Quarterings), and was a perfect Nelson in respect of nailing them to the mast. On the profits of his intrepidity, Mrs Sparkler and Mrs Merdle, inhabiting different floors of the genteel little temple of inconvenience to which the smell of the day before yesterday’s soup and coach-horses was as constant as Death to man, arrayed themselves to fight it out in the lists of Society, sworn rivals. And Little Dorrit, seeing all these things as they developed themselves, could not but wonder, anxiously, into what back corner of the genteel establishment Fanny’s children would be poked by-and-by, and who would take care of those unborn little victims.\n\nArthur being far too ill to be spoken with on subjects of emotion or anxiety, and his recovery greatly depending on the repose into which his weakness could be hushed, Little Dorrit’s sole reliance during this heavy period was on Mr Meagles. He was still abroad; but she had written to him through his daughter, immediately after first seeing Arthur in the Marshalsea and since, confiding her uneasiness to him on the points on which she was most anxious, but especially on one. To that one, the continued absence of Mr Meagles abroad, instead of his comforting presence in the Marshalsea, was referable.\n\nWithout disclosing the precise nature of the documents that had fallen into Rigaud’s hands, Little Dorrit had confided the general outline of that story to Mr Meagles, to whom she had also recounted his fate. The old cautious habits of the scales and scoop at once showed Mr Meagles the importance of recovering the original papers; wherefore he wrote back to Little Dorrit, strongly confirming her in the solicitude she expressed on that head, and adding that he would not come over to England ‘without making some attempt to trace them out.’\n\nBy this time Mr Henry Gowan had made up his mind that it would be agreeable to him not to know the Meagleses. He was so considerate as to lay no injunctions on his wife in that particular; but he mentioned to Mr Meagles that personally they did not appear to him to get on together, and that he thought it would be a good thing if--politely, and without any scene, or anything of that sort--they agreed that they were the best fellows in the world, but were best apart. Poor Mr Meagles, who was already sensible that he did not advance his daughter’s happiness by being constantly slighted in her presence, said ‘Good, Henry! You are my Pet’s husband; you have displaced me, in the course of nature; if you wish it, good!’ This arrangement involved the contingent advantage, which perhaps Henry Gowan had not foreseen, that both Mr and Mrs Meagles were more liberal than before to their daughter, when their communication was only with her and her young child: and that his high spirit found itself better provided with money, without being under the degrading necessity of knowing whence it came.\n\nMr Meagles, at such a period, naturally seized an occupation with great ardour. He knew from his daughter the various towns which Rigaud had been haunting, and the various hotels at which he had been living for some time back. The occupation he set himself was to visit these with all discretion and speed, and, in the event of finding anywhere that he had left a bill unpaid, and a box or parcel behind, to pay such bill, and bring away such box or parcel.\n\nWith no other attendant than Mother, Mr Meagles went upon his pilgrimage, and encountered a number of adventures. Not the least of his difficulties was, that he never knew what was said to him, and that he pursued his inquiries among people who never knew what he said to them. Still, with an unshaken confidence that the English tongue was somehow the mother tongue of the whole world, only the people were too stupid to know it, Mr Meagles harangued innkeepers in the most voluble manner, entered into loud explanations of the most complicated sort, and utterly renounced replies in the native language of the respondents, on the ground that they were ‘all bosh.’ Sometimes interpreters were called in; whom Mr Meagles addressed in such idiomatic terms of speech, as instantly to extinguish and shut up--which made the matter worse. On a balance of the account, however, it may be doubted whether he lost much; for, although he found no property, he found so many debts and various associations of discredit with the proper name, which was the only word he made intelligible, that he was almost everywhere overwhelmed with injurious accusations. On no fewer than four occasions the police were called in to receive denunciations of Mr Meagles as a Knight of Industry, a good-for-nothing, and a thief, all of which opprobrious language he bore with the best temper (having no idea what it meant), and was in the most ignominious manner escorted to steam-boats and public carriages, to be got rid of, talking all the while, like a cheerful and fluent Briton as he was, with Mother under his arm.\n\nBut, in his own tongue, and in his own head, Mr Meagles was a clear, shrewd, persevering man. When he had ‘worked round,’ as he called it, to Paris in his pilgrimage, and had wholly failed in it so far, he was not disheartened. ‘The nearer to England I follow him, you see, Mother,’ argued Mr Meagles, ‘the nearer I am likely to come to the papers, whether they turn up or no. Because it is only reasonable to conclude that he would deposit them somewhere where they would be safe from people over in England, and where they would yet be accessible to himself, don’t you see?’\n\nAt Paris Mr Meagles found a letter from Little Dorrit, lying waiting for him; in which she mentioned that she had been able to talk for a minute or two with Mr Clennam about this man who was no more; and that when she told Mr Clennam that his friend Mr Meagles, who was on his way to see him, had an interest in ascertaining something about the man if he could, he had asked her to tell Mr Meagles that he had been known to Miss Wade, then living in such a street at Calais. ‘Oho!’ said Mr Meagles.\n\nAs soon afterwards as might be in those Diligence days, Mr Meagles rang the cracked bell at the cracked gate, and it jarred open, and the peasant-woman stood in the dark doorway, saying, ‘Ice-say! Seer! Who?’ In acknowledgment of whose address, Mr Meagles murmured to himself that there was some sense about these Calais people, who really did know something of what you and themselves were up to; and returned, ‘Miss Wade, my dear.’ He was then shown into the presence of Miss Wade.\n\n‘It’s some time since we met,’ said Mr Meagles, clearing his throat; ‘I hope you have been pretty well, Miss Wade?’\n\nWithout hoping that he or anybody else had been pretty well, Miss Wade asked him to what she was indebted for the honour of seeing him again? Mr Meagles, in the meanwhile, glanced all round the room without observing anything in the shape of a box.\n\n‘Why, the truth is, Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, in a comfortable, managing, not to say coaxing voice, ‘it is possible that you may be able to throw a light upon a little something that is at present dark. Any unpleasant bygones between us are bygones, I hope. Can’t be helped now. You recollect my daughter? Time changes so! A mother!’\n\nIn his innocence, Mr Meagles could not have struck a worse key-note. He paused for any expression of interest, but paused in vain.\n\n‘That is not the subject you wished to enter on?’ she said, after a cold silence.\n\n‘No, no,’ returned Mr Meagles. ‘No. I thought your good nature might--’\n\n‘I thought you knew,’ she interrupted, with a smile, ‘that my good nature is not to be calculated upon?’\n\n‘Don’t say so,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘you do yourself an injustice. However, to come to the point.’ For he was sensible of having gained nothing by approaching it in a roundabout way. ‘I have heard from my friend Clennam, who, you will be sorry to hear, has been and still is very ill--’\n\nHe paused again, and again she was silent.\n\n‘--that you had some knowledge of one Blandois, lately killed in London by a violent accident. Now, don’t mistake me! I know it was a slight knowledge,’ said Mr Meagles, dexterously forestalling an angry interruption which he saw about to break. ‘I am fully aware of that. It was a slight knowledge, I know. But the question is,’ Mr Meagles’s voice here became comfortable again, ‘did he, on his way to England last time, leave a box of papers, or a bundle of papers, or some papers or other in some receptacle or other--any papers--with you: begging you to allow him to leave them here for a short time, until he wanted them?’\n\n‘The question is?’ she repeated. ‘Whose question is?’\n\n‘Mine,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘And not only mine but Clennam’s question, and other people’s question. Now, I am sure,’ continued Mr Meagles, whose heart was overflowing with Pet, ‘that you can’t have any unkind feeling towards my daughter; it’s impossible. Well! It’s her question, too; being one in which a particular friend of hers is nearly interested. So here I am, frankly to say that is the question, and to ask, Now, did he?’\n\n‘Upon my word,’ she returned, ‘I seem to be a mark for everybody who knew anything of a man I once in my life hired, and paid, and dismissed, to aim their questions at!’\n\n‘Now, don’t,’ remonstrated Mr Meagles, ‘don’t! Don’t take offence, because it’s the plainest question in the world, and might be asked of any one. The documents I refer to were not his own, were wrongfully obtained, might at some time or other be troublesome to an innocent person to have in keeping, and are sought by the people to whom they really belong. He passed through Calais going to London, and there were reasons why he should not take them with him then, why he should wish to be able to put his hand upon them readily, and why he should distrust leaving them with people of his own sort. Did he leave them here? I declare if I knew how to avoid giving you offence, I would take any pains to do it. I put the question personally, but there’s nothing personal in it. I might put it to any one; I have put it already to many people. Did he leave them here? Did he leave anything here?’\n\n‘No.’\n‘Then unfortunately, Miss Wade, you know nothing about them?’\n\n‘I know nothing about them. I have now answered your unaccountable question. He did not leave them here, and I know nothing about them.’\n\n‘There!’ said Mr Meagles rising. ‘I am sorry for it; that’s over; and I hope there is not much harm done.--Tattycoram well, Miss Wade?’\n\n‘Harriet well? O yes!’\n\n‘I have put my foot in it again,’ said Mr Meagles, thus corrected. ‘I can’t keep my foot out of it here, it seems. Perhaps, if I had thought twice about it, I might never have given her the jingling name. But, when one means to be good-natured and sportive with young people, one doesn’t think twice. Her old friend leaves a kind word for her, Miss Wade, if you should think proper to deliver it.’\n\nShe said nothing as to that; and Mr Meagles, taking his honest face out of the dull room, where it shone like a sun, took it to the Hotel where he had left Mrs Meagles, and where he made the Report: ‘Beaten, Mother; no effects!’ He took it next to the London Steam Packet, which sailed in the night; and next to the Marshalsea.\n\nThe faithful John was on duty when Father and Mother Meagles presented themselves at the wicket towards nightfall. Miss Dorrit was not there then, he said; but she had been there in the morning, and invariably came in the evening. Mr Clennam was slowly mending; and Maggy and Mrs Plornish and Mr Baptist took care of him by turns. Miss Dorrit was sure to come back that evening before the bell rang. There was the room the Marshal had lent her, up-stairs, in which they could wait for her, if they pleased. Mistrustful that it might be hazardous to Arthur to see him without preparation, Mr Meagles accepted the offer; and they were left shut up in the room, looking down through its barred window into the jail.\n\nThe cramped area of the prison had such an effect on Mrs Meagles that she began to weep, and such an effect on Mr Meagles that he began to gasp for air. He was walking up and down the room, panting, and making himself worse by laboriously fanning himself with her handkerchief, when he turned towards the opening door.\n\n‘Eh? Good gracious!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘this is not Miss Dorrit! Why, Mother, look! Tattycoram!’\n\nNo other. And in Tattycoram’s arms was an iron box some two feet square. Such a box had Affery Flintwinch seen, in the first of her dreams, going out of the old house in the dead of the night under Double’s arm. This, Tattycoram put on the ground at her old master’s feet: this, Tattycoram fell on her knees by, and beat her hands upon, crying half in exultation and half in despair, half in laughter and half in tears, ‘Pardon, dear Master; take me back, dear Mistress; here it is!’\n\n‘Tatty!’ exclaimed Mr Meagles.\n\n‘What you wanted!’ said Tattycoram. ‘Here it is! I was put in the next room not to see you. I heard you ask her about it, I heard her say she hadn’t got it, I was there when he left it, and I took it at bedtime and brought it away. Here it is!’\n\n‘Why, my girl,’ cried Mr Meagles, more breathless than before, ‘how did you come over?’\n\n‘I came in the boat with you. I was sitting wrapped up at the other end. When you took a coach at the wharf, I took another coach and followed you here. She never would have given it up after what you had said to her about its being wanted; she would sooner have sunk it in the sea, or burnt it. But, here it is!’\n\nThe glow and rapture that the girl was in, with her ‘Here it is!’\n\n‘She never wanted it to be left, I must say that for her; but he left it, and I knew well that after what you said, and after her denying it, she never would have given it up. But here it is! Dear Master, dear Mistress, take me back again, and give me back the dear old name! Let this intercede for me. Here it is!’\n\nFather and Mother Meagles never deserved their names better than when they took the headstrong foundling-girl into their protection again.\n\n‘Oh! I have been so wretched,’ cried Tattycoram, weeping much more, ‘always so unhappy, and so repentant! I was afraid of her from the first time I saw her. I knew she had got a power over me through understanding what was bad in me so well. It was a madness in me, and she could raise it whenever she liked. I used to think, when I got into that state, that people were all against me because of my first beginning; and the kinder they were to me, the worse fault I found in them. I made it out that they triumphed above me, and that they wanted to make me envy them, when I know--when I even knew then--that they never thought of such a thing. And my beautiful young mistress not so happy as she ought to have been, and I gone away from her! Such a brute and a wretch as she must think me! But you’ll say a word to her for me, and ask her to be as forgiving as you two are? For I am not so bad as I was,’ pleaded Tattycoram; ‘I am bad enough, but not so bad as I was, indeed. I have had Miss Wade before me all this time, as if it was my own self grown ripe--turning everything the wrong way, and twisting all good into evil. I have had her before me all this time, finding no pleasure in anything but keeping me as miserable, suspicious, and tormenting as herself. Not that she had much to do, to do that,’ cried Tattycoram, in a closing great burst of distress, ‘for I was as bad as bad could be. I only mean to say, that, after what I have gone through, I hope I shall never be quite so bad again, and that I shall get better by very slow degrees. I’ll try very hard. I won’t stop at five-and-twenty, sir, I’ll count five-and-twenty hundred, five-and-twenty thousand!’\n\nAnother opening of the door, and Tattycoram subsided, and Little Dorrit came in, and Mr Meagles with pride and joy produced the box, and her gentle face was lighted up with grateful happiness and joy. The secret was safe now! She could keep her own part of it from him; he should never know of her loss; in time to come he should know all that was of import to himself; but he should never know what concerned her only. That was all passed, all forgiven, all forgotten.\n\n‘Now, my dear Miss Dorrit,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘I am a man of business--or at least was--and I am going to take my measures promptly, in that character. Had I better see Arthur to-night?’\n\n‘I think not to-night. I will go to his room and ascertain how he is. But I think it will be better not to see him to-night.’\n\n‘I am much of your opinion, my dear,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘and therefore I have not been any nearer to him than this dismal room. Then I shall probably not see him for some little time to come. But I’ll explain what I mean when you come back.’\n\nShe left the room. Mr Meagles, looking through the bars of the window, saw her pass out of the Lodge below him into the prison-yard. He said gently, ‘Tattycoram, come to me a moment, my good girl.’\n\nShe went up to the window.\n\n‘You see that young lady who was here just now--that little, quiet, fragile figure passing along there, Tatty? Look. The people stand out of the way to let her go by. The men--see the poor, shabby fellows--pull off their hats to her quite politely, and now she glides in at that doorway. See her, Tattycoram?’\n\n‘Yes, sir.’\n\n‘I have heard tell, Tatty, that she was once regularly called the child of this place. She was born here, and lived here many years. I can’t breathe here. A doleful place to be born and bred in, Tattycoram?’\n\n‘Yes indeed, sir!’\n\n‘If she had constantly thought of herself, and settled with herself that everybody visited this place upon her, turned it against her, and cast it at her, she would have led an irritable and probably an useless existence. Yet I have heard tell, Tattycoram, that her young life has been one of active resignation, goodness, and noble service. Shall I tell you what I consider those eyes of hers, that were here just now, to have always looked at, to get that expression?’\n\n‘Yes, if you please, sir.’\n\n‘Duty, Tattycoram. Begin it early, and do it well; and there is no antecedent to it, in any origin or station, that will tell against us with the Almighty, or with ourselves.’\n\nThey remained at the window, Mother joining them and pitying the prisoners, until she was seen coming back. She was soon in the room, and recommended that Arthur, whom she had left calm and composed, should not be visited that night.\n\n‘Good!’ said Mr Meagles, cheerily. ‘I have not a doubt that’s best. I shall trust my remembrances then, my sweet nurse, in your hands, and I well know they couldn’t be in better. I am off again to-morrow morning.’\n\nLittle Dorrit, surprised, asked him where?\n\n‘My dear,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I can’t live without breathing. This place has taken my breath away, and I shall never get it back again until Arthur is out of this place.’\n\n‘How is that a reason for going off again to-morrow morning?’\n\n‘You shall understand,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘To-night we three will put up at a City Hotel. To-morrow morning, Mother and Tattycoram will go down to Twickenham, where Mrs Tickit, sitting attended by Dr Buchan in the parlour-window, will think them a couple of ghosts; and I shall go abroad again for Doyce. We must have Dan here. Now, I tell you, my love, it’s of no use writing and planning and conditionally speculating upon this and that and the other, at uncertain intervals and distances; we must have Doyce here. I devote myself at daybreak to-morrow morning, to bringing Doyce here. It’s nothing to me to go and find him. I’m an old traveller, and all foreign languages and customs are alike to me--I never understand anything about any of ‘em. Therefore I can’t be put to any inconvenience. Go at once I must, it stands to reason; because I can’t live without breathing freely; and I can’t breathe freely until Arthur is out of this Marshalsea. I am stifled at the present moment, and have scarcely breath enough to say this much, and to carry this precious box down-stairs for you.’\n\nThey got into the street as the bell began to ring, Mr Meagles carrying the box. Little Dorrit had no conveyance there: which rather surprised him. He called a coach for her and she got into it, and he placed the box beside her when she was seated. In her joy and gratitude she kissed his hand.\n\n‘I don’t like that, my dear,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘It goes against my feeling of what’s right, that _you_ should do homage to _me_--at the Marshalsea Gate.’\n\nShe bent forward, and kissed his cheek.\n\n‘You remind me of the days,’ said Mr Meagles, suddenly drooping--‘but she’s very fond of him, and hides his faults, and thinks that no one sees them--and he certainly is well connected and of a very good family!’\n\nIt was the only comfort he had in the loss of his daughter, and if he made the most of it, who could blame him?\n\n# CHAPTER 34. Gone\n\nOn a healthy autumn day, the Marshalsea prisoner, weak but otherwise restored, sat listening to a voice that read to him. On a healthy autumn day; when the golden fields had been reaped and ploughed again, when the summer fruits had ripened and waned, when the green perspectives of hops had been laid low by the busy pickers, when the apples clustering in the orchards were russet, and the berries of the mountain ash were crimson among the yellowing foliage. Already in the woods, glimpses of the hardy winter that was coming were to be caught through unaccustomed openings among the boughs where the prospect shone defined and clear, free from the bloom of the drowsy summer weather, which had rested on it as the bloom lies on the plum. So, from the seashore the ocean was no longer to be seen lying asleep in the heat, but its thousand sparkling eyes were open, and its whole breadth was in joyful animation, from the cool sand on the beach to the little sails on the horizon, drifting away like autumn-tinted leaves that had drifted from the trees.\n\nChangeless and barren, looking ignorantly at all the seasons with its fixed, pinched face of poverty and care, the prison had not a touch of any of these beauties on it. Blossom what would, its bricks and bars bore uniformly the same dead crop. Yet Clennam, listening to the voice as it read to him, heard in it all that great Nature was doing, heard in it all the soothing songs she sings to man. At no Mother’s knee but hers had he ever dwelt in his youth on hopeful promises, on playful fancies, on the harvests of tenderness and humility that lie hidden in the early-fostered seeds of the imagination; on the oaks of retreat from blighting winds, that have the germs of their strong roots in nursery acorns. But, in the tones of the voice that read to him, there were memories of an old feeling of such things, and echoes of every merciful and loving whisper that had ever stolen to him in his life.\n\nWhen the voice stopped, he put his hand over his eyes, murmuring that the light was strong upon them.\n\nLittle Dorrit put the book by, and presently arose quietly to shade the window. Maggy sat at her needlework in her old place. The light softened, Little Dorrit brought her chair closer to his side.\n\n‘This will soon be over now, dear Mr Clennam. Not only are Mr Doyce’s letters to you so full of friendship and encouragement, but Mr Rugg says his letters to him are so full of help, and that everybody (now a little anger is past) is so considerate, and speaks so well of you, that it will soon be over now.’\n\n‘Dear girl. Dear heart. Good angel!’\n\n‘You praise me far too much. And yet it is such an exquisite pleasure to me to hear you speak so feelingly, and to--and to see,’ said Little Dorrit, raising her eyes to his, ‘how deeply you mean it, that I cannot say Don’t.’\n\nHe lifted her hand to his lips.\n\n‘You have been here many, many times, when I have not seen you, Little Dorrit?’\n\n‘Yes, I have been here sometimes when I have not come into the room.’\n\n‘Very often?’\n\n‘Rather often,’ said Little Dorrit, timidly.\n\n‘Every day?’\n\n‘I think,’ said Little Dorrit, after hesitating, ‘that I have been here at least twice every day.’\n\nHe might have released the little light hand after fervently kissing it again; but that, with a very gentle lingering where it was, it seemed to court being retained. He took it in both of his, and it lay softly on his breast.\n\n‘Dear Little Dorrit, it is not my imprisonment only that will soon be over. This sacrifice of you must be ended. We must learn to part again, and to take our different ways so wide asunder. You have not forgotten what we said together, when you came back?’\n\n‘O no, I have not forgotten it. But something has been--You feel quite strong to-day, don’t you?’\n\n‘Quite strong.’\n\nThe hand he held crept up a little nearer his face.\n\n‘Do you feel quite strong enough to know what a great fortune I have got?’\n\n‘I shall be very glad to be told. No fortune can be too great or good for Little Dorrit.’\n\n‘I have been anxiously waiting to tell you. I have been longing and longing to tell you. You are sure you will not take it?’\n\n‘Never!’\n\n‘You are quite sure you will not take half of it?’\n\n‘Never, dear Little Dorrit!’\n\nAs she looked at him silently, there was something in her affectionate face that he did not quite comprehend: something that could have broken into tears in a moment, and yet that was happy and proud.\n\n‘You will be sorry to hear what I have to tell you about Fanny. Poor Fanny has lost everything. She has nothing left but her husband’s income. All that papa gave her when she married was lost as your money was lost. It was in the same hands, and it is all gone.’\n\nArthur was more shocked than surprised to hear it. ‘I had hoped it might not be so bad,’ he said: ‘but I had feared a heavy loss there, knowing the connection between her husband and the defaulter.’\n\n‘Yes. It is all gone. I am very sorry for Fanny; very, very, very sorry for poor Fanny. My poor brother too!’\n\n‘Had _he_ property in the same hands?’\n\n‘Yes! And it’s all gone.--How much do you think my own great fortune is?’\n\nAs Arthur looked at her inquiringly, with a new apprehension on him, she withdrew her hand, and laid her face down on the spot where it had rested.\n\n‘I have nothing in the world. I am as poor as when I lived here. When papa came over to England, he confided everything he had to the same hands, and it is all swept away. O my dearest and best, are you quite sure you will not share my fortune with me now?’\n\nLocked in his arms, held to his heart, with his manly tears upon her own cheek, she drew the slight hand round his neck, and clasped it in its fellow-hand.\n\n‘Never to part, my dearest Arthur; never any more, until the last! I never was rich before, I never was proud before, I never was happy before, I am rich in being taken by you, I am proud in having been resigned by you, I am happy in being with you in this prison, as I should be happy in coming back to it with you, if it should be the will of GOD, and comforting and serving you with all my love and truth. I am yours anywhere, everywhere! I love you dearly! I would rather pass my life here with you, and go out daily, working for our bread, than I would have the greatest fortune that ever was told, and be the greatest lady that ever was honoured. O, if poor papa may only know how blest at last my heart is, in this room where he suffered for so many years!’\n\nMaggy had of course been staring from the first, and had of course been crying her eyes out long before this. Maggy was now so overjoyed that, after hugging her little mother with all her might, she went down-stairs like a clog-hornpipe to find somebody or other to whom to impart her gladness. Whom should Maggy meet but Flora and Mr F.’s Aunt opportunely coming in? And whom else, as a consequence of that meeting, should Little Dorrit find waiting for herself, when, a good two or three hours afterwards, she went out?\n\nFlora’s eyes were a little red, and she seemed rather out of spirits. Mr F.’s Aunt was so stiffened that she had the appearance of being past bending by any means short of powerful mechanical pressure. Her bonnet was cocked up behind in a terrific manner; and her stony reticule was as rigid as if it had been petrified by the Gorgon’s head, and had got it at that moment inside. With these imposing attributes, Mr F.’s Aunt, publicly seated on the steps of the Marshal’s official residence, had been for the two or three hours in question a great boon to the younger inhabitants of the Borough, whose sallies of humour she had considerably flushed herself by resenting at the point of her umbrella, from time to time.\n\n‘Painfully aware, Miss Dorrit, I am sure,’ said Flora, ‘that to propose an adjournment to any place to one so far removed by fortune and so courted and caressed by the best society must ever appear intruding even if not a pie-shop far below your present sphere and a back-parlour though a civil man but if for the sake of Arthur--cannot overcome it more improper now than ever late Doyce and Clennam--one last remark I might wish to make one last explanation I might wish to offer perhaps your good nature might excuse under pretence of three kidney ones the humble place of conversation.’\n\nRightly interpreting this rather obscure speech, Little Dorrit returned that she was quite at Flora’s disposition. Flora accordingly led the way across the road to the pie-shop in question: Mr F.’s Aunt stalking across in the rear, and putting herself in the way of being run over, with a perseverance worthy of a better cause.\n\nWhen the ‘three kidney ones,’ which were to be a blind to the conversation, were set before them on three little tin platters, each kidney one ornamented with a hole at the top, into which the civil man poured hot gravy out of a spouted can as if he were feeding three lamps, Flora took out her pocket-handkerchief.\n\n‘If Fancy’s fair dreams,’ she began, ‘have ever pictured that when Arthur--cannot overcome it pray excuse me--was restored to freedom even a pie as far from flaky as the present and so deficient in kidney as to be in that respect like a minced nutmeg might not prove unacceptable if offered by the hand of true regard such visions have for ever fled and all is cancelled but being aware that tender relations are in contemplation beg to state that I heartily wish well to both and find no fault with either not the least, it may be withering to know that ere the hand of Time had made me much less slim than formerly and dreadfully red on the slightest exertion particularly after eating I well know when it takes the form of a rash, it might have been and was not through the interruption of parents and mental torpor succeeded until the mysterious clue was held by Mr F. still I would not be ungenerous to either and I heartily wish well to both.’\n\nLittle Dorrit took her hand, and thanked her for all her old kindness.\n\n‘Call it not kindness,’ returned Flora, giving her an honest kiss, ‘for you always were the best and dearest little thing that ever was if I may take the liberty and even in a money point of view a saving being Conscience itself though I must add much more agreeable than mine ever was to me for though not I hope more burdened than other people’s yet I have always found it far readier to make one uncomfortable than comfortable and evidently taking a greater pleasure in doing it but I am wandering, one hope I wish to express ere yet the closing scene draws in and it is that I do trust for the sake of old times and old sincerity that Arthur will know that I didn’t desert him in his misfortunes but that I came backwards and forwards constantly to ask if I could do anything for him and that I sat in the pie-shop where they very civilly fetched something warm in a tumbler from the hotel and really very nice hours after hours to keep him company over the way without his knowing it.’\n\nFlora really had tears in her eyes now, and they showed her to great advantage.\n\n‘Over and above which,’ said Flora, ‘I earnestly beg you as the dearest thing that ever was if you’ll still excuse the familiarity from one who moves in very different circles to let Arthur understand that I don’t know after all whether it wasn’t all nonsense between us though pleasant at the time and trying too and certainly Mr F. did work a change and the spell being broken nothing could be expected to take place without weaving it afresh which various circumstances have combined to prevent of which perhaps not the least powerful was that it was not to be, I am not prepared to say that if it had been agreeable to Arthur and had brought itself about naturally in the first instance I should not have been very glad being of a lively disposition and moped at home where papa undoubtedly is the most aggravating of his sex and not improved since having been cut down by the hand of the Incendiary into something of which I never saw the counterpart in all my life but jealousy is not my character nor ill-will though many faults.’\n\nWithout having been able closely to follow Mrs Finching through this labyrinth, Little Dorrit understood its purpose, and cordially accepted the trust.\n\n‘The withered chaplet my dear,’ said Flora, with great enjoyment, ‘is then perished the column is crumbled and the pyramid is standing upside down upon its what’s-his-name call it not giddiness call it not weakness call it not folly I must now retire into privacy and look upon the ashes of departed joys no more but taking a further liberty of paying for the pastry which has formed the humble pretext of our interview will for ever say Adieu!’\n\nMr F.’s Aunt, who had eaten her pie with great solemnity, and who had been elaborating some grievous scheme of injury in her mind since her first assumption of that public position on the Marshal’s steps, took the present opportunity of addressing the following Sibyllic apostrophe to the relict of her late nephew.\n\n‘Bring him for’ard, and I’ll chuck him out o’ winder!’\n\nFlora tried in vain to soothe the excellent woman by explaining that they were going home to dinner. Mr F.’s Aunt persisted in replying, ‘Bring him for’ard and I’ll chuck him out o’ winder!’ Having reiterated this demand an immense number of times, with a sustained glare of defiance at Little Dorrit, Mr F.’s Aunt folded her arms, and sat down in the corner of the pie-shop parlour; steadfastly refusing to budge until such time as ‘he’ should have been ‘brought for’ard,’ and the chucking portion of his destiny accomplished.\n\nIn this condition of things, Flora confided to Little Dorrit that she had not seen Mr F.’s Aunt so full of life and character for weeks; that she would find it necessary to remain there ‘hours perhaps,’ until the inexorable old lady could be softened; and that she could manage her best alone. They parted, therefore, in the friendliest manner, and with the kindest feeling on both sides.\n\nMr F.’s Aunt holding out like a grim fortress, and Flora becoming in need of refreshment, a messenger was despatched to the hotel for the tumbler already glanced at, which was afterwards replenished. With the aid of its content, a newspaper, and some skimming of the cream of the pie-stock, Flora got through the remainder of the day in perfect good humour; though occasionally embarrassed by the consequences of an idle rumour which circulated among the credulous infants of the neighbourhood, to the effect that an old lady had sold herself to the pie-shop to be made up, and was then sitting in the pie-shop parlour, declining to complete her contract. This attracted so many young persons of both sexes, and, when the shades of evening began to fall, occasioned so much interruption to the business, that the merchant became very pressing in his proposals that Mr F.’s Aunt should be removed. A conveyance was accordingly brought to the door, which, by the joint efforts of the merchant and Flora, this remarkable woman was at last induced to enter; though not without even then putting her head out of the window, and demanding to have him ‘brought for’ard’ for the purpose originally mentioned. As she was observed at this time to direct baleful glances towards the Marshalsea, it has been supposed that this admirably consistent female intended by ‘him,’ Arthur Clennam. This, however, is mere speculation; who the person was, who, for the satisfaction of Mr F.’s Aunt’s mind, ought to have been brought forward and never was brought forward, will never be positively known.\n\nThe autumn days went on, and Little Dorrit never came to the Marshalsea now and went away without seeing him. No, no, no.\n\nOne morning, as Arthur listened for the light feet that every morning ascended winged to his heart, bringing the heavenly brightness of a new love into the room where the old love had wrought so hard and been so true; one morning, as he listened, he heard her coming, not alone.\n\n‘Dear Arthur,’ said her delighted voice outside the door, ‘I have some one here. May I bring some one in?’\n\nHe had thought from the tread there were two with her. He answered ‘Yes,’ and she came in with Mr Meagles. Sun-browned and jolly Mr Meagles looked, and he opened his arms and folded Arthur in them, like a sun-browned and jolly father.\n\n‘Now I am all right,’ said Mr Meagles, after a minute or so. ‘Now it’s over. Arthur, my dear fellow, confess at once that you expected me before.’\n\n‘I did,’ said Arthur; ‘but Amy told me--’\n\n‘Little Dorrit. Never any other name.’ (It was she who whispered it.)\n\n‘--But my Little Dorrit told me that, without asking for any further explanation, I was not to expect you until I saw you.’\n\n‘And now you see me, my boy,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking him by the hand stoutly; ‘and now you shall have any explanation and every explanation. The fact is, I _was_ here--came straight to you from the Allongers and Marshongers, or I should be ashamed to look you in the face this day,--but you were not in company trim at the moment, and I had to start off again to catch Doyce.’\n\n‘Poor Doyce!’ sighed Arthur.\n\n‘Don’t call him names that he don’t deserve,’ said Mr Meagles. ‘_He’s_ not poor; _he’s_ doing well enough. Doyce is a wonderful fellow over there. I assure you he is making out his case like a house a-fire. He has fallen on his legs, has Dan. Where they don’t want things done and find a man to do ‘em, that man’s off his legs; but where they do want things done and find a man to do ‘em, that man’s on his legs. You won’t have occasion to trouble the Circumlocution Office any more. Let me tell you, Dan has done without ‘em!’\n\n‘What a load you take from my mind!’ cried Arthur. ‘What happiness you give me!’\n\n‘Happiness?’ retorted Mr Meagles. ‘Don’t talk about happiness till you see Dan. I assure you Dan is directing works and executing labours over yonder, that it would make your hair stand on end to look at. He’s no public offender, bless you, now! He’s medalled and ribboned, and starred and crossed, and I don’t-know-what all’d, like a born nobleman. But we mustn’t talk about that over here.’\n\n‘Why not?’\n\n‘Oh, egad!’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head very seriously, ‘he must hide all those things under lock and key when he comes over here. They won’t do over here. In that particular, Britannia is a Britannia in the Manger--won’t give her children such distinctions herself, and won’t allow them to be seen when they are given by other countries. No, no, Dan!’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head again. ‘That won’t do here!’\n\n‘If you had brought me (except for Doyce’s sake) twice what I have lost,’ cried Arthur, ‘you would not have given me the pleasure that you give me in this news.’\n\n‘Why, of course, of course,’ assented Mr Meagles. ‘Of course I know that, my good fellow, and therefore I come out with it in the first burst. Now, to go back, about catching Doyce. I caught Doyce. Ran against him among a lot of those dirty brown dogs in women’s nightcaps a great deal too big for ‘em, calling themselves Arabs and all sorts of incoherent races. _You_ know ‘em! Well! He was coming straight to me, and I was going to him, and so we came back together.’\n\n‘Doyce in England!’ exclaimed Arthur.\n\n‘There!’ said Mr Meagles, throwing open his arms. ‘I am the worst man in the world to manage a thing of this sort. I don’t know what I should have done if I had been in the diplomatic line--right, perhaps! The long and short of it is, Arthur, we have both been in England this fortnight. And if you go on to ask where Doyce is at the present moment, why, my plain answer is--here he is! And now I can breathe again at last!’\n\nDoyce darted in from behind the door, caught Arthur by both hands, and said the rest for himself.\n\n‘There are only three branches of my subject, my dear Clennam,’ said Doyce, proceeding to mould them severally, with his plastic thumb, on the palm of his hand, ‘and they’re soon disposed of. First, not a word more from you about the past. There was an error in your calculations. I know what that is. It affects the whole machine, and failure is the consequence. You will profit by the failure, and will avoid it another time. I have done a similar thing myself, in construction, often. Every failure teaches a man something, if he will learn; and you are too sensible a man not to learn from this failure. So much for firstly. Secondly. I was sorry you should have taken it so heavily to heart, and reproached yourself so severely; I was travelling home night and day to put matters right, with the assistance of our friend, when I fell in with our friend as he has informed you. Thirdly. We two agreed, that, after what you had undergone, after your distress of mind, and after your illness, it would be a pleasant surprise if we could so far keep quiet as to get things perfectly arranged without your knowledge, and then come and say that all the affairs were smooth, that everything was right, that the business stood in greater want of you than ever it did, and that a new and prosperous career was opened before you and me as partners. That’s thirdly. But you know we always make an allowance for friction, and so I have reserved space to close in. My dear Clennam, I thoroughly confide in you; you have it in your power to be quite as useful to me as I have, or have had, it in my power to be useful to you; your old place awaits you, and wants you very much; there is nothing to detain you here one half-hour longer.’\n\nThere was silence, which was not broken until Arthur had stood for some time at the window with his back towards them, and until his little wife that was to be had gone to him and stayed by him.\n\n‘I made a remark a little while ago,’ said Daniel Doyce then, ‘which I am inclined to think was an incorrect one. I said there was nothing to detain you here, Clennam, half an hour longer. Am I mistaken in supposing that you would rather not leave here till to-morrow morning? Do I know, without being very wise, where you would like to go, direct from these walls and from this room?’\n\n‘You do,’ returned Arthur. ‘It has been our cherished purpose.’\n\n‘Very well!’ said Doyce. ‘Then, if this young lady will do me the honour of regarding me for four-and-twenty hours in the light of a father, and will take a ride with me now towards Saint Paul’s Churchyard, I dare say I know what we want to get there.’\n\nLittle Dorrit and he went out together soon afterwards, and Mr Meagles lingered behind to say a word to his friend.\n\n‘I think, Arthur, you will not want Mother and me in the morning and we will keep away. It might set Mother thinking about Pet; she’s a soft-hearted woman. She’s best at the Cottage, and I’ll stay there and keep her company.’\n\nWith that they parted for the time. And the day ended, and the night ended, and the morning came, and Little Dorrit, simply dressed as usual and having no one with her but Maggy, came into the prison with the sunshine. The poor room was a happy room that morning. Where in the world was there a room so full of quiet joy!\n\n‘My dear love,’ said Arthur. ‘Why does Maggy light the fire? We shall be gone directly.’\n\n‘I asked her to do it. I have taken such an odd fancy. I want you to burn something for me.’\n\n‘What?’\n\n‘Only this folded paper. If you will put it in the fire with your own hand, just as it is, my fancy will be gratified.’\n\n‘Superstitious, darling Little Dorrit? Is it a charm?’\n\n‘It is anything you like best, my own,’ she answered, laughing with glistening eyes and standing on tiptoe to kiss him, ‘if you will only humour me when the fire burns up.’\n\nSo they stood before the fire, waiting: Clennam with his arm about her waist, and the fire shining, as fire in that same place had often shone, in Little Dorrit’s eyes. ‘Is it bright enough now?’ said Arthur. ‘Quite bright enough now,’ said Little Dorrit. ‘Does the charm want any words to be said?’ asked Arthur, as he held the paper over the flame. ‘You can say (if you don’t mind) “I love you!”’ answered Little Dorrit. So he said it, and the paper burned away.\n\nThey passed very quietly along the yard; for no one was there, though many heads were stealthily peeping from the windows. Only one face, familiar of old, was in the Lodge. When they had both accosted it, and spoken many kind words, Little Dorrit turned back one last time with her hand stretched out, saying, ‘Good-bye, good John! I hope you will live very happy, dear!’\n\nThen they went up the steps of the neighbouring Saint George’s Church, and went up to the altar, where Daniel Doyce was waiting in his paternal character. And there was Little Dorrit’s old friend who had given her the Burial Register for a pillow; full of admiration that she should come back to them to be married, after all.\n\nAnd they were married with the sun shining on them through the painted figure of Our Saviour on the window. And they went into the very room where Little Dorrit had slumbered after her party, to sign the Marriage Register. And there, Mr Pancks, (destined to be chief clerk to Doyce and Clennam, and afterwards partner in the house), sinking the Incendiary in the peaceful friend, looked in at the door to see it done, with Flora gallantly supported on one arm and Maggy on the other, and a back-ground of John Chivery and father and other turnkeys who had run round for the moment, deserting the parent Marshalsea for its happy child. Nor had Flora the least signs of seclusion upon her, notwithstanding her recent declaration; but, on the contrary, was wonderfully smart, and enjoyed the ceremonies mightily, though in a fluttered way.\n\nLittle Dorrit’s old friend held the inkstand as she signed her name, and the clerk paused in taking off the good clergyman’s surplice, and all the witnesses looked on with special interest. ‘For, you see,’ said Little Dorrit’s old friend, ‘this young lady is one of our curiosities, and has come now to the third volume of our Registers. Her birth is in what I call the first volume; she lay asleep, on this very floor, with her pretty head on what I call the second volume; and she’s now a-writing her little name as a bride in what I call the third volume.’\n\nThey all gave place when the signing was done, and Little Dorrit and her husband walked out of the church alone. They paused for a moment on the steps of the portico, looking at the fresh perspective of the street in the autumn morning sun’s bright rays, and then went down.\n\nWent down into a modest life of usefulness and happiness. Went down to give a mother’s care, in the fulness of time, to Fanny’s neglected children no less than to their own, and to leave that lady going into Society for ever and a day. Went down to give a tender nurse and friend to Tip for some few years, who was never vexed by the great exactions he made of her in return for the riches he might have given her if he had ever had them, and who lovingly closed his eyes upon the Marshalsea and all its blighted fruits. They went quietly down into the roaring streets, inseparable and blessed; and as they passed along in sunshine and shade, the noisy and the eager, and the arrogant and the froward and the vain, fretted and chafed, and made their usual uproar.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-963"]="# BOOK THE SECOND: RICHES\n\n나는 이 이야기에 두 해 동안 수많은 작업 시간을 바쳐 왔다. 작품 전체를 통독함으로써 그 장단점이 스스로 드러나도록 맡겨 둘 수 없다면, 나는 무척이나 형편없이 일한 셈일 것이다. 그러나 띄엄띄엄 연재되어 나오는 동안 다른 누구도 그러지 못했을 만큼 내가 그 실타래를 더 끊김 없는 주의로 쥐고 있었으리라 짐작하는 것이 부당하지 않다면, 그 짜임을 완성된 상태에서, 무늬가 마무리된 채로 보아 달라고 청하는 것 또한 부당하지 않을 것이다.\n\n배너클 일가와 에두름국 같은 과장된 허구에 대해 변명을 한마디 한다면, 나는 영국인이라면 누구나 겪는 일상적 경험에서 그것을 찾겠다 — 러시아 전쟁과 첼시 조사위원회의 시절에 내가 예의범절에 그런 폭력을 가했다는 사소한 사실은 굳이 언급하지 않으면서 말이다. 머들 씨라는 그 터무니없는 착상을 감히 변호하고자 한다면, 나는 그것이 철도 주식의 시대 이후, 어느 아일랜드 은행과 그에 못지않게 칭송받던 한두 개의 사업이 있던 시절에 비롯되었다고 넌지시 일러 두겠다. 나쁜 의도가 때로는 선한 의도, 그것도 노골적으로 종교적인 의도라고 자처한다는 그 어처구니없는 상상을 완화하기 위해 무언가를 변론한다면, 그것이 영국 왕립 은행의 전직 이사들에 대한 공개 심문이 있던 시절에 이 지면에서 절정에 이르렀다는 묘한 우연을 들겠다. 그러나 나는 이 모든 점에 대하여, 필요하다면 궐석으로 판결이 내려지는 것을 감수하며, 이 나라에서 그와 같은 일은 결코 알려진 바 없다는 (믿을 만한 출처의) 보증을 받아들이겠다.\n\n내 독자들 가운데 더러는 마셜시 감옥의 어느 부분이라도 아직 남아 있는지 궁금해할지 모르겠다. 나 자신도 이달 6일에 직접 가서 보기 전까지는 알지 못했다. 여기에서 자주 언급한 바깥 앞마당이 버터 가게로 탈바꿈한 것을 발견하고, 나는 그 감옥의 벽돌 한 장까지 모두 사라진 줄로 거의 단념했다. 그러나 인근의 어느 '버몬지로 통하는 앤젤 코트'를 따라 내려가다가 나는 '마셜시 플레이스'에 이르렀다. 그곳의 집들을 나는 옛 감옥의 그 큰 건물 덩어리로 알아보았을 뿐 아니라, 내가 리틀 도릿의 전기 작가가 되었을 때 마음의 눈에 떠올랐던 방들을 고스란히 간직하고 있는 것으로 알아보았다. 내가 여태껏 이야기를 나눈 가장 작은 사내아이가, 내가 여태껏 본 가장 큰 갓난아기를 안고서, 그 일대의 옛 쓰임새에 관해 초자연적으로 영리한 설명을 내놓았는데, 거의 정확했다. 이 어린 뉴턴이(나는 그를 그렇게 판단한다) 어디서 그런 정보를 얻었는지 나는 모른다. 그는 제 힘으로 그것을 알기에는 사반세기나 너무 어렸다. 나는 리틀 도릿이 태어났고 그녀의 아버지가 그토록 오래 살았던 방의 창문을 가리키며, 지금 그 방을 세 들어 사는 사람의 이름이 무엇이냐고 물었다. 아이는 \"톰 피식\"이라고 했다. 나는 톰 피식이 누구냐고 물었고, 아이는 \"조 피식의 삼촌\"이라고 했다.\n\n조금 더 가서 나는, 의례적인 경우가 아니면 아무도 가두지 않던 그 갇힌 안쪽 감옥을 둘러싸던, 더 오래되고 더 낮은 담을 발견했다. 그러나 버몬지로 통하는 앤젤 코트에서 빠져나와 마셜시 플레이스로 들어가는 사람은 누구든, 사라진 마셜시 감옥의 바로 그 포석 위에 자기 발이 놓여 있음을 알게 될 것이며, 좌우로 좁은 마당을, 이곳이 자유로워졌을 때 담이 낮아진 것을 빼면 거의, 아니 전혀 달라지지 않은 그 마당을 보게 될 것이며, 채무자들이 살던 방들을 바라보게 될 것이며, 비참한 여러 해의 몰려드는 유령들 한가운데에 서 있게 될 것이다.\n\n「황량한 집」의 서문에서 나는 그토록 많은 독자를 가져 본 적이 없다고 적었다. 그 뒤를 잇는 작품인 「리틀 도릿」의 서문에서도 나는 여전히 같은 말을 되풀이해야겠다. 우리 사이에 자라난 애정과 신뢰를 깊이 느끼며, 나는 그때 그 서문에 덧붙였던 말을 이 서문에도 덧붙인다. 우리 다시 만나기를!\n\n런던, 1857년 5월\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Sun and Shadow\n\n햇볕이 내리쬐던 어느 날, 삼십 년 전 마르세유는 태양 아래 불타듯 누워 있었다.\n\n그 무렵 남프랑스에서, 사나운 팔월의 어느 날 작열하는 태양이란 그 전이나 그 후나 다른 어느 때보다 더 드문 일이 아니었다. 마르세유 안의, 그리고 마르세유 둘레의 모든 것이 그 이글거리는 하늘을 빤히 노려보았고, 또 그 대가로 노려봄을 당했으니, 마침내 노려보는 버릇이 그곳에 두루 퍼지고 말았다. 낯선 이들은 빤히 노려보는 흰 집들, 빤히 노려보는 흰 담장들, 빤히 노려보는 흰 거리들, 빤히 노려보는 메마른 도로의 자취들, 푸르름이 다 타 버린 채 빤히 노려보는 언덕들에 기가 질려 낯빛이 흐트러졌다. 빤히 노려보거나 번쩍이지 않는 유일한 것이라곤 포도송이의 무게에 늘어진 포도 덩굴뿐이었다. 이것들만은 더운 공기가 그 가녀린 잎을 겨우 흔들 때 이따금 조금씩 눈을 깜박이긴 했다.\n\n항구 안의 더러운 물에도, 바깥의 아름다운 바다에도 잔물결 하나 일으킬 바람이 없었다. 검은빛과 푸른빛, 그 두 색을 가르는 경계선은 맑은 바다가 넘어오지 않을 지점을 보여 주었으나, 결코 섞이지 않는 그 추악한 웅덩이만큼이나 고요히 누워 있었다. 차양 없는 배들은 너무 뜨거워 손을 댈 수 없었고, 묶여 있는 배들은 표면이 부풀어 물집이 잡혔으며, 부두의 돌들은 몇 달 동안 밤이고 낮이고 식을 줄을 몰랐다. 힌두인, 러시아인, 중국인, 스페인인, 포르투갈인, 영국인, 프랑스인, 제노바인, 나폴리인, 베네치아인, 그리스인, 터키인, 바벨탑을 쌓은 온갖 무리의 후예들이 마르세유로 장사하러 와서는 한결같이 그늘을 찾았으니 — 차마 바라볼 수 없을 만큼 너무도 짙푸른 바다와, 하나의 거대한 불꽃 보석이 박힌 자줏빛 하늘을 피해 어디든 숨을 곳으로 몸을 피했다.\n\n그 두루 퍼진 노려봄에 눈이 다 아팠다. 멀리 이탈리아 해안선 쪽으로는 과연 바다의 증발에서 천천히 피어오르는 옅은 안개 구름 덕분에 조금은 누그러졌으나, 그 밖에는 어디에서도 부드러워지지 않았다. 멀리 먼지에 깊이 잠긴 노려보는 도로들이 언덕배기에서 노려보고, 움푹한 곳에서 노려보고, 끝없는 평원에서 노려보았다. 멀리 길가 오두막 위로 늘어진 먼지투성이 포도 덩굴과, 그늘이라곤 없이 바싹 마른 나무들이 늘어선 단조로운 길가의 가로수 길이 하늘과 땅의 노려봄 아래 늘어져 있었다. 졸음에 겨운 방울을 단 말들도 긴 짐수레 행렬을 이루어 내륙을 향해 느릿느릿 기어가며 그러했고, 비스듬히 누운 마부들도 — 좀처럼 깨어 있는 일이 없었으나 — 깨어 있을 때면 그러했으며, 들판에서 지쳐 버린 일꾼들도 그러했다. 살아 있거나 자라는 모든 것이 그 작열에 짓눌렸으니, 거친 돌담 위를 잽싸게 지나가는 도마뱀과, 딸랑이처럼 메마르고 뜨거운 울음을 우는 매미만이 예외였다. 먼지마저 갈색으로 그을렸고, 마치 공기 자체가 헐떡이기라도 하듯 대기 속에서 무언가가 떨렸다.\n\n블라인드며 덧문이며 커튼이며 차양이며 모두 그 노려봄을 막으려 닫히고 쳐져 있었다. 틈새나 열쇠 구멍 하나만 허락해 보라, 그러면 백열의 화살처럼 쏘아 들어왔다. 교회들만이 그것에서 가장 자유로웠다. 깜박이는 등불이 꿈결처럼 점점이 박히고, 추한 늙은 그림자들이 경건하게 졸고 가래를 뱉고 구걸하며 꿈결처럼 모여 있는 기둥과 아치의 어스름에서 밖으로 나오는 일이란, 불의 강에 뛰어들어 가장 가까운 한 줄기 그늘을 향해 목숨 걸고 헤엄치는 것과 같았다. 그리하여, 그늘이 있는 곳이면 어디든 사람들이 빈둥거리고 드러누운 채, 혀를 놀리는 소리도 개 짖는 소리도 거의 없이, 이따금 불협화음의 교회 종이 댕댕거리고 심술궂은 북소리가 덜그럭거리는 가운데, 마르세유는 — 코로 강하게 맡고 입으로 강하게 맛볼 수 있는 하나의 실체로서 — 어느 날 태양 아래 통째로 구워지고 있었다.\n\n그날 마르세유에는 흉악한 감옥 하나가 있었다. 그 어느 방은 어찌나 역겨운 곳이던지 그 주제넘게 끼어드는 노려봄조차 그곳 앞에서는 눈을 깜박이며 외면하고, 그 방이 제 힘으로 찾아낼 수 있는 반사된 빛의 찌꺼기에나 내맡겨 두었는데, 그 안에 두 사내가 있었다. 두 사내 말고는, 벽에서 떼어 낼 수 없게 붙은, 칼로 거칠게 새긴 체커판이 패인 흠집투성이의 망가진 의자 하나와, 낡은 단추와 사골로 만든 체커 말 한 벌, 도미노 한 벌, 멍석 두 장, 그리고 포도주 병 두세 개가 있었다. 눈에 보이는 해충 — 두 사내 — 말고도 쥐를 비롯한 보이지 않는 해충들을 빼고 나면, 그 방에 든 것은 그것이 전부였다.\n\n그 방은 제법 큰 창문 모양으로 짜인 쇠창살을 통해 들어오는 만큼의 빛을 받았는데, 그 창살이 면한 음침한 계단에서는 그 격자를 통해 언제든 방 안을 들여다볼 수 있었다. 이 창살에는 그 밑부분이 석조에 박혀 있는, 땅에서 서너 자쯤 높이의 넓고 단단한 돌 선반이 달려 있었다. 그 위에 두 사내 가운데 하나가 반쯤 앉고 반쯤 누운 채로, 무릎을 끌어당기고 두 발과 두 어깨를 그 구멍의 양쪽 면에 버틴 채 비스듬히 기대어 있었다. 창살은 그가 팔을 팔꿈치까지 내밀 수 있을 만큼 사이가 벌어져 있었기에, 그는 한층 편하게 있으려고 무심히 거기에 매달려 있었다.\n\n그곳의 모든 것에 감옥의 더러움이 배어 있었다. 갇힌 공기, 갇힌 빛, 갇힌 습기, 갇힌 사람들이 모두 그 감금으로 인해 변질되어 있었다. 갇힌 사람들이 빛바래고 초췌하듯, 쇠는 녹슬었고, 돌은 미끈거렸으며, 나무는 썩었고, 공기는 희미했으며, 빛은 어두웠다. 우물처럼, 지하실처럼, 무덤처럼, 그 감옥은 바깥의 밝음을 알지 못했으니, 인도양의 어느 향료 섬 한복판에 놓아 둔다 해도 제 오염된 공기를 고스란히 간직했으리라.\n\n창살 선반에 누운 사내는 추위마저 느꼈다. 그는 한쪽 어깨를 신경질적으로 움직여 커다란 망토를 한층 무겁게 끌어당기고는 으르렁거렸다. \"여기엔 결코 비치지도 않는 이 산적 같은 태양이라니, 망할 놈 같으니!\"\n\n그는 먹을 것이 오기를 기다리며, 계단 아래쪽을 더 멀리 내다보려고 창살 사이로 옆으로 곁눈질하고 있었는데, 그 표정에는 비슷한 기다림에 처한 들짐승의 기색이 다분히 어려 있었다. 그러나 너무 가까이 붙은 그의 두 눈은 짐승의 왕의 눈이 그 머리에 고귀하게 박혀 있는 것만큼 그렇게 박혀 있지 않았고, 밝다기보다는 날카로웠으니 — 그것을 드러낼 표면이라곤 거의 없는 뾰족한 흉기였다. 그 눈에는 깊이도 변화도 없었고, 그저 번득이며 떴다 감겼다 할 뿐이었다. 그것이 그에게 어떤 쓸모가 있는지를 차치하고 본다면, 시계공이라도 그보다 나은 한 쌍을 만들 수 있었으리라. 그는 매부리코를 가졌는데, 그 나름으로는 잘생긴 코였으나, 그의 두 눈이 서로 너무 가까운 만큼 꼭 그만큼 두 눈 사이에서 너무 높이 솟아 있었다. 그 밖에는, 그는 골격이 크고 키가 컸으며, 두툼한 콧수염 사이로 드러나는 곳마다 얇은 입술을 가졌고, 텁수룩한 상태에서는 무어라 규정할 수 없는 빛깔이지만 붉은 기가 도는 푸석한 머리털이 수북했다. 그가 창살을 붙잡은 손은(그 손등은 갓 아문 보기 흉한 생채기로 온통 줄이 가 있었다) 유난히 작고 통통했으며, 감옥의 때만 아니었다면 유난히 희었을 것이었다.\n\n또 다른 사내는 거친 갈색 외투를 덮은 채 돌바닥에 누워 있었다.\n\n\"일어나, 이 돼지야!\" 첫 번째 사내가 으르렁거렸다. \"내가 배고픈데 잠이나 자다니.\"\n\n\"다 마찬가집니다요, 나리.\" 돼지는 순순한 태도로, 그러면서도 명랑함이 없지 않게 말했다. \"저는 깨고 싶을 때 깨고, 자고 싶을 때 잡니다. 다 똑같지요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그는 일어나 몸을 털고 긁적이더니, 갈색 외투를 (그는 그것을 앞서 덮개로 썼다) 소매로 목에 헐겁게 둘러매고는, 창살 맞은편 벽에 등을 기댄 채 하품을 하며 포석 위에 앉았다.\n\n\"지금 몇 시인지 말해 봐.\" 첫 번째 사내가 투덜거렸다.\n\n\"정오 종이 울릴 겁니다 — 사십 분 후에요.\" 그 짧은 사이를 둘 때, 그는 무슨 확실한 정보라도 구하듯 감옥 방 안을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"넌 시계로구나. 어떻게 늘 그렇게 아는 거지?\"\n\n\"제가 어찌 말씀드리겠습니까? 저는 늘 몇 시인지, 제가 어디 있는지 압니다. 저는 밤중에, 그것도 배에서 이리로 끌려 들어왔지만 제가 어디 있는지 압니다. 보십시오! 여기가 마르세유 항구입니다.\" 그는 포석 위에 무릎을 꿇고 거무스름한 집게손가락으로 그 모든 것을 지도처럼 그려 나갔다. \"툴롱(거기엔 갤리선들이 있지요), 저쪽이 스페인, 저쪽이 알제. 여기 왼쪽으로 슬그머니 빠지면 니스. 코르니체를 돌아 제노바로. 제노바 방파제와 항구. 검역 구역. 거기 도시가 있고, 벨라돈나가 붉게 물든 테라스 정원들. 여기, 포르토피노. 리보르노로 나아가고. 다시 치비타베키아로 나가서, 그렇게 — 어이! 나폴리 들어갈 자리가 없네.\" 이쯤에서 그는 벽에 다다라 있었다. \"하지만 다 마찬가집니다. 거긴 저 안쪽에 있어요!\"\n\n그는 무릎을 꿇은 채로 감옥치고는 활기찬 표정으로 동료 죄수를 올려다보았다. 햇볕에 그을린, 날쌔고 날렵한 작은 사내였는데, 다소 다부진 체격이었다. 갈색 귀에 귀고리를 달고, 흰 이가 그 기괴한 갈색 얼굴을 환히 밝히고, 새카만 머리털이 갈색 목둘레에 곱슬곱슬 엉겨 있었으며, 갈색 가슴께가 드러나도록 너덜너덜한 붉은 셔츠를 풀어헤치고 있었다. 헐렁한 뱃사람 바지에, 그런대로 멀쩡한 신발, 길쭉한 붉은 모자, 허리에 두른 붉은 띠, 그리고 그 띠에 꽂은 칼.\n\n\"제가 갈 때처럼 나폴리에서 돌아올 수 있을지 따져 보십시오! 여기 보십시오, 나리! 치비타베키아, 리보르노, 포르토피노, 제노바, 코르니체, 니스 앞바다(그건 저 안쪽에 있지요), 마르세유, 나리와 저. 간수와 그 열쇠들이 있는 방은 이 엄지를 짚은 데고요, 여기 제 손목께에 나라의 면도칼을 그 상자에 넣어 두는데 — 단두대를 잠가 두는 겁니다.\"\n\n또 다른 사내가 느닷없이 포석 위에 침을 뱉고는 목구멍을 그르렁거렸다.\n\n곧이어 어디 아래쪽의 자물쇠가 제 목구멍을 그르렁거리더니, 이어 문 하나가 쾅 닫혔다. 느릿한 발걸음이 계단을 올라오기 시작했고, 그 소리에 어느 달콤하고 작은 목소리의 재잘거림이 섞여 들었다. 그러고는 감옥지기가 세 살이나 네 살쯤 된 딸아이와 바구니 하나를 안고 나타났다.\n\n\"오늘 오전엔 세상이 어떻게 돌아갑니까, 신사 양반들? 보시다시피 제 어린것이 저와 함께 돌면서 아비의 새들을 구경하는 중이지요. 아이고, 그래! 새들을 봐라, 우리 예쁜이, 새들을 봐.\"\n\n그는 아이를 창살께로 들어 올리면서 자신도 그 새들을, 특히 그 작은 새를 날카롭게 바라보았는데, 그 작은 새의 민첩함이 미심쩍은 모양이었다. \"당신 빵을 가져왔소, 조반니 바티스타 씨.\" 그가 말했다(다들 프랑스어로 말했으나, 그 작은 사내는 이탈리아인이었다). \"그리고 노름은 하지 마시라고 권해도 된다면 —\"\n\n\"나리께는 권하지 않으시는군요!\" 조반니 바티스타가 웃으며 이를 드러내고 말했다.\n\n\"오! 하지만 나리는 따고,\" 간수가 또 다른 사내에게 딱히 호감이라곤 없는 눈길을 슬쩍 던지며 대꾸했다. \"당신은 잃잖소. 그건 전혀 다른 얘기지. 당신은 그걸로 푸석한 빵에 시큼한 술이나 얻고, 저 양반은 그걸로 리옹 소시지에, 향긋한 젤리에 든 송아지 고기에, 흰 빵에, 스트라키노 치즈에, 좋은 포도주를 얻으니 말이오. 새들을 봐라, 우리 예쁜이!\"\n\n\"불쌍한 새들!\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n천상의 연민이 어린 그 어여쁘고 작은 얼굴이 창살 사이로 움츠리며 들여다보는 모습은, 감옥 안의 천사 같았다. 조반니 바티스타가 일어나 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 마치 그것이 자신에게 좋은 끌림이라도 되는 듯했다. 또 다른 새는, 바구니를 향한 조급한 눈길만 빼면, 여전히 그대로였다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 간수가 어린 딸을 창살 바깥 선반에 앉히며 말했다. \"이 애가 새들에게 먹이를 줄 거요. 이 큼직한 빵 덩이는 조반니 바티스타 씨 것이지. 새장 안으로 넣으려면 잘라야겠군. 자, 이 작은 손에 입맞춤을 하는 길든 새가 있구나! 포도 잎에 싼 이 소시지는 리고 씨 것. 또 — 향긋한 젤리에 든 이 송아지 고기는 리고 씨 것. 또 — 이 작고 흰 빵 세 개는 리고 씨 것. 또, 이 치즈 — 또, 이 포도주 — 또, 이 담배 — 모두 리고 씨 것. 복도 많은 새지!\"\n\n아이는 이 모든 것을 창살 사이로 그 부드럽고 매끄러우며 잘생긴 손에 넣어 주었는데, 분명 두려워하는 기색으로 — 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 제 손을 도로 빼고는, 절반은 겁먹고 절반은 화가 난 듯한 표정으로 어여쁜 이마를 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 그 사내를 바라보았다. 반면, 조반니 바티스타의 거뭇하고 비늘 같고 마디진 손에는(그의 여덟 손가락과 두 엄지를 다 합쳐도 리고 씨의 손톱 하나 만큼도 못 될 만큼 손톱이 거의 없었다) 선뜻한 믿음으로 거친 빵 덩이를 쥐여 주었으며, 그가 제 손에 입을 맞추자 아이는 손수 그 손을 그의 얼굴에 어루만지듯 쓸어 주기까지 했다. 리고 씨는 이 차별에 아랑곳하지 않고, 아이가 무얼 줄 때마다 딸을 보고 웃으며 고개를 끄덕여 아비의 비위를 맞추었다. 그러고는 자신이 기댄 선반의 편리한 구석구석에 온갖 음식을 다 늘어놓자마자, 식욕이 동하여 먹기 시작했다.\n\n리고 씨가 웃을 때면 그 얼굴에 변화가 일어났는데, 그것은 호감을 주기보다는 더 두드러진 것이었다. 콧수염이 코 밑으로 올라가고 코가 콧수염 위로 내려오는 것이, 무척이나 음험하고 잔인한 모양새였다.\n\n\"자!\" 간수가 부스러기를 털어 내려고 바구니를 거꾸로 뒤집으며 말했다. \"받은 돈은 죄다 썼소. 여기 그 명세가 있고, 그러니 그 일은 마무리됐군. 리고 씨, 어제 내가 예상한 대로, 재판장께서 오늘 정오 한 시간 뒤에 당신과 함께하는 즐거움을 청하실 거요.\"\n\n\"날 재판하겠다, 그거요?\" 리고가 칼을 손에 쥐고 입에 음식을 문 채 멈추며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 말했소. 당신을 재판한다고.\"\n\n\"내겐 소식이 없습니까?\" 흡족하게 빵을 우물거리기 시작한 조반니 바티스타가 물었다.\n\n간수가 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 저는 평생 여기 누워 있어야 하는 겁니까, 아버님?\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠나!\" 간수가 남쪽 사람 특유의 잽쌈으로 그에게 홱 돌아서며, 마치 그를 갈가리 찢어 놓겠다고 으름장이라도 놓듯 두 손과 열 손가락을 다 놀려 가며 소리쳤다. \"이보게, 자네가 여기 얼마나 오래 누워 있을지 내가 어찌 알 수 있단 말인가? 내가 무얼 알겠나, 조반니 바티스타 카발레토? 내 목숨을 걸고! 여기엔 가끔 재판을 받겠다고 그리 지독하게 서두르지 않는 죄수들도 있다네.\"\n\n그는 이 말을 하면서 리고 씨를 비스듬히 흘끔거리는 듯했으나, 리고 씨는 이미 식사를 다시 시작한 터였다. 다만 아까만큼 잽싼 식욕은 아니었다.\n\n\"잘 있거라, 내 새들아!\" 감옥지기가 어여쁜 아이를 품에 안고, 입맞춤과 더불어 그 말을 일러 주며 말했다.\n\n\"잘 있거라, 내 새들아!\" 어여쁜 아이가 따라 했다.\n\n그가 아이를 안고 걸어가며 아이에게 어린이 놀이 노래를 불러 줄 때, 그 천진한 얼굴이 그의 어깨 너머로 어찌나 환하게 뒤돌아보던지,\n\n\"이리 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이 누구인가? 마욜렌의 길동무여! 이리 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이 누구인가? 언제나 즐거이!\"\n\n조반니 바티스타는 창살에서, 비록 좀 쉰 목소리이긴 했으나 박자와 가락을 맞추어 화답하는 것을 명예로운 일로 여겼다.\n\n\"임금님의 기사들 중에 그것은 꽃이라네, 마욜렌의 길동무여! 임금님의 기사들 중에 그것은 꽃이라네, 언제나 즐거이!\"\n\n이 노래는 그 가파른 몇 계단 아래까지 그들을 따라갔기에, 감옥지기는 끝내 걸음을 멈추고 어린 딸이 노래를 끝까지 듣고 아직 그들이 보이는 동안 후렴을 따라 부르게 해 주어야 했다. 그러더니 아이의 머리가 사라지고 감옥지기의 머리가 사라졌으나, 그 작은 목소리는 문이 쾅 닫힐 때까지 그 가락을 길게 이어 갔다.\n\n리고 씨는 메아리가 채 그치기도 전에(메아리조차 감금으로 인해 약해져서 늑장을 부리는 듯했다) 귀 기울여 듣고 있는 조반니 바티스타가 제 앞을 가로막고 있는 것을 보고는, 발로 한 번 떠밀며 그에게 본래의 더 어두운 자리로 돌아가는 편이 낫다고 일깨워 주었다. 그 작은 사내는 포석에 더없이 익숙한 자의 무심한 편안함으로 다시 포석 위에 앉았다. 그러고는 거친 빵 덩이 세 개를 제 앞에 놓고 네 번째 덩이에 달려들더니, 그것들을 다 치우는 일이 일종의 놀이라도 되는 듯 흡족하게 헤쳐 나가기 시작했다.\n\n어쩌면 그는 리옹 소시지를 흘끔거렸을 테고, 어쩌면 향긋한 젤리에 든 송아지 고기를 흘끔거렸을 테지만, 그것들은 그의 입에 군침을 돌게 할 만큼 오래 거기 있지 않았다. 리고 씨가 재판장이며 법정 따위는 아랑곳없이 곧 그것들을 해치우고는, 손가락을 최대한 깨끗이 빨고 포도 잎에 닦았다. 그러더니 술을 마시다 말고 동료 죄수를 바라보려 멈추자, 콧수염이 올라가고 코가 내려왔다.\n\n\"빵 맛이 어떤가?\"\n\n\"좀 퍽퍽하지만, 저는 여기 제 오랜 소스가 있지요.\" 조반니 바티스타가 칼을 들어 보이며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"무슨 소스?\"\n\n\"제 빵을 이렇게 — 멜론처럼 자를 수 있죠. 아니면 이렇게 — 오믈렛처럼. 아니면 이렇게 — 튀긴 생선처럼. 아니면 이렇게 — 리옹 소시지처럼요.\" 조반니 바티스타가 들고 있던 빵에 여러 가지 자르는 시늉을 해 보이며, 입에 든 것을 천연덕스럽게 씹으면서 말했다.\n\n\"자!\" 리고 씨가 소리쳤다. \"마셔도 좋아. 이걸 다 비워도 돼.\"\n\n대단한 선물은 아니었으니, 남은 포도주가 지극히 적었기 때문이다. 그러나 카발레토 씨는 벌떡 일어나 고맙게 그 병을 받아 들고, 입에 거꾸로 대어 들이켜고는 입맛을 다셨다.\n\n\"병은 나머지하고 같이 치워 둬.\" 리고가 말했다.\n\n그 작은 사내는 시키는 대로 하고는, 그에게 불붙인 성냥을 건넬 채비를 하고 섰다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 이제 담배와 함께 들여온 작은 종이 사각 조각들을 써서 담배를 말아 궐련을 만드는 중이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"자! 너도 하나 가져도 좋아.\"\n\n\"천 번 감사합니다, 나리!\" 조반니 바티스타가 제 나라 말로, 제 나라 사람들 특유의 잽싸고 비위 맞추는 태도로 말했다.\n\n리고 씨는 일어나서 궐련에 불을 붙이고, 남은 것을 가슴주머니에 넣고는, 의자 위에 길게 몸을 뻗고 누웠다. 카발레토는 두 손으로 양쪽 발목을 하나씩 잡은 채 포석 위에 앉아 평온히 담배를 피웠다. 리고 씨의 눈이, 지도에서 엄지를 짚었던 그 포석 부분의 바로 그 언저리에 무언가 거북하게 끌리는 듯했다. 그 눈이 어찌나 그쪽으로 끌려가던지, 그 이탈리아인은 한 번 이상 놀란 기색으로 그 눈길을 포석까지 따라갔다가 돌아오곤 했다.\n\n\"이거야말로 지긋지긋한 굴이로군!\" 리고 씨가 긴 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"저 대낮의 빛을 좀 봐. 대낮이라고? 지난주의 빛, 여섯 달 전의 빛, 여섯 해 전의 빛이지. 저토록 늘어지고 죽어 빠진 꼴이라니!\"\n\n그 빛은, 하늘이라곤 — 아니, 그 무엇도 — 결코 보이지 않는, 계단 벽의 창을 가린 네모난 깔때기를 통해 맥없이 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"카발레토,\" 두 사람 다 저도 모르게 눈을 돌렸던 그 깔때기에서 별안간 눈길을 거두며 리고 씨가 말했다. \"넌 내가 신사라는 걸 알지?\"\n\n\"여부가 있습니까, 여부가요!\"\n\n\"우리가 여기 온 지 얼마나 됐지?\"\n\n\"저는 내일 밤 자정이면 열한 주, 나리는 오늘 오후 다섯 시면 아홉 주하고 사흘입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 여기서 무슨 일이라도 한 적이 있나? 빗자루에 손을 댄 적이, 멍석을 깐 적이, 그걸 말아 올린 적이, 체커 말을 찾아온 적이, 도미노를 거둔 적이, 그 어떤 종류의 일에든 손을 댄 적이 있나?\"\n\n\"한 번도요!\"\n\n\"내가 무슨 일이든 하리라고 기대해 본 적이 있나?\"\n\n조반니 바티스타는 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 까딱이는 그 독특한 동작으로 답했는데, 그것은 이탈리아어에서 가장 표현력 풍부한 부정이었다.\n\n\"없지! 넌 여기서 날 처음 본 그 순간부터 내가 신사라는 걸 알았어?\"\n\n\"알트로!\" 조반니 바티스타가 눈을 감고 머리를 더없이 격렬하게 홱 젖히며 대꾸했다. 그 단어는 제노바식 억양에 따라 긍정도, 부정도, 단언도, 부인도, 비웃음도, 칭찬도, 농담도, 그 밖의 쉰 가지도 되었는데, 지금 이 경우에는 글로는 도저히 다 옮길 수 없는 의미를 담아, 우리 영국인에게 친숙한 \"그렇고말고요!\"가 되었다.\n\n\"하하! 옳거니! 나는 신사야! 신사로 살고, 신사로 죽을 것이다! 신사가 되는 게 내 뜻이지. 그게 내 노름이야. 내 영혼을 걸고, 어디를 가든 나는 그걸 끝까지 해낼 테다!\"\n\n그는 자세를 바꿔 앉으며 의기양양한 기색으로 외쳤다.\n\n\"내가 여기 있다! 날 봐! 운명의 주사위 통에서 흔들려 나와 한낱 밀수꾼 따위와 한패가 되다니 — 서류가 잘못된 가엾고 보잘것없는 밀매 장사꾼과 갇혀 버리다니. 게다가 이자는 (국경을 넘으려는 수단으로) 제 배를, 마찬가지로 서류가 잘못된 다른 보잘것없는 자들이 쓰도록 내준 죄로 경찰에 붙잡혔지. 그런데 이자가 이런 빛 속에서도, 이런 곳에서도 본능적으로 내 신분을 알아본단 말이야. 잘된 일이지! 맹세코! 노름이 어찌 흘러가든 나는 이기는 거야.\"\n\n또다시 그의 콧수염이 올라가고 코가 내려왔다.\n\n\"지금 몇 시야?\" 그가 물었는데, 그 위에 마르고 뜨거운 창백함이 어려 있어 즐거움과는 좀처럼 어울리기 어려웠다.\n\n\"정오에서 반 시간 조금 지났습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아! 재판장이 곧 신사 한 사람을 앞에 두게 되겠군. 자! 무슨 죄목인지 말해 줄까? 지금이 아니면 영영 기회가 없을 테니, 나는 여기로 돌아오지 않을 거거든. 풀려나든가, 아니면 면도 준비를 시키러 끌려가든가 하겠지. 면도칼을 어디 두는지 너도 알잖아.\"\n\n카발레토 씨는 벌린 입술 사이에서 궐련을 빼냈는데, 예상보다 더 순간적으로 당황한 기색을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"나는 —\" 리고 씨가 그 말을 하려고 일어섰다. \"나는 사해동포의 신사야. 나는 특정한 나라를 두지 않아. 내 아버지는 스위스 사람 — 보 주 출신이었지. 내 어머니는 핏줄로는 프랑스인, 태생으로는 영국인이었어. 나 자신은 벨기에에서 태어났고. 나는 세계의 시민이야.\"\n\n망토 자락 속에서 한 팔을 허리에 짚고 선 그 연극적인 태도며, 동료는 안중에도 두지 않고 그 대신 맞은편 벽을 향해 말하는 그 투를 보면, 그는 조반니 바티스타 카발레토 같은 보잘것없는 자 하나를 깨우치느라 굳이 수고하는 것이라기보다는, 머지않아 자신이 받게 될 심문의 그 재판장을 위해 예행연습을 하고 있는 듯했다.\n\n\"내 나이는 서른다섯쯤으로 쳐 둬. 나는 세상을 두루 보았어. 여기서도 살아 보고, 저기서도 살아 보고, 어디서나 신사처럼 살았지. 어디서나 신사로 대접받고 존경받았어. 내가 잔재주로 먹고살았다는 식으로 몰아붙여 나를 헐뜯으려 든다면 — 너희 변호사들은, 너희 정치가들은, 너희 모사꾼들은, 너희 거래소 사내들은 대체 어떻게 먹고사는데?\"\n\n그는 그 작고 매끄러운 손을 끊임없이 동원했는데, 마치 그것이 전에도 그에게 종종 좋은 봉사를 해 온, 그의 신사다움을 증언하는 증인이라도 되는 듯했다.\n\n\"두 해 전에 나는 마르세유로 왔어. 내가 가난했다는 건 인정하지. 나는 앓고 있었거든. 너희 변호사들이, 너희 정치가들이, 너희 모사꾼들이, 너희 거래소 사내들이 앓아눕고도 돈을 긁어모아 두지 못했다면, 그자들도 가난해지는 거야. 나는 황금십자가 여관에 묵었는데 — 그땐 앙리 바로노 씨가 꾸려 가고 있었지 — 적어도 예순다섯에, 건강이 기우는 형편이었어. 내가 그 집에 넉 달쯤 살았을 때 앙리 바로노 씨가 죽는 불행을 당했지. 어쨌거나, 그게 그리 드문 불행은 아니잖아. 내 손 하나 안 거치고도 꽤 자주 일어나는 일이지.\"\n\n조반니 바티스타가 궐련을 손가락 끝까지 다 피워 버리자, 리고 씨는 너그럽게도 그에게 하나를 더 던져 주었다. 그는 두 번째 것을 첫 번째 것의 재에 붙여 계속 피우면서, 자기 사건에 골몰하느라 자기를 거의 쳐다보지도 않는 동료를 곁눈질로 바라보았다.\n\n\"바로노 씨는 미망인을 남겼어. 그 여자는 스물둘이었지. 미인이라는 평판을 얻고 있었고, (흔히 그것과는 별개인 일이지만) 실제로도 아름다웠어. 나는 계속 황금십자가에 살았지. 나는 바로노 부인과 결혼했어. 그런 짝이 그리 크게 어울리지 않았는지 어쨌는지는 내가 말할 바가 아니야. 지금 나는 감옥의 오염을 뒤집어쓴 채 여기 서 있지만, 어쩌면 너는 내가 그 여자의 전남편보다 그 여자에게 더 잘 어울린다고 여길지도 모르지.\"\n\n그에게는 잘생긴 사내인 양하는 어떤 분위기가 있었으나 — 그는 잘생기지 않았다. 그리고 교양 있는 사내인 양하는 어떤 분위기가 있었으나 — 그는 교양 있지 않았다. 그저 허세요 도발이었을 뿐이었다. 그러나 다른 많은 일에서도 그렇듯, 이 점에서도 큰소리치는 단언이 세상의 절반에서는 증거로 통하는 법이다.\n\n\"어찌 됐든, 바로노 부인은 나를 마음에 들어 했어. 설마 그게 나를 헐뜯을 거리가 되는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n이 물음과 함께 그의 눈이 마침 조반니 바티스타에게 닿자, 그 작은 사내는 부정의 뜻으로 잽싸게 고개를 가로젓고는, 따지는 듯한 어조로 숨죽여 알트로, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로 — 이루 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 되풀이했다.\n\n\"이제 우리 처지의 곤란함이 닥쳐왔지. 나는 자존심이 강해. 자존심을 변호하려는 건 아니지만, 나는 자존심이 강하단 말이야. 또 다스리는 것이 내 천성이기도 해. 나는 굴복할 수 없어. 나는 다스려야 해. 불행히도 리고 부인의 재산은 그 여자 앞으로 묶여 있었지. 그게 그 죽은 전남편의 미친 짓이었어. 더 불행히도, 그 여자에겐 친척들이 있었어. 아내의 친척들이, 신사이고, 자존심이 강하고, 다스려야만 하는 남편을 거슬러 끼어들면, 그 결과는 평화에 해롭게 마련이지. 우리 사이엔 또 다른 불화의 씨앗이 있었어. 불행히도 리고 부인은 좀 천박했어. 나는 그 여자의 몸가짐을 다듬고 전반적인 품격을 끌어올리려 애썼지. 그런데 그 여자는 (이 일에서도 친척들의 부추김을 받아) 내 노력에 발끈했어. 우리 사이에 싸움이 일기 시작했고, 그것이 리고 부인 친척들의 험담으로 퍼지고 부풀려져서 이웃들에게 소문이 자자해졌지. 내가 리고 부인을 잔인하게 대했다는 말이 돌더군. 내가 그 여자의 뺨을 때리는 게 목격되었을 수는 있어 — 그 이상은 아니야. 나는 손이 가벼워서, 설령 내가 그런 식으로 리고 부인을 바로잡는 것처럼 보였다 해도, 그건 거의 장난삼아 한 거였어.\"\n\n만일 이 대목에서 리고 씨의 그 장난기가 그 미소에 조금이라도 드러났다고 한다면, 리고 부인의 친척들은 차라리 그가 그 불행한 여인을 진지하게 바로잡아 주는 편이 훨씬 나았겠다고 말했으리라.\n\n\"나는 예민하고 용감해. 예민하고 용감한 것을 무슨 미덕인 양 내세우는 건 아니지만, 그게 내 천성이지. 리고 부인의 남자 친척들이 드러내 놓고 나섰더라면, 나는 그자들을 다룰 줄 알았을 거야. 그자들도 그걸 알았기에, 음모를 은밀히 꾸몄지. 그 결과 리고 부인과 나는 자주, 불행하게도 부딪치게 되었어. 내 개인적인 씀씀이로 푼돈을 좀 얻으려 할 때조차, 충돌 없이는 얻을 수가 없었어 — 게다가 나는 다스리는 것이 천성인 사내인데도 말이야! 어느 날 밤, 리고 부인과 나는 바다를 굽어보는 어느 높은 곳에서 다정하게 — 연인처럼이라 해도 좋겠지 — 거닐고 있었어. 불길한 별이 끼어 리고 부인이 자기 친척 얘기를 꺼내게 만들었지. 나는 그 일로 그 여자를 타이르고, 남편을 향한 그자들의 시샘 어린 적의에 휘둘리는 데서 드러나는 도리와 헌신의 결여를 나무랐어. 리고 부인이 대들었고, 나도 맞받아쳤지. 리고 부인이 흥분했고, 나도 흥분해서 그 여자를 자극했어. 그건 인정해. 솔직함도 내 천성의 한 부분이니까. 마침내 리고 부인이, 내가 두고두고 통탄할 격분에 사로잡혀, 격정의 비명을 지르며 (틀림없이 어느 정도 거리에서 들렸다는 그 비명이지) 나에게 달려들어, 내 옷을 찢고, 내 머리털을 쥐어뜯고, 내 손을 할퀴고, 흙먼지를 짓밟고 차더니, 끝내 뛰어내려 아래쪽 바위에 몸을 부딪쳐 죽고 말았어. 이것이 바로 그 일련의 사건인데, 악의가 이것을 비틀어, 내가 리고 부인에게 권리 포기를 강요하려 했고, 그 여자가 내가 요구한 양보를 끝내 거부하자 그 여자와 몸싸움을 벌여 — 그 여자를 죽였다는 식으로 만들어 버린 거야!\"\n\n그는 옆으로 비켜서서, 포도 잎이 아직 여기저기 흩어져 있는 선반으로 가더니, 두세 장을 주워 들고는, 빛을 등진 채 거기에 손을 닦으며 섰다.\n\n\"자,\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 그가 물었다. \"이 모든 얘기에 넌 할 말이 없나?\"\n\n\"흉한 얘깁니다.\" 그 작은 사내가 대꾸했다. 그는 일어나서, 한 팔을 벽에 기댄 채 신발에 칼을 갈아 번쩍이게 하고 있었다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이야?\"\n\n조반니 바티스타는 말없이 칼을 닦았다.\n\n\"내가 사건을 제대로 말하지 않았다는 뜻인가?\"\n\n\"알 — 트로!\" 조반니 바티스타가 대꾸했다. 이제 그 단어는 사과였고, \"오, 천만에요!\"를 뜻했다.\n\n\"그럼 뭐야?\"\n\n\"재판장이며 법정이라는 게 워낙 편견이 심해서요.\"\n\n\"흥,\" 또 다른 사내가 욕설과 함께 망토 자락을 어깨 너머로 거북하게 홱 넘기며 외쳤다. \"마음대로 해 보라지!\"\n\n\"정말이지 그자들은 그럴 겁니다.\" 조반니 바티스타가 칼을 띠에 꽂으려고 고개를 숙이며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다.\n\n양쪽에서 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 둘 다 서성이기 시작하여 돌 때마다 어쩔 수 없이 엇갈렸다. 리고 씨는 이따금 멈춰 서서, 마치 자기 사건을 새로운 관점에서 풀어놓거나 무슨 성난 항변이라도 하려는 듯했다. 그러나 카발레토 씨가 눈을 내리깐 채 기괴한 종종걸음으로 계속 느릿느릿 오락가락하는 통에, 이 멈칫거림에서는 아무것도 나오지 않았다.\n\n이윽고 자물쇠에 열쇠가 들어가는 소리가 두 사람을 다 멈춰 세웠다. 이어 목소리들이 들리고, 발걸음 소리가 뒤따랐다. 문이 쾅 닫히더니, 목소리와 발걸음이 다가왔고, 감옥지기가 천천히 계단을 올라왔는데, 그 뒤를 병사들의 호위대가 따랐다.\n\n\"자, 리고 씨.\" 그가 열쇠를 손에 쥔 채 창살께에 잠시 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"수고스럽지만 나오시오.\"\n\n\"보아하니 격식을 차려 떠나는 모양이군?\"\n\n\"아니, 그러지 않았다간,\" 간수가 대꾸했다. \"당신은 산산조각이 나서 떠나, 다시 끼워 맞추기도 어렵게 될지 모르니 말이오. 군중이 있소, 리고 씨, 그런데 그자들이 당신을 사랑하질 않거든.\"\n\n그는 시야 밖으로 지나가더니, 방 한구석의 낮은 문 하나를 따고 빗장을 풀었다. \"자,\" 그가 그 문을 열고 안에 모습을 드러내며 말했다. \"나오시오.\"\n\n그때의 리고 씨 얼굴빛 같은 흰빛은 태양 아래 온갖 빛깔 가운데 그 어떤 흰빛에도 견줄 수 없었다. 또한 한 줄 한 줄 잔금마다 겁에 질린 심장이 뛰는 것이 들여다보이는 그런 표정 같은 표정은, 인간의 얼굴에 떠오르는 그 어떤 표정에도 견줄 수 없었다. 둘 다 으레 죽음에 빗대지지만, 그 차이란 곧 싸움이 다 끝나 버린 상태와, 가장 절박한 막바지에 이른 싸움 사이의, 그 깊디깊은 심연 전부였다.\n\n그는 동료의 것에 자신의 종이 궐련을 또 하나 붙여, 그것을 이 사이에 꽉 물고는, 부드럽고 챙이 처진 모자로 머리를 덮고, 망토 자락을 다시 어깨 너머로 넘기더니, 카발레토 씨에게는 더 이상 아무 눈길도 주지 않고 그 문이 면한 옆 회랑으로 걸어 나갔다. 그 작은 사내로 말하자면, 그의 온 정신은 그 문 가까이 다가가 밖을 내다보는 데 빨려들어 있었다. 꼭 짐승이 제 굴의 열린 문으로 다가가 그 너머의 자유를 노려보듯, 그는 그 몇 순간을 지켜보고 엿보며 보내다가, 마침내 문이 그를 가두며 닫혔다.\n\n병사들을 지휘하는 장교가 하나 있었는데, 건장하고 쓸모 있어 보이며 더없이 침착한 사내로, 뽑아 든 검을 손에 들고 궐련을 피우고 있었다. 그는 아주 간략하게 리고 씨를 무리 한가운데에 세우도록 지시하고는, 더없는 무심함으로 스스로 그 선두에 서서 \"행진!\" 하고 호령했다. 그리하여 그들은 모두 절그럭거리며 계단을 내려갔다. 문이 쾅 닫히고 — 열쇠가 돌아가고 — 한 줄기 색다른 빛과 한 모금 색다른 공기가 그 감옥을 스쳐 지나간 듯하더니, 궐련에서 피어오른 가느다란 연기 한 자락 속으로 사라졌다.\n\n여전히 갇힌 처지로, 하등 동물처럼 — 어떤 안달하는 원숭이나, 깨어난 작은 종류의 곰처럼 — 이제 홀로 남겨진 죄수는 이 떠나감을 한 자락도 놓치지 않으려고 선반 위로 뛰어올랐다. 그가 여전히 두 손으로 창살을 움켜쥐고 선 채로 있을 때, 한바탕 소란이 그의 귀를 덮쳐 왔다. 아우성, 비명, 욕설, 협박, 저주가 모두 그 안에 담겨 있었으나, (폭풍 속에서처럼) 또렷이 들리는 것이라곤 미친 듯 들끓는 소리의 물결뿐이었다.\n\n더 알고 싶은 조바심에 한층 더 우리에 갇힌 들짐승을 닮아 가며, 죄수는 날렵하게 뛰어내려 방 안을 빙 돌아 달리고, 다시 날렵하게 뛰어올라 창살을 움켜쥐고 흔들어 보려 하고, 뛰어내려 달리고, 뛰어올라 귀를 기울이며, 그 소리가 점점 더 멀어져 사그라들 때까지 한시도 가만있지 못했다. 그보다 훌륭한 얼마나 많은 죄수들이 그렇게 제 고귀한 심장을 닳아 없앴던가. 아무도 그것을 떠올리지 않은 채로, 그 영혼의 사랑하는 이들조차 그것을 깨닫지 못한 채로. 그들을 가둔 위대한 왕들과 통치자들은 햇빛 속을 의기양양하게 내달리고, 사람들은 그들을 향해 환호한다. 그 위대한 인물들이라는 자들마저 침상에서 죽어 가며 모범적인 임종을 맞고 거창한 연설을 늘어놓으니, 그들의 도구보다도 더 비굴한 예의 바른 역사가 그들을 방부 처리하여 길이 남긴다!\n\n마침내 조반니 바티스타는, 이제 그 담장이 두른 범위 안에서 마음 내킬 때 잠드는 제 재주를 부릴 자기만의 자리를 고를 수 있게 되자, 의자에 누워 얼굴을 포갠 두 팔에 묻고 선잠에 들었다. 그 순순함에서, 그 가벼움에서, 그 좋은 성미에서, 그 오래가지 않는 격정에서, 딱딱한 빵과 딱딱한 돌에 대한 그 손쉬운 만족에서, 그 척척 드는 잠에서, 그 변덕과 발작에서, 그는 모든 면에서 그를 태어나게 한 그 땅의 진정한 아들이었다.\n\n그 드넓은 노려봄도 한동안은 제풀에 노려보다 사그라들었다. 태양은 붉고 푸르고 금빛으로 영광스럽게 저물었다. 별들이 하늘에 돋아났고, 반딧불이들이 더 낮은 대기에서 그것들을 흉내 냈으니, 마치 사람들이 더 나은 차원의 존재들의 선함을 어설피 흉내 내는 것 같았다. 길고 먼지투성이인 도로들과 끝없는 평원들이 휴식에 들었다 — 그리고 바다 위에는 어찌나 깊은 정적이 깃들었던지, 제가 거두었던 죽은 자들을 내어 줄 그날에 관해서는 좀처럼 속삭이지도 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 2 Fellow Travellers\n\n\"오늘은 저 너머에서 어제처럼 울부짖는 소리가 더는 없군요. 그렇지요, 선생?\"\n\n\"나는 아무 소리도 못 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 분명 없는 겁니다. 저 사람들이 울부짖을 때는 들으라고 울부짖거든요.\"\n\n\"대부분의 사람들이 그렇지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 저 사람들은 늘 울부짖습니다. 그러지 않고는 도무지 행복하질 못해요.\"\n\n\"마르세유 사람들 말씀이오?\"\n\n\"프랑스 사람들 말입니다. 노상 그러고 있지요. 마르세유로 말하자면, 우리는 마르세유가 어떤 곳인지 알지 않습니까. 여태껏 작곡된 것 가운데 가장 폭동을 부추기는 곡조를 세상에 내보낸 곳이지요. 무언가를 향해 '알롱'이니 '마르숑'이니 하지 않고는—승리든 죽음이든 지옥불이든 뭐든—도무지 존재할 수가 없는 곳입니다.\"\n\n말하는 이는 내내 익살스러운 호기를 띤 채 난간 너머로 마르세유를 더없이 깔보는 눈길로 내려다보았다. 그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 동전을 잘랑잘랑 흔들며 단호한 자세를 취하더니, 짤막한 웃음과 함께 마르세유를 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"'알롱'이고 '마르숑'이고 좋다 이겁니다. 내 생각엔 말이오, 사람들을 검역소에 가둬 둘 게 아니라 저마다 정당한 볼일을 보러 '알롱'하고 '마르숑'하게 내버려 두는 편이 당신네한테 더 명예로울 것 같소만!\"\n\n\"성가시기 짝이 없지요.\" 상대가 말했다. \"하지만 오늘은 풀려날 겁니다.\"\n\n\"오늘 풀려난다!\" 먼저 말한 이가 되받았다. \"오늘 풀려난다는 게 외려 이 만행을 더 부아 돋게 하는군요. 풀려나다니! 대체 우리가 무엇 때문에 갇혔던 거요?\"\n\n\"그리 대단한 이유가 있던 건 아닙니다. 다만 우리가 동방에서 왔고, 동방은 역병의 땅이니—\"\n\n\"역병이라니!\" 상대가 되받았다. \"그게 바로 내 불만이오. 여기 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 역병에 시달려 왔단 말입니다. 마치 멀쩡한 사람이 정신병원에 갇힌 꼴이지요. 그 의심이란 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없어요. 나는 평생 어느 때보다도 멀쩡한 몸으로 여기 들어왔소. 한데 나를 역병 환자로 의심한다는 건 나한테 역병을 옮기는 것이나 다름없소. 그래서 나는 역병에 걸린 거요—지금도 걸려 있고요.\"\n\n\"잘도 견뎌 내시는군요, 미글스 씨.\" 두 번째로 말한 이가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"천만에. 사정의 진상을 아신다면 그런 말씀은 결코 떠올리지도 못하실 게요. 나는 밤마다 깨어나서는 이러고 있었소. 이제 걸렸구나, 이제 증세가 나타났구나, 이제 꼼짝없이 당하는구나, 이제 저자들이 그 예방 조치의 정당성을 입증해 내겠구나, 하고 말이오. 원, 차라리 꼬챙이에 꿰여서 딱정벌레 표본 속 핀에 꽂히는 편이, 여기서 내가 살아온 꼴로 사느니 낫겠소이다.\"\n\n\"자, 미글스 씨, 이제 다 끝난 일이니 더는 그 얘기 마세요.\" 명랑한 여인의 목소리가 타일렀다.\n\n\"끝났다고!\" 미글스 씨가 되받았다. 그는 (악의는 조금도 없으면서도) 남이 마지막으로 한 말이 곧 새로운 모욕처럼 들리는 그 묘한 심사에 빠진 듯했다. \"끝났다고! 끝났다고 해서 내가 왜 그 얘기를 더는 하면 안 된다는 거요?\"\n\n미글스 씨에게 말을 건넨 이는 미글스 부인이었다. 미글스 부인은 미글스 씨와 마찬가지로 곱고 건강했으며, 쉰다섯 해 남짓 정겨운 살림살이를 바라보아 온, 그래서 그 정겨움이 환히 비쳐 빛나는 유쾌한 영국식 얼굴을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"자! 괜찮아요, 여보, 괜찮다니까요!\" 미글스 부인이 말했다. \"제발 펫을 보며 마음을 푸세요.\"\n\n\"펫을 보며?\" 미글스 씨가 여전히 분이 가시지 않은 어투로 되받았다. 그러나 펫이 바로 그의 등 뒤에 있다가 그의 어깨를 살짝 짚자, 미글스 씨는 곧바로 진심에서 우러나 마르세유를 용서했다.\n\n펫은 스무 살쯤이었다. 풍성한 갈색 머리가 자연스러운 곱슬을 이루며 자유로이 늘어진, 살결 고운 처녀였다. 솔직한 얼굴에 놀라운 눈을 가진 사랑스러운 처녀였다. 그토록 크고, 그토록 부드럽고, 그토록 맑은 그 눈은 다정하고 어여쁜 얼굴에 더없이 완벽하게 자리 잡고 있었다. 그녀는 동그스름하고 싱그럽고 보조개가 패었으며 응석받이로 자랐는데, 펫에게는 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 약점이라 할 만한, 수줍음과 의지하려는 분위기가 감돌았다. 그것은 이토록 예쁘고 사랑스러운 처녀가 자칫 갖추지 못했을, 마지막 화룡점정의 매력을 그녀에게 더해 주었다.\n\n\"자, 한번 여쭤 봅시다.\" 미글스 씨는 더없이 부드러운 신뢰를 담아, 자신은 한 걸음 물러서고 딸을 한 걸음 앞으로 내세워 자기 질문의 본보기로 삼으며 말했다. \"그저 사내 대 사내로서 묻는 거요, 아시겠소만. 펫을 검역소에 가두다니, 이런 빌어먹을 말도 안 되는 소리를 들어 보신 적이 있소이까?\"\n\n\"그 덕분에 검역조차 즐거운 일이 되더군요.\"\n\n\"이거 보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그건 그렇소이다. 그 말씀은 참 고맙구려. 자, 펫, 우리 애야, 어머니랑 같이 가서 배 탈 채비를 하는 게 좋겠다. 보건 담당관에다 삼각모를 쓴 갖은 사기꾼들이 드디어 우리를 여기서 풀어 주러 오고 있단다. 우리 같은 죄수들이 저마다 다른 목적지로 날아가기 전에, 다시 한번 그래도 사람 사는 꼴에 가깝게 다 같이 아침을 먹어야지. 태티코럼, 너는 네 젊은 아씨 곁에 바짝 붙어 있거라.\"\n\n그는 윤기 흐르는 검은 머리와 검은 눈을 지닌, 아주 단정히 차려입은 어여쁜 처녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 미글스 부인과 펫의 뒤를 따라 지나가며 반쯤 무릎을 굽혀 인사로 답했다. 세 사람은 한데 어울려 헐벗고 그을린 테라스를 가로지른 뒤, 눈부시게 새하얀 아치문을 지나 사라졌다. 마흔쯤 된 진중하고 가무잡잡한 사내인 미글스 씨의 동행은, 그들이 사라진 뒤에도 여전히 그 아치문 쪽을 바라보며 서 있다가, 미글스 씨가 그의 팔을 톡톡 치고서야 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"실례했습니다.\" 그가 흠칫하며 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n두 사람은 담장 그늘 아래를 말없이 한 번 오갔다. 검역소 막사가 자리 잡은 그 높은 곳에서, 아침 일곱 시에 불어오는 그나마의 시원한 바닷바람으로 더위를 식히면서. 미글스 씨의 동행이 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까.\" 그가 말했다. \"저—그 아가씨 이름이 무엇인지요—\"\n\n\"태티코럼 말이오?\" 미글스 씨가 끼어들었다. \"나도 도무지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 상대가 말했다. \"그게—\"\n\n\"태티코럼?\" 미글스 씨가 다시 거들었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다—태티코럼이 이름인 줄 알았습니다. 한데 그 묘함에 여러 번 의아했지요.\"\n\n\"실은 말이오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"미글스 부인과 나는, 보시다시피, 실용적인 사람들이오.\"\n\n\"이 돌바닥 위를 오르내리며 나눈 그 유쾌하고도 흥미로운 대화 중에 여러 번 말씀하셨지요.\" 상대가 가무잡잡한 얼굴의 진중함을 뚫고 옅은 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"실용적인 사람들이지요. 그래서 어느 날, 이젠 대여섯 해 전 일이오만, 우리가 펫을 데리고 파운들링의 예배당에 갔을 때—런던의 파운들링 병원을 들어 보셨소? 파리의 기아 보호소와 비슷한 곳 말이오.\"\n\n\"본 적이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그래! 어느 날 우리가 펫을 데리고 그 예배당에 음악을 들려주러 갔을 때—실용적인 사람으로서, 그 애를 기쁘게 해 줄 만하다 싶은 건 무엇이든 보여 주는 게 우리 인생의 본분이니 말이오—어머니(나는 미글스 부인을 늘 이렇게 부른다오)가 어찌나 울던지, 데리고 나와야 할 지경이었소. '여보, 왜 그러오?' 어머니가 좀 진정되었을 때 내가 말했지. '여보, 당신이 펫을 놀라게 하잖소.' '네, 알아요, 여보,' 어머니가 말합디다. '하지만 그 애를 너무나 사랑하기 때문에 그런 생각이 떠오른 것 같아요.' '도대체 무슨 생각이 떠올랐다는 거요, 여보?' '아이고, 이를 어째!' 어머니가 다시 울음을 터뜨리며 외칩디다. '저 아이들이 층층이 줄지어 앉아서, 이 땅에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 아버지 대신 하늘에 계신 우리 모두의 위대한 아버지께 호소하는 걸 보니, 이런 생각이 들지 뭐예요. 어느 가엾은 어미가 여기 와서 저 어린 얼굴들을 둘러보며, 이 쓸쓸한 세상에 내가 낳아 놓은 가여운 아이가 어느 애일까, 평생토록 어미의 사랑도, 입맞춤도, 얼굴도, 목소리도, 심지어 이름조차 알지 못할 그 아이가 누굴까, 하고 헤아려 본 적은 없을까!' 자, 그게 바로 어머니다운 실용이었소. 그래서 내가 그렇게 말해 줬지. '여보, 그게 바로 내가 말하는 당신다운 실용이오.'\"\n\n상대도 마음이 동한 듯 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그래서 이튿날 내가 말했소. '여보, 당신이 찬성할 만한 제안이 하나 있소. 저 어린아이들 가운데 하나를 데려다 펫의 어린 하녀로 삼읍시다. 우리는 실용적인 사람이오. 그러니 그 아이의 성미가 좀 모자라거나, 행실이 우리와 좀 어긋난다 해도, 우리는 무엇을 참작해야 할지 알게 될 거요. 우리를 이렇게 길러 온 온갖 영향과 경험 가운데서 얼마나 엄청난 것을 빼고 봐야 하는지 알 테니 말이오—부모도 없고, 형제자매도 없고, 가정이라는 고유한 둥지도 없고, 유리 구두도, 요정 대모도 없는 아이니까.' 그렇게 해서 우리가 태티코럼을 데려오게 된 것이오.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 이름은—\"\n\n\"이런!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"이름 얘기를 깜빡할 뻔했군. 그게 말이오, 그 애는 보호소에서 해리엇 비들이라 불렸소—물론 임의로 붙인 이름이지요. 그래서 우리는 해리엇을 해티로 바꾸고, 다시 태티로 바꿨소. 실용적인 사람으로서, 장난스러운 이름조차 그 애한테는 새로운 경험이 될 테고, 마음을 부드럽고 다정하게 어루만지는 효험이 있을지 모른다고 생각했거든요, 무슨 말인지 아시겠소? 비들로 말하자면, 두말할 것도 없이 아예 논외였소. 무슨 일이 있어도 도무지 참아 줄 수 없는 것, 하급 관리의 거들먹대는 무례와 어처구니없음의 전형이라 할 만한 것, 누구나 그 정체를 다 알아챈 뒤에도 외투에 조끼에 굵직한 지팡이까지 갖추고 허튼소리를 부여잡고 있는 우리 영국식 작태를 대변하는 것이 있다면, 그게 바로 비들이거든요. 요즘 비들을 본 적 있소?\"\n\n\"중국에서 이십 년 넘게 지낸 영국인으로서, 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 미글스 씨가 자못 활기차게 동행의 가슴에 검지를 갖다 대며 말했다. \"되도록이면 앞으로도 비들은 보지 마시오. 비들이 정장을 빼입고 일요일에 자선 학교 아이들을 앞세워 거리를 행진해 오는 걸 보면, 나는 돌아서서 달아나야 한다오. 안 그러면 한 대 칠 테니까. 어쨌든 비들이라는 이름은 논외였고, 이 가엾은 기아들을 위한 보호소를 처음 세운 이가 코럼이라는 복된 분이었던 터라, 우리는 펫의 어린 하녀에게 그 이름을 붙여 준 거요. 한동안은 태티, 한동안은 코럼이었다가, 어느덧 두 이름을 섞어 부르게 됐고, 이제는 늘 태티코럼이라오.\"\n\n\"미글스 씨, 당신 따님은,\" 두 사람이 또 한 번 말없이 오가고, 잠시 담장에 서서 바다를 내려다보다가 다시 걸음을 옮겼을 때 상대가 말했다. \"외동인 줄로 압니다만. 여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까—주제넘은 호기심에서가 아니라, 당신과 함께 지내며 무척 즐거웠고, 이 미궁 같은 세상에서 다시는 당신과 조용히 말 한마디 나누지 못할지도 모르며, 당신과 당신 가족을 정확히 기억해 두고 싶어서 그러는 것입니다만—따님 말고 다른 자녀가 있으셨다는 걸 부인께서 하신 말씀에서 짐작한 게 잘못이 아니라면, 여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니오. 아니오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"정확히 다른 자녀들이라곤 할 수 없소. 자녀 하나가 더 있었지요.\"\n\n\"제가 그만 무심코 아픈 화제를 건드린 게 아닌가 합니다.\"\n\n\"괜찮소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 이 얘기에 진중해지긴 해도, 슬프지는 않다오. 잠시 마음이 차분해질 뿐, 나를 불행하게 만들진 않소. 펫에게는 쌍둥이 누이가 있었는데, 그 애가 식탁 가장자리를 붙잡고 발끝으로 서서 그 눈—펫의 눈과 똑같던 그 눈—을 겨우 식탁 위로 내밀 수 있을 무렵에 죽었다오.\"\n\n\"아! 저런, 저런!\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그리고 실용적인 사람인지라, 미글스 부인과 내 마음속에는 차츰 한 가지 생각이 자라났는데, 당신이라면 이해할지도—혹은 못할지도—모르겠소. 펫과 그 갓난 누이가 어찌나 똑같고 어찌나 완전히 하나였던지, 그 뒤로 우리는 마음속에서 둘을 결코 떼어 놓을 수가 없었소. 죽은 우리 아이가 그저 갓난아기였을 뿐이라고 말해 봐야 우리한테는 소용없는 일이오. 우리는 곁에 남아 늘 함께하는 아이가 자라는 데 맞춰 그 죽은 아이도 자라게 해 왔으니까. 펫이 자라면 그 아이도 자랐고, 펫이 더 철들고 여인다워지면, 그 누이도 꼭 그만큼 더 철들고 여인다워졌소. 내가 내일 저세상으로 건너간다 해도 하느님의 자비로써 펫과 꼭 닮은 딸의 마중을 받지 못하리라 나를 설득하기란, 펫 자신이 내 곁에 있는 실체가 아니라고 나를 설득하기만큼이나 어려운 일일 거요.\"\n\n\"이해합니다.\" 상대가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"그 애로 말하자면,\" 아버지가 말을 이었다. \"제 작은 분신이자 놀이 동무를 갑작스레 잃은 일, 그리고 우리 모두가 똑같이 짊어지고 있으나 좀처럼 아이에게 그토록 강렬하게 들이닥치지는 않는 그 신비를 어린 나이에 일찍 겪은 일이, 그 애의 성격에 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤을 수밖에 없소. 게다가 그 애 어미와 나는 결혼할 때 젊지 않았고, 우리가 그 애한테 맞추려 애쓰긴 했어도 펫은 늘 우리와 더불어 어른스러운 생활을 해 왔지요. 그 애가 좀 시름시름할 때면, 되도록 자주 기후와 공기를 바꿔 주고—특히 그 애 나이 이맘때쯤엔 말이오—즐겁게 해 주라는 권고를 우리는 여러 번 받았소. 그래서, 이제 나는 은행 책상에 매여 있을 필요가 없으니(장담컨대 한때는 무척 가난하기도 했소. 안 그랬으면 미글스 부인과 진작 결혼했을 거요), 우리는 이렇게 세상을 떠돌아다니는 거라오. 그래서 당신은 우리가 나일강이며, 피라미드며, 스핑크스며, 사막이며, 그 밖의 온갖 것을 멍하니 바라보고 있는 꼴로 만난 거고, 그래서 태티코럼은 머지않아 쿡 선장보다 더 대단한 여행가가 될 거요.\"\n\n\"당신의 그 허심탄회한 말씀에,\" 상대가 말했다. \"진심으로 감사드립니다.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 미글스 씨가 답했다. \"정말 별말씀을. 자, 이제, 클레넘 씨, 혹시 다음에 어디로 가실지 결정을 내리셨는지 여쭤 봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"실은, 아닙니다. 저는 어디서나 떠도는 부평초 같은 신세라, 어느 물결이 흐르든 그쪽으로 떠밀려 가기 십상이지요.\"\n\n\"실례를 무릅쓰고 말씀드리자면, 당신이 곧장 런던으로 가지 않는다는 게 나로선 참 이상하구려.\" 미글스 씨가 흉허물 없는 조언자의 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아마 가게 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 한데 굳은 의지를 가지고 말이오.\"\n\n\"제겐 의지가 없습니다. 다시 말해,\" 그는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"지금 당장 행동에 옮길 만한 의지란 거의 없다는 뜻이지요. 억센 힘으로 길들여졌고, 휘어진 게 아니라 꺾였으며, 제가 한 번도 상의받은 적 없고 한 번도 제 것인 적 없던 목적을 위해 무겁게 족쇄가 채워졌고, 성년이 되기도 전에 세상 저편으로 실려 가 일 년 전 아버지가 그곳에서 돌아가실 때까지 거기 유배되어 있었으며, 늘 제가 늘 증오하던 방앗간에서 갈려 온 사람이올시다. 그런 제게 중년에 이르러 무엇을 기대하겠습니까? 의지, 목적, 희망? 그런 빛들은 제가 그 말들을 입에 올리기도 전에 죄다 꺼져 버렸지요.\"\n\n\"다시 불을 밝히시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 말이야 쉽지요. 미글스 씨, 저는 모진 아버지와 어머니의 아들입니다. 저는 모든 것을 달고, 재고, 값을 매기던 부모의 외아들이지요. 그들에게는 달 수도, 잴 수도, 값을 매길 수도 없는 것이라면 아예 존재하지 않는 것이었습니다. 흔히들 말하는 엄격한 사람들, 준엄한 종교의 신봉자들이었으나, 그 종교란 것조차 애당초 제 것인 적 없던 취향과 정서를 음울하게 제물로 바치는 것이었고, 그것은 자기네 재산의 안위를 보장받기 위한 거래의 일부로 바쳐졌지요. 근엄한 얼굴들, 가차 없는 규율, 이승에서의 고행과 저승에서의 공포—어디에도 우아하거나 다정한 것이라곤 없고, 주눅 든 제 마음속 공허만이 사방에 가득했지요—이것이 제 어린 시절이었습니다. 그런 인생의 출발을 두고 그 말을 쓰는 게 잘못된 오용이 아니라면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"정말 그랬소?\" 미글스 씨는 상상 속에 그려진 그 광경에 몹시 불편해하며 말했다. \"참으로 모진 출발이었구려. 하지만 자! 이제는 실용적인 사람답게, 그 너머에 놓인 모든 걸 배우고 활용해야 하오.\"\n\n\"흔히 실용적이라 불리는 이들이 당신 같은 방향으로 실용적이라면—\"\n\n\"아니, 다들 그렇소!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정말 그렇습니까?\"\n\n\"음, 그런 것 같소만.\" 미글스 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 답했다. \"어? 사람이 실용적일 수밖에 없는 거고, 미글스 부인과 나는 그저 그뿐인 사람들이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제 알 수 없는 앞길이 제가 예상했던 것보다 한결 수월하고 도움이 될 것 같군요.\" 클레넘이 진중한 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"제 얘긴 이쯤 합시다. 저기 배가 오는군요.\"\n\n배에는 미글스 씨가 민족적 거부감을 품고 있는 삼각모들이 가득 타고 있었다. 그 삼각모를 쓴 자들이 내려 계단을 올라왔고, 갇혀 있던 여행객들이 모두 한데 모였다. 그러자 삼각모들 쪽에서 서류 무더기가 대거 등장하고, 이름을 일일이 호명하고, 서명하고 봉인하고 도장 찍고 잉크를 묻히고 모래를 뿌리는 대공사가 벌어졌는데, 그 결과물은 한결같이 번지고 까칠까칠하고 도무지 알아볼 수 없는 꼴이었다. 마침내 모든 절차가 규정대로 끝났고, 여행객들은 어디로든 마음대로 떠날 수 있게 되었다.\n\n자유를 되찾은 새로운 기쁨에 들떠 그들은 눈부신 햇살이며 따가운 더위 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고, 흥겨운 보트를 타고 항구를 가로질러 어느 큰 호텔에 다시 모였다. 그곳에서는 닫힌 격자창이 햇빛을 막아 주었고, 헐벗은 포석 바닥과 높은 천장과 메아리치는 복도가 그 극심한 더위를 누그러뜨렸다. 거기 너른 방의 큰 식탁에는 이내 화려한 진수성찬이 푸짐하게 차려졌고, 검역소 숙소는 정말이지 휑한 곳으로, 진귀한 요리며 남방의 과일이며 차게 식힌 포도주며 제노바에서 온 꽃이며 산봉우리에서 가져온 눈이며 거울마다 무지개의 온갖 빛깔이 번쩍이는 그 한가운데서 떠올리는 기억이 되어 버렸다.\n\n\"하지만 이제는 그 단조롭던 벽들에 아무 원한도 없소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"어떤 곳이든 등지고 나서면 곧 용서하게 되는 법이지요. 죄수도 풀려나고 나면 제 감옥을 향한 마음이 풀어지기 시작할 거요.\"\n\n일행은 서른 명쯤이었고 모두 이야기를 나누고 있었으나, 으레 그렇듯 무리를 지어서였다. 미글스 아버지와 어머니는 딸을 가운데 앉히고서, 이 세 사람이 식탁 한쪽에 자리 잡았다. 맞은편에는 클레넘 씨가 앉았고, 칠흑 같은 머리와 수염을 지닌, 가무잡잡하고 무시무시한—점잖게 말하자면 악마 같은—풍모이면서도 더없이 온순한 사람임을 보여 온 키 큰 프랑스 신사가 앉았으며, 또 혼자서 여행하는 어여쁜 젊은 영국 여인이 있었다. 그녀는 도도하고도 살피는 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었는데, 스스로 나머지 사람들에게서 물러난 것인지 나머지 사람들이 그녀를 피한 것인지는—어쩌면 본인을 빼고는—아무도 딱히 가려낼 수 없었다. 나머지 일행은 흔한 부류였다. 볼일로 떠나온 여행객과 즐기러 떠나온 여행객, 휴가 나온 인도 주둔 장교들, 그리스와 터키 무역에 종사하는 상인들, 어린 아내와 신혼여행 중인 온순한 성직자풍 영국인 남편, 다 자라 가는 세 딸을 거느린, 귀족 부류의 위풍당당한 영국인 마마와 파파—그 딸들은 동료 인간들을 곤혹스럽게 만들 일지를 쓰고 있었다—그리고 여행에 이골이 난 귀먹은 늙은 영국인 어머니와, 정말이지 다 자라도 한참 자란 그 딸이 있었는데, 이 딸은 결국 자신을 갈고닦아 결혼이라는 상태로 마무리할 요량으로 온 우주를 누비며 스케치를 하고 다녔다.\n\n내성적인 그 영국 여인이 미글스 씨의 마지막 말을 받았다.\n\n\"죄수가 제 감옥을 용서한다는 말씀인가요?\" 그녀가 천천히, 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"그게 제 생각이었소, 웨이드 양. 죄수가 어떤 심정인지 확실히 안다고 자처하는 건 아니오. 나는 전에 죄수였던 적이 없으니까.\"\n\n\"마드무아젤은 의심하시는군요.\" 프랑스 신사가 제 나라 말로 말했다. \"용서가 그토록 쉽다는 것을 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래요.\"\n\n펫은 이 대목을 미글스 씨에게 통역해 주어야 했다. 미글스 씨는 자기가 여행하는 그 어느 나라의 말도 어쩌다가라도 익힌 적이 없었기 때문이다. \"오!\" 그가 말했다. \"이런! 그것 참 안타까운 일 아니오?\"\n\n\"제가 잘 믿지 못한다는 게 말입니까?\" 웨이드 양이 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 그런 건 아니오. 달리 말합시다. 당신이 용서가 쉽다는 걸 믿지 못한다는 게 말이오.\"\n\n\"제 경험이,\" 그녀가 조용히 받았다. \"여러 면에서 제 믿음을 몇 해째 바로잡아 왔지요. 그게 우리의 자연스러운 흐름이라고 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"원, 원! 하지만 원한을 품는 게 자연스러운 건 아니길 바라오만?\" 미글스 씨가 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어딘가에 갇혀 시들고 고통받았다면, 저는 언제까지나 그곳을 미워하며 불태워 버리거나 땅바닥까지 허물어 버리고 싶을 거예요. 그 이상은 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"강경하지요, 선생?\" 미글스 씨가 프랑스 신사에게 말했다. 어느 나라 사람에게든 영국식 관용어로 말을 걸면서, 상대가 어떻게든 알아들을 수밖에 없다고 굳게 믿는 것이 그의 또 다른 버릇이었다. \"우리 고운 벗께서 좀 과격하다 싶지 않소, 동의하시리라 보오만?\"\n\n프랑스 신사가 정중하게 답했다. \"플레티르(뭐라고요)?\" 이에 미글스 씨가 자못 흡족하게 받았다. \"옳소. 내 생각도 그렇소.\"\n\n이윽고 아침 식사가 시들해지기 시작하자, 미글스 씨가 일동에게 연설을 했다. 연설이라 할 만한 것치고는 충분히 짧고 충분히 분별 있었으며, 진심이 담겨 있었다. 그 요지는 그저 이러했다. 우리 모두가 우연히 한자리에 모였고, 함께 잘 지내 왔으며, 이제 흩어지려 하고, 다시는 다 같이 모일 일이 없을 듯하니, 서로 작별을 고하고, 식탁을 빙 둘러 다 함께 차가운 샴페인 한 잔을 들며 서로의 앞길에 행운을 빌어 주는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 어디 있겠느냐는 것이었다. 그대로 행해졌고, 다 함께 악수를 나누며 그 모임은 영영 흩어졌다.\n\n이 내내 혼자였던 젊은 여인은 더는 말이 없었다. 그녀는 다른 사람들과 함께 일어나, 말없이 그 큰 방의 외진 구석으로 물러나, 창가의 긴 의자에 앉아, 물에 비친 빛이 격자창 살에 은빛으로 일렁이는 모습을 바라보는 듯했다. 그녀는 방 전체에 등을 돌리고 앉아, 마치 제 오만한 선택으로 외로움을 자처하는 듯했다. 그러면서도 그녀가 나머지 사람들을 피하는 것인지 사람들에게 피해지는 것인지는, 여전히 딱 잘라 말하기 어려웠다.\n\n그녀가 앉은 그늘이 음울한 너울처럼 이마를 가로질러 드리워, 그 미모의 성정과 무척 잘 어울렸다. 둥글게 휜 짙은 눈썹과 검은 머리채의 굴곡이 돋보이게 하는, 그토록 고요하고 경멸 어린 그 얼굴을 보고 있노라면, 거기에 변화가 깃든다면 그 표정이 어떨까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다. 그것이 누그러지거나 풀어지는 것은 거의 불가능해 보였다. 그것이 분노로, 혹은 극단의 반항으로 깊어질 수 있으며, 만약 변한다면 반드시 그 방향으로 변하리라는 것이, 대부분의 관찰자에게 남는 그 얼굴 특유의 인상이었을 것이다. 그것은 어떤 격식 차린 표정으로도 매만져지거나 다듬어져 있지 않았다. 비록 열린 얼굴은 아니었으나, 거기에 가식이라곤 없었다. \"나는 자족하고 자립한다. 너의 의견 따위 내겐 아무것도 아니다. 나는 너한테 관심도 없고, 마음 쓰지도 않으며, 너를 보고 듣는 데 무심하다\"—이렇게 그 얼굴은 또렷이 말하고 있었다. 그것은 도도한 눈에서, 치켜든 콧방울에서, 어여쁘되 굳게 다물려 매정하기까지 한 입에서 그렇게 말하고 있었다. 그 표정의 통로 가운데 둘을 가린다 해도, 나머지 하나가 여전히 그렇게 말했을 것이다. 그것을 모조리 가린다 해도, 그저 고개를 돌리는 몸짓만으로 굴복할 줄 모르는 천성이 드러났을 것이다.\n\n펫이 그녀에게 다가가 있었고(이제 그 방에 남은 다른 사람은 가족과 클레넘 씨뿐이었는데, 그들 사이에서 그녀가 화제에 올랐었다), 그녀 곁에 서 있었다.\n\n\"여기서 누구를 만나기로—\" 그녀가 눈을 돌리자 펫은 말을 더듬었다. \"—기다리고 계신가요, 웨이드 양?\"\n\"제가요? 아니오.\"\n\n\"아버지가 유치 우편소에 사람을 보내려는데요. 혹시 당신 앞으로 온 편지가 있는지도 물어보라고 그 심부름꾼에게 일러 주는 기쁨을 누리게 해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"감사합니다만, 올 편지가 없다는 걸 알아요.\"\n\n\"저희는,\" 펫이 그녀 곁에 앉으며, 수줍은 듯 반쯤 다정하게 말했다. \"저희가 다 떠나고 나면 당신이 무척 외로움을 느끼실까 봐 걱정이 돼서요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\"\n\n\"물론,\" 펫이 그녀의 눈빛에 당황하여 변명하듯 말했다. \"물론 저희가 당신께 무슨 벗이 되어 드린다거나, 그래 올 수 있었다거나, 당신이 그러길 바라셨으리라 여겨서가 아니라—\"\n\n\"제가 그러길 바란다는 뜻을 내비친 적은 없습니다만.\"\n\n\"네. 물론이지요. 하지만—요컨대,\" 펫이 두 사람 사이 소파 위에 무심히 놓인 그녀의 손을 머뭇머뭇 만지며 말했다. \"아버지께서 자그마한 도움이나 수고라도 베풀어 드리게 허락해 주시지 않겠어요? 무척 기뻐하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"기쁘다마다요.\" 미글스 씨가 아내와 클레넘과 함께 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"말을 통역하는 것만 아니라면, 무엇이든 기꺼이 맡겠소이다, 정말이오.\"\n\n\"감사합니다.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"하지만 제 일은 다 정해 두었고, 저는 제 방식대로 제 길을 가는 편이 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"그러시다?\" 미글스 씨가 영문 모를 표정으로 그녀를 살피며 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸다. \"원! 그것도 한 성격이로군.\"\n\n\"제가 젊은 숙녀분들과 어울리는 데는 그리 익숙지 못해서, 다른 사람들처럼 그 호의를 제대로 표할 줄 모를까 염려됩니다. 즐거운 여행 되시길 빕니다. 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n그녀는 손을 내밀 생각이 없는 듯했으나, 미글스 씨가 그녀 앞에 어찌나 곧장 손을 내밀던지 그것을 지나칠 수가 없었다. 그녀가 제 손을 그 안에 얹자, 그 손은 조금 전 긴 의자 위에 놓여 있던 그대로 거기 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"안녕히!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"이게 작별 명단의 마지막 작별이오. 어머니와 나는 방금 여기 클레넘 씨에게도 작별을 고했고, 이제 그가 펫에게 작별을 고할 일만 남았소. 안녕히! 우리 다시는 못 만날지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"우리는 인생의 행로에서, 숱한 낯선 곳으로부터 숱한 낯선 길을 거쳐 우리를 만나러 오는 사람들을 만나게 마련이지요.\" 차분한 대답이었다. \"그리고 우리가 그들에게 해야 할 일과, 그들이 우리에게 해야 할 일은, 모두 다 이루어지고 말 거예요.\"\n\n이 말의 투에는 펫의 귀에 거슬리는 무언가가 있었다. 그것은 행해질 일이 으레 악한 것이라는 뜻을 담고 있었고, 그래서 그녀는 \"어머, 아버지!\" 하고 속삭이며, 응석받이답게 어린아이처럼 아버지에게 조금 더 바짝 움츠려 다가들게 되었다. 이것을 말한 이가 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"당신의 어여쁜 따님은,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런 일들을 생각만 해도 움찔하는군요. 하지만,\" 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며. \"이미 길 위에 올라 당신과 볼일을 치를, 그리고 끝내 그 일을 해내고야 말 남자들과 여자들이 있다는 걸 믿으셔도 좋아요. 그들은 틀림없이 해낼 거예요. 그들은 저 바다 너머로 수백, 수천 마일을 건너오고 있는지도 모르고, 지금 바로 코앞에 와 있는지도 모르지요. 당신이 무엇을 알든, 그것을 막으려 무슨 짓을 하든 상관없이, 그들은 바로 이 도시의 가장 추악한 쓰레기 더미 속에서 오고 있는지도 모르고요.\"\n\n더없이 싸늘한 작별 인사를 남기고, 한창때라 하기에도 아직 이른 그 미모에 어딘가 지치고 야윈 듯한 빛을 드리운 채, 그녀는 방을 나섰다.\n\n그런데 그녀가 그 널찍한 집의 그 구역에서 자기가 차지한 방까지 가려면 여러 계단과 복도를 지나야 했다. 그 여정을 거의 다 마치고 자기 방이 있는 회랑을 지나갈 무렵, 그녀는 누군가 성난 듯 중얼거리며 흐느끼는 소리를 들었다. 문 하나가 열려 있었고, 그 안에서 그녀는 방금 떠나온 그 처녀의 시중을 드는 하녀를—그 묘한 이름의 하녀를 보았다.\n\n그녀는 가만히 서서 이 하녀를 바라보았다. 시무룩하고 격정에 찬 처녀였다! 풍성한 검은 머리가 온통 얼굴에 흩어져 있었고, 얼굴은 붉게 달아올라 화끈거렸으며, 흐느끼며 길길이 날뛰는 와중에 그녀는 가차 없는 손길로 제 입술을 쥐어뜯고 있었다.\n\n\"이기적인 짐승들!\" 처녀가 흐느끼며 사이사이 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"내가 어찌 되든 아랑곳 않다니! 나를 여기 굶기고 목마르게 하고 지친 채로 내버려 두고, 굶어 죽든 말든 신경도 안 쓰다니! 짐승들! 악마들! 비열한 것들!\"\n\n\"가엾기도 해라, 무슨 일이니?\"\n\n처녀가 별안간 고개를 들었다. 시뻘게진 눈으로, 새로 시뻘건 자국이 잔뜩 난 제 목을 꼬집으려던 손을 허공에 멈춘 채였다. \"무슨 일이든 댁이랑은 상관없어요. 누구한테도 아무 상관 없는 일이라고요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니야, 상관있단다. 네가 그러는 걸 보니 마음이 아파.\"\n\n\"마음이 아프긴요.\" 처녀가 말했다. \"고소한 거겠지. 고소해하는 거 다 알아요. 저 너머 검역소에서도 내가 딱 두 번 이랬는데, 두 번 다 댁이 날 봤죠. 난 댁이 무서워요.\"\n\n\"내가 무섭다고?\"\n\n\"네. 댁은 꼭 내 분노 같고, 내 악의 같고, 내—그게 뭐든 간에—나도 그게 뭔지 모르겠지만, 그런 것처럼 나타나니까요. 하지만 난 학대받고 있어요, 학대받고 있다고요, 학대받고 있단 말이에요!\" 이쯤에서 처음 놀라움에 멎었던 흐느낌과 눈물과 쥐어뜯던 손길이 다시 한꺼번에 되살아났다.\n\n찾아온 여인은 기이하게도 골똘한 미소를 띤 채 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다. 처녀 안에서 벌어지는 싸움의 격렬함과, 마치 옛날의 악령들에게 갈가리 찢기기라도 하는 듯 그녀가 벌이는 육체의 몸부림을 지켜보는 것은 놀라운 일이었다.\n\n\"내가 그 애보다 두세 살은 어린데도, 마치 내가 늙은이라도 되는 양 그 애를 돌보는 건 나고, 늘 떠받들리며 '아기'라 불리는 건 그 애예요! 난 그 이름이 진절머리 나요. 난 그 애가 미워요! 사람들은 그 애를 바보로 만들고, 응석받이로 망쳐 놓죠. 그 앤 제 생각밖에 안 해요. 내가 무슨 나무토막이나 돌멩이라도 되는 양, 나 따위는 안중에도 없다고요!\" 처녀는 계속 그렇게 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"참아야 한단다.\"\n\n\"안 참을 거예요!\"\n\n\"저들이 제 몸만 끔찍이 위하고 너는 거의, 아니 전혀 위하지 않는다 해도, 너는 마음에 두어선 안 돼.\"\n\n\"마음에 둘 거예요.\"\n\n\"쉿! 좀 더 신중해야지. 네 처지가 남에게 매인 신세라는 걸 잊었구나.\"\n\n\"그딴 거 상관없어요. 도망쳐 버릴 거예요. 무슨 사달을 내고 말 거예요. 못 견뎌요. 견딜 수가 없어요. 견디려 들다간 죽고 말 거라고요!\"\n\n지켜보던 여인은 제 가슴에 손을 얹은 채 처녀를 바라보고 서 있었다. 마치 병든 환부를 지닌 사람이 그와 비슷한 증례를 해부하고 펼쳐 보이는 광경을 호기심 어린 눈으로 지켜보기라도 하듯이.\n\n처녀는 제 젊음과 차오르는 생명력을 다 쏟아 길길이 날뛰며 싸웠고, 그러다 차츰차츰 그 격정에 찬 외침이 마치 아픔에 겨운 듯 끊긴 신음으로 잦아들었다. 그에 맞춰 그녀는 차츰 의자에 주저앉았다가, 무릎을 꿇었다가, 침대 옆 바닥에 쓰러져, 이불을 끌어당겨 부끄러운 머리와 젖은 머리채를 반쯤 그 속에 파묻었다. 그리고 반쯤은, 회개하는 가슴에 끌어안을 무언가가 아무것도 없느니 차라리 그것이라도 끌어안으려는 듯 보였다.\n\n\"저리 가요, 저리 가 줘요! 성미가 발동하면 난 미쳐 버려요. 마음만 단단히 먹으면 그걸 떨쳐 낼 수 있다는 걸 알아요. 어떤 때는 정말 단단히 마음먹기도 하고, 또 어떤 때는 그러지 않고 그러기도 싫어요. 내가 무슨 말을 한 거죠! 그 말을 할 때 이미 다 거짓말인 줄 알았어요. 저들은 내가 어딘가에서 보살핌받으며 원하는 걸 다 누리는 줄 알아요. 저들은 내게 그저 잘해 주기만 할 뿐이에요. 난 저들을 진심으로 사랑해요. 나처럼 은혜도 모르는 것한테, 저들이 늘 그러듯 그토록 다정하게 대해 줄 사람은 세상에 없을 거예요. 제발, 제발 가 줘요. 난 댁이 무서우니까요. 내 성미가 발동하는 게 느껴지면 난 내 자신이 무서워요. 그리고 댁도 그만큼 무서워요. 저리 가 줘요. 나 혼자 기도하고 울면서 마음을 다스리게요!\"\n\n날이 저물어 갔다. 그리고 그 드넓은 햇살이 다시 한번 제풀에 사위었으며, 무더운 밤이 마르세유에 내렸다. 그 밤을 가르며, 아침의 그 대상(隊商)은 죄다 흩어져 저마다 정해진 길을 갔다. 그리하여 낮에도 밤에도, 햇빛 아래서도 별빛 아래서도, 먼지 이는 언덕을 오르고 지친 평원을 터벅터벅 가로지르며, 뭍으로 가고 바다로 가며, 그토록 기이하게 오가며, 서로 만나고 서로에게 작용하고 반작용하며, 우리 쉴 줄 모르는 모든 여행자는 인생이라는 순례의 길을 나아가는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. Home\n\n런던의 어느 일요일 저녁이었다. 음울하고, 후텁지근하고, 퀴퀴했다. 온갖 정도의 불협화음을 내는, 날카롭고 둔탁한, 갈라지고 맑은, 빠르고 느린 미칠 듯한 교회 종소리들이 벽돌과 회반죽의 메아리를 흉측하게 울렸다. 그을음으로 참회복을 두른 듯한 우울한 거리들은, 창밖으로 그것들을 바라보도록 단죄된 사람들의 영혼을 끔찍한 의기소침 속에 담가 두었다. 모든 큰길에서, 거의 모든 골목 위에서, 거의 모든 모퉁이 아래에서, 어떤 구슬픈 종소리가 고동치고, 덜컥거리고, 울려 댔다. 마치 도시에 역병이 돌고 시신을 실은 수레들이 돌아다니기라도 하는 것처럼. 지칠 대로 지친 사람들에게 조금이라도 위안을 줄 수 있을 법한 것은 무엇이든 빗장이 채워지고 막혀 있었다. 그림도, 낯선 동물도, 진귀한 식물이나 꽃도, 고대 세계의 자연적이거나 인공적인 경이도 없었다 — 모두가 그 계몽된 엄격함으로 금기시되어 있어서, 대영 박물관에 있는 흉측한 남태평양의 신상들마저 다시 제 고향에 온 줄 알았을 정도였다. 보이는 것이라곤 거리, 거리, 거리뿐. 들이마실 것이라곤 거리, 거리, 거리뿐. 골똘히 잠긴 마음을 바꾸거나 일으켜 세울 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 기진맥진한 노동자가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 자신의 이렛날의 단조로움을 엿새의 단조로움과 견주어 보고, 자기가 얼마나 고단한 삶을 살고 있는지 생각하고, 그것을 최선으로 — 혹은 형편에 따라 최악으로 — 받아들이는 것뿐이었다.\n\n종교와 도덕의 이익에 그토록 길조인, 그런 행복한 시각에, 도버를 거쳐, 도버발 마차 푸른 눈의 처녀호를 타고 마르세유에서 막 도착한 아서 클레넘 씨는 러드게이트 힐의 어느 커피점 창가에 앉아 있었다. 일만 채의 점잖은 집들이 그를 에워싸고서, 자기들이 이루는 거리들을 향해 무겁게 찌푸리고 있었다. 마치 한 집 한 집마다, 매일 밤 얼굴을 검게 칠하고 자신들의 비참을 탄식했다는 캘린더 이야기 속의 그 열 명의 젊은이가 살고 있기라도 한 것처럼. 오만 개의 소굴이 그를 에워쌌는데, 그곳 사람들은 어찌나 건강에 해롭게 사는지, 토요일 밤에 그들의 비좁은 방 안에 들여놓은 맑은 물도 일요일 아침이면 썩어 있을 정도였다. 그럼에도 그들의 주(州) 하원의원인 나리께서는, 그들이 푸줏간 고기와 한방에서 자지 않는 것을 의아해하셨다. 사방의 나침반 방위 어디로든, 주민들이 공기를 찾아 헐떡이는 비좁은 우물 같고 구덩이 같은 집들이 몇 마일이고 뻗어 있었다. 맑고 깨끗한 강이 흘러야 할 자리에, 도시의 한복판으로는 죽음의 하수가 밀려왔다 밀려갔다 했다. 일주일에 엿새를, 요람과 무덤 사이에서 결코 벗어날 수 없는 그 달콤한 단조로움으로 이루어진 이 아르카디아적 정경 한가운데서 날마다 노동에 매여 사는 백만쯤 되는 인간들에게, 이렛날에 세속적 욕구가 무엇이 있을 수 있겠는가? 분명, 그들이 원할 수 있는 것이라곤 단속을 철저히 하는 경찰관 한 명뿐이었다.\n\n아서 클레넘 씨는 러드게이트 힐의 그 커피점 창가에 앉아, 이웃의 종 하나가 울리는 것을 세고 있었고, 자기도 모르게 그것으로 문장을 짓고 노래의 후렴을 만들고 있었으며, 한 해 동안 그것이 얼마나 많은 병자의 죽음을 재촉할는지 궁금해하고 있었다. 정각이 다가오자, 그 가락의 변화가 점점 더 사람을 부아 나게 했다. 십오 분 전에는 죽도록 활기찬 보챔의 상태로 돌입하여, 군중에게 청산유수로 졸라 댔다, 교회로 오라, 교회로 오라, 교회로 오라! 십 분 전에는 회중이 변변치 못하리라는 것을 알아채고는, 풀 죽은 채로 느릿느릿 두드려 댔다, 안 올 거야, 안 올 거야, 안 올 거야! 오 분 전에는 희망을 버리고, 삼백 초 동안 일대의 모든 집을 흔들어 댔다, 일 초에 한 번씩 음울하게 흔들리며, 절망의 신음처럼.\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다!\" 정각이 울리고 종이 멎자, 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n그러나 그 소리는 비참했던 일요일들의 긴 행렬을 되살려 놓았고, 그 행렬은 종소리와 함께 멎지 않고 계속 행진해 나갔다. \"하느님, 저를 용서하소서,\" 그가 말했다, \"그리고 저를 그렇게 길러 낸 이들도. 내가 이날을 얼마나 미워해 왔던가!\"\n\n손을 앞에 모으고 앉아, 그 끔찍한 소책자에 넋이 나가 있던 어린 시절의 그 음산한 일요일이 있었다. 그 책자는 표제에서부터 그 가엾은 아이에게 어째서 너는 멸망으로 가려느냐고 물으며 일을 시작했는데 — 그건 아동복에 짧은 바지 차림인 그가 정말이지 충족시켜 줄 처지가 못 되는 궁금증이었다 — 게다가 그의 어린 마음을 한층 더 끌어들이기 위해, 한 줄 걸러 한 줄마다 데살로니가 후서 3장 6절 및 7절 따위의 딸꾹질하는 듯한 인용을 괄호 안에 넣어 두었다. 군대 탈영병처럼 교사들의 분견대에 끌려 하루 세 번 예배당으로 행진하던, 다른 아이와 도덕적으로 수갑이 채워진 채로 끌려가던 소년 시절의 그 졸음 겨운 일요일이 있었다. 그때 그는 소화도 안 되는 설교 두 끼를, 그 변변찮은 저녁상의 질 낮은 양고기 한두 온스와 기꺼이 맞바꾸었을 것이다. 그의 미성년 시절의 그 끝없는 일요일이 있었다. 그때 그의 어머니는, 얼굴은 준엄하고 마음은 무자비한 채로, 온종일 성경책 뒤에 앉아 있곤 했다 — 그 성경은, 어머니 자신이 그것을 해석하는 방식 그대로, 가장 단단하고, 가장 휑하고, 가장 빡빡한 표지로 제본되어 있었으며, 표지 위에는 사슬을 끄는 자국 같은 움푹 팬 장식이 하나 있었고, 책장 가장자리에는 분노한 듯 붉은색이 뿌려져 있었다 — 마치 그것이, 하고많은 책 중에서 하필! 온화한 성정과, 천성적인 애정과, 부드러운 교제를 막아 내는 요새이기라도 한 것처럼. 그보다 조금 뒤의 그 원망스러운 일요일이 있었다. 그때 그는 마음속에 부당함을 당했다는 뚱한 감각을 품고서, 더디게 흘러가는 그 긴 하루 내내 노려보고 침울해하며 앉아 있었으되, 신약성서의 그 자애로운 이야기에 관해서는 우상 숭배자들 틈에서 자란 것이나 다름없이 진정으로 아는 것이 없었다. 무수한 일요일들의 군단이, 모두가 쓸모없는 쓰라림과 굴욕의 날들이, 그의 앞을 천천히 지나갔다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 손님,\" 발랄한 종업원이 탁자를 닦으며 말했다. \"방을 보시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그러지. 방금 그러기로 마음을 정했네.\"\n\n\"객실 담당!\" 종업원이 외쳤다. \"칠 번 좌석 신사분 방 보시겠답니다!\"\n\n\"기다리게!\" 클레넘이 정신을 차리며 말했다. \"내가 한 말을 깊이 생각하고 한 게 아니었네. 기계적으로 대답한 거야. 나는 여기서 자지 않을 걸세. 집으로 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"그러십니까, 손님? 객실 담당! 칠 번 좌석 신사분, 여기서 안 주무시고 댁으로 가신답니다.\"\n\n날이 저물어 가는 동안 그는 같은 자리에 앉아, 맞은편의 칙칙한 집들을 바라보며 생각했다, 만약 예전 거주자들의 육체에서 떠난 혼령들이 그 집들을 의식하는 일이 있다면, 자신들이 갇혔던 그 옛 자리를 두고 얼마나 스스로를 가엾이 여길까 하고. 이따금 어떤 얼굴이 창문의 거무튀튀한 유리 뒤에 나타났다가, 마치 삶을 충분히 보고 거기서 사라져 버리기라도 한 것처럼 어둠 속으로 희미하게 스러지곤 했다. 이윽고 그와 그 집들 사이로 비가 비스듬한 선을 그으며 내리기 시작했고, 사람들이 맞은편 공공 통로의 처마 밑에 모여들어, 빗방울이 점점 굵고 빠르게 떨어지는 동안 가망 없이 하늘을 올려다보기 시작했다. 그러더니 젖은 우산들이 나타나기 시작했고, 흙탕물에 끌린 치맛자락들과, 진창이 보이기 시작했다. 그 진창이 그동안 어디서 무엇을 하고 있었는지, 어디서 온 것인지 누가 말할 수 있으랴? 그러나 그것은 군중이 그러하듯 순식간에 모여드는 것 같았고, 오 분 만에 아담의 모든 자손에게 튀어 묻어 버린 것 같았다. 점등부가 이제 순회를 돌고 있었다. 그의 손길 아래 불꽃의 분출이 솟아오를 때면, 그것들이 이토록 음산한 정경에 조금이라도 밝음의 기색을 들여놓도록 허락받았다는 데에 놀라워한다고 상상해 볼 만했다.\n\n아서 클레넘 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 외투의 단추를 채운 다음, 밖으로 나갔다. 시골이었다면, 비는 신선한 향기를 수천 가지 피워 냈을 것이고, 빗방울 하나하나마다 어떤 아름다운 성장이나 생명의 형태와 결부된 밝은 연상을 지녔을 것이다. 도시에서는, 비가 더럽고 퀴퀴한 냄새만 피워 냈고, 도랑에 더해진 병약하고, 미적지근하고, 흙얼룩으로 더럽혀진, 비참한 보탬일 뿐이었다.\n\n그는 세인트폴 성당 옆을 지나, 강과 칩사이드 사이에 놓여 있는(그리고 그때는 더 구불구불하고 더 빽빽하게 놓여 있던) 구부러져 내려가는 길들을 따라, 긴 사선을 그리며 거의 강가에 닿을 만큼 내려갔다. 이제는 한물간 어느 동업 조합의 곰팡내 나는 회관을 지나고, 어느 모험심 강한 벨초니가 파내어 그 내력을 밝혀 주기를 기다리고 있는 듯한, 회중 없는 어느 교회의 불 밝힌 창문을 지나고, 고요한 창고들과 부두들을 지나고, 이따금 강으로 통하는 좁은 골목 — 거기서는 익사체 발견이라는 가엾고 작은 벽보 하나가 젖은 벽 위에서 울고 있었다 — 을 지나, 마침내 그는 자기가 찾던 집에 이르렀다. 어찌나 거무칙칙한지 거의 검다고 할 만한 오래된 벽돌집으로, 대문 안쪽에 홀로 서 있었다. 그 앞에는 네모난 안마당이 있었는데, 거기 자라난 관목 한두 그루와 풀밭 한 뙈기는, 그것들을 둘러싼 쇠난간이 녹슨 만큼이나(이건 대단한 말이다) 무성하게 웃자라 있었다. 그 뒤로는 뿌리들이 어수선하게 엉켜 있었다. 길고, 좁고, 묵직한 테를 두른 창문이 달린 이층 양옆집이었다. 여러 해 전, 그 집은 옆으로 미끄러져 내려앉을 마음을 먹은 적이 있었다. 그러나 떠받쳐져서, 대여섯 개의 거대한 버팀목에 기대고 있었다. 이웃 고양이들의 체육관이 된 그 버팀목들은, 비바람에 얼룩지고, 연기에 검게 그을리고, 잡초가 무성하게 뒤덮여, 요사이에는 그다지 미덥지 못한 의지처로 보였다.\n\n\"하나도 안 변했군,\" 여행자가 둘러보려고 걸음을 멈추며 말했다. \"여전히 어둡고 비참하군. 어머니 방의 불빛은, 내가 일 년에 두 번 학교에서 돌아와 이 포석 위로 내 상자를 끌고 오던 시절 이래 단 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없는 것 같군. 그래, 그래, 그래!\"\n\n그는 문 앞으로 다가갔다. 그 문에는, 한때 인기 있던 기념비 양식을 본떠 만든, 둥글게 늘어뜨린 닦이수건과 뇌수종에 걸린 아이들의 머리를 새긴 조각 세공의 차양이 돌출해 있었다. 그는 문을 두드렸다. 곧 현관의 돌바닥 위로 질질 끄는 발소리가 들렸고, 등이 굽고 비쩍 말랐지만 눈매는 날카로운 한 노인이 문을 열었다.\n\n그는 손에 촛불을 들고 있었는데, 날카로운 눈을 거들기 위해 그것을 잠시 치켜들었다. \"아, 아서 도련님이오?\" 그가 아무런 감정도 없이 말했다, \"드디어 오셨구려? 들어오시오.\"\n\n아서 도련님은 안으로 들어가 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"몸이 떡 벌어지고, 자리를 잡았구려,\" 노인이 다시 촛불을 치켜들고 그를 보려고 돌아서며, 고개를 가로저으며 말했다. \"허나 내 보기엔 부친만 못하시오. 모친만도 못하고.\"\n\n\"어머니는 어떠신가요?\"\n\n\"이제 늘 그러신 그대로요. 실제로 몸져눕지 않을 때도 방에만 계시고, 십몇 년 동안 그 방 밖으로 나오신 게 열다섯 번도 안 되오, 아서 도련님.\" 그들은 휑하고 빈약한 식당으로 걸어 들어와 있었다. 노인은 촛대를 탁자 위에 내려놓고, 왼손으로 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 받친 채, 손님을 바라보며 가죽 같은 턱을 쓸어 만지고 있었다. 손님이 손을 내밀었다. 노인은 꽤 차갑게 그 손을 잡았다가, 자기 턱이 더 좋은 듯, 되도록 빨리 거기로 손을 되돌렸다.\n\n\"도련님이 안식일에 집에 오신 것을 모친께서 좋아하실는지 의문이오, 아서 도련님,\" 그가 조심스레 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 다시 떠나기라도 하란 말인가요?\"\n\n\"오! 내가? 내가? 나는 주인이 아니오. 내가 원하고 말고 할 게 아니지. 나는 여러 해 동안 도련님 부친과 모친 사이에 서 있었소만, 모친과 도련님 사이에 서겠다고 나설 처지는 아니오.\"\n\n\"내가 집에 왔다고 어머니께 전해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"예, 아서 도련님, 예. 오, 물론이지요! 도련님이 집에 오셨다고 전하리다. 여기서 기다리시오. 방은 변한 게 없을 거외다.\" 그는 찬장에서 다른 촛불을 하나 꺼내 불을 붙이고, 첫 번째 것은 탁자 위에 남겨 둔 채, 심부름을 하러 갔다. 그는 키가 작고 대머리인 노인으로, 어깨가 높이 솟은 검은 외투와 조끼에, 황갈색 반바지와 긴 황갈색 각반을 차고 있었다. 옷차림으로 보아서는 서기일 수도 하인일 수도 있었는데, 사실 그는 오랫동안 둘 다였다. 장식이라고는 시계 하나뿐이었는데, 그것은 낡은 검은 리본에 매달려 제 주머니의 깊은 곳으로 내려가 있었고, 그것이 가라앉은 자리를 알려 주려고 위에는 변색된 구리 열쇠가 닻처럼 매여 있었다. 그의 머리는 비뚜름했고, 마치 그 집의 토대가 무너진 것과 거의 같은 시기에 자기 토대도 무너져서, 비슷한 방식으로 떠받쳐졌어야 했던 것처럼, 그에게는 한쪽으로 기운, 게 같은 거동이 있었다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 이리 나약한가,\" 노인이 가고 나자 아서 클레넘이 말했다, \"이런 대접에 눈물을 흘릴 수 있다니! 다른 것이라고는 겪어 본 적도 없는 내가, 다른 것이라고는 기대해 본 적도 없는 내가.\"\n\n그는 눈물을 흘릴 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 정말로 흘렸다. 그것은 지각이 동튼 순간부터 실망해 왔으되 아직 그 희망 어린 갈망을 모두 다 포기하지는 않은, 어떤 천성의 순간적인 무너짐이었다. 그는 그것을 억누르고, 촛불을 집어 들어 방을 살펴보았다. 옛 가구들은 제자리에 그대로 있었고, 이집트의 재앙들을 그린 그림들은 — 런던의 파리와 연기의 재앙으로 한결 더 흐릿해진 채 — 액자에 끼워져 유리를 두르고 벽에 걸려 있었다. 안에 아무것도 들어 있지 않은, 칸칸이 나뉜 일종의 관(棺)처럼 납으로 안을 댄 그 옛 술 보관함이 있었다. 역시 안에 아무것도 없는 그 옛 어두운 벽장도 있었는데, 그는 벌받던 시절에 여러 번 그 유일한 내용물이 되곤 했고, 그때는 그곳을, 그 소책자가 그가 질주해 가고 있다고 본 그 머나먼 세계로 들어가는 진짜 입구로 여겼었다. 찬장 위에는 그 크고, 험상궂은 시계가 있었는데, 그가 공부를 게을리할 때면 그것이 야만스러운 기쁨으로 자기를 향해 숫자 새겨진 눈썹을 찌푸리는 것을 그는 보곤 했고, 일주일에 한 번 쇠손잡이로 태엽을 감을 때면, 그것이 자기를 끌어들일 비참한 일들을 사납게 고대하며 으르렁거리는 것처럼 소리를 내곤 했다. 그러나 어느새 노인이 돌아와서 말했다, \"아서 도련님, 내가 앞장서서 불을 밝혀 드리리다.\"\n\n아서는 그를 따라 계단을 올라갔다. 그 계단은 마치 수많은 추모 명판처럼 칸칸이 판벽으로 나뉘어 있었다. 그렇게 올라간 곳은 어둑한 침실이었는데, 바닥이 차츰 가라앉고 내려앉아서, 벽난로가 움푹한 골짜기 안에 있었다. 이 우묵한 곳에는 검은, 관대(棺臺) 같은 소파가 있었고, 그 위에, 옛 좋은 시절 국사범 처형 때 쓰던 받침대처럼 모가 진 커다란 검은 베개 하나에 등을 기댄 채, 미망인 복장을 한 그의 어머니가 앉아 있었다.\n\n어머니와 아버지는 그가 기억하는 가장 이른 시절부터 사이가 틀어져 있었다. 뻣뻣한 침묵 한가운데서 그 자신도 말없이 앉아, 외면한 한쪽 얼굴에서 다른 쪽 얼굴로 두려움에 차서 흘끔거리는 것이, 그의 어린 시절의 가장 평화로운 일과였다. 어머니는 그에게 유리알 같은 입맞춤 한 번과, 털실로 감싼 뻣뻣한 손가락 네 개를 내주었다. 이 포옹이 끝나자, 그는 어머니의 작은 탁자 맞은편에 앉았다. 십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 벽난로 안에는 불이 있었다. 십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 벽난로 시렁 위에는 주전자가 있었다. 십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 불 위에는 적셔진 재가 작은 둔덕을 이루고 있었고, 또 다른 작은 둔덕은 벽난로 아래 쓸려 모여 있었다. 통풍 안 되는 그 방에는 검은 염료 냄새가 났는데, 불기운이 그것을 십오 개월 동안 미망인 복장의 크레이프와 옷감에서, 그리고 십오 년 동안 관대 같은 소파에서 뽑아내고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"어머니, 예전의 활동적이시던 모습과는 사뭇 달라지셨네요.\"\n\n\"세상이 이만한 크기로 좁아졌단다, 아서,\" 그녀가 방 안을 둘러보며 대답했다. \"내가 세상의 그 헛된 허영에 결코 마음을 두지 않았던 것이 내겐 다행이지.\"\n\n그녀의 존재와 그 준엄하고 강한 목소리가 자아내던 옛 영향력이 아들 주위로 어찌나 짙게 모여드는지, 그는 어린 시절의 그 겁먹은 한기와 위축이 되살아나는 것을 의식했다.\n\n\"방을 결코 떠나지 않으시나요, 어머니?\"\n\n\"내 류머티즘 질환에다, 거기 따라오는 쇠약이라든가 신경의 약함이라든가 — 이제 그 이름이야 아무 상관 없지만 — 그것 때문에 사지를 못 쓰게 되었다. 나는 결코 방을 떠나지 않아. 이 문밖으로 나가 본 지가 — 얼마나 됐는지 저 사람한테 말해 주거라,\" 그녀가 어깨 너머로 말했다.\n\n\"오는 성탄절이면 열두 해입니다,\" 뒤편 어둠 속에서 갈라진 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"애프리인가?\" 아서가 그쪽을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n갈라진 목소리는 애프리라고 대답했다. 그러더니 한 노파가 어슴푸레한 빛 속으로 앞으로 나와, 한 번 그의 손에 입을 맞추고는, 다시 어둠 속으로 물러났다.\n\n\"나는 할 수 있다,\" 클레넘 부인이 털실로 감싼 오른손을, 단단히 닫힌 키 큰 필기용 캐비닛 앞에 놓인 바퀴 달린 의자 쪽으로 살짝 움직이며 말했다, \"나는 내 사무를 처리할 수 있고, 그 특권에 감사한다. 그것은 큰 특권이지. 그러나 오늘은 더 이상 사무 이야기는 그만두자. 사나운 밤이다, 그렇지 않으냐?\"\n\n\"네, 어머니.\"\n\n\"눈이 오느냐?\"\n\n\"눈이요, 어머니? 아직 9월일 뿐인데요?\"\n\n\"내겐 계절이 다 마찬가지다,\" 그녀가 일종의 음울한 호사스러움으로 대답했다. \"이 안에 갇혀서, 나는 여름도 겨울도 모른다. 주께서 나를 그 모든 것 너머에 두기를 기뻐하셨지.\" 그녀의 차가운 잿빛 눈과 차가운 잿빛 머리칼, 그리고 돌처럼 단단한 머리쓰개의 주름만큼이나 뻣뻣한, 그 미동도 없는 얼굴로 보아 — 그녀가 계절의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있다는 것은, 그녀가 변화하는 온갖 감정의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있다는 것의 딱 맞는 귀결로 보일 따름이었다.\n\n그녀의 작은 탁자 위에는 책 두세 권, 손수건, 방금 벗어 놓은 강철테 안경, 그리고 묵직한 이중 뚜껑이 달린 구식 금시계가 놓여 있었다. 이 마지막 물건 위에 이제 아들의 눈과 그녀 자신의 눈이 함께 머물렀다.\n\n\"아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 제가 어머니께 보낸 꾸러미를 무사히 받으셨군요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"보다시피.\"\n\n\"아버지가 어떤 일에 대해서도 그토록 마음 졸이시는 걸 본 적이 없는데, 그 시계를 어머니께 곧장 보내라는 것만은 그렇게 마음 졸이셨지요.\"\n\n\"네 아버지를 기리는 추억으로 그것을 여기 두고 있다.\"\n\n\"바로 마지막에 가서야 그 바람을 입 밖에 내셨지요. 그때는 겨우 그 위에 손을 얹고서, 아주 불분명하게 제게 '네 어머니'라고 말씀하실 수 있을 뿐이었어요. 그 한순간 전만 해도, 저는 아버지가 정신이 흐려져 헤매시는 줄 알았어요. 여러 시간 동안 그러셨거든요 — 짧은 병환 중에 아버지가 통증을 의식하지는 못하셨던 것 같습니다 — 그때 아버지가 침대에서 몸을 돌려 그것을 열려고 애쓰시는 것을 보았지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 네 아버지가 그것을 열려고 하셨을 때는 정신이 흐려진 게 아니었느냐?\"\n\n\"아니었어요. 그때는 아주 또렷하셨습니다.\"\n\n클레넘 부인은 고개를 가로저었다. 고인을 떨쳐 버리려는 것인지, 아들의 의견에 맞서는 것인지는 분명히 드러나지 않았다.\n\n\"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤에 제가 직접 그것을 열어 보았어요. 혹시 제가 모르는 무슨 메모 같은 게 거기 있을까 해서요. 그러나, 어머니께 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없이, 거기에는 구슬로 수놓은 그 옛 비단 시계 종이밖에 없었어요. 그건 어머니도 (틀림없이) 뚜껑들 사이의 제자리에서 발견하셨을 그것으로, 저도 거기서 발견하고 그대로 두었던 겁니다.\"\n\n클레넘 부인은 그렇다는 뜻을 표하고는, 덧붙여 말했다, \"오늘은 더 이상 사무 이야기는 그만.\" 그러고는 또 덧붙였다, \"애프리, 아홉 시다.\"\n\n이 말에, 노파는 작은 탁자를 치우고 방을 나갔다가, 곧 쟁반을 들고 돌아왔다. 그 위에는 작은 러스크가 담긴 접시와, 시원하고, 좌우 대칭이고, 희고, 통통한, 한 조각 단정한 버터가 놓여 있었다. 면담이 진행되는 내내 한 가지 자세로 문가에 서 있던 그 노인은 — 아래층에서 아들을 보았던 것처럼 위층에서 어머니를 바라보면서 — 같은 시각에 나갔다가, 더 오래 자리를 비운 뒤에 또 다른 쟁반을 들고 돌아왔다. 그 위에는 (헐떡이는 모양으로 미루어 지하 저장실에서 가져온 듯한) 거의 한 병 가까이 남은 포트와인과, 레몬 하나와, 설탕 그릇과, 향신료 상자가 놓여 있었다. 이 재료들과 주전자의 도움으로, 그는 의사의 처방만큼이나 꼼꼼하게 분량을 재고 배합하여, 뜨겁고 향내 나는 혼합액을 큰 잔에 가득 채웠다. 클레넘 부인은 이 혼합액에 러스크 몇 개를 담갔다가 먹었고, 그동안 노파는 따로 먹을 다른 러스크 몇 개에 버터를 발랐다. 병약한 부인이 러스크를 다 먹고 혼합액을 다 마시자, 두 쟁반이 치워졌다. 그리고 책들과 촛불, 시계, 손수건, 안경이 탁자 위에 다시 놓였다. 그러자 그녀는 안경을 쓰고 책에서 어떤 구절들을 소리 내어 읽었다 — 준엄하게, 사납게, 노여움에 차서 — 자신의 원수들이(그녀는 어조와 태도로 그들을 명백히 자기의 원수로 만들었다) 칼날에 맞고, 불에 타고, 역병과 나병에 시달리고, 그 뼈가 가루로 빻아지고, 송두리째 멸절되기를 기도하며. 그녀가 계속 읽어 나가는 동안, 세월은 꿈속의 환영처럼 아들에게서 떨어져 나가는 듯했고, 죄 없는 어린아이가 잠자리에 들 채비를 하던 그 흔한 시간에 겪던 옛 어두운 공포들이 다 그를 다시 뒤덮어 오는 듯했다.\n\n그녀는 책을 덮고, 잠시 동안 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 있었다. 노인도 그렇게 했는데, 그 밖에는 여전히 자세를 바꾸지 않았다. 어쩌면 방의 더 어둑한 자리에 있던 노파도 그렇게 했을 것이다. 그런 다음 병든 부인은 잠자리에 들 준비가 되었다.\n\n\"잘 자거라, 아서. 잠자리는 애프리가 봐 줄 게다. 그저 살짝만 닿거라, 내 손이 약하니.\" 그는 털실로 감싼 그녀의 손을 만졌다 — 그건 아무것도 아니었다. 어머니가 놋쇠로 감싸여 있었다 한들, 둘 사이에 새로운 장벽이 생겨났을 리는 없었으니까 — 그러고는 노인과 노파를 따라 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n식당의 짙은 그림자 속에 단둘이 남게 되자, 노파가 그에게 물었다, 야식을 좀 드시겠느냐고.\n\n\"아니, 애프리, 야식은 됐네.\"\n\n\"드시고 싶으면 드셔도 돼요,\" 애프리가 말했다. \"마님 드실 내일 자고새가 식료품 저장실에 있어요 — 올해 첫 거예요. 말씀만 하시면 제가 요리해 드릴게요.\"\n\n아니, 그는 저녁을 먹은 지 얼마 안 됐고, 아무것도 먹을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그럼 뭐든 마실 거라도 드세요,\" 애프리가 말했다. \"원하시면 마님 포트와인을 좀 드시게 해 드릴게요. 도련님이 가져오라고 시키셨다고 제러마이아한테 말할게요.\"\n\n아니, 그는 그것도 마시지 않으려 했다.\n\n\"이건 말이 안 되잖아요, 아서 도련님,\" 노파가 그에게 몸을 숙여 속삭였다, \"제가 무서워서 벌벌 떤다고 해서 도련님까지 그러실 건 없잖아요. 도련님이 재산의 절반을 가지셨잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇지.\"\n\n\"그럼, 기죽지 마세요. 도련님은 영리하시잖아요, 아서 도련님, 안 그래요?\"\n\n그녀가 그렇다는 대답을 기다리는 것 같아, 그는 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그럼 그들한테 맞서세요! 마님은 엄청나게 영리하셔서, 영리한 사람이 아니고서는 아무도 마님께 감히 한마디도 못 해요. 그 양반도 영리해요 — 오, 그 양반은 영리하다니까요! — 그리고 그 양반은 마음만 먹으면 마님한테 따끔하게 해 줘요, 정말 그런다니까요!\"\n\n\"댁의 남편이 말인가?\"\n\n\"그런다니까요? 그 양반이 마님한테 따끔하게 하는 걸 들으면 저는 머리부터 발끝까지 벌벌 떨려요. 제 남편 제러마이아 플린트윈치는 도련님 어머니까지도 꺾을 수 있어요. 그런 일을 해내는 사람이 영리한 사람이 아니고 뭐겠어요!\"\n\n그가 질질 끄는 발걸음으로 그들 쪽으로 다가오자, 노파는 방 저쪽 끝으로 물러났다. 키가 크고, 험상궂고, 근육이 단단한 노파로, 젊었을 적에는 별로 들킬 염려 없이 보병 근위대에 입대할 수도 있었을 그녀가, 그 키 작고 눈매 날카로운 게 같은 노인 앞에서는 움츠러들었다.\n\n\"이봐, 애프리,\" 그가 말했다, \"이봐, 여편네, 뭘 하는 거야? 아서 도련님께 뭐 좀 집어 드실 거라도 찾아 드리지 못하겠어?\"\n\n아서 도련님은 아무것도 집어 먹지 않겠다는 좀 전의 거절을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그럼 좋소,\" 노인이 말했다. \"도련님 잠자리나 봐 드려. 어서 움직여.\" 그의 목은 어찌나 비틀려 있는지 흰 넥타이의 매듭진 끝이 으레 한쪽 귀 밑에 늘어져 있었다. 그의 천성적인 신랄함과 정력은, 늘 억누르는 습관이라는 제2의 천성과 다투면서, 그의 이목구비에 부어오르고 충혈된 듯한 모습을 주었다. 그래서 도무지, 언젠가 한 번 목을 매단 적이 있어서, 어떤 때맞춘 손이 그를 잘라 내린 바로 그 모습 그대로, 올가미고 뭐고 다 매단 채 그 뒤로 줄곧 돌아다니고 있는 듯한, 으스스한 인상을 풍겼다.\n\n\"내일 도련님과 모친이, 도련님은 모친과 모진 말을 주고받게 될 거외다, 아서 도련님,\" 제러마이아가 말했다. \"부친이 돌아가시고 도련님이 사업을 그만두셨다는 게 — 도련님이 직접 말씀하시도록 우리가 남겨 두긴 했지만 모친도 짐작은 하고 계시오 — 순순히 넘어가진 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"저는 그 사업을 위해 인생의 모든 것을 포기해 왔어요. 이제 그것마저 포기할 때가 온 거지요.\"\n\n\"좋소!\" 제러마이아가 외쳤는데, 분명 나쁘다는 뜻이었다. \"아주 좋아! 다만 내가 도련님과 모친 사이에 서 주리라고는 기대하지 마시오, 아서 도련님. 나는 도련님 모친과 부친 사이에 서서, 이것도 막아 내고 저것도 막아 내다가, 그 두 분 사이에서 짓눌리고 빻이고 했소. 그런 일은 이제 신물이 나오.\"\n\n\"다시는 저를 위해 그런 일을 시작하시라고 청하지 않겠습니다, 제러마이아.\"\n\n\"좋소. 그 말을 들으니 다행이오. 청하셨다면 거절해야 했을 테니까. 그만하면 — 모친 말씀처럼 — 됐소, 안식일 밤에 그런 이야기는 차고도 넘쳤소. 애프리, 여편네, 찾을 건 다 찾았어?\"\n\n그녀는 벽장에서 시트와 담요를 모으고 있었는데, 서둘러 그것들을 챙겨 들고 대답했다, \"예, 제러마이아.\" 아서 클레넘은 직접 그 짐을 들어 주며 그녀를 거들었고, 노인에게 잘 자라고 인사한 다음, 그녀와 함께 위층으로, 집 꼭대기까지 올라갔다.\n\n그들은 별로 쓰이지 않는 오래되고 답답한 집의 곰팡내를 뚫고, 위로 또 위로 올라가, 어느 커다란 다락 침실로 들어갔다. 다른 모든 방처럼 빈약하고 휑한 그 방은, 닳아 빠진 가구의 유배지였던 탓에, 나머지보다 한층 더 흉하고 음산했다. 거기 놓인 것들이라곤, 앉는 부분이 닳아 빠진 흉한 옛 의자들과, 앉는 부분이 아예 없는 흉한 옛 의자들, 올이 다 드러나고 무늬도 없는 양탄자, 한쪽이 망가진 탁자, 절름발이가 된 옷장, 죽은 한 벌의 해골 같은 비쩍 마른 벽난로 도구 한 벌, 더러운 비눗물 우박을 오랜 세월 맞으며 서 있었던 것처럼 보이는 세면대, 그리고 네 개의 헐벗은 뼈다귀 같은 기둥이 달린 침대틀이었다. 그 기둥들은 저마다 끝에 뾰족한 못이 박혀 있었는데, 마치 차라리 제 몸을 꿰어 버리고 싶어 할 투숙객들을 위한 음산한 편의 시설인 듯했다. 아서는 길고 나지막한 창을 열고, 비바람에 시들어 검게 그을린 그 옛 굴뚝들의 숲과, 하늘의 그 옛 붉은 불빛을 내다보았다. 그 불빛은, 한때 그에게는, 어린 시절의 공상 속에서 어느 방향으로 눈을 돌리든 사방에 펼쳐져 있던 그 불타는 환경의 밤마다의 반영으로만 보였던 것이었다.\n\n그는 다시 머리를 들이고, 침대맡에 앉아, 잠자리를 펴는 애프리 플린트윈치를 바라보았다.\n\n\"애프리, 내가 떠날 때만 해도 자네는 결혼한 사이가 아니었지.\"\n\n그녀는 입을 '아니요'라고 말하는 모양으로 오므리고, 고개를 가로젓고는, 베개를 베갯잇 속에 넣는 일에 착수했다.\n\n\"어떻게 된 일인가?\"\n\n\"아, 제러마이아 때문이지요, 물론,\" 애프리가 베갯잇 끝을 이로 문 채 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그가 청혼했겠지. 하지만 어떻게 다 그렇게 된 건가? 나는 두 사람 다 결혼하지 않으리라고 생각했을 텐데. 무엇보다도 두 사람이 서로 결혼하리라고는 생각도 못 했을 거야.\"\n\n\"저도 그랬지요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 베개를 베갯잇 속에 단단히 묶으며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그 말일세. 언제부터 달리 생각하기 시작했나?\"\n\n\"달리 생각하기 시작한 적은 아예 없어요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 말했다.\n\n베개를 토닥여 받침베개 위 제자리에 놓으면서, 그가 여전히 자기의 나머지 대답을 기다리기라도 하는 듯 바라보고 있는 것을 보고, 그녀는 베개 한가운데를 한 번 쿡 찌르고는 물었다, \"제가 어쩔 도리가 있었겠어요?\"\n\n\"결혼하지 않을 도리가 어떻게 없었느냐고!\"\n\n\"물론이지요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 말했다. \"그건 제가 한 일이 아니었어요. 저는 그런 생각은 해 본 적도 없는걸요. 정말이지, 생각할 겨를도 없이 할 일이 있었다고요! 마님이 (그 양반도 그랬지만) 돌아다니실 수 있을 때 저를 거기 매어 두셨는데, 그때는 마님이 돌아다니실 수 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 따라 말했다. \"그건 저 자신도 한 말이지요. 그래서요! 따져 본들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요? 그 영리한 두 분이 마음을 정했다면, 제게 남은 게 뭐가 있겠어요? 아무것도 없지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리 어머니의 계획이었나?\"\n\n\"하느님 도련님께 복 주시고, 그런 마음 먹은 저를 용서하시기를!\" 애프리가 줄곧 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"두 분이 그 일에 한마음이 아니었다면, 어떻게 그게 이루어질 수 있었겠어요? 제러마이아는 제게 구애한 적이 없어요. 그렇게 여러 해를 같은 집에서 저랑 살면서 저한테 이래라저래라 부려 먹어 놓고서 그럴 리가 있겠어요. 그 양반이 어느 날 제게 말하더군요, '애프리,' 그러더니, '내 이제 자네한테 할 말이 있어. 플린트윈치라는 성을 어떻게 생각하나?' 하고요. '그걸 어떻게 생각하느냐고요?' 제가 그랬지요. '그래,' 그가 말했어요, '자네가 그 성을 갖게 될 테니까 말이야.' 하고요. '갖는다고요?' 제가 그랬지요. '제-러-마-이아?' 오! 그 양반은 영리하다니까요!\"\n\n플린트윈치 부인은 위쪽 시트를 침대 위에 펼치고, 그 위에 담요를, 또 그 위에 침대보를 펼쳐 나갔다. 마치 이야기를 다 끝맺기라도 한 것처럼.\n\n\"그래서?\" 아서가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 다시 따라 말했다. \"제가 어쩔 도리가 있었겠어요? 그 양반이 제게 말하길, '애프리, 자네하고 나하고는 결혼해야 해. 그 까닭을 말해 주지. 마님이 건강이 나빠지고 있어서, 방에서 꽤 늘 시중을 받으셔야 할 거야. 그러니 우리가 마님 곁에 많이 붙어 있어야 할 텐데, 우리가 마님 곁을 떠나 있을 때 이제 이 집엔 우리 둘밖에 없을 테니, 여러모로 그게 더 편할 거야. 마님도 내 생각과 같으셔,' 그러더군요, '그러니 다음 월요일 아침 여덟 시에 모자를 쓰면, 우리가 그 일을 끝낼 거야.'\" 플린트윈치 부인은 침대를 여몄다.\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 되풀이했다, \"그러게나 말이에요! 저는 주저앉아서 그 말을 했지요. 그래서요!— 그러더니 제러마이아가 제게 말하길, '결혼 예고로 말할 것 같으면, 다음 일요일이 세 번째 공고하는 날이라(내가 두 주 전에 올려 뒀거든) 월요일로 정한 까닭이야. 마님이 직접 자네한테 그 얘길 하실 텐데, 이제 자네가 마음의 준비가 된 걸 보시게 될 거야, 애프리.' 하고요. 바로 그날 마님이 제게 말씀하시길, '그래, 애프리, 너하고 제러마이아가 결혼한다고 들었다. 나도 기쁘고, 너도 기쁘겠지, 그럴 만도 하고. 너한테 아주 좋은 일이고, 이런 형편에 내게도 아주 반가운 일이다. 그는 분별 있는 사람이고, 믿을 만한 사람이고, 끈기 있는 사람이고, 경건한 사람이지.' 하시더군요. 일이 거기까지 갔는데 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어요? 아니, 그게 결혼이 아니라 — 질식이었다 해도,\" 플린트윈치 부인은 이 표현 형식을 찾아내려고 무진 애를 쓰며 머릿속을 더듬었다, \"저는 그 영리한 두 분께 맞서 한마디도 못 했을 거예요.\"\n\n\"진심으로, 나도 그랬을 거라고 믿네.\"\n\n\"그러시는 게 옳아요, 아서 도련님.\"\n\n\"애프리, 방금 어머니 방에 있던 그 처녀는 누구였나?\"\n\n\"처녀요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 다소 날카로운 음조로 말했다.\n\n\"분명 처녀였는데, 자네 가까이에서 봤어 — 어두운 구석에 거의 가려진 채로 말이야.\"\n\n\"오! 그 애요? 리틀 도릿? 그 앤 아무것도 아니에요. 그 애는 마님의 — 변덕이지요.\" 클레넘 부인을 결코 이름으로 부르지 않는 것이 애프리 플린트윈치의 특이한 점이었다. \"하지만 그런 애 말고 다른 종류의 처녀도 있잖아요. 도련님의 옛 연인을 잊으셨어요? 까마득히 오래된 일이긴 하겠지만요.\"\n\n\"어머니가 우리를 떼어 놓으셔서 충분히 괴로웠으니, 그녀를 기억하지. 아주 잘 기억하고 있어.\"\n\n\"새 연인이 생기셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니.\"\n\n\"그럼 도련님께 알려 드릴 소식이 있어요. 그 여자는 이제 살림이 넉넉하고, 미망인이 됐어요. 그래서 도련님이 그 여자를 맞아들이고 싶으시면, 그럴 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"그건 또 어떻게 아나, 애프리?\"\n\n\"그 영리한 두 분이 그 얘길 하고 계셨거든요.— 저기 제러마이아가 계단에 있어요!\" 그녀는 순식간에 사라졌다.\n\n플린트윈치 부인은, 그의 젊은 날의 베틀이 놓여 있던 그 옛 작업장에서 그의 마음이 분주히 짜 나가고 있던 직물 속에, 그 무늬에 빠져 있던 마지막 한 올을 끼워 넣은 것이었다. 소년의 사랑이라는 그 덧없는 어리석음은 그 집 안에까지도 길을 찾아 들었고, 그래서 그는 그 가망 없음 아래에서, 마치 그 집이 로맨스 속의 성(城)이기라도 한 것처럼 비참했었다. 일주일 남짓 전 마르세유에서, 그가 아쉬움 속에 작별했던 그 예쁜 처녀의 얼굴이 그에게 남달리 흥미로웠고, 그의 마음을 애틋하게 사로잡았던 것은, 그것이, 음울했던 그의 삶에서 솟구쳐 올라 공상의 찬란한 영광 속으로 날아오른 그 첫 번째 얼굴과, 실재든 상상이든 어떤 닮은 데가 있었기 때문이다. 그는 길고 나지막한 창턱에 기대어, 다시 그 검게 그을린 굴뚝들의 숲을 내다보며, 몽상에 잠기기 시작했다. 이 사내의 삶에는 — 생각할 거리가 그토록 없었고, 더 나은 방향으로 이끌렸더라면 더 행복했을 만한, 사색할 만한 것이 그토록 없었던 까닭에 — 결국 그를 몽상가로 만드는 것이 한결같은 경향이었으니까.\n\n# CHAPTER 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream\n\n플린트윈치 부인이 꿈을 꿀 때면, 옛 여주인의 아들과는 달리 대개 눈을 감고 꾸었다. 그날 밤 그녀는 묘하게도 생생한 꿈을 꾸었는데, 옛 여주인의 아들과 헤어진 지 몇 시간도 지나지 않아서였다. 사실 그것은 도무지 꿈 같지 않았다. 어느 모로 보나 너무도 생생했던 것이다. 일은 이렇게 벌어졌다.\n\n플린트윈치 부부가 쓰는 침실은 클레넘 부인이 그토록 오래 갇혀 지내던 방에서 몇 걸음 떨어져 있었다. 같은 층은 아니었으니, 그것은 집 옆쪽에 있는 방으로, 클레넘 부인의 방문 거의 맞은편에서 본계단으로부터 갈라져 내려가는 몇 개의 들쭉날쭉한 가파른 계단을 통해 들어가는 곳이었다. 그 오래된 집의 벽과 문, 판벽이 어찌나 둔중한지 부르면 들릴 거리라고는 할 수 없었지만, 한밤중 어느 시각에든, 어떤 차림새로든, 어떤 날씨에든 손쉽게 닿을 수 있는 거리였다. 침대 머리맡, 플린트윈치 부인의 귀에서 한 자도 안 되는 곳에 종이 있었고, 그 줄은 클레넘 부인의 손이 닿도록 늘어뜨려져 있었다. 이 종이 울릴 때마다 애프리는 벌떡 일어나, 잠이 채 깨기도 전에 병실에 가 있곤 했다.\n\n여주인을 잠자리에 들이고, 등불을 켜 주고, 잘 자라는 인사를 마친 뒤, 플린트윈치 부인은 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었다. 다만 그녀의 주인 양반이 아직 모습을 보이지 않은 것만 달랐다. 대부분의 철학자들이 관찰한 바와는 달리—마음속 마지막 화제와는 어긋나게—플린트윈치 부인의 꿈의 주인공이 된 것은 바로 그녀의 주인 양반 자신이었다.\n\n그녀가 몇 시간 자고 난 뒤 깨어 보니 제러마이아가 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 것 같았다. 켜 둔 채로 두었던 촛불을 보고, 앨프레드 대왕처럼 그 시간을 가늠해 보니, 다 타들어 간 그 상태로 미루어 자기가 상당히 오랫동안 잠들어 있었다는 믿음이 굳어졌다. 그래서 그녀는 일어나 실내복으로 몸을 감싸고, 신을 신고, 몹시 의아해하며 제러마이아를 찾으러 계단으로 나갔다.\n\n계단은 더할 나위 없이 목재로 되어 단단했고, 애프리는 꿈 특유의 그 어떤 비틀거림도 없이 곧장 그것을 내려갔다. 미끄러져 내려간 것이 아니라 걸어 내려갔는데, 촛불이 꺼져 버린 탓에 난간을 짚어 가며 길을 더듬었다. 현관 한쪽 구석, 집 대문 뒤에는 작은 대기실이 하나 있었는데, 마치 우물 갱처럼 좁다랗고, 마치 칼로 찢어 낸 듯한 길고 가느다란 창문이 하나 나 있었다. 한 번도 쓰인 적 없는 이 방에 불이 켜져 있었다.\n\n플린트윈치 부인은 양말도 신지 않은 발에 차갑게 와 닿는 바닥을 느끼며 현관을 가로질러, 살짝 열려 있는 문의 녹슨 돌쩌귀 사이로 안을 들여다보았다. 그녀는 제러마이아가 곤히 잠들어 있거나 발작이라도 일으킨 모습을 보리라 예상했지만, 그는 의자에 차분히 앉아 깨어 있었고, 평소처럼 건강한 모습이었다. 그런데 저것은—아니?—하느님 맙소사!—플린트윈치 부인은 그런 뜻의 외마디를 중얼거리며 어지럼증에 휩싸였다.\n\n왜냐하면 깨어 있는 플린트윈치 씨가 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치 씨를 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 그는 작은 탁자 한쪽에 앉아, 맞은편에서 턱을 가슴팍에 푹 떨군 채 코를 골고 있는 자기 자신을 날카롭게 바라보고 있었다. 깨어 있는 플린트윈치는 정면 얼굴을 아내 쪽으로 온전히 드러내고 있었고, 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치는 옆모습이었다. 깨어 있는 플린트윈치가 본디 원본이었고, 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치는 그 분신이었으니, 마치 실체와 거울에 비친 그 상을 구별하듯, 애프리는 머리가 빙글빙글 도는 가운데 이 차이를 알아보았다.\n\n어느 쪽이 자신의 제러마이아인지 의심이 들었다 해도, 그의 조바심을 보면 그 의심은 풀렸을 것이다. 그는 무슨 무기가 없나 사방을 둘러보더니, 촛불 가위를 집어 들었고, 그것을 양배추 머리처럼 타 버린 심지에 대기도 전에, 마치 그 몸을 꿰뚫기라도 할 듯 잠든 자에게 들이댔다.\n\n\"누구야? 무슨 일이야?\" 잠든 자가 흠칫 놀라 소리쳤다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 마치 가위를 동행자의 목구멍에 쑤셔 넣어 입을 다물게 하려는 듯 그것을 휘둘렀다. 동행자는 정신을 차리며 눈을 비비고 말했다. \"내가 여기가 어딘지 깜빡했군.\"\n\n\"넌 자고 있었어.\" 제러마이아가 회중시계를 보며 으르렁거렸다. \"두 시간이나. 잠깐 눈만 붙이면 충분히 쉴 거라고 했잖아.\"\n\n\"잠깐 눈만 붙였지.\" 분신이 말했다.\n\n\"새벽 두 시 반이군.\" 제러마이아가 중얼거렸다. \"네 모자는 어디 있어? 외투는? 상자는?\"\n\n\"다 여기 있어.\" 분신이 졸린 듯 조심스럽게 숄로 목을 감싸며 말했다. \"잠깐만. 자, 소매를 좀 끼워 주게—그 소매 말고, 다른 쪽. 하! 이젠 예전만큼 젊지가 않으니.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 거친 기세로 그를 외투 속에 밀어 넣었다. \"쉬고 나면 두 잔째를 주겠다고 약속했잖아.\"\n\n\"마셔!\" 제러마이아가 대꾸했다. \"그리고—목에 걸려 죽어라, 라고 말하려던 참이었지만—가라는 말이야.\" 동시에 그는 바로 그 포트와인 병을 꺼내 잔을 가득 채웠다.\n\n\"이거 그 사람 포트와인이지?\" 분신이, 마치 부두에서 시간이 남아도는 사람처럼 그것을 음미하며 말했다. \"그 사람의 건강을 위하여.\"\n\n그가 한 모금 들이켰다.\n\n\"네 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n그가 또 한 모금 들이켰다.\n\n\"그분의 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n그가 또 한 모금 들이켰다.\n\n\"그리고 세인트폴 주변의 모든 친구들을 위하여.\" 그는 이 오래된 도시풍 건배사를 반쯤 외우다 말고 잔을 비워 내려놓고는 상자를 집어 들었다. 그것은 사방 두 자쯤 되는 철제 상자로, 그는 그것을 두 팔에 끼고 제법 수월하게 들었다. 제러마이아는 시샘 어린 눈으로 그가 그것을 추스르는 모양새를 지켜보았다. 그것을 단단히 쥐고 있는지 확인하려 두 손으로 만져 보았고, 목숨을 걸고 하는 일이니 조심하라고 일렀으며, 그러고는 그를 위해 문을 열어 주려 발끝으로 살금살금 빠져나갔다. 애프리는 그 마지막 동작을 예감하고 계단에 가 있었다. 일의 순서가 너무도 평범하고 자연스러워, 거기 서 있는 그녀는 문이 열리는 소리를 듣고, 밤공기를 느끼고, 바깥의 별들을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 이제 꿈에서 가장 주목할 만한 대목이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 남편이 어찌나 무서웠던지, 계단에 있으면서도(그가 문을 잠그기 전이라면 쉽게 자기 방으로 물러설 수 있었을 텐데) 물러날 힘이 없어, 그저 거기 서서 멍하니 바라보고만 있었다. 그리하여 그가 촛불을 손에 들고 잠자리에 들려 계단을 올라왔을 때, 그는 그녀와 정면으로 맞닥뜨렸다. 그는 놀란 표정이었지만 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 계속 다가왔고, 그녀는 완전히 그의 위세에 눌려 그 앞에서 계속 물러섰다. 이렇게 그녀는 뒷걸음질 치고 그는 앞으로 걸어, 두 사람은 자기들 방으로 들어왔다. 거기에 갇히기가 무섭게 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀의 목을 움켜쥐고, 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 흔들어 댔다.\n\n\"이런, 애프리, 여보게—애프리!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 거야? 정신 차려, 정신 차려! 무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"무, 무슨 일이라뇨, 제러마이아?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 눈을 굴리며 숨을 헐떡였다.\n\n\"이런, 애프리, 여보게—애프리! 당신 잠결에 침대에서 빠져나왔구먼! 나는 아래층에서 그만 잠이 들었다가 올라와 보니, 당신이 실내복 차림으로 여기 서서 가위눌려 있는 거야. 애프리, 여보게,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 그 풍부한 표정의 얼굴에 다정한 능글맞은 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"다시 이런 꿈을 꾸거든, 그건 당신한테 약이 필요하다는 신호일 거야. 그러면 내가 약을 한 사발 먹여 주지, 여보—한 사발 가득!\"\n\n플린트윈치 부인은 그에게 고맙다고 하고는 슬그머니 침대로 기어들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Family Affairs\n\n월요일 아침 시내의 시계들이 아홉 시를 칠 때, 잔뜩 풀이 죽은 모양새의 제레미아 플린트윈치가 클레넘 부인을 그녀의 키 큰 캐비닛 앞으로 바퀴 의자에 태워 밀어 놓았다. 그녀가 그것을 열쇠로 열어 젖히고 그 책상 앞에 자리를 잡자, 제레미아는 — 어쩌면 좀 더 확실하게 목이라도 매러 가려는 듯 — 물러갔고, 그녀의 아들이 나타났다.\n\n\"오늘 아침은 좀 나으세요, 어머니?\"\n\n그녀는 간밤에 날씨에 관해 말할 때 보였던 것과 똑같은, 호사스러움이 깃든 엄격한 분위기로 고개를 저었다. \"나는 더 나아지는 일은 결코 없을 게다. 아서, 그걸 내가 알고 또 견딜 수 있다는 건 나에겐 다행이지.\"\n\n두 손을 책상 위에 따로따로 얹은 채, 키 큰 캐비닛이 앞에서 우뚝 솟아오른 가운데 앉아 있는 그녀는 마치 소리 없는 교회 오르간을 연주하고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그녀의 아들은 그 옆에 자리를 잡으면서 그렇게 생각했다(그에게는 오래된 생각이었다).\n\n그녀는 서랍을 한두 개 열어 사무 서류들을 들여다보고는 다시 도로 넣었다. 그녀의 엄한 얼굴에는 어떤 탐색자라도 그녀의 음울한 생각의 미궁으로 이끌릴 만한 누그러짐의 실마리 하나 없었다.\n\n\"우리 일에 대해 말씀드려도 될까요, 어머니? 사업 이야기를 시작할 마음이 있으신가요?\"\n\n\"마음이 있느냐고, 아서? 차라리 네가 있느냐고 묻는 게 낫겠구나. 네 아버지가 돌아가신 지 일 년이 넘었다. 그 후로 줄곧 나는 네 처분에 맡겨진 채, 네 뜻을 기다려 왔다.\"\n\n\"제가 떠날 수 있기까지 정리할 일이 많았습니다. 그리고 정작 떠난 뒤에는 휴식과 안정을 위해 조금 여행을 했지요.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 마지막 말을 듣지 못했거나 알아듣지 못한 것처럼 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸다.\n\n\"휴식과 안정을 위해서.\"\n\n그녀는 음침한 방을 둘러보았고, 그 입술의 움직임으로 보아 그 말을 혼자 되뇌는 듯했으니, 마치 이 방이 자신에게 그 둘 가운데 어느 것도 거의 베풀어 주지 않았음을 증언하라는 듯이.\n\n\"게다가 어머니, 어머니가 유일한 유언 집행자로서 재산의 관리와 운영을 맡고 계시니, 어머니가 흡족하실 만큼 일을 정리하실 시간을 갖기 전까지는 제가 처리할 수 있는 사업이라곤 거의 없었습니다, 아니 전혀 없었다고 해도 좋겠지요.\"\n\n\"계산은 다 정리되어 있다.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"여기 있어. 증빙들도 모두 검토되고 통과되었다. 보고 싶을 때 언제든 살펴보아라, 아서. 지금이라도 좋으면.\"\n\n\"사업이 완결되었다는 것을 아는 것만으로 충분합니다, 어머니. 그럼 계속해도 될까요?\"\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 그 얼어붙은 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"어머니, 우리 상사는 지난 몇 해 동안 점점 더 일이 줄어들었고, 거래도 차츰 기울어 왔습니다. 우리는 신용을 많이 보인 적도, 많이 청한 적도 없습니다. 사람을 우리에게 붙잡아 둔 적도 없고요. 우리가 지켜 온 길은 시대의 길이 아니어서, 우리는 한참 뒤처지고 말았습니다. 이 점을 어머니께 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠지요, 어머니. 어머니께서는 당연히 아실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 안다.\" 그녀가 단정 짓는 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"우리가 이렇게 이야기를 나누고 있는 이 낡은 집조차,\" 그녀의 아들이 말을 이었다. \"제가 말씀드린 바를 보여 주는 한 예입니다. 아버지의 젊은 시절, 그리고 그 전 아버지의 숙부 시절에는, 이곳이 사업장이었습니다 — 정말로 사업장이자 거래가 드나드는 곳이었지요. 지금은 그저 이곳에 어울리지 않는 변칙이고 부조화일 뿐이며, 시대에도 목적에도 맞지 않습니다. 우리의 위탁 거래는 오래전부터 모두 위탁 상인인 로빙엄 상회로 넘어가 있고요. 그들을 견제하기 위해, 그리고 아버지의 재산을 관리하시면서 어머니의 판단과 경계심이 활발히 발휘되어 왔다고는 하나, 그래도 그런 자질들은 어머니가 어느 사가에 사셨더라도 아버지의 재산에 똑같이 영향을 미쳤을 겁니다. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"아서, 네 생각엔,\" 그녀가 그의 질문에는 답하지 않은 채 대꾸했다. \"집이라는 게, 병들고 고통받는 — 마땅히 병들고 의롭게 고통받는 — 네 어미를 거두는 데에 아무 쓸모도 없다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"저는 사업상의 쓸모만을 말씀드린 것입니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 의도로?\"\n\n\"이제 그 이야기로 들어가려 합니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 이야기인지 짐작이 가는구나.\" 그녀가 그에게 시선을 고정하며 대꾸했다. \"허나 어떤 시련 아래에서도 내가 불평하는 일이 없도록 주께서 막아 주시기를. 죄 많은 나는 쓰라린 실망을 받아 마땅하니, 나는 그것을 받아들인다.\"\n\n\"어머니, 어머니께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 들으니 마음이 아픕니다. 그러시리라는 염려가 없었던 건 아니지만 —\"\n\n\"네가 그러리라는 걸 알고 있었다. 너는 이 어미를 알았으니까.\" 그녀가 그의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n그녀의 아들은 잠시 멈칫했다. 그는 그녀에게서 불꽃을 일으켜 놓은 것이었고, 놀라웠다. \"좋다!\" 그녀가 다시 돌처럼 굳어지며 말했다. \"계속하려무나. 들어 보자.\"\n\n\"어머니, 제가 제 몫의 일에 관해, 사업을 그만두기로 결심했다는 것을 어머니께서는 미리 짐작하셨을 겁니다. 저는 그 일에서 손을 뗐습니다. 감히 어머니께 조언하려 들지는 않겠습니다. 어머니께서는 그 일을 계속하시겠지요, 알겠습니다. 만약 제가 어머니께 조금이라도 영향력을 가졌다면, 그저 그것으로 어머니께 이런 실망을 안겨 드린 저에 대한 어머니의 판단을 누그러뜨리는 데 쓰고 싶습니다. 즉, 제가 긴 인생의 절반을 살아오는 동안 단 한 번도 제 뜻을 어머니의 뜻에 맞서 세운 적이 없었다는 것을 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 제가 마음과 정신으로 어머니의 규율에 저를 맞출 수 있었다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다. 제 사십 년이 저 자신에게나 그 누구에게 유익하거나 즐거웠다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다. 그러나 저는 늘 순종해 왔으니, 그것만은 기억해 주시기를 청합니다.\"\n\n만약 캐비닛 앞의 그 냉혹한 얼굴에서 어떤 양보를 기대할 탄원자가 있었거나 일찍이 있었다면, 그런 자에게 화 있을진저. 그 준엄한 두 눈이 주재하는 법정에 호소할 채무자에게 화 있을진저. 음울과 어둠에 가려져, 검은 구름 사이로 저주와 복수와 파멸의 번개가 번득이는 그 신비주의적 종교가 이 강철 같은 여인에게는 큰 필요였다. 우리가 우리에게 빚진 자를 용서하듯 우리 빚을 용서하소서, 라는 기도는 그녀에게는 정신이 너무 빈약한 기도였다. 주여, 제 빚진 자들을 치소서, 그들을 시들게 하소서, 그들을 짓이기소서. 제가 하고 싶은 대로 주께서 하시면 제 경배를 받으시리이다. 이것이 그녀가 하늘에 오르려고 쌓아 올린 불경한 돌탑이었다.\n\n\"다 끝냈느냐, 아서, 아니면 나에게 더 할 말이 있느냐? 더는 없을 듯싶구나. 너는 짧게 말했다만, 알맹이는 잔뜩 들었구나!\"\n\n\"어머니, 아직 더 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 오랫동안 밤낮으로 제 마음을 짓눌러 온 것입니다. 방금 드린 말씀보다 훨씬 더 꺼내기 어려운 이야기입니다. 그것은 저 자신에 관한 것이었지만, 이것은 우리 모두에 관한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"우리 모두라니! 우리 모두라는 게 누구냐?\"\n\n\"어머니 자신, 저 자신, 그리고 돌아가신 아버지입니다.\"\n그녀는 두 손을 책상에서 떼어 무릎 위에 포개고는, 고대 이집트 조각상 같은 헤아릴 길 없는 표정으로 불을 향해 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"어머니는 제가 아버지를 알았던 것보다 한없이 더 잘 아버지를 아셨고, 저에게 닫혀 있던 아버지의 마음도 어머니께는 열렸지요. 어머니가 훨씬 더 강하셨고, 아버지를 이끄셨습니다. 어린아이였을 때도 저는 지금만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았습니다. 아버지가 중국으로 건너가 그곳 사업을 돌보시고 어머니가 이곳에서 사업을 돌보시게 된 것이 어머니가 아버지를 지배하신 탓이었음을 알았습니다(물론 그것이 정말로 두 분이 합의하신 별거의 조건이었는지는 지금도 모릅니다만). 그리고 제가 스무 살이 될 때까지 어머니 곁에 머물렀다가, 그 후에 아버지께로 가게 된 것도 어머니의 뜻이었지요. 제가 그렇게 했습니다. 이십 년이 지난 지금 이것을 되새긴다고 해서 노여워하시지는 않겠지요?\"\n\n\"왜 그걸 되새기는지 들어 보려고 기다리는 중이다.\"\n\n그는 목소리를 낮추고, 분명한 망설임을 띤 채, 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 말했다.\n\n\"어머니께 여쭙고 싶습니다. 혹시 어머니께서 한 번이라도 의심해 보신 적이 있는지 —\"\n\n의심이라는 말에 그녀는 어두운 찌푸림으로 잠깐 아들에게 두 눈을 돌렸다. 그러고는 전처럼 두 눈을 불을 향하게 두었으되, 마치 고대 이집트의 조각가가 그 단단한 화강암 얼굴에 영겁토록 찌푸린 채로 있도록 새겨 넣은 것처럼, 그 위에 찌푸림을 굳힌 채였다.\n\n\"— 아버지께서 마음의 괴로움을 — 회한을 — 일으키는 어떤 비밀스러운 기억을 품고 계셨던 건 아닌지를 말입니다. 아버지의 행동에서 그런 것을 암시하는 무언가를 관찰하신 적이 있는지, 혹은 그 일에 관해 아버지와 이야기를 나누신 적이 있는지, 아니면 아버지께서 그런 것을 넌지시 비추는 것을 들으신 적이 있는지를요.\"\n\n\"네가 추론하려는, 네 아버지를 괴롭혔다는 그 비밀스러운 기억이라는 게 어떤 종류의 것인지 나는 모르겠다.\" 그녀가 침묵 끝에 대꾸했다. \"너는 너무 알쏭달쏭하게 말하는구나.\"\n\n\"혹시 그러신 게 아닐까요, 어머니,\" 그녀의 아들은 속삭이며 그녀에게 더 가까이 가려고 몸을 앞으로 숙이고는 초조하게 그녀의 책상 위에 손을 얹었다. \"혹시, 어머니, 아버지께서 불행히도 누군가에게 해를 끼치고는 아무런 보상도 하지 않으신 게 아닐까요?\"\n\n그를 노여워하며 바라보던 그녀는 그를 더 멀리 떼어 놓으려고 의자에 등을 젖혔지만, 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"저는 깊이 느끼고 있습니다, 어머니. 만약 이런 생각이 어머니께는 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없는 것이라면, 이런 은밀한 자리에서나마 제가 그것을 입에 올리는 것이 어머니께는 잔인하고 부자연스럽게 보이리라는 것을요. 그러나 저는 그 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없습니다. 세월도 변화도 (침묵을 깨기 전에 저는 그 둘을 다 시험해 보았습니다) 그것을 닳아 없애지 못합니다. 기억하세요, 저는 아버지 곁에 있었습니다. 기억하세요, 아버지께서 그 시계를 제게 맡기시며, 그것을 어머니께서 알아들으실 정표로 어머니께 보내신다고 애써 표현하려 하시던 그 얼굴을 저는 보았습니다. 기억하세요, 마지막 순간에 아버지께서 힘 빠진 손에 연필을 쥐고, 어머니께서 읽으실 무슨 말을 쓰려 하셨으나 끝내 형태를 이루지 못하시던 모습을 저는 보았습니다. 제가 품은 이 막연한 의심이 멀고 잔인할수록, 저에게 그것이 그럴듯하게 보이도록 만드는 정황은 더 강해집니다. 부디, 우리에게 바로잡으라고 맡겨진 잘못이 있는지 없는지 경건하게 살펴봅시다. 그 일을 거들 수 있는 사람은, 어머니, 어머니밖에 없습니다.\"\n\n그녀가 의자에 너무 깊이 몸을 젖혀, 한쪽으로 쏠린 무게가 이따금 의자를 바퀴 위에서 조금씩 움직여, 사나운 모습의 유령이 그에게서 미끄러져 멀어지는 듯한 형상을 만들어 내는 가운데, 그녀는 왼팔을 팔꿈치에서 굽혀 손등을 제 얼굴 쪽으로 향하게 한 채 자신과 그 사이에 끼워 넣고는, 굳은 침묵 속에서 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"돈을 움켜쥐고 모진 흥정을 벌이는 가운데 — 이미 시작했으니, 어머니, 이제 이런 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다 — 누군가가 처참하게 속고, 다치고, 파멸당했을지도 모릅니다. 제가 태어나기 전부터 이 모든 기계 장치의 동력은 어머니셨습니다. 사십 년이 넘도록 아버지의 모든 거래에는 어머니의 더 강한 정신이 스며들어 있었지요. 어머니가 정말로 진실을 밝히는 데 저를 도와주신다면, 이 의혹들을 가라앉히실 수 있으리라 생각합니다. 도와주시겠습니까, 어머니?\"\n\n그는 그녀가 입을 열기를 바라며 말을 멈췄다. 그러나 두 갈래로 빗어 넘긴 그녀의 잿빛 머리도 그녀의 굳게 다문 입술보다 더 미동도 없지는 못했다.\n\n\"누군가에게 보상이 이루어질 수 있다면, 누군가에게 배상이 이루어질 수 있다면, 그것을 알아내어 그렇게 합시다. 아니, 어머니, 제 형편이 닿는 한, 제가 그것을 하게 해 주십시오. 저는 돈에서 행복이 나오는 것을 거의 본 적이 없습니다. 돈은 이 집에든, 이 집에 딸린 누구에게든 평안을 거의 가져다주지 않았으니, 그것은 저에게는 남들에게보다 가치가 덜합니다. 만약 그 돈이 아버지의 마지막 시간을 회한으로 어둡게 했고, 또 정직하고 정당하게 제 것이 아니라는 의심에 제가 시달린다면, 그것이 저에게 사 줄 수 있는 것이라고는 모두 비난과 비참이 될 뿐입니다.\"\n\n캐비닛에서 두세 야드쯤 떨어진 곳, 벽판에 종 줄이 걸려 있었다. 그녀는 발을 빠르고 갑작스럽게 움직여 바퀴 의자를 그쪽으로 잽싸게 밀어 가서는 그것을 힘껏 잡아당겼다 — 여전히 마치 그가 자신을 치려 하고 자기는 그 일격을 막아 내려는 듯, 팔을 방패 같은 자세로 들어 올린 채였다.\n\n한 처녀가 겁에 질려 허둥지둥 들어왔다.\n\n\"플린트윈치를 이리 보내라!\"\n\n순식간에 처녀는 물러갔고, 노인이 문 안에 섰다. \"아니! 두 분이 벌써 으르렁대고 계셨소?\" 그가 얼굴을 천천히 쓸어내리며 차분하게 말했다. \"그러실 줄 알았소. 거의 확신했지요.\"\n\n\"플린트윈치!\" 어머니가 말했다. \"내 아들을 보게. 저 애를 보게!\"\n\n\"음, 보고 있소이다.\" 플린트윈치가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 자신을 방패처럼 가렸던 팔을 쭉 뻗어, 말을 이으면서 자신의 분노의 대상을 가리켰다.\n\n\"돌아온 지 거의 그 시각도 채 못 되어 — 제 발에 신은 신이 마르기도 전에 — 저 애가 제 어미 앞에서 제 아비의 기억을 더럽히고 있다네! 자기와 함께 평생에 걸친 제 아비의 거래를 캐는 첩자가 되어 달라고 제 어미에게 청하다니! 우리가 이른 새벽부터 밤늦게까지, 닳고 해지고 애써 고생하고 자제하며 어렵사리 그러모은 이 세상의 재물이 죄다 약탈물이 아닌가 하는 의심을 품고는, 그것을 보상이며 배상이라며 누구에게 넘겨주어야 하느냐고 묻고 있다네!\"\n\n이것을 노발대발하며 말하긴 했어도, 그녀의 목소리는 자제력을 잃기는커녕 평소의 어조보다 오히려 더 낮을 정도였다. 그녀는 또한 더없이 또렷하게 말했다.\n\n\"보상이라!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 정말이지! 외국 땅에서 여행과 흥청거림을 막 마치고 와 허영과 향락의 삶을 누리는 저 애에게는 보상을 입에 올리기가 쉽겠지. 허나 여기 감옥에서, 사슬에 매여 있는 이 어미를 보라지. 나는 불평 없이 견딘다. 내 죄를 그렇게 보상하도록 정해져 있기 때문이지. 보상이라! 이 방 안에 그게 없단 말이냐? 지난 십오 년간 여기에 그게 없었단 말이냐?\"\n\n이렇듯 그녀는 늘 하늘의 위엄과 자신의 거래를 따져 맞추며, 자기 쪽 대변에 항목을 기입하고, 자신의 상쇄분을 엄격히 챙기며, 받을 몫을 요구했다. 그녀가 이 점에서 유별난 것은 다만 그것을 행하는 힘과 강조에 있을 뿐이었다. 수천, 수만의 사람들이 저마다의 방식에 따라 날마다 그렇게 한다.\n\n\"플린트윈치, 그 책을 이리 주게!\"\n\n노인은 탁자에서 그것을 집어 그녀에게 건넸다. 그녀는 책장 사이에 손가락 두 개를 끼우고 그 위로 책을 덮어 든 채, 위협하는 자세로 아들에게 높이 들어 보였다.\n\n\"이 주해서에서 다루는 옛날에는, 아서, 이보다 더 작은 일로도 제 아들을 저주했을, 주께서 사랑하시는 경건한 사람들이 있었다. 그자들은 제 아들들을 내쫓았을 것이고, 또 그런 무리가 제 아들들을 편들었다면 온 민족을 내쫓아, 하느님과 사람에게 외면당하고 멸망케 하여, 젖먹이 갓난아이에 이르기까지 죽게 했을 것이다. 허나 나는 너에게 이것만은 일러 두마. 네가 만약 또다시 나에게 그 주제를 끄집어낸다면, 나는 너를 의절할 것이다. 너를 저 문간으로 내쫓아, 너는 요람에서부터 차라리 어미가 없었던 편이 나았다 할 만큼 만들 것이다. 나는 너를 다시는 보지도 알지도 않을 것이다. 그리고 끝내 네가 죽어 누운 나를 보러 이 어두운 방에 들어온다면, 할 수만 있다면, 네가 가까이 올 때 내 시신이 피를 흘릴 것이다.\"\n\n이 위협의 격렬함으로 어느 정도 풀리고, 또 어느 정도는 (이런 사실이 괴이하기는 하나) 그것이 어떤 의미에서는 종교적 행위라는 막연한 인상으로 마음이 풀린 그녀는, 책을 노인에게 도로 건네주고 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"자,\" 제레미아가 말했다. \"내가 두 분 사이에 끼어들 생각은 없다는 걸 전제하고, (이렇게 불려 와 제삼자가 되었으니) 이게 다 무슨 일인지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"어머니께 들으시지요.\" 말할 차례가 자기에게 넘어온 것을 알아챈 아서가 대꾸했다. \"거기서 끝내 둡시다. 제가 한 말은 어머니께만 드린 말이었으니까요.\"\n\n\"오!\" 노인이 대꾸했다. \"어머님께? 어머님께 들으라고? 그렇지! 허나 어머님은 자네가 아버지를 의심해 왔다고 말씀하시던데. 그건 효성스럽지 못한 일이오, 아서 씨. 다음엔 누구를 의심하려고 그러시오?\"\n\n\"그만하면 됐네.\" 클레넘 부인이 잠시 노인에게만 들리도록 얼굴을 돌리며 말했다. \"이 일에 대해서는 더는 말하지 말게.\"\n\n\"예, 허나 잠깐만, 잠깐만요.\" 노인이 고집했다. \"우리가 어떻게 서 있는지 봅시다. 아서 씨에게 그가 제 아비에게 잘못을 뒤집어씌워서는 안 된다고 말씀하셨소? 그가 그럴 권리가 없다고? 근거로 삼을 게 아무것도 없다고?\"\n\n\"지금 그렇게 말하고 있네.\"\n\n\"아! 바로 그거요.\" 노인이 말했다. \"지금 그렇게 말씀하신다. 전에는 그에게 그렇게 말씀하지 않으시고, 이제 와 그렇게 말씀하신다. 옳거니, 옳거니! 그게 옳소! 내가 그토록 오랫동안 마님과 그의 아비 사이에 끼어 있던 터라, 죽음이 와도 달라진 게 없는 듯, 나는 여태 두 분 사이에 끼어 있는 것 같소. 그러니 그렇게 하리다. 그리고 공정을 위해서라도 나는 그걸 분명히 내세워 달라고 요구하는 바요. 아서 씨, 들어 두시오. 자네는 아버지를 불신할 권리도 없고, 근거로 삼을 것도 없소.\"\n\n그는 바퀴 의자 뒤편에 손을 얹고 혼잣말로 중얼대며, 자신의 여주인을 천천히 캐비닛 앞으로 도로 밀고 갔다. \"자,\" 그가 그녀의 뒤에 선 채 말을 이었다. \"혹시라도 내가 일을 절반만 해 놓고 떠났다가, 마님이 나머지 절반에 이르러 또 흥분에 휩싸이실 때 다시 불려 오는 일이 없도록 묻겠는데, 아서 씨가 사업에 관해 어찌하려는지 마님께 말씀드렸소?\"\n\n\"그 애는 그것을 포기했네.\"\n\n\"누구한테도 넘겨주지 않고, 짐작건대?\"\n\n클레넘 부인은 창문 하나에 기대 선 아들에게 시선을 던졌다. 그는 그 눈길을 알아채고 말했다. \"어머니께 넘겨드린 것이지요, 물론. 어머니께서 하고 싶으신 대로 하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 만약,\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 말했다. \"한창나이의 내 아들이 사업에 새로운 젊음과 힘을 불어넣어 크게 이로움과 권세를 누리게 해 주리라던 내 기대가 어그러진 데에서 나에게 어떤 기쁨이라도 솟아날 수 있다면, 그것은 오래되고 충실한 종을 올려 세우는 데 있을 것이네. 제레미아, 선장은 배를 버리지만, 자네와 나는 배와 함께 가라앉든 떠오르든 할 걸세.\"\n\n마치 돈이 보이기라도 하는 듯 눈을 번득이던 제레미아는, '나는 이 일로 자네에게 고마워할 게 없네. 자네는 이를 위해 한 일이 하나도 없으니까!' 하고 말하는 듯한 눈초리를 아들에게 슬쩍 던지고는, 어머니에게 자기가 감사하다고, 또 애프리도 감사해한다고, 자기는 결코 마님을 저버리지 않을 것이며 애프리도 결코 마님을 저버리지 않을 것이라고 말했다. 마침내 그는 깊숙한 곳에서 시계를 끌어올려 보고는 \"열한 시군요. 굴 드실 시간이오!\" 하고 말했고, 표정이나 태도의 변화는 조금도 없이 그렇게 화제를 바꾸며 종을 울렸다.\n\n그러나 클레넘 부인은, 보상이라는 것을 모르는 사람으로 여겨졌다 하여 한층 더 엄격하게 자신을 다루기로 작정하고, 굴이 들어왔을 때 그것을 먹기를 거부했다. 굴은 먹음직스러워 보였다. 흰 냅킨을 덮은 쟁반 위 흰 접시에 동그랗게 여덟 개가 차려져 있고, 그 옆에는 버터를 바른 프랑스식 둥근 빵 한 조각과 차가운 포도주에 물을 탄 자그마하고 야무진 잔이 곁들여져 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 온갖 권유를 다 뿌리치고 그것을 도로 내려보냈으니 — 틀림없이 그 행위를 자신의 영원한 장부에 자기 대변으로 기입하면서.\n\n이 굴 식사를 주관한 것은 애프리가 아니라 종이 울렸을 때 나타났던 처녀였으니, 간밤에 흐릿하게 불 밝힌 방에 있던 바로 그 처녀였다. 이제 그녀를 살펴볼 기회를 얻은 아서는, 그녀의 자그마한 몸집과 작은 이목구비, 그리고 가냘프고 빈약한 옷차림 때문에 그녀가 실제보다 훨씬 어려 보인다는 것을 알았다. 아마 스물두 살은 족히 되었을 여인이건만, 길에서 마주쳤다면 그 절반을 조금 넘는 나이로밖에 보이지 않았을 것이다. 그녀의 얼굴이 그리 앳된 것은 아니었으니, 사실 그 얼굴에는 그녀의 한껏 먹은 나이에 자연스레 어울리는 것보다 더 많은 사려와 근심이 어려 있었던 까닭이다. 그러나 그녀는 너무나 작고 가벼웠고, 너무나 소리 없고 수줍었으며, 세 명의 모진 어른들 틈에서 어울리지 못함을 너무나 의식하는 듯하여, 마치 주눅 든 아이의 태도를 모두, 그리고 그 겉모습의 많은 부분을 지니고 있었다.\n\n모질게, 그리고 후원과 억누름, 물뿌리개에서 흩뿌리는 것과 수압으로 짓누르는 것 사이를 오락가락하는 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로, 클레넘 부인은 이 의지가지없는 처녀에게 관심을 보였다. 종을 거칠게 울려 그녀가 들어선 그 순간, 어머니가 그 별난 동작으로 아들에게서 자신을 가렸을 때조차, 클레넘 부인의 두 눈에는 이 처녀에게만 따로 남겨 둔 듯한 어떤 개별적 알아봄이 담겨 있었다. 가장 단단한 금속에도 단단함의 정도가 있고, 검정 그 자체에도 빛깔의 명암이 있듯이, 다른 온 인류를 향한 클레넘 부인의 모진 태도와 리틀 도릿을 향한 태도 사이에도 미묘한 단계가 있었다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 바느질 일을 하러 다녔다. 하루에 얼마 — 아니, 얼마 안 되는 돈에 — 여덟 시부터 여덟 시까지, 리틀 도릿은 고용되었다. 시각에 꼭 맞추어 리틀 도릿이 나타났고, 시각에 꼭 맞추어 리틀 도릿이 사라졌다. 두 여덟 시 사이에 리틀 도릿이 어찌 되는지는 수수께끼였다.\n\n리틀 도릿에게 얽힌 도덕적 현상 중 또 하나가 있었다. 그녀의 품삯 돈 외에도, 그녀의 하루 계약에는 식사가 포함되어 있었다. 그녀는 남들과 함께 식사하는 것을 유난히 꺼려, 피할 수만 있으면 결코 그러려 하지 않았다. 늘 이쪽 일감을 먼저 시작해야 한다든가, 저쪽 일감을 먼저 끝내야 한다든가 하며 둘러댔고, 혼자 식사하려고 어김없이 궁리하고 꾀를 부렸다 — 그리 교묘하게는 아니었던 듯한데, 아무도 속이지 못했기 때문이다. 이 일에 성공하여, 어디로든 제 접시를 가져가 무릎이나 상자나 바닥을 식탁 삼아, 혹은 짐작하건대 발끝으로 서서 벽난로 선반에 기대어 알맞게 식사를 할 수 있으면, 리틀 도릿의 하루의 큰 근심거리는 가라앉았다.\n\n리틀 도릿의 얼굴을 알아보기란 쉽지 않았다. 그녀는 너무나 내성적이었고, 외진 구석에서 바늘을 놀렸으며, 계단에서 마주치기라도 하면 겁에 질려 화들짝 달아났던 까닭이다. 그러나 그것은 창백하고 투명한 얼굴로, 이목구비가 아름답지는 않았으나 표정이 풍부했고, 다만 그 부드러운 담갈색 눈만은 예외였다. 곱게 숙인 머리, 자그마한 몸매, 부지런히 움직이는 작고 날랜 두 손, 그리고 초라한 옷차림 — 그토록 단정한데도 그렇게 보이려면 정말이지 무척 초라하기 마련이었으리라 — 그것이 일감 앞에 앉아 있는 리틀 도릿이었다.\n\n리틀 도릿에 관한 이런 자질구레한 사실들이나 두루뭉술한 사정들을, 아서 씨는 그날 하루 동안 제 두 눈과 애프리 부인의 혀에 빚졌다. 만약 애프리 부인에게 제 나름의 뜻이나 방식이 있었더라면, 그것은 아마 리틀 도릿에게 호의적이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 '그 영리한 두 사람' — 애프리 부인이 늘 들먹이며, 그 안에 자신의 인격이 통째로 삼켜져 버린 그 두 사람 — 이 리틀 도릿을 당연한 것으로 받아들이기로 합의한 터라, 그녀로서는 따르는 것 말고는 도리가 없었다. 마찬가지로, 만약 그 영리한 두 사람이 촛불 아래 리틀 도릿을 죽이기로 합의했다면, 애프리 부인은 촛불을 들고 있으라는 요구를 받았을 때 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 것이다.\n\n환자 방에 올릴 자고새를 굽고 식당에 낼 쇠고기와 푸딩을 담은 구이 접시를 마련하는 틈틈이, 애프리 부인은 위에 적은 이야기들을 들려주었으니, 머리를 문밖으로 내밀었다가도 어김없이 다시 들이밀어 그 영리한 두 사람에게 저항할 것을 다그쳤다. 외아들을 그들과 맞붙여 세우는 것이 플린트윈치 부인에게는 완연한 열정이 되어 버린 듯했다.\n\n그날 하루 동안 아서는 집안 전체를 둘러보기도 했다. 그는 그곳이 음울하고 어둡다는 것을 알았다. 여러 해 동안 줄곧 방치된 휑한 방들은, 어떤 것으로도 다시는 깨울 수 없는 음울한 무기력에 빠져들어 가라앉아 버린 듯했다. 가구는 빈약하면서도 둔중하여, 방을 채운다기보다는 방 안에 숨어 있는 격이었고, 온 집 안에 빛깔이라곤 없었다. 일찍이 거기 있었던 빛깔이라곤 죄다 오래전 사라진 햇살을 따라 떠나가 — 어쩌면 꽃이며 나비며 새의 깃털이며 보석이며 무엇무엇 따위에 흡수되어 버린 것이었다. 토대에서 지붕까지 반듯한 마룻바닥이라곤 하나도 없었다. 천장은 연기와 먼지로 어찌나 기괴하게 얼룩져 있었던지, 늙은 여인네들이 찻잎 찌꺼기보다 거기서 점을 더 잘 쳤을 법했다. 죽은 듯 차가운 화덕은 굴뚝에서 굴러떨어져 문이 열릴 때마다 작은 거뭇한 회오리를 일으키며 휘도는 검댕 더미 말고는 일찍이 데워진 흔적이라곤 보이지 않았다. 한때 응접실이었던 곳에는 빈약한 거울 한 쌍이 있었는데, 그 액자 둘레로는 검은 화환을 든 검은 인물들의 음산한 행렬이 둘러 걸어가고 있었다. 그러나 이마저도 머리나 다리가 모자랐고, 장의사 같은 큐피드 하나는 제 축을 따라 빙 돌아 거꾸로 매달려 있었으며, 또 하나는 아예 떨어져 나가 버렸다. 아서 클레넘이 그를 처음 기억할 무렵 그의 작고한 아버지가 사업 용도로 쓰던 방은 어찌나 그대로였던지, 마치 그가 위층에서 제 방을 지키는 그의 살아 있는 미망인처럼, 여전히 보이지 않게 그 방을 지키고 있다고 상상할 만했고, 제레미아 플린트윈치가 여전히 둘 사이를 오가며 협상을 하는 것 같았다. 그의 초상은, 어둡고 음울하게, 벽 위에서 사뭇 말없이, 두 눈은 생명이 떠나갈 때 바라보던 것처럼 아들을 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그가 시도했던 그 일을 으스스하게 재촉하는 듯했다. 그러나 어머니 쪽에서 무엇이든 양보하리라는 희망은 이제 그에게 없었고, 그 밖에 자신의 불신을 가라앉힐 다른 방도에 대한 희망은 오래전에 단념해 버렸다. 침실들이 그러했듯 지하 창고에서도, 그가 잘 기억하는 옛 물건들이 세월과 부패로 변해 있었으나 여전히 제자리에 놓여 있었으니, 거미줄로 허옇게 센 빈 맥주통이며, 목구멍이 잔털과 곰팡이로 막힌 빈 포도주병에 이르기까지 그러했다. 그곳에는 또한 별난 술병 시렁들과 위층 마당에서 들어오는 희미하게 비껴든 빛줄기들 사이로, 낡은 장부들로 채워진 금고실이 있었으니, 그 장부들은 마치 죽은 듯 고요한 새벽녘에 옛 부기 담당자들이 밤마다 부활하여 꼬박꼬박 결산이라도 하는 양 케케묵고 썩은 냄새를 풍기고 있었다.\n\n구이 접시는 두 시에 식탁 한쪽 끝, 오그라든 식탁보 위에 참회하는 듯한 모양새로 차려졌고, 그는 거기서 새 동업자인 플린트윈치 씨와 함께 식사했다. 플린트윈치 씨는 그에게 어머니가 이제 평정을 되찾으셨으니, 아침에 있었던 일을 다시 들먹이실까 걱정하지 않아도 된다고 알려 주었다. \"그리고 아버지께 잘못을 뒤집어씌우지 마시오, 아서 씨.\" 제레미아가 덧붙였다. \"한 번 더 말하지만, 그러지 마시오! 자, 그 이야기는 이제 끝냈소이다.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 새 지위에 오른 것을 기리기라도 하듯 이미 자기만의 작은 사무실을 다시 정돈하고 먼지를 털어 내고 있었다. 그는 쇠고기로 배를 가득 채우고 칼날 옆면으로 구이 접시의 육즙을 모조리 핥아 올린 뒤 식기 세척실에서 약한 맥주통을 넉넉히 들이켜고는, 이 일을 다시 시작했다. 그렇게 기운을 차린 그가 셔츠 소매를 걷어붙이고 다시 일에 착수하자, 그가 일을 벌이는 모습을 지켜보던 아서 씨는, 아버지의 초상이나 아버지의 무덤이라도 이 노인만큼은 그에게 입을 열어 주리라는 것을 똑똑히 알았다.\n\n\"자, 애프리, 이 사람아.\" 그녀가 현관을 가로질러 가자 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"내가 아까 위에 올라가 봤더니 아서 씨 잠자리를 안 봐 놨더군. 좀 부지런히 움직여. 서둘러.\"\n\n그러나 아서 씨는 집이 너무나 휑하고 적막하다는 것을 알았고, 또 어머니의 적들을(어쩌면 자기 자신도 그 가운데 하나로) 현세의 흉측한 꼴과 영원한 파멸로 또다시 가차 없이 넘겨 버리는 일에 거들고 싶지 않았으므로, 짐을 맡겨 둔 커피점에서 묵겠다는 뜻을 알렸다. 플린트윈치 씨가 그를 쫓아 버린다는 생각을 반갑게 받아들였고, 그의 어머니도 절약과 관련된 것이 아닌 한 자기 방의 벽 안에 한정되지 않는 대부분의 살림 문제에는 무관심했으므로, 그는 새로운 노여움을 사지 않고 이 점을 쉽게 관철했다. 어머니와 플린트윈치 씨와 그가 함께 장부와 서류를 필요한 만큼 대조하는 데 바칠 매일의 업무 시간이 정해졌고, 그는 그토록 얼마 전에야 다시 찾은 집을 침울한 마음으로 떠났다.\n\n그런데 리틀 도릿은?\n\n업무 시간은, 클레넘이 산책으로 기운을 차리는 동안의 굴이며 자고새며 하는 환자식 섭생 시간을 빼고 나면, 약 보름 동안 열 시부터 여섯 시까지였다. 리틀 도릿은 때로는 바느질 일에 매달렸고, 때로는 그러지 않았으며, 때로는 보잘것없는 방문객으로 나타났으니 — 그가 도착하던 그날 그녀의 처지가 틀림없이 그러했을 것이다. 그가 그녀를 기다리고, 그녀를 보거나 못 보고, 그녀에 관해 이리저리 헤아리는 동안, 처음의 호기심은 날마다 커져 갔다. 그를 사로잡은 그 생각의 영향을 받아, 그는 심지어 그녀가 어떤 식으로든 그 일과 연관되어 있을 가능성을 혼자 따져 보는 버릇에까지 빠져들었다. 마침내 그는 리틀 도릿을 지켜보아 그녀의 내력을 좀 더 알아내기로 결심했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. The Father of the Marshalsea\n\n삼십 년 전, 서더크 자치구의 세인트 조지 교회에서 몇 집 못 미친 곳, 남쪽으로 가는 길의 왼편에 마셜시 감옥이 서 있었다. 그것은 그보다 여러 해 전부터 거기 서 있었고, 그 뒤로도 몇 해 더 그 자리에 남아 있었다. 그러나 지금은 사라졌고, 세상은 그것이 없다고 해서 조금도 더 나빠지지 않았다.\n\n그것은 길쭉한 직사각형의 막사형 건물 덩어리로, 등을 맞댄 누추한 집들로 칸막이가 되어 있어 뒷방이라곤 없었으며, 좁다란 포석 깔린 마당이 둘러싸고, 꼭대기에 빠짐없이 못이 박힌 높은 담장이 에워싸고 있었다. 그 자체가 채무자들을 가두는 답답하고 비좁은 감옥이었지만, 그 안에는 밀수꾼들을 가두는 한층 더 답답하고 더 비좁은 감방이 들어 있었다. 세입법을 어긴 자들과, 물품세나 관세를 체납하여 갚을 수 없는 벌금을 떠안은 자들은, 철판을 댄 문 하나로 막아 놓은 두 번째 감옥 안에 갇히게 되어 있었다. 그 두 번째 감옥은 튼튼한 독방 한둘과, 한 야드 반쯤 너비의 막다른 골목으로 이루어져 있었는데, 그 골목은 마셜시의 채무자들이 자기네 시름을 굴려 쓰러뜨리던 그 지극히 비좁은 볼링장의 신비로운 끝자락을 이루고 있었다.\n\n거기에 갇히게 되어 있었다고 한 것은, 세월이 그 튼튼한 독방과 막다른 골목을 이미 좀 넘어서 버렸기 때문이다. 이론상으로야 그것들은 여전히 예전 못지않게 멀쩡했지만, 실제로는 좀 지나치게 험하다고 여겨지게 되었다. 이는 오늘날에도 도무지 튼튼하지 않은 다른 독방들과, 돌처럼 캄캄하게 막혀 버린 다른 막다른 골목들에서 똑같이 볼 수 있는 일이다. 그리하여 밀수꾼들은 으레 채무자들과 어울려 지냈으며(채무자들은 그들을 두 팔 벌려 맞아 주었다), 다만 어느 관청에선가 누군가가 나와서, 자기도 다른 누구도 도무지 아무것도 알지 못하는 어떤 것을 감독한다는 어떤 형식을 갖추는 헌정적인 순간들만은 예외였다. 이 참으로 영국적인 행사 때면, 밀수꾼이 있을 경우, 그 누군가가 자기의 어떤 것을 하는 척하는 동안 그들은 튼튼한 독방과 막다른 골목으로 걸어 들어가는 시늉을 하고는, 그자가 그것을 다 하지 않자마자 다시 걸어 나오는 일은 실제로 해치웠다 — 우리의 정연하고 단단한 작은 섬에서 대다수 공무가 처리되는 방식을 깔끔하게 요약해 보여 주듯이.\n\n해가 마르세유와 이 이야기의 첫머리를 비추던 그날보다 훨씬 전에, 이 이야기와 다소 관련 있는 한 채무자가 마셜시 감옥에 잡혀 들어와 있었다.\n\n그는 그 무렵, 매우 상냥하고 매우 무력한 중년 신사로서, 곧장 다시 나갈 사람이었다. 당연히 그는 곧장 다시 나갈 사람이었으니, 마셜시의 자물쇠가 곧장 다시 나가지 않을 채무자를 향해 돌아간 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다. 그는 여행 가방 하나를 들고 들어왔는데, 그것을 풀어 놓을 만한 값어치가 있을지 의심스러워했다. 그는 — 나머지 모두와 똑같다고 자물쇠를 지키는 간수가 말했듯이 — 곧장 다시 나갈 것이라고 너무도 확신하고 있었다.\n\n그는 수줍고 내성적인 사내였다. 여성스러운 데가 있긴 했지만 잘생긴 편이었고, 부드러운 목소리에 곱슬머리, 그리고 우유부단한 두 손을 지녔는데 — 그 시절엔 손가락마다 반지를 끼고 있었다 — 그 손은 감옥과 첫 인연을 맺은 첫 삼십 분 동안 그의 떨리는 입술로 백 번도 넘게 초조하게 올라갔다. 그의 가장 큰 근심은 아내에 관한 것이었다.\n\n\"여보시오,\" 그가 간수에게 물었다. \"내 아내가 내일 아침 이 문 앞에 온다면, 몹시 충격을 받을 것 같소?\"\n\n간수는 자기 경험에 비추어 보면 어떤 이들은 그렇고 어떤 이들은 그렇지 않다고 대답했다. 대체로 그렇다는 쪽보다 아니라는 쪽이 더 많다고. \"그러니까 부인이 어떤 분이냐, 그게 문제죠.\" 그가 철학적으로 물었다. \"그게 다 거기 달린 거예요.\"\n\n\"내 아내는 정말이지 무척 연약하고 세상물정에 어둡소.\"\n\n\"그건,\" 간수가 말했다. \"부인한테 불리하군요.\"\n\n\"혼자 외출하는 데 워낙 익숙지 않은 사람이라,\" 채무자가 말했다. \"걸어서 온다면 도대체 어떻게 여기까지 길을 찾아올지 막막하오.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐,\" 간수가 말했다. \"삯마차를 타시겠죠.\"\n\n\"그럴지도.\" 우유부단한 손가락들이 떨리는 입술로 올라갔다. \"그러기를 바라오. 그 생각을 못 할지도 모르지만.\"\n\n\"아니면 뭐,\" 간수가, 자기가 가엾게 여기는 어떤 아이의 약함을 향해 일러 주듯, 닳고 닳은 나무 걸상 위에서 제안을 내놓았다. \"아니면 부인이 오빠나 언니라도 데리고 같이 오시겠죠.\"\n\n\"아내에겐 오빠도 언니도 없소.\"\n\n\"조카딸, 조카, 사촌, 하인, 젊은 여자, 채소 장수.— 에이! 그중에 누구 하나라도 있겠죠.\" 간수가 자기 제안이 죄다 거절당할 것을 미리 물리치며 말했다.\n\n\"아무래도— 부디 규칙에 어긋나지 않기를 바라오만— 아내가 아이들을 데려올 것 같소.\"\n\n\"아이들이요?\" 간수가 말했다. \"규칙이라뇨? 아니, 영감님을 코너 핀처럼 떡하니 세워 드릴까, 여긴 아예 어엿한 아이들 놀이터예요. 아이들이라뇨! 아이들이 우글우글하다니까요. 몇이나 두셨는데요?\"\n\n\"둘이오.\" 채무자가 다시 우유부단한 손을 입술로 들어 올리며 말하고는 감옥 안으로 돌아섰다.\n\n간수는 눈으로 그를 좇았다. \"거기에 당신 하나,\" 그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"해서 셋이라. 그리고 부인 하나 더, 일 크라운 걸지. 해서 넷. 그리고 또 하나 오는 중일 테고, 반 크라운 걸지. 해서 다섯. 그리고 칠 실링 육 펜스 더 걸어서 말해 보지, 누가 제일 무력한지를 — 아직 태어나지 않은 갓난애냐, 아니면 당신이냐!\"\n\n그는 모든 항목에서 옳았다. 이튿날 아내는 세 살배기 사내아이 하나와 두 살배기 여자아이 하나를 데리고 왔고, 그는 빈틈없이 입증된 채로 서 있었다.\n\n\"이제 방은 구했죠, 안 그래요?\" 한두 주가 지난 뒤 간수가 채무자에게 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 아주 좋은 방을 하나 얻었소.\"\n\n\"세간으로 들여놓을 자질구레한 살림은 좀 들어옵니까?\" 간수가 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 오후에 운송업자가 필요한 가구 몇 점을 배달해 주기로 했소.\"\n\n\"부인하고 어린것들도 함께 지내러 옵니까?\" 간수가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 몇 주만이라도 흩어져 지내지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어서.\"\n\n\"몇 주만이라도, 그야 당연하죠.\" 간수가 대답했다. 그리고 그가 가 버리자 다시 눈으로 그를 좇으며 일곱 번 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n이 채무자의 사정은 어떤 동업 관계 때문에 뒤엉켜 있었는데, 그는 거기에 돈을 댔다는 것밖에는 아는 게 없었다. 양도와 정산, 이쪽으로의 권리 이전과 저쪽으로의 권리 이전이라는 법률적인 일들, 이쪽 방향으로는 채권자를 부당하게 편애했다는 의혹, 저쪽 방향으로는 재산을 몰래 빼돌렸다는 의혹 따위로 얽혀 있었다. 그리고 이 세상 어느 누구도 채무자 본인보다 그 혼란의 더미 가운데 단 한 항목이라도 설명할 능력이 더 없을 수는 없었으므로, 그의 사건으로는 도무지 알아들을 만한 것을 만들어 낼 수가 없었다. 그를 붙들고 시시콜콜 캐묻고 그의 대답을 서로 맞춰 보려 하는 것, 파산과 도산의 술수에 밝은 회계사들과 약삭빠른 전문가들과 그를 한방에 가둬 두는 것은, 그저 그 사건을 복리로 불어나는 불가해함에 내맡기는 것일 뿐이었다. 그럴 때마다 우유부단한 손가락들은 떨리는 입술 언저리를 점점 더 부질없이 파닥거렸고, 가장 약삭빠른 전문가들마저 그를 가망 없는 일감으로 단념해 버렸다.\n\n\"나간다고요?\" 간수가 말했다. \"저 양반은 채권자들이 어깨를 붙잡아 떠밀어 내지 않는 한, 절대로 못 나가요.\"\n\n그가 거기 들어온 지 대여섯 달이 되었을 무렵, 어느 날 오전 그는 이 간수에게 숨을 헐떡이며 새파랗게 질려 달려와 아내가 아프다고 알렸다.\n\n\"누가 봐도 그럴 줄 알았죠.\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"우리는,\" 그가 대답했다. \"바로 내일 아내를 시골 하숙으로 보낼 작정이었소. 난 어쩌면 좋소! 아아, 맙소사, 난 어쩌면 좋단 말이오!\"\n\n\"두 손 비비고 손가락이나 깨물며 시간 낭비하지 말고,\" 실무적인 간수가 그의 팔꿈치를 붙들며 대꾸했다. \"나랑 같이 갑시다.\"\n\n간수는 그를 — 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고, 우유부단한 손가락으로 얼굴에 흐르는 눈물을 문질러 대며, 끊임없이 낮은 소리로 난 어쩌면 좋으냐고 흐느끼는 그를 — 감옥의 흔한 계단 하나를 따라 다락층의 어느 문 앞까지 데려갔다. 간수는 그 문을 열쇠 손잡이로 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어와!\" 안에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n간수가 문을 열자, 누추하고 악취 나는 작은 방 안에 쉰 목소리에 부석부석하고 벌건 얼굴을 한 두 인물이 흔들거리는 탁자에 마주 앉아 올포어즈 노름을 하며 파이프를 피우고 브랜디를 마시고 있는 것이 드러났다.\n\n\"선생,\" 간수가 말했다. \"여기 한 신사분의 부인이 일 분도 지체 없이 선생을 찾고 있소!\"\n\n의사의 친구는 쉰 목소리, 부석부석함, 벌건 얼굴, 올포어즈, 담배, 때, 브랜디에 있어 원급(原級)이었고, 의사는 비교급이었다 — 더 쉰 목소리, 더 부석부석함, 더 벌건 얼굴, 더 올포어즈에 절고, 더 담배에 절고, 더 때투성이에, 더 브랜디에 전. 의사는 놀랄 만큼 초라한 행색이었으니, 팔꿈치가 다 해지고 단추가 눈에 띄게 모자란, 찢어지고 기운 거친 날씨용 선원 외투를 걸치고, 인간으로서 상상할 수 있는 가장 더러운 흰 바지에, 양탄자 슬리퍼를 신고, 셔츠라곤 보이지 않았다(그는 한때 여객선에 실려 다니는 노련한 외과의였던 적이 있었다). \"산통이라?\" 의사가 말했다. \"그거라면 내가 적임자지!\" 그러면서 의사는 벽난로 선반에서 빗을 집어 머리카락을 빳빳이 곧추세웠는데 — 이것이 그가 몸을 씻는 방식인 듯했다 — 컵과 받침접시와 석탄을 넣어 둔 찬장에서 더없이 비참한 몰골의 직업용 상자, 즉 왕진 가방을 꺼내고, 목에 두른 후줄근한 천 속에 턱을 파묻고는, 끔찍한 의료용 허수아비가 되었다.\n\n의사와 채무자는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 간수를 자물쇠 자리로 돌려보내고는 채무자의 방으로 향했다. 감옥 안의 부인들은 죄다 그 소식을 입수하여 마당에 나와 있었다. 그중 일부는 이미 두 아이를 차지하여 살갑게 데려가는 중이었고, 다른 이들은 제 빈약한 살림에서 자그마한 위안거리를 빌려주겠노라 내놓고 있었으며, 또 다른 이들은 더없이 수다스럽게 동정을 표하고 있었다. 신사 죄수들은 자기들이 불리한 처지임을 느끼고 대개 자기 방으로 물러갔다고, 아니 슬그머니 빠져들었다고 해야겠는데, 그중 몇은 열린 창문에서 아래를 지나가는 의사에게 휘파람으로 인사를 보냈고, 다른 몇은 여러 층을 사이에 두고 지금 만연한 흥분에 대해 비꼬는 말들을 주고받았다.\n\n무더운 여름날이라, 감옥의 방들은 높은 담장 사이에서 푹푹 익고 있었다. 채무자의 비좁은 방에서는, 죄수는 아니지만(한때는 죄수였던 적이 있다) 바깥세상과 통하는 인기 있는 매개자였던 잡역부 겸 심부름꾼 뱅엄 부인이, 파리잡이 겸 만능 시중꾼으로 자청해 나서 있었다. 벽과 천장이 파리로 시커멨다. 뱅엄 부인은 임기응변에 능한지라, 한 손으로는 양배추 잎으로 산모에게 부채질을 하고, 다른 손으로는 약단지에 식초와 설탕으로 덫을 놓았다. 동시에 그 상황에 맞춰 격려와 축하의 정서를 담은 말들을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"파리가 성가시죠, 안 그래요, 아가씨?\" 뱅엄 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 어쩌면 그게 아가씨 정신을 딴 데로 돌려서 도움이 될지도 몰라요. 묘지에다, 식료품 가게에다, 짐마차 마구간에다, 내장 장사까지 있다 보니 마셜시 파리는 무척 크게 자란다니까요. 우리가 모르고 있을 뿐, 어쩌면 위안으로 보내진 건지도 모르죠. 지금은 좀 어때요, 아가씨? 나아지지 않았어요? 그래요, 아가씨, 그럴 리가 없죠. 나아지기 전에 더 나빠질 거예요, 그건 아가씨도 알죠, 안 그래요? 그럼요. 바로 그거예요! 자물쇠 안에서 어여쁜 어린 천사가 태어난다고 생각해 봐요! 아니, 그거 예쁘지 않아요, 그걸로 즐겁게 이 고비를 넘길 만하지 않아요? 아니, 여기서 이런 일이 일어난 게 말이죠, 아가씨, 내가 언제였는지 댈 수도 없을 만큼 오래됐다니까요. 게다가 울기까지 해요?\" 뱅엄 부인이 산모를 점점 더 다독이려고 말했다. \"아가씨가요! 이렇게 유명해지면서! 약단지에 파리가 쉰 마리씩 떨어지는 판에! 모든 게 이렇게 잘 풀리는데! 그리고 봐요, 저기,\" 문이 열리자 뱅엄 부인이 말했다. \"저기 아가씨의 다정한 신사 양반이 해기지 선생님하고 같이 오시잖아요! 자, 이제 정말로 우리가 다 갖춰진 것 같네요!\"\n\n의사는 산모에게 완벽하게 다 갖춰졌다는 느낌을 불어넣어 줄 만한 모습은 도무지 아니었다. 그러나 그가 곧 \"우리는 더할 나위 없이 괜찮습니다, 뱅엄 부인, 이건 불난 집처럼 단숨에 해치울 겁니다\"라는 의견을 내놓았고, 또 다른 누구나가, 아무나가 늘 그래 왔듯이 그와 뱅엄 부인이 그 가엾고 무력한 두 사람을 떠맡았으므로, 손에 닿는 수단은 전체적으로 보아 더 나은 것이 있었다 한들 그만큼은 충분히 괜찮았다. 해기지 선생이 이 사건을 다루는 데서 특별한 점은, 뱅엄 부인을 정신 똑바로 차리게 다잡으려는 그의 결의였다. 이런 식이었다.\n\n\"뱅엄 부인,\" 그가 거기 온 지 이십 분도 채 되지 않아 말했다. \"밖에 나가서 브랜디를 좀 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 쓰러지겠어.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 하지만 저 때문이라면 됐어요.\" 뱅엄 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"뱅엄 부인,\" 의사가 대꾸했다. \"나는 지금 이 부인을 직업적으로 보살피는 중이고, 당신 쪽에서 왈가왈부하는 건 허락하지 않겠어. 밖에 나가서 브랜디를 좀 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 무너질 게 내 눈엔 훤히 보이니까.\"\n\n\"분부대로 따라야죠, 선생님.\" 뱅엄 부인이 일어서며 말했다. \"선생님께서 직접 입에 대신다 해도 나쁠 건 없지 싶네요, 안색이 영 안 좋아 보이시는걸요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"뱅엄 부인,\" 의사가 대꾸했다. \"나는 당신이 상관할 바가 아니야, 고맙지만. 당신이 내가 상관할 바지. 제발 나는 신경 쓰지 마. 당신이 할 일은, 시키는 대로 하는 거고, 가서 내가 시킨 걸 가져오는 거야.\"\n\n뱅엄 부인은 굴복했다. 그리고 의사는 그녀에게 약을 먹인 뒤, 자기 몫도 들이켰다. 그는 뱅엄 부인에게 아주 단호한 태도로, 한 시간마다 그 처치를 되풀이했다. 서너 시간이 흘렀고, 파리는 수백 마리씩 덫에 떨어졌으며, 마침내 그것들의 목숨보다 별로 더 강하지도 않은 한 작은 생명이 그 숱한 작은 죽음들 한가운데에 나타났다.\n\n\"정말 아주 예쁜 여자아이로군요.\" 의사가 말했다. \"작긴 해도 잘생겼어요. 어어, 뱅엄 부인! 안색이 이상한데! 당장 나가요, 부인, 어서, 브랜디를 좀 더 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 히스테리를 일으키겠어.\"\n\n이 무렵이면 반지들이 겨울나무에서 잎이 지듯 채무자의 우유부단한 손에서 떨어지기 시작했다. 그가 짤랑거리는 무언가를 의사의 기름때 낀 손바닥에 쥐여 주던 그날 밤에는, 그 손에 반지가 단 하나도 남아 있지 않았다. 그동안 뱅엄 부인은 금빛 공 세 개로 장식된 이웃의 어느 가게로 심부름을 다녀왔는데, 거기서 그녀는 꽤나 잘 알려진 사람이었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 의사가 말했다. \"고마워요. 부인은 아주 평온해졌어요. 더없이 잘하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그 말을 들으니 무척 기쁘고 무척 감사하오.\" 채무자가 말했다. \"한때는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소만, 그러니까—\"\n\n\"이런 곳에서 아이가 태어나리라곤 말이지요?\" 의사가 말했다. \"쳇, 쳇, 여보시오, 그게 무슨 상관이오? 여기서 우리한테 필요한 건 좀 더 넉넉한 팔꿈치 공간뿐이오. 여긴 조용하지요. 여기선 들볶이지 않소. 채권자들이 두들겨 대서 사람 심장이 입 밖으로 튀어나오게 만드는 문고리 따위는 여기 없소, 여보시오. 사람이 집에 있는지 물으러 와서, 있을 때까지 문 앞 발판에 버티고 서 있겠다고 하는 자도 여긴 안 오지요. 이런 데로는 돈 갚으라는 협박 편지도 안 날아오고요. 이게 자유요, 여보시오, 이게 자유란 말이오! 나는 오늘 같은 진료를 집에서도, 외국에서도, 행군 중에도, 배 위에서도 해 봤지만, 한마디 하리다. 오늘 여기처럼 이토록 평온한 상황에서 진료를 해 본 적이 있었나 싶소. 다른 데선 사람들이 안절부절못하고, 시달리고, 정신없이 쫓기고, 이것 때문에 노심초사, 저것 때문에 노심초사하지요. 여긴 그런 게 전혀 없소, 여보시오. 우리는 그걸 다 겪었소 — 최악이 뭔지 우리는 알지요. 우리는 바닥까지 내려왔으니 더는 떨어질 수가 없소. 그래서 무엇을 찾았느냐? 평화요. 그게 바로 그 말이오. 평화.\" 이런 신앙 고백과 함께, 늙은 감옥 단골이자 여느 때보다 더 거나하게 취하고, 게다가 주머니에 든 돈이라는 색다른 자극까지 더해진 의사는, 쉰 목소리, 부석부석함, 벌건 얼굴, 올포어즈, 담배, 때, 그리고 브랜디에 있어 자기 동료이자 단짝에게로 돌아갔다.\n\n그런데 채무자는 의사와는 전혀 다른 사람이었지만, 그 역시 원의 반대편 호(弧)를 따라 같은 지점을 향해 이미 나아가기 시작한 터였다. 처음엔 수감 생활에 짓눌렸으나, 그는 곧 거기서 둔한 위안을 발견했다. 그는 자물쇠와 열쇠에 갇혀 있었지만, 그를 안에 가두는 그 자물쇠와 열쇠가 그의 수많은 골칫거리를 밖에 가둬 두었던 것이다. 그가 만약 그 골칫거리들을 마주하고 맞서 싸울 만한 굳센 의지를 지닌 사람이었다면, 자기를 옭아맨 그물을 찢어 버렸거나 아니면 가슴이 터져 버렸을 테지만, 그는 본디 그런 사람이었기에 이 매끄러운 내리막으로 맥없이 미끄러져 들어갔고, 다시는 위로 한 걸음도 내딛지 못했다.\n\n도무지 분명해질 줄 모르는 그 뒤엉킨 사건들이, 그 일의 처음도 중간도 끝도, 또 그라는 사람도 도무지 종잡을 수 없었던 열두 명 남짓의 대리인들 손을 줄줄이 거쳐 다시 그의 손에 떠넘겨짐으로써 그가 그 짐에서 벗어나자, 그는 자기의 그 비참한 도피처가 전보다 한결 더 고요한 도피처임을 알게 되었다. 그는 진작에 여행 가방을 풀어 놓았고, 위의 두 아이는 이제 마당에서 으레 뛰놀았으며, 누구나 그 갓난아이를 알았고 그 아이에게 일종의 소유권을 주장했다.\n\n\"아니, 내가 영감님이 자랑스러워지네요.\" 어느 날 그의 벗인 간수가 말했다. \"곧 가장 오래된 주민이 되시겠어요. 영감님과 식구들이 없으면 이제 마셜시는 마셜시 같지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n간수는 정말로 그를 자랑스러워했다. 그가 등을 돌리고 있을 때면 새로 들어온 자들에게 그를 칭찬조로 입에 올리곤 했다. \"저기 방금 수위실에서 나간 분, 눈여겨봤어요?\" 그가 묻곤 했다.\n\n새로 들어온 자는 십중팔구 그렇다고 대답하곤 했다.\n\n\"신사로 자라난 분이라니까요, 세상에 진짜 신사가 있다면 말이죠. 엄청난 돈을 들여 교육받았고요. 한번은 마셜이 새 피아노를 시험해 보라고 그분을 자기 집으로 들였지요. 듣자 하니 그걸 기막히게 치셨대요 — 아름답게! 언어로 말하자면 — 뭐든 다 하시지요. 한때 여기 프랑스 사람이 하나 있었는데, 내 보기엔 그분이 그 프랑스 사람보다 프랑스 말을 더 많이 알았어요. 한때 여기 이탈리아 사람도 하나 있었는데, 그분이 그자 입을 반 분 만에 막아 버리셨죠. 다른 자물쇠 뒤에도 별난 인물들이 있겠지요, 없다곤 안 합니다. 하지만 내가 말한 그런 점에서 으뜸가는 거물을 원한다면, 마셜시로 와야 한다니까요.\"\n\n그의 막내가 여덟 살이 되었을 때, 오래도록 시들시들 쇠약해져 가던 아내가 — 거처에 대한 민감함을 그보다 더 간직하고 있어서가 아니라, 본디 타고난 허약함 탓에 — 시골에 사는 가난한 친구이자 옛 유모를 찾아갔다가 거기서 죽었다. 그는 그 뒤로 두 주 동안 방에 틀어박혀 지냈다. 그리고 파산 법정 절차를 밟고 있던 어느 변호사 사무원이 그에게 보내는 조문 인사문을 정서해 주었는데, 그것은 마치 임대차 계약서처럼 보였으며, 죄수들이 모두 거기에 서명했다. 그가 다시 모습을 드러냈을 때 그는 더 백발이 되어 있었고(그는 일찌감치 머리가 세기 시작했다), 간수는 그가 처음 들어왔을 때 그러했듯 그의 손이 다시 자주 떨리는 입술로 올라가는 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 그는 한두 달 만에 꽤 잘 추슬렀고, 그동안 아이들은 여느 때처럼 으레 마당에서 뛰놀았지만 검은 상복 차림이었다.\n\n그러고 나서, 오랫동안 바깥세상과 통하는 인기 있는 매개자였던 뱅엄 부인이 쇠약해지기 시작하더니, 여느 때보다 자주 사 온 물건이 담긴 바구니를 엎질러 놓은 채 보도 위에 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 것이 발견되었고, 의뢰인들에게 거슬러 줄 돈이 구 펜스나 모자라곤 했다. 그의 아들이 뱅엄 부인을 대신하기 시작하여, 빈틈없는 솜씨로 심부름을 해내고, 감옥의 것은 감옥답게, 거리의 것은 거리답게 익혀 가게 되었다.\n\n세월이 흘러, 간수가 쇠해 가기 시작했다. 가슴이 부풀고, 다리가 약해지고, 숨이 가빠졌다. 닳고 닳은 그 나무 걸상이 \"이제 자기에겐 벅차다\"고 그는 푸념했다. 그는 방석을 깐 안락의자에 앉았고, 때로는 몇 분이고 내리 어찌나 쌕쌕거리는지 열쇠를 돌리지 못할 지경이었다. 이런 발작에 짓눌릴 때면 채무자가 그를 대신해 자주 열쇠를 돌려 주었다.\n\n\"당신하고 나는,\" 어느 눈 내리는 겨울밤, 환한 불을 피운 수위실에 사람들이 꽤 가득 들어차 있을 때 간수가 말했다. \"가장 오래된 주민이지요. 나도 당신보다 칠 년쯤밖에 더 먼저 들어오지 않았으니까요. 난 오래 못 갈 거요. 내가 이 자물쇠를 영영 떠나면, 당신이 마셜시의 아버지가 되는 거요.\"\n\n간수는 이튿날 이 세상의 자물쇠를 떠났다. 그의 말은 기억되고 되풀이되었다. 그리고 그 뒤로 대대로 — 마셜시의 한 세대는 석 달쯤으로 헤아릴 만했다 — 전해 내려온 전설이, 부드러운 태도에 백발을 지닌 그 초라한 늙은 채무자가 곧 마셜시의 아버지라는 것이었다.\n\n그리고 그는 그 칭호를 자랑스러워하게 되었다. 만약 어떤 사칭꾼이 나타나 그 칭호를 차지하려 들었다면, 그는 자기 권리를 빼앗으려는 그 시도에 분개하여 눈물을 흘렸을 것이다. 그에게는 자기가 거기 머문 햇수를 부풀리는 기색이 보이기 시작했다. 그가 셈하는 햇수에서 몇 해쯤은 빼야 한다는 것이 일반적인 통념이었다. 그는 허영심이 있다고, 덧없이 스쳐 가는 채무자 세대들은 말했다.\n\n새로 들어온 자들은 모두 그에게 소개되었다. 그는 이 의식의 시행에 깐깐했다. 익살꾼들은 과장된 격식과 예의를 갖춰 소개의 임무를 수행하곤 했지만, 그것이 지닌 엄숙함에 대한 그의 감각을 좀처럼 넘어서지는 못했다. 그는 자기의 누추한 방에서 그들을 맞았으며(그저 마당에서 소개받는 것은 격식에 어긋난다 하여 — 누구에게나 일어날 수 있는 일이라 하여 — 그는 싫어했다), 일종의 허리 굽힌 자비로움으로 그들을 대했다. 마셜시에 잘 오셨소, 하고 그는 그들에게 말하곤 했다. 그렇소, 자기가 이곳의 아버지요. 세상이 친절하게도 자기를 그렇게 불러 준다고. 그리고 이십 년이 넘는 거주가 그 칭호에 대한 자격을 준다면, 자기는 정말 그러하다고. 처음엔 보잘것없어 보이지만, 여기엔 — 잡다한 사람들 가운데 — 부득이 잡다할 수밖에 없지만 — 무척 좋은 친구들이 있고, 공기도 무척 좋다고.\n\n밤에 마셜시의 아버지 앞으로 반 크라운이나 두 닢의 반 크라운, 이따금 아주 오랜만에 반 소버린까지도 동봉한 편지가 그의 문 아래로 들이밀어지는 것은 드문 일이 아니게 되었다. \"작별을 고하는 한 동문(同門) 올림.\" 그는 이 선물들을, 한 공인(公人)에게 바치는 흠모자들의 헌사로 받아들였다. 때로 이 편지를 보내는 자들은 우스꽝스러운 이름을 댔으니, 벽돌, 풀무, 늙은 까치밥나무, 정신말짱, 스눅스, 대걸레, 잘라낸 옷자락, 개고기 장수 같은 식이었다. 그러나 그는 이것을 천박한 취향이라 여겨, 늘 그 때문에 조금 마음이 상하곤 했다.\n\n이윽고 때가 차서, 이 서신 왕래가 시들해질 기미를 보이고, 또 편지를 보내는 쪽에서 어떤 수고를 들여야 하는 듯한데 떠나는 다급한 처지에서는 많은 이들이 그만한 수고를 감당하지 못할 듯하자, 그는 어느 정도 연조가 있는 동문들을 문까지 배웅하며 거기서 작별하는 관례를 만들었다. 그 배웅을 받던 동문은 악수를 한 뒤 이따금 멈춰 서서 무언가를 종잇조각에 싸고는, \"이봐요!\" 하고 부르며 다시 돌아오곤 했다.\n\n그는 놀란 듯 둘러보곤 했다. \"나 말이오?\" 그는 미소 지으며 말하곤 했다.\n\n이때쯤이면 동문이 그에게 다가와 있었고, 그는 아버지답게 덧붙이곤 했다. \"무엇을 잊으셨소? 내가 뭘 해 드리면 되겠소?\"\n\n\"이걸 두고 가는 걸 깜빡했지 뭡니까,\" 동문은 으레 대답했다. \"마셜시의 아버지께 드리려고요.\"\n\n\"이런, 여보시오,\" 그는 받아 말하곤 했다. \"그분이 당신께 무한히 감사해하시오.\" 그러나 마지막까지도, 그 옛날의 우유부단한 손은, 그가 마당을 두세 바퀴 도는 동안 슬쩍 돈을 찔러 넣어 둔 주머니 속에 머물러 있곤 했으니, 그 거래가 동문들 전체에게 너무 눈에 띌까 봐서였다.\n\n어느 날 오후, 그는 마침 나가게 된 꽤 큰 무리의 동문들에게 이곳의 예우를 베풀고 있었는데, 돌아오던 길에 가난뱅이 쪽 출신의 한 사내와 마주쳤다. 그는 일주일 전 소액 채무로 집행을 당해 들어왔다가 그날 오후 중에 \"정산\"을 마치고 역시 나가는 참이었다. 그 사내는 작업복 차림의 그저 미장이일 뿐이었는데, 아내를 데리고 보따리를 들고서 잔뜩 들떠 있었다.\n\n\"신의 가호가 있기를, 선생.\" 그가 지나가며 말했다.\n\n\"당신에게도.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 자애롭게 답했다.\n\n각자 제 갈 길로 꽤 멀어졌을 때, 그 미장이가 \"이봐요!— 선생!\" 하고 외치며 그에게 되돌아왔다.\n\n\"많지는 않습니다.\" 미장이가 반 페니짜리 동전 한 무더기를 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"하지만 좋은 뜻으로 드리는 거예요.\"\n\n마셜시의 아버지는 여태껏 동전으로 헌사를 받아 본 적이 없었다. 그의 아이들은 자주 받았고, 그것은 그가 완전히 묵인하는 가운데 공동의 지갑으로 들어가 그가 먹은 고기와 그가 마신 술을 사는 데 쓰였다. 그러나 흰 석회가 튄 퍼스티언 작업복을 입은 자가 면전에서 그에게 반 페니 동전을 베푸는 것은 처음 있는 일이었다.\n\n\"감히 어떻게!\" 그가 그 사내에게 말하고는, 힘없이 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n미장이는 그의 얼굴이 보이지 않도록 그를 담 쪽으로 돌려세웠다. 그 동작이 어찌나 세심하고, 그 사내가 어찌나 후회에 사무쳐, 또 어찌나 진심으로 용서를 비는지, 그는 그에게 이렇게밖에는 달리 응수할 수 없었다. \"당신이 좋은 뜻으로 그런 줄 아오. 더는 말 마시오.\"\n\n\"아이고 선생, 정말이지,\" 미장이가 간곡히 말했다. \"진심이었어요. 제 생각엔 다른 사람들이 하는 것보다 제가 선생께 더 잘해 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"무엇을 해 주겠다는 거요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"나가고 나서, 선생을 보러 다시 오겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 돈을 다시 주시오.\" 상대가 간절히 말했다. \"내가 그걸 간직하고, 절대 쓰지 않겠소. 고맙소, 정말 고맙소! 당신을 다시 보게 되겠지요?\"\n\n\"제가 일주일을 더 산다면, 꼭 뵙겠습니다.\"\n\n그들은 악수를 하고 헤어졌다. 그날 밤 아늑한 휴게실에 좌담회랍시고 모인 동문들은, 자기네 아버지에게 무슨 일이 있었던 건지 의아해했다. 그가 마당의 그늘 속을 너무도 늦게까지 거닐었고, 너무도 의기소침해 보였기 때문이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 7. The Child of the Marshalsea\n\n해기지 의사의 브랜디가 섞인 첫 공기를 들이마셨던 그 갓난아기는, 마치 그들 공통의 어버이에 관한 전설처럼, 수용자 세대들 사이로 대대로 물려 내려갔다. 그녀가 살아온 더 이른 시기에는, 말 그대로 또 글자 그대로의 의미로 물려 내려갔다. 새로 들어온 수용자라면 누구나, 이 감옥에서 태어난 아이를 돌보는 일이 거의 입회 신고식의 일부처럼 되어 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"마땅히,\" 처음 그녀를 보여 주었을 때 간수가 말했다. \"내가 이 아이의 대부가 되어야겠는걸.\"\n\n채무자는 잠시 망설이며 그 일을 생각하더니 말했다. \"정말로 이 아이의 대부가 되어 주시는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠소?\"\n\n\"오! 나는 마다하지 않소,\" 간수가 대답했다. \"당신만 괜찮다면야.\"\n\n이리하여 어느 일요일 오후, 간수가 비번이어서 자물쇠를 떠나 있을 때, 그녀에게 세례가 베풀어졌다. 그리고 간수는 세인트 조지 교회의 세례반 앞으로 나아가, 본인이 돌아와서 말한 대로 \"아주 훌륭하게,\" 그녀를 대신하여 약속하고 맹세하고 단념을 다짐했다.\n\n이로써 간수는 이전의 공적인 몫 위에, 그 아이에 대한 새로운 소유의 몫을 하나 더 얻게 되었다. 그녀가 걷고 말하기 시작하자 그는 그녀를 귀여워하게 되었다. 작은 안락의자를 하나 사서 수위실 난로의 높은 불막이 가에 놓아 두었고, 자물쇠를 지킬 때 그녀가 곁에 있어 주는 것을 좋아했으며, 값싼 장난감으로 그녀를 꾀어 와서 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 아이 쪽에서도 곧 간수를 어찌나 따랐던지, 하루 중 어느 때고 제 발로 수위실 계단을 기어올라 오곤 했다. 그녀가 높은 불막이 가의 작은 안락의자에서 잠이 들면, 간수는 자기 손수건으로 그녀를 덮어 주었다. 그리고 그녀가 그 의자에 앉아, 머지않아 자물쇠 저편의 인형들과는 도무지 닮지 않게 되고 도리어 뱅엄 부인과 끔찍하게도 한 식구처럼 닮아 가던 인형을 입혔다 벗겼다 하고 있으면, 그는 자기 걸상 꼭대기에서 더없이 다정하게 그녀를 바라보곤 했다. 이런 모습들을 지켜본 수용자들은, 독신인 그 간수가 천성적으로 가정을 꾸릴 사람으로 태어났다는 의견을 내놓곤 했다. 그러나 간수는 그들에게 고맙다고 하면서도 이렇게 말했다. \"아니오, 대체로 여기서 남의 아이들을 보는 것으로 충분합니다.\"\n\n그 어린것이, 꼭대기에 뾰족한 쇠창살이 달린 높은 담으로 둘러싸인 좁은 마당에 갇혀 사는 것이 세상 모든 사람의 습관은 아니라는 것을 깨닫기 시작한 것이 어린 시절의 어느 시기였는지를 가려내기란 어려운 문제일 것이다. 그러나 아버지의 손을 잡은 자기 손이, 그 커다란 열쇠가 여는 문 앞에서 언제나 풀려야 한다는 것을, 그리고 자기 가벼운 발걸음은 그 너머로 자유로이 지날 수 있어도 그의 발은 결코 그 선을 넘어서는 안 된다는 것을 어쩐 일인지 알게 되었을 때, 그녀는 정말로 정말로 아주 어린것이었다. 아직 몹시 어렸을 적부터 아버지를 바라보기 시작한 그 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛은, 아마도 이 깨달음의 한 부분이었으리라.\n\n실로 모든 것을 향해 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛을 띠면서도, 오직 그를 향해서만은 무언가 지켜 주려는 듯한 것을 그 눈빛에 담은 채, 이 마셜시의 아이이자 마셜시의 아버지의 아이는, 생애 첫 여덟 해 동안 수위실에서 친구인 간수의 곁에 앉아 있거나, 식구들의 방을 지키거나, 감옥 마당을 이리저리 거닐었다. 제멋대로인 언니를 향해, 게으른 오빠를 향해, 텅 빈 높은 담을 향해, 그 담이 가둬 둔 빛바랜 무리를 향해, 그리고 함성을 지르며 뛰어다니고 숨바꼭질을 하며 안쪽 문의 쇠창살을 '집'으로 삼고 놀던 감옥 아이들의 놀이를 향해, 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛을 띤 채.\n\n여름철이면 그녀는 무언가를 그리워하고 의아해하면서 수위실의 높은 불막이 가에 앉아, 창살 박힌 창문을 통해 하늘을 올려다보곤 했다. 그러다 눈길을 돌리면 그녀와 친구 사이로 빛의 창살들이 떠올라, 그녀는 친구마저도 격자 너머로 보게 되곤 했다.\n\n\"들판을 생각하고 있구나,\" 한번은 그녀를 지켜보던 간수가 말했다. \"그렇지 않니?\"\n\n\"그건 어디 있어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그게 말이다, 저쪽에 있단다, 얘야,\" 간수가 열쇠를 모호하게 한번 휘저으며 말했다. \"바로 저기쯤.\"\n\n\"누가 그걸 열고 닫나요? 잠가져 있어요?\"\n\n간수는 당황했다. \"글쎄,\" 그가 말했다. \"대개는 그렇지 않지.\"\n\n\"아주 예뻐요, 밥?\" 그녀는 그의 각별한 요청과 가르침에 따라 그를 밥이라고 불렀다.\n\n\"근사하지. 꽃이 가득하단다. 미나리아재비도 있고, 데이지도 있고, 또—\" 간수는 꽃 이름이 달려서 머뭇거렸다. \"민들레도 있고, 온갖 놀이가 다 있단다.\"\n\n\"거기 있으면 아주 즐거워요, 밥?\"\n\n\"최고지,\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"아버지도 거기 가 보신 적 있어요?\"\n\n\"흠!\" 간수가 헛기침을 했다. \"오 그럼, 가 보셨지, 가끔은.\"\n\n\"지금 거기 못 계셔서 서운해하세요?\"\n\n\"그—그렇게 특별히는 아니란다,\" 간수가 말했다.\n\n\"다른 사람들도요?\" 그녀가 안에 있는 맥없는 무리를 흘끔 보며 물었다. \"오 정말 확실해요, 밥?\"\n\n대화가 이 곤란한 대목에 이르자 밥은 두 손 들고, 화제를 딱딱한 캐러멜 과자로 돌렸다. 어린 친구가 그를 정치적, 사회적, 또는 신학적 궁지로 몰아넣을 때면 늘 마지막으로 의지하던 수단이었다. 그러나 이것이 이 묘한 두 길동무가 함께한 일련의 일요일 나들이의 발단이 되었다. 그들은 격주로 돌아오는 일요일 오후마다 아주 엄숙하게 수위실을 나서서, 간수가 한 주 동안 공들여 골라 둔 어느 풀밭이나 푸른 오솔길을 향했다. 거기서 그녀는 집에 가져갈 풀과 꽃을 뜯었고, 그동안 그는 파이프를 피웠다. 그러고 나면 찻집 정원이며 새우며 맥주며 그 밖의 별미들이 있었다. 그런 다음 그들은 손을 맞잡고 돌아왔는데, 그녀가 평소보다 더 지쳐서 그의 어깨에 기대 잠이 들었을 때만은 예외였다.\n\n그 무렵 간수는 처음으로 한 가지 문제를 깊이 궁리하기 시작했는데, 그것이 어찌나 많은 정신적 노고를 들이게 했던지 죽는 날까지도 결말을 못 본 채 남았다. 그는 자신의 작은 저축 재산을 대녀에게 물려주기로 마음먹었는데, 어떻게 하면 그것을 그녀만이 그 혜택을 누리도록 '묶어 둘' 수 있느냐 하는 점이 문제로 떠올랐다. 자물쇠를 지킨 경험 덕분에 그는, 돈을 조금이라도 단단히 '묶어 두기'란 엄청나게 어렵다는 것, 그리고 반대로 돈이 풀려나기란 놀랄 만큼 쉽다는 것을 예리하게 깨닫고 있었던 터라, 여러 해에 걸쳐 드나드는 새 파산 대리인이며 그 밖의 전문직 신사들마다 붙들고 이 까다로운 문제를 으레 들이밀곤 했다.\n\n\"가령,\" 그는 전문직 신사의 조끼에 열쇠를 들이대고 사례를 진술하며 말하곤 했다. \"가령 어떤 사람이 자기 재산을 젊은 여자에게 물려주고 싶은데, 다른 누구도 그것에 손을 못 대게끔 묶어 두고 싶다 합시다. 그 재산을 어떻게 묶어 두겠소?\"\n\n\"엄격하게 그 여자 본인에게 한정하여 설정하면 됩니다,\" 전문직 신사는 흡족하게 대답하곤 했다.\n\n\"하지만 들어 보시오,\" 간수가 말하곤 했다. \"가령 그 여자에게, 이를테면 오빠가 있고, 이를테면 아버지가 있고, 이를테면 남편이 있어서, 그녀가 그 재산을 물려받았을 때 그것에 손을 대려 들 법하다면—그건 어떻게 되오?\"\n\n\"그 재산은 그녀 본인에게 한정 설정되어 있으니, 그들에게는 당신만큼이나 법적 청구권이 없습니다,\" 하는 것이 전문가의 대답이었다.\n\n\"잠깐 기다려 보시오,\" 간수가 말했다. \"가령 그 여자가 마음이 여려서, 그들이 그녀를 구슬려 넘겼다 칩시다. 그렇다면 그것을 묶어 둘 법이 어디 있소?\"\n\n간수가 떠본 가장 깊이 있는 인물조차도, 그런 매듭을 묶어 둘 법을 내놓지 못했다. 그래서 간수는 평생 그것을 궁리했으나, 결국 유언장도 없이 죽고 말았다.\n\n그러나 그것은 한참 뒤의 일로, 그의 대녀가 열여섯을 넘겼을 때였다. 그녀의 그 생애 기간 가운데 앞쪽 절반이 막 채워졌을 무렵, 그녀의 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛은 아버지가 홀아비가 된 것을 보았다. 그때부터 그녀의 의아해하던 눈빛이 그를 향해 드러내던 그 보호의 마음은 행동으로 구체화되었고, 마셜시의 아이는 아버지를 향한 새로운 관계를 떠맡았다.\n\n처음에는 그런 갓난것이 할 수 있는 일이란, 높은 불막이 가의 더 활기찬 자기 자리를 버리고 그의 곁에 앉아 조용히 그를 지켜보는 것이 고작이었다. 그러나 이것이 그에게 그녀를 어느 정도 필요한 존재로 만들어, 그는 그녀에게 익숙해졌고 그녀가 곁에 없으면 허전함을 느끼기 시작했다. 이 작은 문을 지나, 그녀는 어린 시절에서 빠져나와 근심으로 가득한 세상으로 들어섰다.\n\n그 이른 시기에 그녀의 가엾은 눈빛이 아버지에게서, 언니에게서, 오빠에게서, 감옥에서 무엇을 보았는지, 그 비참한 진실의 얼마만큼을, 혹은 얼마나 적은 만큼을 신께서 그녀에게 보이도록 허락하셨는지는 숱한 신비와 더불어 감추어져 있다. 다만 그녀가 다른 사람들과는 같지 않은 무언가가 되도록, 그리고 그 다른 사람들을 위하여 남다르고 고된 그 무언가가 되도록 영감을 받았다는 것으로 충분하다. 영감이라고? 그렇다. 시인이나 사제의 영감을 두고는 말하면서, 사랑과 자기희생에 떠밀려 더없이 비천한 삶의 방식으로 더없이 비천한 일을 떠맡은 그 마음의 영감은 말하지 않을 셈인가!\n\n그녀를 도와주기는커녕 들여다봐 주기라도 할 세상의 벗이라곤 그토록 기묘하게 어울린 단 한 사람뿐이었고, 감옥에 갇히지 않은 자유로운 공동체의 보통 사람들이 지닌 일상의 평범한 말투와 습관조차 알지 못했으며, 담 바깥의 가장 거짓된 처지에 견주어 보아도 거짓된 사회적 조건에서 태어나 자랐고, 갓난아기 때부터 그 나름의 독특한 얼룩과 그 나름의 해롭고 부자연스러운 맛을 지닌 우물물을 마셔 온 채로, 마셜시의 아이는 여인으로서의 삶을 시작했다.\n\n어떤 실수와 좌절을 거쳤든, 그녀의 어림과 작은 몸집에 쏟아진 어떤 비웃음(악의는 아니었으나 뼈저리게 느껴진)을 겪었든, 물건을 들어 올리고 나르는 일에서조차 자신이 아직 어리고 힘이 모자란다는 어떤 겸허한 자각을 안았든, 또 얼마나 많은 피로와 절망을, 얼마나 많은 남모를 눈물을 거쳤든 간에, 그녀는 쓸모 있는 사람으로, 나아가 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 인정받기까지 묵묵히 일했다. 그때가 왔다. 그녀는 서열만 빼고 모든 면에서 세 남매 중 맏이의 자리를 차지했다. 몰락한 집안의 가장이 되었으며, 그 집안의 근심과 수치를 제 가슴속에 떠안았다.\n\n열세 살에 그녀는 글을 읽고 셈을 할 줄 알았다. 다시 말해, 그들이 필요로 하는 최소한의 필수품이 얼마나 들지를, 또 그것을 사들일 돈이 얼마나 모자라는지를 말과 숫자로 적어 둘 줄 알았다. 그녀는 서너 해에 걸쳐, 한 번에 몇 주씩 띄엄띄엄 바깥의 야간 학교에 다녔고, 언니와 오빠도 이따금씩 단속적으로 주간 학교에 보내졌다. 집에서는 그들 가운데 누구에게도 가르침이 없었다. 그러나 그녀는—누구보다도 잘—마셜시의 아버지가 될 만큼 망가진 사람은 제 자식들에게도 아버지 노릇을 할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n이 빈약한 향상의 수단에다, 그녀는 제 나름으로 궁리해 낸 또 하나를 보탰다. 한번은 그 잡다한 수감자 무리 가운데 무용 선생이 한 사람 나타났다. 언니는 무용 선생의 기예를 배우고 싶어 몹시 갈망했고, 그쪽으로 소질이 있는 듯도 했다. 열세 살의 마셜시의 아이는 손에 작은 주머니를 들고 무용 선생 앞에 나서서, 자신의 겸손한 청을 올렸다.\n\n\"실례지만, 저는 여기서 태어났어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"오! 그 아가씨로구나, 그렇지?\" 무용 선생이 그 자그마한 몸집과 치켜든 얼굴을 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그래 내가 너를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 수 있겠니?\" 무용 선생이 말했다.\n\n\"저를 위해서는 아무것도 없어요, 선생님, 고맙습니다,\" 그녀가 작은 주머니의 끈을 초조하게 풀며 말했다. \"다만 선생님이 여기 머무시는 동안, 부디 제 언니를 싸게 가르쳐 주실 수 있다면—\"\n\n\"얘야, 거저 가르쳐 주마,\" 무용 선생이 주머니를 닫으며 말했다. 그는 일찍이 파산 법정에 맞춰 춤춘 그 누구 못지않게 마음씨 고운 무용 선생이었고, 약속을 지켰다. 언니는 어찌나 영특한 제자였던지, 또 무용 선생에게는 그녀에게 베풀 한가한 시간이 어찌나 넘쳐났던지(채권자들을 향해 자세를 잡고, 선창으로 이끌고, 위원들을 돌고, 좌우로 다시 제 직업으로 돌아오기까지 무려 열 주가 걸렸으니), 놀라운 진척이 있었다. 실로 무용 선생은 그것이 어찌나 자랑스럽고, 떠나기 전에 수용자들 가운데 선택받은 몇몇 벗에게 그것을 선보이고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 어느 화창한 아침 여섯 시에 마당에서 궁정식 미뉴에트가 펼쳐졌다—감옥의 방들은 그 용도에 비해 너무 비좁았던 것이다—거기서 어찌나 넓은 자리를 누비고 스텝을 어찌나 양심적으로 밟아 냈던지, 곁들여 키트까지 켜야 했던 무용 선생은 완전히 숨이 차 버렸다.\n\n이 시작의 성공은—이로 인해 무용 선생은 석방된 뒤에도 가르침을 이어 갔다—이 가엾은 아이를 대담하게 만들어 다시 한번 시도하게 했다. 그녀는 여러 달 동안 삯바느질꾼을 살피며 기다렸다. 때가 무르익자 모자 만드는 여인이 들어왔고, 그녀는 자신을 위하여 그 여인을 찾아갔다.\n\n\"실례합니다, 부인,\" 그녀는 침대에 누워 눈물짓고 있는 모자 만드는 여인의 방 문간을 머뭇머뭇 들여다보며 말했다. \"저는 여기서 태어났어요.\"\n\n다들 들어오자마자 곧 그녀에 관한 이야기를 듣는 모양이었다. 모자 만드는 여인은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 앉아 눈물을 닦으며, 무용 선생이 했던 그대로 말했던 것이다.\n\n\"오! 네가 그 아이로구나, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"네, 부인.\"\n\n\"미안하지만 네게 줄 게 아무것도 없구나,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 아니에요, 부인. 부디 저는 바느질을 배우고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"왜 그런 걸 배우려는 거니,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 되물었다. \"내 꼴을 눈앞에 두고서? 나한테는 별 도움이 안 되었는데.\"\n\n\"여기 들어오는 누구에게도—그게 무엇이든—별 도움이 된 것은 없는 것 같아요,\" 그녀가 더없이 순진하게 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 그래도 배우고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"네가 너무 약한 게 걱정이구나, 알다시피,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 난색을 표했다.\n\n\"제가 약하다고는 생각하지 않아요, 부인.\"\n\n\"그리고 네가 너무, 너무 작잖니, 알다시피,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 난색을 표했다.\n\n\"네, 제가 정말 아주 작긴 한 것 같아요,\" 마셜시의 아이가 대답했다. 그러고는 늘 그렇게 자기 앞을 가로막던 그 딱한 결함을 두고 흐느끼기 시작했다. 모자 만드는 여인은—성미가 무뚝뚝하거나 모진 사람이 아니라, 그저 갓 파산한 처지였을 뿐이라—마음이 움직여, 호의를 가지고 그녀를 맡았고, 그녀가 더없이 참을성 있고 진지한 제자임을 알게 되었으며, 머지않아 그녀를 솜씨 좋은 바느질꾼으로 길러 냈다.\n\n세월이 흐르는 동안, 바로 그 똑같은 세월이 흐르는 동안, 마셜시의 아버지는 차츰 새로운 성격의 꽃을 피워 냈다. 그가 마셜시에 대해 점점 더 아버지다워지고, 형편이 바뀌는 식구들의 갹출에 점점 더 기대게 될수록, 그는 자기의 처량한 신사 행세를 더욱 떠받들었다. 반 시간 전에 어느 수용자의 반 크라운을 호주머니에 챙겨 넣은 바로 그 손으로, 그는 누가 자기 딸들이 빵을 벌어 온다는 이야기를 꺼내기라도 하면 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 눈물을 닦아 내곤 했다. 그리하여 다른 나날의 근심들에 더하여, 마셜시의 아이는 그들 모두가 다 같이 한가로운 거지일 뿐이라는 그 점잖은 허구를 지켜 내는 짐을 늘 짊어지고 있었다.\n\n언니는 무용수가 되었다. 가족 무리 가운데 몰락한 삼촌이 한 사람 있었는데—그를 몰락시킨 사람은 그의 형, 곧 마셜시의 아버지였고, 그 자신은 몰락시킨 형 못지않게 그 까닭을 알지 못했으나, 그 사실을 어쩔 수 없는 필연으로 받아들이고 있었다—그를 보살피는 일이 그녀에게 떨어졌다. 본디 은둔적이고 단순한 사람이라, 그 재앙이 닥쳤을 때도 그는 자기가 몰락했다는 것을 별달리 의식하는 기색을 보이지 않았다. 다만 그 충격이 알려졌을 때 세수를 그만두었고, 다시는 그 호사를 부리지 않았을 뿐이다. 형편이 나았던 시절에 그는 아주 시원찮은 음악 애호가였는데, 형과 함께 몰락하자 어느 작은 극장 관현악단에서 제 몸뚱이만큼이나 더러운 클라리넷을 부는 것으로 생계를 의지했다. 그 극장은 그의 조카가 무용수가 된 바로 그 극장이었다. 조카가 그곳의 초라한 자리에 들어갔을 때 그는 이미 오래도록 붙박이로 있던 터였고, 그는 조카의 동행이자 보호자로 봉사하는 일을 받아들였다. 마치 병이든, 유산이든, 잔치든, 굶주림이든—비누만 아니라면 무엇이든—받아들였을 그 모양 그대로.\n\n이 소녀가 한 주에 몇 실링을 벌게 해 주려면, 마셜시의 아이는 아버지를 상대로 공들인 형식을 한바탕 치러야 했다.\n\n\"패니가 지금 당장은 우리와 함께 살지 않을 거예요, 아버지. 낮에는 여기 많이 와 있겠지만, 삼촌과 함께 바깥에서 지낼 거예요.\"\n\n\"놀랍구나. 왜?\"\n\n\"삼촌에게 말동무가 필요하신 것 같아요, 아버지. 누가 돌봐 드리고 보살펴 드려야 해요.\"\n\n\"말동무라고? 그 사람은 여기서 많은 시간을 보내지 않니. 그리고 삼촌을 돌보고 보살피는 건, 에이미, 너야말로 네 언니가 앞으로 할 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 하고 있지 않니. 너희는 다들 그렇게 자주 나가는구나. 다들 그렇게 자주 나가.\"\n\n이것은 에이미 자신이 날마다 일하러 나간다는 것을 자기는 전혀 모른다는 듯한 그 예법과 가식을 지켜 내기 위한 것이었다.\n\n\"하지만 우리는 늘 집에 돌아오는 게 기뻐요, 아버지. 안 그래요? 그리고 패니로 말하자면, 삼촌과 벗해 드리고 돌봐 드리는 일 말고도, 언니가 늘 여기서만 사는 게 아닌 편이 나을지도 몰라요. 아시다시피 언니는 저처럼 여기서 태어난 게 아니잖아요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"그래, 에이미, 그래. 네 말을 다 알아듣지는 못하겠다만, 패니가 바깥에 있기를 더 좋아하는 것도, 심지어 너까지 자주 그러는 것도 자연스러운 일이겠지. 그러니, 얘야, 너랑 패니랑 네 삼촌은 너희 좋을 대로 하려무나. 좋아, 좋아. 나는 참견하지 않으마. 나는 신경 쓰지 마라.\"\n\n오빠를 감옥에서 빼내는 일—심부름을 맡아 하는 일에서 뱅엄 부인의 뒤를 잇게 되는 데서, 또 그 두 가지로 인해 매우 미심쩍은 패거리와 은어를 주고받게 되는 데서 그를 빼내는 일—이 그녀의 가장 힘든 과제였다. 열여덟에 그는 그날그날, 한 시간 한 시간, 한 푼 한 푼으로 여든까지 질질 끌어갈 판이었다. 그에게 쓸모 있거나 좋은 것을 가져다줄 사람이라곤 감옥에 들어오지 않았고, 그녀가 그를 위해 찾아낼 수 있는 후원자라곤 옛 친구이자 대부뿐이었다.\n\n\"밥 아저씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"가엾은 팁은 어떻게 되는 거예요?\" 그의 이름은 에드워드였는데, 담장 안에서 테드가 팁으로 바뀌어 있었다.\n\n간수는 가엾은 팁이 어떻게 될지에 대해 강한 개인적 견해를 품고 있었고, 그 결말을 막으려는 뜻에서 팁에게 도망쳐 나라에 봉사하러 가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 떠보기까지 했다. 그러나 팁은 고맙다고 하면서도, 자기는 나라 따위엔 별 관심이 없는 것 같다고 했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야,\" 간수가 말했다. \"저 녀석에게 뭔가 해 주긴 해야겠구나. 내가 저 녀석을 법률 쪽에 넣어 보면 어떻겠니?\"\n\n\"그래 주시면 정말 고마운 일일 거예요, 밥!\"\n\n이제 간수는 드나드는 전문직 신사들에게 들이밀 문제를 두 가지나 갖게 되었다. 그가 이 두 번째 문제를 어찌나 끈질기게 들이밀었던지, 마침내 팰리스 코트라 불리는 어느 위대한 국가의 보루에 있는 어느 변호사의 사무실에 팁을 위한 걸상 하나와 주급 십이 실링이 마련되었다. 그곳은 당시 알비온의 존엄과 안전을 위한 영원한 방벽들의 적잖은 목록 가운데 하나였으나, 이제는 그것들이 있던 자리조차 그것들을 알아보지 못한다.\n\n팁은 클리퍼드 인에서 여섯 달을 빈둥거렸고, 그 기한이 끝나자 어느 날 저녁 호주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아와서는, 다시는 거기로 돌아가지 않겠노라고 누이에게 지나가는 말로 일러 주었다.\n\n\"다시는 돌아가지 않겠다고?\" 가엾고 자그마하며 늘 노심초사하는 마셜시의 아이가, 자기가 떠맡은 짐들 가운데 으뜸 자리에 두고 언제나 팁을 위해 셈하고 궁리하던 그 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"하도 진력이 나서,\" 팁이 말했다. \"때려치웠어.\"\n\n팁은 무엇에든 진력을 냈다. 마셜시에서 빈둥대는 막간과 뱅엄 부인 자리를 잇는 막간을 사이사이 끼우면서, 그의 자그마한 둘째 어머니는 믿음직한 친구의 도움을 받아 그를 어느 창고에, 어느 채소 농장에, 호프 거래소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 경매소에, 어느 양조장에, 어느 증권 중개소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 마차 사무소에, 어느 짐마차 사무소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 잡화상에, 어느 증류소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 양모 상점에, 어느 포목점에, 빌링스게이트 장사에, 외국 과일 장사에, 그리고 부두에 넣어 주었다. 그러나 팁은 무엇에 들어가든 진력이 난 채로 나왔고, 때려치웠노라고 알렸다. 어디로 가든 이 천생 운 나쁜 팁은 감옥의 담을 함께 짊어지고 가서 그 장사나 직업 안에 그것을 세워 두는 듯했고, 그 좁은 한계 안을 예의 그 칠칠치 못하고 목적 없으며 뒤축 닳은 걸음걸이로 어슬렁거리다가, 이윽고 진짜로 꿈쩍 않는 마셜시의 담이 그를 끌어당기는 매력을 발휘하여 그를 도로 데려오곤 했다.\n\n그럼에도 그 용감한 작은 것은 오빠를 구해 내는 데 어찌나 마음을 쏟았던지, 그가 이 구슬픈 변주를 울려 대는 동안에도, 그녀는 그를 캐나다로 보낼 만큼의 돈을 한 푼 한 푼 아끼고 긁어모았다. 할 일이 없는 데 진력이 나서 이번에는 그것마저 때려치울 마음이 들자, 그는 너그러이 캐나다로 가는 데 동의했다. 그를 떠나보내는 것에 그녀의 가슴은 슬픔으로 차올랐으나, 그가 마침내 곧은 길에 들어서리라는 희망에 기쁨도 있었다.\n\n\"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 사랑하는 팁. 출세하거든 너무 우쭐대지 말고 우리를 보러 와.\"\n\n\"알았어!\" 팁은 그렇게 말하고 떠났다.\n\n그러나 캐나다까지 가지는 않았다. 사실 리버풀보다 더 멀리는 가지 못했다. 런던에서 그 항구까지 항해를 마치고 나니, 그는 배를 때려치우고 싶은 마음에 어찌나 강하게 떠밀렸던지, 도로 걸어서 돌아오기로 결심했다. 그 결심을 실행에 옮겨, 그는 한 달이 지나서 누더기를 걸치고 신발도 없이, 그 어느 때보다 훨씬 더 지친 모습으로 그녀 앞에 나타났다.\n\n마침내, 또 한 차례 뱅엄 부인의 뒤를 잇는 막간을 보낸 뒤, 그는 스스로 일거리를 하나 찾아내고는 그것을 알렸다.\n\n\"에이미, 나 일자리를 얻었어.\"\n\n\"정말로, 참말로요, 팁?\"\n\n\"틀림없어. 이제 잘 풀릴 거야. 더는 나 때문에 걱정스러운 얼굴 할 필요 없어, 누이야.\"\n\n\"그게 뭔데요, 팁?\"\n\n\"왜, 슬링고를 얼굴은 알지?\"\n\n\"거래꾼이라 부르는 그 사람 말고요?\"\n\n\"바로 그 친구야. 월요일에 나가는데, 나한테 자리를 하나 줄 거야.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 뭘 거래하는데요, 팁?\"\n\n\"말. 틀림없어! 이제 잘 풀릴 거야, 에이미.\"\n\n그 후 여러 달 동안 그녀는 그의 소식을 잃었고, 단 한 번 그에게서 연락을 받았을 뿐이다. 무어필즈의 어느 가짜 경매장에서 그가 도금된 물건을 묵직한 은제품이라며 사들이는 척하고, 더없이 후하게 은행권으로 값을 치르는 모습이 목격되었다는 소문이 나이 든 수용자들 사이에 떠돌았으나, 그것이 그녀의 귀에까지 닿지는 않았다. 어느 날 저녁 그녀는 홀로 일하고 있었는데—담장 위에 머무는 황혼빛을 아끼려고 창가에 서서—그가 문을 열고 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n그녀는 그에게 입을 맞추며 반겼으나, 무엇 하나 물어보기가 두려웠다. 그는 그녀가 얼마나 걱정스럽고 겁에 질려 있는지 알아채고는 미안한 기색을 띠었다.\n\n\"이번엔 네가 속상해할까 봐 걱정이야, 에이미. 정말이지 그래!\"\n\n\"그런 말을 들으니 무척 슬퍼요, 팁. 돌아오신 거예요?\"\n\n\"음—그래.\"\n\n\"이번엔 찾아낸 일이 그리 잘 풀리리라고 기대하지 않았으니까, 그래도 그러려니 했던 것보다는 덜 놀랍고 덜 슬퍼요, 팁.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 그게 최악은 아니야.\"\n\n\"그게 최악이 아니라고요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마. 그래, 에이미, 그게 최악은 아니야. 보다시피 나는 돌아왔어. 하지만—제발 그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마—나는 말하자면 새로운 방식으로 돌아왔어. 자원자 명단에서는 아예 빠졌어. 이제 나는 정규병의 하나로 들어와 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"오! 갇힌 사람이라고는 말하지 마요, 팁! 제발, 제발!\"\n\n\"글쎄, 나도 그 말을 하고 싶진 않아,\" 그가 마지못한 어조로 대답했다. \"하지만 내가 말하지 않고서는 네가 알아듣지 못하겠다면, 난 어쩌란 말이냐? 사십 파운드 남짓 때문에 들어왔어.\"\n\n그 모든 세월 동안 처음으로, 그녀는 자기 근심들의 무게에 무너져 내렸다. 그녀는 맞잡은 두 손을 머리 위로 치켜든 채, 아버지가 이 일을 아시면 돌아가시고 말 거라고 울부짖었다. 그리고 팁의 그 한심한 발치에 쓰러졌다.\n\n팁이 그녀를 정신 차리게 하기는, 그녀가 그에게 마셜시의 아버지가 진실을 알면 제정신이 아니게 되리라는 것을 이해시키기보다 쉬웠다. 그 일은 팁으로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는, 한낱 허황된 관념이었다. 삼촌과 누이의 간청에 떠받쳐진 그녀의 애원에 굴복하면서도, 그는 오직 그런 관점에서만 그것에 따랐다. 그가 돌아온 것에 대한 선례야 부족함이 없었고, 늘 하던 식으로 아버지에게 둘러대어졌으며, 수용자들은 팁보다 그 경건한 속임수를 더 잘 헤아려, 충실하게 그것을 거들어 주었다.\n\n이것이 스물둘이 된 마셜시의 아이의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다. 자기가 태어나 자란 곳이자 집인 그 비참한 마당과 집채 한 덩이에 여전히 애착이 남아 있었으나, 이제 그녀는 모든 이가 자기를 손가락질한다는 여인다운 자의식을 안은 채 그 안을 움츠리며 오갔다. 담장 너머에서 일하기 시작한 뒤로, 그녀는 자기가 사는 곳을 숨기고, 자유로운 도시와 쇠창살 문 사이를—그 바깥에서는 평생 한 번도 잠든 적이 없었다—될 수 있는 한 남몰래 드나들 필요가 있음을 알게 되었다. 그녀의 타고난 소심함은 이 은폐와 더불어 자라났고, 그녀의 가벼운 발걸음과 작은 몸집은 붐비는 거리를 지나는 동안에도 그 거리를 피해 다녔다.\n\n세상의 모진 가난과 궁핍에 대해서는 닳고 닳도록 영리했으나, 그 밖의 모든 것에 대해서는 순진했다. 아버지를, 감옥을, 그리고 그 안을 흐르고 또 흘러가는 그 탁한 산 사람들의 강을 바라보던 그 안개 속에서 순진했다.\n\n이것이 리틀 도릿의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다. 흐릿한 9월의 어느 저녁, 아서 클레넘에게 멀찍이서 지켜보이며 이제 집으로 돌아가던 그 리틀 도릿의. 이것이 리틀 도릿의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다. 런던 다리 끝에서 돌아서고, 다시 그 다리를 건너고, 또 되돌아가고, 세인트 조지 교회 쪽으로 나아가다가, 별안간 다시 한번 발길을 되돌려, 마셜시의 열린 바깥문과 작은 안마당으로 살그머니 들어서던 그 리틀 도릿의.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Lock\n\n아서 클레넘은 거리에 서서, 지나가는 누군가에게 저곳이 어떤 곳인지 물어보려고 기다리고 있었다. 그는 물어볼 만한 기색이 보이지 않는 사람 몇이 자기 곁을 지나치도록 내버려 두고, 여전히 거리에 멈춰 선 채 머뭇거리고 있었는데, 그때 한 노인이 다가와 안마당 쪽으로 꺾어 들어갔다.\n\n노인은 몸을 잔뜩 구부정하게 굽힌 채, 무언가에 골몰한 듯 느릿느릿 터벅터벅 걸었으니, 그런 걸음걸이는 분주한 런던 큰길을 그에게 그다지 안전하지 못한 곳으로 만들었다. 그는 지저분하고 초라한 차림이었는데, 한때는 푸른빛이었을 닳고 닳은 외투가 발목까지 내려와 턱밑까지 단추가 채워져 있었고, 거기서 그 외투는 벨벳 깃의 창백한 유령으로 사라져 버렸다. 살아 있을 적 그 유령에 빳빳함을 주던 붉은 천 조각은 이제 다 드러난 채로, 노인의 목덜미에서 위로 삐죽 솟아올라, 백발과 녹슨 듯한 목깃과 버클이 한데 뒤엉킨 속으로 파고들어, 그 모두가 그의 모자를 거의 벗겨 낼 지경이었다. 그것은 번들거리고 보풀이 다 빠진 모자로, 눈 위까지 처져 내려와 챙이 갈라지고 구겨졌으며, 그 아래로는 주머니 손수건 한 자락이 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다. 바지는 어찌나 길고 헐렁한지, 또 구두는 어찌나 둔하고 큰지, 그는 코끼리처럼 발을 질질 끌며 걸었다. 다만 그중 얼마가 본래의 걸음걸이이고 얼마가 끌리는 천과 가죽 탓인지는 누구도 가늠할 수 없었으리라. 한쪽 팔 밑에는 어떤 관악기가 든, 흐물흐물하고 다 해진 케이스를 끼고 있었고, 같은 손에는 누르스름한 갈색 종이에 싼 작은 봉지에 든 한 푼어치 코담배를 들고 있어서, 아서 클레넘이 그를 바라보는 동안, 길게 한 줌 집어 가엾고 파리한 늙은 코를 천천히 달래고 있었다.\n\n안마당을 가로지르는 이 노인에게 그는 어깨를 가볍게 두드리며 물음을 건넸다. 노인은 걸음을 멈추고 돌아보았는데, 그 흐릿한 잿빛 눈에는 생각이 멀리 가 있던 사람, 게다가 귀도 좀 어두운 사람의 표정이 떠올라 있었다.\n\n\"실례합니다만,\" 아서가 질문을 되풀이하며 말했다. \"이곳은 어떤 곳입니까?\"\n\n\"아! 이곳 말이오?\" 노인은 코담배 한 줌을 코로 가져가던 도중에 멈추고, 그곳을 쳐다보지도 않은 채 손으로 가리키며 대답했다. \"여기는 마셜시요, 선생.\"\n\n\"채무자 감옥 말입니까?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 노인은 굳이 그런 명칭까지 고집할 필요는 없다고 여기는 듯한 태도로 말했다. \"채무자 감옥이오.\"\n\n그는 몸을 돌려 다시 걸어가려 했다.\n\n\"죄송합니다.\" 아서가 그를 한 번 더 멈춰 세우며 말했다. \"한 가지만 더 여쭤도 되겠습니까? 여기는 아무나 들어갈 수 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아무나 들어갈 수는 있소.\" 노인은 대답하면서, 그 강조하는 말투의 의미로 분명히 이렇게 덧붙이고 있었다. \"하지만 아무나 나올 수 있는 건 아니지.\"\n\n\"다시 한번 양해를 구합니다. 이곳을 잘 아십니까?\"\n\n\"선생,\" 노인은 작은 코담배 봉지를 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 그런 질문이 자기를 아프게 하기라도 하는 듯 묻는 이를 향해 돌아서며 대답했다. \"잘 아오.\"\n\n\"부디 용서하십시오. 무례하게 호기심을 부리려는 게 아니라 좋은 뜻이 있어서입니다. 혹시 여기 도릿이라는 이름을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"내 이름이,\" 노인이 너무도 뜻밖에 대답했다. \"도릿이오, 선생.\"\n\n아서는 그를 향해 모자를 벗었다. \"몇 마디만 나눌 기회를 베풀어 주십시오. 방금 하신 말씀에 저는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다. 부디 그 말씀이, 제가 무례하게 말을 건넨 데 대한 충분한 변명이 되기를 바랍니다. 저는 오랜 부재 끝에 최근 영국으로 돌아왔습니다. 시내에 계신 제 어머니, 클레넘 부인 댁에서 바느질을 하고 있는 한 젊은 여인을 보았는데, 그저 리틀 도릿이라고 불리거나 그렇게 언급되는 것만 들었습니다. 저는 그 여인에게 진심으로 마음이 쓰였고, 그녀에 관해 좀 더 알고 싶은 마음이 간절했습니다. 어르신께서 오시기 한순간 전에, 저는 그 여인이 저 문으로 들어가는 것을 보았습니다.\"\n\n노인은 그를 유심히 바라보았다. \"선생은 뱃사람이오?\" 그가 물었다. 고개를 가로젓는 대답에 그는 조금 실망한 듯했다. \"뱃사람이 아니오? 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 보고 그런가 했소만. 선생, 진심으로 하는 말이오?\"\n\n\"진심임을 확실히 말씀드립니다. 그리고 제가 그저 솔직하고 진실하게 말씀드리는 것임을 믿어 주시기를 간청합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 세상을 잘 모르오, 선생.\" 가냘프고 떨리는 목소리를 지닌 상대가 대답했다. \"나는 그저 해시계 위를 지나가는 그림자처럼 흘러가고 있을 뿐이오. 나를 속이는 일은 누구에게도 보람이 없을 거요. 너무도 쉬운 일일 테고—너무도 보잘것없는 성공이라 아무런 만족도 주지 못할 테니까. 선생이 이리 들어가는 걸 본 그 젊은 여인은 내 형의 딸이오. 내 형은 윌리엄 도릿이고, 나는 프레더릭이오. 선생이 그 애를 어머니 댁에서 보았다고 하니(어머니가 그 애를 거두어 주시는 건 나도 알고 있소), 그 애에게 관심을 두게 되었고, 그 애가 여기서 무얼 하는지 알고 싶다는 거로구려. 따라와서 보시오.\"\n\n그는 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 아서가 그와 함께 걸었다.\n\n\"내 형은,\" 노인이 계단 위에 멈춰 서서 느릿느릿 다시 돌아서며 말했다. \"여기서 여러 해를 지냈소. 그리고 우리끼리, 바깥에서 벌어지는 일들 가운데서도, 지금 내가 굳이 들추지 않겠소만, 여러 이유로 형에게는 알리지 않는 것이 많소. 부디 내 조카가 바느질을 한다는 이야기는 한마디도 하지 말아 주시오. 우리끼리 하는 말 이상으로 나가는 이야기는 한마디도 하지 말아 주시오. 우리가 정한 선 안에만 머문다면 크게 어긋날 일은 없을 거요. 자! 따라와서 보시오.\"\n\n아서는 노인을 따라 좁은 통로를 내려갔는데, 그 끝에서 열쇠가 돌아가더니 안쪽에서 튼튼한 문이 열렸다. 그 문은 그들을 수위실인지 현관인지 하는 곳으로 들였고, 그들은 그곳을 가로질러, 또 다른 문과 쇠창살을 지나 감옥 안으로 들어섰다. 늘 앞장서서 터벅터벅 걷던 노인은, 당번을 서는 간수에게 이르렀을 때, 그 느리고 뻣뻣하며 구부정한 동작으로 돌아서서, 마치 자기 동행을 소개하기라도 하듯 했다. 간수는 고개를 끄덕였고, 동행은 누구를 만나러 왔는지 묻는 것도 없이 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n밤은 어두웠다. 마당의 감옥 등불과, 온갖 비뚤어진 낡은 커튼과 가리개 뒤로 희미하게 비치는 감옥 창문의 촛불들은, 그곳을 더 밝게 만드는 기색이 전혀 없었다. 몇 사람이 어슬렁거리고 있었으나, 거주민 대부분은 안에 들어가 있었다. 노인은 마당의 오른편으로 들어서서, 서너 번째 문간에서 꺾어 들어가, 계단을 오르기 시작했다. \"좀 어둡소, 선생. 그래도 길에 걸리는 건 없을 거요.\"\n\n그는 이층의 어느 문을 열기 전에 잠시 멈췄다. 그가 손잡이를 돌리기 무섭게, 방문객은 리틀 도릿을 보았고, 그녀가 혼자 식사하는 것을 그토록 중히 여긴 까닭을 알았다.\n\n그녀는 자기가 먹었어야 할 고기를 집으로 가져와서, 이미 그것을 석쇠에 올려 불 위에서 데우고 있었는데, 이는 낡은 회색 가운에 검은 모자를 쓰고 식탁에서 저녁을 기다리는 아버지를 위해서였다. 그의 앞에는 깨끗한 천이 펼쳐져 있었고, 나이프와 포크와 숟가락, 소금 그릇, 후추통, 유리잔, 그리고 백랍 맥주잔이 놓여 있었다. 그가 특별히 챙기는 카옌 고춧가루 작은 병이나, 받침 접시에 담긴 한 푼어치 피클 같은 입맛 돋우는 것들도 빠지지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 흠칫하더니, 얼굴이 새빨개졌다가 다시 하얗게 질렸다. 방문객은 손을 가볍게 충동적으로 움직였다기보다 눈빛으로, 안심하고 자기를 믿어 달라고 그녀에게 청했다.\n\n\"이분을 바깥문에서 만났네.\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"클레넘 씨라네, 윌리엄, 에이미 친구분의 아드님이지. 지나가던 길에 인사를 드리고 싶어 하셨는데, 들어올까 말까 망설이고 계시더군. 이쪽이 제 형 윌리엄입니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"바라건대,\" 아서가 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 망설이며 말했다. \"따님에 대한 제 존경이, 어르신께 소개되고 싶었던 제 바람을 설명하고 정당화해 주기를 바랍니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 상대가 일어서서, 손바닥으로 모자를 받쳐 벗어 들고는, 다시 쓸 채비를 한 채 그것을 들고 있으며 대답했다. \"제게 영광입니다. 환영합니다, 선생.\" 그는 깊이 고개를 숙였다. \"프레더릭, 의자를 좀. 부디 앉으시지요, 클레넘 씨.\"\n\n그는 벗을 때와 똑같이 검은 모자를 다시 쓰고, 자기 자리에 도로 앉았다. 그의 태도에는 놀랄 만큼 자애롭고 너그러이 베푸는 듯한 분위기가 어려 있었다. 이것이 바로 그가 동료 수감자들을 맞이할 때 행하던 의례였다.\n\n\"마셜시에 잘 오셨습니다, 선생. 저는 이 담장 안으로 많은 신사분을 맞이해 왔지요. 아마 아실 겁니다만—제 딸 에이미가 말씀드렸을지도 모르겠습니다만, 제가 이곳의 아버지입니다.\"\n\n\"저—그렇게 알고 있습니다.\" 아서가 그 단언에 황급히 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"제 딸 에이미가 여기서 태어났다는 것은 아마 아실 겁니다. 착한 아이지요, 선생, 사랑스러운 아이입니다. 오랫동안 제게 위안이자 버팀목이 되어 주었지요. 에이미야, 이 접시를 좀 올려 다오. 클레넘 씨, 우리가 여기서 어쩔 수 없이 따르는 이 소박한 방식을 너그러이 보아주실 겁니다. 선생, 영광스럽게도 함께—권해 드린다면 실례가 될까요—\"\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 아서가 대답했다. \"한 입도 됐습니다.\"\n\n그는 이 사람의 태도에, 또 그의 딸이 가문 내력에 관해 무언가를 숨겨 왔을 가능성이 그의 머릿속에서 그토록 멀리 동떨어져 있다는 데에, 그저 어리둥절할 따름이었다.\n\n그녀는 그의 잔을 채우고, 자질구레한 것들을 모두 그의 손 닿는 자리에 챙겨 놓고는, 그가 저녁을 드는 동안 곁에 앉았다. 분명 밤마다 지키는 그들만의 관례에 따라, 그녀는 자기 앞에 빵을 조금 놓고, 그의 잔에 입술을 댔으나, 아서가 보기에 그녀는 마음이 불편한 듯 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다. 아버지를 바라보는 그녀의 눈길은, 반쯤은 그를 우러르고 그를 자랑스러워하면서, 반쯤은 그를 대신해 부끄러워하는, 온통 헌신과 사랑이 어린 그 눈길은, 그의 가슴 깊은 곳까지 파고들었다.\n\n마셜시의 아버지는 자기 동생을, 사람 좋고 선의는 있으나 두각을 나타내지 못한 평범한 인물로 여겨 너그러이 대했다. \"프레더릭,\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 너와 패니가 너희 거처에서 저녁을 먹는다지. 패니는 어쩌고 왔느냐, 프레더릭?\"\n\n\"팁하고 산책하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"팁은—아실지 모르겠습니다만—제 아들입니다, 클레넘 씨. 좀 거칠고 자리를 잡기 어려운 아이였습니다만, 세상에 발을 들이는 출발이 좀—\" 그는 희미하게 한숨을 쉬며 어깨를 으쓱하고는 방 안을 둘러보았다. \"—좀 불운했지요. 선생은 여기 처음 오시는 길입니까?\"\n\n\"처음입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 모르는 채로 어릴 적부터 여기 드나드셨을 리는 없겠지요. 누구든—이렇다 할 지체가 있는 분이라면—이렇다 할 지체가 있는 분이라면—제게 소개되지 않고 이곳에 오는 일은 좀처럼 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"하루에 마흔, 쉰 명씩 형님께 소개된 적도 있었습니다.\" 프레더릭이 한 줄기 자랑스러움으로 희미하게 얼굴을 밝히며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지!\" 마셜시의 아버지가 동의했다. \"그 수를 넘긴 적도 있었지요. 개정기의 화창한 일요일이면, 그야말로 알현식 같습니다—그야말로 알현식이지요. 에이미야, 작년 성탄절 주간에, 그 반년 구류형을 받은 사람 좋은 석탄 상인이 내게 소개해 준 캠버웰에서 온 그 신사분 이름을 떠올리려고 오늘 한나절을 애썼단다.\"\n\n\"이름은 기억나지 않아요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"프레더릭, 너는 그 이름이 기억나느냐?\"\n\n프레더릭은 그 이름을 들어 본 적이나 있는지 의심스러워했다. 무언가 정보를 얻으리라는 희망을 품고 그런 질문을 던질 상대로 프레더릭만큼 어울리지 않는 사람이 이 세상에 또 없으리라는 데에는 누구도 의심의 여지가 없었다.\n\n\"내 말은,\" 형이 말했다. \"그토록 섬세하게 그 훌륭한 선행을 베푼 그 신사분 말이다. 허! 쳇! 이름이 영 떠오르질 않는구나. 클레넘 씨, 마침 훌륭하고 섬세한 선행 이야기가 나왔으니, 어쩌면 그게 무엇이었는지 알고 싶으실지도 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"무척 알고 싶습니다.\" 아서가 말했는데, 그는 점점 수그러들기 시작하는 그 가녀린 머리와, 새로운 근심이 슬며시 번지는 그 창백한 얼굴에서 눈길을 거두며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그것은 참으로 너그럽고, 참으로 고운 마음을 보여 주는 일이라, 그 이야기를 하는 것이 거의 의무라 하겠습니다. 저는 그때, 개인적인 거북함은 개의치 않고, 적절한 기회가 있을 때마다 늘 이 이야기를 하겠노라고 말했지요. 그—저—그—사실을 감출 필요가 없겠습니다만—클레넘 씨, 아셔야 할 것은, 이곳에 오는 분들이 가끔 이곳의 아버지에게 약간의—사례를—드리고자 하는 일이 더러 있다는 겁니다.\"\n\n말없이 반쯤 억눌린 애원으로 그의 팔에 얹힌 그녀의 손과, 겁먹은 채 움츠러든 작은 몸이 외면하듯 돌아서는 것을 보는 일은, 참으로 슬프고 슬픈 광경을 보는 일이었다.\n\n\"가끔은,\" 그는 동요하여, 이따금 헛기침을 하면서, 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 이어 갔다. \"가끔은—흠—이런 모양을 띠기도 하고, 또 가끔은 저런 모양을 띠기도 합니다만, 대체로는—허—돈이지요. 그리고 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다만, 그것은 너무도 자주—흠—고맙게 받아들여집니다. 제가 말씀드리는 그 신사분은, 클레넘 씨, 제 마음에 더없이 흡족한 방식으로 제게 소개되었고, 대단히 정중할 뿐만 아니라 대단히—에헴—식견 있게 대화를 나누었지요.\" 이 모든 이야기를 하는 내내, 그는 저녁을 다 먹었는데도, 마치 아직 음식이 남아 있기라도 한 듯, 나이프와 포크로 자기 접시 위를 신경질적으로 더듬고 있었다. \"대화 중에 그분에게 정원이 있다는 것이 드러났습니다. 처음에는 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 조심스러워하셨지요. 정원이란—흠—제게는 닿을 수 없는 것이니까요. 하지만 그분이 자기 온실에서 가져온 아주 고운 제라늄 한 송이—정말이지 아름다운 제라늄 송이를 제가 감탄하는 바람에 그 일이 밝혀졌습니다. 제가 그 짙은 빛깔을 두고 한마디 하자, 그분은 그것을 감싸고 있던 종이 한 장을 보여 주셨는데, 거기에는 '마셜시의 아버지께'라고 적혀 있었고, 그것을 제게 건네셨지요. 그런데 그게—흠—다가 아니었습니다. 그분은 떠나시면서, 반 시간 뒤에 그 종이를 벗겨 보라고 특별히 당부하셨습니다. 저는—허—그렇게 했고, 그 안에—에헴—이 기니가 들어 있는 것을 발견했지요. 클레넘 씨, 분명히 말씀드립니다만, 저는—흠—여러 방식으로, 또 여러 정도의 값어치로 사례를 받아 왔고, 그것들은 늘—허—유감스럽게도 고맙게 받아들여졌습니다. 하지만 이—에헴—이 특별한 사례만큼 제가 더 기뻐한 적은 없었습니다.\"\n\n아서가 그런 주제에 대해 자기가 할 수 있는 짧은 말을 막 꺼내려던 참에, 종이 울리기 시작하고 문 쪽으로 발걸음이 다가왔다. 리틀 도릿보다 훨씬 좋은 몸매에 훨씬 성숙한, 둘을 나란히 놓고 보면 얼굴은 훨씬 어려 보이는 어여쁜 처녀가, 낯선 사람을 보고는 문간에 멈춰 섰고, 그녀와 함께 온 청년도 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 패니다. 제 맏딸과 아들입니다, 클레넘 씨. 저 종은 방문객더러 물러가라는 신호인데, 그래서 작별 인사를 하러 온 거지요. 하지만 시간은 넉넉합니다, 넉넉해요. 얘들아, 너희끼리 처리할 집안일이 있더라도 클레넘 씨가 양해해 주실 거다. 이분도 아마 아실 게다, 내가 여기서 방 하나밖에 쓰지 못한다는 걸.\"\n\n\"전 그저 에이미한테서 제 깨끗한 옷을 받으러 온 거예요, 아버지.\" 둘째 딸이 말했다.\n\n\"저는 제 옷을요.\" 팁이 말했다.\n\n에이미는 위는 서랍장이고 아래는 침대 틀로 된 낡은 가구의 서랍 하나를 열어, 작은 꾸러미 두 개를 꺼내 남동생과 언니에게 건넸다. \"고치고 손질해 놨어?\" 언니가 속삭이듯 묻는 것을 클레넘은 들었다. 거기에 에이미는 \"응\" 하고 대답했다. 그는 이제 일어선 참이라, 그 틈을 타 방 안을 둘러보았다. 휑한 벽은 초록색으로 칠해져 있었는데, 분명 서툰 손길로 한 것이었고, 판화 몇 점으로 빈약하게 꾸며져 있었다. 창에는 커튼이 드리워져 있고 바닥에는 양탄자가 깔려 있었으며, 여러 해를 지나는 동안 쌓인 선반과 못걸이를 비롯한 자질구레한 살림 도구들이 있었다. 그곳은 답답하고 비좁은 방이었고, 가구도 변변치 않았다. 게다가 굴뚝은 연기를 내뿜었으니, 그렇지 않다면 벽난로 위에 달린 양철 가리개가 쓸데없는 것이었으리라. 그러나 끊임없는 정성과 보살핌이 그 방을 깔끔하게, 그 나름으로는 아늑하기까지 하게 만들어 놓았다.\n\n그 내내 종은 울리고 있었고, 삼촌은 어서 가고 싶어 안달이었다. \"어서, 패니, 어서, 패니.\" 그는 너덜너덜한 클라리넷 케이스를 팔 밑에 낀 채 말했다. \"자물쇠다, 얘야, 자물쇠!\"\n\n패니는 아버지에게 잘 자라고 인사하고는 사뿐히 휙 빠져나갔다. 팁은 이미 계단을 요란히 내려간 뒤였다. \"자, 클레넘 씨.\" 삼촌이 그들을 따라 발을 끌며 나가다 뒤를 돌아보며 말했다. \"자물쇠입니다, 선생, 자물쇠.\"\n\n클레넘 씨는 따라 나서기 전에 해야 할 일이 두 가지 있었다. 하나는 그의 딸에게 마음의 상처를 주지 않으면서 마셜시의 아버지에게 사례를 건네는 일이었고, 다른 하나는 그 딸에게, 단 한마디라도, 자기가 이곳에 온 까닭을 설명할 무언가를 말하는 일이었다.\n\n\"제가,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"아래층까지 배웅해 드리지요.\"\n\n그녀는 다른 사람들을 뒤따라 슬며시 빠져나간 뒤라, 그들 둘만 남아 있었다. \"절대 그러지 마십시오.\" 방문객이 황급히 말했다. \"부디 제가—\" 짤랑, 짤랑, 짤랑.\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"제가 깊이, 참으로 깊이—\" 그러나 그의 방문객은 짤랑거리는 소리를 멈추려 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 무서운 속도로 계단을 내려가 버린 뒤였다.\n\n내려가는 길에도, 마당에서도 그는 리틀 도릿을 보지 못했다. 마지막 두세 명의 낙오자들이 수위실로 서둘러 가고 있었고, 그도 그 뒤를 따르려던 참에, 입구에서 첫 번째 집 문간에 서 있는 그녀가 눈에 들어왔다. 그는 황급히 발길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"부디 용서하십시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"이런 곳에서 말을 거는 걸 용서하십시오. 아니, 제가 이곳에 온 것 자체를 용서하십시오! 저는 오늘 밤 당신을 따라왔습니다. 그렇게 한 것은, 당신과 당신 가족에게 어떤 도움이라도 드리고자 애써 보려 함이었습니다. 저와 제 어머니가 어떤 사이인지는 당신도 아실 테니, 제가 어머니 댁에서 우리의 서먹한 관계를 그대로 지켜 온 것을 이상히 여기지 않으실 겁니다. 무심코 어머니를 시샘하게 하거나 원망하게 하거나, 어머니가 당신을 낮춰 보아 당신에게 해를 끼치는 일이 없도록 한 것이지요. 이 짧은 시간 동안 여기서 본 것들이, 당신의 벗이 되고 싶다는 제 진심 어린 바람을 크게 더해 주었습니다. 당신의 신뢰를 얻을 수 있기를 바랄 수 있다면, 그것은 제게 숱한 실망을 보상해 줄 것입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 처음에는 겁을 먹었으나, 그가 말하는 동안 차츰 용기를 내는 듯했다.\n\n\"참 친절하십니다, 선생. 제게 무척 진지하게 말씀해 주시는군요. 하지만 저는—하지만 저를 지켜보지 않으셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 그렇게 말할 때 어린 감정이 아버지를 위한 것임을 알아차렸고, 그것을 존중하여 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"클레넘 부인은 제게 큰 도움을 주셨어요. 그분이 주신 일감이 없었더라면 우리가 어찌 살았을지 모르겠습니다. 그분에게 무언가를 숨기는 사람이 된다면 좋은 보답이 아닐까 두렵습니다. 오늘 밤은 더 이상 말씀드릴 수가 없어요, 선생. 저희에게 친절을 베풀려 하신다는 건 잘 압니다. 고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"떠나기 전에 한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다. 제 어머니를 안 지 오래되었습니까?\"\n\n\"두 해쯤 된 것 같습니다, 선생—종이 멎었네요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 처음 알게 되었습니까? 어머니가 이곳으로 당신을 부르러 보냈습니까?\"\n\n\"아니에요. 그분은 제가 여기 산다는 것조차 모르세요. 저희에게는, 아버지와 제게는 친구가 한 분 있어요—가난한 막노동꾼이지만 더없이 좋은 친구지요—그래서 제가 바느질을 하고 싶다고 적어 그분 주소를 적어 두었어요. 그분은 제가 적은 것을 돈 들지 않는 몇 군데에 내걸어 주셨고, 클레넘 부인이 그렇게 해서 저를 찾아내어 부르러 보내셨어요. 문이 잠기겠어요, 선생!\"\n\n그녀는 어찌나 떨며 동요하고 있었던지, 또 그는 그녀에 대한 연민과, 차츰 자기 앞에 밝혀지는 그녀의 사연에 대한 깊은 관심에 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 차마 발걸음을 떼기가 힘들었다. 그러나 멎은 종과 감옥의 정적이 떠나라는 경고였기에, 그는 다정한 말 몇 마디를 황급히 건네고는, 그녀가 아버지에게로 미끄러지듯 돌아가도록 두고 떠났다.\n\n그러나 그는 너무 늦게까지 남아 있었다. 안쪽 문은 잠겼고, 수위실은 닫혀 있었다. 손으로 부질없이 잠시 두드려 본 뒤, 그는 이 밤을 꼼짝없이 새워야 한다는 달갑지 않은 확신에 사로잡힌 채 그곳에 서 있었는데, 그때 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 그에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"갇히셨군요, 응?\" 그 목소리가 말했다. \"아침까지는 집에 못 가시겠는데요. 아! 클레넘 씨, 당신이군요?\"\n\n그 목소리는 팁의 것이었다. 비가 내리기 시작하는 가운데, 그들은 감옥 마당에 서서 서로를 바라보았다.\n\n\"제대로 당하셨네요.\" 팁이 말했다. \"다음번엔 좀 더 빈틈없으셔야겠어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 당신도 갇혀 있질 않소.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇죠!\" 팁이 비꼬듯 말했다. \"거의 그렇다고 할 수 있죠! 하지만 당신과 같은 처지는 아니에요. 저는 이 가게 소속이거든요. 다만 우리 영감님은 절대 그걸 알아선 안 된다는 게 누이의 지론이지요. 저로선 왜 그래야 하는지 모르겠습니다만.\"\n\n\"제가 묵을 데를 좀 구할 수 있을까요?\" 아서가 물었다. \"어떻게 하는 게 좋겠소?\"\n\n\"무엇보다 먼저 에이미를 찾는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 팁이 무슨 어려운 일이든 당연하다는 듯 그녀에게 떠넘기며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 폐를 끼치느니 차라리 밤새 걸어 다니겠소—그쯤이야 별일도 아니지요.\"\n\n\"잠자리 값을 치르는 게 싫지 않으시다면 그러실 필요 없어요. 돈 치르는 게 싫지 않으시다면, 이런 사정이니 스너거리 탁자 위에 잠자리를 하나 마련해 줄 겁니다. 같이 가시면 제가 거기로 안내해 드리지요.\"\n\n마당을 따라 내려가면서, 아서는 방금 떠나온 방의 창문을 올려다보았는데, 그곳에는 아직 불빛이 타오르고 있었다. \"예, 선생.\" 팁이 그의 시선을 따라가며 말했다. \"저게 영감님 방이에요. 누이는 한 시간쯤 더 곁에 앉아 어제 신문 같은 걸 읽어 드리고, 그러고 나서 작은 유령처럼 빠져나와 소리 없이 사라지지요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"영감님은 저 방에서 주무시고, 누이는 간수 집에 거처를 두고 있어요. 저기 저 첫 번째 집이지요.\" 팁은 그녀가 들어갔던 그 문간을 가리키며 말했다. \"첫 번째 집의 다락방이에요. 바깥에서 두 배는 좋은 방을 얻을 값을 거기에 치르지요. 하지만 가엾은 누이는 밤이고 낮이고 영감님 곁을 지킨답니다.\"\n\n이 이야기를 나누는 사이 그들은 감옥 위쪽 끝에 있는 술집에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서는 동료 수감자들이 막 저녁 친목회를 파한 참이었다. 그 모임이 열리던 일층 방이 바로 그 스너거리였다. 의장이 앉던 단상, 백랍 잔들, 유리잔들, 파이프들, 담뱃재, 그리고 회원들이 풍기던 전반적인 냄새가, 그 흥겨운 모임이 산회하며 남겨 놓은 그대로였다. 스너거리는 흔히 숙녀용 그로그에 필수라 여겨지는 두 가지 성질, 곧 뜨겁고 독하다는 점을 갖추고 있었으나, 그 양이 넉넉해야 한다는 세 번째 비슷한 조건에서는 부족함이 있었으니, 그저 비좁게 갇힌 방에 지나지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n바깥에서 온 익숙지 못한 방문객은, 이곳에 있는 사람은 누구나—주인이든 종업원이든 술집 아가씨든 심부름꾼 소년이든 모두—죄수일 거라고 자연스레 여겼다. 정말로 그런지 아닌지는 드러나지 않았으나, 그들 모두 시들시들한 행색이었다. 앞쪽 거실에서 양초 가게를 하면서 신사 하숙인을 들이던 사람이 잠자리 마련을 거들었다. 그는 한때 재단사였고 사륜마차를 몰고 다닌 적도 있다고 했다. 그는 이 학교의 이익을 위해 소송도 불사하며 떨치고 나섰노라 자랑했다. 또한 그는 마셜시 관리관이 마땅히 동료 수감자들에게 돌아와야 할 '기금'을 가로채고 있다는, 막연하고 규정할 수 없는 생각을 품고 있었다. 그는 그렇게 믿기를 좋아했고, 새로 온 사람과 낯선 이들에게 그 어렴풋한 불만을 늘 새겨 넣곤 했다. 다만 그가 말하는 기금이 무엇인지, 또 그 생각이 어떻게 그의 마음속에 뿌리내리게 되었는지는 죽었다 깨어나도 설명하지 못했을 것이다. 그럼에도 그는 자기 몫의 기금이 주당 삼 실링 구 펜스라고 완전히 스스로를 납득시켜 놓았고, 개개의 수감자로서 그 액수만큼 매주 월요일마다 어김없이 관리관에게 사취당하고 있다고 믿었다. 보아하니 그는 이 사정을 늘어놓을 기회를 놓치지 않으려고 잠자리 마련을 거든 듯했다. 마음속 짐을 그렇게 부려 놓은 뒤, 그리고 신문사에 편지를 써서 관리관을 폭로하겠노라고 (보아하니 늘 그러면서도 아무 결과도 없었던 듯한 그 말을) 선언한 뒤, 그는 나머지 사람들과 이런저런 잡담에 빠져들었다. 그 무리 전체의 전반적인 어조로 보아, 그들이 파산을 인류의 정상적인 상태로, 빚을 갚는 일을 이따금 발병하는 질병쯤으로 여기게 되었음이 분명했다.\n\n이 기이한 광경 속에서, 또 이 기이한 유령들이 자기 주위를 떠도는 가운데, 아서 클레넘은 그 채비를 마치 꿈의 일부인 양 바라보았다. 그러는 동안 오래 단련된 팁은, 스너거리의 자원을 끔찍하게도 즐기면서, 수감자들의 회비로 유지되는 공동 부엌 화덕이며, 같은 방식으로 운영되는 온수용 보일러며, 결국 건강하고 부유하고 현명해지는 길은 마셜시로 오는 것이라는 추론으로 흐르게 마련인 여러 시설을 일러 주었다.\n\n한쪽 구석에 붙여 놓은 두 개의 탁자는 마침내 제법 쓸 만한 침상으로 바뀌었고, 낯선 손님은 윈저 의자들과, 의장의 단상과, 맥주 냄새 밴 공기와, 톱밥과, 파이프 불씨와, 가래통과, 그리고 휴식과 함께 남겨졌다. 그러나 마지막 항목은 한참, 한참, 한참 동안 나머지에 끼어들지 못했다. 그곳의 낯섦, 아무 준비 없이 들이닥친 사정, 갇혀 있다는 느낌, 위층 그 방과 두 형제에 대한 기억, 그리고 무엇보다 물러나던 그 어린아이 같은 모습, 그리고 이제 와 그가 거기서 궁핍까지는 아니더라도 여러 해의 부실한 끼니의 흔적을 읽어 낸 그 얼굴이, 그를 잠 못 들고 불행하게 만들었다.\n\n게다가 갖가지 억측들이, 감옥과 더없이 기묘한 관계를 맺으면서도 늘 감옥에 관한 것들이, 그가 깨어 누워 있는 동안 악몽처럼 그의 머릿속을 헤집고 다녔다. 여기서 죽을지도 모를 사람들을 위해 관이 미리 준비되어 있는지, 있다면 어디에, 어떻게 보관되는지, 감옥에서 죽은 사람들은 어디에 묻히는지, 어떻게 실려 나가는지, 어떤 절차를 따르는지, 무자비한 채권자가 죽은 자까지 붙잡아 둘 수 있는지? 탈옥에 관해서는, 탈출할 가망이 얼마나 되는지? 죄수가 밧줄과 갈고리로 담을 기어오를 수 있는지, 반대편으로는 어떻게 내려설지? 지붕 위에 내려앉아 계단을 살금살금 내려가, 문으로 빠져나와 군중 속으로 사라질 수 있는지? 감옥의 화재에 관해서는, 자기가 여기 누워 있는 사이에 불이라도 나면 어찌 될지?\n\n그리고 이렇게 제멋대로 솟구치는 공상들도, 따지고 보면 세 사람이 그의 앞에 떠나지 않고 머무는 어떤 그림의 배경에 지나지 않았다. 그의 아버지는, 그가 숨을 거둘 때 짓던 그 한결같은 눈빛으로, 마치 예언처럼 초상화 속에 어둡게 드러나 있었고, 그의 어머니는 팔을 치켜들어 그의 의심을 막아 내고 있었으며, 리틀 도릿은 그 비천한 팔에 손을 얹은 채, 수그린 고개를 외면하고 있었다.\n\n만약 그의 어머니가 이 가엾은 처녀에게 누그러질 만한, 스스로 잘 아는 오랜 까닭을 품고 있었다면! 만약 지금 고요히 잠든—제발 그러하기를!—저 죄수가, 위대한 심판의 날의 빛 아래에서 자기 몰락의 자취를 거슬러 올라가 그것이 그녀에게서 비롯되었음을 밝혀낸다면! 만약 그녀와 그의 아버지가 저지른 어떤 행위가, 단지 멀리서나마, 저 두 형제의 백발을 그토록 낮은 곳까지 떨어뜨린 것이었다면!\n\n한 가지 생각이 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 이곳에서의 그 오랜 수감과, 자기 방에 갇힌 어머니 자신의 그 오랜 칩거 속에서, 그의 어머니는 결산해야 할 어떤 균형을 보고 있었던 것일까? '나는 그 사람을 가두는 데 한몫했음을 인정한다. 나는 그 대가를 똑같이 치러 왔다. 그는 자기 감옥에서 쇠락해 갔고, 나는 내 감옥에서 쇠락해 갔다. 나는 그 죗값을 치렀다.'\n\n다른 모든 생각이 스러진 뒤에도, 이 한 가지 생각만은 그를 사로잡고 놓아주지 않았다. 그가 잠들었을 때, 그녀는 바퀴 달린 의자에 앉은 채 이 변명으로 그를 막아 내며 그의 앞에 나타났다. 그가 까닭 없이 화들짝 놀라 깨어났을 때, 그 말들이 그의 귓전에 맴돌았으니, 마치 그녀의 목소리가 그의 잠을 깨뜨리려고 베갯머리에서 천천히 그 말을 읊은 듯했다. '그는 자기 감옥에서 시들어 간다. 나는 내 감옥에서 시들어 간다. 가차 없는 정의가 행해지고 있다. 내가 이 일로 무슨 빚을 졌단 말이냐!'\n\n# 제10장. 통치의 학문 전체를 담음\n\n아침빛은 감옥 담을 기어올라 스너거리 창 안을 들여다보는 데 조금도 서두르지 않았다. 그러다 마침내 들어왔을 때에도, 한바탕 빗줄기를 몰고 오지 않고 홀로 왔더라면 훨씬 반가웠을 것이다. 그러나 추분의 강풍이 바다 쪽에서 불어 나오고 있었고, 사사로움 없는 남서풍은 제 길을 가는 중에 비좁은 마셜시조차 그냥 지나치지 않으려 했다. 세인트조지 교회의 첨탑을 뚫고 울부짖으며 인근의 굴뚝 갓을 죄다 빙빙 돌리는 한편, 한 차례 휙 내리꽂혀 사우스워크의 매연을 감옥 안으로 두들겨 넣었고, 아직 불을 지피던 몇 안 되는 부지런한 재소자들의 굴뚝으로 곤두박질쳐 그들을 거의 질식시켰다.\n\n아서 클레넘은, 설령 그의 잠자리가 좀 더 호젓한 곳에 있었고 어제의 잿불을 긁어내고 오늘 아침 재소자용 보일러 밑에 불을 지피고 그 스파르타식 그릇을 펌프에서 채우고 공동실을 쓸고 톱밥을 뿌리는 따위의 온갖 준비에 덜 시달렸더라도, 침상에서 꾸물거릴 마음이 거의 없었을 것이다. 밤새 거의 쉬지 못했으면서도 아침을 보게 되어 진심으로 기뻤던 그는, 주위의 사물을 분간할 수 있게 되자마자 곧장 일어나, 문이 열리기 전까지 두 시간이나 무겁게 마당을 거닐었다.\n\n담이 서로 너무 가까이 붙어 있었고 거친 구름이 그 위로 너무도 빠르게 내달려서, 바람 거센 하늘을 올려다보면 뱃멀미가 시작되려는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 바람의 변덕에 비스듬히 실려 오는 비는 그가 어젯밤 찾아갔던 중앙 건물의 그쪽 면을 검게 적셨으나, 담의 바람그늘 아래로는 좁다랗게 마른 도랑 같은 자리를 남겼다. 그는 그곳을, 짚 부스러기와 먼지와 종이 조각, 펌프에서 흘린 찌끼, 어제 채소의 흩어진 잎사귀 사이를 오르내리며 거닐었다. 사람이 굳이 바라보아야 할 만한 것 가운데 가장 초췌한 삶의 풍경이었다.\n\n그를 그곳으로 데려온 그 작은 사람의 모습이 언뜻 보였다고 해서 그 풍경이 누그러지지도 않았다. 어쩌면 그의 얼굴이 양쪽 문에서 돌려져 있는 사이에 그녀가 제 방문을 빠져나와 아버지가 사는 방문으로 들어갔는지도 모른다. 그러나 그는 그녀의 모습을 전혀 보지 못했다. 오라비가 나오기에는 너무 이른 시각이었다. 한 번 본 것만으로도 그가 밤에 차지하던 그 퀴퀴한 잠자리가 무엇이든 거기서 벗어나기를 게을리하리라는 것을 알기에는 충분했다. 그래서 아서 클레넘은 문이 열리기를 기다리며 마당을 오르내리는 동안, 현재보다는 앞으로 자신의 탐색을 이어 갈 방도를 마음속으로 궁리했다.\n\n마침내 수위실 문이 돌아갔고, 계단에 서서 머리를 이른 빗질로 빗고 있던 간수가 그를 내보낼 채비가 되어 있었다. 풀려난다는 기쁨에 찬 느낌으로 그는 수위실을 지나, 어젯밤 그 오라비와 이야기를 나누었던 작은 바깥 마당에 다시 서게 되었다.\n\n이미 한 무리의 사람들이 띄엄띄엄 들어오고 있었는데, 이곳의 정체 모를 심부름꾼이며 중개꾼이며 잔심부름꾼임을 알아보기란 어렵지 않았다. 그중 더러는 문이 열리기까지 빗속에서 어슬렁거리고 있었고, 도착 시각을 좀 더 빈틈없이 맞춘 이들은 이제 막 올라와, 식료품점에서 받은 축축한 누르스름한 종이봉지며 빵 덩이며 버터 덩어리며 달걀이며 우유 따위를 들고 안으로 들어갔다. 초라함을 시중드는 이 초라한 자들, 파산을 시중드는 이 파산한 자들의 가난이란 가히 볼 만한 광경이었다. 그토록 닳아빠진 외투와 바지, 그토록 곰팡내 나는 가운과 숄, 그토록 찌부러진 모자와 보닛, 그런 장화와 구두, 그런 우산과 지팡이는 누더기 시장에서도 본 적이 없었다. 그들은 하나같이 다른 남자와 여자가 버린 옷을 걸쳤고, 다른 사람들의 개성에서 떨어져 나온 헝겊과 조각으로 짜 맞춰져 있었으며, 그들 자신만의 의복상의 존재라곤 없었다. 그들의 걸음걸이는 따로 떨어진 한 종족의 걸음걸이였다. 그들은 마치 영원토록 전당포로 가고 있기라도 하다는 듯, 끈질기게 모퉁이로 슬그머니 돌아드는 독특한 방식을 지니고 있었다. 그들은 기침을 할 때면, 문간이나 외풍 드는 통로에서 잊힌 채 빛바랜 잉크로 쓴 편지에 대한 답장을 기다리는 데 익숙한 사람들처럼 기침했는데, 그런 편지를 받은 이들에게는 큰 심란함만 안기고 만족이라곤 주지 못하는 법이었다. 그들은 지나가며 이 낯선 이를 곁눈질할 때, 빌려 쓰는 자의 눈으로 — 자기들이 그에게 소개되었을 경우 그가 얼마나 무를지, 그리고 그가 무언가 푸짐하게 한턱낼 가능성이 얼마나 될지를 가늠하는, 굶주리고 날카롭고 타산적인 눈으로 — 곁눈질했다. 수수료를 받는 구걸이 그들의 솟은 어깨를 구부정하게 만들었고, 후들거리는 다리를 비척거리게 했으며, 그들의 옷을 단추로 채우고 핀으로 꽂고 기워 끌리게 했고, 단춧구멍을 너덜너덜하게 했으며, 더러운 끈의 작은 끄트머리가 되어 그들의 몸에서 새어 나왔고, 술기운 어린 입김이 되어 그들의 입에서 뿜어 나왔다.\n\n이 사람들이 마당에 가만히 서 있는 그의 곁을 지나칠 때, 그중 한 사람이 되돌아와 자기 솜씨로 도와드릴 일이 없겠느냐고 물었다. 그러자 아서 클레넘의 머릿속에, 떠나기 전에 리틀 도릿과 다시 한 번 이야기를 나누어야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그녀도 처음의 놀라움에서 회복되었을 테고, 자기에게 좀 더 편하게 대할지도 모를 일이었다. 그는 이 무리의 한 사람(손에 훈제 청어 두 마리를 들고, 겨드랑이에 빵 한 덩이와 구두약 솔 하나를 끼고 있었다)에게, 커피 한 잔을 마실 만한 가장 가까운 곳이 어디냐고 물었다. 그 정체 모를 자는 힘이 나는 말투로 대답하더니, 그를 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 거리에 있는 거리의 어느 커피점으로 데려다주었다.\n\n\"도릿 양을 아십니까?\" 새 손님이 물었다.\n\n그 정체 모를 자는 도릿 양을 둘 알고 있었다. 한 사람은 안에서 태어났는데 — 바로 그 사람이었다! 그 사람이라고? 그 정체 모를 자는 그녀를 여러 해 동안 알아 왔다. 다른 도릿 양으로 말하자면, 그 정체 모를 자는 그녀와 그녀의 삼촌과 같은 집에 세 들어 살고 있었다.\n\n이로써 손님은, 그 정체 모를 자가 도릿이 거리로 나섰다는 소식을 전해 줄 때까지 커피점에 머물려던 어렴풋한 계획을 바꾸었다. 그는 그 정체 모를 자에게 그녀에게 전할 은밀한 전갈을 맡겼는데, 어젯밤 그녀의 아버지를 찾아뵈었던 방문객이 그녀의 삼촌 거처에서 그녀와 몇 마디 나눌 호의를 청한다는 내용이었다. 같은 출처로부터 그는 매우 가까운 그 집까지의 자세한 길 안내도 얻었고, 반 크라운을 받고 흡족해하는 그 정체 모를 자를 돌려보낸 뒤, 커피점에서 서둘러 요기를 하고는 한껏 빠른 걸음으로 그 클라리넷 연주자의 거처로 향했다.\n\n이 집에는 세입자가 어찌나 많은지, 문기둥이 마치 대성당 오르간에 음전이 빼곡하듯 초인종 손잡이로 빼곡한 듯했다. 어느 것이 클라리넷 음전일지 갈피를 못 잡고 그 점을 따져 보고 있을 때, 깃털공 하나가 응접실 창에서 날아와 그의 모자 위에 내려앉았다. 그제야 그는 응접실 창에 크리플즈 학원이라는 글귀가 적힌 차양이 드리워져 있고, 또 다른 줄에 야간 교습이라 쓰여 있는 것을, 그리고 그 차양 뒤에 버터 바른 빵 한 조각과 깃털공 채를 든, 작고 창백한 얼굴의 사내아이가 있는 것을 알아챘다. 창이 보도에서 닿을 만한 높이여서, 그는 차양 너머로 들여다보고 깃털공을 돌려준 다음 물음을 던졌다.\n\n\"도릿이요?\" 작고 창백한 얼굴의 사내아이가 말했다(실은 크리플즈 도령이었다). \"도릿 씨요? 세 번째 종에 한 번 노크하면 돼요.\"\n\n크리플즈 씨의 학생들은 거리로 난 현관문을 습자책 삼아 써먹은 모양이었으니, 그만큼 온통 연필로 휘갈겨져 있었다. '늙은 도릿'과 '더러운 딕'이라는 글귀가 짝지어 빈번히 적혀 있는 것으로 보아, 크리플즈 씨의 학생들 쪽에서 인신공격의 의도가 있었던 듯싶었다. 가엾은 노인이 몸소 문을 열어 줄 때까지, 이런 관찰을 할 시간은 넉넉했다.\n\n\"하!\" 노인이 아서를 아주 천천히 기억해 내며 말했다. \"어젯밤 안에 갇히셨던 분이로군요?\"\n\n\"네, 도릿 씨. 여기서 곧 조카딸을 뵐 수 있길 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"오!\" 노인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다. \"내 동생의 눈을 피해서요? 그렇지. 위층으로 올라와서 그 애를 기다리시겠소?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다.\"\n\n그는 무엇을 듣거나 말하든 마음속에서 굴리는 것만큼이나 느릿느릿 몸을 돌려, 좁은 계단을 앞장서 올라갔다. 집 안은 몹시 답답했고 건강에 해로운 냄새가 났다. 작은 계단 창은 제 집만큼이나 건강에 해로운 다른 집들의 뒤창을 들여다보았는데, 그 창들에서는 장대와 빨랫줄이 비죽 나와 흉한 빨래가 널려 있었으니, 마치 그 집 사람들이 옷가지를 낚으려고 낚시질을 하다가 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 형편없는 입질이나 몇 번 받았다는 듯했다. 뒤쪽 다락방 — 접이식 침상이 놓인 병약한 방으로, 어찌나 황급히 그리고 방금 막 접어 올렸던지 담요가 마치 끓어 넘치듯 삐져나와 뚜껑을 열어 둔 채였다 — 에는, 두 사람 몫의 커피와 토스트로 차린 채 마저 먹지 못한 아침 식사가 흔들거리는 탁자 위에 아무렇게나 흩어져 있었다.\n\n그곳엔 아무도 없었다. 노인은 잠시 생각하더니 패니가 달아났다고 혼자 중얼거리며, 그 애를 데려오려고 옆방으로 갔다. 방문객은 그녀가 안쪽에서 문을 붙들고 있고, 삼촌이 문을 열려 하자 \"안 돼요, 멍청이!\" 하는 날카로운 호통이 들리며 헐거운 양말과 플란넬 천이 언뜻 비치는 것을 보고, 그 아가씨가 옷을 채 갖춰 입지 못한 상태임을 알아챘다. 삼촌은 어떤 결론에도 이른 기색 없이 다시 발을 끌며 들어와 자기 의자에 앉더니, 불에 손을 쬐기 시작했다. 추워서도 아니었고, 추운지 아닌지 정신 차려 헤아릴 만한 생각이 있어서도 아니었다.\n\n\"내 동생을 어찌 보셨소, 선생?\" 그는 이윽고 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 깨닫고는, 그 짓을 그만두고 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 손을 뻗어 클라리넷 갑을 내리며 물었다.\n\n\"동생분께서 그토록 잘 지내시고 쾌활하신 것을 알게 되어,\" 아서가 무척 난감해하며 말했으니, 그의 생각은 눈앞의 형에게 쏠려 있었던 까닭이다. \"기뻤습니다.\"\n\n\"하!\" 노인이 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래, 그래!\"\n\n아서는 노인이 클라리넷 갑을 가지고 도대체 뭘 하려는 건지 의아했다. 노인은 그것을 가지고 아무것도 하려는 게 아니었다. 그는 한참 만에 그것이 코담배 작은 봉지(이것 역시 벽난로 선반 위에 있었다)가 아니라는 것을 알아채고는, 다시 제자리에 놓고 대신 코담배를 집어 들어, 한 줌으로 스스로를 달랬다. 그는 코담배를 즐기는 데서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 기력 없고 깡마르고 느렸으나, 그것을 즐기는 어떤 가느다란 흐름이 그의 가엾게 닳아빠진 신경 속에서, 눈가와 입가에 어려 일렁였다.\n\n\"에이미 말이오, 클레넘 씨. 그 애를 어찌 보시오?\"\n\n\"제가 그분에 관해 보고 생각한 모든 것에 깊은 인상을 받았습니다, 도릿 씨.\"\n\n\"내 동생은 에이미 없이는 아주 어찌할 바를 몰랐을 거요.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"우리 모두 에이미 없이는 어찌할 바를 몰랐을 거요. 아주 착한 아이지, 에이미는. 제 도리를 다하는 아이요.\"\n\n아서는 이 칭찬에서 어떤 익숙해진 어조를 들은 듯했다. 어젯밤 아버지에게서도 들었던, 마음속으로 항변하고 적의를 느끼게 했던 그 어조였다. 그들이 그녀에 대한 칭찬에 인색했다거나, 그녀가 그들을 위해 하는 일에 무감각했던 것은 아니다. 다만 그들이 자기들 처지의 다른 모든 것에 익숙해졌듯, 그녀에게도 게으르게 익숙해졌던 것이다. 그는, 그들이 날마다 그녀와 서로를, 또 그녀와 자기 자신을 견주어 볼 수단을 눈앞에 두고 있으면서도, 그녀가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리에 있는 것으로 여긴다고 느꼈다. 그녀의 이름이나 나이처럼, 그들 모두에 대해 그녀에게 본디 속하는 어떤 위치를 차지하고 있는 것으로 여긴다고 말이다. 그는, 그들이 그녀를 감옥의 공기에서 솟아올라 벗어난 사람으로 보는 것이 아니라, 그 공기에 속한 사람으로 — 막연히 자기들이 당연히 기대할 권리가 있는 무엇으로, 그 이상은 아닌 것으로 — 본다고 느꼈다.\n\n그녀의 삼촌은 다시 아침을 먹기 시작해, 커피에 적신 토스트를 우물거리며 손님을 까맣게 잊고 있다가, 세 번째 종이 울렸다. 저게 에이미라고 그가 말하고는, 그녀를 들이러 내려갔다. 방문객의 마음속에는 노인의 때 낀 손과 흙때로 닳은 얼굴과 쇠락한 모습이, 마치 그가 여전히 의자에 늘어져 있기라도 하다는 듯 생생한 그림으로 남았다.\n\n그녀가 그를 뒤따라 올라왔다. 여느 때처럼 수수한 옷차림에, 여느 때처럼 머뭇거리는 태도였다. 그녀의 입술이 조금 벌어져 있었으니, 마치 가슴이 평소보다 빨리 뛰기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"에이미, 클레넘 씨가,\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"한동안 너를 기다리고 계셨다.\"\n\n\"제가 외람되이 전갈을 보냈습니다.\"\n\n\"전갈은 받았어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침 저희 어머니 댁에 가시는 길입니까? 평소 시각이 지난 걸 보니 아닌 듯합니다만.\"\n\n\"오늘은 아니에요, 선생님. 오늘은 부르심이 없어요.\"\n\n\"가시는 방향이 어디든, 조금이나마 함께 걸어가도 괜찮겠습니까? 그러면 걸으면서 당신께 말씀을 드릴 수 있으니, 여기서 당신을 붙들지도 않고, 저 또한 여기 더 오래 머물러 폐를 끼치지도 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 난처해 보였으나, 그러시다면 그렇게 하시라고 말했다. 그는 지팡이를 어디 둔지 모르겠다는 시늉을 함으로써, 그녀에게 침상을 바로잡고 벽을 두드리는 언니의 성마른 노크에 응답하고 삼촌에게 나직이 한마디를 건넬 시간을 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 지팡이를 찾았고, 그들은 계단을 내려갔다. 그녀가 앞서고 그가 뒤따랐다. 삼촌은 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었는데, 아마 그들이 일 층에 닿기도 전에 그들을 잊었을 것이다.\n\n이때쯤 학교에 오고 있던 크리플즈 씨의 학생들은, 가방과 책으로 서로를 후려치던 아침 놀이를 멈추고, 더러운 딕을 찾아온 낯선 이를 제 눈을 한껏 동원해 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그들은 그 견디기 어려운 구경거리를 잠자코 견뎌 냈는데, 그 수수께끼의 방문객이 안전한 거리에 이르고 나서야 비로소 자갈과 고함을 터뜨렸고, 또한 욕설 섞인 춤도 추었으며, 어느 모로 보나 어찌나 많은 야만적 의식을 치르며 화평의 담뱃대를 파묻었던지, 만일 크리플즈 씨가 전투 화장을 한 크리플웨이부 부족의 추장이었더라도 그들이 받은 교육에 그보다 더 알맞은 본때를 보이기는 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n이 경의에 찬 의식 한가운데에서, 아서 클레넘 씨가 리틀 도릿에게 팔을 내밀었고, 리틀 도릿이 그 팔을 잡았다. \"철교로 가실까요?\" 그가 말했다. \"거기라면 거리의 소음에서 벗어날 수 있으니까요.\" 리틀 도릿은 그러시다면 좋다고 대답하고는, 이윽고 크리플즈 씨의 아이들을 \"언짢게 여기지 마시기\"를 바란다고 조심스레 말했다. 자기도 비록 변변찮으나마 크리플즈 씨의 야간 학원에서 교육을 받았기 때문이라고 했다. 그는 더없이 흔쾌한 마음으로, 크리플즈 씨의 아이들을 영혼 깊은 바닥에서부터 용서한다고 대답했다. 이리하여 크리플즈는 자기도 모르는 사이에 그들 사이의 의전관이 되어, 만일 그들이 보 내시의 황금기에 살았고 그가 바로 그 일을 위해 사두마차에서 내려섰더라도 해내지 못했을 만큼, 두 사람을 한결 자연스럽게 가까워지게 해 주었다.\n\n아침나절은 여전히 돌풍이 일었고 거리는 비참하리만치 진창이었으나, 그들이 철교를 향해 걷는 동안 비는 내리지 않았다. 그 작은 사람이 그의 눈에는 어찌나 어려 보였던지, 그녀에게 말을 걸지는 않더라도 마치 그녀가 어린아이이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 생각하는 자신을 발견하는 순간들이 있었다. 어쩌면 그녀의 눈에 비친 그가 그녀가 그의 눈에 어려 보이는 만큼이나 늙어 보였는지도 모른다.\n\n\"어젯밤 그렇게 불편을 겪으시고 갇히기까지 하셨다니 죄송해요, 선생님. 무척 안된 일이었어요.\"\n\n아무것도 아니라고 그가 대답했다. 아주 좋은 잠자리에서 잤다고 했다.\n\n\"오, 그래요!\" 그녀가 얼른 말했다. 그 커피점에는 훌륭한 잠자리가 있으리라 믿는다고 했다. 그는 그 커피점이 그녀에게는 자못 위풍당당한 호텔이며, 그녀가 그 명성을 소중히 여긴다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"무척 비싼 곳이라고 들었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"하지만 거기서는 정말 훌륭한 만찬을 들 수 있다고 아버지가 말씀하셨어요. 그리고 술도요.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"거기 가 보신 적이 있나요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니요! 더운물을 가지러 부엌에 들어가 본 것뿐이에요.\"\n\n그 으리으리한 시설, 곧 마셜시 호텔의 사치에 대해 일종의 경외감을 품은 채 자라났다는 것을 생각해 보라!\n\n\"어젯밤에 여쭈었지요,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"어떻게 저희 어머니와 알게 되셨는지. 어머니가 사람을 보내 부르기 전에 그 이름을 들어 본 적이 있나요?\"\n\"아니요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"아버님께서는 들어 보신 적이 있다고 생각하나요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님.\"\n\n그가 그녀의 눈을 마주쳤을 때, 그를 향해 들린 그 눈에는 어찌나 큰 의아함이 담겨 있던지(눈이 마주치자 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 다시 움츠러들었다), 그는 이렇게 말할 필요를 느꼈다.\n\n\"제가 여쭙는 데에는 이유가 있는데, 그걸 잘 설명드릴 수는 없습니다. 하지만 그것이 당신께 조금이라도 놀라움이나 불안을 일으킬 성질의 것이라고는 결코 짐작하지 마십시오. 정반대입니다. 그러면 아버님의 생애 어느 때에도 클레넘이라는 제 이름이 그분께 친숙했던 적이 없다고 생각하시는군요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 선생님.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 말투에서, 그녀가 그 벌어진 입술로 자기를 흘끔 올려다보고 있음을 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 다시 그녀를 당황하게 해 가슴을 한층 빨리 뛰게 하기보다는, 앞을 바라보았다.\n\n이리하여 그들은 철교에 다다랐는데, 으르렁대던 거리에 비하면 그곳은 마치 탁 트인 들판이기라도 한 듯 고요했다. 바람이 거칠게 불었고, 젖은 돌풍이 덜컹거리며 그들 곁을 스쳐 지나가, 길과 보도의 웅덩이를 훑어 그 물을 강물 속으로 뿌려 댔다. 납빛 하늘에서는 구름이 사납게 내달렸고, 매연과 안개가 그 뒤를 쫓아 내달렸으며, 어두운 조수가 같은 방향으로 거세고 세차게 흘렀다. 리틀 도릿은 하늘이 지으신 피조물 가운데 가장 작고 가장 조용하고 가장 약한 존재로 보였다.\n\n\"마차에 태워 드리지요.\" 클레넘이, 하마터면 \"가엾은 아이여\" 하고 덧붙일 뻔하며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 서둘러 사양하면서, 젖든 마르든 자기에게는 별 차이가 없다고, 어떤 날씨든 다니는 데 익숙하다고 말했다. 그는 그것이 사실임을 알았기에 한층 더 연민에 가슴이 저렸다. 그의 곁에 선 그 가냘픈 모습이, 그러한 안식의 처소를 향해 축축하고 어둡고 사나운 밤거리를 밤마다 헤쳐 가는 것을 떠올리니 말이다.\n\n\"어젯밤 제게 그토록 다정하게 말씀해 주셨고, 게다가 나중에 알고 보니 제 아버지께 그토록 너그러이 대해 주셨더군요. 그래서 저는 선생님의 전갈을 뿌리칠 수가 없었어요. 그저 고맙다는 인사를 드리기 위해서라도요. 더구나 선생님께 꼭 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있었거든요—\" 그녀는 머뭇거리며 떨었고, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐으나 흘러내리지는 않았다.\n\n\"제게 말씀하시려던 것이—?\"\n\n\"제 아버지를 오해하지 말아 주십사 하는 거예요. 그분을 저 문밖의 다른 사람들을 판단하듯 판단하지 말아 주세요, 선생님. 그분은 거기 너무 오래 계셨어요! 저는 그분이 밖에 계신 걸 본 적이 없지만, 그분이 그 뒤로 어떤 점에서는 달라지셨으리라는 건 이해할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"제 생각은 결코 그분께 부당하거나 모질지 않을 것입니다. 믿어 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 해서,\" 그녀가 한층 자랑스러운 기색으로 말했으니, 자기가 그분을 저버리는 것처럼 보일지 모른다는 불안이 분명 그녀에게 스며든 까닭이었다. \"그분께서 스스로 부끄러워하실 일이 있다거나, 제가 그분 때문에 부끄러워할 일이 있다는 건 아니에요. 그분은 그저 이해받기를 바라실 뿐이에요. 저는 다만 그분의 삶이 공정하게 기억되기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 그분이 하신 말씀은 모두 사실이었어요. 모두 그분이 말씀하신 그대로 일어난 일이에요. 그분은 무척 존경받으세요. 안에 들어오는 사람은 누구나 그분과 아는 사이가 되기를 기뻐해요. 그분은 다른 누구보다도 더 떠받들어지세요. 마셜 소장보다도 훨씬 더 받들어지신다고요.\"\n\n자존심이 무고한 적이 있다면, 리틀 도릿이 아버지를 두고 으스대게 될 때 그것은 무고했다.\n\n\"그분의 몸가짐이 참된 신사의 것이며 본받을 만하다는 말을 흔히들 해요. 그 안에서 그분 같은 분은 본 적이 없지만, 그분이 다른 모든 사람보다 뛰어나시다는 건 누구나 인정하는 바예요. 사람들이 그분께 선물을 드리는 것도, 그분이 궁핍하시다는 걸 알아서이기도 하지만, 그만큼이나 이런 까닭에서예요. 궁핍하시다고 해서 그분을 탓할 수는 없어요, 가엾은 분. 사반세기를 감옥에 있으면서 형편이 넉넉할 사람이 어디 있겠어요!\"\n\n그녀의 말에 담긴 그 애정, 억눌린 눈물에 담긴 그 연민, 그녀 안에 깃든 그 위대한 충실의 영혼, 그를 둘러싸고 거짓된 광채를 비추는 그 빛이 어찌 그리도 진실했던가!\n\n\"제가 집이 어디인지 숨기는 게 상책이라고 여겨 왔다면, 그건 그분을 부끄러워해서가 아니에요. 천만에요! 또 그곳 자체를 사람들이 짐작할 만큼 그렇게 부끄러워하는 것도 아니고요. 거기 온다고 해서 나쁜 사람들인 건 아니에요. 저는 선량하고 끈기 있고 정직한 사람들이 불운 때문에 거기 오는 걸 숱하게 봐 왔어요. 그들은 거의 다 서로에게 친절해요. 그리고 거기서 조용하고 편안한 시간을 많이 보냈다는 걸 제가 잊는다면 그건 참으로 배은망덕한 일일 거예요. 제가 아주 갓난아기였을 때 거기엔 저를 무척이나 아껴 준 더없이 좋은 벗이 있었고, 거기서 가르침을 받았고, 거기서 일했고, 거기서 곤히 잠들었으니까요. 이 모든 것이 있고 나서도 그곳에 작은 애착 하나 품지 않는다면, 그건 거의 비겁하고 모진 짓이 아닐까 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀는 마음에 가득 찬 충실함을 토로하고 나서, 새 벗에게 호소하듯 눈을 들어 겸손히 말했다. \"이렇게 많은 말을 할 생각은 아니었어요. 이 일을 입에 올린 적은 전에 딱 한 번뿐이었고요. 하지만 이렇게 말하고 나니 어젯밤보다 일이 더 바로잡힌 것 같네요. 저는 선생님께서 저를 따라오지 않으셨더라면 했다고 말씀드렸지요. 이젠 그렇게까지 바라지는 않아요. 다만 선생님께서 혹시—정말이지 그렇게 바라지는 않아요. 제가 너무 두서없이 말씀드려서 선생님이—선생님이 저를 거의 알아들으실 수 없게 되었다면 몰라도요. 그런데 그렇게 되었을까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n그는 결코 그렇지 않다고 더없이 진실하게 말해 주었고, 그녀와 매서운 바람과 비 사이에 자기 몸을 세워, 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 막아 주었다.\n\n\"이제 허락을 얻은 듯하니,\" 그가 말했다. \"아버님에 관해 조금 더 여쭙겠습니다. 채권자가 많습니까?\"\n\n\"오! 아주 많아요.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 그분을 지금 그 자리에 붙들어 두는, 구금시킨 채권자들 말입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 네! 아주 많아요.\"\n\n\"제게 말씀해 주실 수 있겠습니까—물론 당신이 모르시면 다른 데서 알아낼 수도 있겠지만—그들 가운데 가장 영향력 있는 자가 누구인지요?\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 잠시 생각하더니, 오래전에 타이트 배너클 씨가 대단한 권세를 지닌 사람이라는 말을 듣곤 했다고 말했다. 그는 위원이거나, 위원회거나, 관리인이거나, \"뭐 그런 거\"였다. 그녀가 생각하기로 그는 그로스브너 광장에, 아니면 그 아주 가까운 곳에 살았다. 그는 정부 소속으로—에두름국에서 높은 자리에 있었다. 그녀는 어린 시절에, 그로스브너 광장, 아니면 그 아주 가까운 곳에 사는 이 무시무시한 타이트 배너클 씨와 에두름국의 위력에 대해 어떤 끔찍한 인상을 품게 된 듯했으니, 그를 입에 올릴 때 그것이 그녀를 완전히 짓눌렀다.\n\n'이 타이트 배너클 씨를 만나 본들,' 아서는 생각했다. '해 될 건 없겠지.'\n\n그 생각은 그녀의 영민함이 가로채지 못할 만큼 그리 차분하게 떠오르지 못했다. \"아!\" 리틀 도릿이, 한평생 겪어 온 온화한 절망을 담아 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"한때는 제 가엾은 아버지를 빼내려고 생각한 사람이 많았지만, 그게 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지 선생님은 모르세요.\"\n\n그녀는 그 순간 수줍어하는 것도 잊은 채, 그가 끌어올릴 꿈을 꾸는 그 가라앉은 난파선으로부터 그를 진심으로 물러서게 하려 했다. 그러면서 그녀는 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈은—참을성 있는 얼굴, 가냘픈 몸매, 수수한 옷차림, 그리고 바람과 비와 어우러져—그가 그녀를 돕겠다는 목적에서 결코 돌아서게 하지 못했다.\n\n\"설령 그게 가능하다 해도,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 와선 결코 가능하지 않지만요—아버지가 어디서 사실 수 있겠어요. 어떻게 사실 수 있겠어요? 저는 종종 생각했어요. 그런 변화가 온다 해도, 이제 와선 그게 아버지께 조금도 도움이 안 될지 모른다고요. 밖에서는 사람들이 거기서만큼 아버지를 좋게 여기지 않을지도 몰라요. 밖에서는 거기서만큼 아버지를 너그러이 대하지 않을지도 몰라요. 아버지 자신도 거기 생활에 맞는 만큼 밖의 생활에는 맞지 않으실지도 모르고요.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 처음으로 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 참지 못했다. 그토록 부지런할 때 그가 지켜보았던 가늘고 작은 두 손이, 서로를 맞잡으며 떨렸다.\n\n\"제가 돈을 조금 번다는 것, 패니가 돈을 조금 번다는 것을 아시는 것조차 아버지껜 새로운 괴로움이 될 거예요. 보세요, 아버지는 거기 속절없이 갇혀 계신 처지라, 저희를 그토록 걱정하시거든요. 정말 좋은, 좋은 아버지예요!\"\n\n그는 그 짧은 감정의 북받침이 지나가기를 기다린 뒤에야 입을 열었다. 그것은 곧 지나갔다. 그녀는 자기 자신을 생각하거나, 누구든 자기 감정으로 번거롭게 하는 데 익숙하지 않았다. 그가 다만, 매연이 무겁게 굽이치는 도시의 지붕과 굴뚝 더미를, 강 위의 돛대들의 황야를, 강기슭의 첨탑들의 황야를—폭풍의 안개 속에 흐릿하게 뒤섞인 그것들을—흘끔 바라보고 시선을 돌렸을 때, 그녀는 어머니의 방에서 바늘을 놀리고 있기라도 한 듯 다시금 차분해져 있었다.\n\n\"오라버니가 풀려나면 기쁘시겠지요?\"\n\n\"오, 정말, 정말 기쁠 거예요, 선생님!\"\n\n\"그럼 적어도 그분에 대해서는 희망을 가져 봅시다. 어젯밤 친구가 한 분 있다고 말씀하셨지요?\"\n\n그의 이름은 플로니시라고 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n그래서 플로니시는 어디 사는가? 플로니시는 블리딩 하트 야드에 살았다. 그는 \"그저 미장이일 뿐\"이라고 리틀 도릿이 말했는데, 그가 플로니시에 대해 높은 사회적 기대를 품지 않도록 미리 일러두려는 것이었다. 그는 블리딩 하트 야드의 맨 끝 집에 살았고, 작은 출입문 위에 그의 이름이 적혀 있었다.\n\n아서는 그 주소를 적어 두고, 그녀에게 자기 주소를 건넸다. 그는 이제 당장 하려던 일을 다 한 셈이었다. 다만 그녀가 자기를 의지하도록 남겨 두고 싶었고, 그녀가 그 마음을 소중히 간직하겠다는 약속 같은 것을 받고 싶었다.\n\n\"여기 친구가 하나 있군요!\" 그가 수첩을 챙겨 넣으며 말했다. \"당신을 돌려보내 드리면서—돌아가시는 거지요?\"\n\n\"오, 네! 곧장 집으로 가요.\"\n\n\"—당신을 돌려보내 드리면서,\" 집이라는 말이 그에게 거슬렸다. \"한 가지 청을 드릴게요. 당신에게 또 다른 친구가 하나 있다고 스스로 믿어 주십시오. 더는 다짐도 두지 않고, 더 말씀도 드리지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"선생님은 제게 참으로 친절하세요. 더는 필요 없어요, 정말.\"\n\n그들은 비참한 진창 거리를 지나, 가난하고 초라한 가게들 사이를 걸어 돌아왔고, 가난한 동네에 으레 있기 마련인 더러운 행상꾼 무리에게 떠밀렸다. 이 가까운 길에는 다섯 감각 가운데 어느 하나에라도 즐거울 만한 것이라곤 없었다. 그렇지만 클레넘에게는, 이 작고 가냘프고 야무진 사람을 팔에 의지하게 하고 걷는 것이, 흔한 비와 진창과 소음 속을 지나는 흔한 통행은 아니었다. 그녀가 그의 눈에 얼마나 어려 보였는지, 또는 그가 그녀의 눈에 얼마나 늙어 보였는지, 또는 그들의 이야기가 운명적으로 얽혀 들기 시작하는 그 첫머리에 두 사람이 서로에게 어떤 비밀이었는지는 여기서 중요하지 않다. 그는 그녀가 이런 풍경 속에서 태어나 자랐고, 익숙하면서도 어울리지 않는 모습으로 지금 그 속을 움츠리며 지나가는 것을 생각했다. 그는 그녀가 삶의 누추한 궁핍과 오래 친숙해 온 것을, 그리고 그녀의 순결함을, 남을 위하는 그녀의 마음씀을, 그녀의 어린 나이를, 그리고 그녀의 어린아이 같은 모습을 생각했다.\n\n감옥이 서 있는 하이 스트리트로 접어들었을 때, 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. \"작은 엄마, 작은 엄마!\" 리틀 도릿이 멈춰 서서 뒤를 돌아보자, 어떤 이상한 종류의 흥분한 형체가 그들에게 부딪쳐 왔고(여전히 \"작은 엄마\"를 외치면서), 넘어지면서 감자가 가득 든 큰 바구니의 내용물을 진창에 쏟아 흩뜨려 놓았다.\n\n\"오, 매기,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"어쩜 이렇게 덤벙대는 아이람!\"\n\n매기는 다치지 않았고 곧장 몸을 일으키더니 감자를 줍기 시작했는데, 리틀 도릿과 아서 클레넘도 함께 거들었다. 매기는 감자는 아주 조금 줍고 진흙은 잔뜩 주워 담았으나, 어쨌든 다 거두어들여 바구니에 담았다. 그러고 나서 매기는 진흙투성이 얼굴을 숄로 문질러 댔고, 그것을 순결의 본보기인 양 클레넘 씨에게 내보임으로써, 그로 하여금 그녀의 생김새가 어떤지 보게 해 주었다.\n\n그녀는 스물여덟 살쯤으로, 뼈대가 굵고, 이목구비가 크고, 손발이 크고, 눈이 크고, 머리카락은 없었다. 그 큰 눈은 맑고 거의 색이 없었으며, 빛에 거의 영향을 받지 않고 부자연스러우리만치 가만히 멈춰 있는 듯했다. 또한 그녀의 얼굴에는 맹인의 얼굴에서 보이는, 골똘히 귀 기울이는 표정이 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 맹인이 아니었으니, 그런대로 쓸 만한 눈이 하나 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 지독히 추하지는 않았으나, 그것이 추함을 면한 것은 오직 미소 덕분이었다. 마음씨 좋은 미소로 그 자체로는 보기 좋았지만, 늘 그 자리에 머물러 있는 까닭에 가엾어 보이게 했다. 늘 펄럭거리는 불투명한 주름 장식이 잔뜩 달린 커다란 흰 모자가 매기의 대머리를 가려 주는 구실을 했는데, 그 탓에 그녀의 낡은 검은 보닛이 머리 위에 제자리를 지키기란 무척 어려워서, 보닛은 집시의 갓난아기처럼 그녀의 목에 매달려 있었다. 그 나머지 초라한 옷이 무엇으로 만들어졌는지는 잡화상 위원회나 되어야 알아낼 수 있었을 테지만, 전체적으로는 해초와 매우 흡사했고, 군데군데 거대한 찻잎이 박혀 있었다. 그녀의 숄은 특히 오래 우려낸 찻잎과 똑 닮아 보였다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 \"이분이 누구인지 여쭤도 될까요?\" 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 리틀 도릿을 바라보았다. 여전히 그녀를 작은 엄마라 부르며 이 매기가 손을 쓰다듬기 시작한 리틀 도릿이 말로 대답했다(그들은 출입문 아래에 있었는데, 감자 대부분이 그리로 굴러들어 있었다).\n\n\"이 애는 매기예요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"매기예요, 선생님.\" 소개받은 당사자가 따라 말했다. \"작은 엄마!\"\n\n\"이 애는 손녀딸이에요—\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"손녀딸이에요.\" 매기가 따라 말했다.\n\n\"오래전에 돌아가신 제 옛 유모의요. 매기, 너 몇 살이지?\"\n\n\"열 살이에요, 엄마.\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n\"이 애가 얼마나 착한지 선생님은 모르실 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 한없는 다정함으로 말했다.\n\n\"착한 건 이 애예요.\" 매기가 그 대명사를 가장 의미심장한 방식으로 자기 자신에게서 작은 엄마에게로 옮기며 따라 말했다.\n\n\"또 얼마나 영리한지요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"이 애는 누구 못지않게 심부름을 잘해요.\" 매기가 웃었다. \"그리고 잉글랜드 은행만큼이나 믿을 만하고요.\" 매기가 웃었다. \"온전히 제 힘으로 벌어 살아가요. 온전히 말이에요, 선생님!\" 리틀 도릿이 한층 낮고 의기양양한 어조로 말했다. \"정말 그래요!\"\n\n\"이 아이의 내력은 어떻게 됩니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"생각해 봐, 매기!\" 리틀 도릿이 그녀의 커다란 두 손을 잡아 마주 치며 말했다. \"수천 마일 떨어진 데서 오신 신사분이 네 내력을 알고 싶어 하셔!\"\n\n\"내 내력이요?\" 매기가 외쳤다. \"작은 엄마.\"\n\n\"이 애 말은 저를 가리키는 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 당황하며 말했다. \"이 애는 저를 무척 따라요. 이 애의 늙은 할머니가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 이 애에게 친절하지 못했거든요. 그렇지, 매기?\"\n\n매기는 고개를 젓더니, 왼손을 꼭 쥐어 술잔 모양을 만들어 거기서 무언가를 들이켜는 시늉을 하며 \"진.\"이라고 말했다. 그러고는 보이지 않는 아이를 때리는 시늉을 하며 \"빗자루 손잡이랑 부지깽이.\"라고 말했다.\n\n\"매기가 열 살이었을 때,\" 리틀 도릿이 말하면서 매기의 얼굴을 살폈다. \"이 애는 심한 열병을 앓았어요, 선생님. 그리고 그 뒤로 더는 한 살도 자라지 못했어요.\"\n\n\"열 살이에요.\" 매기가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그런데 얼마나 좋은 병원이었는데요! 그렇게 편안했어요, 안 그래요? 오, 정말 좋았어요. 그렇게 천국 같은 곳이라니!\"\n\n\"이 애는 그 전엔 한 번도 편안한 적이 없었어요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 잠깐 아서 쪽으로 돌아서며 나직이 말했다. \"그래서 늘 그 이야기로 빠져들곤 해요.\"\n\n\"거긴 그렇게 좋은 침대가 있었다고요!\" 매기가 외쳤다. \"그렇게 좋은 레모네이드도요! 그렇게 좋은 오렌지도요! 그렇게 맛있는 죽이랑 포도주도요! 그렇게 좋은 닭고기도요! 오, 거긴 가서 머물기에 정말 즐거운 곳 아녜요!\"\n\n\"그래서 매기는 거기 있을 수 있는 데까지 오래 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 아이에게 옛이야기를 들려주는 아까의 어조로 말했으니, 매기의 귀를 위해 마련된 어조였다. \"그리고 마침내 더는 거기 있을 수 없게 되자, 밖으로 나왔지요. 그런데 이 애는 아무리 오래 살아도 열 살을 넘기지 못하게 되었기 때문에—\"\n\n\"아무리 오래 살아도요.\" 매기가 따라 말했다.\n\n\"—그리고 무척 약했기 때문에—정말이지 어찌나 약했던지, 한번 웃기 시작하면 스스로 멈출 수가 없었거든요—그게 참 안된 일이었지요—\"\n\n(매기가 별안간 자못 엄숙해졌다.)\n\n\"—할머니는 이 애를 어찌해야 할지 몰랐고, 몇 해 동안 정말 모질게 대했어요. 그러다 마침내 세월이 흐르면서, 매기는 스스로를 가다듬으려고, 아주 야무지고 아주 부지런하려고 애쓰기 시작했어요. 그래서 차츰차츰 마음대로 드나드는 게 허락되었고, 제 한 몸 건사할 만큼의 일거리도 얻게 되어, 정말로 제 힘으로 살아가요. 그리고 이게,\" 리틀 도릿이 그 커다란 두 손을 다시 마주 치며 말했다. \"매기가 아는 한, 매기의 내력이랍니다!\"\n\n아! 그러나 아서는, 작은 엄마라는 말을 한 번도 들어 보지 못했더라도, 그 작고 야윈 손을 쓰다듬는 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없었더라도, 지금 색 없는 눈에 고인 눈물을 볼 눈이 없었더라도, 그 어설픈 웃음을 가로막은 흐느낌을 들을 귀가 없었더라도, 그 내력의 온전함에 무엇이 빠져 있는지 알았을 것이다. 바람과 비가 휘파람 소리를 내며 지나가는 그 더러운 출입문과, 다시 쏟아지거나 거두어지기를 기다리는 진흙투성이 감자 바구니는, 그가 이런 빛에 비추어 그것을 돌이켜 볼 때면, 결코 실제 그것이었던 흔한 구멍으로는 보이지 않았다. 결코, 결코!\n\n그들의 산책은 거의 끝나 가고 있었고, 이제 그들은 그것을 마무리하려고 출입문에서 나왔다. 매기는 한사코, 목적지에 닿기 전에 식료품점 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서서 자기 학식을 뽐내야겠다고 했다. 그녀는 그런대로 글을 읽을 줄 알았고, 가격표에 적힌 통통한 숫자를 짚어 냈는데, 대체로 정확했다. 그녀는 또 우리 혼합차를 잡숴 보세요, 우리 가정용 홍차를 잡숴 보세요, 화차의 으뜸으로 어떤 경쟁에도 도전하는 우리 오렌지 향 페코를 잡숴 보세요 하는 갖가지 자선적인 권유며, 엉터리 가게와 불순물 섞은 물건을 조심하라는 갖가지 경고를 더듬더듬 읽어 나갔는데, 실패보다 성공이 훨씬 더 많았다. 매기가 하나 맞힐 때마다 즐거움이 리틀 도릿의 얼굴에 발그레한 빛을 띄우는 것을 보고, 그는 비와 바람이 지칠 때까지라도 거기 서서 그 식료품점 진열창으로 도서관 하나를 만들 수 있을 것 같았다.\n\n마침내 마당이 그들을 맞아들였고, 거기서 그는 리틀 도릿에게 작별을 고했다. 늘 작아 보이던 그녀였지만, 큰 아이를 거느린 작은 엄마인 그녀가 마셜시 수위실 통로로 들어가는 것을 그가 보았을 때, 그녀는 그 어느 때보다도 작아 보였다.\n\n새장 문이 열렸고, 갇혀 길러진 그 작은 새가 얌전히 퍼덕이며 들어가자, 그는 그 문이 다시 닫히는 것을 보았다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 자리를 떠났다.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. Containing the whole Science of Government\n\n에두름국은 (말하지 않아도 누구나 알듯이) 정부 아래에서 가장 중요한 부서였다. 어떤 종류의 공무도 에두름국의 묵인 없이는 어느 때고 이루어질 수 없었다. 그 손가락은 가장 커다란 공공의 파이 속에도, 가장 작은 공공의 타르트 속에도 들어가 있었다. 에두름국의 명시적 권한 없이는 가장 명백한 옳음을 행하는 것도, 가장 명백한 그름을 바로잡는 것도 똑같이 불가능했다. 또 한 번의 화약 음모 사건이 도화선에 불을 붙이기 삼십 분 전에 발각되었다 한들, 에두름국 측에서 위원회를 열대여섯 차례 열고, 회의록을 반 부셸쯤 작성하고, 공문 비망록을 몇 자루 쌓고, 비문법적인 서신으로 가문 납골당 하나를 가득 채우기 전까지는, 그 누구도 의회를 구해 낼 정당한 자격이 없었을 것이다.\n\n이 영광스러운 기관은, 한 나라를 통치하는 그 어려운 기술에 깃든 단 하나의 숭고한 원리가 정치가들에게 처음으로 또렷이 계시되었을 때, 일찌감치 그 마당에 나와 있었다. 그 빛나는 계시를 연구하고 그 찬란한 영향력을 공무 전반에 두루 미치게 하는 데 가장 앞장섰던 것이다. 무엇이 행해져야 하든, 에두름국은 그것을 지각하는 기술에서, 즉 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가 하는 기술에서, 모든 공공 부서를 앞질렀다.\n\n이 섬세한 지각력을 통하여, 그것을 한결같이 포착해 내는 그 수완을 통하여, 그리고 늘 그것에 따라 움직이는 그 천재성을 통하여, 에두름국은 모든 공공 부서를 압도하는 자리에 올랐다. 그리고 공공의 형편은 — 지금 그 모양이 된 것이다.\n\n어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가가, 에두름국을 둘러싼 모든 공공 부서와 직업적 정치가들의 위대한 연구이자 목표였던 것은 사실이다. 어떤 일이 행해질 필요가 있다고 주창한 덕분에 권좌에 오른 새 총리와 새 정부가 하나같이, 들어서기가 무섭게 그것을 어떻게 하면 하지 않을 것인가를 알아내는 데 온 역량을 쏟았던 것도 사실이다. 총선이 끝나는 순간부터, 그것이 행해지지 않았다고 유세장에서 핏대를 올리던 모든 당선자가, 반대 진영의 그 존경할 만한 신사의 벗들에게 탄핵을 무릅쓰고서라도 왜 그것이 행해지지 않았는지 답하라고 다그치던 자가, 그것은 반드시 행해져야 한다고 단언하던 자가, 그것이 행해지도록 하겠노라고 스스로 맹세하던 자가, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 궁리하기 시작했던 것도 사실이다. 회기 내내 상하 양원의 토론이 한결같이, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가에 대한 장기간의 심의로 흘러갔던 것도 사실이다. 그러한 회기의 개회식에서 국왕의 연설이 사실상 이렇게 말했던 것도 사실이다. \"경들이여 그리고 신사 여러분, 그대들에게는 처리해야 할 상당한 분량의 일이 있으니, 부디 각자의 의사당으로 물러가, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 논의해 주기 바란다.\" 그러한 회기의 폐회식에서 국왕의 연설이 사실상 이렇게 말했던 것도 사실이다. \"경들이여 그리고 신사 여러분, 그대들은 수고로운 여러 달에 걸쳐 지극한 충성심과 애국심으로 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 숙고하여, 마침내 답을 찾아내었도다. 이제 (정치적인 것이 아니라 자연적인) 수확에 대한 섭리의 가호를 빌며, 나는 그대들을 해산하노라.\" 이 모든 것이 사실이지만, 에두름국은 그것을 넘어섰다.\n\n왜냐하면 에두름국은 날마다 기계처럼, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가라는, 이 경이롭고도 전능한 통치술의 수레바퀴를 줄곧 돌리고 있었기 때문이다. 왜냐하면 에두름국은, 그것을 하려 드는 분별없는 공복이든, 어떤 놀라운 우연으로 그것을 해 버릴 막연한 위험에 처한 듯 보이는 공복이든, 가차 없이 덮쳐, 회의록 한 건과 비망록 한 건과 지시 서한 한 통으로 그를 소멸시켜 버렸기 때문이다. 에두름국으로 하여금 점차 모든 일에 관여하게 만든 것은 바로 이 국가적 효율의 정신이었다. 기계공, 자연철학자, 군인, 선원, 청원자, 진정인, 고충을 품은 사람들, 고충을 막고자 하는 사람들, 고충을 바로잡고자 하는 사람들, 청탁을 부리는 사람들, 청탁에 휘둘린 사람들, 공로를 세우고도 포상받지 못하는 사람들, 과실을 저지르고도 처벌받지 않는 사람들 — 이 모두가 가리지 않고 에두름국의 대판 종이 아래로 쑤셔 넣어졌다.\n\n수많은 사람이 에두름국에서 사라져 갔다. 억울한 일을 당했거나 공공의 복리를 위한 계획을 품은 불운한 자들이 (애초에 후자의 처지에 놓이기보다는 차라리 억울한 일이라도 당하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 후자야말로 억울한 일을 확실히 당하게 되는 그 쓰디쓴 영국식 비방이었으니), 더디게 흐르는 세월과 고뇌 속에서 다른 공공 부서들을 무사히 통과해 온 자들이, 규정에 따라 이 부서에서는 윽박질리고 저 부서에서는 우롱당하고 또 다른 부서에서는 회피당해 온 자들이, 마침내 에두름국으로 회부되어서는 다시는 환한 대낮의 빛 속으로 나타나지 않았다. 위원회가 그들을 두고 회합했고, 비서들이 그들에 관한 회의록을 작성했고, 위원들이 그들에 관해 떠들어 댔고, 서기들이 그들을 등록하고 기입하고 대조하고 점검표에 표시했으며, 그러는 사이 그들은 녹아 사라졌다. 요컨대 이 나라의 모든 사무는 에두름국을 거쳐 갔다. 단, 결코 그곳에서 나오지 못한 사무만은 예외였으니, 그 이름은 군단이었다.\n\n* 군단: 신약성경에서 수많은 악귀의 무리를 가리키는 말(\"내 이름은 군단이니 우리가 많음이라\").\n이따금 노한 영혼들이 에두름국을 공격했다. 이따금 그에 관한 의회 질의가 제기되었고, 심지어 통치의 진짜 비방이란 어떻게 하면 그것을 할 것인가라고 믿을 만큼 천박하고 무지한 선동가들이 그에 관한 의회 동의안을 발의하거나 발의하겠다고 으름장을 놓기도 했다. 그러면 에두름국을 변호하는 일이 소관 업무인 고귀한 경이나 존경할 만한 신사가 주머니에 오렌지 하나를 넣고서, 그 기회를 본격적인 한판 승부의 날로 삼곤 했다. 그러면 그는 탁자를 탁 치며 그 의사당으로 내려와, 그 존경할 만한 신사와 한 치도 물러섬 없이 맞붙곤 했다. 그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국이 이 문제에서 비난받을 데가 없을 뿐 아니라 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 하늘 끝까지 추켜올려져 마땅하다고 일러 주곤 했다. 그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국은 한결같이 옳고 전적으로 옳지만 이 문제에서만큼 옳았던 적은 없다고 일러 주곤 했다. 그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국을 가만히 내버려 두고 이 문제에 결코 손대지 않았더라면 그의 명예에, 그의 신망에, 그의 안목에, 그의 양식에, 그리고 상투적 표현 사전 절반에 더 보탬이 되었을 것이라고 일러 주곤 했다. 그러면 그는 한쪽 눈으로는 의장석 난간 아래에 앉아 있는 에두름국 측 코치나 입막음꾼을 살피면서, 이 문제에 관한 에두름국 측 진술로 그 존경할 만한 신사를 박살 내곤 했다. 그리고 늘 둘 중 하나가 일어났으니, 즉 에두름국이 할 말이 없으면서도 그것을 말하거나, 아니면 할 말이 있되 그 고귀한 경이나 존경할 만한 신사가 그 절반은 더듬거리고 나머지 절반은 잊어버리거나 하는 것이었음에도, 에두름국은 만사를 너그럽게 받아 주는 다수에 의해 늘 흠 하나 없다는 표결을 받았다.\n\n이러한 성격의 오랜 이력 덕분에 에두름국은 정치가의 요람이 되어 있었으니, 여러 근엄한 경들이 오로지 에두름국의 수장으로서 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 연마했다는 그 이유 하나만으로, 그야말로 이 세상 것 같지 않은 사무의 귀재라는 명성을 얻기에 이르렀다. 그 신전의 하급 사제와 시동들로 말하자면, 이 모든 일의 결과로 그들은 두 부류로 나뉘어, 막내 사환에 이르기까지, 에두름국을 무엇이든 마음대로 할 절대적 권리를 지닌 하늘이 내린 기관으로 신봉하거나, 아니면 완전한 불신 속으로 도피하여 그것을 명백한 골칫거리로 여기거나 했다.\n\n배너클 일가는 한동안 에두름국 운영을 거들어 왔다. 사실 타이트 배너클 가계는 으레 자기들이 그 방면에 기득권을 지녔다고 여겼고, 다른 어느 가문이 그에 관해 이러쿵저러쿵 많은 말을 하면 못마땅해했다. 배너클가는 지체 높은 가문이었고, 또 매우 큰 가문이었다. 그들은 온갖 관청에 흩어져 있었고 온갖 공직을 차지하고 있었다. 국가가 배너클가에 막대한 빚을 지고 있거나, 아니면 배너클가가 국가에 막대한 빚을 지고 있거나 둘 중 하나였다. 어느 쪽인지는 만장일치로 정해지지 않았으니, 배너클가는 배너클가대로, 국가는 국가대로 제 의견이 있었다.\n\n지금 문제 삼는 시기에, 에두름국 수장 자리에 앉은 정치가가 어떤 떠돌이 같은 자가 신문 지면에서 그를 향해 창을 겨누는 통에 안장 위에서 다소 불편하게 흔들릴 때면 으레 그를 지도하거나 입막음 거리를 채워 주던 그 타이트 배너클 씨는, 돈보다 핏줄이 더 넉넉한 인물이었다. 배너클가의 일원으로서 그는 제 자리를 차지하고 있었는데, 꽤 짭짤한 자리였다. 그리고 배너클가의 일원으로서 그는 당연히 아들 배너클 주니어를 그 관청에 들여놓았다. 그러나 그는 스틸트스토킹가의 한 가계와 혼인을 맺었는데, 이들 또한 실물 재산이나 동산보다는 핏줄의 관점에서 더 넉넉한 집안이었다. 이 혼인에서 자식이 생겼으니, 배너클 주니어와 세 아가씨였다. 배너클 주니어의 귀족다운 씀씀이며, 세 아가씨며, 스틸트스토킹가 출신인 타이트 배너클 부인이며, 그 자신까지 챙기다 보니, 타이트 배너클 씨는 봉급날과 봉급날 사이의 간격이 바라던 것보다 다소 길다고 느꼈는데, 그는 늘 이 사정을 나라의 인색함 탓으로 돌렸다.\n\n어느 날 아서 클레넘 씨는 타이트 배너클 씨를 찾아 에두름국에서 다섯 번째 문의를 했다. 그 전에도 그 신사를 차례로 한 차례는 어느 홀에서, 또 한 차례는 어느 유리 진열실에서, 또 한 차례는 어느 대기실에서, 또 한 차례는 그 부서가 제 바람을 보관해 두는 듯한 어느 내화 복도에서 기다려 보았던 터였다. 이번에 배너클 씨는 전처럼 그 부서의 수장인 고귀한 귀재와 함께 있지 않고, 자리를 비운 상태였다. 그러나 배너클 주니어가, 비록 더 작은 별이긴 해도 그 관청의 지평선 위에 떠 있는 것으로 안내되었다.\n\n그는 배너클 주니어와 면담하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 그러자 그 젊은 신사가 부친의 난롯불에 종아리를 그슬리며 등을 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 있는 것을 보게 되었다. 그곳은 안락한 방으로, 고위 관료다운 격식에 따라 호화롭게 꾸며져 있었으며, 자리를 비운 배너클의 위풍당당한 자취를 곳곳에서 풍기고 있었다. 두툼한 양탄자, 앉아서 쓰는 가죽 덮인 책상, 서서 쓰는 가죽 덮인 책상, 위압적인 안락의자와 난로 깔개, 가운데 놓인 가리개, 갈가리 찢긴 서류들, 약병이나 죽은 사냥감처럼 작은 꼬리표가 삐죽 튀어나온 공문 상자들, 사방에 밴 가죽과 마호가니 냄새, 그리고 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가 하는 전반적으로 어리둥절하게 만드는 분위기가 그러했다.\n\n지금 이 배너클은, 클레넘 씨의 명함을 손에 든 채, 앳된 모습에다, 아마도 여태껏 본 것 중 가장 솜털 같은 자그마한 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다. 그 보송보송한 솜털 끝이 그의 풋내 나는 턱에 돋아 있어, 마치 깃털이 반쯤 난 어린 새 같았다. 동정심 많은 관찰자라면 그가 종아리를 불에 그슬리지 않았더라면 추워서 죽었으리라고 주장할 만했다. 그는 목에 고급 외알 안경을 늘어뜨리고 있었으나, 안타깝게도 눈자위가 어찌나 평평하고 작은 눈꺼풀이 어찌나 흐물흐물한지, 그것을 끼워 올려도 자리에 박혀 있지 않고 자꾸 흘러내려 조끼 단추에 부딪히며 딸깍 소리를 냈고, 그럴 때마다 그는 몹시 당황했다.\n\n\"아, 저기요. 이거 보세요! 아버지는 안 계시고, 오늘은 안 나오실 겁니다.\" 배너클 주니어가 말했다. \"제가 처리할 수 있는 일인가요?\"\n\n(딸깍! 외알 안경이 떨어졌다. 배너클 주니어는 잔뜩 놀라 제 몸을 더듬거렸으나 그것을 찾지 못했다.)\n\n\"친절하시군요.\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"하지만 저는 배너클 씨를 뵙고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"아 그런데 말이죠. 이거 보세요! 약속을 잡지 않으셨잖아요, 아시다시피.\" 배너클 주니어가 말했다.\n\n(이때쯤 그는 외알 안경을 찾아 다시 끼워 올렸다.)\n\n\"네.\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"바로 그 약속을 잡고 싶은 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아 그런데 말이죠. 이거 보세요! 이게 공무인가요?\" 배너클 주니어가 물었다.\n\n(딸깍! 외알 안경이 또 떨어졌다. 배너클 주니어가 그것을 찾느라 한창인 통에, 클레넘 씨는 지금 대답해 봐야 소용없겠다고 느꼈다.)\n\n\"이게 말이죠,\" 배너클 주니어가 방문객의 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 유심히 살피며 말했다. \"혹시 — 톤수에 관한 — 그런 종류의 일인가요?\"\n\n(대답을 기다리느라 잠시 멈춘 그는, 한 손으로 오른쪽 눈을 벌리고 거기에 안경을 박아 넣었는데, 그 모양이 어찌나 자극적이었던지 눈에서 눈물이 끔찍하게 쏟아지기 시작했다.)\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"톤수에 관한 일이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 이거 보세요. 사적인 일인가요?\"\n\n\"정말 잘 모르겠습니다. 도릿이라는 분과 관련된 일입니다.\"\n\n\"이거 보세요, 그럼 이렇게 하세요! 그쪽으로 가시는 길이라면 저희 집으로 찾아가 보시는 게 낫겠어요. 그로스브너 광장, 뮤즈 가 24번지입니다. 아버지가 통풍 기운이 좀 있으셔서 집에 계세요.\"\n\n(이 갈피를 못 잡는 젊은 배너클은 안경을 낀 쪽 눈이 분명 멀어 가는 듯했으나, 그 고통스러운 조치를 더는 바꾸기가 부끄러운 모양이었다.)\n\n\"감사합니다. 지금 그리로 가 보겠습니다. 안녕히 계십시오.\" 젊은 배너클은 그가 정말 가 버리리라고는 전혀 예상하지 못했던 듯, 이 말에 낭패한 기색이었다.\n\n\"정말 확실한가요,\" 그가 문가에 이르렀을 때 배너클 주니어가 그를 불러 세우며, 자신이 떠올린 그 기발한 사무적 착상을 완전히 단념하기는 싫은 듯 물었다. \"톤수에 관한 일이 아닌 게요?\"\n\n\"확실합니다.\"\n\n그렇게 확언하면서, 그리고 만일 정말로 톤수에 관한 일이었다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을까 자못 궁금해하면서, 클레넘 씨는 문의를 계속하기 위해 물러났다.\n\n그로스브너 광장의 뮤즈 가는 엄밀히 말해 그로스브너 광장 자체는 아니었지만, 그 광장에 아주 가까웠다. 그곳은 막다른 담벼락과 마구간과 두엄 더미로 이루어진 흉측한 작은 거리였고, 마차 보관소 위에는 마부 가족들이 사는 다락방이 있었는데, 그들은 빨래 말리기에 열심이었고 창턱을 미니어처 통행료 징수문으로 장식하길 좋아했다. 그 멋들어진 동네의 으뜸가는 굴뚝 청소부가 뮤즈 가의 막다른 끝에 살았고, 같은 모퉁이에는 이른 아침과 해 질 녘에 빈 포도주병과 부엌 찌꺼기를 사들이느라 사람들이 많이 드나드는 가게가 하나 있었다. 펀치 인형극 무대는 그 주인들이 다른 데서 식사하는 동안 뮤즈 가의 막다른 담벼락에 기대 세워져 있곤 했고, 동네 개들은 바로 그 자리에서 만나기로 약속을 잡곤 했다. 그래도 뮤즈 가 입구 쪽에는 바람도 통하지 않는 작은 집이 두세 채 있었는데, 멋들어진 위치에 비루하게 빌붙어 있다는 이유로 어마어마한 집세에 나갔다. 이 끔찍한 작은 닭장 같은 집 한 채가 세놓일 때마다 (드문 일이었으니, 워낙 인기가 많았다) 부동산 중개인은 그것을 도시에서 가장 귀족적인 구역에 자리한 신사용 주거지로, 오로지 상류 사교계의 엘리트만이 거주하는 곳이라고 광고했다.\n\n이 좁은 테두리에 엄밀히 들어맞는 신사용 주거지가 배너클가의 핏줄에 필수 불가결한 것이 아니었던들, 이 특정 가계는, 이를테면 삼분의 일 값에 쉰 배의 편의를 제공하는 만 채의 집 가운데서 꽤 폭넓게 골랐을 것이다. 사정이 그러하니, 배너클 씨는 자신의 신사용 주거지가 지극히 불편하고 지극히 비싸다는 것을 알면서도, 공복답게 늘 그것을 나라의 탓으로 돌렸으며, 나라의 인색함을 보여 주는 또 하나의 사례로 들먹였다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 우그러진 듯한 집 한 채에 이르렀는데, 다 쓰러져 가는 활처럼 휜 정면에, 작고 거무튀튀한 창들에, 축축한 조끼 주머니 같은 작고 어두운 채광 구덩이가 딸려 있었다. 그곳이 바로 그로스브너 광장 뮤즈 가 24번지였다. 후각으로 보자면 그 집은 뮤즈 가의 진한 증류액으로 채운 일종의 병 같았다. 그리고 하인이 문을 열었을 때, 그는 마치 그 병마개를 뽑는 듯이 보였다.\n\n그 하인은 그로스브너 광장의 하인들 가운데서, 그 집이 그로스브너 광장의 집들 가운데서 차지하는 것과 똑같은 위치를 차지하고 있었다. 나름대로 훌륭했으나, 그 나름이라는 것이 뒷길이요 샛길이었다. 그의 화려함에는 때가 섞여 있지 않은 게 아니었고, 안색으로 보나 됨됨이로 보나 그는 식료품 저장실의 답답함에 시달린 흔적이 있었다. 마개를 뽑아 클레넘 씨의 코에 그 병을 들이밀 때, 그에게는 누런 무기력이 감돌고 있었다.\n\n\"이 명함을 타이트 배너클 씨께 전해 주시고, 제가 방금 젊은 배너클 씨를 뵈었더니 이리로 찾아가 보라고 권하더라고 전해 주십시오.\"\n\n그 하인은 (마치 자신이 그 집안의 금고라도 되는 양, 그리고 은식기와 보석을 단추로 채워 몸에 지니고 다니기라도 하는 양, 주머니 덮개에 배너클가의 문장이 새겨진 큼직한 단추를 잔뜩 달고 있었다) 명함을 잠시 들여다보더니 말했다. \"들어오시지요.\" 안쪽 현관문을 박치기로 열어젖히지 않고, 또 그로 인한 정신적 혼란과 물리적 어둠 속에서 부엌 계단으로 미끄러져 떨어지지 않으면서 들어가려면 약간의 판단력이 필요했다. 그러나 방문객은 무사히 현관 매트 위에 멈추어 섰다.\n\n하인이 여전히 \"들어오시지요\" 하고 말하기에, 방문객은 그를 따라갔다. 안쪽 현관문에서 또 하나의 병이 들이밀어지고 또 하나의 마개가 뽑히는 듯했다. 이 두 번째 약병은 식료품 저장실에서 나온 농축 식료품과 하수구 진액으로 채워진 듯했다. 좁은 복도에서 한바탕 소동이 일었으니, 하인이 자신만만하게 음산한 식당 문을 열었다가 거기 누군가가 있는 것을 보고 기겁하며 방문객 쪽으로 허둥지둥 물러난 탓이었다. 그러고 나서 방문객은 통보가 이루어지는 동안, 답답한 뒤쪽 응접실에 갇혔다. 거기서 그는 두 병의 향기로 동시에 원기를 회복할 기회를 얻으며, 삼 피트 앞의 눈을 멀게 할 듯한 낮은 담장을 내다보았고, 사망 통계표 안에 들어가는 배너클 일가 중에서 제 발로 하인 노릇 하는 선택을 해 이런 토끼장 같은 집에서 사는 가구가 몇이나 될까 헤아려 보았다.\n\n배너클 씨가 그를 만나 주겠다고 했다. 위층으로 올라오시겠습니까? 그러겠다고 했고, 그래서 올라갔다. 그리고 응접실에서, 한쪽 다리를 받침대에 올려놓은 채 있는 배너클 씨 본인을, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가의 살아 있는 형상이자 표상을 보게 되었다.\n\n배너클 씨는 더 좋은 시절, 나라가 이만큼 인색하지 않고 에두름국이 이만큼 시달리지 않던 시절부터 내려온 인물이었다. 그는 나라의 목에 끈과 종이의 주름을 칭칭 감듯이, 제 목에 흰 크라바트의 주름을 칭칭 감았다. 그의 소맷부리와 옷깃은 사람을 짓눌렀고, 그의 목소리와 태도도 사람을 짓눌렀다. 그는 굵직한 시곗줄과 인장 꾸러미를 차고 있었고, 불편할 만큼 단추를 채운 코트와, 불편할 만큼 단추를 채운 조끼와, 주름 하나 없는 바지와, 빳빳한 부츠를 갖추고 있었다. 그는 전체적으로 화려하고 육중하고 압도적이고 다루기 어려운 인물이었다. 그는 평생토록 토머스 로런스 경 앞에 앉아 초상화를 그리게 한 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨인가요?\" 배너클 씨가 말했다. \"앉으시오.\"\n\n클레넘 씨는 앉았다.\n\n\"내게 찾아오셨다지요,\" 배너클 씨가, 마치 스물다섯 음절쯤 되는 단어인 양 그 단어에 거드름을 실어 말했다. \"에두름 — 국으로 말이오.\"\n\n\"외람되이 그랬습니다.\"\n\n배너클 씨는 엄숙하게 고개를 숙였는데, 그 모양이 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그게 외람된 짓임을 부인하지는 않겠소. 또 한 번 외람된 짓을 해서, 용건이나 들려주시오.\"\n\n\"제가 몇 해 동안 중국에 있었고, 본국에서는 영 낯선 사람인지라, 지금 드리려는 문의에 사사로운 동기나 이해관계가 전혀 없다는 점을 말씀드리고자 합니다.\"\n\n배너클 씨는 손가락으로 탁자를 톡톡 두드리며, 이제 새롭고 낯선 화가 앞에 초상화를 그리게 하느라 앉아 있기라도 한 듯, 방문객에게 이렇게 말하는 것처럼 보였다. \"지금 이 고매한 표정 그대로 나를 화폭에 담아 준다면 고맙겠소.\"\n\n\"마셜시 감옥에서 도릿이라는 이름의 채무자를 한 사람 만났는데, 여러 해 동안 그곳에 있었습니다. 저는 그의 뒤엉킨 사정을 조사하여, 이만한 세월이 흐른 지금, 그의 불행한 처지를 개선하는 일이 가능하지 않을지 알아보고 싶습니다. 그의 채권자들 가운데 매우 영향력 있는 이해관계를 대표하는 분으로 타이트 배너클 씨의 성함이 제게 언급되었습니다. 제가 제대로 들은 것입니까?\"\n\n어떤 경우에도 결코 단도직입적인 답을 주지 않는 것이 에두름국의 원칙 중 하나였으므로, 배너클 씨는 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있소.\"\n\n\"왕실을 대신하여 여쭙는 것입니까, 아니면 사적인 개인으로서 여쭙는 것입니까?\"\n\n\"에두름국은 말이오,\" 배너클 씨가 대답했다. \"어쩌면 — 어쩌면 — 잘은 모르겠으나 — 이 사람이 속해 있었을지 모르는 어떤 상회나 합자회사의 지급 불능 재산에 대해 어떤 공공의 청구권을 행사하도록 권고했을 수도 있소. 그 문제가 공무 처리 과정에서 에두름국에 검토 회부되었을 수도 있소. 에두름국이 그러한 권고를 담은 회의록을 작성했거나 승인했을 수도 있소.\"\n\n\"그럼 그렇다고 가정하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"에두름국은,\" 배너클 씨가 말했다. \"그 어느 신사의 가정에도 책임이 없소.\"\n\n\"그 사건의 실상에 관한 공식 정보를 제가 어떻게 얻을 수 있을지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 — 일반 대중,\" 배너클 씨가 그 천한 무리를, 자신의 천적인 양 마지못해 입에 올리며 말했다. \"누구에게나 에두름국에 진정서를 제출할 자격이 있소. 그렇게 함에 있어 지켜야 할 형식들은, 그 부서의 적절한 부문에 신청하시면 알 수 있을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그 적절한 부문이 어디입니까?\"\n\n\"그 문의에 대한 공식 답변은,\" 배너클 씨가 종을 울리며 대답했다. \"부서 자체로 가서 받으시도록 안내하겠소.\"\n\n\"외람되지만 말씀드리자면 —\"\n\n\"그 부서는 — 일반 대중에게,\" 배너클 씨는 그 무엄한 의미의 단어 앞에서 늘 잠시 멈칫했다. \"공식 형식에 따라 접근하기만 한다면 누구에게나 열려 있소. 만일 — 일반 대중이 공식 형식에 따라 접근하지 않는다면, 그건 — 일반 대중 자신이 탓할 일이오.\"\n\n배너클 씨는 가문에 상처 입은 사람이자, 지위에 상처 입은 사람이자, 신사용 주거지에 상처 입은 사람, 이 셋을 하나로 뭉뚱그린 모습으로 그에게 준엄하게 고개 숙여 인사했다. 그러자 그도 배너클 씨에게 고개 숙여 인사했고, 그 흐물흐물한 하인에 의해 뮤즈 가로 내쳐졌다.\n\n이 지경에 이르자, 그는 인내심을 단련하는 셈 치고 다시 한번 에두름국으로 가, 거기서 얼마나 만족스러운 답을 얻을 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 그는 에두름국으로 돌아가, 다시 한번 사환을 통해 배너클 주니어에게 명함을 올려 보냈는데, 그 사환은 그가 또 돌아온 것을 정말이지 못마땅해했고, 현관 난롯가 칸막이 뒤에서 으깬 감자와 그레이비를 먹고 있던 참이었다.\n\n그는 다시 배너클 주니어 앞으로 들여보내졌고, 그 젊은 신사가 이번에는 무릎을 불에 그슬리며 네 시까지 지루하게 하품으로 시간을 때우고 있는 것을 보게 되었다.\n\n\"이거 참. 이거 보세요. 끈질기게도 들러붙으시는군요.\" 배너클 주니어가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다 —\"\n\n\"이거 보세요. 정말이지, 여기 와서 알고 싶은 게 있다고 하시면 안 됩니다, 아시다시피.\" 배너클 주니어가 몸을 돌려 외알 안경을 끼워 올리며 항변했다.\n\n\"알고 싶습니다.\" 한 가지 짧은 문구로 끈질기게 밀어붙이기로 마음을 정한 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"도릿이라는 이름의 채무자에 대해 왕실이 제기한 청구의 정확한 성격을 말입니다.\"\n\n\"이거 참. 이거 보세요. 정말 무서운 속도로 밀어붙이시는군요, 아시다시피. 이런, 약속도 없으시면서.\" 배너클 주니어가 일이 심각해진다는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"알고 싶습니다.\" 아서가 말하고는 자기 용건을 되풀이했다.\n\n배너클 주니어는 외알 안경이 떨어질 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다시 끼워 올리고는 또 떨어질 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다. \"이런 식으로 밀어붙이실 권리는 없습니다.\" 그러고는 더없이 맥없는 투로 말했다. \"이거 보세요. 무슨 뜻입니까? 이게 공무인지 아닌지 모른다고 하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"이제는 공무라는 걸 확인했습니다.\" 청원자가 대답했다. \"그래서 알고 싶습니다 —\" 그러고는 또다시 그 단조로운 문의를 되풀이했다.\n\n그것이 젊은 배너클에게 미친 효과는, 그로 하여금 속수무책으로 이렇게 되풀이하게 만든 것이었다. \"이거 보세요! 정말이지, 여기 와서 알고 싶은 게 있다고 하시면 안 됩니다, 아시다시피!\" 그것이 아서 클레넘에게 미친 효과는, 그로 하여금 앞서와 똑같은 말과 똑같은 어조로 자기 문의를 되풀이하게 만든 것이었다. 그것이 젊은 배너클에게 미친 효과는, 그를 무력과 속수무책의 놀라운 구경거리로 만들어 버린 것이었다.\n\n\"그럼, 이렇게 하시지요. 이거 보세요. 비서과로 가 보시는 게 낫겠습니다.\" 마침내 그가 종 쪽으로 슬금슬금 다가가 그것을 울리며 말했다. \"젱킨슨,\" 으깬 감자를 먹던 사환에게 그가 말했다. \"워블러 씨 방으로!\"\n\n이제 자신이 에두름국 공략에 헌신했으니 끝까지 밀어붙여야 한다고 느낀 아서 클레넘은, 사환을 따라 건물의 다른 층으로 갔고, 거기서 그 직원이 워블러 씨의 방을 가리켜 주었다. 그가 그 방으로 들어가니, 크고 편안한 책상에 두 신사가 마주 앉아 있는 것이 보였는데, 한 사람은 손수건으로 총신을 닦고 있었고, 다른 한 사람은 종이칼로 빵에 마멀레이드를 바르고 있었다.\n\n\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 물었다.\n\n두 신사가 그를 흘끗 보더니, 그 뻔뻔함에 놀란 듯했다.\n\n\"그래서 그 사람이,\" 더없이 굼뜨게 말하는 사람인 총신을 든 신사가 말했다. \"사촌네로 내려갔는데, 그 개를 기차에 태우고 데려갔지. 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 개야. 개 칸에 넣자 짐꾼 녀석한테 덤벼들고, 꺼낼 때는 차장한테 덤벼들었지. 헛간에 대여섯 놈을 불러 모으고 쥐를 잔뜩 들여놓고는 그 개의 시간을 쟀어. 개가 어마어마하게 잘 해낼 수 있다는 걸 알고는, 시합을 잡고 그 개에 거하게 돈을 걸었지. 그런데 막상 시합이 벌어지니, 어떤 악마 같은 놈이 매수당해서, 그래, 개한테 약을 먹였고, 개 주인은 빈털터리가 됐지.\"\n\n\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 물었다.\n\n마멀레이드를 바르던 신사가, 그 일에서 눈도 떼지 않은 채 받아 말했다. \"그 개를 뭐라고 불렀다고?\"\n\n\"러블리라고 불렀지.\" 다른 신사가 말했다. \"그 개가 자기가 유산을 바라던 늙은 고모의 완벽한 초상화라고 했어. 약에 취하니 특히나 고모를 빼닮았더라나.\"\n\n\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 말했다.\n\n두 신사가 한참을 웃었다. 총신을 든 신사는 그것을 살펴본 뒤 만족스러운 상태라고 판단하고는 다른 신사에게 의견을 물었다. 자기 견해가 확인되자, 그는 그것을 앞에 놓인 상자 속 제자리에 끼워 넣고, 개머리판을 꺼내 나직이 휘파람을 불며 그것을 닦았다.\n\n\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그제야 워블러 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 말했다.\n\n\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다 —\" 그러고는 아서 클레넘이 또다시 기계적으로 알고 싶은 바를 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"내가 알려드릴 수 없소.\" 워블러 씨가, 분명 자기 점심을 향해 말했다. \"들어 본 적 없는 일이오. 나하고는 전혀 상관없는 일이고. 다음 복도 왼쪽 두 번째 문, 클라이브 씨한테 가 보시는 게 낫겠소.\"\n\n\"그분도 똑같은 답을 주시지 않을까요.\"\n\n\"그럴 공산이 크지. 나는 그에 관해 아무것도 모르오.\" 워블러 씨가 말했다.\n\n청원자가 돌아서서 방을 나서자, 총을 든 신사가 외쳤다. \"여보시오! 이봐요!\"\n\n그가 다시 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"나가면서 문 좀 닫으시오. 지독한 외풍이 들어오잖소!\"\n\n몇 걸음 만에 그는 다음 복도 왼쪽 두 번째 문에 이르렀다. 그 방에는 세 신사가 있었는데, 일 번은 딱히 하는 일이 없었고, 이 번도 딱히 하는 일이 없었고, 삼 번도 딱히 하는 일이 없었다. 그러나 이들은 앞의 사람들보다 그 관청의 위대한 원리를 효율적으로 집행하는 데 한층 직접 관여하는 듯했으니, 이중문이 달린 으스스한 안쪽 방이 하나 있었기 때문이었다. 거기에 에두름국의 현인들이 회의차 모여 있는 듯했고, 거기서는 서류가 위풍당당하게 나오고 거기로는 서류가 위풍당당하게 들어가는 일이 거의 끊임없이 이어졌는데, 그 안에서는 또 한 신사인 사 번이 능동적 도구 노릇을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다.\" 아서 클레넘이 말하고는, 또다시 그 손풍금 같은 식으로 자기 용건을 늘어놓았다. 일 번이 그를 이 번에게 보내고, 이 번이 그를 삼 번에게 보내는 통에, 그는 그들이 모두 그를 사 번에게 보내기 전까지 그것을 세 번 늘어놓아야 했고, 사 번에게 다시 그것을 늘어놓았다.\n\n사 번은 활기차고 잘생기고 옷차림이 멋들어진 호감 가는 젊은이였는데 — 그는 배너클가 사람이었으나, 그 집안에서도 더 발랄한 쪽이었다 — 그가 스스럼없이 말했다. \"아! 그 일은 신경 쓰지 않으시는 게 낫겠는데요, 제 생각엔.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰지 말라고요?\"\n\n\"네! 신경 쓰지 마시라고 권하는 겁니다.\"\n\n이것은 워낙 새로운 관점이라, 아서 클레넘은 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 막막했다.\n\n\"하고 싶으시면 하셔도 됩니다. 작성하실 양식이라면 얼마든지 드릴 수 있어요. 여기 잔뜩 있으니까요. 원하시면 열두 장이라도 드리지요. 하지만 끝까지 못 가실 겁니다.\" 사 번이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게나 가망 없는 일입니까? 죄송합니다만, 저는 영국이 낯선 사람이라서요.\"\n\n\"가망 없을 거라는 말은 안 했어요.\" 사 번이 솔직한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다. \"그 일에 관해 의견을 내는 게 아니라, 당신에 관해 의견을 내는 겁니다. 제 생각에 당신은 끝까지 못 가실 거예요. 하지만 물론 마음대로 하셔도 됩니다. 계약 이행에 실패가 있었다거나, 뭐 그런 종류의 일이었겠지요, 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"정말 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요! 그건 알아내실 수 있지요. 그러면 그 계약이 어느 부서 소관이었는지 알아내실 테고, 그러면 거기서 그에 관한 걸 다 알아내실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"실례지만, 어떻게 알아냅니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 그야 — 그야 말해 줄 때까지 물으셔야지요. 그런 다음 (알아내시게 될 정규 형식에 따라) 그 부서에 이 부서로 진정서를 낼 수 있도록 허가해 달라는 진정서를 내세요. 만일 그 허가를 받으시면 (얼마 지나면 받으실 수도 있겠지요), 그 진정서는 그 부서에 등록되어야 하고, 이 부서로 보내져 기재되어야 하고, 다시 그 부서로 돌려보내져 서명을 받아야 하고, 다시 이 부서로 돌려보내져 부서명을 받아야 하고, 그제야 그 부서에 정식으로 상정되기 시작합니다. 그 사무가 이 각 단계를 거치는 시점은, 양쪽 부서에 말해 줄 때까지 물으시면 알아내실 수 있고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 이건 분명 일을 처리하는 방식이 아니지 않습니까.\" 아서 클레넘이 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n이 경박한 젊은 배너클은, 그가 잠시나마 이것이 일을 처리하는 방식이라고 짐작한 그 순진함을 자못 재미있어했다. 이 능숙한 젊은 배너클은 그렇지 않다는 것을 완벽하게 알고 있었다. 이 닳고 닳은 젊은 배너클은, 손에 떨어지는 짭짤한 자리라면 무엇이든 차지할 채비를 갖추려고 비서직을 통해 그 부서를 \"익혀\" 둔 터였다. 그리고 그는 그 부서가 나리들을 도와 천한 자들을 쫓아내기 위한, 정치 외교적 눈속임 기계 장치임을 충분히 이해하고 있었다. 한마디로 이 패기 넘치는 젊은 배너클은 장차 정치가가 되어 한자리할 만한 인물이었다.\n\n\"그 사무가, 그게 무엇이든, 그 부서에 정식으로 상정되면,\" 이 영리한 젊은 배너클이 말을 이었다. \"그러면 그 부서를 통해 그것을 수시로 지켜보실 수 있어요. 그것이 이 부서에 정식으로 상정되면, 이 부서를 통해 그것을 수시로 지켜보셔야 하고요. 우리는 그것을 이리저리 회부해야 할 텐데, 어디로든 회부하면 그땐 그것을 찾아내셔야 합니다. 그것이 언제든 우리에게 돌아오면, 그땐 우리를 찾아오시는 게 낫겠고요. 그것이 어디서든 막히면, 그땐 그것을 좀 흔들어 보셔야 하고요. 그에 관해 다른 부서에 편지를 쓰고, 또 이 부서에 편지를 쓰고도, 만족스러운 답을 못 들으시면, 아 그러면 그땐 — 계속 편지를 쓰시는 게 낫겠지요.\"\n\n아서 클레넘은 정말이지 몹시 미심쩍은 표정이었다. \"하지만 아무튼,\" 그가 말했다. \"친절하게 대해 주셔서 감사합니다.\"\n\n\"천만에요.\" 이 매력적인 젊은 배너클이 대꾸했다. \"한번 해 보시고, 마음에 드시는지 보세요. 마음에 안 드시면 언제든 그만두실 수 있으니까요. 양식을 잔뜩 가져가시는 게 낫겠어요. 이분께 양식을 잔뜩 드려!\" 이 번에게 이렇게 지시하면서, 이 반짝이는 젊은 배너클은 일 번과 삼 번으로부터 새로운 서류 한 뭉치를 집어 들고, 에두름국의 좌정한 우상에게 바치러 그 성소로 들고 갔다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 꽤나 침울하게 양식을 주머니에 넣고, 긴 돌 복도와 긴 돌 계단을 따라 제 갈 길을 갔다. 거리로 통하는 여닫이문에 이르러, 자기와 문 사이에 있는 두 사람이 빠져나가 자기도 뒤따를 수 있기를 그다지 인내심 있게는 아니게 기다리고 있을 때, 그중 한 사람의 목소리가 귀에 익숙하게 와닿았다. 그가 그 화자를 보고는 미글스 씨를 알아보았다. 미글스 씨는 얼굴이 매우 붉었는데 — 여행으로 붉어졌다고 보기엔 지나치게 붉었다 — 함께 있던 키 작은 사내의 멱살을 잡고서 말했다. \"나오시오, 이 악당, 나오라고!\"\n\n미글스 씨가 여닫이문을 박차고, 해를 끼칠 것 같지도 않은 외양의 그 키 작은 사내와 함께 거리로 나서는 것은 워낙 뜻밖의 소리이자 워낙 뜻밖의 광경이었던지라, 클레넘은 잠시 가만히 서서 문지기와 놀란 눈빛을 주고받았다. 그러나 그는 곧 재빨리 뒤따라 나가, 미글스 씨가 적을 곁에 끼고 거리를 내려가는 것을 보았다. 그는 곧 옛 여행 동무를 따라잡아 그의 등을 두드렸다. 미글스 씨가 그를 향해 돌린 성난 얼굴은, 상대가 누구인지 알아보자 풀렸고, 그는 다정한 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"잘 지내셨소?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"안녕하셨소? 나는 외국에서 막 건너온 참이오. 만나서 반갑소.\"\n\n\"저도 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"고맙소. 고맙소!\"\n\n\"미글스 부인과 따님은 —?\"\n\n\"더할 나위 없이 잘 지내오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"다만 좀 더 침착한 꼴일 때 나를 만나 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데.\"\n\n더운 날도 아니었건만, 미글스 씨는 행인들의 이목을 끌 만큼 달아오른 상태였다. 더욱이 그는 난간에 등을 기대고 모자와 크라바트를 벗고서, 김이 나는 머리와 얼굴을, 또 벌게진 귀와 목을, 세간의 이목 따위는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 박박 문질러 댔다.\n\n\"휴!\" 미글스 씨가 다시 옷매무새를 가다듬으며 말했다. \"이제 좀 낫군. 한결 시원하오.\"\n\n\"마음이 어지러우셨군요, 미글스 씨. 무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"잠깐만 기다리시오, 곧 이야기해 드리리다. 공원을 한 바퀴 도실 여유가 있으시오?\"\n\n\"얼마든지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 갑시다. 아! 저 사람을 한번 보실 만하오.\" 그가 마침 미글스 씨가 그토록 성나게 멱살을 잡았던 그 죄인 쪽으로 눈을 돌린 참이었다. \"저 친구로 말하면, 한번 볼 만한 인물이오.\"\n\n그는 덩치로 보나 옷차림으로 보나 볼 만한 인물이 못 되었으니, 그저 키 작고 다부지고 실무적으로 생긴 사내였다. 머리가 희끗희끗 세었고, 얼굴과 이마에는 단단한 나무에 새긴 듯한 깊은 사색의 주름이 패어 있었다. 그는 약간 낡은, 점잖은 검정 옷을 입었고, 어떤 수공예의 슬기로운 장인 같은 풍모였다. 그는 손에 안경집을 들고서, 이렇게 화제에 오르는 동안 그것을 이리저리 뒤집었는데, 엄지를 거리낌 없이 놀리는 그 솜씨는 연장에 익숙한 손에서나 볼 수 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"당신은 우리와 같이 계시오.\" 미글스 씨가 어딘지 위협하는 투로 말했다. \"곧 소개해 드릴 테니. 자, 그럼!\"\n\n그들이 공원으로 가는 가장 가까운 길을 잡는 동안, 클레넘은 속으로, 이 낯선 자가 (가장 온순한 태도로 순순히 따르고 있는 이 사람이) 대체 무슨 짓을 저질렀을까 궁금해했다. 그 외양은 그가 미글스 씨의 손수건을 노리다 들켰다는 의심을 도무지 정당화해 주지 않았고, 그렇다고 시비조이거나 난폭해 보이지도 않았다. 그는 조용하고 수수하고 침착한 사내였고, 달아나려는 시도도 하지 않았으며, 다소 풀이 죽어 보일 뿐 부끄러워하거나 뉘우치는 기색은 없었다. 그가 범죄자라면 필시 구제 불능의 위선자일 것이고, 죄인이 아니라면 미글스 씨가 무엇 때문에 에두름국에서 그의 멱살을 잡았겠는가? 그는 그 사내가 자기 머릿속에서만 풀리지 않는 수수께끼가 아니라 미글스 씨의 머릿속에서도 그러하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 공원으로 가는 짧은 길에 그들이 나눈 대화는 도무지 잘 이어지지 않았고, 미글스 씨의 눈은 전혀 다른 이야기를 할 때조차 늘 그 사내에게로 되돌아갔던 것이다.\n\n마침내 나무들 사이에 이르렀을 때, 미글스 씨가 우뚝 멈추어 서서 말했다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 부디 이 사람을 좀 봐 주시겠소? 이름은 도이스, 대니얼 도이스요. 이 사람이 악명 높은 악당이라고는 짐작도 못 하시겠지요?\"\n\n\"분명 못 하겠습니다.\" 사내가 거기 있는 만큼, 정말이지 당혹스러운 질문이었다.\n\n\"못 하겠지요. 그러실 줄 알았소. 이 사람이 공공의 죄인이라고는 짐작도 못 하시겠지요?\"\n\"못 합니다.\"\n\n\"못 하시겠지요. 하지만 그렇다오. 이 사람은 공공의 죄인이오. 무슨 죄를 지었을까요? 살인, 과실치사, 방화, 위조, 사기, 주거 침입, 노상강도, 절도, 공모, 기만? 자, 어느 것이라고 하시겠소?\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 아서 클레넘이 대니얼 도이스의 얼굴에 어린 옅은 미소를 알아채며 대답했다. \"그중 어느 것도 아닐 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"맞소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 이 사람은 창의적이었고, 그 창의력을 나라에 봉사하는 데 쓰려 했소. 그러니 곧바로 공공의 죄인이 된 거요, 선생.\"\n\n아서는 사내 자신을 바라보았으나, 그는 고개만 저을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"이 도이스라는 사람은,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"대장장이이자 기술자요. 큰 사업을 하는 건 아니지만, 매우 창의적인 사람으로 잘 알려져 있소. 십수 년 전, 이 사람은 자기 나라와 동포에게 대단히 중요한 (아주 진기한 비밀 공정이 들어가는) 발명을 완성했소. 그 일에 돈이 얼마나 들었는지, 인생의 몇 해를 거기에 바쳤는지는 말하지 않겠지만, 십수 년 전에 그것을 완성한 거요. 십수 년 됐지요?\" 미글스 씨가 도이스를 향해 말했다. \"이 사람은 세상에서 가장 사람 속을 끓게 하는 인물이오. 도무지 불평을 안 한다니까!\"\n\n\"네. 십이 년보다 조금 더 됐지요.\"\n\n\"조금 더?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"조금 더 못 됐다는 뜻이겠지. 자, 클레넘 씨, 이 사람이 정부에 청원을 한 거요. 정부에 청원을 하는 그 순간, 공공의 죄인이 되는 거요! 선생,\" 미글스 씨가 또다시 지나치게 달아오를 위험에 처해 말했다. \"이 사람은 그 순간부터 죄 없는 시민이기를 그치고 죄인이 되는 거요. 그 순간부터 무슨 흉악한 짓이라도 저지른 사람처럼 취급받는 거지. 따돌림받고, 미뤄지고, 윽박질리고, 비웃음당하고, 지체 높은 이 젊은 또는 늙은 신사한테서 저 지체 높은 젊은 또는 늙은 신사한테로 떠넘겨졌다가 다시 되돌아오는 사람이오. 제 시간에도 제 재산에도 아무 권리가 없는 사람, 어떻게든 없애 버려도 정당한 한낱 무법자, 온갖 가능한 수단을 다 동원해 진을 빼야 마땅한 사람 말이오.\"\n\n오전의 경험을 겪고 난 뒤라, 그것은 미글스 씨가 짐작하는 것만큼 믿기 어려운 이야기는 아니었다.\n\n\"거기 그렇게 서서 안경집만 이리저리 뒤집지 말고, 도이스,\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다. \"클레넘 씨한테 나한테 털어놓은 걸 말해 보시오.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 저는,\" 그 발명가가 말했다. \"마치 제가 무슨 죄라도 지은 것 같은 기분이 들게 되더군요. 여러 관청에 시중을 들며 드나드는 동안, 늘 정도의 차이는 있어도 무슨 아주 못된 죄라도 지은 것처럼 취급받았습니다. 저는 제 마음을 추스르려고 자주 이렇게 곱씹어야 했지요. 나는 정말이지 내 이름을 뉴게이트 범죄 기록부에 올릴 만한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았고, 그저 큰 절약과 큰 개선을 이루고자 했을 뿐이라고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 과장하는지 판단해 보시오. 이제 내가 나머지 사정을 말해 줘도 나를 믿으실 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n이런 서두를 떼고, 미글스 씨는 그 사연을 죽 풀어놓았으니, 이젠 지긋지긋해진 그 판에 박힌 사연, 우리 모두가 외울 만큼 잘 아는 그 당연지사의 사연이었다. 끝없는 시중과 서신 왕래 끝에, 끝없는 무례와 무지와 모욕 끝에, 어떻게 나리들께서 회의록 제3472호를 작성하여 그 죄인이 자기 비용으로 발명품의 일정한 시험을 해 보도록 허락했는지. 그 시험이 어떻게 여섯 명으로 구성된 위원회 앞에서 이루어졌는지, 그중 늙은 위원 둘은 너무 눈이 멀어 그것을 보지 못했고, 다른 늙은 위원 둘은 너무 귀가 멀어 그것을 듣지 못했고, 또 다른 늙은 위원 하나는 너무 다리를 절어 그것에 다가가지 못했고, 마지막 늙은 위원은 너무 고집이 세 그것을 보려 하지도 않았는지. 어떻게 또 여러 해가 흘렀고, 더 많은 무례와 무지와 모욕이 있었는지. 어떻게 나리들께서 회의록 제5103호를 작성하여 그 사무를 에두름국에 넘겼는지. 어떻게 에두름국이 시간이 흐른 뒤, 마치 그것이 전에는 들어 본 적도 없는, 어제 갓 나온 따끈따끈한 신품인 양 그 사무를 떠맡았는지. 그러고는 그 사무를 뒤죽박죽으로 만들고, 흐리멍덩하게 만들고, 젖은 담요로 덮어 내동댕이쳤는지. 어떻게 무례와 무지와 모욕이 구구단처럼 불어났는지. 어떻게 그 발명품이 배너클 셋과 스틸트스토킹 하나에게 회부되었는지, 그들은 그에 관해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그들 머릿속에 그에 관해 아무것도 망치질해 넣을 수 없었으며, 그에 관해 지겨워하더니, 그에 관해 물리적 불가능을 보고했는지. 어떻게 에두름국이 회의록 제8740호에서 \"나리들께서 내리신 결정을 번복할 이유가 없다고 본다\"고 했는지. 어떻게 에두름국이, 나리들께서 아무런 결정도 내린 바 없다는 점을 일깨움받고는, 그 사무를 선반에 처박아 두었는지. 어떻게 바로 그날 아침에 에두름국의 수장과 마지막 면담이 있었고, 그 청동 머리가 입을 열어, 대체로 보아, 그리고 온갖 정황을 고려하여, 또 여러 관점에서 살펴보건대, 이 사무에 관해서는 두 가지 방책 중 하나를 따라야 한다는 의견을 내놓았는지. 즉 그것을 영원토록 가만히 내버려 두든가, 아니면 처음부터 다시 죄다 시작하든가 하라는 것이었다.\n* 청동 머리: 중세 전설 속 로저 베이컨 등이 만들었다는, 신탁처럼 말하는 놋쇠 인공 두상. 권위 있게 모호한 말만 늘어놓는 인물을 비꼬는 표현.\n\n\"그래서,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 실무적인 사람인지라, 바로 그 자리에서, 그 면전에서, 도이스의 멱살을 잡고는, 당신은 명백히 파렴치한 악당이요 정부의 평화를 어지럽히는 반역자임이 내게 분명하니, 데리고 나가겠다고 했소. 그러고는 멱살을 쥔 채 그를 관청 문밖으로 끌고 나왔지. 바로 그 문지기더러도, 내가 그런 인간들에 대한 관청의 평가를 알아주는 실무적인 사람이라는 걸 알라고 말이오. 그래서 우리가 여기 있는 거요!\"\n\n만일 그 경박한 젊은 배너클이 거기 있었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 솔직하게 그들에게 일러 주었을 것이다. 에두름국은 제 소임을 다한 것이라고. 배너클가가 할 일이란 할 수 있는 한 오래도록 국가라는 배에 들러붙어 있는 것이라고. 배를 손질하고, 가볍게 하고, 깨끗이 하는 일은 그들을 떼어 내는 일이 될 텐데, 그들은 한 번밖에 떨어져 나갈 수 없으니, 그들이 여전히 달라붙어 있는 채로 배가 가라앉는다면, 그것은 그들이 알 바 아니라 배가 알아서 할 일이라고 말이다.\n\n\"보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"이제 도이스에 관해 다 아셨소. 다만, 솔직히 말해 이게 내 심사를 나아지게 하진 못하는데, 지금 이 순간에도 당신은 이 사람이 불평하는 소리를 못 듣고 있다는 것 빼고는 말이오.\"\n\n\"인내심이 대단하시겠습니다.\" 아서 클레넘이 다소 경이로운 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"참을성이 대단하시겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니오.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"내가 남보다 더한지는 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 그래도 나보다는 더하시구려!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n도이스가 미소 지으며 클레넘에게 말했다. \"보시다시피, 이런 일에 대한 제 경험은 저 자신에게서 비롯된 게 아닙니다. 때때로 그에 관해 조금씩 알게 될 처지에 있었지요. 제 경우가 특별한 게 아닙니다. 저는 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 백 명보다 더 험하게 당한 게 아니에요 — 다른 모든 이들보다, 라고 말하려던 참이었지만요.\"\n\n\"제 일이라면 그게 위안이 될지 모르겠습니다만, 당신께는 그러하다니 무척 다행입니다.\"\n\n\"제 말을 이해해 주십시오! 그게 한 사람의 수고와 희망에 대한 보상이라는 말은 아닙니다.\" 그가 차분하고 사려 깊은 투로, 마치 그 잿빛 눈으로 거리를 재기라도 하듯 앞쪽 먼 곳을 바라보며 대답했다. \"다만 내가 이렇게 될 줄 미리 헤아릴 수 있었다는 걸 안다는 것이, 어떤 종류의 안도는 되지요.\"\n\n그는 정교하게 따지고 맞추는 기술자들에게서 흔히 볼 수 있는 그 조용하고 신중한 태도로, 그 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그것은 그의 유연한 엄지처럼, 또는 그가 이따금 마치 제 손으로 반쯤 마무리한 작업을 골똘히 들여다보며 궁리하기라도 하듯 모자챙 뒤쪽을 슬쩍 들어 올리는 그 독특한 버릇처럼, 그에게 배어 있는 것이었다.\n\n\"실망했냐고요?\" 그가 나무들 아래 두 사람 사이로 걸으며 말을 이었다. \"그렇습니다. 분명 실망했지요. 상처받았냐고요? 그렇습니다. 분명 상처받았어요. 그건 당연한 노릇이지요. 하지만 같은 처지에 놓이는 사람들이 대개 같은 식으로 당한다고 말할 때 제가 뜻하는 바는 —\"\n\n\"영국에서 말이지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 물론 영국에서 그렇다는 거지요. 그들이 발명품을 외국으로 가져가면, 사정은 사뭇 다릅니다. 그래서 그토록 많은 사람이 그리로 가는 거고요.\"\n\n미글스 씨가 정말이지 또다시 잔뜩 달아올랐다.\n\n\"제 말은, 어쩌다 이게 우리 정부의 일상적 방식이 되었든 간에, 이게 그 일상적 방식이라는 겁니다. 그것이 거의 다가갈 수조차 없음을 깨닫지 않은, 그리고 그것에게 낙담당하고 박대당하지 않은 기획자나 발명가를 단 한 사람이라도 들어 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"들어 본 적 있다고는 말 못 하겠군요.\"\n\n\"그것이 유용한 무언가를 채택하는 데 앞장선 적을 아십니까? 유용한 어떤 종류의 본보기를 보인 적을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"나는 여기 내 친구보다 한참 나이가 많으니,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그건 내가 답하리다. 단 한 번도 없소.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리 셋 모두 알 겁니다,\" 그 발명가가 말했다. \"그것이 우리 나머지보다 몇 마일씩, 몇 해씩 뒤처지기로 굳게 작정한 숱한 경우를요. 그리고 더 나은 것들이 잘 알려지고 두루 채택된 뒤에도, 한참 전에 폐기된 것들을 고집스레 쓰고 있다가 들통난 경우들을요.\"\n\n세 사람 모두 그 점에는 의견이 일치했다.\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 도이스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"어떤 금속이 어떤 온도에서, 어떤 물체가 어떤 압력 아래서 어떻게 될지 제가 아는 것처럼, (마음만 먹고 헤아려 본다면) 이 위대한 나리들과 신사분들이 제 것과 같은 사안을 틀림없이 어떻게 다룰지도 알 수 있는 거지요. 어깨 위에 머리가 달려 있고 그 안에 기억이 들어 있는 제가, 저보다 앞서 온 모든 이들과 같은 대열에 끼게 됐다고 놀랄 권리는 없습니다. 그냥 내버려 뒀어야 했어요. 경고는 충분히 받았으니까요, 분명히.\"\n\n그러면서 그는 안경집을 집어넣고 아서에게 말했다. \"불평은 안 해도, 클레넘 씨, 고마움은 느낄 줄 압니다. 그리고 저는 우리 둘의 벗에게 그 고마움을 느낀다고 분명히 말씀드리지요. 이 사람이 여러 날에 걸쳐, 여러 방식으로 저를 받쳐 주었습니다.\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리 마시오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n뒤이은 침묵 속에서 아서는 대니얼 도이스를 흘끗 보지 않을 수 없었다. 헛된 푸념을 삼가는 것이 분명 그의 천성에, 그리고 제 처지에 대한 자존심에 배어 있는 일이긴 했으나, 그 오랜 분투 탓에 그가 더 늙고 더 굳세고 더 가난해졌음은 분명했다. 아서는 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 한 나라의 사무를 떠맡는 친절을 베푸는 그 신사들에게서 한 수 배워, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 익혔더라면, 이 사람에게 얼마나 복된 일이었을까 하고.\n\n미글스 씨는 한 오 분쯤 달아오른 채 풀이 죽어 있다가, 차츰 식고 마음이 개기 시작했다.\n\n\"자, 자!\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 침울하게 군다고 일이 나아지진 않을 거요. 어디로 갈 생각이오, 댄?\"\n\n\"공장으로 돌아가야지요.\" 댄이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼, 우리 모두 공장으로 돌아갑시다, 아니면 그쪽으로 걸어가든가.\" 미글스 씨가 쾌활하게 대꾸했다. \"그게 블리딩 하트 야드에 있다고 해서 클레넘 씨가 꺼리진 않으실 거요.\"\n\n\"블리딩 하트 야드라고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"거기 가고 싶던 참입니다.\"\n\n\"그거 잘됐군!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다. \"갑시다!\"\n\n그들이 걸어가는 동안, 일행 중 분명 한 사람은, 어쩌면 그 이상은, 나리들 및 배너클가와 공무상의 서신을 주고받아 온 사람에게 블리딩 하트 야드가 그리 어울리지 않는 목적지는 아니라고 생각했다 — 그리고 어쩌면, 만일 브리타니아가 에두름국을 지나치게 부려 먹는다면, 그녀 자신도 언젠가 어느 흉한 날에 블리딩 하트 야드에 셋방을 구하러 오게 될지도 모른다는 불길한 예감마저 품었을 것이다.\n\n# 제12장. 위대한 애국적 회의가 열리다\n\n해 질 무렵, 음울하고 흐릿한 가을밤이 손강 위로 내려앉고 있었다. 그 물줄기는 음침한 곳에 놓인 더럽혀진 거울처럼 구름을 무겁게 비추었고, 낮은 둔덕들은 여기저기서 마치 물에 비친 제 어두워지는 모습을 보고 싶어 반은 호기심에, 반은 두려움에 기우뚱 몸을 기울이는 듯했다. 샬롱 일대의 평평한 들판은 길고 묵직한 띠를 이루어 누워 있었고, 노여운 석양을 배경으로 한 줄로 늘어선 포플러 나무가 이따금 그 띠를 조금 들쭉날쭉하게 만들었다. 손강 기슭은 축축하고, 음울하고, 적막했다. 그리고 밤은 빠르게 깊어 갔다.\n\n샬롱을 향해 천천히 나아가는 한 사내가 그 풍경 속에서 눈에 띄는 유일한 형상이었다. 카인인들 이보다 더 외롭고 더 기피당하는 모습일 수는 없었으리라. 등에는 낡은 양가죽 배낭을 지고, 손에는 어느 숲에서 베어 낸 거칠고 껍질도 벗기지 않은 막대기를 쥐고, 진흙투성이에 발은 부르트고, 신발과 각반은 닳아 해지고, 머리카락과 수염은 손질도 안 된 채, 어깨에 걸친 외투와 입은 옷가지는 빗물에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 아파하며 어렵사리 절뚝거리며 걸어가는 그의 모습은 마치 구름이 그를 피해 서둘러 달아나는 듯, 바람의 울부짖음과 풀의 떨림이 그를 겨누고 있는 듯, 낮고 신비로운 물의 찰랑임이 그를 향해 중얼거리는 듯, 변덕스러운 가을밤이 그로 인해 어지러워진 듯 보였다.\n\n그는 이쪽을 흘끔, 저쪽을 흘끔 보았는데, 시무룩하면서도 움츠러든 눈길이었다. 이따금 멈춰 서서 몸을 돌려 사방을 둘러보았다. 그러고는 다시 절뚝거리며 나아가, 힘겹게 걸으며 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"끝도 없는 이 평원, 악마에게나 가 버려라! 칼날처럼 발을 베는 이 돌들, 악마에게나 가 버려라! 으스스하게 사람을 휘감는 이 음산한 어둠, 악마에게나 가 버려라! 너희가 다 밉다!\"\n\n그리고 만약 할 수만 있었다면, 그는 사방으로 던지는 그 노려보는 눈초리로 그 모든 것에 자신의 증오를 퍼부었을 것이다. 그는 조금 더 터벅터벅 걸었다. 그러다 앞쪽 먼 곳을 바라보며 다시 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"나는 배고프고, 목마르고, 지쳤다. 그런데 너희 멍청이들은, 저기 불빛이 있는 곳에서, 먹고 마시고 불가에서 몸을 녹이고 있구나! 너희 마을을 약탈할 수만 있다면 좋으련만. 내 너희에게 갚아 주마, 이놈들아!\"\n\n그러나 그가 마을을 향해 이를 악물고, 마을을 향해 주먹을 흔들어도 마을은 조금도 가까워지지 않았다. 그가 그 들쭉날쭉한 포석 위에 발을 딛고 서서 주위를 둘러볼 때쯤, 사내는 더더욱 배고프고, 더더욱 목마르고, 더더욱 지쳐 있었다.\n\n거기에는 대문이 달린, 음식 냄새가 구수하게 풍기는 호텔이 있었다. 거기에는 밝은 창문과 도미노 패가 부딪치는 소리가 나는 카페가 있었다. 거기에는 문기둥에 붉은 천 조각들을 늘어뜨린 염색집이 있었다. 거기에는 귀걸이며 제단에 바칠 봉헌물을 진열한 은세공집이 있었다. 거기에는 파이프를 입에 문 채 나오는 군인 손님들이 떠들썩하게 무리 지은 담배 가게가 있었다. 거기에는 마을의 고약한 냄새들과, 빗물과, 시궁창에 고인 쓰레기와, 길을 가로질러 매달린 희미한 등불과, 거대한 역마차와, 그 산더미 같은 짐과, 꼬리를 묶어 올린 여섯 마리 잿빛 말이 합승마차 사무소 앞에서 막 출발하려는 모습이 있었다. 그러나 주머니가 빈 나그네가 들 만한 작은 선술집은 보이지 않아, 그는 어두운 모퉁이를 돌아 하나를 찾아야 했다. 그곳은 양배추 잎이 가장 두껍게 쌓여, 여인들이 아직 물 긷기를 멈추지 않은 공동 우물 둘레로 짓밟혀 있는 곳이었다. 거기, 그 뒷골목에서 그는 하나를 발견했으니, 바로 '동틀 녘'이었다. 커튼이 드리워진 창문이 '동틀 녘'을 흐릿하게 가리고 있었지만, 그 안은 환하고 따뜻해 보였고, 당구 큐와 공을 적절히 그려 넣은 그림으로 장식해 읽기 쉬운 글귀로 알리고 있었다. '동틀 녘'에서는 당구를 칠 수 있고, 말을 타고 오든 걸어서 오든 음식과 술과 잠자리를 구할 수 있으며, 좋은 포도주와 리큐어와 브랜디를 갖추고 있다고. 사내는 '동틀 녘'의 문손잡이를 돌리고 절뚝거리며 들어섰다.\n\n문으로 들어서며 그는 방 안을 차지하고 있던 몇몇 사내들에게 빛바랜 챙 늘어진 모자에 손을 갖다 대 인사했다. 둘은 작은 탁자 하나에서 도미노를 치고 있었고, 서넛은 난로 둘레에 앉아 담배를 피우며 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 한가운데 놓인 당구대는 잠시 방치되어 있었다. '동틀 녘'의 안주인은 작은 계산대 뒤에 앉아, 흐릿한 시럽 병들과 과자 바구니들과 유리잔 물 빼는 납받침 사이에서 바느질에 몰두하고 있었다.\n\n난로 뒤 방 한구석에 있는 빈 작은 탁자로 걸어가, 그는 배낭과 외투를 바닥에 내려놓았다. 그렇게 하느라 몸을 숙였다가 고개를 들어 보니, 안주인이 그의 곁에 와 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 여기서 묵을 수 있겠소, 마담?\"\n\n\"물론이지요!\" 안주인이 높고 노래하듯 명랑한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"좋소. 저녁을—야식을—뭐라 부르든 좋소, 먹을 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"아, 물론이지요!\" 안주인이 아까처럼 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 서둘러 주시오, 마담. 먹을 것을 되도록 빨리, 그리고 포도주를 곧장. 난 기진맥진했소.\"\n\n\"날씨가 아주 사납지요, 무슈.\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n\"빌어먹을 날씨요.\"\n\n\"그리고 길도 무척 멀고요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을 길이오.\"\n\n그의 쉰 목소리가 잦아들었고, 그는 계산대에서 포도주 한 병이 올 때까지 두 손에 머리를 괴고 있었다. 작은 잔을 두 번 채웠다 비우고, 보자기와 냅킨, 수프 접시, 소금, 후추, 기름과 함께 앞에 놓인 큼직한 빵 덩이에서 한쪽 끝을 떼어 낸 뒤, 그는 벽 모서리에 등을 기대고, 앉아 있던 긴 의자를 침상 삼아, 식사가 마련될 때까지 빵 껍질을 씹기 시작했다.\n\n낯선 자가 들어오면 그런 자리에서 으레 따라붙는, 난로 둘레의 그 잠깐의 대화 끊김과 서로에 대한 일시적인 무관심과 산만함이 있었다. 그것은 이제 지나간 터였다. 사내들은 그를 흘끔거리기를 그치고 다시 이야기를 나누고 있었다.\n\n\"그게 진짜 이유라니까.\" 그들 가운데 하나가, 들려주던 이야기를 마무리 지으며 말했다. \"그게 바로 사람들이 악마가 풀려났다고들 했던 진짜 이유라고.\" 말하는 자는 그 교회에 딸린 키 큰 스위스 사람이었는데, 그는 그 논의에 교회의 권위 같은 것을 끌어들였다—더욱이 악마가 화제였으니 말이다.\n\n새 손님 대접에 관해 남편에게 지시를 내린 안주인은—남편은 '동틀 녘'의 요리사 노릇을 했다—계산대 뒤에서 다시 바느질을 시작한 참이었다. 야무지고, 깔끔하고, 발랄한 자그마한 여인으로, 머릿수건도 큼직하고 양말도 두툼한 차림이었는데, 그녀는 일감에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 웃으며 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이며 대화에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"아, 저런, 그래서요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"리옹에서 배가 올라와, 마르세유에서 정말로 악마가 풀려났다는 소식을 가져왔을 때, 어떤 얼간이들은 그걸 곧이곧대로 삼키더군요. 그런데 나는요? 천만에요, 나는 아니지요.\"\n\n\"마담, 당신은 언제나 옳습니다.\" 키 큰 스위스 사람이 응수했다. \"분명 당신은 그 사내한테 잔뜩 화가 나셨겠지요, 마담?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 그렇고말고요!\" 안주인이 일감에서 눈을 들어, 눈을 아주 크게 뜨고 고개를 한쪽으로 까딱하며 외쳤다. \"당연하지요, 그렇고말고요.\"\n\n\"그자는 못된 인간이었지요.\"\n\n\"사악한 망나니였지요.\" 안주인이 말했다. \"그자는 운 좋게도 피한 그 벌을 받아 마땅했어요. 더 안된 일이지요.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 마담! 한번 따져 봅시다.\" 스위스 사람이 입술 사이에서 시가를 돌리며 따지듯 응수했다. \"어쩌면 그건 그자의 불운한 운명이었는지도 모르지요. 그자는 환경의 자식이었는지도 몰라요. 어떻게 찾아내야 할지만 안다면, 그자에게도 선한 면이 있었고 또 있을 가능성은 늘 있으니까요. 철학적 박애주의가 가르치기를—\"\n\n난로 둘레의 자그마한 무리 가운데 나머지 사람들은 그 위협적인 표현이 등장하는 것에 웅성거리며 못마땅함을 드러냈다. 도미노를 치던 두 사람마저 게임에서 눈을 들었으니, 마치 철학적 박애주의가 그 이름으로 '동틀 녘'에 끌려 들어오는 것에 항의하기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그만두세요, 당신하고 당신의 박애주의 따위는.\" 웃음 띤 안주인이 그 어느 때보다 더 고개를 끄덕이며 외쳤다. \"그럼 들어 보세요. 나는 여자예요, 나는요. 철학적 박애주의 같은 건 아무것도 몰라요. 하지만 내가 본 것, 그리고 내가 살아가는 이 세상에서 똑바로 마주 본 것은 알아요. 그리고 당신한테 이 말은 해 두지요, 친구. 이 세상엔 (안타깝게도 남자도 여자도) 선한 면이라곤 조금도 없는 사람들이 있어요—전혀요. 타협 없이 미워해야만 하는 사람들이 있어요. 인류의 적으로 다뤄야만 하는 사람들이 있어요. 인간의 마음이라곤 없어서, 사나운 짐승처럼 짓밟아 길에서 치워 버려야 하는 사람들이 있어요. 그런 자들은 많지 않기를 바라지만, 나는 봤어요 (내가 살아가는 이 세상에서, 심지어 이 자그마한 '동틀 녘'에서도) 그런 사람들이 있다는 걸요. 그리고 나는 이 사내가—그들이 뭐라고 부르든, 이름은 잊어버렸지만—그런 자들 가운데 하나라는 걸 의심하지 않아요.\"\n\n안주인의 활기찬 연설은 '동틀 녘'에서 꽤 호의적으로 받아들여졌는데, 이는 그녀가 그토록 이치에 닿지 않게 반대하던 부류를 점잖게 변호하는 어떤 상냥한 사람들이라면, 그레이트브리튼에 더 가까운 곳에서 끌어냈을 반응보다 훨씬 나은 것이었다.\n\n\"맙소사! 당신의 철학적 박애주의라는 것이,\" 안주인이 일감을 내려놓고 일어나, 옆문으로 수프를 들고 나타난 남편에게서 그것을 받아 그 낯선 손님에게 가져가며 말했다. \"말로든 행동으로든, 아니면 둘 다로든, 그런 사람들과 조금이라도 타협함으로써 누군가를 그자들의 손아귀에 넘기는 거라면, 그딴 건 '동틀 녘'에서 치워 버리세요. 한 푼어치 값도 없으니까요.\"\n\n그녀가 손님 앞에 수프를 놓을 때, 자세를 고쳐 앉은 손님은 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보았는데, 그의 콧수염이 코 밑에서 올라가고 그의 코가 콧수염 위로 내려왔다.\n\n\"자!\" 앞서 말하던 자가 입을 열었다. \"우리 화제로 돌아갑시다. 그 모든 건 제쳐 두고, 여러분, 사람들이 마르세유에서 악마가 풀려났다고 한 건 바로 그 사내가 재판에서 무죄를 받았기 때문이었어요. 그렇게 해서 그 말이 돌기 시작했고, 그게 그 말의 뜻이었지요. 그 이상은 아무것도 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그자를 뭐라고 부른다지요?\" 안주인이 물었다. \"비로, 였던가요?\"\n\n\"리고입니다, 마담.\" 키 큰 스위스 사람이 답했다.\n\n\"리고! 맞아요.\"\n\n나그네의 수프 다음에는 고기 요리가, 그다음에는 채소 요리가 나왔다. 그는 앞에 놓인 것을 모조리 먹고, 포도주 한 병을 비우고, 럼주 한 잔을 청하고, 커피를 마시며 담배를 피웠다. 기운이 돌아오자, 그는 거만해졌다. 그리고 거들먹거리며 '동틀 녘'의 사람들을 상대로, 자기가 거든 자잘한 잡담 속에서 마치 자기 처지가 겉모습보다 한참 높기라도 한 양 행세했다.\n\n사람들은 다른 볼일이 있었는지도, 아니면 자기네가 한 수 아래라고 느꼈는지도 모르나, 어쨌든 차츰 흩어졌고, 다른 손님들이 그 자리를 메우지 않아, 새로 들어온 그 후원자가 '동틀 녘'을 차지하게 되었다. 주인장은 부엌에서 달그락거리고 있었고, 안주인은 조용히 일감에 매달려 있었으며, 기운을 되찾은 나그네는 난로 곁에 앉아 담배를 피우며 해진 발을 녹이고 있었다.\n\n\"실례하오, 마담—그 비로 말이오.\"\n\n\"리고입니다, 무슈.\"\n\n\"리고. 다시 한번 실례하오—어떻게 당신의 노여움을 산 거요?\"\n\n안주인은 한순간엔 이 사내가 잘생긴 사내라고, 또 한순간엔 이 사내가 험상궂게 생긴 사내라고 속으로 생각하던 참이었는데, 코가 내려가고 콧수염이 올라가는 것을 보고는 후자 쪽으로 마음이 강하게 기울었다. 리고는 제 아내를 죽인 범죄자라고 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그래요? 맙소사, 그건 정말 범죄자로군. 한데 그걸 어떻게 아오?\"\n\n\"온 세상이 다 알지요.\"\n\n\"허! 그런데도 그자가 처벌을 면했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"무슈, 법으로는 그자에게 죄를 충분히 입증할 수 없었지요. 법이 그렇게 말한 거예요. 그래도 그자가 저질렀다는 건 온 세상이 다 알아요. 사람들이 어찌나 잘 알았던지, 그자를 갈가리 찢어 죽이려 들었거든요.\"\n\n\"다들 자기 아내들과는 더없이 사이가 좋았던 모양이오?\" 손님이 말했다. \"하하!\"\n\n'동틀 녘'의 안주인은 그를 다시 쳐다보았고, 자신의 마지막 판단이 거의 굳어지는 것을 느꼈다. 하지만 그는 고운 손을 지녔고, 그것을 한껏 과시하며 놀렸다. 그녀는 다시금 그가 결국 험상궂게 생긴 건 아니라고 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n\"혹시 말씀하셨소, 마담—아니면 신사분들 사이에서 나온 이야기였소—그자가 어찌 되었는지?\"\n\n안주인은 고개를 가로저었다. 이것은 그녀의 발랄한 진지함이, 자기가 하는 말에 박자를 맞추어 끄덕이던 그 짓을 멈춘 첫 번째 대화 단계였다. '동틀 녘'에서 신문을 근거로 들먹이기로는, 그자가 제 신변 안전을 위해 감옥에 갇혀 있었다고 했다고 그녀가 말했다. 그게 어찌 되었든, 그자는 마땅한 벌을 면했으니, 더 안된 일이라고.\n\n손님은 마지막 담배를 다 피우며 그녀를 바라보고 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 일감 위로 고개를 숙이고 앉아 있었는데, 그때 그의 얼굴엔 그녀가 만약 보았더라면 그가 잘생겼는지 험상궂은지에 대한 의문을 풀고 한결같은 결론에 이르게 했을 법한 표정이 떠올라 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 정작 눈을 들었을 때, 그 표정은 거기 없었다. 그 손은 그의 텁수룩한 콧수염을 쓰다듬고 있었다.\n\n\"잠자리로 안내해 달라고 청해도 되겠소, 마담?\"\n\n기꺼이지요, 무슈. 어이, 여보! 남편이 그를 이층으로 데려다줄 거예요. 거기엔 나그네 하나가 이미 잠들어 있는데, 피로에 짓눌려 일찌감치 잠자리에 든 사람이지요. 하지만 침대가 둘 딸린 큰 방이라, 스무 명이 들어가도 될 만큼 넉넉해요. 이렇게 '동틀 녘'의 안주인은 재잘재잘 설명하며, 틈틈이 옆문 밖으로 \"어이, 여보!\" 하고 불러 댔다.\n\n마침내 남편이 \"나야, 여보!\" 하고 답하더니, 요리사 모자를 쓴 채 모습을 드러내고는, 가파르고 좁은 계단으로 나그네를 데리고 올라가며 길을 밝혔다. 나그네는 제 외투와 배낭을 직접 들고, 안주인에게 내일 다시 뵐 즐거움에 대한 인사치레를 곁들여 잘 자라고 말했다. 그곳은 큰 방으로, 거칠게 가시 돋친 바닥에, 머리 위로는 회칠도 안 한 서까래가 드러나 있었고, 맞은편 양쪽에 침대 둘이 놓여 있었다. 여기에 '여보'는 들고 온 촛불을 내려놓더니, 배낭 위로 몸을 숙인 손님을 곁눈질로 보며 무뚝뚝하게 \"오른쪽 침대요!\" 하고 일러 주고는 그를 쉬게 두고 떠났다. 주인장은 관상을 잘 보든 못 보든, 그 손님이 험상궂게 생긴 작자라고 단단히 마음을 정해 둔 터였다.\n\n손님은 자기를 위해 준비된 깨끗하고 거친 침구를 경멸하듯 바라보고는, 침대 곁의 골풀 의자에 앉아 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 손바닥에 놓고 세었다. \"먹기는 해야지.\" 그가 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 맙소사, 내일은 다른 놈 돈으로 먹어야겠어!\"\n\n그렇게 앉아 골똘히 생각하며 손바닥 위의 돈을 무심코 가늠하고 있을 때, 다른 침대에 누운 나그네의 깊은 숨소리가 어찌나 규칙적으로 그의 귀에 들어오는지, 그의 눈길이 그쪽으로 끌려갔다. 그 사내는 따뜻하게 이불을 덮고, 머리맡의 흰 커튼을 쳐 두어, 들리기만 할 뿐 보이지는 않았다. 그러나 그 깊고 규칙적인 숨소리는, 이쪽 사내가 해진 신발과 각반을 벗는 동안에도 계속되고, 외투와 넥타이를 벗어 놓았을 때까지도 이어져, 마침내 강한 호기심을 자극하고, 잠든 자의 얼굴을 한번 엿보고 싶은 충동을 불러일으켰다.\n\n그리하여 깨어 있는 나그네는 잠든 나그네의 침대 쪽으로 조금 더, 또 조금 더, 또 조금 더 가까이 살금살금 다가가, 마침내 그 곁에 바짝 섰다. 그래도 얼굴은 볼 수 없었으니, 그가 시트를 얼굴 위로 끌어 올려 두었기 때문이었다. 규칙적인 숨소리가 여전히 이어지자, 그는 매끈한 흰 손을(그 손이 그에게서 슬며시 뻗어 갈 때 어찌나 음흉해 보이던지!) 시트에 갖다 대고는 살그머니 그것을 걷어 냈다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 그가 뒤로 물러서며 속삭였다. \"카발레토잖아!\"\n\n자그마한 이탈리아 사내는, 어쩌면 잠결에 머리맡의 그 은밀한 기척에 영향을 받았는지, 규칙적인 숨을 멈추더니, 길고 깊게 한 번 숨을 들이쉬며 눈을 떴다. 처음엔 떴어도 깨어 있지는 않았다. 그는 몇 초간 누운 채 옛 감옥 동무를 평온하게 바라보다가, 별안간 놀라고 겁에 질린 비명을 지르며 침대에서 벌떡 뛰쳐나왔다.\n\n\"쉿! 왜 이래? 조용히 해! 나야. 날 알아보겠어?\" 다른 사내가 억누른 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 존 뱁티스트는 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고, 여러 가지 기도문과 탄식을 중얼거리며, 부들부들 떨면서 한구석으로 물러나, 바지를 꿰어 입고, 외투의 두 소매를 목에 둘러 묶으면서, 그 옛 친분을 새로 다지기보다는 차라리 문으로 달아나고 싶다는 분명한 기색을 드러냈다. 이를 본 옛 감옥 동무는 문에 등을 기대고 어깨로 그것을 막아섰다.\n\n\"카발레토! 정신 차려, 이 사람아! 눈을 비비고 날 좀 봐. 예전에 날 부르던 이름 말고—그건 쓰지 마—라니에야, 라니에라고 불러!\"\n\n존 뱁티스트는 눈을 한껏 크게 뜨고 그를 바라보며, 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 허공에 여러 번 까딱이는 그 나라 특유의 동작을 해 보였는데, 마치 상대가 평생토록 무슨 말을 꺼내든 미리 다 부정해 버리기로 작정한 듯했다.\n\n\"카발레토! 손을 이리 줘. 너도 라니에라는 신사를 알지. 신사의 손을 잡아 보라고!\"\n\n거들먹거리며 명령하던 그 옛 어조에 굴복해, 존 뱁티스트는 아직 다리가 영 후들거리는 채로 다가가, 후원자의 손에 제 손을 얹었다. 무슈 라니에가 웃었다. 그리고 그것을 한 번 꽉 쥐었다가, 위로 던지듯 놓아주었다.\n\n\"그럼 당신은—\" 존 뱁티스트가 더듬거렸다.\n\n\"면도당하지 않았느냐고? 천만에. 자 봐!\" 라니에가 머리를 휙 돌려 보이며 외쳤다. \"네 것만큼이나 멀쩡히 붙어 있지.\"\n\n존 뱁티스트는 가볍게 몸을 떨며, 자기가 어디 있는지 떠올리려는 듯 방 안을 빙 둘러보았다. 그의 후원자는 그 틈을 타 문에 꽂힌 열쇠를 돌리고, 침대에 가서 앉았다.\n\n\"봐!\" 그가 신발과 각반을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"신사 치고는 영 초라한 행색이라고 너는 말하겠지. 상관없어, 내가 이걸 얼마나 빨리 고치는지 보게 될 테니. 이리 와서 앉아. 옛날 네 자리에 앉으라고!\"\n\n존 뱁티스트는 도무지 안심한 기색이 아닌 채로, 침대 곁 바닥에 앉아, 줄곧 후원자에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"좋아!\" 라니에가 외쳤다. \"이제 우리가 다시 그 지긋지긋한 옛 소굴에 와 있는 셈이로군, 응? 너는 나온 지 얼마나 됐어?\"\n\n\"당신보다 이틀 뒤였어요, 주인님.\"\n\n\"여기엔 어떻게 온 거야?\"\n\n\"거기 머물지 말라고 경고를 받아서, 곧장 그 도시를 떠났지요. 그 뒤로 이리저리 옮겨 다녔어요. 아비뇽에서, 퐁테스프리에서, 리옹에서 잡일을 했고요. 론강에서도, 손강에서도요.\" 말하면서 그는 햇볕에 그을린 손으로 바닥에 그 지명들을 재빠르게 지도 그리듯 짚어 보였다.\n\n\"그래서 어디로 가는 거야?\"\n\n\"가다니요, 주인님?\"\n\n\"그래!\"\n\n존 뱁티스트는 그 질문을 어찌 피해야 할지 모르면서도 피하고 싶어 하는 듯했다. \"바쿠스에 맹세코!\" 그가 마침내, 마치 어쩔 수 없이 털어놓는다는 듯이 말했다. \"파리로 갈까, 어쩌면 영국까지 갈까 하는 생각을 가끔 했지요.\"\n\n\"카발레토. 이건 비밀인데. 나도 파리로 가, 어쩌면 영국까지도. 우리 함께 가자.\"\n\n자그마한 사내는 고개를 끄덕이며 이를 드러내 보였지만, 그것이 더없이 바람직한 약속이라고는 영 확신하지 못하는 눈치였다.\n\n\"우리 함께 가는 거야.\" 라니에가 거듭 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 빨리 나 자신을 신사로 인정받게 만드는지 너도 보게 될 테고, 너도 그 덕을 보게 될 거야. 합의된 거지? 우린 한패지?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 그럼요!\" 자그마한 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 잠들기 전에—그것도 여섯 마디로, 졸리니까—내가 너 앞에 어떻게 나타나는지를 들어 둬. 나, 라니에로 말이야. 그걸 명심해. 다른 이름 말고.\"\n\n\"알트로, 알트로! 리—가 아니라—\" 존 뱁티스트가 그 이름을 미처 다 말하기도 전에, 동무가 그의 턱 밑에 손을 갖다 대고는 사납게 그 입을 틀어막았다.\n\n\"맙소사! 뭐 하는 거야? 내가 짓밟히고 돌에 맞기를 바라는 거냐? 너는 짓밟히고 돌에 맞고 싶어? 그렇게 될 거다. 너는 그들이 나한테 달려들면서 내 감옥 동무는 그냥 놓아둘 거라고 생각하나? 그런 건 꿈도 꾸지 마!\"\n\n그가 친구의 턱을 쥐었던 손을 풀 때 그의 얼굴엔 어떤 표정이 떠올라 있었는데, 친구는 그것으로부터, 만약 사태가 정말로 돌팔매질과 짓밟기로 번진다면 무슈 라니에가 자기를 각별히 점찍어 그 몫을 톡톡히 나눠 받게 해 줄 것임을 짐작했다. 그는 무슈 라니에가 얼마나 세계주의적인 신사인지, 그리고 시시한 차별 따위는 거의 두지 않는 사람인지를 떠올렸다.\n\n\"나는 말이지,\" 무슈 라니에가 말했다. \"네가 마지막으로 본 뒤로 사회로부터 깊이 부당한 대접을 받은 사람이야. 너도 알다시피 나는 예민하고 용감하며, 남을 다스리는 게 내 천성이지. 그런데 사회가 내 그런 자질을 어떻게 대접했지? 나는 거리에서 사람들의 비명을 들었어. 나는 손에 잡히는 무기란 무기는 다 들고 나한테 달려드는 사내들로부터, 특히 여자들로부터 보호받으며 거리를 지나야 했지. 나는 백 대의 매를 맞아 쓰러질까 봐, 끌려 나가 두들겨 맞을까 봐, 갇힌 장소를 비밀로 한 채 신변 보호를 위해 감옥에 누워 있었어. 나는 한밤중에 마르세유에서 짐수레에 실려 나가, 짚에 싸인 채 거기서 몇 리그나 떨어진 곳으로 옮겨졌지. 내 집 근처에 가는 것조차 안전하지 않았고, 주머니엔 거지의 푼돈만 든 채, 그때부터 줄곧 더러운 진창과 궂은 날씨 속을 걸어, 발이 이렇게 절뚝거리게 될 때까지—이 발 좀 봐! 내가 말한, 또 네가 내게 있다고 알고 있는 그런 자질을 지닌 나에게, 사회가 가한 굴욕이 이런 것들이야. 하지만 사회는 그 대가를 치르게 될 거다.\"\n\n이 모든 것을 그는 동무의 귀에 대고, 입술 앞에 손을 가린 채 말했다.\n\n\"여기서조차,\" 그가 같은 식으로 말을 이었다. \"이 보잘것없는 술집에서조차, 사회가 나를 쫓아오는군. 마담이 나를 헐뜯고, 그 손님들이 나를 헐뜯어. 나, 그들쯤은 한 방에 쓰러뜨릴 예법과 교양을 갖춘 이 신사를 말이야! 하지만 사회가 내게 쌓아 올린 부당함은 이 가슴속에 고스란히 간직되어 있지.\"\n\n이 모든 말에, 그 억눌린 쉰 목소리에 귀를 기울이던 존 뱁티스트는 이따금 \"그럼요, 그렇고말고요!\" 하고 말하며, 머리를 흔들고 눈을 감았으니, 마치 더없이 솔직한 사람이라도 사회에 대해 제기할 수 있는 가장 명백한 고발이 거기 있기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"내 신발은 거기 놓아.\" 라니에가 말을 이었다. \"내 외투는 저기 문가에 걸어 말려. 내 모자를 받아.\" 그는 지시가 떨어질 때마다 그대로 따랐다. \"그래서 이게 사회가 나를 처넣은 침대란 말이지, 안 그래? 허. 아주 좋아!\"\n\n그가 침대 위에 길게 몸을 뻗고, 그 사악한 머리에 해진 손수건을 둘러맨 채, 이불 위로 그 사악한 머리만 내놓고 누워 있을 때, 존 뱁티스트는 콧수염이 다시는 그렇게 올라가지 못하고 코가 다시는 그렇게 내려오지 못하게 막을 뻔했던 그 일이 얼마나 아슬아슬하게 일어날 뻔했었는지를 꽤나 생생히 떠올렸다.\n\n\"운명의 주사위 통에서 도로 흔들려 나와 또 네 곁에 떨어졌군, 응? 맙소사! 너한테는 더 잘된 일이지. 너도 덕을 볼 테니까. 나는 오래 쉬어야겠어. 아침엔 자게 내버려 둬.\"\n\n존 뱁티스트는 원하는 만큼 주무시라고 답하고는, 편안한 밤 되시라 빌며 촛불을 껐다. 이쯤이면 그 이탈리아 사내가 다음으로 옷을 벗으리라 짐작했을 법하지만, 그는 정반대로, 신발만 빼고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 옷을 차려입었다. 그렇게 하고 나서 그는 이불 몇 장을 몸에 덮고, 외투는 여전히 목에 둘러맨 채로 침대에 누워, 그 밤을 견뎌 내려 했다.\n\n그가 벌떡 일어났을 때, 대부 격인 동틀 녘이 제 이름의 가게를 빼꼼히 들여다보고 있었다. 그는 일어나, 신발을 손에 들고, 무척 조심스럽게 문의 열쇠를 돌려, 살금살금 계단을 내려갔다. 거기엔 커피와 포도주와 담배와 시럽 냄새 말고는 아무것도 움직이지 않았고, 마담의 자그마한 계산대는 으스스해 보였다. 하지만 그는 간밤에 거기서 마담에게 자기 자그마한 계산서를 치른 터라, 아무도 만나고 싶지 않았다—신발과 배낭을 챙기고, 문을 열고, 달아나는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않았다.\n\n그는 뜻한 바를 이루었다. 그가 문을 열었을 때 아무런 기척도, 목소리도 들리지 않았다. 해진 손수건을 동여맨 사악한 머리가 위층 창문으로 내다보지도 않았다. 해가 평평한 지평선 위로 둥근 원반을 온전히 들어 올려, 자그마한 나무들이 지친 듯 늘어선 가로수와 함께 길게 뻗은 진흙투성이 포석길에서 불꽃을 일으킬 무렵, 검은 점 하나가 길을 따라 움직이며 불타듯 빛나는 빗물 웅덩이들 사이로 첨벙거리며 나아갔으니, 그 검은 점이 바로 후원자에게서 달아나는 존 뱁티스트 카발레토였다.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. Bleeding Heart Yard\n\n런던 자체 안에서도, 명소가 된 어느 교외로 향하는 옛 시골길 위에 — 작가이자 무대 배우였던 윌리엄 셰익스피어의 시절에는 그곳에 왕실 사냥터가 있었으나, 지금은 사람을 사냥하는 자들 말고는 사냥이라 할 것이 남아 있지 않은 그곳에 — '피 흘리는 심장 마당'이 자리하고 있었다. 생김새도 운수도 크게 달라진 곳이었으나, 그래도 옛 영화의 정취가 약간은 남아 있었다. 우람한 굴뚝 더미 두세 개와, 벽으로 막히고 잘게 쪼개져 옛 비례를 알아보지 못하게 되는 신세를 면한 몇 개의 크고 어두운 방들이 이 마당에 한 가닥 풍격을 부여하고 있었다. 이곳에는 가난한 사람들이 살았는데, 사막의 아랍인들이 피라미드의 무너진 돌무더기 사이에 천막을 치듯, 그들도 이 빛바랜 영화 한가운데에 보금자리를 틀었다. 그러나 이 마당에는 이곳이 풍격을 지녔다는, 한 가족과도 같은 정서적 감정이 널리 퍼져 있었다.\n\n마치 야심 찬 도시가 제가 딛고 선 바로 그 땅 위에서 부풀어 오르기라도 한 듯, 땅이 피 흘리는 심장 마당을 둘러싸고 어찌나 높이 솟았던지, 사람들은 본래 진입로의 일부가 아니었던 계단을 한 줄 내려가야만 그곳에 들어설 수 있었고, 나갈 때는 나지막한 문을 지나 초라한 거리들의 미로로 빠져나갔는데, 그 거리들은 빙빙 돌고 또 돌면서 구불구불 다시 본래 높이까지 올라갔다. 마당의 이쪽 끝, 문 위쪽에는 대니얼 도이스의 공장이 있었고, 그곳은 쇠가 쇠에 부딪치는 쟁그랑 소리와 함께, 마치 피 흘리는 쇠의 심장처럼 묵직하게 쿵쿵 두드려 대곤 했다.\n\n마당의 이름이 어디서 유래했는지를 두고 이곳의 의견은 갈라져 있었다. 이곳 거주자들 가운데 더 실리적인 부류는 어느 살인 사건의 전설을 고수했고, 다정다감하고 상상력이 풍부한 부류는, 부드러운 성별 전체를 포함하여, 옛 시절 어느 아가씨의 전설에 충성했다. 그 아가씨는 제 참된 연인에게 충실하기를 고집하며 아버지가 골라 준 구혼자와의 결혼을 거부했다는 이유로, 잔인한 아버지에 의해 제 방에 단단히 갇혔다고 했다. 전설에 따르면 그 아가씨는 창살 너머 창가에 서서, '피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리며 스러지네'를 후렴으로 삼은 상사병의 노래를 흥얼거리는 모습이 자주 보였고, 그러다 끝내 죽었다고 했다. 살인설을 주장하는 쪽에서는, 그 후렴이 실은 아직도 이 마당에 살고 있는, 자수 일을 하는 어느 노처녀이자 낭만주의자의 창작임이 익히 알려진 사실이라며 이의를 제기했다. 그러나 가장 사랑받는 전설이란 모름지기 애정과 결부되어야 하는 법이고, 살인을 저지르는 사람보다 사랑에 빠지는 사람이 훨씬 더 많으니 — 우리가 아무리 못된 존재라 하더라도, 세상이 끝날 때까지 우리가 그 아래에서 살아갈 섭리로 계속 남아 있기를 바라 마지않는 일이니 — '피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리며 스러지네'의 이야기가 압도적 다수로 승리를 거두었다. 어느 쪽도 인근에서 학식 있는 강연을 펼치며, '피 흘리는 심장'이란 한때 이 땅을 소유했던 옛 가문의 문장 표지였다는 사실을 밝혀내는 골동품 연구가들의 말에는 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다. 그리고 그들이 해마다 뒤집어 놓는 모래시계가 가장 흙투성이에 가장 거친 모래로 가득 차 있음을 생각하면, 피 흘리는 심장 마당 사람들이 그 안에서 반짝이는 단 하나의 작은 황금빛 시(詩)의 알갱이를 빼앗기지 않으려 했던 데에는 충분한 이유가 있었던 셈이다.\n\n계단을 통해 마당으로 내려온 사람은 대니얼 도이스, 미글스 씨, 그리고 클레넘이었다. 마당을 따라 걸으며, 한결같이 무거운 아이를 안은 가벼운 아이들로 그득한 양쪽의 열린 문 사이를 지나, 그들은 맞은편 경계인 그 문에 다다랐다. 여기서 아서 클레넘은 멈춰 서서, 미장이 플로니시의 거처를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 런던 사람들의 관습대로, 대니얼 도이스는 그 시각까지도 그 이름을 본 적도 들은 적도 없었다.\n\n그렇지만 리틀 도릿이 말했던 대로, 거처는 충분히 쉽게 찾을 수 있었다. 모퉁이의, 회반죽이 튀어 얼룩진 문 위쪽이었는데, 그 문 안쪽에 플로니시는 사다리 하나와 통 한두 개를 두고 있었다. 그녀가 그의 거처라고 일러 준, 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 마지막 집은 여러 세입자에게 나뉘어 세놓은 큰 집이었다. 그러나 플로니시는 제 이름 아래에 그려 넣은 손 그림으로, 자신이 응접실에 산다는 것을 재치 있게 일러 두었는데, 그 손의 집게손가락(화가가 반지 하나와 가장 점잖은 모양새의 더없이 공들인 손톱을 그려 넣은 손가락)은 묻는 이들을 모두 그 방으로 안내하고 있었다.\n\n미글스 씨와 또 한 번 만날 약속을 잡은 뒤 일행과 헤어진 클레넘은, 혼자 입구로 들어가 손가락 마디로 응접실 문을 두드렸다. 곧 한 여인이 문을 열었는데, 그녀는 한 팔에 아이를 안고 있었고, 한가한 다른 손으로는 옷의 윗부분을 황급히 매만지고 있었다. 이 사람이 플로니시 부인이었고, 이 모성적 동작은 그녀가 깨어 있는 삶의 대부분 동안 행하는 동작이었다.\n\n플로니시 씨가 집에 있느냐고 묻자, 예의 바른 여인인 플로니시 부인이 \"글쎄요, 선생님,\" 하고 말했다. \"속이려는 건 아니지만, 일자리를 찾으러 나갔답니다.\"\n\n'속이려는 건 아니지만'은 플로니시 부인의 말버릇이었다. 그녀는 어떤 경우에도 가능한 한 사람을 속이지 않으려 했으나, 이런 잠정적인 형태로 대답하는 버릇이 있었다.\n\n\"제가 기다리면 곧 돌아올 것 같습니까?\"\n\n\"이 반 시간째 기다리고 있던 참이라,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"이제 곧 들이닥칠 겁니다. 들어오세요, 선생님.\"\n\n아서는 다소 어둡고 답답한(그러면서도 천장은 높은) 응접실로 들어가, 그녀가 놓아 준 의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"속이려는 건 아니지만, 선생님, 제가 그걸 알아챘답니다,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"친절하게 생각합니다.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지 알 수가 없었다. 그래서 그런 기색을 표정으로 드러내자, 그녀가 설명을 해 주었다.\n\n\"이런 가난한 곳에 들어와서 굳이 모자를 벗을 만하다고 여기는 사람은 많지 않거든요,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"그래도 사람들이 생각하는 것보다 그걸 더 마음에 둔답니다.\"\n\n클레넘은, 그토록 사소한 예의가 흔치 않은 일이라는 데에 불편한 마음을 느끼며, 고작 그것뿐인가 싶었다! 그리고 바닥에 앉아 자기를 빤히 쳐다보는 또 다른 어린아이의 볼을 꼬집어 주려 몸을 숙이며, 저 잘생긴 사내아이가 몇 살이냐고 플로니시 부인에게 물었다.\n\n\"이제 막 네 살 됐어요, 선생님,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"정말 잘생긴 꼬마지요, 안 그래요, 선생님? 그런데 이 아이는 좀 병약하답니다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 품에 안은 아기를 다정하게 어르며 달랬다. \"선생님, 혹시 일거리 때문에 오신 건지 여쭤봐도 괜찮으실까요?\" 플로니시 부인이 간절하게 물었다.\n\n어찌나 애타게 묻던지, 만일 그가 어떤 종류든 집을 가지고 있었다면, 아니라고 대답하느니 차라리 한 자 두께로 회반죽을 발라 달라고 했을 것이다. 그러나 그는 아니라고 대답할 수밖에 없었고, 그녀가 한숨을 억누르며 사그라드는 불을 바라볼 때, 그 얼굴에 어린 실망의 그늘을 보았다. 그리고 그는 또한, 플로니시 부인이 가난 탓에 제 몸과 제 살림이 다소 추레해진 젊은 여인이며, 가난과 아이들에게 함께 어찌나 시달렸던지 그 두 힘이 합세하여 그녀의 얼굴을 벌써 주름지게 끌어내려 놓았음을 보았다.\n\n\"일거리란 일거리는 죄다,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"땅속으로 꺼져 버린 것만 같아요, 정말이지.\" (여기서 플로니시 부인은 미장 일에 한정하여 말한 것이었고, 에두름국이나 배너클 가문을 두고 한 말은 아니었다.)\n\n\"일거리를 얻기가 그렇게 어렵습니까?\" 아서 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"플로니시는 그렇게 여긴답니다,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"운이 영 없어요. 정말로요.\"\n\n정말로 그러했다. 그는 인생의 길을 가는 수많은 나그네들 가운데 하나로, 초자연적인 발 티눈이라도 앓는 듯, 절름발이 경쟁자들과조차 보조를 맞추지 못하는 사람이었다. 기꺼이 일하려 들고, 부지런하고, 마음이 여리되 머리는 단단하지 못한 사내인 플로니시는, 제 운수를 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 순순히 받아들였으나, 그것은 거친 운수였다. 누군가 그를 필요로 하는 듯 보이는 일이 어찌나 드물고, 그의 솜씨가 조금이라도 요구되는 일이 어찌나 예외적이던지, 그의 흐릿한 머리로는 어찌하여 그런지를 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 그래서 그는 오는 대로 받아들였다. 그는 온갖 어려움 속으로 굴러떨어졌다가 또 굴러 나왔으며, 그렇게 인생을 굴러 지나오는 동안 적잖이 멍이 들고 말았다.\n\n\"일거리를 찾아다니지 않아서가 아닌 건 확실해요,\" 플로니시 부인이 눈썹을 치켜올리고 쇠살대 틈새에서 문제의 해답을 찾으며 말했다. \"일거리가 있을 때 거기 매달려 일하지 않아서도 아니고요. 제 남편이 일을 두고 불평하는 건 아무도 들어 본 적이 없답니다.\"\n\n이러나저러나 이것이 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 보편적인 불운이었다. 때때로 일손이 부족하다는 호소가 애처롭게 떠돌며 공공연하게 불거지곤 했는데 — 어떤 사람들은 마치 제 조건대로 일손을 부릴 절대적인 권리라도 있는 양 그것을 유난히 못마땅하게 여겼다 — 그러나 피 흘리는 심장 마당은 영국 어느 곳 못지않게 기꺼이 일하려는 마당이었음에도, 그 수요 덕을 본 적이 결코 없었다. 그 고귀한 옛 가문, 배너클 일가는 오래전부터 자신들의 위대한 원칙에 너무도 바빠 그 문제를 들여다볼 겨를이 없었다. 그리고 사실 그 문제는, 스틸트스토킹 일가를 제외한 다른 모든 고귀한 옛 가문들을 압도하려는 그들의 경계심과는 아무런 상관이 없었다.\n\n플로니시 부인이 자기 자리에 없는 남편에 관해 이런 말을 하고 있는 동안, 그 남편이 돌아왔다. 볼이 매끈하고 혈색이 좋으며 모래빛 구레나룻을 기른 서른 살의 사내였다. 다리는 길고, 무릎은 잘 휘청거리고, 얼굴은 어리숙하고, 플란넬 윗옷을 걸쳤으며, 회반죽으로 허옇게 물들어 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람이 플로니시예요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"제가 찾아온 것은,\" 클레넘이 일어서며 말했다. \"도릿 가족에 관해서 잠깐 이야기를 나누는 호의를 부탁드리고 싶어서입니다.\"\n\n플로니시는 의심스러워졌다. 채권자의 냄새를 맡은 듯했다. 그가 말했다. \"아, 예. 그렇군요. 그 가족에 관해서 내가 어느 신사분께 무슨 흡족한 답을 드릴 수 있을지 모르겠는데요. 그게 무슨 일로 그러시는데요?\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 당신 짐작보다 더 잘 알고 있습니다,\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n플로니시는 답례로 미소 짓지 않은 채, 그래도 자기는 이 신사분과 안면을 트는 영광은 누리지 못했다고 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요,\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신의 친절한 행적을 저는 건너서 들었습니다만, 더없이 믿을 만한 출처를 통해서지요. 리틀 도릿을 통해서요. — 제 말은,\" 그가 설명을 덧붙였다. \"도릿 양 말입니다.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 맞으시죠? 오! 선생님 말씀은 들었습니다.\"\n\n\"저도 당신 말을 들었습니다,\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"다시 앉으시지요, 선생님, 환영합니다. — 그러니까, 예,\" 플로니시가 의자를 끌어다 앉고는, 큰아이를 무릎 위에 올려 그 아이 머리 너머로 낯선 이에게 말하는 정신적 지지를 얻으며 말했다. \"저도 한때 자물쇠의 잘못된 쪽에 있어 봤지요, 그렇게 해서 도릿 양을 알게 된 겁니다. 저와 제 아내는 도릿 양을 아주 잘 알지요.\"\n\n\"막역한 사이예요!\" 플로니시 부인이 외쳤다. 사실 그녀는 그 친분을 어찌나 자랑스러워했던지, 도릿 양의 아버지가 파산할 때 진 빚의 액수를 엄청나게 부풀려 떠벌리는 바람에 마당 사람들의 마음에 약간의 씁쓸함을 불러일으키기도 했다. 피 흘리는 심장 마당 사람들은 그녀가 그토록 지체 높은 사람들을 안다고 떠벌리는 것을 못마땅해했다.\n\n\"제가 먼저 알게 된 건 그분 아버지였지요. 그리고 그분과 알고 지내게 되면서, 그러니까 — 음 — 그분의 따님과도 알게 된 겁니다,\" 플로니시가 같은 말을 되풀이하듯 말했다.\n\n\"그렇군요.\"\n\n\"아! 그분 예절이라니! 그 품위라니! 마셜시 감옥에서 시들어 버리기에는 참으로 아까운 신사분이지요! 그런데, 어쩌면 모르실지도 모르겠는데,\" 플로니시가 목소리를 낮추고, 가엾이 여기거나 업신여겨야 마땅할 일을 도리어 비뚤어진 감탄으로 말했다. \"도릿 양과 그 여동생이 생계를 위해 일을 한다는 걸 그분께는 차마 알리지도 못한다는 걸 모르실지도 몰라요. 그럼요!\" 플로니시가 먼저 아내를, 그다음에는 방 안 전체를 우스꽝스러운 의기양양함으로 둘러보며 말했다. \"그분께는 차마 알리지도 못하는 거예요, 차마 못 하는 거라고요!\"\n\n\"그 점을 감탄할 만한 일로 보지는 않습니다만,\" 클레넘이 차분하게 말했다. \"그분이 무척 안쓰럽군요.\" 이 말은 플로니시에게, 그것이 결국 그리 훌륭한 인격의 단면은 아닐지도 모른다는 생각을 처음으로 일깨워 준 듯했다. 그는 잠시 그것을 곰곰이 생각하다가, 이내 포기했다.\n\n\"제 경우로 말하자면,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"도릿 씨는 제게 정말이지 제가 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 다정하게 대해 주신답니다. 우리 사이의 차이와 거리를 생각하면 그 이상이지요. 하지만 우리가 얘기하던 건 도릿 양이었잖습니까.\"\n\n\"그렇지요. 그런데 그분을 어떻게 제 어머니 댁에 소개하게 되었는지요!\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 구레나룻에서 회반죽 한 조각을 떼어 내어 입술 사이에 넣고는, 사탕 알맹이라도 되는 양 혀로 굴리며 곰곰 생각하더니, 명료하게 설명할 능력이 없음을 깨닫고는, 아내에게 도움을 청하며 말했다. \"샐리, 자네가 어찌 된 일인지 얘기해 주는 게 낫겠어, 여보.\"\n\n\"도릿 양이,\" 샐리가 아기를 좌우로 흔들어 어르며, 또다시 가운을 헝클어뜨리려 드는 그 작은 손에 턱을 얹은 채 말했다. \"어느 날 오후에 글이 적힌 쪽지 하나를 들고 여기로 왔어요, 바느질 일감을 원한다면서, 혹시 여기 주소를 적어 놔도 폐가 되지 않겠느냐고 물었지요.\" (플로니시는 마치 교회에서 응답이라도 하듯 낮은 목소리로 \"여기 주소를\"이라고 따라 했다.) \"그래서 저랑 플로니시가, 아니에요, 도릿 양, 폐가 안 돼요, 했지요,\" (플로니시가 \"폐가 안 돼요\"라고 따라 했다.) \"그래서 그분이 거기에 그대로 적어 넣었어요. 그러고 나서 저랑 플로니시가, 어머나 도릿 양!\" (플로니시가 \"어머나 도릿 양\"이라고 따라 했다.) \"한 군데보다 더 여러 군데에 알리는 방법으로, 그걸 서너 번 베껴 두면 어떻겠느냐고 했지요. 아니에요, 그런 생각은 못 했네요, 하지만 그렇게 할게요, 하고 도릿 양이 말했어요. 그러고는 거기에 맞춰 이 탁자 위에서 고운 글씨로 베껴 냈고, 플로니시는 마침 그때 일거리가 있어서 그걸 일하는 데로 가져갔고,\" (플로니시가 \"마침 그때 일거리\"라고 따라 했다.) \"또 마당 집주인한테도 가져갔지요. 그렇게 해서 클레넘 부인이 처음으로 도릿 양에게 일을 맡기게 된 거랍니다.\" 플로니시가 \"도릿 양에게 일을 맡기게\"라고 따라 했고, 이야기를 마친 플로니시 부인은 그 작은 손에 입을 맞추며 손가락을 깨무는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"마당 집주인이라면,\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"그분은 —\"\n\n\"이름이 캐스비 씨예요, 그 사람이,\" 플로니시가 말했다. \"그리고 팽크스라는 사람이 집세를 걷고요. 그게,\" 플로니시 씨가 어떤 특정한 목적과도 연결되지 않고 어디로도 이어지지 않는 듯한 느릿한 사색으로 그 주제에 머물며 덧붙였다. \"그게 그 사람들 사정이라 할 만해요, 믿으시든 말든 좋으실 대로요.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 클레넘이 이번에는 자기가 생각에 잠겨 대꾸했다. \"캐스비 씨라니! 그것도 오래전에 알던 옛 지인이군요!\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 이 사실에 대해 어떤 토를 달 길도 찾지 못했고, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 사실 그가 거기에 조금이라도 관심을 가질 이유가 전혀 없었으므로, 아서 클레넘은 이번 방문의 본래 용건으로 넘어갔다. 곧, 만일 그 젊은이에게 자립심과 자조심의 잔재가 조금이라도 남아 있다고 가정한다면 — 의심할 바 없이 매우 과한 가정이긴 했지만 — 그 자립심과 자조심을 되도록 해치지 않으면서 팁의 석방을 성사시키는 도구로 플로니시를 쓰는 일이었다. 피고 본인의 입에서 소송의 사유를 전해 들은 플로니시는, 원고가 '찬터'라는 것을 — 즉, 성가를 부르는 사람이 아니라 말을 파는 사람이라는 것을 — 아서에게 일러 주었고, 자기(플로니시)가 보기에는 1파운드당 10실링이면 '깔끔하게 마무리될' 것이며 그 이상은 돈 낭비라고 했다. 의뢰인과 그 도구는 곧 함께 마차를 몰아 하이 홀번의 어느 마구간 마당으로 갔다. 그곳에는 최저로 잡아도 75기니는 나가는(체형을 보기 좋게 만들려고 삼키게 한 산탄의 값어치는 셈에 넣지 않더라도) 유난히 훌륭한 회색 거세마 한 마리가 있었는데, 지난주에 첼튼엄의 바버리 대위 부인을 태우고 달아나 버린 탓에 단돈 20파운드 지폐에 처분될 참이었다. 그 부인은 이 말의 혈기를 감당할 깜냥이 못 되었고, 순전히 앙심에서 그 우스꽝스러운 값에 팔아 치우겠다고 — 다시 말해 거저 줘 버리겠다고 — 고집을 부린 것이었다. 플로니시는 의뢰인을 밖에 남겨 둔 채 혼자 그 마당으로 올라갔는데, 거기서 꽉 끼는 황갈색 바지에 다소 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 자그마한 갈고리 지팡이를 들고, 파란 목도리를 두른 한 신사(글로스터셔의 머룬 대위로, 바버리 대위의 절친한 친구였다)를 만났다. 이 신사는 마침, 광고를 보고 그 주소를 들여다볼 법한, 말을 제대로 볼 줄 알고 좋은 물건을 잽싸게 낚아챌 안목을 지닌 진짜 감정가에게, 그 유난히 훌륭한 회색 거세마에 관한 이런 자잘한 사정들을 친절히 일러 주려고 그 자리에 있었던 것이다. 그런데 이 신사가 마침 팁 사건의 원고이기도 했던 터라, 그는 플로니시 씨를 자기 변호사에게 가 보라고 떠넘겼고, 플로니시 씨가 20파운드 지폐를 들고 나타나지 않는 한 그와 거래하기는커녕 마당에 있는 꼴조차 견디지 못하겠다고 했다. 오직 그런 경우라야만, 그 신사는 겉모습으로 미루어 플로니시가 진지하게 거래에 임할 작정임을 점쳐 보고, 그와 이야기를 나눠 볼 마음이 생길 수도 있다는 것이었다. 이 언질에 플로니시 씨는 의뢰인과 상의하러 물러났다가, 곧 요구받은 증표를 들고 돌아왔다. 그러자 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 나머지 20파운드를 마련하는 데 시간이 얼마나 필요하시오? 자, 한 달 드리리다.\" 그러고는 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 그럼 어떻게 해 드리리까. 나머지 20파운드에 대해, 은행에서 지급되는 넉 달짜리 좋은 어음을 하나 끊어 주시오!\" 그러고도 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 이리 와 보시오. 이게 내 마지막 말이오. 지금 10파운드를 더 내놓으면, 내 펜으로 깨끗이 그어 버리리다.\" 그러고도 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 이렇게 말해 두겠소, 이걸로 끝장이오. 그자가 날 험하게 대하긴 했지만, 지금 5파운드를 더 내고 포도주 한 병만 주면 놔주리다. 거래할 셈이면 한다고 하고, 마음에 안 들면 관두시오.\" 그러고도 그것마저 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 마침내 말했다. \"그럼, 이리 내놓으시오!\" — 그러고는 처음 제안한 액수를 받고 영수증을 끊어 죄수를 풀어 주었다.\n\n\"플로니시 씨,\" 아서가 말했다. \"부디 제 비밀을 지켜 주시리라 믿겠습니다. 그 젊은이에게 그가 자유의 몸이 되었음을 알려 주고, 당신이 이름을 밝힐 수 없는 어떤 사람으로부터 빚을 청산하라는 부탁을 받았다고 전해 주신다면, 제게 도움을 주실 뿐 아니라, 그에게도, 또 그의 누이에게도 도움을 주시는 게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"마지막 이유만으로도, 선생님,\" 플로니시가 말했다. \"충분합니다. 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"어느 벗이 그의 석방을 얻어 냈다고, 원하신다면 말씀하셔도 됩니다. 다른 누구를 위해서가 아니더라도 누이를 위해서, 그가 그 자유를 잘 활용하기를 바라는 어느 벗이라고요.\"\n\n\"분부대로 따르겠습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그리고 한 가지만 더 베풀어 주신다면, 그 가족을 더 잘 아시는 만큼, 제게 거리낌 없이 소식을 전해 주시고, 제가 리틀 도릿에게 조심스럽고 진정으로 도움이 될 만한 방법이 있다면 일러 주시기 바랍니다. 그래 주시면 신세를 진 것으로 알겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마세요, 선생님,\" 플로니시가 대답했다. \"그건 똑같이 기쁨이자 또 — 그건 똑같이 기쁨이자 또 —\" 두 번이나 애써 본 끝에도 문장의 균형을 맞추지 못하자, 플로니시 씨는 현명하게도 그것을 그만두었다. 그는 클레넘의 명함과 적당한 사례금을 받아들였다.\n\n그는 자기 임무를 당장 끝내려고 안달이었고, 의뢰인도 같은 마음이었다. 그래서 의뢰인은 그를 마셜시 정문에 내려 주겠다고 했고, 두 사람은 블랙프라이어스 다리를 건너 그쪽으로 마차를 몰았다. 가는 길에 아서는 새로 사귄 벗에게서 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 안살림에 관한 두서없는 요약을 끌어냈다. 거기 사람들은 다들 쪼들린다고, 정말이지 유난히 쪼들린다고 플로니시 씨는 말했다. 글쎄, 어찌 된 일인지는 자기도 모르겠고, 누가 어찌 된 일인지를 안다고도 생각지 않으며, 자기가 아는 거라곤 그저 사정이 그렇다는 것뿐이었다. 사람이 제 등으로, 제 뱃속으로 제가 가난하다는 걸 느낄 때, 그 사람은(플로니시 씨는 이것을 확고한 신념으로 내놓았다) 어찌 됐든 자기가 가난하다는 걸 잘 알고 있으며, 그걸 말로 떼어 낼 수 없는 건 그에게 쇠고기를 말로 먹일 수 없는 것과 매한가지였다. 그런데 보다시피, 형편이 좀 나은 어떤 사람들은 — 그런 사람들 가운데 상당수도 제 처지가 거기 사람들 못지않게 빠듯하거나 그보다 더한 경우도 있다고 들었다지만 — 마당 사람들이 '앞날을 내다보지 못한다'고('내다보지 못한다'가 그들이 즐겨 쓰는 말이었다) 말했다. 이를테면 어떤 사내가 아내와 아이들을 데리고 짐마차를 타고 햄프턴 코트로 가는 걸 보면, 일 년에 한 번쯤 있는 일인데도, 그들은 \"아니! 자네 가난하다더니, 앞날도 못 내다보는 친구 같으니!\" 했다. 아니, 세상에, 그게 사람한테 얼마나 모진 일이냐는 것이다! 사람이 어찌하란 말인가? 미쳐서 우울에 빠질 수도 없고, 설령 그런다 한들 그들에게 득 될 것도 없었다. 플로니시 씨가 판단하기로는 도리어 손해였다. 그런데도 그들은 사람을 미쳐서 우울에 빠지게 만들려는 듯했다. 늘 그러고 있었다 — 오른손으로가 아니면 왼손으로라도. 마당 사람들이 무얼 하고 있느냐고? 직접 한번 그들을 들여다보면 안다는 것이다. 처녀들과 그 어머니들이 바느질이며, 구두 꿰매기며, 단 장식이며, 조끼 짓기에 매달려, 밤낮으로 또 밤낮으로 일하고도, 결국 입에 풀칠이나 겨우 할 정도였다 — 흔히 그조차 못 했다. 댈 수 있는 거의 온갖 직종의 사람들이 다 있었고, 다들 일하고 싶어 했지만, 그래도 일을 얻지 못했다. 평생을 일한 끝에, 구빈원에 들어가 갇혀 지내는 노인들도 있었는데, 그곳에서의 먹거리와 잠자리와 대접은 통틀어 — 플로니시 씨는 '제조업자'라고 말했지만, 아무래도 '범죄자'를 가리킨 듯했다 — 그들보다도 훨씬 못했다. 아니, 사람이 위안의 부스러기 하나를 찾아 어디로 발길을 돌려야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 그게 누구 탓이냐고 하면, 플로니시 씨는 그게 누구 탓인지 알지 못했다. 그는 누가 고통받는지는 말할 수 있어도, 그게 누구 잘못인지는 말할 수 없었다. 그걸 알아내는 게 자기가 할 일도 아니었고, 설령 알아낸들 자기 말에 누가 귀를 기울이겠는가? 그가 아는 거라곤, 그 일을 맡은 자들이 그걸 바로잡지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그것이 저절로 바로잡히지도 않았다는 것뿐이었다. 한마디로, 그의 비논리적인 의견은, 사람을 위해 아무것도 해 줄 수 없다면 차라리 그 일을 해 준답시고 그에게서 아무것도 받아 가지 않는 편이 낫다는 것이었다. 그가 헤아릴 수 있는 한, 결국 그쯤으로 귀결되는 이야기였다. 이렇듯 장황하고, 점잖게 투덜대는, 어리숙한 방식으로 플로니시는 마치 시작이나 끝을 더듬어 찾으려는 눈먼 사람처럼, 제 처지의 엉킨 실타래를 이리저리 돌려 댔고, 그러는 사이 그들은 감옥 정문에 다다랐다. 거기서 그는 의뢰인을 홀로 남겨 두었고, 의뢰인은 마차를 몰아 떠나면서, 에두름국에서 하루 이틀 거리 안에 도대체 얼마나 많은 플로니시들이 있어, 그 영광스러운 기관에서는 귀로 알아듣지도 못하는 똑같은 곡조에 갖가지 묘한 변주를 붙이고 있을지를 궁금해했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. Patriarchal\n\n캐스비 씨의 이름이 다시 나오자, 도착하던 날 밤 플린트윈치 부인이 부채질해 놓은 호기심과 관심의 꺼져 가던 불씨가 클레넘의 기억 속에서 되살아났다. 플로라 캐스비는 그의 소년 시절에 사랑했던 여인이었다. 그리고 플로라는 머리가 나무토막 같은 늙은 크리스토퍼(그와 거래를 해 본 어떤 불경한 자들은 아직도 이따금 그를 그렇게 불렀는데, 친숙함이 그 속담대로의 결과를 낳았던 모양이다)의 딸이자 외동이었다. 그는 주간 임차인을 많이 거느려 부유하다는 평판이 있었고, 별 가망 없어 보이는 여러 골목과 뒷마당의 돌멩이에서 적잖은 피를 짜낸다는 말을 들었다.\n\n며칠간 묻고 알아본 끝에, 아서 클레넘은 마셜시의 아버지의 사정이 정말로 가망 없는 것임을 확신하게 되었고, 다시 그를 자유의 몸으로 만들어 주겠다는 생각을 슬프게 단념했다. 지금으로서는 리틀 도릿에 관해서도 희망적인 문의를 할 만한 것이 없었다. 그러나 그는 이 친분을 다시 잇는다면 — 그가 아는 한으로는 — 그 가엾은 아이에게 도움이 될지도 모른다고 스스로에게 변론했다. 굳이 덧붙이자면, 리틀 도릿이라는 존재가 아예 없었다 하더라도 그가 캐스비 씨의 집 문 앞에 나타났으리라는 것은 의심의 여지가 없다. 우리 모두가 행동의 동기에 관하여 스스로를 어떻게 속이는지 — 다시 말해, 우리의 더 깊은 자아를 제외한 일반적인 사람들이 스스로를 어떻게 속이는지 — 우리 모두 알고 있으니 말이다.\n\n그녀와는 아무 상관도 없는 일을 하면서도 자신이 여전히 리틀 도릿을 후원하고 있는 것이라는, 그 나름으로는 꽤 정직한 편안한 인상을 품은 채, 그는 어느 오후 캐스비 씨의 거리 모퉁이에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다. 캐스비 씨는 그레이즈 인 로드에 있는 어느 거리에 살았는데, 그 거리는 큰길에서 갈라져 나와 단숨에 골짜기로 내려갔다가 다시 펜턴빌 언덕 꼭대기까지 치달아 오르려는 작정으로 출발했지만, 스무 야드 만에 숨이 차 멈춰 서서는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 자리에 서 있었다. 지금은 그 일대에 그런 곳이 없지만, 그 거리는 여러 해 동안 거기 남아, 단숨에 가로질러 버리려 했던 그 황무지 — 열매 맺지 못하는 텃밭으로 군데군데 기워지고 발진처럼 돋아난 여름 별장들로 오톨도톨한 그 황무지를 좌절한 얼굴로 바라보고 있었다.\n\n‘저 집은,’ 클레넘은 문 쪽으로 길을 건너며 생각했다, ‘우리 어머니 집만큼이나 거의 변하지 않았고, 그만큼이나 음울해 보이는군. 하지만 닮은 것은 겉뿐이야. 나는 저 안의 차분한 정적을 알고 있어. 묵은 장미 잎과 라벤더를 담은 항아리들의 냄새가 여기까지 풍겨 오는 것만 같아.’\n\n낡은 형태의 반짝이는 놋쇠 문고리를 그가 두드리자 하녀 하나가 문으로 나왔는데, 그 빛바랜 향기는 정말로 지나간 봄에 대한 희미한 기억을 머금은 겨울 입김처럼 그를 맞이했다. 그는 진중하고, 고요하고, 공기 한 점 새지 않는 그 집으로 발을 들였다 — 동방의 방식으로 벙어리 종복들이 목 졸라 숨통을 막아 놓은 집이라고 상상해도 좋을 만한 집이었다 — 그리고 다시 닫히는 문은 소리와 움직임을 모조리 막아 버리는 듯했다. 가구들은 격식 차리고, 근엄하고, 퀘이커교도처럼 검소했지만 잘 관리되어 있었다. 그리고 인간이라는 존재에서 나무 의자에 이르기까지, 많이 쓰라고 만들어졌으나 거의 쓰이지 않은 채 고이 간직된 것이 자아낼 수 있는, 그 무엇 못지않게 호감 가는 자태를 띠고 있었다. 계단 위 어딘가에서 근엄한 시계가 똑딱이고 있었다. 같은 방향에서 노래하지 않는 새 한 마리가, 마치 저도 똑딱이듯 제 새장을 쪼고 있었다. 응접실 난로의 불도 벽난로 안에서 똑딱였다. 응접실 난롯가에는 단 한 사람이 있었고, 그 주머니 속의 요란한 회중시계가 또렷이 똑딱이고 있었다.\n\n하녀가 ‘클레넘 씨’라는 두 마디를 어찌나 나직이 똑딱여 놓았던지 그 소리가 들리지 않았고, 그래서 그는 하녀가 닫은 문 안쪽에 아무도 알아채지 못한 채 서 있었다. 인생의 황혼에 접어든 한 남자의 형상이, 불빛이 그 위에서 일렁일 때마다 매끄러운 잿빛 눈썹이 똑딱임에 맞추어 움직이는 듯한 그 남자가, 펠트 신발을 신은 발을 깔개에 올린 채 안락의자에 앉아, 두 엄지손가락을 천천히 서로 돌리고 있었다. 이것이 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비였다 — 한눈에 알아볼 수 있었다 — 제 단단한 가구들만큼이나 이십 년이 넘도록 변하지 않은, 자기 도자기 항아리 속의 묵은 장미 잎과 묵은 라벤더만큼이나 계절의 변화에 닿지 않은 사람이었다.\n\n아마 이 성가신 세상에, 소년 시절을 그려 보기가 이토록 상상력을 성가시게 하는 사람은 일찍이 없었을 것이다. 그런데도 그는 살아오는 동안 거의 변하지 않았다. 그가 앉아 있는 방에서 그를 마주 보고 있는 것은 한 소년의 초상화였는데, 그를 본 사람이라면 누구나 그것이 열 살 난 크리스토퍼 캐스비 도련님임을 알아보았을 것이다. 다만 건초 갈퀴를 들고 변장한 모습이었는데, 그 갈퀴에 대해 그는 잠수종에 대해서만큼의 취미나 쓸모밖에 가져 본 적이 없었다. 그리고 (제 다리 하나를 깔고 앉아) 제비꽃 둑에 앉아, 마을 교회의 첨탑에 조숙한 명상에 잠긴 모습이었다. 거기에는 똑같이 매끄러운 얼굴과 이마, 똑같이 평온한 푸른 눈, 똑같이 차분한 분위기가 있었다. 너무 번쩍거려서 그토록 커 보이던 반짝이는 대머리, 그리고 결코 자르지 않아 그토록 자비로워 보이던, 명주실이나 유리솜 같은 옆머리와 뒷머리의 긴 잿빛 머리카락은, 물론 노인에게서처럼 소년에게서 볼 수 있는 것은 아니었다. 그렇더라도 건초 갈퀴를 든 그 천사 같은 아이에게서, 펠트 신발을 신은 가부장의 싹은 분명히 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n가부장이란 많은 이들이 즐겨 그에게 붙여 준 이름이었다. 이웃의 여러 노부인들은 그를 마지막 가부장이라 불렀다. 그토록 잿빛이고, 그토록 느리고, 그토록 조용하고, 그토록 무덤덤하고, 머리가 그토록 울퉁불퉁한 그에게는 가부장이라는 말이 딱 맞았다. 그는 거리에서 붙들려, 화가와 조각가의 가부장 모델이 되어 달라고 정중히 간청받기도 했다. 사실 어찌나 끈질겼던지, 가부장의 특징을 기억해 내거나 하나 지어내는 일이 미술의 능력 밖인 듯 보일 지경이었다. 남녀 박애주의자들은 그가 누구냐고 물었고, ‘데시머스 타이트 배너클 경의 옛 도시 대리인이었던 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비’라는 대답을 듣고는 실망의 환희에 차서 외쳤다. ‘오! 어찌하여 저런 머리를 가지고도 인류의 은인이 되지 못한단 말인가! 오! 어찌하여 저런 머리를 가지고도 고아의 아버지가, 의지할 데 없는 자의 벗이 되지 못한단 말인가!’ 그러나 그런 머리를 가지고도 그는 여전히 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비, 세간의 평판으로 집세 재산이 많다는 그 사람으로 남아 있었다. 그리고 그런 머리를 하고서 그는 지금 자기 고요한 응접실에 앉아 있었다. 사실 그가 그런 머리 없이 거기 앉아 있기를 기대하는 것이야말로 이만저만한 불합리가 아닐 것이다.\n\n아서 클레넘이 그의 주의를 끌려고 움직이자, 잿빛 눈썹이 그쪽으로 돌아왔다.\n\n‘실례합니다.’ 클레넘이 말했다. ‘제가 들어온 것을 알리는 소리를 못 들으신 모양이군요?’\n\n‘예, 손님, 못 들었습니다. 저를 보러 오셨습니까?’\n\n‘인사를 드리러 왔습니다.’\n\n캐스비 씨는 마지막 말에 깃털 하나만큼 실망한 듯했는데, 어쩌면 손님이 다른 무언가를 치르고 싶어 하리라 마음의 준비를 하고 있었던 모양이다.* ‘제가, 손님,’ 그가 말을 이었다 — ‘부디 앉으시지요 — 제가 영광스럽게도 알고 지내는 분을 — ? 아! 정말, 그렇군요, 그런 것 같습니다! 제가 저 이목구비를 아는 분이라고 짐작하는 것이 틀리지 않은 듯합니다. 플린트윈치 씨로부터 이 나라로 돌아오셨다는 소식을 들은 그 신사분을 마주하고 있는 것 같은데요?’\n* 인사(respects)를 드린다는 말을, 캐스비는 빚이나 돈을 치른다(pay)는 뜻으로 기대했다는 말장난.\n\n‘바로 지금 찾아온 손님이 그렇습니다.’\n\n‘정말입니까! 클레넘 씨?’\n\n‘다름 아닌 그 사람입니다, 캐스비 씨.’\n\n‘클레넘 씨, 만나서 반갑습니다. 우리가 만난 이후로 어떻게 지내셨습니까?’\n\n지난 사반세기 동안 건강과 기분에 이따금 자잘한 기복을 겪었노라 설명할 가치까지는 없다고 여기며, 클레넘은 두루뭉술하게 더없이 잘 지냈다든가 그쯤 되는 말로 답하고는, 가부장다운 빛을 그에게 비추고 있는 ‘저 머리’의 임자와 악수를 나누었다.\n\n‘우리도 나이를 먹었군요, 클레넘 씨.’ 크리스토퍼 캐스비가 말했다.\n\n‘우리도 — 젊어지지는 않았지요.’ 클레넘이 말했다. 이런 현명한 발언을 해 놓고 그는 자신이 도무지 재치 있게 빛나지 못하고 있음을 느꼈고, 자기가 긴장하고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n‘그리고 존경하는 춘부장께서는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘이제 안 계시군요! 그 소식을 듣고 비통했습니다, 클레넘 씨, 비통했어요.’\n\n아서는 더없이 감사하다는 통상적인 말로 답했다.\n\n‘한때는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘당신 부모님과 제가 친밀한 사이가 아니던 시절이 있었지요. 우리 사이에 사소한 집안 오해가 있었습니다. 존경하는 당신 어머님께서 아드님을 두고 다소 질투하셨던 모양입니다. 아드님이라 하면, 다름 아닌 훌륭하신 당신 본인, 당신 본인을 두고 하는 말입니다.’\n\n그의 매끄러운 얼굴에는 잘 익은 담장 과실 같은 화색이 돌았다. 그 화색 도는 얼굴과, 저 머리와, 푸른 눈이 어우러져, 그는 보기 드문 지혜와 덕을 담은 말을 전하고 있는 듯이 보였다. 마찬가지로 그의 인상은 자애로움으로 가득 차 넘치는 듯했다. 그 지혜가 어디 있는지, 그 덕이 어디 있는지, 그 자애가 어디 있는지 아무도 말할 수 없었으련만, 그것들은 모두 그의 어딘가에 깃들어 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n‘하지만 그런 시절은,’ 캐스비 씨가 말을 이었다, ‘지나갔고 떠나갔습니다, 지나갔고 떠나갔어요. 저는 이따금 존경하는 당신 어머님을 방문하는 기쁨을 누리며, 그분이 시련을 견디시는, 시련을 견디시는 그 의연함과 정신력에 감탄하곤 합니다.’\n\n이렇게 짤막한 되풀이를 할 때면, 그는 두 손을 앞에 모으고 앉아, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 부드럽게 미소 지으며 그리했다. 마치 말로 옮기기엔 너무도 감미롭고 깊은 무언가를 마음속에 품고 있는 것처럼. 마치 너무 높이 날아오를까 봐 그것을 입에 담는 기쁨을 스스로 삼가는 것처럼. 그래서 그의 온유함은 차라리 아무 의미 없는 쪽을 택했다.\n\n‘그런 기회 가운데 한 번은,’ 아서가 스쳐 지나가는 기회를 붙들며 말했다, ‘친절하게도 우리 어머니께 리틀 도릿 이야기를 하셨다고 들었습니다.’\n\n‘리틀 — ? 도릿? 그건 내 작은 임차인 하나가 내게 얘기해 준 그 삯바느질꾼 아닙니까? 그래요, 그래. 도릿? 그게 그 이름이지요. 아, 그래, 그래요! 당신은 그 여자를 리틀 도릿이라 부르시오?’\n\n그쪽으로는 길이 없었다. 그 샛길에서 나오는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 더 이어지지 않았다.\n\n‘제 딸 플로라는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘아마 들으셨겠지만, 클레넘 씨, 여러 해 전에 결혼해서 가정을 꾸렸지요. 그런데 결혼한 지 몇 달 만에 남편을 여의는 불행을 겪었습니다. 지금은 다시 저와 함께 살고 있어요. 당신이 여기 와 계시다고 제가 알려도 좋다 하시면, 그 애가 당신을 만나 기뻐할 겁니다.’\n\n‘물론입니다.’ 클레넘이 답했다. ‘당신의 친절이 저보다 앞서지 않으셨다면 제가 먼저 청했을 부탁입니다.’\n\n이에 캐스비 씨는 펠트 신발을 신고 일어나, 느릿하고 묵직한 걸음으로(그는 코끼리 같은 체격이었다) 문 쪽으로 갔다. 그는 자락이 길고 넓은 병록색 외투를 걸치고, 병록색 바지에, 병록색 조끼를 입고 있었다. 가부장들이 병록색 모직 천을 입었던 것은 아니지만, 그래도 그의 옷차림은 가부장다워 보였다.\n\n그가 방을 떠나 다시 똑딱이는 소리가 들리게 되기가 무섭게, 어떤 빠른 손이 현관문에 빗장 열쇠를 돌려 문을 열었다가 닫았다. 곧이어 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자 하나가 어찌나 기세 좋게 들이닥쳤던지, 멈추기도 전에 클레넘에게서 한 발짝 거리까지 다가와 있었다.\n\n‘이런!’ 그가 말했다.\n\n클레넘은 자기도 ‘이런!’ 하고 말하지 못할 이유가 없다고 생각했다.\n\n‘무슨 일이오?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 말했다.\n\n‘무슨 일이 있다는 얘기는 못 들었는데요.’ 클레넘이 답했다.\n\n‘캐스비 씨는 어디 계시오?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 두리번거리며 물었다.\n\n‘찾으신다면, 곧 오실 겁니다.’\n\n‘내가 그분을 찾는다고?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 말했다. ‘당신은 안 찾소?’\n\n이 말에 클레넘은 한두 마디 해명을 했고, 그것을 늘어놓는 동안 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자는 숨을 죽이고 그를 바라보았다. 그는 검은색과 녹슨 쇳빛 잿빛 옷을 입고 있었다. 새까만 구슬 같은 눈에, 까칠한 작은 검은 턱, 머리에서 갈래갈래 솟아나온 철사 같은 검은 머리카락은 꼭 쇠스랑이나 머리핀 같았고, 안색은 본바탕이 무척 거무튀튀하거나, 인위적으로 무척 더러워졌거나, 아니면 자연과 인공이 뒤섞인 것이었다. 그는 손이 더러웠고 부서진 손톱도 더러웠으며, 마치 석탄 속에 들어갔다 나온 것 같았다. 그는 땀을 흘리고 있었고, 작은 노동용 증기 기관처럼 콧방귀를 뀌고 코를 훌쩍이고 푹푹대며 숨을 내뿜었다.\n\n‘오!’ 아서가 어떻게 자기가 여기 있게 되었는지 말해 주자 그가 말했다. ‘아주 좋소. 됐습니다. 혹 그분이 팽크스를 찾으시거든, 팽크스가 들어왔다고 좀 전해 주시겠소?’ 그러고는 콧방귀를 뀌고 한 번 푹 숨을 내뿜으며 다른 문으로 빠져나갔다.\n\n그런데 고향에서 지내던 옛날에, 마지막 가부장에 관한 어떤 대담한 의혹들이 공중에 떠돌고 있다가, 이제는 잊힌 어떤 경로로 아서의 감각에 와 닿은 적이 있었다. 그는 그 시절의 분위기에 깃든 의심의 티끌과 부스러기들을 알고 있었다. 그 매개를 통해 보면 크리스토퍼 캐스비는 여관 없는 여관 간판에 지나지 않았다 — 묵을 데도 없고 감사할 거리라고는 전혀 없는데도 쉬어 가며 감사하라고 권하는 간판 말이다. 그는 이 부스러기들 가운데 더러는 크리스토퍼가 ‘저 머리’ 속에 꿍꿍이를 품을 수 있는 자, 교활한 사기꾼이라고까지 여긴다는 것을 알았다. 또 다른 티끌들은 그를 둔하고, 이기적이고, 떠밀려 다니는 멍청이로 보여 주었다. 다른 사람들과 굼뜨게 부대끼며 떠밀려 다니다가, 인생을 편하고 명예롭게 헤쳐 나가려면 그저 입을 다물고, 머리의 벗어진 부분을 잘 윤이 나게 닦아 두고, 나머지 머리카락은 손대지 않고 내버려 두기만 하면 된다는 사실을 어쩌다 발견하고는, 그 생각을 붙잡아 거기 매달릴 만큼의 약은 꾀는 있었던 자라고 말이다. 그가 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경의 도시 대리인이 된 것은, 그에게 사업 능력이 손톱만큼이라도 있어서가 아니라, 그토록 더없이 자애로워 보여서 그런 사람 밑에서 재산이 쥐어짜이거나 농간당하리라고 아무도 생각할 수 없었기 때문이라는 말이 있었다. 또 같은 이유로, 지금 그가 자기의 그 비참한 셋방에서 아무런 의심도 받지 않고 짜내는 돈이, 머리가 그만큼 불룩하지도 않고 그만큼 번쩍이지도 않는 사람이라면 도저히 짜낼 수 없을 만큼 많다는 것이었다. 한마디로, (똑딱이는 응접실에 홀로 있던 클레넘이 떠올려 보건대) 많은 사람들이, 방금 말한 화가들이 모델을 고르는 것과 똑같은 식으로 본보기를 고른다는 것이었다. 왕립 미술원에서는 어떤 사악한 늙은 개도둑 악당이, 속눈썹이나 턱이나 다리 덕분에 해마다 온갖 중요 덕목을 구현한 인물로 그려지곤 하는데(그리하여 자연을 더 면밀히 관찰하는 학생들의 가슴에 혼란의 가시를 심어 놓는데), 그처럼 거대한 사회의 전시회에서도 종종 내면의 인격 대신 부수적인 겉모습이 받아들여진다는 것이었다.\n\n이런 것들을 떠올리며 팽크스 씨를 그것들과 한 줄에 나란히 세워 보고서, 아서 클레넘은 이날, 단정까지는 짓지 못한 채로, 마지막 가부장이란 앞서 말한 그 떠밀려 다니는 멍청이, 머리의 벗어진 부분을 잘 윤내 두겠다는 단 하나의 생각만 가진 자라는 견해 쪽으로 기울었다. 그리고 템스강에서 다루기 힘든 큰 배 한 척이 이따금, 옆구리를 들이밀고, 고물부터, 제멋대로, 또 모든 다른 것들의 앞길을 가로막으며, 항해하는 양 거창하게 굴면서 조류에 묵직하게 떠밀려 가는 모습이 보이다가, 별안간 석탄을 때는 작은 증기 예인선이 그쪽으로 다가와 그것을 끌고 부산하게 떠나 버리듯이, 그 거추장스러운 가부장도 콧김을 내뿜는 팽크스에게 끌려, 이제 그 거무튀튀한 작은 배의 항적을 따라가고 있는 것이라고 말이다.\n\n캐스비 씨가 딸 플로라와 함께 돌아오면서 이 명상은 끝이 났다. 클레넘의 눈이 옛 연정의 대상에게 닿자마자, 그 연정은 부르르 떨며 산산조각이 났다.\n\n대부분의 사람은 옛 관념에 충실할 만큼 제 자신에게는 충분히 진실한 법이다. 그 관념이 현실과의 가까운 비교를 견디지 못하고, 그 대비가 관념에 치명적인 충격이 되는 경우, 그것은 변덕스러운 마음의 증거가 아니라 정확히 그 반대의 증거다. 클레넘의 경우가 그러했다. 젊은 시절 그는 이 여인을 열렬히 사랑했고, 그녀에게 자기 애정과 상상력의 갇혀 있던 보물을 모조리 쏟아부었다. 그 보물은, 그의 황량한 가정에서, 로빈슨 크루소의 돈과도 같았다. 누구와도 바꿀 수 없어, 그가 그녀를 위해 쏟아 부을 때까지, 녹슬도록 어둠 속에 부질없이 놓여 있는 돈 말이다. 그 잊지 못할 시절 이래로, 비록 그는 도착하던 날 밤까지 그녀를 죽은 사람처럼 — 그가 아는 한 정말 죽었을 수도 있었지만 — 자신의 현재나 미래와의 어떤 연관에서도 완전히 떨쳐 냈으면서도, 과거의 그 옛 환상만큼은 그 옛 신성한 자리에 고스란히 간직해 두었다. 그런데 이제, 결국, 마지막 가부장이 태연히 응접실로 걸어 들어오며 사실상 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. ‘부디 그것을 내던지고 그 위에서 춤이라도 추시지요. 이쪽이 플로라요.’\n\n플로라는, 늘 키가 컸는데, 이제 몸피도 무척 넓어지고 숨이 가빠졌다. 하지만 그건 대수롭지 않았다. 플로라는, 그가 백합으로 두고 떠났는데, 작약이 되어 있었다. 하지만 그것도 대수롭지 않았다. 플로라는, 말하고 생각하는 모든 것이 매혹적으로 보이던 여인이었는데, 이제 산만하고 어리석었다. 그것은 대수로웠다. 플로라는, 오래전엔 응석받이에 천진했는데, 이제 응석받이에 천진하기로 작정하고 있었다. 그것이 치명적인 일격이었다.\n\n이쪽이 플로라요!\n\n‘정말이지,’ 플로라가 소녀 시절의 태도를 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 머리를 까딱하고는 킥킥 웃었는데, 그것은 만약 고전 고대에 살다 죽은 어떤 배우라면 제 장례식에서 선보였을 법한 몸짓이었다, ‘클레넘 씨를 뵙기가 부끄러워요, 저는 꼴이 말이 아닌 데다, 그이가 절 보면 끔찍하게 변했다고 할 게 뻔하고, 정말 저는 이제 늙은 할멈이고, 그게 들통나다니 충격이고, 정말로 충격이에요!’\n\n그는 그녀가 자신이 예상한 그대로이며, 자기에게도 세월이 멈춰 있지는 않았노라고 분명히 말했다.\n\n‘오! 하지만 신사한테는 그게 아주 다른 거고, 정말이지 당신은 놀랍도록 좋아 보여서 그런 말씀 하실 자격이 없는데, 저로 말하자면, 아시잖아요 — 오!’ 플로라가 작게 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, ‘저는 형편없어요!’\n\n가부장은, 상연 중인 이 연극에서 자기 배역을 아직 모르는 듯, 텅 빈 평온함으로 환히 빛나고 있었다.\n\n‘하지만 변하지 않았단 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데,’ 무슨 말을 하든 한 번도 마침표에 이르는 법이 없는 플로라가 말했다, ‘아빠를 좀 보세요, 아빠는 당신이 떠나실 때 그 모습 그대로 아니에요, 아빠가 제 친자식한테 저런 면박이 되시다니 잔인하고 부자연스럽지 않아요, 우리가 이런 식으로 좀 더 오래 지내다간 우리를 모르는 사람들은 제가 아빠의 엄마라고 짐작하기 시작할 거예요!’\n\n그건 한참 뒤의 일일 텐데, 하고 아서는 생각했다.\n\n‘오 클레넘 씨 당신은 세상에서 가장 진실하지 못한 분이에요,’ 플로라가 말했다, ‘벌써 알아챘는데 당신은 옛날에 칭찬하던 그 버릇을 안 버리셨군요, 그렇게 감상에 사무친 척하시던 옛날 버릇 말이에요 아시잖아요 — 적어도 그런 뜻은 아니고, 저는 — 오 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요!’ 여기서 플로라는 어쩔 줄 몰라 킥킥 웃으며, 그에게 옛날의 그 눈길 하나를 던졌다.\n\n가부장은, 이제야 이 극에서 자기 배역이 되도록 빨리 무대에서 퇴장하는 것임을 깨닫기 시작한 듯, 일어나서, 팽크스가 빠져나간 그 문으로 가서, 그 예인선을 이름으로 불렀다. 저 너머 어느 작은 선창에서 대답이 들려왔고, 그는 곧장 끌려 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n‘아직 가실 생각은 마세요.’ 플로라가 말했다 — 아서가 우스꽝스러운 당혹감에 빠져, 어쩔 줄 모르고 제 모자를 바라보고 있었던 것이다. ‘떠날 생각을 하실 만큼 매정하실 리 없잖아요, 아서 — 그러니까 아서 씨 — 아니 클레넘 씨라고 하는 게 훨씬 적절하겠지만 — 정말 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요 — 영영 지나가 버린 그리운 옛날에 관해 한마디도 없이는요, 생각해 보니 차라리 그 얘긴 안 하는 게 훨씬 나을 것 같고 당신은 분명 훨씬 더 즐거운 약속이 있으실 테니 부디 제가 그걸 방해하는 세상 마지막 사람이 되게 해 주세요 그래도 한때는, 아 또 헛소리로 빠지네요.’\n\n플로라가 그녀가 말하는 그 시절에도 이렇게 수다스러웠을 리가 있을까? 그를 사로잡았던 그 매력 속에 지금의 이 두서없는 수다 같은 것이 조금이라도 있었을 리가 있을까?\n\n‘정말 의심할 여지가 거의 없는데,’ 플로라가 놀라운 속도로 내달리며, 대화에 쉼표 말고는 아무 구두점도 찍지 않고, 그나마도 아주 드물게 찍으면서 말했다, ‘당신은 어느 중국 여인과 결혼하셨겠죠, 그렇게 오래 중국에 계셨고 사업을 하셨고 당연히 정착해서 인맥을 넓히고 싶으셨을 테니 당신이 중국 여인에게 청혼하셨을 가능성이 가장 높고 그 중국 여인이 당신을 받아들이고 자기도 형편이 아주 좋아졌다고 여겼을 게 무엇보다 자연스럽지요, 다만 그 여인이 탑교 비국교도가 아니길 바랄 뿐이에요.’\n\n‘저는,’ 아서가 자기도 모르게 미소 지으며 답했다, ‘어떤 여인과도 결혼하지 않았습니다, 플로라.’\n\n‘오 세상에 저 때문에 그렇게 오래 독신으로 지내신 건 아니길 바라요!’ 플로라가 킥킥거렸다. ‘하지만 물론 그러셨을 리 없죠 왜 그러셨겠어요, 부디 대답은 마세요, 제가 어디로 내달리는지 모르겠네요, 오 중국 여인들에 관해 좀 말씀해 주세요 그들의 눈이 정말 그렇게 길고 가늘어서 늘 카드놀이의 자개로 만든 물고기 모양을 떠올리게 하는지 그리고 정말 등 뒤로 변발을 늘어뜨리고 그것도 땋아서 그러는지 아니면 남자들만 그러는지, 그리고 머리카락을 이마에서 그렇게 바짝 당겨 묶을 때 아프지 않은지, 그리고 왜 자기네 다리며 사원이며 모자며 온갖 것에 작은 종을 잔뜩 매다는지 아니면 실은 안 그러는지요?’ 플로라가 그에게 또 옛날의 그 눈길을 던졌다. 그러고는 곧장 다시 말을 이어 나갔는데, 마치 그가 한동안 대답이라도 한 것처럼.\n\n‘그럼 다 사실이고 정말로 그러는군요! 세상에 아서! — 부디 용서하세요 — 오랜 버릇이라 — 클레넘 씨가 훨씬 적절하죠 — 그렇게 오랜 세월 살기엔 어떤 나라인지, 그리고 등불도 그렇게 많고 우산도 그렇게 많으니 기후가 얼마나 어둡고 축축할 게 틀림없고 정말로 그렇겠지요, 그리고 누구나 그것들을 들고 다니며 사방에 매달아 두는 그 두 가지 장사로 벌어들이는 돈이 얼마나 클지, 그 작은 신발들도 그렇고 갓난아기 때부터 발을 옥죄어 도로 오므려 놓는다니 정말 놀랍네요, 당신은 어쩜 그런 여행가세요!’\n\n우스꽝스러운 당혹감 속에서, 클레넘은 옛날의 그 눈길을 또 한 번 받았으나, 그것을 어찌해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다.\n\n‘이런 저런,’ 플로라가 말했다, ‘고향의 그 변화들을 생각하면 아서 — 도무지 안 고쳐지네요, 너무 자연스럽게 나와서, 클레넘 씨가 훨씬 적절한데 — 당신이 중국의 풍습과 언어에 익숙해지신 뒤로 말이에요 분명 당신은 그 말을 원주민처럼, 아니 어쩌면 더 잘하시겠죠 당신은 늘 머리가 빠르고 영리하셨으니까요 물론 어마어마하게 어려울 테지만, 정말이지 차 상자만 해도 제가 해 보려 들면 절 죽이고 말 거예요, 그런 변화들이라니 아서 — 또 그러네요, 너무 자연스러워서, 더없이 부적절한데 — 아무도 믿지 못했을 변화들이라니, 핀칭 부인이라니 누가 상상이나 했겠어요 저 자신도 상상이 안 되는걸요!’\n\n‘그게 결혼하신 뒤의 성함입니까?’ 아서가 물었는데, 이 모든 와중에, 비록 묘한 방식으로나마 그녀가 두 사람이 한때 맺고 있던 젊은 날의 관계를 언급할 때 그 어조에 어린 어떤 마음의 따스함에 마음이 움직였던 것이다. ‘핀칭이라고요?’\n\n‘핀칭 오 그래요 끔찍한 이름 아니에요, 하지만 핀칭 씨가 제게 청혼할 때 — 그이는 일곱 번이나 청혼했고 그러고는, 그이 표현대로 일 년간 마음에 드는지 두고 보자며 점잖게 응낙해 주셨는데 — 어쨌거나 그게 그이 잘못은 아니고 어쩔 수 없는 일이었잖아요 안 그래요, 훌륭한 분이었어요, 당신과는 전혀 안 닮았지만 훌륭한 분이었죠!’\n\n플로라는 마침내 한순간 숨이 차 말을 멈췄다. 한순간이었다. 떠나간 핀칭 씨의 망령에 바치는 헌사로, 손수건 한 귀퉁이를 눈가에 들어 올리는 동안 숨을 돌렸으니, 그러고는 다시 시작했던 것이다.\n\n‘아무도 부정 못 할 거예요, 아서 — 클레넘 씨 — 사정이 달라진 마당에 당신이 저한테 격식 차려 다정하게 대하시는 게 옳고 사실 달리 어쩌실 수도 없고, 적어도 제 짐작엔 그렇고 당신이 더 잘 아시겠지만, 그래도 정말 사정이 무척 달랐던 한때가 있었다는 걸 떠올리지 않을 수가 없네요.’\n\n‘친애하는 핀칭 부인,’ 아서가, 그 좋은 어조에 다시 마음이 움직여 입을 열었다.\n\n‘오 그 추하고 흉한 이름 말고, 플로라라고 불러 주세요!’\n\n‘플로라. 분명히 말씀드리는데, 플로라, 저는 당신을 다시 뵙게 되어, 그리고 당신도 저처럼 그 옛날의 어리석은 꿈들을, 우리가 젊음과 희망의 빛 속에서 눈앞의 모든 것을 바라보던 그 시절을 잊지 않으셨음을 알게 되어 기쁩니다.’\n\n‘그래 보이지 않는걸요,’ 플로라가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다, ‘당신은 아주 시큰둥하게 받아들이시네요, 하지만 어쨌든 당신이 저한테 실망하셨다는 건 알아요, 아마 그 중국 여인들이 — 만다린 부인들이라 부른다면 — 그 원인이거나 어쩌면 저 자신이 원인이거나겠죠, 그쪽도 똑같이 그럴듯하니까요.’\n\n‘아뇨, 아뇨,’ 클레넘이 간청했다, ‘그런 말씀 마세요.’\n\n‘오 해야죠 아시잖아요,’ 플로라가 단호한 어조로 말했다, ‘안 한다는 게 무슨 헛소리예요, 제가 당신 기대와 다른 거 저도 알아요, 아주 잘 알아요.’\n\n그 빠른 말의 와중에도, 그녀는 더 영리한 여인다운 날렵한 통찰로 그것을 알아챘던 것이다. 그런데도, 이내 두 사람이 오래전에 버린 소년 소녀 시절의 관계를 지금의 만남과 뒤섞어 짜 나가는 그 모순되고 지극히 터무니없는 방식에, 클레넘은 머리가 어질어질해지는 듯했다.\n\n‘한마디,’ 플로라가, 그들의 대화에 아무 예고도 없이 — 클레넘이 몹시 겁에 질리게도 — 사랑싸움의 어조를 입히며 말했다, ‘드리고 싶은 게 있고, 한 가지 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 당신 어머님이 오셔서 우리 아빠를 상대로 한바탕 소동을 벌이시고 제가 아래층 작은 아침 식사실로 불려 내려가 보니 두 분이 의자 두 개에 마치 미친 황소처럼 앉아 당신 어머님의 양산을 사이에 두고 서로 노려보고 계셨을 때 제가 어쩔 수 있었겠어요?’\n\n‘친애하는 핀칭 부인,’ 클레넘이 채근했다 — ‘그게 다 그렇게 오래전 일이고 그렇게 오래전에 끝난 일인데, 진지하게 그걸 — 그럴 만한 가치가 있을까요 — ’\n\n‘저는 못 해요 아서,’ 플로라가 답했다, ‘바로잡을 기회가 있는데도 중국의 사회 전체로부터 매정한 여자라고 손가락질당하는 걸 가만둘 순 없어요, 그리고 당신도 잘 아시겠지만 돌려줘야 했던 그 『폴과 비르지니』가 있었고 그건 아무 쪽지도 한마디도 없이 돌려보내졌잖아요, 그렇게 감시당하는 처지에 당신이 제게 편지를 쓸 수 있었으리란 얘긴 아니지만, 그래도 표지에 빨간 봉인 하나만 붙어서 돌아왔어도 저는 그게 베이징 난징과 — 세 번째 데가 어디더라, 맨발로 — 그리로 오라는 뜻인 줄 알았을 거예요.’\n\n‘친애하는 핀칭 부인, 당신 잘못이 아니었고, 저는 한 번도 당신을 탓하지 않았습니다. 우리는 둘 다 너무 어렸고, 너무 의지가지없고 무력해서, 우리의 이별을 받아들이는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었지요. — 부디 얼마나 오래전 일인지 생각해 보세요.’ 아서가 부드럽게 타일렀다.\n\n‘한마디 더,’ 플로라가 줄지 않는 수다로 말을 이었다, ‘드리고 싶고, 한 가지 더 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 닷새 동안 저는 울다가 머리에 감기가 들어 그 시간을 몽땅 뒤쪽 응접실에서 보냈는데 — 그 뒤쪽 응접실은 지금도 이 층에 있고 지금도 집 뒤편에 있으니 제 말이 사실이라는 걸 증명해 줄 거예요 — 그 음울한 시기가 지나가자 잠잠한 시기가 뒤따랐고 세월이 흘러 핀칭 씨가 어느 공동의 친구 집에서 우리와 알게 되었는데 그이는 온통 정성이었고 이튿날 찾아왔고 곧 일주일에 세 번 저녁마다 찾아오기 시작했고 야식거리로 자잘한 것들을 보내오기 시작했어요 핀칭 씨로서는 그게 사랑이 아니라 숭배였죠, 핀칭 씨가 아빠의 전적인 동의를 얻어 청혼했는데 제가 어쩔 수 있었겠어요?’\n\n‘아무것도 못 하셨겠죠,’ 아서가 더없이 쾌활한 선뜻함으로 말했다, ‘당신이 하신 그것 말고는요. 옛 친구로서 단언하건대, 당신이 아주 옳게 하셨다고 저는 확신합니다.’\n\n‘마지막으로 한마디,’ 플로라가 평범한 일상사를 손짓 한 번으로 물리치며 말을 이었다, ‘드리고 싶고, 마지막으로 한 가지 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 핀칭 씨가 오해의 여지 없는 관심을 처음 보이기 전 한때가 정말 있었지만, 그건 다 지난 일이고 이루어질 수 없는 일이었고, 친애하는 클레넘 씨 당신은 이제 황금 사슬을 차고 있지 않으니 자유로운 몸이고 부디 행복하시길 바라요, 여기 아빠가 오시는데 늘 성가시게 굴며 불러 주지도 않은 데마다 코를 들이미시지요.’\n\n이 말과 함께, 그리고 소심한 조심성이 가득 밴 황급한 몸짓 — 클레넘의 눈에 옛 시절 익숙했던 그런 몸짓 — 과 함께, 가엾은 플로라는 다시금 열여덟 살의 자신을 까마득히 저 뒤에 남겨 두고, 마침내 완전한 마침표에 다다랐다.\n\n아니, 차라리, 그녀는 열여덟 살의 자신을 절반쯤만 뒤에 남겨 두고, 나머지 절반을 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인에 접붙여, 그리하여 스스로를 도덕적인 인어로 만들어 놓았으니, 한때 그녀의 소년 연인이었던 이는 그 모습을, 슬픔의 감정과 우스움의 감정이 묘하게 뒤섞인 심정으로 바라보았다.\n\n이를테면 이런 식이었다. 마치 그녀와 클레넘 사이에 더없이 짜릿한 종류의 은밀한 양해가 있는 것처럼, 스코틀랜드까지 죽 늘어선 사두마차 행렬의 맨 앞 마차가 바로 그 순간 모퉁이를 돌아와 있는 것처럼, 그리고 마치 그녀가 가문의 우산 그늘 아래, 머리에 가부장의 축복을 받고, 온 인류의 완벽한 동의를 얻어 그와 함께 교구 교회로 걸어 들어갈 수 (또 그러려 하지) 않는 것처럼, 플로라는 들킬까 봐 두려워하는 시늉으로 신비로운 신호를 보내는 고뇌 속에서 제 영혼을 달랬다. 점점 더 머리가 어질어질해지는 느낌 속에서, 클레넘은 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인이, 무대가 먼지투성이가 되고, 배경이 빛바래고, 젊은 배우들이 죽고, 관현악단이 텅 비고, 조명이 꺼진 지금에 와서, 자기와 자신을 옛 자리에 다시 세우고 그 옛 연기를 죄다 되풀이하며 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 즐기는 모습을 보았다. 그러면서도, 한때 그녀에게 어여삐 자연스럽던 것으로 그가 기억하는 그 모든 것을 이렇게 기괴하게 되살려 내는 와중에도, 그는 그것이 자기를 본 탓에 되살아난 것이며, 그 속에 다정한 추억이 깃들어 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n가부장은 그에게 저녁 식사를 하고 가라고 우겼고, 플로라는 ‘좋아요!’라고 신호했다. 클레넘은 차라리 저녁 식사를 하고 가는 것 이상의 무언가를 할 수 있었으면 — 예전의 그 플로라를, 아니 애초에 존재한 적도 없던 그 플로라를 찾아낼 수 있었으면 — 하고 어찌나 간절히 바랐던지, 자기가 거의 부끄럽게 느끼던 그 실망에 대해 할 수 있는 최소한의 속죄는, 이 집안의 바람에 자기를 내맡기는 것이라고 생각했다. 그래서 그는 저녁 식사를 하고 가기로 했다.\n\n팽크스가 그들과 함께 식사를 했다. 팽크스는 여섯 시 십오 분 전에 제 작은 선창에서 증기를 내뿜으며 나와, 곧장 가부장 쪽으로 돌진해 왔는데, 가부장은 마침 그때 블리딩 하트 야드에 관한 따분한 계산 이야기를 멍하니 몰아가고 있던 참이었다. 팽크스는 즉시 그에게 밧줄을 걸어 끌어냈다.\n\n‘블리딩 하트 야드 말입니까?’ 팽크스가 푹 숨을 내뿜고 콧방귀를 뀌며 말했다. ‘성가신 재산이지요. 벌이가 나쁘진 않지만, 거기선 집세를 받아내기가 여간 어려운 게 아닙니다. 사장님의 그 한 군데가, 사장님 소유의 다른 모든 곳을 합친 것보다 더 골치를 썩이지요.’\n\n끌려가는 큰 배가 대부분의 구경꾼들에게는 그 힘센 주체로 비치듯이, 가부장도 으레, 팽크스가 그를 위해 한 말이 무엇이든 자기가 직접 한 것처럼 보이곤 했다.\n\n‘그렇소?’ 클레넘이 답했는데, 윤이 나는 머리의 번뜩임 하나만으로도 그 인상이 어찌나 효과적으로 그에게 박혔던지, 그는 예인선이 아니라 큰 배를 향해 말을 건넸던 것이다. ‘그곳 사람들이 그렇게 가난합니까?’\n\n‘그건 모르는 일이지요,’ 팽크스가, 녹슨 쇳빛 잿빛 주머니에서 더러운 손 하나를 빼내 깨물 손톱이라도 있나 찾으며, 그 구슬 같은 눈을 자기 고용주에게 돌리면서 콧방귀를 뀌었다, ‘그들이 가난한지 아닌지 말입니다. 자기들은 가난하다고 하지만, 다들 그렇게 말하지요. 어떤 자가 부자라고 하면, 대개는 부자가 아니라고 보면 됩니다. 게다가, 설령 그들이 가난하다 한들, 사장님이 어쩔 수 있는 게 아니지요. 집세를 못 받으면 사장님 본인이 가난해질 테니까요.’\n\n‘옳은 말이군요.’ 아서가 말했다.\n\n‘런던의 모든 빈민을 위해 집을 열어 둘 순 없는 노릇이지요,’ 팽크스가 말을 이었다. ‘공짜로 재워 줄 순 없는 겁니다. 대문을 활짝 열고 거저 들어오게 둘 순 없어요. 정신이 박혀 있다면, 그러지 않으시겠지요.’\n\n캐스비 씨는 평온하고 자애로운 두루뭉술함으로 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n‘어떤 자가 일주일에 반 크라운에 방을 빌렸는데, 한 주가 돌아왔을 때 그 반 크라운이 없으면, 그자한테 이렇게 말하는 겁니다. 그럼 방은 왜 빌렸나? 한 가지가 없으면서 다른 한 가지는 왜 가졌나? 자네 돈을 어디다 갖다 쓴 건가? 무슨 속셈이야? 무슨 수작이냐고? 그런 자한텐 그렇게 말하는 겁니다. 그렇게 말하지 않으면 오히려 부끄러운 일이지요!’ 여기서 팽크스 씨는 코 언저리에서 세차게 숨을 내뿜는 노력으로, 그 소리 말고는 아무 결과도 없는 기이하고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 소리를 냈다.\n\n‘그런 재산을 이 동쪽과 북동쪽 일대에 어느 정도 가지고 계신 걸로 압니다만?’ 클레넘이, 둘 중 누구에게 말을 건네야 할지 망설이며 말했다.\n\n‘오, 꽤 됩니다.’ 팽크스가 말했다. ‘사장님은 동쪽이든 북동쪽이든 가리지 않으세요, 나침반의 어느 방향이든 상관없지요. 원하시는 건 좋은 투자와 빠른 회수니까요. 찾을 수 있는 데서 잡으시는 겁니다. 위치를 까다롭게 따지시지 않아요 — 절대 아니지요.’\n\n가부장의 천막 안에는 네 번째이자 가장 독특한 인물이 있었는데, 그 사람 역시 저녁 식사 전에 나타났다. 이는 놀라운 작은 노부인으로, 표정을 담기엔 너무 싸구려인 멍하니 노려보는 나무 인형 같은 얼굴에, 뻣뻣한 노란 가발이 머리 꼭대기에 비뚜름히 얹혀 있었는데, 마치 그 인형의 주인인 아이가 아무 데나 압정을 박아 넣어 겨우 고정해 놓은 것 같았다. 이 작은 노부인의 또 다른 두드러진 점은, 바로 그 아이가 숟가락 비슷한 어떤 뭉툭한 도구로 그녀의 얼굴 두세 군데를 망가뜨려 놓은 듯하다는 것이었다. 그녀의 얼굴은, 특히 코끝은, 그 도구의 우묵한 부분에 대체로 들어맞는 여러 개의 움푹 팬 자국이라는 현상을 보여 주고 있었다. 이 작은 노부인의 또 한 가지 두드러진 점은, 그녀에게 핀칭 씨의 고모라는 것 말고는 아무 이름이 없다는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 다음과 같은 정황에서 손님의 눈앞에 나타났다. 첫 번째 요리가 식탁에 놓일 때, 플로라가 어쩌면 클레넘 씨가 핀칭 씨가 자기에게 유산을 남겼다는 얘기를 못 들으셨을지 모르겠다고 말했다. 클레넘은 답으로, 핀칭 씨가 그토록 흠모하던 아내에게 자신의 세속 재산 전부는 아닐지라도 그 대부분을 물려주었기를 바란다는 뜻을 비쳤다. 플로라는, 오 그래요, 그런 뜻이 아니라며, 핀칭 씨가 훌륭한 유언장을 남기긴 했지만, 자기에게 별도의 유산으로 그분의 고모를 남겼다고 말했다. 그러고는 방을 나가 그 유산을 데려왔고, 돌아와서는 다소 의기양양하게 ‘핀칭 씨의 고모’를 선보였다.\n\n낯선 사람이 핀칭 씨의 고모에게서 알아챌 수 있는 주된 특징은, 극단적인 엄격함과 음울한 과묵함이었다. 이따금 깊고 경고하는 듯한 목소리로 한마디씩 던지는 성향이 그 과묵함을 깨뜨리곤 했는데, 그 말은 누가 한 어떤 말로도 전혀 불려 나올 까닭이 없는 데다 어떤 관념의 연상으로도 추적할 수 없어서, 듣는 이의 정신을 어리둥절하게 하고 겁에 질리게 했다. 핀칭 씨의 고모는 어쩌면 그녀 나름의 어떤 체계에 따라 이런 소견들을 던졌는지도 모르고, 그것이 기발하거나 심지어 정교했는지도 모른다. 다만 그것을 풀 열쇠가 없었다.\n\n말끔하게 차려지고 잘 조리된 저녁 식사는(가부장의 집안에 관한 모든 것이 조용한 소화를 도왔으니), 수프 약간과 가자미 튀김, 새우 소스를 담은 버터 그릇, 그리고 감자 한 접시로 시작되었다. 대화는 여전히 집세 수령에 관한 것이었다. 핀칭 씨의 고모는, 십 분 동안 일행을 악의에 찬 눈초리로 쏘아본 끝에, 다음과 같은 무시무시한 소견을 내놓았다.\n\n‘우리가 헨리에 살 때, 반스네 거위 수컷을 땜장이들이 훔쳐 갔어.’\n\n팽크스 씨는 용감하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. ‘옳은 말씀입니다, 부인.’ 그러나 이 알 수 없는 전언이 클레넘에게 미친 효과는 그를 그야말로 겁에 질리게 하는 것이었다. 게다가 또 한 가지 정황이 이 노부인에게 각별한 공포를 둘러씌웠다. 그녀는 늘 노려보고 있으면서도, 어느 누구를 본다는 것을 결코 인정하지 않았다. 예의 바르고 자상한 낯선 손님이, 말하자면 감자 문제에 관해 그녀의 의향을 물어보고 싶어 한다 치자. 그의 의사를 담은 동작은 그녀에게 가망 없이 헛수고가 되고 말 텐데, 그가 무엇을 어쩌겠는가? 어느 누구도 ‘핀칭 씨의 고모님, 제가 권해 드려도 될까요?’라고 말할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 클레넘이 그러했듯, 누구든 기가 죽고 좌절한 채 숟가락에서 물러나고 말았다.\n\n양고기와 스테이크, 그리고 사과 파이가 나왔다 — 거위 수컷과는 아무리 멀게라도 연관될 것이 없는 음식들이었다 — 그리고 저녁 식사는, 실로 그러했듯, 마법이 풀린 연회처럼 흘러갔다. 한때 클레넘은 그 식탁에 앉아 플로라 말고는 아무것에도 마음을 두지 않은 적이 있었다. 이제 그가 플로라에게 두는 주된 관심이라고는, 자기 뜻과는 달리, 그녀가 흑맥주를 무척 좋아하고, 감상에 셰리주를 잔뜩 곁들이며, 그녀가 좀 너무 자랐다면 그것은 든든한 근거 위에서라는 사실을 관찰하는 것이었다. 마지막 가부장은 늘 대단한 대식가였는데, 마치 다른 누군가를 먹여 주는 착한 영혼 같은 자애로움으로 엄청난 양의 단단한 음식을 처리해 나갔다. 늘 바쁜 팽크스 씨는, 옆에 둔 작고 더러운 수첩을(어쩌면 디저트 삼아 찾아내려는 체납자들의 명단이 적혀 있을지도 모를 그 수첩을) 이따금 들춰 보면서, 마치 석탄을 싣듯이 음식을 먹어 치웠다. 상당한 소음과 함께, 상당히 흘리고 떨어뜨리면서, 그리고 이따금, 마치 곧 증기를 내뿜고 떠날 채비가 된 양 푹 숨을 내뿜고 콧방귀를 뀌면서 말이다.\n\n식사 내내, 플로라는 먹고 마시는 현재의 식욕과 낭만적 사랑을 향한 과거의 식욕을 한데 어우러 놓는 통에, 클레넘은 제 접시에서 눈을 들기가 두려울 지경이었다. 그녀 쪽을 바라보면 영락없이 무언가 신비로운 의미나 경고의 눈길을 받았던 까닭인데, 마치 두 사람이 어떤 음모에 가담하기라도 한 듯했다. 핀칭 씨의 고모는, 식탁보가 걷히고 술병들이 나타날 때까지, 더없이 신랄한 표정으로 말없이 그를 노려보며 앉아 있다가, 그때 또 하나의 소견을 시작했다 — 누구에게 물어보는 법도 없이, 괘종시계처럼 대화에 끼어든 것이다.\n\n플로라가 막 이렇게 말한 참이었다. ‘클레넘 씨, 핀칭 씨의 고모님께 포트와인 한 잔 따라 주시겠어요?’\n\n‘런던교 근처의 그 기념탑은,’ 그 부인이 즉시 선언했다, ‘런던 대화재 뒤에 세워진 거야. 그리고 런던 대화재는 네 삼촌 조지의 작업장이 불타 버린 그 화재가 아니야.’\n\n팽크스 씨는 아까의 그 용기로 말했다. ‘그렇습니까, 부인? 옳은 말씀입니다!’ 그러나 가상의 반박이나 다른 부당한 대우에 격분한 듯, 핀칭 씨의 고모는 침묵에 빠져드는 대신 다음과 같은 추가 선언을 내놓았다.\n\n‘나는 바보가 싫어!’\n\n이 소견은, 그 자체로는 거의 솔로몬의 잠언에 가까운 것이었으나, 그녀가 그것을 손님의 머리를 향해 똑바로 겨눈 탓에, 더없이 모욕적이고 인신공격적인 성격을 띠게 되었으니, 핀칭 씨의 고모를 방에서 데리고 나가야 할 형편이 되었다. 이는 플로라가 조용히 해냈다. 핀칭 씨의 고모는 아무 저항도 하지 않았으나, 나가는 길에 누그러지지 않는 적의로 물었다. ‘그럼 저자는 여기 뭐 하러 온 거야?’\n\n플로라가 돌아와서는, 자기 유산이 영리한 노부인이긴 한데 가끔 좀 별나서 ‘사람을 싫어하기도’ 한다고 설명했다 — 플로라는 오히려 그런 별난 점들을 자랑스러워하는 듯했다. 이 일에서 플로라의 선량함이 빛났으므로, 이제 그 노부인의 존재가 주던 공포에서 벗어난 클레넘으로서는, 그 선량함을 끌어냈다 하여 노부인을 나무랄 마음이 없었다. 그래서 그들은 평온하게 와인을 한두 잔 마셨다. 이윽고 그는 팽크스가 곧 출항할 것이고 가부장이 잠들 것임을 내다보고서, 어머니를 방문해야 할 사정이 있다는 핑계를 대며, 팽크스 씨에게 어느 방향으로 가느냐고 물었다.\n\n‘시내 쪽입니다, 손님.’ 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n‘같이 걸을까요?’ 아서가 말했다.\n\n‘얼마든지요.’ 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n그러는 동안 플로라는 그의 귀에 대고 빠른 토막말로 속삭이고 있었다. 한때가 있었다고, 그래도 과거는 입을 떡 벌린 심연이라고, 그리고 황금 사슬이 이제 더는 그를 옭아매지 않는다고, 그리고 자기는 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 추억을 존중한다고, 그리고 내일 한 시 반에 자기가 집에 있을 거라고, 그리고 운명의 명령은 거두어들일 수 없는 것이라고, 그리고 그가 오후 정확히 네 시에 그레이즈 인 가든의 북서쪽을 걷는다는 것만큼 있을 법하지 않은 일도 없다고 자기는 여긴다고. 헤어질 때 그는 솔직한 마음으로 지금의 플로라에게 — 사라진 플로라도, 그 인어도 아닌 플로라에게 — 손을 내밀려 했으나, 플로라는 그러려 하지 않았고, 그럴 수 없었으며, 자신과 그를 지나간 그 배역에서 떼어 낼 능력이 도무지 없었다. 그는 더없이 비참한 심정으로 그 집을 떠났다. 그리고 어느 때보다도 머리가 어질어질했으니, 만약 끌려가는 행운을 누리지 못했더라면 처음 십오 분쯤은 어디로든 정처 없이 떠밀려 갔을지도 모른다.\n\n서늘한 공기 속에서, 플로라가 없는 곳에서 정신을 차리기 시작하자, 그는 팽크스가 전속력으로, 그나마 찾아낼 수 있는 그 얼마 안 되는 손톱의 목초를 뜯어 먹으며, 이따금 콧방귀를 뀌고 있는 것을 발견했다. 이것들은, 한 손을 주머니에 찌르고 우글쭈글한 모자를 앞뒤로 뒤집어쓴 모습과 더불어, 그가 생각에 잠겼을 때의 분명한 상태였다.\n\n‘쌀쌀한 밤이군요!’ 아서가 말했다.\n\n‘예, 꽤 쌀쌀하네요.’ 팽크스가 동의했다. ‘이방인이시라 저보다 기후를 더 느끼시겠지요. 사실 저야 그걸 느낄 새도 없습니다만.’\n\n‘그렇게 바쁘게 사십니까?’\n\n‘예, 늘 찾아내야 할 누군가가 있거나, 챙겨야 할 무언가가 있지요. 그래도 저는 일이 좋습니다,’ 팽크스가 걸음을 조금 더 빨리하며 말했다. ‘사람이 뭐 하러 생겨났겠습니까?’\n\n‘다른 건 없습니까?’ 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n팽크스가 되물었다. ‘다른 게 뭐가 있습니까?’ 그 말은, 클레넘의 삶을 짓눌러 온 무게를 가장 작은 부피로 압축해 담고 있었다. 그는 아무 대답도 하지 못했다.\n\n‘그게 제가 우리 주간 임차인들한테 묻는 말입니다,’ 팽크스가 말했다. ‘그들 중 더러는 제게 울상을 지으며 말하지요. 보시다시피 가난하지만, 나리, 우린 깨어 있는 매 순간 갈고, 죽도록 일하고, 뼈 빠지게 노동한다고요. 그럼 제가 그들한테 말합니다. 그럼 그것 말고 뭐 하러 생겨났나? 그러면 그들 입이 딱 막히지요. 한마디도 대꾸를 못 합니다. 그것 말고 뭐 하러 생겨났나? 그 말이 못을 박는 겁니다.’\n\n‘아 저런, 저런, 저런!’ 클레넘이 한숨지었다.\n\n‘여기 저를 보십시오,’ 팽크스가 주간 임차인을 상대로 하던 그 논리를 이어 갔다. ‘제가 뭐 하러 생겨났다고 생각하시겠습니까? 아무것도 없지요. 아침 일찍 침대에서 절 흔들어 깨워, 일을 시키고, 끼니 욱여넣을 시간은 마음껏 짧게 주고, 계속 부려 먹는 겁니다. 절 계속 부려 먹으면, 저도 사장님을 계속 부려 먹고, 사장님은 또 다른 누군가를 계속 부려 먹지요. 자, 그게 바로 상업 국가에서 인간이 마땅히 해야 할 모든 의무라는 겁니다.’\n\n말없이 조금 더 걸은 뒤에, 클레넘이 말했다. ‘당신은 무언가에 취미가 없으십니까, 팽크스 씨?’\n\n‘취미가 뭡니까?’ 팽크스가 무뚝뚝하게 되받았다.\n\n‘성향이라고 합시다.’\n\n‘저는 돈을 버는 데 성향이 있지요, 손님,’ 팽크스가 말했다, ‘방법을 알려 주신다면요.’ 그가 다시 그 소리를 내뿜었고, 그의 동행에게 처음으로 그것이 그가 웃는 방식이라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그는 모든 면에서 독특한 사람이었다. 어쩌면 그가 아주 진지한 것은 아니었을지도 모르나, 다만 마치 기계적인 회전으로 하듯 이런 원칙의 잿덩이들을 짧고 단단하고 빠르게 토해 내는 그 방식이, 농담과는 도무지 양립할 수 없어 보였다.\n\n‘책은 많이 안 읽으시겠지요, 짐작건대?’ 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n‘편지와 장부 말고는 아무것도 안 읽습니다. 근친 상속인에 관한 광고 말고는 아무것도 안 모으고요. 그게 취미라면, 저한테 그게 있는 거지요. 당신은 콘월의 클레넘 가문은 아니시죠, 클레넘 씨?’\n\n‘제가 들어 본 바로는 아닙니다.’\n\n‘아니란 거 압니다. 제가 당신 어머님께 여쭤봤거든요, 손님. 그분은 기회를 놓치기엔 너무 야무진 분이시지요.’\n\n‘제가 콘월의 클레넘 가문이었다고 칩시다.’\n\n‘그러면 당신께 이로운 무언가를 들으셨을 겁니다.’\n\n‘그래요! 한동안 제게 이로운 거라곤 들어 본 게 거의 없는데요.’\n\n‘콘월의 어느 재산 하나가 임자를 찾고 있는데, 청하기만 하면 그걸 가질 콘월의 클레넘이 하나도 없지요, 손님.’ 팽크스가 가슴팍 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼냈다가 도로 집어넣으며 말했다. ‘저는 여기서 갈라집니다. 그럼 안녕히 가십시오.’\n\n‘안녕히 가십시오!’ 클레넘이 말했다. 그러나 그 예인선은, 갑자기 짐을 던 채, 끌고 가던 무게에서 풀려나, 벌써 푹푹대며 저 멀리로 멀어져 가고 있었다.\n\n그들은 함께 스미스필드를 가로질러 왔고, 클레넘은 바비컨 모퉁이에 홀로 남겨졌다. 그는 그날 밤 어머니의 음울한 방에 모습을 드러낼 생각이 없었고, 광야에 있다 한들 이보다 더 침울하고 버림받은 기분이 들 수는 없었을 것이다. 그는 천천히 올더스게이트가를 내려가며, 빛과 활기를 누리려고 큰 길 가운데 하나로 들어설 작정으로 세인트폴 쪽으로 길을 더듬어 가고 있었는데, 그때 한 무리의 사람들이 같은 보도 위로 그를 향해 몰려왔고, 그는 그들이 지나가도록 어느 가게에 붙어 비켜섰다. 그들이 다가오자, 그는 그들이 사람들의 어깨에 떠메인 무언가를 둘러싸고 모여 있음을 알아보았다. 그는 곧 그것이 덧문이나 그 비슷한 것으로 급히 만든 들것임을, 그리고 그 위에 누워 있는 형체임을 알아보았으며, 무리 속의 토막난 대화와, 한 남자가 든 진흙투성이 보따리와, 또 다른 남자가 든 진흙투성이 모자가, 사고가 일어났음을 그에게 알려 주었다. 들것은 그를 여섯 걸음쯤 지나가기도 전에, 짐을 다시 손보려고 어느 가로등 아래 멈춰 섰고, 무리도 함께 멈춰 서는 바람에, 그는 그 행렬 한가운데에 서 있게 되었다.\n\n‘병원으로 가는 사고입니까?’ 그가 곁에 있던 한 노인에게 물었는데, 노인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말을 걸어 달라는 듯이 서 있었다.\n\n‘그렇소,’ 노인이 말했다, ‘저 우편마차들 때문이오. 저 우편마차들은 기소해서 벌금을 물려야 하오. 저 우편마차들은 래드 레인이며 우드 스트리트에서 시속 십이 마일이나 십사 마일로 쌩쌩 달려 나온다오, 저 우편마차들이 말이오. 저 우편마차들 때문에 사람이 더 자주 안 죽는 게 그저 신기할 따름이지.’\n\n‘이 사람은 죽지는 않았겠지요?’\n\n‘낸들 아오!’ 노인이 말했다, ‘죽지 않았다 해도 저 우편마차들에 그럴 뜻이 없어서 그런 건 아니오.’ 화자는 팔짱을 끼고서, 들어 줄 만한 구경꾼이라면 누구에게든 저 우편마차들에 대한 자기 비방을 편안히 늘어놓기 시작했고, 순전히 그 부상자에 대한 동정심에서 여러 목소리가 그를 거들었다. 한 목소리는 클레넘에게 ‘저 우편마차들은 공중의 골칫거리예요, 손님’이라 했고, 또 한 목소리는 ‘제가 어젯밤 저것들 중 하나가 한 아이한테서 반 인치도 안 되는 데서 멈추는 걸 봤다니까요’라 했으며, 또 한 목소리는 ‘제가 저것들 중 하나가 고양이 한 마리를 깔아뭉개는 걸 봤어요, 손님 — 그게 댁의 어머니였을 수도 있는 거라고요’라 했다. 그리고 모두가, 만약 그에게 무슨 공적인 영향력이라도 있다면 저 우편마차들에 맞서는 데 쓰는 것보다 더 나은 쓰임은 없으리라는 뜻을 은연중에 내비쳤다.\n\n‘아니, 토박이 영국인도 저 우편마차들로부터 제 목숨을 지키느라 평생 밤마다 곤욕을 치르는 판이오,’ 첫 번째 노인이 주장했다. ‘그리고 그자는 그것들이 모퉁이를 돌아 달려와 제 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려 할 때를 알기라도 하지. 그것들에 관해 아무것도 모르는 가엾은 외국인한테서야 무얼 바랄 수 있겠소!’\n\n‘이 사람이 외국인입니까?’ 클레넘이 들여다보려고 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다.\n\n‘프랑스인입니다, 손님’, ‘포르투갈인입니다, 손님’, ‘네덜란드인입니다, 손님’, ‘프로이센인입니다, 손님’ 따위의, 그리고 그 밖의 서로 엇갈리는 증언들이 오가는 가운데, 그는 이제 어떤 가냘픈 목소리가 이탈리아어로도 프랑스어로도 물을 청하는 것을 들었다. 답으로 ‘아, 가엾은 친구, 자긴 절대 못 이겨 낼 거라는데, 그도 그럴 만하지!’라는 말이 한 바퀴 두루 돌자, 클레넘은 자기가 저 가엾은 사람의 말을 알아듣는다며 지나가게 해 달라고 청했다. 그는 곧장 앞으로 이끌려, 그에게 말을 걸게 되었다.\n\n‘먼저, 이분은 물을 좀 원합니다.’ 그가 둘러보며 말했다. (착한 친구 여남은 명이 그것을 구하러 흩어졌다.) ‘심하게 다치셨소, 친구?’ 그가 이탈리아어로 들것 위의 남자에게 물었다.\n\n‘예, 선생님. 예, 예, 예. 제 다리예요, 제 다리. 하지만 이렇게 몸이 안 좋아도, 그 옛 음악을 들으니 기쁩니다.’\n\n‘당신은 여행자로군! 가만! 자, 물이오! 좀 드시지요.’\n\n그들은 들것을 포석을 쌓아 놓은 무더기 위에 내려놓았다. 그것은 땅에서 알맞은 높이여서, 그는 몸을 숙여 한 손으로 머리를 살짝 들어 올리고 다른 손으로 잔을 그의 입술에 대 줄 수 있었다. 검은 머리에 하얀 이를 가진, 작고 다부진 갈색 피부의 남자였다. 보아하니 생기 있는 얼굴이었다. 귀에는 귀걸이를 했다.\n\n‘됐소. 당신은 여행자요?’\n\n‘그렇습니다, 선생님.’\n\n‘이 도시에 처음 오셨소?’\n\n‘그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요, 완전히요. 저는 이 불행한 저녁에 막 도착했습니다.’\n\n‘어느 나라에서요?’\n\n‘마르세유에서요.’\n\n‘아니, 저것 보시오! 나도 그렇소! 나도 여기서는 거의 당신만큼이나 낯선 사람이라오, 여기서 태어나기는 했지만, 나도 얼마 전에 마르세유에서 왔소. 기운 내시오.’ 그가 얼굴을 닦아 주고 몸을 일으키자, 그 얼굴이 애원하듯 그를 올려다보았고, 그는 몸부림치는 형체를 덮은 외투를 가만히 다시 여며 주었다. ‘당신이 잘 보살핌받게 될 때까지 떠나지 않겠소. 용기를 내요! 반시간만 지나면 훨씬 나아질 거요.’\n\n‘아! 알트로, 알트로!’ 가엾은 작은 남자가 가냘프게, 믿기지 않는다는 어조로 외쳤다. 그리고 그들이 그를 들어 올릴 때, 오른손을 뻗어 손가락을 허공에 대고 손등으로 까딱까딱 흔들어 보였다.\n\n* 알트로(Altro): 이탈리아어로 ‘그럼요’, ‘설마요’ 등을 뜻하는 감탄사.\n아서 클레넘은 돌아서서, 들것 곁을 걸으며, 이따금 격려의 말을 건네면서, 그것을 따라 인근의 세인트바살러뮤 병원까지 동행했다. 무리 가운데 운반하는 자들과 그 말고는 아무도 들이지 않은 채, 그 불구가 된 남자는 곧 서늘하고 질서정연한 방식으로 탁자 위에 뉘어졌고, 마치 재앙의 여신 그 자체만큼이나 가까이 대기해 있다가 곧장 나타날 채비가 된 한 외과의에게 신중히 진찰받았다. ‘이 사람은 영어를 거의 한마디도 모릅니다.’ 클레넘이 말했다. ‘심하게 다쳤습니까?’\n\n‘진단을 내리기 전에,’ 외과의가, 일에 임하는 사람다운 흥미를 띠고 진찰을 이어 가며 말했다, ‘우선 다 알아봅시다.’\n\n손가락 하나로, 또 손가락 둘로, 또 한 손으로, 또 두 손으로, 위로 아래로, 또 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 다리를 살펴보고, 자기에게 합류한 다른 신사에게 흥미로운 점들을 흡족하게 짚어 보인 끝에, 외과의는 마침내 환자의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했다. ‘이 사람 괜찮을 겁니다. 아주 잘 회복될 거예요. 꽤 까다롭긴 해도, 이번엔 다리를 떼어 낼 필요는 없겠어요.’ 클레넘이 이를 환자에게 통역해 주자, 환자는 감사로 가득 차서, 제 특유의 격한 방식으로 통역하는 이의 손과 외과의의 손에 여러 번 입을 맞췄다.\n\n‘심각한 부상인 모양이지요?’ 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n‘그-렇지요,’ 외과의가, 이젤 위의 작품을 음미하는 화가의 사색적인 기쁨으로 답했다. ‘예, 충분히 그렇습니다. 무릎 위로 복합 골절이 있고, 아래로는 탈구가 있어요. 둘 다 아름다운 종류지요.’ 그는 다시 환자의 어깨를 다정하게 탁 쳤는데, 마치 이 사람이 정말로 무척 좋은 친구이며, 과학에 흥미로운 방식으로 다리를 부러뜨린 것에 대해 온갖 칭찬을 받을 만하다고 진심으로 여기는 듯했다.\n\n‘이 사람 프랑스어를 합니까?’ 외과의가 말했다.\n\n‘오 예, 프랑스어를 합니다.’\n\n‘그럼 여기서 곤란할 일은 없겠군. — 당신은 그저 용감한 사내답게 약간의 통증만 견디면 되고, 모든 게 이만큼 잘 풀리는 걸 고맙게 여기면 되오, 친구,’ 그가 그 말로 덧붙였다, ‘그러면 놀랄 만큼 멀쩡히 다시 걷게 될 거요. 자, 이제 다른 데는 이상이 없는지, 갈비뼈는 어떤지 봅시다.’\n\n다른 데는 이상이 없었고, 갈비뼈는 멀쩡했다. 클레넘은 할 수 있는 모든 처치가 능숙하고 신속하게 다 이루어질 때까지 남아 있었고 — 낯선 땅에서 길 잃은 그 가엾은 방랑자가 그 호의를 애처롭게 그에게 간청했던 것이다 — 그가 때맞춰 옮겨진 침대 곁에서, 그가 선잠에 빠질 때까지 머물렀다. 그러고 나서도 그는 그를 위해 자기 명함에 몇 마디를 적어, 내일 다시 오겠다는 약속과 함께, 그가 깨어나면 건네주도록 남겨 두었다.\n\n이 모든 절차가 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 그가 병원 정문을 나설 때 밤 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다. 그는 당분간 코번트 가든에 거처를 빌려 두고 있었고, 스노 힐과 홀번을 거쳐 그쪽으로 가는 가장 가까운 길을 택했다.\n\n마지막 모험의 노심초사와 연민이 지난 뒤, 다시 혼자가 되자, 그는 자연히 생각에 잠긴 기분이 되었다. 또한 자연히, 그는 십 분도 생각에 잠겨 걷지 못하고 플로라를 떠올렸다. 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 자신의 삶을, 그 온갖 어긋남과 보잘것없는 행복과 더불어 떠올리게 했다.\n\n거처에 다다르자, 그는, 옛 방의 창가에 서서 그을음으로 검게 된 굴뚝의 숲을 내다보았던 것처럼, 꺼져 가는 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 자신을 지금 이 삶의 단계까지 데려온 그 음울한 행로를 돌아보았다. 그토록 길고, 그토록 헐벗고, 그토록 텅 빈 행로였다. 유년도 없고, 단 하나의 추억을 빼고는 청춘도 없었으며, 바로 그 하나의 추억마저 바로 그날 한낱 어리석음의 조각이었음이 드러난 터였다.\n\n남에게는 사소한 일일지 몰라도, 그에게는 불행이었다. 그의 기억 속에서 모질고 가혹한 모든 것은, 막상 확인해 보니 여전히 현실로 남아 — 눈으로 보고 손으로 만져 보아도 완강했고, 그 오랜 불굴의 음울함을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았건만 — 그의 경험 가운데 단 하나 다정했던 그 추억만은 같은 시험을 견디지 못하고 녹아 사라졌던 것이다. 그는 지난밤, 눈을 뜬 채 꿈을 꿀 때 이를 예감하기는 했으나, 그때는 그것을 느끼지 못했다. 그런데 이제는 느꼈다.\n\n그가 그런 식으로 몽상가였던 것은, 그가 본성 깊숙이, 자기 삶에 없었던 온갖 다정하고 선한 것들에 대한 믿음을 뿌리내린 사람이었기 때문이다. 옹졸함과 모진 거래 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 명예로운 마음과 후한 손을 지닌 사람이 되게 했다. 냉랭함과 가혹함 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 따뜻하고 공감할 줄 아는 마음을 지니게 했다. 인간을 그 창조주의 형상으로 빚는 것을 거두어 도리어 창조주를 잘못 저지르는 인간의 형상으로 빚는 그 과정을 따라가는, 끝까지 좇기엔 너무도 어둡고 무엄한 신조 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 심판하지 않고, 겸손하게 자비를 베풀며, 희망과 사랑을 품게 했다.\n\n그리고 이 믿음은, 그러한 행복이나 그러한 덕이 자기의 보잘것없는 길에는 찾아오지 않았다거나 자기에게 잘 작용하지 않았다고 해서, 그것이 그 위대한 섭리 안에 없는 것이라거나, 겉으로 드러날 때조차 가장 비천한 요소로 환원될 수 있는 것이라고 여기는 그 칭얼대는 나약함과 잔인한 이기심으로부터도 여전히 그를 지켜 주었다. 그는 실망한 마음을 지녔으되, 그런 해로운 공기에 물들기엔 너무도 굳세고 건강한 마음을 지니고 있었다. 제 자신은 어둠 속에 둔 채로도, 그 마음은 빛 속으로 떠올라, 그 빛이 다른 이들 위에 비치는 것을 보며 그것을 반길 수 있었다.\n\n그리하여 그는 꺼져 가는 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 자신이 그날 밤에 이르기까지 걸어온 그 길을 생각하며 슬퍼했으되, 다른 이들이 그 길에 이르러 온 행로 위에는 독을 뿌리지 않았다. 그가 그토록 많은 것을 놓쳤다는 것, 그리고 그의 나이에 이르러, 내리막 여정에서 그를 벗하며 길동무가 되고 그것을 북돋워 줄 어떤 지팡이라도 찾으려 이렇게 멀리 두리번거려야 한다는 것은, 정당한 회한이었다. 그는, 불꽃이 떠나가고, 그 잔광이 사그라들고, 재가 잿빛으로 변하고, 그 재가 먼지로 부스러져 내리는 난롯불을 바라보며 생각했다. ‘나 또한 머잖아 이런 변화들을 거쳐, 사라지고 말겠지!’\n\n자신의 삶을 돌이켜 보는 것은, 열매와 꽃이 달린 푸른 나무를 내려오면서, 그쪽으로 내려갈수록 모든 가지가 하나씩 하나씩 시들어 떨어지는 것을 보는 일과도 같았다.\n\n‘유년 시절의 그 불행한 억압에서부터, 그 뒤를 이은 엄격하고 사랑 없는 가정을 지나, 나의 떠남, 나의 긴 유랑, 나의 귀향, 어머니의 환영, 그 이래 어머니와의 교류를 거쳐, 가엾은 플로라와 보낸 오늘 오후에 이르기까지,’ 아서 클레넘이 말했다, ‘내가 찾아낸 것이 무엇인가!’\n\n그의 방문이 가만히 열렸고, 이렇게 소리 내어 한 말들이 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했으니, 마치 그것이 하나의 대답이기라도 한 듯 이런 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n‘리틀 도릿이에요.’\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Little Dorrit’s Party\n\n아서 클레넘은 황급히 일어나 문가에 서 있는 그녀를 보았다. 이 이야기는 때로 리틀 도릿의 눈으로 보아야 할 것이며, 그 첫걸음으로 그를 보는 데서 시작하기로 한다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 어둑한 방 안을 들여다보았는데, 그녀에게는 그 방이 널찍하고 호화롭게 꾸며진 것처럼 보였다. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 격조 높은 상념들 — 금실로 장식한 외투를 걸치고 칼을 찬 신사들이 다투고 결투를 벌이던, 이름난 커피하우스들이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 값비싼 상념들 — 겨울에도 한 송이에 1기니씩 하는 꽃이 있고, 한 파운드에 1기니씩 하는 파인애플이 있고, 한 파인트에 1기니씩 하는 완두콩이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 그림 같은 상념들 — 화려하게 차려입은 신사 숙녀들에게 경이롭고 아름다운 광경을 보여 주던, 가엾은 패니나 가엾은 삼촌으로서는 영영 닿을 수 없을 만큼 멀리 있던 거대한 극장이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 황량한 상념들 — 그 모든 아치들이 있어, 방금 그녀가 지나쳐 온, 누더기를 걸친 비참한 아이들이 어린 쥐새끼들처럼 슬그머니 기어들어 숨고, 쓰레기를 주워 먹고, 온기를 찾아 한데 옹송그리고, 이리저리 쫓겨 다니던 곳이라는 상념(어리고 늙은 쥐들을 살펴보라, 너희 모든 배너클들이여, 신께 맹세코 그것들이 우리의 토대를 갉아먹고 있으며, 끝내 지붕을 우리 머리 위로 무너뜨릴 것이니!). 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는, 차고 넘치는 상념들 — 과거와 현재의 신비, 로맨스, 풍요, 결핍, 아름다움, 추함, 아름다운 시골 정원과 더러운 거리의 시궁창이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 이 모든 것이 한데 뒤엉켜 — 문가에서 머뭇거리며 살펴보는 리틀 도릿의 눈에 그 방을 실제보다 더 어둑하게 만들었다.\n\n처음에는 꺼진 불 앞 의자에 앉아 있다가, 이윽고 그녀를 보고 의아해하며 돌아앉은 이는 바로 그녀가 찾던 신사였다. 그토록 유쾌하게 미소 짓고, 태도가 그토록 솔직하고 사려 깊으면서도, 그 진지함 속에는 어딘가 그의 어머니를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있는, 갈색 머리의 근엄한 신사였다 — 다만 그녀가 매서움 속에서 진지했다면 그는 부드러움 속에서 진지하다는 큰 차이가 있었다. 지금 그는 리틀 도릿의 눈길이 늘 떨구어지곤 했던, 그리고 지금도 떨구어지는 그 주의 깊고 캐묻는 듯한 시선으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"가엾은 아이야! 이 한밤중에 여기를?\"\n\n\"일부러 리틀 도릿이라고 말씀드렸어요, 선생님. 미리 마음의 준비를 하시도록요. 무척 놀라실 줄 알았거든요.\"\n\n\"혼자 왔니?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님, 매기를 데려왔어요.\"\n\n자기 이름이 언급되었으니 등장 준비가 충분히 되었다고 여긴 매기가, 바깥 층계참에서 활짝 웃는 얼굴로 나타났다. 그러나 그녀는 이내 그 웃음을 거두고 한결같이 엄숙한 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"그런데 불이 꺼져 있구나.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"게다가 너는—\" 그는 옷을 너무 얇게 입었다고 말하려다가, 그것이 그녀의 가난을 들추는 일이 될까 봐 말을 멈추고는 대신 이렇게 말했다. \"게다가 날이 이렇게 춥고.\"\n\n그는 자기가 앉아 있던 의자를 난로 가까이로 끌어다 그녀를 앉히고, 서둘러 장작과 석탄을 가져와 함께 쌓아 올려 불을 피웠다.\n\n\"네 발이 대리석처럼 차구나, 아이야.\" 그는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 불을 지피다가 우연히 그녀의 발에 손이 닿았다. \"더 따뜻한 데로 가까이 대렴.\" 리틀 도릿은 황급히 고맙다고 했다. 충분히 따뜻하다고, 아주 따뜻하다고 했다! 그녀가 얇고 닳은 신발을 감추는 것을 보자 그의 가슴이 미어졌다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 자기 초라한 신발을 부끄러워하는 것이 아니었다. 그는 그녀의 사정을 알고 있었으니, 그것 때문이 아니었다. 리틀 도릿은 그가 그것을 본다면 자기 아버지를 탓할까 봐 마음이 쓰였던 것이다. 그가 '왜 아버지란 사람은 오늘 만찬을 들면서 이 작은 것을 차가운 돌바닥의 처분에 내맡겼단 말인가!' 하고 생각할까 봐서였다. 그것이 온당한 생각이라고 믿어서가 아니었다. 다만 그녀는 그런 오해가 때로 사람들의 마음에 떠오른다는 것을 경험으로 알고 있을 뿐이었다. 그런 오해가 떠오른다는 것 자체가 아버지가 겪는 불운의 한 부분이었다.\n\n\"다른 말씀을 드리기 전에,\" 리틀 도릿은 희미한 불 앞에 앉아, 관심과 연민과 보호의 빛이 조화롭게 어우러진 그 얼굴 — 자기로서는 도저히 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 까마득히 높은 곳에 있는 신비처럼 느껴지는 그 얼굴로 다시 눈을 들며 입을 열었다. \"한 가지 여쭙고 싶은 게 있어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그래, 아이야.\"\n\n그가 자꾸만 자기를 아이라고 부르는 것에 그녀의 얼굴에 옅은 곤혹의 그림자가 스쳤다. 그가 그것을 알아채거나 그런 사소한 일을 마음에 둔다는 것이 놀라웠지만, 그는 곧바로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"다정한 말을 쓰고 싶었는데, 달리 떠오르는 게 없었단다. 방금 네가 우리 어머니 댁에서 부르는 이름으로 스스로를 일컬었고, 또 나도 늘 그 이름으로 너를 떠올리니, 그냥 리틀 도릿이라고 부르게 해 다오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 어떤 이름보다 그게 더 좋아요.\"\n\n\"리틀 도릿.\"\n\n\"작은엄마.\" 깜빡 졸고 있던 매기가 고쳐 주듯 끼어들었다.\n\n\"다 같은 말이야, 매기.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"다 같은 말이야.\"\n\n\"그게 다 같은 거예요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"꼭 같은 거야.\"\n\n매기는 웃더니 이내 코를 골았다. 리틀 도릿의 눈과 귀에는 그 투박한 모습도, 그 투박한 소리도 더없이 사랑스러웠다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 자기 덩치 큰 아이에 대한 자부심의 빛이 번졌고, 그 얼굴이 다시 근엄한 갈색 머리 신사의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그가 매기와 자신을 바라보며 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 그녀는 궁금했다. 그가 얼마나 좋은 아버지가 될까 하고 생각했다. 저런 눈길로 자기 딸을 가르치고 아껴 주리라고.\n\n\"제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"제 오빠가 풀려났다는 거예요.\"\n\n아서는 그 말을 듣고 기뻐하며, 오빠가 잘 지내기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 작은 몸을 온통 떨며, 목소리마저 떨면서 말했다. \"누구의 너그러움 덕에 오빠가 풀려났는지 저는 알아서는 안 되고, 결코 물어서도 안 되고, 결코 들어서도 안 되고, 이 가슴 가득한 고마움을 담아 그분께 결코 감사를 드려서도 안 된다는 거예요!\"\n\n아마 그분은 감사 따위는 필요로 하지 않을 거라고 클레넘은 말했다. 도리어 그분은 큰 도움을 받아 마땅한 그녀에게 작은 도움이나마 베풀 수단과 기회가 있었음을 스스로 고맙게 여길 것이라고(또 그럴 만한 이유가 있다고) 했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 드리려던 말씀은요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 점점 더 떨면서 말했다. \"만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면, 저는 그분께 말하고 싶어요. 그분의 자비로움을 제가 어떻게 느끼는지, 또 우리 착하신 아버지가 그것을 어떻게 느끼실지, 그분은 결코, 결코 모르실 거라고요. 그리고 제가 드리려던 말씀은요, 선생님, 만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면 — 하지만 저는 그분을 모르고, 알아서도 안 되지요, 그건 알아요! — 저는 그분께 말하고 싶어요. 그분을 축복하고 보답해 달라고 하늘에 기도드리지 않고는 다시는 잠자리에 들지 않겠다고요. 그리고 만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면, 저는 그분 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 손을 잡아 입 맞추고, 손을 거두지 말고 그냥 두어 달라고 — 아, 잠깐만이라도 그냥 두어 달라고 — 그래서 제 고마운 눈물이 그 위에 떨어지게 해 달라고 청하고 싶어요. 그분께 드릴 감사라고는 그것밖에 없으니까요!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그의 손을 자기 입술로 가져갔고, 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 했으나, 그는 부드럽게 그녀를 만류하여 다시 의자에 앉혔다. 그녀의 눈과 목소리의 떨림은 그녀가 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 그에게 감사를 전했다. 그는 평소처럼 침착하게는 말하지 못한 채 입을 열었다. \"자, 리틀 도릿, 자, 자, 자! 네가 정말 그분을 안다고 치고, 그 모든 걸 할 수 있다고 치고, 또 그 모든 게 다 이루어졌다고 치자꾸나. 그러면 이제 말해 보렴. 나는 전혀 다른 사람이니까 — 너에게 자기를 믿어 달라고 청한 친구일 뿐인 나에게 — 어째서 이 한밤중에 나와 있는지, 또 이 늦은 시각에 거리를 그토록 멀리 헤쳐 너를 여기까지 데려온 게 무엇인지를, 가냘프고 여린 나의,\" 다시 아이라는 말이 입가에 맴돌았다. \"리틀 도릿!\"\n\n\"매기랑 저는 오늘 밤,\" 그녀는 오래도록 몸에 밴 그 조용한 노력으로 자신을 가다듬으며 대답했다. \"언니가 일하는 극장에 갔었어요.\"\n\n\"아, 거기 참말로 천국 같은 데 아니에요?\" 마음만 먹으면 언제든 잠들었다 깨어나는 재주를 지닌 듯한 매기가 불쑥 끼어들었다. \"병원만큼이나 좋다니까요. 다만 거기엔 치킨이 없어요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하고 그녀는 몸을 한 번 떨더니 다시 잠들었다.\n\n\"우리가 거기 간 건요,\" 리틀 도릿은 자기가 돌보는 아이를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"이따금 언니가 잘 지내고 있는지 제 눈으로 직접 확인하고 싶어서예요. 언니도 삼촌도 모를 때 제 두 눈으로 거기 있는 언니를 보고 싶어서요. 사실 그렇게 할 수 있는 일은 정말 드물어요. 일하러 나가지 않을 때는 아버지 곁에 있고, 일하러 나가서도 아버지께로 서둘러 돌아가니까요. 하지만 오늘 밤만큼은 파티에 와 있는 척하는 거예요.\"\n\n머뭇거리며 수줍게 그 고백을 하다가, 그녀는 그 얼굴로 눈을 들었고, 거기 떠오른 표정을 너무도 또렷이 읽고는 그에 답했다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 정말로요! 저는 평생 파티에 가 본 적이 없는걸요.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 주의 깊은 눈길 아래 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 다시 말했다. \"이게 나쁜 일은 아니었으면 좋겠어요. 조금이라도 그런 척이라도 하지 않았더라면, 저는 누구에게도 도움이 될 수 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 자기들 몰래, 또 고마움도 받지 못한 채 — 어쩌면 소홀하다는 책망까지 들어 가며 — 그들을 위해 궁리하고, 그들을 위해 생각하고, 그들을 지켜보려 그렇게 꾸민 것을 그가 마음속으로 나무라고 있을까 봐 두려워했다. 그러나 정작 그의 마음에 있던 것은, 강인한 목적을 지닌 그 여린 모습, 얇게 닳은 신발, 변변찮은 옷차림, 그리고 오락과 즐거움이라는 그 가장(假裝)이었다. 그는 그 가상의 파티가 어디서 열렸느냐고 물었다. 자기가 일하는 곳에서요, 라고 리틀 도릿은 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. 그녀는 그 일에 대해 거의 말하지 않았다. 아버지를 안심시킬 몇 마디만 했을 뿐이었다. 아버지는 그것을 대단한 파티라고 믿지는 않았다 — 사실 그렇게 짐작했을 법도 했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 자기가 두른 숄을 잠깐 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"오늘이 처음이에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"집을 떠나 밖에 있어 본 게요. 런던은 어쩌면 이렇게도 크고, 황량하고, 거칠어 보이는지요.\" 리틀 도릿의 눈에는, 검은 하늘 아래 펼쳐진 그 광막함이 무시무시했다. 그 말을 하면서 그녀는 몸을 한 차례 떨었다.\n\n\"하지만 이게,\" 그녀는 다시 그 조용한 노력으로 덧붙였다. \"제가 선생님을 번거롭게 해 드리려고 찾아온 이유는 아니에요, 선생님. 언니가 어떤 친구를, 언니가 제게 말해 주어서 좀 걱정스러워진 어떤 부인을 사귀게 된 것이 제가 집을 나오게 된 첫 번째 까닭이었어요. 그리고 나온 김에, 일부러 선생님이 사시는 곳을 돌아 지나오다가 창에 불빛이 보여서요—\"\n\n이번이 처음은 아니었다. 아니, 처음이 아니었다. 리틀 도릿의 눈에는, 그 창의 바깥쪽이 오늘 밤이 아닌 다른 여러 밤에도 멀리 빛나는 별이었다. 그녀는 지치고 시름에 잠긴 채로, 가던 길을 애써 벗어나 그것을 올려다보며, 그토록 먼 데서 자기에게 친구로서, 보호자로서 말을 건네 준 그 근엄한 갈색 머리 신사에 대해 생각하곤 했던 것이다.\n\n\"세 가지가 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"선생님이 혼자 계시고, 제가 올라와도 된다면 말씀드리고 싶은 게요. 첫째는, 말씀드리려 애썼지만 결코 못 하는 — 결코 하지 못할 —\"\n\n\"쉿, 쉿! 그건 이미 끝나서 처리됐단다. 두 번째로 넘어가자꾸나.\" 클레넘은 미소로 그녀의 동요를 가라앉히며, 불빛이 그녀에게 비치도록 하고, 포도주와 케이크와 과일을 탁자 위로 그녀 쪽으로 밀어 주었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"이게 두 번째예요, 선생님. 제 생각엔 클레넘 부인께서 제 비밀을 알아내신 것 같아요. 제가 어디서 와서 어디로 가는지 아시는 것 같아요. 제가 어디 사는지 말이에요.\"\n\n\"정말이냐!\" 클레넘이 재빨리 되받았다. 잠시 생각한 뒤 그는 어째서 그렇게 여기느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔요,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"플린트윈치 씨가 저를 감시한 것 같아요.\"\n\n그러면 어째서 그렇게 생각하느냐고, 클레넘은 불로 시선을 돌리고 눈썹을 찌푸린 채 다시 곰곰이 헤아리며 물었다.\n\n\"그분을 두 번 마주쳤어요. 두 번 다 집 근처에서요. 두 번 다 밤에, 제가 돌아가던 길에서요. 두 번 다 저는(이건 제 착각일 수도 있지만요) 그분이 우연히 저를 만난 것처럼은 도무지 보이지 않았다고 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말이라도 했니?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그냥 고개를 끄덕이고는 머리를 한쪽으로 기울였어요.\"\n\n\"그자의 머리에 악마나 붙으라지!\" 클레넘은 여전히 불을 바라보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"늘 한쪽으로 기울어져 있으니.\"\n\n그는 정신을 차리고 그녀에게 포도주에 입을 좀 대고 뭔가 먹어 보라고 권했다 — 그녀가 워낙 소심하고 수줍어해서 무척 힘든 일이었다 — 그러고는 다시 생각에 잠겨 말했다.\n\n\"우리 어머니가 너를 대하는 태도가 조금이라도 달라졌니?\"\n\n\"아, 전혀요. 꼭 그대로세요. 제 사정을 말씀드리는 게 나을까 하고 생각해 봤어요. 그래도 될까 하고요 — 그러니까, 선생님께서 제가 말씀드리길 바라실까 하고요. 제가 어떻게 해야 할지 선생님께서 조언해 주실 수 있을까 하고요.\" 리틀 도릿은 애원하듯 그를 바라보다가, 그가 자기를 마주 보자 차츰 눈길을 거두며 말했다.\n\n\"리틀 도릿,\" 클레넘이 말했다 — 이 한마디는 이미 이 두 사람 사이에서, 쓰이는 어조와 맥락에 따라 백 가지 다정한 말을 대신하기 시작하고 있었다. \"아무것도 하지 마라. 내가 내 오랜 벗 애프리 부인과 얘기를 좀 나눠 보마. 아무것도 하지 마라, 리틀 도릿 — 다만 여기 있는 것들로 기운을 차리는 것만 빼고. 부디 그것만은 해 다오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 배고프지 않아요. 그리고,\" 그가 부드럽게 그녀의 잔을 밀어 주자 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"목마르지도 않고요. — 어쩌면 매기는 뭘 좀 먹고 싶어 할지도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"이따가 여기 있는 걸 죄다 매기 주머니에 넣게 해 주자꾸나.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"하지만 그 애를 깨우기 전에, 세 번째로 할 말이 있다고 했지.\"\n\n\"네. 화내지 않으실 거죠, 선생님?\"\n\n\"전혀 화내지 않겠다고 약속하마.\"\n\n\"이상하게 들릴 거예요. 어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요. 제가 분별없거나 고마움을 모른다고 여기지 말아 주세요.\" 리틀 도릿은 다시금 차오르는 동요 속에서 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니, 아니다. 분명 자연스럽고 옳은 일일 거야. 그게 무엇이든 내가 그걸 잘못 받아들일까 봐 걱정하지 않는다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 선생님은 제 아버지를 다시 뵈러 오실 거죠?\"\n\n\"그래.\"\n\n\"내일 가시겠다고 쪽지까지 써 주실 만큼 친절하고 자상하셨고요?\"\n\n\"아, 그건 아무것도 아니야! 그래.\"\n\n\"짐작하시겠어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 작은 두 손을 꼭 맞잡고, 두 눈으로 또렷이 내비치는 온 영혼의 진지함을 담아 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 하지 말아 달라고 부탁드리려는 게 무엇인지요?\"\n\n\"알 것 같구나. 하지만 틀릴 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n\"아니요, 틀리지 않으셨어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"만약 우리가 정말로, 정말로 절박해서 그게 없이는 도저히 안 될 지경이 되거든, 제가 선생님께 부탁드리게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"그러마, — 그러마.\"\n\n\"아버지가 청하도록 부추기지 말아 주세요. 아버지가 청하더라도 알아듣지 말아 주세요. 아버지께 그걸 주지 말아 주세요. 그것만은 면하게 해 드리고 아껴 주세요. 그러면 아버지를 더 좋게 여기실 수 있을 거예요!\"\n\n클레넘은 — 그녀의 근심 어린 눈에 그렁그렁 빛나는 눈물을 보고서 그리 또렷하게는 아니었으나 — 그녀의 바람을 신성히 여기겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"선생님은 아버지가 어떤 분인지 모르세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아버지가 정말 어떤 분인지 모르세요. 어떻게 아시겠어요, 그렇게 단번에 거기서 아버지를 뵈었을 뿐, 제가 그래 왔듯이 차츰차츰 알아 가지 않으셨는데요, 다정하신 분! 선생님은 우리에게 그토록 잘해 주셨어요, 그토록 섬세하고 진실하게 잘해 주셨어요. 그래서 저는 누구의 눈에보다도 선생님의 눈에 아버지가 더 좋게 비치기를 바라요. 그런데 차마 생각할 수 없어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 두 손으로 눈물을 가리며 외쳤다. \"이 세상 누구도 아닌 바로 선생님께서, 아버지가 영락하는 그 단 한 순간을 보시게 된다는 걸 차마 견딜 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"제발,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그렇게 괴로워하지 마라. 제발, 제발, 리틀 도릿! 이제 그건 충분히 알아들었단다.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 고맙습니다! 이 말씀을 드리지 않으려고 무척이나 애썼어요. 며칠 밤낮을 생각했어요. 하지만 선생님이 정말로 다시 오신다는 걸 알게 되니, 말씀드리기로 마음먹었어요. 아버지가 부끄러워서가 아니에요,\" 그녀는 재빨리 눈물을 닦았다. \"그게 아니라 제가 누구보다 아버지를 잘 알고, 아버지를 사랑하고, 아버지를 자랑스러워하기 때문이에요.\"\n\n이 무거운 짐을 내려놓자, 리틀 도릿은 어서 가야겠다는 초조한 마음에 안절부절못했다. 매기가 말짱히 깨어나 과일과 케이크를 멀찍이서 군침을 흘리며 기대에 차 킬킬거리고 있자, 클레넘은 할 수 있는 한 가장 좋은 방법으로 분위기를 바꾸어 그녀에게 포도주 한 잔을 따라 주었다. 매기는 그것을 요란하게 쩝쩝 소리를 내며 마셨는데, 한 모금 들이켤 때마다 손을 목에 갖다 대고는, 눈을 툭 튀어나오게 뜬 채 숨을 헐떡이며 \"아, 정말 끝내주게 맛있어! 정말 병원 같아!\" 하고 말했다. 포도주와 이 찬사를 다 마치고 나자, 그는 그녀에게 바구니(그녀는 늘 바구니를 들고 다녔다)에 탁자 위의 먹을 것을 죄다 담되, 부스러기 하나 남기지 않도록 각별히 신경 쓰라고 일렀다. 그것을 하는 매기의 기쁨과, 매기가 기뻐하는 것을 지켜보는 작은엄마의 기쁨은, 정황이 줄 수 있는 한 방금까지의 대화에 더없이 좋은 전환이 되어 주었다.\n\n\"하지만 문은 이미 한참 전에 잠겼을 텐데.\" 클레넘이 문득 그것을 떠올리고 말했다. \"어디로 갈 셈이냐?\"\n\n\"매기 숙소로 갈 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"거기 가면 아주 안전하고, 잘 보살핌을 받을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 거기까지 바래다줘야겠다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"혼자 가게 둘 수는 없어.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 부디 저희끼리 가게 해 주세요. 제발요!\" 리틀 도릿이 간청했다.\n\n그 청이 어찌나 간절했던지, 클레넘은 그녀에게 자신을 들이미는 것이 결례가 될 듯해 마음이 조심스러웠다. 더구나 매기의 숙소가 더없이 누추한 부류임을 그가 익히 짐작할 수 있었기에 더욱 그러했다. \"가자, 매기.\" 리틀 도릿이 명랑하게 말했다. \"우린 잘 해낼 거야. 이제는 길을 알지, 매기?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 작은엄마. 길 알아요.\" 매기가 킬킬거렸다. 그렇게 둘은 떠났다. 리틀 도릿은 문가에서 돌아서서 \"하느님의 가호가 함께하시길!\" 하고 말했다. 무척이나 나직이 말했지만, 어쩌면 그 말은 저 위에서는 — 누가 알겠는가! — 대성당의 온 성가대만큼이나 또렷이 들렸을지도 모른다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 둘이 길모퉁이를 돌 때까지 두고 보았다가 멀찍이 떨어져 뒤따랐다. 다시 한번 리틀 도릿의 사생활을 침범할 생각에서가 아니라, 그녀가 익숙한 동네에서 무사한 것을 보고 마음을 놓기 위해서였다. 그녀는 어찌나 자그마하고, 어찌나 가냘프고, 이 음산하고 축축한 날씨 앞에 어찌나 무방비해 보이던지, 발을 질질 끄는 자기 식솔의 그림자를 따라 종종걸음 치는 그 모습에, 그는 연민에 잠겨, 또 그녀를 거친 세상의 나머지와는 동떨어진 한 아이로 여기는 버릇에 잠겨, 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 올려 가는 곳까지 데려다주고 싶을 만큼이었다.\n\n이윽고 그녀는 마셜시가 있는 큰길로 들어섰고, 그러자 그는 둘이 걸음을 늦추더니 곧 옆길로 접어드는 것을 보았다. 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 더 따라갈 권리가 없음을 느끼며, 천천히 그들을 떠났다. 그는 그들이 아침까지 집 없이 떠돌 위험에 처했으리라고는 짐작조차 못했다. 그 진실은 한참, 아주 한참 뒤에야 알게 되었다.\n\n그런데 캄캄하게 어둠에 잠긴 어느 초라한 집 앞에 둘이 멈춰 서서, 문에 귀를 대고 들어 보아도 아무 소리도 나지 않자, 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"자, 매기, 여긴 너한테 좋은 숙소니까 폐를 끼치면 안 돼. 그러니까 두 번만, 그것도 너무 크지 않게 두드리자. 그래도 못 깨우면 날이 밝을 때까지 걸어 다녀야 해.\"\n\n한 번, 리틀 도릿은 조심스러운 손길로 두드리고는 귀를 기울였다. 두 번, 리틀 도릿은 조심스러운 손길로 두드리고는 귀를 기울였다. 사방은 닫혀 있고 고요했다. \"매기, 우린 할 수 있는 대로 해야 해, 얘야. 참고 날이 밝길 기다리자.\"\n\n둘이 다시 큰길로 나왔을 때는, 축축한 바람이 부는 쌀쌀하고 캄캄한 밤이었고, 시계들이 한 시 반을 치는 소리가 들렸다. \"다섯 시간 반만 지나면,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"우린 집에 갈 수 있어.\" 집 이야기를 하고 보니, 또 집이 그토록 가까운지라 가서 그것을 바라보는 것은 자연스러운 수순이었다. 둘은 닫힌 문으로 가서 마당 안을 들여다보았다. \"아버지가 푹 주무시고 계셨으면 좋겠어.\" 리틀 도릿이 쇠창살 하나에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"그리고 내가 없는 걸 모르셨으면.\"\n\n그 문이 어찌나 낯익고 또 길동무처럼 정겹던지, 둘은 매기의 바구니를 한쪽 구석에 내려놓아 의자 삼고는, 서로 바싹 붙어 한참을 거기서 쉬었다. 거리가 텅 비고 고요한 동안에는 리틀 도릿도 무섭지 않았다. 그러나 멀리서 발소리가 들리거나, 가로등 사이로 움직이는 그림자가 보이면 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 \"매기, 누가 와. 저리 가자!\" 하고 속삭였다. 그러면 매기는 짜증을 부리며 어렴풋이 깨어났고, 둘은 잠시 이리저리 헤매다가 다시 돌아오곤 했다.\n\n먹는 것이 신기하고 즐거운 동안에는 매기도 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 그 시기가 지나자, 그녀는 추위에 대해 칭얼대며 몸을 떨고 훌쩍이기 시작했다. \"곧 끝날 거야, 얘야.\" 리틀 도릿이 참을성 있게 말했다. \"아, 작은엄마야 다 괜찮으시겠지.\" 매기가 받아쳤다. \"하지만 난 불쌍한 애잖아, 겨우 열 살밖에 안 됐는데.\" 마침내, 거리가 정말로 쥐 죽은 듯 고요해진 한밤중에, 리틀 도릿은 그 무거운 머리를 자기 가슴에 뉘어 매기를 다독여 잠재웠다. 그렇게 그녀는 혼자인 양 문가에 앉아, 별들을 올려다보며, 그것들 위로 사납게 내달아 지나가는 구름들을 바라보았다 — 그것이 리틀 도릿의 파티에서 벌어지는 춤이었다.\n\n'정말로 파티라면!' 그녀는 거기 앉아 한 번 그렇게 생각했다. '밝고 따뜻하고 아름다웠다면, 그게 우리 집이고, 우리 가엾고 사랑하는 아버지가 그 집의 주인이고, 이 담장 안에는 한 번도 발을 들이지 않으셨다면. 그리고 클레넘 씨가 우리 손님 중 한 분이고, 우리가 흥겨운 음악에 맞춰 춤추며, 더없이 즐겁고 마음 가벼울 수 있는 만큼 다 그랬다면! 그러면—' 그토록 아득한 상상의 풍경이 눈앞에 펼쳐져, 그녀는 매기가 다시 칭얼대며 일어나 걷고 싶어 할 때까지, 별들을 올려다보며 넋을 잃고 앉아 있었다.\n\n세 시, 그리고 세 시 반, 그러는 사이 둘은 런던교를 건너 있었다. 둘은 조류가 장애물에 부딪쳐 콸콸 쏟아지는 소리를 들었고, 강 위에 드리운 어두운 안개 너머를 두려운 마음으로 내려다보았다. 다리의 등불이 비치는 작은 물빛 자국들이, 죄와 비참함에 무서운 매혹을 던지는 악마의 눈처럼 빛나는 것을 보았다. 둘은 구석에 웅크려 누운 집 없는 사람들을 피해 움츠리며 지나쳤다. 둘은 술 취한 자들에게서 달아났다. 둘은 으슥한 모퉁이에서 서로 휘파람을 불고 신호를 주고받거나 전속력으로 내빼는, 슬그머니 움직이는 사내들을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다. 가는 곳마다 앞장서 길잡이 노릇을 하면서도, 리틀 도릿은 한 번만은 자기가 어려 보이는 것을 다행으로 여기며, 매기에게 매달려 의지하는 척했다. 그리고 한 번 이상, 둘이 가는 길의 떠들썩하거나 어슬렁거리는 무리 가운데서 어떤 목소리가 나머지 사람들에게 \"저 아낙이랑 애는 그냥 지나가게 둬!\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n그렇게 그 아낙과 아이는 지나쳤고, 계속 나아갔으며, 첨탑들에서 다섯 시를 알리는 소리가 울렸다. 둘은 이미 동녘에서 처음 비치는 희끄무레한 새벽빛을 찾으며 천천히 동쪽으로 걷고 있었는데, 그때 한 여자가 그들을 뒤쫓아 왔다.\n\n\"그 애를 데리고 뭘 하는 거예요?\" 그녀가 매기에게 말했다.\n\n그녀는 젊었다 — 하느님은 아시겠지만, 거기 있기에는 너무도 젊었다! — 그리고 추하지도, 사악해 보이지도 않았다. 거칠게 말했지만 본래 거친 목소리는 아니었다. 그 소리에는 어딘가 음악적인 데까지 있었다.\n\n\"댁은 댁 신세로 뭘 하는 건데요?\" 더 나은 대답이 없어 매기가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"내가 말 안 해도 안 보여요?\"\n\n\"보일 것 같진 않은데요.\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n\"내 신세 망치고 있는 거예요! 자, 내가 대답했으니 댁도 대답해요. 그 애를 데리고 뭘 하는 거예요?\"\n\n아이로 여겨진 그녀는 고개를 푹 떨군 채, 매기 곁에 몸을 바싹 붙이고 있었다.\n\n\"가엾어라!\" 여자가 말했다. \"이런 시각에 이 모진 거리로 애를 데리고 나오다니, 댁은 인정도 없어요? 이 애가 얼마나 여리고 가냘픈지 보이지도 않아요, 눈도 없어요? 이 차갑고 떨리는 작은 손을 더 가엾이 여길 분별도 (별로 있어 보이진 않지만) 없어요?\"\n\n그녀는 그쪽으로 건너와, 그 손을 자기 두 손 사이에 쥐고 비벼 주었다. \"가엾고 길 잃은 사람한테 입맞춤해 주렴, 얘야.\" 그녀가 얼굴을 숙이며 말했다. \"그리고 이 사람이 널 어디로 데려가는지 말해 다오.\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 그녀를 향해 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"세상에, 맙소사!\" 그녀가 뒷걸음치며 말했다. \"당신은 여자잖아!\"\n\n\"그건 신경 쓰지 마세요!\" 리틀 도릿이, 갑자기 자기 손을 놓아 버린 그녀의 한 손을 붙잡으며 말했다. \"전 당신이 무섭지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 무서워하는 게 좋을걸요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"어머니는 없어요?\"\n\n\"없어요.\"\n\n\"아버지는요?\"\n\n\"있어요, 아주 사랑하는 아버지가요.\"\n\n\"아버지께로 돌아가요, 그리고 날 무서워해요. 손 놓아요. 잘 가요!\"\n\n\"먼저 고맙다는 말씀을 드려야겠어요. 제가 정말 아이인 것처럼 당신께 말하게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"그렇겐 못 해요.\" 여자가 말했다. \"당신은 다정하고 순수해요. 하지만 아이의 눈으로 날 바라볼 순 없어요. 당신이 아이인 줄 알았으니까 손을 댔지, 안 그랬으면 결코 손대지 않았을 거예요.\" 그러고는 이상하고 격한 외마디 소리를 내지르며 그녀는 가 버렸다.\n\n하늘에는 아직 날이 밝지 않았지만, 거리의 울리는 돌바닥에는 날이 밝아 오고 있었다. 짐마차와 손수레와 사륜마차에도, 갖가지 일터로 가는 일꾼들에게도, 이른 가게들이 문 여는 데에도, 시장의 거래에도, 강가의 부산함에도 날이 밝아 오고 있었다. 다른 때라면 띠었을 빛깔보다 더 희미해진, 너울거리는 등불들 속에 날이 밝아 오고 있었다. 더 매서워진 공기와, 으스스하게 사위어 가는 밤에도 날이 밝아 오고 있었다.\n\n둘은 이제 문이 열릴 때까지 거기서 기다릴 작정으로 다시 문가로 돌아갔다. 그러나 공기가 어찌나 차고 쌀쌀하던지, 리틀 도릿은 잠결의 매기를 이리저리 데리고 다니며 계속 몸을 움직였다. 교회 쪽으로 돌아가다가 그녀는 거기 불이 켜져 있고 문이 열려 있는 것을 보고는, 층계를 올라가 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"누구냐?\" 마치 지하 납골당에서 잠자리에 들기라도 할 양으로 나이트캡을 쓰고 있던 건장한 노인이 소리쳤다.\n\n\"별것 아닌 사람이에요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 노인이 소리쳤다. \"어디 좀 보자!\"\n\n이 말에 그녀는 막 나가려던 참에 다시 돌아서서, 자기와 자기가 돌보는 아이를 노인 앞에 내보였다.\n\n\"그럴 줄 알았다!\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 너를 알아.\"\n\n\"우린 자주 서로 봤지요.\" 리틀 도릿은 그가 묘지기인지, 교회 관리인인지, 성당지기인지, 아무튼 무엇이었든 그를 알아보고 말했다. \"제가 여기 예배 보러 왔을 때요.\"\n\n\"그뿐인 줄 아느냐, 우리 명부에 네 출생이 올라 있단 말이다. 너는 우리 진귀한 구경거리 중 하나야.\"\n\n\"정말요!\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고. 그—의 자식으로 말이다. 그건 그렇고, 어쩌다 이렇게 일찍 나와 있느냐?\"\n\n\"어젯밤에 밖에 갇혀서, 들어가려고 기다리는 중이에요.\"\n\n\"설마! 게다가 아직 꼬박 한 시간이나 남았는데! 제의실로 들어오너라. 제의실엔 칠장이들 덕에 불이 있을 게다. 나도 칠장이들을 기다리는 게 아니었으면 여기 안 있었을 거다, 그건 믿어도 돼. 우리 진귀한 구경거리 하나가, 우리가 따뜻하게 편안히 데워 줄 수 있는 형편인데 추위에 떨게 둘 순 없지. 어서 오너라.\"\n\n그는 격의 없는 태도에 무척 마음씨 좋은 노인이었다. 제의실 불을 휘저어 돋우고 나서, 그는 명부가 꽂힌 선반들을 둘러보며 특정한 책 한 권을 찾았다. \"자, 여기 있다, 봐라.\" 그는 그것을 꺼내 책장을 넘기며 말했다. \"여기 네가 똑똑히 적혀 있을 게다. 에이미, 윌리엄과 패니 도릿의 딸. 세인트 조지 교구 마셜시 감옥에서 출생. 그리고 우린 사람들한테 말하지, 네가 그때부터 단 하루, 단 하룻밤도 떨어진 적 없이 거기서 살아왔다고 말이다. 정말이냐?\"\n\n\"정말이에요, 어젯밤까지는요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 그러나 감탄 어린 눈길로 그녀를 살피던 노인의 머리에 다른 생각이 떠올랐으니, 곧 이러했다. \"그런데 네가 기진하고 지쳐 보이는구나, 안됐다. 잠깐 있거라. 내가 교회에서 방석을 좀 가져올 테니, 너랑 네 친구는 불 앞에 누워 있거라. 문이 열릴 때 아버지께로 못 들어갈까 봐 걱정은 말고. 내가 불러 주마.\"\n\n그는 곧 방석을 가져와 바닥에 펼쳐 놓았다.\n\n\"자, 여기 있다, 봐라. 또 똑똑히 보이는구나. 아, 고맙단 말은 됐다. 나도 딸자식들이 있는 몸이야. 그 애들이 마셜시 감옥에서 태어나진 않았지만, 내가 사는 꼴이 네 아버지 같은 부류였더라면 그랬을 수도 있었지. 잠깐 있거라. 네 머리 밑에 뭘 좀 받쳐 줘야겠다. 여기 매장 명부가 있구나, 딱 좋겠다! 이 책엔 뱅엄 부인이 들어 있단다. 그런데 사람들 대부분한테 이런 책들이 흥미로운 건 — 누가 들어 있느냐가 아니라, 누가 안 들어 있느냐란 말이지 — 그러니까 누가 들어올 차례냐, 또 언제냐 하는 거야. 그게 흥미로운 물음이지.\"\n\n자기가 급조한 베개를 흐뭇하게 돌아보며, 노인은 둘에게 한 시간의 휴식을 남기고 자리를 떴다. 매기는 벌써 코를 골고 있었고, 리틀 도릿도 이내 그 운명의 봉인된 책 위에 머리를 뉜 채 깊이 잠들었다 — 그 신비로운 백지의 책장에는 조금도 마음 쓰지 않고서.\n\n이것이 리틀 도릿의 파티였다. 그 거대한 수도의 수치와 버림받음과 비참함과 무방비함. 그 음울한 밤의 축축함과 추위와 더디 가는 시간들과 빠르게 흘러가는 구름들. 이것이 리틀 도릿이, 비 내리는 아침의 첫 잿빛 안개 속에서 기진한 채 집으로 돌아간 바로 그 파티였다.\n15장. 플린트윈치 부인이 또 한 번 꿈을 꾸다\n\n# CHAPTER 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream\n\n도시 한복판의 그 쇠약한 노후한 집은 검댕의 망토를 두른 채, 제가 쇠락하는 데 함께하다가 함께 닳아 버린 버팀목에 무겁게 기대고서, 무슨 일이 닥치든 건강하거나 명랑한 시간이라곤 결코 알지 못했다. 행여 햇살이 닿더라도 그것은 한 줄기에 지나지 않았고, 그마저도 반 시간이면 사라졌다. 행여 달빛이 그 위에 내려앉더라도, 그것은 그 음울한 외투에 헝겊 몇 조각을 덧대어 한층 더 비참해 보이게 할 뿐이었다. 물론 별들은, 밤과 연기가 충분히 맑은 때면, 차갑게 그것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 온갖 궂은 날씨는 보기 드문 충직함으로 그 곁을 지켰다. 비도, 우박도, 서리도, 해빙도, 다른 곳에서는 이미 자취를 감춘 뒤에도 그 음산한 울안에서는 머뭇거리는 것을 보게 될 것이며, 눈으로 말하자면, 그곳에서는 몇 주씩이나, 노랗던 것이 검게 변한 지 한참이 지나도록, 더러운 제 목숨을 천천히 울며 흘려보내는 것을 보게 될 것이었다. 그 자리에는 그 밖의 추종자라곤 없었다. 거리의 소음으로 말하자면, 골목을 지나는 바퀴들의 덜컹거림은 그저 대문 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔다가 다시 쏟아져 나갈 뿐이어서, 귀를 기울이던 애프리 부인은 자신이 귀머거리가 되었다가 순간적인 섬광처럼 청력을 되찾는 것 같은 느낌이 들곤 했다. 휘파람, 노래, 이야기, 웃음, 그리고 온갖 즐거운 인간의 소리도 그러했다. 그것들은 한순간에 그 틈새를 뛰어넘어 제 갈 길로 가 버렸다.\n\n클레넘 부인의 방에서 일렁이는 난롯불과 촛불의 빛은 그 자리의 죽은 듯한 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 가장 큰 변화였다. 그녀의 좁고 긴 두 창 안에서, 불은 온종일 시무룩하게 빛났고 밤새도록 시무룩하게 빛났다. 드문 경우에는 그녀가 그러하듯 격정적으로 타올랐다. 그러나 대개는 그녀처럼 억눌린 채, 고르고 느리게 제 자신을 갉아먹었다. 그러나 짧은 겨울날의 여러 시간 동안, 오후 일찍부터 그곳이 어스레해질 때면, 바퀴 달린 의자에 앉은 그녀의 일그러진 형상이, 비뚤어진 목을 한 플린트윈치 씨의 형상이, 오가는 애프리 부인의 형상이 시시각각 변하며 대문 위의 집 벽에 던져져, 거대한 환등기에서 나온 그림자처럼 거기서 어른거리곤 했다. 방에 갇힌 병자가 밤을 맞을 채비를 하면, 이것들은 차츰차츰 사라졌다. 애프리 부인의 확대된 그림자만은 늘 마지막까지 어른거리다가, 마침내 마치 그녀가 마녀 나들이를 떠나기라도 하는 듯 공중으로 미끄러져 사라졌다. 그러면 외로운 불빛이 변함없이 타다가, 동트기 전에 창백하게 타들어, 마침내 애프리 부인의 입김 아래 꺼졌으니, 잠이라는 마녀의 영역에서 그녀의 그림자가 그 위로 내려앉을 때였다.\n\n이상하지 않은가, 그 작은 병실의 불이 사실은 봉화 불이어서, 누군가를, 그것도 세상에서 가장 있을 법하지 않은 누군가를, 반드시 와야만 하는 그 자리로 불러들이는 것이라면. 이상하지 않은가, 그 작은 병실의 불빛이 사실은 망보는 불이어서, 정해진 사건이 끝까지 지켜질 때까지 매일 밤 그 자리에서 타고 있는 것이라면! 해와 별 아래 먼지투성이 언덕을 오르고 지친 평원을 따라 애쓰며, 뭍으로 여행하고 바다로 여행하며, 그토록 기이하게 오가며, 만나고 작용하고 서로에게 반작용하는 그 무수한 여행자들 가운데 — 그 무리 가운데 누가, 여로의 끝을 조금도 의심하지 않은 채, 틀림없이 이리로 오고 있는 것일까?\n\n시간이 우리에게 보여 주리라. 영예의 자리와 치욕의 자리, 장군의 자리와 고수의 자리, 웨스트민스터 사원에 선 귀족의 조상과 깊은 바닷속에 잠긴 뱃사람의 해먹, 주교관과 구빈원, 양털 의자와 교수대, 왕좌와 단두대 — 그 모든 곳으로 가는 여행자들이 큰길 위에 있건만, 그 길에는 놀라운 갈림길들이 있어, 오직 시간만이 우리에게 각 여행자가 어디로 향하는지를 보여 주리라.\n\n어느 겨울날 해 질 녘, 종일 노곤했던 플린트윈치 부인은 이런 꿈을 꾸었다.\n\n그녀는 자신이 부엌에서 차를 끓이려고 주전자를 준비하고 있었다고 생각했고, 양옆으로 깊고 차가운 검은 골짜기가 가장자리를 이룬 화덕 한복판에서 사그라진 불 앞에, 발을 난로 받침대에 올리고 치맛자락을 걷어 올린 채 몸을 녹이고 있었다고 생각했다. 그렇게 앉아서, 삶이란 어떤 이들에게는 다소 따분한 발명품이 아닌가 하는 문제를 곰곰이 생각하고 있노라니, 등 뒤에서 난 갑작스러운 소리에 놀랐다고 그녀는 생각했다. 지난주에도 한 번 비슷하게 놀란 적이 있었으며, 그 소리는 알 수 없는 종류의 것 — 바스락거림과 빠른 발걸음 같은 서너 번의 잰 박자 소리였으며, 한편으로 마치 그 발걸음이 바닥을 흔들었거나, 심지어 어떤 무시무시한 손이 그녀를 건드리기라도 한 듯, 가슴에 충격이랄까 떨림이 전해졌다고 그녀는 생각했다. 이것이 그녀 안에서, 이 집에 귀신이 들렸다는 그녀의 어떤 오랜 두려움을 되살렸으며, 그래서 좀 더 사람 곁으로 가려고 어떻게 올라갔는지도 모르게 부엌 계단을 날듯이 뛰어올랐다고 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n애프리 부인은, 현관에 다다랐을 때 자기 군주 나리의 사무실 문이 열려 있고 방은 비어 있는 것을 보았다고 생각했다. 그래서 거리로 난 문가의 작은 방에 있는, 뜯어 놓은 창문으로 가서, 두근거리는 가슴을 유리 너머로, 귀신 들린 집 바깥의 살아 있는 것들과 이어 보려 했다고. 그러고는 대문 위 벽에, 저 위에서 대화를 나누는 두 똑똑한 양반의 그림자가 비치는 것을 보았다고. 그러고는 신발을 손에 든 채 위층으로 올라갔으니, 한편으로는 웬만한 유령쯤은 당해 낼 그 똑똑한 양반들 가까이에 있고 싶어서였고, 한편으로는 그들이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 들으려는 것이었다고.\n\n\"나한테 그런 헛소리는 안 통하오,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"당신한테서 그런 건 안 받소.\"\n\n플린트윈치 부인은 약간 열린 문 뒤에 서서, 남편이 이런 대담한 말을 하는 것을 아주 또렷이 들었다고 꿈꾸었다.\n\n\"플린트윈치,\" 클레넘 부인이 평소의 강하고 낮은 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"당신 안에는 분노의 악마가 있어요. 그것을 경계해요.\"\n\n\"하나가 있든 열둘이 있든 상관없소,\" 플린트윈치 씨는 그 더 많은 수가 진실에 가깝다고 말투로 강하게 암시하며 말했다. \"쉰이 있다 해도, 그것들 모두 이렇게 말해야 할 거요, 나한테 그런 헛소리는 안 통한다, 당신한테서 그런 건 안 받는다고 — 좋든 싫든 그렇게 말하게 만들 거요.\"\n\n\"내가 무엇을 했다고 그러오, 이 화 잘 내는 양반아?\" 그녀의 강한 목소리가 물었다.\n\n\"했냐고?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"나한테 들이덮쳤지.\"\n\n\"내가 당신에게 충고를 했다는 뜻이라면 —\"\n\n\"내가 뜻하지 않은 말을 내 입에 집어넣지 마시오,\" 제러마이아는 끈질기고 빈틈없는 고집으로 자기 비유적 표현을 고수하며 말했다. \"들이덮쳤다는 뜻이오.\"\n\n\"내가 당신에게 충고를 한 것은,\" 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그건 —\"\n\n\"그건 안 받겠소!\" 제러마이아가 소리쳤다. \"당신은 나한테 들이덮쳤소.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 당신한테 들이덮쳤다고 해 둡시다, 이 고약한 양반아,\" (제러마이아는 그녀가 자기 표현을 쓰도록 만든 데에 낄낄 웃었다.) \"그날 아침 아서한테 쓸데없이 의미심장하게 굴었으니 말이오. 나는 그것을 거의 신뢰를 저버린 일로서 불평할 권리가 있어요. 당신은 그럴 뜻이 없었지만 —\"\n\n\"그건 안 받겠소!\" 어깃장 놓는 제러마이아가 그 양보를 도로 내던지며 끼어들었다. \"나는 그럴 뜻이 있었소.\"\n\n\"당신이 굳이 그러겠다면 혼잣말을 하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에 없겠군요,\" 그녀는 화가 난 듯한 침묵 끝에 대꾸했다. \"내 말을 듣지 않겠다고 작정한, 경솔하고 고집 센 늙은이에게 내가 말을 거는 것은 헛된 일이지요.\"\n\n\"이봐, 그것도 안 받겠소,\" 제러마이아가 말했다. \"나한테는 그런 작정 따위 없소. 내가 그럴 뜻이 있었다고 했잖소. 내가 왜 그럴 뜻이 있었는지 알고 싶소, 이 경솔하고 고집 센 늙은이여?\"\n\n\"결국 당신은 내 말을 그대로 돌려줄 뿐이군요,\" 그녀는 분을 삭이며 말했다. \"그래요.\"\n\n\"그럼 이게 그 까닭이오. 당신이 그 사람한테 그의 아버지에 대해 깨끗이 밝혀 주지 않았으니까, 그리고 마땅히 밝혔어야 했으니까. 당신이 당신 자신에 대해 짜증을 부리기 전에, 당신은 —\"\n\n\"거기서 멈춰요, 플린트윈치!\" 그녀가 달라진 목소리로 외쳤다. \"한마디가 지나칠 수 있으니.\"\n\n노인도 그렇게 생각하는 듯했다. 또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀고, 그가 방 안에서 자리를 옮긴 뒤에야 다시, 좀 더 누그러진 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"내가 왜 그랬는지 말하려던 참이었소. 당신이 당신 자신을 편들기 전에, 마땅히 아서 아버지를 편들었어야 했다고 생각했기 때문이오. 아서의 아버지! 나는 아서의 아버지를 딱히 사랑하진 않았소. 나는 이 집에서 아서 아버지의 삼촌을 섬겼는데, 그때 아서 아버지는 나보다 그리 위에 있지도 않았소 — 주머니 사정으로 치면 더 가난했지 — 게다가 그 삼촌이 그 사람한테 유산을 남기느니 차라리 나한테 남기는 편이 더 그럴 법했을 정도였소. 그 사람은 응접실에서 굶주렸고, 나는 부엌에서 굶주렸으니, 그게 우리 처지의 주된 차이였소. 우리 사이엔 목 부러질 만한 가파른 계단 한 줄 말고는 별다른 게 없었던 거요. 그 시절 나는 그 사람한테 정이 든 적이 없었고, 어느 때고 그 사람한테 크게 정이 들었던 기억이 없소. 그 사람은 우유부단하고 결단력 없는 친구였는데, 젊었을 때 고아의 목숨만 빼고는 다른 모든 게 겁에 질려 빠져나가 버린 사람이었소. 그리고 그 사람이 당신을 이리로 데려왔을 때, 삼촌이 그 사람한테 정해 준 아내였던 당신을 데려왔을 때, 나는 누가 주인이 될지 알아보려고 당신을 두 번 볼 필요도 없었소 (그때 당신은 잘생긴 여자였지). 그 후로 당신은 줄곧 제 힘으로 서 왔소. 지금도 제 힘으로 서시오. 죽은 자에게 기대지 마시오.\"\n\n\"나는 결코 — 당신 말마따나 — 죽은 자에게 기대지 않아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 굴복했더라면 당신은 그러려는 마음이 있었지,\" 제러마이아가 으르렁거렸다. \"그래서 나한테 들이덮친 거요. 당신은 내가 굴복하지 않았다는 걸 잊지 못하지. 내가 아서 아버지한테 정의를 행할 만한 가치가 있다고 여긴다는 게 놀랍겠지? 어? 당신이 대답하든 말든 상관없소, 당신이 놀란 걸 나는 알고, 당신도 알고 있으니까. 자, 그럼, 어찌 된 일인지 말해 주겠소. 나는 성미로 치면 좀 별난 데가 있을지 몰라도, 이게 내 성미요 — 나는 아무도 제멋대로 다 하게 내버려 둘 수 없소. 당신은 결단력 있는 여자고, 똑똑한 여자요. 그리고 당신은 눈앞에 목적을 보면, 무엇으로도 그것에서 돌이켜지지 않소. 그걸 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 어디 있겠소?\"\n\n\"무엇으로도 나는 돌이켜지지 않아요, 플린트윈치, 내 자신에게 그것을 정당하다고 입증한 다음에는. 그 말을 덧붙여요.\"\n\n\"당신 자신에게 정당하다고 입증한 다음에라? 나는 당신이 이 지상에서 가장 결단력 있는 여자라고 했고 (혹은 그렇게 말하려 했고), 만일 당신이 품은 어떤 목적을 정당하다고 입증하기로 작정한다면, 당연히 당신은 그리할 거요.\"\n\n\"이 양반아! 나는 이 책들의 권위로 내 자신을 정당화해요,\" 그녀는 준엄한 강세로 외쳤고, 뒤따른 소리로 미루어 죽은 듯 무거운 팔을 탁자에 내리치는 듯했다.\n\n\"그건 그렇다 치고,\" 제러마이아가 침착하게 대꾸했다. \"지금은 그 문제는 따지지 맙시다. 그게 어떻든 간에, 당신은 당신의 목적을 이뤄 내고, 그 앞에서 모든 걸 무릎 꿇게 하지. 자, 나는 그 앞에서 무릎 꿇지 않겠소. 나는 당신에게 충실했고, 당신에게 쓸모 있었고, 당신에게 애착을 갖고 있소. 하지만 나는 당신 속에 삼켜져 사라지는 데 동의할 수 없고, 동의하지 않겠고, 한 번도 동의한 적 없으며, 앞으로도 결코 동의하지 않겠소. 다른 사람들은 다 삼켜 버리시오, 얼마든지 좋소. 내 성미의 별난 점은, 부인, 산 채로 삼켜지지는 않겠다는 거요.\"\n\n어쩌면 이것이 애초에 두 사람 사이 양해의 핵심 동력이었는지도 모른다. 플린트윈치 씨에게서 이만큼의 강한 성격을 알아보았기에, 어쩌면 클레넘 부인은 그와 손잡는 편이 제게 이롭다고 여겼는지도 모른다.\n\n\"이 이야기는 이만하면 됐고도 남았어요,\" 그녀가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 나한테 다시 들이덮치지만 않는다면,\" 끈질긴 플린트윈치가 대꾸했다. \"그렇지만 또 들이덮친다면, 그땐 또 그 소리를 듣게 될 줄 아시오.\"\n\n애프리 부인은, 이 대목에서 자기 나리의 형상이 울화를 식히려는 듯 방 안을 오락가락하기 시작했고, 그래서 자신이 달아났다고 꿈꾸었다. 그러나 그녀가 그늘진 현관에 서서 잠시 귀 기울이며 떨고 있는 동안에도 그가 나오지 않자, 전과 마찬가지로 귀신과 호기심에 떠밀려 다시 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 또 한 번 문밖에서 몸을 웅크렸다고.\n\n\"양초에 불을 켜요, 플린트윈치,\" 클레넘 부인이 평소의 어조로 그를 되돌리고 싶은 듯 말하고 있었다. \"차 마실 시간이 다 됐어요. 리틀 도릿이 올 텐데, 어두운 데 있는 나를 보게 되겠어요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 잽싸게 양초에 불을 켜고, 그것을 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"리틀 도릿을 어쩔 작정이오? 영영 여기 와서 일하라는 거요? 영영 여기 와서 차를 마시라는 거요? 줄곧 이렇게 영영 여기를 오가라는 거요?\"\n\n\"나 같은 불구의 인간한테 어떻게 '영영'이라는 말을 할 수가 있어요? 우리 모두 들판의 풀처럼 베어지는 게 아닌가요, 그리고 나는 여러 해 전에 낫에 베이지 않았던가요. 그 후로 나는 곳간에 거둬들여지기를 기다리며 여기 누워 있는 거예요.\"\n\n\"옳소, 옳소! 하지만 당신이 여기 누워 있던 동안에 — 죽을 지경도 아니고 — 그 비슷하지도 않은데 — 수많은 아이들과 젊은이들이, 한창인 여자들이, 건장한 남자들이, 그 밖에 별별 사람들이 다 베여 실려 갔소. 그런데도 당신은 보다시피 여기 이렇게, 결국 그리 변하지도 않고 있잖소. 당신과 내 시간은 아직 길지도 모르오. 내가 '영영'이라고 할 때는, (내가 시인은 아니지만) 우리에게 남은 시간 내내라는 뜻이오.\" 플린트윈치 씨는 이 설명을 무척 침착하게 하고는, 침착하게 대답을 기다렸다.\n\n\"리틀 도릿이 얌전하고 부지런하며, 내가 줄 수 있는 작은 도움을 필요로 하고, 또 그럴 만한 자격이 있는 한, 내 생각에는, 그 애가 제 발로 물러가지 않는 한, 내가 살아 있는 동안에는 계속 여기 올 거예요.\"\n\n\"그것뿐이오?\" 플린트윈치가 입과 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다.\n\n\"그것 말고 무엇이 더 있겠어요! 그것 말고 무엇이 더 있을 수 있겠어요!\" 그녀가 준엄하게 의아해하는 투로 내뱉었다.\n\n플린트윈치 부인은, 일이 분 동안 두 사람이 그 사이에 양초를 두고 서로를 바라보고 있었으며, 어쩐지 그들이 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있다는 인상을 받았다고 꿈꾸었다.\n\n\"혹시 알고 있소, 클레넘 부인,\" 애프리의 군주 나리가 이윽고 훨씬 낮은 목소리로, 그리고 단순한 말의 목적에 견주어 영 어울리지 않을 만큼 무게 실린 표정으로 물었다. \"그 애가 어디 사는지?\"\n\"몰라요.\"\n\n\"알고 싶지 않소 — 자, 알고 싶지 않소?\" 제러마이아가 마치 그녀에게 덤벼들기라도 하듯 와락 물었다.\n\n\"내가 알고 싶었다면, 진작 알았겠지요. 아무 날에나 그 애한테 물어볼 수 있지 않았겠어요?\"\n\n\"그럼 알고 싶지 않다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그래요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 길고 의미심장한 숨을 내뱉고는, 앞서와 같은 강세로 말했다. \"내가 우연히 — 명심하시오! — 알아냈거든.\"\n\n\"그 애가 어디 살든,\" 클레넘 부인이 가락 없이 단단한 한 가지 목소리로, 마치 따로따로 떨어진 금속 조각에서 글자를 하나씩 집어 읽어 내듯 또박또박 말을 끊어 가며 말했다. \"그 애는 그것을 비밀로 했고, 앞으로도 줄곧 그것을 나한테 비밀로 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"결국, 어쨌든 그 사실을 차라리 모르는 편이 나았겠지?\" 제러마이아가 말했고, 그는 마치 제 말이 제 자신의 비뚤어진 모양새로 나오기라도 하는 듯 비틀린 투로 그것을 말했다.\n\n\"플린트윈치,\" 그의 주인이자 동업자가 애프리를 흠칫 놀라게 할 만큼 갑작스러운 기세로 번뜩이며 말했다. \"어째서 나를 들쑤시는 거요? 이 방을 둘러봐요. 이 비좁은 한계 안에 오래 갇혀 있는 데 대한 어떤 보상이라도 된다면 — 고통받는 것을 불평하는 건 아니에요, 그건 내가 결코 불평하지 않는다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요 — 이 방에 오래 갇혀 있는 데 대한 어떤 보상이라도 된다면, 즉 내가 온갖 즐거운 변화로부터 차단된 채, 차라리 모르는 편을 택하고 싶을 어떤 것들에 대한 앎으로부터도 차단되어 있다는 것이 보상이 된다면, 하필 당신이, 다른 누구도 아닌 당신이, 어찌하여 내게서 그 믿음을 아까워하는 거요?\"\n\n\"나는 그걸 당신한테 아까워하는 게 아니오,\" 제러마이아가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그럼 더는 말하지 말아요. 더는 말하지 말아요. 리틀 도릿이 그 비밀을 나한테 간직하게 두고, 당신도 그것을 나한테 간직해요. 그 애가 보이지 않게, 캐묻지 않은 채 오가게 해요. 내가 고통받게, 내 처지에 어울리는 위안이나마 갖게 해 둬요. 당신이 악령처럼 나를 괴롭힐 만큼 그게 대단한 일이오?\"\n\n\"나는 당신한테 질문을 했을 뿐이오. 그게 다요.\"\n\n\"나는 그것에 대답했어요. 그러니 더는 말하지 말아요. 더는 말하지 말아요.\" 여기서 바퀴 달린 의자가 바닥을 구르는 소리가 들렸고, 애프리의 종이 황급히 홱 잡아당겨져 울렸다.\n\n그 순간 부엌의 알 수 없는 소리보다도 남편이 더 무서웠던 애프리는, 할 수 있는 한 가볍고 빠르게 살금살금 물러나, 올라갈 때 못지않게 잽싸게 부엌 계단을 내려가, 불 앞의 제자리에 다시 앉아, 또 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고, 마침내 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어썼다. 그러자 종이 한 번 더 울리고, 또 한 번 더 울리더니, 계속해서 울려 댔다. 그 끈질긴 부름에도 아랑곳없이, 애프리는 여전히 앞치마 뒤에 앉아 숨을 고르고 있었다.\n\n마침내 플린트윈치 씨가 줄곧 \"애프리, 이 여편네야!\" 하고 중얼대며 부르면서 계단을 질질 끌고 현관으로 내려왔다. 애프리가 여전히 앞치마 뒤에 남아 있자, 그는 양초를 손에 든 채 부엌 계단을 비틀비틀 내려와, 옆걸음으로 그녀에게 다가가, 앞치마를 홱 잡아채고는 그녀를 깨웠다.\n\n\"오, 제러마이아!\" 애프리가 깨어나며 외쳤다. \"어쩌면 그렇게 사람을 놀라게 해요!\"\n\n\"뭘 하고 있었던 거야, 이 여편네야?\" 제러마이아가 물었다. \"쉰 번도 더 종을 울려 댔는데.\"\n\n\"오, 제러마이아,\" 애프리 부인이 말했다. \"나 꿈을 꾸고 있었어요!\"\n\n그런 식의 지난번 일을 떠올린 플린트윈치 씨는, 마치 부엌을 밝히려고 그녀에게 불이라도 붙일 생각인 양 양초를 그녀의 머리에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"마님 차 드실 시간인 거 모르나?\" 그가 심술궂게 이를 드러내며 묻고는, 애프리 부인의 의자 다리 하나를 걷어찼다.\n\n\"제러마이아? 차 시간? 내가 어떻게 된 건지 모르겠어요. 그런데 꿈을 — 꿈을 꾸기 전에, 제러마이아, 어찌나 무시무시하게 가슴이 철렁했던지, 그래서 그런가 봐요.\"\n\n\"으악! 잠꾸러기 같으니!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 소리를 하는 거야?\"\n\n\"어찌나 이상한 소리였는지, 제러마이아, 그리고 어찌나 묘한 움직임이었는지. 여기 부엌에서 — 바로 여기서.\"\n\n제러마이아는 불을 치켜들어 거뭇해진 천장을 보고, 불을 내려 축축한 돌바닥을 보고, 불을 들고 돌아서서 얼룩지고 거뭇거뭇한 벽들을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"쥐, 고양이, 물, 배수구겠지,\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n애프리 부인은 고개를 저으며 그 하나하나를 부정했다. \"아니에요, 제러마이아. 전에도 느꼈어요. 위층에서도 느꼈고, 한 번은 밤에 마님 방에서 우리 방으로 가는 계단에서도 느꼈어요 — 바스락거리는 소리하고, 등 뒤에서 떨리듯 닿는 어떤 손길을요.\"\n\n\"애프리, 이 여편네야,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 술 냄새를 가려내려는 시험 삼아 코를 그 부인의 입술에 갖다 댄 뒤 음산하게 말했다. \"이 늙은 여편네야, 차를 냉큼 내오지 않으면, 자네는 자네를 부엌 저쪽 끝까지 날려 보낼 바스락 소리와 손길을 똑똑히 느끼게 될 줄 알아.\"\n\n이 예언은 플린트윈치 부인을 부추겨 부지런히 움직이게 했고, 서둘러 위층 클레넘 부인의 방으로 올라가게 했다. 그렇지만 그래도 이제 그녀는 그 음울한 집에 무언가 잘못된 것이 있다는 확고한 확신을 품기 시작했다. 그 후로 그녀는 날이 저문 뒤에는 그 집에서 결코 마음이 편치 않았고, 무언가를 보게 될까 봐 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰지 않고서는 어둠 속에서 결코 계단을 오르내리지 않았다.\n\n이러한 유령에 대한 두려움과 기묘한 꿈들이 겹쳐, 그날 저녁 플린트윈치 부인은 귀신에 들린 듯한 마음 상태에 빠졌는데, 지금 이 이야기가 그녀가 거기서 회복한 흔적을 찾아내기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴지도 모른다. 자신의 모든 새로운 경험과 지각이 어렴풋하고 불분명한 가운데, 자기 주변의 모든 것이 그녀 자신에게 알 수 없는 것이었던 만큼 그녀 역시 남들에게 알 수 없는 사람이 되어 갔다. 그리고 그녀가 그 집과 그 안의 모든 것을 자기 나름으로 알아내기 어려워했듯, 그녀도 누구에게나 만족스럽게 알아내기 어려운 사람이 되었다.\n\n그녀가 아직 클레넘 부인의 차 준비를 끝내지도 않았을 때, 늘 리틀 도릿을 알리는 그 부드러운 노크 소리가 문에 울렸다. 애프리 부인은 리틀 도릿이 현관에서 그 소박한 보닛을 벗는 것을, 그리고 플린트윈치 씨가 턱을 긁적이며 말없이 그녀를 바라보는 것을, 마치 그녀를 혼비백산하게 만들거나 셋 모두를 산산조각 낼 어떤 놀라운 결과가 뒤따르기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯, 지켜보았다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤 또 한 번 문에 노크 소리가 나서 아서가 왔음을 알렸다. 애프리 부인은 내려가 그를 들였고, 그는 들어서며 말했다. \"애프리, 당신이라 다행이군요. 당신한테 물어볼 게 있어요.\" 애프리는 즉시 대답했다. \"제발 부탁이니 나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요, 아서! 나는 한쪽 목숨은 무서워 혼이 다 나갔고, 다른 한쪽은 꿈으로 다 빠져나가 버렸어요. 나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요! 어느 게 어느 건지, 뭐가 뭔지 모르겠다고요!\" 그러고는 곧장 그에게서 달아나, 다시는 그의 곁에 가까이 오지 않았다.\n\n애프리 부인은 독서에 취미가 없었고, 설령 마음이 있다 한들 그 침침한 방에서는 바느질을 할 만한 빛도 충분치 않았으므로, 이제 그녀는 아서 클레넘이 돌아온 그날 저녁에 잠시 모습을 드러냈던 그 어스름 속에서 매일 밤을 보내며, 제 주인마님과 남편과 집안의 그 소리들에 관한 야릇한 추측과 의심을 잔뜩 떠안고 있었다. 그 사나운 신앙 의식이 거행될 때면, 이런 추측들이 애프리 부인의 눈길을 문 쪽으로 흩뜨려 놓곤 했는데, 마치 그 신성한 순간에 어떤 어두운 형상이 나타나 그 모임의 머릿수를 하나 더 채우기라도 할 것처럼 그녀가 기다리는 듯했다.\n\n그 밖에는, 애프리는 그 두 똑똑한 양반의 주의를 자기에게 뚜렷이 끌 만한 말이나 행동을 결코 하지 않았는데, 다만 어떤 경우들, 대개 잠자리에 들 무렵의 그 고요한 시각쯤이면, 그녀가 어둑한 구석에서 갑자기 튀어나와, 클레넘 부인의 작은 탁자 곁에서 신문을 읽고 있는 플린트윈치 씨에게 공포에 질린 얼굴로 속삭이곤 했다.\n\n\"저기, 제러마이아! 지금! 저 소리 뭐예요?\"\n\n그러면 그 소리는, 만일 있었다 한들, 그쳐 버렸을 것이고, 플린트윈치 씨는 마치 그녀가 방금 그를 제 뜻에 거슬러 베어 넘기기라도 한 듯 그녀에게 홱 돌아서서 으르렁댈 것이었다. \"애프리, 이 늙은 여편네야, 자네 한 사발 먹어야겠어, 이 늙은 여편네야, 아주 톡톡한 한 사발을! 자네 또 꿈을 꾸고 있었구먼!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 16. Nobody’s Weakness\n\n미글스 가족과의 교분을 다시 잇기로 한 때가 되자, 클레넘은 블리딩 하트 야드 경내에서 자신과 미글스 씨 사이에 맺은 약속에 따라, 어느 토요일에 트위크넘으로 발길을 돌렸다. 그곳에 미글스 씨는 자기 소유의 별장 한 채를 두고 있었다. 날씨가 맑고 건조한 데다, 영국의 어느 길이든 그토록 오래 외지에 나가 있던 사람에게는 흥미가 가득했으므로, 그는 여행 가방을 역마차로 부쳐 보내고 걸어서 길을 나섰다. 걷는다는 것 자체가 그에게는 새로운 즐거움이었고, 멀리 외국에 있던 시절에는 그의 삶에 좀처럼 변화를 주지 못했던 일이었다.\n\n그는 황야를 거닐어 보는 즐거움을 위해 풀럼과 퍼트니를 거쳐 갔다. 그곳은 환하고 눈부셨다. 트위크넘으로 가는 길에서 그만큼 멀리 와 있음을 깨닫자, 그는 한층 공허하고 덜 실체적인 여러 목적지로 향하는 길에서도 한참이나 와 있는 자신을 발견했다. 건강에 좋은 운동과 기분 좋은 길 위에서 그런 생각들이 그의 앞에 빠르게 떠올랐다. 시골을 홀로 걷는 동안 무언가를 골똘히 생각하지 않기란 쉽지 않은 일이다. 그리고 그에게는 곱씹어 볼 미정의 문제들이 많아서, 설령 랜즈엔드까지 걸어갔다 해도 다 못 끝냈을 정도였다.\n\n먼저, 그의 마음에서 좀처럼 떠나지 않는 문제가 있었다. 앞으로 살아가며 무엇을 할 것인가, 어떤 직업에 자신을 바칠 것이며, 어느 방향에서 그것을 찾는 것이 가장 좋을 것인가 하는 물음이었다. 그는 부자와는 거리가 멀었고, 결단을 내리지 못한 채 아무것도 하지 않는 날이 하루하루 지날수록 물려받은 재산은 그에게 더 큰 불안의 근원이 되었다. 이 유산을 어떻게 늘릴까, 혹은 어떻게 모아 둘까 궁리하기 시작할 때마다, 자신의 정의에 충족되지 못한 권리를 가진 누군가가 있을지 모른다는 불안이 되살아났다. 그리고 그것만으로도 아무리 긴 산책이라도 다 못 끝낼 만한 문제였다. 또한 어머니와의 관계라는 문제도 있었는데, 그 관계는 이제 평온하고 안정되었으나 결코 흉금을 터놓는 사이는 아니었고, 그는 일주일에 몇 번씩 어머니를 보았다. 리틀 도릿은 으뜸가는, 또 변함없는 주제였다. 그 자신의 삶의 사정이 그녀 자신의 사연과 한데 엮여, 그 작은 사람은, 한편으로는 순결한 의지의 끈으로, 다른 한편으로는 다정한 보호의 끈으로 자신과 맺어진 유일한 사람으로 그에게 다가왔다. 연민과 존중, 사심 없는 관심, 감사, 그리고 측은함의 끈이었다. 그녀를 생각하고, 그녀의 아버지가 죽음의 빗장 푸는 손길로 감옥에서 풀려날 가능성을 생각하면서 — 그것이 그가 내다볼 수 있는 유일한 사정의 변화였으니, 그녀의 삶의 방식 전체를 바꾸고, 그녀의 험한 길을 평탄하게 하며, 그녀에게 보금자리를 주어, 그가 바라던 대로 그녀의 벗이 되어 줄 수 있게 할 변화였다 — 그는 그런 관점에서 그녀를 자신의 양녀로, 잠재워 쉬게 한 마셜시의 가엾은 자식으로 여겼다. 만일 그의 생각 속에 마지막 주제가 하나 있어 그것이 트위크넘 쪽을 향해 있었다면, 그 형체가 너무도 뚜렷하지 않아서, 이 다른 주제들이 그의 앞에 떠다니던 그 사방에 깔린 분위기와 별반 다르지 않은 정도였다.\n\n황야를 가로질러 그것을 뒤로 남기고 떠날 무렵, 그는 한동안 앞서 가던 어떤 사람을 따라잡게 되었는데, 다가가면서 그 사람을 아는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그 느낌은 머리를 돌리는 모양새와, 제법 다부진 걸음으로 나아가면서 무언가를 곰곰이 따지는 듯한 그 사람의 거동에서 비롯되었다. 그러나 그 남자가 — 남자의 모습이었으므로 — 모자를 머리 뒤로 밀어 올리고는 앞에 놓인 무엇인가를 살피려 멈춰 서자, 그는 그가 대니얼 도이스임을 알아보았다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 도이스 씨?\" 클레넘이 그를 따라잡으며 말했다. \"다시 뵙게 되어, 그것도 에두름국보다 훨씬 건강에 좋은 곳에서 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다.\"\n\n\"하! 미글스 씨의 친구분이시군요!\" 그 공공의 죄인은 골똘히 머릿속으로 짜 맞추던 생각에서 벗어나며 외치고는 손을 내밀었다. \"반갑습니다, 선생. 성함을 잊었더라도 양해해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"기꺼이요. 유명한 이름은 아니니까요. 배너클은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니지요.\" 대니얼이 웃으며 말했다. \"이제 생각났습니다. 클레넘이지요. 안녕하십니까, 클레넘 씨?\"\n\n\"바라건대,\" 아서가 둘이 함께 걸으며 말했다, \"우리가 같은 곳으로 가고 있는 게 아닐까 합니다, 도이스 씨.\"\n\n\"트위크넘 말씀이지요?\" 대니얼이 받았다. \"그렇다니 반갑군요.\"\n\n둘은 곧 꽤 가까워졌고, 갖가지 대화로 길을 가볍게 했다. 그 재간 있는 죄인은 무척 겸손하고 분별 있는 사람이었다. 비록 수수한 사람이었으나, 발상에서 독창적이고 대담한 것을, 실행에서 끈기 있고 세밀한 것과 결합하는 데 너무도 익숙해진 터라, 결코 평범한 사람일 수는 없었다. 처음에는 그가 자기 이야기를 하게 만들기가 어려웠는데, 아서가 그쪽으로 다가가면 그는 슬쩍 인정만 했다. 아, 그렇지요, 이 일도 했고 저 일도 했으며, 이런 것은 자기가 만든 것이고 저런 것은 자기가 발견한 것이지만, 그게 자기 직업이지 않습니까, 그저 직업일 뿐이라고 하면서 말이다. 그러다가 차차 동행이 자기 이야기에 진정으로 관심을 가졌음을 확신하게 되자, 그는 솔직하게 마음을 열었다. 그러고 보니 그는 북부 지방 대장장이의 아들로, 본디 홀어머니에 의해 자물쇠 장인에게 도제로 보내졌다. 자물쇠 장인 밑에서 '작은 것 몇 가지를 고안해 낸' 덕에 선물 하나와 함께 도제 계약에서 풀려났고, 그 선물 덕분에 어느 현업 기술자에게 자신을 맡기고 싶다는 간절한 소망을 이룰 수 있었다. 그 밑에서 그는 칠 년 동안 고되게 일하고, 고되게 배우고, 고되게 살았다. 도제 기간이 끝나자 다시 칠팔 년을 주급을 받으며 '작업장에서 일했고', 그러고는 클라이드 강가로 가서 거기서 다시 육칠 년 동안 공부하고, 줄질하고, 망치질하며, 이론적 지식과 실무적 지식을 두루 갈고닦았다. 그곳에서 리옹으로 와 달라는 제안을 받아 수락했고, 리옹에서는 독일로 갈 일을 얻었으며, 독일에서는 상트페테르부르크로 와 달라는 제안을 받아, 그곳에서는 정말이지 아주 잘 해냈다 — 그 어느 때보다도 잘했다. 그러나 그는 당연히 자기 나라를 더 좋아하는 마음을, 그곳에서 명성을 얻고, 다른 어디보다도 그곳에서 할 수 있는 봉사를 하고 싶다는 바람을 품었다. 그리하여 그는 고국으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 고국에서 사업을 차렸고, 발명하고 실행하며 차근차근 길을 헤쳐 나가다가, 십수 년에 걸친 끊임없는 청원과 봉직 끝에 위대한 영국 명예 군단 — 에두름국에 퇴짜 맞은 자들의 군단에 입적되었고, 위대한 영국 공로 훈장 — 배너클가와 스틸트스토킹가의 난맥 훈장을 수여받았다.\n\n\"참으로 안타까운 일입니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"도이스 씨, 당신께서 애초에 그쪽으로 생각을 돌리셨다는 것이 말입니다.\"\n\n\"맞는 말씀입니다, 선생, 어느 정도까지는요. 하지만 사람이 어쩌겠습니까? 불행히도 나라에 쓸모 있는 무언가를 고안해 냈다면, 그게 이끄는 대로 따라가야 하는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"차라리 그냥 흘려보내는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 수가 없지요.\" 도이스가 생각에 잠긴 미소를 지으며 고개를 저었다. \"그건 묻어 두라고 머리에 들어온 게 아닙니다. 쓸모 있게 만들라고 들어온 거지요. 사람은 끝까지 목숨을 위해 치열하게 다투어야 한다는 조건으로 목숨을 부지하는 법입니다. 발명을 품은 사람도 누구나 같은 조건으로 그것을 품는 거고요.\"\n\n\"그 말씀은,\" 아서가 이 조용한 동행에 대한 존경이 점점 자라나며 말했다, \"지금에 이르러서도 결국 낙담하지는 않으셨다는 거군요?\"\n\n\"낙담했다 한들, 그럴 자격이 없지요.\" 상대가 받았다. \"그 물건은 예나 지금이나 한결같이 옳으니까요.\"\n\n잠시 말없이 걷던 끝에, 클레넘은 대화의 직접적인 화제를 바꾸되 너무 급작스럽지 않게 바꾸려고, 도이스 씨에게 사업의 근심을 얼마간 덜어 줄 동업자가 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 그가 답했다. \"지금은 없습니다. 처음 사업을 시작했을 때는 있었지요, 좋은 사람이었습니다. 하지만 몇 해 전에 죽었고, 그를 잃은 뒤로는 다른 사람을 들인다는 생각에 좀처럼 마음이 가지 않아서, 그의 몫을 내가 사들였고 그 이후로 줄곧 혼자 해 왔습니다. 그리고 이런 것도 있지요.\" 그가 잠시 멈춰 서서 눈에 선량한 웃음을 띠고, 엄지가 유달리 잘 구부러지는 오른손을 쥔 채 클레넘의 팔에 얹으며 말했다. \"발명가는 사업가가 될 수 없는 법이거든요, 아시다시피.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 사업가들이 그렇게들 말하지요.\" 그가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 대놓고 웃으며 답했다. \"왜 우리같이 불운한 부류가 상식이 없다고들 여기는지 모르겠지만, 으레 그러려니 하는 게 보통이에요. 이 세상에서 내가 가진 가장 좋은 친구, 저쪽에 계신 우리의 훌륭한 친구분조차,\" 도이스가 트위크넘 쪽을 가리키며 끄덕였다, \"나를 제 한 몸 제대로 건사하지 못하는 사람인 양 일종의 보호를 베푸신다니까요, 아시겠어요?\"\n\n아서 클레넘은 그 선량한 웃음에 함께 끼어들지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 묘사가 사실임을 알아본 까닭이었다.\n\n\"그래서 나는 사업가이면서 발명이라곤 일절 저지르지 않는 동업자를 두어야겠다고 보는 겁니다.\" 대니얼 도이스가 모자를 벗어 이마를 손으로 쓸며 말했다. \"그저 세상의 통념을 존중해서라도, 또 공장의 신용을 지키기 위해서라도 말입니다. 내가 공장을 운영해 온 방식이 그리 태만하거나 어수선했다고 그가 보지는 않을 겁니다. 하지만 그건 그가 — 그게 누구든 간에 — 할 말이지, 내가 할 말은 아니지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 아직 그 사람을 고르지는 않으셨군요?\"\n\n\"네, 선생, 못 골랐습니다. 그저 한 사람을 들이기로 막 결심했을 따름이에요. 사실 예전보다 할 일이 많아졌고, 나이가 들어가니 공장만으로도 벅찹니다. 장부와 서신, 또 책임자가 꼭 가야 하는 해외 출장 같은 걸 다 하자니 도저히 혼자서는 못 하겠어요. 오늘과 월요일 아침 사이에 짬이 반 시간이라도 나면, 그 문제를 어떻게 풀어 가는 게 가장 좋을지 나의 — 나의 보모이자 보호자와 의논해 볼 생각입니다.\" 도이스가 다시 눈에 웃음을 띠고 말했다. \"그분은 사업에 영민한 사람이고, 좋은 수련도 거치셨지요.\"\n\n그 뒤로 둘은 여러 가지 화제로 이야기를 나누다 마침내 여정의 끝에 다다랐다. 대니얼 도이스에게는 차분하면서도 드러내지 않는 자기 지탱의 태도가 엿보였다 — 집안의 대양에 우글거리는 모든 배너클가 사람들에도 불구하고 참된 것은 참된 것으로 남게 마련이며, 설령 그 바다마저 말라 버려도 그저 진실 그대로일 뿐 더도 덜도 아니라는 고요한 앎이었는데, 관청식 종류의 위대함은 아니었으나 그 안에는 일종의 위대함이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n그는 그 집을 잘 알았으므로, 가장 좋게 보이는 길로 아서를 안내했다. 그것은 강가 길에 자리한 매력적인 곳이었고(다소 별난 데가 있었지만 그게 흠은 아니었다), 미글스 가족의 거처라면 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 집이었다. 집은 정원 안에 서 있었는데, 그 정원은 한 해의 오월에, 펫이 지금 제 생애의 오월에 그러하듯 싱그럽고 아름다웠음에 틀림없었다. 또 미글스 씨 부부가 펫을 지켜 주듯, 집은 보기 좋은 멋진 나무들과 넓게 우거진 상록수들로 든든히 보호받고 있었다. 그것은 옛 벽돌집을 고쳐 만든 것으로, 일부는 아예 헐어 버렸고 다른 일부는 지금의 별장으로 바꾼 것이었다. 그래서 미글스 씨 부부를 대신할 정정한 노년의 부분이 있었고, 펫을 대신할 젊고 그림처럼 어여쁜 부분이 있었다. 심지어 나중에 덧붙인 온실 하나가 집에 기대어 몸을 숨기고 있었는데, 짙게 물든 유리 탓에 빛깔이 아리송했고, 더 투명한 부분들은 햇살에 번쩍여 어떤 때는 불 같고 어떤 때는 무해한 물방울 같았으니, 태티코럼을 대신할 만했다. 시야에는 평온한 강과 나룻배가 들어왔는데, 그것은 모든 거주자에게 이렇게 타이르는 듯했다. 젊든 늙든, 격정에 찼든 잔잔하든, 안달하든 흡족하든, 그대들이여, 물길은 늘 이렇게 흐른다오. 가슴이 어떤 불협화음으로 부풀어 오르든, 잔물결 이는 물은 늘 나룻배 뱃머리에서 한결같은 가락을 연주한다오. 해마다, 배가 떠밀려 가는 정도는 이만큼, 시냇물 흐르는 속도는 한 시간에 몇 마일, 여기엔 골풀이, 저기엔 백합이, 무엇 하나 불확실하거나 불안한 것 없이, 꾸준히 흘러가는 이 길 위에서는 그러하다오. 한데 그대들은, 흘러가는 그대들의 시간이라는 길 위에서, 이리도 변덕스럽고 산란하구려.\n\n문간의 종이 채 울리기도 전에 미글스 씨가 그들을 맞으러 나왔다. 미글스 씨가 채 나오기도 전에 미글스 부인이 나왔다. 미글스 부인이 채 나오기도 전에 펫이 나왔다. 펫이 채 나오기도 전에 태티코럼이 나왔다. 손님이 이보다 더 환대받은 적은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 우리는 이렇게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"클레넘 씨, 우리 집의 울타리 안에 틀어박혀 있답니다. 마치 다시는 넓혀 나갈 — 그러니까 여행할 — 생각이 없는 사람처럼 말이지요. 마르세유와는 다르지요, 안 그래요? 여기선 알롱하고 마르송할 일이 없으니!\"\n\n\"과연 다른 종류의 아름다움이군요!\" 클레넘이 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이거 참!\" 미글스 씨가 흡족하게 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다. \"검역소에 갇혀 있던 것도 유달리 즐거운 일이었지요, 안 그렇습니까? 저, 내가 그때로 돌아갔으면 하고 종종 바란다는 거 아십니까? 우리 정말 굉장한 일행이었지요.\"\n\n이것이 미글스 씨의 변함없는 버릇이었다. 여행하는 동안에는 늘 모든 것에 트집을 잡고, 여행하지 않을 때는 늘 그리로 돌아가고 싶어 하는 것 말이다.\n\n\"지금이 여름이라면,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"당신을 위해서도, 또 당신이 이곳을 가장 좋을 때 보았으면 하는 마음에서도 여름이었으면 싶은데, 그랬다면 새소리 때문에 제 말소리조차 듣기 어려웠을 겁니다. 우린 실용적인 사람들이라 누구도 새를 쫓지 못하게 하지요. 그래서 새들도, 역시 실용적인 녀석들인지라, 떼를 지어 우리 곁으로 몰려든답니다. 당신을 만나 정말 기쁩니다, 클레넘(괜찮으시다면 '씨' 자는 떼겠습니다). 진심으로 장담하건대, 정말 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"이토록 기분 좋은 환대를 받아 본 적이 없습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다 — 그러다 리틀 도릿이 자기 방에서 그에게 했던 말이 떠올라, 정직하게 덧붙였다. \"단 한 번을 빼고는요 — 우리가 지중해를 내려다보며 마지막으로 거닐었던 그때 이후로 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 미글스 씨가 받았다. \"그게 참 경치 한번 볼만한 곳이었지요, 안 그렇습니까? 군사 정부야 바라지 않지만, 이 동네에도 가끔은 알롱하고 마르송하는 게 — 그저 살짝이라도 — 있어도 나쁘진 않을 텐데요. 여긴 지독하게 고요하거든요.\"\n\n이렇게 자기 은신처의 한적한 정취에 미심쩍게 고개를 저으며 찬사를 보내고는, 미글스 씨가 앞장서서 집 안으로 들어갔다. 집은 꼭 알맞을 만큼 컸지 그 이상은 아니었고, 바깥만큼이나 안도 어여뻤으며, 완벽하게 잘 정돈되어 안락했다. 가족의 떠돌이 습성의 흔적이 천을 씌운 액자며 가구, 싸 둔 휘장 따위에서 엿보였다. 그러나 그들이 없는 동안에도 별장을 늘, 마치 모레쯤 돌아올 것처럼 그대로 유지해 두는 것이 미글스 씨의 별난 고집 가운데 하나임을 쉬이 알 수 있었다. 갖가지 원정에서 그러모은 물건들이 어찌나 잡다하게 많은지, 마치 마음씨 좋은 해적의 거처 같았다. 중부 이탈리아에서 온 골동품들이 있었는데, 그 분야에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 현대 업체가 만든 것이었고, 이집트(어쩌면 버밍엄)에서 온 미라 조각들, 베네치아의 곤돌라 모형, 스위스의 마을 모형, 헤르쿨라네움과 폼페이에서 온 모자이크 포석 부스러기들 — 마치 화석이 된 다진 송아지 고기 같았다 — 무덤에서 나온 재와 베수비오에서 나온 용암, 에스파냐 부채, 스페치아 밀짚모자, 무어식 슬리퍼, 토스카나 머리핀, 카라라 조각, 트라스테베레 스카프, 제노바 벨벳과 금세공, 나폴리 산호, 로마 카메오, 제네바 보석, 아랍 등불, 교황이 손수 두루 축복해 준 묵주, 그리고 무한히 다양한 잡동사니가 있었다. 무수한 장소의, 닮기도 하고 닮지 않기도 한 풍경화들이 있었다. 그리고 작은 그림방 하나가 있었는데, 거기엔 여느 끈적끈적한 옛 성인들 몇이 모셔져 있었으니, 채찍 끈 같은 힘줄에, 넵투누스 같은 머리칼에, 문신 같은 주름에, 어찌나 두껍게 광택제를 발랐는지 거룩한 인물 하나하나가 파리잡이 구실을 하여, 요즘 속된 말로 '산 채로 잡아라 오' 따위가 되어 버렸다. 이렇게 사들인 그림들에 대해 미글스 씨는 늘 같은 식으로 말했다. 자기는 그저 제 마음에 드는 것 말고는 볼 줄 모른다고, 헐값에 주워 온 것인데 사람들이 제법 훌륭하다고 여기더라고 했다. 적어도 이 분야를 좀 안다 할 만한 어느 사람이 단언하기를, '독서하는 현자'(담요를 두르고, 백조 솜털로 수염을 삼고, 진한 파이 껍질처럼 온통 금이 그물처럼 간, 유난히 기름진 노신사)는 훌륭한 게르치노 작품이라고 했다. 저쪽의 세바스티아노 델 피옴보로 말하자면, 직접 판단하시라고 했다. 그의 후기 화풍이 아니라면, 그럼 대체 누구 거란 말인가? 티치아노라면, 맞을 수도 아닐 수도 있지만 — 아마 그가 손만 좀 댔을지도 모른다고 했다. 대니얼 도이스가 아마 손도 안 댔을 거라고 하자, 미글스 씨는 그 말을 짐짓 못 들은 척했다.\n\n전리품을 죄다 보여 주고 나서, 미글스 씨는 그들을 잔디밭이 내다보이는 자신의 아늑한 방으로 데려갔는데, 그 방은 일부는 화장실처럼, 일부는 사무실처럼 꾸며져 있었고, 그 안의 일종의 카운터 책상 위에는 금의 무게를 다는 놋쇠 저울 한 쌍과, 돈을 퍼내는 삽 하나가 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 여기 있습니다.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"삼십오 년 내리 나는 이 두 물건 뒤에 서 있었지요. 그땐 지금 집에 눌러앉을 생각 안 하듯 쏘다닐 생각 따윈 안 했답니다. 은행을 아주 떠날 때, 이것들을 달라고 청해서 가지고 나왔지요. 미리 말씀드리는 겁니다. 안 그러면 당신은 내가 (펫이 말하듯) 〈검은새 스물네 마리〉라는 시 속의 임금님처럼 회계실에 앉아 제 돈이나 세고 있는 줄 아실 테니까요.\"\n\n클레넘의 눈은 벽에 걸린 자연스러운 그림 한 점으로 흘러갔는데, 팔짱을 낀 어여쁜 여자아이 둘을 그린 것이었다. \"그래요, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다. \"저기 둘 다 있지요. 한 십칠 년쯤 전에 그린 겁니다. 어멈한테 종종 말하듯, 그땐 갓난쟁이들이었어요.\"\n\n\"이름이 어떻게 되지요?\" 아서가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지! 펫 말고 다른 이름은 들어 본 적이 없으시겠군요. 펫의 이름은 미니랍니다. 언니는 릴리이고요.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 저 둘 중 하나가 저라는 걸 아시겠어요?\" 펫 자신이 이제 문간에 서서 물었다.\n\n\"둘 다 당신이라고 생각했을 법합니다. 둘 다 여전히 그토록 당신을 닮았으니까요. 정말이지,\" 클레넘이 아리따운 실물에서 그림으로, 다시 실물로 눈길을 옮기며 말했다. \"지금도 어느 쪽이 당신 초상이 아니라고는 말씀드릴 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"여보, 그 말 들었소?\" 미글스 씨가 딸을 뒤따라온 아내에게 외쳤다. \"늘 이렇다니까요, 클레넘. 아무도 분간을 못 해요. 당신 왼편에 있는 아이가 펫입니다.\"\n\n그 그림은 마침 거울 가까이에 있었다. 아서가 다시 그것을 바라보다가, 거울에 비친 모습으로, 문밖을 지나가던 태티코럼이 멈춰 서서 안에서 오가는 말에 귀를 기울이고는, 얼굴에 노여움과 경멸의 찌푸림을 띤 채 지나가는 것을 보았다. 그 찌푸림이 그녀의 아름다움을 추함으로 바꾸어 놓았다.\n\n\"자, 이런!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"먼 길을 걸어오셨으니 부츠를 벗고 싶으실 게요. 여기 대니얼로 말하자면, 부츠 벗기개를 보여 주지 않는 한 자기 부츠를 벗을 생각조차 안 할 양반이지요.\"\n\n\"왜 안 한다는 거지요?\" 대니얼이 클레넘에게 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"아! 자넨 생각할 게 너무 많잖나.\" 미글스 씨가 그의 어깨를 두드리며 받았다. 마치 그의 약점을 무슨 일이 있어도 제멋대로 내버려 두어서는 안 된다는 듯이. \"숫자며, 바퀴며, 톱니며, 지렛대며, 나사며, 실린더며, 그밖에 천 가지 것들 말이야.\"\n\n\"내 직업에서는,\" 대니얼이 재미있어하며 말했다. \"큰 것이 으레 작은 것을 포함하는 법이라네. 하지만 괜찮네, 괜찮아! 자네가 좋다면 나도 좋으이.\"\n\n클레넘은 제 방에서 벽난로 곁에 앉으며, 이 정직하고 다정하며 정 깊은 미글스 씨의 가슴속에, 에두름국이라는 큰 나무로 자라난 그 겨자씨가 현미경으로나 보일 만큼이라도 들어 있는 것은 아닐까 하고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 대니얼 도이스보다 자기가 전반적으로 더 우월하다는 그 묘한 감각은, 도이스의 인품에 있는 무엇 때문이라기보다 그저 그가 무언가를 창안하는 사람이요 여느 사람들이 다니는 길에서 벗어난 사람이라는 단순한 사실에 근거를 둔 듯했는데, 바로 그 점이 그런 생각을 떠올리게 했다. 한 시간 뒤 저녁 식사를 하러 내려가기까지 그 생각이 그를 사로잡았을지도 모르나, 그에게는 곰곰이 따져 볼 또 다른 물음이 있었으니, 그것은 마르세유에서 검역을 받기도 전부터 그의 마음속에 있었고, 이제 다시 되돌아와 그를 몹시도 다그쳤다. 다름 아닌 이런 물음이었다. 펫과 사랑에 빠지도록 자신을 내버려 두어도 좋을 것인가?\n\n그는 그녀의 갑절 나이였다. (그는 한 다리를 다른 다리 위로 바꿔 꼬고 다시 계산해 보았으나, 그 합계를 그보다 적게 낼 수는 없었다.) 그는 그녀의 갑절 나이였다. 그래! 그는 겉보기로 젊었고, 건강과 기력으로도 젊었으며, 마음으로도 젊었다. 마흔에 늙었다고 할 사람은 분명 없으며, 많은 남자들이 그 나이에 이르도록 결혼할 형편이 못 되거나 결혼하지 않기도 했다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 문제는 그가 그 점을 어찌 생각하느냐가 아니라 그녀가 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐였다.\n\n그는 미글스 씨가 자기에게 무르익은 호의를 품으려는 듯하다고 믿었고, 자기도 미글스 씨와 그 선량한 부인에게 진심 어린 호의를 품고 있음을 알았다. 그토록 애지중지하는 이 아름다운 외동딸을 어떤 남편에게든 내어 준다는 것이, 그들의 사랑으로서는 아마 여태껏 차마 헤아려 볼 용기조차 내지 못한 시련이 되리라는 것을 그는 내다볼 수 있었다. 그러나 그녀가 아름답고 사랑스럽고 매혹적일수록, 그들은 늘 그 시련에 다가가야 할 필연에 더욱 가까이 놓일 수밖에 없었다. 그리고 다른 남자가 아니라 자신에게라고 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?\n\n여기까지 이르렀을 때, 문제는 그들이 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐가 아니라 그녀가 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐라는 데에 다시 생각이 미쳤다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 물러서기 잘하는 사람이었고, 자신에게 부족한 점이 많다는 자각이 있었다. 그리하여 그는 마음속으로 아름다운 미니의 장점은 한껏 추켜올리고 자신의 장점은 깎아내렸으므로, 이 대목에 자신을 못 박아 두자 그의 희망은 시들기 시작했다. 저녁 식사를 위해 채비를 갖추며, 그는 마침내 결론에 이르렀다. 펫과 사랑에 빠지도록 자신을 내버려 두지 않겠다는 것이었다.\n\n둥근 식탁에 단 다섯 사람뿐이었으나, 분위기는 정말이지 무척 즐거웠다. 그들은 떠올릴 만한 장소며 사람들이 어찌나 많았는지, 또 다 함께 어찌나 격의 없고 명랑했는지(대니얼 도이스는 카드놀이를 흥미롭게 구경하는 사람처럼 빠져 있거나, 마침 적절한 때엔 자기 나름의 빈틈없는 자잘한 경험담을 끼워 넣곤 했다), 마치 스무 번은 같이 어울려 본 사이라 해도 서로를 이만큼은 알지 못했으리라 싶을 정도였다.\n\n\"그런데 웨이드 양은,\" 미글스 씨가 함께 여행했던 여러 사람을 떠올린 끝에 말했다. \"누구 웨이드 양을 본 사람 있나?\"\n\n\"제가 봤어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다.\n\n그녀는 어린 주인 아가씨가 가져오라 한 작은 망토를 가지고 와, 아가씨에게 몸을 굽혀 그것을 걸쳐 주던 참이었는데, 그때 검은 눈을 들어 이 뜻밖의 대답을 한 것이었다.\n\n\"태티!\" 어린 주인 아가씨가 외쳤다. \"네가 웨이드 양을 봤다고? — 어디서?\"\n\n\"여기서요, 아가씨.\" 태티코럼이 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n태티코럼의 못마땅한 눈길은, 클레넘이 보기에, '제 눈으로요!'라고 답하는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 말로 한 대답은 이것뿐이었다. \"교회 근처에서 그분을 만났어요.\"\n\n\"거기서 대체 뭘 하고 있었을까!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"교회에 가려던 건 아닐 텐데 말이야.\"\n\n\"먼저 제게 편지를 보내셨어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 태티!\" 주인 아가씨가 중얼거렸다. \"손 좀 치워 줘. 마치 다른 누가 날 만지는 것 같단 말이야!\"\n\n그녀는 그 말을 빠르게, 저도 모르게 했으나, 반쯤은 장난스러웠고, 다음 순간 웃음을 터뜨리는 응석받이 아이가 했을 법한 것보다 더 짜증스럽거나 거슬리지는 않았다. 태티코럼은 도톰한 붉은 입술을 꽉 다물고는, 두 팔을 가슴 위로 모아 꼈다.\n\n\"선생님, 웨이드 양이 제게 무슨 편지를 했는지 아시고 싶으세요?\" 그녀가 미글스 씨를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"음, 태티코럼.\" 미글스 씨가 받았다. \"네가 물으니 하는 말이다만, 여기 모인 사람들 다 친구 사이니, 정 그러고 싶거든 말해도 괜찮겠지.\"\n\n\"그분은 우리가 여행할 때, 선생님 댁이 어딘지 아셨어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 영 — 영 —\"\n\n\"영 기분이 좋지 않은 걸 보셨다는 거냐, 태티코럼?\" 미글스 씨가 그 검은 눈을 향해 고개를 저으며 조용히 조심스레 일러 주었다. \"잠깐 진정해라 — 스물다섯까지 세어 보렴, 태티코럼.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 입술을 꾹 다물고는 길고 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"그래서 제게 편지로 이러셨어요. 혹시라도 제가 마음을 다치거나,\" 그녀가 어린 주인 아가씨를 내려다보았다. \"괴로움을 겪는다 싶으면,\" 그녀가 다시 아가씨를 내려다보았다. \"그분께 가도 좋고, 사려 깊은 대접을 받을 수 있다고요. 잘 생각해 보고, 교회 옆에서 말을 나누면 된다고요. 그래서 거기 가서 고맙다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"태티.\" 어린 주인 아가씨가 상대가 잡을 수 있도록 어깨 너머로 손을 올리며 말했다. \"헤어질 때 웨이드 양이 날 거의 무섭게 했었거든. 지금 그분이 내가 알지도 못한 새 그렇게 가까이 와 있었다고 생각하니 영 마음이 안 좋아. 태티, 얘!\"\n\n태티는 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다.\n\n\"이런?\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다. \"다시 스물다섯까지 세어 봐, 태티코럼.\"\n\n그녀가 열둘쯤 세었을까 싶을 때, 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 어루만지는 손에 입술을 갖다 댔다. 그 손이 그녀의 뺨을 토닥였고, 그러면서 손 주인의 어여쁜 곱슬머리를 매만졌다. 그러고는 태티코럼이 물러갔다.\n\n\"자, 보세요.\" 미글스 씨가 오른편의 자동 회전대를 돌려 설탕을 제 쪽으로 빙그르르 가져오며 부드럽게 말했다. \"저 아이는 실용적인 사람들 틈에 있지 않았다면 길을 잃고 신세를 망쳤을 수도 있는 애예요. 어멈과 나는, 오로지 실용적이기에 압니다만, 우리가 펫에게 그토록 푹 빠져 있는 걸 보면 저 아이의 본성 전체가 곤두서는 때가 있다는 걸 알지요. 가엾게도, 저 아이한테는 푹 빠져 줄 아비 어미가 없었으니까요. 저 불행한 아이가, 그 모든 격정과 반항을 품은 채로, 일요일에 십계명 중 제오계를 들을 때 어떤 심정일지 생각하면 영 안쓰럽습니다. 그럴 때마다 나는 이렇게 외치고 싶어진다니까요. 교회야, 스물다섯까지 세어 보렴, 태티코럼.\"\n\n미글스 씨에게는 자동 회전대 말고도, 말 못 하는 회전대와 달리 말을 하는 시중꾼이 둘 더 있었으니, 발그레한 얼굴에 초롱초롱한 눈을 한 두 하녀였다. 이들은 식탁 장식에서 매우 화려한 한 부분을 이루었다. \"왜 안 되겠습니까, 그렇잖아요?\" 이 점에 대해 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"내가 늘 어멈한테 말하듯, 기왕 무엇이든 둘 거면, 보기에 예쁜 걸 두면 안 될 까닭이 무어란 말입니까?\"\n\n티킷 부인이라는 어떤 사람이 — 가족이 집에 있을 때는 요리사이자 가정부였고, 가족이 떠나 있을 때는 가정부 노릇만 했다 — 이 살림을 마저 채웠다. 미글스 씨는 티킷 부인이 맡은 일의 성격상 지금은 손님 앞에 내보일 수 없는 처지라 유감이지만, 내일은 새 손님에게 소개할 수 있기를 바란다고 했다. 그녀는 이 별장의 중요한 일부라고, 자기 친구들은 다 그녀를 안다고 했다. 저기 구석에 걸린 게 그녀의 초상이라고 했다. 가족이 떠나면 그녀는 늘 그 초상에 그려진 대로 비단 가운을 차려입고 새카만 곱슬머리 가발을 쓰고는(부엌에 있을 땐 머리가 불그스름한 잿빛이었다), 아침 식사실에 자리를 잡고, 안경을 부캔 박사의 〈가정 의학〉의 특정한 두 쪽 사이에 끼워 둔 채, 그들이 돌아올 때까지 온종일 블라인드 너머를 내다보며 앉아 있었다. 그들이 아무리 오래 떠나 있어도 티킷 부인을 그 블라인드 앞 자리에서 떠나게 하거나 부캔 박사의 시중을 마다하게 할 만한 어떤 설득도 고안해 낼 수 없으리라고들 했다. 그러나 미글스 씨는, 그 박식한 의사의 노작을 그녀가 평생토록 단 한 마디라도 들춰 본 적이 없으리라고 굳게 믿었다.\n\n저녁에는 옛날식 러버 카드놀이를 했다. 펫은 아버지의 패를 들여다보며 앉아 있거나, 피아노 앞에서 이따금 혼자 흥얼거리며 노래했다. 그녀는 응석받이로 자란 아이였다. 그러나 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠는가? 이토록 순순하고 아름다운 사람과 오래 함께하면서, 누가 그 사랑스러운 영향에 굴복하지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 누가 이 집에서 저녁 한때를 보내며, 그 방에 그녀가 있다는 것만으로도 풍기는 그 우아함과 매력에 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 있었겠는가? 위층에서 그가 이미 내린 최종 결론에도 불구하고, 이것이 클레넘의 생각이었다.\n\n그러다 보니 그는 카드 규칙을 어기고 말았다. \"아니, 무슨 생각을 하고 계신 거요, 선생?\" 그의 짝패였던 미글스 씨가 놀라 물었다. \"죄송합니다. 아무것도 아닙니다.\" 클레넘이 받았다. \"다음번엔 뭐든 생각을 좀 하세요. 그래야 좋은 양반이지.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. 펫은 웃으며 그가 웨이드 양 생각을 하고 있었던 거라고 했다. \"왜 하필 웨이드 양이냐, 펫?\" 아버지가 물었다. \"글쎄 말이에요, 왜일까요!\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. 펫은 살짝 얼굴을 붉히고는 다시 피아노로 갔다.\n\n밤이 깊어 자리를 파할 무렵, 아서는 도이스가 주인에게 내일 아침 식사 전에 반 시간쯤 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 묻는 것을 우연히 들었다. 주인이 흔쾌히 응하자, 아서는 그 화제에 자기도 한마디 보탤 것이 있어 잠시 뒤에 남았다.\n\n\"미글스 씨,\" 둘만 남게 되자 그가 말했다. \"제게 곧장 런던으로 가라고 권하셨던 때를 기억하십니까?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 기억하지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그때 제게 필요했던 또 다른 좋은 조언을 해 주셨던 것도요?\"\n\n\"그게 얼마나 값진 거였는지야 내가 말하지 않겠습니다만,\" 미글스 씨가 답했다. \"물론 우리가 함께 무척 즐겁고 흉허물 없이 지냈던 건 기억하지요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신 조언대로 행했습니다. 그리고 여러 까닭으로 제게 괴로웠던 직무를 떨쳐 버린 터라, 이제 저 자신과 제가 가진 얼마간의 재력을 다른 일에 바치고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"옳은 말씀이오! 아무리 일러도 지나치지 않지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"한데 오늘 내려오면서 알게 되었는데, 당신 친구분인 도이스 씨가 사업에 동업자를 찾고 계시더군요 — 그분의 기계 지식의 동업자가 아니라, 거기서 생기는 사업을 가장 이롭게 굴려 가는 방도와 수단에서의 동업자 말입니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 미글스 씨가 주머니에 손을 찌른 채, 저울과 삽에 어울리던 그 옛 사업가다운 표정을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"도이스 씨가 대화 중에 지나가는 말로, 그런 동업자를 찾는 문제로 당신의 귀한 조언을 받으려 한다고 하시더군요. 만일 우리의 생각과 처지가 조금이라도 맞아떨어질 성싶다 보시거든, 제가 어떤 형편에 있는지 그분께 알려 주시면 좋겠습니다. 물론 저는 세부 사정을 모르고 드리는 말씀이라, 양쪽 모두에게 맞지 않을 수도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요.\" 미글스 씨가 저울과 삽에 어울리는 신중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그건 숫자와 셈의 문제일 테니 —\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소, 그렇고말고요.\" 미글스 씨가 저울과 삽에 어울리는 산술적 견실함으로 말했다.\n\n\"— 그리고 도이스 씨가 응하시고 당신이 좋게 보신다면, 저는 기꺼이 이 일에 뛰어들겠습니다. 그러니 지금으로서는 이 일을 당신 손에 맡기도록 허락해 주신다면, 큰 신세를 지는 셈이 되겠습니다.\"\n\n\"클레넘, 그 소임을 기꺼이 맡지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 당신이 사업가로서 당연히 유보해 두신 어느 대목도 미리 넘겨짚지 않고, 이 일에서 뭔가가 나올 법하다고 솔직히 말씀드리겠습니다. 한 가지만은 완벽히 확신하셔도 좋습니다. 대니얼은 정직한 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 확신하기에, 곧장 마음을 정하고 당신께 말씀드린 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 그를 이끌어야 합니다, 아시겠어요. 키를 잡아 주고, 방향을 잡아 줘야 해요. 그는 좀 별난 부류라서요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했는데, 그가 새로운 일을 하고 새로운 길을 간다는 것 이상의 뜻은 분명 아니었다. \"하지만 햇빛만큼이나 정직한 사람이니, 그럼 안녕히 주무세요!\"\n\n클레넘은 제 방으로 돌아가 다시 벽난로 앞에 앉아, 펫과 사랑에 빠지지 않기로 결심한 것이 다행이라고 마음을 굳혔다. 그녀는 너무도 아름답고, 너무도 사랑스러우며, 그 부드러운 천성과 순결한 마음에 주어지는 어떤 참된 인상이든 받아들여, 그것을 전하는 행운을 누릴 남자를 세상 모든 남자 가운데 가장 운 좋고 부러운 사람으로 만들어 줄 만한 사람이었으므로, 그는 그런 결론에 이른 것이 정말이지 다행이라 여겼다.\n\n그러나 이것은 도리어 정반대의 결론에 이를 만한 이유가 될 수도 있었으므로, 그는 그 주제를 마음속으로 조금 더 따라가 보았다. 어쩌면 자신을 정당화하기 위해서였으리라.\n\n'어떤 남자가 있다고 치자.' 그의 생각은 이렇게 흘러갔다. '스무 해쯤 전부터 성년이 된 남자, 어린 시절의 사정 탓에 자신감이 부족한 남자, 살아온 행로로 보아 다소 무거운 남자, 자기 곁에 마음을 누그러뜨려 줄 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 먼 지방에 오래 있던 탓에 남들에게서는 감탄하던 그 자잘한 사람을 끄는 자질들이 자기에게는 모자란다는 걸 아는 남자, 그녀에게 소개해 줄 다정한 누이도 없는 남자, 그녀를 알릴 만한 마음 맞는 보금자리도 없는 남자, 이 땅에서는 이방인인 남자, 이런 결함들을 조금이라도 보상할 재산도 없는 남자, 자기에게 유리한 거라곤 정직한 사랑과 옳게 행하려는 일반적인 바람밖에 없는 남자 — 그런 남자가 이 집에 와서, 이 매혹적인 아가씨에게 사로잡히고 말아, 그녀를 차지하기를 바랄 수 있다고 스스로를 설득한다면, 그 얼마나 어리석은 약점이겠는가!'\n\n그는 가만히 창문을 열고 고요한 강을 내다보았다. 해마다, 나룻배가 떠밀려 가는 정도는 이만큼, 시냇물 흐르는 속도는 한 시간에 몇 마일, 여기엔 골풀이, 저기엔 백합이, 무엇 하나 불확실하거나 불안한 것 없었다.\n\n어찌하여 그가 속을 끓이거나 마음 아파해야 한단 말인가? 그가 그려 본 것은 그 자신의 약점이 아니었다. 그것은 누구의 약점도 아니었고, 그가 아는 한 어느 누구의 것도 아니었으니, 어찌하여 그것이 그를 괴롭혀야 한단 말인가? 그런데도 그것은 그를 괴롭혔다. 그리고 그는 생각했다 — 때로 한순간이나마 그런 생각을 안 해 본 사람이 누가 있으랴? — 차라리 저 강물처럼 단조롭게 흘러가, 행복을 느끼지 못하는 대신 고통도 느끼지 못하는 것으로 셈을 맞추는 편이 나을지도 모른다고.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. Nobody’s Rival\n\n아침 식사 전, 아서는 주위를 둘러보려고 밖으로 나가 걸었다.\n아침이 화창하고 한 시간 정도 여유가 있었으므로, 그는 나룻배로 강을 건너 목초지를 가로지르는 오솔길을 따라 거닐었다. 다시 견인로로 돌아왔을 때, 나룻배가 건너편에 있는 것과, 어떤 신사가 그것을 부르며 건너편으로 실려 가기를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n이 신사는 서른이 채 안 되어 보였다. 옷차림이 단정했고, 활기차고 쾌활한 인상에, 다부진 체격과 짙고 풍부한 혈색을 지녔다. 아서가 울타리 디딤대를 넘어 물가로 내려오자, 빈둥거리던 그 사람은 잠시 그를 흘끗 보더니, 다시 발로 돌멩이를 무심히 물속으로 차 넣는 일에 몰두했다. 발뒤꿈치로 돌들을 제자리에서 걷어차 내고, 그것들을 원하는 위치로 몰아넣는 그 방식에는 클레넘이 보기에 어딘가 잔인한 데가 있었다. 우리 대부분은 어떤 사람이 아주 사소한 일을 하는 태도에서 — 꽃을 꺾거나, 장애물을 치우거나, 심지어 감각도 없는 물체를 부수는 것에서 — 다소간 자주 그와 비슷한 인상을 받아 본 적이 있다.\n\n그 신사의 생각은 그의 얼굴이 보여주듯 다른 데 쏠려 있었고, 그래서 그는 자기를 주의 깊게 지켜보는 멋진 뉴펀들랜드 개도, 또 차례차례 던져지는 돌멩이마다 주인의 신호를 받으면 강물로 뛰어들려고 안달하며 지켜보는 그 개도 알아채지 못했다. 그러나 나룻배는 그가 아무 신호도 내리지 않은 채로 건너왔고, 배가 모래톱에 닿자 주인은 개의 목덜미를 잡아 배 안으로 데리고 들어갔다.\n\n\"오늘 아침은 아니야.\" 그가 개에게 말했다. \"물에 흠뻑 젖은 채로는 숙녀들 상대로 어울리지 않거든. 엎드려.\"\n\n클레넘은 그 사람과 개를 따라 배에 올라 자리에 앉았다. 개는 명령받은 대로 했다. 사람은 양손을 주머니에 넣은 채 그대로 서서, 클레넘과 풍경 사이에 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 사람도 개도 건너편에 닿자마자 가볍게 뛰어내려 가 버렸다. 클레넘은 그들에게서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤다.\n\n정원 문으로 통하는 작은 골목길을 따라 걸어 올라갈 때, 교회 시계가 아침 식사 시간을 알렸다. 그가 종을 당기자마자, 담장 안에서 깊고 우렁찬 짖는 소리가 그를 덮쳐 왔다.\n\n'어젯밤엔 개가 없었는데.' 클레넘은 생각했다. 혈색 좋은 하녀들 중 하나가 문을 열어 주었고, 잔디밭에는 그 뉴펀들랜드 개와 그 사람이 있었다.\n\n\"미니 아가씨는 아직 안 내려오셨어요, 신사분들.\" 얼굴을 붉히던 문지기 하녀가 그들이 모두 정원에 함께 모였을 때 말했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 개 주인에게 \"클레넘 씨이십니다, 손님\" 하고는 종종걸음으로 가 버렸다.\n\n\"방금 우리가 마주쳤다니 참 묘하군요, 클레넘 씨.\" 그 사람이 말했다. 그러자 개가 잠잠해졌다. \"제 소개를 하겠습니다 — 헨리 가우언입니다. 멋진 곳이지요. 오늘 아침엔 놀랄 만큼 좋아 보입니다!\"\n\n태도는 거리낌이 없었고, 목소리는 듣기 좋았다. 그러나 그래도 클레넘은, 자기가 펫에게 사랑에 빠지지 않겠다고 그토록 단호히 결심하지 않았더라면, 이 헨리 가우언에게 반감을 품었으리라고 생각했다.\n\n\"여기는 처음이시겠지요?\" 아서가 그곳을 칭찬하고 나자 이 가우언이 말했다.\n\n\"아주 처음입니다. 어제 오후에야 비로소 인연을 맺었지요.\"\n\n\"아! 물론 이게 가장 좋은 모습은 아닙니다. 지난번 그분들이 떠나기 전, 봄에는 매혹적이었답니다. 그때 보셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\"\n\n그토록 자주 떠올린 그 결심만 아니었다면, 클레넘은 이 친절에 대한 답례로 그를 에트나 화산 분화구 속에 처넣고 싶었을지도 모른다.\n\n\"저는 지난 삼 년 동안 여러 상황에서 이곳을 보는 기쁨을 누렸는데, 이곳은 — 낙원입니다.\"\n\n(적어도, 그 현명한 결심을 늘 예외로 친다면) 이곳을 낙원이라 부르는 것은 그의 능란한 뻔뻔함다웠다. 그가 이곳을 낙원이라 부른 것은 다만 그녀가 다가오는 것을 먼저 보았기 때문이고, 그래서 그녀가 들을 수 있는 거리에서 그녀를 천사로 만들어 버린 것이었다. 그에게 저주를!\n\n아, 그녀는 얼마나 환하게 빛나 보였고, 또 얼마나 기뻐 보였던가! 그녀가 개를 어떻게 어루만지고, 또 개가 그녀를 어떻게 알아보는지! 그녀의 얼굴에 짙어진 그 홍조며, 들떠 떨리는 그 태도며, 내리깐 눈이며, 망설이는 행복이 얼마나 많은 것을 말해 주던가! 클레넘이 그녀가 이런 모습을 보이는 것을 언제 본 적이 있었던가? 그가 그녀의 이런 모습을 일찍이 보았거나, 볼 수 있었거나, 보았으리라거나, 보았어야 했을 어떤 이유가 있는 것도 아니고, 그가 일찍이 자기 자신이 그녀의 이런 모습을 보기를 바란 적이 있는 것도 아니었지만 — 그래도 — 그가 언제 그녀가 이러는 것을 알았던 적이 있던가!\n\n그는 그들에게서 조금 떨어져 서 있었다. 이 가우언은 낙원에 관해 이야기하고 나서 그녀에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 개는 커다란 앞발을 그녀의 팔에 올리고 머리를 그녀의 사랑스러운 가슴에 기댔다. 그녀는 웃으며 그들을 반겼고, 개에게 지나치게 살갑게 굴었다, 너무, 너무 지나치게 — 다시 말해, 만약 그녀를 사랑하는 어떤 제삼자가 지켜보고 있었더라면 그렇게 보였을 만큼.\n\n이제 그녀는 몸을 빼내어 클레넘에게로 와서, 자기 손을 그의 손에 얹고 그에게 안녕히 주무셨느냐 인사한 뒤, 우아하게 그의 팔을 잡고 집 안으로 안내받으려는 시늉을 했다. 이에 대해 가우언은 아무런 반대도 하지 않았다. 그렇다, 그는 자기가 너무도 안전하다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n그들 셋이(개까지 세면 넷인데, 그 개는 일행 중에서 가장 거슬리는 자 다음으로 거슬렸다) 아침 식사를 하러 들어왔을 때, 미글스 씨의 쾌활한 얼굴 위에 스쳐 지나가는 구름이 한 자락 끼었다. 그 구름도, 그리로 눈길을 돌리던 미글스 부인의 얼굴에 어린 한 가닥 불안의 기색도, 클레넘의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다.\n\n\"자, 가우언.\" 미글스 씨가 한숨까지 억누르며 말했다. \"오늘 아침 자네에게 세상은 어떻게 돌아가고 있나?\"\n\n\"여느 때와 다름없습니다, 어르신. 라이언하고 저는 주간 방문을 조금도 허비하지 않기로 단단히 마음먹고 일찍 나서서, 제가 지금 본거지로 삼은 킹스턴에서 건너왔습니다. 거기서 스케치를 한두 점 그리고 있거든요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 나루터에서 클레넘 씨를 만나 함께 건너왔다고 이야기했다.\n\n\"가우언 부인은 안녕하시지요, 헨리?\" 미글스 부인이 말했다. (클레넘은 귀를 기울이게 되었다.)\n\n\"어머니는 아주 건강하십니다, 감사합니다.\" (클레넘은 다시 흥미를 잃었다.) \"오늘 댁의 가족 만찬에 한 사람을 더하는 무례를 범했는데, 어르신이나 미글스 씨께 불편이 되지 않기를 바랍니다. 도무지 거절할 수가 없었거든요.\" 그가 미글스 씨를 향하며 설명했다. \"그 젊은 친구가 제게 편지를 보내 자기를 초대해 달라고 청했는데, 연줄이 좋은 집안이라 제가 여기로 데려와도 반대하지 않으시리라 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"그 젊은 친구가 누구인가?\" 미글스 씨가 유난히 흡족한 기색으로 물었다.\n\n\"배너클 집안 사람 중 하나입니다. 타이트 배너클의 아들, 클래런스 배너클인데, 자기 아버지의 국에 근무하고 있지요. 적어도 그가 방문한다고 해서 강물이 화를 입지는 않으리란 건 보장합니다. 강에 불을 지를 위인은 못 되거든요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런?\" 미글스가 말했다. \"배너클이라고? 우리도 그 집안에 관해 좀 알지, 안 그런가, 댄? 아무렴, 그 사람들은 그래도 나무 꼭대기에 앉아 있으니 말일세! 어디 보자. 이 젊은 친구가 데시머스 경에게는 어떤 관계가 되는 건가? 그분께서는 천칠백구십칠 년에 제미마 빌베리 영애와 혼인하셨는데, 그분은 세 번째 결혼에서 난 둘째 따님이고 — 아니야! 거기서 틀렸군! 그분은 세라피나 영애였지 — 제미마 영애는 열다섯 번째 스틸트스토킹 백작이 클레멘티나 투젤럼 영애와 한 두 번째 결혼에서 난 첫째 따님이었어. 좋아. 자, 이 젊은 친구의 아버지는 스틸트스토킹 집안 여자와 혼인했고, 그 아버지는 배너클 집안 여자인 사촌과 혼인했지. 배너클 집안 여자와 혼인한 그 아버지의 아버지는 조들비 집안 여자와 혼인했고. — 가우언, 내가 좀 너무 거슬러 올라가는군. 나는 이 젊은 친구가 데시머스 경에게 어떤 관계가 되는지 알아내려는 거였는데.\"\n\n\"그건 쉽게 말씀드릴 수 있습니다. 그의 아버지가 데시머스 경의 조카입니다.\"\n\n\"데시머스 — 경의 — 조카.\" 미글스 씨는 가계도의 그 충만한 향취에서 자기를 흩뜨릴 것이 하나도 없도록 눈을 감은 채 흐뭇하게 되풀이했다. \"아무렴, 자네 말이 맞아, 가우언. 그렇구먼.\"\n\n\"따라서 데시머스 경은 그의 종조부가 되지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 잠깐만!\" 미글스 씨가 새로운 발견에 눈을 뜨며 말했다. \"그렇다면 어머니 쪽으로는 스틸트스토킹 부인이 그의 종조모가 되는군.\"\n\n\"물론 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이런?\" 미글스 씨가 큰 흥미를 보이며 말했다. \"정말로, 정말로? 우린 기꺼이 그를 맞이하겠네. 우리 변변찮은 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 잘 대접하지. 그래도 적어도 굶기지는 않을 걸세, 그건 분명해.\"\n\n이 대화가 시작될 무렵, 클레넘은 미글스 씨에게서, 도이스의 목덜미를 붙잡은 채 그가 에두름국에서 뛰쳐나오게 만들었던 것과 같은 어떤 대단하지만 해롭지는 않은 폭발이 터지기를 기대했었다. 그러나 그의 선량한 친구에게는, 우리 중 누구라도 옆 거리까지 나가지 않아도 찾을 수 있는 약점이, 아무리 많은 에두름국 경험으로도 그 안에서 오래 억누를 수 없는 약점이 하나 있었다. 클레넘은 도이스를 바라보았다. 그러나 도이스는 그 모든 것을 미리 다 알고 있었기에, 자기 접시만 바라보며 아무런 내색도 하지 않고 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"정말 고맙습니다.\" 가우언이 화제를 마무리 짓느라 말했다. \"클래런스는 굉장한 멍청이입니다만, 세상에 둘도 없이 정답고 더없이 좋은 친구지요!\"\n\n아침 식사가 끝나기 전에, 이 가우언이 아는 사람은 누구나 다소간 멍청이거나 다소간 악당이지만, 그런데도 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 매력적이며, 가장 순박하고, 가장 진실하고, 가장 친절하고, 가장 정답고, 가장 좋은 사람이라는 것이 드러났다. 전제가 무엇이든 이 한결같은 결론에 이르는 과정을, 헨리 가우언 씨는 아마 이렇게 진술했으리라. \"저는 모든 사람의 경우를 두고 늘, 유난히 꼼꼼하게 회계 장부를 매기고, 그와 더불어 선과 악에 관한 세심한 작은 계산서를 결산해 두는 것을 제 신조로 삼습니다. 어찌나 양심적으로 이 일을 하는지, 저는 인간들 가운데 가장 쓸모없는 자도 알고 보면 더없이 정다운 친구라는 사실을 발견하게 되어 기쁩니다. 그리고 정직한 사람과 악당 사이에는 여러분이 짐작하시는 것보다 훨씬 차이가 적다는 흐뭇한 보고를 드릴 처지에 있습니다.\" 이 유쾌한 발견이 빚어낸 결과는, 그가 대부분의 사람에게서 양심적으로 선을 찾아내는 것처럼 보이는 동안 실은 선이 있는 곳에서는 그것을 끌어내리고 선이 없는 곳에는 그것을 세워 올렸다는 것이다. 그러나 그것이 그 발견의 유일하게 불쾌하거나 위험한 특징이었다.\n\n그러나 그것은 배너클 가계도가 안겨 준 만큼의 만족을 미글스 씨에게 주지는 못하는 듯했다. 그날 아침까지는 그의 얼굴에서 한 번도 본 적이 없던 그 구름이 여러 차례 다시 그 얼굴을 뒤덮었다. 그리고 그를 불안하게 살피는 그 똑같은 그림자가 그 아내의 고운 얼굴에도 어려 있었다. 펫이 개를 어루만질 때마다 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 클레넘에게는 그녀의 아버지가 그것을 보며 마음 아파하는 것처럼 보였다. 특히 한번은 가우언이 개의 다른 쪽에 서서 동시에 머리를 숙였을 때, 아서는 미글스 씨가 방을 황급히 나가면서 그의 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 본 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 또한 펫 자신도 이런 작은 일들에 무감하지 않다는 것도 사실이거나, 적어도 그가 더 그렇게 느낀 것이거나 했다. 그녀가 평소보다 더 섬세한 애정으로 자기 선량한 아버지에게 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 표현하려 애쓰는 것, 바로 그 때문에 교회로 갈 때나 돌아올 때나 그녀가 나머지 사람들보다 뒤처져 그의 팔짱을 끼는 것이 그렇게 느껴졌다. 그리고 나중에 그가 정원을 홀로 거닐 때, 그녀가 아버지의 방에서 더없는 다정함으로 부모 두 사람에게 매달려 아버지의 어깨에 기대어 우는 모습을 한순간 언뜻 보았노라고 그는 단언할 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n그날 하루의 후반부가 비로 접어들었으므로, 그들은 집 안에 머물며 미글스 씨의 수집품을 둘러보고 대화로 시간을 보낼 수밖에 없었다. 이 가우언은 자기에 관해 할 말이 많았고, 그것을 거리낌 없고 재미있는 투로 늘어놓았다. 그는 직업상 화가인 듯했고, 한동안 로마에 있었던 듯했다. 그런데도 그에게는 어딘가 가볍고 무심한 아마추어 같은 분위기가 — 예술에 대한 헌신에서도 그 성취에서도 눈에 띄는 절뚝거림이 — 있었는데, 클레넘으로서는 도무지 이해하기 어려웠다.\n\n그는 창밖을 함께 내다보며 서 있던 대니얼 도이스에게 도움을 청했다.\n\n\"가우언 씨를 아십니까?\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 본 적이 있지요. 저 집안이 집에 있을 땐 일요일마다 옵니다.\"\n\n\"하는 말로 미루어 화가인가 보군요?\"\n\n\"일종의 화가지요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 퉁명스러운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"어떤 종류의 화가입니까?\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 한가로운 펠멜 거리를 거니는 걸음걸이로 예술 속에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어온 사람이지요.\" 도이스가 말했다. \"그런데 예술이라는 게 그렇게 태평스럽게 다뤄지는 걸 좋아하기나 할지 의문입니다.\"\n\n* 펠멜: 런던의 사교 클럽들이 모여 있던 한가로운 거리.\n질문을 이어 가던 끝에, 클레넘은 가우언 집안이 배너클 집안에서 아주 멀리 뻗어 나간 한 가지임을, 그리고 가우언의 아버지가 본디 해외 공사관에 소속되어 있다가 어디선가 별 대단치 않은 무엇의 위원으로 연금을 받고 물러났으며, 받아 챙긴 봉급을 손에 쥔 채 그것을 마지막 순간까지 고결하게 지키며 제 자리에서 죽었음을 알게 되었다. 이 탁월한 공직 봉사를 참작하여, 당시 권력을 쥐고 있던 배너클이 왕실에 그의 미망인에게 연 이삼백 파운드의 연금을 내리도록 추천했다. 여기에 다음으로 권력을 쥔 배너클이 햄프턴 코트 궁전 안의 어둑하고 차분한 거처 몇 칸을 더해 주었으니, 그 노부인은 여전히 거기서 살며, 남녀 양쪽의 여러 다른 노인들과 어울려 세태의 타락을 한탄하고 있었다. 그녀의 아들 헨리 가우언 씨는, 그 위원이던 아버지에게서 인생의 그 지극히 미심쩍은 밑천, 곧 아주 보잘것없는 독립 재산을 물려받았으니, 자리를 잡기가 어려웠다. 더욱이 공직 자리가 마침 드물었고, 그의 천재성은 젊은 시절 내내 야생 귀리를 가꾸는 데에만 전념하는 그런 오로지 농경적인 성격의 것이었기에 더 그러했다. 마침내 그는 화가가 되겠다고 선언했다. 한편으로는 그쪽으로 늘 한가한 재주가 있었기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 그를 거두어 주지 않은 배너클 수뇌부의 속을 썩이기 위해서였다. 그리하여 차례차례 이런 일들이 벌어졌다. 우선, 몇몇 명망 있는 부인들이 끔찍하게 충격을 받았고, 다음으로 그의 작품들을 담은 화첩이 밤마다 이리저리 돌려지며 완벽한 클로드요, 완벽한 카위프요, 완벽한 경이라고 황홀하게 선언되었으며, 그다음으로 데시머스 경이 그의 그림을 사들이고는 단번에 미술원 원장과 평의원들을 만찬에 초대하여, 그 특유의 위풍당당한 근엄함으로 \"그거 아시오, 저 작품에는 정말로 막대한 가치가 있는 듯하오만\" 하고 말했으며, 요컨대 지체 있는 사람들이 그를 유행의 반열에 올려놓으려고 적잖이 애를 썼다. 그러나 어쩐 일인지 그 모든 것이 다 수포로 돌아갔다. 편견에 찬 대중은 완강하게 그것을 거부했다. 그들은 데시머스 경의 그림을 찬탄하지 않기로 작정했다. 그들은 어떤 직무에서든, 자기네 직무만 빼고는, 사람이란 아침저녁으로 분투하고 온 마음과 영혼을 다해, 있는 힘껏 일함으로써 자격을 갖추어야 한다고 믿기로 작정했다. 그리하여 이제 가우언 씨는, 마호메트의 것도 그 누구의 것도 아니었던 그 낡아빠진 옛 관처럼, 두 지점 사이 한가운데에 매달려 있게 되었다. 떠나온 한쪽에 대해서는 시샘과 질투로 누렇게 떴고, 닿을 수 없는 다른 한쪽에 대해서도 시샘과 질투로 누렇게 떠 있었다.\n\n그 비 내리는 일요일 오후와 그 후에 클레넘이 그에 관해 알아낸 것의 요지는 그러했다.\n\n만찬 시간이 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 젊은 배너클이 외알 안경을 거느린 채 나타났다. 그 집안 연줄에 경의를 표하느라 미글스 씨는 그날 하루 예쁜 응접실 하녀들을 물리고, 그 대신 칙칙한 사내 둘을 근무에 배치해 두었다. 젊은 배너클은 아서를 보고 더없이 아연실색하고 당황하여, 정신을 차리기에 앞서 저도 모르게 \"이것 보게! 정말이지, 자네도 알다시피, 말이야!\" 하고 중얼거렸다.\n\n그러고 나서도 그는 가장 이른 기회를 붙잡아 친구를 창가로 데려가서는, 그의 전반적인 허약함의 일부인 그 콧소리로 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"자네한테 할 말이 있네, 가우언. 이봐. 이것 보게. 저 친구는 누군가?\"\n\n\"우리 주인장의 친구일세. 내 친구는 아니야.\"\n\n\"저 사람 말이야, 끔찍한 급진파라고, 자네도 알다시피.\" 젊은 배너클이 말했다.\n\n\"그래? 자넨 그걸 어떻게 아나?\"\n\n\"이런, 여보게, 저 사람이 며칠 전에 우리 사람들한테 가장 무시무시한 태도로 들이댔단 말일세. 우리 청사로 올라와서는 우리 아버지한테 어찌나 들이대던지 내쫓으라고 명령하는 수밖에 없었지. 우리 국으로 다시 와서는 나한테 또 들이댔어. 이것 보게. 자넨 그런 친구를 본 적이 없을 걸세.\"\n\n\"그가 뭘 원했는데?\"\n\n\"이런, 여보게.\" 젊은 배너클이 대답했다. \"저 사람이 자긴 알고 싶다고 했단 말이야, 자네도 알다시피! 약속도 없이 우리 국을 휘젓고 다니면서 — 자긴 알고 싶다고 하더라니까!\"\n\n이 폭로에 곁들인, 젊은 배너클의 분개에 찬 경이의 응시는, 마침맞게 찾아온 만찬이라는 구원이 아니었던들 그의 눈을 해칠 만큼 짓눌렀을 것이다. (삼촌과 숙모의 안부를 몹시도 궁금해하던) 미글스 씨가 그에게 미글스 부인을 식당으로 모셔 달라고 청했다. 그리고 그가 미글스 부인의 오른편에 앉자, 미글스 씨는 마치 온 집안이 다 거기 모인 양 흐뭇해 보였다.\n\n전날의 그 자연스러운 매력은 죄다 사라지고 없었다. 만찬을 먹는 사람들도, 만찬 그 자체처럼, 미적지근하고 맛없고 너무 익혀져 있었다 — 그리고 이 모든 것이 이 가엾고 굼뜨고 멍청한 젊은 배너클 탓이었다. 평소에도 화제가 없는 그가, 지금은 이 자리에 특유한 어떤 약점의 희생물이 되어 있었고, 그것은 오직 클레넘에게만 연유한 것이었다. 그는 절박하고도 끊임없이 그 신사를 바라보지 않을 수 없었고, 그 바람에 그의 외알 안경이 수프 속으로, 포도주 잔 속으로, 미글스 부인의 접시 속으로 빠지고, 종 끈처럼 등 뒤로 늘어지고, 칙칙한 사내들 중 하나의 손에 의해 망신스럽게도 여러 차례 다시 그의 가슴에 걸쳐졌다. 이 기구를 자꾸 잃어버리는 데다 그것이 그의 눈에 도무지 박혀 있으려 하지 않는 통에 마음이 약해지고, 그 수수께끼 같은 클레넘을 바라볼 때마다 점점 더 지력이 쇠약해진 그는, 숟가락이며 포크며, 식탁의 식기와 관련된 그 밖의 엉뚱한 물건들을 자기 눈에 갖다 댔다. 이런 실수들을 알아차리는 일은 그의 곤경을 한층 더 키웠지만, 그렇다고 그를 클레넘을 바라보아야 할 필연에서 풀어 주지는 못했다. 그리고 클레넘이 입을 열 때마다, 이 불운한 젊은이는 그가 어떤 교묘한 술책으로 자긴 알고 싶다, 자네도 알다시피, 하는 그 대목으로 돌아오고 있다는 두려움에 또렷이 사로잡혔다.\n\n따라서 미글스 씨 말고는 누구도 그 시간을 그다지 즐겼을지 의문이다. 그러나 미글스 씨만큼은 젊은 배너클을 한껏 즐겼다. 옛이야기 속 황금 물 한 병이 쏟아부어지자 가득한 샘이 된 것처럼, 미글스 씨는 이 작은 배너클 향료 한 줌이 자기 식탁에 온 집안 가계도의 풍미를 부어 준다고 느끼는 듯했다. 그 앞에서 그의 솔직하고 훌륭하고 진실한 자질들은 빛이 바랬다. 그는 그토록 편안하지 못했고, 그토록 자연스럽지 못했으며, 자기에게 속하지 않은 무언가를 좇고 있었고, 자기 자신이 아니었다. 미글스 씨 쪽의 이 무슨 기이한 별남이며, 또 이런 사례를 우리가 달리 어디서 찾을 수 있겠는가!\n\n마침내 비 내리는 일요일은 비 내리는 밤 속으로 스러져 갔다. 젊은 배너클은 힘없이 담배를 피우며 삯마차를 타고 집으로 갔고, 거슬리는 가우언은 거슬리는 개를 데리고 걸어서 떠났다. 펫은 온종일 더없이 상냥하게 애를 쓰며 클레넘에게 다정하게 굴었지만, 클레넘은 아침 식사 이후로 조금 서먹해져 있었다 — 다시 말해, 그가 만약 그녀를 사랑했더라면 그랬으리라는 뜻이다.\n\n그가 자기 방으로 가서 다시 난롯가 의자에 몸을 던졌을 때, 도이스 씨가 손에 촛불을 든 채 문을 두드리며, 내일 어떻게, 또 몇 시에 돌아갈 작정이냐고 물었다. 이 문제를 정한 뒤에, 그는 도이스 씨에게 이 가우언에 관해 한마디 했다 — 만약 그가 자기 연적이었더라면 그의 머릿속을 꽤나 들쑤셔 놓았을 그 가우언에 관해.\n\n\"화가에게는 그다지 좋은 전망이 못 되는군요.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 도이스가 대답했다.\n\n도이스 씨는 침실용 촛대를 한 손에 들고 다른 손은 주머니에 넣은 채 서서, 자기 촛불의 불꽃을 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴에는 그들이 무언가 더 이야기하려 한다는 어떤 조용한 알아차림이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"우리의 선량한 친구분이 오늘 아침 그 사람이 온 뒤로 조금 달라지고 풀이 죽으신 듯한데요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 도이스가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 따님은 그렇지 않던데요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지요.\" 도이스가 말했다.\n\n양쪽 다 잠시 말이 없었다. 도이스 씨는 여전히 촛불의 불꽃을 바라보며 천천히 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"실은, 그분은 따님을 가우언 씨에게서 떼어 놓을 셈으로 두 차례나 해외로 데리고 나가셨답니다. 그분은 따님이 그를 좋아하는 쪽으로 기울었다고 짐작하고 계시고, 또 그런 혼인이 과연 희망적일지에 대해 고통스러운 의구심을 품고 계시지요(저도 그분 생각에 전적으로 동감입니다만, 아마 당신도 그러시리라 봅니다).\"\n\n\"그 둘 사이에는 —\" 클레넘은 목이 메어 기침을 하고는 멈추었다.\n\n\"네, 감기가 드셨군요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다. 그러나 그를 쳐다보지는 않았다.\n\n\"— 물론 둘 사이에 약혼이 되어 있겠지요?\" 클레넘이 짐짓 가벼운 투로 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다. 제가 듣기로는 분명히 아닙니다. 그쪽 신사 편에서 청혼은 있었지만, 약혼은 이루어지지 않았어요. 최근에 돌아온 뒤로 우리 친구분이 주 일 회의 방문은 허락하셨지만, 그게 한계지요. 미니가 부모를 속일 사람은 아니거든요. 당신도 그들과 함께 여행을 해 보셨으니, 그들 사이에, 이번 생을 넘어서까지 뻗어 있는 그 유대가 어떤 것인지 아시리라 믿습니다. 미니 양과 가우언 씨 사이에 있는 것은, 우리가 보고 있는 그게 전부라는 데 의심의 여지가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"아! 우리가 본 것만으로도 충분합니다!\" 아서가 외쳤다.\n\n도이스 씨는, 절망까지는 아니더라도 비통한 외침을 들은 사람이, 그 외침을 토해 낸 이의 마음에 어떤 격려와 희망을 불어넣으려 하는 듯한 어조로 그에게 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다. 그런 어조는 아마, 별난 무리의 하나인 그의 괴팍함의 일부였을 것이다. 그게 아니라면, 클레넘도 함께 듣지 않고서야 그가 어떻게 그런 종류의 무언가를 들을 수 있었겠는가?\n\n비가 지붕 위로 세차게 떨어지고, 땅바닥을 후두두 두드리고, 상록수들 사이로, 또 잎 떨어진 나뭇가지들 사이로 똑똑 흘러내렸다. 비는 세차게, 음울하게 내렸다. 눈물의 밤이었다.\n\n만약 클레넘이 펫에게 사랑에 빠지지 않기로 작정하지 않았더라면, 만약 그가 그럴 만큼 마음이 약했더라면, 만약 그가 조금씩 조금씩 자기 본성의 온 진지함과, 자기 희망의 온 힘과, 무르익은 자기 인격의 온 풍요를 그 한 판의 도박에 걸도록 스스로를 설득했더라면, 만약 그가 그렇게 했는데 모든 것을 잃었음을 알게 되었더라면, 그는 그날 밤 말할 수 없이 비참했으리라. 사정이 사정인지라 —\n\n사정이 사정인지라, 비는 세차게, 음울하게 내렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover\n\n리틀 도릿은 스물두 번째 생일을 맞기까지 연모하는 이 하나 없이 지내지는 않았다. 그 얕디얕은 마셜시에서조차, 영원히 늙지 않는 궁수는 곰팡내 나는 활로 깃 없는 화살 몇 대를 이따금 쏘아 보내며, 수용자 한둘쯤은 명중시키곤 했던 것이다.\n\n그러나 리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 수용자가 아니었다. 그는 어느 간수의 다감한 아들이었다. 그의 아버지는 때가 무르익으면 흠 한 점 없는 열쇠를 그에게 물려주기를 바랐고, 어린 시절부터 자기 직무의 의무를, 그리고 그 감옥 자물쇠를 집안에 계속 두고 싶다는 야심을 그에게 익히 가르쳐 두었다. 그 계승이 아직 보류 상태인 동안, 그는 호스몽거 길 모퉁이를 돌아선 곳에서 어머니가 운영하는 아담한 담배 가게 일을 거들었는데(그의 아버지는 비거주 간수였다), 그 가게는 대개 감옥 담장 안에서 쏠쏠한 단골을 거느릴 수 있었다.\n\n여러 해 전, 그의 연정의 대상이 수위실의 높직한 난로 가림막 옆 작은 안락의자에 앉아 있곤 하던 시절, 그녀보다 한 살 많은 영 존(집안 성은 치버리)은 감탄과 경이의 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 마당에서 그녀와 놀 때 그가 가장 좋아하던 놀이는, 그녀를 구석에 가둬 두는 시늉을 하다가 진짜 입맞춤을 받고서 풀어 주는 시늉을 하는 것이었다. 정문의 큰 자물쇠 열쇠구멍으로 들여다볼 만큼 키가 자라자, 그는 여러 차례 아버지의 점심이나 저녁밥을 문 바깥쪽에 내려놓고 식어 가든 말든 내버려둔 채, 그 바람 통하는 구멍으로 그녀를 들여다보느라 한쪽 눈에 찬 바람을 맞으며 서 있곤 했다.\n\n소년 시절, 청춘이라는 것이 구두끈을 풀어헤치고 다니기 일쑤이며 다행히도 제 소화기관 따위는 의식하지 못하던 그 둔감한 나날에 영 존의 일편단심이 행여 느슨해진 적이 있었더라도, 그는 이내 그것을 다시 팽팽히 죄어 단단히 조여 두었다. 열아홉 살 때 그의 손은 그녀의 생일을 맞아 그녀 거처를 마주 보는 담벽에 분필로 이렇게 새겼다. \"요정들이 길러 낸 어여쁜 아기를 환영하노라!\" 스물세 살에는 같은 그 손이 일요일마다 떨리는 손길로, 마셜시의 아버지이자 제 영혼의 여왕의 아버지께 시가를 바쳤다.\n\n영 존은 키가 작았고, 다리가 다소 부실했으며, 옅은 빛깔의 머리카락은 무척이나 가늘었다. 한쪽 눈도(어쩌면 열쇠구멍으로 들여다보던 그 눈이었으리라) 부실해서, 마치 초점을 그러모으지 못하는 듯 다른 쪽보다 더 크게 보였다. 영 존은 또한 온순했다. 그러나 그 영혼만은 위대했다. 시적이고, 도량 넓고, 한결같았다.\n\n제 마음의 지배자 앞에서 너무도 겸손하여 낙관할 수는 없었으나, 영 존은 제 연정의 대상을 온갖 명암 속에서 곰곰이 살펴보았다. 그것을 더없이 행복한 결말까지 따라가 보며, 그는 — 자화자찬 없이 — 거기에 어울림이 있음을 알아보았다. 일이 잘 풀려 두 사람이 맺어진다고 치자. 그녀는 마셜시의 아이, 그는 자물쇠지기. 거기에 어울림이 있었다. 그가 거주 간수가 된다고 치자. 그녀는 그토록 오래 세 들어 있던 그 방을 공식적으로 이어받게 되리라. 거기에 아름다운 합당함이 있었다. 그 방은 발끝으로 서면 담장 너머가 보였고, 진홍색 강낭콩 격자 시렁과 카나리아 한두 마리만 있으면 그야말로 정자가 될 터였다. 거기에 매혹적인 발상이 있었다. 그리고 서로가 서로의 전부가 되면, 그 자물쇠에마저 어울리는 우아함이 깃들었다. 세상을 닫아걸고(닫아 가둘 부분만 빼고), 세상의 근심과 소란은 빚 갚는 성지로 향하던 순례자들이 그들 곁에 머물며 들려주는 풍문으로나 전해 듣고, 위로는 정자가 있고 아래로는 수위실이 있으니, 두 사람은 전원풍의 가정적 행복 속에서 세월의 강물을 미끄러져 내려가리라. 영 존은 그 그림을 마무리 짓되, 감옥 담벼락 바로 옆 인접한 교회 묘지에 다음과 같은 가슴 저미는 비문을 새긴 묘비 하나를 더함으로써 제 눈에서 눈물을 자아냈다. \"이웃한 마셜시의 간수로 육십 년, 수석 간수로 오십 년을 봉직하고, 만인의 존경을 받으며 천팔백팔십육년 십이월 삼십일일에 여든세 살의 나이로 이승을 떠난 존 치버리를 기리며. 아울러 처녀 적 성이 도릿이었던, 그가 참으로 사랑하고 또 그를 참으로 사랑한 아내 에이미를 기리며. 그녀는 남편을 잃은 지 채 마흔여덟 시간도 못 되어 앞서 말한 마셜시에서 마지막 숨을 거두었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 태어났고, 그곳에서 살았으며, 그곳에서 죽었다.\"\n\n치버리 부부가 아들의 연정을 모르는 바 아니었다 — 실제로 그것은 몇몇 유난한 경우에 그를 손님들에게 성마르게 굴게 하고 장사에 해를 끼칠 만한 심사로 몰아넣기도 했다 — 그러나 그들 역시 나름대로 그것을 바람직한 결론으로 끌어내어 두었다. 신중한 여인인 치버리 부인은 남편더러 이 점을 새겨 두라 일렀으니, 곧 그들의 존이 자물쇠를 물려받을 전망은 도릿 양과 맺어지면 틀림없이 더 든든해지리라는 것이었다. 그녀 자신이 그 감옥에 대해 일종의 연고를 가졌고 그곳에서 크게 존경받고 있으니 말이다. 치버리 부인은 남편더러 또 이 점을 새겨 두라 일렀으니, 한편으로 그들의 존에게는 살림 밑천과 믿음직한 직책이 있고, 다른 한편으로 도릿 양에게는 가문이 있으며, 그녀(치버리 부인)의 견해로는 반쪽 둘이 모여 온전한 하나가 된다는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 치버리 부인은 외교가가 아닌 어머니로서, 다른 관점에서 남편더러 이 점을 떠올리라 일렀으니, 곧 그들의 존은 한 번도 튼튼했던 적이 없는데 그 연정이 가뜩이나 그를 충분히 애태우고 졸이고 있으니, 만약 일이 어그러지면 제 몸에 무슨 변고를 일으키지 않으리라고 아무도 장담 못 할 지경까지 그를 몰아붙여서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 이런 논거들은 말수 적은 사내인 치버리 씨의 마음을 어찌나 강하게 움직였던지, 그는 여러 일요일 아침마다 아들에게 그가 이른바 \"행운의 손길\"이라 부르는 것을 베풀었으니, 이는 그날 아들이 사랑을 고백하고 의기양양하게 성공하기에 앞서 행운의 여신께 그를 그렇게 천거하는 뜻이라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 영 존은 한 번도 그 고백을 할 용기를 내지 못했다. 그가 흥분한 채 담배 가게로 돌아와 손님들에게 달려든 것은 주로 이런 때였다.\n\n이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 정작 리틀 도릿 자신은 맨 마지막으로 고려되는 사람이었다. 그녀의 오빠와 언니는 이 사실을 알고서, 그것을 빌미 삼아 비참하게 너덜너덜해진 가문의 체통이라는 해묵은 허구를 거기에 걸어 펄럭이며 일종의 지위를 누렸다. 언니는 그 가엾은 구애자가 사랑하는 이를 한번 보겠다고 감옥 주위를 서성일 때마다 그를 조롱함으로써 가문의 체통을 내세웠다. 팁은 귀족적인 오빠 노릇을 하고 나서서, 작은 구주희 마당에서 어느 이름 모를 신사가 거명되지 않은 어떤 강아지 새끼의 목덜미를 움켜잡을 듯 말 듯한 일을 두고 거만하게 으스댐으로써, 가문의 체통과 제 체통을 함께 내세웠다. 도릿 가문에서 이를 이용한 이가 그들만은 아니었다. 결코, 결코 아니었다. 마셜시의 아버지로 말하자면, 물론 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것으로 되어 있었다. 그의 가련한 위엄이 그토록 낮은 데까지 눈길을 줄 리 없었다. 그러나 그는 일요일마다 시가를 받았고, 받아서 기뻐했으며, 때로는 선물한 이(그럴 때면 그는 우쭐하고 희망에 부풀었다)와 함께 마당을 거닐며 그 곁에서 너그럽게 한 대 피워 주기까지 황송하게 했다. 못지않은 흔쾌함과 자상한 너그러움으로 그는 치버리 영감의 시중도 받아들였는데, 영감은 근무 차례 동안 그가 수위실에 들어오면 언제나 제 안락의자와 신문을 그에게 내주었고, 심지어 그에게, 해 진 뒤 어느 때라도 조용히 앞마당으로 나가 거리를 한번 내다보고 싶으시면 그를 막을 만한 것은 별로 없다고 일러 주기까지 했다. 그가 이 후자의 호의를 누리지 않은 것은 다만 그것에 대한 흥미를 잃었기 때문이었다. 그 밖에 받을 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 받았으니 말이다. 그는 이따금 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"치버리는 지극히 예의 바른 사람이지. 무척 자상하고 무척 공손해. 영 치버리 또한 그렇고. 정말이지 이곳에서의 한 사람의 처지에 대해 거의 섬세한 분별력을 지녔다 할 만하지. 치버리네는 참으로 처신이 반듯한 집안이야. 그들의 행실은 나를 흐뭇하게 한다네.\"\n\n그토록 헌신적인 영 존은 이 내내 그 가족을 경외의 눈으로 바라보았다. 그는 그들의 허세를 따져 볼 엄두조차 내지 못한 채, 그들이 떠받들고 내세우는 그 비참한 우상에 경배를 올렸다. 그녀의 오빠로부터 어떤 모욕을 당하더라도 원망할 마음으로 말하자면, 그는 설령 천성이 더없이 평화로운 기질이 아니었더라도, 그 신성한 신사에게 혀를 놀리거나 손을 쳐드는 것은 불경한 짓이라 여겼을 것이다. 그 고결한 마음이 노여움을 사는 것은 안타까웠으나, 그래도 그는 그 사실이 그 고결함과 모순되지 않는다고 느끼며, 그 늠름한 영혼을 달래고 누그러뜨리고자 애썼다. 그녀의 아버지는, 불운에 처한 신사 — 고매한 기상과 정중한 풍모를 지니고 늘 그를 너그러이 대해 주던 신사 — 였기에 그는 깊이 존경했다. 그녀의 언니는 다소 허영심 있고 거만하다 여겼으나, 지난날을 잊지 못하는, 무한한 재예를 갖춘 아가씨라 보았다. 그 가엾은 젊은이가 리틀 도릿을 다른 모든 식구와 달리 있는 그대로의 모습 때문에 존경하고 사랑했다는 것은, 그녀의 가치와 남다름에 대한 본능적인 증언이었다.\n\n호스몽거 길 모퉁이를 돌아선 곳의 담배 가게는 단층짜리 시골풍 건물에서 운영되었는데, 이곳은 호스몽거 길 감옥 마당에서 불어오는 바람의 혜택을 누렸고, 그 쾌적한 시설의 담벼락 아래로 한적하게 거닐 수 있는 이점도 있었다. 장사 규모가 너무 소박하여 실물 크기의 하일랜드 사람 인형을 내걸 정도는 못 되었으나, 문설주 까치발 위에 작은 것 하나를 두었는데, 그 모습은 마치 킬트를 입지 않을 수 없게 된 추락한 천사 같았다.\n\n이렇게 장식된 문간에서, 어느 일요일 구운 고기로 이른 점심을 든 뒤, 영 존은 여느 일요일의 심부름에 나섰다. 빈손이 아니라 시가를 봉헌물로 들고서였다. 그는 말끔하게 차려입었으니, 제 체구가 감당할 수 있는 한 큼직한 검정 벨벳 깃이 달린 자두 빛 외투에, 금빛 잔가지 무늬가 박힌 비단 조끼를 받쳐 입었고, 황갈색 바탕에 라일락 빛 꿩들의 사냥터를 그린, 당시 크게 유행하던 점잖은 목도리를 둘렀으며, 옆줄이 어찌나 화려하게 들어갔던지 다리 한 짝이 세 줄 류트처럼 보이는 바지를 입었고, 무척 높고 빳빳한 예장용 모자를 썼다. 신중한 치버리 부인이, 이 온갖 치장에 더해 제 아들 존이 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 한 켤레와, 그가 가야 할 길을 가리키는 상아 손이 꼭대기에 달린 작은 이정표 같은 지팡이까지 들고 있음을 알아채고, 그가 이 거창한 출정 차림으로 오른쪽 모퉁이를 도는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 그때 마침 집에 있던 치버리 씨에게 자기는 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 알 것 같다고 한마디 했다.\n\n그 일요일 오후, 수용자들은 상당수의 방문객을 맞고 있었고, 그들의 아버지는 알현을 받고자 자기 방을 지키고 있었다. 마당을 한 바퀴 돈 뒤, 리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 두근거리는 가슴으로 위층에 올라가 손가락 마디로 아버지의 방문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"들어와요, 들어와!\" 너그러운 목소리가 말했다. 아버지의 목소리, 그녀의 아버지, 마셜시의 아버지의 목소리였다. 그는 검정 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 신문을 든 채 앉아 있었고, 탁자에는 우연히 놓인 양 삼 실링 육 펜스가 있었으며, 의자 두 개가 놓여 있었다. 알현을 위한 만반의 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"아아, 영 존! 잘 지냈나, 잘 지냈어!\"\n\n\"덕분에 그럭저럭 지냅니다. 어르신께서도 평안하시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 존 치버리, 그럼. 불평할 것 하나 없네.\"\n\n\"외람되지만 제가, 어르신—\"\n\n\"응?\" 마셜시의 아버지는 이 대목에서 늘 눈썹을 치켜올렸고, 상냥하게 얼이 빠진 듯 미소를 머금은 채 정신을 딴 데 두는 표정이 되었다.\n\n\"—시가를 몇 대 가져왔습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"오!\" (한순간, 더없이 놀란 듯이.) \"고맙네, 영 존, 고마워. 그렇지만 정말이지 내가 너무 과한—아니라고? 그래, 그렇다면 더는 말하지 않겠네. 영 존, 괜찮다면 벽난로 선반 위에 놔 주게. 그리고 앉게, 앉아. 자네는 낯선 사람이 아니지 않나, 존.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 정말로—아가씨는;\" 여기서 영 존은 천천히 돌아가는 쥐장처럼 그 큼직한 모자를 왼손 위에서 빙글빙글 돌렸다. \"에이미 아가씨께서는 안녕하신지요, 어르신?\"\n\n\"그럼, 존, 그럼. 아주 잘 있네. 지금 외출 중이라네.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까, 어르신?\"\n\"그래, 존. 에이미는 바람을 쐬러 나갔네. 우리 집 아이들은 다들 곧잘 나다닌다네. 하지만 그 나이엔 자연스러운 일이지, 존.\"\n\n\"지당하신 말씀입니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"바람을 쐬러. 바람을 쐬러. 그래.\" 그는 탁자 위에 손가락을 부드럽게 두드리며 창문 쪽으로 눈길을 올렸다. \"에이미는 아이언 브리지로 바람을 쐬러 갔네. 요즘 들어 그 아이는 아이언 브리지를 무척 좋아하게 되어서, 어디보다도 거기를 거니는 걸 좋아하는 듯하다네.\" 그가 다시 대화로 돌아왔다. \"자네 부친은 지금은 근무가 아닌 듯한데, 존?\"\n\n\"네, 어르신, 아버지는 오후 늦게 나옵니다.\" 큼직한 모자를 또 한 번 빙글 돌리고 나서, 영 존은 일어서며 말했다. \"이만 물러가야 할 것 같습니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"벌써? 잘 가게, 영 존. 아니, 아니,\" 더없는 너그러움으로, \"장갑은 신경 쓰지 말게, 존. 낀 채로 악수하세. 자네는 여기서 낯선 사람이 아니지 않은가.\"\n\n받아 준 친절에 더없이 흐뭇해하며, 영 존은 계단을 내려갔다. 내려가는 길에 그는 알현시킬 방문객들을 데리고 올라오는 수용자 몇과 마주쳤는데, 바로 그 순간 도릿 씨가 마침 난간 너머로 유난히 또렷하게 외쳤다. \"자네의 자그마한 성의에 참 고맙네, 존!\"\n\n리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 이내 아이언 브리지의 통행료 접시 위에 일 페니를 내려놓고, 그 다리 위에 올라서서 잘 알고 깊이 사랑하는 모습을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 처음에는 그녀가 거기 없을까 봐 두려웠으나, 미들섹스 쪽을 향해 걸어가다가 그는 그녀가 가만히 서서 물을 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 그는 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해했다. 평일보다 매연이 덜한 도시의 지붕과 굴뚝 더미가 있었고, 멀리 돛대와 첨탑들이 있었다. 어쩌면 그녀는 그것들을 생각하고 있는지도 몰랐다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 어찌나 오래 골똘히 생각에 빠져 온통 정신을 쏟고 있던지, 그녀를 연모하는 이가 제 딴에는 한참이라 여길 만큼 가만히 서 있고, 두세 번 물러났다가 다시 본래 자리로 돌아와도, 그녀는 여전히 움직이지 않았다. 그래서 그는 결국 마음을 정하고 다가가, 지나는 길에 우연히 마주친 척 그녀에게 말을 걸기로 했다. 그곳은 한적했고, 지금이 아니면 영영 그녀에게 말을 걸 기회가 없을 듯했다.\n\n그가 걸어가는데, 그녀는 그가 바로 곁에 이를 때까지 그의 발소리를 듣지 못한 듯했다. 그가 \"도릿 양!\" 하고 부르자, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라며 그에게서 물러섰는데, 그 얼굴에는 두려움과 무언가 혐오에 가까운 빛이 어려 있어 그에게 이루 말할 수 없는 낙담을 안겼다. 그녀는 전에도 자주 그를 피했었다 — 사실, 아주 오래오래 전부터 늘 그랬다. 그가 자기 쪽으로 오는 것을 보고는 어찌나 자주 돌아서서 미끄러지듯 사라졌던지, 가엾은 영 존도 그것을 우연이라 여길 수가 없었다. 그러나 그는 그것이 수줍음이거나, 그녀의 내성적인 성품이거나, 제 마음의 상태를 미리 알아챈 까닭이거나, 아무튼 혐오만은 아닌 무언가이기를 바랐다. 그런데 이제 그 순간의 눈빛이 말하고 있었다. \"하필 당신이! 세상 누구라도 당신보다는 차라리 나았을 텐데!\"\n\n그것은 한순간의 눈빛에 지나지 않았으니, 그녀가 이내 그것을 다잡고는 부드럽고 작은 목소리로 말했기 때문이다. \"어머, 존 씨! 당신이었군요?\" 그러나 그녀는 그것이 무엇이었는지를 느꼈고, 그도 그것이 무엇이었는지를 느꼈으며, 두 사람은 똑같이 어쩔 줄 모른 채 서로를 마주 보고 서 있었다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨, 제가 말을 걸어 방해를 한 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 좀요. 저—저는 혼자 있으려고 여기 왔고, 혼자인 줄 알았어요.\"\n\n\"에이미 아가씨, 제가 외람되이 이쪽으로 걸어온 것은, 방금 도릿 어르신을 찾아뵈었을 때 어르신께서 마침 말씀하시기를, 아가씨께서—\"\n\n그녀는 갑자기 가슴 미어지는 어조로 \"아아, 아버지, 아버지!\" 하고 중얼거리며 얼굴을 돌려 버려, 그에게 이전보다 더한 낙담을 안겼다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨, 도릿 어르신을 입에 올려 행여 아가씨를 불편하게 해 드린 것이 아니기를 바랍니다. 정말이지 어르신께서는 아주 평안하시고 더없이 좋은 기분이셨으며, 평소보다도 더한 친절을 제게 베푸셨습니다. 어찌나 친절하신지 제가 그곳에서 낯선 사람이 아니라고 말씀해 주시고, 여러모로 저를 무척 흐뭇하게 해 주셨답니다.\"\n\n연모하는 이가 이루 형용할 수 없는 경악에 빠지게도, 리틀 도릿은 돌린 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고는 마치 고통에 시달리는 듯 선 자리에서 몸을 흔들며 중얼거렸다. \"아아, 아버지, 어떻게 그러실 수가! 아아, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아버지, 어떻게, 어떻게 그러실 수가 있어요!\"\n\n가엾은 젊은이는 연민이 북받쳐 오른 채 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었으나, 이것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다. 마침내 그녀가 손수건을 꺼내 여전히 돌린 얼굴에 갖다 대고는 황급히 가 버렸다. 처음에 그는 못 박힌 듯 서 있었으나, 곧 그녀를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨, 제발! 잠깐만 멈춰 주시겠습니까? 에이미 아가씨, 정 그렇다면, 제가 가겠습니다. 제가 아가씨를 이렇게 내쫓았다고 생각해야 한다면 저는 제정신을 잃고 말 겁니다.\"\n\n떨리는 그의 목소리와 꾸밈없는 간절함에 리틀 도릿은 걸음을 멈췄다. \"아아, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요!\"\n\n그녀가 그 차분한 자제력을 잃은 모습을 한 번도 본 적 없는, 어린 시절부터 늘 그토록 믿음직하고 자신을 억누르는 모습만 보아 온 영 존에게는, 그녀의 비탄이, 그리고 자신이 그 원인으로 거기 얽히게 되었다는 사실이 큰 충격이어서, 그 큼직한 모자에서부터 포장도로까지 그를 뒤흔들어 놓았다. 그는 자기 뜻을 해명할 필요를 느꼈다. 자기가 오해받을 수도 있었다 — 제 상상에 들어온 적도 없는 무언가를 뜻한 것으로, 혹은 한 것으로 여겨질 수도 있었다. 그는 그녀가 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의로서, 자기 해명을 들어 달라고 간청했다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨, 아가씨 가문이 제 가문보다 훨씬 위라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다. 그것을 감추려 해 봐야 헛일이지요. 제가 들어 아는 한 치버리 집안에 신사였던 이는 한 번도 없었고, 저는 이토록 중대한 문제에 대해 거짓을 꾸미는 비열한 짓은 하지 않겠습니다. 에이미 아가씨, 아가씨의 고결하신 오라버니께서, 또한 기개 넘치는 언니께서 높은 데서 저를 멸시하신다는 것도 저는 잘 압니다. 제가 할 일은 그분들을 공경하고, 그분들의 벗으로 받아들여지기를 바라며, 제 낮은 처지에서 그분들이 놓이신 그 높은 자리를 우러러보는 것입니다—담배로 보든 자물쇠로 보든 그것이 낮다는 것을 저는 잘 아니까요—그리고 언제까지나 그분들이 평안하고 행복하시기를 비는 것입니다.\"\n\n그 가엾은 젊은이에게는 참으로 진실함이 있었고, 그 모자의 딱딱함과 그 마음의 부드러움(어쩌면 그 머리의 부드러움도) 사이의 대비가 사람을 감동시켰다. 리틀 도릿은 그에게 자신도 자기 처지도 깎아내리지 말라고, 무엇보다도 자기가 제 처지를 더 높다고 여긴다는 생각일랑 떨쳐 버리라고 간청했다. 이 말이 그에게 약간의 위안을 주었다.\n\n\"에이미 아가씨,\" 그러더니 그가 더듬거렸다. \"저는 오랫동안—제게는 영겁처럼, 돌고 도는 영겁처럼 느껴집니다만—아가씨께 드리고 싶은, 가슴속에 고이 품어 온 소원이 하나 있었습니다. 말씀드려도 될까요?\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 자신도 모르게 또다시 그의 곁에서 흠칫 물러섰는데, 그 얼굴에는 아까 그 눈빛의 희미한 그림자가 어렸다. 그것을 가까스로 억누르며, 그녀는 대답도 없이 빠른 걸음으로 다리를 절반 가까이 가로질러 걸어갔다!\n\n\"드려도—에이미 아가씨, 저는 그저 겸손히 여쭐 뿐입니다—말씀드려도 될까요? 저는 아무 그럴 뜻도 없이 이미 아가씨께 이렇게나 큰 아픔을 드려, 거룩한 하늘에 맹세코! 아가씨의 허락 없이는 결코 말씀드리지 않을 것입니다. 저 혼자 비참해질 수도 있고, 저 혼자 가슴이 찢어질 수도 있는데, 한순간의 기쁨이라도 드릴 수 있다면 저 난간에서 몸을 던지기라도 할 그분을, 어찌하여 함께 비참하게 하고 가슴 찢어지게 하겠습니까! 뭐, 그게 대단한 일도 아니지요. 두 푼만 준대도 그렇게 할 테니까요.\"\n\n그 침울한 심사와 화려한 차림새가 그를 우스꽝스럽게 만들 법도 했으나, 그의 섬세함이 그를 존경할 만한 사람으로 만들어 주었다. 리틀 도릿은 거기서 어떻게 해야 할지를 깨달았다.\n\n\"부디, 존 치버리 씨,\" 그녀가 떨면서, 그러나 차분한 투로 대답했다. \"더 말해도 되겠느냐고 이렇게 사려 깊게 물어 주셨으니—부디, 그러지 마세요.\"\n\n\"영영 안 됩니까, 에이미 아가씨?\"\n\n\"아니요, 제발요. 절대로 안 돼요.\" \"오, 하느님!\" 영 존이 숨을 헐떡였다.\n\n\"하지만 대신 제가 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있는데, 들어 주시겠어요. 저는 진심으로, 표현할 수 있는 한 가장 분명한 뜻으로 이 말을 하고 싶어요. 우리를 떠올리실 때, 존—오빠와 언니와 저 말이에요—우리를 다른 사람들과 조금이라도 다르게 여기지 말아 주세요. 우리가 한때 어떠했든(저로서는 잘 모르겠지만) 오래전에 그러기를 그쳤고, 다시는 결코 그럴 수 없으니까요. 지금 하시는 대로 하시는 것보다 그렇게 해 주시는 편이, 당신께도 훨씬 낫고 다른 사람들에게도 훨씬 나을 거예요.\"\n\n영 존은 그것을 마음에 새기도록 애쓰겠노라고, 그녀가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 진심으로 기꺼이 하겠노라고 구슬프게 다짐했다.\n\n\"저로 말하자면,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"되도록 저를 적게 생각해 주세요. 적을수록 좋아요. 그래도 저를 생각하실 때가 있다면, 존, 그저 감옥에서 늘 한 가지 의무에만 매여 자라는 것을 당신이 보아 온 그 아이로만, 약하고, 내성적이고, 만족할 줄 알고, 보호받지 못하는 한 여자아이로만 여겨 주세요. 특히 이것을 기억해 주셨으면 해요. 제가 그 문 밖으로 나오면, 저는 보호받지 못하는 외로운 신세라는 것을요.\"\n\n그녀가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 애써 보겠다고 그가 말했다. 그런데 에이미 아가씨는 어찌하여 그토록 그것을 기억하라 하시는 걸까?\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"그래야 제가 당신을 온전히 믿을 수 있으니까요. 오늘 일을 잊지 않고, 제게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라고요. 당신은 너그러운 분이라 그 점은 믿을 수 있다는 것을 저는 알아요. 정말 그렇게 믿고, 또 언제까지나 그렇게 믿을 거예요. 제가 당신을 온전히 믿는다는 것을 지금 당장 보여 드릴게요. 저는 지금 우리가 이야기를 나누는 이곳이 제가 아는 그 어떤 곳보다도 좋아요;\" 그녀의 옅은 혈색이 사라져 있었으나, 연모하는 이는 바로 그 순간 그것이 되살아나는 것을 본 듯했다. \"그래서 자주 이곳에 올지도 몰라요. 제가 이렇게 말씀드리기만 하면 당신이 결코 저를 찾아 다시는 이곳에 오지 않으리라는 것을, 저는 확신할 수 있다는 걸 알아요. 그리고 저는—정말 확신해요!\"\n\n믿어도 좋다고 영 존이 말했다. 자기는 비참한 가련뱅이지만, 그녀의 말은 자기에게 법보다 더한 것이라고.\n\n\"그러면 안녕히, 존,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"그리고 언젠가 당신이 좋은 아내를 얻어 행복한 사람이 되기를 바라요. 당신은 분명 행복할 자격이 있고, 또 그렇게 될 거예요, 존.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그녀가 손을 내밀자, 그 잔가지 무늬 조끼 아래에 든 심장이—사실대로 말하자면 그저 싸구려 기성품에 지나지 않았으나—신사의 심장만큼이나 부풀어 올랐고, 그 가엾고 평범하고 보잘것없는 젊은이는 그것을 담아 둘 자리가 없어 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다.\n\n\"오, 울지 마세요,\" 리틀 도릿이 가엾어하며 말했다. \"그러지, 그러지 마세요! 안녕히, 존. 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n\"안녕히, 에이미 아가씨. 안녕히!\"\n\n그렇게 그는 그녀를 떠났다. 떠나기 전에, 그녀가 의자 한 귀퉁이에 앉아 작은 손을 거친 담벼락에 기댈 뿐 아니라 그 얼굴마저 거기에 갖다 대는 것을, 마치 머리가 무겁고 마음이 서글픈 듯이 하는 것을 지켜본 다음이었다.\n\n큼직한 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓰고, 비라도 오는 양 벨벳 깃을 세워 올리고, 금빛 잔가지 무늬 비단 조끼를 감추려 자두 빛 외투의 단추를 채우고, 작은 이정표가 가차 없이 집 쪽을 가리키는 가운데, 더없이 후미진 뒷골목을 따라 살금살금 걸어가며, 가는 길에 세인트 조지 교회 묘지에 세울 묘비의 다음과 같은 새 비문을 짓고 있는 연모하는 이의 모습을 바라보는 것은, 인간의 계획이 얼마나 헛된 것인가를 보여 주는 가슴 저미는 예시였다.\n\n\"여기 존 치버리의 유해가 잠들다. 이렇다 할 것 하나 없던 이로, 천팔백이십육년 끝 무렵 상심으로 세상을 떠났으니, 마지막 숨으로 제 유골 위에 에이미라는 이름을 새겨 달라 청하매, 비통에 잠긴 그의 부모가 이에 따라 그리하도록 분부하였노라.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations\n\n윌리엄과 프레더릭 도릿 형제는 칼리지 마당을 거닐고 있었다 — 물론 귀족적인, 다시 말해 펌프 쪽 마당이었으니, 아버지가 자신의 위신을 위해 일요일 아침이나 성탄절, 그 밖의 의례적인 경우를 제외하고는 가난한 쪽에 있는 자기 자녀들 틈에 끼는 일을 삼가는 것을 하나의 원칙으로 삼았기 때문이다. 그런 의례를 지키는 데에는 그가 무척 시간을 잘 지켰으며, 그런 때에는 자녀들의 어린것들 머리 위에 손을 얹고 그 어린 파산자들에게 자못 교화적인 자비로움으로 축복을 내려 주었다 — 칼리지 마당을 함께 거니는 형제는 인상적인 광경이었다. 자유의 몸인 프레더릭은 그토록 풀이 죽고, 허리가 굽고, 시들고, 빛이 바래 있었고, 갇힌 몸인 윌리엄은 그토록 정중하고, 점잖게 굽어보며, 제 처지를 자애롭게 의식하고 있었으니, 다른 무엇으로는 몰라도 적어도 이 점에서만큼은 형제는 경탄할 만한 구경거리였다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 일요일에 아이언 브리지에서 연인과 만난 그날 저녁에도 두 사람은 마당을 거닐었다. 그날의 국정은 끝났고, 응접실에는 손님이 잘 들었으며, 새로 인사를 드리러 온 이가 여럿이었고, 우연히 탁자 위에 놓아둔 삼 실링 육 펜스는 우연히 십이 실링으로 불어나 있었으며, 마셜시의 아버지는 한 모금의 시가로 기운을 돋우는 중이었다. 마당을 오르내리며, 자기 우월함에 거만을 떨지 않고 그 가엾은 피조물을 헤아려, 형의 발걸음을 동생의 절뚝거리는 걸음에 상냥하게 맞추고, 그를 너그러이 참아 주며, 입술에서 흘러나와 뾰족한 담장을 넘어가려 애쓰는 작은 연기 한 모금 한 모금마다 그 노쇠함에 대한 관용을 내뿜으니, 그는 경탄할 만한 광경이었다.\n\n흐릿한 눈, 마비된 손, 굽은 몸, 더듬거리는 정신의 동생 프레더릭은 순순히 그의 곁에서 발을 끌었고, 길을 잃고 헤매는 그 미궁 같은 세상의 온갖 일을 받아들이듯 형의 보살핌을 받아들였다. 그는 여느 때처럼 비틀어 만 누르스름한 갈색 종이쪽을 손에 들고서, 거기서 이따금 코담배 한 줌을 비틀어 풀어 꺼냈다. 그것을 떨리는 손으로 집어넣고는, 적잖이 감탄하는 눈빛으로 형을 흘끗 보고, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고서 다시 한 줌을 집을 때까지, 아니면 가만히 서서 주위를 둘러볼 때까지 — 어쩌면 문득 자기 클라리넷이 없는 것을 깨닫고서 — 그렇게 형의 곁에서 발을 끌었다.\n\n밤 그림자가 깃들면서 칼리지의 방문객들은 슬슬 빠져나가고 있었으나, 마당은 여전히 꽤 붐볐으니, 수용자들이 대개 나와 친구들을 수위실까지 배웅하고 있었기 때문이다. 형제가 마당을 거니는 동안, 갇힌 몸인 윌리엄은 인사를 받으려고 주위를 둘러보았고, 우아하게 모자를 들어 답례했으며, 매혹적인 태도로 자유의 몸인 프레더릭이 사람들에게 부딪치거나 담장에 밀려 떠밀리지 않도록 막아 주었다. 수용자들이란 집단으로 보면 쉽사리 감동하는 이들이 아니었으나, 그들조차 저마다의 방식으로 경탄하며, 이 두 형제에게서 경탄할 만한 광경을 발견하는 듯했다.\n\n\"오늘 저녁엔 좀 처져 보이는구나, 프레더릭.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 말했다. \"무슨 일이라도 있느냐?\"\n\n\"무슨 일?\" 그는 한순간 멍하니 쳐다보더니, 다시 머리와 눈을 떨구었다. \"아니, 윌리엄, 아니. 아무 일도 없네.\"\n\n\"네가 좀 매무새를 가다듬도록 마음을 먹는다면, 프레더릭—\"\n\n\"아아, 아아!\" 노인이 다급하게 말했다. \"하지만 그럴 수가 없네. 그럴 수가 없어. 그런 말은 말게. 그건 다 끝난 일일세.\"\n\n마셜시의 아버지는 자기와 친분이 있는 한 수용자가 지나가자 그를 흘끗 보았는데, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '쇠약한 늙은이지요, 이 사람이. 하지만 내 동생입니다, 선생, 내 동생이에요. 천륜의 목소리란 힘이 센 법이지요!' 그러고는 닳아빠진 소매를 잡아 동생을 펌프 손잡이에서 비켜 가게 했다. 형제간의 길잡이요, 철학자요, 벗으로서 그의 인격이 완벽함에 이르는 데 부족한 것은 오직 하나, 동생에게 파멸을 가져다준 것이 아니라 동생을 파멸에서 비켜 가게 인도했더라면 하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"윌리엄, 내 생각엔,\" 그의 다정한 배려의 대상이 말했다. \"내가 피곤한 모양이니, 집에 가서 자야겠네.\"\n\n\"이보게 프레더릭,\" 다른 이가 대꾸했다. \"내가 자넬 붙잡지 않겠네. 나 때문에 자네 마음을 희생하지 말게.\"\n\n\"늦은 시각하며, 후끈한 공기하며, 그리고 세월 탓이겠지.\" 프레더릭이 말했다. \"그런 게 나를 약하게 만드는군.\"\n\n\"이보게 프레더릭,\" 마셜시의 아버지가 대꾸했다. \"자넨 자기 몸을 충분히 돌보고 있다고 생각하나? 자네 생활 습관이 — 이를테면 내 습관만큼이나 — 규칙적이고 질서 잡혀 있다고 생각하나? 방금 말한 그 사소한 별난 버릇 이야기를 다시 들추지 않더라도, 나는 자네가 바람과 운동을 충분히 쐬고 하는지 의심스럽네, 프레더릭. 여기 이 산책로가 있잖나, 언제든 자네가 쓰라고. 어째서 좀 더 규칙적으로 이용하지 않는가?\"\n\n\"하!\" 다른 이가 한숨지었다. \"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\"\n\n\"하지만 그래, 그래, 한다고 해서 소용이 없네, 이보게 프레더릭.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 온화한 지혜로 고집스레 말을 이었다. \"그렇게 동의해 놓고 행동으로 옮기지 않는다면 말이야. 내 경우를 보게, 프레더릭. 나는 일종의 본보기일세. 필요와 세월이 내게 무엇을 해야 하는지 가르쳐 주었지. 하루 중 정해진 시각이면 자넨 산책로에서, 내 방에서, 수위실에서, 신문을 읽거나, 손님을 맞거나, 먹고 마시는 나를 보게 될 걸세. 나는 에이미에게 여러 해 동안 (이를테면) 식사를 정확히 시간 맞춰 해야 한다는 것을 일러 두었네. 에이미는 이런 일들의 중요함을 깨달으며 자랐고, 자네도 그 아이가 얼마나 착한 애인지 알지 않나.\"\n\n동생은 꿈결처럼 터덜터덜 걸으며 다시 한숨만 지을 뿐이었다. \"하! 그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\"\n\n\"이보게,\" 마셜시의 아버지가 동생의 어깨에 손을 얹고 부드럽게 그를 다그치며 말했다 — 부드럽게, 가엾은 사람이 약한 탓이었다. \"방금도 그렇게 말했잖나, 그런 말은 별로 의미가 없네, 프레더릭. 설령 의미가 많다 한들 말이야. 자넬 좀 분발하게 만들 수 있다면 좋으련만, 착한 프레더릭. 자넨 분발할 필요가 있어.\"\n\n\"그래, 윌리엄, 그래. 틀림없이 그렇지.\" 다른 이가 흐릿한 눈을 들어 형의 얼굴을 보며 대꾸했다. \"하지만 난 자네 같지가 않아.\"\n\n마셜시의 아버지는 겸손하게 자기를 낮추는 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"오! 자네도 나처럼 될 수 있네, 이보게 프레더릭. 마음만 먹으면 될 수 있어!\" 그러고는 자기 강함의 아량으로, 무너진 동생을 더는 다그치지 않았다.\n\n일요일 밤이면 으레 그렇듯, 구석구석에서 작별이 한창이었고, 여기저기 어둠 속에서 어떤 가엾은 여인이, 아내나 어머니가, 새로 들어온 수용자와 함께 울고 있었다. 이 아버지 자신도 한때는 그 마당 그늘에서, 자기 가엾은 아내가 울었듯이 울던 시절이 있었다. 그러나 그것은 여러 해 전의 일이었고, 이제 그는 긴 항해 길에 오른 배의 승객 같아서, 뱃멀미에서는 이미 회복했건만 마지막 항구에서 새로 올라탄 더 풋풋한 승객들의 그 나약함이 거슬리는 것이었다. 그는 못마땅함을 드러내며, 울지 않고는 못 견디는 사람들이라면 여기 있을 자격이 없다는 견해를 표하고 싶어 했다. 말로는 아니더라도 태도로는 늘 이런 전체의 화목을 깨뜨리는 짓에 대한 불쾌함을 내비쳤고, 그것이 워낙 잘 알려져 있어서, 위반자들은 그가 곁에 있는 줄 알면 으레 물러서곤 했다.\n\n이 일요일 저녁, 그는 인내와 관용의 태도로 동생을 문까지 배웅했으니, 기분이 부드러워져 너그러이 그 눈물들을 못 본 척해 주려는 마음이었던 것이다. 수위실의 일렁이는 가스등 불빛 아래 여러 수용자들이 몸을 쬐고 있었다. 더러는 방문객을 배웅하는 중이었고, 더러는 방문객이 없어 자물쇠가 자주 돌아가는 것을 지켜보며 자기들끼리, 또 치버리 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 이 아버지의 등장은 물론 한바탕 소동을 일으켰고, 치버리 씨는 (다소 짧게나마) 열쇠를 든 채 모자에 손을 대며, 그가 그런대로 잘 지내기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"고맙네, 치버리, 아주 잘 지내네. 자넨 어떤가?\"\n\n치버리 씨는 낮게 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"오! 저야 멀쩡합니다요.\" 이것이 그가 조금 뚱해 있을 때 안부 인사에 응하는 일반적인 방식이었다.\n\n\"오늘 영 존이 나를 보러 왔더군, 치버리. 아주 말쑥해 보이더라고, 정말이지.\"\n\n그래, 치버리 씨도 들어 알고 있었다. 다만, 그 녀석이 거기다 그렇게 돈을 많이 들이지 않았으면 하는 게 자기 바람이라고 고백해야겠다고 했다. 그래 봐야 그 녀석한테 돌아오는 게 뭐냔 말이다. 돌아오는 거라곤 속만 끓는 일뿐이었다. 그런 거야 어디서든 거저 얻을 수 있는 것이고.\n\n\"속이 어째서 끓는다는 건가, 치버리?\" 자애로운 아버지가 물었다.\n\n\"별것 아닙니다.\" 치버리 씨가 대꾸했다. \"신경 쓰지 마십쇼. 프레더릭 씨 나가십니까?\"\n\n\"그래, 치버리, 내 동생이 집에 가서 자려고 하네. 피곤한 데다 몸이 좀 좋지 않아서. 조심하게, 프레더릭, 조심해. 잘 가게, 이보게 프레더릭!\"\n\n형과 악수하고, 수위실 안의 사람들에게 기름때 묻은 모자에 손을 대어 인사하고서, 프레더릭은 치버리 씨가 열어 준 문으로 느릿느릿 발을 끌며 나갔다. 마셜시의 아버지는 동생이 아무 일도 당하지 않게 하려는, 우월한 존재다운 다정한 노심초사를 내보였다.\n\n\"미안하지만 문을 잠깐 열어 두게나, 치버리, 저 사람이 통로를 지나 계단을 내려가는 것을 좀 봐야겠네. 조심하게, 프레더릭! (저 사람은 아주 쇠약하다네.) 계단 조심하게! (저 사람은 정신이 워낙 없어서.) 길 건널 때 조심하게, 프레더릭. (저 사람이 마음대로 떠도는 건 정말 마음에 들지가 않아, 차에 치이기가 어찌나 쉬운지 말이야.)\"\n\n이런 말과 함께, 갖은 불안한 의구심과 노심초사의 보호자다운 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채, 그는 수위실에 모인 사람들에게로 시선을 돌렸으니, 자기 동생이 자물쇠 안에 갇혀 지내지 못하는 처지가 가엾다는 뜻을 어찌나 노골적으로 내비쳤던지, 모여 있던 수용자들 사이에 그런 취지의 의견이 한 바퀴 돌았다.\n\n그러나 그는 그것을 무조건 동의하며 받아들이지는 않았다. 오히려 그는 이렇게 말했다. 아니올시다, 여러분, 아니에요. 오해는 하지 마시길. 내 동생 프레더릭이 많이 망가진 건 틀림없고, 나로서는 (마셜시의 아버지로서는) 저 사람이 이 담장 안에서 안전하다고 알고 있는 편이 더 마음 편할 수도 있겠지요. 그래도 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 여기서 여러 해 동안 살아남으려면 어떤 자질의 결합이 필요하다는 점입니다 — 고매한 자질이라고는 하지 않겠으나, 어떤 자질, 어떤 도덕적 자질 말이지요. 그렇다면 내 동생 프레더릭에게 그런 독특한 자질의 결합이 있겠습니까? 여러분, 저 사람은 더없이 훌륭한 사람이요, 더없이 온화하고 다정하며 존경할 만한 사람으로, 어린아이 같은 천진함을 지녔지요. 하지만 다른 대부분의 자리에는 어울리지 않더라도, 이 자리에는 맞겠습니까? 아니지요. 나는 자신 있게 말합니다, 아니에요! 그리고 말하건대, 프레더릭이 지금 같은 자발적인 처지 말고 다른 어떤 처지로 여기 있게 되는 일은 결코 없기를 빕니다! 여러분, 이 칼리지에 들어와 오래 머무는 자라면 누구든, 숱한 일을 견뎌 내고 숱한 일에서 헤쳐 나올 만한 성격의 강인함이 있어야 합니다. 내 사랑하는 동생 프레더릭이 그런 사람입니까? 아니지요. 여러분도 보시다시피 이렇게나 짓눌려 있습니다. 불운이 저 사람을 짓눌러 버렸지요. 저 사람은 이런 곳에 오래 있으면서도 자존감을 지키고 스스로 신사임을 의식할 만한 반발력이, 탄력이 모자랍니다. 프레더릭에게는 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 그런 처지에서 받을 법한 세심한 작은 호의와 — 그리고 — 그리고 — 정표들 속에서, 인간 본성의 선함을, 한 공동체로서 수용자들을 살아 움직이게 하는 그 고결한 정신을 알아보면서도, 동시에 그것이 자기를 깎아내리는 것도 아니요 신사로서의 권위를 떨어뜨리는 것도 아님을 알아볼 만한 힘이 없습니다. 여러분, 신의 가호가 있기를!\n\n이것이 그가 수위실의 사람들에게 그 자리를 교화하고 훈계 삼아 베푼 설교였고, 그러고 나서 그는 다시 누르스름한 마당으로 들어서서, 코트도 없이 실내복만 걸친 수용자를 지나, 신발도 없이 해변용 슬리퍼만 신은 수용자를 지나, 골덴 반바지를 입고서 근심이라곤 없는 뚱뚱한 채소 장수 수용자를 지나, 단추 떨어진 검은 옷차림에 희망이라곤 없는 비쩍 마른 사무원 수용자를 지나, 제 초라한 계단을 올라 제 초라한 방으로 자기만의 가엾고 초라한 위엄을 지니고 돌아갔다.\n\n그곳에는 그의 저녁상이 차려져 있었고, 낡은 회색 가운이 난롯가 의자 등받이에 걸린 채 그를 기다리고 있었다. 딸은 작은 기도서를 주머니에 넣고서 — 모든 죄수와 포로에게 자비가 내리기를 기도하던 참이었을까! — 일어나 그를 맞았다.\n\n그래, 삼촌은 댁에 가셨고요? 그녀는 그의 코트를 갈아입히고 검은 벨벳 모자를 건네며 물었다. 그래, 삼촌은 집에 갔지. 아버지는 산책이 즐거우셨어요? 글쎄, 별로다, 에이미. 별로야. 아니라고! 어디 편찮으신 거예요?\n\n딸이 그토록 다정하게 그의 의자 위로 몸을 기울인 채 뒤에 서 있는데, 그는 눈을 떨군 채 난롯불을 바라보았다. 부끄러움의 기색과도 같은 불안이 그를 슬그머니 엄습했고, 이윽고 그가 입을 열었을 때 그것은 두서없고 거북한 투였다.\n\n\"무언가가, 나는 — 흠! — 무언지는 모르겠다만, 치버리한테 틀어진 일이 있는 모양이야. 오늘 밤엔 — 하! — 평소처럼 그렇게 살갑고 친절하지가 않더구나. 그게 — 흠! — 사소한 일이긴 하다만, 영 마음에 걸리는구나, 얘야. 잊을 수가 없거든,\" 그는 손을 이리저리 뒤집으며 유심히 들여다보았다. \"나 같은 처지로 살자면 — 흠! — 하루에도 매시간 무언가를 이 사람들에게 의지할 수밖에 없다는 것을 말이다, 가엾게도.\"\n\n딸의 팔은 그의 어깨에 놓여 있었으나, 그가 말하는 동안 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보지 않았다. 고개를 숙인 채 다른 쪽을 보았다.\n\n\"나는 — 흠! — 도무지 모르겠구나, 에이미, 무엇이 치버리의 비위를 거슬렀는지. 평소엔 그렇게나 — 그렇게나 친절하고 깍듯하던 사람인데. 그런데 오늘 밤엔 영 — 영 퉁명스럽더구나, 나한테. 거기 다른 사람들도 있는 데서 말이야! 아니, 맙소사! 내가 만약 치버리와 그 동료 간수들의 후원과 인정을 잃기라도 하면, 나는 여기서 굶어 죽을 수도 있어.\" 그는 말하는 내내 두 손을 판막처럼 폈다 쥐었다 했는데, 그러는 동안에도 그 부끄러움의 기색을 어찌나 의식했던지, 자기 말뜻을 스스로 아는 데서 움츠러들었다.\n\n\"나는 — 하! — 무엇 때문인지 도무지 알 수가 없구나. 정말이지 그 원인이 무엇인지 짐작도 못하겠어. 한때 여기에 잭슨이라는 자가 있었지, 잭슨이라는 이름의 간수였단다 (네가 그를 기억할 성싶지는 않구나, 얘야, 그땐 네가 아주 어렸으니까), 그런데 — 흠! — 그에게 동생이 하나 있었는데, 이 — 젊은 동생이 우리 가운데 한 사람의 — 적어도 구애까지 한 건 아니고 — 흠모했던 거지 — 정중히 흠모했던 거야 — 어느 꽤 명망 있는 수용자의 — 딸이 아니라 누이를 말이다. 정말이지 대단히 명망 있는 분이었어. 이름은 마틴 대위였단다. 그가 나한테 의논을 청하기를, 자기 딸 — 누이가 그 다른 동생한테 너무 — 하! — 너무 솔직하게 굴어서 간수인 형의 비위를 거스를 위험을 무릅쓸 필요가 있겠느냐는 거였지. 마틴 대위는 신사요 명예를 아는 사람이라, 나는 먼저 그에게 그 자신의 — 자기 의견을 말해 보라고 했단다. 그러자 마틴 대위는 (군에서 크게 존경받던 분이지) 망설임 없이 말하기를, 자기 — 흠! — 누이가 그 젊은이를 너무 분명하게 이해해 줄 필요는 없을 듯하고, 아버지를 — 아니, 오라비를 — 위해서 그를 — 이끌어 가도 좋겠다고 하더구나 — '이끌어 간다'가 마틴 대위의 정확한 표현이었는지는 잘 모르겠다만, 실은 그가 그를 봐 준다고 했던 것 같구나. 내가 어쩌다 이 이야기로 빠졌는지 모르겠구나. 치버리를 도무지 이해할 수가 없어서 그랬나 보다. 하지만 그 둘 사이에 무슨 연관이 있는지는, 나도 모르겠—\"\n\n딸이 그의 말을 듣는 고통을 차마 견딜 수 없는 듯, 그의 목소리는 잦아들었고, 어느새 그녀의 손이 슬그머니 그의 입술로 올라가 있었다. 잠시 동안 죽은 듯한 침묵과 고요가 흘렀다. 그는 의자에 움츠린 채로 있었고, 그녀는 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 그의 어깨에 머리를 묻은 채로 있었다.\n\n그의 저녁식사는 난로 위 냄비에서 익고 있었고, 그녀가 몸을 움직였을 때 그것은 그것을 식탁 위에 차려 내기 위함이었다. 그는 늘 앉던 자리에 앉았고, 그녀도 제자리에 앉았으며, 그는 식사를 시작했다. 두 사람은 아직 서로를 바라보지 않았다. 그는 조금씩 시작했으니, 나이프와 포크를 소리 나게 내려놓고, 물건을 거칠게 집어 들고, 빵에 화풀이라도 하듯 베어 물고, 그 밖에도 그런 식으로 자기가 심사가 틀어졌음을 내보였다. 마침내 그는 접시를 밀어내고는, 더없이 기묘하게 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 소리로 큰 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"내가 먹든 굶든 그게 무슨 대수냐? 나처럼 시들어 버린 인생이 지금 끝나든, 다음 주에 끝나든, 내년에 끝나든 그게 무슨 대수란 말이냐? 내가 누구한테 무슨 값어치가 있다고? 적선과 남은 음식이나 얻어먹는 가엾은 죄수, 더럽고 욕된 비렁뱅이일 뿐인데!\"\n\n\"아버지, 아버지!\" 그가 일어서자 그녀는 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그를 향해 두 손을 들어 올렸다.\n\n\"에이미,\" 그는 격하게 떨면서, 미친 사람처럼 그녀를 사납게 바라보며, 억눌린 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"내 말 좀 들어 보아라, 네 어미가 보았던 나를 네가 볼 수 있었다면, 너는 이 우리 창살 사이로만 봐 온 그 인간이 나라고는 믿지 못할 게다. 나는 젊었고, 재주가 있었고, 잘생겼고, 떳떳한 독립된 몸이었어 — 맹세코 그랬다, 얘야! — 사람들이 나를 찾아왔고, 나를 부러워했지. 나를 부러워했단 말이다!\"\n\n\"가엾은 아버지!\" 그녀는 그가 허공에 휘젓는 떨리는 팔을 끌어내리려 했으나, 그는 뿌리치며 그녀의 손을 밀어냈다.\n\n\"그 시절의 내 초상화 한 장만 있었더라면, 아무리 서툴게 그린 것일지라도, 너는 그것을 자랑스러워했을 게다, 그것을 자랑스러워했을 게야. 하지만 그런 건 없구나. 자, 나를 본보기로 삼아라! 어느 누구도,\" 그는 초췌하게 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"제가 번성하고 존경받던 시절의 그 한 조각만이라도 간직하는 데 실패하지 말지어다. 제 자식들에게 제가 어떤 사람이었는지를 알 단서를 남겨 줄지어다. 내가 죽었을 때 내 얼굴이 오래전에 떠나간 그 모습으로 가라앉지 않는다면 — 그런 일이 있다고들 하더라만, 나는 모르겠다 — 내 자식들은 나를 한 번도 보지 못한 셈이 되겠지.\"\n\n\"아버지, 아버지!\"\n\n\"오, 나를 경멸해라, 나를 경멸해! 나한테서 눈길을 돌리고, 내 말을 듣지 말고, 나를 막고, 나 때문에 부끄러워하고, 나 때문에 울어라 — 너마저도, 에이미! 그렇게 해, 그렇게 하란 말이야! 나도 나 자신한테 그러는걸! 나는 이제 굳어 버렸어, 너무 깊이 가라앉아서 이젠 그런 것조차 오래 마음에 두지 못한다.\"\n\n\"가엾은 아버지, 사랑하는 아버지, 제 마음의 보배!\" 그녀는 두 팔로 그에게 매달려, 그를 다시 의자에 주저앉히고, 들어 올린 그의 팔을 붙잡아 자기 목에 두르려 했다.\n\n\"여기 그대로 두세요, 아버지. 저를 보세요, 아버지, 입을 맞춰 주세요, 아버지! 단 한순간만이라도 제 생각만 해 주세요, 아버지!\"\n\n그래도 그는 여전히 그 사나운 투로 말을 이었으나, 그것은 차츰 비참한 흐느낌으로 무너져 갔다.\n\n\"그래도 나는 여기서 어느 정도 존경을 받고 있어. 나는 그것에 어느 만큼 맞서 왔지. 나는 아주 짓밟히지는 않았어. 나가서 물어봐라, 이곳의 으뜸가는 사람이 누구냐고. 네 아버지라고 할 게다. 나가서 물어봐라, 누가 결코 함부로 대접받지 않고, 누가 늘 어느 정도 정중한 대접을 받느냐고. 네 아버지라고 할 게다. 나가서 물어봐라, 여기서 (여기여야지, 다른 데서는 있을 수 없는 일이란 걸 안다) 어떤 장례가 이 문을 나선 어떤 장례보다도 더 많은 이야깃거리가 되고, 어쩌면 더 많은 슬픔을 자아내겠느냐고. 네 아버지의 장례라고 할 게다. 자, 그렇다면. 에이미! 에이미! 네 아버지가 그렇게 모두에게 경멸받는단 말이냐? 그를 건져 줄 것이 아무것도 없단 말이냐? 너는 그의 파멸과 쇠락 말고는 그를 기억할 게 아무것도 없겠느냐? 그가 가고 나면, 가엾이 버려진 그가 가고 나면, 너는 그에게 아무런 애정도 품을 수 없겠느냐?\"\n\n그는 자기 자신을 향한 신파조의 연민에 겨워 눈물을 터뜨렸고, 마침내 그녀가 자기를 끌어안고 보살피게 내버려 둔 채, 회색 머리를 그녀의 뺨에 기대고 자기 비참함을 한탄했다. 이윽고 그는 한탄의 화제를 바꾸어, 그녀가 안아 주는 동안 두 손으로 그녀를 끌어안고 외쳤다. 오, 에이미, 어미 없는 가엾은 내 자식! 오, 그녀가 나를 위해 정성을 다하고 고생하는 것을 보아 온 그 세월이여! 그러다 그는 다시 자기 이야기로 돌아가, 네가 사라진 옛 시절의 나를 알았더라면 나를 훨씬 더 사랑했을 거라고, 또 너를 자기 딸로서 자랑스러워할 신사에게 시집보냈을 거라고, 또 (이 대목에서 그는 다시 울었다) 네가 네 말을 타고 아비인 내 곁에서 먼저 말을 달렸을 것이고, 또 군중이 (그가 사실상 가리킨 것은 그날 주머니에 든 십이 실링을 자기에게 준 그 사람들이었다) 먼지 이는 길을 정중하게 터덜터덜 걸어갔을 거라고, 힘없이 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n이렇게, 이제는 으스대고 이제는 절망하며, 어느 쪽 발작에서나 감옥의 부패가 몸에 밴 죄수요, 감옥의 불결함이 영혼의 결까지 스며든 자가 되어, 그는 자기 타락한 처지를 그 다정한 자식에게 드러내 보였다. 그가 굴욕의 세세한 모습을 다른 어느 누구에게도 보인 적은 없었다. 그날 일요일 밤, 수위실에서 그가 한 연설을 두고 자기 방에서 웃어 대던 수용자들은, 자기들이 마셜시라는 그 어둑한 화랑 속에 얼마나 처연한 그림 한 점을 가지고 있는지 거의 알지 못했다.\n\n옛적에 한 고전적인 딸이 있어 — 어쩌면 — 제 어미가 자기를 보살폈듯이 감옥에 갇힌 제 아비를 보살폈다고들 한다. 리틀 도릿은, 비록 영웅적이지 않은 현대의 핏줄을 타고난 한낱 영국 처녀였으나, 그보다 훨씬 더한 일을 해냈으니, 아버지의 시든 마음을 제 순결한 가슴에 기대게 하여 위로하고, 그 모진 굶주림의 세월 내내 마르거나 시드는 법 없는 사랑과 정성의 샘물을 그 마음에 흘려보낸 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 그를 달랬다. 자기가 불효했거나 불효한 것처럼 보였다면 용서해 달라고 빌었다. 하늘이 아시듯 진실로, 설령 그가 운명의 총아여서 온 세상이 그를 떠받든다 한들 지금보다 더 그를 우러를 수는 없을 거라고 말했다. 그의 눈물이 마르고, 그가 더는 약하게 흐느끼지 않으며, 그 부끄러움의 기색에서 벗어나 평소의 태도를 되찾자, 그녀는 그가 남긴 저녁식사를 새로 차렸고, 그의 곁에 앉아 그가 먹고 마시는 것을 보며 기뻐했다. 이제 그는 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 낡은 회색 가운을 걸친 채 다시금 도량 넓은 모습으로 앉아 있었으니, 누군가 수용자가 조언을 구하러 들렀더라면, 그는 위대한 도덕가 체스터필드 경처럼, 또는 마셜시의 예법을 관장하는 의전관처럼 처신했을 것이다.\n\n그의 주의를 붙들어 두려고 그녀는 그와 옷가지 이야기를 나누었다. 그러자 그는 기꺼이 말하기를, 그래, 정말이지, 네가 말한 그 셔츠들이라면 더없이 반갑겠구나, 지금 것들은 다 닳은 데다 기성품이라 도통 몸에 맞은 적이 없으니 말이다, 했다. 이야기에 빠져들고 기분도 그런대로 풀린 그는, 이번엔 문 뒤에 걸린 자기 코트로 그녀의 주의를 돌리며 한마디 했으니, 이곳의 아버지가 팔꿈치가 다 해진 차림으로 자식들 틈을 다닌다면, 가뜩이나 추레해지기 쉬운 그 아이들에게 좋지 않은 본보기가 될 거라는 것이었다. 그는 자기 구두 뒤축을 두고도 농을 했으나, 넥타이 이야기에 이르러서는 진지해져서, 그녀에게 형편이 되거든 자기에게 새것을 하나 사 주라고 약속받았다.\n\n그가 편안히 시가를 다 피우는 동안, 그녀는 잠자리를 펴고 그가 쉴 수 있도록 작은 방을 정돈했다. 그러고 나서 늦은 시각과 격한 감정 탓에 지친 그는 의자에서 일어나, 그녀를 축복하고 잘 자라고 인사했다. 그러는 내내 그는 그녀의 옷이며, 그녀의 신발이며, 그녀가 무엇 하나 필요한 것이 있는지는 단 한 번도 생각하지 않았다. 이 세상 누구도, 그녀 자신을 빼고는, 그토록 그녀의 궁핍에 무심할 수는 없었으리라.\n\n그는 \"잘 자라, 내 사랑. 잘 자거라, 얘야!\" 하며 여러 번 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n그러나 그녀의 여린 가슴은 그가 보여 준 모습에 너무도 깊이 상처 입어, 그녀는 그를 혼자 두기가 꺼려졌으니, 그가 다시 한탄하고 절망할까 두려웠던 것이다. \"아버지, 저는 안 피곤해요. 아버지가 자리에 드시면 곧 다시 와서 곁에 앉아 있을게요.\"\n\n그는 보호자 같은 태도로, 혼자 있기가 적적하냐고 물었다.\n\n\"네, 아버지.\"\n\n\"그러면 꼭 다시 오너라, 얘야.\"\n\n\"아주 조용히 있을게요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"내 생각은 말거라, 얘야.\" 그는 너그러이 흔쾌히 허락하며 말했다. \"꼭 다시 오너라.\"\n\n그녀가 돌아왔을 때 그는 깜빡 졸고 있는 듯했고, 그녀는 그를 깨우지 않으려고 사위어 가는 불을 아주 조용히 추슬렀다. 그러나 그는 그 소리를 듣고는, 거기 누구냐고 소리쳤다.\n\n\"에이미일 뿐이에요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"에이미, 얘야, 이리 오너라. 너한테 할 말이 있다.\"\n\n그녀가 그의 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 가까이 가져가자, 그는 낮은 침상에서 몸을 조금 일으켰고, 자기 손을 그녀의 두 손 사이에 두었다. 오! 그때 그의 안에서는 한 집안의 아버지와 마셜시의 아버지가 둘 다 강하게 자리하고 있었다.\n\n\"얘야, 너는 여기서 고생스러운 삶을 살아왔구나. 벗도 없고, 즐길 거리도 없고, 근심만 많았겠지, 안 그러냐?\"\n\n\"그런 건 생각지 마세요, 아버지. 저는 한 번도 그런 생각 안 해요.\"\n\n\"내 처지를 너도 알지, 에이미. 나는 너를 위해 많은 걸 해 주지 못했다. 하지만 내가 할 수 있었던 건 다 했어.\"\n\n\"네, 가엾은 아버지.\" 그녀는 그에게 입맞추며 대꾸했다. \"알아요, 알아요.\"\n\n\"내가 여기서 산 게 스물세 해째구나.\" 그는 숨을 한 번 멈칫했는데, 그것은 흐느낌이라기보다는 억누를 수 없는 자기만족의 소리, 고결한 의식이 순간적으로 터져 나온 것에 가까웠다. \"그게 내가 자식들을 위해 할 수 있었던 전부였고 — 나는 그걸 해냈다. 에이미, 얘야, 너는 셋 중에서 단연 가장 사랑받는 아이야. 나는 누구보다 너를 마음에 두어 왔단다 — 너를 위해 내가 한 일이라면 무엇이든, 얘야, 나는 기꺼이, 군말 없이 해 온 거다.\"\n\n모든 마음과 모든 신비의 단서를 쥐고 있는 그 지혜만이, 사람이 — 특히 이 사람처럼 영락한 사람이 — 어느 정도까지 자기 자신을 속일 수 있는지 분명히 알 것이다. 지금 이 자리에서는 이것으로 족하다. 그가 굴욕의 삶을, 그 비참함이 그토록 무겁게 떨어졌고 오직 그 사랑만이 그를 지금의 그로나마 살아남게 해 준, 그 헌신적인 자식에게 일종의 유산처럼 물려주고 나서, 젖은 속눈썹으로, 평온하게, 어딘지 위엄 있는 모습으로 자리에 누웠다는 것으로.\n\n그 자식은 아무런 의심도 품지 않았고, 스스로에게 아무런 물음도 던지지 않았으니, 그의 머리 둘레에 후광이 어린 모습을 보는 것만으로도 너무나 만족스러웠기 때문이다. 가엾은 분, 착한 분, 더없이 진실하고 더없이 자상하고 더없이 사랑스러운 분, 이것이 그녀가 그를 재우며 그에게 건넬 수 있는 유일한 말이었다.\n\n그날 밤 내내 그녀는 그의 곁을 떠나지 않았다. 마치 자기 다정함으로도 좀처럼 갚을 수 없는 잘못이라도 그에게 저지른 듯, 그녀는 그가 잠든 곁에 앉아, 이따금 숨을 죽인 채 살그머니 그에게 입맞추고, 다정한 애칭으로 속삭이며 그를 불렀다. 이따금은 사위어 가는 난롯불을 가리지 않으려고 곁으로 비켜서서, 그 불빛이 그의 잠든 얼굴 위로 떨어질 때 그를 지켜보며, 그가 번성하고 행복하던 시절의 모습을, 그가 그 끔찍한 때에 다시 한번 그 모습이 되어 볼지도 모른다고 상상하며 그녀를 그토록 가슴 아프게 했던 그 모습을 지금 조금이라도 닮았는지 궁금해했다. 그 끔찍한 때를 생각하며 그녀는 다시 그의 침상 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 기도했다. \"오, 그의 목숨을 지켜 주소서! 오, 그를 제 곁에 살려 주소서! 오, 사랑하는, 오래 고통받아 온, 불운하고, 너무도 달라져 버린, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 제 아버지를 굽어살피소서!\"\n\n그를 지켜 주고 북돋아 줄 아침이 오고서야 비로소, 그녀는 그에게 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 그 작은 방을 떠났다. 그녀가 살그머니 계단을 내려와 텅 빈 마당을 가로질러, 자기 높다란 다락방으로 살금살금 올라갔을 때, 맑은 아침 담장 너머로 연기 없는 지붕들과 멀리 시골의 언덕들이 보였다. 그녀가 가만히 창을 열고 동쪽으로 감옥 마당을 내려다보니, 담장 위 쇠꼬챙이들의 끝이 붉게 물들었다가, 이내 태양이 불꽃처럼 하늘로 솟아오를 때 그 위에 음울한 자줏빛 무늬를 드리웠다. 쇠꼬챙이가 그토록 날카롭고 잔인해 보인 적도, 창살이 그토록 무거워 보인 적도, 감옥의 공간이 그토록 음침하고 옹색해 보인 적도 없었다. 그녀는 굽이치는 강 위로 떠오르는 해를, 드넓은 바다 위로 떠오르는 해를, 풍요로운 들판 위로 떠오르는 해를, 새들이 깨어나고 나무들이 사각이는 큰 숲 위로 떠오르는 해를 떠올렸다. 그러고는, 자기 아버지가 스물세 해째 갇혀 있는, 해가 떠오른 그 산 무덤을 내려다보며, 슬픔과 연민이 북받쳐 이렇게 말했다. \"아니야, 아니야, 나는 평생 그분을 본 적이 없어!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 20. Moving in Society\n\n만약 젊은 존 치버리가 가문의 자긍심을 풍자할 마음과 능력이 있었더라면, 그는 보복의 본보기를 찾으러 사랑하는 여인의 집안 밖으로 나갈 필요가 전혀 없었을 것이다. 그는 그것을 그 늠름한 오라비와 그 도도한 누이에게서 넘치도록 발견했을 테니까. 천박한 경험에 그토록 깊이 절어 있으면서도 가문의 이름만큼은 그토록 거만하게 의식하고, 가장 가난한 이에게서까지 구걸하거나 빌리고, 아무의 빵이든 먹고, 아무의 돈이든 쓰고, 아무의 잔에서든 마시고 나서 깨뜨려 버리는 데 그토록 거리낌이 없는 그들 말이다. 그들의 삶의 비루한 사실들을 그려 내고, 그러면서도 그들이 내내 가문의 고귀함이라는 해골 유령을 불러내어 자기네 은인들을 겁주게 만드는 모습을 그렸더라면, 젊은 존은 일급의 풍자가가 되었을 것이다.\n\n팁은 당구장 점수원이 됨으로써 제 자유를 유망하게 써먹었다. 그는 자신이 어떻게 풀려났는가 하는 문제에 너무도 무관심했으므로, 클레넘이 굳이 플로니시 씨의 머릿속에 그 문제를 새겨 넣느라 애쓸 필요조차 거의 없었다. 누가 그에게 그런 호의를 베풀었든, 그는 그 호의를 자신의 인사와 함께 아주 선뜻 받아들였고, 그것으로 끝이었다. 이렇게 손쉬운 조건으로 문을 나선 그는 당구장 점수원이 되었고, 이제는 이따금 (헌) 초록색 뉴마켓 코트에 (새) 번쩍이는 깃과 환한 단추를 달고서 작은 구주희 마당을 들여다보며, 수감자들의 맥주를 마셨다.\n\n이 신사의 헐거운 성격 가운데 한 가지 단단하고 흔들림 없는 점은, 그가 누이 에이미를 존경하고 흠모한다는 것이었다. 그 감정이 그로 하여금 그녀에게 단 한순간의 불편함이라도 덜어 주게 하거나, 그녀를 위해 무슨 자제나 불편을 감수하게 한 적은 결코 없었다. 그러나 그 사랑에 마셜시의 얼룩이 묻어 있긴 했어도, 그는 그녀를 사랑했다. 그 똑같은 짙은 마셜시의 냄새는, 그가 그녀가 아버지에게 제 삶을 희생하고 있음을 또렷이 알아보면서도, 정작 그녀가 자기 자신을 위해 무언가 해 주었다는 생각은 전혀 하지 못한다는 점에서도 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n이 기개 넘치는 젊은이와 그의 누이가 수감자들을 주눅 들게 하려고 가문의 해골을 체계적으로 끄집어내기 시작한 것이 언제부터인지, 이 이야기는 정확히 짚어 말할 수 없다. 아마도 그들이 수감소의 자선으로 끼니를 때우기 시작한 무렵쯤일 것이다. 확실한 것은, 그들이 더 궁색하고 더 곤궁해질수록 그 해골이 무덤에서 더 거창하게 솟아 나왔다는 것, 그리고 무언가 특별히 초라한 일이 닥쳐올 낌새가 있을 때면 그 해골이 언제나 가장 섬뜩한 위세로 나타났다는 것이다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 월요일 아침에 늦었다. 아버지가 늦잠을 잤고, 그 뒤에는 아침 식사를 준비하고 방을 정돈해야 했기 때문이다. 그러나 그날은 일하러 나갈 약속이 없었으므로, 매기의 도움을 받아 아버지에 관한 모든 것을 제대로 챙기고, 그가 신문을 읽으러 (스무 걸음쯤 되는) 아침 산책을 나서서 커피점으로 가는 것을 배웅할 때까지 그의 곁에 머물렀다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 보닛을 쓰고 밖으로 나섰는데, 사실은 훨씬 더 일찍 나가고 싶어 안달이 나 있던 터였다. 그녀가 수위실을 지날 때면 늘 그렇듯 잡담이 뚝 그쳤고, 토요일 밤에 들어온 어느 수감자는 더 노련한 수감자의 팔꿈치로부터 \"조심해. 그녀가 왔다네!\" 하는 귀띔을 받았다.\n\n그녀는 언니를 만나고 싶었지만, 크리플스 씨네 집에 돌아 들어가 보니 언니와 삼촌이 둘 다 일하기로 되어 있는 극장에 가 있었다. 오는 길에 이럴 가능성을 미리 생각해 두었고, 그런 경우라면 그들을 따라가겠다고 마음먹어 두었던지라, 그녀는 다시 극장을 향해 길을 나섰다. 극장은 강의 그쪽 편에 있었고 그리 멀지 않았다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 금광에 관해 모르는 것만큼이나 극장이 돌아가는 방식에 대해서도 무지했다. 그래서 그녀가 어느 은밀한 문 쪽으로 안내받았을 때 — 묘하게도 밤새도록 잠들지 않는 듯한 분위기를 풍기며, 마치 제 모습을 부끄러워하여 골목에 숨어 있는 것처럼 보이는 그 문으로 — 그녀는 다가가기를 망설였다. 게다가 모자를 아주 이상하게 쓴, 말끔히 면도한 신사 대여섯 명이 그 문 주위를 어슬렁거리고 있는 모습이 그녀를 더욱 머뭇거리게 했는데, 그들은 영락없이 수감자들처럼 보였다. 바로 그 닮은 점에 안심하여 그들에게 도릿 양이 있는 곳을 물었더니, 그들은 길을 비켜 주어 그녀가 어두운 복도로 들어가게 해 주었다. 그곳은 무엇보다도 꺼져 버린 커다랗고 음울한 등불 속 같았는데, 거기서 그녀는 멀리서 들려오는 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리를 들을 수 있었다. 바람을 쐬어야 할 필요가 너무도 절실해 온몸에 푸른곰팡이가 핀 듯한 사내 하나가, 거미처럼 한쪽 구석의 구멍에서 그 어두운 곳을 지켜보고 앉아 있다가, 가장 먼저 지나가는 숙녀나 신사 편에 도릿 양에게 전갈을 올려 보내 주겠다고 말했다. 가장 먼저 지나간 숙녀는 악보 두루마리를 토시 안에 반쯤 넣고 반쯤 내놓은 채였는데, 온몸이 어찌나 헝클어진 꼴이던지 그녀를 다림질해 주는 것이 친절한 일이 되겠다 싶을 정도였다. 그러나 그녀는 무척 마음씨가 좋아서 \"이리 와요, 내가 금방 도릿 양을 찾아 줄게요\" 하고 말했고, 도릿 양의 동생은 그녀를 따라갔다. 어둠 속에서 한 걸음 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리에 점점 더 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n마침내 그들은 먼지투성이의 미로 속으로 들어섰다. 그곳에는 수많은 사람들이 서로 부딪치며 넘어질 듯 뒤엉켜 있었고, 들보며 칸막이며 벽돌 벽이며 밧줄이며 도르래며, 도무지 형용할 수 없는 형체들이 어찌나 어지럽게 뒤섞여 있던지, 그리고 가스등 불빛과 햇빛이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이던지, 마치 우주라는 무늬의 뒤편으로 잘못 들어선 것만 같았다. 혼자 남겨진 리틀 도릿은 시시각각 누군가에게 부딪히며 완전히 어리둥절해 있다가, 언니의 목소리를 들었다.\n\n\"아니, 세상에, 에이미, 대체 무슨 일로 여기까지 온 거야?\"\n\n\"언니를 보고 싶었어, 패니 언니. 내일은 하루 종일 일하러 나가야 하고, 언니는 오늘 하루 종일 일에 매여 있을지도 모른다는 걸 알았으니까, 그래서 생각했어 —\"\n\n\"그렇지만 네가 무대 뒤로 들어올 생각을 하다니, 에이미! 정말 별일이네!\" 언니가 그다지 살가운 환영의 어조가 아니게 이렇게 말하면서, 그녀를 미로 가운데서도 좀 더 트인 곳으로 데려갔다. 그곳에는 갖가지 금빛 의자와 탁자가 한데 쌓여 있었고, 젊은 아가씨 여럿이 닥치는 대로 아무 데나 걸터앉아 재잘거리고 있었다. 이 아가씨들은 하나같이 다림질이 필요해 보였고, 재잘거리면서도 하나같이 묘하게도 사방을 두리번거리는 버릇이 있었다.\n\n자매가 이곳에 막 다다랐을 때, 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 단조로운 표정의 소년 하나가 왼쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조용히 좀 하세요, 숙녀분들!\" 하고는 사라졌다. 그 직후에, 긴 검은 머리를 잔뜩 늘어뜨린 활기찬 신사 하나가 오른쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조용히 좀 해요, 그대들!\" 하고는 역시 사라졌다.\n\n\"네가 이런 무대 사람들 틈에 끼어 있다는 발상은, 에이미, 정말이지 내가 도무지 상상조차 못 할 일이었어!\" 언니가 말했다. \"대체 어떻게 여기까지 들어왔니?\"\n\n\"나도 몰라. 언니한테 내가 여기 있다고 전해 준 그 숙녀분이 친절하게도 나를 데리고 들어와 줬어.\"\n\n\"꼭 너희같이 조용하고 자그마한 것들이 그래! 어디든 길을 찾아 들어가더라니까. 나는 세상 물정을 훨씬 더 잘 아는데도, 에이미, 그렇게는 못 했을 거야.\"\n\n그녀가 다른 식구들 같은 위대하고 현명한 경험은 없는, 그저 수수하고 살림이나 하는 자그마한 아이라고 못 박아 두는 것은 집안의 관례였고, 그것을 집안의 법도로 삼는 것이었다. 이 집안의 허구는, 그녀가 해 주는 봉사에 맞서 집안이 제 자존심을 내세우는 방식이었다. 그 봉사를 너무 대수롭게 여기지 않으려는 것이었다.\n\n\"자! 그래 무슨 생각으로 온 거야, 에이미? 당연히 나에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있어서 온 거겠지?\" 패니가 말했다. 그녀는 자기보다 두세 살 어린 동생을 마치 편견에 찬 자기 할머니라도 되는 양 대고 말했다.\n\n\"별건 아니야. 그렇지만 언니가 그 팔찌를 준 숙녀분 얘기를 해 준 뒤로, 패니 언니 —\"\n\n단조로운 소년이 왼쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조심하세요, 숙녀분들!\" 하고는 사라졌다. 검은 머리의 활기찬 신사가 그만큼이나 느닷없이 오른쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조심해요, 그대들!\" 하고는 역시 사라졌다. 그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 모두 일어나 뒤로 치맛자락을 털어 펴기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래, 에이미?\" 패니가 다른 이들이 하는 대로 따라 하며 말했다. \"무슨 말을 하려던 거야?\"\n\n\"언니가 보여 준 그 팔찌를 어떤 숙녀분이 줬다고 한 뒤로, 패니 언니, 나는 언니 일이 영 마음이 놓이지 않아서, 정말이지 언니가 좀 더 털어놓아 준다면 조금 더 알고 싶어.\"\n\n\"자, 숙녀분들!\" 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 소년이 말했다. \"자, 그대들!\" 검은 머리의 신사가 말했다. 그들은 순식간에 한 사람도 남김없이 가 버렸고, 다시 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리가 들려왔다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 금빛 의자에 주저앉았다. 이 잇따른 방해에 머리가 어질어질했다. 언니와 다른 이들은 오랫동안 가 있었다. 그들이 자리를 비운 동안, 한 목소리가 (검은 머리의 신사 목소리인 듯했다) 음악 사이로 끊임없이 외쳐 댔다. \"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작! 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작! 침착하게, 그대들! 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작!\" 마침내 그 목소리가 멎었고, 그들이 모두 숨이 차다는 듯 돌아와서는 숄을 두르고 거리로 나설 채비를 했다. \"잠깐만 있어, 에이미, 저 사람들이 우리보다 먼저 나가게.\" 패니가 속삭였다. 곧 그들만 남게 되었다. 그동안 더 중요한 일이라곤, 소년이 제 낡은 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"내일 다들 열한 시예요, 숙녀분들!\" 한 것과, 검은 머리의 신사가 제 낡은 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"내일 다들 열한 시야, 그대들!\" 하고, 저마다 제 버릇대로 말한 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다.\n\n그들만 남게 되자, 무언가가 둘둘 말리거나 다른 방법으로 치워졌고, 그들 앞에 커다란 텅 빈 우물 같은 구덩이가 나타났다. 그 깊은 바닥을 내려다보며 패니가 말했다. \"자, 삼촌!\" 리틀 도릿은 눈이 어둠에 익숙해지면서, 그 우물 바닥 한쪽 어둑한 구석에 혼자 앉아 있는 삼촌의 모습을 어렴풋이 알아보았다. 너덜너덜한 악기 갑에 든 악기를 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다.\n\n노인은 마치 작은 하늘 한 조각이 비치는 저 멀리 높다란 회랑의 창문이 한때 그의 더 나은 운수의 정점이었는데, 거기서부터 차츰차츰 내려와 마침내 저 아래 바닥까지 가라앉아 버린 것만 같은 모습이었다. 그는 여러 해 동안 일주일에 엿새씩 그 자리에 있어 왔지만, 악보집 위로 눈을 들어 올리는 모습은 한 번도 목격된 적이 없었고, 연극이라곤 한 번도 본 적이 없으리라고 다들 확신했다. 그곳에는 그가 인기 있는 남녀 주인공의 얼굴조차 알아보지 못한다는 전설이 떠돌았고, 어느 저급 희극 배우가 내기를 걸고 쉰 번의 공연에서 그를 향해 가장 능청스러운 솜씨로 익살을 부려 댔는데도 그가 알아챘다는 기색을 도무지 보이지 않았다는 이야기도 있었다. 목수들 사이에는 그가 죽었으면서도 정작 자기가 죽은 줄 모른다는 농담이 있었고, 일반석 단골들은 그가 밤이고 낮이고, 일요일까지도 평생을 오케스트라석에서 보내는 줄로 여겼다. 그들은 몇 번인가 난간 너머로 코담배를 한 줌씩 권하며 그를 떠본 적이 있었는데, 그럴 때마다 그는 잠깐 정신이 깨어나는 듯한 태도로 이 관심에 응했고, 거기에는 신사의 창백한 유령 같은 분위기가 어려 있었다. 그러나 그 이상으로는, 그는 어떤 경우에도 무대 위에서 벌어지는 일에서 클라리넷을 위해 적혀 있는 부분 말고는 아무런 역할도 맡지 않았다. 사생활에서는, 클라리넷을 위한 역할이 없으니, 그는 아예 아무런 역할도 없었다. 어떤 이들은 그가 가난하다 했고, 어떤 이들은 그가 부유한 구두쇠라 했지만, 그 자신은 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 숙인 고개를 들어 올린 적도 없었으며, 용수철 없는 발을 땅에서 떼어 올려 질질 끄는 걸음걸이를 바꾸는 일도 없었다. 지금도 조카에게 불려 가기를 기다리고 있었으면서, 그녀가 서너 번 말을 걸 때까지 듣지 못했다. 또 하나가 아니라 둘이나 되는 조카딸이 와 있는데도 그는 조금도 놀라지 않고, 그저 떨리는 목소리로 \"가마, 가마!\" 하고는, 지하실 같은 냄새를 풍기는 어떤 지하 통로를 통해 기어 나왔다.\n\n\"그래, 에이미,\" 셋이 함께, 다른 문들과는 다르다는 것을 그토록 부끄럽게 의식하는 듯한 그 문을 나설 때 언니가 말했다. 삼촌은 본능적으로 에이미의 팔을, 믿고 의지할 만한 팔로서 잡았다. \"그래, 에이미, 너 내 일을 궁금해하는구나?\"\n\n그녀는 예뻤고, 그것을 의식했으며, 다소 으스대고 있었다. 그리고 자신의 미모와 세상 경험이라는 우월함을 잠시 제쳐 두고 동생을 거의 대등한 사이인 양 대하는 그 겸양 속에는, 집안의 기질이 잔뜩 배어 있었다.\n\n\"나는 언니에게 관련된 일이라면 무엇에든 관심이 있고 마음이 쓰여, 패니 언니.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇겠지, 그렇겠지, 너야말로 에이미 중에서도 제일가는 에이미니까. 내가 가끔 좀 짜증을 부리더라도, 내 처지에 놓여 있으면서 거기보다 내가 더 나은 사람이라는 의식을 갖는다는 게 어떤 건지 너는 분명 헤아려 줄 거야. 다른 애들이 그렇게 천하지만 않다면,\" 마셜시 아버지의 딸이 말했다. \"나도 신경 쓰지 않을 거야. 그 애들 중 누구도 우리처럼 영락한 처지가 아니거든. 그들은 다들 제 본래 위치에 그대로 있는 거지. 천한 거야.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 말하는 언니를 온화하게 바라보았지만, 말을 가로막지는 않았다. 패니는 손수건을 꺼내 다소 화가 난 듯 눈가를 닦았다. \"나는 네가 태어난 곳에서 태어나지 않았잖아, 에이미, 알다시피. 어쩌면 그게 차이를 만드는 건지도 몰라. 우리 사랑하는 동생아, 삼촌을 떼어 놓고 나면 그 일을 다 말해 줄게. 삼촌은 저녁을 먹으러 가는 음식점에 내려 드리자.\"\n\n그들은 삼촌과 함께 걸어, 더러운 거리에 있는 어느 더러운 가게 창문 앞에 이르렀다. 그 창문은 뜨거운 고기와 채소와 푸딩에서 나는 김으로 거의 불투명해져 있었다. 그래도 언뜻언뜻 보이는 것들이 있었다. 육즙이 가득한 금속 통 안에서 세이지와 양파의 눈물을 터뜨리는 구운 돼지 다리, 비슷한 그릇 속에서 부글부글 뜨겁게 끓는 기름진 로스트비프와 부풀어 물집투성이가 된 요크셔푸딩, 빠르게 잘려 나가는 송아지고기 속박이 살, 가는 속도가 어찌나 빠른지 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리는 햄, 제 기름기로 서로 들러붙은 구운 감자가 담긴 얕은 통, 한두 단의 삶은 푸성귀, 그리고 그 밖의 든든한 별미들. 안쪽에는 나무 칸막이가 몇 개 있었고, 그 뒤에서는 저녁거리를 손에 들고 가기보다 뱃속에 담아 가는 편이 더 편하다고 여기는 손님들이 호젓하게 자기가 산 것을 챙겨 넣고 있었다. 그것들을 둘러보는 동안 패니가 그물 손지갑을 열어 그 안에서 1실링을 꺼내 삼촌에게 건넸다. 삼촌은 한참을 그것을 쳐다보지도 않다가, 그 용도를 알아채고는 \"저녁? 하! 그래, 그래, 그래!\" 하고 중얼거리며 천천히 안개 속으로 그들에게서 사라져 갔다.\n\n\"자, 에이미,\" 언니가 말했다. \"캐번디시 광장의 할리 가까지 걸어갈 만큼 지치지 않았다면, 나랑 같이 가자.\"\n\n그녀가 이 고상한 주소를 툭 내던지듯 말하는 태도하며, (실용적이라기보다는 얇디얇은) 새 보닛을 휙 젖히는 몸짓은 동생을 의아하게 만들었다. 그러나 동생은 할리 가에 갈 의향이 있음을 밝혔고, 그들은 그쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다. 그 으리으리한 목적지에 다다르자, 패니는 가장 멋진 집을 골라내어 문을 두드리고는 머들 부인을 찾았다. 문을 열어 준 하인은 머리에 분을 발랐고, 역시 분을 바른 다른 두 하인의 뒷받침을 받고 있었는데, 머들 부인이 댁에 계시다고 인정했을 뿐 아니라 패니에게 안으로 들어오시라고 청했다. 패니는 동생을 데리고 안으로 들어갔다. 그들은 분을 바른 하인이 앞서고 분을 바른 하인이 뒤에 멈춰 선 가운데 위층으로 올라가, 여러 응접실 가운데 하나인 넓은 반원형 응접실에 안내되어 남겨졌다. 그곳에는 금빛 새장 바깥에 매달린 앵무새 한 마리가 부리로 몸을 지탱한 채 비늘 돋친 다리를 공중으로 쳐들고 거꾸로 매달리는 갖가지 괴상한 자세를 취하고 있었다. 이런 특이한 버릇은 금빛 철사를 기어오르는, 깃털이 전혀 다른 새들에게서도 목격된 바 있다.\n\n그 방은 리틀 도릿이 여태껏 상상해 본 그 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 더 화려했고, 누구의 눈에라도 화려하고 값져 보였을 것이다. 그녀는 놀라워하며 언니를 바라보았고 무언가 물어보려 했지만, 패니가 경고하듯 눈살을 찌푸리며 다른 방으로 통하는 커튼 친 출입구를 가리켰다. 다음 순간 커튼이 흔들리더니, 한 부인이 반지를 잔뜩 낀 손으로 그것을 걷어 올리고 들어서면서 다시 등 뒤로 떨구었다.\n\n그 부인은 자연의 손에서 갓 나온 듯 젊고 싱싱한 것이 아니라, 하녀의 손에서 갓 나온 듯 젊고 싱싱했다. 그녀는 무감각한 아름다운 큰 눈과, 무감각한 아름다운 검은 머리카락과, 무감각한 아름다운 넓은 가슴을 지녔고, 어느 한 군데도 최대한으로 돋보이게 꾸며져 있었다. 감기가 들었기 때문인지, 아니면 그것이 얼굴에 어울리기 때문인지, 그녀는 머리 위로 둘러 턱 밑에서 묶은 화려한 흰 머리띠를 두르고 있었다. 그리고 이 세상에, 흔한 말로 사내의 손에 한 번도 '쓰다듬어진' 적이 없는 게 분명해 보이는 무감각한 아름다운 턱이 있다면, 바로 그 끈 달린 굴레로 그토록 빳빳하고 바투 죄어진 턱이었다.\n\n\"머들 부인이세요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"제 동생이에요, 부인.\"\n\n\"동생을 만나게 되어 반가워요, 도릿 양. 동생이 있는 줄은 몰랐네요.\"\n\n\"있다고 말씀드린 적이 없으니까요.\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 머들 부인은 마치 \"내가 너를 잡았구나. 네가 말 안 했다는 거 다 안다!\" 하고 말하려는 듯 왼손 새끼손가락을 둥글게 구부렸다. 그녀의 모든 동작은 보통 왼손으로 이루어졌는데, 두 손이 짝이 맞지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 왼손이 둘 중에 훨씬 더 희고 통통했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 \"앉아요\" 하고 덧붙이고는, 앵무새 가까이에 놓인 오토만 위, 진홍빛과 금빛 방석의 둥지 속에 관능적으로 몸을 파묻었다.\n\n\"이쪽도 무대 사람인가요?\" 머들 부인이 외알 안경 너머로 리틀 도릿을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n패니가 아니라고 대답했다. \"아니로군요.\" 머들 부인이 안경을 떨구며 말했다. \"무대 사람 분위기가 아니네요. 아주 상냥해 보여요. 하지만 무대 사람은 아니에요.\"\n\n\"제 동생이,\" 묘하게도 공손함과 대담함이 뒤섞인 패니가 말했다. \"자매지간이니 제가 어떻게 부인을 알게 되는 영광을 얻었는지 말해 달라고 하더군요. 그리고 제가 부인을 한 번 더 찾아뵙기로 약속했던 터라, 외람되게나마 동생을 데려와도 되겠다 싶었어요. 어쩌면 부인께서 동생에게 말씀해 주실 수도 있겠다고요. 동생이 알았으면 좋겠는데, 부인께서 말씀해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"동생분 나이에 그런 이야기를 —\" 머들 부인이 넌지시 말을 비쳤다.\n\n\"이 애는 보기보다 훨씬 나이가 많아요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"저랑 거의 동갑이나 마찬가지예요.\"\n\n\"사교계라는 건,\" 머들 부인이 새끼손가락을 또 한 번 둥글게 구부리며 말했다. \"젊은 사람들에게 설명하기가 어찌나 어려운지 (사실 대부분의 사람들에게 설명하기가 어찌나 어려운지), 그 말을 들으니 다행이네요. 사교계가 그렇게 제멋대로가 아니었으면, 그렇게 까다롭지 않았으면 좋으련만 — 새야, 조용히 해!\"\n\n앵무새가 마치 제 이름이 사교계이기라도 한 듯, 또 제 까다로움을 부릴 권리를 주장하기라도 하듯, 더없이 날카로운 비명을 질러 댔던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 머들 부인이 말을 이었다. \"우리는 그것을 있는 그대로 받아들여야 해요. 그것이 공허하고 인습적이고 속물적이며 무척이나 충격적이라는 걸 우리도 알지만, 우리가 열대 바다의 미개인이 아닌 이상에야 (나 자신은 미개인이 되는 것도 무척 매력적이었겠다 싶어요 — 더없이 즐거운 삶에 완벽한 기후라고들 하더군요), 우리는 그것을 따라야만 해요. 그게 다들 짊어진 운명이지요. 머들 씨는 더없이 손이 넓은 상인이고, 그분의 거래는 가장 방대한 규모에 이르며, 그분의 재산과 영향력은 대단하지만, 그분조차도 — 새야, 조용히 해!\"\n\n앵무새가 또 한 번 비명을 질렀던 것이다. 그것이 어찌나 함축적으로 문장을 채워 주었던지, 머들 부인은 굳이 말을 맺을 필요가 없었다.\n\n\"동생분이 우리의 사적인 친분을 끝맺기를 청하니,\" 그녀가 리틀 도릿을 향해 다시 입을 열었다. \"동생분에게 무척 명예로운 그 사정을 들려 드림으로써 그렇게 하지요. 그 청에 응하는 것을 마다할 까닭이야 없고말고요. 내게는 스물두셋쯤 된 아들이 하나 있어요 (나는 무척 어린 나이에 첫 결혼을 했거든요).\"\n\n패니가 입술을 꼭 다물었고, 그녀의 눈은 반쯤 의기양양한 빛으로 동생을 바라보았다.\n\n\"스물두셋쯤 된 아들이지요. 그 애는 좀 들떠 있는데, 그건 사교계가 젊은이들에게서 흔히 보아 넘기는 거고, 또 그 애는 정에 무척 잘 휩쓸려요. 어쩌면 그 불운을 물려받은 건지도 모르지요. 나 자신이 천성적으로 정에 아주 잘 휩쓸리거든요. 가장 여린 사람이라 — 내 감정은 순식간에 흔들려 버려요.\"\n\n그녀는 이 말도, 그 밖의 모든 말도, 눈사람 같은 여인처럼 차갑게 내뱉었다. 이따금씩을 빼고는 자매를 완전히 잊어버린 채, 겉보기에는 어떤 추상적인 사교계에다 대고 말하는 듯했다. 또한 바로 그 사교계를 위하여, 그녀는 가끔씩 옷매무새를 다듬거나 오토만 위에 놓인 제 몸의 자태를 매만지곤 했다.\n\n\"그래서 그 애는 정에 아주 잘 휩쓸려요. 우리가 자연 그대로의 상태에 있다면 그건 불운이 아니겠지만, 우리는 자연 그대로의 상태에 있지 않으니까요. 의심할 바 없이 무척 한탄스러운 일이지요, 특히 나처럼 본디 자연의 아이인데도 그것을 드러내 보일 수 없는 사람에게는요. 하지만 사정이 그래요. 사교계가 우리를 억누르고 지배하니까요 — 새야, 조용히 해!\"\n\n앵무새가 구부러진 부리로 새장 창살을 여러 개 비틀어 대고 검은 혀로 핥아 대더니, 격렬한 웃음을 터뜨렸던 것이다.\n\n\"양식이 풍부하고, 경험의 폭이 넓으며, 감정이 세련된 분께 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지만,\" 머들 부인이 진홍빛과 금빛 둥지에서 말하며 — 그러고는 자신이 누구에게 말하고 있는지 기억을 되살리려고 안경을 들어 올렸다. \"그런 부류의 성격을 가진 젊은이들에게는 무대가 때로 매혹으로 다가오지요. 무대라고 하면, 그 위에 선 여자들을 두고 하는 말이에요. 그러니 내 아들이 어떤 무용수에게 홀렸다는 소문을 들었을 때, 나는 그게 사교계에서 흔히 무엇을 뜻하는지 알았고, 그 여자가 오페라 극장의 무용수이리라 믿었어요. 사교계에서 움직이는 젊은이들이 으레 홀리는 곳이 거기니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 이제 자매를 유심히 살피며 하얀 두 손을 맞비볐다. 그러자 손가락의 반지들이 단단한 소리를 내며 서로 부딪혔다.\n\n\"동생분에게 들으면 알겠지만, 그곳이 무슨 극장인지 알게 되었을 때 나는 무척 놀랐고 무척 괴로웠어요. 그러나 동생분이 (덧붙이자면, 뜻밖의 방식으로) 내 아들의 구애를 물리침으로써 그 애를 청혼하겠다는 지경까지 몰고 갔다는 걸 알게 되자, 내 심정은 더없이 깊은 고뇌에 빠졌지요 — 절절한 고뇌에요.\"\n\n그녀는 왼쪽 눈썹의 윤곽을 따라 매만지며 매무새를 바로잡았다.\n\n\"오로지 사교계에서 움직이는 어머니만이 느낄 수 있는 그 심란한 심정으로, 나는 직접 극장에 가서 그 무용수에게 내 마음 상태를 호소하기로 작정했어요. 나는 동생분에게 내 신분을 밝혔지요. 놀랍게도 나는 그분이 여러 면에서 내 예상과 다르다는 걸 알게 되었고, 그중에서도 분명코 가장 다른 점은, 나를 — 뭐라고 해야 할까요 — 자기 나름의 일종의 가문 자긍심으로 맞이했다는 거였어요.\" 머들 부인이 미소 지었다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드렸잖아요, 부인,\" 패니가 낯빛을 붉히며 말했다. \"비록 부인께서 저를 그런 처지에서 발견하셨지만, 저는 다른 이들보다 훨씬 위에 있는 사람이라, 제 가문이 부인 아드님 가문에 못지않다고 여긴다고요. 그리고 제게는 사정을 알면 같은 생각을 할, 그런 인연을 도무지 명예로 여기지 않을 오라비가 하나 있다고요.\"\n\n\"도릿 양,\" 머들 부인이 안경 너머로 그녀를 쌀쌀맞게 바라본 뒤 말했다. \"당신 청에 따라 동생분에게 막 이야기하려던 게 바로 그거였어요. 그렇게 정확히 떠올려서 내 말을 앞질러 주니 무척 고맙군요. 나는 즉시 (나는 충동의 존재거든요),\" 리틀 도릿을 향해 말하며, \"내 팔에서 팔찌를 풀어, 우리가 이 문제를 이만큼이나 공통의 발판 위에서 다룰 수 있게 된 데 대한 기쁨의 표시로, 그것을 동생분의 팔에 채우게 해 달라고 청했어요.\" (이것은 더없이 사실이었다. 그 부인은 면담하러 오는 길에, 전반적으로 매수를 염두에 두고서, 값싸고 번지르르한 물건을 하나 사 두었던 것이다.)\n\n\"그래서 제가 말씀드렸어요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 말했다. \"우리가 불운할 수는 있어도, 천하지는 않다고요.\"\n\n\"바로 그 말이었던 것 같네요, 도릿 양.\" 머들 부인이 동의했다.\n\n\"그리고 제가 말씀드렸어요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 말했다. \"부인께서 제게 아드님이 사교계에서 차지하는 지위의 우월함을 들먹이신다면, 부인께서 제 출신에 대한 짐작에서 다소 스스로를 속이고 계실 가능성도 충분하다고요. 그리고 제 아버지의 지위는, 지금 그분이 움직이는 사교계에서조차 (그게 어떤 곳인지는 제가 가장 잘 알지만요) 두드러지게 우월하며, 누구에게나 인정받고 있다고요.\"\n\n\"아주 정확해요.\" 머들 부인이 응수했다. \"더없이 훌륭한 기억력이군요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인. 나머지 이야기는 부인께서 친절히 제 동생에게 들려주시지요.\"\n\n\"더 들려줄 건 거의 없어요.\" 머들 부인이, 무감각하게 있기에 충분한 공간을 확보하는 데 꼭 필요한 듯 보이는 그 넓은 가슴을 한번 굽어보며 말했다. \"하지만 그건 동생분에게 명예로운 일이지요. 나는 동생분에게 그 일의 명백한 사정을 짚어 드렸어요. 우리가 움직이는 사교계가, 동생분이 움직이는 사교계를 — 의심할 바 없이 매력적이긴 하겠지만 — 인정한다는 것은 불가능하다는 것, 따라서 동생분이 그토록 높이 평가하는 그 가문을, 우리로서는 어쩔 수 없이 경멸하며 내려다볼 수밖에 없고 (사교적으로 말하자면) 혐오감을 품고 물러설 수밖에 없는 그 처지에, 동생분이 엄청나게 불리하게 몰아넣게 되리라는 것을요. 요컨대 나는 동생분의 그 가상한 자긍심에 호소했던 거예요.\"\n\n\"부디 제 동생에게 알려 주세요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 얇은 보닛을 휙 젖히며 입을 삐죽였다. \"저는 이미 아드님께 당신과는 무슨 할 말도 없다고 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"음, 도릿 양,\" 머들 부인이 동의했다. \"어쩌면 그 얘기를 먼저 했어야 했는지도 모르겠네요. 그걸 떠올리지 못했다면, 아마 그건 그 애가 끈질기게 매달리고 당신은 그 애에게 할 말이 생길지도 모른다는, 당시의 그 우려로 내 마음이 되돌아갔기 때문일 거예요. 나는 또 동생분에게도 말했지요 — 다시 무대 사람이 아닌 도릿 양께 말씀드리는데 — 그런 결혼을 하게 되면 내 아들은 한 푼도 받지 못하고 완전한 빈털터리가 될 거라고요. (이건 그저 이야기의 일부인 사실로서 말씀드리는 거지, 그것이 동생분에게 영향을 미쳤으리라고 여겨서는 아니에요. 다만 우리의 인위적인 제도가 짜인 방식상, 우리 모두가 그런 고려에 영향을 받을 수밖에 없는, 그 신중하고 정당한 방식으로라면 모를까요.) 마침내, 동생분 쪽에서 다소 격한 말과 격한 기개를 보인 끝에, 우리는 아무 위험도 없다는 데 완전히 의견을 같이하게 되었어요. 그리고 동생분은 친절하게도 내 양장점에서 내 고마운 마음의 표시를 한두 가지 받아 주었지요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 안쓰러운 표정으로, 괴로운 얼굴을 하고 패니를 흘긋 보았다.\n\n\"또한,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"이렇게 마지막으로 만나 더없이 좋은 사이로 작별하는 기쁨을 내게 주기로 약속해 주었지요. 그래서 이 자리에서,\" 머들 부인이 둥지에서 몸을 일으켜 패니의 손에 무언가를 쥐여 주며 덧붙였다. \"도릿 양, 내 무딘 방식대로나마, 더없는 행운을 빌며 작별 인사를 하게 해 주세요.\"\n\n자매는 동시에 일어섰고, 그들 모두 앵무새 새장 가까이에 섰다. 앵무새는 한 발톱 가득 비스킷을 움켜쥐어 물어뜯고는 뱉어 내더니, 발은 움직이지 않은 채 몸뚱이만 거드름 피우며 춤추는 시늉으로 그들을 조롱하는 듯하다가, 별안간 거꾸로 몸을 뒤집어 잔인한 부리와 검은 혀의 도움으로 금빛 새장 바깥을 온통 기어 다녔다.\n\n\"안녕히, 도릿 양, 행운을 빌어요.\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"우리가 천년왕국이나 그 비슷한 무엇에라도 이를 수 있다면, 나로서는 지금은 만날 수 없는 수많은 매력적이고 재능 있는 분들을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누릴 수도 있을 텐데요. 좀 더 원시적인 상태의 사회가 내게는 더없이 즐거울 거예요. 내가 공부할 때 시 한 편이 있었는데, '보라 저 가련한 인디언, 그 무슨 마음을!' 하는 무슨 그런 거였어요. 사교계에서 움직이는 몇천 명이라도 가서 인디언이 될 수만 있다면, 나는 당장에 내 이름을 적어 넣겠어요. 하지만 사교계에서 움직이는 우리로서는, 안타깝게도 인디언이 될 수가 없으니 — 안녕히 가세요!\"\n\n그들은 앞에도 분 바른 하인, 뒤에도 분 바른 하인을 둔 채 아래층으로 내려왔다. 언니는 거만했고 동생은 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그리고 그들은 분 바르지 않은 캐번디시 광장 할리 가로 내쫓기듯 밀려났다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 한동안 말없이 걷다가 패니가 말했다. \"넌 할 말이 없니, 에이미?\"\n\n\"아, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠어!\" 그녀가 괴로워하며 대답했다. \"그 젊은이가 마음에 들지 않았던 거지, 패니 언니?\"\n\n\"마음에 드냐고? 그 사람은 거의 바보야.\"\n\n\"정말 안됐어 — 기분 상해하지는 마 — 그렇지만, 언니가 내게 할 말이 뭐냐고 물으니까 하는 말인데, 정말이지 너무 안타까워, 패니 언니, 언니가 그 부인이 주는 걸 받아 버렸다는 게.\"\n\n\"이 어리석은 것아!\" 언니가 그녀의 팔을 홱 잡아채며 흔들고 대꾸했다. \"넌 기개라곤 도무지 없니? 하지만 딱 그런 식이지! 넌 자존심도 없고, 마땅한 자긍심도 없어. 꼭 그 하찮은 치버리 따위가 졸졸 따라다니게 내버려 두는 것처럼,\" 더없이 경멸스러운 어조로 힘주어, \"넌 네 가문이 짓밟혀도, 절대 돌아서지 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"그런 말 하지 마, 패니 언니. 나는 가족을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 하고 있어.\"\n\n\"가족을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 한다고!\" 패니가 그녀를 아주 빠르게 걷게 몰아붙이며 되뇌었다. \"이런 여자를 — 네가 무슨 일에 대해서라도 경험이 좀 있었다면 여자가 부릴 수 있는 가장 거짓되고 무례한 짓을 부리는 여자라는 걸 알아챘을 그런 여자를 — 네 가문을 발로 짓밟게 내버려 두고, 그것에 대해 고맙다고 한단 말이야?\"\n\n\"아니, 패니 언니, 정말 아니야.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 값을 치르게 해야지, 이 야무지지 못한 것아. 그 여자한테 달리 뭘 시킬 수 있겠어? 그 값을 치르게 해, 이 멍청한 애야. 그리고 그 돈으로 네 가문 체면이라도 좀 세워 드려!\"\n\n그들은 패니와 삼촌이 사는 하숙집으로 돌아오는 내내 더는 말이 없었다. 거기 도착해 보니, 노인은 방 한구석에서 더없이 구슬프게 클라리넷을 연습하고 있었다. 패니는 양고기 토막과 흑맥주와 차로 이것저것 섞인 끼니를 차려야 했는데, 분개한 듯 제 손으로 차리는 척했지만, 사실 그 모든 일은 동생이 조용히 다 했다. 마침내 패니가 자리에 앉아 먹고 마시기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 식탁 도구들을 함부로 내던지고 빵에 화풀이를 했다. 어젯밤 자기 아버지가 그랬던 것과 흡사했다.\n\n\"내가 무용수라고 나를 깔본다면,\" 그녀가 격렬한 눈물을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"왜 나를 무용수가 되는 길로 들여놓은 거야? 그건 네가 한 일이야. 너는 내가 이 머들 부인 앞에서 땅바닥에 닿도록 굽실거리게 하고, 그 여자가 하고 싶은 말 다 하고 하고 싶은 짓 다 하면서, 우리 모두를 경멸하고, 그걸 내 면전에 대고 말하게 내버려 뒀어. 내가 무용수라고!\"\n\n\"아, 패니 언니!\"\n\n\"그리고 팁도, 가엾은 오라비도. 그 여자가 마음껏 그를 깎아내리는데도 아무런 제지도 못 받았어 — 아마 그가 법률 사무소에도, 부두에도, 이런저런 데에도 있었기 때문이겠지. 아니, 그것도 네가 한 일이야, 에이미. 적어도 그가 변호받는 거라도 찬성해 줄 수는 있잖아.\"\n\n이러는 내내 삼촌은 구석에서 구슬프게 클라리넷을 불고 있었는데, 가끔씩은 입에서 1인치쯤 떼어 내고 잠시 멈추어, 누군가 무슨 말을 한 것 같다는 어렴풋한 느낌으로 그들을 멍하니 바라보았다.\n\n\"그리고 네 아버지, 가엾은 네 아버지도, 에이미. 그분이 자유롭게 나서서 스스로를 변호할 수 없으니까, 너는 그런 사람들이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 그분을 모욕하게 내버려 두는 거야. 네가 일하러 나간다고 해서 네 자신은 챙기지 못한다 쳐도, 그분이 그토록 오래 무슨 일을 겪어 왔는지 알면서, 적어도 그분만큼은 헤아려 드려야 한다고 생각해.\"\n\n가엾은 리틀 도릿은 이 비난의 부당함을 다소 날카롭게 느꼈다. 어젯밤의 기억이 거기에 가시 돋친 끝을 더했다. 그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 식탁에서 의자를 돌려 난롯불 쪽으로 향했다. 삼촌은 한 번 더 멈추었다가, 구슬픈 흐느낌 같은 소리를 불고는 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n패니는 격정이 가시지 않는 동안 찻잔과 빵에 화풀이를 하더니, 이윽고 자신이 세상에서 가장 비참한 처녀라고, 차라리 죽었으면 좋겠다고 토로했다. 그러고 나서 그녀의 울음은 뉘우치는 울음으로 바뀌었고, 그녀는 일어나 동생을 두 팔로 끌어안았다. 리틀 도릿은 언니가 무슨 말이라도 하려는 것을 말리려 했지만, 그녀는 말하겠다고, 꼭 말해야 한다고 대답했다. 그러고는 거듭거듭 \"미안해, 에이미\"라고, \"용서해 줘, 에이미\"라고, 좀 전에 후회스러운 말을 쏟아 낼 때만큼이나 격렬하게 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 정말, 정말이지, 에이미,\" 그들이 자매답게 화목하게 나란히 앉았을 때 그녀가 다시 말을 이었다. \"네가 사교계를 조금만 더 알았더라면, 이 일을 다르게 보았을 거라고 나는 바라고 또 그렇게 생각해.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그랬을지도 모르지, 패니 언니.\" 온화한 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"있잖아, 너는 그곳에 살림하며 체념한 채 갇혀 지내 왔지만, 에이미,\" 언니가 점점 생색을 내기 시작하며 말을 이었다. \"나는 밖으로 나가 사교계에서 더 많이 움직였으니, 어쩌면 내가 거만하고 기개가 세진 건지도 몰라 — 어쩌면 마땅한 정도보다 더 말이야?\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 \"응. 아, 그래!\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"그리고 너는 저녁거리나 옷을 생각하고 있었지만, 나는, 알다시피, 가문을 생각하고 있었던 건지도 몰라. 자, 그렇지 않겠니, 에이미?\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 마음보다는 밝은 얼굴로 다시 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"특히 우리도 알다시피,\" 패니가 말했다. \"네가 그토록 충실히 지켜 온 그곳에는 분명 어떤 기풍이 있고, 그건 그곳에 본디 깃든 것이며, 사교계의 다른 면모들과 그곳을 다르게 만들어 주는 거니까. 그러니 한 번 더 내게 입맞춤해 줘, 사랑하는 에이미. 그리고 우리 둘 다 옳을 수도 있다는 데, 또 너는 차분하고 살림 잘하고 집안을 사랑하는 착한 아이라는 데 뜻을 모으자.\"\n\n이 대화가 오가는 내내 클라리넷은 더없이 애처롭게 탄식하고 있었지만, 이제 그만 가야 할 시간이라는 패니의 선언으로 뚝 끊겼다. 그녀는 삼촌의 악보 조각을 덮어 버리고 그 입에서 클라리넷을 빼냄으로써 그 뜻을 삼촌에게 전했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 문 앞에서 그들과 헤어져 서둘러 마셜시로 돌아갔다. 그곳은 다른 곳보다 일찍 어둠이 내려, 그날 저녁 그 안으로 들어가는 것은 마치 깊은 참호 속으로 들어가는 것과 같았다. 담장의 그림자가 모든 것 위에 드리워 있었다. 그녀가 어둑한 방의 문을 열었을 때 그녀를 향해 돌아서던, 낡은 회색 가운에 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 그 모습 위에도 그 그림자는 어김없이 드리워 있었다.\n\n\"어찌하여 나 또한 그렇지 않으랴!\" 리틀 도릿은 아직 문을 손에 쥔 채 생각했다. \"패니가 그런 건 무리도 아니었어.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint\n\n할리 가, 캐번디시 광장에 있는 그 으리으리한 명문가, 곧 머들 가문의 저택에 드리운 그림자라야, 길 건너편에 늘어선 다른 명문가 저택들의 정면이 드리우는 그림자에서 조금도 더할 것 없는 흔해 빠진 담벼락의 그림자에 불과했다. 흠잡을 데 없는 상류 사회가 그러하듯, 할리 가에서 마주 보고 늘어선 두 줄의 집들은 서로를 무척이나 음울하게 노려보고 있었다. 실로 그 저택들과 그 거주자들은 이 점에서 어찌나 닮았던지, 사람들은 흔히 만찬 식탁의 양쪽에 갈라 앉아, 제 잘난 척의 그늘 속에서, 길 건너편을 집들만큼이나 멍한 표정으로 바라보고 있는 것이었다.\n\n길가에 자리 잡은 두 줄의 만찬 손님들이 거리와 얼마나 닮았는지는 누구나 안다. 표정 없이 똑같은 스무 채의 집들, 모두 똑같은 방식으로 문을 두드리고 초인종을 울려야 하고, 모두 똑같이 칙칙한 계단으로 다가가야 하며, 모두 똑같은 무늬의 난간으로 가로막혀 있고, 모두 똑같이 쓸모없는 화재 비상구를 달고 있으며, 머리 꼭대기엔 똑같이 불편한 부속물들이 붙어 있고, 예외 없이 모든 것에 높은 값을 매겨야 하는 집들 — 이런 집들과 한 식탁에서 식사를 해 본 적 없는 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 음산하게 손볼 데 투성이인 집, 이따금 보이는 활 모양 창문이 달린 집, 회반죽을 바른 집, 정면을 새로 단장한 집, 죄다 모난 방뿐인 모퉁이 집, 늘 차양이 내려져 있는 집, 늘 문장(紋章) 액자가 걸려 있는 집, 사 분기 분의 어떤 발상을 거두러 수금원이 찾아왔으나 아무도 집에 없던 집 — 이런 집들과 한 식탁에서 식사를 해 본 적 없는 사람이 어디 있겠는가? 아무도 사려 하지 않아 헐값에 나온 집 — 그 여자를 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가? 실의에 빠진 신사가 평생 살겠다고 사들였지만 도무지 그에게 맞지 않는 화려한 집 — 그 귀신 들린 거처를 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가?\n\n할리 가, 캐번디시 광장은 머들 부부의 존재를 진작 알고도 남았다. 할리 가에는 그것이 미처 모르는 침입자들도 있었다. 하지만 머들 부부야말로 그것이 기꺼이 떠받드는 이들이었다. 상류 사회는 머들 부부를 알고 있었다. 상류 사회는 이렇게 말했던 것이다. \"저들에게 인가를 내려 주자. 저들과 사귀자.\"\n\n머들 씨는 어마어마한 부자였다. 굉장한 사업가였다. 당나귀 귀만 없는 미다스로, 손대는 것마다 황금으로 바꾸어 놓았다. 은행업에서 건설업에 이르기까지, 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 손을 대고 있었다. 물론 의회에도 들어가 있었다. 당연히 시티에도 있었다. 이것의 회장이요, 저것의 이사요, 또 다른 것의 회장이었다. 가장 비중 있는 인사들이 사업 발기인들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"자, 누구를 잡았소? 머들을 잡았소?\" 그러고는 부정적인 대답이 돌아오면 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"그럼 거들떠보지도 않겠소.\"\n\n이 위대하고도 운 좋은 사내는, 그 안에서 충분히 무정해지려면 그토록 넓은 공간이 필요했던 그 드넓은 가슴팍에다, 한 십오 년쯤 전에 진홍색과 황금색의 보금자리를 마련해 주었다. 그것은 기대어 쉴 만한 가슴팍이 아니라, 보석을 걸어 두기에 더할 나위 없이 좋은 가슴팍이었다. 머들 씨는 보석을 걸어 둘 무언가가 필요했고, 바로 그 목적으로 그것을 사들였다. 스토어와 모티머도 그와 같은 속셈으로 결혼을 했을 법하다.\n\n다른 모든 투기와 마찬가지로, 그것 역시 견실하고 성공적이었다. 보석들은 더없이 값지게 돋보였다. 보석을 진열해 단 그 가슴팍이 상류 사회를 누비고 다니자, 뭇사람의 감탄을 자아냈다. 상류 사회가 흡족해하니, 머들 씨도 만족했다. 그는 더없이 사심 없는 사내였다 — 모든 것을 상류 사회를 위해 했고, 그 모든 이득과 노고에서 자기 몫으로 챙긴 것이라곤 한 인간이 챙길 수 있는 가장 적은 정도였다.\n\n말하자면, 그는 원하는 것은 다 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 있다. 그렇지 않았다면 그 무한한 재산으로 손에 넣었을 테니까. 그러나 그의 욕망은 상류 사회(그것이 무엇이든 간에)를 극진히 만족시키고, 그것이 공물로 그에게 발행하는 어음을 죄다 떠맡는 데 있었다. 그는 사람들 틈에서 빛나는 인물이 아니었다. 자기 입으로 할 말도 그리 많지 않았다. 그는 말수 적은 사내로, 머리는 넓고 앞으로 불거진 채 경계하는 듯했으며, 두 뺨에는 싱싱하다기보다는 오히려 시들한, 그 특유의 칙칙한 붉은빛이 감돌았고, 마치 소맷동이 그의 비밀을 알고 있어 그의 두 손을 감추고 싶어 할 이유라도 있다는 듯, 외투 소맷동 언저리에는 어딘가 불안한 기색이 어려 있었다. 그가 더러 하는 적은 말 속에서는 제법 유쾌한 사내였다. 꾸밈이 없었고, 공적·사적 신용에 관해서는 단호했으며, 모든 일에서 모든 이가 상류 사회에 더없는 경의를 표해야 한다는 데는 집요했다. 바로 그 상류 사회(그의 만찬에, 그리고 머들 부인의 연회와 음악회에 오는 것이 그것이라면) 속에서, 그는 그다지 즐거워하는 것 같지 않았고, 대개는 벽에 붙어 있거나 문 뒤에 숨어 있기 일쑤였다. 또한 그것이 그를 찾아오는 게 아니라 그가 그것을 찾아 나설 때면, 그는 다소 지쳐 보였고, 대체로 잠자리에 들고 싶어 하는 기색이었다. 그럼에도 그는 늘 그것을 가꾸었고, 늘 그 속을 누볐으며 — 늘 더없이 후하게 그것에 돈을 쏟아부었다.\n\n머들 부인의 첫 남편은 어느 대령이었는데, 그 후원 아래 이 가슴팍은 북아메리카의 흰 눈과 경쟁을 벌인 적이 있었고, 흰빛에서는 거의 손색이 없었으며 차가움에서는 전혀 손색이 없었다. 그 대령의 아들이 머들 부인의 외아들이었다. 그는 멍청해 보이는 머리에 어깨가 높이 솟은 체격이었으며, 전체적인 인상이 청년이라기보다는 몸집만 부풀어 오른 사내아이 같았다. 분별의 기미를 어찌나 보이지 않던지, 동료들 사이에서는 그가 태어났던 무렵 뉴브런즈윅 세인트존에 몰아닥친 엄청난 혹한에 두뇌가 얼어붙어 그 시각 이래 한 번도 녹지 않았다는 우스갯소리가 돌았다. 또 다른 우스갯소리는, 그가 갓난아기 시절 유모의 부주의로 높은 창문에서 머리부터 떨어졌는데, 책임 있는 목격자들이 머리뼈가 쩍 갈라지는 소리를 들었다는 것이었다. 이 두 가지 설명은 아마도 사후에 지어낸 것이리라. 이 젊은 신사(그 함축적인 이름은 스파클러였다)는 온갖 탐탁잖은 아가씨들에게 청혼을 해 대는 데 단단히 미쳐 있었고, 청혼한 아가씨마다 두고 \"기차게 멋진 계집애야 — 교양도 있고 — 어쭙잖게 까다로운 구석도 없거든\" 하고 평하는 데 또한 미쳐 있었으니 말이다.\n\n이렇게 보잘것없는 재능을 지닌 사위라면 다른 사내에게는 짐이 되었을지 모른다. 그러나 머들 씨는 자기 자신을 위해 사위가 필요했던 게 아니었다. 상류 사회를 위해 사위가 필요했던 것이다. 스파클러 씨가 근위대에 몸담은 적이 있고, 온갖 경마장과 온갖 사교 휴게실과 온갖 연회에 드나드는 데 익숙하며 널리 알려져 있었으므로, 상류 사회는 그 사위에 만족했다. 이 행복한 결과라면, 설령 스파클러 씨가 더 값비싼 물건이었더라도 머들 씨는 잘 손에 넣었다 여겼을 것이다. 그리고 사실 그대로라 해도, 그는 결코 헐값에 스파클러 씨를 상류 사회에 들여보낸 게 아니었다.\n\n그날 밤 리틀 도릿이 아버지 곁에서 그의 새 셔츠를 깁고 있던 바로 그때, 할리 가의 저택에서는 만찬이 베풀어지고 있었다. 궁정의 거물들과 시티의 거물들, 하원의 거물들과 상원의 거물들, 법관석의 거물들과 변호사석의 거물들, 주교 거물들, 재무성 거물들, 근위 기병대 거물들, 해군성 거물들 — 우리를 굴러가게 하는, 그리고 더러는 우리를 넘어뜨리는 온갖 거물들이 모여 있었다.\n\n\"듣자 하니,\" 주교 거물이 근위 기병대에게 말했다, \"머들 씨가 또 한 번 어마어마한 한탕을 했다더군요. 십만 파운드라던데요.\"\n\n근위 기병대는 이십만 파운드라고 들었다.\n\n재무성은 삼십만 파운드라고 들었다.\n\n변호사는 설득력 있는 양안경을 만지작거리며, 사십만 파운드일지도 모른다는 것을 결코 부정할 수 없다고 했다. 그것은 계산과 결합의 그 절묘한 솜씨 가운데 하나로, 그 결과를 가늠하기란 어려웠다. 그것은 폭넓은 파악력이 습관적인 행운과 특유의 대담성과 결합된 사례 가운데 하나로, 한 시대가 우리에게 보여 주는 것이 몇 안 되는 것이었다. 한데 마침 여기 그 거대한 은행 사건에 관여했던 벨로스 형제가 있으니, 아마도 우리에게 더 자세히 일러 줄 수 있으리라. 벨로스 형제는 이 새로운 성공을 얼마로 보는가?\n\n벨로스 형제는 그 가슴팍에 인사를 올리러 가는 길이었던지라, 지나가며 이렇게 일러 줄 수 있을 뿐이었다. 처음부터 끝까지 따지면 오십만 파운드의 값어치가 있다고, 자못 그럴듯한 모양새로 진술되는 것을 들었노라고.\n\n해군성은 머들 씨가 놀라운 인물이라고 했고, 재무성은 그가 이 나라의 새로운 권력이며 하원 전체를 통째로 사들일 만하다고 했다. 주교는 이 재물이 언제나 상류 사회의 최선의 이익을 지키려는 신사의 금고로 흘러든다고 생각하니 기쁘다고 했다.\n\n머들 씨 본인은 이런 자리에 으레 늦게 나타나곤 했으니, 다른 이들이 하루치의 난쟁이 같은 걱정을 떨쳐 버린 뒤에도 여전히 거대한 사업의 손아귀에 붙들려 있는 사내였던 까닭이다. 이번에도 그가 가장 늦게 도착했다. 재무성은 머들의 일이 그를 좀 괴롭히는 모양이라고 했다. 주교는 이 재물이 그것을 온순하게 받아들이는 신사의 금고로 흘러든다고 생각하니 기쁘다고 했다.\n\n분가루! 대기하는 분가루가 어찌나 많던지, 그것이 만찬에 향을 더할 지경이었다. 분가루 알갱이가 음식에 들어갔고, 상류 사회의 고기에는 일류 하인들의 양념이 배어 있었다. 머들 씨는 어느 백작 부인을 식당으로 안내해 내려갔는데, 그녀는 거대한 드레스의 한복판 어딘가에 파묻혀 있어, 마치 웃자란 양배추 속의 심처럼 그 드레스에 비해 작은 비율을 차지했다. 이토록 저속한 비유를 허락받을 수 있다면, 그 드레스는 화려한 비단으로 짠 '잭 인 더 그린'처럼 계단을 내려갔고, 그것을 떠받친 자그마한 인물이 대체 어떤 사람인지는 아무도 몰랐다.\n\n* 잭 인 더 그린: 영국 오월제 행렬에서 나뭇잎과 꽃으로 만든 거대한 원추형 틀을 뒤집어쓰고 행진하던 인물.\n상류 사회는 만찬을 위해 바랄 수 있는 것은 물론, 바랄 수조차 없는 것까지 죄다 갖추고 있었다. 볼 것도 죄다, 먹을 것도 죄다, 마실 것도 죄다 갖추고 있었다. 부디 그것이 즐겁게 지냈기를 바란다. 머들 씨 자신의 식사 몫이야 십팔 펜스면 치를 수 있었을 테니 말이다. 머들 부인은 장엄했다. 그날의 그다음으로 장엄한 제도는 집사장이었다. 그는 그 자리에서 가장 위풍당당한 사내였다. 그는 아무것도 하지 않았으되, 다른 어떤 사내도 해내지 못할 만큼 위엄 있게 지켜보았다. 그는 머들 씨가 상류 사회에 바친 가장 최근의 선물이었다. 머들 씨는 그를 원치 않았고, 그 위대한 피조물이 자기를 쳐다보면 무안해졌다. 그러나 달랠 길 없는 상류 사회는 그를 가지려 했고 — 결국 가지고 말았다.\n\n여흥의 여느 단계에 이르자 보이지 않는 백작 부인이 그 '그린'을 내가고, 미인들의 행렬은 그 가슴팍이 마무리 지었다. 재무성은 유노라고 했다. 주교는 유디트라고 했다.\n\n변호사는 근위 기병대와 더불어 군법 회의에 관한 토론에 빠져들었다. 벨로스 형제와 법관석 형제도 끼어들었다. 다른 거물들도 짝을 지어 흩어졌다. 머들 씨는 말없이 앉아 식탁보를 들여다보았다. 이따금 어느 거물이 자기 토론의 물줄기를 그에게 돌리려 그에게 말을 건넸으나, 머들 씨는 좀처럼 거기에 큰 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 자기 계산에서 잠깐 깨어나 포도주를 건네주는 것 이상은 하지 않았다.\n\n자리에서 일어났을 때, 무척 많은 거물들이 머들 씨에게 따로 할 말이 있었던지라, 그는 찬장 옆에서 작은 알현식을 열고, 그들이 문을 나설 때마다 하나씩 처리해 갔다.\n\n재무성은 새로운 업적을 두고 영국의 세계적으로 이름난 자본가요 상인 군주 가운데 한 분께 감히 축하를 드려도 되겠느냐고 했다(그는 의사당에서 그 독창적인 정서를 몇 차례 굴려 본 적이 있어, 그 말이 술술 나왔다). 그러한 인물들의 개가를 넓히는 것은 곧 나라의 개가와 자원을 넓히는 일이니, 재무성은 이 점에 관해 — 그는 머들 씨가 알아듣도록 일렀다 — 애국심이 솟구침을 느낀다고 했다.\n\n\"고맙소, 경.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"고맙소. 자랑스럽게 축하를 받겠소. 그대가 흡족해하니 기쁘오.\"\n\n\"이런, 무조건 흡족해하는 건 아니오, 친애하는 머들 씨. 왜냐하면,\" 미소 띤 재무성이 그의 팔을 잡아 찬장 쪽으로 돌려세우며 농담조로 말했다, \"그대가 우리 틈에 끼어 우리를 도와 줄 만한 가치라곤 결코 없을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n머들 씨는 영광스럽다고 느꼈으니 —\n\n\"아니, 아니,\" 재무성이 말했다, \"실무 지식과 위대한 선견지명으로 그토록 두드러진 분이 그렇게 여기실 거라 기대할 수야 없지요. 만일 우리가 어쩌다 사정을 좌우할 위치를 갖게 되어, 운 좋게도 그토록 탁월한 분께 — 우리 틈에 끼어 그의 영향력과 지식과 인품의 무게를 우리에게 실어 달라고 청할 수 있게 된다면, 우리는 오로지 그것을 의무로서 청할 수 있을 따름이오. 실로, 그가 상류 사회에 진 의무로서 말이오.\"\n\n머들 씨는 상류 사회야말로 자기 눈동자처럼 소중하며, 그 요구가 다른 어떤 고려보다 우선한다고 넌지시 밝혔다. 재무성은 자리를 옮겼고, 변호사가 다가왔다.\n\n변호사는 배심원을 구슬릴 때 짓는 그 살짝 고개를 숙이는 버릇과 더불어, 설득력 있는 양안경을 만지작거리며, 만일 자기가 모든 악의 뿌리를 모든 선의 뿌리로 바꾸어 놓는 가장 위대한 전환자 가운데 한 분께 — 우리 상업 국가의 연대기에마저 오랫동안 찬란한 광채를 드리워 온 분께 — 말씀을 드린다면, 그것도 사심 없이, 그리고 우리 법조인들이 현학적인 투로 '법정의 벗'이라 부르는 입장에서, 우연히 그가 알게 된 어떤 사실을 말씀드린다면, 부디 용서해 주십사 했다. 그는 동부의 어느 주에 있는 자못 상당한 규모의 영지의 권리 증서를 검토해 달라는 의뢰를 받은 적이 있었다 — 사실은, 머들 씨도 우리 법조인이 정확함을 즐긴다는 것을 알고 계실 터라 하는 말이지만, 동부의 두 주 경계에 걸쳐 있는 영지였다. 그런데 그 권리 증서는 완벽하게 견실했고, 그 영지는 — 돈을(배심원용 고갯짓과 설득력 있는 양안경) — 마음껏 쓸 수 있는 어떤 이가 자못 유리한 조건에 사들이게 되어 있었다. 이 사실을 변호사가 알게 된 것은 바로 그날이었고, 문득 이런 생각이 떠올랐다고 했다. '오늘 저녁 나는 존경하는 벗 머들 씨와 만찬을 함께할 영광을 누리니, 우리끼리만 은밀히, 그 기회를 일러 드려야겠다.' 그러한 매입에는 거대하고 정당한 정치적 영향력뿐 아니라, 상당한 연수익이 따르는 대여섯 자리의 성직 추천권까지 딸려 온다고 했다. 그런데 머들 씨로서는 이미 자기 자본을 굴릴 방도며 그 활발하고 왕성한 지력을 십분 부려 먹을 방도를 찾는 데 조금도 막힘이 없음을 변호사는 잘 알고 있었다. 그러나 그는 감히 이런 제언을 드리고자 했으니, 곧 그토록 높은 지위와 그토록 유럽적인 명성을 마땅히 얻은 분이라면, 그것은 — 자기 자신에게라고는 하지 않겠고, 상류 사회에게 — 그러한 영향력을 스스로 손에 넣고, 그것을 행사할 빚을 진 것 아니냐는 의문이 마음속에 떠오른다는 것이었다. 그것도 — 그 자신을 위해서라거나 그의 당파를 위해서라고는 하지 않겠고 — 상류 사회의 이익을 위해서 말이다.\n\n머들 씨는 다시금, 자기가 늘 마음 쓰는 그 목적에 전적으로 헌신하고 있노라고 밝혔고, 변호사는 자신의 설득력 있는 양안경을 챙겨 대계단을 올라갔다. 그러자 주교가 무심한 척 찬장 쪽으로 슬그머니 다가왔다.\n\n이 세상의 재물이란, 부유함의 정당한 가치를 알면서도(여기서 주교는 자기야말로 다소 가난한 사람인 척하려 애썼다) 그것이 슬기롭게 운용되고 올바르게 분배될 때 우리 형제들 전반의 복리에 얼마나 중요한지를 아는 현명하고 사려 깊은 이들의 마법 같은 손길 아래 쌓일 때만큼 행복한 물길로 흘러드는 일은 좀처럼 없을 것이라고, 주교는 우연히 그런 생각이 떠올랐다는 식으로 한마디 했다.\n\n머들 씨는 겸손하게, 주교께서 설마 자기를 두고 하시는 말씀일 리는 없을 거라는 확신을 밝혔고, 앞뒤가 맞지 않게도, 주교의 호평에 더없이 흡족하다는 뜻을 밝혔다.\n\n그러자 주교는 — 마치 머들 씨에게 \"이 앞치마는 신경 쓰지 마시오. 한낱 형식일 뿐이외다!\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯, 맵시 있게 빠진 오른쪽 다리를 경쾌하게 살짝 내딛으며 — 자기 좋은 벗에게 이런 가정을 내놓았다.\n\n그토록 사업에 복을 받고, 그 받침대 위에 선 그 본보기가 상류 사회에 그토록 큰 영향을 끼치는 분이라면, 가령 아프리카 선교 사업이나 그 비슷한 방면에 약간의 돈을 흩뿌려 주리라고 상류 사회가 바란대도 부당하지 않으리라는 생각이, 혹 자기 좋은 벗에게 떠오른 적은 없는지요?\n\n머들 씨가 그 생각에 최선의 주의를 기울이겠노라고 표하자, 주교는 또 다른 가정을 내놓았다.\n\n자기 좋은 벗이 혹 우리 '연합 추가 기부 고위 성직자 위원회'의 활동에 조금이라도 관심을 가져 본 적은 없는지요? 그리고 그 방면에 약간의 돈을 흩뿌리는 것이야말로 위대한 구상을 멋지게 실행하는 일이 되리라는 생각이, 혹 그에게 떠오른 적은 없는지요?\n\n머들 씨가 비슷한 대답을 하자, 주교는 자기가 묻는 까닭을 설명했다.\n\n상류 사회가 그러한 일을 해 주기를 기대하는 것은 다름 아닌 자기 좋은 벗과 같은 분들이라고. 그것을 기대하는 것은 자기가 아니라 상류 사회라고. 마치 추가 기부 고위 성직자를 원한 것이 우리 위원회가 아니라, 그들을 얻기 전까지는 더없이 괴로운 마음의 불안에 빠져 있던 상류 사회였던 것과 마찬가지라고. 그는 자기 좋은 벗에게, 모든 경우에 상류 사회의 최선의 이익을 헤아려 주는 그 마음 씀씀이를 자기가 극진히 고맙게 여긴다는 것을 단언해 두고자 했다. 그리고 그가 그 벗에게 변함없는 번영과, 변함없는 부의 증대와, 변함없는 만사형통을 빌어 줄 때, 자기는 그 이익을 헤아리는 동시에 상류 사회의 정서를 표하는 것이라 여긴다고 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 주교는 위층으로 자리를 옮겼고, 다른 거물들도 차츰 그를 따라 떠올라가, 마침내 아래층에는 머들 씨 말고는 아무도 남지 않았다. 그 신사는 집사장의 영혼이 고귀한 분노로 이글거릴 만큼 오래도록 식탁보를 들여다보다가, 천천히 나머지 사람들을 따라 올라가서는, 대계단을 메운 인파의 흐름 속에서 아무 존재감도 없는 사람이 되었다. 머들 부인은 손님을 맞고 있었고, 가장 좋은 보석들은 사람들 눈에 띄도록 내걸려 있었으며, 상류 사회는 찾아온 목적을 얻었고, 머들 씨는 구석에서 이 펜스어치의 차를 마시고는 원하는 것보다 더 많이 마셨다.\n\n저녁의 거물들 가운데에는 모든 이를 알고 모든 이가 아는, 어느 이름난 의사가 있었다. 그는 문으로 들어서다가 구석에서 차를 마시는 머들 씨와 마주쳐, 그의 팔을 건드렸다.\n\n머들 씨가 흠칫 놀랐다. \"오! 자네였군!\"\n\n\"오늘은 좀 나으신가요?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 머들 씨가 말했다, \"나아진 게 없네.\"\n\n\"오늘 아침에 못 뵌 게 안타깝군요. 부디 내일 저를 찾아오시든지, 아니면 제가 가 뵙도록 하지요.\"\n\n\"음!\" 그가 대답했다. \"마차로 지나는 길에 내일 들르겠네.\"\n\n이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 변호사와 주교가 둘 다 곁에 서 있다가, 머들 씨가 인파에 휩쓸려 떠밀려 가자, 그 일을 두고 의사에게 한마디씩 했다. 변호사는, 어느 누구도 넘어설 수 없는 정신적 긴장의 어떤 한계점이 있는 법이며, 그 한계점은 뇌의 다양한 결과 체질의 특이함에 따라 달라진다고, 자기가 학식 높은 여러 동료들에게서 여러 차례 보아 온 바라고 했다. 그러나 그 견딤의 한계점을 머리카락 한 올의 너비만큼이라도 지나치면, 곧 우울과 소화 불량이 뒤따른다고 했다. 의학의 신성한 신비를 침범할 뜻은 없으나, 자기 생각으로는 이제(배심원용 고갯짓과 설득력 있는 양안경과 더불어) 이것이 머들의 경우 아니겠느냐고 했다. 주교는, 자기가 젊은 시절 한동안 토요일마다 설교문을 쓰는 버릇 — 교회의 모든 젊은 자제들이 부지런히 멀리해야 할 버릇 — 에 빠진 적이 있었는데, 그때 짐작건대 과로한 지력에서 비롯된 우울을 자주 느꼈으며, 그 우울에는 당시 자기가 세 들어 살던 집의 착한 안주인이 신선한 달걀노른자에 질 좋은 셰리주 한 잔과 육두구, 그리고 가루 설탕을 넣어 휘저어 준 것이 마법처럼 잘 들었다고 했다. 그토록 심오한 위대한 치유술의 대가께 그토록 단순한 처방을 감히 권할 생각은 없으나, 긴장이 복잡한 계산에서 비롯된 것이라면, 부드러우면서도 넉넉한 자극제로 그 기력을 (인간적으로 말하여) 본래의 활기로 되돌릴 수는 없을지 감히 여쭙고 싶다고 했다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 의사가 말했다, \"그렇소, 두 분 다 옳소. 하지만 이 말씀은 드려도 좋겠소. 나는 머들 씨에게서 아무런 이상도 찾아낼 수 없다는 것이오. 그는 코뿔소의 체질에, 타조의 소화력에, 굴의 집중력을 지녔소. 신경으로 말하자면, 머들 씨는 냉정한 기질이라 예민한 사람이 아니오. 내 보기엔 아킬레우스만큼이나 끄떡없는 사람이오. 그런 사람이 어찌 까닭도 없이 자기를 병들었다 여기는지, 이상하다 생각하실 게요. 하지만 나는 그에게서 아무런 이상도 찾지 못했소. 무언가 깊이 자리 잡은 알 수 없는 병이 있을지도 모르지요. 그건 나도 모르겠소. 다만 내가 말할 수 있는 건, 지금으로서는 그것을 찾아내지 못했다는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n지금 여러 비슷한 화려한 보석 진열대와 겨루며 보석을 자랑하고 있는 그 가슴팍에는, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자가 어려 있지 않았다. 어쭙잖게 까다로운 구석이라곤 없는 충분히 탐탁잖은 아가씨를 단단히 미친 듯이 찾아 헤매며 방들을 누비는 젊은 스파클러에게도, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자는 어려 있지 않았다. 통째로 한 무리씩 와 있던 배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가에게도, 그 자리의 다른 누구에게도, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자는 어려 있지 않았다. 인파 속을 누비며 경의를 받는 그 자신에게마저, 그 그림자는 충분히 희미했다.\n\n머들 씨의 병. 그와 상류 사회는 다른 모든 일에서 그토록 깊이 얽혀 있었으니, 만일 그에게 병이 있다 한들 그 병만은 오로지 그 자신만의 문제이리라고는 상상하기 어려운 노릇이었다. 그에게 그 깊이 자리 잡은 알 수 없는 병이 정말 있었던 것일까, 그리고 어느 의사가 그것을 찾아냈을까? 그러는 사이에, 진득이 기다리자. 마셜시 담벼락의 그림자는 실제로 어둠을 드리우는 힘이었고, 해가 어느 길을 지나든 그 어느 시각에도 도릿 집안 위에 어려 있는 것을 볼 수 있었으니.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. A Puzzle\n\n클레넘 씨는 방문 횟수가 늘어나는 만큼 마셜시의 아버지에게 호감을 얻지는 못했다. 그 위대한 사례금 문제에 대한 그의 둔감함은 아버지의 가슴속에 흠모를 불러일으킬 만한 것이 못 되었고, 오히려 그 예민한 영역에 불쾌감을 안기고, 신사다운 정서의 측면에서 명백한 결함으로 여겨지는 경향이 있었다. 클레넘 씨가, 그 천성을 믿고 그에게 기꺼이 인정해 주려 했던 그 섬세함을 거의 지니지 못했다는 발견에서 비롯된 실망의 인상이, 그 신사와 관련하여 아버지의 마음을 어둡게 물들이기 시작했다. 아버지는 사사로운 가족 모임에서, 클레넘 씨가 고결한 본능을 지닌 사람이 아닐까 염려된다고까지 말하기에 이르렀다. 그는, 칼리지의 지도자이자 대표라는 공적 지위에서 클레넘 씨가 인사를 차리러 찾아올 때 그를 맞이하는 것은 기쁜 일이라고 했으나, 개인적으로는 그와 잘 통하지가 않는다고 했다. 그에게는 뭔가가(그게 무엇인지는 모르겠으나) 빠져 있는 것 같다고 했다. 그렇다고 해도 아버지는 겉으로 드러내는 예의에 있어서는 어긋남이 없었으며, 오히려 그 반대로 그에게 깍듯한 관심을 베풀어 예우했다. 아마도, 비록 청하지도 않은 종전의 사례금을 되풀이할 만큼 충분히 명민하고 자발적인 기질의 사람은 아닐지언정, 그쪽으로 흐르는 어떠한 교신에서든 화답하는 신사의 역할을 감당하는 것 정도는 여전히 그 천성의 범위 안에 있으리라는 희망을 품고 있었을 것이다.\n\n처음 나타난 날 밤에 우연히 안에 갇혔던 바깥세상의 신사로서, 마셜시의 아버지를 빼내겠다는 엄청난 발상으로 그의 사정을 알아본 바깥세상의 신사로서, 또 마셜시의 아이에게 관심을 둔 바깥세상의 신사로서, 클레넘은 그 삼중의 자격으로 곧 주목할 만한 방문객이 되었다. 그는 치버리 씨가 문지기를 설 때 그로부터 받는 관심에 놀라지 않았는데, 치버리 씨의 예의와 다른 간수들의 예의를 별로 구별하지 않았기 때문이다. 그런데 어느 특별한 오후에, 치버리 씨가 그를 단번에 놀라게 하며 동료들 사이에서 두드러지게 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n치버리 씨는 문지기 방을 비우는 자신의 재간을 어떤 교묘한 방식으로 발휘하여, 어슬렁거리던 칼리지 사람들을 모두 그곳에서 내보내는 데 성공했다. 그리하여 클레넘이 감옥에서 나올 때 그 혼자만 근무 중인 것을 보게끔 해 놓았다.\n\n\"(은밀히) 실례합니다만, 선생님,\" 치버리 씨가 비밀스러운 태도로 말했다. \"어느 쪽으로 가시는 길입니까?\"\n\n\"다리를 건너갈 참이오.\" 그는 적잖이 놀라며, 열쇠를 입술에 대고 선 채 그야말로 '침묵의 알레고리'와도 같은 치버리 씨를 보았다.\n\n\"(은밀히) 다시 한번 실례합니다만,\" 치버리 씨가 말했다. \"혹 호스몽거 레인 쪽으로 돌아가 주실 수는 없겠습니까? 어떻게든 짬을 내어 이 주소에 들러 봐 주실 수 있겠습니까?\" 그러면서 그는 작은 명함 한 장을 건넸는데, 그것은 '치버리 상회, 담배상, 순수 하바나 시가·벵골 셰루트·풍미 좋은 쿠바산 수입상, 고급 코담배 등등 취급'의 거래처들 사이에 돌리려고 인쇄해 둔 것이었다.\n\n\"(은밀히) 담배 장사 일이 아닙니다,\" 치버리 씨가 말했다. \"사실은, 제 아내 일입니다. 아내가 선생님께 한 말씀 드리고 싶어 합니다, 어떤 점에 관해서요—그렇습니다,\" 치버리 씨가 클레넘의 염려스러운 표정에 고개를 끄덕여 답하며 말했다. \"그 애에 관해서요.\"\n\n\"부인을 곧장 꼭 뵙도록 하겠소.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 정말 감사합니다. 가시는 길에서 십 분도 안 벗어납니다. 치버리 부인을 찾으시면 됩니다!\" 이 당부의 말을, 이미 그를 내보낸 치버리 씨는 바깥문에 달린 작은 미닫이 구멍을 통해 조심스레 건넸는데, 그것은 내키면 안에서 끌어당겨 방문객을 살펴볼 수 있는 구멍이었다.\n\n아서 클레넘은 명함을 손에 들고, 거기에 적힌 주소로 발길을 옮겨 곧 그곳에 도착했다. 그것은 아주 작은 가게였는데, 점잖아 보이는 여자가 계산대 뒤에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다. 담배가 든 작은 단지 몇 개, 시가가 든 작은 상자 몇 개, 자그마하게 갖춘 파이프들, 코담배가 든 작은 단지 한두 개, 그리고 그것을 떠 담는 구둣주걱 같은 작은 도구가 그 소매점의 상품 전부였다.\n\n아서는 자기 이름을 대고, 치버리 씨의 부탁으로 들르기로 약속했다고 말했다. 도릿 양과 관련된 어떤 일 때문인 것으로 안다고 했다. 치버리 부인은 곧장 바느질감을 내려놓고 계산대 뒤 자리에서 일어나, 한탄스레 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"이제 그 애를 보실 수 있어요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"황송하지만 한번 들여다봐 주신다면요.\"\n\n이 알 수 없는 말과 함께, 그녀는 방문객을 가게 뒤편의 작은 거실로 앞장서 안내했는데, 거기에는 아주 작은 음침한 뒷마당이 내다보이는 작은 창문이 하나 있었다. 그 마당에서는 빨아 놓은 시트며 식탁보가 (공기가 통하지 않아 헛되이) 빨랫줄 한두 가닥에 걸린 채 마르려 애쓰고 있었다. 그리고 그 펄럭이는 빨랫감 사이에, 마치 돛을 거둘 힘도 없이 축축한 배의 갑판 위에 마지막까지 살아남은 뱃사람처럼, 의기소침한 젊은이 하나가 의자에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"우리 존이에요,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다.\n\n관심이 없어 보이지 않으려고, 클레넘은 그가 거기서 무엇을 하고 있는 것이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"저게 저 애가 누리는 유일한 기분 전환이랍니다,\" 치버리 부인이 다시 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"빨래가 없을 때는 뒷마당에조차 안 나가려 해요. 그런데 이웃들 눈을 가려 줄 빨래가 널려 있으면, 거기 몇 시간이고 앉아 있지요. 몇 시간씩요. 마치 숲속에 있는 기분이라나요!\" 치버리 부인은 다시 고개를 젓고, 어머니다운 몸짓으로 앞치마를 눈에 갖다 댄 다음, 방문객을 다시 가게 쪽으로 데리고 나왔다.\n\n\"앉으시지요, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"우리 존을 그렇게 만든 건 도릿 양이에요, 선생님. 그 애가 그 처녀 때문에 가슴이 미어지고 있답니다. 외람되지만 한 말씀 여쭙겠는데, 저 애가 이러다 결딴나면 그게 부모한테 어떻게 보상이 된단 말입니까?\"\n\n치버리 부인은 호스몽거 레인 일대에서 그 정 깊은 마음씨와 말솜씨로 꽤 존경받는, 푸근해 보이는 여자였는데, 이 말을 무서울 만큼 차분하게 내뱉고는 곧바로 다시 고개를 젓고 눈물을 닦기 시작했다.\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"선생님은 그 댁을 잘 아시고, 그 댁 일에 마음을 써 주셨고, 그 댁에 영향력이 있으시지요. 두 젊은이를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 길을 도모해 주실 수 있다면, 우리 존을 위해서, 또 그 둘 모두를 위해서, 부디 그렇게 해 주십사 간청드립니다!\"\n\n\"제가 그분을 알게 된 짧은 동안,\" 아서가 당혹스러워하며 대꾸했다. \"저는 워낙 익숙해진 터라 리틀—저는 도릿 양을, 부인께서 제게 말씀하시는 것과는 전혀 다른 관점에서 생각하는 데 워낙 익숙해진 터라, 정말 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 그분이 부인의 아드님을 압니까?\"\n\n\"같이 자랐답니다, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"같이 놀았고요.\"\n\n\"그분이 아드님을 흠모하는 사람으로 압니까?\"\n\n\"오! 저런, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 일종의 의기양양한 떨림과 함께 말했다. \"일요일에 그 애를 보고도 그걸 몰랐을 리가 없지요. 다른 건 다 제쳐 두더라도 그 애 지팡이 하나만으로도 진작 알았을 거예요. 존 같은 젊은이가 까닭 없이 손가락 모양 상아 장식이 달린 걸 들고 다니진 않거든요. 저 자신은 처음에 그걸 어떻게 알았냐고요? 그렇게 알았지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 도릿 양은 부인만큼 눈치가 빠르지 않을지도 모르지요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 처녀는 말로 들어서 압니다, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"확실합니까?\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"제가 이 집에 있는 것만큼이나 확실하고 분명합니다. 제 두 눈으로 이 집에서 제 아들이 나가는 걸 봤고, 제 두 눈으로 이 집에서 제 아들이 들어오는 걸 봤으니, 그 애가 그렇게 한 걸 압니다!\" 치버리 부인은 이 앞선 상세함과 되풀이에서 놀랄 만한 강조의 힘을 끌어냈다.\n\n\"그토록 부인을 불안케 하는 그 낙심한 상태에 어떻게 빠지게 되었는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그건,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"바로 그 날, 이 집으로 그 존이 돌아오는 걸 이 눈으로 본 바로 그 날에 일어났습니다. 그 뒤로 이 집에서 한 번도 제정신이었던 적이 없어요. 칠 년 전 저와 그 애 아버지가 분기 단위 세입자로 이 집에 들어온 그 시각 이후로, 그 뒤의 그 애 같은 모습은 한 번도 없었지요!\" 치버리 부인 특유의 문장 구성력 덕분에 이 말에서 진술서와도 같은 효력이 우러났다.\n\n\"이 일에 대한 부인의 견해는 어떠한지 감히 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"되고말고요,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"제가 이 가게에 서 있는 게 진실인 만큼, 명예를 걸고 사실 그대로 말씀드리지요. 우리 존은 누구에게나 좋은 말을 듣고 누구에게나 좋은 바람을 받는 아이예요. 그 처녀가 저 마당에서 어린아이로 놀 때 그 애도 어린아이로 같이 놀았답니다. 그때부터 줄곧 그 처녀를 알아 온 거지요. 그 애가 바로 이 거실에서 저녁을 먹고 일요일 오후에 나가서 그 처녀를 만났어요. 약속이 있었는지 없었는지는 제가 뭐라 말씀드리지 않겠습니다. 그 애가 청혼을 했지요. 그 처녀의 오라비와 언니는 콧대가 높아서 우리 존을 반대해요. 그 처녀의 아버지는 만사 제 잇속만 차려서 그 처녀를 누구에게도 나눠 주려 하지 않고요. 그런 사정 아래서 그 처녀가 우리 존에게 대답한 거예요. '안 돼요, 존, 당신을 받아들일 수 없어요. 저는 어떤 남편도 받아들일 수 없어요. 저는 결코 아내가 될 생각이 없어요. 저는 언제까지나 희생물이 될 생각이에요. 잘 가요, 당신에게 어울리는 다른 사람을 찾으세요, 그리고 저를 잊으세요!' 이런 식으로 그 처녀는, 한결같은 종이 되어 줄 가치도 없는 자들에게 한결같은 종으로 매여 살게 운명 지어진 거예요. 이런 식으로 우리 존은, 빨래 사이에서 감기에 걸리는 것과, 저 마당에서, 제가 방금 그 마당에서 선생님께 보여 드린 것처럼, 어미 가슴을 후벼 파는 폐허 같은 꼴을 드러내는 것밖에는 아무 즐거움도 찾지 못하게 된 거랍니다!\" 여기서 그 선량한 여인은, 가락 없는 숲속에 시름겹게 앉아 있는 아들이 보이는 그 작은 창문을 가리켰다. 그리고 다시 고개를 젓고 눈물을 닦으며, 두 젊은이 모두를 위해서 이 음울한 사태가 밝게 뒤집히도록 그의 영향력을 발휘해 달라고 그에게 애원했다.\n\n그녀는 자기 사정 설명에 어찌나 확신에 차 있었던지, 또 리틀 도릿과 그 가족의 상대적 위치에 관한 한 그것이 너무도 부인할 수 없는 올바른 전제에 근거하고 있었던지라, 클레넘으로서는 다른 쪽에 대해 확신을 가질 수가 없었다. 그는 리틀 도릿에게 어떤 독특한 관심을—그녀를 둘러싼 흔하고 거친 것들에서 비롯되었으면서도 그녀를 그것들로부터 떼어 놓는 그런 관심을—붙이게 된 터라, 그녀가 뒷마당의 젊은 치버리 씨라든가 그런 누군가와 사랑에 빠졌으리라고 추측하는 것이 실망스럽고, 마뜩잖고, 거의 고통스럽기까지 했다. 한편으로 그는, 그녀가 사랑에 빠졌든 빠지지 않았든 똑같이 선하고 똑같이 참되다고, 그리고 그녀를 일종의 길든 요정으로 만들어 그녀가 아는 유일한 사람들로부터 마음속으로 떼어 놓는 대가를 치르게 하는 것은 그저 자기 공상의 나약함일 뿐, 결코 친절한 일이 아니라고 스스로 따져 보았다. 그렇더라도 그녀의 앳되고 청초한 모습, 수줍은 태도, 그 예민한 목소리와 눈의 매력, 그녀가 그 자신의 개성으로써 그의 관심을 끌었던 그 숱한 측면들, 그리고 그녀와 그녀 주위 사람들 사이의 뚜렷한 차이—이 모든 것은 이 새로 제시된 생각과 조화를 이루지 못했고, 결단코 조화를 이루려 하지 않았다.\n\n이런 것들을 마음속으로 곱씹은 끝에—사실 그는 그녀가 아직 말하고 있는 동안에도 그러고 있었다—그는 그 갸륵한 치버리 부인에게, 자기가 언제든 도릿 양의 행복을 도모하고, 또 자신의 힘이 닿는 한, 그리고 그녀의 마음속 소망이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있는 한, 그 소망을 이루어 주려고 최선을 다하리라는 것을 믿어도 좋다고 말했다. 동시에 그는 그녀에게 섣부른 추측과 겉모습을 경계하라고 일렀고, 도릿 양이 불행해질까 봐 철저한 침묵과 비밀을 당부했으며, 특히 아들의 신뢰를 얻어 사정의 실상을 확실히 알아내도록 애써 보라고 충고했다. 치버리 부인은 그 마지막 주의는 불필요하다고 여겼으나, 그래도 해 보겠다고 했다. 그녀는 이 면담에서 은근히 기대했던 만큼의 위안을 다 얻지 못한 듯 고개를 저었지만, 그래도 친절히 수고를 베풀어 준 데 대해 그에게 고마워했다. 그렇게 두 사람은 좋은 사이로 헤어졌고, 아서는 걸어 나왔다.\n\n거리의 인파가 그의 마음속 인파와 부딪치고, 그 두 인파가 뒤엉켜 혼란을 빚자, 그는 런던 다리를 피하고 더 한적한 쪽인 철교 방향으로 꺾어 들었다. 그 다리에 막 발을 들여놓자마자, 그는 리틀 도릿이 자기 앞에서 걸어가고 있는 것을 보았다. 가벼운 산들바람이 부는 쾌청한 날이었고, 그녀는 바로 그 순간 바람을 쐬러 거기 나온 듯했다. 그가 한 시간 안에 아버지의 방에 그녀를 두고 떠나온 터였다.\n\n다른 사람이 곁에 없을 때 그녀의 표정과 태도를 살펴보고 싶다는 그의 바람에 알맞은, 때맞춘 기회였다. 그는 걸음을 재촉했다. 그러나 그녀에게 미처 다가가기도 전에, 그녀가 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"제가 놀라게 했나요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"발소리를 아는 것 같다고 생각했어요,\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서, 그 소리를 알아챘나요, 리틀 도릿? 제 발소리는 도무지 예상하지 못했을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"누구의 것도 예상하지 못했어요. 그렇지만 발소리가 들렸을 때, 그게—당신 것처럼 들린다고 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"더 가는 길인가요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님. 그저 기분 전환 삼아 여기서 좀 걷고 있을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n두 사람은 함께 걸었고, 그녀는 그에게 마음을 터놓는 태도를 되찾고는, 주위를 한 번 둘러본 뒤 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"참 이상해요. 어쩌면 좀처럼 이해하지 못하실 거예요. 저는 가끔 여기서 걷는 것이 거의 매정한 짓처럼 느껴질 때가 있어요.\"\n\n\"매정하다고요?\"\n\n\"강을 보고, 그렇게 넓은 하늘을, 그렇게 많은 것들을, 그렇게 많은 변화와 움직임을 보는 게요. 그러고는, 아시겠지만, 돌아가서 똑같이 비좁은 곳에 계신 그분을 보게 되는 게요.\"\n\n\"아 그렇군요! 하지만 돌아갈 때는, 그런 것들의 기운과 영향을 당신이 함께 지니고 가서 그분을 북돋아 드리는 거라고 생각해야 해요.\"\n\n\"제가 그러나요? 그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요! 선생님은 너무 많이 상상하셔서 저를 너무 대단한 사람으로 만드시는 것 같아 걱정이에요. 만일 선생님이 감옥에 계시다면, 제가 선생님께 그런 위안을 가져다드릴 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그럼요, 리틀 도릿, 저는 확신합니다.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 입술에 어린 떨림과, 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가는 큰 동요의 그림자에서, 그녀의 마음이 아버지에게 가 있음을 알아챘다. 그는 그녀가 평정을 되찾을 수 있도록 잠시 말이 없었다. 그의 팔에 매달린 채 떨고 있는 리틀 도릿은 그 어느 때보다도 치버리 부인의 이론과 어울리지 않았지만, 그렇다고 그의 안에서 새로 솟아난 공상—희망 없는 곳에, 더더욱 새로운 공상으로는, 희망 없고 닿을 수 없는 먼 곳에 누군가 다른 사람이 있을지도 모른다는 공상—과 영 맞지 않는 것도 아니었다.\n\n두 사람이 돌아서는데, 클레넘이 저기 매기가 오고 있다고 말했다. 리틀 도릿이 놀라 고개를 들었고, 그들은 매기와 마주쳤는데, 매기는 그들을 보자마자 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 어찌나 골똘하고 분주하게 종종걸음을 치고 있었던지, 그들이 그녀 쪽으로 돌아설 때까지 그들을 알아보지 못했던 것이다. 이제 그녀는 한순간에 어찌나 양심의 가책을 받았던지, 그녀의 바구니까지도 그 변화에 한몫 거드는 듯했다.\n\n\"매기, 아버지 곁에 있겠다고 나한테 약속했잖아.\"\n\n\"그러려고 했어요, 작은 엄마, 그런데 그분이 못 있게 했는걸요. 그분이 나를 내보내겠다고 하면 나는 가야 하잖아요. 그분이 '매기, 이 편지를 가지고 얼른 다녀와라, 답장이 좋은 거면 육 펜스를 주마' 하고 말하면, 나는 받아야지요. 어머나, 작은 엄마, 가엾은 열 살짜리가 어쩌겠어요? 그리고 팁 씨가—내가 나갈 때 마침 그분이 들어오다가, '어디 가니, 매기?' 하고 묻고, 내가 '아무 데 아무 데 가요' 하고 대답하고, 그분이 '나도 한번 해 봐야겠다' 하고는 조지 술집에 들어가 편지를 써서 그걸 나한테 주면서 '이건 같은 데로 가져가라, 답장이 좋은 거면 일 실링을 주마' 하면, 그건 내 잘못이 아니잖아요, 엄마!\"\n\n아서는 리틀 도릿의 내리깐 눈에서, 그 편지들이 누구에게 보내질 것을 그녀가 예감하는지 읽었다.\n\n\"나는 아무 데 아무 데 가요. 거기요! 내가 가는 데가 거기예요,\" 매기가 말했다. \"나는 아무 데 아무 데 가요. 그건 작은 엄마하고는 상관없는 일이에요—그건 당신이지요, 아시잖아요,\" 매기가 아서를 향해 말했다. \"당신이 아무 데 아무 데로 와 주면, 내가 그걸 당신한테 건네줄 수 있는데.\"\n\n\"우리 그렇게까지 까다롭게 굴진 말자꾸나, 매기. 여기서 그것들을 내게 다오,\" 클레넘이 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 길 건너편으로 와요,\" 매기가 아주 큰 소리의 속삭임으로 대답했다. \"작은 엄마는 이걸 아무것도 몰라야 하는데, 당신이 까탈 부리고 어정거리지 말고 아무 데 아무 데로 가기만 했으면 작은 엄마가 아무것도 몰랐을 거예요. 그건 내 잘못이 아니에요. 나는 시키는 대로 해야 하는걸요. 나한테 그런 걸 시키는 사람들이야말로 부끄러운 줄 알아야지요.\"\n\n클레넘은 길 건너편으로 가서 서둘러 편지들을 펴 보았다. 아버지가 보낸 편지에는, 자기가 굳게 믿고 의지하던 시내로부터의 송금이 어그러지는, 난생처음 겪는 처지에 뜻밖에도 놓이게 되어, 스물세 해에 걸친(이중으로 밑줄이 그어져 있었다) 그 불행한 수감의 사정에 매여 있는 탓에—그렇지 않았다면 분명히 그러했을 터인데도—직접 찾아가지 못하고 펜을 들었노라고, 펜을 들어 동봉한 차용 증서를 담보로 삼 파운드 십 실링을 융통해 주십사 클레넘 씨에게 간청한다고 적혀 있었다. 아들이 보낸 편지에는, 자기가 마침내 더없이 만족스러운 성격의 영구적인 일자리를 얻었으며, 인생에서 완전한 성공을 거둘 온갖 전망까지 곁들여졌다는 소식을 클레넘 씨가 들으면 기뻐하실 줄로 안다고 적혀 있었다. 다만 그의 고용주가 현재까지의 밀린 봉급을 지급할 형편이 일시적으로 못 되는 데다(그런 처지에서 그 고용주는, 동료 인간을 향해 결코 모자람이 없기를 바라는 그 너그러운 관용에 호소해 왔다고 했다), 거짓된 벗의 사기 행각과 지금의 높은 물가가 겹쳐, 그날 저녁 여섯 시 십오 분 전까지 팔 파운드를 마련하지 못하면 그는 파멸의 벼랑에 몰리게 된다고 했다. 다행히 클레넘 씨가 들으면 기뻐할 일이지만, 그의 정직함을 활기차게 믿어 주는 여러 벗들의 신속한 도움 덕분에, 그는 일 파운드 십칠 실링 사 펜스라는 사소한 잔액만 빼고 이미 그 돈을 마련했는데, 그 잔액을 한 달간만 빌려 주신다면 늘 그러하듯 자애로운 결과가 가득하리라고 했다.\n\n클레넘은 그 자리에서 연필과 수첩의 도움을 받아 이 편지들에 답을 적었다. 아버지에게는 청한 것을 보내고, 아들의 요구에 응하는 것은 사양하는 말을 했다. 그러고는 매기에게 그 답신들을 가지고 돌아가도록 시켰고, 그녀의 부수적인 사업이 실패하여 그녀가 받지 못할 뻔했던 일 실링을 주었다.\n\n그가 다시 리틀 도릿에게 돌아와 두 사람이 아까처럼 걷기 시작했을 때, 그녀가 느닷없이 말했다.\n\n\"저는 가는 게 좋겠어요. 집에 가는 게 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"괴로워하지 마세요,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"편지에는 답을 했어요. 별것 아니었어요. 어떤 내용인지 아시잖아요. 별것 아니었어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 두려워요,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그분 곁을 떠나는 게 두려워요, 그들 중 누구의 곁이든 떠나는 게 두려워요. 제가 없으면 그들은 비뚤어져요—그러려는 건 아니지만—매기까지도요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것이 떠맡은 건 아주 순진한 심부름이었어요. 그리고 그걸 당신한테 숨긴 건, 분명히 그저 당신의 걱정을 덜어 주려는 것이라고 여겼을 거예요.\"\n\n\"네, 그랬으면 좋겠어요, 그랬으면 좋겠어요. 하지만 저는 집에 가는 게 좋겠어요! 바로 며칠 전에 언니가 그러더군요, 내가 감옥에 너무 익숙해져서 그곳의 분위기와 성격을 그대로 지니게 되었다고요. 틀림없이 그럴 거예요. 이런 것들을 볼 때면 분명 그러리라는 생각이 들어요. 제 자리는 거기예요. 저는 거기 있는 편이 나아요. 거기서 아주 작은 일이라도 할 수 있는데 여기 있는 건 매정한 짓이에요. 안녕히 가세요. 저는 집에 있는 편이 훨씬 나아요!\"\n\n마치 억눌린 가슴에서 저절로 터져 나오는 듯, 그녀가 이 말을 쏟아 내는 그 괴로운 모양새를 보고 들으면서, 클레넘은 눈에서 눈물이 솟는 것을 참기가 어려웠다.\n\n\"그곳을 집이라고 부르지 말아요, 내 아이여!\" 그가 간청했다. \"당신이 그곳을 집이라고 부르는 걸 듣는 건 언제나 제겐 고통스러워요.\"\n\n\"하지만 거기가 집인걸요! 달리 어디를 집이라 부르겠어요? 어째서 제가 단 한순간이라도 그곳을 잊어야 한단 말인가요?\"\n\n\"당신은 결코 잊지 않아요, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿, 그 어떤 선하고 참된 봉사 속에서도요.\"\n\n\"안 잊었으면, 오 안 잊었으면 좋겠어요! 하지만 저는 거기 있는 편이 나아요. 훨씬 낫고, 훨씬 도리에 맞고, 훨씬 행복해요. 부디 저와 함께 가지 마세요, 저 혼자 가게 해 주세요. 안녕히 가세요, 신의 가호가 있기를. 고마워요, 고마워요.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 간청을 존중하는 편이 낫다고 느껴, 그녀의 가냘픈 모습이 그에게서 빠르게 멀어져 가는 동안 움직이지 않았다. 그 모습이 팔랑이며 시야에서 사라지자, 그는 얼굴을 물 쪽으로 돌리고 서서 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n그녀는 언제든 이 편지들의 발각에 괴로워했을 것이다. 그러나 이토록, 그처럼 걷잡을 수 없는 방식으로 괴로워했을까?\n아니다.\n\n그녀가 아버지가 해진 가장(假裝)을 두른 채 구걸하는 것을 보았을 때, 자기 아버지에게 돈을 주지 말아 달라고 그에게 애원했을 때, 그녀는 괴로워했으나 이와 같지는 않았다. 무언가가 바로 지금 그녀를 예리하게, 한층 더 예민하게 만든 것이다. 그렇다면 희망 없고 닿을 수 없는 먼 곳에 누군가가 있는 것일까? 아니면, 다리 아래로 흐르는 그 어지러운 강물과, 그것의 상류에서 똑같이 흐르던 그 강물—나룻배 이물에 부딪치며 변함없는 가락을 내고, 시간당 그토록 여러 마일을 평화로이 흐르며, 여기엔 골풀, 저기엔 백합, 아무것도 불확실하거나 불온하지 않던 그 강물—을 떠올리는 그 자신의 연상 작용이 이런 의혹을 그의 마음에 불러일으킨 것일까?\n\n그는 그곳에서 오래도록 그의 가엾은 아이 리틀 도릿을 생각했다. 그는 그녀가 집으로 돌아가는 것을 생각했다. 그는 밤에 그녀를 생각했다. 그는 다시 날이 밝아 올 때 그녀를 생각했다. 그리고 가엾은 아이 리틀 도릿은, 마셜시 담장의 그늘 아래에서 그를 생각했다—너무도 한결같이, 아, 너무도 한결같이!\n\n# CHAPTER 23. Machinery in Motion\n\n클레넘이 자기에게 맡긴 대니얼 도이스와의 교섭 건에 대해, 미글스 씨는 어찌나 재빠르고 활발하게 움직였던지 이내 그것을 사무적인 궤도에 올려놓았고, 어느 날 아침 아홉 시에 클레넘을 찾아와 보고를 했다.\n\n\"도이스는 자네의 호의를 무척 고맙게 여기고 있네.\" 그는 이렇게 운을 떼며 본론에 들어갔다. \"그리고 무엇보다도 자네가 직접 공장의 사정을 조사하여 그것을 온전히 파악해 주기를 바라고 있어. 그가 자기 장부와 서류 일체의 열쇠를 내게 넘겨주었는데—여기 이 주머니에서 짤랑거리는 게 그거야—그가 내게 준 단 한 가지 당부는 이거였네. '클레넘 씨가 내가 아는 것이라면 무엇이든 알 수 있도록, 나와 완전히 동등한 위치에 설 수단을 갖게 해 주시오. 설령 결국 아무 일도 성사되지 않더라도, 그분은 내 신뢰를 존중해 주실 거요. 처음부터 그걸 확신할 수 없었다면, 나는 그분과 아예 관계를 맺지 않았을 테니.' 자, 보게나.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"여기에 대니얼 도이스라는 사람이 고스란히 드러나 있지 않은가.\"\n\n\"매우 명예로운 인품이군요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지, 암. 의심할 여지가 없네. 별나지만, 아주 명예로워. 하지만 정말 별나단 말이야. 그런데 자네 이걸 믿겠나, 클레넘?\" 미글스 씨가 친구의 괴짜다움을 진심으로 즐기며 말했다. \"내가 그—뭐라더라 그 마당에서 꼬박 한나절을—\"\n\n\"블리딩 하트 말입니까?\"\n\n\"블리딩 하트 마당에서 꼬박 한나절을 보내고서야 겨우 그 사람더러 그 얘기를 꺼내게 만들었다네.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"어째서냐고, 이 사람아? 내가 그 일과 관련해 자네 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 그는 손사래를 치더란 말이야.\"\n\n\"제 때문에 손사래를 쳤다고요?\"\n\n\"내가 자네 이름을 꺼내자마자, 클레넘, 그가 이러더군. '그건 결코 안 될 일이오!' 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 내가 물었지. '아무것도 아니오, 미글스. 그저 결코 안 될 일이라는 거요.' 어째서 결코 안 될 일이냐? 자네 좀처럼 믿기 힘들겠지만, 클레넘,\" 미글스 씨가 속으로 웃으며 말했다. \"이런 사연이 드러났어. 그건 결코 안 될 일이라는 게, 자네와 그 사람이 트위크넘으로 함께 걸어 내려가면서 다정한 대화에 빠져들었는데, 그 도중에 그가 동업자를 들일 작정이라고 언급한 적이 있었기 때문이라는 거야. 그때 그는 자네가 세인트폴 대성당처럼 확고하고 최종적으로 자리 잡은 사람이라고 여겼던 거지. '그런데,' 하고 그가 말하더군. '내가 그분의 제안을 받아들인다면, 클레넘 씨는 이제 와서, 내가 솔직하고 거리낌 없이 한 그 말 속에 음흉하고 계산된 속셈이 있었다고 믿게 될지도 모르오. 그건 나로서는 견딜 수가 없소.' 그가 말했네. '정말이지 나는 그것을 견디기엔 너무 자존심이 강하오.'\"\n\n\"저라면 의심하기는커녕—\"\n\n\"물론 그렇겠지.\" 미글스 씨가 가로막았다. \"나도 그 사람에게 그렇게 말했다네. 그렇지만 그 벽을 넘는 데 한나절이 걸렸어. 나 말고 다른 누가(그는 나와 오래 알고 지냈으니 나를 좋아하지) 그 벽을 넘을 수 있었을지 의문이야. 자, 클레넘. 이 사무적인 장애물을 넘고 나니, 그는 자네와 다시 이야기를 재개하기 전에 내가 먼저 장부를 살펴보고 나름의 의견을 정해 달라는 조건을 붙였네. 그래서 내가 장부를 살펴보고 나름의 의견을 정했지. '전체적으로 보아 찬성이오, 반대요?' 하고 그가 묻더군. '찬성이오.' 내가 답했지. '그러면,' 하고 그가 말하기를, '이제 자네는, 친애하는 벗이여, 클레넘 씨가 스스로 의견을 정할 수단을 그분에게 드려도 좋소. 그분이 어떤 편견도 없이 완전히 자유롭게 그리할 수 있도록, 나는 일주일간 도시를 떠나 있겠소.' 그래서 그는 떠났네.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 이 일의 알찬 결말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 저로서는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그분의 솔직함과 그—그 무엇이라 해야 할지, 그것에 대해 깊은 감명을 받았다고 말씀드려야겠군요.\"\n\n\"별남이지.\" 미글스 씨가 끼어들었다. \"내가 보기엔 그래!\"\n\n그것이 클레넘의 입에 떠오른 단어와 꼭 같지는 않았으나, 그는 기분 좋은 친구의 말을 가로막지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 그러니 이제,\" 미글스 씨가 덧붙였다. \"자네가 적당하다고 생각하는 대로 곧 일에 착수하면 되네. 설명이 필요한 데가 있으면 내가 설명을 맡기로 했네만, 엄정히 공평을 지키고 그 이상은 아무것도 하지 않기로 했어.\"\n\n그들은 바로 그날 오전부터 블리딩 하트 마당에서 면밀한 조사를 시작했다. 도이스 씨가 자기 사업을 운영하는 방식에는 숙련된 눈으로 보면 자잘한 특이점들이 쉽게 눈에 띄었지만, 그것들은 거의 언제나 난점을 기발하게 단순화하는 무언가와, 원하는 목적에 이르는 명백한 지름길을 담고 있었다. 그의 서류가 밀려 있다는 것, 그리고 사업의 역량을 키우려면 도움이 필요하다는 것은 충분히 분명했다. 그러나 여러 해에 걸친 그의 사업 성과는 모두 또렷이 기록되어 있어 어렵잖게 확인할 수 있었다. 이번 조사를 위해 따로 손본 것은 아무것도 없었다. 모든 것이 진짜 작업복 차림 그대로, 어떤 정직하고 투박한 질서 속에 놓여 있었다. 그가 직접 손으로 쓴 계산과 기재가 많았는데, 그것들은 무뚝뚝하게 적혀 있어 그다지 깔끔하거나 정밀하지는 않았지만, 언제나 명료했고 곧장 목적을 향했다. 아서는 훨씬 정교하고 보기 좋은 사업 외양—이를테면 에두름국의 기록 같은 것—이 오히려 훨씬 덜 쓸모 있으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그것은 훨씬 덜 알아먹게 할 의도로 만들어진 것이기 때문이었다.\n\n사나흘 꾸준히 매달리고 나니 그는 꼭 알아 두어야 할 사실을 모두 익혔다. 미글스 씨는 그 내내 곁에 있으면서, 저울과 됫박에 속한 그 작고 밝은 안전등으로 어두운 데를 늘 기꺼이 비춰 주었다. 두 사람은 사업의 절반 지분을 사들이는 데 적정한 금액을 합의했고, 그러자 미글스 씨가 봉인된 종이 한 장을 열어 보였는데, 거기에는 대니얼 도이스가 그 가치를 매겨 적어 둔 액수가 있었다. 그것은 오히려 조금 더 낮은 금액이었다. 이리하여 대니얼이 돌아왔을 때, 그는 일이 거의 매듭지어진 것을 발견했다.\n\n\"이제는 털어놓아도 되겠습니다, 클레넘 씨.\" 그는 진심 어린 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내가 위아래로 샅샅이 뒤져 동업자를 찾았다 한들, 이보다 더 내 마음에 드는 사람을 찾지는 못했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저도 같은 말을 드립니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"나는 두 분 다에 대해 이렇게 말하겠소.\" 미글스 씨가 덧붙였다. \"두 분은 천생연분이오. 클레넘, 자네는 자네의 상식으로 이 사람을 단속하고, 댄, 자네는 공장에 자네의 그—\"\n\n\"비범한 식견으로 말입니까?\" 대니얼이 조용한 미소를 띠며 거들었다.\n\n\"원한다면 그렇게 불러도 좋소—그리고 두 사람은 서로에게 오른팔이 되어 줄 거요. 자, 실무가로서 내 오른손을 여기 두 분께 내밀어 그 약속을 다집니다.\"\n\n매입은 한 달 안에 마무리되었다. 그것으로 아서에게는 사적인 재산이라곤 몇백 파운드를 넘지 않는 정도밖에 남지 않았지만, 활동적이고 전도유망한 진로가 그에게 열렸다. 세 친구는 이 경사스러운 날 함께 식사했다. 공장과 공장 식구들, 아내들과 아이들도 휴일을 맞아 함께 식사했다. 블리딩 하트 마당까지도 식사를 하며 고기로 가득 찼다. 그러나 채 두 달도 지나지 않아 블리딩 하트 마당은 또다시 모자란 배급에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지 그 잔치를 잊어버렸다. 동업에서 새로운 것이라고는 문기둥에 새 페인트로 쓴 글귀, '도이스 앤드 클레넘'밖에 없어 보였다. 클레넘 자신에게조차 자기가 이 회사의 일을 여러 해 동안 머릿속에 담아 온 것처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n그의 전용으로 따로 둔 작은 사무실은, 길고 나지막한 작업장 끝에 있는 나무와 유리로 된 방이었다. 작업장은 작업대와 죔쇠, 연장, 가죽 띠, 바퀴 들로 가득했는데, 이것들이 증기기관과 맞물려 돌아갈 때면, 마치 사업을 가루로 갈아 버리고 공장을 산산조각 내려는 자살의 사명이라도 띤 듯 미친 듯이 돌아갔다. 바닥과 천장에 난 커다란 들창문들이 위층 작업장 및 아래층 작업장과 통해 있어, 이 풍경 속에 한 줄기 빛기둥을 드리웠는데, 그것은 비슷한 빛줄기가 아벨의 살해를 증언하던 어린 시절의 옛 그림책을 클레넘의 마음속에 떠올리게 했다. 소음들은 사무실로부터 충분히 멀리 떨어져 차단되어 있어, 주기적으로 들려오는 쇳소리와 쿵쿵거림 사이로 분주한 윙윙거림으로 뒤섞였다. 묵묵히 일하는 사람들의 모습은 작업대마다 춤추고 널빤지 틈마다 부글거려 올라오는 쇳가루와 강철 부스러기로 거뭇거뭇했다. 작업장은 아래 바깥마당에서 사다리를 타고 올라가게 되어 있었는데, 그 사다리 밑은 연장을 가는 커다란 숫돌의 가림막 노릇을 했다. 그 전체가 클레넘의 눈에는 한꺼번에 환상적이면서도 실용적인 분위기를 띠었고, 그것은 반가운 변화였다. 그래서 그는 사업 서류들을 완벽한 질서로 정돈하는 첫 작업에서 눈을 들 때마다, 자기 일에서 느끼는 새로운 즐거움으로 그것들을 바라보곤 했다.\n\n그러던 어느 날 이렇게 눈을 들다가, 그는 보닛 하나가 사다리를 힘겹게 올라오는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐다. 이 뜻밖의 출현 뒤를 또 다른 보닛이 따랐다. 그제야 그는 첫 번째 보닛이 F씨의 고모 머리에 얹혀 있고, 두 번째 보닛이 플로라의 머리에 얹혀 있음을 알아보았는데, 플로라는 그 유산을 가파른 비탈 위로 상당한 곤란을 무릅쓰고 떠밀어 올린 듯했다.\n\n이 방문객들을 보고 마냥 황홀해진 것은 아니었으나, 클레넘은 지체 없이 사무실 문을 열고 그들을 작업장에서 빼냈다. 이 구조는 더욱 필요한 것이었으니, F씨의 고모가 벌써 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리며 손에 든 돌처럼 단단한 손가방으로 증기 동력을 하나의 제도로서 위협하고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"어머나 세상에, 아서—아니, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 훨씬 적절하겠죠—여기 올라오느라 이 고생이라니, 게다가 비상 사다리도 없이 어떻게 다시 내려가나, F씨의 고모님은 계단 사이로 빠져 온통 멍이 들고, 게다가 당신은 이 기계니 주물이니 하는 일에 들어와 있다니 글쎄 생각 좀 해 봐요, 게다가 우리한테는 한마디도 안 했죠!\"\n\n이렇게 플로라가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. 그동안 F씨의 고모는 자신의 소중한 발등을 우산으로 문지르며 앙심에 찬 눈으로 노려보았다.\n\n\"그날 이후로 우리를 보러 한 번도 돌아오지 않다니 정말 너무 매정해요, 하긴 당연히 우리 집에 무슨 끌릴 만한 게 있을 리 없고 당신은 훨씬 더 즐거운 일에 매여 있었겠죠, 그건 거의 틀림없고, 그런데 그 사람은 금발일까 흑발일까 푸른 눈일까 검은 눈일까 궁금하네요, 하긴 그녀가 모든 면에서 나와 완벽한 대조가 아니리라곤 기대하지 않아요, 나야 나도 잘 알다시피 실망스러운 사람이니까요, 당신이 헌신적인 건 의심할 바 없이 당연한 일이지요, 하긴 내가 무슨 말을 하는 건지 아서 신경 쓰지 말아요 나도 나를 잘 모르겠으니 어머나 세상에!\"\n\n이때쯤 그는 사무실 안에 그들이 앉을 의자를 놓아 두었다. 플로라는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 그에게 옛날 그 눈길을 보냈다.\n\n\"도이스 앤드 클레넘이라니, 게다가 도이스란 또 누구일까요,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"틀림없이 유쾌한 사람일 테고 어쩌면 결혼했거나 아니면 딸이 있을지도, 자 정말 그래요? 그렇다면 동업이 이해가 되고 모든 게 환히 보이네요, 아무 말도 하지 말아요 내가 그런 질문을 할 자격이 없다는 걸 잘 아니까요, 한때 벼려졌던 황금 사슬이 끊어졌으니 그게 마땅하지요.\"\n\n플로라는 그의 손 위에 자기 손을 다정하게 얹고는, 또 한 번 그 젊디젊은 눈길을 그에게 던졌다.\n\n\"친애하는 아서—습관의 힘이란, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 모든 면에서 더 섬세하고 지금 형편에 맞겠죠—이렇게 함부로 끼어드는 무례를 부디 용서해 주세요, 하지만 영영 시들어 다시는 피지 않을 옛 시절을 빌미로 이만한 무람없음은 부려도 되지 않을까 싶어서, F씨의 고모님과 함께 들러 축하하고 행운을 빌어 드리려고 왔어요, 중국보다는 훨씬 우월하다는 건 부인할 수 없고 훨씬 가깝긴 한데 더 높이 올라가야 하긴 했지만요!\"\n\n\"이렇게 와 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그리고 친절히 저를 기억해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다, 플로라.\"\n\n\"적어도 나로서야 차마 입에 담을 수 없는 만큼이죠.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 따로따로 스무 번쯤 죽어 묻혔다 해도 당신이 진정으로 나를, 아니 그 비슷한 무엇이라도 기억하기 전에 그랬을 게 틀림없으니까요, 그럼에도 한 가지 마지막 말을 하고 싶어요, 한 가지 마지막 해명을 드리고 싶어요—\"\n\n\"이런, 핀칭 부인.\" 아서가 깜짝 놀라 만류했다.\n\n\"오 그 듣기 싫은 이름은 말고, 플로라라고 불러요!\"\n\n\"플로라, 새삼 수고스럽게 또 해명을 하실 만한 가치가 있겠습니까? 정말이지 그런 건 전혀 필요 없습니다. 저는 만족합니다—더할 나위 없이 만족합니다.\"\n\n여기서 F씨의 고모가 다음과 같은 가차 없고 무시무시한 진술을 내놓는 바람에 분위기가 한 차례 흐트러졌다.\n\n\"도버 길에는 이정표가 있어!\"\n\n그녀가 인류 전체를 향한 그 살벌한 적의를 담아 이 포탄을 발사하는 통에, 클레넘은 어떻게 막아야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없었다. 더구나 이 노부인이 자기를 극도로 혐오하고 있음이 분명한데도 방문이라는 영예를 베푸는 까닭에 이미 마음이 어리둥절해 있던 터라 더욱 그랬다. 그녀가 신랄함과 경멸을 내뿜으며 까마득히 먼 곳을 노려보고 앉아 있는 동안, 그는 당혹스러운 눈으로 그녀를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 플로라는 그 말이 마치 더없이 적절하고 유쾌한 것이라도 되는 양 받아들이며, F씨의 고모가 기개가 대단하다고 소리 내어 흐뭇하게 평했다. 이 칭찬에 자극받았는지 아니면 타오르는 분노에 자극받았는지, 그 존엄한 여인은 이어서 덧붙였다. \"할 수 있거든 그가 한번 맞서 보라지!\" 그러고는 돌처럼 단단한 손가방(엄청난 크기에 화석 같은 외양을 한 부속물이었다)을 뻣뻣하게 움직여, 그 도전이 던져진 불운한 상대가 바로 클레넘임을 가리켰다.\n\n\"마지막 말을,\" 플로라가 말을 이었다. \"하려던 참이었어요, 한 가지 마지막 해명을 드리고 싶다고요, F씨의 고모님과 나는 영업 시간에 끼어들지 않았을 거예요, F씨도 사업을 했으니까요, 비록 포도주 거래이긴 했지만 그래도 사업은 사업이고 뭐라 부르든 사업이며 사업 습관이란 다 똑같지요, F씨 본인을 봐도 그래요, 그분은 날씨가 밝든 어둡든 어김없이 오후 여섯 시 십 분 전이면 늘 신발을 매트에 올려 두고 아침 여덟 시 십 분 전이면 부츠를 난로 울 안에 넣어 두셨으니까요—그러니 동기 없이는 끼어들지 않았을 거고, 그 동기가 선의에서 나온 것이니 선의로 받아들여 주시기를 바라요 아서, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 훨씬 적절하고, 어쩌면 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 더 사업가다울지도 모르겠네요.\"\n\n\"제발 사과 같은 말씀은 마세요.\" 아서가 간청했다. \"두 분은 언제나 환영입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말해 주니 참 점잖네요 아서—클레넘 씨란 말은 입 밖에 나오기 전까진 떠오르질 않아요, 영영 사라진 옛 시절의 습관이 그러하니까요, 고요한 밤이면 흔히, 잠의 사슬이 사람을 옭아매기도 전에, 애틋한 기억이 지난날의 빛을 사람 둘레에 데려온다더니 정말 그래요—참 점잖긴 한데 점잖긴 해도 진심보다 더 점잖은 게 아닐까 두려워요, 왜냐하면 아빠한테 편지 한 줄, 명함 한 장 보내지 않고 기계 사업에 뛰어들다니—내 얘긴 아니에요 한때는 그런 시절도 있었지만 그건 지나갔고 이젠 냉엄한 현실이 나의 어머나 세상에 신경 쓰지 말아요—그건 점잖아 보이진 않잖아요, 당신도 인정해야 할걸요.\"\n\n이번에는 플로라의 쉼표마저 달아나 버린 듯했다. 그만큼 그녀는 앞서 만났을 때보다 훨씬 더 두서없고 말이 많았다.\n\n\"하긴 정말,\" 그녀가 서둘러 말을 이었다. \"다른 걸 기대할 것도 없고 왜 기대하겠으며 기대할 게 아니라면 왜 그래야겠어요, 나는 당신이나 누구를 탓할 마음은 조금도 없어요, 당신 어머니와 우리 아빠가 우리를 죽도록 들볶아 그 황금 사발을—아니 결속을 끊었을 때, 하긴 무슨 말인지 아실 테고 모른다 해도 잃을 것도 없고 마찬가지로 신경도 안 쓰리란 건 감히 덧붙여 두지요—우리를 묶고 있던 황금 결속을 끊고 우리를 소파 위에서 울음 발작에 빠뜨려 적어도 나는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었을 때, 모든 게 달라졌고 F씨에게 손을 내밀면서 나는 두 눈 똑똑히 뜨고 그리한 줄 알지만 그분이 어찌나 불안정하고 어찌나 의기소침해 있던지 강물을, 아니면 약방에서 파는 무슨 기름이라도, 정신없이 들먹였기에 나는 그게 최선이라 여겨 그리한 거예요.\"\n\n\"고마운 플로라, 그 얘기는 우리가 전에 매듭지었잖습니까. 다 정말 옳은 일이었어요.\"\n\n\"당신이 그렇게 생각하는 게 아주 분명하네요.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 아주 태연하게 받아들이니까요, 중국인 줄 몰랐다면 나는 북극 지방인가 했을 거예요, 친애하는 클레넘 씨 그래도 당신 말이 옳고 나는 당신을 탓할 수 없지만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아빠 소유지 근처에 있다는 것은 우리가 팽크스한테 들었어요, 그 사람이 아니었다면 우리는 그것에 대해 한마디도 못 들었을 거예요 그건 분명해요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니요, 그런 말씀 마세요.\"\n\n\"그런 말 안 할 까닭이 어디 있어요 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘이—클레넘 씨라고 부르는 것보다 내겐 더 쉽고 덜 거북해요—내가 알고 당신도 알고 부인할 수도 없는데요.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 부인합니다, 플로라. 저는 머잖아 친구로서 댁을 찾아뵈었을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"어머!\" 플로라가 고개를 까닥하며 말했다. \"그러시겠죠!\" 그러고는 또 한 번 그 옛날 눈길을 그에게 던졌다. \"그건 그렇고 팽크스가 우리한테 말해 주었을 때, 나는 F씨의 고모님과 함께 와서 들러야겠다고 마음먹었어요, 왜냐하면 아빠가—그건 그 이전 일인데—마침 내게 그녀의 이름을 언급하며 당신이 그녀에게 관심이 있다고 말했을 때, 나는 그 자리에서 어머나 세상에 그렇다면 일거리가 있을 때 밖에다 맡길 게 아니라 여기서 그녀를 쓰면 어떨까 했거든요.\"\n\n\"'그녀'라고 하시는 건,\" 이쯤 되자 어지간히 어리둥절해진 클레넘이 끼어들었다. \"F씨의 고모—\"\n\n\"세상에, 아서—옛 추억을 떠올리면 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 정말 더 편하네요—F씨의 고모님이 삯바느질을 하고 날품팔이로 나간단 말은 또 누가 들어 봤대요?\"\n\n\"날품팔이로 나간다고요! 리틀 도릿을 말하는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"네 물론이죠.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"내가 들어 본 가장 이상한 이름 중에서도 가장 이상한 이름이에요, 통행세 받는 시골 어디 동네 이름 같기도 하고, 좋아하는 조랑말이나 강아지나 새 이름 같기도 하고, 아니면 씨앗 가게에서 사다가 정원이나 화분에 심으면 얼룩덜룩 돋아나는 무슨 풀 이름 같기도 하고요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 플로라,\" 아서가 갑자기 대화에 흥미를 느끼며 말했다. \"캐스비 씨가 친절하게도 댁에게 리틀 도릿 이야기를 하셨단 거군요? 뭐라고 하시던가요?\"\n\n\"오 우리 아빠가 어떤 사람인지 당신도 알잖아요.\" 플로라가 다시 말했다. \"그리고 어찌나 얄밉게도 아름답게 앉아서 엄지손가락을 빙글빙글 돌리는지 눈을 떼지 않고 보고 있으면 어지러울 지경인데, 우리가 당신 얘기를 하다가—누가 그 화제를 꺼냈는지는 모르겠어요 아서(도이스 앤드 클레넘) 하지만 분명 나는 아니었어요, 적어도 아니길 바라지만 그 점에 대해선 더 이상 고백 못 하는 걸 정말 양해해 줘야 해요.\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"얼마든지요.\"\n\n\"참 선뜻이네요.\" 플로라가, 매혹적인 수줍음 속에 별안간 말을 뚝 멈추고는 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. \"그건 인정해야겠어요, 아빠가 당신이 그녀 얘기를 진지하게 했다고 말했고 나는 당신에게 들려준 그 말을 했어요, 그게 전부예요.\"\n\n\"그게 전부입니까?\" 아서가 조금 실망하여 말했다.\n\n\"팽크스가 당신이 이 사업에 뛰어들었다고 말해 주고서 그게 정말 당신이라는 걸 우리한테 겨우 납득시켰을 때, 내가 F씨의 고모님에게 그렇다면 우리가 와서 당신에게 물어봐야겠다고 했어요, 필요할 때 그녀를 우리 집에서 쓰는 게 모두에게 좋을지 말이에요, 왜냐하면 그녀가 당신 어머니 댁에 자주 가는 걸 알고, 또 당신 어머니가 성미가 무척 까다로운 분이란 것도 아니까요 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘—안 그랬다면 나는 F씨와 결혼하지 않았을 수도 있고 지금쯤 이 시각에 다른 처지였을 수도 있겠지만 내가 또 부질없는 소리로 빠지네요.\"\n\n\"이런 생각을 해 주시다니 정말 친절하시군요, 플로라.\"\n\n가엾은 플로라는, 그녀의 가장 젊은 눈길보다 한결 더 잘 어울리는 꾸밈없는 진심으로, 그가 그렇게 생각해 주니 기쁘다고 대답했다. 어찌나 진심을 담아 말하던지, 클레넘은 그 자리에서 그녀가 품은 자신의 옛 모습을 큰돈을 치르고서라도 사들여, 그것과 인어 따위를 영영 내던져 버리고 싶을 지경이었다.\n\n\"플로라, 제 생각에는,\" 그가 말했다. \"댁이 리틀 도릿에게 줄 수 있는 일거리와, 그녀에게 베풀 수 있는 친절은—\"\n\n\"네 그렇게 할게요.\" 플로라가 얼른 말했다.\n\n\"그게—그녀에게 큰 도움과 버팀목이 되리라 확신합니다. 그녀에 관해 제가 아는 바를 댁에게 말씀드릴 권리가 제게 있다고는 느껴지지 않습니다, 그 앎을 비밀리에, 침묵을 지키게끔 묶어 두는 사정 아래서 얻었으니까요. 하지만 저는 그 가여운 사람에게 관심이 있고, 또 댁에게 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼의 존경심을 품고 있습니다. 그녀의 삶은 댁이 거의 상상도 못 할 만큼 시련과 헌신으로, 또 조용한 선량함으로 점철된 삶이었습니다. 저는 그녀를 떠올리기만 해도, 말하기는커녕, 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 없습니다. 그 마음이 제가 댁에게 들려줄 수 있는 모든 것을 대신한다 여겨 주시고, 제 감사와 함께 그녀를 댁의 우정에 맡깁니다.\"\n\n다시 한번 그는 가엾은 플로라에게 솔직하게 손을 내밀었다. 다시 한번 가엾은 플로라는 그것을 솔직하게 받지 못했고, 드러내 놓고는 그것이 아무 값어치 없다 여겼으며, 거기에 옛날의 그 꿍꿍이와 비밀스러움을 입혀야만 했다. 그를 당황케 하는 만큼이나 자기 자신의 즐거움을 위해, 그녀는 그 손을 잡으면서 숄 자락으로 덮어 가렸다. 그러더니 사무실의 유리 정면 쪽을 바라보다 두 사람이 다가오는 것을 보고는, 한껏 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"아빠! 쉿, 아서, 제발!\" 그러고는 두려운 놀라움과 처녀다운 마음의 설렘에 못 이겨 기절할 위험에 처한 척, 놀랍도록 그럴듯한 흉내를 내며 비틀비틀 자기 의자로 물러났다.\n\n그러는 사이 가부장은 팽크스의 뒤를 따라 멍하니 환한 얼굴로 사무실 쪽으로 다가왔다. 팽크스가 그를 위해 문을 열고, 그를 끌어들인 다음, 자기 정박지인 한쪽 구석으로 물러났다.\n\n\"플로라한테서 들었네.\" 가부장이 인자한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"이 애가 자네를 보러 온다고, 보러 온다고 말이야. 나도 마침 나와 있던 길이라 나도 와 봐야겠다, 나도 와 봐야겠다 싶었지.\"\n\n그가 그 자체로는 그리 심오할 것 없는 이 선언에, 그 푸른 눈과 빛나는 머리와 길고 흰 머리카락을 동원하여 불어넣은 자비로운 지혜는 참으로 인상적이었다. 그것은 가장 훌륭한 사람들이 토해 낸 가장 고귀한 격언들 가운데 기록해 둘 만한 가치가 있어 보였다. 또한 그가 권하는 의자에 앉으며 클레넘에게 \"그래, 새 사업을 하신다지요, 클레넘 씨? 잘되길 빕니다, 선생, 잘되길 빌어요!\"라고 말했을 때도, 그는 마치 자비로운 기적이라도 베푼 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"핀칭 부인께서 제게 말씀해 주셨습니다, 선생님.\" 아서가 인사를 마친 뒤 말했다—그동안 고인이 된 F씨의 미망인은 그가 그 점잖은 이름을 쓰는 데 손짓으로 항의했다—\"제가 어머니께 추천해 드렸던 그 젊은 바느질꾼을 이따금 부려 쓰고 싶으시다고요. 그래서 제가 부인께 감사를 드리던 참이었습니다.\"\n\n가부장이 둔중하게 고개를 팽크스 쪽으로 돌리자, 그 보좌역은 골몰해 있던 수첩을 치우고는 그를 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"어르신은 그녀를 추천한 게 아닙니다, 아시잖아요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"어떻게 추천하셨겠어요? 그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르셨는데요. 그저 어르신께 이름이 거론되었고 그걸 넘겨주신 거죠. 어르신이 하신 건 그거예요.\"\n\n\"뭐!\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그녀가 어떤 추천이든 받을 만한 사람임을 증명해 보였으니, 결국 거의 같은 셈이지요.\"\n\n\"그녀가 잘 풀려서 기쁘신 거죠.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"하지만 그녀가 잘못 풀렸다 해도 그게 어르신 잘못은 아니었을 겁니다. 지금 와서 그 공이 어르신 것이 아니듯, 잘못됐다 해도 그 탓이 어르신 것은 아니었을 거예요. 어르신은 아무 보증도 안 하셨으니까요. 그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르셨고요.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은,\" 아서가 무심코 한마디 던져 보았다. \"그녀의 가족 중 누구와도 아는 사이가 아니로군요?\"\n\n\"그녀의 가족 중 누구와 아는 사이냐고요?\" 팽크스가 받았다. \"어떻게 그녀의 가족 중 누구와 아는 사이일 수 있겠습니까? 그들에 대해 들어 본 적도 없으신데요. 들어 본 적도 없는 사람들과 아는 사이일 순 없잖습니까, 안 그래요? 그럴 리 없겠죠!\"\n\n이 내내 가부장은 평온하게 미소 지으며 앉아 있었고, 그때그때 형편에 따라 자비롭게 고개를 끄덕이거나 가로저었다.\n\n\"신원 보증인이 된다는 것 말인데요,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"어르신도 대체로 그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시잖습니까. 다 헛것입니다, 그게! 여기 이 마당 아래 어르신네 세입자들을 보세요. 내버려 두면 다들 서로의 보증인이 되어 줄 겁니다. 그렇게 둬서 뭐가 좋겠어요? 한 사람 대신 두 사람한테 떼인다고 만족스러울 건 없지요. 한 사람이면 충분합니다. 돈 못 갚는 사람이 또 돈 못 갚는 사람을 데려와서 자기가 갚을 수 있다고 보증하게 하는 격이에요. 나무다리 두 짝을 단 사람이 또 나무다리 두 짝을 단 사람을 데려와서 자기한테 진짜 다리 두 짝이 있다고 보증하게 하는 거나 마찬가지죠. 그런다고 둘 중 누구도 경보 시합을 할 수 있게 되진 않습니다. 그리고 나무다리는, 정작 하나도 필요 없을 때엔, 두 짝보다 네 짝이 더 성가신 법이고요.\" 팽크스 씨는 예의 그 김 빠지는 소리를 한 번 내뿜는 것으로 말을 맺었다.\n\n뒤이은 잠깐의 침묵을 깨뜨린 것은 F씨의 고모였는데, 그녀는 마지막으로 좌중 앞에 한마디를 던진 이래 강직증에 걸린 듯 꼿꼿이 앉아 있던 참이었다. 이제 그녀는 격렬한 경련을 일으켜—초심자의 신경에는 소스라칠 효과를 낼 법한 것이었다—더없이 살벌한 적의를 담아 말했다.\n\n\"속이 텅 빈 놋쇠 손잡이로는 머리와 두뇌를 만들 수 없어. 네 조지 삼촌이 살아 있을 때도 못 했는데, 죽고 난 지금이야 더 말할 것도 없지.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 평소의 침착함으로 지체 없이 대꾸했다. \"그렇습니까, 부인! 이런 세상에! 그 말씀을 들으니 놀랍군요.\" 그러나 그의 임기응변에도 불구하고, F씨의 고모의 말은 이 작은 모임에 가라앉는 분위기를 자아냈다. 첫째로, 폄훼당한 그 이성의 신전이 죄 없는 클레넘의 머리라는 것을 감출 도리가 없었기 때문이고, 둘째로, 이런 경우마다 대체 누구의 조지 삼촌을 일컫는 것인지, 또 그 호칭 아래 어떤 유령 같은 존재가 불려 나오는 것인지 아무도 알 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그래서 플로라는, 그래도 자기 유산에 대한 어떤 자랑과 득의가 없지는 않은 채로, F씨의 고모가 \"오늘 무척 기운이 넘치니 우리는 그만 가는 게 좋겠다\"고 말했다. 그러나 F씨의 고모는 어찌나 기운이 넘쳤던지 그 제안을 뜻밖의 격분으로 받아들이며 가지 않겠다고 선언했고, 몇 마디 모욕적인 말과 함께 덧붙이기를, 만약 '저자'가—너무도 분명히 클레넘을 가리키는 것이었다—자기를 떼어 내고 싶다면 '저자더러 자기를 창밖으로 내던지라'고 했으며, '저자'가 그 의식을 거행하는 것을 보고 싶다고 다급히 요구했다.\n\n이 진퇴양난에서, 가부장의 물길에서라면 어떤 위기에도 대처할 만한 재간을 갖춘 듯한 팽크스 씨가 잽싸게 모자를 쓰고 사무실 문밖으로 슬쩍 빠져나가더니, 잠시 후 마치 몇 주 동안 시골에라도 다녀온 듯한 인위적인 생기를 띠고 다시 슬쩍 들어왔다. \"아니, 이런, 부인!\" 팽크스 씨가 머리를 쓱 쓸어 올리며 크게 놀란 척 말했다. \"부인 아니십니까? 어떻게 지내십니까, 부인? 오늘 참 매력적이시네요! 뵙게 되어 정말 기쁩니다. 제게 팔을 좀 내주시지요, 부인. 영광스럽게 함께해 주신다면 우리 둘이 잠깐 산책이나 합시다.\" 그러고는 사무실의 전용 계단으로 F씨의 고모를 큰 정중함과 성공으로 호위하여 내려갔다. 그러자 가부장 캐스비 씨가 마치 자기가 그 일을 해낸 양 일어나 점잖게 뒤따랐고, 그 뒤를 차례로 따르던 딸은 옛 연인에게 정신없는 속삭임으로(그녀는 그것을 무척 즐겼다) 그들이 인생의 잔을 찌꺼기까지 다 마셔 버렸노라고 한마디 했으며, 나아가 고인이 된 F씨가 그 밑바닥에 있다고 알쏭달쏭하게 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n다시 혼자가 된 클레넘은 어머니와 리틀 도릿에 관한 예전의 의혹에 또 사로잡혀, 옛 생각과 의심을 곱씹었다. 그가 기계적으로 처리하던 일거리에 그것들이 뒤섞인 채 모두 머릿속을 맴돌고 있을 때, 서류 위에 드리운 그림자에 그는 까닭을 찾아 고개를 들었다. 그 까닭은 팽크스 씨였다. 모자는 마치 철사처럼 뻣뻣한 머리카락의 갈래들이 용수철처럼 솟구쳐 벗겨 내기라도 한 듯 귀 뒤로 젖혀져 있었고, 칠흑 같은 구슬 같은 눈은 캐묻듯이 날카로웠으며, 손톱을 물어뜯으려고 오른손 손가락은 입속에 들어가 있고, 왼손 손가락은 다음 차례를 위해 주머니 속에 예비해 둔 채로, 팽크스 씨는 유리를 통해 장부와 서류 위로 자기 그림자를 드리웠다.\n\n팽크스 씨가 고개를 살짝 캐묻듯 기울이며, 다시 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물었다. 클레넘은 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕여 답했다. 팽크스 씨는 비집고 들어와 책상 곁에 와서는, 두 팔을 책상에 괴어 자기 몸을 단단히 붙들고, 한 번 푹 내쉬고 한 번 콧김을 뿜으며 대화를 시작했다.\n\n\"F씨의 고모님은 가라앉으셨겠지요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 선생.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 운이 없게도 그 부인 가슴속에 강한 적의를 불러일으킨 모양입니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그 까닭을 아십니까?\"\n\n\"그분이야 그 까닭을 아실까요?\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"모르시겠지요.\"\n\n\"저도 모를 거라고 봅니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n그는 수첩을 꺼내 펼쳤다가 덮었고, 그것을 옆에 책상 위에 놓여 있던 모자 속에 떨어뜨린 다음, 모자 바닥에 놓인 그것을 들여다보았다. 이 모두를 무척 신중을 기하는 모양새로 했다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 그가 이윽고 입을 열었다. \"저는 어떤 정보가 필요합니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"이 회사와 관련된 겁니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"아니요.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 무엇과 관련된 겁니까, 팽크스 씨? 말하자면, 그것을 제게서 얻고 싶으시다면요.\"\n\n\"네, 선생, 그래요, 그걸 선생께 얻고 싶습니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"선생을 설득해 내놓게 할 수 있다면요. 가, 나, 다, 라. 다아, 다어, 다이, 다오. 사전 순서대로. 도릿. 그게 그 이름이지요, 선생?\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 다시 한번 그 특유의 소리를 내뿜고는, 오른손 손톱을 물어뜯기 시작했다. 아서가 그를 캐묻듯 바라보자, 그도 그 눈길을 마주 받았다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠군요, 팽크스 씨.\"\n\n\"제가 알고 싶은 게 그 이름입니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 무엇을 알고 싶으신 겁니까?\"\n\n\"선생께서 말씀해 주실 수 있고 또 해 주실 의향이 있는 것이라면 무엇이든요.\" 자기 욕구를 이렇게 포괄적으로 요약하는 데도 팽크스 씨의 기관은 적잖이 힘겹게 돌아가야 했다.\n\n\"이건 별난 방문이군요, 팽크스 씨. 당신이 그런 목적을 가지고 제게 오셨다는 게 다소 기이하게 느껴집니다.\"\n\n\"통째로 기이할 수도 있겠지요.\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"통상적인 절차에서 벗어났을 수도 있지만, 그래도 사업일 수 있습니다. 요컨대 그건 사업입니다. 저는 사업가예요. 사업에 매달리는 것 말고 제가 이 세상에서 무슨 볼일이 있겠습니까? 아무 볼일도 없죠.\"\n\n이 무미건조하고 딱딱한 인물이 과연 진지한 것인지 다시금 미심쩍어진 클레넘은, 또 한 번 그의 얼굴을 주의 깊게 살폈다. 그것은 여느 때처럼 까칠하고 거뭇거뭇했고, 여느 때처럼 열렬하고 민첩했으며, 아까 목소리에서 귀를 스치는 듯하던 그 잠복한 비웃음을 조금이라도 드러내는 기색은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n\"자,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"이 일을 그 자체의 입장에서 따져 보자면, 이건 제 고용주의 일이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"고용주라 하시면 캐스비 씨를 말씀하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n팽크스가 고개를 끄덕였다. \"제 고용주죠. 한 경우를 가정해 보지요. 가령, 제 고용주 댁에서 제가 어떤 이름을 듣습니다—클레넘 씨가 돕고 싶어 하는 젊은 사람의 이름을요. 가령, 그 이름이 마당의 플로니시를 통해 제 고용주에게 처음 거론됩니다. 가령, 제가 플로니시를 찾아갑니다. 가령, 제가 사업상의 일로 플로니시에게 정보를 청합니다. 가령, 플로니시는, 제 고용주에게 여섯 주나 집세가 밀렸으면서도 거절합니다. 가령, 플로니시 부인도 거절합니다. 가령, 두 사람 다 클레넘 씨를 가리킵니다. 그 경우를 가정해 보세요.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서, 선생,\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"가령, 제가 그분을 찾아옵니다. 가령, 여기 제가 와 있습니다.\"\n\n머리 온통에 그 머리카락 갈래들을 곤두세운 채, 숨을 몹시 가쁘고 짧게 들이쉬고 내쉬며, 분주한 팽크스는 한 발짝 물러섰다가(예인선의 비유로 치면 반 바퀴 후진했다가) 마치 자기의 거뭇거뭇한 선체를 온전히 내보이려는 듯하더니, 다시 앞으로 나아가, 수첩이 든 자기 모자 속과 클레넘의 얼굴로 번갈아 그 재빠른 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"팽크스 씨, 당신의 비밀스러운 영역을 침범하지 않으면서, 제가 할 수 있는 한 솔직하게 말씀드리겠습니다. 두 가지를 묻겠습니다. 첫째로—\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 팽크스가 부러진 손톱이 달린 더러운 집게손가락을 치켜들며 말했다. \"알겠어요! '당신의 동기가 무엇이냐?'겠지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다.\"\n\n\"동기라,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"좋습니다. 제 고용주와는 아무 상관 없고, 지금은 밝힐 수 없으며, 지금 밝히자면 우스운 노릇이겠지만, 그래도 좋은 겁니다. 젊은 사람을, 도릿이라는 이름의 사람을 돕고 싶다는 거죠.\" 팽크스가 여전히 집게손가락을 경고하듯 치켜든 채 말했다. \"동기는 좋은 것으로 인정해 두시는 게 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n\"둘째로, 그리고 마지막으로, 당신은 무엇을 알고 싶으신 겁니까?\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 질문이 채 떨어지기도 전에 수첩을 낚아 올려 안쪽 가슴 주머니에 조심스레 단추를 채워 넣고는, 그 내내 똑바로 클레넘을 바라보면서, 한 번 뜸을 들이고 한 번 푹 내쉰 뒤 대답했다. \"저는 어떤 종류의 것이든 보충 정보를 원합니다.\"\n\n클레넘은 미소를 참을 수 없었으니, 저 거추장스러운 배 캐스비 호에 그토록 요긴한 그 헐떡이는 작은 증기 예인선이, 마치 그가 막아 낼 새도 없이 곁에 바싹 붙어 자기가 원하는 것을 죄다 털어 갈 기회라도 노리는 듯, 그를 시중들며 지켜보고 있었기 때문이다. 다만 팽크스 씨의 그 열렬함에는 클레넘의 마음속에 갖가지 궁금증을 자아내는 무언가가 있기도 했다. 잠시 생각한 끝에, 그는 자기가 전해 줄 수 있는 정도의 요긴한 정보를 팽크스 씨에게 제공하기로 마음먹었다. 팽크스 씨가 이번 탐색에 실패하더라도 다른 수단으로 그것을 알아낼 것이 거의 틀림없음을 잘 알았기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 그는 먼저 팽크스 씨에게, 이 폭로에 자기 고용주는 아무 관여도 없으며 자신의 의도는 선의에서 나온 것이라던 자발적 선언을 기억해 달라고 청한 뒤(그 석탄가루 같은 작은 신사는 더없이 열렬하게 그 두 선언을 되풀이했다), 도릿 가의 혈통이나 예전 거주지에 대해서는 전해 줄 정보가 없으며, 그 가족에 대한 자신의 지식은 그것이 지금 다섯 식구로 줄어든 듯하다는 사실—즉 두 형제가 있는데 하나는 독신이고 하나는 세 자녀를 둔 홀아비라는 것—이상은 미치지 못한다고 솔직히 말해 주었다. 온 가족의 나이도 그가 짐작할 수 있는 한 최대한 가깝게 팽크스 씨에게 알려 주었다. 그리고 끝으로 마셜시의 아버지의 처지와, 그가 그러한 신분을 지니게 된 세월과 사건의 경과를 그에게 설명해 주었다. 이 모든 이야기에, 팽크스 씨는 흥미가 깊어질수록 점점 더 심상치 않은 모양새로 콧김을 뿜고 숨을 몰아쉬며 무척 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. 그는 이야기 중에서도 가장 고통스러운 대목에서 가장 흐뭇한 감흥을 길어 올리는 듯했고, 특히 윌리엄 도릿의 오랜 수감 이야기에는 아주 매료된 듯했다.\n\n\"끝으로 한마디 드리자면, 팽크스 씨,\" 아서가 말했다. \"저는 이 말씀만 드리겠습니다. 제게는 단순한 개인적 호의를 넘어서서, 도릿 가에 대해 가능한 한 적게 말하려는—특히 어머니 댁에서는요(팽크스 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다)—그리고 가능한 한 많이 알아 두려는 이유가 있습니다. 당신처럼 그토록 사업에 충실한 분이라면—응?\"\n\n팽크스 씨가 별안간 평소와 다른 세찬 기세로 그 콧김 뿜는 소리를 냈던 것이다.\n\n\"아무것도 아닙니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"당신처럼 그토록 사업에 충실한 분이라면 공정한 거래가 무엇인지 완벽히 아실 겁니다. 저는 당신과 공정한 거래를 하고 싶습니다. 제가 당신을 깨우쳐 드렸듯, 당신도 도릿 가에 관해 알게 되는 것이 생기면 그것을 제게 알려 주시는 거지요. 제가 미리 조건을 정해 두지 않은 것이 당신에게 제 사업 습관에 대해 그리 미덥지 못한 인상을 줄지도 모르겠습니다만,\" 클레넘이 말을 이었다. \"저는 그것을 명예의 문제로 삼는 편이 좋습니다. 저는 약삭빠른 원칙으로 처리되는 사업을 하도 많이 봐 와서, 솔직히 말씀드리면, 팽크스 씨, 그런 데 진력이 났습니다.\"\n\n팽크스 씨가 웃었다. \"거래 성립입니다, 선생.\" 그가 말했다. \"제가 그것을 지키는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 그는 잠시 서서 클레넘을 바라보며 열 손톱을 빙 둘러 가며 물어뜯었다. 자기가 들은 바를 머릿속에 단단히 새기며, 기억의 빈틈을 메울 수단이 더는 곁에 없게 되기 전에 그것을 꼼꼼히 되짚어 보는 것이 분명했다. \"다 됐습니다.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다. \"그럼 이만 안녕히 계십시오, 마당에서 수금하는 날이라서요. 아 참, 그건 그렇고. 지팡이를 짚은 절름발이 외국인 하나가 있는데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요. 보아하니 당신도 가끔은 보증인을 받기도 하는군요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그자가 갚을 수 있을 때라면요, 선생.\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"받아 낼 수 있는 건 죄다 받아 내고, 억지로 토해 내게 할 수 없는 건 죄다 붙들어 두는 것, 그게 사업이지요. 지팡이를 짚은 그 절름발이 외국인이 마당 아래 꼭대기 방을 원합니다. 그자가 그걸 감당할 만합니까?\"\n\n\"제가 감당하지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그자를 제가 보증하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그거면 됐습니다. 블리딩 하트 마당에서 제가 받아 내야 하는 건,\" 팽크스가 그 건을 자기 수첩에 적으며 말했다. \"제 채권입니다. 보시다시피 저는 제 채권을 원하지요. 갚든지, 아니면 잡힐 물건을 내놓든지! 그게 마당 아래의 구호입니다. 지팡이를 짚은 그 절름발이 외국인은 선생께서 자기를 보냈다고 했지만, 그자야 (그쯤이야) 무굴 대제가 자기를 보냈다고도 할 수 있는 자입니다. 그자가 병원에 있었다지요, 아마?\"\n\n\"네. 사고를 당해서요. 이제 막 퇴원했습니다.\"\n\n\"사람을 병원에 들이는 건 그자를 빈민으로 전락시키는 거라고들 하더군요, 선생, 제가 듣기로는요?\" 팽크스가 말했다. 그러고는 다시 한번 그 별난 소리를 내뿜었다.\n\n\"저도 그렇게 들었습니다.\" 클레넘이 차갑게 말했다.\n\n이때쯤 출발할 채비를 완전히 갖춘 팽크스 씨는, 순식간에 김을 올리더니, 다른 신호도 의례도 없이, 사무실에서 미처 다 빠져나갔다 싶기도 전에 콧김을 뿜으며 사다리를 내려가 블리딩 하트 마당으로 헤집고 들어갔다.\n\n그날 남은 시간 내내, 무서운 팽크스가 그 안을 휘젓고 다니는 통에 블리딩 하트 마당은 공포에 휩싸였다. 그는 주민들에게 집세를 미룬 죄를 늘어놓으며 일장 연설을 하고, 자기 채권을 요구하고, 퇴거 통지와 강제 집행을 으르렁대고, 체납자들을 몰아붙이고, 가는 곳마다 앞으로 공포의 물결을 보내고 뒤로도 그것을 남겼다. 사람들은 무리를 지어, 불길한 끌림에 이끌려, 그가 있는 줄로 알려진 집 밖에 숨어, 그가 거주자들에게 늘어놓는 일장연설의 토막이라도 엿들으려 했다. 그러다 그가 계단을 내려온다는 소문이 돌면, 미처 흩어지기도 전에 그가 느닷없이 그들 한복판에 들이닥쳐 그들 자신의 체납금을 요구하며 그 자리에 못 박아 두는 일이 잦았다. 그날 남은 시간 내내, \"저들이 무슨 수작이지?\" \"저들이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 하려는 거야?\" 하는 팽크스 씨의 소리가 온 마당에 울려 퍼졌다. 팽크스 씨는 변명도 들으려 하지 않고, 불평도 들으려 하지 않고, 수리 요청도 들으려 하지 않고, 무조건 즉석에서 내는 돈 말고는 그 무엇도 들으려 하지 않았다. 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 콧김을 뿜으며 별난 방향으로 휙휙 내달으면서, 매 순간 더 달아오르고 더 거뭇거뭇해지는 채로, 그는 마당의 물결을 더없이 사납고 탁한 상태로 휘저어 놓았다. 그가 계단 꼭대기 지평선 위에서 씩씩대며 사라지는 것이 목격된 지 꼬박 두 시간이 지나서야 그것은 다시 잔잔한 물로 가라앉았다.\n\n그날 밤 마당의 사람 모이는 명소마다 블리딩 하트 주민들이 몇몇 작은 무리를 이루었는데, 그들 사이에서는 팽크스 씨가 상대하기 까다로운 사람이라는 데, 그리고 캐스비 씨 같은 신사가 자기 집세를 그자의 손에 맡기고서 그자의 본색을 끝내 알지 못하다니 참으로 유감스러운 일이라는 데 이견이 없었다. 왜냐하면(블리딩 하트 주민들이 말하기를) 저런 머리카락과 저런 눈을 가진 신사분이 손수 집세를 거두신다면, 부인, 이런 들볶임과 시달림은 하나도 없을 테고 만사가 아주 달라질 거라는 것이었다.\n\n바로 그 저녁 그 시각, 그 분초에—오전에 그 들볶음이 시작되기에 앞서, 자기의 빛나는 혹과 비단 같은 머리카락에 이런 신망을 자아내려는 뚜렷한 의도를 품고 마당을 평온하게 떠다녔던 그 가부장은—바로 그 시각 그 분초에, 천 문의 대포에 맞먹는 그 일류 사기꾼은 집에서 기진맥진한 자기 예인선의 작은 선거 안에 묵직하게 들어앉아 허우적대며, 엄지손가락을 빙글빙글 돌리며 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 일은 아주 형편없었네, 팽크스, 아주 형편없는 하루였어. 내가 보기엔, 자네, 그리고 나 자신을 위해서라도 이 말은 단호히 해 두어야겠는데, 자네는 돈을 훨씬 더 거둬 왔어야 했어, 훨씬 더 많이 말이야.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 24. Fortune-Telling\n\n바로 그날 저녁 리틀 도릿은 플로니시 씨의 방문을 받았다. 그는 그녀와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다는 뜻을, 어찌나 두드러진 일련의 헛기침으로 내비쳤던지, 그녀의 바느질 일에 관한 한 그녀의 아버지가 보려 하지 않는 자만큼 눈먼 자는 없다는 격언의 본보기라는 생각에 힘을 실어 줄 정도였는데, 마침내 문밖의 공동 계단에서 그녀와의 면담을 얻어 냈다.\n\n\"오늘 저희 집에 어떤 부인이 한 분 오셨더랬어요, 도릿 양.\" 플로니시가 낮게 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"그리고 함께 온 또 한 분은, 제가 여태 본 늙은 살쾡이 중에 그런 살쾡이가 또 없습디다. 어찌나 사람 머리를 댕강 물어뜯듯 쏘아붙이는지, 원, 세상에!\"\n\n온순한 플로니시는 처음에는 좀처럼 에프 씨의 고모에게서 마음을 떼어 놓지 못했다. \"글쎄,\" 그가 변명하듯 말했다. \"장담컨대 그분은 정말이지 식초 같은 분이라니까요.\"\n\n마침내 그는 큰 애를 써서 그 화제에서 충분히 벗어나 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 지금 그분 얘긴 여기 끼어들 게 아니죠. 다른 부인 말인데요, 그분은 캐스비 씨의 따님이에요. 그리고 캐스비 씨가 형편이 넉넉하다면, 그보다 더 넉넉한 사람이 없을 만큼 넉넉하다면, 그건 결코 팽크스 잘못이 아니에요. 팽크스로 말하자면, 그 사람은 해요, 정말로 해요, 그럼요, 정말 한다니까요!\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 늘 그렇듯 다소 모호했으나, 양심껏 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그분이 저희 집에 오신 까닭은요,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"도릿 양이 그 카드에 적힌 곳으로—그게 캐스비 씨 댁인데요, 거기 뒤편에 팽크스가 사무실을 두고서 정말 믿기지 않을 만큼 일을 한답니다—찾아와 주신다면, 그분을 기꺼이 고용하고 싶다는 전갈을 남기려는 거였어요. 그분은 클레넘 씨의 오래되고 소중한 친구라고 특별히 말씀하시면서, 그분 친구에게 쓸모 있는 친구가 되어 드리고 싶다고 바라셨어요. 그게 그분 말씀 그대로예요. 도릿 양이 내일 아침에 올 수 있는지 알고 싶다 하시기에, 제가 도릿 양을 만나 여쭤보고, 오늘 밤에 거기 들러서 그러겠다고, 아니면 내일 약속이 있으시면 언제가 좋을지 전하겠다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"내일 갈 수 있어요, 고마워요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"정말 친절하시네요, 하지만 아저씨는 늘 친절하시죠.\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 자기 공을 겸손하게 부인하며 그녀가 다시 들어갈 수 있도록 방문을 열어 주었고, 자기는 도무지 밖에 나간 적이 없다는 듯 어찌나 빤한 시늉을 하며 뒤따라 들어가던지, 그녀의 아버지가 그리 의심 많은 사람이 아니더라도 알아챘을 법했다. 그러나 그는 상냥한 무심함 속에서 아무것도 눈치채지 못했다. 플로니시는 잠시 이야기를 나누었는데, 거기에는 한때 수감자였던 옛 의무감과 지금은 변변찮은 바깥 친구라는 현재의 특권이 뒤섞였고, 다시 그것은 미장이라는 자기의 낮은 처지로 가늠되었다. 그러고는 작별을 고했다. 떠나기 전에 감옥을 한 바퀴 돌고, 스키틀 놀이를 구경하면서, 언젠가 다시 이곳으로 돌아오는 것이 자기 운명일지도 모른다고 믿을 나름의 이유가 있는 옛 거주자의 복잡한 심정으로 바라보았다.\n\n이른 아침, 리틀 도릿은 매기에게 집안일을 단단히 맡겨 두고 가부장의 천막으로 길을 나섰다. 그녀는 일 페니가 들었지만 아이언 브리지로 갔고, 여정의 그 구간에서는 다른 어느 곳에서보다 더 천천히 걸었다. 여덟 시 오 분 전에 그녀의 손은 가부장 댁의 문고리에 닿았는데, 그 문고리는 그녀가 손을 뻗어 닿을 수 있는 꼭 그 높이에 있었다.\n\n그녀는 문을 열어 준 젊은 여자에게 핀칭 부인의 카드를 건넸고, 젊은 여자는 \"플로라 아씨\"가—플로라는 친정 지붕 아래로 돌아오면서 그곳에서 살던 시절의 호칭을 다시 두르고 있었다—아직 침실에서 나오지 않으셨지만, 플로라 아씨의 거실로 올라가 계시면 된다고 말했다. 그녀는 마땅한 도리대로 플로라 아씨의 거실로 올라갔고, 그곳에서 둘을 위해 안락하게 차려진 아침 식탁을 발견했는데, 그 위에는 한 사람 몫이 따로 차려진 쟁반이 곁들여져 있었다. 젊은 여자는 잠시 사라졌다가 다시 돌아와, 난롯가에 의자를 놓고 앉아 보닛을 벗고 편히 계시라고 전했다. 그러나 리틀 도릿은 수줍음이 많았고 그런 자리에서 편히 굴어 본 적이 없었으므로 어찌해야 할지 몰라 막막했다. 그래서 삼십 분 뒤 플로라가 부랴부랴 들어왔을 때에도 그녀는 여전히 보닛을 쓴 채 문 가까이에 앉아 있었다.\n\n플로라는 그녀를 기다리게 해서 너무나 미안하다고, 어머나 세상에 어쩌자고 난롯가에서 신문을 읽으며 계실 줄 알았는데 이 추운 데 나앉아 계셨느냐고, 그 칠칠치 못한 계집애가 전갈을 안 드렸느냐고, 정말로 이 시간 내내 보닛을 쓰고 계셨느냐고, 부디 제발 플로라가 벗겨 드리게 해 달라고 했다! 플로라는 세상에서 가장 너그러운 손길로 보닛을 벗기다가, 드러난 얼굴에 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지 이렇게 말했다. \"어머, 아가씨, 정말 착하고 자그마한 분이네요!\" 그러고는 더없이 다정한 여인답게 두 손으로 그녀의 얼굴을 감싸 쥐었다.\n\n그것은 한순간의 말이요 동작이었다. 리틀 도릿이 그 친절함을 미처 헤아릴 새도 없이, 플로라는 분주하게 아침 식탁으로 달려들어 수다 속으로 머리끝까지 곤두박질쳤다.\n\n\"하고많은 아침 중에 하필 오늘 아침에 늦다니 정말 너무 미안해요 왜냐하면 제 뜻과 바람은 아가씨가 들어오실 때 맞이할 채비를 하고서 아서 클레넘이 그토록 관심을 두는 분이라면 누구든 절반만큼이라도 제게 관심을 끌게 마련이니 진심으로 환영한다고 정말 기쁘다고 말씀드리려던 거였는데 그러기는커녕 아무도 절 깨우지 않았고 사실대로 말하자면 전 아마 아직도 코를 골고 있었을 거예요 그리고 차가운 닭고기든 뜨거운 삶은 햄이든 둘 다 싫으시다면 그야 유대인들 말고도 싫어하는 사람이 많다고 봐요 그건 양심의 가책이니 우리 모두 존중해야 마땅하지만 그래도 정말이지 가짜 물건을 진짜라고 우리한테 팔아넘길 때에도 그 양심이 그만큼 강했으면 좋겠다 싶은데 그 물건들은 분명 값어치도 못하니 그러면 저는 어지간히 속상하겠어요.\" 플로라가 말했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 고맙다고 인사하고, 부끄러운 듯 보통은 버터 바른 빵과 차면 충분하다고—\n\n\"오 말도 안 돼요 아가씨 그런 말은 도무지 못 들어줘요.\" 플로라가 더없이 무모한 손짓으로 탕관 꼭지를 틀고, 찻주전자 안을 들여다보려 몸을 굽히다가 뜨거운 물을 눈에 튀겨 저절로 눈을 깜박이며 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 그런 외람된 말을 좀 하자면 아가씨는 친구이자 동무로서 여기 오시는 거예요 다른 어떤 자격으로 오신다면 정말 제가 부끄러울 거예요 게다가 아서 클레넘이 그런 식으로 말씀하셨으니—아가씨 지쳤군요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 부인.\"\n\n\"안색이 그렇게 창백해지시는 걸 보니 아침도 거른 채 너무 멀리 걸어오셨군요 분명 멀리 사시는데 마차를 타셨어야 했어요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"저런 저런 뭔가 몸에 좋을 만한 게 없을까요?\"\n\n\"정말 저는 아주 괜찮아요, 부인. 거듭거듭 감사드려요, 하지만 정말 괜찮아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 어서 차부터 드세요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그리고 이 닭 날개랑 햄 한 점도요, 저는 신경 쓰지 마시고 기다리지도 마세요 저는 늘 이 쟁반을 에프 씨의 고모님께 직접 가져다드리거든요 침대에서 아침을 드시는데 그분도 참 멋진 노부인이고 아주 영리하시지요 문 뒤에 에프 씨의 초상화가 있는데 아주 닮았어요 이마가 좀 지나치게 넓긴 하지만요 그리고 대리석 바닥에 난간이 딸린 기둥이며 산이 그려져 있는데 저는 그이가 그런 데 가까이 가는 걸 본 적도 없고 포도주 장사를 하던 사람이 그럴 리도 없죠 훌륭한 사람이었지만 도무지 그런 쪽은 아니었어요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그 예술 작품에 대한 언급을 아주 어렴풋이 따라가며 초상화를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"에프 씨는 제게 어찌나 헌신적이었던지 저를 잠시도 눈앞에서 떼어 놓질 못했어요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"물론 제가 새 빗자루였을 때 그이가 갑자기 세상을 뜨지 않았더라면 그게 얼마나 갔을지야 알 수 없지만요 훌륭한 사람이었지만 시적이진 않았고 사내다운 산문이었지 로맨스는 아니었어요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 다시 초상화를 흘끗 보았다. 화가는 지적인 관점에서 보자면 셰익스피어에게도 머리가 너무 무거웠을 법한 두상을 그 인물에게 붙여 놓았다.\n\n\"하지만 로맨스로 말하자면,\" 플로라는 에프 씨 고모의 토스트를 분주히 차려 놓으며 말을 이었다. \"그이가 제게 청혼했을 때 제가 에프 씨에게 대놓고 말한 그대로인데 그이가 일곱 번이나 청혼했다는 걸 들으면 아가씨도 놀라실 거예요 한 번은 삯마차 안에서 한 번은 보트에서 한 번은 교회 좌석에서 한 번은 턴브리지 웰스에서 당나귀를 탄 채로 나머지는 무릎을 꿇고서 했지요, 로맨스는 아서 클레넘의 그 시절과 함께 사라졌고 우리 부모님이 우리를 갈라놓아 우리는 대리석이 되었으며 냉엄한 현실이 그 왕좌를 차지했어요 에프 씨는 참으로 떳떳하게도 자기가 그걸 완전히 알고 있으며 오히려 그런 상태를 더 바란다고 말했고 그리하여 말이 떨어지고 명령이 내려졌으니 인생이란 게 그런 거예요 아가씨 그래도 우리는 부러지지 않고 휘어질 뿐이지요, 부디 든든히 아침을 드세요 저는 쟁반을 들고 들어갈 테니.\"\n\n그녀는 사라지면서 리틀 도릿에게 그 흩어진 말들의 의미를 곱씹도록 남겨 두었다. 그녀는 곧 다시 돌아왔고, 마침내 줄곧 이야기하면서 제 아침을 먹기 시작했다.\n\n\"있잖아요 아가씨,\" 플로라가 브랜디 냄새가 나는 갈색 액체를 한두 숟갈 떠서 자기 차에 넣으며 말했다. \"저는 제 주치의가 일러 준 대로 따르도록 조심해야 해요 맛은 영 달갑지 않지만요 저는 가련한 사람이라 어쩌면 아서와 헤어졌을 때 옆방에서 너무 울어 댄 탓에 젊은 날에 받은 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못했는지도 몰라요, 그분을 안 지 오래되셨나요?\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 이 질문을 받았다는 것을 알아차리자마자—새 후원자의 질주하는 속도가 그녀를 멀찍이 뒤에 떨어뜨려 놓은 터라 그러는 데에는 시간이 필요했다—클레넘 씨가 돌아오신 뒤로 줄곧 알고 지냈다고 대답했다.\n\n\"하긴 아가씨가 중국에 있었거나 편지를 주고받았다면 모를까 그게 둘 다 그럴 법하지 않으니 그 전에 알았을 리는 없죠.\" 플로라가 받아 말했다. \"여행을 다니는 사람들은 대개 살갗이 마호가니처럼 거뭇해지는데 아가씨는 전혀 그렇지 않고 편지로 말하자면 무슨 사연으로요? 그도 그럴 게 차 거래라면 모를까, 그러니 정말 그분을 처음 알게 된 건 그분 어머니 댁에서였나요 무척 분별 있고 굳센 분이지만 끔찍하게 엄하시지요—철가면을 쓴 사내의 어머니로 딱 어울릴 분이에요.\"\n\n\"클레넘 부인은 제게 친절하셨어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"정말요? 그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요 그분이 아서의 어머니이시니 전보다 더 좋게 여기게 되는 게 제 마음에 자연히 흐뭇한 일이거든요 하긴 제가 분명히 그러게 마련인 것처럼 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댈 때 그분이 손수레에 탄 운명의 여신처럼 저를 노려보며 앉아 계시면—정말 흉한 비유지만요—병약한 분이니 그분 탓은 아니죠—그분이 저를 어떻게 생각하시는지는 도무지 알 수도 짐작할 수도 없어요.\"\n\n\"제 일감을 어디서 찾을 수 있을까요, 부인?\" 리틀 도릿이 머뭇머뭇 주위를 둘러보며 물었다. \"가져와도 될까요?\"\n\n\"부지런하고 자그마한 요정님,\" 플로라가 또 한 잔의 차에 주치의가 처방한 약을 또 한 번 타며 받아 말했다. \"조금도 서두를 것 없어요 그보다 우리의 공통된 친구에 관해 흉허물 없이 이야기를 시작하는 편이 나아요—적어도 제게는 너무 차가운 말이지만 그런 뜻은 아니에요, 공통된 친구라는 건 아주 적절한 표현이죠—그저 형식만 차리다가 아가씨가 아니라 제가 여우에게 물린 스파르타 소년처럼 되느니 말이에요, 그런데 그 이야기를 꺼내는 걸 양해해 주셔야겠어요 온갖 자리에 끼어드는 진력나는 소년들 중에서도 그 소년이 제일 진력나니까요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 얼굴이 몹시 창백해진 채 다시 앉아 귀를 기울였다. \"그동안 일을 하는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"저는 일을 하면서도 들을 수 있어요. 괜찮으시다면 그러는 편이 좋겠어요.\"\n\n그녀의 간곡함이 일거리가 없으면 마음이 불편하다는 뜻을 어찌나 잘 드러냈던지, 플로라는 \"그럼 아가씨 가장 좋은 대로 하세요.\" 하고 대답하며 흰 손수건이 든 바구니를 내놓았다. 리틀 도릿은 기쁘게 그것을 곁에 두고, 작은 반짇고리를 꺼내 바늘에 실을 꿰고는 단을 박기 시작했다.\n\n\"손가락이 어찌나 날쌘지.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그런데 정말 몸은 괜찮으세요?\"\n\n\"오 네, 정말이에요!\"\n\n플로라는 두 발을 난로 울에 올리고, 한바탕 제대로 된 낭만적인 고백을 위해 자세를 잡았다. 그녀는 단숨에 내달리듯 시작했다. 머리를 까딱이고, 더없이 과시하듯 한숨을 쉬고, 눈썹을 한껏 놀려 가며, 이따금, 그러나 자주는 아니게, 일감 위로 수그린 그 고요한 얼굴을 흘끗거렸다.\n\n\"아가씨도 아셔야 해요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"하긴 이미 아시리라 믿어 의심치 않지만요 제가 두루뭉술하게나마 이미 흘려 두기도 했고 또 제 이마에 그 뭐라더라 활활 타는 무엇으로 새겨진 채 다니는 게 느껴지기도 하니까요 곧 제가 작고하신 에프 씨에게 소개되기 전에 아서 클레넘과 약혼한 사이였다는 거예요—체면을 차려야 하는 자리에서는 클레넘 씨, 여기서는 아서—우리는 서로에게 전부였고 그것은 인생의 아침이었으며 더없는 행복이자 광란이자 그런 종류의 온갖 것이 극에 달한 상태였는데 갈가리 찢기자 우리는 돌이 되었고 그 처지로 아서는 중국으로 갔으며 저는 작고하신 에프 씨의 석상 신부가 되었지요.\"\n\n플로라는 이 말을 굵은 목소리로 읊으며 더없는 즐거움을 만끽했다.\n\n\"속이 온통 대리석이던 그 아침의 심정을 그려 보자면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"에프 씨의 고모님은 유리 마차에 타고 뒤따랐는데 그게 분명 형편없이 망가진 마차였을 거예요 안 그랬다면 집에서 두 거리 떨어진 데서 고장 날 리가 없었을 테고 에프 씨의 고모님은 마치 십일월 오일 행사 인형처럼 골풀 방석 의자에 실려 집으로 돌아오셨지요 그 심정을 그려 보려는 시도는 하지 않겠어요, 다만 아래층 식당에서 형식뿐인 아침 식사가 치러졌고 절인 연어를 너무 욕심껏 드신 아빠가 몇 주를 앓으셨으며 에프 씨와 저는 칼레로 대륙 여행을 떠났는데 거기 부두에서 사람들이 우리 짐을 서로 들겠다고 다투다가 결국 우리를 떼어 놓고 말았다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분해요 하긴 영원히는 아니었죠 그건 아직 일어날 일이 아니었으니까요.\"\n\n석상 신부는 숨 돌릴 새도 거의 없이, 더없이 흡족하게, 때로 살아 있는 사람에게 따라붙곤 하는 두서없는 식으로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그 꿈결 같은 삶에는 장막을 드리우겠어요, 에프 씨는 기분이 좋았고 식욕이 좋았고 요리를 좋아했고 포도주는 묽지만 마실 만하다고 여겼으니 만사가 평안했어요, 우리는 런던 부두의 리틀 고슬링 가 삼십 번지 바로 근처로 돌아와 정착했고 가정부가 손님용 침대에서 깃털을 빼내 팔아먹는 걸 미처 다 적발하기도 전에 통풍이 위로 솟구쳐 에프 씨와 함께 다른 세계로 날아올라 갔지요.\"\n\n그의 미망인은 초상화를 흘끗 보고는 고개를 저으며 눈물을 훔쳤다.\n\n\"저는 에프 씨를 존경할 만한 분이자 더없이 너그러운 남편으로서 그 기억을 기립니다, 아스파라거스라고 한마디만 하면 그게 나타났고 무슨 자그마한 마실 것을 넌지시 비치기만 해도 마법처럼 일 파인트 병에 담겨 왔으니 황홀경은 아니었지만 안락이었지요, 저는 아빠의 지붕 아래로 돌아와 행복하지는 않을지언정 몇 해를 두문불출하며 지냈어요 그러던 어느 날 아빠가 슬그머니 어물쩍 들어오시더니 아래층에서 아서 클레넘이 저를 기다린다고 하시기에 내려가 보니 그분이 계셨는데 어떤 모습이었는지는 묻지 말아 주세요 다만 그분이 여전히 미혼이고 여전히 변함없더라는 것만은!\"\n\n플로라가 이제 제 몸에 두른 어두운 신비는 그녀 곁에서 일하는 날쌘 손가락이 아닌 다른 손가락이었다면 멈추게 했을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그 손가락들은 쉼 없이 움직였고, 분주한 머리는 그 위로 수그린 채 한 땀 한 땀을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"묻지 말아 주세요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"제가 아직도 그분을 사랑하는지 그분이 아직도 저를 사랑하는지 그 끝이 어떻게 될지 언제일지, 우리는 지켜보는 눈들에 에워싸여 있고 어쩌면 우리는 서로 떨어져 애태우다가 어쩌면 다시는 결코 맺어지지 못할 운명인지도 몰라요 한마디도 한숨도 한 번의 눈길도 우리를 들켜선 안 되고 모든 것이 무덤처럼 비밀이어야 하니 그러므로 제가 아서에게 비교적 차갑게 보이거나 아서가 제게 비교적 차갑게 보인다 해도 놀라지 마세요 우리에게는 숙명적인 사정이 있고 우리가 그것을 알면 그만이에요 쉿!\"\n\n이 모든 것을 플로라는 정말로 그렇게 믿는 양 어찌나 앞뒤 없이 격렬하게 말하던지. 그녀가 완전한 인어 상태로 자신을 몰아넣었을 때에는 그 상태에서 하는 말이 무엇이든 실제로 믿었다는 데에 의심의 여지가 별로 없다.\n\n\"쉿!\" 플로라가 거듭 말했다. \"이제 다 말씀드렸어요 우리 사이에 흉허물 없는 믿음이 생겼으니 쉿, 아서를 위해서라도 저는 언제나 아가씨의 친구가 되어 드리겠어요 사랑스러운 아가씨 그리고 아서의 이름으로 언제든 저를 믿으셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n날쌘 손가락들이 일감을 내려놓았고, 그 자그마한 사람이 일어나 플로라의 손에 입을 맞추었다. \"손이 무척 차네요.\" 플로라가 본래의 따뜻하고 다정한 태도로 돌아오며 말했는데, 그 변화로 한결 보기 좋아졌다. \"오늘은 일하지 마세요. 분명 몸이 좋지 않으신 거예요 분명 기운이 없으신 거예요.\"\n\n\"그저 부인의 친절과, 그토록 오래 알고 아껴 오신 분께 저를 맡기신 클레넘 씨의 친절에 조금 압도되었을 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"글쎄 정말이지 아가씨,\" 곰곰이 생각할 짬이 생기면 늘 정직해지려는 뚜렷한 성향이 있는 플로라가 말했다. \"그건 이제 그냥 놔두는 편이 낫겠어요 어쨌든 제가 단언할 수는 없으니까요 하지만 그런 건 중요치 않아요 잠깐 누우세요!\"\n\n\"저는 늘 하고 싶은 일을 할 만큼은 튼튼했어요, 곧 아주 괜찮아질 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 희미한 미소를 띠며 받아 말했다. \"그저 감사한 마음에 압도되었을 뿐이에요. 잠깐 창가에 있으면 곧 제정신이 들 거예요.\"\n\n플로라는 창을 열고 그 옆 의자에 그녀를 앉힌 뒤, 사려 깊게 제자리로 물러났다. 바람 부는 날이었고, 리틀 도릿의 얼굴을 스치는 바람이 이내 그 얼굴을 환하게 했다. 채 몇 분도 안 되어 그녀는 일감 바구니로 돌아갔고, 날쌘 손가락은 여느 때처럼 날쌨다.\n\n그녀는 조용히 일을 이어 가다가, 클레넘 씨가 자기가 어디 사는지 말씀하셨느냐고 플로라에게 물었다. 플로라가 아니라고 대답하자, 리틀 도릿은 그분이 왜 그토록 세심하게 입을 다무셨는지 이해한다고, 하지만 자기 비밀을 플로라에게 털어놓는 것을 그분이 분명 좋아하시리라 믿는다고, 그러니 플로라가 허락한다면 이제 그러겠다고 말했다. 격려하는 대답을 듣고 그녀는 제 삶의 이야기를 자신에 관한 몇 마디 짧은 말과 아버지에 대한 빛나는 찬사로 압축했다. 플로라는 그 모두를 그것을 온전히 이해하는 천성의 다정함으로 받아들였는데, 거기에는 두서없는 구석이 조금도 없었다.\n\n저녁 식사 때가 되자 플로라는 새로 맡은 사람의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고 아래층으로 데려가, 이미 식당에서 식사를 시작하려고 기다리고 있던 가부장과 팽크스 씨에게 소개했다. (에프 씨의 고모는 그동안 자기 방에 드러누워 출항 준비 중인 배처럼 정박해 있었다.) 그 신사들은 각자의 성격대로 그녀를 맞이했다. 가부장은 만나서 반갑다고, 반갑다고 말하는 것으로 그녀에게 헤아릴 수 없는 은혜라도 베푸는 듯했고, 팽크스 씨는 인사 삼아 자신이 즐겨 내는 소리를 한 번 뿜어냈다.\n\n그 새로운 자리에서라면 어떤 상황에서든 그녀는 충분히 수줍어했을 테고, 더욱이 포도주 한 잔을 마시고 거기 있는 가장 좋은 것을 먹으라고 고집하는 플로라 앞에서는 더했을 테지만, 그녀의 거북함을 한층 키운 것은 팽크스 씨였다. 그 신사의 거동은 처음에 그녀의 마음속에 그가 초상화가일지도 모른다는 생각을 불러일으켰다. 어찌나 그녀를 뚫어지게 보고, 어찌나 자주 자기 곁의 작은 수첩을 흘끗거리던지. 그러나 그가 아무 그림도 그리지 않고 오로지 사업 이야기만 하는 것을 보고서, 그녀는 그가 자기 아버지의 어떤 채권자를 대리하는 사람이고, 갚아야 할 잔액이 그 수첩에 적혀 있는 게 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다. 이런 관점에서 보니 팽크스 씨의 씩씩거림은 손해와 조바심을 나타냈고, 그가 더 크게 콧김을 뿜을 때마다 그것은 지불을 요구하는 것이 되었다.\n\n그러나 여기서도 그녀는 팽크스 씨 자신의 변칙적이고 모순된 행동에 속았음을 깨달았다. 그녀는 식탁을 떠난 지 삼십 분이 지나 혼자 일하고 있었다. 플로라는 옆방으로 \"누우러 갔고,\" 그 은퇴와 동시에 집안에 무언가 마실 것의 냄새가 터져 나왔다. 가부장은 식당에서 박애의 입을 노란 손수건 아래 벌린 채 곤히 잠들어 있었다. 이 고요한 때에 팽크스 씨가 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 그녀 앞에 슬며시 나타났다.\n\n\"좀 따분하시죠, 도릿 양?\" 팽크스가 낮은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 고마워요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"바쁘시군요, 보아하니.\" 팽크스 씨가 한 치씩 슬그머니 방으로 들어오며 말했다. \"그게 지금 뭡니까, 도릿 양?\"\n\n\"손수건이에요.\"\n\n\"아니 그래요!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"전 그렇게 생각 못 했는데요.\" 손수건은 조금도 보지 않고, 리틀 도릿을 보면서 한 말이었다. \"어쩌면 제가 누군지 궁금하시겠죠. 말씀드릴까요? 저는 점쟁이입니다.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 이제 그가 미친 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n\"저는 몸도 마음도 제 주인에게 속해 있습니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"아래층에서 식사하시던 제 주인을 보셨지요. 하지만 가끔은 다른 쪽 일도 조금 합니다. 은밀히, 아주 은밀히요, 도릿 양.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 미심쩍게, 그리고 적잖이 놀란 채 그를 바라보았다. \"손바닥을 좀 보여 주시면 좋겠는데요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"한번 들여다보고 싶어서요. 폐가 되지 않게 해 주세요.\"\n\n폐로 따지자면 그는 거기 전혀 있을 필요가 없다는 점에서 폐가 되었지만, 그녀는 잠시 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고, 골무를 낀 왼손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"수고로 보낸 세월이군요, 그렇죠?\" 팽크스가 뭉툭한 집게손가락으로 손을 짚으며 부드럽게 말했다. \"하지만 우리가 그 말고 무엇 때문에 만들어졌겠습니까? 아무것도 아니지요. 이런!\" 손금을 들여다보며 그가 말했다. \"창살이 있는 이건 뭡니까? 감옥이로군요! 그리고 회색 가운에 검은 우단 모자를 쓴 이건 뭡니까? 아버지로군요! 그리고 클라리넷을 든 이건 뭡니까? 삼촌이로군요! 그리고 무도화를 신은 이건 뭡니까? 누이로군요! 그리고 빈둥대듯 어슬렁대는 이건 뭡니까? 오라비로군요! 그리고 이 모두를 위해 궁리하는 이건 뭡니까? 아니, 이건 바로 당신이로군요, 도릿 양!\"\n\n그녀가 의아해하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았을 때 그녀의 눈과 그의 눈이 마주쳤고, 그녀는 그의 눈이 비록 날카롭기는 해도 그가 저녁 식사 때 짐작했던 것보다 더 환하고 부드러운 인상의 사람이라고 생각했다. 그의 눈은 곧장 다시 그녀의 손으로 향했고, 그 인상을 확인하거나 바로잡을 기회는 사라졌다.\n\n\"이런, 별일이로군.\" 팽크스가 그 투박한 손가락으로 그녀의 손금을 더듬으며 중얼거렸다. \"여기 구석에 있는 이게 저라니! 제가 여기서 뭘 바라는 걸까요? 제 뒤에 있는 건 뭘까요?\"\n\n그는 손가락을 천천히 손목까지 내리더니, 손목을 빙 둘러, 자기 뒤에 있는 것을 보려는 양 손등을 살펴보는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"무슨 해로운 거라도 있나요?\" 리틀 도릿이 미소 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"천만에요!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그 값어치가 얼마나 될 것 같습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 제가 선생님께 여쭐 말이지요. 점쟁이는 제가 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그 값어치가 얼마냐고요? 살아서 직접 보시게 될 겁니다, 도릿 양.\"\n\n그는 천천히 손을 놓아주며, 자기의 뻗친 머리카락 사이로 손가락을 모두 훑어 넣어 그 머리카락이 더없이 불길한 모양으로 곤두서게 했다. 그러고는 천천히 되풀이했다. \"제 말을 기억하세요, 도릿 양. 살아서 보시게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n그녀는 적어도 그가 자기에 관해 그토록 많이 안다는 사실만으로도 몹시 놀랐다는 것을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아! 바로 그거예요!\" 팽크스가 그녀를 가리키며 말했다. \"도릿 양, 절대 그러시면 안 됩니다, 결코요!\"\n\n전보다 더 놀라고 조금 더 겁이 난 채 그녀는 그 마지막 말의 설명을 구하려 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그러지 마세요.\" 팽크스가 자못 진지하게, 본의 아니게 기괴해 보이는 놀란 표정과 몸짓을 흉내 내며 말했다. \"그러지 마세요. 저를 보더라도 절대로요, 언제든, 어디서든. 저는 아무것도 아닌 사람입니다. 저를 신경 쓰지 마세요. 저를 입에 올리지 마세요. 모르는 척하세요. 약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\"\n\n\"뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 어안이 벙벙해져 받아 말했다. \"왜요?\"\n\n\"제가 점쟁이라서요. 집시 팽크스라서요. 도릿 양, 저는 아직 그 작은 손에서 제 뒤에 무엇이 있는지 말씀드릴 만큼 당신의 운명을 다 말해 드리지 않았어요. 살아서 보시게 될 거라고만 말씀드렸지요. 약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\"\n\n\"제가—그러니까—\"\n\n\"여기를 벗어나서는 제가 먼저 아는 척하지 않는 한 저를 모르는 척하시는 거예요. 제가 오고 갈 때 저를 신경 쓰지 않으시는 거지요. 아주 쉬운 일이에요. 저는 잃을 게 없는 사람이고, 잘생기지도 않았고, 좋은 말동무도 아니고, 그저 제 주인의 돈 캐는 일꾼일 뿐이니까요. 그저 이렇게만 생각하시면 돼요. '아! 집시 팽크스가 점을 치는구나—언젠가 내 운명의 나머지를 말해 주겠지—나는 살아서 그걸 알게 되겠지.' 약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\"\n\n\"네—에.\" 그가 자기를 몹시 혼란스럽게 한 리틀 도릿이 더듬더듬 말했다. \"그래야겠지요, 선생님이 해를 끼치지 않으신다면요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 팽크스 씨는 옆방 벽을 흘끗 보고는 앞으로 몸을 숙였다. \"정직한 사람이고, 됨됨이는 훌륭하지만, 조심성이 없고 입이 헤픈 분이지요, 도릿 양.\" 그러고는 면담이 무척 흡족했다는 듯 두 손을 비비고, 헐떡이며 문 쪽으로 가더니, 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 다시 빠져나갔다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 새로 사귄 사람의 이 묘한 행동에, 그리고 이 별난 약조에 휘말린 자신을 보고서 한없이 어리둥절했다면, 그 어리둥절함은 뒤이어 벌어진 일들로도 줄어들지 않았다. 팽크스 씨는 캐스비 씨의 집에서 기회가 주어질 때마다 그녀를 의미심장하게 흘끗거리고 코를 씩씩거렸을 뿐 아니라—이미 한 짓을 생각하면 그쯤이야 별것 아니었지만—그녀의 일상생활 속으로 스며들기 시작했다. 그녀는 거리에서 줄곧 그를 보았다. 캐스비 씨 댁에 가면 그는 늘 거기 있었다. 클레넘 부인 댁에 가면 그는 무슨 핑계를 대서라도 그곳에 나타나, 마치 그녀를 시야에 두려는 듯했다. 일주일도 채 지나기 전에 그녀는 어느 날 밤 수위실에서 당직 간수와 이야기를 나누는, 그것도 보아하니 그의 막역한 동무 중 하나인 듯한 그를 발견하고 깜짝 놀랐다. 그다음 놀라움은 그가 감옥 안에서도 똑같이 편안해하는 것을 본 일이었다. 일요일에 아버지가 베푸는 알현 자리에 손님들 틈에 끼어 모습을 드러냈다는 이야기를 들은 일, 마당에서 수감자 친구와 팔짱을 끼고 다니는 것을 본 일, 어느 날 저녁 스너거리에서 모임을 갖는 사교 클럽에서 회원들에게 연설을 하고 노래를 부르고 일동에게 맥주 다섯 갤런을—소문은 미친 듯이 새우 한 부셸까지 보탰다—대접하여 크게 명성을 떨쳤다는 소문을 들은 일이었다. 이러한 현상들 가운데 충실한 방문 중에 직접 목격한 것들이 플로니시 씨에게 미친 영향은, 그 현상들 자체가 자아낸 인상에 버금가는 인상을 리틀 도릿에게 주었다. 그것들은 그를 재갈 물리고 결박하는 듯했다. 그는 그저 멍하니 쳐다볼 뿐이었고, 이따금 이게 팽크스라는 걸 블리딩 하트 야드에서는 믿지 않을 거라고 힘없이 중얼거릴 뿐이었다. 그러나 그는 그 이상 한마디도 더 하지 않았고, 리틀 도릿에게조차 더는 어떤 내색도 하지 않았다. 팽크스 씨는 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 팁과 친해지고, 일요일에 그 신사의 팔짱을 끼고 감옥으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어옴으로써 자신의 신비를 절정에 이르게 했다. 그러는 내내 그는 리틀 도릿에게 전혀 아는 척을 하지 않았는데, 다만 한두 번 우연히 그녀 가까이 다가왔고 주위에 사람이 아주 가까이 없을 때만은 예외였다. 그런 경우에 그는 다정한 눈길과 격려의 콧김을 한 번 뿜으며 지나가는 말로 이렇게 말했다. \"집시 팽크스—점치기.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 이 모든 것을 의아해하면서도, 어린 시절부터 더 무거운 짐을 수없이 가슴에 담아 두어 온 것처럼 그 의아함도 가슴속에 간직한 채, 여느 때처럼 일하고 애썼다. 그 참을성 많은 마음 위로 어떤 변화가 슬며시 깃들었고, 여전히 깃들어 가고 있었다. 하루하루가 지날 때마다 그녀는 전날보다 한층 더 움츠러들어 있었다. 눈에 띄지 않게 감옥을 드나드는 것, 그리고 다른 곳에서는 간과되고 잊히는 것이 그녀 자신을 위한 가장 큰 바람이었다.\n\n그녀의 섬세한 젊음과 성품에는 어울리지 않게 짝지어진 방이긴 했으나, 자기 방으로도 그녀는 어떤 의무도 저버리지 않는 한 되도록 자주 물러나기를 기뻐했다. 오후에 일거리가 없을 때, 손님들이 들러 아버지와 카드 한판을 벌일 때, 그녀가 없어도 될 때, 아니 차라리 자리를 비우는 편이 나을 때가 있었다. 그러면 그녀는 마당을 가로질러 사뿐히 가서, 제 방으로 이어지는 수십 개의 계단을 올라, 창가에 앉곤 했다. 리틀 도릿이 거기 앉아 골똘히 생각하는 동안, 담장 위의 그 뾰족한 창들은 숱한 형상을 이루었고, 그 단단한 쇠붙이는 스스로 숱한 가벼운 모양으로 엮였으며, 숱한 황금빛 손길이 녹슨 표면에 내려앉았다. 눈물이 왈칵 솟구쳐 그것을 흐릿하게 바라볼 때면 이따금 그 잔혹한 무늬 속에 새로운 지그재그가 돋아나기도 했다. 그러나 아름다워지든 모질어지든, 그녀는 고독 속에서 그 위로, 그 아래로, 그 사이로 늘 바라볼 수밖에 없었고, 모든 것을 그 지울 수 없는 낙인과 더불어 보았다.\n\n다락방, 그것도 타협이라곤 없는 마셜시의 다락방이 리틀 도릿의 방이었다. 곱게 건사되었으되 그 자체로는 흉했고, 청결함과 바람 말고는 그것을 돋보이게 하는 것이 거의 없었다. 그녀가 이제껏 살 수 있었던 장식거리는 모조리 아버지의 방으로 갔기 때문이다. 그럼에도 그녀는 이 초라한 곳에 점점 더 깊은 애정을 보였고, 그곳에 홀로 앉아 있는 것이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 쉼이 되었다.\n\n그리하여 팽크스의 신비가 한창이던 어느 오후, 그녀가 창가에 앉아 있다가 계단을 올라오는 매기의 낯익은 발소리를 들었을 때, 그녀는 어디론가 불려 가게 되지 않을까 하는 염려에 몹시 마음이 어지러웠다. 매기의 발소리가 점점 위로, 점점 가까이 올라올수록 그녀는 떨고 머뭇거렸다. 마침내 매기가 나타났을 때 그녀는 가까스로 입을 뗄 수 있었다.\n\n\"저기요, 작은엄마.\" 매기가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"내려가서 그 사람 좀 만나 봐요. 그 사람 와 있어요.\"\n\n\"누가, 매기?\"\n\n\"누구긴요, 당연히 클레넘 씨죠. 그 사람이 작은엄마 아버지 방에 있는데, 저한테 그러더라고요, 매기, 미안하지만 가서 그냥 나라고 전해 주겠소, 하고요.\"\n\n\"몸이 별로 안 좋아, 매기. 안 내려가는 게 낫겠어. 좀 누워야겠어. 봐! 머리를 식히려고 지금 누울게. 고맙고 정중한 인사를 전하고, 내가 이렇게 누워 있어서 그렇지 안 그랬으면 내려갔을 거라고 말씀드려.\"\n\n\"근데 그것도 별로 예의 바른 건 아니에요, 작은엄마.\" 멍하니 쳐다보던 매기가 말했다. \"얼굴을 그렇게 돌려 버리는 것도요!\"\n\n매기는 자기에게 가해지는 무시에 무척 민감했고, 그런 무시를 지어내는 데에도 무척 재간이 있었다. \"게다가 두 손으로 얼굴까지 가리다니요!\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"불쌍한 사람 꼴이 보기 싫으시면 차라리 단번에 그렇다고 말씀하시는 게 낫지, 그렇게 막아 버리고 열 살배기 가엾은 것 마음을 다치게 하고 가슴을 찢어 놓다니요, 불쌍한 것!\"\n\n\"머리를 식히려는 거야, 매기.\"\n\n\"그럼, 머리 식히려고 우시는 거면, 작은엄마, 저도 같이 울게요. 혼자서 우는 걸 다 차지하지 마세요.\" 매기가 따지듯 말했다. \"그건 욕심부리지 않는 게 아니잖아요.\" 그러고는 곧장 엉엉 울기 시작했다.\n\n그녀를 그 핑계와 함께 돌려보내도록 구슬리기는 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다. 그러나 옛적부터 더없이 좋아하던 이야기를 들려주겠다는 약속이—심부름에 정신을 모으고 한 시간 더 작은 여주인을 혼자 두는 조건으로—계단 밑에 제 좋은 기분을 두고 온 게 아닐까 하는 매기 자신의 불안과 어우러져 효력을 발휘했다. 그리하여 그녀는 잊지 않으려고 가는 내내 전갈을 중얼거리며 떠났고, 정해진 시간에 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그 사람 무척 안타까워했어요, 정말로요.\" 그녀가 알렸다. \"의사를 보내려고 했어요. 그리고 내일 또 온대요, 정말로요, 그리고 작은엄마 머리가 아프다는 소릴 들어서 오늘 밤 잠을 푹 못 잘 것 같아요, 작은엄마. 아이고 참! 우셨던 거 아니에요!\"\n\n\"운 것 같긴 해, 조금, 매기.\"\n\n\"조금이라뇨! 어머!\"\n\n\"하지만 이제 다 끝났어—아주 끝났단다, 매기. 머리도 훨씬 나아지고 시원해져서 아주 편안해. 안 내려가길 정말 잘했어.\"\n\n멍하니 쳐다보던 그녀의 큰 아이가 다정하게 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 머리를 매만져 주고, 이마와 눈을 찬물로 씻겨 준 뒤에(서투른 손이 이런 일에는 능숙해졌다), 다시 한번 꼭 껴안고, 한결 밝아진 그녀의 얼굴빛에 기뻐하며, 창가의 의자에 그녀를 앉혔다. 그 의자 맞은편으로 매기는 전혀 필요도 없는 졸도할 듯한 안간힘을 써서, 이야기를 들을 때 자기 자리가 되어 주는 상자를 끌어다가 그 위에 앉았다. 그러고는 제 무릎을 끌어안고, 이야기에 게걸스러운 식욕을 보이며, 눈을 크게 뜨고 말했다.\n\n\"자, 작은엄마, 멋진 걸로 하나 해 줘요!\"\n\n\"무슨 이야기로 할까, 매기?\"\n\n\"오, 공주님 이야기로 해요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"그리고 제대로 된 공주님으로요. 믿기지 않을 만큼요, 알죠!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 노을빛에 발그레해진 얼굴에 다소 슬픈 미소를 띤 채 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"매기, 옛날 옛적에 훌륭한 임금님이 있었는데, 그분은 바라는 것을 다 가졌고, 그보다 훨씬 더 많이 가졌단다. 금과 은, 다이아몬드와 루비, 온갖 보화를 가졌지. 궁전도 여럿 있었고, 또—\"\n\n\"병원이요.\" 매기가 여전히 제 무릎을 안은 채 끼어들었다. \"병원도 갖게 해 줘요, 병원은 참 편안하니까요. 닭고기가 잔뜩 있는 병원이요.\"\n\n\"그래, 병원도 많이 가졌어, 그리고 뭐든 다 많이 가졌지.\"\n\n\"이를테면 구운 감자도 잔뜩요?\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐든 다 잔뜩.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 매기가 제 무릎을 꼭 끌어안으며 킬킬댔다. \"정말 끝내주네요!\"\n\n\"이 임금님께는 따님이 한 분 있었는데, 여태 본 적 없는 가장 지혜롭고 가장 아름다운 공주님이었어. 어릴 적엔 스승들이 가르치기도 전에 모든 가르침을 다 깨쳤고, 다 자라서는 온 세상의 경이로움이 되었단다. 그런데 이 공주님이 사는 궁전 가까이에 오두막이 하나 있었는데, 거기엔 가난하고 자그마한 여인이 홀로 외따로 살고 있었어.\"\n\n\"늙은 여인이요.\" 매기가 입맛을 쩝쩝 다시며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 늙은 여인이 아니야. 아주 젊은 여인이었어.\"\n\n\"안 무서웠나 봐요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"계속해 줘요, 제발.\"\n\n\"공주님은 거의 날마다 그 오두막 앞을 지나갔는데, 아름다운 마차를 타고 지나갈 때마다 그 가난하고 자그마한 여인이 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣는 것을 보았어. 그리고 공주님은 그 자그마한 여인을 바라보았고, 자그마한 여인은 공주님을 바라보았지. 그래서 어느 날 공주님은 오두막에서 조금 떨어진 데서 마부를 멈추게 하고, 마차에서 내려 걸어가 문 안을 들여다보았는데, 거기엔 여느 때처럼 그 자그마한 여인이 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 있었어. 그리고 여인은 공주님을 바라보았고, 공주님은 여인을 바라보았지.\"\n\n\"서로 눈싸움이라도 하듯이요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"계속해 줘요, 작은엄마.\"\n\n\"공주님은 어찌나 놀라운 공주님이었던지 비밀을 알아내는 힘이 있었어. 그래서 자그마한 여인에게 말했단다, 왜 그것을 거기에 간직하고 있나요? 이 말에 여인은 공주님이 자기가 왜 홀로 외따로 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 사는지 안다는 걸 단번에 알아차렸고, 공주님의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고서 자기를 결코 발설하지 말아 달라고 빌었어. 그러자 공주님이 말했지, 결코 발설하지 않겠어요. 그걸 내게 보여 주세요. 그래서 자그마한 여인은 오두막 창의 덧문을 닫고 문을 걸어 잠근 뒤, 누가 자기를 의심할까 봐 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서, 아주 은밀한 곳을 열어 공주님에게 그림자 하나를 보여 주었어.\"\n\n\"어머나!\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n\"그것은 오래전에 지나가 버린 어떤 사람의 그림자였어. 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리 떠나가 버려, 다시는, 결코 다시는 돌아오지 않을 어떤 사람의 그림자였지. 보기에 환했고, 자그마한 여인이 그것을 공주님에게 보여 줄 때 그녀는 그것을 크나큰, 크나큰 보물처럼 온 마음으로 자랑스러워했어. 공주님은 그것을 잠시 바라보고 나서 자그마한 여인에게 말했지, 그래서 당신은 날마다 이것을 지키고 있나요? 여인은 눈을 내리깔고 속삭였어, 네. 그러자 공주님이 말했지, 왜 그러는지 다시 말해 주세요. 이에 여인이 대답하기를, 그토록 선하고 다정한 이가 그 길로 지나간 적이 없었으니, 그것이 처음의 까닭이었다고 했어. 또 말하기를, 아무도 그것을 아쉬워하지 않고, 아무도 그것 때문에 더 나빠지지 않으며, 그 어떤 사람은 자기를 기다리던 이들에게로 떠나갔다고—\"\n\n\"그럼 그 어떤 사람은 남자였군요?\" 매기가 끼어들었다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 머뭇머뭇 그래, 그런 것 같다고 말하고는 다시 이었다.\n\n\"—자기를 기다리던 이들에게로 떠나갔으며, 이 기억은 그 누구에게서 훔치거나 빼앗은 것이 아니라고 했어. 공주님이 대답했지, 아! 하지만 당신이 죽으면 거기서 그것이 발견될 거예요. 자그마한 여인은 아니라고 했어. 그때가 오면 그것은 조용히 자기 무덤 속으로 가라앉을 것이고, 결코 발견되지 않을 거라고 했지.\"\n\n\"이런, 세상에!\" 매기가 말했다. \"계속해 줘요, 제발.\"\n\n\"공주님은 이 말을 듣고 무척 놀랐단다, 짐작이 가지, 매기.\"\n\n(\"그야 놀랄 만하죠.\" 매기가 말했다.)\n\n\"그래서 공주님은 그 자그마한 여인을 지켜보며 어찌 되는지 보기로 마음먹었어. 날마다 아름다운 마차를 타고 오두막 문 앞을 지나갔는데, 거기엔 늘 그 자그마한 여인이 홀로 외따로 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 있었어. 그리고 공주님은 여인을 바라보았고, 여인은 공주님을 바라보았지. 마침내 어느 날 물레가 멈춰 있었고, 자그마한 여인은 보이지 않았어. 공주님이 왜 물레가 멈췄는지, 자그마한 여인은 어디 있는지 물었을 때, 물레가 멈춘 건 그것을 돌릴 사람이 아무도 없어서이고, 자그마한 여인은 죽었다는 대답을 들었단다.\"\n\n(\"그 여자를 병원으로 데려갔어야 하는데요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"그랬으면 나았을 텐데요.\")\n\n\"공주님은 자그마한 여인을 잃은 슬픔에 아주 조금 운 뒤, 눈물을 닦고 전에 멈추었던 그 자리에서 마차에서 내려, 오두막으로 가 문 안을 들여다보았어. 이제 그녀를 바라볼 이도 없었고, 그녀가 바라볼 이도 없었지. 그래서 그녀는 곧장 안으로 들어가 그 소중히 간직된 그림자를 찾았어. 그러나 그것은 어디서도 흔적조차 찾을 수 없었지. 그제야 공주님은 자그마한 여인이 진실을 말했음을, 그것이 결코 누구에게도 폐를 끼치지 않으리라는 것을, 그것이 조용히 그녀 자신의 무덤 속으로 가라앉았음을, 그리고 그녀와 그것이 함께 안식에 들었음을 알았단다.\n\n이게 다야, 매기.\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 이렇게 이야기의 끝에 이르렀을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 노을빛이 어찌나 환하게 비치던지, 그녀는 손을 들어 그것을 가렸다.\n\n\"그 여자는 늙도록 살았어요?\" 매기가 물었다.\n\n\"그 자그마한 여인 말이니?\"\n\n\"네!\"\n\n\"모르겠어.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"하지만 아무리 늙도록 살았다 해도 마찬가지였을 거야.\"\n\n\"정말요!\" 매기가 말했다. \"음, 하긴 그랬겠죠.\" 그러고는 멍하니 쳐다보며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n그녀가 어찌나 오래도록 눈을 크게 뜨고 앉아 있던지, 마침내 리틀 도릿은 그녀를 상자에서 일으키려고 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다. 마당 아래를 흘끗 내려다보았을 때, 그녀는 팽크스가 들어와 지나가면서 곁눈질로 흘끗 올려다보는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"저 사람 누구예요, 작은엄마?\" 매기가 말했다. 그녀는 창가에서 그녀와 나란히 서서 그녀의 어깨에 기대고 있었다. \"저 사람 들락날락하는 거 자주 봤어요.\"\n\n\"점쟁이라고 부르는 걸 들었어.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"하지만 저 사람이 사람들의 과거나 현재 운명조차 많이 말해 줄 수 있을지 의문이야.\"\n\n\"공주님 운명도 못 말해 줬을까요?\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 어두운 감옥의 골짜기를 골똘히 내려다보며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그 자그마한 여인 운명도요?\" 매기가 말했다.\n\n\"못 했을 거야.\" 리틀 도릿이 노을빛을 환하게 받으며 말했다. \"하지만 이제 창가에서 그만 물러나자.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 25. Conspirators and Others\n\n팽크스 씨의 사저는 펜턴빌에 있었는데, 그는 그곳에서 지극히 영세한 사업을 하는 어느 전문직 신사의 삼층에 세 들어 살았다. 그 신사의 집은 거리로 난 문 안쪽에 또 하나의 문이 있어, 용수철 위에 얹혀 덫처럼 찰칵 소리를 내며 튀어 열렸고, 그는 채광창에 이렇게 써 붙여 두었다. 러그, 종합 대리인, 회계사, 채권 추심.\n\n엄정한 단순함 속에 위엄을 갖춘 이 명문은, 목마른 큰길에 잇닿은 작은 앞뜰 한 뙈기를 환히 밝혀 주었다. 그곳에서는 가장 먼지투성이인 잎사귀 몇 장이 음울한 고개를 떨군 채 숨 막히는 삶을 이어 가고 있었다. 이층에는 글씨 선생이 살았는데, 그는 정원 난간을 유리 진열장으로 활기차게 꾸며 놓았다. 그 진열장에는 그의 제자들이 여섯 번 수업을 받기 전에—온 가족이 식탁을 흔들어 대는 와중에—어떠했는지, 그리고 여섯 번 수업을 받은 뒤에—그 어린 가족이 자제되었을 때—어떻게 달라졌는지를 보여 주는 빼어난 본보기들이 담겨 있었다. 팽크스 씨의 임차 범위는 통풍 잘되는 침실 하나에 한정되어 있었다. 그는 집주인 러그 씨와 더불어, 정확히 규정된 일정 요금표에 따라, 또 일정한 구두 통지를 정식으로 했을 경우, 뒷방에서 러그 씨와 그의 딸 러그 양의 일요일 아침, 점심, 차, 또는 저녁 식사 가운데 어느 하나, 또는 여럿, 또는 전부를 함께할지 선택할 자유를 가진다고 약정하고 합의했다.\n\n러그 양은 약간의 재산을 가진 숙녀였는데, 그 재산과 더불어 이웃에서 상당한 명성을 얻게 된 사연인즉, 그 동네에 사는 어느 중년 제빵사에게 마음을 심하게 찢기고 감정을 짓이겨져, 러그 씨의 주선으로 혼약 파기에 대한 손해배상을 받아내기 위해 법적 절차를 밟지 않을 수 없었던 데 있었다. 그 제빵사는 그 자리에서 러그 양 측 변호인에게, 욕설 한 마디당 약 십팔 펜스 꼴로 도합 이십 기니에 이르도록 신랄하게 규탄당했고, 그에 상응하는 손해배상 판결을 받았으나, 여전히 펜턴빌의 젊은이들로부터 이따금 박해를 받았다. 그러나 러그 양은 법의 위엄에 둘러싸인 데다 손해배상금을 공채에 투자해 둔 터라, 사람들의 존중을 받았다.\n\n러그 씨로 말하자면, 마치 오래전에 그의 혈색이 죄다 뽑혀 나가기라도 한 듯 둥글고 허연 낯에, 닳아빠진 벽난로용 빗자루 같은 누렇고 텁수룩한 머리를 한 사람이었다. 또 러그 양으로 말하자면, 얼굴 곳곳에 셔츠 단추 같은 작은 황갈색 반점이 돋아 있고, 제 누런 머릿단은 풍성하다기보다 차라리 듬성듬성한 여인이었다. 팽크스 씨는 몇 해 동안 일요일이면 으레 이 두 사람과 어울려 식사를 했고, 일주일에 두 번쯤은 빵과 네덜란드 치즈, 그리고 흑맥주로 차린 저녁 간식을 즐겼다. 팽크스 씨는 러그 양에게 아무런 두려움을 주지 않는 극소수의 혼인 적격 남자 가운데 하나였는데, 그가 스스로를 안심시키는 논거는 두 갈래였다. 즉 첫째로 \"두 번씩이나 통할 리는 없다\"는 것이었고, 둘째로 \"자기는 그럴 만한 가치가 없다\"는 것이었다. 이 이중의 갑옷으로 무장한 채, 팽크스 씨는 거리낌 없이 러그 양을 향해 콧방귀를 뀌었다.\n\n이때까지 팽크스 씨는 펜턴빌의 거처에서 잠자는 것 말고는 이렇다 할 일을 거의 또는 전혀 처리하지 않았다. 그러나 이제 점쟁이 노릇을 하게 된 지금, 그는 자정이 넘어서까지 작은 앞방 사무실에서 러그 씨와 곧잘 밀담을 나누었고, 그런 야심한 시각이 지난 뒤에도 침실에서 수지 양초를 태우곤 했다. 집주인의 빚 캐는 일꾼으로서 그의 임무가 조금도 가벼워지지 않았음에도, 또 그 일이 숱한 가시밭이 보여 주는 것 외에는 장미 침대와 닮은 구석이라곤 없었음에도, 어떤 새로운 분야의 일거리가 그에게 끊임없이 손길을 요구했다. 밤에 가부장을 떨궈 놓는 것은 곧 어느 이름 없는 배를 끌고 다른 물길에서 새로이 노 저어 일하기 위함일 뿐이었다.\n\n부친 치버리 씨와의 개인적 친분에서 그의 상냥한 아내와 시름에 잠긴 아들에게 소개받는 데까지 나아가는 일은 쉬웠을 수도 있다. 쉬웠든 아니든, 팽크스 씨는 그 일을 곧 이루어 냈다. 그는 그 교도소에 처음 모습을 보인 지 한두 주 만에 담배 가게의 품속에 자리 잡았고, 특히 청년 존과 좋은 사이를 다지는 데 마음을 쏟았다. 이 노력에서 그는 크게 성공을 거두어, 그 시들어 가던 목동을 숲에서 꾀어내고, 신비로운 임무를 떠맡도록 부추겼다. 그리하여 청년 존은 일정치 않은 간격으로 길게는 이삼 일씩 잇따라 자취를 감추기 시작했다. 신중한 치버리 부인은 이 변화를 몹시 의아해하여, 문기둥에 그려진 하일랜드 사람 그림에 해로운 일이라며 항의했을 법도 했으나, 두 가지 강력한 이유 때문에 그러지 않았다. 하나는 그녀의 존이 이런 외출이 진척시키리라고 여겨지는 일에 강한 흥미를 갖게 되어 분발했다는 것이었는데—이 점은 그녀가 풀 죽은 그의 기운에 이롭다고 여겼다—다른 하나는 팽크스 씨가 그녀의 아들이 쓰는 시간에 대한 대가로 하루에 칠 실링 육 펜스라는 후한 금액을 지불하기로 은밀히 합의했다는 것이었다. 그 제안은 그 자신에게서 비롯된 것으로, 다음과 같이 간명한 말로 표현되었다. \"댁의 존이 그걸 받지 않을 만큼 어수룩하다 해도, 그게 부인까지 그래야 할 이유는 안 되지요, 안 그렇습니까? 그러니, 우리끼리만의 얘깁니다만, 부인, 일은 일이니까요, 자 여기 있습니다!\"\n\n치버리 씨가 이런 일들을 어떻게 생각했는지, 또는 그에 대해 얼마나 알았는지 혹은 몰랐는지는 그 자신에게서 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었다. 그가 말수가 적은 사람이라는 점은 이미 언급한 바 있다. 그리고 여기서 한 가지 덧붙이자면, 그는 모든 것을 잠가 두는 직업적 습관에 젖어 있었다. 그는 마셜시의 채무자들을 가두듯이 자기 자신도 꼼꼼히 잠가 두었다. 식사를 후딱 해치우는 그의 버릇조차 한결같은 전체의 일부였는지도 모른다. 그러나 다른 모든 면에서 그가 마셜시 문을 다루듯 제 입을 다루었다는 데에는 의문의 여지가 없다. 그는 까닭 없이는 결코 그것을 열지 않았다. 무언가를 내보낼 필요가 있을 때면, 그는 입을 조금 열어 꼭 그 목적에 충분할 만큼만 벌린 채 두었다가 다시 잠갔다. 마치 마셜시 문에서 수고를 아끼느라, 밖으로 나가려는 방문객이 있어도 또 다른 방문객이 마당을 가로질러 오는 것을 보면 열쇠 한 번 돌리는 것으로 둘 다 해결되도록 그를 잠시 기다리게 했듯이, 마찬가지로 그는 또 다른 말이 입가로 올라오는 것을 알아채면 곧잘 한마디를 미뤄 두었다가 둘을 한꺼번에 내놓곤 했다. 그의 내심을 들여다볼 열쇠를 그의 얼굴에서 찾는 일로 말하자면, 그 마셜시 열쇠가 돌려지는 대상인 각 인물의 성격과 내력을 보여 주는 색인만큼이나 그의 얼굴은 읽기 어려웠다.\n\n팽크스 씨가 누군가를 펜턴빌의 만찬에 초대하도록 마음이 움직였다는 것은 그의 달력에서 전례 없는 사건이었다. 그러나 그는 청년 존을 만찬에 초대했고, 심지어 그를 (값비싸므로) 위험한 러그 양의 매력의 사정거리 안에까지 끌어들였다. 잔치는 일요일로 정해졌고, 러그 양은 그날을 위해 손수 양다리 고기에 굴을 채워 넣어 제빵사에게 보냈다—단, 바로 그 제빵사가 아니라 경쟁 상대인 가게로 말이다. 오렌지와 사과, 견과류도 마련되었다. 그리고 손님의 마음을 흥겹게 해 줄 럼주는 토요일 밤에 팽크스 씨가 직접 사 들고 왔다.\n\n먹고 마실 것의 비축이 손님 접대의 주된 부분은 아니었다. 그 특별한 묘미는 미리부터 마련된 식구다운 신뢰와 공감에 있었다. 청년 존이 한 시 반에, 상아빛 손잡이 지팡이도 황금빛 잔가지 무늬 조끼도 없이, 마치 재앙 같은 구름에 햇살을 잘린 태양처럼 나타나자, 팽크스 씨는 그를 누런 머리의 러그 부녀에게, 자기가 그토록 자주 입에 올렸던, 도릿 양을 사랑하는 그 청년이라며 소개했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 러그 씨가 특별히 그 자격으로 그를 맞으며 말했다. \"이렇게 알게 되어 더없는 기쁨이외다. 그대의 그 감정은 그대를 영예롭게 하는 것이지요. 그대는 젊으니, 부디 그 감정보다 오래 살아남지 않기를! 만약 내가 내 감정보다 오래 살아남게 된다면 말이오, 선생,\" 말수가 많고 화술이 유난히 좋다고 여겨지던 러그 씨가 말했다. \"만약 내가 내 감정보다 오래 살아남게 된다면, 나를 이 세상에서 없애 줄 사람에게 유언으로 오십 파운드를 남기겠소.\"\n\n러그 양은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"내 딸이오, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"애너스테이샤, 너도 이 청년의 애정의 처지에 낯설지 않을 게다. 내 딸도 시련을 겪었지요, 선생\"—러그 씨로서는 그 말을 더 정확히 단수로 썼어야 했을 테지만—\"그러니 그대의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있을 거외다.\"\n\n청년 존은 이 인사의 가슴 뭉클한 정겨움에 거의 압도당하여, 그런 취지로 자기 마음을 밝혔다.\n\n\"내가 그대에게 부러운 점은 말이오, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"모자를 받아 드리지요—우리 집은 걸이가 좀 부족해서—구석에 둘 테니, 거기선 아무도 밟지 않을 거요—내가 그대에게 부러운 점은, 선생, 자기 감정을 누리는 그 호사외다. 나는 그런 호사가 때로 금지되는 직업에 몸담고 있지요.\"\n\n청년 존은 감사의 뜻과 함께 대답하기를, 자기는 그저 옳은 일을, 그리고 자기가 도릿 양에게 얼마나 온전히 헌신하고 있는지를 보여 주는 일을 하기를 바랄 뿐이라고 했다. 자기는 사심 없기를 바라며, 또 그러기를 소망한다고 했다. 자기를 완전히 뒷전으로 물린 채, 도릿 양에게 도움이 될 일이라면 무엇이든 제 힘닿는 데까지 하기를 바라며, 또 그러고 있기를 소망한다고 했다. 자기가 할 수 있는 일이란 보잘것없지만, 그래도 그 일만은 하고 있기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 러그 씨가 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"그대는 마주치는 것만으로도 사람에게 이로운 청년이외다. 그대야말로 내가 증인석에 세워, 법조계 사람들의 마음을 인간답게 만들어 주고 싶은 청년이오. 입맛도 챙겨 오셨기를 바라며, 칼과 포크를 야무지게 놀려 주시기를 바라오만?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 청년 존이 대답했다. \"요즈음은 많이 먹지를 못합니다.\"\n\n러그 씨는 그를 조금 옆으로 데려갔다. \"내 딸의 소송 때 말이오, 선생,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애가 짓밟힌 감정과 제 성(性)을 옹호하고자 러그 대 보킨스 사건의 원고가 되었던 그때 말이지요. 만약 그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 여겼더라면, 치버리 씨, 그 시절 내 딸이 먹은 든든한 음식의 양이 일주일에 십 온스를 넘지 않았다는 사실을 증거로 제출할 수도 있었을 거외다.\"\n\n\"저는 그보다는 조금 더 먹는 것 같습니다, 선생,\" 상대방이 마치 다소 부끄러운 일을 고백하듯 머뭇거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 그대의 경우엔 사람의 탈을 쓴 악귀가 없잖소,\" 러그 씨가 논증하는 듯한 미소와 손짓을 곁들여 말했다. \"보시오, 치버리 씨! 사람의 탈을 쓴 악귀가 없단 말이오!\"\n\n\"네, 선생, 정말 그렇습니다,\" 청년 존이 순박하게 덧붙였다. \"만약 있다면 저는 무척 마음 아플 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그 마음이야말로,\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"그대가 지닌 것으로 알려진 신조에서 마땅히 기대할 만한 것이외다. 만약 내 딸이 그 말을 들었다면 크게 감동했을 거요, 선생. 양고기가 보이니 하는 말인데, 그 애가 듣지 않아 다행이오. 팽크스 씨, 이번에는 부디 내 맞은편에 앉아 주시오. 얘야, 너는 치버리 씨 맞은편에 앉으렴. 우리가 이제 받으려 하는 것에 대하여, 우리가 (그리고 도릿 양이) 진심으로 감사할 수 있기를!\"\n\n이 식사 개회사를 전하는 러그 씨의 태도에 진중한 익살이 깃들어 있지 않았더라면, 마치 도릿 양이 그 자리의 일원으로 와 있기를 기대하는 듯이 보였을지도 몰랐다. 팽크스는 늘 하던 식으로 그 농을 알아차렸고, 늘 하던 식으로 음식을 먹어 치웠다. 러그 양도, 어쩌면 그동안 밀린 양을 좀 메우려는 듯, 마찬가지로 양고기에 무척 살갑게 달려들어, 고기는 순식간에 뼈만 남도록 줄어들었다. 빵과 버터로 만든 푸딩이 통째로 사라졌고, 상당량의 치즈와 무도 같은 방식으로 자취를 감추었다. 그러고 나서 후식이 나왔다.\n\n그러고 나서, 럼주에 물을 타기 전에, 또한 팽크스 씨의 수첩이 등장했다. 뒤이은 업무 진행은 짧으면서도 기이했으며, 다분히 음모의 성격을 띠고 있었다. 팽크스 씨는 이제 거의 가득 차 가는 수첩을 꼼꼼히 들여다보며, 짤막한 발췌 사항들을 골라내어 식탁 위 별도의 종이쪽지에 적었다. 그동안 러그 씨는 그를 면밀히 주시했고, 청년 존은 갈피 못 잡는 그 눈을 사색의 안개 속에 잃고 있었다. 우두머리 모의자 역할을 맡은 팽크스 씨가 발췌를 마치자, 그는 그것들을 한 번 훑어보고, 손질하고, 수첩을 거둬들인 뒤, 그것들을 카드 패처럼 손에 쥐었다.\n\n\"자, 베드퍼드셔에 묘지가 하나 있소,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"누가 맡겠소?\"\n\n\"아무도 부르지 않거든, 선생, 제가 맡지요,\" 러그 씨가 대답했다.\n\n팽크스 씨는 그에게 카드 한 장을 내준 뒤, 다시 제 패를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"자, 요크에 조회 건이 하나 있소,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"누가 맡겠소?\"\n\n\"나는 요크는 영 자신이 없소,\" 러그 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 아마도,\" 팽크스가 말을 이었다. \"자네가 좀 수고해 주겠나, 존 치버리?\"\n\n청년 존이 응낙하자, 팽크스는 그에게 카드를 내주고 다시 제 패를 살폈다.\n\n\"런던에 교회가 하나 있군. 이건 내가 맡지. 그리고 가족용 성경책이 하나. 이것도 내가 맡고. 그러면 내가 둘이군. 내가 둘,\" 카드 위로 거칠게 숨을 내쉬며 팽크스가 되뇌었다. \"여기 더럼의 사무원 건은 자네 거다, 존. 그리고 던스터블의 늙은 뱃사람 신사 건은 당신 거외다, 러그 씨. 내가 둘이라고 했던가? 그래, 내가 둘. 여기 비석이 하나. 이러면 내가 셋. 그리고 사산아가 하나. 이러면 내가 넷. 그리고 당장은 이게 전부요.\"\n\n이렇게 카드를 다 나누고 나자—모든 일이 아주 조용히, 낮춘 목소리로 이루어졌다—팽크스 씨는 푸푸 숨을 몰아쉬며 제 가슴주머니 속을 더듬어 즈크 자루 하나를 끄집어냈다. 그러고는 인색한 손길로 여비를 두 몫으로 헤아려 냈다. \"현금이 빠르게 나가는군,\" 그가 두 남자 동료에게 각각 한 몫씩 밀어 주며 근심스레 말했다. \"아주 빠르게 말이야.\"\n\n\"제가 드릴 말씀은 이것뿐입니다, 팽크스 씨,\" 청년 존이 말했다. \"제 형편이 제 경비조차 댈 수 없을 만큼인 게, 또 거리를 도보로 가는 데 필요한 시간을 제게 허락하는 게 바람직하지 못한 게 참으로 유감스럽습니다. 사례도 보수도 없이 두 다리가 닳도록 걷는 것보다 더 큰 만족을 줄 일은 제겐 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n이 청년의 사심 없음이 러그 양의 눈에는 어찌나 우스꽝스러워 보였던지, 그녀는 황급히 자리를 빠져나가 웃음이 다할 때까지 계단에 앉아 있어야 했다. 그동안 팽크스 씨는 청년 존을 적잖은 연민을 담아 바라보며, 마치 즈크 자루의 목을 비틀어 짜기라도 하듯 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 그것을 비틀어 말았다. 숙녀는 그가 그것을 다시 주머니에 넣을 즈음 돌아와, 일행을 위해 제 고운 몫도 잊지 않고 럼주에 물을 타서 저마다에게 잔을 건넸다. 모두에게 잔이 돌아가자, 러그 씨가 일어서서 식탁 한가운데 위로 팔을 쭉 뻗어 잔을 말없이 내밀었고, 그 몸짓으로 나머지 셋에게 잔을 보태어 다 함께 모의자다운 건배를 울리자고 청했다. 그 의식은 어느 지점까지는 효과적이었고, 만약 러그 양이 마무리로 잔을 입술로 가져가다가 마침 청년 존을 쳐다보지만 않았던들 끝까지 온전히 그러했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 사심 없음의 가소로운 우스꽝스러움에 또다시 어찌나 사로잡혔던지, 럼주 물의 천상 같은 몇 방울을 사방에 뿜고는 당황한 채 물러나 버렸다.\n\n이것이 팽크스가 펜턴빌에서 베푼 전례 없는 만찬이었다. 또 이것이 팽크스가 영위한 바쁘고도 기이한 삶이었다. 깨어 있는 시간 가운데 그가 근심에서 풀려나, 두루 깔린 목적 없이 어딘가로 가거나 무언가를 말하며 스스로를 즐기는 듯 보이는 유일한 순간은, 블리딩 하트 야드 아래쪽의 지팡이 짚은 절름발이 외국인에게 어렴풋한 관심을 보일 때였다.\n\n그 외국인은—이름은 존 밥티스트 카발레토였으나 야드에서는 그를 밥티스트 씨라고 불렀다—재잘대고 느긋하며 희망에 찬 자그마한 사내여서, 그가 팽크스의 마음을 끈 것은 아마도 대조의 힘에서였으리라. 외로이, 연약하게, 주변 사람들과 의사를 나눌 수 있는 유일한 언어의 가장 필요한 낱말조차 변변히 알지 못한 채로, 그는 그 일대에서는 새로운, 활기찬 태도로 제 운명의 흐름을 따라갔다. 먹을 것은 적고 마실 것은 더 적었으며, 몸에 걸친 것이나 여태껏 본 가장 작은 봇짐 가운데 하나에 꾸려 온 것 말고는 입을 옷도 없었으나, 그는 처음 야드를 절뚝거리며 오르내릴 때 하얀 이를 드러내며 겸손하게 사람들의 호의를 구할 적에도, 마치 더없이 융성한 처지에 있기라도 한 양 환한 낯빛을 지어 보였다.\n\n절름발이든 멀쩡하든, 외국인이 블리딩 하트 사람들과 사이좋게 지내기란 힘겨운 일이었다. 첫째로, 그들은 외국인이라면 누구나 칼을 한 자루 지니고 다닌다고 막연히 믿었다. 둘째로, 외국인이라면 마땅히 제 나라로 돌아가야 한다는 것을 건전한 국민적 헌정의 공리로 여겼다. 그들은 만약 그 원칙이 두루 인정된다면 세계 각지에서 얼마나 많은 자기 동포가 자기들 손으로 되돌아오게 될지는 도무지 따져 볼 생각을 하지 않았고, 그것을 유독 영국 특유의 것으로만 여겼다. 셋째로, 그들은 외국인이 영국인이 아니라는 것 자체가 그에게 내려진 일종의 천벌이며, 그의 나라가 영국이 하지 않는 일을 하고 영국이 하는 일을 하지 않기에 온갖 재앙이 그 나라에 닥치는 것이라는 관념을 품고 있었다. 물론 이 믿음에 있어서 그들은 오래도록 배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가에게서 면밀히 길들여져 왔는데, 이들은 공식적으로 그들에게, 그 두 거대 가문에 복종하기를 거부하는 나라는 결코 신의 섭리의 보호 아래 있기를 바랄 수 없노라고 늘 선포하면서도, 정작 그들이 그것을 곧이듣자 뒤에서는 그들을 해 아래 가장 편견에 찬 족속이라고 헐뜯었다.\n\n그러므로 이것은 블리딩 하트 사람들의 정치적 입장이라 부를 만했다. 그러나 그들은 야드에 외국인을 들이는 것에 대해 또 다른 반대 이유들도 품고 있었다. 그들은 외국인이란 늘 형편이 어렵다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기들 역시 더 바랄 나위 없을 만큼 어려운 형편이었음에도, 그 사실이 반대의 힘을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다. 그들은 외국인이란 군홧발에 짓밟히고 총검에 찔린다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기들도 조금이라도 심사가 사나운 기색을 보이면 곧장 두개골이 박살나곤 했음에도, 그것은 어디까지나 무딘 연장에 의한 것이니 셈에 들지 않았다. 그들은 외국인이란 늘 부도덕하다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기네 나라에도 이따금 순회재판이 열리고 가끔 이혼 소송 따위가 있었음에도, 그것은 이 문제와는 아무 상관이 없었다. 그들은 외국인이란, 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경에게 깃발을 펄럭이고 '룰 브리타니아' 곡조를 울리며 떼 지어 투표소까지 호위받아 가 본 적이 없으니, 독립적 기개라곤 없다고 믿었다. 장황해지지 않기 위해 줄이거니와, 그들은 이와 비슷한 부류의 다른 믿음도 많이 품고 있었다.\n\n이런 장애물들에 맞서, 지팡이 짚은 절름발이 외국인은 제 깜냥껏 헤쳐 나가야 했다. 아주 외돌토리는 아니었으니, 아서 클레넘 씨가 그를 플로니시 부부에게 추천해 둔 덕이었으나(그는 같은 집의 꼭대기 층에 살았다), 그래도 형세는 몹시 불리했다. 그러나 블리딩 하트 사람들은 마음씨가 고운 이들이었다. 그래서 그들은 그 자그마한 사내가 명랑하게 절뚝이며 돌아다니는 것을, 사람 좋은 낯빛으로, 해코지 하나 하지 않고, 칼 한 번 빼 들지 않고, 차마 못 봐줄 부도덕을 저지르는 일도 없이, 주로 곡물과 우유로 끼니를 때우고, 저녁이면 플로니시 부인의 아이들과 놀아 주는 것을 보자, 그가 영국인이 되기를 결코 바랄 수는 없을지언정 그 불운을 그의 머리 위에 들씌우는 것은 가혹한 일이라고 여기기 시작했다. 그들은 그의 수준에 자기들을 맞추어, 그를 '밥티스트 씨'라고 부르면서도 갓난아기 다루듯 대했고, 그의 생기 넘치는 몸짓과 어린애 같은 영어에 한도 끝도 없이 웃어 댔는데—그가 그것을 개의치 않고 함께 웃었기에 더욱 그러했다. 그들은 그가 귀머거리라도 되는 양 아주 큰 목소리로 그에게 말했다. 그들은 순수한 형태의 언어를 가르친답시고, 미개인들이 쿡 선장에게, 혹은 프라이데이가 로빈슨 크루소에게 건넸음 직한 문장을 지어냈다. 플로니시 부인은 특히 이 재주에 뛰어나, \"내 바람 댁 다리 곧 다 낫기\"라는 말로 어찌나 명성을 떨쳤던지, 야드에서는 그것이 이탈리아어를 하는 것과 정말 한 걸음밖에 차이 나지 않는다고들 여겼다. 플로니시 부인 자신마저 자기에게 그 언어에 대한 타고난 소명이 있다고 여기기 시작했다. 그가 더 인기를 끌게 되자, 그에게 풍성한 어휘를 가르치고자 집 안 살림살이까지 동원되었다. 그래서 그가 야드에 나타날 때면 부인들이 저마다 문간으로 뛰쳐나와 외쳐 댔다. \"밥티스트 씨—찻주전자!\" \"밥티스트 씨—쓰레받기!\" \"밥티스트 씨—밀가루 체!\" \"밥티스트 씨—커피 끓이개!\" 그러면서 그 물건들을 내보이며, 앵글로색슨 말의 끔찍한 어려움을 그에게 절감시켰다.\n\n밥티스트 씨가 이런 진척의 단계에 이르렀을 무렵, 또 그가 그곳에 들어와 산 지 세 주째쯤 되었을 때, 팽크스 씨의 호기심이 그 자그마한 사내에게 끌리게 되었다. 통역으로 따라온 플로니시 부인을 데리고 그의 다락방에 올라가 보니, 밥티스트 씨는 바닥에 놓인 침상과 탁자 하나, 의자 하나 말고는 가구라곤 없는 곳에서, 몇 가지 간단한 연장의 도움을 받아 더없이 흥겨운 태도로 무언가를 깎고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 영감님,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"셈을 치르시오!\"\n\n그는 종잇조각에 접어 넣은 돈을 미리 준비해 두었다가, 웃으며 그것을 건넸다. 그러고는 거침없는 동작으로, 실링 액수만큼 오른손 손가락을 펴 보이고, 나머지 육 펜스를 나타내려고 허공에 가로로 한 번 그었다.\n\n\"오!\" 팽크스 씨가 그를 신기한 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"그런 거였군, 그래? 빠릿빠릿한 양반이로구만. 됐소. 하긴 받으리라곤 기대도 안 했지만.\"\n\n여기서 플로니시 부인이 자못 너그러운 태도로 끼어들어 밥티스트 씨에게 설명했다. \"이분 좋아해. 이분 돈 받아 기뻐.\"\n\n자그마한 사내는 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다. 그 환한 얼굴이 팽크스 씨에게는 유난히 매력적으로 보이는 듯했다. \"다리는 좀 어떻소?\" 그가 플로니시 부인에게 물었다.\n\n\"오, 한결 나아졌답니다, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"다음 주쯤이면 지팡이를 아주 놓을 수 있을 것 같아요.\" (이 기회가 놓치기엔 너무 좋았던지라, 플로니시 부인은 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 밥티스트 씨에게 \"내 바람 댁 다리 곧 다 낫기\" 하고 설명함으로써 자신의 그 대단한 재주를 펼쳐 보였다.)\n\n\"게다가 명랑한 친구로군,\" 팽크스 씨가 마치 태엽 장난감이라도 되는 양 그에게 감탄하며 말했다. \"어떻게 먹고삽니까?\"\n\n\"그게 말이죠, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 대답했다. \"지금 보시다시피 저 꽃들을 깎는 데 제법 솜씨가 있다는 게 드러났답니다.\" (밥티스트 씨는 그들이 말하는 동안 그 얼굴들을 살피다가 제 작품을 들어 보였다. 플로니시 부인이 팽크스 씨를 위해 자신의 이탈리아식으로 통역했다. \"이분 좋아해. 갑절로 좋아!\")\n\n\"저걸로 먹고살 수 있답니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"아주 적게 들여서도 살 수 있고요, 선생, 머지않아 꽤 괜찮은 벌이를 할 수 있으리라 기대된답니다. 클레넘 씨가 저 일을 얻어다 주셨고, 그 밖에도 바로 옆집 공장에서 자질구레한 일거리를 주신답니다—말하자면, 저 사람이 필요로 하는 걸 아실 때면 일부러 만들어 주시는 거죠.\"\n\n\"그럼 일에 매달려 있지 않을 때는, 저 친구 뭘 하고 지냅니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 아직은 별로 하는 게 없어요, 선생, 아무래도 많이 걷지를 못해서 그렇겠지요. 그래도 야드를 돌아다니고, 딱히 알아듣거나 알아듣게 하지도 못하면서 잡담을 나누고, 아이들과 놀고, 햇볕 아래 앉아 있고요—마치 안락의자라도 되는 양 아무 데나 털썩 앉지요—노래도 부르고, 웃기도 한답니다!\"\n\n\"웃는다고요!\" 팽크스 씨가 따라 말했다. \"내 보기엔 저 친구 머릿속 이빨이 죄다 늘 웃고 있는 것 같던데.\"\n\n\"하지만 야드 저쪽 끝 계단 꼭대기에 올라설 때마다요, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"어찌나 묘하게 밖을 내다보는지! 그래서 우리 중에는 저 사람이 제 나라가 있는 쪽을 내다본다고 여기는 이도 있고, 보고 싶지 않은 누군가를 찾는 거라고 여기는 이도 있고, 또 뭐라고 생각해야 할지 모르겠다는 이도 있답니다.\"\n\n밥티스트 씨는 그녀의 말뜻을 대강 알아들은 듯했다. 아니면 그 영민함으로 그녀가 내다보는 시늉을 한 가벼운 몸짓을 포착해 적용했는지도 모른다. 어느 쪽이든 그는 눈을 감고 고개를 젖히며, 자기가 한 일에 충분한 이유가 있는 사람의 태도로, 제 나라 말로 그건 아무것도 아니라고 말했다. 알트로!\n\n\"알트로가 뭡니까?\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"흠! 그건 일종의 두루뭉술한 표현이랍니다, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요?\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그렇다면, 영감님, 당신에게도 알트로요. 그럼 이만. 알트로!\"\n\n밥티스트 씨가 제 활기찬 투로 그 말을 여러 번 되뇌자, 팽크스 씨는 제 좀 더 굼뜬 투로 그것을 한 번 되돌려 주었다. 그때부터, 집시 같은 팽크스가 밤에 녹초가 되어 집으로 돌아가는 길에 블리딩 하트 야드를 빙 둘러, 조용히 계단을 올라가 밥티스트 씨의 방문 안을 들여다보고, 그가 방에 있는 것을 보면 \"어이, 영감님! 알트로!\" 하고 인사하는 것이 잦은 버릇이 되었다. 그러면 밥티스트 씨는 한도 없이 환하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 \"알트로, 시뇨레, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로!\" 하고 화답하곤 했다. 이 극도로 압축된 대화를 나눈 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 짐을 던 듯 산뜻해진 모습으로 제 갈 길을 가곤 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Nobody’s State of Mind\n\n만약 아서 클레넘이 펫을 사랑하는 일을 단호히 자제하겠다는 그 현명한 결심에 이르지 못했더라면, 그는 자기 마음과의 어려운 싸움을 동반한 숱한 혼란 속에서 살아갔을 것이다. 그중에서도 가장 작지 않은 것은, 헨리 가우언 씨를, 노골적인 혐오로까지는 아니더라도 싫어하려는 경향과, 그러한 마음이 떳떳하지 못하다는 속삭임 사이에서 그의 마음속에서 끊임없이 벌어지는 다툼이었으리라. 너그러운 천성은 강한 반감에 쉽게 빠지지 않으며, 냉정하게라도 그것을 받아들이는 데 더디다. 그러나 악감정이 자기 안에서 자라나는 것을 발견하고, 때때로 그 기원이 냉정하지 못함을 알아챌 수 있을 때, 그런 천성은 괴로워지게 마련이다.\n\n그러므로 앞서 말한 그 큰 신중함의 결심이 아니었더라면, 헨리 가우언 씨는 클레넘의 마음을 흐려 놓았을 것이며, 더 유쾌한 사람들이나 화제들보다 훨씬 자주 그의 마음에 떠올랐을 것이다. 실상은, 가우언 씨가 대니얼 도이스의 마음으로 옮겨 간 듯했다. 어쨌든 둘이 함께 나누는 다정한 대화에서 그를 입에 올리는 일은 대개 클레넘보다는 도이스 씨의 차지가 되곤 했다. 이런 대화는 이제 자주 있는 일이었는데, 두 동업자가 런던 월 가까이, 잉글랜드 은행에서 멀지 않은 어느 점잖고 고풍스러운 시티 거리에 자리한 널찍한 집의 일부를 함께 쓰고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n도이스 씨는 트위크넘에 가서 하루를 보내고 온 참이었다. 클레넘은 사양했었다. 도이스 씨는 막 집에 돌아왔다. 그는 클레넘의 거실 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀며 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다.\n\n\"들어와요, 들어와요!\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"책을 읽고 계신 걸 보고,\" 도이스가 들어서며 대꾸했다. \"방해받기 싫으실까 봐서요.\"\n\n그가 세운 그 주목할 만한 결심이 아니었더라면, 클레넘은 자기가 무엇을 읽고 있었는지조차 정말로 알지 못했을 것이다. 책이 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있긴 했어도, 정말로 한 시간 내내 책에 눈길을 두지 않고 있었을 것이다. 그는 책을 꽤 빠르게 덮어 버렸다.\n\n\"다들 잘 지내십니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"네.\" 도이스가 말했다. \"다들 잘 지냅니다. 모두 평안해요.\"\n\n대니얼에게는 손수건을 모자 안에 넣어 다니는 오래된 장인다운 버릇이 있었다. 그는 그것을 꺼내 이마를 닦으며 천천히 되풀이했다. \"모두 평안해요. 미니 양은 특히 안색이 좋아 보이던데요.\"\n\n\"오두막에 손님이라도 있었습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 손님은 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 네 분이 어떻게 지내셨습니까?\" 클레넘이 명랑하게 물었다.\n\n\"다섯이었습니다.\" 그의 동업자가 대꾸했다. \"그 뭐냐 하는 사람이 있었지요. 그 사람이 거기 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 누굽니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"헨리 가우언 씨요.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇지!\" 클레넘이 평소와 달리 활기차게 외쳤다. \"그렇네요!—깜빡했습니다.\"\n\n\"전에 말씀드렸을 텐데요, 기억하실지.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다. \"그 사람은 일요일이면 늘 거기 있습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 네.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"이제 기억나는군요.\"\n\n대니얼 도이스는 여전히 이마를 닦으며 끈덕지게 되풀이했다. \"네. 그 사람이 거기 있었지요, 거기 있었습니다. 아, 그래요, 거기 있었어요. 그리고 그 사람의 개도요. 그놈도 거기 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"미글스 양이 그—그 개를—무척 좋아하지요.\" 클레넘이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 동업자가 동의했다. \"제가 그 사람을 좋아하는 것보다 그분이 그 개를 더 좋아하지요.\"\n\n\"말씀하시는 게 그—?\"\n\n\"가우언 씨 말입니다, 아주 분명히요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다.\n\n대화에 빈틈이 생겼고, 클레넘은 그것을 시계 태엽을 감는 데 썼다.\n\n\"어쩌면 판단이 좀 성급하신지도 모르겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리의 판단이라는 게—일반적인 경우를 두고 하는 말입니다만—\"\n\n\"물론이지요.\" 도이스가 말했다.\n\n\"우리도 거의 모르는 사이에 공정하지 못한 여러 고려에 휩쓸리기가 쉬워서, 거기에 경계를 두어야 할 필요가 있습니다. 이를테면, 그—씨는—\"\n\n\"가우언이오.\" 도이스가 조용히 말했다. 그 이름을 입에 올리는 일은 거의 늘 그의 몫이었다.\n\n\"젊고 잘생겼고, 느긋하고 재빠르며, 재능이 있고, 다양한 종류의 삶을 꽤 많이 보아 온 사람입니다. 그에게 편견을 품을 만한 사심 없는 이유를 대기란 어려울지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"나로서는 어렵지 않은 것 같소, 클레넘.\" 동업자가 대꾸했다. \"나는 그가 내 오랜 친구의 집에 지금의 근심을, 그리고 내가 두려워하건대 앞으로의 슬픔을 가져오는 것을 봅니다. 그가 딸의 얼굴에 가까이 다가가고 더 자주 그 얼굴을 바라볼수록, 내 오랜 친구의 얼굴에 더 깊은 주름을 새겨 넣는 것을 봅니다. 한마디로, 나는 그가 결코 행복하게 해 주지 못할 그 예쁘고 다정한 사람에게 그물을 던지고 있는 것을 봅니다.\"\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 압니까,\" 클레넘이 거의 고통스러운 사람의 어조로 말했다. \"그가 그녀를 행복하게 해 주지 못하리라는 것을요.\"\n\n\"우리가 어떻게 알겠소,\" 동업자가 대꾸했다. \"이 땅이 앞으로 백 년을 더 버틸지 말이오. 하지만 그럴 가능성이 매우 높다고 우리는 생각하지요.\"\n\n\"자, 자!\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"우리는 희망을 가져야 하고, 적어도 너그럽지는 못할지언정—이 경우에는 너그러울 기회가 우리에게 없으니까요—공정하려고 애써야 합니다. 이 신사가 야망의 아름다운 대상에게 구애하여 성공을 거두었다고 해서 그를 깎아내리지 맙시다. 그리고 그녀가 자기 사랑을 받을 만하다고 여기는 사람에게 그 사랑을 베풀 타고난 권리에 시비를 걸지도 맙시다.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 친구.\" 도이스가 말했다. \"또한 그녀가 너무 어리고 응석받이로 자라, 너무 잘 믿고 경험이 없어서 제대로 분별하지 못하는지도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"그건,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"우리가 바로잡을 수 있는 능력을 훨씬 넘어서는 일이지요.\"\n\n대니얼 도이스는 무겁게 고개를 가로젓고는 응했다. \"그런 것 같소.\"\n\n\"그러니 한마디로,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"우리는 가우언 씨에 관해 어떤 험담도 하지 않는 것이 우리답다고 마음을 정해야 합니다. 그에 대한 편견을 만족시키는 것은 옹졸한 짓일 테니까요. 그래서 저는, 제 몫으로는, 그를 폄하하지 않기로 결심합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 나 자신에 대해 그렇게까지 확신이 서지 않으니, 그에게 반대할 권리는 남겨 두겠소.\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 내가 나 자신에 대해서는 확신이 없어도, 당신에 대해서는 확신이 있소, 클레넘. 나는 당신이 얼마나 올곧은 사람인지, 얼마나 존경받을 만한지 알지요. 안녕히 주무시오, 나의 친구이자 동업자여!\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 손을 맞잡았는데, 마치 그들의 대화 밑바닥에 무언가 심각한 것이 있었다는 듯했다. 그러고 두 사람은 헤어졌다.\n\n이 무렵 그들은 여러 차례 그 가족을 방문했고, 헨리 가우언 씨가 그들 가운데 없을 때라도 그에 대한 지나가는 언급 한마디가 그 나루터에서의 우연한 만남이 있던 아침 미글스 씨의 햇살을 가렸던 그 구름을 다시 불러온다는 것을 늘 알아챘다. 클레넘이 그 금지된 열정을 가슴에 들인 적이 있었더라면, 이 시기는 진정한 시련의 시기였을 것이다. 실제 상황에서는, 의심할 바 없이 아무것도 아니었다—아무것도.\n\n마찬가지로, 그의 마음이 그 금지된 손님을 맞아들였더라면, 이 시기의 정신 상태를 묵묵히 헤쳐 나간 그의 싸움은 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다. 그의 경험에서 늘 따라다니던 죄악—저열하고 작은 수단으로 이기적인 목적을 좇는 일—의 새로운 국면으로 배신당하지 않으려 끊임없이 애쓰고, 그 대신 명예와 관대함의 어떤 높은 원칙을 붙드는 데에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다. 자신을 이기적으로 아끼느라 딸을, 아버지가 후회하리라 그가 믿는 그 소원해짐의 원인으로 만들어 그녀에게 조금이라도 괴로움을 안길까 봐, 미글스 씨의 집을 피하지조차 않기로 한 결심에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다. 가우언 씨가 나이가 더 많아 더 대등하다는 점과, 그의 용모와 태도가 더 매력적이라는 점을 늘 염두에 두는 그 겸허한 진실함에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다. 그의 삶과 내력만큼이나 특이한 그 안의 고통이 몹시 날카로웠음에도, 이 모든 것을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 것을 조금도 꾸밈없이, 사내답고 차분한 한결같음으로 해내는 데에 어떤 조용한 인격의 힘이 있었을지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 세운 결심 이후로는, 물론 그에게 이런 공이 있을 수 없었다. 그리고 그러한 정신 상태는 그 누구의 것도 아니었다—그 누구의 것도.\n\n가우언 씨는 그것이 그 누구의 것이든 누군가의 것이든 자기와는 상관없는 일로 여겼다. 그는 어떤 경우에도 완벽하게 평온한 태도를 유지했는데, 마치 클레넘이 감히 그 중대한 문제를 논했으리라는 가능성이란 너무도 동떨어지고 우스꽝스러워 상상조차 할 수 없다는 듯했다. 그는 늘 클레넘에게 베풀 상냥함과 그를 대할 여유를 지니고 있었으니, 그것만으로도 (만약 그가 그 현명한 길을 택하지 않았다는 가정 하에서) 그의 정신 상태에 매우 불편한 요소가 되었을 것이다.\n\n\"어제 함께하지 못하셔서 정말 아쉬웠습니다.\" 헨리 가우언 씨가 이튿날 아침 클레넘을 찾아와 말했다. \"거기 강 상류에서 유쾌한 하루를 보냈지요.\"\n\n그렇게 들었습니다, 하고 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"동업자분한테서요?\" 헨리 가우언이 대꾸했다. \"참으로 사랑스러운 노인장이시죠!\"\n\n\"제가 그분을 무척 존경합니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 더없이 훌륭한 분이세요!\" 가우언이 말했다. \"어쩜 그리 싱싱하고, 그리 푸르고, 그렇게 놀라운 것들을 믿으시는지요!\"\n\n여기에 클레넘의 귀에 거슬리는 경향이 있는 그 숱한 작고 거친 가시 중 하나가 있었다. 그는 도이스 씨를 깊이 존경한다고 그저 되풀이하며 그것을 흘려 넘겼다.\n\n\"매력적인 분이세요! 그 연세에 이르도록 그렇게 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오시면서, 길에서 아무것도 내려놓지 않고 길에서 아무것도 주워 담지 않는 걸 보면 얼마나 즐거운지요. 사람 마음을 따뜻하게 합니다. 어찌나 때 묻지 않고, 어찌나 소박하며, 어찌나 좋은 영혼이신지요! 정말이지 클레넘 씨, 그런 순진한 분에 비하면 사람은 자기가 지독히 세속적이고 사악하게 느껴지지요. 덧붙이자면, 이건 제 얘기일 뿐 당신을 포함하는 건 아닙니다. 당신도 진실한 분이시니까요.\"\n\n\"칭찬 고맙습니다.\" 클레넘이 거북하게 말했다. \"당신도 그러시길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"그저 그렇지요.\" 상대가 응했다. \"솔직히 말씀드리자면, 웬만큼은요. 저는 대단한 사기꾼은 아닙니다. 제 그림을 한 점 사 보세요, 그러면 단언컨대, 은밀히 말씀드리는데, 값어치만큼은 못 될 겁니다. 다른 사람 그림을 사 보세요—저를 완전히 능가하는 어떤 대가의 것이라도요—그러면 십중팔구 그에게 더 많이 줄수록, 그가 당신을 더 많이 속여 넘길 겁니다. 다들 그렇게 합니다.\"\n\n\"화가들이 다요?\"\n\n\"화가, 작가, 애국자, 시장에 좌판을 벌인 나머지 모두가요. 제가 아는 거의 누구에게든 십 파운드를 주어 보세요, 그러면 그는 그에 상응하는 만큼 당신을 속일 겁니다. 천 파운드면—그에 상응하는 만큼. 만 파운드면—그에 상응하는 만큼이요. 성공이 클수록, 사기도 큰 법이지요. 하지만 이 얼마나 멋진 세상입니까!\" 가우언이 따뜻한 열정으로 외쳤다. \"이 얼마나 유쾌하고, 훌륭하고, 사랑스러운 세상인지요!\"\n\n\"저는 오히려 생각했습니다만,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"당신이 말씀하시는 그 원리는 주로 다음과 같은 자들이 따르는 것이라고요—\"\n\n\"배너클 일가가요?\" 가우언이 웃으며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"황송하게도 에두름국을 운영하시는 그 정치하는 신사분들이요.\"\n\n\"아! 배너클 일가에게 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요.\" 가우언이 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"그들은 사랑스러운 친구들입니다! 집안에서 타고난 바보인 가엾은 클래런스조차도 더없이 유쾌하고 더없이 사랑스러운 멍청이지요! 게다가, 맙소사, 그에게는 당신을 깜짝 놀라게 할 일종의 영리함까지 있답니다!\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요. 무척이나요.\" 클레넘이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다.\n\n\"게다가 결국,\" 가우언이, 이 너른 세상의 모든 것을 똑같이 가벼운 무게로 줄여 버리는 그 특유의 균형 잡기로 외쳤다. \"에두름국이 끝내 모든 사람과 모든 것을 난파시킬지 모른다는 건 부정할 수 없지만, 그래도 그게 우리 시대에 일어나지는 않을 테고—게다가 그건 신사를 위한 학교거든요.\"\n\n\"그건 학생들을 거기 두느라 돈을 내는 사람들에게는 무척 위험하고, 만족스럽지 못하고, 비싼 학교일까 봐 걱정입니다.\" 클레넘이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다.\n\n\"아! 당신은 무서운 분이로군요.\" 가우언이 가볍게 대꾸했다. \"당신이 그 작은 당나귀 클래런스를, 그 더없이 존경할 만한 얼간이를(전 그를 정말 사랑합니다) 거의 넋이 나가도록 어떻게 겁주었는지 알 만합니다. 하지만 그 사람 이야기도, 나머지 모든 사람 이야기도 그만하지요. 저희 어머니께 당신을 소개하고 싶습니다, 클레넘 씨. 부디 그 기회를 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주세요.\"\n\n그 누구의 것도 아닌 정신 상태에서, 클레넘이 그보다 덜 바라거나, 그것을 어떻게 피할지 더 막막해할 일은 없었다.\n\n\"저희 어머니는 햄프턴 코트의 저 음산한 붉은 벽돌 감옥 같은 곳에서 더없이 원시적인 방식으로 사십니다.\" 가우언이 말했다. \"당신이 직접 약속을 정하시고, 제가 당신을 그곳으로 저녁식사에 모셔갈 날을 직접 골라 주신다면, 당신은 지루해하시겠지만 어머니는 기뻐하실 겁니다. 정말이지 그게 사정입니다.\"\n\n이런 말을 듣고서 클레넘이 무어라 할 수 있었겠는가? 그의 내성적인 성격에는 가장 좋은 의미에서 소박한 면이 많았는데, 그것은 단련되지 않고 익숙하지 않은 데서 온 것이었다. 그리고 그 소박함과 겸손함 속에서 그는 가우언 씨의 뜻에 자신을 맡기게 되어 기쁘다고밖에 말할 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 그렇게 말했고, 날짜가 정해졌다. 그리고 그것은 그로서는 두려운 날이었으며, 막상 그날이 와서 둘이 함께 햄프턴 코트로 내려갈 때는 더없이 달갑지 않은 날이었다.\n\n그 유서 깊은 건물군의 유서 깊은 거주자들은 그 무렵 마치 일종의 문명화된 집시처럼 거기에 야영하고 있는 듯 보였다. 그들의 거처에는 임시변통의 분위기가 감돌았는데, 마치 무언가 더 나은 것을 얻을 수 있는 순간 곧장 떠나갈 것 같았다. 또한 그들 자신에게는 불만에 찬 분위기가 있었는데, 마치 이미 훨씬 더 나은 무언가를 얻지 못한 것을 몹시 못마땅해하는 듯했다. 점잖은 가림막과 임시변통들이 문이 열리자마자 어느 정도 눈에 띄었다. 절반도 채 안 되게 낮아서 아치형 통로를 식당으로 만들어 버리는 칸막이며, 시동(侍童)들이 밤에 나이프와 포크 사이에 머리를 처박고 잠드는 어둑한 구석을 가리는 가림막이며, 아무것도 감추지 않는다고 믿어 달라고 청하는 커튼이며, 자기를 보지 말아 달라고 부탁하는 유리창이며, 여러 형태로 자신들의 떳떳치 못한 비밀, 곧 침대와는 아무 관련이 없는 척하는 갖가지 물건들이며, 분명 석탄 창고인데 벽에 위장한 함정 문이며, 명백히 작은 부엌으로 통하는 문인데 막다른 길인 척하는 것들이 그것이었다. 마음속 유보와 교묘한 비밀들이 이런 것들에서 자라났다. 방문객들은 맞이하는 이의 눈을 빤히 바라보며 석 자 떨어진 곳에서 나는 요리 냄새를 못 맡는 척했고, 우연히 열려 있는 벽장과 마주친 사람들은 술병을 못 본 척했으며, 얇은 천 칸막이에 머리를 기대고 있고 그 너머에서 시동과 젊은 여자가 큰소리로 다투고 있는 방문객들은 태곳적의 침묵 속에 앉아 있는 척했다. 이런 종류의, 점잖은 집시들이 서로에게 끊임없이 발행하고 또 받아 주는 사소한 사교적 융통어음에는 끝이 없었다.\n\n이 보헤미안들 가운데 일부는 짜증을 잘 내는 기질이었는데, 두 가지 정신적 시련으로 끊임없이 마음이 시고 짜증이 나 있었다. 첫째는 대중에게서 결코 충분히 뜯어내지 못했다는 자각이었고, 둘째는 대중이 이 건물 안으로 들여보내진다는 자각이었다. 후자의 큰 부당함 아래서 몇몇은 끔찍하게 괴로워했다—특히 일요일이면, 그들은 한동안 땅이 갈라져 대중을 삼켜 버리기를 기대해 왔는데, 우주의 운영에 있어 어떤 비난받아 마땅한 해이함 탓에 그 바람직한 사건은 아직 일어나지 않았던 것이다.\n\n가우언 부인의 집 문은 여러 해 근무한 집안 하인이 맡고 있었는데, 그는 한동안 고대해 온 우체국 자리를 두고 대중에게 따질 자기 나름의 원한이 있었으나 아직 그 자리에 임명되지 못했다. 그는 대중이 결코 자기를 거기 넣어 줄 수 없었으리라는 것을 완벽히 알고 있었지만, 대중이 자기를 막고 있다는 생각으로 음울하게 스스로를 만족시켰다. 이 억울함의 영향(그리고 어쩌면 임금 문제에서의 약간의 궁핍과 불규칙함의 영향)으로, 그는 몸가짐에 무심해지고 마음이 뚱해졌다. 그리고 이제 클레넘에게서 자기를 억압하는 그 천한 무리 중 하나를 알아보고는 그를 모욕적으로 맞이했다.\n\n그러나 가우언 부인은 그를 거들먹거리며 맞이했다. 그가 보기에 그녀는 한때 미인이었던 궁정풍의 노부인으로, 콧등의 분과 두 눈 밑의 어떤 있을 수 없는 홍조를 생략해도 될 만큼 여전히 충분히 용모가 고왔다. 그녀는 그에게 약간 거만했다. 짙은 눈썹에 코가 높은 또 다른 노부인도 그러했는데, 그녀에게는 무언가 진짜인 것이 있었음이 분명했다. 안 그랬다면 존재할 수도 없었을 테니까. 하지만 그것은 분명 그녀의 머리카락도, 치아도, 몸매도, 안색도 아니었다. 위엄 있고 뚱한 외모의 백발 노신사도 그러했다. 두 사람 다 만찬에 와 있었다. 그러나 그들 모두 지구의 여러 곳에서 영국 대사관 일을 해 왔고, 영국 대사관이란 자기 동포를 한없는 경멸로 대하는 것보다 더 확실히 에두름국에서 그 평판을 세우는 길이 없으므로(안 그러면 다른 나라 대사관들처럼 되어 버릴 테니까), 클레넘은 대체로 그들이 자기를 가볍게 봐주었다고 느꼈다.\n\n그 위엄 있는 노신사는 랭커스터 스틸트스토킹 경으로 밝혀졌는데, 그는 여러 해 동안 에두름국의 비용으로 해외에서 영국 폐하의 대리인 노릇을 해 온 사람이었다. 이 고귀한 냉장고는 당대에 여러 유럽 궁정을 얼려 놓았고, 어찌나 완벽한 성공을 거두었던지, 사반세기의 거리를 두고 그를 기억하는 영광을 누린 외국인들의 위장에 영국인이라는 이름 자체가 여전히 냉기를 끼얹을 정도였다.\n\n그는 이제 은퇴해 있었고, 따라서 (뻣뻣한 눈더미 같은 묵직한 흰 크라바트를 매고) 친절하게도 만찬에 그늘을 드리워 주었다. 유랑하는 듯한 시중과 그 묘하게 잡다한 종류의 접시며 그릇에는 만연한 보헤미안적 기질의 기미가 속삭이고 있었다. 그러나 은쟁반이나 도자기보다 한없이 나은 그 고귀한 냉장고가 그것을 더없이 훌륭하게 만들어 주었다. 그는 만찬에 그늘을 드리우고, 포도주를 식히고, 그레이비를 차갑게 만들고, 채소를 시들게 했다.\n\n방 안에는 단 한 사람이 더 있었다. 현미경으로 봐야 할 만큼 작은 시동이었는데, 우체국에 들어가지 못한 그 악의에 찬 사내의 시중을 들고 있었다. 이 젊은이조차도, 그의 윗도리 단추를 풀고 그 마음을 드러내 보일 수 있었다면, 배너클 가문의 먼 추종자로서, 이미 정부 산하의 자리를 열망하고 있음이 드러났을 것이다.\n\n가우언 부인은, 아들이 인정받는 배너클로서 그 타고난 권리를 주장하며 대중의 코에 코뚜레를 꿰는 대신, 천한 예술의 추종자로서 그 돼지 같은 대중에게 구애하는 신세로 전락한 데서 비롯된 부드러운 우수를 띤 채, 만찬 자리에서 이 험한 시절에 관한 대화를 이끌었다. 클레넘이 이 거대한 세상이 얼마나 작은 굴대들 위에서 돌아가는지를 처음으로 알게 된 것이 바로 그때였다.\n\n\"존 배너클이,\" 시대의 타락이 충분히 확인된 뒤 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"존 배너클이 군중을 회유하겠다는 그 더없이 불운한 생각만 버렸더라면, 만사가 잘되었을 것이고, 나라는 보전되었으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n코가 높은 노부인이 동의했다. 그러나 만약 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹이 일반적으로 기병대에 돌격 명령을 내려 출동시켰더라면, 나라가 보전되었으리라 생각한다고 덧붙였다.\n\n고귀한 냉장고가 동의했다. 그러나 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹이 서로에게 넘어가 그 길이 기억될 연합을 결성했을 때, 대담하게 신문에 재갈을 물리고, 어떤 편집자라는 자가 감히 국내외 임명된 권력의 행위를 논하는 것을 처벌 대상으로 삼았더라면, 나라가 보전되었으리라 생각한다고 덧붙였다.\n\n나라(배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가를 가리키는 또 다른 말)가 보전될 필요가 있다는 데는 의견이 일치했으나, 어쩌다 보전이 필요하게 되었는지는 그리 분명치 않았다. 다만 분명한 것은, 그 문제가 온통 존 배너클, 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹, 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹, 톰, 딕, 또는 해리 배너클이나 스틸트스토킹에 관한 것일 뿐이라는 점이었다. 왜냐하면 그 밖에는 군중 말고 아무도 없었으니까. 그리고 이것이 그 대화에서, 이런 데 익숙지 않은 사람인 클레넘에게 매우 거북하게 와닿은 특징이었다. 거기 앉아 거대한 한 나라가 그토록 작은 테두리로 좁혀지는 것을 잠자코 듣고 있는 것이 과연 옳은 일인지 의심스럽게 만들었던 것이다. 그러나 의회의 토론에서도, 그것이 그 나라 몸의 생명에 관한 것이든 영혼의 생명에 관한 것이든, 그 문제가 대개 온통 존 배너클, 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹, 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹, 톰, 딕, 또는 해리 배너클이나 스틸트스토킹에 관한 것이고 그들 사이의 일일 뿐 아무도 없었음을 떠올리며, 그는 군중을 대변하여 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 군중은 이런 데 익숙하다고 스스로 생각하면서.\n\n헨리 가우언 씨는 그 세 명의 화자를 서로 맞붙여 놓고, 그들이 하는 말에 클레넘이 놀라는 것을 보는 데서 짓궂은 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다. 그는 자기를 내쳐 버린 계급에 대해서도, 자기를 받아들이지 않은 계급에 대해서도 한결같이 지극한 경멸을 품고 있었으므로, 오가는 어떤 말에도 개인적으로 동요가 없었다. 그의 건강한 정신 상태는, 그 훌륭한 사람들 가운데서 클레넘이 처한 당혹과 고립의 처지에서 오히려 만족을 얻는 듯하기까지 했다. 만약 클레넘이 '그 누구'가 끊임없이 싸우고 있던 그 상태에 있었더라면, 그는 그것을 눈치챘을 것이고, 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안에도 그러한 의심을 비열한 것으로 여겨 그와 씨름했을 것이다.\n\n두어 시간이 지나는 동안 그 고귀한 냉장고는, 언제나 그 시대보다 백 년은 뒤처져 있던 사람이, 어느덧 오백 년쯤 뒤처지게 되어, 그 시대에 어울리는 엄숙한 정치적 신탁을 늘어놓았다. 그는 마침내 자기가 마실 차 한 잔을 얼려 놓고는, 가장 낮은 온도로 물러갔다.\n\n그러자, 자기 곁에 비어 있는 안락의자를 두고 거기에 위엄을 불러 앉히던 시절에, 헌신적인 노예들을 하나씩 짧은 알현으로 불러들여 특별한 총애의 표시로 삼는 데 익숙해 있던 가우언 부인이, 부채를 한 번 돌려 클레넘에게 어전(御前)으로 다가오라고 청했다. 그는 따랐고, 방금 랭커스터 스틸트스토킹 경이 비운 그 삼각의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"이 가증스럽도록 불편한 곳—그저 막사일 뿐인 데서나마—당신을 알게 되어 기쁜 마음과는 별개로, 제가 당신께 몹시 이야기하고 싶은 주제가 하나 있습니다. 제 아들이 처음으로 당신과 교분을 쌓는 즐거움을 누리게 된 것도, 제 생각엔 바로 그 주제와 관련해서였지요.\"\n\n클레넘은, 아직 완전히 이해하지 못한 것에 대한 대체로 적절한 대답으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"우선,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"그래, 그 애가 정말 예쁜가요?\"\n\n그 누구의 곤경에 처해서도 그는 대답하기가 무척 어려웠을 것이다. 미소를 지으며 \"누구 말씀이십니까?\" 하고 말하기란 정말이지 무척 어려웠을 것이다.\n\n\"오! 아시잖아요!\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"헨리의 그 불꽃 말이에요. 그 불운한 연정 말입니다. 거 봐요! 제가 그 이름을 먼저 꺼내는 것이 명예가 걸린 문제라면—미클스 양—미글스요.\"\n\n\"미글스 양은,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"매우 아름답습니다.\"\n\n\"남자들은 그런 점에서 어찌나 자주 잘못 보는지요.\" 가우언 부인이 고개를 가로저으며 대꾸했다. \"그래서 솔직히 당신께 고백하건대, 저는 지금도 도무지 확신이 서질 않습니다. 헨리가 그토록 진지하고 힘주어 뒷받침해 주는 것만으로도 다행이긴 합니다만. 그 애가 로마에서 그 사람들을 주워 온 거지요, 아마?\"\n\n그 표현은 어느 누구에게도 끔찍한 모욕이 되지는 않았을 것이다. 클레넘이 대답했다. \"죄송합니다만, 그 표현을 제가 제대로 이해했는지 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람들을 주워 왔다고요.\" 가우언 부인이 (손가리개로 쓰는 커다란 초록 부채인) 접은 부채의 살을 자기 작은 탁자에 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"마주쳤다는 거지요. 찾아냈다고요. 우연히 부딪쳤다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 사람들이요?\"\n\n\"그래요. 그 미글스네 사람들 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제 친구 미글스 씨가 어디서 처음으로 헨리 가우언 씨를 자기 딸에게 소개했는지는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"정말 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 로마에서 그 애를 주워 왔을 거예요. 하지만 어디든 상관없지요—어딘가에서겠죠. 자 그럼(이건 전적으로 우리끼리만의 얘깁니다), 그 애가 아주 천민 출신인가요?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 부인.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"저 자신이 의심할 바 없이 천민이라서, 그것을 판단할 자격이 있다고 느껴지지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"아주 깔끔하군요!\" 가우언 부인이 가리개를 차갑게 펼치며 말했다. \"아주 능란해요! 그 말에서 저는, 당신이 속으로는 그 애의 태도가 그 외모에 걸맞다고 생각한다고 추론합니다만?\"\n\n클레넘은 한순간 뻣뻣해진 뒤 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"그것 참 위안이 되는군요. 당신 말이 맞기를 바랍니다. 헨리가 당신더러 그 사람들과 함께 여행했다고 하던가요?\"\n\n\"제 친구 미글스 씨와, 그분 부인과 따님과 함께 몇 달 동안 여행했습니다.\" (그 누구의 가슴도 그 기억에 쥐어짜였을지 모른다.)\n\n\"정말 위안이 되네요. 그렇다면 당신은 그들에 대해 폭넓은 경험을 했겠군요. 보세요, 클레넘 씨, 이 일이 오래도록 계속되고 있는데, 저는 거기서 아무런 진전도 보지 못하겠어요. 그러니 그 일에 관해 당신처럼 잘 아는 분께 이야기할 기회를 얻는 것은 제게 엄청난 위안입니다. 정말 큰 은혜예요. 정말 축복이고말고요.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"하지만 저는 헨리 가우언 씨의 속내를 아는 사람이 아닙니다. 부인이 생각하시는 만큼 잘 알고 있는 것과는 거리가 멉니다. 부인의 오해가 제 처지를 매우 곤란하게 만드는군요. 이 주제에 관해 헨리 가우언 씨와 저 사이에 단 한마디도 오간 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n가우언 부인은 방 저쪽 끝을 흘끗 보았다. 거기서는 그녀의 아들이 소파에 앉아, 기병대 돌격을 주장하던 그 노부인과 에카르테 놀이를 하고 있었다.\n\n\"그 애의 속내를 모른다고요? 그렇죠.\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"두 분 사이에 한마디도 오가지 않았다고요? 그렇겠죠. 그건 짐작이 갑니다. 하지만 말로 표현되지 않는 속내라는 것도 있지요, 클레넘 씨. 그리고 당신이 그 사람들 틈에서 그토록 친밀하게 지내 왔으니, 지금 이 경우에 그런 종류의 속내가 존재한다는 것을 저는 의심할 수가 없어요. 어쩌면 들으셨을지도 모르겠지만, 저는 헨리가 어떤 일에 손을 댔다는 것에—저런!\" 어깨를 으쓱하며. \"꽤 점잖은 일이긴 하겠지요, 그리고 어떤 예술가들은 예술가로서 꽤 우월한 사람들이긴 합니다. 그래도 우리 집안에서는 아직껏 아마추어 이상으로 나아간 적이 없으니, 조금쯤 마음 쓰이는 것도 용서받을 만한 약점이지요—\"\n\n가우언 부인이 한숨을 쉬느라 말을 끊는 동안, 클레넘은 아무리 너그러워지려고 굳게 마음먹고 있어도, 이 집안이 지금 이대로라도 아마추어 이상으로 나아갈 위험은 지극히 적다는 생각을 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n\"헨리는,\" 어머니가 다시 말을 이었다. \"고집스럽고 단호하답니다. 그리고 이 사람들이 자연히 온갖 수를 다 써서 그 애를 낚으려 드니, 클레넘 씨, 저는 이 일이 깨어지리라는 희망을 거의 품을 수가 없어요. 그 처녀의 재산은 매우 적을 듯합니다. 헨리라면 훨씬 더 나은 혼처를 구할 수 있었을 텐데요. 그런 연줄을 보상해 줄 만한 것이 거의 없습니다. 그래도 그 애는 제 뜻대로 하고 있으니, 머지않아 아무 진전이 없다면, 저로서는 체념하고 이 사람들을 받아들여 최선을 다하는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없겠지요. 당신이 제게 말씀해 주신 것에 대해 무한히 감사드립니다.\"\n\n그녀가 어깨를 으쓱하자, 클레넘은 다시 뻣뻣하게 고개를 숙였다. 그러고는 얼굴에 거북한 홍조를 띠고 머뭇거리는 태도로, 지금까지 취해 온 것보다 한층 더 낮은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"가우언 부인, 제가 의무라고 느끼는 일을 어떻게 다해야 할지 도무지 모르겠습니다. 그래도 그것을 이행하려 애쓰는 데 부인의 너그러운 헤아림을 청하지 않을 수 없습니다. 부인 편에서의 어떤 오해가, 감히 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 매우 큰 오해가 바로잡힐 필요가 있는 듯합니다. 부인은 미글스 씨와 그 가족이 온갖 수를 다 쓴다고, 그렇게 말씀하신 것 같은데요—\"\n\n\"온갖 수를요.\" 가우언 부인이, 얼굴과 난롯불 사이에 초록 부채를 든 채, 차분한 고집으로 그를 바라보며 되풀이했다.\n\n\"헨리 가우언 씨를 차지하려고요?\"\n\n부인이 차분하게 동의했다.\n\n\"그런데 그건 결코 사실이 아닙니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"오히려 저는 미글스 씨가 이 일로 불행해하고 있으며, 이를 끝내고자 하는 바람에서 온갖 합당한 장애물을 끼워 놓았다는 것을 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n가우언 부인은 자기 커다란 초록 부채를 접어, 그것으로 그의 팔을 톡 치고는, 미소 짓는 자기 입술을 톡 쳤다. \"그야, 물론이지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"바로 제 말이 그거예요.\"\n\n아서는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 설명을 들으려고 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈다.\n\n\"정말 진심이세요, 클레넘 씨? 모르시겠어요?\"\n\n아서는 모르겠다고, 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그야, 제가 제 아들을 모르겠어요? 그리고 그게 바로 그 애를 붙들어 두는 방법이라는 걸 제가 모르겠어요?\" 가우언 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그리고 이 미글스네 사람들이 그걸, 적어도 저만큼은 모르겠어요? 오, 약삭빠른 사람들이지요, 클레넘 씨. 보아하니 장사꾼들이에요! 미글스는 어느 은행에 몸담았던 것 같던데요. 그가 그 경영에 많이 관여했더라면, 무척 이문이 많은 은행이었겠지요. 이거 정말이지 아주 잘 꾸민 일이에요.\"\n\n\"제발, 간청드립니다, 부인—\" 아서가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"오, 클레넘 씨, 정말 그렇게 잘 속아 넘어가실 수가 있나요?\"\n\n그녀가 이런 거만한 어조로 말하는 것을 듣고, 경멸 어린 입술을 부채로 톡톡 두드리는 것을 보는 일이 어찌나 고통스러운 인상을 주었던지, 그는 매우 간곡하게 말했다. \"믿어 주십시오, 부인, 이건 부당한, 완전히 근거 없는 의심입니다.\"\n\n\"의심이라고요?\" 가우언 부인이 되풀이했다. \"의심이 아니에요, 클레넘 씨, 확신입니다. 정말이지 아주 영리하게 꾸민 일이고, 당신을 완전히 속여 넘긴 모양이네요.\" 그녀는 웃었다. 그러고는 다시 부채로 입술을 톡톡 두드리며 고개를 까딱이고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 이렇게 덧붙이는 듯했다. \"내게 그런 말 마세요. 그런 사람들은 그런 혼인의 명예를 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 하리라는 걸 나는 압니다.\"\n\n이 알맞은 순간에 카드가 던져졌고, 헨리 가우언 씨가 방을 가로질러 오며 말했다. \"어머니, 이번엔 클레넘 씨를 놓아주실 수 있으시면, 저희가 갈 길이 멀고 시간도 늦어 가고 있어서요.\" 그러자 클레넘은 달리 도리가 없었으므로 일어섰다. 그리고 가우언 부인은 끝까지 그에게 똑같은 눈빛과, 똑같이 톡톡 두드린 경멸 어린 입술을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"어머니와 어마어마하게 긴 알현을 하셨군요.\" 문이 그들 뒤에서 닫히자 가우언이 말했다. \"부디 어머니가 당신을 지루하게 하지 않으셨기를 간절히 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n여행을 위해 그들에게는 작은 무개 사륜마차가 있었고, 곧 그것에 올라 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다. 가우언은 마차를 몰면서 시가에 불을 붙였다. 클레넘은 한 대 권하는 것을 사양했다. 그는 아무리 애를 써도 그런 골똘한 사색의 기분에 빠져들어, 가우언이 다시 말했다. \"아무래도 어머니가 당신을 지루하게 하신 게 무척 걱정스럽군요?\" 이에 그는 정신을 차리고 대답했다. \"전혀 아닙니다!\" 그러고는 곧 다시 사색에 빠져들었다.\n\n그 누구도 불안하게 하지 않는 그 정신 상태에서라면, 그의 골똘한 생각은 주로 자기 곁에 있는 사내에게로 향했을 것이다. 그는 그가 발뒤꿈치로 돌을 파헤치던 것을 처음 본 그 아침을 떠올렸을 것이고, 스스로에게 물었을 것이다. \"저 사람이 나도 저렇게 무심하고 잔인하게 길에서 걷어차 내치는 것일까?\" 그는 생각했을 것이다. 그가 어머니가 무슨 말을 할지 알았기에, 그리하여 자기는 한마디 속내도 털어놓지 않은 채 연적 앞에 자기 처지를 펼쳐 보이고 거만하게 물러나라고 경고할 수 있었기에, 어머니와의 이 만남을 일부러 주선한 것은 아닐까 하고. 그는 생각했을 것이다. 설령 그런 의도까지는 아니었다 하더라도, 자기 억눌린 감정을 가지고 놀고 자기를 괴롭히려고 그를 여기로 데려온 것은 아닐까 하고. 이런 명상의 흐름은 때때로 한바탕 밀려드는 부끄러움에 가로막혔을 것이다. 그 부끄러움은 그 자신의 솔직한 천성에서 비롯된 항변을 그에게 전해 주며, 그런 의심을, 스쳐 가는 한순간일지라도 품는 것은, 그가 지키기로 결심한 그 높고 시샘 없는 길을 가는 것이 아니라고 일러 주었을 것이다. 그럴 때면 그의 안에서 벌어지는 분투가 가장 격렬했을 것이고, 고개를 들어 가우언의 눈과 마주치면, 마치 그에게 잘못이라도 저지른 듯 흠칫 놀랐을 것이다.\n\n그러다 어두운 길과 그 위의 어렴풋한 사물들을 바라보며, 그는 차츰 다시 생각에 잠겨 들었을 것이다. \"이 사람과 나는, 인생이라는 더 어두운 길 위에서 대체 어디로 마차를 몰아가고 있는 걸까? 그 흐릿한 먼 곳에서, 우리는, 그리고 그녀는 어떻게 될까?\" 그녀를 생각하면, 그는 새삼 자책 어린 불안에 시달렸을 것이다. 그를 싫어하는 것은 그녀에게조차 떳떳하지 못한 일이며, 그를 향해 그토록 쉽게 편견을 품는 것은 자기가 처음보다 그녀에게 덜 합당한 사람임을 뜻한다는 불안에.\n\n\"확실히 기분이 가라앉으셨군요.\" 가우언이 말했다. \"아무래도 어머니가 당신을 끔찍이도 지루하게 하신 게 틀림없어 무척 걱정입니다.\"\n\n\"믿어 주세요, 전혀 아닙니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"아무것도 아닙니다—아무것도요!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 27. Five-and-Twenty\n\n팬크스 씨가 도릿 가족에 관한 정보를 수집하려는 욕망이, 그가 오랜 유배에서 돌아온 뒤 어머니에게 털어놓았던 불안과 어떤 식으로든 연관될 수 있지 않을까 하는 의심이 자꾸만 되살아나면서, 이 무렵 아서 클레넘은 적잖은 불편을 느꼈다. 팬크스 씨가 도릿 가족에 대해 이미 알고 있는 것이 무엇인지, 더 알아내고자 하는 것이 정말로 무엇인지, 그리고 대체 무엇 때문에 그 바쁜 머리를 그들 일로 괴롭히는지 하는 의문들이 그를 자주 당혹스럽게 했다. 팬크스 씨는 한가한 호기심에서 비롯된 탐색에 자기 시간과 수고를 허비할 사람이 아니었다. 그에게 특정한 목적이 있다는 것을 클레넘은 의심할 수 없었다. 그리고 팬크스 씨가 그 부지런함으로 그 목적을 달성하는 과정에서, 어머니로 하여금 리틀 도릿에게 손을 내밀게 만든 비밀스러운 이유가 어느 때 아닌 때에 드러나게 되지는 않을까 하는 것은 심각한 추측거리였다.\n\n그렇다고 해서, 만일 잘못이 드러나고 그것을 바로잡을 수 있다면 아버지 시절에 저질러진 잘못을 바로잡겠다는 그의 바람이나 결심이 흔들렸던 것은 결코 아니었다. 아버지가 죽은 이래로 그의 머리 위에 드리워 있던, 부당한 행위가 있었으리라는 그 추정의 그림자는 너무도 어렴풋하고 형체가 없어서, 그가 상상하는 것과는 한참 동떨어진 실제 사실의 결과일 수도 있었다. 그러나 만일 그의 우려가 근거 있는 것으로 밝혀진다면, 그는 언제라도 자신이 가진 모든 것을 내려놓고 세상을 새로 시작할 각오가 되어 있었다. 어린 시절의 그 사납고 어두운 가르침이 결코 그의 가슴에 스며들지 않았던 것과 마찬가지로, 그의 도덕률의 첫째 조항은, 그가 실천적인 겸손함으로 시작해야 한다는 것, 곧 이 땅 위에서 제 발밑을 잘 살피는 일에서 시작해야 하며, 결코 말의 날개를 타고 천국으로 날아오를 수는 없다는 것이었다. 이 땅에서의 의무, 이 땅에서의 보상, 이 땅에서의 행동, 이것들이 먼저였다. 위로 오르는 그 가파른 첫 계단들이었다. 그 문은 좁고 그 길은 협착하였다. 헛된 신앙고백과 헛된 반복, 남의 눈 속의 티끌과 남을 심판에 내어주는 너그러움으로 포장된 그 넓고 큰 길보다 훨씬 더 좁고 더 협착하였으니, 그 길은 죄다 값이라곤 전혀 들지 않는 싸구려 재료로 만들어진 것이었다.\n\n아니었다. 그를 불안하게 만든 것은 이기적인 두려움이나 망설임이 아니라, 팬크스가 둘 사이의 합의에서 자기 몫을 지키지 않고, 무언가를 알아낸 끝에 그것을 자기에게 알리지도 않은 채 어떤 행동을 취하지나 않을까 하는 불신이었다. 한편으로, 팬크스와 나눈 대화를 떠올리고, 그 기묘한 인물이 도대체 그 자취를 좇고 있을 가능성이 있다고 짐작할 만한 근거가 거의 없음을 생각하면, 자기가 이 일을 너무 크게 부풀리고 있는 것이 아닌가 싶은 때도 있었다. 모든 배가 거센 맞물결 속에서 그러하듯 이 바다에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 그는 이리저리 흔들리기만 할 뿐 어떤 항구에도 닿지 못했다.\n\n리틀 도릿 자신이 그들의 익숙한 교제에서 멀어진 것도 사정을 나아지게 하지는 않았다. 그녀는 어찌나 자주 밖에 나가 있고, 또 어찌나 자주 제 방에만 틀어박혀 있던지, 그는 그녀가 그리워지기 시작했고 그녀의 자리에 빈 구멍이 느껴졌다. 그는 그녀에게 좀 나아졌는지 묻는 편지를 썼고, 그녀는 자기는 아주 잘 지내니 자기 때문에 마음 쓰지 말라고 무척 고맙고도 간곡하게 답장을 보내왔다. 그러나 그는, 그들의 교류로 치자면 오랜 동안, 그녀를 보지 못했다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 그는 그녀의 아버지를 만나고 집으로 돌아왔는데, 그 아버지는 그녀가 방문을 나갔다고 했다. 그녀가 아버지의 저녁거리를 마련하느라 고되게 일하고 있을 때면 그가 늘 하는 말이었다. 그런데 돌아와 보니 미글스 씨가 흥분한 상태로 그의 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 그가 문을 열자 미글스 씨는 걸음을 멈추고 휙 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"클레넘!—태티코럼이!\"\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"사라졌소!\"\n\n\"아니, 세상에나!\" 클레넘이 놀라서 외쳤다. \"그게 무슨 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"스물다섯을 세려고 하지 않았소, 여보게. 도무지 시키질 못했지. 여덟에서 멈추더니, 떠나 버렸소.\"\n\n\"댁을 나갔다고요?\"\n\n\"다시는 돌아오지 않을 작정으로 말이오.\" 미글스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"자네는 그 아이의 격정적이고 자존심 강한 성격을 모를 거요. 이젠 말 한 무리로도 그 애를 끌고 올 수 없을 거고, 옛 바스티유의 빗장과 쇠창살로도 그 애를 붙들어 둘 수 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐습니까? 부디 앉으셔서 말씀해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지를 말로 하기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니라오. 가엾고 충동적인 그 아이의 그 불행한 기질을 자네가 먼저 헤아려야만 온전히 이해할 수 있을 테니까. 하지만 일은 이런 식으로 벌어졌소. 펫과 어멈과 나는 요즘 들어 셋이 꽤나 많은 이야기를 나누고 있었소. 클레넘, 그 대화들이 내가 바랄 만큼 밝은 종류는 아니었음을 자네에게 숨기지 않으리다. 다시 떠나는 문제에 관한 이야기였으니까. 그렇게 하자고 제안한 데에는, 사실, 내게 한 가지 목적이 있었소.\"\n\n누구의 가슴도 빠르게 뛰지 않았다.\n\n\"한 가지 목적이오.\" 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"클레넘, 그것 역시 자네에게 숨기지 않으리다. 내 사랑하는 아이 쪽에 마음이 기우는 데가 있는데, 나는 그게 안타깝다오. 아마 그 사람이 누군지 짐작이 갈 거요. 헨리 가우언이오.\"\n\n\"저도 그 말씀을 들을 마음의 준비가 없지는 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"허!\" 미글스 씨가 무거운 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"자네가 그런 말을 들을 일이 아예 없었더라면 얼마나 좋았겠나. 하지만 사정이 그렇소. 어멈과 나는 그걸 이겨 내려고 할 수 있는 건 다 해 봤소, 클레넘. 다정한 충고도 해 봤고, 시간에도 맡겨 봤고, 떼어 놓아도 봤지. 아직껏 아무 소용이 없소. 요즈음 나눈 대화는, 적어도 한 해는 더 멀리 떠나 있자는 이야기였소. 그 기간만큼은 완전히 떼어 놓고 인연을 끊어 보자는 거였지. 그 문제로 펫이 괴로워했고, 그러니 어멈과 나도 괴로웠소.\"\n\n클레넘은 충분히 믿을 만한 일이라고 말했다.\n\n\"허!\" 미글스 씨가 변명하는 투로 말을 이었다. \"현실적인 사람으로서 나는 인정하겠소, 어멈도 현실적인 여인으로서 인정할 게 분명한데, 우리네 식구들이란 자기네 골칫거리를 부풀려서 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 산으로 만들어 버리는 법이오. 곁에서 지켜보는 사람들에게는 — 그러니까 한낱 바깥 사람들에게는 말일세, 클레넘 — 꽤나 진력나게 굴 정도로 말이오. 그래도 펫의 행복이냐 불행이냐 하는 건 우리에게 그야말로 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제라, 우리가 그걸 크게 여기는 건 부디 너그러이 봐주었으면 하오. 어쨌든 그쯤은 태티코럼이 견딜 수 있었어야 했소. 자, 자네도 그렇게 생각하지 않나?\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 클레넘이 그 지극히 온당한 기대에 강하게 동의하며 대답했다.\n\n\"아니었소, 여보게.\" 미글스 씨가 서글피 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 애는 그걸 견디지 못했소. 그 아이가 제 가슴속에서 부글거리고 타오르며, 닳고 찢기던 그 모습이 어찌나 심했던지, 나는 지나치면서 몇 번이고 가만히 일러 주곤 했소. '스물다섯이다, 태티코럼, 스물다섯!' 그 애가 밤이고 낮이고 스물다섯을 계속 셀 수 있었더라면 진심으로 좋았을 것을. 그랬다면 이런 일은 없었을 텐데.\"\n\n미글스 씨는, 그 흥겹고 유쾌하던 때보다 오히려 마음씨의 선함이 더욱 잘 드러나는 풀 죽은 얼굴로, 이마에서 턱까지 손으로 쓸어내리고는 다시 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"내가 어멈에게 말했소(굳이 그럴 필요도 없었지, 어멈 혼자서도 다 그렇게 생각했을 테니). 여보, 우리는 현실적인 사람들이고, 그 애의 내력을 알고 있잖소. 우리는 이 불행한 아이에게서, 이 가엾은 것이 이 세상에 나오기도 전에 그 애 어미의 가슴속에서 들끓던 무언가의 반영을 보는 거요. 우리는 그 애의 성미를 슬쩍 덮어 주고, 여보, 지금은 그걸 모른 척합시다, 언젠가 그 애가 좀 더 나은 기분일 때를 틈타 봅시다 하고. 그래서 우린 아무 말도 하지 않았소. 하지만 우리가 어떻게 하든, 일어날 일은 일어나고야 마는 것 같았소. 그 애가 어느 날 밤 사납게 터져 버렸지.\"\n\n\"어떻게, 또 왜요?\"\n\n\"자네가 만일 '왜'냐고 묻는다면,\" 미글스 씨가 그 질문에 조금 마음이 흔들려서 말했다. 그는 식구들의 처지보다 그 애의 처지를 누그러뜨리는 데 훨씬 더 마음을 쏟고 있었기 때문이다. \"내가 방금 어멈에게 한 거나 다름없는 말이라고 되풀이한 것밖에는 달리 들려줄 게 없소. '어떻게'냐고 하면, 우리는 그 애가 있는 자리에서 펫에게 잘 자라고 인사를 했고(꽤 다정하게, 그건 인정해야겠지), 그 애가 펫을 데리고 위층으로 올라갔소 — 그 애가 펫의 몸종이었던 거 기억하지? 어쩌면 펫이 몸이 좋지 않았던 터라, 그 애에게 시중을 청하면서 평소보다 조금 더 배려가 없었을지도 모르지. 하지만 내가 그렇게 말할 자격이 있는지는 모르겠소. 그 애는 늘 사려 깊고 다정했으니까.\"\n\n\"세상에서 가장 다정한 여주인이지요.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 말했다. \"자네는 그 둘이 함께 있는 걸 자주 봤지. 그런데! 이내 우리는 그 불행한 태티코럼이 큰 소리로 화내는 걸 들었고, 무슨 일인지 미처 물어볼 새도 없이 펫이 부들부들 떨며 돌아와서는, 그 애가 무섭다고 했소. 펫 바로 뒤에 태티코럼이 활활 성을 내며 따라 들어왔소. '난 세 분 다 미워요.' 그 애가 우리를 향해 발을 구르며 말하더군. '이 집 전체가 미워서 터질 것 같아요.'\"\n\n\"그래서 어르신께서는—?\"\n\n\"내가?\" 미글스 씨가, 가우언 부인 자신마저도 믿게 만들 만한 그 꾸밈없는 진심으로 말했다. \"스물다섯을 세거라, 태티코럼, 하고 말했소.\"\n\n미글스 씨는 깊은 뉘우침의 기색으로 다시 얼굴을 쓸어내리고 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그 애는 그게 어찌나 몸에 뱄던지, 클레넘, 그렇게나 격정에 휩싸인 모습은 자네도 본 적이 없을 정도였는데, 바로 그 순간에도 딱 멈추고는 내 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 (내가 알아듣기로는) 여덟까지 세더군. 하지만 더는 자제할 수가 없었던 거요. 거기서 무너져 버렸소, 가엾은 것이. 나머지 열일곱은 사방으로 흩어 버렸지. 그러고는 모든 게 터져 나왔소. 우리가 밉다, 우리와 있으면 비참하다, 견딜 수 없다, 견디지 않겠다, 떠나기로 마음먹었다고. 자기는 어린 여주인보다 어린데, 늘 그 여주인만이 젊고 흥미로운 유일한 존재라며 아끼고 사랑받는 걸 두고만 봐야 하느냐고. 싫다고. 그러지 않겠다, 않겠다, 않겠다고! 우리가 어린 여주인처럼 자기를 어린 시절에 어루만지고 보살펴 주었다면, 자기 태티코럼은 어떤 사람이 되었을 것 같으냐고. 그 여주인만큼은 됐을 거라고? 아! 어쩌면 쉰 배는 더 나은 사람이 됐을 거라고. 우리가 서로 그토록 아끼는 척할 때, 우리는 자기를 두고 우쭐댄 거라고. 그게 우리가 한 짓이라고. 우리가 자기를 두고 우쭐대며 부끄럽게 만들었다고. 그리고 집안 사람들이 다 똑같이 그랬다고. 다들 자기네 아비 어미, 형제자매 얘기를 떠들어 댔고, 자기 앞에서 일부러 그들을 끄집어내는 걸 좋아했다고. 바로 어제도 티킷 부인이, 어린 손주가 와 있을 때, 그 아이가 우리가 자기한테 붙여 준 그 형편없는 이름으로 자기(태티코럼)를 부르려고 애쓰는 걸 재미있어하며 그 이름을 두고 웃었다고. 아니, 안 웃은 사람이 누가 있느냐고. 우리가 대체 뭐기에 자기를 개나 고양이처럼 이름 붙일 권리가 있느냐고. 하지만 자기는 상관없다고. 더는 우리한테서 아무 은혜도 받지 않겠다고. 그 이름을 우리한테 도로 내던지고 떠나겠다고. 바로 그 순간 우리를 떠나겠다, 아무도 못 막는다, 다시는 자기 소식을 듣지 못할 거라고 했소.\"\n\n미글스 씨는 이 모든 것을 그 원래 광경을 어찌나 생생히 떠올리며 읊었던지, 이쯤 되자 자기가 묘사한 그 애만큼이나 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올라 있었다.\n\n\"아, 허!\" 그가 얼굴을 닦으며 말했다. \"그 격하게 헐떡이는 아이를 두고(그 애 어미의 사연이 어떠했을지는 하늘만이 알 일이지) 그때 이치를 따져 봐야 아무 소용이 없었소. 그래서 나는 그 늦은 밤중에 떠나선 안 된다고 조용히 일러 주고, 손을 내밀어 그 애를 방으로 데려다준 다음 집 문들을 잠갔소. 그런데 오늘 아침에 보니 가 버린 거요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 애에 대해 더는 아는 게 없으신 거고요?\"\n\n\"더는 없소.\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다. \"온종일 사방을 뒤지고 다녔소. 아주 이른 시각에 아주 소리 없이 떠났을 거요. 우리 근방에서는 그 애 자취를 하나도 찾지 못했소.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요! 어르신께서는,\" 클레넘이 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다. \"그 애를 만나고 싶으신 거지요? 그렇게 보아도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 물론이오. 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶소. 어멈도 펫도 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶어 하오. 자!\" 미글스 씨가, 화가 날 만한 도발이 자기 쪽 일은 전혀 아니라는 듯 설득하는 투로 말했다. \"자네 자신도 그 가엾고 격정적인 아이에게 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶을 거요. 나는 다 아오, 클레넘.\"\n\n\"여러분이 그토록 너그러운데 제가 그러지 않는다면 정말 이상하고 모진 일이겠지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"제가 여쭤보려던 건, 그 웨이드 양은 생각해 보셨는지 하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"생각해 봤소. 우리 동네를 온통 훑고 다니기 전까지는 그 사람 생각을 못 했지. 집에 돌아가 보니 어멈과 펫이 태티코럼이 분명 그 사람에게 갔을 거라는 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 걸 보지 않았더라면, 그때도 그런 생각을 했을지 모르겠소. 그러고 나니 물론, 자네가 우리와 처음 함께 있던 날 만찬 자리에서 그 사람이 했던 말이 떠올랐소.\"\n\n\"웨이드 양을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 짐작 가는 데가 있으십니까?\"\n\n\"솔직히 말하자면,\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다. \"그 일에 대해 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 짐작이 있어서, 자네가 여기서 나를 기다리고 있는 걸 보게 된 거요. 우리 집에는, 가끔 집안에 신비롭게 스며드는 그런 묘한 인상 중 하나가 있소. 아무도 누구한테서 또렷한 형태로 주워들은 것 같지는 않은데, 그러면서도 다들 누군가한테서 어렴풋이 얻어 듣고는 다시 흘려버린 것 같은 그런 인상 말이오. 그 사람이 이 근처에 산다, 혹은 살고 있었다는 거요.\" 미글스 씨가 그에게 종잇조각 하나를 건넸는데, 거기에는 파크 레인 근처 그로스베너 구역의 칙칙한 뒷골목 중 하나의 이름이 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"번지가 없군요.\" 아서가 그것을 훑어보며 말했다.\n\n\"번지가 없다고, 클레넘?\" 그의 친구가 대답했다. \"아무것도 없소! 거리 이름조차도 어쩌면 그냥 허공에 떠다니던 것일지 모르오. 내가 말했듯이, 우리 집 사람들 가운데 누구도 어디서 그걸 들었는지 말하지 못하니까. 하지만 한번 물어볼 가치는 있소. 그리고 나는 혼자보다는 누군가와 함께 알아보고 싶은데, 자네도 그 꿈쩍 않는 여인과 함께 여행한 길동무였으니, 혹시 자네가—\" 클레넘이 다시 모자를 집어 들고 준비되었다고 말하며 그 문장을 대신 마무리해 주었다.\n\n이제 한여름이었다. 잿빛에, 무덥고, 먼지 자욱한 저녁이었다. 그들은 옥스퍼드 거리 끝까지 마차로 갔다가 거기서 내려, 파크 레인 근처에 미로처럼 얽혀 있는, 우울하리만치 위엄을 부리는 큰 거리들과, 그만큼 위엄을 부리려 애쓰지만 결국 더 우울해지고만 작은 거리들 사이로 파고들었다. 야만스럽게 낡은 주랑과 부속물을 단 모퉁이 집들의 황무지. 어느 그릇된 시대에 어느 그릇된 사람의 손으로 생겨난 그 흉물들은, 무너져 내릴 때까지 그러기로 작정한 듯, 뒤따르는 모든 세대의 맹목적인 찬탄을 여전히 요구하며 황혼을 노려보고 있었다. 광장에 있는 공작 각하 저택을 거대하게 본뜬 난쟁이 같은 현관문에서부터, 마구간 뒷골목의 거름더미가 내다보이는 규방의 찌부러진 창문에 이르기까지, 온통 비좁아 쥐가 난 듯한 기생충 같은 작은 셋집들이 저녁을 구슬프게 만들었다. 의심할 바 없이 유행을 따랐으되 음울한 냄새 말고는 무엇 하나 편안히 담아 둘 만한 용량이 없는 그 허청거리는 집들은, 큰 저택들이 근친끼리 거듭 번식한 끝에 나온 마지막 결과물처럼 보였다. 그 작은 부속 퇴창과 발코니가 가느다란 쇠기둥에 받쳐져 있는 곳에서는, 마치 연주창에라도 걸려 목발에 기대 쉬고 있는 것만 같았다. 여기저기 문장학의 온갖 지식이 담긴 상장(喪章)이, 마치 허영에 대해 설교하는 대주교처럼 거리를 굽어보고 있었다. 몇 안 되는 상점들은 아무런 진열도 하지 않았으니, 그들에게 대중의 평판 따위는 아무것도 아니었기 때문이다. 제과점 주인은 누가 자기 외상 장부에 올라 있는지 알았고, 그 앎에 기대어 태연할 수 있었기에, 진열창에는 유리 원통에 담긴 노부인용 박하사탕 몇 통과 골동품 같은 산딸기 젤리 대여섯 개뿐이었다. 오렌지 몇 알이 청과상이 천박한 대중에게 베푼 유일한 양보였다. 한때 물떼새 알이 담겼던 이끼로 엮은 바구니 하나가, 가금 장수가 무지렁이들에게 할 말의 전부를 담고 있었다. 그 거리의 모든 사람이(이 시각과 이 계절에는 늘 그러하듯) 만찬에 나가 버린 듯했고, 정작 그들이 찾아간 그 만찬을 베푸는 사람은 아무도 없는 것 같았다. 문간 계단에는 밝은 잡색 깃털 같은 제복에 머리에는 흰 분을 바른 종복들이, 멸종한 괴상한 새들의 종족처럼 한가로이 늘어서 있었고, 집사장들은, 다른 모든 집사장을 못 미더워하는 듯한, 은둔자 같은 태도의 고독한 사내들이었다. 공원의 마차 행렬은 하루치가 끝나 있었고, 거리의 가로등에는 불이 켜지고 있었으며, 머릿속에 든 비틀림에 걸맞게 다리도 비틀린, 몸에 꼭 끼는 옷차림의 짓궂은 어린 마부들이 둘씩 짝을 지어 지푸라기를 씹으며 음흉한 비밀을 주고받고 있었다. 마차를 따라 나서곤 하던, 그래서 화려한 마차 행렬과 어찌나 한 짝으로 여겨지던지 그것들 없이 나오는 게 마치 그 짐승들 쪽에서 격을 낮춰 주는 일처럼 보이던 점박이 개들이, 심부름하는 일꾼들을 따라 이리저리 오갔다. 여기저기, 사람들의 어깨에 받쳐질 필요가 없는, 또 제복 벗은 신사들이 그다지 필요치 않은, 한적한 선술집이 한 채씩 있었다.\n\n이 마지막 사실은 두 친구가 수소문을 이어 가던 중에 알아낸 것이었다. 그들이 찾는 거리와 관련해서, 거기서든 어디서든 웨이드 양이라는 인물에 대해 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그곳은 그 기생충 같은 거리들 가운데 하나였다. 길고, 반듯하고, 좁고, 칙칙하고, 음울한 것이, 벽돌과 회반죽으로 치른 장례 행렬 같았다. 그들은 여러 작은 지하 출입구 문에서 물어보았는데, 그곳에는 풀이 죽은 젊은이가 가파른 나무 계단의 꼭대기에 턱을 괴고 서 있었지만, 아무 정보도 얻지 못했다. 그들은 길 한쪽으로는 거리를 거슬러 올라갔다가 다른 쪽으로는 내려왔는데, 그러는 동안 두 명의 떠들썩한 신문팔이가, 결코 일어난 적도 없고 결코 일어나지도 않을 굉장한 사건을 외쳐 대며 그 은밀한 방들 안으로 쉰 목소리를 내질렀지만, 거기서도 아무것도 나오지 않았다. 마침내 그들은 출발했던 그 모퉁이에 다시 섰는데, 날은 완전히 캄캄해져 있었고, 그들은 아무것도 더 알아내지 못한 채였다.\n\n마침 그 거리에서 그들은 칙칙한 집 한 채를 여러 번 지나쳤는데, 창문마다 세놓는다는 광고지가 붙어 있는, 겉보기엔 빈집이었다. 그 광고지들은 장례 행렬 속의 별난 변화로서, 거의 장식이라 할 만했다. 어쩌면 그 집을 마음속에 따로 떼어 두고 있었기 때문인지, 아니면 미글스 씨와 그가 지나치며 두 번이나 \"저기 사는 게 아닌 건 분명하군\" 하고 의견을 같이했기 때문인지, 클레넘은 이제, 아주 떠나기 전에 돌아가서 그 집을 한번 해 보자고 제안했다. 미글스 씨가 동의했고, 그들은 되돌아갔다.\n\n그들은 한 번 문을 두드렸고, 한 번 초인종을 울렸지만, 아무 응답이 없었다. \"비었군.\" 미글스 씨가 귀를 기울이며 말했다. \"한 번 더.\" 클레넘이 말하며 다시 두드렸다. 그렇게 두드린 뒤에 그들은 아래쪽에서 무언가 움직이는 소리와, 누군가가 문 쪽으로 발을 끌며 다가오는 소리를 들었다.\n\n비좁은 입구가 너무 어두워서, 문을 연 사람이 어떤 부류인지 또렷이 알아보기란 불가능했지만, 노파인 듯했다. \"성가시게 해서 미안합니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"혹 웨이드 양이 어디 사는지 알려 주실 수 있을까요?\" 어둠 속의 목소리가 뜻밖에도 대답했다. \"여기 사오.\"\n\n\"지금 댁에 계십니까?\"\n\n아무 대답이 없자 미글스 씨가 다시 물었다. \"혹시 댁에 계신가요?\"\n\n또 한 차례 뜸을 들인 뒤, \"있을 거요.\" 그 목소리가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"들어오는 게 낫겠소, 내가 물어보리다.\"\n\n그들은 즉시 그 답답하고 컴컴한 집 안으로 들여보내졌다. 그러더니 그 형체가 부스럭부스럭 멀어지며, 더 높은 데서 말소리를 내어, \"올라오시오, 괜찮다면. 발에 걸려 넘어질 건 아무것도 없으니까\" 하고 말했다. 그들은 희미한 빛을 향해 더듬더듬 위층으로 올라갔는데, 그 빛은 알고 보니 창문을 통해 비쳐 드는 거리의 불빛이었다. 그러고는 그 형체가 그들을 공기도 통하지 않는 방에 가두어 놓고 가 버렸다.\n\n\"이거 묘하군, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"꽤 묘하긴 합니다.\" 클레넘도 같은 어조로 동의했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 찾아냈으니, 그게 중요한 점이지요. 불이 옵니다!\"\n\n그 불빛은 등잔이었고, 그것을 든 이는 노파였다. 무척 더럽고, 무척 주름지고, 메마른 노파였다. \"있다오.\" 그녀가 말했다(아까 말했던 그 목소리였다). \"곧 나올 거요.\" 등잔을 탁자 위에 내려놓고 나서, 노파는 앞치마에 손을 닦았는데, 그래 봐야 영원히 닦은들 깨끗해지지 않을 손이었다. 그러고는 흐릿한 두 눈으로 손님들을 보고는 뒷걸음질로 나갔다.\n\n그들이 만나러 온 그 여인이, 만일 그녀가 이 집의 현재 거주자라면, 마치 동방의 대상 숙소에 자리를 잡듯 이곳에 거처를 마련한 것 같았다. 방 한가운데 깔린 작은 네모 양탄자 한 장, 분명 그 방에 딸린 것이 아닌 가구 몇 점, 그리고 어지러이 널린 트렁크와 여행용 물품들이 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이었다. 이전의 어느 제대로 된 거주자 시절에는, 이 숨 막히는 작은 방에서도 체경(體鏡)과 금박을 입힌 탁자가 피어났던 모양이지만, 금박은 지난해 꽃처럼 빛이 바랬고, 거울은 어찌나 흐려졌던지 그동안 비춰 온 온갖 안개와 궂은 날씨를 마법으로 보존해 두고 있는 것만 같았다. 손님들이 한두 분쯤 주위를 둘러볼 시간이 있었을 때, 문이 열리고 웨이드 양이 들어왔다.\n\n그녀는 그들이 헤어졌을 때와 똑같았다. 똑같이 아름답고, 똑같이 경멸에 차 있고, 똑같이 억눌려 있었다. 그녀는 그들을 보고도 놀라는 기색이나 다른 어떤 감정도 드러내지 않았다. 그녀는 그들에게 앉기를 청했고, 자신은 자리에 앉기를 마다하며, 그들이 용건을 꺼내기도 전에 곧장 가로챘다.\n\n\"제가 짐작하기로는,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"두 분이 이렇게 찾아 주신 까닭을 알 것 같군요. 곧장 본론으로 들어가도 되겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 까닭이란, 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"태티코럼입니다.\"\n\n\"그러리라 생각했어요.\"\n\n\"웨이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"부디 그 애에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론이죠. 그 애가 여기 저와 함께 있다는 걸 알고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"제가 그 애를 기꺼이 데려가고 싶다는 것, 그리고 제 아내와 딸도 그 애를 기꺼이 다시 맞고 싶어 한다는 것을 알려 드리게 해 주십시오. 그 애는 우리와 오랫동안 함께 지냈습니다. 우리는 그 애가 우리에게 가진 권리를 잊지 않으며, 우리도 사정을 헤아릴 줄 안다고 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"사정을 헤아릴 줄 안다고 믿으신다고요?\" 그녀가 차분하고 절제된 목소리로 되받았다. \"무엇을 헤아린다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"제 친구가 하려는 말씀은, 웨이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 다소 말문이 막히는 것을 보고 아서 클레넘이 끼어들었다. \"그 가엾은 아이에게 이따금 덮쳐 오는, 자기가 불리한 처지에 있다는 그 격정적인 감각을 헤아린다는 뜻일 겁니다. 그것이 가끔 더 나은 기억들을 이겨 버리곤 하니까요.\"\n\n그 여인은 그에게 눈길을 돌리며 빙긋 웃음을 지었다. \"그래요?\" 그것이 그녀의 대답 전부였다.\n\n그녀는 그의 말을 그렇게 받아넘긴 뒤로, 어찌나 완벽하게 침착하고 고요하게 탁자 곁에 서 있던지, 미글스 씨는 일종의 홀린 듯한 기분으로 그녀를 빤히 바라보았고, 다음 수를 두라고 클레넘 쪽을 쳐다볼 엄두조차 내지 못했다. 꽤나 어색하게 한동안 기다린 끝에, 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"미글스 씨가 그 애를 만날 수 있게 해 주시면 좋겠는데요, 웨이드 양?\"\n\n\"그쯤은 쉬운 일이죠.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이리 오너라, 얘야.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 문을 열어, 이제 그 아이의 손을 잡고 안으로 데리고 들어왔다. 그 둘이 나란히 서 있는 모습은 무척 기이했다. 아이는 한가한 손가락으로 옷 앞자락을 반쯤은 우유부단하게, 반쯤은 격정적으로 주름잡고 있었고, 웨이드 양은 침착한 얼굴로 그 아이를 유심히 지켜보면서, 그 침착함 자체로(베일이 그것이 가린 형체를 떠올리게 하듯) 자기 본성 속의 꺼지지 않는 격정을 보는 이에게 유난히 강하게 암시하고 있었다.\n\n\"보세요.\" 그녀가 아까와 똑같이 차분한 어조로 말했다. \"여기 네 후견인, 네 주인이 오셨다. 네가 그 은혜를 알고 가겠다고 한다면, 이분은 너를 기꺼이 도로 데려가실 거란다, 얘야. 너는 다시 그분의 예쁜 딸을 돋보이게 하는 들러리가 되고, 그 딸의 사랑스러운 변덕에 매인 종이 되고, 집안의 선량함을 보여 주는 장난감이 될 수 있어. 너는 다시 그 우스꽝스러운 이름을 가질 수 있지. 너를 장난스레 가리키며 따로 떼어 놓는, 그래 마땅히 너를 가리키며 따로 떼어 놓아야 할 그 이름을 말이야.(네 출생, 알지? 네 출생을 잊으면 안 돼.) 너는 다시 이 신사분의 따님 해리엇 앞에 내보여져, 그 딸 앞에 서서 그 딸 자신의 우월함과 너그러운 겸양을 생생히 일깨워 주는 표지로 머물 수 있어. 너는 이 모든 이점을, 그리고 같은 종류의 다른 많은 것들을 — 내가 말하는 동안에도 네 기억 속에서 솟아오르고 있을, 또 네가 나에게로 피신함으로써 잃게 될 그것들을 — 도로 되찾을 수 있단다. 네가 얼마나 굴복하고 뉘우치는지 이 신사분들께 말씀드리고, 용서받으러 그분들에게 돌아감으로써 모두 되찾을 수 있어. 어떠니, 해리엇? 가겠니?\"\n\n이 말들의 영향으로 점점 분노가 차오르고 얼굴빛이 짙어졌던 그 아이는, 한순간 그 윤기 흐르는 검은 눈을 치켜뜨고, 줄곧 주름잡고 있던 옷자락을 손으로 꽉 움켜쥐며 대답했다. \"차라리 죽고 말겠어요!\"\n\n웨이드 양은 여전히 그 곁에 서서 그 아이의 손을 잡은 채, 조용히 둘러보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다. \"신사분들! 이러시면 어떻게 하시겠어요?\"\n\n가엾은 미글스 씨는 자기의 동기와 행동이 그토록 비틀려 풀이되는 것을 들으며 형언할 수 없는 경악에 빠져 있던 터라 지금까지 한마디도 끼어들지 못했지만, 이제 말할 힘을 되찾았다.\n\n\"태티코럼.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 이름으로 너를 계속 부르마, 착한 아이야. 내가 이 이름을 너에게 지어 줄 때 친절 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었음을 나는 알고, 또 너도 그걸 안다는 걸 나는 아니까—\"\n\n\"몰라요!\" 그 아이가 다시 고개를 들며, 그 분주한 손으로 제 몸을 거의 쥐어뜯다시피 하며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 지금은 모를지도 모르지.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"저 부인의 눈이 너를 그토록 뚫어지게 보고 있는 동안에는, 태티코럼.\" 그 아이가 잠시 그 눈을 흘끗 보았다. \"또 저분이 너에게 휘두르는 게 우리 눈에도 보이는 저 힘이 너를 사로잡고 있는 동안에는 말이다. 지금은 모를지 모르지만, 다른 때에는 알게 될 거다. 태티코럼, 나는 저 부인께, 저분이 한 말을 정말 믿으시는지 여쭙지 않으마. 비록 나와 여기 내 친구가 똑같이 알고 있듯, 저분이 분노와 악감정 속에서 그 말을 하시면서도, 한 번이라도 본 사람이라면 잊기 어려울 그 결연함으로 자신을 억누르고 계신다 해도 말이다. 너에게도, 우리 집과 거기 딸린 모든 것에 대한 네 기억을 두고, 그 말을 믿느냐고 묻지 않으마. 다만 나는 이 한 가지만 말하겠다. 너는 나나 내 식구에게 굴복을 고백할 것도, 용서를 빌 것도 없다는 것을. 그리고 이 세상에서 내가 너에게 바라는 단 한 가지는, 스물다섯을 세는 것, 그뿐이라는 것을, 태티코럼.\"\n\n그 아이는 한순간 그를 바라보다가, 이내 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"싫어요. 웨이드 양, 제발 저를 데려가 주세요.\"\n\n이제 그 아이 안에서 들끓는 갈등에는 누그러짐이라곤 없었다. 그것은 온전히 격정적인 반항과 완고한 반항 사이의 다툼이었다. 짙은 얼굴빛도, 빠르게 도는 피도, 가쁜 숨도, 모두 걸음을 되돌릴 그 기회에 맞서 버티고 있었다. \"싫어요. 싫어요. 싫어요!\" 그 아이가 낮고 잠긴 목소리로 되풀이했다. \"차라리 갈가리 찢기는 게 나아요. 차라리 제 손으로 저를 갈가리 찢어 버리겠어요!\"\n\n붙잡고 있던 손을 놓았던 웨이드 양은, 한순간 그 아이의 목덜미에 보호하듯 손을 얹었다가, 이내 아까처럼 미소를 띠며 둘러보고, 정확히 아까와 똑같은 어조로 말했다. \"신사분들! 이러시면 어떻게 하시겠어요?\"\n\n\"오, 태티코럼, 태티코럼!\" 미글스 씨가 외치며, 거기에 더해 간절한 손짓으로 그 아이에게 호소했다. \"저 부인의 목소리를 들어 보고, 저 부인의 얼굴을 보고, 저 부인의 가슴속에 무엇이 들었는지 헤아려 보고, 네 앞에 어떤 미래가 놓여 있는지 생각해 보아라. 얘야, 네가 어떻게 생각하든, 저 부인이 너에게 미치는 영향력은 — 우리에게도 놀랍고, 보고 있자니 끔찍하다고 해도 지나치지 않을 그 영향력은 — 너의 것보다 더 사나운 격정, 너의 것보다 더 격렬한 성미에 뿌리를 두고 있단다. 너희 둘이 함께 무엇이 될 수 있겠니? 거기서 무엇이 나올 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"저는 이곳에서 혼자랍니다, 신사분들.\" 웨이드 양이 목소리나 태도에 아무 변화 없이 말했다. \"하고 싶은 말씀은 무엇이든 하세요.\"\n\n\"이 그릇 인도된 아이를 위해서는 예의를 접어 두는 수밖에 없겠습니다, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"지금 이 막다른 처지에서는 말입니다. 그렇다 해도, 당신이 내 눈앞에서 이토록 노골적으로 이 아이를 해치고 있음에도, 예의를 아주 내던지고 싶지는 않습니다만. 이 애가 듣는 데서 일깨워 드리는 걸 용서하십시오 — 이 말은 꼭 해야겠습니다 — 이 애가 불행히도 당신과 마주쳤을 때, 당신은 우리 모두에게 수수께끼였고, 우리 누구와도 통하는 데가 없었습니다. 나는 당신이 어떤 사람인지 모르지만, 당신은 자기 안에 얼마나 어두운 영혼을 품고 있는지를 숨기지 못하고, 숨길 수도 없습니다. 만일 당신이, 어떤 까닭에서든, 같은 여인을 자기만큼이나 비참하게 만드는 데서 비뚤어진 기쁨을 느끼는 여자라면(나는 그런 이야기를 들을 만큼은 나이를 먹었습니다), 나는 이 애에게 당신을 경계하라 경고하고, 또 당신에게도 당신 자신을 경계하라 경고하는 바입니다.\"\n\n\"신사분들!\" 웨이드 양이 차분하게 말했다. \"이야기가 끝나셨거든 — 클레넘 씨, 부디 당신 친구분을 좀—\"\n\n\"한 번 더 애써 보지 않고서는 안 됩니다.\" 미글스 씨가 꿋꿋이 말했다. \"태티코럼, 가엾은 내 아가야, 스물다섯을 세거라.\"\n\n\"이 친절한 분이 너에게 주시는 희망을, 그 확실함을 물리치지 말거라.\" 클레넘이 나직하지만 힘 있는 목소리로 말했다. \"네가 잊지 않은 친구들에게로 돌아오너라. 한 번만 더 생각해!\"\n\n\"싫어요! 웨이드 양,\" 그 아이가 가슴을 한껏 부풀리며, 손을 목에 갖다 댄 채 말했다. \"저를 데려가 주세요!\"\n\n\"태티코럼.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"다시 한번만 더! 이 세상에서 내가 너에게 바라는 단 한 가지다, 얘야! 스물다섯을 세거라!\"\n\n그 아이는 두 손으로 귀를 꽉 막았는데, 그 격렬한 동작에 윤기 흐르는 검은 머리채가 어지러이 흘러내렸다. 그러고는 단호히 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌렸다. 웨이드 양은, 마르세유에서 그 아이가 몸부림칠 때 지켜보던 그 기묘하고도 주의 깊은 미소를 띠고, 또 그때 자기 가슴에 얹어 억누르던 그 손짓 그대로, 이 마지막 호소 아래 그 아이를 지켜보고 있다가, 마치 그 아이를 영영 자기 소유로 삼는다는 듯, 그 허리에 팔을 둘렀다.\n\n그리고 그녀가 손님들을 내보내려고 얼굴을 돌렸을 때, 그 얼굴에는 뚜렷한 승리의 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이번이 그 영광을 누릴 마지막일 테니,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"또 두 분이 내가 어떤 사람인지 모르겠다고, 그리고 여기서 내 영향력이 어디에 뿌리를 두고 있느냐고 말씀하셨으니, 이제 그것이 공통의 사연에 뿌리를 두고 있음을 아셔도 되겠지요. 당신이 부숴 버린 그 장난감이 출생에 있어 어떠한지, 나도 똑같습니다. 그 애에게 이름이 없듯, 나도 이름이 없어요. 그 애가 받은 부당함이 곧 내가 받은 부당함입니다. 나는 두 분께 더 드릴 말씀이 없군요.\"\n\n이 말은 미글스 씨를 향한 것이었고, 그는 비탄에 잠겨 밖으로 나갔다. 클레넘이 뒤따르자, 그녀는 똑같이 겉으로는 침착한 태도로, 똑같이 차분한 목소리로, 그러나 잔인한 얼굴에서만 볼 수 있는 미소를 띠고 그에게 말했다. 콧방울을 살짝 들어 올리고, 입술은 거의 건드리지도 않으며, 차츰 스러지는 것이 아니라 할 일이 끝나자마자 즉시 거두어지는, 아주 희미한 미소였다.\n\n\"당신의 그 소중한 친구 가우언 씨의 부인이, 자기 혈통과 이 아이와 나의 혈통이 빚어내는 그 대비에서, 그리고 자기를 기다리고 있는 그 드높은 행운에서, 부디 행복하기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER 28. Nobody’s Disappearance\n\n잃어버린 피보호자를 되찾으려 기울인 노력에 만족하지 못한 미글스 씨는, 오로지 선의로만 가득 찬 충고의 편지를, 그녀에게뿐 아니라 웨이드 양에게도 써 보냈다. 이 서한들에도, 또 옛 젊은 여주인의 손으로 그 고집 센 처녀에게 쓴 또 한 통의 편지에도—만약 무엇이라도 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다면 그 편지가 그러했을 텐데—아무런 답장이 오지 않자(세 통의 편지는 몇 주 뒤 모두 집 문간에서 수취가 거절되었다는 표시와 함께 되돌아왔다), 그는 미글스 부인에게 직접 면담을 시도하는 임무를 맡겼다. 그 갸륵한 부인이 면담을 얻지 못하고 입장을 한사코 거절당하자, 미글스 씨는 아서에게 다시 한번 할 수 있는 일을 해 달라고 간청했다. 그가 응한 결과 알게 된 것이라곤, 텅 빈 집이 한 노파의 관리에 맡겨져 있다는 것, 웨이드 양은 떠나 버렸다는 것, 떠돌이 같던 가구붙이들도 사라졌다는 것, 그리고 그 노파는 반 크라운짜리 동전을 몇 개든 받고 주는 이에게 친절히 고맙다고 인사하면서도, 그 동전과 맞바꿀 만한 정보라곤 전혀 없이, 다만 집 중개인의 젊은 사내가 현관에 두고 간 비품에 관한 비망록을 끊임없이 들여다보라고 내밀 뿐이라는 것이었다.\n\n이런 좌절 속에서도, 그 배은망덕한 처녀의 더 나은 성품이 그 성격의 어두운 면을 이겨 낼 경우를 대비하여, 그녀를 단념하고 절망에 빠뜨려 두기를 꺼린 미글스 씨는, 엿새 동안 연이어 조간신문에 신중하게 에둘러 쓴 광고를 실었다. 그 내용인즉, 최근 깊이 생각지 않고 집을 떠난 어느 젊은 사람이 언제든 트위크넘에 있는 자기 주소로 찾아온다면 모든 것이 예전 그대로 돌아갈 것이며 아무런 책망도 걱정할 필요가 없다는 것이었다. 이 통지가 불러온 뜻밖의 결과로, 당황한 미글스 씨는 비로소 깊이 생각지 않고 집을 떠나는 젊은 사람이 날마다 수백 명은 되겠구나 하고 처음 깨달았다. 엉뚱한 젊은이들이 떼를 지어 트위크넘으로 몰려왔는데, 열렬한 환대를 받지 못하자 대개는 오가는 마차 삯에 더해 손해배상 조로 보상을 요구했기 때문이다. 그리고 이런 자들만이 그 광고가 불러낸 청하지 않은 손님은 아니었다. 아무리 작은 갈고리라도 거기에 편지 한 통을 걸 만한 구실이라면 늘 눈에 불을 켜고 노리는 듯한 구걸 편지꾼들이 떼로 달려들어, 광고를 보고 자신 있게 청하게 되었다며 십 실링에서 오십 파운드에 이르는 갖가지 액수를 요구했다. 그 젊은 사람에 관해 무엇을 알아서가 아니라, 그런 기부금을 내놓으면 광고주의 마음이 크게 가벼워지리라 느꼈기 때문이라는 것이었다. 마찬가지로 여러 사업가들도 이 기회를 틈타 미글스 씨와 서신을 주고받았다. 이를테면, 친구가 그 광고를 알려 주어 주의를 기울이게 되었다며, 만일 그 젊은 사람에 대해 무엇이든 듣게 되면 즉시 알려 드리기를 게을리하지 않겠고, 그동안 어떤 전혀 새로운 종류의 양수기를 완성하는 데 필요한 자금을 베풀어 주신다면 인류에게 더없이 행복한 결과가 따를 것이라고 알려 오는 식이었다.\n\n미글스 씨와 그의 식구들은 이런 갖가지 낙담이 겹친 끝에 마지못해 태티코람을 영영 되찾을 수 없는 사람으로 단념하기 시작했는데, 그 무렵 새로 의욕에 차서 일하던 도이스 앤드 클레넘 상회의 두 사람이 개인 자격으로 토요일에 그 별장에 내려와 월요일까지 머물게 되었다. 선임 동업자는 마차를 탔고, 후임 동업자는 지팡이를 짚었다.\n\n산책의 끝에 다다라 강가의 풀밭을 가로질러 갈 무렵, 고요한 여름 석양이 그를 비추었다. 그에게는 도시에 사는 이들의 가슴에 시골의 정적이 불러일으키는 그 평화로움이, 근심의 무게에서 벗어난 듯한 그 느낌이 깃들어 있었다. 그의 눈에 들어오는 모든 것이 사랑스럽고 잔잔했다. 나무들의 풍성한 잎새, 들꽃이 어우러져 무성한 풀, 강 위의 작은 초록 섬들, 골풀이 우거진 곳, 물줄기 표면에 떠 있는 수련, 잔물결과 저녁 공기에 실려 음악처럼 그에게 다가오는 보트 위의 먼 목소리들, 이 모두가 안식을 말하고 있었다. 이따금 물고기가 튀어 오르는 소리, 노가 물에 잠기는 소리, 아직 둥지에 들지 않은 새의 지저귐, 멀리서 들리는 개 짖는 소리, 소가 우는 소리—이 모든 소리에는 안식의 기운이 가득히 배어 있었고, 그것은 향긋한 공기를 달콤하게 하는 온갖 냄새 속에서 그를 감싸는 듯했다. 하늘에 길게 뻗은 붉고 금빛인 띠들, 그리고 지는 해가 그려 놓은 찬란한 자취가 모두 거룩하리만치 고요했다. 멀리 보랏빛으로 물든 나무 우듬지 위에도, 어스름이 천천히 기어오르는 가까운 초록 언덕 위에도, 한결같은 정적이 깃들어 있었다. 실재하는 풍경과 물에 비친 그 그림자 사이에는 아무런 경계가 없었다. 둘 다 그토록 잔잔하고 맑았으며, 삶과 죽음의 엄숙한 신비로 가득 차 있으면서도, 그토록 부드럽고 자비롭게 아름다웠기에 바라보는 이의 누그러진 마음에 그토록 희망차게 위안을 주었다.\n\n클레넘은 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이나 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보며, 바라보고 있는 그림자가 물속으로 점점 더 깊이 가라앉는 듯이, 자기가 본 것이 영혼 속으로 가라앉도록 내버려 두곤 했다. 그가 천천히 다시 길을 가려는데, 앞쪽 오솔길에 한 사람의 모습이 보였다. 어쩌면 그가 이미 이 저녁과 그 정취에 결부시켜 떠올렸을지도 모를 모습이었다.\n\n미니가 거기, 혼자 있었다. 손에 장미 몇 송이를 들고서, 그를 보자 가만히 멈추어 서서 그를 기다린 듯했다. 그녀는 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌리고 있었는데, 반대편에서 오던 모양이었다. 그녀의 거동에는 클레넘이 여태껏 본 적 없는 떨림이 어려 있었다. 그리고 그가 가까이 다가가자, 그녀가 작정하고 자기에게 말을 건네려고 거기 와 있다는 생각이 문득 그의 마음에 떠올랐다.\n\n그녀가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제가 여기 혼자 있는 게 의아하시죠? 하지만 저녁이 너무 고와서, 처음 생각했던 것보다 멀리까지 거닐어 왔어요. 어쩌면 당신을 만날지도 모른다고 생각했고, 그러니 더 마음이 놓였답니다. 늘 이 길로 오시잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n이 길을 제일 좋아한다고 클레넘이 말할 때, 그는 그녀의 손이 자기 팔 위에서 흔들리는 것을 느꼈고 장미가 떨리는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"한 송이 드려도 될까요, 클레넘 씨? 정원에서 나오면서 꺾은 거예요. 사실은, 당신을 만날 것 같다는 생각에 거의 당신을 위해 꺾은 거나 다름없어요. 도이스 씨는 한 시간도 더 전에 도착하셔서, 당신이 걸어서 내려오고 있다고 말씀해 주셨거든요.\"\n\n그녀의 손에서 장미 한두 송이를 받아 고맙다고 말할 때, 그 자신의 손도 떨렸다. 두 사람은 어느새 나무가 늘어선 가로수길에 이르렀다. 그의 움직임으로 그리로 들어섰는지 그녀의 움직임으로 그랬는지는 대수롭지 않다. 그는 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 끝내 알지 못했다.\n\n\"여기는 무척 엄숙하군요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"하지만 이 시각에는 무척 정겹습니다. 이 깊은 그늘을 지나 저쪽 끝의 저 빛의 아치로 빠져나가면, 가장 좋은 길로 나루터와 별장에 닿게 되지요, 그렇게 생각합니다.\"\n\n소박한 정원 모자를 쓰고 가벼운 여름 옷을 입은 채, 풍성한 갈색 머리칼을 자연스레 얼굴 둘레에 드리우고서, 그 놀라운 두 눈을 잠시 그에게로 들어 올렸을 때—그 눈빛에는 그에 대한 존중과 그를 향한 신뢰가, 그를 향한 일종의 수줍은 슬픔과 두드러지게 어우러져 있었다—그녀는 너무도 아름다워서, 그가 그토록 자주 곱씹어 온 그 굳은 결심을 세워 두었던 것이 그의 마음의 평안을 위해 다행이었다—아니, 어쩌면 불행이었는지도, 그는 어느 쪽인지 확실히 알 수 없었다.\n\n그녀가 잠시의 침묵을 깨고, 아빠가 또 한 번 외국 여행을 생각하고 계신 걸 아느냐고 물었다. 그는 그런 얘기를 들었다고 말했다. 그녀가 또 잠시의 침묵을 깨고, 약간 머뭇거리며, 아빠가 그 생각을 접으셨다고 덧붙였다.\n\n이 말에 그는 곧장 생각했다. '저들이 결혼하려는 거구나.'\n\n\"클레넘 씨.\" 그녀가 한층 더 수줍게 머뭇거리며, 너무도 낮은 목소리로 말해서 그가 들으려고 고개를 숙였다. \"정말이지 제 속마음을 당신께 털어놓고 싶어요, 부디 받아 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주신다면요. 오래전부터 그러고 싶었어요. 왜냐하면—당신이 우리에게 그토록 좋은 친구가 되어 가고 계신다고 느꼈으니까요.\"\n\n\"언제든 그것을 자랑스러워하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠습니까! 부디 제게 털어놓으세요. 부디 저를 믿으세요.\"\n\n\"당신을 믿기를 두려워한 적은 한 번도 없었어요.\" 그녀가 솔직한 눈길을 그의 얼굴로 들어 올리며 대답했다. \"어떻게 하는지만 알았다면 얼마 전에라도 그렇게 했을 거예요. 하지만 지금도 어떻게 해야 할지 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"가우언 씨는,\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"무척 행복할 만한 사람입니다. 그의 아내와 그에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 고맙다고 말하려 애쓰면서 눈물을 흘렸다. 그는 그녀를 안심시키며, 떨리는 장미와 함께 그의 팔 위에 놓여 있던 그녀의 손을 잡고, 거기 남은 장미를 마저 빼낸 뒤, 그 손을 입술에 가져갔다. 그때, 그에게는 누구의 가슴속에선가 그토록 큰 고통과 괴로움으로 깜박이던 그 죽어 가는 희망을 마침내 처음으로 내려놓은 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그리고 그때부터 그는 그와 비슷한 희망이나 전망에 관한 한, 제 눈에 스스로가 그런 삶의 한 부분을 끝내 버린, 훨씬 나이 든 사람이 되었다.\n\n그는 장미를 가슴에 꽂았고, 두 사람은 그늘진 나무들 아래를 잠시 동안 천천히, 말없이 걸었다. 이윽고 그는 명랑하고 다정한 목소리로 그녀에게 물었다. 그녀의 친구이자 그녀의 아버지의 친구로서, 자기보다 여러 해 나이가 많은 사람으로서—그에게 더 들려줄 말은 없는지, 그에게 맡겨 둘 어떤 믿음은 없는지, 부탁할 어떤 도움은 없는지, 그녀의 행복에 보탬이 될 어떤 작은 일이라도, 그것이 자기 힘으로 해 줄 수 있는 일이라고 믿는 영원한 기쁨을 자기에게 줄 만한 것은 없는지.\n\n그녀가 막 대답하려는데, 어떤 작은, 숨겨진 슬픔인지 연민인지에—그것이 무엇이었을까?—너무도 마음이 동해서, 다시 눈물을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"오, 클레넘 씨! 선량하고 너그러우신 클레넘 씨, 제발 저를 탓하지 않는다고 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"제가 당신을 탓한다고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"사랑하는 아가씨! 제가 당신을 탓해요? 아니요!\"\n\n그녀는 두 손으로 그의 팔을 꼭 붙잡고, 미덥게 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 진심으로 그에게 감사한다는 뜻의 다급한 몇 마디를 더듬더듬 내뱉고는(만약 진심이 그런 간절함의 원천이라면, 그녀는 정말로 그러했다), 어두워져 가는 나무들 아래를 천천히, 거의 말없이 걸어가는 동안, 이따금 그가 건네는 격려의 한마디에 힘입어 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다.\n\n\"자, 그럼, 미니 가우언.\" 마침내 클레넘이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"제게 부탁할 게 아무것도 없나요?\"\n\n\"오! 부탁드릴 게 무척 많아요.\"\n\n\"그거 잘됐군요! 그러길 바랐답니다. 실망하지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"제가 집에서 얼마나 사랑받는지, 또 제가 집을 얼마나 사랑하는지 아시잖아요. 어쩌면 잘 믿기지 않으실지도 몰라요, 사랑하는 클레넘 씨.\" 그녀가 몹시 동요하며 말했다. \"제가 제 자유의지와 선택으로 집을 떠나는 걸 보시면서요. 하지만 저는 그토록 진심으로 집을 사랑한답니다!\"\n\n\"그건 저도 확신합니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"제가 그걸 의심한다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요. 하지만 제 자신에게도 이상하게 여겨져요. 그토록 집을 사랑하고 그 안에서 그토록 사랑받으면서도, 그걸 내던져 버릴 수 있다니. 너무도 집을 소홀히 하는 것 같고, 너무도 배은망덕한 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 아가씨.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그건 세월의 자연스러운 흐름이고 변화입니다. 모든 가정이 다 그렇게 떠나가는 법이죠.\"\n\n\"네, 알아요. 하지만 모든 가정이 다, 제가 떠난 뒤 우리 집에 생길 만큼 큰 빈자리를 남기며 떠나는 건 아니에요. 저보다 훨씬 더 낫고, 훨씬 더 사랑스럽고, 훨씬 더 재능 있는 처녀들이 부족해서가 아니라, 제가 대단해서가 아니라, 식구들이 저를 그토록 대단하게 여겨 주었기 때문이에요!\"\n\n펫의 다정한 마음은 벅차올랐고, 그녀는 앞으로 벌어질 일을 그려 보며 흐느꼈다.\n\n\"아빠가 처음에 어떤 변화를 느끼실지 저는 알아요. 그리고 처음에는 제가 이 여러 해 동안 그래 왔던 만큼 아빠에게 무엇이 되어 드릴 수 없으리란 것도 알아요. 바로 그때예요, 클레넘 씨, 다른 어느 때보다도 바로 그때, 제가 당신께 빌고 또 간청드려요. 아빠를 기억해 주시고, 짬을 좀 낼 수 있으실 때 이따금 아빠와 함께해 주시기를요. 그리고 제가 아빠를 떠날 때 평생 어느 때보다도 아빠를 더 사랑했다는 걸 당신이 안다고 아빠께 말씀드려 주세요. 왜냐하면 아무도 없으니까요—바로 오늘 저와 이야기하실 때 아빠가 직접 제게 그렇게 말씀하셨어요—아빠가 당신만큼 좋아하는 사람도, 그토록 믿는 사람도 없으니까요.\"\n\n아버지와 딸 사이에 오간 일에 대한 실마리가 무거운 돌처럼 클레넘의 가슴속 우물에 떨어져, 그 물을 눈가에까지 차오르게 했다. 그는 명랑하게, 그러나 그러려 애쓴 만큼 썩 명랑하지는 못하게, 그렇게 하겠노라고—성심껏 약속하겠노라고 말했다.\n\n\"제가 엄마 얘기를 하지 않는 건요,\" 펫이 말했다. 그 천진한 슬픔에, 클레넘이 차마 곰곰이 생각해 볼 엄두조차 못 낼 만큼 마음이 동했고, 또 그만큼 그녀가 어여뻤기에—바로 그 때문에 그는 두 사람과 스러져 가는 빛 사이에 놓인 나무들을, 천천히 그 수가 줄어들수록 하나하나 세었다—\"엄마는 이 일을 더 잘 이해해 주실 거고, 저를 잃는 걸 다른 식으로 느끼실 거고, 다른 방식으로 앞날을 내다보실 테니까예요. 하지만 엄마가 얼마나 다정하고 헌신적인 어머니인지 당신도 아시잖아요. 엄마도 기억해 주실 거죠? 그러실 거죠?\"\n\n미니가 자기를 믿어 달라고, 미니가 바라는 일을 다 하도록 자기를 믿어 달라고 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고, 사랑하는 클레넘 씨.\" 미니가 말했다. \"아빠와, 굳이 이름을 대지 않아도 될 한 사람이 아직은 서로를 충분히 알아주고 이해하지 못하니까요. 머지않아 그렇게 되겠지만요. 그리고 두 사람이 서로를 더 잘 알도록 이끄는 것, 서로에게 행복이 되어 주는 것, 서로를 자랑스러워하게 하는 것, 서로를 사랑하게 하는 것—두 사람 다 저를 그토록 깊이 사랑하니까요—그것이 제 새로운 삶의 의무이자 긍지이자 기쁨이 될 테니까요. 오, 당신은 친절하고 진실한 분이니! 제가 집과 처음으로 떨어지게 될 때(저는 아주 먼 곳으로 가요), 아빠가 그 사람과 조금이라도 더 화해하도록 애써 주세요. 그리고 당신의 큰 영향력을 발휘하여, 아빠의 마음속에서 그 사람을 편견 없이, 그 사람의 참모습 그대로 남아 있게 해 주세요. 당신은 고결한 마음을 지닌 친구이니, 저를 위해 이렇게 해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n가엾은 펫! 스스로를 속이는, 잘못 생각하는 아이! 사람들 사이의 타고난 관계에 그런 변화가 언제 이루어진 적이 있었던가. 뿌리 깊은 차이의 그런 화해가 언제 이루어진 적이 있었던가! 다른 딸들도 그것을 여러 번 시도했단다, 미니야. 그러나 한 번도 성공한 적이 없었지. 거기서 나온 것이라곤 실패뿐이었다.\n\n클레넘은 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 너무 늦은 일이었다. 그는 그녀가 부탁하는 모든 것을 하겠노라고 자신을 묶었고, 그녀는 그가 그렇게 하리라는 것을 잘 알았다.\n\n이제 두 사람은 가로수길의 마지막 나무에 이르렀다. 그녀가 걸음을 멈추고 팔을 거두었다. 그녀는 두 눈을 들어 그를 올려다보며, 조금 전까지 그의 소매 위에 얹혀 있던 손으로 그의 가슴에 꽂힌 장미 한 송이를 만지며—그에게 거듭 호소하듯—떨면서 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"사랑하는 클레넘 씨, 제 행복 속에서도—제가 우는 걸 보시긴 했지만 저는 정말 행복하답니다—저는 우리 사이에 어떤 구름이라도 남겨 두고 떠날 수가 없어요. 만약 제가 당신께 용서를 구할 일이 있다면(제가 일부러 한 일이 아니라, 의도치 않게, 또 막을 도리도 없이 제가 끼쳐 드렸을지 모를 어떤 괴로움이라도요), 오늘 밤 당신의 고결한 마음으로 저를 용서해 주세요!\"\n\n그는 위축됨 없이 자기를 마주하는 그 꾸밈없는 얼굴을 향해 고개를 숙였다. 그는 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 자기에게는 용서할 것이 아무것도 없음을 하늘이 안다고 대답했다. 그가 그 순결한 얼굴을 다시 한번 마주하려 고개를 숙였을 때, 그녀가 속삭였다. \"안녕히!\" 그도 그 말을 되풀이했다. 그것은 그의 모든 옛 희망에—누구의 것인지 모를 그 모든 옛 안절부절못하던 의심들에 작별을 고하는 것이었다. 다음 순간 두 사람은, 들어설 때와 똑같이 팔짱을 끼고 가로수길에서 나왔다. 그리고 나무들은 그들 뒤로 어둠 속에서, 마치 그들 자신의 지나간 날의 원근법처럼 닫혀 드는 듯했다.\n\n미글스 씨와 미글스 부인, 그리고 도이스가 정원 문 가까이에서 이야기하는 목소리가 곧바로 들려왔다. 그들 사이에서 펫의 이름이 들리자, 클레넘이 외쳤다. \"여기 저와 함께 있습니다.\" 그들이 다가올 때까지 약간의 의아함과 웃음이 오갔지만, 모두가 한자리에 모이자 그것은 그쳤고, 펫은 슬며시 자리를 떴다.\n\n미글스 씨와 도이스와 클레넘은 떠오르는 달빛 속에서 말없이 강가를 몇 분 동안 거닐었다. 그러다가 도이스가 뒤에 처져 머뭇거리더니 집 안으로 들어갔다. 미글스 씨와 클레넘은 몇 분을 더 말없이 함께 거닐었고, 이윽고 앞사람이 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"아서.\" 그들이 서로 이야기를 나누어 온 이래 처음으로 그 친근한 호칭을 쓰며 그가 말했다. \"어느 더운 아침에 우리가 마르세유 항구를 내려다보며 거닐 때, 죽은 펫의 아기 누이가 아내와 내게는 마치 펫이 자란 만큼 자라고, 펫이 변한 만큼 변한 것처럼 여겨진다고 자네에게 말했던 걸 기억하나?\"\n\n\"잘 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"우리 생각이 그 쌍둥이 자매를 도무지 갈라놓을 수 없었다고, 그리고 우리 상상 속에서는 펫이 어떤 모습이든 다른 아이도 꼭 그렇다고 내가 말한 것을 기억하나?\"\n\n\"네, 잘 기억합니다.\"\n\n\"아서.\" 미글스 씨가 한껏 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다. \"오늘 밤 나는 그 상상을 더 멀리 끌고 가 보네. 오늘 밤 나는, 여보게, 마치 자네가 내 죽은 아이를 무척이나 다정하게 사랑했고, 그 아이가 지금의 펫과 같은 모습이었을 때 그 아이를 잃은 것만 같이 느껴진다네.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다!\" 클레넘이 나직이 말했다. \"고맙습니다!\" 그러고는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"들어오겠나?\" 이윽고 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"조금 이따가요.\"\n\n미글스 씨가 멀어지고, 그는 홀로 남았다. 평화로운 달빛 속에서 강가를 반 시간쯤 거닌 뒤, 그는 가슴에 손을 넣어 한 줌의 장미를 다정하게 꺼냈다. 어쩌면 그것을 가슴에 가져다 댔는지도, 어쩌면 입술에 가져다 댔는지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 강기슭에 몸을 굽혀 흐르는 강물 위에 그것을 가만히 띄워 보낸 것만은 분명했다. 달빛 속에서 창백하고 비현실적인 그 강물이 장미를 실어 떠내려 보냈다.\n\n그가 들어섰을 때 집 안의 불빛은 환했고, 그 불빛이 비추는 얼굴들은—그 자신의 얼굴도 예외 없이—이내 조용히 명랑해졌다. 그들은 여러 가지 이야기를 나누었고(그의 동업자는 시간을 즐겁게 보내는 데 끌어다 쓸 화젯거리를 그토록 풍성하게 갖추고 있은 적이 없었다), 그렇게 잠자리에 들어 잠이 들었다. 그동안 그 꽃들은, 달빛 속에서 창백하고 비현실적인 채로, 강물 위를 떠내려갔다. 그리고 한때 우리 가슴속에 있었고 우리 마음 가까이 있었던 더 큰 것들도, 이처럼 우리에게서 흘러나가 영원한 바다로 흘러드는 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming\n\n도심의 그 집은 이 모든 거래가 오가는 동안에도 그 무거운 둔중함을 고스란히 지켰고, 그 안의 병자는 변함없는 삶의 똑같은 순환을 되풀이했다. 아침, 정오, 밤, 다시 아침, 정오, 밤, 저마다 따라붙는 단조로움과 함께 되돌아오고, 언제나 똑같은 기계 장치의 똑같은 순서가 마지못해 되풀이되는 것이, 마치 질질 끌리는 시계태엽 같았다.\n\n바퀴 달린 의자에도, 인간 존재의 거처가 된 모든 장소가 그러하듯, 거기에 얽힌 추억과 몽상이 있었으리라 짐작할 만했다. 헐려 버린 거리와 달라진 집들이, 그 의자의 주인이 한때 그것들과 친숙하던 시절의 모습 그대로 그려지고, 사람들의 모습도 옛 그 모습 그대로, 그들을 본 이래 흘러간 세월에 대한 참작은 거의 또는 전혀 없이 떠올랐다. 침울한 나날의 그 기나긴 일과 속에 그런 것들이 분명 많았으리라. 우리가 몸소 그 흐름에서 격리된 그 시각에 분주한 삶의 시계를 멈춰 세우는 것, 우리가 멈춰 서게 되었을 때 인류도 함께 꼼짝없이 멈췄으리라 여기는 것, 우리 시야 너머의 변화를 우리 자신의 한결같고 옹색한 삶이라는 그 쪼그라든 잣대보다 더 큰 잣대로는 가늠하지 못하는 것, 이는 많은 병자의 병약함이요, 거의 모든 은둔자의 정신적 불건강함이다.\n\n그 엄격한 여인이 철 따라 어두운 단칸방에 앉아 어떤 장면과 어떤 인물들을 가장 자주 되새겼는지는 그녀 자신밖에 알지 못했다. 플린트윈치 씨가, 마치 별난 기계적 힘처럼 매일같이 그 비뚤어진 존재감을 그녀에게 들이대며, 그녀의 저항이 좀 덜했다면 어쩌면 그것을 나사 돌리듯 비틀어 끄집어냈을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 맞서기에 너무 강했다. 애프리 부인으로 말하자면, 자기 주군과 불구가 된 안주인을 멍한 경이의 얼굴로 바라보는 것, 어두워진 뒤에 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰고 집 안을 돌아다니는 것, 늘 그 이상한 소리들에 귀 기울이고 때로 그것을 듣는 것, 그리고 결코 자신의 그 유령 같고 꿈결 같고 잠과 깸의 경계에 있는 상태에서 벗어나지 못하는 것, 그것만으로도 그녀에게는 충분한 일거리였다.\n\n애프리 부인이 알아챈 바로는 제법 짭짤한 사업이 진행되고 있었으니, 남편이 그 작은 사무실에서 일거리가 넘쳐났고, 몇 해 동안 그곳에 드나들던 것보다 훨씬 많은 사람을 만났기 때문이다. 집이 오래 비어 있었으니 그럴 만도 했다. 그래도 그는 편지를 받고 손님을 받고 장부를 적고 서신을 주고받았다. 게다가 그는 다른 회계 사무소며 부두며 선창이며 세관이며 개러웨이 커피점이며 예루살렘 커피점이며 거래소에도 드나들었으니, 들고나는 일이 잦았다. 또한 그는 클레넘 부인이 자기 곁을 굳이 원하지 않을 때면 저녁녘에 가끔씩 근처 선술집을 찾아 석간신문의 해운 소식과 마감 시세를 들여다보고, 그 가게를 자주 드나드는 상선 선장들과 자잘한 사교를 나누기까지 했다. 매일 어느 때엔가 그와 클레넘 부인은 사업 문제를 두고 회의를 열었다. 늘 더듬듯 돌아다니며 엿듣고 살피던 애프리에게는, 그 약삭빠른 두 사람이 돈을 벌고 있는 것으로 보였다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨의 얼이 빠진 아내가 빠져든 정신 상태가 이제는 그녀의 모든 표정과 행동에 워낙 드러나기 시작한지라, 그 약삭빠른 두 사람은 그녀를 본디 지력이 강했던 적도 없는 데다 점점 어리석어져 가는 사람으로 여겨 아주 낮잡아 보았다. 어쩌면 그녀의 외양이 장사꾼다운 데가 없어서였을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그녀를 아내로 삼은 것이 손님들의 마음속에 자신의 판단력을 의심받게 할지 모른다는 생각이 들어서였는지도 모르나, 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀에게 부부 관계라는 화제에 대해 입을 다물 것이며, 식구 셋이 모인 자리에서 더는 자신을 제러마이아라고 부르지 말 것을 명령으로 내렸다. 이 훈계를 그녀가 자주 잊는 통에 그녀의 깜짝깜짝 놀라는 태도는 한층 심해졌으니, 그녀의 태만을 응징하는 플린트윈치 씨의 버릇이 계단에서 그녀에게 달려들어 흔들어 대는 것이었던 탓에, 그녀는 다음번에 또 어느 때 이렇게 매복에 걸려들지 늘 신경이 곤두선 채 짐작도 못 하고 지냈다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 클레넘 부인의 방에서 긴 하루의 일을 마치고, 집으로 가기 전에 자신의 자투리와 토막들을 깔끔하게 거두어 모으던 참이었다. 애프리가 방금 들여보낸 팽크스 씨가 클레넘 부인에게 안부를 묻고 있었는데, '마침 그쪽 방향으로 오게 되어' 자기 고용주를 대신해 그녀가 어떻게 지내는지 여쭤보러 들렀다는 말을 덧붙였다. 클레넘 부인은 미간을 깊이 찌푸린 채 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 변하는 법이 없는 사람이라는 걸 캐스비 씨도 알지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 여기서 기다리는 변화란 그 큰 변화입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 부인?\" 팽크스 씨는 무릎을 꿇고 양탄자에서 실밥과 일감의 보풀을 집어내는 그 작은 바느질꾼의 모습 쪽으로 눈길을 흘리며 대답했다. \"안색이 좋아 보이십니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"나는 내가 견뎌야 할 것을 견디지요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"당신은 당신이 해야 할 일을 하세요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"그러려고 애쓰고 있지요.\"\n\n\"이쪽 방향으로 자주 오시지요, 그렇지 않은가요?\" 클레넘 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그렇습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"요즘 들어 좀 그렇지요. 요사이엔 이런저런 일로 이 근방을 꽤 많이 돌아다녔습니다.\"\n\n\"캐스비 씨와 그 따님에게, 대리를 시켜 나를 두고 수고하지 말라고 전해 주세요. 나를 보고 싶으면, 여기 와서 나를 만날 수 있다는 걸 그들도 압니다. 굳이 사람을 보내느라 애쓸 필요가 없어요. 당신도 굳이 오느라 수고할 필요가 없고요.\"\n\n\"조금도 수고랄 게 없습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 유달리 좋아 보이십니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다. 안녕히 가세요.\"\n\n물러가라는 그 말과, 그에 곁들여 곧장 문을 가리킨 손가락이 어찌나 퉁명스럽고 직설적이던지, 팽크스 씨로서는 방문을 더 끌어갈 도리가 없었다. 그는 더없이 발랄한 표정으로 머리카락을 휘저어 헝클고, 그 작은 모습을 다시 한번 흘긋 보고는 \"안녕히 계십시오, 부인. 내려오지 마세요, 애프리 부인, 문 가는 길은 제가 압니다\" 하고 말하더니 증기 뿜듯 빠져나갔다. 클레넘 부인은 턱을 손에 괴고서 주의 깊고도 어둡게 의심하는 눈으로 그를 좇았고, 애프리는 무언가에 홀린 듯 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n천천히, 또 생각에 잠긴 채, 클레넘 부인의 눈길이 팽크스가 나간 문에서 양탄자에서 일어서는 리틀 도릿에게로 돌아갔다. 턱을 손에 더욱 무겁게 떨군 채, 경계하듯 내리깐 눈으로, 그 병든 여인은 리틀 도릿이 자신의 시선을 알아챌 때까지 앉아서 바라보았다. 리틀 도릿은 그런 눈길을 받고 얼굴을 붉히며 눈을 내리깔았다. 클레넘 부인은 여전히 골똘히 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"리틀 도릿.\" 마침내 그녀가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"넌 저 사람에 대해 무얼 아느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 저분에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다, 부인. 그저 이리저리 다니는 걸 본 적이 있고, 저한테 말을 건 적이 있다는 것밖에는요.\"\n\n\"너에게 무슨 말을 하더냐?\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 하는지 잘 모르겠어요, 워낙 이상하셔서요. 하지만 거칠거나 불쾌한 말은 하나도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"왜 너를 보러 여기 오는 거지?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다, 부인.\" 리틀 도릿이 더없이 솔직하게 말했다.\n\n\"저 사람이 너를 보러 여기 오는 줄은 알고 있지?\"\n\n\"그런가 보다 하고 짐작은 했어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"하지만 무엇 때문에 저분이 여기든 어디든 그런 일로 오는지는, 부인, 도무지 생각이 안 나요.\"\n\n클레넘 부인은 눈을 땅으로 떨구고는, 강하고 굳은 얼굴로, 조금 전 시야에서 사라지는 듯한 그 형체에 쏠려 있던 것만큼이나 마음속 어떤 문제에 골똘히 쏠린 채, 빠져들어 앉아 있었다. 몇 분이 흐른 뒤에야 그녀는 이 깊은 생각에서 빠져나와 그 딱딱한 평정을 되찾았다.\n\n그동안 리틀 도릿은 떠나려고 기다리고 있었으나, 움직여서 그녀를 방해할까 두려웠다. 그녀는 이제 일어선 뒤로 줄곧 서 있던 그 자리를 떠나, 바퀴 달린 의자 곁으로 가만히 돌아 지나갈 엄두를 냈다. 그 곁에 멈춰 서서 \"안녕히 주무세요, 부인\" 하고 말했다.\n\n클레넘 부인이 손을 내밀어 그녀의 팔에 얹었다. 리틀 도릿은 그 손길에 당황하여 머뭇거리며 섰다. 어쩌면 그 공주 이야기에 대한 어떤 순간적인 기억이 그녀의 마음에 스쳤는지도 모른다.\n\n\"말해 보아라, 리틀 도릿.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"넌 이제 친구가 많으냐?\"\n\n\"아주 적어요, 부인. 부인 말고는 플로라 아가씨뿐이고요—그리고 한 사람 더요.\"\n\n\"그 말은,\" 클레넘 부인이 펴진 손가락으로 다시 문을 가리키며 말했다. \"저 사람을 두고 하는 말이냐?\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 부인!\"\n\n\"그럼 그 사람의 친구쯤 되는 사람이냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 부인.\" 리틀 도릿은 간절하게 고개를 저었다. \"아, 아니에요! 그분과 닮았거나 그분과 얽힌 사람은 전혀 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래!\" 클레넘 부인은 거의 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"내가 상관할 바는 아니지. 내가 묻는 건, 네게 관심이 있어서다. 그리고 너를 도와줄 다른 사람이 아무도 없을 때 내가 네 친구였다고 믿기 때문이다. 그렇지 않으냐?\"\n\n\"네, 부인. 정말 그래요. 부인과 부인이 주신 일감이 아니었다면 우리가 모든 것을 다 아쉬워했을 그런 때에, 저는 여러 번 이곳에 왔었어요.\"\n\n\"우리라.\" 클레넘 부인은 죽은 남편의 것이었던, 늘 그녀의 탁자 위에 놓여 있는 그 회중시계 쪽을 바라보며 되뇌었다. \"너희가 식구가 많으냐?\"\n\n\"이제는 아버지와 저뿐이에요. 그러니까, 버는 돈으로 늘 꾸준히 살아가야 하는 건 아버지와 저뿐이라는 말이에요.\"\n\n\"너희는 궁핍을 많이 겪었느냐? 너와 네 아버지와, 또 너희 중에 누가 더 있든 말이다.\" 클레넘 부인이 신중하게 말하며, 생각에 잠긴 듯 시계를 자꾸만 뒤집어 보면서 물었다.\n\n\"이따금 사는 게 꽤 힘들 때도 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 그 부드러운 목소리로, 소심하고 불평 없는 태도로 말했다. \"하지만 그 점에 대해서는—많은 사람들이 겪는 것보다 더 힘들었다고는 생각하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"잘 말했다!\" 클레넘 부인이 얼른 받았다. \"그게 진실이야! 넌 착하고 사려 깊은 아이야. 게다가 고마움을 아는 아이지. 내가 너를 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이다.\"\n\n\"그러는 게 당연한걸요. 그러는 데에 무슨 갸륵함이 있는 건 아니에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"저는 정말로 고맙게 생각하고 있어요.\"\n\n클레넘 부인은, 꿈꾸는 애프리가 그녀에게 그럴 능력이 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했을 그런 다정함으로, 자신의 작은 바느질꾼의 얼굴을 끌어내려 그 이마에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 가거라, 리틀 도릿.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 늦겠다, 가엾은 것아!\"\n\n애프리 부인이 그 일에 빠져든 이래 차곡차곡 쌓아 올린 온갖 꿈들 가운데에도, 그녀는 이보다 더 놀라운 것을 꿈꾼 적이 없었다. 다음번엔 또 그 약삭빠른 다른 한 사람이 리틀 도릿에게 입을 맞추고, 그러고 나면 그 약삭빠른 두 사람이 서로를 끌어안고서 온 인류를 향한 애틋함의 눈물에 녹아드는 광경을 보게 되는 게 아닐까 하는 생각에 그녀는 머리가 다 지끈거렸다. 집 대문을 안심하고 닫을 수 있도록 그 가벼운 발소리를 따라 계단 아래까지 배웅하는 동안, 그 생각이 그녀를 완전히 멍하게 만들었다.\n\n리틀 도릿을 내보내려고 문을 열자, 그녀는 팽크스 씨가, 덜 신기한 곳에서 덜 신기한 현상들 가운데에 있었더라면 응당 그러리라 기대됨 직했던 대로 제 갈 길을 가지 않고, 집 바깥 마당을 이리저리 서성이고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 리틀 도릿을 보자마자 그녀 곁을 잰걸음으로 지나치며, 손가락을 코에 갖다 댄 채 (애프리 부인이 또렷이 들은 바로는) \"집시 팽크스, 점치기\" 하고 말하고는 가 버렸다. \"맙소사, 이제는 집시에다 점쟁이까지 끼어들었네!\" 애프리 부인이 부르짖었다. \"이다음엔 또 뭐람!\"\n\n그녀는 비 내리고 천둥 치는 저녁에, 이 수수께끼에 휘청대며 열린 문가에 서 있었다. 구름은 빠르게 흘러갔고, 바람은 돌풍으로 불어닥쳐, 풀려 덜렁대는 이웃집 덧문 몇 짝을 쾅쾅 두드리고, 녹슨 굴뚝 갓과 풍향계를 빙글빙글 돌리고, 인접한 비좁은 교회 묘지를 빙빙 휩쓸어 돌며, 마치 죽은 시민들을 무덤에서 불어 날려 버릴 작정이라도 한 듯했다. 하늘 사방에서 한꺼번에 낮게 웅얼대는 천둥은, 이 모독의 시도에 보복이라도 하겠다는 듯 으르렁대며 \"그들을 쉬게 두라! 그들을 쉬게 두라!\" 하고 중얼거리는 것 같았다.\n\n천둥과 번개에 대한 두려움이 그 안에 때 이르고 으스스한 어둠이 깃든 유령 들린 집에 대한 무서움과 맞먹을 뿐이었던 애프리 부인은, 들어가야 할지 말지 정하지 못한 채 서 있었는데, 마침 사나운 돌풍에 문이 그녀를 향해 휙 닫히며 그녀를 바깥에 가둬 버림으로써 그 문제가 저절로 결판났다. \"이제 어쩐담, 이제 어쩐담!\" 애프리 부인이 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 마지막의 불안한 꿈속에서 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 부르짖었다. \"안에서 마님이 홀로 외따로 계신데, 교회 묘지의 죽은 이들과 마찬가지로 내려와서 문을 열어 줄 수도 없으니!\"\n\n이 진퇴양난에 빠진 애프리 부인은, 비를 막으려고 앞치마를 두건 삼아 쓴 채, 그 외딴 포석 깔린 마당을 울며 몇 차례 오르내렸다. 그러고서 왜 그녀가 몸을 굽혀, 마치 눈 하나가 문을 열어 주기라도 할 것처럼 문 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보았는지는 설명하기 어렵겠으나, 그래도 그것은 같은 처지에 놓인 대부분의 사람들이 했을 법한 일이고, 또 그녀가 한 일이었다.\n\n이 자세로 있다가 그녀는 어깨에 무언가가 닿는 것을 느끼고는, 반쯤 비명을 지르며 갑자기 벌떡 일어섰다. 그것은 손의 닿음이었으니, 어떤 남자의 손이었다.\n\n그 남자는 여행자 차림으로, 털이 둘린 약모를 쓰고 망토를 한 무더기 걸치고 있었다. 외국인처럼 보였다. 그는 머리털과 콧수염이 숱이 많았는데—새카만 빛깔에, 더부룩한 끝부분만은 불그스름한 기가 돌았다—매부리코가 우뚝했다. 그는 애프리 부인이 깜짝 놀라 지른 비명에 웃었는데, 그가 웃자 콧수염은 코밑으로 치켜올라가고 코는 콧수염 위로 내려앉았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오?\" 그가 또렷한 영어로 물었다. \"뭘 그리 무서워하는 거요?\"\n\n\"당신이요.\" 애프리가 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n\"나 말이오, 부인?\"\n\"그리고 음산한 저녁이며—그리고—그리고 죄다요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"그리고 이것 좀 봐요! 바람이 불어 문을 닫아 버려서, 내가 들어갈 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"하!\" 그 신사는 그 일을 아주 태연하게 받아들이며 말했다. \"그렇군! 이 근방에 클레넘이라는 이름을 아시오?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 알다마다요, 알다마다요!\" 그 물음에 애프리는 발끈하여 또다시 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 부르짖었다.\n\n\"이 근처 어디 말이오?\"\n\n\"어디라뇨!\" 애프리는 부추겨져 또 한 번 열쇠 구멍을 살피며 부르짖었다. \"여기, 바로 이 집 말고 어디겠어요? 마님은 방에 홀로 계신데, 팔다리를 쓰지 못해 당신 몸은커녕 나도 도우려고 꼼짝할 수가 없고, 약삭빠른 그 양반은 나가 있고, 아이고 하느님 용서하소서!\" 애프리는 이렇게 쌓여 가는 걱정거리에 떼밀려 미친 듯 춤을 추다시피 하며 부르짖었다. \"내가 정신줄을 놓고 곤두박질치는 게 아니라면 말이오!\"\n\n이 일이 자기와도 관계되자 한층 적극적인 견지에서 보게 된 그 신사는, 한 걸음 물러서서 그 집을 흘긋 살펴보았고, 그의 눈은 이내 현관 옆 작은 방의 길고 좁은 창에 머물렀다.\n\n\"팔다리를 쓰지 못한다는 그 부인이 어디 계시오, 부인?\" 애프리 부인이 도무지 눈을 떼지 못하던 그 독특한 미소를 띠며 그가 물었다.\n\n\"저 위요!\" 애프리가 말했다. \"저기 저 창문 둘이요.\"\n\n\"하! 내 몸집이 제법 됩니다만, 사다리 없이는 저 방에 들어가 나를 소개하는 영광을 누릴 수가 없겠군. 자, 부인, 솔직히 말하자면—솔직함은 내 성격의 일부지요—내가 당신을 위해 문을 열어 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"네, 복받으세요, 나리, 참 고마운 양반이로구려, 어서 그렇게 해 주세요.\" 애프리가 부르짖었다. \"지금 이 순간에도 마님이 나를 부르고 계실지 모르고, 아니면 불을 지펴 타 죽고 계실지도 모르고, 아니 마님께 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알 수가 없는데, 그 생각만 하면 내가 정신을 놓아 버릴 지경이라오!\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 부인!\" 그가 매끄러운 흰 손으로 그녀의 조바심을 억눌렀다. \"오늘 영업시간은 끝난 모양이지요?\"\n\n\"네, 네, 네.\" 애프리가 부르짖었다. \"벌써 한참 전에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 내가 공정한 제안을 하나 하리다. 공정함은 내 성격의 일부지요. 보다시피 나는 우편선에서 막 내린 참이라오.\" 그는 그녀에게 자기 망토가 흠뻑 젖었고 장화가 물에 절었음을 보여 주었다. 그녀는 이미, 그가 거친 항해를 한 듯 부스스하고 누렇게 떠 있으며, 어찌나 몸이 얼었는지 이가 딱딱 부딪는 것을 막지 못하는 것을 눈여겨본 터였다. \"나는 우편선에서 막 내린 참이오, 부인, 그런데 날씨 때문에 발이 묶였지요. 그 빌어먹을 날씨 말이오! 이 때문에, 부인, 아니었으면 정규 시간 안에 이곳에서 처리했을 어떤 긴요한 용무가 (긴요한 용무라 함은 돈에 관한 일이기 때문인데) 아직 처리되지 못한 채 남아 있소이다. 자, 만약 당신이 그 일을 처리할 만한 권한 있는 이웃 사람을 누구든 데려와 준다면, 그 대가로 내가 문을 열어 드리리다. 이 제안이 마음에 들지 않는다면, 나는—\" 그러더니 똑같은 그 미소를 띠고서 물러가는 시늉을 의미심장하게 해 보였다.\n\n애프리 부인은 그가 제안한 그 타협을 이루게 된 것이 진심으로 반가워, 기꺼이 거기에 따르겠노라 동의했다. 그 신사는 즉시 그녀에게 자기 망토를 들어 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하고는, 좁은 창문 쪽으로 짧게 달려가 창턱으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 벽돌을 붙잡고 기어올라가더니, 순식간에 한 손을 창틀에 대고 그것을 들어 올렸다. 그가 한쪽 다리를 방 안으로 들여놓고 애프리 부인 쪽을 돌아볼 때 그의 눈이 어찌나 음흉해 보이던지, 그녀는 만약 그가 곧장 위층으로 올라가 그 병자를 살해하려 든다면 자기가 무슨 수로 그것을 막을 수 있을까 하는 생각에 별안간 오싹해졌다.\n\n다행히도 그에게는 그런 속셈이 없었으니, 그는 잠깐 사이에 집 대문 앞에 다시 나타났다. \"자, 부인.\" 그가 망토를 되받아 둘러 걸치며 말했다. \"친절을 베풀어 이제—저게 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 소리야!\"\n\n더없이 기이한 소리였다. 공기에 전해지는 그 독특한 진동으로 미루어 분명 바로 가까이에서 나는데도, 멀리 떨어진 데서 나는 듯 잦아든 소리였다. 떨림 한 번, 우르릉거림 한 번, 그리고 무언가 가볍고 메마른 것이 무너져 내리는 소리.\n\n\"저게 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 소리야?\"\n\n\"그게 뭔지는 모르지만, 나는 그 비슷한 소리를 몇 번이고 거듭 들었다오.\" 그의 팔을 붙든 애프리가 말했다.\n\n그녀가 꿈결 같은 놀람과 두려움 속에서 보기에도, 그는 그리 용감한 사람일 수는 없었으니, 그의 떨리는 입술이 핏기를 잃었기 때문이다. 잠깐 귀를 기울이고 나서 그는 그 일을 대수롭지 않게 넘겼다.\n\n\"흥! 아무것도 아니오! 자, 부인, 무슨 약삭빠른 인물에 대해 말씀하셨던 것 같은데. 그 잘난 양반과 나를 대면시켜 주시겠소?\" 그는, 그녀가 못 해내면 다시 그녀를 바깥에 가둬 버릴 채비라도 한 듯, 문을 손에 쥔 채로 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 문에 대한 거랑 나에 대한 거는 아무 말도 하지 말아 줘요.\" 애프리가 속삭였다.\n\n\"한 마디도 안 하리다.\"\n\n\"그리고 내가 모퉁이를 돌아 다녀오는 동안, 여기서 꼼짝도 말고, 마님이 부르셔도 말하지 말아요.\"\n\n\"부인, 나는 석상이외다.\"\n\n애프리는 그가 자기 등을 돌리는 순간 살그머니 위층으로 올라가지나 않을까 하는 두려움이 어찌나 생생하던지, 보이지 않는 데까지 서둘러 갔다가 다시 대문께로 돌아와 그를 엿보았다. 그가 여전히 그 자리, 마치 어둠을 좋아하지도 않고 그 비밀을 캐고 싶어 하지도 않는 듯, 집 안에 든 것보다 바깥으로 나와 있는 채 문턱에 서 있는 것을 보고서야, 그녀는 옆 거리로 날듯이 달려가 선술집 안의 플린트윈치 씨에게 전갈을 넣었고, 그는 곧바로 나왔다. 두 사람이 함께 돌아오는데—아내가 앞서고, 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀가 집으로 들기 전에 한바탕 흔들어 주리라는 기대에 들떠 뒤에서 잰걸음으로 따라붙으며—그 신사가 어둠 속 똑같은 자리에 서 있는 것을 보았고, 클레넘 부인이 자기 방에서 부르는 그 우렁찬 목소리를 들었다. \"누구냐? 무슨 일이냐? 왜 아무도 대답을 않느냐? 저 아래 그 사람은 누구냐?\"\n\n# CHAPTER 30. The Word of a Gentleman\n\n플린트윈치 부부가 황혼 속 그 낡은 집 문간으로 헐떡이며 올라왔을 때, 제러마이아는 애프리보다 일 초쯤 늦었는데, 낯선 사내가 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰다.\n\"내 영혼이 죽을 일이로군!\" 그가 외쳤다. \"아니, 당신이 어떻게 여기 와 있단 말이오?\"\n\n이 말을 들은 플린트윈치 씨는 낯선 사내의 놀라움을 고스란히 되갚았다. 그는 멍한 경악에 휩싸여 사내를 바라보았다. 자신이 미처 알아채지 못한 누군가가 뒤에 서 있을까 싶어 어깨너머를 돌아보았다. 그러고는 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 알 수 없어 말도 못 한 채 다시 낯선 사내를 바라보았다. 설명을 구하려 아내를 쳐다보았다. 아무 대답도 얻지 못하자, 그는 아내에게 달려들어 모자가 머리에서 벗겨질 만큼 격렬하게 흔들어대며, 그러는 동안 이를 악물고 음울한 야유를 내뱉었다. \"애프리, 이 사람아, 약 한 첩 먹어야겠구먼, 이 사람아! 또 무슨 수작이야! 또 꿈을 꾸고 있었구먼, 마누라. 무슨 꿈이야? 이게 누구냐고? 무슨 뜻이냐고! 어서 말해, 안 그러면 목을 졸라 버릴 테다! 내가 줄 수 있는 선택은 그것뿐이야.\"\n\n이 추궁의 순간 애프리 부인에게 무언가를 고를 힘이 있었다고 친다면, 그녀의 선택은 단연 목 졸리는 쪽이었다. 이 호령에 한 음절도 답하지 않은 채, 모자도 없는 머리를 앞뒤로 사납게 흔들며 그저 제 벌을 받아들이고 있었으니 말이다. 그러나 낯선 사내가 정중한 태도로 그녀의 모자를 집어 들며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"제게 맡기시지요.\" 그가 제러마이아의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말하자, 제러마이아는 멈추고 제 희생양을 놓아주었다. \"고맙습니다. 실례했군요. 이 장난기를 보니 부부 사이임을 알겠습니다. 하하! 그 관계가 장난스럽게 유지되는 걸 보는 건 언제나 흐뭇한 일이지요. 들어 보십시오! 위층 어둠 속의 누군가가 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 무척이나 궁금해하고 있는 것 같은데요?\"\n\n클레넘 부인의 목소리에 대한 이 언급에 플린트윈치 씨는 정신이 들어 현관으로 들어가 계단 위쪽을 향해 소리쳤다. \"다 괜찮습니다, 제가 여기 있어요, 애프리가 등불을 가지고 올라갈 겁니다.\" 그러고는 모자를 다시 쓰고 있는, 허둥대던 그 여인에게 말했다. \"어서 나가, 위층으로 올라가!\" 그런 다음 낯선 사내에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"자, 선생, 무슨 용건이신지요?\"\n\n\"송구스럽게도,\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"촛불을 하나 청해야 할 만큼 폐를 끼쳐야겠군요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 제러마이아가 수긍했다. \"막 그러려던 참이었습니다. 제가 가져올 동안 거기 그대로 서 계시지요.\"\n\n손님은 문간에 서 있었으나, 플린트윈치 씨가 몸을 돌리자 집 안 어둠 속으로 조금 몸을 틀어, 그가 인광 성냥갑을 더듬어 찾는 작은 방까지 눈으로 그를 좇았다. 마침내 성냥갑을 찾았을 때 그것은 눅눅했거나 그도 아니면 망가진 상태였다. 그어 대는 성냥마다 더듬거리는 그의 얼굴 둘레로 흐릿한 불빛을 던지고 그의 손에 작고 창백한 불꽃 점들을 흩뿌릴 만큼만 타오를 뿐, 촛불을 붙일 만큼은 타지 않았다. 낯선 사내는 그의 얼굴을 단속적으로 비추는 이 빛을 틈타 그를 골똘하고 의아한 눈으로 바라보았다. 제러마이아는 마침내 촛불을 붙였을 때, 자신의 표정에서 큰 비중을 차지하는 그 미심쩍은 미소로 얼굴이 펴지면서, 음험하게 경계하던 마지막 그늘이 사라지는 것을 보고서, 사내가 그러고 있었음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"수고스럽겠지만,\" 제러마이아가 집 문을 닫고 이번엔 자기 차례로 미소 짓는 손님을 꽤 날카롭게 훑어보며 말했다. \"제 사무실로 들어가시지요.— 다 괜찮대도요!\" 애프리가 거기서 어르는 목소리로 달래고 있는데도 여전히 진정되지 않은 위층의 목소리에 답하느라 그는 짜증스레 말을 끊었다. \"다 괜찮다고 하지 않았소? 맙소사, 저 여편네는 분별이라곤 도무지 없단 말이야!\"\n\n\"겁이 많으신가 봅니다.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"겁이 많다고요?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 촛불을 들고 앞서 가면서 받아치려 고개를 돌리며 말했다. \"단언컨대, 백 명 중 아흔 명보다 더 용감한 분입니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"병약하신데도요?\"\n\n\"여러 해 병약하셨지요. 클레넘 부인. 이제 이 상회에 그 이름으로 남은 유일한 분이지요. 제 동업자입니다.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 현관을 가로지르며, 이 시각엔 본래 손님을 들이는 법이 없이 늘 문을 닫아 두는 터라는 식으로 무언가 변명조의 말을 하면서, 충분히 사무적인 모양새를 갖춘 자기 사무실로 앞장서 들어갔다. 그는 책상에 불을 내려놓고, 입을 한껏 비틀며 낯선 사내에게 말했다. \"분부하시지요.\"\n\n\"제 이름은 블랑두아입니다.\"\n\n\"블랑두아. 모르는 이름이군요.\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n\"혹시\" 상대가 말을 이었다. \"파리에서 통지를 받으셨을지도 모른다고 생각했는데—\"\n\n\"블랑두아라는 이름을 가진 그 누구에 관해서도 파리에서 받은 통지는 없습니다.\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n\"없다고요?\"\n\n\"없습니다.\"\n제러마이아는 좋아하는 자세로 서 있었다. 미소 짓던 블랑두아 씨는 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣으려고 외투를 열다가 잠시 멈추고, 플린트윈치 씨가 보기에 너무 가까이 붙어 있는 듯한 그 반짝이는 눈에 웃음을 띤 채 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 내 친구 하나와 어찌나 닮았는지! 정말로 어스름 속에서 잠시나마 당신을 그 사람으로 착각했을 만큼 똑같지는 않습니다만 — 그 점은 사과드려야겠군요. 사과드리게 해 주십시오. 제 잘못을 기꺼이 인정하는 것이, 부디 바라건대, 제 성품의 솔직함의 한 부분이지요 — 그래도 여전히, 비상하게 닮았습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까?\" 제러마이아가 심술궂게 말했다. \"하지만 저는 블랑두아라는 이름을 가진 그 누구에 관해서도 어디서든 통지 편지를 받은 바가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n클레넘 상회의 거래처들이 보인 이 누락에도 블랑두아 씨는 조금도 당황하지 않고, 가슴 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내어 그 안에서 편지 한 통을 골라 플린트윈치 씨에게 건넸다. \"필체는 잘 아실 겁니다. 아마 편지가 스스로 말해 줄 테니 따로 통지가 필요 없겠지요. 이런 일에 대해선 저보다 당신이 훨씬 유능한 판단가시지요. 제가 사업가라기보다 세상에서 (제멋대로) 신사라 부르는 부류인 것이 제 불운입니다.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 편지를 받아 읽었다. 파리 날짜로, \"당 상사가 깊이 존경하는 거래처를 대신하여, 본 시의 블랑두아 씨를 귀하께 소개합니다.\" 운운, \"그가 필요로 할 만한 편의와 귀하의 능력이 닿는 한의 배려를\" 운운, \"또한 덧붙이건대, 블랑두아 씨의 어음을 일람 즉시, 가령 오십 파운드(50파운드)까지 응해 주신다면\" 운운.\n\n\"매우 좋습니다, 선생.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"의자에 앉으시지요. 우리 상회가 할 수 있는 한 무엇이든 — 우리는 한적하고 구식이며 견실한 방식으로 사업을 하는 곳입니다, 선생 — 기꺼이 최선의 도움을 드리겠습니다. 이 날짜로 보아, 아직 통지를 받을 수 없었던 게로군요. 아마 통지를 전하는 지연된 우편물과 함께 건너오신 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"제가 지연된 우편물과 함께 건너온 것은, 선생,\" 블랑두아 씨가 우뚝 갈고리 진 코를 따라 하얀 손을 쓸어내리며 답했다. \"제 머리와 위장의 대가로 알고 있지요. 그 진저리 나고 견딜 수 없는 날씨가 둘 다 들쑤셔 놓았으니까요. 반 시간 전 배에서 내렸을 때의 그 몰골 그대로의 저를 보고 계신 겁니다. 몇 시간 전에는 도착했어야 했고, 그랬다면 이렇게 터무니없는 시간에 불쑥 찾아와 — 사과드리게 해 주십시오 — 위층 병실에 계신 존경하는 부인, 클레넘 부인을 놀라게 — 아니, 그러고 보니 놀라게 한 건 아니라 하셨지요. 다시 사과드리게 해 주십시오 — 그런 일은 없었겠지요.\"\n\n허세와 으스대는 겸양이라는 게 워낙 위력이 커서, 플린트윈치 씨는 벌써 이 사람을 대단히 신사다운 인물로 여기기 시작했다. 그렇다고 그에게 조금도 누그러지지 않은 채, 그는 턱을 긁적이며 이 영업 시간 외에 오늘 밤 블랑두아 씨를 위해 무엇을 해 드리는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠느냐고 말했다.\n\n\"이런!\" 그 신사가 외투 두른 어깨를 으쓱하며 답했다. \"옷을 갈아입고, 먹고 마시고, 어디든 묵어야지요. 부디 친절을 베푸셔서, 일면식도 없는 제게 어디가 좋을지 일러 주십시오. 돈은 내일까지 전혀 문제 될 게 없습니다. 가까울수록 좋고요. 바로 옆집이라면 더 바랄 게 없지요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨가 느릿느릿 입을 떼었다. \"당신 같은 습성을 지닌 신사분께는, 이 근방 가까이에 호텔이라곤—\" 그때 블랑두아 씨가 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"제 습성이라니, 무슨 말씀을! 이보십시오,\" 그가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 말했다. \"세계의 시민에게는 습성이라는 게 없습니다. 제가 보잘것없는 나름대로 신사인 것은, 맹세코! 부인하지 않겠습니다만, 까다롭고 편협한 습성 따위는 없습니다. 깨끗한 방, 저녁에 따뜻한 요리 한 접시, 그리고 아주 독약은 아닌 포도주 한 병이면 오늘 밤 제가 원하는 전부입니다. 다만 그걸 얻겠다고 불필요하게 한 치라도 더 걸어 다니는 수고는 하고 싶지 않을 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"있긴 합니다.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 잠시 블랑두아 씨의 잠시도 가만있지 않는 번뜩이는 눈과 마주치며 평소보다 더 신중하게 말했다. \"여기서 아주 가까운 곳에 커피점 겸 선술집이 하나 있는데, 그 정도라면 추천할 만합니다. 다만 격식이라곤 없는 곳이지요.\"\n\n\"격식 따위 사양합니다!\" 블랑두아 씨가 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"그 집을 보여 주시고 (제가 너무 폐가 되지 않는다면) 거기 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 무한히 감사하겠습니다.\"\n\n이 말에 플린트윈치 씨는 모자를 찾아 들고, 다시 블랑두아 씨를 위해 현관을 가로질러 불을 밝혔다. 어두운 낡은 벽판이 거의 촛불 끄개 노릇이라도 하는 벽 받침대에 촛불을 올려놓다가, 그는 위층으로 올라가 환자에게 오 분도 안 되어 돌아오겠다고 일러 두어야겠다는 생각이 들었다.\n\n\"부탁드리건대,\" 손님이 그 말에 응해 말했다. \"제 방문 명함을 전해 주십시오. 더불어 이렇게 전해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주십시오. 클레넘 부인을 찾아뵙고 직접 인사 올리며, 이 고요한 구석에 소동을 일으킨 데 대해 사과드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고요. 다만 제가 젖은 옷을 갈아입고 무언가 먹고 마셔 기운을 차린 뒤에, 부인께서 잠시 낯선 자의 존재를 견뎌 주시는 게 편의에 맞으신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n제러마이아는 서둘러 다녀와서 돌아오자 말했다. \"부인께서 기꺼이 뵙겠다 하십니다, 선생. 다만 당신의 병실에 끌릴 만한 데가 없음을 잘 아시는 터라, 혹 마음이 바뀌시거든 그 제안에 매이지 않으셔도 된다고 전하라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n\"마음이 바뀐다는 건,\" 정중한 블랑두아가 답했다. \"숙녀를 가벼이 여기는 일이요, 숙녀를 가벼이 여기는 것은 여성에 대한 기사도가 모자란 것이며, 여성에 대한 기사도는 제 성품의 한 부분입니다!\" 이렇게 자기 뜻을 밝힌 그는 흠뻑 젖어 처진 외투 자락을 어깨 너머로 휙 넘기고 플린트윈치 씨를 따라 선술집으로 향했다. 가는 길에 대문 바깥쪽에서 그의 여행 가방을 들고 기다리던 짐꾼 하나를 데리고 갔다.\n\n그 집은 수수하게 꾸려지고 있었고, 블랑두아 씨의 겸양은 끝이 없었다. 과부인 안주인과 두 딸이 그를 맞이한 작은 바는 그로 인해 불편하리만치 가득 차 보였고, 처음 그를 들이려 했던, 베가텔 놀이판이 놓인 비좁은 판벽 방은 그에게 한참 비좁았으며, 끝내 그에게 내준 가족의 작은 명절용 사실(私室)도 그가 들어서니 완전히 압도되어 버렸다. 여기, 마른 옷에 향내 나는 속옷 차림으로, 머리는 매끈하게 빗어 넘기고 두 집게손가락마다 큼직한 반지를 끼고 묵직한 시곗줄을 드러낸 채, 블랑두아 씨는 저녁 식사를 기다리며 무릎을 끌어당기고 창가 자리에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데, (장식한 보석의 차림새가 아무리 다르다 한들) 한때 마르세유의 흉악한 지하 감방에서 쇠창살 돌 턱에 누워 아침을 그렇게 기다리던 어느 리고 씨와 무섭도록, 또 기이하리만치 닮아 보였다.\n\n저녁 식사에서의 그의 탐욕 또한 아침 식사에서 보였던 리고 씨의 탐욕과 빼닮아 있었다. 먹을 것을 죄다 제 주위로 그러모으고는, 어떤 것은 턱으로 게걸스레 먹으면서 다른 것은 눈으로 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 그 인색한 태도가 똑같았다. 여자 취향의 작고 앙증맞은 가구를 함부로 내던지고, 좋아하는 방석을 발밑에 깔아 더 푹신하게 쉬려 하며, 그 큼직한 몸뚱이와 커다란 검은 머리로 섬세한 덮개를 짓이기는 데서 드러난, 타인에 대한 그 철저한 무시도 그 밑바닥엔 똑같은 짐승 같은 이기심이 깔려 있었다. 접시들 사이를 그토록 분주히 오가던 그 부드럽게 움직이는 손은, 쇠창살을 움켜쥐던 그 손이 지녔던 옛 사악한 능란함을 그대로 지니고 있었다. 그리고 더는 먹을 수 없게 되어 섬세한 손가락을 하나씩 빨고는 천에 닦으며 앉아 있을 때, 그 그림을 완성하는 데 모자란 것이라곤 포도나무 잎사귀를 갖다 놓는 것뿐이었다.\n\n위로 치켜 올라간 콧수염과 아래로 내려온 코로 그 더없이 사악한 미소를 짓고, 마치 염색한 머리에 딸린 듯이, 빛을 반사하는 타고난 능력마저 비슷한 어떤 처리로 멎어 버린 듯한 그 겉만 번드르르한 눈을 지닌 이 사내에게, 언제나 진실하고 결코 헛되이 일하지 않는 자연은, 조심하라! 하는 표지를 새겨 두었다. 그 경고가 헛되이 그쳤다 한들 그것은 자연의 잘못이 아니다. 자연은 그런 어떤 경우에도 결코 탓할 수 없다.\n\n블랑두아 씨는 식사를 마치고 손가락을 닦은 뒤 주머니에서 시가를 꺼내, 다시 창가 자리에 누워 느긋하게 끝까지 피웠다. 가느다란 입술에서 가느다란 줄기로 갈라져 나가는 연기에 이따금 말을 건네면서.\n\n\"블랑두아, 너는 세상에 한 방 먹여 줄 거야, 내 어린 아이야. 하하! 거룩한 푸른 하늘이여, 시작이 좋구나, 블랑두아! 막판에 가서는, 영어든 프랑스어든 가르치는 훌륭한 선생이요, 어느 집안의 품에서든 환영받을 사내지! 너는 눈치가 빠르고, 유머가 있고, 여유가 있고, 사람을 파고드는 매너가 있고, 외모가 좋다. 요컨대 너는 신사야! 너는 신사로 살 거야, 내 꼬마야, 그리고 신사로 죽을 거야. 판이 어찌 돌아가든 너는 이길 거야. 그들 모두가 네 진가를 인정할 거야, 블랑두아. 너를 모질게 짓밟은 그 사회를 네 드높은 기개 앞에 굴복시킬 거야. 내 영혼이 죽을 일이로다! 너는 권리로도, 천성으로도 기개가 높은 사람이야, 나의 블랑두아!\"\n\n이런 자위의 중얼거림과 함께 이 신사는 시가를 끝까지 피우고 포도주 한 병을 다 비웠다. 둘 다 끝나자 그는 몸을 흔들어 앉은 자세로 고쳐 앉고는, 마무리 짓는 진지한 한마디로, \"자, 그럼! 블랑두아, 이 영리한 사람아, 정신 바짝 차려라!\" 하고는 일어나 클레넘 상회의 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n문간에서 그를 맞은 것은 애프리 부인이었는데, 그녀는 주인의 지시대로 현관에 촛불 두 개를, 계단에 세 번째 촛불을 켜 두었고, 그를 클레넘 부인의 방으로 안내했다. 그곳에는 차가 준비되어 있었고, 예고된 손님을 맞이할 때 으레 따르던 약간의 손님맞이 차림이 갖추어져 있었다. 그것은 아무리 큰 일이라 해도 변변찮은 것이어서, 중국제 다기를 내오고 침대를 수수하고 침울한 휘장으로 덮는 정도를 넘지 않았다. 그 밖에는 받침대를 올려놓은 관(棺) 같은 소파와, 마치 처형을 위해 차려입기라도 한 듯 미망인 옷을 입은 인물이 있었고, 눅눅한 잿더미를 얹은 난로와, 또 한 무더기의 작은 잿더미가 쌓인 쇠살대와, 주전자, 그리고 검은 염료 냄새가 있었다. 모든 것이 십오 년 동안 그래 왔던 그대로였다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 클레넘 상회의 배려를 부탁받은 그 신사를 소개했다. 편지를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있던 클레넘 부인은 고개를 숙이며 그에게 앉기를 청했다. 두 사람은 서로를 아주 유심히 바라보았다. 그것은 그저 자연스러운 호기심이었다.\n\n\"저 같은 불구의 여인을 떠올려 주셔서 감사합니다, 선생. 사업차 이곳을 찾는 이들 가운데 이토록 남의 눈에서 멀어진 사람을 기억해 주는 이는 거의 없지요. 그들에게 그러기를 기대하는 것 자체가 부질없는 일입니다. 눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어지는 법이니까요. 저는 그 예외에 감사하면서도, 그 통례를 탓하지는 않습니다.\"\n\n블랑두아 씨는 더없이 신사다운 태도로, 이런 무례한 시각에 불운하게도 불쑥 찾아와 부인을 어지럽힌 게 아닐까 두렵다고 했다. 그 점에 대해선 이미 — 죄송합니다만 — 이름은 영광스럽게도 아직 알지 못하는 그분께 최선의 사과를 올렸노라고 했다.\n\n\"플린트윈치 씨는 여러 해 동안 이 상회와 관계해 온 분입니다.\"\n\n블랑두아 씨는 플린트윈치 씨의 더없이 순종적이고 겸손한 종이었다. 그는 플린트윈치 씨에게 자신의 가장 깊은 경의의 표시를 받아 달라고 간청했다.\n\n\"제 남편이 세상을 떠나고,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"제 아들이 다른 길을 택한 까닭에, 우리 옛 상회를 대표하는 사람은 이제 플린트윈치 씨뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신은 자신을 뭐라 부릅니까?\" 그 신사가 퉁명스레 따져 물었다. \"당신은 두 사람 몫의 머리를 가졌는데.\"\n\n\"제 성별이 자격을 박탈하지요,\" 그녀는 제러마이아 쪽으로 눈을 살짝 돌리며 말을 이었다. \"설령 능력이 있다 한들, 사업에서 책임 있는 역할을 맡는 데 말입니다. 그래서 플린트윈치 씨가 제 몫을 자기 몫과 합쳐 이끌어 나가지요. 예전 같지는 않습니다만, 옛 친구들 가운데 더러는 (주로 이 편지를 쓴 분들이지요) 친절히도 우리를 잊지 않으시고, 우리는 그분들이 맡기는 일을 예전 못지않게 능률적으로 해낼 힘을 지니고 있습니다. 하지만 이런 건 당신께 흥미로운 일이 아니겠지요. 영국분이십니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"이런, 부인, 아닙니다. 저는 영국에서 나지도, 자라지도 않았습니다. 사실 저는 어느 나라 사람도 아니지요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 다리를 쭉 뻗어 탁 치며 말했다. \"저는 예닐곱 나라의 피를 이어받았습니다.\"\n\n\"세상을 두루 떠돌아 다니셨군요?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다. 맹세코, 부인, 저는 여기도 저기도 어디든 가 보았습니다!\"\n\n\"아마 매인 데가 없으시겠군요. 결혼은 안 하셨나요?\"\n\n\"부인,\" 블랑두아 씨가 눈썹을 흉하게 늘어뜨리며 말했다. \"저는 여성을 숭배합니다만, 결혼은 안 했습니다 — 한 적도 없지요.\"\n\n그의 옆 탁자에 서서 차를 따르고 있던 애프리 부인은, 몽롱한 상태에서 마침 그가 이 말을 할 때 그를 쳐다보다가, 그의 눈에서 어떤 표정을 잡아챈 듯한 느낌이 들었는데, 그 표정이 제 눈을 끌어당겨 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없었다. 이 느낌의 결과로 그녀는 찻주전자를 손에 든 채 그를 빤히 쳐다보게 되었고, 이는 자신에게도 몹시 거북했을 뿐 아니라 명백히 그에게도 그러했으며, 두 사람을 통해 클레넘 부인과 플린트윈치 씨에게까지 그러했다. 그리하여 모두가 까닭도 모른 채 어지러이 쳐다보는 으스스한 몇 순간이 이어졌다.\n\n\"애프리,\" 마침내 그녀의 안주인이 먼저 입을 열었다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 애프리 부인이 빈 왼손을 손님 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했다. \"제가 아니에요. 저 사람이에요!\"\n\n\"이 선량한 여인이 무슨 말을 하는 거요?\" 블랑두아 씨가 새파랗게, 또 시뻘겋게 질려 외치며, 그 말의 가벼운 어조와는 놀라우리만치 대조되는 무시무시한 분노의 표정으로 천천히 일어섰다. \"이 선량한 사람의 말을 어찌 알아들을 수 있단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"알아들을 수 없지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 그쪽으로 잽싸게 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"자기가 무슨 소릴 하는지도 몰라요. 머리가 좀 모자란 데다 정신이 헤매는 여편네입니다. 약을 먹여야지, 한 첩 톡톡히 먹여야겠어! 어서 나가, 이 사람아.\" 그가 그녀의 귀에 대고 덧붙였다. \"당신이 애프리인 줄 알 때, 효모 반죽처럼 흔들려 버리기 전에 어서 나가라고.\"\n\n애프리 부인은 자기 정체가 처한 위험을 알아채고, 남편이 찻주전자를 낚아채자 그것을 내주고는, 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 눈 깜짝할 새에 사라져 버렸다. 손님은 차츰 미소를 띠더니 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"저 사람을 용서하십시오, 블랑두아 씨.\" 제러마이아가 직접 차를 따르며 말했다. \"쇠약해져서 무너져 가고 있어요. 그게 저 사람의 형편입니다. 설탕은 넣으십니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다만, 저는 차는 됐습니다.— 이런 말씀 드려 무례합니다만, 그건 참으로 진귀한 시계로군요!\"\n\n찻상은 소파 가까이로 끌어다 놓여, 클레넘 부인의 전용 탁자와는 약간의 간격을 두고 있었다. 블랑두아 씨는 정중하게도 그 부인에게 차를 건네려 일어섰고 (그녀의 토스트 접시는 이미 거기 있었다), 그녀의 손 닿기 좋은 곳에 찻잔을 놓다가, 늘 그렇듯 그녀 앞에 놓여 있던 그 시계가 그의 눈길을 끌었다. 클레넘 부인이 불쑥 그를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"좀 봐도 될까요? 감사합니다. 멋진 구식 시계로군요.\" 그가 그것을 손에 들며 말했다. \"쓰기엔 무겁지만, 묵직하고 진짜배기입니다. 저는 진짜인 것은 무엇이든 좋아하지요. 이런 저 자신도 진짜배기입니다. 하! 옛날식으로 케이스가 두 겹인 신사용 시계로군요. 바깥 케이스에서 빼내도 될까요? 감사합니다. 어라? 구슬로 수놓은 낡은 비단 시계 안감이로군요! 옛 네덜란드 사람들과 벨기에 사람들 사이에서 이런 걸 자주 보았습니다. 진기한 물건이지요!\"\n\n\"그것도 구식이지요.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"무척이나요. 그런데 이건 시계만큼 오래되진 않은 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"그렇겠지요.\"\n\n\"이런 머리글자들을 어쩌면 이리도 복잡하게 만들어 놓았던지!\" 블랑두아 씨가 다시 자기 특유의 미소를 띠며 올려다보고 말했다. \"자, 이게 D. N. F.인가요? 거의 무엇이든 될 수 있겠는데요.\"\n\n\"그 글자들이 맞습니다.\"\n\n이 내내 찻잔을 손에 들고 안의 내용물을 삼킬 준비로 입을 벌린 채 유심히 멈춰 있던 플린트윈치 씨가 마침내 마시기 시작했다. 그는 늘 입을 가득 채운 다음에야 한 모금에 비워 냈고, 다시 채우기 전에는 늘 한참 뜸을 들였다.\n\n\"D. N. F.는 분명 어느 다정하고 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 미인이었겠지요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 케이스를 도로 탁 닫으며 말했다. \"그렇다 치고 저는 그 추억을 숭배합니다. 제 마음의 평안에는 안된 일이지만, 저는 너무도 쉽게 숭배에 빠지지요. 그것이 악덕일 수도, 미덕일 수도 있겠으나, 여성의 아름다움과 가치에 대한 숭배가 제 성품의 네 부분 가운데 셋을 이룬답니다, 부인.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 이때쯤 또 한 잔의 차를 따라, 환자에게 눈길을 둔 채 아까처럼 모금모금 삼키고 있었다.\n\n\"여기서라면 당신의 마음은 자유로워도 되겠군요, 선생.\" 그녀가 블랑두아 씨에게 답했다. \"제 생각엔, 그 글자들은 어떤 이름의 머리글자를 뜻하는 게 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좌우명의 머리글자겠군요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 무심코 말했다.\n\n\"한 문장이지요. 제가 알기로 그것은 늘 '잊지 마라!'를 뜻해 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 당연히,\" 블랑두아 씨가 시계를 도로 놓고 본래 앉았던 의자로 물러서며 말했다. \"당신은 잊지 않으시겠군요.\"\n\n차를 다 마신 플린트윈치 씨는 여태껏보다 더 긴 한 모금을 들이켰을 뿐 아니라, 그다음의 뜸을 새로운 자세로 들였다. 다시 말해, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 잔을 입에 댄 채 그대로 두고서 눈은 여전히 환자를 향한 것이다. 그녀는 신중한 어조의 힘으로 답하면서, 그녀의 경우에 다른 사람이라면 몸짓과 행동으로 드러냈을 것을 대신하는, 그 강인한 얼굴과 굳건함 혹은 완고함을 그러모으는 집중된 기색을 보였다.\n\n\"아니요, 선생, 저는 잊지 않습니다. 여러 해 동안 저처럼 단조로운 삶을 사는 것은 잊는 길이 아니지요. 자기 교정의 삶을 사는 것은 잊는 길이 아닙니다. (우리 모두가, 우리 한 사람 한 사람, 아담의 모든 자손이 그러하듯이!) 속죄할 죄와 이루어야 할 화해가 있음을 자각하는 것은, 잊고자 하는 마음을 정당화해 주지 않습니다. 그러므로 저는 오래전에 그 마음을 떨쳐 버렸고, 저는 잊지도 않거니와 잊기를 바라지도 않습니다.\"\n\n근래 찻잔 바닥의 찌꺼기를 빙빙 돌리고 있던 플린트윈치 씨는 여기서 그것을 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 다 마쳤다는 듯 잔을 찻쟁반에 내려놓고, 마치 그 점을 어찌 생각하느냐고 묻기라도 하듯 블랑두아 씨에게 눈을 돌렸다.\n\n\"전부 다 담겨 있었지요, 부인.\" 블랑두아 씨가 더없이 매끄러운 절과 함께 하얀 손을 가슴에 얹으며 말했다. \"제가 영광스럽게도 충분한 이해력과 안목을 (안목이 없다면 저는 블랑두아일 수 없지요) 갖추어 쓴 그 '당연히'라는 말 한마디에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 선생.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"쾌락과 변화와 예절을 좇으며, 떠받들고 또 떠받들어지는 데 익숙한 신사분이—\"\n\n\"오, 부인! 맹세코!\"\n\n\"—그런 분이 제 처지에서 제게 속한 것을 온전히 이해할 가능성을 제가 의심한다 해서 말입니다. 당신께 교리를 강요하려는 건 아닙니다만,\" 그녀는 앞에 놓인 단단하고 빛바랜 책들의 뻣뻣한 더미를 바라보았다. \"(당신은 당신의 길을 가시고, 그 결과는 당신 자신의 머리 위에 있으니까요) 이 정도는 말씀드리겠습니다. 저는 인도자들을 따라, 엄격히 검증되고 시험된 인도자들을 따라 제 항로를 잡아 나가는데, 그분들 아래서라면 저는 결코 난파할 수 없고 — 결코 그럴 수 없으며 — 만일 제가 그 세 글자에 담긴 훈계를 마음에 두지 않았다면, 저는 지금의 절반만큼도 단련되지 못했을 거라고요.\"\n\n그녀가 그 기회를 붙잡아 보이지 않는 어떤 적수와 논쟁을 벌이는 모습은 묘했다. 어쩌면 늘 자기 자신과 자신의 기만으로 향하는, 자기 안의 더 나은 분별과의 논쟁이었는지도 모른다.\n\n\"만일 건강하고 자유롭던 시절의 제 무지함들을 제가 잊는다면, 지금 제가 처한 이 삶을 두고 불평할 수도 있겠지요. 저는 결코 그러지 않습니다. 결코 그래 본 적이 없습니다. 만일 이 무대, 곧 이 땅이, 그 흙으로 빚어진 피조물들에게 음울과 고난과 어두운 시련의 무대가 되도록 분명히 정해졌음을 제가 잊는다면, 그 헛된 것들에 얼마간의 애정을 품을 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 저는 그런 애정이 없습니다. 만일 우리가, 우리 모두가, 반드시 채워져야 하고 한낱 행위 따위로는 어찌할 수 없는 어떤 진노의 (지극히 정당하게도 그 진노의) 대상임을 제가 모른다면, 여기 갇힌 저와 저기 저 대문을 지나가는 사람들 사이의 차이를 두고 불평할지도 모르지요. 하지만 저는 여기서 제가 치르고 있는 이 보속을 치르도록, 여기서 제가 확실히 아는 것을 알도록, 여기서 제가 풀어낸 것을 풀어내도록 택함받은 것을 은총이요 은혜로 받아들입니다. 그렇지 않았다면 제 고난은 제게 아무 뜻도 없었을지 모릅니다. 그러므로 저는 아무것도 잊지 않으려 하고, 또 잊지 않습니다. 그러므로 저는 만족하며, 제 처지가 수백만의 처지보다 낫다고 말합니다.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 그녀는 시계에 손을 얹어, 작은 탁자 위 그것이 늘 차지하던 바로 그 자리에 도로 놓았다. 손길을 그 위에 머무른 채, 그녀는 그 뒤로 한동안 앉아서, 시계를 한결같이, 또 반쯤 도전하듯이 바라보았다.\n\n이 설명이 이어지는 동안 블랑두아 씨는 두 손으로 골똘히 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며, 부인에게 눈을 박은 채 한껏 주의를 기울이고 있었다. 플린트윈치 씨는 다소 안절부절못하다가, 이제 끼어들었다.\n\n\"자, 자, 자!\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 잘 알았습니다, 클레넘 부인, 경건하고 훌륭하게 말씀하셨어요. 블랑두아 씨는 제가 보기에 경건한 부류는 아닌 듯합니다만.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 선생!\" 그 신사가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 항변했다. \"용서하십시오! 그건 제 성품의 한 부분입니다. 저는 감수성이 풍부하고, 열정적이며, 양심적이고, 상상력이 풍부하지요. 감수성 풍부하고 열정적이며 양심적이고 상상력 풍부한 사람이라면, 플린트윈치 씨, 경건한 사람이거나, 아니면 아무것도 아닌 거지요!\"\n\n그가 의자에서 으스대며 일어나 (이 사내는, 그와 비슷하게 특징지어진 모든 사내가 그러하듯, 무슨 짓을 하든 도가 지나친 것이 특징이었으니, 때로는 그것이 머리카락 한 올 차이일 뿐이라 해도 그러했다) 클레넘 부인에게 작별 인사를 하러 다가갈 때, 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에는 그가 아무것도 아닐지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 의심이 어렸다.\n\n\"병든 늙은 여자의 이기심으로 보이실 테지만, 선생,\" 그러자 그녀가 말했다. \"실은 당신의 우연한 언급 탓에, 저는 저 자신과 제 병약함이라는 주제로 끌려 들어가고 말았습니다. 이토록 사려 깊게 저를 찾아 주셨으니, 부디 그 점도 사려 깊게 눈감아 주시기 바랍니다. 제발 저를 추켜세우지는 마십시오.\" 그가 분명 그러려는 참이었기 때문이다. \"플린트윈치 씨가 기꺼이 어떤 도움이든 드릴 것이며, 이 도시에서의 체류가 즐거운 것이 되기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n블랑두아 씨는 그녀에게 감사하며 여러 번 손에 입을 맞추었다. \"낡은 방이로군요.\" 그가 문 가까이에 이르러 둘러보면서, 별안간 쾌활해진 태도로 말했다. \"너무 흥미로워서 미처 방을 살펴보지 못했습니다. 하지만 진짜배기 낡은 방이군요.\"\n\n\"진짜배기 낡은 집이지요.\" 클레넘 부인이 얼어붙은 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"내세울 것 없는 곳이지만, 오래된 골동품 같은 데지요.\"\n\n\"이런!\" 손님이 외쳤다. \"플린트윈치 씨께서 나가는 길에 방들을 두루 안내해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 더없이 기쁘겠습니다. 낡은 집이라면 제가 사족을 못 씁니다. 약점이야 많습니다만, 이보다 더한 건 없지요. 저는 그림처럼 아름다운 것을 그 온갖 형태로 사랑하고 또 탐구합니다. 저 자신이 그림 같다는 소리도 들어 보았지요. 그림 같다는 게 무슨 미덕은 아닙니다만 — 어쩌면 제겐 더 큰 미덕들이 있겠지요 — 그래도 어쩌다 보니 그럴 수도 있으니까요. 공감, 공감이지요!\"\n\n\"미리 말씀드립니다만, 블랑두아 씨, 무척 칙칙하고 무척 휑하다는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다.\" 제러마이아가 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다. \"보실 가치가 없는 곳이에요.\" 그러나 블랑두아 씨는 그의 등을 다정스레 탁 치며 그저 웃기만 했다. 그리하여 그 블랑두아는 클레넘 부인에게 다시 한번 손에 입을 맞추고는, 두 사람은 함께 방을 나섰다.\n\n\"위층은 보고 싶지 않으시지요?\" 층계참에서 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요, 플린트윈치 씨. 번거롭지 않으시다면, 황홀할 따름이겠습니다!\"\n\n그리하여 플린트윈치 씨는 몸을 비틀며 계단을 올라갔고, 블랑두아 씨가 바짝 뒤따랐다. 그들은 아서가 돌아온 밤에 묵었던 커다란 다락 침실까지 올라갔다. \"자, 블랑두아 씨!\" 제러마이아가 그곳을 보여 주며 말했다. \"이렇게 높이 올라와 볼 가치가 있다고 여기실지 모르겠군요. 솔직히 저는 아닙니다만.\"\n\n블랑두아 씨가 황홀해하기에, 그들은 다른 다락방들과 복도들을 두루 거닐고는 다시 계단을 내려왔다. 이즈음 플린트윈치 씨는, 손님이 어느 방을 보든 한 차례 휙 둘러본 뒤로는 방을 보고 있는 법이 없고 늘 자신을, 곧 플린트윈치 씨를 보고 있음을 알아챘다. 이 발견을 머릿속에 둔 채, 그는 또 한 번 시험해 보려고 계단에서 몸을 돌렸다. 그는 곧장 손님의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그리고 두 사람이 서로 눈을 붙박는 그 순간, 손님은 그 흉한 코와 콧수염의 놀림과 함께, (클레넘 부인의 방을 나선 뒤로 그런 비슷한 순간마다 그래 왔듯이) 악마처럼 소리 없는 웃음을 지었다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 손님보다 훨씬 키가 작은 터라, 이렇게 높은 데서 거북스럽게 내려다보며 곁눈질당하는 신체적 불리함을 안고 있었다. 게다가 그가 앞장서서 계단을 내려가느라 대개 상대보다 한두 칸 아래에 있었으니, 이 불리함은 당시 더욱 커졌다. 그는 두 사람이 고(故) 클레넘 씨의 방으로 들어서서 이 우연한 높이 차가 사라질 때까지 블랑두아 씨를 다시 보는 것을 미루었다. 그러나 그때 별안간 그에게로 몸을 홱 틀어 돌아보니, 그의 눈빛은 변함이 없었다.\n\n\"참으로 감탄스러운 낡은 집이로군요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 미소 지었다. \"어찌나 신비로운지. 여기서 유령이 내는 소리 같은 건 들어 본 적이 없으십니까?\"\n\n\"소리라니요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했다. \"없습니다.\"\n\n\"악마를 본 적도 없고요?\"\n\n\"없지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 질문자에게로 험상궂게 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"적어도 그런 이름과 그런 자격으로 자기를 소개하는 놈은 없었습니다.\"\n\n\"하하! 여기 초상화가 하나 있군요.\"\n\n(여전히 플린트윈치 씨를, 마치 그가 초상화이기라도 한 듯 바라보면서.)\n\n\"보시다시피 초상화지요, 선생.\"\n\n\"누구인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까, 플린트윈치 씨?\"\n\n\"고(故) 클레넘 씨입니다. 부인의 남편이지요.\"\n\n\"그 진귀한 시계의 옛 주인이겠군요?\" 손님이 말했다.\n\n초상화 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸던 플린트윈치 씨가 다시 몸을 비틀어 돌아보니, 또다시 그 똑같은 눈빛과 미소의 대상이 된 자신을 발견했다. \"그렇습니다, 블랑두아 씨.\" 그가 톡 쏘듯 답했다. \"그분 것이었고, 그 전에는 그분 삼촌 것이었고, 그 전에는 또 누구 것이었는지는 하느님이나 아시겠지요. 그 내력에 대해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건 그게 전부입니다.\"\n\n\"위층의 우리 친구분은, 플린트윈치 씨, 개성이 뚜렷한 분이로군요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 선생.\" 제러마이아가, 이 대화 내내 그러했듯, 마치 무는 힘이 모자란 나사 기계처럼 또다시 손님에게로 몸을 비틀며 말했다. 상대는 결코 변하지 않았고, 그는 늘 조금씩 물러서지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다. \"비범한 여인이지요. 대단한 불굴의 정신 — 대단한 마음의 강인함을 지니셨습니다.\"\n\n\"두 분은 무척 행복하셨겠군요.\" 블랑두아가 말했다.\n\n\"누가요?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 또 한 번 그에게 몸을 비틀며 따져 물었다.\n\n블랑두아 씨는 오른 집게손가락을 병실 쪽으로, 왼 집게손가락을 초상화 쪽으로 까딱이고는, 두 팔을 양 허리에 척 짚고 다리를 넓게 벌리고 서서, 나아오는 코와 물러나는 콧수염으로 플린트윈치 씨를 내려다보며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"여느 부부들 대개만큼은 행복했겠지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했다. \"저야 말할 수 없습니다. 모르지요. 어느 집안에나 비밀은 있는 법이니까요.\"\n\n\"비밀이라!\" 블랑두아 씨가 잽싸게 외쳤다. \"한 번 더 말해 보시오, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"제 말은,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했는데, 그가 어찌나 별안간 몸을 부풀렸던지 플린트윈치 씨는 자기 얼굴이 그 부푼 가슴팍에 거의 스칠 지경이었다. \"어느 집안에나 비밀은 있다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고요.\" 상대가 그의 양 어깨를 탁 치며, 그를 앞뒤로 흔들며 외쳤다. \"하하! 당신 말이 옳소. 그렇고말고! 비밀이라니! 거룩한 푸른 하늘이여! 어떤 집안에는 악마의 비밀이라 할 만한 것들이 있지요, 플린트윈치 씨!\" 그러면서 그는, 마치 자기가 한 농담을 두고 다정하고 익살스럽게 놀려 대기라도 하듯 플린트윈치 씨의 양 어깨를 여러 차례 탁탁 치고는, 두 팔을 치켜들고 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 양손을 그 뒤에서 깍지 끼고 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 플린트윈치 씨가 그에게 또 한 번 몸을 비틀어 봐야 소용없었다. 그는 실컷 웃어 댔다.\n\n\"하지만, 잠깐 촛불을 좀 빌려 주시지요.\" 웃음을 그치고 그가 말했다. \"그 비범한 부인의 남편을 한번 봅시다. 하!\" 그가 팔을 쭉 뻗어 불빛을 치켜들었다. \"여기도 얼굴에 단호한 표정이 있군요, 비록 같은 종류는 아닙니다만. 마치 이렇게 말하는 것 같지 않습니까, 그게 뭐였더라 — 잊지 마라 — 라고요, 플린트윈치 씨? 맹세코, 선생, 정말 그렇군요!\"\n\n촛불을 돌려주며 그는 그를 다시 한번 바라보았다. 그러고는 느긋하게 그와 함께 현관으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나오면서, 참으로 매력적인 낡은 집이라고, 자기 마음에 어찌나 쏙 들었던지 백 파운드를 준다 해도 둘러보지 않을 수 없었을 거라고 단언했다.\n\n블랑두아 씨 쪽의 이 기묘한 무람없음이 이어지는 내내 — 그것은 그의 태도 전반을 바꾸어 전보다 훨씬 거칠고 험악하게, 훨씬 난폭하고 대담하게 만들었는데 — 변화가 그리 많을 수 없는 가죽 같은 얼굴의 플린트윈치 씨는 그 부동(不動)함을 고스란히 지켰다. 다만 그 다정한 끌어 내림의 작업 전에 살짝 너무 오래 매달려 있었던 것처럼 이제 보일 뿐, 겉으로는 한결같은 침착함을 유지했다. 그들은 현관 옆 작은 방에서 둘러보기를 마쳤고, 그는 거기 서서 블랑두아 씨를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"그토록 만족하시니 기쁩니다, 선생.\" 그의 차분한 말이었다. \"기대치 못했던 일이군요. 무척 기분이 좋아 보이십니다.\"\n\n\"감탄스러우리만치 좋지요.\" 블랑두아가 답했다. \"맹세코! 이보다 더 기분이 상쾌했던 적이 없습니다. 당신은 예감이라는 걸 가져 보신 적이 있습니까, 플린트윈치 씨?\"\n\n\"그 말로 무엇을 뜻하시는지 제가 안다고 장담하긴 어렵군요, 선생.\" 그 신사가 답했다.\n\n\"이 경우라면, 플린트윈치 씨, 앞으로 다가올 기쁨에 대한 형체 없는 예상이라고 해 두지요.\"\n\n\"지금으로선 그런 느낌을 느끼고 있다고는 말할 수 없군요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 더없이 진지하게 답했다. \"그게 다가오는 걸 느끼게 되면, 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 나는,\" 블랑두아가 말했다. \"나는, 이 사람아, 오늘 밤 우리가 서로 잘 알게 되리라는 예감이 든단 말이오. 그게 다가오는 게 느껴지지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"아—아니요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 신중하게 자신에게 물어보며 답했다. \"느껴진다고는 말 못 하겠군요.\"\n\n\"나는 우리가 아주 가까운 사이가 되리라는 강한 예감이 드는데. — 당신은 아직 그런 느낌이 없습니까?\"\n\n\"아직은요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다.\n\n블랑두아 씨는 또다시 그의 양 어깨를 붙잡고 아까의 그 유쾌한 투로 그를 조금 흔들어 대더니, 자기 팔을 그의 팔에 꿰고는, 점잖고 속 깊은 늙은 친구답게 나가서 포도주나 한 병 마시자고 청했다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그 청을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 해 질 녘부터 줄곧 창과 지붕과 포도(鋪道)를 두드려 대던 거센 빗속을 뚫고, 그 나그네가 묵는 거처로 나갔다. 천둥과 번개는 오래전에 지나갔으나, 비는 사납게 퍼부었다. 블랑두아 씨의 방에 이르자, 그 정중한 신사는 포트와인 한 병을 시켰다. 그러고는 (제 맵시 있는 몸뚱이의 부드러운 처분으로, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 앙증맞은 물건을 죄다 짓이기며) 창가 자리에 몸을 둘둘 말고 앉았고, 플린트윈치 씨는 그 맞은편에 탁자를 사이에 두고 의자를 차지했다. 블랑두아 씨가 그 집에서 가장 큰 잔을 가져오자고 제안하자 플린트윈치 씨도 동의했다. 잔이 가득 채워지자, 블랑두아 씨는 떠들썩한 흥겨움으로 제 잔의 윗부분을 플린트윈치 씨 잔의 밑부분에, 또 제 잔의 밑부분을 플린트윈치 씨 잔의 윗부분에 쨍그랑 부딪치고는, 자신이 예견하는 가까운 사이를 위해 건배했다. 플린트윈치 씨는 진지하게 화답하여, 손에 넣을 수 있는 포도주는 모조리 마셨으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 블랑두아 씨가 잔을 부딪칠 때마다 (그것은 채워질 때마다였다), 플린트윈치 씨는 무덤덤하게 제 몫의 부딪침을 해냈고, 입맛이라는 점만 빼면 그저 한 통의 술통이나 다름없어서, 제 몫의 포도주뿐 아니라 동행의 몫까지도 무덤덤하게 다 마셔 치울 기세였다.\n\n요컨대 블랑두아 씨는, 과묵한 플린트윈치에게 포트와인을 부어 넣는 일이 그를 여는 게 아니라 도리어 닫아 버리는 일임을 알게 되었다. 게다가 그는 밤새도록이든, 필요하다면 다음 날 내내, 또 다음 날 밤 내내든 계속할 수 있는 완벽한 능력을 갖춘 듯한 기색이었다. 반면 블랑두아 씨는 이내 자신이 지나치게 거세고 허풍스럽게 으스대고 있음을 어렴풋이 깨닫게 되었다. 그리하여 그는 세 번째 병이 끝나자 그 자리를 마무리 지었다.\n\n\"내일 우리 앞으로 어음을 끊으시지요, 선생.\" 헤어지면서 플린트윈치 씨가 사무적인 얼굴로 말했다.\n\n\"내 양배추 같은 양반,\" 상대가 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 잡으며 답했다. \"당신 앞으로 끊고말고. 걱정 마시오. 안녕히, 나의 플린트윈치. 헤어지며 받아 두시오.\" 여기서 그는 남국식으로 그를 끌어안고 양 볼에 쩍쩍 입을 맞추었다. \"신사의 약속을! 천 번의 천둥을 걸고, 당신은 나를 다시 보게 될 거요!\"\n\n그는 통지 편지는 제때 도착했음에도 이튿날에는 모습을 드러내지 않았다. 밤에 그의 소식을 수소문하던 플린트윈치 씨는, 그가 셈을 치르고 칼레를 거쳐 대륙으로 돌아가 버렸음을 놀라움 속에 알게 되었다. 그럼에도 제러마이아는 곰곰이 따져 보는 제 얼굴에서, 블랑두아 씨가 이번만큼은 약속을 지켜 다시 나타나리라는 생생한 확신을 긁어내었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. Spirit\n\n누구든 어느 날엔가, 사람들로 붐비는 대도시의 큰길에서, 어떤 비쩍 마르고 주름지고 누런 늙은이를 스쳐 지나갈 수 있다(별들에서 떨어져 내린 것이라고 짐작될 만한 자인데, 하늘에 그토록 미약한 불티를 흘려보냈으리라 의심받을 만큼 흐릿한 별이 있다면 말이다). 그 늙은이는 겁먹은 기색으로, 마치 그 소음과 부산함에 어리둥절하고 조금은 놀란 듯 엉금엉금 길을 따라간다. 이 늙은이는 언제나 자그마한 늙은이다. 한때 큰 늙은이였다 해도 자그마한 늙은이로 오그라들었고, 늘 자그마한 늙은이였다면 더더욱 작은 늙은이로 쪼그라들었다. 그의 외투는 그 빛깔도 마름질도, 어느 곳 어느 시절에도 유행이었던 적이 없는 것이다. 분명 그를 위해 지은 것도, 어떤 개별의 인간을 위해 지은 것도 아니다. 어떤 도매업자가 그런 품질의 외투 오천 벌을 두고 운명에게 치수를 재었고, 운명은 이 낡은 외투를, 끝나지 않을 긴 행렬을 이룬 수많은 늙은이들 가운데 하나에게 빌려주듯 이 늙은이에게 빌려준 것이다. 거기엔 언제나, 다른 어떤 단추와도 닮지 않은 크고 칙칙한 쇠단추가 달려 있다. 이 늙은이는 모자를 쓰고 있는데, 손때가 묻고 보풀이 다 닳았으면서도 고집스러운 모자라, 그의 가엾은 머리 모양에 한 번도 맞춰 준 적이 없다. 그의 거친 셔츠와 거친 목수건도 그의 외투나 모자보다 더 개성이 없다. 그것들은 모두 그의 것이 아니라는—그 누구의 것도 아니라는 똑같은 성격을 지녔다. 그런데도 이 늙은이는 이 옷들을, 마치 평소엔 잠자리 모자와 가운 차림으로 대부분의 시간을 보내다가 큰길에 나서느라 차려입고 꾸민 양, 어딘가 익숙지 않은 분위기로 걸치고 있다. 그리하여, 흉년 두 해째에 시골 쥐가 도시 쥐를 찾아와 고양이들의 도시를 가로질러 도시 쥐의 거처로 겁먹은 채 길을 더듬어 가듯, 이 늙은이는 거리를 지나간다.\n\n가끔 휴일 저녁 무렵이면, 그가 약간 더 비척거리며 걷는 모습을 볼 수 있고, 그의 늙은 두 눈은 축축하고 늪 같은 빛으로 어슴푸레 빛날 것이다. 그럴 때면 그 자그마한 늙은이는 취해 있다. 아주 적은 양으로도 그는 쓰러진다. 반 파인트짜리 잔 하나면 그 불안한 다리가 휘청 무너질 수 있다. 어떤 가엾이 여기는 지인이—흔히는 우연히 만난 지인이—맥주 한턱으로 그의 허약함을 데워 준 것이고, 그 결과 그가 다시 지나가기까지 평소보다 더 긴 시간이 걸리게 된다. 왜냐하면 그 자그마한 늙은이는 구빈원으로 돌아가는 길이고, 행실이 좋을 때면 그들은 그를 자주 내보내 주지 않으며(내 생각엔, 햇빛 아래 나가 볼 날이 그에게 얼마 남지 않았음을 헤아린다면 좀 더 내보내 줄 법도 한데), 행실이 나쁠 때면 마흔아홉 명의 다른 늙은이들이 모인 숲속에 그 어느 때보다 단단히 가두어 버리는데, 그 숲의 늙은이들은 저마다 다른 모두의 냄새를 풍긴다.\n\n플로니시 부인의 아버지는—닳고 닳은 새처럼, 가냘프고 피리 소리 같은 목소리를 내는 가엾은 늙은 신사로, 그가 음악 제본업이라 부르던 일에 종사하다가 큰 불운을 겪었으며, 그 일로 좀처럼 형편을 펼 줄도, 길을 볼 줄도, 갚을 줄도 몰랐고, 그저 막다른 길임을 깨닫는 것 외엔 그 일로 아무것도 할 줄 몰랐던 사람이었다—플로니시 씨를 마셜시 학교로 데려간 그 차압이 매듭지어지자, 제 발로 구빈원으로 물러났다. 그곳은 법에 따라 그가 사는 구역의 선한 사마리아인 노릇을 하도록 정해진 곳이었다(다만 그 두 푼은 없었으니, 그것은 나쁜 정치경제학이었던 것이다). 사위의 곤경이 그 지경에 이르기 전, 노인 낸디는(법적인 은거처에서는 늘 그렇게 불렸으나, 블리딩 하트 사람들 사이에서는 노인 낸디 씨였다) 플로니시 집안 화롯가 한구석에 앉아 플로니시 집안 찬장에서 한 입 베어 먹고 한 모금 마시곤 했다. 그는 운수가 사위에게 미소 지을 때 그 가정 내 자리를 다시 차지하길 여전히 바라고 있었다. 그러는 동안, 운수가 꼼짝 않는 낯을 짓고 있는 한, 그는 한데 어우러진 풍미를 풍기는 자그마한 늙은이들의 숲속에 든 그런 자그마한 늙은이 가운데 하나였으며, 또 그대로 남아 있기로 마음먹고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그에게 깃든 어떤 가난도, 어느 곳 어느 시절에도 유행이었던 적 없는 그의 외투도, 그가 거처로 삼은 그 노인 병동도, 딸의 흠모를 꺼뜨릴 수는 없었다. 플로니시 부인은 아버지의 재능을, 설령 그 재능이 그를 대법관으로 만들어 주었다 해도 그보다 더할 수 없을 만큼 자랑스러워했다. 그녀는 아버지의 태도가 지닌 다정함과 품위를, 설령 그가 시종장이었다 해도 그보다 더 굳게 믿을 수 없을 만큼 굳게 믿었다. 그 가엾은 자그마한 늙은이는, 클로에며 필리스며, 비너스의 아들에게 상처 입은 스트레폰을 노래한, 오래전에 유행이 지난 빛바래고 김빠진 짧은 노래 몇 곡을 알고 있었다. 그리고 플로니시 부인에게는, 아기가 돌리는 약하고 작고 망가진 손풍금처럼 그가 그 가락들을 작게 속에서 떨고 지저귀며 풀어낼 때의 그 소리만큼 오페라에서도 들을 수 없는 음악은 없었다. '나들이 가는 날'이면—다듬어 친 늙은이들의 그 단조로운 풍경 속에 박힌 그 빛 조각들이면—그가 고기를 먹어 기운이 나고 반 페니어치 흑맥주를 가득 들이켰을 때, \"노래 한 곡 해 주세요, 아버지\" 하고 말하는 것이 플로니시 부인의 기쁨이자 슬픔이었다. 그러면 그는 그들에게 클로에를 들려주었고, 기분이 제법 좋을 때면 필리스도 들려주었으며—스트레폰은 은거에 든 뒤로 좀처럼 부를 형편이 못 되었다—그러면 플로니시 부인은 아버지만 한 가수는 정말로 세상에 없었다고 단언하며 눈물을 닦았다.\n\n이런 자리에서 그가 궁정에서 돌아왔다 한들, 아니, 그가 그 고귀한 냉장고 각하라서, 마지막 끔찍한 실패 끝에 외국 궁정에서 의기양양하게 돌아와 알현하고 승진하게 되었다 한들, 플로니시 부인이 그를 블리딩 하트 야드 일대로 끌고 다니며 더 우쭐대게 모실 수는 없었을 것이다. \"여기 우리 아버지예요.\" 이웃에게 그를 소개하며 그녀는 말하곤 했다. \"아버지가 이제 곧 우리 집에 아주 돌아오신답니다. 아버지 안색이 좋아 보이지 않아요? 아버지는 그 어느 때보다 노래를 더 달콤하게 부르신답니다. 방금 한 곡 들으셨다면 잊지 못하셨을 거예요.\" 플로니시 씨로 말하자면, 낸디 씨의 딸과 결혼하면서 이런 신조들도 함께 떠맡은 셈이라, 그토록 재능 있는 늙은 신사가 어찌하여 큰돈을 벌지 못했는지 그저 의아해할 따름이었다. 그는 오래 곱씹은 끝에, 그것을 그의 음악적 천재가 젊은 시절에 학문적으로 계발되지 못한 탓으로 돌렸다. \"그러니까 말이야,\" 플로니시 씨는 논했다. \"제 안에 음악을 갖고 있는데 뭐 하러 음악을 제본하러 다니냐는 거지. 내가 보기엔 거기에 문제가 있어.\"\n\n노인 낸디에게는 후원자가 있었다. 후원자 한 사람. 그에게는 어떤 호화로운 방식으로—변명하는 듯한 방식으로, 마치 자신이 이 늙은이의 단순함과 가난을 헤아려, 사람들이 기대했을 법한 것보다 더 스스럼없이 대할 수밖에 없음을 흠모하는 청중을 끊임없이 증인으로 세우려는 양—그에게 무척 잘해 주는 후원자가 있었다. 노인 낸디는 사위가 마셜시 학교에 짧게 갇혀 있는 동안 그와 연락하느라 여러 차례 그곳에 드나들었고, 그 국가적 기관의 아버지의 후원을 다행히도 손에 넣었으며, 차차 시간이 흐르면서 그 후원을 크게 두텁게 만들어 왔다.\n\n도릿 씨는 이 늙은이를, 마치 늙은이가 어떤 봉건적 차지 관계 아래 자신에게 신하로 속해 있기라도 한 것처럼 맞이하는 버릇이 있었다. 그는 마치 늙은이가 소작인들이 원시적 상태에 있는 어느 변두리 지방에서 충성의 표시를 들고 찾아오기라도 한 양 그를 위해 소소한 대접과 차를 베풀었다. 어떤 순간에는, 이 늙은이가 공로를 세워 충실했던 자신의 오랜 가신이 아니라고는 결코 맹세할 수 없을 것 같은 때가 있는 듯했다. 그는 늙은이를 언급할 때 무심히 자신의 오랜 연금 수급자라 일컬었다. 그는 그를 보는 데에서, 그리고 그가 떠난 뒤 그의 쇠락한 처지를 두고 논평하는 데에서 놀라운 만족을 얻었다. 저 가엾은 자가 그래도 고개를 들고 다닐 수 있다는 것이 그에게는 신기하게만 보였다. \"구빈원이라니, 선생, 연합 구빈원 말이오. 사생활도 없고, 방문객도 없고, 신분도 없고, 존중도 없고, 특별함이라곤 없지. 참으로 통탄할 일이오!\"\n\n노인 낸디의 생일이라, 그들은 그를 내보내 주었다. 그는 생일이라는 말은 한마디도 하지 않았는데, 그랬다간 그들이 그를 가두어 두었을지도 모르기 때문이다. 그런 늙은이들은 태어나지 말았어야 했으니까. 그는 평소처럼 거리를 따라 블리딩 하트 야드로 가서 딸과 사위와 함께 저녁을 먹고, 그들에게 필리스를 들려주었다. 그가 노래를 막 마쳤을 때, 리틀 도릿이 다들 어찌 지내는지 보러 들렀다.\n\n\"도릿 양.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"여기 우리 아버지예요! 멋져 보이시지 않아요? 게다가 목소리가 어찌나 좋으신지!\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 그에게 손을 내밀며, 그를 오랫동안 못 뵈었다고 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그들이 가엾은 아버지에게 좀 야박하게 굴어요.\" 플로니시 부인이 얼굴을 길게 늘이며 말했다. \"아버지께 도움이 될 만큼 바람 쐬고 기분 전환할 시간을 절반도 주질 않는답니다. 하지만 이제 곧 아주 집에 돌아오실 거예요. 그렇죠, 아버지?\"\n\n\"그래, 얘야, 그러길 바란다. 하느님 뜻이라면, 때가 되면 그리되겠지.\"\n\n여기서 플로니시 씨는 그런 기회마다 한 자도 틀림없이 똑같이 늘어놓는 연설을 토해 냈다. 그것은 다음과 같은 말로 이루어졌다.\n\n\"존 에드워드 낸디. 어르신. 지금 이 지붕 아래 먹을 것이든 마실 것이든 무엇이든 한 온스라도 있는 한, 어르신은 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 온전히 있습니다. 지금 이 지붕 아래 불씨 한 줌이든 잠자리 한 입이든 있는 한, 어르신은 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 온전히 있습니다. 만에 하나 지금 이 지붕 아래 아무것도 없게 된다 해도, 어르신은 그것이 많든 적든 무언가가 있을 때와 똑같이 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 있습니다. 이것이 제 뜻이고 그러니 거짓으로 드리는 말씀이 아닙니다, 따라서 결국 남는 결론은 어르신께 간청드리는 것이니, 그러니 어찌 그러지 않으시겠습니까?\"\n\n이 명료한 연설에—플로니시 씨는 늘 자신이 그것을 엄청난 노고를 들여 지어낸 양(틀림없이 그렇긴 했다) 늘어놓았다—플로니시 부인의 아버지는 피리 같은 소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"고맙네, 토머스, 자네 뜻은 잘 아네, 그러니 또한 고맙다는 말일세. 하지만 안 되네, 토머스. 그것이 자네 아이들 입에서 빼앗는 일이 아니게 될 때까지는—빼앗는 일이지, 무어라 이름 붙이든 그건 그대로 남아 똑같이 앗아 가는 일이야, 비록 그날이 오긴 하겠지만, 아무리 빨리 와도 모자랄 그날이 오긴 하겠지만, 안 되네, 토머스, 안 돼!\"\n\n앞치마 한 귀퉁이를 손에 쥐고 얼굴을 조금 돌리고 있던 플로니시 부인이, 도릿 양에게 아버지가 강 건너로 문안을 드리러 가실 참인데 혹시 그게 좋지 않을 무슨 이유라도 아시느냐고 물으며 다시 대화로 돌아왔다.\n\n그녀의 대답은 이러했다. \"저는 곧장 집으로 가는 길이에요. 그러니 같이 가 주신다면 제가 모시고 가게 되어 정말 기쁘겠어요—그분과 함께 가는 게 정말 기뻐요.\" 늘 약한 이들의 마음을 헤아리는 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"보세요, 아버지!\" 플로니시 부인이 외쳤다. \"도릿 양과 함께 산책을 나가시다니, 아버지도 참 멋쟁이 젊은이시네요! 목수건을 제대로 멋진 매듭으로 매 드릴게요, 정말 멋쟁이라는 게 있다면 바로 아버지시니까요.\"\n\n이런 효성 어린 농담과 함께 딸은 그를 말끔하게 단장해 주고는, 사랑스럽게 꼭 끌어안고, 약한 아이를 품에 안고 튼튼한 아이는 층계를 굴러 내려가는 가운데 문간에 서서, 자그마한 늙은 아버지가 리틀 도릿의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고 아장아장 걸어가는 뒷모습을 바라보았다.\n\n그들은 천천히 걸었고, 리틀 도릿은 그를 아이언 브리지로 데려가 거기에 앉혀 쉬게 했으며, 두 사람은 강물을 내려다보며 배들에 관해 이야기를 나누었다. 늙은이는 만약 자기에게 금화를 가득 실은 배가 돌아온다면 무엇을 하겠다고 말했는데(그의 계획은 어느 다과 정원에 플로니시 가족과 자신을 위해 근사한 거처를 얻어, 시중드는 종업원의 시중을 받으며 남은 평생을 그곳에서 사는 것이었다), 그날은 늙은이의 특별한 생일이었다. 목적지까지 오 분 거리도 안 남았을 때, 자기 집 거리 모퉁이에서 두 사람은 새 보닛을 쓰고 같은 항구로 향하던 패니와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 어쩜 이럴 수가, 에이미!\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다. \"설마 그럴 리가!\"\n\n\"무얼 말이야, 패니 언니?\"\n\n\"흥! 너에 대해선 어지간한 건 다 믿을 수 있었지만,\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 활활 타오르는 분노로 응수했다. \"이건, 아무리 너라도, 나조차 믿을 수 없었을 거야!\"\n\n\"패니 언니!\" 리틀 도릿이 상처받고 놀라 외쳤다.\n\n\"아유! 패니 언니 소리 하지 마, 이 못된 것아, 하지 마! 환한 대낮에, 훤히 트인 거리에서, 빈민하고 같이 걸어오다니!\"(마지막 단어를 마치 공기총에서 쏘아 보낸 총알인 양 발사했다.)\n\"아, 패니 언니!\"\n\n\"패니 언니 소리 하지 말라니까, 그런 건 참을 수 없으니까! 이런 일은 본 적이 없어. 기회만 있으면 우리를 망신시키겠다고 작정하고 마음을 다잡는 그 꼴이라니, 정말 파렴치하기 짝이 없어. 이 못된 것!\"\n\n\"이 가엾은 늙은 분을 돌봐 드리는 게,\" 리틀 도릿이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. \"누구한테 망신이 되나요?\"\n\n\"그래, 망신이야, 아가씨.\" 언니가 응수했다. \"너도 그게 망신인 줄 알아야 마땅하고. 너도 그게 망신인 줄 알고 있어, 그게 망신인 줄 아니까 하는 거지. 네 인생의 으뜸가는 즐거움은 가족에게 그 불운을 일깨워 주는 거잖아. 그리고 그다음으로 큰 낙은 천한 사람들과 어울리는 거고. 하지만 어쨌든, 네게 체면이라는 게 없다 해도, 내겐 있어. 나는 길 건너편으로 가게 좀 내버려 둬, 방해받지 않고.\"\n\n이렇게 말하며 그녀는 펄쩍 뛰어 맞은편 보도로 건너갔다. 그 늙은 망신거리는—패니가 입을 열었을 때 리틀 도릿이 놀라 그의 팔을 놓아 버려서—한두 걸음 떨어져 공손히 절을 하고 있었고, 길을 막는다고 성급한 행인들에게 떠밀리고 욕을 먹던 차에, 좀 어지러운 채로 자기 길동무에게 다시 다가와 말했다. \"댁의 존경하는 아버님께 무슨 일이라도 있으신지요, 아가씨? 그 존경하는 가족에게 무슨 탈이라도 난 건 아니길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 응했다. \"아니에요, 고마워요. 다시 제 팔을 잡으세요, 낸디 씨. 이제 곧 도착할 거예요.\"\n\n그래서 그녀는 전처럼 그에게 이야기를 건넸고, 두 사람은 수위실에 이르러 자물쇠 옆에 있는 치버리 씨를 만나 안으로 들어갔다. 그런데 마침, 마셜시의 아버지가 수위실 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오던 그 순간, 두 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 감옥 안으로 들어서며 그곳에서 나오는 길이었다. 그들이 다가오는 광경이 눈에 들어오자, 그는 더할 수 없는 마음의 동요와 낙담을 드러냈으며—모자를 손에 들고 절을 하며 서 있던 노인 낸디는 그 인자한 분 앞에서 늘 그러듯이 서 있었으나 그를 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고—몸을 돌려 제 집 문간으로 급히 들어가 층계를 올라가 버렸다.\n\n불길한 시각에 자기 보호 아래 거두었던 그 늙은 불행한 이를, 곧 돌아오겠다는 다급한 약속과 함께 남겨 둔 채, 리틀 도릿은 아버지를 뒤쫓아 서둘러 갔고, 층계에서 패니가 모욕당한 위엄으로 발끈하며 뒤따라 올라오는 것을 보았다. 세 사람은 거의 동시에 방으로 들어섰다. 아버지는 의자에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 신음을 토했다.\n\n\"그러게 말이에요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"지당하시죠. 가엾고 괴로우신 아버지! 자, 이제 제 말을 믿으시겠죠, 아가씨?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이에요, 아버지?\" 리틀 도릿이 그에게 몸을 숙이며 외쳤다. \"제가 아버지를 슬프게 해 드렸나요, 아버지? 설마 제가 그랬을 리가요!\"\n\n\"그래, 설마라니! 말 다 했다! 아유, 너—\" 패니는 충분히 강한 표현을 찾느라 말을 멈추었다. \"너 천박한 마음을 가진 자그마한 에이미! 완전히 감옥에서 자란 아이!\"\n\n그는 손을 한 번 내저어 이 분노에 찬 비난들을 막고는, 얼굴을 들어 작은딸을 향해 우울한 고개를 가로저으며 흐느껴 말했다. \"에이미, 네 의도가 순수하다는 건 안다. 하지만 너는 내 영혼을 도려냈어.\"\n\n\"의도가 순수하다고요!\" 가차 없는 패니가 끼어들었다. \"의도라니 헛소리예요! 의도가 천한 거죠! 의도가 가문을 깎아내리는 거예요!\"\n\n\"아버지!\" 리틀 도릿이 창백하게 떨며 외쳤다. \"정말 죄송해요. 부디 용서해 주세요. 무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요, 다시는 그러지 않게요!\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고, 이 발뺌하는 어린 것아!\" 패니가 외쳤다. \"무슨 일인지 너도 알면서. 내가 이미 말해 줬잖아, 그러니 그걸 부정하려 들어서 하늘의 섭리에 거역하지 마!\"\n\n\"쉿! 에이미.\" 아버지가 손수건으로 얼굴을 여러 번 문지른 다음, 무릎 위로 떨어뜨린 손에 그것을 경련하듯 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"나는 너를 이곳에서 고상하게 지키려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다. 너에게 이곳에서 어떤 지위를 유지시켜 주려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했어. 성공했을 수도 있고, 못 했을 수도 있지. 너는 그걸 알 수도 있고, 모를 수도 있고. 나는 아무 의견도 내지 않으마. 나는 이곳에서 굴욕만 빼고는 모든 것을 견뎌 왔다. 그 굴욕만은 다행히도 면해 왔어—오늘 이날까지는 말이다.\"\n\n여기서 경련하듯 움켜쥔 그의 손이 풀리더니, 그는 다시 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다. 그의 곁 바닥에 앉아 애원하는 손을 그의 팔에 얹은 리틀 도릿은, 자책하며 그를 지켜보았다. 슬픔의 발작에서 빠져나오며 그는 다시 한번 손수건을 꽉 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"굴욕만은 오늘 이날까지 다행히 면해 왔다. 나의 온갖 고난을 통틀어, 내 안에는 그—기개라는 것이 있었고, 그리고 내 주위 사람들에게는—그 말을 써도 된다면—그 기개에 대한 순응이 있었기에, 그것이 나를—허—굴욕에서 지켜 주었다. 그러나 오늘 이날, 이 순간, 나는 그 굴욕을 뼈저리게 느꼈어.\"\n\n\"그야 당연하죠! 어찌 안 그러겠어요?\" 억누를 수 없는 패니가 외쳤다. \"빈민하고 같이 휘젓고 으스대며 돌아다녔으니!\"(또 공기총이었다.)\n\n\"하지만 사랑하는 아버지,\" 리틀 도릿이 외쳤다. \"아버지의 소중한 마음을 아프게 한 것을 변명하려는 게 아니에요—아니에요! 하늘이 알듯이 그렇지 않아요!\" 그녀는 그야말로 비탄의 고통 속에 두 손을 맞잡았다. \"저는 그저 마음을 푸시고 너그러이 봐 달라고 빌고 또 비는 것밖에 못 해요. 하지만 아버지께서 직접 그 늙은 분께 친절하시고, 그분을 무척 마음에 두시고, 늘 그분을 보면 기뻐하셨다는 걸 몰랐다면, 저는 그분과 함께 이곳에 오지 않았을 거예요, 아버지, 정말 그러지 않았을 거예요. 제가 이토록 불행하게도 저지른 일은, 잘못 알고 한 일이에요. 저는 절대로 일부러 아버지의 눈에 눈물 한 방울 맺히게 하지 않을 거예요, 사랑하는 아버지!\" 거의 가슴이 미어진 채 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"세상이 제게 줄 수 있는 그 무엇을 위해서도, 세상이 제게서 앗아 갈 수 있는 그 무엇을 위해서도 그러지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n패니는 반은 화가 나고 반은 뉘우치는 흐느낌과 함께 자신도 울기 시작하더니, 자기가 차라리 죽었으면 좋겠다고 말했다—이 젊은 아가씨는 반은 격정에 차고 반은 격정에서 빠져나와, 반은 자신에게 심술이 나고 반은 다른 모든 이에게 심술이 날 때면 늘 그렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n그러는 동안 마셜시의 아버지는 작은딸을 가슴에 끌어안고 그녀의 머리를 토닥였다.\n\n\"자, 자! 더는 말하지 마라, 에이미, 더는 말하지 마, 얘야. 되도록 빨리 잊으마. 나는,\" 발작적인 쾌활함을 띠며, \"나는—곧 이 일을 떨쳐 낼 수 있을 거야. 내가 나의 오랜 연금 수급자를 보는 게 언제나 기쁜 건—그런 자로서, 그런 자로서—틀림없는 사실이고, 또 내가—허—그 상한 갈대에게—그를 그렇게 불러도 결례가 아니길 바란다만—내 형편이 닿는 한 보호와 친절을 최대한 베푸는 것도—흠—틀림없는 사실이야. 그것이 사실이라는 건 정말 그래, 사랑하는 내 딸아. 그러면서도 나는 그렇게 하는 가운데, 말하자면—허—이 표현을 써도 된다면—기개를 지켜 왔다. 격에 맞는 기개를 말이야. 그리고 그 기개와는,\" 그는 흐느끼느라 말을 멈추었다. \"도무지 어울릴 수 없는, 그것에 상처를 주는—그것에 깊이 상처를 주는 일들이 있어. 나의 착한 에이미가 내 오랜 연금 수급자에게 신경을 쓰고—허—몸을 낮춰 대하는 것을 본 게 아니야—나를 아프게 한 건 그게 아니다. 이 괴로운 이야기를 분명히 말하고 끝내야 한다면, 그건, 내 아이가, 내 친자식이, 내 친딸이, 공공의 거리에서 이 학교로 들어오는 모습을—웃으면서! 웃으면서!—팔짱을 끼고서—아 하느님, 하인 제복하고 말이다!\"\n\n마름질도 시절도 없는 그 외투를 두고 한 이 말을, 그 불행한 신사는 거의 들리지 않는 목소리로, 움켜쥔 손수건을 허공에 치켜든 채 헐떡이며 토해 냈다. 그의 격앙된 감정은 어쩌면 좀 더 괴로운 토로로 이어졌을지도 모르나, 이미 두 번이나 되풀이된 문 두드리는 소리가 그것을 막았고, 그 소리에 패니가(여전히 자기가 죽었으면 좋겠다고, 아니 이제는 한술 더 떠 땅에 묻혔으면 좋겠다고까지 하면서) \"들어오세요!\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n\"아, 젊은 존이구나!\" 아버지가 달라지고 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐, 젊은 존?\"\n\n\"어르신께 온 편지인데, 방금 막 수위실에 맡겨졌고 전갈도 함께 왔습니다, 마침 제가 그곳에 있던 터라, 어르신 방으로 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠다 싶어서요.\" 말하는 이의 주의는 아버지 발치에서 고개를 돌린 채 있는 리틀 도릿의 가련한 광경에 몹시 흐트러져 있었다.\n\n\"그렇구나, 존? 고맙다.\"\n\n\"편지는 클레남 씨한테서 온 것입니다, 어르신—답장이고요—전갈은, 어르신, 클레남 씨가 안부를 전하면서, 오늘 오후에 찾아뵙는 기쁨을 누리고 싶다고, 어르신을 뵙기를 바라고, 또한,\" 아까보다 더 흐트러진 주의로, \"에이미 양도 뵙기를 바란다고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"오!\" 아버지가 편지를 흘끗 들여다보니(그 안에는 은행권 한 장이 들어 있었다) 조금 얼굴을 붉히며 다시 에이미의 머리를 토닥였다. \"고맙다, 젊은 존. 아주 잘했어. 마음 써 줘서 정말 고맙구나. 기다리는 사람은 없느냐?\"\n\n\"없습니다, 어르신, 기다리는 사람은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 존. 어머니는 어떠시냐, 젊은 존?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 저희가 바라는 만큼 좋으시진 않습니다—사실 아버지만 빼고 저희 모두가 그렇습니다만—그래도 그런대로 잘 지내십니다, 어르신.\"\n\n\"우리가 안부 전하더라고 말씀드려 주겠니? 부디, 따뜻한 안부 전하더라고 말이다, 젊은 존.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 그러겠습니다.\" 그리고 치버리 주니어 씨는 제 갈 길을 갔는데, 그 자리에서 즉흥적으로 자신을 위한 완전히 새로운 묘비명을 지어 냈으니, 그 내용은 이러했다. 여기 존 치버리의 몸이 누웠노라, 모년 모월 모일에, 제 생의 우상이 슬픔과 눈물에 잠긴 모습을 보고, 그 가슴 미어지는 광경을 차마 견딜 수 없어, 즉시 위로할 길 없는 부모의 거처로 돌아가, 제 무모한 손으로 제 목숨을 끝낸 자라.\n\n\"자, 자, 에이미!\" 젊은 존이 문을 닫자 아버지가 말했다. \"이 일에 관해선 더는 말하지 말자.\" 지난 몇 분 사이에 그의 기분이 눈에 띄게 나아져, 그는 무척 가벼워졌다. \"그동안 내 오랜 연금 수급자는 어디 있느냐? 그를 더는 혼자 두어서는 안 돼, 안 그러면 자기가 환영받지 못한다고 여기기 시작할 테고, 그건 내 마음을 아프게 할 테니. 네가 그를 데려오겠느냐, 아니면 내가 가랴?\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면, 아버지.\" 흐느낌을 그치려 애쓰며 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 내가 가마, 얘야. 깜빡했구나. 네 눈이 좀 빨갛구나. 자! 기운 내라, 에이미. 나는 걱정 말고. 나는 이제 완전히 괜찮아졌단다, 얘야, 완전히 괜찮아. 네 방으로 가서, 클레남 씨를 맞이할 수 있게 편안하고 보기 좋게 단장하렴.\"\n\n\"저는 제 방에 그냥 있는 게 좋겠어요, 아버지.\" 아까보다 더 평정을 되찾기 어려워하며 리틀 도릿이 응했다. \"클레남 씨를 뵙지 않는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 저런, 저런, 얘야, 그건 어리석은 소리다. 클레남 씨는 아주 신사다운 분이야—아주 신사답지. 가끔은 좀 말수가 적지만, 그래도 더없이 신사답다고 나는 말하마. 네가 여기서 클레남 씨를 맞이하지 않는다는 건 생각할 수도 없다, 얘야, 특히 오늘 오후엔 말이야. 그러니 가서 산뜻하게 단장하렴, 에이미. 가서 산뜻하게 단장하렴, 착한 아이답게.\"\n\n이렇게 지시받자, 리틀 도릿은 순순히 일어나 따랐다. 다만 방을 나서며 잠시 멈추어, 화해의 입맞춤을 언니에게 건넸을 뿐이다. 그러자 그 젊은 아가씨는 마음이 몹시 어지럽고, 평소 마음을 달래던 그 소원도 당분간 다 써 버린 터라, 노인 낸디가 그런 역겹고 성가시고 고약한 비렁뱅이처럼 와서 귀찮게 굴며 자매 사이에 불화를 일으키느니 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다는 기발한 생각을 떠올리고 실행에 옮겼다.\n\n마셜시의 아버지는, 기분이 어찌나 좋아졌는지 콧노래까지 흥얼거리고 검은 벨벳 모자를 한쪽으로 살짝 비스듬히 쓴 채 마당으로 내려갔고, 거기서 그동안 내내 그러고 서 있던 대로 문 바로 안쪽에 모자를 손에 들고 서 있는 그의 오랜 연금 수급자를 발견했다. \"이리 오게, 낸디!\" 그가 무척 부드럽게 말했다. \"위층으로 올라오게, 낸디. 길은 알지 않나. 어째서 올라오지 않고 있나?\" 이번에는 그가 손까지 내밀며 \"잘 지내나, 낸디? 그런대로 괜찮은가?\" 하고 말하는 데까지 나아갔다. 그러자 그 노래꾼은 \"고맙습니다, 존경하는 어르신, 어르신을 뵈니 한결 좋아졌습니다\" 하고 응했다. 마당을 지나가며 마셜시의 아버지는 그를 최근에 들어온 한 재소자에게 소개했다. \"내 오랜 지인이라오, 선생, 오랜 연금 수급자지요.\" 그러고는 무척 배려 깊게 \"모자를 쓰게, 착한 낸디. 모자를 쓰게나\" 하고 말했다.\n\n그의 후원은 여기서 그치지 않았으니, 그는 매기에게 차를 준비하라 이르고, 차에 곁들일 빵 몇 가지와 신선한 버터, 달걀, 차가운 햄, 새우를 사 오라고 지시했다. 그 음식들을 사 오라며 그는 매기에게 십 파운드짜리 은행권을 건네면서, 거스름돈을 잘 챙기라고 엄히 당부했다. 이 준비가 한창 진행되고, 딸 에이미가 바느질거리를 들고 돌아왔을 무렵 클레남이 모습을 드러냈는데, 그는 클레남을 더없이 정중히 맞으며 식사에 함께해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"에이미, 얘야, 너는 내가 누리는 영광보다도 더 클레남 씨를 잘 알지. 패니, 얘야, 너도 클레남 씨와는 아는 사이고.\" 패니는 거만하게 그를 아는 체했다. 이런 경우마다 그녀가 말없이 취하는 입장은, 가문을 이해하지 못하거나 충분히 떠받들지 않음으로써 가문을 모욕하려는 거대한 음모가 있고 여기 그 음모자 중 하나가 있다는 것이었다. \"이 사람은, 클레남 씨, 아셔야 하는데, 내 오랜 연금 수급자인 노인 낸디라오, 아주 충실한 늙은이지요.\"(그는 늘 그를 아주 까마득한 옛 인물인 양 말했으나, 그는 자신보다 두세 살 아래였다.) \"어디 보자. 당신은 플로니시를 아시지요? 내 딸 에이미가 당신이 그 가엾은 플로니시를 안다고 내게 말한 것 같은데?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요!\" 아서 클레남이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 선생, 이 사람이 바로 플로니시 부인의 아버지라오.\"\n\n\"그렇습니까? 만나 뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\"\n\n\"그의 많은 미덕을 아신다면 더욱 기쁘실 겁니다, 클레남 씨.\"\n\n\"그를 알아 가면서 그 미덕들도 알게 되길 바랍니다.\" 아서가 그 굽은 채 순종하는 모습을 속으로 가엾이 여기며 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람에겐 오늘이 휴일이라, 그를 보면 늘 반가워하는 오랜 친구들을 보러 온 것이라오.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 말했다. 그러고는 손으로 입을 가리며 덧붙였다. (\"연합 구빈원 신세지요, 가엾은 늙은이. 하루 외출 나온 거랍니다.\")\n\n이쯤 되어 매기는, 자기의 리틀 마더의 조용한 도움을 받아 상을 차렸고, 식사가 준비되었다. 더운 날씨에 감옥 안이 무척 답답해, 창문은 밀어 열 수 있는 데까지 활짝 열려 있었다. \"매기가 창턱에 저 신문지를 펴 주면, 얘야,\" 아버지가 흐뭇하게 리틀 도릿에게 반쯤 속삭이며 말했다. \"내 오랜 연금 수급자는 우리가 차를 마시는 동안 거기서 차를 마실 수 있겠구나.\"\n\n그리하여, 정식 척도로 약 한 자 너비의 만(灣)을 사이에 두고 그 훌륭한 일행과 떨어진 채, 플로니시 부인의 아버지는 융숭한 대접을 받았다. 클레남은 저 다른 아버지, 곧 마셜시의 아버지에게서 베풀어지는 그 너그러운 비호 같은 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없었으며, 그 숱한 기이함을 바라보는 데 정신이 팔렸다.\n\n그 가운데 가장 인상적인 것은 아마도, 그가 그 연금 수급자의 노쇠함과 결함을 두고, 마치 자신이 전시하는 그 해롭잖은 짐승의 쇠락에 관해 실황 해설을 늘어놓는 인자한 사육사라도 된 양, 음미하듯 논평하는 태도였을 것이다.\n\n\"아직 햄을 더 들 준비가 안 됐나, 낸디? 아니, 어찌 이리 굼뜬가! (저 친구 마지막 남은 이가,\" 그가 일행에게 설명했다. \"다 빠져 가고 있다오, 가엾은 늙은이.)\"\n\n또 어느 때엔 \"새우는 안 드나, 낸디?\" 하고 말했다가, 그가 곧바로 대답하지 않자 이렇게 말했다. (\"저 친구 청력이 아주 나빠지고 있어요. 곧 귀머거리가 될 겁니다.)\"\n\n또 어느 때엔 그에게 이렇게 물었다. \"그곳 담장 안 마당에서 산책은 많이 하나, 낸디?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 어르신. 안 합니다. 그건 그리 좋아하지 않아서요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇겠지.\" 그가 맞장구쳤다. \"지극히 당연하지.\" 그러고는 좌중에 가만히 알려 주었다. (\"다리가 쇠하고 있어요.)\"\n\n한번은 그를 띄워 주려고 무엇이든 묻고 보는 그 두루뭉술한 관대함으로, 연금 수급자에게 막내 손주가 몇 살이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"존 에드워드 말입니까.\" 연금 수급자가 생각하느라 천천히 나이프와 포크를 내려놓으며 말했다. \"몇 살이냐고요, 어르신? 어디 가만 보자.\"\n\n마셜시의 아버지는 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드렸다. (\"기억력이 약해요.)\"\n\n\"존 에드워드 말입니까, 어르신? 글쎄, 정말 잊었습니다. 지금 당장은 말씀드릴 수가 없네요, 어르신, 그게 두 살 두 달인지, 아니면 두 살 다섯 달인지. 둘 중 하납니다.\"\n\n\"그것 때문에 마음을 졸이며 애쓰지 말게.\" 그가 한없는 너그러움으로 응했다. (\"기능이 확연히 쇠하고 있어요—이런 삶을 사니 늙은이가 녹이 슬지요!)\"\n\n연금 수급자에게서 그런 발견을 했다고 스스로 믿게 될수록, 그는 그를 더욱 마음에 들어 하는 듯했다. 그리고 차를 마신 뒤, 연금 수급자가 죄송하오나 어르신, 시간이 다 되어 가는 것 같다고 넌지시 비치자, 그에게 작별을 고하려고 의자에서 일어서면서, 그는 되도록 꼿꼿하고 튼튼해 보이도록 자세를 가다듬었다.\n\n\"이건 우리끼린 일 실링이라고 부르지 않는 거 알지, 낸디.\" 그가 그의 손에 한 닢을 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"이건 담배라고 부르는 거야.\"\n\n\"존경하는 어르신, 감사합니다. 이걸로 담배를 사겠습니다. 에이미 양과 패니 양께도 감사와 문안의 말씀을. 안녕히 주무십시오, 클레남 씨.\"\n\n\"그리고 명심하게, 우리를 잊으면 안 되네, 낸디.\" 아버지가 말했다. \"또 와야 하네, 명심하고, 오후가 빌 때마다 말이야. 우리를 보지 않고 그냥 나가면 안 돼, 안 그러면 우리가 서운해할 테니. 잘 가게, 낸디. 층계를 내려갈 땐 아주 조심하게, 낸디. 좀 고르지 못하고 닳았으니까.\" 그러고서 그는 층계참에 서서 늙은이가 내려가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 다시 방으로 들어와, 엄숙한 만족감을 띤 채 말했다. \"참 서글픈 광경이지요, 클레남 씨, 그래도 본인은 그걸 느끼지 못한다는 걸 아는 게 위안이긴 합니다만. 저 가엾은 늙은이는 처량한 폐인이라오. 기개가 꺾이고 사라져—가루가 되고—완전히 으스러져 버렸지요, 선생!\"\n\n클레남은 남아 있을 목적이 있었으므로, 이런 소회에 응하여 할 수 있는 말을 했고, 매기와 그녀의 리틀 마더가 다기를 닦아 치우는 동안 그것을 토로하는 사람과 함께 창가에 서 있었다. 그는 자기 길동무가 상냥하고 가까이하기 쉬운 군주 같은 분위기로 창가에 서 있는 것을, 그리고 아래 마당에 있는 자기 백성 가운데 누군가가 올려다볼 때면, 그 인사에 답하는 것이 축복을 내리는 것에 못 미칠 따름임을 알아챘다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 자기 바느질거리를 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 매기가 자기 것을 침상에 올려놓았을 때, 패니는 떠날 채비로 보닛 끈을 매기 시작했다. 아서는 여전히 목적이 있었으므로 여전히 남아 있었다. 이때 문이 아무런 기별도 없이 열리더니 팁 씨가 들어왔다. 그는 자기를 맞으려 벌떡 일어선 에이미에게 입을 맞추고, 패니에게 고개를 끄덕이고, 아버지에게 고개를 끄덕이고, 그 이상의 아는 체는 없이 손님을 침울하게 흘겨보고는 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"팁 오빠,\" 리틀 도릿이 이에 충격을 받아 부드럽게 말했다. \"안 보여요—\"\n\n\"보여, 에이미. 네가 여기 와 있는 손님의 존재를 두고 하는 말이라면—내 말은, 네가 그걸 두고 하는 말이라면,\" 클레남 쪽에 가까운 어깨 쪽으로 힘주어 고개를 홱 젖히며 팁이 대답했다. \"보인다고!\"\n\n\"그게 다예요?\"\n\n\"그게 다야. 그리고 내 짐작에,\" 그 거만한 젊은이가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"저 손님도 내가 그게 다라고 할 때 무슨 말인지 알아들을 거야. 한마디로, 저 손님도 자기가 나를 신사답게 대하지 않았다는 걸 알아들을 거라고.\"\n\n\"나는 그렇게 알아듣지 못하겠는데요.\" 거명된 그 거슬리는 인물이 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"못 알아들어요? 그렇다면, 더 분명히 해 드리지요, 선생, 알려 드리는데, 내가 누군가에게 점잖게 말을 갖춘 부탁, 절박한 부탁, 조심스러운 부탁을, 그 사람 능력으로 손쉽게 들어줄 수 있는—손쉽게 들어줄 수 있는, 명심하시오!—작은 일시적 융통을 청했을 때, 그리고 그 사람이 내게 죄송하지만 사양하겠노라 답장을 써 보냈을 때, 나는 그자가 나를 신사답게 대하지 않은 것으로 칩니다.\"\n\n아들을 말없이 살피고 있던 마셜시의 아버지는, 이 말을 듣자마자 노여운 목소리로 입을 열었다.\n\n\"어찌 감히 네가—\" 그러나 아들이 그를 막았다.\n\n\"자, 어찌 감히냐고는 묻지 마세요, 아버지, 그건 다 헛소리니까. 내가 여기 있는 저 인물에게 취하기로 한 처신의 사실로 말하자면, 아버지는 내가 마땅한 기개를 보이는 걸 자랑스러워하셔야 마땅하다고요.\"\n\n\"그렇고말고!\" 패니가 외쳤다.\n\n\"마땅한 기개라고?\" 아버지가 말했다. \"그래, 마땅한 기개, 격에 맞는 기개라. 이제 내 아들이 나에게—나에게—기개를 가르치는 지경에 이른 것이냐!\"\n\n\"자, 이 일로 골치 썩지도 말고, 시끄럽게 굴지도 맙시다, 아버지. 나는 여기 있는 저 인물이 나를 신사답게 대하지 않았다고 완전히 마음을 굳혔어요. 그걸로 끝입니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 그걸로 끝이 아니다, 이놈아.\" 아버지가 응수했다. \"그걸로 끝나서는 안 돼. 네가 마음을 굳혔다고? 마음을 굳혔다고?\"\n\n\"네, 내가 굳혔어요. 그렇게 계속 들볶아서 좋을 게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 아버지가 잔뜩 열을 내며 응수했다. \"너에겐 그 끔찍한 것에, 그—허—부도덕한 것에, 그—흠—애비를 죽이는 짓 같은 것에 마음을 굳힐 권리가 없기 때문이다. 아니요, 클레남 씨, 부탁이오, 선생. 나더러 그만두라 하지 마시오. 여기에는—흠—일반적인 원칙이 걸려 있고, 그것은—허—환대에 대한 고려보다도 더 위에 있는 것이오. 나는 내 아들이 한 그 주장에 반대하오. 나는—허—나 개인으로서 그것을 물리치오.\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 아버지하고 무슨 상관이에요?\" 아들이 어깨 너머로 응수했다.\n\n\"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이냐고, 이놈아? 나에겐—흠—기개가 있다, 이놈아, 그걸 참아 넘기지 않을 기개 말이다. 나는,\" 그는 다시 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 톡톡 눌렀다. \"그것 때문에 모욕당하고 능멸당했다. 이런 경우를 한번 가정해 보자, 나 자신이 어느 한때—허—혹은 여러 때, 어떤 사람에게 작은 일시적 융통을 청하는—흠—부탁을, 그것도 점잖게 말을 갖춘 부탁, 조심스러운 부탁, 절박한 부탁을 했다고 치자. 그 융통을 손쉽게 베풀 수 있었는데도 베풀어 주지 않고, 그 사람이 내게 죄송하지만 사양하겠노라 알려 왔다고 치자. 그렇다고 해서 내가 신사로서 받아야 할 대접을 받지 못한 것이고, 그래서 내가—허—그것을 감수했다고, 내 친아들에게서 들어야 하는 것이냐?\"\n\n딸 에이미가 부드럽게 그를 진정시키려 했으나, 그는 무슨 일이 있어도 진정하려 들지 않았다. 그는 자기 기개가 솟구쳤으니 이것을 견디지 못하겠노라고 말했다.\n\n자기 친아들에게서, 자기 집 화롯가에서, 자기 면전에 대고 그런 말을 들어야 하느냐고, 그는 다시 알고 싶어 했다. 자기 핏줄에게서 그런 굴욕을 당해야 하느냐고?\n\n\"그건 아버지가 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 거예요, 아버지, 그 모든 상처를 스스로 자초하시는 거라고요!\" 그 젊은 신사가 뚱하게 말했다. \"내가 마음을 굳힌 건 아버지와는 아무 상관 없어요. 내가 한 말도 아버지와는 아무 상관 없었고요. 왜 굳이 남의 모자를 써 보려고 하세요?\"\n\n\"내 대답은, 그것이 나하고 모든 면에서 상관이 있다는 것이다.\" 아버지가 응수했다. \"분개하며 네게 지적하건대, 이놈아—흠—네 아비가 처한 위치의—허—그 미묘함과 특수함이, 다른 무엇으로도 그러지 못한다면, 적어도 너로 하여금 그런—허—그런 천륜에 어긋나는 원칙을 늘어놓을 때 입을 다물게 해야 마땅하다, 이놈아. 게다가, 네가 자식 된 도리를 모른다면, 그 본분을 저버린다면, 너는 적어도—흠—기독교인은 아니란 말이냐? 너는—허—무신론자냐? 묻건대, 어떤 사람이 이번에 사양하겠다고 청했다고 해서—그 같은 사람이—허—다음번에는 청한 융통에 응할 수도 있는데—그 사람을 낙인찍고 규탄하는 것이 기독교인다운 일이냐? 그를 다시—흠—다시 시험해 보지 않는 것이 기독교인의 도리냐?\" 그는 어느새 자못 종교적인 열기와 열정에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 여기서는 분별 있거나 공정한 의논을 얻지 못하겠다는 걸 아주 잘 알겠네요.\" 팁 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그러니 내가 할 수 있는 최선은 가 버리는 겁니다. 잘 있어, 에이미. 속상해하지 마. 하필 여기서, 그리고 네가 있는 데서 이런 일이 벌어져서 정말 미안하다, 정말이야. 하지만 너를 봐서라도 내 기개를 아주 내려놓을 수는 없어, 누이야.\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그는 모자를 쓰고 나가 버렸고, 패니 양이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그녀는 클레남이 늘 알고 지내던 그 거대한 음모자 무리의 하나임을 내비치는 한 차례의 노려봄, 그보다 덜한 적대의 시위로 그에게 작별하는 것은 자기 기개에 어울리지 않는다고 여겼다.\n\n그들이 가고 나자, 마셜시의 아버지는 처음엔 다시 낙담에 빠지려는 기색이었고, 실제로 그리되었을 테지만, 마침 한두 분 뒤에 한 신사가 그를 스너거리로 모시러 올라왔다. 그는 클레남이 우연히 이곳에 갇혔던 그날 밤 보았던 신사로, 마셜이 살을 찌우는 것으로 여겨지는 그 유용된 기금에 관해 손에 잡히지 않는 불만을 품고 있던 자였다. 그는 아버지를 의장석으로 모시는 대표단으로 나선 것이었으니, 그날은 그가 약간의 음악적 화합을 즐기려고 모인 재소자들 위에 좌장으로 앉기로 약속한 자리였다.\n\n\"보시다시피, 클레남 씨,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"이런 것이 여기서의 내 처지가 지닌 부조화라오. 하지만 공적인 의무이니! 내 확신컨대, 누구도 당신 자신보다 더 기꺼이 공적인 의무를 인정하지는 못할 것이오.\"\n\n클레남은 그에게 잠시도 지체하지 말라고 간청했다.\n\n\"에이미, 얘야, 네가 클레남 씨를 더 머물게 설득할 수 있다면, 우리의 변변찮은 살림이나마 그 손님 대접의 영예를 네 손에 안심하고 맡길 수 있겠구나. 그리고 어쩌면 너는 차 마신 뒤로 일어난—허—그 거북하고 불쾌한 일을 클레남 씨의 마음에서 지우는 데 무언가 보탬이 될지도 모르고.\"\n\n클레남은 그것이 자기 마음에 아무런 인상도 남기지 않았으므로 지울 필요도 없다고 그를 안심시켰다.\n\n\"이보시오,\" 검은 모자를 벗고 클레남의 손을 잡으며—이는 그날 오후 받은 그의 편지와 동봉물을 무사히 받았음을 함께 나타내는 것이었다—아버지가 말했다. \"하늘이 늘 당신을 축복하시길!\"\n\n그리하여 마침내, 남아 있던 클레남의 목적이 이루어졌고, 그는 곁에 아무도 없이 리틀 도릿에게 말을 건넬 수 있게 되었다. 매기는 아무도 아닌 것으로 쳤고, 그녀는 곁에 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. More Fortune-Telling\n\n매기는 창가 쪽에 앉아 일을 하고 있었다. 머리에는 커다란 흰색 모자를 썼는데, 불투명한 주름 장식이 잔뜩 달려 있어 그나마 있던 옆얼굴 윤곽을(여분이라 할 만한 게 없었다) 가려 버렸고, 쓸 만한 한쪽 눈만은 일감에 집중하고 있었다. 펄럭이는 모자와 못 쓰는 한쪽 눈 때문에 그녀는 창문 맞은편에 앉은 작은 어머니와 완전히 칸막이로 갈라진 듯했다. 마당 포석을 밟는 발소리와 끄는 소리는 의장석에 누군가 앉은 뒤로 한결 잦아들었으니, 재학생들의 물결이 화합을 향해 거세게 밀려간 까닭이었다. 영혼에 음악이 없거나 주머니에 돈이 없는 몇몇은 어슬렁거렸고, 면회 온 아내와 풀 죽은 채 아직 익숙해지지 못한 죄수가 어우러진 그 오래된 풍경은, 마치 다른 곳들의 구석마다 찢어진 거미줄이며 흉한 너저분함이 늘어져 있듯, 여전히 구석에 머물러 있었다. 재학생들이 잠이라는 법령의 혜택을 누리던 밤 시간을 빼면, 이때가 학교에서 가장 조용한 시간이었다. 이따금 아늑한 방의 탁자를 두드리는 박수 소리가 들려와 화합의 한 토막이 성공적으로 끝났음을, 또는 아버지가 그들에게 내건 어떤 건배사나 좋은 말을 한데 모인 자식들이 호응하며 받아들였음을 알렸다. 가끔은 보통보다 더 우렁찬 노랫가락이 듣는 이에게 어떤 허풍스러운 베이스가 푸른 바다 위에 있다거나, 사냥터에 있다거나, 순록과 함께 있다거나, 산 위에 있다거나, 히스 풀밭 사이에 있다고 일러 주었으나, 마셜시의 간수장은 더 잘 알았으니, 그자를 단단히 붙들어 두고 있었던 것이다.\n\n아서 클레넘이 리틀 도릿 곁에 앉으려고 다가가자, 그녀는 어찌나 떨었던지 바늘을 쥐고 있기조차 버거워했다. 클레넘은 그녀의 일감 위에 가만히 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 이건 내려놓도록 합시다.\"\n\n그녀는 그것을 그에게 내주었고, 그는 한쪽으로 치워 두었다. 그러자 그녀의 두 손은 초조하게 맞잡혔는데, 그가 그중 한 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"요즘 당신을 정말 드물게밖에 못 보았군요, 리틀 도릿!\"\n\n\"제가 바빴어요, 선생님.\"\n\n\"그런데 오늘에서야, 그것도 순전히 우연으로 들었답니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"당신이 내 바로 가까이 사는 그 선량한 사람들과 함께 있었다는 걸 말입니다. 그럼 어째서 나를 찾아오지 않았어요?\"\n\n\"저는—저는 모르겠어요. 아니, 그보다도, 선생님도 바쁘시리라 생각했어요. 요즘 대개 그러시잖아요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n그는 떨고 있는 그녀의 작은 몸과 떨군 얼굴을, 그리고 그를 향해 들렸다가 그 순간 다시 처지는 눈을 보았다—그는 그것을 다정함 못지않은 염려로 바라보았다.\n\n\"얘야, 네 태도가 너무도 달라졌구나!\"\n\n이제 떨림은 그녀가 도저히 어쩌지 못할 지경이 되었다. 그녀는 가만히 손을 빼내어 다른 손 위에 포개고는, 고개를 숙이고 온몸을 떨면서 그의 앞에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"내 소중한 리틀 도릿.\" 클레넘이 측은히 여기며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다. 매기가 갑자기 고개를 돌리더니 적어도 일 분은 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러나 끼어들지는 않았다. 클레넘은 잠시 기다렸다가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 우는 걸 보고 있을 수가 없군요.\" 그때 그가 말했다. \"하지만 이것이 너무 벅찬 가슴을 덜어 주는 거라면 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"네, 그래요, 선생님. 그것뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"자, 자! 나는 당신이 방금 여기서 있었던 일을 너무 깊이 생각하지나 않을까 걱정했어요. 그건 아무것도 아닙니다, 조금도요. 다만 내가 그 자리에 끼어든 것이 불운이었을 뿐이지요. 이 눈물과 함께 흘려보냅시다. 그 일은 눈물 한 방울만큼의 가치도 없어요. 눈물 한 방울이라니? 그런 하찮은 일이라면, 당신의 한순간의 가슴앓이를 덜어 줄 수만 있다면 나는 기꺼이 동의하며 하루에 쉰 번이라도 되풀이하겠습니다, 리틀 도릿.\"\n\n이제 그녀는 용기를 내어, 한결 평소의 태도에 가깝게 대답했다. \"선생님은 정말 좋으신 분이에요! 하지만 거기에 슬퍼하고 부끄러워할 다른 무엇이 없다 하더라도, 이건 선생님께 너무도 잘못된 보답이에요—\"\n\n\"쉿!\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 그녀의 입술에 손을 대고 말했다. \"그토록 많은 것과 많은 사람을 기억하는 당신에게 망각이라니, 그거야말로 정말 새로운 일이겠지요. 내가 당신이 믿기로 한 그 친구일 뿐이고, 여태껏 그 이상의 무엇이었던 적도 없다는 걸 일깨워 드릴까요? 아니죠. 당신은 기억하고 있지요, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"기억하려고 애써요. 그러지 않았다면 방금, 제 오해 많은 오빠가 여기 있었을 때 그 약속을 깨뜨렸을 거예요. 그가 이런 곳에서 자랐다는 걸 헤아려 주시고, 그를 모질게 판단하지 않으시리라는 걸, 가엾은 사람을, 저는 알아요!\" 이 말과 함께 눈을 들면서 그녀는 여태껏보다 더 가까이 그의 얼굴을 살피더니, 빠르게 어조를 바꾸어 말했다. \"어디 편찮으셨던 건 아니에요, 클레넘 씨?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"무슨 시련을 겪으신 것도? 다치신 것도 아니고요?\" 그녀가 걱정스레 물었다.\n\n이제 어떻게 대답해야 할지 썩 분명치 않게 된 쪽은 클레넘이었다. 그는 대답으로 말했다.\n\n\"사실대로 말하자면, 조금 마음고생을 했답니다. 하지만 이젠 다 끝났어요. 내가 그렇게 빤히 드러나 보였나요? 나는 그보다 더한 꿋꿋함과 자제력을 지녀야 마땅한데. 그런 줄로 알았는데 말입니다. 당신에게서 그것들을 배워야겠군요. 당신보다 더 잘 가르쳐 줄 사람이 어디 있겠어요!\"\n\n그는 그녀가 그에게서 다른 누구도 보지 못하는 것을 본다는 사실을 전혀 생각하지 못했다. 온 세상에 그녀의 눈과 같은 빛과 힘으로 그를 바라보는 다른 눈은 없다는 것을 그는 전혀 생각하지 못했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 때문에 내가 하고 싶은 이야기로 들어서게 되었군요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그러니 비밀을 들키고 나를 배신했다고 해서 내 얼굴과 다투지는 않으렵니다. 게다가 내 리틀 도릿에게 속을 털어놓는 건 특권이자 즐거움이지요. 그럼 고백하건대, 내가 얼마나 진지하고, 얼마나 나이 들었으며, 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 그것을 의식조차 못 한 채 한결같음과 자잘한 행복으로 채워진 오랜 세월과 더불어 그런 일들의 때가 이미 나를 지나가 버렸는지를 잊은 채로—이 모든 걸 잊은 채로, 나는 누군가를 사랑한다고 착각했답니다.\"\n\n\"제가 그분을 아나요, 선생님?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 얘야.\"\n\n\"선생님 때문에 제게 친절히 대해 주신 그 부인은 아니고요?\"\n\n\"플로라요. 아니, 아니에요. 당신은 설마—\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각한 적은 한 번도 없었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 그에게라기보다는 거의 혼잣말처럼 말했다. \"조금 의아하긴 했지만요.\"\n\n\"자!\" 클레넘이 말했다. 장미가 핀 그 밤에 가로수 길에서 자신에게 내려앉았던 그 느낌, 곧 자신은 인생의 그 다정한 부분과는 이미 끝낸 더 나이 든 사람이라는 그 느낌을 붙든 채로. \"나는 내 착각을 알아차렸고, 그것에 대해 조금—요컨대 꽤 많이—생각했으며, 그래서 더 현명해졌지요. 더 현명해진 김에 내 나이를 헤아려 보고, 내가 어떤 사람인지 따져 보고, 뒤를 돌아보고 앞을 내다보았더니, 머지않아 백발이 되리라는 걸 알게 되었습니다. 나는 그 언덕을 다 올라, 꼭대기의 평평한 땅을 지나, 빠르게 내려가고 있다는 걸 알았어요.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그가 그 인내심 깊은 가슴에 얼마나 날카로운 아픔을 주고 있는지 알았더라면! 더구나 그녀를 위로하고 도우려는 마음으로 그러고 있었으니.\n\n\"그런 일이 내게 우아하거나, 내게 어울리거나, 나에게든 나와 얽힌 누구에게든 희망차거나 행복할 수 있었을 그런 날은 이미 가 버렸고, 다시는 빛나지 않으리라는 걸 알았습니다.\"\n\n오! 그가 알았더라면, 그가 알았더라면! 자기 손에 쥔 단검을, 그리고 그것이 자신의 리틀 도릿의 충실하게 피 흘리는 가슴에 새기는 그 잔인한 상처들을 그가 볼 수 있었더라면!\n\n\"그 모든 게 다 끝났고, 나는 거기서 얼굴을 돌렸습니다. 그런데 어째서 나는 이걸 리틀 도릿에게 이야기하고 있는 걸까요? 어째서, 얘야, 우리 사이에 놓인 그 세월의 간격을 당신에게 보여 주고, 내가 당신 인생 전체만큼의 시간을, 당신에게는 지금 현재인 그 시간을, 이미 지나 버렸다는 걸 떠올리게 하는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"저를 믿으시기 때문이겠죠, 그러길 바라요. 저를 건드리지 않고는 선생님께 닿는 것이 아무것도 없다는 걸, 선생님께 그토록 감사하는 저를 똑같이 만들지 않고는 선생님을 행복하게도 불행하게도 할 수 있는 것이 아무것도 없다는 걸 아시기 때문이겠죠.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 목소리에 어린 떨림을 들었고, 그녀의 진지한 얼굴을 보았으며, 그녀의 맑고 참된 눈을 보았고, \"나는 그를 사랑해!\"라는 죽어 가는 외침과 함께 그의 가슴을 겨눈 치명적인 일격을 받으려고 기꺼이 그 앞에 몸을 던졌을 그 빠르게 두근거리는 가슴을 보았다—그러나 그 진실에 대한 아주 희미한 의심조차 그의 마음에는 떠오르지 않았다. 그렇다. 그가 본 것은 닳아빠진 신발을 신고, 수수한 옷차림에, 감옥인 자기 집에 있는, 헌신적인 작은 존재였다. 몸은 가냘픈 아이였으나 영혼은 강인한 여걸이었다. 그리고 그녀의 그 가정사의 빛이 그에게는 다른 모든 것을 어둡게 만들었다.\n\n\"분명 그런 이유들 때문이지요, 리틀 도릿. 하지만 또 다른 이유 때문이기도 합니다. 그토록 멀리 떨어져 있고, 그토록 다르며, 그토록 나이가 많으니, 나는 당신의 친구이자 조언자로 더 잘 어울려요. 내 말은, 더 쉽게 믿을 수 있는 상대라는 거지요. 다른 사람한테라면 느낄 법한 자그마한 어색함도 내 앞에서는 사라질 수 있을 겁니다. 어째서 나에게서 그토록 멀찍이 물러나 있었던 거지요? 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"저는 여기가 더 나아요. 제 자리와 쓸모는 여기 있어요. 저는 여기가 훨씬 나아요.\" 리틀 도릿이 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n\"그날 다리 위에서도 그렇게 말했지요. 그 뒤로 그 말을 많이 생각했답니다. 마음만 있다면, 희망과 위안을 가지고 나에게 맡길 수 있는 비밀이 하나도 없나요!\"\n\n\"비밀이요? 아니에요, 저는 비밀이 없어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 곤란해하며 말했다.\n\n그들은 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있었다. 일하고 있는 매기에게 감추려는 어떤 조심스러움 때문이라기보다는, 그들이 나누는 이야기에 그런 어조가 자연스러웠던 까닭이었다. 그런데 별안간 매기가 다시 빤히 쳐다보더니, 이번에는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"저기요! 작은 어머니!\"\n\n\"왜, 매기.\"\n\n\"작은 어머니가 직접 그분께 들려줄 비밀이 없으면, 그 공주님 얘기를 들려드려요. 그 공주님은 비밀이 있었잖아요, 그쵸.\"\n\n\"공주님이 비밀이 있었다고요?\" 클레넘이 다소 놀라며 말했다. \"그게 무슨 공주냐, 매기?\"\n\n\"아유! 어쩜 열 살짜리 계집애를 그렇게 들들 볶으세요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"가엾은 애를 그런 식으로 붙잡고서. 누가 공주님이 비밀이 있었다고 했어요? 나는 그런 말 한 적 없어요.\"\n\n\"미안하구나. 네가 그런 줄 알았다.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 안 그랬어요. 어떻게 그래요, 비밀을 찾아내고 싶어 한 게 바로 그 공주님인데? 비밀을 가진 건 그 작은 여자였고, 그 여자는 늘 물레질을 하고 있었어요. 그래서 공주님이 그 여자한테, 왜 그걸 거기다 두느냐고 물었더니, 그 여자는, 아뇨 안 둬요, 했고, 공주님은, 아니 두잖아요, 했고, 그래서 둘이 다 같이 찬장으로 가니까, 거기 그게 있었어요. 그리고 그 여자는 병원에 들어가려고 하지 않았고, 그래서 죽었어요. 작은 어머니는 알잖아요, 그분께 그 얘기 해 드려요. 그건 정말이지 진짜 멋진 비밀이었으니까요!\" 매기가 제 몸을 끌어안으며 외쳤다.\n\n아서는 이 말을 이해하는 데 도움을 받으려고 리틀 도릿을 바라보았는데, 그녀가 그토록 수줍어하며 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보고 흠칫했다. 그러나 그것이 자기가 언젠가 매기에게 지어 준 동화일 뿐이고, 설령 기억해 낼 수 있다 하더라도 누구에게든 다시 들려주기 부끄럽지 않을 만한 내용은 하나도 없다고 그녀가 말하자, 그는 그 화제를 그대로 두었다.\n\n그러나 그는 자기 화제로 돌아와, 우선 자기를 더 자주 만나 달라고, 그리고 자신만큼 그녀의 안녕에 더 깊은 관심을 가질 사람도, 자신만큼 그것을 도모하는 데 더 마음을 쏟는 사람도 없다는 걸 기억해 달라고 간청했다. 그녀가 열렬히, 자기도 그걸 잘 알며 결코 잊지 않는다고 대답하자, 그는 두 번째이자 더 미묘한 점—그가 품게 된 짐작을 건드렸다.\n\n\"리틀 도릿.\" 그가 다시 그녀의 손을 잡고, 여태껏보다 더 낮은 목소리로, 좁은 방 안의 매기조차 듣지 못하도록 말했다. \"한마디만 더. 나는 이 말을 당신에게 무척이나 하고 싶었어요. 기회를 노려 왔지요. 햇수로 따지면 당신의 아버지뻘이나 삼촌뻘일 수도 있는 나를 신경 쓰지 말아요. 나를 그저 늙은이로만 여겨 줘요. 나는 당신의 헌신이 모두 이 방에 모여 있다는 걸, 그리고 끝까지 어떤 것도 당신을 이곳에서 다하는 의무로부터 떼어 놓도록 유혹하지 못하리라는 걸 압니다. 만약 그걸 확신하지 못했다면, 나는 진작에 당신에게도, 당신의 아버지에게도, 좀 더 어울리는 곳에서 당신을 위해 무언가 마련하게 해 달라고 애원했을 거예요. 하지만 당신에게는 어떤 마음이 있을지도—지금이라고는 말하지 않겠어요, 비록 그것조차 있을 수 있겠지만—언젠가, 다른 누군가에 대한 어떤 마음이 있을지도 모르지요. 이곳을 향한 당신의 애정과 양립하지 못할 게 없는 그런 마음 말입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 몹시, 몹시 창백해진 채 말없이 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그럴 수도 있어요, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿.\"\n\n\"아니에요. 아니에요. 아니에요.\" 그 말을 천천히 한 번씩 되풀이할 때마다 그녀는 고개를 저었는데, 그가 오랜 뒤까지 기억하게 될 그 조용한 황량함의 기색이 어려 있었다. 그가 그것을 또렷이 기억하게 되는 때가 왔으니, 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 저 감옥의 담장 안, 바로 그 방 안에서였다.\n\n\"하지만 혹시라도 언젠가 그렇게 된다면, 내게 말해 줘요, 사랑하는 얘야. 그 진실을 내게 맡기고, 그런 마음의 대상을 내게 가리켜 줘요. 그러면 내가 당신에게 느끼는 온갖 열의와 명예와 우정과 존경을 다해, 내 마음속 착한 리틀 도릿, 당신에게 변치 않을 도움을 베풀려고 애써 보겠어요.\"\n\n\"오, 고맙습니다, 고맙습니다! 하지만, 오 아니에요, 오 아니에요, 오 아니에요!\" 그녀는 일에 닳은 두 손을 맞잡은 채 그를 바라보며, 앞서와 똑같이 체념한 어조로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"지금 당장 털어놓으라고 채근하는 게 아니에요. 나는 다만 망설임 없이 나를 믿어 달라고 부탁하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"선생님이 그토록 좋으신데, 제가 그만한 일도 못 하겠어요!\"\n\n\"그럼 나를 온전히 믿어 줄 거지요? 어떤 남모를 슬픔이나 근심도 내게 숨기지 않을 거지요?\"\n\n\"거의 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그리고 지금은 하나도 없고요?\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 저었다. 그러나 몹시 창백했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 자리에 누워, 내 생각이 다시—그러겠지요, 당신을 보지 못한 날에도 매일 밤 그러니까—이 슬픈 곳으로 돌아올 때, 지금 이 방과 늘 이곳에 사는 사람들 너머에는 리틀 도릿의 마음을 갉아먹는 어떤 슬픔도 없다고 믿어도 될까요?\"\n\n그녀는 이 말을 붙잡으려는 듯했고—그것 또한 그가 오랜 뒤까지 기억했다—한결 밝아진 목소리로 말했다. \"네, 클레넘 씨. 네, 그러셔도 돼요!\"\n\n평소에는 누가 오르내릴 때 알려 주기를 게을리하지 않던 그 삐걱대는 계단이, 이번에는 빠른 발걸음 아래에서 삐걱거렸고, 그 위에서 또 다른 소리가 들려왔으니, 마치 주체 못 할 만큼 김이 가득 찬 작은 증기 기관 하나가 방을 향해 일하며 다가오는 듯했다. 그것이 다가오면서—아주 빠르게 다가왔다—한층 거센 기세로 힘을 쓰더니, 문을 두드린 뒤에는 마치 몸을 숙이고 열쇠 구멍에 대고 콧김을 뿜는 것 같은 소리가 났다.\n\n매기가 문을 열기도 전에, 팽크스 씨가 바깥에서 문을 열고는, 모자도 없이 맨머리를 더없이 산발한 채로 서서, 리틀 도릿의 어깨 너머로 그녀와 클레넘을 바라보았다. 손에는 불붙은 시가가 들려 있었고, 그는 맥주와 담배 연기 냄새를 풍기며 들어섰다.\n\n\"점쟁이 팽크스올시다.\" 그가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"운수를 봐 드리지요.\"\n\n그는 거뭇하게 미소 지으며 그들을 향해 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬었는데, 더없이 묘한 분위기였다. 마치 자기가 고용주의 돈벌이꾼이 아니라, 마셜시와 간수장과 모든 옥졸과 모든 재학생을 거느린 의기양양한 주인이라도 되는 것 같았다. 그는 한껏 흡족해진 나머지 시가를 입에 물고는(분명 담배를 피우는 사람이 아니었다), 그러느라 오른쪽 눈을 질끈 감은 채 어찌나 깊이 빨아들였던지, 부들부들 떨고 캑캑거리는 발작을 겪었다. 그러나 그 발작이 한창인 와중에도 그는 자기를 소개하는 그 애용하는 인사말을 또 한 번 되뇌려 했다. \"저엄쟁이 팽크스올시다, 운수를 봐 드리지요.\"\n\n\"다들이랑 같이 저녁 시간을 보내고 있었답니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"노래를 불렀지요. '흰 모래 회색 모래'에서 한 파트를 맡았어요. 나야 그런 거 아무것도 몰라요. 상관없어요. 뭐든 어느 파트든 맡지요. 다 똑같아요, 목소리만 크면.\"\n\n처음에 클레넘은 그가 취한 줄 알았다. 그러나 곧 그가 비록 맥주 탓에 조금 나빠졌(거나 좋아졌)을지는 몰라도, 그 흥분의 주성분은 엿기름으로 빚거나 어떤 곡물이나 열매로 증류한 것이 아님을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 도릿 양?\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"제가 잠깐 달려와 들여다봐도 신경 안 쓰실 줄 알았어요. 클레넘 씨가 여기 계시다는 건 도릿 씨한테 들었지요. 안녕하십니까, 선생님?\"\n\n클레넘은 고맙다고 하며, 그가 그토록 들떠 있는 걸 보니 기쁘다고 말했다.\n\n\"들떠요!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"기분이 끝내주게 좋답니다, 선생님. 일 분도 못 머물러요, 안 그러면 자리를 비운 게 들통날 텐데, 그러긴 싫거든요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n그는 그녀에게 말을 걸고 그녀를 바라보는 데 만족할 줄 모르는 기쁨을 느끼는 듯했다. 동시에 흥분해서 머리카락을 곤두세웠는데, 마치 거뭇한 종류의 앵무새 같았다.\n\n\"여기 온 지 반 시간도 안 됐어요. 도릿 씨가 의장석에 앉아 계신 걸 알고, '내가 가서 떠받쳐 드려야지!' 했지요. 사실 따지면 블리딩 하트 야드에 내려가 있어야 마땅하지만, 그 사람들이야 내일 들들 볶으면 되니까요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n그의 작은 검은 눈이 전기를 띤 듯 반짝였다. 머리카락을 헝클어뜨리니 그 머리카락마저 반짝이는 것 같았다. 그는 어찌나 충전된 상태였던지, 그의 몸 어느 부위에든 손가락 마디를 갖다 대면 불꽃과 탁탁 소리가 튀어나올 법했다.\n\n\"여기 사람들 정말 멋지네요.\" 팽크스가 말했다.—\"그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n그녀는 그가 반쯤 무서워 무어라 말해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 그는 클레넘을 향해 고갯짓하며 웃었다.\n\n\"저 사람 신경 쓰지 마세요, 도릿 양. 저분도 우리 편이니까요. 사람들 앞에서는 나를 아는 척하지 않기로 약속했지만, 클레넘 씨는 예외였지요. 저분도 우리 편이에요. 한패예요. 안 그렇습니까, 클레넘 씨?—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n이 기이한 인물의 흥분이 빠르게 클레넘에게 옮아가고 있었다. 리틀 도릿은 놀라움 속에 이것을 보았고, 두 사람이 재빠른 눈짓을 주고받는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"내가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는데,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"정말이지 뭐였는지 까먹었네. 아, 알겠다! 여기 사람들 정말 멋지다고요. 다들한테 한턱 쏘고 있었거든요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n\"정말 후하시네요.\" 그녀가 대꾸하며, 두 사람 사이에 오가는 또 한 번의 재빠른 눈짓을 알아챘다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그런 말씀 마세요. 사실 내가 재산을 물려받게 됐거든요. 그러니 한턱 낼 형편이 되지요. 여기서 사람들한테 잔치를 한번 베풀까 봐요. 마당에 식탁을 차리고. 빵을 산더미로. 파이프를 다발로. 담배를 수레 가득. 누구에게나 구운 소고기와 자두 푸딩. 한 사람당 진한 흑맥주 한 쿼트씩. 좋다면 포도주도 한 파인트씩, 당국이 허가만 한다면요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n그의 태도에, 아니 그보다도 그 태도를 점점 알아채 가는 클레넘 때문에(그녀는 팽크스 씨가 또 한 번 새로 말을 걸고 앵무새 같은 시늉을 할 때마다 그를 바라보았으므로) 그녀는 어찌나 혼란에 빠졌던지, 대답이랍시고 입술만 움직일 뿐 아무 말도 이루지 못했다.\n\n\"아, 그러고 보니!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"당신은 그 작은 손 위에 우리 뒤에 무엇이 있었는지를 살아서 알게 되리라 했지요. 그러니 알게 될 거예요, 알게 되고말고요, 내 사랑.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\"\n\n그는 갑자기 자제했다. 큰 불꽃놀이의 큰 변화에서 무수한 점들이 터져 나오듯, 이제 그의 머리 온통 사방으로 솟구쳐 오른 그 추가된 검은 갈래들을 어디서 다 가져왔는지는 놀라운 수수께끼였다.\n\n\"하지만 자리를 비운 게 들통나겠어요.\" 그는 다시 그 말로 돌아갔다. \"그러긴 싫거든요. 클레넘 씨, 우리 둘이 거래를 했지요. 내가 약속을 지키는 사람이란 걸 알게 되실 거라고 했고요. 이제 그걸 보여 드리지요, 선생님, 잠깐 방 밖으로 나와 주신다면요. 도릿 양, 안녕히 주무세요. 도릿 양, 행운을 빕니다.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 두 손을 빠르게 흔들고는 콧김을 뿜으며 계단을 내려갔다. 아서가 어찌나 급한 걸음으로 그를 뒤따랐던지, 마지막 층계참에서 하마터면 그를 넘어뜨려 마당으로 굴려 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n\"대체 무슨 일이오, 제발!\" 두 사람이 함께 마당으로 뛰쳐나오자 아서가 다그쳐 물었다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 선생님. 러그 씨예요. 소개해 드리지요.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 역시 모자도 없고, 역시 시가를 든 데다, 역시 맥주와 담배 연기의 후광에 둘러싸인 또 한 사람을 내보였다. 그 사람은 팽크스 씨만큼 흥분하지는 않았으나, 팽크스 씨의 광란에 견주어 보면 차분한 절도로 옅어졌기에 망정이지 그러지 않았다면 거의 실성한 것과 다름없을 상태였다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 이쪽은 러그 씨예요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"잠깐만요. 펌프로 갑시다.\"\n\n그들은 펌프로 자리를 옮겼다. 팽크스 씨는 곧장 주둥이 아래에 머리를 들이밀고는, 러그 씨에게 손잡이를 힘껏 한 번 세게 돌려 달라고 청했다. 러그 씨가 글자 그대로 따라 주자, 팽크스 씨는 콧김을 뿜고 푸푸거리며 시원하게 나와서는 손수건으로 몸을 닦았다.\n\n\"덕분에 좀 개운해졌습니다.\" 그가 놀라서 서 있는 클레넘에게 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"하지만 정말이지, 우리가 아는 걸 알면서, 그녀의 아버지가 저 의장석에서 연설하는 걸 듣고 있자니, 우리가 아는 걸 알면서, 그녀가 저 방에서 저런 옷차림으로 있는 걸 보고 있자니, 그건 그만—등 좀 내어 주세요, 러그 씨—조금만 더 높게요, 선생님,—됐어요!\"\n\n그러고는 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 마셜시의 그 포석 위에서, 저물어 가는 저녁 어스름 속에서, 하고많은 사람 중에 하필 팽크스 씨가, 펜턴빌의 종합 대리인이자 회계사이자 채권 추심인인 러그 씨의 머리와 어깨 위를 훌쩍 뛰어넘었다. 두 발로 내려선 그는 클레넘의 단춧구멍을 붙잡고 펌프 뒤로 데려가더니, 헐떡이며 주머니에서 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다.\n\n러그 씨도 헐떡이며 주머니에서 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 클레넘이 속삭이며 말했다. \"당신, 무언가를 알아냈군요.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 말로는 도무지 전할 수 없는 감격에 젖어 대답했다. \"아무래도 그런 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"누군가가 연루됩니까?\"\n\n\"연루라니, 무슨 말씀이세요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"어떤 은폐나 부정한 거래 같은 것에 말이오.\"\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"고맙기도 하셔라!\" 클레넘이 속으로 말했다. \"자, 보여 줘요.\"\n\n\"이걸 아셔야 합니다.\" 팽크스가 콧김을 뿜으며 열에 들떠 서류를 펼치고, 짧게 고압으로 터져 나오는 문장들로 말했다. \"혈통서가 어디 있지? 명세표 제4호는 어디 있어요, 러그 씨? 아! 됐다! 여기 있군.—이걸 아셔야 해요, 우리가 바로 오늘로 사실상 다 마쳤다는 걸 말입니다. 법적으로야 하루 이틀은 더 걸리겠지만요. 길게 잡아도 일주일이라고 해 두지요. 우리는 얼마나 됐는지도 모를 만큼 밤낮으로 이 일에 매달렸어요. 러그 씨, 얼마나 됐는지 아시죠? 아니, 됐어요. 말하지 마세요. 나만 헷갈리게 할 테니까. 그녀에게는 당신이 알려 주시는 겁니다, 클레넘 씨. 우리가 허락하기 전엔 안 돼요. 그 대략적인 합계가 어디 있지요, 러그 씨? 아! 여기 있군! 여기요, 선생님! 이게 당신이 그녀에게 깨우쳐 줘야 할 내용입니다. 저 사람이 당신의 마셜시 아버지요!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint\n\n필연적인 운명에 순응하여 미글스 일가, 즉 그 사람들을 최대한 좋게 받아들이고, 아서와의 면담에서 그럴 가능성을 미리 내다보았던바 자신의 철학에 가해질 부담에 머리를 숙이며, 가우언 부인은 아들의 결혼에 반대하지 않기로 너그러이 결심했다. 이 결심에 이르고 또 흡족하게 다다르는 과정에서, 그녀는 아마도 모성애뿐 아니라 세 가지 타산적 고려에도 영향을 받았을 것이다.\n\n그중 첫째는, 아들이 한 번도 그녀의 동의를 구할 의향을 조금도 내비친 적이 없었고, 동의 없이도 해낼 자기 능력에 대해 어떠한 불신도 보인 적이 없었다는 점이었을 테고, 둘째는, 감사할 줄 아는 조국(그리고 어느 배너클)이 그녀에게 베푼 연금이, 자기 헨리가 형편이 매우 넉넉한 사람의 외동 귀염둥이 딸과 결혼하게 되면 자식이 그리로 조금씩 손을 뻗는 일에서 풀려나리라는 점이었을 테고, 셋째는, 헨리의 빚이 명백히 장인 될 사람에 의해 제단 난간 앞에서 곧장 청산되리라는 점이었을 것이다. 이 세 갈래 신중함의 요점에다, 가우언 부인이 미글스 씨가 동의했다는 사실을 안 바로 그 순간에 자기 동의를 내주었다는 점과, 미글스 씨의 반대가 처음부터 줄곧 이 결혼의 유일한 장애였다는 점까지 더해 놓고 보면, 별다른 것도 아닌 어느 작고한 위원의 미망인이 그 명민한 머릿속에서 이런 생각들을 굴렸다는 것은 더없이 그럴듯한 일이 된다.\n\n그러나 친지와 지인들 사이에서 그녀는, 이것이 무척이나 불운한 일이며, 그 일로 자신이 몹시 상심하고 있으며, 헨리가 빠져 있는 것은 완벽한 마법 같은 홀림이며, 자신이 오랫동안 그것을 반대해 왔으나 어미가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐는 둥의 가식을 부지런히 키워 가며 자신의 개인적 위엄과 배너클 가문 혈통의 위엄을 지켰다. 그녀는 이미 미글스 가의 친구로서 아서 클레넘을 불러다 이 꾸민 이야기의 증인으로 세운 바 있었고, 이번에는 같은 목적으로 그 가족 자체를 옭아매는 수순으로 이어 갔다. 미글스 씨에게 허락한 첫 면담에서, 그녀는 거역할 수 없는 압력에 비통하지만 우아하게 굴복하는 자리로 슬그머니 자신을 밀어 넣었다. 극도의 정중함과 좋은 가정교육으로, 그녀는 어려움을 만든 사람이 그가 아니라 자기였고, 결국 양보한 것도 자기였으며, 희생은 그의 것이 아니라 자기의 것이라는 양 꾸며 보였다. 같은 가장을, 같은 정중한 솜씨로, 그녀는 미글스 부인에게도 떠넘겼는데, 마치 마술사가 그 순진한 부인에게 카드를 강요하듯 했다. 그리고 장차 며느리가 될 사람이 아들의 손에 이끌려 그녀 앞에 소개되자, 그녀는 그녀를 끌어안으며 \"얘야, 도대체 헨리에게 무슨 짓을 했길래 그 애를 이토록 홀려 놨니!\" 하고 말했고, 동시에 몇 방울의 눈물이 작은 알약처럼 그녀의 코에 발린 화장 분을 밀어내려 흐르도록 두었으니, 이는 자신이 그 불운을 견디며 보이는 침착함의 이면에서 속으로 얼마나 괴로워하고 있는지를 알리는 섬세하나 가슴 저린 신호였다.\n\n가우언 부인의 친구들(그녀는 자신이 곧 사교계라는 점과, 그 권력과 친밀하고 격의 없는 관계를 유지한다는 점을 동시에 자랑으로 삼았다) 가운데, 머들 부인은 맨 앞줄을 차지했다. 사실 햄프턴 코트의 보헤미안들은 예외 없이 머들을 벼락출세한 자라며 코를 치켜들었지만, 그의 부를 숭배하느라 납작 엎드림으로써 다시 그 코를 내렸다. 이렇게 코를 올렸다 내렸다 보정하는 점에서, 그들은 재무부며 법조계며 주교며 그 밖의 모든 자들과 꽤나 비슷했다.\n\n앞서 말한 자비로운 동의를 내준 뒤, 가우언 부인은 자기 위로의 방문 길에 머들 부인을 찾아갔다. 그녀는 이 목적을 위해, 영국 역사의 그 시기에 불경스럽게도 알약통이라 불리던 외마차를 타고 도시로 들어갔다. 그것은 영세한 마차 대여업자의 소유였는데, 그가 직접 몰았고, 햄프턴 코트 궁의 노부인들 대다수에게 하루 단위나 시간 단위로 빌려주었다. 다만 그 거처에서는 그 마차 한 벌 전체를, 그때그때 빌린 사람의 개인 소유물로 암묵적으로 여기고, 마차 대여업자는 현재 빌린 사람 외에는 아무도 개인적으로 아는 척하지 않는다는 것이 하나의 예법이었다. 그러니 우주에서 가장 큰 마차 대여업자라 할 에두름국의 배너클 일가도, 늘 당장 손에 쥔 일거리 외에는 어떤 일거리도 모르는 척하곤 했다.\n\n머들 부인은 집에 있었고, 진홍과 금빛으로 꾸민 자신의 둥지에 들어 있었으며, 곁의 횃대에 앉은 앵무새가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채, 마치 그녀를 더 큰 종류의 또 다른 화려한 앵무새로 여기기라도 하듯 그녀를 지켜보고 있었다. 그곳으로 가우언 부인이, 발그레한 반점들 위에 떨어지는 빛을 부드럽게 누그러뜨리는 좋아하는 초록 부채를 들고 들어섰다.\n\n\"여보, 내 소중한 이여,\" 가우언 부인은 시시한 잡담을 조금 나눈 뒤 이 부채로 친구의 손등을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"당신은 내 유일한 위안이에요. 내가 말했던 헨리의 그 일이 결국 성사될 모양이에요. 자, 당신은 어떻게 생각하나요? 나는 정말 죽도록 알고 싶어요. 당신만큼 사교계를 잘 대표하고 표현하는 사람이 없으니까요.\"\n\n머들 부인은 사교계가 늘 살펴보곤 하던 그 가슴께를 점검하고는, 머들 씨와 런던 보석상들의 그 진열창이 잘 정돈되어 있음을 확인한 뒤 대답했다.\n\n\"남자 쪽의 결혼이라면 말이에요, 여보, 사교계는 그가 결혼으로 재산을 만회하길 요구하지요. 사교계는 그가 결혼으로 득을 보길 요구해요. 사교계는 그가 결혼으로 번듯한 살림을 차리길 요구하고요. 그렇지 않다면 사교계는 그가 결혼과 무슨 상관이 있는지 알 수가 없답니다. 새야, 조용히 해!\"\n\n머리 위 새장의 앵무새가, 마치 자기가 판사라도 되는 양(실제로 꽤 판사처럼 보이기도 했다) 이 회담을 주재하다가, 그 설명을 한 번의 비명으로 마무리 지었기 때문이었다.\n\n\"경우가 있긴 해요,\" 머들 부인은 좋아하는 손의 새끼손가락을 우아하게 구부려, 그 깔끔한 동작으로 자기 말을 한층 깔끔하게 다듬으며 말했다. \"남자가 젊지도 우아하지도 않으나 부유하고 이미 번듯한 살림을 갖춘 경우가 있지요. 그건 다른 종류예요. 그런 경우엔—\"\n\n머들 부인은 눈처럼 흰 어깨를 으쓱하고는 보석 받침대 위에 손을 얹으며, 작은 기침을 누르는 시늉을 했는데, 마치 \"그야, 남자가 그런 부류를 노리는 거지요, 여보\" 하고 덧붙이려는 듯했다. 그러자 앵무새가 또 비명을 질렀고, 그녀는 안경을 들어 새를 바라보며 말했다. \"새야! 좀 조용히 해!\"\n\n\"하지만 젊은 남자들은,\" 머들 부인이 다시 말을 이었다. \"여보, 젊은 남자라 하면 내가 무슨 뜻인지 알 거예요—나는 앞날이 창창한 남의 집 아들들을 말하는 거예요—그들은 결혼으로 사교계에서 더 나은 위치에 자신을 두어야 해요, 그러지 않으면 사교계는 그들이 바보짓 하는 걸 정말이지 참아 주지 않을 거예요. 이게 다 끔찍이도 속물스럽게 들리지요,\" 머들 부인은 자기 둥지에 등을 기대며 다시 안경을 들어 올리고 말했다. \"그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n\"하지만 사실이잖아요,\" 가우언 부인이 무척 도덕적인 태도로 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 한순간도 다툴 여지가 없는 얘기죠,\" 머들 부인이 대꾸했다. \"사교계가 그 문제에 대해 마음을 정해 버렸으니, 더 할 말이 없는 거예요. 우리가 좀 더 원시적인 상태에 있다면, 우리가 나뭇잎 지붕 아래 살면서 은행 계좌 대신 소며 양이며 짐승을 친다면(그건 참 근사할 거예요. 여보, 나는 천성이 어지간히 목가적인 사람이거든요), 그러면 좋고 좋겠지요. 하지만 우리는 나뭇잎 아래 살지도, 소며 양이며 짐승을 치지도 않잖아요. 나는 가끔 에드먼드 스파클러에게 그 구별을 일깨워 주느라 완전히 진을 빼곤 한답니다.\"\n\n이 젊은 신사의 이름이 언급되자, 가우언 부인은 초록 부채 너머로 바라보며 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"여보, 당신도 이 나라의 비참한 상태를 알잖아요—존 배너클의 그 불행한 양보들 말이에요!—그러니 내가 어쩌고저쩌고만큼이나 가난한 이유도 알 테고요.\"\n\n\"교회 생쥐만큼?\" 머들 부인이 미소 지으며 거들었다.\n\n\"나는 속담에 나오는 또 다른 교회 인물, 욥을 떠올리고 있었어요,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"어느 쪽이든 좋아요. 따라서 당신 아들의 처지와 내 아들의 처지 사이에 큰 차이가 있다는 걸 숨겨 봐야 부질없지요. 게다가 덧붙이자면, 헨리에겐 재능이 있어요—\"\n\n\"에드먼드에겐 분명코 없는 그것 말이지요,\" 머들 부인이 더없이 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"—그리고 그 재능이 좌절과 맞물려,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그 애를 어떤 추구로 이끌었는데, 그게—아아, 저런! 당신도 알잖아요, 여보. 헨리의 처지가 이렇게 다르니, 문제는 내가 받아들일 수 있는 가장 격이 낮은 결혼이 무엇이냐 하는 거예요.\"\n\n머들 부인은 자기 팔(아름답게 빚어진 팔로, 팔찌에 딱 어울리는 그런 팔이었다)을 가만히 들여다보는 데 몰두한 나머지, 한동안 대답하기를 잊었다. 마침내 그 침묵에 정신이 든 그녀는, 그 팔을 모아 끼고는 감탄스러운 침착함으로 친구의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 묻듯이 말했다. \"그—래요? 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서요, 여보,\" 가우언 부인이 아까만큼 살갑지는 않은 투로 말했다. \"당신이 그것에 대해 무슨 말을 해 줄지 듣고 싶어요.\"\n\n이때 마지막으로 비명을 지른 뒤 한쪽 다리로 서 있던 앵무새가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 두 다리로 비웃듯 위아래로 까닥거리다가, 결국 다시 한쪽 다리로 서서, 머리를 비틀 수 있는 한 최대로 갸웃한 채 대답을 기다리며 멈췄다.\n\n\"신사가 그 숙녀와 함께 무엇을 얻게 되느냐고 묻는 게 돈만 밝히는 소리처럼 들리긴 하네요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 사교계가 아마 돈을 좀 밝히긴 하잖아요, 여보, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"내가 알아낼 수 있는 바로는,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"헨리가 빚에서 풀려나리라고 말해도 될 것 같아요—\"\n\n\"빚이 많아요?\" 머들 부인이 안경 너머로 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 어지간하긴 할 거예요,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"흔한 정도란 뜻이군요. 알겠어요. 바로 그거죠,\" 머들 부인이 편안한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그 애 아버지가 그들에게 연 삼백 파운드, 어쩌면 다 합쳐 그보다 좀 더 되는 생활비를 대 주리라는 거예요. 그게 이탈리아에서라면—\"\n\n\"어머! 이탈리아에 가요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"헨리가 공부를 하러요. 왜인지는 굳이 짐작하느라 애쓸 것 없어요, 여보. 그 끔찍한 예술 말이에요—\"\n\n과연 그랬다. 머들 부인은 상심한 친구의 감정을 아껴 주려고 서둘렀다. 알겠어요. 더 말하지 말아요!\n\n\"그리고,\" 가우언 부인이 낙담한 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"그게 다예요. 그게,\" 가우언 부인이 잠시 초록 부채를 접어 그것으로 턱을 톡톡 치며 거듭 말했다(그 턱은 이중 턱이 되어 가는 중이었으니, 지금은 한 턱 반쯤이라 할 만했다). \"그게 다예요! 노인들이 죽고 나면, 더 나올 것이 있겠지요. 하지만 그게 어떻게 제한되거나 묶여 있을지는 모르겠어요. 게다가 그 점으로 말하면, 그들은 영영 살지도 몰라요. 여보, 그들은 딱 그렇게 할 부류의 사람들이거든요.\"\n\n이제 친구인 사교계를 정말로 꽤나 잘 알고, 사교계의 어머니들이 어떤지, 사교계의 딸들이 어떤지, 사교계의 혼인 시장이 어떤지, 그 안에서 시세가 어떻게 매겨지는지, 큰손 매수자들을 두고 어떤 책략과 맞책략이 펼쳐지는지, 어떤 흥정과 행상질이 오가는지를 두루 아는 머들 부인은, 그 넉넉한 가슴 깊은 곳에서 이만하면 꽤 괜찮은 건수라고 생각했다. 그러나 자신에게 기대되는 바가 무엇인지 알고, 키워 줘야 할 허구의 정확한 성질을 알아차린 그녀는, 그것을 섬세하게 두 팔에 안아, 자기 몫으로 요구되는 윤기를 덧칠해 주었다.\n\n\"그래서 그게 다라는 거죠, 여보?\" 그녀가 다정한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다. \"저런, 저런! 잘못은 당신에게 없어요. 당신이 자책할 일은 하나도 없고요. 당신은 그 유명한 굳센 마음을 발휘해서, 최선을 다해 받아들여야 해요.\"\n\n\"그 처녀의 가족은,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"물론 헨리를—법률가들 말마따나—'차지하고 붙들어 두려고' 더없이 안간힘을 썼지요.\"\n\n\"물론 그랬겠죠, 여보,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"나는 가능한 모든 반대를 고집했고, 헨리를 그 인연에서 떼어 놓을 방도를 찾느라 아침이고 한낮이고 밤이고 애를 태웠어요.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그러셨겠죠, 여보,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 다 소용없었어요. 모든 게 내 밑에서 무너져 버렸지요. 자, 말해 줘요, 여보. 결국 헨리가 사교계에 속하지 않은 사람들 사이에서 결혼하는 데 더없이 마지못한 동의를 내준 것이, 내가 정당화될 일인가요? 아니면 내가 변명할 수 없는 나약함으로 행동한 건가요?\"\n\n이 직접적인 호소에 답하여, 머들 부인은(사교계의 여사제로서 말하건대) 가우언 부인을 향해, 그녀는 크게 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 깊이 동정받아 마땅하며, 가장 고귀한 역할을 맡았으며, 시련의 용광로에서 한층 정련되어 나왔노라고 단언했다. 그리고 물론 자기 자신의 닳아빠진 눈가림을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보고, 머들 부인도 그것을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 본다는 것을 알며, 사교계도 그것을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보리라는 것을 아는 가우언 부인은, 그럼에도 불구하고, 들어갈 때와 마찬가지로, 엄청난 만족감과 진중함을 안고 이 의식에서 걸어 나왔다.\n\n이 회담은 오후 네다섯 시쯤에 열렸는데, 그 무렵 캐번디시 광장의 할리 가 일대는 온통 마차 바퀴 소리와 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리로 울려 퍼졌다. 머들 씨가 매일의 업무, 즉 세계적인 상업 진취성과 기술과 자본의 거대한 결합을 감상할 줄 아는 문명 세계의 방방곡곡에서 영국이라는 이름을 점점 더 떠받들게 만드는 일을 마치고 집에 돌아온 것이 바로 그 시점이었다. 머들 씨의 사업이 돈을 찍어 내는 것이라는 점 말고는, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지 조금이라도 아는 사람이 아무도 없었지만, 모든 격식 차린 자리에서 누구나 그의 일을 이런 식으로 정의했고, 낙타와 바늘귀의 비유에 대한 가장 최근의 점잖은 풀이는 그것을 따져 묻지 않고 받아들이는 것이었다.\n\n이처럼 화려한 일거리가 맡겨진 신사치고, 머들 씨는 조금 평범해 보였고, 오히려 그 막대한 거래를 진행하는 와중에 어쩌다 어느 열등한 영혼과 머리를 맞바꾸기라도 한 사람 같았다. 그는 자기 저택을 음울하게 거닐던 중에 두 부인 앞에 나타났는데, 그 거닒이라는 것에는 집사장의 면전을 피하는 것 말고는 별다른 목적이 없어 보였다.\n\n\"실례했습니다,\" 그가 당황하여 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"여기 앵무새 말고 다른 사람이 있는 줄 몰랐어요.\"\n\n그러나 머들 부인이 \"들어와도 돼요!\" 하고 말하고, 가우언 부인이 막 가려던 참이라며 이미 일어나 작별 인사를 하려 했으므로, 그는 들어와서 멀찍이 떨어진 창문 밖을 내다보며 섰는데, 불편한 외투 소맷부리 아래로 두 손을 엇갈려 손목을 움켜쥔 것이 마치 자기 자신을 구금하기라도 하는 듯했다. 이런 자세로 그는 곧장 깊은 상념에 빠졌고, 둘만 십오 분쯤 남게 되자 오토만에 앉은 아내가 그를 부르고 나서야 비로소 거기서 깨어났다.\n\n\"응? 뭐라고?\" 머들 씨가 그녀 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고요?\" 머들 부인이 되받았다. \"내가 보기엔, 당신이 내 불평을 한 마디도 듣지 못했다는 게 무슨 일이지요.\"\n\n\"당신 불평이라니, 머들 부인?\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 병으로 고생하는 줄은 몰랐는데. 무슨 병이오?\"\n\n\"당신에 대한 불평이에요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아! 나에 대한 불평이라,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"도대체 무엇이—내가 무엇을—나한테 무엇을 불평할 게 있다는 거요, 머들 부인?\"\n\n물러서고 멍하고 곱씹는 그 특유의 태도로, 그가 이 질문을 형태로 빚어내는 데에는 어느 정도 시간이 걸렸다. 자기가 이 집의 주인임을 스스로 납득시켜 보려는 일종의 희미한 시도로, 그는 결국 앵무새에게 집게손가락을 내밀었는데, 앵무새는 그 화제에 관한 제 의견을, 즉각 그 손가락에 부리를 박아 넣음으로써 표명했다.\n\n\"방금 말하길, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 상처 난 손가락을 입에 문 채 말했다. \"나한테 불평이 있다고 했지요?\"\n\n\"되풀이해 말해야만 한다는 사실보다 더 절실하게 그 정당함을 보여 줄 수 있는 불평은 없을 거예요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"차라리 벽에다 대고 말하는 편이 나았겠어요. 새한테 말하는 게 훨씬 나았을 거예요. 적어도 새는 비명이라도 질렀을 테니까.\"\n\n\"내가 비명을 지르길 바라는 건 아니겠지요, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 정말 모르겠네요,\" 머들 부인이 쏘아붙였다. \"그렇게 침울하고 정신 나간 듯이 있느니, 차라리 그러는 편이 낫지 않을까 싶어요. 적어도 당신이 주변에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지는 알고 있다는 걸 사람들이 알 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"사람이 비명을 지르고도 그런 상태일 수 있지 않소, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 무겁게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 지금 당신처럼 비명 한번 안 지르고도 음울하게 굴 수 있고요,\" 머들 부인이 되받았다. \"그건 정말 맞는 말이에요. 내가 당신에게 하는 불평이 무엇인지 알고 싶다면, 아주 평범한 몇 마디로 말해서, 당신이 사교계에 자신을 맞추지 못할 거면 정말이지 사교계에 나가지 말아야 한다는 거예요.\"\n\n머들 씨는 머리에 남은 머리카락에 두 손을 비비 꼬아 넣어, 마치 그것으로 자기 몸을 들어 올리듯이 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다.\n\n\"아니, 온갖 지옥의 권능을 두고 말하건대, 머들 부인, 나보다 사교계를 위해 더 많은 일을 하는 사람이 어디 있소? 이 집을 보시오, 머들 부인? 이 가구들을 보시오, 머들 부인? 거울을 들여다보고 당신 자신을 보시오, 머들 부인? 이 모든 것에 든 비용을, 또 이게 다 누구를 위해 마련된 것인지를 당신이 안단 말이오? 그런데도 내가 사교계에 나가선 안 된다고 말하겠소? 이런 식으로 사교계에 돈을 쏟아붓는 나한테? 늘 말할 수 있을 법한 나한테—저—저—돈을 가득 실은 살수차에 나 자신을 매고서, 평생 날마다 돌아다니며 사교계를 흠뻑 적셔 주고 있다고 말이오.\"\n\n\"제발 흥분하지 말아요, 머들 씨,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"흥분이라고?\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"당신은 사람을 절망하게 만드는구려. 내가 사교계에 맞추려고 하는 일의 절반도 당신은 모르오. 내가 그것을 위해 치르는 희생에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고.\"\n\n\"내가 아는 건,\" 머들 부인이 되받았다. \"당신이 이 나라 최고의 사람들을 맞이한다는 거예요. 당신이 이 나라 사교계 전체 안에서 움직인다는 것도 알고요. 그리고 내가 알기로는(아니, 그것에 대해 우스꽝스러운 가식을 부리지 않고 말하자면, 나는 내가 안다는 걸 알아요), 그 안에서 당신을 떠받치는 게 누구인지도 알지요, 머들 씨.\"\n\n\"머들 부인,\" 그 신사는 칙칙한 적황색 얼굴을 닦으며 되받았다. \"그건 나도 당신만큼 잘 알고 있소. 당신이 사교계의 장식품이 아니었고, 내가 사교계의 후원자가 아니었다면, 당신과 나는 결코 맺어지지 않았을 거요. 후원자라 함은, 사교계에 먹고 마시고 구경할 온갖 값비싼 것들을 대 주는 사람을 두고 하는 말이오. 그런데, 내가 그것을 위해 그토록 많은 일을 하고도—그토록 많은 일을 하고도,\" 머들 씨는 아내가 눈꺼풀을 치켜뜨게 만들 만큼 격한 어조로 거듭 말했다. \"그 모든 것을 하고도—전부 다!—그러고도 내가 그것과 어울릴 자격이 없다고 말하다니, 참 대단한 보답이로구려.\"\n\n\"내 말은,\" 머들 부인이 차분하게 대답했다. \"당신이 좀 더 느긋하고 덜 골몰함으로써 거기에 어울리게 처신해야 한다는 거예요. 당신처럼 사업 일거리를 그렇게 끼고 다니는 데에는 확실히 천박한 데가 있어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 어떻게 끼고 다닌다는 거요, 머들 부인?\" 머들 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"그걸 어떻게 끼고 다니느냐고요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"거울에 비친 당신 자신을 보세요.\"\n\n머들 씨는 저도 모르게 가장 가까운 거울 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸고, 탁한 피가 천천히 관자놀이로 몰려드는 채로, 사람이 제 소화 작용을 두고 책임 추궁을 당해야 하는 거냐고 물었다.\n\n\"당신에겐 의사가 있잖아요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그자는 나한테 아무 도움이 안 되오,\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n머들 부인은 발판을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"게다가,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 소화 작용은 말도 안 되는 소리고요. 나는 당신 소화 작용을 말하는 게 아니에요. 나는 당신 태도를 말하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"머들 부인,\" 남편이 되받았다. \"그건 당신한테 기대하는 바요. 태도는 당신이 대고, 돈은 내가 대는 거지.\"\n\n\"나는 당신이,\" 머들 부인이 방석 사이에 편안히 기대며 말했다. \"사람들을 매혹하기를 바라지 않아요. 당신이 수고를 떠맡거나 매력적으로 보이려 애쓰기를 바라지도 않고요. 나는 그저, 다른 모두가 그러듯이, 당신도 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 말기를—아니, 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않는 것처럼 보이기를 청할 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"내가 무언가에 마음 쓴다고 말한 적이라도 있소?\" 머들 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"말이라뇨? 천만에요! 당신이 그런 말을 한들 아무도 귀 기울이지 않을 거예요. 하지만 당신은 그걸 드러내잖아요.\"\n\n\"무얼 드러내? 내가 무얼 드러낸다는 거요?\" 머들 씨가 다급히 캐물었다.\n\n\"이미 말했잖아요. 당신은 사업 걱정거리며 계획들을, 그것들이 속한 시티나 그 어디에든 놔두지 않고 끼고 다니는 걸 드러내요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"아니면 적어도 그래 보이기라도 하든가요. 그래 보이는 것만으로도 충분해요. 나는 그 이상은 바라지도 않아요. 그런데 당신은, 설령 당신이 목수였다 한들 그날의 계산과 궁리에 그보다 더 골몰해 보이지는 못했을 만큼, 늘 그렇게 골몰한 모습을 드러내잖아요.\"\n\n\"목수라!\" 머들 씨가 신음 비슷한 것을 억누르며 되뇌었다. \"목수가 되는 거라면 나는 그리 싫지 않을 것 같소, 머들 부인.\"\n\n\"그리고 내 불평은,\" 부인이 그 낮은 중얼거림을 무시한 채 말을 이었다. \"그게 사교계의 격조가 아니라는 거고, 당신이 그걸 고쳐야 한다는 거예요, 머들 씨. 내 판단이 못 미덥거든, 에드먼드 스파클러한테라도 물어보세요.\" 방문이 열려 있었고, 머들 부인은 이제 안경 너머로 아들의 머리를 살폈다. \"에드먼드, 여기 좀 와 줄래.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 안에 들어오지는 않고 그저 머리만 들이밀어 방 안을 둘러보고 있었는데(마치 잘난 척이라곤 없는 그 젊은 아가씨를 찾아 온 집안을 뒤지기라도 하듯이), 이 말에 머리 뒤로 몸을 들이밀어 그들 앞에 섰다. 머들 부인은 그의 이해력에 맞춘 쉬운 몇 마디로 그에게 쟁점을 설명했다.\n\n그 젊은 신사는, 마치 셔츠 깃이 제 맥박이고 자기가 건강 염려증 환자이기라도 한 양 그 깃을 근심스레 짚어 보더니, 이렇게 말했다. \"친구들이 그런 얘길 하는 걸 들은 적이 있어요.\"\n\n\"에드먼드 스파클러가 그런 얘길 들은 적이 있대요,\" 머들 부인이 나른한 승리감으로 말했다. \"그것 봐요, 분명 누구나 그런 얘길 들었을 거예요!\" 사실 이는 그리 무리한 추론도 아니었으니, 스파클러 씨야말로 인간이라는 종이 모인 어떤 자리에서든, 자기 면전에서 벌어진 일로부터 무언가 인상을 받는 일이 가장 늦을 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그리고 에드먼드 스파클러가, 그걸 어떻게 들었는지 당신한테 말해 줄 거예요, 아마,\" 머들 부인이 좋아하는 손을 남편 쪽으로 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"제가,\" 스파클러 씨가 아까처럼 맥을 짚어 보고 나서 말했다. \"무엇 때문에 그 얘기가 나왔는지는 말씀드릴 수가 없네요—기억력이 형편없이 헐거워서요. 근데 거시기한 멋진 아가씨의—교육도 잘 받고—잘난 척이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없는 그 아가씨의 오라비랑 자리를 같이했을 때—말씀하신 그 무렵에—\"\n\n\"됐다! 누이 얘긴 신경 쓰지 마라,\" 머들 부인이 조금 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"그 오라비가 뭐라고 했니?\"\n\n\"한마디도 안 했어요, 어머니,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"저만큼이나 말없는 친구죠. 할 말이 궁한 것도 저랑 똑같고요.\"\n\n\"누군가가 무슨 말을 했을 거 아니냐,\" 머들 부인이 되받았다. \"누구였는지는 신경 쓰지 말고.\"\n\n[\"정말이지 저도 전혀 신경 안 써요,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.]\n\n\"그게 뭐였는지나 말해 봐라.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 다시 한번 제 맥을 짚어 보고, 대답하기에 앞서 몇 차례 호된 정신 수련을 거쳤다.\n\n\"친구들이 저희 영감을 두고—표현은 제가 지은 게 아니에요—이따금 저희 영감을 아주 근사하게 추켜세워요, 엄청 부자고 박식하다고—매수자며 은행가며 뭐며 완벽한 경이라고요—근데 가게가 그분을 무겁게 짓누른다고 해요. 그분이 가게를 짊어지고 다닌다고요, 등에다 지다시피—일감이 너무 많은 유대인 헌옷 장수들처럼요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게,\" 머들 부인이 너울거리는 휘장을 두른 채 일어서며 말했다. \"정확히 내 불평이에요. 에드먼드, 위층까지 팔 좀 빌려 다오.\"\n\n홀로 남아 사교계에 자신을 더 잘 맞출 방도를 궁리하게 된 머들 씨는, 창문 아홉 개를 연달아 내다보았고, 텅 빈 공허 아홉 자락을 보는 듯했다. 이렇게 자신을 즐겁게 한 뒤 그는 아래층으로 내려가, 일층의 모든 양탄자를 골똘히 들여다보았다. 그러고는 다시 위층으로 올라가, 이층의 모든 양탄자를 골똘히 들여다보았으니, 마치 그것들이 짓눌린 자기 영혼과 어울리는 음울한 심연이기라도 한 듯했다. 그는 언제나 그러듯 모든 방을 돌아다녔는데, 마치 그 방들에 다가갈 자격이 있는 이 세상 최후의 사람이라도 된 듯한 모습이었다. 머들 부인이 한 철에 그토록 여러 밤 집에서 손님을 맞는다고 아무리 목청껏 알린다 한들, 머들 씨가 결코 집에 없다고 알리는 것보다 더 널리, 더 명백히 알릴 수는 없었다.\n\n마침내 그는 집사장과 마주쳤는데, 그 화려한 하인의 모습은 늘 그를 끝장내곤 했다. 이 위대한 피조물에게 기가 꺾인 그는, 슬그머니 자기 화장실로 들어가 거기 틀어박혀 있다가, 머들 부인과 함께 그녀의 그 멋진 마차를 타고 만찬에 나갈 때까지 그러고 있었다. 만찬에서 그는 권세 있는 존재로 부러움과 아첨을 받았고, 재무부 대접, 법조계 대접, 주교 대접을 실컷 받았다. 그리고 자정이 한 시간쯤 지난 뒤 혼자 집에 돌아와, 제 집 현관에서 집사장에 의해 골풀 양초처럼 또다시 꺼져 버린 채, 한숨을 쉬며 잠자리로 향했다.\n\n# CHAPTER 34. A Shoal of Barnacles\n\n헨리 가우언 씨와 그 개는 그 별장의 단골 출입객으로 자리를 굳혔고, 결혼식 날짜도 정해졌다. 이 자리를 위해 배너클 일가의 모임이 열릴 예정이었는데, 이는 그토록 지체 높고 그토록 거대한 가문이, 이처럼 보잘것없는 행사가 받아들일 수 있는 한껏 광채를 그 결혼식에 드리워 주기 위함이었다.\n\n배너클 일가 전체를 한자리에 모으는 일은 두 가지 이유에서 불가능했을 것이다. 첫째, 그 명문가의 모든 구성원과 인척을 다 수용할 수 있는 건물이란 존재하지 않았기 때문이다. 둘째, 해와 달 아래 영국이 점유한 땅에서 공직의 말뚝 하나가 박혀 있는 곳이라면 어디든, 그 말뚝에 들러붙어 있는 것이 바로 배너클이었기 때문이다. 어떤 담대한 항해자가 지상 어느 한 지점에 깃대를 꽂고 영국의 이름으로 그곳을 차지한다 해도, 그 발견이 알려지기가 무섭게 에두름국은 그 땅으로 배너클 한 명과 공문서함 하나를 내보냈다. 그리하여 배너클들은 온 세계, 사방팔방에 퍼져 있었다 — 공문서함으로 나침반의 방위를 일일이 짚어 가면서.\n\n그러나 아무런 할 일도(못된 짓 빼고는) 없으면서 챙길 것은 무엇이든 있는 대양과 육지의 점점이 흩어진 모든 자리로부터 배너클들을 불러 모으는 일은, 저 막강한 프로스페로의 마법으로도 실패했을 터이지만, 상당히 많은 수의 배너클을 모으는 것만큼은 충분히 실현 가능했다. 가우언 부인은 바로 이 일에 매달려, 미글스 씨를 자주 찾아와 명단에 새 이름을 보태었고, 그가 (이 시기에 흔히 그러했듯이) 저울과 됫박이 놓인 방에서 장차 사위가 될 사람의 빚을 따지고 갚아 주는 일에 매여 있지 않을 때면 그 신사와 회담을 가졌다.\n\n미글스 씨가 가장 지체 높으리라 기대되는 배너클의 참석보다도 더 가깝게 흥미와 관심을 느끼는 결혼식 손님이 한 사람 있었다. 물론 그런 일행을 모시는 영예에 그가 무감각했던 것은 결코 아니지만 말이다. 이 손님은 클레넘이었다. 그러나 클레넘은 그 여름밤 나무들 사이에서 스스로 신성하게 여기는 약속을 했고, 마음속 기사도에 따라 그 약속이 자신을 여러 암묵적 의무에 묶어 둔다고 여겼다. 자기 자신은 잊은 채 어떤 경우에도 그녀에게 섬세히 봉사하는 일에서 결코 어긋남이 없으리라 다짐했으니, 그 첫걸음으로 그는 미글스 씨에게 명랑하게 대답했다. \"물론 가야지요.\"\n\n그의 동업자 대니얼 도이스는 미글스 씨에게 다소 걸림돌이었다. 그 훌륭한 신사는 근심 어린 마음속으로, 대니얼이 관청의 배너클주의와 한데 섞이면 결혼 축하연 자리에서조차 무언가 폭발성 화합물을 만들어 내지 않을까 영 마음을 놓지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이 나라의 죄인은 직접 트위커넘으로 내려와, 옛 친구로서의 허물없음으로, 또한 한 친구에게 베푸는 호의로서, 자신을 초대하지 말아 달라고 청함으로써 그의 불안을 덜어 주었다. \"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 신사분들과 내가 얽힌 일이라는 게, 내 쪽에서는 공공의 의무와 공공의 봉사를 하자는 것이었고, 그분들 쪽에서는 내 영혼을 닳아 없어지게 만들어 그걸 막자는 것이었으니, 우리가 한마음인 척하며 함께 먹고 마시지 않는 편이 나을 듯하네.\" 미글스 씨는 친구의 별난 성미에 무척 즐거워했고, 평소보다 한층 더 감싸 주는 너그러움의 태도로 그를 두둔하며 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"그래, 그래, 댄, 자네 그 괴팍한 고집대로 하게나.\"\n\n식이 다가오자 클레넘은 헨리 가우언 씨에게, 온갖 조용하고 꾸밈없는 방식으로, 그가 받아들이려는 어떤 우정이든 솔직하고 사심 없이 건네고 싶다는 뜻을 전하려 애썼다. 가우언 씨는 그 답례로 그를 평소의 편안함으로, 또 평소처럼 신뢰를 내보이는 태도로 대했는데, 그것은 사실 전혀 신뢰가 아니었다.\n\n\"있잖나, 클레넘,\" 결혼식을 일주일 앞둔 어느 날, 두 사람이 별장 근처를 거닐던 중에 그가 마침 이런 말을 꺼냈다. \"나는 실의에 빠진 사람일세. 그건 자네도 이미 알고 있지.\"\n\n\"솔직히 말해,\" 클레넘이 다소 거북해하며 말했다. \"어째서 그런지는 도무지 잘 모르겠는데.\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 말이야,\" 가우언이 대꾸했다. \"나는 어떤 씨족이랄까, 패거리랄까, 가문이랄까, 인척이랄까, 뭐라 부르든 좋네만, 그런 집안에 속해 있고, 그 집안은 쉰 가지 방식 중 어느 하나로든 나를 자리 잡아 줄 수 있었는데도, 아예 그러지 않기로 작정해 버렸거든. 그래서 이렇게, 나는 가난뱅이 화가 신세가 된 거지.\"\n\n클레넘이 \"하지만 다른 한편으로는—\" 하고 말을 꺼내려는데 가우언이 가로챘다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 알아. 나는 아름답고 매력적인 아가씨에게 사랑받는 행운을 누리고 있고, 나 또한 온 마음을 다해 그녀를 사랑하지.\"\n\n('그 마음이라는 게 그리 많기는 한가?' 클레넘은 생각했다. 그리고 그렇게 생각하면서 스스로 부끄러워졌다.)\n\n\"게다가 더없이 훌륭한 사람이자 너그럽고 마음씨 좋은 노신사인 장인까지 얻었지. 그래도, 어릴 적 누군가가 내 머리를 감기고 빗겨 줄 때, 그 어린 머릿속에는 다른 전망들이 같이 감기고 빗겨져 들어왔단 말이야. 그래서 내가 스스로 머리를 감고 빗게 되었을 때 그것들을 사립학교까지 들고 갔는데, 지금은 그것들 없이 여기 이렇게 있단 말이지. 그래서 나는 실의에 빠진 사람인 거야.\"\n\n클레넘은 생각했다(그리고 그렇게 생각하면서 또다시 스스로 부끄러워졌다). 이 신랑이 자기 재산처럼 처가에 들고 들어오는, 인생에서 실의에 빠졌다는 이 관념은, 이미 자기 본업에까지 해롭게 끌어들였던 어떤 신분 의식의 주장이 아닐까? 그리고 그것이 어디에서든 희망적이거나 장래성 있는 일일 수 있을까?\n\n\"그래도 쓰라리게 실의에 빠진 건 아니겠지,\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"제기랄, 그럼, 쓰라리게는 아니지,\" 가우언이 웃었다. \"내 식구들이 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 사람들도 아니거든 — 매력적인 친구들이고 나도 그들을 무척 아끼긴 하지만. 게다가 내가 그들 없이도 잘 지낼 수 있다는 걸, 그들 모두 지옥에나 가도 좋다는 걸 보여 주는 게 즐겁기도 하고. 그리고 또, 사람이란 대개 이런저런 식으로 인생에서 실의를 겪고, 그 실의에 휘둘리며 살아가는 법이지. 그래도 이건 참 사랑스러운 좋은 세상이고, 나는 이 세상이 좋아!\"\n\n\"이제 자네 앞에 그 세상이 환히 펼쳐져 있군,\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"이 여름 강물처럼 환하게 말이지,\" 상대가 열을 올리며 외쳤다. \"그리고 맹세코, 나는 이 세상에 대한 찬탄으로, 이 세상에서 한판 경주를 펼치겠다는 열정으로 달아오른다네. 더없이 좋은 옛 세상이지! 그리고 내 직업은 또 어떻고! 더없이 좋은 옛 직업 아닌가, 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n\"흥미와 야심으로 가득하다고 봅니다,\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"또 사기로도 가득하지,\" 가우언이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"사기를 빼놓을 순 없지. 나도 거기서 무너지지 않기를 바라네만, 글쎄, 바로 그 대목에서 내가 실의에 빠진 사람이라는 게 드러날지도 몰라. 충분히 근엄하게 그걸 밀고 나갈 수가 없을지도 모르거든. 우리끼리 얘기지만, 내가 딱 그걸 못 할 만큼 적당히 비뚤어져 있는 게 아닌가 싶은 위험이 좀 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"무엇을 못 한다는 거죠?\" 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"그 짓을 계속 유지하는 거 말일세. 내 앞 사람이 제 차례에 제 잇속을 챙기듯 내 차례에 내 잇속을 챙기고, 연기 든 병을 다음 사람에게 넘기는 거지. 노동이니, 연구니, 인내니, 내 예술에 헌신한다느니, 그것에 수많은 고독한 나날을 바친다느니, 그것을 위해 숱한 즐거움을 포기한다느니, 그 속에서 산다느니, 그 밖에 이러쿵저러쿵하는 척을 계속 유지하는 것 — 한마디로, 규칙에 따라 연기 든 병을 넘기는 일 말이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 사람이 제 직업이 무엇이든 그것을 존중하고, 그것을 떠받칠 의무가 있다고 여기며, 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 존중을 요구하는 건 좋은 일 아닙니까? 그렇지 않나요?\" 아서가 따져 물었다. \"그리고 가우언, 자네 직업이야말로 정말로 그런 헌신과 봉사를 요구할지 모르지. 솔직히 나는 모든 예술이 다 그렇다고 생각해 왔는데.\"\n\n\"자네 참 좋은 사람이로군, 클레넘!\" 상대가 걸음을 멈추고, 억누를 수 없는 찬탄이라도 담은 듯 그를 바라보며 외쳤다. \"참 훌륭한 친구야! 자네는 한 번도 실의에 빠져 본 적이 없지. 그건 보면 알 수 있어.\"\n\n만약 그가 정말 그런 뜻으로 한 말이라면 너무도 잔인한 일이었을 터라, 클레넘은 그가 그런 뜻이 아니라고 굳게 믿기로 마음먹었다. 가우언은 멈추지 않고 그의 어깨에 손을 얹은 채, 웃으며 가볍게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"클레넘, 나는 자네의 그 너그러운 환상을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않고, (돈이 있다면) 어떤 돈을 주고서라도 그런 장밋빛 안개 속에 살고 싶다네. 하지만 내가 내 일에서 하는 짓은 팔기 위해서 하는 거야. 우리 같은 자들이 하는 일은 죄다 팔려고 하는 짓이지. 우리가 거기서 받아 낼 수 있는 최대치를 받고 팔 생각이 없다면, 애초에 그 짓을 하지도 않을걸. 일이니까 해야 하는 거지만, 사실 그건 쉽게 해치울 수 있어. 나머지는 죄다 눈속임이고. 자, 이게 바로 실의에 빠진 사람을 사귈 때의 이점이라면 이점, 단점이라면 단점이지. 진실을 듣게 된다는 것.\"\n\n그가 무슨 말을 들었든, 그리고 그것이 진실이라는 이름에 값하는 것이든 다른 이름에 값하는 것이든, 그 말은 클레넘의 마음속에 가라앉았다. 그것은 그 안에 깊이 뿌리를 내려, 그는 헨리 가우언이 자신에게 언제까지나 골칫거리가 되리라는 두려움이 들기 시작했고, 또 자신이 그 온갖 모순과 불안과 자가당착을 지닌 '아무도 아닌 자'를 떨쳐 내고서도 여태 얻은 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없다는 생각마저 들었다. 그는 자기 가슴속에서 여전히 끊임없는 싸움이 벌어지고 있음을 깨달았다. 한쪽에는 미글스 씨의 마음 앞에서 가우언을 오직 좋은 면으로만 비춰 주겠다는 약속이 있었고, 다른 쪽에는 좋은 구석이라곤 없는 가우언의 여러 면모를 어쩔 수 없이 목격하게 되는 일이 있었다. 또한 그는, 자신이 그런 발견들을 결코 찾아 나선 적이 없으며 기꺼이, 그것도 크게 안도하며 피했을 것이라고 스스로를 일깨워 보았지만, 자신이 가우언을 일그러뜨리고 나쁘게 색칠하고 있는 것은 아닌가 하는 의구심 앞에서 양심적인 본성을 온전히 지탱하지는 못했다. 자신이 한때 어떤 사람이었는지 결코 잊을 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 가우언이 자기 앞길에 끼어들었다는 것 말고는 더 나은 이유도 없이 한때 그를 싫어했었다는 사실을 알고 있었다.\n\n이런 생각들에 시달리던 그는, 이제 결혼식이 어서 끝나, 가우언과 그의 젊은 아내가 떠나고, 자신은 남아 약속을 지키며 자신이 받아들인 너그러운 소임을 다하게 되기를 바라기 시작했다. 사실 이 마지막 한 주는 온 집안에 불안한 시간이었다. 미글스 씨는 펫 앞에서나 가우언 앞에서는 환하게 빛났다. 그러나 클레넘은 그가 저울과 됫박에 관한 자기 생각이 잔뜩 흐려진 채 홀로 있는 모습을 여러 번 보았고, 또 그가 두 연인에게 보이지 않는 곳에서, 정원이나 다른 데서 그들을 눈으로 좇으며, 가우언이 그림자처럼 내려앉은 그 예의 흐려진 얼굴을 짓고 있는 것을 자주 보았다. 이 큰 행사를 위해 집을 정돈하느라, 아버지와 어머니와 딸이 함께한 옛 여행들을 떠올리게 하는 자잘한 물건들이 여럿 흐트러지고 이 손 저 손으로 옮겨져야 했다. 그러다 보면 함께 보낸 삶에 대한 이 말 없는 증인들 한가운데에서, 때로는 펫 자신조차도 비탄과 눈물에 무너지곤 했다. 어머니들 가운데 가장 쾌활하고 분주한 미글스 부인은 노래를 부르며 모두를 북돋우고 다녔다. 그러나 그 정직한 사람도 이따금 광으로 숨어들어, 눈이 빨개질 때까지 울다가 나오면서는 그 모습을 절인 양파와 후추 탓으로 돌리고는 어느 때보다 더 맑게 노래를 불렀다. 티킷 부인은 뷰캔의 「가정 의학」에서도 상처받은 마음을 달랠 향유를 찾지 못해 깊은 우울에 시달렸고, 미니의 어린 시절에 대한 가슴 저미는 추억으로 괴로워했다. 그 추억이 그녀를 강하게 사로잡을 때면, 그녀는 보통 은밀한 전갈을 위로 올려, 자기 차림새가 응접실에 나설 만한 상태가 아니니 부엌에서 '내 아이'를 한번 보게 해 달라고 간청하곤 했다. 거기서 그녀는 도마와 밀방망이와 파이 반죽 사이에서, 눈물과 축하가 뒤섞인 가운데, 아이의 얼굴에 축복을, 아이의 마음에 축복을 빌며, 오랜 세월 정든 하인의 그 다정함으로 아이를 끌어안았으니, 그것은 참으로 어여쁜 다정함이었다.\n\n그러나 와야 할 모든 날은 오고야 마는 법, 결혼식 날도 와야 했고, 마침내 왔다. 그리고 그날과 함께 잔치에 초대받은 배너클들이 모두 도착했다.\n\n에두름국에서, 그로스브너 광장 뮤스 거리에서 타이트 배너클 씨가 왔는데, 분기 결산일을 그토록 더디게 오는 날로 만들어 버리는, 결혼 전 성이 스틸트스토킹이던 사치스러운 타이트 배너클 부인과, 갖가지 재예를 이중으로 가득 채워 넣고 시집갈 만반의 채비를 갖추었으면서도, 기대됨직한 그 번쩍이고 펑 터지는 날카로움으로 시집가지는 못하고 오히려 불발탄처럼 늑장을 부리고 있는 사치스러운 타이트 배너클 양 셋도 함께였다. 역시 에두름국 소속인 배너클 주니어도 왔는데, 그는 어쩐 영문인지 자기 보호 아래 두기로 되어 있는 이 나라의 선박 톤수 업무를 알아서 굴러가도록 내버려둔 채였으며, 사실대로 말하자면 그렇게 내버려둔다고 해서 그 보호의 효율이 조금도 떨어지지는 않았다. 가문의 발랄한 갈래에서 나온, 역시 에두름국 소속의 매력적인 젊은 배너클도 왔는데, 그는 쾌활하고 상냥하게 이 자리를 거들며, 특유의 반짝이는 태도로 그것을 '안 되게 하는 법' 교회국이 치르는 의례적인 절차와 수수료의 하나쯤으로 취급했다. 다른 세 관청에서 온 또 다른 젊은 배너클 셋도 있었는데, 모든 감각에 밋밋하기 짝이 없고 도무지 양념이 절실히 필요한 자들로, 그들이 나일강이나 고대 로마나 새로 나온 가수나 예루살렘을 '해치우듯' 이 결혼식을 해치우고 있었다.\n\n그러나 이보다 더 큰 사냥감도 있었다. 에두름의 향내를 풍기며 — 그야말로 공문서함 냄새를 몸에 두른 채 — 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경 본인이 와 있었던 것이다. 그렇다, 단 하나의 분노에 찬 발상의 날개를 타고 관직의 정상에 오른 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경이 와 있었다. 그 발상이란 이러했다. 의원 여러분, 이 자유로운 나라의 한 장관으로서, 그 국민의 박애에 한계를 긋고, 그 자선을 옥죄며, 그 공공 정신을 옭아매고, 그 진취성을 위축시키며, 그 독립적 자조 정신에 찬물을 끼얹는 일이 마땅한 도리라는 말을, 나는 여태 들어 본 바가 없습니다. 이를 달리 말하자면, 이 위대한 정치가는, 배의 수로 안내인 된 자가 마땅히 해야 할 일이란, 선원들이 죽어라 펌프질을 해서 그가 없이도 배를 물 위에 띄워 둘 수 있는 동안, 육지에서 빵과 물고기를 사사로이 거래하며 잘 먹고사는 것 말고 다른 무엇이라는 말을 여태 들어 본 바가 없다는 뜻이었다. '안 되게 하는 법'이라는 위대한 기예에서 이뤄 낸 이 숭고한 발견 위에서, 데시머스 경은 오랫동안 배너클 가문 최고의 영광을 떠받쳐 왔다. 그리하여 양원 중 어느 쪽이든 분별없는 의원 하나가 무언가를 하기 위한 법안을 제출함으로써 '되게 하는 법'을 시도하기만 하면, 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경이 자기 자리에서 일어나 엄숙하게, 에두름파의 환호가 그를 에워싸고 솟구쳐 오르는 가운데 자신도 분노에 찬 위엄 속으로 솟구쳐 오르며, 의원 여러분, 이 자유로운 나라의 한 장관으로서 그 국민의 박애에 한계를 긋고, 그 자선을 옥죄며, 그 공공 정신을 옭아매고, 그 진취성을 위축시키며, 그 독립적 자조 정신에 찬물을 끼얹는 일이 마땅한 도리라는 말을 여태 들어 본 바가 없다고 말하는 순간, 그 법안은 이미 죽어 묻힌 거나 다름없었다. 이 '마땅한 도리 기계'의 발견은 곧 정치적 영구 기관의 발견이었다. 그것은 모든 국가 부처 안에서 끊임없이 빙글빙글 돌면서도 결코 닳는 법이 없었다.\n\n그리고 그곳에는, 그의 고귀한 벗이자 친척인 데시머스 경과 함께, 일찍이 튜더 스틸트스토킹과 저 영원히 유명한 연립을 이뤄 낸 윌리엄 배너클이 있었다. 그는 언제나 자기만의 독특한 '안 되게 하는 법' 비방을 준비해 두고 있었다. 때로는 의장을 두드려 그에게서 갓 우려낸 것을 끄집어내며, \"먼저, 의장님, 존경하는 의원께서 우리를 황급히 몰아넣으려는 그 방침에 어떤 선례가 있는지 본 의회에 알려 주시기를 청합니다\" 하였고, 때로는 그 존경하는 의원에게 선례에 대한 본인의 해석을 들려 달라고 부탁했으며, 때로는 그 존경하는 의원에게 자신(윌리엄 배너클)이 선례를 찾아보겠노라고 말했고, 그리고 더 자주는, 그 존경하는 의원에게 선례 따위는 없다고 말함으로써 그 자리에서 그를 납작하게 으스러뜨렸다. 그러나 선례와 황급함이라는 이 한 쌍의 잘 어울리는 전마는, 이 유능한 에두름가의 어떤 상황에서도 변함없는 무기였다. 가련하게도 그 존경하는 의원이 이십오 년 동안 헛되이 윌리엄 배너클을 이 일로 황급히 몰아넣으려 애써 왔다 한들 상관없었다 — 윌리엄 배너클은 여전히 본 의회에, 그리고 (한 다리 건너서나마) 이 나라에, 자신이 이 일로 황급히 몰려야 마땅하냐고 되물었다. 사물의 본성과 사태의 흐름에 비추어, 그 비참한 존경하는 의원이 이 일에 대한 선례를 도저히 제시할 수 없다는 것이 전혀 타협 불가능한 사실이라 한들 상관없었다 — 그럼에도 윌리엄 배너클은 그 비아냥대는 환호에 대해 존경하는 의원에게 감사를 표하고, 바로 그 쟁점을 두고 그와 맞붙어, 그 일에 대한 선례 따위는 없다고 그의 면전에 대고 말하곤 했다. 어쩌면 윌리엄 배너클의 지혜가 그리 높은 지혜는 아니었다고, 만약 그랬다면 그가 우롱한 이 땅은 애초에 만들어지지도 않았거나, 혹은 성급한 착오로 만들어졌다 한들 텅 빈 진흙으로 남았으리라고 반박할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 선례와 황급함이 한데 어우러져 거의 모든 사람에게서 온갖 반박을 겁박해 쫓아냈다.\n\n그리고 그곳에는 또 다른 배너클, 활기 넘치는 자도 있었다. 그는 스무 개의 자리를 잇달아 껑충껑충 뛰어다녔고 늘 두세 자리를 동시에 차지하고 있었으며, 모든 배너클 정부에서 큰 성공과 찬탄 속에 몸소 펼쳐 보인 어떤 기예의 매우 존경받는 발명가였다. 그것인즉, 어느 한 주제에 관한 의회의 질문을 받으면, 전혀 다른 주제에 관한 답변을 내놓는 것이었다. 이 기예는 막대한 기여를 했고, 그를 에두름국에서 높은 평판에 올려놓았다.\n\n그리고 그곳에는 또, 덜 저명한 의회 배너클들도 몇몇 흩뿌려져 있었는데, 이들은 아직 아늑한 자리 하나 차지하지 못한 채 자신의 가치를 입증하려고 수습 기간을 거치는 중이었다. 이 배너클들은 계단에 올라앉거나 복도에 숨어, 정족수를 채우라는 명령이나 채우지 말라는 명령을 기다렸다. 그들은 가문 우두머리들의 지시에 따라, 듣는 일도, 감탄하는 일도, 환호하는 일도, 짖어 대는 일도 죄다 해냈다. 그들은 다른 사람들의 동의안을 가로막을 요량으로 허수아비 동의안을 의사일정에 올려놓았다. 또 그들은 달갑지 않은 안건들을 밤늦은 시각, 회기 막바지까지 미적미적 미뤄 두었다가, 그제야 갸륵한 애국심으로 이미 너무 늦었다고 외쳐 댔다. 그들은 파견될 때마다 지방으로 내려가, 데시머스 경이 실신했던 무역을 되살리고 발작을 일으켰던 상업을 회복시켰으며, 곡물 수확량을 두 배로, 건초 수확량을 네 배로 늘렸고, 끝도 없는 황금이 은행에서 빠져나가는 것을 막았노라고 맹세했다. 또한 이 배너클들은 가문 우두머리들의 손에서, 그림 패 아래의 잔챙이 패처럼, 공개 집회와 만찬 자리에 패로 뿌려졌다. 거기서 그들은 자기네 고귀하고 존경스러운 친척들이 베푼 온갖 봉사를 증언하고, 갖가지 축배 자리에서 배너클들에게 알랑방귀를 뀌어 댔다. 그들은 비슷한 명령에 따라 온갖 선거에 출마했으며, 다른 사람을 들이기 위해서라면 가장 촉박한 통보와 가장 부당한 조건으로도 자기 의석을 내놓았다. 그들은 심부름을 도맡고, 아첨하고, 자리를 사고팔며, 부패하고, 산더미 같은 치욕을 삼켜 가면서도, 공공 봉사에 지칠 줄을 몰랐다. 그리고 에두름국 전체에 걸쳐, 향후 반세기 안에 어디서든 비게 될지 모를 자리들의 명단이라면, 재무부 위원에서 중국 영사에 이르기까지, 또 거기서 다시 인도 총독에 이르기까지, 그 모든 자리의 지원자로 굶주리고 들러붙는 이 배너클들 가운데 몇몇 혹은 전부의 이름이 올라 있지 않은 명단이란 없었다.\n\n결혼식에 참석한 것은 어떤 부류의 배너클이든 어쩔 수 없이 그 일부의 흩뿌림에 지나지 않았으니, 다 합쳐도 마흔이 채 안 되었고, 그것이 그 무리 전체에서 떼어 낸들 무엇이겠는가! 그러나 그 흩뿌림조차도 트위커넘의 별장 안에서는 떼를 이루어 집을 가득 메웠다. 한 배너클이(다른 배너클의 도움을 받아) 행복한 한 쌍의 결혼식을 주례했고, 미글스 부인을 축하연으로 안내하는 것은 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경 본인의 마땅한 도리였다.\n\n연회는 본디 그래야 했을 만큼 유쾌하지도, 자연스럽지도 못했다. 미글스 씨는 그 고귀한 일행을 무척 높이 평가하면서도 그들에게 짓눌려 제정신이 아니었다. 가우언 부인은 평소의 자기 모습 그대로였는데, 그것이 그를 나아지게 하지는 못했다. 길을 막아섰던 것은 미글스 씨가 아니라 가문의 위세였으며, 그 가문의 위세가 양보를 했고, 그래서 이제는 마음을 누그러뜨리는 일치가 이루어졌다는 식의 허구가, 공공연히 입 밖에 내어진 적은 결코 없었으나 행사 전체에 스며들어 있었다. 그러다 보니 배너클들은 이 생색내는 자리만 끝나면 자기네 쪽에서는 미글스 일가와 인연을 끊으리라 여겼고, 미글스 일가도 자기네 쪽에서 똑같이 여겼다. 그러다 가우언은, 가문에 원한을 품은, 또 어쩌면 자기 어머니가 다른 어떤 자비로운 목적에서라기보다 그들에게 다소간 불쾌감을 주리라는 기대에서 그들을 그곳에 불러 모으도록 내버려두었던, 실의에 빠진 사람으로서의 권리를 내세우며, 자신의 화필과 가난을 그들 앞에서 보란 듯이 드러냈다. 그는 머지않아 아내에게 빵 한 조각과 치즈 한 덩이 정도는 마련해 줄 수 있기를 바란다고, 또 그들 가운데 (자기보다 운이 좋아서) 무슨 좋은 자리라도 차지하여 그림 한 점쯤 살 형편이 되는 이가 있거든, 부디 이 가난한 화가를 기억해 달라고 청했다. 그러다 데시머스 경은, 제 의회의 단상 위에서는 경이로운 존재이건만, 이 자리에서는 가장 허풍선이 같은 인물로 드러나고 말았다. 그는 진실한 추종자요 신봉자라면 누구든 머리카락이 곤두설 만한 진부한 말들을 줄줄이 늘어놓으며 신랑 신부에게 행복을 빌었고, 자신이 큰길로 착각한 듯한, 울부짖는 미궁 같은 문장들 사이를, 백치 같은 코끼리의 흐뭇함을 띤 채 터덜터덜 누비며, 거기서 빠져나오고 싶다는 생각조차 도통 하지 않았다. 그러다 타이트 배너클 씨는, 만약 그런 방해가 가능했더라면, 완전한 공직자의 풍모로 토머스 로런스 경 앞에 평생토록 앉아 초상을 그리게 하던 그 자세를 흐트러뜨렸을 인물이 이 자리에 끼어 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 한편 배너클 주니어는 자기 친척인 두 명의 맥빠진 신사에게 분개하며 이렇게 일러바쳤다. 여기 어떤 작자가 하나 있는데, 이보게나, 글쎄 임명장도 없이 우리 국에 찾아와서는, 알다시피, 알고 싶은 게 있다는 거야. 그런데 말이지, 이보게나, 만약 저자가 지금 당장 발끈해서는 — 워낙에 그런 무례한 급진파 자식이 다음 순간에 무슨 짓을 할지는 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이니 — 이보게나, 알다시피 바로 이 순간 그걸 알고 싶다고 떠들어 댄다면, 그거 정말 가관이지 않겠나, 안 그래?\n\n클레넘에게는, 이 자리에서 단연코 가장 흐뭇한 부분이 곧 가장 고통스러운 부분이었다. 마침내 미글스 씨 부부가, 두 점의 그림이 걸린 방에서(일행은 그 방에 없었다), 펫을 둘러싸고 한참 어우러졌을 때, 곧 그녀가 옛 펫, 옛 기쁨으로서는 다시는 되넘어 돌아올 수 없는 그 문턱으로 함께 데려가기 전에, 세 사람보다 더 자연스럽고 더 꾸밈없는 모습은 없었다. 가우언 자신도 마음이 움직여, 미글스 씨의 \"오, 가우언, 저 아이를 잘 돌봐 주게, 잘 돌봐 줘!\"라는 말에 진심 어린 목소리로 답했다. \"그렇게 상심하지 마십시오, 어르신. 하늘에 맹세코 그러겠습니다!\"\n\n그리하여 마지막 흐느낌과 마지막 사랑의 말들과 함께, 또 자신의 약속을 믿어 달라는 클레넘을 향한 마지막 눈길과 함께, 펫은 마차에 몸을 기댔고, 그녀의 남편은 손을 흔들었으며, 두 사람은 도버를 향해 떠나갔다. 다만, 충직한 티킷 부인이 비단 가운에 새까만 곱슬머리 차림으로 어느 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 마차 뒤로 제 신발 두 짝을 다 던지고 난 다음에야 비로소 그러했으니, 그 광경은 창가의 저명한 일행에게 큰 놀라움을 안겨 주었다.\n\n이제 그 일행은 더 머물러 있을 의무에서 벗어났고, 으뜸가는 배너클들은 적이 분주해진 터라(바로 그때 그들은, 플라잉 더치맨처럼 바다를 헤매는 일 없이 곧장 목적지로 직행할 위험에 처한 우편선 한두 척을 띄워 보낼 일과, 그러지 않으면 처리될 위험에 처한 중대한 업무를 한 무더기 가로막을 복잡한 방책을 마련할 일을 손에 쥐고 있었던 것이다) 저마다의 길로 흩어졌다. 그러면서 그들은 미글스 씨 부부에게, 자신들이 그곳에서 했던 일은 미글스 씨 부부의 이익을 위해 희생을 무릅쓰고 한 것이라는 그 일반적인 확언을, 더없이 상냥하게 건넸으니, 그것은 그들이 그 가장 불운한 피조물인 존 불 씨에게 관직의 생색을 베풀 때 늘 건네던 바로 그 확언이었다.\n\n집 안에도, 또 그 아버지와 어머니와 클레넘의 가슴속에도 비참한 공백이 남았다. 미글스 씨는 단 하나의 기억만을 자기 위안으로 불러냈는데, 그것만큼은 정말로 그에게 도움이 되었다.\n\n\"그래도, 아서,\" 그가 말했다. \"결국, 이렇게 돌이켜보면 참 흐뭇한 일이야.\"\n\n\"지난 일 말씀입니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"응 — 그런데 내가 말하는 건 그 일행 말일세.\"\n\n그것은 당시에는 그를 한층 더 풀 죽고 불행하게 만들었건만, 이제는 정말로 그에게 도움이 되었다. \"참 흐뭇한 일이야,\" 그가 그날 저녁 내내 그 말을 거듭 되뇌며 말했다. \"그토록 지체 높은 일행이라니!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand\n\n팽크스 씨가 클레넘과 맺은 약속을 이행하느라 자신의 집시 같은 이야기를 통째로 그에게 털어놓고, 리틀 도릿의 운명을 일러 준 것이 바로 이 무렵이었다. 그녀의 아버지는, 오랫동안 알려지지 않은 채 임자 없이 쌓이기만 하던 큰 재산의 적법한 상속인이었다. 그의 권리는 이제 명백했고, 가로막는 것이 아무것도 없었으며, 마셜시의 대문은 열려 있고 마셜시의 담은 무너져 있었으니, 그가 펜을 몇 번 휘갈기기만 하면 그는 엄청난 부자가 될 터였다.\n\n그 권리를 추적하여 완전히 확립하기까지, 팽크스 씨는 그 무엇으로도 당황시킬 수 없는 명민함과, 그 무엇으로도 지치게 할 수 없는 인내와 비밀스러움을 보였다. \"그날 밤 당신과 제가 스미스필드를 가로질러 가면서, 제가 어떤 부류의 수금원인지 당신께 말했을 때,\" 팽크스가 말했다, \"그게 이런 결과를 낳으리라고는 정말이지 생각조차 못 했습니다. 당신이 콘월의 클레넘 가문 사람이 아니라고 말씀드렸을 때, 제가 도싯셔의 도릿 가문 사람이 누구인지를 장차 당신께 일러 주게 되리라고는 정말이지 생각조차 못 했고요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 상세히 설명해 나갔다. 그 이름을 자기 수첩에 적어 두었던 까닭에, 처음에는 그 이름 하나에 끌렸다는 것을. 똑같은 두 이름이, 심지어 같은 지방에 속하면서도, 가깝건 멀건 추적할 만한 어떠한 혈연관계도 수반하지 않는 경우를 자주 보아 왔던 터라, 처음에는 이것에 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 다만 만약 그 가난한 어린 바느질꾼이 그토록 큰 재산에 어떤 권리가 있음을 밝혀낼 수 있다면 그녀의 처지에 얼마나 놀라운 변화가 일어날까 하는 막연한 추측의 차원에서만 그러했다는 것을. 그 조용한 어린 바느질꾼에게는 그를 즐겁게 하고 호기심을 자극하는 무언가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있었던 까닭에, 그 생각을 다음 단계로까지 추적해 들어간 셈이 되었다고 스스로 짐작한다는 것을. 어떻게 그가 한 치씩 한 치씩 더듬어 나가며, 한 알 한 알씩 \"두더지처럼 파냈는지를\"(이것이 팽크스 씨의 표현이었다). 이 새 동사로 묘사되는 노고의 초입에서—그것을 더 실감 나게 전하려고 팽크스 씨는 그 말을 발음하면서 눈을 감고 머리카락을 그 위로 흔들어 내렸다—갑작스러운 빛과 희망에서 갑작스러운 어둠과 절망으로, 또다시 돌아오고 또다시 돌아오며 오갔는지를. 어떻게 그가, 다른 모든 드나드는 이들처럼 그곳을 드나들 수 있도록 일부러 감옥 안에서 아는 사람들을 사귀었는지를. 그리고 어떻게 그의 첫 한 줄기 빛이 도릿 씨 본인과 그의 아들에게서 자기도 모르게 주어졌는지를—그는 두 사람 모두와 쉽게 가까워졌고, 두 사람 모두와 무심한 척하며 많은 이야기를 나누었다(\"하지만 늘 두더지처럼 파고 있었다는 걸 알아주십시오,\" 하고 팽크스 씨가 말했다)—그리고 그들로부터 조금도 의심받지 않으면서, 두세 가지 자그마한 집안 내력을 얻어 냈으며, 그것이 그가 자기 나름의 단서들을 쥐기 시작하자 또 다른 단서들을 시사해 주었다는 것을. 어떻게 마침내 팽크스 씨에게, 자신이 큰 재산의 적법한 상속인을 진짜로 발견해 냈으며, 그 발견을 법적으로 충분하고 완전한 상태로 무르익히기만 하면 된다는 것이 분명해졌는지를. 그러고 나서 어떻게 그가 자기 집주인인 럭 씨에게 엄숙한 방식으로 비밀을 지키겠다는 맹세를 시키고, 그를 두더지 작업의 동업자로 끌어들였는지를. 어떻게 그들이 존 치버리가 누구에게 헌신적인지를 알아보고서 그를 그들의 유일한 서기 겸 대리인으로 부렸는지를. 그리고 어떻게, 은행에서 막강하고 법에 정통한 권위자들이 그들의 성공한 노고가 끝났음을 선언한 지금 이 시각에 이르기까지, 그들이 다른 어떤 사람에게도 털어놓지 않았는지를.\n\n\"그러니 만약 이 모든 일이 와르르 무너졌다 하더라도, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 결론지었다, \"맨 마지막 순간에, 가령 그저께, 제가 감옥 마당에서 우리 서류를 당신께 보여 드린 그 전날에든, 혹은 바로 그날에든 말입니다, 우리 자신 말고는 아무도 잔인하게 실망할 일도 없었고, 한 푼이라도 손해 볼 일도 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n이야기 내내 거의 쉴 새 없이 그와 악수를 나누고 있던 클레넘은, 이 말에 정신이 들어, 핵심 사실에 대한 마음의 준비조차도 다 누그러뜨리지 못한 놀라움에 잠겨 말했다. \"이보게 팽크스 씨, 이건 자네에게 큰돈이 들었겠군.\"\n\n\"꽤 들었지요, 선생님,\" 의기양양한 팽크스가 말했다. \"할 수 있는 한 가장 싸게 했는데도 적은 돈은 아니었습니다. 그리고 그 지출이 골칫거리였다는 점은 말씀드려 둬야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"골칫거리라!\" 클레넘이 되풀이했다. \"하지만 자네가 이 모든 일에서 그토록 놀랍게 정복해 낸 그 어려움들이라니!\" 하며 다시 그의 손을 잡고 흔들었다.\n\n\"제가 어떻게 했는지 말씀드리지요,\" 기뻐하는 팽크스가, 머리카락을 자기 자신만큼이나 한껏 곤두선 상태로 세우며 말했다. \"먼저, 제가 가진 제 돈을 다 썼습니다. 많지는 않았지요.\"\n\n\"그것 참 안됐군,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"물론 이제는 상관없는 일이지만. 그래서, 그다음엔 어떻게 했나?\"\n\n\"그다음엔,\" 팽크스가 대답했다, \"제 고용주에게서 얼마간을 빌렸습니다.\"\n\n\"캐스비 씨에게서?\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그분은 훌륭한 노인이지.\"\n\n\"고결하신 노인이지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 더없이 메마른 콧방귀를 잇달아 뀌기 시작하며 말했다. \"관대하신 노양반. 사람을 믿어 주시는 노인. 박애로우신 노양반. 자비로우신 노인! 제가 그분께 이십 퍼센트를 치르기로 약속했습니다, 선생님. 하지만 우리 가게에서는 그보다 싸게 거래하는 일이 결코 없지요.\"\n\n아서는, 우쭐해진 상태에서 자기가 조금 성급했다는 어색한 자각이 들었다.\n\n\"제가 그 펄펄 끓는 노기독교인에게 말씀드렸지요,\" 이 묘사적인 호칭을 무척 즐기는 듯한 기색으로 팽크스 씨가 이어 갔다, \"제 손에 작은 사업 하나가 걸려 있는데, 가망 있는 일이라고요—가망 있는 일이라고 말씀드렸습니다—거기에 일정한 소액의 자본이 필요하다고요. 제 어음을 담보로 돈을 빌려 달라고 청했지요. 그분은 그렇게 해 주셨습니다, 이십 퍼센트에요. 그 이십을 사업가답게 떡하니 붙여서, 마치 원금의 일부인 것처럼 보이도록 어음에 끼워 넣어서 말입니다. 그 후에 제가 망했더라면, 저는 앞으로 칠 년 동안 절반의 임금에 두 배의 노역으로 그분 밑에서 땅이나 파는 신세가 됐을 겁니다. 하지만 그분은 완벽한 가부장이시지요. 그런 조건으로—어떤 조건으로든 그분을 섬기는 건 사람에게 이로운 일일 겁니다.\"\n\n아서로서는, 죽었다 깨어나도, 팽크스가 정말로 그렇게 생각하는지 아닌지를 자신 있게 말할 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"그게 다 떨어지자, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 다시 말을 이었다, \"정말이지 피처럼 한 방울 한 방울 흘리듯 썼는데도 그것이 다 떨어지자, 저는 럭 씨를 비밀에 끌어들였습니다. 럭 씨에게서(혹은 럭 양에게서, 같은 얘기지요. 그 여자는 언젠가 민사 법원에서 한 건 투기로 돈을 좀 벌었거든요) 빌리자고 제안했지요. 그분은 십 퍼센트에 빌려주면서도 그게 꽤 높다고 생각하시더군요. 하지만 럭 씨는 빨강 머리 사내라서, 선생님, 머리를 깎고 다니십니다. 모자의 우뚝한 부분으로 말하자면, 높다란데요. 모자의 챙으로 말하자면, 좁고요. 그리고 그분에게서 부글부글 솟아나는 자비라곤, 볼링 핀에서 솟아나는 것만큼도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"이 모든 일에 대한 자네 자신의 보수는, 팽크스 씨,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당연히 후해야겠군.\"\n\n\"그걸 못 받으리라곤 걱정하지 않습니다, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"흥정 같은 건 안 했어요. 그 점에서 저는 당신께 빚이 하나 있었는데, 이제 그걸 갚은 겁니다. 주머니에서 나간 돈을 메우고, 들인 시간을 정당하게 쳐 주고, 럭 씨의 청구서를 정산하고 나면, 천 파운드면 제겐 큰 재산이 될 겁니다. 그 문제는 당신 손에 맡기겠습니다. 이제 저는 당신께, 당신이 가장 좋다고 여기는 어떤 방식으로든 이 모든 것을 가족에게 알릴 권한을 드립니다. 에이미 도릿 양은 오늘 아침 핀칭 부인 댁에 있을 겁니다. 빠를수록 좋아요. 아무리 빨라도 지나치지 않습니다.\"\n\n이 대화는 클레넘의 침실에서, 그가 아직 자리에 누워 있을 때 오갔다. 팽크스 씨가 이른 아침에 집안을 깨워 들어왔던 것이고, 한 번도 앉거나 가만히 서 있지도 않은 채, 침대 곁에서 자신의 모든 세부 사항을(갖가지 서류로 뒷받침하며) 늘어놓았던 것이다. 이제 그는 \"가서 럭 씨를 찾아봐야겠다\"고 말했는데, 흥분한 그의 마음 상태는 또 다른 후원자를 필요로 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 서류를 챙겨 묶고, 클레넘과 한 번 더 힘찬 악수를 나눈 뒤, 전속력으로 계단을 내려가 증기를 내뿜듯 떠나갔다.\n\n클레넘은 물론 곧장 캐스비 씨 댁으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 그는 어찌나 빨리 옷을 입고 나섰던지, 그 가부장의 거리 모퉁이에 그녀가 올 시간보다 거의 한 시간이나 일찍 도착해 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그러나 한가로이 거닐며 마음을 가라앉힐 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지는 않았다.\n\n그가 그 거리로 돌아와 번쩍이는 놋쇠 문고리를 두드리자, 그녀가 와 있다는 말을 들었고, 위층 플로라의 아침 식사 방으로 안내되었다. 리틀 도릿 본인은 거기 없었지만 플로라는 있었고, 그를 보고 더없이 큰 놀라움을 드러냈다.\n\n\"어머나 세상에, 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘!\" 그 부인이 외쳤다, \"이런 광경을 보게 되리라고 누가 상상이나 했을까요 그리고 부디 이 실내복을 양해해 주세요 정말이지 제가 결코 게다가 빛바랜 체크무늬라 그게 더 나쁜데 하지만 우리 작은 친구가 저한테 만들어 주고 있어요, 당신께 이런 걸 언급할 필요까지야 없지만 당신도 아셔야 하니까 그런 게 있잖아요 스커트라는 거 말이에요, 그리고 아침 식사 후에 가봉을 하기로 정해 둔 게 그 이유인데 그래도 풀이 이렇게 심하게 먹지 않았으면 싶어요.\"\n\n\"제가 사과를 드려야겠습니다,\" 아서가 말했다, \"이렇게 이르고 불쑥한 방문에 대해서요. 하지만 그 까닭을 말씀드리면 양해해 주실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"영영 가 버린 시절에는 아서,\" 핀칭 부인이 대꾸했다, \"부디 양해해 주세요 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 훨씬 더 옳지요 그리고 의심할 바 없이 거리가 멀긴 해도 여전히 멀어지면 풍경이 매혹적으로 보이는 법이지요, 적어도 그런 뜻은 아니었고 설령 그렇다 해도 그건 풍경의 성질에 상당히 달려 있을 것 같은데, 하지만 제가 또 주절거리고 있네요 게다가 당신이 제 머릿속을 온통 헝클어 놓으셔서요.\"\n\n그녀는 다정하게 그를 흘끗 보고는 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"영영 가 버린 시절에는 이런 말을 하려던 거였어요, 아서 클레넘이—도이스 앤드 클레넘은 물론 사뭇 다르지만—아무 때나 여기 오는 것에 대해 사과를 한다는 게 정말 이상하게 들렸겠지만, 하지만 그건 지난 일이고 지난 일은 결코 되돌릴 수 없지요, 가엾은 F 씨가 기분이 좋을 때 오이는 그렇다고 말씀하시고는 그래서 결코 먹지 않으셨던 그 경우만 빼고요.\"\n\n아서가 들어왔을 때 그녀는 차를 우리고 있던 참이었고, 이제 서둘러 그 일을 끝냈다.\n\n\"아빠는요,\" 그녀가 찻주전자 뚜껑을 탁 덮으며, 온통 비밀스럽고 속삭이는 투로 말했다, \"뒷방에서 시티 기사를 읽으며 새로 낳은 달걀을 깨고 계세요 꼭 딱따구리가 톡톡 쪼는 것처럼요 그러니 당신이 여기 있는 걸 결코 아실 필요가 없고, 우리 작은 친구는 당신도 잘 아시다시피 위층 큰 탁자에서 재단을 마치고 내려오면 완전히 믿어도 되는 사람이지요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 아서는, 더없이 적은 말로 그녀에게, 자신이 만나러 온 것이 바로 그들의 작은 친구라는 것과, 그 작은 친구에게 알려야 할 것이 무엇인지를 일러 주었다. 그 놀라운 소식에, 플로라는 두 손을 맞잡고 부르르 떨더니, 정말로 본바탕이 착한 사람답게 동정과 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"제발 부탁이니 제가 먼저 자리를 피하게 해 주세요,\" 플로라가 두 손을 귀에 갖다 대고 문 쪽으로 움직이며 말했다, \"안 그러면 분명 저는 죽은 듯이 비명을 지르며 쓰러져서 모두를 더 곤란하게 만들 거예요, 그리고 그 사랑스러운 어린것이 바로 오늘 아침만 해도 그렇게 곱고 단정하고 착해 보였는데 그러면서도 그토록 가난했는데 이제 재산이 생긴다니 정말로요 게다가 받을 자격도 충분하고요! 그리고 이번 한 번만 F 씨의 고모님께 제가 이걸 말씀드려도 될까요 아서 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 아니면 곤란하다면 무슨 일이 있어도 안 하고요.\"\n\n아서는 흔쾌히 허락한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였는데, 플로라가 말로 하는 의사소통을 죄다 차단해 버렸기 때문이었다. 플로라는 답례로 고개를 끄덕여 고마움을 표하고는 서둘러 방을 나갔다.\n\n리틀 도릿의 발소리는 이미 계단에 들려 왔고, 다음 순간 그녀가 문 앞에 와 있었다. 그가 아무리 얼굴을 차분히 하려 해도, 그 표정에 평범한 기색을 충분히 담아 넣을 수는 없었던지라, 그녀는 그 얼굴을 보는 순간 하던 일을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다. \"클레넘 씨! 무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"아무 일도, 아무 일도 아닙니다. 그러니까, 불행한 일이 일어난 건 아니에요. 무언가 말씀드리러 왔습니다만, 그건 큰 행운에 관한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"행운이라고요?\"\n\n\"놀라운 행운이지요!\"\n\n그들은 창가에 서 있었고, 빛으로 가득한 그녀의 두 눈은 그의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다. 그는 그녀가 주저앉을 것 같아 한 팔로 그녀를 감쌌다. 그녀는 한 손을 그 팔에 얹었는데, 한편으로는 그 팔에 기대기 위해서였고, 한편으로는 둘 중 누구의 자세도 바뀌어 그를 향한 자신의 골똘한 시선이 흔들리는 일이 없도록 둘의 상대적 위치를 그대로 지키기 위해서였다. 그녀의 입술이 \"놀라운 행운이라고요?\" 하고 되뇌는 듯했다. 그는 그것을 다시, 소리 내어 되풀이했다.\n\n\"사랑하는 리틀 도릿! 당신 아버님 말입니다.\"\n\n그 말에 창백한 얼굴의 얼음이 깨지며, 자잘한 빛과 표정의 떨림이 얼굴 전체에 번져 갔다. 그것들은 모두 고통의 표정이었다. 그녀의 숨결은 희미하고 가빴다. 그녀의 심장은 빠르게 뛰었다. 그는 그 작은 몸을 더 가까이 끌어안고 싶었지만, 그 두 눈이 자기에게, 움직이지 말아 달라고 호소하는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"당신 아버님은 이번 주 안에 자유로워지실 수 있습니다. 그분은 그걸 모르세요. 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 합니다. 당신 아버님은 며칠 안에 자유로워지실 겁니다. 당신 아버님은 몇 시간 안에 자유로워지실 겁니다. 기억하세요, 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 한다는 걸요!\"\n\n그 말이 그녀를 정신 들게 했다. 그녀의 눈이 감기려 했지만, 다시 떠졌다.\n\n\"이게 다가 아닙니다. 이 놀라운 행운이 이게 다가 아니에요, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿. 더 말씀드릴까요?\"\n\n그녀의 입술이 \"네\"라는 모양을 지었다.\n\n\"당신 아버님은 자유로워지셔도 거지가 되지 않으실 겁니다. 아무것도 부족함이 없으실 거예요. 더 말씀드릴까요? 기억하세요! 그분은 이 일을 전혀 모르세요. 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 합니다!\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 잠시만 시간을 달라고 간청하는 듯했다. 그는 한 팔로 그녀를 안고 있다가, 잠시 후 귀를 기울이려 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"계속하라고 하셨나요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그분은 부자가 되실 겁니다. 이미 부자이시고요. 상속분으로 그분께 지급되기를 기다리고 있는 큰돈이 있습니다. 당신들은 이제부터 모두 대단한 부자예요. 가장 용감하고 가장 착한 자식이여, 당신이 보상을 받게 되어 하늘에 감사합니다!\"\n\n그가 그녀에게 입을 맞추자, 그녀는 머리를 그의 어깨 쪽으로 돌리고 팔을 그의 목 쪽으로 들어 올리며 \"아버지! 아버지! 아버지!\" 하고 외치고는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다.\n\n이에 플로라가 그녀를 돌보러 돌아와, 소파에 누인 그녀 곁을 맴돌며, 친절한 보살핌과 두서없는 말 조각들을 어찌나 헷갈리게 뒤섞던지, 그녀가 마셜시더러 임자 없는 배당금을 한 숟갈 떠먹으라고 권하는 것인지—그게 그녀에게 이로울 테니—아니면 리틀 도릿의 아버지가 십만 개의 냄새 맡는 약병을 손에 넣게 된 것을 축하하는 것인지, 아니면 자기가 라벤더 정기 칠만 오천 방울을 오만 파운드의 각설탕에 떨어뜨렸다고, 그러니 리틀 도릿더러 그 순한 강장제를 들라고 간청하는 것인지, 아니면 도이스 앤드 클레넘의 이마를 식초로 적시며 고인이 된 F 씨에게 바람을 더 넣어 주는 것인지, 어느 정도라도 책임감이 있는 사람이라면 누구도 감히 판가름하려 들 수 없었을 것이다. 게다가 옆방에서도 혼란의 지류 한 줄기가 흘러들었는데, 그 방에서는 F 씨의 고모가 목소리로 미루어 보아, 아침 식사를 기다리며 수평으로 누운 자세를 취하고 있는 듯했다. 그리고 그 규방에서 그 가차 없는 부인은 자기 말이 들릴 만하면 언제든 짤막한 조롱을 톡톡 쏘아붙였으니, 가령 \"저자가 한 일이라고 믿지 마!\"라든가 \"저자가 그 일로 공치사 받을 거 없어!\"라든가 \"내 짐작엔, 저자가 제 돈을 한 푼이라도 내놓을 때까진 어지간히도 오래 걸릴걸!\" 따위였고, 이 모든 것은 그 발견에서 클레넘이 한 몫을 깎아내리고, F 씨의 고모가 그를 향해 품은 그 뿌리 깊은 감정을 풀어내려는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 아버지에게 가고 싶고, 그 기쁜 소식을 그에게 전하고 싶고, 그를 위해 마련된 이 행복을 그가 아직 모른 채로 감옥 안에 단 한순간도 두고 싶지 않은 리틀 도릿의 절박한 마음은, 세상의 그 어떤 솜씨와 보살핌도 해낼 수 없을 만큼 그녀를 빠르게 회복시켰다. \"저랑 같이 제 사랑하는 아버지께 가요. 부디 가서 제 사랑하는 아버지께 말씀드려 주세요!\" 이것이 그녀가 한 첫마디였다. 아버지, 아버지. 그녀는 그분 말고는 아무것도 말하지 않았고, 그분 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜든 채 감사의 마음을 쏟아 내는데, 그 감사는 아버지를 위한 것이었다.\n\n플로라의 다정한 마음은 이에 완전히 무너져, 그녀는 찻잔과 받침 접시들 사이에서 놀라운 눈물과 말의 홍수 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 그녀가 흐느꼈다, \"당신 엄마랑 우리 아빠 이후로 이렇게 가슴이 미어진 적이 없어요 이번 한 번만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 하지만 이 귀한 어린것한테 차 한 잔 주고 적어도 입술에라도 대게 해 줘요 부디 아서 그래 줘요, F 씨의 마지막 병환 때조차 이렇진 않았는데 그건 종류가 다른 것이었고 통풍은 어린아이의 병이 아니지만 모두에게 무척 고통스러운 거예요 그리고 F 씨는 다리를 받침대에 올려놓고 순교자였지요 게다가 포도주 거래라는 게 그 자체로 염증을 일으키는 일이라 자기들끼리 어느 정도는 마시기 마련이고 누가 그걸 의아해하겠어요, 오늘 아침 아무것도 아닌 일을 생각하니 정말 꿈만 같은데 이제 돈더미라니 정말로요, 하지만 당신은 알아야 해요 내 사랑하는 이여 왜냐하면 당신은 결코 그분께 찻숟갈로 떠먹이듯 그 모든 걸 다 말씀드릴 만큼 튼튼하지 못할 테니까요, 차라리 우리 주치의 선생님의 처방을 시험해 보는 게 제일 나을지도 모르겠어요 비록 그 맛이 도무지 좋지 않지만 그래도 저는 처방으로 알고 억지로 마셔서 효험을 보거든요, 안 마시고 싶다고요 아니 그래요 내 사랑 저도 마시고 싶진 않지만 그래도 의무로 알고 마셔요, 다들 당신을 축하해 줄 거예요 어떤 이는 진심으로 어떤 이는 진심 아니게 그리고 많은 이가 마음을 다해 축하해 줄 테지만 제 마음 밑바닥에서 우러나오는 저보다 더한 사람은 없을 거예요 정말이지요 비록 서툴고 어리석은 걸 알지만요, 그리고 이번 한 번만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 아서가 판단해 줄 거예요 그러니 잘 가요 내 사랑 하느님의 축복이 있기를 그리고 부디 아주 행복하기를 그리고 이 무례를 용서해 줘요, 맹세컨대 이 옷은 다른 누구도 결코 완성하지 못하게 하고 지금 이대로 기념품으로 간직해서 리틀 도릿이라고 부를 거예요 비록 어째서 그 이상한 호칭인지는 그동안 저도 통 알 수 없었고 이제 영영 모르겠지만요!\"\n\n이렇게 플로라는 자신이 아끼는 아이와 작별했다. 리틀 도릿은 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고, 몇 번이고 거듭 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 마침내 클레넘과 함께 집을 나서, 마셜시로 가는 마차에 올랐다.\n\n낡고 누추한 거리들을 지나는 그 마차길은 묘하게 비현실적이었으니, 마치 그 거리들 위로 들어 올려져 부와 영화의 공중 세계로 떠오르는 듯한 감각이 들었다. 아서가 그녀에게, 머지않아 자신의 마차를 타고 사뭇 다른 풍경 속을 지나게 될 것이며 그때면 익숙한 경험들이 모조리 사라져 버릴 거라고 말하자, 그녀는 겁에 질린 표정을 지었다. 그러나 그가 그녀 대신 아버지를 주어로 바꾸어, 아버지가 마차를 타고 가시는 모습이며, 그분이 얼마나 위대하고 당당하실지를 일러 주자, 기쁨과 순진한 자부심의 눈물이 그녀의 뺨을 빠르게 타고 흘러내렸다. 그녀의 마음이 실감할 수 있는 행복이라곤 죄다 그분께만 비치고 있음을 본 아서는, 그 한 사람의 모습만을 그녀 앞에 계속 두었다. 그리하여 그들은 감옥 인근의 가난한 거리들을 환한 마음으로 달려, 그에게 그 큰 소식을 전하러 갔다.\n\n당번을 서던 치버리 씨가 그들을 문지기 방으로 들여보냈을 때, 그는 그들의 얼굴에서 자신을 놀라움으로 가득 채우는 무언가를 보았다. 그들이 서둘러 감옥 안으로 들어가는 동안, 그는 마치 그들이 각자 유령을 하나씩 데리고 돌아온 것을 알아챈 것처럼 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었다. 그들이 지나친 두세 명의 수감자들도 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보더니, 곧 치버리 씨에게 합류해 문지기 방 계단에 작은 무리를 이루었고, 그 한가운데서 아버지가 석방을 받게 된다는 속삭임이 저절로 생겨났다. 몇 분 안에 그 소식은 그 수용소의 가장 외진 방에서까지 들려왔다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 밖에서 문을 열어, 두 사람은 함께 안으로 들어갔다. 그는 낡은 회색 가운에 낡은 검은 모자 차림으로, 창가의 햇살 속에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있었다. 그의 안경은 손에 들려 있었고, 그는 막 둘러본 참이었다. 분명 처음에는 밤이 되어서야 그녀가 오리라 여겼던 터라 계단의 그녀 발소리에 놀랐을 것이고, 다시 한번 그녀가 아서 클레넘과 함께 온 것을 보고 놀랐을 것이다. 그들이 들어서자, 이미 아래층 마당에서 주목을 끌었던 두 사람 모두의 그 예사롭지 않은 기색이 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 일어서지도 입을 열지도 않은 채, 안경과 신문을 곁의 탁자에 내려놓고는, 입을 조금 벌리고 입술을 떨며 그들을 바라보았다. 아서가 손을 내밀자 그는 그 손을 만졌지만, 평소의 위엄 있는 태도로는 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 곁에 바싹 앉아 두 손을 그의 어깨에 얹은 딸 쪽으로 돌아서서, 그녀의 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보았다.\n\n\"아버지! 저는 오늘 아침 너무도 행복해졌어요!\"\n\n\"네가 그토록 행복해졌다고, 얘야?\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨 덕분이에요, 아버지. 그분이 아버지에 관해서 그토록 기쁘고 놀라운 소식을 가져다주셨어요! 그분이 그 큰 친절과 다정함으로 제가 그걸 받아들일 마음의 준비를 시켜 주지 않으셨다면, 아버지—제가 그걸 받아들일 준비를 시켜 주지 않으셨다면, 아버지—저는 그걸 견뎌 내지 못했을 것 같아요.\"\n\n그녀의 동요는 더없이 컸고, 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다. 그는 불쑥 손을 가슴에 갖다 대고 클레넘을 바라보았다.\n\n\"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 선생님,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"그리고 잠시 시간을 두고 생각하세요. 인생에서 가장 밝고 가장 운 좋은 우연들을 생각해 보십시오. 우리는 모두 기쁨의 큰 놀라움에 관해 들어 본 적이 있지요. 그런 일은 끝난 게 아닙니다, 선생님. 드물기는 해도, 끝난 게 아니에요.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨? 끝난 게 아니라고요? 끝난 게 아니라니 누구한테—\" 그는 \"나\"라고 말하는 대신 자기 가슴을 짚었다.\n\n\"그렇습니다,\" 클레넘이 대답했다.\n\n\"무슨 놀라움이,\" 그가 왼손을 가슴에 댄 채 거기서 말을 멈추고, 오른손으로는 안경을 탁자 위에 정확히 수평으로 놓으며 물었다, \"무슨 그런 놀라움이 나를 위해 마련되어 있을 수 있다는 말이오?\"\n\n\"제가 또 다른 질문으로 답하게 해 주십시오. 말씀해 보세요, 도릿 씨, 무슨 놀라움이 당신께 가장 뜻밖이고 가장 반가운 것이겠습니까. 그것을 상상해 보거나, 그게 무엇일지 말하기를 두려워하지 마십시오.\"\n\n그는 클레넘을 뚫어지게 바라보았고, 그렇게 그를 바라보는 사이 몹시 늙고 수척한 사람으로 변해 가는 듯했다. 햇살이 창 너머 담벼락 위에, 그리고 그 꼭대기의 쇠창살 위에 환했다. 그는 가슴에 얹고 있던 손을 천천히 뻗어 그 담을 가리켰다.\n\n\"그건 무너졌습니다,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"사라졌어요!\"\n\n그는 같은 자세를 유지한 채, 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"그리고 그 자리에는,\" 클레넘이 천천히 또렷하게 말했다, \"그것이 그토록 오래 가로막아 온 모든 것을 손에 넣고 누릴 수단이 마련되어 있습니다. 도릿 씨, 며칠 안에 당신이 자유로워지고 크게 번창하게 되리라는 것은 손톱만큼의 의심도 없는 일입니다. 이 운명의 변화에 대해, 그리고 당신이 여기서 축복받은 그 보물을—다른 어디서 가질 수 있는 그 모든 부 가운데 으뜸가는 보물을, 곧 당신 곁의 그 보물을—머지않아 안고 들어서게 될 그 행복한 미래에 대해, 저는 온 영혼을 다해 축하드립니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다가 놓아 주었다. 그러자 그의 딸은 자기 얼굴을 아버지의 얼굴에 갖다 대고는, 역경의 긴 세월 동안 사랑과 수고와 진심으로 그를 감싸 안았던 것처럼, 번영의 시간에 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안았다. 그리고 감사와 희망과 기쁨과 더없는 황홀로 가득 찬 마음을 쏟아 냈으니, 그 모든 것이 그를 위한 것이었다.\n\n\"이제껏 한 번도 본 적 없는 모습으로 아버지를 뵐 거예요. 검은 먹구름이 걷힌 제 사랑하는 분을 뵐 거예요. 오래전 제 가엾은 어머니가 아버지를 보았던 그 모습으로 뵐 거예요. 오 사랑하는 분, 사랑하는 분! 오 아버지, 아버지! 오 하느님 감사합니다, 하느님 감사합니다!\"\n\n그는 그녀의 입맞춤과 어루만짐에 몸을 맡겼지만, 그것에 답하지는 않았다. 다만 한 팔로 그녀를 감쌌을 뿐이었다. 또한 그는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그의 뚫어질 듯한 시선은 이제 그녀와 클레넘 사이를 오갔고, 그는 마치 몹시 추운 것처럼 떨기 시작했다. 아서는 리틀 도릿에게 자기가 커피점으로 달려가 포도주 한 병을 가져오겠다고 설명하고는, 쓸 수 있는 모든 속력을 다해 그것을 가져왔다. 술이 지하 저장고에서 카운터로 옮겨지는 동안, 흥분한 여러 사람이 그에게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었고, 그는 서둘러 그들에게 도릿 씨가 재산을 상속받게 되었다고 알렸다.\n\n손에 포도주를 들고 돌아와 보니, 그녀는 아버지를 안락의자에 앉히고 셔츠와 목수건을 풀어 놓은 참이었다. 그들은 큰 잔에 포도주를 채워 그의 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그가 조금 삼키고 나자, 그는 잔을 직접 받아 들어 비웠다. 그 직후 그는 의자에 등을 기대고, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 울었다.\n\n이것이 한참 이어진 후에, 클레넘은 그 세부 사항을 이야기해 줌으로써 그의 주의를 핵심적인 놀라움에서 다른 데로 돌리기에 좋은 때라고 생각했다. 그리하여 천천히, 조용한 목소리로, 그는 할 수 있는 한 잘 그것들을 설명하고, 팽크스가 한 일의 성격에 관해 자세히 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"그 사람은—하—그 사람은 후하게 보상받아야 하오, 선생,\" 아버지가 벌떡 일어나 서둘러 방 안을 돌아다니며 말했다. \"안심하시오, 클레넘 씨, 관련된 모든 이가—하—고귀하게 보답받을 것이오. 나를 상대로 채워지지 못한 청구권이 있다고 말할 자는 아무도 없을 거요, 선생. 당신께 받은—흠—선불금은 각별한 기쁨으로 갚겠소, 선생. 가장 빠른 편하실 때에, 당신이 내 아들에게 얼마를 빌려주었는지 알려 주시기를 청하오.\"\n\n그가 방 안을 돌아다니는 데에 무슨 목적이 있는 것은 아니었지만, 그는 한순간도 가만있지 못했다.\n\n\"모두가,\" 그가 말했다, \"기억될 것이오. 나는 누구에게도 빚을 진 채로 여기서 떠나지 않겠소. 나와 내 가족에게—하—잘 처신해 준 모든 사람이 보답받을 것이오. 치버리도 보답받을 거요. 젊은 존도 보답받을 거요. 나는 특별히, 후하게 처신하기를 바라고 또 그럴 작정이오, 클레넘 씨.\"\n\n\"제가,\" 아서가 지갑을 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했다, \"당장 필요하실 일들에 대비할 돈을 마련해 드려도 되겠습니까, 도릿 씨? 그러려고 얼마간의 돈을 가져오는 게 가장 좋겠다고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 선생, 고맙소. 한 시간 전이라면 양심상 받을 수 없었을 것을, 지금 이 순간에는 기꺼이 받겠소. 임시변통에 신세를 지는구려. 지극히 임시변통이지만, 때를 잘 맞췄소—때를 잘 맞췄어.\" 그의 손이 그 돈을 움켜쥐었고, 그는 그것을 들고 돌아다녔다. \"선생, 부디 그 액수를, 내가 이미 말한 그 이전의 선불금에 더해 주시오. 부탁하건대 내 아들에게 빌려준 것도 빠뜨리지 않도록 유의해 주시고. 총액을 그저 말로 일러 주는 것이면 다 되오—하—그것이면 다 되오.\"\n\n이때 그의 시선이 딸에게 닿았고, 그는 잠시 멈춰 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 머리를 토닥였다.\n\n\"양재사를 구하고, 네 그 너무도 수수한 옷을 빠르고 완전히 바꿔야겠구나, 얘야. 매기에 대해서도 무언가 해야겠다, 그 애는 지금—하—겨우 봐줄 만하지, 겨우 봐줄 만해. 그리고 네 언니, 에이미, 그리고 네 오빠도. 그리고 내 동생, 네 삼촌도—가엾은 사람, 이 일이 그를 분발시켰으면 좋겠구나—그들을 데려오라고 심부름꾼을 보내야겠어. 그들에게 이 일을 알려야지. 조심스럽게 알려 줘야겠지만, 곧바로 알려야 한다. 이 순간부터 그들과 우리 자신에 대한 도리로서, 그들이—흠—그들이 무슨 일도 하지 않게 하는 게 우리의 의무야.\"\n\n이것은 그가, 그들이 생계를 위해 무슨 일을 하고 있다는 사실을 자기가 알고 있었음을 처음으로 내비친 것이었다.\n\n그가 지갑을 손에 움켜쥐고 여전히 방 안을 종종거리며 돌아다니고 있을 때, 마당에서 커다란 환호 소리가 일었다. \"소식이 벌써 퍼졌군요,\" 클레넘이 창밖을 내려다보며 말했다. \"저들에게 모습을 보여 주시겠습니까, 도릿 씨? 무척 간절하고, 분명히 그러기를 바라는 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"나는—흠—하—솔직히 바라기는, 에이미 얘야,\" 그가 아까보다 더 열에 들뜬 떨림 속에 종종거리며 말했다, \"먼저 옷을 좀 갈아입고, 그리고—흠—시계와 줄을 하나 샀으면 했단다. 하지만 이대로 해야 한다면, 그것은—하—그렇게 해야겠지. 내 셔츠 깃을 채워 다오, 얘야. 클레넘 씨, 부탁드리는데—흠—그 팔꿈치 옆 서랍에 파란 목수건이 있을 거요. 가슴께에서 내 외투를 채워 다오, 얘야. 단추를 채우면—하—단추를 채우면 더 넓어 보이는구나.\"\n\n그는 떨리는 손으로 자신의 회색 머리카락을 위로 쓸어 올린 다음, 클레넘과 딸을 부축꾼으로 삼아 그들의 한쪽 팔에 각각 기댄 채 창가에 모습을 드러냈다. 수감자들은 그에게 무척 진심으로 환호했고, 그는 더없이 정중하고 너그러운 태도로 그들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 답례했다. 다시 방 안으로 물러나며 그는 \"가엾은 것들!\" 하고, 그들의 비참한 처지를 몹시 측은히 여기는 어조로 말했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 그가 자리에 누워 마음을 가라앉혔으면 하고 몹시 애가 탔다. 아서가 그녀에게, 이제 팽크스가 원하는 대로 곧 나타나서 이 기쁜 일을 마무리 지어도 좋다는 걸 알리러 가겠노라 말하자, 그녀는 아버지가 완전히 차분해져 안정을 찾을 때까지 곁에 있어 달라고 속삭이며 간청했다. 그는 두 번 청할 필요도 없었다. 그녀는 아버지의 잠자리를 마련하고는 누우시라고 권했다. 그러나 그는 또 반 시간 남짓 동안, 방 안을 돌아다니며, 행정관이 모든 수감자에게 거리를 내다볼 수 있는 관사의 창문으로 가서 자신과 가족이 마차를 타고 영영 떠나가는 모습을 보도록 허락할지 안 할지의 가능성을 혼자 따져 보는 것 말고는 무엇도 하려 들지 않았다—그것이 저들에게는 볼만한 구경거리가 될 것 같다고 그는 말했다. 그러나 점차 그는 기운이 빠지고 지치기 시작하더니, 마침내 침대에 몸을 뉘었다.\n\n그녀는 그의 곁 충실한 자리를 차지하고서 부채질을 해 주고 그의 이마를 식혀 주었다. 그가 (여전히 손에 돈을 쥔 채) 잠이 드는 듯하더니, 뜻밖에도 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 실례하오. 내가 이해하기로는, 선생, 내가—하—지금 이 순간 문지기 방을 통과해서—흠—산책을 좀 할 수 있다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그건 아닐 듯합니다, 도릿 씨,\" 마지못한 대답이었다. \"마쳐야 할 어떤 절차들이 있습니다. 그리고 여기 당신의 구금이 이제 그 자체로 하나의 형식에 불과하긴 합니다만, 조금만 더 지켜져야 하는 형식이기도 한 것 같아 우려됩니다.\"\n\n이 말에 그는 다시 눈물을 흘렸다.\n\n\"불과 몇 시간입니다, 선생님,\" 클레넘이 그에게 명랑하게 다독였다.\n\n\"몇 시간이라, 선생,\" 그가 불쑥 격해져 대꾸했다. \"당신은 시간을 무척 쉽게 말하는구려, 선생! 공기가 없어 숨이 막혀 가는 사람에게 한 시간이 얼마나 긴 것인지 짐작이나 하시오, 선생?\"\n\n이것이 그때로서는 그의 마지막 격앙이었다. 눈물을 좀 더 흘리고는 숨을 쉴 수가 없다고 푸념하듯 투덜대더니, 그는 서서히 선잠에 빠져들었다. 클레넘은 조용한 방에 앉아 침대 위의 아버지와, 그 얼굴에 부채질하는 딸을 지켜보며, 생각에 잠길 거리가 넘쳐 났다.\n\n리틀 도릿도 생각에 잠겨 있었다. 그의 회색 머리카락을 살며시 옆으로 쓸어 넘기고 그의 이마에 입술을 갖다 댄 뒤, 그녀는 아서 쪽을 바라보았고, 그가 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가오자 낮은 속삭임으로 자기 생각의 갈래를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 아버지는 여기를 떠나시기 전에 빚을 다 갚으실까요?\"\n\n\"틀림없이요. 전부 다요.\"\n\n\"여기 갇히게 만든 그 모든 빚을, 제 평생 내내, 아니 그보다 더 오래된 그 빚을 다요?\"\n\n\"틀림없이요.\"\n\n그녀의 표정에는 무언가 미심쩍어하고 항변하는 듯한 것이, 온전한 만족만은 아닌 무언가가 어려 있었다. 그는 그것을 알아채고 의아해하며 말했다.\n\n\"아버님이 그러시는 게 기쁘지 않으신가요?\"\n\n\"당신은요?\" 리틀 도릿이 아쉬운 듯 물었다.\n\n\"제가요? 더없이 진심으로 기쁩니다!\"\n\n\"그렇다면 저도 기뻐해야 마땅한 거겠지요.\"\n\n\"기쁘지 않으세요?\"\n\n\"제게는 가혹하게 느껴져요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다, \"아버지가 그토록 많은 세월을 잃고 그토록 많은 고통을 겪으셨는데, 끝에 가서 그 빚까지 다 갚으셔야 한다는 게요. 인생과 돈을 둘 다 치르셔야 한다는 게 제게는 가혹하게 느껴져요.\"\n\n\"얘야—\" 클레넘이 말을 꺼내려 했다.\n\n\"네, 제가 잘못 생각하는 걸 알아요,\" 그녀가 소심하게 변명했다, \"저를 너무 나쁘게 여기진 마세요. 여기서 저와 함께 자라난 생각이라서요.\"\n\n그토록 많은 것을 망가뜨릴 수 있는 감옥도, 리틀 도릿의 마음을 더럽힌 것이라곤 이것뿐이었다. 그 혼란이라는 것도 가엾은 죄수인 아버지에 대한 연민에서 생겨난 것이었으니, 그것은 클레넘이 그녀에게서 본 첫 번째 얼룩이었고, 그가 그녀에게서 본 마지막 얼룩이기도 했으니, 감옥의 공기가 그녀에게 남긴 자국이라곤 그뿐이었다.\n\n그는 그렇게 생각하고는 더 말하기를 삼갔다. 그 생각과 함께, 그녀의 순수함과 선함이 가장 밝은 빛 속에서 그의 앞에 떠올랐다. 그 작은 얼룩이 그것들을 한층 더 아름답게 만들었다.\n\n자기 자신의 감정에 지쳐, 방 안의 고요에 몸을 맡기면서, 그녀의 손은 부채질하던 움직임이 서서히 느려지다가 멈추었고, 그녀의 머리는 아버지 곁의 베개 위로 떨어졌다. 클레넘은 살며시 일어나, 소리 하나 내지 않고 문을 열었다 닫고는, 감옥을 빠져나와, 소란스러운 거리로 그 고요를 안고 나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan\n\n이제 도릿 씨와 그의 가족이 영원히 감옥을 떠나, 그토록 많이 밟힌 그곳 포석이 더는 그들을 알지 못하게 될 날이 다가왔다.\n\n그 사이의 기간은 짧았으나, 그는 그것이 길다고 몹시 불평했고, 지체되는 일을 두고 러그 씨에게 위압적으로 굴었다. 그는 러그 씨에게 고압적으로 나왔고, 다른 사람을 쓰겠다고 으름장을 놓았다. 그는 러그 씨에게, 자신이 어디에서 그를 발견했는지를 빌미로 주제넘게 굴지 말고, 자네의 본분을 다하게, 그것도 신속하게 다하라고 요구했다. 그는 러그 씨에게, 변호사나 대리인이 어떤 자들인지 자기는 잘 알고 있으며, 농간에 넘어가지 않겠다고 말했다. 그 신사가 자기는 최선을 다해 애쓰고 있다고 겸손하게 변명하자, 패니 양이 그에게 아주 쌀쌀맞게 굴었다. 돈은 문제가 아니라는 말을 수십 번이나 들었으면서 그보다 더 적게 할 일이 무엇이 있겠느냐며 따져 물었고, 자기가 누구와 이야기하고 있는지를 그가 잊은 모양이라는 의심을 드러냈다.\n\n여러 해 동안 자리를 지켜 온, 그리고 전에 한 번도 다툰 적이 없던 간수장에게도 도릿 씨는 엄격하게 처신했다. 그 관리는 몸소 축하 인사를 건네면서, 떠날 때까지 도릿 씨가 쓰도록 자기 집의 방 두 개를 무료로 내어 주겠다고 제안했다. 도릿 씨는 그 자리에서는 고맙다며, 생각해 보겠다고 대답했다. 그러나 간수장이 떠나기가 무섭게 그는 앉아서 신랄한 쪽지를 써 보냈는데, 거기에서 그는 이전에 어떤 경우에도 그의 축하를 받는 영광을 누린 적이 없었다고(이것은 사실이었으니, 실은 그를 축하할 만한 특별한 일이 따로 있었던 것도 아니었다) 지적하고, 자신과 가족을 대신하여 간수장의 제안을 거절하노라고, 그 사심 없는 성격과 온갖 세속적 고려로부터의 완전한 초연함이 요구하는 모든 감사와 함께 거절한다고 적었다.\n\n그의 형은 바뀐 처지에 대해 너무도 흐릿한 관심의 기미밖에 보이지 않아 과연 그가 그것을 이해는 하는지조차 매우 의심스러웠지만, 도릿 씨는 자기를 위해 불러들인 양품점 주인과 재단사, 모자 장수, 구두 장수를 시켜 형의 새 옷 치수를 재게 했고, 형의 헌 옷은 거두어 태워 버리라고 지시했다. 패니 양과 팁 씨는 대단히 멋스럽고 우아한 차림을 하는 데 아무 지시도 필요치 않았다. 세 사람은 이 기간을 인근에서 가장 좋은 호텔에서 함께 보냈다. 비록 패니 양의 말처럼 가장 좋은 곳이라야 매우 형편없었지만 말이다. 그 호텔과 관련하여, 팁 씨는 이륜마차와 말과 마부를 빌렸는데, 아주 말끔한 차림새여서, 보통 한 번에 두세 시간씩 마셜시 안마당 바깥의 버러 하이 스트리트를 장식하며 서 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 거기에는 또 수수하고 작은 임대 사두마차 한 대도 자주 보였는데, 패니 양은 그 마차에서 내리고 타면서 손에 넣을 수 없는 보닛을 과시하여 간수장의 딸들을 들뜨게 했다.\n\n이 짧은 기간에 수많은 일이 처리되었다. 그 가운데 하나로, 모뉴먼트 야드의 사무 변호사 페들 앤드 풀 양반들은 그들의 의뢰인 에드워드 도릿 귀하의 지시를 받아 아서 클레넘 씨에게 편지를 보내며, 거기에 24파운드 9실링 8펜스의 금액을 동봉했는데, 이는 그들의 의뢰인이 클레넘 씨에게 빚졌다고 여기는 원금에, 연 5퍼센트의 이율로 계산한 이자를 더한 액수였다. 이 통지와 송금을 하면서, 페들 앤드 풀 양반들은 그들의 의뢰인으로부터 또한 지시를 받아, 이제 갚는 그 선급금(출입문 수수료를 포함하여)의 호의를 클레넘 씨에게 청한 적이 없었음을 그에게 일깨우고, 만일 그것이 그의 이름으로 공공연히 제안되었더라면 받지 않았을 것이라는 점을 알렸다. 이와 함께 그들은 인지를 붙인 영수증을 청하며, 그의 충실한 종으로 남는다고 적었다. 곧 고아가 될 마셜시 안에서도, 그토록 오래 그곳의 아버지였던 도릿 씨가 처리해야 할 일이 많았는데, 주로 수감자들이 그에게 소액의 돈을 청하는 데서 비롯된 것이었다. 이에 그는 더없이 후하게, 그러면서도 격식을 빠뜨리지 않고 응했으니, 언제나 먼저 청한 자가 자기 방으로 찾아올 시간을 정해 편지를 써 보낸 다음, 산더미처럼 쌓인 서류 한가운데에서 그를 맞아, 자신의 기부에(그는 그런 경우마다 \"이것은 기부지 대여가 아니오\"라고 말했으므로) 숱한 좋은 조언을 곁들였다. 그 요지인즉, 곧 사라질 마셜시의 아버지인 자신이, 사람은 그런 곳에서조차 자기 자신과 모두의 존경을 지킬 수 있다는 본보기로서 오래도록 기억되기를 바란다는 것이었다.\n\n수감자들은 시기하지 않았다. 그토록 여러 해 동안 자리를 지킨 수감자에게 개인적이고 전통적인 경의를 품고 있었던 데다가, 이 사건은 그 학교의 명예가 되는 일이었고 신문에까지 그곳을 유명하게 만들었으니 말이다. 게다가 아마도 그들 가운데 스스로 자각하는 것보다 더 많은 이들이, 운수의 추첨에서 같은 일이 자기에게도 일어날 수 있었으리라고, 혹은 언젠가 그 비슷한 일이 자기에게도 일어날지 모른다고 생각했을 것이다. 그들은 그것을 아주 잘 받아들였다. 몇몇은 자기들만 뒤에 남겨지고, 가난한 채로 남겨지리라는 생각에 풀이 죽었으나, 그런 이들조차 그 가족의 찬란한 운명의 역전을 시기하지는 않았다. 좀 더 점잖은 곳이었다면 훨씬 더 많은 시기가 있었을지도 모른다. 전당포 주인의 손에서 그날의 저녁밥으로—그날그날 손에서 입으로 살아가던 수감자들보다는, 어중간한 형편이 오히려 덜 너그러웠으리라는 것이 그럴듯한 일이다.\n\n그들은 그에게 보내는 헌사를 작성하여, 깔끔한 액자와 유리에 끼워 그에게 바쳤고(다만 그것이 그 뒤로 그 가문의 저택에 걸리거나 가문의 문서로 보존되지는 않았다), 그는 거기에 우아한 답을 보냈다. 그 문서에서 그는, 임금이라도 된 듯한 태도로, 자기들에 대한 그들의 애착의 고백을 그 진실함에 대한 충분한 확신을 가지고 받아들인다고 그들에게 장담했다. 그리고 다시금 두루 자기를 본받으라고 권했는데—적어도 막대한 재산을 물려받는 일에 관한 한, 그들이 그것을 기꺼이 흉내 냈으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다. 그는 같은 기회를 빌려, 학교 전체를 위해 안마당에서 베풀 성대한 연회에 그들을 초대했고, 자기가 곧 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 모든 이들의 건강과 행복을 위해 이별의 술잔을 드는 영광을 누리겠노라고 알렸다.\n\n그는 이 공개 연회에서 직접 식사하지는 않았다(연회는 오후 두 시에 열렸는데, 이제 그의 식사는 여섯 시에 호텔에서 들어왔다). 그러나 그의 아들이 친절하게도 주빈석의 상석에 앉아 매우 격의 없고 다정하게 굴었다. 그는 손수 손님들 사이를 돌아다니며 개개인에게 눈길을 주었고, 음식이 자기가 주문한 품질대로인지, 모두에게 빠짐없이 차려졌는지를 살폈다. 전체적으로 그는 보기 드물게 기분이 좋은 옛 시대의 영주 같았다. 연회가 끝날 무렵, 그는 손님들과 함께 오래된 마데이라 포도주를 가득 채운 잔으로 건배했고, 그들이 즐거운 시간을 보냈기를, 그리고 더 나아가 그 저녁의 남은 시간도 즐겁게 보내기를 바란다고 말했다. 그들이 잘되기를 빌며, 그들을 환영한다고도 했다. 환호 속에 그의 건강을 위한 축배가 들리자, 그는 결국 그렇게까지 영주답지는 못했으니, 답례의 인사를 하려다가 가슴속에 마음을 지닌 한낱 농노처럼 무너져 내려, 모든 이들 앞에서 눈물을 흘리고 말았다. 자기로서는 실패라고 여긴 이 대성공 뒤에, 그는 \"치버리 씨와 그의 동료 관리들\"을 위해 건배를 제의했는데, 그는 미리 그들에게 각각 10파운드씩을 주었고, 그들은 모두 그 자리에 와 있었다. 치버리 씨가 그 건배에 답하여 말하기를, 가두라고 맡겨진 것은 가두되, 다만 그대도, 족쇄 찬 아프리카인의 말처럼, 언제나 한 사람의 인간이요 형제임을 기억하라고 했다. 건배의 목록이 다 처리되자, 도릿 씨는 자기 다음으로 가장 오래 머문 수감자와 함께 스키틀 놀이를 하는 시늉을 점잖게 해 보였고, 입주민들은 저마다 즐기도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 일은 마지막 날에 앞선 것이었다. 그리고 이제 그와 그의 가족이 영원히 감옥을 떠나, 그토록 많이 밟힌 그곳 포석이 더는 그들을 알지 못하게 될 날이 다가왔다.\n\n정오가 출발 시각으로 정해졌다. 그 시각이 다가오자, 안에 있는 수감자는 하나도 없었고, 자리를 비운 간수도 하나 없었다. 뒤의 신사들은 나들이옷 차림으로 나타났고, 수감자들 대부분은 형편이 허락하는 한 한껏 차려입었다. 깃발도 두세 개 내걸렸고, 아이들은 자투리 리본을 둘렀다. 이 고비의 순간에도 도릿 씨 자신은 엄숙하지만 기품 있는 위엄을 잃지 않았다. 그의 크나큰 주의는 형에게 많이 쏠려 있었으니, 그 중대한 자리에서 형의 처신이 어떨지 걱정스러웠던 것이다.\n\n\"형님,\" 그가 말했다. \"팔을 좀 빌려주시면 우리 함께 친구들 사이를 지나가지요. 우리가 팔짱을 끼고 나가는 것이 옳다고 생각합니다, 형님.\"\n\n\"하!\" 프레더릭이 말했다. \"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\"\n\n\"그리고 형님—형님이 너무 무리하지 않고서, 평소의 거동에 조금만(부디 양해해 주세요, 형님), 조금만 광을 더해 주실 수 있다면—\"\n\n\"윌리엄, 윌리엄,\" 형이 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그런 건 다 네가 할 일이다. 나는 어떻게 하는지 모른다. 다 잊었어, 다 잊었어!\"\n\n\"하지만 형님,\" 윌리엄이 대답했다. \"바로 그 이유 때문에라도, 달리 이유가 없더라도, 형님은 반드시 정신을 차리려고 애쓰셔야 합니다. 잊으신 것을 이제 다시 떠올리기 시작하셔야 해요, 형님. 형님의 처지는—\"\n\n\"응?\" 프레더릭이 말했다.\n\n\"형님의 처지 말입니다, 형님.\"\n\n\"나의?\" 그는 먼저 자기 모습을 보고, 다음에 동생의 모습을 보고는, 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 외쳤다. \"하, 그렇지! 그래, 그래, 그래.\"\n\n\"형님의 처지는, 이제 훌륭한 것입니다. 내 형으로서의 형님의 처지는 아주 훌륭한 것이지요. 그리고 나는 형님의 양심적인 천성이라면 그에 걸맞은 사람이 되려고, 그것을 빛내려고 애쓰리라는 걸 압니다, 형님. 그것에 누가 되지 않도록, 오히려 그것을 빛내도록 말이지요.\"\n\n\"윌리엄,\" 형이 힘없이, 한숨과 함께 말했다. \"네가 바라는 건 무엇이든 하마, 동생아, 내 힘이 닿는 한은. 부디 내 힘이 얼마나 보잘것없는지를 기억해 다오. 오늘 내가 무엇을 하기를 바라느냐, 동생아? 그것이 무엇인지 말해 다오, 그저 무엇인지만 말해 다오.\"\n\n\"형님, 아무것도 없어요. 형님처럼 그토록 착한 마음을 번거롭게 할 만한 일이 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"부디 번거롭게 해 다오,\" 형이 대답했다. \"그 마음으로서는, 윌리엄, 너를 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 번거롭지가 않으니까.\"\n\n윌리엄은 손으로 눈을 훔치며, 위엄에 찬 흡족함으로 중얼거렸다. \"형님의 그 애정에 축복이 있기를, 가엾고 가여운 형님!\" 그러고는 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그럼, 형님, 우리가 걸어 나갈 때 그저 이 자리를 의식하고 있다는 것만—그것을 마음에 두고 있다는 것만 보여 주시려고 애써 주시면—\"\n\n\"내가 무엇을 마음에 두기를 권하겠느냐?\" 순종적인 형이 대답했다.\n\n\"오! 형님, 제가 어찌 대답해 드리겠어요? 다만 이 착한 사람들을 떠나면서 제 자신이 무슨 생각을 하는지를 말씀드릴 수밖에요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거다!\" 형이 외쳤다. \"그게 나를 도와줄 게다.\"\n\n\"제가 생각하는 건요, 형님, 그것도 누그러진 연민이 가장 큰 자리를 차지하는 뒤섞인 감정으로 생각하는 건, '내가 없으면 저들은 어떻게 할까!' 하는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"옳다,\" 형이 대답했다. \"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래. 걸어가면서 그 생각을 하마, '내 동생이 없으면 저들은 어떻게 할까! 가엾은 것들! 저 사람이 없으면 어떻게 할까!' 하고.\"\n\n열두 시가 막 울리고, 바깥뜰에 마차가 준비되었다는 전갈이 오자, 형제는 팔짱을 끼고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 에드워드 도릿 귀하(한때의 팁)와 그의 누이 패니가 역시 팔짱을 끼고 뒤따랐다. 가져갈 만하다고 여겨진 가재도구를 옮기는 일을 맡은 플로니시 씨와 매기가, 수레에 실을 보따리와 짐을 들고 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n마당에는 수감자들과 간수들이 있었다. 마당에는, 자기들이 한 일에 마지막 손길이 닿는 것을 보러 온 팽크스 씨와 러그 씨가 있었다. 마당에는, 상심하여 죽는 마당에 자신을 위한 새 묘비명을 짓고 있는 젊은 존이 있었다. 마당에는, 어찌나 어마어마하게 자비로워 보이던지 열성적인 수감자들 다수가 그의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡고, 더 많은 수감자들의 아내와 여자 친척들이 그가 이 모든 일을 해냈다고 추호도 의심하지 않으며 그의 손에 입을 맞추던, 가부장다운 캐스비가 있었다. 마당에는, 간수장이 횡령했다는 그 자금에 관한 어렴풋한 불만을 품은 사내가 있었는데, 그는 그 거래에 관한 도무지 알아먹을 수 없는 내력을 베껴 끝내려고 새벽 다섯 시에 일어났고, 그것을 정부를 깜짝 놀라게 하고 간수장의 몰락을 가져올 더없이 중요한 문서로서 도릿 씨의 손에 맡긴 터였다. 마당에는, 그 온갖 정력을 늘 빚을 지는 데에 쏟던 파산자가 있었는데, 그는 남들이 감옥에서 빠져나오느라 들이는 만큼의 공을 들여 감옥으로 비집고 들어왔고, 늘 빚을 청산받으며 칭송을 들었다. 반면 그의 곁에 있던 파산자는—그저 자그맣고 훌쩍거리며 안간힘 쓰는 장사꾼으로, 빚을 지지 않으려는 노심초사로 반쯤 죽어 가던 자였는데—갖은 질책과 비난과 함께라도 위원에게서 풀려나기가 참으로 어려운 노릇이었다. 마당에는, 자식도 많고 짐도 많은데도 그 파산이 모두를 놀라게 한 사내가 있었다. 마당에는, 자식도 없고 재산도 많은데도 그 파산이 아무도 놀라게 하지 않은 사내가 있었다. 거기에는, 늘 내일 나가겠다고 하면서 늘 미루는 사람들이 있었고, 거기에는, 어제 들어와서는 그 운수의 변덕을 산전수전 다 겪은 새들보다 훨씬 더 시샘하고 분해하는 사람들이 있었다. 거기에는, 순전히 비루한 마음에서 부유해진 수감자와 그 가족 앞에 굽실거리며 절하는 이들이 있었고, 거기에는, 감금과 가난의 어둠에 익은 눈이 그처럼 밝은 햇빛을 견디지 못해 정말로 그렇게 하는 이들도 있었다. 거기에는, 그에게 고기와 술을 사 주려고 자기 실링을 그의 주머니에 넣어 준 이들이 많았으나, 이제 와서 그 도움을 빌미로 주제넘게 \"어이, 친구, 잘 만났네!\" 하는 이는 하나도 없었다. 오히려 새장에 갇힌 새들에게서 눈여겨볼 만한 점은, 그들이 곧 그토록 당당하게 자유로워질 그 새를 조금 어려워하며, 창살 쪽으로 물러나는 경향을 보이고, 그가 지나갈 때 조금 동요하는 듯 보였다는 것이었다.\n\n이 구경꾼들 사이로, 두 형제를 앞세운 작은 행렬이 정문을 향해 천천히 움직였다. 도릿 씨는, 자기가 없으면 저 가엾은 자들이 어떻게 살아갈까 하는 그 막대한 상념에 잠겨, 위대하면서도 슬펐으나, 거기에 온통 빠져 있지는 않았다. 그는 교회에 가는 로저 드 커벌리 경처럼 아이들의 머리를 쓰다듬었고, 뒤편에 있는 사람들에게 세례명으로 말을 건넸으며, 그 자리의 모두에게 너그러이 몸을 낮추었고, 그들의 위안을 위해 황금빛 글자로 쓰인 \"위로받으라, 나의 백성이여! 견디라!\" 하는 구절에 에워싸여 걷는 듯이 보였다.\n\n마침내 정직한 세 번의 환호가, 그가 정문을 지나갔으며 마셜시가 고아가 되었음을 알렸다. 그 소리가 감옥 담장의 메아리 속에서 채 그치기도 전에, 가족은 마차에 올라탔고, 수행원이 발판을 손에 쥐고 있었다.\n\n그제야, 그 전이 아니라 그제야, \"어머나 세상에!\" 하고 패니 양이 별안간 외쳤다. \"에이미는 어디 있어요!\"\n\n아버지는 그애가 언니와 함께 있는 줄 알았다. 언니는 그애가 \"어딘가에\" 있는 줄 알았다. 그들은 모두, 늘 그래 왔듯이, 그애가 알맞은 때에 알맞은 자리에 얌전히 있는 것을 발견하리라 믿어 의심치 않았다. 이번 떠남이야말로, 그애 없이 치러 낸, 그들 공동의 삶에서 어쩌면 맨 첫 번째 일이었다.\n\n이 점들을 확인하는 데 일 분쯤 걸렸을 무렵, 마차의 자기 자리에서 수위실로 통하는 길고 좁은 통로를 내려다볼 수 있던 패니 양이, 분개하여 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"이건 정말이지, 아버지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"수치스러운 일이에요!\"\n\n\"무엇이 수치스럽단 말이냐, 패니?\"\n\n\"제 말은요,\" 그녀가 거듭 말했다. \"이건 정말 더없이 망신스러운 일이라고요! 정말 이런 때에조차, 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 싶을 만큼이라니까요! 저기 저 에이미라는 애가, 그토록 고집을 부리던 그 보기 흉한 낡은 누더기 옷을 입고서, 아버지, 제가 몇 번이고 빌고 사정하며 갈아입으라고 했는데도, 몇 번이고 마다하다가 오늘은 갈아입겠다고 약속해 놓고는, 아버지와 함께 저 안에 머무는 동안만큼은 그걸 계속 입고 싶다던—그건 정말이지 더없이 천한 부류의 낭만적 헛소리였는데—저기 저 에이미라는 애가, 결국 그 옷차림 그대로 들려 나오면서, 마지막 순간까지, 그것도 마지막 순간에 우리를 망신시키고 있다고요. 게다가 저 클레넘 씨 손에 들려서요!\"\n\n그녀가 그 고발장을 늘어놓는 동안, 그 죄목이 증명되었다. 클레넘이 그 작고 정신 잃은 모습을 품에 안고 마차 문 앞에 나타났던 것이다.\n\n\"이 아이가 잊혀 있었더군요.\" 그가 질책이 섞이지 않은 것은 아닌 연민의 어조로 말했다. \"제가 이 아이의 방으로(치버리 씨가 가르쳐 주었습니다) 뛰어 올라가 보니 문이 열려 있었고, 이 가엾은 아이가 바닥에 쓰러져 있었습니다. 옷을 갈아입으러 갔다가, 그만 기력이 다해 주저앉은 모양입니다. 환호 소리 때문이었을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 일어난 일일 수도 있겠지요. 이 가엾고 차가운 손을 조심하세요, 도릿 양. 떨어뜨리지 마시고요.\"\n\n\"고마워요, 선생님.\" 도릿 양이 울음을 터뜨리며 대답했다. \"허락만 해 주시면 어찌해야 할지는 제가 알아요. 에이미, 눈을 떠, 착하지! 오, 에이미, 에이미, 나 정말 너무 속상하고 부끄러워! 제발 정신 차려, 얘야! 오, 왜 어서 가지 않는 거야! 부디, 아버지, 어서 가요!\"\n\n수행원이 클레넘과 마차 문 사이로 끼어들며, 날카롭게 \"실례합니다, 선생님!\" 하고는 발판을 부랴부랴 접어 올렸고, 마차는 떠나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 1. Fellow Travellers\n\n가을이 깊어 가던 무렵, 어둠과 밤이 알프스의 가장 높은 능선들을 향해 슬그머니 기어오르고 있었다.\n\n그랑생베르나르 고개의 스위스 쪽 골짜기들과 제네바 호반을 따라서는 포도 수확철이었다. 그곳의 공기는 거두어들인 포도 향으로 가득했다. 포도가 담긴 바구니와 구유와 통이 어둑한 마을 문간마다 놓여 있고, 가파르고 좁은 마을 길을 가로막았으며, 하루 종일 길과 골목을 따라 실려 다녔다. 발에 밟혀 터지고 으깨진 포도가 사방에 널려 있었다. 짐을 진 채 집으로 터벅터벅 돌아가는 농촌 아낙이 멜빵에 안고 가는 아이는 주워 든 포도를 받아 들고 잠잠해졌고, 폭포로 가는 길가 나무 산장의 잎사귀 아래에서 커다란 갑상선종을 햇볕에 쬐던 백치는 앉아서 포도를 우물거렸으며, 소와 염소의 숨결은 포도 잎과 줄기 냄새로 향긋했고, 작은 선술집마다 모인 사람들은 포도를 먹고 마시며 포도 이야기를 했다. 이 후한 풍요로움의 무르익은 손길이, 어쨌거나 그 포도로 만들어진 그 묽고 거칠고 떫은 포도주에는 조금도 깃들 수 없다는 것이 안타까운 일이었다!\n\n밝은 한낮 내내 공기는 따뜻하고 투명했다. 멀리 좀처럼 보이지 않던 번쩍이는 금속 첨탑과 교회 지붕이 시야 속에서 반짝였고, 눈 덮인 산봉우리들은 어찌나 또렷한지 익숙지 않은 눈으로 보면 그 사이에 놓인 들판을 지워 버리고, 험준한 높이를 우습게 보아 무언가 전설 같은 것으로 여기며, 몇 시간이면 쉬이 닿을 거리쯤으로 가늠했을 법했다. 골짜기에서는 이름 높으면서도 때로는 몇 달 내내 그 존재의 흔적조차 보이지 않던 산봉우리들이, 아침부터 푸른 하늘 속에 또렷하고 가깝게 모습을 드러내고 있었다. 그리고 이제 아래쪽이 어두워지자, 그 봉우리들은 노을의 붉은 물감이 빠져나가 차갑게 흰빛만 남으면서, 마치 사라지려는 유령처럼 엄숙히 물러서는 듯 보였으나, 안개와 그림자 위 저 외로움 속에서는 여전히 그 윤곽이 또렷했다.\n\n이 적막한 곳들, 그 가운데 하나인 그랑생베르나르 고개에서 바라보면, 솟아오르는 밤은 차오르는 물처럼 산을 타고 올라왔다. 마침내 밤이 그랑생베르나르 수도원의 담장에까지 차오르자, 풍상에 시달린 그 건물은 또 하나의 방주가 되어 그림자의 물결 위에 떠 있는 듯했다.\n\n노새를 탄 몇몇 길손을 앞질러, 어둠은 이렇듯 거친 수도원 담장에까지 차올랐고, 그때 그 길손들은 아직 산을 오르는 중이었다. 녹은 얼음과 눈의 물줄기에서 목을 축이려 멈춰 섰던 한낮의 타는 더위가 높은 곳의 서리 내린 희박한 밤공기의 사무치는 추위로 바뀐 것처럼, 낮은 곳 여정의 싱그러운 아름다움도 황량함과 적막함에 자리를 내주었다. 노새들이 한 줄로 바위 덩어리에서 덩어리로 기어오르고 돌아가며, 마치 거대한 폐허의 부서진 계단을 오르는 듯한 험한 바윗길이 이제 그들의 길이었다. 나무 한 그루 보이지 않았고, 바위 틈에서 얼어붙은 초라한 갈색 덤불 이끼 말고는 어떤 식물도 자라지 않았다. 길가에 시커멓게 탄 앙상한 나무 팔뚝들이 수도원 쪽을 위로 가리키고 있었는데, 마치 눈에 파묻혀 죽은 옛 길손들의 유령이 제 고통의 자리를 떠도는 것 같았다. 고드름이 매달린 동굴과, 갑작스러운 폭풍을 피하려고 지은 대피소들은 이곳의 위험을 속삭이는 무수한 입처럼 보였다. 쉴 새 없이 떠도는 안개의 화환과 미로가 신음하는 바람에 쫓겨 떠돌았고, 산에서 가장 끈질긴 위험이자 그 모든 방비가 맞서고 있는 눈은, 날카롭게 휘날려 내렸다.\n\n하루의 고된 일에 지친 노새의 행렬이 깊은 비탈을 천천히 돌고 굽이쳐 올라갔다. 맨 앞에서는 챙 넓은 모자에 짧은 윗도리를 걸치고 어깨에 산악 지팡이 한두 개를 멘 안내인이 걸어서 길을 이끌었고, 또 다른 안내인이 그와 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 줄지어 탄 사람들 사이에는 말이 없었다. 매서운 추위와 여정의 피로, 그리고 마치 방금 아주 맑고 차가운 물에서 빠져나온 것 같기도 하고 한바탕 흐느낀 것 같기도 한, 숨이 막히는 새로운 감각이 그들을 잠잠하게 했다.\n\n마침내 바위 계단의 꼭대기에 켜진 불빛이 눈과 안개를 뚫고 어렴풋이 빛났다. 안내인들이 노새를 부르자 노새들은 늘어뜨렸던 머리를 쫑긋 세웠고, 길손들의 혀도 풀려, 미끄러지고 기어오르고 딸랑거리고 짤랑거리고 떠드는 소리가 한꺼번에 터져 나오는 가운데, 그들은 수도원 문 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n다른 노새들도 얼마 전에 도착해 있었는데, 더러는 농부를 태웠고 더러는 짐을 실은 채, 문 앞의 눈을 짓밟아 진창 웅덩이로 만들어 놓았다. 안장과 굴레, 짐안장과 줄줄이 달린 방울, 노새와 사람, 등불, 횃불, 자루, 사료, 술통, 치즈, 꿀과 버터가 든 작은 통, 짚단, 갖가지 모양의 꾸러미들이 이 녹아내린 수렁과 계단 언저리에 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉켜 있었다. 이 구름 위 높은 곳에서는 모든 것이 구름을 통해 보였고, 모든 것이 구름 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다. 사람들의 숨도 구름, 노새들의 숨도 구름이었고, 불빛은 구름에 둘러싸였으며, 바로 가까이에 있는 사람도 구름 때문에 보이지 않았으나 그 목소리와 다른 모든 소리는 놀랄 만큼 또렷했다. 담장 고리에 황급히 매어 놓은 그 흐릿한 노새 행렬 중 하나가 다른 놈을 물거나 발로 차면, 안개 전체가 술렁였다. 사람들이 그 속으로 뛰어들고, 사람과 짐승의 외침이 그 속에서 흘러나왔지만, 곁에 선 누구도 무엇이 잘못되었는지 분간하지 못했다. 이 한가운데에서, 지하층 전부를 차지하고 지하 문으로 드나드는 수도원의 커다란 마구간은 — 그 모든 소란이 벌어지는 곳이 바로 그 문밖이었다 — 마치 이 험상궂은 건물 전체가 구름 말고는 아무것도 들어 있지 않아서, 제 안을 비우자마자 무너져 내려 헐벗은 산꼭대기에 눈만 내리게 할 것처럼, 제 몫의 구름을 토해 내고 있었다.\n\n이 모든 소음과 부산함이 살아 있는 길손들 사이에 무성하던 그때, 거기에서 여남은 걸음 떨어진 쇠창살 친 집 안에도, 같은 구름에 싸이고 같은 눈송이가 흩날려 들어오는 가운데, 산에서 발견된 죽은 길손들이 말없이 모여 있었다. 여러 겨울 전 폭풍에 발이 묶인 어머니는 가슴에 아기를 안은 채 아직도 구석에 서 있었고, 두려움 혹은 굶주림에 팔을 입가로 들어 올린 채 얼어 죽은 사내는 여러 해가 지난 지금도 그 마른 입술로 여전히 팔을 누르고 있었다. 무시무시한 무리가, 알 수 없는 인연으로 함께 모인 것이다! 그 어머니가 내다보았을 법한 거친 운명이여! \"내가 한 번도 본 적 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 그토록 많은, 그런 동무들에게 둘러싸여, 나와 내 아이는 그랑생베르나르에서 떨어질 수 없이 함께 머무르며, 우리를 보러 올 세대들을 넘겨 살아남으리라. 그러나 그들은 결코 우리의 이름도, 우리 이야기의 단 한 마디도, 그 끝 말고는 알지 못하리라.\"\n\n살아 있는 길손들은 그때 죽은 자들을 거의, 아니 전혀 떠올리지 않았다. 그들은 수도원 문에서 내려 수도원 불가에서 몸을 녹이는 일을 훨씬 더 많이 생각했다. 노새 떼가 마구간에 들어가기 시작하면서 이미 가라앉아 가던 소동에서 벗어나, 그들은 덜덜 떨며 서둘러 계단을 올라 건물 안으로 들어갔다. 안에는 매어 둔 짐승들에게서 풍기는, 바닥에서 올라오는 냄새가 있었는데, 야생 동물 우리의 냄새 같았다. 안에는 튼튼한 아치형 회랑과 거대한 돌기둥, 큰 계단들이 있었고, 두꺼운 벽에는 깊숙이 박힌 작은 창들이 뚫려 있었다 — 산의 폭풍을 마치 인간 적군이기라도 한 듯 막아 내는 요새인 셈이었다. 안에는 음울한 둥근 천장의 침실들이 있었는데, 몹시 추웠으나 깨끗하고 손님을 맞을 채비가 갖추어져 있었다. 끝으로, 손님들이 앉아 저녁을 먹을 응접실이 하나 있었는데, 그곳에는 이미 식탁이 차려져 있었고, 활활 타는 불이 붉고 높게 빛나고 있었다.\n\n두 젊은 수사가 길손들에게 하룻밤 묵을 방을 배정해 준 뒤, 이 방에서 그들은 이윽고 난롯가에 둘러앉았다. 그들은 세 무리였는데, 그중 첫째 무리는 가장 인원이 많고 비중이 큰 만큼 가장 느려서, 올라오는 길에 다른 한 무리에게 따라잡혔다. 이 무리는 나이 지긋한 부인 한 명, 백발의 신사 두 명, 젊은 숙녀 두 명, 그리고 그들의 오라비로 이루어져 있었다. 이들에게는 (안내인 네 명은 말할 것도 없고) 안내원 한 명과 하인 두 명, 시중드는 하녀 두 명이 딸려 있었는데, 이 거추장스러운 큰 무리는 같은 지붕 아래 다른 곳에 묵었다. 이들을 따라잡아 그 뒤를 따라온 무리는 단 세 사람으로, 숙녀 한 명과 신사 두 명이었다. 셋째 무리는 고개의 이탈리아 쪽 골짜기에서 올라와 가장 먼저 도착했는데, 모두 네 사람이었다. 안경을 낀 다혈질에 굶주리고 말 없는 독일인 가정교사 한 명이, 역시 다혈질에 굶주리고 말 없으며 모두 안경을 낀 세 청년 제자와 함께 여행하는 길이었다.\n\n이 세 무리는 불가에 둘러앉아 서로를 무뚝뚝하게 곁눈질하며 저녁을 기다렸다. 그들 가운데 단 한 사람, 세 사람 무리에 속한 신사 한 명만이 대화를 트려고 나섰다. 자기 일행에게 말을 건네는 척하면서 그 비중 큰 무리의 우두머리를 겨냥해 낚싯줄을 던지듯, 그는 좌중 누구든 끼고 싶으면 끼어들 수 있는 어조로, 긴 하루였다고, 숙녀분들이 안쓰럽다고 말했다. 젊은 숙녀 한 분이 튼튼하거나 여행에 익숙한 분 같지 않아 두세 시간 전쯤 지나치게 지쳐 보이지 않았나 걱정스러웠다고. 자기는 뒤쪽에 있던 자리에서, 그분이 기진맥진한 듯 노새에 앉아 계신 것을 보았노라고. 그 뒤로 두세 번, 안내인 한 명이 뒤로 처졌을 때 그분이 어떠신지 물어보는 영광을 누렸다고. 그분이 기운을 되찾으셨고 그저 잠시 지나가는 불편이었을 뿐이라는 말을 듣고 무척 기뻤다고. 그분이 이제 조금도 나빠지지 않으시고, 이 여정을 떠나 오신 것을 후회하지 않으시기를 바란다는 마음을 표해도 좋을지 (이쯤에서 그는 우두머리의 눈길을 사로잡았고, 그를 향해 말했다) 믿어 본다고 했다.\n\n\"내 딸은 말이오, 고맙소이다, 선생,\" 우두머리가 답했다. \"완전히 회복했고, 무척 흥미로워하고 있소.\"\n\n\"산이 처음이신 모양이지요?\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다.\n\n\"처음이오 — 허 — 산이 말이오,\" 우두머리가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 선생께서는 산에 익숙하시지요?\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 넘겨짚었다.\n\n\"나는 — 흠 — 그럭저럭 익숙하오. 근래에는 아니지만. 근래에는 아니오,\" 우두머리가 손을 한 번 휘저으며 답했다.\n\n넌지시 파고드는 길손은 고개를 숙여 그 손짓에 화답하고는, 우두머리에게서 둘째 젊은 숙녀에게로 옮겨 갔다. 그 숙녀는 그가 그토록 예민한 관심을 느낀다던 숙녀들 가운데 한 명으로만 언급되었을 뿐, 그밖에 따로 언급된 적은 없었다.\n\n그는 그분이 오늘의 피로로 불편을 겪지 않으셨기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"불편이야 분명 겪었지요,\" 젊은 숙녀가 답했다. \"하지만 지치지는 않았어요.\"\n\n넌지시 파고드는 길손은 그 구분이 정확하다며 그녀를 칭송했다. 자기가 하려던 말이 바로 그것이라고. 어떤 숙녀라도 노새라는, 속담으로도 다루기 까다롭기로 이름난 그 짐승을 상대하노라면 틀림없이 불편을 겪을 수밖에 없다고.\n\n\"우리야 물론,\" 다소 새침하고 도도한 젊은 숙녀가 말했다, \"마르티니에서 마차며 짐수레를 두고 와야 했지요. 게다가 이런 닿기 어려운 곳에는 원하는 것을 무엇 하나 가져올 수 없고, 온갖 안락함을 다 두고 와야 하니, 편할 리가 없지요.\"\n\n\"참으로 야만스러운 곳이지요,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다.\n\n차림새가 정확함의 본보기요, 기계 장치로 친다면 그 태도가 완벽한 나이 지긋한 부인이, 여기에서 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 한마디 끼어들었다.\n\n\"하지만 다른 불편한 곳들과 마찬가지로,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"한 번은 봐 두어야 하는 곳이지요. 사람들 입에 많이 오르내리는 곳이니, 봐 둘 필요가 있어요.\"\n\n\"오! 봐 두는 거야 저는 조금도 마다하지 않아요, 정말이에요, 제너럴 부인,\" 다른 숙녀가 무심하게 답했다.\n\n\"부인께서는,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다, \"이곳에 전에도 와 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"네,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다. \"전에 와 본 적이 있어요. 얘야, 한마디 일러 두마,\" 앞의 젊은 숙녀에게 하는 말이었다, \"산 공기와 눈을 쐰 뒤이니, 뜨거운 장작불에서 얼굴을 가리도록 하렴. 너도, 얘야,\" 다른, 더 어린 숙녀에게 하는 말에 그 숙녀는 곧바로 그렇게 했으나, 앞의 숙녀는 그저 \"고맙습니다, 제너럴 부인, 저는 아주 편안하니 이대로 있는 게 좋아요\"라고만 했다.\n\n오라비는, 방 안에 놓인 피아노를 열려고 의자를 떠나 그 안에 대고 휘파람을 불어 보고는 다시 닫아 버린 뒤, 한쪽 눈에 외알 안경을 낀 채 어슬렁어슬렁 불가로 돌아왔다. 그는 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 갖춘 여행 차림이었다. 그의 채비에 걸맞은 분량의 여행거리를 내어 주기에는 세상이 도무지 충분치 않아 보일 정도였다.\n\n\"저 친구들, 저녁 차리는 데 엄청 굼뜨군,\" 그가 느릿느릿 말했다. \"뭘 내올지 궁금하군. 누구 짐작 가는 사람 있나?\"\n\n\"사람 구이는 아닐 것 같은데,\" 세 사람 무리 가운데 둘째 신사의 목소리가 답했다.\n\n\"안 그렇겠지요. 무슨 뜻이오?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"당신은 다 같이 먹을 저녁상에 오를 분이 아니니, 다 같이 쬐는 불에 당신 몸을 구워 대지 말아 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주십사 하는 뜻이지요,\" 상대가 답했다.\n\n난롯가에 편안한 자세로 서서, 불길을 등지고 윗도리 자락을 양팔 밑에 끼운 채 좌중을 향해 외알 안경을 치켜올린 그 젊은 신사는, 마치 가금류에 속하는 짐승이 구이용으로 묶여 있는 것 같은 모양새였는데, 이 대꾸에 낯을 잃었다. 그가 막 더 자세한 설명을 요구하려는 참에 — 모두의 눈길이 말한 이에게 쏠리면서 — 그와 함께 온, 젊고 아름다운 부인이 그의 어깨에 머리를 묻고 까무러쳐서 방금 오간 말을 듣지 못했다는 사실이 드러났다.\n\n\"아무래도,\" 신사가 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다, \"곧장 방으로 데려가는 게 낫겠소. 누구든 불러서 등불을 가져오게 해 주겠소?\" 일행에게 하는 말이었다, \"그리고 길을 안내하게 해 주오? 이 낯설고 어수선한 곳에서는 길을 찾을 수 있을지 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"제 하녀를 부르게 해 주세요,\" 두 젊은 숙녀 가운데 키가 더 큰 쪽이 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 물을 입에 좀 대어 드리게 해 주세요,\" 아직 말이 없던 키가 더 작은 쪽이 말했다.\n\n저마다 제안한 대로 하니 도움이 부족할 리 없었다. 사실, 두 하녀가 (길에서 누군가 외국어로 말을 걸어 그들을 벙어리로 만들까 봐 안내원의 호위를 받으며) 들어오자, 도움이 너무 넘칠 지경이었다. 이를 보고, 두 숙녀 가운데 더 가냘프고 어린 쪽에게 몇 마디로 그렇게 일러 준 뒤, 신사는 아내의 팔을 자기 어깨에 두르고 그녀를 안아 들어 데리고 나갔다.\n\n홀로 다른 손님들과 남게 된 그의 친구는, 다시 불가로 오지 않은 채 천천히 방 안을 오르내리며, 마치 아까의 응수에 자기가 책임이 있다고 느끼기라도 하는 양 생각에 잠겨 검은 콧수염을 잡아당겼다. 그 응수의 대상이 된 사람이 한구석에서 분을 씩씩 내뿜고 있는 동안, 우두머리가 거만하게 이 신사에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"선생 친구분은 말이오, 선생,\" 그가 말했다, \"그— 허 — 좀 성마른 분이구려. 그리고 그 성급함 탓에, 자기가 — 흠 — 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 온전히 헤아리지 못하는 듯한데 — 아니, 그 이야기는 접어 둡시다, 접어 둡시다. 선생 친구분은 좀 성마른 분이오, 선생.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요, 선생,\" 상대가 답했다. \"하지만 얼마 전 제네바의 어느 호텔에서, 우리를 비롯해 좋은 분들이 여럿 모였던 그곳에서 저 신사분과 알게 되는 영광을 누렸고, 그 뒤로도 여러 차례 함께 길을 다니며 같이 지내고 이야기를 나누는 영광을 누린 사람으로서, 저는 저 신사분에 대해 해로운 말은 어떤 것도 — 아니, 선생 같은 풍채와 지위를 지닌 분에게서라 해도 — 들을 수 없습니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"내게서 그런 말을 들으실 일은 없을 것이오, 선생. 선생 친구분이 성급함을 보였다고 말한 것이지, 그런 말을 한 것이 아니오. 내가 그 말을 한 것은, 내 아들이 태생으로도 그리고 — 허 — 교육으로도 — 흠 — 신사인 까닭에, 불을 이 자리의 모두가 똑같이 쬘 수 있게 하자는, 정중하게 표한 바람이 있다면 두말없이 거기에 맞추었으리라는 것이 의심할 여지가 없기 때문이오. 이는 원칙적으로 보아도 나는 — 허 — 이런 경우에는 모두가 — 흠 — 평등하니 — 옳다고 여기는 바이오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다,\" 그 대답이었다. \"그것으로 끝입니다! 저는 아드님의 충직한 종입니다. 아드님께 깊은 경의를 받아 주십사 청합니다. 그리고 이제, 선생, 제 친구가 때로 비꼬는 기질이 있다는 점은 기꺼이, 솔직히 인정하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그 부인이 선생 친구분의 아내이시오, 선생?\"\n\n\"그 부인은 제 친구의 아내입니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"매우 아름다우시더이다.\"\n\n\"선생, 견줄 데가 없는 분입니다. 두 분은 아직 결혼 첫해이지요. 두 분은 한편으로는 신혼여행, 한편으로는 예술 기행 중입니다.\"\n\n\"선생 친구분이 예술가시오, 선생?\"\n\n신사는 오른손 손가락에 입을 맞추고는 그 입맞춤을 팔 길이만큼 하늘을 향해 흩날려 보내는 것으로 답했다. 마치, 나는 그를 불멸의 예술가로서 천상의 신들께 바치노라, 하고 말하는 듯이.\n\n\"하지만 그분은 명문가 출신입니다,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"혈연 인맥이 더없이 훌륭하지요. 그분은 예술가 이상입니다. 가문이 대단한 분입니다. 사실, 그분이 자기 인맥을 거만하게, 성마르게, 비꼬듯이 (두 단어 다 양보하겠습니다) 내쳤을지는 몰라도, 그 인맥은 엄연히 있습니다. 우리가 어울리는 동안 튀어나온 불꽃들이 제게 이를 보여 주었지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 그 부인의 탈이,\" 거만한 신사가 그 화제를 마침내 정리해 버리는 투로 말했다, \"일시적인 것에 그치기를 바라오.\"\n\"선생, 저도 그러기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"그저 피로 탓이겠지요, 아마.\"\n\n\"순전히 피로 탓만은 아닙니다, 선생. 오늘 그분의 노새가 발을 헛디뎌 안장에서 떨어지셨거든요. 가볍게 떨어지셔서 부축 없이 곧장 일어나셨고, 웃으며 우리 곁을 떠나 말을 모셨지요. 하지만 저녁 무렵 옆구리에 가벼운 멍이 들었다고 호소하셨습니다. 우리가 선생 일행을 따라 산을 오르는 동안 그분은 그 이야기를 한 번 이상 하셨습니다.\"\n\n그 많은 수행원의 우두머리는, 정중하긴 해도 허물없지는 않은 사람이라, 이쯤 되자 자기가 분에 넘치도록 자세를 낮추었다고 여기는 듯했다. 그는 더는 말이 없었고, 저녁상이 나오기까지 십오 분쯤 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n저녁상과 함께 젊은 수사 한 명이 (나이 든 수사는 없는 듯했다) 식탁 상석에 앉으러 들어왔다. 그것은 여느 스위스 호텔의 저녁상과 같았고, 더 온화한 기후에서 수도원이 직접 빚은 좋은 적포도주도 빠지지 않았다. 예술가 길손은 다른 이들이 자리에 앉자 태연히 와서 자기 자리를 차지했는데, 아까 말끔히 차려입은 길손과 벌인 옥신각신에 대한 기색은 조금도 없어 보였다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 그가 수프를 먹으며 주인 격인 수사에게 물었다, \"이 수도원에는 그 유명한 개가 지금 여러 마리 있나요?\"\n\n\"무슈, 세 마리 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아래 회랑에서 세 마리를 보았소. 분명 바로 그 세 마리겠군요.\"\n\n호리호리하고 눈이 반짝이는, 거무스름한 얼굴의 정중한 젊은 주인은, 멜빵처럼 흰 띠가 가로질러 덧대어진 검은 가운을 걸쳤는데, 그가 흔히들 떠올리는 생베르나르 수사의 전형을 닮지 않은 것은 흔히들 떠올리는 생베르나르 개의 전형을 닮지 않은 것과 매한가지였다. 그는 그렇다, 바로 그 세 마리라고 답했다.\n\n\"그런데 내 생각엔,\" 예술가 길손이 말했다, \"그중 한 마리는 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같소.\"\n\n그럴 법한 일이었다. 그 개는 꽤 널리 알려진 놈이었다. 무슈는 그 개가 수도회의 한 수사와 함께 수도원을 위한 도움을 청하러 내려갔을 때, 골짜기나 호수 어딘가에서 그 개를 쉬이 보았을 수 있었다.\n\n\"그런 일은 해마다 정해진 철에 한다는 거지요, 아마?\"\n\n무슈의 말이 옳았다.\n\n\"그리고 늘 개를 데리고 말이지요. 그 개가 아주 중요한 모양이오.\"\n\n다시 무슈의 말이 옳았다. 그 개는 매우 중요했다. 사람들이 그 개에게 관심을 두는 것은 마땅했다. 어디서나 이름난 그 개들 가운데 한 마리이니, 마드무아젤께서도 보셨을 거라고.\n\n마드무아젤은, 아직 프랑스어에 익숙지 않은 듯, 그 말을 알아채는 데 조금 더뎠다. 하지만 제너럴 부인이 그녀를 대신해 알아챘다.\n\n\"그동안 많은 목숨을 구했는지 물어봐 주세요,\" 아까 낯을 잃었던 젊은이가 모국어인 영어로 말했다.\n\n주인은 그 물음을 통역해 줄 필요가 없었다. 그는 곧바로 프랑스어로 답했다. \"아니요. 이 녀석은 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"왜 아니죠?\" 같은 신사가 물었다.\n\n\"실례지만,\" 주인이 차분하게 답했다, \"기회만 주어진다면 이 녀석은 틀림없이 그렇게 할 겁니다. 이를테면, 저는 확신합니다만,\" 송아지 고기 요리를 잘라 돌리려고 하면서, 낯을 잃었던 젊은이를 향해 점잖게 미소 지으며 말했다, \"무슈께서 이 녀석에게 기회를 주신다면, 녀석은 자기 소임을 다하려고 무척이나 열렬히 달려갈 겁니다.\"\n\n예술가 길손이 웃었다. 넌지시 파고드는 길손은 (저녁을 제 몫만큼 빠짐없이 챙기려는 빈틈없는 조바심을 드러내며) 빵 한 조각으로 콧수염에 묻은 포도주 방울을 닦아 내고는 대화에 끼어들었다.\n\n\"신부님, 관광객들이 다니기에는 철이 늦어 가고 있지 않습니까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다, 늦었지요. 그래도 길어야 두세 주만 지나면, 우리는 겨울눈에 갇히게 됩니다.\"\n\n\"그러고 나면,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다, \"그림에서 보던 대로, 발로 눈을 파헤치는 개들과 파묻힌 아이들의 차례가 오겠군요!\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 주인이 그 비유를 잘 알아듣지 못한 채 말했다. \"어째서, 그림에서 보던 대로, 눈을 파헤치는 개들과 파묻힌 아이들이라는 거죠?\"\n\n대답이 나오기 전에 예술가 길손이 다시 끼어들었다.\n\n\"자네는 모르나,\" 그가 식탁 너머로 일행에게 냉랭하게 물었다, \"겨울에 이 길로 다니는 건 밀수꾼뿐이고, 이 길로 무슨 볼일이 있을 만한 자도 밀수꾼뿐이라는 걸?\"\n\n\"맙소사! 모르는데. 그런 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"그렇다더군. 그리고 그자들은 날씨의 징후를 제법 잘 아니까, 개들을 별로 부려 먹지 않지 — 그래서 개들도 차츰 죽어 나간 셈이야 — 정작 이 숙소는 그자들 자신에게는 자리가 편리하긴 하지만 말이야. 듣자 하니 그자들은 어린 자식들은 보통 집에 두고 온다더군. 하지만 거 참 멋진 발상이야!\" 예술가 길손이 느닷없이 열띤 어조로 목소리를 높이며 외쳤다. \"숭고한 발상이지. 세상에서 가장 근사한 발상이고, 맹세코, 사람 눈에 눈물이 다 나게 하는 발상이야!\" 그러고는 더없이 태연하게 송아지 고기를 마저 먹어 갔다.\n\n이 말의 바닥에는 조롱 섞인 모순이 충분히 깔려 있어서 다소 거슬리는 데가 있었으나, 태도가 세련되고 사람됨이 인물 좋은 데다, 깎아내리는 부분은 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 흘려 던졌는지, 영어에 완전히 익숙하지 않은 사람으로서는 알아듣기조차 어렵고, 설령 알아듣는다 해도 그 어조가 너무 담담하고 격이 없어 노여워하기조차 어려울 정도였다. 침묵 속에서 송아지 고기를 다 먹고 나서, 말한 이는 다시 친구에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"보게,\" 그가 아까의 어조로 말했다, \"여기 우리 주인이신 이 신사분을 말이야. 아직 한창때도 안 되었는데, 저토록 우아하게, 저토록 궁정풍의 정중함과 겸손함으로 우리를 보살펴 주시잖나! 왕관에나 어울릴 예법이지! 런던 시장과 만찬을 해 보고 (초대받을 수 있다면 말이지) 그 차이를 살펴보게나. 내가 여태 본 가장 잘생긴 얼굴을 지닌 이 친구는, 흠 하나 없이 잘 그려진 그 얼굴로, 어디 고된 삶을 버리고 이 위로 — 해발 몇 피트나 되는지도 모를 이 높은 곳으로 — 올라와서는, 자네나 나 같은 한가로운 불쌍한 인간들을 위해 숙소를 지키고 그 계산은 우리 양심에 맡기는 것 말고는 (좋은 식당에서 직접 즐기는 것도 있겠지만, 그러길 바라네) 세상에 다른 목적이라곤 없잖나! 자, 이거 참 아름다운 희생 아닌가? 우리를 감동시키는 데 이보다 더 무엇이 필요한가? 열두 달 중 여덟아홉 달은, 구조된 사람들이 — 그것도 흥미로운 풍채를 한 이들이 — 나무통을 매단 가장 영리한 개의 목에 매달려 여기 들러붙어 있지 않는다고 해서, 이곳을 깎아내려야 하나? 아니지! 이곳에 축복을. 위대한 곳이야, 영광스러운 곳이지!\"\n\n비중 큰 무리의 우두머리인 백발 신사의 가슴은, 자기가 불쌍한 인간들 축에 끼는 데 항의라도 하듯 부풀어 올랐다. 예술가 길손이 말을 마치자마자 그는, 어느 자리에서나 앞장서는 것이 자기 소임인데 잠시 그 소임을 저버리고 있었다는 듯, 자못 위엄 있게 입을 열었다.\n\n그는 주인에게, 겨울철 이곳의 삶이 무척 따분한 삶일 것이 틀림없다는 견해를 묵직하게 전했다.\n\n주인은 무슈에게, 다소 단조롭기는 하다고 인정했다. 공기는 오래 잇따라 들이쉬기에 버거웠다. 추위는 매우 혹독했다. 그것을 견디려면 젊음과 힘이 필요했다. 그러나 그것을 갖추고, 또한 하늘의 축복이 있으면 —\n\n그렇소, 참으로 좋은 말이오. \"하지만 그 갇혀 지냄 말이오,\" 백발 신사가 말했다.\n\n날씨가 궂은 날에도, 바깥을 거닐 수 있는 날이 많았다. 작은 길을 다져 놓고 거기에서 운동을 하는 것이 관례였다.\n\n\"하지만 그 공간이,\" 백발 신사가 따지듯 말했다. \"너무 비좁지 않소. 그— 허 — 매우 한정되어 있지 않소.\"\n\n무슈께서는, 찾아가 볼 대피소들이 있고, 그곳들로 향하는 길도 다져 놓아야 한다는 점을 떠올려 보시라고.\n\n무슈는 그래도, 그 공간이 그— 허 — 흠 — 너무나 옹색하다고 거듭 따졌다. 게다가 늘 똑같지 않으냐고, 언제나 똑같지 않으냐고.\n\n주인은 송구하다는 미소를 띠며 어깨를 가만히 올렸다가 가만히 내렸다. 그것은 사실이라고 그는 말했다. 하지만 거의 모든 사물에는 저마다 다른 관점이 있다는 점을 말씀드리도록 해 달라고. 무슈와 자기는 자기의 이 가난한 삶을 같은 관점에서 보고 있지 않다고. 무슈는 갇혀 지냄에 익숙지 않으시니까.\n\n\"나는 — 허 — 그렇소, 참으로 옳은 말이오,\" 백발 신사가 말했다. 그는 그 논리의 힘에 적잖이 충격을 받은 듯했다.\n\n무슈는, 영국인 길손으로서, 즐겁게 여행할 온갖 수단에 둘러싸여 있고, 틀림없이 재산과 마차와 하인을 거느린 분이시니 —\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소. 의심할 여지 없이,\" 신사가 말했다.\n\n무슈로서는, 내일 여기로 가야지, 모레 저기로 가야지, 하고 고를 힘이 없는 사람, 이 장벽들을 넘어가야지, 저 경계들을 넓혀야지 하고 마음대로 할 수 없는 사람의 처지에 자신을 놓아 보기가 쉽지 않으실 거라고. 무슈는, 그런 일들에서 사람의 마음이 어떻게 필요의 힘에 스스로를 맞추어 가는지를, 아마 실감하지 못하실 거라고.\n\n\"그건 사실이오,\" 무슈가 말했다. \"우리는 — 허 — 이 화제를 더 끌지 맙시다. 선생 말이 — 흠 — 더없이 옳소, 의심치 않소. 더는 말하지 맙시다.\"\n\n저녁이 끝나자 그는 이렇게 말하며 의자를 뒤로 빼고, 불가의 아까 그 자리로 물러갔다. 식탁의 대부분 자리가 몹시 추웠으므로, 다른 손님들도 잠자리에 들기 전에 몸을 충분히 데우려고 불가의 아까 그 자리로 돌아갔다. 주인은 그들이 식탁에서 일어서자 좌중에 모두에게 절하고 안녕히 주무시라 인사한 뒤 물러갔다. 다만 그 전에 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 그에게 포도주를 데워 줄 수 있는지 물었고, 그가 그렇다고 답한 뒤 곧 그것을 들여보내 주었으므로, 그 길손은 무리 한가운데, 불기운을 온전히 받는 자리에 앉아 이내 나머지 사람들에게 그것을 따라 돌리는 일에 분주했다.\n\n이 무렵, 두 젊은 숙녀 가운데 더 어린 쪽이 — 어둑한 구석에서 (등불은 그을음이 끼고 흐릿했으므로, 그 음울한 방의 주된 빛은 난롯불이었다) 그 자리에 없는 부인에 관해 오간 말을 말없이 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 그녀가 — 슬며시 방을 빠져나갔다. 문을 살그머니 닫고 나자 어느 쪽으로 가야 할지 막막했으나, 울리는 복도들과 갈래길 사이에서 잠시 망설이다가, 본 회랑 한구석에 있는, 하인들이 저녁을 먹고 있는 방에 이르렀다. 그들에게서 그녀는 등불 하나와 그 부인의 방으로 가는 길을 얻었다.\n\n그것은 큰 계단을 올라간 위층에 있었다. 군데군데 헐벗은 흰 벽이 쇠창살로 끊겨 있어, 걸어가면서 그녀는 이곳이 어쩐지 감옥 같다고 생각했다. 그 부인의 방, 아니 독방의 아치형 문은 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았다. 두세 번 두드려도 대답이 없자, 그녀는 문을 가만히 밀어 열고 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n부인은 눈을 감은 채 침대 위에 누워 있었는데, 까무러쳤다 깨어났을 때 덮어 주었던 담요와 두르개로 추위를 막고 있었다. 창의 깊숙이 들어간 자리에 놓인 흐릿한 불빛은 아치형 방에 거의 빛을 비추지 못했다. 찾아온 이는 머뭇머뭇 침대로 다가가, 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다. \"좀 나으세요?\"\n\n부인은 선잠에 빠져 있었고, 그 속삭임은 너무 낮아 그녀를 깨우지 못했다. 찾아온 이는 가만히 선 채 그녀를 유심히 바라보았다.\n\n\"참 예쁜 분이야,\" 그녀가 혼잣말을 했다. \"이렇게 아름다운 얼굴은 본 적이 없어. 오, 나와는 어쩌면 이리도 다를까!\"\n\n이상한 말이었지만, 거기엔 어떤 숨은 뜻이 담겨 있었으니, 그 말에 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"내 짐작이 틀림없어. 그날 저녁 그분이 이분 이야기를 했던 게 분명해. 다른 일이라면 얼마든지 틀릴 수 있어도, 이건 아니야, 이것만은 아니야!\"\n\n그녀는 조용하고 다정한 손길로 잠든 이의 흐트러진 머리카락 한 가닥을 옆으로 넘겨 주고는, 이불 밖으로 나와 있는 손을 가만히 만졌다.\n\n\"이분을 바라보는 게 좋아,\" 그녀가 속으로 숨죽여 말했다. \"그분의 마음을 그토록 흔들어 놓은 게 무엇인지 보는 게 좋아.\"\n\n그녀가 미처 손을 거두지 못했을 때, 잠든 이가 눈을 뜨며 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"놀라지 마세요. 저는 아래층에서 온 길손 중 한 명일 뿐이에요. 좀 나으셨는지, 제가 도와드릴 일은 없는지 여쭈러 왔어요.\"\n\n\"이미 친절하게도 하녀를 보내 저를 도와주신 것 같던데요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 제가 아니에요. 그건 제 언니였어요. 좀 나으세요?\"\n\n\"훨씬 나아요. 그저 가벼운 멍이고, 잘 돌봐 주어서 이제 거의 괜찮아졌어요. 그게 한순간에 어지럽고 정신이 아득해지더라고요. 전부터 아프긴 했는데, 마지막에 한꺼번에 저를 덮쳤어요.\"\n\n\"누가 올 때까지 곁에 있어도 될까요? 그러는 게 좋으시겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래 주면 좋겠어요. 여기 혼자 있으니 외로워서요. 하지만 당신이 너무 추울까 봐 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"저는 추위는 괜찮아요. 이래 보여도 약한 사람이 아니거든요.\" 그녀는 두 개의 투박한 의자 가운데 하나를 재빨리 침대 곁으로 옮겨 와 앉았다. 상대도 그만큼 재빨리 자기 여행용 두르개의 한 자락을 풀어 그녀에게 덮어 주었는데, 그것을 여미느라 그녀의 팔이 자기 어깨에 얹히게 되었다.\n\n\"당신은 어찌나 다정한 간병인 같은지,\" 부인이 그녀에게 미소 지으며 말했다, \"꼭 우리 집에서 나를 찾아와 준 사람 같아요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n\"방금 깨어났을 때 집 꿈을 꾸고 있었어요. 옛집 말이에요, 결혼하기 전의.\"\n\n\"그리고 그곳에서 이토록 멀리 떠나오기 전의 집이군요.\"\n\n\"이보다 훨씬 더 멀리 떠나 본 적도 있어요. 하지만 그땐 그 집의 가장 좋은 것을 함께 가지고 가서, 아쉬울 게 없었지요. 여기서 잠이 들 때 외로웠고, 그게 조금 그리워서, 마음이 그 집으로 거슬러 가더군요.\"\n\n그녀의 목소리에는 슬프도록 애틋하고 안타까운 울림이 깃들어 있어서, 찾아온 이는 그 순간 그녀를 차마 바라보지 못했다.\n\n\"당신이 나를 감싸 준 이 두르개 아래에서 우리가 마침내 이렇게 만나게 되다니, 묘한 인연이에요,\" 잠시 뒤 찾아온 이가 말했다. \"실은 말이에요, 제가 얼마 전부터 당신을 찾고 있었거든요.\"\n\n\"저를 찾고 있었다고요?\"\n\n\"여기 작은 쪽지를 하나 가지고 있는 것 같아요. 당신을 찾게 되면 전해 드리라던 거예요. 바로 이거예요. 제가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 당신 앞으로 된 것이지요? 아니에요?\"\n\n부인은 그것을 받아 들고 그렇다고 말한 뒤 읽었다. 찾아온 이는 그녀가 읽는 동안 그녀를 지켜보았다. 쪽지는 아주 짧았다. 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 찾아온 이의 뺨에 입을 맞추고 그 손을 꼭 잡았다.\n\n\"그분이 나에게 소개해 주신 그 다정한 젊은 벗이, 언젠가 나에게 위로가 되어 줄지 모른다고 여기 적혀 있네요. 그런데 그 벗은, 내가 처음 보는 이 순간부터 이미 진정한 위로가 되어 주고 있어요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 당신은,\" 찾아온 이가 머뭇거리며 말했다, \"어쩌면 당신은 제 사연을 모르시겠지요? 어쩌면 그분이 제 사연을 한 번도 말씀하지 않으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"오, 아니, 무엇하러 그러셨겠어요! 저도 지금은 그걸 직접 말씀드릴 처지가 거의 못 돼요. 그러지 말라고 부탁을 받았거든요. 별것 아닌 사연이지만, 어쩌면 그 때문에 제가 여기서는 이 편지에 대해 아무 말도 말아 달라고 부탁드리는 까닭을 짐작하실 수 있을 거예요. 아마 저와 함께 온 제 가족을 보셨겠지요? 그 가운데 몇몇은 — 이 말은 당신께만 드리는 거예요 — 조금 자존심이 세고, 조금 편견이 있어요.\"\n\n\"이건 다시 당신이 가져가는 게 좋겠어요,\" 상대가 말했다. \"그러면 제 남편이 절대 보지 못할 테니까요. 안 그러면 어쩌다 그가 이걸 보고 입에 올릴 수도 있잖아요. 확실히 해 두게, 다시 품속에 넣어 두시겠어요?\"\n\n그녀는 그것을 매우 조심스럽게 그렇게 했다. 작고 가냘픈 그녀의 손이 아직 그 편지 위에 놓여 있을 때, 그들은 바깥 회랑에서 누군가의 인기척을 들었다.\n\n\"제가 약속했거든요,\" 찾아온 이가 일어서며 말했다, \"당신을 만난 뒤에 (조만간 당신을 만나게 될 게 거의 틀림없었으니까요) 그분께 편지를 써서, 당신이 잘 지내고 행복한지 알려 드리겠다고요. 잘 지내고 행복하시다고 적는 게 좋겠어요.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 네! 아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복하다고 적어 주세요. 그리고 그분께 애정 어린 감사를 드리며, 결코 그분을 잊지 않겠다고요.\"\n\n\"아침에 또 뵐게요. 그다음에도 머지않아 분명 다시 만나게 될 거예요. 안녕히 주무세요!\"\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요. 고마워요, 고마워요. 잘 자요, 그대!\"\n\n이 작별 인사를 주고받을 때도, 찾아온 이가 문밖으로 나설 때도, 두 사람 다 마음이 들떠 떨리고 있었다. 그녀는 그 부인의 남편이 문 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 마주칠 줄 알았으나, 회랑에 있는 사람은 그가 아니었다. 빵 조각으로 콧수염에 묻은 포도주 방울을 닦아 내던 그 길손이었다. 등 뒤의 발소리를 듣고 그는 돌아섰다 — 어둠 속에서 저쪽으로 걸어가던 참이었다.\n\n지극히 깍듯한 그의 예의는, 젊은 숙녀가 손수 불을 밝혀 가며 아래층으로 내려가거나 혼자 내려가도록 두고 보지 못했다. 그는 그녀의 등불을 받아 들어 돌계단에 가장 밝게 빛이 비치도록 들고, 저녁 식사를 한 방까지 줄곧 그녀를 따라갔다. 그녀는 자기가 얼마나 움츠러들고 떨고 싶은지를 가까스로 감추며 내려갔다. 이 길손의 모습이 그녀에게는 유독 거북했기 때문이다. 저녁 식사 전 조용한 구석에 앉아, 그녀는 그가 자기 경험 속의 장면과 장소들에 놓이면 어떤 모습일지 상상하다가, 마침내 그에게서 거의 끔찍하다 할 만한 혐오감을 느끼게 되었던 것이다.\n\n그는 미소 띤 정중함으로 그녀를 따라 내려갔고, 그녀를 따라 방으로 들어와, 난롯가의 가장 좋은 자리에 다시 앉았다. 사그라들기 시작한 장작불이 어두운 방 안에서 그의 위로 너울대며 오르내리는 가운데, 그는 두 다리를 쭉 뻗어 녹이며, 벽과 천장에 그를 흉내 내는 괴물 같은 그림자를 드리운 채, 데운 포도주를 찌꺼기까지 들이켜며 앉아 있었다.\n\n지친 일행은 흩어졌고, 불가의 의자에서 졸고 있는 젊은 숙녀의 아버지를 빼고는 나머지 모두가 잠자리에 들었다. 길손은 일부러 한참이나 위층 자기 침실까지 올라가 호주머니에 넣어 두던 브랜디 병을 가져왔다. 그는 남은 포도주에 그 내용물을 부으며 그들에게 그 이야기를 했고, 새로운 맛에 흡족해하며 마셨다.\n\n\"실례지만, 선생, 이탈리아로 가시는 길입니까?\"\n\n백발 신사가 몸을 추스르고 물러갈 채비를 하던 참이었다. 그는 그렇다고 답했다.\n\n\"저도 그렇습니다!\" 길손이 말했다. \"이 음울한 산보다 더 아름다운 풍경 속에서, 더 부드러운 상황 아래에서 다시 인사 올리는 영광을 누리기를 바라겠습니다.\"\n\n신사는 제법 데면데면하게 절하고는, 고맙다고 말했다.\n\n\"우리 같은 가난한 신사들은 말입니다, 선생,\" 길손이 손으로 콧수염을 훔쳐 말렸는데, 포도주와 브랜디에 담갔던 탓이었다, \"우리 가난한 신사들은 왕족처럼 여행하지는 못해도, 삶의 예의와 품위만은 우리에게 소중하지요. 선생의 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n\"선생, 고맙소.\"\n\n\"선생의 명망 높은 가문의 건강을 위하여 — 아리따운 숙녀분들, 따님들의 건강을 위하여!\"\n\n\"선생, 다시 한번 고맙소. 안녕히 주무시기를. 얘야, 우리 — 허 — 우리 사람들이 시중들러 와 있느냐?\"\n\n\"바로 곁에 있어요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"제가 열어 드리지요!\" 길손이 일어나 문을 잡아 주며 말했는데, 신사가 딸의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고 방을 가로질러 문 쪽으로 가던 참이었다. \"편히 쉬시기를! 다시 한번 뵙게 될 기쁨을 위하여! 내일을 위하여!\"\n\n그가 더없이 우아한 태도와 가장 멋스러운 미소로 손에 입을 맞추는 동안, 젊은 숙녀는 아버지 쪽으로 조금 더 바짝 다가서서, 그에게 닿을까 두려워하며 그의 곁을 지나쳤다.\n\n\"흥!\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했는데, 혼자 남게 되자 그의 태도는 움츠러들고 목소리는 가라앉았다. \"다들 잠자리에 든다면, 나도 가야겠군. 거 더럽게도 서두르는군. 이 얼어붙은 적막과 고독 속에서라면, 두 시간 뒤에 잠자리에 든다 해도 밤이 충분히 길 텐데 말이야.\"\n잔을 비우느라 고개를 뒤로 젖히던 그는, 피아노 위에 펜과 잉크가 곁에 놓인 채 펼쳐져 있는 길손 명부에 눈길을 던졌다. 마치 그가 자리에 없는 사이에 오늘 밤 이름들이 적혀 있는 듯했다. 그것을 손에 들고 그는 이 기록들을 읽었다.\n\n윌리엄 도릿 귀하\n프레더릭 도릿 귀하\n\n에드워드 도릿 귀하\n도릿 양\n\n에이미 도릿 양\n제너럴 부인\n\n그리고 수행원 일동.\n프랑스에서 이탈리아로.\n\n헨리 가우언 부부.\n프랑스에서 이탈리아로.\n\n거기에 그는, 마치 나머지 이름들 전부를 향해 던진 올가미처럼 길고 가느다란 장식 획으로 끝맺는, 작고 복잡한 필체로 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n블랑두아. 파리.\n\n# CHAPTER 2. Mrs General\n\n프랑스에서 이탈리아로.\n그러고는, 콧날을 콧수염 위로 내리고 콧수염을 콧날 밑으로 치켜올린 채, 자기에게 배정된 독방으로 물러갔다.\n\n도릿 일가의 수행원 가운데, 여행자 명부에 자기 이름 한 줄을 차지할 만큼 충분히 중요한 인물이었던 그 교양 있는 부인을 소개하는 일은 빼놓을 수 없다.\n제너럴 부인은 어느 대성당 도시에 사는 성직 고위 인사의 딸로, 미혼의 부인이 다다를 수 있는 마흔다섯 살에 거의 근접할 때까지 그곳에서 유행을 선도해 왔다. 예순 살의 한 깐깐한 병참 장교는, 군기에 엄격한 사람으로 이름난 자였는데, 그녀가 대성당 도시의 사교계 한복판으로 예법이라는 사두마차를 몰아 가던 그 위엄에 반하고 말았다. 그래서 그 사두마차가 매여 있는 의례라는 냉랭한 마차의 마부석에서 그녀 곁에 앉게 해 달라고 청했다. 그의 청혼이 부인에게 받아들여지자, 병참 장교는 더없이 점잖게 예법 뒤편에 자기 자리를 잡았고, 제너럴 부인은 병참 장교가 죽을 때까지 마차를 몰았다. 두 사람이 함께한 여정에서 그들은 예법의 길목에 끼어든 여러 사람을 깔아뭉갰지만, 언제나 고상한 품새로, 침착하게 그렇게 했다.\n\n병참 장교가 그 직무에 어울리는 온갖 장식을 갖추고 묻히고 나자(예법이라는 사두마차 전체가 그의 영구차에 매였고, 모두 깃털 장식과 검은 벨벳 마의를 둘렀으며 그 한구석에는 그의 문장이 박혀 있었다), 제너럴 부인은 은행에 먼지와 재가 얼마나 예치되어 있는지 알아보기 시작했다. 그러자 병참 장교가 결혼 몇 해 전에 종신연금을 사 두고도, 청혼 당시 자기 수입이 재산의 이자에서 나온다고만 말하면서 그 사정을 슬쩍 감춤으로써 제너럴 부인에게 한발 앞섰다는 사실이 드러났다. 그 결과 제너럴 부인은 자기 재산이 너무도 줄어든 것을 알게 되어, 만약 그 정신이 완벽하게 다스려지지 않았더라면, 병참 장교가 아무것도 가지고 갈 수 없다고 선언했던 장례 의식의 그 대목이 정확한지 의심하고 싶은 마음이 들었을지도 모른다.\n\n이런 형편에서 제너럴 부인은, 어느 명문가 젊은 아가씨의 '정신을 형성하고' 아울러 그 몸가짐도 가다듬어 줄 수 있겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 또는 어느 부유한 젊은 상속녀나 미망인의 마차에 예법을 매어, 그런 탈것을 몰고 사교계의 미로를 헤쳐 나가는 마부이자 호위병이 단번에 될 수 있겠다는 생각도 들었다. 제너럴 부인이 이 착상을 성직과 병참 쪽 친지들에게 전하자 어찌나 열렬한 박수를 받았던지, 부인의 의심할 바 없는 미덕이 아니었다면 마치 그들이 그녀를 떼어 내고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보였을 정도였다. 제너럴 부인을 신앙심과 학식, 덕성과 품위의 경이라고 일컫는 추천장들이 유력한 곳들로부터 아낌없이 쏟아져 들어왔다. 어느 존경받는 부주교는 평생 단 한 번도 제너럴 부인을 직접 본 영광이나 도덕적 만족을 누린 적이 없으면서도, (믿을 만한 사람들이 그에게 묘사해 준) 그녀의 완벽함을 증언하며 기록할 때 눈물까지 흘렸다.\n\n이렇게 이를테면 교회와 국가로부터 사명을 위임받은 제너럴 부인은, 늘 높은 자리를 차지해 왔던 터라 그 자리를 지킬 만한 처지라 여겼고, 자기 몸값을 아주 높게 매기는 것으로 일을 시작했다. 한동안 시간이 흐르도록 제너럴 부인에게 응찰하는 이가 없었다. 마침내 열네 살 난 딸을 둔 어느 시골의 홀아비가 부인과 협상을 트게 되었다. 그런데 자기가 구하는 것이 아니라 오히려 훨씬 더 구해지는 사람인 양 처신하는 것이 제너럴 부인의 타고난 위엄에서 비롯된 것이든 인위적인 처세술에서 비롯된 것이든(어쨌든 둘 중 하나임은 분명했다) 그 일부였던 까닭에, 홀아비는 부인이 자기 딸의 정신과 몸가짐을 가다듬어 주도록 승낙할 때까지 줄곧 그녀를 좇았다.\n\n이 임무를 수행하는 데 제너럴 부인은 약 칠 년을 들였고, 그동안 유럽을 두루 돌며, 세련된 교양을 갖춘 사람이라면 누구나 제 눈이 아니라 남의 눈으로 보아야 마땅한 그 방대하고 잡다한 볼거리의 대부분을 구경했다. 마침내 맡은 아가씨의 정신이 다 형성되자, 그 젊은 아가씨뿐 아니라 그녀의 아버지인 홀아비의 결혼까지도 결정되었다. 그러자 홀아비는 제너럴 부인이 거추장스럽고 돈이 많이 든다는 것을 깨닫고는, 별안간 부주교 못지않게 그녀의 미덕에 감복한 듯 굴면서, 이 축복을 다른 누군가에게 넘길 기회가 생길 법한 곳이면 어디서든 그녀의 빼어난 가치를 칭송하고 다녔다. 그리하여 제너럴 부인의 이름은 그 어느 때보다 더 명예로워졌다.\n\n이 불사조가 그 드높은 횃대에 세 들 곳을 찾고 있을 무렵, 최근에 재산을 물려받은 도릿 씨가 자기 거래 은행에 일러두기를, 좋은 가문 출신에 교양 있고 인맥이 좋으며 상류 사회에 익숙하여, 자기 딸들의 교육을 마무리해 주는 동시에 그들의 보호자 겸 동반자가 될 자격을 갖춘 부인을 찾고 싶다고 했다. 도릿 씨의 거래 은행은, 그 시골 홀아비의 거래 은행이기도 했던 터라, 즉각 \"제너럴 부인\"이라고 말했다.\n\n이토록 다행스럽게 비친 그 빛줄기를 좇아, 제너럴 부인을 아는 사람들 전부의 한결같은 증언이 앞서 기록한 그 애절한 성격을 띠고 있음을 확인한 도릿 씨는, 수고를 마다하지 않고 그 시골 홀아비의 고장까지 내려가 제너럴 부인을 만나 보았는데, 그녀에게서 자신의 가장 높은 기대마저 뛰어넘는 품격을 갖춘 부인을 발견했다.\n\n\"실례가 안 된다면,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"한 가지 여쭤도—하—보수가 얼마나—\"\n\n\"실은요,\" 제너럴 부인이 그 말을 끊으며 답했다. \"그건 제가 들어가기를 꺼리는 주제랍니다. 여기 제 벗들과도 그 이야기에 들어간 적이 없거니와, 도릿 씨, 저는 늘 그 문제를 대해 온 그 섬세한 마음을 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요. 아시겠지만 저는 가정교사가 아니랍니다—\"\n\n\"아, 천만에요!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"부디, 부인, 제가 한순간이라도 그렇게 여긴다고는 생각지 말아 주십시오.\" 그는 정말로 그런 의심을 받은 것에 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n제너럴 부인은 점잖게 고개를 숙였다. \"그러니 저는, 자발적으로 베풀 수만 있다면 베푸는 것이 제게 기쁨이 되는 봉사에, 그리고 어떤 대가를 바라고서는 결코 베풀 수 없는 봉사에 값을 매길 수가 없답니다. 또한 저는 제 처지와 견줄 만한 사례를 어디서, 어떻게 찾아야 할지도 모르겠어요. 제 처지는 특이하니까요.\"\n\n물론 그렇다. 그렇다면 (도릿 씨가 자연스럽게 넌지시 비추기를) 이 문제에 어떻게 접근하면 좋겠는가?\n\n\"저는 반대하지 않겠어요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"비록 그것조차 제게는 거북한 일이긴 합니다만—도릿 씨가, 여기 제 벗들에게 은밀히 여쭈어, 그들이 분기마다 제 거래 은행의 제 계좌로 얼마를 치러 왔는지 알아보시는 것 말이에요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표했다.\n\n\"한마디 덧붙이게 해 주세요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"이 이상은 제가 결코 그 화제를 다시 꺼낼 수 없다는 것을요. 또한 저는 두 번째 자리나 그보다 못한 자리는 받아들일 수 없습니다. 만약 제가 도릿 씨 가족과 알고 지내는 영광을 제안받는다면—따님이 두 분이라고 하셨던가요?—\"\n\n\"두 딸입니다.\"\n\n\"저는 그저 완벽히 대등한 조건으로만, 동반자이자 보호자이자 멘토이자 벗으로서만 그것을 받아들일 수 있어요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 자신의 위세에 대한 자부심에도 불구하고, 그녀가 어떤 조건으로든 그것을 받아들여 준다면 그야말로 큰 호의라는 느낌이 들었다. 그는 거의 그렇게 말할 뻔했다.\n\n\"제 생각에는,\" 제너럴 부인이 거듭 말했다. \"따님이 두 분이라고 하셨지요?\"\n\n\"두 딸입니다.\" 도릿 씨가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"그러니,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"여기 제 벗들이 제 거래 은행에 치러 오던 그 금액에(그게 얼마로 드러나든) 삼분의 일을 더 보태야 할 필요가 있겠어요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 지체 없이 그 미묘한 문제를 시골 홀아비에게 알아보았고, 그가 제너럴 부인의 계좌로 해마다 삼백 파운드를 치러 왔다는 것을 알게 되자, 자신의 산수에 별 무리를 주지 않고도 자기는 사백 파운드를 치러야 한다는 결론에 이르렀다. 제너럴 부인이 어떤 값을 치러도 아깝지 않을 만큼 광택이 도는 물품이었던 까닭에, 그는 그녀를 자기 가족의 일원으로 여기는 영광과 기쁨을 누리게 해 달라고 정식으로 청을 넣었다. 제너럴 부인은 그 높은 특권을 허락했고, 그리하여 그녀가 여기 있게 된 것이다.\n\n생김새로 말하자면, 제너럴 부인은—거기에 큰 몫을 한 치맛자락까지 포함하여—위엄 있고 당당한 풍채를 지녔다. 풍성하고, 바스락거리며, 묵직하게 부피 있는 모습으로, 늘 예법 뒤편에서 꼿꼿이 서 있었다. 그녀는 알프스 꼭대기에든 헤르쿨라네움 바닥에든 데려가도—실제로 데려가진 적도 있었는데—옷의 주름 하나 흐트러지거나 핀 하나 어긋나지 않았을 것이다. 그녀의 얼굴과 머리카락이 마치 더없이 우아한 어느 방앗간에 사는 것처럼 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 듯 보였다면, 그것은 그녀가 보랏빛 분으로 안색을 손질했거나 머리가 세었기 때문이라기보다, 통째로 백악 같은 피조물이었기 때문이었다. 그녀의 눈에 아무 표정이 없었다면, 그것은 아마 표정으로 드러낼 것이 아무것도 없었기 때문이리라. 그녀에게 주름이 거의 없었다면, 그것은 그녀의 정신이 자기 이름이든 다른 어떤 글귀든 그 얼굴에 새긴 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이었다. 차갑고, 밀랍 같고, 부풀어 오른 여인, 한 번도 제대로 불이 켜진 적 없는 여인이었다.\n\n제너럴 부인에게는 의견이라는 것이 없었다. 그녀가 정신을 형성하는 방식이란 그 정신이 의견을 형성하지 못하게 막는 것이었다. 그녀에게는 작은 원형의 정신적 홈, 곧 궤도가 한 벌 있어서, 그 위로 남들의 의견을 실은 작은 열차들을 출발시켰는데, 그 열차들은 결코 서로를 따라잡지도, 어디에도 다다르지도 않았다. 그녀의 예법조차도 이 세상에 무례함이 존재한다는 것은 부정할 수 없었지만, 제너럴 부인이 그것을 처리하는 방식이란 그것을 눈에 보이지 않게 치워 버리고, 그런 것은 없는 척하는 것이었다. 이것이 그녀가 정신을 형성하는 또 다른 방식이었다—까다로운 물건이란 물건은 모조리 찬장에 쑤셔 넣고 자물쇠를 채운 뒤, 그런 것은 존재하지 않는다고 말하는 것. 그것이 가장 쉬운 방법이자, 비할 데 없이 가장 예법에 맞는 방법이었다.\n\n제너럴 부인에게는 충격적인 일이라곤 일러 줄 수 없었다. 사고나 비참한 일, 잘못된 일은 그녀 앞에서 결코 입에 올려서는 안 되었다. 격정은 제너럴 부인 앞에서는 잠들어야 했고, 피는 우유와 물로 변해야 했다. 이 모든 것을 덜어 내고 세상에 남은 그 자그마한 것에 광택을 입히는 것이 제너럴 부인의 소임이었다. 그 형성 작업에서 그녀는 가장 작은 붓을 가장 큰 통에 담갔다가, 고려 대상이 되는 모든 사물의 표면에 광택을 칠했다. 금이 가 있을수록 제너럴 부인은 더욱 그것에 광택을 입혔다.\n\n제너럴 부인의 목소리에는 광택이 있었고, 제너럴 부인의 손길에도 광택이 있었으며, 제너럴 부인의 자태 둘레에는 광택의 기운이 감돌았다. 제너럴 부인의 꿈에도—만약 꿈이라는 것을 꾸었다면—광택이 입혀져 있었어야 마땅했으리라. 깃털 같은 눈이 지붕 위로 내리는 가운데, 선량한 성 베르나르의 품에 안겨 잠들어 있는 그 꿈에 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 3. On the Road\n\n눈부신 아침 햇살이 두 눈을 어지럽혔고, 눈은 그쳤고, 안개는 사라졌으며, 산 공기가 어찌나 맑고 가벼운지 그것을 들이마시는 새삼스러운 감각이 마치 새로운 존재로 들어선 것 같았다. 그 착각을 거들기라도 하듯, 단단한 땅 자체가 사라진 듯했고, 산은—거대한 흰 무더기와 덩어리가 빛나는 황무지였는데—위로는 푸른 하늘, 저 아래로는 땅 사이를 떠도는 구름의 영역인 듯 보였다.\n\n눈 위의 거뭇한 점들이, 작은 실에 맺힌 매듭처럼, 수도원 문에서 시작되어 내리막을 따라 끊긴 토막토막으로 굽이쳐 내려갔는데, 아직 이어지지 않은 그 자국들은 여러 곳에서 수도사 형제들이 길을 트는 작업을 하고 있음을 보여 주었다. 벌써 문 주위의 눈은 다시 발에 밟혀 녹기 시작하고 있었다. 노새들이 분주히 끌려 나와 벽의 고리에 매이고 짐을 실었다. 방울 줄이 채워지고, 짐이 가지런히 매여지고, 몰이꾼과 탄 사람들의 목소리가 음악처럼 울렸다. 가장 일찍 떠나는 이들 가운데 몇몇은 이미 여정을 다시 시작하기까지 했다. 수도원 근처 검은 물가의 평평한 정상에서도, 어제 올라온 내리막길에서도, 사람과 노새의 작게 움직이는 형상들이, 주위의 광대함에 눌려 축소된 채로, 방울 소리의 맑은 딸랑임과 즐거운 말소리의 화음을 내며 나아갔다.\n\n어젯밤의 그 저녁 식사 방에서는, 지난밤 불의 깃털 같은 잿더미 위에 새로 쌓아 올린 불이, 빵 덩이와 버터와 우유로 차린 소박한 아침상을 비추었다. 그 불은 또한 도릿 일가의 안내인을 비추었는데, 그는 자기가 함께 들고 올라온 비축품—편의를 위한 든든한 짐 속에 주로 챙겨 둔 여러 자잘한 물품과 더불어—으로 일행을 위해 차를 끓이고 있었다. 가우언 씨와 파리의 블랑두아는 이미 아침을 마치고, 호숫가를 오르내리며 시가를 피우고 있었다.\n\n\"가우언이라, 응?\" 별칭이 팁이요 본명은 에드워드 도릿 도련님인 자가, 안내인이 그들에게 아침을 맡기고 자리를 뜬 뒤, 그 명부의 책장을 넘기며 중얼거렸다. \"그럼 가우언이라는 건 어느 풋내기의 이름이로군, 내가 할 말은 그게 다야! 그럴 만한 값어치가 있다면 코를 비틀어 줬을 텐데. 하지만 그럴 값어치가 없어—그자한테는 다행이지. 그자 아내는 좀 어떠냐, 에이미? 너는 알겠지. 그런 일은 대개 네가 아니까.\"\n\n\"좀 나아졌어, 에드워드. 하지만 오늘은 안 떠난대.\"\n\n\"오! 오늘은 안 떠난다고! 그 친구한테도 다행이군.\" 팁이 말했다. \"안 그랬으면 그자랑 내가 부딪칠 뻔했으니까.\"\n\n\"오늘은 가만히 누워 있는 편이 낫겠다고 여기서들 그러더라. 내일까지는 내려가는 길의 흔들림과 피로를 겪지 않는 게 좋다고.\"\n\n\"그거야 좋고말고. 그런데 너 말하는 게 꼭 네가 그 여자를 간호하기라도 한 것 같구나. 너 (제너럴 부인은 여기 안 계시니 하는 말이다만) 옛 버릇으로 도로 빠져든 건 아니지, 에이미?\"\n\n그는 패니 양을 슬쩍, 그리고 아버지까지 곁눈질로 살피며 그 질문을 던졌다.\n\n\"그저 들어가서 뭐 해 드릴 게 없냐고 물어봤을 뿐이야, 팁.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"나를 팁이라고 부를 거 없다, 에이미야.\" 그 젊은 신사가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 대꾸했다. \"그게 옛날 버릇이니까, 너도 좀 버리는 게 좋아.\"\n\n\"그럴 뜻은 아니었어, 에드워드 오빠. 깜빡했어. 한때는 너무 자연스러웠던 거라, 순간적으로 그게 맞는 말처럼 느껴졌나 봐.\"\n\n\"오, 그래!\" 패니 양이 끼어들었다. \"자연스럽고, 맞는 말이고, 한때고, 그 밖에 다 그렇지! 헛소리 마, 이 조그만 것아! 네가 왜 이 가우언 부인이라는 여자한테 그렇게 관심을 쏟는지 나는 아주 잘 알아. 나는 못 속여.\"\n\n\"속이려는 게 아니야, 패니. 화내지 마.\"\n\n\"오! 화라니!\" 그 젊은 숙녀가 몸을 홱 흔들며 대꾸했다. \"참을성이 다 떨어졌어\"(이는 사실이었다).\n\n\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. \"대체 무슨 소리냐? 분명히 말해 봐라.\"\n\n\"오! 신경 쓰지 마세요, 아빠.\" 패니 양이 대답했다. \"별일 아니에요. 에이미는 제 말을 알아들을 테니까요. 쟤는 어제 이전부터 이 가우언 부인을 알았거나, 적어도 들어서 알고 있었으니, 그랬다고 인정하는 게 좋을 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 작은딸에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"네 언니의—이—허—이 묘한 주장에 무슨—근거라도 있느냐?\"\n\n\"우리가 아무리 얌전해도,\" 에이미가 대답하기도 전에 패니 양이 끼어들었다. \"차가운 산꼭대기에서 남의 방으로 슬그머니 기어들어가, 미리 그 사람들에 대해 뭔가 알지 않고서야, 서리 속에 사람들과 함께 얼어 죽도록 앉아 있지는 않잖아요. 가우언 부인이 누구의 친구인지 짐작하기란 그리 어렵지 않죠.\"\n\n\"누구의 친구라는 거냐?\" 아버지가 물었다.\n\n\"아빠, 말씀드리기 유감스럽지만,\" 이때쯤 자기 스스로를 부추겨 몹시 학대받고 억울한 상태로 몰아넣는 데—그녀가 곧잘 큰 애를 들여 하던 일이었다—성공한 패니 양이 대답했다. \"저는 그 여자가 바로 그 아주 못마땅하고 불쾌한 인물의 친구라고 믿어요. 우리 경험으로 미루어 으레 예상할 수 있었던 모든 섬세함이 통째로 결여된 채, 우리끼리는 더는 콕 집어 언급하지 않기로 한 그 자리에서, 그토록 공공연하고 고의적인 방식으로 우리를 모욕하고 우리 감정을 짓밟은 그 사람 말이에요.\"\n\n\"에이미야,\" 도릿 씨가 점잖은 엄격함을 위엄 있는 애정으로 누그러뜨리며 말했다. \"그게 사실이냐?\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 온순하게, 네, 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 그렇다잖아요!\" 패니 양이 외쳤다. \"당연하죠! 제가 그랬잖아요! 그리고 이제, 아빠, 저는 단번에 못 박아 선언하겠어요\"—이 젊은 숙녀는 매일같이, 심지어 하루에도 몇 번씩 같은 것을 단번에 못 박아 선언하는 버릇이 있었다—\"이건 수치스러운 일이라고요! 저는 단번에 못 박아 선언해요, 이건 그만두게 해야 한다고요. 우리끼리만 아는 일들을 다 겪은 것으로도 모자라, 우리 감정을 가장 아껴 줘야 할 바로 그 사람한테서 끈질기고 조직적으로 그 일을 얼굴에 들이밀려야 하나요? 우리는 평생 매 순간 이 부자연스러운 행동에 노출되어야 하는 건가요? 우리는 영영 잊는 것조차 허락받지 못하는 건가요? 다시 말하지만, 이건 정말이지 파렴치한 일이에요!\"\n\n\"그런데, 에이미,\" 그녀의 오빠가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"내가 가능할 때면 언제나, 거의 모든 경우에 네 편을 들어 주는 건 너도 알지. 하지만 한마디 안 할 수 없구나. 정말이지, 한 사내가 다른 사내를 대할 수 있는 가장 신사답지 못한 방식으로 나를 대한 자를 네가 두둔하다니, 이건 자매로서의 애정을 보이는 방식치고는 꽤나 이해할 수 없는 거라고 나는 생각한다. 게다가 그자는,\" 그가 확신에 차 덧붙였다. \"비열한 좀도둑임에 틀림없어, 알겠니, 안 그러고서야 그런 식으로 행동했을 리가 없으니까.\"\n\n\"그리고 보세요,\" 패니 양이 말했다. \"여기에 뭐가 걸려 있는지 보시라고요! 우리가 하인들한테 존경받기를 바랄 수나 있겠어요? 절대로 못하죠. 여기 우리 하녀 둘에, 아빠 시종에, 하인에, 안내인에, 온갖 딸린 사람들이 있는데, 그런 가운데서 우리 식구 중 하나가 하인처럼 찬물 컵을 들고 이리저리 뛰어다녀야 한다니! 아니, 길거리에서 거지가 발작을 일으키면 순경이,\" 패니 양이 말했다. \"물컵을 들고 허둥지둥 뛰어다닐 수밖에 없겠지요, 어젯밤 바로 이 방에서 우리 눈앞에서 바로 이 에이미가 그랬던 것처럼요!\"\n\n\"그건 어쩌다 한 번이라면 나도 그렇게 신경 쓰지 않아.\" 에드워드 도련님이 말했다. \"하지만 자기가 클레넘이라고 부르는 게 적절하다고 여기는 그 작자는 다른 문제지.\"\n\n\"그자도 같은 일의 일부예요.\" 패니 양이 대꾸했다. \"나머지 모든 것과 한통속이죠. 그자는 애당초 우리한테 제멋대로 끼어들었어요. 우리는 그자를 전혀 원하지 않았고요. 적어도 저로 말하자면, 그자가 곁에 없어도 더없이 기꺼이 견딜 수 있다는 걸 늘 보여 줬어요. 그러더니 그자는 우리 감정을 그토록 심하게 짓밟는 짓을 저질렀죠. 우리를 망신 주는 데서 느끼는 기쁨이 아니었다면 결코 저지를 수도, 저지르려고도 하지 않았을 짓을요. 그러고 나서 우리는 그자의 친구들 시중을 들도록 깎아내려져야 한다니요! 아니, 저는 이 가우언 씨가 오빠한테 한 행동도 놀랍지 않아요. 그자가 우리의 지난 불행을 즐기며—그 순간 그걸 고소해하고 있었는데—달리 뭘 기대할 수 있었겠어요!\"\n\n\"아버지—오빠—정말 아니에요!\" 리틀 도릿이 호소했다. \"가우언 씨도 가우언 부인도 우리 이름을 들어 본 적이 없어요. 그분들은 우리 내력을 전혀, 지금도 전혀 모르세요.\"\n\n\"그러면 더 나쁘지.\" 정상참작이 될 만한 건 아무것도 인정하지 않기로 작정한 패니가 되받았다. \"그러면 너한테는 변명거리도 없는 거니까. 그분들이 우리에 대해 알았다면, 네가 그분들을 달래야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈을 수도 있겠지. 그건 나약하고 우스꽝스러운 실수겠지만, 실수라면 나도 봐줄 수 있어. 하지만 우리한테 가장 가깝고 소중해야 할 사람들을 고의로 일부러 깎아내리는 건 봐줄 수 없어. 안 돼. 그건 봐줄 수 없어. 나는 그걸 규탄할 수밖에 없어.\"\n\n\"나는 너한테 일부러 마음 상하게 한 적 없어, 패니.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"네가 나한테 그렇게 모질게 굴어도 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 좀 더 조심해야지, 에이미.\" 언니가 대꾸했다. \"네가 그런 일을 어쩌다 저지른다면, 좀 더 조심해야 한다고. 만약 내가 어쩌다 별난 곳에서, 예법에 대한 분별을 무디게 만드는 별난 처지에서 태어났다면, 나는 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 이렇게 생각하는 게 마땅하다고 여길 것 같아. '내가 무지해서, 가깝고 소중한 친척들을 곤란하게 만들고 있는 건 아닐까?' 만약 내 경우라면 나는 그렇게 할 것 같다는 거야.\"\n\n이제 도릿 씨가, 자신의 권위로 이 고통스러운 화제를 끊는 동시에 자신의 지혜로 그 교훈을 짚어 주려고 끼어들었다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그가 작은딸에게 말했다. \"부탁이니—허—더는 말하지 말아라. 네 언니 패니가 격하게 말하기는 했지만, 상당한 근거가 없는 건 아니다. 너는 이제—흠—지켜야 할 큰 지위가 있다. 그 큰 지위는 너 혼자 차지하는 게 아니라—허—나와, 그리고—허 흠—우리가 차지하고 있는 거야. 우리 말이다. 자, 높은 지위에 있는 모든 사람에게 그것이 마땅한 일이지만, 특히 이 집안에는—내가—허—굳이 자세히 말하지 않을 이유들로 해서—존경받도록 처신하는 것이 마땅하다. 존경받도록 부단히 경계하는 것 말이다. 아랫사람들이 우리를 존경하게 하려면, 그들을—허—거리를 두고—흠—눌러 두어야 한다. 눌러서. 그러니, 네가 언제든 하인들의 시중을 마다하고 그 일을 스스로 한 것처럼 보여 그들의 입길에 오르내리지 않도록 하는 것이—허—대단히 중요하다.\"\n\n\"아니, 누가 그걸 의심하겠어요?\" 패니 양이 외쳤다. \"그게 모든 것의 핵심인걸요.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 그녀의 아버지가 거창하게 대꾸했다. \"잠깐 양보해 다오, 얘야. 그러면 우리는—허—클레넘 씨 문제로 넘어가게 되는구나. 솔직히 말해, 나는, 에이미야, 클레넘 씨에 관해서는 네 언니의 감정에—그러니까 전적으로—흠—전적으로 동의하지는 않는다. 나는 그 사람을—허—대체로—품행이 단정한 사람으로 보는 것으로 만족한다. 흠. 품행이 단정한 사람으로. 또한 나는 클레넘 씨가 언제든—허—내 교제에 제멋대로 끼어들었는지 어떤지도 따지지 않겠다. 그는 내 교제가—흠—사람들이 구하는 것임을 알고 있었으니, 자기는 나를 공적인 인물로 여겼다고 변명할 수도 있겠지. 하지만 내가—허—클레넘 씨를 약간 알게 된 데에는(아주 약간이었다만) 사정들이 따라붙어 있어서,\" 여기서 도릿 씨는 지극히 진지하고 인상 깊은 태도가 되었다. \"그것은 현재의 사정 아래에서 클레넘 씨가—허—나와, 혹은 내 집안의 누구와도 교류를 다시 트려 한다면 대단히 무례한 일이 되게 만들 것이다. 만약 클레넘 씨가 그런 시도가 부적절하다는 것을 알아챌 만큼 섬세함을 갖췄다면, 책임 있는 신사로서 나는—허—그의 그 섬세함에 따라 줄 의무가 있다. 반대로 클레넘 씨가 그런 섬세함을 갖추지 못했다면, 나는 한순간도—허—그토록—흠—거친 정신과는 어떤 교류도 가질 수 없다. 어느 쪽이든, 클레넘 씨는 아예 논의 밖에 놓이는 것으로 보이며, 우리는 그와 아무 상관이 없고 그도 우리와 아무 상관이 없는 것이다. 허—제너럴 부인!\"\n\n그가 알린 그 부인이 아침 식탁의 자리를 차지하러 들어오면서 이 논의는 끝이 났다. 곧이어 안내인이 시종과 마부와 두 하녀와 네 안내꾼과 열네 마리의 노새가 채비되었다고 알렸다. 그리하여 아침을 먹던 일행은 행렬에 합류하러 수도원 문으로 나갔다.\n\n가우언 씨는 시가와 연필을 들고 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으나, 블랑두아 씨는 그 자리에서 숙녀들에게 인사를 올렸다. 그가 챙 늘어진 모자를 벗어 정중하게 리틀 도릿에게 인사할 때, 그녀는 눈 속에 거뭇하고 망토를 두른 채 서 있는 그가 지난밤 불빛 아래에서보다 한층 더 음험한 인상이라고 생각했다. 하지만 아버지도 언니도 그의 경의를 어느 정도 호의로 받아들이는지라, 그녀는 그에 대한 어떤 불신도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그것이 또다시 자신의 감옥 태생에서 비롯된 흠으로 드러날까 두려웠기 때문이다.\n\n그럼에도 험한 길을 굽이굽이 내려가는 동안, 수도원이 아직 보이는 사이에 그녀는 한 번 이상 뒤를 돌아보았고, 굴뚝에서 황금빛 막처럼 곧고 높게 피어오르는 수도원의 연기를 등진 채, 블랑두아 씨가 늘 어느 튀어나온 바위 끝에 서서 그들을 내려다보고 있는 것을 알아보았다. 그가 눈 속의 한낱 검은 막대기에 지나지 않게 된 뒤로도 한참 동안, 그녀는 여전히 그 미소를, 그 높은 코를, 그리고 코에 너무 가까이 붙은 그 두 눈을 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다. 그리고 그것마저 사라진 뒤, 수도원이 자취를 감추고 그 아래 고개를 가벼운 아침 구름이 가렸을 때에도, 길가의 섬뜩한 해골 같은 팔들이 모두 그를 가리키며 위를 향하고 있는 듯했다.\n\n어쩌면 눈보다 더 음흉하고, 가슴은 더 차갑고, 녹이기는 더 어려웠을 파리의 블랑두아는, 그들이 한결 부드러운 지역으로 내려옴에 따라 차츰 그녀의 마음에서 사라져 갔다. 다시 햇살은 따뜻했고, 빙하와 눈 동굴에서 흘러내리는 물줄기는 마시기에 상쾌했으며, 그들은 다시 소나무 숲과 바위 사이 시냇물과 푸른 언덕과 골짜기, 스위스 시골의 나무 산막과 지그재그로 거칠게 엮은 울타리 사이로 들어섰다. 때로 길이 충분히 넓어져 그녀와 아버지가 나란히 말을 탈 수 있었다. 그럴 때 그를 바라보는 것—모피와 고급 모직을 멋지게 차려입고, 부유하고, 자유롭고, 수많은 사람의 시중을 받으며, 그의 두 눈은 풍경의 영광 속을 멀리 떠돌고, 그 앞에는 시야를 어둡게 하고 그에게 그늘을 드리울 비참한 가림막 하나 없는 그 모습을 바라보는 것—만으로도 충분했다.\n\n그녀의 삼촌은 옛 그늘에서 어찌나 벗어났는지, 사람들이 주는 옷을 입었고, 집안의 체면을 위한 제물로 몸을 좀 씻기도 했으며, 데려가는 대로 따라갔는데, 어떤 참을성 있는 동물적 만족감을 보여, 마치 공기와 변화가 그에게 이롭다는 것을 드러내는 듯했다. 그 한 가지를 빼면 다른 모든 면에서 그는 자기 형에게서 비친 빛 말고는 아무런 빛도 내지 않았다. 형의 위대함, 부, 자유, 그리고 위풍당당함이 그 자신과는 아무 상관없이 그를 기쁘게 했다. 말이 없고 물러나 있는 그는, 형이 말하는 것을 들을 수 있는 한 자기가 말할 필요가 없었고, 하인들이 형에게 헌신하는 한 자기가 시중받기를 바라지도 않았다. 그 자신에게서 비롯된 유일하게 눈에 띄는 변화는 작은 조카딸을 대하는 태도의 변화였다. 날마다 그것은 점점 더 두드러진 존경으로 다듬어졌는데, 그것은 노인이 젊은이에게 좀처럼 보이지 않는 것이었고, 또 그가 거기에 부여한 그 어울림을 그가 감당해 낼 수 있으리라고는 더더욱 보기 어려운 종류의 것이었다. 패니 양이 단번에 못 박아 선언하는 그런 경우가 있을 때면, 그는 다음 기회를 잡아 작은 조카딸 앞에서 자신의 백발 머리를 드러내거나, 그녀가 말에서 내리는 것을 거들거나, 마차에 오르도록 손을 내밀거나, 그 밖의 온갖 시중을 더없이 깊은 경의를 담아 들곤 했다. 그러면서도 그것이 부적절하거나 억지스러워 보인 적은 결코 없었으니, 늘 진심으로 소박하고, 자연스럽고, 진실했기 때문이다. 또한 그는 형의 청에도 결코 응하지 않았으니, 어느 자리에서든 그녀보다 먼저 안내받거나, 어떤 일에서든 그녀를 앞서려 하지 않았다. 그녀가 존경받는 것에 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지, 바로 이번 생 베르나르 고개에서 내려오는 여정에서, 그녀가 말에서 내릴 때 마부가 가까이 서 있었으면서도 등자를 잡아 주는 데 소홀하자, 그는 별안간 격렬하게 화를 내며, 고집 센 노새를 타고 그에게 돌진하여 구석으로 몰아붙이고는 짓밟아 죽이겠다고 위협하여 수행원 전부를 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했다.\n\n그들은 훌륭한 일행이었고, 여관 주인들은 거의 그들을 떠받들었다. 그들이 어디를 가든 그들의 위세는, 귀빈실이 준비되었는지 확인하러 앞서 말을 달리는 안내인의 모습으로 그들을 앞질러 갔다. 그는 이 집안 행렬의 전령이었다. 그다음으로 큰 여행용 마차가 왔는데, 안에는 도릿 씨, 도릿 양, 에이미 도릿 양, 그리고 제너럴 부인이 탔고, 바깥에는 하인 몇 명과 (날이 좋을 때면) 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 탔으니, 마부석이 그를 위해 비워져 있었다. 그다음으로 사륜마차가 왔는데, 거기엔 프레더릭 도릿 도련님이 탔고, 비 오는 날엔 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 차지하는 빈자리가 하나 있었다. 그다음으로 짐마차가 왔는데, 거기엔 나머지 하인들과 무거운 짐, 그리고 앞서가는 다른 차량들이 남긴 진흙과 먼지를 실을 수 있는 만큼 잔뜩 실려 있었다.\n\n이 마차들이 마르티니의 호텔 마당을 장식하고 있었으니, 일가가 산행에서 돌아왔을 때였다. 다른 차량들도 거기 있었는데, 길에는 많은 일행이 오가고 있었던 까닭이다. 기운 천을 댄 이탈리아 베투라—영국 장터의 그네 좌석을 바퀴 달린 나무 쟁반에 얹고, 그 위에 바퀴 없는 또 하나의 나무 쟁반을 올려놓은 듯한—에서부터 말끔한 영국 마차에 이르기까지. 그런데 도릿 씨가 예상하지 못한 또 하나의 장식이 호텔에 있었다. 낯선 여행객 둘이 그의 방 가운데 하나를 차지하고 있었던 것이다.\n\n여관 주인은 마당에서 모자를 손에 든 채, 자기는 망했다고, 자기는 비탄에 빠졌다고, 자기는 깊이 괴롭다고, 자기는 짐승 중에서도 가장 비참하고 운 없는 놈이라고, 자기 머리는 나무로 만든 돼지 대가리라고 안내인에게 맹세했다. 결코 그 양보를 하지 말았어야 했다고 그는 말했지만, 그 아주 점잖은 부인이 단지 작은 반 시간만 그 방에서 식사하게 해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 빌었던지 자기가 그만 굴복하고 말았다는 것이었다. 그 작은 반 시간은 이미 지났고, 부인과 신사는 가벼운 후식과 커피 반 잔을 들고 있으며, 셈은 치러졌고, 말도 대기시켰으니, 곧 떠날 것이라고 했다. 그러나 불행한 운명과 하늘의 저주 탓에 그들이 아직 떠나지 않았다는 것이었다.\n\n이 사과를 들으며 계단 발치에서 돌아선 도릿 씨의 분개를 능가할 것은 없었다. 그는 집안의 위엄이 자객의 손에 찔렸다고 느꼈다. 그에게는 더없이 예민한 종류의 위엄 감각이 있었다. 그는 다른 누구도 그 사실을 알아채지 못할 때에도 그것을 노린 음모를 감지해 낼 수 있었다. 자기 위엄을 끊임없이 해부하는 데 매달려 있다고 느껴지는 수많은 정교한 메스 때문에 그의 삶은 하나의 고통이 되어 있었다.\n\n\"이게 가능한 일이오, 선생,\" 도릿 씨가 지나치게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"당신이—허—내 방 가운데 하나를 다른 사람 마음대로 쓰게 할 만큼 뻔뻔했단 말이오?\"\n\n천만번 용서를! 그 너무도 점잖은 부인에게 압도당한 것은 주인의 깊은 불운이었다. 그는 나리께서 노여워하지 마시기를 간청했다. 그는 나리의 자비에 몸을 던졌다. 나리께서 특별히 그를 위해 마련해 둔 다른 응접실을 단 오 분만이라도 너그러이 쓰셔 주신다면 만사가 잘 풀릴 것이라고 했다.\n\n\"안 되오, 선생.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"나는 어떤 응접실도 쓰지 않겠소. 나는 당신 집에서 먹지도 마시지도 않고, 발도 들이지 않고 떠나겠소. 어떻게 감히 이런 식으로 군단 말이오? 당신이—허—나를 다른 신사들과 갈라놓다니, 내가 대체 누구란 말이오?\"\n\n아아! 주인은 나리가 귀족 전체 가운데 가장 상냥하고, 가장 중요하고, 가장 존경할 만하며, 가장 영예로운 분이심을 온 우주가 증언해 달라 외쳤다. 그가 나리를 다른 이들과 갈라놓았다면, 그것은 오로지 나리가 더 뛰어나고, 더 소중하고, 더 너그럽고, 더 명망 높으시기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n\"그런 소리 마시오, 선생.\" 도릿 씨가 엄청난 열을 내며 되받았다. \"당신은 나를 모욕했소. 당신은 내게 모욕을 산더미처럼 쌓아 올렸소. 어떻게 감히? 분명히 말해 보시오.\"\n\n아, 그렇다면, 의로우신 하늘이여, 더는 설명할 것이 없을 때, 그저 사과하고 그 너무도 잘 알려진 나리의 아량에 자신을 맡길 수밖에 없을 때, 주인이 어찌 자신을 설명할 수 있단 말인가!\n\n\"내 말하건대, 선생,\" 도릿 씨가 분노로 헐떡이며 말했다. \"당신은 나를—허—다른 신사들과 갈라놓고 있소. 당신은 나와 다른 재산과 지위를 갖춘 신사들 사이에 차별을 두고 있단 말이오. 나는 당신에게 묻겠소, 왜요? 나는 알고 싶소—허—무슨 권한으로, 누구의 권한으로 그러는지. 대답하시오, 선생. 분명히 말하시오. 왜 그러는지 답하시오.\"\n\n그렇다면 주인이 안내인 나리께 황송하게 아뢰는 것을 허락해 주십사 했다. 평소 그토록 너그러우신 나리께서 까닭 없이 노여워하고 계신다고. 까닭이란 없었다. 안내인 나리께서 나리께 말씀드려 주시기를, 무슨 까닭이 있다고 의심하시는 것은 스스로를 속이시는 일이며, 까닭이란 그의 충실한 종이 이미 영광스럽게 아뢴 것뿐이라고. 그 아주 점잖은 부인이—\n\n\"닥치시오!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다. \"입 다무시오! 나는 그 아주 점잖은 부인 이야기를 더는 듣지 않겠소. 나는 당신 이야기를 더는 듣지 않겠소. 이 집안을 보시오—내 집안을—그 어떤 부인보다도 점잖은 집안이오. 당신은 이 집안을 무례하게 대했소. 당신은 이 집안에 오만하게 굴었소. 내 당신을 파멸시키겠소. 허—말을 부르고, 마차에 짐을 실으시오. 나는 다시는 이자의 집에 발을 들이지 않겠소!\"\n\n이 논쟁에 아무도 끼어들지 않았으니, 그것은 에드워드 도릿 도련님의 프랑스어 회화 실력을 넘어서는 것이었고, 숙녀들의 영역에도 거의 들지 않는 것이었다. 그러나 이제 패니 양이 몹시 신랄하게 아버지를 거들었다. 그녀는 모국어로, 이 사내의 무례함에는 분명 뭔가 특별한 것이 있으며, 이 집안과 다른 부유한 집안들 사이에 차별을 두는 그의 권한을 어떻게든 강제로 포기하게 만드는 것이 중요하다고 여긴다고 선언했다. 그의 주제넘은 짓의 이유가 무엇일지 그녀는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었지만, 이유가 있을 것이 분명하니, 그것을 그에게서 뜯어내야 한다고 했다.\n\n마당의 안내꾼들과 노새 몰이꾼들과 빈둥거리던 자들 모두가 이 성난 논쟁의 당사자가 되었고, 이제 안내인이 마차를 끌어내려 부산을 떠는 데 큰 감명을 받았다. 바퀴마다 십여 명이 달라붙어 거든 끝에, 엄청난 소음을 대가로 그 일을 해냈다. 그러고는 역참에서 말이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안 짐 싣기가 진행되었다.\n\n그런데 그 아주 점잖은 부인의 영국 마차는 이미 말이 매여져 여관 문 앞에 와 있었으므로, 주인은 자신의 딱한 처지를 호소하러 슬며시 위층으로 올라갔다. 그가 신사와 부인을 모시고 계단을 내려오면서, 도릿 씨의 상한 위엄을 의미심장한 손짓으로 그들에게 가리켜 보임으로써 이 사실이 마당에 알려졌다.\n\n\"실례합니다.\" 신사가 부인에게서 떨어져 앞으로 나서며 말했다. \"저는 말수가 적고 설명에는 서툰 사람입니다만—여기 이 부인께서 소동이 일어나지 않기를 몹시 바라십니다. 부인께서는—사실은 제 어머니이신데—소동이 없기를 바라신다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 여전히 자신의 상처에 헐떡이면서, 신사에게 인사하고 부인에게 인사했는데, 거리를 둔, 단호한, 굽힐 수 없는 태도였다.\n\n\"아니, 근데 정말로—이봐요, 영감님, 당신!\" 이것은 신사가 에드워드 도릿 도련님에게 호소하는 방식이었으니, 그는 그를 크고도 천우신조 같은 구원으로 여겨 덥석 붙들었다. \"당신하고 나하고 이걸 다 잘 풀어 봅시다. 부인께서 소동이 없기를 정말 너무도 바라시니까.\"\n\n에드워드 도릿 도련님은 단추를 잡혀 한쪽으로 약간 끌려가서는, 외교적인 표정을 지으며 대답했다. \"아니, 인정하셔야 할 게, 방을 여러 개 미리 예약해 두고 그게 자기 것인데 거기서 다른 사람들이 있는 걸 발견하면, 그게 유쾌한 일은 아니잖소.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 상대가 말했다. \"그게 유쾌하지 않다는 거 압니다. 인정해요. 그래도 당신하고 나하고 이걸 다 잘 풀어서 소동을 피해 봅시다. 잘못은 이 친구한테 있는 게 전혀 아니라 우리 어머니한테 있어요. 워낙 빼어나게 훌륭한 분이라 잘난 척하는 허튼소리라곤 없으신 데다—교육도 잘 받으셔서—이 친구가 당해낼 수가 없었던 거죠. 아주 손바닥 안에 넣고 주무르신 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런 사정이라면—\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"내 영혼을 걸고 장담하건대 정말 그런 사정입니다. 따라서,\" 상대 신사가 자신의 주된 입장으로 물러서며 말했다. \"왜 소동을 벌입니까?\"\n\n\"에드먼드,\" 부인이 문간에서 말했다. \"친절한 여관 주인에게는 잘못이 없다는 걸 이 신사와 그 가족분들이 납득하시도록 설명했거나 설명하고 있는 중이겠지?\"\n\n\"장담합니다, 어머니.\" 에드먼드가 대답했다. \"그걸 해 보느라 완전히 진이 다 빠져 버릴 지경입니다.\" 그러더니 그는 몇 초간 에드워드 도릿 도련님을 한결같이 바라보다가, 별안간 속내를 털어놓듯 덧붙였다. \"영감님! 이제 다 괜찮은 거 맞소?\"\n\n\"그래도 결국,\" 부인이 우아하게 도릿 씨 쪽으로 한두 걸음 다가서며 말했다. \"제가 직접 곧바로 말씀드리는 게 낫겠군요. 이 선량한 분께, 낯선 분의 방 가운데 하나를 그분이 안 계신 동안 식사할 만큼만(혹은 그만큼이나 짧게) 차지하는 데 따르는 모든 책임을 제가 다 지겠다고 제가 장담했답니다. 정당한 주인께서 이렇게 빨리 돌아오시리라곤 전혀 생각지 못했고, 이미 돌아오셨다는 것도 전혀 알지 못했어요. 그렇지 않았다면 부당하게 차지한 이 방을 서둘러 돌려드리고, 제 사정을 설명드리며 사과드렸을 텐데요. 이렇게 말씀드리는 것으로 저는—\"\n\n한순간 그 부인은, 한쪽 눈에 안경을 댄 채로, 두 도릿 양 앞에서 못 박힌 듯 말문이 막혀 섰다. 같은 순간, 패니 양은, 일가와 일가의 마차들과 일가의 하인들이 이룬 웅장한 회화적 구도의 전경에서, 동생을 한쪽 팔로 꽉 붙들어 그 자리에 잡아 두고는, 다른 한 팔로는 기품 있는 태도로 부채질을 하면서 부인을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 무심하게 훑어보았다.\n\n부인은—그녀는 머들 부인이었고 쉽사리 기죽는 사람이 아니었으므로—재빨리 정신을 가다듬고는, 이렇게 말함으로써 자신의 무람없음을 사과하며 이 품행 단정한 여관 주인을, 그에게 그토록 소중한 그 호의에 되돌려 놓는 바라고 덧붙였다. 도릿 씨는, 그의 위엄의 제단에 이 모든 것이 향처럼 피워 올려진 셈인지라, 너그러이 대답하고는, 자기 사람들에게—허—말을 도로 물리라 이르겠으며, 자기는—흠—처음엔 모욕으로 여겼으나 이제는 영광으로 여기게 된 일을 눈감아 주겠노라고 말했다. 이에 그 가슴이 그를 향해 굽혔다. 그 가슴의 주인은 놀라우리만치 표정을 다스리며, 두 자매에게 마음을 끄는 작별의 미소를 보냈으니, 자신이 무척 호감을 품은, 그러나 전에는 만나는 기쁨을 한 번도 누리지 못한 재산 있는 젊은 숙녀들을 대하듯 한 것이었다.\n\n그러나 스파클러 씨는 그렇지 않았다. 이 신사는 자기 모친과 같은 순간에 못 박혔다가, 도무지 다시 풀려나지를 못하고, 패니 양이 전경에 있는 그 구도 전체를 뻣뻣하게 응시하며 서 있었다. 그의 어머니가 \"에드먼드, 우리는 다 채비됐다. 팔을 내주겠니?\"라고 말하자, 그는 입술을 움직여, 그의 빛나는 재능이 가장 자주 발화되곤 하던 그 말투의 형식을 담은 무언가로 대답하는 듯했지만, 근육 하나 풀지 않았다. 그의 자세가 어찌나 고정되었던지, 안에서 모친이 알맞은 때에 당겨 주는 도움을 받지 못했더라면, 그를 마차 문에 들어갈 만큼 충분히 굽히는 것은 다소 어려운 일이었을 것이다. 그가 안에 들어서기가 무섭게 마차 뒤편 작은 창의 덧대개가 사라지고 그 자리를 그의 눈이 차지했다. 그 눈은 그토록 작은 물체가 분간될 수 있는 한 그곳에 머물렀으며, 아마 그보다 훨씬 더 오래, (마치 대구에게 뭔가 말할 수 없이 놀라운 일이 벌어지기라도 할 듯이) 커다란 로켓에 서툴게 그려 넣은 눈처럼 응시하고 있었다.\n\n이 만남은 패니 양에게 지극히 흡족했고, 그 뒤로 두고두고 의기양양하게 곱씹을 거리를 어찌나 많이 안겨 주었는지, 그녀의 가시 돋친 성미를 무척이나 누그러뜨렸다. 이튿날 행렬이 다시 움직이기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 새로운 명랑함으로 자기 자리를 차지했고, 실로 어찌나 흥이 넘쳐흐르는지 제너럴 부인이 다소 놀란 표정을 지을 정도였다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 아무 흠도 잡히지 않은 것이, 그리고 패니가 기뻐하는 것을 보는 것이 다행스러웠다. 그러나 행렬 속에서 그녀의 몫은 골똘히 생각에 잠긴, 조용한 몫이었다. 여행용 마차에서 아버지와 마주 앉아 옛 마셜시의 방을 떠올리노라면, 지금의 그녀의 존재는 한낱 꿈이었다. 그녀가 보는 모든 것이 새롭고 경이로웠지만, 그것은 현실이 아니었다. 그녀에게는 마치 그 산들과 그림 같은 시골의 환영이 언제라도 녹아 사라지고, 마차가 어느 가파른 모퉁이를 돌다가 덜컹하며 옛 마셜시 정문 앞에 와 멈출 것만 같았다.\n\n할 일이 없다는 것은 낯설었으나, 누구를 대신해 생각해 줄 사람도, 짜내고 궁리할 일도, 짊어질 남의 근심도 없는 구석으로 미끄러져 들어온 것에 비하면 그 절반도 낯설지 않았다. 그것도 낯설었지만, 자기와 아버지 사이에 어떤 틈이 생겨, 거기서 다른 이들이 그를 보살피는 일에 매달리고, 자기는 결코 거기 있으리라 기대되지 않는다는 것을 깨닫는 것은 훨씬 더 낯설었다. 처음에 이것은 산들 그 자체보다도 더 그녀의 옛 경험과 동떨어진 일이어서, 그녀는 거기에 자신을 내맡기지 못하고 그의 곁의 옛 자리를 지키려 애썼다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게 따로 이야기를 하여, 사람들이란—허—높은 지위에 있는 사람들이란, 얘야, 아랫사람들에게서 엄격하게 존경을 받아내야 하며, 그의 딸이요 도싯셔 도릿가의 유일하게 남은 가문의 에이미 도릿 양인 그녀가—흠—시종의 직무를 수행하는 일에 몸을 쓴다고—허 흠—알려진다면 그 존경과 양립할 수 없으리라고 말했다. 그러니, 얘야, 그는—허—그녀에게 부모로서 명하건대, 너는 이제—흠—온당한 자긍심을 지니고 처신해야 하고 숙녀의 신분을 지켜야 할 숙녀임을 기억하라고 했다. 따라서 그는 그녀에게—허—불쾌하고 격을 떨어뜨리는 입길을 초래할 만한 일은 삼가 달라고 당부했다. 그녀는 군말 없이 따랐다. 그리하여 이제 그녀가 그 호화로운 마차의 자기 구석에, 작고 참을성 있는 두 손을 앞에 모은 채, 그녀의 두 발이 머뭇거리고 있던 옛 삶의 마지막 발판마저 완전히 잃은 채 앉아 있게 된 것이었다.\n\n그녀가 보는 모든 것이 비현실적으로 보이는 것은 바로 이 처지에서였다. 풍경이 놀라우면 놀라울수록, 그것은 그녀가 하루 종일 텅 빈 자리를 지나며 겪는 자기 내면의 삶의 비현실성을 더욱 닮아 갔다. 심플론의 협곡, 그 어마어마한 깊이와 천둥 치는 폭포들, 그 경이로운 길, 바퀴가 빠지거나 말이 비틀거리면 곧 파멸이었을 위험한 지점들, 이탈리아로의 하강, 험한 산의 틈이 넓어지며 음울하고 어두운 감금에서 그들을 풀어 놓을 때 그 아름다운 땅이 펼쳐지던 것—이 모두가 꿈이었고—오직 옛 초라한 마셜시만이 현실이었다. 아니, 그 옛 초라한 마셜시조차도 그녀가 아버지 없이 그곳을 그려 보면 그 토대까지 흔들렸다. 죄수들이 여전히 그 비좁은 마당에서 어슬렁거리고, 그 초라한 방들이 여전히 하나하나 세 들어 있으며, 간수가 여전히 수위실에 서서 사람들을 들이고 내보내고 있다는 것을, 그 모든 것이 그녀가 잘 아는 그대로라는 것을, 그녀는 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었다.\n\n감옥에서의 아버지의 옛 삶에 대한 기억이 마치 어느 슬픈 곡조의 후렴구처럼 그녀에게 매달려 있는 채로, 리틀 도릿은 자기 출생지의 꿈에서 깨어나 하루 종일 이어지는 또 하나의 꿈으로 들어가곤 했다. 그녀가 잠에서 깨어나는 채색된 방—흔히 황폐해진 궁전의 격이 떨어진 귀빈실이었다—이 그 하루의 꿈을 시작했다. 거친 붉은빛 가을 포도 덩굴 잎이 유리창 위로 드리워지고, 창밖 금 간 흰 테라스에는 오렌지나무들이 서 있고, 아래쪽 작은 거리에는 수도사와 농부 무리가 있고, 아무리 넓고 다채롭게 펼쳐진들 그 전망의 한 뼘 땅마다 비참함과 장려함이 서로 맞붙어 씨름하며, 비참함이 운명의 힘으로 장려함을 메다꽂는 그런 방이었다. 여기에 휑한 복도들과 기둥 늘어선 회랑들의 미로가 뒤따랐는데, 아래쪽 안뜰에서는 하인들이 그날의 여정을 위해 마차와 짐을 한데 모으는 가운데 일가의 행렬이 이미 채비되고 있었다. 그다음에는 또 하나의 채색된 방—습기로 얼룩지고 황량할 만큼 큰—에서의 아침 식사, 그다음에는 출발이었는데, 이는 그녀의 소심함과 그 의식에서 자기 자리를 차지하기엔 충분히 위엄 있지 못하다는 느낌으로 인해 늘 불편한 일이었다. 출발 때면 안내인이(그 자신은 마셜시에서라면 지체 높은 외국인 신사였을 것이다) 나서서 만사가 준비되었다고 보고하고, 그다음엔 아버지의 시종이 거드름을 피우며 그에게 여행용 망토를 둘러 입혔고, 그다음엔 패니의 하녀와 그녀 자신의 하녀가(이 하녀는 리틀 도릿의 마음에 부담이었으니—처음엔 그녀를 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 몰라 정말이지 울음이 나올 지경이었다) 시중을 들었고, 그다음엔 오빠의 하인이 주인의 차림새를 완성했고, 그다음엔 아버지가 제너럴 부인에게 팔을 내밀고 삼촌이 그녀에게 팔을 내밀어, 여관 주인과 여관 하인들의 호위를 받으며 그들은 계단을 휩쓸듯 내려가곤 했다. 거기서는 군중이 모여들어 그들이 마차에 오르는 것을 구경했고, 그들은 숱한 절과 구걸과 날뛰는 말과 채찍질과 덜컹거림 한가운데서 마차에 올랐다. 그러고는 그들은 좁고 고약한 냄새가 나는 거리들을 미친 듯이 내달려, 성문 밖으로 덜컹 튕겨져 나가곤 했다.\n\n그날의 비현실적인 것들 가운데에는, 선명한 붉은 포도 덩굴이 여러 마일에 걸쳐 나무에 고리지고 화환처럼 엮인 길들이 있었고, 올리브 숲이 있었고, 산비탈의 흰 마을과 도시들이 있었으니—겉은 사랑스러웠으나 안의 더러움과 가난은 끔찍했다—길가의 십자가들이 있었고, 요정 같은 섬들이 떠 있고 화려한 색의 차양과 아름다운 모양의 돛을 단 배들이 무리 지은 짙푸른 호수들이 있었고, 먼지로 허물어져 가는 거대한 건물 더미들이 있었고, 잡초가 어찌나 억세게 자랐던지 그 줄기가 박아 넣은 쐐기처럼 아치를 쪼개고 벽을 갈라놓은 공중정원들이 있었고, 돌로 단을 쌓은 골목들에는 도마뱀이 온갖 틈으로 들고 났고, 도처에 온갖 부류의 거지들이 있었으니—가엾고, 그림 같고, 굶주리고, 명랑한, 어린 거지와 늙은 거지들이었다. 역참이나 다른 멈추는 곳에서 이 비참한 존재들은 그녀에게 그날의 유일한 현실로 보이곤 했다. 그리고 여러 번, 그들에게 주려고 가져온 돈을 다 나눠 주고 나면, 그녀는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아, 백발의 아버지를 이끄는 어느 자그마한 소녀를 깊은 생각에 잠겨 바라보곤 했으니, 마치 그 광경이 지나간 날들의 무언가를 떠올리게 하기라도 하는 듯했다.\n\n또한 그들이 화려한 방에서 한 주씩 함께 머무르며, 날마다 연회를 열고, 무수한 경이 사이로 말을 타고 나가고, 몇 마일에 걸친 궁전들을 거닐고, 큰 성당들의 어두운 구석에서 쉬는 곳들도 있었다. 거기서는 기둥과 아치 사이에서 금빛 은빛 등불들이 깜빡였고, 고해소와 포석 위 여기저기에 무릎 꿇은 형상들이 점점이 있었고, 향의 안개와 향내가 자욱했고, 그림들과 환상적인 형상들과 현란한 제단들과 거대한 높이와 거리들이 있었으니, 모두가 스테인드글라스와 문간에 드리운 묵직한 휘장을 통해 부드럽게 비추어졌다. 이런 도시들에서 그들은 다시 포도와 올리브의 길들을 따라, 더러운 마을들을 지나 나아갔으니, 그곳에는 그 추한 벽에 구멍이 나지 않은 오두막이 하나도 없었고, 온전한 한 치의 유리나 종이가 붙은 창이 하나도 없었다. 거기에는 생명을 부지할 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 듯했고, 먹을 것도, 만들 것도, 기를 것도, 바랄 것도 없는 듯했으며, 죽는 것 말고는 할 일이 아무것도 없는 듯했다.\n\n또한 그들은 궁전들로만 이루어진 도시 전체에 이르곤 했는데, 그 마땅한 거주자들은 모두 쫓겨났고, 모든 궁전이 병영으로 바뀌어 있었다. 빈둥거리는 병사 무리가 귀빈실 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 그들의 장구는 대리석 건축물에 걸려 마르고 있었으니, 그 광경은 마음에 이런 것을 떠올리게 했다—그들을 떠받치던 건물의 받침을 (다행스럽게도) 갉아먹고 있는 쥐 떼의 무리이며, 머잖아 그 건물과 더불어, 아래쪽 거리에 남겨져 파멸할 다른 병사 떼와 사제 떼와 첩자 떼—그 험상궂은 주민 전부—의 머리 위로 와르르 무너지고 말 것이라는 것을.\n\n이런 풍경들을 지나 일가의 행렬은 베네치아로 나아갔다. 그리고 여기서 그것은 한동안 흩어졌으니, 그들이 대운하변의 한 궁전(그 자체가 마셜시 전체보다 여섯 배는 컸다)에서 몇 달간 베네치아에 살기로 했기 때문이다.\n\n모든 거리가 물로 포장되어 있고, 낮과 밤의 죽음 같은 고요가 교회 종소리의 누그러진 울림과 물살의 찰랑거림과 흐르는 거리의 모퉁이를 도는 곤돌라 사공의 외침 말고는 아무 소리로도 깨어지지 않는 이 절정의 비현실 속에서, 리틀 도릿은 자기 할 일이 끝나 완전히 길을 잃은 채로 앉아 생각에 잠겼다. 일가는 흥겨운 생활을 시작하여 이곳저곳을 다니고 밤을 낮으로 삼았으나, 그녀는 그들의 흥겨움에 끼는 것이 겁이 나서, 그저 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청할 뿐이었다.\n\n이따금 그녀는, 자신의 안주인이자 몹시 모진 그 짓누르는 하녀의 시중에서 벗어날 수 있을 때면, 문 앞 채색된 기둥에 매여 늘 대기하고 있는 곤돌라 가운데 하나에 올라, 그 낯선 도시를 두루 다니곤 했다. 다른 곤돌라에 탄 사교적인 사람들은 자기들이 스쳐 지나가는, 두 손을 모은 채 배에 앉아 그토록 시름에 잠겨 의아한 듯 주위를 둘러보는 그 외로운 작은 소녀가 누구인지 서로 묻기 시작했다. 자기나 자기 행동을 누가 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 있으리라곤 결코 생각지 않은 채, 리틀 도릿은 그래도 여전히, 그 조용하고 겁먹은, 길 잃은 태도로 도시를 돌아다녔다.\n\n그러나 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 자리는 운하 위로 돌출된 자기 방의 발코니였으니, 아래로는 다른 발코니들이 있었으나 위로는 하나도 없었다. 그것은 세월에 거뭇해진 묵직한 돌로 되어 있었고, 동방에서 비롯되어 그 무수한 기이한 발상들의 집합소로 흘러든 어떤 분방한 발상으로 지어진 것이었다. 그리고 넓은 쿠션을 댄 난간에 기대어 밖을 내다보는 리틀 도릿은 실로 작디작았다. 저녁이면 그녀가 그곳의 절반만큼이라도 좋아하는 자리가 달리 없었으므로, 곧 사람들이 그녀를 눈여겨 찾기 시작했고, 지나가는 곤돌라의 수많은 눈이 위를 올려다보았으며, 많은 사람이 저기 늘 혼자인 영국 소녀의 작은 모습이 있다고 말했다.\n\n그런 사람들은 그 영국 소녀의 작은 모습에게는 현실이 아니었다. 그런 사람들은 모두 그녀에게는 낯선 이들이었다. 그녀는 해 지는 것을 바라보곤 했으니, 길고 나직한 자줏빛과 붉은빛의 띠를 이루며, 그 타오르는 홍조가 하늘 높이 솟구쳐, 건물들 위로 어찌나 이글거리며 그 구조를 환히 밝히는지, 마치 그 단단한 벽이 투명해져 안에서부터 빛나는 듯 보일 정도였다. 그녀는 그 영광들이 스러지는 것을 바라보곤 했고, 그러고는 아래쪽에서 손님들을 음악과 춤으로 실어 나르는 검은 곤돌라들을 바라본 뒤, 빛나는 별들로 눈을 들어 올리곤 했다. 옛날 어느 때엔가, 별들이 비추어 주던 그녀만의 어떤 모임도 있지 않았던가? 이제 그 옛 정문을 떠올리다니!\n\n그녀는 그 옛 정문을 떠올리고, 한밤중에 그 문가에 앉아 매기의 머리를 무릎에 누이던 자신을 떠올리고, 그 다른 시절들과 결부된 다른 장소들과 다른 광경들을 떠올리곤 했다. 그러고는 발코니에 기대어, 마치 그 모든 것이 물 아래에 잠겨 있기라도 한 듯, 물 위를 굽어보곤 했다. 거기에 이르면 그녀는 물이 흐르는 것을, 그 전체의 환영 속에서 그것이 말라 버려 다시금 감옥을, 그리고 자신을, 그 옛 방을, 그 옛 거주자들을, 그 옛 방문객들을—한 번도 변한 적 없는 그 영속하는 현실들 모두를—그녀에게 드러내 보여 주기라도 할 듯, 생각에 잠겨 지켜보곤 했다.\n\n친애하는 클레넘 씨,\n\n저는 지금 베네치아에 있는 제 방에서 당신께 편지를 씁니다. 제 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라 생각하기 때문이에요. 하지만 당신께서 제 소식을 들으시는 기쁨이, 제가 당신께 편지를 쓰는 기쁨만큼 클 수는 없으리란 걸 저는 압니다. 당신을 둘러싼 모든 것은 당신께서 늘 보아 오시던 그대로이고, 당신께선 무엇 하나 아쉬울 게 없으시니까요—혹 저를 그리워하신다면 모르겠지만, 그것도 아주 잠깐, 아주 드물게나 그러시겠지요—그런데 제 삶의 모든 것은 어찌나 낯선지, 저는 아쉬운 것이 너무도 많답니다.\n\n스위스에 있을 때—몇 주 전 일인데도 마치 몇 해 전 일처럼 느껴져요—저는 젊은 가우언 부인을 만났어요. 우리처럼 산으로 소풍을 나오셨더군요. 부인은 자기가 아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복하다고 하셨어요. 저를 통해 당신께 전갈을 보내셨는데, 당신께 다정하게 고마워하며 결코 잊지 않으시겠다고 하셨어요. 부인은 제게 무척이나 마음을 터놓으셨고, 저도 말을 나누자마자 거의 그 즉시 그분을 사랑하게 되었지요. 하지만 거기에 이상할 것은 하나도 없어요. 그토록 아름답고 사람을 사로잡는 분을 누군들 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어요! 저는 누구라도 그분을 사랑하는 것을 조금도 이상히 여길 수 없어요. 정말이지 그렇답니다.\n\n가우언 부인에 관해 이런 말씀을 드린다고 마음 불편해하지는 않으시길 바라요—당신께서 그분에게 진실한 벗으로서 관심을 두고 계신다고 말씀하셨던 것을 기억하니까요—그분이 좀 더 잘 어울리는 분과 결혼하셨더라면 좋았을 텐데 하는 생각이 들었다는 말씀을 드려도요. 가우언 씨는 부인을 아끼는 것 같고, 물론 부인도 그분을 무척 아끼시지만, 제가 보기엔 그분이 그다지 진지하지 못한 듯했어요—그 점을 두고 하는 말은 아니에요—무슨 일에서든 그렇다는 말이에요. 만약 제가 가우언 부인이라면(그게 얼마나 큰 변화일지, 또 그분처럼 되려면 제가 얼마나 달라져야 할지요!) 한결같고 목표가 굳건한 누군가가 곁에 없어서 어쩐지 외롭고 길을 잃은 듯한 기분이 들 거라는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었어요. 심지어 저는 그분도 그런 부족함을, 거의 자기도 모르는 사이에 조금은 느끼고 계신다는 생각마저 들었답니다. 하지만 이 일로 마음 불편해하지는 마세요. 그분은 '아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복'하시니까요. 그리고 더없이 아름다워 보이셨어요.\n\n머지않아 그분을 다시 만나게 되리라 기대하고 있고, 사실 며칠 전부터 이곳에서 그분을 뵙기를 기다리고 있어요. 당신을 위해서라도 저는 언제까지나 그분에게 할 수 있는 한 좋은 벗이 되어 드릴 거예요. 친애하는 클레넘 씨, 제가 다른 벗이 하나도 없을 때(지금도 다른 벗은 없어요, 새로운 벗을 사귀지 않았으니까요) 당신께서 제 벗이 되어 주셨던 일을 당신께선 대수롭지 않게 여기실지도 모르지만, 저는 그 일을 무척 소중히 여기고, 결코 잊을 수가 없답니다.\n\n저는 알고 싶어요—그래도 아무도 제게 편지를 쓰지 않는 편이 가장 좋겠지요—제 사랑하는 아버지께서 그분들을 위해 사 주신 그 가게에서 플로니시 씨 부부가 잘 꾸려 나가고 계신지, 그리고 늙은 낸디 씨가 그분들과 두 손주와 함께 행복하게 지내며 자기 노래들을 몇 번이고 거듭 부르고 계신지를요. 가엾은 우리 매기를 생각하면, 또 모두가 아무리 그 애에게 잘해 준다 해도 처음에 자기 작은 엄마 없이 그 애가 느꼈을 허전함을 생각하면, 저는 눈물이 솟는 것을 도무지 참을 수가 없어요. 그 애에게 가셔서, 제 사랑을 전하며, 엄히 비밀로 하시고서, 우리가 헤어진 것을 그 애가 아쉬워한 것 못지않게 저도 아쉬워했노라고 전해 주시겠어요? 그리고 그분들 모두에게, 제가 날마다 그분들을 생각했고, 어디에 있든 제 마음은 그분들에게 변함없다고 전해 주시겠어요? 아, 그 마음이 얼마나 한결같은지 당신께서 아신다면, 제가 이토록 멀리 떨어져, 이토록 거창하게 지내는 것을 거의 가엾게 여기실 거예요!\n\n분명 기뻐하실 거예요, 제 사랑하는 아버지께서 건강이 아주 좋으시고, 이 모든 변화가 그분께 더없이 이로우며, 당신께서 뵙곤 하시던 예전 모습과는 정말이지 아주 달라지셨다는 소식을 들으시면요. 큰아버지께도 나아진 점이 있는 것 같아요. 비록 그분은 예전에도 결코 불평하지 않으셨고, 지금도 결코 우쭐대지 않으시지만요. 패니는 무척 우아하고, 재빠르고, 영리해요. 그 애에게 귀부인이 되는 것은 자연스러운 일이고, 우리의 새로운 형편에 놀라우리만치 수월하게 적응했답니다.\n\n이 말을 하다 보니, 저는 그렇게 하지 못했고, 때로는 영영 그렇게 하지 못할 것 같아 거의 절망스러워질 때가 있다는 게 떠올라요. 저는 도무지 배울 수가 없는 것 같아요. 제너럴 부인이 늘 우리와 함께 계시고, 우리는 프랑스어로 말하고 이탈리아어로 말하며, 그분은 여러 모로 우리를 다듬으시려 애쓰세요. 우리가 프랑스어와 이탈리아어로 말한다고 했지만, 사실은 그들이 그런다는 뜻이에요. 저로 말하자면, 어찌나 더딘지 거의 나아가질 못한답니다. 제가 계획하고, 생각하고, 애써 보려 하자마자, 제 모든 계획과 생각과 애씀이 옛날 방향으로 흘러가 버려서, 다시 그날그날의 비용이며, 사랑하는 아버지며, 제 일감에 대해 걱정하기 시작하다가, 문득 화들짝 놀라 이제 그런 걱정거리는 하나도 남지 않았다는 걸 떠올려요. 그런데 그 사실 자체가 어찌나 새롭고 믿기지 않는 일인지, 저는 또 정신이 헤매기 시작한답니다. 당신이 아닌 다른 누구에게라면 이런 말을 꺼낼 용기조차 없었을 거예요.\n\n이 모든 새로운 나라들과 놀라운 풍경들에 대해서도 마찬가지예요. 그것들은 무척 아름답고 저를 놀라게 하지만, 저는 충분히 차분하지 못해서—제 말뜻을 온전히 이해하신다면, 제 자신과 충분히 친숙하지 못해서—그것들에서 누릴 수 있었을 즐거움을 다 누리지 못해요. 게다가 그것들 이전에 제가 알던 것들이 어찌나 묘하게 그것들과 뒤섞이는지요. 이를테면 산속에 있을 때, 저는 종종(이런 부질없는 것을 말씀드리기가 망설여져요, 친애하는 클레넘 씨, 당신께라도요) 저 거대한 바위 뒤에 마셜시가 있을 것만 같은, 혹은 제가 그토록 여러 날 일했고 당신을 처음 뵈었던 클레넘 부인의 방이 저 눈 너머에 있을 것만 같은 기분이 들었어요. 제가 매기와 함께 코번트 가든의 당신 숙소로 찾아갔던 그 밤을 기억하세요? 그 방을, 날이 저문 뒤 마차 창밖을 내다볼 때면, 우리 마차 곁을 따라 몇 마일이고 함께 달려오는 모습으로 제가 자주, 또 자주 떠올렸답니다. 그날 밤 우리는 문이 닫혀 들어가지 못하고, 철문 앞에 앉아 있다가 아침까지 이리저리 거닐었지요. 저는 이 방의 발코니에서조차 종종 별들을 올려다보며, 제가 다시 그 거리에 매기와 함께 갇혀 있다고 믿는답니다. 영국에 두고 온 사람들에 대해서도 마찬가지예요.\n\n이곳에서 곤돌라를 타고 다닐 때면, 저는 마치 그들을 보기를 바라기라도 하듯 다른 곤돌라들을 들여다보고 있는 제 모습에 스스로 놀라요. 그들을 본다면 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 모르겠지만, 처음 순간엔 그리 놀라지 않을 것 같아요. 공상에 잠길 때면 저는 그들이 어디에든 있을 수 있다고 상상하고, 다리 위에서나 부두에서 그 정겨운 얼굴들을 보게 될 것만 같은 기분마저 든답니다.\n\n제게 있는 또 다른 어려움은 당신께 무척 이상하게 여겨질 거예요. 저 말고는 누구에게라도 무척 이상하게 보일 것이 분명하고, 저 자신에게조차 그래요. 저는 종종 그 옛날의 슬픈 연민을 느껴요—그 말을 굳이 쓸 필요는 없겠지요—그분에 대해서요. 그분이 그토록 달라지셨고, 그 사실을 알게 된 것을 제가 언제나 이루 말할 수 없이 다행으로, 감사히 여기는데도, 그 옛날의 서글픈 연민의 감정이 이따금 어찌나 거세게 밀려오는지, 저는 그분의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 제가 얼마나 그분을 사랑하는지 말씀드리며, 그분 가슴에 안겨 조금 울고 싶어져요. 그러고 나면 저는 기쁘고, 자랑스럽고, 행복할 거예요. 하지만 저는 그래선 안 된다는 걸 알아요. 그분이 좋아하지 않으시리란 것을, 패니가 화를 내리란 것을, 제너럴 부인이 깜짝 놀라시리란 것을요. 그래서 저는 마음을 가라앉힌답니다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도, 제가 그분에게서 멀어지고 말았다는 느낌과, 그 모든 하인과 시중드는 이들에 둘러싸여 있으면서도 그분이 버림받은 채 저를 필요로 하고 계신다는 느낌과 싸운답니다.\n\n친애하는 클레넘 씨, 저는 제 자신에 관해 무척 많은 것을 썼지만, 그래도 조금 더 써야겠어요. 그러지 않으면 이 가냘픈 편지에서 무엇보다도 가장 하고 싶었던 말이 빠지고 말 테니까요. 제가 이토록 대담하게 당신께 털어놓은 이 모든 어리석은 생각들 속에는—누군가 저를 이해해 줄 수 있다면 그건 당신이시리란 걸 알고, 설령 이해하지 못하신다 해도 다른 누구보다 저를 너그러이 봐주시리란 걸 알기에 털어놓는 것이지만—이 모든 생각들 속에는, 제 기억에서 좀처럼—아니, 결코—떠나지 않는 한 가지 생각이 있어요. 그건 바로 당신께서 이따금 고요한 순간에 저를 한 번쯤 생각해 주시기를 바란다는 거예요. 이 점에 관해 말씀드려야겠어요. 저는 떠나온 이래로 줄곧, 무척이나 풀고 싶은 어떤 불안을 느껴 왔어요. 당신께서 저를 새로운 시선으로, 혹은 새로운 모습으로 생각하실까 봐 두려웠답니다. 그러지 마세요, 저는 그것을 견딜 수 없어요—그것은 당신께서 짐작하시는 것보다 저를 더 불행하게 만들 거예요. 당신께서 제게 그토록 잘해 주셨던 그때보다 저를 당신께 더 낯선 사람으로 만드는 어떤 식으로든 저를 생각하신다고 믿게 된다면, 제 가슴은 미어질 거예요. 제가 당신께 빌고 간청해야 할 것은, 부디 저를 부유한 사람의 딸로 결코 생각하지 마시고, 저를 당신께서 처음 아셨을 때보다 조금이라도 더 좋은 옷을 입거나 조금이라도 더 나은 형편으로 사는 사람으로 결코 생각하지 말아 주시라는 거예요. 부디 저를 당신께서 그토록 다정하게 보살펴 주셨던 그 작고 초라한 소녀로만, 당신께서 그 닳아빠진 옷에 비가 들이치지 않게 막아 주셨고, 그 젖은 발을 당신 난롯불에 말려 주셨던 그 소녀로만 기억해 주세요. 부디 저를(저를 생각하실 때면 언제든), 그리고 저의 진실한 애정과 헌신적인 감사를, 언제나 변함없이 이러한 사람으로 생각해 주세요—\n당신의 가엾은 아이,\n\n리틀 도릿 올림.\n\n# LITTLE DORRIT.\n\n추신.—특히 가우언 부인에 관해서는 마음 불편해하지 마셔야 한다는 걸 기억해 주세요. 그분의 말씀은 '아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복'하다는 것이었어요. 그리고 더없이 아름다워 보이셨답니다.\n\n# CHAPTER 5. Something Wrong Somewhere\n\n가족이 베네치아에 머문 지 한두 달이 지났을 무렵, 백작과 후작들 사이에 자주 어울리느라 여가가 거의 없던 도릿 씨는 어느 날 하루 가운데 한 시간을 미리 따로 떼어 두어, 제너럴 부인과 모종의 회담을 갖기로 했다.\n\n마음속에 정해 둔 시각이 다가오자, 그는 시종 팅클러 씨를 제너럴 부인의 처소로(그곳은 마셜시 면적의 삼분의 일쯤은 너끈히 차지할 만한 넓이였다) 보내, 그 부인에게 안부 인사를 전하고 면담의 영광을 청한다는 자신의 뜻을 알리게 했다. 그때는 가족의 여러 구성원이 각자의 방에서 커피를 마시는 오전 시간으로, 한때는 호화로웠으나 이제는 축축한 물안개와 깊이 자리 잡은 우수의 먹잇감이 되어 버린 빛바랜 홀에서 아침 식사를 위해 모이기 두어 시간쯤 전이었다. 그래서 제너럴 부인은 시종을 들일 수 있는 상태였다. 그 사절은 부인이 작은 사각 양탄자 위에 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그 양탄자는 돌과 대리석으로 된 바닥의 크기에 비하면 어찌나 작던지, 마치 기성품 구두 한 켤레를 신어 보려고 펼쳐 놓은 것 같기도 했고, 혹은 아라비안나이트에 나오는 세 왕자 가운데 하나가 마흔 자루의 돈을 주고 산 그 마법의 양탄자를 손에 넣어, 방금 소원을 빌어 그것을 타고 아무런 연고도 없는 궁전 같은 응접실로 옮겨져 온 것 같기도 했다.\n\n제너럴 부인은 빈 커피잔을 내려놓으며, 도릿 씨가 자신에게로 오는 수고를 덜도록(그는 신사다운 정중함으로 그렇게 하겠노라 제안했었다) 자기가 곧장 그의 처소로 가겠노라고 사절에게 답했다. 그러자 사절은 문을 활짝 열고 제너럴 부인을 그 앞으로 모셔 갔다. 제너럴 부인의 처소에서—좁은 옆골목에 가려진 그곳은, 그 골목에 낮고 음울한 다리가 걸려 있고, 맞은편엔 토굴 같은 셋집들이 늘어서 있었으며, 그 벽들은 수천 갈래의 흘러내린 얼룩과 줄무늬로 더럽혀져 있어, 마치 그 미친 듯한 틈새들 하나하나가 수백 년 동안 아드리아 해를 향해 녹물의 눈물을 흘려 온 것만 같았다—도릿 씨의 처소까지는, 신비로운 계단과 복도를 지나 제법 걸어야 하는 거리였다. 그의 처소는 영국식 집 정면을 통째로 옮겨 놓은 듯한 창이 나 있고, 푸른 하늘로 곧장 솟아오른 아름다운 교회 둥근 지붕들이 그것을 비추는 물 위에서 마주 보이며, 아래 문턱을 씻는 대운하의 잔잔한 속삭임이 들려왔고, 그곳엔 그의 곤돌라와 사공들이 작은 말뚝 숲 속에서 졸린 듯 흔들리며 그의 분부를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n도릿 씨는 화려한 실내복과 모자를 걸친 채로—그토록 오랜 세월 수감자들 사이에서 때를 기다리며 잠자던 굼벵이가 마침내 진귀한 나비로 부화한 것이었다—제너럴 부인을 맞으러 일어섰다. 제너럴 부인에게 의자를. 더 편한 의자를, 여보게. 자네 지금 뭘 하는 건가, 무얼 하고 있는 거야, 무슨 생각인가? 자, 우리만 있게 물러가게!\n\n\"제너럴 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 입을 열었다, \"외람되이—\"\n\n\"천만에요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말을 받았다. \"저는 얼마든지 응할 수 있었답니다. 커피도 이미 마셨고요.\"\n\n\"—외람되이,\" 도릿 씨가 정정 따위는 초월한 사람다운 당당한 평정으로 다시 말했다, \"당신과 잠시 사사로운 대화를 나누는 영광을 청하였습니다. 제가—허—저의 작은딸 문제로 적잖이 마음을 쓰고 있는 까닭입니다. 부인께서도 제 두 딸 사이에 기질의 큰 차이가 있음을 보셨겠지요?\"\n\n제너럴 부인이 이에 답하여, 장갑 낀 두 손을 모아 포개며(그녀는 결코 장갑을 벗는 법이 없었고, 그 장갑은 결코 구겨지지 않았으며 언제나 꼭 맞았다) 말했다. \"큰 차이가 있지요.\"\n\n\"그에 대한 부인의 견해를 들려주시기를 청해도 되겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 위엄 있는 평온함과 어긋나지 않는 정중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"패니는,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"성격에 강단이 있고 자립심이 있습니다. 에이미는 전혀 없고요.\"\n\n전혀 없다고? 오 제너럴 부인, 마셜시의 돌과 쇠창살에게 물어보시오. 오 제너럴 부인, 그 아이에게 바느질을 가르친 모자 만드는 여인에게, 그 아이의 언니에게 춤을 가르친 무용 선생에게 물어보시오. 오 제너럴 부인, 제너럴 부인, 그 아이의 아비인 나에게, 내가 그 아이에게 무엇을 빚졌는지 물어보시오. 그리고 그 어린 시절부터 줄곧 푸대접받아 온 이 작은 생명의 삶에 대한 나의 증언을 들어 보시오!\n\n그런 절절한 호소는 도릿 씨의 머릿속에 들어오지 않았다. 그는 예법이라는 마부석 위에 늘 그렇듯 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 있는 제너럴 부인을 바라보며, 사려 깊은 태도로 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀입니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"제 말을,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"패니에게 고칠 점이 전혀 없다는 뜻으로 받아들이지는 말아 주세요. 다만 그 아이에게는 다듬을 재료가 있습니다—사실, 어쩌면 조금 지나치게 많을 정도지요.\"\n\n\"부인, 조금 더—허—분명히 말씀해 주실 수 있겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"제 큰딸이—흠—재료가 지나치게 많다는 게 잘 이해되지 않는군요. 무슨 재료 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"패니는,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"지금으로서는 의견을 지나치게 많이 형성합니다. 완벽한 교양은 의견을 형성하지 않으며, 결코 감정을 드러내지 않지요.\"\n\n자기 자신이 완벽한 교양에 모자란 사람으로 비칠까 저어하여, 도릿 씨는 황급히 답했다. \"두말할 나위 없이 옳으신 말씀입니다, 부인.\" 제너럴 부인은 감정도 표정도 없는 투로 답했다. \"저도 그렇게 믿습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 부인께서도 아시다시피,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제 딸들은 아주 어린 나이에 애통하게도 그 어미를 잃는 불행을 겪었습니다. 그리고 제가 최근까지는 제 재산의 정식 상속인으로 인정받지 못한 탓에, 그 아이들은 저와 함께, 비교적 가난하나 언제나 자긍심만은 잃지 않은 신사의 곁에서, —허 흠—은거하며 살아왔지요!\"\n\n\"저는,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"그 사정을 놓치지 않고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다, \"제 딸 패니로 말하면, 지금의 지도 아래, 그리고 그토록 한결같은 본보기를 늘 곁에 두고 있으니—\"\n\n(제너럴 부인이 눈을 감았다.)\n\n\"—저는 걱정이 없습니다. 패니에게는 성격의 적응력이 있어요. 하지만 제 작은딸은, 제너럴 부인, 제 마음을 적잖이 졸이게 하고 괴롭힙니다. 말씀드려야겠습니다만, 그 아이는 늘 제가 가장 아끼는 자식이었습니다.\"\n\n\"이런 편애란,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"설명할 수가 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n\"허—그렇습니다,\" 도릿 씨가 수긍했다. \"그렇지요. 그런데 부인, 저는 에이미가 말하자면 우리네 사람들 중 하나가 아니라는 걸 알아채고 마음이 쓰입니다. 그 아이는 우리와 함께 다니는 걸 내켜 하지 않고, 우리가 여기서 어울리는 사교계에서 갈피를 못 잡습니다. 우리의 취향이 분명 그 아이의 취향이 아니에요. 다시 말해,\" 도릿 씨가 재판관 같은 근엄함으로 요약하며 말했다, \"—허—에이미에게 무언가 잘못된 점이 있다는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"혹 이렇게 짐작해 볼 수도 있지 않을까요,\" 제너럴 부인이 약간의 윤을 더해 가며 말했다, \"그 무언가가 처지의 새로움에서 비롯된 것이라고요?\"\n\n\"실례지만 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 다소 빠르게 받았다. \"한 신사의 딸이라면—그 자신이—허—한때 비교적 부유함과는 거리가 멀었다 하더라도—비교적—그리고 그 딸 또한—흠—은거하며 자랐다 하더라도, 이 처지를 그렇게까지 새롭게 느낄 필요는 없지요.\"\n\n\"옳으신 말씀입니다,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"옳으신 말씀이에요.\"\n\"그러니 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제가 외람되이\"(그는 이 표현에 힘을 주어 되풀이했는데, 다시는 반박당하지 않겠노라고 정중하나 단호하게 못 박는 듯했다) \"이 면담을 청한 것은, 이 화제를 부인께 말씀드리고, 제게 어떻게 권하실지 여쭙기 위함이었습니다.\"\n\n\"도릿 씨,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"저는 우리가 이곳에 머무는 동안 여러 차례 에이미와 대화를 나누었습니다, 거동을 갖추는 일이라는 일반적인 주제에 관하여서요. 그 아이는 제게 베네치아가 몹시 경이롭다고 토로하더군요. 저는 경이로워하지 않는 편이 낫다고 일러 주었습니다. 고전 여행기로 유명한 유스터스 씨가 이곳을 그리 높이 치지 않았으며, 리알토 다리를 웨스트민스터 다리며 블랙프라이어스 다리와 견주어 크게 깎아내렸다는 점도 지적해 주었지요. 도릿 씨께서 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 제 논변이 아직 성공을 거두지 못했다는 건 굳이 덧붙일 것도 없겠군요. 도릿 씨께서 제게 무엇을 권할지 물어 주시니 영광입니다. 제겐 늘 이렇게 여겨집니다만(이것이 근거 없는 짐작으로 드러난다면 용서해 주시기를), 도릿 씨께서는 다른 이들의 마음에 영향력을 행사하는 데 익숙하셨던 듯합니다.\"\n\n\"흠—부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"저는—허—상당한 규모의 공동체의 수장 자리에 있었습니다. 제가—영향력 있는 지위에—익숙하지 않은 사람이 아니라고 짐작하신 건 옳습니다.\"\n\n\"이렇게 확인해 주시니 기쁩니다,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다. \"그러니 저는 더욱 자신 있게 권하겠습니다, 도릿 씨께서 직접 에이미에게 말씀하시어, 당신의 의향과 바람을 그 아이에게 알리시라고요. 게다가 그 아이는 당신이 가장 아끼는 딸이고, 분명 당신을 따르고 있으니, 당신의 영향력에 더더욱 잘 따를 것입니다.\"\n\n\"저도 부인의 제안을 미리 짐작하고 있었습니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"다만—허—제가—흠—부인의 영역을 침범하는 것은 아닐지 확신이 서지 않아서—\"\n\n\"제 영역을 말씀이신가요, 도릿 씨?\" 제너럴 부인이 너그러이 말했다. \"그런 말씀은 마세요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 부인의 허락을 얻어,\" 도릿 씨가 시종을 부르려 작은 종을 울리며 다시 말했다, \"그 아이를 지금 곧 부르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"제가 남아 있기를 바라십니까, 도릿 씨?\"\n\n\"혹시 다른 약속이 없으시다면, 일이 분 정도 함께해 주셔도 괜찮으시다면—\"\n\n\"전혀 상관없습니다.\"\n\n그리하여 시종 팅클러는 에이미 아가씨의 하녀를 찾아, 그 아랫사람에게 도릿 씨가 자기 방에서 에이미 아가씨를 보고 싶어 한다고 전하라는 지시를 받았다. 이 임무를 팅클러에게 맡기면서 도릿 씨는 그를 매섭게 노려보았고, 또한 그가 문밖으로 나갈 때까지 질투 어린 눈으로 감시했는데, 혹여 그자가 가문의 위엄을 해칠 무언가를 마음에 품고 있는 건 아닌지, 심지어 이 집에 들어오기 전에 그 수감 시절의 농담 같은 것을 어디서 주워듣고는 지금 이 순간 비웃듯 그 기억을 되살리고 있는 건 아닌지 의심한 것이었다. 만약 팅클러가, 아무리 희미하고 순진하게라도 웃기라도 했다면, 도릿 씨는 죽는 날까지 그것이 사실이라고 믿어 의심치 않았을 것이다. 그러나 자기 자신에게는 매우 다행스럽게도, 팅클러는 진지하고 차분한 얼굴을 하고 있던 덕분에 그를 위협하던 그 은밀한 위기를 모면했다. 그리고 돌아왔을 때—도릿 씨가 다시 그를 노려보았을 때—마치 장례식에라도 온 듯한 투로 에이미 아가씨가 왔음을 알린 덕분에, 그는 도릿 씨의 마음속에 막연하나마, 홀어머니 밑에서 교리문답을 익히며 자란 행실 바른 젊은이라는 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"에이미,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"방금 너에 관하여 나와 제너럴 부인이 잠시 대화를 나누었다. 우리는 네가 이곳에서 좀처럼 편안해 보이지 않는다는 데 의견을 같이했단다. 허—어찌 된 일이냐?\"\n\n침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"아버지, 제겐 시간이 조금 필요할 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"파파라고 부르는 편이 더 낫단다,\" 제너럴 부인이 지적했다. \"아버지란 말은 다소 천박하지요, 얘야. 게다가 파파라는 말은 입술 모양을 예쁘게 만들어 준답니다. 파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬, 그리고 프리즘은 모두 입술에 아주 좋은 말이에요. 특히 프룬과 프리즘이 그렇지요. 거동을 갖추는 데 도움이 될 거예요, 사람들과 함께 있을 때—이를테면 방에 들어설 때—이따금 속으로 이렇게 되뇌면요. 파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬과 프리즘, 프룬과 프리즘.\"\n\n\"부디, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제너럴 부인의—흠—가르침에 따르려무나.\"\n\n가엾은 리틀 도릿은 그 탁월한 윤내기 장인을 다소 막막한 눈길로 바라보며, 노력해 보겠노라 약속했다.\n\n\"네 말이,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다, \"에이미, 시간이 필요할 것 같다고 했지. 무엇을 위한 시간 말이냐?\"\n\n또 한 번의 침묵.\n\n\"제 생활의 새로움에 익숙해질 시간이라는 뜻이었어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 아버지를 향한 다정한 눈길로 말했다. 제너럴 부인에게 순종하고 아버지를 기쁘게 하려는 마음에서, 그녀는 하마터면 아버지를 폴트리라고, 어쩌면 프룬과 프리즘이라고까지 부를 뻔했다.\n\n도릿 씨는 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 도무지 흡족하지 않은 표정이었다. \"에이미,\" 그가 답했다, \"내 말해야겠다만, 너는 그러기에 충분한 시간을 가졌던 것으로 보이는구나. 허—너는 나를 놀라게 하는구나. 너는 나를 실망시켜. 패니는 그런 사소한 어려움 따위는 다 극복했는데—흠—어찌 너는 못 한단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"머지않아 더 잘하게 되기를 바라요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그러길 바란다,\" 아버지가 답했다. \"나는—허—진심으로 그러길 바라, 에이미. 내가 너를 부른 것은—흠—분명히 말하기 위해서다, 우리 모두가 이런—허—이런 자리든 다른 어떤 자리든 너그러이 함께해 주시어 그토록 큰 은혜를 입고 있는 제너럴 부인 앞에서,\" 제너럴 부인이 눈을 감았다, \"내가—허 흠—너에게 흡족하지 않다는 것을. 너는 제너럴 부인의 노고를 보람 없게 만들고 있어. 너는—허—나를 몹시 난처하게 한다. 너는 늘(내가 제너럴 부인께 말씀드렸듯이) 내가 가장 아끼는 자식이었고, 나는 늘 너를—흠—벗이자 동반자로 여겨 왔다. 그 보답으로 내가 청하건대—나는—허—진심으로 청하건대, 네가—흠—형편에 좀 더 잘 맞추어, 네—네 신분에 걸맞은 바를 충실히 행하기를 바란다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 이 주제에 흥분한 데다 유난히 힘주어 말하고 싶은 마음에, 평소보다도 더 토막토막 끊어 말했다.\n\n\"내가 진심으로 청하건대,\" 그가 되풀이했다, \"이 일에 유념하여, 진지하게 애를 쓰고, —허—에이미 도릿 아가씨라는 네 신분에 걸맞으면서 동시에 나와 제너럴 부인을 흡족하게 할 방식으로 처신하도록 노력해 다오.\"\n\n다시 자기가 거론되자 제너럴 부인은 또 한 번 눈을 감았다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 뜨고 일어서며 이런 말을 덧붙였다.\n\n\"만약 에이미 도릿 아가씨가 표면을 갖추는 일에 스스로 주의를 기울이고 저의 변변찮은 도움을 받아 주신다면, 도릿 씨께서는 더 이상 근심하실 일이 없을 거예요. 이 기회를 빌려, 적절한 예로서 한 말씀 드리자면, 제가 아주 아끼는 어느 젊은 벗에게서 본 바와 같은 그런 주의를 기울여 부랑자들을 바라보는 것은 좀처럼 우아한 일이 못 됩니다. 그런 이들은 바라보아서는 안 됩니다. 불쾌한 것은 무엇이든 결코 바라보아서는 안 되지요. 그런 습관은 좋은 교양을 그토록 잘 드러내 주는 그 우아한 표면의 평정을 가로막을 뿐 아니라, 정신의 세련됨과도 거의 양립하기 어렵습니다. 참으로 세련된 정신은 완벽하게 적절하고 평온하며 유쾌하지 않은 것이라면 그 존재 자체를 모르는 듯 보이는 법이지요.\" 이 고상한 견해를 늘어놓은 뒤, 제너럴 부인은 위엄 있게 깊이 절하고는, 프룬과 프리즘을 머금은 듯한 입 모양을 한 채 물러났다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 말을 하든 잠자코 있든, 그 차분한 진지함과 다정한 눈빛을 잃지 않았다. 그것은 지금까지, 한순간 스쳐 간 때를 빼고는 흐려진 적이 없었다. 그러나 이제 아버지와 단둘이 남게 되자, 가볍게 맞잡고 있던 그녀의 손가락들이 떨렸고, 얼굴에는 억눌린 감정이 어렸다.\n\n자기 자신을 위해서가 아니었다. 그녀는 어쩌면 조금 상처를 받았을지도 모르나, 그녀의 근심은 자기 자신을 향한 것이 아니었다. 그녀의 생각은 늘 그래 왔듯 여전히 아버지를 향해 있었다. 부를 얻은 이후로 그녀에게 줄곧 매달려 있던 어렴풋한 불안—이제 와서도 그녀는 결코 아버지를 감옥 시절 이전의 모습으로는 볼 수 없으리라는 불안—이 차츰 그녀의 마음속에서 형태를 갖추기 시작했다. 그녀는 느꼈다, 아버지가 방금 그녀에게 한 말에서도, 그녀를 대하는 그의 태도 전체에서도, 그 익히 아는 마셜시 담장의 그림자가 드리워 있음을. 그것은 새로운 형태를 띠었으나, 그 옛날의 슬픈 그림자였다. 그녀는 슬프고도 내키지 않는 마음으로, 인간의 생애 그 어떤 세월로도 쇠창살 뒤의 그 사반세기를 이겨낼 수 없으리라는 두려움을 막아 낼 만큼 자신이 강하지 못함을 스스로 인정하기 시작했다. 그러므로 그녀에게는 아버지를 탓할 거리가 없었다. 그를 책망할 것도, 그 충실한 마음속에 어떤 감정도 없었으니, 다만 깊은 연민과 한없는 다정함뿐이었다.\n\n이것이 바로, 환한 이탈리아의 하루의 눈부신 빛 속에서, 밖에는 그 경이로운 도시가, 안에는 오래된 궁전의 화려함이 펼쳐진 가운데, 아버지가 소파에 앉아 그녀 앞에 있는 그 순간에도, 그녀가 오랜 세월 익숙했던 마셜시 거처의 우중충함 속에 있는 그를 보았던 까닭이며, 그의 곁에 다시 앉아 그를 위로하고, 그와 다시 마음을 터놓고, 그에게 다시 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶어 했던 까닭이다. 만약 그가 그녀의 마음속에 든 것을 헤아렸다 하더라도, 그 자신의 마음은 그것과 가락이 맞지 않았다. 자리에서 한동안 불편하게 들썩이더니, 그는 일어나 이리저리 거닐었고, 몹시 못마땅한 기색이었다.\n\n\"제게 더 하실 말씀이 있으신가요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"아니, 아니다. 더는 없다.\"\n\n\"제가 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리지 못해 죄송해요. 부디 지금 저를 언짢게 여기지 말아 주세요. 저는 그 어느 때보다 더 애써, 아버지의 바람대로 제 주위 것들에 저를 맞추어 보겠어요—정말이지 저는 그동안 줄곧 애써 왔답니다, 비록 실패한 줄은 저도 알지만요.\"\n\n\"에이미,\" 그가 그녀에게 홱 돌아서며 답했다. \"너는—허—번번이 나에게 상처를 주는구나.\"\n\n\"제가 상처를 드린다고요, 아버지! 제가요!\"\n\"한 가지—흠—화제가 있다,\" 도릿 씨가 방 천장을 이리저리 둘러볼 뿐, 그 가만히 귀 기울이는, 군말 없이 충격받은 얼굴은 한 번도 보지 않으며 말했다, \"괴로운 화제, 내가—허—온전히 지워 버리고 싶은 일련의 사건들 말이다. 이는 네 언니도 알고 있어서, 이미 내 앞에서 너를 타이른 바 있고, 네 오라비도 알고 있으며, —허 흠—섬세함과 감수성을 지닌 이라면 누구나 알고 있다, 너 하나만 빼고—허—유감스럽게도 너 하나만 빼고 말이다. 너는, 에이미—흠—오직 너만이, 오직 너 혼자만이, 끊임없이 그 화제를 되살린다, 말로는 아닐지언정.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다. 그뿐이었다. 그저 가만히 그를 어루만졌다. 그 떨리는 손은 어쩌면 어떤 표정을 담아 이렇게 말했는지도 모른다, \"저를 생각해 주세요, 제가 어떻게 일해 왔는지, 제 그 많은 근심들을 생각해 주세요!\" 그러나 그녀 자신은 단 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n그렇게 그에게 건넨 그 손길 속에는 그녀가 미처 예상치 못한 책망이 깃들어 있었다—예상했더라면 그녀는 손을 거두었을 것이다. 그는 발끈하여, 더듬거리며, 성난 태도로 자기를 변호하기 시작했는데, 그 손길의 의미 따위는 안중에도 없었다.\n\n\"나는 그 모든 세월 동안 거기 있었다. 나는—허—누구나 인정하는 그곳의 수장이었어. 나는—흠—내가 거기서 너를 존경받게 했다, 에이미. 나는—허 흠—내가 거기서 내 가족에게 지위를 마련해 주었어. 나는 보답을 받을 자격이 있다. 나는 보답을 요구한다. 그것을 지상에서 깨끗이 쓸어 없애고 새로 시작하자는 거다. 그게 그리 큰 요구냐? 묻겠다만, 그게 그리 큰 요구냐?\"\n\n이렇게 두서없이 떠들어 대는 동안, 그는 그녀를 한 번도 보지 않았다. 다만 허공을 향해 손짓하고, 허공에 대고 호소할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"나는 고통받았다. 아마 내가 얼마나 고통받았는지는 그 누구보다도 내가 더 잘 알 게다—허—내 말하건대 그 누구보다도! 만약 내가 그것을 제쳐 둘 수 있다면, 만약 내가 견뎌 온 것의 자국들을 지워 없애고, 세상 앞에 나설 수 있다면—허—더럽혀지지 않고 얼룩지지 않은 신사로서 말이다—그게 그리 큰 바람이냐—다시 말하건대, 그게 그리 큰 바람이냔 말이다—내 자식들이—흠—나처럼 똑같이 하여 그 저주받은 경험을 지상에서 쓸어 없애기를 바라는 것이?\"\n\n이렇게 흥분한 상태에서도, 그는 시종이 무엇이라도 엿들을까 저어하여 이 모든 외침을 조심스레 죽인 목소리로 토해 냈다.\n\n\"그래서, 다들 그렇게 한다. 네 언니도 그렇게 해. 네 오라비도 그렇게 한다. 오직 너만이, 네가 그저—흠—갓난아기였을 적부터 내가 내 인생의 벗이자 동반자로 삼아 온 내 가장 아끼는 자식인 너만이, 그렇게 하지 않아. 오직 너만이 그렇게 할 수 없다고 말하지. 나는 네가 그것을 할 수 있도록 값진 조력을 마련해 주었다. 나는 그 일을 위해 교양 있고 지체 높은 부인을—허—제너럴 부인을 네게 붙여 주었어. 내가 언짢아하는 것이 그리 놀랄 일이냐? 내가 언짢음을 드러내는 데 변명을 늘어놓아야 할 까닭이 있느냔 말이다? 없다!\"\n\n그럼에도 그는 그 달아오른 기분을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채 계속해서 자기를 변호했다.\n\n\"나는 조금이라도 언짢음을 드러내기에 앞서, 그 부인에게 확인을 구하는 데에까지 신경을 썼다. 나는—흠—그 확인을 구할 때 어쩔 수 없이 그 한계를 두었어, 그러지 않으면 나는—허—내가 지워 없애고 싶은 것을 그 부인이 읽어 낼 수 있게 만들 테니까. 내가 이기적이냐? 내가 나 자신을 위해 불평하는 것이냐? 아니다. 아니야. 무엇보다도—허 흠—너를 위해서다, 에이미.\"\n\n이 마지막 고려는, 그가 그것을 이어 가는 태도로 보아, 바로 그 순간에야 머릿속에 떠오른 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"내가 상처받았다고 했다. 정말로 그렇다. 그러니 나는—허—무슨 말로 반박하든 상처받기로 작정했어. 부의—흠—품에 안긴 내 딸이, 우울에 잠겨 물러나 앉아 제 운명을 감당할 수 없노라 공언하는 것이 나는 상처가 된다. 그 아이가—허—나머지 우리가 지워 없애려는 것을 줄기차게 되살리고, 부유하고 명망 높은 사교계를 향해, 자기가—허 흠—나 자신은 그 이름을 입에 담기조차 거부하는 어떤 곳에서 태어나고 자랐노라고—흠—거의 안달하다시피 알리려는 듯 보이는 것이 나는 상처가 돼. 그러나 거기에는 모순이 없다—허—조금도 없어, 내가 상처받았다고 느끼면서, 그러면서도 무엇보다 너를 위해 불평하는 데에는 말이다, 에이미. 그래, 다시 말하건대, 정말로 그렇다. 내가 제너럴 부인의 후원 아래 네가—흠—표면을 갖추기를 바라는 것은 너를 위해서다. 내가 네게—허—참으로 세련된 정신이 깃들기를, 그리고 (제너럴 부인의 인상적인 말씀대로) 완벽하게 적절하고 평온하며 유쾌하지 않은 것이라면 무엇이든 모르는 사람이 되기를 바라는 것은 너를 위해서야.\"\n\n이 마지막 일장 연설을 하는 동안, 그는 잘못 맞춰진 자명종처럼 띄엄띄엄 기운이 다해 가고 있었다. 그 손길은 여전히 그의 팔 위에 있었다. 그는 입을 다물었고, 잠시 다시 천장을 둘러보더니, 그녀를 내려다보았다. 그녀의 고개는 숙여져 있어 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으나, 그녀의 손길은 부드럽고 고요했으며, 그 풀 죽은 모습의 표정 속에는 책망이라곤 없었다—오직 사랑뿐이었다. 그는 흐느끼기 시작했다, 꼭 그 옛날 감옥에서 그 밤에 그랬듯이—그날 밤 그녀는 아침까지 그의 머리맡에 앉아 있었다. 그는 부유함 한가운데서 자기가 가엾은 폐허요 가엾은 비참한 인간이라고 부르짖으며, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다. \"쉿, 쉿, 내 사랑하는 아이야! 입맞춰 다오!\" 그녀가 그에게 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. 그의 눈물은 곧 말랐는데, 이전 때보다 훨씬 빨리 말랐다. 그러더니 그는 곧이어 시종에게 자못 거만하게 굴었으니, 눈물을 흘린 일에 대해 스스로를 추스르는 한 방편이었다.\n\n뒤에 제자리에서 기록될 한 가지 주목할 만한 예외를 빼면, 이것이 그의 자유롭고 부유한 생애에서 딸 에이미에게 옛 시절을 이야기한 유일한 때였다.\n\n그러나 이제 아침 식사 시간이 다가왔고, 그와 더불어 패니 아가씨가 제 처소에서, 에드워드 도련님이 제 처소에서 나타났다. 이 지체 높은 두 젊은이는 둘 다 늦은 시각까지 깨어 있던 탓에 다소 상해 있었다. 패니 아가씨로 말하면, 그녀는 자칭 '사교계 출입'이라는 것에 대한 끝없는 광증의 희생물이 되어 있어서, 기회만 마음껏 주어졌더라면 해 질 녘부터 해 뜰 녘까지 쉰 번이라도 곤두박질치듯 그 속으로 뛰어들었을 것이다. 에드워드 도련님으로 말하면, 그 역시 폭넓은 교우 관계를 지녀, 밤마다 거의 대부분의 시간 동안(주로 노름판이나 그와 비슷한 부류의 자리에서) 분주했다. 이 신사로 말하자면, 그는 운수가 바뀌었을 때 이미 가장 지체 높은 부류와 어울릴 채비가 갖추어져 있고 새로 배울 것도 거의 없다는 큰 이점을 누리고 있었으니, 그가 말 거래며 당구장 점수 기록과 친숙해지게 만들어 준 그 다행한 우연들에 그만큼 큰 빚을 진 셈이었다.\n\n아침 식사에는 프레더릭 도릿 씨도 모습을 보였다. 그 노신사는 궁전의 맨 위층에 거처하고 있었는데, 그곳에서라면 다른 거주자들에게 들킬 염려 없이 권총 사격 연습을 해도 무방했을 것이다. 그래서 작은 조카딸은 용기를 내어, 도릿 씨가 압수하라 명했으나 그녀가 감히 간직해 둔 그의 클라리넷을 그에게 돌려주자고 제안했다. 그것은 천한 악기인 데다 그 소리가 질색이라는 패니 아가씨의 몇몇 반대에도 불구하고, 그 양보는 받아들여졌다. 그러나 막상 돌려주고 보니, 이제 그것이 더는 그의 밥벌이 수단이 아니게 되자 그는 그것에 싫증이 났는지, 통 불지 않는 것이었다. 그는 어느새 손에 비틀어 만 코담배 종이를 늘 든 채로 미술관을 어슬렁어슬렁 드나드는 새 습관을 들였고(가문이 망신당하지 않도록 그에게 금 코담뱃갑을 사 주자고 제안했던 패니 아가씨로서는 분통 터지는 일이었으니, 그는 막상 그것을 사 주자 들고 다니기를 한사코 거부했다), 이름난 베네치아인들의 초상화 앞에서 몇 시간이고 보내곤 했다. 그 멍한 두 눈이 그 그림들 속에서 무엇을 보았는지는 끝내 알 수 없었으니, 그저 그림으로서 흥미를 느꼈던 것인지, 아니면 그것들을 자기 정신의 기력처럼 이미 떠나 버린 어떤 영광과 어렴풋이 동일시했던 것인지 알 길이 없었다. 그러나 그는 더없이 정확하게 그것들에게 경의를 표했고, 그 일에서 분명 즐거움을 얻었다. 처음 며칠이 지난 어느 아침, 리틀 도릿은 우연히 이 경의의 자리에 함께하게 되었다. 그것이 그의 기쁨을 어찌나 뚜렷이 돋우어 주던지, 그 뒤로 그녀는 자주 그와 동행했고, 노인이 몰락 이후로 느낄 수 있었던 가장 큰 즐거움이 바로 이 나들이에서 비롯되었으니, 그럴 때면 그는 그녀를 위해 의자를 이 그림 저 그림으로 들고 다니며, 그녀의 온갖 만류에도 아랑곳없이 그 뒤에 서서, 말없이 고귀한 베네치아인들에게 그녀를 소개하는 것이었다.\n\n마침 이 가족 아침 식사 자리에서, 그가 전날 어느 미술관에서 그들이 그레이트 세인트버나드에서 마주쳤던 그 부인과 신사를 보았던 일을 언급했다. \"이름은 잊어버렸구나,\" 그가 말했다. \"넌 기억하겠지, 윌리엄? 넌 기억하지, 에드워드?\"\n\n\"저야 잘 기억하지요,\" 후자가 말했다.\n\n\"그러시겠죠,\" 패니 아가씨가 고개를 한 번 까딱하고 동생을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"하지만 큰아버지가 그 일에 발이 걸려 넘어지지만 않으셨어도, 우리 기억 속에 다시 떠오를 일은 없었을 거예요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 참 묘한 표현이구나,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"무심코 마주치게 되었다거나 우연히 거론되었다고 하는 편이 낫지 않겠니?\"\n\n\"정말 감사합니다, 제너럴 부인,\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 답했다, \"하지만 아뇨, 그렇지 않은 것 같아요. 전반적으로 저는 제 표현이 더 좋네요.\"\n\n이것이 제너럴 부인에게서 받은 제안을 대하는 패니 아가씨의 한결같은 방식이었다. 그러나 그녀는 늘 그것을 마음에 갈무리해 두었다가 다른 때에 써먹었다.\n\n\"큰아버지가 아니더라도 제가 가우언 부부를 만난 이야기를 했을 거예요, 패니 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"그 뒤로 언니 얼굴을 거의 못 봤잖아요. 저는 아침 식사 때 그 얘기를 하려고 했어요. 가우언 부인을 찾아뵙고 더 가까이 알고 지내고 싶거든요, 아버지와 제너럴 부인께서 반대만 안 하신다면요.\"\n\n\"그래, 에이미,\" 패니가 말했다, \"네가 마침내 베네치아에서 누구든 더 가까이 알고 지내고 싶다는 바람을 입에 올리다니 정말 기쁘구나. 다만 가우언 부부가 사귈 만한 사람들인지는 두고 봐야 할 일이지만.\"\n\n\"제가 말한 건 가우언 부인이에요, 언니.\"\n\n\"물론 그렇겠지,\" 패니가 말했다. \"하지만 의회 법령이라도 통과시키지 않는 한 그 여자를 그 남편한테서 떼어 놓을 수는 없을걸.\"\n\n\"아버지는 어떻게 생각하세요,\" 리틀 도릿이 조심스럽고도 머뭇거리며 물었다, \"제가 이 방문을 하는 데 무슨 반대가 있으실까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다,\" 그가 답했다, \"나는—허—제너럴 부인의 견해는 어떠신지요?\"\n\n제너럴 부인의 견해는, 거론된 그 부인과 신사와 아무런 친분의 영광이 없는 터라, 이번 물건에는 윤을 입혀 드릴 처지가 못 된다는 것이었다. 다만 윤내기 업계에서 통하는 일반 원칙으로서, 도릿 가문처럼 사교의 전당에 그토록 두드러진 자리를 차지한 가문에, 문제의 그 부인이 어느 방면으로부터 천거받아 들어오느냐에 많은 것이 달려 있다는 점만은 짚어 둘 수 있다고 했다.\n\n이 말에 도릿 씨의 얼굴이 적잖이 어두워졌다. 그는(이 천거라는 것을, 자기가 어느 전생에서인가 어렴풋이 기억하는 클레넘이라는 이름의 주제넘은 인물과 결부시키면서) 가우언이라는 이름에 끝내 반대표를 던지려던 참이었는데, 마침 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 눈에 외알 안경을 낀 채로 대화에 끼어들었으니, 그 서두는 \"이봐—거기 자네들! 좀 나가 주겠나!\"라는 말이었다—이는 접시를 돌리고 있던 두 하인을 향한 것으로, 그들의 시중을 잠시 사양하겠다는 정중한 통고였다.\n\n그 하인들이 명에 따라 물러가자, 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"여러분 모두에게 알려 두는 게 상책일지도 모르겠는데, 이 가우언 부부는—제 자신이 그들에게, 적어도 그 신사 쪽에 그리 호감을 품고 있다고 볼 수는 없습니다만—지체 높은 사람들과 알고 지내는 사이입니다, 그게 무슨 차이라도 만든다면 말이죠.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 그 어여쁜 윤내기 장인이 말했다, \"그것은 가장 큰 차이를 만들지요. 문제의 그 인맥이 정말로 지체 높고 명망 있는 사람들이라면—\"\n\n\"그 점이라면,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말했다, \"직접 판단하실 거리를 드리지요. 혹시 그 유명한 머들이라는 이름을 알고 계십니까?\"\n\n\"위대한 머들 말씀이군요!\" 제너럴 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그 머들입니다,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말했다. \"그들은 그와 아는 사이예요. 가우언 부인이—제 말은 그 노부인, 제 정중한 친구의 어머니 말입니다만—머들 부인과 가깝고, 이 두 집안이 서로 왕래하는 사이라는 걸 제가 알고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 그보다 더 부인할 수 없는 보증은 없겠군요,\" 제너럴 부인이 도릿 씨에게 말하며, 마치 눈에 보이는 어떤 우상에게 경배라도 드리듯 장갑 낀 손을 들어 올리고 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"내—아—호기심에서, 아들에게 묻고 싶구나,\" 도릿 씨가 태도를 확연히 바꾸어 말했다, \"이—흠—때맞은 정보를 어찌 알게 되었는지 말이다.\"\n\n\"긴 얘기는 아닙니다, 아버지,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 답했다, \"당장 들려드리지요. 우선, 머들 부인은 아버지가 그 뭐라더라 하는 곳에서 담소를 나누셨던 바로 그 부인이에요.\"\n\n\"마르티니였죠,\" 패니 아가씨가 한없이 나른한 태도로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"마르티니,\" 그녀의 오라비가 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이고 슬쩍 눈을 찡긋하며 맞장구쳤다. 그것을 알아챈 패니 아가씨는 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 웃음을 터뜨리며 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"그게 어찌 그럴 수 있느냐, 에드워드?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"네가 나에게 알려 주기로는, 네가 이야기를 나눈 그 신사의 이름이—허—스파클러라 했다. 실은 네가 내게 그의 명함도 보여 주었지. 흠. 스파클러라고.\"\n\n\"틀림없습니다, 아버지. 하지만 그렇다고 그 어머니의 이름까지 같아야 하는 건 아니죠. 머들 부인은 재혼한 분이고, 그는 그 부인의 아들입니다. 그분은 지금 로마에 계세요. 아버지가 그곳에서 겨울을 나기로 정하셨으니, 거기서 아마 그분에 대해 더 알게 되겠지요. 스파클러는 막 이곳에 도착했습니다. 어젯밤을 저는 스파클러와 함께 보냈어요. 스파클러는 대체로 아주 괜찮은 친구입니다, 어떤 한 가지 주제에서는 좀 지겨운 구석이 있긴 하지만요, 어떤 젊은 아가씨한테 엄청나게 반해 버린 탓에 말이죠.\" 여기서 에드워드 도릿 도련님은 외알 안경 너머로 식탁 건너편의 패니 아가씨를 바라보았다. \"어젯밤에 우리는 마침 여행담을 주고받게 되었고, 제가 여러분께 들려드린 정보는 스파클러 본인에게서 직접 들은 거예요.\" 여기서 그는 말을 멈추었으나, 외알 안경 너머로 계속 패니 아가씨를 바라보았으니, 그의 얼굴은 한편으로는 안경을 눈에 고정해 두느라, 또 한편으로는 그 야릇하기 짝이 없는 미소 탓에, 보기 좋지 않게 잔뜩 일그러져 있었다.\n\n\"이런 사정이라면,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"내가—허—제너럴 부인의 심정도, 나 자신의 심정 못지않게 대변하는 것이라 믿는다만, 네 바람을 들어주는 데 아무 반대가 없다, 아니—허 흠—오히려 정반대다, 에이미. 나는—허—이 바람을 길조로—\" 도릿 씨가 격려하고 너그러이 용서하는 투로 말했다, \"맞이할 수 있기를 바란다. 그런 사람들과 알고 지내는 것은 지극히 옳은 일이다. 매우 마땅한 일이지. 머들 씨로 말하면 그 이름이—허—세계적인 명성을 떨치는 분이다. 머들 씨의 사업은 어마어마하지. 그 사업들이 그에게 막대한 돈을 벌어다 주어, 사람들이 그것을—흠—국익으로 여길 정도다. 머들 씨야말로 이 시대의 인물이지. 머들이라는 이름은 이 시대의 이름이야. 부디 나를 대신해 가우언 부부에게 갖출 예의는 다 갖추어라, 우리가—허—우리가 반드시 그들을 알아줄 테니.\"\n\n도릿 씨가 이토록 거창하게 인정해 주자 그 일은 결판이 났다. 큰아버지가 접시를 밀쳐 놓고 아침 식사를 잊어버린 것은 아무도 눈여겨보지 못했으나, 그는 어느 때든 리틀 도릿 말고는 누구의 눈길도 거의 받지 못하는 사람이었다. 하인들이 다시 불려 왔고, 식사는 마무리를 향해 진행되었다. 제너럴 부인이 일어나 식탁을 떴다. 리틀 도릿이 일어나 식탁을 떴다. 에드워드와 패니가 식탁을 사이에 두고 소곤대며 남아 있고, 도릿 씨가 무화과를 먹으며 프랑스 신문을 읽고 있을 때, 큰아버지가 느닷없이 의자에서 일어나 식탁을 손으로 내리치며 이렇게 말하여 세 사람의 주의를 한꺼번에 사로잡았다. \"아우야! 나는 이 일에 반대한다!\"\n\n설령 그가 알 수 없는 언어로 포고를 내리고는 곧바로 숨을 거두었다 한들, 그 자리의 청중을 이보다 더 경악시키지는 못했을 것이다. 도릿 씨의 손에서 신문이 떨어졌고, 그는 무화과를 입으로 가져가다 만 채 굳어 버려 그대로 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"아우야!\" 노인이 떨리는 목소리에 놀라운 기운을 실어 말했다, \"나는 이 일에 반대한다! 나는 너를 사랑한다. 내가 너를 끔찍이 사랑하는 줄은 너도 알 게다. 이 오랜 세월 동안 나는 단 한 번도 마음으로조차 네게 등을 돌린 적이 없어. 이렇게 약한 나라도, 너를 헐뜯는 자가 있으면 그게 누구든 언제라도 후려쳤을 게다. 하지만 아우야, 아우야, 아우야, 나는 이 일에 반대한다!\"\n\n그토록 노쇠한 사람이 어떻게 그런 진정의 분출을 해낼 수 있는지 놀라웠다. 그의 두 눈이 형형해지고, 백발이 머리 위에서 곤두서고, 이십오 년 동안 사그라들어 있던 의지의 결기가 그의 이마와 얼굴에 다시 또렷이 솟아났으며, 그 손에는 다시금 동작을 떨리게 만드는 기운이 깃들었다.\n\n\"내 사랑하는 프레더릭 형님!\" 도릿 씨가 힘없이 외쳤다. \"무엇이 잘못됐습니까? 무슨 일입니까?\"\n\n\"네가 어찌 감히,\" 노인이 패니에게로 홱 돌아서며 말했다, \"어찌 감히 그럴 수 있느냐? 너는 기억이라곤 없느냐? 너는 마음이라곤 없느냐?\"\n\n\"큰아버지?\" 패니가 겁에 질려 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다, \"어찌 이리 잔인하게 저를 몰아세우세요? 제가 무얼 했다고요?\"\n\n\"무얼 했느냐고?\" 노인이 그녀 동생의 자리를 가리키며 답했다, \"네 다정하고 더없이 소중한 벗은 어디 있느냐? 네 헌신적인 보호자는 어디 있어? 네게 어미보다 더한 그 아이는 어디 있느냔 말이다? 네 동생 하나에게 한데 모인 그 모든 됨됨이에 맞서 어찌 감히 잘난 체를 한단 말이냐? 부끄러운 줄 알아라, 이 그릇된 계집애야, 부끄러운 줄 알아!\"\n\n\"저는 에이미를 사랑해요,\" 패니 아가씨가 흐느껴 울며 외쳤다, \"제 목숨만큼이나—제 목숨보다 더요. 저는 이렇게 대접받을 까닭이 없어요. 저는 에이미에게 고마워하고, 사람이 그럴 수 있는 한껏 에이미를 아껴요. 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠어요. 이토록 부당하게 누명을 쓴 적이 없어요. 그저 제가 가문의 체면을 염려한 것뿐인데요.\"\n\n\"가문의 체면 따위는 바람에 날려 버려라!\" 노인이 크나큰 경멸과 분노로 외쳤다. \"아우야, 나는 자만심에 반대한다. 나는 배은망덕에 반대해. 나는 우리 가운데, 우리가 겪어 본 것을 겪고 우리가 본 것을 본 사람이라면 그 누구든, 에이미를 한순간이라도 불리하게 하거나 한순간의 고통이라도 치르게 하는 그 어떤 잘난 척에도 반대한다. 그런 잘난 척이 그런 효과를 낸다는 것만으로도 우리는 그것이 비열한 잘난 척임을 알 수 있어. 그것은 마땅히 우리에게 심판을 불러와야 한다. 아우야, 나는 하느님 앞에서 이 일에 반대한다!\"\n\n그의 손이 머리 위로 올라갔다가 식탁 위로 내리꽂힐 때, 그것은 대장장이의 손이라 해도 좋았을 것이다. 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그 손은 평소의 힘없는 상태로 풀어져 있었다. 그는 평소의 그 끄는 걸음으로 아우에게 다가가, 그 손을 아우의 어깨에 얹고는, 누그러진 목소리로 말했다, \"윌리엄, 아우야, 나는 이 말을 해야만 했다. 용서해 다오, 나는 이 말을 하지 않을 수 없었어!\" 그러고는 구부정한 모습으로 궁전의 홀을 나섰으니, 꼭 마셜시의 방을 나서던 그 모습 그대로였다.\n\n이 모든 동안 패니는 줄곧 흐느끼며 울고 있었고, 여전히 그러고 있었다. 에드워드는 놀라움에 입을 벌렸을 뿐 한마디도 떼지 못하고, 멍하니 바라보기만 했다. 도릿 씨 또한 완전히 당황하여, 어떤 식으로도 위신을 세우지 못했다. 이제 먼저 입을 연 것은 패니였다.\n\n\"이렇게, 이렇게, 이렇게 당해 본 적은 한 번도 없어!\" 그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"이토록 모질고 부당한 일은, 이토록 망신스럽게 난폭하고 잔인한 일은 세상에 없었어! 게다가 다정하고 착하고 얌전한 우리 에이미가, 자기가 영문도 모른 채 나를 이런 꼴을 당하게 만든 빌미였다는 걸 알게 되면 어떤 심정이겠어! 하지만 절대 그 애한테는 말 안 할 거야! 그래, 착한 우리 아가, 절대 말하지 않을 거야!\"\n\n이 말이 도릿 씨가 침묵을 깨는 데 도움이 되었다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그가 말했다, \"나는—허—네 결심에 찬성한다. 이 일을 에이미에게 말하지 않는 편이—허 흠—훨씬 나을 게다. 그건—흠—그 아이를 괴롭게 할지도 모른다. 허. 분명 그 아이를 몹시 괴롭게 할 게다. 그러지 않는 것이 사려 깊고 옳은 일이지. 우리는—허—이 일을 우리끼리만 묻어 두자꾸나.\"\n\n\"하지만 큰아버지의 그 잔인함이라니!\" 패니 아가씨가 외쳤다. \"오, 저는 큰아버지의 그 까닭 없는 잔인함을 결코 용서할 수 없어요!\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 평소답지 않게 창백한 낯빛은 여전했으나 어조만은 되찾으며 말했다, \"그런 말은 하지 말아 다오. 네 큰아버지는—허—예전의 그분이 아니심을 기억해야 한다. 네 큰아버지의 상태는—흠—우리에게 큰 인내를, 큰 인내를 요한다는 걸 명심해야 해.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 패니가 가엾게 외쳤다, \"그분 어딘가에 무언가 잘못된 데가 있다고 너그러이 여기는 수밖에 없겠어요, 안 그러고서야 세상 모든 사람 중에 하필 저를 그렇게 몰아세우셨을 리가 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 깊은 형제애 어린 어조로 답했다, \"너도 알다시피, 그 무수한 좋은 점에도 불구하고 네 큰아버지가 얼마나—흠—폐인이 되셨는지를. 그러니 내가 그분에게 품은 정으로써, 그리고 내가 그분에게 늘 보여 온 줄을 너도 아는 그 한결같음으로써 간청하건대—허—네 스스로 결론을 내리고, 형제로서의 내 심정을 배려해 다오.\"\n\n이로써 그 소동은 끝났다. 에드워드 도릿 도련님은 처음부터 끝까지 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 마지막까지 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 표정이었다. 패니 아가씨는 그날 하루 대부분을 격렬하게 동생을 끌어안기를 발작처럼 거듭하며, 또 번갈아 가며 동생에게 브로치를 안겨 주기도 하고 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다고 하기도 하며 보냈으니, 그 통에 동생의 마음속에 적잖은 애틋한 불안을 불러일으켰다.\n\n# CHAPTER 6. Something Right Somewhere\n\n헨리 가우언 씨처럼 어정쩡한 처지에 놓인다는 것 — 두 권력 가운데 하나를 넌더리를 내며 떠나 버리고, 다른 하나에서 출세하는 데 필요한 자격은 갖추지 못한 채, 어느 편에도 속하지 않은 중립지대를 침울하게 어슬렁거리며 양쪽 모두를 저주한다는 것 — 그것은 정신에 해로운 처지이며, 세월이 흐른들 나아질 가망이 없는 처지이다. 일상의 세계에서 풀리는 가장 나쁜 부류의 셈은, 남의 공적과 성공에 관해서는 언제나 뺄셈의 법칙에 매달리면서 제 것에 관해서는 결코 덧셈을 하지 않는 병든 산술가들에 의해 계산된다.\n\n또한 실망했노라는 불평 어린 자랑 속에서 일종의 보상을 구하려는 버릇은 타락이 깃든 버릇이다. 이내 게으른 무신경과 일관성에 대한 무책임함이 거기서 자라난다. 받을 자격 없는 것을 치켜세움으로써 받을 자격 있는 것을 끌어내리는 일은 그 비뚤어진 즐거움 가운데 하나다. 그리고 어떤 놀음에서든 진실을 가지고 줄타기를 하면서 그 때문에 더 나빠지지 않는 법은 없다.\n\n회화 예술의 작품들 가운데 도무지 아무런 가치도 없는 것들에 관해 의견을 밝힐 때, 가우언은 세상에서 가장 너그러운 사람이었다. 그는 어떤 작자가 (실은 아무것도 없는데도) 그 새끼손가락 하나에, 다른 어떤 작자가 (실은 많은 것을 지녔는데도) 온 정신과 온몸에 지닌 것보다 더 큰 역량을 지녔다고 단언하곤 했다. 칭찬받은 그 물건이 쓰레기라는 이의가 제기되면, 그는 자신의 예술을 변호하여 이렇게 대답하곤 했다. \"이보게, 우리가 만들어 내는 게 쓰레기 말고 뭐가 있나? 나야말로 쓰레기밖에 못 만들어 내지, 그 고백을 자네에게 선물로 주겠네.\"\n\n가난을 떠벌리는 것 또한 그 신경질적인 상태에서 비롯된 한 가지 버릇이었는데, 다만 거기에는 자신이 마땅히 부유해야 한다는 점을 드러내려는 의도가 담겨 있었는지도 모른다. 마치 자신이 그 가문에 속해 있다는 사실이 잊히지 않도록 공공연히 배너클 일가를 추켜세웠다가 깎아내리곤 했던 것처럼. 어쨌든 이 두 가지 화제는 그의 입에 무척 자주 올랐고, 그는 그것을 워낙 잘 다루어서, 몇 달 내내 줄곧 자기 자랑을 늘어놓았다 해도, 남의 호의를 받을 자격이 자기에게 있다는 점을 가볍게 깎아내림으로써 스스로를 내세운 만큼 절반도 중요한 사람으로 만들어 내지는 못했을 것이다.\n\n그가 늘어놓는 바로 이 허황된 이야기에서, 그와 그의 아내가 가는 곳마다 이내 사람들이 이해하게 되는 것이 있었다. 곧 그가 지체 높은 친척들의 뜻을 거슬러 결혼했으며, 그들이 그녀를 인정하도록 설득하느라 무진 애를 먹었다는 것이었다. 그는 결코 그런 말을 입에 올리지 않았고, 도리어 그런 생각을 비웃어 일소에 부치는 듯했다. 하지만 자신을 깎아내리려는 그 온갖 노력에도 불구하고, 그는 언제나 우월한 위치에 있게 되는 것이 사실이었다. 신혼 시절부터 미니 가우언은, 자신이 으레 어떤 남자의 아내로, 곧 그녀와 결혼함으로써 신분을 낮춘 남자이되 그녀를 향한 기사도적인 사랑으로 그 불평등을 상쇄한 남자의 아내로 여겨진다는 것을 느끼고 있었다.\n\n베네치아까지 그들은 파리의 블랑두아 씨와 동행했고, 베네치아에서 파리의 블랑두아 씨는 가우언과 무척이나 어울려 지냈다. 제네바에서 이 늠름한 신사를 처음 만났을 때, 가우언은 그를 걷어차 버릴지 부추겨 줄지 결정을 내리지 못했다. 그리고 그 점을 만족스럽게 매듭짓느라 어찌나 골머리를 앓았던지 꼬박 스물네 시간 동안 그 상태로 머물렀으며, 결국 \"뒷면이면 걷어차고, 앞면이면 부추긴다\"는 조건으로 오 프랑짜리 동전을 던져 그 신탁의 목소리에 따를까 하는 생각까지 했다. 그러나 마침 그의 아내가 그 매력적인 블랑두아를 싫어한다고 말했고, 호텔 안의 감정의 저울추도 그에게 불리하게 기울어 있었다. 그러자 가우언은 그를 부추기기로 작정했다.\n\n이 비뚤어진 처사가 너그러운 변덕에서 나온 것이 아니라면 — 실제로 그렇지 않았으므로 — 대체 무슨 까닭이었을까? 파리의 블랑두아보다 훨씬 우월하고, 그 그럴싸한 신사를 산산조각 내어 그가 무엇으로 빚어진 인간인지 능히 알아낼 수 있는 가우언이, 어째서 그런 자와 어울렸을까? 첫째로, 그는 아내에게서 처음으로 관찰된 독립된 소망에 반대했는데, 그녀의 아버지가 자신의 빚을 갚아 준 터라 일찌감치 기회를 잡아 자신의 독립성을 내세우는 것이 바람직했기 때문이다. 둘째로, 그는 그 우세한 감정에 반대했는데, 달리 될 수 있는 여러 자질을 지녔으면서도 그가 심성이 비뚤어진 사람이었기 때문이다. 그는 블랑두아처럼 세련된 예법을 갖춘 정신(廷臣)이라면 어느 교양 있는 나라에서든 마땅히 가장 높은 영예에까지 올라야 한다고 선언하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다. 그는 블랑두아를 우아함의 전형으로 떠받들고, 그를 빌미로 자기 몸가짐의 기품을 뽐내는 다른 자들을 풍자하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다. 그는 블랑두아의 인사가 완벽하다고, 블랑두아의 말솜씨가 거역할 수 없을 만큼 매혹적이라고, 그리고 블랑두아의 그림 같은 여유로움이라면 (그것이 타고난 천품이어서 값을 매길 수 없는 것이 아니라면) 십만 프랑에 사들여도 싼 값이라고 진지하게 주장했다. 본래의 가정교육이 어떠하든, 태양이 이 태양계에 속하는 것만큼이나 틀림없이 블랑두아와 그런 부류의 모든 사람에게 들러붙어 있다고 앞서 언급된 그 몸짓의 과장됨을, 가우언은 하나의 희화(戲畵)로서 기꺼이 받아들였다. 그는 그것을, 블랑두아가 도를 넘겨 하는 짓을 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 어쩔 수 없이 따라 하는 숱한 사람들을 비웃을 때 곁에 두고 쓸 수 있는, 익살스러운 밑천으로 여겼던 것이다. 이렇게 해서 그는 그와 어울리게 되었다. 그리고 이렇게 무심코 이런 성향을 습관으로 굳혀 가며 그의 이야기에서 빈둥빈둥 얼마간 재미를 끌어내다가, 그는 어느새 그를 동무로 삼는 생활로 미끄러져 들어가 있었다. 그는 그가 도박판 따위에서 잔꾀로 먹고산다고 짐작했으면서도, 자신은 대담하고 용감한 반면에 그는 겁쟁이라고 의심했으면서도, 그가 미니에게 미움받고 있음을 익히 알았으면서도, 그리고 결국 그를 워낙 대수롭지 않게 여긴 나머지 만약 그가 아내에게 그를 혐오할 만한 손에 잡히는 개인적 빌미라도 주었더라면 베네치아에서 가장 높은 창문 밖, 도시의 가장 깊은 물속으로 그를 내던지는 데 일말의 거리낌도 없었을 것이면서도, 이렇게 그와 어울렸던 것이다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 가우언 부인을 혼자 찾아뵙고 싶었다. 하지만 패니가 — 비록 그 일이 스물네 시간이나 묵은 것이긴 했어도 아직 삼촌의 항의에서 헤어나지 못한 채 — 함께 가겠다고 끈질기게 나서는 바람에, 두 자매는 도릿 씨의 창문 아래 매여 있던 곤돌라 가운데 하나에 나란히 올라타, 안내인을 거느린 채 한껏 위세를 부리며 가우언 부인의 거처로 실려 갔다. 사실 그들의 위세는 그 거처에 비해 다소 지나치게 거창했다. 패니가 투덜댄 대로 그곳은 \"끔찍하게 외진 곳\"에 있었고, 같은 숙녀가 \"한낱 도랑\"이라고 깎아내린 좁은 물길의 복잡한 미로를 지나야 다다를 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n작은 무인도 위에 선 그 집은, 어딘가 다른 곳에서 떨어져 나와 우연히 지금의 정박지로 흘러든 것처럼 보였는데, 그 잎사귀 밑에 드러누워 있는 가엾은 자들 못지않게 손질이 필요한 포도덩굴 한 그루를 동무 삼아 흘러온 듯했다. 주위 풍경의 특징을 이루는 것은 다음과 같았다. 둘레에 널빤지 가림막과 비계를 둘러친 교회 하나 — 보수 공사를 한다는 명목으로 어찌나 오래도록 방치되었는지 그 보수의 수단들이 백 년은 묵은 듯 보이고 그 자체가 도리어 썩어 들어가 있었다. 햇볕에 말리려고 널어 둔 수북한 빨래. 서로 어긋나 기괴하게 수직에서 벗어나 기울어진 집들 — 마치 기상천외한 모양으로 잘려 좀벌레가 들끓는, 아담 이전 시대의 썩은 치즈 같았다. 그리고 격자 덧창이 죄다 비뚜름하게 매달려 있고 그 대부분에서 무언가 너저분하고 더러운 것이 늘어뜨려진, 열에 들뜬 듯 어지러운 창문들이었다.\n\n그 집 이층에는 은행이 있었는데 — 인류 전체에게 통할 법규를 영국의 어느 상업 도시에서 가져온, 장사를 업으로 삼는 신사라면 누구든 놀랄 만한 경험이었다 — 그곳에서는 말라빠진 용기병(龍騎兵) 같은 비쩍 마른 점원 둘이, 금빛 술로 장식된 초록빛 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 수염을 기른 채, 작은 방 안의 작은 계산대 뒤에 서 있었다. 그 방에는 문이 열린 텅 빈 무쇠 금고 하나, 물 주전자 하나, 장미 화환 무늬 벽지 말고는 눈에 띄는 물건이라곤 없었다. 그러나 합당한 요구가 있으면 이들은 그저 손을 보이지 않는 곳에 슬쩍 담그기만 해도 오 프랑짜리 동전을 끝없이 무더기로 꺼내 놓을 수 있었다. 은행 아래에는 창살을 친 창문이 달린 서너 칸짜리 방들이 늘어서 있었는데, 마치 범죄자 쥐들을 가두는 감옥 같은 꼴이었다. 은행 위가 가우언 부인의 거처였다.\n\n벽에는 마치 지리 지식을 전하려는 선교회의 지도가 거기서 터져 나오기라도 한 듯 얼룩이 번져 있었음에도, 기괴한 가구들이 처량하게 빛바래고 곰팡내가 났음에도, 그리고 배 밑바닥 고인 물과 해초 무성한 기슭의 썰물에서 풍기는 베네치아 특유의 냄새가 무척 짙었음에도 불구하고, 그 안은 겉모습이 예고한 것보다는 나았다. 개과천선한 자객 같은 미소 띤 사내가 — 임시로 부리는 하인이었다 — 문을 열어 주며, 아름다운 영국 숙녀 두 분이 마님을 뵈러 오셨다고 알리고는 가우언 부인이 앉아 있는 방으로 그들을 안내했다.\n\n바느질에 매달려 있던 가우언 부인은 일감을 뚜껑 달린 바구니에 치워 두고 다소 황급히 일어섰다. 패니 양은 그녀에게 더없이 정중하게 굴며, 노련한 사람다운 능숙함으로 으레 하는 빈말들을 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"아빠께서는 오늘 일이 있으셔서 무척 안타까워하셨어요.\" 패니가 말을 이었다. \"(여기서 어찌나 일이 많으신지 몰라요, 아는 사람이 끔찍하게도 많은 탓이지요!) 그래서 가우언 씨께 당신의 명함을 꼭 전해 드리라고 제게 각별히 당부하셨답니다. 아빠께서 적어도 열두 번은 다짐을 두신 그 심부름을 틀림없이 해내려면, 어디 양심의 짐을 덜 겸 지금 당장 이 탁자 위에 놓아 두는 걸 허락해 주세요.\"\n\n그러고는 노련한 사람다운 여유로움으로 그렇게 했다.\n\n\"저희는,\" 패니가 말했다. \"댁에서 머들 부부와 아는 사이라는 걸 알고 무척 반가웠어요. 그게 저희를 또 한번 가깝게 이어 줄 인연이 되었으면 해요.\"\n\n\"그분들은,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"가우언 씨 집안의 친구들이세요. 저는 아직 머들 부인과 직접 인사를 나누는 기쁨은 누리지 못했지만, 로마에 가면 그분께 소개받게 될 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그러세요?\" 패니가, 자기 쪽의 우월함을 상냥하게 누그러뜨리는 듯한 기색으로 대답했다. \"마음에 드실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그분을 아주 잘 아시나요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 그러니까,\" 패니가 예쁜 어깨를 솔직하게 한번 으쓱이며 말했다. \"런던에서는 누구나 다 알고 지내거든요. 저희는 이리로 오는 길에 그분을 만났는데, 솔직히 말하자면 아빠께서는 처음엔 그분이 저희 일행이 잡아 둔 방 하나를 차지하셨다고 좀 언짢아하셨어요. 하지만 물론 그것도 곧 풀려서, 다시 다들 좋은 친구가 되었지요.\"\n\n이 방문이 아직 리틀 도릿에게 가우언 부인과 이야기를 나눌 기회를 주지는 못했으나, 두 사람 사이에는 말없는 이해가 흘렀고, 그것으로도 충분했다. 리틀 도릿은 날카롭고도 식지 않는 관심으로 가우언 부인을 바라보았다. 그녀의 목소리는 리틀 도릿의 가슴을 떨리게 했다. 그녀 곁에 있는 것, 그녀와 관련된 것, 그녀에게 조금이라도 얽힌 것은 무엇 하나 리틀 도릿의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다. 그녀는 다른 어떤 경우에서보다 — 단 하나의 경우만을 빼고는 — 여기서 가장 사소한 일까지도 더 재빨리 알아챘다.\n\n\"그날 밤 이후로 줄곧 잘 지내셨나요?\" 이제 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"아주 잘 지냈어요, 얘. 당신은요?\"\n\n\"아! 저는 늘 건강해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 수줍게 말했다. \"저는 — 네, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n그녀가 말을 더듬다 끊은 데에는, 가우언 부인이 말을 건네며 그녀의 손을 스쳤고 두 사람의 눈길이 마주쳤다는 것 말고는 아무 까닭도 없었다. 그 크고 부드러운 눈동자에 어린 무언가 생각에 잠긴 듯한 불안이 리틀 도릿을 한순간에 멈칫하게 했던 것이다.\n\n\"당신이 제 남편이 아끼는 사람이라는 걸, 그래서 제가 당신을 거의 질투하지 않을 수 없다는 걸 모르시죠?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"그이가 저한테 하는 말을 당신한테도 한다면, 당신더러 자기가 여태껏 본 누구보다도 차분하고 임기응변에 빠른 사람이라고 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"제 칭찬을 너무 지나치게 하시네요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"그건 잘 모르겠지만, 당신이 여기 와 있다고 그이에게 알려야 한다는 건 조금도 의심하지 않아요. 그러지 않고 당신과 — 도릿 양을 — 그냥 보내 드린다면 결코 용서받지 못할 거예요. 그래도 될까요? 화가의 화실이 어지럽고 누추하더라도 양해해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n이 물음들은 패니 양을 향한 것이었는데, 패니는 무엇보다도 흥미롭고 황홀할 거라고 우아하게 대답했다. 가우언 부인은 어느 문으로 가서 그 너머를 들여다보고는 돌아왔다. \"헨리한테 들어와 달라고 청해도 되겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그이가 좋아할 줄 알았어요!\"\n\n먼저 들어선 리틀 도릿과 맨 처음 맞닥뜨린 것은, 커다란 망토를 두르고 음험하게 푹 눌러쓴 모자를 쓴 채 한구석의 높은 단(壇) 위에 서 있는 파리의 블랑두아였다. 그것은 그가 그랑 생베르나르에서, 경고하듯 뻗은 표지판의 팔들이 죄다 그를 가리키는 듯하던 그곳에 서 있던 모습 그대로였다. 그 형상이 그녀를 향해 미소를 짓자, 그녀는 흠칫 물러섰다.\n\n\"놀라지 마십시오.\" 문 뒤 화가(畵架)에서 나오며 가우언이 말했다. \"블랑두아일 뿐입니다. 오늘은 모델 노릇을 하고 있어요. 그를 습작하는 중이지요. 그를 어디든 써먹으면 돈이 절약되니까요. 우리 가난한 화가들은 남아도는 돈이 없거든요.\"\n\n파리의 블랑두아는 푹 눌러쓴 모자를 벗어, 제 구석에서 나오지는 않은 채 숙녀들에게 인사했다.\n\n\"천만번 용서를 빕니다!\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 여기 이 화백님께서 어찌나 저를 가차 없이 다루시는지, 움직이기가 겁이 나서요.\"\n\n\"그럼 움직이지 마.\" 자매가 화가에게 다가서자 가우언이 태연하게 말했다. \"이 숙녀분들께서 적어도 이 졸작의 원본은 보셔야 무엇을 그린 건지 아실 게 아닌가. 자, 보십시오, 저기 그가 서 있습니다. 제물을 노리는 자객, 조국을 구하려고 기다리는 고매한 귀족, 누군가에게 못된 짓을 하려고 도사린 만인의 적, 누군가에게 좋은 일을 베풀려고 기다리는 천사의 사자(使者) — 무엇이든 여러분이 보시기에 그와 가장 닮았다 싶은 것이지요!\"\n\n\"이렇게 말씀하시지요, 화백님, 우아함과 아름다움에 경의를 표하려고 기다리는 가난한 신사라고요.\" 블랑두아가 한마디 했다.\n\n\"아니면 이렇게 말하든가, 못된 인간 양반,\" 실제 얼굴이 움직인 부분에 붓을 대며 가우언이 응수했다. \"범행을 저지른 뒤의 살인자라고. 그 흰 손을 보여 봐, 블랑두아. 망토 밖으로 내놔. 가만히 둬.\"\n\n블랑두아의 손은 떨렸다. 하지만 그가 웃었으니, 그 손이 떨리는 것도 당연했다.\n\n\"이자는 예전에 다른 살인자, 혹은 어떤 희생자와 한바탕 격투를 벌인 적이 있는 모양입니다.\" 빠르고 성마르며 서투른 붓질로 손의 흔적을 그려 넣으며 가우언이 말했다. \"이게 그 자국들이지요. 망토 밖으로 내라니까, 이 사람아! — 성 마르코의 몸에 맹세코, 대체 무슨 생각을 하는 거야?\"\n\n파리의 블랑두아가 다시 웃음으로 몸을 흔들어 대는 바람에 그의 손은 더욱 떨렸다. 이번에는 그 손을 들어 올려, 축축해 보이는 콧수염을 비비 꼬았다. 그러고는 새삼스레 조금 거들먹거리며 요구받은 자세로 섰다.\n\n그의 얼굴은 리틀 도릿이 화가 곁에 서 있는 자리를 향하도록 놓여 있어, 그는 내내 그녀를 바라보았다. 한번 그의 기묘한 두 눈에 사로잡히자 그녀는 자신의 눈을 거둘 수가 없었고, 그래서 두 사람은 줄곧 서로를 마주 보고 있었다. 그녀가 이제 몸을 떨었다. 가우언은 그것을 느끼고는, 그녀가 손으로 머리를 쓰다듬어 주고 있던, 곁에 있던 커다란 개가 방금 낮게 으르렁댄 탓에 그녀가 놀란 줄로 짐작하고, 그녀를 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"이 녀석은 해치지 않습니다, 도릿 양.\"\n\n\"이 개가 무섭진 않아요.\" 그녀가 같은 숨결로 대답했다. \"그런데 이 개를 좀 봐 주시겠어요?\"\n\n순식간에 가우언은 붓을 내던지고 두 손으로 개의 목걸이를 움켜잡았다.\n\n\"블랑두아! 어쩌자고 이 녀석을 약 올리는 그런 바보짓을 하나! 맹세코, 그리고 지옥에 두고서도 맹세코, 이 녀석이 자넬 갈가리 찢어 놓을 걸세! 엎드려! 라이언! 내 목소리가 안 들리느냐, 이 반항하는 놈아!\"\n\n그 커다란 개는 목걸이에 반쯤 목이 졸리는 것도 아랑곳없이, 죽은 듯한 제 무게를 실어 주인에게 한사코 버티며, 기어이 방을 가로질러 가겠다는 듯이 끌어 댔다. 주인이 붙잡은 그 순간에 녀석은 뛰어오르려고 잔뜩 웅크리고 있던 참이었다.\n\n\"라이언! 라이언!\" 녀석이 뒷다리로 곧추서니, 주인과 개의 씨름이 벌어졌다. \"물러서! 엎드려, 라이언! 이 녀석 눈에 안 띄게 비키게, 블랑두아! 대체 이 개한테 무슨 악마를 불러들인 건가?\"\n\n\"난 녀석한테 아무 짓도 안 했소.\"\n\n\"이 녀석 눈에 안 띄게 비키게, 안 그러면 이 사나운 짐승을 붙들 수가 없네! 방에서 나가게! 내 영혼에 걸고, 녀석이 자넬 죽이고 말 걸세!\"\n\n블랑두아가 사라지자 개는 사나운 짖음과 함께 다시 한 번 발버둥을 쳤다. 그러다 개가 굴복하는 순간, 개 못지않게 화가 난 주인은 머리를 한 대 후려쳐 녀석을 쓰러뜨리고는, 그 위에 우뚝 서서 부츠 뒤축으로 여러 차례 모질게 내리쳤다. 그래서 이내 녀석의 주둥이에서 피가 났다.\n\n\"자, 저 구석으로 가서 엎드려 있어.\" 가우언이 말했다. \"안 그러면 끌고 나가 쏘아 죽일 테다.\"\n\n라이언은 명령대로 따라, 엎드려서 제 주둥이와 가슴을 핥았다. 라이언의 주인은 잠시 숨을 고르려고 멈추었다가, 평소의 냉정한 태도를 되찾고는, 겁에 질린 아내와 그 방문객들에게 말을 건네려 돌아섰다. 아마 이 모든 소동은 채 이 분도 걸리지 않았을 것이다.\n\n\"자자, 미니! 이 녀석이 언제나 온순하고 말 잘 듣는다는 걸 당신도 알잖소. 블랑두아가 녀석을 자극한 게 분명해 — 얼굴을 찡그려 약을 올렸겠지. 이 개도 좋고 싫음이 있는데, 블랑두아는 녀석이 그리 좋아하는 상대가 아니거든. 하지만 미니, 당신은 분명 이 녀석이 전엔 한 번도 이런 적이 없었다고 증언해 줄 거요.\"\n\n미니는 너무 동요한 나머지 조리 있는 대답을 하지 못했다. 리틀 도릿은 벌써 그녀를 달래는 데 마음을 쏟고 있었다. 두세 번 비명을 질렀던 패니는 보호를 받으려는 듯 가우언의 팔을 붙들고 있었다. 라이언은 이런 소동을 일으킨 것이 몹시 부끄럽다는 듯 몸을 바닥에 질질 끌며 여주인의 발치로 다가왔다.\n\n\"이 사나운 짐승 같으니.\" 가우언이 다시 발로 녀석을 차며 말했다. \"이 일을 두고 벌을 받아야 해.\" 그러고는 또 한 번, 그리고 또 한 번 녀석을 찼다.\n\n\"오, 제발 더는 벌하지 마세요.\" 리틀 도릿이 외쳤다. \"이 녀석을 아프게 하지 마세요. 보세요, 얼마나 얌전한지!\" 그녀의 간청에 가우언은 녀석을 봐주었다. 그리고 녀석은 그녀의 두둔을 받을 만했으니, 정말이지 개가 그럴 수 있는 한 더없이 순종적이고, 더없이 미안해하며, 더없이 비참한 꼴이었다.\n\n설령 패니가 아무리 좋은 상황에서라도 손톱만큼도 거치적거리지 않았다 한들, 이 충격에서 벗어나 거리낌 없이 방문을 이어 가기란 쉽지 않았을 것이다. 자매가 떠나기 전에 그들 사이에 오간 더 이상의 대화에서, 리틀 도릿은 가우언 씨가 아내를 — 그토록 다정한 순간에조차 — 너무 지나치게 예쁜 아이 대하듯 한다는 것을 깨닫게 된 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그는 그 겉모습 아래 틀림없이 깊은 감정이 자리하고 있을 것이라는 점을 도무지 짐작조차 못 하는 것 같았다. 그래서 그녀는 그 자신에게 과연 그런 깊이가 있을까 의심스러웠다. 그의 진지함의 결여가 그런 자질의 결여에서 비롯된 자연스러운 결과는 아닐까, 그리고 사람도 배와 같아서 너무 얕고 바위투성이인 물에서는 닻이 걸릴 데가 없어 정처 없이 떠돌게 되는 것은 아닐까 하고 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n그는 자매를 계단 아래까지 배웅하며, 자기 같은 가난뱅이에게나 어울리는 누추한 거처라고 익살스레 변명을 늘어놓았다. 그러고는, 자기를 몹시 부끄럽게 여기는 그 잘나고 거만한 친척 배너클 일가가 더 나은 거처를 마련해 준다면, 그들의 비위를 맞추느라 더 나은 곳에서 살아 주겠노라고 덧붙였다. 물가에서 그들은 블랑두아의 인사를 받았는데, 그는 방금 겪은 모험 탓에 얼굴이 제법 창백했으나 그럼에도 그 일을 무척 대수롭지 않게 여기며 — 라이언 이야기가 나오자 웃어넘겼다.\n\n둑길 위 한 조각 포도덩굴 아래 두 사람을 — 가우언은 덩굴에서 잎사귀를 따 무심히 물 위에 흩뿌리고 있었고 블랑두아는 담배에 불을 붙이고 있었다 — 남겨 둔 채, 자매는 올 때처럼 위세를 부리며 노 저어 떠나갔다. 미끄러져 나아간 지 몇 분 되지 않아, 리틀 도릿은 패니가 상황이 요구하는 것보다 더 요란하게 굴고 있음을 알아챘다. 그래서 그 까닭을 찾아 창문 너머로, 또 열린 문 너머로 둘러보다가, 또 다른 곤돌라 한 척이 분명 그들을 따라붙고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n이 곤돌라가 갖은 교묘한 방식으로 그들의 행로를 따라붙는 동안 — 때로는 앞으로 쏜살같이 나아갔다가 그들을 지나가도록 멈추어 서고, 때로는 물길이 넉넉히 넓으면 나란히 미끄러져 가고, 또 때로는 바짝 뒤를 따르고 — 그리고 패니가 그 안에 탄 누군가를 향해 우아한 자태를 부리고 있으면서도 그 사실을 모르는 체하는 것을 차츰 숨기지 않게 되자, 리틀 도릿은 마침내 그가 누구냐고 물었다.\n\n이에 패니는 짤막하게 대답했다. \"저 멍청이 말이야.\"\n\n\"누구?\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 패니가 (삼촌의 항의가 있기 전이었다면 '이 바보 같은 것아'라고 했을 법한 어조로) 대꾸했다. \"넌 어쩜 그리 굼뜨니! 젊은 스파클러 말이야.\"\n\n그녀는 제 쪽 창문을 내리고, 몸을 뒤로 기대 팔꿈치를 무심히 창턱에 올려놓은 채, 검은빛과 금빛이 어우러진 화려한 스페인 부채로 부채질을 했다. 뒤따르던 곤돌라가, 창 안에서 재빠르게 한 줄기 눈길을 흘리며 다시 앞으로 미끄러져 나아가자, 패니는 교태롭게 웃으며 말했다. \"얘, 저렇게 바보 같은 사람 본 적 있니?\"\n\n\"저 사람이 끝까지 언니를 따라올 작정일까?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다.\n\n\"내 귀여운 것아,\" 패니가 대꾸했다. \"절망에 빠진 멍청이가 무슨 짓을 할지야 내가 도무지 장담할 수 없지만, 그럴 가능성이 아주 높다고 봐. 그렇게 엄청난 거리도 아니잖니. 베네치아를 통틀어도 그만한 거리는 거의 안 될 거야, 저 사람이 날 한 번 보겠다고 죽기 살기로 매달린다면 말이지.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가요?\" 리틀 도릿이 더없이 순진하게 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄, 얘, 그건 정말이지 내가 대답하기 곤란한 질문이구나.\" 그녀의 언니가 말했다. \"그런 것 같긴 해. 에드워드한테 물어보는 게 낫겠다. 저 사람이 에드워드한테는 그렇다고 말하는 모양이야, 아마. 듣자 하니 카지노 같은 데서 내 얘기를 늘어놓으며 아주 꼴불견을 한다더라. 하지만 알고 싶으면 에드워드한테 물어보는 게 나아.\"\n\n\"그런데 왜 찾아오지 않을까요?\" 리틀 도릿이 잠시 생각하다 말했다.\n\n\"얘, 에이미, 내가 제대로 들은 거라면 네 궁금증은 곧 풀릴 거야. 저 사람이 오늘 찾아온대도 난 조금도 놀라지 않을걸. 아무래도 저 작자는 그저 용기를 그러모으느라 여태 기다린 것 같아.\"\n\n\"만나 줄 거예요?\"\n\n\"글쎄다, 얘,\" 패니가 말했다. \"그건 그때 가 봐야 알지. 저기 또 오는구나. 좀 봐. 오, 이 얼간이 같으니!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는, 부인할 수 없이, 모자라 보이는 행색이었다. 유리에 박힌 옹이처럼 창에 눈을 댄 채, 갑자기 제 배(舟)를 멈춰 세울 이유라곤 세상에 그 진짜 이유 말고는 도무지 없었으니 말이다.\n\n\"내가 그를 만나 줄 거냐고 물었는데, 얘,\" 패니가, 그 우아하게 무심한 자태에서만큼은 머들 부인 자신에 거의 못지않게 침착한 태도로 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻이니?\"\n\n\"제 말은,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"언니야말로 무슨 뜻인지 묻고 싶었다는 거예요, 패니 언니.\"\n\n패니는 다시, 짐짓 너그러우면서도 짓궂고 상냥한 태도로 웃었다. 그러고는 장난스레 다정하게 동생의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 내 귀여운 동생아, 말해 보렴. 우리가 마르티니에서 그 여자를 봤을 때, 그 여자가 어떻게 그 일을 받아넘기던지 넌 어떻게 봤니? 그 여자가 한순간에 무얼 작정했는지 봤니?\"\n\n\"아니요, 패니 언니.\"\n\"그럼 내가 말해 주마, 에이미. 그 여자는 속으로 이렇게 정한 거야. '이렇게 처지가 다른데 그때 만난 일은 결코 입에 올리지 않겠어, 그리고 이 애들이 그때 그 애들이라는 건 결코 아는 척하지 않겠어.' 그게 그 여자가 곤경을 벗어나는 방식이지. 우리가 그때 할리 가에서 나오면서 내가 너한테 뭐라고 했었니? 그 여자는 세상 어느 여자 못지않게 오만하고 거짓되다고. 하지만 그 첫 번째 자질에 관해서라면, 얘야, 그 여자도 자기 맞수가 될 사람을 만나게 될 거야.\"\n\n스페인 부채를 패니의 가슴 쪽으로 의미심장하게 돌린 것이, 그런 사람 가운데 하나가 어디서 발견될지를 자못 또렷하게 가리켜 보였다.\n\n\"그뿐 아니라,\" 패니가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자는 젊은 스파클러한테도 똑같은 지시를 내려서는, 그자의 그 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없는 머리통 속에 (정말이지 그걸 머리라고 부를 수도 없지만) 그자가 그 여관 마당에서 처음으로 내게 반한 척해야 한다는 걸 단단히 박아 넣기 전까지는 날 따라오지 못하게 한 거야.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다.\n\n\"왜라니? 어머나, 얘!\" (또다시 '이 멍청한 것아' 하는 어조로) \"어떻게 그런 걸 묻니? 내가 그 머리통한테 꽤 탐나는 혼처가 되었을지도 모른다는 걸 모르겠어? 그리고 그 여자가 자기 어깨에서 (정말이지 무척 고운 어깨이긴 하지만) 그 짐을 떨쳐 내면서, 우리 기분을 헤아려 주는 척 시늉을 하며 우리한테 그 속임수를 떠넘긴다는 걸 모르겠니?\" 패니 양이 흐뭇하게 제 모습을 흘긋 보며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 우리는 언제든 있는 그대로의 진실로 돌아갈 수 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 미안하지만 우린 그러지 않을 거야.\" 패니가 받아쳤다. \"안 돼. 난 그렇게 되도록 두지 않을 거야, 에이미. 그 핑계는 내 게 아니라 그 여자 거니까, 그 여자가 실컷 그 맛을 봐야지.\"\n\n의기양양하게 들뜬 기분에 패니 양은 한 손으로는 스페인 부채를 부치면서, 다른 손으로는 마치 머들 부인을 짓이기기라도 하는 듯 동생의 허리를 꽉 끌어안았다.\n\n\"안 돼.\" 패니가 되풀이했다. \"그 여자는 내가 자기 방식대로 나가는 걸 보게 될 거야. 그 여자가 택한 길이니, 나도 그걸 따라가겠어. 그리고 운명과 행운의 가호가 있다면, 난 그 여자와의 교분을 계속 다져 나가서, 마침내 그 여자가 보는 앞에서, 언젠가 그 여자가 자기 양재사한테서 받은 걸 내게 준 것보다 열 배는 더 곱고 값비싼 물건들을 내 양재사한테서 받아 그 여자 하녀한테 줄 거야!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 잠자코 있었다. 가문의 체면에 관한 어떤 문제에서도 자기 말은 받아들여지지 않으리라는 것을 알았고, 새로이, 뜻밖에 되찾은 언니의 호의를 공연히 잃고 싶지 않았기 때문이다. 그녀는 동의할 수 없었으나, 잠자코 있었다. 패니는 동생이 무슨 생각을 하는지 잘 알았다. 어찌나 잘 알았던지 이내 동생에게 물어보았다.\n\n동생의 대답은 이러했다. \"언니, 스파클러 씨를 부추길 작정이에요?\"\n\n\"그를 부추긴다고, 얘?\" 언니가 경멸하듯 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그야 네가 부추긴다는 걸 어떻게 보느냐에 달렸지. 아니, 난 그를 부추길 작정은 없어. 하지만 그를 내 종으로 만들어 버리겠어.\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 진지하고도 미심쩍은 눈으로 언니의 얼굴을 흘긋 보았으나, 패니는 그쯤으로 멈출 사람이 아니었다. 그녀는 검은빛과 금빛 부채를 접어, 그것으로 동생의 코를 톡톡 두드렸다. 마치 도도한 미인이자 기개 넘치는 여인이, 수수한 동무를 가지고 놀며 장난스레 가르치기라도 하는 듯한 태도였다.\n\n\"난 그가 이리저리 심부름을 하게 만들 거야, 얘, 그리고 그를 내게 복종하게 만들 거야. 그리고 그의 어머니까지 내게 복종하게 만들지 못한다면, 그건 내 탓이 아닐 테고.\"\n\n\"패니 언니 — 부디 언짢아하진 마요, 우린 지금 이렇게 사이좋게 지내고 있으니까 — 그 길의 끝이 어떻게 될지 언니는 다 내다볼 수 있다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"아직 그것까지 내다본 적은 없다고 해야겠구나, 얘.\" 패니가 더없이 무심하게 대답했다. \"다 때가 되면 알게 되겠지. 내 작정은 그렇다는 거야. 그런데 정말이지 이 얘기를 풀어놓느라 어찌나 오래 걸렸는지, 어느새 집에 다 왔네. 그리고 젊은 스파클러가 문간에서 안에 누가 있느냐고 묻고 있고. 물론 순전히 우연으로 말이지!\"\n\n과연 그 구애자는 명함갑을 손에 든 채 곤돌라 안에 일어서서, 어느 하인에게 그 질문을 하는 시늉을 하고 있었다. 이런 정황이 한데 어우러져, 그는 곧이어 젊은 숙녀들 앞에 어떤 자세로 나타나게 되었는데, 옛날 같았으면 그의 구애에 길조로 여겨지지는 않았을 자세였다. 추격에 다소 애를 먹은 젊은 숙녀들의 곤돌라 사공들이, 자기네 배를 어찌나 깔끔하게 몰아 스파클러 씨의 배에 더없이 부드럽게 부딪쳐 놓았는지, 그 신사를 큼직한 볼링 핀처럼 발랑 자빠뜨려, 사모해 마지않는 대상에게 제 구둣발 밑창을 드러내 보이게 만들었던 것이다. 한편 그의 더 고귀한 부위들은 하인 가운데 하나의 팔에 안긴 채 배 바닥에서 허우적거리고 있었다.\n\n그러나 패니 양이 자못 걱정스러운 목소리로 그 신사가 다치지는 않았느냐고 외치자, 스파클러 씨는 예상보다 멀쩡한 모습으로 몸을 일으켜, 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬더듬 제 입으로 \"조금도 안 다쳤습니다\"라고 말했다. 패니 양은 전에 그를 본 기억이 도무지 없는 듯, 멀찍이 고개만 까딱 숙이고 지나치려다가, 그가 제 이름을 밝히자 멈추었다. 그러고도 그녀는 그 이름을 떠올리지 못해 난처해하다가, 그가 마르티니에서 그녀를 뵙는 영광을 누렸노라고 설명하자 비로소 그를 기억해 냈고, 그의 어머님께서 안녕하시냐고 물었다.\n\n\"고맙습니다,\" 스파클러 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"어머니는 유난히 잘 지내십니다 — 적어도, 편찮으시지요.\"\n\n\"베네치아에 계신가요?\" 패니 양이 말했다.\n\n\"로마에 계십니다.\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"저는 여기 혼자, 저 혼자 와 있습니다. 에드워드 도릿 씨를 뵈러 제가 직접 찾아온 겁니다. 사실, 도릿 씨도 마찬가지로요. 요컨대, 온 가족을 뵈러요.\"\n\n패니 양은 우아하게 시중꾼들을 돌아보며, 자기 아빠나 오라버니가 안에 계시냐고 물었다. 두 분 다 안에 계신다는 대답이 돌아오자, 스파클러 씨가 공손히 제 팔을 내밀었다. 패니 양은 그것을 받아들여, 스파클러 씨의 시중을 받으며 그 큰 계단을 올라갔다. 스파클러 씨는 그녀에게 허튼 구석이라곤 없다고 (그가 그렇게 여겼음을 의심할 까닭은 전혀 없다) 여전히 믿고 있었다면, 자기 자신을 적잖이 속이고 있는 셈이었다.\n\n빛바랜 휘장이 — 서글픈 바다 빛 초록색의 — 어찌나 닳고 시들었던지 창문 아래로 떠도는 부유물 같은 해초붙이와 친척뻘이라고 주장하거나, 혹은 벽에 들러붙어 갇혀 있는 친척들을 위해 흐느끼는 듯이 보이는, 그 곰팡이 핀 응접실에 다다르자, 패니 양은 사람을 보내 아버지와 오라버니를 모셔 오게 했다. 그들이 나타나기를 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 소파에 앉아 한껏 자태를 뽐내며, 단테에 관한 몇 마디로 스파클러 씨의 함락을 완성했다. 그 신사가 알기로 단테란, 머리에 잎사귀를 둘러 쓰고 알 수 없는 까닭으로 피렌체 대성당 밖 걸상에 앉아 있곤 했다는, 골동품 같은 부류의 괴짜였다.\n\n도릿 씨는 더없이 정중하고 가장 고상한 예법으로 손님을 맞이했다. 그는 머들 부인의 안부를 각별히 물었다. 머들 씨의 안부도 각별히 물었다. 스파클러 씨는, 아니 차라리 셔츠 깃을 잡아당기며 자기 자신을 잘게 토막 내듯 띄엄띄엄 말하기를, 머들 부인이 시골 별장에서 지낼 만큼 지냈고 브라이턴 저택에서도 지낼 만큼 지냈는데, 보시다시피 런던에 사람이라곤 한 명도 없을 때 거기 머물 수가 없는 데다, 올해는 이 사람 저 사람 별장을 돌아다닐 만큼 기력도 그리 좋지 않으셔서, 로마에 한번 가 보기로 작정하셨다고 했다. 자기 어머니 같은 여인은, 익히 소문난 고운 자태에 허튼 구석이라곤 없으니, 로마에서 틀림없이 큰 보탬이 될 것이 분명하다는 것이었다. 머들 씨로 말하자면, 그분은 시티며 그 밖의 여러 곳의 사람들이 어찌나 찾는지, 또 매입이며 은행업이며 그런 일에 어찌나 엄청나게 비범한 현상이신지, 스파클러 씨가 의심하기로는 이 나라의 화폐 제도가 그분 없이 굴러갈 수 있을지 모를 지경이라고 했다. 다만 그분의 일이 이따금 그분에게 벅찰 때가 있어, 전혀 새로운 풍광과 기후로 잠시 바람을 쐬는 편이 그분께 두루 이로울 것이라는 점만큼은 스파클러 씨도 숨기지 않았다. 정작 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 스파클러 씨는 도릿 일가가 어디로 가든 자기도 다소 특별한 용무로 그리로 갈 작정이라는 뜻을 전했다.\n\n이 어마어마한 대화의 위업에는 시간이 필요했으나, 어쨌든 해냈다. 그것이 이루어지자 도릿 씨는 스파클러 씨가 조만간 함께 만찬을 들기를 바란다고 했다. 스파클러 씨가 그 제안을 어찌나 반갑게 받아들였던지, 도릿 씨는 이를테면 오늘은 무엇을 할 작정이냐고 물었다. 그날 그는 아무것도 할 일이 없었으므로 (그것은 그의 평소 일과였고, 그가 특히 적격인 일이었다), 그는 미룰 것도 없이 붙들렸다. 게다가 저녁에는 숙녀들을 오페라에 모시고 가기로 단단히 약속까지 받게 되었다.\n\n만찬 시각이 되자 스파클러 씨는, 어머니를 빼닮은 비너스의 아들인 양 바다에서 솟아올라, 그 큰 계단을 오르며 훌륭한 모습을 선보였다. 아침에 패니가 매혹적이었다면, 이제 그녀는 세 곱절 매혹적이었다. 더없이 잘 어울리는 색으로 가장 알맞게 차려입고, 거기에 무심한 듯한 분위기까지 두르고 있어 스파클러 씨의 족쇄를 두 배로 채워 단단히 박아 넣었던 것이다.\n\n\"스파클러 씨, 듣자 하니 — 하 — 가우언 씨와 아는 사이라더군요. 헨리 가우언 씨 말이오.\" 만찬 자리에서 주인이 말했다.\n\n\"잘 알지요, 어르신.\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"사실 그의 어머니와 제 어머니가 막역한 사이거든요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 생각을 했더라면, 에이미,\" 데시머스 경 자신 못지않게 거창한 생색을 내며 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그분들께 오늘 만찬에 오시라는 쪽지를 보내라고 너에게 일렀을 텐데. 우리 사람들 가운데 누가 — 하 — 그분들을 모셔 오고, 또 댁까지 모셔다 드릴 수도 있었을 게다. 그 일에 — 흠 — 곤돌라 한 척쯤은 내줄 수 있었을 테고. 이걸 잊은 게 유감이구나. 부디 내일 그분들을 일깨워 다오.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 헨리 가우언 씨가 그들의 생색을 어떻게 받아들일지 의심스럽지 않은 것은 아니었으나, 잊지 않고 일깨우겠다고 약속했다.\n\n\"한데 헨리 가우언 씨는 — 하 — 초상화를 그리오?\" 도릿 씨가 물었다.\n\n스파클러 씨는, 일감만 얻을 수 있다면 그는 무엇이든 그린다고 보았다.\n\n\"특정한 분야는 없소?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n사랑에 자극받아 재기가 번득인 스파클러 씨는, 특정한 분야를 가지려면 사람은 특정한 신발 한 켤레가 있어야 한다고 대답했다. 이를테면 사냥에는 사냥 신발, 크리켓에는 크리켓 신발 하는 식으로 말이다. 그런데 그가 알기로 헨리 가우언에게는 특정한 신발 한 켤레가 없다는 것이었다.\n\n\"전문 분야가 없소?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n이것은 스파클러 씨에게 무척 긴 단어인 데다 방금 전의 분발로 머리가 바닥난 터라, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"아뇨, 괜찮습니다. 저는 그건 좀처럼 안 합니다.\"\n\n\"음!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그토록 좋은 연줄을 둔 신사께, 그의 이익을 도모하고 그의 — 흠 — 천재성의 싹을 틔워 주고픈 내 마음의 — 하 — 증표가 될 무언가를 선사할 수 있다면 무척 기쁘겠소. 아무래도 내 초상을 그리도록 가우언 씨에게 맡겨야겠소. 그 결과가 — 하 — 서로 만족스럽다면, 그 뒤엔 내 가족에게도 그의 솜씨를 한번 발휘해 보라고 맡길 수도 있겠지.\"\n\n스파클러 씨에게는, 가족 가운데 어떤 화가도 제대로 그려 낼 수 없는 분들이 ('어떤'에 자못 힘을 주어) 더러 있다고 말할 틈이 여기 있다는, 더없이 대담하고 독창적인 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 그 생각을 담아낼 말의 형태가 없어, 그것은 다시 하늘로 돌아가 버렸다.\n\n그것이 더욱 안타까운 까닭은, 패니 양이 그 초상화라는 발상에 크게 박수를 보내며 아빠에게 어서 실행하라고 부추겼기 때문이다. 그녀가 짐작하기로 가우언 씨는 그 예쁜 아내와 결혼함으로써 더 낫고 더 높은 기회들을 잃었으며, 끼니를 위해 그림을 그리는 오두막 속 사랑이란 너무도 황홀하게 흥미로운 것이라, 그가 초상을 그릴 줄 알든 모르든 아빠께서 그에게 일을 맡겨 주십사 청한다고 했다. 하기야 그녀도 에이미도, 그날 그의 화가 위에서 살아 있는 듯한 초상을 보고 또 그것을 원본과 견주어 볼 기회를 가진 터라, 그가 그릴 줄 안다는 것은 알고 있었지만 말이다. 이런 말들은 (어쩌면 그러려고 의도된 것이었을 테지만) 스파클러 씨를 거의 미칠 지경으로 만들었다. 한편으로 그 말들은 패니 양이 애틋한 연정에 마음이 끌리기 쉽다는 것을 드러내면서도, 정작 그녀 자신은 그의 흠모를 그토록 천진하게 모르는 체하는 바람에, 그는 알지 못할 연적에 대한 질투로 두 눈이 부릅떠진 채 튀어나올 지경이었던 것이다.\n\n만찬 뒤에 다시 바다로 내려갔다가 오페라 극장 계단에서 다시 솟아오른 그들은, 사공 가운데 하나가 시중드는 인어처럼 커다란 천 등롱을 들고 앞장선 가운데 자기네 칸막이 좌석으로 들어섰고, 스파클러 씨는 고뇌의 저녁으로 들어섰다. 극장 안은 어둡고 좌석은 환했으므로, 공연 도중에 여러 방문객이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔다. 패니는 그들에게 어찌나 관심을 보이고, 또 그들과 이야기를 나누며 어찌나 매혹적인 자태를 짓던지 — 그들과 은밀한 속닥임을 주고받고, 먼 좌석에 있는 사람들이 누구인가를 두고 자잘한 입씨름을 벌이는 동안 — 가엾은 스파클러는 온 인류를 증오하게 되었다. 그러나 공연이 끝날 무렵 그에게는 두 가지 위안이 있었다. 그녀가 망토를 여미는 동안 자기 부채를 그에게 들고 있게 한 것, 그리고 다시 계단을 내려가며 그에게 자기 팔을 잡게 해 준 그 복된 특권이었다. 이 격려의 부스러기들이면 그럭저럭 버틸 수 있겠다고 스파클러 씨는 생각했고, 도릿 양 역시 그렇게 생각했을 가능성도 없지 않았다.\n\n등불을 든 인어가 좌석 문간에 대기하고 있었고, 다른 등불을 든 다른 인어들도 여러 문간에 대기하고 있었다. 도릿네 인어는 계단을 비추려고 등롱을 낮게 들었고, 스파클러 씨는 그녀의 빛나는 발이 제 곁에서 계단을 깡총깡총 내려가는 것을 바라보며, 먼젓번 족쇄 위에 또 한 벌의 묵직한 족쇄를 덧채웠다. 이곳에서 어슬렁거리던 자들 가운데 파리의 블랑두아가 있었다. 그는 말을 건네며 패니 곁으로 다가왔다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 오라버니, 제너럴 부인과 함께 앞쪽에 있었으나 (도릿 씨는 집에 남아 있었다), 부두 가장자리에서 그들 모두가 한데 모이게 되었다. 그녀는 블랑두아가 패니를 배에 태워 주며 바로 자기 곁에 와 있는 것을 보고 다시 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"가우언이 오늘 고운 숙녀분들의 방문으로 기뻐했었는데, 그 뒤에 그만 한 가지 잃은 게 있답니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"잃다니요?\" 사별한 듯 풀이 죽은 스파클러에게서 풀려나 자리에 앉으며 패니가 되물었다.\n\n\"잃었지요.\" 블랑두아가 말했다. \"그의 개 라이언 말입니다.\"\n\n그가 그렇게 말할 때, 리틀 도릿의 손은 그의 손 안에 있었다.\n\n\"녀석이 죽었습니다.\" 블랑두아가 말했다.\n\n\"죽었다고요?\" 리틀 도릿이 따라 말했다. \"그 의젓한 개가요?\"\n\n\"이런, 고운 숙녀분들!\" 블랑두아가 미소 지으며 어깨를 으쓱하고 말했다. \"누군가가 그 의젓한 개를 독살했지 뭡니까. 도제(總督)들만큼이나 죽어 버렸답니다!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism\n\n제너럴 부인은 언제나 자기 마부석에 앉아 예의범절을 잘 단속하면서, 그 매우 사랑하는 젊은 벗에게 매끄러운 겉면을 입히려 애를 썼고, 제너럴 부인의 그 매우 사랑하는 젊은 벗은 그것을 받아들이려 무던히 애를 썼다. 고된 인생을 살며 수많은 목표를 이루려 애써 왔지만, 그녀는 제너럴 부인의 손에 윤이 발리도록 하는 이 일만큼 열심히 애써 본 적이 없었다. 그 매끄럽게 다듬는 손에 다루어지는 것이 그녀를 불안하고 거북하게 만든 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 그녀는 식구들의 자잘한 요구에 자신을 내맡겨 왔듯이, 식구들의 큰 요구에도 그 큰 그대로 자신을 내맡겼고, 아버지가 저녁을 들 수 있도록 자기 점심을 아껴 두던 시절에 자기 허기 그 자체에 굴복하지 않았던 것 이상으로 이 일에서 자기 자신의 마음에 굴복하지 않았다.\n\n제너럴 부인에 의한 시련 속에서 그녀가 누린 한 가지 위안은, 그녀의 분투와 희생에 익숙하지 않은, 덜 헌신적이고 덜 다정한 사람에게는 자못 합당해 보일 것 이상으로, 그녀를 더 든든히 지탱해 주었고 더 고맙게 느끼게 해 주었다. 그리고 실제로 인생에서 자주 보게 되는 일이지만, 리틀 도릿 같은 사람들은 자기를 누르고 이기는 이들의 절반만큼도 신중히 따져 보지 않는 것처럼 보인다. 언니가 계속 베풀어 주는 친절, 이것이 리틀 도릿에게는 그 위안이었다. 그 친절이 너그러운 후견의 형태를 띤다는 것은 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 거기에 익숙했다. 그것이 그녀를 종속된 자리에 두고, 패니 양이 높은 자리에 앉아 경의를 강요하는 그 불타는 수레를 시중드는 처지에 보이게 한다는 것도 그녀에게는 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 더 나은 자리를 바라지 않았다. 언제나 패니의 아름다움과 우아함과 재치를 흠모하면서, 그리고 패니에게 강하게 매여 있고 싶어 하는 자기 성향이 얼마만큼은 제 마음에서 나온 것이고 얼마만큼은 패니의 마음에서 나온 것인지 이제는 스스로에게 묻지도 않으면서, 그녀는 자신의 너른 가슴에 담긴 자매로서의 온갖 애정을 언니에게 다 주었다.\n\n제너럴 부인이 가정생활에 들이부은 자두와 프리즘의 막대한 양은, 패니가 사교계로 끊임없이 뛰어드는 일과 어우러져, 그 혼합물의 바닥에 자연스럽게 가라앉는 침전물이라곤 아주 적은 찌꺼기밖에 남기지 않았다. 이 때문에 패니와 나누는 속내 이야기가 리틀 도릿에게는 갑절로 소중했고, 그것이 그녀에게 주는 안도감이 한층 커졌다.\n\n\"에이미,\" 어느 날 밤 둘만 있을 때 패니가 그녀에게 말했다. 그날은 어찌나 고단했던지 리틀 도릿은 완전히 녹초가 되었으나, 패니는 세상 무엇보다도 기꺼이 사교계에 한 번 더 발을 담그고 싶어 했을 정도였다. \"네 그 작은 머릿속에 뭘 좀 넣어 줄까 해. 그게 뭔지 넌 짐작도 못 할걸, 내 짐작에는.\"\n\n\"그럴 것 같진 않아요, 언니.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"자, 단서를 하나 줄게, 얘.\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인.\"\n\n자두와 프리즘이 천 가지 조합으로 종일 지겹도록 위세를 떨치고 있었던 터라—모든 것이 알맹이 없는 겉면이요 윤칠이요 겉치레였던 터라—리틀 도릿은 제너럴 부인이 몇 시간째 침대에 안전히 파묻혀 있기를 바랐던 사람 같은 얼굴을 했다.\n\n\"이제 짐작이 가, 에이미?\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 언니. 내가 뭔가 한 게 아니라면요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 놀라며 말했는데, 윤칠을 갈라지게 하고 겉면을 흐트러뜨릴 만한 무언가를 했다는 뜻이었다.\n\n패니는 그 걱정스러운 표정이 어찌나 재미있던지, 자기가 가장 아끼는 부채를 집어 들고서(그때 그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 잔혹한 무기들을 늘어놓고 있었는데, 그 대부분이 스파클러의 심장에서 갓 뽑혀 김이 나는 듯했다) 그것으로 동생의 코를 연신 톡톡 치며 줄곧 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 우리 에이미, 우리 에이미!\" 패니가 말했다. \"우리 에이미는 어쩜 이리 겁 많은 작은 거위람! 하지만 이건 웃을 일이 아니야. 오히려 난 무척 화가 나, 얘.\"\n\n\"나한테 난 게 아니라면, 언니, 난 상관없어요.\" 동생이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아! 하지만 난 상관있어.\" 패니가 말했다. \"그리고 너도 그럴 거야, 얘, 내가 알려 주면. 에이미, 누군가가 제너럴 부인에게 어이없을 만큼 깍듯이 군다는 생각, 한 번도 안 들었어?\"\n\n\"누구나 제너럴 부인에게는 깍듯해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"왜냐하면—\"\n\n\"그 부인이 사람들을 얼려서 그렇게 만드니까?\" 패니가 끼어들었다. \"내 말은 그게 아니야. 그것과는 영 다른 얘기야. 자! 에이미, 아빠가 제너럴 부인에게 어이없을 만큼 깍듯이 군다는 생각, 한 번도 안 들었느냐고.\"\n\n에이미는 \"아니요\" 하고 중얼거리며 어리둥절한 얼굴을 했다.\n\n\"그래, 안 들었겠지. 하지만 그러셔.\" 패니가 말했다. \"그러신다니까, 에이미. 그리고 내 말 잘 기억해 둬. 제너럴 부인은 아빠한테 흑심을 품고 있어!\"\n\n\"패니 언니, 제너럴 부인이 누구한테든 흑심을 품을 수 있다고 생각해요?\"\n\n\"그게 가능하다고 생각하느냐고?\" 패니가 받아쳤다. \"얘, 난 확실히 알아. 내 말하잖니, 그 여자는 아빠한테 흑심이 있다고. 그뿐 아니라, 아빠는 그 여자를 어찌나 대단한 사람으로, 교양의 표본으로, 우리 집안에 어찌나 큰 보탬으로 여기시는지, 언제라도 그 여자한테 홀딱 빠져 버릴 태세야. 그러면 참 보기 좋은 그림이 펼쳐지겠지, 안 그래? 제너럴 부인을 엄마로 둔 나를 생각해 봐!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 \"제너럴 부인을 엄마로 둔 나를 생각해 봐\"라고 대꾸하지는 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 근심스러운 얼굴로, 무엇 때문에 패니가 이런 결론에 이르렀는지 진지하게 물었다.\n\n\"세상에, 얘.\" 패니가 톡 쏘며 말했다. \"그럼 넌 어떤 남자가 나한테 반했을 때 내가 그걸 어떻게 아느냐고도 묻겠구나! 하지만 난 당연히 알지. 꽤 자주 있는 일이거든. 하지만 난 늘 알아챈다고. 이번 일도 거의 그런 식으로 알아챈 거야, 아마. 어쨌든 난 알아.\"\n\n\"아빠가 무슨 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적은 없죠?\"\n\n\"무슨 말을 해?\" 패니가 되뇌었다. \"내 가장 소중한 동생아, 아직까지 아빠가 무슨 말을 할 필요가 어디 있었겠니?\"\n\n\"그럼 제너럴 부인이 무슨 말 하는 것도 들은 적이 없고요?\"\n\n\"세상에나, 에이미.\" 패니가 대꾸했다. \"그 여자가 무슨 말을 할 그런 부류로 보여? 지금 그 여자가 할 일이라곤 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고, 그 거슬리는 장갑을 낀 채로, 우아하게 휩쓸고 다니는 것밖에 없다는 게 너무 빤하고 분명하지 않아? 무슨 말을 해! 휘스트에서 으뜸패의 에이스를 손에 쥐고 있어도, 그 여자는 한마디도 안 할걸, 얘. 그건 패를 낼 때 비로소 드러나는 법이지.\"\n\n\"적어도 언니가 잘못 짚은 걸 수도 있잖아요, 패니. 자, 그럴 수도 있지 않아요?\"\n\n\"오 그래, 그럴 수도 있지.\" 패니가 말했다. \"하지만 난 틀리지 않았어. 그래도 네가 그런 탈출구를 떠올릴 수 있다니 기쁘구나, 얘. 그리고 네가 지금 이 일을 충분히 냉정하게 받아들이고 그런 가능성을 생각할 수 있다니 기뻐. 그걸 보니 네가 이 인연을 견딜 수 있을지도 모르겠다는 희망이 생기네. 나라면 못 견뎌, 견디려고 하지도 않을 거고. 차라리 저 젊은 스파클러랑 결혼하고 말지.\"\n\n\"오, 언니는 어떤 상황에서도 그 사람과 결혼하지 않을 거잖아요, 패니.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 얘,\" 그 젊은 숙녀가 지극히 무심하게 받아쳤다. \"그것조차 단언하진 못하겠어. 무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠니. 게다가 나중에 그 여자, 그 사람 어머니를 그 여자 식대로 갚아 줄 기회가 숱하게 생길 테고. 그 기회를 난 결코 굼뜨게 흘려보내지 않을 거야, 에이미.\"\n\n그때 자매 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었다. 그러나 오간 그 말들 덕에 제너럴 부인과 스파클러 씨라는 두 화제가 리틀 도릿의 마음속에서 크게 부각되었고, 그때부터 그녀는 그 둘을 무척 많이 생각하게 되었다.\n\n제너럴 부인은 오래전부터 자기 겉면을 어찌나 완벽하게 다듬어 두었던지 그 아래에 무엇이 있든(있기나 하다면) 다 가려 버렸으므로, 그쪽에서는 아무것도 관찰할 수가 없었다. 도릿 씨가 그녀에게 매우 깍듯하고 그녀를 높이 평가한다는 것은 부인할 수 없었다. 그러나 대개는 충동적인 패니인지라, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 쉽사리 틀릴 수 있었다. 반면에 스파클러 문제는 사정이 달라서, 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지는 누구든 알아볼 수 있었고, 리틀 도릿도 그것을 보며 숱한 의문과 궁금증을 품고 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n스파클러 씨의 헌신은 그를 노예로 부리는 여인의 변덕과 잔인함에나 견줄 만했다. 어떤 때는 그녀가 그를 어찌나 두드러지게 챙겨 주는지 그가 기뻐서 큰 소리로 낄낄거렸다가도, 이튿날이나 다음 순간이면 그를 어찌나 완벽하게 못 본 척하고 캄캄한 무명의 심연으로 떨어뜨리는지 그가 기침하는 척 어설피 가장하며 끙끙댔다. 그의 한결같은 시중도 패니의 마음을 결코 움직이지 못했다. 그는 에드워드와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사이여서, 그 신사가 다른 부류의 사람들과 어울리고 싶을 때면 변장한 음모자처럼 위장한 곤돌라를 타고 비밀의 문과 뒷길로 몰래 빠져나가야 하는 거북한 처지에 놓였는데도 그러했다. 그는 도릿 씨의 안부를 어찌나 걱정스레 궁금해했던지, 마치 도릿 씨가 간헐열의 먹이라도 되는 양 하루걸러 찾아와 안부를 물었는데도 그러했다. 그는 주요 창문들 앞을 어찌나 끊임없이 노 저어 오르내렸던지, 천 시간에 천 마일을 노 저어 가는 큰 내기라도 건 사람처럼 보일 지경이었는데도 그러했다. 그가 섬기는 여인의 곤돌라가 대문을 떠날 때면, 마치 그녀가 어여쁜 밀수꾼이고 그가 세관 관리이기라도 한 듯, 스파클러 씨의 곤돌라가 어느 물가의 매복처에서 튀어나와 뒤를 쫓았는데도 그러했다. 스파클러 씨가 겉보기에 야위지 않은 것은 아마도 이렇게 바깥 공기와 짠 바닷바람에 그리도 자주 노출되어 타고난 체질의 힘이 한층 다져진 덕이었을 것이다. 그러나 원인이 무엇이든, 그는 시들시들 병약한 모습으로 섬기는 여인의 마음을 움직일 가망이라곤 전혀 없을뿐더러, 날이 갈수록 더 혈색 좋고 우람해졌으며, 청년이라기보다 퉁퉁 부은 소년 같아 보이던 그 특유의 인상이 불그레하게 부풀어 오른 비범한 정도로까지 발달했다.\n\n블랑두아가 인사차 찾아오자, 도릿 씨는 그를 가우언 씨의 친구로서 상냥하게 맞이했고, 가우언 씨에게 자신의 모습을 후세에 전하도록 의뢰하려는 생각을 그에게 말했다. 블랑두아가 그 생각을 한껏 칭송하자, 그 친구에게 마련된 이 큰 기회를 블랑두아가 직접 전해 주면 좋아하겠다는 생각이 도릿 씨에게 떠올랐다. 블랑두아는 특유의 자유롭고 우아한 태도로 그 임무를 받아들이며, 한 시간이 채 지나기 전에 그것을 완수하겠노라 맹세했다. 그가 가우언에게 그 소식을 전하자, 그 거장은 매우 너그럽게도 도릿 씨를 열 몇 번이나 악마에게 내던졌고(그는 후견을 받는 것을 후견이 없는 것 못지않게 분개했으므로), 그 전갈을 가져왔다고 친구와 다투려 들었다.\n\n\"내 정신의 시야에 결함이 있는 건지도 모르겠네만, 블랑두아,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일이 자네와 무슨 상관인지 내가 안다면 차라리 죽어 버리지.\"\n\n\"내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대,\" 블랑두아가 대답했다. \"나도 모르겠네, 그저 내 친구를 도와주는 줄 알았다는 것 말고는.\"\n\n\"벼락출세한 자의 품삯을 내 호주머니에 찔러 넣어서?\" 가우언이 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"그런 뜻인가? 자네 그 다른 친구한테 가서, 무슨 선술집 간판으로 쓸 얼굴이나 그리라고 하게, 그것도 간판장이를 시켜서 말이야. 내가 누군데, 또 그자가 누군데?\"\n\n\"프로페소레,\" 그 사절이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 블랑두아는 누구지?\"\n\n가우언은 후자의 질문에는 조금도 관심을 보이지 않은 채, 화가 나서 휘파람으로 도릿 씨를 쫓아 보내듯 했다. 그러나 이튿날 그는 그 화제를 다시 꺼내, 특유의 무심한 태도로 깔보는 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그래, 블랑두아, 자네 그 마에케나스님께는 언제 가지? 우리 같은 품팔이꾼들은 일감이 생길 때 잡아야 하는 법이거든. 이 일감을 보러 언제 가 볼까?\"\n\n\"자네가 원할 때,\" 마음 상한 블랑두아가 말했다. \"자네 좋을 대로. 그게 나랑 무슨 상관인가? 나한테 뭐가 어떻다고?\"\n\n\"나한테 뭐가 어떤지는 내가 말해 주지,\" 가우언이 말했다. \"빵하고 치즈일세. 사람은 먹어야 하지 않나! 그러니 가세, 내 친구 블랑두아.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 자기 딸들과, 어떤 놀라운 우연으로 마침 그곳에 찾아와 있던 스파클러 씨가 있는 자리에서 두 사람을 맞이했다. \"잘 지내나, 스파클러?\" 가우언이 무심하게 말했다. \"자네가 타고난 머리로 먹고살아야 할 때가 오거든, 부디 나보다는 잘 헤쳐 나가기를 비네.\"\n\n이윽고 도릿 씨가 자신의 제안을 꺼냈다. \"선생,\" 가우언이 그 제안을 제법 우아하게 받아들인 뒤 웃으며 말했다. \"저는 이 일에 풋내기여서 그 비법에 능숙하질 못합니다. 마땅히 저는 선생을 여러 각도에서 살펴보고, 선생이 더없이 훌륭한 소재라고 말씀드리고, 제가 그리고자 하는 그 멋진 그림에 필요한 만큼의 열정을 다 쏟아부을 수 있을 만큼 한가해질 때가 언제일지 가늠해 봐야 하겠지요. 정말이지,\" 그가 또 웃었다. \"저는 그 사랑스럽고 재능 있고 선량하고 고결한 친구들, 제 동료 화가들의 진영에서, 이 눈속임을 더 그럴듯하게 하지 못하니 영락없는 배신자 같은 기분이 듭니다. 그러나 저는 그렇게 자라지 못했고, 이제 와 배우기에는 너무 늦었지요. 그런데 사실인즉, 저는 형편없는 화가입니다, 다만 보통내기들보다 그리 많이 나쁘지는 않을 뿐이지요. 선생께서 백 기니쯤 내던지실 작정이라면, 저야 으레 지체 높은 분들의 가난한 친척이 그렇듯 가난뱅이니, 그것을 저한테 내던져 주신다면 무척 감사하겠습니다. 그 돈값을 하려 최선을 다하지요. 그리고 그 최선이 형편없다 한들, 그럼 그때는 큰 이름이 붙은 형편없는 그림 대신 작은 이름이 붙은 형편없는 그림을 손에 넣으시게 될 테니까요.\"\n\n이런 어조는 그가 예상했던 바는 아니었으나, 대체로 도릿 씨에게는 놀라우리만치 잘 맞았다. 그것은 이 신사가, 지체 높은 집안과 연이 닿아 있고 한낱 직공이 아닌 사람이, 자기에게 신세를 지게 되리라는 것을 보여 주었다. 그는 가우언 씨의 손에 자신을 맡기게 되어 만족스럽다고 말했고, 두 사람이 사사로운 신사로서 교분을 더 두텁게 할 즐거움을 누리기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n\"참으로 너그러우십니다,\" 가우언이 말했다. \"저는 이 붓의 형제단(이 지상에서 가장 유쾌한 친구들이지요)에 들어온 뒤로도 사교를 끊은 적이 없고, 이따금 그 좋은 옛 화약 냄새를 맡는 것이 꽤나 반갑습니다—그 화약이 저를 공중으로 날려 지금의 이 직업에 떨어뜨려 놓긴 했지만요. 그런데 도릿 씨, 제가 시간과 장소에 대해 한 가지 조건을 내건다 해서,\" 여기서 그가 또 더없이 편하게 웃었다. \"제가 이 업계의 비밀 결사 같은 것에 빠져들었다고 여기진 마십시오—그런 건 아니거든요. 맹세코, 저는 어딜 가든 그것을 드러내지 않고는 못 배기지만요, 비록 주피터께 맹세코 제 온 힘을 다해 이 업을 사랑하고 존경합니다만 말입니다.\"\n\n하! 도릿 씨로서는 가우언 씨의 솔직함에 그런—흠—의심 따위는 도무지 품을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"또다시 참으로 너그러우십니다,\" 가우언이 말했다. \"도릿 씨, 듣자 하니 로마로 가신다더군요. 저도 로마로 갑니다, 거기에 친구들이 있거든요. 제가 선생께 끼치기로 작정한 그 무례를, 여기가 아니라 거기서 시작하게 해 주십시오. 여기 머무는 동안에는 우리 모두 정신없이 바쁠 테니까요. 그리고 베네치아에 저만큼 팔꿈치 성한 가난뱅이는 없습니다만, 저는 아직 제 안의 아마추어 기질을 다 빼내지 못해서요—보시다시피 또 업계 얘기로군요!—몇 푼 안 되는 돈만 보고 서둘러 주문대로 작업에 들러붙진 못합니다.\"\n\n이 말들도 앞의 말들 못지않게 도릿 씨에게 호의적으로 받아들여졌다. 그것은 가우언 부부를 처음으로 만찬에 맞이하는 일의 서곡이었고, 그 말들은 가우언을 새 집안에서 그의 늘 그러한 위치에 능란하게 자리 잡게 해 주었다.\n\n그의 아내 또한 그 말들이 그녀의 늘 그러한 위치에 자리 잡게 했다. 패니 양은 특히 또렷하게 이해했으니, 가우언 부인의 고운 용모가 남편에게 무척 비싼 대가를 치르게 했다는 것, 그녀를 두고 배너클 집안에 큰 소동이 있었다는 것, 그리고 미망인 가우언 부인이 거의 가슴이 찢어진 채로 모성의 정에 눌려 굴복하기 전까지는 그 결혼에 단호히 반대했다는 것이었다. 제너럴 부인 역시 그 애정이 집안에 많은 슬픔과 불화를 일으켰다는 것을 분명히 이해했다. 정직한 미글스 씨에 대해서는 아무런 언급도 없었다. 다만 그런 부류의 사람이 제 딸을 자신의 미천함에서 끌어올리고 싶어 하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이며, 그러려고 최선을 다한다 해서 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없으리라는 정도였다.\n\n이렇게 손쉽게 받아들여진 믿음의 그 고운 대상에 대한 리틀 도릿의 관심은 너무도 진실하고 세심해서 정확한 관찰에서 어긋나지 않았다. 그녀는 그것이 가우언 부인이 그 아래에서 살아가는 그 그림자의 기운을 그녀에게 드리우는 데 한몫한다는 것을 볼 수 있었고, 심지어 그 안에 진실이라곤 털끝만큼도 없다는 것을 본능적으로 알았다. 그러나 그것은 자두와 프리즘 학파 사람들로 하여금 가우언 부인에게 지나치게 깍듯하되 그리 가깝지는 않게 만듦으로써, 그녀가 가우언 부인과 가까워지는 길에 장애물을 놓는 영향을 끼쳤다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 그 학교의 떠밀려 들어간 사이저로서, 그 규율에 겸허히 자신을 내맡겨야만 했다.\n\n* 사이저: 옛 케임브리지 대학에서 잡일을 하며 학비를 면제받던 가난한 학생.\n그럼에도 두 사람 사이에는 이미 공감의 이해가 자리 잡고 있어서, 더 큰 어려움도 넘어서게 해 주고 더 제약된 교류에서도 우정을 빚어낼 만했다. 마치 우연들이 그 우정에 우호적이기로 작정이라도 한 듯, 두 사람은 서로가 파리의 블랑두아에게 품고 있음을 알아챈 그 혐오감에서 새로운 동질감의 확신을 얻었으니, 그것은 파충류 부류의 역겨운 생물에 대한 타고난 반감의 거부감과 공포에 이르는 혐오였다.\n\n그리고 이 능동적인 동질감 말고도 두 사람 사이에는 수동적인 동질감이 있었다. 블랑두아는 두 사람에게 똑같은 방식으로 행동했고, 두 사람을 대하는 그의 태도에는 한결같이 무언가가 깃들어 있었는데, 그것이 다른 이들을 대하는 그의 거동과는 다르다는 것을 둘 다 알았다. 그 차이는 표현이 너무도 미묘하여 다른 이들은 알아챌 수 없었으나, 두 사람은 그것이 거기 있음을 알았다. 그의 사악한 눈의 단순한 한 가닥 버릇, 매끄러운 흰 손의 단순한 한 번 놀림, 그의 얼굴이 가장 자주 짓는 움직임에서 코끝이 처지고 콧수염이 들리는 그 머리카락 한 올만큼의 단순한 변화가, 두 사람에게 똑같이, 그들 자신을 겨냥한 어떤 으스댐을 전했다. 그것은 마치 그가 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"내겐 이쪽으로 은밀한 힘이 있지. 나는 내가 아는 것을 알고 있어.\"\n\n이 점을 둘이 그토록 크게 느낀 적도, 또 서로 상대가 그것을 느낀다는 것을 그토록 완벽하게 안 적도, 그가 베네치아를 떠나기 전 작별 인사를 하러 도릿 씨의 집에 온 그날만큼 일찍이 없었다. 가우언 부인도 마침 같은 목적으로 거기 와 있었고, 그는 둘이 함께 있는 자리에 들이닥쳤다. 나머지 식구들은 외출 중이었다. 두 사람이 함께 있은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았는데, 그 특유의 태도가 그들에게 이렇게 전하는 듯했다. \"당신들 내 얘기를 하려던 참이었지. 하! 그걸 막으려고 내가 여기 떡하니 와 있는 거야!\"\n\n\"가우언이 여기로 옵니까?\" 블랑두아가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n가우언 부인은 그가 오지 않는다고 대답했다.\n\n\"오지 않는다고요!\" 블랑두아가 말했다. \"그럼 여기를 나가실 때, 댁의 충직한 종이 댁까지 모시게 허락해 주시지요.\"\n\n\"고맙지만, 저는 집으로 가지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"집으로 가지 않으신다고요!\" 블랑두아가 말했다. \"그럼 저는 가엾이 버려진 신세로군요.\"\n\n그럴지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그는 자리를 떠 두 사람을 함께 남겨 둘 만큼 가엾이 버려진 신세는 아니었다. 그는 자기의 가장 멋들어진 찬사와 가장 빼어난 화술로 두 사람을 즐겁게 해 주려 앉아 있었으나, 그러면서도 줄곧 이렇게 전했다. \"안 되지, 안 돼, 안 돼, 사랑스러운 숙녀분들. 내가 여기 있는 건 바로 그걸 막기 위해서요!\"\n\n그가 어찌나 의미심장하게 그것을 전하던지, 또 그에게 어찌나 악마 같은 끈질김이 있던지, 마침내 가우언 부인이 떠나려고 일어섰다. 그가 가우언 부인을 계단 아래로 인도하려 손을 내밀자, 그녀는 리틀 도릿의 손을 자기 손에 조심스레 꼭 쥔 채로 말했다. \"아니요, 괜찮습니다. 다만 제 뱃사공이 와 있는지 좀 봐 주시면 고맙겠어요.\"\n\n이로써 그는 두 사람보다 먼저 내려가는 수밖에 없게 되었다. 그가 모자를 손에 들고 그렇게 내려가는 사이, 가우언 부인이 속삭였다.\n\n\"저 사람이 그 개를 죽였어요.\"\n\n\"가우언 씨도 그걸 아나요?\" 리틀 도릿이 속삭였다.\n\n\"아무도 몰라요. 나를 보지 말아요, 그를 봐요. 곧 얼굴을 돌릴 테니까. 아무도 모르지만, 나는 그가 그랬다고 확신해요. 당신도 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"저—저도 그렇게 생각해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다.\n\n\"헨리는 그를 좋아해서 그를 나쁘게 보려 하지 않아요. 그이 자신이 워낙 너그럽고 솔직하거든요. 하지만 당신과 나는 그를 그가 마땅히 받을 만큼으로 본다고 확신하죠. 그는 헨리에게, 그 개가 그렇게 돌변해 자기에게 달려들었을 때 이미 독에 당한 상태였다고 우겼어요. 헨리는 그걸 믿지만, 우리는 안 믿어요. 보니 그가 귀를 기울이고 있긴 한데, 들리진 않는 모양이에요. 잘 가요, 내 사랑! 잘 가요!\"\n\n마지막 말들은 큰 소리로 했는데, 빈틈없는 블랑두아가 걸음을 멈추고 고개를 돌려 계단 밑에서 두 사람을 올려다본 때였다. 분명 그때 그는, 비록 더없이 정중한 표정을 짓고 있긴 했으나, 마치 어떤 진정한 박애주의자라도 그의 목에 커다란 돌을 묶어, 그가 서 있는 그 어두운 아치형 통로 너머로 흐르는 물속에 그를 떨어뜨리는 것보다 더 좋은 소일거리를 바랄 수 없을 만한 그런 모습이었다. 인류를 위한 그런 은인이 그 자리에 없었으므로, 그는 가우언 부인을 그녀의 배에 태워 드린 뒤, 그 배가 좁은 시야 밖으로 쏜살같이 사라질 때까지 거기 서 있었다. 그러고는 자기 배에 몸을 실어 뒤따랐다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 이따금, 그리고 지금 계단을 되짚어 올라가면서 다시, 그가 아버지의 집에 너무 쉽사리 발을 들였다고 생각했다. 그러나 도릿 씨가 큰딸의 사교계 열병에 가담한 탓에 그토록 많고 갖가지인 사람들이 똑같이 그러했으므로, 그것은 딱히 예외적인 경우라 할 수 없었다. 자신들의 부와 권세를 각인시킬 상대를 사귀려는 완전한 광기가 도릿 가를 사로잡고 있었다.\n\n대체로 리틀 도릿 자신이 보기에, 그들이 사는 바로 이 사교계는 한층 고급스러운 종류의 마셜시와 무척이나 닮아 있었다. 수많은 사람들이 외국으로 나오는 모양새가, 사람들이 그 감옥으로 들어오던 것과 거의 다를 바 없어 보였다. 빚 때문에, 게으름 때문에, 인척 관계 때문에, 호기심 때문에, 그리고 본국에서 도무지 잘 살아가지 못하는 전반적인 부적응 때문에 말이다. 채무자들이 감옥으로 끌려 들어왔던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그들도 안내인들과 현지의 추종자들에게 호송되어 이 외국 도시들로 끌려 들어왔다. 그들은 그 옛날의 음울한 감옥 마당에서나 다름없는 태도로 교회며 미술관을 어슬렁거렸다. 그들은 으레 내일이나 다음 주면 다시 떠날 참이었고, 좀처럼 제 마음을 알지 못했으며, 하겠다던 일을 하는 일도, 가겠다던 곳으로 가는 일도 드물었다. 이 모든 점에서 또한 감옥의 채무자들과 무척이나 닮아 있었다. 그들은 변변찮은 숙소에 비싼 값을 치르면서도, 어느 곳을 좋아하는 척하면서 그곳을 헐뜯었다. 이는 영락없는 마셜시의 풍습이었다. 그들이 떠날 때면, 떠나기 싫지 않은 척하며 뒤에 남는 이들이 그들을 부러워했다. 이 또한 한결같은 마셜시의 습성이었다. 대학과 스너거리가 그 감옥에 속하던 것만큼이나 여행객들에게 속한 일정한 어휘와 어구가 늘 그들의 입에 붙어 있었다. 그들은 죄수들이 그러했던 것과 똑같이 무엇 하나에도 진득하게 자리 잡지 못하는 무능을 보였다. 그들은 죄수들이 그러했듯이 서로를 오히려 망가뜨렸다. 그리고 그들은 단정치 못한 옷차림을 하고 흐트러진 생활 방식에 빠져들었다. 여전히, 한결같이 마셜시 사람들과 닮은 채로.\n\n그 집안이 베네치아에 머무르던 기간도 어느덧 끝에 이르렀고, 그들은 수행원들을 거느린 채 로마로 옮겨 갔다. 앞서 거쳐 온 이탈리아 풍경의 반복을 지나, 갈수록 더 지저분하고 더 황량해지는 그 길을 통해, 마침내 공기 그 자체가 병들어 있는 곳에까지 이르러, 그들은 목적지로 나아갔다. 그들을 위해 코르소 가에 멋진 거처가 마련되어 있어, 그들은 거기에 거처를 잡았다. 그 도시에서는, 영원한 법칙을 따라 그 찬란히 무수한 분수에서 쏟아져 흐르고 굽이치는 물을 빼고는, 모든 것이 다른 무언가의 폐허 위에서 영원히 멈춰 서 있으려 애쓰는 듯 보였다.\n\n여기서 리틀 도릿이 보기에, 그들 사교계의 마셜시 기질에 변화가 찾아왔고, 자두와 프리즘이 우위를 점하게 되었다. 모두가 남의 코르크 의족을 짚고 성 베드로 대성당과 바티칸을 거닐었고, 눈에 보이는 모든 것을 남의 체로 걸러 냈다. 누구도 무엇이 어떻다고 스스로 말하지 않았고, 다들 제너럴 부인들이나 유스터스 씨나 그 밖의 누군가가 그것을 어떻다고 했다더라고만 말했다. 여행객의 무리 전체가 손발이 묶인 채 자진해 바쳐진 인간 제물들의 집합 같았으니, 유스터스 씨와 그 시종들에게 넘겨져, 그 신성한 사제단의 취향에 맞게 자기 지성의 내장을 정리당하는 처지였다. 옛 시절의 신전과 무덤과 궁전과 원로원과 극장과 원형 경기장의 울퉁불퉁한 잔해 사이를, 혀가 묶이고 눈이 가려진 현대인들의 무리가 조심조심 더듬어 길을 찾아가며, 정해진 형식에 맞게 입술을 모으려 애쓰느라 끊임없이 자두와 프리즘을 되뇌고 있었다. 제너럴 부인은 자기의 순수한 본령에 들어선 셈이었다. 누구도 자기 의견이라곤 없었다. 그녀 둘레로 놀라운 규모의 겉면 형성이 진행 중이었고, 거기에는 용기나 정직한 자유 발언이라곤 한 점의 흠집도 없었다.\n\n자두와 프리즘의 또 다른 변형이 그들이 도착한 직후 곧 리틀 도릿의 눈에 슬며시 들어왔다. 그들은 머들 부인의 이른 방문을 받았는데, 그 부인은 그해 겨울 이 영원의 도시에서 그 광대한 사교 영역을 주름잡고 있었다. 그리고 그 자리에서 그녀와 패니가 서로 능란하게 칼을 맞대고 견주는 모습은, 그 조용한 동생의 눈을 거의 깜박이게 할 지경이었으니, 마치 작은 검들이 번뜩이는 듯했다.\n\n\"이렇게 기쁠 데가요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"마르티니에서 그토록 불길하게 시작된 인연을 다시 잇게 되다니요.\"\n\n\"마르티니, 그렇고말고요,\" 패니가 말했다. \"정말이지, 황홀하답니다!\"\n\n\"제 아들 에드먼드 스파클러에게서 듣자니,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"그 애가 그 우연한 기회를 벌써 잘 살렸다더군요. 베네치아에 아주 푹 빠져서 돌아왔답니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 무심한 패니가 받았다. \"오래 거기 있었나요?\"\n\n\"그 질문은 도릿 씨께 돌려야 할까 보네요,\" 머들 부인이 그 가슴을 도릿 씨 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다. \"에드먼드가 그곳 체류를 즐겁게 보내도록 그분께 어찌나 큰 신세를 졌는지요.\"\n\n\"오, 부디 그런 말씀 마십시오,\" 패니가 대꾸했다. \"아빠가 스파클러 씨를 두어 번 초대하는 기쁨을 누리신 것 같긴 하지만요—하지만 그건 아무것도 아니었어요. 저희 주변에 사람이 어찌나 많았고 손님을 어찌나 자유로이 맞았던지, 설령 아빠가 그런 기쁨을 누리셨다 한들, 그건 아무것도 아닌 것만도 못한 일이었답니다.\"\n\n\"다만, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"다만—하—머들 씨처럼 그토록 출중하고 제왕다운 인물에 대해—하, 흠—이 세상 나머지 사람들과—하—다름없이 내가 품고 있는 그 높은 평가를, 아무리 사소하고 보잘것없는 수단으로나마—흠—드러낼 수 있었다는 것이 나에게 유다른 기쁨을 안겨 주었던 만큼은 예외다만.\"\n\n그 가슴은 이 찬사를 더없이 매혹적인 태도로 받아들였다. \"머들 씨는 말이죠,\" 패니가 스파클러 씨를 뒷전으로 물려 보내려는 수단으로 말했다. \"아셔야 할 게, 아빠가 늘 입에 올리시는 화제랍니다, 머들 부인.\"\n\n\"저는—하—실망했습니다, 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"스파클러 씨에게서 듣자니 머들 씨께서 외국으로 나오실—흠—가망이 그리 크지 않다고 하니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그러게나요, 정말,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"그이는 워낙 일에 매여 있고 찾는 데가 많아서, 안 될까 봐 걱정이에요. 그이는 몇 해째 외국에 나오질 못했답니다. 도릿 양은, 제가 알기로, 오랫동안 거의 줄곧 외국에 계셨다지요.\"\n\n\"오 그럼요,\" 패니가 더없이 뻔뻔하게 늘어뜨려 말했다. \"엄청나게 오랜 세월을요.\"\n\n\"그러리라 짐작했어요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇답니다,\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 바라건대,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다. \"이쪽 알프스나 지중해 이편에서 머들 씨와 알고 지내는—흠—큰 영광을 누리지 못한다 하더라도, 영국으로 돌아가면 그 영광을 누리게 되기를요. 그것은 제가 각별히 바라고 또 각별히 귀히 여길 영광입니다.\"\n\n\"머들 씨도,\" 안경 너머로 패니를 흠모하듯 바라보고 있던 머들 부인이 말했다. \"분명 그에 못지않게 귀히 여길 거예요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은, 이제는 혼자가 아니었으나 여전히 버릇처럼 생각에 잠겨 외로이 지내던 터라, 처음에는 이것을 그저 자두와 프리즘이려니 여겼다. 그러나 머들 부인 댁의 화려한 연회에 다녀온 뒤, 아버지가 제 집안의 아침 식탁에서까지 머들 씨와 알고 지내고 싶다는 바람을 거듭 읊으며, 그 비범한 인물의 조언을 빌려 자기 재산을 운용하는 데 덕을 볼지도 모른다는 속셈을 내비치자, 그녀는 거기에 실제 의미가 담겨 있다고 여기기 시작했고, 자기 나름으로도 이 시대의 그 빛나는 인물을 보고 싶다는 호기심을 품게 되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’\n\n베네치아의 물결과 로마의 폐허가 도릿 일가의 즐거움을 위해 햇볕을 쬐고 있었고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 여행자의 연필에 의해 날마다 온갖 지상의 비례와 윤곽과 닮음을 벗어난 모습으로 사생되고 있는 동안, 도이스 클레넘 상회는 블리딩 하트 야드에서 쉴 새 없이 망치질을 했고, 쇠와 쇠가 부딪는 힘찬 쟁그랑 소리가 작업 시간 내내 그곳에서 들려왔다.\n\n이 무렵 젊은 동업자는 사업을 견실한 궤도에 올려놓았고, 자기만의 독창적인 궁리를 자유로이 좇게 된 손위 동업자는 공장의 명성을 드높이는 데 크게 이바지했다. 독창적인 사람으로서 그는, 오랜 세월 동안 지배 권력이 어떤 수단으로든 이런 부류의 죄인들 앞길에 놓을 수 있었던 온갖 낙담거리를 마주할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그것은 권력 측의 합당한 자기방어에 지나지 않았으니, 일을 하는 법은 일을 하지 않는 법의 자연스럽고도 불구대천의 적으로 여겨질 수밖에 없었기 때문이다. 바로 여기에, 에두름국이 이를 악물고 손톱이 닳도록 떠받든 그 현명한 제도의 토대가 있었다 — 독창적인 영국 신민이라면 누구든 제 목숨을 걸고나 독창적이 되라고 경고하고, 그를 괴롭히고 방해하고, (그의 구제책을 불확실하고 비싸게 만듦으로써) 강도들을 불러들여 그를 약탈하게 하고, 잘해 봐야 짧은 향유 기간이 지난 뒤 그의 재산을 몰수하는 — 마치 발명이 중죄와 동급이라도 되는 양 말이다. 이 제도는 한결같이 배너클 일가의 큰 호의를 누렸고, 그 또한 합당한 일에 지나지 않았다. 가치 있게 발명하는 자는 반드시 진지하기 마련인데, 배너클 일가가 진지함만큼 혐오하고 두려워하는 것은 달리 없었기 때문이다. 그것 역시 매우 합당했다. 막대한 양의 진지함이라는 재앙에 시달리는 나라에서라면, 머지않아 어떤 자리에든 들러붙어 있는 배너클이 단 하나도 남지 않게 될지도 모르는 일이니까.\n\n대니얼 도이스는 거기에 딸린 고통과 형벌을 모두 떠안은 채 자신의 처지를 마주했고, 일 그 자체를 위해 묵묵히 일을 계속했다. 클레넘은 진심 어린 협력으로 그를 격려하며 정신적 지주가 되어 주었고, 그 밖에도 사업상의 관계에서 훌륭한 역할을 해냈다. 사업은 번창했고, 두 동업자는 둘도 없는 벗이 되었다.\n\n그러나 대니얼은 그토록 오랜 세월 품어 온 그 옛 설계를 잊을 수 없었다. 그가 그것을 잊으리라 기대하는 것은 이치에 맞지 않았다. 만약 그가 그것을 가벼이 잊을 수 있는 사람이었다면, 애초에 그것을 착안하지도, 그것을 완성해 낼 인내와 끈기를 지니지도 못했을 것이다. 클레넘은 이따금 어느 저녁에 그가 모형과 도면을 들여다보다가, 다시 그것들을 치우며 한숨과 함께 그 물건이 예나 지금이나 변함없이 옳다고 중얼거리며 스스로를 위로하는 모습을 볼 때면 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n그토록 많은 노력과 그토록 큰 실망에 아무런 공감도 보이지 않는 것은, 클레넘이 동업 관계의 암묵적 의무 가운데 하나로 여기던 바를 저버리는 일이 될 터였다. 에두름국 문 앞에서 우연히 일깨워졌던 그 주제에 대한 일시적 관심이 되살아난 것은 이런 마음에서 비롯되었다. 그는 동업자에게 그 발명을 설명해 달라고 청하면서, \"내가 직공이 아니라는 점을 너그러이 헤아려 주시오, 도이스\" 하고 단서를 달았다.\n\n\"직공이 아니라니요?\" 도이스가 말했다. \"당신이 마음만 먹었다면 완벽한 직공이 되었을 겁니다. 이런 것들을 이해하는 머리로 말하자면, 내가 만나 본 누구 못지않게 좋은 머리를 가지셨소.\"\n\n\"안타깝게도 전혀 교육받지 못한 머리지요.\" 클레넘이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그건 모를 일이오.\" 도이스가 대답했다. \"그리고 나는 당신이 그런 말을 하지 않았으면 합니다. 두루 향상되고 스스로를 향상시킨 분별 있는 사람이라면, 어떤 것에 대해서도 전혀 교육받지 못했다고 말할 수는 없는 법이지요. 나는 비밀스러운 것을 특별히 좋아하지 않소. 공정하고 명료한 설명만 주어진다면, 내가 말한 자질을 갖춘 사람이기만 하다면, 어느 부류의 사람에게 판단받든 마찬가지요.\"\n\n\"어쨌든,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"이건 마치 서로 치사를 주고받는 것처럼 들리지만, 우리 둘 다 그렇지 않다는 걸 알지요. 어쨌든 나는 가능한 한 평이한 설명을 듣는 이점을 누리게 되겠군요.\"\n\n\"그럼!\" 대니얼이 그 특유의 차분하고 한결같은 어조로 말했다. \"그렇게 되도록 애써 보겠소.\"\n\n그는 그런 성격의 사람에게서 흔히 발견되는 능력, 곧 자기 자신이 인식하고 의도한 바를, 그것이 자기 마음에 와닿은 그 직접적인 힘과 또렷함 그대로 설명해 내는 능력을 지니고 있었다. 그의 시연 방식은 어찌나 정연하고 깔끔하고 단순한지, 그를 오해하기란 쉽지 않았다. 그가 몽상가일 것이라는 막연하고 통념적인 관념과, 도면 위를 옮겨 다니는 그의 눈과 엄지의 정확하고 명민한 움직임, 특정 지점에서의 끈기 있는 멈춤, 설명의 작은 물줄기들을 거슬러 짚어 가야 할 다른 지점들로의 신중한 되돌아감, 그리고 중요한 단계마다 듣는 이를 한 줄 너비라도 더 앞으로 데려가기에 앞서 모든 것을 옳고 견실하게 다져 놓는 그 한결같은 태도 — 이 둘의 완전한 양립 불가능함에는 거의 우스꽝스러운 데가 있었다. 그가 자신의 설명에서 스스로를 지워 버리는 태도 또한 그에 못지않게 주목할 만했다. 그는 결코 내가 이런 응용을 발견했다거나 저런 조합을 발명했다고 말하지 않았다. 오히려 그것을 마치 신성한 장인이 만들어 놓은 것을 자기가 우연히 찾아낸 것처럼 통째로 보여 주었다. 그만큼 그는 그것에 관해 겸손했고, 그것을 향한 조용한 감탄에는 그토록 흐뭇한 경의의 빛이 어려 있었으며, 그것이 반박할 수 없는 법칙 위에 세워졌다고 그토록 차분히 확신하고 있었다.\n\n그날 저녁뿐만 아니라 그 뒤로 며칠 저녁 동안, 클레넘은 이 탐구에 완전히 매료되었다. 그것을 좇으면 좇을수록, 그 위로 숙인 잿빛 머리와 그 일에 대한 기쁨과 애정으로 빛나는 그 명민한 눈 — 비록 그것은 열두 해라는 긴 세월 동안 그의 마음을 후벼 파는 도구가 되어 왔지만 — 을 자주 흘끗거릴수록, 한 번 더 애써 보지도 않고 그것을 놓아 버리는 일을 자신의 젊은 혈기와 화해시키기가 점점 어려워졌다. 마침내 그가 말했다.\n\n\"도이스, 결국 이렇게 된 거로군요 — 그 사업을, 하늘만이 아는 숱한 난파선들과 함께 가라앉히거나, 아니면 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작하거나?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 도이스가 대답했다. \"그게 바로 그 귀족 나리들과 신사 양반들이 열두 해 만에 만들어 낸 결론이었지요.\"\n\n\"꽤나 잘난 양반들이로군요!\" 클레넘이 쓰라리게 말했다.\n\n\"흔한 일이지요!\" 도이스가 말했다. \"이렇게 많은 무리 가운데 하나인 처지에, 내 스스로를 순교자로 만들 수야 없지요.\"\n\n\"포기하거나, 아니면 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작하거나라.\" 클레넘이 곰곰이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"바로 그게 요점이고 전부였소.\" 도이스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 친구여,\" 클레넘이 벌떡 일어나 그의 노동으로 거칠어진 손을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"처음부터 다시 시작합시다!\"\n\n도이스는 깜짝 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 그로서는 보기 드물게 황급히 대답했다. \"아니, 아니오. 그냥 치워 두는 게 낫소. 훨씬 낫지요. 언젠가는 세상이 알아줄 거요. 나는 그걸 치워 둘 수 있소. 잊으셨군요, 친애하는 클레넘. 나는 이미 그걸 치워 두었소. 다 끝난 일이오.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 도이스.\" 클레넘이 대답했다. \"당신의 노력과 퇴짜에 관한 한 끝난 일이라는 건 나도 인정하지요. 하지만 내 노력에 관해선 아니오. 나는 당신보다 젊고, 그 잘난 관청에 발을 들인 게 딱 한 번뿐이라 그들에게는 신선한 먹잇감이지요. 자! 내가 그들을 한번 시험해 보겠소. 당신은 우리가 함께한 이래 해 오던 그대로만 하시오. 나는 (얼마든지 할 수 있는 일이니) 지금까지 내가 해 오던 일에다, 당신을 위해 공공의 정의를 받아 내려는 시도를 보태겠소. 그래서 보고할 만한 성과가 없거든, 다시는 이 이야기를 꺼내지 않으리다.\"\n\n대니얼 도이스는 여전히 동의하기를 꺼렸고, 그냥 치워 두는 편이 낫다고 거듭거듭 우겼다. 그러나 그가 점차 클레넘의 설득에 못 이겨 양보하게 된 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 그는 끝내 양보했다. 그리하여 아서는 에두름국을 상대로 길을 뚫어 보려는, 그 길고도 가망 없는 노고를 다시 시작했다.\n\n그 부서의 대기실들은 곧 그의 출입에 익숙해지기 시작했고, 그는 대개 그곳 수위들에 의해 마치 소매치기가 경찰서로 끌려가듯 그 안으로 안내되었다. 다만 주된 차이라면, 후자의 공무는 소매치기를 붙잡아 두는 것이 목적인 데 반해, 에두름국의 목적은 클레넘을 떼어 내는 것이었다는 점이다. 그러나 그는 그 위대한 부서에 끈질기게 들러붙기로 작심했다. 그리하여 양식 작성, 서신 왕래, 의사록 작성, 비망록 작성, 서명, 부서명, 부부서명, 앞뒤로의 회부, 그리고 옆으로, 가로로, 지그재그로의 회부 작업이 다시 시작되었다.\n\n여기서 에두름국의 한 특징이 떠오르는데, 이 기록에서 아직 언급되지 않은 것이다. 그 감탄스러운 부서가 곤경에 처했을 때, 그러니까 작은 배너클들이 거의 악마에 들린 게 아닌가 의심할 지경인 어느 격분한 국회의원들에게, 개별 사건의 시비를 두고서가 아니라 통째로 가증스럽고 정신병원 같은 기관이라며 공격당했을 때, 그러면 하원에서 그 부서를 대표하는 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클이 그 의원을 후려쳐 두 동강 내곤 했으니, 에두름국이 (업무 방지를 위해) 처리한 업무량의 진술로써 그렇게 했다. 그러면 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 몇 개의 수치가 적힌 종이 한 장을 손에 들고, 하원의 허락을 얻어 그것에 주목해 줄 것을 간청하곤 했다. 그러면 하급 배너클들이 명령에 따라 \"옳소, 옳소, 옳소!\" 그리고 \"읽으시오!\"라고 외쳐 댔다. 그러면 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 이렇게 인지하곤 했으니, 의장 각하, 가장 비뚤어진 정신에까지도 확신을 안겨 주리라 여겨지는 이 작은 문서로부터 (배너클 송사리들로부터 조롱 섞인 폭소와 환호) 알 수 있는바, 지난 회계 반년이라는 짧은 기간 안에 이 숱한 비방을 받아 온 부서가 (환호) 일만 오천 통의 편지를 쓰고 받았으며 (큰 환호), 이만 사천 건의 의사록을 작성했고 (더 큰 환호), 삼만 이천 오백 십칠 건의 비망록을 작성했다는 (격렬한 환호) 것이었다. 아니, 이 부서에 관계된 어느 독창적인 신사이자 그 자신이 귀중한 공복이기도 한 분이, 같은 기간 그 안에서 소비된 문구류의 양을 흥미롭게 계산해 주는 호의를 베풀었다. 그것 또한 바로 이 작은 문서의 일부였는데, 그로부터 그는 다음과 같은 주목할 만한 사실을 끌어냈다 — 이 부서가 공무에 바친 대판 양지의 낱장들을 깔면 옥스퍼드가 양쪽 보도를 끝에서 끝까지 포장하고도 거의 사분의 일 마일이 공원 몫으로 남으리라는 것이며 (엄청난 환호와 폭소), 끈으로 말하자면 — 붉은 끈으로 말하자면 — 우아한 꽃 줄 모양으로 하이드 파크 코너에서 중앙우체국까지 늘일 만큼 충분히 썼다는 것이었다. 그런 다음, 관변의 환희가 터져 나오는 가운데, 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 그 의원의 토막 난 잔해를 전장에 남겨 둔 채 자리에 앉곤 했다. 그토록 모범적인 박살을 당한 뒤로는, 에두름국이 일을 많이 하면 할수록 정작 이루어지는 일은 적어진다든가, 불행한 대중에게 그것이 베풀 수 있는 최대의 축복은 아무 일도 하지 않는 것이라든가 하는 암시를 감히 내비칠 만한 배짱을 가진 자가 아무도 없었다.\n\n이제 이 추가 임무까지 떠안아 손에 일이 넉넉해진 — 그런 임무로 말하자면, 그의 시대 이전에 숱하게 많은 유능한 사람들이 그 때문에 목숨을 잃었던 그런 임무였다 — 아서 클레넘은 별다른 변화 없는 삶을 살았다. 어머니의 음울한 병실로의 정기적인 방문과, 트위크넘의 미글스 씨를 찾는 그에 못지않게 정기적인 방문이, 여러 달 동안 그 삶의 유일한 변화였다.\n\n그는 슬프고도 사무치게 리틀 도릿을 그리워했다. 그녀가 무척 그리울 것이라고 각오는 하고 있었으나, 이토록 그리울 줄은 몰랐다. 그녀의 정겹고 자그마한 모습이 그의 삶에서 사라졌을 때 얼마나 큰 자리가 텅 비게 되는지를, 그는 오직 겪어 봄으로써 비로소 온전히 알게 되었다. 또한 그는 그녀가 돌아오리라는 희망을 단념해야 한다고 느꼈으니, 그 가족의 성격을 충분히 잘 이해한 터라 그와 그녀가 넓은 단절의 들판으로 갈라져 있음을 확신했기 때문이다. 그가 그녀에게 품었던 옛 관심과, 그를 향한 그녀의 옛 신뢰는 그의 마음속에서 우울한 빛으로 물들어 있었다. 그토록 빨리 변화가 그것들 위로 몰래 깃들었고, 그토록 빨리 그것들은 다른 은밀한 애틋함과 더불어 과거 속으로 미끄러져 갔던 것이다.\n\n그녀의 편지를 받았을 때 그는 크게 감동했으나, 그렇다고 그녀가 거리 이상의 무언가로 자기와 멀리 갈라져 있다는 것을 덜 절실히 느낀 것은 아니었다. 그 편지는 그 가족이 자기에게 할당한 자리를 더 또렷하고 예리하게 깨닫게 해 주었다. 그는 자신이 그녀의 감사 어린 기억 속에 은밀히 소중히 간직되어 있다는 것, 그리고 그들이 그 감옥과 거기에 딸린 나머지 모든 것과 함께 자기에게 원한을 품고 있다는 것을 알았다.\n\n그의 삶의 하루하루마다 그녀를 둘러싸고 몰려드는 이 모든 사색을 거치면서도, 그는 여전히 옛 방식대로 그녀를 다르게 생각했다. 그녀는 그의 순결한 벗이요, 그의 가냘픈 아이요, 그의 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿이었다. 바로 이 처지의 변화가, 장미꽃이 떠내려가던 그날 밤 시작된 습관 — 자신을 실제 나이보다 훨씬 더 늙은 사람으로 여기는 습관 — 과 묘하게 맞아떨어졌다. 그는 어떤 시점에서 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그 시점은 비록 다정하긴 했으나 그 아득함으로 말미암아 그녀에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 고통이 되리라는 것을 그는 거의 생각하지 못했다. 그는 그녀의 미래의 운명에 관하여, 그리고 그녀가 맞이할 남편에 관하여 헤아려 보았는데, 그것은 그녀를 향한 애정에서 비롯된 것이었으되 만일 그녀가 그것을 알았더라면 그녀의 마음에서 희망의 가장 소중한 한 방울까지 말려 버리고 그 마음을 부수어 버렸을 그런 애정이었다.\n\n그를 둘러싼 모든 것이, 그가 자신을 노년의 남자로 — 미니 가우언의 경우에 그가 떨쳐 내려 애썼던 그런 갈망 따위는 (그 일도 달과 계절로 따지자면 그리 오래된 것은 아니었지만) 이미 영영 떠나 버린 그런 남자로 — 바라보는 습관을 굳히는 쪽으로 작용했다. 그녀의 아버지, 어머니와 맺은 관계는 마치 사위였다가 홀아비가 된 사람이 섰을 법한 그런 관계와도 같았다. 만일 죽은 쌍둥이 자매가 살아남아 여인의 한창때에 세상을 떠났고 그가 그녀의 남편이었더라면, 미글스 씨 부부와의 교제의 성격은 아마 지금 그대로였을 것이다. 이것이 알게 모르게, 자신은 인생의 그 부분과는 끝을 냈고 떨쳐 버렸다는 그의 내면의 인상을 습관으로 만드는 데 일조했다.\n\n그는 그들에게서 변함없이 미니의 소식을 들었으니, 그녀가 편지에 자신이 얼마나 행복한지, 남편을 얼마나 사랑하는지를 적어 보낸다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 화제와 떼려야 뗄 수 없이, 그는 변함없이 미글스 씨의 얼굴에 어린 그 옛 그늘을 보았다. 미글스 씨는 결혼 이후로 예전만큼 환한 적이 없었다. 펫과의 이별에서 끝내 완전히 회복되지 못했던 것이다. 그는 여전히 한결같이 쾌활하고 솔직한 사람이었다. 그러나 마치 그의 얼굴이, 오직 한 가지 표정밖에 보여 줄 수 없는 두 아이의 초상을 하도 자주 향하다 보니 무의식중에 거기서 한 가지 특징을 따 온 것처럼, 그 얼굴에는 이제 온갖 표정의 변화를 거치면서도 늘 상실의 빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n어느 겨울날 토요일, 클레넘이 그 별장에 있을 때, 미망인 가우언 부인이 마차를 타고 당도했는데, 그것은 햄프턴 코트의 그토록 많은 개별 소유자들이 저마다 자기 전용 마차라고 우기는 그 마차였다. 그녀는 초록 부채의 그늘진 매복 속에서 내려와, 미글스 씨 부부에게 방문의 은혜를 베풀었다.\n\n\"그래, 두 분 다 어떻게 지내셨나요, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님?\" 그녀가 자신의 미천한 인척들을 격려하듯 말했다. \"그래, 내 가엾은 아이의 소식이나 그 애에 관한 소식을 마지막으로 들으신 게 언제인가요?\"\n\n내 가엾은 아이란 그녀의 아들이었고, 이렇게 그를 점잖게 일컫는 방식은, 그가 미글스 일가의 술수에 희생되었다는 가식을, 세상 어디에도 거슬릴 데 없이 살려 두는 것이었다.\n\n\"그리고 그 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아이는요?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"내가 아는 것보다 더 최근 소식을 가지고 계신가요?\"\n\n이 또한 미묘하게, 자기 아들이 한낱 미모에 사로잡혀, 그 매혹 아래 온갖 세속적 이득을 다 포기했다는 것을 넌지시 비추는 것이었다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 가우언 부인이, 자기가 받는 대답에는 그다지 주의를 기울이지도 않으면서 말했다. \"그 애들이 여전히 행복하다는 걸 아는 건 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이에요. 내 가엾은 아이는 워낙 들썩이는 성미인 데다, 여기저기 떠돌아다니고 온갖 부류의 사람들 사이에서 변덕스럽고 인기 있는 데 익숙했던 터라, 이건 인생 최대의 위안이지요. 그 애들은 생쥐만큼이나 가난하겠지요, 미글스 아버님?\"\n\n미글스 씨는 그 질문에 안절부절못하며 대답했다. \"그러지 않기를 바랍니다, 부인. 그 애들이 자기네 적은 수입을 잘 꾸려 가기를 바라지요.\"\n\n\"오! 내 가장 친애하는 미글스 씨!\" 부인이 초록 부채로 그의 팔을 톡톡 치고는, 능란하게 그것을 하품과 좌중 사이에 끼워 넣으며 대꾸했다. \"세상 물정에 밝으신 분이자 인간들 중에서도 가장 사업가다운 분이신 당신이 — 당신은 사업가다우시잖아요, 게다가 사업가답지 못한 우리에게는 너무 과할 만큼 그러시니까요 —\"\n\n(이는 미글스 씨를 교활한 책략가로 만들어 버림으로써 앞서의 의도를 다시 좇은 것이었다.)\n\n\"— 어떻게 그 애들이 그 알량한 살림을 꾸려 간다는 말씀을 하실 수가 있어요? 내 가엾고 사랑스러운 아이라니! 그 애가 수백 파운드를 꾸려 간다는 생각이라니! 게다가 그 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아이까지요. 그 애가 살림을 꾸린다는 발상이라니! 미글스 아버님! 그러지 마세요!\"\n\n\"그렇군요, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그렇다면 유감스럽지만 헨리가 분명 제 수입을 미리 당겨 쓰고 있다는 점은 인정해야겠군요.\"\n\n\"내 친애하고 선량한 양반 — 우리는 일종의 친척이니 격식은 차리지 않겠어요. 정말이지, 미글스 어머님,\" 가우언 부인이, 마치 그 어처구니없는 우연이 그제야 처음으로 머릿속에 번뜩 떠오르기라도 한 듯 명랑하게 외쳤다. \"일종의 친척이라니까요! 내 친애하고 선량한 양반, 이 세상에서는 우리 누구도 모든 일을 제 뜻대로 가질 수는 없는 법이지요.\"\n\n이 또한 앞서의 요점을 다시 좇은 것으로, 미글스 씨가 거기까지는 그 깊은 책략으로 눈부신 성공을 거두었음을, 더없이 점잖게 그에게 일러 주는 것이었다. 가우언 부인은 그 한 방이 어찌나 잘 들어맞았다고 여겼는지 거기에 머물러 곱씹었으니, \"모든 일은 아니지요. 아니고말고요. 이 세상에서 우리는 모든 일을 기대해서는 안 되지요, 미글스 아버님\" 하고 되뇌었다.\n\n\"여쭤봐도 되겠습니까, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 얼굴빛이 약간 붉어진 채 받아쳤다. \"대체 누가 모든 일을 기대한다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"오, 아무도, 아무도 안 하지요!\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"내가 말하려던 건 — 그런데 당신이 내 말을 끊으셨군요. 아버님이 끼어드시는 바람에, 내가 무슨 말을 하려고 했더라?\"\n\n그녀는 그 커다란 초록 부채를 늘어뜨린 채, 그것을 곰곰이 생각하며 미글스 씨를 골똘히 바라보았는데, 그 행동은 다소 달아오른 그 신사의 심기를 식히는 데 도움이 되지는 않았다.\n\n\"아! 그래요, 그렇지!\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"내 가엾은 아이가 늘 기대 속에서 살아 온 데 익숙하다는 걸 당신은 기억하셔야 해요. 그 기대가 실현되었을 수도 있고, 실현되지 않았을 수도 있고 —\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 실현되지 않았을 수도 있다고 해 두지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n미망인은 한순간 그에게 성난 눈길을 던졌으나, 머리와 부채를 흔들어 그것을 떨쳐 버리고는, 앞서의 방식대로 자기 이야기의 가락을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그건 아무 차이도 없어요. 내 가엾은 아이는 그런 부류의 것에 익숙했고, 물론 당신도 그걸 알고 그 결과에 대비하고 계셨겠지요. 나 자신은 늘 그 결과를 분명히 내다보았고, 그래서 놀라지 않아요. 그러니 당신도 놀라셔서는 안 돼요. 사실, 놀라실 수가 없지요. 분명 그에 대비하고 계셨을 테니까요.\"\n\n미글스 씨는 아내를, 그리고 클레넘을 바라보더니, 입술을 깨물고는 헛기침을 했다.\n\n\"그런데 이제 내 가엾은 아이가,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다. \"아기를 기대해야 한다는 통지를, 그리고 식구가 그렇게 하나 늘어나는 데 따르는 온갖 비용을 떠안아야 한다는 통지를 받았다는군요! 가엾은 헨리! 하지만 이제는 어쩔 수가 없어요. 이제 와서는 손쓰기엔 너무 늦었지요. 다만 수입을 미리 당겨 쓴다는 이야기를 무슨 대단한 발견인 양 하지는 마세요, 미글스 아버님. 그건 너무 과한 일일 테니까요.\"\n\n\"과하다니요, 부인?\" 미글스 씨가 설명을 구하듯 말했다.\n\n\"자, 자!\" 가우언 부인이 손을 의미심장하게 놀려 그를 자기보다 낮은 자리에 앉히며 말했다. \"내 가엾은 아이의 어미가 이 나이에 견디기엔 너무 과한 일이지요. 그 애들은 단단히 결혼했고, 결혼을 무를 수도 없어요. 자, 자! 그건 나도 알아요! 그걸 굳이 일러 주실 필요 없어요, 미글스 아버님. 나도 아주 잘 알고 있으니까요. 내가 방금 뭐라고 했지요? 그 애들이 여전히 행복하다는 게 큰 위안이라고 했지요. 부디 그 애들이 앞으로도 계속 행복하기를 바랄 뿐이에요. 부디 그 예쁜 아이가 내 가엾은 아이를 행복하게 해 주고 만족하게 해 주려고 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다 하기를 바라요. 미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님, 우리 이 이야기는 더 하지 않는 게 낫겠어요. 우리는 이 문제를 같은 편에서 바라본 적이 한 번도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 못할 테니까요. 자, 자! 이제 나는 착해졌어요.\"\n\n참으로, 이로써 자신의 그 놀랍도록 신화적인 입장을 지키기 위해, 또 미글스 씨에게 동맹의 영예를 너무 헐값에 누리려 해서는 안 된다고 훈계하기 위해 할 수 있는 말은 다 한 터라, 가우언 부인은 나머지는 그만둘 마음이 있었다. 만약 미글스 씨가 미글스 부인의 간청 어린 눈길과 클레넘의 의미심장한 몸짓에 따랐더라면, 그는 그녀를 이런 마음 상태에 흐트러짐 없이 만족하도록 내버려 두었을 것이다. 그러나 펫은 그의 마음의 사랑이자 자랑이었다. 그래서 만일 그가 그녀를, 그녀가 집안의 햇살이었던 시절보다 더 헌신적으로 옹호하거나 더 깊이 사랑할 수 있는 때가 있었다면, 그것은 바로 지금, 곧 집안의 나날의 우아함이요 기쁨이던 그녀를 잃어버린 지금이었을 것이다.\n\n\"가우언 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"저는 평생 솔직한 사람으로 살아왔습니다. 제가 — 제 자신에게든, 다른 누구에게든, 아니면 둘 다에게든 그건 상관없습니다만 — 무슨 점잖은 눈속임을 부려 보려 한다면, 저는 아마 거기서 성공하지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"미글스 아버님,\" 미망인이 상냥한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했는데, 다만 주변 살갗이 더 창백해지면서 두 뺨의 홍조는 평소보다 좀 더 또렷이 도드라져 보였다. \"아마 성공하지 못하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"그러니, 선량하신 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 자신을 억누르려 무진 애를 쓰며 말했다. \"부디 거슬리지 않으시기를 바라며 청하건대, 제게 그런 눈속임을 부리지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n\"미글스 어머님,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"당신네 선량한 양반은 도무지 이해할 수가 없네요.\"\n\n그녀가 그 점잖은 부인에게로 화살을 돌린 것은, 그 부인을 논쟁 속으로 끌어들여 시비를 걸고 굴복시키려는 술수였다. 미글스 씨는 그런 결말을 막으려 끼어들었다.\n\n\"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 서투른 사람이오, 여보. 이건 공정한 싸움이 못 되오. 부디 잠자코 있어 주시오. 자, 가우언 부인, 자! 우리 분별 있게 굴어 봅시다. 호의를 가져 봅시다. 공정해 봅시다. 부인께서 헨리를 가엾이 여기지 않으신다면, 저도 펫을 가엾이 여기지 않겠습니다. 그리고 한쪽으로만 치우치지 마십시오, 부인. 그건 사려 깊지 못하고 친절하지도 못합니다. 펫이 헨리를 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다거나, 심지어 헨리가 펫을 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다거나 하는 말은 하지 맙시다.\" (이 말을 하는 미글스 씨 자신도 행복해 보이지는 않았다.) \"다만 그 애들이 서로를 행복하게 해 주기를 바라도록 합시다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 정말이지, 거기서 그만 두세요, 여보.\" 마음씨 곱고 푸근한 미글스 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 여보, 안 되오.\" 미글스 씨가 대꾸했다. \"거기서 딱 끝낼 수는 없소. 거기서 영 끝낼 수가 없어요. 꼭 대여섯 마디만 더 해야겠소. 가우언 부인, 저는 제가 너무 예민한 사람은 아니기를 바랍니다. 그렇게 보이지는 않을 거라고 믿습니다만.\"\n\n\"정말 그렇게 보이지 않으세요.\" 가우언 부인이, 강조를 위해 머리와 그 커다란 초록 부채를 함께 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인. 그것 참 다행이군요. 그럼에도 불구하고, 저는 좀 — 강한 말을 쓰고 싶지는 않습니다만 — 자, 상처받았다고 해 둘까요?\" 미글스 씨가, 솔직하면서도 절제된 태도로, 화해를 청하는 어조를 담아 단번에 물었다.\n\n\"하고 싶은 대로 말씀하세요.\" 가우언 부인이 대답했다. \"저로서는 전혀 상관없는 일이니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 아니, 그런 말씀 마십시오.\" 미글스 씨가 강권했다. \"그건 다정하게 응하시는 게 아니니까요. 결과가 이미 예견되었다느니, 이제는 너무 늦었다느니 하는 따위의 언급을 들으면 저는 좀 상처를 받습니다.\"\n\n\"그러세요, 미글스 아버님?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"놀랍지도 않네요.\"\n\n\"이런, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 따지듯 말했다. \"저는 적어도 부인께서 놀라시기라도 했으면 했습니다. 이토록 민감한 문제로 저를 고의로 상처 입히는 건 분명 너그러운 일이 못 되니까요.\"\n\n\"저는 당신의 양심에 대해서는,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"책임이 없답니다, 아시잖아요.\"\n\n가엾은 미글스 씨는 경악하여 어안이 벙벙해진 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"제가 불운하게도, 당신 것이며 당신께 꼭 맞는 모자를 하나 지니고 다녀야 하는 처지라 해도,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그 무늬를 두고 저를 탓하지는 마세요, 미글스 아버님, 부디!\"\n\n\"이런, 맙소사, 부인!\" 미글스 씨가 버럭 내뱉었다. \"그건 곧 이렇게 말씀하시는 거나 다름없군요 —\"\n\n\"자, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 아버님.\" 가우언 부인이, 그 신사가 조금이라도 달아오를 때면 으레 더없이 신중하고 사람을 끄는 태도가 되어 말했다. \"혼란을 막기 위해, 당신의 친절을 빌려 제 말을 대신하시게 하느니, 차라리 제가 직접 말하는 편이 낫겠네요. '그건 곧 이렇게 말하는 거나 다름없다'고 당신이 시작하셨지요. 괜찮으시다면, 제가 그 문장을 끝맺지요. 그건 곧 이렇게 말하는 거나 다름없어요 — 제가 그걸 강조하거나 다시 들추려는 게 아니에요, 이제 와선 아무 소용도 없고, 제 유일한 바람은 그저 현재의 상황에서 최선을 다하는 것뿐이니까요 — 처음부터 끝까지 저는 늘 당신네의 이 혼사에 반대했고, 아주 뒤늦게야 더없이 마지못해 동의를 내주었다는 것 말이에요.\"\n\n\"여보!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다. \"이 말을 들었소! 아서! 자네도 이 말을 들었나!\"\n\n\"방이 적당히 넓고,\" 가우언 부인이 부채질을 하며 둘러보면서 말했다. \"또 모든 면에서 대화에 아주 매력적으로 잘 맞으니, 방 어느 구석에서든 제 말이 들릴 거라고 생각합니다만.\"\n\n미글스 씨가 다음에 입을 열 한마디에 자리를 박차고 일어나지 않을 만큼 충분히 의자에 자신을 붙들어 매기까지, 얼마간 침묵의 시간이 흘렀다. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"부인, 저는 그 일들을 다시 들추고 싶은 마음이 정말 없습니다만, 그 불행한 문제에 관해 줄곧 제 견해와 제 처신이 어떠했는지를 부인께 일깨워 드려야겠군요.\"\n\n\"오, 친애하는 선생!\" 가우언 부인이 미소 지으며, 비난 어린 통찰을 담아 머리를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그건 제가 잘 이해하고 있었답니다, 정말이에요.\"\n\n\"부인, 저는,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그때 이전에는 불행이란 걸 알지 못했고, 그때 이전에는 근심이란 걸 알지 못했습니다. 그건 제게 어찌나 고통스러운 시간이었던지 —\" 요컨대 미글스 씨는 더 이상 그 일에 관해 정말로 아무 말도 할 수 없어, 손수건을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다.\n\n\"저는 그 모든 일을 다 이해했어요.\" 가우언 부인이 부채 너머로 침착하게 바라보며 말했다. \"당신이 클레넘 씨께 호소하셨으니, 저 역시 클레넘 씨께 호소해도 되겠지요. 제가 그랬는지 안 그랬는지는 저분이 아시니까요.\"\n\n\"저는 이 논쟁에 어떤 식으로든 끼어드는 것이 정말 내키지 않습니다.\" 좌중 모두의 시선을 받으며 클레넘이 말했다. \"더욱이 저는 헨리 가우언 씨와 더없이 좋은 양해와 더없이 분명한 관계를 지켜 가고자 하기 때문입니다. 저는 정말이지 그런 바람을 품을 아주 강한 이유들이 있습니다. 가우언 부인께서는 그 결혼이 이루어지기 전에 저와 나눈 대화에서, 이 결혼을 추진하려는 어떤 속셈을 여기 계신 제 친구분의 것으로 돌리셨고, 저는 그 오해를 풀어 드리려 애썼습니다. 저는 그가 (제가 알았던 대로, 또 지금도 아는 대로) 견해로나 행동으로나 그 결혼에 한사코 반대했다는 것을 분명히 밝혔습니다.\"\n\n\"보셨지요?\" 가우언 부인이, 마치 자신이 정의의 여신이라도 되는 양 미글스 씨를 향해 두 손바닥을 펼쳐 보이며, 그가 발붙일 데가 없으니 차라리 자백하는 편이 낫겠다고 일러 주듯 말했다. \"보셨지요? 아주 좋아요! 자, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님, 두 분 다!\" 여기서 그녀는 자리에서 일어났다. \"외람되지만 이 자못 만만찮은 논쟁에 종지부를 찍는 것을 허락해 주세요. 저는 이 일의 시비에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않겠어요. 다만, 온갖 경험에서 익히 아는 바를 다시금 입증해 주는 사례라는 것만 말씀드리지요. 이런 부류의 일은 결코 잘되는 법이 없다는 것 — 제 가엾은 아이라면 이렇게 말하겠지만, 결코 수지가 맞는 법이 없다는 것 — 한마디로, 결코 잘 풀리는 법이 없다는 것 말이에요.\"\n\n미글스 씨가 어떤 부류의 일을 말하는 거냐고 물었다.\n\n\"그렇게도 극단적으로 다른 내력을 지닌 사람들이,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"이런 우연하고 혼인이랍시고 하는 식으로 서로 뒤섞여 부대끼게 된 사람들이, 그리고 자기들을 한데 흔들어 놓은 이 고약한 사정을 같은 시각으로 바라볼 수 없는 사람들이, 함께 잘 살아 보려고 애쓰는 건 헛된 일이에요. 그건 결코 잘되는 법이 없어요.\"\n\n미글스 씨가 막 입을 열려 했다. \"부인, 한 말씀 드리도록 허락해 주시면 —\"\n\n\"아니요, 그러지 마세요.\" 가우언 부인이 대꾸했다. \"왜 그러시겠어요! 그건 이미 확인된 사실이니까요. 결코 잘되는 법이 없어요. 그러니 괜찮으시다면, 저는 제 길을 가겠어요. 당신들도 당신들 길을 가시도록 두고요. 저는 언제든 제 가엾은 아이의 예쁜 아내를 기꺼이 맞이할 것이고, 늘 그 애와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내기를 잊지 않을 거예요. 하지만 이런 식의 관계로 말하자면, 반쯤 가족이고 반쯤 남남이며, 반쯤 들이받고 반쯤 지루하게 하는 이런 관계는, 그 실행 불가능함에서 자못 우스꽝스러운 상태를 이루지요. 정말이지, 결코 잘되는 법이 없어요.\"\n\n여기서 미망인은 방 안의 누구에게랄 것도 없이 차라리 방을 향해 미소 띤 채 절을 하고는, 그것으로 미글스 아버님과 미글스 어머님에게 마지막 작별을 고했다. 클레넘이 앞으로 나서서, 햄프턴 코트 궁의 온갖 알약들이 마음껏 쓰는 그 알약통 같은 마차로 그녀를 안내했다. 그녀는 그 마차에 더없이 의젓하고 평온하게 올라타 떠나갔다.\n* 알약통(Pill-Box): 작고 답답한 마차를 빗댄 말장난. 햄프턴 코트 궁에 기거하던 노부인들('알약들')이 공동으로 쓰던 마차를 가리킨다.\n\n그날 이후로 미망인은 가볍고 천연덕스러운 익살로, 자신의 절친한 지인들에게 곧잘 이런 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 자신이 모진 시험을 거친 끝에, 헨리의 아내에게 딸린 그 사람들, 그를 낚으려고 그토록 필사적으로 덤벼들었던 그 사람들과 알고 지낸다는 것이 불가능함을 알게 되었노라고. 그들을 떼어 내는 것이 자신이 애지중지하는 가식에 더 그럴싸한 분위기를 입혀 줄 것이며, 이따금의 불편을 덜어 줄 수 있고, 또 아무런 손실의 위험도 없으리라는 (그 예쁜 아이는 단단히 결혼했고 그 아비는 그 아이에게 헌신하고 있으니) 결론에, 그녀가 미리 도달해 있었는지 어떤지는 그녀 자신만이 가장 잘 알 일이었다. 비록 이 이야기는 그 점에 관해서도 나름의 견해를 지니고 있으니, 단연코 그렇다는 쪽이긴 하지만 말이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 9. Appearance and Disappearance\n\n\"아서, 이 친구야,\" 다음 날 저녁 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"어머니하고 내가 이 일을 곰곰이 의논해 봤는데, 우리는 지금 이대로 있기가 영 마음이 편치 않네그려. 우리의 그 고상한 인척 말이야 — 어제 여기 왔던 그 친애하는 부인 말일세 —\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그 상냥하고 겸허한 사교계의 장식품께서도 말이야,\" 미글스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"우리를 잘못 전할까 봐 걱정이라네. 그분을 위해서라면 우리가 많은 걸 견딜 수도 있지, 아서. 하지만 우리는 그건 견디지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어. 그분에게도 매한가지라면 말이야.\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"계속하시지요.\"\n\n\"보게나,\" 미글스 씨가 이어 갔다. \"그게 우리를 사위와 사이가 틀어지게 만들지도 모르고, 심지어 딸아이와도 틀어지게 만들지도 모르고, 또 집안에 큰 분란을 불러올지도 모른단 말일세. 알겠지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 아서가 대답했다. \"말씀하신 데에는 일리가 많습니다.\" 그는 미글스 부인을 흘끗 보았는데, 그녀는 언제나 선량하고 분별 있는 편에 서 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 정직한 얼굴에서는, 지금 미글스 씨가 기울이는 마음을 아서가 지지해 주기를 바라는 간청이 환히 비쳐 나오고 있었다.\n\n\"그래서 어머니하고 나는 말이야,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"짐이고 보따리고 다 꾸려서, 다시 한번 '알롱제'와 '마르숑제'들 틈으로 떠나 볼까 하는 마음이 아주 굴뚝같다네. 그러니까 내 말은, 훌훌 떠나서 프랑스를 곧장 가로질러 이탈리아로 들어가, 우리 펫을 보고 오자는 거지.\"\n\n\"제 생각에는,\" 미글스 부인의 밝은 얼굴에 어린 어머니다운 기대(그녀는 한때 딸과 무척 닮았음이 틀림없었다)에 마음이 움직여 아서가 대답했다. \"그보다 더 좋은 일을 하실 수는 없을 겁니다. 그리고 제게 조언을 구하신다면, 내일 당장 떠나시라는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"정말 그런가, 진짜로?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"여보, 이거 우리 생각에 든든한 지지를 얻은 셈이구려!\"\n\n어머니는, 클레넘에게 그가 무척 흐뭇하게 느낄 만한 방식으로 고마움을 전하는 눈빛으로, 정말 그렇다고 대답했다.\n\n\"게다가 사실은 말일세, 아서,\" 미글스 씨가 말했고, 그 옛 먹구름이 그의 얼굴 위로 다시 드리워졌다. \"내 사위가 또 빚을 졌단 말이지. 그러니 내가 또 그걸 갚아 줘야 할 모양이야. 이 점만 봐도, 내가 거기 한번 건너가서 친구로서 그를 살펴보는 게 좋을 것 같네. 그리고 또, 여기 어머니가 펫의 건강 상태를 두고 어리석을 만큼(그러면서도 자연스럽게도) 안절부절못하고 있다네. 게다가 이런 시기에 그 애가 외로움을 느끼도록 내버려 둬서는 안 된다는 거지. 부인할 수 없는 일이야, 아서, 거긴 너무 멀고, 이 모든 사정을 생각하면 가엾은 우리 딸에게는 낯선 곳이라네. 그곳에서 어떤 귀부인 못지않게 잘 보살핌을 받는다 해도, 그래도 너무 멀어. 집이 아무리 초라해도 집은 집인 것처럼, 그러니까 자네도 알겠지만,\" 미글스 씨가 그 속담에 새 변주를 덧붙이며 말했다. \"로마가 아무리 로마답지 않아도, 로마는 로마인 거야.\"\n\n\"전부 지당하신 말씀입니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그리고 전부 떠나실 충분한 이유가 되고요.\"\n\n\"자네가 그렇게 생각해 주니 기쁘군. 그걸로 마음이 정해졌네. 여보, 준비를 시작하구려. 우리는 그 솜씨 좋은 통역사를 잃었지(그 애는 외국어 세 가지를 아름답게 했다네, 아서. 자네도 여러 번 들었지). 그러니 당신이 나를 잘 끌고 가 줘야 해, 여보, 할 수 있는 만큼은. 나는 끌어 줄 데가 많은 사람이야, 아서,\" 미글스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"끌어 줄 데가 아주 많지. 보통명사만 넘어가면 죄다 막혀 버린다네 — 그리고 상대가 조금이라도 까다로운 자라면, 그 사람한테도 막혀 버려.\"\n\n\"그러고 보니 말입니다,\" 클레넘이 대답했다. \"카발레토가 있지 않습니까. 원하신다면 그를 함께 보내 드리지요. 저로서는 그를 잃으면 곤란하지만, 두 분께서 무사히 데려와 주실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이런! 정말 고맙네, 친구.\" 미글스 씨가 그 제안을 곰곰이 따져 보며 말했다. \"하지만 그러지 않는 게 좋겠어. 아니, 나는 어머니한테 끌려가는 편이 낫겠네. 카발루로(우선 그 이름부터가 막혀 버리는데다, 무슨 희극 노래 후렴구처럼 들린단 말이야)는 자네한테 그렇게 꼭 필요한 사람이니, 데려간다는 생각이 영 내키질 않아. 게다가 우리가 언제 다시 집에 돌아올지도 알 수 없는 노릇이고. 정해지지 않은 기간 동안 그를 데려간다는 건 도저히 안 될 일이지. 그 오두막은 예전 같지가 않아. 늘 살던 것보다 단지 두 사람이 줄었을 뿐인데 말이야 — 펫과, 그 가엾고 불운한 하녀 태티코럼이지 — 그런데도 이제는 텅 빈 것만 같다네. 한번 거길 떠나면, 언제 다시 돌아올 수 있을지 알 수가 없어. 아니야, 아서, 나는 어머니한테 끌려가겠네.\"\n\n어쩌면 결국 두 분끼리 가시는 게 가장 좋을 거라고 클레넘은 생각했다. 그래서 그는 자기 제안을 밀어붙이지 않았다.\n\n\"성가시지 않을 때 가끔 내려와서 머물러 준다면,\" 미글스 씨가 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"나는 — 그리고 어머니도 그럴 거라고 분명히 아네만 — 자네가 이 옛집을, 사람들로 북적이던 시절에 익숙했던 그 약간의 생기로 환하게 밝혀 주고 있다고 생각하면 기쁠 거야. 그리고 저 벽에 걸린 아기들이 가끔이라도 다정한 눈길을 받는다고 생각하면 말이지. 자네는 이 자리와, 또 저 아이들에게 그토록 속한 사람이라네, 아서. 그리고 만약 일이 그렇게 풀렸다면 우리 모두 무척 행복했을 텐데 — 하지만, 어디 보자 — 지금 여행하기에 날씨는 어떤가?\" 미글스 씨가 말을 뚝 그치고, 목청을 가다듬더니, 일어나서 창밖을 내다보았다.\n\n날씨가 자못 기대해 볼 만하다는 데에 그들은 의견을 모았다. 그리고 클레넘은 그 안전한 방향으로 대화를 이어 가다가, 이야기가 다시 편안해지자 슬며시 화제를 헨리 가우언에게로 돌렸다. 섬세하게 대해 주기만 하면 그가 보이는 재빠른 감각과 호감 가는 자질들에 관해서였다. 그는 마찬가지로 가우언이 아내에게 품은 의심할 여지 없는 애정에 대해서도 자세히 이야기했다. 클레넘은 선량한 미글스 씨에게 그 효과를 거두는 데 실패하지 않았다. 이런 칭찬들은 그를 크게 기쁘게 했고, 그는 어머니를 증인 삼아, 딸의 남편에 관하여 자기 마음의 유일하고도 진심 어린 바람은 우정에는 우정으로, 신뢰에는 신뢰로 화목하게 화답하는 것뿐이라고 했다. 몇 시간 안에 오두막의 가구들이 가족이 비운 동안 보존하기 위해 싸이기 시작했고 — 미글스 씨의 표현을 빌리면, 집이 머리를 종이로 마는 일에 들어갔고 — 며칠 안에 아버지와 어머니는 떠났으며, 틱킷 부인과 부캔 의사는 예전처럼 거실 블라인드 뒤에 자리 잡았고, 정원 산책로의 마른 낙엽들 사이로 아서의 외로운 발소리가 바스락거렸다.\n\n그는 이곳을 좋아했으므로, 한 주가 그냥 지나가도록 방문하지 않는 일이 드물었다. 어떤 때는 토요일부터 월요일까지 혼자 내려가기도 했고, 어떤 때는 동업자가 함께 가기도 했으며, 어떤 때는 그저 한두 시간 집과 정원을 거닐며 모든 게 제대로 되어 있는지 확인하고는 다시 런던으로 돌아가기도 했다. 어느 때든, 어떤 상황에서든, 검은 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 틱킷 부인과 부캔 의사는 거실 창가에 앉아 가족이 돌아오기를 내다보고 있었다.\n\n그가 방문한 어느 날, 틱킷 부인은 이런 말로 그를 맞이했다. \"클레넘 씨, 깜짝 놀라실 일을 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\" 문제의 그 일은 어찌나 놀라운 것이었던지, 그것은 실제로 틱킷 부인을 거실 창가에서 끌어내어 정원 산책로에 나타나게 만들었고, 그리하여 클레넘이 열린 대문으로 들어설 때 그녀가 거기 있었다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까, 틱킷 부인?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나리,\" 그 충직한 가정부가 그를 거실로 데리고 들어가 문을 닫고는 대답했다. \"제 평생 그 꾐에 넘어가 정신이 홀린 아이를 본 적이 있다면, 바로 어제 저녁 어스름 속에서 영락없이 그 애를 봤다니까요.\"\n\n\"설마 태티를 말하는 건 —\"\n\n\"코럼이요, 그래요, 그 애를 말하는 거예요!\" 틱킷 부인이 단숨에 그 폭로를 건너뛰며 외쳤다.\n\n\"어디서요?\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 틱킷 부인이 대답했다. \"제가 눈이 좀 무거웠더랬어요. 그때 메리 제인이 차를 끓이고 있었는데, 평소보다 차 한 잔을 더 오래 기다리고 있었거든요. 잠을 자고 있던 것도 아니고, 사람들이 흔히 정확히 말하는 졸고 있던 것도 아니에요. 그보다는, 사람들이 엄밀히 말하자면 눈을 감고 망을 보고 있었다고나 할까요.\"\n\n이 기묘하고 비정상적인 상태에 대한 탐구로 들어가는 일 없이, 클레넘이 말했다. \"그렇군요. 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그래서요, 나리,\" 틱킷 부인이 이어 갔다. \"저는 이 생각도 했다가 저 생각도 했다가 했지요. 나리께서도 그러시듯이요. 누구든 그러듯이 말이에요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그래서요?\"\n\n\"그리고 제가 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 할 때면,\" 틱킷 부인이 계속했다. \"클레넘 씨, 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없지만, 저는 가족분들 생각을 한답니다. 왜냐하면, 어머나! 사람의 생각이란,\" 틱킷 부인은 이 말을 논쟁적이고 철학적인 태도로 했다. \"아무리 여기저기 헤매더라도, 마음속에 가장 위에 떠 있는 것으로 다소간 가게 마련이거든요. 정말 그렇답니다, 나리. 그건 사람이 막을 수가 없는 노릇이에요.\"\n\n아서는 이 발견에 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다.\n\n\"나리께서도 직접 그러시다는 걸 아실 거예요, 제가 감히 말씀드리는데요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"우리 모두가 그렇다는 걸요. 우리를 바꾸는 건 살아가는 신분 따위가 아니에요, 클레넘 씨. 생각은 자유랍니다! — 말씀드렸다시피, 저는 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 하면서, 가족분들 생각을 아주 많이 했어요. 지금 시절의 가족분들만이 아니라, 지난 시절의 가족분들까지요. 왜냐하면 어두워질 무렵에 사람이 그런 식으로 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 하기 시작하면, 제가 드리는 말씀은, 모든 시절이 다 지금인 것처럼 느껴진다는 거예요. 그래서 그 상태에서 빠져나와 곰곰이 따져 봐야만 어느 게 어느 건지 말할 수 있게 되거든요.\"\n\n그는 다시 고개를 끄덕였다. 틱킷 부인의 화술에 새로운 물꼬를 터 줄까 봐 한마디도 입에 올리기가 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n\"그 결과로요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"제가 눈을 깜빡여 떠 보니 그 애의 실제 모습과 형상이 대문으로 들여다보고 있더란 말이에요. 저는 깜짝 놀라기는커녕 그냥 다시 눈을 감아 버렸어요. 그 실제 모습과 형상이라는 게, 그 애가 제 것이나 나리 것이나 다름없이 이 집에 속해 있던 그 시절과 어찌나 딱 들어맞던지, 그 순간에는 그 애가 떠났다는 생각이 도무지 들지를 않았거든요. 하지만 나리, 제가 다시 눈을 깜빡여 떠 보니 거기에 없는 거예요. 그제야 무서운 마음이 와락 밀려들어서, 저는 벌떡 일어났지요.\"\n\n\"곧장 뛰어나가셨고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"뛰어나갔지요,\" 틱킷 부인이 수긍했다. \"발이 닿는 한 빨리요. 그런데 믿으시겠어요, 클레넘 씨, 저 빛나는 온 하늘을 통틀어, 그 젊은 여자의 손가락 하나조차 없더라니까요.\"\n\n이 새로운 별자리가 창공에서 사라진 일은 그냥 넘기고, 아서는 틱킷 부인에게 본인이 대문 밖까지는 나가 보았느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"이리저리, 높은 데 낮은 데 다 가 봤지요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 그 애 그림자도 못 봤어요!\"\n\n그러자 그는 틱킷 부인에게, 그녀가 경험한 두 차례의 눈 깜빡임 사이에 시간 간격이 얼마쯤 있었으리라 짐작하느냐고 물었다. 틱킷 부인은 대답이 자질구레하게 상세하기는 했지만, 5초에서 10분 사이에서 확실한 의견을 정하지 못했다. 그녀는 이 부분에 관해서는 너무도 분명히 갈피를 못 잡았고, 또 분명히 선잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깬 것이었으므로, 클레넘은 그 출현을 한낱 꿈으로 여기는 쪽으로 기우는 마음이 컸다. 그는 그 신앙심 없는 풀이로 그녀의 수수께끼를 깨뜨려 틱킷 부인의 마음을 상하게 하지는 않으면서, 그 결론을 오두막에서 가지고 나왔다. 그리고 만약 곧 그의 의견을 바꿔 놓을 사건이 일어나지 않았더라면, 아마 그는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 생각을 간직했을 것이다.\n\n그가 해 질 무렵 스트랜드 거리를 따라 지나가고 있었는데, 점등부가 그보다 앞서 걸어가고 있었다. 그의 손길이 닿는 대로 안개 낀 공기에 흐릿해진 가로등들이 하나씩 차례로 확 피어났는데, 마치 활짝 만개하는 수많은 이글거리는 해바라기들이 한꺼번에 피어나는 것 같았다. — 그때 강가 부두에서 힘겹게 올라오는 석탄 마차 행렬 때문에 보도가 막히면서, 그는 걸음을 멈추게 되었다. 그는 빠르게 걷고 있었고, 어떤 생각의 흐름에 빠져 있었는데, 두 작용이 갑자기 멈춰 버리자, 그런 상황에 처한 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 새삼스레 주위를 둘러보게 되었다.\n\n그 순간 그는 앞쪽에서 — 몇 사람이 사이에 끼어 있었지만, 그래도 팔을 뻗으면 닿을 만큼 가까운 곳에서 — 태티코럼과, 눈에 띄는 행색을 한 낯선 사내를 보았다. 거들먹거리는 사내로, 코는 높았고, 검은 콧수염은 그 빛깔이 가짜였으니, 그 눈빛이 표정에서 가짜인 것과 매한가지였다. 그는 외국인 특유의 분위기로 두툼한 망토를 걸치고 있었다. 그의 옷차림과 전반적인 행색은 여행 중인 사람의 것이었고, 그는 아주 최근에야 그 처녀와 합류한 듯했다. (그는 처녀보다 훨씬 키가 컸으므로) 몸을 숙여 그녀가 하는 말이 무엇이든 귀 기울이면서, 그는 어깨 너머로, 자기 발자취가 미행당할까 봐 의심하는 데 익숙한 사람의 미심쩍은 눈길을 던졌다. 바로 그때 클레넘은 그의 얼굴을 보았다. 그의 눈이 자기 뒤에 있는 사람들을 한 덩어리로 내리훑었을 뿐, 클레넘의 얼굴이나 다른 어느 누구의 얼굴에 특별히 머무르지는 않았던 것이다.\n\n그가 고개를 채 다시 돌리기도 전에, 그러니까 아직 처녀의 말에 귀 기울이느라 고개를 숙인 채로 있을 때, 길막힘이 풀리면서 가로막혔던 사람들의 물결이 흘러갔다. 여전히 고개를 숙이고 처녀의 말에 귀 기울이며, 그는 그녀 곁에서 걸어갔고, 클레넘은 이 뜻밖의 연극을 끝까지 지켜보아 그들이 어디로 가는지 보기로 마음먹고 그들을 뒤따랐다.\n\n그가 그 결심을 채 굳히기도 전에(그렇다고 오래 걸린 것은 아니지만), 그는 아까 길이 막혔을 때처럼 또다시 별안간 걸음을 멈추게 되었다. 그들이 아델파이로 홱 꺾어 들어간 것이다 — 처녀가 분명히 앞장서고 있었다 — 그러고는 마치 강 위로 비스듬히 솟은 테라스로 가려는 듯 곧장 나아갔다.\n\n오늘날까지도 그곳에는 큰길의 굉음이 갑자기 잦아드는 지점이 늘 있다. 온갖 소음이 어찌나 죽어 버리는지, 그 변화는 마치 귀에 솜을 틀어막거나 머리를 두툼하게 싸맨 것 같다. 그 시절에는 그 대조가 훨씬 더 컸다. 강에는 작은 증기선들도 없었고, 미끄러운 나무 계단과 보행용 둑길 말고는 선착장도 없었으며, 맞은편 강둑에 철도도 없었고, 가까이에 매달린 다리나 어시장도 없었으며, 가장 가까운 돌다리에도 통행이 없었고, 강물 위에서 움직이는 것이라고는 뱃사공의 거룻배와 석탄 운반선뿐이었다. 후자, 곧 석탄 운반선들의 길고 넓은 검은 무리가, 마치 다시는 움직이지 않을 것처럼 진흙 속에 단단히 매여 있어, 어둠이 내린 뒤의 강기슭을 음산하고 적막하게 만들었으며, 그나마 얼마 안 되는 물의 움직임을 강 한복판 쪽 멀리로 밀어내고 있었다. 해가 진 뒤 어느 시각이든, 그리고 집에 먹을 것이 있는 대다수 사람들이 그것을 먹으러 집으로 돌아가고 있고, 먹을 것이 아무것도 없는 대다수 사람들이 아직 구걸이나 도둑질을 하러 슬그머니 기어 나오지도 않은 그 시각에는 특히 더, 그곳은 인적이 끊긴 장소였고 인적이 끊긴 풍경을 굽어보고 있었다.\n\n클레넘이 모퉁이에 멈춰 서서 처녀와 낯선 사내가 거리를 따라 내려가는 것을 지켜본 것이 바로 그런 시각이었다. 사내의 발소리는 울리는 돌바닥 위에서 어찌나 요란했던지, 클레넘은 거기에 자기 발소리까지 보태고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 그들이 모퉁이를 돌아 테라스로 이어지는 어두운 모퉁이의 어둠 속으로 들어서자, 그는 자기가 우연히 제 갈 길을 가는 행인인 양 짐짓 무심한 기색을 한껏 꾸미며 그들을 뒤쫓았다.\n\n그가 어두운 모퉁이를 돌아 나왔을 때, 그들은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 어떤 형체를 향해 테라스를 따라 걷고 있었다. 만약 그가 그 형체만을, 그런 가스등과 안개와 거리라는 조건 아래에서 보았더라면, 첫눈에 알아보지 못했을지도 모른다. 그러나 처녀의 형체가 그에게 단서를 주었으므로, 그는 단번에 그것이 웨이드 양임을 알아보았다.\n\n그는 모퉁이에 멈춰 서서, 마치 누군가와 거기서 만나기로 약속이라도 한 듯 거리 위쪽을 기대에 차 돌아보는 척했다. 그러나 그는 그 세 사람을 조심스레 주시했다. 그들이 한자리에 모이자, 사내는 모자를 벗어 웨이드 양에게 인사를 했다. 처녀는 마치 그를 소개하거나, 그가 늦었거나 빨랐거나 하는 사정을 설명하는 듯 몇 마디 하는 것 같더니, 한 걸음쯤 뒤로 물러나 혼자 떨어져 섰다. 그러자 웨이드 양과 사내는 거닐기 시작했다. 사내는 태도가 극도로 정중하고 아첨하는 듯한 행색이었고, 웨이드 양은 극도로 오만한 행색이었다.\n\n그들이 모퉁이까지 내려와 몸을 돌렸을 때, 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"내가 그걸 위해 허리띠를 졸라매든 말든, 그건 내 일이에요. 당신은 당신 일에나 신경 쓰고, 나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 부인!\" 그가 그녀에게 또 한 번 인사를 하며 대답했다. \"그건 당신 성품의 강인함에 대한 제 깊은 존경과, 당신의 아름다움에 대한 제 찬탄이었을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"나는 누구한테서도 그 둘 중 어느 것도 원하지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"더구나 하고많은 사람 중에서도 당신한테서는 확실히 아니고요. 보고나 계속하세요.\"\n\n\"용서받은 건가요?\" 그가 반쯤 무안해진 정중함을 띠며 물었다.\n\n\"당신은 돈을 받잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그게 당신이 원하는 전부고요.\"\n\n처녀가 뒤로 처져 있는 것이, 그 일을 들어서는 안 되기 때문인지, 아니면 이미 그에 관해 충분히 알고 있기 때문인지, 클레넘은 단정할 수 없었다. 그들이 몸을 돌리자 그녀도 몸을 돌렸다. 그녀는 두 손을 앞으로 모은 채 걸으면서 강 쪽을 멀리 바라보았다. 자기 얼굴을 드러내지 않고서 그가 그녀에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 건 그게 다였다. 다행스럽게도, 정말로 누군가를 기다리며 어슬렁거리는 사람이 하나 있었다. 그는 때로는 난간 너머로 강물을 내려다보고, 때로는 어두운 모퉁이로 와서 거리 위쪽을 올려다보았으므로, 아서를 덜 눈에 띄게 해 주었다.\n\n웨이드 양과 사내가 다시 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"내일까지 기다려야 해요.\"\n\n\"천 번 용서를 빕니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"이런! 그러면 오늘 밤은 곤란하다는 거군요?\"\n\n\"네. 말했잖아요, 내가 그걸 먼저 구해야 당신한테 줄 수 있다고요.\"\n\n그녀는 마치 이 회담을 끝내려는 듯 차도 위에 멈춰 섰다. 사내도 물론 멈췄다. 그리고 처녀도 멈췄다.\n\n\"좀 곤란하군요.\" 사내가 말했다. \"좀요. 하지만, 거룩한 성모님! 이런 임무를 하는 마당에 그건 아무것도 아니지요. 제가 오늘 밤은 공교롭게도 돈이 없어서요. 이 도시에 든든한 거래 은행이 있긴 합니다만, 큰 액수를 한꺼번에 찾을 그때까지는 그 은행에서 돈을 빼고 싶지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"해리엇,\" 웨이드 양이 말했다. \"이분과 — 여기 이 신사분과 — 내일 돈을 좀 보내 드리는 걸로 처리해 다오.\" 그녀는 신사라는 말을 어떤 강조보다도 더 경멸스럽게 끌듯이 내뱉으며 말하고는, 천천히 걸어 나갔다.\n\n사내는 다시 고개를 숙였고, 둘 다 그녀를 뒤따라가며 처녀가 그에게 말을 걸었다. 그들이 멀어져 갈 때 클레넘은 큰맘 먹고 처녀를 바라보았다. 그는 그녀의 그윽한 검은 눈이 무언가를 살피는 표정으로 사내에게 고정되어 있는 것, 그리고 그들이 나란히 테라스 저쪽 끝으로 걸어가는 동안 그녀가 그에게서 약간의 거리를 두고 있는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 채 알아보기도 전에, 보도 위에서 크고도 달라진 쩔걱거리는 발소리가 그에게 사내 혼자 돌아오고 있음을 일러 주었다. 클레넘은 어슬렁어슬렁 차도로 나가 난간 쪽으로 갔다. 사내는 망토 자락을 어깨 위로 휙 걸친 채 프랑스 노래 한 토막을 부르며 빠른 걸음으로 휙 지나갔다.\n\n이제 그 전망 전체에는 그 자신 말고는 아무도 없었다. 어슬렁거리던 사람은 어슬렁어슬렁 시야에서 사라졌고, 웨이드 양과 태티코럼도 가 버렸다. 그들이 어떻게 되는지 보고, 또 자기의 선량한 친구 미글스 씨에게 전해 줄 무언가를 얻으려는 마음이 그 어느 때보다 간절해진 그는, 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 테라스 저쪽 끝으로 나갔다. 그는 적어도 처음에는 그들이 방금 헤어진 동행과는 반대 방향으로 가리라고 옳게 판단했다. 그는 곧 그들을 이웃한 골목길에서 발견했는데, 그곳은 한길로 통하지 않는 막다른 길이어서, 분명히 사내가 자기들 길에서 충분히 멀어질 시간을 벌어 주고 있는 것이었다. 그들은 거리 한쪽을 따라 느긋하게 팔짱을 끼고 내려갔다가 맞은편으로 되돌아왔다. 그들이 거리 모퉁이로 다시 왔을 때, 그들은 걸음걸이를 바꾸어, 목적지와 갈 길이 정해진 사람들의 걸음으로 꾸준히 걸어갔다. 클레넘은 못지않게 꾸준히 그들을 시야에 두었다.\n\n그들은 스트랜드 거리를 건너, 코번트 가든을 지나(그 옛 하숙집 창문 아래를 지났는데, 바로 그날 밤 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿이 거기 찾아왔었다), 북동쪽으로 비스듬히 나아가더니, 태티코럼이 자기 이름을 따온 그 큰 건물을 지나 그레이스 인 로드로 접어들었다. 클레넘은 플로라 덕분에 — 가장과 팽크스는 말할 것도 없고 — 이곳을 제집처럼 잘 알았으므로, 그들을 손쉽게 시야에 두었다. 그들이 다음에는 어디로 갈까 궁금해하기 시작할 무렵, 그 궁금증은 그들이 가장의 거리로 접어드는 것을 본 더 큰 궁금증에 묻혀 버렸다. 그리고 그 궁금증은 또다시, 그들이 가장의 집 문 앞에 멈춰 서는 것을 본 더 큰 궁금증에 삼켜져 버렸다. 반들거리는 놋쇠 문고리를 낮게 두 번 두드리는 소리, 열린 문에서 거리로 새어 나오는 한 줄기 불빛, 묻고 답하는 짧은 사이 — 그러고는 문이 닫혔고, 그들은 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n자기가 이상한 꿈속에 있는 게 아니라는 확신을 얻으려고 주위의 사물들을 둘러보고, 또 집 앞을 잠시 서성인 뒤에, 아서는 문을 두드렸다. 문은 늘 보던 하녀가 열어 주었고, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 민첩하게 그를 곧장 플로라의 거실로 안내했다.\n\n플로라 곁에는 'F 씨의 아주머니' 말고는 아무도 없었다. 이 점잖은 노부인은 차와 토스트가 풍기는 향긋한 공기를 쬐며 난롯가의 안락의자에 폭 파묻혀 있었고, 팔꿈치 옆에는 작은 탁자가, 무릎 위에는 깨끗한 흰 손수건이 펼쳐져 있었으며, 그 위에는 바로 그 순간 먹히기를 기다리는 토스트 두 조각이 놓여 있었다. 김이 모락모락 나는 찻잔 위로 몸을 숙여, 그 김을 통해 내다보며, 그 김을 토해 내는 모습이, 마치 부정한 의식을 거행하는 사악한 중국 여자 마법사 같았는데, 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 자기의 커다란 찻잔을 내려놓고 외쳤다. \"빌어먹을, 저자가 또 돌아왔잖아!\"\n\n앞의 외침으로 미루어 보건대, 작고하신 'F 씨'의 이 타협을 모르는 친척은 시간을 시계가 아니라 자기 감정의 격렬함으로 재는 모양이어서, 클레넘이 최근에 떠났다고 여기는 것 같았다. 그러나 그가 감히 그녀 앞에 나타난 지는 적어도 한 분기는 지나 있었다.\n\n\"세상에 아서!\" 플로라가 그를 다정하게 맞으려고 일어서며 외쳤다. \"도이스 앤드 클레넘 깜짝이야 놀라워라 비록 기계니 주물 공장 일이니 하는 데서 그리 멀지 않으니 다른 때는 몰라도 한낮쯤이면 셰리주 한 잔에 식료품실에 있는 찬 고기가 뭐든 간에 그걸로 만든 변변찮은 샌드위치 하나쯤은 곁들여도 나쁘지 않을 테고 친하다고 해서 맛이 더 나빠질 것도 없는데 왜냐하면 그건 어딘가에서 사 오는 거고 어디서 사 오든 이윤이 남아야지 안 그러면 가게를 유지할 수가 없으니 이치상 동기 없이는 안 되는 일이지만 그래도 통 못 뵈었으니 이제 기대도 안 한다는 걸 배웠어요 왜냐하면 F 씨가 직접 말씀하셨듯이 본다는 게 믿는 거라면 안 보는 것도 믿는 거고 안 보이면 잊혔다고 충분히 믿어도 되는데 그렇다고 내가 당신을 아서 도이스 앤드 클레넘 나를 기억해 주기를 바라는 건 아니에요 내가 왜 그러겠어요 그 시절은 다 갔는걸요 하지만 여기 찻잔 하나 더 가져오고 저 애한테 토스트 새로 굽고 그리고 제발 불 가까이 앉아요.\"\n\n아서는 자기가 찾아온 목적을 설명하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으나, 이 말들에서 그가 알아들은 책망의 취지와, 그를 보고 그녀가 드러낸 진심 어린 기쁨 때문에, 본의 아니게 잠시 미뤄질 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 제발 그 다정하고 착한 조용한 그 애에 관해서,\" 플로라가 자기 의자를 그의 의자 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다. \"그리고 그 애 운명의 온갖 변화에 관해서 아는 걸 전부 좀 들려줘요 이제 틀림없이 마차 타는 사람들이고 셀 수 없이 많은 말들이고 더없이 낭만적이지 물론 가문 문장도 있고 짐승들이 뒷다리로 서서 그걸 떠받치고 있을 텐데 마치 무슨 본보기라도 베껴 그린 것처럼 입이 귀밑까지 찢어진 채로 어머나 세상에 그리고 그 애는 건강한가요 그게 결국은 무엇보다 먼저 챙길 일이지 그게 없으면 재물이 다 무슨 소용이겠어요 F 씨가 직접 통증이 도질 때마다 그렇게 자주 말씀하셨지 하루 육 펜스에 제 입은 제가 벌어 먹고 통풍이 없는 게 훨씬 낫다고 그렇다고 그분이 그런 걸로 살 수 있었던 건 아니에요 그럴 사람이 못 되셨으니까 아니면 그 예전의 작은 그 애가 지금은 너무 허물없는 표현이지만 그런 쪽 기미가 있었던 것도 아니고 너무 가냘프고 작긴 했지만 그래도 어찌나 연약해 보이던지 가엾기도 하지?\"\n\n토스트 한 조각을 가장자리 빵껍질만 남기고 다 먹은 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 여기서 그 빵껍질을 엄숙하게 플로라에게 건넸고, 플로라는 으레 하는 일이라는 듯 그것을 대신 먹어 주었다. 그러고 나서 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 열 손가락을 입술에 대고 차례차례 천천히 적시더니, 정확히 똑같은 순서로 그것들을 흰 손수건에 닦았다. 그런 다음 다른 토스트 한 조각을 집어 들고 거기에 달려들었다. 이 일과를 수행하는 동안, 그녀는 어찌나 매서운 표정으로 클레넘을 바라보았던지, 그는 본인의 마음과는 달리 마지못해 그녀를 마주 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그 애는 식구들 모두와 함께 이탈리아에 있어요, 플로라.\" 그 무시무시한 부인이 다시 일에 몰두하자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"이탈리아에 정말요?\" 플로라가 말했다. \"어디에나 포도가 자라고 용암으로 만든 목걸이랑 팔찌도 있고 그 시의 나라 불타는 산들이 있는 믿기지 않을 만큼 그림 같은 곳이지 비록 손풍금 켜는 소년들이 그 동네에서 데어 죽지 않으려고 떠나온다 해도 누가 뭐라겠어요 그렇게 어린데다 흰 생쥐까지 데리고 오니 참 인정도 많지 그런데 그 애가 정말 그 축복받은 땅에 있는 거예요 둘레에는 온통 파란 하늘뿐이고 죽어 가는 검투사들이랑 벨베데레들이 있는 데 말이에요 비록 F 씨는 직접 그걸 안 믿으셨지만요 기분 좋으실 때 그분 반론은 그 조각상들이 진짜일 리 없다는 거였어요 왜냐하면 비싼 양의 천을 형편없이 손질해서 온통 구김투성이로 두르거나 아니면 아예 한 오라기도 안 걸치거나 그 중간이 없다는 거였지 사실 그럴 법하진 않지만 어쩌면 빈부의 극단 때문에 그런 건지도 모르지 그걸로 설명이 될 수도 있고요.\"\n\n아서는 한마디 끼워 넣어 보려 했으나, 플로라가 또다시 줄달음쳤다.\n\n\"구원받은 베네치아 말인데요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"거기 가 보신 적 있는 것 같은데 잘 구원받았어요 아니면 잘못 구원받았어요 사람마다 의견이 어찌나 다른지 그리고 마카로니 말인데 그걸 정말 곡예사들처럼 먹는다면 왜 좀 더 짧게 자르지 않는 거예요 당신은 아시죠 아서 — 친애하는 도이스 앤드 클레넘 적어도 친애하는 것도 아니고 도이스는 더더욱 아니지 그분과는 알고 지내는 기쁨이 없으니까요 하지만 부디 용서해요 — 만토바를 아실 거예요 그게 망토 짓는 일하고 무슨 상관이 있는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없는데 말이에요?\"\n\n\"그 둘 사이에는 아무 관련도 없을 겁니다, 플로라.\" 아서가 막 말을 꺼내는데, 그녀가 다시 그를 가로챘다.\n\n\"맙소사 정말 없어요 그런가요 난 한 번도 안 그랬는데 하지만 그게 나다워요 어떤 생각이 떠오르면 거기 휩쓸려서 여분도 없으니 그냥 붙들고 있거든요 아아 한때는 있었지요 친애하는 아서 그러니까 분명히 친애하는 것도 아니고 아서도 아니지만 무슨 말인지 아시겠죠 어떤 빛나는 생각 하나가 무어라더라 그 지평선이며 기타 등등을 금빛으로 물들이던 때가 하지만 이제는 어둡게 구름이 끼었고 다 끝나 버렸어요.\"\n\n뭔가 아주 다른 이야기를 하고 싶은 아서의 점점 커져 가는 바람이 이제는 그의 얼굴에 너무도 또렷이 쓰여 있었으므로, 플로라는 다정한 눈길로 말을 멈추고 그게 무엇이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"플로라, 저는 지금 이 집에 와 있는 누군가와 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 간절합니다 — 틀림없이 캐스비 씨와 함께 있겠지요. 제가 들어오는 걸 봤던 어떤 사람인데, 그릇되고 개탄스러운 방식으로 제 친구의 집을 버리고 떠난 사람입니다.\"\n\n\"아빠는 워낙 많은 사람을, 그것도 별별 이상한 사람들을 만나서,\" 플로라가 일어서며 말했다. \"당신 말고는 누구를 위해서도 감히 내려갈 엄두를 못 내겠지만 아서 당신을 위해서라면 잠수종 속이라도 기꺼이 내려가겠어요 하물며 식당쯤이야 그리고 곧 돌아올 테니 내가 없는 동안 부디 F 씨의 아주머니를 신경 써 주고 동시에 신경 쓰지 마세요.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 작별의 눈길을 남기고, 플로라는 부산스레 나가 버려, 이 무시무시한 책무를 떠안은 클레넘을 끔찍한 불안 속에 남겨 두었다.\n\n'F 씨의 아주머니'가 토스트 한 조각을 다 먹고 나서 그녀의 거동에 나타난 첫 번째 변화는, 크고도 길게 끄는 코 훌쩍임이었다. 그 음울한 의미가 오해의 여지 없이 분명한 터라, 이 시위를 자기에 대한 도전으로 해석하지 않기란 불가능하다는 걸 깨닫고, 클레넘은 그 시위가 흘러나온 그 훌륭하지만 편견에 찬 부인을 애처로이 바라보았다. 온순하게 복종하면 그녀의 마음이 누그러지지 않을까 하는 바람에서였다.\n\n\"나한테 그 눈깔 부라리지 마.\" 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 적의에 부르르 떨며 말했다. \"이거나 받아.\"\n\n'이거'란 토스트 조각의 빵껍질이었다. 클레넘은 감사하는 표정으로 그 선물을 받아 들고, 약간의 당혹감에 눌린 채 그것을 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 목청을 상당한 위력의 고함으로 끌어올려 \"이 녀석은 배알이 뒤틀린 놈이라니까! 그걸 먹기엔 너무 잘난 척하는 놈이야!\"라고 외치며 의자에서 일어나, 그 점잖은 주먹을 그의 코끝에 표면을 간질일 만큼 바싹 들이대며 휘둘렀을 때, 그 당혹감은 가시기는커녕 더해졌다. 플로라가 때맞춰 돌아와 그가 이 곤란한 처지에 빠진 것을 발견하지 않았더라면, 더한 일이 벌어졌을지도 몰랐다. 플로라는 조금도 당황하거나 놀라는 기색 없이, 오히려 노부인에게 \"오늘 밤 아주 정정하시네요\"라고 칭찬조로 추어올리며 그녀를 의자로 도로 데려다 앉혔다.\n\n\"이 녀석은 배알이 뒤틀린 놈이야.\" 'F 씨'의 친척이 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"여물이나 한 끼 먹여!\"\n\n\"어머! 그건 별로 좋아하지 않을 거예요, 아주머니.\" 플로라가 대답했다.\n\n\"여물이나 한 끼 먹이라니까.\" 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 플로라 너머로 자기 원수를 노려보며 말했다. \"배알이 뒤틀린 놈한테는 그게 약이야. 한 부스러기도 남김없이 다 먹게 해. 빌어먹을 놈, 여물이나 한 끼 먹여!\"\n\n이 다과를 그에게 갖다주는 척하는 일반적인 핑계로, 플로라는 그를 계단으로 데리고 나갔다. 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 그때까지도 형언할 수 없는 신랄함으로 그가 '녀석'이며 '배알이 뒤틀렸다'는 말을 끊임없이 되뇌었고, 자기가 이미 그토록 강하게 처방한 그 말[馬]에게나 줄 사료를 그에게 마련해 주라고 거듭거듭 우겨 댔다.\n\n\"계단이 어찌나 불편하고 모퉁이 계단이 또 어찌나 많은지요 아서,\" 플로라가 속삭였다. \"제 망토 밑으로 팔을 둘러 주셔도 괜찮을까요?\"\n\n지극히 우스꽝스러운 꼴로 계단을 내려가고 있다는 느낌과 함께, 클레넘은 요구받은 자세로 내려갔고, 식당 문 앞에 이르러서야 그 아리따운 짐을 내려놓았다. 사실 거기서조차 그녀를 떨쳐 내기는 다소 어려웠으니, 그녀는 그의 품에 남아 \"아서, 제발 부탁이에요, 아빠한테는 한마디도 흘리지 말아요!\"라고 속삭였던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 아서와 함께 방으로 들어갔고, 그 안에는 가장이 홀로 앉아, 펠트 슬리퍼를 신은 발을 난로 망에 올린 채, 마치 한 번도 멈춘 적이 없다는 듯 엄지손가락을 빙빙 돌리고 있었다. 그의 머리 위 그림 액자 속에서 내다보는 열 살배기의 젊은 가장도 그에 못지않게 차분한 기색이었다. 두 매끈한 머리는 똑같이 환하게 빛나고, 어수룩하고, 울룩불룩했다.\n\n\"클레넘 군, 만나서 반갑네. 건강하길 바라네, 군, 건강하길 바라네. 부디 앉게, 부디 앉게.\"\n\n\"저는 바랐습니다만, 어르신,\" 클레넘이 자리에 앉아 멍한 실망의 표정으로 둘러보며 말했다. \"어르신께서 혼자 계신 게 아니기를요.\"\n\n\"아아, 그런가?\" 가장이 상냥하게 말했다. \"아아, 그런가?\"\n\n\"제가 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요 아빠.\" 플로라가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아아, 그렇지!\" 가장이 대꾸했다. \"그래, 바로 그래. 아아, 그렇지!\"\n\n\"여쭙건대, 어르신,\" 클레넘이 애타게 물었다. \"웨이드 양은 갔습니까?\"\n\n\"양? — 오, 자네는 그녀를 웨이드라고 부르는군.\" 캐스비 씨가 대꾸했다. \"지극히 온당한 일이지.\"\n\n아서가 재빨리 되물었다. \"어르신은 그녀를 무어라 부르십니까?\"\n\n\"웨이드.\" 캐스비 씨가 말했다. \"오, 늘 웨이드지.\"\n\n그 박애주의자다운 얼굴과 길고 비단결 같은 백발을 몇 초간 바라보는 동안, 캐스비 씨는 엄지손가락을 빙빙 돌리며, 마치 불에게 자기를 태워 주기를 자비롭게 바라기라도 하는 듯, 그래야 그 불을 용서해 줄 수 있다는 듯 불을 향해 미소 짓고 있었다. 그러다 아서가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 캐스비 어르신 —\"\n\n\"천만에, 천만에.\" 가장이 말했다. \"천만에.\"\n\n\"— 하지만, 웨이드 양에게는 시중드는 이가 하나 함께 있었습니다 — 제 친구분들이 키운 젊은 여자인데, 웨이드 양이 그 아이에게 미치는 영향이 그리 좋지 않다고 여겨집니다. 그리고 저는 그 아이에게, 아직 그 보호자들의 관심을 잃은 것이 아니라는 확언을 전할 기회를 얻을 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요?\" 가장이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"그러니 부디 호의를 베푸시어 웨이드 양의 주소를 제게 알려 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이런!\" 가장이 말했다. \"참으로 안타깝구먼! 그들이 여기 있을 때 내게 사람을 들여보내기만 했어도! 나도 그 젊은 여자를 보았다네, 클레넘 군. 혈색 좋고 풍만한 젊은 여자였지, 클레넘 군. 머리도 아주 검고 눈도 아주 검었고. 내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 잘못 본 게 아니라면?\"\n\n아서는 그렇다고 수긍하고, 새삼스러운 어조로 다시 한번 말했다. \"부디 호의를 베푸시어 그 주소를 알려 주신다면.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 이런!\" 가장이 달콤한 유감을 담아 외쳤다. \"쯧, 쯧, 쯧! 참으로 애석하네, 참으로 애석해! 나는 주소가 없다네, 군. 웨이드 양은 대개 외국에서 산다네, 클레넘 군. 몇 해째 그래 왔지. 그리고 그녀는(같은 사람이자 숙녀를 두고 이렇게 말해도 된다면) 흠이라 할 만큼 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수가 없네, 클레넘 군. 나도 오래오래 그녀를 다시 못 볼지도 모르지. 영영 다시 못 볼지도 모르고. 참으로 애석해, 참으로 애석해!\"\n\n이제 클레넘은 그 초상화에서 도움을 얻을 가망이나 이 가장에게서 도움을 얻을 가망이나 매한가지임을 알았다. 그래도 그는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"캐스비 어르신, 제가 말씀드린 친구분들의 안심을 위하여, 그리고 어르신께서 부과하는 것이 자기 의무라 여기시는 어떠한 비밀 엄수의 조건 아래에서라도, 웨이드 양에 관하여 무엇이든 알려 주실 수는 없겠습니까? 저는 그녀를 외국에서도 보았고 이 나라에서도 보았습니다만, 그녀에 관해 아는 것이 아무것도 없습니다. 무엇이든 그녀에 관한 이야기를 좀 들려주실 수 없겠습니까?\"\n\n\"전혀 없네.\" 가장이 한껏 자비로운 태도로 그 큰 머리를 흔들며 대꾸했다. \"전혀 없어. 이런, 이런, 이런! 그녀가 그토록 잠깐만 머물렀고 자네는 늑장을 부렸으니 참으로 애석한 노릇이야! 비밀스러운 대리 업무로서, 대리 업무로서, 나는 이따금 이 부인에게 돈을 지급한 적이 있네. 하지만 그걸 안다 한들 자네한테 무슨 만족이 되겠나, 군?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 아무 만족도 안 됩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"정말로,\" 가장이 박애주의자답게 불을 향해 미소 지으며 환한 얼굴로 수긍했다. \"아무 만족도 안 되지, 군. 자네가 현명한 답을 짚었네, 클레넘 군. 정말로, 아무 만족도 안 되지, 군.\"\n\n그가 거기 앉아 매끈한 두 엄지손가락을 서로 빙빙 돌리는 모양은, 만약 이 화제를 계속 파고든다면 그가 그것을 어떤 식으로 빙빙 돌리기만 할 뿐, 그 새로운 면이라고는 결코 보여 주지 않고 조금의 진전도 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 클레넘에게 너무도 전형적으로 보여 주는 것이어서, 그것은 자기의 수고가 헛되었음을 그에게 확신시키는 데 큰 몫을 했다. 그는 얼마든지 시간을 들여 그 일을 생각해 보아도 좋았을 것이다. 모든 것을 자기의 울룩불룩한 머리와 백발에 맡겨 두고도 어디서든 일을 풀어 나가는 데 익숙한 캐스비 씨는, 자기의 힘이 침묵에 있음을 알고 있었으니까. 그리하여 캐스비는 거기 앉아 빙빙, 또 빙빙 돌리며, 자기의 윤이 나는 머리와 이마를 혹마다 더없이 자비로워 보이게 만들고 있었다.\n\n이 광경을 눈앞에 두고 아서가 가려고 막 일어섰을 때, 순항할 만한 곳이 못 되면 끌어 올려져 놓이는 그 훌륭한 배 '팽크스 호'가 정박해 있는 안쪽 선거(船渠)에서, 그 증기선이 그를 향해 힘겹게 다가오는 소리가 들려왔다. 그 소리가 마치 누구든 그 일에 대해 생각할 사람에게, 자기는 들리지 않는 곳에서부터 일하며 오고 있노라고 각인시키려는 듯, 떠들썩하게 멀찍이서부터 시작된다는 느낌이 아서에게 들었다.\n\n팽크스 씨와 그는 악수를 했고, 팽크스 씨는 고용주에게 서명할 편지 한두 통을 가져왔다. 팽크스 씨는 악수를 하면서 그저 왼쪽 집게손가락으로 자기 눈썹을 긁고 한 번 콧방귀를 뀌었을 뿐이지만, 이제는 예전보다 그를 더 잘 이해하게 된 클레넘은, 그가 오늘 저녁 일을 거의 끝냈으며 밖에서 그에게 한마디 하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 캐스비 씨에게, 그리고 (그보다 더 까다로운 절차였던) 플로라에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 팽크스 씨가 다니는 길목 근처를 어슬렁거렸다.\n\n그가 잠깐 기다리자 곧 팽크스 씨가 나타났다. 팽크스 씨가 또 한 번 의미심장한 콧방귀와 함께 다시 악수를 하고는, 머리를 매만지려고 모자를 벗자, 아서는 방금 일어난 일을 꽤 잘 아는 사람으로서 그에게 말을 걸어도 좋다는 신호를 받은 셈이라고 생각했다. 그래서 그는 서두 없이 말했다.\n\n\"정말로 그들은 가 버렸겠지요, 팽크스?\"\n\n\"네.\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"정말로 가 버렸습니다.\"\n\n\"저분은 그 부인을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 압니까?\"\n\n\"뭐라 말씀 못 드리겠습니다. 알 거라고 봅니다만.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 모르는가? 아니, 팽크스 씨는 몰랐다. 팽크스 씨는 그녀에 관해 무어라도 아는가?\n\n\"제 짐작에는,\" 그 훌륭한 사내가 대꾸했다. \"그녀가 자기 자신에 관해 아는 만큼은 저도 그녀에 관해 압니다. 그녀는 누군가의 자식이지요 — 아무의 자식이기도 하고, 누구의 자식도 아니기도 합니다. 여기 런던의 어느 방에 그녀를 부모뻘 되는 나이 든 사람 여섯 명과 함께 넣어 두면, 그 부모가 거기 있다 한들 그녀로서는 알 길이 없습니다. 그들은 그녀가 보는 어느 집에 있을 수도 있고, 그녀가 지나치는 어느 묘지에 있을 수도 있고, 그녀가 어느 거리에서든 부딪칠 수도 있고, 그녀가 언제든 우연히 알게 될 수도 있는데 — 그러고도 결코 모르지요. 그녀는 그들에 관해 아무것도 모릅니다. 그 어떤 친척에 관해서도 아무것도 몰라요. 안 적도 없고요. 앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"캐스비 씨가 어쩌면 그녀를 깨우쳐 줄 수도 있겠군요?\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있지요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"제 짐작엔 그렇습니다만, 잘은 모릅니다. 그분은 오래전부터 돈을(제가 알기로 그리 많지는 않습니다만) 위탁받아서, 그녀가 도저히 없이는 못 지낼 때 조금씩 나눠 주고 있지요. 어떤 때는 자존심이 세서 한참 동안 손도 안 대고, 어떤 때는 너무 궁해서 안 받고는 못 배기지요. 그녀는 제 인생에 몸부림치며 괴로워합니다. 그녀보다 더 화 많고, 격정적이고, 무모하고, 복수심에 찬 여자는 살았던 적이 없을 겁니다. 오늘 밤에는 돈 때문에 왔지요. 그게 각별히 필요한 사정이 있다고 했습니다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 클레넘이 골똘히 생각하며 말했다. \"그 사정이 무엇인지 제가 우연히 아는 것 같습니다 — 그러니까, 그 돈이 누구 주머니로 들어갈지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"만약 그게 무슨 계약이라면, 저는 그 당사자에게 정확히 이행하라고 권하겠습니다. 만약 제가 그 여자한테 잘못을 저질렀다면, 그녀가 아무리 젊고 잘생겼다 해도 저는 그녀를 믿고 제 몸을 맡기지 않겠어요. 아니, 제 고용주 돈의 두 배를 준다 해도요! 다만,\" 팽크스가 단서를 달았다. \"제게 오래 끄는 병이 있어서 그걸 어서 끝내고 싶은 게 아니라면 말입니다.\"\n\n아서는 자기가 그녀를 관찰한 바를 서둘러 되짚어 보고는, 그것이 팽크스 씨의 견해와 상당히 가깝게 들어맞는다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"제가 놀라운 건 말입니다,\" 팽크스가 이어 갔다. \"그녀가 자기 사연과 연관된 유일하게 붙들 수 있는 사람인 제 고용주를 여태 끝장내지 않았다는 거예요. 그 말이 나온 김에, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저 자신도 이따금 그분을 끝장내고 싶은 유혹을 느낀다는 걸 말씀드리지요.\"\n\n아서는 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"이런, 팽크스, 그런 말 하지 마세요!\"\n\n\"제 말을 알아들으세요.\" 팽크스가 석탄 가루로 시커메진 짤막한 손톱 다섯 개를 아서의 팔에 갖다 대며 말했다. \"그분 목을 따겠다는 게 아닙니다. 하지만 세상 모든 소중한 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 그분이 너무 지나치면, 저는 그분 머리카락을 잘라 버릴 겁니다!\"\n\n이 어마어마한 협박을 토해 내는 새로운 면모로 자신을 드러내 보인 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 자못 심각한 표정으로 몇 차례 콧방귀를 뀌고는 증기를 내뿜으며 떠나갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken\n\n그가 살아 있는 채로 그 형틀에 매달려 부서질 판결을 받은 온갖 골치 아픈 죄수들과 어울려 적잖은 시간을 보낸 에두름국의 그늘진 대기실들은, 사나흘 연이은 날 동안 아서 클레넘에게 미스 웨이드와 태티코럼을 얼핏 본 일에 관한 생각을 다 짜내고도 남을 한가한 시간을 안겨 주었다. 그는 거기서 더 많은 것도 더 적은 것도 끌어낼 수 없었고, 이 미진한 상태로 그 일을 내버려 둘 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이 기간 동안 그는 어머니의 음울한 옛집에 가지 않았다. 그곳을 찾아가는 평소 저녁 가운데 하루가 다시 돌아오자, 그는 거의 아홉 시 무렵에 자기 거처와 동업자를 떠나, 젊은 시절의 그 음산한 집을 향해 천천히 걸어갔다.\n\n그 집은 언제나 그의 상상 속에서 분노에 차 있고, 신비롭고, 슬픈 것으로 다가왔다. 그리고 그의 상상은 충분히 예민해서, 그 일대 전체를 그 어두운 그림자의 어떤 빛깔로 물든 채 바라보았다. 음울한 밤, 그가 지나가는 동안 그가 걷는 어슴푸레한 거리들은 죄다 짓누르는 비밀을 간직한 저장소처럼 보였다. 책과 서류의 비밀을 궤짝과 금고에 잠가 둔 채 텅 빈 계산소들, 견고한 방과 금고실의 비밀을 간직한 은행들 — 그 열쇠는 극소수의 비밀 주머니와 극소수의 비밀스러운 가슴속에 들어 있었다. 그 거대한 방앗간 속에 흩어진 모든 분쇄자들의 비밀들 — 그들 가운데에는 의심할 바 없이 온갖 종류의 약탈자, 위조범, 신의를 저버린 자들이 있었고, 동이 트는 어느 날의 빛이 그들을 드러낼 수도 있었다. 그는 이런 것들이 숨어서 공기에 무거움을 더한다고 상상할 수도 있었다. 그 근원에 다가갈수록 그림자가 짙어지고 또 짙어지자, 그는 외로운 교회 지하 납골당의 비밀들을 떠올렸다. 쇠궤짝 속에 돈을 쌓고 감춰 두었던 사람들이 이제 그들 차례가 되어 비슷하게 쌓여 있되, 아직 해악을 끼치는 일에서 벗어나 안식하지 못하고 있는 그곳을. 그러고는 강의 비밀들을 떠올렸다 — 강은 두 갈래의 험상궂은 비밀의 황야 사이로 탁한 물결을 굴리며, 두텁고 빽빽하게 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어, 바람과 새의 날개가 휩쓸고 가는 자유로운 공기와 자유로운 들판을 가로막았다.\n\n그가 집에 가까워질수록 그림자가 여전히 짙어지자, 한때 아버지가 머물렀던 그 우울한 방이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다. 침상 곁에 다른 지켜보는 이가 없을 때 아버지와 함께 스러져 가는 것을 그 자신이 보았던 그 애원하는 얼굴이 그 방에 떠돌고 있었다. 그 방의 답답한 공기가 비밀스러웠다. 그 집 전체의 음울함과 곰팡내와 먼지가 비밀스러웠다. 그 중심에 그의 어머니가 군림하고 있었다 — 얼굴은 흔들림이 없고, 의지는 굽힐 줄 모르며, 자신과 남편 생애의 모든 비밀을 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 모든 생의 그 거대한 마지막 비밀에 맞서 정면으로, 준엄하게 자신을 버티고 서 있었다.\n\n그가 집이 서 있는 둘러막힌 안마당으로 통하는, 좁고 가파른 거리로 막 접어들었을 때, 또 다른 발소리가 그의 뒤에서 그 거리로 접어들었다 — 그것은 그의 발소리에 너무도 바짝 붙어 있어서 그는 벽 쪽으로 떠밀렸다. 그의 마음이 이런 생각들로 가득 차 있던 터라, 이 마주침은 그를 전혀 무방비로 덮쳤고, 그래서 다른 행인은 호기롭게 \"실례! 내 잘못이 아니오!\" 하고 말하고는, 그가 자기 주위의 현실을 되찾는 데 필요한 그 순간이 채 지나기도 전에 앞서 지나가 버렸다.\n\n그 순간이 번쩍 스쳐 지나가자, 그는 자기 앞에서 성큼성큼 걸어가는 사내가 지난 며칠간 그토록 그의 마음을 차지하고 있던 바로 그 사내임을 알아보았다. 그것은 그 사내가 그에게 남긴 인상의 힘에 떠받쳐진 우연한 닮음이 아니었다. 바로 그 사내였다. 그가 그 처녀와 함께 뒤따랐고, 미스 웨이드에게 말하는 것을 엿들었던 그 사내였다.\n\n거리는 가파른 내리막인 데다 구불구불하기도 했는데, 그 사내는(취하지는 않았으나 무슨 독한 술기운으로 얼굴이 붉어진 기색이었다) 어찌나 빨리 내려가는지, 클레넘은 보고 있는 사이에 그를 놓쳐 버렸다. 그를 따라가겠다는 뚜렷한 의도는 없었으나, 그 모습을 조금만 더 시야에 두고 싶은 충동에서, 클레넘은 걸음을 재촉해 그를 시야에서 가렸던 거리의 굽이를 돌아섰다. 그 굽이를 돌자, 그는 그 사내를 더는 볼 수 없었다.\n\n이제 어머니의 집 대문 가까이 서서 그는 거리 아래쪽을 내려다보았다. 그러나 거리는 텅 비어 있었다. 그 사내를 가릴 만큼 큰 돌출된 그늘도 없었고, 그가 접어들 만한 가까운 모퉁이도 없었으며, 문이 열리고 닫히는 소리도 들리지 않았다. 그럼에도 그는, 그 사내가 손에 열쇠를 쥐고 있었음에 틀림없고, 그 수많은 집 문 가운데 하나를 열고 안으로 들어갔음에 틀림없다고 결론지었다.\n\n이 기이한 우연과 기이한 일별을 곱씹으며, 그는 안마당으로 접어들었다. 그저 습관처럼 어머니 방의 희미하게 불 켜진 창문 쪽으로 시선을 던지는데, 그의 눈이 방금 놓쳤던 그 모습과 마주쳤다. 그 사내는 작은 황량한 빈터의 쇠난간에 기대어 서서 그 창문들을 올려다보며 혼자 웃고 있었다. 밤이면 늘 그 주위를 어슬렁거리던 수많은 떠돌이 고양이들 가운데 더러는, 그에게 겁을 먹고 그가 멈춘 자리에 함께 멈춰 선 듯, 담장 꼭대기와 현관 차양과 안전하게 쉴 만한 다른 지점들에서 그 사내 자신의 눈과 조금도 다르지 않은 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그는 이렇게 혼자 즐기느라 잠깐 멈춰 섰을 뿐, 곧장 앞으로 나아가, 가면서 외투 자락을 어깨에서 휙 젖히고, 고르지 않게 내려앉은 계단을 올라, 문을 쾅쾅 울리도록 두드렸다.\n\n클레넘의 놀라움이 아무리 컸어도 그가 한 점 망설임 없이 결심을 굳히지 못할 만큼은 아니었다. 그 역시 문으로 다가가, 그 역시 계단을 올랐다. 그의 그 친구는 허세 부리는 태도로 그를 바라보며 혼자 노래를 흥얼거렸다.\n\n\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가? 마졸렌의 길동무여, 이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가? 언제나 즐거운 자!\"\n\n그러고는 다시 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"성미가 급하시군요, 선생.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소이다, 선생. 목숨을 걸고서라도, 선생.\" 낯선 사내가 받았다. \"성미가 급한 건 내 천성이오!\"\n\n애프리 부인이 문을 열기 전에 조심스럽게 사슬을 거는 소리에 두 사람 다 그쪽을 바라보았다. 애프리는 손에 너울거리는 촛불을 들고 문을 아주 조금만 열고는, 이 야밤에 그런 식으로 문을 두드리는 게 누구냐고 물었다. \"어머, 아서 도련님!\" 그를 먼저 알아보고 그녀가 놀라며 덧붙였다. \"정말 도련님이 아니에요? 아이고, 맙소사! 아니야,\" 다른 사내를 보고 그녀가 외쳤다. \"또 저 사람이야!\"\n\n\"맞소! 또 나요, 사랑스러운 플린트윈치 부인.\" 낯선 사내가 외쳤다. \"문을 여시오, 내 친애하는 벗 제러마이어를 품에 안게 해 주시오! 문을 여시오, 어서 가서 내 플린트윈치를 껴안게 해 주시오!\"\n\n\"그이는 집에 없어요.\" 애프리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"데려오시오!\" 낯선 사내가 외쳤다. \"내 플린트윈치를 데려오시오! 잉글랜드에 막 도착한 그의 옛 블랑두아가 왔다고 전하시오. 여기 그의 어린 소년이, 그의 양배추가, 그의 더없이 사랑받는 이가 와 있다고 전하시오! 문을 여시오, 아름다운 플린트윈치 부인, 그러는 동안 나는 위층으로 올라가, 마님께 인사를 — 블랑두아의 경의를 — 올리게 해 주시오! 마님께서는 여전히 살아 계시오? 잘됐군. 자, 어서 여시오!\"\n\n아서의 놀라움을 더욱 키운 것은, 애프리 부인이 이 사람은 도련님이 끼어들 만한 신사가 아니라고 경고하듯 그를 향해 눈을 휘둥그레 뜬 채, 사슬을 풀고 문을 열었다는 것이었다. 낯선 사내는 격식도 없이 현관으로 걸어 들어와, 아서더러 자기를 따라오게 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"그러니 서두르시오! 어서 해치우시오! 내 플린트윈치를 데려오시오! 마님께 내가 왔다고 아뢰시오!\" 낯선 사내가 돌바닥을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"애프리, 말 좀 해 주오.\" 아서가 분노에 차 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 크고 엄한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 신사가 누구요?\"\n\n\"애프리, 말 좀 해 주오.\" 낯선 사내가 이번엔 자기가 따라 했다. \"누구냐고 — 하, 하, 하! — 이 신사가 누구냐고?\"\n\n마침맞게 위층 방에서 클레넘 부인의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"애프리, 둘 다 올라오게 해라. 아서, 곧장 내게로 오너라!\"\n\n\"아서?\" 블랑두아가 팔을 쭉 뻗어 모자를 벗고는, 성큼 내디딘 자세에서 두 발꿈치를 모으며 그에게 거창한 절을 올리며 외쳤다. \"마님의 아드님이시군요? 나는 마님 아드님께 온 마음을 다 바치는 종복이올시다!\"\n\n아서는 그를 앞서와 조금도 다르지 않은 호의 없는 태도로 다시 한번 바라보고는, 아무 응대도 없이 발꿈치를 돌려 위층으로 올라갔다. 손님이 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다. 애프리 부인은 문 뒤에서 열쇠를 집어 들고는, 자기 주인을 데려오려고 날렵하게 빠져나갔다.\n\n블랑두아 씨가 앞서 그 방에 나타났던 일을 알고 있는 구경꾼이라면, 클레넘 부인이 이번에 그를 맞이하는 데서 어떤 차이를 알아챘을 것이다. 그녀의 얼굴은 그것을 드러낼 만한 얼굴이 아니었고, 억눌린 태도와 가라앉은 목소리도 똑같이 그녀의 통제 아래 있었다. 그 차이란 오로지, 그가 들어선 순간부터 그녀가 그의 얼굴에서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그가 시끄러워질 무렵이면 두세 차례, 똑바로 앉은 의자에서 두 손을 팔걸이 위에 미동 없이 둔 채 몸을 아주 조금 앞으로 기울였다는 것에 있었다 — 마치 그가 하고 싶은 만큼 얼마든지 길게 말해도 곧 들어주겠다는 보장을 그에게 주는 듯이. 아서도 이것을 놓치지 않고 보았다. 비록 이번 경우와 지난번 사이의 차이는 그가 알아챌 수 있는 범위 밖이었지만.\n\n\"부인,\" 블랑두아가 말했다. \"영광스럽게도 저를 아드님이신 무슈께 소개해 주십시오. 제가 보기에 부인, 아드님이신 무슈께서는 저를 두고 불평하실 작정인 듯합니다. 그분은 정중하지가 않으십니다.\"\n\n\"선생,\" 아서가 재빨리 끼어들며 말했다. \"당신이 누구든, 또 어떻게 여기 오게 되었든, 만약 내가 이 집의 주인이라면 나는 지체 없이 당신을 이 집 바깥으로 내보낼 것이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 너는 주인이 아니다.\" 어머니가 그를 보지도 않은 채 말했다. \"네 그 분별없는 성미를 풀어 줄 만큼 운이 따르지 않게도, 너는 주인이 아니다, 아서.\"\n\n\"제가 주인이라고 주장하는 게 아닙니다, 어머니. 제가 이 사람이 여기서 처신하는 방식에 반대한다면 — 그것도 어찌나 반대하는지, 만약 제게 이곳에서 어떤 권한이라도 있다면 단 일 분도 그가 머무는 것을 결코 용납하지 않을 만큼 반대한다면 — 그것은 어머니를 위해 반대하는 것입니다.\"\n\n\"반대가 필요한 경우라면,\" 그녀가 받았다. \"나는 나 스스로 반대할 수 있다. 그리고 당연히 그리할 것이다.\"\n\n이들 다툼의 대상은, 자리에 앉은 채로, 소리 내어 웃으며 손으로 자기 다리를 탁탁 쳤다.\n\n\"너에게는 권리가 없다.\" 클레넘 부인이, 아무리 아들에게 곧장 말을 건네더라도 시선만은 늘 블랑두아에게 고정한 채로 말했다. \"어떤 신사든(더구나 다른 나라에서 온 신사라면 더욱) 그가 네 기준에 맞추지 않는다고, 또는 네 규칙에 자기 행동을 맞추지 않는다고 해서, 그를 헐뜯어 말할 권리는 없다. 어쩌면 그 신사도 비슷한 이유로 너를 못마땅해할지도 모른다.\"\n\n\"그러기를 바랍니다.\" 아서가 받았다.\n\n\"그 신사는,\" 클레넘 부인이 말을 이었다. \"전번에 우리에게 대단히 존경받고 신뢰할 만한 거래처들로부터 추천장을 가져왔다. 나는 그 신사가 지금 여기 온 목적을 전혀 알지 못한다. 나는 그것을 도무지 모르며, 그 성격을 조금이라도 짐작할 수 있으리라 여겨질 리도 없다.\" 그녀가 그 말들을 아주 천천히 무게 있게 힘주어 말하자, 늘 짓던 찌푸림이 한층 짙어졌다. \"하지만 그 신사가 그 목적을 설명하기에 이르면 — 플린트윈치가 돌아오면 나와 플린트윈치에게 그리 친절을 베풀어 달라고 청할 참이다만 — 그것은 틀림없이, 어느 정도는 우리 사업의 일상적인 방식에 속하는 일로 드러날 테고, 그것을 진척시키는 것은 우리의 일이자 기쁨이 될 것이다. 그 외의 다른 것일 리가 없다.\"\n\n\"두고 보지요, 부인!\" 그 사업가가 말했다.\n\n\"두고 보자꾸나.\" 그녀가 동의했다. \"그 신사는 플린트윈치와 아는 사이다. 그리고 그 신사가 지난번 런던에 있었을 때, 나는 그와 플린트윈치가 함께 무슨 흥겨운 자리나 친목을 가졌다는 말을 들은 기억이 있다. 나는 이 방 바깥에서 일어나는 일을 많이 알 처지가 못 되고, 이 방 너머의 자질구레한 세속사의 소란에는 별 관심이 없다만, 그것만은 들은 기억이 있다.\"\n\n\"옳습니다, 부인. 사실이고말고요.\" 그가 다시 웃고는, 문 앞에서 불렀던 곡조의 후렴을 휘파람으로 불었다.\n\n\"그러니 아서,\" 그의 어머니가 말했다. \"그 신사는 아는 사람으로서 여기에 온 것이지, 낯선 이로서 온 것이 아니다. 네 그 분별없는 성미가 그분에게서 거슬리는 점을 찾아냈다니 심히 유감스러운 일이다. 나는 그것을 유감으로 여긴다. 그 신사께 그렇다고 말씀드린다. 너는 그리 말하지 않으리라는 걸 알고 있으니, 나와 플린트윈치를 대신해 내가 말하는 것이다 — 그 신사의 용무는 우리 두 사람과 얽힌 것이므로.\"\n\n이제 아래층 문의 열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂히는 소리가 들리고, 문이 열렸다가 닫히는 소리가 들렸다. 차례대로 플린트윈치 씨가 나타났다. 그가 들어서자 손님이 의자에서 일어나 큰 소리로 웃으며 그를 꼭 껴안았다.\n\n\"어떻게 지내시오, 내 소중한 벗이여!\" 그가 말했다. \"세상살이는 어떻소, 내 플린트윈치? 장밋빛이오? 그렇다면 더없이 좋소, 더없이 좋소! 아, 그런데 보기 좋구려! 아, 그런데 봄꽃처럼 젊고 싱그러워 보이는구려! 아, 착한 어린 소년! 용감한 아이, 용감한 아이!\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨에게 이런 찬사를 잔뜩 퍼붓는 동안, 그는 두 손을 그의 양어깨에 얹은 채 그를 이리저리 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 이런 형편에 그 어느 때보다 더 메마르고 더 비틀린 그 신사의 비틀거림은, 거의 힘이 다한 팽이의 그것과 흡사해졌다.\n\n\"지난번에 나는 우리가 더 친해지고 더 가깝게 알고 지내게 되리라는 예감이 들었소. 그게 당신에게 다가오고 있소, 플린트윈치? 아직 다가오고 있소?\"\n\n\"글쎄올시다, 선생.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 되받았다. \"별달리는 아니올시다. 앉으시는 게 낫지 않겠소? 그 포도주를 좀 더 청하셨던 것 같습니다만, 선생, 그렇지요?\"\n\n\"아, 귀여운 익살꾼! 귀여운 새끼 돼지!\" 손님이 외쳤다. \"하 하 하 하!\" 그러고는 마무리 삼아 한바탕 놀리듯 플린트윈치 씨를 휙 내던지고 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n이 모든 광경을 바라보는 아서를 사로잡은 놀라움, 의혹, 분노, 수치심이 그를 말문이 막히게 했다. 마지막으로 받은 그 힘에 떠밀려 두세 야드쯤 뒤로 빙그르르 밀려났던 플린트윈치 씨는, 숨이 가빠진 것 말고는 그 둔감한 표정이 조금도 변하지 않은 얼굴로 몸을 바로 세우고는, 아서를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 겉보기에 플린트윈치 씨는 여느 때와 다름없이 입을 다물고 나무토막 같았다. 그에게서 알아챌 수 있는 단 하나의 차이라면, 평소 귀 밑에 있던 넥타이 매듭이 머리 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 마치 가발 끄트머리 같은 장식물을 이루어, 그에게 어딘가 궁정 신하다운 풍모를 더해 주고 있다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n클레넘 부인이 블랑두아에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았듯이(그것은 마치 낮은 종류의 개에게 던지는 한결같은 눈길처럼 그에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤다), 제러마이어도 아서에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 마치 그들이 각자의 관할을 나누기로 암묵적으로 합의한 것 같았다. 그리하여 뒤이은 침묵 속에서, 제러마이어는 턱을 쓱쓱 문지르며, 마치 어떤 연장으로 아서에게서 그 속마음을 비틀어 빼내려는 듯 그를 바라보며 서 있었다.\n\n잠시 뒤, 손님은 그 침묵이 거북스러운 듯, 일어나서 그토록 오랜 세월 타올라 온 그 신성한 난롯불에 등을 대고 못마땅하게 자리 잡았다. 그러자 클레넘 부인이, 처음으로 한 손을 움직이며, 그것도 물러가라는 동작으로 아주 살짝 움직이며 말했다.\n\n\"우리 일은 우리에게 맡기고 자리를 비켜 주렴, 아서.\"\n\n\"어머니, 저는 내키지 않는 마음으로 그리합니다.\"\n\n\"무엇이 내키는지,\" 그녀가 받았다. \"혹은 무엇이 내키지 않는지는 상관없다. 자리를 비켜 주렴. 여기서 반 시간을 따분하게 묻어 두는 것을 의무라 여길 만한 다른 어느 때든 다시 오너라. 잘 자거라.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 평소 습관대로 자기 손가락에 그의 손가락을 갖다 댈 수 있도록 천에 감싼 손가락을 들어 올렸고, 그는 입술로 그녀의 얼굴을 만지려 바퀴 달린 의자 위로 몸을 숙였다. 그때 그는 그녀의 뺨이 여느 때보다 더 팽팽하고, 더 차갑다고 느꼈다. 다시 몸을 일으키며 그녀의 눈길이 향하는 쪽을 따라 플린트윈치 씨의 좋은 벗 블랑두아 씨 쪽을 바라보자, 블랑두아 씨가 엄지와 검지로 경멸에 찬 소리를 한 번 크게 딱 튕겼다.\n\n\"플린트윈치 씨, 나는 어머니의 방에 당신의 — 당신의 사업상 아는 분을 남겨 두고 갑니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"참으로 큰 놀라움과 참으로 큰 내키지 않음을 안고서 말입니다.\"\n\n방금 언급된 그 사람이 다시 엄지와 검지를 딱 튕겼다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무세요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"잘 자거라.\"\n\n\"내게도 한때 친구가 하나 있었소, 나의 선량한 동무 플린트윈치.\" 블랑두아가 난롯불 앞에 두 다리를 떡 벌리고 서서 말했는데, 그것이 너무도 분명히 클레넘의 물러가는 발걸음을 붙들려고 하는 말이어서, 그는 문 가까이에서 머뭇거렸다. \"내게도 한때 친구가 하나 있었소. 그는 이 도시의 어두운 면과 그 수법들에 관해 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 자기를 땅속에 묻어 버리는 데 이해관계가 걸린 두 사람과는 밤에 단둘이 자기 몸을 맡기려 하지 않았소 — 정말이지! 이처럼 점잖은 집에서조차 말이오 — 자기가 그들보다 몸으로 너무나 강하지 않은 한은 말이오. 흥! 얼마나 겁쟁이요, 내 플린트윈치! 응?\"\n\n\"개 같은 놈이지요, 선생.\"\n\"동감이오! 개 같은 놈. 하지만 그 친구는 말이오, 내 플린트윈치, 그들이 자기를 침묵시킬 의지는 있어도 힘은 없다는 걸 알지 못하는 한, 그런 짓은 하지 않았을 거요. 그런 형편에서는 물 한 잔도 마시지 않았을 거요 — 이처럼 점잖은 집에서조차도, 내 플린트윈치 — 그들 중 하나가 먼저 마시고, 그것도 삼키는 걸 보지 않는 한은 말이오!\"\n\n말을 하는 것조차 경멸스럽다는 듯, 실은 반쯤 숨이 막혀 제대로 말할 수도 없었던 클레넘은, 빠져나가면서 그저 손님을 한 번 흘끗 보았을 뿐이다. 손님은 작별 인사로 또 한 번 손가락을 딱 튕겨 그에게 답례했고, 그의 코는 콧수염 위로 내려오고 콧수염은 코 밑으로 올라가며, 불길하고 흉한 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"제발 부탁이오, 애프리.\" 그녀가 어두운 현관에서 그를 위해 문을 열어 주고 그가 더듬더듬 밤하늘이 보이는 쪽으로 길을 찾아 나가는 동안, 클레넘이 속삭였다. \"여기서 대체 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는 거요?\"\n\n앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 채 어둠 속에 서서, 그 뒤에서 낮고 죽은 듯한 목소리로 말하는 그녀 자신의 모습도 충분히 섬뜩했다.\n\n\"내게 아무것도 묻지 마세요, 아서. 나는 한참 전부터 꿈속에 있었으니까요. 어서 가세요!\"\n\n그가 나가자 그녀는 그의 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 그가 어머니 방의 창문들을 올려다보니, 노란 차양에 가려 죽은 듯이 흐려진 희미한 불빛이 애프리의 말에 화답하기라도 하듯, 이렇게 중얼거리는 것 같았다. \"내게 아무것도 묻지 마세요. 어서 가세요!\"\n\n친애하는 클레넘 씨께,\n\n지난번 편지에서 아무도 제게 편지를 쓰지 않는 편이 가장 좋겠다고 말씀드렸으니, 제가 또 이렇게 짧은 편지를 보낸다 해도 읽으시는 수고(어쩌면 그럴 짬조차 내지 못하실지도 모르지만, 언젠가는 그러시리라 바랍니다) 말고는 다른 폐를 끼치지 않을 테지요. 그래서 저는 이제 한 시간을 들여 다시 한번 당신께 편지를 쓰려고 합니다. 이번에는 로마에서 씁니다.\n\n우리는 가우언 부부보다 먼저 베네치아를 떠났지만, 그들은 우리만큼 길에서 오래 걸리지 않았고, 같은 길로 오지도 않았습니다. 그래서 우리가 도착했을 때 그들은 이곳, 비아 그레고리아나라는 곳의 셋집에 이미 들어 있었습니다. 아마 당신께서도 그곳을 아시리라 짐작합니다.\n\n이제 그들에 관해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 모든 것을 들려 드리려 합니다. 당신께서 가장 듣고 싶어 하시는 것이 바로 그것임을 제가 알기 때문입니다. 그들의 셋집은 그리 편안한 곳이 못 됩니다만, 처음 보았을 때 제가 당신보다 더 못한 곳으로 여겼을지도 모르겠습니다. 당신께서는 여러 나라를 다니셨고 여러 풍습을 보아 오셨으니까요. 물론 그곳은, 제가 근래까지 익숙했던 그 어떤 곳보다도 — 수백만 배나 — 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 곳입니다. 그래서 제가 그곳을 제 눈이 아니라 그분의 눈으로 보고 있는 것 같다는 생각이 듭니다. 그분이 큰 애정을 담아 제게 말씀하시지 않았더라도, 늘 다정하고 행복한 집안에서 자라났다는 것을 알아보기란 쉬웠을 테니까요.\n\n자, 그곳은 다소 어두컴컴한 공용 계단을 올라가 닿는 다소 휑한 셋집인데, 거의 전부가 가우언 씨가 그림을 그리는 크고 칙칙한 방 하나입니다. 밖을 내다볼 만한 곳의 창문은 죄다 막혀 있고, 벽이란 벽은 앞서 그곳에 살던 이들이 분필과 목탄으로 온통 끼적여 놓았는데 — 아, 짐작건대 — 여러 해에 걸쳐 그랬을 것 같습니다! 붉은빛이라기보다는 먼지 빛깔에 가까운 휘장이 방을 나누고 있고, 그 휘장 뒤의 부분이 사사로운 거실 노릇을 합니다. 제가 처음 그곳에서 그분을 보았을 때, 그분은 혼자였고, 손에서 일감이 떨어져 있었으며, 창문 윗부분으로 비쳐 드는 하늘을 올려다보고 있었습니다. 이런 말씀을 드려도 부디 마음 졸이지는 마시되, 그곳은 제가 바랐던 만큼 바람이 잘 통하지도, 환하지도, 명랑하지도, 또 전체적으로 행복하고 젊은 기운이 감돌지도 않았습니다.\n\n가우언 씨가 아버지의 초상화를 그리고 계신 덕분에(그가 그리는 모습을 직접 보지 못했더라면 그 닮음새만으로 아버지인 줄 알아보았을지 제 자신도 썩 자신이 없습니다만), 그 일이 없었더라면 좀처럼 갖지 못했을 그분과 함께 있을 기회를 그 다행스러운 우연 덕에 그 뒤로 더 많이 가질 수 있었습니다. 그분은 무척 외롭게 지냅니다. 정말이지 무척이나 외롭게요.\n\n제가 그분을 두 번째로 만났을 때의 이야기를 들려 드릴까요? 어느 날 마침 혼자 잠깐 들를 수 있게 되어, 오후 네다섯 시쯤 찾아갔습니다. 그때 그분은 혼자 식사를 하고 있었는데, 외로운 그 식사는 불을 담은 일종의 화로 위에 얹혀 어딘가에서 날라져 온 것이었고, 제가 보기에 그것을 가져온 노인 말고는 함께할 사람도, 함께할 기미도 없었습니다. 그 노인은 그분을 즐겁게 해 드리려고 긴 이야기를(성벽 밖의 도적들이 어느 성인의 돌 조각상에게 붙들려 갔다는 이야기를) 들려주고 있었는데 — 제가 나올 때 제게 한 말로는, \"자기에게도 딸이 하나 있는데, 비록 이 아가씨만큼 예쁘지는 않지만\" 그래서라더군요.\n\n그분에 관해 더 드릴 말씀이 얼마 남지 않았으니, 이제는 그 전에 가우언 씨 이야기를 해야겠습니다. 그는 분명 그분의 아름다움에 감탄할 테고, 그분을 자랑스러워할 것입니다. 모두가 그 아름다움을 칭찬하니까요. 또 그분을 아낄 것이 틀림없고, 저도 그가 그러하리라 의심치 않습니다 — 다만 그 나름의 방식으로요. 당신께서는 그 방식을 아시지요. 그것이 제 눈에 비치는 것처럼 당신의 눈에도 무심하고 불만스러워 보인다면, 그것이 그분께는 차라리 더 잘 어울렸으리라고 제가 생각하는 것도 그릇된 일은 아닐 것입니다. 만일 당신께는 그렇게 보이지 않는다면, 제가 아주 단단히 잘못 생각하고 있는 것이 분명합니다. 당신의 변함없는 가엾은 아이는, 애써 말로 다 표현할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더, 당신의 식견과 선량함을 믿기 때문입니다. 하지만 놀라지 마세요, 저는 그것을 말로 다 표현해 보려는 것이 아니니까요.\n\n(당신께서도 그렇게 생각하신다면, 제 생각으로는) 가우언 씨의 안정되지 못하고 만족할 줄 모르는 태도 때문에, 그는 자신의 일에 거의 마음을 쏟지 않습니다. 그는 무엇이든 꾸준하거나 끈기 있게 하지 않고, 똑같이 무언가를 집어 들었다가는 내던지고, 마음에 두는 법 없이 하다가도 내버려 둡니다. 초상화를 그리는 동안 그가 아버지와 이야기하는 것을 들으며, 저는 그가 자기 자신을 믿지 못하기에 다른 누구도 믿지 못하는 것은 아닐까 하고 앉아서 생각하곤 했습니다. 정말 그런 것일까요? 당신께서 이 대목에 이르면 무어라 말씀하실지 궁금합니다! 당신께서 어떤 표정을 지으실지 알 것 같고, 아이언 브리지 위에서라면 그 일을 제게 들려주실 그 목소리가 거의 들리는 듯합니다.\n\n가우언 씨는 이곳에서 가장 좋은 사교계라 여겨지는 곳에 자주 드나듭니다 — 정작 그 안에 있을 때면 그것을 즐기거나 좋아하는 것처럼 보이지 않습니다만 — 그분도 가끔 그를 따라가지만, 요즘 들어서는 거의 나가지 않습니다. 사람들이 그분에 관해 이야기하는 데에 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 구석이 있음을 제가 눈여겨본 것 같습니다. 마치 그분이 가우언 씨와 결혼함으로써 무슨 대단한 잇속을 챙긴 성공을 거두기라도 한 것처럼 말하면서도, 정작 바로 그 똑같은 사람들은 자기 자신이나 자기 딸이 그를 남편으로 삼는다는 것은 꿈에도 생각하지 않았을 테니까요. 그러고는 그는 또 시골로 나가 스케치를 할 궁리를 합니다. 그리고 방문객이 있는 곳이라면 어디서든 아는 이가 많고 아주 널리 알려져 있습니다. 이 모든 것에 더해, 집에서나 밖에서나 그와 자주 어울리는 친구가 하나 있는데, 그는 이 친구를 아주 쌀쌀맞게 대하고, 그를 대하는 태도가 종잡을 수 없습니다. 저는 (그분이 제게 그렇게 말씀하셨으니) 그분이 이 친구를 좋아하지 않는다는 것을 분명히 압니다. 그는 제게도 어찌나 역겨운지, 그가 지금 이곳을 떠나 있다는 것만으로도 제 마음이 한결 놓입니다. 하물며 그분의 마음은 얼마나 더 그렇겠습니까!\n\n하지만 제가 특별히 당신께 알려 드리고 싶은 것, 그리고 공연히 당신을 조금 불편하게 해 드릴까 두려우면서도 이토록 많은 것을 말씀드리기로 결심한 까닭은 이것입니다. 그분은 어찌나 진실하고 헌신적인지, 또 자신의 모든 사랑과 의무가 영원히 그의 것임을 어찌나 온전히 알고 계신지, 그분이 죽는 날까지 그를 사랑하고, 그에게 감탄하고, 그를 칭송하며, 그의 온갖 결점을 감싸 안으리라는 것을 당신께서는 확실히 믿으셔도 좋습니다. 저는 그분이 그 결점들을 감추고 있고, 앞으로도 늘 감출 것이라고 믿습니다, 심지어 자기 자신에게서까지도요. 그분은 그에게 결코 거두어들일 수 없는 마음을 주었습니다. 그가 아무리 그것을 시험하더라도, 그 애정을 결코 닳아 없어지게 하지는 못할 것입니다. 당신께서는 모든 것을 아시듯이 이것의 참됨도 저보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더 잘 아시겠지요. 하지만 그분이 어떤 천성을 지녔는지, 또 그분을 아무리 좋게 생각하셔도 지나치지 않다는 것을, 저는 당신께 말씀드리지 않을 수가 없습니다.\n\n이 편지에서 저는 아직 그분의 이름을 부르지 않았습니다만, 우리는 이제 무척 가까운 사이가 되어, 둘이 조용히 함께 있을 때면 저도 이름을 부르고, 그분도 제 이름으로 저를 부릅니다 — 제 세례명이 아니라, 당신께서 제게 지어 주신 이름으로요. 그분이 저를 에이미라고 부르기 시작했을 때, 저는 제 짧은 사연을 들려 드리며 당신께서 늘 저를 리틀 도릿이라 부르셨다고 말씀드렸습니다. 그 이름이 다른 어떤 이름보다도 제게 훨씬 더 소중하다고 했더니, 그분도 저를 리틀 도릿이라 부르신답니다.\n\n아마 당신께서는 아직 그분의 아버지나 어머니로부터 소식을 듣지 못하셨을 테고, 그래서 그분에게 사내아이가 태어났다는 것을 모르실 듯합니다. 아이는 불과 이틀 전에, 그들이 이곳에 온 지 꼭 일주일 만에 태어났습니다. 그 일로 그들은 무척 행복해합니다. 그렇지만 모든 것을 말씀드리기로 했으니 이 말씀도 드려야겠습니다. 제 생각에 그들은 가우언 씨 앞에서 어떤 거북함을 느끼는 것 같고, 그가 그들을 대하며 비웃는 듯한 태도를 보일 때면 그것이 그분을 향한 그들의 사랑에 대한 모욕처럼 느껴지는 모양입니다. 바로 어제, 제가 그곳에 있을 때에도, 미글스 씨가 낯빛이 변하더니 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나가는 것을 보았는데, 마치 그렇게라도 자신을 다잡지 않으면 그 말을 입 밖에 낼까 봐 두려워하는 것 같았습니다. 그렇지만 저는 그 두 분이 어찌나 사려 깊고 너그럽고 분별 있는지를 잘 알기에, 그가 그들을 좀 너그러이 대해도 좋으련만 싶습니다. 그가 그들을 조금이라도 더 헤아려 주지 않는 것은 모진 일입니다.\n\n저는 마지막 마침표에서 멈추고 이 모든 것을 다시 읽어 보았습니다. 처음에는 제가 이토록 많은 것을 제멋대로 이해하고 설명하려 든 것처럼 보여, 이 편지를 부치지 말까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들었습니다. 그러나 조금 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 제가 그저 당신을 위해 주의 깊게 살폈을 뿐이며, 제가 눈여겨보았다고 여기는 것들을 눈여겨본 까닭도 오로지 당신께서 그 일에 관심을 두신 덕에 제 마음이 움직였기 때문임을, 당신께서 단번에 알아주시리라는 희망이 더 솟아났습니다. 정말이지, 그것이 진실임을 믿으셔도 좋습니다.\n\n자, 이제 이번 편지에서는 그 이야기를 마치겠습니다. 남은 이야기는 별로 없습니다.\n\n우리는 모두 아주 잘 지내고 있고, 패니는 날마다 나아지고 있습니다. 그 언니가 제게 얼마나 다정한지, 또 저를 위해 얼마나 애를 쓰는지 당신께서는 좀처럼 짐작하지 못하실 것입니다. 언니에게는 구애하는 이가 하나 있는데, 처음에는 스위스에서부터 줄곧 따라왔고, 그다음에는 베네치아에서부터 줄곧 따라왔습니다. 그리고 방금 제게 어디든 언니를 따라다닐 작정이라고 털어놓았습니다. 그가 그 일을 제게 이야기하는 바람에 저는 무척 당황했지만, 그는 굳이 그러더군요. 저는 무어라 해야 할지 몰랐다가, 마침내 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각한다고 말했습니다. 패니는(이 말은 그에게 하지 않았습니다만) 그에게 어울리기에는 너무나 기개가 넘치고 영리하기 때문입니다. 그래도 그는 그래도 따라다닐 것이라고 했습니다. 저는 물론 구애하는 이가 없습니다.\n\n이 긴 편지에서 여기까지 읽으셨다면, 어쩌면 이렇게 말씀하실지도 모르겠습니다. \"리틀 도릿이 자기 여행 이야기를 조금도 들려주지 않고 끝맺지는 않겠지, 이제 들려줄 때도 되었고.\" 정말 그럴 때가 되었다고 저도 생각합니다만, 무엇을 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠습니다. 베네치아를 떠난 뒤로 우리는 제노바와 피렌체를 비롯해 무척 경이로운 곳을 숱하게 거쳤고, 어찌나 경이로운 광경을 많이 보았는지, 그것들이 한데 모여 이루는 무리를 떠올리면 거의 어지러울 지경입니다. 하지만 그것들에 관해서라면 제가 당신께 들려 드릴 수 있는 것보다 당신께서 제게 들려주실 수 있는 것이 훨씬 더 많으실 텐데, 어찌하여 제 이야기며 묘사로 당신을 지치게 해 드리겠습니까?\n\n친애하는 클레넘 씨, 예전에 여행하는 제 마음속에 늘 자리하던 어려움이 무엇인지 용기 내어 말씀드렸으니, 이제 와서 비겁해지지는 않겠습니다. 제가 자주 하는 생각 가운데 하나는 이것입니다. — 이 도시들이 아무리 오래되었다 한들, 제 사색에는 그 나이 자체보다도, 제가 그중 두셋의 존재조차 알지 못하던 그 시절, 우리의 그 낡은 담장 바깥의 무엇도 거의 알지 못하던 그 시절 내내, 이 도시들이 제자리에 있었다는 사실이 더 신기하게 다가옵니다. 그 안에는 어딘가 서글픈 무언가가 있는데, 그 까닭은 저도 모르겠습니다. 피사의 그 유명한 기울어진 탑을 보러 갔을 때, 그날은 환하고 볕 좋은 날이었고, 그 탑과 가까이 있는 건물들은 무척 오래되어 보였으며, 땅과 하늘은 무척 젊어 보였고, 땅 위에 드리운 그 그림자는 어찌나 부드럽고 조용했는지요! 처음에는 그것이 얼마나 아름다운지, 얼마나 신기한지 미처 헤아리지 못했지만, 이런 생각이 들었습니다. \"아, 담장의 그림자가 우리 방에 드리우고, 그 지친 발걸음 소리가 마당을 오르내리던 그 시절에도 — 아, 얼마나 여러 번 이곳이 오늘처럼 이토록 고요하고 사랑스러웠을까!\" 그 생각이 저를 온통 사로잡았습니다. 가슴이 어찌나 벅차오르던지, 애써 참아 보려 했지만 눈에서 눈물이 터져 나왔습니다. 그리고 저는 종종 — 자주 — 같은 감정을 느낍니다.\n\n아세요? 우리 형편이 달라진 뒤로, 제가 보기에는 전보다 꿈을 더 많이 꾸게 된 것 같은데, 꿈속의 저는 늘 정말이지 무척 어린 모습이랍니다! 제가 그리 나이를 먹지 않았다고 말씀하실지도 모르겠습니다. 그래요, 하지만 제가 말씀드리려는 것은 그게 아닙니다. 꿈속에서 저는 늘 바느질을 배우는 어린아이입니다. 거의 잊은 줄로만 알았던, 마당에서 보던 그리 낯익지 않은 얼굴들을 다시 보며, 그 옛 자리로 돌아가 있는 꿈을 자주 꾸었습니다. 하지만 더러는, 이곳 이국땅에 — 스위스든 프랑스든 이탈리아든, 우리가 다녀온 어딘가에 — 와 있는 꿈도 꾸는데, 그래도 늘 그 어린아이의 모습으로요. 제가 맨 처음 기억하는 제 모습 그대로, 옷에 헝겊을 덧댄 채로 제너럴 부인께 내려가는 꿈을 꾸기도 했습니다. 또 베네치아에서 손님이 많이 모인 자리에 앉아 식사를 하는 꿈을 거듭거듭 꾸었는데, 그때 저는 여덟 살 적에 입던, 가엾은 어머니를 여의고 입던 상복 차림이었습니다. 그 상복은 올이 다 닳고 더는 기울 수도 없게 된 뒤까지도 오래도록 입던 것이었지요. 그 손님들이 그 차림새를 아버지의 재산과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것으로 여길 테고, 또 식구들이 비밀로 감추고 싶어 하는 것을 제가 이토록 노골적으로 드러냄으로써 아버지와 패니와 에드워드를 얼마나 언짢게 하고 욕보일까 하는 생각에 저는 무척 괴로웠습니다. 하지만 그것을 생각하는 동안에도 저는 그 어린아이의 모습에서 벗어나지 못했고, 바로 그 같은 순간에, 식탁에 앉아 가슴 아파하며 그 식사 비용을 셈해 보고, 그 비용을 대체 어떻게 메워야 할지 골몰하느라 정신이 다 산란해지는 꿈을 꾸었습니다. 정작 우리 형편이 달라진 그 일 자체는 한 번도 꿈에 본 적이 없고, 그 잊지 못할 아침에 당신께서 저와 함께 돌아와 그 소식을 전해 주시던 일도 꿈에 본 적이 없으며, 심지어 당신을 꿈에 본 적조차 없습니다.\n\n친애하는 클레넘 씨, 어쩌면 제가 낮 동안 당신을 — 그리고 다른 이들을 — 너무도 많이 생각한 나머지, 밤에 당신 곁을 맴돌 생각이 더는 남아 있지 않은 것인지도 모르겠습니다. 이제 당신께 고백해야겠는데, 저는 향수병을 앓고 있습니다 — 집을 어찌나 간절하고 애틋하게 그리워하는지, 때로는 아무도 보지 않을 때면 그리움에 야위어 갈 지경입니다. 집에서 더 멀리 얼굴을 돌리는 것을 저는 견딜 수가 없습니다. 비록 몇 마일에 지나지 않더라도, 또 머잖아 다시 등을 돌리게 되리라는 것을 알면서도, 집 쪽으로 향할 때면 제 마음은 조금 가벼워집니다. 저는 제 가난과 당신의 친절이 깃든 그 풍경을 그토록 애틋이 사랑합니다. 아, 그토록 애틋이, 아, 그토록 애틋이요!\n\n당신의 가엾은 아이가 언제 다시 영국을 보게 될지는 하늘만이 아십니다. 우리는 모두 이곳의 생활을 좋아하고(저만 빼고요), 돌아갈 계획은 전혀 없습니다. 사랑하는 아버지께서는 재산과 관련된 무슨 일로 내년 늦봄에 런던을 방문할 이야기를 하시지만, 저를 함께 데려가시리라는 희망은 없습니다.\n\n저는 제너럴 부인의 가르침 아래 조금이라도 더 나아지려 애써 왔고, 예전만큼 그렇게 둔하지는 않게 된 것 같기를 바랍니다. 전에 말씀드렸던 그 어려운 언어들을 이제 거의 어려움 없이 말하고 알아듣기 시작했습니다. 지난번 편지를 쓸 때에는 당신께서 그 두 언어를 다 아신다는 것을 미처 떠올리지 못했지만, 나중에 그 생각이 나서 큰 도움이 되었습니다. 하느님의 가호가 있으시기를, 친애하는 클레넘 씨. 부디 잊지 마세요,\n\n언제까지나 감사하고 정겨운 마음을 담아\n\n# LITTLE DORRIT.\n\n리틀 도릿 올림.\n추신 — 미니 가우언이 당신께서 품으실 수 있는 가장 좋은 기억을 받을 자격이 있는 사람임을 특히 기억해 주세요. 그분을 아무리 너그럽게, 아무리 높이 생각하셔도 지나치지 않습니다. 지난번에는 팽크스 씨를 깜박했습니다. 부디 그분을 만나시거든 당신의 리틀 도릿이 안부를 전하더라고 일러 주세요. 그분은 리틀 D에게 무척 잘 대해 주셨답니다.\n\n# CHAPTER 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden\n\n머들이라는 그 이름난 이름은 날이 갈수록 이 나라에서 더욱 이름을 떨쳤다. 그토록 드높은 명성을 누리는 머들이 살아 있는 사람에게든 죽은 사람에게든, 아니 이 세상 그 어떤 것에든 무슨 좋은 일을 한 적이 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다. 그가 무슨 능력이나 어떤 견식을 지니고 있어, 아담의 아들들이 밟아 온 미궁 같은 그 수많은 길 가운데, 의무든 오락이든, 고통이든 기쁨이든, 고역이든 휴식이든, 사실이든 공상이든, 그 어떤 길 위에 누구를 위해서든 가장 희미한 동전 한 닢짜리 양초 불빛만큼이라도 빛을 비춰 준 적이 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다. 이 숭배의 대상이 빚어진 진흙이, 인간의 형상이 산산이 부서지지 않도록 붙들어 두는, 속에서 그을음만 피우는 막힌 심지를 품은 그 가장 흔해 빠진 진흙과 다를 것이라고 짐작할 만한 근거를 가진 자는 아무도 없었다. 모든 사람이 그가 스스로 어마어마한 부자가 되었다는 것을 알았고(혹은 안다고 여겼고), 오직 그 이유 하나만으로 그 앞에 엎드렸으니, 가장 미개한 야만인이 제 무지몽매한 영혼의 신을 통나무나 파충류 속에서 달래려 땅속 굴에서 기어 나오는 것보다 더 비굴하고 덜 변명할 만한 방식이었다.\n\n아니, 이 숭배의 대제사장들은 자기네의 비열함에 대한 항변으로서 그 사람을 바로 눈앞에 두고 있었다. 군중은 — 비록 언제나 그 까닭을 똑똑히 알고 있긴 했으나 — 믿음만으로 숭배했지만, 제단에서 의식을 집전하는 자들은 그 사람을 늘 눈앞에 두고 있었다. 그들은 그의 연회에 앉았고, 그도 그들의 연회에 앉았다. 늘 그를 따라다니는 유령이 하나 있어, 이 대제사장들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"그대들이 신뢰하고 기꺼이 떠받드는 징표가 이런 것들이오? 이 머리, 이 두 눈, 이 말투, 이 사람의 어조와 태도가? 그대들은 에두름국의 지렛대이며 인간들의 통치자요. 그대들 가운데 대여섯이 서로 멱살을 잡고 싸우면, 어머니 대지는 다른 통치자를 도무지 낳지 못할 것만 같소. 이 사람을 받아들이고, 떠받들고, 부풀려 주는 그 빼어난 사람 보는 안목에 그대들의 자격이 있는 것이오? 아니면, 그가 그대들 사이에 나타날 때마다 내가 어김없이 보여 주는 징표들을 옳게 판단할 능력이 그대들에게 있다면, 그대들의 빼어난 정직함이 곧 그대들의 자격인 것이오?\" 머들 씨를 따라 늘 시내를 돌아다니던 자못 추한 두 가지 질문이었으니, 그것들은 반드시 입막음되어야 한다는 암묵의 합의가 있었다.\n\n머들 부인이 외국으로 떠나 자리를 비운 동안에도, 머들 씨는 방문객들의 물결이 지나다닐 수 있도록 그 큰 저택을 여전히 열어 두었다. 그들 가운데 몇몇은 그 집을 제집처럼 스스럼없이 차지했다. 지체 높고 활달한 부인 서넛이 서로 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"다음 목요일에는 우리 사랑하는 머들 댁에서 식사합시다. 누구를 부를까요?\" 그러면 우리 사랑하는 머들은 지시를 받았고, 식탁의 손님들 사이에 묵직하게 앉아 있다가, 그 후에는 응접실들을 굼뜨게 어슬렁거리며 돌아다닐 뿐이었으니, 그 연회와는 거리에 거치적거린다는 것 말고는 아무 상관도 없어 보이는 점에서만 눈에 띄었다.\n\n이 대단한 사람의 인생을 따라다니는 복수의 정령인 집사장은 자기 엄격함을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았다. 그는 그 가슴이 있을 때 다른 만찬들을 지켜보던 것과 똑같이, 그 가슴이 없는 이런 만찬들도 지켜보았으며, 그의 눈길은 머들 씨에게 바실리스크와도 같았다. 그는 깐깐한 사람이어서 은식기 한 온스도, 포도주 한 병도 결코 깎으려 하지 않았다. 자기 기준에 미치지 못하면 만찬을 베푸는 것을 허락하지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신의 위엄을 위하여 식탁을 차렸다. 손님들이 차려진 음식을 들고 싶어 한다면 그로서는 반대할 까닭이 없었으나, 그것은 자기 지위를 유지하기 위하여 차려진 것이었다. 그가 찬장 옆에 서 있을 때면 이렇게 선언하는 듯했다. \"나는 지금 내 앞에 놓인 이것을 보기 위하여, 그리고 결코 이만 못한 것은 보지 않기 위하여 이 직책을 맡았노라.\" 좌중을 주재하는 그 가슴이 없으면, 그는 그것을 마치 자기 위엄의 한 부분으로 여겨, 불가피한 사정으로 잠시 빼앗긴 것처럼 아쉬워했으니, 마치 식탁 중앙 장식이나 골라 둔 포도주 냉각기가 은행가에게 보내져 자리를 비운 것을 아쉬워하듯 했다.\n\n머들 씨는 배너클 일가를 위한 만찬에 초대장을 보냈다. 데시머스 경이 올 예정이었고, 타이트 배너클 씨가 올 예정이었으며, 그 호감 가는 젊은 배너클이 올 예정이었다. 그리고 의회가 휴회하면 지방을 돌아다니며 그 우두머리의 칭송을 지저귀던 의회 배너클 합창단도 그 자리에 대표를 보낼 예정이었다. 그것은 대단한 행사로 여겨졌다. 머들 씨가 배너클 일가를 후원하려는 참이었다. 그와 고귀한 데시머스 사이에 — 그 매력적인 태도를 지닌 젊은 배너클이 교섭자 노릇을 하여 — 미묘한 작은 협상들이 오갔고, 머들 씨는 자신의 위대한 청렴과 위대한 재력의 무게를 배너클 쪽 저울에 실어 주기로 마음먹었다. 악의 있는 자들은 부정한 거래를 의심했으니, 어쩌면 그것은, 인류의 불멸의 원수*의 협력을 부정한 거래로 확보할 수 있었더라면 배너클 일가가 그자에게도 부정한 거래를 했으리라는 — 나라를 위하여, 나라를 위하여 — 것이 부인할 수 없는 사실이었기 때문이리라.\n\n* 인류의 불멸의 원수: 악마를 가리킴.\n머들 부인은 — 그를 휘팅턴 시절 이래의 모든 영국 상인을 하나로 뭉뚱그려 온통 세 자 두께로 도금한 존재보다 못한 무엇으로 여기는 것은 이단이었거니와 — 이 으리으리한 남편에게 로마에서 잇따라 여러 통의 편지를 보내, 에드먼드 스파클러의 앞날을 마련해 줄 때는 바로 지금이며 지금이 아니면 영영 안 된다고 줄기차게 다그쳤다. 머들 부인은 에드먼드의 처지가 급하다는 것을, 그리고 그에게 당장 무슨 좋은 자리가 생기면 무한한 이득이 따를 수 있다는 것을 그에게 일러 주었다. 이 막중한 주제에 관한 머들 부인의 동사 문법에는 오직 하나의 법(法), 곧 명령법만이 있었고, 그 법에는 오직 하나의 시제, 곧 현재시제만이 있었다. 머들 부인의 동사들은 어찌나 다급하게 머들 씨에게 들이밀어져 활용하라고 강요되었던지, 그의 굼뜬 피와 그의 긴 소맷부리까지도 자못 동요했다.\n\n그렇게 동요한 상태에서 머들 씨는, 그 어마어마한 인물의 생각이 가리키는 표지까지 눈을 들어 올리지는 못한 채 집사장의 구두 둘레로 슬그머니 눈알을 굴리면서, 특별 만찬을 베풀 작정임을 그에게 알렸다. 아주 큰 만찬은 아니고, 아주 특별한 만찬이라고. 집사장은 그에 답하여, 그런 식으로 할 수 있는 가장 값비싼 것을 지켜보는 데는 자기로서 반대가 없다고 알려 주었다. 그리하여 이제 만찬의 날이 다가왔다.\n\n머들 씨는 응접실 하나에 서서 난로를 등진 채, 중요한 손님들이 도착하기를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 완전히 혼자 있지 않은 한 난로를 등지고 서는 무례를 좀처럼, 아니 결코 부리지 않았다. 집사장이 있는 자리에서라면 그런 짓은 할 수 없었을 것이다. 만약 그 위압적인 하인이 바로 그 순간에 방에 나타났더라면, 그는 그 순경 같은 태도로 두 손목을 마주 잡고 난롯가 깔개 위를 왔다 갔다 하거나, 호화로운 가구들 사이를 슬금슬금 기어다녔을 것이다. 불길이 솟아오를 때면 숨어 있다가 튀어나오고 불길이 사그라들 때면 다시 숨어드는 듯한 그 교활한 그림자들이, 그가 그렇게 편안히 구는 것을 지켜보는 충분한 증인이었다. 그가 그 그림자들을 향해 던지는 불편한 눈길에 무슨 뜻이 담겨 있다고 본다면, 그 증인들은 충분하고도 남았다.\n\n머들 씨의 오른손에는 석간신문이 가득 쥐여 있었고, 석간신문에는 머들 씨가 가득했다. 그의 놀라운 사업, 그의 놀라운 재산, 그의 놀라운 은행이 그날 밤 석간신문을 살찌우는 양식이었다. 그가 주된 발기인이자 설립자이자 경영자인 그 놀라운 은행은 수많은 머들의 경이 가운데 가장 최근의 것이었다. 그토록 찬란한 업적들 한가운데서도 머들 씨가 어찌나 겸손해 보였던지, 작은 배들이 만찬을 향해 들어오는 동안 자기 난롯가 깔개 위에 떡 버티고 선 상업의 거인이라기보다는, 압류당한 채 자기 집에 들어앉아 있는 사람처럼 훨씬 더 보였다.\n\n보라, 배들이 항구로 들어온다! 매력적인 젊은 배너클이 가장 먼저 도착했으나, 바가 계단에서 그를 앞질렀다. 여느 때처럼 이중 안경과 그 잔뜩 처진 배심원용 표정으로 무장한 바는 매력적인 젊은 배너클을 보고 무척이나 반가워했다. 그러고는 우리가, 우리 법조인들이 말하는 식으로, 특별 변론을 듣기 위해 전원합의부*로 앉을 모양이냐고 물었다.\n* 전원합의부(in Banco): 법관 전원이 함께 앉아 심리하는 법정을 가리키는 라틴어 법률 용어.\n\n\"그래요,\" 페르디난드라는 이름의 그 발랄한 젊은 배너클이 말했다. \"어째서 그렇소?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 바가 미소 지었다. \"그대가 모른다면 내가 어찌 알겠소? 그대는 신전의 가장 깊은 지성소 안에 있고, 나는 바깥 들판에서 우러러보는 군중 가운데 하나일 뿐이오.\"\n\n바는 상대해야 할 손님에 따라 가볍게 다루기도, 무겁게 다루기도 했다. 페르디난드 배너클에게는 거미줄처럼 가뿐했다. 또한 바는 늘 — 제 나름의 방식으로 — 겸손하고 자기를 낮추었다. 바는 변화무쌍한 사람이었으나, 그 모든 무늬의 씨실을 관통하는 하나의 주된 실이 있었다. 그가 상대해야 하는 모든 사람은 그의 눈에 배심원이었고, 그는 할 수 있는 한 그 배심원을 자기편으로 넘어오게 만들어야 했다.\n\n\"우리의 저 빛나는 주인이자 벗,\" 바가 말했다. \"우리의 저 찬란한 상업의 별이 — 정치에 발을 들이려는 거요?\"\n\n\"들이다니요? 알다시피 그분은 이미 한동안 의회에 계셨는걸요,\" 매력적인 젊은 배너클이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"맞소,\" 특별 배심원을 위한 그 경희극풍 웃음을 띠며 바가 말했다 — 이것은 일반 배심에 앉은 우스꽝스러운 장사치들을 위한 그의 저급희극풍 웃음과는 사뭇 다른 것이었다. \"그분은 한동안 의회에 계셨소. 하지만 지금까지 우리의 그 별은 흔들리고 어른거리는 별이었지요? 흠?\"\n\n평범한 증인이라면 그 \"흠?\"에 넘어가 긍정의 대답을 했을 것이다. 그러나 페르디난드 배너클은 계단을 어슬렁어슬렁 올라가며 바를 의미심장하게 쳐다보고는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"바로 그렇지, 바로 그래,\" 그런 식으로 물러설 사람이 아닌지라 바는 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그래서 내가 특별 변론을 듣기 위해 전원합의부로 앉는다고 말한 것이오 — 이것이 높고 엄숙한 자리라는 뜻으로 말이오. 매키스 대위가 말하듯, '판관들이 모였으니, 무시무시한 광경이로다!' 하는 그런 자리 말이오. 보다시피 우리 법조인들은 그 대위를 인용할 만큼 너그럽다오, 비록 그 대위가 우리를 가혹하게 다루기는 하지만. 그렇긴 해도, 나는 그 대위의 한 가지 인정을 증거로 제출할 수 있을 것 같소,\" 바가 익살스럽게 고개를 살짝 까딱이며 말했다. 법률적인 말투의 흐름을 탈 때면, 그는 늘 세상에서 가장 우아한 태도로 제 자신을 놀리는 듯한 분위기를 풍겼던 것이다. \"법이, 대체로, 적어도 공정하게 의도되어 있다는 그 대위의 인정 말이오. 내가 그를 옳게 인용한다면 — 만약 그렇지 않다면,\" 이중 안경으로 동행의 어깨를 경희극풍으로 가볍게 톡 치며, \"여기 내 박식한 벗이 바로잡아 줄 터이지만 — 그 대위가 뭐라 했던가 하니:\n'법이란 만인을 위해 만들어진 것이라,\n\n남들의 악덕도 내 악덕도 다스리려 함이니,\n타이번 교수목* 위에 더 나은 동무가 없음을\n\n나는 의아해하노라!'\"\n* 타이번 교수목: 옛 런던의 공개 교수형장.\n\n이 말들과 함께 그들은 머들 씨가 난로 앞에 서 있는 응접실에 이르렀다. 그런 인용을 입에 담은 바가 들어서는 것을 보고 머들 씨가 어찌나 어마어마하게 놀랐던지, 바는 자기가 게이\\를 인용한 것이라고 해명했다. \"확실히 우리 웨스트민스터 홀\\의 권위자 가운데 하나는 아니지요,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 폭넓은 실무 지식을 갖춘 머들 씨처럼 세상 물정에 밝은 분께는 결코 얕볼 수 없는 권위자랍니다.\"\n* 게이: 「거지 오페라」를 쓴 극작가 존 게이. * 웨스트민스터 홀: 영국 법정이 열리던 곳.\n\n머들 씨는 무슨 말을 할까 생각하는 듯이 보이더니, 이내 하지 않는 게 좋겠다고 생각하는 듯이 보였다. 그 사이에 비숍*이 당도했음이 알려졌다.\n* 비숍: 주교를 가리키는 호칭이자 별명으로 쓰임.\n\n비숍은 온순하게 들어왔으나, 그러면서도 칠 리그 장화를 신은 정장 구두라도 신고서 온 세상을 한 바퀴 돌며 모든 이가 흡족한 상태인지 둘러보고 싶어 하는 듯한, 강하고 빠른 걸음걸이였다. 비숍은 이 자리에 무슨 의미심장한 일이 있다는 것을 전혀 알지 못했다. 그것이 그의 거동에서 가장 두드러진 특징이었다. 그는 산뜻하고, 생기 있고, 쾌활하고, 상냥하고, 부드러웠다. 그러면서도 놀라우리만치 순진했다.\n바는 슬그머니 다가가 비숍 부인의 건강에 관해 더없이 정중한 안부를 여쭈었다. 비숍 부인은 견진성사 때 감기에 걸린 점에서 조금 운이 나빴으나, 그 밖에는 평안하다고 했다. 젊은 비숍 씨도 평안했다. 그는 어린 아내와 어린 자식들과 함께 자기 사목지*에 내려가 있다고 했다.\n\n* 사목지(Cure of Souls): 성직자가 영혼을 돌보는 교구.\n다음으로 배너클 합창단의 대표들이 들어왔고, 그다음으로 머들 씨의 주치의가 들어왔다. 누구와 이야기를 나누든, 무슨 이야기를 하든, 문으로 들어오는 사람마다 한쪽 눈의 일부와 이중 안경의 일부를 늘 보내던 바는, 무슨 능숙한 수단을 써서, 그들에게 다가가는 것이 눈에 띄지 않으면서도 그들 모두 사이로 끼어들었고, 배심원 한 사람 한 사람의 저마다 좋아하는 급소를 건드렸다. 합창단 가운데 몇몇과는, 며칠 전 밤에 졸다가 로비로 나가 엉뚱한 쪽에 표를 던진 그 의원에 관해 함께 웃었다. 또 다른 몇몇과는, 공공의 직무와 공공의 돈에 대해 부자연스러운 관심을 갖는 것조차 막을 수 없는 그 시대의 혁신적 기풍을 함께 개탄했다. 주치의와는 전반적인 건강에 관해 한마디 나누었다. 또한 그에게 작은 정보를 하나 구하려 했으니, 어느 직업인에 관한 것으로 — 학식은 의심할 바 없고 태도는 세련되었으되, 그 두 자질이 가장 높은 경지로 발휘되는 것은 다른 치료술 종사자들의 소유라고 그가 믿는 인물(배심원용 처진 표정) — 그저께 그가 우연히 증인석에 세웠던 사람이며, 반대신문에서 그자가 스스로를 바로 이 새로운 치료법의 주창자 가운데 하나라 자처한다는 사실을 끌어냈는데, 바가 보기에 그 치료법은 — 어떻소? — 글쎄, 바는 그렇게 생각했다. 바는 주치의가 자기에게 그렇게 말해 주리라 생각했고, 또 그러기를 바랐다. 의사들끼리 의견이 갈리는 데서 감히 판단을 내리려는 것은 아니나, 이른바 법률적 통찰이 아니라 상식의 문제로 보건대, 이 새로운 체계는 — 이토록 위대한 권위자 앞에서 말하건대 — 말하자면, 엉터리*가 아니겠소? 아! 그런 격려에 힘입어 그는 감히 엉터리라고 말할 수 있었다. 그리하여 이제 바의 마음은 후련해졌다.\n\n* 엉터리(Humbug): 사기, 허튼소리를 뜻함.\n존슨 박사의 그 유명한 지인처럼, 머릿속에 단 하나의 생각밖에 없고 그마저도 틀린 생각이었던 타이트 배너클 씨가, 이때쯤 모습을 드러냈다. 이 저명한 신사와 머들 씨는, 난롯불 빛 속에서 노란 오토만 위에 서로 다른 방향을 향해 골똘히 되새기는 표정으로 앉아, 아무런 말도 주고받지 않은 채, 자기들 맞은편에 걸린 카위프*의 그림 속 두 마리 소와 전반적으로 강한 닮음을 보였다.\n\n* 카위프: 17세기 네덜란드 풍경화가 알베르트 카위프.\n그런데 이제 데시머스 경이 도착했다. 그때까지 들어오는 손님들을 (호의보다는 적의를 담아) 바라보는 것으로 자기 평소 직무의 한 갈래에 머물러 있던 집사장이, 그를 위해서는 분에 넘치게 몸소 계단을 함께 올라와 그의 당도를 알렸다. 데시머스 경이 위압적인 귀족인지라, 배너클 일가가 낚은 마지막에서 두 번째 물고기이자 이 자리에 자기 포획을 기념하려 초대된, 수줍은 젊은 하원의원은, 그 경이 들어서자 두 눈을 감아 버렸다.\n\n그렇지만 데시머스 경은 그 의원을 보고 기뻐했다. 머들 씨를 보고도 기뻐했고, 비숍을 보고도 기뻐했고, 바를 보고도 기뻐했고, 주치의를 보고도 기뻐했고, 타이트 배너클을 보고도 기뻐했고, 합창단을 보고도 기뻐했고, 자기 개인비서인 페르디난드를 보고도 기뻐했다. 데시머스 경은 세상에서 가장 위대한 인물 가운데 하나이긴 했으나 사람의 환심을 사는 태도로는 그리 두드러지지 못했는데, 페르디난드가 그곳에서 만날 만한 모든 친구를 알아보고 만나서 기쁘다고 말하는 데까지 그를 미리 일러 두었던 것이다. 이 활기와 겸양의 발작을 다 해내고 나자, 그 경은 카위프풍 그림 속으로 가라앉아, 그 무리에 세 번째 소가 되었다.\n\n배심원의 나머지를 다 확보했으니 이제는 배심장을 붙잡아야겠다고 느낀 바는, 이중 안경을 손에 들고 곧 슬그머니 다가왔다. 바는 공직의 신중함과는 얌전히 동떨어진 화제로서 날씨를 배심장의 고려에 올렸다. 바는 (누구나 늘 듣게 되는 말이지만, 누가 무엇 때문에 그런 말을 해 주는지는 영영 수수께끼인) 올해는 담장 과실이 없을 거라는 이야기를 들었노라고 했다. 데시머스 경은 자기 복숭아에 대해서는 무슨 나쁜 소식을 듣지 못했으나, 자기 집 사람들의 말이 옳다면 사과는 없을 듯하다고 했다. 사과가 없다고요? 바는 놀라움과 걱정에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 사실 지상에 사과 한 알이 없다 한들 그에게는 매한가지였겠으나, 이 사과 문제에 대한 그의 관심 어린 시늉은 그야말로 고통스러울 지경이었다. 그런데, 데시머스 경, 이것을 어디에 — 귀찮은 우리 법조인들은 정보를 모으기를 좋아하고, 그것이 우리에게 언제 얼마나 쓸모 있을지 도무지 알 수 없으니까요 — 데시머스 경, 이것을 어디에 돌려야 하겠습니까? 데시머스 경은 그에 관해 어떤 이론도 내놓겠다고 나설 수 없었다. 다른 사람이라면 여기서 멈췄을 법도 했으나, 바는 여느 때처럼 싱싱하게 그에게 들러붙으며 말했다. \"그러면 배는 어떻습니까, 자?\"\n\n바가 검찰총장이 되고 한참 뒤까지도 이 일은 그의 묘수로 회자되었다. 데시머스 경에게는 옛날 이튼 시절 사감 집 뒤뜰 근처 정원에서 자라던 배나무 한 그루에 얽힌 추억이 있었으니, 그 배나무 위에서 그의 평생 유일한 농담이 해마다 꽃을 피웠다. 그것은 짜임새 있고 간편한 종류의 농담으로, 이튼 배(pears)와 의회의 짝짓기 표결(pairs)* 사이의 차이를 가지고 노는 것이었다. 그러나 그것은, 그 나무와 속속들이 친밀하게 사귀지 않고서는 그 정묘한 묘미를 도무지 맛볼 수 없다고 데시머스 경에게는 여겨지는 듯한 농담이었다. 그러므로 그 이야기는 처음에는 그런 나무가 있다는 생각조차 없었다가, 차차 겨울 속에서 그것을 찾아내고, 변하는 계절을 거쳐 그것을 끌고 나가며, 그것이 움트는 것을 보고, 꽃피는 것을 보고, 열매 맺는 것을 보고, 열매가 익는 것을 보았다. 요컨대, 침실 창문을 빠져나가 그 열매를 훔치러 가기에 앞서 어찌나 부지런하고 세세하게 그 나무를 가꾸었던지, 그 나무가 데시머스 경 시대 이전에 심어지고 접붙여졌다는 데 대해, 늦게까지 붙들려 듣던 이들의 감사가 숱하게 바쳐졌을 정도였다. 사과에 대한 바의 관심은, 데시머스 경이 \"배 이야기를 하시니 어느 배나무 한 그루가 생각나는구려\" 하고 엄숙하게 운을 떼는 순간부터 \"그리하여 우리는 인생의 갖가지 변천을 거쳐, 이튼의 배에서 의회의 짝짓기 표결로 넘어가는 것이라오\" 하는 풍성한 결말에 이르기까지, 이 배들의 변천을 좇으며 빠져든 넋 나간 긴장에 어찌나 압도되었던지, 그는 데시머스 경과 함께 아래층으로 내려가야 했고, 그러고도 이 일화를 끝까지 듣기 위해 식탁에서 그의 옆자리에 앉아야 했다. 그쯤 되자 바는 배심장을 확보했다고 느꼈고, 좋은 식욕으로 만찬에 임할 수 있었다.\n* 배(pears)와 짝짓기 표결(pairs): 발음이 같음을 이용한 말장난. 의회의 'pair'는 표결 때 입장이 반대인 두 의원이 함께 기권하기로 짝을 짓는 관행.\n\n비록 식욕이 없었더라도 식욕을 돋울 만한 만찬이었다. 더없이 진귀한 요리들이 호사스럽게 조리되어 호사스럽게 차려졌고, 가장 고른 과일들, 가장 빼어난 포도주들, 금과 은, 도자기와 유리로 된 솜씨의 경이들, 미각과 후각과 시각을 만족시키는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 것들이 그 구성 속에 슬며시 짜여 들었다. 오, 이 머들이라는 자는 얼마나 놀라운 사람인가, 얼마나 위대한 사람인가, 얼마나 능란한 사람인가, 얼마나 복되고 부럽게 타고났는가 — 한마디로, 얼마나 부유한 사람인가!\n\n그는 여느 때처럼 소화도 안 되는 식으로 평소의 그 빈약한 일 실링 육 페니어치 음식을 들었고, 놀라운 사람치고 자기에 관해 할 말이 그토록 적은 사람도 없었다. 다행히도 데시머스 경은, 말을 걸어 줄 필요가 전혀 없는 그런 숭고한 인물 가운데 하나였으니, 언제든 제 자신의 위대함을 관조하는 것만으로 충분히 시간을 보낼 수 있는 까닭이었다. 덕분에 그 수줍은 젊은 의원은 한 번에 충분히 오래 눈을 떠서 자기 식사를 볼 수 있었다. 그러나 데시머스 경이 입을 열 때마다, 그는 다시 눈을 감았다.\n\n그 호감 가는 젊은 배너클과 바가 좌중의 이야기꾼이었다. 비숍도 무척이나 호감 가는 이야기꾼이 되었을 테지만, 그의 순진함이 걸림돌이었다. 그는 너무도 쉽게 뒤처졌다. 무슨 일이 진행되고 있다는 작은 낌새라도 있으면, 그는 곧장 길을 잃었다. 세상사는 그에게 벅찼다. 그는 그것을 도무지 알아채지 못했다.\n\n이 점은 바가 지나가는 말로, 우리가 머지않아 우리 벗 스파클러 씨의 — 과시하거나 떠벌리는 것이 아니라 철저히 건전하고 실무적인 — 그 건전하고 평이한 분별력을 좋은 편에 끌어들이는 이점을 누리게 되리라는 이야기를 들어 기쁘다고 했을 때 드러났다.\n\n페르디난드 배너클은 웃으며, 오 그렇소, 그런 줄로 안다고 했다. 한 표는 한 표이고, 언제나 환영받는 법이니까.\n\n바는 오늘 우리의 좋은 벗 스파클러 씨를 만나지 못해 아쉽다고 했다, 머들 씨.\n\n\"그 애는 머들 부인과 함께 떠나 있소,\" 그 신사가, 줄곧 식탁용 큰 숟가락을 자기 소매에 끼워 넣고 있던 긴 멍한 상태에서 천천히 깨어나며 대답했다. \"그 애가 꼭 이 자리에 있어야 하는 건 아니니까.\"\n\n\"머들이라는 그 마법의 이름이면,\" 바가 배심원용 처진 표정으로 말했다. \"틀림없이 모든 게 다 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"글쎄 — 그렇소 — 그런 줄로 아오,\" 머들 씨는 숟가락을 옆에 내려놓고 두 손을 어색하게 서로 상대 손의 소맷부리에 감추며 수긍했다. \"거기 내 영향권 안에 있는 사람들이 별 까다롭게 굴지는 않으리라 보오.\"\n\n\"본받을 만한 사람들이군요!\" 바가 말했다.\n\n\"그들을 그리 좋게 봐 주시니 기쁘오,\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 그 다른 두 곳의 사람들은요, 자,\" 바가 자기 으리으리한 옆 사람 쪽으로 살짝 눈길을 돌리며, 날카로운 눈에 환한 반짝임을 띠고 말을 이었다. \"우리 법조인들은 늘 호기심이 많고, 늘 캐묻기를 좋아하며, 우리 누덕이 같은 머릿속에 자투리들을 늘 주워 모으지요, 그것이 언제 어디서 어느 구석에 들어맞을지 알 수 없으니까요 — 그러면 그 다른 두 곳의 사람들은요? 그들도 이토록 가상하게, 그런 사업과 그런 명성의 광대하고 누적된 영향에 순순히 따르는지요? 그 작은 시냇물들도, 주위의 땅을 비옥하게 하며 경이로운 길을 따라 흐르는 그 장엄한 강물의 휩쓸림 속으로, 그토록 조용하고 수월하게, 말하자면 자연법칙의 작용에 의해 그토록 아름답게 흡수되어, 그 물길이 완벽하게 계산되고 또렷이 예측될 수 있는지요?\"\n\n바의 능변에 다소 곤혹스러워진 머들 씨는, 잠깐 동안 가장 가까운 소금통 둘레를 두서없이 바라보더니, 이윽고 머뭇거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그들은, 선생, 사회에 대한 자기네 의무를 완벽하게 알고 있소. 내가 그 목적으로 보내는 사람이면 누구든 당선시켜 줄 거요.\"\n\n\"기쁜 소식이군요,\" 바가 말했다. \"기쁜 소식입니다.\"\n\n문제의 그 세 곳은 이 섬나라의 자그마한 썩은 구멍 셋으로, 무지하고 술 취하고 게걸스럽고 지저분하며 외진 작은 선거구 셋을 품고 있었는데, 그것들이 비틀거리며 머들 씨의 호주머니 속으로 굴러떨어진 것이었다. 페르디난드 배너클은 그 느긋한 투로 웃으며, 참 괜찮은 친구들이라고 가볍게 말했다. 평화의 길을 거닐며 마음을 헤매던 비숍은 멍한 상태에 온통 삼켜져 있었다.\n\n\"한데,\" 데시머스 경이 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 물었다. \"채무자 감옥에 오래 갇혀 있던 어느 신사가 알고 보니 부유한 집안 출신이고, 막대한 돈의 유산을 물려받게 되었다는, 내가 들은 그 이야기는 대체 무엇이오? 그에 관해 이런저런 언급을 여러 번 접했소. 자네는 그에 관해 뭔가 아는 바가 있는가, 페르디난드?\"\n\n\"저는 이 정도밖에 모릅니다,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"그자가, 제가 몸담는 영광을 누리는 부처에\" — 이 반짝이는 젊은 배너클은 마치 '우리야 이런 말투를 빤히 알지만, 그래도 계속 써 줘야지, 이 놀음을 살려 두어야지' 하는 듯이 그 표현을 장난스럽게 내던졌다 — \"끝없는 골칫거리를 안겼고, 우리를 숱한 곤경에 빠뜨렸다는 것 정도지요.\"\n\n\"곤경이라?\" 데시머스 경이, 그 수줍은 의원으로 하여금 두 눈을 꼭 감게 만든, 위엄 있는 멈춤과 곱씹음으로 그 단어를 되뇌었다. \"곤경이라?\"\n\n\"실로 무척이나 골치 아픈 일이었습니다,\" 타이트 배너클 씨가 점잖은 분개의 기색으로 말했다.\n\n\"그래,\" 데시머스 경이 말했다. \"그자의 일이란 어떤 성격의 것이었는가? 그 — 어 — 곤경이라는 것의 본질이 무엇이었나, 페르디난드?\"\n\n\"오, 이야기로 치자면 괜찮은 이야기입니다,\" 그 신사가 대답했다. \"그런 종류로는 더 바랄 게 없는 이야기지요. 이 도릿 씨라는 자가(이름이 도릿입니다) 어떤 계약의 이행을 위해 서명한 증서에 따라 우리에게 책임을 지고 있었는데, 그 계약이 전혀 이행되지 않았던 거지요. 그 요정이 은행에서 나와 그에게 재산을 안겨 주기 오래전 일입니다. 그자는 무슨 큰 사업을 하는 상사의 동업자였는데 — 증류주든, 단추든, 포도주든, 구두약이든, 귀리든, 모직물이든, 돼지고기든, 갈고리단추든, 쇠붙이든, 당밀이든, 신발이든, 군대나 선원이나 누군가에게 필요한 그 무엇이든 — 아무튼 그 상사가 파산했고, 우리도 채권자 가운데 있던지라, 왕실 측에서 학술적인 방식으로 구금 신청을 걸고, 그 밖에 온갖 절차를 다 밟았지요. 요정이 나타나 그가 우리에게 빚을 갚으려 했을 때, 어럽쇼, 우리는 점검과 재점검, 서명과 부서(副署)의 그 본보기 같은 상태에 빠져 있어서, 그 돈을 어떻게 받아야 할지, 어떻게 영수증을 써 줘야 할지 알아내는 데 여섯 달이 걸렸습니다. 그것은 공무의 개가였지요,\" 이 잘생긴 젊은 배너클이 진심으로 껄껄 웃으며 말했다. \"평생 그렇게 많은 서식은 본 적이 없으실 겁니다. '아니' 하고 어느 날 그 변호사가 제게 말하더군요. '내가 이 부처더러 돈을 받으라는 게 아니라 나한테 이삼천 파운드를 내놓으라고 했어도, 이보다 더 애를 먹지는 않았겠소.' '맞는 말이오, 친구,' 제가 말했지요. '앞으로는 우리가 여기서 뭔가 하는 일이 있다는 걸 알게 될 거요.'\" 그 유쾌한 젊은 배너클은 다시 한 번 진심으로 껄껄 웃으며 말을 맺었다. 그는 실로 무척이나 느긋하고 유쾌한 친구였고, 그 태도는 더없이 사람을 끌어당겼다.\n\n이 일에 대한 타이트 배너클 씨의 견해는 덜 가뿐한 성격의 것이었다. 그는 도릿 씨가 돈을 갚으려 들어 부처를 성가시게 한 것을 못마땅하게 여겼고, 그토록 여러 해가 지난 뒤에 그런 짓을 한 것을 지극히 비정상적인 일로 여겼다. 그러나 타이트 배너클 씨는 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람이었고, 따라서 묵직한 사람이었다. 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람은 다들 묵직하다. 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람은 다들 신뢰받는다. 단추를 끄르는, 그러나 결코 행사되지 않는 비축된 권능이 인류를 매혹하는 것인지, 아니면 지혜란 단추를 채우면 응축되고 부풀며 단추를 끄르면 증발한다고 여겨지는 것인지는 모르겠으나, 중요성을 부여받는 사람이 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람이라는 것만은 확실하다. 타이트 배너클 씨는, 만약 그 외투가 늘 흰 크라바트까지 단추가 꼭꼭 채워져 있지 않았더라면, 결코 그 통용되는 가치의 절반만큼도 행세하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"여쭤도 되겠소,\" 데시머스 경이 말했다. \"그 대릿 씨가 — 아니 도릿 씨가 — 가족이 있소?\"\n\n아무도 대답하지 않자, 주인이 말했다. \"딸이 둘 있습니다, 각하.\"\n\n\"오! 그대가 그자를 아는 거요?\" 데시머스 경이 물었다.\n\n\"머들 부인이 압니다. 스파클러 씨도 압니다. 실은,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"그 젊은 숙녀 중 하나가 에드먼드 스파클러에게 인상을 남긴 게 아닌가 싶기도 합니다. 그 애가 마음이 잘 흔들리는 데다 — 제 — 생각엔 — 그 정복이 —\" 여기서 머들 씨는 말을 멈추고, 자기가 주목받거나 경청되고 있음을 깨달았을 때 늘 그러듯 식탁보를 내려다보았다.\n\n바는 머들 집안과 이 집안이 이미 접촉했다는 사실을 알고 유난히 기뻐했다. 그는 식탁 너머로 비숍에게 낮은 목소리로, 그것은 유유상종이라는 그 물리법칙의 일종의 유비적 예증이라고 했다. 그는 부가 부를 제게로 끌어당기는 이 인력을 무언가 자못 흥미롭고 신기한 것으로 — 자석이나 만유인력과 어딘지 모르게 닮은 무엇으로 — 여겼다. 지금의 주제가 꺼내질 때 다시 땅 위로 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아왔던 비숍은 이에 동의했다. 그는 말하기를, 뜻하지 않게 자기가 사회에서 선이든 악이든 행할 권능을 떠안게 되었음을 알게 된 그 곤란한 처지에 놓인 자가, 더 정당하고 더 거대하게 자라난 우월한 권능 — 그 영향력이 (우리가 그 식탁에 앉아 있는 우리 벗의 경우처럼) 사회의 최선의 이익과 조화롭게 늘 행사되는 그 권능 — 속으로 말하자면 흡수되어 들어가는 것은 실로 사회에 대단히 중요한 일이라고 했다. 그리하여, 저마다 음산하고 불확실한 불빛으로 타오르는, 크고 작은 두 개의 경쟁하고 다투는 불꽃 대신, 우리는 섞이고 누그러진 하나의 빛을 갖게 되며, 그 따스한 빛살이 온 나라에 고른 온기를 퍼뜨린다고 했다. 비숍은 그 비유를 자기 식으로 풀어놓은 것이 무척 마음에 드는 듯 그것을 자못 곱씹었고, 그동안 바는 (배심원 하나도 놓치지 않으려고) 그의 발치에 앉아 그 가르침을 받아먹는 시늉을 했다.\n\n만찬과 후식이 세 시간이나 이어지는 동안, 수줍은 의원은 음식과 술로 데워지는 것보다 더 빠르게 데시머스 경의 그늘 속에서 차게 식어, 으슬으슬한 시간을 보냈다. 데시머스 경은 평평한 들판에 우뚝 선 높은 탑처럼 식탁보를 가로질러 제 몸을 내미는 듯했고, 그 명예로운 의원에게서 빛을 가렸으며, 그 명예로운 의원의 골수까지 식혔고, 그에게 거리에 대한 비참한 관념을 안겨 주었다. 그가 이 불운한 길손에게 술을 들라 권할 때면, 그 비틀거리는 발걸음을 가장 음울한 그늘로 에워쌌고, \"당신의 건강을 위하여, 선생!\" 하고 말하면, 그 둘레는 온통 황량하고 적막했다.\n\n마침내 데시머스 경은 커피잔을 손에 들고 그림들 사이를 맴돌기 시작하더니, 그가 맴돌기를 그쳐 더 작은 새들이 위층으로 푸드덕 올라갈 수 있게 해 줄 가능성이 얼마나 되는가를 두고 모든 이의 마음에 흥미로운 추측이 일게 만들었으니, 그가 자기 고귀한 깃을 그쪽으로 펼치기 전에는 그렇게 될 수 없는 일이었다. 얼마간 미적거리고, 별 소득 없이 몇 차례 날개를 펴 보인 끝에, 그는 응접실들로 솟구쳐 올랐다.\n\n그리고 여기서, 만찬에서 두 사람이 서로 의논하라고 특별히 한자리에 모일 때면 늘 생기는 난처함이 일어났다. 모두가 (그런 낌새를 전혀 눈치채지 못한 비숍만 빼고) 이 만찬이, 데시머스 경과 머들 씨가 단둘이 오 분간 대화를 나누게 한다는 바로 그 목적을 위해 먹고 마셔진 것임을 완벽하게 알고 있었다. 그토록 공들여 마련된 기회가 이제 다가왔건만, 그 순간부터 인간의 그 어떤 묘책으로도 이 두 우두머리를 같은 방 안에 들여놓는 일조차 해낼 수 없을 것만 같았다. 머들 씨와 그 고귀한 손님은 원근법 풍경의 양 끝에서 어슬렁거리기를 고집했다. 매력적인 페르디난드가 데시머스 경을 머들 씨 곁의 청동 말들을 보러 데려오려 해도 헛수고였다. 그러면 머들 씨가 슬쩍 비켜 다른 데로 가 버렸다. 머들 씨를 데시머스 경에게 데려와 그 진귀한 드레스덴 화병들의 내력을 들려주게 하려 해도 헛수고였다. 그가 자기 사람을 그 자리까지 끌어올려 놓으면, 이번엔 데시머스 경이 슬쩍 비켜 다른 데로 가 버렸다.\n\n\"이런 일 본 적 있소?\" 스무 번이나 좌절한 페르디난드가 바에게 말했다.\n\n\"자주 봤지,\" 바가 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 한쪽을 정해 둔 구석으로 들이받아 몰고, 당신이 다른 쪽을 들이받아 몰지 않는 한,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"결국 성사되지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"좋소,\" 바가 말했다. \"원한다면 내가 머들을 들이받지요. 하지만 각하는 안 되오.\"\n\n페르디난드는 짜증이 난 와중에도 웃었다. \"둘 다 빌어먹을!\" 그가 시계를 보며 말했다. \"나는 자리를 뜨고 싶소. 대체 어째서 둘이 한자리에 오지 못한단 말이오! 둘 다 자기가 원하는 게 뭔지, 무얼 하려는지 알고 있건만. 저 꼴을 좀 보시오!\"\n\n그들은 여전히 원근법 풍경의 양 끝에서 어렴풋이 버티고 서서, 저마다 상대를 마음에 두지 않은 척 터무니없이 굴고 있었으니, 자기의 진짜 속마음이 등에 분필로 적혀 있었다 한들 그보다 더 빤히 우스꽝스러웠을 리 없었다. 방금 바와 페르디난드와 어울려 셋이 되었으나, 그 순진함이 다시 그를 화제에서 도려내어 향유로 씻어내 버린 비숍은, 데시머스 경에게 다가가 슬며시 대화 속으로 미끄러져 드는 것이 보였다.\n\n\"머들의 주치의를 시켜 그를 붙잡아 묶어 두게 해야겠소,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"그러고 나서 내가 저 빛나는 친척 양반을 붙들어, 할 수 있으면 꾀어서 — 안 되면 끌고서라도 — 회담으로 데려가야지.\"\n\n\"이 보잘것없는 나의 도움을 청하는 영광을 베푸시니,\" 바가 그 가장 능청스러운 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"더없이 기쁘게 그대 것이 되어 드리지요. 이건 한 사람으로 될 일이 아닌 듯하오. 하지만 그대가 각하를 지금 그토록 깊이 빠져 있는 저 가장 안쪽 응접실에 가둬 두기로 한다면, 나는 우리 사랑하는 머들을, 빠져나갈 가망 없이 그 면전으로 데려오기로 하지요.\"\n\n\"좋소!\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"좋소!\" 바가 말했다.\n\n이중 안경을 그 끈으로 멋들어지게 흔들고, 온 우주의 배심원들에게 멋들어지게 처진 표정을 지으며, 바가, 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 우연한 모양새로 머들 씨의 어깨 곁에 와 있는 모습은, 가히 볼 만한 진풍경이었고 또 속으로 꿍꿍이가 가득했다. 그러고는 그 기회를 빌려, 머들 씨의 실무 지식의 빛으로 특별히 인도받고 싶은 작은 문제 하나를 그에게 꺼냈다. (여기서 그는 머들 씨의 팔을 붙들고 슬며시 데려갔다.) 우리가 A. B.라 부를 어느 은행가가, 우리가 P. Q.라 부를 자기 고객 혹은 거래처에게, 우리가 만오천 파운드라 부를 상당한 금액을 빌려주었다고 합시다. (여기서, 그들이 데시머스 경 쪽으로 다가가고 있었으므로, 그는 머들 씨를 꽉 붙들었다.) 우리가 미망인이라 부를 그 P. Q.에게 이 대출금을 상환받기 위한 담보로, 우리가 블링키터 도들즈라 부를 어느 자유보유지의 권리증서가 A. B.의 손에 맡겨졌다고 합시다. 그런데, 요점은 이렇소. 블링키터 도들즈의 숲에서 나무를 베고 가지를 칠 제한된 권리가, 그때 이미 성년을 넘긴, 우리가 X. Y.라 부를 P. Q.의 아들에게 있었는데 — 아니, 이건 정말 너무하군요! 데시머스 경이 계신 자리에서, 우리의 메마른 법률 검불이나 썰어 주인을 붙들어 두다니, 정말 너무합니다! 다음 기회에! 바는 진심으로 뉘우치며, 다시는 한마디도 하지 않으리라 했다. 비숍이 자기에게 대여섯 마디만 베풀어 주시겠소? (이제 그는 머들 씨를 데시머스 경과 나란히 긴 의자에 앉혀 놓았으니, 지금이 아니면 영영 안 될 일이라 둘은 본론으로 들어가야 했다.)\n\n그리고 이제 나머지 좌중은, 무슨 일이 진행되고 있다는 것을 털끝만큼도 짐작하지 못한 비숍은 언제나처럼 예외로 하고, 몹시 들떠 흥미진진한 채로 옆 응접실 난로 둘레에 한 무리로 모여, 끝없이 다양한 자질구레한 화제로 스스럼없이 잡담하는 척했으나, 모두의 생각과 눈길은 은밀히 그 외딴 한 쌍을 향해 가고 있었다. 합창단은 지나치게 초조해했으니, 어쩌면 무슨 좋은 자리가 자기들에게서 빼돌려지려는 것이 아닌가 하는 무시무시한 불안에 시달리고 있었기 때문이리라! 오직 비숍만이 꾸준하고 차분하게 이야기했다. 그는 그 위대한 주치의와 더불어, 젊은 부목사들이 너무도 빈번히 시달리는 그 목쉼 증세에 관해, 그리고 교회에 그토록 만연한 그 병을 줄이는 방도에 관해 이야기를 나누었다. 주치의는 일반론으로서, 그것을 피하는 가장 좋은 방법은 낭독을 직업으로 삼기 전에 낭독하는 법을 아는 것이라는 의견이었다. 비숍은 미심쩍은 듯, 정말 그렇게 생각하시오, 하고 물었다. 주치의는 단호히, 그렇소, 그렇게 생각하오, 하고 답했다.\n\n한편 페르디난드는, 그 무리 바깥에서 어슬렁거리는 유일한 사람이었다. 그는 그 무리와 두 사람 사이의 한가운데쯤에 서서, 마치 데시머스 경이 머들 씨에게, 또는 머들 씨가 데시머스 경에게 무슨 외과 수술이라도 하고 있어, 자기 도움이 언제 보조의로 필요할지 모른다는 듯이 있었다. 과연 십오 분도 채 못 되어 데시머스 경이 그를 향해 \"페르디난드!\" 하고 불렀고, 그는 가서 다시 오 분쯤 그 회담에 자리를 차지했다. 그러자 합창단 사이로 반쯤 억눌린 헉 소리가 터져 나왔으니, 데시머스 경이 작별을 고하려 일어섰기 때문이었다. 또 한 번 페르디난드에게 인기를 끄는 데까지 미리 일러진 그는, 더없이 눈부신 솜씨로 좌중 모두와 악수를 나누었고, 심지어 바에게는 \"내 배 이야기에 지루하시지 않았소?\" 하고까지 말했다. 이에 바는 \"이튼의 것이었습니까, 각하, 아니면 의회의 것이었습니까?\" 하고 응수하여, 그 농담을 완벽히 익혔음을 깔끔하게 내보이며, 자기 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 결코 그것을 잊을 수 없으리라는 것을 은근히 내비쳤다.\n\n타이트 배너클 씨에게 단추로 꼭꼭 채워져 있던 그 온갖 점잖은 중요성이 다음으로 자리를 떴고, 그다음으로 페르디난드가 오페라를 보러 자리를 떴다. 나머지 몇몇은 잠시 머무적거리며, 머들 씨가 무슨 말이라도 하지 않을까 하는 가망 없는 기대에, 황금빛 리큐어 잔을 끈적한 고리 자국으로 불 세공 탁자에 맞붙여 놓았다. 그러나 머들은 여느 때처럼 자기 응접실을 굼뜨고 흐릿하게 스며 돌아다닐 뿐, 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n하루 이틀 사이에 온 도시에, 세계적 명성을 떨치는 저명한 머들 씨의 사위인 에드먼드 스파클러 귀하가 에두름국의 위원 가운데 하나로 임명되었다는 소식이 알려졌다. 그리고 모든 참된 신자에게, 이 훌륭한 임명은 우아하고 자비로운 데시머스가 그 상업적 이익에 — 위대한 상업 국가에서 마땅히 언제나 그러해야 할 — 그 어쩌고저쩌고에 바친 우아하고 자비로운 경의의 표시로 환영받아야 한다는 포고가, 나팔 소리와 함께 발해졌다. 그리하여 이 정부의 경의 표시에 떠받쳐져, 그 놀라운 은행과 그 밖의 온갖 놀라운 사업들은 계속되고 또 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그리고 구경꾼들은 캐번디시 광장 할리 가로 몰려와, 그저 그 황금의 경이가 사는 집을 구경하려 했다.\n\n그리고 집사장이 너그러운 순간에 현관문 밖을 내다보는 것을 보면, 구경꾼들은 그가 얼마나 부유해 보이는지 모르겠다고 했고, 그가 그 놀라운 은행에 돈을 얼마나 넣어 두었을까 궁금해했다. 그러나 그들이 그 존경스러운 응보의 여신을 좀 더 잘 알았더라면, 그것을 두고 궁금해하지 않았을 것이며, 그 액수를 더없이 정확하게 댈 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 13. The Progress of an Epidemic\n\n도덕적 감염을 막는 일이 신체적 감염을 막는 일 못지않게 적어도 어렵다는 것, 그러한 병이 흑사병의 악성과 신속함으로 퍼져 나가리라는 것, 그 전염이 일단 기세를 얻으면 어떤 직업도 신분도 가리지 않고, 가장 건강한 사람마저 붙들어 가장 있을 법하지 않은 체질에서까지 발현되리라는 것 — 이것은 우리 인간이라는 존재가 대기를 들이마신다는 사실만큼이나 경험으로 단단히 굳어진 사실이다. 이 독성 강한 병이 그 약함이나 사악함 속에서 자라나는 오염된 자들을, 독이 옮겨지기 전에 즉각 붙잡아 엄중히 감금할 수 있다면(아예 즉결로 질식시킨다고는 하지 않겠다) 인류에게 헤아릴 수 없는 축복이 내려질 것이다.\n\n거대한 불길이 멀리까지 그 포효로 공기를 가득 채우듯, 막강한 배너클 일가가 부채질한 그 신성한 불꽃은 공기를 점점 더 머들의 이름으로 울려 퍼지게 했다. 그 이름은 모든 입술에 얹혀 모든 귀로 실려 들어갔다. 머들 씨 같은 사람은 일찍이 없었고, 있었던 적도 없었으며, 다시는 있지 않을 것이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 아무도 그가 무엇을 했는지는 몰랐다. 그러나 누구나 그가 여태껏 나타난 가장 위대한 인물임은 알았다.\n\n임자 없는 반 페니 한 닢조차 없는 저 아래 '피 흘리는 심장의 마당'에서도, 이 인간 중의 표본에 대한 관심은 증권 거래소에서만큼이나 생생했다. 플로니시 부인은 이제 마당의 그 위쪽 끝, 계단 꼭대기에 자리한 아늑한 작은 가게에서 소규모 식료품 잡화상을 차려 놓고, 자그마한 노부친과 매기를 점원으로 부리며, 계산대 너머로 손님들과 대화를 나누면서 으레 그에 대해 떠벌리곤 했다. 인근의 작은 건축업체에 작은 지분을 가진 플로니시 씨는, 흙손을 쥐고 비계 꼭대기에서, 또 집의 기왓장 위에서 말하기를, 사람들이 자기한테 그러는데, 머들 씨야말로, 알겠수, 우리 모두가 바라는 그것에 관해서 우리를 죄다 바로잡아 줄 바로 그 사람이고, 우리 모두가 필요한 만큼, 알겠수, 우리를 죄다 무사히 데려다줄 그런 사람이라고 했다. 플로니시 부부의 유일한 하숙인인 밥티스트 씨는, 소박하고 절제된 삶의 결실로 모은 저축을 머들 씨의 확실한 사업 가운데 하나에 투자하려고 따로 챙겨 둔다는 소문이 수군수군 돌았다. '피 흘리는 심장'의 여인들은 차 몇 온스와 수다 몇 백 무게를 사러 올 때면 플로니시 부인에게 이렇게 일러 주었다. 글쎄, 부인, 그쪽 일을 하는 사촌 메리 앤한테서 들었는데, 그 양반 부인의 드레스만 해도 짐마차 세 대를 채운다지 뭐예요. 그 부인이 어디 사는 사람이든 둘째가라면 서러울 미인이고, 가슴받이는 대리석 그 자체 같다지 뭐예요. 그리고 들은 바로는, 부인, 정부에 들어간 건 그 부인이 전 남편한테서 낳은 아들이라는데, 그 아들은 장군이었고, 들은 게 다 사실이라면 군대를 거느리고 진군해서 승리로 영광을 얻었다지 뭐예요. 또 소문에 따르면 머들 씨가 한 말이, 자기한테 그럴 만한 값어치만 해 준다면 정부 전체를 떠맡되 이문 한 푼 안 남기고 떠맡을 텐데, 다만 떠맡고서 손해까지 보고 있을 수는 없다고 했다지 뭐예요. 그리고 부인, 그분이 손해를 보길 바랄 수야 없는 노릇이지요, 그분이 가는 길은, 거짓 한마디 안 보태고 말하자면, 황금으로 깔려 있다니까요. 다만 그분에게 그럴 값어치를 만들어 줄 만큼 뭔가 번듯한 게 마련되지 못한 건 참으로 안타까운 일이에요, 빵값과 푸줏간 고깃값이 얼마나 치솟았는지 아는 건 오직 그런 분뿐이고, 그 값을 끌어내릴 수 있고 또 끌어내릴 분도 오직 그런 분뿐이니까요.\n\n'피 흘리는 심장의 마당'에서 이 열병이 어찌나 만연하고 강력했던지, 팽크스 씨의 집세 받는 날조차 환자 발생을 멈추게 하지 못했다. 그런 날이면 병은 기이한 형태를 띠어, 감염된 자들로 하여금 그 마법 같은 이름에 빗대어 헤아릴 수 없는 핑계와 위안을 찾게 만들었다.\n\n\"자, 어서!\" 팽크스 씨는 집세를 밀린 하숙인에게 말하곤 했다. \"내시오! 어서!\"\n\n\"가진 게 없어요, 팽크스 씨.\" 체납자는 대답하곤 했다. \"사실대로 말씀드리는 겁니다, 나리. 내 한 몸 위로할 단돈 육 펜스도 없다니까요.\"\n\n\"이래선 안 되는 거, 알잖소.\" 팽크스 씨가 받아치곤 했다. \"이래도 된다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 응?\"\n\n체납자는 풀 죽은 \"안 됩니다, 나리\" 한마디로, 그런 기대는 없다고 인정하곤 했다.\n\n\"우리 집주인이 이걸 그냥 둘 사람이 아니라는 거, 알잖소.\" 팽크스 씨는 이어 가곤 했다. \"이러라고 날 여기 보낸 게 아니란 말이오. 내시오! 어서!\"\n\n체납자는 이렇게 대답하곤 했다. \"아아, 팽크스 씨. 내가 만약 누구나 입에 올리는 그 부자 양반이라면 — 내 이름이 머들이라면, 나리 — 당장 내고말고요, 기꺼이 그러겠어요.\"\n\n집세 문제에 관한 이런 대화는 대개 집 문간이나 출입구에서, 깊은 관심을 가진 '피 흘리는 심장'들 여럿이 보는 앞에서 벌어졌다. 그들은 이런 식의 비유가 나올 때면 늘 그것이 설득력 있다는 듯 낮은 웅성거림으로 호응했다. 그리고 체납자는 그 전까지 아무리 침울하고 풀이 죽어 있었어도, 그 말을 하는 동안만큼은 늘 조금 기운을 차렸다.\n\n\"내가 만약 머들 씨라면, 나리, 당신은 나에 대해 불평할 일이 없을 거예요. 정말이라니까요!\" 체납자는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 이어 가곤 했다. \"그러면 정말 후딱 내드릴 테니, 팽크스 씨, 당신이 달라고 청할 필요도 없을 거라고요.\"\n\n여기서 다시 호응이 들려오곤 했는데, 그보다 더 공정하게 말할 수는 없으며 이것이야말로 돈을 현장에서 내는 것 바로 다음가는 일이라는 뜻이었다.\n\n이제 팽크스 씨는 그 건을 장부에 적으며 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없게 되곤 했다. \"좋소! 당신은 압류 집행인을 맞아들이고 쫓겨나게 될 거요. 당신한테 닥칠 일이 그거란 말이오. 머들 씨 이야기를 나한테 해 봤자 소용없소. 당신은 머들 씨가 아니오, 내가 아닌 것이나 마찬가지지.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 나리.\" 체납자는 대답하곤 했다. \"그저 나리가 그분이었으면 싶을 뿐이라고요.\"\n\n여기서 호응이 재빨리 그 말을 받아, 깊은 감정을 담아 이렇게 답하곤 했다. \"그저 나리가 그분이었으면 싶을 뿐이라고요.\"\n\n\"나리가 만약 머들 씨라면 우리한테 더 너그러우실 거예요, 나리.\" 체납자는 기운이 솟아 이어 가곤 했다. \"그러면 모두한테 더 좋을 거고요. 우리한테도 좋고, 나리한테도 좋고요. 그러면 나리는 아무도 들들 볶을 필요가 없을 거예요. 우리를 볶을 필요도 없고, 나리 자신을 볶을 필요도 없겠죠. 마음이 더 편하실 거고, 다른 사람들도 더 편하게 두실 거예요, 나리가 머들 씨라면 정말 그러실 거라고요.\"\n\n이런 사람을 빗댄 칭찬이 팽크스 씨에게는 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 머쓱함을 자아냈던지라, 그는 그런 공격을 받고 나면 결코 기를 회복하지 못했다. 그저 손톱을 깨물며 씩씩거리고는 다음 체납자에게로 옮겨 가는 수밖에 없었다. 그러면 호응하던 '피 흘리는 심장'들이 그가 방금 포기하고 간 체납자 주위로 모여들었고, 그들 사이에서는 머들 씨의 현금이 얼마나 되는지에 관해 더없이 터무니없는 소문들이 떠돌며 큰 위안을 안겨 주곤 했다.\n\n수많은 집세 받는 날 가운데 어느 하루, 수많은 그런 패배 중 하나를 맛본 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 그날의 수금을 마치고 수첩을 옆구리에 낀 채 플로니시 부인의 모퉁이로 향했다. 팽크스 씨의 목적은 일이 아니라 친목이었다. 그는 고된 하루를 보낸 터라 기분을 좀 밝게 풀고 싶었다. 이즈음 그는 플로니시 가족과 친한 사이가 되어 있었으니, 비슷한 때에 자주 그들을 들여다보며 도릿 양에 관한 추억담에 한몫을 거들곤 했기 때문이다.\n\n플로니시 부인의 가게 안방은 그녀 자신의 안목 아래 꾸며진 것으로, 가게 쪽을 향한 면에는 그녀가 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐하는 작은 허구 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다. 안방을 이렇게 시적으로 한껏 돋운 것은, 벽을 초가집의 외관으로 그려 놓은 데 있었다. 화가는 (그것들의 심히 어긋난 크기와 양립할 수 있다고 본 한도 내에서 최대한 효과적으로) 실제 문과 창문을 그림 안에 끌어들여 놓았다. 소박한 해바라기와 접시꽃이 이 시골집 위에서 무성하게 만발한 것으로 그려졌고, 굴뚝에서 짙은 연기가 잔뜩 피어오르는 것은 안에서의 단란한 정경을 가리켰으며, 어쩌면 굴뚝을 근래 청소하지 않았음을 가리키기도 했다. 충직한 개 한 마리가 문지방에서 다정한 방문객의 다리를 향해 달려드는 것으로 그려졌고, 비둘기 떼 구름에 감싸인 둥근 비둘기장이 정원 울타리 뒤에서 솟아 있었다. 문에는 (그 문이 닫혀 있을 때) 놋쇠 명패 비슷한 것이 나타났는데, 거기에는 '행복한 오두막, T. & M. 플로니시'라는 글귀가 적혀 있었으니, 그 동업 관계는 부부를 뜻했다. 그 어떤 시도, 그 어떤 예술도 이 가짜 오두막 안에서 둘이 하나로 어우러진 모습이 플로니시 부인의 상상을 매혹한 것만큼 누군가의 상상을 매혹한 적은 없었다. 플로니시가 일을 마친 뒤 파이프 담배를 피우며 그 벽에 기대는 버릇이 있어서, 그의 모자가 비둘기장과 비둘기를 죄다 지워 버리고, 그의 등이 그 집을 통째로 삼켜 버리며, 주머니에 찔러 넣은 그의 두 손이 만발한 정원을 뿌리째 뽑고 인근 들판을 황폐하게 만든다 한들, 그녀에게는 아무래도 상관없었다. 플로니시 부인에게 그것은 여전히 더없이 아름다운 오두막이요, 더없이 놀라운 눈속임이었다. 그리고 플로니시 씨의 눈이 초가지붕 박공 침실보다 몇 인치 위에 있다 한들 아무 차이가 없었다. 가게 문을 닫은 뒤 가게로 나와, 이 오두막 안에서 아버지가 노래 부르는 것을 듣는 일은 플로니시 부인에게 완벽한 전원시였으니, 황금시대가 되살아난 것이었다. 그리고 정말로 그 유명한 시대가 되살아났거나 애당초 존재한 적이 있었다 한들, 그 시대가 이 가난한 여인보다 더 진심으로 흠모하는 딸들을 그리 많이 낳았을지는 의심스러운 일이다.\n\n가게 문에 달린 종이 딸랑거려 손님이 왔음을 알리자, 플로니시 부인은 누구인지 보려고 '행복한 오두막'에서 나왔다. \"팽크스 씨일 거라고 짐작했어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"딱 당신이 오는 날이잖아요, 그렇죠? 보세요, 아버지가 종소리에 손님 맞으러 나오셨어요, 부지런한 젊은 점원처럼요. 정정해 보이시죠? 아버지는 당신을 손님으로 맞는 것보다 더 반가워하세요, 수다 떠는 걸 무척 좋아하시거든요. 그리고 이야기가 도릿 양 쪽으로 흐르면 더더욱 좋아하시고요. 요즘처럼 아버지 목소리가 좋을 때를 들어 보신 적 없을 거예요.\" 플로니시 부인은 자랑스럽고 흐뭇한 나머지 제 목소리마저 떨렸다. \"어젯밤엔 스트레폰을 어찌나 잘 불러 주셨는지, 플로니시가 자리에서 일어나 식탁 너머로 이런 말을 다 했다니까요. '존 에드워드 낸디.' 플로니시가 아버지한테 그러더라고요. '제가 오늘 밤 당신 트릴 노래를 들은 것만큼 트릴 노래를 잘 부르시는 걸 들어 본 적이 없어요.' 정말 흐뭇한 일 아니에요, 팽크스 씨, 안 그래요?\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 더없이 다정한 태도로 노인을 향해 콧방귀를 한번 뀐 뒤 그렇다고 대답하고는, 그 활기찬 알트로 녀석이 아직 안 들어왔느냐고 무심코 물었다. 플로니시 부인은 아니라고, 아직이라고, 일거리를 들고 웨스트엔드에 갔는데 차 마실 시간쯤이면 돌아오겠다고 했더라고 답했다. 그러자 팽크스 씨는 융숭한 권유에 밀려 '행복한 오두막'으로 들어갔고, 거기서 학교에서 막 돌아온 큰 플로니시 도련님과 마주쳤다. 그는 그 어린 학생에게 그날의 학업 진척에 대해 가볍게 물어보다가, 큰 글씨와 알파벳 M을 배우는 윗반 학생들에게 '머들, 백만장자'라는 베껴 쓰기 본보기가 주어졌음을 알게 되었다.\n\n\"그래, 부인은 어떻게 지내십니까.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"백만장자 얘기가 나온 김에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"아주 꾸준하답니다, 정말로요, 나리.\" 플로니시 부인이 대답했다. \"아버지, 저, 차 마시기 전에 가게에 가서 진열창을 좀 정돈해 주시겠어요? 아버지 안목이 워낙 빼어나시잖아요.\"\n\n존 에드워드 낸디는 딸의 부탁을 들어주려고 흐뭇해하며 종종걸음으로 갔다. 플로니시 부인은 노인 앞에서 금전 문제를 입에 올리는 것을 늘 죽기보다 두려워했으니, 자기가 무슨 말이라도 흘렸다가 노인의 자존심을 건드려 구빈원으로 달아나게 만들까 봐서였다. 이렇게 해서 그녀는 자유로이 팽크스 씨와 은밀한 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 되었다.\n\n\"장사가 정말이지 아주 꾸준한 건 사실이에요.\" 플로니시 부인이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다. \"거래처도 훌륭하고요. 단 한 가지 걸림돌은요, 나리, 바로 외상이에요.\"\n\n이 결함은, '피 흘리는 심장의 마당' 주민들과 상거래에 발을 들인 대부분의 사람들이 꽤나 호되게 느끼는 것으로, 플로니시 부인의 장사에도 큰 걸림돌이었다. 도릿 씨가 그녀에게 이 장사를 차려 주었을 때, '피 흘리는 심장'들은 인간 본성에 영광을 돌릴 만한 감격과, 그녀를 떠받쳐 주겠다는 결의를 보였다. 그들의 공동체에 오래 몸담아 온 일원으로서 그들의 너그러운 정에 호소할 자격이 있음을 인정하여, 그들은 깊은 감정에 차서 무슨 일이 있어도 플로니시 부인하고만 거래하고 다른 어떤 가게에도 단골이 되어 주지 않겠노라 맹세했다. 이 고귀한 정서에 감화된 나머지, 그들은 평소 익숙하지 않던 식료품이나 버터 같은 자그마한 사치품까지 일부러 사들이기도 했다. 서로에게 이르기를, 비록 좀 무리하더라도 그게 이웃이자 친구를 위해서가 아니냐, 이런 사람을 위해서가 아니라면 누구를 위해 무리를 하겠느냐고 했다. 이렇게 부추겨진 덕에 장사는 대단히 활기를 띠었고, 재고품은 더없이 빠르게 빠져나갔다. 요컨대, '피 흘리는 심장'들이 돈을 내기만 했다면 이 사업은 완전한 성공을 거두었을 것이다. 그러나 그들이 외상 지는 일에만 오로지 매달린 탓에, 실제로 실현된 이윤은 아직 장부에 모습을 드러내기 시작조차 하지 않았다.\n\n팽크스 씨가 이 회계 상태를 곰곰이 들여다보며 머리카락을 곤두세워 영락없는 호저 꼴이 되어 가고 있을 때, 노인 낸디 씨가 수상쩍은 기색으로 오두막에 다시 들어와, 밥티스트 씨의 이상한 거동을 와서 좀 보라고 그들에게 청했다. 무언가 그를 겁먹게 한 일을 만난 것 같다는 것이었다. 셋이 모두 가게로 들어가 창문 너머로 지켜보니, 창백하고 동요한 밥티스트 씨가 다음과 같은 기이한 행동을 펼치고 있었다. 먼저 그는 마당으로 내려가는 계단 꼭대기에 몸을 숨긴 채, 가게 문 옆에 바짝 붙어 조심스레 고개를 내밀고는 거리를 위아래로 살피는 모습이었다. 무척이나 불안하게 살핀 끝에 그는 은신처에서 나와, 아주 떠나가기라도 하는 듯 거리를 따라 잰걸음으로 내려갔다. 그러다 갑자기 돌아서서는, 똑같은 걸음으로, 똑같은 위장을 하며 거리를 따라 올라갔다. 그는 내려갔던 만큼만 올라가고는 길을 가로질러 사라졌다. 이 마지막 술책의 목적은, 그가 계단 쪽에서 다시 불쑥 몸을 비틀며 가게로 들어왔을 때에야 비로소 드러났으니, 그가 마당의 반대쪽 끝, 곧 도이스앤클레넘 끝까지 멀고 은밀하게 빙 돌아, 마당을 가로질러 안으로 뛰어들었다는 것이었다. 그때쯤 그는 그럴 만도 하게 숨이 차 있었고, 그가 황급히 문을 닫는 바람에 뒤에서 떨리며 딸랑거리는 작은 가게 종보다도 그의 심장이 더 빠르게 두근거리는 듯했다.\n\n\"이봐, 친구!\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"알트로, 이 사람아! 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n밥티스트 씨, 곧 카발레토 씨는 이제 영어를 팽크스 씨 자신만큼이나 잘 알아들었고, 말도 아주 잘했다. 그럼에도 플로니시 부인은 자신을 거의 이탈리아 사람으로 만들어 주는 그 재주에 대한 가히 용서받을 만한 허영심에서, 통역으로 나섰다.\n\n\"이이가 알고 싶대요.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"무어가 잘모대써?\"\n\n\"행복한 작은 오두막으로 들어갑시다, 파드로나.\" 밥티스트 씨가 당황하여 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 흔들면서, 그 동작에 큰 은밀함을 담아 대답했다. \"저기로요!\"\n\n플로니시 부인은 파드로나라는 호칭이 자랑스러웠으니, 그것이 집의 안주인이라기보다 이탈리아 말의 안주인을 뜻한다고 여겼던 것이다. 그녀는 즉시 밥티스트 씨의 청을 따랐고, 모두 오두막으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이이가 바라기를 다미 안 노라긔 바래요.\" 그제야 플로니시 부인이 늘 그렇듯 풍부한 임기응변으로 팽크스를 새로운 방식으로 통역하며 말했다. \"무순 이리야? 마래봐 파드로나!\"\n\n\"누군가를 봤어요.\" 밥티스트가 대답했다. \"그 사람을 린콘트라토했어요.\"\n\n\"그 사람? 누구요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"나쁜 사람. 제일 나쁜 사람. 다시는 그를 안 보길 바랐는데요.\"\n\n\"그 사라미 나뿐 거 어떠케 아라요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"그건 중요하지 않아요, 파드로나. 너무도 잘 알아요.\"\n\n\"그 사라미 다실 봐써요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"아니요. 안 봤기를 바라요. 안 봤다고 믿어요.\"\n\n\"이이 말이,\" 그러자 플로니시 부인이 온화한 겸양으로 아버지와 팽크스를 향해 통역했다. \"나쁜 사람을 만났는데, 그 나쁜 사람이 자기를 못 봤기를 바란대요. 그런데 — 어째서,\" 플로니시 부인이 이탈리아 말로 되돌아가 물었다. \"어째서 나뿐 사라미 안 보기 바래요?\"\n\n\"파드로나, 사랑하는 분.\" 그녀가 그토록 다정하게 보호해 주는 그 작은 외국인이 대답했다. \"묻지 말아 주세요, 부탁이에요. 다시 한번 말하지만 그건 중요하지 않아요. 나는 이 사람이 두려워요. 그를 보고 싶지 않고, 그에게 알려지고 싶지 않아요 — 다시는요! 그걸로 충분해요, 더없이 아름다운 분. 그냥 두세요.\"\n\n이 화제가 그에게 어찌나 거북했던지, 또 그의 평소 활기를 어찌나 무너뜨렸던지, 플로니시 부인은 더 캐묻기를 삼갔다. 차가 화덕 선반 위에서 한참 우러나고 있던 터라 더욱 그러했다. 그러나 더 묻지 않았다고 해서 놀라움과 호기심이 덜한 것은 아니었으며, 팽크스 씨도 마찬가지였으니, 그 작은 사내가 들어온 뒤로 그의 표정 풍부한 숨소리는, 무거운 짐을 끌고 가파른 비탈을 오르는 기관차처럼 힘겹게 헐떡이고 있었다. 매기는 이제 예전보다 옷차림이 나아졌으되 여전히 그 괴상한 모자의 됨됨이에는 충실했는데, 처음부터 입을 떡 벌리고 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜬 채 뒷전에 있었으니, 화제가 때아니게 억눌렸다고 해서 그 멍하니 벌어진 이목구비의 너비가 줄어들지는 않았다. 어쨌든 그 일에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 나오지 않았으나, 모두가 속으로는 많은 생각을 하는 듯했다. 두 어린 플로니시도 결코 예외가 아니었으니, 그들은 마치 사람 중에 가장 못된 자가 곧 그들을 잡아먹으려고 들이닥치리라는 괴로운 가능성 탓에 빵과 버터를 먹는 일이 거의 부질없게 된 것처럼 저녁을 먹었다. 밥티스트 씨는 차츰 조금씩 재잘거리기 시작했으나, 평소 자기 자리도 아니건만 문 뒤, 창문 가까이 잡은 자리에서는 결코 움직이지 않았다. 작은 종이 울릴 때마다 그는 흠칫 놀라, 작은 커튼 자락을 손에 쥐고 나머지로 얼굴을 가린 채 몰래 밖을 엿보았으니, 자기가 두려워하는 그 사람이 무서운 사냥개의 확실함으로 자기의 온갖 우회와 방향 전환을 죄다 추적해 왔으리라는 의심을 도무지 떨치지 못하는 게 분명했다.\n\n이따금 손님 두셋과 플로니시 씨가 들어오는 통에, 밥티스트 씨는 꼭 그만큼 이 일에 매달려 좌중의 주의를 자기에게 붙들어 두기에 충분했다. 차도 다 끝나고 아이들도 잠자리에 든 뒤, 플로니시 부인이 아버지더러 '클로이'를 한 곡 들려주시는 게 자식 된 도리로 마땅하지 않겠느냐는 제안을 슬슬 꺼내려던 참에, 종이 다시 울리며 클레넘 씨가 들어왔다.\n\n클레넘은 늦도록 장부와 편지를 들여다보고 있었으니, 에두름국의 대기실들이 그의 시간을 모질게 갉아먹은 탓이었다. 그뿐 아니라 그는 근래 어머니 댁에서 있었던 일로 침울하고 불안한 상태였다. 그는 지치고 외로워 보였다. 실제로 그렇게 느끼기도 했다. 그럼에도 그는 도릿 양에게서 또 한 통의 편지를 받았다는 소식을 그들에게 전하려고, 사무소에서 집으로 돌아가는 길에 마당의 그 끝을 택해 들른 것이었다.\n\n그 소식은 오두막에 일대 파문을 일으켜 좌중의 관심을 밥티스트 씨에게서 거두어 갔다. 즉시 앞쪽으로 비집고 나온 매기는, 마치 제 작은 어머니의 소식을 귀로, 코로, 입으로, 눈으로 한꺼번에 빨아들이는 듯 보였을 테지만, 마지막 것은 눈물에 가로막혀 있었다. 로마에도 병원이, 그것도 무척 친절하게 운영되는 병원이 있다고 클레넘이 그녀를 안심시키자, 그녀는 유난히 기뻐했다. 팽크스 씨는 편지에 특별히 그 이름이 기억되어 있는 덕에 새로운 명예에 올랐다. 모두가 기뻐하고 흥미로워했으니, 클레넘은 수고에 대한 충분한 보답을 받은 셈이었다.\n\n\"하지만 지치셨네요, 나리. 제가 차 한 잔 타 드릴게요.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"이 오두막에서 그런 걸 드시는 걸 마다하지 않으신다면요. 그리고 저희를 이렇게 다정하게 마음에 담아 주셔서 정말 감사드려요.\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 집주인으로서 자기 나름의 사례를 보태는 것이 도리라 여겨, 격식과 진심의 결합에 관한 자신의 최고 이상을 늘 표현해 온 그 형식으로 사례를 올렸다.\n\n\"존 에드워드 낸디.\" 플로니시 씨가 노신사를 향해 말했다. \"나리. 거만함이라곤 티끌만큼도 없는, 꾸밈없는 행동을 보는 일이 그리 흔치가 않으니, 그러므로 그런 걸 보거든 거기에 감사한 영예를 돌려야 하는 법이지요. 안 그러고서 그런 걸 아쉬워할 때까지 살게 된다면, 그건 자업자득이 따르는 거니까요.\"\n\n이에 낸디 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"진심으로 자네 의견에 동감일세, 토머스, 그리고 자네 의견이 곧 내 의견인즉, 그러므로 더 말이 필요 없고, 그 의견을 내놓기를 주저하지 않으니, 그 의견을 그렇다고 내놓는바, 토머스, 그렇다고 내놓는바, 그건 자네와 나가 모든 이와 더불어 언제나 만장일치로 한데 묶일 수밖에 없는 그 의견이고, 의견의 차이가 없는 곳에는 오직 하나의 의견밖엔 있을 수 없으니, 그것은 완전히 아니로다, 토머스, 토머스, 아니로다!\"\n\n아서는 그보다 덜 격식을 차려, 그토록 사소한 자신의 관심을 그들이 높이 평가해 주니 흐뭇하다고 표하고는, 차에 관해서는 아직 저녁을 들지 않은 데다 긴 하루의 노동 끝에 곧장 집으로 가서 기운을 차리려던 참이라고, 그렇지 않았다면 기꺼이 그 융숭한 권유를 받아들였을 거라고 설명했다. 팽크스 씨가 떠날 채비로 다소 요란하게 증기를 올리고 있던지라, 아서는 끝으로 그에게 함께 걸어가겠느냐고 물었다. 팽크스 씨는 그보다 더 바라는 일이 없다고 했고, 두 사람은 '행복한 오두막'에 작별을 고했다.\n\n\"우리 집에 같이 가서, 팽크스.\" 거리로 나서자 아서가 말했다. \"있는 대로 저녁이든 야참이든 나눠 들면, 그건 자선 행위나 다름없을 걸세. 오늘 밤 내가 지치고 기분이 영 좋지 않거든.\"\n\n\"그보다 더한 일을 시키시구려.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"필요할 때 시키시면 해 드리리다.\"\n\n이 별난 인물과 클레넘 사이에는, 팽크스 씨가 마셜시 마당에서 러그 씨의 등 너머로 펄쩍 뛰어오른 그날 이후로 줄곧 무언의 이해와 교감이 깊어져 왔다. 가족이 떠나던 그 잊지 못할 날, 마차가 달려가 버렸을 때 이 두 사람은 함께 그 뒤를 바라보았고, 함께 천천히 걸어 나왔던 것이다. 리틀 도릿에게서 첫 편지가 왔을 때, 그녀의 소식을 듣는 일에 팽크스 씨보다 더 관심을 가진 사람은 없었다. 지금 클레넘의 가슴 주머니에 든 두 번째 편지는 특별히 그의 이름을 기억해 주고 있었다. 그가 전에는 클레넘에게 어떤 고백이나 다짐도 한 적이 없었고, 방금 한 말도 그 표현된 어구로만 보자면 미미하기 짝이 없었으나, 클레넘은 오래전부터 팽크스 씨가 제 나름의 별난 방식으로 자신에게 정이 들고 있다는 믿음이 점점 자라나던 터였다. 이 모든 끈이 한데 얽혀, 그날 밤 팽크스는 그야말로 닻줄 같은 존재가 되어 주었다.\n\n\"나는 완전히 혼자라네.\" 걸어가며 아서가 설명했다. \"동업자는 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 우리 사업의 자기 부문 일로 분주히 매달려 있거든. 그러니 자넨 좋을 대로 하게나.\"\n\n\"고맙소. 헌데 아까 어린 알트로를 눈여겨보지 않으셨지요?\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\"그래. 왜 그러나?\"\n\n\"명민한 친구라 내가 좋아하는데.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"오늘 뭔가 그에게 잘못된 일이 있었구려. 그를 그렇게 뒤흔들어 놓을 만한 무슨 까닭이 있는지 짚이는 데가 있소?\"\n\n\"자네가 날 놀라게 하는군! 전혀 모르겠는데.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 그렇게 물은 까닭을 댔다. 아서는 그에 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았고, 그에 대한 설명도 도무지 내놓을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"그자한테 한번 물어보시구려.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"외국 사람이니까 말이오.\"\n\n\"무엇을 물어보란 말인가?\" 클레넘이 되물었다.\n\n\"무슨 근심이 있는지를요.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 먼저 그에게 정말 무슨 근심이 있는지를 내 눈으로 확인해야 할 것 같네.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"나는 그가 무슨 일에서나 그토록 부지런하고, (그 적은 것에도) 그토록 고마워하고, 그토록 믿음직한 사람임을 알게 되었으니, 그러면 그를 의심하는 것처럼 보일 거야. 그건 무척 부당한 일이겠지.\"\n\n\"맞소.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"허나, 그게 말이오! 클레넘 씨, 당신은 누구의 집주인 노릇도 해선 안 될 사람이오. 너무 마음이 여리거든.\"\n\n\"그 점으로 말하자면,\" 클레넘이 웃으며 대답했다. \"나는 카발레토에 대해 큰 소유주 지분이 있는 게 아닐세. 그의 조각이 그의 생계지. 그는 공장 열쇠를 맡아 두고, 격일로 밤마다 그곳을 지키고, 대체로 일종의 관리인 노릇을 하지. 다만 우리한테는 그의 솜씨를 쓸 일감이 거의 없어. 있는 만큼은 그에게 주지만 말이야. 아니야! 나는 그의 소유주라기보다 차라리 그의 조언자에 가깝지. 그의 고문이자 은행 노릇을 한다고 하는 편이 사실에 더 가까울 걸세. 그의 은행 노릇 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 팽크스, 요즘 그토록 많은 사람들 머릿속에 도는 그 투기가 어린 카발레토의 머릿속에까지 돈다는 게 묘하지 않은가?\"\n\n\"투기요?\" 팽크스가 콧방귀를 뀌며 받아쳤다. \"무슨 투기 말이오?\"\n\n\"이 머들의 사업들 말일세.\"\n\n\"아아! 투자 말이군요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그렇지, 그렇지! 당신이 투자 얘기를 하는 줄 몰랐소.\"\n\n그가 재빨리 대답하는 품에, 클레넘은 그가 말 이상의 무언가를 뜻하는 게 아닌가 하는 의심으로 그를 쳐다보았다. 그러나 그 대답에 발걸음이 빨라지고 그에 걸맞게 기관 가동이 더 힘차지는 것이 따라붙는지라, 아서는 그 문제를 더 캐지 않았고, 두 사람은 곧 그의 집에 다다랐다.\n\n수프 한 그릇과 비둘기 파이로 차린 저녁이 난롯불 앞 작은 둥근 탁자에 올라, 좋은 포도주 한 병으로 풍미를 더하니, 팽크스 씨의 작동부에 더없이 효과적으로 기름칠을 해 주었다. 그리하여 클레넘이 자신의 동방산 파이프를 꺼내고 팽크스 씨에게도 다른 동방산 파이프를 건넸을 때, 후자의 신사는 더없이 편안한 상태였다.\n\n두 사람은 한동안 말없이 담배를 피웠다. 팽크스 씨는 바람도, 조류도, 잔잔한 물도, 그 밖의 모든 항해 조건도 죄다 자기편인 증기선 같았다. 먼저 입을 연 것은 그였고, 그는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그래요. 투자가 맞는 말이오.\"\n\n클레넘은 아까와 같은 표정으로 \"아!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n\"보다시피 그 얘기로 되돌아가는 거요.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 자네가 그 얘기로 되돌아가는 게 보이네.\" 클레넘이 왜 그러나 궁금해하며 대답했다.\n\n\"그게 어린 알트로 머릿속에까지 돈다는 게 묘한 일 아니오, 응?\" 팽크스가 담배를 피우며 말했다. \"당신이 그렇게 말하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"내가 한 말이 그거지.\"\n\n\"그렇소! 헌데 마당 전체가 그걸 손에 넣었다고 생각해 보시오. 그자들이 죄다 그걸로 날 맞이한다고 생각해 보란 말이오, 수금하는 날이면, 여기서도 저기서도 사방에서. 집세를 내든 안 내든 말이오. 머들, 머들, 머들. 노상 머들이오.\"\n\n\"이런 열광이 번지는 것이 참 이상도 하지.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇잖소?\" 팽크스가 대꾸했다. 방금 받은 기름칠에 어울리지 않게 다소 메마르게 잠시 담배를 피우고 나서, 그가 덧붙였다. \"왜냐면 보시오, 그 사람들은 이 문제를 이해하지 못하거든.\"\n\n\"조금도 못 하지.\" 클레넘이 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"조금도요.\" 팽크스가 외쳤다. \"숫자라곤 아무것도 모르고. 돈 문제라곤 아무것도 모르고. 계산이라곤 해 본 적이 없고. 따져 본 적이 없단 말이오, 나리!\"\n\n\"만약 그들이 해 봤다면—\" 클레넘이 말을 이어 가려는데, 팽크스 씨가 표정 하나 바꾸지 않고서, 평소 콧소리나 기관지에서 내던 그 어떤 노력도 까마득히 능가하는 소리를 내는 바람에, 클레넘은 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"만약 그들이 해 봤다면?\" 팽크스가 묻는 투로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"자네가—말을 하는 줄 알았네.\" 아서는 그 방해음을 무어라 불러야 할지 망설이며 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"아직은 아니오. 잠시 후엔 할지 모르지. 그들이 해 봤다면?\"\n\n\"그들이 해 봤다면,\" 친구를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 다소 갈피를 못 잡으며 클레넘이 말했다. \"글쎄, 더 잘 알게 됐겠지.\"\n\n\"어째서요, 클레넘 씨?\" 팽크스가 재빨리, 그리고 대화가 시작될 때부터 묵직한 장약을 잔뜩 장전하고 있다가 이제야 발사하는 듯한 묘한 효과를 풍기며 물었다. \"그 사람들이 옳단 말이오, 알겠소. 그럴 작정은 아니지만, 그들이 옳단 말이오.\"\n\n\"머들 씨에게 투기하려는 카발레토의 마음에 동조하는 게 옳다고?\"\n\n\"완—벽하게 옳소, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"내가 파고들어 봤소. 계산을 해 봤단 말이오. 따져 봤소. 안전하고 진짜요.\" 여기까지 말하고 나니 후련해진 팽크스 씨는, 동방산 파이프를 폐가 허락하는 한 길게 한 모금 빨았고, 들이쉬고 또 내쉬는 동안에도 슬기롭고 한결같은 눈으로 클레넘을 바라보았다.\n\n그 순간, 팽크스 씨는 자기가 잔뜩 짊어진 그 위험한 전염병을 내뿜기 시작했다. 이런 병이란 그렇게 옮는 것이며, 그렇게 교묘한 방식으로 퍼져 다니는 것이다.\n\n\"여보게 팽크스, 자네 말은,\" 클레넘이 힘주어 물었다. \"자네라면, 가령 예를 들어 그 천 파운드를 이런 종류의 이자에 내놓겠다는 건가?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"벌써 했소, 나리.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 다시 한번 길게 들이쉬고, 다시 한번 길게 내쉬고, 다시 한번 길게 슬기로운 눈으로 클레넘을 바라보았다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 내가 말하잖소, 내가 파고들어 봤다고.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그분은 막대한 자원을 가진 사람이오—엄청난 자본—정부의 영향력. 떠도는 사업 중에 최고요. 안전하오. 확실하오.\"\n\n\"흠!\" 클레넘이 먼저 그를 진지하게 보고는 다음으로 불을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다. \"자네가 날 놀라게 하는군!\"\n\n\"쳇!\" 팽크스가 받아쳤다. \"그런 말 마시오, 나리. 당신 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일이란 말이오! 왜 나처럼 하지 않는 거요?\"\n\n팽크스 씨가 이 만연한 병을 누구한테서 옮았는지는, 그가 무의식중에 열병에 걸렸다 한들 알 수 없었을 것과 마찬가지로 알 길이 없었다. 많은 신체적 질병이 그러하듯 처음에는 인간의 사악함 속에서 자라나, 그다음엔 그들의 무지 속에서 퍼져 나가는 이런 유행병은, 한동안 시간이 지나면 무지하지도 사악하지도 않은 수많은 사람에게까지 옮겨 간다. 팽크스 씨가 이 부류에 속한 자에게서 그 병을 옮았는지 아닌지는 모를 일이나, 그는 그런 부류로서 클레넘 앞에 나타났고, 그가 내뿜는 전염은 그만큼 더 독성이 강했다.\n\n\"그래서 자네는 정말로, 자네 그 천 파운드를 투자했다는 건가, 팽크스?\" 클레넘은 이미 그 단어로 넘어가 있었다.\n\n\"틀림없소, 나리!\" 팽크스가 담배 연기를 한 모금 내뿜으며 대담하게 대답했다. \"차라리 만 파운드였으면 싶소!\"\n\n자, 그날 밤 클레넘의 외로운 마음을 무겁게 누르는 화제가 둘 있었다. 하나는 그토록 오래 미뤄진 동업자의 희망이요, 다른 하나는 그가 어머니 댁에서 보고 들은 것이었다. 이렇게 길동무가 생기고, 그를 믿을 수 있다고 느끼는 안도감에서 그는 두 화제로 옮겨 갔으나, 둘 다 그를 다시금, 더 큰 힘으로 더 빠르게, 출발점으로 데려다 놓았다.\n\n그것은 더없이 단순하게 일어났다. 파이프 연기 너머로 불을 말없이 바라보던 막간 끝에, 그는 투자 이야기를 떠나, 자신이 어쩌다 어떤 까닭으로 그 거대한 국가 부서에 얽매여 있는지를 팽크스에게 들려주었다. \"힘든 일이었고, 도이스에게는 지금도 힘든 일일세.\" 그는 그 화제가 그의 안에 불러일으킨 온갖 정직한 감정을 담아, 이렇게 끝맺었다.\n\n\"정말 힘들겠소.\" 팽크스가 동의했다. \"허나 당신이 그분을 위해 일을 봐주는 거요, 클레넘 씨?\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말인가?\"\n\n\"사업의 돈 쪽을 관리한단 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇지. 내가 할 수 있는 한은.\"\n\n\"더 잘 관리하시오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그분의 노고와 실망을 보상해 드리시오. 이 시대의 기회를 잡아 드리란 말이오. 그분은 그런 식으로는 결코 제 잇속을 챙기지 못할 거요, 끈기 있고 한 가지에만 골몰하는 일꾼이니까. 그분은 당신만 바라보고 있소, 나리.\"\n\n\"나도 최선을 다하네, 팽크스.\" 클레넘이 거북하게 대답했다. \"다만 내가 겪어 본 적 없는 이런 새로운 사업들을 제대로 따져 보고 가늠하는 일이라면, 내가 거기 적합한지 의심스럽네. 나도 나이를 먹어 가고 있으니.\"\n\n\"나이를 먹어 간다고요?\" 팽크스가 외쳤다. \"하, 하!\"\n\n그 생각에 대한 팽크스 씨의 경악과 전적인 거부에서 우러난 그 놀라운 웃음, 그리고 일련의 콧방귀와 뿜어 대는 소리에는 의심할 여지 없이 진실한 무언가가 담겨 있어서, 그가 정말로 진지하다는 것은 의심할 수 없었다.\n\n\"나이를 먹어 간다고요?\" 팽크스가 외쳤다. \"들어 보소, 들어 보소, 들어 보소! 늙었다고? 저 양반 말 좀 들어 보소, 들어 보소!\"\n\n그 생각을 단 한 순간도 받아들이지 않겠다는, 이 외침들 못지않게 팽크스 씨의 계속되는 콧방귀에 담긴 단호한 거부는, 아서를 그 생각에서 멀리 떼어 놓았다. 사실 그는 팽크스 씨가 제 몸에서 끌어내듯 토해 내는 숨과, 제 몸으로 빨아들이듯 들이켜는 연기 사이에 벌어지는 격렬한 다툼 속에서 그에게 무슨 일이 생기지나 않을까 걱정스러웠다. 이렇게 두 번째 화제를 접게 되면서 그는 세 번째 화제로 떠밀렸다.\n\n\"젊었든, 늙었든, 중년이든, 팽크스.\" 알맞게 말이 끊긴 틈에 그가 말했다. \"나는 무척 불안하고 불확실한 상태에 있다네. 지금 내 것처럼 보이는 것 가운데 무엇이든 정말 내 것일까 의심하게까지 만드는 그런 상태일세. 이게 어찌 된 일인지 자네한테 말해도 될까? 자네를 크게 믿어도 될까?\"\n\n\"믿어도 되오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"내가 그럴 만하다고 여기신다면 말이오.\"\n\n\"여기네.\"\n\n\"믿으셔도 되오!\" 팽크스 씨의 짧고 날카로운 응수는, 석탄검댕이 묻은 손을 불쑥 내밀어 굳히니, 더없이 표정 풍부하고 설득력 있었다. 아서는 그 손을 따뜻하게 흔들었다.\n\n그러고서 그는, 자신의 오랜 우려를 알아들을 수 있게 하는 한도 내에서 최대한 그 성격을 누그러뜨리고, 어머니를 이름으로 언급하는 일은 결코 없이, 다만 자기 친척이라고 막연히 일컬으며, 자신이 품은 불길한 예감의 큰 윤곽과 자신이 목격한 면담을 팽크스 씨에게 털어놓았다. 팽크스 씨는 어찌나 흥미진진하게 들었던지, 동방산 파이프의 매력도 아랑곳없이 그것을 부삽 사이 화격자에 처박아 두고는, 이야기가 진행되는 내내 두 손으로 머리 위 온통 머리카락의 고리와 갈고리를 곤두세우는 데 매달려, 이야기가 끝났을 무렵엔 마치 아버지의 혼령과 대화를 나누는 풋내기 햄릿처럼 보였다.\n\n\"그게 날 도로 데려가는구려, 나리.\" 그제야 그가 클레넘의 무릎을 깜짝 놀라게 탁 치며 외쳤다. \"그게 날 도로 투자로 데려가는구려, 나리! 당신이 저지르지도 않은 잘못을 바로잡겠다고 스스로를 가난뱅이로 만드는 일에 대해선 아무 말 않겠소. 그게 당신이지. 사람은 제 자신이어야 하니까. 허나 이건 말하겠소, 당신이 제 핏줄을 폭로와 치욕에서 구하기 위해 돈이 필요할까 봐 하는 말이오만—할 수 있는 한 많이 버시오!\"\n\n아서는 고개를 가로저었으나, 또한 그를 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다.\n\n\"할 수 있는 한 부자가 되시오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 그 충고에 자신의 온 기력을 강력히 모아 그에게 간곡히 권했다. \"정직하게 할 수 있는 한 부자가 되란 말이오. 그게 당신의 의무요. 당신 자신을 위해서가 아니라 남들을 위해서. 시간의 앞머리 갈기를 붙잡으시오. 가엾은 도이스 씨는(이분이야말로 정말 나이를 먹어 가고 있소) 당신만 의지하고 있소. 당신 친척도 당신만 의지하고 있고. 당신은 얼마나 많은 것이 당신한테 달려 있는지 모른단 말이오.\"\n\n\"알았네, 알았어, 알았어!\" 아서가 대답했다. \"오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐네.\"\n\n\"한마디만 더 하겠소, 클레넘 씨.\" 팽크스가 받아쳤다. \"그러고 나면 오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐소. 어째서 그 모든 이득을 탐욕꾼, 악당, 사기꾼들한테 죄다 넘겨주려는 거요? 어째서 손에 넣을 수 있는 그 모든 이득을 내 집주인이나 그자 같은 부류한테 죄다 넘겨주려는 거요? 헌데 당신은 늘 그 짓을 하고 있단 말이오. 내가 당신이라고 할 때, 나는 당신 같은 사람들을 뜻하는 거요. 당신도 그런 줄 알잖소. 그야, 나는 그걸 내 삶의 매일같이 본단 말이오. 그것밖엔 안 보여요. 그걸 보는 게 내 직업이거든. 그러니 내 말은,\" 팽크스가 채근했다. \"뛰어들어서 이기란 말이오!\"\n\n\"허나 뛰어들어서 잃으면 어쩌나?\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 리 없소, 나리.\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"내가 들여다봤소. 이름은 사방에 자자하고—막대한 자원—엄청난 자본—대단한 지위—드높은 연줄—정부의 영향력. 그럴 리 없단 말이오!\"\n\n이 마무리 설파를 끝으로 팽크스 씨는 차츰 가라앉았다. 머리카락은 아무리 구슬려도 늘어질 수 있는 한껏 늘어지게 내버려 두었고, 화격자에서 파이프를 도로 거두어 새로 채워 끝까지 피웠다. 두 사람은 그 뒤로 별말이 없었으나, 같은 화제를 말없이 곱씹으며 서로에게 길동무가 되어 주었고, 자정이 되어서야 헤어졌다. 작별 인사를 하면서 팽크스 씨는 클레넘과 악수를 나눈 뒤, 문밖으로 증기를 뿜으며 나가기 전에 그를 완전히 한 바퀴 빙 돌았다. 아서는 이것을, 혹여 자기가 도움이 필요한 처지에 이르게 된다면—그날 밤 그들이 이야기한 어떤 일에서든, 아니면 자신에게 어떤 식으로든 영향을 미칠 수 있는 다른 어떤 일에서든—팽크스를 전적으로 믿어도 된다는 보증으로 받아들였다.\n\n이튿날 종일토록 틈틈이, 심지어 다른 일에 정신이 쏠려 있을 때조차, 그는 팽크스 씨가 자기 천 파운드를 투자한 일과 그가 그걸 '들여다봤다'고 한 일을 생각했다. 그는 팽크스 씨가 이 일에 그토록 낙관적이라는 것을, 그리고 그가 평소엔 낙관적인 성격이 아니라는 것을 생각했다. 그는 그 거대한 국가 부서를 생각했고, 도이스가 형편이 나아지는 것을 보면 자기가 얼마나 기쁠지를 생각했다. 그는 자신의 기억 속에서 집이라는 이름으로 통하는 그 음울하게 위협적인 곳을, 그리고 그곳을 옛날보다 한층 더 음울하게 위협적으로 만드는, 몰려드는 그림자들을 생각했다. 그는 어디를 가든 그 유명한 머들의 이름을 보거나 듣거나 만지게 된다는 것을 새삼 깨달았다. 어떤 경로로든 자신의 몸의 감각 중 하나에 그 이름이 들이밀어지지 않고서는 책상에 두어 시간 앉아 있기조차 어려웠다. 그는 그 이름이 어디에나 있다는 것도, 그리고 자기 말고는 아무도 그것을 미덥지 않게 여기지 않는 듯하다는 것도 이상하다고 생각하기 시작했다. 하기야 여기까지 생각이 미치자, 그는 자기 자신마저도 그것을 미덥지 않게 여기는 건 아니었음을 떠올리기 시작했다. 다만 그것에서 멀찍이 떨어져 있었을 뿐이었다.\n\n이런 종류의 병이 만연할 때 이러한 증상들은, 대개 병이 들기 시작했다는 징후인 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 14. Taking Advice\n\n누런 테베레 강가의 영국인들 사이에, 자기들의 영민한 동포인 스파클러 씨가 에두름국의 장관 중 한 사람이 되었다는 소식이 알려지자, 그들은 그것을 영국 신문에 실린 다른 어떤 소식보다 — 다른 어떤 사고나 비행보다 — 더 가까울 것 없는 한낱 소식거리로 받아들였다. 더러는 웃었고, 더러는 완벽한 변명이랍시고 그 자리가 사실상 명목뿐인 한직이라, 제 이름 철자나 쓸 줄 아는 바보면 누구든 그 자리에 충분하다고 말했으며, 더러는 — 이쪽이 더 엄숙한 정치 신탁가들이었는데 — 데시무스가 제 세력을 굳히는 것은 현명한 처사이며, 데시무스의 임명권 안에 든 모든 자리의 유일한 헌법적 목적은 데시무스가 제 세력을 굳히는 것이라고 말했다. 이 신조 조항에 동의하지 않으려는 까다로운 영국인도 몇몇 있었지만, 그들의 반대는 순전히 이론적인 것이었다. 실제적 관점에서 그들은 그 일을, 어딘가 혹은 아무 데도 아닌 곳에 있는, 이름 모를 다른 영국인들의 일이라 여기며 무심히 내버려 두었다. 같은 식으로, 본국에서도 수많은 영국인들이 무려 스물네 시간 연속으로, 그 보이지 않고 이름 없는 영국인들이 '이 문제를 떠맡아야 한다'고, 그리고 그들이 잠자코 묵인한다면 그런 꼴을 당해도 싸다고 주장했다. 그러나 그 태만한 영국인들이 어떤 부류로 이루어져 있는지, 그 불운한 자들이 어디에 숨었는지, 왜 숨었는지, 또 그토록 많은 다른 영국인들은 그들이 자기 이익을 돌보지 않는 까닭을 도무지 알 수 없어 하는 마당에, 어찌하여 그들은 늘 자기 이익을 등한히 하게 되는지는, 누런 테베레 강가에서도 검은 템스 강가에서도 사람들에게 밝혀지지 않았다.\n\n머들 부인은 이 소식에 대한 축하를 받으면서, 그것을 무심한 우아함으로 퍼뜨렸는데, 그 무심함이 보석을 돋보이게 하는 받침대처럼 소식을 더없이 돋보이게 했다. 그래요, 에드먼드가 그 자리를 맡았답니다, 하고 그녀는 말했다. 머들 씨가 그 애더러 맡으라 했고, 그래서 맡았지요. 에드먼드가 그 일을 좋아하면 좋겠지만, 정말 모르겠어요. 그러면 도시에 꽤 매여 있어야 할 텐데, 그 애는 시골을 더 좋아하거든요. 그래도 못마땅한 자리는 아니지요 — 어쨌든 자리니까요. 그 일이 머들 씨에게 하나의 영예라는 것은 부인할 수 없고, 에드먼드가 좋아한다면 그 애에게도 나쁘지 않은 일이지요. 그 애가 할 일이 생긴 것도 잘된 일이고, 그 일을 한 대가로 무언가를 받게 된 것도 잘된 일이고요. 그것이 에드먼드에게 군 생활보다 더 마음에 들지 어떨지는 두고 봐야겠지요.\n\n가슴은 그렇게 말했다 — 무언가를 대수롭지 않게 보이도록 만드는 기술에 능숙하면서, 실은 그 과정에서 그것을 한껏 추켜올리는 것이다. 한편, 데시무스가 내버린 헨리 가우언은 인민의 문에서 알바노 마을에 이르는 사이의 온갖 지인들을 두루 찾아다니며, 거의 (그러나 완전히는 아니게) 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 맹세하기를, 스파클러야말로 공유지에서 풀을 뜯어 본 그 어떤 멍청이보다도 성품이 가장 곱고 마음이 가장 순박하며 도무지 사랑스럽기 그지없는 얼간이라고 했다. 그리고 자기(가우언)를 그자(사랑스러운 얼간이)가 이 자리를 얻은 일보다 더 기쁘게 할 수 있었던 사정은 단 하나뿐인데, 그것은 자기(가우언)가 그 자리를 직접 얻는 것이었으리라고 했다. 그는 그 자리가 스파클러에게 안성맞춤이라고 했다. 할 일이 없으니 그는 그 일을 멋들어지게 해낼 것이고, 두둑한 봉급이 나오니 그는 그 봉급을 멋들어지게 받아 챙길 것이며, 그것은 유쾌하고 적절하고 더할 나위 없는 임명이라고 했다. 그래서 그는, 그토록 깊은 애정을 품은 그 사랑스러운 당나귀가 그토록 훌륭한 마구간에 들어앉은 것이 기뻐, 자기를 푸대접한 그 시혜자를 거의 용서할 지경이 되었다. 그의 자비심은 거기서 그치지 않았다. 그는 온갖 사교 자리마다 애써 스파클러 씨의 말문을 끌어내어 좌중 앞에서 그를 두드러지게 만들었는데, 그 사려 깊은 행동은 늘 그 젊은 신사가 스스로 음울하고 처량한 정신적 구경거리가 되는 결과로 이어졌지만, 그 우정 어린 의도만큼은 의심할 여지가 없었다.\n\n물론, 스파클러 씨가 애정을 바치는 대상이 그것을 의심하는 경우는 예외였지만 말이다. 패니 양은 이제, 세상에 그런 의미로 널리 알려져 있으면서도, 아무리 변덕스럽게 스파클러 씨를 부려먹어 봤자 그를 내쫓은 것은 아닌, 난처한 처지에 놓여 있었다. 그리하여 그녀는 그 신사와 충분히 한데 엮여 있는 셈이어서, 그가 평소보다 더 우스꽝스러워질 때면 자기 체면이 깎인다고 느꼈다. 또한 그녀는 결코 재치가 모자란 사람이 아니었던지라, 때때로 가우언에 맞서 그를 구해 주며 그에게 큰 도움을 베풀기도 했다. 그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 그가 부끄러웠고, 그를 떨쳐 버릴지 아니면 더 단호하게 부추길지 마음을 정하지 못했으며, 날마다 점점 더 자신의 불확실함 속으로 옭매여 든다는 불안에 마음이 산란했고, 머들 부인이 자기 곤경을 보며 의기양양해하리라는 불길한 예감에 시달렸다. 마음속에 이런 소용돌이가 있었으니, 어느 날 밤 패니 양이 머들 부인 댁의 음악회와 무도회에서 흥분한 상태로 집에 돌아와, 동생이 다정하게 달래려 하자 화장대 앞에 앉아 성난 채로 울려고 애쓰던 그 화장대에서 동생을 밀쳐 내고, 가슴을 들썩이며 모두가 다 밉고 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다고 선언한 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다.\n\n\"패니 언니, 무슨 일이야? 말해 봐.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이냐고, 이 작은 두더지야,\" 패니가 말했다. \"네가 눈먼 자들 중에서도 가장 눈먼 애가 아니라면, 나한테 물어볼 까닭도 없을 거야. 감히 머릿속에 눈이 박혀 있다고 우겨 대면서, 그러고도 무슨 일이냐고 나한테 묻다니!\"\n\n\"스파클러 씨 일이야, 언니?\"\n\n\"스파아클러 씨라니!\" 패니는 마치 그가 태양계에서 자기 마음에 가까이 다가올 수 있는 가장 동떨어진 주제라도 되는 양, 한없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다. \"아니야, 박쥐 아가씨, 그게 아니란다.\"\n\n곧이어 그녀는 동생에게 별명을 붙여 부른 것을 뉘우치며, 흐느끼는 소리로, 자기가 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만드는 줄 알지만 다들 자기를 그렇게 몰아간다고 했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 언니 몸이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아, 패니 언니.\"\n\n\"쓸데없는 소리!\" 젊은 아가씨가 다시 발끈하며 대꾸했다. \"나는 너만큼 멀쩡해. 어쩌면 더 멀쩡하다고 할 수도 있겠지만, 그렇다고 그걸 자랑할 생각은 없어.\"\n\n가엾은 리틀 도릿은 거부당하지 않을 만한 달래는 말을 무엇 하나 찾아낼 길이 보이지 않아, 잠자코 있는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다. 처음에는 패니가 이것마저 못마땅해했다. 거울을 향해 항변하기를, 한 여자아이가 가질 수 있는 온갖 성가신 언니 중에서도 가장 성가신 언니는 맹탕인 동생을 둔 언니일 거라고 했다. 자기가 때때로 형편없는 성미를 부리는 줄 안다고, 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만드는 줄 안다고, 그리고 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만들 때면 그렇다고 일러 주는 것만큼 자기에게 약이 되는 것이 없는데, 맹탕인 동생을 둔 탓에 도무지 그런 말을 듣지 못하고, 그 결과 자기가 못되게 굴도록 그야말로 부추김당하고 내몰린다고 했다. 게다가 (그녀는 성난 채로 거울에게 말했다) 자기는 용서받고 싶지도 않다고 했다. 동생보다 윗사람이면서 늘 굽실거리며 동생에게 용서받는 것은 옳은 본보기가 못 된다고 했다. 그리고 바로 이게 그 수법이라고 — 자기가 좋든 싫든 늘 용서받는 처지에 놓이게 된다는 것이었다. 마침내 그녀는 격렬하게 울음을 터뜨렸고, 동생이 다가와 곁에 바짝 앉아 위로하자 이렇게 말했다. \"에이미, 너는 천사야!\"\n\n\"하지만 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 우리 귀염둥이,\" 동생의 다정함에 마음이 가라앉자 패니가 말했다. \"이제 결론은 이거야. 일이 지금 흘러가는 대로 흘러가서는 안 되고 그래서도 안 된다는 것, 그리고 이런 상태에 이런 식으로든 저런 식으로든 끝장을 봐야 한다는 거지.\"\n\n그 선언이 매우 단호하기는 했어도 막연했으므로, 리틀 도릿은 이렇게 받았다. \"그 얘기를 해 보자.\"\n\n\"바로 그거야, 얘,\" 패니가 눈물을 닦으며 동의했다. \"그 얘기를 해 보자. 이제 다시 차분해졌으니, 네가 나한테 조언을 해 주렴. 나한테 조언해 주겠니, 우리 귀여운 아가?\"\n\n에이미조차 이 생각에는 미소를 지었지만, 이렇게 말했다. \"그럴게, 패니 언니, 내가 할 수 있는 만큼.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 사랑하는 에이미,\" 패니가 동생에게 입을 맞추며 대꾸했다. \"너는 내 닻이야.\"\n\n자기 닻을 무척 다정하게 끌어안은 다음, 패니는 탁자에서 향긋한 화장수 병을 집어 들고 하녀를 불러 고운 손수건을 가져오게 했다. 그러고는 그 시중꾼을 그날 밤 일에서 물러가게 하고, 이따금 눈과 이마를 톡톡 두드려 식히면서 조언을 받기 시작했다.\n\n\"얘,\" 패니가 입을 열었다. \"우리 둘의 성격이며 보는 눈은 (다시 한번 입 맞춰 주렴, 우리 예쁜이) 제법 다르단다. 그래서 내가 이제 하려는 말에 네가 놀랄 가능성이 아주 크지. 내가 하려는 말은 말이야, 우리가 재산은 있어도, 사교적으로 말하자면 불리한 처지에서 애를 쓰고 있다는 거야. 내 말이 잘 이해가 안 가지, 에이미?\"\n\n\"몇 마디만 더 들으면,\" 에이미가 부드럽게 말했다. \"분명 이해할 수 있을 거야.\"\n\n\"그래, 얘, 내 말은, 어쨌든 우리는 상류 생활에 새로 들어온 사람들이라는 거야.\"\n\n\"그래도 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 열렬한 감탄을 담아 끼어들었다. \"언니한테서 그걸 알아챌 사람은 아무도 없을 거야.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 얘, 아마 그럴지도 모르지,\" 패니가 말했다. \"비록 우리 귀한 아가야, 네가 그렇게 말해 주는 건 더없이 친절하고 더없이 다정한 일이지만 말이야.\" 여기서 그녀는 동생의 이마를 톡톡 두드리고 그 위에 입김을 살짝 불었다. \"하지만 너는,\" 패니가 말을 이었다. \"잘 알려진 대로, 세상에 둘도 없는 사랑스러운 아이란다! 다시 본론으로 돌아가자, 얘. 아빠는 더없이 신사답고 더없이 박식하시지만, 몇몇 사소한 점에서 같은 재산을 가진 다른 신사들과 조금 다르셔. 한편으로는 가엾게도 겪으신 일 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 내 생각에, 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 동안에도 저들이 그 일을 떠올리고 있으리라는 생각이 자꾸 머릿속에 맴돌기 때문이지. 삼촌은, 얘, 도무지 남 앞에 내세울 수가 없는 분이야. 내가 애틋하게 따르는 사랑스러운 분이긴 하지만, 사교적으로 말하면 차마 못 봐줄 지경이지. 에드워드는 끔찍하게 돈을 헤프게 쓰고 방탕해. 그렇다고 그 자체가 천박하다는 말은 아니야 — 천만의 말씀이지 — 하지만 내 말은, 그 애가 그걸 제대로 못 한다는 거고, 이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 자기한테 따라붙는 그 방탕한 평판에서 돈값을 못 뽑아낸다는 거야.\"\n\n\"가엾은 에드워드!\" 리틀 도릿이 그 한숨 속에 온 집안의 내력을 담아 한숨지었다.\n\n\"그래. 그리고 가엾은 너와 나도 마찬가지지,\" 패니가 다소 날카롭게 받았다. \"딱 맞는 말이야! 그리고 얘, 우리한테는 어머니가 없고, 대신 제너럴 부인이 있어. 다시 말하지만, 얘, 제너럴 부인은 흔한 속담을 뒤집어 그녀에게 맞춰 보자면, 쥐를 기어이 잡고 마는 장갑 낀 고양이야. 그 여자는, 나는 아주 확신하고 단언컨대, 우리 시어머니가 될 사람이야.\"\n\n\"그렇게는 생각하기 어려운데, 언니—\" 패니가 동생의 말을 막았다.\n\n\"이 일을 두고 나하고 따지지 마, 에이미,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 더 잘 아니까.\" 또 날카로워졌다는 것을 느끼고, 그녀는 동생의 이마를 다시 톡톡 두드리고 그 위에 다시 입김을 불었다. \"다시 한번 본론으로 돌아가자, 얘. 그러면 나한테 이런 문제가 생기는 거야 (나는 자존심이 세고 기개가 있지, 에이미, 너도 잘 알다시피. 아마 지나치게 그럴 테고) — 내가 마음을 다잡고 이 집안을 끌고 나갈 책임을 떠맡을지 말지 하는 문제 말이야.\"\n\n\"어떻게?\" 동생이 불안하게 물었다.\n\n\"나는,\" 패니가 그 물음에는 대답하지 않고 말했다. \"제너럴 부인한테 시어머니 노릇을 당하며 굴복하지 않을 거야. 그리고 머들 부인한테, 그 어떤 점에서든, 생색을 받거나 시달림을 받으며 굴복하지도 않을 거야.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 향긋한 화장수 병을 든 그 손 위에, 한층 더 불안한 눈빛으로 자기 손을 얹었다. 패니는 이제 자기 이마를 격렬하게 톡톡 두드려 그야말로 벌하다시피 하면서, 변덕스럽게 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그가 어떻게든, 그 방법이야 어떻든 상관없지만, 아주 좋은 지위에 올랐다는 건 아무도 부인 못 해. 그게 아주 좋은 혼처라는 것도 아무도 부인 못 하고. 그리고 영리하냐 아니냐의 문제로 말하자면, 영리한 남편이 나한테 어울릴지는 무척 의심스러워. 나는 굴복 못 하니까. 그한테 충분히 고분고분 따를 수가 없을 테니까.\"\n\n\"오, 패니 언니!\" 언니가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 알아채면서 일종의 공포가 스며들던 리틀 도릿이 항변했다. \"언니가 누군가를 사랑한다면, 이 모든 마음이 달라질 거야. 언니가 누군가를 사랑한다면, 더는 언니 자신이 아니라, 그에 대한 헌신 속에서 자신을 완전히 잃고 잊어버리게 될 거야. 언니가 그를 사랑한다면, 패니 언니—\" 패니는 톡톡 두드리던 손을 멈추고, 동생을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"오, 정말이지!\" 패니가 외쳤다. \"그래? 어머나, 어떤 주제에 대해선 어떤 사람들이 어찌 그리 많이 아는지! 다들 사람마다 잘 아는 주제가 하나씩 있다고들 하던데, 내가 정말로 네 주제를 콕 짚어낸 모양이로구나, 에이미. 자, 이 작은 것아, 농담이었어,\" 동생의 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"하지만 너는 어리석은 새침데기가 되지 말고, 이루어질 수도 없는 한심한 것들을 두고 들떠서 그럴듯하게 늘어놓지도 마. 자! 이제 다시 내 얘기로 돌아갈게.\"\n\n\"패니 언니, 먼저 이 말만 하게 해 줘. 언니가 부자가 되어 스파클러 씨와 결혼하는 걸 보느니, 차라리 우리가 다시 빠듯하게 입에 풀칠하며 일하는 게 나는 훨씬 더 좋아.\"\n\n\"하게 해 달라고, 얘?\" 패니가 받아쳤다. \"아니, 당연히, 너는 무슨 말이든 하게 해 줄게. 너한테 무슨 강제가 있는 건 아니길 바라. 우리는 함께 이 일을 의논하는 중이잖니. 그리고 스파클러 씨와 결혼하는 일로 말하자면, 나는 오늘 밤에 그럴 생각이 조금도 없어, 얘, 내일 아침에도 마찬가지고.\"\n\n\"그래도 언젠가는?\"\n\n\"지금 내가 아는 한, 언제고 절대 아니야,\" 패니가 무심하게 대답했다. 그러더니 갑자기 그 무심함을 타는 듯한 안절부절못함으로 바꾸며 덧붙였다. \"너는 영리한 남자 얘기를 하는구나, 이 작은 것아! 영리한 남자 얘기를 하는 건 다 좋고 쉽지. 그런데 그런 남자들이 어디 있어? 내 주변 어디에도 그런 남자는 안 보인다고!\"\n\n\"패니 언니, 이렇게 짧은 시간에는—\"\n\n\"짧은 시간이든 긴 시간이든,\" 패니가 말을 끊었다. \"나는 우리 처지가 못 견디게 갑갑해. 우리 처지가 마음에 안 들고, 그걸 바꾸는 데는 아주 사소한 계기만으로도 충분해. 다르게 자라고 처지가 완전히 다른 다른 여자아이들은 내가 하는 말이나 하려는 일에 의아해할지도 모르지. 그러라지. 그 애들은 제 삶과 성격에 떠밀려 가는 거고, 나는 내 것에 떠밀려 가는 거니까.\"\n\n\"패니, 패니 언니, 언니는 스파클러 씨보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람의 아내가 될 만한 자질이 있다는 걸 알잖아.\"\n\n\"에이미, 에이미야,\" 패니가 동생의 말을 흉내 내며 받아쳤다. \"나는 그 무례한 여자에게 더 큰 효과로 나 자신을 내세울 수 있는, 더 명확하고 확고한 지위를 갖고 싶다는 걸 안단다.\"\n\n\"그래서 — 이런 걸 물어 미안하지만, 패니 언니 — 그래서 그 여자의 아들과 결혼하겠다는 거야?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 어쩌면,\" 패니가 의기양양한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"그 목적에 이르는 데는 그보다 가망 없는 길이 얼마든지 있을 수 있어, 얘. 그 무례한 여자는 지금, 자기 아들을 나한테 떠넘기고 나를 한쪽으로 치워 버리면 큰 성공이라고 여길지도 모르지. 하지만, 어쩌면, 내가 그 아들과 결혼하면 그 여자한테 어떻게 앙갚음할지 그녀는 짐작도 못 할걸. 나는 모든 일에서 그 여자에게 맞서고, 그 여자와 겨룰 거야. 그걸 내 평생의 과업으로 삼을 거야.\"\n\n여기에 이르자 패니는 병을 내려놓고 방 안을 거닐었다. 말을 할 때면 늘 멈춰 서서 가만히 있었다.\n\n\"한 가지는 확실히 할 수 있어, 얘. 나는 그 여자를 더 늙어 보이게 만들 수 있어. 그리고 그렇게 할 거야!\"\n\n이 말에 이어 또 한 차례 거닐었다.\n\n\"나는 그 여자를 노파라고 떠들어 댈 거야. 그 여자 나이에 대해 다 아는 척할 거야 — 비록 몰라도, 그 아들한테서 알아낼 테니까. 그리고 에이미, 그 여자가 내 말을 듣게 만들 거야. 다정하게, 아주 효성스럽고 다정하게, 그 연세를 생각하면 참 정정해 보이신다고 말이야. 나는 내가 그렇게나 훨씬 젊으니까, 그 여자를 단번에 더 늙어 보이게 만들 수 있어. 내가 그 여자만큼 예쁘진 않을지도 모르지. 그 문제에 대해선 내가 공정한 판관은 못 되겠지만, 그 여자의 옆구리에 박힌 가시 노릇은 충분히 할 만큼 예쁘다는 건 알아. 그리고 그렇게 할 거야!\"\n\n\"언니, 이런 일 때문에 스스로를 불행한 삶에 옭아매겠다는 거야?\"\n\n\"불행한 삶은 아닐 거야, 에이미. 그건 내가 어울리는 삶일 거야. 타고난 기질 탓이든 처지 탓이든 그건 상관없어. 나는 그런 삶에 다른 거의 어떤 삶보다 더 잘 맞으니까.\"\n\n그 말에는 어딘가 황량한 어조가 깃들어 있었지만, 그녀는 짤막하게 거만한 웃음을 짓고 다시 한번 거닐다가, 커다란 거울 앞을 지나치고는 또 한 번 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"몸매! 몸매라, 에이미! 그래. 그 여자는 몸매가 좋지. 마땅한 건 인정해 주고 부인하지 않을게. 그렇지만 그게 다른 모든 사람을 멀찍이 뛰어넘어 도무지 따라잡을 수 없을 정도일까? 정말이지, 나는 그렇게 확신하지 않아. 그 여자가 결혼한 몸이라 누리는 만큼의 옷차림 여유를, 훨씬 젊은 여자한테 주어 봐. 그러면 그 문제는 두고 보자고, 얘!\"\n\n그 생각 속에 무언가 흐뭇하고 우쭐한 것이 있어, 그녀는 한결 명랑한 기분으로 자리로 돌아왔다. 그녀는 동생의 두 손을 자기 손에 쥐고, 동생의 얼굴을 들여다보며 웃으면서 네 손을 모두 머리 위로 마주쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 에이미, 그 여자가 까맣게 잊어버린 그 무희 말이야 — 나하고는 전혀 닮은 데가 없고, 내가 결코 그 여자한테 떠올려 주지 않는 그 무희 — 오, 천만에! — 그 무희가 그 여자의 삶을 가로지르며 춤을 추는 거야. 그 여자의 길 위에서, 그 거만한 평온을 조금쯤 흔들어 놓을 만한 곡조에 맞춰서. 딱 조금만, 에이미, 딱 조금만!\"\n\n에이미의 얼굴에서 진지하고 애원하는 듯한 눈빛을 마주하자, 그녀는 네 손을 내리고 그중 한 손만을 에이미의 입술 위에 얹었다.\n\n\"자, 이 일로 나하고 따지지 마, 얘,\" 그녀가 한층 엄한 투로 말했다. \"아무 소용 없으니까. 나는 이런 주제들을 너보다 훨씬 더 잘 알아. 아직 마음을 거의 정하진 못했지만, 어쩌면 그렇게 될지도 몰라. 이제 이 얘기를 편하게 나누었으니, 자러 가도 되겠어. 너야말로 더없이 착하고 더없이 사랑스러운 작은 생쥐야, 잘 자거라!\" 그 말과 함께 패니는 자기 닻을 끌어올렸고 — 그토록 많은 조언을 받았으니 — 그날만큼은 조언받기를 그만두었다.\n\n그때부터 에이미는, 스파클러 씨가 그를 사로잡은 여자에게 어떻게 다루어지는지를, 두 사람 사이에 오가는 모든 일에 새삼 무게를 둘 새로운 이유를 안고 지켜보았다. 패니가 그의 정신적 둔함을 도무지 견딜 수 없어 하는 듯한 때가 있었고, 그것에 어찌나 날카롭게 짜증을 내던지 하마터면 그를 영영 내쫓아 버릴 뻔한 때도 있었다. 또 그녀가 그와 훨씬 잘 지내는 때도 있었으니, 그가 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주고, 그녀의 우월감이 저울의 반대편 접시와 균형을 이루는 듯한 때였다. 만약 스파클러 씨가 더없이 충직하고 더없이 순종적인 연모자가 아니었더라면, 그는 그 시련의 무대에서 달아나, 적어도 로마에서 런던에 이르는 그 온 거리만큼이나 자기와 자기 마법사 사이를 벌려 놓을 만큼 호되게 시달렸을 것이다. 그러나 그는 증기선에 끌려가는 거룻배만큼도 제 의지가 없었으므로, 거친 데서나 잔잔한 데서나 한결같이 강한 강압에 떠밀려 자기의 잔혹한 여주인을 따라다녔다.\n\n머들 부인은 이 무렵 패니에게는 거의 말을 하지 않았으나, 그녀에 관한 말은 더 많이 했다. 그녀는, 말하자면, 외알 안경 너머로 패니를 바라볼 수밖에 없었고, 일반적인 대화에서는 그 거역할 수 없는 요구에 떠밀려 패니의 미모에 대한 찬사를 어쩔 수 없이 내뱉곤 했다. 패니가 이런 칭송을 들을 때면 (대개 듣게 마련이었는데) 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오르는 도전적인 기색은, 그 공평무사한 가슴에 양보하는 표정은 결코 아니었다. 그러나 그 가슴이 취하는 최대의 복수란, 들으라는 듯이 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"버릇없이 자란 미인이지요 — 하지만 저런 얼굴과 몸매라면, 누가 놀라겠어요?\"\n\n그 새로운 조언이 있던 밤으로부터 한 달이나 여섯 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 리틀 도릿은 스파클러 씨와 패니 사이에 무언가 새로운 양해가 생긴 것을 눈치챘다고 생각하기 시작했다. 스파클러 씨는 무슨 약조라도 지키는 듯, 먼저 패니 쪽을 바라보며 허락을 구하지 않고는 거의 입을 떼는 법이 없었다. 그 젊은 아가씨는 너무도 신중해 결코 마주 보아 답하는 법이 없었지만, 스파클러 씨가 말해도 좋다는 허락을 받으면 그녀는 잠자코 있었고, 허락받지 못하면 그녀 자신이 말했다. 게다가, 헨리 가우언이 그의 말문을 끌어내는 그 우정 어린 일을 해 보려 할 때마다, 그가 도무지 끌려 나오지 않으리라는 것이 분명해졌다. 그뿐 아니라, 패니는 곧, 세상에 콕 집어 누구를 겨냥한 것도 아니면서, 어쩌다 가시가 잔뜩 박힌 한마디를 던지곤 했는데, 그러면 가우언은 마치 벌집 속에 손을 넣었다가 뺀 사람처럼 움찔 물러서곤 했다.\n\n리틀 도릿이 자기 두려움을 굳히는 데 큰 몫을 한 사정이 또 하나 있었으니, 그 자체로 대단한 일은 아니었다. 스파클러 씨가 그녀를 대하는 태도가 달라진 것이다. 그것이 오누이처럼 되었다. 때때로 그녀가 모임의 바깥쪽 가장자리에 있을 때면 — 자기들 거처에서든, 머들 부인 댁에서든, 다른 어디에서든 — 어느새 스파클러 씨의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 슬그머니 감싸 받치고 있는 것을 알아채곤 했다. 스파클러 씨는 이 살가운 행동에 대해 조금도 설명하는 법이 없었고, 다만 어설프고 흡족하며 사람 좋은 소유주 같은 태도로 미소 지을 뿐이었는데, 그토록 둔한 신사에게서 나오니 그것이 불길하게도 의미심장했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 어느 날 집에서, 무거운 마음으로 패니를 생각하고 있었다. 그들의 응접실 한쪽 끝에는, 거의 전체가 불규칙한 퇴창으로 이루어져 거리 위로 돌출해 있고, 코르소 거리의 그 그림 같은 활기와 다채로움을 위아래로 죄다 굽어볼 수 있는 방이 하나 있었다. 영국 시각으로 오후 서너 시쯤이면, 이 창에서 내다보이는 풍경은 무척 환하고 독특했다. 그래서 리틀 도릿은 베네치아에서 발코니에 앉아 시간을 흘려보내곤 하던 것과 흡사하게, 여기 앉아 곧잘 생각에 잠기곤 했다. 어느 날 이렇게 앉아 있는데, 누군가 부드럽게 그녀의 어깨를 건드렸고, 패니가 \"에이미, 잘 있었니,\" 하고는 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 그들이 앉은 자리는 그 창의 일부였다. 무슨 행렬 같은 것이 지나갈 때면, 그들은 창밖으로 화사한 휘장을 늘어뜨리고, 이 자리에 무릎을 꿇거나 앉아서, 그 찬란한 빛깔에 기대어 행렬을 내다보곤 했다. 그러나 그날은 행렬이 없었고, 리틀 도릿은 평소 그 시각이면 으레 말을 타고 나가 있던 패니가 그 시간에 집에 있는 것이 다소 의아했다.\n\n\"그래, 에이미,\" 패니가 말했다. \"무슨 생각을 하고 있었니, 꼬마야?\"\n\n\"언니 생각을 하고 있었어, 패니.\"\n\n\"설마? 이런 우연이 다 있나! 어머, 여기 또 다른 누가 왔네. 너 이 다른 누군가 생각도 하고 있었던 건 아니지, 그렇지, 에이미?\"\n\n에이미는 사실 이 다른 누군가에 대해서도 생각하고 있었다. 그것은 바로 스파클러 씨였으니까. 그러나 그녀는 그렇게 말하지 않고 그에게 손을 내밀었다. 스파클러 씨는 다가와 그녀의 반대편에 앉았고, 그녀는 그 오누이 같은 난간이 자기 등 뒤로 둘러지더니, 패니까지 아우르려는 듯 죽 뻗는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그래, 내 어린 동생,\" 패니가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인지 짐작하겠지?\"\n\n\"이분은 사랑받는 만큼이나 아름다우시고,\" 스파클러 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"게다가 도무지 허튼 데가 없는 분이지요 — 이제 정해졌습니다—\"\n\n\"굳이 설명할 것 없어요, 에드먼드,\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"한마디로, 얘,\" 패니가 말을 이었다. \"결국, 우리는 약혼했단다. 기회를 봐서 오늘 밤이나 내일, 아빠한테 이 얘기를 해야 해. 그러면 끝난 거고, 더 할 말도 거의 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 스파클러 씨가 공손하게 말했다. \"에이미한테 한마디 하고 싶은데요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요! 제발 말해 봐요,\" 젊은 아가씨가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"나는 확신합니다, 에이미,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"당신의 더없이 자질이 빼어나고 아름다운 언니 다음으로, 만약 세상에 도무지 허튼 데가 없는 아가씨가 있었다면 그건—\"\n\n\"그건 우리도 다 알아요, 에드먼드,\" 패니 양이 끼어들었다. \"그 얘긴 됐어요. 제발 우리한테 허튼 데가 없다는 것 말고 다른 얘기로 넘어가요.\"\n\n\"네, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 단언하건대, 에이미, 내게 이보다 더 큰 행복은 없을 겁니다, 내게는요 — 티끌만큼도 허튼 데가 없는, 그 빛나는 아가씨의 선택을 받는 더없는 영광의 행복 다음으로 말입니다—\"\n\n\"제발요, 에드먼드, 제발!\" 패니가 그 예쁜 발로 마룻바닥을 가볍게 톡 치며 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"내 사랑, 당신 말이 옳소,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"내게 그런 버릇이 있다는 거 압니다. 내가 하고 싶었던 말은, 더없이 빼어나게 빛나는 아가씨와 맺어지는 행복 다음으로, 내게 이보다 더 큰 행복은 없으리라는 것 — 내게는요 — 곧 에이미와 다정한 교분을 쌓아 가는 행복 말입니다. 다른 몇몇 주제에 대해선 갑작스레 닥치면 내가 수준에 못 미칠지도 모르고,\" 스파클러 씨가 사내답게 말했다. \"또 사교계에 여론을 물으면 다들 내가 못 미친다고 할 거라는 것도 압니다. 하지만 에이미라는 주제에 대해선 나도 수준에 충분히 미칩니다!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 그 증거로 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"나이프와 포크 한 벌, 그리고 방 한 칸은,\" 스파클러 씨가, 자기의 지난 연설 솜씨에 비하면 제법 술술 풀려 나오며 말을 이었다. \"언제든 에이미가 마음대로 쓸 수 있도록 늘 마련될 겁니다. 우리 영감님께서도, 분명, 내가 그토록 존경하는 사람을 손님으로 맞는 걸 늘 자랑스러워하실 겁니다. 그리고 우리 어머니로 말하자면,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 빼어난 여성이신데, 게다가—\"\n\n\"에드먼드, 에드먼드!\" 패니 양이 또다시 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 말만은 봐주오, 내 영혼,\" 스파클러 씨가 사정했다. \"내게 그런 버릇이 있다는 거 알고, 그걸 바로잡아 주려 애써 주는 당신께, 나의 사랑스러운 아가씨, 진심으로 고맙소. 하지만 우리 어머니는 누구나 인정하다시피 참으로 빼어난 여성이신데, 정말이지 조금도 허튼—\"\n\n\"그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠죠,\" 패니가 받았다. \"하지만 제발 그 얘긴 더 이상 꺼내지 말아요.\"\n\n\"안 하겠소, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러면, 사실상, 더 할 말은 없는 거죠, 에드먼드, 그렇죠?\" 패니가 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 없고말고요, 나의 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"오히려 이렇게 말을 많이 한 걸 사과드립니다.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 일종의 영감으로, 그 물음이 이제 그만 가는 게 낫지 않겠느냐는 뜻을 담고 있음을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 오누이 같은 난간을 거두고는, 송구하지만 이만 물러가는 게 좋겠다고 정중히 말했다. 그는 에이미에게서, 그녀가 들뜨고 괴로운 심정 속에서나마 그 임무를 다해 할 수 있는 만큼의 축하를 받고서야 떠났다.\n\n그가 가고 나자, 그녀는 \"오, 패니, 패니!\" 하고 환한 창가에 있는 언니에게로 돌아서더니, 그 가슴에 안겨 거기서 울었다. 패니는 처음엔 웃었지만, 곧 자기 얼굴을 동생의 얼굴에 맞대고 그녀도 울었다 — 조금. 패니가 이 일에 관하여 자기 안에 숨겨졌거나 억눌렸거나 정복당한 어떤 감정이 있음을 내비친 것은 그때가 마지막이었다. 그 시각부터, 그녀가 택한 길이 그녀 앞에 놓였고, 그녀는 그 길을 제 거만하고 고집스러운 걸음으로 밟아 나갔다.\n\n장녀로부터, 자신이 스파클러 씨의 청혼을 받아들였으며 그에게 백년가약을 맺었노라는 통보를 받자, 도릿 씨는 그 소식을 일변 크나큰 위엄으로, 일변 부성적 자긍심을 한껏 펼쳐 보이며 받아들였다. 유리한 처지에서 교제를 넓혀 갈 전망이 한층 넓어지자 그의 위엄은 부풀어 올랐고, 그의 부성적 자긍심은 자기 생애의 그 위대한 목적에 패니 양이 선뜻 공명해 준 덕에 발현되었다. 그는 그녀의 고결한 야심이 자기 가슴속에서 조화로운 메아리를 찾았음을 알려 주었으며, 의무감과 올바른 신조로 가득 차서 집안의 명성을 드높이는 데 스스로를 바친 자식에게 하듯 그녀에게 축복을 내렸다.\n\n패니 양이 그가 모습을 드러내도록 허락했을 때, 도릿 씨는 스파클러 씨에게 이렇게 말했다. 스파클러 씨가 영광스럽게도 제의해 온 이 결연이 자기 심정에 더없이 흡족하다는 사실을 굳이 감추지 않겠노라고. 그것은 딸 패니의 자발적인 애정과 부합하는 동시에, 당대의 거두인 머들 씨와 흐뭇한 성격의 인척 관계를 열어 주기 때문이라고. 그는 또한 머들 부인에 대해서도, 품격과 우아함과 기품과 미모가 넘치는 일류 부인이라며 무척 칭송하는 어조로 언급했다. 그는 자신의 의무로서 한마디 일러둘 일이 있다고 했다(스파클러 씨처럼 고매한 분별을 갖춘 신사라면 그 말을 더없이 섬세하게 헤아려 주리라 믿는다고 했다). 곧, 머들 씨와 몇 차례 서신을 주고받는 특권을 누리고, 그 저명한 신사의 견해와도 어느 정도 부합하는지를 확인하기 전까지는, 이 제의가 확정되었다고 볼 수 없다는 것이었다. 다시 말해, 자기 딸이 마땅히 누려야 할 지위 — 그녀의 인생에서의 신분과 그녀의 지참금과 장래의 기대가 보장하는, 그리고 그가 딸에게 요구할 권리가 있다고 믿는 그 지위 — 곧, 돈에 집착하는 것처럼 비치지 않으면서 부른다면, 이른바 대(大)사교계의 눈길 속에서 그녀가 유지해야 할 그 발판 위에서 받아들여질 것인지를 확인하기 전까지는 그렇다는 말이었다. 어느 정도 지위 있는 신사로서의 입장과 아버지로서의 입장이 똑같이 요구하는 이 말을 하면서도, 그는 외교적으로 굴어 이 제의가 희망적인 보류 상태에서 조건부로 수락되었음을 감추지는 않겠노라고 했고, 또 자신과 자기 집안에 베풀어진 영예에 대해 스파클러 씨에게 감사한다고 했다. 그는 — 하 — 독립적 신사의 성품과, — 흠 — 아마도 지나치게 편애하고 찬탄하기 쉬운 부모의 성품에 관해 몇 마디 더 일반적인 소회를 덧붙이며 말을 맺었다. 요컨대 짧게 간추리자면, 그는 스파클러 씨의 청혼을, 지난 시절에 그에게서 반 크라운짜리 동전 서너 닢을 받았을 때와 거의 다름없는 태도로 받아들였다.\n\n이렇게 자신의 무해한 머리 위로 쏟아 부어진 말들에 어리벙벙해진 스파클러 씨는, 짧지만 적절한 응수를 했다. 그것은 다름 아니라, 그는 패니 양에게 시건방진 구석이 없다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있었으며, 자기 부친께서도 다 흔쾌히 여기시리라 의심치 않는다는 말이었다. 그 대목에서 그의 애정의 대상은 용수철 뚜껑 달린 상자처럼 그를 탁 닫아 버리고는 돌려보냈다.\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 도릿 씨는 그 '가슴'에게 경의를 표하러 갔고, 그 '가슴'은 그를 크나큰 호의로 맞이했다. 머들 부인은 에드먼드에게서 이 일에 관해 들은 터였다. 처음에는 놀랐다고 했는데, 에드먼드가 결혼할 사람이라고는 생각지 않았기 때문이라 했다. 사교계도 에드먼드를 결혼할 사람이라고 여기지 않았다고. 그렇지만 물론 자기는 여자로서 진작에 알아챘다고 했다(우리 여자들은 본능적으로 그런 것들을 알아보는 법이지요, 도릿 씨!). 에드먼드가 도릿 양에게 홀딱 사로잡혀 있었다는 것을 말이다. 그래서 자기는, 그토록 매력적인 처녀를 외국에 데려와 자기 나라 사내들의 넋을 빼놓다니 도릿 씨께서 책임지실 일이 많다고 대놓고 말한 적이 있다고 했다.\n\n\"부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그렇다면 스파클러 씨의 애정이 향한 그 방향을 — 하 — 부인께서 승인하시는 것으로 결론 내려도 영광이겠습니까?\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리지만, 도릿 씨,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"저 개인적으로는 매료되었답니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨에게는 그 말이 무척 흐뭇했다.\n\n\"개인적으로는,\" 머들 부인이 거듭 말했다. \"매료되었지요.\"\n\n이렇게 무심코 되풀이된 '개인적으로'라는 말에 도릿 씨는, 머들 씨의 승인 또한 빠지지 않기를 바란다는 뜻을 표하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"제가,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"머들 씨를 대신해 단정적으로 답해 드릴 수는 없습니다. 신사분들이란, 특히 사교계에서 자본가라 부르는 신사분들이란, 이런 문제에 대해 나름의 생각을 갖고 계시니까요. 하지만 제 생각으로는 — 어디까지나 한낱 의견을 말씀드리는 것입니다만, 도릿 씨 — 제 생각으로는 머들 씨도 대체로,\" 여기서 그녀는 잠시 자기 자신을 점검하고는 느긋하게 덧붙였다. \"꽤나 매료되시리라 봅니다.\"\n\n사교계에서 자본가라 부르는 신사분들이라는 말이 나오자, 도릿 씨는 마치 속에서 무슨 이의가 터져 나오기라도 하는 듯 헛기침을 했다. 머들 부인은 그것을 알아채고는, 그 신호를 이어받아 말을 계속했다.\n\n\"하기야, 도릿 씨, 굳이 그런 말씀을 드릴 필요는 없겠지요. 다만 제가 그토록 높이 우러르는 분께, 더욱 흐뭇한 관계로 맺어지는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바라는 그런 분께, 마음속에 떠오르는 바를 솔직히 털어놓는다는 의미에서 드리는 말씀입니다. 어찌하든, 선생님께서 이런 일을 머들 씨 자신의 관점에서 헤아리실 공산이 크다는 것은 누구나 알 수 있는 노릇이니까요. 다만 여러 사정이 머들 씨로 하여금 우연히도 — 그게 행운인지 불운인지 — 사업 거래에 종사하게 만들었고, 그 거래가 아무리 방대하다 한들 그분의 시야를 다소 옥죌 수도 있다는 점만은 다르겠지요. 저는 사업에 관한 한 갓난아이나 다름없습니다만,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"도릿 씨, 사업이란 게 그런 경향을 띠지나 않을까 염려스럽습니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨와 머들 부인이 이렇게 능숙하게 시소를 타듯, 서로를 한 번씩 띄워 올리고 한 번씩 내려놓으며, 어느 쪽도 우위를 점하지 않는 그 솜씨는 도릿 씨의 기침에 진정제처럼 작용했다. 그는 더없이 정중하게, 머들 씨의 사업 같은 사업은 — 나머지 인간들의 하찮은 사업과는 동떨어진 것으로서 — 그것을 잉태한 천재를 더 넓히고 확장하는 것 말고는 어떤 저급한 경향도 가질 수 없다고, 박학하고 우아한 머들 부인조차도(그녀는 이 찬사에 몸을 숙였다) 그렇게 여기는 일에는 부디 반대하게 해 달라고 항변했다. \"선생님은 관대함 그 자체시군요,\" 머들 부인이 더없이 환한 미소를 지으며 응답했다. \"그러기를 바라야지요. 하지만 고백하건대 저는 사업에 관해서는 거의 미신적일 만큼 생각이 어둡답니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 여기서 또 한마디 찬사를 던져 넣었으니, 사업이란, 그 안에서 귀중한 시간과 마찬가지로, 노예들을 위해 생겨난 것이며, 더없는 흥에 따라 만인의 마음을 다스리는 머들 부인 같은 분이 그런 것과 얽힐 까닭이 없다는 취지였다. 머들 부인은 웃었고, 도릿 씨에게 그 '가슴'이 발그레해진 듯한 인상을 전했다 — 이는 그녀의 가장 빼어난 효과 중 하나였다.\n\n\"제가 이렇게 길게 말씀드리는 것은,\" 그녀가 이어서 설명했다. \"다만 머들 씨가 늘 에드먼드에게 지대한 관심을 보여 오셨고, 늘 그 아이의 앞길을 열어 주고 싶다는 강한 바람을 표하셨기 때문입니다. 에드먼드의 공적인 처지는 선생님께서도 아시리라 봅니다. 그 아이의 사적인 처지는 오로지 머들 씨께 달려 있고요. 사업에 어두운 제 어리석은 무능 탓에, 정말이지 저는 그 이상은 아무것도 모릅니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 다시금, 사업이란 사람을 사로잡는 이들과 마법을 거는 여인들의 안목 밑에 놓인 것이라는 취지의 말을 자기 식으로 표했다. 그러고는 신사로서, 또 아버지로서 머들 씨에게 편지를 쓰겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 머들 부인은 진심을 다해 — 아니, 그녀의 온갖 기교를 다해, 그것은 그녀에게는 똑같은 말이었다 — 그 뜻에 찬동했고, 다음 우편으로 세계 제8대 불가사의에게 미리 알리는 편지를 손수 띄웠다.\n\n이 일에 관련된 큰 문제를 두고 나눈 대화나 담론에서와 마찬가지로, 도릿 씨는 서신에서도 그 주제를 갖은 장식으로 둘러쌌다. 마치 습자 선생들이 습자책과 셈본을 꾸미는 것과 같았으니, 거기서는 산술 기초 규칙의 표제들이 백조, 독수리, 그리핀, 그 밖의 갖가지 서법적 유희로 갈라져 나가고, 대문자들이 정신과 육체를 잃고서 펜과 잉크의 황홀경으로 빠져드는 것이었다. 그렇지만 그는 편지의 요지만은 충분히 분명하게 전달했으니, 머들 씨가 그 출처에서 그 내용을 알게 되었노라고 그럴듯하게 시치미 떼기에 부족함이 없었다. 머들 씨도 그에 맞춰 답장을 보냈다. 도릿 씨가 머들 씨에게 답하고, 머들 씨가 도릿 씨에게 답하고, 도릿 씨가 다시 머들 씨에게 답했다. 그리고 머지않아 서신을 주고받던 두 세력이 만족스러운 합의에 이르렀다는 발표가 나왔다.\n\n이제야, 그 전에는 결코 아니었으나, 패니 양이 자신의 새 배역을 위해 완벽하게 차려입고 무대 위로 활짝 등장했다. 이제야, 그 전에는 결코 아니었으나, 그녀는 스파클러 씨를 자신의 광채 속에 온통 흡수해 버렸고, 두 사람 몫으로, 아니 그 이상의 스무 사람 몫으로 빛났다. 그토록 그녀를 괴롭혀 온, 정해진 자리와 정체성의 결여를 더는 느끼지 않게 되자, 이 아름다운 배는 정해진 항로를 따라 꾸준히 키를 잡고 나아가기 시작했고, 자신의 항해 능력을 발휘시키는 무게와 균형으로 헤엄쳐 나가기 시작했다.\n\n\"예비 절차가 그토록 만족스럽게 매듭지어졌으니, 이제 나는, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인에게 — 하 — 정식으로 알려야겠다고 — \"\n\n\"아빠,\" 패니가 그 이름이 나오자 말을 뚝 끊으며 대꾸했다. \"제너럴 부인이 이 일과 무슨 상관인지 저는 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그것은 — 흠 — 교양 있고 세련된 부인에 대한 예의의 행동이 될 거다 — \"\n\n\"오! 저는 제너럴 부인의 교양이니 세련이니 하는 것에 신물이 났어요, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이라면 진력이 나요.\"\n\n\"진력이 난다고,\" 도릿 씨가 나무라듯 놀라며 되뇌었다. \"— 하 — 제너럴 부인이 말이냐.\"\n\n\"정말이지 그분이라면 넌더리가 나요, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 제 결혼과 무슨 상관인지 정말 모르겠어요. 그분은 그분 자신의 혼사 계획에나 매달리라죠 — 그런 게 있다면 말이에요.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했는데, 그 위에는 딸의 경박함과 뚜렷이 대비되는 진중하고 묵직한 느릿함이 깔려 있었다. \"부디 — 하 — 네 말이 무슨 뜻인지를 설명해 주는 호의를 베풀어 다오.\"\n\n\"제 말은요, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 혹시 자기 자신의 혼사 계획을 갖고 있다면, 그것만으로도 그분의 여가 시간을 채우기에 충분하고도 남으리라는 거예요. 그리고 그런 게 없다면, 차라리 더 잘된 일이고요. 하지만 어쨌든 저는 그분께 무슨 발표를 하는 영광 따위는 누리고 싶지 않아요.\"\n\n\"패니, 한 가지 물어보자,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"어째서 그러느냐?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 그분은 제 약혼을 혼자 알아낼 수 있을 테니까요, 아빠,\" 패니가 쏘아붙였다. \"그분은 충분히 주의 깊으시잖아요, 정말이지. 제가 그런 걸 본 것 같아요. 혼자 알아내라죠. 만약 혼자 알아내지 못한다 해도, 제가 결혼하면 알게 될 테고요. 그리고 아빠, 제너럴 부인께는 그 정도면 아주 충분하다는 생각이 든다고 제가 말씀드린다고 해서, 제가 아빠에 대한 애정이 부족하다고 여기지는 말아 주세요.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 놀랍구나, 나는 불쾌하다, 이런 — 흠 — 이런 변덕스럽고 영문 모를, — 하 — 제너럴 부인에 대한 적의의 표출에 말이다.\"\n\n\"제발, 아빠,\" 패니가 다그쳤다. \"그걸 적의라고 부르지는 말아 주세요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 제너럴 부인이 제 적의를 살 만한 가치가 있다고는 생각지 않으니까요.\"\n\n이 말에 도릿 씨는 의자에서 일어나, 준엄한 질책의 표정을 고정한 채로 딸 앞에 위엄 있게 우뚝 섰다. 딸은 팔에 찬 팔찌를 돌리며, 아버지를 쳐다보았다가 또 그에게서 시선을 돌렸다가 하면서 말했다. \"좋아요, 아빠. 아빠가 마음에 안 들어 하신다니 정말 죄송해요. 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요. 저는 어린애가 아니고, 에이미도 아니니까, 말을 해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 위엄 있는 침묵 끝에 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"내가 너에게, 여기 남아 있어 달라고 청하면서, 그동안 제너럴 부인께 — 흠 — 이 집안에서 신임받는 일원인 그 모범적인 부인께, — 하 — 우리 사이에 고려되고 있는 변화를 정식으로 알린다면, 만약 내가 — 하 — 그것을 청할 뿐 아니라 — 흠 — 그것을 고집한다면 — \"\n\n\"오, 아빠,\" 패니가 날카로운 의미를 담아 말을 끊었다. \"그 일을 그렇게까지 대단하게 여기신다면, 저는 도리상 따르는 것 말고는 할 일이 없겠지요. 다만 이 일에 대한 제 생각만은 간직하게 해 주세요. 이런 사정에서야 정말 어쩔 수가 없으니까요.\" 그리하여 패니는, 극과 극이 맞닿은 지점에서 도리어 반항이 되어 버린 그런 순종의 태도로 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 그녀의 아버지는, 대답할 가치를 두지 않았거나 무어라 대답해야 할지를 몰랐거나, 어느 쪽이든 간에, 팅클러 씨를 불러들였다.\n\n\"제너럴 부인을.\"\n\n그 고운 광택칠하는 여인과 관련해 그토록 짧은 명령을 받는 데 익숙하지 않은 팅클러 씨는 멈칫했다. 도릿 씨는 그 멈칫함 속에서 마셜시 감옥 전체와 그곳의 온갖 증표를 보고는, 즉시 그에게 달려들 듯 호통쳤다. \"어찌 감히 그러느냐, 이놈? 무슨 뜻이냐?\"\n\n\"용서하십시오, 나리,\" 팅클러 씨가 변명했다. \"저는 알고 싶었을 뿐 — \"\n\n\"너는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않았다, 이놈,\" 도릿 씨가 시뻘게진 얼굴로 외쳤다. \"알고 싶었다고 내게 말하지 마라. 하. 너는 그렇지 않았어. 너는 조롱의 죄를 지었다, 이놈.\"\n\n\"분명히 말씀드리지만, 나리 — \" 팅클러 씨가 입을 떼었다.\n\n\"내게 분명히 말하지 마라!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"나는 하인에게서 보증 따위 받지 않겠다. 너는 조롱의 죄를 지었어. 너는 내 곁을 떠나야 한다 — 흠 — 이 집안 식솔 전부가 나를 떠나야 한다. 무얼 기다리고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"그저 분부만 기다리고 있습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"거짓말이다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"너는 이미 분부를 받았다. 하 — 흠. 제너럴 부인께 안부를 전하고, 괜찮으시다면 잠시 내게 와 주시는 호의를 청한다고 여쭈어라. 그것이 네가 받은 분부다.\"\n\n이 심부름을 수행하면서 팅클러 씨는 어쩌면 도릿 씨가 노발대발하고 있다는 사정을 전했는지도 모른다. 어찌 되었든, 곧이어 제너럴 부인의 치맛자락이 바깥에서 다가오는 소리가 — 거의 통통 튀어 오며 다가온다고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 평소답지 않게 서둘러 들려왔다. 그렇긴 해도, 그 치맛자락은 문간에서 진정되어, 여느 때의 침착함으로 방 안을 쓸 듯이 들어왔다.\n\n\"제너럴 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"의자에 앉으시지요.\"\n\n제너럴 부인은 우아한 곡선을 그리는 답례로, 도릿 씨가 권한 의자에 내려앉았다.\n\n\"부인,\" 그 신사가 말을 이었다. \"부인께서 친절하게도 제 딸들의 — 흠 — 도야를 맡아 주셨고, 또 그 아이들과 가까이 관련된 일이라면 무엇이든 — 하 — 부인께 무관한 일일 수 없으리라고 제가 확신하는 까닭에 — \"\n\n\"전혀 그럴 수 없지요,\" 제너럴 부인이 더없이 차분한 태도로 말했다.\n\n\" — 그러므로 부인께 알려 드리고자 합니다. 지금 여기 있는 제 딸이 — \"\n\n제너럴 부인은 패니에게 머리를 살짝 숙여 보였고, 패니는 제너럴 부인에게 머리를 아주 낮게 숙였다가 다시 거만하게 꼿꼿이 세웠다.\n\n\" — 제 딸 패니가, — 하 — 부인께서도 아시는 스파클러 씨와 혼약을 맺게 되었습니다. 그러니, 부인, 부인께서는 힘겨운 책무의 절반을 — 하 — 힘겨운 책무를 덜게 되시는 것입니다.\" 도릿 씨는 성난 눈으로 패니를 보며 그 말을 되풀이했다. \"하지만 부인께서 지금 친절하게도 우리 집안에서 차지하고 계신 그 발판의, 직접적이든 간접적이든 다른 어떤 부분도, — 흠 — 줄어드는 일이 없기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"도릿 씨께서는,\" 제너럴 부인이 장갑 낀 두 손을 모범적인 평온함으로 포개어 놓은 채 대답했다. \"언제나 사려 깊으시고, 또 언제나 제 친선의 봉사를 너무도 높이 평가해 주시는군요.\"\n\n(패니 양은, '맞는 말이군요'라고 하기라도 하듯 헛기침을 했다.)\n\n\"도릿 양은 분명 사정이 허락하는 한 가장 건전한 분별력을 발휘하셨으리라 봅니다. 그리고 부디 제가 도릿 양께 진심 어린 축하를 드리도록 허락해 주시기를 바랍니다. 정념의 굴레에서 벗어나 이루어질 때,\" 제너럴 부인은 그 단어를 입에 담으면서 — 그 말을 입에 올리고도 누군가의 얼굴을 마주 볼 수는 없다는 듯 — 눈을 감았다. \"가까운 친척의 승인을 얻어 이루어질 때, 그리고 한 가문이라는 당당한 건축물의 구조를 굳건히 다질 때, 이런 일들은 대개 상서로운 사건이지요. 부디 도릿 양께 제 진심 어린 축하를 드리도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n여기서 제너럴 부인은 멈추고는, 표정을 가다듬기 위해 속으로 이렇게 덧붙였다. '파파, 포테이토, 포울트리, 프룬, 프리즘.'\n\n\"도릿 씨께서는,\" 그녀가 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"언제나 더없이 친절하시지요. 그리고 이 신뢰를 이토록 이른 시기에 선생님과 도릿 양께서 손수 제게 전해 주신 그 배려와, 감히 덧붙이자면 영예에 대하여, 저는 감사의 헌사를 올리고자 합니다. 제 감사와 제 축하는, 도릿 씨와 도릿 양 두 분께 똑같이 마땅한 보답입니다.\"\n\n\"제게는,\" 패니 양이 한마디 했다. \"그 말씀이 더없이 흐뭇하군요 — 이루 말할 수 없이요. 제너럴 부인, 부인께서 아무런 이의도 없으시다는 걸 알게 되니 정말이지 마음의 짐이 한결 덜어지는걸요. 만약 부인께서 무슨 이의라도 제기하셨더라면,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제가 어찌해야 했을지 도무지 모르겠어요, 제너럴 부인.\"\n\n제너럴 부인은 오른쪽 장갑이 위로, 왼쪽 장갑이 아래로 가도록, 프룬과 프리즘의 미소를 띤 채 두 손을 바꾸어 포갰다.\n\n\"부인의 승인을 지켜 가는 것이,\" 패니가, 그 어떤 재료의 흔적도 없는 미소로 부인의 미소에 답하며 말했다. \"물론 제 결혼 생활의 가장 높은 목표가 될 거예요. 그것을 잃는다면, 물론 더없는 비참이겠지요. 부인의 크나큰 친절이 마다하지 않으시리라 믿고, 또 아빠께서도 마다하지 않으시리라 믿으며, 한 가지 부인께서 저지르신 작은 착오를 바로잡을까 합니다. 우리 가운데 가장 훌륭한 사람조차 착오에 빠지기 쉬운 법이라, 부인 같으신 분조차 작은 오류에 빠지셨군요, 제너럴 부인. 부인께서 이 신뢰에 결부된 것으로 그토록 인상 깊게 말씀하신 그 배려와 영예는, 의심할 바 없이 더없이 찬사에 차고 흐뭇한 성격의 것이긴 합니다만, 그것은 결코 저에게서 비롯된 것이 아니랍니다. 이 일을 두고 부인께 의논을 드렸다는 그 공로는 제게 너무도 큰 것이라, 정작 그것이 제 것이 아닌 이상 그 공을 가로챌 수는 없다는 생각이 들어요. 그것은 온전히 아빠의 것이지요. 부인의 격려와 후원에는 깊이 감사드립니다만, 그것을 청한 것은 아빠셨답니다. 제 약혼에 그토록 너그러이 승낙해 주셔서 제 가슴에 얹힌 큰 짐을 덜어 주신 점은 제가 부인께 감사드려야 할 일이지만, 부인께서 정작 제게 감사하실 일은 전혀 없는 거예요. 제가 집을 떠난 뒤에도 부인께서 늘 제 행실을 인정해 주시기를, 그리고 제 동생 또한 부인의 황송하신 호의를 받는 사랑스러운 대상으로 오래도록 남기를 바랍니다, 제너럴 부인.\"\n\n이 인사를, 더없이 정중한 태도로 늘어놓고 나서, 패니는 우아하고 명랑한 기색으로 방을 나섰다 — 그러고는 남의 귀에 들리지 않을 데까지 가자마자 상기된 얼굴로 위층으로 내달려, 동생에게 불쑥 뛰어들어, 그 애를 자그마한 겨울잠쥐라고 부르고, 눈이 좀 번쩍 뜨이도록 흔들어 대고, 아래층에서 벌어진 일을 일러 주고, 이제 아빠를 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물었다.\n\n머들 부인을 대하는 이 젊은 숙녀의 태도는 무척 독립적이고 자신만만했지만, 아직 그 이상으로 뚜렷하게 적대 행위의 포문을 여는 정도는 아니었다. 이따금 둘은 가벼운 접전을 벌였으니, 이를테면 패니가 그 부인에게 등을 두드려지는 느낌을 받았을 때라든지, 머들 부인이 유난히 젊고 고와 보였을 때가 그러했다. 그러나 머들 부인은 언제나, 더없이 우아한 무심함으로 방석들 사이에 묻혀 들어가 다른 데로 주의를 돌려 버림으로써 그런 무력 충돌을 곧 끝내곤 했다. 사교계는(그 신비로운 피조물은 일곱 언덕 위에도 자리 잡고 있었으니) 패니 양이 약혼으로 무척 나아졌다고 보았다. 그녀는 훨씬 더 가까이하기 쉽고, 훨씬 더 거리낌 없고 사람을 끄는 데가 있고, 훨씬 덜 까다로워졌다. 그리하여 이제 그녀는 수많은 추종자와 찬미자를 거느리게 되었으니, 시집보낼 딸을 둔 부인들의 쓰라린 분개를 샀다. 그 부인들은 도릿 양 사태를 두고 사교계에 반기를 들고 반란의 깃발을 세운 자들로 여겨질 만했다. 자신이 일으킨 동요를 즐기면서, 도릿 양은 그 한가운데를 자기 한 몸으로 거만하게 누비고 다녔을 뿐 아니라, 스파클러 씨까지도 거만하게, 아니 보란 듯이 그 속으로 끌고 다녔다. 그것은 마치 그들 모두에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '내가 더 강한 포로가 아니라 이 사슬에 묶인 나약한 포로를 거느리고 너희들 사이를 개선 행렬처럼 행진하는 것이 옳다고 여긴다면, 그건 내 일이다. 내가 그러기로 택했다는 것으로 충분하다!' 한편 스파클러 씨로 말하자면, 그는 아무것도 따지지 않았다. 그저 데려가는 곳이면 어디든 가고, 시키는 일이면 무엇이든 하고, 자신의 약혼녀가 두드러지는 것이 곧 가장 손쉬운 조건으로 자기가 두드러지는 일이라 여기며, 이토록 공공연히 인정받는 데 진심으로 감사했다.\n\n이런 형국이 이어지는 가운데 겨울이 봄을 향해 흘러가자, 스파클러 씨가 영국으로 건너가, 그 나라의 천재성과 학문, 상업, 기상, 그리고 분별을 표현하고 이끄는 데서 자신에게 맡겨진 역할을 떠맡아야 할 필요가 생겼다. 셰익스피어와 밀턴, 베이컨, 뉴턴, 와트의 나라, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 과거와 현재의 추상철학자, 자연철학자, 그리고 무수한 형태로 자연과 기예를 정복한 이들의 나라가, 스파클러 씨에게 와서 자기를 돌보아 달라고, 그러지 않으면 멸망하고 말리라고 외쳐 불렀다. 조국의 영혼 깊은 곳에서 터져 나오는 그 고뇌의 외침을 차마 외면할 수 없었던 스파클러 씨는, 자기가 가야만 한다고 선언했다.\n\n그리하여, 스파클러 씨가 이 온 세상에서 시건방진 데라곤 없는 으뜸가는 처녀와 언제, 어디서, 어떻게 결혼해야 하는가 하는 문제가 시급해졌다. 약간의 비밀과 은밀함을 거친 끝에, 패니 양 자신이 그 해결책을 동생에게 알려 주었다.\n\n\"자, 얘야,\" 어느 날 동생을 찾아내고는 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 너한테 할 얘기가 있어. 바로 지금 막 꺼내진 일이야. 그래서 당연히 꺼내지자마자 너한테 달려온 거고.\"\n\n\"언니 결혼 말이야, 패니?\"\n\n\"내 소중한 아가,\" 패니가 말했다. \"내 말을 앞질러 가지 마. 이 안달복달하는 꼬마야, 내 비밀을 내 방식대로 너한테 털어놓게 해 줘. 네 짐작으로 말하자면, 곧이곧대로 답하자면 아니라고 해야겠지. 사실 지금 문제가 되는 건 내 결혼이라기보다는, 그 절반쯤이나 될까, 에드먼드의 결혼이거든.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은, 어쩌면 전혀 까닭이 없는 것도 아니게, 이 미묘한 구별을 알아듣기에 다소 곤혹스러운 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"내가 곤란할 것도 없고,\" 패니가 외쳤다. \"서두를 것도 없어. 나는 무슨 관청에서 필요로 하는 것도 아니고, 어디 가서 표를 던져야 하는 것도 아니니까. 하지만 에드먼드는 그래. 그리고 에드먼드는 혼자 떠난다는 생각에 몹시 풀이 죽어 있어. 게다가 정말이지 나도 그이를 혼자 내버려두는 게 못 미더워. 어리석은 짓을 저지를 여지가 있으면 — 대개는 있게 마련이지만 — 그이는 영락없이 그 짓을 저지르고 마니까.\"\n\n장차 남편 될 사람에게 얼마나 안심하고 의지할 수 있는지에 관한 이 공정한 총평을 마무리하면서, 그녀는 사무적인 태도로 쓰고 있던 보닛을 벗어, 끈을 쥐고 바닥에 대롱대롱 흔들었다.\n\n\"그러니 이건 내 문제라기보다 훨씬 더 에드먼드의 문제인 거야. 어쨌든 그 얘긴 더 할 필요 없어. 그건 보기에도 자명하니까. 자, 사랑하는 에이미야! 그이가 혼자 갈 것이냐, 혼자 가지 않을 것이냐 하는 문제가 생기니, 거기서 또 다른 문제가 생기는 거지. 우리가 여기서 곧 결혼할 것이냐, 아니면 몇 달 뒤에 본국에 가서 결혼할 것이냐 하는 문제 말이야.\"\n\n\"내가 언니를 잃게 되는 거구나, 패니.\"\n\n\"넌 어쩜 그렇게,\" 패니가 반쯤은 너그럽게, 반쯤은 못 참겠다는 듯 외쳤다. \"남의 말을 앞질러 가는 꼬마람! 제발, 얘야, 내 말을 끝까지 들어 봐. 그 여자는,\" 물론 머들 부인을 두고 하는 말이었다. \"부활절이 지나서까지 여기 머물 거야. 그러니 내가 여기서 결혼해서 에드먼드와 런던으로 간다면, 그 여자보다 앞서 가는 셈이지. 그게 뭐라도 되는 일이야. 게다가, 에이미. 그 여자만 비켜나 있다면, 우리 집이 정해지고 꾸며질 때까지 에드먼드와 내가 그 집에 — 너도 알지, 네가 언젠가 어떤 무용수와 함께 갔던 그 집 말이야 — 들어가 살라는 머들 씨의 제안을 아빠께 한 것도, 내가 딱히 크게 반대할 일은 아니지 싶어. 더 나아가서, 에이미. 아빠는 늘 봄에 직접 런던으로 가실 작정이셨으니 — 봐, 에드먼드와 내가 여기서 결혼한다면, 우리는 피렌체로 떠나 거기서 아빠와 합류해서, 우리 셋이 함께 본국으로 여행해 갈 수 있는 거야. 머들 씨가 아빠께, 내가 말한 바로 그 저택에 함께 머물러 달라고 간청했으니, 아마 그러실 거야. 하지만 아빠는 당신 행동의 주인이시니까. 그 점에 대해선(그건 전혀 중요한 게 아니지만) 나도 단정적으로는 말 못 하겠어.\"\n\n아빠는 당신 행동의 주인이고 스파클러 씨는 전혀 그렇지 못하다는 그 차이가, 패니가 이 사정을 늘어놓는 태도 속에 강렬하게 드러났다. 동생이 그것을 눈치챈 것은 아니었으니, 그녀는 다가올 이별에 대한 아쉬움과, 자기도 영국 방문 계획에 끼었으면 하는 미련 사이에서 마음이 갈라져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그게 그 계획이구나, 패니 언니?\"\n\n\"계획이라니!\" 패니가 되받았다. \"정말이지, 얘야, 너 좀 사람을 힘들게 하는구나. 알다시피 나는 내 말이 그런 식으로 해석될 여지가 없도록 특별히 조심했잖아. 내가 한 말은, 어떤 문제들이 제기된다는 거였고, 그게 바로 그 문제들이라는 거야.\"\n\n리틀 도릿의 사려 깊은 눈이, 다정하고 고요하게 언니의 눈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"자, 내 사랑스러운 아가,\" 패니가 끈을 쥐고 보닛의 무게를 가늠하며 자못 못 참겠다는 듯 말했다. \"그렇게 빤히 쳐다봐야 소용없어. 새끼 올빼미라도 그쯤은 쳐다보겠다. 나는 너한테 조언을 구하는 거야, 에이미. 나더러 어떻게 하라고 권하겠니?\"\n\n\"있잖아,\" 리틀 도릿이 잠시 망설인 끝에 설득하듯 물었다. \"패니 언니, 있잖아, 만약 언니가 그걸 몇 달 미룬다면, 여러 가지를 생각할 때, 그게 제일 낫지 않을까?\"\n\n\"아니, 이 꼬마 거북아,\" 패니가 자못 매섭게 쏘아붙였다. \"나는 그런 건 조금도 그렇게 생각 안 해.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 보닛을 아예 멀찍이 내던지고, 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다. 그러나 거의 곧바로 다정해져서, 다시 휙 일어나더니, 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 의자째로 동생을 끌어안았다.\n\n\"내가 성급하다거나 매정하다고 여기지 마, 얘야. 정말로 그런 게 아니니까. 하지만 넌 어쩜 그렇게 별난 꼬마람! 누가 더없이 달래 주려고 하는데도 네가 사람 화를 돋우잖니. 내가 말했지, 이 귀여운 아가야, 에드먼드는 혼자 내버려둘 수가 없다고? 그리고 너도 그걸 알잖아?\"\n\n\"응, 응, 패니 언니. 언니가 그렇게 말했어, 알아.\"\n\n\"그리고 너도 알고 있잖아, 나도 안다고,\" 패니가 되받았다. \"그래, 내 소중한 아가야! 그이를 혼자 내버려둘 수 없다면, 그럼 내가 그이와 함께 가야 한다는 게 당연한 결론 아니겠니?\"\n\n\"그런 — 것 같아, 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다.\n\n\"그러니, 그 목적을 이루기에 실행 가능한 그 계획들을 다 들은 마당에, 사랑하는 에이미야, 결국 네가 나더러 그 계획들을 세우라고 권하는 것으로 알아들으면 되겠니?\"\n\n\"그런 — 것 같아, 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 다시 말했다.\n\n\"좋아,\" 패니가 체념하는 기색으로 외쳤다. \"그럼 그렇게 해야겠지! 나는, 얘야, 그 의구심과 결정을 내려야 할 필요를 본 순간 너한테 온 거야. 이제 결정을 내렸어. 그러니 그렇게 하기로 하자.\"\n\n이렇게 모범적으로, 자매의 조언과 사정의 힘에 자신을 내맡기고 나자, 패니는 무척이나 인자해졌다. 마치 자기 자신의 뜻을 더없이 친한 벗의 발치에 내려놓고는, 그 희생을 치렀다는 데서 양심의 뿌듯한 빛을 느끼는 사람 같았다. \"어쨌든, 내 에이미야,\" 그녀가 동생에게 말했다. \"넌 작은 피조물들 가운데 으뜸가는 아이고, 양식으로 가득 차 있어. 너 없이 내가 대체 어떻게 살아갈지 모르겠구나!\"\n\n이 말과 함께 그녀는 동생을 한층 꼭, 정말로 애틋하게 끌어안았다.\n\n\"그렇다고 내가 너 없이 지낼 작정이라는 건 결코 아니야, 에이미. 우리는 앞으로도 늘 떨어질 수 없을 만큼 가까이 지낼 거라고 바라니까. 자, 우리 귀염둥이야, 내가 한마디 조언을 해 줄게. 네가 여기 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 되면 — \"\n\n\"내가 여기서 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 된다고?\" 리틀 도릿이 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 당연하지, 얘야, 아빠가 돌아오실 때까지는 말이야! 에드워드를 동무라고 친다면 모를까, 그 애는 분명 동무가 못 되지, 여기 있을 때조차도 그렇고, 나폴리나 시칠리아에 가 있을 때야 더더욱 그렇잖아. 내가 말하려던 건 — 하지만 넌 어쩜 그렇게 사람 말을 끊는 사랑스러운 훼방꾼이람 — 네가 여기서 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 되면, 에이미, 그 여자가 너랑 무슨 교묘한 묵계 같은 걸 슬그머니 맺어서, 자기가 아빠를 마음에 두고 있다거나 아빠가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다거나 하는 식으로 흘러가게 두면 안 돼. 할 수만 있다면 그 여자는 그렇게 할 테니까. 나는 그 여자가 장갑 낀 손으로 살금살금 떠보는 그 음흉한 태도를 알아. 하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 그 여자 말을 알아들은 척하지 마. 그리고 만약 아빠가 돌아오셔서, 제너럴 부인을 네 엄마로 삼을 생각을 품고 계신다고 너한테 말씀하시거든(내가 떠나고 나면 그럴 가능성이 더 커지면 커졌지 작지는 않을 테니), 너한테 권하는 건, 곧바로 이렇게 말하라는 거야. '아빠, 저는 가장 단호히 반대한다고 말씀드려요. 패니가 이 일을 미리 일러 줬고, 그 애도 반대했고, 저도 반대해요.' 에이미, 네가 무슨 반대를 한다고 해서 그게 조금이라도 효력이 있으리라는 말은 아니고, 또 네가 그걸 어느 정도라도 단호하게 해낼 거라고 생각하는 것도 아니야. 하지만 거기엔 한 가지 원칙이 걸려 있어 — 자식 된 자의 원칙이 — 그러니 부탁이건대, 네 주위 모두를 가능한 한 불편하게 만들면서라도 그 원칙을 주장하지 않은 채로 제너럴 부인에게 새엄마 노릇을 당하지는 말아 줘. 네가 그걸 끝까지 밀고 나가리라고 기대하지는 않아 — 사실 너는 그러지 못할 거야, 아빠가 걸린 일이니 — 그래도 나는 네게 의무감을 일깨워 주고 싶은 거야. 내가 도울 수 있는 부분이라든지, 그런 혼사에 내가 맞설 수 있는 부분에 관해서라면, 너를 곤경에 내버려두지는 않을 거야, 얘야. 적잖이 매력이 없지도 않은 기혼 여성이라는 내 위치에서 끌어낼 수 있는 무게라면 무엇이든 — 그 위치는 늘 그 여자에게 맞서는 데 쓰일 테니까 — 분명히 말하지만, 제너럴 부인의 머리와 그 가짜 머리카락(그게 다 진짜가 아니라고 나는 확신해. 보기 흉하기 짝이 없고, 제정신인 사람이라면 그걸 돈 주고 살 리도 없어 보이긴 하지만)을 향해 죄다 들이댈 거야!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 감히 거기에 반대하지도 않았지만, 그렇다고 자기가 그대로 행동할 작정이라고 패니가 믿을 만한 어떤 빌미도 주지 않은 채, 이 충고를 들었다. 이제 말하자면 자신의 독신 생활을 정식으로 청산하고 세속의 일들을 정리해 둔 패니는, 특유의 열정으로 자기 처지의 그 중대한 변화를 준비하는 일에 착수했다.\n\n그 준비란, 신부를 위한 그 일습을 사들이기 위해, 자기 하녀를 그 길잡이의 보호하에 파리로 보내는 일을 두고 하는 말이었다 — 그 일습에 영어 이름을 붙이는 것은 이 이야기에서 지극히 천박한 노릇일 테고, 그렇다고(이 이야기가 쓰였다고 표방하는 그 언어를 고수한다는 속된 원칙을 지키느라) 프랑스어 이름을 붙이는 것도 사양하는 바이다. 이 대리인들이 사들인 그 호화롭고 아름다운 혼수는, 몇 주에 걸쳐, 세관들이 빽빽이 들어선 사이의 땅을 가로질러 나아갔다. 그곳은 제복 차림의 초라한 거지들로 이루어진 거대한 군대가 지키고 있었는데, 그들은 마치 자기들 병사 하나하나가 저 옛날의 벨리사리우스라도 되는 양, 그 혼수 위에다 끊임없이 거지의 탄원을 되뇌어 댔다. 그런 군단이 어찌나 많던지, 길잡이가 그들의 궁핍을 달래는 데 은화 꼭 한 부셸 반을 쏟아붓지 않았더라면, 그들은 그 혼수가 로마에 닿기도 전에 이리저리 뒤집어 가며 죄다 닳게 했을 것이다. 그러나 그 모든 위험을 뚫고, 그 혼수는 한 치 한 치 의기양양하게 옮겨져, 멀쩡한 상태로 여정의 끝에 다다랐다.\n\n거기서 그것은, 가려 뽑힌 여성 관람객 무리에게 전시되었고, 그 점잖은 가슴속에 누그러질 줄 모르는 감정을 일깨웠다. 그와 동시에, 그 보물들 가운데 일부가 공공연히 진열될 그 날을 위해 활발한 준비가 이루어졌다. 로물루스의 도시에 사는 영국인의 절반에게 아침 식사 초대장이 발송되었고, 나머지 절반은 그 엄숙한 행사의 바깥 여러 지점에서, 비평하는 의용병으로서 무장 태세를 갖추기로 작정했다. 더없이 고귀하고 저명한 영국인, 에드가르도 도릿 시뇨르께서, (날로 발전하는 나폴리 귀족들이 그 위에 표면을 깔아 놓은) 깊은 진창과 바큇자국을 뚫고 역마차로 달려와, 그 행사를 빛내 주었다. 가장 좋은 호텔과 그곳의 온갖 주방 병졸들이 그 잔치를 준비하느라 동원되었다. 도릿 씨가 발행한 어음들은 거의 토를로니아 은행에 대한 예금 인출 사태나 다름없었다. 영국 영사는 그의 영사 재임 전 기간을 통틀어 이런 결혼식을 치러 본 적이 없었다.\n\n마침내 그 날이 왔고, 카피톨리노의 암늑대도, 섬나라 야만인들이 요즘 들어 이런 일들을 어찌 꾸려 내는지를 보았더라면 시샘에 으르렁댔을 법했다. 조각가들이 그 흉악스러운 추함을 아무리 아첨으로도 덜어 주지 못했던, 군대 출신 사악한 황제들의 살벌한 머리통을 한 입상들이, 받침대에서 내려와 신부를 채 가려 했을 법했다. 옛적에 검투사들이 몸을 씻던, 막혀 버린 그 늙은 분수가 다시 생기를 얻어 솟구쳐 그 예식을 기리려 했을 법했다. 베스타 신전이 그 폐허에서 새로이 솟아올라, 오로지 그 행사에 위신을 보태려 했을 법했다. 그랬을 법은 했으나, 그러지 않았다. 감각 있는 존재들처럼 — 더러는 만물의 영장인 귀족 나리와 귀부인들조차 그러하듯 — 많은 것을 할 법은 했으나, 아무것도 하지 않았다. 그 식은 감탄스러운 장엄함 속에 치러졌다. 검은 옷, 흰 옷, 적갈색 옷을 입은 수도사들이 걸음을 멈추고 마차들을 바라보았다. 양털을 두른 떠돌이 농민들이 집집의 창문 아래에서 구걸하며 피리를 불었다. 영국인 의용병들이 줄지어 행진했다. 날은 저녁 기도 시각으로 기울어 갔다. 축제는 사위어 갔다. 천 개의 교회들이 그 일과는 아무 상관 없이 종을 울렸다. 그리고 성 베드로는 자기가 그 일과 무슨 관련이 있다는 것을 부인했다.\n\n그러나 그 무렵 신부는 피렌체를 향한 첫째 날 여정의 끝에 거의 다다라 있었다. 이 혼례의 특이한 점은, 온통 신부뿐이었다는 것이다. 신랑에게는 아무도 눈길을 주지 않았다. 첫째 들러리에게도 아무도 눈길을 주지 않았다. 설령 많은 이가 그녀를 찾았다 한들, 그 눈부신 광채 때문에 리틀 도릿을(그 자리를 맡은 사람이었다) 알아본 이는 거의 없었으리라. 그리하여 신부는 멋들어진 사두마차에 올라탔고, 어쩌다 보니 신랑이 함께 탔다. 그러고는 평탄한 포장도로 위를 몇 분 매끄럽게 굴러간 뒤, 절망의 수렁을 지나며, 또 길고 긴 파멸과 황폐의 가로수길을 지나며 덜컹대기 시작했다. 다른 혼례 마차들도 그 전이나 후에 같은 길을 갔다고들 한다.\n\n만약 그날 밤 리틀 도릿이 다소 외롭고 다소 쓸쓸한 처지에 놓였다면, 그 우울한 기분을 떨치는 데, 옛날처럼 아버지 곁에 앉아 바느질하며 그분의 저녁상과 휴식을 거들어 드릴 수 있는 일만큼 효험 있는 것은 없었을 것이다. 그러나 마부석에 제너럴 부인을 앉히고 의전용 마차에 앉아 있는 지금에 와서는, 그런 일은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 더구나 저녁상이라니! 만약 도릿 씨가 저녁상을 원했다면, 이탈리아인 요리사가 있고 스위스인 제과사가 있어서, 그들이 교황의 삼중관만큼이나 높은 모자를 쓰고서, 아래층의 구리 냄비를 갖춘 실험실에서 연금술사의 신비를 행하고 난 뒤라야 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그날 밤 그는 격언조에 훈계조였다. 그저 다정하기만 했더라면 리틀 도릿에게 더 큰 위안이 되었으련만, 그녀는 그를 있는 그대로 받아들였다 — 그녀가 그를 있는 그대로 받아들이지 않은 적이 언제 있었던가! — 그리하여 그에게서 가장 많은 것을, 가장 좋은 것을 끌어냈다. 마침내 제너럴 부인이 물러갔다. 밤이 되어 물러가는 일은 그녀에게 늘 가장 쌀쌀맞은 의식이었으니, 마치 인간의 상상력이 자기를 뒤따르지 못하도록 그것을 돌처럼 얼려 버려야 한다고 여기는 듯했다. 일종의 점잖은 소대 훈련에 맞먹는 그 뻣뻣한 예비 절차를 다 마치고 나서야, 그녀는 물러갔다. 그러고 나서 리틀 도릿은 아버지의 목에 팔을 둘러 밤 인사를 했다.\n\n\"에이미, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"오늘은, — 하 — 나를 크게 감동시키고 흐뭇하게 한 하루의 마무리로구나.\"\n\n\"그리고 좀 피곤하시기도 하시지요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"아니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"아니야. 이렇게 — 흠 — 더없이 순수한 종류의 흐뭇함으로 가득 찬 행사에서 비롯된 것이라면, 나는 피로를 느끼지 않는단다.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그가 그토록 기운찬 것을 보고 기뻤고, 자기 마음에서 우러나 미소 지었다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"이건 — 하 — 좋은 본보기로 가득 찬 행사다. 좋은 본보기로 말이다, 내가 아끼고 정든 자식아, — 흠 — 너에게 말이다.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그의 말에 가슴이 두근거려 무어라 해야 할지 몰랐다. 그가 마치 그녀가 무슨 말이라도 하기를 기대하는 듯 말을 멈췄는데도 그러했다.\n\n\"에이미,\" 그가 다시 말했다. \"네 사랑하는 언니, 우리 패니가 — 하 흠 — 혼약을 맺었으니, 그것은 우리의 — 하 — 연줄의 기반을 넓히고, 우리의 — 흠 — 사회적 관계를 공고히 하는 데 더없이 알맞은 것이다. 내 사랑하는 아이야, 나는 머지않아 너에게도 — 하 — 어울리는 짝이 나타날 날이 멀지 않기를 바란다.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요! 저는 아버지 곁에 있게 해 주세요. 부디 간청하건대, 아버지 곁에 머물게 해 주세요! 저는 그저 곁에 남아 아버지를 보살펴 드리는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않아요!\"\n\n그녀는 갑작스러운 두려움에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 에이미, 에이미야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그건 나약하고 어리석은 말이다, 나약하고 어리석어. 너에게는 너의 처지가 지운 — 하 — 책무가 있다. 그것은 그 처지를 발전시키고, 그 처지에 — 흠 — 어울리는 사람이 되는 일이다. 나를 보살피는 일로 말하자면, 나는 — 하 — 내 한 몸은 내가 건사할 수 있다. 아니,\" 그는 잠시 후 덧붙였다. \"혹여 내가 누군가의 보살핌을 받아야 할 처지가 된다면, 나는 — 흠 — 섭리의 — 하 — 축복으로 보살핌을 받을 수 있을 거다. 나는 — 하 흠 — 내 사랑하는 아이야, 너를 독차지하고, — 하 — 말하자면, 너를 희생시키는 일은 생각할 수 없구나.\"\n\n오, 저런 자기희생의 선언을 꺼내기에 어찌 이리도 늦은 시각이며, 그것을 마치 그 공을 자기 것으로 치는 듯한 태도로 늘어놓기에는, 또 만약 그런 일이 가능하기라도 하다면 그것을 정말로 믿기에는!\n\n\"말하지 마라, 에이미. 나는 단언컨대 그럴 수 없다. 나는 — 하 — 그래서는 안 된다. 내 — 흠 — 양심이 그것을 허락지 않을 거다. 그러므로, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 나는 이 흐뭇하고 인상 깊은 행사가 마련해 준 기회를 빌려 — 하 — 엄숙히 말해 두는 바다. 너를 — 하 — 어울리게(거듭 말하건대 어울리게) 시집보내는 일이, 이제 내가 소중히 품은 소망이요 목적이라고 말이다.\"\n\n\"오, 안 돼요, 아버지! 제발요!\"\n\n\"에이미,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"이 화제를, 더 높은 사회적 식견과, 더 높은 섬세함과 분별을 갖춘 누군가에게 — 이를테면, — 하 — 제너럴 부인 같은 분께 — 가져간다면, 내 심정의 — 흠 — 애정 어린 성격과 그 온당함에 대해 두 가지 의견이 나올 리 없으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다. 그러나 나는 너의 사랑스럽고 효성스러운 천성을 — 흠 — 경험으로 알기에, 더는 말할 필요가 없다는 데 아주 흡족하구나. 나는 — 흠 — 지금 당장 제안할 남편감이 있는 것도 아니다, 얘야. 점찍어 둔 사람조차 없어. 나는 다만 우리가 — 하 — 서로를 이해했으면 할 뿐이다. 흠. 잘 자거라, 내게 남은 단 하나의 사랑하는 딸아. 잘 자거라. 신의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n그날 밤 만약 리틀 도릿의 머릿속에, 아버지가 이제 형편이 펴진 마당에, 게다가 자기를 두 번째 아내로 대신할 마음을 품고서, 자기를 가벼이 내칠 수 있겠구나 하는 생각이 떠올랐다 한들, 그녀는 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다. 그를 홀로 떠받쳐 온 그 가장 험난한 시절에 그랬듯이 여전히 그에게 충실하게, 그녀는 그 생각을 떨쳐 버렸다. 그리고 눈물 어린 안타까움 속에서도, 그가 이제 모든 것을 그들의 재물을 통해, 그리고 그들이 계속 부유하기를, 더욱 부유해지기를 바라는 늘 마음에 품은 그 근심을 통해 바라보게 되었구나, 하는 것 이상으로 모진 생각은 품지 않았다.\n\n그들은 마부석에 제너럴 부인을 앉힌 채 의전용 마차에 앉아 삼 주를 더 보냈고, 그러고 나서 그는 패니와 합류하러 피렌체로 떠났다. 리틀 도릿은 오로지 자신의 애정을 위해서라도 거기까지 그와 동행했다가, 사랑하는 영국을 생각하며 혼자 되돌아오고 싶었으리라. 그러나 길잡이는 신부와 함께 떠나 버렸고, 그다음 순번은 시종이었으니, 돈으로 부릴 수 있는 사람이 하나라도 남아 있는 한 그 차례가 그녀에게까지 돌아올 리 없었다.\n\n로마의 살림이 그들 두 사람만의 차지로 남게 되자, 제너럴 부인은 마음 편히 — 즉, 무슨 일이든 그녀가 마음 편히 받아들일 수 있는 그만큼 마음 편히 — 지냈다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 그들에게 남겨진 세낸 마차를 타고 자주 나가서는, 혼자 내려 고대 로마의 폐허 사이를 거닐곤 했다. 그 광대한 옛 원형경기장의 폐허, 옛 신전들의 폐허, 옛 기념 개선문들의 폐허, 옛적에 사람들이 밟고 다니던 큰길들의 폐허, 옛 무덤들의 폐허는, 그것들 본연의 모습인 동시에, 그녀에게는 옛 마셜시의 폐허이기도 했다 — 그녀 자신의 옛 삶의 폐허, 옛적에 그곳을 채웠던 얼굴들과 모습들의 폐허, 그곳의 사랑과 희망과 근심과 기쁨의 폐허이기도 했다. 무너진 어느 파편 위에 자주 홀로 앉아 있는 그 외로운 처녀 앞에는, 행위와 고난의 두 폐허가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그 적막한 곳에서, 푸른 하늘 아래에서, 그녀는 그 둘을 함께 보았다.\n\n그러면 어김없이 제너럴 부인이 나타났다. 자연과 기예가 자기 자신에게서 온갖 색채를 다 빼내 버렸듯이, 그녀는 모든 것에서 색채를 빼내 버리면서. 손을 댈 수 있는 데라면 어디든 유스터스 씨의 본문대로 프룬과 프리즘을 적어 넣으면서. 어디서나 유스터스 씨와 그 일행을 찾으며 그 밖에는 아무것도 보지 못하면서. 고대의 가장 메마른 잔뼈들을 긁어모아, 인간다운 감흥이라곤 하나 없이 통째로 삼키면서 — 마치 장갑 낀 식시귀(食屍鬼)처럼.\n\n# CHAPTER 16. Getting on\n\n할리 가, 캐번디시 광장, 런던에 막 도착한 신혼부부는 집사장의 영접을 받았다. 그 위대한 인물은 그들에게 관심이 없었으나, 대체로는 참아 주었다. 사람들이 결혼하고 시집 장가 가는 일이 계속되어야 집사장도 필요해지는 법이다. 국가가 세금을 매기기 위해 만들어지듯, 가문은 집사를 두기 위해 만들어진다. 집사장은 의심할 바 없이, 자연의 이치란 자신을 위해서라도 부유한 인구가 유지되기를 요구한다고 곰곰이 생각하고 있었을 것이다.\n\n그리하여 그는 현관에서 마차를 내려다보되 눈살을 찌푸리지 않을 만큼은 너그러이 굴었고, 매우 점잖은 태도로 부하 하나에게 일렀다. \"토머스, 짐 옮기는 걸 거들어라.\" 그는 신부를 위층의 머들 씨 앞으로까지 안내하기도 했지만, 이는 그가 가문과 자신을 결부시키는 행위로서가 아니라, 여성에 대한 경의의 표시로 보아야 마땅하다(그는 여성의 숭배자였으니, 어느 공작 부인의 매력에 사로잡혀 있다는 사실은 익히 알려진 바였다).\n\n머들 씨는 벽난로 앞 깔개 주위를 어슬렁거리며 스파클러 부인을 맞이하려고 기다리고 있었다. 그가 그러려고 다가서는데 그의 손은 소맷자락 속으로 움츠러드는 듯했고, 외투 소맷부리를 어찌나 잔뜩 내미는지 마치 사람들이 흔히 떠올리는 가이 포크스의 형상에게 영접받는 것 같았다. 게다가 그가 그녀의 입술에 제 입술을 갖다 댈 때는, 마치 제 손목을 스스로 붙잡아 구금하듯 하더니, 자기 자신의 경찰관이라도 된 양 오토만과 의자와 탁자 사이로 뒷걸음질 치며 속으로 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다. \"자, 허튼짓은 안 돼! 가자! 내가 널 붙잡았으니, 얌전히 따라와야지!\"\n\n귀빈실에 들여진 스파클러 부인은—솜털과 비단과 친츠와 고운 아마포의 가장 깊숙한 성소에서—여기까지 자신의 개선이 훌륭했으며, 한 걸음 한 걸음 자기 길을 닦아 왔다고 느꼈다. 결혼 전날, 그녀는 머들 부인이 보는 앞에서 머들 부인의 하녀에게 우아한 무심함을 가장하며 소소한 기념품(팔찌와 보닛과 새 옷 두 벌, 모두 새것)을 건넸는데, 그것은 예전에 머들 부인이 그녀에게 주었던 선물의 네 배쯤 값나가는 것이었다. 이제 그녀는 머들 부인 자신의 방에 자리 잡았는데, 그 방은 그녀의 거처로 더욱 걸맞도록 몇 가지 손질이 더해진 터였다. 마음의 눈으로, 부가 살 수 있고 창의가 고안할 수 있는 온갖 호사스러운 장식에 둘러싸여 거기 비스듬히 기대 있노라니, 그녀는 자기 생각의 환희와 한 박자로 고동치는 그 어여쁜 가슴이, 오래도록 명성을 떨쳐 온 그 가슴과 겨루어, 그것을 능가하고, 그것을 폐위시키는 광경을 보았다. 행복했느냐고? 패니는 분명 행복했으리라. 이제 더는 차라리 죽었으면 하고 바라는 일 따위는 없었다.\n\n쿠리어는 도릿 씨가 친구의 집에 머무르는 것을 탐탁지 않게 여겨, 그를 그로스버너 광장 브룩 가의 어느 호텔로 모시는 편을 택했다. 머들 씨는 아침을 들자마자 곧바로 도릿 씨를 찾아뵐 수 있도록 마차를 이른 아침에 대기시키라 명했다.\n\n마차는 번쩍번쩍 빛났고, 말들은 윤기가 흘렀으며, 마구는 반들거렸고, 제복은 화사하면서도 오래갈 듯 보였다. 호화롭고 묵직한 위세의 행차였다. 머들에게 어울리는 의장이었다. 아침 일찍 나온 사람들은 그것이 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달려갈 때 그 뒤를 바라보며, 숨결에 경외를 담아 이렇게 말했다. \"저기 그분이 가신다!\"\n\n거기 그분이 가셨다, 브룩 가가 그를 멈춰 세울 때까지. 그러자 그 으리으리한 함에서 보석이 나왔으니, 그 자체로는 광채라곤 없었고, 오히려 정반대였다.\n\n호텔 사무실에 한바탕 소동이 일었다. 머들이라니! 막 순종마 한 쌍을 도시로 몰고 들어온 오만한 기질의 신사인 호텔 주인이, 몸소 나와 그를 위층으로 안내했다. 점원들과 하인들은 뒤쪽 복도로 질러 가서, 그를 한 번 보려고 우연인 척 문간이며 모퉁이에 서성였다. 머들이라니! 오, 해여, 달이여, 별들이여, 저 위대한 인물! 어떤 의미로는 신약 성서를 개정하고, 이미 천국에 들어선 부자. 누구든 마음에 드는 이를 골라 함께 식사할 수 있는 사람, 그 돈을 벌어들인 사람! 그가 계단을 올라가는 동안, 사람들은 벌써 아래쪽 계단에 자리를 잡고 서서, 그가 내려올 때 그의 그림자라도 자기들 위에 드리워지기를 바랐다. 마치 병자들을 데려다가 사도가 지나는 길목에 누이던 것과 같았으니—다만 그 사도는 상류 사회에 들지도 못했고, 그 돈을 벌어들이지도 못했지만 말이다.\n\n도릿 씨는 실내복 차림으로 신문을 들고 아침을 들고 있었다. 쿠리어가 떨리는 목소리로 \"메어들 씨!\"라고 아뢰었다. 도릿 씨는 긴장으로 한껏 부풀어 오른 가슴을 안고 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"머들 씨, 이건—하—참으로 영광입니다. 부디 제가 이—흠—이토록—하 흠—더없이 흐뭇한 배려에 대해 품고 있는 마음을, 그 깊은 마음을 표하도록 허락해 주십시오. 저는, 선생님, 선생님의 시간에 얼마나 많은 요구가 쏟아지는지, 또 그 시간이—하—얼마나 막대한 가치를 지니는지 잘 알고 있습니다.\" 도릿 씨는 자기 만족에 차도록 '막대한'이라는 말을 충분히 둥글게 발음하지 못했다. \"그런데도 선생님께서—하—이렇게 이른 시각에 그 값을 매길 수 없는 시간을 제게 베풀어 주신다는 것은—하—제가 더없는 존경으로 받들어 인정하는 영예입니다.\" 도릿 씨는 그 위대한 인물에게 말을 건네며 그야말로 떨고 있었다.\n\n머들 씨는 낮고 안으로 잦아드는 머뭇거리는 목소리로, 아무 뜻도 없는 소리를 몇 마디 내뱉더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했다. \"뵙게 되어 기쁩니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"진실로 친절하십니다.\" 이때쯤 손님은 자리에 앉아, 지친 이마 위로 그 큼직한 손을 쓸어내리고 있었다. \"강녕하시지요, 머들 씨?\"\n\n\"저야 뭐—그렇습니다, 평소만큼은 괜찮습니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"하시는 일이 어마어마하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럭저럭 그렇지요. 하지만—아니, 천만에, 저야 별로 문제될 게 없습니다.\" 머들 씨가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"소화가 좀 안 되시는지요?\" 도릿 씨가 넌지시 물었다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 저는—아, 저는 멀쩡합니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n그의 입술이 맞닿는 자리에는 마치 작은 화약 도화선이라도 터진 듯 거뭇한 자국이 나 있었다. 그리고 그는, 타고난 기질이 좀 더 빨랐더라면 그날 아침 몹시 신열에 시달렸을 법한 사람처럼 보였다. 이것과, 그가 이마 위로 손을 무겁게 쓸어내리는 모양이 도릿 씨의 근심 어린 물음을 자아낸 것이었다.\n\n\"머들 부인은요,\" 도릿 씨가 알랑거리듯 말을 이었다. \"선생님께서도 짐작하시겠지만, 제가 떠나올 적에—하—모든 이가 주목하는 분이요—흠—모든 이가 흠모하는 분이요, 로마 사교계의 으뜸가는 매혹이자 매력이셨습니다. 제가 그곳을 떠날 때 부인께서는 놀랍도록 좋아 보이셨습니다.\"\n\n\"머들 부인은,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"대개 매우 매력적인 여인으로 여겨지지요. 그리고 분명 그렇습니다. 나도 그 점은 잘 알고 있고요.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 응수했다.\n\n머들 씨는 다문 입속에서 혀를 굴렸는데—꽤나 뻣뻣하고 다루기 힘든 혀 같았다—입술을 축이고, 다시 이마 위로 손을 쓸고, 또다시 방 안을 죽 둘러보았으니, 주로 의자 밑을 살폈다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그가 처음으로 도릿 씨의 얼굴을 바라보고는 곧바로 도릿 씨의 조끼 단추로 눈을 떨구며 말했다. \"매력을 말하자면, 우리 대화의 주제가 되어야 할 분은 따님이지요. 그분은 더없이 아름답습니다. 얼굴이며 자태가 참으로 비범하시지요. 어젯밤 젊은이들이 도착했을 때, 나는 그런 매력을 보고 정말이지 놀랐습니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨의 흡족함이란 어찌나 컸던지, 그는—하—이미 편지로 전한 바를 머들 씨에게 직접 말씀드리지 않을 수 없다며, 두 가문의 이 결합으로 자기가 느끼는 영예와 행복을 토로했다. 그리고 손을 내밀었다. 머들 씨는 잠시 그 손을 바라보더니, 마치 자기 손이 노란 쟁반이나 생선용 칼이라도 되는 양 한순간 그 위에 얹었다가, 도릿 씨에게 도로 돌려주었다.\n\n\"무엇보다 먼저 마차를 몰고 들러야겠다고 생각했습니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"혹시 제가 도와드릴 일이 있을지 모르니 제 도움을 드리고자 했고, 또 부디 오늘 저와 식사하는 영광을 베풀어 주십사, 그리고 도시에 머무시는 동안 더 나은 약속이 없는 날이면 언제든 그래 주십사 청하려고요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 이러한 배려에 황홀해했다.\n\n\"오래 머무십니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"지금으로서는,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"보름을—하—넘길 생각은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그토록 긴 여정 끝에 그건 너무 짧은 체류로군요.\" 머들 씨가 응했다.\n\n\"흠. 그렇지요.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 사실은—하—친애하는 머들 씨, 제가 외국 생활이 제 건강과 취향에 어찌나 잘 맞는지를 깨달아서, 저는—흠—이번 런던 방문에 두 가지 목적밖에 없습니다. 첫째는—하—제가 지금 누리며 감사히 여기는 이—하—각별한 행복과—하—특권이요, 둘째는 제—하, 흠—돈을 가장 좋은 방식으로 관리하는—흠—말하자면 운용하는 일입니다.\"\n\n\"그러시군요, 선생.\" 머들 씨가 다시 혀를 굴린 뒤 말했다. \"그 점에 대해 제가 무슨 쓸모가 있다면, 얼마든지 분부만 내리시지요.\"\n\n도릿 씨의 말투는 이 까다로운 화제에 다가설수록 평소보다 더 많은 머뭇거림을 담고 있었으니, 그토록 드높은 권세가가 이를 어찌 받아들일지 도무지 분명치 않았던 까닭이다. 그는 어느 한 개인의 자본이나 재산을 들먹이는 일이, 그토록 도매로 거래하는 이에게는 한심하리만치 소매 같은 일로 비치지 않을까 미심쩍어했다. 머들 씨가 상냥하게 도움을 자청하자 크게 안도한 그는, 곧장 그 제안에 매달리며 감사를 산더미처럼 쏟아냈다.\n\n\"저는 감히—하—꿈도 꾸지 못했습니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"정말이지, 선생님의 직접적인 조언과 도움이라는—흠—그토록 막대한 이득을 바랄 엄두를요. 물론 저야 어떤 상황에서든 문명 세계의 나머지 모든 이들처럼—하, 흠—머들 씨의 행렬을 뒤따랐겠지만요.\"\n\n\"우리는 거의 친척이라 할 수도 있지 않습니까, 선생.\" 머들 씨가 양탄자 무늬에 묘하게 관심을 두며 말했다. \"그러니 저를 마음껏 부리셔도 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"하. 참으로 점잖으십니다!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다. \"하. 더없이 점잖으십니다!\"\n\n\"이를테면 한낱 외부인이라 할 만한 사람이 지금 이 시점에 그 좋은 거리들 가운데—물론 제 거리들을 말하는 겁니다만—그 어느 하나에라도 끼어들기란 쉬운 일이 아닐 겁니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요, 물론입니다!\" 도릿 씨가 다른 좋은 거리란 따로 없다는 듯한 어조로 외쳤다.\n\n\"—아주 높은 값을 치르지 않고서는 말이지요. 우리가 흔히 매우 긴 숫자라 부르는 그것 말입니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 들뜬 기분에 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 하, 하, 하! 긴 숫자라. 좋군요. 하. 참으로 적절한 표현이고말고요!\n\n\"하지만,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"저는 대개 어느 정도의 우대를 행사할 권한을—사람들은 흔히 그것을 호의라 부르고 싶어 하지요—제 손안에 쥐고 있습니다. 제 수고와 노고에 대한 일종의 사례 같은 것으로요.\"\n\n\"그리고 공익심과 천재성에 대한 사례로요.\" 도릿 씨가 거들었다.\n\n머들 씨는 무언가를 삼키는 듯 메마른 동작으로, 그 자질들을 알약처럼 꿀꺽 처리해 버리는 것 같더니, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그에 대한 일종의 보답으로요. 괜찮으시다면, 제가 이 제한된 권한을(사람들이 시샘하는 터라 제한되어 있지요) 어떻게 선생께 유리하게 발휘할 수 있을지 살펴보겠습니다.\"\n\n\"참으로 친절하십니다.\" 도릿 씨가 답했다. \"정말로 친절하십니다.\"\n\n\"물론,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"이런 거래에는 더없이 엄격한 청렴과 정직이 있어야 합니다. 사람과 사람 사이에 더없이 순수한 신의가 있어야 하고요. 흠 잡을 데 없고 흠 잡힐 데 없는 신뢰가 있어야 합니다. 그러지 않고서는 사업이란 굴러갈 수 없으니까요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 이 너그러운 소회를 열렬히 반겼다.\n\n\"그러므로,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"저는 어느 한도까지만 선생께 우대를 드릴 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다. 정해진 한도까지 말씀이시군요.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"정해진 한도까지요. 그리고 완전히 떳떳하게요. 하지만 제 조언에 관해서라면,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"그건 별개의 문제입니다. 그것은, 변변찮은 것이나마—\"\n\n오! 변변찮다니요! (도릿 씨는, 설령 머들 씨 자신이 하는 말일지언정, 그것이 깎아내려지는 기미라곤 조금도 견딜 수 없었다.)\n\n\"—그것은, 제가 원한다면 나와 동포 사이의 티 없이 깨끗한 명예의 결속에 비추어 보아도 나누어 드리지 못할 까닭이 없는 것이지요. 그리고 그것은,\" 머들 씨가 이제 창밖을 지나가는 쓰레기 수레에 골똘히 빠져들며 말했다. \"선생께서 적절하다 여기실 때면 언제든 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨의 새로운 감사. 머들 씨가 이마 위로 손을 쓸어내리는 새로운 동작. 고요와 침묵. 머들 씨가 도릿 씨의 조끼 단추를 응시함.\n\n\"제 시간이 다소 귀한지라,\" 머들 씨가, 마치 그동안 제 다리가 오기를 기다리다가 이제 막 그것이 도착하기라도 한 듯 불쑥 일어서며 말했다. \"이제 시티 쪽으로 움직여야겠습니다. 어디든 모셔다 드릴까요, 선생? 기꺼이 내려 드리거나 보내 드리겠습니다. 제 마차를 마음껏 쓰시지요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 자기 은행에 볼일이 있다는 데 생각이 미쳤다. 그의 은행은 시티에 있었다. 잘된 일이었으니, 머들 씨가 그를 시티로 데려다 줄 터였다. 하지만 정말이지, 자기가 외투를 걸치는 동안 머들 씨를 붙들어 둘 수야 없지 않은가? 아니, 그래도 되고말고, 그래야만 한다고 머들 씨가 우겼다. 그리하여 도릿 씨는 옆방으로 물러가 하인의 손에 몸을 맡겼고, 오 분 만에 의기양양하게 돌아왔다.\n\n그러자 머들 씨가 말했다. \"선생, 제게 기대시지요. 제 팔을 잡으십시오!\" 그리하여 도릿 씨는 머들 씨의 팔에 기대어 계단을 내려가며, 계단에 모인 숭배자들을 보았고, 머들 씨의 광채가 자기 안에서도 반사되어 빛나는 것을 느꼈다. 이어서 마차, 그리고 시티로의 행차. 그들을 바라보는 사람들. 희끗희끗한 머리에서 벗겨져 날아오르는 모자들. 이 경이로운 인간 앞에서 모두가 굽실거리고 움츠리는 광경이라니, 그와 같은 영혼의 부복은—아니, 드높은 하늘에 맹세코, 어디서도 볼 수 없었으니!—온갖 종파의 아첨꾼들이 한 번쯤 곱씹어 볼 만한 일이거니와, 일 년 중 어느 일요일에 웨스트민스터 사원과 세인트 폴 대성당을 합쳐 놓는다 해도 그만한 광경은 볼 수 없으리라. 이 공개된 개선의 수레 위에 높이 떠받들려, 그에 걸맞은 목적지인 황금빛 롬바르드 거리를 향해 으리으리한 행진을 펼치는 자신을 발견한 것은 도릿 씨에게 황홀한 꿈이었다.\n\n거기서 머들 씨는 굳이 내려서 걸어가겠다고 우기며, 자신의 변변찮은 마차를 도릿 씨가 마음대로 쓰도록 남겨 두었다. 그리하여 도릿 씨가 홀로 은행에서 나오고, 사람들이 머들 씨 대신 그를 바라보고, 그가 매끄럽게 굴러가는 동안 마음의 귀로 \"머들 씨의 친구라니 대단한 인물이로군!\" 하는 외침을 자꾸만 들을 때, 그 꿈은 더욱 황홀해졌다.\n\n그날 만찬에서는, 비록 미리 예견되거나 마련된 자리가 아니었음에도, 땅의 흙으로 빚어진 것이 아니라 아직은 알려지지 않은 어떤 우월한 재료로 빚어진 듯한 이들의 눈부신 무리가, 도릿 씨 딸의 결혼에 그 광채로운 축복을 내려 주었다. 그리고 그날 도릿 씨의 딸은 그 자리에 없는 그 여인과의 경쟁을 본격적으로 시작했으니, 어찌나 잘 시작했는지, 도릿 씨는 요구만 받는다면, 스파클러 부인이 평생토록 호사의 무릎에 길게 누워 지냈으며 영어에 마셜시 같은 험한 단어가 있다는 것조차 들어 본 적이 없노라고 거의 선서라도 할 수 있을 지경이었다.\n\n이튿날도, 그다음 날도, 그리고 날마다, 더 많은 만찬 손님으로 빛나는 가운데, 도릿 씨에게는 무대 위의 가짜 눈처럼 명함이 쏟아져 내렸다. 저명한 머들의 친구이자 혼인으로 맺어진 친척으로서, 법조계도, 주교도, 재무성도, 합창단도, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 도릿 씨와 사귀거나 친분을 두텁게 하고자 했다. 시티에 산더미처럼 늘어선 머들 씨의 사무실들 가운데 어디든, 도릿 씨가 자기 볼일로 동쪽으로 향할 때면(그런 일이 잦았으니, 사업이 놀랍도록 번창했던 까닭이다) 도릿이라는 이름은 언제나 머들이라는 위대한 존재 앞으로 통하는 통행증이 되었다. 그리하여 이 인연이 자기를 정말이지 출세시켜 주었다는 것을 도릿 씨가 점점 더 절감할수록, 그 꿈은 시시각각 더욱 황홀해졌다.\n\n오직 한 가지만이 도릿 씨의 마음에 황금빛으로도, 동시에 가볍게도 얹히지 않았다. 바로 집사장이었다. 그 어마어마한 인물은 만찬을 공식적으로 살피는 과정에서 도릿 씨를 바라보았는데, 그 방식이 도릿 씨가 보기에 미심쩍었다. 그는 도릿 씨가 만찬에 가려고 홀을 가로질러 계단을 올라갈 때, 도릿 씨가 영 마뜩잖게 여기는 그 유리알처럼 굳은 응시로 그를 바라보았다. 식탁에 앉아 술을 마시는 와중에도, 도릿 씨는 자기 포도주 잔 너머로 여전히 그를 보았으니, 그는 차갑고 유령 같은 눈으로 그를 살피고 있었다. 도릿 씨는 집사장이 어느 수감자를 알고 있었던 게 아닐까, 그를 그 감옥에서 본 적이 있는 게 아닐까—어쩌면 그에게 소개받은 적이 있는 게 아닐까 하는 불안에 사로잡혔다. 그는 그런 인물을 살필 수 있는 만큼 바짝 집사장을 살폈지만, 그를 다른 어디선가 본 적이 있다는 기억은 떠오르지 않았다. 끝내 그는, 그 사내에게는 경외심이라곤 없으며, 그 위대한 피조물에게는 어떤 감정도 없다고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그러나 그렇다고 마음이 놓이지는 않았으니, 그가 무슨 생각을 하든 집사장은 거만한 눈으로 그를 붙들어 두고 있었고, 심지어 그 눈이 접시며 다른 식탁 장식물에 가 있을 때조차도 결코 그를 그 눈에서 놓아주지 않았던 까닭이다. 그 사내에게 자기를 이렇게 눈 속에 가두는 것이 불쾌하다고 넌지시 이르거나, 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻는 일은 감히 무릅쓰기에 너무 대담한 짓이었으니, 그가 고용주들과 그 손님들을 대하는 위엄이 가공할 만했고, 자기에게 조금이라도 허물없이 다가오는 것을 결코 용납하지 않았던 까닭이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 17. Missing\n\n도릿 씨의 방문 기한은 이틀밖에 남지 않은 시점이었고, 그는 다시 한번 집사장의 검열을 받기 위해 — 그 희생자들은 언제나 집사장만을 위하여 차려입었다 — 옷을 갈아입으려던 참이었다. 그때 호텔 하인 하나가 명함 한 장을 들고 나타났다. 도릿 씨는 그것을 받아 들고 읽었다.\n\n'핀칭 부인.'\n\n하인은 말없이 공손히 기다렸다.\n\n\"이보게, 자네,\" 도릿 씨가 비통한 분노에 차서 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이런 우스꽝스러운 이름을 내게 가져온 이유를 설명해 보게. 나는 이 이름을 전혀 모르네. 핀칭이라니, 자네?\" 도릿 씨는 어쩌면 집사장에게 받은 분풀이를 대리로 갚고 있는지도 몰랐다. \"허! 핀칭이라니, 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n이보게, 그 자네는 어쩌면 무엇보다도 움찔(플린칭)한다는 뜻인 듯했다. 도릿 씨의 준엄한 눈초리에서 슬금슬금 물러서며 대답했으니 말이다. \"어느 부인입니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"나는 그런 부인을 모르네, 자네,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"이 명함을 치우게. 나는 남자든 여자든 핀칭이란 사람을 모르네.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다, 나리. 그 부인께서 이름으로는 모르실 줄 안다고 하셨습니다. 다만, 나리, 예전에 도릿 양과 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸다고 전해 달라 부탁하셨습니다. 나리, 막내 도릿 양이라 하셨습니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 이맛살을 찌푸리고는 한두 순간 뒤에 대꾸했다. \"핀칭 부인께 전하게, 자네,\" 마치 그 무고한 사내가 그 이름에 전적으로 책임이 있다는 듯 이름을 힘주어 말하며, \"올라오셔도 좋다고.\"\n\n그는 그 잠깐의 멈춤 동안, 만일 들이지 않으면 그녀가 무슨 전언을 남기거나, 아래층에서 그 옛 시절의 처지에 관한 수치스러운 무언가를 말할지도 모른다고 생각했던 것이다. 그래서 양보가 나왔고, 그래서 플로라가 그 이보게 자네의 안내를 받으며 모습을 드러내게 되었다.\n\n\"이 이름도, 부인 자신도 알게 되는,\" 도릿 씨가 명함을 손에 들고 서서, 설령 그런 영광을 누린다 한들 결코 일류의 영광은 못 되리라는 기색을 풍기며 말했다. \"그 기쁨을 저는 누리지 못하고 있습니다, 부인. 의자를 놓게, 자네.\"\n\n책임을 진 그 사내가 흠칫하더니 시키는 대로 하고는 발끝걸음으로 나갔다. 플로라는 수줍은 떨림에 휩싸인 채 베일을 걷어 올리고 자기소개를 시작했다. 그와 동시에 묘한 향기의 조합이 방 안에 퍼졌다. 마치 라벤더 물병에 실수로 브랜디를 넣었거나, 혹은 브랜디 병에 실수로 라벤더 물을 넣은 것 같았다.\n\n\"도릿 씨께 천 번이나 사과를 드리고 싶고 사실 이런 무례에는 그것도 턱없이 부족하겠지만 숙녀가 그것도 혼자서 이러는 것이 몹시 대담해 보일 줄은 저도 잘 알지만 그래도 전체적으로 이것이 최선이라 여겼는데 아무리 어렵고 게다가 부적절해 보일지라도 F 씨의 고모님이 기꺼이 동행해 주셨을 테고 또 대단히 강하고 기개 있는 분이라 그토록 숱한 변화를 겪으며 틀림없이 익혔을 그런 세상 물정에 밝은 분께는 깊은 인상을 주셨을 테지만 F 씨 본인이 자주 말씀하시기를 비록 블랙히스 인근에서 팔십 기니나 들여 잘 교육받았는데 이는 부모로서 큰돈이고 게다가 떠날 때 식기까지 돌려주지 않았으니 그건 값어치라기보다 인색함이지만 그래도 와인 사업에 한참 앞서 아무도 들어 주지 않을뿐더러 사기는커녕 거들떠도 안 보는 물건을 큰 수수료를 받고 파는 외판원으로 보낸 첫 몇 해에 그 학사 출신이 운영하던 학원에서 보낸 육 년 내내보다 더 많이 배웠다고 하셨는데 다만 어째서 학사가 기혼남보다 더 영리한지는 저는 모르겠고 알았던 적도 없지만 부디 용서하세요 그건 요점이 아니에요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 어리둥절함의 석상처럼 양탄자에 못 박힌 채 서 있었다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 자격이 있다고 내세울 수 없음은 솔직히 인정하지만,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨를 알고 지냈던 터라 처지가 달라진 지금 이러는 게 주제넘어 보이겠지만 그런 뜻은 아니고 하느님께서 아시듯 그런 바느질 솜씨를 가진 분에게 하루 반 크라운을 준 건 결코 은혜가 아니라 오히려 그 반대였고 거기에 무슨 격을 떨어뜨리는 것이 있다면 천만의 말씀 일꾼이 제 삯을 받는 것은 마땅하고 정말이지 저는 그분이 그걸 더 자주 받고 동물성 음식을 더 많이 먹고 등과 다리에 류머티즘을 덜 앓았으면 했어요 가엾은 분.\"\n\n\"부인,\" 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인이 숨을 돌리려 잠시 멈추자 도릿 씨가 큰 노력 끝에 숨을 되찾으며 말했다. \"부인,\" 얼굴이 새빨개진 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"제가 부인의 말씀을 — 허 — 제 딸의 — 음 — 지난 일 가운데 — 허 음 — 날삯을 받는 일과 관련된 무언가를 가리키시는 것으로 이해한다면, 부인, 저는 그 — 허 — 사실이, 설령 그것이 — 허 — 사실이라 한들, 결코 제가 아는 바가 아니었음을 분명히 말씀드리고 싶군요. 음. 저라면 그것을 허락하지 않았을 겁니다. 허. 결코! 결코!\"\n\n\"그 이야기를 더 끌 필요는 없고요,\" 플로라가 대꾸했다. \"어떤 경우든 그저 호의적이고 유일한 소개의 구실로 여겨 꺼냈을 뿐 결코 다른 의도는 없었지만 그것이 사실인 점에는 추호의 의심도 없고 마음 푹 놓으셔도 좋아요 지금 제가 입고 있는 바로 이 옷이 그걸 증명할 수 있으니까요 곱게도 지었지만 더 나은 몸매에 더 잘 어울릴 거라는 건 부인할 수 없지만 제 몸은 너무 살이 쪄서 어떻게 줄여야 할지 모르겠어요 부디 용서하세요 또 옆길로 새고 있네요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 돌처럼 굳은 채 자기 의자로 물러나 앉았고, 플로라는 그에게 누그러뜨리는 듯한 눈길을 던지며 양산을 만지작거렸다.\n\n\"그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨가,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"아서가 — 우리 어린 시절의 어리석은 버릇이고 클레넘 씨라 하는 편이 지금 형편에 훨씬 어울리는데 특히 낯선 사람을 그것도 높은 지위의 신사를 부를 때는 더 그렇지요 — 팽크스라는 사람이 전해 준 기쁜 소식을 알려 준 그날 아침 제 집에서, 아니 적어도 아빠 집에서, 자유보유권은 아니지만 그래도 명목상의 임대료로 빌린 긴 임대인데, 완전히 축 늘어져 하얗고 차갑게 졸도해 버렸던 일이 제게 용기를 줍니다.\"\n\n이 두 이름이 언급되자 도릿 씨는 이맛살을 찌푸리고, 빤히 쳐다보고, 다시 찌푸리더니, 오래전에 그러했듯 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 망설이다가 말했다. \"부디 — 허 — 용건을 말씀해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주십시오, 부인.\"\n\n\"도릿 씨,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"허락해 주시니 참 친절하시고 친절하신 게 제겐 지극히 자연스러워 보이는 게 더 위엄 있어지셨지만 닮은 점이 보여요 물론 살은 좀 붙으셨지만 그래도 닮은 점이요, 제가 이렇게 끼어드는 목적은 어떤 사람과도 조금도 의논하지 않은 순전히 제 뜻이고 더더구나 아서와는 결코 의논하지 않았는데 — 부디 용서하세요 도이스 앤드 클레넘 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요 클레넘 씨 혼자서요 — 모든 것이 천상의 빛으로 가득하던 그 보랏빛 시절에 황금 사슬로 매여 있던 그분을 어떤 근심에서 벗어나게 해 드린다면 제겐 한 군주의 몸값만큼이나 값진 일이지만 그게 얼마나 될지는 도무지 짐작도 못 하면서 다만 제가 이 세상에 가진 전부, 아니 그 이상의 총합으로 그것을 쓰는 거예요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 이 뒷말의 진심에는 크게 마음 쓰지 않고 되풀이했다. \"용건을 말씀해 주십시오, 부인.\"\n\n\"가망이 없는 줄은 저도 잘 알지만,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그래도 가능은 하고 가능한 일이니 신문에서 당신이 이탈리아에서 오셨다가 돌아가신다는 글을 읽고 흐뭇해하던 차에 시도해 보기로 마음먹었어요 그분과 마주치시거나 그분 소식을 들으실지도 모르고 만약 그러면 모두에게 얼마나 큰 축복이자 안도가 되겠어요!\"\n\n\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 머릿속이 마구 뒤엉킨 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"누구를 — 허 — 누구를,\" 그는 순전한 절망감에 목소리를 높여 그것을 되풀이했다. \"지금 가리키시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"이탈리아에서 온 그 외국인이요 저와 마찬가지로 당신도 틀림없이 신문에서 읽으셨을 텐데 그가 시티에서 사라진 일 말이에요,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"팽크스라는 이름의 사사로운 출처를 두고 하는 말은 아니지만 거기서 어떤 사람들이 사악하게도 얼마나 끔찍하게 심술궂은 말을 수군거리는지 짐작할 수 있고 아마 남을 제 잣대로 재는 거겠지요 그리고 아서의 — 도이스 앤드 클레넘 도무지 그 버릇을 못 고치겠네요 — 불안과 분노가 어떠할지는 뻔하잖아요.\"\n\n알아들을 만한 결론을 끌어내는 데에는 다행스럽게도, 도릿 씨가 그 일에 관해 들은 것도 읽은 것도 전혀 없었다. 그 덕분에 핀칭 부인은, 옷의 줄무늬 사이에서 주머니를 찾는 데 실질적으로 큰 어려움을 겪은 데 대해 거듭 사과한 끝에, 마침내 경찰 전단을 한 장 꺼내 들었다. 거기에는 마지막으로 베네치아에서 온 블랑두아라는 이름의 외국 신사가 모월 모일 밤 런던 시의 모처에서 까닭 모르게 사라졌다는 것, 그가 모 시각에 모 집에 들어간 것으로 알려졌다는 것, 그 집 거주자들의 진술에 따르면 자정 몇 분 전쯤 그곳을 떠났다는 것, 그리고 그 후로 다시는 그를 본 사람이 없다는 것이 적혀 있었다. 시각과 장소의 정확한 세부 사항과 함께, 그토록 불가사의하게 사라진 그 외국 신사에 대한 상세한 묘사가 잘 곁들여진 이 전단을, 도릿 씨는 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다.\n\n\"블랑두아!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"베네치아라! 그리고 이 인상착의! 나는 이 신사를 압니다. 그가 우리 집에 온 적이 있어요. 그는 좋은 가문 출신이지만 형편은 변변치 못한 어느 신사와 절친한 사이인데, 내가 그 신사의 — 음 — 후원자입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 제 변변찮으면서도 간절한 부탁은 더욱 절실해지는데요,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"돌아가시는 길에 부디 모든 길을 따라가며 모든 모퉁이를 오르내리며 이 외국 신사를 찾아봐 주시고 모든 호텔이며 오렌지 나무며 포도밭이며 화산이며 갖은 곳에서 그분을 수소문해 주세요 어딘가에는 분명 있을 텐데 어째서 나서서 자기가 여기 있다고 말하고 모두의 의혹을 풀어 주지 않는 걸까요?\"\n\n\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 도릿 씨가 다시 전단을 들여다보며 말했다. \"클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니란 누구입니까? 허. 여기 블랑두아 씨가 들어가는 것이 목격된 그 집의 사용과 관련해 이 이름이 적혀 있는데, 클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니란 누구입니까? 예전에 제가 — 음 — 어떤 — 허 — 잠깐 스치듯 알았던, 그리고 부인이 가리키신 것으로 짐작되는 그 사람입니까? 그 — 허 — 그 사람입니까?\"\n\n\"정말이지 아주 다른 사람이에요,\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"팔다리가 없고 대신 바퀴가 달렸고 어머니이긴 해도 그 어머니가 세상에서 가장 무서운 여자거든요.\"\n\n\"클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니가 — 음 — 어머니라고요!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"게다가 노인도 한 분 있고요,\" 플로라가 말했다.\n\n도릿 씨는 이 설명에 당장 정신이 나가 버릴 것 같은 표정이었다. 게다가 플로라가 느닷없이 플린트윈치 씨의 목도리를 재빠르게 분석하며, 그의 정체와 클레넘 부인의 정체 사이에 일말의 경계선도 긋지 않은 채 그를 각반 찬 녹슨 나사라고 묘사하는 바람에, 제정신을 유지하기에는 더더욱 불리해졌다. 남자와 여자, 팔다리 없음, 바퀴, 녹슨 나사, 무서움, 그리고 각반이라는 이 혼합물이 도릿 씨를 어찌나 완전히 멍하게 만들었던지, 그는 측은히 여길 만한 구경거리가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 더는 한순간도 붙들지 않겠어요,\" 그의 그런 상태에 영향을 받았으면서도 자기가 그 상태를 만들었다는 것은 전혀 의식하지 못한 플로라가 말했다. \"다만 신사로서 약속해 주시는 친절을 베푸시어 이탈리아로 돌아가시는 길에서나 이탈리아에서나 이 블랑두아 씨를 샅샅이 찾아봐 주시고 만약 찾거나 소식을 들으시면 모두의 의혹을 풀도록 그분을 나서게 해 주신다면요.\"\n\n그 무렵 도릿 씨는 당혹감에서 어지간히 회복되어, 그것을 자신의 의무로 여기겠노라고 제법 조리 있게 말할 수 있게 되었다. 플로라는 자신의 성공에 기뻐하며 작별을 고하려 일어섰다.\n\n\"백만 번 감사드리고요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"직접 전할 일이 생길 경우를 위해 제 명함에 주소를 적어 두었어요 그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨에게는 안부를 전하지 않겠어요 반갑지 않을지도 모르니까요 게다가 그렇게 변신한 마당에 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨는 이제 남아 있지도 않으니 왜 그러겠어요 하지만 저도 F 씨의 고모님도 늘 그분이 잘되기를 바라고 우리 쪽에서 무슨 은혜를 베풀었다는 주장은 추호도 하지 않으니 그건 안심하셔도 좋고 오히려 그 반대예요 그분은 하기로 한 일을 해냈고 그건 우리 중 숱한 사람이 하지 못하는 일이며 그것도 더할 나위 없이 잘 해냈으니 말할 것도 없고 저 자신도 그 못 하는 사람 중 하나인 게 F 씨의 죽음이라는 충격에서 벗어나기 시작한 이래로 줄곧 제가 무척 좋아하는 오르간을 배우겠다고 말해 왔으면서도 부끄럽게도 아직 음표 하나 모르거든요, 안녕히 계세요!\"\n\n방문까지 그녀를 배웅한 도릿 씨가 잠깐 정신을 추스를 시간을 갖고 보니, 그 면담이 머들 만찬 식탁과는 어울리지 않는, 내버려 두었던 옛 기억들을 불러내 놓았음을 알게 되었다. 그는 그날은 사양한다는 짤막한 편지를 써서 보내고, 곧바로 호텔의 자기 방에서 저녁 식사를 준비하게 했다. 여기에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 런던에서의 그의 시간은 거의 다 끝나 가고 있었고 약속들이 그 시간을 앞당겨 잡아먹고 있었다. 돌아갈 계획은 이미 짜여 있었다. 그리고 그는 자신의 중요한 위치를 보아서라도 블랑두아 실종 사건을 직접 조사하여, 헨리 가우언 씨에게 자신의 친히 한 조사 결과를 가지고 돌아갈 수 있어야 마땅하다고 여겼다. 그래서 그는 그날 저녁의 한가함을 이용해, 전단에 적힌 안내대로라면 쉽게 찾을 수 있을 클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니로 내려가서 그 장소를 보고 거기서 직접 한두 가지를 물어보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n호텔과 수행원이 허락하는 한 더없이 검소하게 저녁을 먹고, 핀칭 부인의 후유증에서 좀 더 잘 회복하려 난롯가에서 짧게 한숨 자고 난 그는, 삯마차를 잡아타고 혼자 길을 나섰다. 그가 이 타락한 시절에 머리도 없이 쓸쓸히 서 있는 템플 바의 그림자 밑을 지날 때 세인트폴 대성당의 묵직한 종이 아홉 시를 치고 있었다.\n\n뒷골목과 강가 길을 지나 목적지에 다가갈수록, 런던의 그 일대는 이런 시각에 그가 여태껏 짐작했던 것보다 더 흉한 곳으로 보였다. 그가 그곳을 본 지 여러 해가 흘렀고, 본디 그곳을 잘 알지도 못했으니, 그의 눈에는 그곳이 음산하고 으스스한 모습을 띠고 있었다. 그것이 그의 상상력에 어찌나 강하게 인상을 남겼던지, 마부가 길을 한 번 이상 물어본 끝에 멈춰 서서 자기가 믿기로는 이곳이 찾던 그 문간이라고 말했을 때, 도릿 씨는 마차 문을 손에 쥔 채, 그 장소의 어두운 모습이 반쯤 두려워 머뭇거리며 서 있었다.\n\n과연 그곳은 그날 밤, 일찍이 보였던 그 어느 때 못지않게 음울해 보였다. 전단 두 장이 입구 벽에 한쪽씩 나뉘어 붙어 있었고, 등불이 밤공기에 흔들릴 때마다 그 위로 그림자가 스쳐 갔으니, 마치 글줄을 따라가는 손가락의 그림자 같았다. 분명 그곳에 감시가 붙어 있었다. 도릿 씨가 멈춰 서 있는 동안 한 사내가 길 건너편에서 안으로 들어오고, 또 한 사내가 안쪽의 어느 어두운 구석에서 밖으로 나왔다. 둘 다 지나가며 그를 쳐다보았고, 둘 다 그 근처에 우두커니 서 있었다.\n\n그 울안에는 집이 하나뿐이었으므로 헷갈릴 여지가 없었다. 그래서 그는 그 집의 계단을 올라가 문을 두드렸다. 이층의 창문 둘에 흐릿한 불빛이 있었다. 문은 마치 집이 빈 듯 황량하고 텅 빈 소리를 되울렸다. 그러나 빈 것은 아니었으니, 거의 곧바로 불빛이 보이고 발소리가 들렸기 때문이다. 그 둘이 함께 문으로 다가왔고, 사슬이 삐걱거리더니, 앞치마를 얼굴과 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 여자가 그 틈에 서 있었다.\n\n\"누구세요?\" 여자가 말했다.\n\n이 모습에 몹시 놀란 도릿 씨는, 자기가 이탈리아에서 왔고 자기가 아는 그 실종된 사람에 관해 한 가지 물어보고 싶다고 대답했다.\n\n\"여보!\" 여자가 갈라진 목소리를 높여 외쳤다. \"제러마이아!\"\n\n이에 마른 노인 하나가 나타났는데, 도릿 씨는 그의 각반을 보고 그가 바로 그 녹슨 나사라고 알아본 듯했다. 여자는 그 마른 노인을 두려워하고 있었으니, 그가 다가오자 앞치마를 휙 걷어 내려 핼쑥하니 겁먹은 얼굴을 드러냈기 때문이다. \"문을 열어, 이 멍청아,\" 노인이 말했다. \"신사분을 들이라고.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 어깨 너머로 자기 마부와 삯마차 쪽을 한 번 흘끗 보고 나서, 흐릿한 현관으로 걸어 들어갔다. \"자, 나리,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"여기서 적당하다 여기시는 건 무엇이든 물으셔도 됩니다. 여기엔 비밀이 없습니다, 나리.\"\n\n대답이 나오기도 전에, 여자의 목소리이긴 하나 강하고 준엄한 목소리가 위에서 불렀다. \"누구냐?\"\n\n\"누구냐고요?\" 제러마이아가 되받았다. \"또 무슨 조사랍니다. 이탈리아에서 온 신사랍니다.\"\n\n\"이리로 모셔 올려라!\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 그럴 필요 없다고 여기는 듯 투덜거렸지만, 도릿 씨에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"클레넘 부인입니다. 저분은 하고 싶은 대로 하시지요. 제가 길을 안내하겠습니다.\" 그러고는 도릿 씨를 앞장세워 시커멓게 그을린 계단을 올라갔다. 도릿 씨가, 그도 그럴 것이, 가는 길에 뒤를 돌아보니, 아까처럼 그 섬뜩한 모양으로 다시 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 그 여자가 따라오고 있었다.\n\n클레넘 부인은 작은 탁자 위에 장부를 펼쳐 놓고 있었다. \"오!\" 그녀가 흔들림 없는 눈길로 손님을 바라보며 불쑥 말했다. \"이탈리아에서 오셨다고요. 그래서요?\"\n\n도릿 씨는 그 순간 \"허 — 그래서요?\"보다 더 또렷한 대꾸를 찾지 못해 쩔쩔맸다.\n\n\"이 실종된 사람이 어디 있나요? 그가 어디 있는지 알려 주러 오셨나요? 그러셨으면 좋겠는데요?\"\n\n\"그러기는커녕, 저는 — 음 — 정보를 구하러 왔습니다.\"\n\n\"우리로서는 안된 일이지만, 여기서 얻을 정보는 없어요. 플린트윈치, 신사분께 전단을 보여 드려요. 가져가시게 몇 장 드리고요. 읽으시게 등불을 비춰 드려요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨가 시키는 대로 했고, 도릿 씨는 마치 전에 본 적이 없는 듯 그것을 끝까지 읽었다. 이 집과 그 안에 있는 사람들의 분위기에 다소 흐트러진 그의 침착함을 가다듬을 기회가 생겨 충분히 다행스러웠다. 종이에 눈을 두고 있는 동안 그는 플린트윈치 씨와 클레넘 부인의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 느꼈다. 고개를 들어 보니, 그 느낌이 공연한 것이 아니었음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"이제 당신도 우리가 아는 만큼은 알게 되셨어요, 나리,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"블랑두아 씨가 당신의 친구인가요?\"\n\n\"아니요 — 그 — 음 — 아는 사이입니다,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"혹시 그에게서 무슨 의뢰를 받은 건 아니고요?\"\n\n\"제가요? 허. 결단코 아닙니다.\"\n\n그 캐묻는 눈길은 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴을 거쳐 가더니 차츰 바닥으로 향했다. 묻는 사람이 아니라 묻는 대상이 되어 있음을 알고 당황한 도릿 씨는, 그 뜻밖에 뒤바뀐 상황을 되돌리는 데 매달렸다.\n\n\"저는 — 허 — 재산이 있는 신사로, 지금은 가족과 하인들과 — 음 — 제법 큰 살림을 거느리고 이탈리아에 살고 있습니다. — 허 — 제 영지와 관련된 일로 잠시 런던에 와 있다가 이 기이한 실종 소식을 듣고, 저로서는 직접 그 정황을 알고 싶었습니다. 왜냐하면 — 허 음 — 이탈리아에 한 영국 신사가 있는데, 돌아가면 분명 만날 사람이고, 그가 블랑두아 씨와 가까이 날마다 친밀하게 지내 왔거든요. 헨리 가우언 씨입니다. 그 이름을 아실지도 모르겠군요.\"\n\n\"들어 본 적 없어요.\"\n\n클레넘 부인이 그렇게 말했고, 플린트윈치 씨가 그 말을 따라 했다.\n\n\"그에게 — 허 — 이야기를 조리 있고 차근차근 들려주고 싶어서요,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"여쭤도 될까요 — 가령, 세 가지를?\"\n\n\"원하신다면 서른 가지라도.\"\n\n\"블랑두아 씨를 안 지 오래되셨습니까?\"\n\n\"채 일 년도 안 됐어요. 여기 플린트윈치 씨가 장부를 찾아보고 언제, 누구에 의해 파리에서 그가 우리에게 소개되었는지 알려 드릴 거예요. 만약 그것이,\" 클레넘 부인이 덧붙였다. \"당신께 무슨 만족이 된다면요. 우리에겐 변변찮은 만족이지만.\"\n\n\"그를 자주 보셨습니까?\"\n\n\"아뇨. 두 번이요. 한 번은 전에, 그리고 —\"\n\n\"그 한 번 말이지요,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 거들었다.\n\n\"그리고 그 한 번이요.\"\n\n\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 도릿 씨가 자신의 중요한 위치를 되찾으면서 자기가 어떤 상급의 자격으로 치안판사직을 맡고 있는 듯한 망상이 점점 커지는 가운데 말했다. \"부인, 제가 — 허 — 거느리는, 아니 보호하는, 아니 말하자면 — 음 — 아는, 아는 그 신사의 더 큰 만족을 위해 여쭙겠습니다만 — 블랑두아 씨가 이 종이에 적힌 그날 밤 여기에 볼일이 있어 온 것입니까?\"\n\n\"본인 말로는 볼일이라더군요,\" 클레넘 부인이 대답했다.\n\n\"그 — 허 — 실례지만 — 그 볼일의 성격을 알려 주실 수 있습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n그 대답의 장벽을 넘기란 분명 불가능했다.\n\n\"그 질문은 전에도 받았어요,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"그리고 대답은 아니요였지요. 우리는 거래를, 아무리 사소한 것이라도, 온 도시에 떠벌리고 싶지 않아요. 우리는 아니요라고 말합니다.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 가령 그가 돈을 가지고 가지는 않았느냐는 겁니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그는 우리 돈은 한 푼도 가져가지 않았고, 여기서 한 푼도 얻지 못했어요, 나리.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 도릿 씨가 클레넘 부인에게서 플린트윈치 씨에게로, 플린트윈치 씨에게서 클레넘 부인에게로 시선을 옮기며 말했다. \"두 분은 이 수수께끼를 스스로 설명할 길이 없으신 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각하세요?\" 클레넘 부인이 되받았다.\n\n그 차갑고 매정한 물음에 당황한 도릿 씨는, 자기가 왜 그렇게 생각하는지 아무런 이유도 대지 못했다.\n\n\"저는 이렇게 설명해요, 나리,\" 도릿 씨 쪽의 어색한 침묵이 흐른 뒤 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그가 어딘가로 여행을 다니거나 어딘가에 숨어 있다는 데에 의심이 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그가 — 허 — 어딘가에 숨을 이유가 있는지 아십니까?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n그것은 앞서와 똑같은 아니요였고, 또 하나의 장벽을 세웠다.\n\n\"당신은 제가 그 실종을 제 스스로에게 설명하느냐고 물으셨지,\" 클레넘 부인이 준엄하게 일깨웠다. \"당신께 설명하느냐고 물으신 게 아니에요. 저는 당신께 그것을 설명하려 들지 않아요, 나리. 그것이 제 일이 아닌 것은, 그것을 요구하는 것이 당신 일이 아닌 것과 마찬가지라고 생각하니까요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 사과하듯 고개를 숙여 대답했다. 더 물을 것이 없다고 말하려 뒤로 물러서면서, 그는 그녀가 얼마나 음울하게, 시선을 바닥에 못 박은 채, 무언가를 단호히 기다리는 듯한 기색으로 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있는지 보지 않을 수 없었다. 또한 그녀의 의자에서 조금 떨어져 선 플린트윈치 씨에게도 똑같은 표정이 고스란히 비치고 있음을, 그 역시 시선을 바닥에 두고 오른손으로 턱을 가만히 문지르고 있음을 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 애프리 부인이 (물론 그 앞치마를 쓴 여자였다) 손에 들고 있던 촛대를 떨어뜨리며 소리쳤다. \"저기! 오 맙소사! 또 저거예요. 들어 봐요, 제러마이아! 지금요!\"\n\n소리가 조금이라도 있었다 한들 너무나 희미했으니, 그녀가 소리에 귀 기울이는 버릇이 단단히 들었음에 틀림없었다. 그러나 도릿 씨는 마른 잎이 떨어지는 것 같은 무언가를 듣긴 들었다고 믿었다. 그 여자의 공포는, 아주 잠깐 동안, 세 사람을 다 건드리는 듯했다. 그리고 그들 모두 귀를 기울였다.\n\n먼저 움직인 것은 플린트윈치 씨였다. \"애프리, 이 사람아,\" 그가 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 그녀를 흔들어 대고 싶어 안달이 난 팔꿈치를 부들부들 떨며 그녀에게 슬그머니 다가서서 말했다. \"또 옛 버릇이 도졌군. 다음엔 잠결에 돌아다니면서 네 그 정신 나간 꼴을 한바탕 부리겠지, 이 사람아. 약을 좀 먹어야겠어. 이 신사분을 배웅하고 나서, 내가 아주 편안한 약을 한 첩 지어 주지, 이 사람아. 아주 편안한 약으로!\"\n\n애프리 부인에게는 그것이 기대 속에서 그리 편안하게 다가오지 않는 듯했다. 그러나 제러마이아는 자신의 치료약은 더 언급하지 않고, 클레넘 부인의 탁자에서 다른 초 하나를 집어 들며 말했다. \"자, 나리, 아래까지 불을 밝혀 드릴까요?\"\n\n도릿 씨는 고맙다고 하고는 아래로 내려갔다. 플린트윈치 씨는 한순간도 지체 없이 그를 내보내고 사슬로 잠가 버렸다. 그는 다시 그 두 사내를 지나쳤는데, 하나는 나가고 하나는 들어오는 중이었다. 그는 기다리게 해 둔 마차에 올라타고 떠났다.\n\n멀리 가기도 전에 마부가 마차를 세우더니, 그 두 사내가 함께 요구하기에 자기 이름과 마차 번호와 주소를 그들에게 알려 주었으며, 도릿 씨를 태운 곳의 주소며 마차 대기소에서 불려 온 시각이며 거쳐 온 길까지 다 알려 주었다고 일렀다. 이 일로 그날 밤의 모험은, 도릿 씨가 다시 난롯가에 앉았을 때나 잠자리에 들었을 때나, 그의 마음속에서 조금도 덜 뜨겁게 들끓지 않았다. 밤새도록 그는 그 음산한 집을 떠돌았고, 두 사람이 단호히 기다리는 것을 보았고, 앞치마로 얼굴을 가린 여자가 그 소리에 대해 외치는 것을 들었으며, 실종된 블랑두아의 시체를 발견했으니, 그것은 어떤 때는 지하실에 묻혀 있었고 어떤 때는 벽 속에 벽돌로 봉해져 있었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 18. A Castle in the Air\n\n부귀와 지위에는 갖가지 근심이 따르는 법이다. 도릿 씨는 자신이 클레넘 상회에 신분을 밝히거나, 그 클레넘이라는 주제넘은 자를 알고 지냈다는 사실을 내비칠 필요가 없었다는 점을 떠올리며 흡족해했으나, 그 만족은 아직 신선할 때 간밤에 한풀 꺾이고 말았다. 돌아오는 길에 마셜시를 들러 옛 문을 한번 들여다보아야 할지 말아야 할지를 두고 그의 마음속에서 논쟁이 일었던 것이다. 그는 그러지 않기로 결정했다. 그리고 런던 다리를 건너 워털루 다리로 강을 다시 건너자고 — 그렇게 가면 옛 거처가 거의 눈에 들어올 만큼 가까운 길이었으므로 — 제안한 마부에게 몹시 사납게 굴어 그를 놀라게 했다. 그래도, 그럼에도 불구하고, 그 문제는 그의 가슴속에 갈등을 불러일으켰고, 무슨 묘한 이유에서인지 아니면 아무 이유도 없이, 그는 막연히 불만스러웠다. 이튿날 머들의 만찬 식탁에서조차 그는 그 일로 어찌나 심기가 불편했던지, 자신을 둘러싼 상류 사회와 무섭도록 어울리지 않는 모양새로 틈만 나면 그 일을 거듭 곱씹었다. 그 무거운 눈으로 집사장이 그의 명상의 흐름을 가늠할 수 있었다면 자신을 어떻게 보았을까 생각하니 그는 얼굴이 화끈거렸다.\n\n작별 만찬은 화려하기 그지없었고, 그의 방문을 더없이 빛나는 방식으로 마무리지었다. 패니는 젊음과 미모의 매력에 더하여, 마치 결혼한 지 스무 해는 된 듯한 어떤 진중한 자기 안정감을 갖추고 있었다. 그는 마음 편히 그녀를 두고 떠나 그녀가 명예로운 길을 걷도록 맡길 수 있겠다고 느꼈고, 또 자신이 총애하는 막내딸의 조신한 미덕을 깎아내리지 않고, 또 후견인다운 너그러움을 거두지도 않으면서, 자신에게 그런 딸이 하나 더 있었으면 좋겠다고 바랐다.\n\n\"얘야,\" 헤어질 때 그가 그녀에게 말했다. \"우리 집안은 네가 — 하 — 그 위엄을 떨치고 — 험 — 그 중요함을 지켜 주기를 바란단다. 네가 결코 그것을 저버리지 않으리라 믿는다.\"\n\n\"네, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. \"그건 믿으셔도 될 거예요. 사랑하는 에이미에게 제 깊은 사랑을 전해 주시고, 저도 곧 편지를 쓰겠어요.\"\n\n\"내가 — 하 — 다른 누구에게 전할 말은 없느냐?\" 도릿 씨가 슬며시 떠보는 투로 물었다.\n\n\"아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. 그 앞에 즉각 제너럴 부인의 모습이 어른거렸다. \"아뇨, 됐어요. 친절하시네요, 아빠, 하지만 사양하겠어요. 아빠께서 전해 주실 만한, 아빠 마음에 드실 다른 전언은 없어요, 고마워요, 사랑하는 아빠.\"\n\n그들은 바깥쪽 응접실에서 헤어졌는데, 거기서는 스파클러 씨만이 자기 숙녀의 시중을 들며 의무를 다해 악수할 차례를 기다리고 있었다. 스파클러 씨가 이 마지막 알현에 들게 되자, 머들 씨가 슬그머니 들어왔는데, 소맷자락에 팔이 들어 있는 기색이라곤 비핀 양의 쌍둥이 형제라고 해도 좋을 만큼 거의 없는 모습으로, 도릿 씨를 아래층까지 배웅하겠노라 고집했다. 도릿 씨의 만류가 모두 헛되어, 그는 이 저명한 인물이 현관문까지 동행해 주는 영예를 누렸으며, 그 인물은 (도릿 씨가 계단에서 악수하며 그에게 말했듯이) 이 기억할 만한 방문 동안 그야말로 온갖 배려와 호의로 그를 압도해 주었다. 그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다. 도릿 씨는 벅찬 가슴을 안고 마차에 올랐으며, 아래층 영역에 작별 인사를 하러 와 있던 자신의 안내원이 그의 위풍당당한 출발을 지켜볼 기회를 가졌다는 사실에 조금도 서운하지 않았다.\n\n도릿 씨가 호텔에서 내릴 때까지도 앞서 말한 그 위풍당당함은 여전히 그를 가득 채우고 있었다. 안내원과 호텔 하인 대여섯의 부축을 받으며 그는 차분한 위엄을 풍기며 현관을 지나고 있었는데, 보라! 그를 벙어리처럼 굳어 버리게 만든 광경이 눈앞에 나타났다. 존 치버리가, 가장 좋은 옷차림에, 높다란 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 상아 손잡이 지팡이를 점잖게 들었으나 그 때문에 거동이 어색해진 채로, 손에 시가 한 다발을 들고 서 있는 것이 아닌가!\n\n\"자, 젊은이,\" 짐꾼이 말했다. \"이분이 그 신사분이라네. 이 젊은이가 한사코 기다리겠다고 했습니다, 나리. 나리께서 반가워하실 거라면서요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 그 젊은이를 노려보며 목이 멘 듯하더니, 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다. \"아! 영존이로구나! 영존이지, 아마? 그렇지 않니?\"\n\n\"네, 나리,\" 영존이 대답했다.\n\n\"내가 — 하 — 영존이라고 생각했지!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"저 젊은이는 올라와도 좋다.\" 그는 지나가며 시중꾼들을 돌아보았다. \"오 그래, 올라와도 돼. 영존을 따라오게 하라. 위에서 그와 이야기하겠다.\"\n\n영존은 미소 지으며, 무척 흐뭇해하며 따라갔다. 도릿 씨의 방에 이르렀다. 촛불이 켜졌다. 시중꾼들이 물러갔다.\n\n\"자, 이보게,\" 도릿 씨는 안전하게 둘만 남게 되자 그를 향해 돌아서며 멱살을 움켜쥐고 말했다. \"이게 무슨 짓인가?\"\n\n가엾은 존의 얼굴에 떠오른 경악과 공포는 — 그는 차라리 다음 순간에 포옹을 받으리라 기대했던 터였다 — 어찌나 강렬하게 드러나는 것이었던지, 도릿 씨는 손을 거두고 그저 그를 노려보기만 했다.\n\n\"감히 이런 짓을 해?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"어떻게 여기 올 생각을 했지? 감히 나를 모욕해?\"\n\n\"제가 나리를 모욕한다고요?\" 영존이 외쳤다. \"오!\"\n\n\"그래, 이 사람아,\" 도릿 씨가 대꾸했다. \"나를 모욕하는 거지. 네가 여기 온 것은 모욕이고, 무례이며, 뻔뻔함이다. 여기서 너를 원하는 사람은 없어. 누가 너를 여기로 보냈나? 도대체 — 하 — 무슨 짓을 하러 여기 온 거야?\"\n\n\"저는 생각하기를, 나리,\" 영존이, 평생 — 칼리지 시절을 통틀어서도 — 도릿 씨를 향해 보인 적 없는 만큼 창백하고 충격받은 얼굴로 말했다. \"저는 생각하기를, 나리, 나리께서 다발 하나를 받아 주시는 친절을 베푸시는 데 반대하지 않으실 거라고 —\"\n\n\"그놈의 다발은 집어치워, 이 사람아!\" 도릿 씨가 걷잡을 수 없는 분노로 외쳤다. \"나는 — 험 — 담배 안 피워.\"\n\n\"황공하게도 용서를 빕니다, 나리. 예전엔 피우셨지요.\"\n\n\"그 말을 다시 해 봐,\" 도릿 씨가 완전히 제정신을 잃고 외쳤다. \"그러면 부지깽이를 들어 네게 휘두를 테니!\"\n\n존 치버리는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다.\n\n\"멈춰, 이 사람아!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다. \"멈춰! 앉게. 빌어먹을, 앉으라니까!\"\n\n존 치버리는 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 털썩 주저앉았고, 도릿 씨는 방 안을 오갔다. 처음엔 빠르게, 그러다가 점점 느리게. 한번은 창가로 가서 이마를 유리에 대고 서 있었다. 그러다 별안간 그가 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"또 무슨 일로 온 건가, 이 사람아?\"\n\n\"세상에 다른 일은 전혀 없습니다, 나리. 오 저런! 그저, 나리, 나리께서 안녕하시기를 바란다는 말씀을 드리려고, 그리고 에이미 양께서 잘 지내시는지 여쭈려고요.\"\n\n\"그게 자네와 무슨 상관인가?\" 도릿 씨가 쏘아붙였다.\n\n\"마땅히 따지면 저와는 아무 상관 없지요, 나리. 우리 사이의 거리를 좁히려는 생각은 결코 한 적 없습니다, 정말로요. 주제넘은 일인 줄 압니다, 나리, 하지만 나리께서 이를 언짢게 여기시리라고는 생각도 못 했습니다. 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 나리,\" 영존이 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"저는 비록 보잘것없는 처지라도, 만일 그런 줄 알았다면 너무 자존심이 강해서 오지 않았을 겁니다, 정말입니다.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 부끄러웠다. 그는 다시 창가로 가서 한동안 이마를 유리에 기댔다. 돌아섰을 때 그는 손수건을 손에 들고 있었고, 그것으로 눈물을 닦은 뒤였으며, 지치고 병든 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n\"영존, 자네에게 성급하게 굴어 무척 미안하네, 하지만 — 하 — 어떤 기억들은 행복한 기억이 아니라서, 그리고 — 험 — 자네는 오지 말았어야 했어.\"\n\n\"이제 저도 그렇게 느낍니다, 나리,\" 존 치버리가 대답했다. \"하지만 전엔 몰랐고, 하늘에 맹세코 나쁜 뜻은 없었습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"아니야. 아니지,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"나는 — 험 — 그건 확신하네. 하. 손을 주게, 영존, 손을 줘.\"\n\n영존은 손을 내밀었으나, 도릿 씨가 이미 그 손에서 마음을 몰아낸 뒤였고, 이제 무엇으로도 그 창백하고 충격받은 표정에서 그의 얼굴을 바꿔 놓을 수는 없었다.\n\n\"자!\" 도릿 씨가 천천히 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"다시 앉게, 영존.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리 — 하지만 그냥 서 있는 게 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n대신 도릿 씨가 앉았다. 한동안 괴로이 머리를 받치고 있다가, 그는 손님 쪽으로 머리를 돌리며, 애써 편안한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 자네 부친께선 어떠신가, 영존? 어떤가 — 하 — 다들 어떻게 지내나, 영존?\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 나리, 다들 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 나리. 별로 불평할 것 없이요.\"\n\n\"흠. 자네는 — 하 — 옛날 그 일을 하고 있나 보군, 존?\" 도릿 씨가 자신이 저주했던 그 거슬리는 다발을 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n\"일부는요, 나리. 저는 또한 제—\" 존은 조금 망설였다. \"아버지의 일도 하고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"오 그렇군!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"자네는 — 하 험 — 그 — 하 —\"\n\n\"문지기 일 말씀이십니까, 나리? 네, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"할 일이 많은가, 존?\"\n\"네, 나리. 요즘은 꽤 바쁩니다. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 저희는 대체로 꽤 바쁩니다.\"\n\n\"이맘때라서 그런가, 영존?\"\n\n\"거의 일 년 내내 그렇습니다, 나리. 저희한테 별 차이를 만드는 철이라는 게 있는 줄 모르겠어요. 그럼 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 있게, 존 — 하 — 잠깐만. 흠. 시가는 두고 가게, 존, 내가 — 하 — 부탁하네.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 존은 떨리는 손으로 그것을 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n\"잠깐만 있게, 영존. 잠시만 더. 자네 같은 믿음직한 전령을 통해 약간의 — 험 — 성의를 보내는 것이 내게는 — 하 — 기쁨이 되겠네. 그들의 — 하 험 — 그들에게 — 그들에게 — 형편에 따라 나누어 주도록 말이야. 그걸 가져가는 게 싫지는 않겠지, 존?\"\n\n\"전혀 싫지 않습니다, 나리. 그것으로 형편이 나아질 사람이 그곳에 많을 겁니다, 정말로요.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 존. 내가 — 하 — 그것을 적어 주겠네, 존.\"\n\n그의 손이 어찌나 떨렸던지 그것을 쓰는 데 한참이 걸렸고, 결국 떨리는 휘갈김으로 써냈다. 그것은 백 파운드짜리 수표였다. 그는 그것을 접어 영존의 손에 쥐여 주고, 자기 손으로 그 손을 꼭 눌렀다.\n\n\"지난 일은 — 하 — 부디 — 험 — 너그러이 보아 주기를 바라네, 존.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 나리, 절대로요. 저는 결코 앙심 따위 품지 않습니다, 정말입니다.\"\n\n그러나 존이 거기 있는 동안에는 어떤 것으로도 존의 얼굴을 본래의 혈색과 표정으로 되돌리거나, 존의 본래 태도를 회복시킬 수 없었다.\n\n\"그리고, 존,\" 도릿 씨가 그의 손을 마지막으로 한번 꼭 누른 뒤 놓아주며 말했다. \"우리가 — 하 — 비밀로 이야기를 나누었다는 데 의견이 일치하길 바라네. 그리고 자네가 나가는 길에, 한때 내가 — 하 — 그러니까 — 그런 것을 — 험 — 누구에게든 암시할 만한 말은 일절 삼가 주기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"오! 안심하십시오, 나리,\" 존 치버리가 대답했다. \"제 비천하고 보잘것없는 처지에서도, 나리, 저는 그런 짓을 하기엔 너무 자존심이 세고 명예를 압니다, 나리.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 존이 정말로 곧장 나가는지, 아니면 누군가와 이야기를 나누려고 머뭇거리는지를 직접 확인하려고 문에 귀를 대고 엿듣는 일에 대해서만큼은 너무 자존심이 세거나 명예를 따지지 않았다. 존이 곧장 문으로 나가, 빠른 걸음으로 거리를 따라 사라진 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 한 시간 동안 홀로 있은 뒤, 도릿 씨는 안내원을 불렀는데, 안내원은 그가 의자를 벽난로 깔개 위에 놓고 등을 자기 쪽으로, 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 둔 채 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다. \"원한다면 저 시가 다발을 여행 중에 피우게 가져가게,\" 도릿 씨가 무심히 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"하 — 내 옛 — 험 — 소작인의 아들이 — 하 — 가져온 작은 선물이라네.\"\n\n이튿날 아침 해는 도버 가도 위의 도릿 씨의 마차를 비추었는데, 그곳에서는 붉은 윗도리를 입은 역마차 마부 하나하나가, 여행자를 무자비하게 약탈하기 위해 세워진 잔혹한 집의 표시였다. 런던과 도버 사이 인류의 온갖 일거리란 곧 강탈이어서, 도릿 씨는 다트퍼드에서 매복을 당하고, 그레이브젠드에서 노략질당하고, 로체스터에서 털리고, 시팅본에서 등쳐 먹히고, 캔터베리에서 약탈당했다. 그러나 그를 그 도적 떼의 손아귀에서 빼내는 것이 안내원의 임무였으므로, 안내원은 매 역참마다 그를 구해 냈다. 그리하여 붉은 윗도리들은 봄날의 풍경을 따라 즐겁게 번뜩이며, 일정한 박자로 오르락내리락하면서, 아늑한 구석에 앉은 도릿 씨와, 먼지 자욱한 큰길의 다음번 백악질 언덕 사이를 달려갔다.\n\n또 하루의 해는 칼레에 있는 그를 비추었다. 이제 자신과 존 치버리 사이에 해협을 두게 되자, 그는 안전하다고 느끼기 시작했고, 이국의 공기가 영국의 공기보다 숨쉬기에 한결 가볍다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다.\n\n다시 파리를 향해 험한 프랑스 길을 달려갔다. 이제 평정을 완전히 되찾은 도릿 씨는 아늑한 구석에 앉아, 마차를 타고 가는 동안 공중누각을 짓기 시작했다. 그가 매우 큰 누각을 짓고 있다는 것이 분명했다. 온종일 그는 탑을 세웠다가 허물고, 여기에 별채를 더하고, 저기에 흉벽을 얹고, 성벽을 살피고, 방어 시설을 보강하고, 실내에 장식적인 손질을 가하면서, 모든 면에서 그것을 굉장한 누각으로 만들어 갔다. 그의 골몰한 얼굴은 그가 무슨 일에 몰두해 있는지를 어찌나 분명히 드러냈던지, 역참마다 눈멀지 않은 불구자라면 누구나, 하늘의 이름으로, 성모의 이름으로, 모든 성인의 이름으로 자선을 구하며 찌그러진 작은 양철통을 마차 창 안으로 들이밀던 그 누구나, 그가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지를 그들의 동포 르 브룅 자신이 알았을 만큼 잘 알아챘다 — 비록 르 브룅이 그 영국인 여행자를 특별한 관상학 논문의 주제로 삼았더라도 말이다.\n\n파리에 도착하여 사흘을 머무는 동안, 도릿 씨는 홀로 거리를 많이 거닐며 상점 진열창을, 특히 보석상의 진열창을 들여다보았다. 마침내 그는 가장 유명한 보석상에 들어가, 한 숙녀에게 줄 작은 선물을 사고 싶다고 말했다.\n\n그가 그렇게 말한 상대는 매력적인 작은 여인이었다 — 더없이 세련되게 차려입은 발랄한 작은 여인으로, 그녀는 그를 응대하기 위해 초록색 벨벳 정자에서 나왔는데, 그곳에서 그녀는 그 자체가 마치 사탕과자처럼 보이는 앙증맞고 반짝이는 작은 책상에 앉아, 입맞춤보다 더 상업적인 어떤 품목을 기입하기 위해 줄을 친 것이라곤 도무지 상상하기 어려운 앙증맞은 작은 장부를 적고 있던 참이었다.\n\n자, 예를 들어, 작은 여인이 말했다, 무슈께서는 어떤 종류의 선물을 원하시는지요? 사랑의 선물인가요?\n\n도릿 씨는 미소 지으며 말했다. 에, 글쎄요! 아마도요. 누가 알겠소? 늘 가능한 일이지요. 여인이라는 존재가 그토록 매력적이니. 몇 가지 좀 보여 주겠소?\n\n기꺼이요, 작은 여인이 말했다. 여러 가지를 보여 드리게 되어 영광이고 황홀합니다. 하지만 죄송합니다! 우선, 사랑의 선물이 있고 혼인의 선물이 있다는 것을 부디 유념해 주시겠어요. 예를 들어, 이 황홀한 귀걸이와 그에 어울리는 이 더없이 멋진 목걸이는 사랑의 선물이라 부르는 것입니다. 이 브로치들과 이 반지들은, 그토록 우아하고 천상적인 아름다움을 지닌 것으로, 무슈의 허락을 얻어 말씀드리자면, 혼인의 선물이라 부르는 것이지요.\n\n어쩌면 좋은 방법은, 도릿 씨가 미소 지으며 넌지시 말했다, 둘 다 사서, 사랑의 선물을 먼저 주고, 혼인의 예물로 마무리하는 것이겠군요?\n\n아 저런! 작은 여인이 두 작은 손의 손끝을 마주 대며 말했다, 그것이야말로 참으로 후하신 일이고, 각별한 멋이겠습니다! 그토록 선물 세례를 받은 숙녀라면 틀림없이 그것을 거역하지 못할 것입니다.\n\n도릿 씨는 그 점이 확실치는 않았다. 하지만, 예를 들어, 그 발랄한 작은 여인은 그것을 매우 확신한다고 말했다. 그리하여 도릿 씨는 각 종류의 선물을 하나씩 사고, 그 값을 후하게 치렀다. 나중에 호텔로 걸어 돌아갈 때, 그는 고개를 높이 쳐들고 있었다. 이제 그의 누각을 노트르담의 두 네모난 탑보다 훨씬 더 높은 곳까지 끌어올린 것이 분명했다.\n\n온 힘을 다해 짓되, 자기 누각의 설계도는 오직 자기 눈에만 간직한 채, 도릿 씨는 마르세유를 향해 마차를 몰아갔다. 짓고 또 짓고, 바삐, 바삐, 아침부터 밤까지. 잠이 들어 거대한 건축 자재 덩어리들을 허공에 매달아 둔 채로 두었다가, 다시 깨어나 작업을 이어 그것들을 제자리에 앉히고. 그동안 마차 뒷자리의 안내원은 영존의 가장 좋은 시가를 피우며 가느다란 한 줄기 옅은 연기를 뒤에 남겼는데 — 어쩌면 그 역시 도릿 씨의 흩어진 돈 몇 푼으로 누각을 한두 채 짓고 있었는지도 모른다.\n\n그들이 온 여정에서 지나친 어떤 요새 도시도 도릿 씨의 누각만큼 견고하지 않았고, 어떤 대성당 첨탑도 그만큼 높지 않았다. 손강도 론강도 그 비할 데 없는 건축물의 빠르기로 흐르지 못했고, 지중해도 그 토대보다 깊지 않았으며, 코르니체 가도에서 보이는 먼 풍경도, 장엄한 제노바의 언덕과 만도 그보다 더 아름답지 않았다. 도릿 씨와 그의 비할 데 없는 누각은 치비타베키아의 더러운 흰 집들과 더 더러운 죄인들 사이에 내려졌고, 거기서부터 길에 곪아 있는 오물을 헤치며 할 수 있는 대로 로마까지 기어가듯 나아갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air\n\n해가 진 지 족히 네 시간은 지났고, 도릿 씨의 마차가 여전히 마지막 고단한 여정을 달리며 적막한 캄파냐 위를 덜컹덜컹 가로지를 무렵은, 어지간한 여행자라면 로마 성벽 바깥에 있고 싶지 않을 만큼 늦은 시각이었다. 빛이 남아 있는 동안 길을 군데군데 채우던 사나운 목동들과 험상궂은 농부들은 모두 해와 함께 자취를 감추었고, 황야를 텅 비워 두었다. 길이 굽이지는 어느 모퉁이들에서는, 폐허가 흩뿌려진 땅에서 피어오르는 김처럼 지평선 위에 희미한 불빛이 어른거려 도시가 아직 멀었음을 알려 주었다. 그러나 이 가련한 위안은 드물고도 덧없었다. 마차는 다시 검고 메마른 바다의 우묵한 곳으로 내려갔고, 오랫동안 보이는 것이라곤 돌처럼 굳어 버린 그 너울과 음울한 하늘뿐이었다.\n\n도릿 씨는 마음을 붙들어 둘 공중누각 짓기가 있었음에도, 그 황량한 곳에서 영 마음을 놓을 수가 없었다. 그는 런던을 떠난 이래 그 어느 때보다도, 마차가 흔들릴 때마다, 마부들이 소리칠 때마다 훨씬 더 신경을 곤두세웠다. 마부석에 앉은 시종은 분명 벌벌 떨고 있었다. 뒷자리에 앉은 안내인도 마음이 썩 편치 않았다. 도릿 씨가 유리창을 내리고 그를 돌아볼 때마다(그는 매우 자주 그랬다), 안내인이 존 치버리식 담배 연기를 뿜어내고 있는 것은 사실이었으나, 그러면서도 대개는 그러는 내내 일어서서 주위를 두리번거리는 것이, 무언가 의심을 품고 경계를 늦추지 않는 사람 같았다. 그러면 도릿 씨는 다시 유리창을 올리며, 저 마부들이 살인자처럼 험악하게 생긴 놈들이라는 것, 그리고 차라리 치비타베키아에서 자고 아침 일찍 출발하는 편이 나았으리라는 것을 곱씹곤 했다. 그러면서도 그는 그 사이사이에 자신의 누각을 짓는 일에 매달렸다.\n\n이제 허물어진 울타리의 잔해, 입을 떡 벌린 창문 구멍과 금이 간 담, 버려진 집들, 물이 새는 우물들, 부서진 물탱크들, 유령 같은 사이프러스 나무들, 뒤엉킨 포도 덩굴 무더기들, 그리고 보기 흉한 건물들에서부터 덜컹거리는 길에 이르기까지 모든 것이 허물어져 가는, 길고 불규칙하고 어수선한 골목길로 바뀌어 가는 것 — 이제 이런 것들이 그들이 로마에 가까워지고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그러더니 마차가 별안간 휙 돌며 멈춰 서자, 도릿 씨는 도적이 자기를 도랑에 처박고 털어 갈 순간이 왔다는 불안에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 다시 유리창을 내리고 내다보니, 자기를 덮친 것은 다름 아닌 장례 행렬일 뿐이었다. 더러운 제의(祭衣)와 음산한 횃불과 흔들리는 향로가 어렴풋이 보이고, 사제 앞에는 커다란 십자가가 들려 있는 그 행렬은 기계적으로 성가를 읊조리며 지나갔다. 횃불에 비친 그 사제는 못생긴 사제였다. 침울한 인상에 이마가 툭 튀어나와 있었다. 마차에서 모자도 벗은 채 내다보던 도릿 씨와 그의 눈이 마주쳤을 때, 성가를 읊느라 움직이는 그의 입술은 이 중요한 여행자를 위협하는 듯했고, 사실은 여행자의 인사에 답하는 동작이었던 그의 손짓도 그 위협에 가세하는 것만 같았다. 누각을 짓고 여행을 하느라 지쳐 공상에 빠진 도릿 씨는 그렇게 생각했고, 그러는 사이 사제는 그의 곁을 떠밀려 가듯 지나갔으며, 행렬은 죽은 자를 데리고 흩어지듯 멀어져 갔다. 도릿 씨 일행 역시 그토록 다른 자신들의 길을 갔다. 그리하여 곧, 유럽의 두 위대한 수도에서 사들인 사치품을 가득 실은 마차와 더불어, 그들은 (거꾸로 된 고트족처럼) 로마의 성문을 두드리고 있었다.\n\n그날 밤 도릿 씨의 집안 사람들은 그를 기다리고 있지 않았다. 기다리긴 했으나, 그가 그런 지역에서 이렇게 늦은 시각까지 바깥에 있고 싶어 할 리 없다고 여겨 내일까지는 오지 않으리라 단념해 둔 터였다. 그래서 그의 마차가 제 집 대문 앞에 섰을 때, 그를 맞으러 나온 것은 문지기뿐이었다. 도릿 양은 외출했느냐고 그가 물었다. 아닙니다. 안에 계십니다. 좋다, 하고 도릿 씨는 모여드는 하인들에게 말했다. 다들 있던 자리에 있거라. 마차에서 짐 부리는 것이나 거들어라. 도릿 양은 내가 직접 찾겠다.\n\n그리하여 그는 자신의 으리으리한 계단을 천천히, 지친 걸음으로 올라갔고, 여러 빈 방들을 들여다보다가, 마침내 어느 작은 대기실에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다. 그곳은 다른 두 방 사이에 텐트처럼 커튼이 드리워진 아늑한 구석방이었는데, 그 방들이 만들어 내는 어두운 통로를 지나 다가갈수록 따뜻하고 환한 빛으로 보였다.\n\n휘장이 드리운 문간은 있었으나 문은 없었다. 그가 그곳에 멈춰 서서 들키지 않은 채 안을 들여다보다가, 그는 가슴이 아릿함을 느꼈다. 설마 질투 같은 것은 아니겠지? 어째서 질투 같은 것이겠는가? 그곳에는 그의 딸과 그의 아우뿐이었다. 아우는 의자를 화롯가로 끌어당겨 저녁의 장작불 온기를 즐기고 있었고, 딸은 작은 탁자 앞에 앉아 무슨 자수 일에 골몰해 있었다. 그림 속 정물(靜物)의 큰 차이를 감안한다면, 인물들은 옛날과 거의 같았다. 그의 아우는 한순간 그 구도 속에서 그 자신을 대신할 만큼 그와 충분히 닮아 있었다. 그렇게 그는 저 멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 석탄불 앞에 앉아 숱한 밤을 보냈었다. 그렇게 딸은 그에게 헌신하며 앉아 있었었다. 그러나 분명 그 옛날의 비참한 가난 속에 질투할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그렇다면 그의 가슴속 아릿함은 어디서 온 것인가?\n\n\"아세요, 작은아버지, 제 생각엔 작은아버지가 다시 젊어지고 계신 것 같아요.\"\n\n작은아버지는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"언제부터, 얘야. 언제부터 말이냐?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 리틀 도릿이 바늘을 놀리며 대답했다. \"몇 주 전부터 작은아버지가 점점 젊어지고 계세요. 그렇게 명랑하시고, 그렇게 선뜻 나서시고, 그렇게 무엇에든 관심을 보이시고요.\"\n\n\"얘야, 그게 다 너 덕분이란다.\"\n\n\"다 저 덕분이라뇨, 작은아버지!\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 너는 내게 더없는 좋은 일을 해 주었어. 나를 그토록 헤아려 주고, 그토록 다정하게 대해 주고, 또 그 마음 씀씀이를 내게 숨기려고 그토록 섬세하게 애써 주어서, 나는 — 글쎄다, 글쎄다, 글쎄다! 다 마음에 간직해 두었단다, 얘야, 간직해 두었어.\"\n\n\"그건 다 작은아버지의 새로운 공상일 뿐이에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄다, 글쎄다, 글쎄다!\" 노인이 중얼거렸다. \"하느님 감사합니다!\"\n\n그녀는 일손을 잠시 멈추고 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈길이 그녀 아버지의 가슴속에 그 앞서의 아픔을 되살렸다. 모순과 동요와 일관성 없음으로, 이 무지한 삶의 자질구레하고 까다로운 당혹감으로, 밤 없는 아침만이 걷어 낼 수 있는 안개로 가득 찬 그 가련하고 약한 가슴속에.\n\n\"있잖니, 얘야, 우리 둘만 남고 나서는 내가 너한테 좀 더 허물없이 굴었지.\" 노인이 말했다. \"둘만 남았다고 한 건, 제너럴 부인은 치지 않으니 하는 말이다. 나는 그 사람한테는 관심이 없어. 그 사람은 나와 상관없는 사람이지. 하지만 패니가 나를 못 견뎌 한 건 안다. 그렇다고 그걸 이상하게 여기거나 탓하지는 않아. 나도 내가 거치적거리는 존재라는 걸 잘 아니까. 되도록 비키려고 애는 쓰지만 말이다. 나는 우리 집안 손님들에게 어울리는 사람이 못 돼. 내 형 윌리엄은,\" 노인이 감탄하듯 말했다. \"임금님들에게도 어울릴 사람이지. 하지만 네 작은아버지는 그렇지 못해, 얘야. 프레더릭 도릿은 윌리엄 도릿의 얼굴에 먹칠이나 하는 존재고, 자기도 그걸 아주 잘 안단다. 아! 아니, 얘야 에이미, 네 아버지가 오셨구나! 사랑하는 형님 윌리엄, 돌아온 걸 환영하오! 내 사랑하는 형님, 형님을 뵈니 기쁘기 그지없소!\"\n\n(말하면서 고개를 돌린 그는 문간에 서 있는 형을 보았던 것이다.)\n\n리틀 도릿은 기쁨의 탄성을 지르며 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추었다. 아버지는 조금 짜증스럽고 조금 투덜대는 기색이었다. \"마침내 너를 찾아서 기쁘구나, 에이미.\" 그가 말했다. \"하. 정말이지 마침내 — 흠 — 나를 맞아 줄 누군가를 찾게 되어 기쁘다. 아무래도 나는 — 하 — 어찌나 안 기다려졌던지, 정말이지 나는 — 하 흠 — 애초에 돌아오는 — 하 — 결례를 무릅쓴 데 대해 사과라도 드려야 하지 않을까 생각이 들기 시작하더구나.\"\n\n\"너무 늦으셔서요, 사랑하는 형님.\" 아우가 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 안 오시려니 단념하고 있었답니다.\"\n\n\"나는 너보다 튼튼해, 프레더릭.\" 형이 형제애를 한껏 강조하면서도 그 안에 준엄함이 서린 말투로 대꾸했다. \"그리고 나는 — 하 — 내가 택하는 어느 시각에든 탈 없이 다닐 수 있기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 물론.\" 상대가, 자기가 비위를 거슬렀나 하는 불안에 사로잡혀 대꾸했다. \"물론입니다, 윌리엄.\"\n\n\"고맙다, 에이미.\" 도릿 씨는 딸이 외투를 벗는 것을 거들자 말을 이었다. \"혼자서도 할 수 있어. 나는 — 하 — 너를 번거롭게 할 필요가 없다, 에이미. 빵 한 조각하고 포도주 한 잔 좀 들 수 있겠느냐, 아니면 — 흠 — 너무 폐가 되겠느냐?\"\n\n\"아버지, 곧 저녁 식사를 차려 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"고맙구나, 얘야.\" 도릿 씨는 책망하는 듯한 서늘한 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"나는 — 하 — 폐를 끼치는 게 아닌가 싶구나. 흠. 제너럴 부인은 잘 지내느냐?\"\n\n\"제너럴 부인은 두통이 있다고, 또 피곤하다고 하셔서요. 그래서 아버지가 안 오시려니 단념했을 때 잠자리에 드셨어요.\"\n\n도릿 씨는 어쩌면 제너럴 부인이 자기가 도착하지 못한 실망감에 그렇게 압도된 것이 잘한 일이라고 생각했는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 그의 얼굴은 누그러졌고, 그는 역력히 흡족한 기색으로 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 편찮으시다니 참으로 안됐구나.\"\n\n이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안, 그의 딸은 여느 때보다 더한 관심을 가지고 그를 살펴보고 있었다. 그녀의 눈에는 그가 어딘가 달라졌거나 수척해 보이는 듯했고, 그는 그것을 알아채고는 못마땅해했다. 여행용 망토를 벗고 화롯가로 다가왔을 때, 그가 새삼 짜증스럽게 말했기 때문이다.\n\n\"에이미, 무얼 그리 보느냐? 내게서 무얼 보았기에 그렇게 — 하 — 그렇게 — 흠 — 유난스러운 식으로 내게 걱정을 쏟는 게냐?\"\n\n\"몰랐어요, 아버지. 죄송해요. 아버지를 다시 뵈니 눈이 즐거워서 그래요. 그뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그뿐이라고 하지 마라, 왜냐하면 — 하 — 그뿐이 아니니까. 너는 — 흠 — 너는,\" 도릿 씨가 따지듯 힘주어 말했다. \"내 안색이 안 좋다고 생각하는 거지.\"\n\n\"좀 피곤해 보이셔서요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 네가 잘못 본 거다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"하, 나는 피곤하지 않아. 하, 흠. 나는 떠날 때보다 훨씬 더 생기가 넘친다.\"\n\n그가 어찌나 화를 내려는 기색인지, 그녀는 더 이상 변명하지 않고 그저 그의 곁에 조용히 서서 그의 팔을 끌어안았다. 그가 그렇게 한쪽에는 아우를 두고 서 있는 동안, 그는 일 분도 채 안 되는 묵직한 졸음에 빠졌다가 흠칫 깨어났다.\n\n\"프레더릭,\" 그가 아우를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"당장 잠자리에 들기를 권하네.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 윌리엄. 형님 저녁 드시는 걸 보고 기다릴게요.\"\n\n\"프레더릭,\" 그가 받아쳤다. \"잠자리에 들기를 청하네. 나는 — 하 — 자네가 잠자리에 들기를 개인적인 부탁으로 하는 걸세. 자네는 진작 잠자리에 들었어야 해. 자네는 아주 쇠약해.\"\n\n\"하!\" 그를 기쁘게 해 주는 것밖에 바라지 않는 노인이 말했다. \"글쎄, 글쎄, 글쎄! 그런가 보군.\"\n\n\"내 사랑하는 프레더릭.\" 도릿 씨는 아우의 쇠해 가는 기력에 견주어 놀랄 만큼 우월한 태도로 대꾸했다. \"그건 의심할 여지가 없어. 자네가 그렇게 약해진 걸 보니 내가 다 괴롭네. 하. 마음이 아파. 흠. 안색이 영 안 좋아 보여. 자네는 이런 일을 감당할 몸이 아냐. 더 조심해야 하네, 아주 조심해야 해.\"\n\n\"잠자리에 들까요?\" 프레더릭이 물었다.\n\n\"사랑하는 프레더릭,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그러게, 간청하네! 잘 자게, 아우님. 내일은 좀 더 튼튼해지기를 바라네. 자네 안색이 영 마음에 들지 않아. 잘 자게, 사랑하는 사람아.\" 이렇게 정중한 방식으로 아우를 물린 뒤, 그는 노인이 미처 방을 다 나서기도 전에 다시 졸음에 빠져들었다. 딸이 붙들어 주지 않았더라면 그대로 장작 더미 위로 고꾸라질 뻔했다.\n\n\"네 작은아버지는 정신이 무척 오락가락하는구나, 에이미.\" 그렇게 깨어났을 때 그가 말했다. \"예전보다 말이 — 하 — 조리가 없고, 이야기가 더 — 흠 — 끊어진다, 내가 — 하, 흠 — 여태껏 본 것보다 말이다. 내가 떠나 있는 동안 무슨 병이라도 앓았느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"너는 — 하 — 그가 많이 변했다고 보느냐, 에이미?\"\n\n\"저는 못 느꼈어요, 아버지.\"\n\n\"많이 쇠했어.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"많이 쇠했다. 가엾고 다정한, 기력이 다해 가는 내 프레더릭! 하. 예전 모습을 감안하더라도, 그는 — 흠 — 안타깝게도 많이 쇠했어!\"\n\n그곳으로 가져와 그가 딸이 일하던 모습을 보았던 그 작은 탁자 위에 차려진 저녁 식사가 그의 주의를 돌려놓았다. 그녀는 지나간 날들에서처럼 그의 곁에 앉았는데, 그 날들이 끝난 이래 처음 있는 일이었다. 그들은 단둘이었고, 그녀는 감옥에서 늘 그러던 대로 그에게 고기를 덜어 주고 마실 것을 따라 주었다. 이 모든 일이, 그들이 부유해진 이래 처음으로 지금 일어나고 있었다. 그가 화를 낸 뒤라 그녀는 그를 자주 바라보기가 두려웠다. 그러나 그녀는 식사하는 동안 두 번, 그가 별안간 그녀를 보고는 주위를 둘러보는 것을 알아챘는데, 마치 그 연상이 너무도 강렬해 자기들이 옛 감옥 방에 있지 않다는 것을 눈으로 직접 확인할 필요가 있다는 듯했다. 두 번 다 그는 손을 머리로 가져갔는데, 마치 옛날의 검은 모자를 찾는 것 같았다 — 그 모자는 마셜시에서 수치스럽게 남에게 넘어간 뒤 그 시각까지도 풀려나지 못한 채, 여전히 후임자의 머리 위에서 그 마당들을 맴돌고 있었건만.\n\n그는 저녁을 아주 조금밖에 들지 않았으나 오래도록 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 자주 아우의 쇠약해진 상태로 화제를 돌렸다. 그는 아우를 더없이 가엾어하는 듯하면서도 거의 신랄하게 굴었다. 그는 가엾은 프레더릭이 — 하 흠 — 노망이 들었다고 했다. 달리 표현할 말이 없다는 것이었다. 노망이라고. 가엾은 사람! 만일 제너럴 부인이라는 위안이 없었더라면, 에이미가 그토록 지루하기 짝이 없는 그의 말동무 노릇을 하느라 — 정신을 놓고 횡설수설하며, 가엾고 사랑스럽고 존경할 만한 사람이지만, 정신을 놓고 횡설수설하며 — 얼마나 시달렸을지 생각하면 우울하다고 했다. 그러더니 그는 그 — 하 — 빼어난 여성이 편찮으시다니 참으로 안됐다고, 앞서의 흡족한 기색으로 되풀이했다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 그 살뜰한 사랑으로, 그날 밤을 나중에 돌이켜 볼 까닭이 없었더라도, 그가 그날 밤 한 가장 사소한 말이나 행동까지 기억했을 것이다. 그녀는 늘 기억했다. 그가 옛 연상의 강한 영향 아래 주위를 둘러볼 때면, 그것을 그녀의 마음에서, 어쩌면 자기 자신의 마음에서도 몰아내려고, 곧바로 자기가 떠나 있는 동안 자기를 에워쌌던 대단한 부와 대단한 사람들에 대해, 또 그와 그의 가족이 지탱해야 할 드높은 지위에 대해 장황하게 늘어놓았던 것을. 그녀는 또한 잊지 않고 기억했다. 그의 모든 말과 모든 태도에는 두 가지 저류(底流)가 나란히 흐르고 있었다는 것을. 하나는 그가 그녀 없이도 얼마나 잘 지냈는지, 또 자기가 그녀에게서 얼마나 독립해 있는지를 그녀에게 보여 주는 것이었고, 다른 하나는 변덕스럽고 알 수 없는 방식으로, 마치 그가 떠나 있는 동안 그녀가 자기를 소홀히 했을 리라도 있다는 듯이 그녀를 거의 원망하는 것이었다.\n\n머들 씨가 누리는 그 영화로운 형편에 대해, 또 그 앞에 머리를 조아리는 사람들에 대해 그녀에게 이야기하다 보니, 그는 자연스럽게 머들 부인에게로 넘어갔다. 어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 그의 이야기 대부분이 유달리 앞뒤가 맞지 않았음에도 그는 단번에 그녀에게로 넘어가 그녀가 어떻게 지내는지 물었다.\n\n\"아주 잘 지내세요. 다음 주에 떠나신대요.\"\n\n\"본국으로?\" 도릿 씨가 물었다.\n\n\"도중에 몇 주 머물고 나서요.\"\n\n\"여기로서는 큰 손실이겠구나.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"본국으로서는 큰 — 하 — 소득이겠고. 패니에게도, 그리고 — 흠 — 그 밖의 — 하 — 상류 사회에도 말이다.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 앞으로 벌어질 경쟁을 떠올리며 아주 나직이 수긍했다.\n\n\"머들 부인이 성대한 송별 모임을 여신대요, 아버지. 그 전에 만찬도 있고요. 아버지가 제때 돌아오셔야 한다고 노심초사하셨어요. 만찬에 아버지와 저를 둘 다 초대하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그분은 — 하 — 참 친절하구나. 그날이 언제냐?\"\n\n\"모레예요.\"\n\n\"아침에 회답을 보내, 내가 돌아왔고 — 흠 — 기쁘게 가겠다고 전해라.\"\n\n\"방까지 모셔다 드릴까요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"아니!\" 그가 화난 듯 주위를 돌아보며 대답했다. 작별 인사도 잊은 듯 자리를 뜨려던 참이었다. \"그럴 것 없다, 에이미. 도움 따위 필요 없어. 나는 네 아비지, 병약한 네 작은아비가 아냐!\" 그는 이렇게 불쑥 받아치던 말을 그만큼 불쑥 멈추고는 말했다. \"에이미, 네가 내게 입을 안 맞췄구나. 잘 자거라, 얘야! 이제 우리는 — 하 — 이제 너를 시집보내야겠다.\" 그러고서 그는 한층 느리고 한층 지친 걸음으로 계단을 올라 자기 방으로 갔고, 거기 들어서자마자 시종을 물렸다. 다음으로 그가 한 일은 파리에서 사 온 물건들을 둘러보는 것이었는데, 상자들을 열어 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤 자물쇠를 채워 치워 두었다. 그러고 나서는 졸기도 하고 누각을 짓기도 하며 오래도록 자신을 잊고 있었으니, 그가 살금살금 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 적막한 캄파냐의 동쪽 가장자리에 아침의 기미가 어려 있었다.\n\n이튿날 제너럴 부인은 때맞춰 안부를 올려, 그 고된 여정을 마치고 잘 쉬셨기를 바란다고 전했다. 그는 안부를 내려보내며, 제너럴 부인에게 자기는 정말이지 아주 잘 쉬었으며 더없이 좋은 상태라고 알려 드린다고 했다. 그럼에도 그는 늦은 오후가 되어서야 자기 방에서 나왔다. 그러고 나서 제너럴 부인과 딸을 데리고 마차를 타러 나가기 위해 자신을 으리으리하게 차려입게 했음에도, 그의 행색은 스스로 묘사한 바에는 좀처럼 미치지 못했다.\n\n그날은 집안에 손님이 없었으므로 네 식구가 단출하게 함께 식사했다. 그는 자기 오른편 자리로 제너럴 부인을 더없이 격식을 차려 이끌었다. 리틀 도릿은 작은아버지와 함께 뒤따르며 알아채지 않을 수 없었으니, 아버지가 다시금 공들여 차려입었다는 것, 그리고 제너럴 부인을 대하는 태도가 무척 각별하다는 것이었다. 그 교양 있는 부인의 완벽하게 다듬어진 겉면은 그 점잖은 광택에서 티끌 하나조차 떼어 내기 어렵게 만들었으나, 리틀 도릿은 그 서릿발 같은 눈 한구석에서 의기양양함이 살짝 녹아내리는 기미를 본 것 같았다.\n\n이 지면에서 자두와 프리즘식이라 부를 만한 그 가족 만찬의 성격에도 불구하고, 도릿 씨는 식사가 진행되는 동안 여러 번 잠이 들었다. 졸음 발작은 간밤처럼 불쑥 찾아왔고, 그만큼 짧고 깊었다. 처음으로 이 졸음이 그를 덮쳤을 때 제너럴 부인은 거의 어안이 벙벙한 얼굴이었다. 그러나 그 증상이 되풀이될 때마다 그녀는 자신의 정중한 묵주를 굴렸으니, 파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬스, 프리즘이었다. 그리고 그 어김없는 의식을 아주 천천히 끝까지 외우는 솜씨로, 도릿 씨가 잠에서 깨어나는 것과 거의 같은 때에 묵주 기도를 마치는 듯했다.\n\n그는 또다시 프레더릭에게서 졸음 기운이 있다는 것을(그것은 그 자신의 상상 속에만 존재하는 것이었다) 괴롭게 의식했고, 식사 후 프레더릭이 물러가자 제너럴 부인에게 그 가엾은 사람에 대해 은밀히 양해를 구했다. \"더없이 존경스럽고 다정한 아우입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 — 하, 흠 — 완전히 무너져 버렸지요. 안타깝게도 빠르게 쇠해 가고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"프레더릭 씨는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"늘 정신이 딴 데 가 계시고 기운이 없으시지요. 하지만 그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않기를 바랍시다.\"\n\n그러나 도릿 씨는 그를 그냥 넘길 작정이 아니었다. \"빠르게 쇠해 가고 있습니다, 부인. 폐선(廢船)이지요. 폐허입니다. 우리 눈앞에서 허물어져 가고 있어요. 흠. 착한 프레더릭!\"\n\n\"스파클러 부인은 아주 잘 지내고 행복하게 두고 오셨겠지요?\" 제너럴 부인이 프레더릭을 위해 차가운 한숨을 한 차례 내쉰 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"둘러싸여 있지요.\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다. \"취향을 매혹할 — 하 — 모든 것에, 그리고 — 흠 — 마음을 드높일 모든 것에 둘러싸여서요. 행복합니다, 친애하는 부인, — 흠 — 남편을 두고서요.\"\n\n제너럴 부인은 조금 동요했다. 마치 그 말이 어디로 이어질지 알 수 없다는 듯, 자기 장갑으로 그 단어를 점잖게 밀어내려는 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"패니는요,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다. \"패니는, 제너럴 부인, 빼어난 자질을 지녔습니다. 하. 야심 — 흠 — 목적의식, — 하 — 지위에 대한 자각, 그 지위를 지키려는 결의 — 하, 흠 — 우아함과 미모와 타고난 고귀함을요.\"\n\n\"의심할 바 없지요.\" 제너럴 부인이 (다소 한층 뻣뻣하게) 말했다.\n\n\"이런 자질들과 더불어, 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"패니는 — 하 — 한 가지 흠을 드러내어 나를 — 흠 — 나를 불편하게, 그리고 — 하 — 덧붙이건대, 화나게 만들었습니다. 그러나 이제는 그것이 끝났다고 여겨도 좋으리라 믿습니다, 그 애 자신에 대해서까지도요. 그리고 — 하 — 다른 사람들에 대해서는 의심할 바 없이 끝났습니다.\"\n\n\"무엇을 두고, 도릿 씨,\" 제너럴 부인이 또다시 다소 들뜬 장갑을 매만지며 대꾸했다. \"말씀하시는 건지요? 저는 도무지 — \"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 친애하는 부인.\" 도릿 씨가 가로막았다.\n\n제너럴 부인의 목소리는 잦아들며 \"도무지 짐작이 안 갑니다.\"라는 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n그 뒤 도릿 씨는 일 분쯤 졸음에 사로잡혔다가, 경련하듯 날쌘 몸짓으로 벌떡 깨어났다.\n\n\"내가 말하는 것은, 제너럴 부인, 그 — 하 — 강한 반발심, 또는 — 흠 — 말하자면 — 하 — 패니의 질투심입니다. 그것이 이따금 고개를 들어 — 하 — 내가 지금 더불어 이야기 나누는 영광을 누리고 있는 — 흠 — 그 숙녀분의 — 하 — 자격에 대해 내가 품은 — 흠 — 인식에 맞서곤 했지요.\"\n\n\"도릿 씨는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 대꾸했다. \"언제나 너무도 친절하시고, 언제나 너무도 후하게 평가해 주십니다. 도릿 씨가 제 봉사에 대해 형성하신 호의적인 의견에 도릿 양이 정말로 반감을 품은 것은 아닐까 상상했던 순간들이 있었다면, 저는 바로 그 너무도 높은 의견에서 위안과 보답을 찾았답니다.\"\n\n\"부인의 봉사에 대한 의견이라고요?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그,\" 제너럴 부인이 우아하면서도 인상적인 태도로 되풀이했다. \"제 봉사에 대한 의견 말이지요.\"\n\n\"부인의 봉사에만 대한 것이겠습니까, 친애하는 부인?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"제 짐작에는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 앞서의 인상적인 태도로 받아쳤다. \"제 봉사에만 대한 것이겠지요. 그렇지 않다면, 달리 무엇에,\" 제너럴 부인이 장갑으로 살짝 묻는 듯한 동작을 하며 말했다. \"제가 그것을 돌릴 수 있겠습니까 — \"\n\n\"부인 — 하 — 자신에게요, 제너럴 부인. 하, 흠. 부인 자신과 부인의 미덕에 말입니다.\" 이것이 도릿 씨의 응수였다.\n\n\"도릿 씨께서 용서해 주시겠지요.\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"지금이 이 대화를 이어 갈 때나 장소가 아니라고 제가 말씀드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 양해해 주시겠지요. 도릿 양이 옆방에 있고, 제가 그 이름을 입에 올리는 동안 그분이 제 눈에 보인다는 것을 일깨워 드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 너그러이 봐주시겠지요. 제가 마음이 흔들리고 있다는 것, 그리고 제가 다스렸다고 여겼던 약함이 갑절의 힘으로 되살아나는 순간들이 있음을 알게 되었다고 말씀드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 제가 물러가도록 허락해 주시겠지요.\"\n\n\"흠. 어쩌면 이 — 하 — 흥미로운 대화를 다시 이어 갈 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"다른 때에 말이지요. 다만 그것이, 제가 그러지 않기를 바라는 바이지만 — 흠 — 어떤 식으로든 — 아 — 제너럴 부인께 불쾌한 일이 아니라면요.\"\n\n\"도릿 씨께서는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 몸을 숙여 일어서며 눈을 내리깔고 말했다. \"언제까지나 제 경의와 순종을 받으실 분이십니다.\"\n\n그러고 나서 제너럴 부인은 위엄 있게 자리를 떴는데, 그보다 덜 비범한 여인에게서라면 응당 예상되었을 만큼의 동요는 보이지 않았다. 도릿 씨는 자기 몫의 대화를 어떤 위풍당당하고도 흐뭇한 겸양의 태도로 — 흡사 어떤 사람들이 교회에서 처신하며 예배에서 자기 몫을 수행할 때 보이는 모습으로 — 이끌어 왔던 터라, 대체로 자기 자신에 대해서도 또 제너럴 부인에 대해서도 무척 만족스러워 보였다. 그 부인이 차를 마시러 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 분과 포마드를 약간 발라 단장했고, 또한 정신적 매혹도 없지 않았으니, 그 매혹은 도릿 양을 대하는 더없이 다정스러운 후견인의 태도로, 또 엄격한 예법에 어긋나지 않는 한도 안에서 도릿 씨에게 보이는 다정한 관심의 분위기로 드러났다. 저녁이 끝나 갈 무렵 그녀가 물러가려고 일어섰을 때, 도릿 씨는 마치 그녀를 광장으로 이끌고 나가 달빛 아래 미뉴에트라도 출 듯이 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 더없이 엄숙하게 방문까지 그녀를 안내하여 그곳에서 그녀의 손마디를 입술로 가져갔다. 화장품 향이 나는 다소 뼈가 만져지는 입맞춤이었으리라 짐작되는 것으로 그녀와 작별한 뒤, 그는 우아하게 딸에게 축복을 내렸다. 그리고 이렇게 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 일어나고 있음을 넌지시 비친 뒤, 그는 다시 잠자리에 들었다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그는 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있었다. 그러나 이른 오후에 그는 팅클러 씨를 시켜 제너럴 부인에게 정중한 안부를 내려보내며, 자기 없이 도릿 양과 함께 바람을 쐬러 다녀오시기를 청했다. 그가 모습을 드러내기 전에 그의 딸은 머들 부인의 만찬에 갈 채비를 마쳤다. 이윽고 그가 나타났는데, 차림새로 보면 눈부신 상태였으나, 어딘지 모르게 쪼그라들고 늙어 보였다. 그러나 그녀가 안색이 어떠시냐고 묻기만 해도 화를 낼 작정인 것이 분명했으므로, 그녀는 감히 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추기만 하고, 불안한 마음으로 그와 함께 머들 부인 댁으로 갔다.\n\n가야 할 거리는 무척 짧았으나, 마차가 그 길을 절반도 가기 전에 그는 다시 누각 짓는 일에 빠져 있었다. 머들 부인은 그를 대단히 깍듯하게 맞이했다. 그 가슴은 감탄스러우리만치 잘 보존되어 있었고 더없이 흡족한 상태였다. 만찬은 무척 고급스러웠고 손님들은 무척 엄선되어 있었다.\n\n손님들은 주로 영국인이었는데, 다만 으레 빠지지 않는 프랑스인 백작과 으레 빠지지 않는 이탈리아인 후작이 끼어 있었다. 어떤 자리에서는 으레 발견되며 외양이 거의 변하지 않는, 사교계의 장식용 이정표 같은 이들이었다. 식탁은 길었고 만찬도 길었다. 리틀 도릿은 커다란 검은 구레나룻과 커다란 흰 크라바트에 가려, 한 하인이 그녀의 손에 종잇조각을 쥐어 주며 그것을 곧바로 읽어 달라는 머들 부인의 속삭이는 부탁을 전할 때까지, 아버지를 아예 시야에서 놓치고 있었다. 머들 부인은 그 위에 연필로 이렇게 써 두었다. \"도릿 씨에게 가서 말씀 좀 나눠 주세요. 편찮으신 게 아닌가 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀가 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 그에게 서둘러 가려던 참에, 그가 의자에서 일어나 식탁 위로 몸을 기울이고는, 그녀가 아직 제자리에 있는 줄 알고 그녀를 불렀다.\n\n\"에이미, 에이미, 얘야!\"\n\n그 행동이 어찌나 예사롭지 않은지, 그의 야릇하게 다급한 모습과 야릇하게 다급한 목소리는 말할 것도 없이, 그 즉시 깊은 정적이 내려앉았다.\n\n\"에이미, 얘야.\" 그가 되풀이했다. \"가서 밥이 자물쇠를 보고 있는지 좀 봐 주겠니?\"\n\n그녀는 그의 곁에 와서 그를 만지고 있었으나, 그는 여전히 엉뚱하게도 그녀가 제자리에 있는 줄 알고, 여전히 식탁 위로 몸을 기울인 채 외쳤다. \"에이미, 에이미. 나는 영 내 정신이 아니구나. 하. 내가 왜 이러는지 모르겠다. 나는 밥을 꼭 만나야겠어. 하. 모든 간수들 중에서도 그는 너만큼이나 내 벗이지. 밥이 수위실에 있는지 보고, 내게 와 달라고 청해라.\"\n\n이제 손님들은 모두 경악했고, 다들 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"아버지, 저는 거기 있지 않아요. 저 여기, 아버지 곁에 있어요.\"\n\n\"오! 너 여기 있구나, 에이미! 좋다. 흠. 좋아. 하. 밥을 불러라. 그가 교대해서 자물쇠를 보고 있지 않거든, 뱅엄 부인더러 가서 그를 데려오라고 해라.\"\n\n그녀는 그를 살며시 데려가려 했으나, 그는 버티며 가려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 말하잖니, 얘야.\" 그가 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"나는 밥 없이는 그 좁은 계단을 올라갈 수가 없어. 하. 밥을 부르러 보내라. 흠. 밥을 부르러 보내 — 모든 간수들 중에 으뜸가는 밥을 — 밥을 부르러 보내라!\"\n\n그는 어리둥절하게 주위를 둘러보다가, 자기를 에워싼 수많은 얼굴들을 의식하게 되자, 그들에게 말했다.\n\n\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 여러분을 — 하 — 마셜시에 맞이하는 — 흠 — 소임이 제게 떨어졌습니다! 마셜시에 오신 것을 환영합니다! 공간이 — 하 — 비좁습니다 — 비좁아요 — 산책로가 좀 더 넓었으면 좋으련만요. 하지만 얼마쯤 지나고 나면 — 얼마쯤 지나고 나면, 신사 숙녀 여러분 — 겉보기에 더 넓어지는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다. 그리고 공기는, 이것저것 다 따져 보면, 아주 좋습니다. — 하 — 서리 구릉 너머에서 불어오지요. 서리 구릉 너머에서 불어옵니다. 이곳이 스너거리입니다. 흠. — 하 — 수용소 일원들의 소액 갹출로 운영되지요. 그 대가로 — 더운물 — 공동 취사장 — 그리고 자잘한 살림의 편의를 누립니다. — 하 — 마셜시에 익숙해진 분들은 저를 그곳의 아버지라 부르기를 좋아하십니다. 저는 외지에서 오신 분들로부터 — 하 — 마셜시의 아버지라 칭송받는 데 익숙합니다. 분명, 여러 해의 거주가 그토록 — 하 — 명예로운 칭호에 대한 자격을 세워 줄 수 있다면, 저는 그 — 흠 — 부여된 영예를 받아들여도 좋겠지요. 제 아이입니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분. 제 딸입니다. 여기서 태어났지요!\"\n\n그녀는 그것을 부끄러워하지도, 그를 부끄러워하지도 않았다. 그녀는 창백하고 겁에 질려 있었으나, 그를 달래어 사랑하는 그 자신을 위해 그 자리에서 데리고 나가는 것 말고는 다른 마음이 없었다. 그녀는 그와 의아해하는 얼굴들 사이에 서서, 그의 가슴께로 돌아서 자기 얼굴을 그의 얼굴로 들어 올렸다. 그는 그녀를 왼팔에 꼭 끌어안았고, 사이사이 그와 함께 자리를 떠나자고 다정하게 애원하는 그녀의 나직한 목소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"여기서 태어났습니다.\" 그가 눈물을 흘리며 되풀이했다. \"여기서 자랐지요. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 제 딸입니다. 불운한 아비의 자식이지만, — 하 — 언제나 신사였던 아비의 자식이지요. 가난한 것은 틀림없으나, — 흠 — 자존심이 있습니다. 언제나 자존심이 있지요. — 흠 — 드물지 않은 관습이 되었습니다만, 제 — 하 — 개인적 흠모자들이 — 오로지 개인적 흠모자들이 — 이곳에서의 제 반(半) 공식적인 지위를 인정하고 싶다는 뜻을 기꺼이 표하시며, — 하 — 자그마한 헌사를 바치시는데, 그것은 대개 — 하 — 이곳의 품격을 — 하나의 품격을 — 지탱하려는 제 미천한 노력에 대한 자발적인 답례의 형태를 띱니다 — 제가 결코 처지를 떨어뜨렸다고 여기지 않음을 알아주시기 바랍니다. 하. 처지를 떨어뜨린 것이 아닙니다. 하. 거지가 아니에요. 아닙니다. 저는 그 호칭을 거부합니다! 동시에, 제 너그러운 벗들을 움직이는 그 고운 마음씨에 대해 — 흠 — 그 헌사들이 — 흠 — 더없이 고마운 것임을 인정하기를 주저하는 무례를 범하는 일은 결단코 제게서 멀리 있습니다. 오히려, 그것들은 더없이 고마운 것입니다. 제 이름으로는 아니더라도 제 아이의 이름으로, 저는 그것을 더없이 충실하게 인정하는 바입니다. 동시에 — 하 — 제 개인적 존엄이라 해야 할까요? — 그것은 간직하면서 말입니다. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 모두에게 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\"\n\n이 무렵, 그 가슴께가 겪은 지나친 굴욕감 때문에 손님들 대부분은 다른 방들로 물러간 뒤였다. 그토록 오래 머뭇거리던 몇몇도 나머지를 따라갔고, 리틀 도릿과 그녀의 아버지는 하인들과 그들 둘만 남게 되었다. 그녀에게 가장 사랑스럽고 가장 소중한 그가, 이제 그녀와 함께 가 주겠지, 그렇지 않겠는가? 그는 그녀의 간절한 애원에, 밥 없이는 그 좁은 계단을 결코 올라갈 수 없을 거라고, 밥이 어디 있느냐고, 아무도 밥을 데려오지 않느냐고 대답했다. 밥을 찾는다는 핑계로, 그녀는 이제 저녁 모임을 위해 밀려드는 화사한 손님들의 물결을 거슬러 그를 빼냈고, 마침 손님을 막 내려놓은 마차에 그를 태워, 그를 집으로 데려갔다.\n\n그의 로마 저택의 널따란 계단은 흐려져 가는 그의 눈에는 런던 감옥의 그 좁은 계단으로 오그라들었다. 그리고 그는 그녀와 아우 말고는 아무도 자기를 건드리지 못하게 했다. 그들은 도움 없이 그를 방으로 올려, 침대에 눕혔다. 그리고 그 시각부터 그 가엾고 상처 입은 영혼은, 자기 날개가 부러진 그 장소만을 기억하며, 그 뒤로 더듬어 헤쳐 온 꿈을 지워 버리고, 마셜시 너머의 어떤 것도 알지 못했다. 거리에서 발소리가 들리면, 그는 그것을 마당의 그 지치고 낡은 발소리로 여겼다. 문을 잠그는 시각이 오면, 그는 외지 사람들이 모두 밤을 위해 차단된 줄로 알았다. 문을 여는 시각이 다시 오면, 그는 밥을 너무도 보고 싶어 해서, 사람들은 그 밥이 — 그 무렵엔 죽은 지 여러 해 된, 그 점잖은 간수가 — 감기에 걸렸지만 내일이나 모레, 늦어도 그다음 날에는 나오기를 바라고 있다는 이야기를 짜맞춰야 했다.\n\n그는 손조차 들지 못할 만큼 극도로 쇠약해졌다. 그러나 그는 오랜 습관대로 여전히 아우를 보살폈고, 아우가 자기 침대 곁에 서 있는 것을 보면 하루에도 쉰 번씩 다소 흐뭇한 듯이 말하곤 했다. \"착한 프레더릭, 앉게. 자네는 정말이지 아주 쇠약해.\"\n\n사람들이 제너럴 부인을 그에게 데려와 보았으나, 그는 그녀를 조금도 알아보지 못했다. 그녀가 뱅엄 부인을 밀어내려 하며 술을 즐긴다는 어떤 해로운 의심이 그의 머릿속에 자리 잡았다. 그는 거리낌 없는 말로 그녀를 몰아세웠고, 딸에게 어찌나 다급하게 마셜 댁에 가서 그녀를 내쫓아 달라고 간청하라고 졸랐던지, 그녀는 그 첫 실패 뒤로 다시는 그 앞에 나타나지 못했다.\n\n그가 한 번 \"팁이 바깥에 나갔느냐?\"고 물은 것을 빼면, 곁에 없는 두 자식에 대한 기억은 그에게서 떠나 버린 듯했다. 그러나 그를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 했으면서도 그토록 박하게 보답받은 그 아이는, 한 번도 그의 마음에서 떠난 적이 없었다. 그렇다고 그가 그녀를 아낀 것도, 그녀가 간병과 피로로 지쳐 버릴까 염려한 것도 아니었다. 그 점에 대해서는 평소보다 더 마음 쓰지 않았다. 아니었다. 그는 옛 방식으로 그녀를 사랑했다. 그들은 다시 감옥에 있었고, 그녀는 그를 보살폈으며, 그는 끊임없이 그녀를 필요로 하여 그녀 없이는 몸을 돌리지도 못했다. 그는 심지어 이따금 그녀에게, 그녀를 위해 많은 것을 견뎌 온 것이 흡족하다고 말하기도 했다. 그녀로 말하자면, 그녀는 조용한 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 댄 채 그의 침대 위로 몸을 숙였고, 그를 되살릴 수만 있다면 제 목숨이라도 내놓았으리라.\n\n그가 이렇게 고통 없이 가라앉기를 이삼 일 했을 무렵, 그녀는 그가 회중시계의 똑딱거리는 소리에 시달리는 것을 알아챘다 — 마치 저 자신과 시간 말고는 가는 것이 아무것도 없다는 듯이 제가 가는 데 대해 그토록 야단스러운 소리를 내는, 거창한 금시계였다. 그녀는 시계가 멎도록 두었으나, 그는 여전히 불안해했고, 그것이 자기가 원하는 바가 아님을 드러냈다. 마침내 그는 정신을 추슬러, 이 시계를 잡혀 돈을 마련하기를 바란다고 설명했다. 그녀가 그러려고 시계를 가져가는 척하자 그는 무척 기뻐했고, 그 후로는 전에 없던 입맛으로 포도주와 젤리를 조금씩 즐겼다.\n\n그가 그렇다는 것을 그는 곧 분명히 드러냈으니, 하루 이틀 더 지나자 그는 소맷부리 단추와 반지들을 내보냈던 것이다. 그는 그녀에게 이런 심부름을 맡기는 데서 놀라운 만족을 느꼈고, 그것을 더없이 빈틈없고 신중한 채비를 하는 것과 마찬가지로 여기는 듯했다. 그의 패물들이, 혹은 그가 주위에서 챙길 수 있었던 것들이 다 나가자, 그의 옷가지가 그의 관심을 끌었다. 그리고 그가 며칠을 더 살아 있었던 것은, 그 옷가지를 한 점씩 상상 속의 전당포로 보내는 만족감 덕분이었을 공산이 크다.\n\n이렇게 열흘 동안 리틀 도릿은 그의 베개 위로 몸을 숙인 채, 자기 뺨을 그의 뺨에 댔다. 때로는 그녀가 너무도 지친 나머지 몇 분간 둘이 함께 잠들기도 했다. 그러다 그녀는 깨어나, 자기 얼굴에 닿는 것이 무엇인지를 소리 없이 줄줄 흐르는 눈물과 함께 떠올리고, 베개 위 그 사랑하는 얼굴 위로, 마셜시 담장의 그림자보다 더 짙은 그림자가 살며시 번져 가는 것을 보곤 했다.\n\n조용히, 조용히, 그 거대한 누각의 설계도를 이루는 모든 선들이 하나씩 하나씩 녹아 사라졌다. 조용히, 조용히, 그 선들이 그어졌던, 줄과 엇줄이 가득했던 그 얼굴이 맑고 텅 비어 갔다. 조용히, 조용히, 감옥 창살과 담장 꼭대기의 지그재그 철책이 비쳐 새겨졌던 자국들이 흐려져 갔다. 조용히, 조용히, 그 얼굴은 그녀가 그 백발 아래에서 여태 본 적 없는, 그녀 자신을 훨씬 더 젊게 닮은 모습으로 가라앉았고, 영원한 안식에 잠겼다.\n\n처음에 그녀의 작은아버지는 완전히 넋을 잃었다. \"오 내 형님! 오 윌리엄, 윌리엄! 형님이 나보다 먼저 가다니, 형님 혼자 가다니, 형님이 가고 내가 남다니! 그토록 뛰어나고, 그토록 훌륭하고, 그토록 고귀하던 형님이, 아무짝에도 쓸모없고 아무도 아쉬워하지 않았을 가엾고 무용한 인간인 내가 남다니!\"\n\n그것은 한동안, 그가 마음 쓰고 보살필 대상을 가지게 해 주는 좋은 일을 그녀에게 해 주었다.\n\n\"작은아버지, 사랑하는 작은아버지, 부디 몸을 아끼세요, 저를 아껴 주세요!\"\n\n노인은 그 마지막 말에 귀를 닫지 않았다. 그가 스스로를 억누르기 시작한 것은, 그녀를 아끼기 위해서였다. 그는 자기 자신은 돌보지 않았다. 다만 그토록 오래 멍하니 잠들어 있다가 이제 깨어나 부서지려 하는 그 정직한 마음의 남은 온 힘을 다해, 그는 그녀를 떠받들고 축복했다.\n\n\"오 하느님.\" 방을 나서기 전에 그가 주름진 두 손을 그녀 위로 맞잡고 외쳤다. \"당신께서는 제 사랑하는 죽은 형님의 이 딸을 보고 계십니다! 제가 반쯤 멀고 죄 많은 눈으로 바라본 모든 것을, 당신께서는 또렷이, 환하게 분별하셨습니다. 당신 앞에서 이 아이의 머리카락 한 올도 상하지 않을 것입니다. 당신께서 이 아이를 그 마지막 시각까지 이곳에서 떠받쳐 주실 것입니다. 그리고 저는 압니다, 당신께서 이 아이를 이후에 보상해 주시리라는 것을!\"\n\n그들은 거의 자정이 될 때까지, 가까운 어느 어둑한 방에 함께 조용하고 슬프게 머물렀다. 때로 그의 슬픔은 처음 터져 나왔던 것과 같은 폭발 속에서 위안을 찾으려 했다. 그러나 그의 작은 기력으로는 그런 격정을 곧 감당할 수 없었을 뿐만 아니라, 그는 어김없이 그녀의 말을 떠올리고는 스스로를 책망하며 진정시켰다. 그가 자신의 슬픔에 빠져드는 유일한 발설은, 형님이 혼자 가셨다는, 둘이 인생의 출발점에서 함께였고, 함께 불운에 빠졌으며, 그 숱한 가난의 세월 동안 함께 견뎠고, 그날까지 함께 남아 있었건만, 형님이 혼자, 혼자 가셨다는 잦은 탄식뿐이었다.\n\n그들은 무겁고 슬프게 헤어졌다. 그녀는 그를 그 자신의 방 말고는 어디에도 두려 하지 않았고, 그가 옷을 입은 채 침대에 눕는 것을 지켜보며 제 손으로 그를 덮어 주었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 자기 침대에 몸을 던지고는 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다. 비탄이 온통 배어든 의식에서 완전히 풀려난 것은 아니었으나, 지칠 대로 지쳐 쉬는 잠이었다. 잠들거라, 착한 리틀 도릿. 밤새 잠들거라!\n\n달이 뜬 밤이었다. 그러나 보름이 한참 지난 터라 달은 늦게 떠올랐다. 달이 평온한 창공에 높이 떴을 때, 그것은 반쯤 닫힌 격자 덧문 사이로, 한 생애의 비틀거림과 방황이 그토록 얼마 전에 끝난 그 엄숙한 방 안으로 비쳐 들었다. 방 안에는 두 고요한 모습이 있었다. 똑같이 미동도 없고 무심한, 곧 그 속에 누우게 될 터이건만 들끓는 이 대지와 그 안의 모든 것으로부터 똑같이 건널 수 없는 거리만큼 떨어진 두 모습이었다.\n\n한 모습은 침대 위에 누워 있었다. 다른 모습은 마룻바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그 위로 수그러져, 두 팔은 편안하고 평화롭게 침대보 위에 얹혀 있었고, 얼굴은 깊이 숙여, 마지막 숨과 함께 그 위로 굽혔던 그 손에 입술이 닿아 있었다. 두 형제는 그들의 아버지 앞에 있었다. 이 세상의 어스름한 심판을 아득히 넘어선 곳에, 그 안개와 어둠을 까마득히 위에서 굽어보는 곳에.\n\n# CHAPTER 20. Introduces the next\n\n승객들이 칼레 부두에서 우편선을 내리고 있었다. 칼레는 낮게 가라앉은 곳, 그리고 가라앉은 기분의 곳이었으니, 조수가 저조선을 향해 빠져나가는 중이었다. 모래톱 위에는 우편선을 띄워 들이기에 겨우 족할 만큼의 물밖에 없었고, 이제 그 모래톱 자체가 그 위로 얕은 파도가 부서지는 채, 막 수면 위로 떠오른 게으른 바다 괴물처럼, 잠든 채 누워 그 형체를 흐릿하게 드러내고 있었다. 온통 하얀 그 빈약한 등대는, 한때 색채와 풍만함을 지녔던 어느 건물의 유령이기라도 한 듯 바닷가를 떠돌며, 방금 파도에 두들겨 맞고서 우울한 눈물을 떨구고 있었다. 끈적거리고 축축하며 비바람에 닳은, 길게 늘어선 앙상한 검은 말뚝들은, 방금 지나간 조수가 그 둘레에 해초의 장례 화환을 비비 꼬아 두른 채로, 보기 흉한 바다의 묘지를 나타내는 것이라 해도 좋았을 것이다. 파도에 부딪히고 폭풍에 두들겨 맞은 모든 것이, 그 넓은 잿빛 하늘 아래, 바람과 바다의 소음 속에서, 그리고 사납게 달려드는 굽이치는 너울의 선들 앞에서 어찌나 낮고 어찌나 작던지, 도리어 칼레가 조금이라도 남아 있다는 것이, 그리고 그 낮은 성문과 낮은 성벽과 낮은 지붕과 낮은 도랑과 낮은 모래언덕과 낮은 성루와 평평한 거리들이, 마치 아이들이 바닷가에 쌓는 요새처럼, 침식하고 포위해 오는 바다에 진작 굴복해 버리지 않았다는 것이 놀라울 지경이었다.\n\n미끈거리는 말뚝과 널빤지 사이를 미끄러지듯 지나고, 젖은 계단을 비틀거리며 오르고, 짠내 나는 갖은 곤경과 맞닥뜨린 끝에, 승객들은 부두를 따라 이어지는 그 안락함이라곤 없는 행로에 들어섰다. 그곳에는 이 도시의 프랑스인 부랑자와 영국인 무법자들이(주민의 절반이었다) 죄다 나와, 그들이 어리둥절한 상태에서 헤어나지 못하도록 막아섰다. 모든 영국인에게 샅샅이 살펴지고, 길이가 사분의 삼 마일에 이르는 육박전 속에서 모든 프랑스인에게 전리품으로 주장되고 또 주장되고 맞주장되고 난 끝에, 그들은 마침내 거리로 들어서서 저마다의 방향으로, 맹렬한 추격을 받으며 빠져나갈 수 있게 되었다.\n\n한 가지가 아닌 여러 근심에 시달리던 클레넘도 이 가엾은 무리에 끼여 있었다. 동포들 가운데 가장 무방비한 자들을 큰 곤경에서 구해 낸 뒤, 그는 이제 홀로, 아니 홀로일 수 있는 한 거의 홀로 길을 갔는데, 기름때 묻은 양복에 같은 재질의 모자를 쓴 토박이 신사 하나가 오십 야드쯤 거리를 두고 뒤쫓으며 끊임없이 그를 향해 외쳐 댔다. \"이! 아이세이! 당신! 시어! 아이세이! 좋은 오텔!\"\n\n그러나 이 친절한 자조차도 마침내 뒤처졌고, 클레넘은 방해받지 않고 제 갈 길을 갔다. 해협과 바닷가의 소란이 지나간 뒤라 도시에는 고요한 기운이 감돌았고, 그에 견주어 보면 그 따분함마저 도리어 반가웠다. 그는 동포들의 새로운 무리들과 마주쳤는데, 그들은 하나같이 한때 어떤 불편한 종류의 꽃들처럼 제 멋에 활짝 피어 버렸다가 이제는 한낱 잡초가 되어 버린 듯한, 어딘가 산만한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다. 그들은 또한 하나같이 날마다 한정된 동선 안을 어슬렁거리며 빈둥대는 분위기를 풍겼는데, 그것이 그에게 마셜시를 강하게 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 그는 그 생각이 떠오를 만큼만 그들에게 주의를 두고는 더 이상 마음에 두지 않은 채, 머릿속에 새겨 두었던 어느 거리와 번지를 찾아 나섰다.\n\n\"팽크스가 그렇게 말했으니,\" 그는 그 주소에 들어맞는 칙칙한 집 앞에서 멈춰 서며 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"그가 알아낸 정보가 정확하고, 캐스비 씨의 어수선한 서류들 틈에서 그가 찾아낸 것이 의심의 여지가 없으리라 짐작은 하네만, 그게 없었더라면 나로서는 여기가 그럴 법한 곳이라고는 좀처럼 생각지 못했을 게야.\"\n\n생기 없는 종류의 집이었으니, 길 건너로는 생기 없는 담이 있고 옆으로는 생기 없는 대문이 있어, 거기 늘어진 종 손잡이를 잡아당기자 생기 없는 딸랑 소리가 두 번 났고, 문고리는 갈라진 문조차 뚫고 들어갈 만한 깊이가 없는 듯 생기 없고 밋밋한, 표면만 두드리는 소리를 냈다. 그래도 문은 생기 없는 종류의 용수철에 삐걱이며 열렸고, 그는 들어서며 등 뒤로 문을 닫고는 칙칙한 안마당으로 들어섰는데, 그 마당은 또 하나의 생기 없는 담에 이내 가로막혀 끝났다. 그곳에는 무언가 기어오르는 관목을 길러 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 죽어 있었고, 동굴에 작은 분수를 만들어 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 말라 있었으며, 그 분수를 작은 조상으로 꾸며 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 사라지고 없었다.\n\n집으로 드는 입구는 왼편에 있었고, 바깥 대문이 그러했듯 프랑스어와 영어로 된 인쇄 광고지 두 장이 붙어 가구 딸린 셋방을 즉시 입주 가능 조건으로 내놓는다고 알리고 있었다. 온통 양말과 페티코트와 흰 모자와 귀고리뿐인, 기운차고 명랑한 시골 아낙 하나가 어두운 문간에 서서 보기 좋게 이를 드러내며 말했다. \"아이세이! 시어! 누구를?\"\n\n클레넘은 프랑스어로 답하여 영국 부인이라고 했다. 그는 그 영국 부인을 만나고 싶다고 했다. \"그러면 들어와서, 괜찮으시다면 올라오세요.\" 아낙도 마찬가지로 프랑스어로 대꾸했다. 그는 들어가 올라갔고, 어둡고 휑한 계단을 따라 그녀를 좇아 이층 뒤편의 한 방으로 갔다. 거기서는 칙칙한 마당과, 죽은 관목과, 마른 분수와, 사라지고 없는 조상의 받침대가 음울하게 내려다보였다.\n\n\"블랑두아 씨를.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"기꺼이요, 무슈.\"\n\n그러고서 아낙은 물러가 그가 방을 둘러보도록 내버려 두었다. 그것은 그런 집에서 늘 보게 되는 본보기 같은 방이었다. 서늘하고, 칙칙하고, 어두웠다. 밀랍 칠을 한 바닥은 몹시 미끄러웠다. 스케이트를 탈 만큼 크지도 않았고, 다른 어떤 소일거리를 편히 즐기기에도 알맞지 않았다. 빨강과 흰색 커튼을 친 창들, 작은 짚 매트, 아래로는 다리들이 어수선하게 모여 있는 작은 둥근 탁자, 투박한 골풀 방석의 의자들, 불편하게 앉아 있을 공간만은 넉넉히 내어 주는 커다란 빨간 벨벳 안락의자 둘, 책상 하나, 여러 조각이면서 한 장인 척하는 벽난로 거울, 지극히 조화로 만든 꽃을 꽂은 요란한 꽃병 한 쌍, 그 사이로는 투구를 벗은 그리스 전사가 프랑스의 수호신에게 시계를 제물로 바치고 있었다.\n\n얼마쯤 뜸을 들인 뒤, 다른 방으로 통하는 문이 열리고 한 부인이 들어왔다. 그녀는 클레넘을 보고 크게 놀란 기색을 드러내며, 또 다른 누군가를 찾는 듯 방 안을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 웨이드 양. 저 혼자입니다.\"\n\n\"제게 전해진 것은 당신 이름이 아니었는데요.\"\n\n\"네, 알고 있습니다. 죄송합니다. 제 이름으로는 당신이 면담에 응할 마음이 들지 않으리란 것을 이미 겪어 보았기에, 제가 찾는 어떤 사람의 이름을 감히 댔습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서,\" 그녀가 어찌나 차갑게 의자를 권하던지 그가 도리어 선 채로 있게 만들며 말했다. \"당신이 댄 이름이 무엇이었나요?\"\n\n\"블랑두아라는 이름을 댔습니다.\"\n\n\"블랑두아라고요?\"\n\n\"당신이 아는 이름이지요.\"\n\n\"이상하군요,\" 그녀가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"당신은 여전히 저에게, 저의 지인들에게, 저와 저의 일에, 청하지도 않은 관심을 들이대시는군요, 클레넘 씨. 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"용서하십시오. 그 이름을 아시지요?\"\n\n\"당신이 그 이름과 무슨 상관이 있나요? 제가 그 이름과 무슨 상관이 있고요? 제가 어떤 이름을 알든 모르든 당신이 무슨 상관이 있나요? 저는 숱한 이름을 알고, 그보다 더 많은 이름을 잊었어요. 그 이름은 아는 쪽일 수도, 잊은 쪽일 수도, 아예 들어 본 적 없는 것일 수도 있지요. 저는 그것에 관하여 제 자신을 따져 볼 까닭도, 따짐을 당할 까닭도 알지 못해요.\"\n\n\"허락하신다면,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"이 일을 거듭 여쭙는 제 까닭을 말씀드리겠습니다. 제가 거듭 여쭙고 있음은 인정하며, 그러는 것을 부디 너그러이 봐 주시길 진심으로 간청합니다. 그 까닭은 온전히 제 것이지, 결코 당신의 것이라고 넌지시 비치는 게 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 선생님.\" 그녀가 아까보다는 조금 덜 거만하게, 앉으라던 앞서의 권유를 되풀이하며 대꾸했다. 그러더니 그녀가 자리에 앉자 이번에는 그도 따랐다. \"적어도 이것이, 자유로운 선택권을 빼앗긴 채 제가 빼돌렸다는 당신 친구의 또 다른 종속녀가 아니라는 걸 알게 되어 다행이군요. 괜찮으시다면 당신 까닭을 들어 보지요.\"\n\n\"먼저, 우리가 말하는 자가 누구인지 확실히 하기 위해,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그가 얼마 전 당신이 런던에서 만난 사람임을 일러 두겠습니다. 강 근처에서 그를 만나셨던 일을 기억하실 겁니다—아델파이에서요!\"\n\n\"당신은 도무지 까닭 모르게 제 일에 끼어드는군요.\" 그녀가 엄한 불쾌함을 띤 채 그를 똑바로 바라보며 대꾸했다. \"그걸 어떻게 아시지요?\"\n\n\"부디 언짢게 여기지 마십시오. 순전히 우연이었습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 우연이요?\"\n\n\"오로지 거리에서 당신과 마주쳐 그 만남을 보게 된 우연일 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"당신 자신을 두고 하는 말인가요, 아니면 다른 누군가를 두고 하는 말인가요?\"\n\n\"제 자신입니다. 제가 보았습니다.\"\n\"하긴 그건 트인 거리에서였지요.\" 점점 누그러지는 노여움 속에 잠깐 생각하더니 그녀가 말했다. \"오십 명쯤이 그걸 보았을 수도 있겠네요. 보았다 한들 아무 의미도 없었을 테고요.\"\n\n\"저 또한 그걸 본 일을 무슨 대수로 삼지 않으며, (제가 여기 온 데 대한 해명이 아니고서는) 제 방문을 그 일이나 제가 청하려는 부탁과 결부시키지도 않습니다.\"\n\n\"오! 부탁이 있으시군요! 클레넘 씨, 당신 태도가 부드러워졌다 싶었지요.\" 그 아름다운 얼굴이 씁쓸하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했다.\n\n그는 말로 다투지는 않은 채, 가벼운 몸짓으로 그것에 항의하는 데 그쳤다. 그러고 나서 그는 블랑두아의 실종을 언급하며, 아마 그녀도 들었으리라고 했다. 그에게는 아무리 그럴듯해 보여도, 그녀는 그런 일을 들은 바 없었다. 둘러보고서(라고 그녀는 말했다) 스스로 판단해 보라고, 그 소문이 자자할 적에 이곳에 갇혀 제 마음을 갉아먹고 있던 여자의 귀에 어떤 세상 소식이 닿을 법했겠느냐고 했다. 그가 사실이라 믿은 이 부인의 말을 마치고 나서, 그녀는 실종이란 무슨 뜻이냐고 그에게 물었다. 그 때문에 그는 정황을 소상히 이야기하게 되었고, 그자가 정말로 어찌 되었는지를 알아내어 어머니의 집을 에워싼 어두운 의혹을 떨쳐 내고 싶은 자신의 근심을 어느 정도 드러냈다. 그녀는 역력한 놀라움을 띠고, 그가 그녀에게서 본 적 있는 것보다 더 많은 억눌린 관심의 기색을 띤 채 그의 말을 들었다. 그래도 그것들이 그녀의 거리감 있고 도도하며 스스로를 가두는 태도를 이겨 내지는 못했다. 그가 말을 마치자 그녀는 이 말밖에 하지 않았다.\n\n\"선생님, 아직 그게 저와 무슨 상관인지, 그 부탁이 무엇인지는 말씀하지 않으셨는데요. 그 얘기로 좀 넘어가 주시겠어요?\"\n\n\"제 짐작입니다만,\" 그녀의 경멸하는 태도를 누그러뜨리려 애쓰며 아서가 끈질기게 말했다. \"이 사람과 연락을—말하자면, 은밀한 연락을—주고받는 사이이시니—\"\n\n\"물론 당신이야 무슨 말이든 마음대로 하시면 되지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 클레넘 씨, 저는 당신의 짐작에도, 그 누구의 짐작에도 동의하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"—적어도 그와 직접 연락을 주고받는 사이이시니,\" 클레넘은 그 표현이 거슬리지 않게 되기를 바라며 말투를 바꾸어 말했다. \"그자의 내력, 일거리, 습관, 평소 거처에 관해 제게 무언가 일러 주실 수 있을 겁니다. 가장 가망 있는 방식으로 그를 찾아낼 작은 실마리라도 주시어, 그를 데려오게 하시든가, 그가 어찌 되었는지를 밝혀 주실 수 있을 겁니다. 이것이 제가 청하는 부탁이며, 부디 당신이 좀 헤아려 주시기를 바라는 마음의 괴로움 속에서 청합니다. 제게 어떤 조건을 다실 까닭이 있으시다면, 그것이 무엇인지 묻지 않고 존중하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 우연히 거리에서 저와 그자를 함께 보았다는 거지요.\" 그가 분하게도, 그녀는 그의 호소보다 그 일에 대한 자기 생각에 더 몰두한 게 분명한 채로 말했다. \"그렇다면 그 전부터 그자를 알았던 건가요?\"\n\n\"전부터는 아니고, 그 뒤에요. 그를 전에 본 적은 없습니다만, 바로 그가 실종된 그날 밤에 다시 보았습니다. 사실은 어머니의 방에서요. 거기 그를 두고 나왔습니다. 그에 관해 알려진 전부를 이 종이에서 읽으실 수 있습니다.\"\n\n그가 인쇄된 광고지 한 장을 그녀에게 건네자, 그녀는 차분하고 주의 깊은 얼굴로 그것을 읽었다.\n\n\"이건 저도 그자에 대해 알던 것보다 더한 내용이네요.\" 그녀가 그것을 돌려주며 말했다. 클레넘의 표정에는 깊은 실망이, 어쩌면 불신이 어렸다. 그러자 그녀가 똑같이 냉담한 어조로 덧붙였다. \"당신은 믿지 않으시는군요. 그래도 사실이에요. 직접 연락에 관해서라면, 그자와 당신 어머니 사이에는 직접 연락이 있었던 모양이지요. 그런데도 당신은, 그자에 대해 더 아는 게 없다는 어머니의 말은 믿는다고 하시는군요!\"\n\n이 말 속에, 그리고 그 말에 곁들여진 미소 속에는, 클레넘의 두 뺨에 피가 오르게 하기에 충분할 만큼 의혹의 암시가 또렷이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"자, 선생님.\" 그녀가 그 일격을 되풀이하는 데서 잔인한 즐거움을 느끼며 말했다. \"당신이 바라는 만큼 제가 솔직해지지요. 만일 제가 제 평판을 아낀다면(저는 아끼지 않습니다만), 또는 지킬 만한 좋은 이름이 제게 있다면(없습니다, 저는 그것이 좋게 여겨지든 나쁘게 여겨지든 전혀 개의치 않으니까요), 저는 그런 자와 무슨 관계든 가졌다는 것만으로도 제가 심하게 누를 끼친 셈이라 여길 거라고 고백하지요. 하지만 그자는 제 문으로 들어온 적도 없고, 한밤중까지 저와 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눈 적도 없어요.\"\n\n그녀는 이렇게 그의 화제를 그에게 되돌려 줌으로써 오랜 원한에 대한 복수를 했다. 그녀의 천성은 그를 봐줄 만한 것이 아니었고, 그녀에게는 가책이라곤 없었다.\n\n\"그자가 비천하고 돈에 매수되는 가련한 자라는 것, 제가 이탈리아에서(저는 얼마 전까지 거기 있었어요) 그자가 어슬렁거리는 것을 처음 보고 거기서 마침 제게 있던 어떤 목적에 알맞은 도구로 그를 고용했다는 것, 저는 당신에게 말하기를 꺼리지 않아요. 요컨대, 제 자신의 만족을 위해—어떤 강렬한 감정을 충족시키기 위해—돈을 받고 심부름을 해 줄 첩자에게 보수를 줄 만한 가치가 제게 있었던 거지요. 저는 이 작자에게 돈을 주었어요. 그리고 단언컨대, 제가 그런 거래를 원했고, 그에게 충분히 줄 수 있었으며, 그가 어둠 속에서 아무 위험 없이 그 일을 해낼 수 있었다면, 그는 제 돈을 받았던 것만큼이나 거리낌 없이 누구의 목숨이든 거두었을 거예요. 적어도 그게 그자에 대한 제 생각이고, 보아하니 당신 생각과 그리 멀지 않군요. 당신 어머니의 그자에 대한 생각은(이것저것 짐작하는 당신 본을 따르자면) 사뭇 달랐다고 봐야겠지요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 일러 두겠는데,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"제 어머니는 불운한 사업의 과정에서 처음으로 그와 연락을 트게 된 것입니다.\"\n\n\"마지막으로 그를 어머니와 연락이 닿게 한 것도 불운한 사업의 과정이었던 모양이네요.\" 웨이드 양이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 그때의 영업 시간은 늦은 시각이었고요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지,\" 이미 그 무게를 깊이 느낀 바 있는 이 냉정한 일격들에 쓰라려하며 아서가 말했다. \"무언가가 있었다는 뜻으로—\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 그녀가 침착하게 말을 가로막았다. \"제가 그자에 관해 넌지시 말하는 게 아님을 잊지 마세요. 거듭, 숨김없이 말하건대 그는 비천하고 돈에 매수되는 가련한 자예요. 그런 자는 자기가 필요한 곳에 간다고 봐요. 제가 그를 필요로 하지 않았다면 당신은 그와 저를 함께 보지 못했을 거고요.\"\n\n자기 가슴속에도 반쯤 숨은 그림자가 있는 그 어두운 면을 그녀가 끈질기게 눈앞에 들이미는 데 괴로워하며, 클레넘은 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"제가 그자를 아직 살아 있는 것처럼 말했지만,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"어쩌면 제가 모르는 사이에 누가 그를 처치해 버렸을지도 모르지요. 그리고 제가 아랑곳할 바도 아니고요. 저는 더 이상 그를 필요로 하지 않으니까요.\"\n\n무거운 한숨과 낙담한 기색으로 아서 클레넘은 천천히 일어섰다. 그녀는 함께 일어서지 않은 채, 그러는 동안 의혹의 시선을 그에게 못 박고 입술을 성나게 앙다물고 있다가 말했다.\n\n\"그자는 당신의 그 친애하는 친구 가우언 씨가 골라 사귄 동무 아니었나요? 어째서 그 친애하는 친구분께 도와 달라 하지 않으시고요?\"\n\n그가 친애하는 친구가 아니라는 부정의 말이 아서의 입가에 떠올랐으나, 그는 지난날의 다툼과 결심을 떠올리며 그것을 억누르고 말했다.\n\n\"블랑두아가 영국으로 떠난 뒤로 그를 본 적이 없다는 것 외에, 가우언 씨가 그에 관해 따로 아는 것은 없습니다. 그는 외국에서 우연히 사귄 사람이었지요.\"\n\n\"외국에서 우연히 사귄 사람이라!\" 그녀가 되풀이했다. \"그렇겠지요. 당신의 친애하는 친구분은 그런 아내를 두었으니 사귈 수 있는 온갖 사람들로 기분을 풀어야겠지요. 저는 그 아내가 미워요, 선생님.\"\n\n그렇게 말하는 그녀의 노여움은, 그토록 억눌려 있어 더욱 두드러진 까닭에 클레넘의 주의를 붙들어 그를 그 자리에 머물게 했다. 그것은 그를 바라보는 그녀의 검은 눈에서 번쩍였고, 콧방울에서 떨렸으며, 그녀가 내쉬는 숨결마저 달구었다. 그러나 그 밖으로는 그녀의 얼굴이 경멸 어린 평온함으로 다잡혀 있었고, 그 자태는 마치 완전한 무관심의 기분에 잠긴 듯 차분하고 도도하게 우아했다.\n\n\"한 가지만 말씀드리지요, 웨이드 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 품으신 그 감정에는, 제가 믿건대 함께할 이가 아무도 없을뿐더러, 그 감정을 자아낼 만한 어떤 도발도 당신이 받으신 적이 없을 거라는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"원하신다면 당신의 친애하는 친구분께,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그 문제에 관한 의견을 물어보셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n\"제가 그 친애하는 친구와는,\" 결심에도 불구하고 아서가 말했다. \"그 화제를 꺼낼 법한 그런 가까운 사이가 거의 못 됩니다, 웨이드 양.\"\n\n\"저는 그가 미워요.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그의 아내보다 더 미워요. 제가 한때는 어리석게도, 또 제 자신에게 진실되지 못하게도 그를 거의 사랑할 뻔했으니까요. 선생님, 당신은 그저 평범한 상황에서만 저를 보셨고, 그래서 저를 보통 사람보다 좀 더 고집 센 평범한 여자쯤으로 여기셨겠지요. 저를 그 정도로만 아신다면, 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 당신은 모르세요. 제가 얼마나 세심하게 제 자신과 제 주위 사람들을 살펴 왔는지를 모르고서는 아실 수 없어요. 그래서 저는 얼마 전부터 제 삶이 어떠했는지를 당신에게 들려줄까 마음이 기울었어요—당신의 호의를 사려는 게 아니라, 그건 제가 아무 값어치도 두지 않으니까요. 다만 당신이 그 친애하는 친구와 그 친애하는 아내를 떠올릴 때, 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지를 헤아릴 수 있도록요. 제가 써서 당신더러 읽으라고 따로 간직해 둔 것을 드릴까요, 아니면 그만둘까요?\"\n\n아서는 그것을 자기에게 달라고 청했다. 그녀는 책상으로 가서 자물쇠를 풀고, 안쪽 서랍에서 접힌 종이 몇 장을 꺼냈다. 그를 누그러뜨리려 들지도 않고, 그에게 말을 거는 둥 마는 둥, 차라리 제 고집을 정당화하느라 제 거울에다 대고 말하는 듯이, 그녀는 그것을 그에게 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시게 될 거예요! 그 얘긴 그만하지요. 선생님, 당신이 저를 텅 빈 런던 집에서 임시로 값싸게 셋방을 들어 사는 것으로 보든, 칼레의 한 셋방에서 보든, 제 곁에는 해리엇이 있습니다. 떠나시기 전에 그 애를 보고 싶으실지도 모르겠네요. 해리엇, 들어와요!\" 그녀는 다시 해리엇을 불렀다. 두 번째 부름에 해리엇이, 한때 태티코럼이었던 그 아이가 나타났다.\n\n\"여기 클레넘 씨가 오셨어.\" 웨이드 양이 말했다. \"너를 데리러 온 게 아니야. 이분은 너를 단념하셨거든—이제쯤은 그러셨지요?\"\n\n\"권한도 영향력도 없으니—그렇습니다.\" 클레넘이 수긍했다.\n\n\"보다시피 너를 찾으러 온 게 아니라, 그래도 어떤 사람을 찾고 계셔. 그 블랑두아라는 자를 찾으셔.\"\n\n\"런던 스트랜드에서 너와 함께 있는 것을 내가 본 그 사람이지.\" 아서가 일러 주었다.\n\n\"해리엇, 그자가 베네치아에서 왔다는 것 외에—그건 우리 모두 아는 거고—그에 대해 아는 게 있다면, 클레넘 씨께 거리낌 없이 말씀드려라.\"\n\n\"그에 대해 더 아는 건 없어요.\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"만족하셨나요?\" 웨이드 양이 아서에게 물었다.\n\n그는 그들을 의심할 이유가 없었다. 아이의 태도가 어찌나 자연스럽던지, 그가 미리 어떤 의심을 품었다 한들 거의 납득될 정도였다. 그가 대답했다. \"다른 데서 소식을 찾아봐야겠군요.\"\n\n그는 그 말끝에 곧장 떠날 참은 아니었으나, 아이가 들어오기 전에 이미 일어서 있었던 터라 아이는 그가 떠나려는 줄로 안 게 분명했다. 아이가 그를 얼른 쳐다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그분들 안녕하세요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"누구 말이냐?\"\n아이는 \"그분들 모두\"라고 할 뻔한 말을 스스로 그치고는 웨이드 양을 흘끗 보더니 말했다. \"미글스 씨와 미글스 부인이요.\"\n\n\"내가 마지막으로 소식을 들었을 때는 안녕하셨다. 지금은 집에 안 계신단다. 그러고 보니, 한 가지 물어보자. 네가 거기서 목격됐다는 게 사실이냐?\"\n\n\"어디서요? 누가 제가 어디서 목격됐다고 하던가요?\" 아이가 침울하게 눈을 내리깔며 되물었다.\n\n\"그 별장 정원 문 안을 들여다보고 있었다더구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 웨이드 양이 말했다. \"그 애는 그 근처에 간 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"그게 아니에요.\" 아이가 말했다. \"우리가 마지막으로 런던에 있을 때 저는 거기 갔어요. 어느 날 오후 당신이 저를 혼자 두었을 때 갔어요. 그리고 정말로 들여다봤고요.\"\n\n\"이 비굴한 것아.\" 웨이드 양이 한없는 경멸을 담아 대꾸했다. \"우리가 함께한 그 모든 시간이, 우리가 나눈 그 모든 이야기가, 네가 늘어놓던 그 모든 옛 푸념이 고작 그것밖에 안 된다는 거니?\"\n\n\"문 안을 잠깐 들여다보는 게 무슨 잘못이에요.\" 아이가 말했다. \"창문을 보고서 식구들이 거기 없다는 걸 알았는걸요.\"\n\n\"왜 그 근처에 갔던 거니?\"\n\n\"보고 싶었으니까요. 한 번 더 보고 싶다는 마음이 들었으니까요.\"\n\n그 두 아름다운 얼굴이 서로를 마주 보는 동안, 클레넘은 그 두 천성이 끊임없이 서로를 갈가리 찢어 놓고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"오!\" 웨이드 양이 차갑게 시선을 거두고 가라앉히며 말했다. \"네가, 그게 어떤 것인지 알아챘기에 내가 너를 구해 낸 그 삶을 살던 곳을 다시 보고 싶은 바람이 있었다면, 그건 또 다른 얘기지. 하지만 그게 나에 대한 너의 진심이니? 그게 나에 대한 너의 충실함이니? 그게 내가 너와 함께 도모하는 공동의 대의야? 너는 내가 너에게 둔 신뢰를 받을 자격이 없어. 너는 내가 너에게 베푼 호의를 받을 자격이 없어. 너는 스패니얼만도 못한 것이니, 차라리 채찍질보다 더 못된 짓을 한 그 사람들에게 돌아가는 게 낫겠다.\"\n\n\"누가 듣는 데서 그분들을 그렇게 말하면, 제가 그분들 편을 들도록 부추기시는 거예요.\" 아이가 말했다.\n\n\"그들에게 돌아가.\" 웨이드 양이 쏘아붙였다. \"그들에게 돌아가라고.\"\n\n\"잘 아시잖아요.\" 이번에는 해리엇이 쏘아붙였다. \"저는 그분들에게 돌아가지 않을 거예요. 제가 그분들을 떨쳐 냈고, 다시는 돌아갈 수 없고, 돌아가지 않을 거고, 결코 돌아가지 않으리란 걸 잘 아시잖아요. 그러니 그분들은 그냥 내버려 두세요, 웨이드 양.\"\n\n\"여기서의 덜 기름진 살림보다 그들의 풍족함을 더 좋아하는 거지.\" 그녀가 되받았다. \"너는 그들을 치켜세우고 나를 깔보는구나. 내가 달리 무엇을 기대했겠어? 진작 알았어야 했는데.\"\n\n\"그게 아니에요.\" 아이가 벌겋게 달아올라 말했다. \"그리고 당신은 속마음을 말하고 있지 않아요. 저는 당신이 무슨 뜻인지 알아요. 당신은 제가 의지할 데라곤 당신밖에 없다는 걸로 저를 음흉하게 나무라고 있는 거예요. 그리고 제가 의지할 데라곤 당신밖에 없으니, 당신 마음대로 저를 부리거나 못 하게 하거나 마음대로 할 수 있고, 무슨 모욕이든 저에게 안길 수 있다고 여기시는 거고요. 당신은 그분들만큼이나 나빠요, 하나도 빠짐없이. 하지만 저는 그렇게 고분고분 길들여져 순종하지는 않을 거예요. 다시 말하지만 저는 그 집을 보러 갔어요, 한 번 더 보고 싶다고 종종 생각해 왔으니까요. 다시 묻겠어요, 그분들 안녕하세요. 저는 한때 그분들을 좋아했고, 때로는 그분들이 저에게 다정했다고 생각했으니까요.\"\n\n이에 클레넘은, 만약 그녀가 언제든 돌아가고 싶어진다면 그들이 여전히 다정하게 맞아 줄 것이 분명하다고 말했다.\n\n\"절대로요!\" 아이가 격하게 말했다. \"절대 그러지 않을 거예요. 웨이드 양보다 그걸 더 잘 아는 사람은 없지요, 비록 저를 자기에게 매인 신세로 만들어 놓고 저를 비웃지만요. 그리고 제가 그런 신세란 걸 저도 알아요. 또 그녀는 그걸 제 마음에 떠올리게 할 수 있을 때면 더없이 기뻐한다는 것도 알고요.\"\n\n\"그럴듯한 핑계로구나!\" 웨이드 양이 못지않은 노여움과 거만함과 쓰라림을 담아 말했다. \"하지만 내가 이 일에서 빤히 보는 것을 가리기엔 너무 닳아빠진 핑계지. 내 가난이 그들의 돈과는 겨룰 수 없다는 거잖아. 차라리 당장 돌아가, 차라리 당장 돌아가서 끝을 봐라!\"\n\n아서 클레넘은 그 칙칙하고 좁은 방 안에서 조금 떨어져 선 채, 저마다 제 노여움을 오만하게 품고, 저마다 한결같은 결의로 제 가슴을 괴롭히고 또 상대의 가슴을 괴롭히는 그들을 바라보았다. 그는 작별의 말을 한두 마디 했으나, 웨이드 양은 겨우 고개만 까딱했고, 해리엇은 비굴한 식객이자 종의 가장된 굴종으로(그러면서도 그 와중에 반항이 없지 않은 채), 제가 너무 미천하여 누구를 알아보지도 누구의 눈에 띄지도 않는다는 양 행세했다.\n\n그는 어둡고 구불구불한 계단을 내려와 마당으로 나섰는데, 죽은 담의 음울함과, 죽은 관목의 음울함과, 마른 분수의 음울함과, 사라지고 없는 조상의 음울함이 한층 더 짙게 그를 짓눌렀다. 그 집에서 보고 들은 것을, 그리고 행방불명된 그 수상한 인물을 좇으려던 자신의 온갖 노력이 모조리 수포로 돌아간 것을 깊이 곱씹으면서, 그는 자기를 건네주었던 그 우편선을 타고 런던으로, 영국으로 돌아갔다. 가는 길에 그는 그 종이들을 펼쳐, 다음 장에 옮겨 적은 것을 그 안에서 읽었다.\n\n# CHAPTER 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor\n\n나는 바보가 아니라는 불행을 안고 있다. 아주 어릴 적부터 나는 내 주위 사람들이 내게 숨기고 있다고 여기는 것들을 간파해 왔다. 만약 내가 늘 진실을 꿰뚫어 보는 대신 늘 속아넘어갈 수 있는 사람이었다면, 나도 여느 바보들처럼 순탄하게 살았을지 모른다.\n\n내 어린 시절은 할머니와 함께 보냈다. 다시 말해, 내게 그 친척 노릇을 하며 스스로 그 칭호를 떠맡은 어느 부인과 함께 보냈다. 그 부인에게는 그럴 자격이 없었지만, 나는—그만큼은 어린 바보였던지라—그녀를 의심하지 않았다. 그녀의 집에는 자기 집안의 아이들도 몇 있었고, 남의 집 아이들도 몇 있었다. 모두 여자아이였고, 나를 포함해 열 명이었다. 우리는 다 함께 살며 다 함께 교육을 받았다.\n\n그 여자아이들이 얼마나 작정하고 나를 보살펴 주는 척하는지 알아채기 시작했을 때, 나는 열두 살쯤이었던 것 같다. 사람들은 내가 고아라고 했다. 우리 가운데 다른 고아는 없었다. 그리고 나는 (여기서 바보가 아니라는 데서 오는 첫 번째 불리함이 드러났다) 그들이 무례한 연민으로, 또 우월감으로 내 비위를 맞추고 있음을 알아차렸다. 나는 이것을 경솔하게 발견이라고 단정하지 않았다. 나는 그들을 자주 시험해 보았다. 그들이 나와 다투게 만들기란 좀처럼 쉽지 않았다. 누군가와 다투는 데 성공해도, 그들은 반드시 한두 시간 뒤에 찾아와 화해를 시작하곤 했다. 나는 그들을 거듭거듭 시험해 보았지만, 그들이 내가 먼저 다가오기를 기다리는 일은 한 번도 본 적이 없다. 그들은 늘 자기들의 허영과 겸손한 척하는 마음으로 나를 용서하고 있었다. 다 큰 어른들의 작은 축소판이었다!\n\n그들 가운데 한 아이가 내가 택한 친구였다. 나는 그 어리석은 꼬마를 격정적으로 사랑했는데, 그 아이는 그런 사랑을 받을 자격이 없었고, 나 또한 비록 어린아이에 불과했다지만 부끄러움 없이는 그 일을 떠올릴 수 없다. 그 아이에게는 사람들이 상냥한 성품, 다정한 성품이라 부르는 것이 있었다. 그 아이는 누구에게나 어여쁜 눈길과 미소를 나누어 줄 수 있었고, 또 실제로 나누어 주었다. 그 아이가 나를 상처 입히고 괴롭히려고 일부러 그런다는 것을 아는 사람은, 나 말고는 그곳에 한 사람도 없었으리라 믿는다!\n\n그럼에도 나는 그 못난 아이를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 그 아이를 향한 애정 때문에 내 삶이 폭풍에 휩싸였다. 나는 '그 아이를 시험한다'는 일로—다시 말해, 그 아이의 자잘한 배신을 들이대고, 내가 그 마음을 읽고 있음을 보여 주어 눈물 흘리게 만든 일로—끊임없이 훈계를 듣고 망신을 당했다. 그래도 나는 그 아이를 한결같이 사랑했다. 그리고 한번은 방학 때 그 아이와 함께 그 집에 갔다.\n\n그 아이는 학교에서보다 집에서 더 심했다. 사촌이며 친지들이 한 무리나 있었고, 우리는 그 집에서 춤을 추기도 하고 다른 집으로 춤을 추러 나가기도 했는데, 집에서나 밖에서나 그 아이는 내 사랑을 견딜 수 없을 만큼 괴롭혔다. 그 아이의 수법은 모두가 자기를 좋아하게 만들어—그렇게 나를 질투로 미쳐 버리게 하는 것이었다. 모두와 허물없이 다정하게 굴어—그렇게 그들을 부러워하다 못해 내가 정신을 잃게 하는 것이었다. 밤에 우리 둘만 침실에 남으면, 나는 그 아이의 비열함을 내가 완벽히 알고 있다고 책망하곤 했다. 그러면 그 아이는 울고 또 울면서 내가 잔인하다고 했고, 그러면 나는 아침까지 그 아이를 품에 안고 있었다. 변함없이 사랑하면서, 또 흔히 이렇게 괴로워하느니 차라리 이대로 그 아이를 품에 안고 강 밑바닥까지 가라앉아 버리는 편이—우리 둘 다 죽은 뒤에도 그곳에서 여전히 그 아이를 안고 있는 편이 낫겠다는 심정으로.\n\n그것도 끝이 났고, 나는 안도했다. 그 집안에는 나를 좋아하지 않는 숙모가 있었다. 그 집안의 누구라도 나를 그리 좋아했을지 의문이지만, 나는 그 한 아이에게 온통 매여 있었기에 그들이 나를 좋아하기를 바란 적이 없었다. 그 숙모는 젊은 여자였는데, 진지한 눈빛으로 나를 지켜보는 버릇이 있었다. 그녀는 대담한 여자였고, 드러내 놓고 나를 가엾다는 듯 바라보았다. 내가 앞서 말한 그런 밤들 가운데 어느 하루가 지난 뒤, 나는 아침 식사 전에 온실로 내려갔다. 샬럿(내 거짓된 어린 친구의 이름이다)이 나보다 먼저 내려가 있었고, 내가 들어설 때 그 숙모가 나에 관해 그 아이에게 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 나는 잎사귀들 사이 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다.\n\n숙모가 말했다. \"샬럿, 웨이드 양이 너를 기진맥진하게 만들고 있구나. 이대로 계속되어선 안 돼.\" 내가 들은 바로 그 말을 그대로 옮긴다.\n\n그러면, 그 아이가 무어라 답했을까? \"그 애를 기진맥진하게 만드는 건 저예요. 그 애를 고문대에 매달아 두고 형 집행인 노릇을 하는 건 저라고요. 그런데도 그 애는 제가 자기에게 무얼 견디게 하는지 알면서도, 매일 밤 저를 헌신적으로 사랑한다고 말한답니다\"라고 했을까? 아니다. 내 첫 번째 잊지 못할 경험은 내가 알던 그 아이의 됨됨이에, 그리고 내 모든 경험에 충실했다. 그 아이는 (숙모의 동정을 제 편으로 끌어들이려고) 흐느끼며 울기 시작하더니 이렇게 말했다. \"숙모, 저 애는 불행한 성미를 가졌어요. 저 말고도 학교의 다른 여자아이들이 그걸 고쳐 주려고 무던히 애쓰고 있어요. 우리 모두 무던히 애쓰고 있다고요.\"\n\n그 말에 숙모는 마치 그 아이가 비열하고 거짓된 말이 아니라 무언가 고귀한 말이라도 한 듯 그 아이를 어루만지며, 그 파렴치한 가식을 이어 받아 이렇게 답했다. \"하지만 무슨 일에든 합당한 한도라는 게 있는 거란다, 얘야. 내가 보기에 그 가엾고 비참한 아이는, 그토록 갸륵한 노력으로도 정당화될 수 없을 만큼, 네게 끊임없고 부질없는 괴로움을 끼치고 있어.\"\n\n그 가엾고 비참한 아이는, 짐작하셨겠지만, 숨어 있던 곳에서 나와 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 집으로 보내 주세요.\" 나는 두 사람에게든, 그 집 누구에게든, \"저를 집으로 보내 주세요, 안 그러면 밤이고 낮이고 혼자서라도 걸어서 가겠어요!\"라는 말 외에는 다른 말을 하지 않았다. 집에 돌아와서 나는 할머니라는 그 사람에게, 저 아이가 돌아오기 전에, 아니 저들 가운데 누구라도 돌아오기 전에 나를 어딘가 다른 곳으로 보내 학업을 마치게 해 주지 않는다면, 저들의 음모를 꾸미는 얼굴을 보고 견디느니 차라리 불 속에 몸을 던져 두 눈을 태워 버리겠다고 말했다.\n\n다음으로 나는 젊은 여자들 사이로 들어갔는데, 그들도 조금도 나을 것이 없었다. 고운 말과 고운 척. 그러나 나는 그 자화자찬과 나에 대한 폄하 아래까지 꿰뚫어 보았고, 그들도 조금도 나을 것이 없었다. 그들을 떠나기 전에, 나는 내게 할머니도 없고 인정된 친척도 없다는 것을 알게 되었다. 나는 그 소식의 빛을 내 과거에도, 내 미래에도 비추어 보았다. 그것은 사람들이 나를 배려해 대하는 척하거나 내게 호의를 베푸는 척할 때, 실은 나를 짓밟고 우쭐대던 수많은 새로운 경우들을 내게 보여 주었다.\n\n어느 사업가가 나를 위해 적은 재산을 신탁으로 맡아 두고 있었다. 나는 가정교사가 되기로 되어 있었고, 가정교사가 되었다. 그리고 어느 가난한 귀족 집안으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에는 두 딸이 있었다. 아직 어린아이들이었지만, 부모는 가능하다면 한 교사 밑에서 자라기를 바랐다. 어머니는 젊고 예뻤다. 처음부터 그녀는 나를 무척 세심하게 대하는 듯 꾸몄다. 나는 분개함을 속으로 삭였지만, 그것이 자기가 내 주인이라는 사실을 어루만지며 즐기는 그녀만의 방식임을, 또 마음만 내켰다면 하인을 대하듯 달리 굴 수도 있었으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n분개하지 않았다고 말은 하지만, 실제로 분개하지는 않았다. 다만 나는 그녀의 기분을 채워 주지 않음으로써, 내가 그녀를 꿰뚫어 보고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그녀가 포도주를 권하면 나는 물을 마셨다. 식탁에 귀한 음식이 있으면 그녀는 늘 그것을 내게 보냈지만, 나는 늘 그것을 사양하고 물려진 접시의 음식을 먹었다. 그녀의 후원을 이렇게 좌절시키는 것은 날카로운 반격이었고, 나로 하여금 독립된 사람이라는 느낌이 들게 했다.\n\n아이들은 마음에 들었다. 소심했지만, 대체로 나를 따르려는 성향이었다. 그러나 그 집에는 유모가 하나 있었는데, 늘 명랑하고 쾌활한 척 요란하게 꾸며 대는 발그레한 얼굴의 여자였다. 그 여자는 두 아이를 다 길렀고, 내가 보기 전부터 아이들의 애정을 차지하고 있었다. 이 여자만 없었더라면 나는 거의 내 운명에 안주할 수 있었을지도 모른다. 나와 끊임없이 경쟁하며 아이들 앞에 자기를 내세우려는 그녀의 교묘한 술책에는, 내 처지에 놓인 많은 사람이라면 속아 넘어갔을 테지만, 나는 처음부터 그것을 꿰뚫어 보았다. 내 방을 정돈하고 시중을 들고 내 옷을 챙긴다는 (실제로 그 모든 일을 분주히 하긴 했다) 핑계로, 그녀는 한시도 자리를 비우지 않았다. 그녀의 그 많은 교묘한 수작 가운데 가장 약삭빠른 것은, 아이들이 나를 더 좋아하게 만들려 애쓰는 척하는 것이었다. 그녀는 아이들을 내게 데려와 내게로 어르며 보냈다. \"착한 웨이드 양에게 가렴, 다정한 웨이드 양에게 가렴, 예쁜 웨이드 양에게 가렴. 웨이드 양은 너희를 무척 사랑한단다. 웨이드 양은 영리한 분이라 책을 산더미처럼 읽으셨고, 내가 아는 것보다 훨씬 더 멋지고 재미난 이야기를 들려줄 수 있단다. 가서 웨이드 양 이야기를 들으렴!\" 이 무지한 술책에 내 가슴이 분노로 타오르는데, 어떻게 내가 아이들의 관심을 끌 수 있었겠는가? 아이들의 천진한 얼굴이 움츠러들며, 그 팔이 내 목이 아니라 그녀의 목을 휘감는 것을 보았으니, 어찌 이상하다 할 수 있었겠는가? 그러면 그녀는 아이들의 곱슬머리를 제 얼굴에서 떨쳐 내며 나를 올려다보고 말하곤 했다. \"아이들이 곧 정이 들 거예요, 웨이드 양. 아주 순수하고 다정한 아이들이랍니다, 마님. 그 일로 조금도 낙심하지 마세요, 마님.\" 나를 두고 의기양양해하며!\n\n그 여자가 한 짓이 또 하나 있었다. 이따금 그런 수법으로 나를 무사히 어두운 낙담의 골똘함 속으로 몰아넣었다 싶으면, 그녀는 아이들의 주의를 그리로 돌려 자기와 나의 차이를 보여 주곤 했다. \"쉿! 가엾은 웨이드 양이 편찮으시단다. 떠들지 마, 얘들아, 머리가 아프시대. 가서 위로해 드리렴. 가서 좀 나아지셨는지 여쭤 봐. 가서 누워 계시라고 말씀드려. 마음에 걸리는 일이 없으셨으면 좋겠네요, 마님. 너무 상심하지 마세요, 마님, 슬퍼하지도 마시고요!\"\n\n견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었다. 내가 혼자 있던 어느 날, 더는 견딜 수 없다는 심정이 극에 달했을 때, 마님이라는 그분이 들어왔고, 나는 떠나야겠다고 말했다. 그 도즈라는 여자가 곁에 있는 것을 견딜 수 없다고.\n\n\"웨이드 양! 가엾은 도즈는 당신에게 헌신적이에요. 당신을 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 거라고요!\"\n\n나는 그녀가 그렇게 말하리라는 것을 미리 알고 있었고, 그에 대한 대비도 단단히 되어 있었다. 나는 그저, 주인의 말을 반박하는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니며, 떠나야겠다고만 답했다.\n\n\"웨이드 양.\" 그녀는 늘 그토록 얄팍하게 감추어 오던 우월의 어조를 즉각 취하며 받았다. \"우리가 함께 지내는 동안 내가 한 말이나 행동 가운데, 당신이 '주인'이라는 그 거슬리는 말을 쓸 만한 빌미를 준 것이 결코 없기를 바라요. 만약 있었다면 전적으로 내 부주의였겠지요. 부디 무엇이 문제인지 말해 줘요.\"\n\n나는 주인에 관해서든 주인에게든 불평할 것은 없으나, 떠나야겠다고 답했다.\n\n그녀는 잠시 망설이더니, 내 곁에 앉아 자기 손을 내 손 위에 얹었다. 마치 그 영광이 모든 기억을 지워 주기라도 할 것처럼!\n\n\"웨이드 양, 당신이 불행한 것 같아 걱정이에요. 내 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없는 어떤 원인들 때문에요.\"\n\n나는 그 말이 일깨운 경험을 떠올리며 미소 짓고는 말했다. \"저는 불행한 성미를 타고난 모양이지요.\"\n\n\"나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일이든 설명하기에는 손쉬운 방법이지요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요. 내가 다가가려는 건 아주 다른 것이에요. 당신이 우리와 함께 마음 편히 지내지 못하는 것을 우리가 가슴 아프게 지켜보며, 내 남편과 나는 그 일에 관해 몇 마디 말을 나누었답니다.\"\n\n\"편하게요? 오! 마님, 당신들은 워낙 대단한 분들이시잖아요.\" 내가 말했다.\n\n\"내 의도와는 정반대의 뜻을 전할 수도 있는—그리고 분명히 그렇게 된—말을 쓰다니 운이 나빴네요.\" (그녀는 내 대답을 예상하지 못했고, 그 대답에 무안해했다.) \"내 말은 그저, 우리와 함께 행복하지 못하다는 뜻이에요. 꺼내기 어려운 화제이긴 하지만, 한 젊은 여자가 다른 젊은 여자에게 하는 말로서, 아마도—요컨대, 우리는 당신 자신이 누구보다도 결백한 어떤 집안 사정을, 당신이 마음에 두고 시달리도록 내버려 두는 게 아닐까 염려해 왔어요. 만약 그렇다면, 부디 그것을 슬픔의 원인으로 삼지 말기를 간청해요. 내 남편 자신도, 잘 알려진 대로, 예전에 법적으로는 누이가 아니었지만 모두에게 사랑받고 존경받던 무척 소중한 누이가 한 분 있었는데—\"\n\n나는 그들이 그 죽은 여인이 누구였든 그 여인을 생각해서 나를 받아들였다는 것을, 그리고 나를 두고 그것을 자랑거리로 삼고 나를 이용하려 했다는 것을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 유모가 그 사실을 알고 있다는 데서, 그녀가 그토록 나를 들쑤셔 댄 까닭이 부추겨졌음을 보았고, 아이들이 움츠러드는 데서, 내가 다른 사람들과 다르다는 어렴풋한 인상이 있었음을 보았다. 나는 그날 밤 그 집을 떠났다.\n\n지금 이야기와는 상관없는, 한두 차례의 짧고 아주 비슷한 경험을 거친 뒤, 나는 또 다른 집안으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서 내가 가르칠 학생은 단 한 명, 외동딸인 열다섯 살 소녀였다. 그 집 부모는 나이 지긋한 사람들로, 지체 있고 부유한 이들이었다. 그들이 길러 온 조카 하나가 많은 다른 손님들과 더불어 그 집에 자주 드나들었는데, 그가 내게 관심을 두기 시작했다. 나는 단호히 그를 물리쳤다. 그곳에 들어갈 때, 누구도 나를 가엾이 여기거나 내게 겸양을 베푸는 일이 없게 하겠다고 작정했기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 내게 편지를 보냈다. 그것이 우리가 약혼하기에 이르게 했다.\n\n그는 나보다 한 살 아래였고, 그 점을 감안하더라도 어려 보였다. 그는 인도에서 휴가를 받아 와 있었는데, 그곳에서 곧 아주 좋은 자리로 자라날 직책을 맡고 있었다. 여섯 달 뒤에 우리는 결혼해 인도로 갈 예정이었다. 나는 그 집에 머물다 그 집에서 결혼식을 올리기로 되어 있었다. 그 계획의 어느 부분에도 아무도 이의를 달지 않았다.\n\n그가 나를 흠모했다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없는데, 할 수만 있다면 하지 않으련다. 이 고백에 허영심은 조금도 관계가 없으니, 그의 흠모는 나를 괴롭혔기 때문이다. 그는 그것을 감추려는 수고를 조금도 하지 않았고, 그 부유한 사람들 사이에서 나로 하여금, 그가 내 외모를 보고 나를 사 들였으며 제 행동을 정당화하려고 그 구매물을 과시하는 것 같은 느낌이 들게 했다. 나는 그들이 마음속으로 나를 값매기며, 내 온전한 값어치가 얼마인지 알아내려 호기심을 품는 것을 알아차렸다. 나는 그들이 결코 알지 못하게 하리라 결심했다. 나는 그들 앞에서 꼼짝도 하지 않았고 침묵했다. 그들의 인정을 사려고 나 자신을 내보이느니, 차라리 그들 가운데 누구라도 나를 죽이게 내버려 두었을 것이다.\n\n그는 내가 나 자신을 제대로 대접하지 않는다고 했다. 나는 제대로 대접하고 있으며, 또 끝까지 그러려 하기에 그들 가운데 누구의 비위도 맞추려 굽히지 않는 것이라고 했다. 내가 그들 앞에서 당신의 애정을 과시하지 않았으면 좋겠다고 덧붙이자, 그는 걱정하다 못해 충격까지 받았다. 그러나 그는 내 마음의 평안을 위해서라면 자기 애정의 진솔한 충동마저 희생하겠다고 말했다.\n\n그는 그 핑계로 내게 앙갚음을 시작했다. 몇 시간이고 그는 나와 거리를 둔 채, 나 아닌 다른 누구와든 이야기를 나누곤 했다. 나는 저녁의 절반을 혼자 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채로 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그는 자기 어린 사촌, 즉 내 학생과 담소했다. 나는 그 내내 사람들의 눈빛에서, 저 두 사람이 그와 나보다 더 어울리는 한 쌍으로 보인다고 여기는 것을 알아챘다. 나는 그들의 생각을 헤아리며 앉아 있다가, 그의 젊어 보이는 모습이 나를 우스꽝스럽게 만든다고 느끼기에 이르렀고, 그를 사랑하게 된 나 자신에게 격분했다.\n\n한때 나는 정말로 그를 사랑했으니까. 그가 그럴 자격이 없었고, 그를 위해 내가 치른 그 모든 고뇌를—그를 평생토록 온전히, 고마운 마음으로 내 사람이 되게 했어야 마땅한 그 고뇌를—그가 거의 헤아리지 않았음에도, 나는 그를 사랑했다. 나는 그의 사촌이 내 면전에서 그를 칭찬하는 것을, 또 그 아이가 그것이 나를 기쁘게 한다고 여기는 척하는 것을—그러면서도 그것이 내 가슴에 사무친다는 것을 너무도 잘 알면서—그를 위해 견뎌 냈다. 그가 있는 자리에 앉아 내가 받은 온갖 멸시와 부당함을 떠올리며, 당장 그 집을 뛰쳐나가 다시는 그를 보지 말아야 하지 않을까 곰곰이 따지는 동안에도—나는 그를 사랑했다.\n\n그의 숙모(부디 내 주인이었음을 기억해 두시기를)는 신중하게, 고의로 내 시련과 괴로움을 더해 주었다. 그가 승진하면 우리가 인도에서 어떤 식으로 살게 될지, 어떤 살림을 꾸리게 될지, 어떤 손님들을 대접하게 될지를 늘어놓는 것이 그녀의 낙이었다. 내 자존심은 내 결혼 생활이 당시의 종속적이고 열등한 처지와 어떤 대조를 이룰지를 그토록 뻔뻔스럽게 들이대는 그 방식에 맞서 곤두섰다. 나는 분노를 억눌렀지만, 그녀의 의도가 내게 놓치지 않고 닿았음을 보여 주었고, 겸손한 척함으로써 그녀의 약을 올린 데 되갚았다. 나는 그녀에게, 당신이 묘사한 것은 분명 내게는 너무도 과분한 영광일 것이라 말하곤 했다. 그토록 큰 변화를 감당하지 못할까 두렵다고. 한낱 가정교사가, 그것도 당신 딸의 가정교사가, 그런 드높은 영예에 이르다니요! 내가 이런 식으로 대답하면 그녀는 불편해했고, 그들 모두 불편해했다. 그들은 내가 그녀의 속을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있음을 알았다.\n\n내 괴로움이 절정에 달했을 때, 그리고 그 때문에 내가 겪은 헤아릴 수 없는 고통과 굴욕을 그토록 하찮게 여기는 그의 배은망덕에 내 연인에게 가장 격분해 있을 때, 당신의 소중한 친구인 가우언 씨가 그 집에 나타났다. 그는 오래전부터 그 집과 가까운 사이였지만, 외국에 나가 있었다. 그는 한눈에 사정을 알아챘고, 또 나를 알아챘다.\n\n그는 내 평생 나를 이해한 첫 번째 사람이었다. 그가 그 집에 세 번 드나들기도 전에, 나는 그가 내 마음의 모든 움직임을 따라온다는 것을 알았다. 그들 모두에게, 그리고 내게, 또 그 모든 일에 대한 그의 차갑고 태연한 태도 속에서, 나는 그것을 분명히 보았다. 내 미래의 남편에 대한 그의 가벼운 흠모의 말 속에서, 우리의 약혼과 우리의 앞날에 대한 그의 열의 속에서, 우리의 장래의 부에 대한 희망에 찬 축하와 자기 자신의 가난에 대한 낙담 어린 언급 속에서—모두 똑같이 공허하고, 농담조이고, 조롱으로 가득한 그 말들 속에서—나는 그것을 분명히 보았다. 그는 내 주위의 모든 것을 늘 어떤 새롭고 가증스러운 빛 아래 내게 내보이면서도, 그것을 내 흠모와 자기 자신의 흠모를 위해 가장 좋은 면으로 드러내 보이는 척했고, 그렇게 나를 점점 더 분개하게, 점점 더 비참하게 만들었다. 그는 네덜란드 연작화 속의 차려입은 죽음 같았다. 그가 팔에 끼는 형상이 무엇이든—젊음이든 늙음이든, 아름다움이든 추함이든—그것과 춤을 추든, 노래를 부르든, 놀이를 하든, 기도를 하든, 그는 그것을 섬뜩하게 만들었다.\n\n그러니 당신도 이해하시리라. 당신의 소중한 친구가 내게 찬사를 보낼 때 그는 실은 나를 조위(弔慰)했고, 그가 내 괴로움을 어루만질 때 그는 내가 가진 쓰라린 상처를 하나하나 드러냈으며, 그가 내 '충실한 연인'을 두고 '세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 청년, 일찍이 뛰어 본 적 없는 더없이 다정한 심장의 소유자'라 선언할 때 그는 내가 우스꽝스럽게 되었다는 오랜 불안을 건드렸음을. 대단한 봉사는 아니었다고 말할지도 모르겠다. 그러나 그것들은 내 마음을 그대로 메아리치고 내 인식을 확증해 주었기에 내게 반가운 것이었다. 나는 곧 당신의 소중한 친구와 어울리는 것을 다른 무엇보다 좋아하게 되었다.\n\n(거의 그와 동시에 알아차린 것이지만) 여기서 질투가 자라나는 것을 알아채자, 나는 이 어울림이 더욱더 좋아졌다. 나는 질투에 시달려 오지 않았던가, 또 견뎌야 할 것은 모조리 내 몫이어야만 했던가? 아니다. 그도 그것이 무엇인지 알게 하라! 나는 그가 그것을 알게 되어 기뻤고, 그가 사무치게 느끼게 되어 기뻤으며, 또 그가 그러기를 바랐다. 그뿐만이 아니었다. 그는 가우언 씨에 비하면 시시했다. 가우언 씨는 나를 대등한 사람으로 대하는 법을, 또 우리 주위의 가련한 사람들을 낱낱이 해부하는 법을 알았다.\n\n이런 일이 이어지다가, 마침내 숙모, 곧 내 주인이 내게 한마디 하기로 마음먹었다. 거론할 가치도 거의 없는 일이며, 내게 아무 뜻도 없음을 자기도 안다고 했다. 그러나 그녀는, 넌지시 일러 주기만 하면 된다는 것을 알기에, 제 딴에 넌지시 일렀다. 내가 가우언 씨와 좀 덜 친하게 지내는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다고.\n\n나는 그녀에게, 어떻게 내게 아무 뜻도 없다고 장담할 수 있느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 내가 그릇된 뜻을 품지 않았다는 것이라면 언제든 장담할 수 있다고 답했다. 나는 고맙다고 했지만, 나 자신에 관해서는 나 스스로에게 장담하는 편을 택하겠다고 했다. 그녀의 다른 하인들은 좋은 평판을 고마워할지 모르나, 나는 그런 것을 원하지 않는다고.\n\n다른 대화가 이어졌고, 나는 그녀에게, 내게 넌지시 일러 주기만 하면 그대로 따르리라는 것을 어떻게 아느냐고 묻게 되었다. 그녀는 내 출신을 빌미로 삼는가, 아니면 나를 고용한 것을 빌미로 삼는가? 나는 몸도 영혼도 팔린 사람이 아니다. 그녀는 자기의 고귀한 조카가 노예 시장에 들어가 아내를 하나 사 왔다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n조만간 결국 그렇게 끝났을 일이지만, 그녀가 단번에 결말로 몰아갔다. 그녀는 짐짓 동정하는 척하며, 내가 불행한 성미를 타고났다고 말했다. 그 오래된 사악한 상처를 되풀이하자, 나는 더는 참지 않고, 내가 그녀에 관해 알고 그녀에게서 본 모든 것을, 또 그녀의 조카와 약혼한 그 비천한 처지에 놓인 이래 내 안에서 겪은 모든 것을 그녀에게 낱낱이 폭로했다. 나는 가우언 씨가 그 굴욕 속에서 내게 유일한 위안이었다고, 그것을 너무 오래 견뎠고 너무 늦게 떨쳐 냈다고, 그러나 이제 저들 가운데 누구도 다시는 보지 않겠다고 말했다. 그리고 정말로 다시는 보지 않았다.\n\n당신의 소중한 친구는 내 은신처까지 나를 따라와, 그 관계가 끊어진 일을 두고 매우 익살을 부렸다. 그러면서도 그 훌륭한 사람들(그 나름으로는 그가 여태 만난 가장 훌륭한 이들)에 대해 안타까워했고, 한낱 집파리를 형거(刑車)에 매달아 부수어야 하는 처지를 개탄했다. 그는 머지않아, 그리고 그때 내가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 진실하게, 자기는 그런 자질과 그런 인격의 힘을 지닌 여자에게 받아들여질 만한 가치가 없는 사람이라고 강변했다. 하지만—뭐, 글쎄—!\n\n당신의 소중한 친구는 자기 마음에 드는 동안만큼 나를 즐겁게 했고 자기 자신도 즐겼다. 그러고는 우리 둘 다 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이며, 둘 다 인간을 이해하고, 둘 다 낭만 따위는 없다는 것을 알며, 둘 다 분별 있는 사람들답게 제각기 다른 길로 운을 찾아 나설 준비가 되어 있고, 또 언제든 다시 마주치면 세상에서 가장 좋은 벗으로 만나리라는 것을 둘 다 내다보고 있노라고 내게 일깨웠다. 그가 그렇게 말했고, 나는 반박하지 않았다.\n\n오래지 않아 나는 그가 지금의 아내에게 구애하고 있으며, 그 여자가 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 끌려갔다는 것을 알게 되었다. 나는 그때 그 여자를, 지금 미워하는 것과 꼭 같은 만큼 미워했다. 그러니 당연히, 그 여자가 그와 결혼하는 것보다 더 바람직한 일은 없었다. 그러나 나는 그 여자를 한번 보고 싶어 안절부절못할 만큼 호기심이 일었다—어찌나 호기심이 일었던지, 그것이 내게 남은 몇 안 되는 즐길 거리 가운데 하나라고 느낄 정도였다. 나는 조금 여행을 했다. 그러다 그 여자의 일행 속에, 그리고 당신의 일행 속에 내가 끼어 있는 것을 발견하기에 이르렀다. 그때는 당신의 소중한 친구가 당신에게 알려져 있지 않았던 것 같고, 그가 당신에게 베푼 그 두드러진 우정의 표시들도 아직 당신에게 주지 않았던 것 같다.\n\n그 무리 속에서 나는 한 소녀를 발견했는데, 그 처지의 여러 정황이 내 처지와 묘하게 닮아 있었고, 그 성격 속에서 나는, 친절이니 보호니 자비니 하는 그 밖의 그럴듯한 이름을 내세우는 비대한 후원과 이기심에 맞서 일어나는—내가 내 천성에 내재한 것이라 묘사했던 그 기질을 많이 보고 흥미와 기쁨을 느꼈다. 또한 나는 그 소녀가 '불행한 성미를 타고났다'는 말도 자주 들었다. 그 편리한 문구가 뜻하는 바를 잘 이해하고 있었고, 내가 아는 것을 아는 동무를 원하던 터라, 나는 그 소녀를 속박과 부당함의 인식에서 풀어 주려 해 보기로 마음먹었다. 내가 성공했다는 것은 굳이 이야기할 필요가 없겠다.\n\n우리는 그 이후로 줄곧 함께 지내며, 내 얼마 안 되는 재산을 나누어 쓰고 있다.\n\n# CHAPTER 22. Who passes by this Road so late?\n\n아서 클레넘은 대단히 일이 몰린 와중에 칼레로 갔다가 헛걸음을 하고 돌아왔다. 세계 지도 위에서 값진 영토를 거느린 어느 미개한 권력이, 착상이 빠르고 실행이 단호한 기술자 한둘의 도움을 필요로 하게 되었던 것이다. 손닿는 곳에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 재료로, 자신의 재간이 필요하다고 판단한 사람과 수단을 만들어 낼 수 있는 실무가들, 그리고 그 목적 자체를 구상하는 데서만큼이나 그러한 재료를 목적에 맞게 응용하는 데서도 대담하고 풍부한 발상을 지닌 자들 말이다. 이 권력은 미개한 것이었던 까닭에, 위대한 국가적 목표를 에두름국 안에 처박아 두려는 생각이 전혀 없었다. 마치 독한 포도주가, 그 불기운과 젊음이 다 가시고 포도밭에서 일하며 포도를 짜던 일꾼들이 흙으로 돌아갈 때까지 지하실에서 빛을 피해 숨겨지듯이 말이다. 그 권력은 특유의 무지함으로, 어떻게 해낼 것인가에 관한 가장 단호하고 정력적인 관념에 따라 움직였다. 그리고 어떻게 안 할 것인가라는 저 위대한 정치 과학에 대해서는 조금도 경의를 표하지 않았으며 한 치의 봐줌도 베풀지 않았다. 실로 그 권력은, 그 기술을 실천하는 깨인 신민이 있으면 그 사람을 통해 이 후자의 기예와 신비를 단숨에 때려죽이는 야만적인 방식을 지니고 있었다.\n\n그리하여 필요한 사람들을 수소문해 찾아냈는데, 이는 그 자체로 가장 미개하고 변칙적인 일 처리 방식이었다. 찾아낸 다음에는 그들을 크나큰 신임과 명예로 대했고(이 또한 정치적으로 빽빽한 무지를 드러내는 것이었다), 당장 와서 해야 할 일을 해 달라고 청했다. 요컨대 그들은, 그 일이 이루어지기를 바라는 다른 사람들과 손잡고 그 일을 해내려는 사람들로 여겨졌던 것이다.\n\n대니얼 도이스가 그 선택된 이들 가운데 하나였다. 그때로서는 그가 몇 달을 떠나 있게 될지 몇 해를 떠나 있게 될지 내다볼 수가 없었다. 그의 출발 준비, 그리고 두 사람이 함께 벌인 사업의 온갖 세부와 결과를 그를 위해 양심껏 정리하는 일은 짧은 기간 안에 막대한 수고를 요구했고, 그 일이 클레넘을 밤낮으로 붙잡아 두었다. 그는 첫 짬이 나자 바다 건너로 슬쩍 다녀왔고, 도이스와의 작별 면담을 위해 또 그만큼 빨리 슬쩍 돌아왔다.\n\n아서는 이제 도이스에게, 두 사람의 손익과 부담과 전망의 상태를 정성껏 공들여 보여 주었다. 대니얼은 그 특유의 참을성 있는 태도로 모든 것을 살펴보고는 모조리 더할 나위 없이 감탄했다. 그는 그 회계 장부를, 자신이 여태껏 만들어 본 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 더 정교한 기계 장치라도 되는 양 검토했고, 그 뒤에는 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 머리 위로 무게를 가늠하듯 들었다 놓았다 하면서, 무슨 경이로운 기관을 골똘히 들여다보기라도 하는 양 그것을 바라보고 서 있었다.\n\n\"전부 아름답군요, 클레넘. 그 규칙성과 질서가 말입니다. 이보다 더 명료할 수가 없어요. 이보다 더 나을 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"마음에 드신다니 기쁩니다, 도이스. 자, 그럼 당신이 떠나 계신 동안 자본을 운용하는 일에 관해서, 그리고 사업이 그때그때 필요로 하는 만큼의 자본을 현금화하는 일에 관해서는—\" 그의 동업자가 그를 막았다.\n\n\"그 일이며, 그런 종류의 다른 모든 일이며, 전부 당신에게 맡깁니다. 당신은 그런 일이라면 지금까지 해 오신 대로 우리 둘을 위해 계속 처리해 주시고, 내가 많이 덜어 냈던 그 짐을 내 마음에서 더 가볍게 해 주시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 제가 자주 말씀드리듯이,\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"당신은 까닭 없이 자신의 사업 수완을 깎아내리시는군요.\"\n\n\"아마 그럴지도요.\" 도이스가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아닐지도 모르고요. 어쨌든 나는 그런 일들보다 더 많이 연구해 온, 그리고 더 잘 어울리는 천직이 있습니다. 나는 내 동업자를 완전히 믿고, 그가 가장 좋은 일을 하리라고 만족하고 있어요. 돈과 돈의 셈에 관해서 내게 하나의 편견이 있다면,\" 도이스는 장인 특유의 그 잘 굽는 엄지손가락을 동업자의 외투 깃에 얹으며 말을 이었다. \"그건 투기에 대한 반감입니다. 그 밖에 다른 편견은 없는 것 같아요. 아마 나는, 이 주제에 한 번도 마음을 온전히 쏟아 본 적이 없는 까닭에 그런 편견을 품게 된 것이겠지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그걸 편견이라 부르시면 안 됩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"이보세요, 도이스, 그건 가장 건전한 분별이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각해 주시니 기쁘군요.\" 도이스가 회색 눈을 다정하고 환하게 빛내며 대답했다.\n\n\"마침,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"바로 조금 전, 당신이 내려오시기 채 반시간도 전에, 여기 들렀던 팽크스에게도 똑같은 말을 하던 참이었습니다. 우리 둘 다, 안전한 투자에서 벗어나는 것은 흔히 악덕이라 불러도 마땅한 어리석음들 가운데 가장 흔한 것인 동시에 가장 위험한 것 중 하나라는 데 의견이 일치했지요.\"\n\n\"팽크스라고요?\" 도이스가 모자를 뒤로 젖혀 올리며, 신뢰가 가득한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그렇지, 그렇고말고요! 그 사람은 신중한 친구예요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 무척 신중한 친구지요.\" 아서가 대답했다. \"신중함의 표본이라 할 만합니다.\"\n\n두 사람은 팽크스 씨의 신중한 성품에서, 대화의 표면만으로 판단해서는 도무지 이해되지 않을 만큼이나 큰 만족을 끌어내는 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"자, 그럼,\" 대니얼이 시계를 보며 말했다. \"세월과 물때는 사람을 기다려 주지 않는 법이고, 나의 믿음직한 동업자여, 나는 봇짐이며 짐가방이며 아래 대문에서 떠날 채비가 다 되었으니, 마지막으로 한마디 하게 해 주십시오. 당신이 내 부탁 하나를 들어주셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 하실 수 있는 부탁이라면 무엇이든—다만,\" 클레넘은 재빨리 예외를 달았는데, 동업자의 얼굴이 그 예외를 재빨리 암시하고 있었던 까닭이다. \"당신의 발명을 포기하라는 것만은 빼고요.\"\n\n\"그게 바로 그 부탁입니다. 당신도 알고 있지요.\" 도이스가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 안 됩니다. 단연코 안 된다고 말하겠습니다. 일단 시작한 이상, 저는 그자들에게서 어떤 분명한 이유, 책임 있는 진술, 진짜 답변이라 할 만한 무언가를 받아 낼 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"그러지 못할 겁니다.\" 도이스가 고개를 저으며 대꾸했다. \"내 말을 믿으세요, 결코 못 할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"적어도 시도는 해 보겠습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"시도한다고 제게 해될 건 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"그건 나도 장담 못 합니다.\" 도이스가 설득하듯 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 응수했다. \"내겐 해가 되었어요, 친구. 그건 나를 늙게 했고, 지치게 했고, 속을 끓게 했고, 실망시켰습니다. 인내가 닳아 없어지고 자신이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 느끼는 건 누구에게도 좋을 게 없어요. 내가 보기엔, 벌써부터도, 지연과 회피에 헛되이 매달리느라 당신은 예전만큼 활기 있는 사람이 못 된 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"사사로운 근심이 잠시 그렇게 만들었을지는 몰라도,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"관청에 시달려서 그런 건 아닙니다. 아직은요. 저는 아직 상하지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"그러면 내 부탁을 들어주지 않으시겠다는 거군요?\"\n\n\"단연코, 안 됩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"훨씬 나이 많고 훨씬 더 예민하게 이해관계가 걸린 분이 그토록 오래 꿋꿋이 버텨 온 그 싸움터에서, 제가 이렇게 빨리 쫓겨나는 데 굴복한다면 부끄러울 겁니다.\"\n\n그를 움직일 수 없음을 알자, 대니얼 도이스는 그의 손을 마주 잡고, 사무실 안을 한 바퀴 작별의 눈길로 둘러본 뒤, 그와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다. 도이스는 사우샘프턴으로 가서 함께 여행할 소규모 인원에 합류하기로 되어 있었고, 짐을 그득 싣고 잘 꾸려진 마차가 그를 그곳까지 데려가려고 대문에 와 있었다. 일꾼들이 그를 배웅하러 대문에 나와 있었고, 그를 무척이나 자랑스러워했다. \"행운을 빕니다, 도이스 씨!\" 그 무리 가운데 한 사람이 말했다. \"어딜 가시든, 사람들은 자기네 가운데 진짜 사내가 하나 끼었다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 제 연장을 알고 제 연장도 그를 아는 사내, 의지가 있고 능력도 있는 사내, 이런 사람이 사내가 아니라면 대체 사내가 어디 있단 말입니까!\" 뒤편에 서 있던, 그런 방면에 무슨 재주가 있으리라고는 전혀 의심받지 않던 어느 무뚝뚝한 자원자의 이 일장 연설은 우렁찬 만세 삼창으로 환영받았고, 그 연설가는 그 뒤로 영원히 명사가 되었다. 우렁찬 만세 삼창이 한창인 가운데, 대니얼은 그들 모두에게 진심 어린 \"잘들 있게, 친구들!\"을 건넸고, 마차는, 마치 공기의 충격에 떠밀려 블리딩 하트 야드 밖으로 날아간 듯이, 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n밥티스트 씨는 신임받는 자리에 있는 고마운 작은 사내로서 일꾼들 틈에 끼어, 한낱 외국인이 할 수 있는 만큼 환호에 힘을 보탰다. 사실, 영국인만큼 환호할 줄 아는 사람은 이 세상에 없으니, 그들이 진심으로 환호할 때면 서로의 피와 기운을 어찌나 북돋우는지, 그 술렁임은 색슨족 앨프레드 이래로 모든 깃발이 일제히 나부끼는 가운데 그들의 온 역사가 밀려드는 것만 같았다. 밥티스트 씨는 그 기세에 어쩐지 휩쓸려 갔다가, 클레넘이 위층으로 따라 올라와 책과 서류를 제자리에 돌려놓으라고 손짓했을 때에야 잔뜩 겁먹은 모습으로 숨을 고르고 있었다.\n\n\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?\n마졸렌의 길동무여,\n\n이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?\n언제나 명랑하게!\"\n\n두 사람이 나란히 서 있던 동안 그 사내가 흥얼거리던, 어린아이들 놀이 노래의 이 구절을 그가 떠올린 것은, 결코 처음이 아니라 여러 번째였다. 그러나 그는 자신이 그것을 소리 내어 읊조렸다는 것을 너무도 의식하지 못한 터라, 다음 구절이 들려오자 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\"임금의 기사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 꽃이라네,\n\n마졸렌의 길동무여,\n임금의 기사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 꽃이라네,\n\n언제나 명랑하게!\"\n카발레토는 그가 더 떠오르지 않아 말이 막힌 줄로 알고, 공손하게 그 가사와 곡조를 일러 주었다.\n\n\"아! 자네도 그 노래를 아는가, 카발레토?\"\n\n\"바쿠스를 두고 맹세컨대, 압니다, 나리! 프랑스에서는 다들 알아요. 어린아이들이 부르는 걸 저는 여러 번 들었습니다. 마지막으로 그걸 제가 들었던 때는,\" 본디 카발레토였던 밥티스트 씨는, 기억이 고향 가까이로 갈 때면 으레 모국식 문장 구성으로 되돌아가곤 하면서 말했다. \"어느 어여쁜 작은 목소리에게서였지요. 작은 목소리, 아주 곱고, 아주 순진한. 알트로!\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 마지막으로 들었던 건,\" 아서가 대꾸했다. \"곱다는 것과는 정반대요, 순진하다는 것과도 정반대인 목소리에서였네.\" 그는 동행에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 그렇게 말했고, 그 사내가 뒤이어 했던 말을 되뇌며 혼잣말로 덧붙였다. \"맙소사, 나리, 참을성이 없는 게 제 천성이올시다!\"\n\n\"엇!\" 카발레토가 소스라치게 놀라 외쳤고, 순식간에 얼굴빛이 싹 가셨다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"나리! 제가 그 노래를 마지막으로 어디서 들었는지 아십니까?\"\n\n특유의 민첩한 몸짓으로, 그는 두 손으로 높다란 갈고리 코의 윤곽을 그렸고, 두 눈을 바짝 좁혀 모았으며, 머리카락을 헝클어뜨렸고, 윗입술을 부풀려 두툼한 콧수염을 흉내 냈으며, 가상의 망토 자락 무거운 끝을 어깨 너머로 휙 둘러 넘겼다. 이탈리아 농부를 지켜본 적 없는 사람으로서는 믿기 힘들 만큼 잽싸게 이 모든 동작을 해내면서, 그는 아주 기묘하고 음흉한 미소를 내비쳤다. 그 모든 변화가 한 줄기 빛처럼 그의 위로 스쳐 지나갔고, 바로 그 찰나에 그는 다시, 창백하게 놀란 얼굴로 자신의 후원자 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"운명과 경악의 이름으로,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"대체 무슨 소린가? 자네 블랑두아라는 이름의 사내를 아는가?\"\n\n\"아니요!\" 밥티스트 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"방금 자네는 그 노래를 들었을 때 곁에 있던 사내를 묘사한 것 아닌가? 그렇지 않나?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다!\" 밥티스트 씨가 쉰 번이나 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 그자가 블랑두아라고 불리지 않았다고?\"\n\n\"아닙니다!\" 밥티스트 씨가 말했다. \"알트로, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로!\" 그는 고개와 오른쪽 집게손가락을 동시에 내저으면서, 그 이름을 거부하고 또 거부해도 모자란 듯했다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 클레넘이 책상 위에 그 전단지를 펼쳐 놓으며 외쳤다. \"이 사내였나? 내가 소리 내어 읽는 걸 알아들을 수 있나?\"\n\n\"전부요. 완벽하게요.\"\n\n\"하지만 자네도 보게나. 이리 와서 내 어깨 너머로 보아, 내가 읽는 동안.\"\n\n밥티스트 씨가 다가와, 한 마디 한 마디를 그 재빠른 눈으로 좇으며, 더할 나위 없는 조바심으로 그 모든 것을 보고 들었다. 그러더니 무슨 해로운 짐승이라도 사납게 움켜잡은 듯 두 손바닥을 전단지 위에 철썩 내리치고는, 클레넘을 간절히 바라보며 외쳤다. \"그 사내예요! 보세요, 이자라고요!\"\n\n\"이건 내게는 자네가 상상하는 것보다 훨씬 더 중대한 일이라네.\" 클레넘이 몹시 동요하며 말했다. \"이 사내를 자네가 어디서 알게 되었는지 말해 주게.\"\n\n밥티스트 씨는 아주 천천히, 그리고 몹시 거북해하며 종이를 놓고, 두세 걸음 뒤로 물러서서, 손을 터는 시늉을 하더니, 무척 내키지 않는 기색으로 대답했다.\n\n\"마르실리아에서요—마르세유에서요.\"\n\n\"그자는 뭐였나?\"\n\n\"죄수였습니다. 그리고—알트로! 그래, 그런 것 같아요!—한 사람의,\" 밥티스트 씨는 다시 가까이 다가와 속삭였다. \"살인자였지요!\"\n\n그 말이 그에게 일격을 가하기라도 한 듯 클레넘은 뒤로 물러섰다. 어머니가 그 사내와 거래했다는 사실이 그토록 끔찍해 보였던 것이다. 카발레토는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 자기가 어쩌다 그런 추악한 무리에 끼게 되었는지 들어 달라고, 넘쳐나는 몸짓을 곁들여 그에게 애원했다.\n\n그는 그것이 어떤 자그마한 밀무역에서 비롯되었는지, 자기가 때가 되어 어떻게 감옥에서 풀려났는지, 그리고 그 전력에서 어떻게 떠나왔는지를 한 점 거짓 없이 이야기했다. 손강 변 샬롱에 있는 '동틀 녘'이라는 여인숙에서, 어떻게 밤에 침대에 누워 있다가 바로 그 살인자에게 깨워졌는지—그자는 본디 이름이 리고였으나 그때는 라니에라는 이름을 쓰고 있었다—, 그 살인자가 어떻게 둘이서 운명을 함께 묶자고 제안했는지, 그가 그 살인자를 어찌나 두려워하고 혐오했던지 날이 밝자 그에게서 도망쳐 나왔는지, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 살인자를 다시 보게 되어 아는 사이라고 붙들리지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 어떻게 시달려 왔는지를. 그가 자기네 언어 특유의, 그리고 클레넘에게 그 말을 조금도 덜 끔찍하게 들리게 하지 않는, 강조와 무게를 '살인자'라는 낱말에 실어 이 이야기를 다 마치고는, 별안간 벌떡 일어나 다시 그 전단지를 덮치더니, 북방 태생의 사람에게서라면 완전한 광기로 여겨졌을 격렬함으로 외쳤다. \"바로 그 살인자를 보세요! 여기 있잖습니까!\"\n\n격정에 휩싸인 황홀경 속에서, 그는 처음에는 자기가 최근 런던에서 그 살인자를 보았다는 사실을 잊고 있었다. 그가 그것을 기억해 내자, 클레넘에게는 그 알아봄이 어머니 집을 방문한 그날 밤보다 나중의 일이었으면 하는 희망이 떠올랐다. 그러나 카발레토는 시간과 장소에 관해 너무도 정확하고 분명해서, 그 일이 그날 밤보다 앞섰다는 데 의심의 여지를 조금도 남기지 않았다.\n\n\"잘 듣게.\" 아서가 아주 진지하게 말했다. \"이 사내는, 우리가 여기서 읽었다시피, 완전히 자취를 감추었네.\"\n\n\"그 점에 대해서는 저도 무척 만족합니다!\" 카발레토가 경건하게 눈을 치뜨며 말했다. \"하늘에 천 번 감사를! 저주받을 살인자!\"\n\n\"그렇지 않아.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"그자에 대해 무언가 더 들리기 전까지는, 나는 한시도 마음 편할 수가 없으니 말일세.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 은인이여. 그건 전혀 다른 문제군요. 백만 번 용서를 빕니다!\"\n\n\"자, 카발레토.\" 클레넘이 그의 팔을 가만히 돌려 서로 눈을 마주 보게 하며 말했다. \"내가 자네에게 해 줄 수 있었던 그 보잘것없는 일에 대해서도, 자네가 세상 누구보다 진심으로 고마워하는 사람이라는 걸 나는 확신하네.\"\n\n\"맹세합니다!\" 상대가 외쳤다.\n\n\"나도 알아. 만약 자네가 이 사내를 찾아내거나, 그가 어찌 되었는지 알아내거나, 그에 관한 그 어떤 최근 소식이라도 알아낼 수 있다면, 자네는 내가 이 세상에서 받을 수 있는 그 어떤 도움보다도 큰 도움을 내게 베푸는 것이고, 나를 (훨씬 더 마땅한 이유로) 자네가 내게 고마워하는 만큼 자네에게 고마워하는 사람으로 만들어 주는 걸세.\"\n\n\"어디를 찾아봐야 할지 모르겠습니다.\" 그 작은 사내가 황홀경에 빠져 아서의 손에 입 맞추며 외쳤다. \"어디서 시작해야 할지 모르겠어요. 어디로 가야 할지도 모르겠고요. 하지만, 용기를! 됐어요! 상관없습니다! 저는 갑니다, 바로 이 순간에!\"\n\n\"나 말고는 누구에게도 한마디도 말게, 카발레토.\"\n\n\"알-트로!\" 카발레토가 외쳤다. 그러고는 쏜살같이 사라졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams\n\n홀로 남겨진 클레넘은, 침례교도 밥티스트, 다시 말해 조반니 밥티스타 카발레토 씨의 표정과 몸짓이 또렷이 눈앞에 어른거리는 가운데, 고단한 하루를 맞이했다. 어떤 업무에든, 어떤 사고의 흐름에든 주의를 돌려 다잡으려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그의 생각은 그 떠나지 않는 화제에 닻을 내린 채, 다른 어떤 생각에도 붙들리려 하지 않았다. 마치 어느 죄인이 맑고 깊은 강 위에 멈춰 선 배에 사슬로 묶여, 수없이 많은 리그의 물이 곁을 흘러가도, 자신이 익사시킨 동료의 시신이 강바닥에 가라앉아, 소용돌이가 그것을 넓히거나 길게 늘이며 그 끔찍한 윤곽을 이제 부풀렸다가 이제 오므렸다 하는 것 말고는 꼼짝도 않고 변함도 없이 누워 있는 모습을, 영영 바라보도록 선고받은 것과도 같았다. 그렇게 아서도, 떠올랐다가 떠오르자마자 다른 것에 자리를 내주며 흘러가는 투명한 생각과 공상의 변덕스러운 물결 아래에, 한자리에서 흔들리지 않고 묵직하고 어둡게 가라앉아 있는, 온 힘을 다해 떨쳐 버리려 했으나 도무지 달아날 수 없는 그 하나의 주제를 보았다.\n\n블랑두아가, 본명이 무엇이든, 가장 못된 인간 가운데 하나라는 사실을 이제 확신하게 되니, 불안의 짐이 한층 무거워졌다. 설령 내일 그의 실종이 해명된다 하더라도, 자신의 어머니가 그런 사내와 연락을 주고받았다는 사실만은 바뀌지 않을 터였다. 그 연락이 은밀한 성질의 것이었다는 점, 그리고 어머니가 그에게 순종했고 그를 두려워했다는 점은, 자신 말고는 누구도 알지 못하기를 바랐다. 그러나 그것을 알고 있는 이상, 어떻게 그것을 자신의 오래되고 막연한 두려움과 떼어 놓을 수 있으며, 그런 관계에 사악한 구석이 전혀 없다고 어떻게 믿을 수 있겠는가?\n\n그 문제를 자신과 더불어 따져 보지 않겠다는 어머니의 결심, 그리고 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 성격을 잘 아는 까닭에, 그의 무력감은 더욱 깊어졌다. 어머니와 아버지의 기억 위로 수치와 폭로가 임박해 있는데도, 마치 놋쇠 벽에 막힌 듯 그들을 도울 길에서 차단되어 있다고 믿는 것은, 꿈에 짓눌리는 것과도 같은 압박이었다. 그가 고국으로 가져온 목적, 그 뒤로 줄곧 마음에 품어 온 목적은, 다른 어느 때보다도 절박하게 다가왔다고 느껴지던 바로 그 시점에, 어머니 자신의 더없이 단호한 결의로써 좌절되고 말았다. 그의 충고도, 정력도, 활동도, 돈도, 신용도, 그가 가진 온갖 수단이 모조리 쓸모없게 되었다. 어머니가 옛 전설 속의 힘을 지녀, 자신을 바라보는 자들을 돌로 만들었다 한들, 음울한 방에서 굽힐 줄 모르는 얼굴을 그에게 돌렸을 때보다 그를 더 철저히 무력하게 만들 수는 없었을 것이다(괴로운 마음에 그에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다).\n\n그러나 그날의 발견이 던진 빛이 이런 생각들 위를 비추자, 그는 분기하여 한층 결연한 행동의 길로 나아가게 되었다. 자기 목적의 정당함을 확신하고, 사방에서 좁혀 드는 임박한 위험을 느끼며, 그는 만일 어머니가 여전히 어떤 접근도 허락하지 않는다면 애프리에게 필사적으로 호소해 보기로 결심했다. 그녀를 입을 열게 만들 수 있고, 이 집을 뒤덮은 비밀의 주술을 깨뜨리는 데 제 몫을 하게 할 수 있다면, 한 시간 한 시간 지날수록 더욱 절실히 느껴지는 그 마비 상태를 떨쳐 낼 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 이것이 그날 하루 불안의 결실이었고, 날이 저물자 그가 실행에 옮긴 결정이었다.\n\n집에 다다랐을 때 그가 처음 맞닥뜨린 실망은, 문이 열려 있고 플린트윈치 씨가 계단에 앉아 파이프를 피우고 있는 것을 본 일이었다. 형편이 그저 그만큼만 순조로웠더라면, 애프리 부인이 그의 노크에 문을 열어 주었을 것이다. 형편이 유달리 어긋난 탓에, 문은 열려 있었고 플린트윈치 씨가 계단에서 파이프를 피우고 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하시오.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"안녕하시오.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다.\n\n연기는 플린트윈치 씨의 입에서 비뚤어진 채 흘러나왔다. 마치 그 비뚤어진 몸뚱이 전체를 한 바퀴 돌고 비뚤어진 목구멍을 거쳐 다시 돌아와서야 밖으로 나와, 비뚤어진 굴뚝에서 나는 연기와, 비뚤어진 강에서 피어오르는 안개와 뒤섞이려는 듯했다.\n\n\"무슨 소식이라도 있소?\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"소식이라곤 없소.\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n\"그 외국인 말이오.\" 아서가 설명했다.\n\n\"나도 그 외국인 말이오.\" 제러마이아가 말했다.\n\n목에 두른 크라바트 매듭이 귀밑으로 비뚜름히 가 있는 채, 그가 몸을 기울이고 선 모습이 어찌나 험상궂던지, 클레넘의 머릿속에는 한 가지 생각이 스쳤다 — 그것도 결코 처음이 아니었는데, 플린트윈치가 제 나름의 속셈으로 블랑두아를 없애 버린 것은 아닐까? 위태로운 것은 그의 비밀과 그의 안위가 아니었을까? 그는 작고 굽었으며 어쩌면 기운차게 힘이 세지는 않을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 오래된 주목나무처럼 질겼고, 늙은 갈까마귀처럼 심술궂었다. 그런 사내가, 훨씬 젊고 정력적인 사내 뒤로 다가가, 그를 끝장낼 의지를 품고 망설임 없이 임한다면, 늦은 시각의 그 외진 곳에서라면 꽤 확실하게 해낼 법도 했다.\n\n병적인 사고의 상태에서 이런 생각들이, 클레넘의 마음속에 늘 자리한 그 주된 생각 위로 떠다니는 동안, 플린트윈치 씨는 목을 비틀고 한쪽 눈을 감은 채 대문 너머 맞은편 집을 바라보며, 악의에 찬 표정으로 담배를 피우고 서 있었다. 즐기는 것이라기보다는 차라리 파이프 자루를 물어뜯으려는 것처럼 보였다. 그러면서도 그는 제 나름의 방식으로 그것을 즐기고 있었다.\n\n\"다음번에 들를 때는 내 초상화를 그릴 수 있겠구먼, 아서, 내 생각엔 말이야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말하며, 몸을 굽혀 재를 털어 냈다.\n\n다소 멋쩍고 당황한 아서는, 무례하게 빤히 쳐다보았다면 용서를 구한다고 했다. \"다만 내 마음이 이 일에 너무 쏠려 있어서,\" 그가 말했다. \"정신을 놓아 버립니다.\"\n\n\"하! 그래도 난 모르겠는걸,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 아주 느긋하게 받았다. \"어째서 그게 자네를 괴롭혀야 하는지 말이야, 아서.\"\n\n\"모르시겠다고요?\"\n\"모르겠어.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 아주 짧고 단호하게 말했다. 마치 개과의 짐승이라도 되는 양, 아서의 손을 덥석 무는 듯한 투였다.\n\n\"저 벽보들이 나붙은 걸 보는 것이 아무 일도 아니란 말입니까? 어머니의 이름과 거처가 그런 일과 엮여 여기저기 떠벌려지는 것을 보는 것이, 제게 아무 일도 아니란 말입니까?\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 가죽처럼 거친 뺨을 긁으며 받았다. \"그게 자네한테 그리 큰 의미가 있을 것 같진 않은데. 하지만 내가 무얼 보는지 말해 주지, 아서,\" 그가 위쪽 창들을 흘끗 올려다보며 말했다. \"자네 어머니 방에 불빛이랑 촛불 빛이 보이는구먼!\"\n\n\"그게 이 일과 무슨 상관입니까?\"\n\n\"이런, 여보게, 난 그걸로 이렇게 읽는단 말이야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 그를 향해 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"잠자는 개는 그냥 자게 두는 게 (속담에서 이르듯) 상책이라면, 사라진 개 역시 그냥 두는 게 똑같이 상책일지도 모른다는 거지. 내버려 둬. 그런 것들은 대개 머지않아 제풀에 나타나는 법이거든.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 이 말을 마치고 홱 돌아서더니 어두운 현관으로 들어갔다. 클레넘은 그 자리에 서서 눈으로 그를 좇았다. 그가 옆방의 작은 인광 성냥갑에 손을 넣어 불을 더듬어 찾고, 서너 번 더듬은 끝에 하나를 얻어, 벽에 걸린 흐릿한 등에 불을 붙이는 동안 줄곧. 그러는 내내 클레넘은 — 자기 스스로 그것을 떠올린다기보다는, 보이지 않는 손이 그에게 보여 주기라도 하듯 — 플린트윈치 씨가 그 더 음험한 짓을 저지를 만한 방법과 수단, 그리고 주위에 깔린 어둠의 그 검은 샛길 가운데 어느 하나로 그 흔적을 지워 버릴 가능성을 따라가고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 여보게,\" 신경질적인 제러마이아가 말했다. \"이제 위층으로 올라가시는 게 좋겠나?\"\n\n\"어머니는 혼자 계시겠지요?\"\n\n\"혼자가 아니야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"캐스비 씨와 그 딸이 함께 있어. 내가 담배를 피우는 동안 들어왔길래, 나는 남아서 마저 피운 거지.\"\n\n이것이 두 번째 실망이었다. 아서는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고 어머니의 방으로 향했다. 그곳에서는 캐스비 씨와 플로라가 차와 멸치 페이스트, 그리고 뜨겁게 버터를 바른 토스트를 들고 있던 참이었다. 그 진미들의 잔해는 식탁에서도, 애프리의 그을린 얼굴에서도 아직 치워지지 않았다. 그녀는 부엌에서 쓰는 토스트용 포크를 여태 손에 든 채, 무슨 우의적인 인물상처럼 보였다. 다만 그런 인물상들에서 흔히 보이는 것에 비해, 의미심장한 상징적 목적이라는 점에서 그녀가 상당한 우위를 차지하고 있다는 점만은 달랐다.\n\n플로라는 한동안 머무를 작정임을 드러내는 정성으로 보닛과 숄을 침대 위에 펼쳐 두었다. 캐스비 씨 역시 난로 선반 가까이에서 환히 빛을 발하고 있었다. 그의 자애로운 머리 혹들은 토스트의 따뜻한 버터가 그 가부장의 두개골을 뚫고 배어 나오기라도 하는 듯 번들거렸고, 그의 얼굴은 멸치 페이스트의 색소가 그 가부장의 면상에 번지기라도 하는 듯 불그레했다. 으레 하는 인사를 주고받으며 이 광경을 본 클레넘은, 미루지 않고 어머니에게 말을 건네기로 마음먹었다.\n\n어머니는 결코 방을 옮기는 일이 없었으므로, 그녀에게 따로 할 말이 있는 사람은 그녀를 책상 쪽으로 밀어 가는 것이 오랜 관례였다. 거기서 그녀는 대개 의자 등을 방 안 나머지 사람들 쪽으로 돌린 채 앉았고, 그녀와 이야기하는 사람은 한쪽 구석에, 늘 그 목적을 위해 그 자리에 놓아 두는 걸상에 앉았다. 모자와 아들이 제삼자의 개입 없이 단둘이 이야기를 나눈 지 오래되었다는 점만 빼면, 클레넘 부인에게 방해해서 미안하다는 한마디와 함께 사무적인 일로 말을 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 묻고, 그녀가 그렇다고 답하면 앞서 말한 자리로 밀고 가는 것은, 방문객들이 으레 겪는 예사로운 일이었다.\n\n그러므로 이제 아서가 그렇게 양해를 구하고, 그렇게 청하고, 그녀를 책상 쪽으로 밀고 가서 걸상에 앉자, 핀칭 부인은 그저 더 크고 빠르게 떠들기 시작했다. 자기는 아무것도 엿들을 수 없다는 점을 은근히 일러 주려는 것이었고, 캐스비 씨는 졸린 듯 차분하게 길고 흰 머리타래를 쓰다듬었다.\n\n\"어머니, 오늘 어떤 이야기를 들었는데, 어머니는 모르고 계신 일이라고 확신합니다. 또 어머니가 마땅히 아셔야 한다고 생각하는 일이고요. 여기서 제가 본 그 사내의 지난 행적에 관한 것입니다.\"\n\n\"네가 여기서 본 그 사내의 지난 행적이라면 나는 아무것도 모른다, 아서.\"\n\n그녀는 큰 소리로 말했다. 그는 제 목소리를 낮추었으나, 그녀는 다른 모든 친밀함의 시도를 거부하듯 이 시도도 거부하고, 늘 쓰는 음조로, 늘 쓰는 준엄한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"빙 둘러 들은 이야기가 아닙니다. 제게 곧장 전해진 이야기입니다.\"\n\n그녀는 아까와 똑같이, 네가 그것을 내게 말하려고 거기 있는 것이냐고 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"어머니가 아셔야 옳다고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그게 무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"그자는 프랑스의 어느 감옥에 갇혔던 죄수였습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 침착하게 대답했다. \"그럴 법도 하겠구나.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 죄인들을 가두는 감옥이었습니다, 어머니. 살인 혐의로 말입니다.\"\n\n그 말에 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐고, 표정에는 자연스러운 공포가 떠올랐다. 그래도 그녀는 여전히 큰 소리로 따져 물었다 —\n\n\"누가 네게 그런 말을 하더냐?\"\n\n\"그자와 함께 갇혀 있던 사내입니다.\"\n\n\"그 사내의 지난 행적은, 그가 네게 말하기 전까지는 너도 몰랐겠지?\"\n\"몰랐습니다.\"\n\n\"그 사내 자체는 알았으면서도?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"이 다른 사내를 두고 보자면, 나와 플린트윈치의 처지로구나! 하긴 그 닮음이 그렇게까지 꼭 맞지는 않겠지. 네게 그 일을 전한 자가, 돈을 맡겨 둔 거래 상대에게서 온 편지를 통해 네게 알려진 것은 아닐 테니까? 그 대목의 비교는 어떻게 되는 게냐?\"\n\n아서로서는, 자기에게 그 일을 전한 자가 그런 신원 증명서를 통해, 아니 실은 어떤 신원 증명서를 통해서도 자기에게 알려진 것이 아니라고 말할 수밖에 없었다. 클레넘 부인의 주의 깊게 찌푸려진 얼굴은 차츰 준엄한 승리의 표정으로 펴졌고, 그녀는 힘주어 반박했다. \"그렇다면 남을 어떻게 판단할지 조심해라. 너 잘되라고 하는 말이다, 아서, 남을 어떻게 판단할지 조심해라!\"\n\n그녀가 실은 말에 둔 힘 못지않게, 그 힘은 그녀의 두 눈에서도 나왔다. 그녀는 계속 그를 바라보았다. 그가 이 집에 들어설 때 조금이라도 그녀를 이겨 보려는 어떤 은밀한 희망을 품었더라면, 그녀는 이제 그것을 그의 가슴에서 노려보아 몰아내고 있었다.\n\n\"어머니, 어머니를 돕기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없습니까?\"\n\n\"없다.\"\n\n\"제게 어떤 신뢰도, 어떤 임무도, 어떤 해명도 맡기지 않으시렵니까? 저와 의논하지 않으시렵니까? 제가 가까이 가는 것도 허락하지 않으시렵니까?\"\n\n\"어떻게 그런 걸 내게 묻느냐? 너는 내 일에서 스스로 손을 뗐다. 내가 그런 게 아니라 네가 한 일이다. 어떻게 앞뒤 안 맞게 그런 질문을 내게 하느냐? 너는 나를 플린트윈치에게 맡겼고, 그가 네 자리를 차지하고 있다는 걸 알지 않느냐.\"\n\n제러마이아를 흘끗 본 클레넘은, 그가 벽에 기대어 턱을 긁으며, 정신을 쏙 빼놓을 만큼 어수선한 방식으로 온갖 주제를 — 거기서는 고등어며, 그네에 탄 에프 씨의 고모며, 풍뎅이며, 포도주 무역이 한데 뒤엉켜 버렸다 — 늘어놓는 플로라의 말에 귀 기울이는 척하고 있었지만, 그 각반을 보아도, 그의 주의가 자기들에게 바짝 쏠려 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"프랑스 감옥에, 살인 혐의로 갇혔던 죄수라.\" 클레넘 부인이 아들이 한 말을 차근차근 짚어 가며 되뇌었다. \"함께 갇혀 있던 그 사내한테서 들은 게 그게 전부냐?\"\n\n\"요컨대, 전부입니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 함께 갇혀 있던 그 사내는 그자의 공범이자 역시 살인자였단 말이냐? 하긴, 그자야 제 친구보다는 제 이야기를 더 그럴듯하게 했겠지. 물을 것도 없다. 이건 여기 나머지 사람들에게도 떠들 새 이야깃거리가 되겠구나. 캐스비, 아서 말이—\"\n\n\"멈추세요, 어머니! 멈추세요, 멈추세요!\" 그가 황급히 가로막았다. 어머니가 자기가 한 말을 공공연히 떠벌리리라고는 미처 상상도 못 했던 것이다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 못마땅하게 말했다. \"또 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"용서해 주십시오, 캐스비 씨 — 그리고 핀칭 부인, 당신도요 — 어머니와 잠깐만 더 이야기를 나누어야겠습니다 —\"\n\n그가 그녀의 의자에 손을 얹었다. 안 그랬으면 그녀가 발로 바닥을 디뎌 의자를 홱 돌려 버렸을 것이다. 두 사람은 여전히 얼굴을 마주하고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 그는 카발레토의 폭로가 세상에 알려진 일이 됨으로써 자기가 의도하지도, 예견하지도 못한 어떤 결과가 빚어질 가능성들을 머릿속에서 빠르게 짚어 보다가, 그 일은 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 서둘러 이르렀다. 다만 어쩌면 그를 이끈 것은, 어머니가 그것을 자기와 그 동업자만의 비밀로 묻어 두리라 당연히 여겼다는 것 이상의 뚜렷한 이유는 아니었을지도 몰랐다.\n\n\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 다시 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"어머니, 제가 전한 말을 되풀이하시라는 뜻은 아니었습니다. 그건 되풀이하지 않으시는 편이 낫겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그걸 내게 조건으로 다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"음! 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 똑똑히 봐라! 이걸 비밀로 만드는 건 너지,\" 그녀가 한 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"내가 아니다. 의심과 혐의와 해명해 달라는 간청을 여기 가져오는 건 너다, 아서, 비밀을 여기 가져오는 것도 너다, 아서. 그 사내가 어디 있었든, 무엇이었든, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이라고 생각하느냐? 그게 나와 무슨 상관일 수 있겠느냐? 온 세상이 알고 싶으면 다 알아도 좋다. 내겐 아무것도 아니다. 자, 이제 나를 놓아 다오.\"\n\n그는 위압적이면서도 의기양양한 그녀의 표정에 굴복하여, 의자를 밀어 왔던 자리로 도로 돌려놓았다. 그러면서 그는 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 의기양양함을 보았는데, 그것이 결코 플로라가 자아낸 것이 아님은 분명했다. 자기의 정보와 시도와 계획 전체가 이렇게 자기에게 도리어 불리하게 돌아간 일은, 어머니의 단호함과 굳건함보다도 더, 그녀를 상대로 한 자신의 노력이 헛되다는 것을 그에게 일깨워 주었다. 이제 남은 것은 옛 벗 애프리에게 호소하는 일뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 그 호소를 하는, 매우 미심쩍은 첫 단계에 이르는 것조차도, 인간이 떠맡을 만한 일 가운데 가장 가망 없는 축에 드는 것처럼 보였다. 그녀는 두 영리한 자의 손아귀에 너무도 완전히 쥐여 있었고, 둘 중 하나에게 늘 빈틈없이 감시당했으며, 게다가 집 안을 돌아다니는 것조차 너무 두려워했으므로, 그녀에게 단둘이 말을 걸 기회는 모조리 차단되어 있는 듯했다. 그뿐 아니라 애프리 부인은, 어떤 수단으로(짐작하기란 그리 어렵지 않았으니, 자기 주인 나리의 날카로운 논리를 통해서였다) 어떤 상황에서든 무어라도 입에 올리는 일이 위험하다는 생생한 확신을 얻은 터라, 줄곧 한구석에서 그 상징적인 도구로 접근을 막으며 버티고 있었다. 그리하여 플로라가, 아니 심지어 그 병녹색 가부장 본인이 한두 마디 건넸을 때조차도, 그녀는 마치 벙어리 여자처럼 토스트용 포크로 대화를 막아 냈다.\n\n애프리가 식탁을 치우고 다기를 씻는 동안 그녀의 눈길을 끌어 보려 여러 차례 헛수고를 한 끝에, 아서는 플로라가 발단을 맡아 줄 만한 한 가지 방편을 떠올렸다. 그리하여 그는 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"집을 한번 둘러보고 싶다고 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n그런데 가엾은 플로라는, 클레넘이 소년 시절로 되돌아가 다시 자기에게 미친 듯이 빠져들 그 순간을 늘 들떠 기대하고 있던 터라, 그 속삭임을 더없는 기쁨으로 받아들였다. 그 은밀한 성격 때문에 귀하게 여겨졌을 뿐 아니라, 그가 제 마음의 상태를 고백할 다정한 밀회의 길을 닦아 주는 것으로 여겼던 것이다. 그녀는 곧장 그 암시를 풀어내기 시작했다.\n\n\"아 이런 이 가엾고 낡은 방,\" 플로라가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다. \"예나 지금이나 똑같아 보이는군요 클레넘 부인 보고 있자니 가슴이 뭉클해요 다만 더 그을린 것만 빼면요 그야 세월이 가면 당연한 일이고 좋든 싫든 우리 모두가 예상하고 받아들여야 할 일이지요 저 자신도 분명 그래야 했으니까요 꼭 더 그을렸다기보다는 끔찍하게 더 살이 쪘는데 그게 그거거나 더 나쁜 일이지요 아빠가 저를 이리로 데려오시던 시절을 생각하면요 동상투성이 자그마한 계집애였던 제가 의자에 붙들려 앉아 발을 가로대에 올린 채 아서를 빤히 쳐다보던 — 부디 용서하세요 — 클레넘 씨 말이에요 — 더없이 끔찍한 주름 깃과 재킷 차림의 자그마한 사내아이였던 그를요 에프 씨가 아직 지평선 위 흐릿한 그림자로도 나타나기 전에 그 독일 어느 지방의 비(B)로 시작하는 그 유명한 유령처럼 추근거리던 일을 말이에요 그건 인생의 모든 길이 잉글랜드 북부의 그 길들과 똑같다는 도덕적 교훈을 가르쳐 주지요 거기선 석탄을 캐고 쇠며 뭐며를 만들고 길에 재를 깔잖아요!\"\n\n인간 존재의 덧없음에 한숨이라는 헌사를 바친 뒤, 플로라는 서둘러 제 목적을 향해 나아갔다.\n\n\"하긴 어느 시절에도,\" 그녀가 이어 갔다. \"이 집의 최악의 적이라 한들 이곳이 명랑한 집이라고는 못 했을 거예요 본디 그렇게 지어진 게 아니라 늘 무척 인상 깊은 집이었으니까요 그리운 기억이 떠올려 주는 건 젊은 시절의 어느 일인데 아직 분별이 무르익기 전에 아서가 — 굳어진 버릇이라 — 클레넘 씨가 곰팡내로 이름난 어느 안 쓰는 부엌으로 저를 데려가서는 거기다 평생 저를 숨겨 두고 휴일이라 집에 없을 때면 식사에서 몰래 빼돌릴 수 있는 걸로 먹이고 또 그 행복하던 시절에 너무도 자주 일어났던 일이지만 야단맞아 신세지면 마른 빵으로 먹이겠다고 한 일이에요 그 장면들을 되살려 보고 집 안을 거니는 걸 허락해 달라고 청하는 게 폐가 되거나 너무 지나친 부탁일까요?\"\n\n클레넘 부인은, 핀칭 부인이 거기 와 있어 준 그 호의 자체에 — 아서가 뜻밖에 닥치기 전까지만 해도 그녀의 방문은 의심할 바 없이 순전한 호의의 행위였지 자기만족이 아니었다 — 마지못한 우아함으로 응하며, 집 전체가 그녀에게 열려 있다고 일러 주었다. 플로라는 일어서서 동행을 청하듯 아서를 바라보았다. \"물론이지요.\" 그가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"애프리가 우리를 위해 불을 밝혀 줄 겁니다, 아마도.\"\n\n애프리가 \"내겐 아무것도 청하지 마요, 아서!\" 하며 손사래를 치는데, 플린트윈치 씨가 \"왜 안 돼? 애프리, 이 여편네야, 대체 뭐가 문제야? 왜 안 되냐고, 이 칠칠치 못한 것아!\" 하고 막아섰다. 이렇게 호통을 듣자, 그녀는 마지못해 구석에서 나와, 토스트용 포크를 남편의 한 손에 넘겨주고, 그가 다른 손으로 내민 촛대를 받아 들었다.\n\n\"앞장서, 이 멍청아!\" 제러마이아가 말했다. \"위로 갈 거요, 아래로 갈 거요, 핀칭 부인?\"\n\n플로라가 대답했다. \"아래로요.\"\n\n\"그럼 앞장서서 아래로 가, 이 애프리야.\" 제러마이아가 말했다. \"제대로 해, 안 그러면 내가 난간을 타고 굴러 내려가서 네 위로 자빠져 버릴 테니까!\"\n\n애프리가 탐험대의 선두에 섰고, 제러마이아가 후미를 막았다. 그는 그들을 떠날 마음이 전혀 없었다. 클레넘은 뒤를 돌아보다가, 그가 더없이 태연하고 더없이 빈틈없는 태도로 세 계단 뒤에서 따라오는 것을 보고는 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"저자를 떼어 놓을 방법은 없는 겁니까!\" 플로라가 얼른 대답하여 그의 마음을 안심시켰다. \"글쎄 딱히 예의에 맞진 않고 더 젊은 남자나 낯선 사람 앞이라면 생각도 못 할 일이지만 그래도 당신이 그토록 간곡히 바라신다면 저는 저 사람은 개의치 않겠어요 다만 부디 저를 너무 꽉 끌어안지는 말아 주세요.\"\n\n그것이 자기가 뜻한 바가 전혀 아니라고 차마 설명할 용기가 나지 않아, 아서는 플로라의 몸을 받쳐 주는 팔을 둘렀다. \"어머나 세상에.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신 정말 말을 잘 들으시는군요 정말로 그건 더없이 명예롭고 신사다운 일이에요 분명히요 하지만 그래도 동시에 그것보다 좀 더 꽉 안고 싶으시다면 저는 그걸 주제넘은 일이라고 여기진 않겠어요.\"\n\n그 어처구니없는 자세로, 그의 불안한 마음과는 이루 말할 수 없이 어긋난 채로, 클레넘은 집의 지하층으로 내려갔다. 그러면서 그는, 어디든 다른 데보다 더 어두워지는 곳에서는 플로라가 더 무거워지고, 집 안이 가장 환할 때도 그녀가 역시 무겁다는 것을 알아챘다. 더없이 음산하기 짝이 없는 그 우울한 부엌 구역에서 돌아 나오며, 애프리 부인은 등불을 들고 그의 아버지의 옛 방으로, 이어 옛 식당으로 옮겨 갔다. 그녀는 늘 따라잡을 수 없는 유령처럼 앞서 지나가기만 했고, 그가 \"애프리! 당신에게 할 말이 있어요!\" 하고 속삭여도 돌아보지도, 대답하지도 않았다.\n\n식당에서, 플로라는 소년 시절의 아서를 그토록 자주 삼켜 버리곤 했던 그 용(龍)의 벽장을 들여다보고 싶다는 감상에 사로잡혔다 — 그곳이 무척 어두운 벽장이라 무겁게 굴기에 알맞은 자리였으니 아마 그래서였으리라. 절망에 빠르게 가라앉고 있던 아서가 그 벽장을 막 열었을 때, 바깥문에서 노크 소리가 들렸다.\n\n애프리 부인은 비명을 억누르며 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어썼다.\n\n\"뭐야? 한 차례 더 맞고 싶은 게로군!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"그래, 받게 해 주지, 이 여편네야, 호되게 한 방 받게 해 주겠어! 오! 코가 뻥 뚫리게, 정신이 번쩍 들게 해 주마!\"\n\n\"그 사이에 누가 문을 열러 가긴 합니까?\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그 사이에, 내가 문을 열러 가지, 여보게.\" 노인이 어찌나 사납게 받던지, 이러지도 저러지도 못하는 형편에서 가고 싶지 않은데도 자기가 가야 한다고 느끼고 있음이 분명했다. \"다들 그동안 여기 있어! 애프리, 이 여편네야, 어리석게 한 치라도 움직이거나 한마디라도 지껄였다간, 받을 매를 세 배로 늘려 주마!\"\n\n그가 사라지는 순간 아서는 핀칭 부인을 풀어 주었다. 그 부인이 그의 의도를 오해하고, 느슨하게 하기는커녕 더 꽉 죄려고 채비를 갖추는 바람에, 다소 애를 먹어야 했다.\n\n\"애프리, 이제 내게 말해 줘요!\"\n\n\"내게 손대지 마요, 아서!\" 그녀가 그에게서 움츠러들며 외쳤다. \"가까이 오지 마요. 그가 당신을 볼 거예요. 제러마이아가요. 그러지 마요.\"\n\n\"그가 나를 볼 수는 없어요.\" 아서가 말에 맞춰 행동하며 받았다. \"내가 촛불을 끄면요.\"\n\n\"그가 당신 소리를 들을 거예요.\" 애프리가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그가 내 소리를 들을 수는 없어요.\" 아서가 다시 말에 맞춰 행동하며 받았다. \"내가 당신을 이 검은 벽장 안으로 끌어들여 여기서 말하면요. 왜 얼굴을 가리는 거예요?\"\n\n\"무언가를 보게 될까 봐 두려워서요.\"\n\n\"이 어둠 속에서는 아무것도 보게 될 리 없어요, 애프리.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 두려워요. 밝을 때보다 훨씬 더요.\"\n\n\"왜 두려운데요?\"\n\n\"이 집은 수수께끼와 비밀로 가득하니까요. 속삭임과 모의로 가득하니까요. 온갖 소리로 가득하니까요. 이런 소리투성이 집은 또 없을 거예요. 제러마이아가 먼저 날 목 졸라 죽이지 않는다면, 난 그 소리들 때문에 죽고 말 거예요. 그가 그럴 것 같지만요.\"\n\n\"여기서 그럴듯한 소리는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없는데요.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 당신도 이 집에 살면서 나처럼 집 안을 돌아다녀야 한다면 들을걸요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"그리고 그 소리들이 어찌나 입에 올릴 만한지, 입에 올리는 게 허락되지 않아서 그 때문에 터져 버릴 것만 같다고 느낄걸요. 제러마이아가 와요! 당신 때문에 내가 죽고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 애프리, 내가 엄숙히 맹세하건대, 나는 현관 바닥에 열린 문에서 들어오는 불빛이 보여요. 당신도 얼굴을 가린 걸 벗고 보면 보일 거예요.\"\n\n\"난 그럴 엄두가 안 나요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"절대 못 해요, 아서. 제러마이아가 안 볼 때면 난 늘 눈을 가리고 있어요. 가끔은 그가 볼 때조차도요.\"\n\n\"내가 보지 않고서는 그가 문을 닫을 수 없어요.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그가 오십 마일 떨어진 데 있는 것이나 마찬가지로 당신은 나와 함께 있으면 안전해요.\"\n\n[\"그가 그랬으면 좋겠어요!\" 애프리가 외쳤다.]\n\n\"애프리, 나는 여기서 무엇이 잘못되어 있는지 알고 싶어요. 이 집의 비밀에 약간의 빛을 비추고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"말했잖아요, 아서,\" 그녀가 가로막았다. \"소리들이 바로 그 비밀이에요. 바스락거리고 살금살금 돌아다니는 소리, 부르르 떠는 소리, 머리 위에서 나는 발소리, 발밑에서 나는 발소리요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 비밀의 전부는 아닐 텐데요.\"\n\n\"난 몰라요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"더는 내게 묻지 마요. 당신 옛 연인이 멀지 않은 데 있어요, 그 사람은 입이 가볍거든요.\"\n\n그의 옛 연인은 사실 손 닿을 만큼 가까이, 그때 마흔다섯 도쯤의 아주 실팍한 각도로 그에게 기댄 채 안절부절못하고 있었는데, 여기서 끼어들어 애프리 부인에게, 단호함보다는 간곡함이 앞서는 단언으로 확언했다. 자기가 듣는 것은 결코 더 멀리 새어 나가지 않고 어김없이 비밀로 지켜질 것이라고, \"다른 무엇 때문이 아니라도 아서 — 너무 허물없이 군 점은 주제넘은 줄 알지만 — 도이스 앤드 클레넘 상사를 봐서라도요.\"\n\n\"애프리, 당신에게 간곡히 호소합니다. 내게 몇 안 되는 즐거운 옛 기억 가운데 하나인 당신에게요. 내 어머니를 위해서, 당신 남편을 위해서, 나 자신을 위해서, 우리 모두를 위해서요. 마음만 먹으면 당신은 이 사내가 여기 온 것과 관련된 무언가를 내게 말해 줄 수 있을 거라고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 말해 주죠, 아서,\" 애프리가 받았다. \"제러마이아가 와요!\"\n\n\"아니, 정말로 오지 않아요. 문이 열려 있고, 그는 밖에 서서 이야기를 나누고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 말해 주죠,\" 애프리가 귀를 기울인 뒤 말했다. \"그가 처음 왔을 때 그 소리들을 자기 귀로 직접 들었어요. '저게 뭐야?' 하고 그가 내게 물었어요. '저게 뭔지 나도 몰라요,' 하고 내가 그를 붙들며 말했어요, '하지만 몇 번이고 들었어요.' 내가 그 말을 하는 동안 그는 온통 부들부들 떨면서 나를 빤히 쳐다보고 서 있었어요, 정말로요.\"\n\n\"그가 여기 자주 왔나요?\"\n\n\"그날 밤과 마지막 밤뿐이었어요.\"\n\n\"마지막 밤에, 내가 가고 난 뒤 그에게서 무얼 보았나요?\"\n\n\"그 영리한 두 사람이 그를 자기들끼리만 데리고 있었어요. 내가 당신을 내보낸 뒤, 제러마이아가 옆걸음으로 내게 춤추듯 다가왔어요(나를 해치려 할 때면 그는 늘 옆걸음으로 춤추듯 다가오거든요). 그러고는 내게 말했어요, '자, 애프리,' 하고요, '네 뒤로 다가가서, 이 여편네야, 너를 위로 몰아붙여 주마.' 그러더니 손으로 내 목덜미를 움켜쥐고 짓눌러서, 내가 입을 떡 벌릴 때까지 그러더니, 내내 짓누르면서 나를 앞세워 침대로 떠밀었어요. 그게 그가 말하는 '위로 몰아붙인다'는 거예요, 정말로요. 오, 그는 사악한 인간이에요!\"\n\n\"그리고 더는 듣지도 보지도 못했나요, 애프리?\"\n\n\"내가 침대로 보내졌다고 하잖아요, 아서! 그가 와요!\"\n\n\"분명히 말하지만 그는 아직 문가에 있어요. 당신이 말한 그 속삭임과 모의 말이에요, 애프리. 그게 다 뭐예요?\"\n\n\"내가 어떻게 알겠어요? 그것들에 대해선 아무것도 묻지 마요, 아서. 저리 가요!\"\n\n\"하지만 애프리, 내가 당신 남편을 무릅쓰고 또 내 어머니를 무릅쓰고 이 숨겨진 일들을 얼마간이라도 들여다보지 못한다면, 거기서 파멸이 올 거예요.\"\n\n\"내게 아무것도 묻지 마요.\" 애프리가 되풀이했다. \"난 아주 오랫동안 꿈속에 있었어요. 저리 가요, 저리 가요!\"\n\n\"아까도 그렇게 말했어요.\" 아서가 받았다. \"그날 밤 문가에서, 내가 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있느냐고 물었을 때도 똑같은 표현을 썼지요. 꿈속에 있다는 게 무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n\"말 안 할 거예요. 저리 가요! 당신 혼자였어도 말 안 할 텐데, 하물며 당신 옛 연인이 여기 있는데요.\"\n\n아서가 애원하는 것도, 플로라가 항변하는 것도 똑같이 헛수고였다. 애프리는 내내 부들부들 떨며 몸부림치다가, 모든 간청에 귀를 막고, 기어이 벽장 밖으로 몸을 빼내려고 작정했다.\n\n\"한마디라도 더 하느니 차라리 제러마이아한테 비명을 지르겠어요! 그를 부를 거예요, 아서, 당신이 내게 말 거는 걸 그만두지 않으면요. 자, 이제 그를 부르기 전에 정말로 마지막으로 할 말이 있어요 — 만일 당신이 그 영리한 두 사람을 당신 손으로 직접 이기기 시작한다면(당신이 처음 집에 돌아왔을 때 내가 말했듯이 그래야 마땅해요, 당신은 나처럼 오랜 세월 여기 살면서 목숨이 두렵도록 만들어진 게 아니니까요), 그럼 내 눈앞에서 그들을 이기세요. 그러고 나서 내게 말해 줘요, 애프리, 네 꿈들을 이야기해 봐, 하고요! 그럼, 어쩌면 그때 이야기해 줄지도 몰라요!\"\n\n문이 닫히는 소리에 아서는 대답을 멈췄다. 그들은 제러마이아가 두고 떠난 자리로 미끄러져 돌아갔고, 클레넘은 그 노인이 돌아오자 앞으로 나서며, 자기가 실수로 촛불을 꺼뜨렸노라고 일러 주었다. 플린트윈치 씨는 그가 현관의 등에 다시 불을 붙이는 것을 지켜보았고, 자기와 이야기를 나누고 있던 사람에 관해서는 깊은 침묵을 지켰다. 어쩌면 그의 노여움은, 그 방문객이 그에게 늘어놓은 어떤 지루한 잔소리에 대한 보상을 요구하고 있었는지도 몰랐다. 어찌 됐든, 그는 아내가 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰고 있는 것을 보고 어찌나 화가 났던지, 그녀에게 달려들어, 천에 덮인 코를 엄지와 손가락 사이에 잡고는, 제 온몸의 비트는 힘을 다 쏟아 비틀어 대는 듯했다.\n\n이제 줄곧 무거워진 플로라는, 그 둘러봄이 마침내 그의 옛 다락방 침실에까지 미칠 때까지 아서를 집 구경에서 풀어 주지 않았다. 그의 생각은 그 시찰 행차가 아닌 다른 데 쏠려 있었다. 그래도 그는 그때, 나중에 떠올릴 일이 생기게 되거니와, 집 안의 환기 안 되는 답답함과 갑갑함을, 그들이 위층 바닥들의 먼지 위에 발자국 자취를 남긴 것을, 그리고 어느 방문은 여는 데 저항이 있어서 애프리가 안에 누가 숨어 있다고 외쳐 댔고, 누군가를 찾았으나 발견되지 않았는데도 여전히 그렇게 믿고 있었던 것을, 유독 눈여겨보았다. 마침내 그들이 그의 어머니의 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 천에 감싼 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 난로 앞에 선 가부장과 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그들이 들어서자 가부장의 파란 눈과 반들거리는 머리와 비단결 같은 머리타래가 그들 쪽으로 돌려졌고, 그것이 그의 말에 헤아릴 수 없는 가치와 동족에 대한 마르지 않는 사랑을 부여했다.\n\n\"그래, 집을 둘러보고 오셨군요, 집을 둘러보고 — 집을 — 집을 둘러보고 오셨군요!\"\n\n그 자체로는 자비의 보석도 지혜의 보석도 아니었으나, 그는 그것을 둘 다의 본보기로 만들어, 누구라도 한 부 베껴 두고 싶어질 만한 것으로 빚어냈다.\n\n# CHAPTER 24. The Evening of a Long Day\n\n저 명망 높은 인물이자 위대한 국가적 장식물인 머들 씨는 빛나는 행로를 이어 갔다. 사회에서 그토록 많은 돈을 벌어 줌으로써 사회에 그토록 훌륭한 봉사를 한 사람을 평민으로 남겨 둘 수는 없다는 인식이 널리 퍼지기 시작했다. 준남작 작위가 자신 있게 거론되었고, 귀족 작위도 자주 입에 오르내렸다. 소문에 따르면 머들 씨는 준남작 작위에 대해 그 황금빛 얼굴을 굳혔으며, 데시머스 경에게 준남작 작위로는 자신에게 충분치 않다고 분명히 비쳤고, \"아니오 — 귀족 작위든가, 아니면 그냥 머들이든가\" 하고 말했다는 것이었다. 이 말은 그토록 고귀한 인물이 가라앉을 수 있는 한 가장 깊이, 그 고귀한 턱밑까지 데시머스 경을 의혹의 수렁에 빠뜨렸다고 전해졌다. 왜냐하면 배너클 일가는 그 자체로 창조의 한 무리로서, 그러한 영예가 자기들에게 속한다는 생각을 품고 있었기 때문이다. 군인이나 선원이나 법률가가 작위를 받게 되면, 그들은 말하자면 겸양의 행위로써 그 사람을 가문의 문턱으로 들여보냈다가 곧 다시 문을 닫아 버린다는 식이었다. (소문에 의하면) 곤혹스러운 데시머스에게는 이 인식에 대한 자신의 세습적 몫이 있을 뿐 아니라, 그는 이미 명부에 올라 있는 몇몇 배너클 가문의 청구가 그 거장의 청구와 충돌한다는 것도 알고 있었다. 옳든 그르든, 소문은 무척 바빴다. 그리고 데시머스 경은 그 난제를 위풍당당하게 궁리하는 중이거나 그러하리라 여겨지는 동안, 여러 공식 석상에서 무성하게 자라난 문장들의 정글을 헤치며 코끼리처럼 육중하게 종종걸음을 치고, 머들 씨를 그 코로 거대한 사업, 영국의 부, 탄력성, 신용, 자본, 번영, 그리고 온갖 종류의 축복으로 흔들어 보임으로써 소문에 다소 신빙성을 더해 주었다.\n\n낡은 큰 낫의 베어 넘김은 어찌나 조용히 진행되었던지, 두 영국인 형제가 로마의 외국인 묘지 한 무덤에 나란히 묻힌 이래로 꼬박 석 달이 알아채이지 않은 채 흘러갔다. 스파클러 부부는 자기들 집에 자리를 잡았다. 그것은 자그마한 저택으로, 다분히 타이트 배너클 부류에 속하는, 불편함의 완벽한 승리이자, 그제 끓인 수프와 마차용 말 냄새가 영영 가시지 않는 곳이었으나, 사람이 살 만한 지구의 정중앙에 위치한다는 이유로 무척이나 비쌌다. 이 부러움 살 만한 거처에서(실제로도 많은 사람이 부러워했다) 스파클러 부인은 즉시 그 가슴을 허물어뜨리는 일에 착수할 작정이었으나, 그 활발한 적대 행위는 부고를 전하는 급사가 도착하는 바람에 중단되었다. 무정한 사람은 아니었던 스파클러 부인은 그 소식을 격렬한 슬픔의 폭발로 받아들였고, 그것은 열두 시간 동안 지속되었다. 그 후 그녀는 일어나 상복을 챙기고, 그것이 머들 부인의 것만큼이나 잘 어울리도록 보장할 수 있는 온갖 예방 조치를 취했다. 그리하여(가장 점잖은 소식통에 따르면) 한 명 이상의 저명한 가문에 우울이 드리워졌고, 급사는 다시 돌아갔다.\n\n스파클러 부부는 자기들에게 드리운 우울 속에서 단둘이 만찬을 마쳤고, 스파클러 부인은 응접실 소파에 기대어 누워 있었다. 무더운 여름 일요일 저녁이었다. 사람이 살 만한 지구의 정중앙에 자리한 그 거처는, 머리에 고칠 수 없는 감기라도 든 양 늘 답답하고 막혀 있었지만, 그날 저녁은 유독 숨이 막혔다. 교회들의 종은 거리의 가락 없는 메아리 속에서 땡땡거리며 그 최악의 짓을 다 해 놓았고, 교회들의 불 밝힌 창들은 잿빛 어스름 속에서 노란빛을 잃고는 불투명한 검정으로 사그라들었다. 스파클러 부인은 소파에 누워, 열린 창 너머로 좁은 길 맞은편을, 목서초와 꽃들이 담긴 상자들 위로 바라보다가 그 풍경에 싫증이 났다. 스파클러 부인은 남편이 발코니에 서 있는 다른 창을 바라보다가 그 풍경에도 싫증이 났다. 스파클러 부인은 상복 차림의 자기 자신을 바라보다가 그 풍경에마저 싫증이 났다. 물론 그 풍경에는 앞의 두 가지만큼 싫증이 나지는 않았지만 말이다.\n\n\"우물 속에 누워 있는 것 같아.\" 스파클러 부인이 짜증스레 자세를 바꾸며 말했다. \"어머나, 에드먼드, 할 말이 있으면 왜 하지 않는 거예요?\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 솔직하게 \"여보, 나는 할 말이 없소\" 하고 대꾸할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 재치 있는 말이 떠오르지 않았으므로, 그는 발코니에서 들어와 아내의 침상 옆에 서는 것으로 만족했다.\n\n\"세상에, 에드먼드!\" 스파클러 부인이 더욱 짜증스레 말했다. \"당신, 지금 목서초를 코에 처박고 있잖아요! 제발 그러지 마요!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 정신이 딴 데 가 있던 차에 — 어쩌면 흔히 그 표현으로 이해되는 것보다 더 문자 그대로 정신이 나간 채로 — 손에 든 작은 가지를 너무 세게 냄새 맡다가 그만 문제의 그 무례 직전에 이르렀던 것이다. 그는 미소 지으며 \"미안하오, 여보\" 하고는 그것을 창밖으로 던졌다.\n\n\"당신이 그 자세로 서 있으면 머리가 아파요, 에드먼드.\" 스파클러 부인이 또 일 분쯤 지난 뒤 그를 올려다보며 말했다. \"이 불빛 아래서 당신은 정말이지 거슬리게 커 보여요. 좀 앉아요.\"\n\n\"그러지, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 말하며 바로 그 자리에 의자를 가져다 앉았다.\n\n\"가장 긴 날이 이미 지났다는 걸 몰랐다면,\" 패니가 음울하게 하품하며 말했다. \"오늘이 분명 가장 긴 날이라고 확신했을 거예요. 이런 날은 한 번도 겪어 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"이게 당신 부채요, 여보?\" 스파클러 씨가 부채 하나를 집어 건네며 물었다.\n\n\"에드먼드,\" 그의 아내가 한층 더 지친 어조로 대꾸했다. \"제발 부탁이니 어리석은 질문은 하지 말아요. 내 것 말고 누구 것이겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래, 당신 것인 줄 알았소.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 묻지 말았어야지요.\" 패니가 쏘아붙였다. 잠시 후 그녀는 소파에서 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"어머나, 어머나, 이렇게 긴 날은 또 없었어!\" 또 잠시 후 그녀는 천천히 일어나 방 안을 거닐다가 다시 돌아왔다.\n\n\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 기발한 착상이 번뜩이는 표정으로 말했다. \"당신, 좀 안절부절못하는 것 같소.\"\n\n\"어머, 안절부절!\" 스파클러 부인이 되받았다. \"그러지 마요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑스러운 여인,\" 스파클러 씨가 권했다. \"방향초 식초를 좀 써 봐요. 내 어머니가 그걸 쓰는 걸 자주 봤는데, 보아하니 기운을 회복하시더군. 그리고 당신도 알다시피, 어머니는 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없는 참으로—\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 패니가 또다시 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"더는 못 참겠어! 이날은 세상에 동튼 날 중에서 가장 지긋지긋한 날이에요, 분명히.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 아내가 방 안을 어슬렁거리는 것을 온순하게 지켜보았고, 다소 겁먹은 듯이 보였다. 그녀가 자질구레한 물건 몇 개를 이리저리 던지고, 세 창문 모두로 어두워져 가는 거리를 내려다본 뒤, 그녀는 소파로 돌아와 쿠션들 사이로 몸을 던졌다.\n\n\"자 에드먼드, 이리 와요! 좀 더 가까이 와요. 부채로 당신을 건드릴 수 있어야 내가 하려는 말을 당신 머리에 단단히 박아 넣을 테니까. 됐어요. 아주 가깝군요. 어머, 당신은 정말 어쩌면 그렇게 커 보일까!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 그 점에 대해 사과하며, 어쩔 도리가 없다고 변명하고는, \"우리 패거리\"가 — 누구의 패거리인지는 더 구체적으로 밝히지 않은 채 — 자기를 퀸버스 플레스트린 주니어, 또는 젊은 인간 산이라는 이름으로 부르곤 했다고 말했다.\n\n\"진작 그 얘기를 했어야지요.\" 패니가 불평했다.\n\n\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 다소 흐뭇해하며 대꾸했다. \"그게 당신 흥미를 끌 줄 몰랐소. 알았더라면 꼭 얘기했을 텐데.\"\n\n\"됐어요! 제발이지, 말하지 마요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"내가 직접 말하고 싶으니까. 에드먼드, 우리 더는 단둘이 있어선 안 돼요. 오늘 저녁 내가 빠진 이 끔찍한 우울의 상태로 다시는 떨어지지 않도록 그런 예방 조치를 취해야겠어요.\"\n\n\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"당신은 잘 알려졌다시피, 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없는 참으로 훌륭한 여인이—\"\n\n\"아이, 맙소사!\" 패니가 소리쳤다.\n\n스파클러 씨는 이 외침의 기세에, 그리고 그것에 따라온 소파에서 펄쩍 일어났다 펄쩍 다시 앉는 동작에 어찌나 당황했던지, 일이 분이 지나서야 비로소 변명조로 이렇게 말할 수 있을 만한 상태가 되었다.\n\n\"내 말은, 여보, 당신이 사교계에서 빛나도록 타고난 사람이라는 걸 누구나 안다는 거요.\"\n\n\"사교계에서 빛나도록 타고났다고요.\" 패니가 몹시 신경질적으로 되받았다. \"그래요, 정말이지! 그런데 그래서 어떻게 되었나요? 불쌍한 아버지의 죽음과 가엾은 삼촌의 죽음이 준 충격에서 방문 다닐 만큼 막 회복하자마자 — 물론 마지막 건 다행스러운 해방이었다는 걸 스스로 부정하지는 않지만요. 남 앞에 내놓을 만하지 못하다면 차라리 죽는 편이 훨씬 나으니까—\"\n\n\"여보, 설마 나를 두고 하는 말은 아니겠지요?\" 스파클러 씨가 겸손하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"에드먼드, 에드먼드, 당신은 성인이라도 진을 빼겠어요. 내가 가엾은 삼촌 얘기를 하고 있다고 분명히 말하지 않았어요?\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 어찌나 표정 가득 쳐다보던지, 여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"좀 불편했소. 고맙소, 여보.\"\n\n\"이제 당신이 내 말을 끊어 놨네요.\" 패니가 단념한 듯 부채를 휙 내저으며 말했다. \"차라리 자러 가는 게 낫겠어요.\"\n\n\"그러지 마오, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 권했다. \"천천히 해요.\"\n\n패니는 한참을 들였다. 눈을 감고 뒤로 기댄 채, 마치 이 세상사를 완전히 포기해 버린 듯 절망적인 표정으로 눈썹을 치켜올리고 있었다. 마침내 아무런 예고도 없이 그녀는 다시 눈을 뜨고는 짧고 날카로운 어조로 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그래서 어떻게 되었느냐고요, 묻겠어요! 어떻게 되었나요? 글쎄, 내가 사교계에서 가장 빛날 수 있을 바로 그 시기에, 그리고 무척 중대한 이유로 사교계에서 가장 빛나고 싶을 바로 그때에 — 어느 정도까지는 사교계에 나갈 자격을 잃게 만드는 처지에 놓인 나를 발견하게 되었지요. 정말 너무하잖아요!\"\n\n\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"그것 때문에 집에만 있어야 한다고는 생각하지 않소.\"\n\n\"에드먼드, 우스꽝스러운 양반 같으니라고.\" 패니가 큰 분노를 담아 대꾸했다. \"한창 젊음의 꽃을 피우고 있고 개인적인 매력도 전혀 없지 않은 여자가, 이런 때에, 모든 면에서 자기보다 못한 여자와 자태를 두고 경쟁에 나설 수 있다고 생각하는 거예요? 정말 그렇게 생각한다면, 당신의 어리석음은 끝이 없군요.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 \"그건 넘어갈 수 있을지도\" 모른다고 생각했다고 조심스럽게 내놓았다.\n\n\"넘어가요!\" 패니가 헤아릴 수 없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다.\n\n\"한동안만 말이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 조심스레 덧붙였다.\n\n이 마지막 힘없는 제안에는 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않은 채, 스파클러 부인은 쓰디쓴 어조로 정말이지 너무한 일이며, 차라리 죽었으면 싶을 만큼 지긋지긋한 일이라고 단언했다!\n\n\"하지만,\" 그녀는 개인적으로 부당한 대우를 받았다는 느낌에서 어느 정도 회복되자 말했다. \"분통이 터지고 잔인하게 여겨지긴 해도, 이건 받아들일 수밖에 없겠지요.\"\n\n\"더구나 예상했던 일이고 말이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"에드먼드,\" 그의 아내가 대꾸했다. \"역경에 처한 자기 손으로 당신을 영광스럽게 해 준 여인을 모욕하려 드는 것 말고는 더 어울리는 할 일이 없다면, 차라리 당신이 자러 가는 게 낫겠어요!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 그 비난에 크게 괴로워하며, 더없이 다정하고 진지한 사과를 올렸다. 그의 사과는 받아들여졌으나, 스파클러 부인은 그에게 소파 반대편으로 돌아가 창문 커튼 안에 앉아 기세를 좀 가라앉히라고 청했다.\n\n\"자, 에드먼드,\" 그녀가 부채를 뻗어 팔 길이만큼 떨어진 채 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"당신이 늘 그렇듯 지루하게 늘어놓으며 사람을 괴롭히기 시작했을 때 내가 하려던 말은, 우리가 더는 단둘이 있지 않도록 내가 단속하겠다는 것, 그리고 사정상 내가 만족스럽게 외출할 수 없을 때에는 누군가가 항상 여기 와 있도록 조처하겠다는 거예요. 정말이지 오늘 같은 날을 또 겪는 건 못 견디겠고, 견디지 않을 거니까요.\"\n\n그 계획에 대한 스파클러 씨의 의견은, 한마디로 그것이 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없다는 것이었다. 그는 덧붙였다. \"게다가 말이오, 머잖아 당신 여동생이 올 것 같지 않소—\"\n\n\"사랑스러운 에이미, 그래요!\" 스파클러 부인이 애정 어린 한숨을 쉬며 외쳤다. \"귀여운 우리 아가! 하지만, 에이미 혼자로는 여기서 어림없지요.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 의문조로 \"아니라고?\" 하고 말하려 했으나, 위험을 감지하고는 동의조로 말했다. \"그래, 아 그렇고말고, 그 애 혼자로는 여기서 어림없지.\"\n\n\"그래요, 에드먼드. 그 소중한 아이의 미덕은, 그토록 차분한 성품이라서 대조가 필요하거든요 — 그 미덕을 제 빛깔로 드러내고 무엇보다 사랑스럽게 만들려면 주위에 생기와 움직임이 필요하지요. 게다가 그 애는 여러 가지 이유로 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요.\"\n\n\"바로 그거요.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"분발시킨다.\"\n\n\"제발 그러지 마요, 에드먼드! 세상에 할 말이라곤 조금도 없으면서 끼어드는 당신 버릇은 사람을 산만하게 해요. 그 버릇을 고쳐야 해요. 에이미 얘기로 돌아가자면 — 가엾은 내 어린 동생은 불쌍한 아버지께 헌신적으로 매여 있었으니, 분명 그분을 잃고 몹시 비탄에 잠겼을 테고 무척 슬퍼했을 거예요. 나도 그랬어요. 끔찍하게 느꼈지요. 하지만 에이미는 분명 더 절절히 느꼈을 거예요. 그 애는 내내 그곳에 있었고, 불쌍한 아버지의 임종을 지켰으니까요. 안타깝게도 나는 그러지 못했고요.\"\n\n여기서 패니는 멈춰 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. \"사랑하고 사랑하는, 그리운 아버지! 그분은 얼마나 진정으로 신사다우셨던지! 가엾은 삼촌과는 얼마나 대조적이었던지!\"\n\n\"그 힘든 시기의 여파로,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"착한 내 어린 생쥐는 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요. 또한 에드워드의 병환을 오래 간호해 온 여파로도 그래요. 그 간호는 아직 끝나지도 않았고, 어쩌면 더 오래 이어질지도 모르며, 그러는 사이에 불쌍한 아버지의 재산 정리가 늦춰져 우리 모두를 불안하게 만들고 있어요. 그래도 다행히, 아버지가 섭리에 따라 영국에 오셨을 때 두고 가신 그대로 이곳 대리인들에게 있는 서류들이 모두 봉인되고 잠겨 있어서, 그 일들은 오빠 에드워드가 시칠리아에서 건강을 회복해 건너와 관리하든 집행하든, 해야 할 일이 무엇이든 그것을 할 수 있을 때까지 기다릴 수 있는 정돈된 상태에 있지요.\"\n\n\"오빠를 일으켜 세우는 데 그 애보다 더 나은 간병인은 없을 거요.\" 스파클러 씨가 대담하게 의견을 내놓았다.\n\n\"웬일로 당신 말에 동의할 수 있겠네요.\" 그의 아내가 나른하게 눈꺼풀을 그쪽으로 조금 돌리며(그녀는 대개 응접실 가구를 향해 말하듯 했다) 대꾸했다. \"당신 말을 그대로 받아들일 수 있겠어요. 오빠를 일으켜 세우는 데 그 애보다 더 나은 간병인은 없을 거예요. 활동적인 정신을 가진 사람에게 내 사랑스러운 동생이 좀 지치게 굴 때도 있긴 하지만, 간병인으로서는 그 애는 완벽함 그 자체예요. 에이미들 중의 최고지!\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 최근의 성공에 우쭐해져서는, 맙소사, 에드워드가 한참을 호되게 앓았지 않소, 여보, 하고 말했다.\n\n\"호되게 앓는다는 게, 에드먼드,\" 스파클러 부인이 대꾸했다. \"몸져눕는 것을 가리키는 속어라면, 그래요 그랬지요. 그게 아니라면, 당신이 에드워드의 누이에게 건네는 그 야만적인 언사에 대해선 나는 뭐라 의견을 낼 수가 없네요. 오빠가 어딘가에서, 밤낮으로 달려 로마에 갔다가 — 그래 봤자 불쌍한 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에 뵙기엔 너무 늦게 도착했지만요 — 아니면 다른 어떤 해로운 상황에서 말라리아 열병에 걸렸다는 건, 당신이 말하려는 게 그거라면, 의심할 여지가 없어요. 마찬가지로 오빠의 지극히 무절제한 생활이 그 병에 정말이지 아주 취약한 체질로 만들었다는 것도요.\"\n\n스파클러 씨는 그것이 서인도제도에서 황열병에 걸린 우리 패거리 몇몇의 경우와 비슷한 사례라고 여겼다. 스파클러 부인은 다시 눈을 감고는, 서인도제도의 우리 패거리든 황열병이든 일절 의식하기를 거부했다.\n\n\"그러니 에이미는,\" 그녀가 눈꺼풀을 다시 열며 말을 이었다. \"수많은 지루하고 불안했던 주들의 여파에서 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요. 그리고 마지막으로, 그 애 마음 깊은 곳에 자리 잡고 있다는 걸 내가 아주 잘 아는 어떤 침울한 경향에서도 분발시켜 줘야 해요. 그게 뭐냐고는 묻지 마요, 에드먼드. 당신한테 말하기는 거절할 테니까.\"\n\n\"안 물을 거요, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니 내 사랑스러운 동생을 두고 손볼 일이 많을 거예요.\" 스파클러 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그러니 그 애를 한시라도 빨리 곁에 두지 않을 수 없지요. 사랑스럽고 정겨운 우리 꼬마 신발 한 켤레! 불쌍한 아버지의 재산 정리에 대해 말하자면, 내 관심은 그리 이기적인 게 아니에요. 아버지는 내가 결혼할 때 무척 후하게 대해 주셨으니, 내가 기대할 게 거의 없어요. 효력을 발휘할 유언장을 만들어 제너럴 부인에게 유산을 남기는 일만 없었다면, 나는 만족해요. 그리운 아버지, 그리운 아버지.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 눈물을 흘렸으나, 제너럴 부인은 최고의 회복제였다. 그 이름은 곧 그녀를 자극해 눈물을 닦고 이렇게 말하게 했다.\n\n\"오빠의 병환에서 무척 고무적인 점은, 생각하면 감사한 일이지만, 오빠의 분별력이 손상되지 않았고 마땅한 기개도 약해지지 않았다는 — 적어도 불쌍한 아버지가 돌아가실 무렵까지는요 — 그것에 큰 확신을 준다는 거예요. 오빠가 제너럴 부인을 즉시 청산하고 집에서 내쫓았다는 것 말이에요. 나는 그 일로 오빠를 칭찬해요. 그렇게 신속하게, 내가 직접 했을 법한 바로 그 일을 해 준 것에 대해서라면 많은 걸 용서해 줄 수 있어요!\"\n\n스파클러 부인이 만족감에 한껏 달아올라 있을 때, 문에서 노크 소리가 두 번 들려왔다. 무척 기묘한 노크였다. 소리를 내거나 주목을 끌지 않으려는 듯 낮았다. 노크하는 사람이 무언가에 골몰해 멈추는 것을 잊은 듯 길었다.\n\n\"이런!\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"누구지?\"\n\n\"통기도 없이 마차도 없이 에이미와 에드워드일 리는 없는데!\" 스파클러 부인이 말했다. \"내다봐요.\"\n\n방 안은 어두웠으나 거리는 가로등 덕분에 더 환했다. 발코니 너머로 내다보는 스파클러 씨의 머리가 어찌나 부피 크고 묵직해 보이던지, 금방이라도 균형을 잃고 아래에 있는 미지의 인물을 납작하게 짓눌러 버릴 것만 같았다.\n\n\"한 사람이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"누군지 못 알아보겠—아니, 잠깐!\"\n\n이 두 번째 생각에 그는 다시 발코니로 나가 한 번 더 보았다. 문이 열릴 즈음 그는 돌아와, \"우리 영감님의 모자\"를 알아본 것 같다고 알렸다. 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 곧이어 그의 영감님이 모자를 손에 든 채 안으로 안내되었다.\n\n\"촛불을!\" 스파클러 부인이 어둠에 대해 양해의 말을 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"나한텐 충분히 밝소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n촛불이 들어오자, 머들 씨가 문 뒤에 서서 입술을 매만지고 있는 것이 드러났다. \"한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\" 그가 말했다. \"지금 좀 유난히 바쁘긴 한데, 마침 산책 삼아 나온 김에 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\"\n\n그가 만찬 차림이었으므로, 패니는 어디서 식사를 하고 왔느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"글쎄,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"딱히 어디서 식사를 하진 않았소.\"\n\n\"물론 식사는 하셨겠죠?\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 — 아니, 정확히 식사를 하진 않았소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n그는 누런 이마를 손으로 쓸며, 그 점에 대해 확신이 없는 듯 곰곰이 생각했다. 무언가 먹을 것을 권했다. \"고맙지만 됐소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"그럴 마음이 안 드는구려. 원래 머들 부인과 함께 밖에서 식사할 예정이었소. 그런데 식사할 마음이 안 들어서, 마차에 막 올라타려던 참에 머들 부인 혼자 가게 두고, 나는 대신 산책이나 해야겠다 싶었소.\"\n\n차나 커피를 들겠느냐고 물었다. \"고맙지만 됐소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"클럽에 들러서 포도주 한 병 마셨소.\"\n\n방문이 이 대목에 이르자, 머들 씨는 에드먼드 스파클러가 권했던 의자에 앉았다. 그때까지 그는 그 의자를 마치 스케이트를 처음 신어 보고도 출발할 결심을 못 하는 둔한 사람처럼 앞으로 천천히 밀고 다녔던 것이다. 그는 이제 모자를 옆의 다른 의자에 올려놓고, 마치 그것이 스무 자쯤 되는 깊이라도 되는 양 그 안을 들여다보며 다시 말했다. \"보다시피 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\"\n\n\"우리한텐 영광이네요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"방문 다니시는 분이 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇소, 그렇소.\" 이즈음 두 소맷자락 아래로 자신을 가두고 있던 머들 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그렇소, 나는 방문 다니는 사람이 아니오.\"\n\n\"그러기엔 하실 일이 너무 많으시죠.\" 패니가 말했다. \"하실 일이 그렇게 많으시니, 머들 씨, 식욕을 잃는 건 당신한테 심각한 일이에요. 살펴보셔야 해요. 편찮으시면 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"오! 나는 아주 멀쩡하오.\" 머들 씨가 그 점을 곰곰이 생각한 뒤 대답했다. \"평소만큼 멀쩡하오. 충분히 멀쩡하오. 내가 원하는 만큼 멀쩡하다오.\"\n\n언제나 자기에 대해 할 말이 가능한 한 적고 그것을 말하는 데에도 큰 어려움을 겪는다는 특징에 충실한, 이 시대의 거장 정신은 다시 입을 다물었다. 스파클러 부인은 그 거장 정신이 얼마나 오래 머물 작정인지 궁금해지기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신이 들어왔을 때 나는 불쌍한 아버지 얘기를 하고 있었어요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"아! 참으로 공교롭군.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n패니는 어디가 공교롭다는 건지 알 수 없었지만, 말을 계속해야 할 의무를 느꼈다. \"내 말은,\" 그녀가 이어 갔다. \"오빠의 병환 때문에 아버지의 재산을 조사하고 정리하는 일이 미뤄지고 있다는 거였어요.\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"그렇소. 미뤄졌지.\"\n\n\"그게 중요하다는 건 아니지만요.\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"중요하다는,\" 머들 씨가 자기 시야에 들어오는 방의 그 부분 처마 장식을 죄다 살펴본 뒤 동의했다. \"건 아니지요.\"\n\n\"내 유일한 걱정은,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 아무것도 받지 못하게 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그 여자는 아무것도 못 받을 거요.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n패니는 그가 그런 의견을 내놓는 것을 듣고 기뻐했다. 머들 씨는 마치 모자 바닥에 뭔가 보이기라도 하는 듯 다시 그 깊은 곳을 들여다본 뒤, 머리카락을 비비며 자신의 마지막 말에 확언의 말을 천천히 덧붙였다. \"오 그렇고말고. 못 받지. 그 여자는 못 받소. 어림없는 일이오.\"\n\n화제가 다한 듯하고 머들 씨도 그러한 듯하자, 패니는 그가 돌아가는 길에 머들 부인과 마차를 데리러 들를 거냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 가장 가까운 길로 갈 테고, 머들 부인은 — \" 여기서 그는 마치 자기 운명을 점치기라도 하는 듯 두 손바닥을 죄다 들여다보았다. \"알아서 하도록 둘 거요. 자기가 알아서 잘 처리하겠지.\"\n\n\"아마도요.\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n그러고 나서 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 스파클러 부인은 다시 소파에 기대어 누워, 눈을 감고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 앞서처럼 세상사에서 물러나 있었다.\n\n\"하지만, 어쨌든,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"내가 당신을 붙들고 나도 붙드는 셈이군. 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었던 거요, 알다시피.\"\n\n\"정말 매력적이세요.\" 패니가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 이만 가겠소.\" 머들 씨가 일어나며 덧붙였다. \"주머니칼 하나 빌려줄 수 있겠소?\"\n\n편지 한 통 쓰는 것조차 좀처럼 마음먹지 못하는 자기가, 머들 씨처럼 그토록 사업이 방대한 사람에게 빌려준다는 건 묘한 일이네요, 하고 패니가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그렇지 않소?\" 머들 씨가 수긍했다. \"하지만 나는 하나 필요하오. 그리고 당신에게 가위니 족집게니 그런 것들이 든 자그마한 결혼 기념품이 여럿 있다는 걸 아오. 내일 돌려주리다.\"\n\n\"에드먼드,\" 스파클러 부인이 말했다. \"저기 내 작은 탁자 위에 있는 자개 상자를 열어서(자, 아주 조심해서요, 제발 부탁이에요, 당신은 워낙 서투르니까) 머들 씨에게 자개 주머니칼을 드려요.\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 손잡이가 더 어두운 게 있으면, 손잡이가 더 어두운 쪽이 좋을 것 같소.\"\n\n\"거북 등딱지요?\"\n\n\"고맙소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"그래요. 거북 등딱지가 좋을 것 같소.\"\n\n그리하여 에드먼드는 거북 등딱지 상자를 열어 머들 씨에게 거북 등딱지 칼을 드리라는 지시를 받았다. 그가 그렇게 하자, 그의 아내가 그 거장에게 너그러이 말했다.\n\n\"잉크만 묻히지 않으면 용서해 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"잉크는 묻히지 않겠다고 약속하리다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다.\n\n그러고서 그 명망 높은 방문객은 소맷부리를 내밀어, 한순간 스파클러 부인의 손을 — 손목과 팔찌까지 죄다 — 묻어 버렸다. 그 자신의 손이 어디로 움츠러들었는지는 드러나지 않았으나, 마치 그가 더없이 공로 많은 첼시의 노병이나 그리니치 연금 수급자라도 되는 양 스파클러 부인의 촉각으로부터 아득히 멀리 떨어져 있었다.\n\n방을 나서면서, 마침내 끝장을 본 날 중에서 가장 긴 날이었으며, 개인적인 매력이 전혀 없지 않은 여자가 멍청하고 둔한 사람들에게 그토록 지쳐 버린 일은 결코 없었다고 철저히 확신한 채, 패니는 바람을 좀 쐬러 발코니로 나갔다. 분한 마음의 눈물이 그녀의 눈을 채웠고, 그 눈물은 거리를 내려가는 그 유명한 머들 씨가 마치 여러 악마에라도 들린 듯 펄쩍 뛰고 왈츠를 추고 빙빙 도는 것처럼 보이게 하는 효과를 냈다.\n\n# CHAPTER 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office\n\n만찬 모임은 저명한 의사의 집에서 열렸다. 법조계 인사도 참석했고, 그것도 한껏 위세를 떨치고 있었다. 퍼디낸드 배너클도 참석했는데, 더없이 사람을 끌어당기는 태도였다. 의사에게는 인생의 어떤 면도 좀처럼 가려져 있지 않았으니, 그는 주교보다도 더 자주 인생의 가장 어두운 곳에 발을 들였다. 런던에는 그를 더없이 매력적인 사람이자 더없이 유쾌한 인물이라며 자기야, 사족을 못 쓰는 화려한 귀부인들이 있었는데, 그 사려 깊은 눈이 한두 시간 전에 어떤 광경에 머물렀는지를, 또 그의 차분한 모습이 누구의 침상 곁에, 어떤 지붕 아래에 서 있었는지를 알았더라면, 그렇게 가까이 앉아 있다는 사실에 그녀들은 소스라쳤을 것이다. 그러나 의사는 차분한 사람이어서, 제 나팔도 남의 나팔도 불지 않았다. 그는 숱한 놀라운 일들을 보고 들었으며, 화해될 수 없는 도덕적 모순들 한가운데에서 일생을 보냈다. 그럼에도 그의 한결같은 연민은, 모든 치유의 신성한 주님의 그것이 그러하듯, 조금도 흐트러지지 않았다. 그는 비처럼 의로운 자와 불의한 자 사이를 두루 다니며 할 수 있는 모든 선을 행했으되, 그것을 회당에서도 거리 모퉁이에서도 떠벌리지 않았다.\n\n인간에 대한 폭넓은 경험을 지닌 사람이라면, 그것을 아무리 조용히 지니고 있더라도, 그런 앎의 소유에서 비롯되는 독특한 흥미를 풍기지 않을 수 없는 법이니, 의사는 매력적인 사람이었다. 그의 비밀을 짐작조차 못 하고, 만약 그가 그들에게 \"와서 내가 보는 것을 보시오!\"라고 제안한다면 그 끔찍한 무례함에 가진 정신을 죄다 놓아 버렸을 더 까다로운 신사 숙녀들조차도 그의 매력만은 인정했다. 그가 있는 곳에는 무언가 진실한 것이 있었다. 그리고 진실의 반 톨은, 다른 희귀한 천연물의 가장 작은 조각이 그러하듯, 어마어마한 양의 희석제에 풍미를 더한다.\n\n그리하여 의사의 자그마한 만찬에서는 사람들이 늘 가장 덜 인습적인 모습으로 드러나기 마련이었다. 손님들은 스스로 의식하든 못 하든 이렇게 중얼거렸다. \"여기 우리를 있는 그대로 정말로 아는 사람이 있다. 우리 가운데 더러는 가발과 분칠을 벗은 채로 날마다 그에게 받아들여지고, 둘 다 우리 통제를 벗어났을 때 그는 우리 마음의 방황을 듣고 가식 없는 얼굴 표정을 본다. 그러니 그 앞에서는 진실에 한 발짝 다가가는 편이 낫겠다. 이 사람은 우리를 이겨 냈고, 우리에게는 너무 강하니까.\" 그래서 의사의 손님들은 그 둥근 식탁에서 어찌나 놀랍도록 본모습을 드러내던지, 거의 자연스러울 정도였다.\n\n배심원들의 그 집합체, 곧 인류라 불리는 것에 관한 법조계 인사의 지식은 면도날처럼 예리했다. 그러나 면도날이라는 게 두루 쓰기에 편리한 도구는 아니어서, 의사의 소박하고 빛나는 메스는 훨씬 덜 날카롭긴 해도 훨씬 더 넓은 용도에 들어맞았다. 법조계 인사는 사람들의 잘 속는 성질과 교활함에 대해서라면 죄다 꿰고 있었다. 그러나 의사라면 자기 왕진 한 주만으로도, 웨스트민스터 홀과 모든 순회 재판을 합쳐 칠십 년 동안 얻을 수 있는 것보다 그들의 다정함과 애정에 대해 더 나은 통찰을 그에게 줄 수 있었을 것이다. 법조계 인사는 늘 이를 어렴풋이 짐작했고, 어쩌면 그 짐작을 기꺼이 부추겼을 것이다(만약 세상이 정말로 거대한 법정이라면, 종기일의 마지막 날이 아무리 빨리 와도 이르지 않으리라 여겨질 테니). 그래서 그는 다른 어떤 부류의 사람 못지않게 의사를 좋아하고 존경했다.\n\n머들 씨가 빠진 탓에 식탁에는 뱅코의 빈자리가 생겼다. 그러나 그가 거기 있었더라도 그저 거기 앉은 뱅코만큼의 차이밖에 못 냈을 테니, 그의 부재는 손실이 아니었다. 만약 까마귀가 웨스트민스터 홀에서 그만큼 시간을 보냈다면 그랬을 법한 모양으로, 그곳의 온갖 잡동사니를 주워 모으던 법조계 인사는 요즈음 지푸라기를 잔뜩 주워 이리저리 던져 보며 머들의 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 가늠하고 있었다. 그는 이제 그 문제를 두고 머들 부인 본인과 잠시 이야기를 나누었다. 물론 두 겹 안경에 배심원을 다룰 때의 고갯짓을 곁들이며 그 부인 곁으로 슬며시 다가가서였다.\n\n\"어떤 새 한 마리가,\" 법조계 인사가 말했는데, 그 새가 까치 말고 다른 새일 리는 없다는 표정이었다, \"요즘 우리 법조인들 사이에서 속삭이기를, 이 나라의 작위 가진 분들 명단에 한 분이 더해질 거라더군요.\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 법조계 인사가 말했다. \"그 새가 우리와는 사뭇 다른 귀에다가도—어여쁜 귀에다가도 속삭이지 않았던가요?\" 그는 머들 부인의 가장 가까운 귀고리를 의미심장하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"제 귀를 말씀하시는 건가요?\" 머들 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"제가 어여쁘다고 할 때는,\" 법조계 인사가 말했다, \"언제나 부인을 뜻합니다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 당신은 늘 아무 뜻도 없으신 것 같은데요.\" 머들 부인이 (기분이 상하지는 않은 채로) 받았다.\n\n\"오, 너무도 매정한 누명이로군요!\" 법조계 인사가 말했다. \"한데, 그 새 말입니다.\"\n\n\"저야 세상에서 소식을 가장 늦게 듣는 사람이지요.\" 머들 부인이 무심히 제 요새를 매만지며 말했다. \"그게 누군가요?\"\n\n\"부인이라면 얼마나 훌륭한 증인이 되실는지요!\" 법조계 인사가 말했다. \"어떤 배심원도(눈먼 자들로만 배심을 꾸리지 않는 한) 부인을 당해 내지 못할 겁니다. 설령 부인이 형편없는 증인이더라도 말이지요. 한데 부인은 정말 훌륭한 증인이 되실 테니까요!\"\n\n\"어머나, 이 우스꽝스러운 양반아, 어째서요?\" 머들 부인이 웃으며 물었다.\n\n법조계 인사는 자기와 그 가슴팍 사이에서 두 겹 안경을 서너 번 흔들어 응수를 대신하더니, 더없이 알랑거리는 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"몇 주 뒤, 어쩌면 며칠 뒤에, 저는 여인들 가운데 가장 우아하고 가장 교양 있고 가장 매혹적인 분을 무어라 불러야 하는지요?\"\n\n\"당신네 새가 그분을 무어라 부르라고는 안 일러 주던가요?\" 머들 부인이 받았다. \"내일 한번 물어보시고, 다음에 저를 보실 때 새가 뭐라 하는지 일러 주세요.\"\n\n이리하여 둘 사이에 비슷한 농담이 더 오갔다. 그러나 법조계 인사는 그 온갖 예리함에도 거기서 아무것도 얻어 내지 못했다. 반면에 의사는 머들 부인을 마차까지 바래다주며 그녀가 망토를 걸치는 것을 거들면서, 늘 그렇듯 차분하고 직설적으로 증상을 캐물었다.\n\n\"여쭤도 될지요,\" 그가 말했다, \"머들에 관한 그 이야기가 사실입니까?\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 그녀가 받았다, \"제가 선생님께 여쭐까 반쯤 마음먹었던 바로 그 질문을 하시는군요.\"\n\n\"제게 묻겠다고요! 어째서 제게요?\"\n\n\"정말이지, 머들 씨가 누구보다도 선생님께 더 큰 신뢰를 두고 있다고 생각하거든요.\"\n\n\"천만에요, 그분은 제게 아무것도, 진료에 관해서조차 말하지 않습니다. 부인도 물론 그 소문을 들으셨겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론 들었지요. 하지만 머들 씨가 어떤 사람인지 아시잖아요. 얼마나 말수가 없고 속을 안 드러내는지요. 정말이지 저는 그 소문에 무슨 근거가 있는지 짐작도 못 하겠어요. 사실이었으면 싶긴 해요. 선생님 앞에서 그걸 왜 감추겠어요? 제가 감춘다면 선생님이 더 잘 아실 텐데요!\"\n\n\"바로 그렇지요.\" 의사가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 죄다 사실인지, 일부만 사실인지, 아니면 전부 거짓인지는 도무지 말씀드릴 수가 없네요. 참으로 약 오르는 처지예요, 참으로 어처구니없는 처지지요. 하지만 선생님은 머들 씨를 아시니, 놀라지도 않으시겠죠.\"\n\n의사는 놀라지 않은 채 그녀를 마차에 태우고 안녕히 가시라 인사했다. 그는 자기 집 현관에 잠시 서서, 그 우아한 마차가 덜커덕거리며 멀어져 가는 것을 차분히 바라보았다. 위층으로 돌아오니 나머지 손님들도 곧 흩어졌고, 그는 홀로 남았다. 온갖 종류의 책을 즐겨 읽는 사람이었기에(그리고 그 약점에 대해 결코 변명하지 않았기에), 그는 편안하게 자리를 잡고 책을 읽었다.\n\n서재 탁자 위 시계가 자정에서 몇 분 모자라는 시각을 가리킬 무렵, 현관 초인종 소리가 그의 주의를 그쪽으로 돌렸다. 검소한 습관의 사람인지라 그는 하인들을 잠자리에 들여보낸 터여서 손수 내려가 문을 열어야 했다. 그가 내려가 보니 모자도 외투도 걸치지 않은 한 사내가 있었는데, 셔츠 소매를 어깨까지 단단히 걷어 올린 차림이었다. 그는 한순간 그 사내가 싸움이라도 한 줄 알았다. 사내가 몹시 흥분해 있고 숨이 차 있었기 때문에 더욱 그랬다. 그러나 다시 보니 그 사내는 유난히 깨끗했고, 차림새에 관한 한 이 묘사에 들어맞는 것 말고는 흐트러진 데가 없었다.\n\n\"근처 거리에 있는 온천 목욕탕에서 왔습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"한데 온천 목욕탕에 무슨 일이오?\"\n\n\"부디 곧장 와 주십시오, 선생님. 탁자 위에 이게 놓여 있었습니다.\"\n\n그는 의사의 손에 종잇조각 하나를 쥐여 주었다. 의사가 들여다보니 연필로 적힌 제 이름과 주소가 보였다. 그뿐이었다. 그는 그 필체를 더 가까이 들여다보고, 사내를 보고, 못에 걸린 모자를 집어 들고, 문 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고는, 둘이서 함께 서둘러 떠났다.\n\n온천 목욕탕에 다다르니, 그곳에 딸린 다른 사람들이 모두 문간에서 그들을 내다보며 통로를 오르내리고 있었다. \"다들 부디 물러서 있으라 일러 주시오.\" 의사가 주인에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그리고 자네는 나를 곧장 그 자리로 데려가게, 친구.\" 심부름꾼에게는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n심부름꾼이 작은 방들이 늘어선 줄을 따라 그보다 앞서 서둘러 가더니, 그 줄 끝의 한 방으로 돌아 들어가 문 안을 둘러보았다. 의사도 바짝 뒤따라가 마찬가지로 문 안을 둘러보았다.\n\n그 구석에 욕조가 하나 있었는데, 물은 황급히 빼낸 뒤였다. 무덤이나 석관 속에 누인 듯 그 안에 누워 있는 것은, 시트와 담요가 황급히 덮인 채로, 육중하게 생긴 한 사내의 시신이었다. 둔한 머리에, 거칠고 천하고 평범한 이목구비였다. 방을 가득 채웠던 증기를 빼내려고 천창을 열어 둔 터였으나, 증기는 물방울로 응결되어 벽에도 무겁게, 욕조 속 얼굴과 몸에도 무겁게 매달려 있었다. 방은 아직 후끈했고 욕조의 대리석도 아직 따뜻했다. 그러나 얼굴과 몸은 손에 닿는 감촉이 끈적하고 차가웠다. 욕조 바닥의 흰 대리석은 무시무시한 붉은빛으로 줄무늬져 있었다. 옆쪽 선반 위에는 빈 아편팅크 병 하나와 거북등껍질 손잡이가 달린 주머니칼 하나가 있었는데, 칼은 더럽혀져 있었으되 잉크로 더럽혀진 것은 아니었다.\n\n\"경정맥 절단—급사—적어도 삼십 분 전에 사망.\" 의사의 이 말이 메아리쳐 통로와 작은 방들을 지나, 온 집 안으로 퍼져 나갔다. 그가 아직 욕조 바닥에 손을 뻗으려 굽혔던 몸을 펴는 중이었고, 아직 두 손을 물에 적시는 중이었으며, 그 물이 대리석이 줄무늬졌던 것처럼 붉게 줄무늬지다가 이내 한 빛깔로 뒤섞이기 전이었다.\n\n그는 소파 위의 옷가지로, 또 탁자 위의 시계와 돈과 수첩으로 눈을 돌렸다. 수첩에 반쯤 끼워지고 반쯤 비어져 나온 접힌 쪽지 하나가 그의 살피는 눈길을 사로잡았다. 그는 그것을 바라보고, 만져 보고, 책장 사이에서 조금 더 끌어내며 조용히 말했다. \"이건 내게 보낸 것이군.\" 그러고는 그것을 펴서 읽었다.\n\n그가 내려야 할 지시 사항은 없었다. 그 집 사람들은 무엇을 해야 하는지 알고 있었다. 관계 당국이 곧 불려 왔고, 그들은 시계의 태엽을 마저 감을 때 흔히 따르는 정도의 동요밖에는 태도에도 안색에도 드러내지 않은 채, 고인과 그가 소유했던 것들을 차분하고 사무적으로 인수했다. 의사는 밤공기 속으로 걸어 나오게 되어 다행이었다—그 풍부한 경험에도 불구하고, 잠시 문간 계단에 앉아 있게 되어 다행이기까지 했다. 속이 메스껍고 어지러웠던 것이다.\n\n법조계 인사는 그의 가까운 이웃이었는데, 그 집에 이르렀을 때 의사는 친구가 종종 늦도록 앉아 일을 준비하던 방에 불이 켜져 있는 것을 보았다. 법조계 인사가 없을 때는 그 방에 불이 켜진 적이 없었으니, 그것은 그가 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다는 확신을 주었다. 사실 이 부지런한 꿀벌은 증거에 맞서 내일 평결을 얻어 내야 했고, 배심원 여러분에게 덫을 놓느라 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보내는 중이었다.\n\n의사의 노크 소리에 법조계 인사는 놀랐다. 그러나 그는 누군가 자기 물건을 훔치고 있다거나, 아니면 어떻게든 자기를 이기려 든다는 것을 알리러 온 사람이 있다고 곧장 의심했기에, 잽싸고 살그머니 내려왔다. 그는 배심원들의 머리에 끼얹을 뜨거운 물을 마련하기 좋은 준비로 찬물 세정제로 제 머리를 식히던 참이었고, 맞은편 증인들의 목을 더 자유로이 졸라매고자 셔츠 깃을 활짝 풀어헤친 채 책을 읽던 참이었다. 그래서 내려오는 그의 모습은 자못 사나워 보였다. 가장 예상치 못한 사람인 의사를 보자 그는 더욱 사나운 표정으로 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"언젠가 자네가 내게 머들의 병이 무엇이냐고 물었지.\"\n\n\"별난 대답이로군! 그랬지, 그래.\"\n\n\"못 알아냈다고 내가 말했고.\"\n\n\"그래. 그랬다는 거 알아.\"\n\n\"그걸 알아냈네.\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 법조계 인사가 화들짝 물러서며 상대의 가슴팍에 손을 탁 짚으며 말했다. \"나도 알아냈어! 자네 얼굴에 쓰여 있군.\"\n\n그들은 가장 가까운 방으로 들어갔고, 거기서 의사가 그에게 편지를 건네 읽게 했다. 그는 그것을 대여섯 번 읽었다. 분량으로 치면 별것 아니었으나, 그의 면밀하고 지속적인 주의를 크게 요했다. 그는 이 일의 단서를 제 손으로 찾아내지 못한 데 대한 후회를 이루 다 말로 표현하지 못했다. 아주 작은 단서만 있었어도 그 사건을 손에 넣을 수 있었을 거라고, 또 바닥까지 파헤쳐 보았다면 얼마나 대단한 사건이었겠느냐고 그는 말했다.\n\n의사는 할리 가에 그 소식을 전하기로 되어 있었다. 법조계 인사는 자기가 그 자리에서 일찍이 그 배심석에서 본 가장 계몽되고 비범한 배심원들을 꾀어 들이는 일—학식 높은 친구에게 말할 수 있거니와, 어떤 얄팍한 궤변도 그들에게는 통하지 않으며, 어떤 가련하게 오용된 직업적 재치와 솜씨도 먹히지 않을 그 배심원들(이것이 그가 그들에게 운을 떼려던 방식이었다)—로 당장 돌아갈 수는 없었다. 그래서 자기도 함께 가서, 친구가 집 안에 있는 동안 근처를 오락가락하며 어슬렁대겠다고 말했다. 둘은 바깥 공기 속에서 평정을 더 잘 되찾고자 그곳까지 걸어갔다. 의사가 문을 두드릴 무렵에는 날의 날개가 밤을 펄럭이게 하고 있었다.\n\n세상의 눈에 무지갯빛으로 비치는 하인 하나가 주인을 기다리며 깨어 있었다—말하자면, 양초 두 자루와 신문 한 부를 앞에 두고 부엌에서 곯아떨어져 있었으니, 집이 우연히 불타게 될 개연성을 거스르는 수학적 확률의 막대한 누적을 몸소 증명하는 셈이었다. 이 하인을 깨우고 나서도 의사는 집사장이 깨어나기를 또 기다려야 했다. 마침내 그 고귀한 존재가 플란넬 가운에 펠트 신을 신은 채 식당으로 들어왔다. 그러나 넥타이는 매고 있었으며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 영락없는 집사장이었다. 이제 아침이었다. 의사는 기다리는 동안 빛을 보려고 창 하나의 덧문을 열어 두었다.\n\n\"머들 부인의 시녀를 불러서, 머들 부인을 깨우고, 나를 만날 수 있도록 가능한 한 조심스레 마음의 준비를 시키라고 일러 주시오. 부인께 전해야 할 끔찍한 소식이 있소.\"\n\n의사가 집사장에게 이렇게 말했다. 손에 양초를 들고 있던 집사장은 자기 부하를 불러 그것을 치우게 했다. 그러고는 위엄 있게 창가로 다가가더니, 바로 그 방에서 만찬을 지켜보던 것과 똑같은 태도로 의사의 소식을 지켜보았다.\n\n\"머들 씨가 돌아가셨소.\"\n\n\"한 달 전에 미리 사직 통보를 드리고 싶습니다.\" 집사장이 말했다.\n\n\"머들 씨가 스스로 목숨을 끊었소.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 집사장이 말했다, \"그건 제 위치에 있는 사람의 감정에 매우 불쾌한 일이며, 편견을 불러일으킬 만한 일입니다. 그러니 저는 즉시 떠나고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"충격을 받지 않았다 치더라도, 자네는 놀랍지도 않은가, 이 사람아?\" 의사가 발끈하여 따져 물었다.\n\n집사장은 꼿꼿하고 침착하게 다음과 같은 기억할 만한 말로 대답했다. \"선생님, 머들 씨는 애당초 신사가 아니었습니다. 그러니 머들 씨가 어떤 신사답지 못한 행동을 했더라도 저는 놀라지 않습니다. 제가 떠나기 전에 선생님께 더 보내 드릴 사람이 있을지요, 아니면 선생님이 처리되기를 바라시는 일에 관해 제가 남길 다른 지시가 있을지요?\"\n\n의사는 위층에서 자기 소임을 다하고 나서 거리에서 법조계 인사와 다시 만났을 때, 머들 부인과의 면담에 대해서는 그저 아직 그녀에게 다 말하지는 못했지만 자기가 전한 것만큼은 그녀가 제법 잘 견뎌 냈다고만 말했다. 법조계 인사는 거리에서의 여가를 배심원 전원을 단번에 잡아들일 더없이 교묘한 덫을 고안하는 데 바쳤다. 그 문제를 마음속으로 정리해 두고 나니 머릿속이 그 최근의 참사 쪽으로 명료해져서, 둘은 그것을 온갖 측면에서 논하며 천천히 집으로 걸었다. 의사의 집 문 앞에서 헤어지기 전, 둘은 햇살 비치는 아침 하늘을 올려다보았는데, 그곳으로 몇몇 이른 불의 연기와 몇몇 이른 기상자들의 입김과 목소리가 평화로이 피어오르고 있었다. 그러고 나서 둘은 그 거대한 도시를 둘러보며 이렇게 말했다. 아직 잠들어 있는 저 수백 수천의 빈털터리가 된 사람들이, 우리 둘이 이야기하는 지금 자기네 위에 닥쳐오는 파멸을 알 수만 있다면, 한 가련한 영혼을 향한 얼마나 무시무시한 외침이 하늘로 치솟겠는가!\n\n그 위대한 인물이 죽었다는 소식은 놀라운 속도로 퍼졌다. 처음에 그는 일찍이 알려진 온갖 병으로, 그리고 그 상황의 수요에 맞춰 빛의 속도로 발명된 몇 가지 신종 질환으로 죽은 셈이 되었다. 그는 갓난아기 적부터 수종을 감춰 왔고, 할아버지에게서 가슴에 물이 차는 거대한 유산을 물려받았으며, 평생 십팔 년 동안 매일 아침 수술을 받았고, 폭죽처럼 몸속 중요한 혈관들이 터지는 일을 겪어 왔으며, 폐에 무슨 탈이 있었고, 심장에 무슨 탈이 있었고, 뇌에 무슨 탈이 있었다. 이 문제에 대해 전혀 아는 바 없이 아침 식사에 앉은 오백 명의 사람들이, 식사를 마치기도 전에, 의사가 머들 씨에게 \"언젠가 촛불 심지처럼 꺼져 버릴 각오를 하셔야 합니다\"라고 말하는 것을 자기네가 개인적으로 직접 들어 알고 있으며, 또 머들 씨가 의사에게 \"사람은 한 번만 죽는 법이지요\"라고 말하는 것을 자기네가 알고 있다고 믿게 되었다. 오전 열한 시쯤에는 뇌에 무슨 탈이 있었다는 설이 다른 모든 설을 제치고 가장 인기 있는 학설이 되었고, 열두 시에는 그 무슨 탈이라는 게 '압박'이라고 분명히 확정되었다.\n\n압박은 대중의 마음에 더없이 흡족했고 모두를 더없이 편안하게 해 주는 듯해서, 법조계 인사가 아홉 시 반에 그 사건의 진상을 법정으로 가져가지만 않았더라면 하루 종일 갔을지도 모른다. 이로 인해 한 시쯤에는 머들 씨가 자살했다는 소문이 온 런던에 두루 속삭여지기 시작했다. 그러나 압박은 그 발견으로 뒤집히기는커녕 그 어느 때보다 더 인기를 끌게 되었다. 거리마다 압박에 대한 일반적인 교훈이 늘어놓아졌다. 돈을 벌어 보려다 그러지 못한 사람들은 죄다 이렇게 말했다, 거 봐! 부의 추구에 몸 바치기 시작하자마자 압박이 오는 거라고. 게으른 사람들도 비슷한 식으로 그 기회를 활용했다. 봐, 그들은 말했다, 일하고 또 일하고 또 일해서 네 꼴이 어찌 됐는지 보라고! 너는 끈질기게 일했고, 도를 넘게 했어. 압박이 닥쳤고, 너는 끝장난 거야! 이런 생각은 여러 방면에서 매우 강력했으나, 도를 넘게 일할 위험이라고는 털끝만큼도 없었던 젊은 서기들과 동업자들 사이에서보다 더 강력한 데는 없었다. 이들은 너 나 할 것 없이, 자못 경건하게, 살아 있는 한 그 경고를 결코 잊지 않기를 바라며, 자기네 처신을 잘 단속하여 압박을 막고 오랜 세월 벗들에게 위안이 되도록 제 몸을 지키기를 바란다고 선언했다.\n\n그러나 거래소가 가장 붐비는 시각쯤 되자 압박은 시들기 시작했고, 동서남북으로 소름 끼치는 속삭임이 돌기 시작했다. 처음에 그것은 희미하여, 머들 씨의 재산이 짐작했던 만큼 막대한 것으로 드러날지, 그것을 '현금화'하는 데 일시적인 어려움이 있지는 않을지, 심지어 그 놀라운 은행 쪽에서 일시적인 지급 정지(이를테면 한 달쯤)가 있지는 않을지 하는 의심에 그쳤다. 그 속삭임은, 그때부터 매 순간 그러했듯이, 점점 커지면서 점점 더 위협적이 되어 갔다. 그는 누구도 설명할 수 없는 어떤 자연스러운 성장이나 과정도 없이 무에서 솟아난 자였고, 결국은 비천하고 무지한 작자였으며, 늘 눈을 내리깔던 사내여서 누구도 그의 눈을 마주칠 수 없었고, 도무지 까닭 모를 방식으로 온갖 부류의 사람들에게 떠받들어진 자였으며, 제 돈이라고는 가져 본 적이 없고, 그의 투기는 더없이 무모했으며, 그의 지출은 더없이 어마어마했다. 날이 저물어 감에 따라 꾸준히, 그 입소문은 소리와 목적에서 점점 높아졌다. 그는 목욕탕에 자기 주치의 앞으로 편지를 한 통 남겼고, 그 주치의가 편지를 받았으며, 그 편지가 내일 검시 심문에서 공개될 텐데, 그것은 그가 속여 온 군중에게 벼락처럼 떨어질 것이라고 했다. 온갖 직업과 업종의 수많은 사람들이 그의 파산으로 짓밟힐 것이며, 평생 넉넉히 살아온 노인들은 그를 믿었던 것을 뉘우칠 곳이라곤 구빈원밖에 없을 것이며, 무수한 여자와 아이들이 이 거대한 악당의 손에 자기네 온 앞날을 황폐하게 당할 것이었다. 그의 호화로운 잔치에 참석했던 자는 누구나 무수한 가정을 약탈한 데 한몫한 자로 드러날 것이며, 그를 받침대 위에 올려놓는 데 거든, 부를 비굴하게 숭배하던 자는 누구나 차라리 악마를 정면으로 숭배하는 편이 나았을 것이었다. 그리하여 그 입소문은 확인에 확인이 거듭되고 석간신문이 판을 거듭 찍어 냄에 따라 점점 더 크고 높이 채찍질당해, 밤이 오자 어찌나 큰 포효로 부풀어 올랐던지, 세인트 폴 대성당 돔 위 회랑에서 홀로 지켜보는 자가 있었다면 밤공기에 머들이라는 이름이 온갖 형태의 저주와 짝지어 무겁게 웅얼대며 실려 있음을 느꼈으리라 믿게 할 만했다.\n\n이는 그때쯤 고 머들 씨의 병이 다름 아닌 위조와 도둑질이었음이 알려졌기 때문이다. 그토록 널리 퍼진 아첨의 투박한 대상이요, 위인들의 잔치에 앉던 자요, 귀부인들 모임의 로크 새 알이요, 배타성을 굴복시킨 자요, 오만을 평탄케 한 자요, 후원자들의 후원자요, 에두름국의 작위들을 두고 장관과 흥정을 벌이던 자요, 길어야 한 십 년이나 십오 년 안에, 적어도 두 세기 동안 영국에서 모든 평화로운 공공 후원자들과 그들의 업적을 증언할 모든 작품을 지닌 모든 예술과 과학의 지도자 전부에게 베풀어진 것보다 더 많은 찬사를 받은 자요, 빛나는 경이요, 선물을 든 현인들이 따라가던 새로운 별자리—욕조 바닥의 어떤 썩은 고깃덩이 위에서 멈춰 사라질 때까지 그들이 따라가던 그 별자리—였던 그가, 그는 다름 아니라 일찍이 교수대를 속여 넘긴 가장 위대한 위조범이요 가장 위대한 도둑이었던 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER 26. Reaping the Whirlwind\n\n그 회오리바람을 거두어들이며\n가쁜 숨소리와 다급한 발소리가 미리 들려오더니, 팽크스 씨가 아서 클레넘의 사무실로 달려들었다. 심문은 끝났고, 그 편지는 세상에 공개되었으며, 은행은 무너졌고, 짚으로 지어진 다른 모범적 구조물들도 불이 붙어 연기가 되어 버렸다. 모두가 우러르던 그 해적선은 온갖 등급의 함선들과 온갖 크기의 작은 배들로 이루어진 거대한 함대 한가운데서 폭발했고, 깊은 바다에는 파멸 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 불타는 선체들, 터지는 화약고들, 저절로 폭발하여 벗과 이웃을 갈가리 찢어 놓는 대포들, 부서진 돛대 조각에 매달려 매 순간 가라앉는 익사자들, 기진한 헤엄꾼들, 떠다니는 시신들, 그리고 상어들 말고는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n공장 사무실의 여느 부지런함과 질서는 무너져 있었다. 뜯지 않은 편지들과 정리되지 않은 서류들이 책상 위에 어지러이 흩어져 있었다. 꺾여 버린 기력과 사라진 희망의 이 표지들 한가운데에, 사무실의 주인은 여느 때 서 있던 자리에 멍하니 서서, 두 팔을 책상 위에 포개고 그 위에 머리를 떨군 채였다.\n\n팽크스 씨는 달려들어와 그를 보고는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 잠시 뒤, 팽크스 씨의 두 팔도 책상 위에 놓였고, 팽크스 씨의 머리도 그 위에 떨궈졌다. 한동안 그들은 이런 자세로, 작은 방의 너비만큼 떨어진 채, 멍하니 말없이 그대로 있었다.\n\n먼저 머리를 들고 입을 연 것은 팽크스 씨였다.\n\n\"제가 권한 거였습니다, 클레넘 씨. 압니다. 무슨 말씀이든 하십시오. 제가 스스로에게 하는 말보다 더 심하게는 못 하실 겁니다. 제가 받아 마땅한 것보다 더 심하게는 못 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 팽크스, 팽크스!\" 클레넘이 대답했다. \"받아 마땅하다는 말은 하지 마시오. 그렇다면 나 자신은 무엇을 받아 마땅하단 말이오!\"\n\n\"더 나은 운이겠지요.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 말이오,\" 클레넘이 그의 말은 듣는 둥 마는 둥 이어 갔다. \"내 동업자를 망쳐 놓은 사람이오! 팽크스, 팽크스, 나는 도이스를 망쳤소! 정직하고, 제 힘으로 일어서고, 지칠 줄 모르고 평생 길을 헤쳐 온 그 노인을. 그토록 많은 좌절에 맞서 싸우면서도, 그 속에서 그토록 선하고 희망에 찬 천성을 길어 올린 사람을. 내가 그토록 마음을 쓰고, 그토록 참되고 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고자 했던 그 사람을. 나는 그를 망쳤소—수치와 불명예로 끌어내렸소—그를 망쳤어, 망쳤단 말이오!\"\n\n이 생각이 그의 마음을 몰아넣은 고통이 보기에 너무도 괴로워서, 팽크스 씨는 그 광경에 절망하며 제 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 쥐어뜯었다.\n\n\"저를 나무라십시오!\" 팽크스가 외쳤다. \"저를 나무라세요, 선생님. 안 그러면 제가 제 몸에 상처라도 내겠습니다. 말씀하세요—이 멍청아, 이 악당아. 말씀하세요—이 얼간아, 어떻게 그럴 수 있었느냐. 이 짐승아, 무슨 속셈이었느냐! 제 어디라도 붙잡으십시오. 저한테 욕설을 퍼부어 주세요!\" 그러는 내내 팽크스 씨는 더없이 무자비하고 잔인하게 제 억센 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 이 끔찍한 광기에 굴복하지만 않았더라도, 팽크스,\" 클레넘이 앙갚음이라기보다는 가엾이 여기는 마음으로 말했다. \"당신에게도 얼마나 더 나았겠으며, 내게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소!\"\n\n\"또 저를 나무라세요, 선생님!\" 팽크스가 회한에 이를 갈며 외쳤다. \"또 나무라세요!\"\n\n\"당신이 그 저주받을 계산에 들어가지만 않았더라도, 그 가증스러우리만치 명확한 결과를 끄집어내지만 않았더라도,\" 클레넘이 신음했다. \"당신에게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소, 팽크스. 내게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소!\"\n\n\"또 저를 나무라세요, 선생님!\" 팽크스가 머리카락 쥔 손을 풀며 외쳤다. \"또, 또 나무라세요!\"\n\n그러나 클레넘은 그가 벌써 진정되기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 하고 싶었던 말, 아니 그 이상을 다 한 셈이었다. 그는 그저 그의 손을 꽉 쥐며 덧붙였다. \"눈먼 자가 눈먼 자를 이끌었구려, 팽크스! 눈먼 자가 눈먼 자를 이끌었어! 하지만 도이스, 도이스, 도이스. 내가 해를 끼친 동업자!\" 그 말과 함께 그의 머리는 다시 한 번 책상 위로 떨궈졌다.\n\n그들의 아까 그 자세와 아까 그 침묵을, 또 먼저 깬 것은 팽크스였다.\n\n\"이 일이 새어 나가기 시작한 뒤로 잠자리에 들지 못했습니다, 선생님. 불길 속에서 타다 남은 재 한 줌이라도 건질 가망이 있을까 싶어 여기저기 뛰어다녔어요. 다 헛수고였습니다. 다 사라졌어요. 다 없어졌습니다.\"\n\n\"나도 알고 있소,\" 클레넘이 대답했다. \"너무도 잘 알고 있어요.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳에서 우러나온 신음으로 그 침묵을 메웠다.\n\n\"바로 어제 말이오, 팽크스,\" 아서가 말했다. \"바로 어제, 월요일에, 나는 팔아치우고, 현금으로 바꾸고, 이 일에서 손을 떼겠다고 마음을 굳혔었소.\"\n\n\"저로서는 그런 말씀도 못 드리겠습니다, 선생님.\" 팽크스가 대답했다. \"하지만 일 년 삼백예순닷새 중에 하필 어제 현금화하려 했었다는 사람을 제가 얼마나 많이 들었는지 놀라울 따름입니다. 너무 늦지만 않았더라면 말이지요!\"\n\n증기처럼 내뿜는 그의 숨소리는, 평소에는 우스꽝스러운 효과를 내곤 했으나, 지금은 그 어떤 신음보다도 더 비극적이었다. 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그가 때 묻고 얼룩지고 돌보지 않은 꼴이어서, 마치 너무 더러워 알아보기조차 어려운, 불운을 그린 진품 초상화라 해도 될 지경이었다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 전부 다… 쏟아부으셨습니까?\" 그는 마지막 낱말 앞에서 한 번 말을 끊었다가 가까스로 넘겼고, 그 마지막 낱말 자체도 무척 힘겹게 내뱉었다.\n\n\"전부 다요.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 다시 제 억센 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 어찌나 세게 잡아당겼는지 몇 가닥이 뽑혀 나왔다. 사나운 증오의 눈으로 그것을 바라보더니, 그는 그것을 주머니에 집어넣었다.\n\n\"내가 갈 길은,\" 클레넘이 얼굴에 소리 없이 흘러내리던 눈물 몇 방울을 닦으며 말했다. \"당장 정해야겠소. 내가 할 수 있는 비참한 보상이나마, 해야만 하오. 나는 불운한 내 동업자의 명예를 깨끗이 해야 하오. 나 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 남겨 두어서는 안 되오. 내가 그토록 함부로 휘둘렀던 경영권을 채권자들에게 넘겨주어야 하며, 내 잘못—아니, 죄—을, 남은 평생 동안 갚아낼 수 있는 만큼은 갚아내야 하오.\"\n\n\"지금 이 고비를 넘기는 것은 불가능합니까, 선생님?\"\n\n\"말도 안 되오. 지금은 그 무엇도 넘길 수 없소, 팽크스. 이 사업이 내 손에서 빨리 떠날수록 사업에는 더 나은 거요. 이번 주에 갚아야 할 약정들이 있어서, 내가 아는 바를 남몰래 알면서도 단 하루라도 이어 가며 그만큼 미루려 한다 해도, 며칠 지나지 않아 파국이 닥칠 거요. 어젯밤 내내 나는 무엇을 해야 할지 생각했소. 이제 남은 건 그것을 실행하는 일이오.\"\n\n\"혼자서 다 하시려는 건 아니겠지요?\" 팽크스가 말했다. 그의 얼굴은, 침울하게 증기를 내뿜는 속도만큼이나 빨리 그 증기가 물로 변하기라도 하듯 축축했다. \"법률적인 도움을 좀 받으십시오.\"\n\n\"그게 낫겠지요.\"\n\n\"러그를 부르십시오.\"\n\n\"할 일이 많지는 않소. 그가 다른 사람만큼은 해줄 거요.\"\n\n\"러그를 데려올까요, 클레넘 씨?\"\n\n\"시간을 내줄 수 있다면, 정말 고맙겠소.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 그 즉시 모자를 쓰고는 펜턴빌을 향해 증기처럼 내달렸다. 그가 가 있는 동안 아서는 책상에서 머리를 한 번도 들지 않고, 그 한 가지 자세 그대로 있었다.\n\n팽크스 씨는 자기 벗이자 직업상의 조언자인 러그 씨를 데리고 돌아왔다. 러그 씨는 오는 길 내내 팽크스 씨가 지금 이성을 잃은 상태라는 것을 충분히 겪은 터라, 그 신사에게 비켜 있어 달라고 청하면서 직업적 중재를 시작했다. 짓눌리고 고분고분해진 팽크스 씨는 그 말에 따랐다.\n\n\"저 친구는, 우리가 러그 대 보킨스의 혼인 약속 위반 소송을 시작했을 때의 제 딸과 비슷합니다, 선생님. 그 소송에서 딸이 원고였지요.\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"사건에 너무 강하고 직접적으로 마음을 쏟거든요. 감정이 휘둘리는 겁니다. 우리 같은 직업에서는, 감정이 휘둘려서는 일이 되질 않습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n장갑을 벗어 모자 안에 넣으면서, 그는 곁눈질 한두 번에 자기 의뢰인에게 큰 변화가 닥쳤음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"이거 안타깝습니다, 선생님.\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"선생님께서 스스로 감정에 휘둘리도록 내버려 두셨군요. 자, 부디 그러지 마십시오, 부디. 이 손실들은 참으로 통탄할 일입니다, 선생님. 하지만 우리는 그것을 똑바로 마주 보아야 합니다.\"\n\n\"내가 날려 버린 그 돈이 전부 내 것이었다면, 러그 씨,\" 클레넘 씨가 한숨지었다. \"훨씬 덜 마음이 쓰였을 거요.\"\n\n\"정말이십니까, 선생님?\" 러그 씨가 유쾌한 기색으로 두 손을 비비며 말했다. \"놀랍습니다. 그건 별난 일이군요, 선생님. 제 경험으로는, 사람들이 가장 까다롭게 구는 건 대개 자기 돈이거든요. 남의 돈이라면 꽤 많이 날려 놓고도 아주 잘 견뎌 내는 사람들을 저는 봐 왔습니다. 정말이지 아주 잘 견디더군요.\"\n\n이런 위로의 말들과 함께 러그 씨는 책상 앞 사무용 걸상에 앉아 본론으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"자, 클레넘 씨, 괜찮으시다면 사안을 짚어 봅시다. 사건의 상태를 봅시다. 문제는 간단합니다. 늘 그렇듯 명료하고 단도직입적이고 상식적인 문제예요. 우리가 우리 자신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐? 우리가 우리 자신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐?\"\n\n\"그건 나에게는 문제가 아니오, 러그 씨.\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신은 처음부터 잘못 짚고 있소. 문제는, 내가 내 동업자를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐, 어떻게 그에게 가장 잘 배상할 수 있느냐 하는 거요.\"\n\n\"이거 송구합니다만, 선생님, 아십니까,\" 러그 씨가 구슬리듯 따졌다. \"선생님께서는 여전히 감정에 휘둘리고 계십니다. 저는 '배상'이라는 말을 좋아하지 않습니다, 선생님. 변호인의 손에 쥐어진 지렛대로 쓰일 때라면 모를까요. 외람되지만, 정말로 감정에 휘둘리셔서는 안 된다는 점을 일러 드리는 게 제 의무라고 느낍니다.\"\n\n\"러그 씨,\" 클레넘이 결심한 일을 끝까지 해내려고 마음을 다잡으며 말했는데, 그 의기소침함 속에서도 확고한 목적의식을 드러내어 그 신사를 놀라게 했다. \"당신은 내가 마음먹은 길을 그다지 따르고 싶어하지 않으리라는 인상을 주는구려. 그것을 못마땅히 여겨 거기에 필요한 일을 맡기를 꺼린다면, 안타까운 일이지만 나는 다른 도움을 구해야 할 거요. 그러나 분명히 말해 두겠소. 그 일을 두고 나와 따져 봐야 소용없다는 것을.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 선생님.\" 러그 씨가 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다. \"좋습니다, 선생님. 누군가의 손으로 해야 할 일이라면, 제 손으로 하지요. 러그 대 보킨스 사건에서도 그게 제 원칙이었습니다. 대개의 사건에서 그게 제 원칙이고요.\"\n\n이어 클레넘은 러그 씨에게 자기의 확고한 결심을 설명했다. 그는 러그 씨에게, 자기 동업자가 더없이 소박하고 청렴한 사람이라는 것, 그리고 자기가 하려는 모든 일에서 무엇보다도 그 동업자의 성품에 대한 이해와 그 감정에 대한 존중을 지침으로 삼고 있다는 것을 말했다. 그는, 동업자가 지금 중요한 사업차 멀리 나가 있다는 것, 그리고 자기가 경솔하게 저지른 일에 대한 책임을 공개적으로 받아들이고, 동업자가 그 책임에 조금도 가담하지 않았음을 공개적으로 밝혀 주는 것이 특히 자기에게 마땅한 도리라는 것을 설명했다. 다른 나라에 있는 동업자의 명예와 신용에 부당하게 조금이라도 의심이 따라붙어, 그 사업의 성공적인 수행이 위태로워지는 일이 없도록 말이다. 그는 러그 씨에게, 동업자의 도덕적 결백을 더없이 온전하게 밝혀 주는 것, 그리고 그 상사(商社)의 일원인 자기 아서 클레넘이 오로지 단독으로, 그것도 동업자의 만류를 명백히 거스르면서까지, 최근에 파산한 그 사기 사업에 사업 자금을 쏟아부었음을 공개적이고 거리낌 없이 선언하는 것이야말로, 자기 힘이 닿는 유일한 진정한 속죄라고 말했다. 그것은 다른 여러 사람에게 하는 것보다 그 특정한 사람에게는 더 나은 속죄이며, 그렇기에 자기가 가장 먼저 해야 할 속죄라는 것이었다. 이런 취지에서 그의 뜻은, 앞서 말한 내용을 담아 이미 작성해 둔 선언문을 인쇄하여, 그 상사와 거래하던 모든 이에게 돌리는 것은 물론, 신문에 광고로도 싣겠다는 것이었다. 이 조치와 더불어(이를 설명하는 동안 러그 씨는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 찡그린 표정을 짓고 사지를 크게 들썩였다), 그는 모든 채권자에게 편지를 보내, 엄숙한 방식으로 동업자의 결백을 밝히고, 그들의 뜻을 알 수 있고 동업자와 연락이 닿을 때까지 상사의 영업을 중단함을 알리며, 겸허히 자기 자신을 그들의 처분에 맡기려 했다. 만일 그들이 동업자의 결백을 헤아려, 사업을 다시 수익성 있게 재개하고 지금의 몰락을 극복할 수 있을 만한 궤도에 일이 오르게 된다면, 그때는 자기 몫을 동업자에게 돌려주겠다는 것이었다. 자기가 불행히도 그에게 안긴 고통과 손실에 대해 금전적 가치로 갚을 수 있는 유일한 배상이 그것이기 때문이었다. 그리고 자기 자신은, 먹고살 수 있는 한도에서 가장 적은 봉급을 받으며, 충실한 사무원으로서 그 사업에 봉사하도록 허락받기를 청하겠다고 했다.\n\n러그 씨는 이 일이 실행되는 것을 막을 수 없음을 빤히 알았으나, 그래도 찡그린 그의 얼굴과 들썩이는 사지가 항의 한마디로 달래지기를 어찌나 절실히 요구하는지, 그는 항의를 하나 내놓았다. \"저는 반대하지 않습니다, 선생님.\" 그가 말했다. \"한 가지도 따지지 않겠습니다. 선생님의 뜻을 받들겠습니다, 선생님. 다만, 항의는 해 두겠습니다.\" 이어 러그 씨는 장황하지 않다 할 수 없게 자기 항의의 요점들을 늘어놓았다. 그 요점들은, 요컨대 이런 것이었다. 온 도시가, 아니 온 나라가 이번 사건의 발각으로 처음의 광란에 빠져 있고, 피해자들에 대한 분노가 몹시 강할 것이다. 속아 넘어가지 않은 자들은 피해자들이 자기들처럼 영리하지 못했다며 틀림없이 길길이 화를 낼 것이고, 속아 넘어간 자들은 자기 자신에게는 변명과 이유를 찾아내면서도, 다른 피해자들에게는 그런 것이 전혀 없다고 똑같이 확신할 것이 틀림없으니 말이다. 게다가 피해자 한 사람 한 사람이, 다른 모든 피해자가 본을 보이지만 않았던들 자기는 결코 고통받을 처지에 빠지지 않았으리라고 스스로를 격렬한 분개 속에 설득할 공산이 크다는 것은 말할 것도 없었다. 또한, 이런 때에 내놓는 클레넘의 그런 선언은 틀림없이 그에게 적의의 폭풍을 불러내려, 채권자들 사이에서 관용을 기대하거나 그들의 의견 일치를 기대하기란 불가능해지고, 그를 외로운 표적으로 만들어, 산발적인 십자포화 속에 놓아, 한꺼번에 대여섯 방향에서 그를 쓰러뜨릴지도 모른다는 것이었다.\n\n이 모든 것에 클레넘은 그저, 항의 전체를 인정한다 해도, 그 안의 그 무엇도 동업자를 자발적이고 공개적으로 면죄해 주는 일의 효력을 줄이지 못하며 줄일 수도 없다고만 대답했다. 그러므로 그는 단번에 영원히, 일이 신속히 처리되도록 러그 씨가 곧바로 도와줄 것을 청했다. 그러자 러그 씨는 일에 착수했다. 그리고 아서는 자기 옷가지와 책들, 그리고 약간의 잔돈 말고는 아무 재산도 자기 몫으로 남기지 않은 채, 자기의 보잘것없는 개인 은행 계좌를 사업 서류들과 함께 내놓았다.\n\n진상이 공개되었고, 폭풍은 무섭게 휘몰아쳤다. 수천 명의 사람들이 비난을 퍼부을 산 사람 하나를 찾아 미친 듯이 두리번거리고 있었다. 그리고 이 세상의 이목을 끄는 두드러진 사건은, 그토록 절실히 찾던 그 산 사람 하나를 처형대 위에 세워 놓았다. 이 사건과 아무 상관도 없는 사람들조차 그 파렴치함을 이토록 절감했으니, 이 일로 돈을 잃은 사람들이 그것을 너그럽게 다루기를 기대하기란 어려운 일이었다. 채권자들로부터 비난과 욕설의 편지가 빗발쳤다. 그리고 날마다 그 높은 걸상에 앉아 그것을 모조리 읽던 러그 씨는, 일주일 안에 의뢰인에게 영장이 발부된 듯하다고 알려 왔다.\n\n\"내가 한 일의 결과는 내가 감당해야지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"영장은 여기서 나를 찾아낼 거요.\"\n\n바로 그다음 날 아침, 그가 블리딩 하트 야드로 들어서면서 플로니시 부인의 모퉁이를 돌 때, 플로니시 부인이 문가에 서서 그를 기다리고 있다가, 비밀스럽게 해피 코티지로 들어와 달라고 청했다. 그곳에서 그는 러그 씨를 만났다.\n\n\"여기서 선생님을 기다리는 게 낫겠다 싶었습니다. 제가 선생님이라면 오늘 아침 사무실에는 안 나가겠습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇소, 러그 씨?\"\n\n\"제가 아는 것만 해도 다섯 건이나 나와 있습니다.\"\n\n\"빠를수록 좋소.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"당장 나를 잡아가게 합시다.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만요,\" 러그 씨가 그와 문 사이를 가로막으며 말했다. \"이치를 들어 보십시오, 이치를 들어 보세요. 그들은 곧 선생님을 잡아갈 겁니다, 클레넘 씨, 그건 의심치 않습니다. 하지만 이치를 들어 보세요. 이런 경우에는 거의 늘, 별것 아닌 사소한 건이 앞으로 밀고 나와 제딴에 으스대게 마련입니다. 그런데 보니 작은 게 하나 나와 있어요—한낱 궁정재판소 관할의 것이지요—그런데 제가 보기엔 그걸로 체포가 이루어질 듯합니다. 저라면 그걸로는 잡혀가지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇소?\" 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"저라면 제대로 격을 갖춘 큰 건으로 잡혀가겠습니다, 선생님.\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"체면은 차리는 게 좋지요. 선생님의 직업적 조언자로서, 외람되나마 저를 봐서라도, 상급 법원들 가운데 하나에서 나온 영장으로 잡혀가시는 편이 좋겠습니다. 그게 보기에 더 낫습니다.\"\n\n\"러그 씨,\" 아서가 의기소침하게 말했다. \"내 유일한 바람은, 이 일이 끝나는 것뿐이오. 나는 그냥 가서, 운에 맡기겠소.\"\n\n\"이치를 한마디만 더 들어 보십시오, 선생님!\" 러그 씨가 외쳤다. \"자, 이건 이치입니다. 아까 그건 취향일 수도 있겠지요. 하지만 이건 이치입니다. 작은 건으로 잡혀가시면, 선생님은 마셜시로 가게 됩니다. 자, 마셜시가 어떤 곳인지 아시지요. 아주 좁습니다. 지나치게 비좁아요. 그에 비해 왕좌재판소라면—\" 러그 씨는 널찍한 공간을 나타내듯 오른손을 시원하게 내저었다.\n\n\"나는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"다른 어떤 감옥보다도 마셜시로 잡혀가는 편이 낫소.\"\n\n\"정말로 그러십니까, 선생님?\" 러그 씨가 대꾸했다. \"그렇다면 그것도 취향이로군요. 그럼 가 보도록 하지요.\"\n\n그는 처음엔 다소 언짢아했으나, 곧 그 일을 넘겨 버렸다. 그들은 야드를 가로질러 반대편 끝으로 걸어갔다. 블리딩 하트의 주민들은 아서가 몰락을 겪은 뒤로 전보다 그에게 더 큰 관심을 보였다. 이제 그를 이 동네에 충실하여 그 자유권을 얻은 사람쯤으로 여기는 것이었다. 그들 가운데 많은 이가 나와서 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 \"이 일로 풀이 죽었다\"고 서로에게 자못 능청스럽게 속삭였다. 플로니시 부인과 그녀의 아버지는 자기들 쪽 끝 층계 꼭대기에 서서, 몹시 침울하게 고개를 절레절레 젓고 있었다.\n\n아서와 러그 씨가 사무실에 이르렀을 때 기다리는 사람은 보이지 않았다. 그러나 럼주에 절여진 유대교 신앙의 한 노인이 그들을 바짝 뒤따라와, 러그 씨가 그날 온 편지 하나를 채 뜯기도 전에 유리창 너머로 안을 들여다보았다. \"오!\" 러그 씨가 고개를 들며 말했다. \"안녕하시오? 들어오시오—클레넘 씨, 제가 말씀드렸던 그 신사인 것 같습니다.\"\n\n이 신사는 자기가 찾아온 목적이 \"쪼끄만 일거리\"라고 설명하고는, 자기 법적 직무를 수행했다.\n* 럼주에 절여진: 술에 절어 사는 모습을 익살스럽게 표현한 말.\n\n\"제가 동행할까요, 클레넘 씨?\" 러그 씨가 두 손을 비비며 정중히 물었다.\n\n\"혼자 가는 편이 낫겠소, 고맙소. 내 옷가지나 보내 주시면 고맙겠소.\" 러그 씨는 가벼운 어조로 그러겠다고 대답하고는, 그와 악수했다. 그러고 나서 그와 그의 동행자는 아래층으로 내려가, 처음 눈에 띈 탈것에 올라타고는, 그 오래된 문들을 향해 마차를 몰았다.\n\n\"하느님, 용서하소서. 내가 이런 식으로 이곳에 들어오게 되리라고는,\" 클레넘이 혼잣말을 했다. \"꿈에도 생각지 못했건만!\"\n\n치버리 씨가 자물쇠를 맡고 있었고, 젊은 존은 수위실에 있었다. 막 근무를 마치고 나온 참이거나, 아니면 제 차례의 당번을 맡으려고 기다리는 중이었다. 두 사람은 죄수가 누구인지 알아보고는, 간수라면 그러려니 짐작할 정도보다 훨씬 더 놀랐다. 나이 든 치버리 씨는 면구스러운 듯한 태도로 그와 악수하며 말했다. \"선생님, 선생님을 뵙고 이토록 반갑지 않았던 적은 없는 것 같습니다.\" 더 서먹한 젊은 치버리 씨는 그와 악수도 전혀 하지 않았다. 그는 망설이는 기색이 어찌나 뚜렷했던지, 무거운 눈과 무거운 마음을 한 클레넘의 눈에까지 들어올 정도로, 그를 바라보며 서 있었다. 잠시 뒤, 젊은 존은 감옥 안으로 사라졌다.\n\n클레넘은 이곳을 충분히 알고 있어서, 자기가 수위실에 일정 시간 머물러 있어야 한다는 것을 알았으므로, 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고, 주머니에서 꺼낸 편지들을 읽는 척했다. 그러나 그 편지들이 그의 주의를 다 사로잡지는 못했기에, 그는 나이 든 치버리 씨가 죄수들이 수위실에 들어오지 못하게 하는 모습을 고맙게 지켜보았다. 어떤 이들에게는 열쇠로 들어오지 말라는 신호를 보내고, 또 어떤 이들은 팔꿈치로 슬쩍 밀어 내보내며, 그가 겪는 비참함을 되도록 덜어 주려 애쓰는 모습을 말이다.\n\n아서는 눈을 바닥에 고정한 채, 과거를 떠올리고 현재를 곱씹으면서도 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 마음을 두지 못한 채 앉아 있었는데, 그때 누군가 자기 어깨를 건드리는 것을 느꼈다. 젊은 존이었다. 그가 말했다. \"이제 들어오셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n그는 일어나 젊은 존을 따라갔다. 안쪽 철문을 한두 걸음 들어선 뒤, 젊은 존이 돌아서서 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"방이 필요하시겠지요. 하나 마련해 두었습니다.\"\n\n\"진심으로 고맙소.\"\n\n젊은 존은 다시 돌아서서, 그를 그 옛 문간으로 들여, 그 옛 층계를 올라, 그 옛 방으로 데려갔다. 아서가 손을 내밀었다. 젊은 존은 그 손을 보고, 그를 보더니—매섭게—울컥하여, 목이 메어, 말했다.\n\n\"악수는 못 하겠습니다. 아니요, 못 하겠어요. 하지만 이 방을 마음에 들어 하실 것 같아서요. 그러니 여기, 선생님 방입니다.\"\n\n이 모순된 행동에 대한 놀라움은, 그가 가 버리자(그는 곧장 떠났다), 텅 빈 방이 클레넘의 상처 입은 가슴에 불러일으킨 감정들에, 그리고 이 방을 거룩하게 했던 그 선하고 다정한 한 사람과 얽힌 밀려드는 회상들에 자리를 내주었다. 처지가 뒤바뀐 지금 그녀가 없다는 것이, 이 방을, 그리고 그 안의 그를, 너무도 황량하게, 그리고 사랑과 진실이 어린 그런 얼굴을 너무도 절실히 필요로 하게 만들어서, 그는 벽을 향해 돌아서서 흐느꼈다. 마음이 풀려나면서, 흐느끼며 그는 토해 냈다. \"오, 나의 리틀 도릿!\"\n\n# CHAPTER 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea\n\n날은 화창했고, 뜨거운 한낮의 볕이 내리쬐는 마셜시는 평소와 달리 고요했다. 아서 클레넘은 이 감옥의 어느 채무자 못지않게 빛바랜 외딴 안락의자에 몸을 묻고서, 자신의 상념에 몸을 내맡겼다.\n\n그토록 두려워하던 체포를 다 겪고 마침내 이곳에 들어와 버린 데서 오는 부자연스러운 평온 속에서—이는 감옥이 가장 흔히 불러일으키는 첫 번째 감정의 변화로, 그토록 많은 사람이 그 위험한 안식처에서 그토록 많은 길을 거쳐 타락과 치욕의 나락으로 미끄러져 내려간 그 평온이었는데—그는 자신이 마치 그 일들에서 떨어져 나와 또 다른 존재의 상태로 옮겨 간 것처럼, 제 인생의 몇몇 대목을 떠올릴 수 있었다. 그가 지금 있는 곳, 자유로이 멀리할 수 있었음에도 처음 그를 이곳으로 이끌었던 관심, 그리고 그를 둘러싼 벽과 창살에서도, 또 어떤 벽이나 창살로도 가둘 수 없는 만년의 손에 잡히지 않는 추억들에서도 똑같이 떼어 놓을 수 없던 그 다정한 존재—이 모든 것을 헤아려 보면, 그의 기억이 어디로 향하든 끝내 다시 리틀 도릿에게로 돌아오는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그렇지만 그에게는 그것이 놀라웠다. 그 사실 자체 때문이 아니라, 그 사실이 함께 가져다주는 일깨움 때문이었다—그 사랑스러운 작은 존재가 그의 더 나은 결심들에 얼마나 큰 영향을 끼쳤는지를.\n\n우리 가운데 누구도, 인생이라는 빙글빙글 도는 수레바퀴에 어떤 뚜렷한 멈춤이 와서 올바른 깨달음을 함께 가져다주기 전까지는, 이런 식으로 우리가 누구에게 또는 무엇에 빚지고 있는지 분명히 알지 못한다. 그 깨달음은 병과 함께 오고, 슬픔과 함께 오며, 끔찍이 사랑하던 이를 잃을 때 오니, 역경이 주는 가장 흔한 쓸모 가운데 하나다. 그것은 역경에 처한 클레넘에게 강렬하면서도 부드럽게 찾아왔다. '내가 처음으로 정신을 다잡고서,' 그는 생각했다, '지친 두 눈앞에 무언가 목적 비슷한 것을 세웠을 때, 내 앞에는 누가 있었던가, 격려도 없고 주목도 없이, 받들어 모셔진 영웅과 영웅호걸들의 군대조차 등 돌리게 만들었을 그 비천한 장애들에 맞서, 선한 목적을 위해 묵묵히 애쓰던 이가? 연약한 처녀 하나였다! 내가 잘못 둔 사랑을 이겨 내고, 나보다 더 행복한 그 사내에게—그가 결코 알지도 못하고 다정한 말 한마디로 갚지도 않을 텐데도—너그럽게 대하려 애썼을 때, 인내와 자기 부정, 자기 억제, 자비로운 헤아림, 애정의 가장 고귀한 너그러움을 내가 지켜본 이는 누구였던가? 바로 그 가엾은 처녀였다! 만일 나, 한 사내로서 사내가 지닌 이점과 수단과 기력을 갖춘 내가, 아버지가 잘못을 저질렀다면 그 허물을 감추고 바로잡는 것이 나의 첫째 의무라고 가슴속에서 속삭이는 그 소리를 무시했더라면, 축축한 땅 위를 거의 맨발로 디디며, 야윈 두 손으로 쉼 없이 일하고, 가냘픈 몸을 매서운 날씨로부터 절반밖에 가리지 못한 채, 그 어떤 젊은 모습이 내 앞에 서서 나를 부끄럽게 했을 것인가? 바로 리틀 도릿의 모습이었으리라.' 빛바랜 의자에 홀로 앉아 그는 늘 이렇게 생각에 잠겼다. 언제나, 리틀 도릿이었다. 마침내 그에게는, 그녀에게서 멀리 떠돌아다니며 그녀의 덕에 대한 자신의 기억과 자기 사이에 무엇이든 끼어들도록 내버려 둔 데 대한 응보를 만나는 것처럼 여겨질 지경이었다.\n\n그의 방문이 열리더니, 늙은 치버리의 머리가 그를 향해 돌아보지도 않은 채 아주 조금만 안으로 들이밀어졌다.\n\n\"저는 이제 문지기 당번에서 빠져서 나가는 길입니다, 클레넘 씨. 뭐 해 드릴 일이라도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"고맙소만. 없소이다.\"\n\n\"문을 열어서 죄송합니다.\" 치버리 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 아무리 불러도 못 들으시더군요.\"\n\n\"노크를 했소?\" \"여섯 번쯤 했습니다.\"\n\n정신을 차리고 보니, 클레넘은 감옥이 한낮의 졸음에서 깨어났고, 수감자들이 그늘진 마당을 어슬렁거리고 있으며, 늦은 오후가 되었음을 알아차렸다. 그는 몇 시간을 줄곧 생각에 잠겨 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"짐이 도착했습니다.\" 치버리 씨가 말했다. \"제 아들놈이 들고 올라갈 겁니다. 제가 올려보냈을 텐데, 녀석이 제 손으로 가져가겠다고 해서요. 정말이지 제가 직접 하겠다고 우기는 통에, 제가 올려보낼 수가 없었습니다. 클레넘 씨, 한 말씀 드려도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"부디 들어오시오.\" 아서가 말했다. 치버리 씨의 머리는 여전히 문틈으로 아주 조금만 들이밀어져 있었고, 두 눈을 다 그에게 두는 대신 한쪽 귀만 그에게 향하고 있었기 때문이다. 이는 치버리 씨 특유의 섬세함이었다—참된 예의 바름이었다. 비록 그 외양은 간수의 티가 다분하고 신사다운 구석이라곤 조금도 없었지만 말이다.\n\n\"감사합니다, 선생님.\" 치버리 씨가 안으로 들어오지 않은 채 말했다. \"제가 들어가고 자시고 할 것 없습니다. 클레넘 씨, 제 아들놈이 어떻게든 까칠하게 굴더라도 (괜찮으시다면) 너무 마음에 두지 말아 주십시오. 제 아들놈한테는 마음이 있고, 그 녀석 마음은 제자리에 똑바로 박혀 있습니다. 저랑 그 어미는 그게 어디 있는지 알고 있고, 또 제대로 자리 잡고 있는 걸 확인했습죠.\"\n\n이 알쏭달쏭한 말을 남기고서, 치버리 씨는 귀를 거두고 문을 닫았다. 그가 떠난 지 십 분쯤 되었을 무렵, 그의 아들이 뒤를 이었다.\n\n\"여기 선생님 여행 가방입니다.\" 그가 아서에게 말하며 조심스레 가방을 내려놓았다.\n\n\"정말 친절하시군요. 이런 수고를 끼쳐 부끄럽습니다.\"\n\n그 말이 끝나기도 전에 그는 나가 버렸지만, 곧 돌아와 아까와 똑같은 투로 말했다. \"여기 선생님 검은 상자입니다.\" 그러고는 이것 또한 조심스레 내려놓았다.\n\n\"이렇게 신경 써 주시니 깊이 고맙소. 이제 우리 악수라도 합시다, 존 군.\"\n\n그러나 젊은 존은 뒤로 물러서며, 왼손 엄지와 가운뎃손가락으로 만든 고리에 오른쪽 손목을 끼워 돌리더니, 처음에 했던 것과 똑같이 말했다. \"그럴 수 있을지 모르겠습니다. 아니, 못 하겠는데요!\" 그러고는 죄수를 엄하게 바라보며 서 있었는데, 그 두 눈에는 연민처럼 보이는 벅찬 감정이 차오르고 있었다.\n\n\"어찌하여 내게 화가 나 있으면서도,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"이토록 기꺼이 이런 친절을 베푸는 거요? 우리 사이에 무슨 오해가 있는 모양이오. 내가 그럴 만한 일을 했다면 미안하오.\"\n\n\"오해는 없습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 손목을 고리 속에서 앞뒤로 돌리며 대꾸했다. 고리가 다소 빡빡했다. \"지금 이 순간 제 두 눈이 선생님을 바라보는 그 심정에는 오해가 없습니다! 제가 선생님 체급에 조금이라도 걸맞기만 하다면—그렇지 못합니다만—그리고 선생님이 곤경에 처해 있지만 않다면—처해 있습니다만—그리고 그게 마셜시의 온갖 규칙에 어긋나지만 않는다면—어긋납니다만—그 심정이란 게 어찌나 들끓는지, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 어떤 일보다도 지금 바로 이 자리에서 선생님과 한판 붙고 싶게 만든답니다.\"\n\n아서는 잠시 다소 의아하고 또 조금 노여운 마음으로 그를 바라보았다. \"원, 이런!\" 그가 말했다. \"오해요, 오해라니까!\" 그는 몸을 돌려, 무거운 한숨과 함께 다시 빛바랜 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n젊은 존은 눈으로 그를 좇다가, 잠깐 사이를 두고 외쳤다. \"죄송합니다!\"\n\n\"흔쾌히 받아들이리다.\" 클레넘이 수그린 고개를 들지도 않은 채 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"더 말하지 마시오. 나는 그럴 값어치도 없는 사람이오.\"\n\n\"이 가구는요, 선생님,\" 젊은 존이 온화하고 부드럽게 설명하는 어조로 말했다, \"제 것입니다. 저는 방은 있지만 가구가 없는 분들한테 이걸 빌려드리곤 합니다. 대단한 건 아닙니다만, 선생님 마음대로 쓰십시오. 공짜로 말입니다. 다른 조건으로 빌려드릴 생각은 도저히 못 하겠습니다. 거저 쓰셔도 좋습니다.\"\n\n아서는 다시 고개를 들어 고마움을 표하고는, 그 호의를 받을 수 없다고 말하려 했다. 존은 여전히 손목을 돌리고 있었고, 여전히 아까처럼 마음이 둘로 갈린 채 자신과 씨름하고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 사이에 대체 무슨 문제가 있는 거요?\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 입에 올리지 않겠습니다, 선생님.\" 젊은 존이 갑자기 크고 날카로운 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"아무 문제 없습니다.\"\n\n아서는 그의 행동을 설명해 줄 무언가를 찾으려 다시 그를 바라보았으나 허사였다. 잠시 뒤 아서는 다시 고개를 돌렸다. 곧이어 젊은 존이 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"선생님 팔꿈치 가까이에 있는 저 작고 둥근 탁자는요—그분 것이었습니다—누구인지 굳이 말씀드리지 않아도—그분은 훌륭한 신사로 돌아가셨지요. 저는 그분이 그걸 준 어떤 사람한테서 샀습니다. 그분 뒤에 여기 살던 사람이었지요. 그렇지만 그 사람은 도무지 그분에 견줄 만한 사람이 아니었습니다. 웬만한 사람은 그분 수준에 이르기가 어려울 겁니다.\"\n\n아서는 그 작은 탁자를 더 가까이 끌어당겨, 그 위에 팔을 얹고는 그대로 두었다.\n\n\"아마 모르고 계실지도 모르겠습니다만, 선생님,\" 젊은 존이 말했다, \"그분이 여기 런던에 와 계실 때 제가 찾아뵌 적이 있습니다. 따지고 보면 그분은 그게 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기시는 눈치였습니다만, 그래도 친절하게 앉으라 하시고 아버지며 다른 옛 친구분들 안부를 물어 주셨지요. 하기야 가장 미천한 지인들이라고 해야겠습니다만. 제가 보기엔 그분은 꽤나 달라지신 듯해서, 돌아와서 그렇게 말했습니다. 제가 에이미 양은 잘 지내시느냐고 여쭸더니—\"\n\n\"그래서, 잘 지내고 있었소?\"\n\"저 같은 사람한테 묻지 않으셔도 선생님은 알고 계시리라 생각했습니다만.\" 젊은 존이 커다란 보이지 않는 알약을 삼키는 듯한 표정을 짓고 나서 대꾸했다. \"기어이 제게 물으시니, 답해 드릴 수 없어 죄송합니다. 사실인즉슨, 그분은 그 물음을 무엄한 짓으로 여기시고는 '그게 자네랑 무슨 상관인가?' 하셨거든요. 그제야 저는 제가 주제넘은 짓을 하고 있다는 걸 똑똑히 깨달았습니다. 그 전부터 그럴까 봐 두려워하긴 했습니다만. 그렇지만 그 뒤로는 아주 점잖게 말씀해 주셨습니다. 아주 점잖게요.\"\n\n두 사람 다 몇 분 동안 말이 없었다. 다만 젊은 존이 그 침묵의 한중간쯤에서 한마디 덧붙였을 뿐이다. \"말씀도 점잖으셨고, 행동도 아주 점잖으셨습니다.\"\n\n침묵을 다시 깬 것도 젊은 존이었으니, 그가 물었다.\n\n\"외람된 말씀이 아니라면, 선생님, 대체 언제까지 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 않으실 작정입니까?\"\n\n\"아직 아무것도 아쉽지가 않소.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"지금은 입맛이 없소이다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 더더욱 뭐라도 좀 드셔야 합니다, 선생님.\" 젊은 존이 권했다. \"입맛이 없다고 해서 아무것도 안 드시고 여기 몇 시간이고 앉아 계실 작정이시라면, 입맛이 없더라도 뭐라도 드셔야 하고 또 드셔야만 합니다. 저는 제 방에서 차를 마시려던 참입니다. 외람되지 않다면, 오셔서 한 잔 드시지요. 아니면 제가 이 분 안에 쟁반을 여기로 가져오겠습니다.\"\n\n거절하면 젊은 존이 그 수고를 제 손으로 떠맡으리라는 것을 느꼈고, 또 늙은 치버리 씨의 간청과 젊은 치버리 씨의 사과를 둘 다 마음에 새기고 있음을 보여 주고 싶기도 하여, 아서는 자리에서 일어나 존 군의 방에서 차 한 잔을 들겠노라고 흔쾌히 응했다. 젊은 존은 나가면서 그를 위해 문을 잠그고는, 아주 능숙한 솜씨로 열쇠를 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣고, 자기 거처로 앞장서 갔다.\n\n그 방은 정문에서 가장 가까운, 건물 맨 꼭대기에 있었다. 부유해진 가족이 영영 감옥을 떠나던 날 클레넘이 황급히 달려갔던 바로 그 방, 그가 정신을 잃은 그녀를 마룻바닥에서 안아 올렸던 그 방이었다. 두 사람의 발이 계단에 닿자마자 그는 어디로 가는지를 미리 알아챘다. 방은 이제 벽지가 발리고 새로 페인트칠이 되었으며 훨씬 안락하게 꾸며져 있어 많이 달라져 있었다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바닥에서 안아 올려 마차로 데려가던 그 한 번의 눈길에서 본 그대로 그 방을 떠올릴 수 있었다.\n\n젊은 존은 손가락을 깨물며 그를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"이 방을 기억하시는군요, 클레넘 씨?\"\n\n\"잘 기억하오. 그녀에게 하늘의 축복이 있기를!\"\n\n차는 까맣게 잊은 채, 젊은 존은 손님이 방 안을 두리번거리는 내내 손가락을 깨물며 그를 바라보았다. 마침내 그는 찻주전자를 와락 집어 들더니, 통에서 차를 한 움큼 요란하게 쏟아 넣고는, 뜨거운 물을 채우러 공동 부엌으로 향했다.\n\n비참한 마셜시로 돌아온 그 달라진 처지에서, 그 방은 클레넘에게 어찌나 많은 것을 말해 주던지. 그 방은 어찌나 애잔하게 그녀를, 또 그녀를 잃은 일을 그에게 들려주던지. 혼자가 아니었더라도 그것을 견뎌 내기는 힘들었으리라. 혼자였기에, 그는 견디려 들지도 않았다. 그는 마치 그녀를 만지듯 다정하게 무정한 벽에 손을 얹고는, 낮은 목소리로 그녀의 이름을 불렀다. 그는 창가에 서서, 음산한 대못이 박힌 가장자리가 둘린 감옥의 흉벽 너머를 바라보며, 여름 안개 사이로, 그녀가 부유하고 번창하게 지내는 저 머나먼 땅을 향해 축복의 기도를 불어 보냈다.\n\n젊은 존은 한참을 나가 있었는데, 돌아왔을 때 그가 밖에 다녀왔음을 보여 주었으니, 양배추 잎에 싼 신선한 버터와 또 다른 양배추 잎에 싼 얇게 저민 삶은 햄 몇 점, 그리고 물냉이와 샐러드용 풀이 담긴 작은 바구니를 들고 온 것이었다. 이것들을 흡족하게 탁자에 차려 놓은 뒤, 두 사람은 차를 마시려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n클레넘은 그 식사를 잘 받들어 보려 애썼으나 소용이 없었다. 햄은 속을 메스껍게 했고, 빵은 입안에서 모래로 변하는 듯했다. 그가 억지로 넘길 수 있는 것이라곤 차 한 잔뿐이었다.\n\n\"푸성귀라도 좀 드셔 보세요.\" 젊은 존이 바구니를 건네며 말했다.\n\n그는 물냉이를 한두 줄기 집어 다시 먹어 보려 했으나, 빵은 전보다 더 무거운 모래로 변했고, 햄은(그 자체로는 충분히 괜찮았는데도) 마셜시 전체에 햄의 희미한 모래바람을 불어 보내는 듯했다.\n\n\"푸성귀를 좀 더 드셔 보세요, 선생님.\" 젊은 존이 말하며 다시 바구니를 건넸다.\n\n그것은 마치 갇혀 풀이 죽은 새의 새장 안으로 푸른 먹이를 넣어 주는 것과 어찌나 비슷하던지, 또 존이 그 작은 바구니를 감옥의 케케묵고 뜨거운 포석과 벽돌로부터 한 줌의 신선한 위안으로 가져온 것이 어찌나 분명하던지, 클레넘은 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"이걸 창살 사이로 넣어 줄 생각을 하다니 참 친절하시오. 하지만 오늘은 이것조차 넘기질 못하겠소.\"\n\n마치 그 곤란함이 옮기라도 하는 듯, 젊은 존도 곧 제 접시를 밀쳐 두고, 햄을 쌌던 양배추 잎을 접기 시작했다. 그것을 여러 겹 차곡차곡 접어 손바닥에 들어올 만큼 작게 만든 뒤, 두 손 사이에 끼워 납작하게 누르며 클레넘을 유심히 바라보았다.\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 그가 마침내 그 푸른 꾸러미를 다소 힘주어 누르며 말했다, \"선생님 자신을 위해서는 몸을 돌볼 가치가 없다 하더라도, 다른 누군가를 위해서라면 그럴 가치가 있지 않을까 싶습니다만.\"\n\n\"솔직히,\" 아서가 한숨과 미소를 섞어 대꾸했다, \"그게 누굴 위해서인지 나는 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨,\" 존이 발끈하여 말했다, \"선생님 같은 솔직함을 갖추신 신사분이 제게 그런 답변을 하는 옹졸한 짓을 하실 수 있다니 놀랍습니다. 클레넘 씨, 자기 가슴을 지니신 신사분이 그렇게 남의 가슴을 짓밟는 매정한 짓을 하실 수 있다니 놀랍습니다. 저는 그게 놀랍습니다, 선생님. 정말로 진심으로 놀랍습니다!\"\n\n끝맺는 말에 힘을 싣느라 자리에서 벌떡 일어났던 젊은 존은 다시 앉아, 그 푸른 꾸러미를 자기 오른쪽 다리에 대고 굴리기 시작했다. 클레넘에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 분개에 찬 비난의 시선으로 그를 줄곧 살펴보았다.\n\n\"저는 그걸 다 이겨 냈었습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 말했다. \"반드시 이겨 내야만 한다는 걸 알기에 그걸 정복하고, 두 번 다시 그 일을 생각하지 않기로 결심까지 했었습니다. 만일 선생님이 이 감옥으로 끌려오시지만 않았더라면, 그것도 제게 하필이면 불운한 시각에, 오늘 같은 날에 말입니다, 저는 다시 그 일에 마음을 두지 않았을 겁니다!\" (격앙된 나머지 젊은 존은 어머니 특유의 위풍당당한 문장 구조를 빌려 쓰고 있었다.) \"오늘 선생님이 처음 수위실에서 제 앞에 나타나셨을 때, 마치 일개 사사로운 피고가 아니라 한 그루 우파스 나무가 사로잡혀 온 것 같았는데, 제 안에서 그 뒤섞인 감정의 물줄기가 다시 터져 나와, 처음 몇 분간은 그 앞에서 모든 게 휩쓸려 가 버리고, 저는 소용돌이 속을 빙글빙글 돌았습니다. 저는 거기서 빠져나왔습니다. 안간힘을 써서 빠져나왔습니다. 이게 제가 남길 마지막 말이라 해도, 저는 온 힘을 다해 그 소용돌이에 맞서 싸웠고, 거기서 빠져나왔습니다. 제가 무례했다면 사과가 마땅하다고 스스로 따져, 비굴해질 것도 없이 그 사과를 했습니다. 그런데 이제, 한 가지 생각이 제게는 거의 거룩한 것이나 다름없고 다른 무엇보다 앞선다는 걸 그토록 보여 드리고 싶었건만—이제, 결국에 가서, 제가 그걸 아무리 점잖게 넌지시 비쳐도 선생님은 슬쩍 피해 버리시고, 저를 도로 제 안으로 내동댕이치시는군요. 그러니, 선생님,\" 젊은 존이 말했다, \"그렇게 피하셨다는 걸, 또 저를 도로 제 안으로 내던지셨다는 걸 부인하는 비열한 짓일랑 하지 말아 주십시오!\"\n\n온통 어리둥절해진 아서는 마치 길을 잃은 사람처럼 그를 응시하며, 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"대체 무슨 말이오? 무슨 뜻이오, 존?\" 그러나 존은, 어떤 부류의 사람들에게는 답을 내놓는 것보다 더 불가능해 보이는 일이 없을 그런 심정에 빠져 있었기에, 앞뒤 가리지 않고 내달렸다.\n\n\"저는,\" 존이 단언했다, \"아니, 저는 그러지 않았고, 모든 게 끝장났을 뿐이라는 것 말고 다른 무언가를 감히 생각해 본 적이 결코 없습니다, 정말입니다. 그런 복을 누릴 수 있으리라는 희망 따위는 저는 품지 않았습니다—아니, 품은 적이 없는데 어찌 품었다고 하겠습니까—그런 말이 오간 뒤에는, 넘을 수 없는 장벽이 세워지지 않았더라도 말입니다! 그렇지만 그렇다고 해서 제가 추억도 가지면 안 되고, 생각도 가지면 안 되고, 거룩한 자리 하나, 그 무엇도 가지면 안 된다는 이유가 됩니까?\"\n\n\"대체 무슨 뜻이오?\" 아서가 외쳤다.\n\n\"그걸 짓밟는 거야 얼마든지 좋습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 미친 듯한 말의 광야를 내달리며 계속했다, \"그런 짓을 저지르기로 마음먹을 수 있는 사람이라면 말입니다. 그걸 짓밟는 거야 얼마든지 좋습니다만, 그건 거기 있습니다. 거기 있지 않다면 짓밟힐 수도 없을지 모르지요. 그러나 그렇다고 그게 신사다운 짓이 되는 것도 아니고, 명예로운 짓이 되는 것도 아니며, 나비처럼 제 껍데기에서 안간힘으로 빠져나온 사람을 도로 제 안으로 내던지는 짓이 정당화되는 것도 아닙니다. 세상은 간수를 비웃을지 모르나, 그래도 그는 사내입니다—여죄수들 사이에서 여자 노릇을 하길 요구받을 때 빼고는 말이지요.\"\n\n그의 횡설수설이 우스꽝스럽기는 했어도, 젊은 존의 소박하고 감상적인 성품에는 진실함이 깃들어 있었고, 어떤 몹시 여린 구석을 다친 듯한 느낌이 그의 달아오른 얼굴과 떨리는 목소리와 몸짓에 드러나 있어, 아서가 그것을 무시했더라면 무정한 사람이 되었으리라. 그는 이 영문 모를 상처의 출발점으로 생각을 되돌렸다. 그러는 동안 젊은 존은 그 푸른 꾸러미를 제법 동그랗게 굴려 놓고는, 그것을 조심스레 세 조각으로 잘라, 마치 무슨 특별한 진미라도 되는 양 접시에 담았다.\n\n\"내 생각에 어쩌면,\" 아서가 물냉이까지 거슬러 올라갔다가 다시 더듬어 내려온 끝에 말했다, \"당신이 도릿 양에 관해 무언가 언급한 듯하오만.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르지요, 선생님.\" 존 치버리가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"나는 도무지 모르겠소. 또 당신의 기분을 상하게 할 생각이라고 여겨지는 불운만은 없기를 바라오—나는 아직껏 당신의 기분을 상하게 할 생각 따윈 한 번도 없었으니 말이오—이렇게 말한다 해서 말이오, 나는 정말 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 젊은 존이 말했다, \"제가 도릿 양을 향해—사랑이라는 주제넘은 말은 쓰지 않겠습니다만, 흠모와 헌신을 품어 왔다는 걸, 그리고 그걸 오래전부터 알고 계셨다는 걸 부인하는 배신을 저지르시렵니까?\"\n\n\"진정이오, 존, 내가 아는 한 나는 어떤 배신도 저지르지 않겠소. 어찌하여 당신이 나를 그런 사람으로 의심하는지 나는 도무지 모르겠소. 당신 어머니 치버리 부인한테서, 내가 한번 그분을 찾아갔다는 얘기를 들은 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님.\" 존이 퉁명스레 대꾸했다. \"그런 얘긴 들은 적 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 찾아갔소. 왜 그랬는지 짐작이 가오?\"\n\n\"아니요, 선생님.\" 존이 퉁명스레 대꾸했다. \"왜 그러셨는지 짐작이 안 갑니다.\"\n\n\"내가 말해 주리다. 나는 도릿 양의 행복을 도모하고 싶었소. 그래서 만일 도릿 양이 당신의 마음에 화답하고 있다고 짐작할 수만 있었더라면—\"\n\n가엾은 존 치버리는 귀 끝까지 새빨개졌다. \"도릿 양은 한 번도 그러지 않았습니다, 선생님. 저는 제 미천한 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 명예롭고 진실하고자 합니다. 그분이 한 번이라도 그랬다거나, 한 번이라도 제게 그렇게 믿게 만드셨다고 꾸며 대는 짓일랑 한순간도 경멸합니다. 아니, 차분히 이성으로 따지자면 그분이 그러시리라거나 그러실 수 있으리라 기대할 만한 구석조차 결코 없었습니다. 그분은 언제나 모든 면에서 저보다 한참 높은 분이셨습니다. 마찬가지로,\" 존이 덧붙였다, \"그분의 점잖은 집안도 그러했고요.\"\n\n그녀에게 속한 모든 것을 향한 그 기사도적인 마음씨가 그를 어찌나 떳떳해 보이게 했던지, 그 자그마한 키와 다소 부실한 다리, 몹시 가는 머리칼, 시인 기질에도 불구하고, 골리앗이 그 자리에 앉아 있었더라도 아서에게서 그만한 존중을 끌어내지는 못했으리라.\n\n\"당신은, 존,\" 그가 진심 어린 감탄을 담아 말했다, \"사내답게 말하는구려.\"\n\n\"그렇다면요, 선생님,\" 존이 손등으로 두 눈을 훔치며 대꾸했다, \"선생님께서도 그렇게 해 주셨으면 합니다.\"\n\n이 뜻밖의 응수가 어찌나 재빨랐던지, 그것은 또 한 번 아서로 하여금 의아한 표정으로 그를 바라보게 만들었다.\n\n\"하기야,\" 존이 찻쟁반 너머로 손을 뻗으며 말했다, \"너무 지나친 말이라면 거두겠습니다! 하지만 왜 안 됩니까, 왜 안 되느냐는 말입니다? 클레넘 씨, 제가 선생님께 다른 누군가를 위해서라도 몸을 돌보시라고 말씀드릴 때, 비록 간수일지언정, 어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하십니까? 어찌하여 제가 선생님이 가장 마음에 들어 하실 줄 아는 방을 마련해 드렸겠습니까? 어찌하여 제가 선생님 짐을 들어 올렸겠습니까? 짐이 무거워서 그러는 게 아닙니다. 그런 이유로 들먹이는 게 아니에요. 천만의 말씀입니다. 어찌하여 제가 오늘 아침부터 그토록 선생님을 정성껏 모셨겠습니까? 선생님 자신의 됨됨이 때문에요? 아닙니다. 그 됨됨이가 매우 훌륭하다는 건 저도 조금도 의심치 않습니다. 그렇지만 그 때문은 아닙니다. 다른 분의 됨됨이가 무게를 지녔고, 제게는 그게 훨씬 더 무겁게 다가왔던 겁니다. 그러니 어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하느냐는 겁니다?\"\n\n\"꾸밈없이 말하리다, 존,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당신은 참으로 좋은 사람이고 나는 당신의 인품을 진실로 존경하고 있소. 그러니 오늘 당신이 내게 베푼 친절한 봉사가 도릿 양에게 그녀의 벗으로 신뢰받은 데서 비롯되었다는 사실을, 내가 실제로 느끼는 것보다 덜 절감하는 것처럼 비쳤다면—나는 그것을 잘못으로 인정하며, 당신의 용서를 구하오.\"\n\n\"오! 어찌하여,\" 존이 다시 경멸을 되살려 되풀이했다, \"어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하느냐는 말입니다!\"\n\n\"단언컨대,\" 아서가 대꾸했다, \"나는 당신 말을 못 알아듣겠소. 나를 보시오. 내가 처해 온 곤경을 헤아려 보시오. 내가 다른 자책들에다 일부러, 당신에게 은혜를 저버렸다거나 배신했다는 자책까지 더 보태려 들 것 같소? 나는 당신 말을 못 알아듣겠소.\"\n\n존의 못 믿겠다는 얼굴이 서서히 누그러져 미심쩍어하는 얼굴로 바뀌었다. 그는 일어나, 방의 다락 창문 쪽으로 물러서더니, 아서에게 그리로 오라 손짓하고는, 생각에 잠긴 채 그를 바라보며 섰다.\n\n\"클레넘 씨, 선생님은 정녕 모르신다는 말씀이십니까?\"\n\n\"무얼 말이오, 존?\"\n\n\"맙소사,\" 젊은 존이 숨을 헐떡이며 담장의 대못을 향해 호소하듯 말했다. \"이분이 '무얼'이라시네.\"\n\n클레넘은 대못을 보고, 또 존을 보고, 또 대못을 보고, 또 존을 보았다.\n\n\"'무얼'이라시는군요! 게다가 더 기막힌 건,\" 젊은 존이 비통한 어리둥절함 속에 그를 훑어보며 외쳤다, \"정말로 진심으로 하시는 말씀 같다는 겁니다! 이 창문이 보이십니까, 선생님?\"\n\n\"물론 이 창문이 보이오.\"\n\n\"이 방이 보이십니까?\"\n\n\"아니, 물론 이 방이 보이오.\"\n\n\"맞은편 저 벽, 그리고 저 아래 마당이 보이십니까? 저것들이 죄다 그 증인이었습니다, 날이면 날마다, 밤이면 밤마다, 주가 가고 주가 가도록, 달이 가고 달이 가도록 말입니다. 도릿 양이 저를 보지 못할 때 제가 여기서 그분을 본 게 몇 번이던지요!\"\n\n\"무엇의 증인이란 말이오?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"도릿 양의 사랑의 증인입니다.\"\n\n\"누구를 향한?\"\n\n\"선생님을요.\" 존이 말했다. 그러고는 손등으로 그의 가슴을 툭 건드리고는, 의자로 물러나 거기 앉았는데, 얼굴이 창백한 채 두 팔걸이를 붙들고 그를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다.\n\n그 가벼운 손길을 얹는 대신 클레넘에게 묵직한 일격을 가했더라도, 그를 이보다 더 뒤흔들어 놓지는 못했으리라. 그는 얼이 빠져 서 있었다. 두 눈은 존을 바라보고, 입술은 벌어진 채 이따금 '나를!'이라는 말을 소리 내지 않으며 빚어내는 듯했고, 두 손은 옆구리로 축 늘어졌으며, 그 온 모습은 잠에서 깨어났으되 자신이 온전히 헤아릴 수 없는 소식에 멍해진 사람의 그것이었다.\n\n\"나를!\" 그가 마침내 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 젊은 존이 신음했다. \"선생님을요!\"\n\n그는 애써 미소를 끌어모으며 대꾸했다. \"당신의 착각이오. 완전히 잘못 짚었소.\"\n\n\"제가 착각이라고요, 선생님!\" 젊은 존이 말했다. \"제가 그 일에 대해 완전히 착각을 했다고요! 아니요, 클레넘 씨, 제게 그런 말씀 마십시오. 다른 일이라면 얼마든지 좋습니다. 저는 제가 통찰력 있는 인간이라고 내세우지도 않고, 제 부족함을 잘 알고 있으니까요. 하지만, 제가, 야만인들이 쏘아 댄 화살 떼보다도 더 제 가슴을 쓰라리게 한 그 일에 대해 착각이라니요! 무덤이 담배 장사며 아버지 어머니 마음과만 맞아떨어질 수 있었다면 이따금 차라리 그러기를 바랐을 만큼, 저를 거의 무덤으로 몰아넣은 그 일에 대해 제가 착각이라니요! 사람들 말마따나 다 큰 계집애처럼 지금 이 순간조차 손수건을 꺼내 들게 만드는 그 일에 대해 제가 착각이라니요. 하기야 어찌하여 다 큰 계집애가 흉이 되는 말이어야 하는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다만, 제대로 된 사내 마음이라면 누구나 크든 작든 그들을 사랑하는 법이니까요. 그런 말씀 마십시오, 그런 말씀 마시라고요!\"\n\n겉으로는 충분히 우스꽝스러웠으나 속으로는 여전히 떳떳했던 젊은 존은, 과시도 감춤도 없이 진정으로 손수건을 꺼내 들었는데, 이는 두 눈을 닦으려고 손수건을 꺼내 드는, 속에 선량함을 가득 지닌 사람한테서만 볼 수 있는 모습이었다. 두 눈을 닦고는, 흐느낌 한 번과 코를 한 번 훌쩍이는 무해한 호사를 누린 뒤, 그는 그것을 도로 집어넣었다.\n\n그 손길은 여전히 일격이나 다름없는 위력으로 남아 있어, 아서는 그 화제를 매듭지을 만한 말을 몇 마디 모으지도 못했다. 존 치버리가 손수건을 도로 주머니에 넣자, 아서는 그에게, 그의 사심 없음과 도릿 양에 대한 한결같은 추억을 더없이 높이 산다고 다짐해 주었다. 방금 떨쳐 낸 그 마음속 인상에 관해서는—여기서 존이 끼어들어 \"인상이 아닙니다! 확신입니다!\"라고 말했다—그 일에 관해서는 다음에 이야기할 수 있을지언정, 지금은 더 말하지 않겠다고 했다. 마음이 가라앉고 지친 그는 존이 허락한다면 제 방으로 돌아가, 그날 밤은 다시 나오지 않겠노라고 했다. 존이 응낙하자, 그는 담장의 그늘을 따라 슬그머니 제 거처로 돌아갔다.\n\n그 일격의 느낌이 여전히 어찌나 강하게 그를 짓누르던지, 그가 방문 밖 계단에 앉아 잠자리를 봐 주려고 기다리던—그러면서 자기는 치버리 씨, '늙은 양반이 아니라 젊은 양반'한테 지시를 받았다고 알려 주던—그 지저분한 노파가 떠나고 나서도, 그는 마치 얻어맞은 사람처럼 두 손으로 머리를 감싸 쥔 채 빛바랜 안락의자에 주저앉았다. 리틀 도릿이 그를 사랑한다니! 그에게는 자신의 비참함보다도 한층 더, 훨씬 더 얼떨떨한 일이었다.\n\n그 있을 법하지 않음을 헤아려 보라. 그는 그녀를 자기 아이라고, 사랑하는 아이라고 부르는 데 익숙했고, 둘 사이의 나이 차를 늘 들먹이며 그녀가 마음을 털어놓도록 청했으며, 자신을 늙어 가는 사람이라 일컫곤 했다. 그렇지만 그녀는 그를 늙었다고 여기지 않았을지도 모른다. 그 장미꽃들이 강물 위로 떠내려가 버리기 전까지는 그 자신도 스스로를 늙었다고 여기지 않았음을, 무언가가 그에게 일깨워 주었다.\n\n그는 상자 속 다른 서류들 틈에 그녀의 편지 두 통을 간직하고 있었는데, 그것을 꺼내어 읽어 보았다. 그 안에는 그녀의 감미로운 목소리 같은 울림이 깃들어 있는 듯했다. 그것은 다정함의 온갖 가락으로 그의 귓가에 내려앉았고, 그 가락은 그 새로운 의미를 받아들이지 못할 것도 아니었다. 바로 그제야, 그날 밤 바로 그 방에서—그녀의 달라진 운명의 여명을 그가 처음 알게 된 그날 밤, 그리고 그가 굴욕 속에 갇힌 몸이 되어 떠올리도록 운명 지어진 다른 말들이 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 그날 밤—그에게 한 그녀의 대답, \"아니요, 아니요, 아니요\"라는 그 조용한 적막감이 그의 마음속으로 와락 밀려들었다.\n\n그 있을 법하지 않음을 헤아려 보라.\n\n그러나 그것은, 헤아려 볼수록 점점 더 희미해지는 쪽으로 기울어 갔다. 그와 더불어 점점 더 강해지는, 제 가슴속의 또 다른 묘한 물음이 있었다. 그녀가 누군가를 사랑한다고 믿기를 그가 꺼렸던 그 마음속에, 그 물음을 잠재우고 싶어 했던 그 마음속에, 그녀가 누군가를 사랑하도록 자신이 돕는 데에 일종의 고결함이 있으리라고 어렴풋이 의식했던 그 마음속에, 떠오르자마자 그가 눌러 잠재운 무언가가 제 쪽에는 없었던가? 그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 따위는 생각해서는 안 된다고, 그녀의 감사를 이용해서는 안 된다고, 자신의 경험을 경고이자 책망으로 기억에 새겨 두어야 한다고, 그런 젊은 날의 희망들은 자기 벗의 죽은 딸이 가 버렸듯 가 버린 것으로 여겨야 한다고, 자기에게는 그 시절이 이미 지나가 버렸으며 자기는 너무 슬픔에 절고 너무 늙었다고 스스로에게 흔들림 없이 되뇌어야 한다고—그가 자신에게 속삭인 적이 있지 않았던가?\n\n그토록 한결같이, 또 그토록 또렷이 잊힌 그날, 그녀를 바닥에서 안아 올리며 그는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다. 그녀가 의식이 있었더라도 그가 했음 직한 바로 그런 입맞춤을? 아무 차이가 없었던가?\n\n어둠이 내릴 무렵에도 그는 이런 생각들에 사로잡혀 있었다. 어둠은 또한 플로니시 부부가 그의 방문을 두드리는 것을 맞이했다. 그들은 그토록 빨리 팔려 나가면서도 그토록 더디게 본전을 돌려주던 그 재고품 가운데서 골라 담은 것들로 가득 찬 바구니를 들고 왔다. 플로니시 부인은 눈물을 글썽였다. 플로니시 씨는 자기 특유의 철학적이되 명료하지는 않은 투로, 세상에는 오르막이 있고 또 내리막이 있는 법이라고 다정하게 투덜거렸다. 어찌하여 오르막이고 어찌하여 내리막이냐고 물어 봐야 소용없으니, 그저 거기 그렇게 있는 거지요. 세상이 빙글빙글 돈다는데, 의심할 바 없이 돌고 도는 거니까요, 그러다 보면 아무리 훌륭한 신사라도 머리를 거꾸로 처박고 머리털이 죄다 엉뚱한 쪽으로, 말하자면 허공 속으로 휘날리는 차례를 한 번은 맞아야 한다는 걸, 그는 진실로 들어 알고 있다는 거였다. 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면. 플로니시 씨가 한 말은, 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면, 이었다. 그 신사의 머리는 차례가 오면 다시 위로 솟을 것이고, 그 신사의 머리털은 다시 죄다 매끈하게 가지런해져 바라보기에 흐뭇할 것이니, 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면!\n\n이미 말한 바와 같이, 플로니시 부인은 철학적이지 못한지라 울었다. 게다가 플로니시 부인은 철학적이지 못한지라 알아듣게 말했다. 그것은 누그러진 그녀의 심정에서 비롯되었는지도, 여자 특유의 재치에서 비롯되었는지도, 여자다운 빠른 연상에서 비롯되었는지도, 아니면 여자다운 무연상에서 비롯되었는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 어찌어찌하여 플로니시 부인의 알아들을 만한 그 말이 하필 아서가 골똘히 생각하던 바로 그 화제 위에서 펼쳐졌다.\n\n\"아버지가 클레넘 씨 얘기를 어찌나 하시는지요,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다, \"도무지 못 믿으실 거예요. 그 일로 영 풀이 죽으셨답니다. 목소리로 말하자면, 이 불운이 그걸 앗아가 버렸어요. 아버지가 얼마나 노래를 곱게 부르는 분인지 아시잖아요. 그런데 차 마실 때 아이들한테 한 가락도 못 뽑아내셨다니까요, 제 말 믿으세요.\"\n\n말하는 동안 플로니시 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 두 눈을 훔치며, 방 안을 회상에 잠겨 둘러보았다.\n\n\"밥티스트 씨로 말하자면요,\" 플로니시 부인이 이어 말했다, \"이 일을 알게 되면 어쩔는지, 저는 짐작도 못 하겠고 상상도 못 하겠어요. 진작 여기 와 있었을 게 분명한데, 선생님 일로 은밀한 볼일을 보느라 나가 있거든요. 그 일을 어찌나 끈질기게 좇으며 도무지 쉬지를 않는지—정말이지,\" 플로니시 부인이 이탈리아어 투로 마무리하며 말했다, \"제가 그한테 늘 말하지요, 무샤토니샤 파드로나라고요.\"\n\n거만한 사람은 아니었지만, 플로니시 부인은 이 토스카나 문장을 유난히 우아하게 굴려 냈다고 느꼈다. 플로니시 씨는 언어학자로서의 아내의 재주에 대한 자랑스러움을 감추지 못했다.\n\n\"그렇지만 제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은요, 클레넘 씨,\" 그 선량한 여인이 계속했다, \"언제나 감사할 일이 한 가지는 있는 법이고, 그건 선생님도 분명 인정하시리라는 거예요. 이 방에서 말하자면, 지금 그 한 가지가 무엇인지 헤아리기란 어렵지 않지요. 도릿 양이 여기 없어서 이 일을 모른다는 것, 그게 정말이지 감사할 일이랍니다.\"\n\n아서는 그녀가 유난스러운 표정으로 자기를 바라보는 것 같았다.\n\n\"정말이지,\" 플로니시 부인이 되풀이했다, \"도릿 양이 멀리 떨어져 있다는 게 감사할 일이에요. 부디 그분이 이 일을 들을 일이 없기를 바라요. 만일 그분이 여기서 이걸 보았더라면요, 선생님, 선생님이,\" 플로니시 부인이 그 말을 되풀이했다—\"의심할 바 없이, 선생님이—불운과 곤경에 빠지신 모습을 보는 게 그분의 정 많은 가슴엔 거의 견디기 힘든 일이었으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없지요. 그것만큼 도릿 양을 모질게 아프게 했을 일은 달리 생각나는 게 없어요.\"\n\n확실히 플로니시 부인은 이제 정겨운 감정 속에 일종의 떨리는 도전기를 담아 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"맞아요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"게다가 아버지가, 그 연세에도, 얼마나 살피시는지 이걸 보면 알 수 있어요, 오늘 오후에 제게 이러시지 뭐예요—이 '행복한 오두막'이 증인인데 제가 지어내는 것도 아니고 어떤 식으로 부풀리는 것도 아니에요—'메리야, 도릿 양이 이 자리에서 이걸 안 보는 게 크게 기뻐할 일이로구나.' 그게 아버지 말씀이었어요. 아버지가 직접 하신 말씀이 '크게 기뻐할 일이야, 메리야, 도릿 양이 이 자리에서 이걸 안 보는 게' 였다니까요. 그래서 제가 아버지께 그랬죠, 이랬어요, '아버지, 옳으신 말씀이에요!' 그게요,\" 플로니시 부인이 아주 꼼꼼한 법정 증인 같은 태도로 끝맺었다, \"아버지와 저 사이에 오간 얘기랍니다. 그리고 저는 저와 아버지 사이에 실제로 오간 것 말고는 아무것도 말씀드리지 않은 거예요.\"\n\n플로니시 씨는 좀 더 과묵한 기질이었던지라, 이 틈을 타 이제 클레넘 씨를 혼자 있게 해 드리는 게 어떻겠느냐고 끼어들었다. \"이봐, 자네도 알다시피,\" 플로니시 씨가 근엄하게 말했다, \"내가 어떤 건지 다 알거든, 마누라.\" 그러면서 그는 마치 그 말이 무슨 위대한 도덕적 비밀이라도 담고 있다는 듯, 그 값진 한마디를 여러 번 되풀이했다. 마침내 그 갸륵한 부부는 팔짱을 끼고 떠나갔다.\n\n리틀 도릿, 리틀 도릿. 또다시, 몇 시간 동안. 언제나 리틀 도릿!\n\n다행히도, 설령 한때 그러했다 한들, 이제는 다 지나간 일이었고, 지나간 게 차라리 나았다. 그녀가 그를 사랑했고 그도 그것을 알아 스스로 그녀를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었다 치자, 그 길은 그녀를 어디로 이끄는 길이었겠는가—그녀를 이 비참한 곳으로 되돌려 데려올 길이 아니었겠는가! 그녀가 이곳을 영영 벗어났다는 것, 그녀가 이미 결혼했거나 곧 결혼하리라는 것(여동생의 결혼 소식과 함께, 그 방면을 향한 그녀 아버지의 계획에 관한 어렴풋한 소문이 '피 흘리는 심장 마당'까지 닿아 있었다), 그리고 지나가 버린 시절의 그 모든 어지러운 가능성 위로 마셜시의 문이 영영 닫혀 버렸다는 것을 떠올리며, 그는 크게 위안을 얻어야 마땅했다.\n\n사랑스러운 리틀 도릿.\n\n그 자신의 가련한 이야기를 돌이켜 보면, 그녀는 그 이야기의 소실점이었다. 그 원근법 속의 모든 것이 그녀의 순결한 모습으로 이어졌다. 그는 그것을 향해 수천 마일을 여행해 왔고, 이전의 불안한 희망과 의혹들도 그 앞에서 스스로 풀려 나갔으며, 그것은 그의 인생에서 관심의 중심이었고, 그의 인생 안에 있던 선하고 즐거운 모든 것의 종착점이었다. 그 너머에는 그저 황량함과 어두워진 하늘 말고는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n그 음울한 벽 안에 처음 누워 잠을 청하던 밤만큼이나 마음이 편치 않은 채, 그는 그런 생각들로 밤을 지새웠다. 한편 젊은 존은 제 베개 위에 다음과 같은 묘비명을 지어 가다듬은 뒤, 평온한 잠에 폭 싸여 누워 있었다—\n\n나그네여! 존 치버리 2세의 무덤에 경의를 표하라. 그는 굳이 밝힐 필요 없는 노령에 이르러 세상을 떠났노라. 그는 곤경에 처한 자신의 연적과 마주쳤고, 그와 한판 붙고 싶은 마음이 일었으나, 사랑하는 이를 위하여, 그 쓰라린 감정을 정복하고, 너그러운 사람이 되었노라.\n\n# CHAPTER 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea\n\n감옥 문밖 사회의 여론은 시간이 흐를수록 클레넘에게 가혹해져 갔고, 그는 감옥 안의 무리 가운데서도 친구를 사귀지 못했다. 마당에 모여 근심을 잊으려는 무리와 어울리기에는 너무 의기소침했고, 술집의 초라한 사교에 끼기에는 너무 내성적이고 너무 불행했던 그는 제 방에 틀어박혀 지냈으며, 그 때문에 불신을 샀다. 어떤 이들은 그가 거만하다 했고, 어떤 이들은 음울하고 속을 드러내지 않는다고 트집을 잡았으며, 어떤 이들은 빚에 짓눌려 야위어 가는 기개 없는 개라며 그를 경멸했다. 감옥의 주민 전부가 이런저런 죄목을 들어 그를 멀리했는데, 특히 마지막 죄목—일종의 가정 내 반역에 해당하는—이 컸다. 그리하여 그는 곧 칩거에 더욱 굳어져, 그가 이리저리 거닐 수 있는 유일한 시간이라곤 저녁의 클럽이 노래와 건배와 감상에 젖어 모여 있을 때, 그리하여 마당이 거의 여자들과 아이들에게만 남겨졌을 때뿐이었다.\n\n투옥은 그에게 영향을 미치기 시작했다. 그는 자신이 빈둥거리고 풀이 죽어 있음을 알았다. 자신이 차지하고 있는 바로 그 작은 방의 네 벽 안에서 투옥이 어떤 영향을 끼치는지 익히 보아 온 터라, 이 자각은 그로 하여금 스스로를 두려워하게 만들었다. 다른 사람들의 시선에서 움츠러들고, 또 제 자신의 시선에서도 움츠러들면서 그는 매우 뚜렷하게 변해 가기 시작했다. 담장의 그늘이 그의 위에 어둡게 드리워 있음을 누구라도 알아볼 수 있었다.\n\n투옥된 지 한 열두 주쯤 되었을 무렵의 어느 날, 책을 읽으려 애쓰면서도 그 책 속의 상상 속 인물들조차 마셜시에서 풀려나게 하지 못하고 있을 때, 발소리 하나가 그의 문 앞에 멈추더니 손 하나가 문을 두드렸다. 그가 일어나 문을 열자, 듣기 좋은 목소리가 그에게 인사를 건넸다. \"안녕하십니까, 클레넘 씨? 제가 찾아뵙는 것이 달갑잖은 일은 아니길 바랍니다.\"\n\n쾌활한 젊은 배너클, 퍼디낸드였다. 그는 누추한 감옥과 대조되어 압도적으로 명랑하고 거리낌이 없었지만, 매우 선량하고 호감 가는 인상이었다.\n\n\"제가 찾아오니 놀라셨겠군요, 클레넘 씨.\" 그는 클레넘이 권한 자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"무척 놀랐다고 고백해야겠습니다.\"\n\n\"불쾌하실 정도는 아니길 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"천만에요.\"\n\n\"감사합니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면,\" 호감 가는 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"여기서 잠시 은거하실 처지가 되셨다는 소식을 듣고 무척이나 안타까웠습니다. 그리고 (물론 두 사사로운 신사 사이의 일로) 저희 관청이 그 일과 아무 상관이 없기를 바랍니다만?\"\n\n\"댁의 관청 말입니까?\"\n\n\"저희 에두름국 말입니다.\"\n\n\"제 불운의 어느 한 부분이라도 그 주목할 만한 기관 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없습니다.\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 활기찬 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"그 말씀을 들으니 진심으로 기쁩니다. 그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 마음이 놓입니다. 저희 관청이 선생의 곤경과 무슨 관련이라도 있었다면 저는 더없이 후회했을 것입니다.\"\n\n클레넘은 그 책임에서 관청을 면제해 준다고 다시 한번 확언했다.\n\n\"잘됐습니다.\" 퍼디낸드가 말했다. \"그 말씀을 들으니 정말 기쁩니다. 사실 제 마음 한구석에선 저희가 선생을 쓰러뜨리는 데 일조하지 않았을까 다소 걱정했었지요. 저희가 이따금 그런 류의 일을 하고 마는 것이 저희의 불운임은 분명하니까요. 일부러 그러려는 건 아닙니다만, 사람들이 곤경에 빠지기를 자처한다면야—어쩌겠습니까.\"\n\n\"당신 말씀에 전적으로 동의하는 것은 아니나,\" 아서가 침울하게 응수했다, \"저를 염려해 주시니 무척 고맙습니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 정말입니다! 저희 관청은,\" 느긋한 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"있을 수 있는 한 가장 무해한 곳입니다. 저희가 사기꾼이라고 하시겠지요. 아니라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 하지만 그런 류의 일은 다 그렇게 의도된 것이고, 또 그래야만 하는 것입니다. 아시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"올바른 관점에서 보시질 않는군요. 관점이야말로 핵심입니다. 저희 관청을, 그저 저희를 가만 내버려 두기만 바란다는 관점에서 보십시오. 그러면 저희는 어디서도 찾기 힘들 만큼 훌륭한 부처입니다.\"\n\n\"댁의 관청이 가만 내버려 두라고 있는 곳입니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다.\n\n\"정확히 짚으셨습니다.\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다. \"모든 일이 가만 내버려지도록 하겠다는 명확한 의도로 거기 있는 것이지요. 그게 그 뜻입니다. 그게 그 용도입니다. 물론 다른 무언가를 위한 것인 양 일정한 격식을 갖추기는 해야 하지만, 그건 그저 격식일 뿐입니다. 아니, 맙소사, 저희는 격식 그 자체일 뿐이지요! 선생께서 저희의 격식을 얼마나 많이 거치셨는지 생각해 보십시오. 그러고도 끝에는 조금도 더 가까이 다가가지 못하셨지요?\"\n\n\"한 번도요.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"올바른 관점에서 보시면, 거기 저희가 있습니다—공식적이고 효과적으로요. 그건 일종의 제한된 크리켓 경기 같은 겁니다. 한 무리의 외부인들이 늘 들어와 공무에다 공을 던지려 들고, 저희는 그 공을 막아 냅니다.\"\n\n클레넘은 그 투수들은 어떻게 되느냐고 물었다. 가벼운 젊은 배너클은, 그들이 지쳐서 녹초가 되고, 절름발이가 되고, 허리가 부러지고, 하나둘 죽어 나가고, 포기하고, 다른 경기로 옮겨 간다고 대답했다.\n\n\"바로 이 점이 다시금 제게 자축할 거리를 줍니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"저희 관청이 선생의 일시적인 은거와 아무 관련이 없다는 사정 말입니다. 충분히 한몫했을 수도 있었지요. 저희를 가만 내버려 두지 않는 사람들에게 미치는 영향에서, 저희가 때때로 더없이 불운한 곳이라는 것은 부정할 수 없으니까요. 클레넘 씨, 저는 선생께 아주 거리낌이 없습니다. 선생과 저 사이라면 그래도 된다는 걸 알지요. 처음 선생께서 저희를 가만 내버려 두지 않는 실수를 저지르시는 걸 보았을 때부터 저는 그랬습니다. 선생께서 경험이 없고 낙관적이며, 그리고—이런 말 해도 언짢지 않으시길 바랍니다만—다소 순진하신 데가 있다고 느꼈으니까요?\"\n\n\"전혀요.\"\n\n\"다소 순진하신 데가요. 그래서 저는 참 안타깝다고 느꼈고, 일부러 시간을 내어 선생께 넌지시 일러 드린 것입니다(사실 그건 공무가 아니었지요. 저는 가능한 한 공무적으로 굴지 않으니까요). 제가 선생이라면 굳이 골머리를 앓지 않겠노라는 취지로 말입니다. 그런데도 선생께선 골머리를 앓으셨고, 그 후로도 계속 골머리를 앓으셨지요. 자, 이제 더는 그러지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"그럴 기회조차 없을 것 같습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 있고말고요! 여기서 나가시게 될 겁니다. 누구나 여기서 나갑니다. 여기서 나가는 길은 끝도 없지요. 자, 다시 저희에게로 돌아오지 마십시오. 그 간청이 제가 찾아온 두 번째 목적입니다. 부디 저희에게 돌아오지 마십시오. 명예를 걸고,\" 퍼디낸드가 매우 다정하고 흉허물 없는 투로 말했다, \"지난 일을 교훈 삼아 저희를 멀리하지 않으신다면, 저는 몹시 속이 상할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 발명은요?\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"이보십시오,\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다, \"이런 식의 허물없는 호칭을 용서해 주신다면, 그 발명에 대해 알고자 하는 사람은 아무도 없고, 그 따위에 한 푼 반어치라도 관심 두는 사람도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"즉, 관청 안에는 아무도 없다는 말씀이지요?\"\n\n\"바깥에도 없습니다. 누구나 어떤 발명이든 싫어하고 비웃을 채비가 되어 있지요. 가만 내버려지기를 바라는 사람이 얼마나 많은지 선생께선 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 이 나라의 천재성이라는 것이(이 말의 의회적인 성격은 눈감아 주시고, 지루해하지는 마십시오) 얼마나 가만 내버려지는 쪽으로 기우는지 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 제 말을 믿으십시오, 클레넘 씨,\" 쾌활한 젊은 배너클이 더없이 유쾌한 태도로 말했다, \"저희 관청은 전속력으로 돌진해 칠 사악한 거인이 아닙니다. 그저 어마어마한 양의 겨를 갈아 내며 이 나라의 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지를 보여 주는 풍차일 뿐이지요.\"\n\n\"그 말을 믿을 수 있다면,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"그것은 우리 모두에게 암담한 전망이겠습니다.\"\n\n\"오! 그런 말씀 마십시오!\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다. \"다 괜찮습니다. 우리에겐 사기가 있어야 하고, 우리 모두 사기를 좋아하며, 사기 없이는 살아갈 수 없습니다. 약간의 사기와, 한 줄기 정해진 홈, 그리고 가만 내버려 두기만 하면, 만사가 훌륭하게 굴러가지요.\"\n\n여자의 몸에서 태어나, 그들이 철저히 부인하고 믿지 않는 갖가지 구호 아래 뒤를 이을 신진 배너클 일가의 우두머리로서, 이런 희망찬 신앙고백을 마치고 퍼디낸드는 일어섰다. 그의 솔직하고 정중한 태도보다, 또 신사다운 본능으로 이 방문의 정황에 그토록 잘 맞춰진 태도보다 더 유쾌한 것은 없었다.\n\n\"여쭤 봐도 될는지요,\" 클레넘이 그의 솔직함과 유쾌함에 진심 어린 고마움을 느끼며 손을 내밀자 그가 말했다, \"고인이 되신 우리의 애석한 머들 씨가 이 지나가는 불편의 원인이라는 것이 사실인지요?\"\n\n\"제가 그에게 파멸당한 수많은 사람 가운데 하나입니다. 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"대단히 영리한 작자였음에 틀림없군요.\" 퍼디낸드 배너클이 말했다.\n\n고인의 기억을 칭송할 기분이 아니었던 아서는 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"물론 더할 나위 없는 악당이었지요.\" 퍼디낸드가 말했다, \"그러나 놀랍도록 영리했어요! 그 작자를 감탄하지 않을 수가 없습니다. 사기의 대가였음에 틀림없어요. 사람들을 어찌나 잘 알았던지—어찌나 완벽하게 구워삶았던지—그들을 가지고 얼마나 많은 일을 해냈던지!\"\n\n그 느긋한 투에서, 그는 정말로 진심 어린 감탄에 사로잡혀 있었다.\n\n\"바라건대,\" 아서가 말했다, \"그와 그에게 속은 자들이, 사람들이 다시는 그렇게 농락당하지 않도록 하는 경계가 되었으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"이보십시오, 클레넘 씨,\" 퍼디낸드가 웃으며 응수했다, \"정말 그런 풋내 나는 희망을 품고 계십니까? 사기에 그만한 역량과 그만한 진정한 취향을 지닌 다음 사람도 똑같이 성공할 것입니다. 죄송합니다만, 인간이라는 벌 떼가 낡은 양철 주전자 두드리는 소리에 얼마나 몰려드는지 선생께선 정말 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 바로 그 사실 속에 그들을 다스리는 완벽한 지침서가 들어 있지요. 그 주전자가 귀금속으로 만들어졌다고 그들이 믿게 될 때, 바로 그 사실 속에 우리의 애석한 고인 같은 자들의 온갖 권능이 들어 있는 겁니다. 물론 여기저기,\" 퍼디낸드가 정중하게 말했다, \"예외적인 경우도 있지요. 사람들이 보기에 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 이유로 속아 넘어간 경우 말입니다. 그런 경우를 찾자고 멀리 갈 것도 없습니다만, 그렇다고 그 법칙이 무효가 되지는 않습니다. 그럼 안녕히 계십시오! 다음번에 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 때엔 이 지나가는 먹구름이 햇살에 자리를 내주었기를 바랍니다. 문 밖으로는 한 발짝도 나오지 마십시오. 나가는 길은 제가 완벽하게 압니다. 안녕히 계십시오!\"\n\n이 말과 함께, 배너클 일가 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 빛나는 그는 아래층으로 내려가, 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 수위실을 지나, 앞마당에서 말에 올라타고는, 자신의 고귀한 친척과의 약속을 지키러 말을 몰아 떠났다. 그 친척은, 노브들의 치국 능력을 따져 물으려는 어떤 불경한 속물들에게 의기양양하게 답할 수 있으려면, 약간의 사전 지도가 필요했던 것이다.\n\n그는 나가는 길에 러그 씨와 마주쳤음이 분명했다. 일이 분쯤 지나자, 그 불그스름한 머리의 신사가 늙은 포이보스처럼 문간에서 환히 빛났으니 말이다.\n\n\"오늘은 어떠십니까, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 제가 해 드릴 자잘한 일이라도 있을는지요, 선생?\"\n\n\"아니요, 고맙습니다.\"\n\n곤란해진 사건을 두고 러그 씨가 누리는 즐거움이란, 절이고 저장하는 일에서 가정부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 산더미 같은 빨랫감에서 세탁부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 넘쳐 나는 쓰레기통에서 청소부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 그 어떤 직업이든 일거리로서의 난장판에서 누리는 직업적 즐거움과도 같은 것이었다.\n\n\"저는 여전히 이따금씩 둘러봅니다, 선생.\" 러그 씨가 쾌활하게 말했다, \"혹시 남은 채권자들이 문간에 쌓이고 있지나 않은지 보려고요. 꽤나 두껍게 몰려들었습니다, 선생. 저희가 예상했던 만큼이나 두껍게요.\"\n\n그는 이 사정을 마치 축하할 일인 양 언급하며, 두 손을 활기차게 비비고 고개를 약간 갸웃거렸다.\n\n\"예상했던 만큼이나 두껍게요.\" 러그 씨가 되풀이했다. \"마치 소나기처럼 쏟아져 내렸지요. 둘러볼 때 요즘은 자주 폐를 끼치지 않습니다. 선생께서 사람을 반기지 않으시는 걸 알고 있고, 또 저를 보고 싶으시면 수위실에 말씀을 남기시리란 걸 아니까요. 하지만 저는 거의 매일 여기 와 있습니다, 선생. 한 가지 소견을 말씀드리기에 지금이 때 아닌 때는 아닐는지요, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 구슬리듯 물었다.\n\n\"여느 때만큼이나 적당한 때입니다.\"\n\n\"흠! 여론이, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다, \"선생을 두고 분주합니다.\"\n\n\"의심치 않습니다.\"\n\n\"이제, 마침내, 모든 걸 따져 본 끝에, 여론에 자그마한 양보를 하시는 게 좋지 않겠습니까, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 한층 더 구슬리듯 말했다. \"우리 모두 이런저런 식으로 그렇게 하지요. 사실, 우리는 그래야만 합니다.\"\n\n\"저는 여론과 화해할 수가 없습니다, 러그 씨. 그리고 언젠가 그러리라 기대할 자격도 없지요.\"\n\n\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 선생, 그런 말씀 마세요. 상급 감옥으로 옮기는 비용은 거의 보잘것없고, 선생께서 거기 계셔야 한다는 전반적인 정서가 강하다면야, 그야—정말이지—\"\n\n\"러그 씨, 제가 여기 남겠다고 결심한 것은 취향의 문제라고 결론지으신 줄 알았는데요.\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생, 글쎄요! 하지만 그게 좋은 취향입니까, 좋은 취향이냐고요? 그게 문제입니다.\" 러그 씨는 어찌나 달래듯 설득하던지 거의 측은해 보일 지경이었다. \"거의 좋은 정서냐고 말할 뻔했습니다. 이것은 선생의 큰 사건입니다. 그런데 일 이 파운드 때문에 사람이 들어올 수 있는 이런 곳에 선생께서 남아 계신 것을, 격에 맞지 않는다고들 합니다. 격에 맞지 않지요. 선생, 제가 얼마나 여러 곳에서 그 말이 오가는 걸 들었는지 이루 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다. 어젯밤에도, 제가 가끔 들여다보지 않는다면 최고의 법조계 인사들이라 부를 만한 이들이 드나드는 어느 응접실에서 그에 관한 논평을 들었지요—거기서, 듣기 안타까운 논평을 들었습니다. 선생을 생각하니 마음이 아프더군요. 또, 바로 오늘 아침 식사 자리에서도요. 제 딸이(선생께선 한낱 여자일 뿐이라 하시겠지요. 하지만 이런 일에 대한 감각이 있고, 게다가 러그 대 보킨스 소송의 원고로서 약간의 개인적 경험까지 있는 아이입니다) 큰 놀라움을 표하더군요. 큰 놀라움을요. 자, 이런 사정에서, 또 우리 가운데 누구도 여론 위에 완전히 군림할 수는 없음을 생각하면, 그 여론에 자그마한 양보를 하시는 게—자, 선생,\" 러그가 말했다, \"논의의 가장 낮은 근거에 놓고 말씀드리지요. 호감 가는 일이 아니겠습니까?\"\n\n아서의 생각은 또다시 리틀 도릿에게로 흘러가 버렸고, 그 물음은 답을 얻지 못한 채 남았다.\n\n\"저로 말씀드리면, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 자신의 웅변이 그를 망설임의 상태로 몰아넣었기를 바라며 말했다, \"의뢰인의 의향이 저울 위에 놓여 있을 때는 제 자신을 고려하지 않는 것이 제 신조입니다. 하지만 선생의 사려 깊은 성품과 남을 기쁘게 하려는 전반적인 마음씨를 알기에, 다시 말씀드리거니와 저는 선생께서 상급 감옥에 계시는 편을 선호합니다. 선생의 사건은 떠들썩한 화제가 되었지요. 직업적으로 관여하기에 명예로운 사건입니다. 선생께서 상급 감옥으로 가신다면, 저는 제 인맥 사이에서 더 나은 입지를 얻을 것입니다. 그러나 그것에 휘둘리지는 마십시오, 선생. 저는 그저 사실을 말씀드릴 뿐입니다.\"\n\n고독과 낙담 속에서 죄수의 주의는 이미 어찌나 산만해졌던지, 또 늘 찌푸린 벽 안에서 단 하나의 말 없는 형상하고만 마음을 나누는 데 어찌나 익숙해졌던지, 클레넘은 일종의 멍한 상태를 떨쳐 낸 뒤에야 러그 씨를 바라보고, 그가 한 말의 실마리를 되살리며, 황급히 말할 수 있었다. \"제 결심은 변함이 없고, 변할 수도 없습니다. 부디, 그냥 두십시오. 그냥 두세요!\" 러그 씨는, 자신이 약이 오르고 분했음을 감추지도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"오! 그야 의심할 여지가 없지요, 선생. 그 점을 선생께 들이댄 것은, 제가 기록을 벗어난 일임을 압니다. 하지만 정말이지, 여러 모임에서, 그것도 매우 훌륭한 모임에서, 마셜시에 남는 것은 외국인에게야 어울릴지언정, 이 섬나라 고향의 영광스러운 자유가 상급 감옥으로의 이감을 허락하는 마당에 영국인의 기개에는 어울리지 않는다는 말을 들었을 때, 저는 제게 그어진 좁은 직업적 선을 벗어나서라도 이를 언급해야겠다고 생각한 것입니다. 개인적으로는,\" 러그 씨가 말했다, \"이 화제에 대해 아무 의견이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"그거 다행이군요.\" 아서가 응수했다.\n\n\"오! 전혀 없습니다, 선생!\" 러그 씨가 말했다. \"만약 있었다면, 저는 조금 전 제 의뢰인이 안장 말을 탄 명문가의 신사로부터 이런 곳에서 방문을 받는 것을 보기를 꺼렸을 것입니다. 하지만 그건 제 일이 아니었지요. 만약 있었다면, 저는 지금 수위실에서 기다리는 또 다른 신사, 군인 같은 풍채의 신사에게, 제 의뢰인은 결코 여기 남을 의도가 없었으며 더 나은 거처로 이감되기 직전이라고 말할 권한이 부여되기를 바랐을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 직업적 기계로서 제가 갈 길은 명확하니, 저는 그 일과 아무 상관이 없습니다. 그 신사를 만나 보시겠습니까, 선생?\"\n\n\"누가 저를 만나려고 기다린다고 하셨지요?\"\n\n\"그 직업적이지 못한 결례를 제가 저지르긴 했습니다, 선생. 제가 선생의 직업적 자문가임을 듣고서, 그 신사는 제 매우 제한된 역할이 수행되기 전에 끼어들기를 사양하더군요. 다행히도,\" 러그 씨가 빈정대며 말했다, \"저는 그 신사에게 이름을 묻는 데까지 기록을 벗어나지는 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"만나는 것밖에 달리 도리가 없겠지요.\" 클레넘이 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 만나시겠다는 거지요, 선생?\" 러그가 되받았다. \"제가 나가는 길에 그 신사에게 그렇게 전하라는 지시를 받는 영광을 누리는 것입니까? 그렇습니까? 감사합니다, 선생. 이만 물러가겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그는 부아가 난 채로 물러갔다.\n\n군인 같은 풍채의 그 신사는, 지금의 마음 상태에서 클레넘의 호기심을 너무도 어렴풋이만 일깨웠던 터라, 그런 방문객이 언급되었다는 사실에 대한 절반쯤의 망각이, 이제 거의 늘 그의 마음을 흐리게 하던 그 음울한 장막의 일부로서 이미 그 위로 슬며시 기어들고 있었는데, 그때 계단을 밟는 무거운 발소리가 그를 일깨웠다. 그 발소리는 계단을 오르는 듯했는데, 그리 신속하지도 자발적이지도 않으면서도, 모욕하려는 의도가 담긴 성큼성큼한 보폭과 덜그럭거림을 과시하고 있었다. 그것이 그의 문밖 층계참에서 잠시 멈추었을 때, 그는 그 소리의 특이함과 자신의 연상을 떠올리지 못했으나, 무언가 연상되는 게 있다고는 생각했다. 그에게 곱씹을 시간은 한순간밖에 주어지지 않았다. 그의 문은 곧장 쿵 하고 활짝 열렸고, 그 문간에는 수많은 근심의 원인인, 행방불명이었던 블랑두아가 서 있었다.\n\n\"안녕하신가, 동료 감방새여!\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 원했다지. 자, 여기 왔네!\"\n\n아서가 분노 어린 경악 속에 그에게 말을 건네기도 전에, 카발레토가 그를 뒤따라 방으로 들어왔다. 팽크스 씨가 카발레토의 뒤를 따랐다. 두 사람 모두 현재의 거주자가 이 방을 차지한 이래로 이곳에 온 적이 없었다. 팽크스 씨는 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 창가로 비스듬히 다가가 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고는, 두 손으로 머리카락을 헝클어뜨린 다음 팔짱을 꼈으니, 고된 하루 일과 중 한숨 돌리러 멈춘 사람 같았다. 밥티스트 씨는 옛날의 두려운 동무에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 문에 등을 대고 양손으로 제 발목을 하나씩 쥐고 바닥에 가만히 주저앉았으니, 마르세유의 어느 무더운 아침에 또 다른 감옥의 더 깊은 그늘 속에서 바로 그 사람 앞에 앉아 있던 그 자세를(이제는 깜박이지 않는 경계심을 드러낸다는 점만 빼면) 그대로 되풀이한 것이었다.\n\n\"이 두 미치광이의 증언에 따르면,\" 블랑두아 씨가, 다른 이름으로는 라니에, 또 다른 이름으로는 리고가 말했다, \"자네가 나를 원한다더군, 동료 새여. 자, 여기 왔네!\" 낮에는 접어 올려져 있는 침대 틀을 경멸하듯 둘러본 그는, 모자를 머리에서 벗지도 않은 채 거기에 등을 기대고 쉬더니, 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 도전적으로 어슬렁거리며 섰다.\n\n\"이 흉조 같은 악당아!\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신은 일부러 우리 어머니 댁에 끔찍한 의혹을 씌웠소. 왜 그런 짓을 했소? 무엇이 당신을 그 악마 같은 꾸밈수로 부추긴 거요?\"\n\n리고 씨는 잠시 그를 노려보다가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"이 고귀하신 신사 좀 들어 보게! 온 세상이여, 이 미덕의 화신 좀 들어 보게! 하지만 조심하게, 조심해. 이보게, 친구, 자네의 열정이 좀 자네 입장을 곤란하게 만들지도 모르거든. 맙소사! 그럴 수도 있다네.\"\n\n\"시뇨레!\" 카발레토가 끼어들며, 그 역시 아서에게 말했다. \"우선, 제 말을 들어 보십시오! 제가 그를, 리고를 찾으라는 지시를 받았지요. 그렇지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"사실이오.\"\n\n\"그래서 저는, 결과적으롭니다,\"—만일 그가 이따금 이런 식으로 부사를 늘여 빼는 것이 그의 영어에서 으뜸가는 흠이라고 플로니시 부인을 설득할 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 무척 마음을 놓았을 것이다,—\"먼저 제 동포들 사이로 갔습니다. 런던에 도착한 외국인들 소식이 뭐가 있는지 그들에게 물었지요. 그다음 프랑스 사람들 사이로 갔습니다. 그다음 독일 사람들 사이로 갔지요. 다들 제게 말해 줍니다. 우리 대부분은 서로를 잘 알거든요. 그래서 다들 제게 말해 줍니다. 하지만!—그 누구도 그에 대해, 리고에 대해 아무것도 말해 주지 못했습니다. 열다섯 번이나,\" 카발레토는 손가락을 모두 펼친 왼손을 세 차례나 내뻗었는데, 어찌나 빨리 했던지 눈으로는 그 동작을 좇기 힘들 정도였다, \"외국인들이 가는 곳마다 그에 대해 물었습니다. 그런데 열다섯 번이나,\" 같은 날쌘 동작을 되풀이하며, \"그들은 아무것도 몰랐지요. 하지만!—\"\n\n이 의미심장한 이탈리아식 '하지만'에서의 쉼과 함께, 그의 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 방향으로 까딱이는 동작이 등장했으니, 아주 조금, 아주 조심스럽게였다.\n\n\"하지만!—오랜 시간이 지나도 그가 여기 런던에 있다는 걸 찾아내지 못하고 있을 때, 누군가 제게 흰 머리의 군인 하나 이야기를 해 주더군요—그렇지?—이렇게 그가 기르고 있는 그런 머리가 아니라—흰색—어느 일정한 곳에서 은밀하게 은거하며 사는 사람이라고요. 하지만!—\" 그 단어에서 또 한 번 쉼을 두며, \"이따금 식사 후에 산책을 하고 담배를 피운다고요. 이탈리아에서들 하는 말처럼(그리고 그 가엾은 사람들이 아는 대로), 인내심을 가질 필요가 있지요. 저는 인내심이 있습니다. 저는 그 일정한 곳이 어디냐고 물었지요. 어떤 이는 여기라 하고, 어떤 이는 저기라 합니다. 그래! 여기도 아니고, 저기도 아니었지요. 저는 더없이 끈기 있게 기다렸습니다. 마침내 그곳을 찾아냈지요. 그러고는 지켜보았고, 그가 산책하며 담배 피울 때까지 숨어 있었습니다. 그는 잿빛 머리의 군인이었지요—하지만!—\" 정말로 매우 단호한 쉼과 함께, 손등 방향의 집게손가락을 좌우로 아주 힘차게 놀리며—\"그가 또한 여러분이 보시는 이 사람입니다.\"\n\n자신에게 우월함을 내세우느라 애써 온 자에게 복종하던 옛 습관 때문에, 그가 이렇게 리고를 가리킨 뒤에조차, 어수선하게 고개를 한 번 그에게 숙였다는 점이 눈에 띄었다.\n\n\"그래, 시뇨레!\" 그가 결론짓듯 외치며 다시 아서에게 말했다. \"저는 좋은 기회를 기다렸습니다. 시뇨르 판코께,\"—이 호칭과 함께 팽크스 씨에게 신기한 기색이 어렸다,—\"몇 자 적어 보내, 와서 도와 달라고 했지요. 낮에 종종 정탐을 맡았던 시뇨르 판코께, 창가에 있는 리고를 보여 드렸습니다. 밤에는 그 집 문 가까이서 잤지요. 마침내 우리는, 바로 오늘에야 들어왔고, 이제 그를 보시는 겁니다! 그가 그 저명한 변호사님 앞에서는 올라오려 하지 않아서,\"—이것이 러그 씨에 대한 밥티스트 씨의 영예로운 언급이었다,—\"우리는 저 아래에서 함께 기다렸고, 시뇨르 판코께서 거리를 지키셨습니다.\"\n\n이 이야기가 끝나자, 아서는 그 뻔뻔하고 사악한 얼굴로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그 얼굴이 그의 눈길과 마주치자, 코는 콧수염 위로 내려오고 콧수염은 코 밑으로 올라갔다. 코와 콧수염이 다시 제자리에 자리 잡자, 리고 씨는 여섯 번쯤 손가락을 요란하게 튕겼는데, 몸을 앞으로 기울여 아서에게 그 튕김을 휙휙 던졌으니, 마치 그것이 그의 얼굴에 던지는 손에 잡히는 발사체라도 되는 양했다.\n\n\"자, 철학자 양반!\" 리고가 말했다. \"나한테 뭘 원하나?\"\n\n\"알고 싶소,\" 아서가 자신의 혐오감을 감추지 않은 채 응수했다, \"당신이 어찌 감히 우리 어머니 댁에 살인 의혹을 들이댄 거요?\"\n\n\"감히라!\" 리고가 외쳤다. \"호, 호! 이 친구 좀 보게! 감히라? 감히라고? 맙소사, 이 애송이야, 자네 좀 경솔하군!\"\n\n\"나는 그 의혹이 말끔히 씻기기를 원하오.\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신을 거기로 데려가, 사람들 앞에 공공연히 보이도록 할 거요. 그리고 또 알고 싶소. 내가 당신을 계단 아래로 내던지고 싶은 불타는 욕망에 사로잡혔던 그때, 당신이 거기서 무슨 볼일이 있었던 거요. 나한테 인상 쓰지 마시오! 나는 당신이 약자에게 강한 척하는 겁쟁이임을 알 만큼 충분히 봐 왔소. 이렇게 명백한 사실을, 게다가 당신 스스로도 익히 아는 사실을 말해 주는 데에, 이 비참한 곳의 영향에서 기운을 되살릴 필요 따윈 없소.\"\n\n리고는 입술까지 새하얘져서 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 중얼거렸다. \"맙소사, 이 애송이야, 자네 좀 우리 마님, 자네의 존경스러운 어머니를 곤란하게 만드는군\"—그러고는 잠시 어찌할지 결정하지 못한 듯 보였다. 그러나 그의 망설임은 곧 사라졌다. 그는 위협하듯 거들먹거리며 자리에 앉아 말했다.\n\n\"포도주 한 병 다오. 여기서 포도주를 살 수 있지. 저 미치광이 하나를 보내 포도주 한 병 가져오게 해. 포도주 없이는 자네와 이야기하지 않겠네. 자! 어떤가? 좋은가, 싫은가?\"\n\n\"카발레토, 그가 원하는 걸 가져다주게.\" 아서가 경멸하듯 말하며 돈을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"밀수꾼 짐승아,\" 리고가 덧붙였다, \"포트와인을 가져와! 나는 포르토-포르토밖에 안 마시겠다.\"\n\n그러나 그 밀수꾼 짐승은, 의미심장한 손가락으로 모두에게, 문간의 제 자리를 떠나기를 단호히 거부한다고 확언했고, 시뇨르 판코가 자청해 나섰다. 그는 곧 포도주 병을 들고 돌아왔는데, 그 병은 (코르크 따개가 부족했던—이는 그 밖의 많은 것이 부족했던 것과 마찬가지였다—재소자들 사이에서 비롯된) 그곳의 관례에 따라, 이미 따져서 쓸 수 있게 되어 있었다.\n\n\"미치광이! 큰 잔으로.\" 리고가 말했다.\n\n시뇨르 판코는 그의 앞에 큰 잔을 놓았는데, 그것을 그의 머리에 내던질까 하는 문제로 눈에 보이는 감정의 갈등이 없지 않았다.\n\n\"하하!\" 리고가 으스댔다. \"한번 신사면 영원히 신사지. 처음부터 신사요, 끝까지 신사라네. 빌어먹을! 신사라면 시중을 받아야지, 안 그런가? 시중받는 것이 내 성품의 일부거든!\"\n\n그는 그 말을 하면서 잔을 절반쯤 채웠고, 말을 마치자 그 내용물을 단숨에 들이켰다.\n\n\"하!\" 입맛을 다시며. \"그리 오래된 죄수는 아니구먼, 이건! 자네 안색을 보니, 용감하신 양반, 투옥은 이 독한 포도주를 부드럽게 하는 것보다 훨씬 빨리 자네 피를 길들이겠어. 자네는 익어 가고 있군—벌써 몸피와 혈색을 잃어 가고 있어. 자네에게 경배를!\"\n\n그는 또 반 잔을 들이켰는데, 마시기 전후로 잔을 치켜들어 자신의 작고 흰 손을 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"본론으로.\" 그가 이어 말했다. \"대화로 들어가지. 자네는 몸보다 입이 더 자유로운 걸 보여 줬어, 양반.\"\n\n\"나는 당신이 스스로 어떤 인간인 줄 아는 그대로 말해 준 자유를 누렸을 뿐이오. 당신은, 우리 모두가 당신을 아는 대로, 그보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 인간임을 스스로 알고 있소.\"\n\n\"거기에 '항상 신사'를 덧붙이면, 그런 건 아무래도 좋아. 그 한 가지만 빼면 우리는 다 똑같지. 예를 들어, 자네는 죽었다 깨어나도 신사가 될 수 없고, 나는 죽었다 깨어나도 신사 아닌 존재가 될 수 없네. 그 차이가 얼마나 큰가! 계속하지. 양반, 말이라는 건 결코 카드의 흐름이나 주사위의 흐름에 영향을 주지 못해. 그걸 아나? 알아? 나 또한 한판의 도박을 벌이는데, 말 따위는 그 위에 아무 힘도 없지.\"\n\n이제 카발레토와 맞닥뜨려, 자신의 내력이 알려졌음을 알게 된 그는—그동안 걸치고 있던 얄팍한 가면이 무엇이었든 그것을 벗어 던지고는, 본래의 파렴치한 악당답게 맨얼굴로 버텨 냈다.\n\n\"아니, 이 사람아,\" 그가 손가락을 한 번 튕기며 말을 이었다. \"나는 말 따위에 아랑곳없이 내 판을 끝까지 밀어붙일 것이고—내 몸의 죽음과 내 영혼의 죽음을 걸고! 기어이 이길 거야. 자네가 방해한 이 자그마한 속임수를 내가 왜 벌였는지 알고 싶나? 그럼 알아 두게. 나에겐 팔 물건이 있었고, 또 있다네—내 말 알아듣겠나? 있단 말이야—자네의 존경스러운 어머니, 우리 마님께 팔 물건이 말이지. 나는 내 귀한 물건을 설명하고 값을 매겼어. 그 거래에 관한 한, 자네의 훌륭하신 어머니는 좀 지나치게 침착하고, 지나치게 무덤덤하고, 지나치게 요지부동이고 석상 같았지. 요컨대, 자네의 훌륭하신 어머니가 나를 짜증 나게 했단 말이야. 내 처지에 변화를 주고 나도 좀 즐기려고—뭐! 신사라면 누군가의 희생으로 즐겨야 하지 않겠나!—나는 사라진다는 멋진 착상을 떠올렸지. 보다시피, 자네의 그 별난 어머니와 우리 플린트윈치라면 기꺼이 실행에 옮겼을 만한 착상이거든. 아! 바, 바, 바, 나를 위에서 아래로 깔보듯 보지 말게! 다시 말하네. 기꺼이도 정도가 아니라, 지나치도록 황홀해하며, 진심으로 넋이 나가도록 좋아했을 거야. 얼마나 강하게 말해 줘야 믿겠나?\"\n\n그는 잔에 남은 찌꺼기를 바닥에 내던졌는데, 하마터면 카발레토에게 튈 뻔했다. 이것이 그의 주의를 새삼 그에게 돌린 모양이었다. 그는 잔을 내려놓고 말했다.\n\n\"내가 직접 채우진 않겠다. 뭐라고! 나는 시중받으려고 태어난 사람이야. 자, 그럼, 너 카발레토, 와서 채워라!\"\n\n그 작은 사내는 클레넘을 바라보았는데, 그의 눈은 리고에게 쏠려 있었고, 제지하는 기색이 없자, 그는 바닥에서 일어나 병의 술을 잔에 따랐다. 그렇게 하는 동안, 그의 옛 복종심과 무언가 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 뒤섞이고, 또 그것이 한순간에 불꽃을 튀길 수도 있었던 어떤 들끓는 사나움과 다투며(타고난 신사는 그렇게 여기는 듯, 그를 경계하는 눈으로 지켜보았다), 그러면서도 그 모든 것이 다시 바닥에 주저앉으려는 선량하고 무심하며 우세한 성향에 손쉽게 굴복하는 모습은, 매우 주목할 만한 성격의 조합을 이루었다.\n\n\"이 멋진 착상은, 용감하신 양반,\" 술을 마신 뒤 리고가 다시 말을 이었다, \"여러 이유로 멋진 착상이었지. 나를 즐겁게 했고, 자네의 사랑하는 어머니와 우리 플린트윈치를 애태웠고, 자네에게 고통을 안겨 주었으며(신사에 대한 예절을 가르친 수업의 대가지), 또 관심 있는 그 모든 상냥한 분들에게, 자네에게 전적으로 헌신하는 이 몸이 두려워해야 할 사람임을 일깨워 주었지. 맙소사, 그는 두려워해야 할 사람이라네! 그뿐인가. 그 일로 자네 어머니, 우리 마님이 정신을 되찾았을지도 모르지—자네의 지혜가 알아챈 그 자그마하고도 절박한 의혹의 압박 아래, 마침내 신문에다 넌지시, 어떤 계약의 난점들이 그 계약의 어떤 중요한 당사자가 나타나면 해소될 것이라고 공표하도록 설득했을지도 몰라. 어쩌면 그랬을 수도, 어쩌면 아닐 수도. 하지만 그걸 자네가 방해했지. 자, 이제 뭐라고 할 텐가? 뭘 원하나?\"\n\n자신 앞에 이 사내를 두고도 그를 어머니의 집으로 데려갈 수 없을 때만큼, 클레넘이 자신이 사슬에 묶인 죄수임을 절절히 느낀 적은 없었다. 그가 여태껏 두려워해 온 온갖 분간할 수 없는 난점과 위험들이, 손발 하나 까딱할 수 없는 바로 그때 죄어들고 있었다.\n\n\"어쩌면, 내 친구, 철학자, 미덕의 인간, 멍청이, 뭐라 부르든 말이지. 어쩌면,\" 리고가 술을 마시다 멈추고 그 끔찍한 미소를 띤 채 잔 너머로 내다보며 말했다, \"자네는 나를 가만 내버려 두는 편이 나았을지도 몰라?\"\n\n\"아니오! 적어도,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당신이 살아 있고 무사하다는 게 알려졌소. 적어도 당신은 이 두 증인에게서 달아날 수 없고, 그들은 어떤 공적 권위 앞에서든, 또는 수백 명의 사람들 앞에서든 당신을 내세울 수 있소!\"\n\n\"하지만 단 한 명 앞에서도 나를 내세우지는 못할걸.\" 리고가 의기양양한 위협의 기색으로 다시 손가락을 튕기며 말했다. \"자네의 증인들 따위는 악마에게나 줘 버려! 자네가 내세운다는 것도 악마에게나 줘 버려! 자네 자신도 악마에게나 줘 버리고! 뭐라고! 그렇다고 내가 아는 것을 내가 모르나? 그렇다고 내 물건이 팔려고 내놓아져 있지 않나? 바, 가엾은 채무자여! 자네는 내 자그마한 계획을 방해했어. 그건 됐고. 그래서? 무엇이 남았나? 자네에겐, 아무것도. 나에겐, 전부. 나를 내세우라고! 그게 자네가 원하는 건가? 내가 직접 나를 내세우지, 너무도 빠르게 말이야. 밀수꾼! 펜과 잉크와 종이를 다오.\"\n\n카발레토는 다시 아까처럼 일어나, 아까의 그 태도로 그것들을 그의 앞에 놓아 주었다. 리고는 무언가 악랄한 생각에 잠겨 미소 짓더니, 다음과 같이 쓰고 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\"클레넘 부인께.\n\n답장을 기다림.\n\n마셜시 감옥에서.\n귀하의 아드님의 거처에서.\n\n친애하는 부인,\n\n오늘 이곳의 우리 죄수(정략상의 이유로 은거하며 사는 저를 찾아내고자 친히 정탐꾼들을 부린 분이지요)에게서, 부인께서 제 안위를 염려하셨다는 소식을 전해 듣고 저는 절망에 빠졌습니다.\n\n안심하소서, 친애하는 부인. 저는 잘 있으며, 강건하고 한결같습니다.\n\n저는 더없이 조바심을 내며 부인의 댁으로 날아가고 싶으나, 사정상 부인께서 제가 영광스럽게도 부인께 제출한 그 자그마한 제안을 아직 완전히 확정적으로 정리하지 못하셨을 수도 있음을 예견합니다. 이날로부터 일주일 뒤를, 저로서는 마지막 최종 방문일로 정하겠습니다. 그때 부인께서는 그 제안을, 그에 따른 일련의 결과와 함께, 조건 없이 받아들이시거나 거절하시면 됩니다.\n\n부인을 포옹하고 이 흥미로운 일을 마무리하고픈 제 열정을 억누르는 것은, 부인께서 그 세부 사항을 우리 양쪽의 완벽한 만족에 맞도록 조정하실 여유를 가지시도록 하기 위함입니다.\n\n그동안, (우리 죄수가 제 살림을 어지럽혀 놓았으니) 호텔에서의 제 숙박비와 식대를 부인께서 지불하시기를 제안하는 것은 지나친 일이 아닐 것입니다.\n\n받아 주소서, 친애하는 부인, 저의 가장 높고 가장 각별한 경의의 표시를.\n리고 블랑두아.\n\n저 사랑하는 플린트윈치에게 천 번의 우정을.\n\nF 부인의 두 손에 입맞춤을.\"\n\n이 서한을 다 쓴 뒤, 리고는 그것을 접어 과장된 몸짓으로 클레넘의 발치에 던졌다. \"이봐, 자네! 내세운다는 이야기가 나온 김에, 누군가 저것을 받는 이의 주소로 내세우고, 그 답장을 여기로 가져오게 하게.\"\n\n\"카발레토,\" 아서가 말했다. \"이자의 편지를 가져가 주겠나?\"\n\n그러나 카발레토의 의미심장한 손가락은, 이토록 애써서 리고를 찾아낸 마당에 자신의 자리는 그를 감시하는 문간이며, 또 그 자리의 임무란 문에 등을 대고 바닥에 앉아 리고를 바라보며 제 발목을 쥐고 있는 것이라고 다시금 표현했다. 그리하여 시뇨르 판코가 또 한 번 자청해 나섰다. 그의 봉사가 받아들여지자, 카발레토는 문을 자신이 비집고 나갈 만큼만 빠끔히 열렸다가, 곧장 그의 뒤에서 닫히도록 했다.\n\n\"내가 여기 앉아 마음껏 포도주를 마시는데, 손가락 하나라도 나를 건드리거나, 욕설 한마디라도 나를 건드리거나, 내 우월함에 의문을 제기해 봐,\" 리고가 말했다, \"그러면 나는 편지대로 따라 일주일의 유예를 취소해 버리겠어. 자네가 나를 원했지? 자네는 나를 손에 넣었어! 내가 마음에 드나?\"\n\n\"당신도 알겠지만,\" 클레넘이 자신의 무력함에 쓰라림을 느끼며 응수했다, \"내가 당신을 찾으려 했을 때, 나는 죄수가 아니었소.\"\n\n\"자네도 자네 감옥도 악마에게나 줘 버려.\" 리고가 느긋하게 되받으며, 주머니에서 궐련 마는 재료가 든 갑을 꺼내, 그 날렵한 손으로 당장 쓸 몇 개를 마는 데 몰두했다. \"나는 자네도 자네 감옥도 아랑곳없어. 밀수꾼! 불을.\"\n\n다시 카발레토가 일어나 그가 원하는 것을 주었다. 그 차갑고 흰 손이 소리 없이 놀리는 솜씨에는 무언가 끔찍한 것이 있었으니, 손가락들이 유연하게 비틀거리며 서로 휘감기는 모습이 마치 뱀들 같았다. 클레넘은 마치 그 생물들의 둥지를 들여다보고 있기라도 한 듯, 속으로 진저리치는 것을 막을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이봐, 돼지!\" 리고가, 카발레토가 마치 이탈리아 말이나 노새이기라도 한 듯, 요란하게 부추기는 소리로 외쳤다. \"뭐라고! 그 지옥 같은 옛 감옥이 이곳에 비하면 점잖은 곳이었어. 그곳의 쇠창살과 돌에는 위엄이 있었지. 그건 사내들을 위한 감옥이었어. 그런데 이건? 바! 멍청이들을 위한 병원이로군!\"\n\n그는 궐련을 끝까지 피웠는데, 그 추악한 미소가 얼굴에 어찌나 고정되어 있던지, 마치 입이 아니라 축 늘어진 부리 같은 코로 담배를 피우는 것처럼 보였으니, 기이한 그림 속의 환상 같았다. 첫 번째 궐련의 아직 타고 있는 끝에 두 번째 궐련을 붙여 불을 댕긴 그는 클레넘에게 말했다.\n\n\"미치광이가 없는 동안 시간을 보내야지. 이야기를 해야겠어. 독한 포도주를 하루 종일 마실 수는 없는 노릇이거든, 안 그러면 한 병 더 청했을 텐데. 그 여자는 미인이야, 양반. 딱 내 취향은 아니지만, 그래도, 천둥과 번개를 걸고! 미인이지. 자네의 그 감탄에 축하를 보내네.\"\n\n\"누구를 말하는지 나는 알지도 못하고, 묻지도 않겠소.\" 클레넘이 말했다.\n\n\"이탈리아에서들 말하듯, 델라 벨라 가우아나 말이야, 양반. 가우언, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인.\"\n\n\"내 생각엔, 당신이 그 부인 남편의—추종자였던 모양인데?\"\n\n\"양반? 추종자라? 무례하군. 친구였지.\"\n\n\"당신은 친구를 죄다 팔아 치우오?\"\n\n리고는 입에서 궐련을 떼고, 순간 놀라움을 드러내며 그를 쳐다보았다. 그러나 그는 다시 그것을 입술 사이에 물고, 냉정하게 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 값이 나가는 것이라면 무엇이든 팔지. 자네네 변호사들은, 정치가들은, 모사꾼들은, 증권가의 인간들은 어떻게 먹고사나? 자네는 어떻게 먹고사나? 자네는 어떻게 여기 왔나? 자네는 친구를 한 번도 팔지 않았나? 내가 모실 마님이여! 내 생각엔, 팔았을걸!\"\n\n클레넘은 그에게서 등을 돌려 창문 쪽으로, 벽을 내다보며 앉았다.\n\n\"실로, 양반,\" 리고가 말했다, \"사회는 자신을 팔고 또 나를 파네. 그리고 나는 사회를 팔지. 자네가 또 다른 부인과 아는 사이임을 알겠군. 그 여자도 미인이야. 강한 정신의 소유자고. 어디 보자. 그 이름을 뭐라 부르더라? 웨이드.\"\n\n그는 아무 대답도 듣지 못했지만, 자신이 정곡을 찔렀음을 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n\"그래,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"그 아름답고 강한 정신의 부인이 길거리에서 내게 말을 걸더군, 그리고 나는 무심하지 않았지. 나는 응답했어. 그 아름답고 강한 정신의 부인이 호의를 베풀어, 아주 신뢰하는 투로 이렇게 말하더군. '나는 호기심이 있고, 또 분한 일들이 있어요. 당신은 어쩌면 보통 사람들보다 더 명예롭지는 않은 분이겠지요?' 나는 나를 이렇게 밝혔지. '부인, 저는 나면서부터 신사요, 죽을 때까지 신사이나, 보통 사람들보다 더 명예롭지는 않습니다. 저는 그런 나약한 환상을 경멸하지요.' 그러자 그 부인이 기꺼이 칭찬을 하더군. '당신과 나머지 사람들의 차이는,' 그녀가 대답하길, '당신은 그렇다고 말한다는 점이에요.' 그녀는 사회를 아니까. 나는 그녀의 찬사를 정중하고 예의 바르게 받아들였지. 예의와 자그마한 정중함은 내 성품과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이거든. 그러더니 그녀가 한 가지 제안을 하더군. 요컨대, 그녀가 우리를 자주 함께 있는 걸 보았고, 자기 보기에 내가 그 무렵 그 집의 고양이, 그 가족의 친구인 것 같다는 거였어. 자기의 호기심과 분한 일들이, 그들의 동정을 알고 싶고, 그들의 생활 방식을, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인이 어떻게 사랑받는지, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인이 어떻게 아낌받는지, 등등을 알고 싶은 마음을 일으킨다는 거였지. 그녀는 부유하지는 않지만, 그런 수고와 번거로움의 자그마한 봉사에 이러이러한 자그마한 보답을 하겠노라 제안했어. 그래서 나는 너그럽게—모든 일을 너그럽게 하는 것이 내 성품의 일부거든—그것을 받아들이기로 했지. 오, 그래! 세상이란 그렇게 돌아가는 거야. 그게 유행이지.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안 클레넘은 등을 돌리고 있었고, 그 후로도 면담이 끝날 때까지 그러했지만, 리고는 너무 가까이 붙어 있는 그 번득이는 두 눈으로 그를 계속 지켜보았으며, 자신이 허풍스러운 무모함으로 한 구절 한 구절 늘어놓는 동안, 바로 그 고개를 든 자세에서, 자신이 클레넘이 이미 알지 못하는 것이라곤 아무것도 말하지 않고 있음을 분명히 알아챘다.\n\n\"훅! 아름다운 가우언 부인!\" 그는 마치 자신의 가장 가벼운 숨결로도 그녀를 날려 버릴 수 있다는 듯한 소리를 내며 세 번째 궐련에 불을 붙이고 말했다. \"매혹적이지만, 경솔해! 그 아름다운 가우언 부인이, 산 위의 침실에서, 남편이 못 보도록 옛 연인들에게서 온 편지들을 비밀로 한 것은 잘한 일이 아니었거든. 아니, 아니. 그건 잘한 일이 아니었어. 훅! 가우언 부인이 그 점에서 실수한 거지.\"\n\n\"진심으로 바라건대,\" 아서가 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"팽크스가 오래 걸리지 않았으면 좋겠소. 이자의 존재가 이 방을 더럽히고 있으니.\"\n\n\"아! 하지만 나는 여기서도, 어디서든 번성할 거야.\" 리고가 의기양양한 표정으로 손가락을 튕기며 말했다. \"나는 늘 그래 왔고, 늘 그럴 거야!\" 클레넘이 앉은 의자 말고 방에 있는 단 세 개의 의자에 몸을 쭉 뻗고 누운 그는, 노래 속 늠름한 인물처럼 제 가슴을 치며 노래했다.\n\n\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가? 마졸렌의 동무여! 이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가? 언제나 즐겁다네!\n\n후렴을 불러라, 돼지야! 너는 다른 감옥에서 이걸 한 번 부른 적이 있지. 불러! 안 그러면, 돌에 맞아 죽은 모든 성인을 걸고, 나는 모욕을 느끼고 곤란하게 굴 테다. 그러면 아직 죽지 않은 어떤 사람들은, 차라리 그 성인들과 함께 돌에 맞아 죽는 편이 나았을 거야!\"\n\n\"임금의 모든 기사 중 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 마졸렌의 동무여! 임금의 모든 기사 중 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 언제나 즐겁다네!\"\n\n한편으로는 옛 복종의 습관 때문에, 한편으로는 그러지 않으면 자신의 은인에게 해가 될까 봐, 또 한편으로는 다른 무엇을 하느니 차라리 그러는 게 낫겠다는 마음에서, 카발레토는 이번에는 후렴을 받아 불렀다. 리고는 웃더니, 눈을 감고 담배를 피우기 시작했다.\n\n팽크스 씨의 발소리가 계단에서 들리기까지 아마도 또 한 십오 분쯤 흘렀을 테지만, 그 시간은 클레넘에게 견딜 수 없이 길게 느껴졌다. 그의 발소리에는 또 다른 발소리가 함께했고, 카발레토가 문을 열자, 그는 팽크스 씨와 플린트윈치 씨를 들여보냈다. 후자가 모습을 보이기 무섭게, 리고가 그에게 달려들어 요란하게 그를 끌어안았다.\n\n\"좀 어떠신가, 선생?\" 플린트윈치 씨가, 거의 격식이라곤 없이 안간힘을 써서 몸을 떼어 낼 수 있게 되자마자 말했다. \"고맙지만, 됐네. 더는 필요 없어.\" 이는 되찾은 친구가 또 한 번 애정 공세를 위협한 데 대한 말이었다. \"그래, 아서. 잠자는 개와 행방불명된 개에 대해 자네에게 했던 말 기억하나? 보다시피, 그게 사실로 드러났네.\"\n\n그는 겉보기엔 여전히 평소처럼 태연했으며, 방 안을 둘러보면서 훈계조로 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"그리고 이것이 채무자를 가두는 마셜시 감옥이라는 거지!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"허! 자네는 돼지들을 영 시원찮은 시장에 끌고 왔구먼, 아서.\"\n\n아서에게는 인내심이 있었으나, 리고에게는 없었다. 그는 사납고 장난스럽게 자신의 작은 플린트윈치를 외투의 두 옷깃을 잡고 외쳤다.\n\n\"시장 따위는 악마에게나 줘 버리고, 돼지들도 악마에게나 줘 버리고, 돼지치기도 악마에게나 줘 버려라! 자! 내 편지에 대한 답을 다오.\"\n\n\"잠깐만 놓아주시는 게 편하시다면, 선생,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 응수했다, \"먼저 아서 씨에게 전할 짧은 쪽지를 건네야겠습니다.\"\n\n그는 그렇게 했다. 그것은 그의 어머니의 불구가 된 필체로, 종잇조각에 적혀 있었고, 다음의 말만이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"네가 네 자신을 파멸시킨 것으로 족하기를 바란다. 더 이상의 파멸 없이 만족하며 지내라. 제레마이어 플린트윈치가 나의 전령이자 대리인이다. 너를 사랑하는 어미, M. C.\"\n\n클레넘은 이것을 잠자코 두 번 읽고는, 갈가리 찢어 버렸다. 그동안 리고는 의자 위로 올라서서, 발을 좌석에 올린 채 등받이에 걸터앉았다.\n\n\"자, 멋쟁이 플린트윈치,\" 그가 그 쪽지가 결딴나는 것까지 유심히 지켜본 뒤 말했다, \"내 편지에 대한 답은?\"\n\n\"클레넘 부인께서는 손이 경련을 일으켜 쓰지 못하셨고, 또 저를 통해 구두로 전하는 편이 낫겠다 여기셨습니다, 블랑두아 씨.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 마지못해, 녹슨 듯 뻑뻑하게 이 말을 쥐어짜 냈다. \"부인께서 안부를 전하시며, 대체로 당신을 분별없는 자라 칭하고 싶지는 않으며, 동의하신다고 하셨습니다. 다만 이날로부터 일주일 뒤로 잡힌 약속에는 손상이 없도록 하면서요.\"\n\n리고 씨는 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨린 뒤, 자신의 옥좌에서 내려오며 말했다. \"좋아! 호텔을 찾으러 가야겠군!\" 그런데 그때 그의 눈이 여전히 제 자리를 지키고 있는 카발레토와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"이리 와, 돼지.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"나는 내 뜻과 다르게 너를 추종자로 두었었지. 이번엔 네 뜻과 다르게 너를 두겠다. 내 작은 파충류들아, 일러두는데, 나는 시중받으려고 태어난 사람이야. 나는 이 밀수꾼이 이날로부터 일주일 뒤까지 내 하인 노릇을 할 것을 요구한다.\"\n\n카발레토의 묻는 듯한 눈길에 답하여, 클레넘은 그에게 가라는 신호를 보냈다. 그러나 그는 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"자네가 그를 두려워하는 게 아니라면 말일세.\" 카발레토는 손가락으로 매우 단호하게 아니라는 뜻을 표하며 대답했다. \"아니요, 주인님, 저는 그가 두렵지 않습니다. 그가 한때 제 동료였음을 더 이상 비밀로 간직하지 않게 되었으니까요.\" 리고는 마지막 궐련에 불을 붙이고 걸을 채비를 완전히 갖출 때까지는 어느 쪽의 말에도 아랑곳하지 않았다.\n\n\"그가 두렵다고,\" 그가 그제야 그들 모두를 둘러보며 말했다. \"훅! 내 아이들아, 내 갓난쟁이들아, 내 작은 인형들아, 너희는 모두 그를 두려워하지. 너희는 여기서 그에게 포도주 한 병을 주고, 저기서 그에게 먹을 것과 마실 것과 잠자리를 주고, 손가락 하나로도 욕설 한마디로도 감히 그를 건드리지 못해. 그래. 승리하는 것이 그의 성품이거든! 훅!\n\n\"임금의 모든 기사 중 그가 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 그리고 그는 언제나 즐겁다네!\"\n\n이렇게 그 후렴을 자신에게 맞추어 고쳐 부르며, 그는 카발레토를 바짝 뒤에 거느리고 거들먹거리며 방을 나섰으니, 어쩌면 그를 떼어 내기가 쉽지 않으리란 걸 제법 잘 알았기에 그를 자기 시중에 끌어들인 것이었다. 플린트윈치 씨는 턱을 긁적이고는, 돼지 시장에 대한 신랄한 멸시를 담아 주위를 둘러본 뒤, 아서에게 고개를 끄덕이고 따라 나섰다. 여전히 뉘우치며 풀이 죽어 있던 팽크스 씨도, 아서에게서 은밀한 지시 한두 마디를 매우 주의 깊게 받고, 이 일을 끝까지 지켜보며 끝까지 함께하겠노라 속삭여 답한 뒤, 따라 나섰다. 죄수는, 전보다 더 멸시받고, 더 경멸받고 배척당하며, 더 무력하고, 통틀어 더 비참하고 추락한 신세가 되었다는 느낌과 함께, 또다시 홀로 남겨졌다.\n\n# CHAPTER 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea\n\n초췌한 근심과 회한은 함께 갇혀 지내기에 나쁜 동무다. 온종일 골똘히 시름에 잠기고 밤에는 거의 쉬지 못하는 것으로는 사람을 비참함에 맞서도록 무장시켜 주지 못한다. 이튿날 아침, 클레넘은 자신의 건강이 가라앉고 있음을, 이미 기력이 가라앉아 버린 것처럼, 그리고 그가 짓눌려 굽어 있는 그 무게가 자신을 짓이겨 내리누르고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n밤마다 그는 비참한 잠자리에서 열두 시나 한 시쯤에 일어나, 창가에 앉아 마당의 병약한 등불을 지켜보며, 하늘이 그에게 그것을 보여 줄 수 있기까지 아직 여러 시간이나 남았는데도 위를 올려다보며 동트는 첫 창백한 자취를 찾았다. 그런데 이제 밤이 오자, 그는 옷을 벗으라고 스스로를 설득할 수조차 없었다.\n\n타는 듯한 안절부절못함이 찾아왔기 때문이다. 감옥에 대한 고통스러운 초조, 그리고 자신이 이곳에서 가슴이 미어져 죽고 말리라는 확신이 그에게 형언할 수 없는 괴로움을 안겼다. 이곳에 대한 그의 두려움과 증오가 너무도 격렬해져서, 그는 이 안에서 숨을 들이쉬는 것조차 노역처럼 느껴졌다. 질식하는 듯한 감각이 때로는 그를 너무도 압도하여, 그는 목을 움켜쥐고 헐떡이며 창가에 서 있곤 했다. 동시에 다른 공기를 향한 갈망, 저 눈먼 텅 빈 담장 너머로 나가고 싶은 간절함이, 그 욕망의 격렬함에 자신이 미쳐 버릴 것만 같은 느낌이 들게 했다.\n\n그보다 앞서 다른 많은 죄수들도 이런 상태를 겪은 바 있었고, 그 격렬함과 끈질김은 그의 경우에서처럼 그들의 경우에서도 제풀에 닳아 없어졌다. 이틀 밤과 하루가 그것을 소진시켰다. 발작처럼 되돌아오곤 했으나, 그 발작은 점점 희미해졌고 점점 긴 간격을 두고 돌아왔다. 황량한 고요가 뒤를 이었고, 그 주의 중반에 이르러서는 낮고 더딘 미열의 의기소침 속에 그가 가라앉아 있는 것이 보였다.\n\n카발레토와 팽크스가 자리를 비웠으니, 그가 두려워할 방문객이라곤 플로니시 부부밖에 없었다. 그 선량한 부부에 관한 그의 근심은, 그들이 그에게 가까이 오지 않기를 바라는 것이었다. 병적인 신경 상태에서 그는 홀로 남겨지기를 바랐고, 이토록 풀이 죽고 약해진 모습을 보이는 일을 면하고 싶었던 것이다. 그는 플로니시 부인에게 쪽지를 써서, 자신이 일에 매여 있으며 거기에 몰두해야 할 필요에 묶여 있어, 당분간은 부인의 다정한 얼굴을 보는 그 즐거운 방해조차 없이 지내야 한다고 둘러댔다. 매일 일정한 시각에, 간수들이 교대할 때면 들여다보며 무엇이든 해 드릴 일이 없느냐고 묻는 젊은 존에게는, 그는 늘 글을 쓰는 척하며 명랑하게 없다고 대답했다. 그들 사이에 단 한 번 길게 나눈 대화의 주제는 두 사람 사이에서 다시 들춰진 적이 없었다. 그러나 이 모든 불행의 변천을 거치는 동안에도, 그 주제는 클레넘의 마음을 결코 놓아주지 않았다.\n\n정해진 한 주의 엿새째 날은 축축하고 무덥고 안개 낀 날이었다. 마치 감옥의 가난과 초라함과 더러움이 후텁지근한 대기 속에서 자라나는 것만 같았다. 머리가 지끈거리고 마음이 지친 채로, 클레넘은 그 비참한 밤을 지새우며, 마당 포석 위로 떨어지는 빗소리를 듣고, 그것이 시골 땅 위로 더 부드럽게 내리는 모습을 생각했다. 해 대신 누런 안개의 흐릿한 둥근 테두리가 하늘에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것이 자기 벽에 던지는 얼룩을, 마치 감옥의 누더기 한 조각 같은 그 얼룩을 지켜보았다. 그는 문이 열리는 소리를, 밖에서 기다리던 신발 시원찮게 신은 발들이 끌리며 들어오는 소리를, 그리고 쓸고 물 푸고 이리저리 움직이는 소리가 시작되는 것을 들었으니, 그것이 감옥의 아침을 여는 일이었다. 너무도 아프고 기운이 없어 몸을 씻는 동안에도 여러 번 쉬어야 했던 그는, 마침내 열린 창가의 의자로 기어가듯 다가갔다. 그 의자에 앉아 그는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았고, 그의 방을 정돈하는 노파는 아침 일과를 해 나갔다.\n\n잠이 모자라고 음식이 모자라(식욕도, 미각조차도 그를 떠나 버렸다) 머리가 어질했던 그는, 밤중에 두세 번 정신이 흐려지는 것을 의식한 적이 있었다. 그는 따뜻한 바람 속에서 가락과 노래의 조각들을 들었는데, 그것이 실재하지 않는 것임을 그도 알고 있었다. 이제 그가 기진맥진하여 졸기 시작하자, 그는 그것들을 다시 들었고, 여러 목소리가 그에게 말을 거는 듯하여, 그는 대답하다가 화들짝 깨곤 했다.\n\n졸고 꿈꾸며, 시간을 가늠할 힘도 없이—그래서 일 분이 한 시간 같고 한 시간이 일 분 같았는데—어떤 정원의 머무는 인상이 그에게 슬며시 스며들었다. 축축하고 따뜻한 바람이 향기를 부드럽게 흔드는, 꽃들이 핀 정원이었다. 이것을 살펴보려, 혹은 무엇이든 살펴보려 머리를 드는 데에 너무나 고통스러운 노력이 필요해서, 그가 주위를 둘러볼 즈음에는 그 인상이 아주 오래되고 끈질긴 것이 되어 버린 듯했다. 그러자 탁자 위의 찻잔 옆에, 그는 활짝 핀 작은 꽃다발을 보았다. 가장 고르고 가장 어여쁜 꽃들을 놀랍도록 한 아름 모은 것이었다.\n\n그의 눈에 일찍이 그토록 아름다워 보인 것은 없었다. 그는 그것을 집어 들어 향기를 들이마셨고, 그것을 자신의 뜨거운 머리로 들어 올렸으며, 그것을 내려놓고는 메마른 두 손을 그쪽으로 펼쳤으니, 마치 차가운 손을 불의 온기를 받으려 펼치는 것 같았다. 한참을 그것들로 기뻐한 뒤에야 비로소 그는 누가 보냈을까 궁금해졌고, 분명 그것을 거기 두었을 그 노파에게 어떻게 그 꽃이 그녀의 손에 들어왔는지 물으려 문을 열었다. 그러나 노파는 가고 없었고, 떠난 지 오래된 듯했다. 탁자 위에 그를 위해 남겨 둔 차가 식어 있었기 때문이다. 그는 좀 마셔 보려 했으나 그 냄새를 견딜 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 다시 열린 창가의 의자로 기어가 돌아왔고, 꽃들을 예의 그 작고 둥근 탁자 위에 놓았다.\n\n이리저리 움직인 데서 비롯된 처음의 어지러움이 가시자, 그는 다시 이전 상태로 가라앉았다. 밤의 가락 중 하나가 바람 속에서 흐르고 있을 때, 그의 방문이 가벼운 손길에 열리는 듯했고, 한순간 멈칫한 뒤, 검은 망토를 두른 조용한 형체가 거기 서 있는 듯했다. 그 형체가 망토를 벗어 바닥에 떨어뜨리는 듯하더니, 그러자 그것은 낡고 해진 옷을 입은 그의 리틀 도릿인 듯했다. 그 형체는 떨고 있는 듯, 두 손을 맞잡는 듯, 미소 짓는 듯, 그리고 와락 눈물을 터뜨리는 듯했다.\n\n그는 정신을 차리고 소리쳤다. 그러자 그는 그 사랑스럽고, 가엾어하고, 슬퍼하는, 정다운 얼굴 속에서, 거울에서처럼, 자신이 얼마나 변해 버렸는지를 보았다. 그리고 그녀가 그에게로 다가왔다. 그를 의자에 붙잡아 두려고 두 손을 그의 가슴에 얹은 채, 그의 발치 바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그에게 입맞추려 입술을 들어 올린 채, 그리고 마치 하늘에서 내린 비가 꽃들 위로 떨어졌듯 그녀의 눈물을 그에게 떨어뜨린 채, 리틀 도릿이, 살아 있는 실재가, 그의 이름을 불렀다.\n\n\"오, 나의 가장 좋은 벗이여! 친애하는 클레넘 씨, 제가 당신이 우는 것을 보게 하지 마세요! 저를 보고 기뻐서 우시는 게 아니라면요. 그러시기를 바라요. 당신의 가엾은 아이가 돌아왔어요!\"\n\n그토록 한결같고, 다정하며, 운명에 의해 조금도 더럽혀지지 않았다. 그녀의 목소리에서, 그녀의 눈빛에서, 그녀의 손길에서, 그토록 천사처럼 위로가 되고 참되었다!\n\n그가 그녀를 끌어안자, 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"그분들이 당신이 아프시다는 걸 제게 전혀 말해 주지 않았어요.\" 그러고는 부드럽게 한 팔을 그의 목에 두르고, 그의 머리를 자기 가슴에 누이며, 한 손을 그의 머리에 얹고, 그 손에 자기 뺨을 기댄 채, 그녀가 아직 갓난아기에 불과해, 자신이 남들에게 베푸는 그 모든 보살핌을 도리어 남들로부터 받아야 했던 시절에 바로 그 방에서 자기 아버지를 돌보았던 것처럼, 그렇게 사랑스럽게, 그리고 하느님은 아시거니와 그렇게 천진하게, 그를 간호했다.\n\n말을 할 수 있게 되자, 그가 말했다. \"당신이 내게로 오다니, 이게 가능한 일이오? 게다가 이 옷차림으로?\"\n\n\"당신이 이 옷을 입은 저를 다른 어떤 차림보다 더 좋아하시길 바랐어요. 저는 늘 이 옷을 곁에 두어 저를 일깨우게 했어요. 일깨워질 필요는 없었지만요. 보세요, 저는 혼자가 아니에요. 옛 벗을 한 사람 데려왔답니다.\"\n\n둘러보니, 그는 오래전에 버려졌던 그 큰 모자를 쓰고, 지난날처럼 팔에 바구니를 건 매기가 황홀하게 키득거리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"바로 어제저녁에야 오빠와 함께 런던에 왔어요. 도착하자마자 거의 곧장 플로니시 부인에게 사람을 보내, 당신 소식을 듣고 제가 왔다는 것을 알리려 했어요. 그래서 당신이 여기 계시다는 걸 들었어요. 혹시 밤에 저를 생각하셨나요? 당신이 저를 조금은 생각하셨으리라 거의 믿어요. 저는 당신을 너무도 애타게 생각했고, 아침이 오기까지가 어찌나 길게 느껴지던지요.\"\n\n\"나는 당신을 생각했소—\" 그는 그녀를 무어라 불러야 할지 망설였다. 그녀는 그것을 단번에 알아챘다.\n\n\"당신은 아직 저를 제 본래 이름으로 부르지 않으셨어요. 당신에게 제 본래 이름이 늘 무엇인지 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"나는 당신을 생각했소, 리틀 도릿, 여기 온 뒤로 매일, 매시간, 매분.\"\n\n\"정말요? 정말이세요?\"\n\n그는 그녀 얼굴의 환한 기쁨을, 거기 타오르는 홍조를, 수치심을 느끼며 보았다. 그는 망가지고, 파산하고, 병들고, 명예를 잃은 죄수였다.\n\n\"문이 열리기 전부터 여기 와 있었지만, 곧장 당신께 오기가 두려웠어요. 처음엔 제가 당신께 득보다 해를 끼쳤을 거예요. 이 감옥이 너무나 익숙하면서도 너무나 낯설었고, 가엾은 제 아버지에 대한, 그리고 당신에 대한 수많은 기억을 되살려서, 처음엔 그것이 저를 압도했거든요. 그래서 우리는 문에 오기 전에 치버리 씨께 갔고, 그분이 우리를 안으로 들여 존의 방을 내주셨어요—저의 그 가엾은 옛 방 말이에요, 아시잖아요—그래서 거기서 잠시 기다렸지요. 제가 꽃을 문 앞에 가져다 두었지만, 당신은 제 소리를 못 들으셨어요.\"\n\n그녀는 떠났을 때보다 다소 더 여성스러워 보였고, 이탈리아 햇볕에 무르익은 손길이 그녀의 얼굴에 보였다. 그러나 그 밖에는 그녀는 조금도 변하지 않았다. 그가 그녀에게서 늘 보아 왔던, 그리고 한 번도 감동 없이는 본 적이 없는 그 깊고 수줍은 진지함을, 그는 여전히 보았다. 만약 그것이 그의 가슴을 후벼 파는 새로운 의미를 띠었다면, 그 변화는 그의 인식에 있는 것이지 그녀에게 있는 것이 아니었다.\n\n그녀는 낡은 보닛을 벗어 예전 자리에 걸고, 매기의 도움을 받아, 그의 방을 할 수 있는 한 산뜻하고 깔끔하게 꾸미고, 기분 좋은 향이 나는 물을 방 안에 뿌리는 일을 소리 없이 시작했다. 그 일이 끝나자, 포도와 다른 과일이 가득 담긴 바구니를 풀어, 그 안의 것들을 모두 조용히 정리해 두었다. 그 일이 끝나자, 한순간의 속삭임으로 매기를 보내어 또 다른 누군가를 시켜 바구니를 다시 채우게 했고, 바구니는 이내 새 물건들로 가득 채워져 돌아왔는데, 그중에서 우선 식힌 음료와 젤리가, 그리고 앞으로 마련될 통닭구이와 포도주와 물이 첫 번째로 꺼내졌다. 이런 여러 채비가 끝나자, 그녀는 그의 창에 걸 커튼을 만들어 주려고 낡은 반짇고리를 꺼냈다. 그리하여, 다른 데서는 시끄러운 감옥에까지 두루 퍼져 나가는 듯한 고요가 방 안을 다스리는 가운데, 그는 의자에 마음이 가라앉은 자신을 발견했고, 리틀 도릿은 그의 곁에서 바느질을 했다.\n\n그 겸손한 머리가 다시금 일감 위로 숙여지고, 날렵한 손가락들이 예전의 그 일에 분주한 것을 보는 일—비록 그녀는 거기 그렇게 몰두하지는 않아서, 가엾어하는 눈길을 자주 그의 얼굴로 들어 올렸고, 그 눈이 다시 떨궈질 때면 그 안에 눈물이 고여 있었지만—그렇게 위로받고 위안받으며, 이 위대한 천성의 모든 헌신이 역경에 처한 그에게로 돌려져 그 마르지 않는 선의의 풍요를 그에게 쏟아붓고 있다고 믿는 일은, 클레넘의 떨리는 목소리나 손을 진정시키지 못했고, 그의 쇠약함 속에서 그를 강하게 해 주지도 못했다. 그러나 그것은 그의 사랑과 함께 솟아오르는 내면의 꿋꿋함을 그에게 불어넣었다. 그리고 그가 이제 그녀를 얼마나 애틋이 사랑하는지, 무슨 말로 다 할 수 있으랴!\n\n그들이 담장의 그늘 속에 나란히 앉아 있을 때, 그 그늘은 그에게 빛처럼 내렸다. 그녀는 그가 많은 말을 하도록 두지 않았고, 그는 의자에 등을 기댄 채 그녀를 바라보았다. 이따금 그녀는 일어나 그가 마시도록 잔을 건네주거나, 그의 머리가 기댄 자리를 매만져 주었다. 그러고는 다시 그의 곁 제자리에 가만히 앉아, 일감 위로 다시 몸을 굽혔다.\n\n그늘은 해를 따라 움직였으나, 그녀는 그를 시중들 때 외에는 그의 곁을 결코 떠나지 않았다. 해가 졌어도 그녀는 여전히 거기 있었다. 그녀는 이제 일감을 마쳤고, 마지막으로 그를 돌본 이래 그의 의자 팔걸이에서 머뭇거리던 그녀의 손은 아직도 거기서 망설이고 있었다. 그가 그 위에 자기 손을 얹자, 그 손이 떨리는 간청으로 그를 그러쥐었다.\n\n\"친애하는 클레넘 씨, 가기 전에 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있어요. 시간마다 미루어 왔지만, 꼭 말씀드려야 해요.\"\n\n\"나도 그렇소, 친애하는 리틀 도릿. 나도 꼭 해야 할 말을 미뤄 왔소.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 말을 막으려는 듯 초조하게 손을 그의 입술 쪽으로 옮겼다. 그러더니 그 손은 떨면서 이전의 자리로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"저는 다시 외국으로 가지 않아요. 오빠는 가지만, 저는 가지 않아요. 오빠는 늘 제게 정이 깊었고, 지금은 제게 너무도 고마워해요—너무도 과하게 고마워하지요, 그저 제가 우연히 오빠의 병중에 곁에 있었던 것뿐인데도요—그래서 오빠는 제가 가장 좋아하는 곳에 머물고, 가장 좋아하는 일을 할 자유가 있다고 말해요. 그저 제가 행복하기만을 바란다고 해요.\"\n\n하늘에 환한 별 하나가 빛나고 있었다. 그녀는 말하면서 그것을 올려다보았는데, 마치 그것이 자기 머리 위에서 빛나는, 그녀 자신의 마음속 간절한 뜻인 듯했다.\n\n\"오빠가 제 사랑하는 아버지의 유언장을 찾아 그 재산을 차지하려고 고향에 돌아왔다는 걸, 제가 말씀드리지 않아도 당신은 아마 아실 거예요. 오빠는 말하길, 유언장이 있다면 제게 분명 많은 재산이 남겨졌을 테고, 없다면 자기가 저를 그렇게 만들어 주겠다고 해요.\"\n\n그가 말하려 했으나, 그녀가 다시 떨리는 손을 들어 올리자 그는 멈추었다.\n\n\"저는 돈이 필요 없고, 돈을 바라지도 않아요. 그것은 당신을 위해서가 아니면 제게 아무 가치도 없어요. 당신이 여기 계신데 제가 부유할 수는 없어요. 당신이 곤경에 처해 있는데, 저는 늘 가난한 것보다 훨씬 더 비참할 수밖에 없어요. 제가 가진 모든 것을 당신께 빌려드리게 해 주시겠어요? 그것을 당신께 드리게 해 주시겠어요? 이곳이 제 집이었을 때 당신이 저를 보호해 주신 일을 제가 결코 잊지 않았으며, 결코 잊을 수 없다는 것을 보여드리게 해 주시겠어요? 친애하는 클레넘 씨, '그래'라고 말씀하셔서 저를 온 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람으로 만들어 주시겠어요? 오늘 밤엔 아무 말씀도 하지 않으시고, 당신이 그것을 너그러이 생각해 주시리라는 희망을 안고 제가 떠나게 해 주셔서, 당신을 여기 두고 떠나는 제가 누릴 수 있는 한 가장 행복하게 만들어 주세요. 그리고 저를 위해서—당신을 위해서가 아니라, 저를 위해서, 그 누구도 아닌 오직 저만을 위해서!—제가 이 세상에서 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨을, 곧 제가 당신께 보탬이 되었다는, 그리고 제 애정과 감사의 그 큰 빚을 조금이나마 갚았다는 것을 아는 기쁨을 제게 주세요. 제가 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 수가 없어요. 제가 그토록 오래 살았던 이곳에서 당신을 찾아뵐 수도 없고, 제가 그토록 많은 것을 보았던 이곳에서 당신을 생각하면서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 차분하고 다독여드릴 수가 없어요. 제 눈물이 자꾸 흘러나오네요. 도저히 참을 수가 없어요. 하지만 부디, 부디, 부디, 지금 당신의 괴로움 속에서 당신의 리틀 도릿에게서 등 돌리지 마세요! 부디, 부디, 부디, 슬퍼하는 제 온 마음으로 당신께 빌고 애원해요, 나의 벗이여—나의 그리운 이여!—제가 가진 모든 것을 받으셔서, 그것을 제게 축복으로 만들어 주세요!\"\n\n별은 지금껏 그녀의 얼굴을 비추고 있었는데, 이제 그녀의 얼굴이 그의 손과 자신의 손 위로 떨궈졌다.\n\n그가 두 팔로 감싸안아 그녀를 일으키고 부드럽게 대답했을 때에는 더 어두워져 있었다.\n\n\"안 되오, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿. 안 되오, 내 아이여. 나는 그런 희생에 대해 들을 수 없소. 자유와 희망이 그토록 비싼 값으로 산 것이라면 너무나 귀해서, 나는 결코 그 무게를 견디지 못할 것이고, 그것을 지녔다는 책망을 결코 감당하지 못할 것이오. 그러나 내가 이 말을 얼마나 뜨거운 감사와 사랑으로 하는지는, 하늘을 증인으로 부를 수 있소!\"\n\n\"그런데도 당신은 당신의 괴로움 속에서 제가 당신께 충실하도록 두지 않으시겠다는 건가요?\"\n\n\"이렇게 말하구려, 가장 사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 그래도 나는 당신에게 충실하려 애쓰겠소. 만약, 이곳이 당신의 집이고 이 옷이 당신의 옷이던 지난날에, 내가 나 자신을(나는 오직 나 자신에 대해서만 말하는 거요) 더 잘 이해하고, 내 가슴속 비밀을 더 또렷이 읽어 냈더라면. 만약 나의 조심성과 자기 불신을 뚫고서, 이제 그것이 멀리 지나가 버려 나의 약한 발걸음이 결코 따라잡을 수 없게 된 지금에야 환히 보이는 그 빛을 내가 그때 알아보았더라면. 만약 내가 그때 깨닫고, 당신을 사랑하고 존경한다고—내가 부르곤 했던 그 가엾은 아이로서가 아니라, 그 진실한 손이 나를 나 자신보다 훨씬 높이 끌어올려 나를 훨씬 더 행복하고 나은 사람으로 만들어 줄 한 여인으로서—당신에게 말했더라면. 만약 내가 그 다시 돌이킬 수 없는 기회를 그렇게 썼더라면—내가 그랬더라면 얼마나 좋았을까, 오 그랬더라면 좋았을 것을!—그리고 만약 내가 그런대로 형편이 나았고 당신이 가난했던 그때에 무언가가 우리를 갈라놓았더라면. 나는 당신 재산을 내놓는 그 고귀한 제안을, 가장 사랑하는 이여, 이런 말이 아닌 다른 말로 맞이했을지도 모르오. 그러고도 그것에 손을 대기를 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉혔을 거요. 그러나 지금 이대로는, 나는 결코 그것에 손을 대서는 안 되오, 결코!\"\n\n그녀는 어떤 말로도 할 수 있었을 것보다 더 애처롭고 간절하게, 그 작은 애원하는 손으로 그에게 빌었다.\n\n\"나는 이미 충분히 치욕을 당했소, 나의 리틀 도릿. 나는 그토록 낮게 떨어져서, 당신을—그토록 그립고, 그토록 너그럽고, 그토록 선한 당신을—나와 함께 끌어내려서는 안 되오. 하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를, 하느님이 당신에게 보답하시기를! 다 지난 일이오.\"\n\n그는 마치 그녀가 자기 딸이라도 되는 양 그녀를 두 팔로 안았다.\n\n\"나는 늘 훨씬 더 나이가 많고, 훨씬 더 거칠고, 훨씬 덜 자격 없는 사람이오. 내가 한때 어떤 사람이었든 그조차도 우리 둘 다 떨쳐 버려야 하고, 당신은 지금의 나만을 보아야 하오. 나는 이 작별의 입맞춤을 당신의 뺨에 남기오, 내 아이여—내게 더 가까운 사람이 될 수도 있었던, 그러나 이보다 더 그리운 사람일 수는 없었던—당신에게서 멀리 떨어진, 영원히 당신과 갈라진, 그 인생행로가 이미 다한 한 망가진 사내가 말이오. 당신의 행로는 이제 막 시작될 뿐인데. 나는 내 굴욕 속에서 당신에게 잊어 달라고 청할 용기는 없소. 그러나 오직 지금의 나로서만 기억되기를 청하오.\"\n\n종이 울리기 시작하여, 방문객들에게 떠나라고 알렸다. 그는 벽에서 그녀의 망토를 가져와 다정하게 그녀를 감싸 주었다.\n\n\"한 마디만 더 하겠소, 나의 리틀 도릿. 내게는 힘겨운 말이지만, 꼭 해야 하는 말이오. 당신과 이 감옥이 무언가 공통점을 가졌던 시절은 오래전에 지나갔소. 알아듣겠소?\"\n\n\"오! 당신은 결코 제게,\" 그녀가 비통하게 울며, 맞잡은 두 손을 들어 올려 애원하며 외쳤다. \"다시는 돌아오지 말라고 말씀하지 않으시겠죠! 설마 저를 그렇게 저버리지는 않으시겠죠!\"\n\n\"할 수만 있다면 그렇게 말하고 싶소. 그러나 나는 이 그리운 얼굴을 아주 닫아 버리고, 그것이 다시 돌아오리라는 모든 희망을 단념할 용기가 도무지 없소. 그러나 곧 오지는 마오, 자주 오지는 마오! 이곳은 이제 오염된 곳이고, 나는 그 오염이 내게 달라붙어 있음을 잘 알고 있소. 당신은 훨씬 더 밝고 나은 무대에 속한 사람이오. 당신은 여기를 돌아보아서는 안 되오, 나의 리틀 도릿. 당신은 아주 다르고 훨씬 더 행복한 길들을 향해 눈을 돌려야 하오. 거듭, 그 길들 위에서 하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를! 하느님이 당신에게 보답하시기를!\"\n\n몹시 기운이 풀죽어 있던 매기가 여기서 외쳤다. \"오, 저분을 병원에 넣어 드려요. 어머니, 저분을 병원에 꼭 넣어 드려요! 병원에 들어가지 못하면 저분은 다시는 본래 모습으로 돌아오지 못할 거예요. 그러면 늘 물레질을 하던 그 작은 여인이 공주님이랑 같이 찬장으로 가서, 닭을 거기 왜 두고 있느냐고 물으면, 그걸 꺼내서 저분에게 줄 수 있고, 그러면 다들 행복해질 거예요!\"\n\n그 방해는 때마침 알맞았으니, 종이 거의 다 울려 끝나 가고 있었던 것이다. 다시 다정하게 그녀의 망토를 감싸 주고, 그녀를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채(비록 그녀의 방문이 없었더라면 그는 거의 걸을 수도 없을 만큼 약했지만), 아서는 리틀 도릿을 데리고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그녀는 수위실을 빠져나가는 마지막 방문객이었고, 문이 그녀의 등 뒤에서 묵직하고 가망 없게 삐걱거리며 닫혔다.\n\n그 문이 아서의 가슴속으로 울려 보낸 장례식 같은 쇳소리와 더불어, 그의 쇠약한 느낌이 되돌아왔다. 자기 방으로 올라가는 길은 고된 여정이었고, 그는 형언할 수 없는 비참함 속에서 그 어둡고 외로운 영역으로 다시 들어섰다.\n\n거의 자정이 되어, 감옥이 오래도록 조용해졌을 때, 조심스러운 삐걱 소리가 계단을 올라왔고, 그의 문에 조심스럽게 열쇠를 두드리는 소리가 났다. 젊은 존이었다. 그는 양말 바람으로 미끄러지듯 들어와, 문을 닫아 잡은 채 속삭이며 말했다.\n\n\"규칙에 다 어긋나는 일이지만, 저는 개의치 않아요. 꼭 들어와서, 당신께 오기로 마음먹었거든요.\"\n\n\"무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 없어요, 선생님. 도릿 양이 나오실 때 안마당에서 기다리고 있었어요. 그분이 무사한지 누군가 지켜보길 당신이 바라실 것 같아서요.\"\n\n\"고맙네, 고마워! 그녀를 집까지 데려다주었나, 존?\"\n\n\"호텔까지 모셨어요. 도릿 씨가 묵으셨던 바로 그 호텔이요. 도릿 양은 내내 걸으셨고, 어찌나 다정하게 제게 말씀하시던지, 저는 완전히 무너져 내렸어요. 그분이 왜 마차를 타지 않고 걸으셨다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"모르겠네, 존.\"\n\n\"당신에 관해 이야기하시려고요. 그분이 제게 말씀하셨어요. '존, 너는 늘 명예로운 사람이었으니, 내가 곁에 없을 때 그분을 보살피고, 그분이 도움과 위안이 부족하지 않도록 하겠다고 내게 약속해 준다면, 그만큼은 내 마음이 놓일 거야.' 저는 그분께 약속했어요. 그리고 저는 당신 곁을 지킬 거예요,\" 존 치버리가 말했다. \"영원히요!\"\n\n몹시 감동한 클레넘은 이 정직한 영혼에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"그 손을 잡기 전에,\" 문에서 떨어지지 않은 채 그 손을 바라보며 존이 말했다. \"도릿 양이 제게 무슨 전갈을 주셨는지 맞혀 보세요.\"\n\n클레넘은 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"'그분께 전해 줘,'\" 또렷하지만 떨리는 목소리로 존이 되풀이했다. \"'그분의 리틀 도릿이 그분께 변치 않는 사랑을 보낸다고.' 자, 이제 전해 드렸어요. 제가 명예로웠나요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"아주, 아주 그랬네!\"\n\n\"도릿 양께 제가 명예로웠다고 말씀해 주시겠어요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"꼭 그러겠네.\"\n\n\"여기 제 손이 있어요, 선생님,\" 존이 말했다. \"그리고 저는 영원히 당신 곁을 지킬게요!\"\n\n진심 어린 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그는 똑같이 조심스러운 삐걱 소리를 내며 계단으로 사라졌고, 신발 없이 마당 포석 위를 살금살금 건너가, 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고는, 신발을 벗어 둔 앞쪽으로 나갔다. 만약 같은 길이 불타는 쟁기날로 깔려 있었다 해도, 존은 같은 목적을 위해 같은 헌신으로 그 길을 건넜으리라는 것이 전혀 있음 직하지 않은 일은 아니다.\n\n# CHAPTER 30. Closing in\n\n마셜시 정문의 쇠창살에 정해진 한 주의 마지막 날이 닿았다. 리틀 도릿을 안에 가둔 채 철문이 쾅 닫힌 그날부터 밤새 까맣던 그 쇠줄무늬가, 일찍 떠오른 햇살에 황금빛 줄무늬로 바뀌어 있었다. 도시를 가로질러 비스듬히, 뒤죽박죽 얽힌 지붕들 위로, 교회 첨탑들의 뚫린 격자무늬 사이로, 길고 환한 빛줄기가 내리꽂혔다 — 이 하계(下界)라는 감옥의 창살들이.\n\n하루 내내 정문 안쪽의 그 낡은 집은 어떤 방문객의 발길에도 흔들리지 않은 채 남아 있었다. 그러나 해가 낮게 기울었을 때, 세 사내가 정문으로 꺾어 들어와 그 허물어져 가는 집으로 향했다.\n\n리고가 맨 앞이었고, 혼자서 담배를 피우며 걸었다. 바티스트 씨가 둘째로, 다른 데는 눈길도 주지 않은 채 그의 바로 뒤를 종종거리며 따라왔다. 팽크스 씨가 셋째였는데, 날이 몹시 더운지라 제멋대로 뻗치는 머리카락을 풀어 주려고 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다. 그들은 다 함께 문간 계단에 이르렀다.\n\n\"이 미치광이 한 쌍 같으니!\" 리고가 홱 돌아서며 말했다. \"아직 가지 말게!\"\n\n\"그럴 생각 없소.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그 대답에 대한 답으로 음험한 눈길을 한 번 던지고는, 리고가 문을 요란하게 두드렸다. 그는 제 놀음을 끝까지 펼쳐 보이기 위해 술을 잔뜩 채워 둔 터라, 어서 시작하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다. 길게 울려 퍼지는 노크 한 번을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그는 다시 문고리로 돌아가 또 한 번을 시작했다. 그것이 채 끝나기도 전에 제리마이아 플린트윈치가 문을 열었고, 그들 모두 절그럭거리며 돌바닥 현관으로 들어섰다. 리고는 플린트윈치 씨를 옆으로 밀치며 곧장 위층으로 올라갔다. 그의 두 수행원이 그를 따랐고, 플린트윈치 씨가 그들을 따랐으며, 그들 모두 우르르 클레넘 부인의 고요한 방으로 몰려 들어갔다. 방은 평소 그대로였다. 다만 창문 하나가 활짝 열려 있고, 애프리가 그 구식 창가 자리에 앉아 양말을 깁고 있는 것만 달랐다. 작은 탁자 위에는 늘 놓이던 물건들이 있었고, 화로 안에는 늘 그렇듯 죽어 가는 불이 있었으며, 침대에는 늘 덮여 있던 천이 덮여 있었다. 그리고 이 모든 것의 안주인은 검은 상여 같은 소파에 앉아, 형 집행인의 단두대 받침처럼 생긴 검고 각진 등받이 베개에 몸을 기대고 있었다.\n\n그럼에도 방 안에는 무언가 채비를 갖춘 듯한, 어떤 사태를 위해 팽팽히 당겨진 듯한 이름 없는 기운이 감돌았다. 방의 그 자잘한 물건 하나하나가 수년간 차지해 온 제자리에 그대로 박혀 있었으므로, 그 기운이 어디서 비롯되는지는, 안주인을 주의 깊게 살피지 않고서는, 그것도 그녀의 얼굴을 미리 알고 있지 않고서는 아무도 말할 수 없었을 것이다. 변함없는 검은 옷이 주름 하나하나까지 예전 그대로였고, 변함없는 자세도 빳빳하게 유지되고 있었지만, 그녀의 이목구비에 아주 미미하게 더해진 굳음과 음울한 이마의 수축이 어찌나 강렬하게 새겨져 있던지, 그것이 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것에 새겨져 있었다.\n\n\"이 사람들은 누구죠?\" 두 수행원이 들어서자 그녀가 의아한 듯 말했다. \"이 사람들이 여기서 뭘 원하는 거예요?\"\n\n\"이 사람들이 누구냐, 그 말씀이신지요, 친애하는 부인?\" 리고가 받아쳤다. \"맹세코, 이들은 죄수가 된 부인의 아드님 친구들입니다. 그리고 이들이 여기서 뭘 원하느냐, 그 말씀이시지요? 원, 부인, 저야 모르지요. 직접 물어보시는 게 좋겠습니다.\"\n\n\"문간에서 아직 가지 말라고 한 건 당신이잖소.\" 팽크스가 말했다.\n\n\"문간에서 갈 생각 없다고 한 건 당신이잖나.\" 리고가 되받았다. \"한마디로, 부인, 죄수의 첩자 두 명을 소개하게 해 주십시오 — 미치광이들이긴 하나, 첩자들입니다. 우리의 짧은 대화가 진행되는 동안 이들이 여기 남아 있기를 바라신다면, 한마디만 하십시오. 저로서는 아무래도 좋습니다.\"\n\n\"내가 왜 이들이 여기 남아 있기를 바라겠어요?\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"내가 이들과 무슨 상관이 있어서?\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 더없이 친애하는 부인,\" 리고가 안락의자에 어찌나 묵직하게 몸을 던지던지 낡은 방이 흔들렸다, \"이들을 내보내시는 게 좋겠습니다. 부인의 일이지요. 이들은 제 첩자도, 제 부하들도 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"이봐요! 거기 팽크스,\" 클레넘 부인이 그에게 화난 듯 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다, \"거기 캐스비 사무원! 당신 고용주의 일과 당신 자신의 일이나 챙겨요. 가요. 그리고 저 다른 사람도 데려가요.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 받아쳤다, \"우리 둘 다 물러가는 데 아무 이의 없음을 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다. 우리는 클레넘 씨를 위해 떠맡은 일을 다 했습니다. 그분의 한결같은 근심은(죄수가 된 뒤로 더 심해졌지요), 이 유쾌하신 신사분이 슬그머니 빠져나간 바로 이 자리로 다시 끌려오는 것이었습니다. 자, 여기 있군요 — 끌려왔습니다. 그리고 한마디 하겠는데,\" 팽크스 씨가 덧붙였다, \"그 흉한 낯짝에다 대고 말하건대, 내 생각으론 그가 이 세상에서 아주 슬그머니 빠져나간다 해도 세상은 조금도 나빠지지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"당신 생각은 묻지 않았어요.\" 클레넘 부인이 대답했다. \"가요.\"\n\n\"더 나은 사람들 곁에 부인을 두고 가지 못해 죄송합니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그리고 클레넘 씨가 함께하지 못해 또한 죄송하고요. 그건 제 잘못입니다, 그건요.\"\n\n\"그 사람 자신의 잘못이겠죠.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다.\n\n\"아니요, 제 잘못입니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그분을 파멸을 부르는 투자로 이끈 게 제 불운이었으니까요.\" (팽크스 씨는 여전히 그 말에 매달렸고, 결코 투기라고는 말하지 않았다.) \"물론 숫자로 증명할 수 있습니다만,\" 팽크스 씨가 근심스러운 낯빛으로 덧붙였다, \"그건 마땅히 좋은 투자가 됐어야 했습니다. 실패한 이래로 제 평생 하루도 빠짐없이 다시 검토해 봤는데, 결과는 — 숫자의 문제로 보자면 — 의기양양하게 나옵니다. 지금은 그 숫자를 따져 볼 때도 자리도 아닙니다만,\" 팽크스 씨는 자기 계산을 넣어 두는 모자 속을 그리운 눈길로 흘긋 보며 말을 이었다, \"숫자에는 이론의 여지가 없습니다. 클레넘 씨는 지금 이 순간 자기 마차에 쌍두마를 매고 있어야 마땅하고, 저는 삼천에서 오천 파운드의 재산을 가지고 있어야 마땅합니다.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 그 금액이 제 주머니에 있었더라도 더할 나위 없었을 만큼의 자신감 어린 전반적인 태도로 머리카락을 곧추세웠다. 이 반박할 수 없는 숫자들은 돈을 잃은 이래로 그의 모든 한가한 순간을 차지해 온 일거리였고, 그의 여생이 다할 때까지 그에게 위안을 안겨 줄 운명이었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다, \"그 얘긴 이쯤 하지요. 알트로, 친구, 자네도 그 숫자를 봤으니 결과가 어떻게 나오는지 알 거야.\" 이런 식으로 자기를 위로할 산술적 능력이라곤 조금도 없는 바티스트 씨가, 하얀 이를 멋지게 드러내 보이며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n플린트윈치 씨가 줄곧 그를 바라보고 있다가, 그제야 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"오! 자네였군, 그렇지? 자네 얼굴을 기억하는 것 같긴 했는데, 자네 이를 보기 전까진 확신할 수 없었어. 아! 그래, 그렇고말고. 바로 이 주제넘은 망명자가,\" 제리마이아가 클레넘 부인에게 말했다, \"아서와 그 수다쟁이가 여기 있던 날 밤 문을 두드리고는, 블랑두아 씨에 관해 교리문답 한 통만큼이나 많은 질문을 내게 퍼부었지요.\"\n\n\"맞습니다.\" 바티스트 씨가 쾌활하게 인정했다. \"그리고 보십시오, 주인님! 제가 그 결과로(consequentementally) 그를 찾아냈습니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 그 결과로 목이 부러졌더라도,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 받아쳤다, \"나는 반대하지 않았을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그럼 이제,\" 그동안 그의 눈길이 자주 창가 자리와 거기서 깁고 있는 양말로 슬그머니 흘러갔던 팽크스 씨가 말했다, \"가기 전에 딱 한마디만 더 하겠습니다. 클레넘 씨가 여기 계셨다면 — 하지만 안타깝게도, 이 훌륭하신 신사를 그 의사에 반하여 이곳으로 되돌려 보낼 만큼은 이겼다 해도, 그분은 병들어 감옥에 갇혀 있지요 — 병들어 감옥에, 가엾은 분이 — 그분이 여기 계셨다면,\" 팽크스 씨가 창가 자리 쪽으로 한 걸음 옮겨 오른손을 양말 위에 얹으며 말했다, \"이렇게 말씀하셨을 겁니다. '애프리, 당신의 꿈을 이야기해요!'\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 으스스하게 경고하는 분위기로 오른쪽 집게손가락을 코와 양말 사이에 치켜들고는, 돌아서서 칙칙폭폭 나가며 바티스트 씨를 뒤로 끌고 갔다. 집 대문이 그들 뒤로 닫히는 소리가 들렸고, 메아리치는 안마당의 둔탁한 포석 위로 그들의 발소리가 지나가는 게 들렸지만, 여전히 아무도 한마디도 보태지 않았다. 클레넘 부인과 제리마이아는 눈빛을 주고받았다. 그러고는 양말을 부지런히 깁고 앉아 있는 애프리를 바라보았고, 또 바라보았다.\n\n\"자!\" 마침내 플린트윈치 씨가 창가 자리 쪽으로 몸을 한두 번 비틀어 돌리며, 무언가를 할 채비라도 하는 듯 두 손바닥을 자기 외투 자락에 문지르며 말했다. \"우리 사이에서 해야 할 말이 무엇이든, 더 이상 시간 낭비 말고 시작하는 게 좋겠어. — 그러니, 애프리, 여보, 자네는 물러가게!\"\n\n순식간에 애프리는 양말을 내던지고 벌떡 일어나, 오른손으로 창턱을 붙잡고, 오른쪽 무릎을 창가 자리에 얹은 채, 예상되는 공격자들을 물리치듯 왼손을 휘둘러 댔다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 가요, 제리마이아 — 안 가, 안 가요 — 안 가요! 안 갈 거예요! 여기 있을 거예요. 내가 모르는 건 다 듣고, 내가 아는 건 다 말할 거예요. 그러고 말겠어요, 마침내, 그러다 죽는 한이 있어도. 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요!\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 분노와 경악으로 뻣뻣하게 굳은 채, 한 손가락들을 입술에 적셔, 다른 손바닥에 살그머니 동그라미를 그리고는, 위협적인 히죽임을 지으며 계속해서 아내 쪽으로 몸을 비틀어 다가갔다. 다가가면서 무언가 한마디를 헐떡이며 내뱉었는데, 목이 막힐 듯한 분노 속에서 알아들을 수 있는 것이라곤 \"이런 한 방을!\" 하는 말뿐이었다.\n\n\"한 발짝도 더 가까이 오지 마요, 제리마이아!\" 애프리가 허공을 향한 손짓을 한순간도 멈추지 않으며 외쳤다. \"나한테 한 발짝도 더 가까이 오면, 동네방네 사람들을 불러낼 거예요! 창밖으로 몸을 던질 거예요. 불이야, 사람 죽인다, 소리칠 거예요! 죽은 사람도 깨울 거예요! 거기 그대로 있어요, 안 그러면 죽은 사람도 깨울 만큼 비명을 질러 댈 테니까!\"\n\n클레넘 부인의 단호한 목소리가 \"멈춰요!\" 하고 울렸다. 제리마이아는 이미 멈춰 있었다.\n\n\"좁혀 들어오고 있어요, 플린트윈치. 저 사람은 내버려 둬요. 애프리, 이 여러 해가 지난 뒤에 자네가 나한테 등을 돌리는 건가?\"\n\n\"그래요, 내가 모르는 걸 듣고 내가 아는 걸 말하는 게 마님께 등을 돌리는 거라면요. 난 이제 터뜨려 버렸고, 돌이킬 수 없어요. 그러기로 마음먹었어요. 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요! 그게 마님께 등을 돌리는 거라면, 그래요, 난 영리한 두 분 다한테 등을 돌릴 거예요. 아서 도련님이 처음 집에 돌아왔을 때 두 분께 맞서라고 일러 줬어요. 내가 목숨이 두려워 벌벌 떤다고 해서 도련님까지 그래야 할 이유는 없다고 일러 줬다고요. 그때 이후로 온갖 일이 벌어졌고, 난 더 이상 제리마이아한테 휘둘리지도, 정신이 멍해지고 겁먹지도, 내가 알지도 못하는 일에 한패가 되지도 않을 거예요. 안 그래요, 안 그래, 안 그럴 거예요! 아서 도련님이 아무것도 남지 않고, 병들고, 감옥에 갇혀, 스스로 일어설 수 없을 때, 내가 도련님을 위해 일어설 거예요. 그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요!\"\n\n\"네가 어떻게 아니, 이 뒤죽박죽 덩어리야,\" 클레넘 부인이 엄하게 물었다, \"지금 네가 하는 짓이 아서를 돕기라도 하는 거라고?\"\n\n\"전 뭐든 제대로 아는 게 하나도 없어요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"그리고 마님이 평생 단 한 번이라도 옳은 말을 하셨다면, 그건 절 뒤죽박죽 덩어리라고 부르실 때예요. 왜냐하면 영리한 두 분이 절 그렇게 만들려고 갖은 짓을 다 하셨으니까요. 제가 좋든 싫든 절 결혼시키셨고, 그 이후로 줄곧 꿈과 겁박으로 점철된, 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 그런 삶을 살게 하셨는데, 제가 뒤죽박죽 덩어리 말고 뭐가 되길 바라셨겠어요? 절 그렇게 만들고 싶어 하셨고, 전 그렇게 됐어요. 하지만 더는 굴복하지 않을 거예요. 안 해요, 안 해, 안 해, 안 할 거예요!\" 그녀는 여전히 다가오는 누구든 막으려 허공에 손을 휘둘러 댔다.\n\n말없이 그녀를 응시하던 클레넘 부인이 리고에게로 돌아섰다. \"이 어리석은 것이 보이고 들리시지요. 저런 정신 나간 것이 저 자리에 그대로 있는 게 거슬리세요?\"\n\n\"제가, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다, \"거슬리느냐고요? 그건 부인께 드릴 질문이지요.\"\n\n\"난 안 거슬려요.\" 그녀가 음울하게 말했다. \"이제 고를 여지가 거의 없으니까요. 플린트윈치, 좁혀 들어오고 있어요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 아내에게 시뻘건 복수의 눈길을 던지는 것으로 대답하고는, 마치 그녀에게 달려들지 못하도록 제 몸을 붙들어 매려는 듯, 팔짱 낀 두 팔을 조끼 가슴팍에 비틀어 넣고, 턱을 한쪽 팔꿈치에 바싹 댄 채 한쪽 구석에 서서, 더없이 괴상한 자세로 리고를 지켜보았다. 리고는 리고대로 의자에서 일어나 다리를 늘어뜨린 채 탁자 위에 걸터앉았다. 이 편안한 자세로, 그는 콧수염을 치켜올리고 코를 내리깐 채 클레넘 부인의 굳은 얼굴을 마주 보았다.\n\n\"부인, 저는 신사입니다 —\"\n\n\"그에 관해서라면,\" 그녀가 흔들림 없는 어조로 말을 끊었다, \"프랑스 감옥과 살인 혐의에 관련하여 헐뜯는 말을 들은 바 있어요.\"\n\n그는 과장된 정중함으로 그녀에게 손에 입을 맞추는 시늉을 했다.\n\n\"완벽하십니다. 정확합니다. 게다가 한 부인을요! 얼마나 터무니없는지! 얼마나 믿기 힘든지! 그때 저는 큰 성공을 거두는 영광을 누렸습니다. 지금도 큰 성공을 거두는 영광을 누리기를 바랍니다. 손에 입맞춤을 드립니다. 부인, 저는 신사입니다(이렇게 말씀드리려던 참이었지요). '이번 자리에서 이러저러한 일을 반드시 끝내겠다'고 말하면, 반드시 끝내는 신사 말입니다. 우리의 자그마한 용무에 관해 우리가 마지막 자리에 이르렀음을 선언합니다. 잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시겠지요?\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 그에게 고정한 채 눈살을 찌푸리고 있었다. \"그래요.\"\n\n\"또한, 저는 한낱 돈벌이를 위한 거래 따위는 모르는 신사입니다만, 제 쾌락을 좇는 수단으로서의 돈은 언제나 환영하는 신사입니다. 잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시지요?\"\n\n\"물어볼 필요도 거의 없겠다고 할 만하군요. 그래요.\"\n\n\"또한, 저는 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 다정한 기질의 신사입니다만, 농락당하면 격분하는 신사이기도 합니다. 고귀한 천성을 지닌 자는 그런 상황에서 격분하는 법이지요. 저는 고귀한 천성을 지녔습니다. 사자가 깨어나면 — 즉, 제가 격분하면 — 제 적의를 채우는 것이 저에겐 돈만큼이나 환영할 만한 일입니다. 늘 잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시지요?\"\n\n\"그래요.\" 그녀가 아까보다 다소 큰 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"제가 부인을 어지럽히게 하지 마십시오. 부디 평온하시길. 우리가 이제 마지막 자리에 이르렀다고 제가 말씀드렸지요. 우리가 가졌던 두 차례의 자리를 되짚어 보게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그럴 필요 없어요.\"\n\n\"원, 부인,\" 그가 불쑥 내뱉었다, \"그게 제 취향입니다! 게다가, 그래야 길이 트입니다. 첫 번째 자리는 제한적이었습니다. 저는 부인과 알게 되는 영광을 — 제 소개장을 내미는 영광을 누렸지요. 저는 협잡의 기사입니다, 부인, 분부대로 하겠습니다만, 제 세련된 매너 덕분에 어학 선생으로서 큰 성공을 거두었더랬습니다. 서로에게는 제 풀 먹인 옷깃만큼이나 빳빳하면서도, 세련된 매너를 갖춘 외국 신사에게는 기꺼이 풀어지는 부인의 동포들 사이에서 말이지요 — 그리고 이 명망 높은 댁에 관한 한두 가지 자그마한 것들을 살피는 영광도요,\" 그는 방을 둘러보며 미소 지었다, \"그것들을 알아야 제가 찾던 부인과 알게 되는 출중한 기쁨을 누렸음을 확신하고 납득할 수 있었으니까요. 저는 이를 이루었습니다. 우리의 친애하는 플린트윈치에게 다시 돌아오겠노라 명예를 걸고 약속드렸지요. 저는 우아하게 떠났습니다.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 수긍하지도 이의를 달지도 않았다. 그가 말을 멈출 때나 말할 때나 한결같이, 그 얼굴은 아직껏 그에게 늘 그 하나의 골똘한 찌푸림과, 앞서 언급한바 이 사태를 위해 단단히 마음먹은 그녀의 어두운 표정을 내보였다.\n\n\"우아하게 떠났다고 말씀드리는 것은, 부인을 놀래지 않고 물러가는 것이 우아한 일이었기 때문입니다. 육체적으로뿐 아니라 도덕적으로도 우아한 것, 그것이 리고 블랑두아라는 인물의 됨됨이의 일부입니다. 그것은 또한 영리한 처사였습니다. 부인께 무언가가 머리 위에 드리워진 채로 두어, 정해지지 않은 어느 날 약간의 불안 속에서 저를 다시 기다리시게 했으니까요. 그러나 부인의 종은 영리합니다. 맹세코, 부인, 영리하지요! 다시 이어 갑시다. 정해지지 않은 그날, 저는 다시 부인 댁에 저 자신을 들이는 영광을 누렸습니다. 저는 팔 것이 있는데, 사지 않으시면 제가 높이 존경하는 부인을 곤란하게 할 것이라고 넌지시 비쳤지요. 대략적으로 제 뜻을 밝혔습니다. 저는 요구했습니다 — 천 파운드였던 것 같습니다. 바로잡아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그녀는 마지못해 대답했다. \"당신은 천 파운드나 되는 돈을 요구했어요.\"\n\n\"지금은 이천을 요구합니다. 지체의 폐해가 그런 것이지요. 그러나 다시 한 번 이어 갑시다. 우리는 합의에 이르지 못했습니다. 그때 우리는 의견이 갈렸지요. 저는 장난기가 있습니다. 장난기는 제 사랑스러운 됨됨이의 일부니까요. 장난삼아, 저는 살해되어 숨겨진 사람처럼 되었습니다. 제 우스꽝스러운 착상이 불러일으키는 의심에서 벗어나는 것만으로도 부인께는 그 금액의 절반은 족히 값어치가 있을 테니 말이지요. 우연과 첩자들이 제 장난기에 끼어들어, 어쩌면 — 누가 알겠습니까? 부인과 플린트윈치만 알겠지요 — 막 익었을 때 그 열매를 망쳐 버립니다. 그리하여, 부인, 저는 마지막으로 여기 와 있습니다. 들으십시오! 분명코 마지막입니다.\"\n\n그가 제멋대로 뻗은 부츠 뒤축을 탁자의 늘어진 덮개에 부딪치며, 그녀의 찌푸림을 거만한 응시로 맞받는 동안, 그는 어조를 사나운 쪽으로 바꾸기 시작했다.\n\n\"바! 잠깐 멈추시지요! 단계를 밟아 나갑시다. 여기 계약에 따라 지불되어야 할 제 호텔 청구서가 있습니다. 오 분 뒤면 우리는 서로 칼끝을 겨누게 될지도 모르지요. 그때까지 이걸 미루지 않겠습니다, 안 그러면 부인이 저를 속일 테니까요. 지불하십시오! 제게 돈을 세어 주십시오!\"\n\n\"그 손에서 받아 지불해 줘요, 플린트윈치.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다.\n\n노인이 그것을 받으러 다가오자 그는 그것을 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에 휙 내던지고는, 손을 내밀며 시끄럽게 되풀이했다. \"지불하시오! 세어 내놓으시오! 좋은 돈으로!\" 제리마이아는 청구서를 집어 들고 충혈된 눈으로 총액을 보고는, 주머니에서 작은 천 주머니를 꺼내 그 액수를 그의 손에 세어 주었다.\n\n리고는 돈을 짤랑거리고, 손으로 무게를 가늠하고, 조금 위로 던졌다 받고, 다시 짤랑거렸다.\n\n\"대담한 리고 블랑두아에게 이 소리는, 호랑이에게 갓 잡은 고기 맛과도 같지요. 자, 그럼, 부인. 얼마입니까?\"\n\n그는 돈을 움켜쥔 묵직한 손으로 위협하는 몸짓을 하며, 마치 그것으로 그녀를 내려치기라도 할 듯 갑자기 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"전에도 말했고 다시 말하지만, 우리는 당신이 짐작하는 만큼 여기서 부유하지 않으며, 당신의 요구는 과도해요. 그럴 마음이 제아무리 크다 한들, 그런 요구에 응할 만한 수단이 지금 내겐 없어요.\"\n\n\"한들이라니!\" 리고가 외쳤다. \"한들이라니, 이 부인의 말 좀 들어 보시오! 마음이 없다고는 말하지 못하시겠지요?\"\n\n\"나는 내게 떠오르는 대로 말하지, 당신에게 떠오르는 대로 말하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"그럼 말하시지요. 그 마음에 관해서. 어서! 마음 얘기로 와 보시오, 그럼 제가 어떻게 할지 압니다.\"\n\n그녀의 대답은 더 빠르지도 더 느리지도 않았다. \"당신이 어떤 서류 — 혹은 여러 서류 — 를 손에 넣은 모양인데, 그건 확실히 내가 되찾고 싶은 마음이 있는 것이군요.\"\n\n리고는 큰 소리로 웃으며 뒤축으로 탁자를 두드리고 돈을 짤랑거렸다. \"그럴 줄 알았지! 그 점은 믿어요!\"\n\n\"그 서류는 내게 어느 정도의 돈만큼 값어치가 있을지도 모르죠. 얼마나 많을지, 얼마나 적을지는 말할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"빌어먹을!\" 그가 사납게 물었다. \"일주일이나 유예를 두고 궁리하고도 그 모양이오?\"\n\n\"그래요! 그 빈약한 수단으로는 — 다시 말하지만 우리는 여기서 가난하지 부유하지 않으니까요 — 그 최악과 최대치를 알지 못하는 위력에 무슨 값도 치르지 않겠어요. 이번이 당신이 넌지시 비추며 위협하는 세 번째예요. 분명하게 말해야 해요, 안 그러면 마음대로 가서 마음대로 해요. 저런 고양이의 변덕에 놀아나는 생쥐가 되느니, 단번에 갈가리 찢기는 편이 나아요.\"\n\n그는 너무 가까이 붙은 그 두 눈으로 그녀를 어찌나 빤히 노려보던지, 한쪽 눈의 불길한 시선이 다른 쪽 시선과 엇갈려, 그 갈고리진 코의 콧대를 비뚤어지게 만드는 듯했다. 한참을 살피더니, 그는 안에서 새어 나오는 미소를 한층 더 도드라지게 하며 말했다.\n\n\"부인은 대담한 여인이군요!\"\n\n\"나는 결심한 여인이에요.\"\n\n\"늘 그러셨지요. 뭐라고요? 부인은 늘 그러셨지요. 그렇지 않나, 나의 작은 플린트윈치?\"\n\n\"플린트윈치, 저 사람한테 아무 말도 하지 말아요. 지금 여기서 그가 할 수 있는 말을 다 하든가, 아니면 여기서 나가서 할 수 있는 일을 다 하든가, 그건 저 사람이 정할 일이에요. 이게 우리의 결정인 걸 당신도 알잖아요. 저 사람이 그에 따라 어떻게 하든 내버려 둬요.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 사악한 곁눈질에 움츠러들지도, 피하지도 않았다. 그가 다시 그 눈길을 그녀에게로 돌렸지만, 그녀는 스스로 정해 놓은 지점에 흔들림 없이 머물러 있었다. 그는 탁자에서 내려와 소파 가까이에 의자를 놓고 거기 앉아, 한쪽 팔을 그녀의 팔에 바싹 붙여 소파에 기대고, 그 손으로 그녀의 팔을 건드렸다. 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 찌푸린 채, 골똘하고, 굳어 있었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 부인, 이 자그마한 집안 모임에서 제가 집안 내력 한 조각을 들려드리는 게 부인의 뜻이군요.\" 리고가 그녀의 팔 위에서 유연한 손가락을 경고하듯 놀리며 말했다. \"저는 제법 의사 노릇도 합니다. 부인의 맥을 좀 짚어 보지요.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 자기 손목을 손에 쥐도록 내버려 두었다. 그것을 쥔 채 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"기이한 결혼, 기이한 어머니, 복수, 그리고 은폐에 관한 내력입니다. — 어허, 어허, 어허? 이 맥박이 묘하게 뛰는군요! 제가 짚고 있는 동안 두 배로 빨라지는 것 같습니다. 이것이 부인의 지병에서 흔히 일어나는 변화입니까?\"\n\n그녀가 자신의 불구가 된 팔을 비틀어 빼내는 동안 거기엔 안간힘이 있었으나, 그녀의 얼굴에는 아무것도 없었다. 그의 얼굴에는 그만의 그 미소가 있었다.\n\n\"저는 모험으로 가득한 삶을 살아왔습니다. 저는 모험적인 인물이지요. 많은 모험가를 알아 왔습니다. 흥미로운 영혼들 — 사랑스러운 무리지요! 그중 한 사람에게서 저는 제 지식과 제 증거를 얻었습니다 — 다시 말하지만, 존경할 만한 부인, 증거를요 — 이제 시작하려는 이 매혹적인 자그마한 집안 내력의 증거 말입니다. 부인도 매료되실 겁니다. 한데, 바! 깜빡했군요. 내력에는 이름을 붙여야지요. 어느 집의 내력이라 부를까요? 한데, 또 바. 집은 너무 많으니까요. 이 집의 내력이라 부를까요?\"\n\n소파 위로 몸을 기울이고, 의자의 두 다리와 자기 왼쪽 팔꿈치로 몸을 떠받친 채, 그 손은 자기 말을 못 박듯 그녀의 팔을 자주 톡톡 치고, 다리는 꼰 채, 오른손은 때로 머리를 매만지고, 때로 콧수염을 쓰다듬고, 때로 코를 치면서, 무엇을 하든 늘 그녀를 위협하며, 거칠고, 거만하고, 탐욕스럽고, 잔인하고, 위세 등등한 그는 느긋하게 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"하면, 그러니까, 저는 이것을 이 집의 내력이라 부르겠습니다. 시작하지요. 여기 어느 삼촌과 조카가 산다고 칩시다. 삼촌은 강인한 성품을 지닌 완고한 노신사이고, 조카는 천성적으로 소심하고, 억눌리고, 제약 아래 있는 자입니다.\"\n\n창가 자리에서 골똘히 주의를 기울인 채, 둘둘 만 앞치마 끝자락을 깨물며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있던 애프리 부인이 여기서 외쳤다. \"제리마이아, 나한테서 떨어져요! 난 내 꿈속에서, 아서 도련님 아버지와 그분 삼촌에 관해 들었어요. 저 사람이 그분들 얘기를 하고 있어요. 그건 내가 여기 오기 전 일이지만, 내 꿈속에서 들었다고요. 아서 도련님 아버지는 가엾고, 우유부단하고, 겁에 질린 사람이라, 젊었을 때 고아 신세인 목숨만 빼고 모든 게 겁박당해 빠져나가 버려서, 아내를 고르는 데조차 한마디 거들 권리도 없이 삼촌이 골라 줬다고요. 저기 그 여자가 앉아 있어요! 난 그걸 꿈속에서 들었고, 당신이 그 여자한테 직접 그렇게 말했어요.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨가 그녀에게 주먹을 흔들고, 클레넘 부인이 그녀를 응시하는 동안, 리고는 그녀에게 손에 입맞춤을 보냈다.\n\n\"완벽하게 옳습니다, 친애하는 플린트윈치 부인. 부인께는 꿈꾸는 재주가 있으시군요.\"\n\n\"당신 칭찬 따위 필요 없어요.\" 애프리가 받아쳤다. \"당신하곤 할 말이라곤 하나도 없어요. 하지만 제리마이아가 그게 꿈이라고 했으니, 난 그걸 꿈으로 말할 거예요!\" 여기서 그녀는 마치 다른 누군가의 입을 — 어쩌면 제리마이아의 입을, 사납게 추위라도 타듯 위협의 말로 덜덜 떨리는 그 입을 — 틀어막기라도 하듯 다시 앞치마를 입에 물었다.\n\n\"우리의 사랑스러운 플린트윈치 부인은,\" 리고가 말했다, \"느닷없이 고운 감수성과 영성을 펼쳐 보이시며, 놀랍도록 정확하십니다. 그렇습니다. 내력은 그렇게 흘러갑니다. 삼촌 되시는 분이 조카에게 결혼하라 명합니다. 삼촌이 실상 그에게 이렇게 말하지요. '조카야, 너에게 나처럼 강인한 성품을 지닌 부인을 소개하마 — 결심이 굳은 부인, 엄격한 부인, 약한 자를 가루로 부숴 버릴 의지를 지닌 부인, 연민도 없고, 사랑도 없고, 가차 없고, 복수심 강하고, 돌처럼 차갑되 불처럼 타오르는 부인을.' 아! 얼마나 굳센지! 아, 얼마나 빼어난 지력의 강인함인지! 참으로, 삼촌 되시는 분의 짐작 속 말로 제가 묘사하는 것은 자랑스럽고 고귀한 성품이지요. 하, 하, 하! 내 영혼이 죽을 노릇이지만, 이 사랑스러운 부인이 마음에 드는군요!\"\n\n클레넘 부인의 얼굴이 달라져 있었다. 거기엔 두드러지게 짙은 빛이 어렸고, 이마는 더 깊이 찌푸려져 있었다. \"부인, 부인,\" 리고가 마치 잔인한 손으로 악기라도 울리듯 그녀의 팔을 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"제가 부인의 흥미를 돋우는 걸 알겠습니다. 제가 부인의 공감을 일깨우는 걸 알겠습니다. 계속해 봅시다.\"\n\n그러나 늘어진 코와 치켜올라간 콧수염은, 그가 계속하기 전에, 잠시 그 하얀 손으로 가려져야 했다. 그는 자기가 만들어 낸 효과를 무척이나 즐기고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"조카는, 그 명민하신 플린트윈치 부인이 말씀하신 대로, 고아 신세인 목숨만 빼고 모든 게 겁박당하고 굶주려 빠져나가 버린 가엾은 자라서, 머리를 조아리며 대답합니다. '삼촌, 분부대로 하겠습니다. 뜻대로 하소서!' 삼촌 되시는 분은 뜻대로 하지요. 그분이 늘 하는 그대로. 상서로운 혼례가 거행되고, 갓 결혼한 부부가 이 매혹적인 저택으로 돌아옵니다. 부인이 맞아들여지지요, 가령, 플린트윈치에게 말입니다. 어이, 늙은 음모꾼?\"\n\n제리마이아는 안주인에게 눈을 둔 채 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 리고는 이쪽저쪽을 보더니, 흉한 코를 치고, 혀를 차 쩝쩝 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"머지않아 부인은 기이하고 충격적인 사실을 발견합니다. 그리하여, 분노로 가득 차고, 질투로 가득 차고, 복수심으로 가득 차서, 그녀는 짜냅니다 — 보십시오, 부인! — 응징의 계략을, 그 무게를, 짓밟힌 자기 남편으로 하여금 직접 짊어지게 하는 동시에 자기 원수에게 집행하도록 교묘하게 강요하지요. 얼마나 빼어난 지력인지!\"\n\n\"떨어져요, 제리마이아!\" 가슴 두근거리는 애프리가 다시 앞치마를 입에서 빼며 외쳤다. \"하지만 그것도 내 꿈 중 하나였어요. 어느 겨울 저녁 땅거미 질 무렵 당신이 그 여자와 다툴 때, 당신이 그 여자한테 말했죠 — 저기 그 여자가 앉아 있고 당신이 그 여자를 보고 있네요 — 아서 도련님이 돌아왔을 때 아버지만을 의심하도록 내버려 둬선 안 됐다고, 힘과 권세는 늘 그 여자한테 있었다고, 아버지를 위해 아서 도련님께 더 맞섰어야 했다고요. 같은 꿈속에서 당신이 그 여자한테, 그 여자가 — 뭐라더라, 뭔지는 모르겠지만 — 아니라고 말했어요. 그 여자가 어마어마하게 발끈하며 당신 말을 막았으니까요. 당신도 나만큼 그 꿈을 잘 알잖아요. 당신이 촛불을 손에 들고 부엌으로 내려와 내 머리에서 앞치마를 홱 벗겨 냈을 때 말이에요. 내가 꿈을 꾼 거라고 당신이 말했을 때요. 그 소리들을 당신이 믿지 않으려 했을 때요.\" 이렇게 터뜨린 뒤 애프리는 다시 앞치마를 입에 물었다. 자기 주인이자 남편이 다가오면 소리치거나 뛰어내릴 수 있도록, 늘 한 손은 창턱에, 한 무릎은 창가 자리에 둔 채로.\n\n리고는 이 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"하하!\" 그가 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 팔짱을 끼고, 의자에 등을 기대며 외쳤다. \"분명코, 플린트윈치 부인은 신탁이로군요! 이 신탁을 어떻게 풀이할까요, 부인과 저와 늙은 음모꾼이? 그는 부인이 — 아니라고 말했지요? 그러자 부인이 발끈하며 그를 막았다! 부인이 무엇이 아니었습니까? 부인이 무엇이 아닙니까? 자, 말씀하시지요, 부인!\"\n\n이 사나운 조롱 아래, 그녀는 숨을 더 거칠게 몰아쉬며 앉아 있었고, 입가가 흐트러졌다. 가만히 두려는 그녀의 안간힘에도 불구하고 입술이 떨리며 벌어졌다.\n\n\"자 그럼, 부인! 말씀하시지요! 우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 부인이 — 아니라고 말했고, 부인이 그를 막았습니다. 그는 부인이 — 무엇이 아니라고 말하려던 참이었지요? 저는 이미 알고 있습니다만, 부인에게서 약간의 확언을 듣고 싶습니다. 어떻습니까? 부인은 무엇이 아닙니까?\"\n\n그녀는 다시 자신을 억누르려 했으나, 격렬하게 터뜨렸다. \"아서의 어머니가 아니야!\"\n\n\"좋습니다.\" 리고가 말했다. \"부인은 말을 들으시는군요.\"\n\n격정의 폭발로 그녀의 얼굴에 굳어 있던 표정이 죄다 뜯겨 나가고, 그토록 오래 가둬 둔 채 속으로 타오르던 불길이 갈가리 찢긴 이목구비 하나하나에서 터져 나오며, 그녀가 외쳤다. \"내가 직접 말하겠어! 당신 입에서, 당신의 사악함의 오점이 묻은 채로 그걸 듣지는 않겠어. 기어이 드러나야 한다면, 내가 섰던 그 빛으로 드러나게 하겠어. 한마디도 더 하지 마. 내 말을 들어요!\"\n\n\"당신이 내가 아는 것보다도 더 고집 세고 더 끈질긴 여자가 아니라면,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 끼어들었다, \"리고 씨든, 블랑두아 씨든, 베엘제붑 씨든, 저 사람이 자기 방식대로 말하도록 내버려 두는 게 나을 거야. 저 사람이 다 알고 있는데 그게 무슨 상관이람?\"\n\n\"저 사람은 다 아는 게 아니야.\"\n\n\"신경 쓰는 건 다 알지.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 짜증스레 다그쳤다.\n\n\"저 사람은 나를 몰라.\"\n\n\"저 사람이 당신을 두고 뭘 신경이나 쓴다고 그래, 이 자만에 찬 여자야?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"플린트윈치, 분명히 말하는데, 내가 말하겠어. 분명히 말하지만, 일이 이 지경에 이른 이상, 내 입으로 직접 말하고, 처음부터 끝까지 내 식대로 표현하겠어. 뭐라고! 내가 이 방에서 아무것도 겪지 않았던가, 아무런 결핍도, 아무런 유폐도? 그래서 마침내 저 따위 거울에 비친 나를 들여다보는 데까지 몸을 낮춰야 한다고? 저 사람이 보여요? 저 사람 말이 들려요? 당신 아내가 지금의 백 배로 배은망덕하다 해도, 그리고 저 사람을 입막음하면 그녀를 잠자코 있게 할 가망이 지금의 천 배로 없다 해도, 저 사람 입에서 그걸 듣는 고문을 견디느니 내가 직접 말하겠어.\"\n\n리고는 의자를 조금 뒤로 밀고, 다리를 자기 앞으로 곧게 뻗고, 팔짱을 끼고 그녀를 마주한 채 앉았다.\n\n\"당신은 엄격하고 빡빡하게 길러진다는 게 어떤 건지 몰라요.\" 그녀가 그에게 말을 이어 갔다. \"나는 그렇게 길러졌어요. 내 젊음은 죄 많은 유흥과 쾌락의 가벼운 젊음이 아니었어요. 내 나날은 유익한 억압과 처벌과 두려움의 나날이었어요. 우리 마음의 부패, 우리 행실의 악함, 우리에게 내린 저주, 우리를 둘러싼 공포 — 이런 것들이 내 유년의 주제였어요. 그것들이 내 성품을 빚었고, 악행자에 대한 혐오로 나를 가득 채웠어요. 늙은 길버트 클레넘 씨가 자기 고아 조카를 내 남편 삼으라고 우리 아버지께 제안했을 때, 아버지는 그의 양육 또한 나와 마찬가지로 엄혹한 억제의 양육이었음을 내게 각인시켰어요. 아버지는 내게 말했어요, 그가 받은 정신의 훈육 외에도, 그는 굶주린 집에서 살아왔는데, 거기선 흥청거림이나 유흥이라곤 알려진 바 없고, 하루하루가 지난날과 다름없는 수고와 시련의 날이었다고요. 아버지는 내게 말했어요, 그는 삼촌이 그를 어른으로 인정해 주기 한참 전부터 나이로는 이미 어른이었고, 학창 시절부터 그 순간까지 삼촌의 지붕이 그에게 불경하고 방탕한 자들의 전염으로부터의 성소가 되어 주었다고요. 우리가 결혼한 지 일 년 안에, 내 아버지가 그를 두고 말했던 바로 그 사람이, 내 자리에 죄 많은 피조물을 두어 주님께 죄짓고 나를 능멸했음을 알게 되었을 때, 그 발견을 하도록 내가 정해진 자였음을, 그리고 그 멸망의 피조물에게 처벌의 손을 얹도록 내가 정해진 자였음을 내가 의심할 수 있었겠어요? 한순간에 떨쳐 버릴 수 있었겠어요 — 내 자신의 억울함이 아니라 — 내가 뭐라고! — 내가 길러지며 배운 죄에 대한 그 모든 배척과, 그것에 맞선 그 모든 싸움을 말이에요?\"\n\n그녀는 분노에 찬 손을 탁자 위의 회중시계에 얹었다.\n\n\"안 돼! '잊지 말라.' 그 말의 머리글자들이 지금도 이 안에 있고, 그때도 이 안에 있었어요. 그 글자들이 가리키는 옛 편지를 찾도록 내가 정해진 자였어요. 그것들이 무슨 뜻인지, 누가 새긴 것인지, 왜 새긴 것인지를 내게 일러 준 그 편지가, 이 시계와 함께 그의 비밀 서랍에 놓여 있었지요. 그 정함이 아니었다면 발견도 없었을 거예요. '잊지 말라.' 그것은 성난 구름에서 들려오는 목소리처럼 내게 말했어요. 그 죽음에 이르는 죄를 잊지 말라, 정해진 발견을 잊지 말라, 정해진 고난을 잊지 말라. 나는 잊지 않았어요. 내가 기억한 게 나 자신의 억울함이었던가요? 내 것이라니! 나는 한낱 종이자 일꾼이었을 뿐. 그들이 자기 죄의 결박에 묶여 내게 넘겨졌다는 것 말고, 내가 그들에게 무슨 권세를 부릴 수 있었겠어요!\"\n\n이 결연한 여인이 회상하는 그때 이후로 그녀의 희끗한 머리 위로 사십 년이 넘는 세월이 지나갔다. 자기가 앙심 어린 자존심과 분노를 어떤 이름으로 부르든, 영원토록 그 본질은 결코 바뀌지 않으리라는 속삭임과 다투고 씨름해 온 사십 년이 넘는 세월이. 그러나 그 사십 년이 넘는 세월이 흘러가고, 이제 이 응보의 여신이 그녀의 얼굴을 마주 보고 있건만, 그녀는 여전히 자신의 그 오랜 불경에 머물러 있었다 — 여전히 창조의 질서를 뒤집어, 자기 창조주의 진흙 형상에 자기 자신의 숨결을 불어넣고 있었다. 참으로, 참으로, 여행자들은 여러 나라에서 숱한 괴물 같은 우상을 보아 왔다. 그러나 우리 티끌 같은 피조물이 우리 자신의 못된 정념으로, 우리 자신을 본떠 빚어내는 신성(神性)의 형상보다 더 대담하고, 더 야비하고, 더 충격적인 형상을 본 인간의 눈은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"내가 그에게서 그녀의 이름과 거처를 대게 하여 그녀를 넘겨받았을 때,\" 그녀가 분노와 항변의 격류 속에서 말을 이어 갔다, \"내가 그녀를 단죄하고, 그녀가 얼굴을 가린 채 내 발치에 쓰러졌을 때, 내가 주장한 게 내 상처였던가요? 내가 그녀에게 퍼부은 게 내 비난이었던가요? 옛적에 악한 왕들에게 가서 그들을 단죄하도록 정해진 이들 — 그들은 일꾼이자 종이 아니었던가요? 그러니 그들보다 훨씬 못하고 동떨어진 나에게도, 규탄해야 할 죄가 있지 않았던가요? 그녀가 내게 자기 젊음과, 그의 비참하고 고된 삶(그것은 그가 저버린 그 덕성스러운 양육을 두고 그녀가 쓴 표현이었지요)과, 둘 사이에 몰래 있었던 모독당한 혼인 예식과, 내가 그들의 처벌의 도구로 처음 정해졌을 때 둘 다를 짓눌렀던 궁핍과 수치의 공포와, 그리고 사랑을(그녀가 내 발치에 엎드려 내게 그 말을 썼으니까요) — 그녀가 그를 버리고 내게 넘겼던 그 사랑을 호소했을 때, 내 발받침이 된 게 내 원수였던가요? 그녀를 움츠리고 떨게 만든 게 내 분노의 말이었던가요! 그 힘을 내게 돌리지 말지니, 그 속죄의 쥐어짜냄을 내게 돌리지 말지어다!\"\n\n그녀가 손가락조차 자유로이 쓰지 못한 지 여러 해가 가고 또 왔건만, 주목할 만하게도 그녀는 이미 한 번 이상 움켜쥔 손으로 탁자를 힘차게 내리쳤고, 이 말을 할 때는 마치 그것이 자기에게 예사로운 동작이기라도 한 듯 팔 전체를 허공에 치켜들었다.\n\n\"그러면 그녀의 굳은 마음과 시커먼 타락에서 짜낸 그 회개란 무엇이었나요? 내가, 앙심에 차고 가차 없다고요? 의로움이라곤 모르고, 사탄의 정함 말고는 아무 정함도 모르는 당신 같은 자에게는 그렇게 보일지도 모르죠. 비웃어요. 하지만 나는 내가 나 자신을 아는 대로, 플린트윈치가 나를 아는 대로 알려질 거예요. 비록 당신과 이 얼간이 여자한테뿐일지라도.\"\n\n\"부인 자신에게도, 라고 덧붙이시지요.\" 리고가 말했다. \"부인이 오히려 자기 자신에게 정당함을 입증하려 애쓰시는 게 아닌가 하는 자그마한 의심이 제겐 있습니다.\"\n\n\"거짓말이에요. 그렇지 않아요. 그럴 필요가 내겐 없어요.\" 그녀가 굉장한 기세와 분노로 말했다.\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 리고가 받아쳤다. \"하!\"\n\n\"내가 묻겠어요, 그녀에게 요구된 행실로서의 참회란 무엇이었나요? '너에게는 아이가 있고, 내게는 없다. 너는 그 아이를 사랑한다. 그를 내게 다오. 그는 자신이 내 아들이라고 믿게 될 것이며, 모든 이가 그를 내 아들로 믿게 될 것이다. 너의 들통남을 막기 위해, 그의 아버지는 다시는 너를 보거나 너와 연락하지 않겠다고 맹세할 것이다. 마찬가지로 그가 삼촌에게 모든 걸 빼앗기는 것을 막고, 너의 아이가 거지가 되는 것을 막기 위해, 너는 다시는 둘 중 누구도 보거나 연락하지 않겠다고 맹세할 것이다. 그렇게 하고, 내 남편에게서 나오는 너의 현재 생계 수단을 단념하면, 나는 너의 부양을 떠맡겠다. 그러면 너는, 네 은신처를 아무도 모르게 한 채, 원한다면, 네가 나 말고는 누구의 기억에서도 사라졌을 때 좋은 평판을 얻을 만했다는 그 거짓을 내가 반박하지 않은 채로 남겨 두고 떠나도 좋다.' 그게 다였어요. 그녀는 자기 죄 많고 수치스러운 정을 희생하기만 하면 됐어요. 그뿐. 그러면 그녀는 자유로이 자기 죄의 짐을 남몰래 짊어지고, 남몰래 가슴이 찢어지면 됐어요. 그리고 그런 현재의 비참함을(내 생각엔 그녀에게 충분히 가벼운 것이었지요!) 통하여, 할 수만 있다면 끝없는 비참함으로부터의 구원을 사들이면 됐어요. 이 일로 내가 그녀를 이승에서 처벌했다 한들, 저승으로 가는 길을 그녀에게 열어 주지 않았던가요? 그녀가 자신이 채울 길 없는 복수와 꺼지지 않는 불길에 둘러싸여 있음을 알았다 한들, 그것이 내 것이었던가요? 내가 그때나 그 후에나, 그녀를 에워싼 그 공포로 그녀를 위협했다 한들, 내가 그것들을 내 오른손에 쥐고 있었던가요?\"\n\n그녀는 탁자 위의 시계를 돌려 열고는, 누그러짐 없는 얼굴로 그 안에 새겨진 글자들을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그들은 잊지 않았어요. 이런 잘못에 대해서는 잘못한 자들이 잊을 수 없도록 정해진 거예요. 아서의 존재가 그의 아버지에게 날마다의 책망이었고, 아서의 부재가 그의 어머니에게 날마다의 고통이었다면, 그것은 여호와의 공의로운 처분이었어요. 깨어난 양심의 가시가 그녀를 미치게 만들었다는 것도, 그녀가 그렇게 여러 해를 살도록 한 것이 만물을 주관하시는 분의 뜻이었다는 것도, 내 탓으로 돌릴 수 없기는 마찬가지예요. 나는 달리 예정되어 잃어버린 그 아이를 되찾는 데, 그에게 떳떳한 혈통의 평판을 주는 데, 그를 두려움과 떨림 속에서, 그리고 이 단죄받은 세상에 들어오기도 전에 그 머리 위에 무겁게 얹혀 있던 죄들에 대한 실천적 통회의 삶으로 길러 내는 데 나를 바쳤어요. 그게 잔인한 일이었나요? 나 또한, 내가 가담하지도 않은 그 원죄의 결과를 겪지 않았던가요? 아서의 아버지와 나는, 지구 반 바퀴를 사이에 두고 떨어져 있던 때나, 이 집에서 함께 있던 때나, 조금도 더 멀리 떨어져 산 게 아니었어요. 그는 죽으며 이 시계를 그 '잊지 말라'와 함께 내게 돌려보냈어요. 나는 잊지 않아요, 비록 그가 읽은 것과 똑같이 읽지는 않지만요. 나는 그 안에서, 이 일들을 행하도록 내가 정해진 자였음을 읽어요. 이 글자들이 이 탁자 위에 놓여 있게 된 이래로 나는 그렇게 읽어 왔고, 그것들이 수천 마일 떨어져 있을 때도 똑같이 또렷하게 그렇게 읽었어요.\"\n\n그녀가 손에 자유가 생긴 데 대한 자각이라곤 전혀 내보이지 않은 채, 그 새로운 자유로움으로 시계갑을 손에 쥐고, 마치 그것이 자기를 움직일 테면 움직여 보라고 도전하듯 그 위에 눈을 박는 동안, 리고가 손가락을 요란하고 경멸스럽게 탁 튕기며 외쳤다. \"자, 부인! 시간이 다 됐습니다. 자, 경건하신 부인, 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요! 부인은 제가 모르는 건 아무것도 말씀하지 못합니다. 도둑맞은 돈 얘기로 오시지요, 아니면 제가 하겠습니다! 내 영혼이 죽을 노릇이지, 부인의 그 다른 횡설수설은 이제 충분합니다. 곧장 도둑맞은 돈 얘기로 오시지요!\"\n\n\"이 흉악한 자야,\" 그녀가 대답했고, 이제 두 손은 자기 머리를 움켜쥐고 있었다. \"플린트윈치의 어떤 치명적인 실수로, 이 일들을 거든 유일한 다른 사람이자 그것들을 맡았던 그의 어떤 미진함으로, 누가, 무엇으로 태운 서류의 재를 그러모은 일로, 당신이 그 추가 유언을 손에 넣게 되었는지, 나는 당신이 여기서 가진 나머지 위력을 어떻게 얻었는지 아는 것 이상으로는 모르지만 —\"\n\n\"그런데도,\" 리고가 말을 끊었다, \"제가 아는 어느 편리한 곳에 그것을, 길버트 클레넘 씨의 유언에 더해진 바로 그 짧은 부록을 가지고 있다는 게 제 묘한 운수지요. 한 부인이 쓰고, 같은 부인과 우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 증인을 선 그것을요! 아, 바, 늙은 음모꾼, 비뚜름한 작은 꼭두각시! 부인, 계속합시다. 시간이 촉박합니다. 부인이 끝내시겠습니까, 제가 끝내겠습니까?\"\n\n\"내가!\" 그녀가, 가능하다면 한층 더해진 결의로 대답했다. \"내가 하겠어, 당신의 그 끔찍한 왜곡을 뒤집어쓴 채 나 자신을 보이고, 누구에게든 나 자신이 보여지는 걸 견딜 수 없으니까. 당신은, 악명 높은 외국 감옥과 갤리선에서 익힌 그 짓거리들로, 마치 돈이 나를 움직인 것처럼 만들겠지. 돈이 아니었어.\"\n\n\"바, 바, 바! 잠시 제 정중함은 거두고 말하지요, 거짓말, 거짓말, 거짓말입니다. 부인이 그 증서를 숨기고 돈을 챙긴 걸 부인 자신이 알지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"돈을 위해서가 아니었어, 흉악한 자야!\" 그녀가 마치 벌떡 일어서기라도 할 듯, 심지어 그 격렬함 속에서 못 쓰는 발을 딛고 거의 일어선 듯 안간힘을 썼다. \"길버트 클레넘이 치매에 이르러 죽음의 문턱에서, 자기 조카가 한때 어떤 처녀를 마음에 두었다가 그 마음을 짓눌러 없앴고, 그 처녀가 그 뒤 우울 속으로 시들어 그녀를 알던 모든 이에게서 물러나 버렸다는 말을 듣고는, 그 처녀를 향한 어떤 상상 속 마음의 누그러짐이라는 망상에 사로잡혀서, 그 무력한 상태에서, 그녀의 죄로 내 삶을 어둡게 한, 그리고 그녀의 사악함을 그녀 자신의 손과 입으로 알도록 정해졌던 나에게, 부당하다고 여겨진 고통에 대한 보상으로 의도된 유증을 받아쓰게 했다면, 그 불의를 내가 물리친 것과, 한낱 돈을 — 당신과 당신의 감옥 동료들이 아무에게서나 훔쳐 낼 수 있는 그 따위 것을 탐낸 것 사이에 아무 차이도 없었단 말이야?\"\n\n\"시간이 촉박합니다, 부인. 조심하시지요!\"\n\n\"이 집이 지붕에서 바닥까지 활활 타오른다 해도,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다, \"나는 내 의로운 동기가 칼잡이며 도둑들의 동기와 한데 묶이는 데 맞서 나 자신을 변호하기 위해 이 안에 머물겠어.\"\n\n리고는 그녀의 얼굴에 대고 조롱하듯 손가락을 탁 튕겼다. \"부인이 서서히 죽음으로 몰아간 그 작은 미인에게 천 기니. 그 후원자가 쉰에 두었을 가장 어린 딸에게, 혹은(딸이 없다면) 동생의 가장 어린 딸에게, 그녀가 성년이 될 때 천 기니. '의지가지없는 어린 고아 처녀를 보호해 준 데 대한, 그의 사심 없음의 가장 마음에 드는 기념으로서'. 이천 기니. 뭐라고요! 부인은 도무지 돈 얘기로 오지 않으시는군요?\"\n\n\"그 후원자가,\" 그녀가 격렬하게 이어 가려는데 그가 가로막았다.\n\n\"이름을! 그를 프레더릭 도릿 씨라고 부르시지요. 더는 둘러대지 마시고.\"\n\n\"그 프레더릭 도릿이 이 모든 일의 시작이었어. 그가 음악 연주자만 아니었더라면, 그리고 그 젊고 번창하던 시절에, 가수며 악사며 그런 따위의 악의 자식들이 빛에 등을 돌리고 어둠에 얼굴을 향하는 그 게으른 집을 두지만 않았더라면, 그녀는 자기 비천한 처지에 머물러 있었을 테고, 거기서 끌어올려졌다가 다시 내동댕이쳐지지도 않았을 거야. 하지만, 아니지. 사탄이 그 프레더릭 도릿 속으로 들어가, 그가 선행을 베푸는, 순결하고 칭송할 만한 취향의 사내라고, 여기 노래할 목소리를 지닌 가엾은 처녀가 있다고 부추겼어. 그러더니 그는 그녀를 가르치게 하지. 그러더니, 줄곧 덕성스러운 험난함의 길에서 그 예술이라 불리는 저주받은 올무들을 남몰래 갈망해 온 아서의 아버지가 그녀와 알게 돼. 그렇게, 가수가 되려고 수련하던 한 추레한 고아가, 그 프레더릭 도릿의 주선으로 나를 이기고, 나는 낮춰지고 기만당했어! — 그러니까, 내가 아니라,\" 얼굴에 핏기가 솟구치자 그녀가 얼른 덧붙였다, \"나보다 더 크신 분이. 내가 뭐라고?\"\n\n그동안 차츰차츰 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 비틀어 다가와, 그녀도 모르는 사이에 이제 그녀의 팔꿈치 바로 곁까지 와 있던 제리마이아 플린트윈치는, 그녀가 이 말을 하자 유난히 일그러진 못마땅한 표정을 지었고, 게다가 마치 그런 주장이 자기 다리에 박히는 작은 가시라도 되는 양 각반을 씰룩거렸다.\n\n\"마지막으로,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다, \"이 일들도 이제 끝이고, 나는 이에 대해 더는 말하지 않겠고, 당신도 더는 말하지 않을 것이며, 남은 건 오직 이 일들에 대한 앎이 여기 모인 우리끼리만 간직될 수 있을지를 정하는 것뿐이니까. 마지막으로, 내가 아서의 아버지가 아는 가운데 그 서류를 숨겼을 때 —\"\n\n\"하지만 그의 동의를 받고는 아니었지, 알다시피.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"누가 그의 동의를 받았다고 했어?\" 제리마이아가 그렇게 가까이 있는 것에 그녀는 흠칫 놀라, 고개를 뒤로 빼고는 솟아나는 불신의 빛으로 그를 바라보았다. \"그가 그걸 내보이라 했으나 내가 그러지 않으려 했을 때, 우리 사이에 당신이 끼어 있던 적이 충분히 많았으니, 내가 '그의 동의를 받고'라고 말했다면 당신이 반박했겠지. 분명히 말하는데, 내가 그 서류를 숨겼을 때, 나는 그것을 없애려는 어떤 시도도 하지 않고, 여러 해 동안 여기 이 집에서 곁에 두었어. 길버트 재산의 나머지가 아서의 아버지에게 남겨졌으니, 나는 언제든, 그 두 액수 말고는 더 흔들지 않으면서, 그것을 찾아낸 척할 수 있었어. 하지만 그런 척을 직접적인 거짓으로(큰 책임이지) 떠받쳐야 했을 거라는 점 말고도, 내가 여기서 시련을 겪어 온 그 모든 세월 동안, 그것을 밝힐 어떤 새로운 이유도 보지 못했어. 그것은 죄에 상을 주는 일이었고, 망상의 그릇된 결과였어. 나는 내가 정해진 일을 행했고, 이 네 벽 안에서, 내가 겪도록 정해진 것을 겪어 왔어. 마침내 그 서류가 — 내 생각엔 — 내 앞에서 없애졌을 때, 그녀는 이미 오래전에 죽었고, 그녀의 후원자 프레더릭 도릿은 오래전에 마땅하게도 파산하고 치매에 이르렀어. 그에게는 딸이 없었어. 나는 그 전에 조카딸을 찾아냈고, 내가 그녀를 위해 한 일은, 그녀에게 아무 득도 되지 않았을 그 돈보다 훨씬 더 그녀에게 나은 것이었어.\" 그녀는 잠시 후, 마치 시계에 대고 말하듯 덧붙였다. \"그녀 자신은 죄가 없었지. 어쩌면 내 죽음에 이르러 그것을 그녀에게 넘겨주는 걸 잊지 않았을지도 모르지.\" 그러고는 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"제가 부인께 무언가를 상기시켜 드릴까요, 존경할 만한 부인?\" 리고가 말했다. \"그 작은 서류는, 우리의 벗인 죄수가 — 내 영혼의 옥살이 동무가 — 외국에서 집으로 돌아오던 그날 밤, 이 집에 있었습니다. 무언가 더 상기시켜 드릴까요? 미처 깃털도 나지 않은 그 작은 노래하는 새는, 부인이 지정한 어느 보호자에 의해 오랫동안 새장에 갇혀 있었지요. 여기 우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 익히 잘 아는 자에게요. 우리의 늙은 음모꾼을 구슬려, 그가 그자를 마지막으로 본 게 언제인지 말하게 해 볼까요?\"\n\n\"내가 말하겠어요!\" 애프리가 입을 떼며 외쳤다. \"그게 내 모든 꿈 중에 맨 처음 꾼 꿈이었어요. 제리마이아, 지금 나한테 가까이 오면, 세인트폴 성당까지 들리게 비명을 지를 거예요! 이 사람이 말한 그 사람은 제리마이아 자신의 쌍둥이 형제였어요. 그리고 그는 아서 도련님이 집에 돌아오던 날 밤, 한밤중에 여기 있었고, 제리마이아가 제 손으로 그에게 이 서류를, 또 뭔지 모를 다른 것들과 함께 건넸고, 그는 그걸 쇠 상자에 담아 가져갔어요 — 사람 살려! 사람 죽인다! 제리—마이—아한테서 날 구해 줘요!\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨가 그녀에게 달려들었으나, 리고가 중간에서 그를 팔로 붙잡았다. 잠시 그와 몸싸움을 벌인 끝에, 플린트윈치는 단념하고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었다.\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 리고가 팔꿈치로 그를 쿡쿡 찌르고 홱홱 밀어내며 그를 놀려 댔다. \"꿈꾸는 데 저런 재주를 지닌 부인께 손찌검을 하다니! 하, 하, 하! 원, 이 부인은 구경거리로 내세우면 당신에게 한밑천이 되겠소. 부인이 꿈꾸는 건 죄다 사실로 드러나니. 하, 하, 하! 당신은 그 사람을 어찌나 닮았는지, 작은 플린트윈치. 내가 알던 그 사람을 어찌나 닮았는지(주인에게 그를 위해 처음 영어로 통역해 줬을 때 말이오), 앤트워프 부두 곁, 높은 지붕들의 그 작은 골목에 있는 '당구대 셋'이라는 술집에서요! 아, 정말이지 그는 술을 들이켜는 데는 용감한 친구였지. 아, 정말이지 그는 담배를 피우는 데도 용감한 친구였고! 아, 정말이지 그는 어느 아늑한 독신자 거처에서 살았어 — 가구 딸린, 오 층에, 장작과 숯 장수와, 양재사와, 의자 장수와, 통 만드는 자 위층에 말이지 — 거기서 나도 그를 알았고, 그곳에서 그는 코냑과 담배로 하루에 열두 번 잠을 자고 발작을 한 번 일으켰는데, 마침내 발작을 한 번 너무 많이 일으켜 하늘로 올라가 버렸지. 하, 하, 하! 내가 그의 쇠 상자에 든 서류들을 어떻게 손에 넣었는지가 무슨 상관이오? 어쩌면 그가 부인을 위해 그것을 내 손에 맡겼을 수도, 어쩌면 그게 잠겨 있어 내 호기심이 동했을 수도, 어쩌면 내가 그걸 가로챘을 수도 있지. 하, 하, 하! 내가 그걸 안전하게 가지고 있으니, 무슨 상관이오? 우리는 여기서 까다롭게 굴지 않잖나, 어이, 플린트윈치? 우리는 여기서 까다롭게 굴지 않지요, 안 그렇습니까, 부인?\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 자기 팔꿈치로 사납게 맞받아 홱홱 밀어내며 그의 앞에서 물러나, 다시 자기 구석으로 돌아갔다. 거기서 그는 이제 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 서서 숨을 고르며, 클레넘 부인의 응시를 되받아 보았다.\n\"하, 하, 하! 한데 이게 어찌 된 일이오?\" 리고가 외쳤다. \"두 분이 서로를 모르는 듯하군요. 은폐하시는 클레넘 부인께, 음모를 꾸미시는 플린트윈치 씨를 소개하게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n플린트윈치 씨는 턱을 긁으려고 한 손을 주머니에서 꺼내, 그 자세로 한두 걸음 나아오며, 여전히 클레넘 부인의 시선을 되받은 채 그녀에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"자, 당신이 나한테 그렇게 눈을 크게 뜨는 게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 알지만, 수고할 것 없어, 나는 그딴 거 신경 안 쓰니까. 내가 당신한테 몇 해 동안 말해 왔잖아, 당신이 여자들 중에 가장 고집 세고 가장 완고한 축에 든다고. 그게 바로 당신이야. 당신은 스스로 겸손하고 죄 많은 사람이라 부르지만, 당신은 당신 성별 중에 가장 거만한 사람이야. 그게 바로 당신이라고. 우리가 다툴 때마다 내가 당신한테 거듭거듭 말했지, 당신은 모든 걸 당신 앞에 굴복시키고 싶어 하지만 나는 당신 앞에 굴복하지 않겠다고 — 당신은 모두를 산 채로 삼켜 버리고 싶어 하지만 나는 산 채로 삼켜지지 않겠다고. 왜 처음 그 서류를 손에 넣었을 때 없애지 않았어? 내가 그러라고 권했잖아. 하지만 안 되지, 충고를 받아들이는 건 당신 방식이 아니니까. 굳이 그걸 간직해야 했지. 어쩌면 언젠가 그걸 실행에 옮길지도 모른다, 굳이 그러면서. 마치 내가 그보다 더 잘 모르기라도 하는 것처럼! 당신의 그 자존심이, 그걸 곁에 간직해 온 게 의심받을 위험을 무릅쓰면서 그걸 실행에 옮기는 꼴이 눈에 선해. 하지만 그게 당신이 자기 자신을 속이는 방식이지. 꼭 당신이, 이 모든 일을 한 게 당신이 엄혹한 여자라서, 죄다 멸시와 앙심과 권세와 용서 없음 때문이 아니라, 당신이 종이자 일꾼이며 그걸 하도록 정해진 자라서라고 자기를 속여 우겨 대듯이 말이야. 당신이 뭐라고, 그걸 하도록 정해졌다는 거야? 그게 당신 신앙일지는 몰라도, 나한텐 헛소리야. 그리고 이왕 말 나온 김에 진실을 다 말해 주자면,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 팔짱을 끼며 성마른 외고집 그 자체의 형상이 되어 말했다, \"나는 짜증이 나 왔어 — 이 사십 년 동안 짜증이 나 왔다고 — 더 잘 아는 나한테까지 당신이 그렇게 고상한 자리를 차지하는 통에 말이야. 그 결과 나는 보란 듯이 낮은 자리에 놓이게 됐지. 나는 당신을 무척 우러러봐. 당신은 머리가 좋고 재주가 대단한 여자야. 하지만 제아무리 좋은 머리에 제아무리 대단한 재주라도, 사십 년 동안 사람을 짜증 나게 하면서 그를 쓰라리게 하지 않을 수는 없는 법이야. 그러니 지금 당신 눈초리 따위 신경 안 써. 자, 이제 그 서류 얘기로 들어가니, 내 말을 새겨들어. 당신은 그걸 어딘가에 치워 두고, 어디인지는 혼자만 알았지. 그때 당신은 활동적인 여자라, 그 서류가 갖고 싶으면 가질 수 있었어. 하지만 새겨들어. 당신이 지금 같은 꼴이 되는 때가 오고, 그러면 그 서류가 갖고 싶어도 가질 수가 없게 되지. 그래서 그건 그 은닉처에서 오랜 세월 누워 있었어. 마침내, 우리가 아서가 날마다 돌아오기를 기다리고, 어느 날이라도 그가 돌아올 수 있고, 그가 집 안을 뒤지며 무슨 일을 벌일지 알 수 없게 됐을 때, 나는 당신한테 오천 번도 더 권했어, 당신이 그걸 손에 넣을 수 없거든 내가 손에 넣게 해 달라고, 불에 던져 버리게 말이야. 하지만 안 되지 — 그게 어디 있는지는 당신 말고는 아무도 모르고, 그게 권세니까. 그리고 당신이 자기를 무슨 겸손한 이름으로 부르든, 나는 당신을 권세에 굶주린 여자 루시퍼라 부른다! 어느 일요일 밤, 아서가 집에 돌아오지. 그가 이 방에 들어온 지 십 분도 안 돼서, 자기 아버지의 시계 얘기를 꺼내. 당신도 잘 알다시피, 그의 아버지가 그 시계를 당신한테 보냈을 때의 그 '잊지 말라'는, 나머지 이야기는 그때 이미 다 죽어 끝나 버렸으니, 오직 그 은폐를 잊지 말라는 뜻일 수밖에 없었어. 돌려놓으라는! 아서의 태도에 당신은 좀 겁을 먹고, 그래서 그 서류를 결국 태워 버리기로 하지. 그래서, 저 깡충대는 화냥년 이세벨이,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 자기 아내를 향해 히죽 웃었다, \"당신을 침대에 누이기 전에, 당신은 마침내 나한테 말하지, 그 서류를 어디에 뒀는지를, 지하실의 낡은 장부들 사이에 말이야. 바로 그 이튿날 아침 아서가 직접 어슬렁거리며 들어갔던 그곳에. 하지만 그건 일요일 밤에 태워서는 안 되지. 안 돼. 당신은 엄격하니까, 당신은 그러니까. 우리는 열두 시를 넘겨 월요일로 들어설 때까지 기다려야 해. 자, 이 모든 게 나를 산 채로 삼키는 짓이라 짜증이 나니까, 좀 부아가 치밀고, 당신만큼 엄격하지도 않은 나는, 열두 시 전에 그 문서를 한번 들여다봐, 그 생김새를 다시 기억해 두려고 — 지하실에 있는 그것과 비슷한, 누렇게 바랜 숱한 낡은 종이 중 하나를 그것처럼 접어 두지 — 그러고 나서, 우리가 월요일 아침으로 들어서고, 내가 당신 등잔불에 의지해, 저 침대에 누운 당신한테서 이 화로까지 걸어가야 할 때, 마술사처럼 살짝 바꿔치기를 해서 그에 맞게 태우는 거야. 내 형 에프라임은, 정신병자 간수였는데(차라리 자기가 구속복을 입고 갇혔어야 했지), 당신한테서 받은 그 긴 일거리가 끝난 뒤로 여러 일을 했지만, 잘 풀리지 못했어. 그의 아내가 죽었고(그건 대수도 아니지. 차라리 내 아내가 죽었더라면 좋았을걸), 정신병자들로 투기를 했다가 실패했고, 환자를 정신 차리게 한답시고 너무 달군 일로 곤경에 빠졌고, 빚을 졌어. 그는 긁어모을 수 있었던 돈과 나한테서 받은 약간으로 자취를 감추려던 참이었어. 그 월요일 이른 아침 그는 여기서 물때를 기다리고 있었지. 한마디로, 그는 앤트워프로 가려던 참이었고, 거기서(이런 말 하면 당신이 충격받을까 두렵네만, 그놈 지옥에나 떨어져라!) 이 신사 양반과 알게 된 거야. 그는 먼 길을 와서, 그때 나는 그저 졸린 거라고 생각했지만, 지금 생각하니 술에 취해 있었던 거지. 아서의 어머니가 그와 그의 아내의 보살핌을 받고 있을 때, 그녀는 늘 편지를 썼어, 쉴 새 없이 써 댔지 — 대개는 당신한테 보내는 고백 편지와 용서를 비는 기도였어. 내 형은 그 종이들 뭉치를 이따금 나한테 건네줬어. 나는 그것들마저 산 채로 삼켜지게 하느니 차라리 내가 갖고 있는 게 낫겠다 싶어서, 그것들을 상자에 넣어 두고, 기분이 동할 때 들여다봤어. 아서가 그 일로 어슬렁거리니, 그 서류를 그곳에서 빼내 두는 게 상책이라는 확신이 들어, 나는 그것을 바로 그 같은 상자에 넣고, 자물쇠 두 개로 전부 잠가, 형한테 맡겨 가져가서 간직하라고 했어, 내가 그에 관해 편지를 쓸 때까지. 나는 그에 관해 편지를 썼지만, 답을 받지 못했어. 나는 영문을 알 수 없었지, 이 신사 양반이 우리한테 첫 방문을 베풀어 주실 때까지는. 물론 그때부터 어찌 된 일인지 의심하기 시작했지. 그리고 그가 내 종이들과, 당신 종이와, 내 형의 코냑과 담배에 절은 입놀림(그놈 입에 재갈이라도 물렸어야 했지)에서 어떻게 자기 앎을 얻어 냈는지를 이해하는 데, 지금 그자의 말 따위는 필요 없어. 자, 이제 딱 한 가지만 더 말하겠어, 이 멍청한 여자야, 그건, 내가 그 추가 유언 일로 당신을 골치 아프게 했을지 안 했을지, 도무지 마음을 정하지 못했다는 거야. 안 그랬을 것 같아. 당신을 이겼다는 것, 당신에 대한 권세를 쥐고 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 나는 아주 흡족했을 거야. 지금 형편으로는, 내일 이맘때 밤까지 당신한테 더 해 줄 설명은 없어. 그러니,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 몸을 한번 비틀어 돌리며 연설을 마무리했다, \"다른 누구한테나 눈을 부릅뜨고 있으라고, 나한테 부릅떠 봐야 소용없으니까.\"\n\n그가 말을 그치자 그녀는 천천히 눈을 거두고, 이마를 손에 떨구었다. 다른 손은 탁자를 세게 누르고 있었고, 다시 그녀에게서 그 기이한 움직임이 — 마치 일어서려는 듯한 움직임이 — 보였다.\n\n\"이 상자는 다른 어디서도, 여기서 받을 값을 결코 받지 못해. 이 앎은, 다른 누구에게 팔든, 나에게 파는 것과 똑같은 이득을 결코 당신에게 가져다주지 못해. 하지만 나는 당신이 요구한 액수를 마련할 수단이 지금 없어. 나는 잘되지 못했어. 지금은 얼마를 받겠어, 그리고 나중에는 얼마를, 그리고 당신의 침묵을 내가 어떻게 보장받지?\"\n\n\"나의 천사여,\" 리고가 말했다, \"제가 무엇을 받겠는지는 말씀드렸고, 시간이 촉박합니다. 여기 오기 전에, 저는 이 서류들 중 가장 중요한 것들의 사본을 다른 사람 손에 맡겨 두었습니다. 마셜시 정문이 밤을 위해 닫힐 때까지 시간을 끄시면, 흥정하기엔 너무 늦어질 겁니다. 죄수가 그것들을 읽었을 테니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 다시 두 손을 머리에 갖다 대고, 큰 소리로 외치며, 벌떡 일어섰다. 잠시, 마치 쓰러질 듯 비틀거리더니, 이내 꼿꼿이 섰다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻인지 말해. 무슨 뜻인지 말하라고, 이 자야!\"\n\n그토록 오래 곧추선 자세에 익숙하지 않아 그 자세로 굳어 버린 그녀의 유령 같은 형상 앞에서, 리고는 뒤로 물러서며 목소리를 낮췄다. 세 사람 모두에게, 그것은 거의 죽은 여인이 일어선 듯했다.\n\n\"도릿 양은,\" 리고가 대답했다, \"제가 바다 건너에서 알았던 프레더릭 씨의 어린 조카딸 말입니다만, 그 죄수에게 정이 들어 있습니다. 프레더릭 씨의 어린 조카딸인 도릿 양은, 이 순간 병든 그 죄수를 지키고 있지요. 저는 여기 오는 길에, 그녀를 위해 제 손으로 감옥에 꾸러미를 하나 남겨 두었습니다. '그를 위하여'라는 지시 편지와 함께요 — 그녀는 그를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 테니까요 — 오늘 밤 폐쇄 시각 전에 그것을 되찾아 가지 않을 경우에 대비해, 봉인을 뜯지 말고 간직하라고요 — 감옥의 종이 울리기 전에 그것을 되찾아 가지 않을 경우에는\n그것을 그에게 주라고. 그리고 거기에는 그녀 자신을 위한 두 번째 사본이 동봉되어 있으니, 그것은 그가 그녀에게 주어야 하오. 무슨 소리! 이만큼 일이 진척된 마당에, 내 비밀에 두 번째 생명을 주어 두지 않고서야 당신들 틈에 내 한 몸을 맡길 수는 없지. 그리고 그것이 다른 곳에서는 여기서 받을 값을 받아 주지 못한다는 점에 대해서는, 그러면 말해 보시오, 마담, 그 어린 조카가 — 그자를 위하여 — 이 일을 덮으려고 치를 값을 당신은 정하고 매겨 두었소? 다시 한번 말하지만, 시간이 촉박하오. 오늘 밤 종이 울리기 전에 그 봉투를 되찾아 가지 않으면, 당신은 살 수 없소. 그렇다면 나는 그 어린 아가씨에게 팔겠소!”\n\n다시 한번 그녀의 안에서 동요와 갈등이 일었고, 그녀는 벽장으로 달려가 문을 홱 열어젖히고 두건인지 숄인지를 끌어내려 머리에 둘러썼다. 공포에 질려 그녀를 지켜보던 애프리가 방 한가운데로 달려들어 그녀의 옷자락을 붙잡고 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n“그러지 마세요, 그러지 마세요, 그러지 마세요! 무얼 하시는 거예요? 어디로 가시려는 거예요? 마님은 무서운 분이지만, 저는 마님께 아무런 원한도 없어요. 이제 보니 저는 가엾은 아서를 위해서 해 드릴 수 있는 게 없어요. 그러니 저를 두려워하실 필요 없어요. 마님의 비밀을 지켜 드릴게요. 나가지 마세요, 거리에서 쓰러져 돌아가실 거예요. 다만 한 가지만 약속해 주세요. 여기 몰래 갇혀 있는 게 그 가엾은 사람이라면, 제가 그 사람을 맡아 돌볼 수 있게, 제가 그 사람의 간병인이 되게 해 주시겠다고요. 그 한 가지만 약속해 주세요, 그러면 절대 저를 두려워하지 마세요.”\n\n클레넘 부인은 그 황급한 다급함의 절정에서 한순간 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 준엄한 놀라움으로 말했다.\n\n“여기 갇혀 있다고? 그 여자는 죽은 지 스무 해도 넘었어. 플린트윈치에게 물어봐 — 그에게 물어보라고. 두 사람 다 말해 줄 수 있을 거다, 아서가 외국으로 떠났을 때 그 여자가 죽었다고.”\n\n“그렇다면 더더욱 나빠요.” 애프리가 몸서리치며 말했다. “그러면 그 여자가 이 집을 떠도는 거니까요. 그렇지 않다면 누가 그토록 살며시 먼지를 떨어뜨려 신호를 보내며 집 안을 바스락거리고 다니겠어요? 우리가 다 잠자리에 들었을 때 누가 오가며 벽에 길고 구불구불한 자국을 남기겠어요? 또 누가 가끔 문을 붙들고 있겠어요? 하지만 나가지 마세요 — 나가지 마세요! 마님, 거리에서 돌아가실 거예요!”\n\n그녀의 마님은 애원하는 손에서 옷자락을 떼어 낼 뿐, 리고에게 “내가 돌아올 때까지 여기서 기다려!”라고 말하고는 방 밖으로 달려 나갔다. 두 사람은 창문 너머로, 그녀가 안마당을 미친 듯이 가로질러 정문 밖으로 달려 나가는 것을 보았다.\n\n잠시 동안 그들은 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다. 먼저 움직인 것은 애프리였고, 그녀는 두 손을 비틀며 마님의 뒤를 쫓았다. 다음으로 제러마이아 플린트윈치가 한 손은 주머니에 넣고 다른 손으로 턱을 문지르며 천천히 문 쪽으로 뒷걸음질하더니, 한마디 말도 없이 그 특유의 입 다문 태도로 몸을 비틀어 빠져나갔다. 홀로 남은 리고는 열린 창문의 창턱에 옛 마르세유 감옥에서의 그 자세 그대로 자리를 잡았다. 그는 담배와 부싯깃통을 손이 닿는 곳에 놓아두고 담배를 피우기 시작했다.\n\n“흥! 그 지긋지긋한 옛 감옥만큼이나 따분하군. 더 따뜻하긴 해도, 거의 그만큼 음울해. 그녀가 돌아올 때까지 기다린다? 그래, 물론이지. 하지만 그녀는 어디로 갔고, 얼마나 오래 가 있을까? 상관없다! 리고 라니에 블랑두아, 나의 사랑스러운 양반, 너는 네 돈을 받게 될 거야. 너는 부자가 될 거야. 너는 신사로 살아왔으니, 신사로 죽을 거야. 너는 승리한다, 나의 어린 사내야. 하지만 승리하는 것이야말로 네 본성이지. 흥!”\n\n승리의 순간에, 그가 각별한 만족감으로 머리 위의 커다란 들보를 곁눈질하자, 그의 콧수염은 위로 올라가고 코는 아래로 내려갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER 31. Closed\n\n해가 지고, 그 거리에 그토록 오랫동안 익숙지 않던 형체가 길을 서둘러 나아갈 무렵, 거리는 먼지 낀 황혼 속에서 어둑했다. 옛집 바로 근처에서는 그것이 거의 눈길을 끌지 못했으니, 그것을 알아챌 사람이라곤 드문드문 흩어진 몇뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 강가에서 런던 다리로 통하는 구불구불한 길을 따라 올라가 큰 한길로 들어서자, 그것은 경악에 에워싸이게 되었다.\n\n단호하고 사나운 표정에, 발걸음은 빠르면서도 미약하고 불안정했으며, 검은 의복과 황급히 둘러쓴 머릿수건으로 눈에 띄었고, 수척하고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 창백함을 띤 채, 그것은 군중을 몽유병자만큼도 개의치 않고 앞으로 밀고 나아갔다. 단상 위에 올려져 보이게 된 것보다도, 자신이 섞여 있는 군중에서 그토록 동떨어져 있음으로써 더욱 두드러진 그 형체는 모든 눈길을 끌어모았다. 어슬렁대던 자들은 그것을 살피느라 주의를 곤두세웠고, 바쁜 사람들은 그것과 엇갈리며 걸음을 늦추고 고개를 돌렸으며, 길동무들은 멈춰 서서 비켜선 채 다가오는 이 유령 같은 여인을 보라며 서로 속삭였다. 그리고 지나가는 그 형체의 휩쓸림은 소용돌이를 일으켜, 가장 한가하고 가장 호기심 많은 자들을 그 뒤로 끌어들이는 듯했다.\n\n여러 해를 갇혀 있던 자신의 독방으로 이 수많은 응시하는 얼굴들이 어지러이 쳐들어온 탓에, 바깥 공기 속에 있다는 혼란스러운 감각과 두 발로 걷고 있다는 더욱 혼란스러운 감각 탓에, 어렴풋이 기억하던 사물들의 뜻밖의 변화 탓에, 그리고 그녀가 격리되어 있던 삶에 대해 상상이 곧잘 그려 내던 통제 가능한 그림들과 현실의 압도적인 쇄도 사이의 닮음 없음 탓에, 그녀는 어질어질해졌고, 외부의 인간 무리와 그 관찰에 둘러싸였다기보다 정신을 흩뜨리는 상념들에 에워싸인 듯이 길을 갔다. 그러나 다리를 건너 곧장 얼마쯤 나아간 뒤, 그녀는 길을 물어야 한다는 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그제야, 멈춰 서서 물어볼 만한 곳을 찾으려 주위를 둘러보려 돌아섰을 때, 그녀는 자신이 열띠게 번득이는 얼굴들에 둘러싸여 있음을 알았다.\n\n\"왜 나를 에워싸는 거죠?\" 그녀가 떨면서 물었다.\n\n가장 가까이 있던 자들 가운데 아무도 대답하지 않았으나, 바깥쪽 둘레에서 날카로운 외침이 솟아올랐다. \"당신이 미쳤으니까!\"\n\n\"나는 여기 있는 누구 못지않게 멀쩡해요. 마셜시 감옥을 찾고 싶습니다.\"\n\n날카로운 바깥쪽 무리가 다시 받아쳤다. \"그것만으로도 당신이 미쳤다는 게 드러나죠, 바로 맞은편이니까!\"\n\n이 대답에 함성이 뒤따르는 가운데, 키 작고 온순하며 조용해 보이는 젊은이 하나가 그녀에게로 헤치고 들어와 말했다. \"마셜시를 찾으셨습니까? 저도 거기 근무하러 가는 길입니다. 저와 함께 건너가시죠.\"\n\n그녀가 그의 팔에 손을 얹자, 그는 그녀를 데리고 길을 건넜다. 군중은 그녀를 곧 놓치게 된다는 전망에 다소 분개하여, 앞뒤로 또 양옆으로 밀려들며 베들럼으로 옮겨 가기를 권했다. 바깥 마당에서 한순간 소용돌이가 인 뒤, 감옥 문이 열렸다가 그들을 안에 들이고 닫혔다. 바깥의 소음에 견주어 피난처이자 평온의 장소처럼 보이는 수위실에서는 노란 등불이 벌써 감옥의 그늘과 다투고 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 존!\" 그들을 들인 간수가 말했다. \"무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"아무 일도 아니에요, 아버지. 그저 이 부인이 길을 모르셔서 애들한테 시달리고 계셨어요. 누구를 찾으십니까, 부인?\"\n\n\"도릿 양이요. 여기 있나요?\"\n\n젊은이는 더욱 흥미를 보였다. \"예, 여기 있습니다. 성함이 어떻게 되십니까?\"\n\n\"클레넘 부인이에요.\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨의 모친이십니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다.\n\n그녀는 입술을 꾹 다물고 망설였다. \"그래요. 그분의 어머니라고 전해 주는 편이 낫겠군요.\"\n\n\"보십시오,\" 젊은이가 말했다. \"지금 교도소장 가족이 시골에 살고 있어서, 소장이 도릿 양에게 자기 집 방 하나를 마음대로 쓰라고 내주었습니다. 그리로 올라가시고, 제가 도릿 양을 데려오는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n그녀가 동의를 표하자, 그는 문 하나를 열고 옆 계단을 따라 그녀를 위층의 살림집으로 안내했다. 그는 그녀를 어둑해지는 방으로 들여보내고 떠났다. 그 방은 어둑해지는 감옥 마당을 내려다보고 있었는데, 그 안의 수감자들은 여기저기 거닐기도 하고, 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 떠나가는 친구들과 되도록 떨어진 채 이야기를 나누기도 하며, 대체로 그 여름 저녁을 저마다 형편껏 갇힌 시간을 견뎌 내고 있었다. 공기는 무겁고 더웠으며, 그곳의 답답함은 짓누르듯 했다. 그리고 바깥에서는 자유로운 소리들의 쇄도가 솟아올랐는데, 마치 두통과 가슴앓이 속에서 떠오르는 그런 것들에 대한 거슬리는 기억 같았다. 그녀가 어리둥절한 채 창가에 서서, 마치 자신만의 또 다른 감옥에서 내다보듯 이 감옥을 내려다보고 있을 때, 놀라움을 담은 부드러운 한두 마디 말에 그녀는 흠칫했고, 리틀 도릿이 그녀 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"클레넘 부인, 정말로 이렇게 다행히도 회복하셔서—\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 말을 멈췄다. 그녀를 향한 그 얼굴에는 다행스러움도 건강함도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"이건 회복이 아니에요. 기력도 아니고요. 나도 이게 무엇인지 모르겠어요.\" 그녀는 동요한 듯 손을 내저으며 그 모든 것을 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다. \"당신에게 맡겨진 꾸러미가 있죠, 오늘 밤 이곳이 문을 닫기 전에 되찾아 가지 않으면 아서에게 건네주기로 한 것 말이에요.\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"내가 그것을 되찾겠어요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그것을 품에서 꺼내 그녀의 손에 건넸다. 그녀의 손은 그것을 받은 뒤에도 내민 채로 있었다.\n\n\"그 안에 든 게 무엇인지 짐작하나요?\"\n\n그녀 자신이 말했듯 기력이라 할 수 없는, 그리고 마치 그림이나 조각상이 살아 움직이는 듯 보기에 비현실적인 그 새로운 운신의 힘을 지닌 채 그녀가 거기 있다는 데 겁을 먹은 리틀 도릿은 \"아니요\"라고 대답했다.\n\n\"읽어 봐요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 여전히 내밀려 있는 손에서 꾸러미를 받아 봉인을 뜯었다. 그러자 클레넘 부인은 자기 앞으로 되어 있는 안쪽 꾸러미를 그녀에게 주고, 다른 하나는 쥐고 있었다. 정오에도 방을 어둑하게 만드는 담과 감옥 건물들의 그림자는, 황혼이 빠르게 짙어지는 가운데, 창가를 빼고는 거기서 글을 읽기에 너무 어둡게 만들었다. 환한 여름 저녁 하늘이 조금이나마 그녀에게 비쳐 드는 창가에 리틀 도릿은 서서 읽었다. 놀라움과 공포의 끊긴 탄성을 한두 번 내뱉은 뒤로, 그녀는 말없이 읽었다. 다 읽고 나서 그녀가 주위를 둘러보자, 옛 안주인은 그녀 앞에 몸을 숙였다.\n\n\"이제, 내가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠죠.\"\n\n\"그런 것 같아요. 그런 것 같아 두려워요. 다만 제 마음이 너무도 다급하고, 너무도 슬프고, 가엾이 여길 것이 너무 많아서, 제가 읽은 모든 것을 다 따라가지는 못했어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"당신에게서 감춰 온 것을 당신에게 되돌려 주겠어요. 나를 용서해 줘요. 용서할 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"용서할 수 있어요, 그리고 하느님께서 아시듯이 용서해요! 제 옷에 입 맞추고 제게 무릎 꿇지 마세요. 부인은 제게 무릎 꿇기엔 너무 연로하세요. 그러지 않으셔도 저는 기꺼이 용서합니다.\"\n\n\"아직 더 청할 것이 있어요.\"\n\n\"그런 자세로는 안 돼요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"부인의 백발이 제 머리보다 낮게 있는 걸 보는 건 자연스럽지 않아요. 부디 일어나세요. 제가 도와드릴게요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그녀를 일으켜 세웠고, 그녀에게서 다소 움츠러든 채 서 있으면서도 그녀를 간절히 바라보았다.\n\n\"내가 당신에게 드리는 큰 청은 (거기서 비롯되는 또 하나가 있어요), 당신의 자비롭고 부드러운 마음에 올리는 큰 간구는, 내가 죽을 때까지 이것을 아서에게 밝히지 말아 달라는 거예요. 곰곰이 생각할 시간을 가진 뒤에, 내가 아직 살아 있는 동안 그가 이를 아는 것이 그에게 조금이라도 이로우리라 여겨지면, 그때는 그에게 말해요. 하지만 당신은 그렇게 여기지 않을 거예요. 그렇다면, 내가 죽을 때까지 나를 봐주겠다고 약속해 주겠어요?\"\n\n\"제가 너무도 안타깝고, 읽은 것이 제 생각을 너무도 어지럽혀 놔서,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"확고한 답을 드리기가 어려워요. 만약 이를 아는 것이 클레넘 씨에게 아무런 이로움도 되지 않으리라 제가 확신하게 된다면—\"\n\n\"당신이 그를 애틋이 여기고 있다는 걸, 그를 가장 먼저 헤아리리라는 걸 나도 알아요. 그가 가장 먼저 고려되는 게 마땅하지요. 그걸 청하는 거예요. 다만 그를 헤아린 뒤에도, 내가 이 땅에 머물 짧은 시간 동안 나를 봐줄 수 있다고 여전히 여긴다면, 그렇게 해 주겠어요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하느님께서 당신을 축복하시기를!\"\n그녀는 그늘 속에 서 있어서, 빛 속에 있는 리틀 도릿에게는 베일에 가린 형체일 뿐이었다. 그러나 그 고마움 어린 세 마디 말을 할 때 그녀의 목소리는 간절하면서도 끊겨 있었으니—그 얼어붙은 눈에 행동이 낯설듯, 그 얼어붙은 사지에 동작이 낯설듯, 감정에 끊긴 것이었다.\n\n\"아마 의아하겠지요,\" 그녀가 더 힘찬 어조로 말했다. \"내가 해를 끼친 당신에게 알려지는 것이, 나를 해한 내 원수의 아들에게 알려지는 것보다 더 견디기 쉽다는 게요.—그래요, 그 여자는 정말로 나를 해쳤어요! 그 여자는 주님께 끔찍한 죄를 지었을 뿐 아니라, 나를 해쳤어요. 아서의 아버지가 내게 어떤 사람이 되었는지, 그건 그 여자가 만든 거예요. 우리의 혼인날부터 나는 그의 두려움이었고, 그건 그 여자가 만든 일이에요. 나는 두 사람 모두의 채찍이었고, 그것은 그 여자의 탓이에요. 당신은 아서를 사랑하지요 (당신 얼굴에 떠오르는 홍조가 보여요. 그것이 두 사람 모두에게 더 행복한 날들의 새벽이기를!), 그리고 당신은 이미 그가 당신만큼이나 자비롭고 다정하다고 생각했겠지요, 그런데 어째서 나는 당신에게 그러듯 그에게는 선뜻 나를 맡기지 못하느냐고요. 그렇게 생각하지 않았나요?\"\n\n\"클레넘 씨가 다정하고 너그럽고 선하다는 점에서 언제나 믿을 만한 분이라는 앎에서 솟아나는 어떤 생각도,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"제 마음에 영 낯설 수는 없어요.\"\n\n\"나도 의심하지 않아요. 그렇지만 아서는, 온 세상에서, 내가 이 세상에 있는 동안 이것을 숨기고 싶은 단 한 사람이에요. 그가 처음 기억하던 시절, 어린아이였던 그에게 나는 절제하고 바로잡는 손을 내내 얹어 두었어요. 부모의 죄과가 그 자손에게 미친다는 것을 알았기에, 그리고 그가 태어날 때부터 노여움의 표지가 그에게 새겨져 있었기에, 나는 그에게 엄했어요. 나는 그와 그의 아버지와 함께 앉아, 그의 아버지의 나약함이 그에게 마음을 풀어 놓기를 갈망하는 것을 보면서, 그것을 도로 억눌렀어요. 그 아이가 속박과 고난 속에서 자신의 구원을 이뤄 내도록 말이에요. 나는 그가, 제 어미의 얼굴을 한 채, 작은 책에서 눈을 들어 두려움에 차 나를 올려다보며, 나를 굳어지게 만든 제 어미의 방식으로 나를 누그러뜨리려 애쓰는 것을 보았어요.\"\n\n회상에 잠긴 음울한 목소리로 쏟아 내던 그녀의 말은, 듣는 이가 움츠러드는 바람에 한순간 멈췄다.\n\n\"그의 선을 위해서였어요. 내가 받은 상처에 대한 만족을 위해서가 아니라. 하늘의 저주 앞에서 나는 무엇이며, 그것에 무슨 값어치가 있겠어요! 나는 그 아이가 자라는 것을 보았어요. 정해진 방식으로 경건해지지는 못했지만 (그러기엔 제 어미의 영향이 그에게 너무 무겁게 드리워 있었어요), 그래도 정직하고 올곧게, 그리고 내게 순종하며 자라는 것을요. 그는, 내가 한때 반쯤 바랐던 만큼 나를 사랑한 적이 없어요—우리는 그토록 연약하고, 육신의 타락한 정은 우리의 본분과 소임과 그토록 다투지요. 그러나 그는 언제나 나를 존경했고, 나에게 본분을 다해 자신을 다스렸어요. 지금 이 순간까지도 그래요. 그는 자신이 그 뜻을 한 번도 알지 못한 텅 빈 자리를 가슴에 품은 채, 나에게서 등을 돌려 제 길을 떠나갔지만, 그것조차 사려 깊게, 또 공손하게 했어요. 이것이 나에 대한 그의 처신이었어요. 당신의 처신은 훨씬 가벼운 것이었고, 훨씬 짧은 시간에 걸친 것이었지요. 당신이 내 방에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있을 때, 당신은 나를 두려워했지만, 내가 당신에게 친절을 베풀고 있다고 여겼어요. 이제 당신은 더 잘 알게 되었고, 내가 당신에게 해를 끼쳤다는 것을 알아요. 내가 이 일을 이뤄 낸 그 명분과 동기에 대한 당신의 오해와 그릇된 짐작은, 그의 것이 될 오해보다 견디기 한결 가벼워요. 내가 상상할 수 있는 그 어떤 세속의 보상을 준다 해도, 그가 한순간에, 아무리 무턱대고라도, 평생 그의 앞에서 내가 지켜 온 자리에서 나를 끌어내려, 나를 자기 존경에서 내쳐 버릴 무언가로, 들통나고 폭로된 것으로 여길 무언가로 송두리째 바꿔 버리게 두지는 않을 거예요. 정 그래야 한다면, 내가 그것을 볼 자리에 없을 때 그가 그렇게 하게 하세요. 내가 아직 살아 있는 동안, 그의 면전에서 죽어 가는 것을, 벼락에 타 버리고 지진에 삼켜진 자처럼 그에게서 송두리째 사라져 버리는 것을 결코 느끼지 않게 해 주세요.\"\n\n이렇게 자신을 표현할 때, 그녀 안의 자존심은 매우 강했고, 그 자존심의 고통과 그녀의 오랜 격정의 고통은 그녀에게 매우 날카로웠다. 그녀가 다음 말을 덧붙일 때도 그에 못지않았다.\n\n\"지금조차, 마치 내가 잔인하기라도 했던 것처럼, 당신이 나에게서 움츠러드는 게 보여요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그것을 부인할 수 없었다. 그녀는 드러내지 않으려 했으나, 그토록 맹렬히 타오르고 그토록 오래 지속된 그 마음의 상태에서 두려움에 차 물러섰다. 그것은 아무런 궤변도 덧입지 않은, 제 그대로의 적나라한 본성으로 그녀에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"나는,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"내게 주어진 일을 했어요. 나는 악에 맞섰지, 선에 맞선 게 아니에요. 나는 죄악에 대한 준엄함의 도구였어요. 나 같은 한낱 죄인들이 모든 시대에 걸쳐 죄악을 무너뜨리도록 위임받지 않았던가요?\"\n\n\"모든 시대에 걸쳐서요?\" 리틀 도릿이 되뇌었다.\n\n\"설령 내 자신의 상처가 내 안에서 우세했고 내 자신의 복수심이 나를 움직였다 한들, 내가 아무런 정당함도 찾을 수 없었을까요? 무고한 자가 죄 있는 자와 함께, 천 대 하나로 멸하던 옛 시절에는 그런 게 없었을까요? 의롭지 못한 자를 미워하는 이의 분노가 피로도 가시지 않았으면서도 도리어 가납받던 그 시절에는요?\"\n\n\"오, 클레넘 부인, 클레넘 부인,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"노여운 감정과 용서 없는 행위는 부인께도 저에게도 위안이 못 되고 인도가 못 돼요. 제 삶은 이 가련한 감옥에서 보내졌고, 제가 받은 가르침은 매우 부족했어요. 그러나 부디 더 나중의, 더 나은 날들을 기억해 주시기를 간청해요. 병든 자를 고치시고, 죽은 자를 일으키시고, 고통받고 버림받은 모든 이의 벗이 되시고, 우리의 연약함을 가엾이 여겨 눈물을 흘리신 인내의 주님, 오직 그분께만 인도받으세요. 나머지를 모두 치우고, 그분을 기억하며 모든 일을 행한다면, 우리는 옳을 수밖에 없어요. 그분의 삶에는 복수도, 고통을 가하는 일도 없다는 걸 저는 확신해요. 그분을 따르며 다른 어떤 발자취도 구하지 않는다면 거기에 어떤 혼란도 있을 수 없다는 걸 저는 확신해요.\"\n\n창가의 부드러워진 빛 속에서, 자신의 어린 시절 시련의 무대로부터 빛나는 하늘로 눈길을 돌리며 서 있는 그녀는, 그녀가 의지하는 삶과 가르침이 그 형체의 내력과 대립하는 것보다 결코 덜하지 않게, 그늘 속의 검은 형체와 강하게 대립하고 있었다. 그 형체는 다시 고개를 낮게 숙이고 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그것은 첫 경고 종이 울리기 시작할 때까지 그렇게 있었다.\n\n\"들어 봐요!\" 클레넘 부인이 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다. \"내게 또 다른 청이 있다고 했지요. 그건 지체를 용납하지 않는 거예요. 당신에게 이 꾸러미를 가져다주었고 이 증거들을 쥐고 있는 그 사내가, 지금 내 집에서 매수당하기를 기다리고 있어요. 그를 매수해야만 이것을 아서에게 숨길 수 있어요. 그는 거액을 요구하는데, 시간을 두지 않고는 그에게 지불할 만큼 마련할 수 없는 액수예요. 그는 한 푼도 깎아 주려 하지 않아요. 나와 거래가 안 되면 당신에게 오겠다고 으름장을 놓으니까요. 나와 함께 돌아가서 당신이 이미 이를 알고 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주겠어요? 나와 함께 돌아가서 그를 설득해 보겠어요? 가서 그를 상대하는 일을 나와 함께 도와주겠어요? 비록 아서를 위해서라고는 감히 청하지 못하지만, 아서의 이름으로 내가 청하는 것을 거절하지 말아 줘요!\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 기꺼이 응했다. 그녀는 잠시 감옥 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다가 돌아와, 갈 채비가 되었다고 말했다. 그들은 수위실을 피해 다른 계단으로 빠져나왔고, 이제는 온통 고요하고 인적 없는 앞마당으로 나와 거리로 들어섰다.\n\n긴 황혼보다 더 깊은 어둠이 없는, 그런 여름 저녁의 하나였다. 거리와 다리의 전경이 또렷이 보였고, 하늘은 잔잔하고 아름다웠다. 사람들은 제집 문간에 서거나 앉아, 아이들과 놀고 저녁을 즐기고 있었다. 바람을 쐬러 거니는 이들이 무수히 많았다. 하루의 분주함은 거의 제풀에 잦아들었고, 그들 자신을 빼면 서두르는 이가 드물었다. 그들이 다리를 건널 때, 숱한 교회의 또렷한 첨탑들은 마치 평소 그것들을 감싸던 음울함에서 벗어나 한결 가까이 다가온 듯 보였다. 하늘로 피어오르는 연기는 그 칙칙한 빛을 잃고 밝음을 띠었다. 지평선에 평화로이 누운 길고 가느다란 구름 막에는 석양의 아름다움이 아직 바래지 않았다. 빛나는 한 중심에서, 고요한 창공의 전 길이와 너비에 걸쳐, 커다란 빛줄기들이 일찍 뜬 별들 사이로 뻗어 나왔으니, 가시 면류관을 영광으로 바꾸어 놓은, 평화와 희망의 그 복된 새 언약의 표지 같았다.\n\n이제는 혼자가 아니고 더 어두워진 까닭에 덜 눈에 띈 채, 클레넘 부인은 리틀 도릿 곁에서 시달림 없이 서둘러 나아갔다. 그들은 그녀가 들어섰던 길모퉁이에서 큰 한길을 벗어나, 고요하고 텅 빈 골목길들 사이로 굽이굽이 내려갔다. 그들의 발이 대문께에 이르렀을 때, 천둥 같은 갑작스러운 굉음이 났다.\n\n\"저게 뭐죠! 어서 안으로 들어가요,\" 클레넘 부인이 외쳤다.\n\n그들은 대문 안에 있었다. 리틀 도릿이 찢어지는 듯한 비명을 지르며 그녀를 붙들어 막았다.\n\n순식간의 한 찰나, 그 옛집이 그들 앞에 있었고, 한 사내가 창가에 누워 담배를 피우고 있었다. 또 한 번 천둥 같은 소리가 나더니, 집이 들썩이며 바깥으로 부풀어 오르듯 솟구치고, 쉰 군데에서 갈라져 무너지며 쓰러졌다. 굉음에 귀가 먹먹해지고, 먼지에 숨이 막히고 목이 메고 눈이 멀어, 그들은 얼굴을 가린 채 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다. 그들과 잔잔한 하늘 사이를 휘몰아치던 먼지 폭풍이 한순간 갈라지며 별들을 보여 주었다. 그들이 미친 듯 도움을 청하며 위를 올려다보았을 때, 회오리바람 속의 탑처럼 그때 홀로 남아 서 있던 그 거대한 굴뚝 무더기가 흔들리다 부서지더니, 떨어져 내리는 모든 조각이 짓눌린 가련한 자를 더 깊이 묻어 버리려 작정한 듯, 폐허 더미 위로 우박처럼 쏟아져 내렸다.\n\n날아드는 잔해 부스러기에 새카맣게 뒤덮여 알아볼 수 없게 된 채, 그들은 대문에서 거리로 도로 달려 나오며 울부짖고 비명을 질렀다. 거기서 클레넘 부인이 돌바닥 위에 쓰러졌다. 그리고 그 시각 이후로 그녀는 다시는 손가락 하나조차 움직이지 못했고, 한마디도 말할 힘을 잃었다. 삼 년 남짓 그녀는 바퀴 달린 의자에 기대앉아, 주위 사람들을 유심히 바라보며 그들이 하는 말을 알아듣는 듯 보였다. 그러나 그토록 오래 지켜 온 그 굳은 침묵이 이제는 영영 그녀에게 강제되었고, 눈을 움직이고 머리로 희미하게 부정과 긍정을 표할 수 있는 것 말고는, 그녀는 조각상으로 살다 조각상으로 죽었다.\n\n애프리는 감옥에서 그들을 찾고 있다가, 다리 위 멀리서 그들을 발견했다. 그녀는 옛 안주인을 두 팔로 받아 안으려, 인근의 집으로 옮기는 것을 도우려, 그리고 그녀에게 충실하려 다가왔다. 그 소리들의 수수께끼는 이제 풀렸다. 애프리는, 더 대단한 사람들이 그러하듯, 사실에 관해서는 늘 옳았으나, 그 사실에서 끌어낸 이론에 관해서는 늘 그릇되어 있었다.\n\n먼지 폭풍이 걷히고 여름밤이 다시 잠잠해지자, 무수한 사람들이 접근로란 접근로를 모조리 메웠고, 폐허 사이를 파헤치며 교대로 일할 발굴 패들이 꾸려졌다. 집이 무너질 때 그 안에 백 명이 있었다고도, 쉰 명이 있었다고도, 열다섯 명이 있었다고도, 둘이 있었다고도 했다. 소문은 마침내 그 수를 둘로 정착시켰다. 그 외국인과 플린트윈치 씨였다.\n\n발굴자들은 짧은 밤 내내 활활 타오르는 가스관 불빛에 의지해 파 내려갔고, 일찍 떠오른 해와 나란한 높이에서도, 해가 중천으로 솟아오를수록 그보다 더 깊고 깊은 곳에서도, 해가 기울 때는 그에 비스듬히, 해가 저물 때는 다시 그와 나란한 높이에서 파 내려갔다. 억센 파헤침과 삽질, 그리고 수레와 손수레와 바구니로 실어 나르는 일이 밤낮없이 쉼 없이 이어졌다. 그러나 그들이, 자기를 짓누르며 깔린 큰 들보에 머리가 마치 유리 조각처럼 산산이 부서지기 전의 그 외국인이었던 더러운 잔해 더미를 찾아냈을 때는, 다시 두 번째로 맞이한 밤이었다.\n\n그래도 그들은 아직 플린트윈치는 찾아내지 못했다. 그래서 억센 파헤침과 삽질과 실어 나르기가 밤낮없이 쉼 없이 이어졌다. 그 옛집에 훌륭한 지하실이 있었다는 소문이 퍼졌고 (실제로 사실이었다), 플린트윈치가 그 순간 지하실에 있었거나 지하실로 피할 틈이 있었으리라는, 그래서 그 튼튼한 아치 밑에 무사히 있으리라는, 심지어 그가 \"나 여기 있소!\"라고 공허하고 지하 깊은 곳에서 나오는 듯 숨 막힌 소리로 외치는 것을 들었다는 소문까지 돌았다. 도시 반대편 끝에서는, 발굴자들이 관을 통해 그와 연락을 틀 수 있었고, 그가 그 통로로 수프와 브랜디를 받았으며, 그가 감탄스러운 의연함으로 쇄골만 빼면 멀쩡하다고, 다들 괜찮네, 친구들 하고 말했다는 소문까지 퍼졌다. 그러나 파헤침과 삽질과 실어 나르기는 폐허가 다 파헤쳐지고 지하실들이 빛에 드러날 때까지 쉼 없이 이어졌다. 그래도 플린트윈치는, 산 채로든 죽은 채로든, 멀쩡하든 엉망이든, 곡괭이나 삽에 들춰지지 않았다.\n\n그제야 사람들은 플린트윈치가 무너질 당시 거기에 없었음을 알아채기 시작했다. 그리고 그가 다른 곳에서 꽤나 바삐 움직이고 있었음을, 곧 증권들을 가장 짧은 통고로 받아낼 수 있는 한 많은 돈으로 바꾸고, 회사를 대리할 자기 권한을 자기 한 사람의 잇속으로 돌리고 있었음을 알아채기 시작했다. 애프리는, 그 영리한 자가 스물네 시간 안에 자신을 더 자세히 설명해 주겠다고 말했던 것을 떠올리며, 그자가 그 기간 안에 챙길 수 있는 모든 것을 가지고 사라져 버린 것이야말로 그가 약속한 설명의 최종적이고 흡족한 총괄이자 알맹이라고 나름대로 단정했다. 그러나 그녀는 그자에게서 벗어난 것을 진심으로 감사하며 잠자코 있었다. 한 번도 묻힌 적 없는 사람이 파묻혀 나올 수는 없다고 결론짓는 것이 이치에 맞는 듯하여, 발굴자들은 일을 마치자 그를 단념했고, 땅속 깊은 곳까지 그를 찾아 파 내려가지는 않았다.\n\n이것을 많은 사람들이 못마땅하게 받아들였으니, 그들은 플린트윈치가 런던의 지질층 어딘가에 누워 있다고 끝끝내 믿었다. 그들의 믿음은, 한쪽 귀 밑에 목도리 매듭을 묶고 다니는, 영국인임이 매우 잘 알려진 어느 노인이, 헤이그의 운치 있는 운하 둑과 암스테르담의 술집들에서, 미인헤이르 폰 플린테빈게라는 이름과 호칭으로 네덜란드인들과 어울려 다닌다는 소식이 세월이 흐르며 거듭 전해져 와도, 그다지 흔들리지 않았다.\n\n# CHAPTER 32. Going\n\n아서가 마셜시에서 여전히 위중하게 누워 있었고, 러그 씨로서는 그의 석방에 희망을 줄 법적 하늘의 틈새가 도무지 보이지 않았으므로, 팽크스 씨는 자책에 처참하게 시달렸다. 아서가 옥에 갇혀 야위어 갈 것이 아니라 쌍두마차를 타고 산책을 다녀야 마땅하며, 팽크스 씨도 서기 봉급에 묶일 것이 아니라 삼천에서 오천 파운드쯤은 제 손으로 당장 굴릴 수 있어야 한다는 그 틀림없는 숫자가 아니었더라면, 이 불행한 산술가는 아마 자리보전을 하고 누워, 고인이 된 머들 씨의 위대함에 바치는 마지막 제물로서 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 죽어 간 수많은 이름 없는 이들 가운데 하나가 되었을 것이다. 오직 그 반박할 수 없는 계산에만 떠받쳐진 채, 팽크스 씨는 불행하고 안절부절못하는 삶을 이어 갔다. 그는 늘 그 숫자들을 모자 속에 넣고 다니며 틈만 나면 스스로 되짚어 볼 뿐 아니라, 손에 잡히는 사람이라면 누구에게나 함께 따져 보고 이것이 얼마나 명백한 사건인지 보아 달라고 간청했다. 블리딩 하트 야드에는 팽크스 씨가 그 증명을 들려주지 않은 변변한 주민이 거의 없었고, 숫자란 옮는 법이라, 그 일대에 일종의 셈하기 홍역이 번져 야드 전체가 들떠서 정신을 못 차렸다.\n\n팽크스 씨는 마음속이 안절부절못할수록 그 가장에게 더욱 참을성을 잃어 갔다. 나중의 회담에서 그의 씩씩거리는 콧소리는 가장에게 결코 좋을 것 없는 짜증스러운 기색을 띠었다. 또한 팽크스 씨는 여러 차례, 자기가 화가도 아니고 생모델을 찾는 가발장이도 아니라는 사실과는 도무지 어울리지 않을 만큼 그 가장의 머리 혹들을 뚫어지게 쳐다보곤 했다.\n\n그러나 그는 가장 앞에 불려 가느냐 마느냐에 따라 자기의 작은 뒤편 선거를 들락날락 증기를 뿜으며 드나들었고, 일은 늘 하던 대로 굴러갔다. 블리딩 하트 야드는 정해진 철마다 팽크스 씨에게 갈아엎어지고 캐스비 씨에게 거두어졌다. 팽크스 씨는 그 일의 온갖 고역과 온갖 더러움을 자기 몫으로 떠맡았고, 캐스비 씨는 온갖 이문과 온갖 영묘한 김과 온갖 헛소리를 제 몫으로 가져갔다. 그리고 그 자애로운 빛을 뿜는 사람이 한 주의 결산을 맞춰 놓고 통통한 엄지를 빙빙 돌리며 토요일 저녁마다 으레 쓰던 말로 하자면, \"모든 일이 모두에게 만족스러웠습니다 — 모두에게 — 만족스러웠지요, 선생, 모두에게 말입니다.\"\n\n증기 예인선 팽크스의 선거에는 납지붕이 덮여 있었는데, 무척 더운 햇볕에 지글지글 달구어진 그것이 어쩌면 그 배까지 데웠는지도 몰랐다. 어쨌든 어느 이글거리는 토요일 저녁, 그 묵직한 병녹색 배에게 부름을 받자 예인선은 곧장 잔뜩 달아오른 상태로 선거에서 빠져나왔다.\n\n\"팽크스 씨,\" 가장의 발언이었다. \"자네 게을렀어, 게을렀단 말이야, 선생.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\" 짤막한 응수였다.\n\n가장의 상태는 늘 침착하고 차분한 상태였지만, 그날 저녁만은 유난히 평온하여 사람을 약 올릴 지경이었다. 사망 통계표 안의 다른 모든 이가 더위에 시달렸으나, 가장은 더할 나위 없이 시원했다. 모두가 목이 말랐으나, 가장은 마시고 있었다. 그에게서는 라임이나 레몬 향이 감돌았고, 그는 황금빛 셰리주를 큼직한 잔에 따라 마셨는데, 마치 저녁 햇살을 마시는 것처럼 잔 속에서 그것이 빛났다. 이것만으로도 고약했으나, 가장 고약한 것은 따로 있었다. 가장 고약한 것은, 그 큼직한 푸른 눈과 윤기 도는 머리통과 길고 흰 머리카락, 그리고 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 병녹색 다리가 발등께에서 느긋이 포개진 편안한 신발로 끝나는 그 모습으로, 마치 그가 자신의 드넓은 자비심으로 인류를 위해 그 음료를 만들어 두고서, 정작 자기 자신은 제 안의 인정이라는 젖 말고는 아무것도 원하지 않는다는 듯 환히 빛나는 자태였다는 점이다.\n\n그리하여 팽크스 씨는 \"그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\" 하고 말하며 두 손으로 머리카락을 자못 불길한 모양새로 곤두세웠다.\n\n\"내 말은, 팽크스 씨, 자네가 사람들한테 더 매섭게 굴어야 한다는 거야, 더 매섭게, 훨씬 더 매섭게 굴어야 해, 선생. 자네는 그들을 쥐어짜지 않아. 쥐어짜지 않는단 말이야. 자네 수금액이 기준에 못 미쳐. 쥐어짜야 해, 선생, 안 그러면 우리 거래가 내가 바라는 만큼 모두에게 만족스럽게 이어지지 않을 거야. 모두에게 말이지.\"\n\n\"제가 쥐어짜지 않는다고요?\" 팽크스 씨가 되받았다. \"그럼 제가 달리 무엇에 쓰이려고 만들어졌습니까?\"\n\n\"자네는 달리 아무것에도 쓰이려고 만들어진 게 아니야, 팽크스 씨. 자네는 자기 본분을 다하라고 만들어졌는데, 본분을 다하지 않아. 자네는 쥐어짜라고 삯을 받는 거고, 삯을 받자면 쥐어짜야 하는 거야.\" 가장은 전혀 예상하지도 의도하지도 않았던 이 존슨 박사풍의 빛나는 표현에 스스로도 어찌나 놀랐던지 소리 내어 웃었다. 그러고는 엄지를 빙빙 돌리며 제 젊은 시절 초상화를 향해 고개를 끄덕이고는, 자못 흡족하게 되풀이했다. \"쥐어짜라고 삯을 받고, 삯을 받자면 쥐어짜야 하는 거야, 선생.\"\n\n\"오,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"또 있습니까?\"\n\n\"있지, 선생, 있고말고. 더 있어. 팽크스 씨, 월요일 아침에 제일 먼저 그 야드를 다시 쥐어짜 주게나.\"\n\n\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그건 너무 이르지 않습니까? 오늘 바싹 말라붙도록 쥐어짰는데요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야, 선생. 기준 근처에도 못 갔어, 근처에도.\"\n\n\"오!\" 팽크스가, 그가 그 혼합 음료를 한 모금 자애롭게 꿀꺽 들이켜는 것을 지켜보며 말했다. \"또 있습니까?\"\n\n\"있지, 선생, 있어, 더 있어. 팽크스 씨, 나는 내 딸이 도무지 마음에 들지 않아. 도무지 마음에 안 들어. 클레넘 부인의 안부를 너무 자주 물으러 다니는 것도 모자라 — 클레넘 부인은 지금 무슨 일이든 모두에게 만족스러울 만한 그런 처지가 결코 못 되는데 말이야 — 그 애가, 팽크스 씨, 내가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 옥에 갇힌 클레넘 씨의 안부까지 물으러 다닌단 말이지. 옥에 말이야.\"\n\n\"그가 앓아누웠으니까요.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"아마 친절을 베푸는 걸 겁니다.\"\n\n\"쳇, 쳇, 팽크스 씨. 그 애가 거기에 상관할 바 아니야, 상관할 바 아니라고. 나는 그걸 용납할 수 없어. 빚을 갚고 나오라고 해, 나오라고 해. 빚을 갚고 나오란 말이야.\"\n\n팽크스 씨의 머리카락은 빳빳한 철사처럼 곤두서 있었지만, 그는 두 손으로 그것을 다시 한 번 수직으로 곧추세우고는, 더없이 흉측한 모양새로 자기 고용주를 향해 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"내 딸한테 전해 주게나, 팽크스 씨, 나는 그걸 용납할 수 없다고, 용납할 수 없다고 말이야.\" 가장이 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"직접 말씀하실 수는 없습니까?\"\n\n\"없지, 선생, 없어. 자네가 그걸 전하라고 삯을 받는 거야.\" 그 얼간이 늙은이는 다시 한 번 써먹어 보고 싶은 유혹을 떨치지 못했다. \"그러니 삯을 받자면 그걸 전해야 하는 거지, 전해야 삯을 받는 거고.\"\n\n\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"또 있습니까?\"\n\n\"있지, 선생. 내가 보기엔 말이야, 팽크스 씨, 자네 자신이 그쪽으로 너무 자주, 너무 많이 드나드는 것 같아, 그쪽으로 말이야. 팽크스 씨, 나는 자네한테 권하건대, 자네 자신의 손실이든 남의 손실이든 다 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버리고, 자네 일에나 신경을 쓰게, 자네 일에나.\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 이 권고를 어찌나 유별나게 불쑥, 짧게, 그리고 큰 소리로 \"오!\"라는 한 음절로 받았던지, 그 둔중한 가장조차 푸른 눈을 다소 황급히 움직여 그를 쳐다볼 정도였다. 그러자 팽크스 씨는 그에 걸맞은 강도로 한 번 콧방귀를 뀌고는 덧붙였다. \"또 있습니까?\"\n\n\"지금은 없어, 선생, 지금은 없어. 나는 나가 봐야겠네.\" 가장이 혼합 음료를 다 비우고 상냥한 기색으로 일어서며 말했다. \"잠깐 산책이나 하려고, 잠깐 거닐려고 말이야. 돌아왔을 때 자네가 여기 있을지도 모르겠군. 만약 없다면, 선생, 본분이야, 본분. 월요일엔 쥐어짜, 쥐어짜, 쥐어짜라고. 월요일엔 쥐어짜!\"\n\n팽크스 씨는 머리카락을 한 번 더 빳빳이 세운 뒤, 가장이 챙 넓은 모자를 집어 쓰는 모습을, 한순간 망설임과 모욕감이 서로 다투는 듯한 표정으로 지켜보았다. 그는 또한 처음보다 더 달아올라 더 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬었다. 그러나 그는 더는 한마디도 건네지 않고 캐스비 씨가 나가도록 내버려 두었다가, 작은 초록색 차양 너머로 그를 슬쩍 엿보았다. \"그럴 줄 알았어.\" 그가 중얼거렸다. \"당신이 어디로 향할지 다 알고 있었지. 좋아!\" 그러고는 다시 증기를 뿜으며 자기 선거로 돌아가 그곳을 꼼꼼히 정돈하고, 모자를 내려 쓰고, 선거를 한 바퀴 둘러보더니, \"잘 있어라!\" 하고는 제 볼일을 보러 푹푹거리며 떠났다. 그는 블리딩 하트 야드의 플로니시 부인 쪽 끝을 향해 곧장 키를 잡았고, 그 계단 꼭대기에 도착했을 때는 어느 때보다도 더 달아올라 있었다.\n\n계단 꼭대기에서, 들어와 해피 코티지에서 아버님과 함께 앉아 있으라는 플로니시 부인의 권유를 — 토요일이라 그 권유가 다른 어느 밤보다는 적어서 그로서는 다행이었는데, 돈만 빼고 온갖 것으로 그토록 늠름하게 가게를 받쳐 주는 단골들이 토요일이면 거리낌 없이 주문을 넣었기 때문이다 — 뿌리치며, 팽크스 씨는 계단 꼭대기에 그대로 머물러, 늘 반대편 끝으로 야드에 들어서는 가장이 천천히 다가오는 모습을, 빛을 뿜으며 청원자들에게 둘러싸여 오는 모습을 보게 되었다. 그러자 팽크스 씨는 계단을 내려가, 증기를 한껏 압축해 그를 향해 들이닥쳤다.\n\n가장은 여느 때의 인자함으로 다가오다가 팽크스 씨를 보고 놀랐으나, 그가 그 쥐어짜는 작업을 월요일로 미루지 않고 당장 해치우고 싶어 자극받았으려니 짐작했다. 야드의 주민들은 그 만남에 깜짝 놀랐는데, 가장 나이 많은 블리딩 하트의 기억으로도 그 두 권력이 이곳에서 함께 있는 모습은 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 말로 다 할 수 없는 경악에 휩싸였으니, 팽크스 씨가 그 더없이 존경스러운 사람에게 바짝 다가가 병녹색 조끼 앞에 멈춰 서더니, 오른손 엄지와 검지로 방아쇠 모양을 만들어 챙 넓은 모자의 챙에 갖다 대고는, 유별나게 날렵하고 정확하게, 마치 커다란 구슬을 튕기듯 그 윤기 도는 머리통에서 모자를 튕겨 날려 버렸던 것이다.\n\n가장이라는 인물에게 이런 작은 무례를 범한 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 또렷이 들리는 목소리로 이렇게 말하여 블리딩 하트들을 한층 더 놀라게 하고 끌어들였다. \"자, 이 달콤한 사기꾼아, 내 너하고 끝장을 봐야겠다!\"\n\n팽크스 씨와 가장은 순식간에, 온통 눈과 귀뿐인 군중의 한복판이 되었다. 창문이 활짝 열리고 문간 계단이 사람들로 북적였다.\n\n\"당신은 자기를 뭐라고 내세웁니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"당신의 도덕 놀음은 뭡니까? 당신은 뭘 표방합니까? 자비, 그렇지요? 당신이 자비롭다고요!\" 여기서 팽크스 씨는, 그를 때릴 의도는 딱히 없이 그저 마음을 풀고 남아도는 힘을 건전한 운동으로 써 버리려는 양, 그 혹투성이 머리통을 향해 한 방 겨누었고, 혹투성이 머리통은 그것을 피하려 몸을 숙였다. 이 기묘한 동작은 팽크스 씨의 연설이 한 대목씩 끝날 때마다 되풀이되어, 구경꾼들의 감탄을 점점 더 키웠다.\n\n\"내가 당신 밑에서 스스로 그만두었으니,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"당신이 어떤 자인지 일러 주겠소. 당신은 세상에서 만날 수 있는 온갖 무리 가운데 가장 못된 무리에 속하는 사기꾼이오. 양쪽 다에 당한 사람으로서 말하건대, 나는 당신 무리보다는 차라리 머들 무리를 택하지 않겠다고 장담 못 하겠소. 당신은 변장한 몰이꾼이고, 대리로 죄어 대는 자고, 대신 시켜 짜내고 쥐어짜고 벗겨 먹는 자요. 당신은 자선가인 척하는 비열한 놈이오. 당신은 추레한 사기꾼이오!\"\n\n(이 대목에서 그 동작이 되풀이되자 웃음이 와르르 터졌다.)\n\n\"여기 이 선량한 사람들한테 누가 모진 사람이냐고 물어보시오. 다들 팽크스라고 할 거요, 내가 보기엔.\"\n\n이 말은 \"그렇고말고,\" \"옳소!\" 하는 외침으로 확인되었다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 일러 주리다, 선량한 양반들 — 캐스비요! 이 온순함의 더미, 이 사랑의 덩어리, 이 병녹색 미소꾼, 이자가 바로 당신들의 몰이꾼이오!\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"당신들을 산 채로 가죽 벗길 자를 보고 싶거든 — 바로 여기 있소! 주급 삼십 실링짜리인 나한테서 그자를 찾지 말고, 일 년에 얼마인지도 모를 캐스비한테서 그자를 찾으시오!\"\n\n\"옳소!\" 여러 목소리가 외쳤다. \"팽크스 씨 말을 들어 봅시다!\"\n\n\"팽크스 씨 말을 들어 보자고요?\" 그 신사가 (그 인기 있는 동작을 한 번 더 되풀이한 뒤) 외쳤다. \"그래요, 그래야 하고말고요! 이제 팽크스 씨 말을 들을 때도 됐지요. 팽크스 씨는 바로 당신들이 자기 말을 듣게 하려고 오늘 밤 이 야드로 내려온 거요. 팽크스는 그저 기계 장치일 뿐이오. 하지만 여기 태엽 감는 자가 있소!\"\n\n청중은 남자고 여자고 아이고 할 것 없이 한 몸이 되어 팽크스 씨 편으로 넘어갔을 테지만, 저 길고 잿빛인 비단 같은 머리타래와 챙 넓은 모자 때문에 망설였다.\n\n\"여기 멈춤쇠가 있소.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"갈아 낼 가락을 정해 주는 멈춤쇠 말이오. 그런데 그 가락은 단 하나뿐이고, 그 이름은 갈아라, 갈아라, 갈아라요! 여기 소유주가 있고, 여기 그자의 막일꾼이 있소. 자, 선량한 양반들, 이자가 오늘 밤 굼뜨고 자애로운 팽이처럼 야드를 매끄럽게 빙빙 돌며 다가올 때, 그리고 당신들이 막일꾼에 대한 불평을 들고 그를 에워쌀 때, 당신들은 그 소유주가 얼마나 사기꾼인지 알지 못하오! 월요일에 모든 비난을 내가 다 뒤집어쓰도록 이자가 오늘 밤 제 모습을 드러낸 것을 어떻게 생각하시오? 내가 당신들을 충분히 쥐어짜지 않는다고 바로 오늘 저녁에 이자가 나를 호되게 닦달한 것을 어떻게 생각하시오? 내가 지금 이 순간, 월요일에 당신들을 바싹 말라붙도록 쥐어짜라는 특별 명령을 받은 처지인 것을 어떻게 생각하시오?\"\n\n그 대답은 \"창피한 줄 알아라!\" \"추레하다!\" 하는 웅성거림으로 나왔다.\n\n\"추레하다고요?\" 팽크스가 콧방귀를 뀌었다. \"그래요, 그렇고말고요! 당신네 캐스비가 속한 무리는 온갖 무리 가운데서도 가장 추레한 무리요. 비참할 만큼 쥐꼬리만 한 삯을 주고 제 막일꾼들을 풀어, 자기들은 부끄럽고 두려워 못 하는 짓, 안 하는 척하면서도 기어이 시키고야 마는 짓, 안 하면 사람을 가만두지 않는 그런 짓을 하게 하지요! 당신들을 속여서, 그 막일꾼들한테는 비난만 안기고, 자기들한테는 공치사만 돌리게 하고 말이오! 아니, 이 온 도시에서 거짓 핑계로 일 실링 육 펜스어치를 우려내는 가장 흉악해 보이는 사기꾼도, 여기 이 '캐스비 머리' 간판만큼 사기꾼은 절반도 못 되오!\"\n\n\"맞는 말이오!\" \"그렇고말고요!\" 하는 외침이 터졌다.\n\n\"게다가 이런 자들한테서 당신들이 또 무엇을 얻는지 보시오.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"이 잘난 팽이들한테서 당신들이 또 무엇을 얻는지 보란 말이오. 어찌나 매끄럽게 당신들 사이를 빙빙 도는지, 그 위에 그려진 무늬도, 그 안의 작은 창문도 당신들은 짐작조차 못 하오. 잠깐 내게 주목해 주시오. 나는 호감 가는 종류의 사내가 아니오, 그건 나도 아주 잘 아오.\"\n\n청중은 이 점에서 의견이 갈렸으니, 더 타협 없는 축들은 \"아니, 당신은 그렇지 않소,\" 하고 외쳤고, 더 점잖은 부류는 \"맞소, 당신은 그렇소,\" 하고 외쳤다.\n\n\"나는 대체로,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"메마르고 거북하고 음울한, 터덜터덜 일하는 막일꾼이오. 그게 바로 당신들의 미천한 종이오. 여기 그자가 손수 그려 당신들에게 바치는, 똑 닮았다고 보증하는 전신 초상화가 있소! 그러나 이런 자를 소유주로 둔 사람이 무엇이 될 수 있겠소? 그에게서 무엇을 기대할 수 있겠소? 누가 코코넛 속에서 삶은 양고기에 케이퍼 소스가 자라는 걸 본 적이 있소?\"\n\n블리딩 하트 가운데 그런 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 그들의 잽싼 호응으로 보아 분명했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"그러니 나 같은 막일꾼한테서, 이런 자를 소유주로 둔 막일꾼한테서 당신들은 유쾌한 자질을 찾지 못할 거요. 나는 어릴 적부터 막일꾼이었소. 내 인생이 뭐였소? 죽도록 일하고 갈아 대고, 죽도록 일하고 갈아 대고, 바퀴를 돌리고 또 돌리고! 나는 나 자신한테도 유쾌하지 못했고, 남한테 유쾌할 리도 없었소. 십 년 뒤에 내가 주급 일 실링어치만큼 덜 쓸모 있어지면, 이 사기꾼은 내 주급을 일 실링 깎을 거요. 나만큼 쓸모 있는 사람을 육 펜스 더 싸게 구할 수 있으면, 내 자리에 그자를 육 펜스 더 싸게 들일 거요. 흥정하고 사고팔기지, 원 참! 확고한 원칙이라나! 참 훌륭한 간판이오, 이 '캐스비 머리'라는 건.\" 팽크스 씨가 감탄과는 전혀 거리가 먼 눈길로 그것을 훑어보며 말했다. \"하지만 이 집의 진짜 이름은 '가짜의 문장'이오. 그 좌우명은, 막일꾼을 늘 부려 먹어라요. 여기 계신 분 가운데,\" 팽크스 씨가 말을 끊고 둘러보며 말했다. \"영문법에 밝은 신사분이 있소?\"\n\n블리딩 하트 야드는 그런 데 밝다고 나서기를 꺼렸다.\n\n\"상관없소.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"그저 한마디 하고 싶은 건, 이 소유주가 내게 맡긴 과업이 '늘 부려 먹다'라는 동사의 명령법 현재 시제를 끊임없이 활용하는 일이었다는 거요. 너는 늘 부려 먹어라. 그로 하여금 늘 부려 먹게 하라. 우리는 늘 부려 먹자. 너희는 늘 부려 먹어라. 그들로 하여금 늘 부려 먹게 하라. 여기 캐스비라는 자애로운 가장이 있고, 저기 그자의 황금률이 있소. 그는 보기에 더없이 교화적인데, 나는 전혀 그렇지가 않소. 그는 꿀처럼 달고, 나는 도랑물처럼 따분하오. 그는 끈끈한 역청을 대 주고, 나는 그걸 만지고, 그게 내게 들러붙소. 자,\" 팽크스 씨가, 야드에 그를 더 잘 보여 주려고 잠시 물러나 있다가 다시 그 전직 소유주에게 바싹 다가서며 말했다. \"나는 사람들 앞에서 말하는 데 익숙지 않은 데다, 이런저런 사정을 다 따져도 꽤나 긴 연설을 했으니, 당신더러 여기서 꺼지라고 청하면서 내 소견을 마무리하겠소.\"\n\n가장들의 마지막 후예는 워낙 기습을 당한 데다, 한 가지 생각을 붙잡는 데도 그토록 많은 여유가 필요하고 그것을 굴리는 데는 더더욱 많은 여유가 필요한 위인이라, 대꾸할 말이 한마디도 없었다. 그가 이 난처한 처지에서 빠져나갈 무슨 가장다운 방도를 궁리하는 듯 보일 때, 팽크스 씨가 다시 한 번 불쑥 그의 모자에 방아쇠를 갖다 대어 예의 그 솜씨로 또 튕겨 날려 버렸다. 앞서 한 번은 블리딩 하트 야드 사람 한둘이 굽실거리며 그것을 주워 주인에게 건네주었으나, 팽크스 씨가 이제 청중을 어찌나 사로잡아 놓았던지 가장은 손수 돌아서서 몸을 굽혀 그것을 주워야 했다.\n\n번개처럼 빠르게, 한동안 오른손을 외투 주머니에 넣고 있던 팽크스 씨가 가위 한 자루를 휙 꺼내 가장의 등 뒤로 덮쳐들더니, 그의 어깨 위로 흘러내리던 그 신성한 머리타래를 싹둑 짧게 잘라 버렸다. 그러고는 적의와 신속함이 발작하듯 치솟아, 팽크스 씨는 어안이 벙벙해진 가장의 손에서 챙 넓은 모자를 낚아채 그것을 한낱 스튜 냄비 꼴로 잘라 내고는, 가장의 머리에 눌러 씌웠다.\n\n이 무모한 행동이 빚어낸 끔찍한 결과 앞에서, 팽크스 씨 자신도 경악하여 뒤로 물러섰다. 민머리에 눈이 휘둥그렇고 머리통이 큼직한 둔중한 인물이, 조금도 인상적이지도 조금도 위엄 있지도 않은 모습으로 그를 멀거니 쳐다보고 서 있었는데, 마치 땅속에서 불쑥 솟아나 캐스비가 어찌 되었느냐고 묻는 것만 같았다. 이 유령을 마주 멀거니 쳐다보며 말없이 두려움에 잠겼다가, 팽크스 씨는 가위를 내던지고 제 죄의 결과로부터 몸을 피해 숨을 곳을 찾아 달아났다. 블리딩 하트 야드에 울려 퍼지며 공중에 잔물결처럼 번져 다시금 메아리치는 웃음소리 말고는 아무것도 그를 뒤쫓지 않았으나, 팽크스 씨는 있는 힘껏 서둘러 달아나는 것이 현명하다고 여겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER 33. Going!\n\n열병에 시달리는 방 안의 변화는 더디고 출렁이지만, 열병에 시달리는 세상의 변화는 빠르고 돌이킬 수 없다.\n\n두 종류의 변화를 모두 보살피는 것이 리틀 도릿의 몫이었다. 하루의 일부 동안, 마셜시의 담장은 다시금 그 그림자로 그녀를 제 자식처럼 품었고, 그동안 그녀는 클레넘을 위해 궁리하고, 그를 위해 일하고, 그를 지켜보았으며, 오직 그에게 자신의 온 사랑과 정성을 쏟기 위해서만 그의 곁을 떠났다. 문 밖의 삶에서 맡은 그녀의 몫 또한 절박하게 권리를 주장하며 그녀를 다그쳤고, 그녀의 인내는 지치지 않고 거기에 응했다. 거기엔 패니가 있었다. 거만하고 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 그녀는, 거북 등딱지 손잡이 칼 사건이 있던 그 저녁 그토록 그녀를 안달하게 만들었던 그 사교계 부적격 상태로 한층 더 깊이 나아가, 늘 위안을 갈구하기로 작정하고, 결코 위안받지 않기로 작정하고, 깊이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여기기로 작정하고, 그러면서도 그 누구도 감히 자신을 그런 처지로 여겨서는 안 된다고 작정한 상태였다. 거기엔 그녀의 오라비도 있었다. 약하고 거만하며 술에 절은, 젊은 늙은이로, 머리부터 발끝까지 떨면서, 스스로 그토록 으스대던 그 돈의 일부가 입속에 들어가 빠져나오지 못하기라도 한 듯 발음이 불분명했고, 삶의 그 어떤 행동도 혼자서는 해내지 못했으며, 자기를 이끌어 주는 것을 허락한다는 이유로(불운하게 태어나 불운하게 출발한 팁! 그에게는 늘 그 소극적인 미덕만은 있었다) 이기적으로나마 사랑하는 누이를 짐짓 거두는 시늉을 했다. 거기엔 머들 부인이 얇은 상복 차림으로 있었다. 그 원래의 모자는 아마 슬픔의 발작 속에 갈가리 찢겼을지 모르나, 분명 파리 시장에서 온 무척 잘 어울리는 물건에 자리를 내준 상태였고, 그녀는 패니와 한 치도 물러섬 없이 다투며 하루 종일 시시각각 그 황량한 가슴을 들이대며 맞섰다. 거기엔 가엾은 스파클러 씨도 있었다. 그는 두 사람 사이의 평화를 어떻게 지켜야 할지 몰라, 두 분 다 대단히 훌륭한 여성이며 어느 쪽에도 허튼 구석이라곤 없다는 데에 의견을 모으는 것보다 더 나은 길은 없으리라는 의견으로 겸손히 기울었으나, 그 점잖은 권유에 대해 두 사람은 한데 뭉쳐 그에게 무섭게 달려들었다. 그리고 또한 거기엔 제너럴 부인이 있었다. 외국에서 돌아와, 하루걸러 자두와 프리즘을 우편으로 보내며, 이러저러한 빈자리의 일자리에 추천받겠다는 명목으로 새 추천장을 요구했다. 이 주목할 만한 귀부인에 관해 마지막으로 일러둘 만한 것은, 이 지상의 그 어떤 빈자리에든 그녀가 더없이 어울린다는 데 대해 그토록 많은 사람이(그녀의 추천장들의 그 열렬함이 증명하듯) 그토록 완벽하게 만족한 귀부인은 일찍이 없었으리라는 점이며, 또한 열정적이고 저명한 찬미자들을 큰 무리로 거느렸으되 그들 가운데 누구도 정작 그녀를 어떤 직책으로든 필요로 한 적이 없었으니, 그토록 운이 없는 귀부인 또한 일찍이 없었으리라는 점이다.\n\n저명한 머들 씨의 별세라는 첫 충격이 닥쳤을 때, 많은 중요 인사들은 머들 부인과 절교해야 할지 위로해야 할지 결정을 내리지 못했다. 그러나 그녀가 잔인하게 속았다고 인정하는 것이 자기들 입장의 근거를 굳히는 데 긴요해 보였으므로, 그들은 너그러이 그 사실을 인정하고는 계속 그녀와 교제했다. 그리하여 머들 부인은, 천박한 야만인의 간계에 희생된 사교계의 교양 있는 숙녀로서(머들 씨는 주머니 사정이 들통난 그 순간, 머리 꼭대기부터 발바닥까지 죄다 들통이 났던 것이다) 자기 계급을 위해 그 계급에 의해 적극적으로 옹호받아야 마땅한 처지가 되었다. 그녀는 이 충성에 보답하고자, 고인의 그 흉악한 망령에 대해 자신이 그 누구보다도 더 분개하고 있음을 사람들이 알아차리게끔 만들었다. 그리하여 결국 그녀는 현명한 여인답게 그 용광로에서 무사히 빠져나왔고, 더없이 잘 처신했다.\n\n스파클러 씨의 관직은 다행히도, 더 짭짤한 높이로 끌어올릴 만한 배너클 기중기를 쓸 이유가 생기지 않는 한 신사가 평생 처박혀 두어지는 그런 선반 중 하나였다. 그리하여 그 애국적인 공복은 자기 깃발(네 가지 문장을 박은 가문기)을 고수했고, 그 깃발을 돛대에 못 박아 두는 일에서는 완벽한 넬슨이었다. 그 대담함의 수익으로, 스파클러 부인과 머들 부인은 — 그제 끓인 수프와 마차용 말의 냄새가 인간에게 죽음만큼이나 끈질기게 따라붙는, 그 점잖은 불편함의 자그마한 신전, 그 서로 다른 층에 살면서 — 사교계의 결투장에서 끝까지 겨루기 위해 단단히 차려입은, 맹세코 둘도 없는 맞수였다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 이 모든 일이 전개되는 것을 지켜보면서, 머지않아 패니의 아이들이 그 점잖은 살림의 어느 뒷구석으로 처박힐지, 그리고 아직 태어나지 않은 그 가엾은 희생자들을 누가 돌볼지 불안하게 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n아서는 감정이나 근심에 관한 화제를 입에 올리기에는 너무도 위중했고, 그의 회복은 그 쇠약함이 가라앉아 안식에 이르는 데 크게 달려 있었으므로, 이 힘겨운 시기에 리틀 도릿이 의지할 수 있는 유일한 상대는 미글스 씨였다. 그는 여전히 외국에 있었으나, 그녀는 마셜시에서 아서를 처음 본 직후부터, 그리고 그 후로도 줄곧, 그의 딸을 통해 그에게 편지를 보내, 자신이 가장 불안해하는 여러 사항, 특히 한 가지 사항에 관한 불안을 그에게 털어놓았다. 미글스 씨가 마셜시에서 위안이 되어 주는 대신 외국에 계속 머무르는 것은 바로 그 한 가지 사항에 기인한 것이었다.\n\n리고의 손에 들어간 그 문서들의 정확한 성격은 밝히지 않은 채, 리틀 도릿은 그 사연의 대략적인 윤곽을 미글스 씨에게 털어놓았고, 또한 그에게 그자의 최후도 들려주었다. 저울과 됫박을 다루던 옛 신중한 습관 덕분에 미글스 씨는 그 원본 서류들을 되찾는 일의 중요성을 단번에 알아차렸다. 그리하여 그는 리틀 도릿에게 답장을 보내, 그 점에 관해 그녀가 드러낸 우려를 강력히 뒷받침하면서, \"그것들의 행방을 찾으려는 어떤 시도라도 해보지 않고는\" 영국으로 건너오지 않겠다고 덧붙였다.\n\n이 무렵 헨리 가우언 씨는 미글스 일가와 알고 지내지 않는 편이 자기에게 더 유쾌하리라고 마음을 정한 터였다. 그는 그 점에 관해 아내에게 아무런 명령도 내리지 않을 만큼은 사려 깊었으나, 미글스 씨에게는 자기들이 개인적으로 서로 잘 맞지 않는 듯하니, 정중하게, 아무런 소동도 그런 따위의 일도 없이, 서로 세상에 둘도 없는 좋은 사람들이지만 떨어져 지내는 편이 가장 낫다는 데 합의하면 좋은 일이리라고 말했다. 딸이 보는 앞에서 끊임없이 무시당함으로써 딸의 행복을 더하지 못하고 있음을 이미 절감하던 가엾은 미글스 씨는 이렇게 말했다. \"좋네, 헨리! 자네는 내 펫의 남편이고, 자연의 이치대로 나를 밀어냈으니, 자네가 원한다면 좋네!\" 이 합의에는, 아마 헨리 가우언이 미처 내다보지 못한 부수적 이점이 딸려 있었다. 즉 미글스 부부는, 오직 딸과 그 어린 아이만 상대하게 되자 전보다 딸에게 더 후해졌고, 그의 그 도도한 기개는 그 돈이 어디서 나온 것인지 알아야 하는 굴욕적인 처지에 놓이지 않으면서도 더 넉넉히 돈을 갖추게 되었다는 점이었다.\n\n이런 시기에 미글스 씨는 자연스레 어떤 일거리를 큰 열의로 붙들었다. 그는 딸을 통해, 리고가 출몰하던 여러 도시며, 얼마 전부터 그가 묵어 온 여러 호텔을 알고 있었다. 그가 스스로에게 부과한 일거리는, 온갖 신중함과 신속함으로 이곳들을 찾아가, 어디든 그가 계산하지 않은 청구서와 남겨 둔 상자나 꾸러미가 있을 경우, 그 청구서를 갚고 그 상자나 꾸러미를 가져오는 것이었다.\n\n어머니 외에 다른 수행원 없이, 미글스 씨는 순례에 나섰고 숱한 모험과 마주쳤다. 그가 겪은 곤란 가운데 적지 않은 것은, 그가 남이 자기에게 하는 말을 도무지 알아듣지 못한다는 것, 그리고 자기가 하는 말을 도무지 알아듣지 못하는 사람들 사이에서 탐문을 이어 갔다는 것이었다. 그래도, 영어란 어쩐지 온 세상의 모국어이며 단지 사람들이 너무 어리석어 그것을 모를 뿐이라는 흔들림 없는 확신을 품은 채, 미글스 씨는 더없이 청산유수로 여관 주인들을 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓고, 더없이 복잡한 종류의 설명을 큰 소리로 펼쳤으며, 응답자들의 모국어로 된 대답은 \"죄다 헛소리\"라는 이유로 깡그리 무시해 버렸다. 때로는 통역이 불려 왔는데, 미글스 씨는 그들에게 어찌나 관용구로 가득 찬 말투로 말을 거는지 그들은 즉각 입을 다물고 말문이 막혀 버렸으니 — 이는 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이었다. 그러나 손익을 따져 보면, 그가 크게 손해를 보았는지는 의문이다. 왜냐하면 그는 아무런 재산도 찾아내지 못한 대신, 그가 알아들을 수 있게 만든 유일한 단어인 그 고유명사와 결부된 숱한 빚과 갖가지 불명예의 연루를 찾아냈으므로, 그는 거의 어디서나 모욕적인 비난에 파묻혔던 것이다. 무려 네 차례나 경찰이 불려 와 미글스 씨를 사기꾼 기사이자, 쓸모없는 자이며, 도둑이라 고발하는 소리를 접수했는데, 그는 그 모든 욕설을 더없이 좋은 기분으로 견뎌 냈고(그게 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알지 못했으므로), 가장 치욕적인 방식으로 증기선과 합승마차까지 호송되어 쫓겨났으니, 그러는 내내, 어머니를 팔짱에 낀 채, 명랑하고 거침없는 영국인답게 끊임없이 떠들어 댔다.\n\n그러나 자기 모국어로, 그리고 자기 머릿속에서, 미글스 씨는 명료하고 영리하며 끈질긴 사람이었다. 그가 그 순례 도중, 자기 표현대로 \"빙 돌아\" 파리에 이르렀을 때, 그리고 거기까지 완전히 실패한 채였을 때도, 그는 낙심하지 않았다. \"내가 그자를 따라 영국에 가까워질수록, 알겠소, 어머니,\" 미글스 씨가 논했다. \"그 서류들이 나오든 안 나오든, 나는 그만큼 그 서류에 더 가까워질 공산이 크다는 거요. 왜냐하면 그자가 그것들을, 영국 쪽 사람들 손에는 안전하면서도 자기 자신은 손쉽게 닿을 수 있는 어딘가에 맡겨 두었으리라고 결론짓는 게 이치에 맞으니까, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n파리에서 미글스 씨는 리틀 도릿이 보낸 편지가 자기를 기다리며 놓여 있는 것을 발견했다. 거기서 그녀는, 이제는 세상에 없는 그 사내에 관하여 클레넘 씨와 잠깐 일이 분 정도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다고 했고, 그를 만나러 오는 길인 친구 미글스 씨가 가능하다면 그 사내에 관해 무언가 알아내는 데 관심이 있다고 클레넘 씨에게 전하자, 그가 미글스 씨에게 이렇게 전해 달라더라고 했다. 즉 그자가 와이드 양에게 알려져 있었으며, 그녀는 당시 칼레의 어느 거리에 살고 있다는 것이었다. \"오호!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다.\n\n그 역마차 시절에 가능한 한 빠른 시일 안에, 미글스 씨는 그 금이 간 대문의 금이 간 종을 울렸고, 문은 삐걱대며 열렸으며, 그 시골 아낙이 어두운 문간에 서서 말했다. \"글-쎄-요! 선생님! 누구를?\" 그 말에 응답하여, 미글스 씨는 혼잣말로, 이 칼레 사람들에게는 그래도 분별이라는 게 좀 있어서 당신과 자기들이 무슨 볼일로 만나는지를 정말로 어느 정도는 알아듣는다고 중얼거리고는, 이렇게 대답했다. \"와이드 양 말이오, 아주머니.\" 그리하여 그는 와이드 양 앞으로 안내되었다.\n\n\"우리가 만난 지도 꽤 됐구려.\" 미글스 씨가 목청을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"그동안 잘 지내셨기를 바라오, 와이드 양?\"\n\n그나 다른 누군가가 잘 지냈기를 바라지도 않으면서, 와이드 양은 그를 다시 보는 영광을 자기가 무엇 덕분에 누리게 되었느냐고 물었다. 그러는 동안 미글스 씨는 방 안을 죽 둘러보았으나 상자 비슷한 것은 아무것도 눈에 띄지 않았다.\n\n\"실은 말이오, 와이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 편안하게, 능숙하게, 구슬리는 듯하다고까지는 못 해도, 그런 목소리로 말했다. \"지금으로선 어두운 작은 일 하나에 당신이 어쩌면 빛을 비춰 줄 수 있을지도 모르겠소. 우리 사이의 어떤 불쾌한 지난 일들은 다 지난 일이오, 바라건대. 이제 와 어쩔 수 없는 일이지. 내 딸을 기억하시오? 세월이 이렇게도 변하다니! 한 아이의 어머니라오!\"\n\n그 순진함 속에서, 미글스 씨는 이보다 더 못한 첫음을 짚을 수가 없었을 것이다. 그는 어떤 관심의 표현이라도 나오기를 기다리며 잠시 멈췄으나, 헛되이 멈춘 것이었다.\n\n\"그건 당신이 꺼내고자 하던 화제가 아니지요?\" 차가운 침묵 끝에 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니오, 아니오.\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다. \"아니오. 나는 당신의 그 선한 천성이라면 혹시—\"\n\n\"내 선한 천성이란 건 기대할 만한 게 못 된다는 걸 당신은 알고 있는 줄 알았는데요?\" 그녀가 미소를 띠며 말을 가로챘다.\n\n\"그런 말 마시오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"스스로를 부당하게 깎아내리는 거요. 어쨌든, 본론으로 들어갑시다.\" 빙 돌아 다가가서는 아무것도 얻지 못했음을 그는 깨달았던 것이다. \"내 친구 클레넘에게서 들었는데, 그가, 당신이 들으면 안타까워하겠지만, 아주 심하게 앓았고 지금도 앓고 있소—\"\n\n그는 다시 말을 멈췄고, 그녀는 다시 잠자코 있었다.\n\n\"—당신이 블랑두아라는 자를 어느 정도 알고 있었다더군. 얼마 전 런던에서 격렬한 사고로 죽은 그자 말이오. 자, 오해는 마시오! 그게 가벼운 안면이었다는 건 나도 아오.\" 미글스 씨가, 막 터지려는 성난 끼어듦을 능숙하게 미리 막으며 말했다. \"그 점은 충분히 알고 있소. 가벼운 안면이었다는 것, 나도 아오. 한데 문제는 말이오,\" 여기서 미글스 씨의 목소리가 다시 편안해졌다. \"그자가 지난번 영국으로 가는 길에, 서류 상자나, 서류 꾸러미나, 무슨 그릇에 담긴 무슨 서류 따위를—어떤 서류든—당신에게 맡기지 않았소? 잠시 동안, 그것들이 필요해질 때까지만 여기 두게 해 달라고 청하면서 말이오.\"\n\n\"문제라뇨?\" 그녀가 되풀이했다. \"누구의 문제죠?\"\n\n\"내 문제요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 내 문제일 뿐 아니라 클레넘의 문제이고, 다른 사람들의 문제이기도 하오. 자, 나는 확신하오,\" 가슴이 펫에 대한 마음으로 넘쳐흐르던 미글스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"당신이 내 딸에게 어떤 모진 감정을 품고 있을 리는 없다고 말이오. 그건 있을 수 없는 일이지. 자! 이건 그 애의 문제이기도 하오. 그 애의 어느 각별한 친구가 깊이 얽혀 있는 문제이니까. 그러니 이렇게 와서, 솔직히 그게 문제라고 말하고, 묻는 것이오. 자, 그자가 그랬소?\"\n\n\"맹세코,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"나는 내 평생 단 한 번 고용해서 삯을 주고 내보낸 사내에 관해 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 누구나 와서 질문을 겨누는 과녁이 된 것 같군요!\"\n\n\"자, 그러지 마시오,\" 미글스 씨가 타일렀다. \"그러지 마시오! 기분 상해하지 마시오. 이건 세상에서 가장 간단한 질문이고, 누구에게나 물을 법한 것이니까. 내가 말하는 그 문서들은 그자의 것이 아니었고, 부당하게 손에 넣은 것이며, 언젠가는 죄 없는 사람이 그걸 가지고 있으면 곤란해질 수도 있는 것이고, 그게 정말로 속한 사람들이 찾고 있는 것이오. 그자는 런던으로 가는 길에 칼레를 거쳐 갔고, 그때 그것들을 가지고 가서는 안 될 이유들이, 손쉽게 그걸 다시 손에 넣을 수 있기를 바랄 이유들이, 자기와 같은 부류의 사람들에게 맡기는 걸 못 미더워할 이유들이 있었소. 그자가 그것들을 여기 두고 갔소? 분명히 말하건대, 당신 기분을 상하게 하지 않을 방법을 안다면, 어떤 수고라도 마다하지 않고 그렇게 하겠소. 내가 사사로이 묻기는 하지만, 여기엔 사사로운 게 하나도 없소. 누구에게라도 물을 법한 것이고, 이미 많은 사람에게 물어 본 것이오. 그자가 그것들을 여기 두고 갔소? 그자가 여기에 뭐라도 두고 갔소?\"\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"그럼 유감스럽게도, 와이드 양, 당신은 그것들에 관해 아무것도 모른다는 거요?\"\n\n\"그것들에 관해 나는 아무것도 몰라요. 이제 당신의 그 영문 모를 질문에 답했어요. 그자는 그것들을 여기 두고 가지 않았고, 나는 그것들에 관해 아무것도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"그렇군!\" 미글스 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그것 참 안타깝소. 그럼 이걸로 끝이오. 그리고 너무 큰 피해는 없었기를 바라오.—태티코럼은 잘 있소, 와이드 양?\"\n\n\"해리엇이 잘 있느냐고요? 오, 그럼요!\"\n\n\"내가 또 실수를 했군.\" 그렇게 정정당하자 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"여기선 도무지 실수를 면할 수가 없는 모양이오. 어쩌면 두 번 생각했더라면, 나는 그 애한테 그런 딸랑이는 이름은 결코 붙이지 않았을지도 모르오. 한데 젊은 사람들에게 다정하고 장난스럽게 굴려고 마음먹으면, 두 번 생각하지를 않게 되거든. 그 옛 친구가 그 애에게 다정한 말 한마디를 남기오, 와이드 양, 혹시 전해 주는 게 좋겠다 싶으면 말이오.\"\n\n그녀는 그에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그리하여 미글스 씨는, 태양처럼 빛나던 그 정직한 얼굴을 그 음울한 방에서 거두어 내, 미글스 부인을 남겨 둔 호텔로 가져갔고, 거기서 보고를 올렸다. \"패배요, 어머니. 빈손이오!\" 그는 그 얼굴을, 밤에 출항하는 런던행 증기 우편선으로 가져갔고, 그다음에는 마셜시로 가져갔다.\n\n해 질 녘 미글스 부부가 쪽문에 나타났을 때, 충직한 존이 당직을 서고 있었다. 도릿 양은 지금은 여기 없다고 그가 말했다. 하지만 아침에 왔었고, 저녁에는 어김없이 온다고 했다. 클레넘 씨는 더디게 차도를 보이고 있으며, 매기와 플로니시 부인과 뱁티스트 씨가 번갈아 그를 돌본다고 했다. 도릿 양은 그날 저녁 종이 울리기 전에 틀림없이 돌아올 것이었다. 위층에는 간수장이 그녀에게 빌려준 방이 있으니, 원하시면 거기서 그녀를 기다릴 수 있다고 했다. 준비 없이 자기를 보는 것이 아서에게 해로울지도 모른다고 못 미더워하며, 미글스 씨는 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그리하여 그들은 그 방에 들어가 갇힌 채, 창살 박힌 창문 너머로 감옥을 내려다보게 되었다.\n\n비좁은 감옥 마당은 미글스 부인에게 어찌나 영향을 미쳤던지 그녀는 울기 시작했고, 미글스 씨에게는 어찌나 영향을 미쳤던지 그는 숨을 헐떡이기 시작했다. 그가 헐떡이며 방 안을 오르락내리락하면서, 아내의 손수건으로 애써 부채질을 하는 통에 도리어 사태를 더 악화시키고 있을 때, 그는 열리는 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"어? 이런 세상에!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"이건 도릿 양이 아니잖아! 아니, 어머니, 보시오! 태티코럼이오!\"\n\n다름 아닌 바로 그녀였다. 그리고 태티코럼의 팔에는 사방 두 자쯤 되는 쇠 상자가 안겨 있었다. 그런 상자를, 애프리 플린트윈치는 자기 첫 꿈속에서 한밤중에 더블의 팔에 끼인 채 그 낡은 집을 빠져나가는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 이것을, 태티코럼은 옛 주인의 발치 땅바닥에 내려놓았다. 이것 곁에, 태티코럼은 무릎을 꿇고 그 위를 두 손으로 두드리며, 반은 환희에 반은 절망에, 반은 웃음에 반은 눈물에 젖어 외쳤다. \"용서해 주세요, 친애하는 주인님. 저를 다시 받아 주세요, 친애하는 마님. 여기 있어요!\"\n\n\"태티!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다.\n\n\"이게 바로 어르신이 찾으시던 거예요!\" 태티코럼이 말했다. \"여기 있어요! 어르신을 보지 못하도록 저는 옆방에 들어가 있었어요. 어르신이 그분에게 그걸 묻는 것도 들었고, 그분이 그걸 갖고 있지 않다고 말하는 것도 들었어요. 그가 그걸 두고 갈 때 저는 거기 있었고, 잠자리에 들 시간에 제가 그걸 챙겨 가지고 빠져나왔어요. 여기 있어요!\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야,\" 미글스 씨가 전보다 더 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다. \"어떻게 건너왔니?\"\n\n\"어르신과 같은 배를 타고 왔어요. 저는 반대편 끝에 몸을 싸매고 앉아 있었어요. 어르신이 부두에서 마차를 잡으셨을 때, 저는 다른 마차를 잡아 어르신을 따라 여기까지 왔어요. 어르신이 그게 필요하다고 그분에게 말씀하신 뒤로, 그분은 결코 그걸 내놓지 않았을 거예요. 차라리 바다에 가라앉히거나 불태워 버렸을 거예요. 하지만, 여기 있어요!\"\n\n그 \"여기 있어요!\"를 외치며 소녀가 빠져 있던 그 환희와 황홀이라니.\n\n\"그분은 그게 맡겨지는 걸 결코 원치 않았어요. 그건 그분을 위해 꼭 말해 둬야겠어요. 하지만 그가 그걸 두고 갔고, 어르신이 그렇게 말씀하신 뒤로, 또 그분이 그걸 부인한 뒤로, 그분이 결코 그걸 내놓지 않으리라는 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었어요. 하지만 여기 있어요! 친애하는 주인님, 친애하는 마님, 저를 다시 받아 주세요. 그리고 그 정다운 옛 이름을 제게 돌려주세요! 이게 저를 위해 변론해 주게 해 주세요. 여기 있어요!\"\n\n미글스 부부가 그 고집 센 고아 소녀를 다시 자기들의 보호 아래 거두어들였을 때만큼, 그들이 자기들 이름에 합당했던 적은 일찍이 없었다.\n\n\"오! 저는 너무나 비참했어요,\" 태티코럼이 한결 더 많이 울며 외쳤다. \"늘 그렇게 불행했고, 그렇게 후회스러웠어요! 저는 그분을 처음 본 순간부터 두려웠어요. 그분은 제 안의 나쁜 것을 너무도 잘 꿰뚫어 봄으로써 저를 휘어잡는 힘을 가졌다는 걸 알았어요. 그건 제 안의 광기였고, 그분은 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그걸 불러일으킬 수 있었어요. 그런 상태에 빠지면 저는, 제 출신이 그래서 사람들이 다 저를 거스른다고 여기곤 했고, 그들이 제게 친절할수록 저는 그들에게서 더 못된 점을 찾아냈어요. 저는 그들이 저를 짓밟고 우쭐댄다고, 그들이 저로 하여금 자기들을 부러워하게 만들려 한다고 멋대로 단정했는데, 사실은—그때조차도 사실 저는 알고 있었는데—그들은 그런 건 꿈에도 생각지 않았다는 걸요. 게다가 제 아름다운 젊은 마님은 마땅히 누려야 할 만큼 행복하지도 못한데, 저마저 곁을 떠나 버렸으니! 그분이 저를 얼마나 짐승 같고 형편없는 것으로 여기실까요! 하지만 어르신이 그분에게 저를 위해 한마디 해 주시고, 두 분처럼 너그러이 용서해 달라고 청해 주시겠지요? 저는 예전만큼 나쁘진 않아요,\" 태티코럼이 애원했다. \"저는 충분히 나쁘긴 하지만, 정말이지 예전만큼 나쁘진 않아요. 줄곧 제 눈앞에 와이드 양을 두고 봐 왔어요. 마치 무르익은 제 자신을 보는 것처럼요—모든 걸 잘못된 쪽으로 틀고, 온갖 선한 것을 악으로 비틀어 버리는 사람을요. 줄곧 제 눈앞에 그분을 두고 봐 왔어요. 저를 자기처럼 비참하고 의심 많고 괴로운 상태로 붙들어 두는 것 말고는 아무 데서도 즐거움을 찾지 못하는 사람을요. 하기야 그렇게 만드는 데 그분이 애쓸 것도 별로 없었지만요,\" 태티코럼이 마지막으로 크나큰 비탄을 터뜨리며 외쳤다. \"왜냐하면 저는 더없이 나쁠 대로 나빴으니까요. 제가 그저 하고 싶은 말은, 제가 겪어 온 일들을 겪고 나니, 다시는 결코 그렇게까지 나빠지지 않기를 바라며, 아주 더디나마 차츰 나아지리라는 거예요. 정말 열심히 노력할게요. 스물다섯에서 멈추지 않겠어요, 어르신. 이천오백까지, 이만 오천까지 세겠어요!\"\n\n또다시 문이 열렸고, 태티코럼은 잠잠해졌으며, 리틀 도릿이 들어왔고, 미글스 씨는 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 그 상자를 꺼내 보였으니, 그녀의 부드러운 얼굴이 감사의 행복과 기쁨으로 환히 밝아졌다. 이제 비밀은 안전했다! 그녀는 그 비밀에서 자기 몫만은 그에게 감출 수 있었다. 그는 결코 그녀의 손실을 알지 못할 것이었다. 때가 되면 그는 자기 자신에게 중요한 모든 것은 알게 되겠지만, 오직 그녀에게만 관계된 것은 결코 알지 못할 것이었다. 그것은 모두 지나갔고, 모두 용서되었으며, 모두 잊혔다.\n\n\"자, 도릿 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 사업가요—적어도 한때는 그랬소—그러니 그 자격으로, 신속하게 조치를 취할 작정이오. 오늘 밤 아서를 보는 게 좋겠소?\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 안 될 것 같아요. 제가 그분 방에 가서 어떤 상태인지 확인해 볼게요. 하지만 오늘 밤은 안 보시는 편이 좋을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"나도 자네 의견에 크게 동의하오, 도릿 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그래서 이 음울한 방보다 그에게 더 가까이 가지 않은 거요. 그렇다면 아마 앞으로 얼마간은 그를 보지 못하겠지. 하지만 자네가 돌아오면 내가 뜻하는 바를 설명하겠소.\"\n\n그녀는 방을 나갔다. 미글스 씨는 창살 너머로 내다보다가, 그녀가 아래쪽 수위실에서 나와 감옥 마당으로 들어서는 것을 보았다. 그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"태티코럼, 잠깐 이리 와 보렴, 착한 아이야.\"\n\n그녀가 창가로 다가갔다.\n\n\"방금 여기 있었던 저 젊은 아가씨가 보이지—저기 지나가는, 저 작고 조용하고 가냘픈 모습 말이다, 태티? 보렴. 사람들이 길을 비켜 그녀를 지나가게 해 주는구나. 저 사내들이—저 가엾고 추레한 친구들을 보렴—그녀에게 자못 정중하게 모자를 벗는구나. 그리고 이제 그녀가 저 문간으로 스르르 들어가는구나. 보이느냐, 태티코럼?\"\n\n\"네, 어르신.\"\n\n\"내가 들은 바로는, 태티, 그녀는 한때 이 장소의 자식이라고 정식으로 불렸다는구나. 여기서 태어났고, 여기서 여러 해를 살았지. 나는 여기선 숨을 쉴 수가 없구나. 태어나고 자라기에 참 처량한 곳이로구나, 태티코럼?\"\n\n\"정말 그래요, 어르신!\"\n\n\"만일 그녀가 끊임없이 자기 자신만 생각하고, 모두가 이 장소를 두고 자기를 탓하며, 그걸 자기에게 불리하게 돌리고, 자기에게 들이댄다고 마음속으로 결론지었더라면, 그녀는 늘 짜증나고 아마 쓸모없는 삶을 살았을 게다. 그러나 내가 들은 바로는, 태티코럼, 그녀의 젊은 삶은 능동적인 체념과 선함과 고귀한 헌신의 삶이었다는구나. 방금 여기 있었던 그녀의 그 두 눈이 무엇을 늘 바라봄으로써 그런 표정을 얻게 되었다고 내가 생각하는지, 말해 줄까?\"\n\n\"네, 괜찮으시다면요, 어르신.\"\n\n\"의무란다, 태티코럼. 일찍 시작하고, 잘 해 나가거라. 그러면 그 어떤 출신이나 신분에도, 전능하신 분 앞에서나 우리 자신 앞에서나 우리에게 불리하게 작용할 그 무엇도 그 의무에 앞서지 못한단다.\"\n\n그들은 창가에 머물렀고, 어머니도 그들 곁에 와서 죄수들을 가엾어했으니, 마침내 그녀가 돌아오는 것이 보일 때까지 그러했다. 그녀는 이내 방에 들어와, 차분하고 안정된 상태로 두고 온 아서를 그날 밤에는 찾아가지 않는 것이 좋겠다고 권했다.\n\n\"좋소!\" 미글스 씨가 쾌활하게 말했다. \"그게 가장 좋다는 걸 나는 조금도 의심치 않소. 그렇다면 내 추억거리들을, 다정한 간호사 양반, 자네 손에 맡기겠소. 그것들이 그보다 더 나은 손에 맡겨질 수 없다는 걸 나는 잘 아오. 나는 내일 아침 또 떠나오.\"\n\n리틀 도릿이 놀라서, 어디로 가시느냐고 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"이보게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 숨을 쉬지 않고는 살 수 없소. 이곳이 내 숨을 앗아가 버렸고, 아서가 이곳에서 나오기 전까지는 나는 결코 그걸 되찾지 못할 거요.\"\n\n\"그게 어째서 내일 아침 또 떠나실 이유가 되나요?\"\n\n\"설명해 주겠소,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 우리 셋은 시내의 한 호텔에 묵을 거요. 내일 아침, 어머니와 태티코럼은 트위크넘으로 내려갈 거고, 거기서 응접실 창가에 부캔 박사를 모시고 앉아 있던 티킷 부인은 그 둘을 한 쌍의 유령으로 여길 거요. 그리고 나는 도이스를 데리러 다시 외국으로 가겠소. 우리는 댄을 이리로 데려와야 하오. 자, 내 말 들어보오, 이보게, 이러저러한 일들을 두고 띄엄띄엄 불확실한 간격과 거리를 두고 편지를 쓰고 궁리하고 이러쿵저러쿵 가정해 봐야 아무 소용이 없소. 우리는 도이스를 이리로 데려와야 하오. 나는 내일 동틀 녘에 도이스를 이리로 데려오는 일에 전념하겠소. 그를 찾아가는 건 내게 아무것도 아니오. 나는 노련한 여행자고, 모든 외국어와 외국 풍습은 내게 다 매한가지요—나는 그중 어느 것도 도무지 알아듣지 못하니까. 그러니 나는 어떤 불편도 겪을 게 없소. 가야 하는 건 당연하오, 이치가 그렇소. 나는 자유로이 숨 쉬지 않고는 살 수 없고, 아서가 이 마셜시에서 나오기 전까지는 자유로이 숨 쉴 수 없으니까. 지금 이 순간 나는 숨이 막혀, 이만큼 말할 숨조차, 그리고 자네를 위해 이 소중한 상자를 아래층으로 들고 내려갈 숨조차 간신히 있을 뿐이오.\"\n\n종이 울리기 시작할 무렵 그들은 거리로 나섰고, 미글스 씨가 상자를 들고 있었다. 리틀 도릿은 거기에 탈 것이 없었는데, 이것이 그를 다소 놀라게 했다. 그는 그녀를 위해 마차를 불렀고, 그녀가 그 안에 올라타자, 그녀가 자리에 앉았을 때 그는 상자를 그녀 곁에 놓아 주었다. 기쁨과 감사에 겨워 그녀는 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"그건 싫소, 이보게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"자네가 나에게 경의를 표하다니—그것도 마셜시 정문에서 말이오—내가 옳다고 여기는 바에 어긋나는 일이오.\"\n\n그녀는 몸을 앞으로 숙여, 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"자네는 그 시절을 떠올리게 하는구려,\" 미글스 씨가 갑자기 풀이 죽으며 말했다. \"—하지만 그 애는 그를 무척 사랑하고, 그의 흠을 감싸며, 아무도 그걸 못 본다고 여기지—게다가 그는 분명 인맥도 좋고 아주 좋은 집안 출신이고 말이지!\"\n\n그것은 딸을 잃은 슬픔 속에서 그가 가진 유일한 위안이었으니, 그가 그것을 한껏 우려낸들, 누가 그를 탓할 수 있으랴?\n\n# CHAPTER 34. Gone\n\n건강한 가을날, 마셜시 죄수는 쇠약하지만 그밖에는 회복된 몸으로 앉아, 자신에게 책을 읽어 주는 목소리에 귀 기울이고 있었다. 건강한 가을날, 황금빛 들판이 추수되고 다시 갈아엎어진 때, 여름 과일이 익었다가 시든 때, 푸르게 늘어선 홉밭이 바쁜 손길의 따는 이들에게 베여 쓰러진 때, 과수원에 송이송이 맺힌 사과가 적갈색으로 물들고 마가목 열매가 노래져 가는 잎새 사이에서 진홍으로 빛나던 때였다. 숲속에서는 어느덧, 졸음 어린 여름 날씨가 자두에 분이 내려앉듯 풍경 위에 내려앉아 있던 그 뽀얀 기운이 가신 채, 정경이 또렷하고 맑게 빛나는, 평소 보지 못하던 가지 사이의 틈을 통해 다가오는 모진 겨울의 모습을 언뜻언뜻 엿볼 수 있었다. 그리하여 바닷가에서도 바다는 더 이상 열기 속에 잠들어 누운 모습이 아니라, 그 수천 개의 반짝이는 눈을 뜬 채, 해변의 서늘한 모래에서부터 지평선 위의 작은 돛들에 이르기까지 — 나무에서 흩날려 떨어진 가을빛 나뭇잎처럼 멀어져 가는 그 돛들에 이르기까지 — 그 너른 폭 전체가 기쁨에 찬 생기로 가득했다.\n\n변함없고 메마른 채, 가난과 근심에 찌든 굳은 얼굴로 모든 계절을 무지하게 바라보던 그 감옥에는 이런 아름다움이라곤 한 점도 닿아 있지 않았다. 무엇이 피어나든, 그 벽돌과 쇠창살은 한결같이 똑같은 죽은 작물을 맺을 뿐이었다. 그러나 자신에게 책을 읽어 주는 그 목소리에 귀 기울이며, 클레넘은 그 안에서 저 위대한 자연이 하고 있는 모든 것을 들었고, 자연이 인간에게 불러 주는 온갖 어루만지는 노래를 들었다. 그 어머니의 무릎이 아니고서는, 그는 젊은 시절 단 한 번도 희망에 찬 약속에, 장난스러운 공상에, 일찍 길러진 상상의 씨앗 속에 숨어 있는 다정함과 겸손함의 수확에, 어린 도토리 속에 강한 뿌리의 싹을 품은 채 매서운 바람을 피해 자라난 은신의 떡갈나무에 마음을 기울여 본 적이 없었다. 그러나 그에게 책을 읽어 주는 그 목소리의 가락 속에는, 그런 것들에 대한 어떤 오래된 느낌의 기억이, 그리고 평생 그에게 살며시 스며든 적 있는 온갖 자비롭고 사랑 어린 속삭임의 메아리가 깃들어 있었다.\n\n목소리가 멈추자, 그는 손으로 눈을 가리며, 빛이 너무 강하다고 중얼거렸다.\n\n리틀 도릿은 책을 옆에 내려놓고, 곧 조용히 일어나 창에 그늘을 드리웠다. 매기는 늘 앉던 자리에서 바느질을 하고 있었다. 빛이 부드러워지자, 리틀 도릿은 의자를 그의 곁으로 더 가까이 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"이제 곧 다 끝날 거예요, 클레넘 씨. 도이스 씨가 당신께 보내 온 편지들이 우정과 격려로 가득할 뿐 아니라, 러그 씨 말로는 그분이 자기에게 보낸 편지들도 도움이 될 만한 이야기로 가득하다고 하고, (이제 조금 분이 가신 지금은) 모두가 당신을 그토록 배려하고 그토록 좋게 말한다고 하니, 이제 곧 다 끝날 거예요.\"\n\n\"사랑스러운 아가씨. 사랑스러운 마음. 착한 천사 같으니!\"\n\n\"당신은 저를 너무 지나치게 칭찬하세요. 그래도 당신이 그토록 마음 깊이 말씀하시는 걸 듣는 게, 그리고—또 당신이 얼마나 진심으로 그러시는지 보는 게,\" 리틀 도릿은 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 말했다, \"제게는 너무나 더없이 큰 기쁨이라서, 그만두시라는 말을 할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 손을 들어 입술에 갖다 댔다.\n\n\"당신은 여기 여러 번, 아주 여러 번 왔었지요, 내가 당신을 보지 못한 때에도, 리틀 도릿?\"\n\n\"네, 방에 들어오지 않은 채로 여기 와 있던 적이 더러 있었어요.\"\n\n\"아주 자주?\"\n\n\"꽤 자주요,\" 리틀 도릿이 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"날마다?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 리틀 도릿은 머뭇거리다가 말했다, \"적어도 하루에 두 번씩은 여기 와 있었던 것 같아요.\"\n\n그는 그 가볍고 작은 손에 다시 한 번 뜨겁게 입맞춘 뒤 놓아주었을 법도 했다. 그러나 그 손은 제자리에 아주 부드럽게 머무르며, 붙잡혀 있기를 청하는 듯했다. 그는 두 손으로 그 손을 감싸 쥐었고, 그 손은 그의 가슴 위에 부드럽게 놓였다.\n\n\"사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 이제 곧 끝날 것은 내 감금만이 아니오. 당신의 이 희생도 끝나야만 하오. 우리는 다시 헤어지는 법을, 그리고 그토록 멀리 갈라진 서로 다른 길을 가는 법을 배워야만 하오. 당신이 돌아왔을 때 우리가 함께 나눈 이야기를 잊지 않았겠지요?\"\n\n\"오, 아뇨, 잊지 않았어요. 하지만 무슨 일이 있었어요—오늘은 기운이 아주 좋으시죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"아주 좋소.\"\n\n그가 쥐고 있던 손이 그의 얼굴 쪽으로 조금 더 가까이 기어 올라왔다.\n\n\"제가 얼마나 큰 재산을 얻었는지 들으실 만큼 기운이 충분히 좋으세요?\"\n\n\"들으면 무척 기쁘겠소. 어떤 재산도 리틀 도릿에게는 지나치게 크거나 좋을 수 없으니.\"\n\n\"저는 당신께 말씀드리려고 애타게 기다려 왔어요. 말씀드리고 싶어 정말 정말 안달이 났었어요. 정말 받지 않으실 거죠?\"\n\n\"절대로!\"\n\n\"정말 그 절반도 받지 않으실 거죠?\"\n\n\"절대로, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿!\"\n\n그녀가 말없이 그를 바라볼 때, 그 다정한 얼굴에는 그가 잘 이해할 수 없는 무언가가 있었다. 금방이라도 눈물로 무너질 듯하면서도, 행복하고 자랑스러운 무언가가.\n\n\"제가 패니에 대해 말씀드릴 이야기를 들으시면 마음이 아프실 거예요. 가엾은 패니는 모든 걸 잃었어요. 이제 남은 거라곤 남편의 수입뿐이에요. 패니가 결혼할 때 아버지가 주신 것이 모두 당신의 돈처럼 사라져 버렸어요. 같은 사람 손에 맡겨져 있었고, 그래서 전부 없어졌어요.\"\n\n아서는 그 말을 듣고 놀랐다기보다 충격을 받았다. \"그렇게까지 나쁘진 않기를 바랐는데,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 그녀의 남편과 그 파산자 사이의 관계를 아는 터라, 그쪽에서 큰 손실이 있을까 봐 두려워하고는 있었소.\"\n\n\"네. 전부 사라졌어요. 패니가 정말 안됐어요. 가엾은 패니가 정말, 정말, 정말 안됐어요. 가엾은 오라버니도요!\"\n\n\"그 사람도 같은 사람 손에 재산을 맡겼소?\"\n\n\"네! 그것도 전부 사라졌어요.—제 큰 재산이 얼마나 된다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n아서가 새로운 불안에 사로잡힌 채 캐묻듯 그녀를 바라보자, 그녀는 손을 거두어, 조금 전까지 그 손이 놓여 있던 자리에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"저는 세상에 아무것도 없어요. 여기 살던 때만큼이나 가난해요. 아버지가 영국으로 건너오셨을 때, 가진 것을 모두 그 같은 사람 손에 맡기셨고, 그래서 전부 쓸려가 버렸어요. 오, 가장 사랑하고 가장 소중한 분, 이제 정말로 저와 제 재산을 함께 나누지 않으실 거예요?\"\n\n그의 팔에 안긴 채, 그의 가슴에 꼭 끌어안긴 채, 사내다운 그의 눈물이 자기 뺨 위로 흐르는 가운데, 그녀는 그 가녀린 손을 그의 목에 둘러 다른 손으로 맞잡았다.\n\n\"절대로 헤어지지 말아요, 가장 사랑하는 아서. 마지막 순간까지 다시는 헤어지지 말아요! 저는 한 번도 부유했던 적이 없고, 한 번도 자랑스러웠던 적이 없고, 한 번도 행복했던 적이 없어요. 저는 당신에게 받아들여져 부유하고, 당신에게 단념받았기에 자랑스럽고, 이 감옥에서 당신과 함께 있어 행복해요. 하느님의 뜻이라면 당신과 함께 이곳으로 다시 돌아와 제 모든 사랑과 진심을 다해 당신을 위로하고 섬기는 것도 똑같이 행복할 거예요. 저는 어디서든, 어디에서나 당신의 것이에요! 저는 당신을 진심으로 사랑해요! 여태껏 들어 본 가장 큰 재산을 가지고 여태껏 떠받들린 가장 위대한 귀부인이 되느니, 차라리 여기서 당신과 함께 살며 날마다 나가서 우리 먹을 빵을 위해 일하는 편이 더 좋아요. 오, 가엾은 아버지가, 그토록 여러 해 고통받으신 이 방에서 마침내 제 마음이 얼마나 큰 축복을 받았는지 알아만 주신다면!\"\n\n매기는 물론 처음부터 줄곧 빤히 쳐다보고 있었고, 물론 이미 한참 전부터 눈이 빠지도록 울고 있었다. 매기는 이제 너무도 기뻐서, 자기 작은 어머니를 있는 힘껏 끌어안은 뒤, 자기 기쁨을 전할 누군가를 찾으러 나막신 호른파이프 춤이라도 추듯 아래층으로 내려갔다. 그런데 매기가 마주친 사람이 누구였겠는가, 마침 들어오던 플로라와 에프 씨의 고모가 아닌가? 그리고 그 만남의 결과로, 두세 시간이 족히 지난 뒤 리틀 도릿이 밖으로 나갔을 때 자신을 기다리고 있는 것을 발견한 사람은 또 누구였겠는가?\n\n플로라의 눈은 조금 붉었고, 기운이 다소 없어 보였다. 에프 씨의 고모는 어찌나 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있던지, 강력한 기계적 압력이 아니고서는 도무지 구부러지지 않을 것 같은 모습이었다. 그녀의 보닛은 뒤쪽이 무시무시하게 치켜올려져 있었고, 돌처럼 단단한 손가방은 마치 고르곤의 머리에 닿아 돌로 굳어 버린 듯, 그리고 바로 그 순간 그 머리를 안에 담고 있기라도 한 듯 빳빳했다. 이렇게 위압적인 모습을 갖춘 채, 에프 씨의 고모는 마셜시 간수장 관사 계단에 떡하니 앉아, 문제의 두세 시간 동안 자치구의 어린것들에게 큰 구경거리가 되어 주었는데, 그 아이들의 우스갯소리에 이따금 우산 끝을 들이대며 분개하는 통에 그녀 자신이 상당히 얼굴을 붉히곤 했다.\n\n\"도릿 양, 운명에 의해 그토록 높이 멀어진 데다 최상류 사교계의 그토록 떠받들림과 어루만짐을 받는 분께 어딘가로 자리를 옮기자 청한다는 것이 늘 주제넘게 비치리란 걸 저도 정말 가슴 아프게 잘 알지만, 비록 지금의 신분보다야 한참 아래인 파이 가게라 한들 그리고 안쪽 거실이라 한들 그래도 점잖은 사람이라서, 하지만 아서를 봐서라도—도무지 그걸 떨쳐 낼 수가 없네요 이제 와선 그 어느 때보다 부적절하지만 고인이 된 도이스 앤드 클레넘—마지막으로 한마디 드리고 싶은 게 있고 마지막으로 설명 하나 올리고 싶은 게 있으니 어쩌면 콩팥 파이 세 개를 핑계로 삼아 그 소박한 대화의 자리를 당신의 너그러움으로 너그러이 봐주실지도,\" 플로라가 말했다.\n\n이 다소 모호한 말을 제대로 알아들은 리틀 도릿은, 자신은 전적으로 플로라가 하자는 대로 하겠다고 답했다. 그러자 플로라는 길 건너 문제의 파이 가게로 앞장서 갔다. 에프 씨의 고모는 그 뒤에서 성큼성큼 걸으며, 차에 치이는 길에 제 몸을 들이미는 짓을 더 나은 명분에나 어울릴 끈기로 거듭했다.\n\n대화의 눈가림이 될 그 \"콩팥 파이 세 개\"가 작은 양철 접시 셋에 담겨 그들 앞에 놓이고, 콩팥 파이마다 위쪽에 구멍이 하나씩 장식처럼 뚫려 있어 점잖은 사내가 마치 등잔 세 개에 기름을 채우듯 주둥이 달린 깡통에서 뜨거운 그레이비를 그 구멍에 부어 넣자, 플로라는 손수건을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"공상의 고운 꿈이,\" 그녀가 입을 열었다, \"혹시라도 아서가—도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없네요 부디 용서하세요—자유의 몸이 되었을 때, 지금 이것처럼 도무지 바삭하지도 않고 콩팥이 모자라 그 점에선 다진 육두구나 다름없는 파이라 한들 참된 정성의 손길로 내민다면 받아 주지 않으리란 법도 없으리라 그려 본 적이 있다 해도, 그런 환상은 이제 영영 달아나 버렸고 모든 게 무효가 되었지만, 다정한 인연이 무르익고 있다는 걸 아는 터라 두 분 모두 진심으로 잘되시길 바라며 어느 쪽에도 아무런 흠을 잡지 않으니 조금도 흠잡지 않으니, 세월의 손길이 저를 예전보다 훨씬 덜 날씬하게 만들고 조금만 움직여도 끔찍하게 붉어지게 만들기 전에 특히 뭘 먹고 나면 더 그렇고 그게 발진의 모양으로 나타날 땐 저도 잘 알지만, 그럴 수도 있었으나 부모의 훼방으로 그러지 못했고 그 신비로운 실마리를 에프 씨가 쥐기 전까지 마음의 마비가 이어졌다는 걸 안다는 게 가슴이 시들 만큼 쓰라리겠지만, 그래도 저는 어느 쪽에도 인색하게 굴고 싶지 않으며 두 분 모두 진심으로 잘되시길 바라요.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 그녀의 손을 잡고, 예전에 베풀어 준 온갖 친절에 감사했다.\n\n\"친절이라 부르지 마세요,\" 플로라는 그녀에게 진심 어린 입맞춤을 건네며 말했다, \"당신은 외람되이 말하자면 여태껏 있은 가장 착하고 가장 사랑스러운 작은 사람이었고 돈의 관점에서 보더라도 그야말로 양심 그 자체였으니까요 다만 한마디 보태자면 제 양심이 제게 그랬던 것보다야 훨씬 더 상냥한 양심이었지만 제 양심은 남들 것보다 더 무겁진 않기를 바라지만 그래도 늘 사람을 편하게 해 주기보단 불편하게 만드는 데 훨씬 더 빠르고 그러는 걸 더 즐기는 게 분명했으니까요 하지만 또 옆길로 새네요, 막이 내리기 전에 한 가지 바람을 말씀드리고 싶은데 그것은 옛 시절과 옛 진심을 봐서 아서가 꼭 알아 주기를 바란다는 거예요 제가 그분의 불행 속에서 그분을 저버리지 않았다는 걸 그분이 알아 주기를, 그리고 제가 끊임없이 오가며 그분을 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일이 없는지 물었다는 걸, 그리고 길 건너편에서 그분 모르게 곁을 지켜 주려고, 호텔에서 텀블러에 따뜻한 걸 아주 정중히 가져다 주고 정말 아주 좋았던 그 파이 가게에서 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 앉아 있었다는 걸 알아 주기를 바란다는 거예요.\"\n\n이제 플로라의 눈에는 정말로 눈물이 고여 있었고, 그 눈물은 그녀를 무척 돋보이게 했다.\n\n\"그뿐만 아니라,\" 플로라가 말했다, \"여태껏 있은 가장 사랑스러운 사람인 당신께 간곡히 부탁드리는데 아주 다른 세계에서 노니는 사람의 이 격의 없음을 그래도 용서해 주신다면, 아서가 이걸 알게 해 주세요 결국 우리 사이의 그 모든 게 부질없는 일이 아니었는지 저도 잘 모르겠지만 그때는 즐거웠고 또 가슴 아프기도 했지만 분명 에프 씨가 변화를 일으켰고 그 주문이 깨진 뒤로는 새로 엮어 내지 않고서야 아무 일도 일어날 수 없었는데 여러 사정이 겹쳐 그것을 막았고 그중에서도 가장 강력했던 건 아마 그게 이루어질 운명이 아니었다는 것이며, 그게 아서에게도 흡족했고 애초에 자연스럽게 그렇게 되었더라면 제가 무척 기뻐하지 않았으리라고는 말 못 하겠으니 제가 본래 활달한 성미인 데다 집에선 시무룩하게 지냈고 거기선 아버지가 분명 남자 중에 가장 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 양반이고 방화범의 손에 잘려 나가 제 평생 본 적 없는 무언가가 되어 버린 뒤로는 조금도 나아지지 않았으니까요 하지만 질투는 제 성품이 아니고 악의도 아니에요 비록 흠이야 많지만.\"\n\n리틀 도릿은 핀칭 부인의 이 미로를 빠짐없이 따라가지는 못했으나, 그 취지만은 알아들었고, 그 부탁을 진심으로 받아들였다.\n\n\"시든 화관은요 아가씨,\" 플로라가 무척 즐거운 듯 말했다, \"이제 다 말라 버렸고 기둥은 무너졌으며 피라미드는 그 뭐라더라 위에 거꾸로 서 있는데 그걸 어지럼증이라 부르지도 말고 나약함이라 부르지도 말고 어리석음이라 부르지도 마세요 저는 이제 홀로 물러나 사라진 기쁨의 잿더미를 더는 바라보지 않으렵니다만 우리 만남의 소박한 핑계가 되어 준 이 파이 값을 치르는 한 가지 격의 없음을 또 한 번 부려, 영영 작별을 고하겠어요!\"\n\n에프 씨의 고모는 자기 파이를 무척 엄숙하게 먹어 치웠고, 간수장 계단에 그 공공연한 자리를 처음 차지한 이래로 마음속에 어떤 통탄할 보복의 계략을 골똘히 꾸며 오던 차였는데, 이 기회를 틈타 고인이 된 조카의 미망인을 향해 다음과 같은 무녀 같은 호령을 내질렀다.\n\n\"그자를 앞으로 끌어내, 그럼 내 창밖으로 내던질 테니!\"\n\n플로라는 우리는 이제 저녁을 먹으러 집에 가는 거라고 설명하며 이 훌륭한 부인을 달래려 했으나 헛수고였다. 에프 씨의 고모는 한사코 \"그자를 앞으로 끌어내, 그럼 내 창밖으로 내던질 테니!\"라고만 되받았다. 리틀 도릿을 향해 도전적인 노려봄을 줄곧 거두지 않은 채 이 요구를 엄청난 횟수로 거듭한 뒤, 에프 씨의 고모는 팔짱을 끼고 파이 가게 거실 구석에 주저앉아, \"그자가\" \"앞으로 끌려 나오고\" 그자의 운명 가운데 내던지는 대목이 완수되기 전에는 꿈쩍도 하지 않겠다고 단호히 버텼다.\n\n이런 상황에서, 플로라는 리틀 도릿에게 에프 씨의 고모가 이렇게 활기와 기개로 가득 찬 모습을 몇 주 만에 처음 본다고, 저 완고한 노부인이 누그러질 때까지 자기가 \"어쩌면 몇 시간이고\" 여기 머물러야 할 것 같다고, 그리고 자기 혼자서 가장 잘 다룰 수 있을 거라고 털어놓았다. 그리하여 두 사람은 더없이 다정하게, 그리고 양쪽 모두 더없이 따뜻한 마음으로 헤어졌다.\n\n에프 씨의 고모가 음산한 요새처럼 버티고, 플로라는 원기를 돋울 것이 필요해지자, 앞서 언급한 그 텀블러를 가지러 호텔로 심부름꾼을 보냈고, 그것은 나중에 다시 채워졌다. 그 내용물과 신문 한 부, 그리고 파이 재고에서 떠낸 크림 약간의 도움으로, 플로라는 그날의 나머지를 더없이 좋은 기분으로 보냈다. 다만 동네의 잘 속는 어린것들 사이에 떠도는 한가한 소문 때문에 이따금 곤란을 겪었는데, 그 소문인즉 어떤 노부인이 자기 몸을 재료로 만들어지라고 파이 가게에 팔아넘겼고, 지금 파이 가게 거실에 앉아 그 계약을 마저 이행하기를 거부하고 있다는 것이었다. 이 소문이 남녀 가릴 것 없이 어찌나 많은 젊은이를 불러 모았는지, 그리고 저녁 어스름이 깔리기 시작할 무렵엔 장사에 어찌나 큰 지장을 주었는지, 가게 주인은 에프 씨의 고모를 데려가 달라고 무척 다급하게 청해 왔다. 그래서 탈것 하나가 문 앞에 대령되었고, 주인과 플로라의 합심한 노력으로 이 놀라운 여인은 마침내 그 안에 들어가도록 구슬려졌으나, 그러면서도 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 애초에 말한 그 목적으로 그자를 \"앞으로 끌어내라\"고 요구하는 것만은 빼놓지 않았다. 이때 그녀가 마셜시 쪽으로 사악한 눈길을 던지는 것이 목격된 까닭에, 이 감탄스러우리만치 일관된 여인이 \"그자\"로 가리킨 것은 아서 클레넘이었으리라고 추측되어 왔다. 그러나 이는 한낱 억측일 뿐이다. 에프 씨의 고모의 마음을 흡족하게 하기 위해 응당 앞으로 끌려 나왔어야 했으나 끝내 끌려 나오지 않은 그 사람이 누구였는지는 영영 확실히 알려지지 않을 것이다.\n\n가을날이 흘러가는 동안, 리틀 도릿은 이제 마셜시에 와서 그를 보지 않은 채 돌아가는 일이 결코 없었다. 결코, 결코, 결코.\n\n어느 날 아침, 아서가 매일 아침 날개 돋친 듯 그의 가슴으로 올라오던, 옛 사랑이 그토록 애써 일하고 그토록 진실했던 그 방으로 새로운 사랑의 천상의 광휘를 실어 오던 그 가벼운 발소리에 귀 기울이고 있을 때, 어느 날 아침 귀 기울이고 있을 때, 그는 그녀가 혼자가 아니게 다가오는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"아서 씨,\" 문밖에서 그녀의 들뜬 목소리가 들려왔다, \"여기 누구를 한 분 모셔 왔어요. 들어가도 될까요?\"\n\n발소리로 보아 그녀와 함께 둘이 있다고 그는 생각했다. 그가 \"네\"라고 답하자, 그녀는 미글스 씨와 함께 들어왔다. 햇볕에 그을리고 유쾌해 보이는 미글스 씨는 두 팔을 벌려, 햇볕에 그을리고 유쾌한 아버지처럼 아서를 감싸 안았다.\n\n\"자, 이제 됐네,\" 잠시 후 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"이제 끝났어. 아서, 이 친구야, 자네 진작 나를 기다렸다고 당장 실토하게.\"\n\n\"그랬습니다,\" 아서가 말했다. \"하지만 에이미가 제게 말하길—\"\n\n\"리틀 도릿이에요. 다른 이름은 절대 안 돼요.\" (속삭여 그렇게 말한 것은 그녀였다.)\n\n\"—하지만 제 리틀 도릿이 말하길, 더 캐묻지 말고, 직접 뵙기 전까지는 어르신을 기다리지 말라더군요.\"\n\n\"그리고 이제 자네가 나를 보는 게로군, 이 사람아,\" 미글스 씨가 그의 손을 힘차게 흔들며 말했다. \"그러니 이제 어떤 설명이든 죄다 들려줌세. 사실은 말일세, 내가 여기 와 있었어—'알롱제'와 '마르송제'들 한복판에서 곧장 자네에게 달려왔지, 안 그랬다면 내가 오늘 자네 얼굴을 볼 낯이 없었을 게야—하지만 그때 자넨 사람 만날 형편이 아니었고, 나는 도이스를 붙잡으러 다시 떠나야 했네.\"\n* 알롱제·마르송제: 미글스 씨가 프랑스어 \"allons(가자)\", \"marchons(걷자)\"를 멋대로 비틀어 만든, 외국인을 가리키는 우스개 말.\n\n\"가엾은 도이스!\" 아서가 한숨지었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 받지도 않을 이름으로 부르지 말게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그 사람은 가엾지 않아. 아주 잘 지내고 있어. 도이스는 저쪽에선 대단한 인물이야. 정말이지 일을 불붙듯 척척 풀어내고 있다네. 댄, 그 친구 제대로 자리를 잡았어. 일이 되는 걸 바라지 않으면서 그 일을 할 사람을 만나면, 그 사람은 발붙일 데가 없어지지. 하지만 일이 되기를 바라면서 그 일을 할 사람을 만나면, 그 사람은 든든히 발을 딛고 서게 되는 법이야. 자넨 이제 에두름국을 더 성가시게 할 일이 없을 걸세. 내 말 들어 보게, 댄은 그자들 없이도 해냈다네!\"\n\n\"이렇게 제 마음의 짐을 덜어 주시다니요!\" 아서가 외쳤다. \"이렇게 큰 행복을 주시다니요!\"\n\n\"행복이라?\" 미글스 씨가 되받았다. \"댄을 보기 전까진 행복 얘긴 꺼내지도 말게. 정말이지 댄은 저쪽에서 공사를 지휘하고 역사를 척척 해내고 있어서, 그걸 보면 자네 머리카락이 쭈뼛 설 정도라네. 이제 그 사람은 절대 공공의 범죄자가 아니야, 천만에! 메달에 리본에 별 훈장에 십자 훈장에, 그 밖에 뭐가 뭔지 모를 것까지 줄줄이 받아서, 타고난 귀족이나 다름없다네. 하지만 그 얘긴 여기 이쪽에선 해선 안 되네.\"\n\n\"왜 안 됩니까?\"\n\n\"오, 이런!\" 미글스 씨가 아주 진지하게 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그 사람은 여기 이쪽으로 건너오면 그런 것들을 죄다 자물쇠 채워 감춰 둬야 해. 여기선 그게 통하지 않거든. 바로 그 점에서, 브리타니아는 여물통 위의 개 같은 브리타니아야—제 자식들에게 그런 영예를 스스로 주지도 않으면서, 다른 나라들이 줬을 땐 그걸 드러내는 것조차 허락하지 않으니 말일세. 안 되지, 안 돼, 댄!\" 미글스 씨가 다시 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"여기선 그게 통하지 않아!\"\n\n\"제가 잃은 것의 곱절을 (도이스를 위해서가 아니라면) 가져다주셨더라도,\" 아서가 외쳤다. \"이 소식으로 제게 주시는 이 기쁨만큼은 주지 못하셨을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"암, 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고,\" 미글스 씨가 맞장구쳤다. \"물론 나도 그걸 알지, 이 사람아, 그래서 맨 먼저 이 얘기부터 꺼낸 걸세. 자, 다시 도이스를 붙잡은 얘기로 돌아가서. 내가 도이스를 붙잡았네. 자기들한텐 너무 큰 여자용 나이트캡을 쓴 그 지저분한 갈색 개 같은 작자들, 스스로를 아랍인이네 뭐네 별 두서없는 인종이라 일컫는 작자들 한복판에서 그를 딱 맞닥뜨렸지. 자네도 그치들 알지! 그래! 그 사람은 곧장 내게로 오던 참이었고, 나도 그에게 가던 참이라, 그렇게 우리 둘이 함께 돌아왔다네.\"\n\n\"도이스가 영국에 와 있다고요!\" 아서가 외쳤다.\n\n\"바로 그거야!\" 미글스 씨가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 말했다. \"이런 일을 처리하는 데는 내가 세상에서 가장 서투른 사람이라네. 내가 외교 쪽 일을 했더라면 어쩔 뻔했는지 모르겠어—하긴 잘했을지도 모르지! 요컨대 말일세, 아서, 우리 둘 다 이 주째 영국에 와 있었네. 그리고 도이스가 지금 이 순간 어디 있느냐고 자네가 묻겠다면, 뭐, 내 솔직한 대답은—여기 있다는 걸세! 그러니 이제야 나도 마침내 한숨 돌리는구먼!\"\n\n도이스가 문 뒤에서 불쑥 나와, 두 손으로 아서를 붙잡고는, 나머지 이야기를 직접 했다.\n\n\"내 이야기엔 가지가 셋뿐이라네, 친애하는 클레넘,\" 도이스가 자기 손바닥 위에 그 가지들을 하나하나 빚어내듯 능숙한 엄지로 매만지며 말했다. \"그리고 금방 끝날 거야. 첫째, 지난 일에 대해선 자네 입에서 더는 한마디도 듣지 않겠네. 자네 계산에 오류가 있었지. 나도 그게 뭔지 안다네. 그 오류가 기계 전체에 영향을 미치고, 그 결과로 실패가 따르지. 자넨 그 실패에서 배울 것이고, 다음번엔 그걸 피할 걸세. 나 자신도 설계하다가 그런 일을 숱하게 겪었어. 실패는 배우려는 사람에게 무언가를 가르쳐 주는 법이고, 자넨 이 실패에서 배우지 못할 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아니야. 첫째는 그쯤 해 두지. 둘째. 자네가 그 일을 그토록 무겁게 가슴에 담고 그토록 모질게 자책한 게 안타까웠어. 나는 우리 친구의 도움을 받아 일을 바로잡으려고 밤낮없이 귀국길에 오르던 차에, 그가 자네에게 알려 준 대로, 우리 친구와 마주쳤네. 셋째. 우리 둘은 이렇게 합의했네, 자네가 그토록 고초를 겪고, 그토록 마음고생을 하고, 또 병까지 앓은 뒤이니, 자네 모르게 일을 완벽히 정리해 두었다가 찾아와서, 모든 사정이 순조롭다고, 만사가 잘되었다고, 사업이 그 어느 때보다 자네를 절실히 필요로 한다고, 그리고 동업자인 자네와 나 앞에 새롭고 번창하는 앞날이 열렸다고 말해 줄 수 있다면 그것이 유쾌한 깜짝 선물이 되리라고 말이야. 그게 셋째일세. 하지만 자네도 알다시피 우리는 늘 마찰을 감안해 여유를 두는 법이니, 마무리를 위해 자리를 좀 남겨 두었네. 친애하는 클레넘, 나는 자네를 온전히 믿네. 자넨 내게, 내가 자네에게 도움이 될 수 있었거나 도움이 되어 온 만큼이나, 똑같이 도움이 될 힘을 지니고 있어. 자네의 옛 자리가 자네를 기다리고 있고, 자네를 몹시 필요로 한다네. 자네가 여기 단 반 시간이라도 더 머물러 있을 이유가 전혀 없어.\"\n\n침묵이 흘렀고, 그 침묵은 아서가 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 한동안 창가에 서 있은 뒤, 그리고 장차 그의 작은 아내가 될 이가 그에게로 가 곁에 머무를 때까지 깨어지지 않았다.\n\n\"내가 좀 전에 한마디 했는데,\" 그때 대니얼 도이스가 말했다, \"아무래도 그게 잘못된 말이었던 듯싶네. 나는 자네가 여기 반 시간이라도 더 머물러 있을 이유가 없다고 했지, 클레넘. 그런데 자네가 내일 아침까지는 여길 떠나고 싶지 않으리라고 짐작한다면, 내가 틀린 걸까? 그리 대단한 현자가 아니라도, 자네가 이 담장에서, 이 방에서 곧장 어디로 가고 싶어 하는지 내가 알지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"아시는군요,\" 아서가 답했다. \"그건 우리가 소중히 품어 온 뜻이었습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아!\" 도이스가 말했다. \"그렇다면, 이 젊은 아가씨가 스물네 시간 동안 나를 아버지로 여겨 주는 영광을 베풀어, 지금 나와 함께 세인트폴 교회 묘지 쪽으로 마차를 타고 가 준다면, 우리가 거기서 무엇을 마련해야 하는지 내가 안다고 감히 말하겠네.\"\n\n리틀 도릿과 도이스는 곧 함께 나갔고, 미글스 씨는 친구에게 한마디 하려고 뒤에 남았다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 말일세, 아서, 자넨 아침에 어머니와 나를 필요로 하지 않을 테니, 우린 자리를 비켜 줌세. 어머니가 펫 생각을 하게 될지도 모르거든. 마음 여린 사람이라서. 어머닌 별장에 있는 게 제일 좋고, 나도 거기 남아 곁을 지켜 줄 참이네.\"\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 그때를 위해 헤어졌다. 그리고 낮이 저물고, 밤이 저물고, 아침이 왔다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 여느 때처럼 수수하게 차려입고, 매기 외에는 아무도 데리지 않은 채, 햇살과 함께 감옥으로 들어왔다. 그 초라한 방은 그날 아침 행복한 방이었다. 세상 어디에 이토록 고요한 기쁨으로 가득 찬 방이 있었으랴!\n\n\"내 사랑,\" 아서가 말했다. \"왜 매기가 불을 피우는 거요? 우린 곧 떠날 텐데.\"\n\n\"제가 시킨 거예요. 좀 이상한 변덕이 들어서요. 당신이 저를 위해 뭔가 하나 태워 주셨으면 해요.\"\n\n\"무엇을?\"\n\n\"이 접은 종이뿐이에요. 당신이 손수, 이대로, 불 속에 넣어 주시면 제 변덕이 흡족해질 거예요.\"\n\n\"미신을 믿는 거요, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿? 부적인가요?\"\n\n\"당신이 가장 좋아하는 그 무엇이든이에요, 내 사람,\" 그녀는 눈을 반짝이며 웃고, 발끝으로 서서 그에게 입맞추며 답했다, \"불이 활활 타오를 때 제 변덕을 받아 주시기만 한다면요.\"\n\n그리하여 두 사람은 불 앞에 서서 기다렸다. 클레넘은 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고, 불은 바로 그 자리에서 종종 그러했듯, 리틀 도릿의 눈 속에서 빛나고 있었다. \"이제 충분히 활활 타오르오?\" 아서가 말했다. \"이제 아주 충분히 활활 타올라요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"그 부적엔 무슨 말을 해야 하는 거요?\" 아서가 종이를 불꽃 위에 들고서 물었다. \"(괜찮으시다면) '사랑해요!'라고 하시면 돼요,\" 리틀 도릿이 답했다. 그래서 그는 그 말을 했고, 종이는 타서 사라졌다.\n\n두 사람은 마당을 아주 조용히 지나갔다. 거기엔 아무도 없었으나, 많은 머리들이 창문에서 슬며시 엿보고 있었다. 오래전부터 낯익은 단 하나의 얼굴만이 수위실에 있었다. 두 사람이 그에게 인사를 건네고 다정한 말을 많이 나눈 뒤, 리틀 도릿은 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아서서 손을 내밀며 말했다, \"잘 있어요, 착한 존! 부디 아주 행복하게 살기를 바라요, 그대!\"\n\n그러고서 두 사람은 이웃한 세인트조지 교회의 계단을 올라, 대니얼 도이스가 아버지의 역할로 기다리고 있는 제단 앞으로 나아갔다. 그리고 그곳에는, 그녀에게 매장 등록부를 베개 삼아 내주었던 리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 있었으니, 그녀가 결국 결혼하러 자기들에게 돌아왔다는 사실에 감탄을 금치 못했다.\n\n그리고 두 사람은 창문에 그려진 우리 구주의 형상을 통해 햇살이 그들 위로 비쳐 드는 가운데 혼인을 맺었다. 그리고 두 사람은 결혼 등록부에 서명하러, 리틀 도릿이 그 파티가 끝난 뒤 잠들었던 바로 그 방으로 들어갔다. 그리고 그곳에서, 팽크스 씨가 (장차 도이스 앤드 클레넘의 수석 서기가 되고, 나중엔 그 회사의 동업자가 될 운명의 인물이) 그 방화범의 모습을 평화로운 벗의 모습 속에 가라앉힌 채, 한쪽 팔엔 플로라를, 다른 쪽 팔엔 매기를 늠름하게 부축하고서 문안을 들여다보며 그 광경을 지켜보았는데, 그 뒤편으로는 존 치버리와 그의 아버지, 그리고 잠시 어버이 마셜시를 버리고 그 행복한 자식을 위해 한걸음에 달려온 다른 간수들이 배경을 이루고 있었다. 그리고 플로라에게는 얼마 전의 선언에도 불구하고 은둔의 기색이라곤 조금도 없었으며, 오히려 놀랍도록 말쑥하게 차려입고, 비록 들뜬 모양새이긴 했으나 그 의식을 더할 나위 없이 즐겼다.\n\n리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 그녀가 이름을 적는 동안 잉크병을 들어 주었고, 서기는 선량한 목사의 제의를 벗기다 말고 손을 멈추었으며, 모든 증인이 각별한 관심으로 지켜보았다. \"보세요,\" 리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 말했다, \"이 젊은 아가씨는 우리 명물 가운데 하나로, 이제 우리 등록부의 셋째 권에까지 이르렀답니다. 이 아가씨의 출생은 내가 첫째 권이라 부르는 데 적혀 있고, 바로 이 마룻바닥 위에서 그 예쁜 머리를 내가 둘째 권이라 부르는 것에 베고 잠들어 있었으며, 이제는 내가 셋째 권이라 부르는 데에 신부로서 그 자그마한 이름을 적고 있으니 말입니다.\"\n\n서명이 끝나자 모두가 자리를 비켜 주었고, 리틀 도릿과 그녀의 남편은 둘이서만 교회 밖으로 걸어 나갔다. 두 사람은 현관 계단에서 잠시 멈추어, 가을 아침 햇살의 환한 빛줄기 속에 새로이 펼쳐진 거리의 정경을 바라보다가, 이윽고 내려갔다.\n\n쓸모 있고 행복한 소박한 삶 속으로 내려갔다. 때가 무르익으면 자기들 자식 못지않게 패니의 방치된 아이들에게도 어머니의 보살핌을 베풀어 주고, 그 부인은 영영 사교계로 드나들게 내버려 두고자 내려갔다. 몇 해 동안 팁에게 다정한 간병인이자 벗이 되어 주고자 내려갔으니, 팁은 만약 자기에게 재물이 있었더라면 그녀에게 주었을지도 모를 그 재물에 대한 보답으로 그녀에게 큰 요구를 해 댔어도 그녀는 결코 언짢아하는 법이 없었고, 사랑으로 그의 두 눈을 감겨 마셜시와 그 시들어 버린 온갖 열매에 작별을 고하게 해 주었다. 두 사람은 떼려야 뗄 수 없이, 또 축복받은 채로, 떠들썩한 거리 속으로 조용히 내려갔다. 그리고 두 사람이 햇빛과 그늘 속을 지나는 동안, 시끄러운 자들과 안달하는 자들, 거만한 자들과 고집스러운 자들과 허영에 찬 자들은 안달복달 부대끼며 늘 그러하듯 소란을 피워 댔다.";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-963"]=[[["# BOOK THE SECOND: RICHES", "# BOOK THE SECOND: RICHES"]], [["BOOK THE FIRST: POVERTY 1. Sun and Shadow 2. Fellow Travellers 3. Home 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream 5. Family Affairs 6. The Father of the Marshalsea 7. The Child of the Marshalsea 8. The Lock 9. little Mother 10. Containing the whole Science of Government 11. Let Loose 12. Bleeding Heart Yard 13. Patriarchal 14. Little Dorrit’s Party 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream 16. Nobody’s Weakness 17. Nobody’s Rival 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations 20. Moving in Society 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint 22. A Puzzle 23. Machinery in Motion 24. Fortune-Telling 25. Conspirators and Others 26. Nobody’s State of Mind 27. Five-and-Twenty 28. Nobody’s Disappearance 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming 30. The Word of a Gentleman 31. Spirit 32. More Fortune-Telling 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint 34. A Shoal of Barnacles 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan\n1. Fellow Travellers 2. Mrs General 3. On the Road 4. A Letter from Little Dorrit 5. Something Wrong Somewhere 6. Something Right Somewhere 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’ 9. Appearance and Disappearance 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken 11. A Letter from Little Dorrit 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden 13. The Progress of an Epidemic 14. Taking Advice 15. No just Cause or Impediment why these Two Persons should not be joined together 16. Getting on 17. Missing 18. A Castle in the Air 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air 20. Introduces the next 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor 22. Who Passes by this Road so late? 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams 24. The Evening of a Long Day 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office 26. Reaping the Whirlwind 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea 30. Closing in 31. Closed 32. Going 33. Going! 34. Gone", "나는 이 이야기에 두 해 동안 수많은 작업 시간을 바쳐 왔다. 작품 전체를 통독함으로써 그 장단점이 스스로 드러나도록 맡겨 둘 수 없다면, 나는 무척이나 형편없이 일한 셈일 것이다. 그러나 띄엄띄엄 연재되어 나오는 동안 다른 누구도 그러지 못했을 만큼 내가 그 실타래를 더 끊김 없는 주의로 쥐고 있었으리라 짐작하는 것이 부당하지 않다면, 그 짜임을 완성된 상태에서, 무늬가 마무리된 채로 보아 달라고 청하는 것 또한 부당하지 않을 것이다."]], [["I have been occupied with this story, during many working hours of two years. I must have been very ill employed, if I could not leave its merits and demerits as a whole, to express themselves on its being read as a whole.", "배너클 일가와 에두름국 같은 과장된 허구에 대해 변명을 한마디 한다면, 나는 영국인이라면 누구나 겪는 일상적 경험에서 그것을 찾겠다 — 러시아 전쟁과 첼시 조사위원회의 시절에 내가 예의범절에 그런 폭력을 가했다는 사소한 사실은 굳이 언급하지 않으면서 말이다."], ["But, as it is not unreasonable to suppose that I may have held its threads with a more continuous attention than anyone else can have given them during its desultory publication, it is not unreasonable to ask that the weaving may be looked at in its completed state, and with the pattern finished.", "머들 씨라는 그 터무니없는 착상을 감히 변호하고자 한다면, 나는 그것이 철도 주식의 시대 이후, 어느 아일랜드 은행과 그에 못지않게 칭송받던 한두 개의 사업이 있던 시절에 비롯되었다고 넌지시 일러 두겠다."], ["If I might offer any apology for so exaggerated a fiction as the Barnacles and the Circumlocution Office, I would seek it in the common experience of an Englishman, without presuming to mention the unimportant fact of my having done that violence to good manners, in the days of a Russian war, and of a Court of Inquiry at Chelsea. If I might make so bold as to defend that extravagant conception, Mr Merdle, I would hint that it originated after the Railroad-share epoch, in the times of a certain Irish bank, and of one or two other equally laudable enterprises.", "나쁜 의도가 때로는 선한 의도, 그것도 노골적으로 종교적인 의도라고 자처한다는 그 어처구니없는 상상을 완화하기 위해 무언가를 변론한다면, 그것이 영국 왕립 은행의 전직 이사들에 대한 공개 심문이 있던 시절에 이 지면에서 절정에 이르렀다는 묘한 우연을 들겠다."], ["If I were to plead anything in mitigation of the preposterous fancy that a bad design will sometimes claim to be a good and an expressly religious design, it would be the curious coincidence that it has been brought to its climax in these pages, in the days of the public examination of late Directors of a Royal British Bank. But, I submit myself to suffer judgment to go by default on all these counts, if need be, and to accept the assurance (on good authority) that nothing like them was ever known in this land.", "그러나 나는 이 모든 점에 대하여, 필요하다면 궐석으로 판결이 내려지는 것을 감수하며, 이 나라에서 그와 같은 일은 결코 알려진 바 없다는 (믿을 만한 출처의) 보증을 받아들이겠다."]], [["Some of my readers may have an interest in being informed whether or no any portions of the Marshalsea Prison are yet standing.", "내 독자들 가운데 더러는 마셜시 감옥의 어느 부분이라도 아직 남아 있는지 궁금해할지 모르겠다."], ["I did not know, myself, until the sixth of this present month, when I went to look.", "나 자신도 이달 6일에 직접 가서 보기 전까지는 알지 못했다."], ["I found the outer front courtyard, often mentioned here, metamorphosed into a butter shop; and I then almost gave up every brick of the jail for lost.", "여기에서 자주 언급한 바깥 앞마당이 버터 가게로 탈바꿈한 것을 발견하고, 나는 그 감옥의 벽돌 한 장까지 모두 사라진 줄로 거의 단념했다."], ["Wandering, however, down a certain adjacent ‘Angel Court, leading to Bermondsey’, I came to ‘Marshalsea Place:’ the houses in which I recognised, not only as the great block of the former prison, but as preserving the rooms that arose in my mind’s-eye when I became Little Dorrit’s biographer.", "그러나 인근의 어느 '버몬지로 통하는 앤젤 코트'를 따라 내려가다가 나는 '마셜시 플레이스'에 이르렀다. 그곳의 집들을 나는 옛 감옥의 그 큰 건물 덩어리로 알아보았을 뿐 아니라, 내가 리틀 도릿의 전기 작가가 되었을 때 마음의 눈에 떠올랐던 방들을 고스란히 간직하고 있는 것으로 알아보았다."], ["The smallest boy I ever conversed with, carrying the largest baby I ever saw, offered a supernaturally intelligent explanation of the locality in its old uses, and was very nearly correct.", "내가 여태껏 이야기를 나눈 가장 작은 사내아이가, 내가 여태껏 본 가장 큰 갓난아기를 안고서, 그 일대의 옛 쓰임새에 관해 초자연적으로 영리한 설명을 내놓았는데, 거의 정확했다."], ["How this young Newton (for such I judge him to be) came by his information, I don’t know; he was a quarter of a century too young to know anything about it of himself.", "이 어린 뉴턴이(나는 그를 그렇게 판단한다) 어디서 그런 정보를 얻었는지 나는 모른다. 그는 제 힘으로 그것을 알기에는 사반세기나 너무 어렸다."], ["I pointed to the window of the room where Little Dorrit was born, and where her father lived so long, and asked him what was the name of the lodger who tenanted that apartment at present?", "나는 리틀 도릿이 태어났고 그녀의 아버지가 그토록 오래 살았던 방의 창문을 가리키며, 지금 그 방을 세 들어 사는 사람의 이름이 무엇이냐고 물었다. 아이는 \"톰 피식\"이라고 했다."], ["He said, ‘Tom Pythick.’ I asked him who was Tom Pythick? and he said, ‘Joe Pythick’s uncle.’", "나는 톰 피식이 누구냐고 물었고, 아이는 \"조 피식의 삼촌\"이라고 했다."]], [["A little further on, I found the older and smaller wall, which used to enclose the pent-up inner prison where nobody was put, except for ceremony.", "조금 더 가서 나는, 의례적인 경우가 아니면 아무도 가두지 않던 그 갇힌 안쪽 감옥을 둘러싸던, 더 오래되고 더 낮은 담을 발견했다."], ["But, whosoever goes into Marshalsea Place, turning out of Angel Court, leading to Bermondsey, will find his feet on the very paving-stones of the extinct Marshalsea jail; will see its narrow yard to the right and to the left, very little altered if at all, except that the walls were lowered when the place got free; will look upon rooms in which the debtors lived; and will stand among the crowding ghosts of many miserable years.", "그러나 버몬지로 통하는 앤젤 코트에서 빠져나와 마셜시 플레이스로 들어가는 사람은 누구든, 사라진 마셜시 감옥의 바로 그 포석 위에 자기 발이 놓여 있음을 알게 될 것이며, 좌우로 좁은 마당을, 이곳이 자유로워졌을 때 담이 낮아진 것을 빼면 거의, 아니 전혀 달라지지 않은 그 마당을 보게 될 것이며, 채무자들이 살던 방들을 바라보게 될 것이며, 비참한 여러 해의 몰려드는 유령들 한가운데에 서 있게 될 것이다."]], [["In the Preface to Bleak House I remarked that I had never had so many readers.", "「황량한 집」의 서문에서 나는 그토록 많은 독자를 가져 본 적이 없다고 적었다."], ["In the Preface to its next successor, Little Dorrit, I have still to repeat the same words.", "그 뒤를 잇는 작품인 「리틀 도릿」의 서문에서도 나는 여전히 같은 말을 되풀이해야겠다."], ["Deeply sensible of the affection and confidence that have grown up between us, I add to this Preface, as I added to that, May we meet again!", "우리 사이에 자라난 애정과 신뢰를 깊이 느끼며, 나는 그때 그 서문에 덧붙였던 말을 이 서문에도 덧붙인다. 우리 다시 만나기를!"]], [["London May 1857", "런던, 1857년 5월"]], [["# CHAPTER 1. Sun and Shadow", "# CHAPTER 1. Sun and Shadow"]], [["Thirty years ago, Marseilles lay burning in the sun, one day.", "햇볕이 내리쬐던 어느 날, 삼십 년 전 마르세유는 태양 아래 불타듯 누워 있었다."]], [["A blazing sun upon a fierce August day was no greater rarity in southern France then, than at any other time, before or since.", "그 무렵 남프랑스에서, 사나운 팔월의 어느 날 작열하는 태양이란 그 전이나 그 후나 다른 어느 때보다 더 드문 일이 아니었다."], ["Everything in Marseilles, and about Marseilles, had stared at the fervid sky, and been stared at in return, until a staring habit had become universal there.", "마르세유 안의, 그리고 마르세유 둘레의 모든 것이 그 이글거리는 하늘을 빤히 노려보았고, 또 그 대가로 노려봄을 당했으니, 마침내 노려보는 버릇이 그곳에 두루 퍼지고 말았다."], ["Strangers were stared out of countenance by staring white houses, staring white walls, staring white streets, staring tracts of arid road, staring hills from which verdure was burnt away.", "낯선 이들은 빤히 노려보는 흰 집들, 빤히 노려보는 흰 담장들, 빤히 노려보는 흰 거리들, 빤히 노려보는 메마른 도로의 자취들, 푸르름이 다 타 버린 채 빤히 노려보는 언덕들에 기가 질려 낯빛이 흐트러졌다."], ["The only things to be seen not fixedly staring and glaring were the vines drooping under their load of grapes.", "빤히 노려보거나 번쩍이지 않는 유일한 것이라곤 포도송이의 무게에 늘어진 포도 덩굴뿐이었다."], ["These did occasionally wink a little, as the hot air barely moved their faint leaves.", "이것들만은 더운 공기가 그 가녀린 잎을 겨우 흔들 때 이따금 조금씩 눈을 깜박이긴 했다."]], [["There was no wind to make a ripple on the foul water within the harbour, or on the beautiful sea without.", "항구 안의 더러운 물에도, 바깥의 아름다운 바다에도 잔물결 하나 일으킬 바람이 없었다."], ["The line of demarcation between the two colours, black and blue, showed the point which the pure sea would not pass; but it lay as quiet as the abominable pool, with which it never mixed.", "검은빛과 푸른빛, 그 두 색을 가르는 경계선은 맑은 바다가 넘어오지 않을 지점을 보여 주었으나, 결코 섞이지 않는 그 추악한 웅덩이만큼이나 고요히 누워 있었다."], ["Boats without awnings were too hot to touch; ships blistered at their moorings; the stones of the quays had not cooled, night or day, for months.", "차양 없는 배들은 너무 뜨거워 손을 댈 수 없었고, 묶여 있는 배들은 표면이 부풀어 물집이 잡혔으며, 부두의 돌들은 몇 달 동안 밤이고 낮이고 식을 줄을 몰랐다."], ["Hindoos, Russians, Chinese, Spaniards, Portuguese, Englishmen, Frenchmen, Genoese, Neapolitans, Venetians, Greeks, Turks, descendants from all the builders of Babel, come to trade at Marseilles, sought the shade alike--taking refuge in any hiding-place from a sea too intensely blue to be looked at, and a sky of purple, set with one great flaming jewel of fire.", "힌두인, 러시아인, 중국인, 스페인인, 포르투갈인, 영국인, 프랑스인, 제노바인, 나폴리인, 베네치아인, 그리스인, 터키인, 바벨탑을 쌓은 온갖 무리의 후예들이 마르세유로 장사하러 와서는 한결같이 그늘을 찾았으니 — 차마 바라볼 수 없을 만큼 너무도 짙푸른 바다와, 하나의 거대한 불꽃 보석이 박힌 자줏빛 하늘을 피해 어디든 숨을 곳으로 몸을 피했다."]], [["The universal stare made the eyes ache.", "그 두루 퍼진 노려봄에 눈이 다 아팠다."], ["Towards the distant line of Italian coast, indeed, it was a little relieved by light clouds of mist, slowly rising from the evaporation of the sea, but it softened nowhere else.", "멀리 이탈리아 해안선 쪽으로는 과연 바다의 증발에서 천천히 피어오르는 옅은 안개 구름 덕분에 조금은 누그러졌으나, 그 밖에는 어디에서도 부드러워지지 않았다."], ["Far away the staring roads, deep in dust, stared from the hill-side, stared from the hollow, stared from the interminable plain.", "멀리 먼지에 깊이 잠긴 노려보는 도로들이 언덕배기에서 노려보고, 움푹한 곳에서 노려보고, 끝없는 평원에서 노려보았다."], ["Far away the dusty vines overhanging wayside cottages, and the monotonous wayside avenues of parched trees without shade, drooped beneath the stare of earth and sky.", "멀리 길가 오두막 위로 늘어진 먼지투성이 포도 덩굴과, 그늘이라곤 없이 바싹 마른 나무들이 늘어선 단조로운 길가의 가로수 길이 하늘과 땅의 노려봄 아래 늘어져 있었다."], ["So did the horses with drowsy bells, in long files of carts, creeping slowly towards the interior; so did their recumbent drivers, when they were awake, which rarely happened; so did the exhausted labourers in the fields.", "졸음에 겨운 방울을 단 말들도 긴 짐수레 행렬을 이루어 내륙을 향해 느릿느릿 기어가며 그러했고, 비스듬히 누운 마부들도 — 좀처럼 깨어 있는 일이 없었으나 — 깨어 있을 때면 그러했으며, 들판에서 지쳐 버린 일꾼들도 그러했다."], ["Everything that lived or grew, was oppressed by the glare; except the lizard, passing swiftly over rough stone walls, and the cicala, chirping his dry hot chirp, like a rattle.", "살아 있거나 자라는 모든 것이 그 작열에 짓눌렸으니, 거친 돌담 위를 잽싸게 지나가는 도마뱀과, 딸랑이처럼 메마르고 뜨거운 울음을 우는 매미만이 예외였다."], ["The very dust was scorched brown, and something quivered in the atmosphere as if the air itself were panting.", "먼지마저 갈색으로 그을렸고, 마치 공기 자체가 헐떡이기라도 하듯 대기 속에서 무언가가 떨렸다."]], [["Blinds, shutters, curtains, awnings, were all closed and drawn to keep out the stare.", "블라인드며 덧문이며 커튼이며 차양이며 모두 그 노려봄을 막으려 닫히고 쳐져 있었다."], ["Grant it but a chink or keyhole, and it shot in like a white-hot arrow.", "틈새나 열쇠 구멍 하나만 허락해 보라, 그러면 백열의 화살처럼 쏘아 들어왔다."], ["The churches were the freest from it.", "교회들만이 그것에서 가장 자유로웠다."], ["To come out of the twilight of pillars and arches--dreamily dotted with winking lamps, dreamily peopled with ugly old shadows piously dozing, spitting, and begging--was to plunge into a fiery river, and swim for life to the nearest strip of shade.", "깜박이는 등불이 꿈결처럼 점점이 박히고, 추한 늙은 그림자들이 경건하게 졸고 가래를 뱉고 구걸하며 꿈결처럼 모여 있는 기둥과 아치의 어스름에서 밖으로 나오는 일이란, 불의 강에 뛰어들어 가장 가까운 한 줄기 그늘을 향해 목숨 걸고 헤엄치는 것과 같았다."], ["So, with people lounging and lying wherever shade was, with but little hum of tongues or barking of dogs, with occasional jangling of discordant church bells and rattling of vicious drums, Marseilles, a fact to be strongly smelt and tasted, lay broiling in the sun one day.", "그리하여, 그늘이 있는 곳이면 어디든 사람들이 빈둥거리고 드러누운 채, 혀를 놀리는 소리도 개 짖는 소리도 거의 없이, 이따금 불협화음의 교회 종이 댕댕거리고 심술궂은 북소리가 덜그럭거리는 가운데, 마르세유는 — 코로 강하게 맡고 입으로 강하게 맛볼 수 있는 하나의 실체로서 — 어느 날 태양 아래 통째로 구워지고 있었다."]], [["In Marseilles that day there was a villainous prison.", "그날 마르세유에는 흉악한 감옥 하나가 있었다."], ["In one of its chambers, so repulsive a place that even the obtrusive stare blinked at it, and left it to such refuse of reflected light as it could find for itself, were two men.", "그 어느 방은 어찌나 역겨운 곳이던지 그 주제넘게 끼어드는 노려봄조차 그곳 앞에서는 눈을 깜박이며 외면하고, 그 방이 제 힘으로 찾아낼 수 있는 반사된 빛의 찌꺼기에나 내맡겨 두었는데, 그 안에 두 사내가 있었다."], ["Besides the two men, a notched and disfigured bench, immovable from the wall, with a draught-board rudely hacked upon it with a knife, a set of draughts, made of old buttons and soup bones, a set of dominoes, two mats, and two or three wine bottles.", "두 사내 말고는, 벽에서 떼어 낼 수 없게 붙은, 칼로 거칠게 새긴 체커판이 패인 흠집투성이의 망가진 의자 하나와, 낡은 단추와 사골로 만든 체커 말 한 벌, 도미노 한 벌, 멍석 두 장, 그리고 포도주 병 두세 개가 있었다."], ["That was all the chamber held, exclusive of rats and other unseen vermin, in addition to the seen vermin, the two men.", "눈에 보이는 해충 — 두 사내 — 말고도 쥐를 비롯한 보이지 않는 해충들을 빼고 나면, 그 방에 든 것은 그것이 전부였다."]], [["It received such light as it got through a grating of iron bars fashioned like a pretty large window, by means of which it could be always inspected from the gloomy staircase on which the grating gave.", "그 방은 제법 큰 창문 모양으로 짜인 쇠창살을 통해 들어오는 만큼의 빛을 받았는데, 그 창살이 면한 음침한 계단에서는 그 격자를 통해 언제든 방 안을 들여다볼 수 있었다."], ["There was a broad strong ledge of stone to this grating where the bottom of it was let into the masonry, three or four feet above the ground.", "이 창살에는 그 밑부분이 석조에 박혀 있는, 땅에서 서너 자쯤 높이의 넓고 단단한 돌 선반이 달려 있었다."], ["Upon it, one of the two men lolled, half sitting and half lying, with his knees drawn up, and his feet and shoulders planted against the opposite sides of the aperture.", "그 위에 두 사내 가운데 하나가 반쯤 앉고 반쯤 누운 채로, 무릎을 끌어당기고 두 발과 두 어깨를 그 구멍의 양쪽 면에 버틴 채 비스듬히 기대어 있었다."], ["The bars were wide enough apart to admit of his thrusting his arm through to the elbow; and so he held on negligently, for his greater ease.", "창살은 그가 팔을 팔꿈치까지 내밀 수 있을 만큼 사이가 벌어져 있었기에, 그는 한층 편하게 있으려고 무심히 거기에 매달려 있었다."]], [["A prison taint was on everything there.", "그곳의 모든 것에 감옥의 더러움이 배어 있었다."], ["The imprisoned air, the imprisoned light, the imprisoned damps, the imprisoned men, were all deteriorated by confinement.", "갇힌 공기, 갇힌 빛, 갇힌 습기, 갇힌 사람들이 모두 그 감금으로 인해 변질되어 있었다."], ["As the captive men were faded and haggard, so the iron was rusty, the stone was slimy, the wood was rotten, the air was faint, the light was dim.", "갇힌 사람들이 빛바래고 초췌하듯, 쇠는 녹슬었고, 돌은 미끈거렸으며, 나무는 썩었고, 공기는 희미했으며, 빛은 어두웠다."], ["Like a well, like a vault, like a tomb, the prison had no knowledge of the brightness outside, and would have kept its polluted atmosphere intact in one of the spice islands of the Indian ocean.", "우물처럼, 지하실처럼, 무덤처럼, 그 감옥은 바깥의 밝음을 알지 못했으니, 인도양의 어느 향료 섬 한복판에 놓아 둔다 해도 제 오염된 공기를 고스란히 간직했으리라."]], [["The man who lay on the ledge of the grating was even chilled.", "창살 선반에 누운 사내는 추위마저 느꼈다."], ["He jerked his great cloak more heavily upon him by an impatient movement of one shoulder, and growled, ‘To the devil with this Brigand of a Sun that never shines in here!’", "그는 한쪽 어깨를 신경질적으로 움직여 커다란 망토를 한층 무겁게 끌어당기고는 으르렁거렸다. \"여기엔 결코 비치지도 않는 이 산적 같은 태양이라니, 망할 놈 같으니!\""]], [["He was waiting to be fed, looking sideways through the bars that he might see the further down the stairs, with much of the expression of a wild beast in similar expectation.", "그는 먹을 것이 오기를 기다리며, 계단 아래쪽을 더 멀리 내다보려고 창살 사이로 옆으로 곁눈질하고 있었는데, 그 표정에는 비슷한 기다림에 처한 들짐승의 기색이 다분히 어려 있었다."], ["But his eyes, too close together, were not so nobly set in his head as those of the king of beasts are in his, and they were sharp rather than bright--pointed weapons with little surface to betray them.", "그러나 너무 가까이 붙은 그의 두 눈은 짐승의 왕의 눈이 그 머리에 고귀하게 박혀 있는 것만큼 그렇게 박혀 있지 않았고, 밝다기보다는 날카로웠으니 — 그것을 드러낼 표면이라곤 거의 없는 뾰족한 흉기였다."], ["They had no depth or change; they glittered, and they opened and shut.", "그 눈에는 깊이도 변화도 없었고, 그저 번득이며 떴다 감겼다 할 뿐이었다."], ["So far, and waiving their use to himself, a clockmaker could have made a better pair.", "그것이 그에게 어떤 쓸모가 있는지를 차치하고 본다면, 시계공이라도 그보다 나은 한 쌍을 만들 수 있었으리라."], ["He had a hook nose, handsome after its kind, but too high between the eyes by probably just as much as his eyes were too near to one another.", "그는 매부리코를 가졌는데, 그 나름으로는 잘생긴 코였으나, 그의 두 눈이 서로 너무 가까운 만큼 꼭 그만큼 두 눈 사이에서 너무 높이 솟아 있었다."], ["For the rest, he was large and tall in frame, had thin lips, where his thick moustache showed them at all, and a quantity of dry hair, of no definable colour, in its shaggy state, but shot with red.", "그 밖에는, 그는 골격이 크고 키가 컸으며, 두툼한 콧수염 사이로 드러나는 곳마다 얇은 입술을 가졌고, 텁수룩한 상태에서는 무어라 규정할 수 없는 빛깔이지만 붉은 기가 도는 푸석한 머리털이 수북했다."], ["The hand with which he held the grating (seamed all over the back with ugly scratches newly healed), was unusually small and plump; would have been unusually white but for the prison grime.", "그가 창살을 붙잡은 손은(그 손등은 갓 아문 보기 흉한 생채기로 온통 줄이 가 있었다) 유난히 작고 통통했으며, 감옥의 때만 아니었다면 유난히 희었을 것이었다."]], [["The other man was lying on the stone floor, covered with a coarse brown coat.", "또 다른 사내는 거친 갈색 외투를 덮은 채 돌바닥에 누워 있었다."]], [["‘Get up, pig!’ growled the first.", "\"일어나, 이 돼지야!\" 첫 번째 사내가 으르렁거렸다."], ["‘Don’t sleep when I am hungry.’", "\"내가 배고픈데 잠이나 자다니.\""]], [["‘It’s all one, master,’ said the pig, in a submissive manner, and not without cheerfulness; ‘I can wake when I will, I can sleep when I will.", "\"다 마찬가집니다요, 나리.\" 돼지는 순순한 태도로, 그러면서도 명랑함이 없지 않게 말했다."], ["It’s all the same.’", "\"저는 깨고 싶을 때 깨고, 자고 싶을 때 잡니다. 다 똑같지요.\""]], [["As he said it, he rose, shook himself, scratched himself, tied his brown coat loosely round his neck by the sleeves (he had previously used it as a coverlet), and sat down upon the pavement yawning, with his back against the wall opposite to the grating.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 일어나 몸을 털고 긁적이더니, 갈색 외투를 (그는 그것을 앞서 덮개로 썼다) 소매로 목에 헐겁게 둘러매고는, 창살 맞은편 벽에 등을 기댄 채 하품을 하며 포석 위에 앉았다."]], [["‘Say what the hour is,’ grumbled the first man.", "\"지금 몇 시인지 말해 봐.\" 첫 번째 사내가 투덜거렸다."]], [["‘The mid-day bells will ring--in forty minutes.’ When he made the little pause, he had looked round the prison-room, as if for certain information.", "\"정오 종이 울릴 겁니다 — 사십 분 후에요.\" 그 짧은 사이를 둘 때, 그는 무슨 확실한 정보라도 구하듯 감옥 방 안을 둘러보았다."]], [["‘You are a clock.", "\"넌 시계로구나."], ["How is it that you always know?’", "어떻게 늘 그렇게 아는 거지?\""]], [["‘How can I say?", "\"제가 어찌 말씀드리겠습니까?"], ["I always know what the hour is, and where I am.", "저는 늘 몇 시인지, 제가 어디 있는지 압니다."], ["I was brought in here at night, and out of a boat, but I know where I am.", "저는 밤중에, 그것도 배에서 이리로 끌려 들어왔지만 제가 어디 있는지 압니다."], ["See here!", "보십시오!"], ["Marseilles harbour;’ on his knees on the pavement, mapping it all out with a swarthy forefinger; ‘Toulon (where the galleys are), Spain over there, Algiers over _there_.", "여기가 마르세유 항구입니다.\" 그는 포석 위에 무릎을 꿇고 거무스름한 집게손가락으로 그 모든 것을 지도처럼 그려 나갔다."], ["Creeping away to the left here, Nice.", "\"툴롱(거기엔 갤리선들이 있지요), 저쪽이 스페인, 저쪽이 알제."], ["Round by the Cornice to Genoa.", "여기 왼쪽으로 슬그머니 빠지면 니스."], ["Genoa Mole and Harbour.", "코르니체를 돌아 제노바로."], ["Quarantine Ground.", "제노바 방파제와 항구. 검역 구역."], ["City there; terrace gardens blushing with the bella donna.", "거기 도시가 있고, 벨라돈나가 붉게 물든 테라스 정원들. 여기, 포르토피노."], ["Here, Porto Fino.", "리보르노로 나아가고."], ["Stand out for Leghorn.", "다시 치비타베키아로 나가서, 그렇게 — 어이!"], ["Out again for Civita Vecchia, so away to--hey!", "나폴리 들어갈 자리가 없네.\" 이쯤에서 그는 벽에 다다라 있었다."], ["there’s no room for Naples;’ he had got to the wall by this time; ‘but it’s all one; it’s in there!’", "\"하지만 다 마찬가집니다. 거긴 저 안쪽에 있어요!\""]], [["He remained on his knees, looking up at his fellow-prisoner with a lively look for a prison.", "그는 무릎을 꿇은 채로 감옥치고는 활기찬 표정으로 동료 죄수를 올려다보았다."], ["A sunburnt, quick, lithe, little man, though rather thickset.", "햇볕에 그을린, 날쌔고 날렵한 작은 사내였는데, 다소 다부진 체격이었다."], ["Earrings in his brown ears, white teeth lighting up his grotesque brown face, intensely black hair clustering about his brown throat, a ragged red shirt open at his brown breast.", "갈색 귀에 귀고리를 달고, 흰 이가 그 기괴한 갈색 얼굴을 환히 밝히고, 새카만 머리털이 갈색 목둘레에 곱슬곱슬 엉겨 있었으며, 갈색 가슴께가 드러나도록 너덜너덜한 붉은 셔츠를 풀어헤치고 있었다."], ["Loose, seaman-like trousers, decent shoes, a long red cap, a red sash round his waist, and a knife in it.", "헐렁한 뱃사람 바지에, 그런대로 멀쩡한 신발, 길쭉한 붉은 모자, 허리에 두른 붉은 띠, 그리고 그 띠에 꽂은 칼."]], [["‘Judge if I come back from Naples as I went!", "\"제가 갈 때처럼 나폴리에서 돌아올 수 있을지 따져 보십시오!"], ["See here, my master!", "여기 보십시오, 나리!"], ["Civita Vecchia, Leghorn, Porto Fino, Genoa, Cornice, Off Nice (which is in there), Marseilles, you and me.", "치비타베키아, 리보르노, 포르토피노, 제노바, 코르니체, 니스 앞바다(그건 저 안쪽에 있지요), 마르세유, 나리와 저."], ["The apartment of the jailer and his keys is where I put this thumb; and here at my wrist they keep the national razor in its case--the guillotine locked up.’", "간수와 그 열쇠들이 있는 방은 이 엄지를 짚은 데고요, 여기 제 손목께에 나라의 면도칼을 그 상자에 넣어 두는데 — 단두대를 잠가 두는 겁니다.\""]], [["The other man spat suddenly on the pavement, and gurgled in his throat.", "또 다른 사내가 느닷없이 포석 위에 침을 뱉고는 목구멍을 그르렁거렸다."]], [["Some lock below gurgled in _its_ throat immediately afterwards, and then a door crashed.", "곧이어 어디 아래쪽의 자물쇠가 제 목구멍을 그르렁거리더니, 이어 문 하나가 쾅 닫혔다."], ["Slow steps began ascending the stairs; the prattle of a sweet little voice mingled with the noise they made; and the prison-keeper appeared carrying his daughter, three or four years old, and a basket.", "느릿한 발걸음이 계단을 올라오기 시작했고, 그 소리에 어느 달콤하고 작은 목소리의 재잘거림이 섞여 들었다. 그러고는 감옥지기가 세 살이나 네 살쯤 된 딸아이와 바구니 하나를 안고 나타났다."]], [["‘How goes the world this forenoon, gentlemen?", "\"오늘 오전엔 세상이 어떻게 돌아갑니까, 신사 양반들?"], ["My little one, you see, going round with me to have a peep at her father’s birds.", "보시다시피 제 어린것이 저와 함께 돌면서 아비의 새들을 구경하는 중이지요."], ["Fie, then!", "아이고, 그래!"], ["Look at the birds, my pretty, look at the birds.’", "새들을 봐라, 우리 예쁜이, 새들을 봐.\""]], [["He looked sharply at the birds himself, as he held the child up at the grate, especially at the little bird, whose activity he seemed to mistrust.", "그는 아이를 창살께로 들어 올리면서 자신도 그 새들을, 특히 그 작은 새를 날카롭게 바라보았는데, 그 작은 새의 민첩함이 미심쩍은 모양이었다. \"당신 빵을 가져왔소, 조반니 바티스타 씨.\""], ["‘I have brought your bread, Signor John Baptist,’ said he (they all spoke in French, but the little man was an Italian); ‘and if I might recommend you not to game--’", "그가 말했다(다들 프랑스어로 말했으나, 그 작은 사내는 이탈리아인이었다). \"그리고 노름은 하지 마시라고 권해도 된다면 —\""]], [["‘You don’t recommend the master!’ said John Baptist, showing his teeth as he smiled.", "\"나리께는 권하지 않으시는군요!\" 조반니 바티스타가 웃으며 이를 드러내고 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["but the master wins,’ returned the jailer, with a passing look of no particular liking at the other man, ‘and you lose.", "하지만 나리는 따고,\" 간수가 또 다른 사내에게 딱히 호감이라곤 없는 눈길을 슬쩍 던지며 대꾸했다."], ["It’s quite another thing.", "\"당신은 잃잖소. 그건 전혀 다른 얘기지."], ["You get husky bread and sour drink by it; and he gets sausage of Lyons, veal in savoury jelly, white bread, strachino cheese, and good wine by it.", "당신은 그걸로 푸석한 빵에 시큼한 술이나 얻고, 저 양반은 그걸로 리옹 소시지에, 향긋한 젤리에 든 송아지 고기에, 흰 빵에, 스트라키노 치즈에, 좋은 포도주를 얻으니 말이오."], ["Look at the birds, my pretty!’", "새들을 봐라, 우리 예쁜이!\""]], [["‘Poor birds!’ said the child.", "\"불쌍한 새들!\" 아이가 말했다."]], [["The fair little face, touched with divine compassion, as it peeped shrinkingly through the grate, was like an angel’s in the prison.", "천상의 연민이 어린 그 어여쁘고 작은 얼굴이 창살 사이로 움츠리며 들여다보는 모습은, 감옥 안의 천사 같았다."], ["John Baptist rose and moved towards it, as if it had a good attraction for him.", "조반니 바티스타가 일어나 그쪽으로 다가갔는데, 마치 그것이 자신에게 좋은 끌림이라도 되는 듯했다."], ["The other bird remained as before, except for an impatient glance at the basket.", "또 다른 새는, 바구니를 향한 조급한 눈길만 빼면, 여전히 그대로였다."]], [["‘Stay!’ said the jailer, putting his little daughter on the outer ledge of the grate, ‘she shall feed the birds.", "\"잠깐!\" 간수가 어린 딸을 창살 바깥 선반에 앉히며 말했다."], ["This big loaf is for Signor John Baptist.", "\"이 애가 새들에게 먹이를 줄 거요."], ["We must break it to get it through into the cage.", "이 큼직한 빵 덩이는 조반니 바티스타 씨 것이지."], ["So, there’s a tame bird to kiss the little hand!", "새장 안으로 넣으려면 잘라야겠군."], ["This sausage in a vine leaf is for Monsieur Rigaud.", "자, 이 작은 손에 입맞춤을 하는 길든 새가 있구나!"], ["Again--this veal in savoury jelly is for Monsieur Rigaud.", "포도 잎에 싼 이 소시지는 리고 씨 것."], ["Again--these three white little loaves are for Monsieur Rigaud.", "또 — 향긋한 젤리에 든 이 송아지 고기는 리고 씨 것."], ["Again, this cheese--again, this wine--again, this tobacco--all for Monsieur Rigaud.", "또 — 이 작고 흰 빵 세 개는 리고 씨 것. 또, 이 치즈 — 또, 이 포도주 — 또, 이 담배 — 모두 리고 씨 것."], ["Lucky bird!’", "복도 많은 새지!\""]], [["The child put all these things between the bars into the soft, Smooth, well-shaped hand, with evident dread--more than once drawing back her own and looking at the man with her fair brow roughened into an expression half of fright and half of anger.", "아이는 이 모든 것을 창살 사이로 그 부드럽고 매끄러우며 잘생긴 손에 넣어 주었는데, 분명 두려워하는 기색으로 — 한 번도 아니고 여러 번 제 손을 도로 빼고는, 절반은 겁먹고 절반은 화가 난 듯한 표정으로 어여쁜 이마를 잔뜩 찌푸린 채 그 사내를 바라보았다."], ["Whereas she had put the lump of coarse bread into the swart, scaled, knotted hands of John Baptist (who had scarcely as much nail on his eight fingers and two thumbs as would have made out one for Monsieur Rigaud), with ready confidence; and, when he kissed her hand, had herself passed it caressingly over his face.", "반면, 조반니 바티스타의 거뭇하고 비늘 같고 마디진 손에는(그의 여덟 손가락과 두 엄지를 다 합쳐도 리고 씨의 손톱 하나 만큼도 못 될 만큼 손톱이 거의 없었다) 선뜻한 믿음으로 거친 빵 덩이를 쥐여 주었으며, 그가 제 손에 입을 맞추자 아이는 손수 그 손을 그의 얼굴에 어루만지듯 쓸어 주기까지 했다."], ["Monsieur Rigaud, indifferent to this distinction, propitiated the father by laughing and nodding at the daughter as often as she gave him anything; and, so soon as he had all his viands about him in convenient nooks of the ledge on which he rested, began to eat with an appetite.", "리고 씨는 이 차별에 아랑곳하지 않고, 아이가 무얼 줄 때마다 딸을 보고 웃으며 고개를 끄덕여 아비의 비위를 맞추었다. 그러고는 자신이 기댄 선반의 편리한 구석구석에 온갖 음식을 다 늘어놓자마자, 식욕이 동하여 먹기 시작했다."]], [["When Monsieur Rigaud laughed, a change took place in his face, that was more remarkable than prepossessing.", "리고 씨가 웃을 때면 그 얼굴에 변화가 일어났는데, 그것은 호감을 주기보다는 더 두드러진 것이었다."], ["His moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache, in a very sinister and cruel manner.", "콧수염이 코 밑으로 올라가고 코가 콧수염 위로 내려오는 것이, 무척이나 음험하고 잔인한 모양새였다."]], [["‘There!’ said the jailer, turning his basket upside down to beat the crumbs out, ‘I have expended all the money I received; here is the note of it, and _that’s_ a thing accomplished. Monsieur Rigaud, as I expected yesterday, the President will look for the pleasure of your society at an hour after mid-day, to-day.’", "\"자!\" 간수가 부스러기를 털어 내려고 바구니를 거꾸로 뒤집으며 말했다. \"받은 돈은 죄다 썼소. 여기 그 명세가 있고, 그러니 그 일은 마무리됐군. 리고 씨, 어제 내가 예상한 대로, 재판장께서 오늘 정오 한 시간 뒤에 당신과 함께하는 즐거움을 청하실 거요.\""]], [["‘To try me, eh?’ said Rigaud, pausing, knife in hand and morsel in mouth.", "\"날 재판하겠다, 그거요?\" 리고가 칼을 손에 쥐고 입에 음식을 문 채 멈추며 말했다."]], [["‘You have said it.", "\"그렇게 말했소."], ["To try you.’", "당신을 재판한다고.\""]], [["‘There is no news for me?’ asked John Baptist, who had begun, contentedly, to munch his bread.", "\"내겐 소식이 없습니까?\" 흡족하게 빵을 우물거리기 시작한 조반니 바티스타가 물었다."]], [["The jailer shrugged his shoulders.", "간수가 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["‘Lady of mine!", "\"맙소사!"], ["Am I to lie here all my life, my father?’", "저는 평생 여기 누워 있어야 하는 겁니까, 아버님?\""]], [["‘What do I know!’ cried the jailer, turning upon him with southern quickness, and gesticulating with both his hands and all his fingers, as if he were threatening to tear him to pieces.", "\"내가 어찌 알겠나!\" 간수가 남쪽 사람 특유의 잽쌈으로 그에게 홱 돌아서며, 마치 그를 갈가리 찢어 놓겠다고 으름장이라도 놓듯 두 손과 열 손가락을 다 놀려 가며 소리쳤다."], ["‘My friend, how is it possible for me to tell how long you are to lie here?", "\"이보게, 자네가 여기 얼마나 오래 누워 있을지 내가 어찌 알 수 있단 말인가?"], ["What do I know, John Baptist Cavalletto?", "내가 무얼 알겠나, 조반니 바티스타 카발레토?"], ["Death of my life!", "내 목숨을 걸고!"], ["There are prisoners here sometimes, who are not in such a devil of a hurry to be tried.’", "여기엔 가끔 재판을 받겠다고 그리 지독하게 서두르지 않는 죄수들도 있다네.\""]], [["He seemed to glance obliquely at Monsieur Rigaud in this remark; but Monsieur Rigaud had already resumed his meal, though not with quite so quick an appetite as before.", "그는 이 말을 하면서 리고 씨를 비스듬히 흘끔거리는 듯했으나, 리고 씨는 이미 식사를 다시 시작한 터였다. 다만 아까만큼 잽싼 식욕은 아니었다."]], [["‘Adieu, my birds!’ said the keeper of the prison, taking his pretty child in his arms, and dictating the words with a kiss.", "\"잘 있거라, 내 새들아!\" 감옥지기가 어여쁜 아이를 품에 안고, 입맞춤과 더불어 그 말을 일러 주며 말했다."]], [["‘Adieu, my birds!’ the pretty child repeated.", "\"잘 있거라, 내 새들아!\" 어여쁜 아이가 따라 했다."]], [["Her innocent face looked back so brightly over his shoulder, as he walked away with her, singing her the song of the child’s game:", "그가 아이를 안고 걸어가며 아이에게 어린이 놀이 노래를 불러 줄 때, 그 천진한 얼굴이 그의 어깨 너머로 어찌나 환하게 뒤돌아보던지,"]], [["‘Who passes by this road so late?", "\"이리 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이 누구인가?"], ["Compagnon de la Majolaine!", "마욜렌의 길동무여!"], ["Who passes by this road so late?", "이리 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이 누구인가?"], ["Always gay!’", "언제나 즐거이!\""]], [["that John Baptist felt it a point of honour to reply at the grate, and in good time and tune, though a little hoarsely:", "조반니 바티스타는 창살에서, 비록 좀 쉰 목소리이긴 했으나 박자와 가락을 맞추어 화답하는 것을 명예로운 일로 여겼다."]], [["‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine!", "\"임금님의 기사들 중에 그것은 꽃이라네, 마욜렌의 길동무여!"], ["Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’", "임금님의 기사들 중에 그것은 꽃이라네, 언제나 즐거이!\""]], [["Which accompanied them so far down the few steep stairs, that the prison-keeper had to stop at last for his little daughter to hear the song out, and repeat the Refrain while they were yet in sight.", "이 노래는 그 가파른 몇 계단 아래까지 그들을 따라갔기에, 감옥지기는 끝내 걸음을 멈추고 어린 딸이 노래를 끝까지 듣고 아직 그들이 보이는 동안 후렴을 따라 부르게 해 주어야 했다."], ["Then the child’s head disappeared, and the prison-keeper’s head disappeared, but the little voice prolonged the strain until the door clashed.", "그러더니 아이의 머리가 사라지고 감옥지기의 머리가 사라졌으나, 그 작은 목소리는 문이 쾅 닫힐 때까지 그 가락을 길게 이어 갔다."]], [["Monsieur Rigaud, finding the listening John Baptist in his way before the echoes had ceased (even the echoes were the weaker for imprisonment, and seemed to lag), reminded him with a push of his foot that he had better resume his own darker place.", "리고 씨는 메아리가 채 그치기도 전에(메아리조차 감금으로 인해 약해져서 늑장을 부리는 듯했다) 귀 기울여 듣고 있는 조반니 바티스타가 제 앞을 가로막고 있는 것을 보고는, 발로 한 번 떠밀며 그에게 본래의 더 어두운 자리로 돌아가는 편이 낫다고 일깨워 주었다."], ["The little man sat down again upon the pavement with the negligent ease of one who was thoroughly accustomed to pavements; and placing three hunks of coarse bread before himself, and falling to upon a fourth, began contentedly to work his way through them as if to clear them off were a sort of game.", "그 작은 사내는 포석에 더없이 익숙한 자의 무심한 편안함으로 다시 포석 위에 앉았다. 그러고는 거친 빵 덩이 세 개를 제 앞에 놓고 네 번째 덩이에 달려들더니, 그것들을 다 치우는 일이 일종의 놀이라도 되는 듯 흡족하게 헤쳐 나가기 시작했다."]], [["Perhaps he glanced at the Lyons sausage, and perhaps he glanced at the veal in savoury jelly, but they were not there long, to make his mouth water; Monsieur Rigaud soon dispatched them, in spite of the president and tribunal, and proceeded to suck his fingers as clean as he could, and to wipe them on his vine leaves.", "어쩌면 그는 리옹 소시지를 흘끔거렸을 테고, 어쩌면 향긋한 젤리에 든 송아지 고기를 흘끔거렸을 테지만, 그것들은 그의 입에 군침을 돌게 할 만큼 오래 거기 있지 않았다. 리고 씨가 재판장이며 법정 따위는 아랑곳없이 곧 그것들을 해치우고는, 손가락을 최대한 깨끗이 빨고 포도 잎에 닦았다."], ["Then, as he paused in his drink to contemplate his fellow-prisoner, his moustache went up, and his nose came down.", "그러더니 술을 마시다 말고 동료 죄수를 바라보려 멈추자, 콧수염이 올라가고 코가 내려왔다."]], [["‘How do you find the bread?’", "\"빵 맛이 어떤가?\""]], [["‘A little dry, but I have my old sauce here,’ returned John Baptist, holding up his knife.", "\"좀 퍽퍽하지만, 저는 여기 제 오랜 소스가 있지요.\" 조반니 바티스타가 칼을 들어 보이며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘How sauce?’", "\"무슨 소스?\""]], [["‘I can cut my bread so--like a melon.", "\"제 빵을 이렇게 — 멜론처럼 자를 수 있죠."], ["Or so--like an omelette.", "아니면 이렇게 — 오믈렛처럼."], ["Or so--like a fried fish.", "아니면 이렇게 — 튀긴 생선처럼."], ["Or so--like Lyons sausage,’ said John Baptist, demonstrating the various cuts on the bread he held, and soberly chewing what he had in his mouth.", "아니면 이렇게 — 리옹 소시지처럼요.\" 조반니 바티스타가 들고 있던 빵에 여러 가지 자르는 시늉을 해 보이며, 입에 든 것을 천연덕스럽게 씹으면서 말했다."]], [["‘Here!’ cried Monsieur Rigaud.", "\"자!\" 리고 씨가 소리쳤다."], ["‘You may drink.", "\"마셔도 좋아."], ["You may finish this.’", "이걸 다 비워도 돼.\""]], [["It was no great gift, for there was mighty little wine left; but Signor Cavalletto, jumping to his feet, received the bottle gratefully, turned it upside down at his mouth, and smacked his lips.", "대단한 선물은 아니었으니, 남은 포도주가 지극히 적었기 때문이다. 그러나 카발레토 씨는 벌떡 일어나 고맙게 그 병을 받아 들고, 입에 거꾸로 대어 들이켜고는 입맛을 다셨다."]], [["‘Put the bottle by with the rest,’ said Rigaud.", "\"병은 나머지하고 같이 치워 둬.\" 리고가 말했다."]], [["The little man obeyed his orders, and stood ready to give him a lighted match; for he was now rolling his tobacco into cigarettes by the aid of little squares of paper which had been brought in with it.", "그 작은 사내는 시키는 대로 하고는, 그에게 불붙인 성냥을 건넬 채비를 하고 섰다. 그도 그럴 것이, 그는 이제 담배와 함께 들여온 작은 종이 사각 조각들을 써서 담배를 말아 궐련을 만드는 중이었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Here!", "\"자!"], ["You may have one.’", "너도 하나 가져도 좋아.\""]], [["‘A thousand thanks, my master!’ John Baptist said in his own language, and with the quick conciliatory manner of his own countrymen.", "\"천 번 감사합니다, 나리!\" 조반니 바티스타가 제 나라 말로, 제 나라 사람들 특유의 잽싸고 비위 맞추는 태도로 말했다."]], [["Monsieur Rigaud arose, lighted a cigarette, put the rest of his stock into a breast-pocket, and stretched himself out at full length upon the bench.", "리고 씨는 일어나서 궐련에 불을 붙이고, 남은 것을 가슴주머니에 넣고는, 의자 위에 길게 몸을 뻗고 누웠다."], ["Cavalletto sat down on the pavement, holding one of his ankles in each hand, and smoking peacefully.", "카발레토는 두 손으로 양쪽 발목을 하나씩 잡은 채 포석 위에 앉아 평온히 담배를 피웠다."], ["There seemed to be some uncomfortable attraction of Monsieur Rigaud’s eyes to the immediate neighbourhood of that part of the pavement where the thumb had been in the plan.", "리고 씨의 눈이, 지도에서 엄지를 짚었던 그 포석 부분의 바로 그 언저리에 무언가 거북하게 끌리는 듯했다."], ["They were so drawn in that direction, that the Italian more than once followed them to and back from the pavement in some surprise.", "그 눈이 어찌나 그쪽으로 끌려가던지, 그 이탈리아인은 한 번 이상 놀란 기색으로 그 눈길을 포석까지 따라갔다가 돌아오곤 했다."]], [["‘What an infernal hole this is!’ said Monsieur Rigaud, breaking a long pause.", "\"이거야말로 지긋지긋한 굴이로군!\" 리고 씨가 긴 침묵을 깨고 말했다."], ["‘Look at the light of day. Day?", "\"저 대낮의 빛을 좀 봐."], ["the light of yesterday week, the light of six months ago, the light of six years ago.", "대낮이라고? 지난주의 빛, 여섯 달 전의 빛, 여섯 해 전의 빛이지."], ["So slack and dead!’", "저토록 늘어지고 죽어 빠진 꼴이라니!\""]], [["It came languishing down a square funnel that blinded a window in the staircase wall, through which the sky was never seen--nor anything else.", "그 빛은, 하늘이라곤 — 아니, 그 무엇도 — 결코 보이지 않는, 계단 벽의 창을 가린 네모난 깔때기를 통해 맥없이 흘러내렸다."]], [["‘Cavalletto,’ said Monsieur Rigaud, suddenly withdrawing his gaze from this funnel to which they had both involuntarily turned their eyes, ‘you know me for a gentleman?’", "\"카발레토,\" 두 사람 다 저도 모르게 눈을 돌렸던 그 깔때기에서 별안간 눈길을 거두며 리고 씨가 말했다. \"넌 내가 신사라는 걸 알지?\""]], [["‘Surely, surely!’", "\"여부가 있습니까, 여부가요!\""]], [["‘How long have we been here?’", "\"우리가 여기 온 지 얼마나 됐지?\""]], [["‘I, eleven weeks, to-morrow night at midnight. You, nine weeks and three days, at five this afternoon.’", "\"저는 내일 밤 자정이면 열한 주, 나리는 오늘 오후 다섯 시면 아홉 주하고 사흘입니다.\""]], [["‘Have I ever done anything here?", "\"내가 여기서 무슨 일이라도 한 적이 있나?"], ["Ever touched the broom, or spread the mats, or rolled them up, or found the draughts, or collected the dominoes, or put my hand to any kind of work?’", "빗자루에 손을 댄 적이, 멍석을 깐 적이, 그걸 말아 올린 적이, 체커 말을 찾아온 적이, 도미노를 거둔 적이, 그 어떤 종류의 일에든 손을 댄 적이 있나?\""]], [["‘Never!’", "\"한 번도요!\""]], [["‘Have you ever thought of looking to me to do any kind of work?’", "\"내가 무슨 일이든 하리라고 기대해 본 적이 있나?\""]], [["John Baptist answered with that peculiar back-handed shake of the right forefinger which is the most expressive negative in the Italian language.", "조반니 바티스타는 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 까딱이는 그 독특한 동작으로 답했는데, 그것은 이탈리아어에서 가장 표현력 풍부한 부정이었다."]], [["‘No!", "\"없지!"], ["You knew from the first moment when you saw me here, that I was a gentleman?’", "넌 여기서 날 처음 본 그 순간부터 내가 신사라는 걸 알았어?\""]], [["‘ALTRO!’ returned John Baptist, closing his eyes and giving his head a most vehement toss.", "\"알트로!\" 조반니 바티스타가 눈을 감고 머리를 더없이 격렬하게 홱 젖히며 대꾸했다."], ["The word being, according to its Genoese emphasis, a confirmation, a contradiction, an assertion, a denial, a taunt, a compliment, a joke, and fifty other things, became in the present instance, with a significance beyond all power of written expression, our familiar English ‘I believe you!’", "그 단어는 제노바식 억양에 따라 긍정도, 부정도, 단언도, 부인도, 비웃음도, 칭찬도, 농담도, 그 밖의 쉰 가지도 되었는데, 지금 이 경우에는 글로는 도저히 다 옮길 수 없는 의미를 담아, 우리 영국인에게 친숙한 \"그렇고말고요!\"가 되었다."]], [["‘Haha!", "\"하하!"], ["You are right!", "옳거니!"], ["A gentleman I am!", "나는 신사야!"], ["And a gentleman I’ll live, and a gentleman I’ll die!", "신사로 살고, 신사로 죽을 것이다!"], ["It’s my intent to be a gentleman.", "신사가 되는 게 내 뜻이지."], ["It’s my game.", "그게 내 노름이야."], ["Death of my soul, I play it out wherever I go!’", "내 영혼을 걸고, 어디를 가든 나는 그걸 끝까지 해낼 테다!\""]], [["He changed his posture to a sitting one, crying with a triumphant air:", "그는 자세를 바꿔 앉으며 의기양양한 기색으로 외쳤다."]], [["‘Here I am!", "\"내가 여기 있다!"], ["See me!", "날 봐!"], ["Shaken out of destiny’s dice-box into the company of a mere smuggler;--shut up with a poor little contraband trader, whose papers are wrong, and whom the police lay hold of besides, for placing his boat (as a means of getting beyond the frontier) at the disposition of other little people whose papers are wrong; and he instinctively recognises my position, even by this light and in this place.", "운명의 주사위 통에서 흔들려 나와 한낱 밀수꾼 따위와 한패가 되다니 — 서류가 잘못된 가엾고 보잘것없는 밀매 장사꾼과 갇혀 버리다니. 게다가 이자는 (국경을 넘으려는 수단으로) 제 배를, 마찬가지로 서류가 잘못된 다른 보잘것없는 자들이 쓰도록 내준 죄로 경찰에 붙잡혔지."], ["It’s well done!", "그런데 이자가 이런 빛 속에서도, 이런 곳에서도 본능적으로 내 신분을 알아본단 말이야."], ["By Heaven!", "잘된 일이지!"], ["I win, however the game goes.’", "맹세코! 노름이 어찌 흘러가든 나는 이기는 거야.\""]], [["Again his moustache went up, and his nose came down.", "또다시 그의 콧수염이 올라가고 코가 내려왔다."]], [["‘What’s the hour now?’ he asked, with a dry hot pallor upon him, rather difficult of association with merriment.", "\"지금 몇 시야?\" 그가 물었는데, 그 위에 마르고 뜨거운 창백함이 어려 있어 즐거움과는 좀처럼 어울리기 어려웠다."]], [["‘A little half-hour after mid-day.’", "\"정오에서 반 시간 조금 지났습니다.\""]], [["‘Good!", "\"좋아!"], ["The President will have a gentleman before him soon.", "재판장이 곧 신사 한 사람을 앞에 두게 되겠군."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Shall I tell you on what accusation?", "무슨 죄목인지 말해 줄까?"], ["It must be now, or never, for I shall not return here.", "지금이 아니면 영영 기회가 없을 테니, 나는 여기로 돌아오지 않을 거거든."], ["Either I shall go free, or I shall go to be made ready for shaving.", "풀려나든가, 아니면 면도 준비를 시키러 끌려가든가 하겠지."], ["You know where they keep the razor.’", "면도칼을 어디 두는지 너도 알잖아.\""]], [["Signor Cavalletto took his cigarette from between his parted lips, and showed more momentary discomfiture than might have been expected.", "카발레토 씨는 벌린 입술 사이에서 궐련을 빼냈는데, 예상보다 더 순간적으로 당황한 기색을 내비쳤다."]], [["‘I am a’--Monsieur Rigaud stood up to say it--‘I am a cosmopolitan gentleman.", "\"나는 —\" 리고 씨가 그 말을 하려고 일어섰다."], ["I own no particular country.", "\"나는 사해동포의 신사야. 나는 특정한 나라를 두지 않아."], ["My father was Swiss--Canton de Vaud.", "내 아버지는 스위스 사람 — 보 주 출신이었지."], ["My mother was French by blood, English by birth.", "내 어머니는 핏줄로는 프랑스인, 태생으로는 영국인이었어."], ["I myself was born in Belgium.", "나 자신은 벨기에에서 태어났고."], ["I am a citizen of the world.’", "나는 세계의 시민이야.\""]], [["His theatrical air, as he stood with one arm on his hip within the folds of his cloak, together with his manner of disregarding his companion and addressing the opposite wall instead, seemed to intimate that he was rehearsing for the President, whose examination he was shortly to undergo, rather than troubling himself merely to enlighten so small a person as John Baptist Cavalletto.", "망토 자락 속에서 한 팔을 허리에 짚고 선 그 연극적인 태도며, 동료는 안중에도 두지 않고 그 대신 맞은편 벽을 향해 말하는 그 투를 보면, 그는 조반니 바티스타 카발레토 같은 보잘것없는 자 하나를 깨우치느라 굳이 수고하는 것이라기보다는, 머지않아 자신이 받게 될 심문의 그 재판장을 위해 예행연습을 하고 있는 듯했다."]], [["‘Call me five-and-thirty years of age.", "\"내 나이는 서른다섯쯤으로 쳐 둬."], ["I have seen the world.", "나는 세상을 두루 보았어."], ["I have lived here, and lived there, and lived like a gentleman everywhere.", "여기서도 살아 보고, 저기서도 살아 보고, 어디서나 신사처럼 살았지."], ["I have been treated and respected as a gentleman universally.", "어디서나 신사로 대접받고 존경받았어."], ["If you try to prejudice me by making out that I have lived by my wits--how do your lawyers live--your politicians--your intriguers--your men of the Exchange?’", "내가 잔재주로 먹고살았다는 식으로 몰아붙여 나를 헐뜯으려 든다면 — 너희 변호사들은, 너희 정치가들은, 너희 모사꾼들은, 너희 거래소 사내들은 대체 어떻게 먹고사는데?\""]], [["He kept his small smooth hand in constant requisition, as if it were a witness to his gentility that had often done him good service before.", "그는 그 작고 매끄러운 손을 끊임없이 동원했는데, 마치 그것이 전에도 그에게 종종 좋은 봉사를 해 온, 그의 신사다움을 증언하는 증인이라도 되는 듯했다."]], [["‘Two years ago I came to Marseilles.", "\"두 해 전에 나는 마르세유로 왔어."], ["I admit that I was poor; I had been ill.", "내가 가난했다는 건 인정하지. 나는 앓고 있었거든."], ["When your lawyers, your politicians, your intriguers, your men of the Exchange fall ill, and have not scraped money together, _they_ become poor.", "너희 변호사들이, 너희 정치가들이, 너희 모사꾼들이, 너희 거래소 사내들이 앓아눕고도 돈을 긁어모아 두지 못했다면, 그자들도 가난해지는 거야."], ["I put up at the Cross of Gold,--kept then by Monsieur Henri Barronneau--sixty-five at least, and in a failing state of health.", "나는 황금십자가 여관에 묵었는데 — 그땐 앙리 바로노 씨가 꾸려 가고 있었지 — 적어도 예순다섯에, 건강이 기우는 형편이었어."], ["I had lived in the house some four months when Monsieur Henri Barronneau had the misfortune to die;--at any rate, not a rare misfortune, that.", "내가 그 집에 넉 달쯤 살았을 때 앙리 바로노 씨가 죽는 불행을 당했지. 어쨌거나, 그게 그리 드문 불행은 아니잖아."], ["It happens without any aid of mine, pretty often.’", "내 손 하나 안 거치고도 꽤 자주 일어나는 일이지.\""]], [["John Baptist having smoked his cigarette down to his fingers’ ends, Monsieur Rigaud had the magnanimity to throw him another.", "조반니 바티스타가 궐련을 손가락 끝까지 다 피워 버리자, 리고 씨는 너그럽게도 그에게 하나를 더 던져 주었다."], ["He lighted the second at the ashes of the first, and smoked on, looking sideways at his companion, who, preoccupied with his own case, hardly looked at him.", "그는 두 번째 것을 첫 번째 것의 재에 붙여 계속 피우면서, 자기 사건에 골몰하느라 자기를 거의 쳐다보지도 않는 동료를 곁눈질로 바라보았다."]], [["‘Monsieur Barronneau left a widow.", "\"바로노 씨는 미망인을 남겼어."], ["She was two-and-twenty.", "그 여자는 스물둘이었지."], ["She had gained a reputation for beauty, and (which is often another thing) was beautiful.", "미인이라는 평판을 얻고 있었고, (흔히 그것과는 별개인 일이지만) 실제로도 아름다웠어."], ["I continued to live at the Cross of Gold.", "나는 계속 황금십자가에 살았지."], ["I married Madame Barronneau.", "나는 바로노 부인과 결혼했어."], ["It is not for me to say whether there was any great disparity in such a match.", "그런 짝이 그리 크게 어울리지 않았는지 어쨌는지는 내가 말할 바가 아니야."], ["Here I stand, with the contamination of a jail upon me; but it is possible that you may think me better suited to her than her former husband was.’", "지금 나는 감옥의 오염을 뒤집어쓴 채 여기 서 있지만, 어쩌면 너는 내가 그 여자의 전남편보다 그 여자에게 더 잘 어울린다고 여길지도 모르지.\""]], [["He had a certain air of being a handsome man--which he was not; and a certain air of being a well-bred man--which he was not.", "그에게는 잘생긴 사내인 양하는 어떤 분위기가 있었으나 — 그는 잘생기지 않았다. 그리고 교양 있는 사내인 양하는 어떤 분위기가 있었으나 — 그는 교양 있지 않았다."], ["It was mere swagger and challenge; but in this particular, as in many others, blustering assertion goes for proof, half over the world.", "그저 허세요 도발이었을 뿐이었다. 그러나 다른 많은 일에서도 그렇듯, 이 점에서도 큰소리치는 단언이 세상의 절반에서는 증거로 통하는 법이다."]], [["‘Be it as it may, Madame Barronneau approved of me.", "\"어찌 됐든, 바로노 부인은 나를 마음에 들어 했어."], ["_That_ is not to prejudice me, I hope?’", "설마 그게 나를 헐뜯을 거리가 되는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["His eye happening to light upon John Baptist with this inquiry, that little man briskly shook his head in the negative, and repeated in an argumentative tone under his breath, altro, altro, altro, altro--an infinite number of times.", "이 물음과 함께 그의 눈이 마침 조반니 바티스타에게 닿자, 그 작은 사내는 부정의 뜻으로 잽싸게 고개를 가로젓고는, 따지는 듯한 어조로 숨죽여 알트로, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로 — 이루 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Now came the difficulties of our position.", "\"이제 우리 처지의 곤란함이 닥쳐왔지. 나는 자존심이 강해."], ["I am proud. I say nothing in defence of pride, but I am proud.", "자존심을 변호하려는 건 아니지만, 나는 자존심이 강하단 말이야."], ["It is also my character to govern.", "또 다스리는 것이 내 천성이기도 해."], ["I can’t submit; I must govern.", "나는 굴복할 수 없어."], ["Unfortunately, the property of Madame Rigaud was settled upon herself.", "나는 다스려야 해. 불행히도 리고 부인의 재산은 그 여자 앞으로 묶여 있었지."], ["Such was the insane act of her late husband.", "그게 그 죽은 전남편의 미친 짓이었어."], ["More unfortunately still, she had relations.", "더 불행히도, 그 여자에겐 친척들이 있었어."], ["When a wife’s relations interpose against a husband who is a gentleman, who is proud, and who must govern, the consequences are inimical to peace.", "아내의 친척들이, 신사이고, 자존심이 강하고, 다스려야만 하는 남편을 거슬러 끼어들면, 그 결과는 평화에 해롭게 마련이지."], ["There was yet another source of difference between us.", "우리 사이엔 또 다른 불화의 씨앗이 있었어."], ["Madame Rigaud was unfortunately a little vulgar.", "불행히도 리고 부인은 좀 천박했어."], ["I sought to improve her manners and ameliorate her general tone; she (supported in this likewise by her relations) resented my endeavours.", "나는 그 여자의 몸가짐을 다듬고 전반적인 품격을 끌어올리려 애썼지. 그런데 그 여자는 (이 일에서도 친척들의 부추김을 받아) 내 노력에 발끈했어."], ["Quarrels began to arise between us; and, propagated and exaggerated by the slanders of the relations of Madame Rigaud, to become notorious to the neighbours.", "우리 사이에 싸움이 일기 시작했고, 그것이 리고 부인 친척들의 험담으로 퍼지고 부풀려져서 이웃들에게 소문이 자자해졌지."], ["It has been said that I treated Madame Rigaud with cruelty.", "내가 리고 부인을 잔인하게 대했다는 말이 돌더군."], ["I may have been seen to slap her face--nothing more.", "내가 그 여자의 뺨을 때리는 게 목격되었을 수는 있어 — 그 이상은 아니야."], ["I have a light hand; and if I have been seen apparently to correct Madame Rigaud in that manner, I have done it almost playfully.’", "나는 손이 가벼워서, 설령 내가 그런 식으로 리고 부인을 바로잡는 것처럼 보였다 해도, 그건 거의 장난삼아 한 거였어.\""]], [["If the playfulness of Monsieur Rigaud were at all expressed by his smile at this point, the relations of Madame Rigaud might have said that they would have much preferred his correcting that unfortunate woman seriously.", "만일 이 대목에서 리고 씨의 그 장난기가 그 미소에 조금이라도 드러났다고 한다면, 리고 부인의 친척들은 차라리 그가 그 불행한 여인을 진지하게 바로잡아 주는 편이 훨씬 나았겠다고 말했으리라."]], [["‘I am sensitive and brave.", "\"나는 예민하고 용감해."], ["I do not advance it as a merit to be sensitive and brave, but it is my character.", "예민하고 용감한 것을 무슨 미덕인 양 내세우는 건 아니지만, 그게 내 천성이지."], ["If the male relations of Madame Rigaud had put themselves forward openly, I should have known how to deal with them.", "리고 부인의 남자 친척들이 드러내 놓고 나섰더라면, 나는 그자들을 다룰 줄 알았을 거야."], ["They knew that, and their machinations were conducted in secret; consequently, Madame Rigaud and I were brought into frequent and unfortunate collision.", "그자들도 그걸 알았기에, 음모를 은밀히 꾸몄지. 그 결과 리고 부인과 나는 자주, 불행하게도 부딪치게 되었어."], ["Even when I wanted any little sum of money for my personal expenses, I could not obtain it without collision--and I, too, a man whose character it is to govern!", "내 개인적인 씀씀이로 푼돈을 좀 얻으려 할 때조차, 충돌 없이는 얻을 수가 없었어 — 게다가 나는 다스리는 것이 천성인 사내인데도 말이야!"], ["One night, Madame Rigaud and myself were walking amicably--I may say like lovers--on a height overhanging the sea.", "어느 날 밤, 리고 부인과 나는 바다를 굽어보는 어느 높은 곳에서 다정하게 — 연인처럼이라 해도 좋겠지 — 거닐고 있었어."], ["An evil star occasioned Madame Rigaud to advert to her relations; I reasoned with her on that subject, and remonstrated on the want of duty and devotion manifested in her allowing herself to be influenced by their jealous animosity towards her husband.", "불길한 별이 끼어 리고 부인이 자기 친척 얘기를 꺼내게 만들었지. 나는 그 일로 그 여자를 타이르고, 남편을 향한 그자들의 시샘 어린 적의에 휘둘리는 데서 드러나는 도리와 헌신의 결여를 나무랐어."], ["Madame Rigaud retorted; I retorted; Madame Rigaud grew warm; I grew warm, and provoked her.", "리고 부인이 대들었고, 나도 맞받아쳤지. 리고 부인이 흥분했고, 나도 흥분해서 그 여자를 자극했어."], ["I admit it.", "그건 인정해."], ["Frankness is a part of my character.", "솔직함도 내 천성의 한 부분이니까."], ["At length, Madame Rigaud, in an access of fury that I must ever deplore, threw herself upon me with screams of passion (no doubt those that were overheard at some distance), tore my clothes, tore my hair, lacerated my hands, trampled and trod the dust, and finally leaped over, dashing herself to death upon the rocks below.", "마침내 리고 부인이, 내가 두고두고 통탄할 격분에 사로잡혀, 격정의 비명을 지르며 (틀림없이 어느 정도 거리에서 들렸다는 그 비명이지) 나에게 달려들어, 내 옷을 찢고, 내 머리털을 쥐어뜯고, 내 손을 할퀴고, 흙먼지를 짓밟고 차더니, 끝내 뛰어내려 아래쪽 바위에 몸을 부딪쳐 죽고 말았어."], ["Such is the train of incidents which malice has perverted into my endeavouring to force from Madame Rigaud a relinquishment of her rights; and, on her persistence in a refusal to make the concession I required, struggling with her--assassinating her!’", "이것이 바로 그 일련의 사건인데, 악의가 이것을 비틀어, 내가 리고 부인에게 권리 포기를 강요하려 했고, 그 여자가 내가 요구한 양보를 끝내 거부하자 그 여자와 몸싸움을 벌여 — 그 여자를 죽였다는 식으로 만들어 버린 거야!\""]], [["He stepped aside to the ledge where the vine leaves yet lay strewn about, collected two or three, and stood wiping his hands upon them, with his back to the light.", "그는 옆으로 비켜서서, 포도 잎이 아직 여기저기 흩어져 있는 선반으로 가더니, 두세 장을 주워 들고는, 빛을 등진 채 거기에 손을 닦으며 섰다."]], [["‘Well,’ he demanded after a silence, ‘have you nothing to say to all that?’", "\"자,\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 그가 물었다. \"이 모든 얘기에 넌 할 말이 없나?\""]], [["‘It’s ugly,’ returned the little man, who had risen, and was brightening his knife upon his shoe, as he leaned an arm against the wall.", "\"흉한 얘깁니다.\" 그 작은 사내가 대꾸했다. 그는 일어나서, 한 팔을 벽에 기댄 채 신발에 칼을 갈아 번쩍이게 하고 있었다."]], [["‘What do you mean?’", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이야?\""]], [["John Baptist polished his knife in silence.", "조반니 바티스타는 말없이 칼을 닦았다."]], [["‘Do you mean that I have not represented the case correctly?’", "\"내가 사건을 제대로 말하지 않았다는 뜻인가?\""]], [["‘Al-tro!’ returned John Baptist.", "\"알 — 트로!\" 조반니 바티스타가 대꾸했다."], ["The word was an apology now, and stood for ‘Oh, by no means!’", "이제 그 단어는 사과였고, \"오, 천만에요!\"를 뜻했다."]], [["‘What then?’", "\"그럼 뭐야?\""]], [["‘Presidents and tribunals are so prejudiced.’", "\"재판장이며 법정이라는 게 워낙 편견이 심해서요.\""]], [["‘Well,’ cried the other, uneasily flinging the end of his cloak over his shoulder with an oath, ‘let them do their worst!’", "\"흥,\" 또 다른 사내가 욕설과 함께 망토 자락을 어깨 너머로 거북하게 홱 넘기며 외쳤다. \"마음대로 해 보라지!\""]], [["‘Truly I think they will,’ murmured John Baptist to himself, as he bent his head to put his knife in his sash.", "\"정말이지 그자들은 그럴 겁니다.\" 조반니 바티스타가 칼을 띠에 꽂으려고 고개를 숙이며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."]], [["Nothing more was said on either side, though they both began walking to and fro, and necessarily crossed at every turn.", "양쪽에서 더는 아무 말도 오가지 않았으나, 둘 다 서성이기 시작하여 돌 때마다 어쩔 수 없이 엇갈렸다."], ["Monsieur Rigaud sometimes stopped, as if he were going to put his case in a new light, or make some irate remonstrance; but Signor Cavalletto continuing to go slowly to and fro at a grotesque kind of jog-trot pace with his eyes turned downward, nothing came of these inclinings.", "리고 씨는 이따금 멈춰 서서, 마치 자기 사건을 새로운 관점에서 풀어놓거나 무슨 성난 항변이라도 하려는 듯했다. 그러나 카발레토 씨가 눈을 내리깐 채 기괴한 종종걸음으로 계속 느릿느릿 오락가락하는 통에, 이 멈칫거림에서는 아무것도 나오지 않았다."]], [["By-and-by the noise of the key in the lock arrested them both.", "이윽고 자물쇠에 열쇠가 들어가는 소리가 두 사람을 다 멈춰 세웠다."], ["The sound of voices succeeded, and the tread of feet.", "이어 목소리들이 들리고, 발걸음 소리가 뒤따랐다."], ["The door clashed, the voices and the feet came on, and the prison-keeper slowly ascended the stairs, followed by a guard of soldiers.", "문이 쾅 닫히더니, 목소리와 발걸음이 다가왔고, 감옥지기가 천천히 계단을 올라왔는데, 그 뒤를 병사들의 호위대가 따랐다."]], [["‘Now, Monsieur Rigaud,’ said he, pausing for a moment at the grate, with his keys in his hands, ‘have the goodness to come out.’", "\"자, 리고 씨.\" 그가 열쇠를 손에 쥔 채 창살께에 잠시 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"수고스럽지만 나오시오.\""]], [["‘I am to depart in state, I see?’", "\"보아하니 격식을 차려 떠나는 모양이군?\""]], [["‘Why, unless you did,’ returned the jailer, ‘you might depart in so many pieces that it would be difficult to get you together again.", "\"아니, 그러지 않았다간,\" 간수가 대꾸했다."], ["There’s a crowd, Monsieur Rigaud, and it doesn’t love you.’", "\"당신은 산산조각이 나서 떠나, 다시 끼워 맞추기도 어렵게 될지 모르니 말이오. 군중이 있소, 리고 씨, 그런데 그자들이 당신을 사랑하질 않거든.\""]], [["He passed on out of sight, and unlocked and unbarred a low door in the corner of the chamber.", "그는 시야 밖으로 지나가더니, 방 한구석의 낮은 문 하나를 따고 빗장을 풀었다. \"자,\""], ["‘Now,’ said he, as he opened it and appeared within, ‘come out.’", "그가 그 문을 열고 안에 모습을 드러내며 말했다. \"나오시오.\""]], [["There is no sort of whiteness in all the hues under the sun at all like the whiteness of Monsieur Rigaud’s face as it was then.", "그때의 리고 씨 얼굴빛 같은 흰빛은 태양 아래 온갖 빛깔 가운데 그 어떤 흰빛에도 견줄 수 없었다."], ["Neither is there any expression of the human countenance at all like that expression in every little line of which the frightened heart is seen to beat.", "또한 한 줄 한 줄 잔금마다 겁에 질린 심장이 뛰는 것이 들여다보이는 그런 표정 같은 표정은, 인간의 얼굴에 떠오르는 그 어떤 표정에도 견줄 수 없었다."], ["Both are conventionally compared with death; but the difference is the whole deep gulf between the struggle done, and the fight at its most desperate extremity.", "둘 다 으레 죽음에 빗대지지만, 그 차이란 곧 싸움이 다 끝나 버린 상태와, 가장 절박한 막바지에 이른 싸움 사이의, 그 깊디깊은 심연 전부였다."]], [["He lighted another of his paper cigars at his companion’s; put it tightly between his teeth; covered his head with a soft slouched hat; threw the end of his cloak over his shoulder again; and walked out into the side gallery on which the door opened, without taking any further notice of Signor Cavalletto.", "그는 동료의 것에 자신의 종이 궐련을 또 하나 붙여, 그것을 이 사이에 꽉 물고는, 부드럽고 챙이 처진 모자로 머리를 덮고, 망토 자락을 다시 어깨 너머로 넘기더니, 카발레토 씨에게는 더 이상 아무 눈길도 주지 않고 그 문이 면한 옆 회랑으로 걸어 나갔다."], ["As to that little man himself, his whole attention had become absorbed in getting near the door and looking out at it.", "그 작은 사내로 말하자면, 그의 온 정신은 그 문 가까이 다가가 밖을 내다보는 데 빨려들어 있었다."], ["Precisely as a beast might approach the opened gate of his den and eye the freedom beyond, he passed those few moments in watching and peering, until the door was closed upon him.", "꼭 짐승이 제 굴의 열린 문으로 다가가 그 너머의 자유를 노려보듯, 그는 그 몇 순간을 지켜보고 엿보며 보내다가, 마침내 문이 그를 가두며 닫혔다."]], [["There was an officer in command of the soldiers; a stout, serviceable, profoundly calm man, with his drawn sword in his hand, smoking a cigar.", "병사들을 지휘하는 장교가 하나 있었는데, 건장하고 쓸모 있어 보이며 더없이 침착한 사내로, 뽑아 든 검을 손에 들고 궐련을 피우고 있었다."], ["He very briefly directed the placing of Monsieur Rigaud in the midst of the party, put himself with consummate indifference at their head, gave the word ‘march!’ and so they all went jingling down the staircase.", "그는 아주 간략하게 리고 씨를 무리 한가운데에 세우도록 지시하고는, 더없는 무심함으로 스스로 그 선두에 서서 \"행진!\" 하고 호령했다."], ["The door clashed--the key turned--and a ray of unusual light, and a breath of unusual air, seemed to have passed through the jail, vanishing in a tiny wreath of smoke from the cigar.", "그리하여 그들은 모두 절그럭거리며 계단을 내려갔다. 문이 쾅 닫히고 — 열쇠가 돌아가고 — 한 줄기 색다른 빛과 한 모금 색다른 공기가 그 감옥을 스쳐 지나간 듯하더니, 궐련에서 피어오른 가느다란 연기 한 자락 속으로 사라졌다."]], [["Still, in his captivity, like a lower animal--like some impatient ape, or roused bear of the smaller species--the prisoner, now left solitary, had jumped upon the ledge, to lose no glimpse of this departure.", "여전히 갇힌 처지로, 하등 동물처럼 — 어떤 안달하는 원숭이나, 깨어난 작은 종류의 곰처럼 — 이제 홀로 남겨진 죄수는 이 떠나감을 한 자락도 놓치지 않으려고 선반 위로 뛰어올랐다."], ["As he yet stood clasping the grate with both hands, an uproar broke upon his hearing; yells, shrieks, oaths, threats, execrations, all comprehended in it, though (as in a storm) nothing but a raging swell of sound distinctly heard.", "그가 여전히 두 손으로 창살을 움켜쥐고 선 채로 있을 때, 한바탕 소란이 그의 귀를 덮쳐 왔다. 아우성, 비명, 욕설, 협박, 저주가 모두 그 안에 담겨 있었으나, (폭풍 속에서처럼) 또렷이 들리는 것이라곤 미친 듯 들끓는 소리의 물결뿐이었다."]], [["Excited into a still greater resemblance to a caged wild animal by his anxiety to know more, the prisoner leaped nimbly down, ran round the chamber, leaped nimbly up again, clasped the grate and tried to shake it, leaped down and ran, leaped up and listened, and never rested until the noise, becoming more and more distant, had died away.", "더 알고 싶은 조바심에 한층 더 우리에 갇힌 들짐승을 닮아 가며, 죄수는 날렵하게 뛰어내려 방 안을 빙 돌아 달리고, 다시 날렵하게 뛰어올라 창살을 움켜쥐고 흔들어 보려 하고, 뛰어내려 달리고, 뛰어올라 귀를 기울이며, 그 소리가 점점 더 멀어져 사그라들 때까지 한시도 가만있지 못했다. 그보다 훌륭한 얼마나 많은 죄수들이 그렇게 제 고귀한 심장을 닳아 없앴던가."], ["How many better prisoners have worn their noble hearts out so; no man thinking of it; not even the beloved of their souls realising it; great kings and governors, who had made them captive, careering in the sunlight jauntily, and men cheering them on.", "아무도 그것을 떠올리지 않은 채로, 그 영혼의 사랑하는 이들조차 그것을 깨닫지 못한 채로. 그들을 가둔 위대한 왕들과 통치자들은 햇빛 속을 의기양양하게 내달리고, 사람들은 그들을 향해 환호한다."], ["Even the said great personages dying in bed, making exemplary ends and sounding speeches; and polite history, more servile than their instruments, embalming them!", "그 위대한 인물들이라는 자들마저 침상에서 죽어 가며 모범적인 임종을 맞고 거창한 연설을 늘어놓으니, 그들의 도구보다도 더 비굴한 예의 바른 역사가 그들을 방부 처리하여 길이 남긴다!"]], [["At last, John Baptist, now able to choose his own spot within the compass of those walls for the exercise of his faculty of going to sleep when he would, lay down upon the bench, with his face turned over on his crossed arms, and slumbered.", "마침내 조반니 바티스타는, 이제 그 담장이 두른 범위 안에서 마음 내킬 때 잠드는 제 재주를 부릴 자기만의 자리를 고를 수 있게 되자, 의자에 누워 얼굴을 포갠 두 팔에 묻고 선잠에 들었다."], ["In his submission, in his lightness, in his good humour, in his short-lived passion, in his easy contentment with hard bread and hard stones, in his ready sleep, in his fits and starts, altogether a true son of the land that gave him birth.", "그 순순함에서, 그 가벼움에서, 그 좋은 성미에서, 그 오래가지 않는 격정에서, 딱딱한 빵과 딱딱한 돌에 대한 그 손쉬운 만족에서, 그 척척 드는 잠에서, 그 변덕과 발작에서, 그는 모든 면에서 그를 태어나게 한 그 땅의 진정한 아들이었다."]], [["The wide stare stared itself out for one while; the Sun went down in a red, green, golden glory; the stars came out in the heavens, and the fire-flies mimicked them in the lower air, as men may feebly imitate the goodness of a better order of beings; the long dusty roads and the interminable plains were in repose--and so deep a hush was on the sea, that it scarcely whispered of the time when it shall give up its dead.", "그 드넓은 노려봄도 한동안은 제풀에 노려보다 사그라들었다. 태양은 붉고 푸르고 금빛으로 영광스럽게 저물었다. 별들이 하늘에 돋아났고, 반딧불이들이 더 낮은 대기에서 그것들을 흉내 냈으니, 마치 사람들이 더 나은 차원의 존재들의 선함을 어설피 흉내 내는 것 같았다. 길고 먼지투성이인 도로들과 끝없는 평원들이 휴식에 들었다 — 그리고 바다 위에는 어찌나 깊은 정적이 깃들었던지, 제가 거두었던 죽은 자들을 내어 줄 그날에 관해서는 좀처럼 속삭이지도 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 2 Fellow Travellers", "# CHAPTER 2 Fellow Travellers"]], [["‘No more of yesterday’s howling over yonder to-day, Sir; is there?’", "\"오늘은 저 너머에서 어제처럼 울부짖는 소리가 더는 없군요. 그렇지요, 선생?\""]], [["‘I have heard none.’", "\"나는 아무 소리도 못 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘Then you may be sure there _is_ none.", "\"그럼 분명 없는 겁니다."], ["When these people howl, they howl to be heard.’", "저 사람들이 울부짖을 때는 들으라고 울부짖거든요.\""]], [["‘Most people do, I suppose.’", "\"대부분의 사람들이 그렇지 않습니까.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["but these people are always howling.", "하지만 저 사람들은 늘 울부짖습니다."], ["Never happy otherwise.’", "그러지 않고는 도무지 행복하질 못해요.\""]], [["‘Do you mean the Marseilles people?’", "\"마르세유 사람들 말씀이오?\""]], [["‘I mean the French people.", "\"프랑스 사람들 말입니다."], ["They’re always at it.", "노상 그러고 있지요."], ["As to Marseilles, we know what Marseilles is.", "마르세유로 말하자면, 우리는 마르세유가 어떤 곳인지 알지 않습니까."], ["It sent the most insurrectionary tune into the world that was ever composed.", "여태껏 작곡된 것 가운데 가장 폭동을 부추기는 곡조를 세상에 내보낸 곳이지요."], ["It couldn’t exist without allonging and marshonging to something or other--victory or death, or blazes, or something.’", "무언가를 향해 '알롱'이니 '마르숑'이니 하지 않고는—승리든 죽음이든 지옥불이든 뭐든—도무지 존재할 수가 없는 곳입니다.\""]], [["The speaker, with a whimsical good humour upon him all the time, looked over the parapet-wall with the greatest disparagement of Marseilles; and taking up a determined position by putting his hands in his pockets and rattling his money at it, apostrophised it with a short laugh.", "말하는 이는 내내 익살스러운 호기를 띤 채 난간 너머로 마르세유를 더없이 깔보는 눈길로 내려다보았다. 그러고는 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 동전을 잘랑잘랑 흔들며 단호한 자세를 취하더니, 짤막한 웃음과 함께 마르세유를 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘Allong and marshong, indeed.", "\"'알롱'이고 '마르숑'이고 좋다 이겁니다."], ["It would be more creditable to you, I think, to let other people allong and marshong about their lawful business, instead of shutting ‘em up in quarantine!’", "내 생각엔 말이오, 사람들을 검역소에 가둬 둘 게 아니라 저마다 정당한 볼일을 보러 '알롱'하고 '마르숑'하게 내버려 두는 편이 당신네한테 더 명예로울 것 같소만!\""]], [["‘Tiresome enough,’ said the other.", "\"성가시기 짝이 없지요.\" 상대가 말했다."], ["‘But we shall be out to-day.’", "\"하지만 오늘은 풀려날 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Out to-day!’ repeated the first.", "\"오늘 풀려난다!\" 먼저 말한 이가 되받았다."], ["‘It’s almost an aggravation of the enormity, that we shall be out to-day.", "\"오늘 풀려난다는 게 외려 이 만행을 더 부아 돋게 하는군요. 풀려나다니!"], ["Out! What have we ever been in for?’", "대체 우리가 무엇 때문에 갇혔던 거요?\""]], [["‘For no very strong reason, I must say.", "\"그리 대단한 이유가 있던 건 아닙니다."], ["But as we come from the East, and as the East is the country of the plague--’", "다만 우리가 동방에서 왔고, 동방은 역병의 땅이니—\""]], [["‘The plague!’ repeated the other.", "\"역병이라니!\" 상대가 되받았다."], ["‘That’s my grievance.", "\"그게 바로 내 불만이오."], ["I have had the plague continually, ever since I have been here.", "여기 들어온 뒤로 줄곧 역병에 시달려 왔단 말입니다."], ["I am like a sane man shut up in a madhouse; I can’t stand the suspicion of the thing.", "마치 멀쩡한 사람이 정신병원에 갇힌 꼴이지요. 그 의심이란 걸 도무지 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["I came here as well as ever I was in my life; but to suspect me of the plague is to give me the plague.", "나는 평생 어느 때보다도 멀쩡한 몸으로 여기 들어왔소. 한데 나를 역병 환자로 의심한다는 건 나한테 역병을 옮기는 것이나 다름없소."], ["And I have had it--and I have got it.’", "그래서 나는 역병에 걸린 거요—지금도 걸려 있고요.\""]], [["‘You bear it very well, Mr Meagles,’ said the second speaker, smiling.", "\"잘도 견뎌 내시는군요, 미글스 씨.\" 두 번째로 말한 이가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘No.", "\"천만에."], ["If you knew the real state of the case, that’s the last observation you would think of making.", "사정의 진상을 아신다면 그런 말씀은 결코 떠올리지도 못하실 게요."], ["I have been waking up night after night, and saying, _now_ I have got it, _now_ it has developed itself, _now_ I am in for it, _now_ these fellows are making out their case for their precautions.", "나는 밤마다 깨어나서는 이러고 있었소. 이제 걸렸구나, 이제 증세가 나타났구나, 이제 꼼짝없이 당하는구나, 이제 저자들이 그 예방 조치의 정당성을 입증해 내겠구나, 하고 말이오."], ["Why, I’d as soon have a spit put through me, and be stuck upon a card in a collection of beetles, as lead the life I have been leading here.’", "원, 차라리 꼬챙이에 꿰여서 딱정벌레 표본 속 핀에 꽂히는 편이, 여기서 내가 살아온 꼴로 사느니 낫겠소이다.\""]], [["‘Well, Mr Meagles, say no more about it now it’s over,’ urged a cheerful feminine voice.", "\"자, 미글스 씨, 이제 다 끝난 일이니 더는 그 얘기 마세요.\" 명랑한 여인의 목소리가 타일렀다."]], [["‘Over!’ repeated Mr Meagles, who appeared (though without any ill-nature) to be in that peculiar state of mind in which the last word spoken by anybody else is a new injury.", "\"끝났다고!\" 미글스 씨가 되받았다."], ["‘Over!", "그는 (악의는 조금도 없으면서도) 남이 마지막으로 한 말이 곧 새로운 모욕처럼 들리는 그 묘한 심사에 빠진 듯했다."], ["and why should I say no more about it because it’s over?’", "\"끝났다고! 끝났다고 해서 내가 왜 그 얘기를 더는 하면 안 된다는 거요?\""]], [["It was Mrs Meagles who had spoken to Mr Meagles; and Mrs Meagles was, like Mr Meagles, comely and healthy, with a pleasant English face which had been looking at homely things for five-and-fifty years or more, and shone with a bright reflection of them.", "미글스 씨에게 말을 건넨 이는 미글스 부인이었다. 미글스 부인은 미글스 씨와 마찬가지로 곱고 건강했으며, 쉰다섯 해 남짓 정겨운 살림살이를 바라보아 온, 그래서 그 정겨움이 환히 비쳐 빛나는 유쾌한 영국식 얼굴을 하고 있었다."]], [["‘There!", "\"자!"], ["Never mind, Father, never mind!’ said Mrs Meagles.", "괜찮아요, 여보, 괜찮다니까요!\" 미글스 부인이 말했다."], ["‘For goodness sake content yourself with Pet.’", "\"제발 펫을 보며 마음을 푸세요.\""]], [["‘With Pet?’ repeated Mr Meagles in his injured vein.", "\"펫을 보며?\" 미글스 씨가 여전히 분이 가시지 않은 어투로 되받았다."], ["Pet, however, being close behind him, touched him on the shoulder, and Mr Meagles immediately forgave Marseilles from the bottom of his heart.", "그러나 펫이 바로 그의 등 뒤에 있다가 그의 어깨를 살짝 짚자, 미글스 씨는 곧바로 진심에서 우러나 마르세유를 용서했다."]], [["Pet was about twenty.", "펫은 스무 살쯤이었다."], ["A fair girl with rich brown hair hanging free in natural ringlets.", "풍성한 갈색 머리가 자연스러운 곱슬을 이루며 자유로이 늘어진, 살결 고운 처녀였다."], ["A lovely girl, with a frank face, and wonderful eyes; so large, so soft, so bright, set to such perfection in her kind good head.", "솔직한 얼굴에 놀라운 눈을 가진 사랑스러운 처녀였다. 그토록 크고, 그토록 부드럽고, 그토록 맑은 그 눈은 다정하고 어여쁜 얼굴에 더없이 완벽하게 자리 잡고 있었다."], ["She was round and fresh and dimpled and spoilt, and there was in Pet an air of timidity and dependence which was the best weakness in the world, and gave her the only crowning charm a girl so pretty and pleasant could have been without.", "그녀는 동그스름하고 싱그럽고 보조개가 패었으며 응석받이로 자랐는데, 펫에게는 세상에서 가장 어여쁜 약점이라 할 만한, 수줍음과 의지하려는 분위기가 감돌았다. 그것은 이토록 예쁘고 사랑스러운 처녀가 자칫 갖추지 못했을, 마지막 화룡점정의 매력을 그녀에게 더해 주었다."]], [["‘Now, I ask you,’ said Mr Meagles in the blandest confidence, falling back a step himself, and handing his daughter a step forward to illustrate his question: ‘I ask you simply, as between man and man, you know, DID you ever hear of such damned nonsense as putting Pet in quarantine?’", "\"자, 한번 여쭤 봅시다.\" 미글스 씨는 더없이 부드러운 신뢰를 담아, 자신은 한 걸음 물러서고 딸을 한 걸음 앞으로 내세워 자기 질문의 본보기로 삼으며 말했다. \"그저 사내 대 사내로서 묻는 거요, 아시겠소만. 펫을 검역소에 가두다니, 이런 빌어먹을 말도 안 되는 소리를 들어 보신 적이 있소이까?\""]], [["‘It has had the result of making even quarantine enjoyable.’", "\"그 덕분에 검역조차 즐거운 일이 되더군요.\""]], [["‘Come!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘that’s something to be sure.", "\"이거 보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["I am obliged to you for that remark.", "\"그건 그렇소이다. 그 말씀은 참 고맙구려."], ["Now, Pet, my darling, you had better go along with Mother and get ready for the boat.", "자, 펫, 우리 애야, 어머니랑 같이 가서 배 탈 채비를 하는 게 좋겠다."], ["The officer of health, and a variety of humbugs in cocked hats, are coming off to let us out of this at last: and all we jail-birds are to breakfast together in something approaching to a Christian style again, before we take wing for our different destinations.", "보건 담당관에다 삼각모를 쓴 갖은 사기꾼들이 드디어 우리를 여기서 풀어 주러 오고 있단다. 우리 같은 죄수들이 저마다 다른 목적지로 날아가기 전에, 다시 한번 그래도 사람 사는 꼴에 가깝게 다 같이 아침을 먹어야지."], ["Tattycoram, stick you close to your young mistress.’", "태티코럼, 너는 네 젊은 아씨 곁에 바짝 붙어 있거라.\""]], [["He spoke to a handsome girl with lustrous dark hair and eyes, and very neatly dressed, who replied with a half curtsey as she passed off in the train of Mrs Meagles and Pet.", "그는 윤기 흐르는 검은 머리와 검은 눈을 지닌, 아주 단정히 차려입은 어여쁜 처녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 미글스 부인과 펫의 뒤를 따라 지나가며 반쯤 무릎을 굽혀 인사로 답했다."], ["They crossed the bare scorched terrace all three together, and disappeared through a staring white archway.", "세 사람은 한데 어울려 헐벗고 그을린 테라스를 가로지른 뒤, 눈부시게 새하얀 아치문을 지나 사라졌다."], ["Mr Meagles’s companion, a grave dark man of forty, still stood looking towards this archway after they were gone; until Mr Meagles tapped him on the arm.", "마흔쯤 된 진중하고 가무잡잡한 사내인 미글스 씨의 동행은, 그들이 사라진 뒤에도 여전히 그 아치문 쪽을 바라보며 서 있다가, 미글스 씨가 그의 팔을 톡톡 치고서야 정신을 차렸다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ said he, starting.", "\"실례했습니다.\" 그가 흠칫하며 말했다."]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"천만에요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["They took one silent turn backward and forward in the shade of the wall, getting, at the height on which the quarantine barracks are placed, what cool refreshment of sea breeze there was at seven in the morning.", "두 사람은 담장 그늘 아래를 말없이 한 번 오갔다. 검역소 막사가 자리 잡은 그 높은 곳에서, 아침 일곱 시에 불어오는 그나마의 시원한 바닷바람으로 더위를 식히면서."], ["Mr Meagles’s companion resumed the conversation.", "미글스 씨의 동행이 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘May I ask you,’ he said, ‘what is the name of--’", "\"여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까.\" 그가 말했다. \"저—그 아가씨 이름이 무엇인지요—\""]], [["‘Tattycoram?’ Mr Meagles struck in.", "\"태티코럼 말이오?\" 미글스 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["‘I have not the least idea.’", "\"나도 도무지 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘I thought,’ said the other, ‘that--’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 상대가 말했다. \"그게—\""]], [["‘Tattycoram?’ suggested Mr Meagles again.", "\"태티코럼?\" 미글스 씨가 다시 거들었다."]], [["‘Thank you--that Tattycoram was a name; and I have several times wondered at the oddity of it.’", "\"고맙습니다—태티코럼이 이름인 줄 알았습니다. 한데 그 묘함에 여러 번 의아했지요.\""]], [["‘Why, the fact is,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘Mrs Meagles and myself are, you see, practical people.’", "\"실은 말이오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"미글스 부인과 나는, 보시다시피, 실용적인 사람들이오.\""]], [["‘That you have frequently mentioned in the course of the agreeable and interesting conversations we have had together, walking up and down on these stones,’ said the other, with a half smile breaking through the gravity of his dark face.", "\"이 돌바닥 위를 오르내리며 나눈 그 유쾌하고도 흥미로운 대화 중에 여러 번 말씀하셨지요.\" 상대가 가무잡잡한 얼굴의 진중함을 뚫고 옅은 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Practical people.", "\"실용적인 사람들이지요."], ["So one day, five or six years ago now, when we took Pet to church at the Foundling--you have heard of the Foundling Hospital in London?", "그래서 어느 날, 이젠 대여섯 해 전 일이오만, 우리가 펫을 데리고 파운들링의 예배당에 갔을 때—런던의 파운들링 병원을 들어 보셨소?"], ["Similar to the Institution for the Found Children in Paris?’", "파리의 기아 보호소와 비슷한 곳 말이오.\""]], [["‘I have seen it.’", "\"본 적이 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"그래!"], ["One day when we took Pet to church there to hear the music--because, as practical people, it is the business of our lives to show her everything that we think can please her--Mother (my usual name for Mrs Meagles) began to cry so, that it was necessary to take her out.", "어느 날 우리가 펫을 데리고 그 예배당에 음악을 들려주러 갔을 때—실용적인 사람으로서, 그 애를 기쁘게 해 줄 만하다 싶은 건 무엇이든 보여 주는 게 우리 인생의 본분이니 말이오—어머니(나는 미글스 부인을 늘 이렇게 부른다오)가 어찌나 울던지, 데리고 나와야 할 지경이었소."], ["“What’s the matter, Mother?”", "'여보, 왜 그러오?'"], ["said I, when we had brought her a little round: “you are frightening Pet, my dear.”", "어머니가 좀 진정되었을 때 내가 말했지. '여보, 당신이 펫을 놀라게 하잖소.'"], ["“Yes, I know that, Father,”", "'네, 알아요, 여보,'"], ["says Mother, “but I think it’s through my loving her so much, that it ever came into my head.”", "어머니가 말합디다. '하지만 그 애를 너무나 사랑하기 때문에 그런 생각이 떠오른 것 같아요.'"], ["“That ever what came into your head, Mother?”", "'도대체 무슨 생각이 떠올랐다는 거요, 여보?' '아이고, 이를 어째!'"], ["“O dear, dear!”", "어머니가 다시 울음을 터뜨리며 외칩디다."], ["cried Mother, breaking out again, “when I saw all those children ranged tier above tier, and appealing from the father none of them has ever known on earth, to the great Father of us all in Heaven, I thought, does any wretched mother ever come here, and look among those young faces, wondering which is the poor child she brought into this forlorn world, never through all its life to know her love, her kiss, her face, her voice, even her name!”", "'저 아이들이 층층이 줄지어 앉아서, 이 땅에서 한 번도 본 적 없는 아버지 대신 하늘에 계신 우리 모두의 위대한 아버지께 호소하는 걸 보니, 이런 생각이 들지 뭐예요. 어느 가엾은 어미가 여기 와서 저 어린 얼굴들을 둘러보며, 이 쓸쓸한 세상에 내가 낳아 놓은 가여운 아이가 어느 애일까, 평생토록 어미의 사랑도, 입맞춤도, 얼굴도, 목소리도, 심지어 이름조차 알지 못할 그 아이가 누굴까, 하고 헤아려 본 적은 없을까!'"], ["Now that was practical in Mother, and I told her so.", "자, 그게 바로 어머니다운 실용이었소. 그래서 내가 그렇게 말해 줬지."], ["I said, “Mother, that’s what I call practical in you, my dear.”’", "'여보, 그게 바로 내가 말하는 당신다운 실용이오.'\""]], [["The other, not unmoved, assented.", "상대도 마음이 동한 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘So I said next day: Now, Mother, I have a proposition to make that I think you’ll approve of.", "\"그래서 이튿날 내가 말했소. '여보, 당신이 찬성할 만한 제안이 하나 있소."], ["Let us take one of those same little children to be a little maid to Pet.", "저 어린아이들 가운데 하나를 데려다 펫의 어린 하녀로 삼읍시다."], ["We are practical people.", "우리는 실용적인 사람이오."], ["So if we should find her temper a little defective, or any of her ways a little wide of ours, we shall know what we have to take into account.", "그러니 그 아이의 성미가 좀 모자라거나, 행실이 우리와 좀 어긋난다 해도, 우리는 무엇을 참작해야 할지 알게 될 거요."], ["We shall know what an immense deduction must be made from all the influences and experiences that have formed us--no parents, no child-brother or sister, no individuality of home, no Glass Slipper, or Fairy Godmother.", "우리를 이렇게 길러 온 온갖 영향과 경험 가운데서 얼마나 엄청난 것을 빼고 봐야 하는지 알 테니 말이오—부모도 없고, 형제자매도 없고, 가정이라는 고유한 둥지도 없고, 유리 구두도, 요정 대모도 없는 아이니까.'"], ["And that’s the way we came by Tattycoram.’", "그렇게 해서 우리가 태티코럼을 데려오게 된 것이오.\""]], [["‘And the name itself--’", "\"그럼 그 이름은—\""]], [["‘By George!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I was forgetting the name itself.", "\"이런!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Why, she was called in the Institution, Harriet Beadle--an arbitrary name, of course.", "\"이름 얘기를 깜빡할 뻔했군. 그게 말이오, 그 애는 보호소에서 해리엇 비들이라 불렸소—물론 임의로 붙인 이름이지요."], ["Now, Harriet we changed into Hattey, and then into Tatty, because, as practical people, we thought even a playful name might be a new thing to her, and might have a softening and affectionate kind of effect, don’t you see?", "그래서 우리는 해리엇을 해티로 바꾸고, 다시 태티로 바꿨소. 실용적인 사람으로서, 장난스러운 이름조차 그 애한테는 새로운 경험이 될 테고, 마음을 부드럽고 다정하게 어루만지는 효험이 있을지 모른다고 생각했거든요, 무슨 말인지 아시겠소?"], ["As to Beadle, that I needn’t say was wholly out of the question.", "비들로 말하자면, 두말할 것도 없이 아예 논외였소."], ["If there is anything that is not to be tolerated on any terms, anything that is a type of Jack-in-office insolence and absurdity, anything that represents in coats, waistcoats, and big sticks our English holding on by nonsense after every one has found it out, it is a beadle.", "무슨 일이 있어도 도무지 참아 줄 수 없는 것, 하급 관리의 거들먹대는 무례와 어처구니없음의 전형이라 할 만한 것, 누구나 그 정체를 다 알아챈 뒤에도 외투에 조끼에 굵직한 지팡이까지 갖추고 허튼소리를 부여잡고 있는 우리 영국식 작태를 대변하는 것이 있다면, 그게 바로 비들이거든요."], ["You haven’t seen a beadle lately?’", "요즘 비들을 본 적 있소?\""]], [["‘As an Englishman who has been more than twenty years in China, no.’", "\"중국에서 이십 년 넘게 지낸 영국인으로서, 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Then,’ said Mr Meagles, laying his forefinger on his companion’s breast with great animation, ‘don’t you see a beadle, now, if you can help it.", "\"그렇다면,\" 미글스 씨가 자못 활기차게 동행의 가슴에 검지를 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["Whenever I see a beadle in full fig, coming down a street on a Sunday at the head of a charity school, I am obliged to turn and run away, or I should hit him.", "\"되도록이면 앞으로도 비들은 보지 마시오. 비들이 정장을 빼입고 일요일에 자선 학교 아이들을 앞세워 거리를 행진해 오는 걸 보면, 나는 돌아서서 달아나야 한다오."], ["The name of Beadle being out of the question, and the originator of the Institution for these poor foundlings having been a blessed creature of the name of Coram, we gave that name to Pet’s little maid.", "안 그러면 한 대 칠 테니까. 어쨌든 비들이라는 이름은 논외였고, 이 가엾은 기아들을 위한 보호소를 처음 세운 이가 코럼이라는 복된 분이었던 터라, 우리는 펫의 어린 하녀에게 그 이름을 붙여 준 거요."], ["At one time she was Tatty, and at one time she was Coram, until we got into a way of mixing the two names together, and now she is always Tattycoram.’", "한동안은 태티, 한동안은 코럼이었다가, 어느덧 두 이름을 섞어 부르게 됐고, 이제는 늘 태티코럼이라오.\""]], [["‘Your daughter,’ said the other, when they had taken another silent turn to and fro, and, after standing for a moment at the wall glancing down at the sea, had resumed their walk, ‘is your only child, I know, Mr Meagles.", "\"미글스 씨, 당신 따님은,\" 두 사람이 또 한 번 말없이 오가고, 잠시 담장에 서서 바다를 내려다보다가 다시 걸음을 옮겼을 때 상대가 말했다."], ["May I ask you--in no impertinent curiosity, but because I have had so much pleasure in your society, may never in this labyrinth of a world exchange a quiet word with you again, and wish to preserve an accurate remembrance of you and yours--may I ask you, if I have not gathered from your good wife that you have had other children?’", "\"외동인 줄로 압니다만. 여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까—주제넘은 호기심에서가 아니라, 당신과 함께 지내며 무척 즐거웠고, 이 미궁 같은 세상에서 다시는 당신과 조용히 말 한마디 나누지 못할지도 모르며, 당신과 당신 가족을 정확히 기억해 두고 싶어서 그러는 것입니다만—따님 말고 다른 자녀가 있으셨다는 걸 부인께서 하신 말씀에서 짐작한 게 잘못이 아니라면, 여쭤 봐도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아니오."], ["No,’ said Mr Meagles.", "아니오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Not exactly other children.", "\"정확히 다른 자녀들이라곤 할 수 없소."], ["One other child.’", "자녀 하나가 더 있었지요.\""]], [["‘I am afraid I have inadvertently touched upon a tender theme.’", "\"제가 그만 무심코 아픈 화제를 건드린 게 아닌가 합니다.\""]], [["‘Never mind,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"괜찮소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘If I am grave about it, I am not at all sorrowful.", "\"내가 이 얘기에 진중해지긴 해도, 슬프지는 않다오."], ["It quiets me for a moment, but does not make me unhappy.", "잠시 마음이 차분해질 뿐, 나를 불행하게 만들진 않소."], ["Pet had a twin sister who died when we could just see her eyes--exactly like Pet’s--above the table, as she stood on tiptoe holding by it.’", "펫에게는 쌍둥이 누이가 있었는데, 그 애가 식탁 가장자리를 붙잡고 발끝으로 서서 그 눈—펫의 눈과 똑같던 그 눈—을 겨우 식탁 위로 내밀 수 있을 무렵에 죽었다오.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["indeed, indeed!’", "저런, 저런!\""]], [["‘Yes, and being practical people, a result has gradually sprung up in the minds of Mrs Meagles and myself which perhaps you may--or perhaps you may not--understand.", "\"그렇소. 그리고 실용적인 사람인지라, 미글스 부인과 내 마음속에는 차츰 한 가지 생각이 자라났는데, 당신이라면 이해할지도—혹은 못할지도—모르겠소."], ["Pet and her baby sister were so exactly alike, and so completely one, that in our thoughts we have never been able to separate them since.", "펫과 그 갓난 누이가 어찌나 똑같고 어찌나 완전히 하나였던지, 그 뒤로 우리는 마음속에서 둘을 결코 떼어 놓을 수가 없었소."], ["It would be of no use to tell us that our dead child was a mere infant.", "죽은 우리 아이가 그저 갓난아기였을 뿐이라고 말해 봐야 우리한테는 소용없는 일이오."], ["We have changed that child according to the changes in the child spared to us and always with us.", "우리는 곁에 남아 늘 함께하는 아이가 자라는 데 맞춰 그 죽은 아이도 자라게 해 왔으니까."], ["As Pet has grown, that child has grown; as Pet has become more sensible and womanly, her sister has become more sensible and womanly by just the same degrees.", "펫이 자라면 그 아이도 자랐고, 펫이 더 철들고 여인다워지면, 그 누이도 꼭 그만큼 더 철들고 여인다워졌소."], ["It would be as hard to convince me that if I was to pass into the other world to-morrow, I should not, through the mercy of God, be received there by a daughter, just like Pet, as to persuade me that Pet herself is not a reality at my side.’", "내가 내일 저세상으로 건너간다 해도 하느님의 자비로써 펫과 꼭 닮은 딸의 마중을 받지 못하리라 나를 설득하기란, 펫 자신이 내 곁에 있는 실체가 아니라고 나를 설득하기만큼이나 어려운 일일 거요.\""]], [["‘I understand you,’ said the other, gently.", "\"이해합니다.\" 상대가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘As to her,’ pursued her father, ‘the sudden loss of her little picture and playfellow, and her early association with that mystery in which we all have our equal share, but which is not often so forcibly presented to a child, has necessarily had some influence on her character.", "\"그 애로 말하자면,\" 아버지가 말을 이었다."], ["Then, her mother and I were not young when we married, and Pet has always had a sort of grown-up life with us, though we have tried to adapt ourselves to her.", "\"제 작은 분신이자 놀이 동무를 갑작스레 잃은 일, 그리고 우리 모두가 똑같이 짊어지고 있으나 좀처럼 아이에게 그토록 강렬하게 들이닥치지는 않는 그 신비를 어린 나이에 일찍 겪은 일이, 그 애의 성격에 어느 정도 영향을 미쳤을 수밖에 없소."], ["We have been advised more than once when she has been a little ailing, to change climate and air for her as often as we could--especially at about this time of her life--and to keep her amused.", "게다가 그 애 어미와 나는 결혼할 때 젊지 않았고, 우리가 그 애한테 맞추려 애쓰긴 했어도 펫은 늘 우리와 더불어 어른스러운 생활을 해 왔지요. 그 애가 좀 시름시름할 때면, 되도록 자주 기후와 공기를 바꿔 주고—특히 그 애 나이 이맘때쯤엔 말이오—즐겁게 해 주라는 권고를 우리는 여러 번 받았소."], ["So, as I have no need to stick at a bank-desk now (though I have been poor enough in my time I assure you, or I should have married Mrs Meagles long before), we go trotting about the world.", "그래서, 이제 나는 은행 책상에 매여 있을 필요가 없으니(장담컨대 한때는 무척 가난하기도 했소. 안 그랬으면 미글스 부인과 진작 결혼했을 거요), 우리는 이렇게 세상을 떠돌아다니는 거라오."], ["This is how you found us staring at the Nile, and the Pyramids, and the Sphinxes, and the Desert, and all the rest of it; and this is how Tattycoram will be a greater traveller in course of time than Captain Cook.’", "그래서 당신은 우리가 나일강이며, 피라미드며, 스핑크스며, 사막이며, 그 밖의 온갖 것을 멍하니 바라보고 있는 꼴로 만난 거고, 그래서 태티코럼은 머지않아 쿡 선장보다 더 대단한 여행가가 될 거요.\""]], [["‘I thank you,’ said the other, ‘very heartily for your confidence.’", "\"당신의 그 허심탄회한 말씀에,\" 상대가 말했다. \"진심으로 감사드립니다.\""]], [["‘Don’t mention it,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘I am sure you are quite welcome.", "\"천만에요.\" 미글스 씨가 답했다."], ["And now, Mr Clennam, perhaps I may ask you whether you have yet come to a decision where to go next?’", "\"정말 별말씀을. 자, 이제, 클레넘 씨, 혹시 다음에 어디로 가실지 결정을 내리셨는지 여쭤 봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["‘Indeed, no.", "\"실은, 아닙니다."], ["I am such a waif and stray everywhere, that I am liable to be drifted where any current may set.’", "저는 어디서나 떠도는 부평초 같은 신세라, 어느 물결이 흐르든 그쪽으로 떠밀려 가기 십상이지요.\""]], [["‘It’s extraordinary to me--if you’ll excuse my freedom in saying so--that you don’t go straight to London,’ said Mr Meagles, in the tone of a confidential adviser.", "\"실례를 무릅쓰고 말씀드리자면, 당신이 곧장 런던으로 가지 않는다는 게 나로선 참 이상하구려.\" 미글스 씨가 흉허물 없는 조언자의 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps I shall.’", "\"아마 가게 되겠지요.\""]], [["‘Ay!", "\"그렇소!"], ["But I mean with a will.’", "한데 굳은 의지를 가지고 말이오.\""]], [["‘I have no will.", "\"제겐 의지가 없습니다. 다시 말해,\""], ["That is to say,’--he coloured a little,--‘next to none that I can put in action now.", "그는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"지금 당장 행동에 옮길 만한 의지란 거의 없다는 뜻이지요."], ["Trained by main force; broken, not bent; heavily ironed with an object on which I was never consulted and which was never mine; shipped away to the other end of the world before I was of age, and exiled there until my father’s death there, a year ago; always grinding in a mill I always hated; what is to be expected from me in middle life?", "억센 힘으로 길들여졌고, 휘어진 게 아니라 꺾였으며, 제가 한 번도 상의받은 적 없고 한 번도 제 것인 적 없던 목적을 위해 무겁게 족쇄가 채워졌고, 성년이 되기도 전에 세상 저편으로 실려 가 일 년 전 아버지가 그곳에서 돌아가실 때까지 거기 유배되어 있었으며, 늘 제가 늘 증오하던 방앗간에서 갈려 온 사람이올시다. 그런 제게 중년에 이르러 무엇을 기대하겠습니까?"], ["Will, purpose, hope?", "의지, 목적, 희망?"], ["All those lights were extinguished before I could sound the words.’", "그런 빛들은 제가 그 말들을 입에 올리기도 전에 죄다 꺼져 버렸지요.\""]], [["‘Light ‘em up again!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"다시 불을 밝히시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Easily said.", "말이야 쉽지요."], ["I am the son, Mr Meagles, of a hard father and mother.", "미글스 씨, 저는 모진 아버지와 어머니의 아들입니다."], ["I am the only child of parents who weighed, measured, and priced everything; for whom what could not be weighed, measured, and priced, had no existence.", "저는 모든 것을 달고, 재고, 값을 매기던 부모의 외아들이지요. 그들에게는 달 수도, 잴 수도, 값을 매길 수도 없는 것이라면 아예 존재하지 않는 것이었습니다."], ["Strict people as the phrase is, professors of a stern religion, their very religion was a gloomy sacrifice of tastes and sympathies that were never their own, offered up as a part of a bargain for the security of their possessions.", "흔히들 말하는 엄격한 사람들, 준엄한 종교의 신봉자들이었으나, 그 종교란 것조차 애당초 제 것인 적 없던 취향과 정서를 음울하게 제물로 바치는 것이었고, 그것은 자기네 재산의 안위를 보장받기 위한 거래의 일부로 바쳐졌지요."], ["Austere faces, inexorable discipline, penance in this world and terror in the next--nothing graceful or gentle anywhere, and the void in my cowed heart everywhere--this was my childhood, if I may so misuse the word as to apply it to such a beginning of life.’", "근엄한 얼굴들, 가차 없는 규율, 이승에서의 고행과 저승에서의 공포—어디에도 우아하거나 다정한 것이라곤 없고, 주눅 든 제 마음속 공허만이 사방에 가득했지요—이것이 제 어린 시절이었습니다. 그런 인생의 출발을 두고 그 말을 쓰는 게 잘못된 오용이 아니라면 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Really though?’ said Mr Meagles, made very uncomfortable by the picture offered to his imagination.", "\"정말 그랬소?\" 미글스 씨는 상상 속에 그려진 그 광경에 몹시 불편해하며 말했다."], ["‘That was a tough commencement.", "\"참으로 모진 출발이었구려."], ["But come!", "하지만 자!"], ["You must now study, and profit by, all that lies beyond it, like a practical man.’", "이제는 실용적인 사람답게, 그 너머에 놓인 모든 걸 배우고 활용해야 하오.\""]], [["‘If the people who are usually called practical, were practical in your direction--’", "\"흔히 실용적이라 불리는 이들이 당신 같은 방향으로 실용적이라면—\""]], [["‘Why, so they are!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"아니, 다들 그렇소!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Are they indeed?’", "\"정말 그렇습니까?\""]], [["‘Well, I suppose so,’ returned Mr Meagles, thinking about it.", "\"음, 그런 것 같소만.\" 미글스 씨가 곰곰이 생각하며 답했다."], ["‘Eh?", "\"어?"], ["One can but _be_ practical, and Mrs Meagles and myself are nothing else.’", "사람이 실용적일 수밖에 없는 거고, 미글스 부인과 나는 그저 그뿐인 사람들이오.\""]], [["‘My unknown course is easier and more helpful than I had expected to find it, then,’ said Clennam, shaking his head with his grave smile.", "\"그렇다면 제 알 수 없는 앞길이 제가 예상했던 것보다 한결 수월하고 도움이 될 것 같군요.\" 클레넘이 진중한 미소를 띤 채 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘Enough of me.", "\"제 얘긴 이쯤 합시다."], ["Here is the boat.’", "저기 배가 오는군요.\""]], [["The boat was filled with the cocked hats to which Mr Meagles entertained a national objection; and the wearers of those cocked hats landed and came up the steps, and all the impounded travellers congregated together.", "배에는 미글스 씨가 민족적 거부감을 품고 있는 삼각모들이 가득 타고 있었다. 그 삼각모를 쓴 자들이 내려 계단을 올라왔고, 갇혀 있던 여행객들이 모두 한데 모였다."], ["There was then a mighty production of papers on the part of the cocked hats, and a calling over of names, and great work of signing, sealing, stamping, inking, and sanding, with exceedingly blurred, gritty, and undecipherable results.", "그러자 삼각모들 쪽에서 서류 무더기가 대거 등장하고, 이름을 일일이 호명하고, 서명하고 봉인하고 도장 찍고 잉크를 묻히고 모래를 뿌리는 대공사가 벌어졌는데, 그 결과물은 한결같이 번지고 까칠까칠하고 도무지 알아볼 수 없는 꼴이었다."], ["Finally, everything was done according to rule, and the travellers were at liberty to depart whithersoever they would.", "마침내 모든 절차가 규정대로 끝났고, 여행객들은 어디로든 마음대로 떠날 수 있게 되었다."]], [["They made little account of stare and glare, in the new pleasure of recovering their freedom, but flitted across the harbour in gay boats, and reassembled at a great hotel, whence the sun was excluded by closed lattices, and where bare paved floors, lofty ceilings, and resounding corridors tempered the intense heat.", "자유를 되찾은 새로운 기쁨에 들떠 그들은 눈부신 햇살이며 따가운 더위 따위는 아랑곳하지 않고, 흥겨운 보트를 타고 항구를 가로질러 어느 큰 호텔에 다시 모였다. 그곳에서는 닫힌 격자창이 햇빛을 막아 주었고, 헐벗은 포석 바닥과 높은 천장과 메아리치는 복도가 그 극심한 더위를 누그러뜨렸다."], ["There, a great table in a great room was soon profusely covered with a superb repast; and the quarantine quarters became bare indeed, remembered among dainty dishes, southern fruits, cooled wines, flowers from Genoa, snow from the mountain tops, and all the colours of the rainbow flashing in the mirrors.", "거기 너른 방의 큰 식탁에는 이내 화려한 진수성찬이 푸짐하게 차려졌고, 검역소 숙소는 정말이지 휑한 곳으로, 진귀한 요리며 남방의 과일이며 차게 식힌 포도주며 제노바에서 온 꽃이며 산봉우리에서 가져온 눈이며 거울마다 무지개의 온갖 빛깔이 번쩍이는 그 한가운데서 떠올리는 기억이 되어 버렸다."]], [["‘But I bear those monotonous walls no ill-will now,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"하지만 이제는 그 단조롭던 벽들에 아무 원한도 없소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘One always begins to forgive a place as soon as it’s left behind; I dare say a prisoner begins to relent towards his prison, after he is let out.’", "\"어떤 곳이든 등지고 나서면 곧 용서하게 되는 법이지요. 죄수도 풀려나고 나면 제 감옥을 향한 마음이 풀어지기 시작할 거요.\""]], [["They were about thirty in company, and all talking; but necessarily in groups.", "일행은 서른 명쯤이었고 모두 이야기를 나누고 있었으나, 으레 그렇듯 무리를 지어서였다. 미글스 아버지와 어머니는 딸을 가운데 앉히고서, 이 세 사람이 식탁 한쪽에 자리 잡았다."], ["Father and Mother Meagles sat with their daughter between them, the last three on one side of the table: on the opposite side sat Mr Clennam; a tall French gentleman with raven hair and beard, of a swart and terrible, not to say genteelly diabolical aspect, but who had shown himself the mildest of men; and a handsome young Englishwoman, travelling quite alone, who had a proud observant face, and had either withdrawn herself from the rest or been avoided by the rest--nobody, herself excepted perhaps, could have quite decided which.", "맞은편에는 클레넘 씨가 앉았고, 칠흑 같은 머리와 수염을 지닌, 가무잡잡하고 무시무시한—점잖게 말하자면 악마 같은—풍모이면서도 더없이 온순한 사람임을 보여 온 키 큰 프랑스 신사가 앉았으며, 또 혼자서 여행하는 어여쁜 젊은 영국 여인이 있었다. 그녀는 도도하고도 살피는 듯한 얼굴을 하고 있었는데, 스스로 나머지 사람들에게서 물러난 것인지 나머지 사람들이 그녀를 피한 것인지는—어쩌면 본인을 빼고는—아무도 딱히 가려낼 수 없었다."], ["The rest of the party were of the usual materials: travellers on business, and travellers for pleasure; officers from India on leave; merchants in the Greek and Turkey trades; a clerical English husband in a meek strait-waistcoat, on a wedding trip with his young wife; a majestic English mama and papa, of the patrician order, with a family of three growing-up daughters, who were keeping a journal for the confusion of their fellow-creatures; and a deaf old English mother, tough in travel, with a very decidedly grown-up daughter indeed, which daughter went sketching about the universe in the expectation of ultimately toning herself off into the married state.", "나머지 일행은 흔한 부류였다. 볼일로 떠나온 여행객과 즐기러 떠나온 여행객, 휴가 나온 인도 주둔 장교들, 그리스와 터키 무역에 종사하는 상인들, 어린 아내와 신혼여행 중인 온순한 성직자풍 영국인 남편, 다 자라 가는 세 딸을 거느린, 귀족 부류의 위풍당당한 영국인 마마와 파파—그 딸들은 동료 인간들을 곤혹스럽게 만들 일지를 쓰고 있었다—그리고 여행에 이골이 난 귀먹은 늙은 영국인 어머니와, 정말이지 다 자라도 한참 자란 그 딸이 있었는데, 이 딸은 결국 자신을 갈고닦아 결혼이라는 상태로 마무리할 요량으로 온 우주를 누비며 스케치를 하고 다녔다."]], [["The reserved Englishwoman took up Mr Meagles in his last remark.", "내성적인 그 영국 여인이 미글스 씨의 마지막 말을 받았다."]], [["‘Do you mean that a prisoner forgives his prison?’ said she, slowly and with emphasis.", "\"죄수가 제 감옥을 용서한다는 말씀인가요?\" 그녀가 천천히, 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘That was my speculation, Miss Wade.", "\"그게 제 생각이었소, 웨이드 양."], ["I don’t pretend to know positively how a prisoner might feel.", "죄수가 어떤 심정인지 확실히 안다고 자처하는 건 아니오."], ["I never was one before.’", "나는 전에 죄수였던 적이 없으니까.\""]], [["‘Mademoiselle doubts,’ said the French gentleman in his own language, ‘it’s being so easy to forgive?’", "\"마드무아젤은 의심하시는군요.\" 프랑스 신사가 제 나라 말로 말했다. \"용서가 그토록 쉽다는 것을 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I do.’", "\"그래요.\""]], [["Pet had to translate this passage to Mr Meagles, who never by any accident acquired any knowledge whatever of the language of any country into which he travelled.", "펫은 이 대목을 미글스 씨에게 통역해 주어야 했다. 미글스 씨는 자기가 여행하는 그 어느 나라의 말도 어쩌다가라도 익힌 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["‘Oh!’ said he.", "\"오!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Dear me!", "\"이런!"], ["But that’s a pity, isn’t it?’", "그것 참 안타까운 일 아니오?\""]], [["‘That I am not credulous?’ said Miss Wade.", "\"제가 잘 믿지 못한다는 게 말입니까?\" 웨이드 양이 말했다."]], [["‘Not exactly that.", "\"꼭 그런 건 아니오."], ["Put it another way.", "달리 말합시다."], ["That you can’t believe it easy to forgive.’", "당신이 용서가 쉽다는 걸 믿지 못한다는 게 말이오.\""]], [["‘My experience,’ she quietly returned, ‘has been correcting my belief in many respects, for some years.", "\"제 경험이,\" 그녀가 조용히 받았다."], ["It is our natural progress, I have heard.’", "\"여러 면에서 제 믿음을 몇 해째 바로잡아 왔지요. 그게 우리의 자연스러운 흐름이라고 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘Well, well!", "\"원, 원!"], ["But it’s not natural to bear malice, I hope?’ said Mr Meagles, cheerily.", "하지만 원한을 품는 게 자연스러운 건 아니길 바라오만?\" 미글스 씨가 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["‘If I had been shut up in any place to pine and suffer, I should always hate that place and wish to burn it down, or raze it to the ground.", "\"제가 어딘가에 갇혀 시들고 고통받았다면, 저는 언제까지나 그곳을 미워하며 불태워 버리거나 땅바닥까지 허물어 버리고 싶을 거예요."], ["I know no more.’", "그 이상은 모르겠군요.\""]], [["‘Strong, sir?’ said Mr Meagles to the Frenchman; it being another of his habits to address individuals of all nations in idiomatic English, with a perfect conviction that they were bound to understand it somehow.", "\"강경하지요, 선생?\" 미글스 씨가 프랑스 신사에게 말했다."], ["‘Rather forcible in our fair friend, you’ll agree with me, I think?’", "어느 나라 사람에게든 영국식 관용어로 말을 걸면서, 상대가 어떻게든 알아들을 수밖에 없다고 굳게 믿는 것이 그의 또 다른 버릇이었다. \"우리 고운 벗께서 좀 과격하다 싶지 않소, 동의하시리라 보오만?\""]], [["The French gentleman courteously replied, ‘Plait-il?’ To which Mr Meagles returned with much satisfaction, ‘You are right. My opinion.’", "프랑스 신사가 정중하게 답했다. \"플레티르(뭐라고요)?\" 이에 미글스 씨가 자못 흡족하게 받았다. \"옳소. 내 생각도 그렇소.\""]], [["The breakfast beginning by-and-by to languish, Mr Meagles made the company a speech.", "이윽고 아침 식사가 시들해지기 시작하자, 미글스 씨가 일동에게 연설을 했다."], ["It was short enough and sensible enough, considering that it was a speech at all, and hearty.", "연설이라 할 만한 것치고는 충분히 짧고 충분히 분별 있었으며, 진심이 담겨 있었다."], ["It merely went to the effect that as they had all been thrown together by chance, and had all preserved a good understanding together, and were now about to disperse, and were not likely ever to find themselves all together again, what could they do better than bid farewell to one another, and give one another good-speed in a simultaneous glass of cool champagne all round the table?", "그 요지는 그저 이러했다. 우리 모두가 우연히 한자리에 모였고, 함께 잘 지내 왔으며, 이제 흩어지려 하고, 다시는 다 같이 모일 일이 없을 듯하니, 서로 작별을 고하고, 식탁을 빙 둘러 다 함께 차가운 샴페인 한 잔을 들며 서로의 앞길에 행운을 빌어 주는 것보다 더 좋은 일이 어디 있겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["It was done, and with a general shaking of hands the assembly broke up for ever.", "그대로 행해졌고, 다 함께 악수를 나누며 그 모임은 영영 흩어졌다."]], [["The solitary young lady all this time had said no more.", "이 내내 혼자였던 젊은 여인은 더는 말이 없었다."], ["She rose with the rest, and silently withdrew to a remote corner of the great room, where she sat herself on a couch in a window, seeming to watch the reflection of the water as it made a silver quivering on the bars of the lattice.", "그녀는 다른 사람들과 함께 일어나, 말없이 그 큰 방의 외진 구석으로 물러나, 창가의 긴 의자에 앉아, 물에 비친 빛이 격자창 살에 은빛으로 일렁이는 모습을 바라보는 듯했다."], ["She sat, turned away from the whole length of the apartment, as if she were lonely of her own haughty choice.", "그녀는 방 전체에 등을 돌리고 앉아, 마치 제 오만한 선택으로 외로움을 자처하는 듯했다."], ["And yet it would have been as difficult as ever to say, positively, whether she avoided the rest, or was avoided.", "그러면서도 그녀가 나머지 사람들을 피하는 것인지 사람들에게 피해지는 것인지는, 여전히 딱 잘라 말하기 어려웠다."]], [["The shadow in which she sat, falling like a gloomy veil across her forehead, accorded very well with the character of her beauty.", "그녀가 앉은 그늘이 음울한 너울처럼 이마를 가로질러 드리워, 그 미모의 성정과 무척 잘 어울렸다."], ["One could hardly see the face, so still and scornful, set off by the arched dark eyebrows, and the folds of dark hair, without wondering what its expression would be if a change came over it.", "둥글게 휜 짙은 눈썹과 검은 머리채의 굴곡이 돋보이게 하는, 그토록 고요하고 경멸 어린 그 얼굴을 보고 있노라면, 거기에 변화가 깃든다면 그 표정이 어떨까 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["That it could soften or relent, appeared next to impossible.", "그것이 누그러지거나 풀어지는 것은 거의 불가능해 보였다."], ["That it could deepen into anger or any extreme of defiance, and that it must change in that direction when it changed at all, would have been its peculiar impression upon most observers.", "그것이 분노로, 혹은 극단의 반항으로 깊어질 수 있으며, 만약 변한다면 반드시 그 방향으로 변하리라는 것이, 대부분의 관찰자에게 남는 그 얼굴 특유의 인상이었을 것이다. 그것은 어떤 격식 차린 표정으로도 매만져지거나 다듬어져 있지 않았다."], ["It was dressed and trimmed into no ceremony of expression.", "비록 열린 얼굴은 아니었으나, 거기에 가식이라곤 없었다."], ["Although not an open face, there was no pretence in it.", "\"나는 자족하고 자립한다. 너의 의견 따위 내겐 아무것도 아니다."], ["‘I am self-contained and self-reliant; your opinion is nothing to me; I have no interest in you, care nothing for you, and see and hear you with indifference’--this it said plainly.", "나는 너한테 관심도 없고, 마음 쓰지도 않으며, 너를 보고 듣는 데 무심하다\"—이렇게 그 얼굴은 또렷이 말하고 있었다."], ["It said so in the proud eyes, in the lifted nostril, in the handsome but compressed and even cruel mouth.", "그것은 도도한 눈에서, 치켜든 콧방울에서, 어여쁘되 굳게 다물려 매정하기까지 한 입에서 그렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["Cover either two of those channels of expression, and the third would have said so still.", "그 표정의 통로 가운데 둘을 가린다 해도, 나머지 하나가 여전히 그렇게 말했을 것이다."], ["Mask them all, and the mere turn of the head would have shown an unsubduable nature.", "그것을 모조리 가린다 해도, 그저 고개를 돌리는 몸짓만으로 굴복할 줄 모르는 천성이 드러났을 것이다."]], [["Pet had moved up to her (she had been the subject of remark among her family and Mr Clennam, who were now the only other occupants of the room), and was standing at her side.", "펫이 그녀에게 다가가 있었고(이제 그 방에 남은 다른 사람은 가족과 클레넘 씨뿐이었는데, 그들 사이에서 그녀가 화제에 올랐었다), 그녀 곁에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Are you’--she turned her eyes, and Pet faltered--‘expecting any one to meet you here, Miss Wade?’", "\"여기서 누구를 만나기로—\" 그녀가 눈을 돌리자 펫은 말을 더듬었다. \"—기다리고 계신가요, 웨이드 양?\" \"제가요? 아니오.\""]], [["‘I? No.’\n‘Father is sending to the Poste Restante.", "\"아버지가 유치 우편소에 사람을 보내려는데요."], ["Shall he have the pleasure of directing the messenger to ask if there are any letters for you?’", "혹시 당신 앞으로 온 편지가 있는지도 물어보라고 그 심부름꾼에게 일러 주는 기쁨을 누리게 해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I thank him, but I know there can be none.’", "\"감사합니다만, 올 편지가 없다는 걸 알아요.\""]], [["‘We are afraid,’ said Pet, sitting down beside her, shyly and half tenderly, ‘that you will feel quite deserted when we are all gone.’", "\"저희는,\" 펫이 그녀 곁에 앉으며, 수줍은 듯 반쯤 다정하게 말했다. \"저희가 다 떠나고 나면 당신이 무척 외로움을 느끼실까 봐 걱정이 돼서요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’", "\"그래요?\""]], [["‘Not,’ said Pet, apologetically and embarrassed by her eyes, ‘not, of course, that we are any company to you, or that we have been able to be so, or that we thought you wished it.’", "\"물론,\" 펫이 그녀의 눈빛에 당황하여 변명하듯 말했다. \"물론 저희가 당신께 무슨 벗이 되어 드린다거나, 그래 올 수 있었다거나, 당신이 그러길 바라셨으리라 여겨서가 아니라—\""]], [["‘I have not intended to make it understood that I did wish it.’", "\"제가 그러길 바란다는 뜻을 내비친 적은 없습니다만.\""]], [["‘No.", "\"네."], ["Of course.", "물론이지요. 하지만—요컨대,\""], ["But--in short,’ said Pet, timidly touching her hand as it lay impassive on the sofa between them, ‘will you not allow Father to tender you any slight assistance or service?", "펫이 두 사람 사이 소파 위에 무심히 놓인 그녀의 손을 머뭇머뭇 만지며 말했다. \"아버지께서 자그마한 도움이나 수고라도 베풀어 드리게 허락해 주시지 않겠어요?"], ["He will be very glad.’", "무척 기뻐하실 거예요.\""]], [["‘Very glad,’ said Mr Meagles, coming forward with his wife and Clennam.", "\"기쁘다마다요.\" 미글스 씨가 아내와 클레넘과 함께 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["‘Anything short of speaking the language, I shall be delighted to undertake, I am sure.’", "\"말을 통역하는 것만 아니라면, 무엇이든 기꺼이 맡겠소이다, 정말이오.\""]], [["‘I am obliged to you,’ she returned, ‘but my arrangements are made, and I prefer to go my own way in my own manner.’", "\"감사합니다.\" 그녀가 받았다. \"하지만 제 일은 다 정해 두었고, 저는 제 방식대로 제 길을 가는 편이 좋습니다.\""]], [["‘_Do_ you?’ said Mr Meagles to himself, as he surveyed her with a puzzled look.", "\"그러시다?\" 미글스 씨가 영문 모를 표정으로 그녀를 살피며 혼잣말처럼 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Well!", "\"원!"], ["There’s character in that, too.’", "그것도 한 성격이로군.\""]], [["‘I am not much used to the society of young ladies, and I am afraid I may not show my appreciation of it as others might.", "\"제가 젊은 숙녀분들과 어울리는 데는 그리 익숙지 못해서, 다른 사람들처럼 그 호의를 제대로 표할 줄 모를까 염려됩니다."], ["A pleasant journey to you.", "즐거운 여행 되시길 빕니다."], ["Good-bye!’", "안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["She would not have put out her hand, it seemed, but that Mr Meagles put out his so straight before her that she could not pass it.", "그녀는 손을 내밀 생각이 없는 듯했으나, 미글스 씨가 그녀 앞에 어찌나 곧장 손을 내밀던지 그것을 지나칠 수가 없었다."], ["She put hers in it, and it lay there just as it had lain upon the couch.", "그녀가 제 손을 그 안에 얹자, 그 손은 조금 전 긴 의자 위에 놓여 있던 그대로 거기 놓여 있었다."]], [["‘Good-bye!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"안녕히!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘This is the last good-bye upon the list, for Mother and I have just said it to Mr Clennam here, and he only waits to say it to Pet.", "\"이게 작별 명단의 마지막 작별이오. 어머니와 나는 방금 여기 클레넘 씨에게도 작별을 고했고, 이제 그가 펫에게 작별을 고할 일만 남았소."], ["Good-bye!", "안녕히!"], ["We may never meet again.’", "우리 다시는 못 만날지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘In our course through life we shall meet the people who are coming to meet _us_, from many strange places and by many strange roads,’ was the composed reply; ‘and what it is set to us to do to them, and what it is set to them to do to us, will all be done.’", "\"우리는 인생의 행로에서, 숱한 낯선 곳으로부터 숱한 낯선 길을 거쳐 우리를 만나러 오는 사람들을 만나게 마련이지요.\" 차분한 대답이었다. \"그리고 우리가 그들에게 해야 할 일과, 그들이 우리에게 해야 할 일은, 모두 다 이루어지고 말 거예요.\""]], [["There was something in the manner of these words that jarred upon Pet’s ear.", "이 말의 투에는 펫의 귀에 거슬리는 무언가가 있었다."], ["It implied that what was to be done was necessarily evil, and it caused her to say in a whisper, ‘O Father!’ and to shrink childishly, in her spoilt way, a little closer to him.", "그것은 행해질 일이 으레 악한 것이라는 뜻을 담고 있었고, 그래서 그녀는 \"어머, 아버지!\" 하고 속삭이며, 응석받이답게 어린아이처럼 아버지에게 조금 더 바짝 움츠려 다가들게 되었다."], ["This was not lost on the speaker.", "이것을 말한 이가 놓치지 않았다."]], [["‘Your pretty daughter,’ she said, ‘starts to think of such things. Yet,’ looking full upon her, ‘you may be sure that there are men and women already on their road, who have their business to do with _you_, and who will do it. Of a certainty they will do it. They may be coming hundreds, thousands, of miles over the sea there; they may be close at hand now; they may be coming, for anything you know or anything you can do to prevent it, from the vilest sweepings of this very town.’", "\"당신의 어여쁜 따님은,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런 일들을 생각만 해도 움찔하는군요. 하지만,\" 그녀를 똑바로 바라보며. \"이미 길 위에 올라 당신과 볼일을 치를, 그리고 끝내 그 일을 해내고야 말 남자들과 여자들이 있다는 걸 믿으셔도 좋아요. 그들은 틀림없이 해낼 거예요. 그들은 저 바다 너머로 수백, 수천 마일을 건너오고 있는지도 모르고, 지금 바로 코앞에 와 있는지도 모르지요. 당신이 무엇을 알든, 그것을 막으려 무슨 짓을 하든 상관없이, 그들은 바로 이 도시의 가장 추악한 쓰레기 더미 속에서 오고 있는지도 모르고요.\""]], [["With the coldest of farewells, and with a certain worn expression on her beauty that gave it, though scarcely yet in its prime, a wasted look, she left the room.", "더없이 싸늘한 작별 인사를 남기고, 한창때라 하기에도 아직 이른 그 미모에 어딘가 지치고 야윈 듯한 빛을 드리운 채, 그녀는 방을 나섰다."]], [["Now, there were many stairs and passages that she had to traverse in passing from that part of the spacious house to the chamber she had secured for her own occupation.", "그런데 그녀가 그 널찍한 집의 그 구역에서 자기가 차지한 방까지 가려면 여러 계단과 복도를 지나야 했다."], ["When she had almost completed the journey, and was passing along the gallery in which her room was, she heard an angry sound of muttering and sobbing.", "그 여정을 거의 다 마치고 자기 방이 있는 회랑을 지나갈 무렵, 그녀는 누군가 성난 듯 중얼거리며 흐느끼는 소리를 들었다."], ["A door stood open, and within she saw the attendant upon the girl she had just left; the maid with the curious name.", "문 하나가 열려 있었고, 그 안에서 그녀는 방금 떠나온 그 처녀의 시중을 드는 하녀를—그 묘한 이름의 하녀를 보았다."]], [["She stood still, to look at this maid.", "그녀는 가만히 서서 이 하녀를 바라보았다."], ["A sullen, passionate girl!", "시무룩하고 격정에 찬 처녀였다!"], ["Her rich black hair was all about her face, her face was flushed and hot, and as she sobbed and raged, she plucked at her lips with an unsparing hand.", "풍성한 검은 머리가 온통 얼굴에 흩어져 있었고, 얼굴은 붉게 달아올라 화끈거렸으며, 흐느끼며 길길이 날뛰는 와중에 그녀는 가차 없는 손길로 제 입술을 쥐어뜯고 있었다."]], [["‘Selfish brutes!’ said the girl, sobbing and heaving between whiles.", "\"이기적인 짐승들!\" 처녀가 흐느끼며 사이사이 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다."], ["‘Not caring what becomes of me!", "\"내가 어찌 되든 아랑곳 않다니!"], ["Leaving me here hungry and thirsty and tired, to starve, for anything they care!", "나를 여기 굶기고 목마르게 하고 지친 채로 내버려 두고, 굶어 죽든 말든 신경도 안 쓰다니!"], ["Beasts!", "짐승들!"], ["Devils!", "악마들!"], ["Wretches!’", "비열한 것들!\""]], [["‘My poor girl, what is the matter?’", "\"가엾기도 해라, 무슨 일이니?\""]], [["She looked up suddenly, with reddened eyes, and with her hands suspended, in the act of pinching her neck, freshly disfigured with great scarlet blots.", "처녀가 별안간 고개를 들었다. 시뻘게진 눈으로, 새로 시뻘건 자국이 잔뜩 난 제 목을 꼬집으려던 손을 허공에 멈춘 채였다."], ["‘It’s nothing to you what’s the matter.", "\"무슨 일이든 댁이랑은 상관없어요."], ["It don’t signify to any one.’", "누구한테도 아무 상관 없는 일이라고요.\""]], [["‘O yes it does; I am sorry to see you so.’", "\"오, 아니야, 상관있단다. 네가 그러는 걸 보니 마음이 아파.\""]], [["‘You are not sorry,’ said the girl.", "\"마음이 아프긴요.\" 처녀가 말했다."], ["‘You are glad.", "\"고소한 거겠지."], ["You know you are glad.", "고소해하는 거 다 알아요."], ["I never was like this but twice over in the quarantine yonder; and both times you found me.", "저 너머 검역소에서도 내가 딱 두 번 이랬는데, 두 번 다 댁이 날 봤죠."], ["I am afraid of you.’", "난 댁이 무서워요.\""]], [["‘Afraid of me?’", "\"내가 무섭다고?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["You seem to come like my own anger, my own malice, my own--whatever it is--I don’t know what it is.", "댁은 꼭 내 분노 같고, 내 악의 같고, 내—그게 뭐든 간에—나도 그게 뭔지 모르겠지만, 그런 것처럼 나타나니까요."], ["But I am ill-used, I am ill-used, I am ill-used!’ Here the sobs and the tears, and the tearing hand, which had all been suspended together since the first surprise, went on together anew.", "하지만 난 학대받고 있어요, 학대받고 있다고요, 학대받고 있단 말이에요!\" 이쯤에서 처음 놀라움에 멎었던 흐느낌과 눈물과 쥐어뜯던 손길이 다시 한꺼번에 되살아났다."]], [["The visitor stood looking at her with a strange attentive smile.", "찾아온 여인은 기이하게도 골똘한 미소를 띤 채 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["It was wonderful to see the fury of the contest in the girl, and the bodily struggle she made as if she were rent by the Demons of old.", "처녀 안에서 벌어지는 싸움의 격렬함과, 마치 옛날의 악령들에게 갈가리 찢기기라도 하는 듯 그녀가 벌이는 육체의 몸부림을 지켜보는 것은 놀라운 일이었다."]], [["‘I am younger than she is by two or three years, and yet it’s me that looks after her, as if I was old, and it’s she that’s always petted and called Baby!", "\"내가 그 애보다 두세 살은 어린데도, 마치 내가 늙은이라도 되는 양 그 애를 돌보는 건 나고, 늘 떠받들리며 '아기'라 불리는 건 그 애예요! 난 그 이름이 진절머리 나요."], ["I detest the name. I hate her!", "난 그 애가 미워요!"], ["They make a fool of her, they spoil her.", "사람들은 그 애를 바보로 만들고, 응석받이로 망쳐 놓죠. 그 앤 제 생각밖에 안 해요."], ["She thinks of nothing but herself, she thinks no more of me than if I was a stock and a stone!’ So the girl went on.", "내가 무슨 나무토막이나 돌멩이라도 되는 양, 나 따위는 안중에도 없다고요!\" 처녀는 계속 그렇게 쏟아 냈다."]], [["‘You must have patience.’", "\"참아야 한단다.\""]], [["‘I _won’t_ have patience!’", "\"안 참을 거예요!\""]], [["‘If they take much care of themselves, and little or none of you, you must not mind it.’", "\"저들이 제 몸만 끔찍이 위하고 너는 거의, 아니 전혀 위하지 않는다 해도, 너는 마음에 두어선 안 돼.\""]], [["I _will_ mind it.’", "\"마음에 둘 거예요.\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Be more prudent.", "좀 더 신중해야지."], ["You forget your dependent position.’", "네 처지가 남에게 매인 신세라는 걸 잊었구나.\""]], [["‘I don’t care for that.", "\"그딴 거 상관없어요."], ["I’ll run away.", "도망쳐 버릴 거예요."], ["I’ll do some mischief.", "무슨 사달을 내고 말 거예요. 못 견뎌요."], ["I won’t bear it; I can’t bear it; I shall die if I try to bear it!’", "견딜 수가 없어요. 견디려 들다간 죽고 말 거라고요!\""]], [["The observer stood with her hand upon her own bosom, looking at the girl, as one afflicted with a diseased part might curiously watch the dissection and exposition of an analogous case.", "지켜보던 여인은 제 가슴에 손을 얹은 채 처녀를 바라보고 서 있었다. 마치 병든 환부를 지닌 사람이 그와 비슷한 증례를 해부하고 펼쳐 보이는 광경을 호기심 어린 눈으로 지켜보기라도 하듯이."]], [["The girl raged and battled with all the force of her youth and fulness of life, until by little and little her passionate exclamations trailed off into broken murmurs as if she were in pain.", "처녀는 제 젊음과 차오르는 생명력을 다 쏟아 길길이 날뛰며 싸웠고, 그러다 차츰차츰 그 격정에 찬 외침이 마치 아픔에 겨운 듯 끊긴 신음으로 잦아들었다."], ["By corresponding degrees she sank into a chair, then upon her knees, then upon the ground beside the bed, drawing the coverlet with her, half to hide her shamed head and wet hair in it, and half, as it seemed, to embrace it, rather than have nothing to take to her repentant breast.", "그에 맞춰 그녀는 차츰 의자에 주저앉았다가, 무릎을 꿇었다가, 침대 옆 바닥에 쓰러져, 이불을 끌어당겨 부끄러운 머리와 젖은 머리채를 반쯤 그 속에 파묻었다. 그리고 반쯤은, 회개하는 가슴에 끌어안을 무언가가 아무것도 없느니 차라리 그것이라도 끌어안으려는 듯 보였다."]], [["‘Go away from me, go away from me!", "\"저리 가요, 저리 가 줘요!"], ["When my temper comes upon me, I am mad.", "성미가 발동하면 난 미쳐 버려요."], ["I know I might keep it off if I only tried hard enough, and sometimes I do try hard enough, and at other times I don’t and won’t.", "마음만 단단히 먹으면 그걸 떨쳐 낼 수 있다는 걸 알아요. 어떤 때는 정말 단단히 마음먹기도 하고, 또 어떤 때는 그러지 않고 그러기도 싫어요."], ["What have I said!", "내가 무슨 말을 한 거죠!"], ["I knew when I said it, it was all lies.", "그 말을 할 때 이미 다 거짓말인 줄 알았어요."], ["They think I am being taken care of somewhere, and have all I want.", "저들은 내가 어딘가에서 보살핌받으며 원하는 걸 다 누리는 줄 알아요."], ["They are nothing but good to me.", "저들은 내게 그저 잘해 주기만 할 뿐이에요."], ["I love them dearly; no people could ever be kinder to a thankless creature than they always are to me.", "난 저들을 진심으로 사랑해요. 나처럼 은혜도 모르는 것한테, 저들이 늘 그러듯 그토록 다정하게 대해 줄 사람은 세상에 없을 거예요."], ["Do, do go away, for I am afraid of you.", "제발, 제발 가 줘요. 난 댁이 무서우니까요."], ["I am afraid of myself when I feel my temper coming, and I am as much afraid of you.", "내 성미가 발동하는 게 느껴지면 난 내 자신이 무서워요. 그리고 댁도 그만큼 무서워요."], ["Go away from me, and let me pray and cry myself better!’", "저리 가 줘요. 나 혼자 기도하고 울면서 마음을 다스리게요!\""]], [["The day passed on; and again the wide stare stared itself out; and the hot night was on Marseilles; and through it the caravan of the morning, all dispersed, went their appointed ways.", "날이 저물어 갔다. 그리고 그 드넓은 햇살이 다시 한번 제풀에 사위었으며, 무더운 밤이 마르세유에 내렸다."], ["And thus ever by day and night, under the sun and under the stars, climbing the dusty hills and toiling along the weary plains, journeying by land and journeying by sea, coming and going so strangely, to meet and to act and react on one another, move all we restless travellers through the pilgrimage of life.", "그 밤을 가르며, 아침의 그 대상(隊商)은 죄다 흩어져 저마다 정해진 길을 갔다. 그리하여 낮에도 밤에도, 햇빛 아래서도 별빛 아래서도, 먼지 이는 언덕을 오르고 지친 평원을 터벅터벅 가로지르며, 뭍으로 가고 바다로 가며, 그토록 기이하게 오가며, 서로 만나고 서로에게 작용하고 반작용하며, 우리 쉴 줄 모르는 모든 여행자는 인생이라는 순례의 길을 나아가는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 3. Home", "# CHAPTER 3. Home"]], [["It was a Sunday evening in London, gloomy, close, and stale.", "런던의 어느 일요일 저녁이었다. 음울하고, 후텁지근하고, 퀴퀴했다."], ["Maddening church bells of all degrees of dissonance, sharp and flat, cracked and clear, fast and slow, made the brick-and-mortar echoes hideous.", "온갖 정도의 불협화음을 내는, 날카롭고 둔탁한, 갈라지고 맑은, 빠르고 느린 미칠 듯한 교회 종소리들이 벽돌과 회반죽의 메아리를 흉측하게 울렸다."], ["Melancholy streets, in a penitential garb of soot, steeped the souls of the people who were condemned to look at them out of windows, in dire despondency.", "그을음으로 참회복을 두른 듯한 우울한 거리들은, 창밖으로 그것들을 바라보도록 단죄된 사람들의 영혼을 끔찍한 의기소침 속에 담가 두었다."], ["In every thoroughfare, up almost every alley, and down almost every turning, some doleful bell was throbbing, jerking, tolling, as if the Plague were in the city and the dead-carts were going round.", "모든 큰길에서, 거의 모든 골목 위에서, 거의 모든 모퉁이 아래에서, 어떤 구슬픈 종소리가 고동치고, 덜컥거리고, 울려 댔다. 마치 도시에 역병이 돌고 시신을 실은 수레들이 돌아다니기라도 하는 것처럼."], ["Everything was bolted and barred that could by possibility furnish relief to an overworked people.", "지칠 대로 지친 사람들에게 조금이라도 위안을 줄 수 있을 법한 것은 무엇이든 빗장이 채워지고 막혀 있었다."], ["No pictures, no unfamiliar animals, no rare plants or flowers, no natural or artificial wonders of the ancient world--all _taboo_ with that enlightened strictness, that the ugly South Sea gods in the British Museum might have supposed themselves at home again.", "그림도, 낯선 동물도, 진귀한 식물이나 꽃도, 고대 세계의 자연적이거나 인공적인 경이도 없었다 — 모두가 그 계몽된 엄격함으로 금기시되어 있어서, 대영 박물관에 있는 흉측한 남태평양의 신상들마저 다시 제 고향에 온 줄 알았을 정도였다."], ["Nothing to see but streets, streets, streets.", "보이는 것이라곤 거리, 거리, 거리뿐."], ["Nothing to breathe but streets, streets, streets.", "들이마실 것이라곤 거리, 거리, 거리뿐."], ["Nothing to change the brooding mind, or raise it up.", "골똘히 잠긴 마음을 바꾸거나 일으켜 세울 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Nothing for the spent toiler to do, but to compare the monotony of his seventh day with the monotony of his six days, think what a weary life he led, and make the best of it--or the worst, according to the probabilities.", "기진맥진한 노동자가 할 수 있는 일이라곤, 자신의 이렛날의 단조로움을 엿새의 단조로움과 견주어 보고, 자기가 얼마나 고단한 삶을 살고 있는지 생각하고, 그것을 최선으로 — 혹은 형편에 따라 최악으로 — 받아들이는 것뿐이었다."]], [["At such a happy time, so propitious to the interests of religion and morality, Mr Arthur Clennam, newly arrived from Marseilles by way of Dover, and by Dover coach the Blue-eyed Maid, sat in the window of a coffee-house on Ludgate Hill.", "종교와 도덕의 이익에 그토록 길조인, 그런 행복한 시각에, 도버를 거쳐, 도버발 마차 푸른 눈의 처녀호를 타고 마르세유에서 막 도착한 아서 클레넘 씨는 러드게이트 힐의 어느 커피점 창가에 앉아 있었다."], ["Ten thousand responsible houses surrounded him, frowning as heavily on the streets they composed, as if they were every one inhabited by the ten young men of the Calender’s story, who blackened their faces and bemoaned their miseries every night.", "일만 채의 점잖은 집들이 그를 에워싸고서, 자기들이 이루는 거리들을 향해 무겁게 찌푸리고 있었다. 마치 한 집 한 집마다, 매일 밤 얼굴을 검게 칠하고 자신들의 비참을 탄식했다는 캘린더 이야기 속의 그 열 명의 젊은이가 살고 있기라도 한 것처럼."], ["Fifty thousand lairs surrounded him where people lived so unwholesomely that fair water put into their crowded rooms on Saturday night, would be corrupt on Sunday morning; albeit my lord, their county member, was amazed that they failed to sleep in company with their butcher’s meat.", "오만 개의 소굴이 그를 에워쌌는데, 그곳 사람들은 어찌나 건강에 해롭게 사는지, 토요일 밤에 그들의 비좁은 방 안에 들여놓은 맑은 물도 일요일 아침이면 썩어 있을 정도였다. 그럼에도 그들의 주(州) 하원의원인 나리께서는, 그들이 푸줏간 고기와 한방에서 자지 않는 것을 의아해하셨다."], ["Miles of close wells and pits of houses, where the inhabitants gasped for air, stretched far away towards every point of the compass.", "사방의 나침반 방위 어디로든, 주민들이 공기를 찾아 헐떡이는 비좁은 우물 같고 구덩이 같은 집들이 몇 마일이고 뻗어 있었다."], ["Through the heart of the town a deadly sewer ebbed and flowed, in the place of a fine fresh river.", "맑고 깨끗한 강이 흘러야 할 자리에, 도시의 한복판으로는 죽음의 하수가 밀려왔다 밀려갔다 했다."], ["What secular want could the million or so of human beings whose daily labour, six days in the week, lay among these Arcadian objects, from the sweet sameness of which they had no escape between the cradle and the grave--what secular want could they possibly have upon their seventh day?", "일주일에 엿새를, 요람과 무덤 사이에서 결코 벗어날 수 없는 그 달콤한 단조로움으로 이루어진 이 아르카디아적 정경 한가운데서 날마다 노동에 매여 사는 백만쯤 되는 인간들에게, 이렛날에 세속적 욕구가 무엇이 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["Clearly they could want nothing but a stringent policeman.", "분명, 그들이 원할 수 있는 것이라곤 단속을 철저히 하는 경찰관 한 명뿐이었다."]], [["Mr Arthur Clennam sat in the window of the coffee-house on Ludgate Hill, counting one of the neighbouring bells, making sentences and burdens of songs out of it in spite of himself, and wondering how many sick people it might be the death of in the course of the year.", "아서 클레넘 씨는 러드게이트 힐의 그 커피점 창가에 앉아, 이웃의 종 하나가 울리는 것을 세고 있었고, 자기도 모르게 그것으로 문장을 짓고 노래의 후렴을 만들고 있었으며, 한 해 동안 그것이 얼마나 많은 병자의 죽음을 재촉할는지 궁금해하고 있었다."], ["As the hour approached, its changes of measure made it more and more exasperating.", "정각이 다가오자, 그 가락의 변화가 점점 더 사람을 부아 나게 했다."], ["At the quarter, it went off into a condition of deadly-lively importunity, urging the populace in a voluble manner to Come to church, Come to church, Come to church!", "십오 분 전에는 죽도록 활기찬 보챔의 상태로 돌입하여, 군중에게 청산유수로 졸라 댔다, 교회로 오라, 교회로 오라, 교회로 오라!"], ["At the ten minutes, it became aware that the congregation would be scanty, and slowly hammered out in low spirits, They _won’t_ come, they _won’t_ come, they _won’t_ come!", "십 분 전에는 회중이 변변치 못하리라는 것을 알아채고는, 풀 죽은 채로 느릿느릿 두드려 댔다, 안 올 거야, 안 올 거야, 안 올 거야!"], ["At the five minutes, it abandoned hope, and shook every house in the neighbourhood for three hundred seconds, with one dismal swing per second, as a groan of despair.", "오 분 전에는 희망을 버리고, 삼백 초 동안 일대의 모든 집을 흔들어 댔다, 일 초에 한 번씩 음울하게 흔들리며, 절망의 신음처럼."]], [["‘Thank Heaven!’ said Clennam, when the hour struck, and the bell stopped.", "\"하느님 감사합니다!\" 정각이 울리고 종이 멎자, 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["But its sound had revived a long train of miserable Sundays, and the procession would not stop with the bell, but continued to march on.", "그러나 그 소리는 비참했던 일요일들의 긴 행렬을 되살려 놓았고, 그 행렬은 종소리와 함께 멎지 않고 계속 행진해 나갔다. \"하느님, 저를 용서하소서,\""], ["‘Heaven forgive me,’ said he, ‘and those who trained me.", "그가 말했다, \"그리고 저를 그렇게 길러 낸 이들도."], ["How I have hated this day!’", "내가 이날을 얼마나 미워해 왔던가!\""]], [["There was the dreary Sunday of his childhood, when he sat with his hands before him, scared out of his senses by a horrible tract which commenced business with the poor child by asking him in its title, why he was going to Perdition?--a piece of curiosity that he really, in a frock and drawers, was not in a condition to satisfy--and which, for the further attraction of his infant mind, had a parenthesis in every other line with some such hiccupping reference as 2 Ep.", "손을 앞에 모으고 앉아, 그 끔찍한 소책자에 넋이 나가 있던 어린 시절의 그 음산한 일요일이 있었다. 그 책자는 표제에서부터 그 가엾은 아이에게 어째서 너는 멸망으로 가려느냐고 물으며 일을 시작했는데 — 그건 아동복에 짧은 바지 차림인 그가 정말이지 충족시켜 줄 처지가 못 되는 궁금증이었다 — 게다가 그의 어린 마음을 한층 더 끌어들이기 위해, 한 줄 걸러 한 줄마다 데살로니가 후서 3장 6절 및 7절 따위의 딸꾹질하는 듯한 인용을 괄호 안에 넣어 두었다."], ["Thess. c.", "군대 탈영병처럼 교사들의 분견대에 끌려 하루 세 번 예배당으로 행진하던, 다른 아이와 도덕적으로 수갑이 채워진 채로 끌려가던 소년 시절의 그 졸음 겨운 일요일이 있었다."], ["iii, v. 6 & 7.", "그때 그는 소화도 안 되는 설교 두 끼를, 그 변변찮은 저녁상의 질 낮은 양고기 한두 온스와 기꺼이 맞바꾸었을 것이다."], ["There was the sleepy Sunday of his boyhood, when, like a military deserter, he was marched to chapel by a picquet of teachers three times a day, morally handcuffed to another boy; and when he would willingly have bartered two meals of indigestible sermon for another ounce or two of inferior mutton at his scanty dinner in the flesh.", "그의 미성년 시절의 그 끝없는 일요일이 있었다."], ["There was the interminable Sunday of his nonage; when his mother, stern of face and unrelenting of heart, would sit all day behind a Bible--bound, like her own construction of it, in the hardest, barest, and straitest boards, with one dinted ornament on the cover like the drag of a chain, and a wrathful sprinkling of red upon the edges of the leaves--as if it, of all books!", "그때 그의 어머니는, 얼굴은 준엄하고 마음은 무자비한 채로, 온종일 성경책 뒤에 앉아 있곤 했다 — 그 성경은, 어머니 자신이 그것을 해석하는 방식 그대로, 가장 단단하고, 가장 휑하고, 가장 빡빡한 표지로 제본되어 있었으며, 표지 위에는 사슬을 끄는 자국 같은 움푹 팬 장식이 하나 있었고, 책장 가장자리에는 분노한 듯 붉은색이 뿌려져 있었다 — 마치 그것이, 하고많은 책 중에서 하필!"], ["were a fortification against sweetness of temper, natural affection, and gentle intercourse.", "온화한 성정과, 천성적인 애정과, 부드러운 교제를 막아 내는 요새이기라도 한 것처럼."], ["There was the resentful Sunday of a little later, when he sat down glowering and glooming through the tardy length of the day, with a sullen sense of injury in his heart, and no more real knowledge of the beneficent history of the New Testament than if he had been bred among idolaters.", "그보다 조금 뒤의 그 원망스러운 일요일이 있었다. 그때 그는 마음속에 부당함을 당했다는 뚱한 감각을 품고서, 더디게 흘러가는 그 긴 하루 내내 노려보고 침울해하며 앉아 있었으되, 신약성서의 그 자애로운 이야기에 관해서는 우상 숭배자들 틈에서 자란 것이나 다름없이 진정으로 아는 것이 없었다."], ["There was a legion of Sundays, all days of unserviceable bitterness and mortification, slowly passing before him.", "무수한 일요일들의 군단이, 모두가 쓸모없는 쓰라림과 굴욕의 날들이, 그의 앞을 천천히 지나갔다."]], [["‘Beg pardon, sir,’ said a brisk waiter, rubbing the table.", "\"실례합니다, 손님,\" 발랄한 종업원이 탁자를 닦으며 말했다."], ["‘Wish see bed-room?’", "\"방을 보시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그러지."], ["I have just made up my mind to do it.’", "방금 그러기로 마음을 정했네.\""]], [["‘Chaymaid!’ cried the waiter.", "\"객실 담당!\" 종업원이 외쳤다."], ["‘Gelen box num seven wish see room!’", "\"칠 번 좌석 신사분 방 보시겠답니다!\""]], [["‘Stay!’ said Clennam, rousing himself.", "\"기다리게!\" 클레넘이 정신을 차리며 말했다."], ["‘I was not thinking of what I said; I answered mechanically.", "\"내가 한 말을 깊이 생각하고 한 게 아니었네. 기계적으로 대답한 거야."], ["I am not going to sleep here.", "나는 여기서 자지 않을 걸세."], ["I am going home.’", "집으로 갈 거야.\""]], [["‘Deed, sir?", "\"그러십니까, 손님?"], ["Chaymaid!", "객실 담당!"], ["Gelen box num seven, not go sleep here, gome.’", "칠 번 좌석 신사분, 여기서 안 주무시고 댁으로 가신답니다.\""]], [["He sat in the same place as the day died, looking at the dull houses opposite, and thinking, if the disembodied spirits of former inhabitants were ever conscious of them, how they must pity themselves for their old places of imprisonment.", "날이 저물어 가는 동안 그는 같은 자리에 앉아, 맞은편의 칙칙한 집들을 바라보며 생각했다, 만약 예전 거주자들의 육체에서 떠난 혼령들이 그 집들을 의식하는 일이 있다면, 자신들이 갇혔던 그 옛 자리를 두고 얼마나 스스로를 가엾이 여길까 하고."], ["Sometimes a face would appear behind the dingy glass of a window, and would fade away into the gloom as if it had seen enough of life and had vanished out of it.", "이따금 어떤 얼굴이 창문의 거무튀튀한 유리 뒤에 나타났다가, 마치 삶을 충분히 보고 거기서 사라져 버리기라도 한 것처럼 어둠 속으로 희미하게 스러지곤 했다."], ["Presently the rain began to fall in slanting lines between him and those houses, and people began to collect under cover of the public passage opposite, and to look out hopelessly at the sky as the rain dropped thicker and faster.", "이윽고 그와 그 집들 사이로 비가 비스듬한 선을 그으며 내리기 시작했고, 사람들이 맞은편 공공 통로의 처마 밑에 모여들어, 빗방울이 점점 굵고 빠르게 떨어지는 동안 가망 없이 하늘을 올려다보기 시작했다."], ["Then wet umbrellas began to appear, draggled skirts, and mud.", "그러더니 젖은 우산들이 나타나기 시작했고, 흙탕물에 끌린 치맛자락들과, 진창이 보이기 시작했다."], ["What the mud had been doing with itself, or where it came from, who could say?", "그 진창이 그동안 어디서 무엇을 하고 있었는지, 어디서 온 것인지 누가 말할 수 있으랴?"], ["But it seemed to collect in a moment, as a crowd will, and in five minutes to have splashed all the sons and daughters of Adam.", "그러나 그것은 군중이 그러하듯 순식간에 모여드는 것 같았고, 오 분 만에 아담의 모든 자손에게 튀어 묻어 버린 것 같았다."], ["The lamplighter was going his rounds now; and as the fiery jets sprang up under his touch, one might have fancied them astonished at being suffered to introduce any show of brightness into such a dismal scene.", "점등부가 이제 순회를 돌고 있었다. 그의 손길 아래 불꽃의 분출이 솟아오를 때면, 그것들이 이토록 음산한 정경에 조금이라도 밝음의 기색을 들여놓도록 허락받았다는 데에 놀라워한다고 상상해 볼 만했다."]], [["Mr Arthur Clennam took up his hat and buttoned his coat, and walked out.", "아서 클레넘 씨는 모자를 집어 들고 외투의 단추를 채운 다음, 밖으로 나갔다."], ["In the country, the rain would have developed a thousand fresh scents, and every drop would have had its bright association with some beautiful form of growth or life.", "시골이었다면, 비는 신선한 향기를 수천 가지 피워 냈을 것이고, 빗방울 하나하나마다 어떤 아름다운 성장이나 생명의 형태와 결부된 밝은 연상을 지녔을 것이다."], ["In the city, it developed only foul stale smells, and was a sickly, lukewarm, dirt-stained, wretched addition to the gutters.", "도시에서는, 비가 더럽고 퀴퀴한 냄새만 피워 냈고, 도랑에 더해진 병약하고, 미적지근하고, 흙얼룩으로 더럽혀진, 비참한 보탬일 뿐이었다."]], [["He crossed by St Paul’s and went down, at a long angle, almost to the water’s edge, through some of the crooked and descending streets which lie (and lay more crookedly and closely then) between the river and Cheapside.", "그는 세인트폴 성당 옆을 지나, 강과 칩사이드 사이에 놓여 있는(그리고 그때는 더 구불구불하고 더 빽빽하게 놓여 있던) 구부러져 내려가는 길들을 따라, 긴 사선을 그리며 거의 강가에 닿을 만큼 내려갔다."], ["Passing, now the mouldy hall of some obsolete Worshipful Company, now the illuminated windows of a Congregationless Church that seemed to be waiting for some adventurous Belzoni to dig it out and discover its history; passing silent warehouses and wharves, and here and there a narrow alley leading to the river, where a wretched little bill, FOUND DROWNED, was weeping on the wet wall; he came at last to the house he sought.", "이제는 한물간 어느 동업 조합의 곰팡내 나는 회관을 지나고, 어느 모험심 강한 벨초니가 파내어 그 내력을 밝혀 주기를 기다리고 있는 듯한, 회중 없는 어느 교회의 불 밝힌 창문을 지나고, 고요한 창고들과 부두들을 지나고, 이따금 강으로 통하는 좁은 골목 — 거기서는 익사체 발견이라는 가엾고 작은 벽보 하나가 젖은 벽 위에서 울고 있었다 — 을 지나, 마침내 그는 자기가 찾던 집에 이르렀다."], ["An old brick house, so dingy as to be all but black, standing by itself within a gateway.", "어찌나 거무칙칙한지 거의 검다고 할 만한 오래된 벽돌집으로, 대문 안쪽에 홀로 서 있었다."], ["Before it, a square court-yard where a shrub or two and a patch of grass were as rank (which is saying much) as the iron railings enclosing them were rusty; behind it, a jumble of roots.", "그 앞에는 네모난 안마당이 있었는데, 거기 자라난 관목 한두 그루와 풀밭 한 뙈기는, 그것들을 둘러싼 쇠난간이 녹슨 만큼이나(이건 대단한 말이다) 무성하게 웃자라 있었다. 그 뒤로는 뿌리들이 어수선하게 엉켜 있었다."], ["It was a double house, with long, narrow, heavily-framed windows.", "길고, 좁고, 묵직한 테를 두른 창문이 달린 이층 양옆집이었다. 여러 해 전, 그 집은 옆으로 미끄러져 내려앉을 마음을 먹은 적이 있었다."], ["Many years ago, it had had it in its mind to slide down sideways; it had been propped up, however, and was leaning on some half-dozen gigantic crutches: which gymnasium for the neighbouring cats, weather-stained, smoke-blackened, and overgrown with weeds, appeared in these latter days to be no very sure reliance.", "그러나 떠받쳐져서, 대여섯 개의 거대한 버팀목에 기대고 있었다. 이웃 고양이들의 체육관이 된 그 버팀목들은, 비바람에 얼룩지고, 연기에 검게 그을리고, 잡초가 무성하게 뒤덮여, 요사이에는 그다지 미덥지 못한 의지처로 보였다."]], [["‘Nothing changed,’ said the traveller, stopping to look round.", "\"하나도 안 변했군,\" 여행자가 둘러보려고 걸음을 멈추며 말했다."], ["‘Dark and miserable as ever.", "\"여전히 어둡고 비참하군."], ["A light in my mother’s window, which seems never to have been extinguished since I came home twice a year from school, and dragged my box over this pavement.", "어머니 방의 불빛은, 내가 일 년에 두 번 학교에서 돌아와 이 포석 위로 내 상자를 끌고 오던 시절 이래 단 한 번도 꺼진 적이 없는 것 같군."], ["Well, well, well!’", "그래, 그래, 그래!\""]], [["He went up to the door, which had a projecting canopy in carved work of festooned jack-towels and children’s heads with water on the brain, designed after a once-popular monumental pattern, and knocked.", "그는 문 앞으로 다가갔다. 그 문에는, 한때 인기 있던 기념비 양식을 본떠 만든, 둥글게 늘어뜨린 닦이수건과 뇌수종에 걸린 아이들의 머리를 새긴 조각 세공의 차양이 돌출해 있었다."], ["A shuffling step was soon heard on the stone floor of the hall, and the door was opened by an old man, bent and dried, but with keen eyes.", "그는 문을 두드렸다. 곧 현관의 돌바닥 위로 질질 끄는 발소리가 들렸고, 등이 굽고 비쩍 말랐지만 눈매는 날카로운 한 노인이 문을 열었다."]], [["He had a candle in his hand, and he held it up for a moment to assist his keen eyes.", "그는 손에 촛불을 들고 있었는데, 날카로운 눈을 거들기 위해 그것을 잠시 치켜들었다."], ["‘Ah, Mr Arthur?’ he said, without any emotion, ‘you are come at last?", "\"아, 아서 도련님이오?\" 그가 아무런 감정도 없이 말했다, \"드디어 오셨구려?"], ["Step in.’", "들어오시오.\""]], [["Mr Arthur stepped in and shut the door.", "아서 도련님은 안으로 들어가 문을 닫았다."]], [["‘Your figure is filled out, and set,’ said the old man, turning to look at him with the light raised again, and shaking his head; ‘but you don’t come up to your father in my opinion.", "\"몸이 떡 벌어지고, 자리를 잡았구려,\" 노인이 다시 촛불을 치켜들고 그를 보려고 돌아서며, 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."], ["Nor yet your mother.’", "\"허나 내 보기엔 부친만 못하시오. 모친만도 못하고.\""]], [["‘How is my mother?’", "\"어머니는 어떠신가요?\""]], [["‘She is as she always is now.", "\"이제 늘 그러신 그대로요."], ["Keeps her room when not actually bedridden, and hasn’t been out of it fifteen times in as many years, Arthur.’ They had walked into a spare, meagre dining-room.", "실제로 몸져눕지 않을 때도 방에만 계시고, 십몇 년 동안 그 방 밖으로 나오신 게 열다섯 번도 안 되오, 아서 도련님.\""], ["The old man had put the candlestick upon the table, and, supporting his right elbow with his left hand, was smoothing his leathern jaws while he looked at the visitor.", "그들은 휑하고 빈약한 식당으로 걸어 들어와 있었다. 노인은 촛대를 탁자 위에 내려놓고, 왼손으로 오른쪽 팔꿈치를 받친 채, 손님을 바라보며 가죽 같은 턱을 쓸어 만지고 있었다."], ["The visitor offered his hand.", "손님이 손을 내밀었다."], ["The old man took it coldly enough, and seemed to prefer his jaws, to which he returned as soon as he could.", "노인은 꽤 차갑게 그 손을 잡았다가, 자기 턱이 더 좋은 듯, 되도록 빨리 거기로 손을 되돌렸다."]], [["‘I doubt if your mother will approve of your coming home on the Sabbath, Arthur,’ he said, shaking his head warily.", "\"도련님이 안식일에 집에 오신 것을 모친께서 좋아하실는지 의문이오, 아서 도련님,\" 그가 조심스레 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["‘You wouldn’t have me go away again?’", "\"그럼 내가 다시 떠나기라도 하란 말인가요?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I? I?", "내가?"], ["I am not the master.", "내가? 나는 주인이 아니오."], ["It’s not what _I_ would have.", "내가 원하고 말고 할 게 아니지."], ["I have stood between your father and mother for a number of years. I don’t pretend to stand between your mother and you.’", "나는 여러 해 동안 도련님 부친과 모친 사이에 서 있었소만, 모친과 도련님 사이에 서겠다고 나설 처지는 아니오.\""]], [["‘Will you tell her that I have come home?’", "\"내가 집에 왔다고 어머니께 전해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Yes, Arthur, yes.", "\"예, 아서 도련님, 예."], ["Oh, to be sure!", "오, 물론이지요!"], ["I’ll tell her that you have come home.", "도련님이 집에 오셨다고 전하리다."], ["Please to wait here.", "여기서 기다리시오."], ["You won’t find the room changed.’ He took another candle from a cupboard, lighted it, left the first on the table, and went upon his errand.", "방은 변한 게 없을 거외다.\" 그는 찬장에서 다른 촛불을 하나 꺼내 불을 붙이고, 첫 번째 것은 탁자 위에 남겨 둔 채, 심부름을 하러 갔다."], ["He was a short, bald old man, in a high-shouldered black coat and waistcoat, drab breeches, and long drab gaiters.", "그는 키가 작고 대머리인 노인으로, 어깨가 높이 솟은 검은 외투와 조끼에, 황갈색 반바지와 긴 황갈색 각반을 차고 있었다."], ["He might, from his dress, have been either clerk or servant, and in fact had long been both.", "옷차림으로 보아서는 서기일 수도 하인일 수도 있었는데, 사실 그는 오랫동안 둘 다였다."], ["There was nothing about him in the way of decoration but a watch, which was lowered into the depths of its proper pocket by an old black ribbon, and had a tarnished copper key moored above it, to show where it was sunk.", "장식이라고는 시계 하나뿐이었는데, 그것은 낡은 검은 리본에 매달려 제 주머니의 깊은 곳으로 내려가 있었고, 그것이 가라앉은 자리를 알려 주려고 위에는 변색된 구리 열쇠가 닻처럼 매여 있었다."], ["His head was awry, and he had a one-sided, crab-like way with him, as if his foundations had yielded at about the same time as those of the house, and he ought to have been propped up in a similar manner.", "그의 머리는 비뚜름했고, 마치 그 집의 토대가 무너진 것과 거의 같은 시기에 자기 토대도 무너져서, 비슷한 방식으로 떠받쳐졌어야 했던 것처럼, 그에게는 한쪽으로 기운, 게 같은 거동이 있었다."]], [["‘How weak am I,’ said Arthur Clennam, when he was gone, ‘that I could shed tears at this reception!", "\"내가 어찌 이리 나약한가,\" 노인이 가고 나자 아서 클레넘이 말했다, \"이런 대접에 눈물을 흘릴 수 있다니!"], ["I, who have never experienced anything else; who have never expected anything else.’", "다른 것이라고는 겪어 본 적도 없는 내가, 다른 것이라고는 기대해 본 적도 없는 내가.\""]], [["He not only could, but did.", "그는 눈물을 흘릴 수 있었을 뿐 아니라, 정말로 흘렸다."], ["It was the momentary yielding of a nature that had been disappointed from the dawn of its perceptions, but had not quite given up all its hopeful yearnings yet.", "그것은 지각이 동튼 순간부터 실망해 왔으되 아직 그 희망 어린 갈망을 모두 다 포기하지는 않은, 어떤 천성의 순간적인 무너짐이었다."], ["He subdued it, took up the candle, and examined the room.", "그는 그것을 억누르고, 촛불을 집어 들어 방을 살펴보았다."], ["The old articles of furniture were in their old places; the Plagues of Egypt, much the dimmer for the fly and smoke plagues of London, were framed and glazed upon the walls.", "옛 가구들은 제자리에 그대로 있었고, 이집트의 재앙들을 그린 그림들은 — 런던의 파리와 연기의 재앙으로 한결 더 흐릿해진 채 — 액자에 끼워져 유리를 두르고 벽에 걸려 있었다."], ["There was the old cellaret with nothing in it, lined with lead, like a sort of coffin in compartments; there was the old dark closet, also with nothing in it, of which he had been many a time the sole contents, in days of punishment, when he had regarded it as the veritable entrance to that bourne to which the tract had found him galloping.", "안에 아무것도 들어 있지 않은, 칸칸이 나뉜 일종의 관(棺)처럼 납으로 안을 댄 그 옛 술 보관함이 있었다. 역시 안에 아무것도 없는 그 옛 어두운 벽장도 있었는데, 그는 벌받던 시절에 여러 번 그 유일한 내용물이 되곤 했고, 그때는 그곳을, 그 소책자가 그가 질주해 가고 있다고 본 그 머나먼 세계로 들어가는 진짜 입구로 여겼었다."], ["There was the large, hard-featured clock on the sideboard, which he used to see bending its figured brows upon him with a savage joy when he was behind-hand with his lessons, and which, when it was wound up once a week with an iron handle, used to sound as if it were growling in ferocious anticipation of the miseries into which it would bring him.", "찬장 위에는 그 크고, 험상궂은 시계가 있었는데, 그가 공부를 게을리할 때면 그것이 야만스러운 기쁨으로 자기를 향해 숫자 새겨진 눈썹을 찌푸리는 것을 그는 보곤 했고, 일주일에 한 번 쇠손잡이로 태엽을 감을 때면, 그것이 자기를 끌어들일 비참한 일들을 사납게 고대하며 으르렁거리는 것처럼 소리를 내곤 했다."], ["But here was the old man come back, saying, ‘Arthur, I’ll go before and light you.’", "그러나 어느새 노인이 돌아와서 말했다, \"아서 도련님, 내가 앞장서서 불을 밝혀 드리리다.\""]], [["Arthur followed him up the staircase, which was panelled off into spaces like so many mourning tablets, into a dim bed-chamber, the floor of which had gradually so sunk and settled, that the fire-place was in a dell.", "아서는 그를 따라 계단을 올라갔다. 그 계단은 마치 수많은 추모 명판처럼 칸칸이 판벽으로 나뉘어 있었다."], ["On a black bier-like sofa in this hollow, propped up behind with one great angular black bolster like the block at a state execution in the good old times, sat his mother in a widow’s dress.", "그렇게 올라간 곳은 어둑한 침실이었는데, 바닥이 차츰 가라앉고 내려앉아서, 벽난로가 움푹한 골짜기 안에 있었다. 이 우묵한 곳에는 검은, 관대(棺臺) 같은 소파가 있었고, 그 위에, 옛 좋은 시절 국사범 처형 때 쓰던 받침대처럼 모가 진 커다란 검은 베개 하나에 등을 기댄 채, 미망인 복장을 한 그의 어머니가 앉아 있었다."]], [["She and his father had been at variance from his earliest remembrance.", "어머니와 아버지는 그가 기억하는 가장 이른 시절부터 사이가 틀어져 있었다."], ["To sit speechless himself in the midst of rigid silence, glancing in dread from the one averted face to the other, had been the peacefullest occupation of his childhood.", "뻣뻣한 침묵 한가운데서 그 자신도 말없이 앉아, 외면한 한쪽 얼굴에서 다른 쪽 얼굴로 두려움에 차서 흘끔거리는 것이, 그의 어린 시절의 가장 평화로운 일과였다."], ["She gave him one glassy kiss, and four stiff fingers muffled in worsted.", "어머니는 그에게 유리알 같은 입맞춤 한 번과, 털실로 감싼 뻣뻣한 손가락 네 개를 내주었다."], ["This embrace concluded, he sat down on the opposite side of her little table.", "이 포옹이 끝나자, 그는 어머니의 작은 탁자 맞은편에 앉았다."], ["There was a fire in the grate, as there had been night and day for fifteen years.", "십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 벽난로 안에는 불이 있었다."], ["There was a kettle on the hob, as there had been night and day for fifteen years.", "십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 벽난로 시렁 위에는 주전자가 있었다."], ["There was a little mound of damped ashes on the top of the fire, and another little mound swept together under the grate, as there had been night and day for fifteen years.", "십오 년 동안 밤낮으로 그래 왔듯이, 불 위에는 적셔진 재가 작은 둔덕을 이루고 있었고, 또 다른 작은 둔덕은 벽난로 아래 쓸려 모여 있었다."], ["There was a smell of black dye in the airless room, which the fire had been drawing out of the crape and stuff of the widow’s dress for fifteen months, and out of the bier-like sofa for fifteen years.", "통풍 안 되는 그 방에는 검은 염료 냄새가 났는데, 불기운이 그것을 십오 개월 동안 미망인 복장의 크레이프와 옷감에서, 그리고 십오 년 동안 관대 같은 소파에서 뽑아내고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘Mother, this is a change from your old active habits.’", "\"어머니, 예전의 활동적이시던 모습과는 사뭇 달라지셨네요.\""]], [["‘The world has narrowed to these dimensions, Arthur,’ she replied, glancing round the room.", "\"세상이 이만한 크기로 좁아졌단다, 아서,\" 그녀가 방 안을 둘러보며 대답했다."], ["‘It is well for me that I never set my heart upon its hollow vanities.’", "\"내가 세상의 그 헛된 허영에 결코 마음을 두지 않았던 것이 내겐 다행이지.\""]], [["The old influence of her presence and her stern strong voice, so gathered about her son, that he felt conscious of a renewal of the timid chill and reserve of his childhood.", "그녀의 존재와 그 준엄하고 강한 목소리가 자아내던 옛 영향력이 아들 주위로 어찌나 짙게 모여드는지, 그는 어린 시절의 그 겁먹은 한기와 위축이 되살아나는 것을 의식했다."]], [["‘Do you never leave your room, mother?’", "\"방을 결코 떠나지 않으시나요, 어머니?\""]], [["‘What with my rheumatic affection, and what with its attendant debility or nervous weakness--names are of no matter now--I have lost the use of my limbs.", "\"내 류머티즘 질환에다, 거기 따라오는 쇠약이라든가 신경의 약함이라든가 — 이제 그 이름이야 아무 상관 없지만 — 그것 때문에 사지를 못 쓰게 되었다."], ["I never leave my room.", "나는 결코 방을 떠나지 않아."], ["I have not been outside this door for--tell him for how long,’ she said, speaking over her shoulder.", "이 문밖으로 나가 본 지가 — 얼마나 됐는지 저 사람한테 말해 주거라,\" 그녀가 어깨 너머로 말했다."]], [["‘A dozen year next Christmas,’ returned a cracked voice out of the dimness behind.", "\"오는 성탄절이면 열두 해입니다,\" 뒤편 어둠 속에서 갈라진 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["‘Is that Affery?’ said Arthur, looking towards it.", "\"애프리인가?\" 아서가 그쪽을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["The cracked voice replied that it was Affery: and an old woman came forward into what doubtful light there was, and kissed her hand once; then subsided again into the dimness.", "갈라진 목소리는 애프리라고 대답했다. 그러더니 한 노파가 어슴푸레한 빛 속으로 앞으로 나와, 한 번 그의 손에 입을 맞추고는, 다시 어둠 속으로 물러났다."]], [["‘I am able,’ said Mrs Clennam, with a slight motion of her worsted-muffled right hand toward a chair on wheels, standing before a tall writing cabinet close shut up, ‘I am able to attend to my business duties, and I am thankful for the privilege.", "\"나는 할 수 있다,\" 클레넘 부인이 털실로 감싼 오른손을, 단단히 닫힌 키 큰 필기용 캐비닛 앞에 놓인 바퀴 달린 의자 쪽으로 살짝 움직이며 말했다, \"나는 내 사무를 처리할 수 있고, 그 특권에 감사한다."], ["It is a great privilege.", "그것은 큰 특권이지."], ["But no more of business on this day.", "그러나 오늘은 더 이상 사무 이야기는 그만두자."], ["It is a bad night, is it not?’", "사나운 밤이다, 그렇지 않으냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, mother.’", "\"네, 어머니.\""]], [["‘Does it snow?’", "\"눈이 오느냐?\""]], [["‘Snow, mother?", "\"눈이요, 어머니?"], ["And we only yet in September?’", "아직 9월일 뿐인데요?\""]], [["‘All seasons are alike to me,’ she returned, with a grim kind of luxuriousness.", "\"내겐 계절이 다 마찬가지다,\" 그녀가 일종의 음울한 호사스러움으로 대답했다."], ["‘I know nothing of summer and winter, shut up here.", "\"이 안에 갇혀서, 나는 여름도 겨울도 모른다."], ["The Lord has been pleased to put me beyond all that.’ With her cold grey eyes and her cold grey hair, and her immovable face, as stiff as the folds of her stony head-dress,--her being beyond the reach of the seasons seemed but a fit sequence to her being beyond the reach of all changing emotions.", "주께서 나를 그 모든 것 너머에 두기를 기뻐하셨지.\" 그녀의 차가운 잿빛 눈과 차가운 잿빛 머리칼, 그리고 돌처럼 단단한 머리쓰개의 주름만큼이나 뻣뻣한, 그 미동도 없는 얼굴로 보아 — 그녀가 계절의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있다는 것은, 그녀가 변화하는 온갖 감정의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있다는 것의 딱 맞는 귀결로 보일 따름이었다."]], [["On her little table lay two or three books, her handkerchief, a pair of steel spectacles newly taken off, and an old-fashioned gold watch in a heavy double case.", "그녀의 작은 탁자 위에는 책 두세 권, 손수건, 방금 벗어 놓은 강철테 안경, 그리고 묵직한 이중 뚜껑이 달린 구식 금시계가 놓여 있었다."], ["Upon this last object her son’s eyes and her own now rested together.", "이 마지막 물건 위에 이제 아들의 눈과 그녀 자신의 눈이 함께 머물렀다."]], [["‘I see that you received the packet I sent you on my father’s death, safely, mother.’", "\"아버지가 돌아가셨을 때 제가 어머니께 보낸 꾸러미를 무사히 받으셨군요, 어머니.\""]], [["‘You see.’", "\"보다시피.\""]], [["‘I never knew my father to show so much anxiety on any subject, as that his watch should be sent straight to you.’", "\"아버지가 어떤 일에 대해서도 그토록 마음 졸이시는 걸 본 적이 없는데, 그 시계를 어머니께 곧장 보내라는 것만은 그렇게 마음 졸이셨지요.\""]], [["‘I keep it here as a remembrance of your father.’", "\"네 아버지를 기리는 추억으로 그것을 여기 두고 있다.\""]], [["‘It was not until the last, that he expressed the wish; when he could only put his hand upon it, and very indistinctly say to me “your mother.”", "\"바로 마지막에 가서야 그 바람을 입 밖에 내셨지요. 그때는 겨우 그 위에 손을 얹고서, 아주 불분명하게 제게 '네 어머니'라고 말씀하실 수 있을 뿐이었어요."], ["A moment before, I thought him wandering in his mind, as he had been for many hours--I think he had no consciousness of pain in his short illness--when I saw him turn himself in his bed and try to open it.’", "그 한순간 전만 해도, 저는 아버지가 정신이 흐려져 헤매시는 줄 알았어요. 여러 시간 동안 그러셨거든요 — 짧은 병환 중에 아버지가 통증을 의식하지는 못하셨던 것 같습니다 — 그때 아버지가 침대에서 몸을 돌려 그것을 열려고 애쓰시는 것을 보았지요.\""]], [["‘Was your father, then, not wandering in his mind when he tried to open it?’", "\"그럼 네 아버지가 그것을 열려고 하셨을 때는 정신이 흐려진 게 아니었느냐?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아니었어요."], ["He was quite sensible at that time.’", "그때는 아주 또렷하셨습니다.\""]], [["Mrs Clennam shook her head; whether in dismissal of the deceased or opposing herself to her son’s opinion, was not clearly expressed.", "클레넘 부인은 고개를 가로저었다. 고인을 떨쳐 버리려는 것인지, 아들의 의견에 맞서는 것인지는 분명히 드러나지 않았다."]], [["‘After my father’s death I opened it myself, thinking there might be, for anything I knew, some memorandum there.", "\"아버지가 돌아가신 뒤에 제가 직접 그것을 열어 보았어요. 혹시 제가 모르는 무슨 메모 같은 게 거기 있을까 해서요."], ["However, as I need not tell you, mother, there was nothing but the old silk watch-paper worked in beads, which you found (no doubt) in its place between the cases, where I found and left it.’", "그러나, 어머니께 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없이, 거기에는 구슬로 수놓은 그 옛 비단 시계 종이밖에 없었어요. 그건 어머니도 (틀림없이) 뚜껑들 사이의 제자리에서 발견하셨을 그것으로, 저도 거기서 발견하고 그대로 두었던 겁니다.\""]], [["Mrs Clennam signified assent; then added, ‘No more of business on this day,’ and then added, ‘Affery, it is nine o’clock.’", "클레넘 부인은 그렇다는 뜻을 표하고는, 덧붙여 말했다, \"오늘은 더 이상 사무 이야기는 그만.\" 그러고는 또 덧붙였다, \"애프리, 아홉 시다.\""]], [["Upon this, the old woman cleared the little table, went out of the room, and quickly returned with a tray on which was a dish of little rusks and a small precise pat of butter, cool, symmetrical, white, and plump.", "이 말에, 노파는 작은 탁자를 치우고 방을 나갔다가, 곧 쟁반을 들고 돌아왔다. 그 위에는 작은 러스크가 담긴 접시와, 시원하고, 좌우 대칭이고, 희고, 통통한, 한 조각 단정한 버터가 놓여 있었다."], ["The old man who had been standing by the door in one attitude during the whole interview, looking at the mother up-stairs as he had looked at the son down-stairs, went out at the same time, and, after a longer absence, returned with another tray on which was the greater part of a bottle of port wine (which, to judge by his panting, he had brought from the cellar), a lemon, a sugar-basin, and a spice box.", "면담이 진행되는 내내 한 가지 자세로 문가에 서 있던 그 노인은 — 아래층에서 아들을 보았던 것처럼 위층에서 어머니를 바라보면서 — 같은 시각에 나갔다가, 더 오래 자리를 비운 뒤에 또 다른 쟁반을 들고 돌아왔다. 그 위에는 (헐떡이는 모양으로 미루어 지하 저장실에서 가져온 듯한) 거의 한 병 가까이 남은 포트와인과, 레몬 하나와, 설탕 그릇과, 향신료 상자가 놓여 있었다."], ["With these materials and the aid of the kettle, he filled a tumbler with a hot and odorous mixture, measured out and compounded with as much nicety as a physician’s prescription.", "이 재료들과 주전자의 도움으로, 그는 의사의 처방만큼이나 꼼꼼하게 분량을 재고 배합하여, 뜨겁고 향내 나는 혼합액을 큰 잔에 가득 채웠다."], ["Into this mixture Mrs Clennam dipped certain of the rusks, and ate them; while the old woman buttered certain other of the rusks, which were to be eaten alone.", "클레넘 부인은 이 혼합액에 러스크 몇 개를 담갔다가 먹었고, 그동안 노파는 따로 먹을 다른 러스크 몇 개에 버터를 발랐다."], ["When the invalid had eaten all the rusks and drunk all the mixture, the two trays were removed; and the books and the candle, watch, handkerchief, and spectacles were replaced upon the table.", "병약한 부인이 러스크를 다 먹고 혼합액을 다 마시자, 두 쟁반이 치워졌다. 그리고 책들과 촛불, 시계, 손수건, 안경이 탁자 위에 다시 놓였다."], ["She then put on the spectacles and read certain passages aloud from a book--sternly, fiercely, wrathfully--praying that her enemies (she made them by her tone and manner expressly hers) might be put to the edge of the sword, consumed by fire, smitten by plagues and leprosy, that their bones might be ground to dust, and that they might be utterly exterminated.", "그러자 그녀는 안경을 쓰고 책에서 어떤 구절들을 소리 내어 읽었다 — 준엄하게, 사납게, 노여움에 차서 — 자신의 원수들이(그녀는 어조와 태도로 그들을 명백히 자기의 원수로 만들었다) 칼날에 맞고, 불에 타고, 역병과 나병에 시달리고, 그 뼈가 가루로 빻아지고, 송두리째 멸절되기를 기도하며."], ["As she read on, years seemed to fall away from her son like the imaginings of a dream, and all the old dark horrors of his usual preparation for the sleep of an innocent child to overshadow him.", "그녀가 계속 읽어 나가는 동안, 세월은 꿈속의 환영처럼 아들에게서 떨어져 나가는 듯했고, 죄 없는 어린아이가 잠자리에 들 채비를 하던 그 흔한 시간에 겪던 옛 어두운 공포들이 다 그를 다시 뒤덮어 오는 듯했다."]], [["She shut the book and remained for a little time with her face shaded by her hand.", "그녀는 책을 덮고, 잠시 동안 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 있었다."], ["So did the old man, otherwise still unchanged in attitude; so, probably, did the old woman in her dimmer part of the room.", "노인도 그렇게 했는데, 그 밖에는 여전히 자세를 바꾸지 않았다. 어쩌면 방의 더 어둑한 자리에 있던 노파도 그렇게 했을 것이다."], ["Then the sick woman was ready for bed.", "그런 다음 병든 부인은 잠자리에 들 준비가 되었다."]], [["‘Good night, Arthur.", "\"잘 자거라, 아서."], ["Affery will see to your accommodation.", "잠자리는 애프리가 봐 줄 게다. 그저 살짝만 닿거라, 내 손이 약하니.\""], ["Only touch me, for my hand is tender.’ He touched the worsted muffling of her hand--that was nothing; if his mother had been sheathed in brass there would have been no new barrier between them--and followed the old man and woman down-stairs.", "그는 털실로 감싼 그녀의 손을 만졌다 — 그건 아무것도 아니었다. 어머니가 놋쇠로 감싸여 있었다 한들, 둘 사이에 새로운 장벽이 생겨났을 리는 없었으니까 — 그러고는 노인과 노파를 따라 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["The latter asked him, when they were alone together among the heavy shadows of the dining-room, would he have some supper?", "식당의 짙은 그림자 속에 단둘이 남게 되자, 노파가 그에게 물었다, 야식을 좀 드시겠느냐고."]], [["‘No, Affery, no supper.’", "\"아니, 애프리, 야식은 됐네.\""]], [["‘You shall if you like,’ said Affery.", "\"드시고 싶으면 드셔도 돼요,\" 애프리가 말했다."], ["‘There’s her tomorrow’s partridge in the larder--her first this year; say the word and I’ll cook it.’", "\"마님 드실 내일 자고새가 식료품 저장실에 있어요 — 올해 첫 거예요. 말씀만 하시면 제가 요리해 드릴게요.\""]], [["No, he had not long dined, and could eat nothing.", "아니, 그는 저녁을 먹은 지 얼마 안 됐고, 아무것도 먹을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Have something to drink, then,’ said Affery; ‘you shall have some of her bottle of port, if you like.", "\"그럼 뭐든 마실 거라도 드세요,\" 애프리가 말했다."], ["I’ll tell Jeremiah that you ordered me to bring it you.’", "\"원하시면 마님 포트와인을 좀 드시게 해 드릴게요. 도련님이 가져오라고 시키셨다고 제러마이아한테 말할게요.\""]], [["No; nor would he have that, either.", "아니, 그는 그것도 마시지 않으려 했다."]], [["‘It’s no reason, Arthur,’ said the old woman, bending over him to whisper, ‘that because I am afeared of my life of ‘em, you should be.", "\"이건 말이 안 되잖아요, 아서 도련님,\" 노파가 그에게 몸을 숙여 속삭였다, \"제가 무서워서 벌벌 떤다고 해서 도련님까지 그러실 건 없잖아요."], ["You’ve got half the property, haven’t you?’", "도련님이 재산의 절반을 가지셨잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes.’", "\"그래, 그렇지.\""]], [["‘Well then, don’t you be cowed.", "\"그럼, 기죽지 마세요."], ["You’re clever, Arthur, an’t you?’", "도련님은 영리하시잖아요, 아서 도련님, 안 그래요?\""]], [["He nodded, as she seemed to expect an answer in the affirmative.", "그녀가 그렇다는 대답을 기다리는 것 같아, 그는 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Then stand up against them!", "\"그럼 그들한테 맞서세요!"], ["She’s awful clever, and none but a clever one durst say a word to her.", "마님은 엄청나게 영리하셔서, 영리한 사람이 아니고서는 아무도 마님께 감히 한마디도 못 해요."], ["_He’s_ a clever one--oh, he’s a clever one!--and he gives it her when he has a mind to’t, he does!’", "그 양반도 영리해요 — 오, 그 양반은 영리하다니까요! — 그리고 그 양반은 마음만 먹으면 마님한테 따끔하게 해 줘요, 정말 그런다니까요!\""]], [["‘Your husband does?’", "\"댁의 남편이 말인가?\""]], [["‘Does?", "\"그런다니까요?"], ["It makes me shake from head to foot, to hear him give it her.", "그 양반이 마님한테 따끔하게 하는 걸 들으면 저는 머리부터 발끝까지 벌벌 떨려요."], ["My husband, Jeremiah Flintwinch, can conquer even your mother.", "제 남편 제러마이아 플린트윈치는 도련님 어머니까지도 꺾을 수 있어요."], ["What can he be but a clever one to do that!’", "그런 일을 해내는 사람이 영리한 사람이 아니고 뭐겠어요!\""]], [["His shuffling footstep coming towards them caused her to retreat to the other end of the room.", "그가 질질 끄는 발걸음으로 그들 쪽으로 다가오자, 노파는 방 저쪽 끝으로 물러났다."], ["Though a tall, hard-favoured, sinewy old woman, who in her youth might have enlisted in the Foot Guards without much fear of discovery, she collapsed before the little keen-eyed crab-like old man.", "키가 크고, 험상궂고, 근육이 단단한 노파로, 젊었을 적에는 별로 들킬 염려 없이 보병 근위대에 입대할 수도 있었을 그녀가, 그 키 작고 눈매 날카로운 게 같은 노인 앞에서는 움츠러들었다."]], [["‘Now, Affery,’ said he, ‘now, woman, what are you doing?", "\"이봐, 애프리,\" 그가 말했다, \"이봐, 여편네, 뭘 하는 거야?"], ["Can’t you find Master Arthur something or another to pick at?’", "아서 도련님께 뭐 좀 집어 드실 거라도 찾아 드리지 못하겠어?\""]], [["Master Arthur repeated his recent refusal to pick at anything.", "아서 도련님은 아무것도 집어 먹지 않겠다는 좀 전의 거절을 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Very well, then,’ said the old man; ‘make his bed. Stir yourself.’ His neck was so twisted that the knotted ends of his white cravat usually dangled under one ear; his natural acerbity and energy, always contending with a second nature of habitual repression, gave his features a swollen and suffused look; and altogether, he had a weird appearance of having hanged himself at one time or other, and of having gone about ever since, halter and all, exactly as some timely hand had cut him down.", "\"그럼 좋소,\" 노인이 말했다. \"도련님 잠자리나 봐 드려. 어서 움직여.\" 그의 목은 어찌나 비틀려 있는지 흰 넥타이의 매듭진 끝이 으레 한쪽 귀 밑에 늘어져 있었다. 그의 천성적인 신랄함과 정력은, 늘 억누르는 습관이라는 제2의 천성과 다투면서, 그의 이목구비에 부어오르고 충혈된 듯한 모습을 주었다. 그래서 도무지, 언젠가 한 번 목을 매단 적이 있어서, 어떤 때맞춘 손이 그를 잘라 내린 바로 그 모습 그대로, 올가미고 뭐고 다 매단 채 그 뒤로 줄곧 돌아다니고 있는 듯한, 으스스한 인상을 풍겼다."]], [["‘You’ll have bitter words together to-morrow, Arthur; you and your mother,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"내일 도련님과 모친이, 도련님은 모친과 모진 말을 주고받게 될 거외다, 아서 도련님,\" 제러마이아가 말했다."], ["‘Your having given up the business on your father’s death--which she suspects, though we have left it to you to tell her--won’t go off smoothly.’", "\"부친이 돌아가시고 도련님이 사업을 그만두셨다는 게 — 도련님이 직접 말씀하시도록 우리가 남겨 두긴 했지만 모친도 짐작은 하고 계시오 — 순순히 넘어가진 않을 거요.\""]], [["‘I have given up everything in life for the business, and the time came for me to give up that.’", "\"저는 그 사업을 위해 인생의 모든 것을 포기해 왔어요. 이제 그것마저 포기할 때가 온 거지요.\""]], [["‘Good!’ cried Jeremiah, evidently meaning Bad.", "\"좋소!\" 제러마이아가 외쳤는데, 분명 나쁘다는 뜻이었다."], ["‘Very good!", "\"아주 좋아!"], ["only don’t expect me to stand between your mother and you, Arthur.", "다만 내가 도련님과 모친 사이에 서 주리라고는 기대하지 마시오, 아서 도련님."], ["I stood between your mother and your father, fending off this, and fending off that, and getting crushed and pounded betwixt em; and I’ve done with such work.’", "나는 도련님 모친과 부친 사이에 서서, 이것도 막아 내고 저것도 막아 내다가, 그 두 분 사이에서 짓눌리고 빻이고 했소. 그런 일은 이제 신물이 나오.\""]], [["‘You will never be asked to begin it again for me, Jeremiah.’", "\"다시는 저를 위해 그런 일을 시작하시라고 청하지 않겠습니다, 제러마이아.\""]], [["‘Good.", "\"좋소."], ["I’m glad to hear it; because I should have had to decline it, if I had been.", "그 말을 들으니 다행이오. 청하셨다면 거절해야 했을 테니까."], ["That’s enough--as your mother says--and more than enough of such matters on a Sabbath night.", "그만하면 — 모친 말씀처럼 — 됐소, 안식일 밤에 그런 이야기는 차고도 넘쳤소."], ["Affery, woman, have you found what you want yet?’", "애프리, 여편네, 찾을 건 다 찾았어?\""]], [["She had been collecting sheets and blankets from a press, and hastened to gather them up, and to reply, ‘Yes, Jeremiah.’ Arthur Clennam helped her by carrying the load himself, wished the old man good night, and went up-stairs with her to the top of the house.", "그녀는 벽장에서 시트와 담요를 모으고 있었는데, 서둘러 그것들을 챙겨 들고 대답했다, \"예, 제러마이아.\" 아서 클레넘은 직접 그 짐을 들어 주며 그녀를 거들었고, 노인에게 잘 자라고 인사한 다음, 그녀와 함께 위층으로, 집 꼭대기까지 올라갔다."]], [["They mounted up and up, through the musty smell of an old close house, little used, to a large garret bed-room.", "그들은 별로 쓰이지 않는 오래되고 답답한 집의 곰팡내를 뚫고, 위로 또 위로 올라가, 어느 커다란 다락 침실로 들어갔다."], ["Meagre and spare, like all the other rooms, it was even uglier and grimmer than the rest, by being the place of banishment for the worn-out furniture.", "다른 모든 방처럼 빈약하고 휑한 그 방은, 닳아 빠진 가구의 유배지였던 탓에, 나머지보다 한층 더 흉하고 음산했다."], ["Its movables were ugly old chairs with worn-out seats, and ugly old chairs without any seats; a threadbare patternless carpet, a maimed table, a crippled wardrobe, a lean set of fire-irons like the skeleton of a set deceased, a washing-stand that looked as if it had stood for ages in a hail of dirty soapsuds, and a bedstead with four bare atomies of posts, each terminating in a spike, as if for the dismal accommodation of lodgers who might prefer to impale themselves.", "거기 놓인 것들이라곤, 앉는 부분이 닳아 빠진 흉한 옛 의자들과, 앉는 부분이 아예 없는 흉한 옛 의자들, 올이 다 드러나고 무늬도 없는 양탄자, 한쪽이 망가진 탁자, 절름발이가 된 옷장, 죽은 한 벌의 해골 같은 비쩍 마른 벽난로 도구 한 벌, 더러운 비눗물 우박을 오랜 세월 맞으며 서 있었던 것처럼 보이는 세면대, 그리고 네 개의 헐벗은 뼈다귀 같은 기둥이 달린 침대틀이었다. 그 기둥들은 저마다 끝에 뾰족한 못이 박혀 있었는데, 마치 차라리 제 몸을 꿰어 버리고 싶어 할 투숙객들을 위한 음산한 편의 시설인 듯했다."], ["Arthur opened the long low window, and looked out upon the old blasted and blackened forest of chimneys, and the old red glare in the sky, which had seemed to him once upon a time but a nightly reflection of the fiery environment that was presented to his childish fancy in all directions, let it look where it would.", "아서는 길고 나지막한 창을 열고, 비바람에 시들어 검게 그을린 그 옛 굴뚝들의 숲과, 하늘의 그 옛 붉은 불빛을 내다보았다. 그 불빛은, 한때 그에게는, 어린 시절의 공상 속에서 어느 방향으로 눈을 돌리든 사방에 펼쳐져 있던 그 불타는 환경의 밤마다의 반영으로만 보였던 것이었다."]], [["He drew in his head again, sat down at the bedside, and looked on at Affery Flintwinch making the bed.", "그는 다시 머리를 들이고, 침대맡에 앉아, 잠자리를 펴는 애프리 플린트윈치를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Affery, you were not married when I went away.’", "\"애프리, 내가 떠날 때만 해도 자네는 결혼한 사이가 아니었지.\""]], [["She screwed her mouth into the form of saying ‘No,’ shook her head, and proceeded to get a pillow into its case.", "그녀는 입을 '아니요'라고 말하는 모양으로 오므리고, 고개를 가로젓고는, 베개를 베갯잇 속에 넣는 일에 착수했다."]], [["‘How did it happen?’", "\"어떻게 된 일인가?\""]], [["‘Why, Jeremiah, o’ course,’ said Affery, with an end of the pillow-case between her teeth.", "\"아, 제러마이아 때문이지요, 물론,\" 애프리가 베갯잇 끝을 이로 문 채 말했다."]], [["‘Of course he proposed it, but how did it all come about?", "\"물론 그가 청혼했겠지. 하지만 어떻게 다 그렇게 된 건가?"], ["I should have thought that neither of you would have married; least of all should I have thought of your marrying each other.’", "나는 두 사람 다 결혼하지 않으리라고 생각했을 텐데. 무엇보다도 두 사람이 서로 결혼하리라고는 생각도 못 했을 거야.\""]], [["‘No more should I,’ said Mrs Flintwinch, tying the pillow tightly in its case.", "\"저도 그랬지요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 베개를 베갯잇 속에 단단히 묶으며 말했다."]], [["‘That’s what I mean.", "\"바로 그 말일세."], ["When did you begin to think otherwise?’", "언제부터 달리 생각하기 시작했나?\""]], [["‘Never begun to think otherwise at all,’ said Mrs Flintwinch.", "\"달리 생각하기 시작한 적은 아예 없어요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 말했다."]], [["Seeing, as she patted the pillow into its place on the bolster, that he was still looking at her as if waiting for the rest of her reply, she gave it a great poke in the middle, and asked, ‘How could I help myself?’", "베개를 토닥여 받침베개 위 제자리에 놓으면서, 그가 여전히 자기의 나머지 대답을 기다리기라도 하는 듯 바라보고 있는 것을 보고, 그녀는 베개 한가운데를 한 번 쿡 찌르고는 물었다, \"제가 어쩔 도리가 있었겠어요?\""]], [["‘How could you help yourself from being married!’", "\"결혼하지 않을 도리가 어떻게 없었느냐고!\""]], [["‘O’ course,’ said Mrs Flintwinch.", "\"물론이지요,\" 플린트윈치 부인이 말했다."], ["‘It was no doing o’ mine.", "\"그건 제가 한 일이 아니었어요."], ["I’d never thought of it.", "저는 그런 생각은 해 본 적도 없는걸요."], ["I’d got something to do, without thinking, indeed!", "정말이지, 생각할 겨를도 없이 할 일이 있었다고요!"], ["She kept me to it (as well as he) when she could go about, and she could go about then.’", "마님이 (그 양반도 그랬지만) 돌아다니실 수 있을 때 저를 거기 매어 두셨는데, 그때는 마님이 돌아다니실 수 있었거든요.\""]], [["‘Well?’", "\"그래서?\""]], [["‘Well?’ echoed Mrs Flintwinch.", "\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 따라 말했다."], ["‘That’s what I said myself.", "\"그건 저 자신도 한 말이지요."], ["Well!", "그래서요!"], ["What’s the use of considering?", "따져 본들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?"], ["If them two clever ones have made up their minds to it, what’s left for _me_ to do?", "그 영리한 두 분이 마음을 정했다면, 제게 남은 게 뭐가 있겠어요?"], ["Nothing.’", "아무것도 없지요.\""]], [["‘Was it my mother’s project, then?’", "\"그럼 우리 어머니의 계획이었나?\""]], [["‘The Lord bless you, Arthur, and forgive me the wish!’ cried Affery, speaking always in a low tone.", "\"하느님 도련님께 복 주시고, 그런 마음 먹은 저를 용서하시기를!\" 애프리가 줄곧 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["‘If they hadn’t been both of a mind in it, how could it ever have been?", "\"두 분이 그 일에 한마음이 아니었다면, 어떻게 그게 이루어질 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Jeremiah never courted me; t’ant likely that he would, after living in the house with me and ordering me about for as many years as he’d done.", "제러마이아는 제게 구애한 적이 없어요. 그렇게 여러 해를 같은 집에서 저랑 살면서 저한테 이래라저래라 부려 먹어 놓고서 그럴 리가 있겠어요."], ["He said to me one day, he said, “Affery,”", "그 양반이 어느 날 제게 말하더군요, '애프리,'"], ["he said, “now I am going to tell you something.", "그러더니, '내 이제 자네한테 할 말이 있어."], ["What do you think of the name of Flintwinch?”", "플린트윈치라는 성을 어떻게 생각하나?' 하고요."], ["“What do I think of it?”", "'그걸 어떻게 생각하느냐고요?'"], ["I says. “Yes,”", "제가 그랬지요."], ["he said, “because you’re going to take it,”", "'그래,' 그가 말했어요, '자네가 그 성을 갖게 될 테니까 말이야.'"], ["he said.", "하고요."], ["“Take it?”", "'갖는다고요?'"], ["I says.", "제가 그랬지요."], ["“Jere-_mi_-ah?”", "'제-러-마-이아?'"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["he’s a clever one!’", "그 양반은 영리하다니까요!\""]], [["Mrs Flintwinch went on to spread the upper sheet over the bed, and the blanket over that, and the counterpane over that, as if she had quite concluded her story.", "플린트윈치 부인은 위쪽 시트를 침대 위에 펼치고, 그 위에 담요를, 또 그 위에 침대보를 펼쳐 나갔다. 마치 이야기를 다 끝맺기라도 한 것처럼."]], [["‘Well?’ said Arthur again.", "\"그래서?\" 아서가 다시 말했다."]], [["‘Well?’ echoed Mrs Flintwinch again.", "\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 다시 따라 말했다."], ["‘How could I help myself?", "\"제가 어쩔 도리가 있었겠어요?"], ["He said to me, “Affery, you and me must be married, and I’ll tell you why.", "그 양반이 제게 말하길, '애프리, 자네하고 나하고는 결혼해야 해. 그 까닭을 말해 주지."], ["She’s failing in health, and she’ll want pretty constant attendance up in her room, and we shall have to be much with her, and there’ll be nobody about now but ourselves when we’re away from her, and altogether it will be more convenient.", "마님이 건강이 나빠지고 있어서, 방에서 꽤 늘 시중을 받으셔야 할 거야. 그러니 우리가 마님 곁에 많이 붙어 있어야 할 텐데, 우리가 마님 곁을 떠나 있을 때 이제 이 집엔 우리 둘밖에 없을 테니, 여러모로 그게 더 편할 거야."], ["She’s of my opinion,”", "마님도 내 생각과 같으셔,'"], ["he said, “so if you’ll put your bonnet on next Monday morning at eight, we’ll get it over.”’ Mrs Flintwinch tucked up the bed.", "그러더군요, '그러니 다음 월요일 아침 여덟 시에 모자를 쓰면, 우리가 그 일을 끝낼 거야.'\" 플린트윈치 부인은 침대를 여몄다."]], [["‘Well?’", "\"그래서?\""]], [["‘Well?’ repeated Mrs Flintwinch, ‘I think so!", "\"그래서요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 되풀이했다, \"그러게나 말이에요!"], ["I sits me down and says it.", "저는 주저앉아서 그 말을 했지요."], ["Well!--Jeremiah then says to me, “As to banns, next Sunday being the third time of asking (for I’ve put ‘em up a fortnight), is my reason for naming Monday.", "그래서요!— 그러더니 제러마이아가 제게 말하길, '결혼 예고로 말할 것 같으면, 다음 일요일이 세 번째 공고하는 날이라(내가 두 주 전에 올려 뒀거든) 월요일로 정한 까닭이야."], ["She’ll speak to you about it herself, and now she’ll find you prepared, Affery.”", "마님이 직접 자네한테 그 얘길 하실 텐데, 이제 자네가 마음의 준비가 된 걸 보시게 될 거야, 애프리.'"], ["That same day she spoke to me, and she said, “So, Affery, I understand that you and Jeremiah are going to be married.", "하고요. 바로 그날 마님이 제게 말씀하시길, '그래, 애프리, 너하고 제러마이아가 결혼한다고 들었다."], ["I am glad of it, and so are you, with reason.", "나도 기쁘고, 너도 기쁘겠지, 그럴 만도 하고."], ["It is a very good thing for you, and very welcome under the circumstances to me.", "너한테 아주 좋은 일이고, 이런 형편에 내게도 아주 반가운 일이다."], ["He is a sensible man, and a trustworthy man, and a persevering man, and a pious man.”", "그는 분별 있는 사람이고, 믿을 만한 사람이고, 끈기 있는 사람이고, 경건한 사람이지.' 하시더군요."], ["What could I say when it had come to that?", "일이 거기까지 갔는데 제가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Why, if it had been--a smothering instead of a wedding,’ Mrs Flintwinch cast about in her mind with great pains for this form of expression, ‘I couldn’t have said a word upon it, against them two clever ones.’", "아니, 그게 결혼이 아니라 — 질식이었다 해도,\" 플린트윈치 부인은 이 표현 형식을 찾아내려고 무진 애를 쓰며 머릿속을 더듬었다, \"저는 그 영리한 두 분께 맞서 한마디도 못 했을 거예요.\""]], [["‘In good faith, I believe so.’", "\"진심으로, 나도 그랬을 거라고 믿네.\""]], [["‘And so you may, Arthur.’", "\"그러시는 게 옳아요, 아서 도련님.\""]], [["‘Affery, what girl was that in my mother’s room just now?’", "\"애프리, 방금 어머니 방에 있던 그 처녀는 누구였나?\""]], [["‘Girl?’ said Mrs Flintwinch in a rather sharp key.", "\"처녀요?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 다소 날카로운 음조로 말했다."]], [["‘It was a girl, surely, whom I saw near you--almost hidden in the dark corner?’", "\"분명 처녀였는데, 자네 가까이에서 봤어 — 어두운 구석에 거의 가려진 채로 말이야.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["She?", "그 애요?"], ["Little Dorrit?", "리틀 도릿? 그 앤 아무것도 아니에요."], ["_She_‘s nothing; she’s a whim of--hers.’ It was a peculiarity of Affery Flintwinch that she never spoke of Mrs Clennam by name.", "그 애는 마님의 — 변덕이지요.\" 클레넘 부인을 결코 이름으로 부르지 않는 것이 애프리 플린트윈치의 특이한 점이었다."], ["‘But there’s another sort of girls than that about.", "\"하지만 그런 애 말고 다른 종류의 처녀도 있잖아요."], ["Have you forgot your old sweetheart?", "도련님의 옛 연인을 잊으셨어요?"], ["Long and long ago, I’ll be bound.’", "까마득히 오래된 일이긴 하겠지만요.\""]], [["‘I suffered enough from my mother’s separating us, to remember her.", "\"어머니가 우리를 떼어 놓으셔서 충분히 괴로웠으니, 그녀를 기억하지."], ["I recollect her very well.’", "아주 잘 기억하고 있어.\""]], [["‘Have you got another?’", "\"새 연인이 생기셨어요?\""]], [["‘No.’", "\"아니.\""]], [["‘Here’s news for you, then.", "\"그럼 도련님께 알려 드릴 소식이 있어요."], ["She’s well to do now, and a widow.", "그 여자는 이제 살림이 넉넉하고, 미망인이 됐어요."], ["And if you like to have her, why you can.’", "그래서 도련님이 그 여자를 맞아들이고 싶으시면, 그럴 수 있어요.\""]], [["‘And how do you know that, Affery?’", "\"그건 또 어떻게 아나, 애프리?\""]], [["‘Them two clever ones have been speaking about it.--There’s Jeremiah on the stairs!’ She was gone in a moment.", "\"그 영리한 두 분이 그 얘길 하고 계셨거든요.— 저기 제러마이아가 계단에 있어요!\" 그녀는 순식간에 사라졌다."]], [["Mrs Flintwinch had introduced into the web that his mind was busily weaving, in that old workshop where the loom of his youth had stood, the last thread wanting to the pattern.", "플린트윈치 부인은, 그의 젊은 날의 베틀이 놓여 있던 그 옛 작업장에서 그의 마음이 분주히 짜 나가고 있던 직물 속에, 그 무늬에 빠져 있던 마지막 한 올을 끼워 넣은 것이었다."], ["The airy folly of a boy’s love had found its way even into that house, and he had been as wretched under its hopelessness as if the house had been a castle of romance.", "소년의 사랑이라는 그 덧없는 어리석음은 그 집 안에까지도 길을 찾아 들었고, 그래서 그는 그 가망 없음 아래에서, 마치 그 집이 로맨스 속의 성(城)이기라도 한 것처럼 비참했었다."], ["Little more than a week ago at Marseilles, the face of the pretty girl from whom he had parted with regret, had had an unusual interest for him, and a tender hold upon him, because of some resemblance, real or imagined, to this first face that had soared out of his gloomy life into the bright glories of fancy.", "일주일 남짓 전 마르세유에서, 그가 아쉬움 속에 작별했던 그 예쁜 처녀의 얼굴이 그에게 남달리 흥미로웠고, 그의 마음을 애틋하게 사로잡았던 것은, 그것이, 음울했던 그의 삶에서 솟구쳐 올라 공상의 찬란한 영광 속으로 날아오른 그 첫 번째 얼굴과, 실재든 상상이든 어떤 닮은 데가 있었기 때문이다."], ["He leaned upon the sill of the long low window, and looking out upon the blackened forest of chimneys again, began to dream; for it had been the uniform tendency of this man’s life--so much was wanting in it to think about, so much that might have been better directed and happier to speculate upon--to make him a dreamer, after all.", "그는 길고 나지막한 창턱에 기대어, 다시 그 검게 그을린 굴뚝들의 숲을 내다보며, 몽상에 잠기기 시작했다. 이 사내의 삶에는 — 생각할 거리가 그토록 없었고, 더 나은 방향으로 이끌렸더라면 더 행복했을 만한, 사색할 만한 것이 그토록 없었던 까닭에 — 결국 그를 몽상가로 만드는 것이 한결같은 경향이었으니까."]], [["# CHAPTER 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream", "# CHAPTER 4. Mrs Flintwinch has a Dream"]], [["When Mrs Flintwinch dreamed, she usually dreamed, unlike the son of her old mistress, with her eyes shut.", "플린트윈치 부인이 꿈을 꿀 때면, 옛 여주인의 아들과는 달리 대개 눈을 감고 꾸었다."], ["She had a curiously vivid dream that night, and before she had left the son of her old mistress many hours.", "그날 밤 그녀는 묘하게도 생생한 꿈을 꾸었는데, 옛 여주인의 아들과 헤어진 지 몇 시간도 지나지 않아서였다."], ["In fact it was not at all like a dream; it was so very real in every respect.", "사실 그것은 도무지 꿈 같지 않았다. 어느 모로 보나 너무도 생생했던 것이다."], ["It happened in this wise.", "일은 이렇게 벌어졌다."]], [["The bed-chamber occupied by Mr and Mrs Flintwinch was within a few paces of that to which Mrs Clennam had been so long confined.", "플린트윈치 부부가 쓰는 침실은 클레넘 부인이 그토록 오래 갇혀 지내던 방에서 몇 걸음 떨어져 있었다."], ["It was not on the same floor, for it was a room at the side of the house, which was approached by a steep descent of a few odd steps, diverging from the main staircase nearly opposite to Mrs Clennam’s door.", "같은 층은 아니었으니, 그것은 집 옆쪽에 있는 방으로, 클레넘 부인의 방문 거의 맞은편에서 본계단으로부터 갈라져 내려가는 몇 개의 들쭉날쭉한 가파른 계단을 통해 들어가는 곳이었다."], ["It could scarcely be said to be within call, the walls, doors, and panelling of the old place were so cumbrous; but it was within easy reach, in any undress, at any hour of the night, in any temperature.", "그 오래된 집의 벽과 문, 판벽이 어찌나 둔중한지 부르면 들릴 거리라고는 할 수 없었지만, 한밤중 어느 시각에든, 어떤 차림새로든, 어떤 날씨에든 손쉽게 닿을 수 있는 거리였다."], ["At the head of the bed and within a foot of Mrs Flintwinch’s ear, was a bell, the line of which hung ready to Mrs Clennam’s hand.", "침대 머리맡, 플린트윈치 부인의 귀에서 한 자도 안 되는 곳에 종이 있었고, 그 줄은 클레넘 부인의 손이 닿도록 늘어뜨려져 있었다."], ["Whenever this bell rang, up started Affery, and was in the sick room before she was awake.", "이 종이 울릴 때마다 애프리는 벌떡 일어나, 잠이 채 깨기도 전에 병실에 가 있곤 했다."]], [["Having got her mistress into bed, lighted her lamp, and given her good night, Mrs Flintwinch went to roost as usual, saving that her lord had not yet appeared.", "여주인을 잠자리에 들이고, 등불을 켜 주고, 잘 자라는 인사를 마친 뒤, 플린트윈치 부인은 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었다. 다만 그녀의 주인 양반이 아직 모습을 보이지 않은 것만 달랐다."], ["It was her lord himself who became--unlike the last theme in the mind, according to the observation of most philosophers--the subject of Mrs Flintwinch’s dream.", "대부분의 철학자들이 관찰한 바와는 달리—마음속 마지막 화제와는 어긋나게—플린트윈치 부인의 꿈의 주인공이 된 것은 바로 그녀의 주인 양반 자신이었다."]], [["It seemed to her that she awoke after sleeping some hours, and found Jeremiah not yet abed.", "그녀가 몇 시간 자고 난 뒤 깨어 보니 제러마이아가 아직 잠자리에 들지 않은 것 같았다."], ["That she looked at the candle she had left burning, and, measuring the time like King Alfred the Great, was confirmed by its wasted state in her belief that she had been asleep for some considerable period.", "켜 둔 채로 두었던 촛불을 보고, 앨프레드 대왕처럼 그 시간을 가늠해 보니, 다 타들어 간 그 상태로 미루어 자기가 상당히 오랫동안 잠들어 있었다는 믿음이 굳어졌다."], ["That she arose thereupon, muffled herself up in a wrapper, put on her shoes, and went out on the staircase, much surprised, to look for Jeremiah.", "그래서 그녀는 일어나 실내복으로 몸을 감싸고, 신을 신고, 몹시 의아해하며 제러마이아를 찾으러 계단으로 나갔다."]], [["The staircase was as wooden and solid as need be, and Affery went straight down it without any of those deviations peculiar to dreams.", "계단은 더할 나위 없이 목재로 되어 단단했고, 애프리는 꿈 특유의 그 어떤 비틀거림도 없이 곧장 그것을 내려갔다."], ["She did not skim over it, but walked down it, and guided herself by the banisters on account of her candle having died out.", "미끄러져 내려간 것이 아니라 걸어 내려갔는데, 촛불이 꺼져 버린 탓에 난간을 짚어 가며 길을 더듬었다."], ["In one corner of the hall, behind the house-door, there was a little waiting-room, like a well-shaft, with a long narrow window in it as if it had been ripped up.", "현관 한쪽 구석, 집 대문 뒤에는 작은 대기실이 하나 있었는데, 마치 우물 갱처럼 좁다랗고, 마치 칼로 찢어 낸 듯한 길고 가느다란 창문이 하나 나 있었다."], ["In this room, which was never used, a light was burning.", "한 번도 쓰인 적 없는 이 방에 불이 켜져 있었다."]], [["Mrs Flintwinch crossed the hall, feeling its pavement cold to her stockingless feet, and peeped in between the rusty hinges on the door, which stood a little open.", "플린트윈치 부인은 양말도 신지 않은 발에 차갑게 와 닿는 바닥을 느끼며 현관을 가로질러, 살짝 열려 있는 문의 녹슨 돌쩌귀 사이로 안을 들여다보았다."], ["She expected to see Jeremiah fast asleep or in a fit, but he was calmly seated in a chair, awake, and in his usual health.", "그녀는 제러마이아가 곤히 잠들어 있거나 발작이라도 일으킨 모습을 보리라 예상했지만, 그는 의자에 차분히 앉아 깨어 있었고, 평소처럼 건강한 모습이었다."], ["But what--hey?--Lord forgive us!--Mrs Flintwinch muttered some ejaculation to this effect, and turned giddy.", "그런데 저것은—아니?—하느님 맙소사!—플린트윈치 부인은 그런 뜻의 외마디를 중얼거리며 어지럼증에 휩싸였다."]], [["For, Mr Flintwinch awake, was watching Mr Flintwinch asleep.", "왜냐하면 깨어 있는 플린트윈치 씨가 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치 씨를 지켜보고 있었던 것이다."], ["He sat on one side of the small table, looking keenly at himself on the other side with his chin sunk on his breast, snoring.", "그는 작은 탁자 한쪽에 앉아, 맞은편에서 턱을 가슴팍에 푹 떨군 채 코를 골고 있는 자기 자신을 날카롭게 바라보고 있었다."], ["The waking Flintwinch had his full front face presented to his wife; the sleeping Flintwinch was in profile.", "깨어 있는 플린트윈치는 정면 얼굴을 아내 쪽으로 온전히 드러내고 있었고, 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치는 옆모습이었다."], ["The waking Flintwinch was the old original; the sleeping Flintwinch was the double, just as she might have distinguished between a tangible object and its reflection in a glass, Affery made out this difference with her head going round and round.", "깨어 있는 플린트윈치가 본디 원본이었고, 잠들어 있는 플린트윈치는 그 분신이었으니, 마치 실체와 거울에 비친 그 상을 구별하듯, 애프리는 머리가 빙글빙글 도는 가운데 이 차이를 알아보았다."]], [["If she had had any doubt which was her own Jeremiah, it would have been resolved by his impatience.", "어느 쪽이 자신의 제러마이아인지 의심이 들었다 해도, 그의 조바심을 보면 그 의심은 풀렸을 것이다."], ["He looked about him for an offensive weapon, caught up the snuffers, and, before applying them to the cabbage-headed candle, lunged at the sleeper as though he would have run him through the body.", "그는 무슨 무기가 없나 사방을 둘러보더니, 촛불 가위를 집어 들었고, 그것을 양배추 머리처럼 타 버린 심지에 대기도 전에, 마치 그 몸을 꿰뚫기라도 할 듯 잠든 자에게 들이댔다."]], [["‘Who’s that?", "\"누구야?"], ["What’s the matter?’ cried the sleeper, starting.", "무슨 일이야?\" 잠든 자가 흠칫 놀라 소리쳤다."]], [["Mr Flintwinch made a movement with the snuffers, as if he would have enforced silence on his companion by putting them down his throat; the companion, coming to himself, said, rubbing his eyes, ‘I forgot where I was.’", "플린트윈치 씨는 마치 가위를 동행자의 목구멍에 쑤셔 넣어 입을 다물게 하려는 듯 그것을 휘둘렀다. 동행자는 정신을 차리며 눈을 비비고 말했다. \"내가 여기가 어딘지 깜빡했군.\""]], [["‘You have been asleep,’ snarled Jeremiah, referring to his watch, ‘two hours.", "\"넌 자고 있었어.\" 제러마이아가 회중시계를 보며 으르렁거렸다."], ["You said you would be rested enough if you had a short nap.’", "\"두 시간이나. 잠깐 눈만 붙이면 충분히 쉴 거라고 했잖아.\""]], [["‘I have had a short nap,’ said Double.", "\"잠깐 눈만 붙였지.\" 분신이 말했다."]], [["‘Half-past two o’clock in the morning,’ muttered Jeremiah.", "\"새벽 두 시 반이군.\" 제러마이아가 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Where’s your hat? Where’s your coat?", "\"네 모자는 어디 있어?"], ["Where’s the box?’", "외투는? 상자는?\""]], [["‘All here,’ said Double, tying up his throat with sleepy carefulness in a shawl.", "\"다 여기 있어.\" 분신이 졸린 듯 조심스럽게 숄로 목을 감싸며 말했다."], ["‘Stop a minute.", "\"잠깐만."], ["Now give me the sleeve--not that sleeve, the other one.", "자, 소매를 좀 끼워 주게—그 소매 말고, 다른 쪽."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["I’m not as young as I was.’ Mr Flintwinch had pulled him into his coat with vehement energy.", "이젠 예전만큼 젊지가 않으니.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 거친 기세로 그를 외투 속에 밀어 넣었다."], ["‘You promised me a second glass after I was rested.’", "\"쉬고 나면 두 잔째를 주겠다고 약속했잖아.\""]], [["‘Drink it!’ returned Jeremiah, ‘and--choke yourself, I was going to say--but go, I mean.’ At the same time he produced the identical port-wine bottle, and filled a wine-glass.", "\"마셔!\" 제러마이아가 대꾸했다. \"그리고—목에 걸려 죽어라, 라고 말하려던 참이었지만—가라는 말이야.\" 동시에 그는 바로 그 포트와인 병을 꺼내 잔을 가득 채웠다."]], [["‘Her port-wine, I believe?’ said Double, tasting it as if he were in the Docks, with hours to spare.", "\"이거 그 사람 포트와인이지?\" 분신이, 마치 부두에서 시간이 남아도는 사람처럼 그것을 음미하며 말했다."], ["‘Her health.’", "\"그 사람의 건강을 위하여.\""]], [["He took a sip.", "그가 한 모금 들이켰다."]], [["‘Your health!’", "\"네 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["He took another sip.", "그가 또 한 모금 들이켰다."]], [["‘His health!’", "\"그분의 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["He took another sip.", "그가 또 한 모금 들이켰다."]], [["‘And all friends round St Paul’s.’ He emptied and put down the wine-glass half-way through this ancient civic toast, and took up the box.", "\"그리고 세인트폴 주변의 모든 친구들을 위하여.\" 그는 이 오래된 도시풍 건배사를 반쯤 외우다 말고 잔을 비워 내려놓고는 상자를 집어 들었다."], ["It was an iron box some two feet square, which he carried under his arms pretty easily.", "그것은 사방 두 자쯤 되는 철제 상자로, 그는 그것을 두 팔에 끼고 제법 수월하게 들었다."], ["Jeremiah watched his manner of adjusting it, with jealous eyes; tried it with his hands, to be sure that he had a firm hold of it; bade him for his life be careful what he was about; and then stole out on tiptoe to open the door for him.", "제러마이아는 시샘 어린 눈으로 그가 그것을 추스르는 모양새를 지켜보았다. 그것을 단단히 쥐고 있는지 확인하려 두 손으로 만져 보았고, 목숨을 걸고 하는 일이니 조심하라고 일렀으며, 그러고는 그를 위해 문을 열어 주려 발끝으로 살금살금 빠져나갔다."], ["Affery, anticipating the last movement, was on the staircase.", "애프리는 그 마지막 동작을 예감하고 계단에 가 있었다."], ["The sequence of things was so ordinary and natural, that, standing there, she could hear the door open, feel the night air, and see the stars outside.", "일의 순서가 너무도 평범하고 자연스러워, 거기 서 있는 그녀는 문이 열리는 소리를 듣고, 밤공기를 느끼고, 바깥의 별들을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["But now came the most remarkable part of the dream.", "그러나 이제 꿈에서 가장 주목할 만한 대목이 찾아왔다."], ["She felt so afraid of her husband, that being on the staircase, she had not the power to retreat to her room (which she might easily have done before he had fastened the door), but stood there staring.", "그녀는 남편이 어찌나 무서웠던지, 계단에 있으면서도(그가 문을 잠그기 전이라면 쉽게 자기 방으로 물러설 수 있었을 텐데) 물러날 힘이 없어, 그저 거기 서서 멍하니 바라보고만 있었다."], ["Consequently when he came up the staircase to bed, candle in hand, he came full upon her.", "그리하여 그가 촛불을 손에 들고 잠자리에 들려 계단을 올라왔을 때, 그는 그녀와 정면으로 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["He looked astonished, but said not a word.", "그는 놀란 표정이었지만 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He kept his eyes upon her, and kept advancing; and she, completely under his influence, kept retiring before him.", "그는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 계속 다가왔고, 그녀는 완전히 그의 위세에 눌려 그 앞에서 계속 물러섰다."], ["Thus, she walking backward and he walking forward, they came into their own room.", "이렇게 그녀는 뒷걸음질 치고 그는 앞으로 걸어, 두 사람은 자기들 방으로 들어왔다."], ["They were no sooner shut in there, than Mr Flintwinch took her by the throat, and shook her until she was black in the face.", "거기에 갇히기가 무섭게 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀의 목을 움켜쥐고, 얼굴이 시퍼레지도록 흔들어 댔다."]], [["‘Why, Affery, woman--Affery!’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"이런, 애프리, 여보게—애프리!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What have you been dreaming of?", "\"무슨 꿈을 꾸고 있었던 거야?"], ["Wake up, wake up!", "정신 차려, 정신 차려!"], ["What’s the matter?’", "무슨 일이야?\""]], [["‘The--the matter, Jeremiah?’ gasped Mrs Flintwinch, rolling her eyes.", "\"무, 무슨 일이라뇨, 제러마이아?\" 플린트윈치 부인이 눈을 굴리며 숨을 헐떡였다."]], [["‘Why, Affery, woman--Affery!", "\"이런, 애프리, 여보게—애프리!"], ["You have been getting out of bed in your sleep, my dear!", "당신 잠결에 침대에서 빠져나왔구먼!"], ["I come up, after having fallen asleep myself, below, and find you in your wrapper here, with the nightmare.", "나는 아래층에서 그만 잠이 들었다가 올라와 보니, 당신이 실내복 차림으로 여기 서서 가위눌려 있는 거야. 애프리, 여보게,\""], ["Affery, woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with a friendly grin on his expressive countenance, ‘if you ever have a dream of this sort again, it’ll be a sign of your being in want of physic.", "플린트윈치 씨가 그 풍부한 표정의 얼굴에 다정한 능글맞은 웃음을 띠며 말했다. \"다시 이런 꿈을 꾸거든, 그건 당신한테 약이 필요하다는 신호일 거야."], ["And I’ll give you such a dose, old woman--such a dose!’", "그러면 내가 약을 한 사발 먹여 주지, 여보—한 사발 가득!\""]], [["Mrs Flintwinch thanked him and crept into bed.", "플린트윈치 부인은 그에게 고맙다고 하고는 슬그머니 침대로 기어들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 5. Family Affairs", "# CHAPTER 5. Family Affairs"]], [["As the city clocks struck nine on Monday morning, Mrs Clennam was wheeled by Jeremiah Flintwinch of the cut-down aspect to her tall cabinet.", "월요일 아침 시내의 시계들이 아홉 시를 칠 때, 잔뜩 풀이 죽은 모양새의 제레미아 플린트윈치가 클레넘 부인을 그녀의 키 큰 캐비닛 앞으로 바퀴 의자에 태워 밀어 놓았다."], ["When she had unlocked and opened it, and had settled herself at its desk, Jeremiah withdrew--as it might be, to hang himself more effectually--and her son appeared.", "그녀가 그것을 열쇠로 열어 젖히고 그 책상 앞에 자리를 잡자, 제레미아는 — 어쩌면 좀 더 확실하게 목이라도 매러 가려는 듯 — 물러갔고, 그녀의 아들이 나타났다."]], [["‘Are you any better this morning, mother?’", "\"오늘 아침은 좀 나으세요, 어머니?\""]], [["She shook her head, with the same austere air of luxuriousness that she had shown over-night when speaking of the weather.", "그녀는 간밤에 날씨에 관해 말할 때 보였던 것과 똑같은, 호사스러움이 깃든 엄격한 분위기로 고개를 저었다."], ["‘I shall never be better any more.", "\"나는 더 나아지는 일은 결코 없을 게다."], ["It is well for me, Arthur, that I know it and can bear it.’", "아서, 그걸 내가 알고 또 견딜 수 있다는 건 나에겐 다행이지.\""]], [["Sitting with her hands laid separately upon the desk, and the tall cabinet towering before her, she looked as if she were performing on a dumb church organ.", "두 손을 책상 위에 따로따로 얹은 채, 키 큰 캐비닛이 앞에서 우뚝 솟아오른 가운데 앉아 있는 그녀는 마치 소리 없는 교회 오르간을 연주하고 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Her son thought so (it was an old thought with him), while he took his seat beside it.", "그녀의 아들은 그 옆에 자리를 잡으면서 그렇게 생각했다(그에게는 오래된 생각이었다)."]], [["She opened a drawer or two, looked over some business papers, and put them back again.", "그녀는 서랍을 한두 개 열어 사무 서류들을 들여다보고는 다시 도로 넣었다."], ["Her severe face had no thread of relaxation in it, by which any explorer could have been guided to the gloomy labyrinth of her thoughts.", "그녀의 엄한 얼굴에는 어떤 탐색자라도 그녀의 음울한 생각의 미궁으로 이끌릴 만한 누그러짐의 실마리 하나 없었다."]], [["‘Shall I speak of our affairs, mother?", "\"우리 일에 대해 말씀드려도 될까요, 어머니?"], ["Are you inclined to enter upon business?’", "사업 이야기를 시작할 마음이 있으신가요?\""]], [["‘Am I inclined, Arthur?", "\"마음이 있느냐고, 아서?"], ["Rather, are you? Your father has been dead a year and more.", "차라리 네가 있느냐고 묻는 게 낫겠구나."], ["I have been at your disposal, and waiting your pleasure, ever since.’", "네 아버지가 돌아가신 지 일 년이 넘었다. 그 후로 줄곧 나는 네 처분에 맡겨진 채, 네 뜻을 기다려 왔다.\""]], [["‘There was much to arrange before I could leave; and when I did leave, I travelled a little for rest and relief.’", "\"제가 떠날 수 있기까지 정리할 일이 많았습니다. 그리고 정작 떠난 뒤에는 휴식과 안정을 위해 조금 여행을 했지요.\""]], [["She turned her face towards him, as not having heard or understood his last words.", "그녀는 그의 마지막 말을 듣지 못했거나 알아듣지 못한 것처럼 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌렸다."]], [["‘For rest and relief.’", "\"휴식과 안정을 위해서.\""]], [["She glanced round the sombre room, and appeared from the motion of her lips to repeat the words to herself, as calling it to witness how little of either it afforded her.", "그녀는 음침한 방을 둘러보았고, 그 입술의 움직임으로 보아 그 말을 혼자 되뇌는 듯했으니, 마치 이 방이 자신에게 그 둘 가운데 어느 것도 거의 베풀어 주지 않았음을 증언하라는 듯이."]], [["‘Besides, mother, you being sole executrix, and having the direction and management of the estate, there remained little business, or I might say none, that I could transact, until you had had time to arrange matters to your satisfaction.’", "\"게다가 어머니, 어머니가 유일한 유언 집행자로서 재산의 관리와 운영을 맡고 계시니, 어머니가 흡족하실 만큼 일을 정리하실 시간을 갖기 전까지는 제가 처리할 수 있는 사업이라곤 거의 없었습니다, 아니 전혀 없었다고 해도 좋겠지요.\""]], [["‘The accounts are made out,’ she returned.", "\"계산은 다 정리되어 있다.\""], ["‘I have them here.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["The vouchers have all been examined and passed.", "\"여기 있어. 증빙들도 모두 검토되고 통과되었다."], ["You can inspect them when you like, Arthur; now, if you please.’", "보고 싶을 때 언제든 살펴보아라, 아서. 지금이라도 좋으면.\""]], [["‘It is quite enough, mother, to know that the business is completed.", "\"사업이 완결되었다는 것을 아는 것만으로 충분합니다, 어머니."], ["Shall I proceed then?’", "그럼 계속해도 될까요?\""]], [["‘Why not?’ she said, in her frozen way.", "\"안 될 게 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 그 얼어붙은 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘Mother, our House has done less and less for some years past, and our dealings have been progressively on the decline.", "\"어머니, 우리 상사는 지난 몇 해 동안 점점 더 일이 줄어들었고, 거래도 차츰 기울어 왔습니다. 우리는 신용을 많이 보인 적도, 많이 청한 적도 없습니다."], ["We have never shown much confidence, or invited much; we have attached no people to us; the track we have kept is not the track of the time; and we have been left far behind.", "사람을 우리에게 붙잡아 둔 적도 없고요. 우리가 지켜 온 길은 시대의 길이 아니어서, 우리는 한참 뒤처지고 말았습니다."], ["I need not dwell on this to you, mother.", "이 점을 어머니께 길게 늘어놓을 필요는 없겠지요, 어머니."], ["You know it necessarily.’", "어머니께서는 당연히 아실 테니까요.\""]], [["‘I know what you mean,’ she answered, in a qualified tone.", "\"네가 무슨 말을 하려는지 안다.\" 그녀가 단정 짓는 어조로 대답했다."]], [["‘Even this old house in which we speak,’ pursued her son, ‘is an instance of what I say.", "\"우리가 이렇게 이야기를 나누고 있는 이 낡은 집조차,\" 그녀의 아들이 말을 이었다."], ["In my father’s earlier time, and in his uncle’s time before him, it was a place of business--really a place of business, and business resort.", "\"제가 말씀드린 바를 보여 주는 한 예입니다. 아버지의 젊은 시절, 그리고 그 전 아버지의 숙부 시절에는, 이곳이 사업장이었습니다 — 정말로 사업장이자 거래가 드나드는 곳이었지요."], ["Now, it is a mere anomaly and incongruity here, out of date and out of purpose.", "지금은 그저 이곳에 어울리지 않는 변칙이고 부조화일 뿐이며, 시대에도 목적에도 맞지 않습니다. 우리의 위탁 거래는 오래전부터 모두 위탁 상인인 로빙엄 상회로 넘어가 있고요."], ["All our consignments have long been made to Rovinghams’ the commission-merchants; and although, as a check upon them, and in the stewardship of my father’s resources, your judgment and watchfulness have been actively exerted, still those qualities would have influenced my father’s fortunes equally, if you had lived in any private dwelling: would they not?’", "그들을 견제하기 위해, 그리고 아버지의 재산을 관리하시면서 어머니의 판단과 경계심이 활발히 발휘되어 왔다고는 하나, 그래도 그런 자질들은 어머니가 어느 사가에 사셨더라도 아버지의 재산에 똑같이 영향을 미쳤을 겁니다. 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘Do you consider,’ she returned, without answering his question, ‘that a house serves no purpose, Arthur, in sheltering your infirm and afflicted--justly infirm and righteously afflicted--mother?’", "\"아서, 네 생각엔,\" 그녀가 그의 질문에는 답하지 않은 채 대꾸했다. \"집이라는 게, 병들고 고통받는 — 마땅히 병들고 의롭게 고통받는 — 네 어미를 거두는 데에 아무 쓸모도 없다는 게냐?\""]], [["‘I was speaking only of business purposes.’", "\"저는 사업상의 쓸모만을 말씀드린 것입니다.\""]], [["‘With what object?’", "\"무슨 의도로?\""]], [["‘I am coming to it.’", "\"이제 그 이야기로 들어가려 합니다.\""]], [["‘I foresee,’ she returned, fixing her eyes upon him, ‘what it is.", "\"무슨 이야기인지 짐작이 가는구나.\" 그녀가 그에게 시선을 고정하며 대꾸했다."], ["But the Lord forbid that I should repine under any visitation.", "\"허나 어떤 시련 아래에서도 내가 불평하는 일이 없도록 주께서 막아 주시기를."], ["In my sinfulness I merit bitter disappointment, and I accept it.’", "죄 많은 나는 쓰라린 실망을 받아 마땅하니, 나는 그것을 받아들인다.\""]], [["‘Mother, I grieve to hear you speak like this, though I have had my apprehensions that you would--’", "\"어머니, 어머니께서 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 들으니 마음이 아픕니다. 그러시리라는 염려가 없었던 건 아니지만 —\""]], [["‘You knew I would.", "\"네가 그러리라는 걸 알고 있었다."], ["You knew _me_,’ she interrupted.", "너는 이 어미를 알았으니까.\" 그녀가 그의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["Her son paused for a moment.", "그녀의 아들은 잠시 멈칫했다."], ["He had struck fire out of her, and was surprised.", "그는 그녀에게서 불꽃을 일으켜 놓은 것이었고, 놀라웠다."], ["‘Well!’ she said, relapsing into stone.", "\"좋다!\" 그녀가 다시 돌처럼 굳어지며 말했다."], ["‘Go on.", "\"계속하려무나."], ["Let me hear.’", "들어 보자.\""]], [["‘You have anticipated, mother, that I decide for my part, to abandon the business.", "\"어머니, 제가 제 몫의 일에 관해, 사업을 그만두기로 결심했다는 것을 어머니께서는 미리 짐작하셨을 겁니다. 저는 그 일에서 손을 뗐습니다."], ["I have done with it.", "감히 어머니께 조언하려 들지는 않겠습니다."], ["I will not take upon myself to advise you; you will continue it, I see.", "어머니께서는 그 일을 계속하시겠지요, 알겠습니다. 만약 제가 어머니께 조금이라도 영향력을 가졌다면, 그저 그것으로 어머니께 이런 실망을 안겨 드린 저에 대한 어머니의 판단을 누그러뜨리는 데 쓰고 싶습니다."], ["If I had any influence with you, I would simply use it to soften your judgment of me in causing you this disappointment: to represent to you that I have lived the half of a long term of life, and have never before set my own will against yours.", "즉, 제가 긴 인생의 절반을 살아오는 동안 단 한 번도 제 뜻을 어머니의 뜻에 맞서 세운 적이 없었다는 것을 말씀드리고 싶습니다. 제가 마음과 정신으로 어머니의 규율에 저를 맞출 수 있었다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다."], ["I cannot say that I have been able to conform myself, in heart and spirit, to your rules; I cannot say that I believe my forty years have been profitable or pleasant to myself, or any one; but I have habitually submitted, and I only ask you to remember it.’", "제 사십 년이 저 자신에게나 그 누구에게 유익하거나 즐거웠다고는 말씀드리지 못하겠습니다. 그러나 저는 늘 순종해 왔으니, 그것만은 기억해 주시기를 청합니다.\""]], [["Woe to the suppliant, if such a one there were or ever had been, who had any concession to look for in the inexorable face at the cabinet.", "만약 캐비닛 앞의 그 냉혹한 얼굴에서 어떤 양보를 기대할 탄원자가 있었거나 일찍이 있었다면, 그런 자에게 화 있을진저."], ["Woe to the defaulter whose appeal lay to the tribunal where those severe eyes presided.", "그 준엄한 두 눈이 주재하는 법정에 호소할 채무자에게 화 있을진저."], ["Great need had the rigid woman of her mystical religion, veiled in gloom and darkness, with lightnings of cursing, vengeance, and destruction, flashing through the sable clouds.", "음울과 어둠에 가려져, 검은 구름 사이로 저주와 복수와 파멸의 번개가 번득이는 그 신비주의적 종교가 이 강철 같은 여인에게는 큰 필요였다. 우리가 우리에게 빚진 자를 용서하듯 우리 빚을 용서하소서, 라는 기도는 그녀에게는 정신이 너무 빈약한 기도였다."], ["Forgive us our debts as we forgive our debtors, was a prayer too poor in spirit for her.", "주여, 제 빚진 자들을 치소서, 그들을 시들게 하소서, 그들을 짓이기소서."], ["Smite Thou my debtors, Lord, wither them, crush them; do Thou as I would do, and Thou shalt have my worship: this was the impious tower of stone she built up to scale Heaven.", "제가 하고 싶은 대로 주께서 하시면 제 경배를 받으시리이다. 이것이 그녀가 하늘에 오르려고 쌓아 올린 불경한 돌탑이었다."]], [["‘Have you finished, Arthur, or have you anything more to say to me?", "\"다 끝냈느냐, 아서, 아니면 나에게 더 할 말이 있느냐?"], ["I think there can be nothing else.", "더는 없을 듯싶구나."], ["You have been short, but full of matter!’", "너는 짧게 말했다만, 알맹이는 잔뜩 들었구나!\""]], [["‘Mother, I have yet something more to say.", "\"어머니, 아직 더 드릴 말씀이 있습니다."], ["It has been upon my mind, night and day, this long time.", "오랫동안 밤낮으로 제 마음을 짓눌러 온 것입니다."], ["It is far more difficult to say than what I have said.", "방금 드린 말씀보다 훨씬 더 꺼내기 어려운 이야기입니다."], ["That concerned myself; this concerns us all.’\n‘Us all! Who are us all?’", "그것은 저 자신에 관한 것이었지만, 이것은 우리 모두에 관한 것입니다.\""]], [["‘Yourself, myself, my dead father.’", "\"우리 모두라니! 우리 모두라는 게 누구냐?\""]], [["She took her hands from the desk; folded them in her lap; and sat looking towards the fire, with the impenetrability of an old Egyptian sculpture.", "\"어머니 자신, 저 자신, 그리고 돌아가신 아버지입니다.\" 그녀는 두 손을 책상에서 떼어 무릎 위에 포개고는, 고대 이집트 조각상 같은 헤아릴 길 없는 표정으로 불을 향해 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘You knew my father infinitely better than I ever knew him; and his reserve with me yielded to you.", "\"어머니는 제가 아버지를 알았던 것보다 한없이 더 잘 아버지를 아셨고, 저에게 닫혀 있던 아버지의 마음도 어머니께는 열렸지요."], ["You were much the stronger, mother, and directed him.", "어머니가 훨씬 더 강하셨고, 아버지를 이끄셨습니다."], ["As a child, I knew it as well as I know it now.", "어린아이였을 때도 저는 지금만큼이나 그것을 잘 알았습니다."], ["I knew that your ascendancy over him was the cause of his going to China to take care of the business there, while you took care of it here (though I do not even now know whether these were really terms of separation that you agreed upon); and that it was your will that I should remain with you until I was twenty, and then go to him as I did.", "아버지가 중국으로 건너가 그곳 사업을 돌보시고 어머니가 이곳에서 사업을 돌보시게 된 것이 어머니가 아버지를 지배하신 탓이었음을 알았습니다(물론 그것이 정말로 두 분이 합의하신 별거의 조건이었는지는 지금도 모릅니다만). 그리고 제가 스무 살이 될 때까지 어머니 곁에 머물렀다가, 그 후에 아버지께로 가게 된 것도 어머니의 뜻이었지요."], ["You will not be offended by my recalling this, after twenty years?’", "제가 그렇게 했습니다. 이십 년이 지난 지금 이것을 되새긴다고 해서 노여워하시지는 않겠지요?\""]], [["‘I am waiting to hear why you recall it.’", "\"왜 그걸 되새기는지 들어 보려고 기다리는 중이다.\""]], [["He lowered his voice, and said, with manifest reluctance, and against his will:", "그는 목소리를 낮추고, 분명한 망설임을 띤 채, 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 말했다."]], [["‘I want to ask you, mother, whether it ever occurred to you to suspect--’", "\"어머니께 여쭙고 싶습니다. 혹시 어머니께서 한 번이라도 의심해 보신 적이 있는지 —\""]], [["At the word Suspect, she turned her eyes momentarily upon her son, with a dark frown.", "의심이라는 말에 그녀는 어두운 찌푸림으로 잠깐 아들에게 두 눈을 돌렸다."], ["She then suffered them to seek the fire, as before; but with the frown fixed above them, as if the sculptor of old Egypt had indented it in the hard granite face, to frown for ages.", "그러고는 전처럼 두 눈을 불을 향하게 두었으되, 마치 고대 이집트의 조각가가 그 단단한 화강암 얼굴에 영겁토록 찌푸린 채로 있도록 새겨 넣은 것처럼, 그 위에 찌푸림을 굳힌 채였다."]], [["‘--that he had any secret remembrance which caused him trouble of mind--remorse?", "\"— 아버지께서 마음의 괴로움을 — 회한을 — 일으키는 어떤 비밀스러운 기억을 품고 계셨던 건 아닌지를 말입니다."], ["Whether you ever observed anything in his conduct suggesting that; or ever spoke to him upon it, or ever heard him hint at such a thing?’", "아버지의 행동에서 그런 것을 암시하는 무언가를 관찰하신 적이 있는지, 혹은 그 일에 관해 아버지와 이야기를 나누신 적이 있는지, 아니면 아버지께서 그런 것을 넌지시 비추는 것을 들으신 적이 있는지를요.\""]], [["‘I do not understand what kind of secret remembrance you mean to infer that your father was a prey to,’ she returned, after a silence.", "\"네가 추론하려는, 네 아버지를 괴롭혔다는 그 비밀스러운 기억이라는 게 어떤 종류의 것인지 나는 모르겠다.\" 그녀가 침묵 끝에 대꾸했다."], ["‘You speak so mysteriously.’", "\"너는 너무 알쏭달쏭하게 말하는구나.\""]], [["‘Is it possible, mother,’ her son leaned forward to be the nearer to her while he whispered it, and laid his hand nervously upon her desk, ‘is it possible, mother, that he had unhappily wronged any one, and made no reparation?’", "\"혹시 그러신 게 아닐까요, 어머니,\" 그녀의 아들은 속삭이며 그녀에게 더 가까이 가려고 몸을 앞으로 숙이고는 초조하게 그녀의 책상 위에 손을 얹었다. \"혹시, 어머니, 아버지께서 불행히도 누군가에게 해를 끼치고는 아무런 보상도 하지 않으신 게 아닐까요?\""]], [["Looking at him wrathfully, she bent herself back in her chair to keep him further off, but gave him no reply.", "그를 노여워하며 바라보던 그녀는 그를 더 멀리 떼어 놓으려고 의자에 등을 젖혔지만, 그에게 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘I am deeply sensible, mother, that if this thought has never at any time flashed upon you, it must seem cruel and unnatural in me, even in this confidence, to breathe it.", "\"저는 깊이 느끼고 있습니다, 어머니. 만약 이런 생각이 어머니께는 단 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없는 것이라면, 이런 은밀한 자리에서나마 제가 그것을 입에 올리는 것이 어머니께는 잔인하고 부자연스럽게 보이리라는 것을요."], ["But I cannot shake it off.", "그러나 저는 그 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없습니다."], ["Time and change (I have tried both before breaking silence) do nothing to wear it out.", "세월도 변화도 (침묵을 깨기 전에 저는 그 둘을 다 시험해 보았습니다) 그것을 닳아 없애지 못합니다."], ["Remember, I was with my father.", "기억하세요, 저는 아버지 곁에 있었습니다."], ["Remember, I saw his face when he gave the watch into my keeping, and struggled to express that he sent it as a token you would understand, to you.", "기억하세요, 아버지께서 그 시계를 제게 맡기시며, 그것을 어머니께서 알아들으실 정표로 어머니께 보내신다고 애써 표현하려 하시던 그 얼굴을 저는 보았습니다."], ["Remember, I saw him at the last with the pencil in his failing hand, trying to write some word for you to read, but to which he could give no shape.", "기억하세요, 마지막 순간에 아버지께서 힘 빠진 손에 연필을 쥐고, 어머니께서 읽으실 무슨 말을 쓰려 하셨으나 끝내 형태를 이루지 못하시던 모습을 저는 보았습니다."], ["The more remote and cruel this vague suspicion that I have, the stronger the circumstances that could give it any semblance of probability to me.", "제가 품은 이 막연한 의심이 멀고 잔인할수록, 저에게 그것이 그럴듯하게 보이도록 만드는 정황은 더 강해집니다."], ["For Heaven’s sake, let us examine sacredly whether there is any wrong entrusted to us to set right.", "부디, 우리에게 바로잡으라고 맡겨진 잘못이 있는지 없는지 경건하게 살펴봅시다."], ["No one can help towards it, mother, but you.’", "그 일을 거들 수 있는 사람은, 어머니, 어머니밖에 없습니다.\""]], [["Still so recoiling in her chair that her overpoised weight moved it, from time to time, a little on its wheels, and gave her the appearance of a phantom of fierce aspect gliding away from him, she interposed her left arm, bent at the elbow with the back of her hand towards her face, between herself and him, and looked at him in a fixed silence.", "그녀가 의자에 너무 깊이 몸을 젖혀, 한쪽으로 쏠린 무게가 이따금 의자를 바퀴 위에서 조금씩 움직여, 사나운 모습의 유령이 그에게서 미끄러져 멀어지는 듯한 형상을 만들어 내는 가운데, 그녀는 왼팔을 팔꿈치에서 굽혀 손등을 제 얼굴 쪽으로 향하게 한 채 자신과 그 사이에 끼워 넣고는, 굳은 침묵 속에서 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘In grasping at money and in driving hard bargains--I have begun, and I must speak of such things now, mother--some one may have been grievously deceived, injured, ruined.", "\"돈을 움켜쥐고 모진 흥정을 벌이는 가운데 — 이미 시작했으니, 어머니, 이제 이런 것을 말씀드려야겠습니다 — 누군가가 처참하게 속고, 다치고, 파멸당했을지도 모릅니다."], ["You were the moving power of all this machinery before my birth; your stronger spirit has been infused into all my father’s dealings for more than two score years.", "제가 태어나기 전부터 이 모든 기계 장치의 동력은 어머니셨습니다. 사십 년이 넘도록 아버지의 모든 거래에는 어머니의 더 강한 정신이 스며들어 있었지요."], ["You can set these doubts at rest, I think, if you will really help me to discover the truth.", "어머니가 정말로 진실을 밝히는 데 저를 도와주신다면, 이 의혹들을 가라앉히실 수 있으리라 생각합니다."], ["Will you, mother?’", "도와주시겠습니까, 어머니?\""]], [["He stopped in the hope that she would speak.", "그는 그녀가 입을 열기를 바라며 말을 멈췄다."], ["But her grey hair was not more immovable in its two folds, than were her firm lips.", "그러나 두 갈래로 빗어 넘긴 그녀의 잿빛 머리도 그녀의 굳게 다문 입술보다 더 미동도 없지는 못했다."]], [["‘If reparation can be made to any one, if restitution can be made to any one, let us know it and make it.", "\"누군가에게 보상이 이루어질 수 있다면, 누군가에게 배상이 이루어질 수 있다면, 그것을 알아내어 그렇게 합시다."], ["Nay, mother, if within my means, let _me_ make it.", "아니, 어머니, 제 형편이 닿는 한, 제가 그것을 하게 해 주십시오."], ["I have seen so little happiness come of money; it has brought within my knowledge so little peace to this house, or to any one belonging to it, that it is worth less to me than to another.", "저는 돈에서 행복이 나오는 것을 거의 본 적이 없습니다. 돈은 이 집에든, 이 집에 딸린 누구에게든 평안을 거의 가져다주지 않았으니, 그것은 저에게는 남들에게보다 가치가 덜합니다."], ["It can buy me nothing that will not be a reproach and misery to me, if I am haunted by a suspicion that it darkened my father’s last hours with remorse, and that it is not honestly and justly mine.’", "만약 그 돈이 아버지의 마지막 시간을 회한으로 어둡게 했고, 또 정직하고 정당하게 제 것이 아니라는 의심에 제가 시달린다면, 그것이 저에게 사 줄 수 있는 것이라고는 모두 비난과 비참이 될 뿐입니다.\""]], [["There was a bell-rope hanging on the panelled wall, some two or three yards from the cabinet.", "캐비닛에서 두세 야드쯤 떨어진 곳, 벽판에 종 줄이 걸려 있었다."], ["By a swift and sudden action of her foot, she drove her wheeled chair rapidly back to it and pulled it violently--still holding her arm up in its shield-like posture, as if he were striking at her, and she warding off the blow.", "그녀는 발을 빠르고 갑작스럽게 움직여 바퀴 의자를 그쪽으로 잽싸게 밀어 가서는 그것을 힘껏 잡아당겼다 — 여전히 마치 그가 자신을 치려 하고 자기는 그 일격을 막아 내려는 듯, 팔을 방패 같은 자세로 들어 올린 채였다."]], [["A girl came hurrying in, frightened.", "한 처녀가 겁에 질려 허둥지둥 들어왔다."]], [["‘Send Flintwinch here!’", "\"플린트윈치를 이리 보내라!\""]], [["In a moment the girl had withdrawn, and the old man stood within the door.", "순식간에 처녀는 물러갔고, 노인이 문 안에 섰다."], ["‘What!", "\"아니!"], ["You’re hammer and tongs, already, you two?’ he said, coolly stroking his face.", "두 분이 벌써 으르렁대고 계셨소?\" 그가 얼굴을 천천히 쓸어내리며 차분하게 말했다."], ["‘I thought you would be.", "\"그러실 줄 알았소."], ["I was pretty sure of it.’", "거의 확신했지요.\""]], [["‘Flintwinch!’ said the mother, ‘look at my son.", "\"플린트윈치!\" 어머니가 말했다."], ["Look at him!’", "\"내 아들을 보게. 저 애를 보게!\""]], [["‘Well, I _am_ looking at him,’ said Flintwinch.", "\"음, 보고 있소이다.\" 플린트윈치가 말했다."]], [["She stretched out the arm with which she had shielded herself, and as she went on, pointed at the object of her anger.", "그녀는 자신을 방패처럼 가렸던 팔을 쭉 뻗어, 말을 이으면서 자신의 분노의 대상을 가리켰다."]], [["‘In the very hour of his return almost--before the shoe upon his foot is dry--he asperses his father’s memory to his mother!", "\"돌아온 지 거의 그 시각도 채 못 되어 — 제 발에 신은 신이 마르기도 전에 — 저 애가 제 어미 앞에서 제 아비의 기억을 더럽히고 있다네!"], ["Asks his mother to become, with him, a spy upon his father’s transactions through a lifetime!", "자기와 함께 평생에 걸친 제 아비의 거래를 캐는 첩자가 되어 달라고 제 어미에게 청하다니!"], ["Has misgivings that the goods of this world which we have painfully got together early and late, with wear and tear and toil and self-denial, are so much plunder; and asks to whom they shall be given up, as reparation and restitution!’", "우리가 이른 새벽부터 밤늦게까지, 닳고 해지고 애써 고생하고 자제하며 어렵사리 그러모은 이 세상의 재물이 죄다 약탈물이 아닌가 하는 의심을 품고는, 그것을 보상이며 배상이라며 누구에게 넘겨주어야 하느냐고 묻고 있다네!\""]], [["Although she said this raging, she said it in a voice so far from being beyond her control that it was even lower than her usual tone.", "이것을 노발대발하며 말하긴 했어도, 그녀의 목소리는 자제력을 잃기는커녕 평소의 어조보다 오히려 더 낮을 정도였다."], ["She also spoke with great distinctness.", "그녀는 또한 더없이 또렷하게 말했다."]], [["‘Reparation!’ said she.", "\"보상이라!\""], ["‘Yes, truly!", "그녀가 말했다."], ["It is easy for him to talk of reparation, fresh from journeying and junketing in foreign lands, and living a life of vanity and pleasure.", "\"그래, 정말이지! 외국 땅에서 여행과 흥청거림을 막 마치고 와 허영과 향락의 삶을 누리는 저 애에게는 보상을 입에 올리기가 쉽겠지."], ["But let him look at me, in prison, and in bonds here.", "허나 여기 감옥에서, 사슬에 매여 있는 이 어미를 보라지."], ["I endure without murmuring, because it is appointed that I shall so make reparation for my sins.", "나는 불평 없이 견딘다. 내 죄를 그렇게 보상하도록 정해져 있기 때문이지."], ["Reparation!", "보상이라!"], ["Is there none in this room?", "이 방 안에 그게 없단 말이냐?"], ["Has there been none here this fifteen years?’", "지난 십오 년간 여기에 그게 없었단 말이냐?\""]], [["Thus was she always balancing her bargains with the Majesty of heaven, posting up the entries to her credit, strictly keeping her set-off, and claiming her due.", "이렇듯 그녀는 늘 하늘의 위엄과 자신의 거래를 따져 맞추며, 자기 쪽 대변에 항목을 기입하고, 자신의 상쇄분을 엄격히 챙기며, 받을 몫을 요구했다."], ["She was only remarkable in this, for the force and emphasis with which she did it.", "그녀가 이 점에서 유별난 것은 다만 그것을 행하는 힘과 강조에 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Thousands upon thousands do it, according to their varying manner, every day.", "수천, 수만의 사람들이 저마다의 방식에 따라 날마다 그렇게 한다."]], [["‘Flintwinch, give me that book!’", "\"플린트윈치, 그 책을 이리 주게!\""]], [["The old man handed it to her from the table.", "노인은 탁자에서 그것을 집어 그녀에게 건넸다."], ["She put two fingers between the leaves, closed the book upon them, and held it up to her son in a threatening way.", "그녀는 책장 사이에 손가락 두 개를 끼우고 그 위로 책을 덮어 든 채, 위협하는 자세로 아들에게 높이 들어 보였다."]], [["‘In the days of old, Arthur, treated of in this commentary, there were pious men, beloved of the Lord, who would have cursed their sons for less than this: who would have sent them forth, and sent whole nations forth, if such had supported them, to be avoided of God and man, and perish, down to the baby at the breast.", "\"이 주해서에서 다루는 옛날에는, 아서, 이보다 더 작은 일로도 제 아들을 저주했을, 주께서 사랑하시는 경건한 사람들이 있었다. 그자들은 제 아들들을 내쫓았을 것이고, 또 그런 무리가 제 아들들을 편들었다면 온 민족을 내쫓아, 하느님과 사람에게 외면당하고 멸망케 하여, 젖먹이 갓난아이에 이르기까지 죽게 했을 것이다."], ["But I only tell you that if you ever renew that theme with me, I will renounce you; I will so dismiss you through that doorway, that you had better have been motherless from your cradle.", "허나 나는 너에게 이것만은 일러 두마. 네가 만약 또다시 나에게 그 주제를 끄집어낸다면, 나는 너를 의절할 것이다."], ["I will never see or know you more.", "너를 저 문간으로 내쫓아, 너는 요람에서부터 차라리 어미가 없었던 편이 나았다 할 만큼 만들 것이다."], ["And if, after all, you were to come into this darkened room to look upon me lying dead, my body should bleed, if I could make it, when you came near me.’", "나는 너를 다시는 보지도 알지도 않을 것이다. 그리고 끝내 네가 죽어 누운 나를 보러 이 어두운 방에 들어온다면, 할 수만 있다면, 네가 가까이 올 때 내 시신이 피를 흘릴 것이다.\""]], [["In part relieved by the intensity of this threat, and in part (monstrous as the fact is) by a general impression that it was in some sort a religious proceeding, she handed back the book to the old man, and was silent.", "이 위협의 격렬함으로 어느 정도 풀리고, 또 어느 정도는 (이런 사실이 괴이하기는 하나) 그것이 어떤 의미에서는 종교적 행위라는 막연한 인상으로 마음이 풀린 그녀는, 책을 노인에게 도로 건네주고 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Jeremiah; ‘premising that I’m not going to stand between you two, will you let me ask (as I _have_ been called in, and made a third) what is all this about?’", "\"자,\" 제레미아가 말했다. \"내가 두 분 사이에 끼어들 생각은 없다는 걸 전제하고, (이렇게 불려 와 제삼자가 되었으니) 이게 다 무슨 일인지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["‘Take your version of it,’ returned Arthur, finding it left to him to speak, ‘from my mother.", "\"어머니께 들으시지요.\" 말할 차례가 자기에게 넘어온 것을 알아챈 아서가 대꾸했다."], ["Let it rest there.", "\"거기서 끝내 둡시다."], ["What I have said, was said to my mother only.’", "제가 한 말은 어머니께만 드린 말이었으니까요.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ returned the old man.", "\"오!\" 노인이 대꾸했다."], ["‘From your mother?", "\"어머님께?"], ["Take it from your mother?", "어머님께 들으라고? 그렇지!"], ["Well! But your mother mentioned that you had been suspecting your father.", "허나 어머님은 자네가 아버지를 의심해 왔다고 말씀하시던데."], ["That’s not dutiful, Mr Arthur.", "그건 효성스럽지 못한 일이오, 아서 씨."], ["Who will you be suspecting next?’", "다음엔 누구를 의심하려고 그러시오?\""]], [["‘Enough,’ said Mrs Clennam, turning her face so that it was addressed for the moment to the old man only.", "\"그만하면 됐네.\" 클레넘 부인이 잠시 노인에게만 들리도록 얼굴을 돌리며 말했다."], ["‘Let no more be said about this.’", "\"이 일에 대해서는 더는 말하지 말게.\""]], [["‘Yes, but stop a bit, stop a bit,’ the old man persisted.", "\"예, 허나 잠깐만, 잠깐만요.\" 노인이 고집했다."], ["‘Let us see how we stand.", "\"우리가 어떻게 서 있는지 봅시다."], ["Have you told Mr Arthur that he mustn’t lay offences at his father’s door?", "아서 씨에게 그가 제 아비에게 잘못을 뒤집어씌워서는 안 된다고 말씀하셨소?"], ["That he has no right to do it?", "그가 그럴 권리가 없다고?"], ["That he has no ground to go upon?’", "근거로 삼을 게 아무것도 없다고?\""]], [["‘I tell him so now.’", "\"지금 그렇게 말하고 있네.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Exactly,’ said the old man.", "바로 그거요.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["‘You tell him so now.", "\"지금 그렇게 말씀하신다."], ["You hadn’t told him so before, and you tell him so now. Ay, ay!", "전에는 그에게 그렇게 말씀하지 않으시고, 이제 와 그렇게 말씀하신다."], ["That’s right!", "옳거니, 옳거니!"], ["You know I stood between you and his father so long, that it seems as if death had made no difference, and I was still standing between you.", "그게 옳소! 내가 그토록 오랫동안 마님과 그의 아비 사이에 끼어 있던 터라, 죽음이 와도 달라진 게 없는 듯, 나는 여태 두 분 사이에 끼어 있는 것 같소."], ["So I will, and so in fairness I require to have that plainly put forward.", "그러니 그렇게 하리다. 그리고 공정을 위해서라도 나는 그걸 분명히 내세워 달라고 요구하는 바요."], ["Arthur, you please to hear that you have no right to mistrust your father, and have no ground to go upon.’", "아서 씨, 들어 두시오. 자네는 아버지를 불신할 권리도 없고, 근거로 삼을 것도 없소.\""]], [["He put his hands to the back of the wheeled chair, and muttering to himself, slowly wheeled his mistress back to her cabinet.", "그는 바퀴 의자 뒤편에 손을 얹고 혼잣말로 중얼대며, 자신의 여주인을 천천히 캐비닛 앞으로 도로 밀고 갔다. \"자,\""], ["‘Now,’ he resumed, standing behind her: ‘in case I should go away leaving things half done, and so should be wanted again when you come to the other half and get into one of your flights, has Arthur told you what he means to do about the business?’", "그가 그녀의 뒤에 선 채 말을 이었다. \"혹시라도 내가 일을 절반만 해 놓고 떠났다가, 마님이 나머지 절반에 이르러 또 흥분에 휩싸이실 때 다시 불려 오는 일이 없도록 묻겠는데, 아서 씨가 사업에 관해 어찌하려는지 마님께 말씀드렸소?\""]], [["‘He has relinquished it.’", "\"그 애는 그것을 포기했네.\""]], [["‘In favour of nobody, I suppose?’", "\"누구한테도 넘겨주지 않고, 짐작건대?\""]], [["Mrs Clennam glanced at her son, leaning against one of the windows.", "클레넘 부인은 창문 하나에 기대 선 아들에게 시선을 던졌다."], ["He observed the look and said, ‘To my mother, of course.", "그는 그 눈길을 알아채고 말했다. \"어머니께 넘겨드린 것이지요, 물론."], ["She does what she pleases.’", "어머니께서 하고 싶으신 대로 하실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘And if any pleasure,’ she said after a short pause, ‘could arise for me out of the disappointment of my expectations that my son, in the prime of his life, would infuse new youth and strength into it, and make it of great profit and power, it would be in advancing an old and faithful servant.", "\"그리고 만약,\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그녀가 말했다."], ["Jeremiah, the captain deserts the ship, but you and I will sink or float with it.’", "\"한창나이의 내 아들이 사업에 새로운 젊음과 힘을 불어넣어 크게 이로움과 권세를 누리게 해 주리라던 내 기대가 어그러진 데에서 나에게 어떤 기쁨이라도 솟아날 수 있다면, 그것은 오래되고 충실한 종을 올려 세우는 데 있을 것이네. 제레미아, 선장은 배를 버리지만, 자네와 나는 배와 함께 가라앉든 떠오르든 할 걸세.\""]], [["Jeremiah, whose eyes glistened as if they saw money, darted a sudden look at the son, which seemed to say, ‘I owe _you_ no thanks for this; _you_ have done nothing towards it!’ and then told the mother that he thanked her, and that Affery thanked her, and that he would never desert her, and that Affery would never desert her.", "마치 돈이 보이기라도 하는 듯 눈을 번득이던 제레미아는, '나는 이 일로 자네에게 고마워할 게 없네. 자네는 이를 위해 한 일이 하나도 없으니까!'"], ["Finally, he hauled up his watch from its depths, and said, ‘Eleven.", "하고 말하는 듯한 눈초리를 아들에게 슬쩍 던지고는, 어머니에게 자기가 감사하다고, 또 애프리도 감사해한다고, 자기는 결코 마님을 저버리지 않을 것이며 애프리도 결코 마님을 저버리지 않을 것이라고 말했다. 마침내 그는 깊숙한 곳에서 시계를 끌어올려 보고는 \"열한 시군요."], ["Time for your oysters!’ and with that change of subject, which involved no change of expression or manner, rang the bell.", "굴 드실 시간이오!\" 하고 말했고, 표정이나 태도의 변화는 조금도 없이 그렇게 화제를 바꾸며 종을 울렸다."]], [["But Mrs Clennam, resolved to treat herself with the greater rigour for having been supposed to be unacquainted with reparation, refused to eat her oysters when they were brought.", "그러나 클레넘 부인은, 보상이라는 것을 모르는 사람으로 여겨졌다 하여 한층 더 엄격하게 자신을 다루기로 작정하고, 굴이 들어왔을 때 그것을 먹기를 거부했다. 굴은 먹음직스러워 보였다."], ["They looked tempting; eight in number, circularly set out on a white plate on a tray covered with a white napkin, flanked by a slice of buttered French roll, and a little compact glass of cool wine and water; but she resisted all persuasions, and sent them down again--placing the act to her credit, no doubt, in her Eternal Day-Book.", "흰 냅킨을 덮은 쟁반 위 흰 접시에 동그랗게 여덟 개가 차려져 있고, 그 옆에는 버터를 바른 프랑스식 둥근 빵 한 조각과 차가운 포도주에 물을 탄 자그마하고 야무진 잔이 곁들여져 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 온갖 권유를 다 뿌리치고 그것을 도로 내려보냈으니 — 틀림없이 그 행위를 자신의 영원한 장부에 자기 대변으로 기입하면서."]], [["This refection of oysters was not presided over by Affery, but by the girl who had appeared when the bell was rung; the same who had been in the dimly-lighted room last night.", "이 굴 식사를 주관한 것은 애프리가 아니라 종이 울렸을 때 나타났던 처녀였으니, 간밤에 흐릿하게 불 밝힌 방에 있던 바로 그 처녀였다."], ["Now that he had an opportunity of observing her, Arthur found that her diminutive figure, small features, and slight spare dress, gave her the appearance of being much younger than she was.", "이제 그녀를 살펴볼 기회를 얻은 아서는, 그녀의 자그마한 몸집과 작은 이목구비, 그리고 가냘프고 빈약한 옷차림 때문에 그녀가 실제보다 훨씬 어려 보인다는 것을 알았다."], ["A woman, probably of not less than two-and-twenty, she might have been passed in the street for little more than half that age.", "아마 스물두 살은 족히 되었을 여인이건만, 길에서 마주쳤다면 그 절반을 조금 넘는 나이로밖에 보이지 않았을 것이다."], ["Not that her face was very youthful, for in truth there was more consideration and care in it than naturally belonged to her utmost years; but she was so little and light, so noiseless and shy, and appeared so conscious of being out of place among the three hard elders, that she had all the manner and much of the appearance of a subdued child.", "그녀의 얼굴이 그리 앳된 것은 아니었으니, 사실 그 얼굴에는 그녀의 한껏 먹은 나이에 자연스레 어울리는 것보다 더 많은 사려와 근심이 어려 있었던 까닭이다. 그러나 그녀는 너무나 작고 가벼웠고, 너무나 소리 없고 수줍었으며, 세 명의 모진 어른들 틈에서 어울리지 못함을 너무나 의식하는 듯하여, 마치 주눅 든 아이의 태도를 모두, 그리고 그 겉모습의 많은 부분을 지니고 있었다."]], [["In a hard way, and in an uncertain way that fluctuated between patronage and putting down, the sprinkling from a watering-pot and hydraulic pressure, Mrs Clennam showed an interest in this dependent.", "모질게, 그리고 후원과 억누름, 물뿌리개에서 흩뿌리는 것과 수압으로 짓누르는 것 사이를 오락가락하는 종잡을 수 없는 방식으로, 클레넘 부인은 이 의지가지없는 처녀에게 관심을 보였다."], ["Even in the moment of her entrance, upon the violent ringing of the bell, when the mother shielded herself with that singular action from the son, Mrs Clennam’s eyes had had some individual recognition in them, which seemed reserved for her.", "종을 거칠게 울려 그녀가 들어선 그 순간, 어머니가 그 별난 동작으로 아들에게서 자신을 가렸을 때조차, 클레넘 부인의 두 눈에는 이 처녀에게만 따로 남겨 둔 듯한 어떤 개별적 알아봄이 담겨 있었다."], ["As there are degrees of hardness in the hardest metal, and shades of colour in black itself, so, even in the asperity of Mrs Clennam’s demeanour towards all the rest of humanity and towards Little Dorrit, there was a fine gradation.", "가장 단단한 금속에도 단단함의 정도가 있고, 검정 그 자체에도 빛깔의 명암이 있듯이, 다른 온 인류를 향한 클레넘 부인의 모진 태도와 리틀 도릿을 향한 태도 사이에도 미묘한 단계가 있었다."]], [["Little Dorrit let herself out to do needlework.", "리틀 도릿은 바느질 일을 하러 다녔다."], ["At so much a day--or at so little--from eight to eight, Little Dorrit was to be hired.", "하루에 얼마 — 아니, 얼마 안 되는 돈에 — 여덟 시부터 여덟 시까지, 리틀 도릿은 고용되었다."], ["Punctual to the moment, Little Dorrit appeared; punctual to the moment, Little Dorrit vanished.", "시각에 꼭 맞추어 리틀 도릿이 나타났고, 시각에 꼭 맞추어 리틀 도릿이 사라졌다."], ["What became of Little Dorrit between the two eights was a mystery.", "두 여덟 시 사이에 리틀 도릿이 어찌 되는지는 수수께끼였다."]], [["Another of the moral phenomena of Little Dorrit.", "리틀 도릿에게 얽힌 도덕적 현상 중 또 하나가 있었다."], ["Besides her consideration money, her daily contract included meals.", "그녀의 품삯 돈 외에도, 그녀의 하루 계약에는 식사가 포함되어 있었다."], ["She had an extraordinary repugnance to dining in company; would never do so, if it were possible to escape.", "그녀는 남들과 함께 식사하는 것을 유난히 꺼려, 피할 수만 있으면 결코 그러려 하지 않았다."], ["Would always plead that she had this bit of work to begin first, or that bit of work to finish first; and would, of a certainty, scheme and plan--not very cunningly, it would seem, for she deceived no one--to dine alone.", "늘 이쪽 일감을 먼저 시작해야 한다든가, 저쪽 일감을 먼저 끝내야 한다든가 하며 둘러댔고, 혼자 식사하려고 어김없이 궁리하고 꾀를 부렸다 — 그리 교묘하게는 아니었던 듯한데, 아무도 속이지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Successful in this, happy in carrying off her plate anywhere, to make a table of her lap, or a box, or the ground, or even as was supposed, to stand on tip-toe, dining moderately at a mantel-shelf; the great anxiety of Little Dorrit’s day was set at rest.", "이 일에 성공하여, 어디로든 제 접시를 가져가 무릎이나 상자나 바닥을 식탁 삼아, 혹은 짐작하건대 발끝으로 서서 벽난로 선반에 기대어 알맞게 식사를 할 수 있으면, 리틀 도릿의 하루의 큰 근심거리는 가라앉았다."]], [["It was not easy to make out Little Dorrit’s face; she was so retiring, plied her needle in such removed corners, and started away so scared if encountered on the stairs.", "리틀 도릿의 얼굴을 알아보기란 쉽지 않았다. 그녀는 너무나 내성적이었고, 외진 구석에서 바늘을 놀렸으며, 계단에서 마주치기라도 하면 겁에 질려 화들짝 달아났던 까닭이다."], ["But it seemed to be a pale transparent face, quick in expression, though not beautiful in feature, its soft hazel eyes excepted.", "그러나 그것은 창백하고 투명한 얼굴로, 이목구비가 아름답지는 않았으나 표정이 풍부했고, 다만 그 부드러운 담갈색 눈만은 예외였다."], ["A delicately bent head, a tiny form, a quick little pair of busy hands, and a shabby dress--it must needs have been very shabby to look at all so, being so neat--were Little Dorrit as she sat at work.", "곱게 숙인 머리, 자그마한 몸매, 부지런히 움직이는 작고 날랜 두 손, 그리고 초라한 옷차림 — 그토록 단정한데도 그렇게 보이려면 정말이지 무척 초라하기 마련이었으리라 — 그것이 일감 앞에 앉아 있는 리틀 도릿이었다."]], [["For these particulars or generalities concerning Little Dorrit, Mr Arthur was indebted in the course of the day to his own eyes and to Mrs Affery’s tongue.", "리틀 도릿에 관한 이런 자질구레한 사실들이나 두루뭉술한 사정들을, 아서 씨는 그날 하루 동안 제 두 눈과 애프리 부인의 혀에 빚졌다."], ["If Mrs Affery had had any will or way of her own, it would probably have been unfavourable to Little Dorrit.", "만약 애프리 부인에게 제 나름의 뜻이나 방식이 있었더라면, 그것은 아마 리틀 도릿에게 호의적이지 않았을 것이다."], ["But as ‘them two clever ones’--Mrs Affery’s perpetual reference, in whom her personality was swallowed up--were agreed to accept Little Dorrit as a matter of course, she had nothing for it but to follow suit.", "그러나 '그 영리한 두 사람' — 애프리 부인이 늘 들먹이며, 그 안에 자신의 인격이 통째로 삼켜져 버린 그 두 사람 — 이 리틀 도릿을 당연한 것으로 받아들이기로 합의한 터라, 그녀로서는 따르는 것 말고는 도리가 없었다."], ["Similarly, if the two clever ones had agreed to murder Little Dorrit by candlelight, Mrs Affery, being required to hold the candle, would no doubt have done it.", "마찬가지로, 만약 그 영리한 두 사람이 촛불 아래 리틀 도릿을 죽이기로 합의했다면, 애프리 부인은 촛불을 들고 있으라는 요구를 받았을 때 틀림없이 그렇게 했을 것이다."]], [["In the intervals of roasting the partridge for the invalid chamber, and preparing a baking-dish of beef and pudding for the dining-room, Mrs Affery made the communications above set forth; invariably putting her head in at the door again after she had taken it out, to enforce resistance to the two clever ones.", "환자 방에 올릴 자고새를 굽고 식당에 낼 쇠고기와 푸딩을 담은 구이 접시를 마련하는 틈틈이, 애프리 부인은 위에 적은 이야기들을 들려주었으니, 머리를 문밖으로 내밀었다가도 어김없이 다시 들이밀어 그 영리한 두 사람에게 저항할 것을 다그쳤다."], ["It appeared to have become a perfect passion with Mrs Flintwinch, that the only son should be pitted against them.", "외아들을 그들과 맞붙여 세우는 것이 플린트윈치 부인에게는 완연한 열정이 되어 버린 듯했다."]], [["In the course of the day, too, Arthur looked through the whole house.", "그날 하루 동안 아서는 집안 전체를 둘러보기도 했다."], ["Dull and dark he found it.", "그는 그곳이 음울하고 어둡다는 것을 알았다."], ["The gaunt rooms, deserted for years upon years, seemed to have settled down into a gloomy lethargy from which nothing could rouse them again.", "여러 해 동안 줄곧 방치된 휑한 방들은, 어떤 것으로도 다시는 깨울 수 없는 음울한 무기력에 빠져들어 가라앉아 버린 듯했다."], ["The furniture, at once spare and lumbering, hid in the rooms rather than furnished them, and there was no colour in all the house; such colour as had ever been there, had long ago started away on lost sunbeams--got itself absorbed, perhaps, into flowers, butterflies, plumage of birds, precious stones, what not.", "가구는 빈약하면서도 둔중하여, 방을 채운다기보다는 방 안에 숨어 있는 격이었고, 온 집 안에 빛깔이라곤 없었다. 일찍이 거기 있었던 빛깔이라곤 죄다 오래전 사라진 햇살을 따라 떠나가 — 어쩌면 꽃이며 나비며 새의 깃털이며 보석이며 무엇무엇 따위에 흡수되어 버린 것이었다."], ["There was not one straight floor from the foundation to the roof; the ceilings were so fantastically clouded by smoke and dust, that old women might have told fortunes in them better than in grouts of tea; the dead-cold hearths showed no traces of having ever been warmed but in heaps of soot that had tumbled down the chimneys, and eddied about in little dusky whirlwinds when the doors were opened.", "토대에서 지붕까지 반듯한 마룻바닥이라곤 하나도 없었다. 천장은 연기와 먼지로 어찌나 기괴하게 얼룩져 있었던지, 늙은 여인네들이 찻잎 찌꺼기보다 거기서 점을 더 잘 쳤을 법했다."], ["In what had once been a drawing-room, there were a pair of meagre mirrors, with dismal processions of black figures carrying black garlands, walking round the frames; but even these were short of heads and legs, and one undertaker-like Cupid had swung round on its own axis and got upside down, and another had fallen off altogether.", "죽은 듯 차가운 화덕은 굴뚝에서 굴러떨어져 문이 열릴 때마다 작은 거뭇한 회오리를 일으키며 휘도는 검댕 더미 말고는 일찍이 데워진 흔적이라곤 보이지 않았다. 한때 응접실이었던 곳에는 빈약한 거울 한 쌍이 있었는데, 그 액자 둘레로는 검은 화환을 든 검은 인물들의 음산한 행렬이 둘러 걸어가고 있었다."], ["The room Arthur Clennam’s deceased father had occupied for business purposes, when he first remembered him, was so unaltered that he might have been imagined still to keep it invisibly, as his visible relict kept her room up-stairs; Jeremiah Flintwinch still going between them negotiating.", "그러나 이마저도 머리나 다리가 모자랐고, 장의사 같은 큐피드 하나는 제 축을 따라 빙 돌아 거꾸로 매달려 있었으며, 또 하나는 아예 떨어져 나가 버렸다. 아서 클레넘이 그를 처음 기억할 무렵 그의 작고한 아버지가 사업 용도로 쓰던 방은 어찌나 그대로였던지, 마치 그가 위층에서 제 방을 지키는 그의 살아 있는 미망인처럼, 여전히 보이지 않게 그 방을 지키고 있다고 상상할 만했고, 제레미아 플린트윈치가 여전히 둘 사이를 오가며 협상을 하는 것 같았다."], ["His picture, dark and gloomy, earnestly speechless on the wall, with the eyes intently looking at his son as they had looked when life departed from them, seemed to urge him awfully to the task he had attempted; but as to any yielding on the part of his mother, he had now no hope, and as to any other means of setting his distrust at rest, he had abandoned hope a long time.", "그의 초상은, 어둡고 음울하게, 벽 위에서 사뭇 말없이, 두 눈은 생명이 떠나갈 때 바라보던 것처럼 아들을 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그가 시도했던 그 일을 으스스하게 재촉하는 듯했다. 그러나 어머니 쪽에서 무엇이든 양보하리라는 희망은 이제 그에게 없었고, 그 밖에 자신의 불신을 가라앉힐 다른 방도에 대한 희망은 오래전에 단념해 버렸다."], ["Down in the cellars, as up in the bed-chambers, old objects that he well remembered were changed by age and decay, but were still in their old places; even to empty beer-casks hoary with cobwebs, and empty wine-bottles with fur and fungus choking up their throats.", "침실들이 그러했듯 지하 창고에서도, 그가 잘 기억하는 옛 물건들이 세월과 부패로 변해 있었으나 여전히 제자리에 놓여 있었으니, 거미줄로 허옇게 센 빈 맥주통이며, 목구멍이 잔털과 곰팡이로 막힌 빈 포도주병에 이르기까지 그러했다."], ["There, too, among unusual bottle-racks and pale slants of light from the yard above, was the strong room stored with old ledgers, which had as musty and corrupt a smell as if they were regularly balanced, in the dead small hours, by a nightly resurrection of old book-keepers.", "그곳에는 또한 별난 술병 시렁들과 위층 마당에서 들어오는 희미하게 비껴든 빛줄기들 사이로, 낡은 장부들로 채워진 금고실이 있었으니, 그 장부들은 마치 죽은 듯 고요한 새벽녘에 옛 부기 담당자들이 밤마다 부활하여 꼬박꼬박 결산이라도 하는 양 케케묵고 썩은 냄새를 풍기고 있었다."]], [["The baking-dish was served up in a penitential manner on a shrunken cloth at an end of the dining-table, at two o’clock, when he dined with Mr Flintwinch, the new partner.", "구이 접시는 두 시에 식탁 한쪽 끝, 오그라든 식탁보 위에 참회하는 듯한 모양새로 차려졌고, 그는 거기서 새 동업자인 플린트윈치 씨와 함께 식사했다."], ["Mr Flintwinch informed him that his mother had recovered her equanimity now, and that he need not fear her again alluding to what had passed in the morning.", "플린트윈치 씨는 그에게 어머니가 이제 평정을 되찾으셨으니, 아침에 있었던 일을 다시 들먹이실까 걱정하지 않아도 된다고 알려 주었다. \"그리고 아버지께 잘못을 뒤집어씌우지 마시오, 아서 씨.\""], ["‘And don’t you lay offences at your father’s door, Mr Arthur,’ added Jeremiah, ‘once for all, don’t do it!", "제레미아가 덧붙였다. \"한 번 더 말하지만, 그러지 마시오!"], ["Now, we have done with the subject.’", "자, 그 이야기는 이제 끝냈소이다.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch had been already rearranging and dusting his own particular little office, as if to do honour to his accession to new dignity.", "플린트윈치 씨는 새 지위에 오른 것을 기리기라도 하듯 이미 자기만의 작은 사무실을 다시 정돈하고 먼지를 털어 내고 있었다."], ["He resumed this occupation when he was replete with beef, had sucked up all the gravy in the baking-dish with the flat of his knife, and had drawn liberally on a barrel of small beer in the scullery.", "그는 쇠고기로 배를 가득 채우고 칼날 옆면으로 구이 접시의 육즙을 모조리 핥아 올린 뒤 식기 세척실에서 약한 맥주통을 넉넉히 들이켜고는, 이 일을 다시 시작했다."], ["Thus refreshed, he tucked up his shirt-sleeves and went to work again; and Mr Arthur, watching him as he set about it, plainly saw that his father’s picture, or his father’s grave, would be as communicative with him as this old man.", "그렇게 기운을 차린 그가 셔츠 소매를 걷어붙이고 다시 일에 착수하자, 그가 일을 벌이는 모습을 지켜보던 아서 씨는, 아버지의 초상이나 아버지의 무덤이라도 이 노인만큼은 그에게 입을 열어 주리라는 것을 똑똑히 알았다."]], [["‘Now, Affery, woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch, as she crossed the hall.", "\"자, 애프리, 이 사람아.\" 그녀가 현관을 가로질러 가자 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You hadn’t made Mr Arthur’s bed when I was up there last.", "\"내가 아까 위에 올라가 봤더니 아서 씨 잠자리를 안 봐 놨더군."], ["Stir yourself.", "좀 부지런히 움직여."], ["Bustle.’", "서둘러.\""]], [["But Mr Arthur found the house so blank and dreary, and was so unwilling to assist at another implacable consignment of his mother’s enemies (perhaps himself among them) to mortal disfigurement and immortal ruin, that he announced his intention of lodging at the coffee-house where he had left his luggage.", "그러나 아서 씨는 집이 너무나 휑하고 적막하다는 것을 알았고, 또 어머니의 적들을(어쩌면 자기 자신도 그 가운데 하나로) 현세의 흉측한 꼴과 영원한 파멸로 또다시 가차 없이 넘겨 버리는 일에 거들고 싶지 않았으므로, 짐을 맡겨 둔 커피점에서 묵겠다는 뜻을 알렸다."], ["Mr Flintwinch taking kindly to the idea of getting rid of him, and his mother being indifferent, beyond considerations of saving, to most domestic arrangements that were not bounded by the walls of her own chamber, he easily carried this point without new offence.", "플린트윈치 씨가 그를 쫓아 버린다는 생각을 반갑게 받아들였고, 그의 어머니도 절약과 관련된 것이 아닌 한 자기 방의 벽 안에 한정되지 않는 대부분의 살림 문제에는 무관심했으므로, 그는 새로운 노여움을 사지 않고 이 점을 쉽게 관철했다."], ["Daily business hours were agreed upon, which his mother, Mr Flintwinch, and he, were to devote together to a necessary checking of books and papers; and he left the home he had so lately found, with depressed heart.", "어머니와 플린트윈치 씨와 그가 함께 장부와 서류를 필요한 만큼 대조하는 데 바칠 매일의 업무 시간이 정해졌고, 그는 그토록 얼마 전에야 다시 찾은 집을 침울한 마음으로 떠났다."]], [["But Little Dorrit?", "그런데 리틀 도릿은?"]], [["The business hours, allowing for intervals of invalid regimen of oysters and partridges, during which Clennam refreshed himself with a walk, were from ten to six for about a fortnight.", "업무 시간은, 클레넘이 산책으로 기운을 차리는 동안의 굴이며 자고새며 하는 환자식 섭생 시간을 빼고 나면, 약 보름 동안 열 시부터 여섯 시까지였다."], ["Sometimes Little Dorrit was employed at her needle, sometimes not, sometimes appeared as a humble visitor: which must have been her character on the occasion of his arrival.", "리틀 도릿은 때로는 바느질 일에 매달렸고, 때로는 그러지 않았으며, 때로는 보잘것없는 방문객으로 나타났으니 — 그가 도착하던 그날 그녀의 처지가 틀림없이 그러했을 것이다."], ["His original curiosity augmented every day, as he watched for her, saw or did not see her, and speculated about her.", "그가 그녀를 기다리고, 그녀를 보거나 못 보고, 그녀에 관해 이리저리 헤아리는 동안, 처음의 호기심은 날마다 커져 갔다."], ["Influenced by his predominant idea, he even fell into a habit of discussing with himself the possibility of her being in some way associated with it.", "그를 사로잡은 그 생각의 영향을 받아, 그는 심지어 그녀가 어떤 식으로든 그 일과 연관되어 있을 가능성을 혼자 따져 보는 버릇에까지 빠져들었다."], ["At last he resolved to watch Little Dorrit and know more of her story.", "마침내 그는 리틀 도릿을 지켜보아 그녀의 내력을 좀 더 알아내기로 결심했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 6. The Father of the Marshalsea", "# CHAPTER 6. The Father of the Marshalsea"]], [["Thirty years ago there stood, a few doors short of the church of Saint George, in the borough of Southwark, on the left-hand side of the way going southward, the Marshalsea Prison.", "삼십 년 전, 서더크 자치구의 세인트 조지 교회에서 몇 집 못 미친 곳, 남쪽으로 가는 길의 왼편에 마셜시 감옥이 서 있었다."], ["It had stood there many years before, and it remained there some years afterwards; but it is gone now, and the world is none the worse without it.", "그것은 그보다 여러 해 전부터 거기 서 있었고, 그 뒤로도 몇 해 더 그 자리에 남아 있었다. 그러나 지금은 사라졌고, 세상은 그것이 없다고 해서 조금도 더 나빠지지 않았다."]], [["It was an oblong pile of barrack building, partitioned into squalid houses standing back to back, so that there were no back rooms; environed by a narrow paved yard, hemmed in by high walls duly spiked at top.", "그것은 길쭉한 직사각형의 막사형 건물 덩어리로, 등을 맞댄 누추한 집들로 칸막이가 되어 있어 뒷방이라곤 없었으며, 좁다란 포석 깔린 마당이 둘러싸고, 꼭대기에 빠짐없이 못이 박힌 높은 담장이 에워싸고 있었다."], ["Itself a close and confined prison for debtors, it contained within it a much closer and more confined jail for smugglers.", "그 자체가 채무자들을 가두는 답답하고 비좁은 감옥이었지만, 그 안에는 밀수꾼들을 가두는 한층 더 답답하고 더 비좁은 감방이 들어 있었다."], ["Offenders against the revenue laws, and defaulters to excise or customs who had incurred fines which they were unable to pay, were supposed to be incarcerated behind an iron-plated door closing up a second prison, consisting of a strong cell or two, and a blind alley some yard and a half wide, which formed the mysterious termination of the very limited skittle-ground in which the Marshalsea debtors bowled down their troubles.", "세입법을 어긴 자들과, 물품세나 관세를 체납하여 갚을 수 없는 벌금을 떠안은 자들은, 철판을 댄 문 하나로 막아 놓은 두 번째 감옥 안에 갇히게 되어 있었다. 그 두 번째 감옥은 튼튼한 독방 한둘과, 한 야드 반쯤 너비의 막다른 골목으로 이루어져 있었는데, 그 골목은 마셜시의 채무자들이 자기네 시름을 굴려 쓰러뜨리던 그 지극히 비좁은 볼링장의 신비로운 끝자락을 이루고 있었다."]], [["Supposed to be incarcerated there, because the time had rather outgrown the strong cells and the blind alley.", "거기에 갇히게 되어 있었다고 한 것은, 세월이 그 튼튼한 독방과 막다른 골목을 이미 좀 넘어서 버렸기 때문이다."], ["In practice they had come to be considered a little too bad, though in theory they were quite as good as ever; which may be observed to be the case at the present day with other cells that are not at all strong, and with other blind alleys that are stone-blind.", "이론상으로야 그것들은 여전히 예전 못지않게 멀쩡했지만, 실제로는 좀 지나치게 험하다고 여겨지게 되었다. 이는 오늘날에도 도무지 튼튼하지 않은 다른 독방들과, 돌처럼 캄캄하게 막혀 버린 다른 막다른 골목들에서 똑같이 볼 수 있는 일이다."], ["Hence the smugglers habitually consorted with the debtors (who received them with open arms), except at certain constitutional moments when somebody came from some Office, to go through some form of overlooking something which neither he nor anybody else knew anything about.", "그리하여 밀수꾼들은 으레 채무자들과 어울려 지냈으며(채무자들은 그들을 두 팔 벌려 맞아 주었다), 다만 어느 관청에선가 누군가가 나와서, 자기도 다른 누구도 도무지 아무것도 알지 못하는 어떤 것을 감독한다는 어떤 형식을 갖추는 헌정적인 순간들만은 예외였다."], ["On these truly British occasions, the smugglers, if any, made a feint of walking into the strong cells and the blind alley, while this somebody pretended to do his something: and made a reality of walking out again as soon as he hadn’t done it--neatly epitomising the administration of most of the public affairs in our right little, tight little, island.", "이 참으로 영국적인 행사 때면, 밀수꾼이 있을 경우, 그 누군가가 자기의 어떤 것을 하는 척하는 동안 그들은 튼튼한 독방과 막다른 골목으로 걸어 들어가는 시늉을 하고는, 그자가 그것을 다 하지 않자마자 다시 걸어 나오는 일은 실제로 해치웠다 — 우리의 정연하고 단단한 작은 섬에서 대다수 공무가 처리되는 방식을 깔끔하게 요약해 보여 주듯이."]], [["There had been taken to the Marshalsea Prison, long before the day when the sun shone on Marseilles and on the opening of this narrative, a debtor with whom this narrative has some concern.", "해가 마르세유와 이 이야기의 첫머리를 비추던 그날보다 훨씬 전에, 이 이야기와 다소 관련 있는 한 채무자가 마셜시 감옥에 잡혀 들어와 있었다."]], [["He was, at that time, a very amiable and very helpless middle-aged gentleman, who was going out again directly.", "그는 그 무렵, 매우 상냥하고 매우 무력한 중년 신사로서, 곧장 다시 나갈 사람이었다."], ["Necessarily, he was going out again directly, because the Marshalsea lock never turned upon a debtor who was not.", "당연히 그는 곧장 다시 나갈 사람이었으니, 마셜시의 자물쇠가 곧장 다시 나가지 않을 채무자를 향해 돌아간 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다."], ["He brought in a portmanteau with him, which he doubted its being worth while to unpack; he was so perfectly clear--like all the rest of them, the turnkey on the lock said--that he was going out again directly.", "그는 여행 가방 하나를 들고 들어왔는데, 그것을 풀어 놓을 만한 값어치가 있을지 의심스러워했다. 그는 — 나머지 모두와 똑같다고 자물쇠를 지키는 간수가 말했듯이 — 곧장 다시 나갈 것이라고 너무도 확신하고 있었다."]], [["He was a shy, retiring man; well-looking, though in an effeminate style; with a mild voice, curling hair, and irresolute hands--rings upon the fingers in those days--which nervously wandered to his trembling lip a hundred times in the first half-hour of his acquaintance with the jail.", "그는 수줍고 내성적인 사내였다. 여성스러운 데가 있긴 했지만 잘생긴 편이었고, 부드러운 목소리에 곱슬머리, 그리고 우유부단한 두 손을 지녔는데 — 그 시절엔 손가락마다 반지를 끼고 있었다 — 그 손은 감옥과 첫 인연을 맺은 첫 삼십 분 동안 그의 떨리는 입술로 백 번도 넘게 초조하게 올라갔다."], ["His principal anxiety was about his wife.", "그의 가장 큰 근심은 아내에 관한 것이었다."]], [["‘Do you think, sir,’ he asked the turnkey, ‘that she will be very much shocked, if she should come to the gate to-morrow morning?’", "\"여보시오,\" 그가 간수에게 물었다. \"내 아내가 내일 아침 이 문 앞에 온다면, 몹시 충격을 받을 것 같소?\""]], [["The turnkey gave it as the result of his experience that some of ‘em was and some of ‘em wasn’t.", "간수는 자기 경험에 비추어 보면 어떤 이들은 그렇고 어떤 이들은 그렇지 않다고 대답했다. 대체로 그렇다는 쪽보다 아니라는 쪽이 더 많다고."], ["In general, more no than yes.", "\"그러니까 부인이 어떤 분이냐, 그게 문제죠.\""], ["‘What like is she, you see?’ he philosophically asked: ‘that’s what it hinges on.’", "그가 철학적으로 물었다. \"그게 다 거기 달린 거예요.\""]], [["‘She is very delicate and inexperienced indeed.’", "\"내 아내는 정말이지 무척 연약하고 세상물정에 어둡소.\""]], [["‘That,’ said the turnkey, ‘is agen her.’", "\"그건,\" 간수가 말했다. \"부인한테 불리하군요.\""]], [["‘She is so little used to go out alone,’ said the debtor, ‘that I am at a loss to think how she will ever make her way here, if she walks.’", "\"혼자 외출하는 데 워낙 익숙지 않은 사람이라,\" 채무자가 말했다. \"걸어서 온다면 도대체 어떻게 여기까지 길을 찾아올지 막막하오.\""]], [["‘P’raps,’ quoth the turnkey, ‘she’ll take a ackney coach.’", "\"그럼 뭐,\" 간수가 말했다. \"삯마차를 타시겠죠.\""]], [["‘Perhaps.’ The irresolute fingers went to the trembling lip.", "\"그럴지도.\" 우유부단한 손가락들이 떨리는 입술로 올라갔다."], ["‘I hope she will.", "\"그러기를 바라오."], ["She may not think of it.’", "그 생각을 못 할지도 모르지만.\""]], [["‘Or p’raps,’ said the turnkey, offering his suggestions from the the top of his well-worn wooden stool, as he might have offered them to a child for whose weakness he felt a compassion, ‘p’raps she’ll get her brother, or her sister, to come along with her.’", "\"아니면 뭐,\" 간수가, 자기가 가엾게 여기는 어떤 아이의 약함을 향해 일러 주듯, 닳고 닳은 나무 걸상 위에서 제안을 내놓았다. \"아니면 부인이 오빠나 언니라도 데리고 같이 오시겠죠.\""]], [["‘She has no brother or sister.’", "\"아내에겐 오빠도 언니도 없소.\""]], [["‘Niece, nevy, cousin, serwant, young ‘ooman, greengrocer.--Dash it!", "\"조카딸, 조카, 사촌, 하인, 젊은 여자, 채소 장수.— 에이!"], ["One or another on ‘em,’ said the turnkey, repudiating beforehand the refusal of all his suggestions.", "그중에 누구 하나라도 있겠죠.\" 간수가 자기 제안이 죄다 거절당할 것을 미리 물리치며 말했다."]], [["‘I fear--I hope it is not against the rules--that she will bring the children.’", "\"아무래도— 부디 규칙에 어긋나지 않기를 바라오만— 아내가 아이들을 데려올 것 같소.\""]], [["‘The children?’ said the turnkey.", "\"아이들이요?\" 간수가 말했다."], ["‘And the rules?", "\"규칙이라뇨?"], ["Why, lord set you up like a corner pin, we’ve a reg’lar playground o’ children here.", "아니, 영감님을 코너 핀처럼 떡하니 세워 드릴까, 여긴 아예 어엿한 아이들 놀이터예요."], ["Children!", "아이들이라뇨!"], ["Why we swarm with ‘em.", "아이들이 우글우글하다니까요."], ["How many a you got?’", "몇이나 두셨는데요?\""]], [["‘Two,’ said the debtor, lifting his irresolute hand to his lip again, and turning into the prison.", "\"둘이오.\" 채무자가 다시 우유부단한 손을 입술로 들어 올리며 말하고는 감옥 안으로 돌아섰다."]], [["The turnkey followed him with his eyes.", "간수는 눈으로 그를 좇았다. \"거기에 당신 하나,\""], ["‘And you another,’ he observed to himself, ‘which makes three on you.", "그가 혼잣말을 했다. \"해서 셋이라."], ["And your wife another, I’ll lay a crown.", "그리고 부인 하나 더, 일 크라운 걸지."], ["Which makes four on you.", "해서 넷."], ["And another coming, I’ll lay half-a-crown.", "그리고 또 하나 오는 중일 테고, 반 크라운 걸지."], ["Which’ll make five on you.", "해서 다섯."], ["And I’ll go another seven and sixpence to name which is the helplessest, the unborn baby or you!’", "그리고 칠 실링 육 펜스 더 걸어서 말해 보지, 누가 제일 무력한지를 — 아직 태어나지 않은 갓난애냐, 아니면 당신이냐!\""]], [["He was right in all his particulars.", "그는 모든 항목에서 옳았다."], ["She came next day with a little boy of three years old, and a little girl of two, and he stood entirely corroborated.", "이튿날 아내는 세 살배기 사내아이 하나와 두 살배기 여자아이 하나를 데리고 왔고, 그는 빈틈없이 입증된 채로 서 있었다."]], [["‘Got a room now; haven’t you?’ the turnkey asked the debtor after a week or two.", "\"이제 방은 구했죠, 안 그래요?\" 한두 주가 지난 뒤 간수가 채무자에게 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, I have got a very good room.’", "\"그렇소, 아주 좋은 방을 하나 얻었소.\""]], [["‘Any little sticks a coming to furnish it?’ said the turnkey.", "\"세간으로 들여놓을 자질구레한 살림은 좀 들어옵니까?\" 간수가 물었다."]], [["‘I expect a few necessary articles of furniture to be delivered by the carrier, this afternoon.’", "\"오늘 오후에 운송업자가 필요한 가구 몇 점을 배달해 주기로 했소.\""]], [["‘Missis and little ‘uns a coming to keep you company?’ asked the turnkey.", "\"부인하고 어린것들도 함께 지내러 옵니까?\" 간수가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, yes, we think it better that we should not be scattered, even for a few weeks.’", "\"그렇소, 몇 주만이라도 흩어져 지내지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어서.\""]], [["‘Even for a few weeks, _of_ course,’ replied the turnkey.", "\"몇 주만이라도, 그야 당연하죠.\" 간수가 대답했다."], ["And he followed him again with his eyes, and nodded his head seven times when he was gone.", "그리고 그가 가 버리자 다시 눈으로 그를 좇으며 일곱 번 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["The affairs of this debtor were perplexed by a partnership, of which he knew no more than that he had invested money in it; by legal matters of assignment and settlement, conveyance here and conveyance there, suspicion of unlawful preference of creditors in this direction, and of mysterious spiriting away of property in that; and as nobody on the face of the earth could be more incapable of explaining any single item in the heap of confusion than the debtor himself, nothing comprehensible could be made of his case.", "이 채무자의 사정은 어떤 동업 관계 때문에 뒤엉켜 있었는데, 그는 거기에 돈을 댔다는 것밖에는 아는 게 없었다. 양도와 정산, 이쪽으로의 권리 이전과 저쪽으로의 권리 이전이라는 법률적인 일들, 이쪽 방향으로는 채권자를 부당하게 편애했다는 의혹, 저쪽 방향으로는 재산을 몰래 빼돌렸다는 의혹 따위로 얽혀 있었다."], ["To question him in detail, and endeavour to reconcile his answers; to closet him with accountants and sharp practitioners, learned in the wiles of insolvency and bankruptcy; was only to put the case out at compound interest and incomprehensibility.", "그리고 이 세상 어느 누구도 채무자 본인보다 그 혼란의 더미 가운데 단 한 항목이라도 설명할 능력이 더 없을 수는 없었으므로, 그의 사건으로는 도무지 알아들을 만한 것을 만들어 낼 수가 없었다. 그를 붙들고 시시콜콜 캐묻고 그의 대답을 서로 맞춰 보려 하는 것, 파산과 도산의 술수에 밝은 회계사들과 약삭빠른 전문가들과 그를 한방에 가둬 두는 것은, 그저 그 사건을 복리로 불어나는 불가해함에 내맡기는 것일 뿐이었다."], ["The irresolute fingers fluttered more and more ineffectually about the trembling lip on every such occasion, and the sharpest practitioners gave him up as a hopeless job.", "그럴 때마다 우유부단한 손가락들은 떨리는 입술 언저리를 점점 더 부질없이 파닥거렸고, 가장 약삭빠른 전문가들마저 그를 가망 없는 일감으로 단념해 버렸다."]], [["‘Out?’ said the turnkey, ‘_he_‘ll never get out, unless his creditors take him by the shoulders and shove him out.’", "\"나간다고요?\" 간수가 말했다. \"저 양반은 채권자들이 어깨를 붙잡아 떠밀어 내지 않는 한, 절대로 못 나가요.\""]], [["He had been there five or six months, when he came running to this turnkey one forenoon to tell him, breathless and pale, that his wife was ill.", "그가 거기 들어온 지 대여섯 달이 되었을 무렵, 어느 날 오전 그는 이 간수에게 숨을 헐떡이며 새파랗게 질려 달려와 아내가 아프다고 알렸다."]], [["‘As anybody might a known she would be,’ said the turnkey.", "\"누가 봐도 그럴 줄 알았죠.\" 간수가 말했다."]], [["‘We intended,’ he returned, ‘that she should go to a country lodging only to-morrow.", "\"우리는,\" 그가 대답했다."], ["What am I to do!", "\"바로 내일 아내를 시골 하숙으로 보낼 작정이었소."], ["Oh, good heaven, what am I to do!’", "난 어쩌면 좋소! 아아, 맙소사, 난 어쩌면 좋단 말이오!\""]], [["‘Don’t waste your time in clasping your hands and biting your fingers,’ responded the practical turnkey, taking him by the elbow, ‘but come along with me.’", "\"두 손 비비고 손가락이나 깨물며 시간 낭비하지 말고,\" 실무적인 간수가 그의 팔꿈치를 붙들며 대꾸했다. \"나랑 같이 갑시다.\""]], [["The turnkey conducted him--trembling from head to foot, and constantly crying under his breath, What was he to do! while his irresolute fingers bedabbled the tears upon his face--up one of the common staircases in the prison to a door on the garret story.", "간수는 그를 — 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고, 우유부단한 손가락으로 얼굴에 흐르는 눈물을 문질러 대며, 끊임없이 낮은 소리로 난 어쩌면 좋으냐고 흐느끼는 그를 — 감옥의 흔한 계단 하나를 따라 다락층의 어느 문 앞까지 데려갔다."], ["Upon which door the turnkey knocked with the handle of his key.", "간수는 그 문을 열쇠 손잡이로 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in!’ cried a voice inside.", "\"들어와!\" 안에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["The turnkey, opening the door, disclosed in a wretched, ill-smelling little room, two hoarse, puffy, red-faced personages seated at a rickety table, playing at all-fours, smoking pipes, and drinking brandy.", "간수가 문을 열자, 누추하고 악취 나는 작은 방 안에 쉰 목소리에 부석부석하고 벌건 얼굴을 한 두 인물이 흔들거리는 탁자에 마주 앉아 올포어즈 노름을 하며 파이프를 피우고 브랜디를 마시고 있는 것이 드러났다."]], [["‘Doctor,’ said the turnkey, ‘here’s a gentleman’s wife in want of you without a minute’s loss of time!’", "\"선생,\" 간수가 말했다. \"여기 한 신사분의 부인이 일 분도 지체 없이 선생을 찾고 있소!\""]], [["The doctor’s friend was in the positive degree of hoarseness, puffiness, red-facedness, all-fours, tobacco, dirt, and brandy; the doctor in the comparative--hoarser, puffier, more red-faced, more all-fourey, tobaccoer, dirtier, and brandier.", "의사의 친구는 쉰 목소리, 부석부석함, 벌건 얼굴, 올포어즈, 담배, 때, 브랜디에 있어 원급(原級)이었고, 의사는 비교급이었다 — 더 쉰 목소리, 더 부석부석함, 더 벌건 얼굴, 더 올포어즈에 절고, 더 담배에 절고, 더 때투성이에, 더 브랜디에 전."], ["The doctor was amazingly shabby, in a torn and darned rough-weather sea-jacket, out at elbows and eminently short of buttons (he had been in his time the experienced surgeon carried by a passenger ship), the dirtiest white trousers conceivable by mortal man, carpet slippers, and no visible linen.", "의사는 놀랄 만큼 초라한 행색이었으니, 팔꿈치가 다 해지고 단추가 눈에 띄게 모자란, 찢어지고 기운 거친 날씨용 선원 외투를 걸치고, 인간으로서 상상할 수 있는 가장 더러운 흰 바지에, 양탄자 슬리퍼를 신고, 셔츠라곤 보이지 않았다(그는 한때 여객선에 실려 다니는 노련한 외과의였던 적이 있었다)."], ["‘Childbed?’ said the doctor.", "\"산통이라?\" 의사가 말했다."], ["‘I’m the boy!’ With that the doctor took a comb from the chimney-piece and stuck his hair upright--which appeared to be his way of washing himself--produced a professional chest or case, of most abject appearance, from the cupboard where his cup and saucer and coals were, settled his chin in the frowsy wrapper round his neck, and became a ghastly medical scarecrow.", "\"그거라면 내가 적임자지!\" 그러면서 의사는 벽난로 선반에서 빗을 집어 머리카락을 빳빳이 곧추세웠는데 — 이것이 그가 몸을 씻는 방식인 듯했다 — 컵과 받침접시와 석탄을 넣어 둔 찬장에서 더없이 비참한 몰골의 직업용 상자, 즉 왕진 가방을 꺼내고, 목에 두른 후줄근한 천 속에 턱을 파묻고는, 끔찍한 의료용 허수아비가 되었다."]], [["The doctor and the debtor ran down-stairs, leaving the turnkey to return to the lock, and made for the debtor’s room.", "의사와 채무자는 계단을 뛰어 내려가, 간수를 자물쇠 자리로 돌려보내고는 채무자의 방으로 향했다."], ["All the ladies in the prison had got hold of the news, and were in the yard.", "감옥 안의 부인들은 죄다 그 소식을 입수하여 마당에 나와 있었다."], ["Some of them had already taken possession of the two children, and were hospitably carrying them off; others were offering loans of little comforts from their own scanty store; others were sympathising with the greatest volubility.", "그중 일부는 이미 두 아이를 차지하여 살갑게 데려가는 중이었고, 다른 이들은 제 빈약한 살림에서 자그마한 위안거리를 빌려주겠노라 내놓고 있었으며, 또 다른 이들은 더없이 수다스럽게 동정을 표하고 있었다."], ["The gentlemen prisoners, feeling themselves at a disadvantage, had for the most part retired, not to say sneaked, to their rooms; from the open windows of which some of them now complimented the doctor with whistles as he passed below, while others, with several stories between them, interchanged sarcastic references to the prevalent excitement.", "신사 죄수들은 자기들이 불리한 처지임을 느끼고 대개 자기 방으로 물러갔다고, 아니 슬그머니 빠져들었다고 해야겠는데, 그중 몇은 열린 창문에서 아래를 지나가는 의사에게 휘파람으로 인사를 보냈고, 다른 몇은 여러 층을 사이에 두고 지금 만연한 흥분에 대해 비꼬는 말들을 주고받았다."]], [["It was a hot summer day, and the prison rooms were baking between the high walls.", "무더운 여름날이라, 감옥의 방들은 높은 담장 사이에서 푹푹 익고 있었다."], ["In the debtor’s confined chamber, Mrs Bangham, charwoman and messenger, who was not a prisoner (though she had been once), but was the popular medium of communication with the outer world, had volunteered her services as fly-catcher and general attendant.", "채무자의 비좁은 방에서는, 죄수는 아니지만(한때는 죄수였던 적이 있다) 바깥세상과 통하는 인기 있는 매개자였던 잡역부 겸 심부름꾼 뱅엄 부인이, 파리잡이 겸 만능 시중꾼으로 자청해 나서 있었다."], ["The walls and ceiling were blackened with flies.", "벽과 천장이 파리로 시커멨다."], ["Mrs Bangham, expert in sudden device, with one hand fanned the patient with a cabbage leaf, and with the other set traps of vinegar and sugar in gallipots; at the same time enunciating sentiments of an encouraging and congratulatory nature, adapted to the occasion.", "뱅엄 부인은 임기응변에 능한지라, 한 손으로는 양배추 잎으로 산모에게 부채질을 하고, 다른 손으로는 약단지에 식초와 설탕으로 덫을 놓았다. 동시에 그 상황에 맞춰 격려와 축하의 정서를 담은 말들을 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘The flies trouble you, don’t they, my dear?’ said Mrs Bangham.", "\"파리가 성가시죠, 안 그래요, 아가씨?\" 뱅엄 부인이 말했다."], ["‘But p’raps they’ll take your mind off of it, and do you good.", "\"하지만 어쩌면 그게 아가씨 정신을 딴 데로 돌려서 도움이 될지도 몰라요."], ["What between the buryin ground, the grocer’s, the waggon-stables, and the paunch trade, the Marshalsea flies gets very large.", "묘지에다, 식료품 가게에다, 짐마차 마구간에다, 내장 장사까지 있다 보니 마셜시 파리는 무척 크게 자란다니까요."], ["P’raps they’re sent as a consolation, if we only know’d it.", "우리가 모르고 있을 뿐, 어쩌면 위안으로 보내진 건지도 모르죠."], ["How are you now, my dear?", "지금은 좀 어때요, 아가씨?"], ["No better?", "나아지지 않았어요?"], ["No, my dear, it ain’t to be expected; you’ll be worse before you’re better, and you know it, don’t you?", "그래요, 아가씨, 그럴 리가 없죠. 나아지기 전에 더 나빠질 거예요, 그건 아가씨도 알죠, 안 그래요?"], ["Yes.", "그럼요."], ["That’s right!", "바로 그거예요!"], ["And to think of a sweet little cherub being born inside the lock!", "자물쇠 안에서 어여쁜 어린 천사가 태어난다고 생각해 봐요!"], ["Now ain’t it pretty, ain’t _that_ something to carry you through it pleasant?", "아니, 그거 예쁘지 않아요, 그걸로 즐겁게 이 고비를 넘길 만하지 않아요?"], ["Why, we ain’t had such a thing happen here, my dear, not for I couldn’t name the time when.", "아니, 여기서 이런 일이 일어난 게 말이죠, 아가씨, 내가 언제였는지 댈 수도 없을 만큼 오래됐다니까요."], ["And you a crying too?’ said Mrs Bangham, to rally the patient more and more.", "게다가 울기까지 해요?\" 뱅엄 부인이 산모를 점점 더 다독이려고 말했다."], ["‘You! Making yourself so famous!", "\"아가씨가요!"], ["With the flies a falling into the gallipots by fifties!", "이렇게 유명해지면서! 약단지에 파리가 쉰 마리씩 떨어지는 판에!"], ["And everything a going on so well!", "모든 게 이렇게 잘 풀리는데! 그리고 봐요, 저기,\""], ["And here if there ain’t,’ said Mrs Bangham as the door opened, ‘if there ain’t your dear gentleman along with Dr Haggage!", "문이 열리자 뱅엄 부인이 말했다. \"저기 아가씨의 다정한 신사 양반이 해기지 선생님하고 같이 오시잖아요!"], ["And now indeed we _are_ complete, I _think_!’", "자, 이제 정말로 우리가 다 갖춰진 것 같네요!\""]], [["The doctor was scarcely the kind of apparition to inspire a patient with a sense of absolute completeness, but as he presently delivered the opinion, ‘We are as right as we can be, Mrs Bangham, and we shall come out of this like a house afire;’ and as he and Mrs Bangham took possession of the poor helpless pair, as everybody else and anybody else had always done, the means at hand were as good on the whole as better would have been.", "의사는 산모에게 완벽하게 다 갖춰졌다는 느낌을 불어넣어 줄 만한 모습은 도무지 아니었다. 그러나 그가 곧 \"우리는 더할 나위 없이 괜찮습니다, 뱅엄 부인, 이건 불난 집처럼 단숨에 해치울 겁니다\"라는 의견을 내놓았고, 또 다른 누구나가, 아무나가 늘 그래 왔듯이 그와 뱅엄 부인이 그 가엾고 무력한 두 사람을 떠맡았으므로, 손에 닿는 수단은 전체적으로 보아 더 나은 것이 있었다 한들 그만큼은 충분히 괜찮았다."], ["The special feature in Dr Haggage’s treatment of the case, was his determination to keep Mrs Bangham up to the mark.", "해기지 선생이 이 사건을 다루는 데서 특별한 점은, 뱅엄 부인을 정신 똑바로 차리게 다잡으려는 그의 결의였다."], ["As thus:", "이런 식이었다."]], [["‘Mrs Bangham,’ said the doctor, before he had been there twenty minutes, ‘go outside and fetch a little brandy, or we shall have you giving in.’", "\"뱅엄 부인,\" 그가 거기 온 지 이십 분도 채 되지 않아 말했다. \"밖에 나가서 브랜디를 좀 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 쓰러지겠어.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님."], ["But none on my accounts,’ said Mrs Bangham.", "하지만 저 때문이라면 됐어요.\" 뱅엄 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Mrs Bangham,’ returned the doctor, ‘I am in professional attendance on this lady, and don’t choose to allow any discussion on your part.", "\"뱅엄 부인,\" 의사가 대꾸했다."], ["Go outside and fetch a little brandy, or I foresee that you’ll break down.’", "\"나는 지금 이 부인을 직업적으로 보살피는 중이고, 당신 쪽에서 왈가왈부하는 건 허락하지 않겠어. 밖에 나가서 브랜디를 좀 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 무너질 게 내 눈엔 훤히 보이니까.\""]], [["‘You’re to be obeyed, sir,’ said Mrs Bangham, rising.", "\"분부대로 따라야죠, 선생님.\" 뱅엄 부인이 일어서며 말했다."], ["‘If you was to put your own lips to it, I think you wouldn’t be the worse, for you look but poorly, sir.’", "\"선생님께서 직접 입에 대신다 해도 나쁠 건 없지 싶네요, 안색이 영 안 좋아 보이시는걸요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Mrs Bangham,’ returned the doctor, ‘I am not your business, thank you, but you are mine.", "\"뱅엄 부인,\" 의사가 대꾸했다."], ["Never you mind _me_, if you please.", "\"나는 당신이 상관할 바가 아니야, 고맙지만. 당신이 내가 상관할 바지."], ["What you have got to do, is, to do as you are told, and to go and get what I bid you.’", "제발 나는 신경 쓰지 마. 당신이 할 일은, 시키는 대로 하는 거고, 가서 내가 시킨 걸 가져오는 거야.\""]], [["Mrs Bangham submitted; and the doctor, having administered her potion, took his own.", "뱅엄 부인은 굴복했다. 그리고 의사는 그녀에게 약을 먹인 뒤, 자기 몫도 들이켰다."], ["He repeated the treatment every hour, being very determined with Mrs Bangham.", "그는 뱅엄 부인에게 아주 단호한 태도로, 한 시간마다 그 처치를 되풀이했다."], ["Three or four hours passed; the flies fell into the traps by hundreds; and at length one little life, hardly stronger than theirs, appeared among the multitude of lesser deaths.", "서너 시간이 흘렀고, 파리는 수백 마리씩 덫에 떨어졌으며, 마침내 그것들의 목숨보다 별로 더 강하지도 않은 한 작은 생명이 그 숱한 작은 죽음들 한가운데에 나타났다."]], [["‘A very nice little girl indeed,’ said the doctor; ‘little, but well-formed.", "\"정말 아주 예쁜 여자아이로군요.\" 의사가 말했다."], ["Halloa, Mrs Bangham!", "\"작긴 해도 잘생겼어요."], ["You’re looking queer!", "어어, 뱅엄 부인!"], ["You be off, ma’am, this minute, and fetch a little more brandy, or we shall have you in hysterics.’", "안색이 이상한데! 당장 나가요, 부인, 어서, 브랜디를 좀 더 가져와요, 안 그러면 당신이 히스테리를 일으키겠어.\""]], [["By this time, the rings had begun to fall from the debtor’s irresolute hands, like leaves from a wintry tree.", "이 무렵이면 반지들이 겨울나무에서 잎이 지듯 채무자의 우유부단한 손에서 떨어지기 시작했다."], ["Not one was left upon them that night, when he put something that chinked into the doctor’s greasy palm.", "그가 짤랑거리는 무언가를 의사의 기름때 낀 손바닥에 쥐여 주던 그날 밤에는, 그 손에 반지가 단 하나도 남아 있지 않았다."], ["In the meantime Mrs Bangham had been out on an errand to a neighbouring establishment decorated with three golden balls, where she was very well known.", "그동안 뱅엄 부인은 금빛 공 세 개로 장식된 이웃의 어느 가게로 심부름을 다녀왔는데, 거기서 그녀는 꽤나 잘 알려진 사람이었다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ said the doctor, ‘thank you.", "\"고맙습니다.\" 의사가 말했다."], ["Your good lady is quite composed.", "\"고마워요. 부인은 아주 평온해졌어요."], ["Doing charmingly.’", "더없이 잘하고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I am very happy and very thankful to know it,’ said the debtor, ‘though I little thought once, that--’", "\"그 말을 들으니 무척 기쁘고 무척 감사하오.\" 채무자가 말했다. \"한때는 꿈에도 생각지 못했소만, 그러니까—\""]], [["‘That a child would be born to you in a place like this?’ said the doctor.", "\"이런 곳에서 아이가 태어나리라곤 말이지요?\" 의사가 말했다."], ["‘Bah, bah, sir, what does it signify?", "\"쳇, 쳇, 여보시오, 그게 무슨 상관이오?"], ["A little more elbow-room is all we want here.", "여기서 우리한테 필요한 건 좀 더 넉넉한 팔꿈치 공간뿐이오. 여긴 조용하지요."], ["We are quiet here; we don’t get badgered here; there’s no knocker here, sir, to be hammered at by creditors and bring a man’s heart into his mouth.", "여기선 들볶이지 않소. 채권자들이 두들겨 대서 사람 심장이 입 밖으로 튀어나오게 만드는 문고리 따위는 여기 없소, 여보시오."], ["Nobody comes here to ask if a man’s at home, and to say he’ll stand on the door mat till he is.", "사람이 집에 있는지 물으러 와서, 있을 때까지 문 앞 발판에 버티고 서 있겠다고 하는 자도 여긴 안 오지요."], ["Nobody writes threatening letters about money to this place.", "이런 데로는 돈 갚으라는 협박 편지도 안 날아오고요."], ["It’s freedom, sir, it’s freedom!", "이게 자유요, 여보시오, 이게 자유란 말이오!"], ["I have had to-day’s practice at home and abroad, on a march, and aboard ship, and I’ll tell you this: I don’t know that I have ever pursued it under such quiet circumstances as here this day.", "나는 오늘 같은 진료를 집에서도, 외국에서도, 행군 중에도, 배 위에서도 해 봤지만, 한마디 하리다. 오늘 여기처럼 이토록 평온한 상황에서 진료를 해 본 적이 있었나 싶소."], ["Elsewhere, people are restless, worried, hurried about, anxious respecting one thing, anxious respecting another.", "다른 데선 사람들이 안절부절못하고, 시달리고, 정신없이 쫓기고, 이것 때문에 노심초사, 저것 때문에 노심초사하지요. 여긴 그런 게 전혀 없소, 여보시오."], ["Nothing of the kind here, sir.", "우리는 그걸 다 겪었소 — 최악이 뭔지 우리는 알지요."], ["We have done all that--we know the worst of it; we have got to the bottom, we can’t fall, and what have we found?", "우리는 바닥까지 내려왔으니 더는 떨어질 수가 없소. 그래서 무엇을 찾았느냐?"], ["Peace.", "평화요."], ["That’s the word for it.", "그게 바로 그 말이오."], ["Peace.’ With this profession of faith, the doctor, who was an old jail-bird, and was more sodden than usual, and had the additional and unusual stimulus of money in his pocket, returned to his associate and chum in hoarseness, puffiness, red-facedness, all-fours, tobacco, dirt, and brandy.", "평화.\" 이런 신앙 고백과 함께, 늙은 감옥 단골이자 여느 때보다 더 거나하게 취하고, 게다가 주머니에 든 돈이라는 색다른 자극까지 더해진 의사는, 쉰 목소리, 부석부석함, 벌건 얼굴, 올포어즈, 담배, 때, 그리고 브랜디에 있어 자기 동료이자 단짝에게로 돌아갔다."]], [["Now, the debtor was a very different man from the doctor, but he had already begun to travel, by his opposite segment of the circle, to the same point.", "그런데 채무자는 의사와는 전혀 다른 사람이었지만, 그 역시 원의 반대편 호(弧)를 따라 같은 지점을 향해 이미 나아가기 시작한 터였다."], ["Crushed at first by his imprisonment, he had soon found a dull relief in it.", "처음엔 수감 생활에 짓눌렸으나, 그는 곧 거기서 둔한 위안을 발견했다."], ["He was under lock and key; but the lock and key that kept him in, kept numbers of his troubles out.", "그는 자물쇠와 열쇠에 갇혀 있었지만, 그를 안에 가두는 그 자물쇠와 열쇠가 그의 수많은 골칫거리를 밖에 가둬 두었던 것이다."], ["If he had been a man with strength of purpose to face those troubles and fight them, he might have broken the net that held him, or broken his heart; but being what he was, he languidly slipped into this smooth descent, and never more took one step upward.", "그가 만약 그 골칫거리들을 마주하고 맞서 싸울 만한 굳센 의지를 지닌 사람이었다면, 자기를 옭아맨 그물을 찢어 버렸거나 아니면 가슴이 터져 버렸을 테지만, 그는 본디 그런 사람이었기에 이 매끄러운 내리막으로 맥없이 미끄러져 들어갔고, 다시는 위로 한 걸음도 내딛지 못했다."]], [["When he was relieved of the perplexed affairs that nothing would make plain, through having them returned upon his hands by a dozen agents in succession who could make neither beginning, middle, nor end of them or him, he found his miserable place of refuge a quieter refuge than it had been before.", "도무지 분명해질 줄 모르는 그 뒤엉킨 사건들이, 그 일의 처음도 중간도 끝도, 또 그라는 사람도 도무지 종잡을 수 없었던 열두 명 남짓의 대리인들 손을 줄줄이 거쳐 다시 그의 손에 떠넘겨짐으로써 그가 그 짐에서 벗어나자, 그는 자기의 그 비참한 도피처가 전보다 한결 더 고요한 도피처임을 알게 되었다."], ["He had unpacked the portmanteau long ago; and his elder children now played regularly about the yard, and everybody knew the baby, and claimed a kind of proprietorship in her.", "그는 진작에 여행 가방을 풀어 놓았고, 위의 두 아이는 이제 마당에서 으레 뛰놀았으며, 누구나 그 갓난아이를 알았고 그 아이에게 일종의 소유권을 주장했다."]], [["‘Why, I’m getting proud of you,’ said his friend the turnkey, one day.", "\"아니, 내가 영감님이 자랑스러워지네요.\" 어느 날 그의 벗인 간수가 말했다."], ["‘You’ll be the oldest inhabitant soon.", "\"곧 가장 오래된 주민이 되시겠어요."], ["The Marshalsea wouldn’t be like the Marshalsea now, without you and your family.’", "영감님과 식구들이 없으면 이제 마셜시는 마셜시 같지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["The turnkey really was proud of him.", "간수는 정말로 그를 자랑스러워했다."], ["He would mention him in laudatory terms to new-comers, when his back was turned.", "그가 등을 돌리고 있을 때면 새로 들어온 자들에게 그를 칭찬조로 입에 올리곤 했다."], ["‘You took notice of him,’ he would say, ‘that went out of the lodge just now?’", "\"저기 방금 수위실에서 나간 분, 눈여겨봤어요?\" 그가 묻곤 했다."]], [["New-comer would probably answer Yes.", "새로 들어온 자는 십중팔구 그렇다고 대답하곤 했다."]], [["‘Brought up as a gentleman, he was, if ever a man was.", "\"신사로 자라난 분이라니까요, 세상에 진짜 신사가 있다면 말이죠."], ["Ed’cated at no end of expense.", "엄청난 돈을 들여 교육받았고요."], ["Went into the Marshal’s house once to try a new piano for him.", "한번은 마셜이 새 피아노를 시험해 보라고 그분을 자기 집으로 들였지요."], ["Played it, I understand, like one o’clock--beautiful!", "듣자 하니 그걸 기막히게 치셨대요 — 아름답게!"], ["As to languages--speaks anything.", "언어로 말하자면 — 뭐든 다 하시지요."], ["We’ve had a Frenchman here in his time, and it’s my opinion he knowed more French than the Frenchman did.", "한때 여기 프랑스 사람이 하나 있었는데, 내 보기엔 그분이 그 프랑스 사람보다 프랑스 말을 더 많이 알았어요."], ["We’ve had an Italian here in his time, and he shut _him_ up in about half a minute.", "한때 여기 이탈리아 사람도 하나 있었는데, 그분이 그자 입을 반 분 만에 막아 버리셨죠."], ["You’ll find some characters behind other locks, I don’t say you won’t; but if you want the top sawyer in such respects as I’ve mentioned, you must come to the Marshalsea.’", "다른 자물쇠 뒤에도 별난 인물들이 있겠지요, 없다곤 안 합니다. 하지만 내가 말한 그런 점에서 으뜸가는 거물을 원한다면, 마셜시로 와야 한다니까요.\""]], [["When his youngest child was eight years old, his wife, who had long been languishing away--of her own inherent weakness, not that she retained any greater sensitiveness as to her place of abode than he did--went upon a visit to a poor friend and old nurse in the country, and died there.", "그의 막내가 여덟 살이 되었을 때, 오래도록 시들시들 쇠약해져 가던 아내가 — 거처에 대한 민감함을 그보다 더 간직하고 있어서가 아니라, 본디 타고난 허약함 탓에 — 시골에 사는 가난한 친구이자 옛 유모를 찾아갔다가 거기서 죽었다."], ["He remained shut up in his room for a fortnight afterwards; and an attorney’s clerk, who was going through the Insolvent Court, engrossed an address of condolence to him, which looked like a Lease, and which all the prisoners signed.", "그는 그 뒤로 두 주 동안 방에 틀어박혀 지냈다. 그리고 파산 법정 절차를 밟고 있던 어느 변호사 사무원이 그에게 보내는 조문 인사문을 정서해 주었는데, 그것은 마치 임대차 계약서처럼 보였으며, 죄수들이 모두 거기에 서명했다."], ["When he appeared again he was greyer (he had soon begun to turn grey); and the turnkey noticed that his hands went often to his trembling lips again, as they had used to do when he first came in.", "그가 다시 모습을 드러냈을 때 그는 더 백발이 되어 있었고(그는 일찌감치 머리가 세기 시작했다), 간수는 그가 처음 들어왔을 때 그러했듯 그의 손이 다시 자주 떨리는 입술로 올라가는 것을 알아챘다."], ["But he got pretty well over it in a month or two; and in the meantime the children played about the yard as regularly as ever, but in black.", "그러나 그는 한두 달 만에 꽤 잘 추슬렀고, 그동안 아이들은 여느 때처럼 으레 마당에서 뛰놀았지만 검은 상복 차림이었다."]], [["Then Mrs Bangham, long popular medium of communication with the outer world, began to be infirm, and to be found oftener than usual comatose on pavements, with her basket of purchases spilt, and the change of her clients ninepence short.", "그러고 나서, 오랫동안 바깥세상과 통하는 인기 있는 매개자였던 뱅엄 부인이 쇠약해지기 시작하더니, 여느 때보다 자주 사 온 물건이 담긴 바구니를 엎질러 놓은 채 보도 위에 정신을 잃고 쓰러져 있는 것이 발견되었고, 의뢰인들에게 거슬러 줄 돈이 구 펜스나 모자라곤 했다."], ["His son began to supersede Mrs Bangham, and to execute commissions in a knowing manner, and to be of the prison prisonous, of the streets streety.", "그의 아들이 뱅엄 부인을 대신하기 시작하여, 빈틈없는 솜씨로 심부름을 해내고, 감옥의 것은 감옥답게, 거리의 것은 거리답게 익혀 가게 되었다."]], [["Time went on, and the turnkey began to fail.", "세월이 흘러, 간수가 쇠해 가기 시작했다."], ["His chest swelled, and his legs got weak, and he was short of breath.", "가슴이 부풀고, 다리가 약해지고, 숨이 가빠졌다."], ["The well-worn wooden stool was ‘beyond him,’ he complained.", "닳고 닳은 그 나무 걸상이 \"이제 자기에겐 벅차다\"고 그는 푸념했다."], ["He sat in an arm-chair with a cushion, and sometimes wheezed so, for minutes together, that he couldn’t turn the key.", "그는 방석을 깐 안락의자에 앉았고, 때로는 몇 분이고 내리 어찌나 쌕쌕거리는지 열쇠를 돌리지 못할 지경이었다."], ["When he was overpowered by these fits, the debtor often turned it for him.", "이런 발작에 짓눌릴 때면 채무자가 그를 대신해 자주 열쇠를 돌려 주었다."]], [["‘You and me,’ said the turnkey, one snowy winter’s night when the lodge, with a bright fire in it, was pretty full of company, ‘is the oldest inhabitants.", "\"당신하고 나는,\" 어느 눈 내리는 겨울밤, 환한 불을 피운 수위실에 사람들이 꽤 가득 들어차 있을 때 간수가 말했다."], ["I wasn’t here myself above seven year before you.", "\"가장 오래된 주민이지요. 나도 당신보다 칠 년쯤밖에 더 먼저 들어오지 않았으니까요."], ["I shan’t last long.", "난 오래 못 갈 거요."], ["When I’m off the lock for good and all, you’ll be the Father of the Marshalsea.’", "내가 이 자물쇠를 영영 떠나면, 당신이 마셜시의 아버지가 되는 거요.\""]], [["The turnkey went off the lock of this world next day.", "간수는 이튿날 이 세상의 자물쇠를 떠났다."], ["His words were remembered and repeated; and tradition afterwards handed down from generation to generation--a Marshalsea generation might be calculated as about three months--that the shabby old debtor with the soft manner and the white hair, was the Father of the Marshalsea.", "그의 말은 기억되고 되풀이되었다. 그리고 그 뒤로 대대로 — 마셜시의 한 세대는 석 달쯤으로 헤아릴 만했다 — 전해 내려온 전설이, 부드러운 태도에 백발을 지닌 그 초라한 늙은 채무자가 곧 마셜시의 아버지라는 것이었다."]], [["And he grew to be proud of the title.", "그리고 그는 그 칭호를 자랑스러워하게 되었다."], ["If any impostor had arisen to claim it, he would have shed tears in resentment of the attempt to deprive him of his rights.", "만약 어떤 사칭꾼이 나타나 그 칭호를 차지하려 들었다면, 그는 자기 권리를 빼앗으려는 그 시도에 분개하여 눈물을 흘렸을 것이다. 그에게는 자기가 거기 머문 햇수를 부풀리는 기색이 보이기 시작했다."], ["A disposition began to be perceived in him to exaggerate the number of years he had been there; it was generally understood that you must deduct a few from his account; he was vain, the fleeting generations of debtors said.", "그가 셈하는 햇수에서 몇 해쯤은 빼야 한다는 것이 일반적인 통념이었다. 그는 허영심이 있다고, 덧없이 스쳐 가는 채무자 세대들은 말했다."]], [["All new-comers were presented to him.", "새로 들어온 자들은 모두 그에게 소개되었다."], ["He was punctilious in the exaction of this ceremony.", "그는 이 의식의 시행에 깐깐했다."], ["The wits would perform the office of introduction with overcharged pomp and politeness, but they could not easily overstep his sense of its gravity.", "익살꾼들은 과장된 격식과 예의를 갖춰 소개의 임무를 수행하곤 했지만, 그것이 지닌 엄숙함에 대한 그의 감각을 좀처럼 넘어서지는 못했다."], ["He received them in his poor room (he disliked an introduction in the mere yard, as informal--a thing that might happen to anybody), with a kind of bowed-down beneficence.", "그는 자기의 누추한 방에서 그들을 맞았으며(그저 마당에서 소개받는 것은 격식에 어긋난다 하여 — 누구에게나 일어날 수 있는 일이라 하여 — 그는 싫어했다), 일종의 허리 굽힌 자비로움으로 그들을 대했다."], ["They were welcome to the Marshalsea, he would tell them.", "마셜시에 잘 오셨소, 하고 그는 그들에게 말하곤 했다."], ["Yes, he was the Father of the place.", "그렇소, 자기가 이곳의 아버지요."], ["So the world was kind enough to call him; and so he was, if more than twenty years of residence gave him a claim to the title.", "세상이 친절하게도 자기를 그렇게 불러 준다고. 그리고 이십 년이 넘는 거주가 그 칭호에 대한 자격을 준다면, 자기는 정말 그러하다고."], ["It looked small at first, but there was very good company there--among a mixture--necessarily a mixture--and very good air.", "처음엔 보잘것없어 보이지만, 여기엔 — 잡다한 사람들 가운데 — 부득이 잡다할 수밖에 없지만 — 무척 좋은 친구들이 있고, 공기도 무척 좋다고."]], [["It became a not unusual circumstance for letters to be put under his door at night, enclosing half-a-crown, two half-crowns, now and then at long intervals even half-a-sovereign, for the Father of the Marshalsea.", "밤에 마셜시의 아버지 앞으로 반 크라운이나 두 닢의 반 크라운, 이따금 아주 오랜만에 반 소버린까지도 동봉한 편지가 그의 문 아래로 들이밀어지는 것은 드문 일이 아니게 되었다."], ["‘With the compliments of a collegian taking leave.’ He received the gifts as tributes, from admirers, to a public character.", "\"작별을 고하는 한 동문(同門) 올림.\" 그는 이 선물들을, 한 공인(公人)에게 바치는 흠모자들의 헌사로 받아들였다."], ["Sometimes these correspondents assumed facetious names, as the Brick, Bellows, Old Gooseberry, Wideawake, Snooks, Mops, Cutaway, the Dogs-meat Man; but he considered this in bad taste, and was always a little hurt by it.", "때로 이 편지를 보내는 자들은 우스꽝스러운 이름을 댔으니, 벽돌, 풀무, 늙은 까치밥나무, 정신말짱, 스눅스, 대걸레, 잘라낸 옷자락, 개고기 장수 같은 식이었다. 그러나 그는 이것을 천박한 취향이라 여겨, 늘 그 때문에 조금 마음이 상하곤 했다."]], [["In the fulness of time, this correspondence showing signs of wearing out, and seeming to require an effort on the part of the correspondents to which in the hurried circumstances of departure many of them might not be equal, he established the custom of attending collegians of a certain standing, to the gate, and taking leave of them there.", "이윽고 때가 차서, 이 서신 왕래가 시들해질 기미를 보이고, 또 편지를 보내는 쪽에서 어떤 수고를 들여야 하는 듯한데 떠나는 다급한 처지에서는 많은 이들이 그만한 수고를 감당하지 못할 듯하자, 그는 어느 정도 연조가 있는 동문들을 문까지 배웅하며 거기서 작별하는 관례를 만들었다."], ["The collegian under treatment, after shaking hands, would occasionally stop to wrap up something in a bit of paper, and would come back again calling ‘Hi!’", "그 배웅을 받던 동문은 악수를 한 뒤 이따금 멈춰 서서 무언가를 종잇조각에 싸고는, \"이봐요!\" 하고 부르며 다시 돌아오곤 했다."]], [["He would look round surprised.’Me?’ he would say, with a smile.", "그는 놀란 듯 둘러보곤 했다. \"나 말이오?\" 그는 미소 지으며 말하곤 했다."]], [["By this time the collegian would be up with him, and he would paternally add, VWhat have you forgotten?", "이때쯤이면 동문이 그에게 다가와 있었고, 그는 아버지답게 덧붙이곤 했다. \"무엇을 잊으셨소?"], ["What can I do for you?’", "내가 뭘 해 드리면 되겠소?\""]], [["‘I forgot to leave this,’ the collegian would usually return, ‘for the Father of the Marshalsea.’", "\"이걸 두고 가는 걸 깜빡했지 뭡니까,\" 동문은 으레 대답했다. \"마셜시의 아버지께 드리려고요.\""]], [["‘My good sir,’ he would rejoin, ‘he is infinitely obliged to you.’ But, to the last, the irresolute hand of old would remain in the pocket into which he had slipped the money during two or three turns about the yard, lest the transaction should be too conspicuous to the general body of collegians.", "\"이런, 여보시오,\" 그는 받아 말하곤 했다. \"그분이 당신께 무한히 감사해하시오.\" 그러나 마지막까지도, 그 옛날의 우유부단한 손은, 그가 마당을 두세 바퀴 도는 동안 슬쩍 돈을 찔러 넣어 둔 주머니 속에 머물러 있곤 했으니, 그 거래가 동문들 전체에게 너무 눈에 띌까 봐서였다."]], [["One afternoon he had been doing the honours of the place to a rather large party of collegians, who happened to be going out, when, as he was coming back, he encountered one from the poor side who had been taken in execution for a small sum a week before, had ‘settled’ in the course of that afternoon, and was going out too.", "어느 날 오후, 그는 마침 나가게 된 꽤 큰 무리의 동문들에게 이곳의 예우를 베풀고 있었는데, 돌아오던 길에 가난뱅이 쪽 출신의 한 사내와 마주쳤다. 그는 일주일 전 소액 채무로 집행을 당해 들어왔다가 그날 오후 중에 \"정산\"을 마치고 역시 나가는 참이었다."], ["The man was a mere Plasterer in his working dress; had his wife with him, and a bundle; and was in high spirits.", "그 사내는 작업복 차림의 그저 미장이일 뿐이었는데, 아내를 데리고 보따리를 들고서 잔뜩 들떠 있었다."]], [["‘God bless you, sir,’ he said in passing.", "\"신의 가호가 있기를, 선생.\" 그가 지나가며 말했다."]], [["‘And you,’ benignantly returned the Father of the Marshalsea.", "\"당신에게도.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 자애롭게 답했다."]], [["They were pretty far divided, going their several ways, when the Plasterer called out, ‘I say!--sir!’ and came back to him.", "각자 제 갈 길로 꽤 멀어졌을 때, 그 미장이가 \"이봐요!— 선생!\" 하고 외치며 그에게 되돌아왔다."]], [["‘It ain’t much,’ said the Plasterer, putting a little pile of halfpence in his hand, ‘but it’s well meant.’", "\"많지는 않습니다.\" 미장이가 반 페니짜리 동전 한 무더기를 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 말했다. \"하지만 좋은 뜻으로 드리는 거예요.\""]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea had never been offered tribute in copper yet.", "마셜시의 아버지는 여태껏 동전으로 헌사를 받아 본 적이 없었다."], ["His children often had, and with his perfect acquiescence it had gone into the common purse to buy meat that he had eaten, and drink that he had drunk; but fustian splashed with white lime, bestowing halfpence on him, front to front, was new.", "그의 아이들은 자주 받았고, 그것은 그가 완전히 묵인하는 가운데 공동의 지갑으로 들어가 그가 먹은 고기와 그가 마신 술을 사는 데 쓰였다. 그러나 흰 석회가 튄 퍼스티언 작업복을 입은 자가 면전에서 그에게 반 페니 동전을 베푸는 것은 처음 있는 일이었다."]], [["‘How dare you!’ he said to the man, and feebly burst into tears.", "\"감히 어떻게!\" 그가 그 사내에게 말하고는, 힘없이 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["The Plasterer turned him towards the wall, that his face might not be seen; and the action was so delicate, and the man was so penetrated with repentance, and asked pardon so honestly, that he could make him no less acknowledgment than, ‘I know you meant it kindly.", "미장이는 그의 얼굴이 보이지 않도록 그를 담 쪽으로 돌려세웠다. 그 동작이 어찌나 세심하고, 그 사내가 어찌나 후회에 사무쳐, 또 어찌나 진심으로 용서를 비는지, 그는 그에게 이렇게밖에는 달리 응수할 수 없었다."], ["Say no more.’", "\"당신이 좋은 뜻으로 그런 줄 아오. 더는 말 마시오.\""]], [["‘Bless your soul, sir,’ urged the Plasterer, ‘I did indeed.", "\"아이고 선생, 정말이지,\" 미장이가 간곡히 말했다."], ["I’d do more by you than the rest of ‘em do, I fancy.’", "\"진심이었어요. 제 생각엔 다른 사람들이 하는 것보다 제가 선생께 더 잘해 드릴 수 있을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["‘What would you do?’ he asked.", "\"무엇을 해 주겠다는 거요?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["‘I’d come back to see you, after I was let out.’", "\"나가고 나서, 선생을 보러 다시 오겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Give me the money again,’ said the other, eagerly, ‘and I’ll keep it, and never spend it.", "\"그 돈을 다시 주시오.\" 상대가 간절히 말했다."], ["Thank you for it, thank you!", "\"내가 그걸 간직하고, 절대 쓰지 않겠소. 고맙소, 정말 고맙소!"], ["I shall see you again?’", "당신을 다시 보게 되겠지요?\""]], [["‘If I live a week you shall.’", "\"제가 일주일을 더 산다면, 꼭 뵙겠습니다.\""]], [["They shook hands and parted.", "그들은 악수를 하고 헤어졌다."], ["The collegians, assembled in Symposium in the Snuggery that night, marvelled what had happened to their Father; he walked so late in the shadows of the yard, and seemed so downcast.", "그날 밤 아늑한 휴게실에 좌담회랍시고 모인 동문들은, 자기네 아버지에게 무슨 일이 있었던 건지 의아해했다. 그가 마당의 그늘 속을 너무도 늦게까지 거닐었고, 너무도 의기소침해 보였기 때문이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 7. The Child of the Marshalsea", "# CHAPTER 7. The Child of the Marshalsea"]], [["The baby whose first draught of air had been tinctured with Doctor Haggage’s brandy, was handed down among the generations of collegians, like the tradition of their common parent.", "해기지 의사의 브랜디가 섞인 첫 공기를 들이마셨던 그 갓난아기는, 마치 그들 공통의 어버이에 관한 전설처럼, 수용자 세대들 사이로 대대로 물려 내려갔다."], ["In the earlier stages of her existence, she was handed down in a literal and prosaic sense; it being almost a part of the entrance footing of every new collegian to nurse the child who had been born in the college.", "그녀가 살아온 더 이른 시기에는, 말 그대로 또 글자 그대로의 의미로 물려 내려갔다. 새로 들어온 수용자라면 누구나, 이 감옥에서 태어난 아이를 돌보는 일이 거의 입회 신고식의 일부처럼 되어 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘By rights,’ remarked the turnkey when she was first shown to him, ‘I ought to be her godfather.’", "\"마땅히,\" 처음 그녀를 보여 주었을 때 간수가 말했다. \"내가 이 아이의 대부가 되어야겠는걸.\""]], [["The debtor irresolutely thought of it for a minute, and said, ‘Perhaps you wouldn’t object to really being her godfather?’", "채무자는 잠시 망설이며 그 일을 생각하더니 말했다. \"정말로 이 아이의 대부가 되어 주시는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠소?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 나는 마다하지 않소,\""], ["_I_ don’t object,’ replied the turnkey, ‘if you don’t.’", "간수가 대답했다. \"당신만 괜찮다면야.\""]], [["Thus it came to pass that she was christened one Sunday afternoon, when the turnkey, being relieved, was off the lock; and that the turnkey went up to the font of Saint George’s Church, and promised and vowed and renounced on her behalf, as he himself related when he came back, ‘like a good ‘un.’", "이리하여 어느 일요일 오후, 간수가 비번이어서 자물쇠를 떠나 있을 때, 그녀에게 세례가 베풀어졌다. 그리고 간수는 세인트 조지 교회의 세례반 앞으로 나아가, 본인이 돌아와서 말한 대로 \"아주 훌륭하게,\" 그녀를 대신하여 약속하고 맹세하고 단념을 다짐했다."]], [["This invested the turnkey with a new proprietary share in the child, over and above his former official one.", "이로써 간수는 이전의 공적인 몫 위에, 그 아이에 대한 새로운 소유의 몫을 하나 더 얻게 되었다."], ["When she began to walk and talk, he became fond of her; bought a little arm-chair and stood it by the high fender of the lodge fire-place; liked to have her company when he was on the lock; and used to bribe her with cheap toys to come and talk to him.", "그녀가 걷고 말하기 시작하자 그는 그녀를 귀여워하게 되었다. 작은 안락의자를 하나 사서 수위실 난로의 높은 불막이 가에 놓아 두었고, 자물쇠를 지킬 때 그녀가 곁에 있어 주는 것을 좋아했으며, 값싼 장난감으로 그녀를 꾀어 와서 이야기를 나누곤 했다."], ["The child, for her part, soon grew so fond of the turnkey that she would come climbing up the lodge-steps of her own accord at all hours of the day.", "아이 쪽에서도 곧 간수를 어찌나 따랐던지, 하루 중 어느 때고 제 발로 수위실 계단을 기어올라 오곤 했다."], ["When she fell asleep in the little armchair by the high fender, the turnkey would cover her with his pocket-handkerchief; and when she sat in it dressing and undressing a doll which soon came to be unlike dolls on the other side of the lock, and to bear a horrible family resemblance to Mrs Bangham--he would contemplate her from the top of his stool with exceeding gentleness.", "그녀가 높은 불막이 가의 작은 안락의자에서 잠이 들면, 간수는 자기 손수건으로 그녀를 덮어 주었다. 그리고 그녀가 그 의자에 앉아, 머지않아 자물쇠 저편의 인형들과는 도무지 닮지 않게 되고 도리어 뱅엄 부인과 끔찍하게도 한 식구처럼 닮아 가던 인형을 입혔다 벗겼다 하고 있으면, 그는 자기 걸상 꼭대기에서 더없이 다정하게 그녀를 바라보곤 했다."], ["Witnessing these things, the collegians would express an opinion that the turnkey, who was a bachelor, had been cut out by nature for a family man.", "이런 모습들을 지켜본 수용자들은, 독신인 그 간수가 천성적으로 가정을 꾸릴 사람으로 태어났다는 의견을 내놓곤 했다."], ["But the turnkey thanked them, and said, ‘No, on the whole it was enough to see other people’s children there.’", "그러나 간수는 그들에게 고맙다고 하면서도 이렇게 말했다. \"아니오, 대체로 여기서 남의 아이들을 보는 것으로 충분합니다.\""]], [["At what period of her early life the little creature began to perceive that it was not the habit of all the world to live locked up in narrow yards surrounded by high walls with spikes at the top, would be a difficult question to settle.", "그 어린것이, 꼭대기에 뾰족한 쇠창살이 달린 높은 담으로 둘러싸인 좁은 마당에 갇혀 사는 것이 세상 모든 사람의 습관은 아니라는 것을 깨닫기 시작한 것이 어린 시절의 어느 시기였는지를 가려내기란 어려운 문제일 것이다."], ["But she was a very, very little creature indeed, when she had somehow gained the knowledge that her clasp of her father’s hand was to be always loosened at the door which the great key opened; and that while her own light steps were free to pass beyond it, his feet must never cross that line.", "그러나 아버지의 손을 잡은 자기 손이, 그 커다란 열쇠가 여는 문 앞에서 언제나 풀려야 한다는 것을, 그리고 자기 가벼운 발걸음은 그 너머로 자유로이 지날 수 있어도 그의 발은 결코 그 선을 넘어서는 안 된다는 것을 어쩐 일인지 알게 되었을 때, 그녀는 정말로 정말로 아주 어린것이었다."], ["A pitiful and plaintive look, with which she had begun to regard him when she was still extremely young, was perhaps a part of this discovery.", "아직 몹시 어렸을 적부터 아버지를 바라보기 시작한 그 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛은, 아마도 이 깨달음의 한 부분이었으리라."]], [["With a pitiful and plaintive look for everything, indeed, but with something in it for only him that was like protection, this Child of the Marshalsea and the child of the Father of the Marshalsea, sat by her friend the turnkey in the lodge, kept the family room, or wandered about the prison-yard, for the first eight years of her life.", "실로 모든 것을 향해 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛을 띠면서도, 오직 그를 향해서만은 무언가 지켜 주려는 듯한 것을 그 눈빛에 담은 채, 이 마셜시의 아이이자 마셜시의 아버지의 아이는, 생애 첫 여덟 해 동안 수위실에서 친구인 간수의 곁에 앉아 있거나, 식구들의 방을 지키거나, 감옥 마당을 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["With a pitiful and plaintive look for her wayward sister; for her idle brother; for the high blank walls; for the faded crowd they shut in; for the games of the prison children as they whooped and ran, and played at hide-and-seek, and made the iron bars of the inner gateway ‘Home.’", "제멋대로인 언니를 향해, 게으른 오빠를 향해, 텅 빈 높은 담을 향해, 그 담이 가둬 둔 빛바랜 무리를 향해, 그리고 함성을 지르며 뛰어다니고 숨바꼭질을 하며 안쪽 문의 쇠창살을 '집'으로 삼고 놀던 감옥 아이들의 놀이를 향해, 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛을 띤 채."]], [["Wistful and wondering, she would sit in summer weather by the high fender in the lodge, looking up at the sky through the barred window, until, when she turned her eyes away, bars of light would arise between her and her friend, and she would see him through a grating, too.", "여름철이면 그녀는 무언가를 그리워하고 의아해하면서 수위실의 높은 불막이 가에 앉아, 창살 박힌 창문을 통해 하늘을 올려다보곤 했다. 그러다 눈길을 돌리면 그녀와 친구 사이로 빛의 창살들이 떠올라, 그녀는 친구마저도 격자 너머로 보게 되곤 했다."]], [["‘Thinking of the fields,’ the turnkey said once, after watching her, ‘ain’t you?’", "\"들판을 생각하고 있구나,\" 한번은 그녀를 지켜보던 간수가 말했다. \"그렇지 않니?\""]], [["‘Where are they?’ she inquired.", "\"그건 어디 있어요?\" 그녀가 물었다."]], [["‘Why, they’re--over there, my dear,’ said the turnkey, with a vague flourish of his key.", "\"그게 말이다, 저쪽에 있단다, 얘야,\" 간수가 열쇠를 모호하게 한번 휘저으며 말했다."], ["‘Just about there.’", "\"바로 저기쯤.\""]], [["‘Does anybody open them, and shut them?", "\"누가 그걸 열고 닫나요?"], ["Are they locked?’", "잠가져 있어요?\""]], [["The turnkey was discomfited.", "간수는 당황했다. \"글쎄,\""], ["‘Well,’ he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["‘Not in general.’", "\"대개는 그렇지 않지.\""]], [["‘Are they very pretty, Bob?’ She called him Bob, by his own particular request and instruction.", "\"아주 예뻐요, 밥?\" 그녀는 그의 각별한 요청과 가르침에 따라 그를 밥이라고 불렀다."]], [["‘Lovely.", "\"근사하지."], ["Full of flowers.", "꽃이 가득하단다. 미나리아재비도 있고, 데이지도 있고, 또—\""], ["There’s buttercups, and there’s daisies, and there’s’--the turnkey hesitated, being short of floral nomenclature--‘there’s dandelions, and all manner of games.’", "간수는 꽃 이름이 달려서 머뭇거렸다. \"민들레도 있고, 온갖 놀이가 다 있단다.\""]], [["‘Is it very pleasant to be there, Bob?’", "\"거기 있으면 아주 즐거워요, 밥?\""]], [["‘Prime,’ said the turnkey.", "\"최고지,\" 간수가 말했다."]], [["‘Was father ever there?’", "\"아버지도 거기 가 보신 적 있어요?\""]], [["‘Hem!’ coughed the turnkey.", "\"흠!\" 간수가 헛기침을 했다."], ["‘O yes, he was there, sometimes.’", "\"오 그럼, 가 보셨지, 가끔은.\""]], [["‘Is he sorry not to be there now?’", "\"지금 거기 못 계셔서 서운해하세요?\""]], [["‘N-not particular,’ said the turnkey.", "\"그—그렇게 특별히는 아니란다,\" 간수가 말했다."]], [["‘Nor any of the people?’ she asked, glancing at the listless crowd within.", "\"다른 사람들도요?\" 그녀가 안에 있는 맥없는 무리를 흘끔 보며 물었다."], ["‘O are you quite sure and certain, Bob?’", "\"오 정말 확실해요, 밥?\""]], [["At this difficult point of the conversation Bob gave in, and changed the subject to hard-bake: always his last resource when he found his little friend getting him into a political, social, or theological corner.", "대화가 이 곤란한 대목에 이르자 밥은 두 손 들고, 화제를 딱딱한 캐러멜 과자로 돌렸다. 어린 친구가 그를 정치적, 사회적, 또는 신학적 궁지로 몰아넣을 때면 늘 마지막으로 의지하던 수단이었다."], ["But this was the origin of a series of Sunday excursions that these two curious companions made together.", "그러나 이것이 이 묘한 두 길동무가 함께한 일련의 일요일 나들이의 발단이 되었다."], ["They used to issue from the lodge on alternate Sunday afternoons with great gravity, bound for some meadows or green lanes that had been elaborately appointed by the turnkey in the course of the week; and there she picked grass and flowers to bring home, while he smoked his pipe.", "그들은 격주로 돌아오는 일요일 오후마다 아주 엄숙하게 수위실을 나서서, 간수가 한 주 동안 공들여 골라 둔 어느 풀밭이나 푸른 오솔길을 향했다. 거기서 그녀는 집에 가져갈 풀과 꽃을 뜯었고, 그동안 그는 파이프를 피웠다."], ["Afterwards, there were tea-gardens, shrimps, ale, and other delicacies; and then they would come back hand in hand, unless she was more than usually tired, and had fallen asleep on his shoulder.", "그러고 나면 찻집 정원이며 새우며 맥주며 그 밖의 별미들이 있었다. 그런 다음 그들은 손을 맞잡고 돌아왔는데, 그녀가 평소보다 더 지쳐서 그의 어깨에 기대 잠이 들었을 때만은 예외였다."]], [["In those early days, the turnkey first began profoundly to consider a question which cost him so much mental labour, that it remained undetermined on the day of his death.", "그 무렵 간수는 처음으로 한 가지 문제를 깊이 궁리하기 시작했는데, 그것이 어찌나 많은 정신적 노고를 들이게 했던지 죽는 날까지도 결말을 못 본 채 남았다."], ["He decided to will and bequeath his little property of savings to his godchild, and the point arose how could it be so ‘tied up’ as that only she should have the benefit of it?", "그는 자신의 작은 저축 재산을 대녀에게 물려주기로 마음먹었는데, 어떻게 하면 그것을 그녀만이 그 혜택을 누리도록 '묶어 둘' 수 있느냐 하는 점이 문제로 떠올랐다."], ["His experience on the lock gave him such an acute perception of the enormous difficulty of ‘tying up’ money with any approach to tightness, and contrariwise of the remarkable ease with which it got loose, that through a series of years he regularly propounded this knotty point to every new insolvent agent and other professional gentleman who passed in and out.", "자물쇠를 지킨 경험 덕분에 그는, 돈을 조금이라도 단단히 '묶어 두기'란 엄청나게 어렵다는 것, 그리고 반대로 돈이 풀려나기란 놀랄 만큼 쉽다는 것을 예리하게 깨닫고 있었던 터라, 여러 해에 걸쳐 드나드는 새 파산 대리인이며 그 밖의 전문직 신사들마다 붙들고 이 까다로운 문제를 으레 들이밀곤 했다."]], [["‘Supposing,’ he would say, stating the case with his key on the professional gentleman’s waistcoat; ‘supposing a man wanted to leave his property to a young female, and wanted to tie it up so that nobody else should ever be able to make a grab at it; how would you tie up that property?’", "\"가령,\" 그는 전문직 신사의 조끼에 열쇠를 들이대고 사례를 진술하며 말하곤 했다. \"가령 어떤 사람이 자기 재산을 젊은 여자에게 물려주고 싶은데, 다른 누구도 그것에 손을 못 대게끔 묶어 두고 싶다 합시다. 그 재산을 어떻게 묶어 두겠소?\""]], [["‘Settle it strictly on herself,’ the professional gentleman would complacently answer.", "\"엄격하게 그 여자 본인에게 한정하여 설정하면 됩니다,\" 전문직 신사는 흡족하게 대답하곤 했다."]], [["‘But look here,’ quoth the turnkey.", "\"하지만 들어 보시오,\" 간수가 말하곤 했다."], ["‘Supposing she had, say a brother, say a father, say a husband, who would be likely to make a grab at that property when she came into it--how about that?’", "\"가령 그 여자에게, 이를테면 오빠가 있고, 이를테면 아버지가 있고, 이를테면 남편이 있어서, 그녀가 그 재산을 물려받았을 때 그것에 손을 대려 들 법하다면—그건 어떻게 되오?\""]], [["‘It would be settled on herself, and they would have no more legal claim on it than you,’ would be the professional answer.", "\"그 재산은 그녀 본인에게 한정 설정되어 있으니, 그들에게는 당신만큼이나 법적 청구권이 없습니다,\" 하는 것이 전문가의 대답이었다."]], [["‘Stop a bit,’ said the turnkey.", "\"잠깐 기다려 보시오,\" 간수가 말했다."], ["‘Supposing she was tender-hearted, and they came over her.", "\"가령 그 여자가 마음이 여려서, 그들이 그녀를 구슬려 넘겼다 칩시다."], ["Where’s your law for tying it up then?’", "그렇다면 그것을 묶어 둘 법이 어디 있소?\""]], [["The deepest character whom the turnkey sounded, was unable to produce his law for tying such a knot as that.", "간수가 떠본 가장 깊이 있는 인물조차도, 그런 매듭을 묶어 둘 법을 내놓지 못했다."], ["So, the turnkey thought about it all his life, and died intestate after all.", "그래서 간수는 평생 그것을 궁리했으나, 결국 유언장도 없이 죽고 말았다."]], [["But that was long afterwards, when his god-daughter was past sixteen.", "그러나 그것은 한참 뒤의 일로, 그의 대녀가 열여섯을 넘겼을 때였다."], ["The first half of that space of her life was only just accomplished, when her pitiful and plaintive look saw her father a widower.", "그녀의 그 생애 기간 가운데 앞쪽 절반이 막 채워졌을 무렵, 그녀의 가엾고 애처로운 눈빛은 아버지가 홀아비가 된 것을 보았다."], ["From that time the protection that her wondering eyes had expressed towards him, became embodied in action, and the Child of the Marshalsea took upon herself a new relation towards the Father.", "그때부터 그녀의 의아해하던 눈빛이 그를 향해 드러내던 그 보호의 마음은 행동으로 구체화되었고, 마셜시의 아이는 아버지를 향한 새로운 관계를 떠맡았다."]], [["At first, such a baby could do little more than sit with him, deserting her livelier place by the high fender, and quietly watching him.", "처음에는 그런 갓난것이 할 수 있는 일이란, 높은 불막이 가의 더 활기찬 자기 자리를 버리고 그의 곁에 앉아 조용히 그를 지켜보는 것이 고작이었다."], ["But this made her so far necessary to him that he became accustomed to her, and began to be sensible of missing her when she was not there.", "그러나 이것이 그에게 그녀를 어느 정도 필요한 존재로 만들어, 그는 그녀에게 익숙해졌고 그녀가 곁에 없으면 허전함을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["Through this little gate, she passed out of childhood into the care-laden world.", "이 작은 문을 지나, 그녀는 어린 시절에서 빠져나와 근심으로 가득한 세상으로 들어섰다."]], [["What her pitiful look saw, at that early time, in her father, in her sister, in her brother, in the jail; how much, or how little of the wretched truth it pleased God to make visible to her; lies hidden with many mysteries.", "그 이른 시기에 그녀의 가엾은 눈빛이 아버지에게서, 언니에게서, 오빠에게서, 감옥에서 무엇을 보았는지, 그 비참한 진실의 얼마만큼을, 혹은 얼마나 적은 만큼을 신께서 그녀에게 보이도록 허락하셨는지는 숱한 신비와 더불어 감추어져 있다."], ["It is enough that she was inspired to be something which was not what the rest were, and to be that something, different and laborious, for the sake of the rest.", "다만 그녀가 다른 사람들과는 같지 않은 무언가가 되도록, 그리고 그 다른 사람들을 위하여 남다르고 고된 그 무언가가 되도록 영감을 받았다는 것으로 충분하다."], ["Inspired? Yes.", "영감이라고?"], ["Shall we speak of the inspiration of a poet or a priest, and not of the heart impelled by love and self-devotion to the lowliest work in the lowliest way of life!", "그렇다. 시인이나 사제의 영감을 두고는 말하면서, 사랑과 자기희생에 떠밀려 더없이 비천한 삶의 방식으로 더없이 비천한 일을 떠맡은 그 마음의 영감은 말하지 않을 셈인가!"]], [["With no earthly friend to help her, or so much as to see her, but the one so strangely assorted; with no knowledge even of the common daily tone and habits of the common members of the free community who are not shut up in prisons; born and bred in a social condition, false even with a reference to the falsest condition outside the walls; drinking from infancy of a well whose waters had their own peculiar stain, their own unwholesome and unnatural taste; the Child of the Marshalsea began her womanly life.", "그녀를 도와주기는커녕 들여다봐 주기라도 할 세상의 벗이라곤 그토록 기묘하게 어울린 단 한 사람뿐이었고, 감옥에 갇히지 않은 자유로운 공동체의 보통 사람들이 지닌 일상의 평범한 말투와 습관조차 알지 못했으며, 담 바깥의 가장 거짓된 처지에 견주어 보아도 거짓된 사회적 조건에서 태어나 자랐고, 갓난아기 때부터 그 나름의 독특한 얼룩과 그 나름의 해롭고 부자연스러운 맛을 지닌 우물물을 마셔 온 채로, 마셜시의 아이는 여인으로서의 삶을 시작했다."]], [["No matter through what mistakes and discouragements, what ridicule (not unkindly meant, but deeply felt) of her youth and little figure, what humble consciousness of her own babyhood and want of strength, even in the matter of lifting and carrying; through how much weariness and hopelessness, and how many secret tears; she drudged on, until recognised as useful, even indispensable.", "어떤 실수와 좌절을 거쳤든, 그녀의 어림과 작은 몸집에 쏟아진 어떤 비웃음(악의는 아니었으나 뼈저리게 느껴진)을 겪었든, 물건을 들어 올리고 나르는 일에서조차 자신이 아직 어리고 힘이 모자란다는 어떤 겸허한 자각을 안았든, 또 얼마나 많은 피로와 절망을, 얼마나 많은 남모를 눈물을 거쳤든 간에, 그녀는 쓸모 있는 사람으로, 나아가 없어서는 안 될 사람으로 인정받기까지 묵묵히 일했다."], ["That time came.", "그때가 왔다."], ["She took the place of eldest of the three, in all things but precedence; was the head of the fallen family; and bore, in her own heart, its anxieties and shames.", "그녀는 서열만 빼고 모든 면에서 세 남매 중 맏이의 자리를 차지했다. 몰락한 집안의 가장이 되었으며, 그 집안의 근심과 수치를 제 가슴속에 떠안았다."]], [["At thirteen, she could read and keep accounts, that is, could put down in words and figures how much the bare necessaries that they wanted would cost, and how much less they had to buy them with.", "열세 살에 그녀는 글을 읽고 셈을 할 줄 알았다. 다시 말해, 그들이 필요로 하는 최소한의 필수품이 얼마나 들지를, 또 그것을 사들일 돈이 얼마나 모자라는지를 말과 숫자로 적어 둘 줄 알았다."], ["She had been, by snatches of a few weeks at a time, to an evening school outside, and got her sister and brother sent to day-schools by desultory starts, during three or four years.", "그녀는 서너 해에 걸쳐, 한 번에 몇 주씩 띄엄띄엄 바깥의 야간 학교에 다녔고, 언니와 오빠도 이따금씩 단속적으로 주간 학교에 보내졌다."], ["There was no instruction for any of them at home; but she knew well--no one better--that a man so broken as to be the Father of the Marshalsea, could be no father to his own children.", "집에서는 그들 가운데 누구에게도 가르침이 없었다. 그러나 그녀는—누구보다도 잘—마셜시의 아버지가 될 만큼 망가진 사람은 제 자식들에게도 아버지 노릇을 할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["To these scanty means of improvement, she added another of her own contriving.", "이 빈약한 향상의 수단에다, 그녀는 제 나름으로 궁리해 낸 또 하나를 보탰다."], ["Once, among the heterogeneous crowd of inmates there appeared a dancing-master.", "한번은 그 잡다한 수감자 무리 가운데 무용 선생이 한 사람 나타났다."], ["Her sister had a great desire to learn the dancing-master’s art, and seemed to have a taste that way.", "언니는 무용 선생의 기예를 배우고 싶어 몹시 갈망했고, 그쪽으로 소질이 있는 듯도 했다."], ["At thirteen years old, the Child of the Marshalsea presented herself to the dancing-master, with a little bag in her hand, and preferred her humble petition.", "열세 살의 마셜시의 아이는 손에 작은 주머니를 들고 무용 선생 앞에 나서서, 자신의 겸손한 청을 올렸다."]], [["‘If you please, I was born here, sir.’", "\"실례지만, 저는 여기서 태어났어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You are the young lady, are you?’ said the dancing-master, surveying the small figure and uplifted face.", "그 아가씨로구나, 그렇지?\" 무용 선생이 그 자그마한 몸집과 치켜든 얼굴을 살피며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 선생님.\""]], [["‘And what can I do for you?’ said the dancing-master.", "\"그래 내가 너를 위해 무엇을 해 줄 수 있겠니?\" 무용 선생이 말했다."]], [["‘Nothing for me, sir, thank you,’ anxiously undrawing the strings of the little bag; ‘but if, while you stay here, you could be so kind as to teach my sister cheap--’", "\"저를 위해서는 아무것도 없어요, 선생님, 고맙습니다,\" 그녀가 작은 주머니의 끈을 초조하게 풀며 말했다. \"다만 선생님이 여기 머무시는 동안, 부디 제 언니를 싸게 가르쳐 주실 수 있다면—\""]], [["‘My child, I’ll teach her for nothing,’ said the dancing-master, shutting up the bag.", "\"얘야, 거저 가르쳐 주마,\" 무용 선생이 주머니를 닫으며 말했다."], ["He was as good-natured a dancing-master as ever danced to the Insolvent Court, and he kept his word.", "그는 일찍이 파산 법정에 맞춰 춤춘 그 누구 못지않게 마음씨 고운 무용 선생이었고, 약속을 지켰다."], ["The sister was so apt a pupil, and the dancing-master had such abundant leisure to bestow upon her (for it took him a matter of ten weeks to set to his creditors, lead off, turn the Commissioners, and right and left back to his professional pursuits), that wonderful progress was made.", "언니는 어찌나 영특한 제자였던지, 또 무용 선생에게는 그녀에게 베풀 한가한 시간이 어찌나 넘쳐났던지(채권자들을 향해 자세를 잡고, 선창으로 이끌고, 위원들을 돌고, 좌우로 다시 제 직업으로 돌아오기까지 무려 열 주가 걸렸으니), 놀라운 진척이 있었다."], ["Indeed the dancing-master was so proud of it, and so wishful to display it before he left to a few select friends among the collegians, that at six o’clock on a certain fine morning, a minuet de la cour came off in the yard--the college-rooms being of too confined proportions for the purpose--in which so much ground was covered, and the steps were so conscientiously executed, that the dancing-master, having to play the kit besides, was thoroughly blown.", "실로 무용 선생은 그것이 어찌나 자랑스럽고, 떠나기 전에 수용자들 가운데 선택받은 몇몇 벗에게 그것을 선보이고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 어느 화창한 아침 여섯 시에 마당에서 궁정식 미뉴에트가 펼쳐졌다—감옥의 방들은 그 용도에 비해 너무 비좁았던 것이다—거기서 어찌나 넓은 자리를 누비고 스텝을 어찌나 양심적으로 밟아 냈던지, 곁들여 키트까지 켜야 했던 무용 선생은 완전히 숨이 차 버렸다."]], [["The success of this beginning, which led to the dancing-master’s continuing his instruction after his release, emboldened the poor child to try again.", "이 시작의 성공은—이로 인해 무용 선생은 석방된 뒤에도 가르침을 이어 갔다—이 가엾은 아이를 대담하게 만들어 다시 한번 시도하게 했다."], ["She watched and waited months for a seamstress.", "그녀는 여러 달 동안 삯바느질꾼을 살피며 기다렸다."], ["In the fulness of time a milliner came in, and to her she repaired on her own behalf.", "때가 무르익자 모자 만드는 여인이 들어왔고, 그녀는 자신을 위하여 그 여인을 찾아갔다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, ma’am,’ she said, looking timidly round the door of the milliner, whom she found in tears and in bed: ‘but I was born here.’", "\"실례합니다, 부인,\" 그녀는 침대에 누워 눈물짓고 있는 모자 만드는 여인의 방 문간을 머뭇머뭇 들여다보며 말했다. \"저는 여기서 태어났어요.\""]], [["Everybody seemed to hear of her as soon as they arrived; for the milliner sat up in bed, drying her eyes, and said, just as the dancing-master had said:", "다들 들어오자마자 곧 그녀에 관한 이야기를 듣는 모양이었다. 모자 만드는 여인은 침대에서 몸을 일으켜 앉아 눈물을 닦으며, 무용 선생이 했던 그대로 말했던 것이다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["_You_ are the child, are you?’", "네가 그 아이로구나, 그렇지?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am.’", "\"네, 부인.\""]], [["‘I am sorry I haven’t got anything for you,’ said the milliner, shaking her head.", "\"미안하지만 네게 줄 게 아무것도 없구나,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s not that, ma’am.", "\"그게 아니에요, 부인."], ["If you please I want to learn needle-work.’", "부디 저는 바느질을 배우고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘Why should you do that,’ returned the milliner, ‘with me before you?", "\"왜 그런 걸 배우려는 거니,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 되물었다."], ["It has not done me much good.’", "\"내 꼴을 눈앞에 두고서? 나한테는 별 도움이 안 되었는데.\""]], [["‘Nothing--whatever it is--seems to have done anybody much good who comes here,’ she returned in all simplicity; ‘but I want to learn just the same.’", "\"여기 들어오는 누구에게도—그게 무엇이든—별 도움이 된 것은 없는 것 같아요,\" 그녀가 더없이 순진하게 대답했다. \"하지만 저는 그래도 배우고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘I am afraid you are so weak, you see,’ the milliner objected.", "\"네가 너무 약한 게 걱정이구나, 알다시피,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 난색을 표했다."]], [["‘I don’t think I am weak, ma’am.’", "\"제가 약하다고는 생각하지 않아요, 부인.\""]], [["‘And you are so very, very little, you see,’ the milliner objected.", "\"그리고 네가 너무, 너무 작잖니, 알다시피,\" 모자 만드는 여인이 난색을 표했다."]], [["‘Yes, I am afraid I am very little indeed,’ returned the Child of the Marshalsea; and so began to sob over that unfortunate defect of hers, which came so often in her way.", "\"네, 제가 정말 아주 작긴 한 것 같아요,\" 마셜시의 아이가 대답했다."], ["The milliner--who was not morose or hard-hearted, only newly insolvent--was touched, took her in hand with goodwill, found her the most patient and earnest of pupils, and made her a cunning work-woman in course of time.", "그러고는 늘 그렇게 자기 앞을 가로막던 그 딱한 결함을 두고 흐느끼기 시작했다. 모자 만드는 여인은—성미가 무뚝뚝하거나 모진 사람이 아니라, 그저 갓 파산한 처지였을 뿐이라—마음이 움직여, 호의를 가지고 그녀를 맡았고, 그녀가 더없이 참을성 있고 진지한 제자임을 알게 되었으며, 머지않아 그녀를 솜씨 좋은 바느질꾼으로 길러 냈다."]], [["In course of time, and in the very self-same course of time, the Father of the Marshalsea gradually developed a new flower of character.", "세월이 흐르는 동안, 바로 그 똑같은 세월이 흐르는 동안, 마셜시의 아버지는 차츰 새로운 성격의 꽃을 피워 냈다."], ["The more Fatherly he grew as to the Marshalsea, and the more dependent he became on the contributions of his changing family, the greater stand he made by his forlorn gentility.", "그가 마셜시에 대해 점점 더 아버지다워지고, 형편이 바뀌는 식구들의 갹출에 점점 더 기대게 될수록, 그는 자기의 처량한 신사 행세를 더욱 떠받들었다."], ["With the same hand that he pocketed a collegian’s half-crown half an hour ago, he would wipe away the tears that streamed over his cheeks if any reference were made to his daughters’ earning their bread.", "반 시간 전에 어느 수용자의 반 크라운을 호주머니에 챙겨 넣은 바로 그 손으로, 그는 누가 자기 딸들이 빵을 벌어 온다는 이야기를 꺼내기라도 하면 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 눈물을 닦아 내곤 했다."], ["So, over and above other daily cares, the Child of the Marshalsea had always upon her the care of preserving the genteel fiction that they were all idle beggars together.", "그리하여 다른 나날의 근심들에 더하여, 마셜시의 아이는 그들 모두가 다 같이 한가로운 거지일 뿐이라는 그 점잖은 허구를 지켜 내는 짐을 늘 짊어지고 있었다."]], [["The sister became a dancer.", "언니는 무용수가 되었다."], ["There was a ruined uncle in the family group--ruined by his brother, the Father of the Marshalsea, and knowing no more how than his ruiner did, but accepting the fact as an inevitable certainty--on whom her protection devolved.", "가족 무리 가운데 몰락한 삼촌이 한 사람 있었는데—그를 몰락시킨 사람은 그의 형, 곧 마셜시의 아버지였고, 그 자신은 몰락시킨 형 못지않게 그 까닭을 알지 못했으나, 그 사실을 어쩔 수 없는 필연으로 받아들이고 있었다—그를 보살피는 일이 그녀에게 떨어졌다."], ["Naturally a retired and simple man, he had shown no particular sense of being ruined at the time when that calamity fell upon him, further than that he left off washing himself when the shock was announced, and never took to that luxury any more.", "본디 은둔적이고 단순한 사람이라, 그 재앙이 닥쳤을 때도 그는 자기가 몰락했다는 것을 별달리 의식하는 기색을 보이지 않았다. 다만 그 충격이 알려졌을 때 세수를 그만두었고, 다시는 그 호사를 부리지 않았을 뿐이다."], ["He had been a very indifferent musical amateur in his better days; and when he fell with his brother, resorted for support to playing a clarionet as dirty as himself in a small Theatre Orchestra.", "형편이 나았던 시절에 그는 아주 시원찮은 음악 애호가였는데, 형과 함께 몰락하자 어느 작은 극장 관현악단에서 제 몸뚱이만큼이나 더러운 클라리넷을 부는 것으로 생계를 의지했다. 그 극장은 그의 조카가 무용수가 된 바로 그 극장이었다."], ["It was the theatre in which his niece became a dancer; he had been a fixture there a long time when she took her poor station in it; and he accepted the task of serving as her escort and guardian, just as he would have accepted an illness, a legacy, a feast, starvation--anything but soap.", "조카가 그곳의 초라한 자리에 들어갔을 때 그는 이미 오래도록 붙박이로 있던 터였고, 그는 조카의 동행이자 보호자로 봉사하는 일을 받아들였다. 마치 병이든, 유산이든, 잔치든, 굶주림이든—비누만 아니라면 무엇이든—받아들였을 그 모양 그대로."]], [["To enable this girl to earn her few weekly shillings, it was necessary for the Child of the Marshalsea to go through an elaborate form with the Father.", "이 소녀가 한 주에 몇 실링을 벌게 해 주려면, 마셜시의 아이는 아버지를 상대로 공들인 형식을 한바탕 치러야 했다."]], [["‘Fanny is not going to live with us just now, father.", "\"패니가 지금 당장은 우리와 함께 살지 않을 거예요, 아버지."], ["She will be here a good deal in the day, but she is going to live outside with uncle.’", "낮에는 여기 많이 와 있겠지만, 삼촌과 함께 바깥에서 지낼 거예요.\""]], [["‘You surprise me.", "\"놀랍구나."], ["Why?’", "왜?\""]], [["‘I think uncle wants a companion, father.", "\"삼촌에게 말동무가 필요하신 것 같아요, 아버지."], ["He should be attended to, and looked after.’", "누가 돌봐 드리고 보살펴 드려야 해요.\""]], [["‘A companion?", "\"말동무라고?"], ["He passes much of his time here.", "그 사람은 여기서 많은 시간을 보내지 않니."], ["And you attend to him and look after him, Amy, a great deal more than ever your sister will.", "그리고 삼촌을 돌보고 보살피는 건, 에이미, 너야말로 네 언니가 앞으로 할 것보다 훨씬 더 많이 하고 있지 않니."], ["You all go out so much; you all go out so much.’", "너희는 다들 그렇게 자주 나가는구나. 다들 그렇게 자주 나가.\""]], [["This was to keep up the ceremony and pretence of his having no idea that Amy herself went out by the day to work.", "이것은 에이미 자신이 날마다 일하러 나간다는 것을 자기는 전혀 모른다는 듯한 그 예법과 가식을 지켜 내기 위한 것이었다."]], [["‘But we are always glad to come home, father; now, are we not?", "\"하지만 우리는 늘 집에 돌아오는 게 기뻐요, 아버지. 안 그래요?"], ["And as to Fanny, perhaps besides keeping uncle company and taking care of him, it may be as well for her not quite to live here, always.", "그리고 패니로 말하자면, 삼촌과 벗해 드리고 돌봐 드리는 일 말고도, 언니가 늘 여기서만 사는 게 아닌 편이 나을지도 몰라요."], ["She was not born here as I was, you know, father.’", "아시다시피 언니는 저처럼 여기서 태어난 게 아니잖아요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Well, Amy, well.", "\"그래, 에이미, 그래."], ["I don’t quite follow you, but it’s natural I suppose that Fanny should prefer to be outside, and even that you often should, too.", "네 말을 다 알아듣지는 못하겠다만, 패니가 바깥에 있기를 더 좋아하는 것도, 심지어 너까지 자주 그러는 것도 자연스러운 일이겠지."], ["So, you and Fanny and your uncle, my dear, shall have your own way.", "그러니, 얘야, 너랑 패니랑 네 삼촌은 너희 좋을 대로 하려무나."], ["Good, good.", "좋아, 좋아."], ["I’ll not meddle; don’t mind me.’", "나는 참견하지 않으마. 나는 신경 쓰지 마라.\""]], [["To get her brother out of the prison; out of the succession to Mrs Bangham in executing commissions, and out of the slang interchange with very doubtful companions consequent upon both; was her hardest task.", "오빠를 감옥에서 빼내는 일—심부름을 맡아 하는 일에서 뱅엄 부인의 뒤를 잇게 되는 데서, 또 그 두 가지로 인해 매우 미심쩍은 패거리와 은어를 주고받게 되는 데서 그를 빼내는 일—이 그녀의 가장 힘든 과제였다."], ["At eighteen he would have dragged on from hand to mouth, from hour to hour, from penny to penny, until eighty.", "열여덟에 그는 그날그날, 한 시간 한 시간, 한 푼 한 푼으로 여든까지 질질 끌어갈 판이었다."], ["Nobody got into the prison from whom he derived anything useful or good, and she could find no patron for him but her old friend and godfather.", "그에게 쓸모 있거나 좋은 것을 가져다줄 사람이라곤 감옥에 들어오지 않았고, 그녀가 그를 위해 찾아낼 수 있는 후원자라곤 옛 친구이자 대부뿐이었다."]], [["‘Dear Bob,’ said she, ‘what is to become of poor Tip?’ His name was Edward, and Ted had been transformed into Tip, within the walls.", "\"밥 아저씨,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"가엾은 팁은 어떻게 되는 거예요?\" 그의 이름은 에드워드였는데, 담장 안에서 테드가 팁으로 바뀌어 있었다."]], [["The turnkey had strong private opinions as to what would become of poor Tip, and had even gone so far with the view of averting their fulfilment, as to sound Tip in reference to the expediency of running away and going to serve his country.", "간수는 가엾은 팁이 어떻게 될지에 대해 강한 개인적 견해를 품고 있었고, 그 결말을 막으려는 뜻에서 팁에게 도망쳐 나라에 봉사하러 가는 게 어떻겠느냐고 떠보기까지 했다."], ["But Tip had thanked him, and said he didn’t seem to care for his country.", "그러나 팁은 고맙다고 하면서도, 자기는 나라 따위엔 별 관심이 없는 것 같다고 했다."]], [["‘Well, my dear,’ said the turnkey, ‘something ought to be done with him.", "\"그래, 얘야,\" 간수가 말했다."], ["Suppose I try and get him into the law?’", "\"저 녀석에게 뭔가 해 주긴 해야겠구나. 내가 저 녀석을 법률 쪽에 넣어 보면 어떻겠니?\""]], [["‘That would be so good of you, Bob!’", "\"그래 주시면 정말 고마운 일일 거예요, 밥!\""]], [["The turnkey had now two points to put to the professional gentlemen as they passed in and out.", "이제 간수는 드나드는 전문직 신사들에게 들이밀 문제를 두 가지나 갖게 되었다."], ["He put this second one so perseveringly that a stool and twelve shillings a week were at last found for Tip in the office of an attorney in a great National Palladium called the Palace Court; at that time one of a considerable list of everlasting bulwarks to the dignity and safety of Albion, whose places know them no more.", "그가 이 두 번째 문제를 어찌나 끈질기게 들이밀었던지, 마침내 팰리스 코트라 불리는 어느 위대한 국가의 보루에 있는 어느 변호사의 사무실에 팁을 위한 걸상 하나와 주급 십이 실링이 마련되었다. 그곳은 당시 알비온의 존엄과 안전을 위한 영원한 방벽들의 적잖은 목록 가운데 하나였으나, 이제는 그것들이 있던 자리조차 그것들을 알아보지 못한다."]], [["Tip languished in Clifford’s Inns for six months, and at the expiration of that term sauntered back one evening with his hands in his pockets, and incidentally observed to his sister that he was not going back again.", "팁은 클리퍼드 인에서 여섯 달을 빈둥거렸고, 그 기한이 끝나자 어느 날 저녁 호주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아와서는, 다시는 거기로 돌아가지 않겠노라고 누이에게 지나가는 말로 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Not going back again?’ said the poor little anxious Child of the Marshalsea, always calculating and planning for Tip, in the front rank of her charges.", "\"다시는 돌아가지 않겠다고?\" 가엾고 자그마하며 늘 노심초사하는 마셜시의 아이가, 자기가 떠맡은 짐들 가운데 으뜸 자리에 두고 언제나 팁을 위해 셈하고 궁리하던 그 아이가 말했다."]], [["‘I am so tired of it,’ said Tip, ‘that I have cut it.’", "\"하도 진력이 나서,\" 팁이 말했다. \"때려치웠어.\""]], [["Tip tired of everything.", "팁은 무엇에든 진력을 냈다."], ["With intervals of Marshalsea lounging, and Mrs Bangham succession, his small second mother, aided by her trusty friend, got him into a warehouse, into a market garden, into the hop trade, into the law again, into an auctioneers, into a brewery, into a stockbroker’s, into the law again, into a coach office, into a waggon office, into the law again, into a general dealer’s, into a distillery, into the law again, into a wool house, into a dry goods house, into the Billingsgate trade, into the foreign fruit trade, and into the docks.", "마셜시에서 빈둥대는 막간과 뱅엄 부인 자리를 잇는 막간을 사이사이 끼우면서, 그의 자그마한 둘째 어머니는 믿음직한 친구의 도움을 받아 그를 어느 창고에, 어느 채소 농장에, 호프 거래소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 경매소에, 어느 양조장에, 어느 증권 중개소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 마차 사무소에, 어느 짐마차 사무소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 잡화상에, 어느 증류소에, 다시 법률 쪽에, 어느 양모 상점에, 어느 포목점에, 빌링스게이트 장사에, 외국 과일 장사에, 그리고 부두에 넣어 주었다."], ["But whatever Tip went into, he came out of tired, announcing that he had cut it.", "그러나 팁은 무엇에 들어가든 진력이 난 채로 나왔고, 때려치웠노라고 알렸다."], ["Wherever he went, this foredoomed Tip appeared to take the prison walls with him, and to set them up in such trade or calling; and to prowl about within their narrow limits in the old slip-shod, purposeless, down-at-heel way; until the real immovable Marshalsea walls asserted their fascination over him, and brought him back.", "어디로 가든 이 천생 운 나쁜 팁은 감옥의 담을 함께 짊어지고 가서 그 장사나 직업 안에 그것을 세워 두는 듯했고, 그 좁은 한계 안을 예의 그 칠칠치 못하고 목적 없으며 뒤축 닳은 걸음걸이로 어슬렁거리다가, 이윽고 진짜로 꿈쩍 않는 마셜시의 담이 그를 끌어당기는 매력을 발휘하여 그를 도로 데려오곤 했다."]], [["Nevertheless, the brave little creature did so fix her heart on her brother’s rescue, that while he was ringing out these doleful changes, she pinched and scraped enough together to ship him for Canada.", "그럼에도 그 용감한 작은 것은 오빠를 구해 내는 데 어찌나 마음을 쏟았던지, 그가 이 구슬픈 변주를 울려 대는 동안에도, 그녀는 그를 캐나다로 보낼 만큼의 돈을 한 푼 한 푼 아끼고 긁어모았다."], ["When he was tired of nothing to do, and disposed in its turn to cut even that, he graciously consented to go to Canada.", "할 일이 없는 데 진력이 나서 이번에는 그것마저 때려치울 마음이 들자, 그는 너그러이 캐나다로 가는 데 동의했다."], ["And there was grief in her bosom over parting with him, and joy in the hope of his being put in a straight course at last.", "그를 떠나보내는 것에 그녀의 가슴은 슬픔으로 차올랐으나, 그가 마침내 곧은 길에 들어서리라는 희망에 기쁨도 있었다."]], [["‘God bless you, dear Tip.", "\"하느님의 가호가 있기를, 사랑하는 팁."], ["Don’t be too proud to come and see us, when you have made your fortune.’", "출세하거든 너무 우쭐대지 말고 우리를 보러 와.\""]], [["‘All right!’ said Tip, and went.", "\"알았어!\" 팁은 그렇게 말하고 떠났다."]], [["But not all the way to Canada; in fact, not further than Liverpool.", "그러나 캐나다까지 가지는 않았다. 사실 리버풀보다 더 멀리는 가지 못했다."], ["After making the voyage to that port from London, he found himself so strongly impelled to cut the vessel, that he resolved to walk back again.", "런던에서 그 항구까지 항해를 마치고 나니, 그는 배를 때려치우고 싶은 마음에 어찌나 강하게 떠밀렸던지, 도로 걸어서 돌아오기로 결심했다."], ["Carrying out which intention, he presented himself before her at the expiration of a month, in rags, without shoes, and much more tired than ever.", "그 결심을 실행에 옮겨, 그는 한 달이 지나서 누더기를 걸치고 신발도 없이, 그 어느 때보다 훨씬 더 지친 모습으로 그녀 앞에 나타났다."]], [["At length, after another interval of successorship to Mrs Bangham, he found a pursuit for himself, and announced it.", "마침내, 또 한 차례 뱅엄 부인의 뒤를 잇는 막간을 보낸 뒤, 그는 스스로 일거리를 하나 찾아내고는 그것을 알렸다."]], [["‘Amy, I have got a situation.’", "\"에이미, 나 일자리를 얻었어.\""]], [["‘Have you really and truly, Tip?’", "\"정말로, 참말로요, 팁?\""]], [["‘All right.", "\"틀림없어."], ["I shall do now.", "이제 잘 풀릴 거야."], ["You needn’t look anxious about me any more, old girl.’", "더는 나 때문에 걱정스러운 얼굴 할 필요 없어, 누이야.\""]], [["‘What is it, Tip?’", "\"그게 뭔데요, 팁?\""]], [["‘Why, you know Slingo by sight?’", "\"왜, 슬링고를 얼굴은 알지?\""]], [["‘Not the man they call the dealer?’", "\"거래꾼이라 부르는 그 사람 말고요?\""]], [["‘That’s the chap.", "\"바로 그 친구야."], ["He’ll be out on Monday, and he’s going to give me a berth.’", "월요일에 나가는데, 나한테 자리를 하나 줄 거야.\""]], [["‘What is he a dealer in, Tip?’", "\"그 사람이 뭘 거래하는데요, 팁?\""]], [["‘Horses.", "\"말."], ["All right!", "틀림없어!"], ["I shall do now, Amy.’", "이제 잘 풀릴 거야, 에이미.\""]], [["She lost sight of him for months afterwards, and only heard from him once.", "그 후 여러 달 동안 그녀는 그의 소식을 잃었고, 단 한 번 그에게서 연락을 받았을 뿐이다."], ["A whisper passed among the elder collegians that he had been seen at a mock auction in Moorfields, pretending to buy plated articles for massive silver, and paying for them with the greatest liberality in bank notes; but it never reached her ears.", "무어필즈의 어느 가짜 경매장에서 그가 도금된 물건을 묵직한 은제품이라며 사들이는 척하고, 더없이 후하게 은행권으로 값을 치르는 모습이 목격되었다는 소문이 나이 든 수용자들 사이에 떠돌았으나, 그것이 그녀의 귀에까지 닿지는 않았다."], ["One evening she was alone at work--standing up at the window, to save the twilight lingering above the wall--when he opened the door and walked in.", "어느 날 저녁 그녀는 홀로 일하고 있었는데—담장 위에 머무는 황혼빛을 아끼려고 창가에 서서—그가 문을 열고 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["She kissed and welcomed him; but was afraid to ask him any questions.", "그녀는 그에게 입을 맞추며 반겼으나, 무엇 하나 물어보기가 두려웠다."], ["He saw how anxious and timid she was, and appeared sorry.", "그는 그녀가 얼마나 걱정스럽고 겁에 질려 있는지 알아채고는 미안한 기색을 띠었다."]], [["‘I am afraid, Amy, you’ll be vexed this time.", "\"이번엔 네가 속상해할까 봐 걱정이야, 에이미."], ["Upon my life I am!’", "정말이지 그래!\""]], [["‘I am very sorry to hear you say so, Tip.", "\"그런 말을 들으니 무척 슬퍼요, 팁."], ["Have you come back?’", "돌아오신 거예요?\""]], [["‘Why--yes.’", "\"음—그래.\""]], [["‘Not expecting this time that what you had found would answer very well, I am less surprised and sorry than I might have been, Tip.’", "\"이번엔 찾아낸 일이 그리 잘 풀리리라고 기대하지 않았으니까, 그래도 그러려니 했던 것보다는 덜 놀랍고 덜 슬퍼요, 팁.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But that’s not the worst of it.’", "하지만 그게 최악은 아니야.\""]], [["‘Not the worst of it?’", "\"그게 최악이 아니라고요?\""]], [["‘Don’t look so startled.", "\"그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마."], ["No, Amy, not the worst of it.", "그래, 에이미, 그게 최악은 아니야."], ["I have come back, you see; but--_don’t_ look so startled--I have come back in what I may call a new way.", "보다시피 나는 돌아왔어. 하지만—제발 그렇게 놀란 얼굴 하지 마—나는 말하자면 새로운 방식으로 돌아왔어."], ["I am off the volunteer list altogether.", "자원자 명단에서는 아예 빠졌어."], ["I am in now, as one of the regulars.’", "이제 나는 정규병의 하나로 들어와 있는 거야.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Don’t say you are a prisoner, Tip!", "갇힌 사람이라고는 말하지 마요, 팁!"], ["Don’t, don’t!’", "제발, 제발!\""]], [["‘Well, I don’t want to say it,’ he returned in a reluctant tone; ‘but if you can’t understand me without my saying it, what am I to do?", "\"글쎄, 나도 그 말을 하고 싶진 않아,\" 그가 마지못한 어조로 대답했다."], ["I am in for forty pound odd.’", "\"하지만 내가 말하지 않고서는 네가 알아듣지 못하겠다면, 난 어쩌란 말이냐? 사십 파운드 남짓 때문에 들어왔어.\""]], [["For the first time in all those years, she sunk under her cares.", "그 모든 세월 동안 처음으로, 그녀는 자기 근심들의 무게에 무너져 내렸다."], ["She cried, with her clasped hands lifted above her head, that it would kill their father if he ever knew it; and fell down at Tip’s graceless feet.", "그녀는 맞잡은 두 손을 머리 위로 치켜든 채, 아버지가 이 일을 아시면 돌아가시고 말 거라고 울부짖었다. 그리고 팁의 그 한심한 발치에 쓰러졌다."]], [["It was easier for Tip to bring her to her senses than for her to bring _him_ to understand that the Father of the Marshalsea would be beside himself if he knew the truth.", "팁이 그녀를 정신 차리게 하기는, 그녀가 그에게 마셜시의 아버지가 진실을 알면 제정신이 아니게 되리라는 것을 이해시키기보다 쉬웠다."], ["The thing was incomprehensible to Tip, and altogether a fanciful notion.", "그 일은 팁으로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는, 한낱 허황된 관념이었다."], ["He yielded to it in that light only, when he submitted to her entreaties, backed by those of his uncle and sister.", "삼촌과 누이의 간청에 떠받쳐진 그녀의 애원에 굴복하면서도, 그는 오직 그런 관점에서만 그것에 따랐다."], ["There was no want of precedent for his return; it was accounted for to the father in the usual way; and the collegians, with a better comprehension of the pious fraud than Tip, supported it loyally.", "그가 돌아온 것에 대한 선례야 부족함이 없었고, 늘 하던 식으로 아버지에게 둘러대어졌으며, 수용자들은 팁보다 그 경건한 속임수를 더 잘 헤아려, 충실하게 그것을 거들어 주었다."]], [["This was the life, and this the history, of the child of the Marshalsea at twenty-two.", "이것이 스물둘이 된 마셜시의 아이의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다."], ["With a still surviving attachment to the one miserable yard and block of houses as her birthplace and home, she passed to and fro in it shrinkingly now, with a womanly consciousness that she was pointed out to every one.", "자기가 태어나 자란 곳이자 집인 그 비참한 마당과 집채 한 덩이에 여전히 애착이 남아 있었으나, 이제 그녀는 모든 이가 자기를 손가락질한다는 여인다운 자의식을 안은 채 그 안을 움츠리며 오갔다."], ["Since she had begun to work beyond the walls, she had found it necessary to conceal where she lived, and to come and go as secretly as she could, between the free city and the iron gates, outside of which she had never slept in her life.", "담장 너머에서 일하기 시작한 뒤로, 그녀는 자기가 사는 곳을 숨기고, 자유로운 도시와 쇠창살 문 사이를—그 바깥에서는 평생 한 번도 잠든 적이 없었다—될 수 있는 한 남몰래 드나들 필요가 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["Her original timidity had grown with this concealment, and her light step and her little figure shunned the thronged streets while they passed along them.", "그녀의 타고난 소심함은 이 은폐와 더불어 자라났고, 그녀의 가벼운 발걸음과 작은 몸집은 붐비는 거리를 지나는 동안에도 그 거리를 피해 다녔다."]], [["Worldly wise in hard and poor necessities, she was innocent in all things else.", "세상의 모진 가난과 궁핍에 대해서는 닳고 닳도록 영리했으나, 그 밖의 모든 것에 대해서는 순진했다."], ["Innocent, in the mist through which she saw her father, and the prison, and the turbid living river that flowed through it and flowed on.", "아버지를, 감옥을, 그리고 그 안을 흐르고 또 흘러가는 그 탁한 산 사람들의 강을 바라보던 그 안개 속에서 순진했다."]], [["This was the life, and this the history, of Little Dorrit; now going home upon a dull September evening, observed at a distance by Arthur Clennam.", "이것이 리틀 도릿의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다. 흐릿한 9월의 어느 저녁, 아서 클레넘에게 멀찍이서 지켜보이며 이제 집으로 돌아가던 그 리틀 도릿의."], ["This was the life, and this the history, of Little Dorrit; turning at the end of London Bridge, recrossing it, going back again, passing on to Saint George’s Church, turning back suddenly once more, and flitting in at the open outer gate and little court-yard of the Marshalsea.", "이것이 리틀 도릿의 삶이었고, 그 내력이었다. 런던 다리 끝에서 돌아서고, 다시 그 다리를 건너고, 또 되돌아가고, 세인트 조지 교회 쪽으로 나아가다가, 별안간 다시 한번 발길을 되돌려, 마셜시의 열린 바깥문과 작은 안마당으로 살그머니 들어서던 그 리틀 도릿의."]], [["# CHAPTER 8. The Lock", "# CHAPTER 8. The Lock"]], [["Arthur Clennam stood in the street, waiting to ask some passer-by what place that was.", "아서 클레넘은 거리에 서서, 지나가는 누군가에게 저곳이 어떤 곳인지 물어보려고 기다리고 있었다."], ["He suffered a few people to pass him in whose face there was no encouragement to make the inquiry, and still stood pausing in the street, when an old man came up and turned into the courtyard.", "그는 물어볼 만한 기색이 보이지 않는 사람 몇이 자기 곁을 지나치도록 내버려 두고, 여전히 거리에 멈춰 선 채 머뭇거리고 있었는데, 그때 한 노인이 다가와 안마당 쪽으로 꺾어 들어갔다."]], [["He stooped a good deal, and plodded along in a slow pre-occupied manner, which made the bustling London thoroughfares no very safe resort for him.", "노인은 몸을 잔뜩 구부정하게 굽힌 채, 무언가에 골몰한 듯 느릿느릿 터벅터벅 걸었으니, 그런 걸음걸이는 분주한 런던 큰길을 그에게 그다지 안전하지 못한 곳으로 만들었다."], ["He was dirtily and meanly dressed, in a threadbare coat, once blue, reaching to his ankles and buttoned to his chin, where it vanished in the pale ghost of a velvet collar.", "그는 지저분하고 초라한 차림이었는데, 한때는 푸른빛이었을 닳고 닳은 외투가 발목까지 내려와 턱밑까지 단추가 채워져 있었고, 거기서 그 외투는 벨벳 깃의 창백한 유령으로 사라져 버렸다."], ["A piece of red cloth with which that phantom had been stiffened in its lifetime was now laid bare, and poked itself up, at the back of the old man’s neck, into a confusion of grey hair and rusty stock and buckle which altogether nearly poked his hat off.", "살아 있을 적 그 유령에 빳빳함을 주던 붉은 천 조각은 이제 다 드러난 채로, 노인의 목덜미에서 위로 삐죽 솟아올라, 백발과 녹슨 듯한 목깃과 버클이 한데 뒤엉킨 속으로 파고들어, 그 모두가 그의 모자를 거의 벗겨 낼 지경이었다."], ["A greasy hat it was, and a napless; impending over his eyes, cracked and crumpled at the brim, and with a wisp of pocket-handkerchief dangling out below it.", "그것은 번들거리고 보풀이 다 빠진 모자로, 눈 위까지 처져 내려와 챙이 갈라지고 구겨졌으며, 그 아래로는 주머니 손수건 한 자락이 너덜너덜 늘어져 있었다."], ["His trousers were so long and loose, and his shoes so clumsy and large, that he shuffled like an elephant; though how much of this was gait, and how much trailing cloth and leather, no one could have told.", "바지는 어찌나 길고 헐렁한지, 또 구두는 어찌나 둔하고 큰지, 그는 코끼리처럼 발을 질질 끌며 걸었다. 다만 그중 얼마가 본래의 걸음걸이이고 얼마가 끌리는 천과 가죽 탓인지는 누구도 가늠할 수 없었으리라."], ["Under one arm he carried a limp and worn-out case, containing some wind instrument; in the same hand he had a pennyworth of snuff in a little packet of whitey-brown paper, from which he slowly comforted his poor blue old nose with a lengthened-out pinch, as Arthur Clennam looked at him.", "한쪽 팔 밑에는 어떤 관악기가 든, 흐물흐물하고 다 해진 케이스를 끼고 있었고, 같은 손에는 누르스름한 갈색 종이에 싼 작은 봉지에 든 한 푼어치 코담배를 들고 있어서, 아서 클레넘이 그를 바라보는 동안, 길게 한 줌 집어 가엾고 파리한 늙은 코를 천천히 달래고 있었다."]], [["To this old man crossing the court-yard, he preferred his inquiry, touching him on the shoulder.", "안마당을 가로지르는 이 노인에게 그는 어깨를 가볍게 두드리며 물음을 건넸다."], ["The old man stopped and looked round, with the expression in his weak grey eyes of one whose thoughts had been far off, and who was a little dull of hearing also.", "노인은 걸음을 멈추고 돌아보았는데, 그 흐릿한 잿빛 눈에는 생각이 멀리 가 있던 사람, 게다가 귀도 좀 어두운 사람의 표정이 떠올라 있었다."]], [["‘Pray, sir,’ said Arthur, repeating his question, ‘what is this place?’", "\"실례합니다만,\" 아서가 질문을 되풀이하며 말했다. \"이곳은 어떤 곳입니까?\""]], [["‘Ay!", "\"아!"], ["This place?’ returned the old man, staying his pinch of snuff on its road, and pointing at the place without looking at it.", "이곳 말이오?\" 노인은 코담배 한 줌을 코로 가져가던 도중에 멈추고, 그곳을 쳐다보지도 않은 채 손으로 가리키며 대답했다."], ["‘This is the Marshalsea, sir.’", "\"여기는 마셜시요, 선생.\""]], [["‘The debtors’ prison?’", "\"채무자 감옥 말입니까?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said the old man, with the air of deeming it not quite necessary to insist upon that designation, ‘the debtors’ prison.’", "\"선생,\" 노인은 굳이 그런 명칭까지 고집할 필요는 없다고 여기는 듯한 태도로 말했다. \"채무자 감옥이오.\""]], [["He turned himself about, and went on.", "그는 몸을 돌려 다시 걸어가려 했다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ said Arthur, stopping him once more, ‘but will you allow me to ask you another question?", "\"죄송합니다.\" 아서가 그를 한 번 더 멈춰 세우며 말했다."], ["Can any one go in here?’", "\"한 가지만 더 여쭤도 되겠습니까? 여기는 아무나 들어갈 수 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Any one can _go in_,’ replied the old man; plainly adding by the significance of his emphasis, ‘but it is not every one who can go out.’", "\"아무나 들어갈 수는 있소.\" 노인은 대답하면서, 그 강조하는 말투의 의미로 분명히 이렇게 덧붙이고 있었다. \"하지만 아무나 나올 수 있는 건 아니지.\""]], [["‘Pardon me once more.", "\"다시 한번 양해를 구합니다."], ["Are you familiar with the place?’", "이곳을 잘 아십니까?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ returned the old man, squeezing his little packet of snuff in his hand, and turning upon his interrogator as if such questions hurt him.", "\"선생,\" 노인은 작은 코담배 봉지를 손에 꽉 움켜쥐고는, 그런 질문이 자기를 아프게 하기라도 하는 듯 묻는 이를 향해 돌아서며 대답했다."], ["‘I am.’", "\"잘 아오.\""]], [["‘I beg you to excuse me.", "\"부디 용서하십시오."], ["I am not impertinently curious, but have a good object.", "무례하게 호기심을 부리려는 게 아니라 좋은 뜻이 있어서입니다."], ["Do you know the name of Dorrit here?’", "혹시 여기 도릿이라는 이름을 아십니까?\""]], [["‘My name, sir,’ replied the old man most unexpectedly, ‘is Dorrit.’", "\"내 이름이,\" 노인이 너무도 뜻밖에 대답했다. \"도릿이오, 선생.\""]], [["Arthur pulled off his hat to him.", "아서는 그를 향해 모자를 벗었다."], ["‘Grant me the favour of half-a-dozen words.", "\"몇 마디만 나눌 기회를 베풀어 주십시오."], ["I was wholly unprepared for your announcement, and hope that assurance is my sufficient apology for having taken the liberty of addressing you.", "방금 하신 말씀에 저는 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았습니다. 부디 그 말씀이, 제가 무례하게 말을 건넨 데 대한 충분한 변명이 되기를 바랍니다."], ["I have recently come home to England after a long absence.", "저는 오랜 부재 끝에 최근 영국으로 돌아왔습니다."], ["I have seen at my mother’s--Mrs Clennam in the city--a young woman working at her needle, whom I have only heard addressed or spoken of as Little Dorrit.", "시내에 계신 제 어머니, 클레넘 부인 댁에서 바느질을 하고 있는 한 젊은 여인을 보았는데, 그저 리틀 도릿이라고 불리거나 그렇게 언급되는 것만 들었습니다."], ["I have felt sincerely interested in her, and have had a great desire to know something more about her.", "저는 그 여인에게 진심으로 마음이 쓰였고, 그녀에 관해 좀 더 알고 싶은 마음이 간절했습니다."], ["I saw her, not a minute before you came up, pass in at that door.’", "어르신께서 오시기 한순간 전에, 저는 그 여인이 저 문으로 들어가는 것을 보았습니다.\""]], [["The old man looked at him attentively.", "노인은 그를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["‘Are you a sailor, sir?’ he asked.", "\"선생은 뱃사람이오?\""], ["He seemed a little disappointed by the shake of the head that replied to him.", "그가 물었다. 고개를 가로젓는 대답에 그는 조금 실망한 듯했다."], ["‘Not a sailor?", "\"뱃사람이 아니오?"], ["I judged from your sunburnt face that you might be.", "햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 보고 그런가 했소만."], ["Are you in earnest, sir?’", "선생, 진심으로 하는 말이오?\""]], [["‘I do assure you that I am, and do entreat you to believe that I am, in plain earnest.’", "\"진심임을 확실히 말씀드립니다. 그리고 제가 그저 솔직하고 진실하게 말씀드리는 것임을 믿어 주시기를 간청합니다.\""]], [["‘I know very little of the world, sir,’ returned the other, who had a weak and quavering voice.", "\"나는 세상을 잘 모르오, 선생.\" 가냘프고 떨리는 목소리를 지닌 상대가 대답했다."], ["‘I am merely passing on, like the shadow over the sun-dial.", "\"나는 그저 해시계 위를 지나가는 그림자처럼 흘러가고 있을 뿐이오."], ["It would be worth no man’s while to mislead me; it would really be too easy--too poor a success, to yield any satisfaction.", "나를 속이는 일은 누구에게도 보람이 없을 거요. 너무도 쉬운 일일 테고—너무도 보잘것없는 성공이라 아무런 만족도 주지 못할 테니까."], ["The young woman whom you saw go in here is my brother’s child.", "선생이 이리 들어가는 걸 본 그 젊은 여인은 내 형의 딸이오."], ["My brother is William Dorrit; I am Frederick.", "내 형은 윌리엄 도릿이고, 나는 프레더릭이오."], ["You say you have seen her at your mother’s (I know your mother befriends her), you have felt an interest in her, and you wish to know what she does here.", "선생이 그 애를 어머니 댁에서 보았다고 하니(어머니가 그 애를 거두어 주시는 건 나도 알고 있소), 그 애에게 관심을 두게 되었고, 그 애가 여기서 무얼 하는지 알고 싶다는 거로구려."], ["Come and see.’", "따라와서 보시오.\""]], [["He went on again, and Arthur accompanied him.", "그는 다시 걸음을 옮겼고, 아서가 그와 함께 걸었다."]], [["‘My brother,’ said the old man, pausing on the step and slowly facing round again, ‘has been here many years; and much that happens even among ourselves, out of doors, is kept from him for reasons that I needn’t enter upon now.", "\"내 형은,\" 노인이 계단 위에 멈춰 서서 느릿느릿 다시 돌아서며 말했다."], ["Be so good as to say nothing of my niece’s working at her needle.", "\"여기서 여러 해를 지냈소. 그리고 우리끼리, 바깥에서 벌어지는 일들 가운데서도, 지금 내가 굳이 들추지 않겠소만, 여러 이유로 형에게는 알리지 않는 것이 많소."], ["Be so good as to say nothing that goes beyond what is said among us.", "부디 내 조카가 바느질을 한다는 이야기는 한마디도 하지 말아 주시오. 우리끼리 하는 말 이상으로 나가는 이야기는 한마디도 하지 말아 주시오."], ["If you keep within our bounds, you cannot well be wrong.", "우리가 정한 선 안에만 머문다면 크게 어긋날 일은 없을 거요."], ["Now!", "자!"], ["Come and see.’", "따라와서 보시오.\""]], [["Arthur followed him down a narrow entry, at the end of which a key was turned, and a strong door was opened from within.", "아서는 노인을 따라 좁은 통로를 내려갔는데, 그 끝에서 열쇠가 돌아가더니 안쪽에서 튼튼한 문이 열렸다."], ["It admitted them into a lodge or lobby, across which they passed, and so through another door and a grating into the prison.", "그 문은 그들을 수위실인지 현관인지 하는 곳으로 들였고, 그들은 그곳을 가로질러, 또 다른 문과 쇠창살을 지나 감옥 안으로 들어섰다."], ["The old man always plodding on before, turned round, in his slow, stiff, stooping manner, when they came to the turnkey on duty, as if to present his companion.", "늘 앞장서서 터벅터벅 걷던 노인은, 당번을 서는 간수에게 이르렀을 때, 그 느리고 뻣뻣하며 구부정한 동작으로 돌아서서, 마치 자기 동행을 소개하기라도 하듯 했다."], ["The turnkey nodded; and the companion passed in without being asked whom he wanted.", "간수는 고개를 끄덕였고, 동행은 누구를 만나러 왔는지 묻는 것도 없이 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["The night was dark; and the prison lamps in the yard, and the candles in the prison windows faintly shining behind many sorts of wry old curtain and blind, had not the air of making it lighter.", "밤은 어두웠다. 마당의 감옥 등불과, 온갖 비뚤어진 낡은 커튼과 가리개 뒤로 희미하게 비치는 감옥 창문의 촛불들은, 그곳을 더 밝게 만드는 기색이 전혀 없었다."], ["A few people loitered about, but the greater part of the population was within doors.", "몇 사람이 어슬렁거리고 있었으나, 거주민 대부분은 안에 들어가 있었다."], ["The old man, taking the right-hand side of the yard, turned in at the third or fourth doorway, and began to ascend the stairs.", "노인은 마당의 오른편으로 들어서서, 서너 번째 문간에서 꺾어 들어가, 계단을 오르기 시작했다."], ["‘They are rather dark, sir, but you will not find anything in the way.’", "\"좀 어둡소, 선생. 그래도 길에 걸리는 건 없을 거요.\""]], [["He paused for a moment before opening a door on the second story.", "그는 이층의 어느 문을 열기 전에 잠시 멈췄다."], ["He had no sooner turned the handle than the visitor saw Little Dorrit, and saw the reason of her setting so much store by dining alone.", "그가 손잡이를 돌리기 무섭게, 방문객은 리틀 도릿을 보았고, 그녀가 혼자 식사하는 것을 그토록 중히 여긴 까닭을 알았다."]], [["She had brought the meat home that she should have eaten herself, and was already warming it on a gridiron over the fire for her father, clad in an old grey gown and a black cap, awaiting his supper at the table.", "그녀는 자기가 먹었어야 할 고기를 집으로 가져와서, 이미 그것을 석쇠에 올려 불 위에서 데우고 있었는데, 이는 낡은 회색 가운에 검은 모자를 쓰고 식탁에서 저녁을 기다리는 아버지를 위해서였다."], ["A clean cloth was spread before him, with knife, fork, and spoon, salt-cellar, pepper-box, glass, and pewter ale-pot.", "그의 앞에는 깨끗한 천이 펼쳐져 있었고, 나이프와 포크와 숟가락, 소금 그릇, 후추통, 유리잔, 그리고 백랍 맥주잔이 놓여 있었다."], ["Such zests as his particular little phial of cayenne pepper and his pennyworth of pickles in a saucer, were not wanting.", "그가 특별히 챙기는 카옌 고춧가루 작은 병이나, 받침 접시에 담긴 한 푼어치 피클 같은 입맛 돋우는 것들도 빠지지 않았다."]], [["She started, coloured deeply, and turned white.", "그녀는 흠칫하더니, 얼굴이 새빨개졌다가 다시 하얗게 질렸다."], ["The visitor, more with his eyes than by the slight impulsive motion of his hand, entreated her to be reassured and to trust him.", "방문객은 손을 가볍게 충동적으로 움직였다기보다 눈빛으로, 안심하고 자기를 믿어 달라고 그녀에게 청했다."]], [["‘I found this gentleman,’ said the uncle--‘Mr Clennam, William, son of Amy’s friend--at the outer gate, wishful, as he was going by, of paying his respects, but hesitating whether to come in or not. This is my brother William, sir.’", "\"이분을 바깥문에서 만났네.\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"클레넘 씨라네, 윌리엄, 에이미 친구분의 아드님이지. 지나가던 길에 인사를 드리고 싶어 하셨는데, 들어올까 말까 망설이고 계시더군. 이쪽이 제 형 윌리엄입니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘I hope,’ said Arthur, very doubtful what to say, ‘that my respect for your daughter may explain and justify my desire to be presented to you, sir.’", "\"바라건대,\" 아서가 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 망설이며 말했다. \"따님에 대한 제 존경이, 어르신께 소개되고 싶었던 제 바람을 설명하고 정당화해 주기를 바랍니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ returned the other, rising, taking his cap off in the flat of his hand, and so holding it, ready to put on again, ‘you do me honour.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 상대가 일어서서, 손바닥으로 모자를 받쳐 벗어 들고는, 다시 쓸 채비를 한 채 그것을 들고 있으며 대답했다."], ["You are welcome, sir;’ with a low bow.", "\"제게 영광입니다. 환영합니다, 선생.\""], ["‘Frederick, a chair.", "그는 깊이 고개를 숙였다."], ["Pray sit down, Mr Clennam.’", "\"프레더릭, 의자를 좀. 부디 앉으시지요, 클레넘 씨.\""]], [["He put his black cap on again as he had taken it off, and resumed his own seat.", "그는 벗을 때와 똑같이 검은 모자를 다시 쓰고, 자기 자리에 도로 앉았다."], ["There was a wonderful air of benignity and patronage in his manner.", "그의 태도에는 놀랄 만큼 자애롭고 너그러이 베푸는 듯한 분위기가 어려 있었다."], ["These were the ceremonies with which he received the collegians.", "이것이 바로 그가 동료 수감자들을 맞이할 때 행하던 의례였다."]], [["‘You are welcome to the Marshalsea, sir.", "\"마셜시에 잘 오셨습니다, 선생."], ["I have welcomed many gentlemen to these walls.", "저는 이 담장 안으로 많은 신사분을 맞이해 왔지요."], ["Perhaps you are aware--my daughter Amy may have mentioned that I am the Father of this place.’", "아마 아실 겁니다만—제 딸 에이미가 말씀드렸을지도 모르겠습니다만, 제가 이곳의 아버지입니다.\""]], [["‘I--so I have understood,’ said Arthur, dashing at the assertion.", "\"저—그렇게 알고 있습니다.\" 아서가 그 단언에 황급히 맞장구쳤다."]], [["‘You know, I dare say, that my daughter Amy was born here.", "\"제 딸 에이미가 여기서 태어났다는 것은 아마 아실 겁니다."], ["A good girl, sir, a dear girl, and long a comfort and support to me.", "착한 아이지요, 선생, 사랑스러운 아이입니다. 오랫동안 제게 위안이자 버팀목이 되어 주었지요."], ["Amy, my dear, put this dish on; Mr Clennam will excuse the primitive customs to which we are reduced here.", "에이미야, 이 접시를 좀 올려 다오. 클레넘 씨, 우리가 여기서 어쩔 수 없이 따르는 이 소박한 방식을 너그러이 보아주실 겁니다."], ["Is it a compliment to ask you if you would do me the honour, sir, to--’", "선생, 영광스럽게도 함께—권해 드린다면 실례가 될까요—\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ returned Arthur.", "\"고맙습니다.\" 아서가 대답했다."], ["‘Not a morsel.’", "\"한 입도 됐습니다.\""]], [["He felt himself quite lost in wonder at the manner of the man, and that the probability of his daughter’s having had a reserve as to her family history, should be so far out of his mind.", "그는 이 사람의 태도에, 또 그의 딸이 가문 내력에 관해 무언가를 숨겨 왔을 가능성이 그의 머릿속에서 그토록 멀리 동떨어져 있다는 데에, 그저 어리둥절할 따름이었다."]], [["She filled his glass, put all the little matters on the table ready to his hand, and then sat beside him while he ate his supper.", "그녀는 그의 잔을 채우고, 자질구레한 것들을 모두 그의 손 닿는 자리에 챙겨 놓고는, 그가 저녁을 드는 동안 곁에 앉았다."], ["Evidently in observance of their nightly custom, she put some bread before herself, and touched his glass with her lips; but Arthur saw she was troubled and took nothing.", "분명 밤마다 지키는 그들만의 관례에 따라, 그녀는 자기 앞에 빵을 조금 놓고, 그의 잔에 입술을 댔으나, 아서가 보기에 그녀는 마음이 불편한 듯 아무것도 입에 대지 않았다."], ["Her look at her father, half admiring him and proud of him, half ashamed for him, all devoted and loving, went to his inmost heart.", "아버지를 바라보는 그녀의 눈길은, 반쯤은 그를 우러르고 그를 자랑스러워하면서, 반쯤은 그를 대신해 부끄러워하는, 온통 헌신과 사랑이 어린 그 눈길은, 그의 가슴 깊은 곳까지 파고들었다."]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea condescended towards his brother as an amiable, well-meaning man; a private character, who had not arrived at distinction.", "마셜시의 아버지는 자기 동생을, 사람 좋고 선의는 있으나 두각을 나타내지 못한 평범한 인물로 여겨 너그러이 대했다. \"프레더릭,\""], ["‘Frederick,’ said he, ‘you and Fanny sup at your lodgings to-night, I know.", "그가 말했다. \"오늘 밤 너와 패니가 너희 거처에서 저녁을 먹는다지."], ["What have you done with Fanny, Frederick?’", "패니는 어쩌고 왔느냐, 프레더릭?\""]], [["‘She is walking with Tip.’", "\"팁하고 산책하고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Tip--as you may know--is my son, Mr Clennam.", "\"팁은—아실지 모르겠습니다만—제 아들입니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["He has been a little wild, and difficult to settle, but his introduction to the world was rather’--he shrugged his shoulders with a faint sigh, and looked round the room--‘a little adverse.", "좀 거칠고 자리를 잡기 어려운 아이였습니다만, 세상에 발을 들이는 출발이 좀—\" 그는 희미하게 한숨을 쉬며 어깨를 으쓱하고는 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["Your first visit here, sir?’", "\"—좀 불운했지요. 선생은 여기 처음 오시는 길입니까?\""]], [["‘My first.’", "\"처음입니다.\""]], [["‘You could hardly have been here since your boyhood without my knowledge.", "\"제가 모르는 채로 어릴 적부터 여기 드나드셨을 리는 없겠지요."], ["It very seldom happens that anybody--of any pretensions--any pretensions--comes here without being presented to me.’", "누구든—이렇다 할 지체가 있는 분이라면—이렇다 할 지체가 있는 분이라면—제게 소개되지 않고 이곳에 오는 일은 좀처럼 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘As many as forty or fifty in a day have been introduced to my brother,’ said Frederick, faintly lighting up with a ray of pride.", "\"하루에 마흔, 쉰 명씩 형님께 소개된 적도 있었습니다.\" 프레더릭이 한 줄기 자랑스러움으로 희미하게 얼굴을 밝히며 말했다."]], [["‘Yes!’ the Father of the Marshalsea assented.", "\"그렇지!\" 마셜시의 아버지가 동의했다."], ["‘We have even exceeded that number.", "\"그 수를 넘긴 적도 있었지요."], ["On a fine Sunday in term time, it is quite a Levee--quite a Levee.", "개정기의 화창한 일요일이면, 그야말로 알현식 같습니다—그야말로 알현식이지요."], ["Amy, my dear, I have been trying half the day to remember the name of the gentleman from Camberwell who was introduced to me last Christmas week by that agreeable coal-merchant who was remanded for six months.’", "에이미야, 작년 성탄절 주간에, 그 반년 구류형을 받은 사람 좋은 석탄 상인이 내게 소개해 준 캠버웰에서 온 그 신사분 이름을 떠올리려고 오늘 한나절을 애썼단다.\""]], [["‘I don’t remember his name, father.’", "\"이름은 기억나지 않아요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Frederick, do _you_ remember his name?’", "\"프레더릭, 너는 그 이름이 기억나느냐?\""]], [["Frederick doubted if he had ever heard it.", "프레더릭은 그 이름을 들어 본 적이나 있는지 의심스러워했다."], ["No one could doubt that Frederick was the last person upon earth to put such a question to, with any hope of information.", "무언가 정보를 얻으리라는 희망을 품고 그런 질문을 던질 상대로 프레더릭만큼 어울리지 않는 사람이 이 세상에 또 없으리라는 데에는 누구도 의심의 여지가 없었다."]], [["‘I mean,’ said his brother, ‘the gentleman who did that handsome action with so much delicacy.", "\"내 말은,\" 형이 말했다."], ["Ha! Tush!", "\"그토록 섬세하게 그 훌륭한 선행을 베푼 그 신사분 말이다."], ["The name has quite escaped me.", "허! 쳇!"], ["Mr Clennam, as I have happened to mention handsome and delicate action, you may like, perhaps, to know what it was.’", "이름이 영 떠오르질 않는구나. 클레넘 씨, 마침 훌륭하고 섬세한 선행 이야기가 나왔으니, 어쩌면 그게 무엇이었는지 알고 싶으실지도 모르겠군요.\""]], [["‘Very much,’ said Arthur, withdrawing his eyes from the delicate head beginning to droop and the pale face with a new solicitude stealing over it.", "\"무척 알고 싶습니다.\" 아서가 말했는데, 그는 점점 수그러들기 시작하는 그 가녀린 머리와, 새로운 근심이 슬며시 번지는 그 창백한 얼굴에서 눈길을 거두며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["‘It is so generous, and shows so much fine feeling, that it is almost a duty to mention it.", "\"그것은 참으로 너그럽고, 참으로 고운 마음을 보여 주는 일이라, 그 이야기를 하는 것이 거의 의무라 하겠습니다."], ["I said at the time that I always would mention it on every suitable occasion, without regard to personal sensitiveness.", "저는 그때, 개인적인 거북함은 개의치 않고, 적절한 기회가 있을 때마다 늘 이 이야기를 하겠노라고 말했지요."], ["A--well--a--it’s of no use to disguise the fact--you must know, Mr Clennam, that it does sometimes occur that people who come here desire to offer some little--Testimonial--to the Father of the place.’", "그—저—그—사실을 감출 필요가 없겠습니다만—클레넘 씨, 아셔야 할 것은, 이곳에 오는 분들이 가끔 이곳의 아버지에게 약간의—사례를—드리고자 하는 일이 더러 있다는 겁니다.\""]], [["To see her hand upon his arm in mute entreaty half-repressed, and her timid little shrinking figure turning away, was to see a sad, sad sight.", "말없이 반쯤 억눌린 애원으로 그의 팔에 얹힌 그녀의 손과, 겁먹은 채 움츠러든 작은 몸이 외면하듯 돌아서는 것을 보는 일은, 참으로 슬프고 슬픈 광경을 보는 일이었다."]], [["‘Sometimes,’ he went on in a low, soft voice, agitated, and clearing his throat every now and then; ‘sometimes--hem--it takes one shape and sometimes another; but it is generally--ha--Money.", "\"가끔은,\" 그는 동요하여, 이따금 헛기침을 하면서, 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 이어 갔다."], ["And it is, I cannot but confess it, it is too often--hem--acceptable.", "\"가끔은—흠—이런 모양을 띠기도 하고, 또 가끔은 저런 모양을 띠기도 합니다만, 대체로는—허—돈이지요. 그리고 고백하지 않을 수 없습니다만, 그것은 너무도 자주—흠—고맙게 받아들여집니다."], ["This gentleman that I refer to, was presented to me, Mr Clennam, in a manner highly gratifying to my feelings, and conversed not only with great politeness, but with great--ahem--information.’ All this time, though he had finished his supper, he was nervously going about his plate with his knife and fork, as if some of it were still before him.", "제가 말씀드리는 그 신사분은, 클레넘 씨, 제 마음에 더없이 흡족한 방식으로 제게 소개되었고, 대단히 정중할 뿐만 아니라 대단히—에헴—식견 있게 대화를 나누었지요.\" 이 모든 이야기를 하는 내내, 그는 저녁을 다 먹었는데도, 마치 아직 음식이 남아 있기라도 한 듯, 나이프와 포크로 자기 접시 위를 신경질적으로 더듬고 있었다."], ["‘It appeared from his conversation that he had a garden, though he was delicate of mentioning it at first, as gardens are--hem--are not accessible to me.", "\"대화 중에 그분에게 정원이 있다는 것이 드러났습니다. 처음에는 그 이야기를 꺼내기를 조심스러워하셨지요."], ["But it came out, through my admiring a very fine cluster of geranium--beautiful cluster of geranium to be sure--which he had brought from his conservatory.", "정원이란—흠—제게는 닿을 수 없는 것이니까요. 하지만 그분이 자기 온실에서 가져온 아주 고운 제라늄 한 송이—정말이지 아름다운 제라늄 송이를 제가 감탄하는 바람에 그 일이 밝혀졌습니다."], ["On my taking notice of its rich colour, he showed me a piece of paper round it, on which was written, “For the Father of the Marshalsea,” and presented it to me.", "제가 그 짙은 빛깔을 두고 한마디 하자, 그분은 그것을 감싸고 있던 종이 한 장을 보여 주셨는데, 거기에는 '마셜시의 아버지께'라고 적혀 있었고, 그것을 제게 건네셨지요."], ["But this was--hem--not all.", "그런데 그게—흠—다가 아니었습니다."], ["He made a particular request, on taking leave, that I would remove the paper in half an hour.", "그분은 떠나시면서, 반 시간 뒤에 그 종이를 벗겨 보라고 특별히 당부하셨습니다."], ["I--ha--I did so; and I found that it contained--ahem--two guineas.", "저는—허—그렇게 했고, 그 안에—에헴—이 기니가 들어 있는 것을 발견했지요."], ["I assure you, Mr Clennam, I have received--hem--Testimonials in many ways, and of many degrees of value, and they have always been--ha--unfortunately acceptable; but I never was more pleased than with this--ahem--this particular Testimonial.’", "클레넘 씨, 분명히 말씀드립니다만, 저는—흠—여러 방식으로, 또 여러 정도의 값어치로 사례를 받아 왔고, 그것들은 늘—허—유감스럽게도 고맙게 받아들여졌습니다. 하지만 이—에헴—이 특별한 사례만큼 제가 더 기뻐한 적은 없었습니다.\""]], [["Arthur was in the act of saying the little he could say on such a theme, when a bell began to ring, and footsteps approached the door.", "아서가 그런 주제에 대해 자기가 할 수 있는 짧은 말을 막 꺼내려던 참에, 종이 울리기 시작하고 문 쪽으로 발걸음이 다가왔다."], ["A pretty girl of a far better figure and much more developed than Little Dorrit, though looking much younger in the face when the two were observed together, stopped in the doorway on seeing a stranger; and a young man who was with her, stopped too.", "리틀 도릿보다 훨씬 좋은 몸매에 훨씬 성숙한, 둘을 나란히 놓고 보면 얼굴은 훨씬 어려 보이는 어여쁜 처녀가, 낯선 사람을 보고는 문간에 멈춰 섰고, 그녀와 함께 온 청년도 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, Fanny.", "\"클레넘 씨, 패니다."], ["My eldest daughter and my son, Mr Clennam.", "제 맏딸과 아들입니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["The bell is a signal for visitors to retire, and so they have come to say good night; but there is plenty of time, plenty of time.", "저 종은 방문객더러 물러가라는 신호인데, 그래서 작별 인사를 하러 온 거지요. 하지만 시간은 넉넉합니다, 넉넉해요."], ["Girls, Mr Clennam will excuse any household business you may have together.", "얘들아, 너희끼리 처리할 집안일이 있더라도 클레넘 씨가 양해해 주실 거다."], ["He knows, I dare say, that I have but one room here.’", "이분도 아마 아실 게다, 내가 여기서 방 하나밖에 쓰지 못한다는 걸.\""]], [["‘I only want my clean dress from Amy, father,’ said the second girl.", "\"전 그저 에이미한테서 제 깨끗한 옷을 받으러 온 거예요, 아버지.\" 둘째 딸이 말했다."]], [["‘And I my clothes,’ said Tip.", "\"저는 제 옷을요.\" 팁이 말했다."]], [["Amy opened a drawer in an old piece of furniture that was a chest of drawers above and a bedstead below, and produced two little bundles, which she handed to her brother and sister.", "에이미는 위는 서랍장이고 아래는 침대 틀로 된 낡은 가구의 서랍 하나를 열어, 작은 꾸러미 두 개를 꺼내 남동생과 언니에게 건넸다. \"고치고 손질해 놨어?\""], ["‘Mended and made up?’ Clennam heard the sister ask in a whisper.", "언니가 속삭이듯 묻는 것을 클레넘은 들었다. 거기에 에이미는 \"응\""], ["To which Amy answered ‘Yes.’ He had risen now, and took the opportunity of glancing round the room.", "하고 대답했다. 그는 이제 일어선 참이라, 그 틈을 타 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["The bare walls had been coloured green, evidently by an unskilled hand, and were poorly decorated with a few prints.", "휑한 벽은 초록색으로 칠해져 있었는데, 분명 서툰 손길로 한 것이었고, 판화 몇 점으로 빈약하게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["The window was curtained, and the floor carpeted; and there were shelves and pegs, and other such conveniences, that had accumulated in the course of years.", "창에는 커튼이 드리워져 있고 바닥에는 양탄자가 깔려 있었으며, 여러 해를 지나는 동안 쌓인 선반과 못걸이를 비롯한 자질구레한 살림 도구들이 있었다. 그곳은 답답하고 비좁은 방이었고, 가구도 변변치 않았다."], ["It was a close, confined room, poorly furnished; and the chimney smoked to boot, or the tin screen at the top of the fireplace was superfluous; but constant pains and care had made it neat, and even, after its kind, comfortable.", "게다가 굴뚝은 연기를 내뿜었으니, 그렇지 않다면 벽난로 위에 달린 양철 가리개가 쓸데없는 것이었으리라. 그러나 끊임없는 정성과 보살핌이 그 방을 깔끔하게, 그 나름으로는 아늑하기까지 하게 만들어 놓았다."]], [["All the while the bell was ringing, and the uncle was anxious to go.", "그 내내 종은 울리고 있었고, 삼촌은 어서 가고 싶어 안달이었다. \"어서, 패니, 어서, 패니.\""], ["‘Come, Fanny, come, Fanny,’ he said, with his ragged clarionet case under his arm; ‘the lock, child, the lock!’", "그는 너덜너덜한 클라리넷 케이스를 팔 밑에 낀 채 말했다. \"자물쇠다, 얘야, 자물쇠!\""]], [["Fanny bade her father good night, and whisked off airily.", "패니는 아버지에게 잘 자라고 인사하고는 사뿐히 휙 빠져나갔다."], ["Tip had already clattered down-stairs.", "팁은 이미 계단을 요란히 내려간 뒤였다. \"자, 클레넘 씨.\""], ["‘Now, Mr Clennam,’ said the uncle, looking back as he shuffled out after them, ‘the lock, sir, the lock.’", "삼촌이 그들을 따라 발을 끌며 나가다 뒤를 돌아보며 말했다. \"자물쇠입니다, 선생, 자물쇠.\""]], [["Mr Clennam had two things to do before he followed; one, to offer his testimonial to the Father of the Marshalsea, without giving pain to his child; the other to say something to that child, though it were but a word, in explanation of his having come there.", "클레넘 씨는 따라 나서기 전에 해야 할 일이 두 가지 있었다. 하나는 그의 딸에게 마음의 상처를 주지 않으면서 마셜시의 아버지에게 사례를 건네는 일이었고, 다른 하나는 그 딸에게, 단 한마디라도, 자기가 이곳에 온 까닭을 설명할 무언가를 말하는 일이었다."]], [["‘Allow me,’ said the Father, ‘to see you down-stairs.’", "\"제가,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"아래층까지 배웅해 드리지요.\""]], [["She had slipped out after the rest, and they were alone.", "그녀는 다른 사람들을 뒤따라 슬며시 빠져나간 뒤라, 그들 둘만 남아 있었다."], ["‘Not on any account,’ said the visitor, hurriedly.", "\"절대 그러지 마십시오.\" 방문객이 황급히 말했다."], ["‘Pray allow me to--’ chink, chink, chink.", "\"부디 제가—\" 짤랑, 짤랑, 짤랑."]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ said the Father, ‘I am deeply, deeply--’ But his visitor had shut up his hand to stop the clinking, and had gone down-stairs with great speed.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 아버지가 말했다. \"제가 깊이, 참으로 깊이—\" 그러나 그의 방문객은 짤랑거리는 소리를 멈추려 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 무서운 속도로 계단을 내려가 버린 뒤였다."]], [["He saw no Little Dorrit on his way down, or in the yard.", "내려가는 길에도, 마당에서도 그는 리틀 도릿을 보지 못했다."], ["The last two or three stragglers were hurrying to the lodge, and he was following, when he caught sight of her in the doorway of the first house from the entrance.", "마지막 두세 명의 낙오자들이 수위실로 서둘러 가고 있었고, 그도 그 뒤를 따르려던 참에, 입구에서 첫 번째 집 문간에 서 있는 그녀가 눈에 들어왔다."], ["He turned back hastily.", "그는 황급히 발길을 돌렸다."]], [["‘Pray forgive me,’ he said, ‘for speaking to you here; pray forgive me for coming here at all!", "\"부디 용서하십시오.\" 그가 말했다."], ["I followed you to-night.", "\"이런 곳에서 말을 거는 걸 용서하십시오."], ["I did so, that I might endeavour to render you and your family some service.", "아니, 제가 이곳에 온 것 자체를 용서하십시오! 저는 오늘 밤 당신을 따라왔습니다."], ["You know the terms on which I and my mother are, and may not be surprised that I have preserved our distant relations at her house, lest I should unintentionally make her jealous, or resentful, or do you any injury in her estimation.", "그렇게 한 것은, 당신과 당신 가족에게 어떤 도움이라도 드리고자 애써 보려 함이었습니다. 저와 제 어머니가 어떤 사이인지는 당신도 아실 테니, 제가 어머니 댁에서 우리의 서먹한 관계를 그대로 지켜 온 것을 이상히 여기지 않으실 겁니다."], ["What I have seen here, in this short time, has greatly increased my heartfelt wish to be a friend to you.", "무심코 어머니를 시샘하게 하거나 원망하게 하거나, 어머니가 당신을 낮춰 보아 당신에게 해를 끼치는 일이 없도록 한 것이지요. 이 짧은 시간 동안 여기서 본 것들이, 당신의 벗이 되고 싶다는 제 진심 어린 바람을 크게 더해 주었습니다."], ["It would recompense me for much disappointment if I could hope to gain your confidence.’", "당신의 신뢰를 얻을 수 있기를 바랄 수 있다면, 그것은 제게 숱한 실망을 보상해 줄 것입니다.\""]], [["She was scared at first, but seemed to take courage while he spoke to her.", "그녀는 처음에는 겁을 먹었으나, 그가 말하는 동안 차츰 용기를 내는 듯했다."]], [["‘You are very good, sir.", "\"참 친절하십니다, 선생."], ["You speak very earnestly to me.", "제게 무척 진지하게 말씀해 주시는군요."], ["But I--but I wish you had not watched me.’", "하지만 저는—하지만 저를 지켜보지 않으셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""]], [["He understood the emotion with which she said it, to arise in her father’s behalf; and he respected it, and was silent.", "그는 그녀가 그렇게 말할 때 어린 감정이 아버지를 위한 것임을 알아차렸고, 그것을 존중하여 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘Mrs Clennam has been of great service to me; I don’t know what we should have done without the employment she has given me; I am afraid it may not be a good return to become secret with her; I can say no more to-night, sir.", "\"클레넘 부인은 제게 큰 도움을 주셨어요. 그분이 주신 일감이 없었더라면 우리가 어찌 살았을지 모르겠습니다."], ["I am sure you mean to be kind to us.", "그분에게 무언가를 숨기는 사람이 된다면 좋은 보답이 아닐까 두렵습니다. 오늘 밤은 더 이상 말씀드릴 수가 없어요, 선생."], ["Thank you, thank you.’", "저희에게 친절을 베풀려 하신다는 건 잘 압니다. 고맙습니다, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["‘Let me ask you one question before I leave.", "\"떠나기 전에 한 가지만 여쭙겠습니다."], ["Have you known my mother long?’", "제 어머니를 안 지 오래되었습니까?\""]], [["‘I think two years, sir,--The bell has stopped.’", "\"두 해쯤 된 것 같습니다, 선생—종이 멎었네요.\""]], [["‘How did you know her first?", "\"어떻게 처음 알게 되었습니까?"], ["Did she send here for you?’", "어머니가 이곳으로 당신을 부르러 보냈습니까?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아니에요."], ["She does not even know that I live here.", "그분은 제가 여기 산다는 것조차 모르세요."], ["We have a friend, father and I--a poor labouring man, but the best of friends--and I wrote out that I wished to do needlework, and gave his address.", "저희에게는, 아버지와 제게는 친구가 한 분 있어요—가난한 막노동꾼이지만 더없이 좋은 친구지요—그래서 제가 바느질을 하고 싶다고 적어 그분 주소를 적어 두었어요."], ["And he got what I wrote out displayed at a few places where it cost nothing, and Mrs Clennam found me that way, and sent for me.", "그분은 제가 적은 것을 돈 들지 않는 몇 군데에 내걸어 주셨고, 클레넘 부인이 그렇게 해서 저를 찾아내어 부르러 보내셨어요."], ["The gate will be locked, sir!’", "문이 잠기겠어요, 선생!\""]], [["She was so tremulous and agitated, and he was so moved by compassion for her, and by deep interest in her story as it dawned upon him, that he could scarcely tear himself away.", "그녀는 어찌나 떨며 동요하고 있었던지, 또 그는 그녀에 대한 연민과, 차츰 자기 앞에 밝혀지는 그녀의 사연에 대한 깊은 관심에 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 차마 발걸음을 떼기가 힘들었다."], ["But the stoppage of the bell, and the quiet in the prison, were a warning to depart; and with a few hurried words of kindness he left her gliding back to her father.", "그러나 멎은 종과 감옥의 정적이 떠나라는 경고였기에, 그는 다정한 말 몇 마디를 황급히 건네고는, 그녀가 아버지에게로 미끄러지듯 돌아가도록 두고 떠났다."]], [["But he remained too late.", "그러나 그는 너무 늦게까지 남아 있었다."], ["The inner gate was locked, and the lodge closed.", "안쪽 문은 잠겼고, 수위실은 닫혀 있었다."], ["After a little fruitless knocking with his hand, he was standing there with the disagreeable conviction upon him that he had got to get through the night, when a voice accosted him from behind.", "손으로 부질없이 잠시 두드려 본 뒤, 그는 이 밤을 꼼짝없이 새워야 한다는 달갑지 않은 확신에 사로잡힌 채 그곳에 서 있었는데, 그때 등 뒤에서 한 목소리가 그에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["‘Caught, eh?’ said the voice.", "\"갇히셨군요, 응?\" 그 목소리가 말했다."], ["‘You won’t go home till morning.", "\"아침까지는 집에 못 가시겠는데요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["It’s you, is it, Mr Clennam?’", "클레넘 씨, 당신이군요?\""]], [["The voice was Tip’s; and they stood looking at one another in the prison-yard, as it began to rain.", "그 목소리는 팁의 것이었다. 비가 내리기 시작하는 가운데, 그들은 감옥 마당에 서서 서로를 바라보았다."]], [["‘You’ve done it,’ observed Tip; ‘you must be sharper than that next time.’", "\"제대로 당하셨네요.\" 팁이 말했다. \"다음번엔 좀 더 빈틈없으셔야겠어요.\""]], [["‘But you are locked in too,’ said Arthur.", "\"그런데 당신도 갇혀 있질 않소.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘I believe I am!’ said Tip, sarcastically.", "\"그렇죠!\" 팁이 비꼬듯 말했다."], ["‘About!", "\"거의 그렇다고 할 수 있죠!"], ["But not in your way.", "하지만 당신과 같은 처지는 아니에요."], ["I belong to the shop, only my sister has a theory that our governor must never know it.", "저는 이 가게 소속이거든요. 다만 우리 영감님은 절대 그걸 알아선 안 된다는 게 누이의 지론이지요."], ["I don’t see why, myself.’", "저로선 왜 그래야 하는지 모르겠습니다만.\""]], [["‘Can I get any shelter?’ asked Arthur.", "\"제가 묵을 데를 좀 구할 수 있을까요?\" 아서가 물었다."], ["‘What had I better do?’", "\"어떻게 하는 게 좋겠소?\""]], [["‘We had better get hold of Amy first of all,’ said Tip, referring any difficulty to her as a matter of course.", "\"무엇보다 먼저 에이미를 찾는 게 좋겠습니다.\" 팁이 무슨 어려운 일이든 당연하다는 듯 그녀에게 떠넘기며 말했다."]], [["‘I would rather walk about all night--it’s not much to do--than give that trouble.’", "\"그렇게 폐를 끼치느니 차라리 밤새 걸어 다니겠소—그쯤이야 별일도 아니지요.\""]], [["‘You needn’t do that, if you don’t mind paying for a bed.", "\"잠자리 값을 치르는 게 싫지 않으시다면 그러실 필요 없어요."], ["If you don’t mind paying, they’ll make you up one on the Snuggery table, under the circumstances.", "돈 치르는 게 싫지 않으시다면, 이런 사정이니 스너거리 탁자 위에 잠자리를 하나 마련해 줄 겁니다."], ["If you’ll come along, I’ll introduce you there.’", "같이 가시면 제가 거기로 안내해 드리지요.\""]], [["As they passed down the yard, Arthur looked up at the window of the room he had lately left, where the light was still burning.", "마당을 따라 내려가면서, 아서는 방금 떠나온 방의 창문을 올려다보았는데, 그곳에는 아직 불빛이 타오르고 있었다. \"예, 선생.\""], ["‘Yes, sir,’ said Tip, following his glance.", "팁이 그의 시선을 따라가며 말했다."], ["‘That’s the governor’s.", "\"저게 영감님 방이에요."], ["She’ll sit with him for another hour reading yesterday’s paper to him, or something of that sort; and then she’ll come out like a little ghost, and vanish away without a sound.’", "누이는 한 시간쯤 더 곁에 앉아 어제 신문 같은 걸 읽어 드리고, 그러고 나서 작은 유령처럼 빠져나와 소리 없이 사라지지요.\""]], [["‘I don’t understand you.’", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘The governor sleeps up in the room, and she has a lodging at the turnkey’s.", "\"영감님은 저 방에서 주무시고, 누이는 간수 집에 거처를 두고 있어요."], ["First house there,’ said Tip, pointing out the doorway into which she had retired.", "저기 저 첫 번째 집이지요.\" 팁은 그녀가 들어갔던 그 문간을 가리키며 말했다."], ["‘First house, sky parlour.", "\"첫 번째 집의 다락방이에요."], ["She pays twice as much for it as she would for one twice as good outside.", "바깥에서 두 배는 좋은 방을 얻을 값을 거기에 치르지요."], ["But she stands by the governor, poor dear girl, day and night.’", "하지만 가엾은 누이는 밤이고 낮이고 영감님 곁을 지킨답니다.\""]], [["This brought them to the tavern-establishment at the upper end of the prison, where the collegians had just vacated their social evening club.", "이 이야기를 나누는 사이 그들은 감옥 위쪽 끝에 있는 술집에 이르렀는데, 그곳에서는 동료 수감자들이 막 저녁 친목회를 파한 참이었다."], ["The apartment on the ground-floor in which it was held, was the Snuggery in question; the presidential tribune of the chairman, the pewter-pots, glasses, pipes, tobacco-ashes, and general flavour of members, were still as that convivial institution had left them on its adjournment.", "그 모임이 열리던 일층 방이 바로 그 스너거리였다. 의장이 앉던 단상, 백랍 잔들, 유리잔들, 파이프들, 담뱃재, 그리고 회원들이 풍기던 전반적인 냄새가, 그 흥겨운 모임이 산회하며 남겨 놓은 그대로였다."], ["The Snuggery had two of the qualities popularly held to be essential to grog for ladies, in respect that it was hot and strong; but in the third point of analogy, requiring plenty of it, the Snuggery was defective; being but a cooped-up apartment.", "스너거리는 흔히 숙녀용 그로그에 필수라 여겨지는 두 가지 성질, 곧 뜨겁고 독하다는 점을 갖추고 있었으나, 그 양이 넉넉해야 한다는 세 번째 비슷한 조건에서는 부족함이 있었으니, 그저 비좁게 갇힌 방에 지나지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["The unaccustomed visitor from outside, naturally assumed everybody here to be prisoners--landlord, waiter, barmaid, potboy, and all.", "바깥에서 온 익숙지 못한 방문객은, 이곳에 있는 사람은 누구나—주인이든 종업원이든 술집 아가씨든 심부름꾼 소년이든 모두—죄수일 거라고 자연스레 여겼다."], ["Whether they were or not, did not appear; but they all had a weedy look.", "정말로 그런지 아닌지는 드러나지 않았으나, 그들 모두 시들시들한 행색이었다."], ["The keeper of a chandler’s shop in a front parlour, who took in gentlemen boarders, lent his assistance in making the bed.", "앞쪽 거실에서 양초 가게를 하면서 신사 하숙인을 들이던 사람이 잠자리 마련을 거들었다."], ["He had been a tailor in his time, and had kept a phaeton, he said.", "그는 한때 재단사였고 사륜마차를 몰고 다닌 적도 있다고 했다."], ["He boasted that he stood up litigiously for the interests of the college; and he had undefined and undefinable ideas that the marshal intercepted a ‘Fund,’ which ought to come to the collegians.", "그는 이 학교의 이익을 위해 소송도 불사하며 떨치고 나섰노라 자랑했다. 또한 그는 마셜시 관리관이 마땅히 동료 수감자들에게 돌아와야 할 '기금'을 가로채고 있다는, 막연하고 규정할 수 없는 생각을 품고 있었다."], ["He liked to believe this, and always impressed the shadowy grievance on new-comers and strangers; though he could not, for his life, have explained what Fund he meant, or how the notion had got rooted in his soul.", "그는 그렇게 믿기를 좋아했고, 새로 온 사람과 낯선 이들에게 그 어렴풋한 불만을 늘 새겨 넣곤 했다. 다만 그가 말하는 기금이 무엇인지, 또 그 생각이 어떻게 그의 마음속에 뿌리내리게 되었는지는 죽었다 깨어나도 설명하지 못했을 것이다."], ["He had fully convinced himself, notwithstanding, that his own proper share of the Fund was three and ninepence a week; and that in this amount he, as an individual collegian, was swindled by the marshal, regularly every Monday.", "그럼에도 그는 자기 몫의 기금이 주당 삼 실링 구 펜스라고 완전히 스스로를 납득시켜 놓았고, 개개의 수감자로서 그 액수만큼 매주 월요일마다 어김없이 관리관에게 사취당하고 있다고 믿었다."], ["Apparently, he helped to make the bed, that he might not lose an opportunity of stating this case; after which unloading of his mind, and after announcing (as it seemed he always did, without anything coming of it) that he was going to write a letter to the papers and show the marshal up, he fell into miscellaneous conversation with the rest.", "보아하니 그는 이 사정을 늘어놓을 기회를 놓치지 않으려고 잠자리 마련을 거든 듯했다. 마음속 짐을 그렇게 부려 놓은 뒤, 그리고 신문사에 편지를 써서 관리관을 폭로하겠노라고 (보아하니 늘 그러면서도 아무 결과도 없었던 듯한 그 말을) 선언한 뒤, 그는 나머지 사람들과 이런저런 잡담에 빠져들었다."], ["It was evident from the general tone of the whole party, that they had come to regard insolvency as the normal state of mankind, and the payment of debts as a disease that occasionally broke out.", "그 무리 전체의 전반적인 어조로 보아, 그들이 파산을 인류의 정상적인 상태로, 빚을 갚는 일을 이따금 발병하는 질병쯤으로 여기게 되었음이 분명했다."]], [["In this strange scene, and with these strange spectres flitting about him, Arthur Clennam looked on at the preparations as if they were part of a dream.", "이 기이한 광경 속에서, 또 이 기이한 유령들이 자기 주위를 떠도는 가운데, 아서 클레넘은 그 채비를 마치 꿈의 일부인 양 바라보았다."], ["Pending which, the long-initiated Tip, with an awful enjoyment of the Snuggery’s resources, pointed out the common kitchen fire maintained by subscription of collegians, the boiler for hot water supported in like manner, and other premises generally tending to the deduction that the way to be healthy, wealthy, and wise, was to come to the Marshalsea.", "그러는 동안 오래 단련된 팁은, 스너거리의 자원을 끔찍하게도 즐기면서, 수감자들의 회비로 유지되는 공동 부엌 화덕이며, 같은 방식으로 운영되는 온수용 보일러며, 결국 건강하고 부유하고 현명해지는 길은 마셜시로 오는 것이라는 추론으로 흐르게 마련인 여러 시설을 일러 주었다."]], [["The two tables put together in a corner, were, at length, converted into a very fair bed; and the stranger was left to the Windsor chairs, the presidential tribune, the beery atmosphere, sawdust, pipe-lights, spittoons and repose.", "한쪽 구석에 붙여 놓은 두 개의 탁자는 마침내 제법 쓸 만한 침상으로 바뀌었고, 낯선 손님은 윈저 의자들과, 의장의 단상과, 맥주 냄새 밴 공기와, 톱밥과, 파이프 불씨와, 가래통과, 그리고 휴식과 함께 남겨졌다."], ["But the last item was long, long, long, in linking itself to the rest.", "그러나 마지막 항목은 한참, 한참, 한참 동안 나머지에 끼어들지 못했다."], ["The novelty of the place, the coming upon it without preparation, the sense of being locked up, the remembrance of that room up-stairs, of the two brothers, and above all of the retiring childish form, and the face in which he now saw years of insufficient food, if not of want, kept him waking and unhappy.", "그곳의 낯섦, 아무 준비 없이 들이닥친 사정, 갇혀 있다는 느낌, 위층 그 방과 두 형제에 대한 기억, 그리고 무엇보다 물러나던 그 어린아이 같은 모습, 그리고 이제 와 그가 거기서 궁핍까지는 아니더라도 여러 해의 부실한 끼니의 흔적을 읽어 낸 그 얼굴이, 그를 잠 못 들고 불행하게 만들었다."]], [["Speculations, too, bearing the strangest relations towards the prison, but always concerning the prison, ran like nightmares through his mind while he lay awake.", "게다가 갖가지 억측들이, 감옥과 더없이 기묘한 관계를 맺으면서도 늘 감옥에 관한 것들이, 그가 깨어 누워 있는 동안 악몽처럼 그의 머릿속을 헤집고 다녔다."], ["Whether coffins were kept ready for people who might die there, where they were kept, how they were kept, where people who died in the prison were buried, how they were taken out, what forms were observed, whether an implacable creditor could arrest the dead?", "여기서 죽을지도 모를 사람들을 위해 관이 미리 준비되어 있는지, 있다면 어디에, 어떻게 보관되는지, 감옥에서 죽은 사람들은 어디에 묻히는지, 어떻게 실려 나가는지, 어떤 절차를 따르는지, 무자비한 채권자가 죽은 자까지 붙잡아 둘 수 있는지?"], ["As to escaping, what chances there were of escape?", "탈옥에 관해서는, 탈출할 가망이 얼마나 되는지?"], ["Whether a prisoner could scale the walls with a cord and grapple, how he would descend upon the other side?", "죄수가 밧줄과 갈고리로 담을 기어오를 수 있는지, 반대편으로는 어떻게 내려설지?"], ["whether he could alight on a housetop, steal down a staircase, let himself out at a door, and get lost in the crowd?", "지붕 위에 내려앉아 계단을 살금살금 내려가, 문으로 빠져나와 군중 속으로 사라질 수 있는지?"], ["As to Fire in the prison, if one were to break out while he lay there?", "감옥의 화재에 관해서는, 자기가 여기 누워 있는 사이에 불이라도 나면 어찌 될지?"]], [["And these involuntary starts of fancy were, after all, but the setting of a picture in which three people kept before him.", "그리고 이렇게 제멋대로 솟구치는 공상들도, 따지고 보면 세 사람이 그의 앞에 떠나지 않고 머무는 어떤 그림의 배경에 지나지 않았다."], ["His father, with the steadfast look with which he had died, prophetically darkened forth in the portrait; his mother, with her arm up, warding off his suspicion; Little Dorrit, with her hand on the degraded arm, and her drooping head turned away.", "그의 아버지는, 그가 숨을 거둘 때 짓던 그 한결같은 눈빛으로, 마치 예언처럼 초상화 속에 어둡게 드러나 있었고, 그의 어머니는 팔을 치켜들어 그의 의심을 막아 내고 있었으며, 리틀 도릿은 그 비천한 팔에 손을 얹은 채, 수그린 고개를 외면하고 있었다."]], [["What if his mother had an old reason she well knew for softening to this poor girl!", "만약 그의 어머니가 이 가엾은 처녀에게 누그러질 만한, 스스로 잘 아는 오랜 까닭을 품고 있었다면!"], ["What if the prisoner now sleeping quietly--Heaven grant it!--by the light of the great Day of judgment should trace back his fall to her.", "만약 지금 고요히 잠든—제발 그러하기를!—저 죄수가, 위대한 심판의 날의 빛 아래에서 자기 몰락의 자취를 거슬러 올라가 그것이 그녀에게서 비롯되었음을 밝혀낸다면!"], ["What if any act of hers and of his father’s, should have even remotely brought the grey heads of those two brothers so low!", "만약 그녀와 그의 아버지가 저지른 어떤 행위가, 단지 멀리서나마, 저 두 형제의 백발을 그토록 낮은 곳까지 떨어뜨린 것이었다면!"]], [["A swift thought shot into his mind.", "한 가지 생각이 번개처럼 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["In that long imprisonment here, and in her own long confinement to her room, did his mother find a balance to be struck?", "이곳에서의 그 오랜 수감과, 자기 방에 갇힌 어머니 자신의 그 오랜 칩거 속에서, 그의 어머니는 결산해야 할 어떤 균형을 보고 있었던 것일까?"], ["‘I admit that I was accessory to that man’s captivity.", "'나는 그 사람을 가두는 데 한몫했음을 인정한다."], ["I have suffered for it in kind.", "나는 그 대가를 똑같이 치러 왔다."], ["He has decayed in his prison: I in mine.", "그는 자기 감옥에서 쇠락해 갔고, 나는 내 감옥에서 쇠락해 갔다."], ["I have paid the penalty.’", "나는 그 죗값을 치렀다.'"]], [["When all the other thoughts had faded out, this one held possession of him. When he fell asleep, she came before him in her wheeled chair, warding him off with this justification. When he awoke, and sprang up causelessly frightened, the words were in his ears, as if her voice had slowly spoken them at his pillow, to break his rest: ‘He withers away in his prison; I wither away in mine; inexorable justice is done; what do I owe on this score!’", "다른 모든 생각이 스러진 뒤에도, 이 한 가지 생각만은 그를 사로잡고 놓아주지 않았다. 그가 잠들었을 때, 그녀는 바퀴 달린 의자에 앉은 채 이 변명으로 그를 막아 내며 그의 앞에 나타났다. 그가 까닭 없이 화들짝 놀라 깨어났을 때, 그 말들이 그의 귓전에 맴돌았으니, 마치 그녀의 목소리가 그의 잠을 깨뜨리려고 베갯머리에서 천천히 그 말을 읊은 듯했다. '그는 자기 감옥에서 시들어 간다. 나는 내 감옥에서 시들어 간다. 가차 없는 정의가 행해지고 있다. 내가 이 일로 무슨 빚을 졌단 말이냐!'"]], [["# CHAPTER 9. Little Mother", "# 제10장. 통치의 학문 전체를 담음"]], [["The morning light was in no hurry to climb the prison wall and look in at the Snuggery windows; and when it did come, it would have been more welcome if it had come alone, instead of bringing a rush of rain with it.", "아침빛은 감옥 담을 기어올라 스너거리 창 안을 들여다보는 데 조금도 서두르지 않았다. 그러다 마침내 들어왔을 때에도, 한바탕 빗줄기를 몰고 오지 않고 홀로 왔더라면 훨씬 반가웠을 것이다."], ["But the equinoctial gales were blowing out at sea, and the impartial south-west wind, in its flight, would not neglect even the narrow Marshalsea.", "그러나 추분의 강풍이 바다 쪽에서 불어 나오고 있었고, 사사로움 없는 남서풍은 제 길을 가는 중에 비좁은 마셜시조차 그냥 지나치지 않으려 했다."], ["While it roared through the steeple of St George’s Church, and twirled all the cowls in the neighbourhood, it made a swoop to beat the Southwark smoke into the jail; and, plunging down the chimneys of the few early collegians who were yet lighting their fires, half suffocated them.", "세인트조지 교회의 첨탑을 뚫고 울부짖으며 인근의 굴뚝 갓을 죄다 빙빙 돌리는 한편, 한 차례 휙 내리꽂혀 사우스워크의 매연을 감옥 안으로 두들겨 넣었고, 아직 불을 지피던 몇 안 되는 부지런한 재소자들의 굴뚝으로 곤두박질쳐 그들을 거의 질식시켰다."]], [["Arthur Clennam would have been little disposed to linger in bed, though his bed had been in a more private situation, and less affected by the raking out of yesterday’s fire, the kindling of to-day’s under the collegiate boiler, the filling of that Spartan vessel at the pump, the sweeping and sawdusting of the common room, and other such preparations.", "아서 클레넘은, 설령 그의 잠자리가 좀 더 호젓한 곳에 있었고 어제의 잿불을 긁어내고 오늘 아침 재소자용 보일러 밑에 불을 지피고 그 스파르타식 그릇을 펌프에서 채우고 공동실을 쓸고 톱밥을 뿌리는 따위의 온갖 준비에 덜 시달렸더라도, 침상에서 꾸물거릴 마음이 거의 없었을 것이다."], ["Heartily glad to see the morning, though little rested by the night, he turned out as soon as he could distinguish objects about him, and paced the yard for two heavy hours before the gate was opened.", "밤새 거의 쉬지 못했으면서도 아침을 보게 되어 진심으로 기뻤던 그는, 주위의 사물을 분간할 수 있게 되자마자 곧장 일어나, 문이 열리기 전까지 두 시간이나 무겁게 마당을 거닐었다."]], [["The walls were so near to one another, and the wild clouds hurried over them so fast, that it gave him a sensation like the beginning of sea-sickness to look up at the gusty sky.", "담이 서로 너무 가까이 붙어 있었고 거친 구름이 그 위로 너무도 빠르게 내달려서, 바람 거센 하늘을 올려다보면 뱃멀미가 시작되려는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["The rain, carried aslant by flaws of wind, blackened that side of the central building which he had visited last night, but left a narrow dry trough under the lee of the wall, where he walked up and down among the waits of straw and dust and paper, the waste droppings of the pump, and the stray leaves of yesterday’s greens.", "바람의 변덕에 비스듬히 실려 오는 비는 그가 어젯밤 찾아갔던 중앙 건물의 그쪽 면을 검게 적셨으나, 담의 바람그늘 아래로는 좁다랗게 마른 도랑 같은 자리를 남겼다. 그는 그곳을, 짚 부스러기와 먼지와 종이 조각, 펌프에서 흘린 찌끼, 어제 채소의 흩어진 잎사귀 사이를 오르내리며 거닐었다."], ["It was as haggard a view of life as a man need look upon.", "사람이 굳이 바라보아야 할 만한 것 가운데 가장 초췌한 삶의 풍경이었다."]], [["Nor was it relieved by any glimpse of the little creature who had brought him there.", "그를 그곳으로 데려온 그 작은 사람의 모습이 언뜻 보였다고 해서 그 풍경이 누그러지지도 않았다. 어쩌면 그의 얼굴이 양쪽 문에서 돌려져 있는 사이에 그녀가 제 방문을 빠져나와 아버지가 사는 방문으로 들어갔는지도 모른다."], ["Perhaps she glided out of her doorway and in at that where her father lived, while his face was turned from both; but he saw nothing of her.", "그러나 그는 그녀의 모습을 전혀 보지 못했다. 오라비가 나오기에는 너무 이른 시각이었다."], ["It was too early for her brother; to have seen him once, was to have seen enough of him to know that he would be sluggish to leave whatever frowsy bed he occupied at night; so, as Arthur Clennam walked up and down, waiting for the gate to open, he cast about in his mind for future rather than for present means of pursuing his discoveries.", "한 번 본 것만으로도 그가 밤에 차지하던 그 퀴퀴한 잠자리가 무엇이든 거기서 벗어나기를 게을리하리라는 것을 알기에는 충분했다. 그래서 아서 클레넘은 문이 열리기를 기다리며 마당을 오르내리는 동안, 현재보다는 앞으로 자신의 탐색을 이어 갈 방도를 마음속으로 궁리했다."]], [["At last the lodge-gate turned, and the turnkey, standing on the step, taking an early comb at his hair, was ready to let him out.", "마침내 수위실 문이 돌아갔고, 계단에 서서 머리를 이른 빗질로 빗고 있던 간수가 그를 내보낼 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["With a joyful sense of release he passed through the lodge, and found himself again in the little outer court-yard where he had spoken to the brother last night.", "풀려난다는 기쁨에 찬 느낌으로 그는 수위실을 지나, 어젯밤 그 오라비와 이야기를 나누었던 작은 바깥 마당에 다시 서게 되었다."]], [["There was a string of people already straggling in, whom it was not difficult to identify as the nondescript messengers, go-betweens, and errand-bearers of the place.", "이미 한 무리의 사람들이 띄엄띄엄 들어오고 있었는데, 이곳의 정체 모를 심부름꾼이며 중개꾼이며 잔심부름꾼임을 알아보기란 어렵지 않았다."], ["Some of them had been lounging in the rain until the gate should open; others, who had timed their arrival with greater nicety, were coming up now, and passing in with damp whitey-brown paper bags from the grocers, loaves of bread, lumps of butter, eggs, milk, and the like.", "그중 더러는 문이 열리기까지 빗속에서 어슬렁거리고 있었고, 도착 시각을 좀 더 빈틈없이 맞춘 이들은 이제 막 올라와, 식료품점에서 받은 축축한 누르스름한 종이봉지며 빵 덩이며 버터 덩어리며 달걀이며 우유 따위를 들고 안으로 들어갔다."], ["The shabbiness of these attendants upon shabbiness, the poverty of these insolvent waiters upon insolvency, was a sight to see.", "초라함을 시중드는 이 초라한 자들, 파산을 시중드는 이 파산한 자들의 가난이란 가히 볼 만한 광경이었다."], ["Such threadbare coats and trousers, such fusty gowns and shawls, such squashed hats and bonnets, such boots and shoes, such umbrellas and walking-sticks, never were seen in Rag Fair.", "그토록 닳아빠진 외투와 바지, 그토록 곰팡내 나는 가운과 숄, 그토록 찌부러진 모자와 보닛, 그런 장화와 구두, 그런 우산과 지팡이는 누더기 시장에서도 본 적이 없었다."], ["All of them wore the cast-off clothes of other men and women, were made up of patches and pieces of other people’s individuality, and had no sartorial existence of their own proper.", "그들은 하나같이 다른 남자와 여자가 버린 옷을 걸쳤고, 다른 사람들의 개성에서 떨어져 나온 헝겊과 조각으로 짜 맞춰져 있었으며, 그들 자신만의 의복상의 존재라곤 없었다."], ["Their walk was the walk of a race apart.", "그들의 걸음걸이는 따로 떨어진 한 종족의 걸음걸이였다."], ["They had a peculiar way of doggedly slinking round the corner, as if they were eternally going to the pawnbroker’s.", "그들은 마치 영원토록 전당포로 가고 있기라도 하다는 듯, 끈질기게 모퉁이로 슬그머니 돌아드는 독특한 방식을 지니고 있었다."], ["When they coughed, they coughed like people accustomed to be forgotten on doorsteps and in draughty passages, waiting for answers to letters in faded ink, which gave the recipients of those manuscripts great mental disturbance and no satisfaction.", "그들은 기침을 할 때면, 문간이나 외풍 드는 통로에서 잊힌 채 빛바랜 잉크로 쓴 편지에 대한 답장을 기다리는 데 익숙한 사람들처럼 기침했는데, 그런 편지를 받은 이들에게는 큰 심란함만 안기고 만족이라곤 주지 못하는 법이었다."], ["As they eyed the stranger in passing, they eyed him with borrowing eyes--hungry, sharp, speculative as to his softness if they were accredited to him, and the likelihood of his standing something handsome.", "그들은 지나가며 이 낯선 이를 곁눈질할 때, 빌려 쓰는 자의 눈으로 — 자기들이 그에게 소개되었을 경우 그가 얼마나 무를지, 그리고 그가 무언가 푸짐하게 한턱낼 가능성이 얼마나 될지를 가늠하는, 굶주리고 날카롭고 타산적인 눈으로 — 곁눈질했다."], ["Mendicity on commission stooped in their high shoulders, shambled in their unsteady legs, buttoned and pinned and darned and dragged their clothes, frayed their button-holes, leaked out of their figures in dirty little ends of tape, and issued from their mouths in alcoholic breathings.", "수수료를 받는 구걸이 그들의 솟은 어깨를 구부정하게 만들었고, 후들거리는 다리를 비척거리게 했으며, 그들의 옷을 단추로 채우고 핀으로 꽂고 기워 끌리게 했고, 단춧구멍을 너덜너덜하게 했으며, 더러운 끈의 작은 끄트머리가 되어 그들의 몸에서 새어 나왔고, 술기운 어린 입김이 되어 그들의 입에서 뿜어 나왔다."]], [["As these people passed him standing still in the court-yard, and one of them turned back to inquire if he could assist him with his services, it came into Arthur Clennam’s mind that he would speak to Little Dorrit again before he went away.", "이 사람들이 마당에 가만히 서 있는 그의 곁을 지나칠 때, 그중 한 사람이 되돌아와 자기 솜씨로 도와드릴 일이 없겠느냐고 물었다. 그러자 아서 클레넘의 머릿속에, 떠나기 전에 리틀 도릿과 다시 한 번 이야기를 나누어야겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["She would have recovered her first surprise, and might feel easier with him.", "그녀도 처음의 놀라움에서 회복되었을 테고, 자기에게 좀 더 편하게 대할지도 모를 일이었다."], ["He asked this member of the fraternity (who had two red herrings in his hand, and a loaf and a blacking brush under his arm), where was the nearest place to get a cup of coffee at.", "그는 이 무리의 한 사람(손에 훈제 청어 두 마리를 들고, 겨드랑이에 빵 한 덩이와 구두약 솔 하나를 끼고 있었다)에게, 커피 한 잔을 마실 만한 가장 가까운 곳이 어디냐고 물었다."], ["The nondescript replied in encouraging terms, and brought him to a coffee-shop in the street within a stone’s throw.", "그 정체 모를 자는 힘이 나는 말투로 대답하더니, 그를 돌 던지면 닿을 만한 거리에 있는 거리의 어느 커피점으로 데려다주었다."]], [["‘Do you know Miss Dorrit?’ asked the new client.", "\"도릿 양을 아십니까?\" 새 손님이 물었다."]], [["The nondescript knew two Miss Dorrits; one who was born inside--That was the one!", "그 정체 모를 자는 도릿 양을 둘 알고 있었다. 한 사람은 안에서 태어났는데 — 바로 그 사람이었다!"], ["That was the one?", "그 사람이라고?"], ["The nondescript had known her many years.", "그 정체 모를 자는 그녀를 여러 해 동안 알아 왔다."], ["In regard of the other Miss Dorrit, the nondescript lodged in the same house with herself and uncle.", "다른 도릿 양으로 말하자면, 그 정체 모를 자는 그녀와 그녀의 삼촌과 같은 집에 세 들어 살고 있었다."]], [["This changed the client’s half-formed design of remaining at the coffee-shop until the nondescript should bring him word that Dorrit had issued forth into the street.", "이로써 손님은, 그 정체 모를 자가 도릿이 거리로 나섰다는 소식을 전해 줄 때까지 커피점에 머물려던 어렴풋한 계획을 바꾸었다."], ["He entrusted the nondescript with a confidential message to her, importing that the visitor who had waited on her father last night, begged the favour of a few words with her at her uncle’s lodging; he obtained from the same source full directions to the house, which was very near; dismissed the nondescript gratified with half-a-crown; and having hastily refreshed himself at the coffee-shop, repaired with all speed to the clarionet-player’s dwelling.", "그는 그 정체 모를 자에게 그녀에게 전할 은밀한 전갈을 맡겼는데, 어젯밤 그녀의 아버지를 찾아뵈었던 방문객이 그녀의 삼촌 거처에서 그녀와 몇 마디 나눌 호의를 청한다는 내용이었다. 같은 출처로부터 그는 매우 가까운 그 집까지의 자세한 길 안내도 얻었고, 반 크라운을 받고 흡족해하는 그 정체 모를 자를 돌려보낸 뒤, 커피점에서 서둘러 요기를 하고는 한껏 빠른 걸음으로 그 클라리넷 연주자의 거처로 향했다."]], [["There were so many lodgers in this house that the doorpost seemed to be as full of bell-handles as a cathedral organ is of stops.", "이 집에는 세입자가 어찌나 많은지, 문기둥이 마치 대성당 오르간에 음전이 빼곡하듯 초인종 손잡이로 빼곡한 듯했다."], ["Doubtful which might be the clarionet-stop, he was considering the point, when a shuttlecock flew out of the parlour window, and alighted on his hat.", "어느 것이 클라리넷 음전일지 갈피를 못 잡고 그 점을 따져 보고 있을 때, 깃털공 하나가 응접실 창에서 날아와 그의 모자 위에 내려앉았다."], ["He then observed that in the parlour window was a blind with the inscription, MR CRIPPLES’s ACADEMY; also in another line, EVENING TUITION; and behind the blind was a little white-faced boy, with a slice of bread-and-butter and a battledore.", "그제야 그는 응접실 창에 크리플즈 학원이라는 글귀가 적힌 차양이 드리워져 있고, 또 다른 줄에 야간 교습이라 쓰여 있는 것을, 그리고 그 차양 뒤에 버터 바른 빵 한 조각과 깃털공 채를 든, 작고 창백한 얼굴의 사내아이가 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["The window being accessible from the footway, he looked in over the blind, returned the shuttlecock, and put his question.", "창이 보도에서 닿을 만한 높이여서, 그는 차양 너머로 들여다보고 깃털공을 돌려준 다음 물음을 던졌다."]], [["‘Dorrit?’ said the little white-faced boy (Master Cripples in fact).", "\"도릿이요?\" 작고 창백한 얼굴의 사내아이가 말했다(실은 크리플즈 도령이었다)."], ["‘_Mr_ Dorrit?", "\"도릿 씨요?"], ["Third bell and one knock.’", "세 번째 종에 한 번 노크하면 돼요.\""]], [["The pupils of Mr Cripples appeared to have been making a copy-book of the street-door, it was so extensively scribbled over in pencil.", "크리플즈 씨의 학생들은 거리로 난 현관문을 습자책 삼아 써먹은 모양이었으니, 그만큼 온통 연필로 휘갈겨져 있었다."], ["The frequency of the inscriptions, ‘Old Dorrit,’ and ‘Dirty Dick,’ in combination, suggested intentions of personality on the part Of Mr Cripples’s pupils.", "'늙은 도릿'과 '더러운 딕'이라는 글귀가 짝지어 빈번히 적혀 있는 것으로 보아, 크리플즈 씨의 학생들 쪽에서 인신공격의 의도가 있었던 듯싶었다."], ["There was ample time to make these observations before the door was opened by the poor old man himself.", "가엾은 노인이 몸소 문을 열어 줄 때까지, 이런 관찰을 할 시간은 넉넉했다."]], [["‘Ha!’ said he, very slowly remembering Arthur, ‘you were shut in last night?’", "\"하!\" 노인이 아서를 아주 천천히 기억해 내며 말했다. \"어젯밤 안에 갇히셨던 분이로군요?\""]], [["‘Yes, Mr Dorrit.", "\"네, 도릿 씨."], ["I hope to meet your niece here presently.’", "여기서 곧 조카딸을 뵐 수 있길 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said he, pondering.", "\"오!\" 노인이 곰곰이 생각하며 말했다."], ["‘Out of my brother’s way?", "\"내 동생의 눈을 피해서요?"], ["True.", "그렇지."], ["Would you come up-stairs and wait for her?’", "위층으로 올라와서 그 애를 기다리시겠소?\""]], [["‘Thank you.’", "\"고맙습니다.\""]], [["Turning himself as slowly as he turned in his mind whatever he heard or said, he led the way up the narrow stairs.", "그는 무엇을 듣거나 말하든 마음속에서 굴리는 것만큼이나 느릿느릿 몸을 돌려, 좁은 계단을 앞장서 올라갔다."], ["The house was very close, and had an unwholesome smell.", "집 안은 몹시 답답했고 건강에 해로운 냄새가 났다."], ["The little staircase windows looked in at the back windows of other houses as unwholesome as itself, with poles and lines thrust out of them, on which unsightly linen hung; as if the inhabitants were angling for clothes, and had had some wretched bites not worth attending to.", "작은 계단 창은 제 집만큼이나 건강에 해로운 다른 집들의 뒤창을 들여다보았는데, 그 창들에서는 장대와 빨랫줄이 비죽 나와 흉한 빨래가 널려 있었으니, 마치 그 집 사람들이 옷가지를 낚으려고 낚시질을 하다가 거들떠볼 가치도 없는 형편없는 입질이나 몇 번 받았다는 듯했다."], ["In the back garret--a sickly room, with a turn-up bedstead in it, so hastily and recently turned up that the blankets were boiling over, as it were, and keeping the lid open--a half-finished breakfast of coffee and toast for two persons was jumbled down anyhow on a rickety table.", "뒤쪽 다락방 — 접이식 침상이 놓인 병약한 방으로, 어찌나 황급히 그리고 방금 막 접어 올렸던지 담요가 마치 끓어 넘치듯 삐져나와 뚜껑을 열어 둔 채였다 — 에는, 두 사람 몫의 커피와 토스트로 차린 채 마저 먹지 못한 아침 식사가 흔들거리는 탁자 위에 아무렇게나 흩어져 있었다."]], [["There was no one there.", "그곳엔 아무도 없었다."], ["The old man mumbling to himself, after some consideration, that Fanny had run away, went to the next room to fetch her back.", "노인은 잠시 생각하더니 패니가 달아났다고 혼자 중얼거리며, 그 애를 데려오려고 옆방으로 갔다."], ["The visitor, observing that she held the door on the inside, and that, when the uncle tried to open it, there was a sharp adjuration of ‘Don’t, stupid!’ and an appearance of loose stocking and flannel, concluded that the young lady was in an undress.", "방문객은 그녀가 안쪽에서 문을 붙들고 있고, 삼촌이 문을 열려 하자 \"안 돼요, 멍청이!\" 하는 날카로운 호통이 들리며 헐거운 양말과 플란넬 천이 언뜻 비치는 것을 보고, 그 아가씨가 옷을 채 갖춰 입지 못한 상태임을 알아챘다."], ["The uncle, without appearing to come to any conclusion, shuffled in again, sat down in his chair, and began warming his hands at the fire; not that it was cold, or that he had any waking idea whether it was or not.", "삼촌은 어떤 결론에도 이른 기색 없이 다시 발을 끌며 들어와 자기 의자에 앉더니, 불에 손을 쬐기 시작했다. 추워서도 아니었고, 추운지 아닌지 정신 차려 헤아릴 만한 생각이 있어서도 아니었다."]], [["‘What did you think of my brother, sir?’ he asked, when he by-and-by discovered what he was doing, left off, reached over to the chimney-piece, and took his clarionet case down.", "\"내 동생을 어찌 보셨소, 선생?\" 그는 이윽고 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 깨닫고는, 그 짓을 그만두고 벽난로 선반 쪽으로 손을 뻗어 클라리넷 갑을 내리며 물었다."]], [["‘I was glad,’ said Arthur, very much at a loss, for his thoughts were on the brother before him; ‘to find him so well and cheerful.’", "\"동생분께서 그토록 잘 지내시고 쾌활하신 것을 알게 되어,\" 아서가 무척 난감해하며 말했으니, 그의 생각은 눈앞의 형에게 쏠려 있었던 까닭이다. \"기뻤습니다.\""]], [["‘Ha!’ muttered the old man, ‘yes, yes, yes, yes, yes!’", "\"하!\" 노인이 중얼거렸다. \"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래, 그래!\""]], [["Arthur wondered what he could possibly want with the clarionet case.", "아서는 노인이 클라리넷 갑을 가지고 도대체 뭘 하려는 건지 의아했다."], ["He did not want it at all.", "노인은 그것을 가지고 아무것도 하려는 게 아니었다."], ["He discovered, in due time, that it was not the little paper of snuff (which was also on the chimney-piece), put it back again, took down the snuff instead, and solaced himself with a pinch.", "그는 한참 만에 그것이 코담배 작은 봉지(이것 역시 벽난로 선반 위에 있었다)가 아니라는 것을 알아채고는, 다시 제자리에 놓고 대신 코담배를 집어 들어, 한 줌으로 스스로를 달랬다."], ["He was as feeble, spare, and slow in his pinches as in everything else, but a certain little trickling of enjoyment of them played in the poor worn nerves about the corners of his eyes and mouth.", "그는 코담배를 즐기는 데서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로 기력 없고 깡마르고 느렸으나, 그것을 즐기는 어떤 가느다란 흐름이 그의 가엾게 닳아빠진 신경 속에서, 눈가와 입가에 어려 일렁였다."]], [["‘Amy, Mr Clennam.", "\"에이미 말이오, 클레넘 씨."], ["What do you think of her?’", "그 애를 어찌 보시오?\""]], [["‘I am much impressed, Mr Dorrit, by all that I have seen of her and thought of her.’", "\"제가 그분에 관해 보고 생각한 모든 것에 깊은 인상을 받았습니다, 도릿 씨.\""]], [["‘My brother would have been quite lost without Amy,’ he returned.", "\"내 동생은 에이미 없이는 아주 어찌할 바를 몰랐을 거요.\" 노인이 대답했다."], ["‘We should all have been lost without Amy.", "\"우리 모두 에이미 없이는 어찌할 바를 몰랐을 거요."], ["She is a very good girl, Amy.", "아주 착한 아이지, 에이미는."], ["She does her duty.’", "제 도리를 다하는 아이요.\""]], [["Arthur fancied that he heard in these praises a certain tone of custom, which he had heard from the father last night with an inward protest and feeling of antagonism.", "아서는 이 칭찬에서 어떤 익숙해진 어조를 들은 듯했다. 어젯밤 아버지에게서도 들었던, 마음속으로 항변하고 적의를 느끼게 했던 그 어조였다."], ["It was not that they stinted her praises, or were insensible to what she did for them; but that they were lazily habituated to her, as they were to all the rest of their condition.", "그들이 그녀에 대한 칭찬에 인색했다거나, 그녀가 그들을 위해 하는 일에 무감각했던 것은 아니다. 다만 그들이 자기들 처지의 다른 모든 것에 익숙해졌듯, 그녀에게도 게으르게 익숙해졌던 것이다."], ["He fancied that although they had before them, every day, the means of comparison between her and one another and themselves, they regarded her as being in her necessary place; as holding a position towards them all which belonged to her, like her name or her age.", "그는, 그들이 날마다 그녀와 서로를, 또 그녀와 자기 자신을 견주어 볼 수단을 눈앞에 두고 있으면서도, 그녀가 마땅히 있어야 할 자리에 있는 것으로 여긴다고 느꼈다. 그녀의 이름이나 나이처럼, 그들 모두에 대해 그녀에게 본디 속하는 어떤 위치를 차지하고 있는 것으로 여긴다고 말이다."], ["He fancied that they viewed her, not as having risen away from the prison atmosphere, but as appertaining to it; as being vaguely what they had a right to expect, and nothing more.", "그는, 그들이 그녀를 감옥의 공기에서 솟아올라 벗어난 사람으로 보는 것이 아니라, 그 공기에 속한 사람으로 — 막연히 자기들이 당연히 기대할 권리가 있는 무엇으로, 그 이상은 아닌 것으로 — 본다고 느꼈다."]], [["Her uncle resumed his breakfast, and was munching toast sopped in coffee, oblivious of his guest, when the third bell rang.", "그녀의 삼촌은 다시 아침을 먹기 시작해, 커피에 적신 토스트를 우물거리며 손님을 까맣게 잊고 있다가, 세 번째 종이 울렸다."], ["That was Amy, he said, and went down to let her in; leaving the visitor with as vivid a picture on his mind of his begrimed hands, dirt-worn face, and decayed figure, as if he were still drooping in his chair.", "저게 에이미라고 그가 말하고는, 그녀를 들이러 내려갔다. 방문객의 마음속에는 노인의 때 낀 손과 흙때로 닳은 얼굴과 쇠락한 모습이, 마치 그가 여전히 의자에 늘어져 있기라도 하다는 듯 생생한 그림으로 남았다."]], [["She came up after him, in the usual plain dress, and with the usual timid manner.", "그녀가 그를 뒤따라 올라왔다. 여느 때처럼 수수한 옷차림에, 여느 때처럼 머뭇거리는 태도였다."], ["Her lips were a little parted, as if her heart beat faster than usual.", "그녀의 입술이 조금 벌어져 있었으니, 마치 가슴이 평소보다 빨리 뛰기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, Amy,’ said her uncle, ‘has been expecting you some time.’", "\"에이미, 클레넘 씨가,\" 삼촌이 말했다. \"한동안 너를 기다리고 계셨다.\""]], [["‘I took the liberty of sending you a message.’", "\"제가 외람되이 전갈을 보냈습니다.\""]], [["‘I received the message, sir.’", "\"전갈은 받았어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Are you going to my mother’s this morning?", "\"오늘 아침 저희 어머니 댁에 가시는 길입니까?"], ["I think not, for it is past your usual hour.’", "평소 시각이 지난 걸 보니 아닌 듯합니다만.\""]], [["‘Not to-day, sir.", "\"오늘은 아니에요, 선생님."], ["I am not wanted to-day.’", "오늘은 부르심이 없어요.\""]], [["‘Will you allow Me to walk a little way in whatever direction you may be going?", "\"가시는 방향이 어디든, 조금이나마 함께 걸어가도 괜찮겠습니까?"], ["I can then speak to you as we walk, both without detaining you here, and without intruding longer here myself.’", "그러면 걸으면서 당신께 말씀을 드릴 수 있으니, 여기서 당신을 붙들지도 않고, 저 또한 여기 더 오래 머물러 폐를 끼치지도 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["She looked embarrassed, but said, if he pleased.", "그녀는 난처해 보였으나, 그러시다면 그렇게 하시라고 말했다."], ["He made a pretence of having mislaid his walking-stick, to give her time to set the bedstead right, to answer her sister’s impatient knock at the wall, and to say a word softly to her uncle.", "그는 지팡이를 어디 둔지 모르겠다는 시늉을 함으로써, 그녀에게 침상을 바로잡고 벽을 두드리는 언니의 성마른 노크에 응답하고 삼촌에게 나직이 한마디를 건넬 시간을 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 지팡이를 찾았고, 그들은 계단을 내려갔다."], ["Then he found it, and they went down-stairs; she first, he following; the uncle standing at the stair-head, and probably forgetting them before they had reached the ground floor.", "그녀가 앞서고 그가 뒤따랐다. 삼촌은 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었는데, 아마 그들이 일 층에 닿기도 전에 그들을 잊었을 것이다."]], [["Mr Cripples’s pupils, who were by this time coming to school, desisted from their morning recreation of cuffing one another with bags and books, to stare with all the eyes they had at a stranger who had been to see Dirty Dick.", "이때쯤 학교에 오고 있던 크리플즈 씨의 학생들은, 가방과 책으로 서로를 후려치던 아침 놀이를 멈추고, 더러운 딕을 찾아온 낯선 이를 제 눈을 한껏 동원해 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["They bore the trying spectacle in silence, until the mysterious visitor was at a safe distance; when they burst into pebbles and yells, and likewise into reviling dances, and in all respects buried the pipe of peace with so many savage ceremonies, that, if Mr Cripples had been the chief of the Cripplewayboo tribe with his war-paint on, they could scarcely have done greater justice to their education.", "그들은 그 견디기 어려운 구경거리를 잠자코 견뎌 냈는데, 그 수수께끼의 방문객이 안전한 거리에 이르고 나서야 비로소 자갈과 고함을 터뜨렸고, 또한 욕설 섞인 춤도 추었으며, 어느 모로 보나 어찌나 많은 야만적 의식을 치르며 화평의 담뱃대를 파묻었던지, 만일 크리플즈 씨가 전투 화장을 한 크리플웨이부 부족의 추장이었더라도 그들이 받은 교육에 그보다 더 알맞은 본때를 보이기는 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["In the midst of this homage, Mr Arthur Clennam offered his arm to Little Dorrit, and Little Dorrit took it.", "이 경의에 찬 의식 한가운데에서, 아서 클레넘 씨가 리틀 도릿에게 팔을 내밀었고, 리틀 도릿이 그 팔을 잡았다. \"철교로 가실까요?\""], ["‘Will you go by the Iron Bridge,’ said he, ‘where there is an escape from the noise of the street?’ Little Dorrit answered, if he pleased, and presently ventured to hope that he would ‘not mind’ Mr Cripples’s boys, for she had herself received her education, such as it was, in Mr Cripples’s evening academy.", "그가 말했다. \"거기라면 거리의 소음에서 벗어날 수 있으니까요.\""], ["He returned, with the best will in the world, that Mr Cripples’s boys were forgiven out of the bottom of his soul.", "리틀 도릿은 그러시다면 좋다고 대답하고는, 이윽고 크리플즈 씨의 아이들을 \"언짢게 여기지 마시기\"를 바란다고 조심스레 말했다. 자기도 비록 변변찮으나마 크리플즈 씨의 야간 학원에서 교육을 받았기 때문이라고 했다."], ["Thus did Cripples unconsciously become a master of the ceremonies between them, and bring them more naturally together than Beau Nash might have done if they had lived in his golden days, and he had alighted from his coach and six for the purpose.", "그는 더없이 흔쾌한 마음으로, 크리플즈 씨의 아이들을 영혼 깊은 바닥에서부터 용서한다고 대답했다. 이리하여 크리플즈는 자기도 모르는 사이에 그들 사이의 의전관이 되어, 만일 그들이 보 내시의 황금기에 살았고 그가 바로 그 일을 위해 사두마차에서 내려섰더라도 해내지 못했을 만큼, 두 사람을 한결 자연스럽게 가까워지게 해 주었다."]], [["The morning remained squally, and the streets were miserably muddy, but no rain fell as they walked towards the Iron Bridge.", "아침나절은 여전히 돌풍이 일었고 거리는 비참하리만치 진창이었으나, 그들이 철교를 향해 걷는 동안 비는 내리지 않았다."], ["The little creature seemed so young in his eyes, that there were moments when he found himself thinking of her, if not speaking to her, as if she were a child.", "그 작은 사람이 그의 눈에는 어찌나 어려 보였던지, 그녀에게 말을 걸지는 않더라도 마치 그녀가 어린아이이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 생각하는 자신을 발견하는 순간들이 있었다."], ["Perhaps he seemed as old in her eyes as she seemed young in his.", "어쩌면 그녀의 눈에 비친 그가 그녀가 그의 눈에 어려 보이는 만큼이나 늙어 보였는지도 모른다."]], [["‘I am sorry to hear you were so inconvenienced last night, sir, as to be locked in.", "\"어젯밤 그렇게 불편을 겪으시고 갇히기까지 하셨다니 죄송해요, 선생님."], ["It was very unfortunate.’", "무척 안된 일이었어요.\""]], [["It was nothing, he returned.", "아무것도 아니라고 그가 대답했다."], ["He had had a very good bed.", "아주 좋은 잠자리에서 잤다고 했다."]], [["‘Oh yes!’ she said quickly; ‘she believed there were excellent beds at the coffee-house.’ He noticed that the coffee-house was quite a majestic hotel to her, and that she treasured its reputation.", "\"오, 그래요!\" 그녀가 얼른 말했다. 그 커피점에는 훌륭한 잠자리가 있으리라 믿는다고 했다. 그는 그 커피점이 그녀에게는 자못 위풍당당한 호텔이며, 그녀가 그 명성을 소중히 여긴다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘I believe it is very expensive,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘but my father has told me that quite beautiful dinners may be got there. And wine,’ she added timidly.", "\"무척 비싼 곳이라고 들었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"하지만 거기서는 정말 훌륭한 만찬을 들 수 있다고 아버지가 말씀하셨어요. 그리고 술도요.\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 덧붙였다."]], [["‘Were you ever there?’", "\"거기 가 보신 적이 있나요?\""]], [["‘Oh no!", "\"오, 아니요!"], ["Only into the kitchen to fetch hot water.’", "더운물을 가지러 부엌에 들어가 본 것뿐이에요.\""]], [["To think of growing up with a kind of awe upon one as to the luxuries of that superb establishment, the Marshalsea Hotel!", "그 으리으리한 시설, 곧 마셜시 호텔의 사치에 대해 일종의 경외감을 품은 채 자라났다는 것을 생각해 보라!"]], [["‘I asked you last night,’ said Clennam, ‘how you had become acquainted with my mother. Did you ever hear her name before she sent for you?’", "\"어젯밤에 여쭈었지요,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"어떻게 저희 어머니와 알게 되셨는지. 어머니가 사람을 보내 부르기 전에 그 이름을 들어 본 적이 있나요?\" \"아니요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘No, sir.’\n‘Do you think your father ever did?’", "\"아버님께서는 들어 보신 적이 있다고 생각하나요?\""]], [["‘No, sir.’", "\"아니요, 선생님.\""]], [["He met her eyes raised to his with so much wonder in them (she was scared when the encounter took place, and shrunk away again), that he felt it necessary to say:", "그가 그녀의 눈을 마주쳤을 때, 그를 향해 들린 그 눈에는 어찌나 큰 의아함이 담겨 있던지(눈이 마주치자 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 다시 움츠러들었다), 그는 이렇게 말할 필요를 느꼈다."]], [["‘I have a reason for asking, which I cannot very well explain; but you must, on no account, suppose it to be of a nature to cause you the least alarm or anxiety.", "\"제가 여쭙는 데에는 이유가 있는데, 그걸 잘 설명드릴 수는 없습니다. 하지만 그것이 당신께 조금이라도 놀라움이나 불안을 일으킬 성질의 것이라고는 결코 짐작하지 마십시오."], ["Quite the reverse.", "정반대입니다."], ["And you think that at no time of your father’s life was my name of Clennam ever familiar to him?’", "그러면 아버님의 생애 어느 때에도 클레넘이라는 제 이름이 그분께 친숙했던 적이 없다고 생각하시는군요?\""]], [["‘No, sir.’", "\"아닙니다, 선생님.\""]], [["He felt, from the tone in which she spoke, that she was glancing up at him with those parted lips; therefore he looked before him, rather than make her heart beat quicker still by embarrassing her afresh.", "그는 그녀의 말투에서, 그녀가 그 벌어진 입술로 자기를 흘끔 올려다보고 있음을 느꼈다. 그래서 그는 다시 그녀를 당황하게 해 가슴을 한층 빨리 뛰게 하기보다는, 앞을 바라보았다."]], [["Thus they emerged upon the Iron Bridge, which was as quiet after the roaring streets as though it had been open country.", "이리하여 그들은 철교에 다다랐는데, 으르렁대던 거리에 비하면 그곳은 마치 탁 트인 들판이기라도 한 듯 고요했다."], ["The wind blew roughly, the wet squalls came rattling past them, skimming the pools on the road and pavement, and raining them down into the river.", "바람이 거칠게 불었고, 젖은 돌풍이 덜컹거리며 그들 곁을 스쳐 지나가, 길과 보도의 웅덩이를 훑어 그 물을 강물 속으로 뿌려 댔다."], ["The clouds raced on furiously in the lead-coloured sky, the smoke and mist raced after them, the dark tide ran fierce and strong in the same direction.", "납빛 하늘에서는 구름이 사납게 내달렸고, 매연과 안개가 그 뒤를 쫓아 내달렸으며, 어두운 조수가 같은 방향으로 거세고 세차게 흘렀다."], ["Little Dorrit seemed the least, the quietest, and weakest of Heaven’s creatures.", "리틀 도릿은 하늘이 지으신 피조물 가운데 가장 작고 가장 조용하고 가장 약한 존재로 보였다."]], [["‘Let me put you in a coach,’ said Clennam, very nearly adding ‘my poor child.’", "\"마차에 태워 드리지요.\" 클레넘이, 하마터면 \"가엾은 아이여\" 하고 덧붙일 뻔하며 말했다."]], [["She hurriedly declined, saying that wet or dry made little difference to her; she was used to go about in all weathers.", "그녀는 서둘러 사양하면서, 젖든 마르든 자기에게는 별 차이가 없다고, 어떤 날씨든 다니는 데 익숙하다고 말했다."], ["He knew it to be so, and was touched with more pity; thinking of the slight figure at his side, making its nightly way through the damp dark boisterous streets to such a place of rest.", "그는 그것이 사실임을 알았기에 한층 더 연민에 가슴이 저렸다. 그의 곁에 선 그 가냘픈 모습이, 그러한 안식의 처소를 향해 축축하고 어둡고 사나운 밤거리를 밤마다 헤쳐 가는 것을 떠올리니 말이다."]], [["‘You spoke so feelingly to me last night, sir, and I found afterwards that you had been so generous to my father, that I could not resist your message, if it was only to thank you; especially as I wished very much to say to you--’ she hesitated and trembled, and tears rose in her eyes, but did not fall.", "\"어젯밤 제게 그토록 다정하게 말씀해 주셨고, 게다가 나중에 알고 보니 제 아버지께 그토록 너그러이 대해 주셨더군요. 그래서 저는 선생님의 전갈을 뿌리칠 수가 없었어요. 그저 고맙다는 인사를 드리기 위해서라도요. 더구나 선생님께 꼭 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있었거든요—\" 그녀는 머뭇거리며 떨었고, 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐으나 흘러내리지는 않았다."]], [["‘To say to me--?’", "\"제게 말씀하시려던 것이—?\""]], [["‘That I hope you will not misunderstand my father.", "\"제 아버지를 오해하지 말아 주십사 하는 거예요."], ["Don’t judge him, sir, as you would judge others outside the gates.", "그분을 저 문밖의 다른 사람들을 판단하듯 판단하지 말아 주세요, 선생님."], ["He has been there so long!", "그분은 거기 너무 오래 계셨어요!"], ["I never saw him outside, but I can understand that he must have grown different in some things since.’", "저는 그분이 밖에 계신 걸 본 적이 없지만, 그분이 그 뒤로 어떤 점에서는 달라지셨으리라는 건 이해할 수 있어요.\""]], [["‘My thoughts will never be unjust or harsh towards him, believe me.’", "\"제 생각은 결코 그분께 부당하거나 모질지 않을 것입니다. 믿어 주십시오.\""]], [["‘Not,’ she said, with a prouder air, as the misgiving evidently crept upon her that she might seem to be abandoning him, ‘not that he has anything to be ashamed of for himself, or that I have anything to be ashamed of for him.", "\"그렇다고 해서,\" 그녀가 한층 자랑스러운 기색으로 말했으니, 자기가 그분을 저버리는 것처럼 보일지 모른다는 불안이 분명 그녀에게 스며든 까닭이었다."], ["He only requires to be understood.", "\"그분께서 스스로 부끄러워하실 일이 있다거나, 제가 그분 때문에 부끄러워할 일이 있다는 건 아니에요."], ["I only ask for him that his life may be fairly remembered.", "그분은 그저 이해받기를 바라실 뿐이에요. 저는 다만 그분의 삶이 공정하게 기억되기를 바랄 뿐이에요."], ["All that he said was quite true.", "그분이 하신 말씀은 모두 사실이었어요."], ["It all happened just as he related it.", "모두 그분이 말씀하신 그대로 일어난 일이에요."], ["He is very much respected.", "그분은 무척 존경받으세요."], ["Everybody who comes in, is glad to know him.", "안에 들어오는 사람은 누구나 그분과 아는 사이가 되기를 기뻐해요."], ["He is more courted than anyone else.", "그분은 다른 누구보다도 더 떠받들어지세요."], ["He is far more thought of than the Marshal is.’", "마셜 소장보다도 훨씬 더 받들어지신다고요.\""]], [["If ever pride were innocent, it was innocent in Little Dorrit when she grew boastful of her father.", "자존심이 무고한 적이 있다면, 리틀 도릿이 아버지를 두고 으스대게 될 때 그것은 무고했다."]], [["‘It is often said that his manners are a true gentleman’s, and quite a study.", "\"그분의 몸가짐이 참된 신사의 것이며 본받을 만하다는 말을 흔히들 해요."], ["I see none like them in that place, but he is admitted to be superior to all the rest.", "그 안에서 그분 같은 분은 본 적이 없지만, 그분이 다른 모든 사람보다 뛰어나시다는 건 누구나 인정하는 바예요."], ["This is quite as much why they make him presents, as because they know him to be needy.", "사람들이 그분께 선물을 드리는 것도, 그분이 궁핍하시다는 걸 알아서이기도 하지만, 그만큼이나 이런 까닭에서예요."], ["He is not to be blamed for being in need, poor love.", "궁핍하시다고 해서 그분을 탓할 수는 없어요, 가엾은 분."], ["Who could be in prison a quarter of a century, and be prosperous!’", "사반세기를 감옥에 있으면서 형편이 넉넉할 사람이 어디 있겠어요!\""]], [["What affection in her words, what compassion in her repressed tears, what a great soul of fidelity within her, how true the light that shed false brightness round him!", "그녀의 말에 담긴 그 애정, 억눌린 눈물에 담긴 그 연민, 그녀 안에 깃든 그 위대한 충실의 영혼, 그를 둘러싸고 거짓된 광채를 비추는 그 빛이 어찌 그리도 진실했던가!"]], [["‘If I have found it best to conceal where my home is, it is not because I am ashamed of him.", "\"제가 집이 어디인지 숨기는 게 상책이라고 여겨 왔다면, 그건 그분을 부끄러워해서가 아니에요."], ["God forbid!", "천만에요!"], ["Nor am I so much ashamed of the place itself as might be supposed.", "또 그곳 자체를 사람들이 짐작할 만큼 그렇게 부끄러워하는 것도 아니고요."], ["People are not bad because they come there.", "거기 온다고 해서 나쁜 사람들인 건 아니에요."], ["I have known numbers of good, persevering, honest people come there through misfortune.", "저는 선량하고 끈기 있고 정직한 사람들이 불운 때문에 거기 오는 걸 숱하게 봐 왔어요."], ["They are almost all kind-hearted to one another.", "그들은 거의 다 서로에게 친절해요."], ["And it would be ungrateful indeed in me, to forget that I have had many quiet, comfortable hours there; that I had an excellent friend there when I was quite a baby, who was very very fond of me; that I have been taught there, and have worked there, and have slept soundly there.", "그리고 거기서 조용하고 편안한 시간을 많이 보냈다는 걸 제가 잊는다면 그건 참으로 배은망덕한 일일 거예요. 제가 아주 갓난아기였을 때 거기엔 저를 무척이나 아껴 준 더없이 좋은 벗이 있었고, 거기서 가르침을 받았고, 거기서 일했고, 거기서 곤히 잠들었으니까요."], ["I think it would be almost cowardly and cruel not to have some little attachment for it, after all this.’", "이 모든 것이 있고 나서도 그곳에 작은 애착 하나 품지 않는다면, 그건 거의 비겁하고 모진 짓이 아닐까 싶어요.\""]], [["She had relieved the faithful fulness of her heart, and modestly said, raising her eyes appealingly to her new friend’s, ‘I did not mean to say so much, nor have I ever but once spoken about this before. But it seems to set it more right than it was last night. I said I wished you had not followed me, sir. I don’t wish it so much now, unless you should think--indeed I don’t wish it at all, unless I should have spoken so confusedly, that--that you can scarcely understand me, which I am afraid may be the case.’", "그녀는 마음에 가득 찬 충실함을 토로하고 나서, 새 벗에게 호소하듯 눈을 들어 겸손히 말했다. \"이렇게 많은 말을 할 생각은 아니었어요. 이 일을 입에 올린 적은 전에 딱 한 번뿐이었고요. 하지만 이렇게 말하고 나니 어젯밤보다 일이 더 바로잡힌 것 같네요. 저는 선생님께서 저를 따라오지 않으셨더라면 했다고 말씀드렸지요. 이젠 그렇게까지 바라지는 않아요. 다만 선생님께서 혹시—정말이지 그렇게 바라지는 않아요. 제가 너무 두서없이 말씀드려서 선생님이—선생님이 저를 거의 알아들으실 수 없게 되었다면 몰라도요. 그런데 그렇게 되었을까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["He told her with perfect truth that it was not the case; and putting himself between her and the sharp wind and rain, sheltered her as well as he could.", "그는 결코 그렇지 않다고 더없이 진실하게 말해 주었고, 그녀와 매서운 바람과 비 사이에 자기 몸을 세워, 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 막아 주었다."]], [["‘I feel permitted now,’ he said, ‘to ask you a little more concerning your father.", "\"이제 허락을 얻은 듯하니,\" 그가 말했다."], ["Has he many creditors?’", "\"아버님에 관해 조금 더 여쭙겠습니다. 채권자가 많습니까?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["a great number.’", "아주 많아요.\""]], [["‘I mean detaining creditors, who keep him where he is?’", "\"제 말은, 그분을 지금 그 자리에 붙들어 두는, 구금시킨 채권자들 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Oh yes!", "\"오, 네!"], ["a great number.’", "아주 많아요.\""]], [["‘Can you tell me--I can get the information, no doubt, elsewhere, if you cannot--who is the most influential of them?’", "\"제게 말씀해 주실 수 있겠습니까—물론 당신이 모르시면 다른 데서 알아낼 수도 있겠지만—그들 가운데 가장 영향력 있는 자가 누구인지요?\""]], [["Little Dorrit said, after considering a little, that she used to hear long ago of Mr Tite Barnacle as a man of great power.", "리틀 도릿은 잠시 생각하더니, 오래전에 타이트 배너클 씨가 대단한 권세를 지닌 사람이라는 말을 듣곤 했다고 말했다."], ["He was a commissioner, or a board, or a trustee, ‘or something.’ He lived in Grosvenor Square, she thought, or very near it.", "그는 위원이거나, 위원회거나, 관리인이거나, \"뭐 그런 거\"였다. 그녀가 생각하기로 그는 그로스브너 광장에, 아니면 그 아주 가까운 곳에 살았다."], ["He was under Government--high in the Circumlocution Office.", "그는 정부 소속으로—에두름국에서 높은 자리에 있었다."], ["She appeared to have acquired, in her infancy, some awful impression of the might of this formidable Mr Tite Barnacle of Grosvenor Square, or very near it, and the Circumlocution Office, which quite crushed her when she mentioned him.", "그녀는 어린 시절에, 그로스브너 광장, 아니면 그 아주 가까운 곳에 사는 이 무시무시한 타이트 배너클 씨와 에두름국의 위력에 대해 어떤 끔찍한 인상을 품게 된 듯했으니, 그를 입에 올릴 때 그것이 그녀를 완전히 짓눌렀다."]], [["‘It can do no harm,’ thought Arthur, ‘if I see this Mr Tite Barnacle.’", "'이 타이트 배너클 씨를 만나 본들,' 아서는 생각했다. '해 될 건 없겠지.'"]], [["The thought did not present itself so quietly but that her quickness intercepted it.", "그 생각은 그녀의 영민함이 가로채지 못할 만큼 그리 차분하게 떠오르지 못했다."], ["‘Ah!’ said Little Dorrit, shaking her head with the mild despair of a lifetime.", "\"아!\" 리틀 도릿이, 한평생 겪어 온 온화한 절망을 담아 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘Many people used to think once of getting my poor father out, but you don’t know how hopeless it is.’", "\"한때는 제 가엾은 아버지를 빼내려고 생각한 사람이 많았지만, 그게 얼마나 가망 없는 일인지 선생님은 모르세요.\""]], [["She forgot to be shy at the moment, in honestly warning him away from the sunken wreck he had a dream of raising; and looked at him with eyes which assuredly, in association with her patient face, her fragile figure, her spare dress, and the wind and rain, did not turn him from his purpose of helping her.", "그녀는 그 순간 수줍어하는 것도 잊은 채, 그가 끌어올릴 꿈을 꾸는 그 가라앉은 난파선으로부터 그를 진심으로 물러서게 하려 했다. 그러면서 그녀는 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈은—참을성 있는 얼굴, 가냘픈 몸매, 수수한 옷차림, 그리고 바람과 비와 어우러져—그가 그녀를 돕겠다는 목적에서 결코 돌아서게 하지 못했다."]], [["‘Even if it could be done,’ said she--‘and it never can be done now--where could father live, or how could he live?", "\"설령 그게 가능하다 해도,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["I have often thought that if such a change could come, it might be anything but a service to him now.", "\"이제 와선 결코 가능하지 않지만요—아버지가 어디서 사실 수 있겠어요. 어떻게 사실 수 있겠어요?"], ["People might not think so well of him outside as they do there.", "저는 종종 생각했어요. 그런 변화가 온다 해도, 이제 와선 그게 아버지께 조금도 도움이 안 될지 모른다고요."], ["He might not be so gently dealt with outside as he is there.", "밖에서는 사람들이 거기서만큼 아버지를 좋게 여기지 않을지도 몰라요."], ["He might not be so fit himself for the life outside as he is for that.’", "밖에서는 거기서만큼 아버지를 너그러이 대하지 않을지도 몰라요. 아버지 자신도 거기 생활에 맞는 만큼 밖의 생활에는 맞지 않으실지도 모르고요.\""]], [["Here for the first time she could not restrain her tears from falling; and the little thin hands he had watched when they were so busy, trembled as they clasped each other.", "여기서 그녀는 처음으로 눈물이 흘러내리는 것을 참지 못했다. 그토록 부지런할 때 그가 지켜보았던 가늘고 작은 두 손이, 서로를 맞잡으며 떨렸다."]], [["‘It would be a new distress to him even to know that I earn a little money, and that Fanny earns a little money.", "\"제가 돈을 조금 번다는 것, 패니가 돈을 조금 번다는 것을 아시는 것조차 아버지껜 새로운 괴로움이 될 거예요."], ["He is so anxious about us, you see, feeling helplessly shut up there.", "보세요, 아버지는 거기 속절없이 갇혀 계신 처지라, 저희를 그토록 걱정하시거든요."], ["Such a good, good father!’", "정말 좋은, 좋은 아버지예요!\""]], [["He let the little burst of feeling go by before he spoke.", "그는 그 짧은 감정의 북받침이 지나가기를 기다린 뒤에야 입을 열었다."], ["It was soon gone.", "그것은 곧 지나갔다."], ["She was not accustomed to think of herself, or to trouble any one with her emotions.", "그녀는 자기 자신을 생각하거나, 누구든 자기 감정으로 번거롭게 하는 데 익숙하지 않았다."], ["He had but glanced away at the piles of city roofs and chimneys among which the smoke was rolling heavily, and at the wilderness of masts on the river, and the wilderness of steeples on the shore, indistinctly mixed together in the stormy haze, when she was again as quiet as if she had been plying her needle in his mother’s room.", "그가 다만, 매연이 무겁게 굽이치는 도시의 지붕과 굴뚝 더미를, 강 위의 돛대들의 황야를, 강기슭의 첨탑들의 황야를—폭풍의 안개 속에 흐릿하게 뒤섞인 그것들을—흘끔 바라보고 시선을 돌렸을 때, 그녀는 어머니의 방에서 바늘을 놀리고 있기라도 한 듯 다시금 차분해져 있었다."]], [["‘You would be glad to have your brother set at liberty?’", "\"오라버니가 풀려나면 기쁘시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Oh very, very glad, sir!’", "\"오, 정말, 정말 기쁠 거예요, 선생님!\""]], [["‘Well, we will hope for him at least.", "\"그럼 적어도 그분에 대해서는 희망을 가져 봅시다."], ["You told me last night of a friend you had?’", "어젯밤 친구가 한 분 있다고 말씀하셨지요?\""]], [["His name was Plornish, Little Dorrit said.", "그의 이름은 플로니시라고 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["And where did Plornish live?", "그래서 플로니시는 어디 사는가?"], ["Plornish lived in Bleeding Heart Yard.", "플로니시는 블리딩 하트 야드에 살았다."], ["He was ‘only a plasterer,’ Little Dorrit said, as a caution to him not to form high social expectations of Plornish.", "그는 \"그저 미장이일 뿐\"이라고 리틀 도릿이 말했는데, 그가 플로니시에 대해 높은 사회적 기대를 품지 않도록 미리 일러두려는 것이었다."], ["He lived at the last house in Bleeding Heart Yard, and his name was over a little gateway.", "그는 블리딩 하트 야드의 맨 끝 집에 살았고, 작은 출입문 위에 그의 이름이 적혀 있었다."]], [["Arthur took down the address and gave her his.", "아서는 그 주소를 적어 두고, 그녀에게 자기 주소를 건넸다."], ["He had now done all he sought to do for the present, except that he wished to leave her with a reliance upon him, and to have something like a promise from her that she would cherish it.", "그는 이제 당장 하려던 일을 다 한 셈이었다. 다만 그녀가 자기를 의지하도록 남겨 두고 싶었고, 그녀가 그 마음을 소중히 간직하겠다는 약속 같은 것을 받고 싶었다."]], [["‘There is one friend!’ he said, putting up his pocketbook.", "\"여기 친구가 하나 있군요!\" 그가 수첩을 챙겨 넣으며 말했다."], ["‘As I take you back--you are going back?’", "\"당신을 돌려보내 드리면서—돌아가시는 거지요?\""]], [["‘Oh yes!", "\"오, 네!"], ["going straight home.’", "곧장 집으로 가요.\""]], [["‘--As I take you back,’ the word home jarred upon him, ‘let me ask you to persuade yourself that you have another friend. I make no professions, and say no more.’", "\"—당신을 돌려보내 드리면서,\" 집이라는 말이 그에게 거슬렸다. \"한 가지 청을 드릴게요. 당신에게 또 다른 친구가 하나 있다고 스스로 믿어 주십시오. 더는 다짐도 두지 않고, 더 말씀도 드리지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You are truly kind to me, sir.", "\"선생님은 제게 참으로 친절하세요."], ["I am sure I need no more.’", "더는 필요 없어요, 정말.\""]], [["They walked back through the miserable muddy streets, and among the poor, mean shops, and were jostled by the crowds of dirty hucksters usual to a poor neighbourhood.", "그들은 비참한 진창 거리를 지나, 가난하고 초라한 가게들 사이를 걸어 돌아왔고, 가난한 동네에 으레 있기 마련인 더러운 행상꾼 무리에게 떠밀렸다."], ["There was nothing, by the short way, that was pleasant to any of the five senses.", "이 가까운 길에는 다섯 감각 가운데 어느 하나에라도 즐거울 만한 것이라곤 없었다."], ["Yet it was not a common passage through common rain, and mire, and noise, to Clennam, having this little, slender, careful creature on his arm.", "그렇지만 클레넘에게는, 이 작고 가냘프고 야무진 사람을 팔에 의지하게 하고 걷는 것이, 흔한 비와 진창과 소음 속을 지나는 흔한 통행은 아니었다."], ["How young she seemed to him, or how old he to her; or what a secret either to the other, in that beginning of the destined interweaving of their stories, matters not here.", "그녀가 그의 눈에 얼마나 어려 보였는지, 또는 그가 그녀의 눈에 얼마나 늙어 보였는지, 또는 그들의 이야기가 운명적으로 얽혀 들기 시작하는 그 첫머리에 두 사람이 서로에게 어떤 비밀이었는지는 여기서 중요하지 않다."], ["He thought of her having been born and bred among these scenes, and shrinking through them now, familiar yet misplaced; he thought of her long acquaintance with the squalid needs of life, and of her innocence; of her solicitude for others, and her few years, and her childish aspect.", "그는 그녀가 이런 풍경 속에서 태어나 자랐고, 익숙하면서도 어울리지 않는 모습으로 지금 그 속을 움츠리며 지나가는 것을 생각했다. 그는 그녀가 삶의 누추한 궁핍과 오래 친숙해 온 것을, 그리고 그녀의 순결함을, 남을 위하는 그녀의 마음씀을, 그녀의 어린 나이를, 그리고 그녀의 어린아이 같은 모습을 생각했다."]], [["They were come into the High Street, where the prison stood, when a voice cried, ‘Little mother, little mother!’ Little Dorrit stopping and looking back, an excited figure of a strange kind bounced against them (still crying ‘little mother’), fell down, and scattered the contents of a large basket, filled with potatoes, in the mud.", "감옥이 서 있는 하이 스트리트로 접어들었을 때, 어떤 목소리가 외쳤다. \"작은 엄마, 작은 엄마!\" 리틀 도릿이 멈춰 서서 뒤를 돌아보자, 어떤 이상한 종류의 흥분한 형체가 그들에게 부딪쳐 왔고(여전히 \"작은 엄마\"를 외치면서), 넘어지면서 감자가 가득 든 큰 바구니의 내용물을 진창에 쏟아 흩뜨려 놓았다."]], [["‘Oh, Maggy,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘what a clumsy child you are!’", "\"오, 매기,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"어쩜 이렇게 덤벙대는 아이람!\""]], [["Maggy was not hurt, but picked herself up immediately, and then began to pick up the potatoes, in which both Little Dorrit and Arthur Clennam helped.", "매기는 다치지 않았고 곧장 몸을 일으키더니 감자를 줍기 시작했는데, 리틀 도릿과 아서 클레넘도 함께 거들었다."], ["Maggy picked up very few potatoes and a great quantity of mud; but they were all recovered, and deposited in the basket.", "매기는 감자는 아주 조금 줍고 진흙은 잔뜩 주워 담았으나, 어쨌든 다 거두어들여 바구니에 담았다."], ["Maggy then smeared her muddy face with her shawl, and presenting it to Mr Clennam as a type of purity, enabled him to see what she was like.", "그러고 나서 매기는 진흙투성이 얼굴을 숄로 문질러 댔고, 그것을 순결의 본보기인 양 클레넘 씨에게 내보임으로써, 그로 하여금 그녀의 생김새가 어떤지 보게 해 주었다."]], [["She was about eight-and-twenty, with large bones, large features, large feet and hands, large eyes and no hair.", "그녀는 스물여덟 살쯤으로, 뼈대가 굵고, 이목구비가 크고, 손발이 크고, 눈이 크고, 머리카락은 없었다."], ["Her large eyes were limpid and almost colourless; they seemed to be very little affected by light, and to stand unnaturally still.", "그 큰 눈은 맑고 거의 색이 없었으며, 빛에 거의 영향을 받지 않고 부자연스러우리만치 가만히 멈춰 있는 듯했다."], ["There was also that attentive listening expression in her face, which is seen in the faces of the blind; but she was not blind, having one tolerably serviceable eye.", "또한 그녀의 얼굴에는 맹인의 얼굴에서 보이는, 골똘히 귀 기울이는 표정이 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 맹인이 아니었으니, 그런대로 쓸 만한 눈이 하나 있었다."], ["Her face was not exceedingly ugly, though it was only redeemed from being so by a smile; a good-humoured smile, and pleasant in itself, but rendered pitiable by being constantly there.", "그녀의 얼굴은 지독히 추하지는 않았으나, 그것이 추함을 면한 것은 오직 미소 덕분이었다. 마음씨 좋은 미소로 그 자체로는 보기 좋았지만, 늘 그 자리에 머물러 있는 까닭에 가엾어 보이게 했다."], ["A great white cap, with a quantity of opaque frilling that was always flapping about, apologised for Maggy’s baldness, and made it so very difficult for her old black bonnet to retain its place upon her head, that it held on round her neck like a gipsy’s baby.", "늘 펄럭거리는 불투명한 주름 장식이 잔뜩 달린 커다란 흰 모자가 매기의 대머리를 가려 주는 구실을 했는데, 그 탓에 그녀의 낡은 검은 보닛이 머리 위에 제자리를 지키기란 무척 어려워서, 보닛은 집시의 갓난아기처럼 그녀의 목에 매달려 있었다."], ["A commission of haberdashers could alone have reported what the rest of her poor dress was made of, but it had a strong general resemblance to seaweed, with here and there a gigantic tea-leaf.", "그 나머지 초라한 옷이 무엇으로 만들어졌는지는 잡화상 위원회나 되어야 알아낼 수 있었을 테지만, 전체적으로는 해초와 매우 흡사했고, 군데군데 거대한 찻잎이 박혀 있었다."], ["Her shawl looked particularly like a tea-leaf after long infusion.", "그녀의 숄은 특히 오래 우려낸 찻잎과 똑 닮아 보였다."]], [["Arthur Clennam looked at Little Dorrit with the expression of one saying, ‘May I ask who this is?’ Little Dorrit, whose hand this Maggy, still calling her little mother, had begun to fondle, answered in words (they were under a gateway into which the majority of the potatoes had rolled).", "아서 클레넘은 \"이분이 누구인지 여쭤도 될까요?\" 하고 말하는 듯한 표정으로 리틀 도릿을 바라보았다. 여전히 그녀를 작은 엄마라 부르며 이 매기가 손을 쓰다듬기 시작한 리틀 도릿이 말로 대답했다(그들은 출입문 아래에 있었는데, 감자 대부분이 그리로 굴러들어 있었다)."]], [["‘This is Maggy, sir.’", "\"이 애는 매기예요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Maggy, sir,’ echoed the personage presented.", "\"매기예요, 선생님.\" 소개받은 당사자가 따라 말했다."], ["‘Little mother!’", "\"작은 엄마!\""]], [["‘She is the grand-daughter--’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"이 애는 손녀딸이에요—\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Grand-daughter,’ echoed Maggy.", "\"손녀딸이에요.\" 매기가 따라 말했다."]], [["‘Of my old nurse, who has been dead a long time.", "\"오래전에 돌아가신 제 옛 유모의요."], ["Maggy, how old are you?’", "매기, 너 몇 살이지?\""]], [["‘Ten, mother,’ said Maggy.", "\"열 살이에요, 엄마.\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["‘You can’t think how good she is, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, with infinite tenderness.", "\"이 애가 얼마나 착한지 선생님은 모르실 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 한없는 다정함으로 말했다."]], [["‘Good _she_ is,’ echoed Maggy, transferring the pronoun in a most expressive way from herself to her little mother.", "\"착한 건 이 애예요.\" 매기가 그 대명사를 가장 의미심장한 방식으로 자기 자신에게서 작은 엄마에게로 옮기며 따라 말했다."]], [["‘Or how clever,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"또 얼마나 영리한지요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘She goes on errands as well as any one.’ Maggy laughed.", "\"이 애는 누구 못지않게 심부름을 잘해요.\" 매기가 웃었다."], ["‘And is as trustworthy as the Bank of England.’ Maggy laughed.", "\"그리고 잉글랜드 은행만큼이나 믿을 만하고요.\" 매기가 웃었다."], ["‘She earns her own living entirely.", "\"온전히 제 힘으로 벌어 살아가요."], ["Entirely, sir!’ said Little Dorrit, in a lower and triumphant tone.", "온전히 말이에요, 선생님!\" 리틀 도릿이 한층 낮고 의기양양한 어조로 말했다."], ["‘Really does!’", "\"정말 그래요!\""]], [["‘What is her history?’ asked Clennam.", "\"이 아이의 내력은 어떻게 됩니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘Think of that, Maggy?’ said Little Dorrit, taking her two large hands and clapping them together.", "\"생각해 봐, 매기!\" 리틀 도릿이 그녀의 커다란 두 손을 잡아 마주 치며 말했다."], ["‘A gentleman from thousands of miles away, wanting to know your history!’", "\"수천 마일 떨어진 데서 오신 신사분이 네 내력을 알고 싶어 하셔!\""]], [["‘_My_ history?’ cried Maggy.", "\"내 내력이요?\" 매기가 외쳤다."], ["‘Little mother.’", "\"작은 엄마.\""]], [["‘She means me,’ said Little Dorrit, rather confused; ‘she is very much attached to me. Her old grandmother was not so kind to her as she should have been; was she, Maggy?’", "\"이 애 말은 저를 가리키는 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 당황하며 말했다. \"이 애는 저를 무척 따라요. 이 애의 늙은 할머니가 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 이 애에게 친절하지 못했거든요. 그렇지, 매기?\""]], [["Maggy shook her head, made a drinking vessel of her clenched left hand, drank out of it, and said, ‘Gin.’ Then beat an imaginary child, and said, ‘Broom-handles and pokers.’", "매기는 고개를 젓더니, 왼손을 꼭 쥐어 술잔 모양을 만들어 거기서 무언가를 들이켜는 시늉을 하며 \"진.\"이라고 말했다. 그러고는 보이지 않는 아이를 때리는 시늉을 하며 \"빗자루 손잡이랑 부지깽이.\"라고 말했다."]], [["‘When Maggy was ten years old,’ said Little Dorrit, watching her face while she spoke, ‘she had a bad fever, sir, and she has never grown any older ever since.’", "\"매기가 열 살이었을 때,\" 리틀 도릿이 말하면서 매기의 얼굴을 살폈다. \"이 애는 심한 열병을 앓았어요, 선생님. 그리고 그 뒤로 더는 한 살도 자라지 못했어요.\""]], [["‘Ten years old,’ said Maggy, nodding her head.", "\"열 살이에요.\" 매기가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘But what a nice hospital!", "\"그런데 얼마나 좋은 병원이었는데요!"], ["So comfortable, wasn’t it?", "그렇게 편안했어요, 안 그래요?"], ["Oh so nice it was.", "오, 정말 좋았어요."], ["Such a Ev’nly place!’", "그렇게 천국 같은 곳이라니!\""]], [["‘She had never been at peace before, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, turning towards Arthur for an instant and speaking low, ‘and she always runs off upon that.’", "\"이 애는 그 전엔 한 번도 편안한 적이 없었어요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 잠깐 아서 쪽으로 돌아서며 나직이 말했다. \"그래서 늘 그 이야기로 빠져들곤 해요.\""]], [["‘Such beds there is there!’ cried Maggy.", "\"거긴 그렇게 좋은 침대가 있었다고요!\" 매기가 외쳤다."], ["‘Such lemonades!", "\"그렇게 좋은 레모네이드도요!"], ["Such oranges!", "그렇게 좋은 오렌지도요!"], ["Such d’licious broth and wine!", "그렇게 맛있는 죽이랑 포도주도요!"], ["Such Chicking!", "그렇게 좋은 닭고기도요!"], ["Oh, AIN’T it a delightful place to go and stop at!’", "오, 거긴 가서 머물기에 정말 즐거운 곳 아녜요!\""]], [["‘So Maggy stopped there as long as she could,’ said Little Dorrit, in her former tone of telling a child’s story; the tone designed for Maggy’s ear, ‘and at last, when she could stop there no longer, she came out.", "\"그래서 매기는 거기 있을 수 있는 데까지 오래 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 아이에게 옛이야기를 들려주는 아까의 어조로 말했으니, 매기의 귀를 위해 마련된 어조였다."], ["Then, because she was never to be more than ten years old, however long she lived--’", "\"그리고 마침내 더는 거기 있을 수 없게 되자, 밖으로 나왔지요. 그런데 이 애는 아무리 오래 살아도 열 살을 넘기지 못하게 되었기 때문에—\""]], [["‘However long she lived,’ echoed Maggy.", "\"아무리 오래 살아도요.\" 매기가 따라 말했다."]], [["‘--And because she was very weak; indeed was so weak that when she began to laugh she couldn’t stop herself--which was a great pity--’", "\"—그리고 무척 약했기 때문에—정말이지 어찌나 약했던지, 한번 웃기 시작하면 스스로 멈출 수가 없었거든요—그게 참 안된 일이었지요—\""]], [["(Maggy mighty grave of a sudden.)", "(매기가 별안간 자못 엄숙해졌다.)"]], [["‘--Her grandmother did not know what to do with her, and for some years was very unkind to her indeed.", "\"—할머니는 이 애를 어찌해야 할지 몰랐고, 몇 해 동안 정말 모질게 대했어요. 그러다 마침내 세월이 흐르면서, 매기는 스스로를 가다듬으려고, 아주 야무지고 아주 부지런하려고 애쓰기 시작했어요."], ["At length, in course of time, Maggy began to take pains to improve herself, and to be very attentive and very industrious; and by degrees was allowed to come in and out as often as she liked, and got enough to do to support herself, and does support herself.", "그래서 차츰차츰 마음대로 드나드는 게 허락되었고, 제 한 몸 건사할 만큼의 일거리도 얻게 되어, 정말로 제 힘으로 살아가요. 그리고 이게,\""], ["And that,’ said Little Dorrit, clapping the two great hands together again, ‘is Maggy’s history, as Maggy knows!’", "리틀 도릿이 그 커다란 두 손을 다시 마주 치며 말했다. \"매기가 아는 한, 매기의 내력이랍니다!\""]], [["Ah!", "아!"], ["But Arthur would have known what was wanting to its completeness, though he had never heard of the words Little mother; though he had never seen the fondling of the small spare hand; though he had had no sight for the tears now standing in the colourless eyes; though he had had no hearing for the sob that checked the clumsy laugh.", "그러나 아서는, 작은 엄마라는 말을 한 번도 들어 보지 못했더라도, 그 작고 야윈 손을 쓰다듬는 광경을 한 번도 본 적이 없었더라도, 지금 색 없는 눈에 고인 눈물을 볼 눈이 없었더라도, 그 어설픈 웃음을 가로막은 흐느낌을 들을 귀가 없었더라도, 그 내력의 온전함에 무엇이 빠져 있는지 알았을 것이다."], ["The dirty gateway with the wind and rain whistling through it, and the basket of muddy potatoes waiting to be spilt again or taken up, never seemed the common hole it really was, when he looked back to it by these lights.", "바람과 비가 휘파람 소리를 내며 지나가는 그 더러운 출입문과, 다시 쏟아지거나 거두어지기를 기다리는 진흙투성이 감자 바구니는, 그가 이런 빛에 비추어 그것을 돌이켜 볼 때면, 결코 실제 그것이었던 흔한 구멍으로는 보이지 않았다."], ["Never, never!", "결코, 결코!"]], [["They were very near the end of their walk, and they now came out of the gateway to finish it.", "그들의 산책은 거의 끝나 가고 있었고, 이제 그들은 그것을 마무리하려고 출입문에서 나왔다."], ["Nothing would serve Maggy but that they must stop at a grocer’s window, short of their destination, for her to show her learning.", "매기는 한사코, 목적지에 닿기 전에 식료품점 진열창 앞에 멈춰 서서 자기 학식을 뽐내야겠다고 했다."], ["She could read after a sort; and picked out the fat figures in the tickets of prices, for the most part correctly.", "그녀는 그런대로 글을 읽을 줄 알았고, 가격표에 적힌 통통한 숫자를 짚어 냈는데, 대체로 정확했다."], ["She also stumbled, with a large balance of success against her failures, through various philanthropic recommendations to Try our Mixture, Try our Family Black, Try our Orange-flavoured Pekoe, challenging competition at the head of Flowery Teas; and various cautions to the public against spurious establishments and adulterated articles.", "그녀는 또 우리 혼합차를 잡숴 보세요, 우리 가정용 홍차를 잡숴 보세요, 화차의 으뜸으로 어떤 경쟁에도 도전하는 우리 오렌지 향 페코를 잡숴 보세요 하는 갖가지 자선적인 권유며, 엉터리 가게와 불순물 섞은 물건을 조심하라는 갖가지 경고를 더듬더듬 읽어 나갔는데, 실패보다 성공이 훨씬 더 많았다."], ["When he saw how pleasure brought a rosy tint into Little Dorrit’s face when Maggy made a hit, he felt that he could have stood there making a library of the grocer’s window until the rain and wind were tired.", "매기가 하나 맞힐 때마다 즐거움이 리틀 도릿의 얼굴에 발그레한 빛을 띄우는 것을 보고, 그는 비와 바람이 지칠 때까지라도 거기 서서 그 식료품점 진열창으로 도서관 하나를 만들 수 있을 것 같았다."]], [["The court-yard received them at last, and there he said goodbye to Little Dorrit.", "마침내 마당이 그들을 맞아들였고, 거기서 그는 리틀 도릿에게 작별을 고했다."], ["Little as she had always looked, she looked less than ever when he saw her going into the Marshalsea lodge passage, the little mother attended by her big child.", "늘 작아 보이던 그녀였지만, 큰 아이를 거느린 작은 엄마인 그녀가 마셜시 수위실 통로로 들어가는 것을 그가 보았을 때, 그녀는 그 어느 때보다도 작아 보였다."]], [["The cage door opened, and when the small bird, reared in captivity, had tamely fluttered in, he saw it shut again; and then he came away.", "새장 문이 열렸고, 갇혀 길러진 그 작은 새가 얌전히 퍼덕이며 들어가자, 그는 그 문이 다시 닫히는 것을 보았다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 자리를 떠났다."]], [["# CHAPTER 10. Containing the whole Science of Government", "# CHAPTER 10. Containing the whole Science of Government"]], [["The Circumlocution Office was (as everybody knows without being told) the most important Department under Government.", "에두름국은 (말하지 않아도 누구나 알듯이) 정부 아래에서 가장 중요한 부서였다."], ["No public business of any kind could possibly be done at any time without the acquiescence of the Circumlocution Office.", "어떤 종류의 공무도 에두름국의 묵인 없이는 어느 때고 이루어질 수 없었다."], ["Its finger was in the largest public pie, and in the smallest public tart.", "그 손가락은 가장 커다란 공공의 파이 속에도, 가장 작은 공공의 타르트 속에도 들어가 있었다."], ["It was equally impossible to do the plainest right and to undo the plainest wrong without the express authority of the Circumlocution Office.", "에두름국의 명시적 권한 없이는 가장 명백한 옳음을 행하는 것도, 가장 명백한 그름을 바로잡는 것도 똑같이 불가능했다."], ["If another Gunpowder Plot had been discovered half an hour before the lighting of the match, nobody would have been justified in saving the parliament until there had been half a score of boards, half a bushel of minutes, several sacks of official memoranda, and a family-vault full of ungrammatical correspondence, on the part of the Circumlocution Office.", "또 한 번의 화약 음모 사건이 도화선에 불을 붙이기 삼십 분 전에 발각되었다 한들, 에두름국 측에서 위원회를 열대여섯 차례 열고, 회의록을 반 부셸쯤 작성하고, 공문 비망록을 몇 자루 쌓고, 비문법적인 서신으로 가문 납골당 하나를 가득 채우기 전까지는, 그 누구도 의회를 구해 낼 정당한 자격이 없었을 것이다."]], [["This glorious establishment had been early in the field, when the one sublime principle involving the difficult art of governing a country, was first distinctly revealed to statesmen.", "이 영광스러운 기관은, 한 나라를 통치하는 그 어려운 기술에 깃든 단 하나의 숭고한 원리가 정치가들에게 처음으로 또렷이 계시되었을 때, 일찌감치 그 마당에 나와 있었다."], ["It had been foremost to study that bright revelation and to carry its shining influence through the whole of the official proceedings.", "그 빛나는 계시를 연구하고 그 찬란한 영향력을 공무 전반에 두루 미치게 하는 데 가장 앞장섰던 것이다."], ["Whatever was required to be done, the Circumlocution Office was beforehand with all the public departments in the art of perceiving--HOW NOT TO DO IT.", "무엇이 행해져야 하든, 에두름국은 그것을 지각하는 기술에서, 즉 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가 하는 기술에서, 모든 공공 부서를 앞질렀다."]], [["Through this delicate perception, through the tact with which it invariably seized it, and through the genius with which it always acted on it, the Circumlocution Office had risen to overtop all the public departments; and the public condition had risen to be--what it was.", "이 섬세한 지각력을 통하여, 그것을 한결같이 포착해 내는 그 수완을 통하여, 그리고 늘 그것에 따라 움직이는 그 천재성을 통하여, 에두름국은 모든 공공 부서를 압도하는 자리에 올랐다. 그리고 공공의 형편은 — 지금 그 모양이 된 것이다."]], [["It is true that How not to do it was the great study and object of all public departments and professional politicians all round the Circumlocution Office.", "어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가가, 에두름국을 둘러싼 모든 공공 부서와 직업적 정치가들의 위대한 연구이자 목표였던 것은 사실이다."], ["It is true that every new premier and every new government, coming in because they had upheld a certain thing as necessary to be done, were no sooner come in than they applied their utmost faculties to discovering How not to do it.", "어떤 일이 행해질 필요가 있다고 주창한 덕분에 권좌에 오른 새 총리와 새 정부가 하나같이, 들어서기가 무섭게 그것을 어떻게 하면 하지 않을 것인가를 알아내는 데 온 역량을 쏟았던 것도 사실이다."], ["It is true that from the moment when a general election was over, every returned man who had been raving on hustings because it hadn’t been done, and who had been asking the friends of the honourable gentleman in the opposite interest on pain of impeachment to tell him why it hadn’t been done, and who had been asserting that it must be done, and who had been pledging himself that it should be done, began to devise, How it was not to be done.", "총선이 끝나는 순간부터, 그것이 행해지지 않았다고 유세장에서 핏대를 올리던 모든 당선자가, 반대 진영의 그 존경할 만한 신사의 벗들에게 탄핵을 무릅쓰고서라도 왜 그것이 행해지지 않았는지 답하라고 다그치던 자가, 그것은 반드시 행해져야 한다고 단언하던 자가, 그것이 행해지도록 하겠노라고 스스로 맹세하던 자가, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 궁리하기 시작했던 것도 사실이다."], ["It is true that the debates of both Houses of Parliament the whole session through, uniformly tended to the protracted deliberation, How not to do it.", "회기 내내 상하 양원의 토론이 한결같이, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가에 대한 장기간의 심의로 흘러갔던 것도 사실이다. 그러한 회기의 개회식에서 국왕의 연설이 사실상 이렇게 말했던 것도 사실이다."], ["It is true that the royal speech at the opening of such session virtually said, My lords and gentlemen, you have a considerable stroke of work to do, and you will please to retire to your respective chambers, and discuss, How not to do it.", "\"경들이여 그리고 신사 여러분, 그대들에게는 처리해야 할 상당한 분량의 일이 있으니, 부디 각자의 의사당으로 물러가, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 논의해 주기 바란다.\" 그러한 회기의 폐회식에서 국왕의 연설이 사실상 이렇게 말했던 것도 사실이다."], ["It is true that the royal speech, at the close of such session, virtually said, My lords and gentlemen, you have through several laborious months been considering with great loyalty and patriotism, How not to do it, and you have found out; and with the blessing of Providence upon the harvest (natural, not political), I now dismiss you.", "\"경들이여 그리고 신사 여러분, 그대들은 수고로운 여러 달에 걸쳐 지극한 충성심과 애국심으로 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 숙고하여, 마침내 답을 찾아내었도다. 이제 (정치적인 것이 아니라 자연적인) 수확에 대한 섭리의 가호를 빌며, 나는 그대들을 해산하노라.\""], ["All this is true, but the Circumlocution Office went beyond it.", "이 모든 것이 사실이지만, 에두름국은 그것을 넘어섰다."]], [["Because the Circumlocution Office went on mechanically, every day, keeping this wonderful, all-sufficient wheel of statesmanship, How not to do it, in motion.", "왜냐하면 에두름국은 날마다 기계처럼, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가라는, 이 경이롭고도 전능한 통치술의 수레바퀴를 줄곧 돌리고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Because the Circumlocution Office was down upon any ill-advised public servant who was going to do it, or who appeared to be by any surprising accident in remote danger of doing it, with a minute, and a memorandum, and a letter of instructions that extinguished him.", "왜냐하면 에두름국은, 그것을 하려 드는 분별없는 공복이든, 어떤 놀라운 우연으로 그것을 해 버릴 막연한 위험에 처한 듯 보이는 공복이든, 가차 없이 덮쳐, 회의록 한 건과 비망록 한 건과 지시 서한 한 통으로 그를 소멸시켜 버렸기 때문이다."], ["It was this spirit of national efficiency in the Circumlocution Office that had gradually led to its having something to do with everything.", "에두름국으로 하여금 점차 모든 일에 관여하게 만든 것은 바로 이 국가적 효율의 정신이었다."], ["Mechanicians, natural philosophers, soldiers, sailors, petitioners, memorialists, people with grievances, people who wanted to prevent grievances, people who wanted to redress grievances, jobbing people, jobbed people, people who couldn’t get rewarded for merit, and people who couldn’t get punished for demerit, were all indiscriminately tucked up under the foolscap paper of the Circumlocution Office.", "기계공, 자연철학자, 군인, 선원, 청원자, 진정인, 고충을 품은 사람들, 고충을 막고자 하는 사람들, 고충을 바로잡고자 하는 사람들, 청탁을 부리는 사람들, 청탁에 휘둘린 사람들, 공로를 세우고도 포상받지 못하는 사람들, 과실을 저지르고도 처벌받지 않는 사람들 — 이 모두가 가리지 않고 에두름국의 대판 종이 아래로 쑤셔 넣어졌다."]], [["Numbers of people were lost in the Circumlocution Office.", "수많은 사람이 에두름국에서 사라져 갔다."], ["Unfortunates with wrongs, or with projects for the general welfare (and they had better have had wrongs at first, than have taken that bitter English recipe for certainly getting them), who in slow lapse of time and agony had passed safely through other public departments; who, according to rule, had been bullied in this, over-reached by that, and evaded by the other; got referred at last to the Circumlocution Office, and never reappeared in the light of day.", "억울한 일을 당했거나 공공의 복리를 위한 계획을 품은 불운한 자들이 (애초에 후자의 처지에 놓이기보다는 차라리 억울한 일이라도 당하는 편이 나았을 것이다. 후자야말로 억울한 일을 확실히 당하게 되는 그 쓰디쓴 영국식 비방이었으니), 더디게 흐르는 세월과 고뇌 속에서 다른 공공 부서들을 무사히 통과해 온 자들이, 규정에 따라 이 부서에서는 윽박질리고 저 부서에서는 우롱당하고 또 다른 부서에서는 회피당해 온 자들이, 마침내 에두름국으로 회부되어서는 다시는 환한 대낮의 빛 속으로 나타나지 않았다."], ["Boards sat upon them, secretaries minuted upon them, commissioners gabbled about them, clerks registered, entered, checked, and ticked them off, and they melted away.", "위원회가 그들을 두고 회합했고, 비서들이 그들에 관한 회의록을 작성했고, 위원들이 그들에 관해 떠들어 댔고, 서기들이 그들을 등록하고 기입하고 대조하고 점검표에 표시했으며, 그러는 사이 그들은 녹아 사라졌다."], ["In short, all the business of the country went through the Circumlocution Office, except the business that never came out of it; and _its_ name was Legion.", "요컨대 이 나라의 모든 사무는 에두름국을 거쳐 갔다. 단, 결코 그곳에서 나오지 못한 사무만은 예외였으니, 그 이름은 군단이었다."]], [["Sometimes, angry spirits attacked the Circumlocution Office.", "* 군단: 신약성경에서 수많은 악귀의 무리를 가리키는 말(\"내 이름은 군단이니 우리가 많음이라\")."], ["Sometimes, parliamentary questions were asked about it, and even parliamentary motions made or threatened about it by demagogues so low and ignorant as to hold that the real recipe of government was, How to do it.", "이따금 노한 영혼들이 에두름국을 공격했다. 이따금 그에 관한 의회 질의가 제기되었고, 심지어 통치의 진짜 비방이란 어떻게 하면 그것을 할 것인가라고 믿을 만큼 천박하고 무지한 선동가들이 그에 관한 의회 동의안을 발의하거나 발의하겠다고 으름장을 놓기도 했다."], ["Then would the noble lord, or right honourable gentleman, in whose department it was to defend the Circumlocution Office, put an orange in his pocket, and make a regular field-day of the occasion.", "그러면 에두름국을 변호하는 일이 소관 업무인 고귀한 경이나 존경할 만한 신사가 주머니에 오렌지 하나를 넣고서, 그 기회를 본격적인 한판 승부의 날로 삼곤 했다."], ["Then would he come down to that house with a slap upon the table, and meet the honourable gentleman foot to foot.", "그러면 그는 탁자를 탁 치며 그 의사당으로 내려와, 그 존경할 만한 신사와 한 치도 물러섬 없이 맞붙곤 했다."], ["Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that the Circumlocution Office not only was blameless in this matter, but was commendable in this matter, was extollable to the skies in this matter.", "그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국이 이 문제에서 비난받을 데가 없을 뿐 아니라 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 하늘 끝까지 추켜올려져 마땅하다고 일러 주곤 했다."], ["Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that, although the Circumlocution Office was invariably right and wholly right, it never was so right as in this matter.", "그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국은 한결같이 옳고 전적으로 옳지만 이 문제에서만큼 옳았던 적은 없다고 일러 주곤 했다."], ["Then would he be there to tell that honourable gentleman that it would have been more to his honour, more to his credit, more to his good taste, more to his good sense, more to half the dictionary of commonplaces, if he had left the Circumlocution Office alone, and never approached this matter.", "그러면 그는 그 자리에 서서 그 존경할 만한 신사에게, 에두름국을 가만히 내버려 두고 이 문제에 결코 손대지 않았더라면 그의 명예에, 그의 신망에, 그의 안목에, 그의 양식에, 그리고 상투적 표현 사전 절반에 더 보탬이 되었을 것이라고 일러 주곤 했다."], ["Then would he keep one eye upon a coach or crammer from the Circumlocution Office sitting below the bar, and smash the honourable gentleman with the Circumlocution Office account of this matter.", "그러면 그는 한쪽 눈으로는 의장석 난간 아래에 앉아 있는 에두름국 측 코치나 입막음꾼을 살피면서, 이 문제에 관한 에두름국 측 진술로 그 존경할 만한 신사를 박살 내곤 했다."], ["And although one of two things always happened; namely, either that the Circumlocution Office had nothing to say and said it, or that it had something to say of which the noble lord, or right honourable gentleman, blundered one half and forgot the other; the Circumlocution Office was always voted immaculate by an accommodating majority.", "그리고 늘 둘 중 하나가 일어났으니, 즉 에두름국이 할 말이 없으면서도 그것을 말하거나, 아니면 할 말이 있되 그 고귀한 경이나 존경할 만한 신사가 그 절반은 더듬거리고 나머지 절반은 잊어버리거나 하는 것이었음에도, 에두름국은 만사를 너그럽게 받아 주는 다수에 의해 늘 흠 하나 없다는 표결을 받았다."]], [["Such a nursery of statesmen had the Department become in virtue of a long career of this nature, that several solemn lords had attained the reputation of being quite unearthly prodigies of business, solely from having practised, How not to do it, as the head of the Circumlocution Office.", "이러한 성격의 오랜 이력 덕분에 에두름국은 정치가의 요람이 되어 있었으니, 여러 근엄한 경들이 오로지 에두름국의 수장으로서 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 연마했다는 그 이유 하나만으로, 그야말로 이 세상 것 같지 않은 사무의 귀재라는 명성을 얻기에 이르렀다."], ["As to the minor priests and acolytes of that temple, the result of all this was that they stood divided into two classes, and, down to the junior messenger, either believed in the Circumlocution Office as a heaven-born institution that had an absolute right to do whatever it liked; or took refuge in total infidelity, and considered it a flagrant nuisance.", "그 신전의 하급 사제와 시동들로 말하자면, 이 모든 일의 결과로 그들은 두 부류로 나뉘어, 막내 사환에 이르기까지, 에두름국을 무엇이든 마음대로 할 절대적 권리를 지닌 하늘이 내린 기관으로 신봉하거나, 아니면 완전한 불신 속으로 도피하여 그것을 명백한 골칫거리로 여기거나 했다."]], [["The Barnacle family had for some time helped to administer the Circumlocution Office.", "배너클 일가는 한동안 에두름국 운영을 거들어 왔다."], ["The Tite Barnacle Branch, indeed, considered themselves in a general way as having vested rights in that direction, and took it ill if any other family had much to say to it.", "사실 타이트 배너클 가계는 으레 자기들이 그 방면에 기득권을 지녔다고 여겼고, 다른 어느 가문이 그에 관해 이러쿵저러쿵 많은 말을 하면 못마땅해했다."], ["The Barnacles were a very high family, and a very large family.", "배너클가는 지체 높은 가문이었고, 또 매우 큰 가문이었다."], ["They were dispersed all over the public offices, and held all sorts of public places.", "그들은 온갖 관청에 흩어져 있었고 온갖 공직을 차지하고 있었다."], ["Either the nation was under a load of obligation to the Barnacles, or the Barnacles were under a load of obligation to the nation.", "국가가 배너클가에 막대한 빚을 지고 있거나, 아니면 배너클가가 국가에 막대한 빚을 지고 있거나 둘 중 하나였다."], ["It was not quite unanimously settled which; the Barnacles having their opinion, the nation theirs.", "어느 쪽인지는 만장일치로 정해지지 않았으니, 배너클가는 배너클가대로, 국가는 국가대로 제 의견이 있었다."]], [["The Mr Tite Barnacle who at the period now in question usually coached or crammed the statesman at the head of the Circumlocution Office, when that noble or right honourable individual sat a little uneasily in his saddle by reason of some vagabond making a tilt at him in a newspaper, was more flush of blood than money.", "지금 문제 삼는 시기에, 에두름국 수장 자리에 앉은 정치가가 어떤 떠돌이 같은 자가 신문 지면에서 그를 향해 창을 겨누는 통에 안장 위에서 다소 불편하게 흔들릴 때면 으레 그를 지도하거나 입막음 거리를 채워 주던 그 타이트 배너클 씨는, 돈보다 핏줄이 더 넉넉한 인물이었다."], ["As a Barnacle he had his place, which was a snug thing enough; and as a Barnacle he had of course put in his son Barnacle Junior in the office.", "배너클가의 일원으로서 그는 제 자리를 차지하고 있었는데, 꽤 짭짤한 자리였다. 그리고 배너클가의 일원으로서 그는 당연히 아들 배너클 주니어를 그 관청에 들여놓았다."], ["But he had intermarried with a branch of the Stiltstalkings, who were also better endowed in a sanguineous point of view than with real or personal property, and of this marriage there had been issue, Barnacle junior and three young ladies.", "그러나 그는 스틸트스토킹가의 한 가계와 혼인을 맺었는데, 이들 또한 실물 재산이나 동산보다는 핏줄의 관점에서 더 넉넉한 집안이었다. 이 혼인에서 자식이 생겼으니, 배너클 주니어와 세 아가씨였다."], ["What with the patrician requirements of Barnacle junior, the three young ladies, Mrs Tite Barnacle nee Stiltstalking, and himself, Mr Tite Barnacle found the intervals between quarter day and quarter day rather longer than he could have desired; a circumstance which he always attributed to the country’s parsimony.", "배너클 주니어의 귀족다운 씀씀이며, 세 아가씨며, 스틸트스토킹가 출신인 타이트 배너클 부인이며, 그 자신까지 챙기다 보니, 타이트 배너클 씨는 봉급날과 봉급날 사이의 간격이 바라던 것보다 다소 길다고 느꼈는데, 그는 늘 이 사정을 나라의 인색함 탓으로 돌렸다."]], [["For Mr Tite Barnacle, Mr Arthur Clennam made his fifth inquiry one day at the Circumlocution Office; having on previous occasions awaited that gentleman successively in a hall, a glass case, a waiting room, and a fire-proof passage where the Department seemed to keep its wind.", "어느 날 아서 클레넘 씨는 타이트 배너클 씨를 찾아 에두름국에서 다섯 번째 문의를 했다. 그 전에도 그 신사를 차례로 한 차례는 어느 홀에서, 또 한 차례는 어느 유리 진열실에서, 또 한 차례는 어느 대기실에서, 또 한 차례는 그 부서가 제 바람을 보관해 두는 듯한 어느 내화 복도에서 기다려 보았던 터였다."], ["On this occasion Mr Barnacle was not engaged, as he had been before, with the noble prodigy at the head of the Department; but was absent.", "이번에 배너클 씨는 전처럼 그 부서의 수장인 고귀한 귀재와 함께 있지 않고, 자리를 비운 상태였다."], ["Barnacle Junior, however, was announced as a lesser star, yet visible above the office horizon.", "그러나 배너클 주니어가, 비록 더 작은 별이긴 해도 그 관청의 지평선 위에 떠 있는 것으로 안내되었다."]], [["With Barnacle junior, he signified his desire to confer; and found that young gentleman singeing the calves of his legs at the parental fire, and supporting his spine against the mantel-shelf.", "그는 배너클 주니어와 면담하고 싶다는 뜻을 밝혔다. 그러자 그 젊은 신사가 부친의 난롯불에 종아리를 그슬리며 등을 벽난로 선반에 기댄 채 있는 것을 보게 되었다."], ["It was a comfortable room, handsomely furnished in the higher official manner; and presenting stately suggestions of the absent Barnacle, in the thick carpet, the leather-covered desk to sit at, the leather-covered desk to stand at, the formidable easy-chair and hearth-rug, the interposed screen, the torn-up papers, the dispatch-boxes with little labels sticking out of them, like medicine bottles or dead game, the pervading smell of leather and mahogany, and a general bamboozling air of How not to do it.", "그곳은 안락한 방으로, 고위 관료다운 격식에 따라 호화롭게 꾸며져 있었으며, 자리를 비운 배너클의 위풍당당한 자취를 곳곳에서 풍기고 있었다. 두툼한 양탄자, 앉아서 쓰는 가죽 덮인 책상, 서서 쓰는 가죽 덮인 책상, 위압적인 안락의자와 난로 깔개, 가운데 놓인 가리개, 갈가리 찢긴 서류들, 약병이나 죽은 사냥감처럼 작은 꼬리표가 삐죽 튀어나온 공문 상자들, 사방에 밴 가죽과 마호가니 냄새, 그리고 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가 하는 전반적으로 어리둥절하게 만드는 분위기가 그러했다."]], [["The present Barnacle, holding Mr Clennam’s card in his hand, had a youthful aspect, and the fluffiest little whisker, perhaps, that ever was seen.", "지금 이 배너클은, 클레넘 씨의 명함을 손에 든 채, 앳된 모습에다, 아마도 여태껏 본 것 중 가장 솜털 같은 자그마한 구레나룻을 기르고 있었다."], ["Such a downy tip was on his callow chin, that he seemed half fledged like a young bird; and a compassionate observer might have urged that, if he had not singed the calves of his legs, he would have died of cold.", "그 보송보송한 솜털 끝이 그의 풋내 나는 턱에 돋아 있어, 마치 깃털이 반쯤 난 어린 새 같았다. 동정심 많은 관찰자라면 그가 종아리를 불에 그슬리지 않았더라면 추워서 죽었으리라고 주장할 만했다."], ["He had a superior eye-glass dangling round his neck, but unfortunately had such flat orbits to his eyes and such limp little eyelids that it wouldn’t stick in when he put it up, but kept tumbling out against his waistcoat buttons with a click that discomposed him very much.", "그는 목에 고급 외알 안경을 늘어뜨리고 있었으나, 안타깝게도 눈자위가 어찌나 평평하고 작은 눈꺼풀이 어찌나 흐물흐물한지, 그것을 끼워 올려도 자리에 박혀 있지 않고 자꾸 흘러내려 조끼 단추에 부딪히며 딸깍 소리를 냈고, 그럴 때마다 그는 몹시 당황했다."]], [["‘Oh, I say.", "\"아, 저기요."], ["Look here!", "이거 보세요!"], ["My father’s not in the way, and won’t be in the way to-day,’ said Barnacle Junior.", "아버지는 안 계시고, 오늘은 안 나오실 겁니다.\" 배너클 주니어가 말했다."], ["‘Is this anything that I can do?’", "\"제가 처리할 수 있는 일인가요?\""]], [["(Click!", "(딸깍!"], ["Eye-glass down.", "외알 안경이 떨어졌다."], ["Barnacle Junior quite frightened and feeling all round himself, but not able to find it.)", "배너클 주니어는 잔뜩 놀라 제 몸을 더듬거렸으나 그것을 찾지 못했다.)"]], [["‘You are very good,’ said Arthur Clennam.", "\"친절하시군요.\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘I wish however to see Mr Barnacle.’", "\"하지만 저는 배너클 씨를 뵙고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘But I say.", "\"아 그런데 말이죠."], ["Look here!", "이거 보세요!"], ["You haven’t got any appointment, you know,’ said Barnacle Junior.", "약속을 잡지 않으셨잖아요, 아시다시피.\" 배너클 주니어가 말했다."]], [["(By this time he had found the eye-glass, and put it up again.)", "(이때쯤 그는 외알 안경을 찾아 다시 끼워 올렸다.)"]], [["‘No,’ said Arthur Clennam.", "\"네.\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘That is what I wish to have.’", "\"바로 그 약속을 잡고 싶은 겁니다.\""]], [["‘But I say.", "\"아 그런데 말이죠."], ["Look here!", "이거 보세요!"], ["Is this public business?’ asked Barnacle junior.", "이게 공무인가요?\" 배너클 주니어가 물었다."]], [["(Click!", "(딸깍!"], ["Eye-glass down again.", "외알 안경이 또 떨어졌다."], ["Barnacle Junior in that state of search after it that Mr Clennam felt it useless to reply at present.)", "배너클 주니어가 그것을 찾느라 한창인 통에, 클레넘 씨는 지금 대답해 봐야 소용없겠다고 느꼈다.)"]], [["‘Is it,’ said Barnacle junior, taking heed of his visitor’s brown face, ‘anything about--Tonnage--or that sort of thing?’", "\"이게 말이죠,\" 배너클 주니어가 방문객의 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴을 유심히 살피며 말했다. \"혹시 — 톤수에 관한 — 그런 종류의 일인가요?\""]], [["(Pausing for a reply, he opened his right eye with his hand, and stuck his glass in it, in that inflammatory manner that his eye began watering dreadfully.)", "(대답을 기다리느라 잠시 멈춘 그는, 한 손으로 오른쪽 눈을 벌리고 거기에 안경을 박아 넣었는데, 그 모양이 어찌나 자극적이었던지 눈에서 눈물이 끔찍하게 쏟아지기 시작했다.)"]], [["‘No,’ said Arthur, ‘it is nothing about tonnage.’", "\"아닙니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"톤수에 관한 일이 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Then look here.", "\"그럼 이거 보세요."], ["Is it private business?’", "사적인 일인가요?\""]], [["‘I really am not sure.", "\"정말 잘 모르겠습니다."], ["It relates to a Mr Dorrit.’", "도릿이라는 분과 관련된 일입니다.\""]], [["‘Look here, I tell you what!", "\"이거 보세요, 그럼 이렇게 하세요!"], ["You had better call at our house, if you are going that way.", "그쪽으로 가시는 길이라면 저희 집으로 찾아가 보시는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Twenty-four, Mews Street, Grosvenor Square.", "그로스브너 광장, 뮤즈 가 24번지입니다."], ["My father’s got a slight touch of the gout, and is kept at home by it.’", "아버지가 통풍 기운이 좀 있으셔서 집에 계세요.\""]], [["(The misguided young Barnacle evidently going blind on his eye-glass side, but ashamed to make any further alteration in his painful arrangements.)", "(이 갈피를 못 잡는 젊은 배너클은 안경을 낀 쪽 눈이 분명 멀어 가는 듯했으나, 그 고통스러운 조치를 더는 바꾸기가 부끄러운 모양이었다.)"]], [["‘Thank you.", "\"감사합니다."], ["I will call there now.", "지금 그리로 가 보겠습니다."], ["Good morning.’ Young Barnacle seemed discomfited at this, as not having at all expected him to go.", "안녕히 계십시오.\" 젊은 배너클은 그가 정말 가 버리리라고는 전혀 예상하지 못했던 듯, 이 말에 낭패한 기색이었다."]], [["‘You are quite sure,’ said Barnacle junior, calling after him when he got to the door, unwilling wholly to relinquish the bright business idea he had conceived; ‘that it’s nothing about Tonnage?’", "\"정말 확실한가요,\" 그가 문가에 이르렀을 때 배너클 주니어가 그를 불러 세우며, 자신이 떠올린 그 기발한 사무적 착상을 완전히 단념하기는 싫은 듯 물었다. \"톤수에 관한 일이 아닌 게요?\""]], [["‘Quite sure.’", "\"확실합니다.\""]], [["With such assurance, and rather wondering what might have taken place if it _had_ been anything about tonnage, Mr Clennam withdrew to pursue his inquiries.", "그렇게 확언하면서, 그리고 만일 정말로 톤수에 관한 일이었다면 무슨 일이 벌어졌을까 자못 궁금해하면서, 클레넘 씨는 문의를 계속하기 위해 물러났다."]], [["Mews Street, Grosvenor Square, was not absolutely Grosvenor Square itself, but it was very near it.", "그로스브너 광장의 뮤즈 가는 엄밀히 말해 그로스브너 광장 자체는 아니었지만, 그 광장에 아주 가까웠다."], ["It was a hideous little street of dead wall, stables, and dunghills, with lofts over coach-houses inhabited by coachmen’s families, who had a passion for drying clothes and decorating their window-sills with miniature turnpike-gates.", "그곳은 막다른 담벼락과 마구간과 두엄 더미로 이루어진 흉측한 작은 거리였고, 마차 보관소 위에는 마부 가족들이 사는 다락방이 있었는데, 그들은 빨래 말리기에 열심이었고 창턱을 미니어처 통행료 징수문으로 장식하길 좋아했다."], ["The principal chimney-sweep of that fashionable quarter lived at the blind end of Mews Street; and the same corner contained an establishment much frequented about early morning and twilight for the purchase of wine-bottles and kitchen-stuff.", "그 멋들어진 동네의 으뜸가는 굴뚝 청소부가 뮤즈 가의 막다른 끝에 살았고, 같은 모퉁이에는 이른 아침과 해 질 녘에 빈 포도주병과 부엌 찌꺼기를 사들이느라 사람들이 많이 드나드는 가게가 하나 있었다."], ["Punch’s shows used to lean against the dead wall in Mews Street, while their proprietors were dining elsewhere; and the dogs of the neighbourhood made appointments to meet in the same locality.", "펀치 인형극 무대는 그 주인들이 다른 데서 식사하는 동안 뮤즈 가의 막다른 담벼락에 기대 세워져 있곤 했고, 동네 개들은 바로 그 자리에서 만나기로 약속을 잡곤 했다."], ["Yet there were two or three small airless houses at the entrance end of Mews Street, which went at enormous rents on account of their being abject hangers-on to a fashionable situation; and whenever one of these fearful little coops was to be let (which seldom happened, for they were in great request), the house agent advertised it as a gentlemanly residence in the most aristocratic part of town, inhabited solely by the elite of the beau monde.", "그래도 뮤즈 가 입구 쪽에는 바람도 통하지 않는 작은 집이 두세 채 있었는데, 멋들어진 위치에 비루하게 빌붙어 있다는 이유로 어마어마한 집세에 나갔다. 이 끔찍한 작은 닭장 같은 집 한 채가 세놓일 때마다 (드문 일이었으니, 워낙 인기가 많았다) 부동산 중개인은 그것을 도시에서 가장 귀족적인 구역에 자리한 신사용 주거지로, 오로지 상류 사교계의 엘리트만이 거주하는 곳이라고 광고했다."]], [["If a gentlemanly residence coming strictly within this narrow margin had not been essential to the blood of the Barnacles, this particular branch would have had a pretty wide selection among, let us say, ten thousand houses, offering fifty times the accommodation for a third of the money.", "이 좁은 테두리에 엄밀히 들어맞는 신사용 주거지가 배너클가의 핏줄에 필수 불가결한 것이 아니었던들, 이 특정 가계는, 이를테면 삼분의 일 값에 쉰 배의 편의를 제공하는 만 채의 집 가운데서 꽤 폭넓게 골랐을 것이다."], ["As it was, Mr Barnacle, finding his gentlemanly residence extremely inconvenient and extremely dear, always laid it, as a public servant, at the door of the country, and adduced it as another instance of the country’s parsimony.", "사정이 그러하니, 배너클 씨는 자신의 신사용 주거지가 지극히 불편하고 지극히 비싸다는 것을 알면서도, 공복답게 늘 그것을 나라의 탓으로 돌렸으며, 나라의 인색함을 보여 주는 또 하나의 사례로 들먹였다."]], [["Arthur Clennam came to a squeezed house, with a ramshackle bowed front, little dingy windows, and a little dark area like a damp waistcoat-pocket, which he found to be number twenty-four, Mews Street, Grosvenor Square.", "아서 클레넘은 우그러진 듯한 집 한 채에 이르렀는데, 다 쓰러져 가는 활처럼 휜 정면에, 작고 거무튀튀한 창들에, 축축한 조끼 주머니 같은 작고 어두운 채광 구덩이가 딸려 있었다. 그곳이 바로 그로스브너 광장 뮤즈 가 24번지였다."], ["To the sense of smell the house was like a sort of bottle filled with a strong distillation of Mews; and when the footman opened the door, he seemed to take the stopper out.", "후각으로 보자면 그 집은 뮤즈 가의 진한 증류액으로 채운 일종의 병 같았다. 그리고 하인이 문을 열었을 때, 그는 마치 그 병마개를 뽑는 듯이 보였다."]], [["The footman was to the Grosvenor Square footmen, what the house was to the Grosvenor Square houses.", "그 하인은 그로스브너 광장의 하인들 가운데서, 그 집이 그로스브너 광장의 집들 가운데서 차지하는 것과 똑같은 위치를 차지하고 있었다."], ["Admirable in his way, his way was a back and a bye way.", "나름대로 훌륭했으나, 그 나름이라는 것이 뒷길이요 샛길이었다."], ["His gorgeousness was not unmixed with dirt; and both in complexion and consistency he had suffered from the closeness of his pantry.", "그의 화려함에는 때가 섞여 있지 않은 게 아니었고, 안색으로 보나 됨됨이로 보나 그는 식료품 저장실의 답답함에 시달린 흔적이 있었다."], ["A sallow flabbiness was upon him when he took the stopper out, and presented the bottle to Mr Clennam’s nose.", "마개를 뽑아 클레넘 씨의 코에 그 병을 들이밀 때, 그에게는 누런 무기력이 감돌고 있었다."]], [["‘Be so good as to give that card to Mr Tite Barnacle, and to say that I have just now seen the younger Mr Barnacle, who recommended me to call here.’", "\"이 명함을 타이트 배너클 씨께 전해 주시고, 제가 방금 젊은 배너클 씨를 뵈었더니 이리로 찾아가 보라고 권하더라고 전해 주십시오.\""]], [["The footman (who had as many large buttons with the Barnacle crest upon them on the flaps of his pockets, as if he were the family strong box, and carried the plate and jewels about with him buttoned up) pondered over the card a little; then said, ‘Walk in.’ It required some judgment to do it without butting the inner hall-door open, and in the consequent mental confusion and physical darkness slipping down the kitchen stairs.", "그 하인은 (마치 자신이 그 집안의 금고라도 되는 양, 그리고 은식기와 보석을 단추로 채워 몸에 지니고 다니기라도 하는 양, 주머니 덮개에 배너클가의 문장이 새겨진 큼직한 단추를 잔뜩 달고 있었다) 명함을 잠시 들여다보더니 말했다. \"들어오시지요.\""], ["The visitor, however, brought himself up safely on the door-mat.", "안쪽 현관문을 박치기로 열어젖히지 않고, 또 그로 인한 정신적 혼란과 물리적 어둠 속에서 부엌 계단으로 미끄러져 떨어지지 않으면서 들어가려면 약간의 판단력이 필요했다. 그러나 방문객은 무사히 현관 매트 위에 멈추어 섰다."]], [["Still the footman said ‘Walk in,’ so the visitor followed him.", "하인이 여전히 \"들어오시지요\" 하고 말하기에, 방문객은 그를 따라갔다."], ["At the inner hall-door, another bottle seemed to be presented and another stopper taken out.", "안쪽 현관문에서 또 하나의 병이 들이밀어지고 또 하나의 마개가 뽑히는 듯했다."], ["This second vial appeared to be filled with concentrated provisions and extract of Sink from the pantry.", "이 두 번째 약병은 식료품 저장실에서 나온 농축 식료품과 하수구 진액으로 채워진 듯했다."], ["After a skirmish in the narrow passage, occasioned by the footman’s opening the door of the dismal dining-room with confidence, finding some one there with consternation, and backing on the visitor with disorder, the visitor was shut up, pending his announcement, in a close back parlour.", "좁은 복도에서 한바탕 소동이 일었으니, 하인이 자신만만하게 음산한 식당 문을 열었다가 거기 누군가가 있는 것을 보고 기겁하며 방문객 쪽으로 허둥지둥 물러난 탓이었다. 그러고 나서 방문객은 통보가 이루어지는 동안, 답답한 뒤쪽 응접실에 갇혔다."], ["There he had an opportunity of refreshing himself with both the bottles at once, looking out at a low blinding wall three feet off, and speculating on the number of Barnacle families within the bills of mortality who lived in such hutches of their own free flunkey choice.", "거기서 그는 두 병의 향기로 동시에 원기를 회복할 기회를 얻으며, 삼 피트 앞의 눈을 멀게 할 듯한 낮은 담장을 내다보았고, 사망 통계표 안에 들어가는 배너클 일가 중에서 제 발로 하인 노릇 하는 선택을 해 이런 토끼장 같은 집에서 사는 가구가 몇이나 될까 헤아려 보았다."]], [["Mr Barnacle would see him.", "배너클 씨가 그를 만나 주겠다고 했다."], ["Would he walk up-stairs?", "위층으로 올라오시겠습니까?"], ["He would, and he did; and in the drawing-room, with his leg on a rest, he found Mr Barnacle himself, the express image and presentment of How not to do it.", "그러겠다고 했고, 그래서 올라갔다. 그리고 응접실에서, 한쪽 다리를 받침대에 올려놓은 채 있는 배너클 씨 본인을, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가의 살아 있는 형상이자 표상을 보게 되었다."]], [["Mr Barnacle dated from a better time, when the country was not so parsimonious and the Circumlocution Office was not so badgered.", "배너클 씨는 더 좋은 시절, 나라가 이만큼 인색하지 않고 에두름국이 이만큼 시달리지 않던 시절부터 내려온 인물이었다."], ["He wound and wound folds of white cravat round his neck, as he wound and wound folds of tape and paper round the neck of the country.", "그는 나라의 목에 끈과 종이의 주름을 칭칭 감듯이, 제 목에 흰 크라바트의 주름을 칭칭 감았다."], ["His wristbands and collar were oppressive; his voice and manner were oppressive.", "그의 소맷부리와 옷깃은 사람을 짓눌렀고, 그의 목소리와 태도도 사람을 짓눌렀다."], ["He had a large watch-chain and bunch of seals, a coat buttoned up to inconvenience, a waistcoat buttoned up to inconvenience, an unwrinkled pair of trousers, a stiff pair of boots.", "그는 굵직한 시곗줄과 인장 꾸러미를 차고 있었고, 불편할 만큼 단추를 채운 코트와, 불편할 만큼 단추를 채운 조끼와, 주름 하나 없는 바지와, 빳빳한 부츠를 갖추고 있었다."], ["He was altogether splendid, massive, overpowering, and impracticable.", "그는 전체적으로 화려하고 육중하고 압도적이고 다루기 어려운 인물이었다."], ["He seemed to have been sitting for his portrait to Sir Thomas Lawrence all the days of his life.", "그는 평생토록 토머스 로런스 경 앞에 앉아 초상화를 그리게 한 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam?’ said Mr Barnacle.", "\"클레넘 씨인가요?\" 배너클 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Be seated.’", "\"앉으시오.\""]], [["Mr Clennam became seated.", "클레넘 씨는 앉았다."]], [["‘You have called on me, I believe,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘at the Circumlocution--’ giving it the air of a word of about five-and-twenty syllables--‘Office.’", "\"내게 찾아오셨다지요,\" 배너클 씨가, 마치 스물다섯 음절쯤 되는 단어인 양 그 단어에 거드름을 실어 말했다. \"에두름 — 국으로 말이오.\""]], [["‘I have taken that liberty.’", "\"외람되이 그랬습니다.\""]], [["Mr Barnacle solemnly bent his head as who should say, ‘I do not deny that it is a liberty; proceed to take another liberty, and let me know your business.’", "배너클 씨는 엄숙하게 고개를 숙였는데, 그 모양이 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"그게 외람된 짓임을 부인하지는 않겠소. 또 한 번 외람된 짓을 해서, 용건이나 들려주시오.\""]], [["‘Allow me to observe that I have been for some years in China, am quite a stranger at home, and have no personal motive or interest in the inquiry I am about to make.’", "\"제가 몇 해 동안 중국에 있었고, 본국에서는 영 낯선 사람인지라, 지금 드리려는 문의에 사사로운 동기나 이해관계가 전혀 없다는 점을 말씀드리고자 합니다.\""]], [["Mr Barnacle tapped his fingers on the table, and, as if he were now sitting for his portrait to a new and strange artist, appeared to say to his visitor, ‘If you will be good enough to take me with my present lofty expression, I shall feel obliged.’", "배너클 씨는 손가락으로 탁자를 톡톡 두드리며, 이제 새롭고 낯선 화가 앞에 초상화를 그리게 하느라 앉아 있기라도 한 듯, 방문객에게 이렇게 말하는 것처럼 보였다. \"지금 이 고매한 표정 그대로 나를 화폭에 담아 준다면 고맙겠소.\""]], [["‘I have found a debtor in the Marshalsea Prison of the name of Dorrit, who has been there many years.", "\"마셜시 감옥에서 도릿이라는 이름의 채무자를 한 사람 만났는데, 여러 해 동안 그곳에 있었습니다."], ["I wish to investigate his confused affairs so far as to ascertain whether it may not be possible, after this lapse of time, to ameliorate his unhappy condition.", "저는 그의 뒤엉킨 사정을 조사하여, 이만한 세월이 흐른 지금, 그의 불행한 처지를 개선하는 일이 가능하지 않을지 알아보고 싶습니다."], ["The name of Mr Tite Barnacle has been mentioned to me as representing some highly influential interest among his creditors.", "그의 채권자들 가운데 매우 영향력 있는 이해관계를 대표하는 분으로 타이트 배너클 씨의 성함이 제게 언급되었습니다."], ["Am I correctly informed?’", "제가 제대로 들은 것입니까?\""]], [["It being one of the principles of the Circumlocution Office never, on any account whatever, to give a straightforward answer, Mr Barnacle said, ‘Possibly.’", "어떤 경우에도 결코 단도직입적인 답을 주지 않는 것이 에두름국의 원칙 중 하나였으므로, 배너클 씨는 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있소.\""]], [["‘On behalf of the Crown, may I ask, or as private individual?’", "\"왕실을 대신하여 여쭙는 것입니까, 아니면 사적인 개인으로서 여쭙는 것입니까?\""]], [["‘The Circumlocution Department, sir,’ Mr Barnacle replied, ‘may have possibly recommended--possibly--I cannot say--that some public claim against the insolvent estate of a firm or copartnership to which this person may have belonged, should be enforced.", "\"에두름국은 말이오,\" 배너클 씨가 대답했다."], ["The question may have been, in the course of official business, referred to the Circumlocution Department for its consideration.", "\"어쩌면 — 어쩌면 — 잘은 모르겠으나 — 이 사람이 속해 있었을지 모르는 어떤 상회나 합자회사의 지급 불능 재산에 대해 어떤 공공의 청구권을 행사하도록 권고했을 수도 있소. 그 문제가 공무 처리 과정에서 에두름국에 검토 회부되었을 수도 있소."], ["The Department may have either originated, or confirmed, a Minute making that recommendation.’", "에두름국이 그러한 권고를 담은 회의록을 작성했거나 승인했을 수도 있소.\""]], [["‘I assume this to be the case, then.’", "\"그럼 그렇다고 가정하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘The Circumlocution Department,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘is not responsible for any gentleman’s assumptions.’", "\"에두름국은,\" 배너클 씨가 말했다. \"그 어느 신사의 가정에도 책임이 없소.\""]], [["‘May I inquire how I can obtain official information as to the real state of the case?’", "\"그 사건의 실상에 관한 공식 정보를 제가 어떻게 얻을 수 있을지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘It is competent,’ said Mr Barnacle, ‘to any member of the--Public,’ mentioning that obscure body with reluctance, as his natural enemy, ‘to memorialise the Circumlocution Department.", "\"그건 — 일반 대중,\" 배너클 씨가 그 천한 무리를, 자신의 천적인 양 마지못해 입에 올리며 말했다."], ["Such formalities as are required to be observed in so doing, may be known on application to the proper branch of that Department.’", "\"누구에게나 에두름국에 진정서를 제출할 자격이 있소. 그렇게 함에 있어 지켜야 할 형식들은, 그 부서의 적절한 부문에 신청하시면 알 수 있을 것이오.\""]], [["‘Which is the proper branch?’", "\"그 적절한 부문이 어디입니까?\""]], [["‘I must refer you,’ returned Mr Barnacle, ringing the bell, ‘to the Department itself for a formal answer to that inquiry.’", "\"그 문의에 대한 공식 답변은,\" 배너클 씨가 종을 울리며 대답했다. \"부서 자체로 가서 받으시도록 안내하겠소.\""]], [["‘Excuse my mentioning--’", "\"외람되지만 말씀드리자면 —\""]], [["‘The Department is accessible to the--Public,’ Mr Barnacle was always checked a little by that word of impertinent signification, ‘if the--Public approaches it according to the official forms; if the--Public does not approach it according to the official forms, the--Public has itself to blame.’", "\"그 부서는 — 일반 대중에게,\" 배너클 씨는 그 무엄한 의미의 단어 앞에서 늘 잠시 멈칫했다. \"공식 형식에 따라 접근하기만 한다면 누구에게나 열려 있소. 만일 — 일반 대중이 공식 형식에 따라 접근하지 않는다면, 그건 — 일반 대중 자신이 탓할 일이오.\""]], [["Mr Barnacle made him a severe bow, as a wounded man of family, a wounded man of place, and a wounded man of a gentlemanly residence, all rolled into one; and he made Mr Barnacle a bow, and was shut out into Mews Street by the flabby footman.", "배너클 씨는 가문에 상처 입은 사람이자, 지위에 상처 입은 사람이자, 신사용 주거지에 상처 입은 사람, 이 셋을 하나로 뭉뚱그린 모습으로 그에게 준엄하게 고개 숙여 인사했다. 그러자 그도 배너클 씨에게 고개 숙여 인사했고, 그 흐물흐물한 하인에 의해 뮤즈 가로 내쳐졌다."]], [["Having got to this pass, he resolved as an exercise in perseverance, to betake himself again to the Circumlocution Office, and try what satisfaction he could get there.", "이 지경에 이르자, 그는 인내심을 단련하는 셈 치고 다시 한번 에두름국으로 가, 거기서 얼마나 만족스러운 답을 얻을 수 있는지 시험해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["So he went back to the Circumlocution Office, and once more sent up his card to Barnacle junior by a messenger who took it very ill indeed that he should come back again, and who was eating mashed potatoes and gravy behind a partition by the hall fire.", "그래서 그는 에두름국으로 돌아가, 다시 한번 사환을 통해 배너클 주니어에게 명함을 올려 보냈는데, 그 사환은 그가 또 돌아온 것을 정말이지 못마땅해했고, 현관 난롯가 칸막이 뒤에서 으깬 감자와 그레이비를 먹고 있던 참이었다."]], [["He was readmitted to the presence of Barnacle junior, and found that young gentleman singeing his knees now, and gaping his weary way on to four o’clock.", "그는 다시 배너클 주니어 앞으로 들여보내졌고, 그 젊은 신사가 이번에는 무릎을 불에 그슬리며 네 시까지 지루하게 하품으로 시간을 때우고 있는 것을 보게 되었다."]], [["‘I say.", "\"이거 참."], ["Look here.", "이거 보세요."], ["You stick to us in a devil of a manner,’ Said Barnacle junior, looking over his shoulder.", "끈질기게도 들러붙으시는군요.\" 배너클 주니어가 어깨 너머로 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["‘I want to know--’", "\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다 —\""]], [["‘Look here.", "\"이거 보세요."], ["Upon my soul you mustn’t come into the place saying you want to know, you know,’ remonstrated Barnacle junior, turning about and putting up the eye-glass.", "정말이지, 여기 와서 알고 싶은 게 있다고 하시면 안 됩니다, 아시다시피.\" 배너클 주니어가 몸을 돌려 외알 안경을 끼워 올리며 항변했다."]], [["‘I want to know,’ said Arthur Clennam, who had made up his mind to persistence in one short form of words, ‘the precise nature of the claim of the Crown against a prisoner for debt, named Dorrit.’", "\"알고 싶습니다.\" 한 가지 짧은 문구로 끈질기게 밀어붙이기로 마음을 정한 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"도릿이라는 이름의 채무자에 대해 왕실이 제기한 청구의 정확한 성격을 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I say. Look here.", "\"이거 참."], ["You really are going it at a great pace, you know.", "이거 보세요. 정말 무서운 속도로 밀어붙이시는군요, 아시다시피."], ["Egad, you haven’t got an appointment,’ said Barnacle junior, as if the thing were growing serious.", "이런, 약속도 없으시면서.\" 배너클 주니어가 일이 심각해진다는 듯이 말했다."]], [["‘I want to know,’ said Arthur, and repeated his case.", "\"알고 싶습니다.\" 아서가 말하고는 자기 용건을 되풀이했다."]], [["Barnacle junior stared at him until his eye-glass fell out, and then put it in again and stared at him until it fell out again.", "배너클 주니어는 외알 안경이 떨어질 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보다가, 다시 끼워 올리고는 또 떨어질 때까지 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["‘You have no right to come this sort of move,’ he then observed with the greatest weakness.", "\"이런 식으로 밀어붙이실 권리는 없습니다.\" 그러고는 더없이 맥없는 투로 말했다."], ["‘Look here.", "\"이거 보세요."], ["What do you mean?", "무슨 뜻입니까?"], ["You told me you didn’t know whether it was public business or not.’", "이게 공무인지 아닌지 모른다고 하셨잖아요.\""]], [["‘I have now ascertained that it is public business,’ returned the suitor, ‘and I want to know’--and again repeated his monotonous inquiry.", "\"이제는 공무라는 걸 확인했습니다.\" 청원자가 대답했다. \"그래서 알고 싶습니다 —\" 그러고는 또다시 그 단조로운 문의를 되풀이했다."]], [["Its effect upon young Barnacle was to make him repeat in a defenceless way, ‘Look here!", "그것이 젊은 배너클에게 미친 효과는, 그로 하여금 속수무책으로 이렇게 되풀이하게 만든 것이었다. \"이거 보세요!"], ["Upon my SOUL you mustn’t come into the place saying you want to know, you know!’ The effect of that upon Arthur Clennam was to make him repeat his inquiry in exactly the same words and tone as before.", "정말이지, 여기 와서 알고 싶은 게 있다고 하시면 안 됩니다, 아시다시피!\" 그것이 아서 클레넘에게 미친 효과는, 그로 하여금 앞서와 똑같은 말과 똑같은 어조로 자기 문의를 되풀이하게 만든 것이었다."], ["The effect of that upon young Barnacle was to make him a wonderful spectacle of failure and helplessness.", "그것이 젊은 배너클에게 미친 효과는, 그를 무력과 속수무책의 놀라운 구경거리로 만들어 버린 것이었다."]], [["‘Well, I tell you what.", "\"그럼, 이렇게 하시지요. 이거 보세요."], ["Look here.", "비서과로 가 보시는 게 낫겠습니다.\""], ["You had better try the Secretarial Department,’ he said at last, sidling to the bell and ringing it.", "마침내 그가 종 쪽으로 슬금슬금 다가가 그것을 울리며 말했다. \"젱킨슨,\""], ["‘Jenkinson,’ to the mashed potatoes messenger, ‘Mr Wobbler!’", "으깬 감자를 먹던 사환에게 그가 말했다. \"워블러 씨 방으로!\""]], [["Arthur Clennam, who now felt that he had devoted himself to the storming of the Circumlocution Office, and must go through with it, accompanied the messenger to another floor of the building, where that functionary pointed out Mr Wobbler’s room.", "이제 자신이 에두름국 공략에 헌신했으니 끝까지 밀어붙여야 한다고 느낀 아서 클레넘은, 사환을 따라 건물의 다른 층으로 갔고, 거기서 그 직원이 워블러 씨의 방을 가리켜 주었다."], ["He entered that apartment, and found two gentlemen sitting face to face at a large and easy desk, one of whom was polishing a gun-barrel on his pocket-handkerchief, while the other was spreading marmalade on bread with a paper-knife.", "그가 그 방으로 들어가니, 크고 편안한 책상에 두 신사가 마주 앉아 있는 것이 보였는데, 한 사람은 손수건으로 총신을 닦고 있었고, 다른 한 사람은 종이칼로 빵에 마멀레이드를 바르고 있었다."]], [["‘Mr Wobbler?’ inquired the suitor.", "\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 물었다."]], [["Both gentlemen glanced at him, and seemed surprised at his assurance.", "두 신사가 그를 흘끗 보더니, 그 뻔뻔함에 놀란 듯했다."]], [["‘So he went,’ said the gentleman with the gun-barrel, who was an extremely deliberate speaker, ‘down to his cousin’s place, and took the Dog with him by rail.", "\"그래서 그 사람이,\" 더없이 굼뜨게 말하는 사람인 총신을 든 신사가 말했다."], ["Inestimable Dog. Flew at the porter fellow when he was put into the dog-box, and flew at the guard when he was taken out.", "\"사촌네로 내려갔는데, 그 개를 기차에 태우고 데려갔지."], ["He got half-a-dozen fellows into a Barn, and a good supply of Rats, and timed the Dog.", "값을 헤아릴 수 없는 개야. 개 칸에 넣자 짐꾼 녀석한테 덤벼들고, 꺼낼 때는 차장한테 덤벼들었지."], ["Finding the Dog able to do it immensely, made the match, and heavily backed the Dog.", "헛간에 대여섯 놈을 불러 모으고 쥐를 잔뜩 들여놓고는 그 개의 시간을 쟀어."], ["When the match came off, some devil of a fellow was bought over, Sir, Dog was made drunk, Dog’s master was cleaned out.’", "개가 어마어마하게 잘 해낼 수 있다는 걸 알고는, 시합을 잡고 그 개에 거하게 돈을 걸었지. 그런데 막상 시합이 벌어지니, 어떤 악마 같은 놈이 매수당해서, 그래, 개한테 약을 먹였고, 개 주인은 빈털터리가 됐지.\""]], [["‘Mr Wobbler?’ inquired the suitor.", "\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 물었다."]], [["The gentleman who was spreading the marmalade returned, without looking up from that occupation, ‘What did he call the Dog?’", "마멀레이드를 바르던 신사가, 그 일에서 눈도 떼지 않은 채 받아 말했다. \"그 개를 뭐라고 불렀다고?\""]], [["‘Called him Lovely,’ said the other gentleman.", "\"러블리라고 불렀지.\" 다른 신사가 말했다."], ["‘Said the Dog was the perfect picture of the old aunt from whom he had expectations.", "\"그 개가 자기가 유산을 바라던 늙은 고모의 완벽한 초상화라고 했어."], ["Found him particularly like her when hocussed.’", "약에 취하니 특히나 고모를 빼닮았더라나.\""]], [["‘Mr Wobbler?’ said the suitor.", "\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 말했다."]], [["Both gentlemen laughed for some time.", "두 신사가 한참을 웃었다."], ["The gentleman with the gun-barrel, considering it, on inspection, in a satisfactory state, referred it to the other; receiving confirmation of his views, he fitted it into its place in the case before him, and took out the stock and polished that, softly whistling.", "총신을 든 신사는 그것을 살펴본 뒤 만족스러운 상태라고 판단하고는 다른 신사에게 의견을 물었다. 자기 견해가 확인되자, 그는 그것을 앞에 놓인 상자 속 제자리에 끼워 넣고, 개머리판을 꺼내 나직이 휘파람을 불며 그것을 닦았다."]], [["‘Mr Wobbler?’ said the suitor.", "\"워블러 씨인가요?\" 청원자가 말했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ then said Mr Wobbler, with his mouth full.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 그제야 워블러 씨가 입에 음식을 가득 문 채 말했다."]], [["‘I want to know--’ and Arthur Clennam again mechanically set forth what he wanted to know.", "\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다 —\" 그러고는 아서 클레넘이 또다시 기계적으로 알고 싶은 바를 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘Can’t inform you,’ observed Mr Wobbler, apparently to his lunch.", "\"내가 알려드릴 수 없소.\" 워블러 씨가, 분명 자기 점심을 향해 말했다."], ["‘Never heard of it.", "\"들어 본 적 없는 일이오."], ["Nothing at all to do with it.", "나하고는 전혀 상관없는 일이고."], ["Better try Mr Clive, second door on the left in the next passage.’", "다음 복도 왼쪽 두 번째 문, 클라이브 씨한테 가 보시는 게 낫겠소.\""]], [["‘Perhaps he will give me the same answer.’", "\"그분도 똑같은 답을 주시지 않을까요.\""]], [["‘Very likely.", "\"그럴 공산이 크지."], ["Don’t know anything about it,’ said Mr Wobbler.", "나는 그에 관해 아무것도 모르오.\" 워블러 씨가 말했다."]], [["The suitor turned away and had left the room, when the gentleman with the gun called out ‘Mister!", "청원자가 돌아서서 방을 나서자, 총을 든 신사가 외쳤다. \"여보시오!"], ["Hallo!’", "이봐요!\""]], [["He looked in again.", "그가 다시 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Shut the door after you.", "\"나가면서 문 좀 닫으시오."], ["You’re letting in a devil of a draught here!’", "지독한 외풍이 들어오잖소!\""]], [["A few steps brought him to the second door on the left in the next passage.", "몇 걸음 만에 그는 다음 복도 왼쪽 두 번째 문에 이르렀다."], ["In that room he found three gentlemen; number one doing nothing particular, number two doing nothing particular, number three doing nothing particular.", "그 방에는 세 신사가 있었는데, 일 번은 딱히 하는 일이 없었고, 이 번도 딱히 하는 일이 없었고, 삼 번도 딱히 하는 일이 없었다."], ["They seemed, however, to be more directly concerned than the others had been in the effective execution of the great principle of the office, as there was an awful inner apartment with a double door, in which the Circumlocution Sages appeared to be assembled in council, and out of which there was an imposing coming of papers, and into which there was an imposing going of papers, almost constantly; wherein another gentleman, number four, was the active instrument.", "그러나 이들은 앞의 사람들보다 그 관청의 위대한 원리를 효율적으로 집행하는 데 한층 직접 관여하는 듯했으니, 이중문이 달린 으스스한 안쪽 방이 하나 있었기 때문이었다. 거기에 에두름국의 현인들이 회의차 모여 있는 듯했고, 거기서는 서류가 위풍당당하게 나오고 거기로는 서류가 위풍당당하게 들어가는 일이 거의 끊임없이 이어졌는데, 그 안에서는 또 한 신사인 사 번이 능동적 도구 노릇을 하고 있었다."]], [["‘I want to know,’ said Arthur Clennam,--and again stated his case in the same barrel-organ way.", "\"알고 싶은 게 있습니다.\" 아서 클레넘이 말하고는, 또다시 그 손풍금 같은 식으로 자기 용건을 늘어놓았다."], ["As number one referred him to number two, and as number two referred him to number three, he had occasion to state it three times before they all referred him to number four, to whom he stated it again.", "일 번이 그를 이 번에게 보내고, 이 번이 그를 삼 번에게 보내는 통에, 그는 그들이 모두 그를 사 번에게 보내기 전까지 그것을 세 번 늘어놓아야 했고, 사 번에게 다시 그것을 늘어놓았다."]], [["Number four was a vivacious, well-looking, well-dressed, agreeable young fellow--he was a Barnacle, but on the more sprightly side of the family--and he said in an easy way, ‘Oh!", "사 번은 활기차고 잘생기고 옷차림이 멋들어진 호감 가는 젊은이였는데 — 그는 배너클가 사람이었으나, 그 집안에서도 더 발랄한 쪽이었다 — 그가 스스럼없이 말했다. \"아!"], ["you had better not bother yourself about it, I think.’", "그 일은 신경 쓰지 않으시는 게 낫겠는데요, 제 생각엔.\""]], [["‘Not bother myself about it?’", "\"신경 쓰지 말라고요?\""]], [["‘No!", "\"네!"], ["I recommend you not to bother yourself about it.’", "신경 쓰지 마시라고 권하는 겁니다.\""]], [["This was such a new point of view that Arthur Clennam found himself at a loss how to receive it.", "이것은 워낙 새로운 관점이라, 아서 클레넘은 그것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 막막했다."]], [["‘You can if you like.", "\"하고 싶으시면 하셔도 됩니다."], ["I can give you plenty of forms to fill up.", "작성하실 양식이라면 얼마든지 드릴 수 있어요."], ["Lots of ‘em here.", "여기 잔뜩 있으니까요."], ["You can have a dozen if you like.", "원하시면 열두 장이라도 드리지요."], ["But you’ll never go on with it,’ said number four.", "하지만 끝까지 못 가실 겁니다.\" 사 번이 말했다."]], [["‘Would it be such hopeless work?", "\"그렇게나 가망 없는 일입니까?"], ["Excuse me; I am a stranger in England.’", "죄송합니다만, 저는 영국이 낯선 사람이라서요.\""]], [["‘_I_ don’t say it would be hopeless,’ returned number four, with a frank smile.", "\"가망 없을 거라는 말은 안 했어요.\" 사 번이 솔직한 미소를 지으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I don’t express an opinion about that; I only express an opinion about you.", "\"그 일에 관해 의견을 내는 게 아니라, 당신에 관해 의견을 내는 겁니다."], ["_I_ don’t think you’d go on with it.", "제 생각에 당신은 끝까지 못 가실 거예요."], ["However, of course, you can do as you like.", "하지만 물론 마음대로 하셔도 됩니다."], ["I suppose there was a failure in the performance of a contract, or something of that kind, was there?’", "계약 이행에 실패가 있었다거나, 뭐 그런 종류의 일이었겠지요, 아닌가요?\""]], [["‘I really don’t know.’", "\"정말 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"그래요!"], ["That you can find out.", "그건 알아내실 수 있지요."], ["Then you’ll find out what Department the contract was in, and then you’ll find out all about it there.’", "그러면 그 계약이 어느 부서 소관이었는지 알아내실 테고, 그러면 거기서 그에 관한 걸 다 알아내실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon. How shall I find out?’", "\"실례지만, 어떻게 알아냅니까?\""]], [["‘Why, you’ll--you’ll ask till they tell you.", "\"아니, 그야 — 그야 말해 줄 때까지 물으셔야지요."], ["Then you’ll memorialise that Department (according to regular forms which you’ll find out) for leave to memorialise this Department.", "그런 다음 (알아내시게 될 정규 형식에 따라) 그 부서에 이 부서로 진정서를 낼 수 있도록 허가해 달라는 진정서를 내세요."], ["If you get it (which you may after a time), that memorial must be entered in that Department, sent to be registered in this Department, sent back to be signed by that Department, sent back to be countersigned by this Department, and then it will begin to be regularly before that Department.", "만일 그 허가를 받으시면 (얼마 지나면 받으실 수도 있겠지요), 그 진정서는 그 부서에 등록되어야 하고, 이 부서로 보내져 기재되어야 하고, 다시 그 부서로 돌려보내져 서명을 받아야 하고, 다시 이 부서로 돌려보내져 부서명을 받아야 하고, 그제야 그 부서에 정식으로 상정되기 시작합니다."], ["You’ll find out when the business passes through each of these stages by asking at both Departments till they tell you.’", "그 사무가 이 각 단계를 거치는 시점은, 양쪽 부서에 말해 줄 때까지 물으시면 알아내실 수 있고요.\""]], [["‘But surely this is not the way to do the business,’ Arthur Clennam could not help saying.", "\"하지만 이건 분명 일을 처리하는 방식이 아니지 않습니까.\" 아서 클레넘이 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["This airy young Barnacle was quite entertained by his simplicity in supposing for a moment that it was.", "이 경박한 젊은 배너클은, 그가 잠시나마 이것이 일을 처리하는 방식이라고 짐작한 그 순진함을 자못 재미있어했다."], ["This light in hand young Barnacle knew perfectly that it was not.", "이 능숙한 젊은 배너클은 그렇지 않다는 것을 완벽하게 알고 있었다."], ["This touch and go young Barnacle had ‘got up’ the Department in a private secretaryship, that he might be ready for any little bit of fat that came to hand; and he fully understood the Department to be a politico-diplomatic hocus pocus piece of machinery for the assistance of the nobs in keeping off the snobs.", "이 닳고 닳은 젊은 배너클은, 손에 떨어지는 짭짤한 자리라면 무엇이든 차지할 채비를 갖추려고 비서직을 통해 그 부서를 \"익혀\" 둔 터였다."], ["This dashing young Barnacle, in a word, was likely to become a statesman, and to make a figure.", "그리고 그는 그 부서가 나리들을 도와 천한 자들을 쫓아내기 위한, 정치 외교적 눈속임 기계 장치임을 충분히 이해하고 있었다. 한마디로 이 패기 넘치는 젊은 배너클은 장차 정치가가 되어 한자리할 만한 인물이었다."]], [["‘When the business is regularly before that Department, whatever it is,’ pursued this bright young Barnacle, ‘then you can watch it from time to time through that Department.", "\"그 사무가, 그게 무엇이든, 그 부서에 정식으로 상정되면,\" 이 영리한 젊은 배너클이 말을 이었다."], ["When it comes regularly before this Department, then you must watch it from time to time through this Department.", "\"그러면 그 부서를 통해 그것을 수시로 지켜보실 수 있어요. 그것이 이 부서에 정식으로 상정되면, 이 부서를 통해 그것을 수시로 지켜보셔야 하고요."], ["We shall have to refer it right and left; and when we refer it anywhere, then you’ll have to look it up.", "우리는 그것을 이리저리 회부해야 할 텐데, 어디로든 회부하면 그땐 그것을 찾아내셔야 합니다."], ["When it comes back to us at any time, then you had better look _us_ up.", "그것이 언제든 우리에게 돌아오면, 그땐 우리를 찾아오시는 게 낫겠고요."], ["When it sticks anywhere, you’ll have to try to give it a jog.", "그것이 어디서든 막히면, 그땐 그것을 좀 흔들어 보셔야 하고요."], ["When you write to another Department about it, and then to this Department about it, and don’t hear anything satisfactory about it, why then you had better--keep on writing.’", "그에 관해 다른 부서에 편지를 쓰고, 또 이 부서에 편지를 쓰고도, 만족스러운 답을 못 들으시면, 아 그러면 그땐 — 계속 편지를 쓰시는 게 낫겠지요.\""]], [["Arthur Clennam looked very doubtful indeed.", "아서 클레넘은 정말이지 몹시 미심쩍은 표정이었다. \"하지만 아무튼,\""], ["‘But I am obliged to you at any rate,’ said he, ‘for your politeness.’", "그가 말했다. \"친절하게 대해 주셔서 감사합니다.\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ replied this engaging young Barnacle.", "\"천만에요.\" 이 매력적인 젊은 배너클이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Try the thing, and see how you like it.", "\"한번 해 보시고, 마음에 드시는지 보세요."], ["It will be in your power to give it up at any time, if you don’t like it.", "마음에 안 드시면 언제든 그만두실 수 있으니까요."], ["You had better take a lot of forms away with you.", "양식을 잔뜩 가져가시는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Give him a lot of forms!’ With which instruction to number two, this sparkling young Barnacle took a fresh handful of papers from numbers one and three, and carried them into the sanctuary to offer to the presiding Idol of the Circumlocution Office.", "이분께 양식을 잔뜩 드려!\" 이 번에게 이렇게 지시하면서, 이 반짝이는 젊은 배너클은 일 번과 삼 번으로부터 새로운 서류 한 뭉치를 집어 들고, 에두름국의 좌정한 우상에게 바치러 그 성소로 들고 갔다."]], [["Arthur Clennam put his forms in his pocket gloomily enough, and went his way down the long stone passage and the long stone staircase.", "아서 클레넘은 꽤나 침울하게 양식을 주머니에 넣고, 긴 돌 복도와 긴 돌 계단을 따라 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["He had come to the swing doors leading into the street, and was waiting, not over patiently, for two people who were between him and them to pass out and let him follow, when the voice of one of them struck familiarly on his ear.", "거리로 통하는 여닫이문에 이르러, 자기와 문 사이에 있는 두 사람이 빠져나가 자기도 뒤따를 수 있기를 그다지 인내심 있게는 아니게 기다리고 있을 때, 그중 한 사람의 목소리가 귀에 익숙하게 와닿았다."], ["He looked at the speaker and recognised Mr Meagles.", "그가 그 화자를 보고는 미글스 씨를 알아보았다."], ["Mr Meagles was very red in the face--redder than travel could have made him--and collaring a short man who was with him, said, ‘come out, you rascal, come Out!’", "미글스 씨는 얼굴이 매우 붉었는데 — 여행으로 붉어졌다고 보기엔 지나치게 붉었다 — 함께 있던 키 작은 사내의 멱살을 잡고서 말했다. \"나오시오, 이 악당, 나오라고!\""]], [["It was such an unexpected hearing, and it was also such an unexpected sight to see Mr Meagles burst the swing doors open, and emerge into the street with the short man, who was of an unoffending appearance, that Clennam stood still for the moment exchanging looks of surprise with the porter.", "미글스 씨가 여닫이문을 박차고, 해를 끼칠 것 같지도 않은 외양의 그 키 작은 사내와 함께 거리로 나서는 것은 워낙 뜻밖의 소리이자 워낙 뜻밖의 광경이었던지라, 클레넘은 잠시 가만히 서서 문지기와 놀란 눈빛을 주고받았다."], ["He followed, however, quickly; and saw Mr Meagles going down the street with his enemy at his side.", "그러나 그는 곧 재빨리 뒤따라 나가, 미글스 씨가 적을 곁에 끼고 거리를 내려가는 것을 보았다."], ["He soon came up with his old travelling companion, and touched him on the back.", "그는 곧 옛 여행 동무를 따라잡아 그의 등을 두드렸다."], ["The choleric face which Mr Meagles turned upon him smoothed when he saw who it was, and he put out his friendly hand.", "미글스 씨가 그를 향해 돌린 성난 얼굴은, 상대가 누구인지 알아보자 풀렸고, 그는 다정한 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘How are you?’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"잘 지내셨소?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘How d’ye _do?_ I have only just come over from abroad.", "\"안녕하셨소? 나는 외국에서 막 건너온 참이오."], ["I am glad to see you.’", "만나서 반갑소.\""]], [["‘And I am rejoiced to see you.’", "\"저도 뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다.\""]], [["‘Thank’ee.", "\"고맙소."], ["Thank’ee!’", "고맙소!\""]], [["‘Mrs Meagles and your daughter--?’", "\"미글스 부인과 따님은 —?\""]], [["‘Are as well as possible,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"더할 나위 없이 잘 지내오.\""], ["‘I only wish you had come upon me in a more prepossessing condition as to coolness.’", "미글스 씨가 말했다. \"다만 좀 더 침착한 꼴일 때 나를 만나 주셨더라면 좋았을 텐데.\""]], [["Though it was anything but a hot day, Mr Meagles was in a heated state that attracted the attention of the passersby; more particularly as he leaned his back against a railing, took off his hat and cravat, and heartily rubbed his steaming head and face, and his reddened ears and neck, without the least regard for public opinion.", "더운 날도 아니었건만, 미글스 씨는 행인들의 이목을 끌 만큼 달아오른 상태였다. 더욱이 그는 난간에 등을 기대고 모자와 크라바트를 벗고서, 김이 나는 머리와 얼굴을, 또 벌게진 귀와 목을, 세간의 이목 따위는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고 박박 문질러 댔다."]], [["‘Whew!’ said Mr Meagles, dressing again.", "\"휴!\" 미글스 씨가 다시 옷매무새를 가다듬으며 말했다."], ["‘That’s comfortable.", "\"이제 좀 낫군."], ["Now I am cooler.’", "한결 시원하오.\""]], [["‘You have been ruffled, Mr Meagles.", "\"마음이 어지러우셨군요, 미글스 씨."], ["What is the matter?’", "무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["‘Wait a bit, and I’ll tell you.", "\"잠깐만 기다리시오, 곧 이야기해 드리리다."], ["Have you leisure for a turn in the Park?’", "공원을 한 바퀴 도실 여유가 있으시오?\""]], [["‘As much as you please.’", "\"얼마든지요.\""]], [["‘Come along then.", "\"그럼 갑시다."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["you may well look at him.’ He happened to have turned his eyes towards the offender whom Mr Meagles had so angrily collared.", "저 사람을 한번 보실 만하오.\" 그가 마침 미글스 씨가 그토록 성나게 멱살을 잡았던 그 죄인 쪽으로 눈을 돌린 참이었다."], ["‘He’s something to look at, that fellow is.’", "\"저 친구로 말하면, 한번 볼 만한 인물이오.\""]], [["He was not much to look at, either in point of size or in point of dress; being merely a short, square, practical looking man, whose hair had turned grey, and in whose face and forehead there were deep lines of cogitation, which looked as though they were carved in hard wood.", "그는 덩치로 보나 옷차림으로 보나 볼 만한 인물이 못 되었으니, 그저 키 작고 다부지고 실무적으로 생긴 사내였다. 머리가 희끗희끗 세었고, 얼굴과 이마에는 단단한 나무에 새긴 듯한 깊은 사색의 주름이 패어 있었다."], ["He was dressed in decent black, a little rusty, and had the appearance of a sagacious master in some handicraft.", "그는 약간 낡은, 점잖은 검정 옷을 입었고, 어떤 수공예의 슬기로운 장인 같은 풍모였다."], ["He had a spectacle-case in his hand, which he turned over and over while he was thus in question, with a certain free use of the thumb that is never seen but in a hand accustomed to tools.", "그는 손에 안경집을 들고서, 이렇게 화제에 오르는 동안 그것을 이리저리 뒤집었는데, 엄지를 거리낌 없이 놀리는 그 솜씨는 연장에 익숙한 손에서나 볼 수 있는 것이었다."]], [["‘You keep with us,’ said Mr Meagles, in a threatening kind of Way, ‘and I’ll introduce you presently.", "\"당신은 우리와 같이 계시오.\" 미글스 씨가 어딘지 위협하는 투로 말했다."], ["Now then!’", "\"곧 소개해 드릴 테니. 자, 그럼!\""]], [["Clennam wondered within himself, as they took the nearest way to the Park, what this unknown (who complied in the gentlest manner) could have been doing.", "그들이 공원으로 가는 가장 가까운 길을 잡는 동안, 클레넘은 속으로, 이 낯선 자가 (가장 온순한 태도로 순순히 따르고 있는 이 사람이) 대체 무슨 짓을 저질렀을까 궁금해했다."], ["His appearance did not at all justify the suspicion that he had been detected in designs on Mr Meagles’s pocket-handkerchief; nor had he any appearance of being quarrelsome or violent.", "그 외양은 그가 미글스 씨의 손수건을 노리다 들켰다는 의심을 도무지 정당화해 주지 않았고, 그렇다고 시비조이거나 난폭해 보이지도 않았다."], ["He was a quiet, plain, steady man; made no attempt to escape; and seemed a little depressed, but neither ashamed nor repentant.", "그는 조용하고 수수하고 침착한 사내였고, 달아나려는 시도도 하지 않았으며, 다소 풀이 죽어 보일 뿐 부끄러워하거나 뉘우치는 기색은 없었다."], ["If he were a criminal offender, he must surely be an incorrigible hypocrite; and if he were no offender, why should Mr Meagles have collared him in the Circumlocution Office?", "그가 범죄자라면 필시 구제 불능의 위선자일 것이고, 죄인이 아니라면 미글스 씨가 무엇 때문에 에두름국에서 그의 멱살을 잡았겠는가?"], ["He perceived that the man was not a difficulty in his own mind alone, but in Mr Meagles’s too; for such conversation as they had together on the short way to the Park was by no means well sustained, and Mr Meagles’s eye always wandered back to the man, even when he spoke of something very different.", "그는 그 사내가 자기 머릿속에서만 풀리지 않는 수수께끼가 아니라 미글스 씨의 머릿속에서도 그러하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 공원으로 가는 짧은 길에 그들이 나눈 대화는 도무지 잘 이어지지 않았고, 미글스 씨의 눈은 전혀 다른 이야기를 할 때조차 늘 그 사내에게로 되돌아갔던 것이다."]], [["At length they being among the trees, Mr Meagles stopped short, and said:", "마침내 나무들 사이에 이르렀을 때, 미글스 씨가 우뚝 멈추어 서서 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, will you do me the favour to look at this man?", "\"클레넘 씨, 부디 이 사람을 좀 봐 주시겠소?"], ["His name is Doyce, Daniel Doyce.", "이름은 도이스, 대니얼 도이스요."], ["You wouldn’t suppose this man to be a notorious rascal; would you?’", "이 사람이 악명 높은 악당이라고는 짐작도 못 하시겠지요?\""]], [["‘I certainly should not.’ It was really a disconcerting question, with the man there.", "\"분명 못 하겠습니다.\" 사내가 거기 있는 만큼, 정말이지 당혹스러운 질문이었다."]], [["‘No. You would not.", "\"못 하겠지요."], ["I know you would not.", "그러실 줄 알았소."], ["You wouldn’t suppose him to be a public offender; would you?’", "이 사람이 공공의 죄인이라고는 짐작도 못 하시겠지요?\" \"못 합니다.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘No. But he is.", "\"못 하시겠지요."], ["He is a public offender.", "하지만 그렇다오."], ["What has he been guilty of?", "이 사람은 공공의 죄인이오."], ["Murder, manslaughter, arson, forgery, swindling, house-breaking, highway robbery, larceny, conspiracy, fraud?", "무슨 죄를 지었을까요? 살인, 과실치사, 방화, 위조, 사기, 주거 침입, 노상강도, 절도, 공모, 기만?"], ["Which should you say, now?’", "자, 어느 것이라고 하시겠소?\""]], [["‘I should say,’ returned Arthur Clennam, observing a faint smile in Daniel Doyce’s face, ‘not one of them.’", "\"제가 보기엔,\" 아서 클레넘이 대니얼 도이스의 얼굴에 어린 옅은 미소를 알아채며 대답했다. \"그중 어느 것도 아닐 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘You are right,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"맞소.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But he has been ingenious, and he has been trying to turn his ingenuity to his country’s service.", "\"하지만 이 사람은 창의적이었고, 그 창의력을 나라에 봉사하는 데 쓰려 했소."], ["That makes him a public offender directly, sir.’", "그러니 곧바로 공공의 죄인이 된 거요, 선생.\""]], [["Arthur looked at the man himself, who only shook his head.", "아서는 사내 자신을 바라보았으나, 그는 고개만 저을 뿐이었다."]], [["‘This Doyce,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘is a smith and engineer.", "\"이 도이스라는 사람은,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["He is not in a large way, but he is well known as a very ingenious man.", "\"대장장이이자 기술자요. 큰 사업을 하는 건 아니지만, 매우 창의적인 사람으로 잘 알려져 있소."], ["A dozen years ago, he perfects an invention (involving a very curious secret process) of great importance to his country and his fellow-creatures.", "십수 년 전, 이 사람은 자기 나라와 동포에게 대단히 중요한 (아주 진기한 비밀 공정이 들어가는) 발명을 완성했소."], ["I won’t say how much money it cost him, or how many years of his life he had been about it, but he brought it to perfection a dozen years ago.", "그 일에 돈이 얼마나 들었는지, 인생의 몇 해를 거기에 바쳤는지는 말하지 않겠지만, 십수 년 전에 그것을 완성한 거요."], ["Wasn’t it a dozen?’ said Mr Meagles, addressing Doyce.", "십수 년 됐지요?\" 미글스 씨가 도이스를 향해 말했다."], ["‘He is the most exasperating man in the world; he never complains!’", "\"이 사람은 세상에서 가장 사람 속을 끓게 하는 인물이오. 도무지 불평을 안 한다니까!\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["Rather better than twelve years ago.’", "십이 년보다 조금 더 됐지요.\""]], [["‘Rather better?’ said Mr Meagles, ‘you mean rather worse.", "\"조금 더?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Well, Mr Clennam, he addresses himself to the Government.", "\"조금 더 못 됐다는 뜻이겠지. 자, 클레넘 씨, 이 사람이 정부에 청원을 한 거요."], ["The moment he addresses himself to the Government, he becomes a public offender!", "정부에 청원을 하는 그 순간, 공공의 죄인이 되는 거요! 선생,\""], ["Sir,’ said Mr Meagles, in danger of making himself excessively hot again, ‘he ceases to be an innocent citizen, and becomes a culprit.", "미글스 씨가 또다시 지나치게 달아오를 위험에 처해 말했다. \"이 사람은 그 순간부터 죄 없는 시민이기를 그치고 죄인이 되는 거요."], ["He is treated from that instant as a man who has done some infernal action.", "그 순간부터 무슨 흉악한 짓이라도 저지른 사람처럼 취급받는 거지."], ["He is a man to be shirked, put off, brow-beaten, sneered at, handed over by this highly-connected young or old gentleman, to that highly-connected young or old gentleman, and dodged back again; he is a man with no rights in his own time, or his own property; a mere outlaw, whom it is justifiable to get rid of anyhow; a man to be worn out by all possible means.’", "따돌림받고, 미뤄지고, 윽박질리고, 비웃음당하고, 지체 높은 이 젊은 또는 늙은 신사한테서 저 지체 높은 젊은 또는 늙은 신사한테로 떠넘겨졌다가 다시 되돌아오는 사람이오. 제 시간에도 제 재산에도 아무 권리가 없는 사람, 어떻게든 없애 버려도 정당한 한낱 무법자, 온갖 가능한 수단을 다 동원해 진을 빼야 마땅한 사람 말이오.\""]], [["It was not so difficult to believe, after the morning’s experience, as Mr Meagles supposed.", "오전의 경험을 겪고 난 뒤라, 그것은 미글스 씨가 짐작하는 것만큼 믿기 어려운 이야기는 아니었다."]], [["‘Don’t stand there, Doyce, turning your spectacle-case over and over,’ cried Mr Meagles, ‘but tell Mr Clennam what you confessed to me.’", "\"거기 그렇게 서서 안경집만 이리저리 뒤집지 말고, 도이스,\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다. \"클레넘 씨한테 나한테 털어놓은 걸 말해 보시오.\""]], [["‘I undoubtedly was made to feel,’ said the inventor, ‘as if I had committed an offence.", "\"틀림없이 저는,\" 그 발명가가 말했다."], ["In dancing attendance at the various offices, I was always treated, more or less, as if it was a very bad offence.", "\"마치 제가 무슨 죄라도 지은 것 같은 기분이 들게 되더군요. 여러 관청에 시중을 들며 드나드는 동안, 늘 정도의 차이는 있어도 무슨 아주 못된 죄라도 지은 것처럼 취급받았습니다."], ["I have frequently found it necessary to reflect, for my own self-support, that I really had not done anything to bring myself into the Newgate Calendar, but only wanted to effect a great saving and a great improvement.’", "저는 제 마음을 추스르려고 자주 이렇게 곱씹어야 했지요. 나는 정말이지 내 이름을 뉴게이트 범죄 기록부에 올릴 만한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았고, 그저 큰 절약과 큰 개선을 이루고자 했을 뿐이라고 말입니다.\""]], [["‘There!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Judge whether I exaggerate.", "\"내가 과장하는지 판단해 보시오."], ["Now you’ll be able to believe me when I tell you the rest of the case.’", "이제 내가 나머지 사정을 말해 줘도 나를 믿으실 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["With this prelude, Mr Meagles went through the narrative; the established narrative, which has become tiresome; the matter-of-course narrative which we all know by heart.", "이런 서두를 떼고, 미글스 씨는 그 사연을 죽 풀어놓았으니, 이젠 지긋지긋해진 그 판에 박힌 사연, 우리 모두가 외울 만큼 잘 아는 그 당연지사의 사연이었다."], ["How, after interminable attendance and correspondence, after infinite impertinences, ignorances, and insults, my lords made a Minute, number three thousand four hundred and seventy-two, allowing the culprit to make certain trials of his invention at his own expense.", "끝없는 시중과 서신 왕래 끝에, 끝없는 무례와 무지와 모욕 끝에, 어떻게 나리들께서 회의록 제3472호를 작성하여 그 죄인이 자기 비용으로 발명품의 일정한 시험을 해 보도록 허락했는지."], ["How the trials were made in the presence of a board of six, of whom two ancient members were too blind to see it, two other ancient members were too deaf to hear it, one other ancient member was too lame to get near it, and the final ancient member was too pig-headed to look at it.", "그 시험이 어떻게 여섯 명으로 구성된 위원회 앞에서 이루어졌는지, 그중 늙은 위원 둘은 너무 눈이 멀어 그것을 보지 못했고, 다른 늙은 위원 둘은 너무 귀가 멀어 그것을 듣지 못했고, 또 다른 늙은 위원 하나는 너무 다리를 절어 그것에 다가가지 못했고, 마지막 늙은 위원은 너무 고집이 세 그것을 보려 하지도 않았는지. 어떻게 또 여러 해가 흘렀고, 더 많은 무례와 무지와 모욕이 있었는지."], ["How there were more years; more impertinences, ignorances, and insults.", "어떻게 나리들께서 회의록 제5103호를 작성하여 그 사무를 에두름국에 넘겼는지."], ["How my lords then made a Minute, number five thousand one hundred and three, whereby they resigned the business to the Circumlocution Office.", "어떻게 에두름국이 시간이 흐른 뒤, 마치 그것이 전에는 들어 본 적도 없는, 어제 갓 나온 따끈따끈한 신품인 양 그 사무를 떠맡았는지. 그러고는 그 사무를 뒤죽박죽으로 만들고, 흐리멍덩하게 만들고, 젖은 담요로 덮어 내동댕이쳤는지."], ["How the Circumlocution Office, in course of time, took up the business as if it were a bran new thing of yesterday, which had never been heard of before; muddled the business, addled the business, tossed the business in a wet blanket.", "어떻게 무례와 무지와 모욕이 구구단처럼 불어났는지. 어떻게 그 발명품이 배너클 셋과 스틸트스토킹 하나에게 회부되었는지, 그들은 그에 관해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그들 머릿속에 그에 관해 아무것도 망치질해 넣을 수 없었으며, 그에 관해 지겨워하더니, 그에 관해 물리적 불가능을 보고했는지."], ["How the impertinences, ignorances, and insults went through the multiplication table.", "어떻게 에두름국이 회의록 제8740호에서 \"나리들께서 내리신 결정을 번복할 이유가 없다고 본다\"고 했는지."], ["How there was a reference of the invention to three Barnacles and a Stiltstalking, who knew nothing about it; into whose heads nothing could be hammered about it; who got bored about it, and reported physical impossibilities about it.", "어떻게 에두름국이, 나리들께서 아무런 결정도 내린 바 없다는 점을 일깨움받고는, 그 사무를 선반에 처박아 두었는지."], ["How the Circumlocution Office, in a Minute, number eight thousand seven hundred and forty, ‘saw no reason to reverse the decision at which my lords had arrived.’ How the Circumlocution Office, being reminded that my lords had arrived at no decision, shelved the business.", "어떻게 바로 그날 아침에 에두름국의 수장과 마지막 면담이 있었고, 그 청동 머리가 입을 열어, 대체로 보아, 그리고 온갖 정황을 고려하여, 또 여러 관점에서 살펴보건대, 이 사무에 관해서는 두 가지 방책 중 하나를 따라야 한다는 의견을 내놓았는지. 즉 그것을 영원토록 가만히 내버려 두든가, 아니면 처음부터 다시 죄다 시작하든가 하라는 것이었다."], ["How there had been a final interview with the head of the Circumlocution Office that very morning, and how the Brazen Head had spoken, and had been, upon the whole, and under all the circumstances, and looking at it from the various points of view, of opinion that one of two courses was to be pursued in respect of the business: that was to say, either to leave it alone for evermore, or to begin it all over again.", "* 청동 머리: 중세 전설 속 로저 베이컨 등이 만들었다는, 신탁처럼 말하는 놋쇠 인공 두상. 권위 있게 모호한 말만 늘어놓는 인물을 비꼬는 표현."]], [["‘Upon which,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘as a practical man, I then and there, in that presence, took Doyce by the collar, and told him it was plain to me that he was an infamous rascal and treasonable disturber of the government peace, and took him away. I brought him out of the office door by the collar, that the very porter might know I was a practical man who appreciated the official estimate of such characters; and here we are!’", "\"그래서,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"나는 실무적인 사람인지라, 바로 그 자리에서, 그 면전에서, 도이스의 멱살을 잡고는, 당신은 명백히 파렴치한 악당이요 정부의 평화를 어지럽히는 반역자임이 내게 분명하니, 데리고 나가겠다고 했소. 그러고는 멱살을 쥔 채 그를 관청 문밖으로 끌고 나왔지. 바로 그 문지기더러도, 내가 그런 인간들에 대한 관청의 평가를 알아주는 실무적인 사람이라는 걸 알라고 말이오. 그래서 우리가 여기 있는 거요!\""]], [["If that airy young Barnacle had been there, he would have frankly told them perhaps that the Circumlocution Office had achieved its function.", "만일 그 경박한 젊은 배너클이 거기 있었더라면, 그는 어쩌면 솔직하게 그들에게 일러 주었을 것이다. 에두름국은 제 소임을 다한 것이라고."], ["That what the Barnacles had to do, was to stick on to the national ship as long as they could.", "배너클가가 할 일이란 할 수 있는 한 오래도록 국가라는 배에 들러붙어 있는 것이라고."], ["That to trim the ship, lighten the ship, clean the ship, would be to knock them off; that they could but be knocked off once; and that if the ship went down with them yet sticking to it, that was the ship’s look out, and not theirs.", "배를 손질하고, 가볍게 하고, 깨끗이 하는 일은 그들을 떼어 내는 일이 될 텐데, 그들은 한 번밖에 떨어져 나갈 수 없으니, 그들이 여전히 달라붙어 있는 채로 배가 가라앉는다면, 그것은 그들이 알 바 아니라 배가 알아서 할 일이라고 말이다."]], [["‘There!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘now you know all about Doyce.", "\"보시오!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Except, which I own does not improve my state of mind, that even now you don’t hear him complain.’", "\"이제 도이스에 관해 다 아셨소. 다만, 솔직히 말해 이게 내 심사를 나아지게 하진 못하는데, 지금 이 순간에도 당신은 이 사람이 불평하는 소리를 못 듣고 있다는 것 빼고는 말이오.\""]], [["‘You must have great patience,’ said Arthur Clennam, looking at him with some wonder, ‘great forbearance.’", "\"인내심이 대단하시겠습니다.\" 아서 클레넘이 다소 경이로운 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"참을성이 대단하시겠어요.\""]], [["‘No,’ he returned, ‘I don’t know that I have more than another man.’", "\"아니오.\" 그가 대꾸했다. \"내가 남보다 더한지는 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘By the Lord, you have more than I have, though!’ cried Mr Meagles.", "\"맙소사, 그래도 나보다는 더하시구려!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["Doyce smiled, as he said to Clennam, ‘You see, my experience of these things does not begin with myself.", "도이스가 미소 지으며 클레넘에게 말했다. \"보시다시피, 이런 일에 대한 제 경험은 저 자신에게서 비롯된 게 아닙니다."], ["It has been in my way to know a little about them from time to time.", "때때로 그에 관해 조금씩 알게 될 처지에 있었지요."], ["Mine is not a particular case.", "제 경우가 특별한 게 아닙니다."], ["I am not worse used than a hundred others who have put themselves in the same position--than all the others, I was going to say.’", "저는 같은 처지에 놓인 다른 백 명보다 더 험하게 당한 게 아니에요 — 다른 모든 이들보다, 라고 말하려던 참이었지만요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know that I should find that a consolation, if it were my case; but I am very glad that you do.’", "\"제 일이라면 그게 위안이 될지 모르겠습니다만, 당신께는 그러하다니 무척 다행입니다.\""]], [["‘Understand me!", "\"제 말을 이해해 주십시오! 그게 한 사람의 수고와 희망에 대한 보상이라는 말은 아닙니다.\""], ["I don’t say,’ he replied in his steady, planning way, and looking into the distance before him as if his grey eye were measuring it, ‘that it’s recompense for a man’s toil and hope; but it’s a certain sort of relief to know that I might have counted on this.’", "그가 차분하고 사려 깊은 투로, 마치 그 잿빛 눈으로 거리를 재기라도 하듯 앞쪽 먼 곳을 바라보며 대답했다. \"다만 내가 이렇게 될 줄 미리 헤아릴 수 있었다는 걸 안다는 것이, 어떤 종류의 안도는 되지요.\""]], [["He spoke in that quiet deliberate manner, and in that undertone, which is often observable in mechanics who consider and adjust with great nicety.", "그는 정교하게 따지고 맞추는 기술자들에게서 흔히 볼 수 있는 그 조용하고 신중한 태도로, 그 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["It belonged to him like his suppleness of thumb, or his peculiar way of tilting up his hat at the back every now and then, as if he were contemplating some half-finished work of his hand and thinking about it.", "그것은 그의 유연한 엄지처럼, 또는 그가 이따금 마치 제 손으로 반쯤 마무리한 작업을 골똘히 들여다보며 궁리하기라도 하듯 모자챙 뒤쪽을 슬쩍 들어 올리는 그 독특한 버릇처럼, 그에게 배어 있는 것이었다."]], [["‘Disappointed?’ he went on, as he walked between them under the trees.", "\"실망했냐고요?\" 그가 나무들 아래 두 사람 사이로 걸으며 말을 이었다."], ["‘Yes.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["No doubt I am disappointed.", "분명 실망했지요. 상처받았냐고요?"], ["Hurt? Yes.", "그렇습니다."], ["No doubt I am hurt.", "분명 상처받았어요."], ["That’s only natural.", "그건 당연한 노릇이지요."], ["But what I mean when I say that people who put themselves in the same position are mostly used in the same way--’", "하지만 같은 처지에 놓이는 사람들이 대개 같은 식으로 당한다고 말할 때 제가 뜻하는 바는 —\""]], [["‘In England,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"영국에서 말이지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["of course I mean in England.", "물론 영국에서 그렇다는 거지요."], ["When they take their inventions into foreign countries, that’s quite different.", "그들이 발명품을 외국으로 가져가면, 사정은 사뭇 다릅니다."], ["And that’s the reason why so many go there.’", "그래서 그토록 많은 사람이 그리로 가는 거고요.\""]], [["Mr Meagles very hot indeed again.", "미글스 씨가 정말이지 또다시 잔뜩 달아올랐다."]], [["‘What I mean is, that however this comes to be the regular way of our government, it is its regular way.", "\"제 말은, 어쩌다 이게 우리 정부의 일상적 방식이 되었든 간에, 이게 그 일상적 방식이라는 겁니다."], ["Have you ever heard of any projector or inventor who failed to find it all but inaccessible, and whom it did not discourage and ill-treat?’", "그것이 거의 다가갈 수조차 없음을 깨닫지 않은, 그리고 그것에게 낙담당하고 박대당하지 않은 기획자나 발명가를 단 한 사람이라도 들어 보셨습니까?\""]], [["‘I cannot say that I ever have.’", "\"들어 본 적 있다고는 말 못 하겠군요.\""]], [["‘Have you ever known it to be beforehand in the adoption of any useful thing?", "\"그것이 유용한 무언가를 채택하는 데 앞장선 적을 아십니까?"], ["Ever known it to set an example of any useful kind?’", "유용한 어떤 종류의 본보기를 보인 적을 아십니까?\""]], [["‘I am a good deal older than my friend here,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘and I’ll answer that.", "\"나는 여기 내 친구보다 한참 나이가 많으니,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Never.’", "\"그건 내가 답하리다. 단 한 번도 없소.\""]], [["‘But we all three have known, I expect,’ said the inventor, ‘a pretty many cases of its fixed determination to be miles upon miles, and years upon years, behind the rest of us; and of its being found out persisting in the use of things long superseded, even after the better things were well known and generally taken up?’", "\"하지만 우리 셋 모두 알 겁니다,\" 그 발명가가 말했다. \"그것이 우리 나머지보다 몇 마일씩, 몇 해씩 뒤처지기로 굳게 작정한 숱한 경우를요. 그리고 더 나은 것들이 잘 알려지고 두루 채택된 뒤에도, 한참 전에 폐기된 것들을 고집스레 쓰고 있다가 들통난 경우들을요.\""]], [["They all agreed upon that.", "세 사람 모두 그 점에는 의견이 일치했다."]], [["‘Well then,’ said Doyce, with a sigh, ‘as I know what such a metal will do at such a temperature, and such a body under such a pressure, so I may know (if I will only consider), how these great lords and gentlemen will certainly deal with such a matter as mine.", "\"그렇다면,\" 도이스가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["I have no right to be surprised, with a head upon my shoulders, and memory in it, that I fall into the ranks with all who came before me.", "\"어떤 금속이 어떤 온도에서, 어떤 물체가 어떤 압력 아래서 어떻게 될지 제가 아는 것처럼, (마음만 먹고 헤아려 본다면) 이 위대한 나리들과 신사분들이 제 것과 같은 사안을 틀림없이 어떻게 다룰지도 알 수 있는 거지요. 어깨 위에 머리가 달려 있고 그 안에 기억이 들어 있는 제가, 저보다 앞서 온 모든 이들과 같은 대열에 끼게 됐다고 놀랄 권리는 없습니다."], ["I ought to have let it alone.", "그냥 내버려 뒀어야 했어요."], ["I have had warning enough, I am sure.’", "경고는 충분히 받았으니까요, 분명히.\""]], [["With that he put up his spectacle-case, and said to Arthur, ‘If I don’t complain, Mr Clennam, I can feel gratitude; and I assure you that I feel it towards our mutual friend.", "그러면서 그는 안경집을 집어넣고 아서에게 말했다. \"불평은 안 해도, 클레넘 씨, 고마움은 느낄 줄 압니다."], ["Many’s the day, and many’s the way in which he has backed me.’", "그리고 저는 우리 둘의 벗에게 그 고마움을 느낀다고 분명히 말씀드리지요. 이 사람이 여러 날에 걸쳐, 여러 방식으로 저를 받쳐 주었습니다.\""]], [["‘Stuff and nonsense,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"쓸데없는 소리 마시오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Arthur could not but glance at Daniel Doyce in the ensuing silence.", "뒤이은 침묵 속에서 아서는 대니얼 도이스를 흘끗 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Though it was evidently in the grain of his character, and of his respect for his own case, that he should abstain from idle murmuring, it was evident that he had grown the older, the sterner, and the poorer, for his long endeavour.", "헛된 푸념을 삼가는 것이 분명 그의 천성에, 그리고 제 처지에 대한 자존심에 배어 있는 일이긴 했으나, 그 오랜 분투 탓에 그가 더 늙고 더 굳세고 더 가난해졌음은 분명했다. 아서는 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He could not but think what a blessed thing it would have been for this man, if he had taken a lesson from the gentlemen who were so kind as to take a nation’s affairs in charge, and had learnt How not to do it.", "한 나라의 사무를 떠맡는 친절을 베푸는 그 신사들에게서 한 수 배워, 어떻게 하면 그것을 하지 않을 것인가를 익혔더라면, 이 사람에게 얼마나 복된 일이었을까 하고."]], [["Mr Meagles was hot and despondent for about five minutes, and then began to cool and clear up.", "미글스 씨는 한 오 분쯤 달아오른 채 풀이 죽어 있다가, 차츰 식고 마음이 개기 시작했다."]], [["‘Come, come!’ said he.", "\"자, 자!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘We shall not make this the better by being grim.", "\"우리가 침울하게 군다고 일이 나아지진 않을 거요."], ["Where do you think of going, Dan?’", "어디로 갈 생각이오, 댄?\""]], [["‘I shall go back to the factory,’ said Dan.", "\"공장으로 돌아가야지요.\" 댄이 말했다."]], [["‘Why then, we’ll all go back to the factory, or walk in that direction,’ returned Mr Meagles cheerfully.", "\"그럼, 우리 모두 공장으로 돌아갑시다, 아니면 그쪽으로 걸어가든가.\" 미글스 씨가 쾌활하게 대꾸했다."], ["‘Mr Clennam won’t be deterred by its being in Bleeding Heart Yard.’", "\"그게 블리딩 하트 야드에 있다고 해서 클레넘 씨가 꺼리진 않으실 거요.\""]], [["‘Bleeding Heart Yard?’ said Clennam.", "\"블리딩 하트 야드라고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘I want to go there.’", "\"거기 가고 싶던 참입니다.\""]], [["‘So much the better,’ cried Mr Meagles.", "\"그거 잘됐군!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Come along!’", "\"갑시다!\""]], [["As they went along, certainly one of the party, and probably more than one, thought that Bleeding Heart Yard was no inappropriate destination for a man who had been in official correspondence with my lords and the Barnacles--and perhaps had a misgiving also that Britannia herself might come to look for lodgings in Bleeding Heart Yard some ugly day or other, if she over-did the Circumlocution Office.", "그들이 걸어가는 동안, 일행 중 분명 한 사람은, 어쩌면 그 이상은, 나리들 및 배너클가와 공무상의 서신을 주고받아 온 사람에게 블리딩 하트 야드가 그리 어울리지 않는 목적지는 아니라고 생각했다 — 그리고 어쩌면, 만일 브리타니아가 에두름국을 지나치게 부려 먹는다면, 그녀 자신도 언젠가 어느 흉한 날에 블리딩 하트 야드에 셋방을 구하러 오게 될지도 모른다는 불길한 예감마저 품었을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 11. Let Loose", "# 제12장. 위대한 애국적 회의가 열리다"]], [["A late, dull autumn night was closing in upon the river Saone.", "해 질 무렵, 음울하고 흐릿한 가을밤이 손강 위로 내려앉고 있었다."], ["The stream, like a sullied looking-glass in a gloomy place, reflected the clouds heavily; and the low banks leaned over here and there, as if they were half curious, and half afraid, to see their darkening pictures in the water.", "그 물줄기는 음침한 곳에 놓인 더럽혀진 거울처럼 구름을 무겁게 비추었고, 낮은 둔덕들은 여기저기서 마치 물에 비친 제 어두워지는 모습을 보고 싶어 반은 호기심에, 반은 두려움에 기우뚱 몸을 기울이는 듯했다."], ["The flat expanse of country about Chalons lay a long heavy streak, occasionally made a little ragged by a row of poplar trees against the wrathful sunset.", "샬롱 일대의 평평한 들판은 길고 묵직한 띠를 이루어 누워 있었고, 노여운 석양을 배경으로 한 줄로 늘어선 포플러 나무가 이따금 그 띠를 조금 들쭉날쭉하게 만들었다."], ["On the banks of the river Saone it was wet, depressing, solitary; and the night deepened fast.", "손강 기슭은 축축하고, 음울하고, 적막했다. 그리고 밤은 빠르게 깊어 갔다."]], [["One man slowly moving on towards Chalons was the only visible figure in the landscape.", "샬롱을 향해 천천히 나아가는 한 사내가 그 풍경 속에서 눈에 띄는 유일한 형상이었다."], ["Cain might have looked as lonely and avoided.", "카인인들 이보다 더 외롭고 더 기피당하는 모습일 수는 없었으리라."], ["With an old sheepskin knapsack at his back, and a rough, unbarked stick cut out of some wood in his hand; miry, footsore, his shoes and gaiters trodden out, his hair and beard untrimmed; the cloak he carried over his shoulder, and the clothes he wore, sodden with wet; limping along in pain and difficulty; he looked as if the clouds were hurrying from him, as if the wail of the wind and the shuddering of the grass were directed against him, as if the low mysterious plashing of the water murmured at him, as if the fitful autumn night were disturbed by him.", "등에는 낡은 양가죽 배낭을 지고, 손에는 어느 숲에서 베어 낸 거칠고 껍질도 벗기지 않은 막대기를 쥐고, 진흙투성이에 발은 부르트고, 신발과 각반은 닳아 해지고, 머리카락과 수염은 손질도 안 된 채, 어깨에 걸친 외투와 입은 옷가지는 빗물에 흠뻑 젖은 채, 아파하며 어렵사리 절뚝거리며 걸어가는 그의 모습은 마치 구름이 그를 피해 서둘러 달아나는 듯, 바람의 울부짖음과 풀의 떨림이 그를 겨누고 있는 듯, 낮고 신비로운 물의 찰랑임이 그를 향해 중얼거리는 듯, 변덕스러운 가을밤이 그로 인해 어지러워진 듯 보였다."]], [["He glanced here, and he glanced there, sullenly but shrinkingly; and sometimes stopped and turned about, and looked all round him.", "그는 이쪽을 흘끔, 저쪽을 흘끔 보았는데, 시무룩하면서도 움츠러든 눈길이었다. 이따금 멈춰 서서 몸을 돌려 사방을 둘러보았다."], ["Then he limped on again, toiling and muttering.", "그러고는 다시 절뚝거리며 나아가, 힘겹게 걸으며 중얼거렸다."]], [["‘To the devil with this plain that has no end!", "\"끝도 없는 이 평원, 악마에게나 가 버려라!"], ["To the devil with these stones that cut like knives!", "칼날처럼 발을 베는 이 돌들, 악마에게나 가 버려라!"], ["To the devil with this dismal darkness, wrapping itself about one with a chill!", "으스스하게 사람을 휘감는 이 음산한 어둠, 악마에게나 가 버려라!"], ["I hate you!’", "너희가 다 밉다!\""]], [["And he would have visited his hatred upon it all with the scowl he threw about him, if he could.", "그리고 만약 할 수만 있었다면, 그는 사방으로 던지는 그 노려보는 눈초리로 그 모든 것에 자신의 증오를 퍼부었을 것이다."], ["He trudged a little further; and looking into the distance before him, stopped again.", "그는 조금 더 터벅터벅 걸었다. 그러다 앞쪽 먼 곳을 바라보며 다시 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘I, hungry, thirsty, weary.", "\"나는 배고프고, 목마르고, 지쳤다."], ["You, imbeciles, where the lights are yonder, eating and drinking, and warming yourselves at fires!", "그런데 너희 멍청이들은, 저기 불빛이 있는 곳에서, 먹고 마시고 불가에서 몸을 녹이고 있구나!"], ["I wish I had the sacking of your town; I would repay you, my children!’", "너희 마을을 약탈할 수만 있다면 좋으련만. 내 너희에게 갚아 주마, 이놈들아!\""]], [["But the teeth he set at the town, and the hand he shook at the town, brought the town no nearer; and the man was yet hungrier, and thirstier, and wearier, when his feet were on its jagged pavement, and he stood looking about him.", "그러나 그가 마을을 향해 이를 악물고, 마을을 향해 주먹을 흔들어도 마을은 조금도 가까워지지 않았다. 그가 그 들쭉날쭉한 포석 위에 발을 딛고 서서 주위를 둘러볼 때쯤, 사내는 더더욱 배고프고, 더더욱 목마르고, 더더욱 지쳐 있었다."]], [["There was the hotel with its gateway, and its savoury smell of cooking; there was the cafe with its bright windows, and its rattling of dominoes; there was the dyer’s with its strips of red cloth on the doorposts; there was the silversmith’s with its earrings, and its offerings for altars; there was the tobacco dealer’s with its lively group of soldier customers coming out pipe in mouth; there were the bad odours of the town, and the rain and the refuse in the kennels, and the faint lamps slung across the road, and the huge Diligence, and its mountain of luggage, and its six grey horses with their tails tied up, getting under weigh at the coach office. But no small cabaret for a straitened traveller being within sight, he had to seek one round the dark corner, where the cabbage leaves lay thickest, trodden about the public cistern at which women had not yet left off drawing water. There, in the back street he found one, the Break of Day. The curtained windows clouded the Break of Day, but it seemed light and warm, and it announced in legible inscriptions with appropriate pictorial embellishment of billiard cue and ball, that at the Break of Day one could play billiards; that there one could find meat, drink, and lodgings, whether one came on horseback, or came on foot; and that it kept good wines, liqueurs, and brandy. The man turned the handle of the Break of Day door, and limped in.", "거기에는 대문이 달린, 음식 냄새가 구수하게 풍기는 호텔이 있었다. 거기에는 밝은 창문과 도미노 패가 부딪치는 소리가 나는 카페가 있었다. 거기에는 문기둥에 붉은 천 조각들을 늘어뜨린 염색집이 있었다. 거기에는 귀걸이며 제단에 바칠 봉헌물을 진열한 은세공집이 있었다. 거기에는 파이프를 입에 문 채 나오는 군인 손님들이 떠들썩하게 무리 지은 담배 가게가 있었다. 거기에는 마을의 고약한 냄새들과, 빗물과, 시궁창에 고인 쓰레기와, 길을 가로질러 매달린 희미한 등불과, 거대한 역마차와, 그 산더미 같은 짐과, 꼬리를 묶어 올린 여섯 마리 잿빛 말이 합승마차 사무소 앞에서 막 출발하려는 모습이 있었다. 그러나 주머니가 빈 나그네가 들 만한 작은 선술집은 보이지 않아, 그는 어두운 모퉁이를 돌아 하나를 찾아야 했다. 그곳은 양배추 잎이 가장 두껍게 쌓여, 여인들이 아직 물 긷기를 멈추지 않은 공동 우물 둘레로 짓밟혀 있는 곳이었다. 거기, 그 뒷골목에서 그는 하나를 발견했으니, 바로 '동틀 녘'이었다. 커튼이 드리워진 창문이 '동틀 녘'을 흐릿하게 가리고 있었지만, 그 안은 환하고 따뜻해 보였고, 당구 큐와 공을 적절히 그려 넣은 그림으로 장식해 읽기 쉬운 글귀로 알리고 있었다. '동틀 녘'에서는 당구를 칠 수 있고, 말을 타고 오든 걸어서 오든 음식과 술과 잠자리를 구할 수 있으며, 좋은 포도주와 리큐어와 브랜디를 갖추고 있다고. 사내는 '동틀 녘'의 문손잡이를 돌리고 절뚝거리며 들어섰다."]], [["He touched his discoloured slouched hat, as he came in at the door, to a few men who occupied the room.", "문으로 들어서며 그는 방 안을 차지하고 있던 몇몇 사내들에게 빛바랜 챙 늘어진 모자에 손을 갖다 대 인사했다. 둘은 작은 탁자 하나에서 도미노를 치고 있었고, 서넛은 난로 둘레에 앉아 담배를 피우며 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Two were playing dominoes at one of the little tables; three or four were seated round the stove, conversing as they smoked; the billiard-table in the centre was left alone for the time; the landlady of the Daybreak sat behind her little counter among her cloudy bottles of syrups, baskets of cakes, and leaden drainage for glasses, working at her needle.", "한가운데 놓인 당구대는 잠시 방치되어 있었다. '동틀 녘'의 안주인은 작은 계산대 뒤에 앉아, 흐릿한 시럽 병들과 과자 바구니들과 유리잔 물 빼는 납받침 사이에서 바느질에 몰두하고 있었다."]], [["Making his way to an empty little table in a corner of the room behind the stove, he put down his knapsack and his cloak upon the ground.", "난로 뒤 방 한구석에 있는 빈 작은 탁자로 걸어가, 그는 배낭과 외투를 바닥에 내려놓았다."], ["As he raised his head from stooping to do so, he found the landlady beside him.", "그렇게 하느라 몸을 숙였다가 고개를 들어 보니, 안주인이 그의 곁에 와 있었다."]], [["‘One can lodge here to-night, madame?’", "\"오늘 밤 여기서 묵을 수 있겠소, 마담?\""]], [["‘Perfectly!’ said the landlady in a high, sing-song, cheery voice.", "\"물론이지요!\" 안주인이 높고 노래하듯 명랑한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Good.", "\"좋소."], ["One can dine--sup--what you please to call it?’", "저녁을—야식을—뭐라 부르든 좋소, 먹을 수 있겠소?\""]], [["‘Ah, perfectly!’ cried the landlady as before.", "\"아, 물론이지요!\" 안주인이 아까처럼 외쳤다."]], [["‘Dispatch then, madame, if you please.", "\"그럼 서둘러 주시오, 마담."], ["Something to eat, as quickly as you can; and some wine at once.", "먹을 것을 되도록 빨리, 그리고 포도주를 곧장."], ["I am exhausted.’", "난 기진맥진했소.\""]], [["‘It is very bad weather, monsieur,’ said the landlady.", "\"날씨가 아주 사납지요, 무슈.\" 안주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Cursed weather.’", "\"빌어먹을 날씨요.\""]], [["‘And a very long road.’", "\"그리고 길도 무척 멀고요.\""]], [["‘A cursed road.’", "\"빌어먹을 길이오.\""]], [["His hoarse voice failed him, and he rested his head upon his hands until a bottle of wine was brought from the counter.", "그의 쉰 목소리가 잦아들었고, 그는 계산대에서 포도주 한 병이 올 때까지 두 손에 머리를 괴고 있었다."], ["Having filled and emptied his little tumbler twice, and having broken off an end from the great loaf that was set before him with his cloth and napkin, soup-plate, salt, pepper, and oil, he rested his back against the corner of the wall, made a couch of the bench on which he sat, and began to chew crust, until such time as his repast should be ready.", "작은 잔을 두 번 채웠다 비우고, 보자기와 냅킨, 수프 접시, 소금, 후추, 기름과 함께 앞에 놓인 큼직한 빵 덩이에서 한쪽 끝을 떼어 낸 뒤, 그는 벽 모서리에 등을 기대고, 앉아 있던 긴 의자를 침상 삼아, 식사가 마련될 때까지 빵 껍질을 씹기 시작했다."]], [["There had been that momentary interruption of the talk about the stove, and that temporary inattention to and distraction from one another, which is usually inseparable in such a company from the arrival of a stranger.", "낯선 자가 들어오면 그런 자리에서 으레 따라붙는, 난로 둘레의 그 잠깐의 대화 끊김과 서로에 대한 일시적인 무관심과 산만함이 있었다. 그것은 이제 지나간 터였다."], ["It had passed over by this time; and the men had done glancing at him, and were talking again.", "사내들은 그를 흘끔거리기를 그치고 다시 이야기를 나누고 있었다."]], [["‘That’s the true reason,’ said one of them, bringing a story he had been telling, to a close, ‘that’s the true reason why they said that the devil was let loose.’ The speaker was the tall Swiss belonging to the church, and he brought something of the authority of the church into the discussion--especially as the devil was in question.", "\"그게 진짜 이유라니까.\" 그들 가운데 하나가, 들려주던 이야기를 마무리 지으며 말했다. \"그게 바로 사람들이 악마가 풀려났다고들 했던 진짜 이유라고.\" 말하는 자는 그 교회에 딸린 키 큰 스위스 사람이었는데, 그는 그 논의에 교회의 권위 같은 것을 끌어들였다—더욱이 악마가 화제였으니 말이다."]], [["The landlady having given her directions for the new guest’s entertainment to her husband, who acted as cook to the Break of Day, had resumed her needlework behind her counter.", "새 손님 대접에 관해 남편에게 지시를 내린 안주인은—남편은 '동틀 녘'의 요리사 노릇을 했다—계산대 뒤에서 다시 바느질을 시작한 참이었다."], ["She was a smart, neat, bright little woman, with a good deal of cap and a good deal of stocking, and she struck into the conversation with several laughing nods of her head, but without looking up from her work.", "야무지고, 깔끔하고, 발랄한 자그마한 여인으로, 머릿수건도 큼직하고 양말도 두툼한 차림이었는데, 그녀는 일감에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 웃으며 몇 번이고 고개를 끄덕이며 대화에 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Ah Heaven, then,’ said she.", "\"아, 저런, 그래서요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘When the boat came up from Lyons, and brought the news that the devil was actually let loose at Marseilles, some fly-catchers swallowed it.", "\"리옹에서 배가 올라와, 마르세유에서 정말로 악마가 풀려났다는 소식을 가져왔을 때, 어떤 얼간이들은 그걸 곧이곧대로 삼키더군요. 그런데 나는요?"], ["But I? No, not I.’", "천만에요, 나는 아니지요.\""]], [["‘Madame, you are always right,’ returned the tall Swiss.", "\"마담, 당신은 언제나 옳습니다.\" 키 큰 스위스 사람이 응수했다."], ["‘Doubtless you were enraged against that man, madame?’", "\"분명 당신은 그 사내한테 잔뜩 화가 나셨겠지요, 마담?\""]], [["‘Ay, yes, then!’ cried the landlady, raising her eyes from her work, opening them very wide, and tossing her head on one side.", "\"아, 그럼요, 그렇고말고요!\" 안주인이 일감에서 눈을 들어, 눈을 아주 크게 뜨고 고개를 한쪽으로 까딱하며 외쳤다."], ["‘Naturally, yes.’", "\"당연하지요, 그렇고말고요.\""]], [["‘He was a bad subject.’", "\"그자는 못된 인간이었지요.\""]], [["‘He was a wicked wretch,’ said the landlady, ‘and well merited what he had the good fortune to escape.", "\"사악한 망나니였지요.\" 안주인이 말했다."], ["So much the worse.’", "\"그자는 운 좋게도 피한 그 벌을 받아 마땅했어요. 더 안된 일이지요.\""]], [["‘Stay, madame!", "\"잠깐만요, 마담!"], ["Let us see,’ returned the Swiss, argumentatively turning his cigar between his lips.", "한번 따져 봅시다.\" 스위스 사람이 입술 사이에서 시가를 돌리며 따지듯 응수했다."], ["‘It may have been his unfortunate destiny.", "\"어쩌면 그건 그자의 불운한 운명이었는지도 모르지요."], ["He may have been the child of circumstances.", "그자는 환경의 자식이었는지도 몰라요."], ["It is always possible that he had, and has, good in him if one did but know how to find it out.", "어떻게 찾아내야 할지만 안다면, 그자에게도 선한 면이 있었고 또 있을 가능성은 늘 있으니까요."], ["Philosophical philanthropy teaches--’", "철학적 박애주의가 가르치기를—\""]], [["The rest of the little knot about the stove murmured an objection to the introduction of that threatening expression.", "난로 둘레의 자그마한 무리 가운데 나머지 사람들은 그 위협적인 표현이 등장하는 것에 웅성거리며 못마땅함을 드러냈다."], ["Even the two players at dominoes glanced up from their game, as if to protest against philosophical philanthropy being brought by name into the Break of Day.", "도미노를 치던 두 사람마저 게임에서 눈을 들었으니, 마치 철학적 박애주의가 그 이름으로 '동틀 녘'에 끌려 들어오는 것에 항의하기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["‘Hold there, you and your philanthropy,’ cried the smiling landlady, nodding her head more than ever.", "\"그만두세요, 당신하고 당신의 박애주의 따위는.\" 웃음 띤 안주인이 그 어느 때보다 더 고개를 끄덕이며 외쳤다."], ["‘Listen then.", "\"그럼 들어 보세요."], ["I am a woman, I.", "나는 여자예요, 나는요."], ["I know nothing of philosophical philanthropy.", "철학적 박애주의 같은 건 아무것도 몰라요."], ["But I know what I have seen, and what I have looked in the face in this world here, where I find myself.", "하지만 내가 본 것, 그리고 내가 살아가는 이 세상에서 똑바로 마주 본 것은 알아요."], ["And I tell you this, my friend, that there are people (men and women both, unfortunately) who have no good in them--none.", "그리고 당신한테 이 말은 해 두지요, 친구. 이 세상엔 (안타깝게도 남자도 여자도) 선한 면이라곤 조금도 없는 사람들이 있어요—전혀요."], ["That there are people whom it is necessary to detest without compromise.", "타협 없이 미워해야만 하는 사람들이 있어요."], ["That there are people who must be dealt with as enemies of the human race.", "인류의 적으로 다뤄야만 하는 사람들이 있어요."], ["That there are people who have no human heart, and who must be crushed like savage beasts and cleared out of the way.", "인간의 마음이라곤 없어서, 사나운 짐승처럼 짓밟아 길에서 치워 버려야 하는 사람들이 있어요."], ["They are but few, I hope; but I have seen (in this world here where I find myself, and even at the little Break of Day) that there are such people.", "그런 자들은 많지 않기를 바라지만, 나는 봤어요 (내가 살아가는 이 세상에서, 심지어 이 자그마한 '동틀 녘'에서도) 그런 사람들이 있다는 걸요."], ["And I do not doubt that this man--whatever they call him, I forget his name--is one of them.’", "그리고 나는 이 사내가—그들이 뭐라고 부르든, 이름은 잊어버렸지만—그런 자들 가운데 하나라는 걸 의심하지 않아요.\""]], [["The landlady’s lively speech was received with greater favour at the Break of Day, than it would have elicited from certain amiable whitewashers of the class she so unreasonably objected to, nearer Great Britain.", "안주인의 활기찬 연설은 '동틀 녘'에서 꽤 호의적으로 받아들여졌는데, 이는 그녀가 그토록 이치에 닿지 않게 반대하던 부류를 점잖게 변호하는 어떤 상냥한 사람들이라면, 그레이트브리튼에 더 가까운 곳에서 끌어냈을 반응보다 훨씬 나은 것이었다."]], [["‘My faith! If your philosophical philanthropy,’ said the landlady, putting down her work, and rising to take the stranger’s soup from her husband, who appeared with it at a side door, ‘puts anybody at the mercy of such people by holding terms with them at all, in words or deeds, or both, take it away from the Break of Day, for it isn’t worth a sou.’", "\"맙소사! 당신의 철학적 박애주의라는 것이,\" 안주인이 일감을 내려놓고 일어나, 옆문으로 수프를 들고 나타난 남편에게서 그것을 받아 그 낯선 손님에게 가져가며 말했다. \"말로든 행동으로든, 아니면 둘 다로든, 그런 사람들과 조금이라도 타협함으로써 누군가를 그자들의 손아귀에 넘기는 거라면, 그딴 건 '동틀 녘'에서 치워 버리세요. 한 푼어치 값도 없으니까요.\""]], [["As she placed the soup before the guest, who changed his attitude to a sitting one, he looked her full in the face, and his moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache.", "그녀가 손님 앞에 수프를 놓을 때, 자세를 고쳐 앉은 손님은 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보았는데, 그의 콧수염이 코 밑에서 올라가고 그의 코가 콧수염 위로 내려왔다."]], [["‘Well!’ said the previous speaker, ‘let us come back to our subject.", "\"자!\" 앞서 말하던 자가 입을 열었다."], ["Leaving all that aside, gentlemen, it was because the man was acquitted on his trial that people said at Marseilles that the devil was let loose.", "\"우리 화제로 돌아갑시다. 그 모든 건 제쳐 두고, 여러분, 사람들이 마르세유에서 악마가 풀려났다고 한 건 바로 그 사내가 재판에서 무죄를 받았기 때문이었어요."], ["That was how the phrase began to circulate, and what it meant; nothing more.’", "그렇게 해서 그 말이 돌기 시작했고, 그게 그 말의 뜻이었지요. 그 이상은 아무것도 아니에요.\""]], [["‘How do they call him?’ said the landlady.", "\"그자를 뭐라고 부른다지요?\" 안주인이 물었다."], ["‘Biraud, is it not?’", "\"비로, 였던가요?\""]], [["‘Rigaud, madame,’ returned the tall Swiss.", "\"리고입니다, 마담.\" 키 큰 스위스 사람이 답했다."]], [["‘Rigaud!", "\"리고!"], ["To be sure.’", "맞아요.\""]], [["The traveller’s soup was succeeded by a dish of meat, and that by a dish of vegetables.", "나그네의 수프 다음에는 고기 요리가, 그다음에는 채소 요리가 나왔다."], ["He ate all that was placed before him, emptied his bottle of wine, called for a glass of rum, and smoked his cigarette with his cup of coffee.", "그는 앞에 놓인 것을 모조리 먹고, 포도주 한 병을 비우고, 럼주 한 잔을 청하고, 커피를 마시며 담배를 피웠다."], ["As he became refreshed, he became overbearing; and patronised the company at the Daybreak in certain small talk at which he assisted, as if his condition were far above his appearance.", "기운이 돌아오자, 그는 거만해졌다. 그리고 거들먹거리며 '동틀 녘'의 사람들을 상대로, 자기가 거든 자잘한 잡담 속에서 마치 자기 처지가 겉모습보다 한참 높기라도 한 양 행세했다."]], [["The company might have had other engagements, or they might have felt their inferiority, but in any case they dispersed by degrees, and not being replaced by other company, left their new patron in possession of the Break of Day.", "사람들은 다른 볼일이 있었는지도, 아니면 자기네가 한 수 아래라고 느꼈는지도 모르나, 어쨌든 차츰 흩어졌고, 다른 손님들이 그 자리를 메우지 않아, 새로 들어온 그 후원자가 '동틀 녘'을 차지하게 되었다."], ["The landlord was clinking about in his kitchen; the landlady was quiet at her work; and the refreshed traveller sat smoking by the stove, warming his ragged feet.", "주인장은 부엌에서 달그락거리고 있었고, 안주인은 조용히 일감에 매달려 있었으며, 기운을 되찾은 나그네는 난로 곁에 앉아 담배를 피우며 해진 발을 녹이고 있었다."]], [["‘Pardon me, madame--that Biraud.’", "\"실례하오, 마담—그 비로 말이오.\""]], [["‘Rigaud, monsieur.’", "\"리고입니다, 무슈.\""]], [["‘Rigaud.", "\"리고."], ["Pardon me again--has contracted your displeasure, how?’", "다시 한번 실례하오—어떻게 당신의 노여움을 산 거요?\""]], [["The landlady, who had been at one moment thinking within herself that this was a handsome man, at another moment that this was an ill-looking man, observed the nose coming down and the moustache going up, and strongly inclined to the latter decision.", "안주인은 한순간엔 이 사내가 잘생긴 사내라고, 또 한순간엔 이 사내가 험상궂게 생긴 사내라고 속으로 생각하던 참이었는데, 코가 내려가고 콧수염이 올라가는 것을 보고는 후자 쪽으로 마음이 강하게 기울었다."], ["Rigaud was a criminal, she said, who had killed his wife.", "리고는 제 아내를 죽인 범죄자라고 그녀가 말했다."]], [["‘Ay, ay?", "\"아, 그래요?"], ["Death of my life, that’s a criminal indeed.", "맙소사, 그건 정말 범죄자로군."], ["But how do you know it?’", "한데 그걸 어떻게 아오?\""]], [["‘All the world knows it.’", "\"온 세상이 다 알지요.\""]], [["‘Hah!", "\"허!"], ["And yet he escaped justice?’", "그런데도 그자가 처벌을 면했단 말이오?\""]], [["‘Monsieur, the law could not prove it against him to its satisfaction.", "\"무슈, 법으로는 그자에게 죄를 충분히 입증할 수 없었지요."], ["So the law says.", "법이 그렇게 말한 거예요."], ["Nevertheless, all the world knows he did it.", "그래도 그자가 저질렀다는 건 온 세상이 다 알아요."], ["The people knew it so well, that they tried to tear him to pieces.’", "사람들이 어찌나 잘 알았던지, 그자를 갈가리 찢어 죽이려 들었거든요.\""]], [["‘Being all in perfect accord with their own wives?’ said the guest.", "\"다들 자기 아내들과는 더없이 사이가 좋았던 모양이오?\" 손님이 말했다."], ["‘Haha!’", "\"하하!\""]], [["The landlady of the Break of Day looked at him again, and felt almost confirmed in her last decision.", "'동틀 녘'의 안주인은 그를 다시 쳐다보았고, 자신의 마지막 판단이 거의 굳어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He had a fine hand, though, and he turned it with a great show.", "하지만 그는 고운 손을 지녔고, 그것을 한껏 과시하며 놀렸다."], ["She began once more to think that he was not ill-looking after all.", "그녀는 다시금 그가 결국 험상궂게 생긴 건 아니라고 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["‘Did you mention, madame--or was it mentioned among the gentlemen--what became of him?’", "\"혹시 말씀하셨소, 마담—아니면 신사분들 사이에서 나온 이야기였소—그자가 어찌 되었는지?\""]], [["The landlady shook her head; it being the first conversational stage at which her vivacious earnestness had ceased to nod it, keeping time to what she said.", "안주인은 고개를 가로저었다. 이것은 그녀의 발랄한 진지함이, 자기가 하는 말에 박자를 맞추어 끄덕이던 그 짓을 멈춘 첫 번째 대화 단계였다."], ["It had been mentioned at the Daybreak, she remarked, on the authority of the journals, that he had been kept in prison for his own safety.", "'동틀 녘'에서 신문을 근거로 들먹이기로는, 그자가 제 신변 안전을 위해 감옥에 갇혀 있었다고 했다고 그녀가 말했다."], ["However that might be, he had escaped his deserts; so much the worse.", "그게 어찌 되었든, 그자는 마땅한 벌을 면했으니, 더 안된 일이라고."]], [["The guest sat looking at her as he smoked out his final cigarette, and as she sat with her head bent over her work, with an expression that might have resolved her doubts, and brought her to a lasting conclusion on the subject of his good or bad looks if she had seen it.", "손님은 마지막 담배를 다 피우며 그녀를 바라보고 앉아 있었고, 그녀는 일감 위로 고개를 숙이고 앉아 있었는데, 그때 그의 얼굴엔 그녀가 만약 보았더라면 그가 잘생겼는지 험상궂은지에 대한 의문을 풀고 한결같은 결론에 이르게 했을 법한 표정이 떠올라 있었다."], ["When she did look up, the expression was not there.", "그러나 그녀가 정작 눈을 들었을 때, 그 표정은 거기 없었다."], ["The hand was smoothing his shaggy moustache.", "그 손은 그의 텁수룩한 콧수염을 쓰다듬고 있었다."]], [["‘May one ask to be shown to bed, madame?’", "\"잠자리로 안내해 달라고 청해도 되겠소, 마담?\""]], [["Very willingly, monsieur.", "기꺼이지요, 무슈."], ["Hola, my husband!", "어이, 여보!"], ["My husband would conduct him up-stairs.", "남편이 그를 이층으로 데려다줄 거예요."], ["There was one traveller there, asleep, who had gone to bed very early indeed, being overpowered by fatigue; but it was a large chamber with two beds in it, and space enough for twenty.", "거기엔 나그네 하나가 이미 잠들어 있는데, 피로에 짓눌려 일찌감치 잠자리에 든 사람이지요. 하지만 침대가 둘 딸린 큰 방이라, 스무 명이 들어가도 될 만큼 넉넉해요."], ["This the landlady of the Break of Day chirpingly explained, calling between whiles, ‘Hola, my husband!’ out at the side door.", "이렇게 '동틀 녘'의 안주인은 재잘재잘 설명하며, 틈틈이 옆문 밖으로 \"어이, 여보!\" 하고 불러 댔다."]], [["My husband answered at length, ‘It is I, my wife!’ and presenting himself in his cook’s cap, lighted the traveller up a steep and narrow staircase; the traveller carrying his own cloak and knapsack, and bidding the landlady good night with a complimentary reference to the pleasure of seeing her again to-morrow.", "마침내 남편이 \"나야, 여보!\" 하고 답하더니, 요리사 모자를 쓴 채 모습을 드러내고는, 가파르고 좁은 계단으로 나그네를 데리고 올라가며 길을 밝혔다."], ["It was a large room, with a rough splintery floor, unplastered rafters overhead, and two bedsteads on opposite sides.", "나그네는 제 외투와 배낭을 직접 들고, 안주인에게 내일 다시 뵐 즐거움에 대한 인사치레를 곁들여 잘 자라고 말했다."], ["Here ‘my husband’ put down the candle he carried, and with a sidelong look at his guest stooping over his knapsack, gruffly gave him the instruction, ‘The bed to the right!’ and left him to his repose.", "그곳은 큰 방으로, 거칠게 가시 돋친 바닥에, 머리 위로는 회칠도 안 한 서까래가 드러나 있었고, 맞은편 양쪽에 침대 둘이 놓여 있었다. 여기에 '여보'는 들고 온 촛불을 내려놓더니, 배낭 위로 몸을 숙인 손님을 곁눈질로 보며 무뚝뚝하게 \"오른쪽 침대요!\""], ["The landlord, whether he was a good or a bad physiognomist, had fully made up his mind that the guest was an ill-looking fellow.", "하고 일러 주고는 그를 쉬게 두고 떠났다. 주인장은 관상을 잘 보든 못 보든, 그 손님이 험상궂게 생긴 작자라고 단단히 마음을 정해 둔 터였다."]], [["The guest looked contemptuously at the clean coarse bedding prepared for him, and, sitting down on the rush chair at the bedside, drew his money out of his pocket, and told it over in his hand.", "손님은 자기를 위해 준비된 깨끗하고 거친 침구를 경멸하듯 바라보고는, 침대 곁의 골풀 의자에 앉아 주머니에서 돈을 꺼내 손바닥에 놓고 세었다. \"먹기는 해야지.\""], ["‘One must eat,’ he muttered to himself, ‘but by Heaven I must eat at the cost of some other man to-morrow!’", "그가 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"하지만 맙소사, 내일은 다른 놈 돈으로 먹어야겠어!\""]], [["As he sat pondering, and mechanically weighing his money in his palm, the deep breathing of the traveller in the other bed fell so regularly upon his hearing that it attracted his eyes in that direction.", "그렇게 앉아 골똘히 생각하며 손바닥 위의 돈을 무심코 가늠하고 있을 때, 다른 침대에 누운 나그네의 깊은 숨소리가 어찌나 규칙적으로 그의 귀에 들어오는지, 그의 눈길이 그쪽으로 끌려갔다."], ["The man was covered up warm, and had drawn the white curtain at his head, so that he could be only heard, not seen.", "그 사내는 따뜻하게 이불을 덮고, 머리맡의 흰 커튼을 쳐 두어, 들리기만 할 뿐 보이지는 않았다."], ["But the deep regular breathing, still going on while the other was taking off his worn shoes and gaiters, and still continuing when he had laid aside his coat and cravat, became at length a strong provocative to curiosity, and incentive to get a glimpse of the sleeper’s face.", "그러나 그 깊고 규칙적인 숨소리는, 이쪽 사내가 해진 신발과 각반을 벗는 동안에도 계속되고, 외투와 넥타이를 벗어 놓았을 때까지도 이어져, 마침내 강한 호기심을 자극하고, 잠든 자의 얼굴을 한번 엿보고 싶은 충동을 불러일으켰다."]], [["The waking traveller, therefore, stole a little nearer, and yet a little nearer, and a little nearer to the sleeping traveller’s bed, until he stood close beside it.", "그리하여 깨어 있는 나그네는 잠든 나그네의 침대 쪽으로 조금 더, 또 조금 더, 또 조금 더 가까이 살금살금 다가가, 마침내 그 곁에 바짝 섰다."], ["Even then he could not see his face, for he had drawn the sheet over it.", "그래도 얼굴은 볼 수 없었으니, 그가 시트를 얼굴 위로 끌어 올려 두었기 때문이었다."], ["The regular breathing still continuing, he put his smooth white hand (such a treacherous hand it looked, as it went creeping from him!) to the sheet, and gently lifted it away.", "규칙적인 숨소리가 여전히 이어지자, 그는 매끈한 흰 손을(그 손이 그에게서 슬며시 뻗어 갈 때 어찌나 음흉해 보이던지!) 시트에 갖다 대고는 살그머니 그것을 걷어 냈다."]], [["‘Death of my soul!’ he whispered, falling back, ‘here’s Cavalletto!’", "\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 그가 뒤로 물러서며 속삭였다. \"카발레토잖아!\""]], [["The little Italian, previously influenced in his sleep, perhaps, by the stealthy presence at his bedside, stopped in his regular breathing, and with a long deep respiration opened his eyes.", "자그마한 이탈리아 사내는, 어쩌면 잠결에 머리맡의 그 은밀한 기척에 영향을 받았는지, 규칙적인 숨을 멈추더니, 길고 깊게 한 번 숨을 들이쉬며 눈을 떴다."], ["At first they were not awake, though open.", "처음엔 떴어도 깨어 있지는 않았다."], ["He lay for some seconds looking placidly at his old prison companion, and then, all at once, with a cry of surprise and alarm, sprang out of bed.", "그는 몇 초간 누운 채 옛 감옥 동무를 평온하게 바라보다가, 별안간 놀라고 겁에 질린 비명을 지르며 침대에서 벌떡 뛰쳐나왔다."]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["What’s the matter?", "왜 이래?"], ["Keep quiet!", "조용히 해!"], ["It’s I.", "나야."], ["You know me?’ cried the other, in a suppressed voice.", "날 알아보겠어?\" 다른 사내가 억누른 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["But John Baptist, widely staring, muttering a number of invocations and ejaculations, tremblingly backing into a corner, slipping on his trousers, and tying his coat by the two sleeves round his neck, manifested an unmistakable desire to escape by the door rather than renew the acquaintance.", "그러나 존 뱁티스트는 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨고, 여러 가지 기도문과 탄식을 중얼거리며, 부들부들 떨면서 한구석으로 물러나, 바지를 꿰어 입고, 외투의 두 소매를 목에 둘러 묶으면서, 그 옛 친분을 새로 다지기보다는 차라리 문으로 달아나고 싶다는 분명한 기색을 드러냈다."], ["Seeing this, his old prison comrade fell back upon the door, and set his shoulders against it.", "이를 본 옛 감옥 동무는 문에 등을 기대고 어깨로 그것을 막아섰다."]], [["‘Cavalletto!", "\"카발레토!"], ["Wake, boy! Rub your eyes and look at me.", "정신 차려, 이 사람아!"], ["Not the name you used to call me--don’t use that--Lagnier, say Lagnier!’", "눈을 비비고 날 좀 봐. 예전에 날 부르던 이름 말고—그건 쓰지 마—라니에야, 라니에라고 불러!\""]], [["John Baptist, staring at him with eyes opened to their utmost width, made a number of those national, backhanded shakes of the right forefinger in the air, as if he were resolved on negativing beforehand everything that the other could possibly advance during the whole term of his life.", "존 뱁티스트는 눈을 한껏 크게 뜨고 그를 바라보며, 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 허공에 여러 번 까딱이는 그 나라 특유의 동작을 해 보였는데, 마치 상대가 평생토록 무슨 말을 꺼내든 미리 다 부정해 버리기로 작정한 듯했다."]], [["‘Cavalletto!", "\"카발레토!"], ["Give me your hand.", "손을 이리 줘."], ["You know Lagnier, the gentleman.", "너도 라니에라는 신사를 알지."], ["Touch the hand of a gentleman!’", "신사의 손을 잡아 보라고!\""]], [["Submitting himself to the old tone of condescending authority, John Baptist, not at all steady on his legs as yet, advanced and put his hand in his patron’s.", "거들먹거리며 명령하던 그 옛 어조에 굴복해, 존 뱁티스트는 아직 다리가 영 후들거리는 채로 다가가, 후원자의 손에 제 손을 얹었다."], ["Monsieur Lagnier laughed; and having given it a squeeze, tossed it up and let it go.", "무슈 라니에가 웃었다. 그리고 그것을 한 번 꽉 쥐었다가, 위로 던지듯 놓아주었다."]], [["‘Then you were--’ faltered John Baptist.", "\"그럼 당신은—\" 존 뱁티스트가 더듬거렸다."]], [["‘Not shaved?", "\"면도당하지 않았느냐고? 천만에."], ["No.", "자 봐!\""], ["See here!’ cried Lagnier, giving his head a twirl; ‘as tight on as your own.’", "라니에가 머리를 휙 돌려 보이며 외쳤다. \"네 것만큼이나 멀쩡히 붙어 있지.\""]], [["John Baptist, with a slight shiver, looked all round the room as if to recall where he was.", "존 뱁티스트는 가볍게 몸을 떨며, 자기가 어디 있는지 떠올리려는 듯 방 안을 빙 둘러보았다."], ["His patron took that opportunity of turning the key in the door, and then sat down upon his bed.", "그의 후원자는 그 틈을 타 문에 꽂힌 열쇠를 돌리고, 침대에 가서 앉았다."]], [["‘Look!’ he said, holding up his shoes and gaiters.", "\"봐!\" 그가 신발과 각반을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["‘That’s a poor trim for a gentleman, you’ll say.", "\"신사 치고는 영 초라한 행색이라고 너는 말하겠지."], ["No matter, you shall see how soon I’ll mend it.", "상관없어, 내가 이걸 얼마나 빨리 고치는지 보게 될 테니."], ["Come and sit down.", "이리 와서 앉아."], ["Take your old place!’", "옛날 네 자리에 앉으라고!\""]], [["John Baptist, looking anything but reassured, sat down on the floor at the bedside, keeping his eyes upon his patron all the time.", "존 뱁티스트는 도무지 안심한 기색이 아닌 채로, 침대 곁 바닥에 앉아, 줄곧 후원자에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["‘That’s well!’ cried Lagnier.", "\"좋아!\" 라니에가 외쳤다."], ["‘Now we might be in the old infernal hole again, hey?", "\"이제 우리가 다시 그 지긋지긋한 옛 소굴에 와 있는 셈이로군, 응?"], ["How long have you been out?’", "너는 나온 지 얼마나 됐어?\""]], [["‘Two days after you, my master.’", "\"당신보다 이틀 뒤였어요, 주인님.\""]], [["‘How do you come here?’", "\"여기엔 어떻게 온 거야?\""]], [["‘I was cautioned not to stay there, and so I left the town at once, and since then I have changed about. I have been doing odds and ends at Avignon, at Pont Esprit, at Lyons; upon the Rhone, upon the Saone.’ As he spoke, he rapidly mapped the places out with his sunburnt hand upon the floor.", "\"거기 머물지 말라고 경고를 받아서, 곧장 그 도시를 떠났지요. 그 뒤로 이리저리 옮겨 다녔어요. 아비뇽에서, 퐁테스프리에서, 리옹에서 잡일을 했고요. 론강에서도, 손강에서도요.\" 말하면서 그는 햇볕에 그을린 손으로 바닥에 그 지명들을 재빠르게 지도 그리듯 짚어 보였다."]], [["‘And where are you going?’", "\"그래서 어디로 가는 거야?\""]], [["‘Going, my master?’", "\"가다니요, 주인님?\""]], [["‘Ay!’", "\"그래!\""]], [["John Baptist seemed to desire to evade the question without knowing how.", "존 뱁티스트는 그 질문을 어찌 피해야 할지 모르면서도 피하고 싶어 하는 듯했다. \"바쿠스에 맹세코!\""], ["‘By Bacchus!’ he said at last, as if he were forced to the admission, ‘I have sometimes had a thought of going to Paris, and perhaps to England.’", "그가 마침내, 마치 어쩔 수 없이 털어놓는다는 듯이 말했다. \"파리로 갈까, 어쩌면 영국까지 갈까 하는 생각을 가끔 했지요.\""]], [["‘Cavalletto.", "\"카발레토."], ["This is in confidence.", "이건 비밀인데."], ["I also am going to Paris and perhaps to England.", "나도 파리로 가, 어쩌면 영국까지도."], ["We’ll go together.’", "우리 함께 가자.\""]], [["The little man nodded his head, and showed his teeth; and yet seemed not quite convinced that it was a surpassingly desirable arrangement.", "자그마한 사내는 고개를 끄덕이며 이를 드러내 보였지만, 그것이 더없이 바람직한 약속이라고는 영 확신하지 못하는 눈치였다."]], [["‘We’ll go together,’ repeated Lagnier.", "\"우리 함께 가는 거야.\" 라니에가 거듭 말했다."], ["‘You shall see how soon I will force myself to be recognised as a gentleman, and you shall profit by it.", "\"내가 얼마나 빨리 나 자신을 신사로 인정받게 만드는지 너도 보게 될 테고, 너도 그 덕을 보게 될 거야."], ["It is agreed?", "합의된 거지?"], ["Are we one?’", "우린 한패지?\""]], [["‘Oh, surely, surely!’ said the little man.", "\"오, 그럼요, 그럼요!\" 자그마한 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Then you shall hear before I sleep--and in six words, for I want sleep--how I appear before you, I, Lagnier.", "\"그럼 내가 잠들기 전에—그것도 여섯 마디로, 졸리니까—내가 너 앞에 어떻게 나타나는지를 들어 둬. 나, 라니에로 말이야."], ["Remember that.", "그걸 명심해."], ["Not the other.’", "다른 이름 말고.\""]], [["‘Altro, altro!", "\"알트로, 알트로!"], ["Not Ri----’ Before John Baptist could finish the name, his comrade had got his hand under his chin and fiercely shut up his mouth.", "리—가 아니라—\" 존 뱁티스트가 그 이름을 미처 다 말하기도 전에, 동무가 그의 턱 밑에 손을 갖다 대고는 사납게 그 입을 틀어막았다."]], [["‘Death!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what are you doing?", "뭐 하는 거야?"], ["Do you want me to be trampled upon and stoned?", "내가 짓밟히고 돌에 맞기를 바라는 거냐?"], ["Do _you_ want to be trampled upon and stoned?", "너는 짓밟히고 돌에 맞고 싶어?"], ["You would be.", "그렇게 될 거다."], ["You don’t imagine that they would set upon me, and let my prison chum go?", "너는 그들이 나한테 달려들면서 내 감옥 동무는 그냥 놓아둘 거라고 생각하나?"], ["Don’t think it!’", "그런 건 꿈도 꾸지 마!\""]], [["There was an expression in his face as he released his grip of his friend’s jaw, from which his friend inferred that if the course of events really came to any stoning and trampling, Monsieur Lagnier would so distinguish him with his notice as to ensure his having his full share of it.", "그가 친구의 턱을 쥐었던 손을 풀 때 그의 얼굴엔 어떤 표정이 떠올라 있었는데, 친구는 그것으로부터, 만약 사태가 정말로 돌팔매질과 짓밟기로 번진다면 무슈 라니에가 자기를 각별히 점찍어 그 몫을 톡톡히 나눠 받게 해 줄 것임을 짐작했다."], ["He remembered what a cosmopolitan gentleman Monsieur Lagnier was, and how few weak distinctions he made.", "그는 무슈 라니에가 얼마나 세계주의적인 신사인지, 그리고 시시한 차별 따위는 거의 두지 않는 사람인지를 떠올렸다."]], [["‘I am a man,’ said Monsieur Lagnier, ‘whom society has deeply wronged since you last saw me.", "\"나는 말이지,\" 무슈 라니에가 말했다."], ["You know that I am sensitive and brave, and that it is my character to govern.", "\"네가 마지막으로 본 뒤로 사회로부터 깊이 부당한 대접을 받은 사람이야."], ["How has society respected those qualities in me?", "너도 알다시피 나는 예민하고 용감하며, 남을 다스리는 게 내 천성이지."], ["I have been shrieked at through the streets.", "그런데 사회가 내 그런 자질을 어떻게 대접했지?"], ["I have been guarded through the streets against men, and especially women, running at me armed with any weapons they could lay their hands on.", "나는 거리에서 사람들의 비명을 들었어. 나는 손에 잡히는 무기란 무기는 다 들고 나한테 달려드는 사내들로부터, 특히 여자들로부터 보호받으며 거리를 지나야 했지."], ["I have lain in prison for security, with the place of my confinement kept a secret, lest I should be torn out of it and felled by a hundred blows.", "나는 백 대의 매를 맞아 쓰러질까 봐, 끌려 나가 두들겨 맞을까 봐, 갇힌 장소를 비밀로 한 채 신변 보호를 위해 감옥에 누워 있었어."], ["I have been carted out of Marseilles in the dead of night, and carried leagues away from it packed in straw.", "나는 한밤중에 마르세유에서 짐수레에 실려 나가, 짚에 싸인 채 거기서 몇 리그나 떨어진 곳으로 옮겨졌지."], ["It has not been safe for me to go near my house; and, with a beggar’s pittance in my pocket, I have walked through vile mud and weather ever since, until my feet are crippled--look at them!", "내 집 근처에 가는 것조차 안전하지 않았고, 주머니엔 거지의 푼돈만 든 채, 그때부터 줄곧 더러운 진창과 궂은 날씨 속을 걸어, 발이 이렇게 절뚝거리게 될 때까지—이 발 좀 봐!"], ["Such are the humiliations that society has inflicted upon me, possessing the qualities I have mentioned, and which you know me to possess.", "내가 말한, 또 네가 내게 있다고 알고 있는 그런 자질을 지닌 나에게, 사회가 가한 굴욕이 이런 것들이야."], ["But society shall pay for it.’", "하지만 사회는 그 대가를 치르게 될 거다.\""]], [["All this he said in his companion’s ear, and with his hand before his lips.", "이 모든 것을 그는 동무의 귀에 대고, 입술 앞에 손을 가린 채 말했다."]], [["‘Even here,’ he went on in the same way, ‘even in this mean drinking-shop, society pursues me.", "\"여기서조차,\" 그가 같은 식으로 말을 이었다."], ["Madame defames me, and her guests defame me.", "\"이 보잘것없는 술집에서조차, 사회가 나를 쫓아오는군."], ["I, too, a gentleman with manners and accomplishments to strike them dead!", "마담이 나를 헐뜯고, 그 손님들이 나를 헐뜯어. 나, 그들쯤은 한 방에 쓰러뜨릴 예법과 교양을 갖춘 이 신사를 말이야!"], ["But the wrongs society has heaped upon me are treasured in this breast.’", "하지만 사회가 내게 쌓아 올린 부당함은 이 가슴속에 고스란히 간직되어 있지.\""]], [["To all of which John Baptist, listening attentively to the suppressed hoarse voice, said from time to time, ‘Surely, surely!’ tossing his head and shutting his eyes, as if there were the clearest case against society that perfect candour could make out.", "이 모든 말에, 그 억눌린 쉰 목소리에 귀를 기울이던 존 뱁티스트는 이따금 \"그럼요, 그렇고말고요!\" 하고 말하며, 머리를 흔들고 눈을 감았으니, 마치 더없이 솔직한 사람이라도 사회에 대해 제기할 수 있는 가장 명백한 고발이 거기 있기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["‘Put my shoes there,’ continued Lagnier.", "\"내 신발은 거기 놓아.\" 라니에가 말을 이었다."], ["‘Hang my cloak to dry there by the door.", "\"내 외투는 저기 문가에 걸어 말려."], ["Take my hat.’ He obeyed each instruction, as it was given.", "내 모자를 받아.\" 그는 지시가 떨어질 때마다 그대로 따랐다."], ["‘And this is the bed to which society consigns me, is it?", "\"그래서 이게 사회가 나를 처넣은 침대란 말이지, 안 그래?"], ["Hah.", "허."], ["_Very_ well!’", "아주 좋아!\""]], [["As he stretched out his length upon it, with a ragged handkerchief bound round his wicked head, and only his wicked head showing above the bedclothes, John Baptist was rather strongly reminded of what had so very nearly happened to prevent the moustache from any more going up as it did, and the nose from any more coming down as it did.", "그가 침대 위에 길게 몸을 뻗고, 그 사악한 머리에 해진 손수건을 둘러맨 채, 이불 위로 그 사악한 머리만 내놓고 누워 있을 때, 존 뱁티스트는 콧수염이 다시는 그렇게 올라가지 못하고 코가 다시는 그렇게 내려오지 못하게 막을 뻔했던 그 일이 얼마나 아슬아슬하게 일어날 뻔했었는지를 꽤나 생생히 떠올렸다."]], [["‘Shaken out of destiny’s dice-box again into your company, eh?", "\"운명의 주사위 통에서 도로 흔들려 나와 또 네 곁에 떨어졌군, 응?"], ["By Heaven!", "맙소사!"], ["So much the better for you.", "너한테는 더 잘된 일이지."], ["You’ll profit by it.", "너도 덕을 볼 테니까."], ["I shall need a long rest.", "나는 오래 쉬어야겠어."], ["Let me sleep in the morning.’", "아침엔 자게 내버려 둬.\""]], [["John Baptist replied that he should sleep as long as he would, and wishing him a happy night, put out the candle.", "존 뱁티스트는 원하는 만큼 주무시라고 답하고는, 편안한 밤 되시라 빌며 촛불을 껐다."], ["One might have supposed that the next proceeding of the Italian would have been to undress; but he did exactly the reverse, and dressed himself from head to foot, saving his shoes.", "이쯤이면 그 이탈리아 사내가 다음으로 옷을 벗으리라 짐작했을 법하지만, 그는 정반대로, 신발만 빼고 머리끝부터 발끝까지 옷을 차려입었다."], ["When he had so done, he lay down upon his bed with some of its coverings over him, and his coat still tied round his neck, to get through the night.", "그렇게 하고 나서 그는 이불 몇 장을 몸에 덮고, 외투는 여전히 목에 둘러맨 채로 침대에 누워, 그 밤을 견뎌 내려 했다."]], [["When he started up, the Godfather Break of Day was peeping at its namesake.", "그가 벌떡 일어났을 때, 대부 격인 동틀 녘이 제 이름의 가게를 빼꼼히 들여다보고 있었다."], ["He rose, took his shoes in his hand, turned the key in the door with great caution, and crept downstairs.", "그는 일어나, 신발을 손에 들고, 무척 조심스럽게 문의 열쇠를 돌려, 살금살금 계단을 내려갔다."], ["Nothing was astir there but the smell of coffee, wine, tobacco, and syrups; and madame’s little counter looked ghastly enough.", "거기엔 커피와 포도주와 담배와 시럽 냄새 말고는 아무것도 움직이지 않았고, 마담의 자그마한 계산대는 으스스해 보였다."], ["But he had paid madame his little note at it over night, and wanted to see nobody--wanted nothing but to get on his shoes and his knapsack, open the door, and run away.", "하지만 그는 간밤에 거기서 마담에게 자기 자그마한 계산서를 치른 터라, 아무도 만나고 싶지 않았다—신발과 배낭을 챙기고, 문을 열고, 달아나는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않았다."]], [["He prospered in his object.", "그는 뜻한 바를 이루었다."], ["No movement or voice was heard when he opened the door; no wicked head tied up in a ragged handkerchief looked out of the upper window.", "그가 문을 열었을 때 아무런 기척도, 목소리도 들리지 않았다. 해진 손수건을 동여맨 사악한 머리가 위층 창문으로 내다보지도 않았다."], ["When the sun had raised his full disc above the flat line of the horizon, and was striking fire out of the long muddy vista of paved road with its weary avenue of little trees, a black speck moved along the road and splashed among the flaming pools of rain-water, which black speck was John Baptist Cavalletto running away from his patron.", "해가 평평한 지평선 위로 둥근 원반을 온전히 들어 올려, 자그마한 나무들이 지친 듯 늘어선 가로수와 함께 길게 뻗은 진흙투성이 포석길에서 불꽃을 일으킬 무렵, 검은 점 하나가 길을 따라 움직이며 불타듯 빛나는 빗물 웅덩이들 사이로 첨벙거리며 나아갔으니, 그 검은 점이 바로 후원자에게서 달아나는 존 뱁티스트 카발레토였다."]], [["# CHAPTER 12. Bleeding Heart Yard", "# CHAPTER 12. Bleeding Heart Yard"]], [["In London itself, though in the old rustic road towards a suburb of note where in the days of William Shakespeare, author and stage-player, there were Royal hunting-seats--howbeit no sport is left there now but for hunters of men--Bleeding Heart Yard was to be found; a place much changed in feature and in fortune, yet with some relish of ancient greatness about it.", "런던 자체 안에서도, 명소가 된 어느 교외로 향하는 옛 시골길 위에 — 작가이자 무대 배우였던 윌리엄 셰익스피어의 시절에는 그곳에 왕실 사냥터가 있었으나, 지금은 사람을 사냥하는 자들 말고는 사냥이라 할 것이 남아 있지 않은 그곳에 — '피 흘리는 심장 마당'이 자리하고 있었다. 생김새도 운수도 크게 달라진 곳이었으나, 그래도 옛 영화의 정취가 약간은 남아 있었다."], ["Two or three mighty stacks of chimneys, and a few large dark rooms which had escaped being walled and subdivided out of the recognition of their old proportions, gave the Yard a character.", "우람한 굴뚝 더미 두세 개와, 벽으로 막히고 잘게 쪼개져 옛 비례를 알아보지 못하게 되는 신세를 면한 몇 개의 크고 어두운 방들이 이 마당에 한 가닥 풍격을 부여하고 있었다."], ["It was inhabited by poor people, who set up their rest among its faded glories, as Arabs of the desert pitch their tents among the fallen stones of the Pyramids; but there was a family sentimental feeling prevalent in the Yard, that it had a character.", "이곳에는 가난한 사람들이 살았는데, 사막의 아랍인들이 피라미드의 무너진 돌무더기 사이에 천막을 치듯, 그들도 이 빛바랜 영화 한가운데에 보금자리를 틀었다. 그러나 이 마당에는 이곳이 풍격을 지녔다는, 한 가족과도 같은 정서적 감정이 널리 퍼져 있었다."]], [["As if the aspiring city had become puffed up in the very ground on which it stood, the ground had so risen about Bleeding Heart Yard that you got into it down a flight of steps which formed no part of the original approach, and got out of it by a low gateway into a maze of shabby streets, which went about and about, tortuously ascending to the level again.", "마치 야심 찬 도시가 제가 딛고 선 바로 그 땅 위에서 부풀어 오르기라도 한 듯, 땅이 피 흘리는 심장 마당을 둘러싸고 어찌나 높이 솟았던지, 사람들은 본래 진입로의 일부가 아니었던 계단을 한 줄 내려가야만 그곳에 들어설 수 있었고, 나갈 때는 나지막한 문을 지나 초라한 거리들의 미로로 빠져나갔는데, 그 거리들은 빙빙 돌고 또 돌면서 구불구불 다시 본래 높이까지 올라갔다."], ["At this end of the Yard and over the gateway, was the factory of Daniel Doyce, often heavily beating like a bleeding heart of iron, with the clink of metal upon metal.", "마당의 이쪽 끝, 문 위쪽에는 대니얼 도이스의 공장이 있었고, 그곳은 쇠가 쇠에 부딪치는 쟁그랑 소리와 함께, 마치 피 흘리는 쇠의 심장처럼 묵직하게 쿵쿵 두드려 대곤 했다."]], [["The opinion of the Yard was divided respecting the derivation of its name.", "마당의 이름이 어디서 유래했는지를 두고 이곳의 의견은 갈라져 있었다."], ["The more practical of its inmates abided by the tradition of a murder; the gentler and more imaginative inhabitants, including the whole of the tender sex, were loyal to the legend of a young lady of former times closely imprisoned in her chamber by a cruel father for remaining true to her own true love, and refusing to marry the suitor he chose for her.", "이곳 거주자들 가운데 더 실리적인 부류는 어느 살인 사건의 전설을 고수했고, 다정다감하고 상상력이 풍부한 부류는, 부드러운 성별 전체를 포함하여, 옛 시절 어느 아가씨의 전설에 충성했다. 그 아가씨는 제 참된 연인에게 충실하기를 고집하며 아버지가 골라 준 구혼자와의 결혼을 거부했다는 이유로, 잔인한 아버지에 의해 제 방에 단단히 갇혔다고 했다."], ["The legend related how that the young lady used to be seen up at her window behind the bars, murmuring a love-lorn song of which the burden was, ‘Bleeding Heart, Bleeding Heart, bleeding away,’ until she died.", "전설에 따르면 그 아가씨는 창살 너머 창가에 서서, '피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리며 스러지네'를 후렴으로 삼은 상사병의 노래를 흥얼거리는 모습이 자주 보였고, 그러다 끝내 죽었다고 했다."], ["It was objected by the murderous party that this Refrain was notoriously the invention of a tambour-worker, a spinster and romantic, still lodging in the Yard.", "살인설을 주장하는 쪽에서는, 그 후렴이 실은 아직도 이 마당에 살고 있는, 자수 일을 하는 어느 노처녀이자 낭만주의자의 창작임이 익히 알려진 사실이라며 이의를 제기했다."], ["But, forasmuch as all favourite legends must be associated with the affections, and as many more people fall in love than commit murder--which it may be hoped, howsoever bad we are, will continue until the end of the world to be the dispensation under which we shall live--the Bleeding Heart, Bleeding Heart, bleeding away story, carried the day by a great majority.", "그러나 가장 사랑받는 전설이란 모름지기 애정과 결부되어야 하는 법이고, 살인을 저지르는 사람보다 사랑에 빠지는 사람이 훨씬 더 많으니 — 우리가 아무리 못된 존재라 하더라도, 세상이 끝날 때까지 우리가 그 아래에서 살아갈 섭리로 계속 남아 있기를 바라 마지않는 일이니 — '피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리는 심장, 피 흘리며 스러지네'의 이야기가 압도적 다수로 승리를 거두었다."], ["Neither party would listen to the antiquaries who delivered learned lectures in the neighbourhood, showing the Bleeding Heart to have been the heraldic cognisance of the old family to whom the property had once belonged.", "어느 쪽도 인근에서 학식 있는 강연을 펼치며, '피 흘리는 심장'이란 한때 이 땅을 소유했던 옛 가문의 문장 표지였다는 사실을 밝혀내는 골동품 연구가들의 말에는 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다."], ["And, considering that the hour-glass they turned from year to year was filled with the earthiest and coarsest sand, the Bleeding Heart Yarders had reason enough for objecting to be despoiled of the one little golden grain of poetry that sparkled in it.", "그리고 그들이 해마다 뒤집어 놓는 모래시계가 가장 흙투성이에 가장 거친 모래로 가득 차 있음을 생각하면, 피 흘리는 심장 마당 사람들이 그 안에서 반짝이는 단 하나의 작은 황금빛 시(詩)의 알갱이를 빼앗기지 않으려 했던 데에는 충분한 이유가 있었던 셈이다."]], [["Down in to the Yard, by way of the steps, came Daniel Doyce, Mr Meagles, and Clennam.", "계단을 통해 마당으로 내려온 사람은 대니얼 도이스, 미글스 씨, 그리고 클레넘이었다."], ["Passing along the Yard, and between the open doors on either hand, all abundantly garnished with light children nursing heavy ones, they arrived at its opposite boundary, the gateway.", "마당을 따라 걸으며, 한결같이 무거운 아이를 안은 가벼운 아이들로 그득한 양쪽의 열린 문 사이를 지나, 그들은 맞은편 경계인 그 문에 다다랐다."], ["Here Arthur Clennam stopped to look about him for the domicile of Plornish, plasterer, whose name, according to the custom of Londoners, Daniel Doyce had never seen or heard of to that hour.", "여기서 아서 클레넘은 멈춰 서서, 미장이 플로니시의 거처를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 런던 사람들의 관습대로, 대니얼 도이스는 그 시각까지도 그 이름을 본 적도 들은 적도 없었다."]], [["It was plain enough, nevertheless, as Little Dorrit had said; over a lime-splashed gateway in the corner, within which Plornish kept a ladder and a barrel or two.", "그렇지만 리틀 도릿이 말했던 대로, 거처는 충분히 쉽게 찾을 수 있었다. 모퉁이의, 회반죽이 튀어 얼룩진 문 위쪽이었는데, 그 문 안쪽에 플로니시는 사다리 하나와 통 한두 개를 두고 있었다."], ["The last house in Bleeding Heart Yard which she had described as his place of habitation, was a large house, let off to various tenants; but Plornish ingeniously hinted that he lived in the parlour, by means of a painted hand under his name, the forefinger of which hand (on which the artist had depicted a ring and a most elaborate nail of the genteelest form) referred all inquirers to that apartment.", "그녀가 그의 거처라고 일러 준, 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 마지막 집은 여러 세입자에게 나뉘어 세놓은 큰 집이었다. 그러나 플로니시는 제 이름 아래에 그려 넣은 손 그림으로, 자신이 응접실에 산다는 것을 재치 있게 일러 두었는데, 그 손의 집게손가락(화가가 반지 하나와 가장 점잖은 모양새의 더없이 공들인 손톱을 그려 넣은 손가락)은 묻는 이들을 모두 그 방으로 안내하고 있었다."]], [["Parting from his companions, after arranging another meeting with Mr Meagles, Clennam went alone into the entry, and knocked with his knuckles at the parlour-door.", "미글스 씨와 또 한 번 만날 약속을 잡은 뒤 일행과 헤어진 클레넘은, 혼자 입구로 들어가 손가락 마디로 응접실 문을 두드렸다."], ["It was opened presently by a woman with a child in her arms, whose unoccupied hand was hastily rearranging the upper part of her dress.", "곧 한 여인이 문을 열었는데, 그녀는 한 팔에 아이를 안고 있었고, 한가한 다른 손으로는 옷의 윗부분을 황급히 매만지고 있었다."], ["This was Mrs Plornish, and this maternal action was the action of Mrs Plornish during a large part of her waking existence.", "이 사람이 플로니시 부인이었고, 이 모성적 동작은 그녀가 깨어 있는 삶의 대부분 동안 행하는 동작이었다."]], [["Was Mr Plornish at home?", "플로니시 씨가 집에 있느냐고 묻자, 예의 바른 여인인 플로니시 부인이 \"글쎄요, 선생님,\""], ["‘Well, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish, a civil woman, ‘not to deceive you, he’s gone to look for a job.’", "하고 말했다. \"속이려는 건 아니지만, 일자리를 찾으러 나갔답니다.\""]], [["‘Not to deceive you’ was a method of speech with Mrs Plornish.", "'속이려는 건 아니지만'은 플로니시 부인의 말버릇이었다."], ["She would deceive you, under any circumstances, as little as might be; but she had a trick of answering in this provisional form.", "그녀는 어떤 경우에도 가능한 한 사람을 속이지 않으려 했으나, 이런 잠정적인 형태로 대답하는 버릇이 있었다."]], [["‘Do you think he will be back soon, if I wait for him?’", "\"제가 기다리면 곧 돌아올 것 같습니까?\""]], [["‘I have been expecting him,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘this half an hour, at any minute of time.", "\"이 반 시간째 기다리고 있던 참이라,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."], ["Walk in, sir.’", "\"이제 곧 들이닥칠 겁니다. 들어오세요, 선생님.\""]], [["Arthur entered the rather dark and close parlour (though it was lofty too), and sat down in the chair she placed for him.", "아서는 다소 어둡고 답답한(그러면서도 천장은 높은) 응접실로 들어가, 그녀가 놓아 준 의자에 앉았다."]], [["‘Not to deceive you, sir, I notice it,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘and I take it kind of you.’", "\"속이려는 건 아니지만, 선생님, 제가 그걸 알아챘답니다,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"친절하게 생각합니다.\""]], [["He was at a loss to understand what she meant; and by expressing as much in his looks, elicited her explanation.", "그는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하는지 알 수가 없었다. 그래서 그런 기색을 표정으로 드러내자, 그녀가 설명을 해 주었다."]], [["‘It ain’t many that comes into a poor place, that deems it worth their while to move their hats,’ said Mrs Plornish.", "\"이런 가난한 곳에 들어와서 굳이 모자를 벗을 만하다고 여기는 사람은 많지 않거든요,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."], ["‘But people think more of it than people think.’", "\"그래도 사람들이 생각하는 것보다 그걸 더 마음에 둔답니다.\""]], [["Clennam returned, with an uncomfortable feeling in so very slight a courtesy being unusual, Was that all!", "클레넘은, 그토록 사소한 예의가 흔치 않은 일이라는 데에 불편한 마음을 느끼며, 고작 그것뿐인가 싶었다!"], ["And stooping down to pinch the cheek of another young child who was sitting on the floor, staring at him, asked Mrs Plornish how old that fine boy was?", "그리고 바닥에 앉아 자기를 빤히 쳐다보는 또 다른 어린아이의 볼을 꼬집어 주려 몸을 숙이며, 저 잘생긴 사내아이가 몇 살이냐고 플로니시 부인에게 물었다."]], [["‘Four year just turned, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish.", "\"이제 막 네 살 됐어요, 선생님,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."], ["‘He _is_ a fine little fellow, ain’t he, sir?", "\"정말 잘생긴 꼬마지요, 안 그래요, 선생님?"], ["But this one is rather sickly.’ She tenderly hushed the baby in her arms, as she said it.", "그런데 이 아이는 좀 병약하답니다.\" 그렇게 말하며 그녀는 품에 안은 아기를 다정하게 어르며 달랬다."], ["‘You wouldn’t mind my asking if it happened to be a job as you was come about, sir, would you?’ asked Mrs Plornish wistfully.", "\"선생님, 혹시 일거리 때문에 오신 건지 여쭤봐도 괜찮으실까요?\" 플로니시 부인이 간절하게 물었다."]], [["She asked it so anxiously, that if he had been in possession of any kind of tenement, he would have had it plastered a foot deep rather than answer No.", "어찌나 애타게 묻던지, 만일 그가 어떤 종류든 집을 가지고 있었다면, 아니라고 대답하느니 차라리 한 자 두께로 회반죽을 발라 달라고 했을 것이다."], ["But he was obliged to answer No; and he saw a shade of disappointment on her face, as she checked a sigh, and looked at the low fire.", "그러나 그는 아니라고 대답할 수밖에 없었고, 그녀가 한숨을 억누르며 사그라드는 불을 바라볼 때, 그 얼굴에 어린 실망의 그늘을 보았다."], ["Then he saw, also, that Mrs Plornish was a young woman, made somewhat slatternly in herself and her belongings by poverty; and so dragged at by poverty and the children together, that their united forces had already dragged her face into wrinkles.", "그리고 그는 또한, 플로니시 부인이 가난 탓에 제 몸과 제 살림이 다소 추레해진 젊은 여인이며, 가난과 아이들에게 함께 어찌나 시달렸던지 그 두 힘이 합세하여 그녀의 얼굴을 벌써 주름지게 끌어내려 놓았음을 보았다."]], [["‘All such things as jobs,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘seems to me to have gone underground, they do indeed.’ (Herein Mrs Plornish limited her remark to the plastering trade, and spoke without reference to the Circumlocution Office and the Barnacle Family.)", "\"일거리란 일거리는 죄다,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"땅속으로 꺼져 버린 것만 같아요, 정말이지.\" (여기서 플로니시 부인은 미장 일에 한정하여 말한 것이었고, 에두름국이나 배너클 가문을 두고 한 말은 아니었다.)"]], [["‘Is it so difficult to get work?’ asked Arthur Clennam.", "\"일거리를 얻기가 그렇게 어렵습니까?\" 아서 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘Plornish finds it so,’ she returned.", "\"플로니시는 그렇게 여긴답니다,\""], ["‘He is quite unfortunate.", "그녀가 대답했다. \"운이 영 없어요."], ["Really he is.’", "정말로요.\""]], [["Really he was.", "정말로 그러했다."], ["He was one of those many wayfarers on the road of life, who seem to be afflicted with supernatural corns, rendering it impossible for them to keep up even with their lame competitors.", "그는 인생의 길을 가는 수많은 나그네들 가운데 하나로, 초자연적인 발 티눈이라도 앓는 듯, 절름발이 경쟁자들과조차 보조를 맞추지 못하는 사람이었다."], ["A willing, working, soft hearted, not hard-headed fellow, Plornish took his fortune as smoothly as could be expected; but it was a rough one.", "기꺼이 일하려 들고, 부지런하고, 마음이 여리되 머리는 단단하지 못한 사내인 플로니시는, 제 운수를 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 순순히 받아들였으나, 그것은 거친 운수였다."], ["It so rarely happened that anybody seemed to want him, it was such an exceptional case when his powers were in any request, that his misty mind could not make out how it happened.", "누군가 그를 필요로 하는 듯 보이는 일이 어찌나 드물고, 그의 솜씨가 조금이라도 요구되는 일이 어찌나 예외적이던지, 그의 흐릿한 머리로는 어찌하여 그런지를 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["He took it as it came, therefore; he tumbled into all kinds of difficulties, and tumbled out of them; and, by tumbling through life, got himself considerably bruised.", "그래서 그는 오는 대로 받아들였다. 그는 온갖 어려움 속으로 굴러떨어졌다가 또 굴러 나왔으며, 그렇게 인생을 굴러 지나오는 동안 적잖이 멍이 들고 말았다."]], [["‘It’s not for want of looking after jobs, I am sure,’ said Mrs Plornish, lifting up her eyebrows, and searching for a solution of the problem between the bars of the grate; ‘nor yet for want of working at them when they are to be got.", "\"일거리를 찾아다니지 않아서가 아닌 건 확실해요,\" 플로니시 부인이 눈썹을 치켜올리고 쇠살대 틈새에서 문제의 해답을 찾으며 말했다."], ["No one ever heard my husband complain of work.’", "\"일거리가 있을 때 거기 매달려 일하지 않아서도 아니고요. 제 남편이 일을 두고 불평하는 건 아무도 들어 본 적이 없답니다.\""]], [["Somehow or other, this was the general misfortune of Bleeding Heart Yard.", "이러나저러나 이것이 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 보편적인 불운이었다."], ["From time to time there were public complaints, pathetically going about, of labour being scarce--which certain people seemed to take extraordinarily ill, as though they had an absolute right to it on their own terms--but Bleeding Heart Yard, though as willing a Yard as any in Britain, was never the better for the demand.", "때때로 일손이 부족하다는 호소가 애처롭게 떠돌며 공공연하게 불거지곤 했는데 — 어떤 사람들은 마치 제 조건대로 일손을 부릴 절대적인 권리라도 있는 양 그것을 유난히 못마땅하게 여겼다 — 그러나 피 흘리는 심장 마당은 영국 어느 곳 못지않게 기꺼이 일하려는 마당이었음에도, 그 수요 덕을 본 적이 결코 없었다."], ["That high old family, the Barnacles, had long been too busy with their great principle to look into the matter; and indeed the matter had nothing to do with their watchfulness in out-generalling all other high old families except the Stiltstalkings.", "그 고귀한 옛 가문, 배너클 일가는 오래전부터 자신들의 위대한 원칙에 너무도 바빠 그 문제를 들여다볼 겨를이 없었다. 그리고 사실 그 문제는, 스틸트스토킹 일가를 제외한 다른 모든 고귀한 옛 가문들을 압도하려는 그들의 경계심과는 아무런 상관이 없었다."]], [["While Mrs Plornish spoke in these words of her absent lord, her lord returned.", "플로니시 부인이 자기 자리에 없는 남편에 관해 이런 말을 하고 있는 동안, 그 남편이 돌아왔다."], ["A smooth-cheeked, fresh-coloured, sandy-whiskered man of thirty.", "볼이 매끈하고 혈색이 좋으며 모래빛 구레나룻을 기른 서른 살의 사내였다."], ["Long in the legs, yielding at the knees, foolish in the face, flannel-jacketed, lime-whitened.", "다리는 길고, 무릎은 잘 휘청거리고, 얼굴은 어리숙하고, 플란넬 윗옷을 걸쳤으며, 회반죽으로 허옇게 물들어 있었다."]], [["‘This is Plornish, sir.’", "\"이 사람이 플로니시예요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘I came,’ said Clennam, rising, ‘to beg the favour of a little conversation with you on the subject of the Dorrit family.’", "\"제가 찾아온 것은,\" 클레넘이 일어서며 말했다. \"도릿 가족에 관해서 잠깐 이야기를 나누는 호의를 부탁드리고 싶어서입니다.\""]], [["Plornish became suspicious.", "플로니시는 의심스러워졌다."], ["Seemed to scent a creditor.", "채권자의 냄새를 맡은 듯했다."], ["Said, ‘Ah, yes.", "그가 말했다. \"아, 예."], ["Well.", "그렇군요."], ["He didn’t know what satisfaction _he_ could give any gentleman, respecting that family.", "그 가족에 관해서 내가 어느 신사분께 무슨 흡족한 답을 드릴 수 있을지 모르겠는데요."], ["What might it be about, now?’", "그게 무슨 일로 그러시는데요?\""]], [["‘I know you better,’ said Clennam, smiling, ‘than you suppose.’", "\"제가 당신을 당신 짐작보다 더 잘 알고 있습니다,\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Plornish observed, not smiling in return, And yet he hadn’t the pleasure of being acquainted with the gentleman, neither.", "플로니시는 답례로 미소 짓지 않은 채, 그래도 자기는 이 신사분과 안면을 트는 영광은 누리지 못했다고 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ said Arthur, ‘I know your kind offices at second hand, but on the best authority; through Little Dorrit.--I mean,’ he explained, ‘Miss Dorrit.’", "\"그렇지요,\" 아서가 말했다. \"당신의 친절한 행적을 저는 건너서 들었습니다만, 더없이 믿을 만한 출처를 통해서지요. 리틀 도릿을 통해서요. — 제 말은,\" 그가 설명을 덧붙였다. \"도릿 양 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam, is it?", "\"클레넘 씨, 맞으시죠?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["I’ve heard of you, Sir.’", "선생님 말씀은 들었습니다.\""]], [["‘And I of you,’ said Arthur.", "\"저도 당신 말을 들었습니다,\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Please to sit down again, Sir, and consider yourself welcome.--Why, yes,’ said Plornish, taking a chair, and lifting the elder child upon his knee, that he might have the moral support of speaking to a stranger over his head, ‘I have been on the wrong side of the Lock myself, and in that way we come to know Miss Dorrit. Me and my wife, we are well acquainted with Miss Dorrit.’", "\"다시 앉으시지요, 선생님, 환영합니다. — 그러니까, 예,\" 플로니시가 의자를 끌어다 앉고는, 큰아이를 무릎 위에 올려 그 아이 머리 너머로 낯선 이에게 말하는 정신적 지지를 얻으며 말했다. \"저도 한때 자물쇠의 잘못된 쪽에 있어 봤지요, 그렇게 해서 도릿 양을 알게 된 겁니다. 저와 제 아내는 도릿 양을 아주 잘 알지요.\""]], [["‘Intimate!’ cried Mrs Plornish.", "\"막역한 사이예요!\""], ["Indeed, she was so proud of the acquaintance, that she had awakened some bitterness of spirit in the Yard by magnifying to an enormous amount the sum for which Miss Dorrit’s father had become insolvent.", "플로니시 부인이 외쳤다. 사실 그녀는 그 친분을 어찌나 자랑스러워했던지, 도릿 양의 아버지가 파산할 때 진 빚의 액수를 엄청나게 부풀려 떠벌리는 바람에 마당 사람들의 마음에 약간의 씁쓸함을 불러일으키기도 했다."], ["The Bleeding Hearts resented her claiming to know people of such distinction.", "피 흘리는 심장 마당 사람들은 그녀가 그토록 지체 높은 사람들을 안다고 떠벌리는 것을 못마땅해했다."]], [["‘It was her father that I got acquainted with first.", "\"제가 먼저 알게 된 건 그분 아버지였지요."], ["And through getting acquainted with him, you see--why--I got acquainted with her,’ said Plornish tautologically.", "그리고 그분과 알고 지내게 되면서, 그러니까 — 음 — 그분의 따님과도 알게 된 겁니다,\" 플로니시가 같은 말을 되풀이하듯 말했다."]], [["‘I see.’", "\"그렇군요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["And there’s manners!", "그분 예절이라니!"], ["There’s polish!", "그 품위라니!"], ["There’s a gentleman to have run to seed in the Marshalsea jail!", "마셜시 감옥에서 시들어 버리기에는 참으로 아까운 신사분이지요! 그런데, 어쩌면 모르실지도 모르겠는데,\""], ["Why, perhaps you are not aware,’ said Plornish, lowering his voice, and speaking with a perverse admiration of what he ought to have pitied or despised, ‘not aware that Miss Dorrit and her sister dursn’t let him know that they work for a living.", "플로니시가 목소리를 낮추고, 가엾이 여기거나 업신여겨야 마땅할 일을 도리어 비뚤어진 감탄으로 말했다. \"도릿 양과 그 여동생이 생계를 위해 일을 한다는 걸 그분께는 차마 알리지도 못한다는 걸 모르실지도 몰라요."], ["No!’ said Plornish, looking with a ridiculous triumph first at his wife, and then all round the room.", "그럼요!\" 플로니시가 먼저 아내를, 그다음에는 방 안 전체를 우스꽝스러운 의기양양함으로 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Dursn’t let him know it, they dursn’t!’", "\"그분께는 차마 알리지도 못하는 거예요, 차마 못 하는 거라고요!\""]], [["‘Without admiring him for that,’ Clennam quietly observed, ‘I am very sorry for him.’ The remark appeared to suggest to Plornish, for the first time, that it might not be a very fine trait of character after all. He pondered about it for a moment, and gave it up.", "\"그 점을 감탄할 만한 일로 보지는 않습니다만,\" 클레넘이 차분하게 말했다. \"그분이 무척 안쓰럽군요.\" 이 말은 플로니시에게, 그것이 결국 그리 훌륭한 인격의 단면은 아닐지도 모른다는 생각을 처음으로 일깨워 준 듯했다. 그는 잠시 그것을 곰곰이 생각하다가, 이내 포기했다."]], [["‘As to me,’ he resumed, ‘certainly Mr Dorrit is as affable with me, I am sure, as I can possibly expect.", "\"제 경우로 말하자면,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["Considering the differences and distances betwixt us, more so.", "\"도릿 씨는 제게 정말이지 제가 기대할 수 있는 만큼은 다정하게 대해 주신답니다. 우리 사이의 차이와 거리를 생각하면 그 이상이지요."], ["But it’s Miss Dorrit that we were speaking of.’", "하지만 우리가 얘기하던 건 도릿 양이었잖습니까.\""]], [["‘True.", "\"그렇지요."], ["Pray how did you introduce her at my mother’s!’", "그런데 그분을 어떻게 제 어머니 댁에 소개하게 되었는지요!\""]], [["Mr Plornish picked a bit of lime out of his whisker, put it between his lips, turned it with his tongue like a sugar-plum, considered, found himself unequal to the task of lucid explanation, and appealing to his wife, said, ‘Sally, _you_ may as well mention how it was, old woman.’", "플로니시 씨는 구레나룻에서 회반죽 한 조각을 떼어 내어 입술 사이에 넣고는, 사탕 알맹이라도 되는 양 혀로 굴리며 곰곰 생각하더니, 명료하게 설명할 능력이 없음을 깨닫고는, 아내에게 도움을 청하며 말했다. \"샐리, 자네가 어찌 된 일인지 얘기해 주는 게 낫겠어, 여보.\""]], [["‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Sally, hushing the baby from side to side, and laying her chin upon the little hand as it tried to disarrange the gown again, ‘came here one afternoon with a bit of writing, telling that how she wished for needlework, and asked if it would be considered any ill-conwenience in case she was to give her address here.’ (Plornish repeated, her address here, in a low voice, as if he were making responses at church.) ‘Me and Plornish says, No, Miss Dorrit, no ill-conwenience,’ (Plornish repeated, no ill-conwenience,) ‘and she wrote it in, according. Which then me and Plornish says, Ho Miss Dorrit!’ (Plornish repeated, Ho Miss Dorrit.) ‘Have you thought of copying it three or four times, as the way to make it known in more places than one? No, says Miss Dorrit, I have not, but I will. She copied it out according, on this table, in a sweet writing, and Plornish, he took it where he worked, having a job just then,’ (Plornish repeated job just then,) ‘and likewise to the landlord of the Yard; through which it was that Mrs Clennam first happened to employ Miss Dorrit.’ Plornish repeated, employ Miss Dorrit; and Mrs Plornish having come to an end, feigned to bite the fingers of the little hand as she kissed it.", "\"도릿 양이,\" 샐리가 아기를 좌우로 흔들어 어르며, 또다시 가운을 헝클어뜨리려 드는 그 작은 손에 턱을 얹은 채 말했다. \"어느 날 오후에 글이 적힌 쪽지 하나를 들고 여기로 왔어요, 바느질 일감을 원한다면서, 혹시 여기 주소를 적어 놔도 폐가 되지 않겠느냐고 물었지요.\" (플로니시는 마치 교회에서 응답이라도 하듯 낮은 목소리로 \"여기 주소를\"이라고 따라 했다.) \"그래서 저랑 플로니시가, 아니에요, 도릿 양, 폐가 안 돼요, 했지요,\" (플로니시가 \"폐가 안 돼요\"라고 따라 했다.) \"그래서 그분이 거기에 그대로 적어 넣었어요. 그러고 나서 저랑 플로니시가, 어머나 도릿 양!\" (플로니시가 \"어머나 도릿 양\"이라고 따라 했다.) \"한 군데보다 더 여러 군데에 알리는 방법으로, 그걸 서너 번 베껴 두면 어떻겠느냐고 했지요. 아니에요, 그런 생각은 못 했네요, 하지만 그렇게 할게요, 하고 도릿 양이 말했어요. 그러고는 거기에 맞춰 이 탁자 위에서 고운 글씨로 베껴 냈고, 플로니시는 마침 그때 일거리가 있어서 그걸 일하는 데로 가져갔고,\" (플로니시가 \"마침 그때 일거리\"라고 따라 했다.) \"또 마당 집주인한테도 가져갔지요. 그렇게 해서 클레넘 부인이 처음으로 도릿 양에게 일을 맡기게 된 거랍니다.\" 플로니시가 \"도릿 양에게 일을 맡기게\"라고 따라 했고, 이야기를 마친 플로니시 부인은 그 작은 손에 입을 맞추며 손가락을 깨무는 시늉을 했다."]], [["‘The landlord of the Yard,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘is--’", "\"마당 집주인이라면,\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다. \"그분은 —\""]], [["‘He is Mr Casby, by name, he is,’ said Plornish, ‘and Pancks, he collects the rents. That,’ added Mr Plornish, dwelling on the subject with a slow thoughtfulness that appeared to have no connection with any specific object, and to lead him nowhere, ‘that is about what _they_ are, you may believe me or not, as you think proper.’", "\"이름이 캐스비 씨예요, 그 사람이,\" 플로니시가 말했다. \"그리고 팽크스라는 사람이 집세를 걷고요. 그게,\" 플로니시 씨가 어떤 특정한 목적과도 연결되지 않고 어디로도 이어지지 않는 듯한 느릿한 사색으로 그 주제에 머물며 덧붙였다. \"그게 그 사람들 사정이라 할 만해요, 믿으시든 말든 좋으실 대로요.\""]], [["‘Ay?’ returned Clennam, thoughtful in his turn.", "\"그래요?\" 클레넘이 이번에는 자기가 생각에 잠겨 대꾸했다."], ["‘Mr Casby, too!", "\"캐스비 씨라니!"], ["An old acquaintance of mine, long ago!’", "그것도 오래전에 알던 옛 지인이군요!\""]], [["Mr Plornish did not see his road to any comment on this fact, and made none. As there truly was no reason why he should have the least interest in it, Arthur Clennam went on to the present purport of his visit; namely, to make Plornish the instrument of effecting Tip’s release, with as little detriment as possible to the self-reliance and self-helpfulness of the young man, supposing him to possess any remnant of those qualities: without doubt a very wide stretch of supposition. Plornish, having been made acquainted with the cause of action from the Defendant’s own mouth, gave Arthur to understand that the Plaintiff was a ‘Chaunter’--meaning, not a singer of anthems, but a seller of horses--and that he (Plornish) considered that ten shillings in the pound ‘would settle handsome,’ and that more would be a waste of money. The Principal and instrument soon drove off together to a stable-yard in High Holborn, where a remarkably fine grey gelding, worth, at the lowest figure, seventy-five guineas (not taking into account the value of the shot he had been made to swallow for the improvement of his form), was to be parted with for a twenty-pound note, in consequence of his having run away last week with Mrs Captain Barbary of Cheltenham, who wasn’t up to a horse of his courage, and who, in mere spite, insisted on selling him for that ridiculous sum: or, in other words, on giving him away. Plornish, going up this yard alone and leaving his Principal outside, found a gentleman with tight drab legs, a rather old hat, a little hooked stick, and a blue neckerchief (Captain Maroon of Gloucestershire, a private friend of Captain Barbary); who happened to be there, in a friendly way, to mention these little circumstances concerning the remarkably fine grey gelding to any real judge of a horse and quick snapper-up of a good thing, who might look in at that address as per advertisement. This gentleman, happening also to be the Plaintiff in the Tip case, referred Mr Plornish to his solicitor, and declined to treat with Mr Plornish, or even to endure his presence in the yard, unless he appeared there with a twenty-pound note: in which case only, the gentleman would augur from appearances that he meant business, and might be induced to talk to him. On this hint, Mr Plornish retired to communicate with his Principal, and presently came back with the required credentials. Then said Captain Maroon, ‘Now, how much time do you want to make the other twenty in? Now, I’ll give you a month.’ Then said Captain Maroon, when that wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, I’ll tell what I’ll do with you. You shall get me a good bill at four months, made payable at a banking-house, for the other twenty!’ Then said Captain Maroon, when _that_ wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, come; Here’s the last I’ve got to say to you. You shall give me another ten down, and I’ll run my pen clean through it.’ Then said Captain Maroon when _that_ wouldn’t suit, ‘Now, I’ll tell you what it is, and this shuts it up; he has used me bad, but I’ll let him off for another five down and a bottle of wine; and if you mean done, say done, and if you don’t like it, leave it.’ Finally said Captain Maroon, when _that_ wouldn’t suit either, ‘Hand over, then!’--And in consideration of the first offer, gave a receipt in full and discharged the prisoner.", "플로니시 씨는 이 사실에 대해 어떤 토를 달 길도 찾지 못했고, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 사실 그가 거기에 조금이라도 관심을 가질 이유가 전혀 없었으므로, 아서 클레넘은 이번 방문의 본래 용건으로 넘어갔다. 곧, 만일 그 젊은이에게 자립심과 자조심의 잔재가 조금이라도 남아 있다고 가정한다면 — 의심할 바 없이 매우 과한 가정이긴 했지만 — 그 자립심과 자조심을 되도록 해치지 않으면서 팁의 석방을 성사시키는 도구로 플로니시를 쓰는 일이었다. 피고 본인의 입에서 소송의 사유를 전해 들은 플로니시는, 원고가 '찬터'라는 것을 — 즉, 성가를 부르는 사람이 아니라 말을 파는 사람이라는 것을 — 아서에게 일러 주었고, 자기(플로니시)가 보기에는 1파운드당 10실링이면 '깔끔하게 마무리될' 것이며 그 이상은 돈 낭비라고 했다. 의뢰인과 그 도구는 곧 함께 마차를 몰아 하이 홀번의 어느 마구간 마당으로 갔다. 그곳에는 최저로 잡아도 75기니는 나가는(체형을 보기 좋게 만들려고 삼키게 한 산탄의 값어치는 셈에 넣지 않더라도) 유난히 훌륭한 회색 거세마 한 마리가 있었는데, 지난주에 첼튼엄의 바버리 대위 부인을 태우고 달아나 버린 탓에 단돈 20파운드 지폐에 처분될 참이었다. 그 부인은 이 말의 혈기를 감당할 깜냥이 못 되었고, 순전히 앙심에서 그 우스꽝스러운 값에 팔아 치우겠다고 — 다시 말해 거저 줘 버리겠다고 — 고집을 부린 것이었다. 플로니시는 의뢰인을 밖에 남겨 둔 채 혼자 그 마당으로 올라갔는데, 거기서 꽉 끼는 황갈색 바지에 다소 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 자그마한 갈고리 지팡이를 들고, 파란 목도리를 두른 한 신사(글로스터셔의 머룬 대위로, 바버리 대위의 절친한 친구였다)를 만났다. 이 신사는 마침, 광고를 보고 그 주소를 들여다볼 법한, 말을 제대로 볼 줄 알고 좋은 물건을 잽싸게 낚아챌 안목을 지닌 진짜 감정가에게, 그 유난히 훌륭한 회색 거세마에 관한 이런 자잘한 사정들을 친절히 일러 주려고 그 자리에 있었던 것이다. 그런데 이 신사가 마침 팁 사건의 원고이기도 했던 터라, 그는 플로니시 씨를 자기 변호사에게 가 보라고 떠넘겼고, 플로니시 씨가 20파운드 지폐를 들고 나타나지 않는 한 그와 거래하기는커녕 마당에 있는 꼴조차 견디지 못하겠다고 했다. 오직 그런 경우라야만, 그 신사는 겉모습으로 미루어 플로니시가 진지하게 거래에 임할 작정임을 점쳐 보고, 그와 이야기를 나눠 볼 마음이 생길 수도 있다는 것이었다. 이 언질에 플로니시 씨는 의뢰인과 상의하러 물러났다가, 곧 요구받은 증표를 들고 돌아왔다. 그러자 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 나머지 20파운드를 마련하는 데 시간이 얼마나 필요하시오? 자, 한 달 드리리다.\" 그러고는 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 그럼 어떻게 해 드리리까. 나머지 20파운드에 대해, 은행에서 지급되는 넉 달짜리 좋은 어음을 하나 끊어 주시오!\" 그러고도 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 이리 와 보시오. 이게 내 마지막 말이오. 지금 10파운드를 더 내놓으면, 내 펜으로 깨끗이 그어 버리리다.\" 그러고도 그게 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 말했다. \"자, 이렇게 말해 두겠소, 이걸로 끝장이오. 그자가 날 험하게 대하긴 했지만, 지금 5파운드를 더 내고 포도주 한 병만 주면 놔주리다. 거래할 셈이면 한다고 하고, 마음에 안 들면 관두시오.\" 그러고도 그것마저 안 맞자, 머룬 대위가 마침내 말했다. \"그럼, 이리 내놓으시오!\" — 그러고는 처음 제안한 액수를 받고 영수증을 끊어 죄수를 풀어 주었다."]], [["‘Mr Plornish,’ said Arthur, ‘I trust to you, if you please, to keep my secret.", "\"플로니시 씨,\" 아서가 말했다."], ["If you will undertake to let the young man know that he is free, and to tell him that you were employed to compound for the debt by some one whom you are not at liberty to name, you will not only do me a service, but may do him one, and his sister also.’", "\"부디 제 비밀을 지켜 주시리라 믿겠습니다. 그 젊은이에게 그가 자유의 몸이 되었음을 알려 주고, 당신이 이름을 밝힐 수 없는 어떤 사람으로부터 빚을 청산하라는 부탁을 받았다고 전해 주신다면, 제게 도움을 주실 뿐 아니라, 그에게도, 또 그의 누이에게도 도움을 주시는 게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["‘The last reason, sir,’ said Plornish, ‘would be quite sufficient.", "\"마지막 이유만으로도, 선생님,\" 플로니시가 말했다."], ["Your wishes shall be attended to.’", "\"충분합니다. 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘A Friend has obtained his discharge, you can say if you please.", "\"어느 벗이 그의 석방을 얻어 냈다고, 원하신다면 말씀하셔도 됩니다."], ["A Friend who hopes that for his sister’s sake, if for no one else’s, he will make good use of his liberty.’", "다른 누구를 위해서가 아니더라도 누이를 위해서, 그가 그 자유를 잘 활용하기를 바라는 어느 벗이라고요.\""]], [["‘Your wishes, sir, shall be attended to.’", "\"분부대로 따르겠습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘And if you will be so good, in your better knowledge of the family, as to communicate freely with me, and to point out to me any means by which you think I may be delicately and really useful to Little Dorrit, I shall feel under an obligation to you.’", "\"그리고 한 가지만 더 베풀어 주신다면, 그 가족을 더 잘 아시는 만큼, 제게 거리낌 없이 소식을 전해 주시고, 제가 리틀 도릿에게 조심스럽고 진정으로 도움이 될 만한 방법이 있다면 일러 주시기 바랍니다. 그래 주시면 신세를 진 것으로 알겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Don’t name it, sir,’ returned Plornish, ‘it’ll be ekally a pleasure an a--it’l be ekally a pleasure and a--’ Finding himself unable to balance his sentence after two efforts, Mr Plornish wisely dropped it. He took Clennam’s card and appropriate pecuniary compliment.", "\"그런 말씀 마세요, 선생님,\" 플로니시가 대답했다. \"그건 똑같이 기쁨이자 또 — 그건 똑같이 기쁨이자 또 —\" 두 번이나 애써 본 끝에도 문장의 균형을 맞추지 못하자, 플로니시 씨는 현명하게도 그것을 그만두었다. 그는 클레넘의 명함과 적당한 사례금을 받아들였다."]], [["He was earnest to finish his commission at once, and his Principal was in the same mind.", "그는 자기 임무를 당장 끝내려고 안달이었고, 의뢰인도 같은 마음이었다."], ["So his Principal offered to set him down at the Marshalsea Gate, and they drove in that direction over Blackfriars Bridge.", "그래서 의뢰인은 그를 마셜시 정문에 내려 주겠다고 했고, 두 사람은 블랙프라이어스 다리를 건너 그쪽으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["On the way, Arthur elicited from his new friend a confused summary of the interior life of Bleeding Heart Yard.", "가는 길에 아서는 새로 사귄 벗에게서 피 흘리는 심장 마당의 안살림에 관한 두서없는 요약을 끌어냈다."], ["They was all hard up there, Mr Plornish said, uncommon hard up, to be sure.", "거기 사람들은 다들 쪼들린다고, 정말이지 유난히 쪼들린다고 플로니시 씨는 말했다."], ["Well, he couldn’t say how it was; he didn’t know as anybody _could_ say how it was; all he know’d was, that so it was.", "글쎄, 어찌 된 일인지는 자기도 모르겠고, 누가 어찌 된 일인지를 안다고도 생각지 않으며, 자기가 아는 거라곤 그저 사정이 그렇다는 것뿐이었다."], ["When a man felt, on his own back and in his own belly, that poor he was, that man (Mr Plornish gave it as his decided belief) know’d well that he was poor somehow or another, and you couldn’t talk it out of him, no more than you could talk Beef into him.", "사람이 제 등으로, 제 뱃속으로 제가 가난하다는 걸 느낄 때, 그 사람은(플로니시 씨는 이것을 확고한 신념으로 내놓았다) 어찌 됐든 자기가 가난하다는 걸 잘 알고 있으며, 그걸 말로 떼어 낼 수 없는 건 그에게 쇠고기를 말로 먹일 수 없는 것과 매한가지였다."], ["Then you see, some people as was better off said, and a good many such people lived pretty close up to the mark themselves if not beyond it so he’d heerd, that they was ‘improvident’ (that was the favourite word) down the Yard.", "그런데 보다시피, 형편이 좀 나은 어떤 사람들은 — 그런 사람들 가운데 상당수도 제 처지가 거기 사람들 못지않게 빠듯하거나 그보다 더한 경우도 있다고 들었다지만 — 마당 사람들이 '앞날을 내다보지 못한다'고('내다보지 못한다'가 그들이 즐겨 쓰는 말이었다) 말했다."], ["For instance, if they see a man with his wife and children going to Hampton Court in a Wan, perhaps once in a year, they says, ‘Hallo!", "이를테면 어떤 사내가 아내와 아이들을 데리고 짐마차를 타고 햄프턴 코트로 가는 걸 보면, 일 년에 한 번쯤 있는 일인데도, 그들은 \"아니! 자네 가난하다더니, 앞날도 못 내다보는 친구 같으니!\""], ["I thought you was poor, my improvident friend!’ Why, Lord, how hard it was upon a man!", "했다. 아니, 세상에, 그게 사람한테 얼마나 모진 일이냐는 것이다!"], ["What was a man to do?", "사람이 어찌하란 말인가?"], ["He couldn’t go mollancholy mad, and even if he did, you wouldn’t be the better for it.", "미쳐서 우울에 빠질 수도 없고, 설령 그런다 한들 그들에게 득 될 것도 없었다."], ["In Mr Plornish’s judgment you would be the worse for it.", "플로니시 씨가 판단하기로는 도리어 손해였다."], ["Yet you seemed to want to make a man mollancholy mad.", "그런데도 그들은 사람을 미쳐서 우울에 빠지게 만들려는 듯했다."], ["You was always at it--if not with your right hand, with your left.", "늘 그러고 있었다 — 오른손으로가 아니면 왼손으로라도."], ["What was they a doing in the Yard?", "마당 사람들이 무얼 하고 있느냐고?"], ["Why, take a look at ‘em and see.", "직접 한번 그들을 들여다보면 안다는 것이다."], ["There was the girls and their mothers a working at their sewing, or their shoe-binding, or their trimming, or their waistcoat making, day and night and night and day, and not more than able to keep body and soul together after all--often not so much.", "처녀들과 그 어머니들이 바느질이며, 구두 꿰매기며, 단 장식이며, 조끼 짓기에 매달려, 밤낮으로 또 밤낮으로 일하고도, 결국 입에 풀칠이나 겨우 할 정도였다 — 흔히 그조차 못 했다."], ["There was people of pretty well all sorts of trades you could name, all wanting to work, and yet not able to get it.", "댈 수 있는 거의 온갖 직종의 사람들이 다 있었고, 다들 일하고 싶어 했지만, 그래도 일을 얻지 못했다."], ["There was old people, after working all their lives, going and being shut up in the workhouse, much worse fed and lodged and treated altogether, than--Mr Plornish said manufacturers, but appeared to mean malefactors.", "평생을 일한 끝에, 구빈원에 들어가 갇혀 지내는 노인들도 있었는데, 그곳에서의 먹거리와 잠자리와 대접은 통틀어 — 플로니시 씨는 '제조업자'라고 말했지만, 아무래도 '범죄자'를 가리킨 듯했다 — 그들보다도 훨씬 못했다."], ["Why, a man didn’t know where to turn himself for a crumb of comfort.", "아니, 사람이 위안의 부스러기 하나를 찾아 어디로 발길을 돌려야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["As to who was to blame for it, Mr Plornish didn’t know who was to blame for it.", "그게 누구 탓이냐고 하면, 플로니시 씨는 그게 누구 탓인지 알지 못했다."], ["He could tell you who suffered, but he couldn’t tell you whose fault it was.", "그는 누가 고통받는지는 말할 수 있어도, 그게 누구 잘못인지는 말할 수 없었다."], ["It wasn’t _his_ place to find out, and who’d mind what he said, if he did find out?", "그걸 알아내는 게 자기가 할 일도 아니었고, 설령 알아낸들 자기 말에 누가 귀를 기울이겠는가?"], ["He only know’d that it wasn’t put right by them what undertook that line of business, and that it didn’t come right of itself.", "그가 아는 거라곤, 그 일을 맡은 자들이 그걸 바로잡지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그것이 저절로 바로잡히지도 않았다는 것뿐이었다."], ["And, in brief, his illogical opinion was, that if you couldn’t do nothing for him, you had better take nothing from him for doing of it; so far as he could make out, that was about what it come to.", "한마디로, 그의 비논리적인 의견은, 사람을 위해 아무것도 해 줄 수 없다면 차라리 그 일을 해 준답시고 그에게서 아무것도 받아 가지 않는 편이 낫다는 것이었다. 그가 헤아릴 수 있는 한, 결국 그쯤으로 귀결되는 이야기였다."], ["Thus, in a prolix, gently-growling, foolish way, did Plornish turn the tangled skein of his estate about and about, like a blind man who was trying to find some beginning or end to it; until they reached the prison gate.", "이렇듯 장황하고, 점잖게 투덜대는, 어리숙한 방식으로 플로니시는 마치 시작이나 끝을 더듬어 찾으려는 눈먼 사람처럼, 제 처지의 엉킨 실타래를 이리저리 돌려 댔고, 그러는 사이 그들은 감옥 정문에 다다랐다."], ["There, he left his Principal alone; to wonder, as he rode away, how many thousand Plornishes there might be within a day or two’s journey of the Circumlocution Office, playing sundry curious variations on the same tune, which were not known by ear in that glorious institution.", "거기서 그는 의뢰인을 홀로 남겨 두었고, 의뢰인은 마차를 몰아 떠나면서, 에두름국에서 하루 이틀 거리 안에 도대체 얼마나 많은 플로니시들이 있어, 그 영광스러운 기관에서는 귀로 알아듣지도 못하는 똑같은 곡조에 갖가지 묘한 변주를 붙이고 있을지를 궁금해했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 13. Patriarchal", "# CHAPTER 13. Patriarchal"]], [["The mention of Mr Casby again revived in Clennam’s memory the smouldering embers of curiosity and interest which Mrs Flintwinch had fanned on the night of his arrival.", "캐스비 씨의 이름이 다시 나오자, 도착하던 날 밤 플린트윈치 부인이 부채질해 놓은 호기심과 관심의 꺼져 가던 불씨가 클레넘의 기억 속에서 되살아났다. 플로라 캐스비는 그의 소년 시절에 사랑했던 여인이었다."], ["Flora Casby had been the beloved of his boyhood; and Flora was the daughter and only child of wooden-headed old Christopher (so he was still occasionally spoken of by some irreverent spirits who had had dealings with him, and in whom familiarity had bred its proverbial result perhaps), who was reputed to be rich in weekly tenants, and to get a good quantity of blood out of the stones of several unpromising courts and alleys.", "그리고 플로라는 머리가 나무토막 같은 늙은 크리스토퍼(그와 거래를 해 본 어떤 불경한 자들은 아직도 이따금 그를 그렇게 불렀는데, 친숙함이 그 속담대로의 결과를 낳았던 모양이다)의 딸이자 외동이었다. 그는 주간 임차인을 많이 거느려 부유하다는 평판이 있었고, 별 가망 없어 보이는 여러 골목과 뒷마당의 돌멩이에서 적잖은 피를 짜낸다는 말을 들었다."]], [["After some days of inquiry and research, Arthur Clennam became convinced that the case of the Father of the Marshalsea was indeed a hopeless one, and sorrowfully resigned the idea of helping him to freedom again.", "며칠간 묻고 알아본 끝에, 아서 클레넘은 마셜시의 아버지의 사정이 정말로 가망 없는 것임을 확신하게 되었고, 다시 그를 자유의 몸으로 만들어 주겠다는 생각을 슬프게 단념했다."], ["He had no hopeful inquiry to make at present, concerning Little Dorrit either; but he argued with himself that it might--for anything he knew--it might be serviceable to the poor child, if he renewed this acquaintance.", "지금으로서는 리틀 도릿에 관해서도 희망적인 문의를 할 만한 것이 없었다. 그러나 그는 이 친분을 다시 잇는다면 — 그가 아는 한으로는 — 그 가엾은 아이에게 도움이 될지도 모른다고 스스로에게 변론했다."], ["It is hardly necessary to add that beyond all doubt he would have presented himself at Mr Casby’s door, if there had been no Little Dorrit in existence; for we all know how we all deceive ourselves--that is to say, how people in general, our profounder selves excepted, deceive themselves--as to motives of action.", "굳이 덧붙이자면, 리틀 도릿이라는 존재가 아예 없었다 하더라도 그가 캐스비 씨의 집 문 앞에 나타났으리라는 것은 의심의 여지가 없다. 우리 모두가 행동의 동기에 관하여 스스로를 어떻게 속이는지 — 다시 말해, 우리의 더 깊은 자아를 제외한 일반적인 사람들이 스스로를 어떻게 속이는지 — 우리 모두 알고 있으니 말이다."]], [["With a comfortable impression upon him, and quite an honest one in its way, that he was still patronising Little Dorrit in doing what had no reference to her, he found himself one afternoon at the corner of Mr Casby’s street.", "그녀와는 아무 상관도 없는 일을 하면서도 자신이 여전히 리틀 도릿을 후원하고 있는 것이라는, 그 나름으로는 꽤 정직한 편안한 인상을 품은 채, 그는 어느 오후 캐스비 씨의 거리 모퉁이에 서 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Mr Casby lived in a street in the Gray’s Inn Road, which had set off from that thoroughfare with the intention of running at one heat down into the valley, and up again to the top of Pentonville Hill; but which had run itself out of breath in twenty yards, and had stood still ever since.", "캐스비 씨는 그레이즈 인 로드에 있는 어느 거리에 살았는데, 그 거리는 큰길에서 갈라져 나와 단숨에 골짜기로 내려갔다가 다시 펜턴빌 언덕 꼭대기까지 치달아 오르려는 작정으로 출발했지만, 스무 야드 만에 숨이 차 멈춰 서서는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 자리에 서 있었다."], ["There is no such place in that part now; but it remained there for many years, looking with a baulked countenance at the wilderness patched with unfruitful gardens and pimpled with eruptive summerhouses, that it had meant to run over in no time.", "지금은 그 일대에 그런 곳이 없지만, 그 거리는 여러 해 동안 거기 남아, 단숨에 가로질러 버리려 했던 그 황무지 — 열매 맺지 못하는 텃밭으로 군데군데 기워지고 발진처럼 돋아난 여름 별장들로 오톨도톨한 그 황무지를 좌절한 얼굴로 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘The house,’ thought Clennam, as he crossed to the door, ‘is as little changed as my mother’s, and looks almost as gloomy.", "‘저 집은,’ 클레넘은 문 쪽으로 길을 건너며 생각했다, ‘우리 어머니 집만큼이나 거의 변하지 않았고, 그만큼이나 음울해 보이는군."], ["But the likeness ends outside.", "하지만 닮은 것은 겉뿐이야."], ["I know its staid repose within.", "나는 저 안의 차분한 정적을 알고 있어."], ["The smell of its jars of old rose-leaves and lavender seems to come upon me even here.’", "묵은 장미 잎과 라벤더를 담은 항아리들의 냄새가 여기까지 풍겨 오는 것만 같아.’"]], [["When his knock at the bright brass knocker of obsolete shape brought a woman-servant to the door, those faded scents in truth saluted him like wintry breath that had a faint remembrance in it of the bygone spring.", "낡은 형태의 반짝이는 놋쇠 문고리를 그가 두드리자 하녀 하나가 문으로 나왔는데, 그 빛바랜 향기는 정말로 지나간 봄에 대한 희미한 기억을 머금은 겨울 입김처럼 그를 맞이했다."], ["He stepped into the sober, silent, air-tight house--one might have fancied it to have been stifled by Mutes in the Eastern manner--and the door, closing again, seemed to shut out sound and motion.", "그는 진중하고, 고요하고, 공기 한 점 새지 않는 그 집으로 발을 들였다 — 동방의 방식으로 벙어리 종복들이 목 졸라 숨통을 막아 놓은 집이라고 상상해도 좋을 만한 집이었다 — 그리고 다시 닫히는 문은 소리와 움직임을 모조리 막아 버리는 듯했다."], ["The furniture was formal, grave, and quaker-like, but well-kept; and had as prepossessing an aspect as anything, from a human creature to a wooden stool, that is meant for much use and is preserved for little, can ever wear.", "가구들은 격식 차리고, 근엄하고, 퀘이커교도처럼 검소했지만 잘 관리되어 있었다. 그리고 인간이라는 존재에서 나무 의자에 이르기까지, 많이 쓰라고 만들어졌으나 거의 쓰이지 않은 채 고이 간직된 것이 자아낼 수 있는, 그 무엇 못지않게 호감 가는 자태를 띠고 있었다."], ["There was a grave clock, ticking somewhere up the staircase; and there was a songless bird in the same direction, pecking at his cage, as if he were ticking too.", "계단 위 어딘가에서 근엄한 시계가 똑딱이고 있었다. 같은 방향에서 노래하지 않는 새 한 마리가, 마치 저도 똑딱이듯 제 새장을 쪼고 있었다."], ["The parlour-fire ticked in the grate.", "응접실 난로의 불도 벽난로 안에서 똑딱였다."], ["There was only one person on the parlour-hearth, and the loud watch in his pocket ticked audibly.", "응접실 난롯가에는 단 한 사람이 있었고, 그 주머니 속의 요란한 회중시계가 또렷이 똑딱이고 있었다."]], [["The servant-maid had ticked the two words ‘Mr Clennam’ so softly that she had not been heard; and he consequently stood, within the door she had closed, unnoticed.", "하녀가 ‘클레넘 씨’라는 두 마디를 어찌나 나직이 똑딱여 놓았던지 그 소리가 들리지 않았고, 그래서 그는 하녀가 닫은 문 안쪽에 아무도 알아채지 못한 채 서 있었다."], ["The figure of a man advanced in life, whose smooth grey eyebrows seemed to move to the ticking as the fire-light flickered on them, sat in an arm-chair, with his list shoes on the rug, and his thumbs slowly revolving over one another.", "인생의 황혼에 접어든 한 남자의 형상이, 불빛이 그 위에서 일렁일 때마다 매끄러운 잿빛 눈썹이 똑딱임에 맞추어 움직이는 듯한 그 남자가, 펠트 신발을 신은 발을 깔개에 올린 채 안락의자에 앉아, 두 엄지손가락을 천천히 서로 돌리고 있었다."], ["This was old Christopher Casby--recognisable at a glance--as unchanged in twenty years and upward as his own solid furniture--as little touched by the influence of the varying seasons as the old rose-leaves and old lavender in his porcelain jars.", "이것이 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비였다 — 한눈에 알아볼 수 있었다 — 제 단단한 가구들만큼이나 이십 년이 넘도록 변하지 않은, 자기 도자기 항아리 속의 묵은 장미 잎과 묵은 라벤더만큼이나 계절의 변화에 닿지 않은 사람이었다."]], [["Perhaps there never was a man, in this troublesome world, so troublesome for the imagination to picture as a boy.", "아마 이 성가신 세상에, 소년 시절을 그려 보기가 이토록 상상력을 성가시게 하는 사람은 일찍이 없었을 것이다."], ["And yet he had changed very little in his progress through life.", "그런데도 그는 살아오는 동안 거의 변하지 않았다."], ["Confronting him, in the room in which he sat, was a boy’s portrait, which anybody seeing him would have identified as Master Christopher Casby, aged ten: though disguised with a haymaking rake, for which he had had, at any time, as much taste or use as for a diving-bell; and sitting (on one of his own legs) upon a bank of violets, moved to precocious contemplation by the spire of a village church.", "그가 앉아 있는 방에서 그를 마주 보고 있는 것은 한 소년의 초상화였는데, 그를 본 사람이라면 누구나 그것이 열 살 난 크리스토퍼 캐스비 도련님임을 알아보았을 것이다. 다만 건초 갈퀴를 들고 변장한 모습이었는데, 그 갈퀴에 대해 그는 잠수종에 대해서만큼의 취미나 쓸모밖에 가져 본 적이 없었다."], ["There was the same smooth face and forehead, the same calm blue eye, the same placid air.", "그리고 (제 다리 하나를 깔고 앉아) 제비꽃 둑에 앉아, 마을 교회의 첨탑에 조숙한 명상에 잠긴 모습이었다."], ["The shining bald head, which looked so very large because it shone so much; and the long grey hair at its sides and back, like floss silk or spun glass, which looked so very benevolent because it was never cut; were not, of course, to be seen in the boy as in the old man.", "거기에는 똑같이 매끄러운 얼굴과 이마, 똑같이 평온한 푸른 눈, 똑같이 차분한 분위기가 있었다. 너무 번쩍거려서 그토록 커 보이던 반짝이는 대머리, 그리고 결코 자르지 않아 그토록 자비로워 보이던, 명주실이나 유리솜 같은 옆머리와 뒷머리의 긴 잿빛 머리카락은, 물론 노인에게서처럼 소년에게서 볼 수 있는 것은 아니었다."], ["Nevertheless, in the Seraphic creature with the haymaking rake, were clearly to be discerned the rudiments of the Patriarch with the list shoes.", "그렇더라도 건초 갈퀴를 든 그 천사 같은 아이에게서, 펠트 신발을 신은 가부장의 싹은 분명히 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["Patriarch was the name which many people delighted to give him.", "가부장이란 많은 이들이 즐겨 그에게 붙여 준 이름이었다."], ["Various old ladies in the neighbourhood spoke of him as The Last of the Patriarchs.", "이웃의 여러 노부인들은 그를 마지막 가부장이라 불렀다."], ["So grey, so slow, so quiet, so impassionate, so very bumpy in the head, Patriarch was the word for him.", "그토록 잿빛이고, 그토록 느리고, 그토록 조용하고, 그토록 무덤덤하고, 머리가 그토록 울퉁불퉁한 그에게는 가부장이라는 말이 딱 맞았다."], ["He had been accosted in the streets, and respectfully solicited to become a Patriarch for painters and for sculptors; with so much importunity, in sooth, that it would appear to be beyond the Fine Arts to remember the points of a Patriarch, or to invent one.", "그는 거리에서 붙들려, 화가와 조각가의 가부장 모델이 되어 달라고 정중히 간청받기도 했다. 사실 어찌나 끈질겼던지, 가부장의 특징을 기억해 내거나 하나 지어내는 일이 미술의 능력 밖인 듯 보일 지경이었다."], ["Philanthropists of both sexes had asked who he was, and on being informed, ‘Old Christopher Casby, formerly Town-agent to Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle,’ had cried in a rapture of disappointment, ‘Oh!", "남녀 박애주의자들은 그가 누구냐고 물었고, ‘데시머스 타이트 배너클 경의 옛 도시 대리인이었던 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비’라는 대답을 듣고는 실망의 환희에 차서 외쳤다. ‘오!"], ["why, with that head, is he not a benefactor to his species!", "어찌하여 저런 머리를 가지고도 인류의 은인이 되지 못한단 말인가!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["why, with that head, is he not a father to the orphan and a friend to the friendless!’ With that head, however, he remained old Christopher Casby, proclaimed by common report rich in house property; and with that head, he now sat in his silent parlour.", "어찌하여 저런 머리를 가지고도 고아의 아버지가, 의지할 데 없는 자의 벗이 되지 못한단 말인가!’ 그러나 그런 머리를 가지고도 그는 여전히 늙은 크리스토퍼 캐스비, 세간의 평판으로 집세 재산이 많다는 그 사람으로 남아 있었다. 그리고 그런 머리를 하고서 그는 지금 자기 고요한 응접실에 앉아 있었다."], ["Indeed it would be the height of unreason to expect him to be sitting there without that head.", "사실 그가 그런 머리 없이 거기 앉아 있기를 기대하는 것이야말로 이만저만한 불합리가 아닐 것이다."]], [["Arthur Clennam moved to attract his attention, and the grey eyebrows turned towards him.", "아서 클레넘이 그의 주의를 끌려고 움직이자, 잿빛 눈썹이 그쪽으로 돌아왔다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ said Clennam, ‘I fear you did not hear me announced?’", "‘실례합니다.’ 클레넘이 말했다. ‘제가 들어온 것을 알리는 소리를 못 들으신 모양이군요?’"]], [["‘No, sir, I did not.", "‘예, 손님, 못 들었습니다."], ["Did you wish to see me, sir?’", "저를 보러 오셨습니까?’"]], [["‘I wished to pay my respects.’", "‘인사를 드리러 왔습니다.’"]], [["Mr Casby seemed a feather’s weight disappointed by the last words, having perhaps prepared himself for the visitor’s wishing to pay something else. ‘Have I the pleasure, sir,’ he proceeded--‘take a chair, if you please--have I the pleasure of knowing--?", "캐스비 씨는 마지막 말에 깃털 하나만큼 실망한 듯했는데, 어쩌면 손님이 다른 무언가를 치르고 싶어 하리라 마음의 준비를 하고 있었던 모양이다.* ‘제가, 손님,’ 그가 말을 이었다 — ‘부디 앉으시지요 — 제가 영광스럽게도 알고 지내는 분을 — ?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["truly, yes, I think I have!", "정말, 그렇군요, 그런 것 같습니다!"], ["I believe I am not mistaken in supposing that I am acquainted with those features?", "제가 저 이목구비를 아는 분이라고 짐작하는 것이 틀리지 않은 듯합니다."], ["I think I address a gentleman of whose return to this country I was informed by Mr Flintwinch?’", "플린트윈치 씨로부터 이 나라로 돌아오셨다는 소식을 들은 그 신사분을 마주하고 있는 것 같은데요?’\n* 인사(respects)를 드린다는 말을, 캐스비는 빚이나 돈을 치른다(pay)는 뜻으로 기대했다는 말장난."]], [["‘That is your present visitor.’", "‘바로 지금 찾아온 손님이 그렇습니다.’"]], [["‘Really!", "‘정말입니까!"], ["Mr Clennam?’", "클레넘 씨?’"]], [["‘No other, Mr Casby.’", "‘다름 아닌 그 사람입니다, 캐스비 씨.’"]], [["‘Mr Clennam, I am glad to see you.", "‘클레넘 씨, 만나서 반갑습니다."], ["How have you been since we met?’", "우리가 만난 이후로 어떻게 지내셨습니까?’"]], [["Without thinking it worth while to explain that in the course of some quarter of a century he had experienced occasional slight fluctuations in his health and spirits, Clennam answered generally that he had never been better, or something equally to the purpose; and shook hands with the possessor of ‘that head’ as it shed its patriarchal light upon him.", "지난 사반세기 동안 건강과 기분에 이따금 자잘한 기복을 겪었노라 설명할 가치까지는 없다고 여기며, 클레넘은 두루뭉술하게 더없이 잘 지냈다든가 그쯤 되는 말로 답하고는, 가부장다운 빛을 그에게 비추고 있는 ‘저 머리’의 임자와 악수를 나누었다."]], [["‘We are older, Mr Clennam,’ said Christopher Casby.", "‘우리도 나이를 먹었군요, 클레넘 씨.’ 크리스토퍼 캐스비가 말했다."]], [["‘We are--not younger,’ said Clennam.", "‘우리도 — 젊어지지는 않았지요.’ 클레넘이 말했다."], ["After this wise remark he felt that he was scarcely shining with brilliancy, and became aware that he was nervous.", "이런 현명한 발언을 해 놓고 그는 자신이 도무지 재치 있게 빛나지 못하고 있음을 느꼈고, 자기가 긴장하고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["‘And your respected father,’ said Mr Casby, ‘is no more!", "‘그리고 존경하는 춘부장께서는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘이제 안 계시군요!"], ["I was grieved to hear it, Mr Clennam, I was grieved.’", "그 소식을 듣고 비통했습니다, 클레넘 씨, 비통했어요.’"]], [["Arthur replied in the usual way that he felt infinitely obliged to him.", "아서는 더없이 감사하다는 통상적인 말로 답했다."]], [["‘There was a time,’ said Mr Casby, ‘when your parents and myself were not on friendly terms.", "‘한때는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘당신 부모님과 제가 친밀한 사이가 아니던 시절이 있었지요."], ["There was a little family misunderstanding among us.", "우리 사이에 사소한 집안 오해가 있었습니다."], ["Your respected mother was rather jealous of her son, maybe; when I say her son, I mean your worthy self, your worthy self.’", "존경하는 당신 어머님께서 아드님을 두고 다소 질투하셨던 모양입니다. 아드님이라 하면, 다름 아닌 훌륭하신 당신 본인, 당신 본인을 두고 하는 말입니다.’"]], [["His smooth face had a bloom upon it like ripe wall-fruit.", "그의 매끄러운 얼굴에는 잘 익은 담장 과실 같은 화색이 돌았다."], ["What with his blooming face, and that head, and his blue eyes, he seemed to be delivering sentiments of rare wisdom and virtue.", "그 화색 도는 얼굴과, 저 머리와, 푸른 눈이 어우러져, 그는 보기 드문 지혜와 덕을 담은 말을 전하고 있는 듯이 보였다."], ["In like manner, his physiognomical expression seemed to teem with benignity.", "마찬가지로 그의 인상은 자애로움으로 가득 차 넘치는 듯했다."], ["Nobody could have said where the wisdom was, or where the virtue was, or where the benignity was; but they all seemed to be somewhere about him.", "그 지혜가 어디 있는지, 그 덕이 어디 있는지, 그 자애가 어디 있는지 아무도 말할 수 없었으련만, 그것들은 모두 그의 어딘가에 깃들어 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["‘Those times, however,’ pursued Mr Casby, ‘are past and gone, past and gone.", "‘하지만 그런 시절은,’ 캐스비 씨가 말을 이었다, ‘지나갔고 떠나갔습니다, 지나갔고 떠나갔어요."], ["I do myself the pleasure of making a visit to your respected mother occasionally, and of admiring the fortitude and strength of mind with which she bears her trials, bears her trials.’", "저는 이따금 존경하는 당신 어머님을 방문하는 기쁨을 누리며, 그분이 시련을 견디시는, 시련을 견디시는 그 의연함과 정신력에 감탄하곤 합니다.’"]], [["When he made one of these little repetitions, sitting with his hands crossed before him, he did it with his head on one side, and a gentle smile, as if he had something in his thoughts too sweetly profound to be put into words.", "이렇게 짤막한 되풀이를 할 때면, 그는 두 손을 앞에 모으고 앉아, 머리를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 부드럽게 미소 지으며 그리했다. 마치 말로 옮기기엔 너무도 감미롭고 깊은 무언가를 마음속에 품고 있는 것처럼."], ["As if he denied himself the pleasure of uttering it, lest he should soar too high; and his meekness therefore preferred to be unmeaning.", "마치 너무 높이 날아오를까 봐 그것을 입에 담는 기쁨을 스스로 삼가는 것처럼. 그래서 그의 온유함은 차라리 아무 의미 없는 쪽을 택했다."]], [["‘I have heard that you were kind enough on one of those occasions,’ said Arthur, catching at the opportunity as it drifted past him, ‘to mention Little Dorrit to my mother.’", "‘그런 기회 가운데 한 번은,’ 아서가 스쳐 지나가는 기회를 붙들며 말했다, ‘친절하게도 우리 어머니께 리틀 도릿 이야기를 하셨다고 들었습니다.’"]], [["‘Little--?", "‘리틀 — ?"], ["Dorrit?", "도릿?"], ["That’s the seamstress who was mentioned to me by a small tenant of mine?", "그건 내 작은 임차인 하나가 내게 얘기해 준 그 삯바느질꾼 아닙니까?"], ["Yes, yes.", "그래요, 그래."], ["Dorrit?", "도릿?"], ["That’s the name.", "그게 그 이름이지요."], ["Ah, yes, yes!", "아, 그래, 그래요!"], ["You call her Little Dorrit?’", "당신은 그 여자를 리틀 도릿이라 부르시오?’"]], [["No road in that direction.", "그쪽으로는 길이 없었다."], ["Nothing came of the cross-cut.", "그 샛길에서 나오는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["It led no further.", "더 이어지지 않았다."]], [["‘My daughter Flora,’ said Mr Casby, ‘as you may have heard probably, Mr Clennam, was married and established in life, several years ago.", "‘제 딸 플로라는,’ 캐스비 씨가 말했다, ‘아마 들으셨겠지만, 클레넘 씨, 여러 해 전에 결혼해서 가정을 꾸렸지요."], ["She had the misfortune to lose her husband when she had been married a few months.", "그런데 결혼한 지 몇 달 만에 남편을 여의는 불행을 겪었습니다."], ["She resides with me again.", "지금은 다시 저와 함께 살고 있어요."], ["She will be glad to see you, if you will permit me to let her know that you are here.’", "당신이 여기 와 계시다고 제가 알려도 좋다 하시면, 그 애가 당신을 만나 기뻐할 겁니다.’"]], [["‘By all means,’ returned Clennam.", "‘물론입니다.’ 클레넘이 답했다."], ["‘I should have preferred the request, if your kindness had not anticipated me.’", "‘당신의 친절이 저보다 앞서지 않으셨다면 제가 먼저 청했을 부탁입니다.’"]], [["Upon this Mr Casby rose up in his list shoes, and with a slow, heavy step (he was of an elephantine build), made for the door.", "이에 캐스비 씨는 펠트 신발을 신고 일어나, 느릿하고 묵직한 걸음으로(그는 코끼리 같은 체격이었다) 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["He had a long wide-skirted bottle-green coat on, and a bottle-green pair of trousers, and a bottle-green waistcoat.", "그는 자락이 길고 넓은 병록색 외투를 걸치고, 병록색 바지에, 병록색 조끼를 입고 있었다."], ["The Patriarchs were not dressed in bottle-green broadcloth, and yet his clothes looked patriarchal.", "가부장들이 병록색 모직 천을 입었던 것은 아니지만, 그래도 그의 옷차림은 가부장다워 보였다."]], [["He had scarcely left the room, and allowed the ticking to become audible again, when a quick hand turned a latchkey in the house-door, opened it, and shut it.", "그가 방을 떠나 다시 똑딱이는 소리가 들리게 되기가 무섭게, 어떤 빠른 손이 현관문에 빗장 열쇠를 돌려 문을 열었다가 닫았다."], ["Immediately afterwards, a quick and eager short dark man came into the room with so much way upon him that he was within a foot of Clennam before he could stop.", "곧이어 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자 하나가 어찌나 기세 좋게 들이닥쳤던지, 멈추기도 전에 클레넘에게서 한 발짝 거리까지 다가와 있었다."]], [["‘Halloa!’ he said.", "‘이런!’ 그가 말했다."]], [["Clennam saw no reason why he should not say ‘Halloa!’ too.", "클레넘은 자기도 ‘이런!’ 하고 말하지 못할 이유가 없다고 생각했다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ said the short dark man.", "‘무슨 일이오?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 말했다."]], [["‘I have not heard that anything is the matter,’ returned Clennam.", "‘무슨 일이 있다는 얘기는 못 들었는데요.’ 클레넘이 답했다."]], [["‘Where’s Mr Casby?’ asked the short dark man, looking about.", "‘캐스비 씨는 어디 계시오?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 두리번거리며 물었다."]], [["‘He will be here directly, if you want him.’", "‘찾으신다면, 곧 오실 겁니다.’"]], [["‘_I_ want him?’ said the short dark man.", "‘내가 그분을 찾는다고?’ 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t you?’", "‘당신은 안 찾소?’"]], [["This elicited a word or two of explanation from Clennam, during the delivery of which the short dark man held his breath and looked at him.", "이 말에 클레넘은 한두 마디 해명을 했고, 그것을 늘어놓는 동안 키 작고 가무잡잡한 남자는 숨을 죽이고 그를 바라보았다."], ["He was dressed in black and rusty iron grey; had jet black beads of eyes; a scrubby little black chin; wiry black hair striking out from his head in prongs, like forks or hair-pins; and a complexion that was very dingy by nature, or very dirty by art, or a compound of nature and art.", "그는 검은색과 녹슨 쇳빛 잿빛 옷을 입고 있었다. 새까만 구슬 같은 눈에, 까칠한 작은 검은 턱, 머리에서 갈래갈래 솟아나온 철사 같은 검은 머리카락은 꼭 쇠스랑이나 머리핀 같았고, 안색은 본바탕이 무척 거무튀튀하거나, 인위적으로 무척 더러워졌거나, 아니면 자연과 인공이 뒤섞인 것이었다."], ["He had dirty hands and dirty broken nails, and looked as if he had been in the coals; he was in a perspiration, and snorted and sniffed and puffed and blew, like a little labouring steam-engine.", "그는 손이 더러웠고 부서진 손톱도 더러웠으며, 마치 석탄 속에 들어갔다 나온 것 같았다. 그는 땀을 흘리고 있었고, 작은 노동용 증기 기관처럼 콧방귀를 뀌고 코를 훌쩍이고 푹푹대며 숨을 내뿜었다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said he, when Arthur told him how he came to be there.", "‘오!’ 아서가 어떻게 자기가 여기 있게 되었는지 말해 주자 그가 말했다."], ["‘Very well.", "‘아주 좋소."], ["That’s right.", "됐습니다."], ["If he should ask for Pancks, will you be so good as to say that Pancks is come in?’ And so, with a snort and a puff, he worked out by another door.", "혹 그분이 팽크스를 찾으시거든, 팽크스가 들어왔다고 좀 전해 주시겠소?’ 그러고는 콧방귀를 뀌고 한 번 푹 숨을 내뿜으며 다른 문으로 빠져나갔다."]], [["Now, in the old days at home, certain audacious doubts respecting the last of the Patriarchs, which were afloat in the air, had, by some forgotten means, come in contact with Arthur’s sensorium.", "그런데 고향에서 지내던 옛날에, 마지막 가부장에 관한 어떤 대담한 의혹들이 공중에 떠돌고 있다가, 이제는 잊힌 어떤 경로로 아서의 감각에 와 닿은 적이 있었다."], ["He was aware of motes and specks of suspicion in the atmosphere of that time; seen through which medium, Christopher Casby was a mere Inn signpost, without any Inn--an invitation to rest and be thankful, when there was no place to put up at, and nothing whatever to be thankful for.", "그는 그 시절의 분위기에 깃든 의심의 티끌과 부스러기들을 알고 있었다. 그 매개를 통해 보면 크리스토퍼 캐스비는 여관 없는 여관 간판에 지나지 않았다 — 묵을 데도 없고 감사할 거리라고는 전혀 없는데도 쉬어 가며 감사하라고 권하는 간판 말이다."], ["He knew that some of these specks even represented Christopher as capable of harbouring designs in ‘that head,’ and as being a crafty impostor.", "그는 이 부스러기들 가운데 더러는 크리스토퍼가 ‘저 머리’ 속에 꿍꿍이를 품을 수 있는 자, 교활한 사기꾼이라고까지 여긴다는 것을 알았다."], ["Other motes there were which showed him as a heavy, selfish, drifting Booby, who, having stumbled, in the course of his unwieldy jostlings against other men, on the discovery that to get through life with ease and credit, he had but to hold his tongue, keep the bald part of his head well polished, and leave his hair alone, had had just cunning enough to seize the idea and stick to it.", "또 다른 티끌들은 그를 둔하고, 이기적이고, 떠밀려 다니는 멍청이로 보여 주었다. 다른 사람들과 굼뜨게 부대끼며 떠밀려 다니다가, 인생을 편하고 명예롭게 헤쳐 나가려면 그저 입을 다물고, 머리의 벗어진 부분을 잘 윤이 나게 닦아 두고, 나머지 머리카락은 손대지 않고 내버려 두기만 하면 된다는 사실을 어쩌다 발견하고는, 그 생각을 붙잡아 거기 매달릴 만큼의 약은 꾀는 있었던 자라고 말이다."], ["It was said that his being town-agent to Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle was referable, not to his having the least business capacity, but to his looking so supremely benignant that nobody could suppose the property screwed or jobbed under such a man; also, that for similar reasons he now got more money out of his own wretched lettings, unquestioned, than anybody with a less nobby and less shining crown could possibly have done.", "그가 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경의 도시 대리인이 된 것은, 그에게 사업 능력이 손톱만큼이라도 있어서가 아니라, 그토록 더없이 자애로워 보여서 그런 사람 밑에서 재산이 쥐어짜이거나 농간당하리라고 아무도 생각할 수 없었기 때문이라는 말이 있었다. 또 같은 이유로, 지금 그가 자기의 그 비참한 셋방에서 아무런 의심도 받지 않고 짜내는 돈이, 머리가 그만큼 불룩하지도 않고 그만큼 번쩍이지도 않는 사람이라면 도저히 짜낼 수 없을 만큼 많다는 것이었다."], ["In a word, it was represented (Clennam called to mind, alone in the ticking parlour) that many people select their models, much as the painters, just now mentioned, select theirs; and that, whereas in the Royal Academy some evil old ruffian of a Dog-stealer will annually be found embodying all the cardinal virtues, on account of his eyelashes, or his chin, or his legs (thereby planting thorns of confusion in the breasts of the more observant students of nature), so, in the great social Exhibition, accessories are often accepted in lieu of the internal character.", "한마디로, (똑딱이는 응접실에 홀로 있던 클레넘이 떠올려 보건대) 많은 사람들이, 방금 말한 화가들이 모델을 고르는 것과 똑같은 식으로 본보기를 고른다는 것이었다. 왕립 미술원에서는 어떤 사악한 늙은 개도둑 악당이, 속눈썹이나 턱이나 다리 덕분에 해마다 온갖 중요 덕목을 구현한 인물로 그려지곤 하는데(그리하여 자연을 더 면밀히 관찰하는 학생들의 가슴에 혼란의 가시를 심어 놓는데), 그처럼 거대한 사회의 전시회에서도 종종 내면의 인격 대신 부수적인 겉모습이 받아들여진다는 것이었다."]], [["Calling these things to mind, and ranging Mr Pancks in a row with them, Arthur Clennam leaned this day to the opinion, without quite deciding on it, that the last of the Patriarchs was the drifting Booby aforesaid, with the one idea of keeping the bald part of his head highly polished: and that, much as an unwieldy ship in the Thames river may sometimes be seen heavily driving with the tide, broadside on, stern first, in its own way and in the way of everything else, though making a great show of navigation, when all of a sudden, a little coaly steam-tug will bear down upon it, take it in tow, and bustle off with it; similarly the cumbrous Patriarch had been taken in tow by the snorting Pancks, and was now following in the wake of that dingy little craft.", "이런 것들을 떠올리며 팽크스 씨를 그것들과 한 줄에 나란히 세워 보고서, 아서 클레넘은 이날, 단정까지는 짓지 못한 채로, 마지막 가부장이란 앞서 말한 그 떠밀려 다니는 멍청이, 머리의 벗어진 부분을 잘 윤내 두겠다는 단 하나의 생각만 가진 자라는 견해 쪽으로 기울었다. 그리고 템스강에서 다루기 힘든 큰 배 한 척이 이따금, 옆구리를 들이밀고, 고물부터, 제멋대로, 또 모든 다른 것들의 앞길을 가로막으며, 항해하는 양 거창하게 굴면서 조류에 묵직하게 떠밀려 가는 모습이 보이다가, 별안간 석탄을 때는 작은 증기 예인선이 그쪽으로 다가와 그것을 끌고 부산하게 떠나 버리듯이, 그 거추장스러운 가부장도 콧김을 내뿜는 팽크스에게 끌려, 이제 그 거무튀튀한 작은 배의 항적을 따라가고 있는 것이라고 말이다."]], [["The return of Mr Casby with his daughter Flora, put an end to these meditations.", "캐스비 씨가 딸 플로라와 함께 돌아오면서 이 명상은 끝이 났다."], ["Clennam’s eyes no sooner fell upon the subject of his old passion than it shivered and broke to pieces.", "클레넘의 눈이 옛 연정의 대상에게 닿자마자, 그 연정은 부르르 떨며 산산조각이 났다."]], [["Most men will be found sufficiently true to themselves to be true to an old idea.", "대부분의 사람은 옛 관념에 충실할 만큼 제 자신에게는 충분히 진실한 법이다."], ["It is no proof of an inconstant mind, but exactly the opposite, when the idea will not bear close comparison with the reality, and the contrast is a fatal shock to it.", "그 관념이 현실과의 가까운 비교를 견디지 못하고, 그 대비가 관념에 치명적인 충격이 되는 경우, 그것은 변덕스러운 마음의 증거가 아니라 정확히 그 반대의 증거다."], ["Such was Clennam’s case.", "클레넘의 경우가 그러했다."], ["In his youth he had ardently loved this woman, and had heaped upon her all the locked-up wealth of his affection and imagination.", "젊은 시절 그는 이 여인을 열렬히 사랑했고, 그녀에게 자기 애정과 상상력의 갇혀 있던 보물을 모조리 쏟아부었다."], ["That wealth had been, in his desert home, like Robinson Crusoe’s money; exchangeable with no one, lying idle in the dark to rust, until he poured it out for her.", "그 보물은, 그의 황량한 가정에서, 로빈슨 크루소의 돈과도 같았다. 누구와도 바꿀 수 없어, 그가 그녀를 위해 쏟아 부을 때까지, 녹슬도록 어둠 속에 부질없이 놓여 있는 돈 말이다."], ["Ever since that memorable time, though he had, until the night of his arrival, as completely dismissed her from any association with his Present or Future as if she had been dead (which she might easily have been for anything he knew), he had kept the old fancy of the Past unchanged, in its old sacred place.", "그 잊지 못할 시절 이래로, 비록 그는 도착하던 날 밤까지 그녀를 죽은 사람처럼 — 그가 아는 한 정말 죽었을 수도 있었지만 — 자신의 현재나 미래와의 어떤 연관에서도 완전히 떨쳐 냈으면서도, 과거의 그 옛 환상만큼은 그 옛 신성한 자리에 고스란히 간직해 두었다."], ["And now, after all, the last of the Patriarchs coolly walked into the parlour, saying in effect, ‘Be good enough to throw it down and dance upon it.", "그런데 이제, 결국, 마지막 가부장이 태연히 응접실로 걸어 들어오며 사실상 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. ‘부디 그것을 내던지고 그 위에서 춤이라도 추시지요."], ["This is Flora.’", "이쪽이 플로라요.’"]], [["Flora, always tall, had grown to be very broad too, and short of breath; but that was not much.", "플로라는, 늘 키가 컸는데, 이제 몸피도 무척 넓어지고 숨이 가빠졌다. 하지만 그건 대수롭지 않았다."], ["Flora, whom he had left a lily, had become a peony; but that was not much.", "플로라는, 그가 백합으로 두고 떠났는데, 작약이 되어 있었다. 하지만 그것도 대수롭지 않았다."], ["Flora, who had seemed enchanting in all she said and thought, was diffuse and silly.", "플로라는, 말하고 생각하는 모든 것이 매혹적으로 보이던 여인이었는데, 이제 산만하고 어리석었다."], ["That was much.", "그것은 대수로웠다."], ["Flora, who had been spoiled and artless long ago, was determined to be spoiled and artless now.", "플로라는, 오래전엔 응석받이에 천진했는데, 이제 응석받이에 천진하기로 작정하고 있었다."], ["That was a fatal blow.", "그것이 치명적인 일격이었다."]], [["This is Flora!", "이쪽이 플로라요!"]], [["‘I am sure,’ giggled Flora, tossing her head with a caricature of her girlish manner, such as a mummer might have presented at her own funeral, if she had lived and died in classical antiquity, ‘I am ashamed to see Mr Clennam, I am a mere fright, I know he’ll find me fearfully changed, I am actually an old woman, it’s shocking to be found out, it’s really shocking!’", "‘정말이지,’ 플로라가 소녀 시절의 태도를 우스꽝스럽게 흉내 내며 머리를 까딱하고는 킥킥 웃었는데, 그것은 만약 고전 고대에 살다 죽은 어떤 배우라면 제 장례식에서 선보였을 법한 몸짓이었다, ‘클레넘 씨를 뵙기가 부끄러워요, 저는 꼴이 말이 아닌 데다, 그이가 절 보면 끔찍하게 변했다고 할 게 뻔하고, 정말 저는 이제 늙은 할멈이고, 그게 들통나다니 충격이고, 정말로 충격이에요!’"]], [["He assured her that she was just what he had expected and that time had not stood still with himself.", "그는 그녀가 자신이 예상한 그대로이며, 자기에게도 세월이 멈춰 있지는 않았노라고 분명히 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "‘오!"], ["But with a gentleman it’s so different and really you look so amazingly well that you have no right to say anything of the kind, while, as to me, you know--oh!’ cried Flora with a little scream, ‘I am dreadful!’", "하지만 신사한테는 그게 아주 다른 거고, 정말이지 당신은 놀랍도록 좋아 보여서 그런 말씀 하실 자격이 없는데, 저로 말하자면, 아시잖아요 — 오!’ 플로라가 작게 비명을 지르며 외쳤다, ‘저는 형편없어요!’"]], [["The Patriarch, apparently not yet understanding his own part in the drama under representation, glowed with vacant serenity.", "가부장은, 상연 중인 이 연극에서 자기 배역을 아직 모르는 듯, 텅 빈 평온함으로 환히 빛나고 있었다."]], [["‘But if we talk of not having changed,’ said Flora, who, whatever she said, never once came to a full stop, ‘look at Papa, is not Papa precisely what he was when you went away, isn’t it cruel and unnatural of Papa to be such a reproach to his own child, if we go on in this way much longer people who don’t know us will begin to suppose that I am Papa’s Mama!’", "‘하지만 변하지 않았단 얘기가 나왔으니 말인데,’ 무슨 말을 하든 한 번도 마침표에 이르는 법이 없는 플로라가 말했다, ‘아빠를 좀 보세요, 아빠는 당신이 떠나실 때 그 모습 그대로 아니에요, 아빠가 제 친자식한테 저런 면박이 되시다니 잔인하고 부자연스럽지 않아요, 우리가 이런 식으로 좀 더 오래 지내다간 우리를 모르는 사람들은 제가 아빠의 엄마라고 짐작하기 시작할 거예요!’"]], [["That must be a long time hence, Arthur considered.", "그건 한참 뒤의 일일 텐데, 하고 아서는 생각했다."]], [["‘Oh Mr Clennam you insincerest of creatures,’ said Flora, ‘I perceive already you have not lost your old way of paying compliments, your old way when you used to pretend to be so sentimentally struck you know--at least I don’t mean that, I--oh I don’t know what I mean!’ Here Flora tittered confusedly, and gave him one of her old glances.", "‘오 클레넘 씨 당신은 세상에서 가장 진실하지 못한 분이에요,’ 플로라가 말했다, ‘벌써 알아챘는데 당신은 옛날에 칭찬하던 그 버릇을 안 버리셨군요, 그렇게 감상에 사무친 척하시던 옛날 버릇 말이에요 아시잖아요 — 적어도 그런 뜻은 아니고, 저는 — 오 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요!’ 여기서 플로라는 어쩔 줄 몰라 킥킥 웃으며, 그에게 옛날의 그 눈길 하나를 던졌다."]], [["The Patriarch, as if he now began to perceive that his part in the piece was to get off the stage as soon as might be, rose, and went to the door by which Pancks had worked out, hailing that Tug by name.", "가부장은, 이제야 이 극에서 자기 배역이 되도록 빨리 무대에서 퇴장하는 것임을 깨닫기 시작한 듯, 일어나서, 팽크스가 빠져나간 그 문으로 가서, 그 예인선을 이름으로 불렀다."], ["He received an answer from some little Dock beyond, and was towed out of sight directly.", "저 너머 어느 작은 선창에서 대답이 들려왔고, 그는 곧장 끌려 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["‘You mustn’t think of going yet,’ said Flora--Arthur had looked at his hat, being in a ludicrous dismay, and not knowing what to do: ‘you could never be so unkind as to think of going, Arthur--I mean Mr Arthur--or I suppose Mr Clennam would be far more proper--but I am sure I don’t know what I am saying--without a word about the dear old days gone for ever, when I come to think of it I dare say it would be much better not to speak of them and it’s highly probable that you have some much more agreeable engagement and pray let Me be the last person in the world to interfere with it though there _was_ a time, but I am running into nonsense again.’", "‘아직 가실 생각은 마세요.’ 플로라가 말했다 — 아서가 우스꽝스러운 당혹감에 빠져, 어쩔 줄 모르고 제 모자를 바라보고 있었던 것이다. ‘떠날 생각을 하실 만큼 매정하실 리 없잖아요, 아서 — 그러니까 아서 씨 — 아니 클레넘 씨라고 하는 게 훨씬 적절하겠지만 — 정말 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요 — 영영 지나가 버린 그리운 옛날에 관해 한마디도 없이는요, 생각해 보니 차라리 그 얘긴 안 하는 게 훨씬 나을 것 같고 당신은 분명 훨씬 더 즐거운 약속이 있으실 테니 부디 제가 그걸 방해하는 세상 마지막 사람이 되게 해 주세요 그래도 한때는, 아 또 헛소리로 빠지네요.’"]], [["Was it possible that Flora could have been such a chatterer in the days she referred to?", "플로라가 그녀가 말하는 그 시절에도 이렇게 수다스러웠을 리가 있을까?"], ["Could there have been anything like her present disjointed volubility in the fascinations that had captivated him?", "그를 사로잡았던 그 매력 속에 지금의 이 두서없는 수다 같은 것이 조금이라도 있었을 리가 있을까?"]], [["‘Indeed I have little doubt,’ said Flora, running on with astonishing speed, and pointing her conversation with nothing but commas, and very few of them, ‘that you are married to some Chinese lady, being in China so long and being in business and naturally desirous to settle and extend your connection nothing was more likely than that you should propose to a Chinese lady and nothing was more natural I am sure than that the Chinese lady should accept you and think herself very well off too, I only hope she’s not a Pagodian dissenter.’", "‘정말 의심할 여지가 거의 없는데,’ 플로라가 놀라운 속도로 내달리며, 대화에 쉼표 말고는 아무 구두점도 찍지 않고, 그나마도 아주 드물게 찍으면서 말했다, ‘당신은 어느 중국 여인과 결혼하셨겠죠, 그렇게 오래 중국에 계셨고 사업을 하셨고 당연히 정착해서 인맥을 넓히고 싶으셨을 테니 당신이 중국 여인에게 청혼하셨을 가능성이 가장 높고 그 중국 여인이 당신을 받아들이고 자기도 형편이 아주 좋아졌다고 여겼을 게 무엇보다 자연스럽지요, 다만 그 여인이 탑교 비국교도가 아니길 바랄 뿐이에요.’"]], [["‘I am not,’ returned Arthur, smiling in spite of himself, ‘married to any lady, Flora.’", "‘저는,’ 아서가 자기도 모르게 미소 지으며 답했다, ‘어떤 여인과도 결혼하지 않았습니다, 플로라.’"]], [["‘Oh good gracious me I hope you never kept yourself a bachelor so long on my account!’ tittered Flora; ‘but of course you never did why should you, pray don’t answer, I don’t know where I’m running to, oh do tell me something about the Chinese ladies whether their eyes are really so long and narrow always putting me in mind of mother-of-pearl fish at cards and do they really wear tails down their back and plaited too or is it only the men, and when they pull their hair so very tight off their foreheads don’t they hurt themselves, and why do they stick little bells all over their bridges and temples and hats and things or don’t they really do it?’ Flora gave him another of her old glances.", "‘오 세상에 저 때문에 그렇게 오래 독신으로 지내신 건 아니길 바라요!’ 플로라가 킥킥거렸다. ‘하지만 물론 그러셨을 리 없죠 왜 그러셨겠어요, 부디 대답은 마세요, 제가 어디로 내달리는지 모르겠네요, 오 중국 여인들에 관해 좀 말씀해 주세요 그들의 눈이 정말 그렇게 길고 가늘어서 늘 카드놀이의 자개로 만든 물고기 모양을 떠올리게 하는지 그리고 정말 등 뒤로 변발을 늘어뜨리고 그것도 땋아서 그러는지 아니면 남자들만 그러는지, 그리고 머리카락을 이마에서 그렇게 바짝 당겨 묶을 때 아프지 않은지, 그리고 왜 자기네 다리며 사원이며 모자며 온갖 것에 작은 종을 잔뜩 매다는지 아니면 실은 안 그러는지요?’ 플로라가 그에게 또 옛날의 그 눈길을 던졌다."], ["Instantly she went on again, as if he had spoken in reply for some time.", "그러고는 곧장 다시 말을 이어 나갔는데, 마치 그가 한동안 대답이라도 한 것처럼."]], [["‘Then it’s all true and they really do!", "‘그럼 다 사실이고 정말로 그러는군요!"], ["good gracious Arthur!--pray excuse me--old habit--Mr Clennam far more proper--what a country to live in for so long a time, and with so many lanterns and umbrellas too how very dark and wet the climate ought to be and no doubt actually is, and the sums of money that must be made by those two trades where everybody carries them and hangs them everywhere, the little shoes too and the feet screwed back in infancy is quite surprising, what a traveller you are!’", "세상에 아서! — 부디 용서하세요 — 오랜 버릇이라 — 클레넘 씨가 훨씬 적절하죠 — 그렇게 오랜 세월 살기엔 어떤 나라인지, 그리고 등불도 그렇게 많고 우산도 그렇게 많으니 기후가 얼마나 어둡고 축축할 게 틀림없고 정말로 그렇겠지요, 그리고 누구나 그것들을 들고 다니며 사방에 매달아 두는 그 두 가지 장사로 벌어들이는 돈이 얼마나 클지, 그 작은 신발들도 그렇고 갓난아기 때부터 발을 옥죄어 도로 오므려 놓는다니 정말 놀랍네요, 당신은 어쩜 그런 여행가세요!’"]], [["In his ridiculous distress, Clennam received another of the old glances without in the least knowing what to do with it.", "우스꽝스러운 당혹감 속에서, 클레넘은 옛날의 그 눈길을 또 한 번 받았으나, 그것을 어찌해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."]], [["‘Dear dear,’ said Flora, ‘only to think of the changes at home Arthur--cannot overcome it, and seems so natural, Mr Clennam far more proper--since you became familiar with the Chinese customs and language which I am persuaded you speak like a Native if not better for you were always quick and clever though immensely difficult no doubt, I am sure the tea chests alone would kill me if I tried, such changes Arthur--I am doing it again, seems so natural, most improper--as no one could have believed, who could have ever imagined Mrs Finching when I can’t imagine it myself!’", "‘이런 저런,’ 플로라가 말했다, ‘고향의 그 변화들을 생각하면 아서 — 도무지 안 고쳐지네요, 너무 자연스럽게 나와서, 클레넘 씨가 훨씬 적절한데 — 당신이 중국의 풍습과 언어에 익숙해지신 뒤로 말이에요 분명 당신은 그 말을 원주민처럼, 아니 어쩌면 더 잘하시겠죠 당신은 늘 머리가 빠르고 영리하셨으니까요 물론 어마어마하게 어려울 테지만, 정말이지 차 상자만 해도 제가 해 보려 들면 절 죽이고 말 거예요, 그런 변화들이라니 아서 — 또 그러네요, 너무 자연스러워서, 더없이 부적절한데 — 아무도 믿지 못했을 변화들이라니, 핀칭 부인이라니 누가 상상이나 했겠어요 저 자신도 상상이 안 되는걸요!’"]], [["‘Is that your married name?’ asked Arthur, struck, in the midst of all this, by a certain warmth of heart that expressed itself in her tone when she referred, however oddly, to the youthful relation in which they had stood to one another.", "‘그게 결혼하신 뒤의 성함입니까?’ 아서가 물었는데, 이 모든 와중에, 비록 묘한 방식으로나마 그녀가 두 사람이 한때 맺고 있던 젊은 날의 관계를 언급할 때 그 어조에 어린 어떤 마음의 따스함에 마음이 움직였던 것이다."], ["‘Finching?’", "‘핀칭이라고요?’"]], [["‘Finching oh yes isn’t it a dreadful name, but as Mr F. said when he proposed to me which he did seven times and handsomely consented I must say to be what he used to call on liking twelve months, after all, he wasn’t answerable for it and couldn’t help it could he, Excellent man, not at all like you but excellent man!’", "‘핀칭 오 그래요 끔찍한 이름 아니에요, 하지만 핀칭 씨가 제게 청혼할 때 — 그이는 일곱 번이나 청혼했고 그러고는, 그이 표현대로 일 년간 마음에 드는지 두고 보자며 점잖게 응낙해 주셨는데 — 어쨌거나 그게 그이 잘못은 아니고 어쩔 수 없는 일이었잖아요 안 그래요, 훌륭한 분이었어요, 당신과는 전혀 안 닮았지만 훌륭한 분이었죠!’"]], [["Flora had at last talked herself out of breath for one moment.", "플로라는 마침내 한순간 숨이 차 말을 멈췄다."], ["One moment; for she recovered breath in the act of raising a minute corner of her pocket-handkerchief to her eye, as a tribute to the ghost of the departed Mr F., and began again.", "한순간이었다. 떠나간 핀칭 씨의 망령에 바치는 헌사로, 손수건 한 귀퉁이를 눈가에 들어 올리는 동안 숨을 돌렸으니, 그러고는 다시 시작했던 것이다."]], [["‘No one could dispute, Arthur--Mr Clennam--that it’s quite right you should be formally friendly to me under the altered circumstances and indeed you couldn’t be anything else, at least I suppose not you ought to know, but I can’t help recalling that there _was_ a time when things were very different.’", "‘아무도 부정 못 할 거예요, 아서 — 클레넘 씨 — 사정이 달라진 마당에 당신이 저한테 격식 차려 다정하게 대하시는 게 옳고 사실 달리 어쩌실 수도 없고, 적어도 제 짐작엔 그렇고 당신이 더 잘 아시겠지만, 그래도 정말 사정이 무척 달랐던 한때가 있었다는 걸 떠올리지 않을 수가 없네요.’"]], [["‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ Arthur began, struck by the good tone again.", "‘친애하는 핀칭 부인,’ 아서가, 그 좋은 어조에 다시 마음이 움직여 입을 열었다."]], [["‘Oh not that nasty ugly name, say Flora!’", "‘오 그 추하고 흉한 이름 말고, 플로라라고 불러 주세요!’"]], [["‘Flora.", "‘플로라."], ["I assure you, Flora, I am happy in seeing you once more, and in finding that, like me, you have not forgotten the old foolish dreams, when we saw all before us in the light of our youth and hope.’", "분명히 말씀드리는데, 플로라, 저는 당신을 다시 뵙게 되어, 그리고 당신도 저처럼 그 옛날의 어리석은 꿈들을, 우리가 젊음과 희망의 빛 속에서 눈앞의 모든 것을 바라보던 그 시절을 잊지 않으셨음을 알게 되어 기쁩니다.’"]], [["‘You don’t seem so,’ pouted Flora, ‘you take it very coolly, but however I know you are disappointed in me, I suppose the Chinese ladies--Mandarinesses if you call them so--are the cause or perhaps I am the cause myself, it’s just as likely.’", "‘그래 보이지 않는걸요,’ 플로라가 입을 삐죽이며 말했다, ‘당신은 아주 시큰둥하게 받아들이시네요, 하지만 어쨌든 당신이 저한테 실망하셨다는 건 알아요, 아마 그 중국 여인들이 — 만다린 부인들이라 부른다면 — 그 원인이거나 어쩌면 저 자신이 원인이거나겠죠, 그쪽도 똑같이 그럴듯하니까요.’"]], [["‘No, no,’ Clennam entreated, ‘don’t say that.’", "‘아뇨, 아뇨,’ 클레넘이 간청했다, ‘그런 말씀 마세요.’"]], [["‘Oh I must you know,’ said Flora, in a positive tone, ‘what nonsense not to, I know I am not what you expected, I know that very well.’", "‘오 해야죠 아시잖아요,’ 플로라가 단호한 어조로 말했다, ‘안 한다는 게 무슨 헛소리예요, 제가 당신 기대와 다른 거 저도 알아요, 아주 잘 알아요.’"]], [["In the midst of her rapidity, she had found that out with the quick perception of a cleverer woman.", "그 빠른 말의 와중에도, 그녀는 더 영리한 여인다운 날렵한 통찰로 그것을 알아챘던 것이다."], ["The inconsistent and profoundly unreasonable way in which she instantly went on, nevertheless, to interweave their long-abandoned boy and girl relations with their present interview, made Clennam feel as if he were light-headed.", "그런데도, 이내 두 사람이 오래전에 버린 소년 소녀 시절의 관계를 지금의 만남과 뒤섞어 짜 나가는 그 모순되고 지극히 터무니없는 방식에, 클레넘은 머리가 어질어질해지는 듯했다."]], [["‘One remark,’ said Flora, giving their conversation, without the slightest notice and to the great terror of Clennam, the tone of a love-quarrel, ‘I wish to make, one explanation I wish to offer, when your Mama came and made a scene of it with my Papa and when I was called down into the little breakfast-room where they were looking at one another with your Mama’s parasol between them seated on two chairs like mad bulls what was I to do?’", "‘한마디,’ 플로라가, 그들의 대화에 아무 예고도 없이 — 클레넘이 몹시 겁에 질리게도 — 사랑싸움의 어조를 입히며 말했다, ‘드리고 싶은 게 있고, 한 가지 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 당신 어머님이 오셔서 우리 아빠를 상대로 한바탕 소동을 벌이시고 제가 아래층 작은 아침 식사실로 불려 내려가 보니 두 분이 의자 두 개에 마치 미친 황소처럼 앉아 당신 어머님의 양산을 사이에 두고 서로 노려보고 계셨을 때 제가 어쩔 수 있었겠어요?’"]], [["‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ urged Clennam--‘all so long ago and so long concluded, is it worth while seriously to--’", "‘친애하는 핀칭 부인,’ 클레넘이 채근했다 — ‘그게 다 그렇게 오래전 일이고 그렇게 오래전에 끝난 일인데, 진지하게 그걸 — 그럴 만한 가치가 있을까요 — ’"]], [["‘I can’t Arthur,’ returned Flora, ‘be denounced as heartless by the whole society of China without setting myself right when I have the opportunity of doing so, and you must be very well aware that there was Paul and Virginia which had to be returned and which was returned without note or comment, not that I mean to say you could have written to me watched as I was but if it had only come back with a red wafer on the cover I should have known that it meant Come to Pekin Nankeen and What’s the third place, barefoot.’", "‘저는 못 해요 아서,’ 플로라가 답했다, ‘바로잡을 기회가 있는데도 중국의 사회 전체로부터 매정한 여자라고 손가락질당하는 걸 가만둘 순 없어요, 그리고 당신도 잘 아시겠지만 돌려줘야 했던 그 『폴과 비르지니』가 있었고 그건 아무 쪽지도 한마디도 없이 돌려보내졌잖아요, 그렇게 감시당하는 처지에 당신이 제게 편지를 쓸 수 있었으리란 얘긴 아니지만, 그래도 표지에 빨간 봉인 하나만 붙어서 돌아왔어도 저는 그게 베이징 난징과 — 세 번째 데가 어디더라, 맨발로 — 그리로 오라는 뜻인 줄 알았을 거예요.’"]], [["‘My dear Mrs Finching, you were not to blame, and I never blamed you.", "‘친애하는 핀칭 부인, 당신 잘못이 아니었고, 저는 한 번도 당신을 탓하지 않았습니다."], ["We were both too young, too dependent and helpless, to do anything but accept our separation.--Pray think how long ago,’ gently remonstrated Arthur.", "우리는 둘 다 너무 어렸고, 너무 의지가지없고 무력해서, 우리의 이별을 받아들이는 것 말고는 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었지요. — 부디 얼마나 오래전 일인지 생각해 보세요.’ 아서가 부드럽게 타일렀다."]], [["‘One more remark,’ proceeded Flora with unslackened volubility, ‘I wish to make, one more explanation I wish to offer, for five days I had a cold in the head from crying which I passed entirely in the back drawing-room--there is the back drawing-room still on the first floor and still at the back of the house to confirm my words--when that dreary period had passed a lull succeeded years rolled on and Mr F. became acquainted with us at a mutual friend’s, he was all attention he called next day he soon began to call three evenings a week and to send in little things for supper it was not love on Mr F.’s part it was adoration, Mr F. proposed with the full approval of Papa and what could I do?’", "‘한마디 더,’ 플로라가 줄지 않는 수다로 말을 이었다, ‘드리고 싶고, 한 가지 더 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 닷새 동안 저는 울다가 머리에 감기가 들어 그 시간을 몽땅 뒤쪽 응접실에서 보냈는데 — 그 뒤쪽 응접실은 지금도 이 층에 있고 지금도 집 뒤편에 있으니 제 말이 사실이라는 걸 증명해 줄 거예요 — 그 음울한 시기가 지나가자 잠잠한 시기가 뒤따랐고 세월이 흘러 핀칭 씨가 어느 공동의 친구 집에서 우리와 알게 되었는데 그이는 온통 정성이었고 이튿날 찾아왔고 곧 일주일에 세 번 저녁마다 찾아오기 시작했고 야식거리로 자잘한 것들을 보내오기 시작했어요 핀칭 씨로서는 그게 사랑이 아니라 숭배였죠, 핀칭 씨가 아빠의 전적인 동의를 얻어 청혼했는데 제가 어쩔 수 있었겠어요?’"]], [["‘Nothing whatever,’ said Arthur, with the cheerfulest readiness, ‘but what you did.", "‘아무것도 못 하셨겠죠,’ 아서가 더없이 쾌활한 선뜻함으로 말했다, ‘당신이 하신 그것 말고는요."], ["Let an old friend assure you of his full conviction that you did quite right.’", "옛 친구로서 단언하건대, 당신이 아주 옳게 하셨다고 저는 확신합니다.’"]], [["‘One last remark,’ proceeded Flora, rejecting commonplace life with a wave of her hand, ‘I wish to make, one last explanation I wish to offer, there _was_ a time ere Mr F. first paid attentions incapable of being mistaken, but that is past and was not to be, dear Mr Clennam you no longer wear a golden chain you are free I trust you may be happy, here is Papa who is always tiresome and putting in his nose everywhere where he is not wanted.’", "‘마지막으로 한마디,’ 플로라가 평범한 일상사를 손짓 한 번으로 물리치며 말을 이었다, ‘드리고 싶고, 마지막으로 한 가지 해명을 드리고 싶은데, 핀칭 씨가 오해의 여지 없는 관심을 처음 보이기 전 한때가 정말 있었지만, 그건 다 지난 일이고 이루어질 수 없는 일이었고, 친애하는 클레넘 씨 당신은 이제 황금 사슬을 차고 있지 않으니 자유로운 몸이고 부디 행복하시길 바라요, 여기 아빠가 오시는데 늘 성가시게 굴며 불러 주지도 않은 데마다 코를 들이미시지요.’"]], [["With these words, and with a hasty gesture fraught with timid caution--such a gesture had Clennam’s eyes been familiar with in the old time--poor Flora left herself at eighteen years of age, a long long way behind again; and came to a full stop at last.", "이 말과 함께, 그리고 소심한 조심성이 가득 밴 황급한 몸짓 — 클레넘의 눈에 옛 시절 익숙했던 그런 몸짓 — 과 함께, 가엾은 플로라는 다시금 열여덟 살의 자신을 까마득히 저 뒤에 남겨 두고, 마침내 완전한 마침표에 다다랐다."]], [["Or rather, she left about half of herself at eighteen years of age behind, and grafted the rest on to the relict of the late Mr F.; thus making a moral mermaid of herself, which her once boy-lover contemplated with feelings wherein his sense of the sorrowful and his sense of the comical were curiously blended.", "아니, 차라리, 그녀는 열여덟 살의 자신을 절반쯤만 뒤에 남겨 두고, 나머지 절반을 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인에 접붙여, 그리하여 스스로를 도덕적인 인어로 만들어 놓았으니, 한때 그녀의 소년 연인이었던 이는 그 모습을, 슬픔의 감정과 우스움의 감정이 묘하게 뒤섞인 심정으로 바라보았다."]], [["For example.", "이를테면 이런 식이었다."], ["As if there were a secret understanding between herself and Clennam of the most thrilling nature; as if the first of a train of post-chaises and four, extending all the way to Scotland, were at that moment round the corner; and as if she couldn’t (and wouldn’t) have walked into the Parish Church with him, under the shade of the family umbrella, with the Patriarchal blessing on her head, and the perfect concurrence of all mankind; Flora comforted her soul with agonies of mysterious signalling, expressing dread of discovery.", "마치 그녀와 클레넘 사이에 더없이 짜릿한 종류의 은밀한 양해가 있는 것처럼, 스코틀랜드까지 죽 늘어선 사두마차 행렬의 맨 앞 마차가 바로 그 순간 모퉁이를 돌아와 있는 것처럼, 그리고 마치 그녀가 가문의 우산 그늘 아래, 머리에 가부장의 축복을 받고, 온 인류의 완벽한 동의를 얻어 그와 함께 교구 교회로 걸어 들어갈 수 (또 그러려 하지) 않는 것처럼, 플로라는 들킬까 봐 두려워하는 시늉으로 신비로운 신호를 보내는 고뇌 속에서 제 영혼을 달랬다."], ["With the sensation of becoming more and more light-headed every minute, Clennam saw the relict of the late Mr F. enjoying herself in the most wonderful manner, by putting herself and him in their old places, and going through all the old performances--now, when the stage was dusty, when the scenery was faded, when the youthful actors were dead, when the orchestra was empty, when the lights were out.", "점점 더 머리가 어질어질해지는 느낌 속에서, 클레넘은 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인이, 무대가 먼지투성이가 되고, 배경이 빛바래고, 젊은 배우들이 죽고, 관현악단이 텅 비고, 조명이 꺼진 지금에 와서, 자기와 자신을 옛 자리에 다시 세우고 그 옛 연기를 죄다 되풀이하며 더없이 놀라운 방식으로 즐기는 모습을 보았다."], ["And still, through all this grotesque revival of what he remembered as having once been prettily natural to her, he could not but feel that it revived at sight of him, and that there was a tender memory in it.", "그러면서도, 한때 그녀에게 어여삐 자연스럽던 것으로 그가 기억하는 그 모든 것을 이렇게 기괴하게 되살려 내는 와중에도, 그는 그것이 자기를 본 탓에 되살아난 것이며, 그 속에 다정한 추억이 깃들어 있음을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["The Patriarch insisted on his staying to dinner, and Flora signalled ‘Yes!’ Clennam so wished he could have done more than stay to dinner--so heartily wished he could have found the Flora that had been, or that never had been--that he thought the least atonement he could make for the disappointment he almost felt ashamed of, was to give himself up to the family desire.", "가부장은 그에게 저녁 식사를 하고 가라고 우겼고, 플로라는 ‘좋아요!’라고 신호했다. 클레넘은 차라리 저녁 식사를 하고 가는 것 이상의 무언가를 할 수 있었으면 — 예전의 그 플로라를, 아니 애초에 존재한 적도 없던 그 플로라를 찾아낼 수 있었으면 — 하고 어찌나 간절히 바랐던지, 자기가 거의 부끄럽게 느끼던 그 실망에 대해 할 수 있는 최소한의 속죄는, 이 집안의 바람에 자기를 내맡기는 것이라고 생각했다."], ["Therefore, he stayed to dinner.", "그래서 그는 저녁 식사를 하고 가기로 했다."]], [["Pancks dined with them.", "팽크스가 그들과 함께 식사를 했다."], ["Pancks steamed out of his little dock at a quarter before six, and bore straight down for the Patriarch, who happened to be then driving, in an inane manner, through a stagnant account of Bleeding Heart Yard.", "팽크스는 여섯 시 십오 분 전에 제 작은 선창에서 증기를 내뿜으며 나와, 곧장 가부장 쪽으로 돌진해 왔는데, 가부장은 마침 그때 블리딩 하트 야드에 관한 따분한 계산 이야기를 멍하니 몰아가고 있던 참이었다."], ["Pancks instantly made fast to him and hauled him out.", "팽크스는 즉시 그에게 밧줄을 걸어 끌어냈다."]], [["‘Bleeding Heart Yard?’ said Pancks, with a puff and a snort.", "‘블리딩 하트 야드 말입니까?’ 팽크스가 푹 숨을 내뿜고 콧방귀를 뀌며 말했다."], ["‘It’s a troublesome property.", "‘성가신 재산이지요."], ["Don’t pay you badly, but rents are very hard to get there.", "벌이가 나쁘진 않지만, 거기선 집세를 받아내기가 여간 어려운 게 아닙니다."], ["You have more trouble with that one place than with all the places belonging to you.’", "사장님의 그 한 군데가, 사장님 소유의 다른 모든 곳을 합친 것보다 더 골치를 썩이지요.’"]], [["Just as the big ship in tow gets the credit, with most spectators, of being the powerful object, so the Patriarch usually seemed to have said himself whatever Pancks said for him.", "끌려가는 큰 배가 대부분의 구경꾼들에게는 그 힘센 주체로 비치듯이, 가부장도 으레, 팽크스가 그를 위해 한 말이 무엇이든 자기가 직접 한 것처럼 보이곤 했다."]], [["‘Indeed?’ returned Clennam, upon whom this impression was so efficiently made by a mere gleam of the polished head that he spoke the ship instead of the Tug.", "‘그렇소?’ 클레넘이 답했는데, 윤이 나는 머리의 번뜩임 하나만으로도 그 인상이 어찌나 효과적으로 그에게 박혔던지, 그는 예인선이 아니라 큰 배를 향해 말을 건넸던 것이다."], ["‘The people are so poor there?’", "‘그곳 사람들이 그렇게 가난합니까?’"]], [["‘_You_ can’t say, you know,’ snorted Pancks, taking one of his dirty hands out of his rusty iron-grey pockets to bite his nails, if he could find any, and turning his beads of eyes upon his employer, ‘whether they’re poor or not.", "‘그건 모르는 일이지요,’ 팽크스가, 녹슨 쇳빛 잿빛 주머니에서 더러운 손 하나를 빼내 깨물 손톱이라도 있나 찾으며, 그 구슬 같은 눈을 자기 고용주에게 돌리면서 콧방귀를 뀌었다, ‘그들이 가난한지 아닌지 말입니다."], ["They say they are, but they all say that.", "자기들은 가난하다고 하지만, 다들 그렇게 말하지요."], ["When a man says he’s rich, you’re generally sure he isn’t.", "어떤 자가 부자라고 하면, 대개는 부자가 아니라고 보면 됩니다."], ["Besides, if they _are_ poor, you can’t help it.", "게다가, 설령 그들이 가난하다 한들, 사장님이 어쩔 수 있는 게 아니지요."], ["You’d be poor yourself if you didn’t get your rents.’", "집세를 못 받으면 사장님 본인이 가난해질 테니까요.’"]], [["‘True enough,’ said Arthur.", "‘옳은 말이군요.’ 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘You’re not going to keep open house for all the poor of London,’ pursued Pancks.", "‘런던의 모든 빈민을 위해 집을 열어 둘 순 없는 노릇이지요,’ 팽크스가 말을 이었다."], ["‘You’re not going to lodge ‘em for nothing.", "‘공짜로 재워 줄 순 없는 겁니다."], ["You’re not going to open your gates wide and let ‘em come free.", "대문을 활짝 열고 거저 들어오게 둘 순 없어요."], ["Not if you know it, you ain’t.’", "정신이 박혀 있다면, 그러지 않으시겠지요.’"]], [["Mr Casby shook his head, in Placid and benignant generality.", "캐스비 씨는 평온하고 자애로운 두루뭉술함으로 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘If a man takes a room of you at half-a-crown a week, and when the week comes round hasn’t got the half-crown, you say to that man, Why have you got the room, then?", "‘어떤 자가 일주일에 반 크라운에 방을 빌렸는데, 한 주가 돌아왔을 때 그 반 크라운이 없으면, 그자한테 이렇게 말하는 겁니다. 그럼 방은 왜 빌렸나?"], ["If you haven’t got the one thing, why have you got the other?", "한 가지가 없으면서 다른 한 가지는 왜 가졌나?"], ["What have you been and done with your money?", "자네 돈을 어디다 갖다 쓴 건가?"], ["What do you mean by it?", "무슨 속셈이야?"], ["What are you up to?", "무슨 수작이냐고?"], ["That’s what _you_ say to a man of that sort; and if you didn’t say it, more shame for you!’ Mr Pancks here made a singular and startling noise, produced by a strong blowing effort in the region of the nose, unattended by any result but that acoustic one.", "그런 자한텐 그렇게 말하는 겁니다. 그렇게 말하지 않으면 오히려 부끄러운 일이지요!’ 여기서 팽크스 씨는 코 언저리에서 세차게 숨을 내뿜는 노력으로, 그 소리 말고는 아무 결과도 없는 기이하고 깜짝 놀랄 만한 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘You have some extent of such property about the east and north-east here, I believe?’ said Clennam, doubtful which of the two to address.", "‘그런 재산을 이 동쪽과 북동쪽 일대에 어느 정도 가지고 계신 걸로 압니다만?’ 클레넘이, 둘 중 누구에게 말을 건네야 할지 망설이며 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, pretty well,’ said Pancks.", "‘오, 꽤 됩니다.’ 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘You’re not particular to east or north-east, any point of the compass will do for you.", "‘사장님은 동쪽이든 북동쪽이든 가리지 않으세요, 나침반의 어느 방향이든 상관없지요."], ["What you want is a good investment and a quick return.", "원하시는 건 좋은 투자와 빠른 회수니까요."], ["You take it where you can find it.", "찾을 수 있는 데서 잡으시는 겁니다."], ["You ain’t nice as to situation--not you.’", "위치를 까다롭게 따지시지 않아요 — 절대 아니지요.’"]], [["There was a fourth and most original figure in the Patriarchal tent, who also appeared before dinner.", "가부장의 천막 안에는 네 번째이자 가장 독특한 인물이 있었는데, 그 사람 역시 저녁 식사 전에 나타났다."], ["This was an amazing little old woman, with a face like a staring wooden doll too cheap for expression, and a stiff yellow wig perched unevenly on the top of her head, as if the child who owned the doll had driven a tack through it anywhere, so that it only got fastened on.", "이는 놀라운 작은 노부인으로, 표정을 담기엔 너무 싸구려인 멍하니 노려보는 나무 인형 같은 얼굴에, 뻣뻣한 노란 가발이 머리 꼭대기에 비뚜름히 얹혀 있었는데, 마치 그 인형의 주인인 아이가 아무 데나 압정을 박아 넣어 겨우 고정해 놓은 것 같았다."], ["Another remarkable thing in this little old woman was, that the same child seemed to have damaged her face in two or three places with some blunt instrument in the nature of a spoon; her countenance, and particularly the tip of her nose, presenting the phenomena of several dints, generally answering to the bowl of that article.", "이 작은 노부인의 또 다른 두드러진 점은, 바로 그 아이가 숟가락 비슷한 어떤 뭉툭한 도구로 그녀의 얼굴 두세 군데를 망가뜨려 놓은 듯하다는 것이었다. 그녀의 얼굴은, 특히 코끝은, 그 도구의 우묵한 부분에 대체로 들어맞는 여러 개의 움푹 팬 자국이라는 현상을 보여 주고 있었다."], ["A further remarkable thing in this little old woman was, that she had no name but Mr F.’s Aunt.", "이 작은 노부인의 또 한 가지 두드러진 점은, 그녀에게 핀칭 씨의 고모라는 것 말고는 아무 이름이 없다는 것이었다."]], [["She broke upon the visitor’s view under the following circumstances: Flora said when the first dish was being put on the table, perhaps Mr Clennam might not have heard that Mr F.", "그녀는 다음과 같은 정황에서 손님의 눈앞에 나타났다. 첫 번째 요리가 식탁에 놓일 때, 플로라가 어쩌면 클레넘 씨가 핀칭 씨가 자기에게 유산을 남겼다는 얘기를 못 들으셨을지 모르겠다고 말했다."], ["had left her a legacy? Clennam in return implied his hope that Mr F.", "클레넘은 답으로, 핀칭 씨가 그토록 흠모하던 아내에게 자신의 세속 재산 전부는 아닐지라도 그 대부분을 물려주었기를 바란다는 뜻을 비쳤다."], ["had endowed the wife whom he adored, with the greater part of his worldly substance, if not with all. Flora said, oh yes, she didn’t mean that, Mr F.", "플로라는, 오 그래요, 그런 뜻이 아니라며, 핀칭 씨가 훌륭한 유언장을 남기긴 했지만, 자기에게 별도의 유산으로 그분의 고모를 남겼다고 말했다."], ["had made a beautiful will, but he had left her as a separate legacy, his Aunt. She then went out of the room to fetch the legacy, and, on her return, rather triumphantly presented ‘Mr F.’s Aunt.’", "그러고는 방을 나가 그 유산을 데려왔고, 돌아와서는 다소 의기양양하게 ‘핀칭 씨의 고모’를 선보였다."]], [["The major characteristics discoverable by the stranger in Mr F.’s Aunt, were extreme severity and grim taciturnity; sometimes interrupted by a propensity to offer remarks in a deep warning voice, which, being totally uncalled for by anything said by anybody, and traceable to no association of ideas, confounded and terrified the Mind.", "낯선 사람이 핀칭 씨의 고모에게서 알아챌 수 있는 주된 특징은, 극단적인 엄격함과 음울한 과묵함이었다. 이따금 깊고 경고하는 듯한 목소리로 한마디씩 던지는 성향이 그 과묵함을 깨뜨리곤 했는데, 그 말은 누가 한 어떤 말로도 전혀 불려 나올 까닭이 없는 데다 어떤 관념의 연상으로도 추적할 수 없어서, 듣는 이의 정신을 어리둥절하게 하고 겁에 질리게 했다."], ["Mr F.’s Aunt may have thrown in these observations on some system of her own, and it may have been ingenious, or even subtle: but the key to it was wanted.", "핀칭 씨의 고모는 어쩌면 그녀 나름의 어떤 체계에 따라 이런 소견들을 던졌는지도 모르고, 그것이 기발하거나 심지어 정교했는지도 모른다. 다만 그것을 풀 열쇠가 없었다."]], [["The neatly-served and well-cooked dinner (for everything about the Patriarchal household promoted quiet digestion) began with some soup, some fried soles, a butter-boat of shrimp sauce, and a dish of potatoes.", "말끔하게 차려지고 잘 조리된 저녁 식사는(가부장의 집안에 관한 모든 것이 조용한 소화를 도왔으니), 수프 약간과 가자미 튀김, 새우 소스를 담은 버터 그릇, 그리고 감자 한 접시로 시작되었다."], ["The conversation still turned on the receipt of rents.", "대화는 여전히 집세 수령에 관한 것이었다."], ["Mr F.’s Aunt, after regarding the company for ten minutes with a malevolent gaze, delivered the following fearful remark:", "핀칭 씨의 고모는, 십 분 동안 일행을 악의에 찬 눈초리로 쏘아본 끝에, 다음과 같은 무시무시한 소견을 내놓았다."]], [["‘When we lived at Henley, Barnes’s gander was stole by tinkers.’", "‘우리가 헨리에 살 때, 반스네 거위 수컷을 땜장이들이 훔쳐 갔어.’"]], [["Mr Pancks courageously nodded his head and said, ‘All right, ma’am.’ But the effect of this mysterious communication upon Clennam was absolutely to frighten him.", "팽크스 씨는 용감하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. ‘옳은 말씀입니다, 부인.’ 그러나 이 알 수 없는 전언이 클레넘에게 미친 효과는 그를 그야말로 겁에 질리게 하는 것이었다."], ["And another circumstance invested this old lady with peculiar terrors.", "게다가 또 한 가지 정황이 이 노부인에게 각별한 공포를 둘러씌웠다."], ["Though she was always staring, she never acknowledged that she saw any individual.", "그녀는 늘 노려보고 있으면서도, 어느 누구를 본다는 것을 결코 인정하지 않았다."], ["The polite and attentive stranger would desire, say, to consult her inclinations on the subject of potatoes.", "예의 바르고 자상한 낯선 손님이, 말하자면 감자 문제에 관해 그녀의 의향을 물어보고 싶어 한다 치자."], ["His expressive action would be hopelessly lost upon her, and what could he do?", "그의 의사를 담은 동작은 그녀에게 가망 없이 헛수고가 되고 말 텐데, 그가 무엇을 어쩌겠는가?"], ["No man could say, ‘Mr F.’s Aunt, will you permit me?’ Every man retired from the spoon, as Clennam did, cowed and baffled.", "어느 누구도 ‘핀칭 씨의 고모님, 제가 권해 드려도 될까요?’라고 말할 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 클레넘이 그러했듯, 누구든 기가 죽고 좌절한 채 숟가락에서 물러나고 말았다."]], [["There was mutton, a steak, and an apple-pie--nothing in the remotest way connected with ganders--and the dinner went on like a disenchanted feast, as it truly was.", "양고기와 스테이크, 그리고 사과 파이가 나왔다 — 거위 수컷과는 아무리 멀게라도 연관될 것이 없는 음식들이었다 — 그리고 저녁 식사는, 실로 그러했듯, 마법이 풀린 연회처럼 흘러갔다."], ["Once upon a time Clennam had sat at that table taking no heed of anything but Flora; now the principal heed he took of Flora was to observe, against his will, that she was very fond of porter, that she combined a great deal of sherry with sentiment, and that if she were a little overgrown, it was upon substantial grounds.", "한때 클레넘은 그 식탁에 앉아 플로라 말고는 아무것에도 마음을 두지 않은 적이 있었다. 이제 그가 플로라에게 두는 주된 관심이라고는, 자기 뜻과는 달리, 그녀가 흑맥주를 무척 좋아하고, 감상에 셰리주를 잔뜩 곁들이며, 그녀가 좀 너무 자랐다면 그것은 든든한 근거 위에서라는 사실을 관찰하는 것이었다."], ["The last of the Patriarchs had always been a mighty eater, and he disposed of an immense quantity of solid food with the benignity of a good soul who was feeding some one else.", "마지막 가부장은 늘 대단한 대식가였는데, 마치 다른 누군가를 먹여 주는 착한 영혼 같은 자애로움으로 엄청난 양의 단단한 음식을 처리해 나갔다."], ["Mr Pancks, who was always in a hurry, and who referred at intervals to a little dirty notebook which he kept beside him (perhaps containing the names of the defaulters he meant to look up by way of dessert), took in his victuals much as if he were coaling; with a good deal of noise, a good deal of dropping about, and a puff and a snort occasionally, as if he were nearly ready to steam away.", "늘 바쁜 팽크스 씨는, 옆에 둔 작고 더러운 수첩을(어쩌면 디저트 삼아 찾아내려는 체납자들의 명단이 적혀 있을지도 모를 그 수첩을) 이따금 들춰 보면서, 마치 석탄을 싣듯이 음식을 먹어 치웠다. 상당한 소음과 함께, 상당히 흘리고 떨어뜨리면서, 그리고 이따금, 마치 곧 증기를 내뿜고 떠날 채비가 된 양 푹 숨을 내뿜고 콧방귀를 뀌면서 말이다."]], [["All through dinner, Flora combined her present appetite for eating and drinking with her past appetite for romantic love, in a way that made Clennam afraid to lift his eyes from his plate; since he could not look towards her without receiving some glance of mysterious meaning or warning, as if they were engaged in a plot.", "식사 내내, 플로라는 먹고 마시는 현재의 식욕과 낭만적 사랑을 향한 과거의 식욕을 한데 어우러 놓는 통에, 클레넘은 제 접시에서 눈을 들기가 두려울 지경이었다. 그녀 쪽을 바라보면 영락없이 무언가 신비로운 의미나 경고의 눈길을 받았던 까닭인데, 마치 두 사람이 어떤 음모에 가담하기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Mr F.’s Aunt sat silently defying him with an aspect of the greatest bitterness, until the removal of the cloth and the appearance of the decanters, when she originated another observation--struck into the conversation like a clock, without consulting anybody.", "핀칭 씨의 고모는, 식탁보가 걷히고 술병들이 나타날 때까지, 더없이 신랄한 표정으로 말없이 그를 노려보며 앉아 있다가, 그때 또 하나의 소견을 시작했다 — 누구에게 물어보는 법도 없이, 괘종시계처럼 대화에 끼어든 것이다."]], [["Flora had just said, ‘Mr Clennam, will you give me a glass of port for Mr F.’s Aunt?’", "플로라가 막 이렇게 말한 참이었다. ‘클레넘 씨, 핀칭 씨의 고모님께 포트와인 한 잔 따라 주시겠어요?’"]], [["‘The Monument near London Bridge,’ that lady instantly proclaimed, ‘was put up arter the Great Fire of London; and the Great Fire of London was not the fire in which your uncle George’s workshops was burned down.’", "‘런던교 근처의 그 기념탑은,’ 그 부인이 즉시 선언했다, ‘런던 대화재 뒤에 세워진 거야. 그리고 런던 대화재는 네 삼촌 조지의 작업장이 불타 버린 그 화재가 아니야.’"]], [["Mr Pancks, with his former courage, said, ‘Indeed, ma’am?", "팽크스 씨는 아까의 그 용기로 말했다. ‘그렇습니까, 부인?"], ["All right!’ But appearing to be incensed by imaginary contradiction, or other ill-usage, Mr F.’s Aunt, instead of relapsing into silence, made the following additional proclamation:", "옳은 말씀입니다!’ 그러나 가상의 반박이나 다른 부당한 대우에 격분한 듯, 핀칭 씨의 고모는 침묵에 빠져드는 대신 다음과 같은 추가 선언을 내놓았다."]], [["‘I hate a fool!’", "‘나는 바보가 싫어!’"]], [["She imparted to this sentiment, in itself almost Solomonic, so extremely injurious and personal a character by levelling it straight at the visitor’s head, that it became necessary to lead Mr F.’s Aunt from the room.", "이 소견은, 그 자체로는 거의 솔로몬의 잠언에 가까운 것이었으나, 그녀가 그것을 손님의 머리를 향해 똑바로 겨눈 탓에, 더없이 모욕적이고 인신공격적인 성격을 띠게 되었으니, 핀칭 씨의 고모를 방에서 데리고 나가야 할 형편이 되었다. 이는 플로라가 조용히 해냈다."], ["This was quietly done by Flora; Mr F.’s Aunt offering no resistance, but inquiring on her way out, ‘What he come there for, then?’ with implacable animosity.", "핀칭 씨의 고모는 아무 저항도 하지 않았으나, 나가는 길에 누그러지지 않는 적의로 물었다. ‘그럼 저자는 여기 뭐 하러 온 거야?’"]], [["When Flora returned, she explained that her legacy was a clever old lady, but was sometimes a little singular, and ‘took dislikes’--peculiarities of which Flora seemed to be proud rather than otherwise.", "플로라가 돌아와서는, 자기 유산이 영리한 노부인이긴 한데 가끔 좀 별나서 ‘사람을 싫어하기도’ 한다고 설명했다 — 플로라는 오히려 그런 별난 점들을 자랑스러워하는 듯했다."], ["As Flora’s good nature shone in the case, Clennam had no fault to find with the old lady for eliciting it, now that he was relieved from the terrors of her presence; and they took a glass or two of wine in peace.", "이 일에서 플로라의 선량함이 빛났으므로, 이제 그 노부인의 존재가 주던 공포에서 벗어난 클레넘으로서는, 그 선량함을 끌어냈다 하여 노부인을 나무랄 마음이 없었다. 그래서 그들은 평온하게 와인을 한두 잔 마셨다."], ["Foreseeing then that the Pancks would shortly get under weigh, and that the Patriarch would go to sleep, he pleaded the necessity of visiting his mother, and asked Mr Pancks in which direction he was going?", "이윽고 그는 팽크스가 곧 출항할 것이고 가부장이 잠들 것임을 내다보고서, 어머니를 방문해야 할 사정이 있다는 핑계를 대며, 팽크스 씨에게 어느 방향으로 가느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Citywards, sir,’ said Pancks.", "‘시내 쪽입니다, 손님.’ 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘Shall we walk together?’ said Arthur.", "‘같이 걸을까요?’ 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Quite agreeable,’ said Pancks.", "‘얼마든지요.’ 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["Meanwhile Flora was murmuring in rapid snatches for his ear, that there was a time and that the past was a yawning gulf however and that a golden chain no longer bound him and that she revered the memory of the late Mr F.", "그러는 동안 플로라는 그의 귀에 대고 빠른 토막말로 속삭이고 있었다."], ["and that she should be at home to-morrow at half-past one and that the decrees of Fate were beyond recall and that she considered nothing so improbable as that he ever walked on the north-west side of Gray’s-Inn Gardens at exactly four o’clock in the afternoon.", "한때가 있었다고, 그래도 과거는 입을 떡 벌린 심연이라고, 그리고 황금 사슬이 이제 더는 그를 옭아매지 않는다고, 그리고 자기는 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 추억을 존중한다고, 그리고 내일 한 시 반에 자기가 집에 있을 거라고, 그리고 운명의 명령은 거두어들일 수 없는 것이라고, 그리고 그가 오후 정확히 네 시에 그레이즈 인 가든의 북서쪽을 걷는다는 것만큼 있을 법하지 않은 일도 없다고 자기는 여긴다고."], ["He tried at parting to give his hand in frankness to the existing Flora--not the vanished Flora, or the mermaid--but Flora wouldn’t have it, couldn’t have it, was wholly destitute of the power of separating herself and him from their bygone characters.", "헤어질 때 그는 솔직한 마음으로 지금의 플로라에게 — 사라진 플로라도, 그 인어도 아닌 플로라에게 — 손을 내밀려 했으나, 플로라는 그러려 하지 않았고, 그럴 수 없었으며, 자신과 그를 지나간 그 배역에서 떼어 낼 능력이 도무지 없었다."], ["He left the house miserably enough; and so much more light-headed than ever, that if it had not been his good fortune to be towed away, he might, for the first quarter of an hour, have drifted anywhere.", "그는 더없이 비참한 심정으로 그 집을 떠났다. 그리고 어느 때보다도 머리가 어질어질했으니, 만약 끌려가는 행운을 누리지 못했더라면 처음 십오 분쯤은 어디로든 정처 없이 떠밀려 갔을지도 모른다."]], [["When he began to come to himself, in the cooler air and the absence of Flora, he found Pancks at full speed, cropping such scanty pasturage of nails as he could find, and snorting at intervals.", "서늘한 공기 속에서, 플로라가 없는 곳에서 정신을 차리기 시작하자, 그는 팽크스가 전속력으로, 그나마 찾아낼 수 있는 그 얼마 안 되는 손톱의 목초를 뜯어 먹으며, 이따금 콧방귀를 뀌고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["These, in conjunction with one hand in his pocket and his roughened hat hind side before, were evidently the conditions under which he reflected.", "이것들은, 한 손을 주머니에 찌르고 우글쭈글한 모자를 앞뒤로 뒤집어쓴 모습과 더불어, 그가 생각에 잠겼을 때의 분명한 상태였다."]], [["‘A fresh night!’ said Arthur.", "‘쌀쌀한 밤이군요!’ 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, it’s pretty fresh,’ assented Pancks.", "‘예, 꽤 쌀쌀하네요.’ 팽크스가 동의했다."], ["‘As a stranger you feel the climate more than I do, I dare say.", "‘이방인이시라 저보다 기후를 더 느끼시겠지요."], ["Indeed I haven’t got time to feel it.’", "사실 저야 그걸 느낄 새도 없습니다만.’"]], [["‘You lead such a busy life?’", "‘그렇게 바쁘게 사십니까?’"]], [["‘Yes, I have always some of ‘em to look up, or something to look after.", "‘예, 늘 찾아내야 할 누군가가 있거나, 챙겨야 할 무언가가 있지요."], ["But I like business,’ said Pancks, getting on a little faster.", "그래도 저는 일이 좋습니다,’ 팽크스가 걸음을 조금 더 빨리하며 말했다."], ["‘What’s a man made for?’", "‘사람이 뭐 하러 생겨났겠습니까?’"]], [["‘For nothing else?’ said Clennam.", "‘다른 건 없습니까?’ 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["Pancks put the counter question, ‘What else?’ It packed up, in the smallest compass, a weight that had rested on Clennam’s life; and he made no answer.", "팽크스가 되물었다. ‘다른 게 뭐가 있습니까?’ 그 말은, 클레넘의 삶을 짓눌러 온 무게를 가장 작은 부피로 압축해 담고 있었다. 그는 아무 대답도 하지 못했다."]], [["‘That’s what I ask our weekly tenants,’ said Pancks.", "‘그게 제가 우리 주간 임차인들한테 묻는 말입니다,’ 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Some of ‘em will pull long faces to me, and say, Poor as you see us, master, we’re always grinding, drudging, toiling, every minute we’re awake.", "‘그들 중 더러는 제게 울상을 지으며 말하지요. 보시다시피 가난하지만, 나리, 우린 깨어 있는 매 순간 갈고, 죽도록 일하고, 뼈 빠지게 노동한다고요."], ["I say to them, What else are you made for?", "그럼 제가 그들한테 말합니다. 그럼 그것 말고 뭐 하러 생겨났나?"], ["It shuts them up.", "그러면 그들 입이 딱 막히지요."], ["They haven’t a word to answer.", "한마디도 대꾸를 못 합니다."], ["What else are you made for?", "그것 말고 뭐 하러 생겨났나?"], ["That clinches it.’", "그 말이 못을 박는 겁니다.’"]], [["‘Ah dear, dear, dear!’ sighed Clennam.", "‘아 저런, 저런, 저런!’ 클레넘이 한숨지었다."]], [["‘Here am I,’ said Pancks, pursuing his argument with the weekly tenant.", "‘여기 저를 보십시오,’ 팽크스가 주간 임차인을 상대로 하던 그 논리를 이어 갔다."], ["‘What else do you suppose I think I am made for?", "‘제가 뭐 하러 생겨났다고 생각하시겠습니까? 아무것도 없지요."], ["Nothing. Rattle me out of bed early, set me going, give me as short a time as you like to bolt my meals in, and keep me at it.", "아침 일찍 침대에서 절 흔들어 깨워, 일을 시키고, 끼니 욱여넣을 시간은 마음껏 짧게 주고, 계속 부려 먹는 겁니다."], ["Keep me always at it, and I’ll keep you always at it, you keep somebody else always at it.", "절 계속 부려 먹으면, 저도 사장님을 계속 부려 먹고, 사장님은 또 다른 누군가를 계속 부려 먹지요."], ["There you are with the Whole Duty of Man in a commercial country.’", "자, 그게 바로 상업 국가에서 인간이 마땅히 해야 할 모든 의무라는 겁니다.’"]], [["When they had walked a little further in silence, Clennam said: ‘Have you no taste for anything, Mr Pancks?’", "말없이 조금 더 걸은 뒤에, 클레넘이 말했다. ‘당신은 무언가에 취미가 없으십니까, 팽크스 씨?’"]], [["‘What’s taste?’ drily retorted Pancks.", "‘취미가 뭡니까?’ 팽크스가 무뚝뚝하게 되받았다."]], [["‘Let us say inclination.’", "‘성향이라고 합시다.’"]], [["‘I have an inclination to get money, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘if you will show me how.’ He blew off that sound again, and it occurred to his companion for the first time that it was his way of laughing.", "‘저는 돈을 버는 데 성향이 있지요, 손님,’ 팽크스가 말했다, ‘방법을 알려 주신다면요.’ 그가 다시 그 소리를 내뿜었고, 그의 동행에게 처음으로 그것이 그가 웃는 방식이라는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["He was a singular man in all respects; he might not have been quite in earnest, but that the short, hard, rapid manner in which he shot out these cinders of principles, as if it were done by mechanical revolvency, seemed irreconcilable with banter.", "그는 모든 면에서 독특한 사람이었다. 어쩌면 그가 아주 진지한 것은 아니었을지도 모르나, 다만 마치 기계적인 회전으로 하듯 이런 원칙의 잿덩이들을 짧고 단단하고 빠르게 토해 내는 그 방식이, 농담과는 도무지 양립할 수 없어 보였다."]], [["‘You are no great reader, I suppose?’ said Clennam.", "‘책은 많이 안 읽으시겠지요, 짐작건대?’ 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Never read anything but letters and accounts.", "‘편지와 장부 말고는 아무것도 안 읽습니다."], ["Never collect anything but advertisements relative to next of kin.", "근친 상속인에 관한 광고 말고는 아무것도 안 모으고요."], ["If _that’s_ a taste, I have got that.", "그게 취미라면, 저한테 그게 있는 거지요."], ["You’re not of the Clennams of Cornwall, Mr Clennam?’", "당신은 콘월의 클레넘 가문은 아니시죠, 클레넘 씨?’"]], [["‘Not that I ever heard of.’", "‘제가 들어 본 바로는 아닙니다.’"]], [["‘I know you’re not.", "‘아니란 거 압니다."], ["I asked your mother, sir.", "제가 당신 어머님께 여쭤봤거든요, 손님."], ["She has too much character to let a chance escape her.’", "그분은 기회를 놓치기엔 너무 야무진 분이시지요.’"]], [["‘Supposing I had been of the Clennams of Cornwall?’", "‘제가 콘월의 클레넘 가문이었다고 칩시다.’"]], [["‘You’d have heard of something to your advantage.’", "‘그러면 당신께 이로운 무언가를 들으셨을 겁니다.’"]], [["‘Indeed!", "‘그래요!"], ["I have heard of little enough to my advantage for some time.’", "한동안 제게 이로운 거라곤 들어 본 게 거의 없는데요.’"]], [["‘There’s a Cornish property going a begging, sir, and not a Cornish Clennam to have it for the asking,’ said Pancks, taking his note-book from his breast pocket and putting it in again.", "‘콘월의 어느 재산 하나가 임자를 찾고 있는데, 청하기만 하면 그걸 가질 콘월의 클레넘이 하나도 없지요, 손님.’ 팽크스가 가슴팍 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼냈다가 도로 집어넣으며 말했다."], ["‘I turn off here.", "‘저는 여기서 갈라집니다."], ["I wish you good night.’", "그럼 안녕히 가십시오.’"]], [["‘Good night!’ said Clennam.", "‘안녕히 가십시오!’ 클레넘이 말했다."], ["But the Tug, suddenly lightened, and untrammelled by having any weight in tow, was already puffing away into the distance.", "그러나 그 예인선은, 갑자기 짐을 던 채, 끌고 가던 무게에서 풀려나, 벌써 푹푹대며 저 멀리로 멀어져 가고 있었다."]], [["They had crossed Smithfield together, and Clennam was left alone at the corner of Barbican.", "그들은 함께 스미스필드를 가로질러 왔고, 클레넘은 바비컨 모퉁이에 홀로 남겨졌다."], ["He had no intention of presenting himself in his mother’s dismal room that night, and could not have felt more depressed and cast away if he had been in a wilderness.", "그는 그날 밤 어머니의 음울한 방에 모습을 드러낼 생각이 없었고, 광야에 있다 한들 이보다 더 침울하고 버림받은 기분이 들 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["He turned slowly down Aldersgate Street, and was pondering his way along towards Saint Paul’s, purposing to come into one of the great thoroughfares for the sake of their light and life, when a crowd of people flocked towards him on the same pavement, and he stood aside against a shop to let them pass.", "그는 천천히 올더스게이트가를 내려가며, 빛과 활기를 누리려고 큰 길 가운데 하나로 들어설 작정으로 세인트폴 쪽으로 길을 더듬어 가고 있었는데, 그때 한 무리의 사람들이 같은 보도 위로 그를 향해 몰려왔고, 그는 그들이 지나가도록 어느 가게에 붙어 비켜섰다."], ["As they came up, he made out that they were gathered around a something that was carried on men’s shoulders.", "그들이 다가오자, 그는 그들이 사람들의 어깨에 떠메인 무언가를 둘러싸고 모여 있음을 알아보았다."], ["He soon saw that it was a litter, hastily made of a shutter or some such thing; and a recumbent figure upon it, and the scraps of conversation in the crowd, and a muddy bundle carried by one man, and a muddy hat carried by another, informed him that an accident had occurred.", "그는 곧 그것이 덧문이나 그 비슷한 것으로 급히 만든 들것임을, 그리고 그 위에 누워 있는 형체임을 알아보았으며, 무리 속의 토막난 대화와, 한 남자가 든 진흙투성이 보따리와, 또 다른 남자가 든 진흙투성이 모자가, 사고가 일어났음을 그에게 알려 주었다."], ["The litter stopped under a lamp before it had passed him half-a-dozen paces, for some readjustment of the burden; and, the crowd stopping too, he found himself in the midst of the array.", "들것은 그를 여섯 걸음쯤 지나가기도 전에, 짐을 다시 손보려고 어느 가로등 아래 멈춰 섰고, 무리도 함께 멈춰 서는 바람에, 그는 그 행렬 한가운데에 서 있게 되었다."]], [["‘An accident going to the Hospital?’ he asked an old man beside him, who stood shaking his head, inviting conversation.", "‘병원으로 가는 사고입니까?’ 그가 곁에 있던 한 노인에게 물었는데, 노인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말을 걸어 달라는 듯이 서 있었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the man, ‘along of them Mails.", "‘그렇소,’ 노인이 말했다, ‘저 우편마차들 때문이오."], ["They ought to be prosecuted and fined, them Mails.", "저 우편마차들은 기소해서 벌금을 물려야 하오."], ["They come a racing out of Lad Lane and Wood Street at twelve or fourteen mile a hour, them Mails do.", "저 우편마차들은 래드 레인이며 우드 스트리트에서 시속 십이 마일이나 십사 마일로 쌩쌩 달려 나온다오, 저 우편마차들이 말이오."], ["The only wonder is, that people ain’t killed oftener by them Mails.’", "저 우편마차들 때문에 사람이 더 자주 안 죽는 게 그저 신기할 따름이지.’"]], [["‘This person is not killed, I hope?’", "‘이 사람은 죽지는 않았겠지요?’"]], [["‘I don’t know!’ said the man, ‘it an’t for the want of a will in them Mails, if he an’t.’ The speaker having folded his arms, and set in comfortably to address his depreciation of them Mails to any of the bystanders who would listen, several voices, out of pure sympathy with the sufferer, confirmed him; one voice saying to Clennam, ‘They’re a public nuisance, them Mails, sir;’ another, ‘_I_ see one on ‘em pull up within half a inch of a boy, last night;’ another, ‘_I_ see one on ‘em go over a cat, sir--and it might have been your own mother;’ and all representing, by implication, that if he happened to possess any public influence, he could not use it better than against them Mails.", "‘낸들 아오!’ 노인이 말했다, ‘죽지 않았다 해도 저 우편마차들에 그럴 뜻이 없어서 그런 건 아니오.’ 화자는 팔짱을 끼고서, 들어 줄 만한 구경꾼이라면 누구에게든 저 우편마차들에 대한 자기 비방을 편안히 늘어놓기 시작했고, 순전히 그 부상자에 대한 동정심에서 여러 목소리가 그를 거들었다. 한 목소리는 클레넘에게 ‘저 우편마차들은 공중의 골칫거리예요, 손님’이라 했고, 또 한 목소리는 ‘제가 어젯밤 저것들 중 하나가 한 아이한테서 반 인치도 안 되는 데서 멈추는 걸 봤다니까요’라 했으며, 또 한 목소리는 ‘제가 저것들 중 하나가 고양이 한 마리를 깔아뭉개는 걸 봤어요, 손님 — 그게 댁의 어머니였을 수도 있는 거라고요’라 했다. 그리고 모두가, 만약 그에게 무슨 공적인 영향력이라도 있다면 저 우편마차들에 맞서는 데 쓰는 것보다 더 나은 쓰임은 없으리라는 뜻을 은연중에 내비쳤다."]], [["‘Why, a native Englishman is put to it every night of his life, to save his life from them Mails,’ argued the first old man; ‘and _he_ knows when they’re a coming round the corner, to tear him limb from limb.", "‘아니, 토박이 영국인도 저 우편마차들로부터 제 목숨을 지키느라 평생 밤마다 곤욕을 치르는 판이오,’ 첫 번째 노인이 주장했다. ‘그리고 그자는 그것들이 모퉁이를 돌아 달려와 제 팔다리를 갈가리 찢어 놓으려 할 때를 알기라도 하지."], ["What can you expect from a poor foreigner who don’t know nothing about ‘em!’", "그것들에 관해 아무것도 모르는 가엾은 외국인한테서야 무얼 바랄 수 있겠소!’"]], [["‘Is this a foreigner?’ said Clennam, leaning forward to look.", "‘이 사람이 외국인입니까?’ 클레넘이 들여다보려고 몸을 앞으로 기울이며 말했다."]], [["In the midst of such replies as ‘Frenchman, sir,’ ‘Porteghee, sir,’ ‘Dutchman, sir,’ ‘Prooshan, sir,’ and other conflicting testimony, he now heard a feeble voice asking, both in Italian and in French, for water.", "‘프랑스인입니다, 손님’, ‘포르투갈인입니다, 손님’, ‘네덜란드인입니다, 손님’, ‘프로이센인입니다, 손님’ 따위의, 그리고 그 밖의 서로 엇갈리는 증언들이 오가는 가운데, 그는 이제 어떤 가냘픈 목소리가 이탈리아어로도 프랑스어로도 물을 청하는 것을 들었다."], ["A general remark going round, in reply, of ‘Ah, poor fellow, he says he’ll never get over it; and no wonder!’ Clennam begged to be allowed to pass, as he understood the poor creature.", "답으로 ‘아, 가엾은 친구, 자긴 절대 못 이겨 낼 거라는데, 그도 그럴 만하지!’라는 말이 한 바퀴 두루 돌자, 클레넘은 자기가 저 가엾은 사람의 말을 알아듣는다며 지나가게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He was immediately handed to the front, to speak to him.", "그는 곧장 앞으로 이끌려, 그에게 말을 걸게 되었다."]], [["‘First, he wants some water,’ said he, looking round.", "‘먼저, 이분은 물을 좀 원합니다.’ 그가 둘러보며 말했다."], ["(A dozen good fellows dispersed to get it.) ‘Are you badly hurt, my friend?’ he asked the man on the litter, in Italian.", "(착한 친구 여남은 명이 그것을 구하러 흩어졌다.) ‘심하게 다치셨소, 친구?’ 그가 이탈리아어로 들것 위의 남자에게 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, sir; yes, yes, yes.", "‘예, 선생님. 예, 예, 예."], ["It’s my leg, it’s my leg.", "제 다리예요, 제 다리."], ["But it pleases me to hear the old music, though I am very bad.’", "하지만 이렇게 몸이 안 좋아도, 그 옛 음악을 들으니 기쁩니다.’"]], [["‘You are a traveller!", "‘당신은 여행자로군!"], ["Stay!", "가만!"], ["See, the water!", "자, 물이오!"], ["Let me give you some.’", "좀 드시지요.’"]], [["They had rested the litter on a pile of paving stones.", "그들은 들것을 포석을 쌓아 놓은 무더기 위에 내려놓았다."], ["It was at a convenient height from the ground, and by stooping he could lightly raise the head with one hand and hold the glass to his lips with the other.", "그것은 땅에서 알맞은 높이여서, 그는 몸을 숙여 한 손으로 머리를 살짝 들어 올리고 다른 손으로 잔을 그의 입술에 대 줄 수 있었다."], ["A little, muscular, brown man, with black hair and white teeth.", "검은 머리에 하얀 이를 가진, 작고 다부진 갈색 피부의 남자였다."], ["A lively face, apparently.", "보아하니 생기 있는 얼굴이었다."], ["Earrings in his ears.", "귀에는 귀걸이를 했다."]], [["‘That’s well.", "‘됐소."], ["You are a traveller?’", "당신은 여행자요?’"]], [["‘Surely, sir.’", "‘그렇습니다, 선생님.’"]], [["‘A stranger in this city?’", "‘이 도시에 처음 오셨소?’"]], [["‘Surely, surely, altogether.", "‘그렇고말고요, 그렇고말고요, 완전히요."], ["I am arrived this unhappy evening.’", "저는 이 불행한 저녁에 막 도착했습니다.’"]], [["‘From what country?’", "‘어느 나라에서요?’"]], [["‘Marseilles.’", "‘마르세유에서요.’"]], [["‘Why, see there! I also!", "‘아니, 저것 보시오!"], ["Almost as much a stranger here as you, though born here, I came from Marseilles a little while ago.", "나도 그렇소! 나도 여기서는 거의 당신만큼이나 낯선 사람이라오, 여기서 태어나기는 했지만, 나도 얼마 전에 마르세유에서 왔소."], ["Don’t be cast down.’ The face looked up at him imploringly, as he rose from wiping it, and gently replaced the coat that covered the writhing figure.", "기운 내시오.’ 그가 얼굴을 닦아 주고 몸을 일으키자, 그 얼굴이 애원하듯 그를 올려다보았고, 그는 몸부림치는 형체를 덮은 외투를 가만히 다시 여며 주었다."], ["‘I won’t leave you till you shall be well taken care of. Courage!", "‘당신이 잘 보살핌받게 될 때까지 떠나지 않겠소."], ["You will be very much better half an hour hence.’", "용기를 내요! 반시간만 지나면 훨씬 나아질 거요.’"]], [["‘Ah!", "‘아!"], ["Altro, Altro!’ cried the poor little man, in a faintly incredulous tone; and as they took him up, hung out his right hand to give the forefinger a back-handed shake in the air.", "알트로, 알트로!’ 가엾은 작은 남자가 가냘프게, 믿기지 않는다는 어조로 외쳤다. 그리고 그들이 그를 들어 올릴 때, 오른손을 뻗어 손가락을 허공에 대고 손등으로 까딱까딱 흔들어 보였다."]], [["Arthur Clennam turned; and walking beside the litter, and saying an encouraging word now and then, accompanied it to the neighbouring hospital of Saint Bartholomew.", "* 알트로(Altro): 이탈리아어로 ‘그럼요’, ‘설마요’ 등을 뜻하는 감탄사. 아서 클레넘은 돌아서서, 들것 곁을 걸으며, 이따금 격려의 말을 건네면서, 그것을 따라 인근의 세인트바살러뮤 병원까지 동행했다."], ["None of the crowd but the bearers and he being admitted, the disabled man was soon laid on a table in a cool, methodical way, and carefully examined by a surgeon who was as near at hand, and as ready to appear as Calamity herself.", "무리 가운데 운반하는 자들과 그 말고는 아무도 들이지 않은 채, 그 불구가 된 남자는 곧 서늘하고 질서정연한 방식으로 탁자 위에 뉘어졌고, 마치 재앙의 여신 그 자체만큼이나 가까이 대기해 있다가 곧장 나타날 채비가 된 한 외과의에게 신중히 진찰받았다."], ["‘He hardly knows an English word,’ said Clennam; ‘is he badly hurt?’", "‘이 사람은 영어를 거의 한마디도 모릅니다.’ 클레넘이 말했다. ‘심하게 다쳤습니까?’"]], [["‘Let us know all about it first,’ said the surgeon, continuing his examination with a businesslike delight in it, ‘before we pronounce.’", "‘진단을 내리기 전에,’ 외과의가, 일에 임하는 사람다운 흥미를 띠고 진찰을 이어 가며 말했다, ‘우선 다 알아봅시다.’"]], [["After trying the leg with a finger, and two fingers, and one hand and two hands, and over and under, and up and down, and in this direction and in that, and approvingly remarking on the points of interest to another gentleman who joined him, the surgeon at last clapped the patient on the shoulder, and said, ‘He won’t hurt.", "손가락 하나로, 또 손가락 둘로, 또 한 손으로, 또 두 손으로, 위로 아래로, 또 이쪽으로 저쪽으로 다리를 살펴보고, 자기에게 합류한 다른 신사에게 흥미로운 점들을 흡족하게 짚어 보인 끝에, 외과의는 마침내 환자의 어깨를 탁 치며 말했다. ‘이 사람 괜찮을 겁니다."], ["He’ll do very well.", "아주 잘 회복될 거예요."], ["It’s difficult enough, but we shall not want him to part with his leg this time.’ Which Clennam interpreted to the patient, who was full of gratitude, and, in his demonstrative way, kissed both the interpreter’s hand and the surgeon’s several times.", "꽤 까다롭긴 해도, 이번엔 다리를 떼어 낼 필요는 없겠어요.’ 클레넘이 이를 환자에게 통역해 주자, 환자는 감사로 가득 차서, 제 특유의 격한 방식으로 통역하는 이의 손과 외과의의 손에 여러 번 입을 맞췄다."]], [["‘It’s a serious injury, I suppose?’ said Clennam.", "‘심각한 부상인 모양이지요?’ 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Ye-es,’ replied the surgeon, with the thoughtful pleasure of an artist contemplating the work upon his easel.", "‘그-렇지요,’ 외과의가, 이젤 위의 작품을 음미하는 화가의 사색적인 기쁨으로 답했다."], ["‘Yes, it’s enough.", "‘예, 충분히 그렇습니다."], ["There’s a compound fracture above the knee, and a dislocation below.", "무릎 위로 복합 골절이 있고, 아래로는 탈구가 있어요."], ["They are both of a beautiful kind.’ He gave the patient a friendly clap on the shoulder again, as if he really felt that he was a very good fellow indeed, and worthy of all commendation for having broken his leg in a manner interesting to science.", "둘 다 아름다운 종류지요.’ 그는 다시 환자의 어깨를 다정하게 탁 쳤는데, 마치 이 사람이 정말로 무척 좋은 친구이며, 과학에 흥미로운 방식으로 다리를 부러뜨린 것에 대해 온갖 칭찬을 받을 만하다고 진심으로 여기는 듯했다."]], [["‘He speaks French?’ said the surgeon.", "‘이 사람 프랑스어를 합니까?’ 외과의가 말했다."]], [["‘Oh yes, he speaks French.’", "‘오 예, 프랑스어를 합니다.’"]], [["‘He’ll be at no loss here, then.--You have only to bear a little pain like a brave fellow, my friend, and to be thankful that all goes as well as it does,’ he added, in that tongue, ‘and you’ll walk again to a marvel.", "‘그럼 여기서 곤란할 일은 없겠군. — 당신은 그저 용감한 사내답게 약간의 통증만 견디면 되고, 모든 게 이만큼 잘 풀리는 걸 고맙게 여기면 되오, 친구,’ 그가 그 말로 덧붙였다, ‘그러면 놀랄 만큼 멀쩡히 다시 걷게 될 거요."], ["Now, let us see whether there’s anything else the matter, and how our ribs are?’", "자, 이제 다른 데는 이상이 없는지, 갈비뼈는 어떤지 봅시다.’"]], [["There was nothing else the matter, and our ribs were sound.", "다른 데는 이상이 없었고, 갈비뼈는 멀쩡했다."], ["Clennam remained until everything possible to be done had been skilfully and promptly done--the poor belated wanderer in a strange land movingly besought that favour of him--and lingered by the bed to which he was in due time removed, until he had fallen into a doze.", "클레넘은 할 수 있는 모든 처치가 능숙하고 신속하게 다 이루어질 때까지 남아 있었고 — 낯선 땅에서 길 잃은 그 가엾은 방랑자가 그 호의를 애처롭게 그에게 간청했던 것이다 — 그가 때맞춰 옮겨진 침대 곁에서, 그가 선잠에 빠질 때까지 머물렀다."], ["Even then he wrote a few words for him on his card, with a promise to return to-morrow, and left it to be given to him when he should awake.", "그러고 나서도 그는 그를 위해 자기 명함에 몇 마디를 적어, 내일 다시 오겠다는 약속과 함께, 그가 깨어나면 건네주도록 남겨 두었다."]], [["All these proceedings occupied so long that it struck eleven o’clock at night as he came out at the Hospital Gate.", "이 모든 절차가 어찌나 오래 걸렸던지, 그가 병원 정문을 나설 때 밤 열한 시를 알리는 종이 울렸다."], ["He had hired a lodging for the present in Covent Garden, and he took the nearest way to that quarter, by Snow Hill and Holborn.", "그는 당분간 코번트 가든에 거처를 빌려 두고 있었고, 스노 힐과 홀번을 거쳐 그쪽으로 가는 가장 가까운 길을 택했다."]], [["Left to himself again, after the solicitude and compassion of his last adventure, he was naturally in a thoughtful mood.", "마지막 모험의 노심초사와 연민이 지난 뒤, 다시 혼자가 되자, 그는 자연히 생각에 잠긴 기분이 되었다."], ["As naturally, he could not walk on thinking for ten minutes without recalling Flora.", "또한 자연히, 그는 십 분도 생각에 잠겨 걷지 못하고 플로라를 떠올렸다."], ["She necessarily recalled to him his life, with all its misdirection and little happiness.", "그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 자신의 삶을, 그 온갖 어긋남과 보잘것없는 행복과 더불어 떠올리게 했다."]], [["When he got to his lodging, he sat down before the dying fire, as he had stood at the window of his old room looking out upon the blackened forest of chimneys, and turned his gaze back upon the gloomy vista by which he had come to that stage in his existence.", "거처에 다다르자, 그는, 옛 방의 창가에 서서 그을음으로 검게 된 굴뚝의 숲을 내다보았던 것처럼, 꺼져 가는 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 자신을 지금 이 삶의 단계까지 데려온 그 음울한 행로를 돌아보았다. 그토록 길고, 그토록 헐벗고, 그토록 텅 빈 행로였다."], ["So long, so bare, so blank. No childhood; no youth, except for one remembrance; that one remembrance proved, only that day, to be a piece of folly.", "유년도 없고, 단 하나의 추억을 빼고는 청춘도 없었으며, 바로 그 하나의 추억마저 바로 그날 한낱 어리석음의 조각이었음이 드러난 터였다."]], [["It was a misfortune to him, trifle as it might have been to another.", "남에게는 사소한 일일지 몰라도, 그에게는 불행이었다."], ["For, while all that was hard and stern in his recollection, remained Reality on being proved--was obdurate to the sight and touch, and relaxed nothing of its old indomitable grimness--the one tender recollection of his experience would not bear the same test, and melted away.", "그의 기억 속에서 모질고 가혹한 모든 것은, 막상 확인해 보니 여전히 현실로 남아 — 눈으로 보고 손으로 만져 보아도 완강했고, 그 오랜 불굴의 음울함을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았건만 — 그의 경험 가운데 단 하나 다정했던 그 추억만은 같은 시험을 견디지 못하고 녹아 사라졌던 것이다."], ["He had foreseen this, on the former night, when he had dreamed with waking eyes, but he had not felt it then; and he had now.", "그는 지난밤, 눈을 뜬 채 꿈을 꿀 때 이를 예감하기는 했으나, 그때는 그것을 느끼지 못했다. 그런데 이제는 느꼈다."]], [["He was a dreamer in such wise, because he was a man who had, deep-rooted in his nature, a belief in all the gentle and good things his life had been without.", "그가 그런 식으로 몽상가였던 것은, 그가 본성 깊숙이, 자기 삶에 없었던 온갖 다정하고 선한 것들에 대한 믿음을 뿌리내린 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["Bred in meanness and hard dealing, this had rescued him to be a man of honourable mind and open hand.", "옹졸함과 모진 거래 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 명예로운 마음과 후한 손을 지닌 사람이 되게 했다."], ["Bred in coldness and severity, this had rescued him to have a warm and sympathetic heart.", "냉랭함과 가혹함 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 따뜻하고 공감할 줄 아는 마음을 지니게 했다."], ["Bred in a creed too darkly audacious to pursue, through its process of reserving the making of man in the image of his Creator to the making of his Creator in the image of an erring man, this had rescued him to judge not, and in humility to be merciful, and have hope and charity.", "인간을 그 창조주의 형상으로 빚는 것을 거두어 도리어 창조주를 잘못 저지르는 인간의 형상으로 빚는 그 과정을 따라가는, 끝까지 좇기엔 너무도 어둡고 무엄한 신조 속에서 자랐으되, 이 믿음이 그를 건져 내어 심판하지 않고, 겸손하게 자비를 베풀며, 희망과 사랑을 품게 했다."]], [["And this saved him still from the whimpering weakness and cruel selfishness of holding that because such a happiness or such a virtue had not come into his little path, or worked well for him, therefore it was not in the great scheme, but was reducible, when found in appearance, to the basest elements.", "그리고 이 믿음은, 그러한 행복이나 그러한 덕이 자기의 보잘것없는 길에는 찾아오지 않았다거나 자기에게 잘 작용하지 않았다고 해서, 그것이 그 위대한 섭리 안에 없는 것이라거나, 겉으로 드러날 때조차 가장 비천한 요소로 환원될 수 있는 것이라고 여기는 그 칭얼대는 나약함과 잔인한 이기심으로부터도 여전히 그를 지켜 주었다."], ["A disappointed mind he had, but a mind too firm and healthy for such unwholesome air.", "그는 실망한 마음을 지녔으되, 그런 해로운 공기에 물들기엔 너무도 굳세고 건강한 마음을 지니고 있었다."], ["Leaving himself in the dark, it could rise into the light, seeing it shine on others and hailing it.", "제 자신은 어둠 속에 둔 채로도, 그 마음은 빛 속으로 떠올라, 그 빛이 다른 이들 위에 비치는 것을 보며 그것을 반길 수 있었다."]], [["Therefore, he sat before his dying fire, sorrowful to think upon the way by which he had come to that night, yet not strewing poison on the way by which other men had come to it.", "그리하여 그는 꺼져 가는 난롯불 앞에 앉아, 자신이 그날 밤에 이르기까지 걸어온 그 길을 생각하며 슬퍼했으되, 다른 이들이 그 길에 이르러 온 행로 위에는 독을 뿌리지 않았다."], ["That he should have missed so much, and at his time of life should look so far about him for any staff to bear him company upon his downward journey and cheer it, was a just regret.", "그가 그토록 많은 것을 놓쳤다는 것, 그리고 그의 나이에 이르러, 내리막 여정에서 그를 벗하며 길동무가 되고 그것을 북돋워 줄 어떤 지팡이라도 찾으려 이렇게 멀리 두리번거려야 한다는 것은, 정당한 회한이었다."], ["He looked at the fire from which the blaze departed, from which the afterglow subsided, in which the ashes turned grey, from which they dropped to dust, and thought, ‘How soon I too shall pass through such changes, and be gone!’", "그는, 불꽃이 떠나가고, 그 잔광이 사그라들고, 재가 잿빛으로 변하고, 그 재가 먼지로 부스러져 내리는 난롯불을 바라보며 생각했다. ‘나 또한 머잖아 이런 변화들을 거쳐, 사라지고 말겠지!’"]], [["To review his life was like descending a green tree in fruit and flower, and seeing all the branches wither and drop off, one by one, as he came down towards them.", "자신의 삶을 돌이켜 보는 것은, 열매와 꽃이 달린 푸른 나무를 내려오면서, 그쪽으로 내려갈수록 모든 가지가 하나씩 하나씩 시들어 떨어지는 것을 보는 일과도 같았다."]], [["‘From the unhappy suppression of my youngest days, through the rigid and unloving home that followed them, through my departure, my long exile, my return, my mother’s welcome, my intercourse with her since, down to the afternoon of this day with poor Flora,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘what have I found!’", "‘유년 시절의 그 불행한 억압에서부터, 그 뒤를 이은 엄격하고 사랑 없는 가정을 지나, 나의 떠남, 나의 긴 유랑, 나의 귀향, 어머니의 환영, 그 이래 어머니와의 교류를 거쳐, 가엾은 플로라와 보낸 오늘 오후에 이르기까지,’ 아서 클레넘이 말했다, ‘내가 찾아낸 것이 무엇인가!’"]], [["His door was softly opened, and these spoken words startled him, and came as if they were an answer:", "그의 방문이 가만히 열렸고, 이렇게 소리 내어 한 말들이 그를 흠칫 놀라게 했으니, 마치 그것이 하나의 대답이기라도 한 듯 이런 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["‘Little Dorrit.’", "‘리틀 도릿이에요.’"]], [["# CHAPTER 14. Little Dorrit’s Party", "# CHAPTER 14. Little Dorrit’s Party"]], [["Arthur Clennam rose hastily, and saw her standing at the door.", "아서 클레넘은 황급히 일어나 문가에 서 있는 그녀를 보았다."], ["This history must sometimes see with Little Dorrit’s eyes, and shall begin that course by seeing him.", "이 이야기는 때로 리틀 도릿의 눈으로 보아야 할 것이며, 그 첫걸음으로 그를 보는 데서 시작하기로 한다."]], [["Little Dorrit looked into a dim room, which seemed a spacious one to her, and grandly furnished. Courtly ideas of Covent Garden, as a place with famous coffee-houses, where gentlemen wearing gold-laced coats and swords had quarrelled and fought duels; costly ideas of Covent Garden, as a place where there were flowers in winter at guineas a-piece, pine-apples at guineas a pound, and peas at guineas a pint; picturesque ideas of Covent Garden, as a place where there was a mighty theatre, showing wonderful and beautiful sights to richly-dressed ladies and gentlemen, and which was for ever far beyond the reach of poor Fanny or poor uncle; desolate ideas of Covent Garden, as having all those arches in it, where the miserable children in rags among whom she had just now passed, like young rats, slunk and hid, fed on offal, huddled together for warmth, and were hunted about (look to the rats young and old, all ye Barnacles, for before God they are eating away our foundations, and will bring the roofs on our heads!); teeming ideas of Covent Garden, as a place of past and present mystery, romance, abundance, want, beauty, ugliness, fair country gardens, and foul street gutters; all confused together,--made the room dimmer than it was in Little Dorrit’s eyes, as they timidly saw it from the door.", "리틀 도릿은 어둑한 방 안을 들여다보았는데, 그녀에게는 그 방이 널찍하고 호화롭게 꾸며진 것처럼 보였다. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 격조 높은 상념들 — 금실로 장식한 외투를 걸치고 칼을 찬 신사들이 다투고 결투를 벌이던, 이름난 커피하우스들이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 값비싼 상념들 — 겨울에도 한 송이에 1기니씩 하는 꽃이 있고, 한 파운드에 1기니씩 하는 파인애플이 있고, 한 파인트에 1기니씩 하는 완두콩이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 그림 같은 상념들 — 화려하게 차려입은 신사 숙녀들에게 경이롭고 아름다운 광경을 보여 주던, 가엾은 패니나 가엾은 삼촌으로서는 영영 닿을 수 없을 만큼 멀리 있던 거대한 극장이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는 황량한 상념들 — 그 모든 아치들이 있어, 방금 그녀가 지나쳐 온, 누더기를 걸친 비참한 아이들이 어린 쥐새끼들처럼 슬그머니 기어들어 숨고, 쓰레기를 주워 먹고, 온기를 찾아 한데 옹송그리고, 이리저리 쫓겨 다니던 곳이라는 상념(어리고 늙은 쥐들을 살펴보라, 너희 모든 배너클들이여, 신께 맹세코 그것들이 우리의 토대를 갉아먹고 있으며, 끝내 지붕을 우리 머리 위로 무너뜨릴 것이니!). 코번트 가든을 두고 떠올리는, 차고 넘치는 상념들 — 과거와 현재의 신비, 로맨스, 풍요, 결핍, 아름다움, 추함, 아름다운 시골 정원과 더러운 거리의 시궁창이 있던 곳이라는 상념. 이 모든 것이 한데 뒤엉켜 — 문가에서 머뭇거리며 살펴보는 리틀 도릿의 눈에 그 방을 실제보다 더 어둑하게 만들었다."]], [["At first in the chair before the gone-out fire, and then turned round wondering to see her, was the gentleman whom she sought.", "처음에는 꺼진 불 앞 의자에 앉아 있다가, 이윽고 그녀를 보고 의아해하며 돌아앉은 이는 바로 그녀가 찾던 신사였다."], ["The brown, grave gentleman, who smiled so pleasantly, who was so frank and considerate in his manner, and yet in whose earnestness there was something that reminded her of his mother, with the great difference that she was earnest in asperity and he in gentleness.", "그토록 유쾌하게 미소 짓고, 태도가 그토록 솔직하고 사려 깊으면서도, 그 진지함 속에는 어딘가 그의 어머니를 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있는, 갈색 머리의 근엄한 신사였다 — 다만 그녀가 매서움 속에서 진지했다면 그는 부드러움 속에서 진지하다는 큰 차이가 있었다."], ["Now he regarded her with that attentive and inquiring look before which Little Dorrit’s eyes had always fallen, and before which they fell still.", "지금 그는 리틀 도릿의 눈길이 늘 떨구어지곤 했던, 그리고 지금도 떨구어지는 그 주의 깊고 캐묻는 듯한 시선으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["‘My poor child!", "\"가엾은 아이야!"], ["Here at midnight?’", "이 한밤중에 여기를?\""]], [["‘I said Little Dorrit, sir, on purpose to prepare you.", "\"일부러 리틀 도릿이라고 말씀드렸어요, 선생님."], ["I knew you must be very much surprised.’", "미리 마음의 준비를 하시도록요. 무척 놀라실 줄 알았거든요.\""]], [["‘Are you alone?’", "\"혼자 왔니?\""]], [["‘No sir, I have got Maggy with me.’", "\"아니요, 선생님, 매기를 데려왔어요.\""]], [["Considering her entrance sufficiently prepared for by this mention of her name, Maggy appeared from the landing outside, on the broad grin.", "자기 이름이 언급되었으니 등장 준비가 충분히 되었다고 여긴 매기가, 바깥 층계참에서 활짝 웃는 얼굴로 나타났다."], ["She instantly suppressed that manifestation, however, and became fixedly solemn.", "그러나 그녀는 이내 그 웃음을 거두고 한결같이 엄숙한 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘And I have no fire,’ said Clennam. ‘And you are--’ He was going to say so lightly clad, but stopped himself in what would have been a reference to her poverty, saying instead, ‘And it is so cold.’", "\"그런데 불이 꺼져 있구나.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"게다가 너는—\" 그는 옷을 너무 얇게 입었다고 말하려다가, 그것이 그녀의 가난을 들추는 일이 될까 봐 말을 멈추고는 대신 이렇게 말했다. \"게다가 날이 이렇게 춥고.\""]], [["Putting the chair from which he had risen nearer to the grate, he made her sit down in it; and hurriedly bringing wood and coal, heaped them together and got a blaze.", "그는 자기가 앉아 있던 의자를 난로 가까이로 끌어다 그녀를 앉히고, 서둘러 장작과 석탄을 가져와 함께 쌓아 올려 불을 피웠다."]], [["‘Your foot is like marble, my child;’ he had happened to touch it, while stooping on one knee at his work of kindling the fire; ‘put it nearer the warmth.’ Little Dorrit thanked him hastily.", "\"네 발이 대리석처럼 차구나, 아이야.\" 그는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고 불을 지피다가 우연히 그녀의 발에 손이 닿았다."], ["It was quite warm, it was very warm!", "\"더 따뜻한 데로 가까이 대렴.\" 리틀 도릿은 황급히 고맙다고 했다."], ["It smote upon his heart to feel that she hid her thin, worn shoe.", "충분히 따뜻하다고, 아주 따뜻하다고 했다! 그녀가 얇고 닳은 신발을 감추는 것을 보자 그의 가슴이 미어졌다."]], [["Little Dorrit was not ashamed of her poor shoes.", "리틀 도릿은 자기 초라한 신발을 부끄러워하는 것이 아니었다. 그는 그녀의 사정을 알고 있었으니, 그것 때문이 아니었다."], ["He knew her story, and it was not that.", "리틀 도릿은 그가 그것을 본다면 자기 아버지를 탓할까 봐 마음이 쓰였던 것이다. 그가 '왜 아버지란 사람은 오늘 만찬을 들면서 이 작은 것을 차가운 돌바닥의 처분에 내맡겼단 말인가!'"], ["Little Dorrit had a misgiving that he might blame her father, if he saw them; that he might think, ‘why did he dine to-day, and leave this little creature to the mercy of the cold stones!’ She had no belief that it would have been a just reflection; she simply knew, by experience, that such delusions did sometimes present themselves to people.", "하고 생각할까 봐서였다. 그것이 온당한 생각이라고 믿어서가 아니었다."], ["It was a part of her father’s misfortunes that they did.", "다만 그녀는 그런 오해가 때로 사람들의 마음에 떠오른다는 것을 경험으로 알고 있을 뿐이었다. 그런 오해가 떠오른다는 것 자체가 아버지가 겪는 불운의 한 부분이었다."]], [["‘Before I say anything else,’ Little Dorrit began, sitting before the pale fire, and raising her eyes again to the face which in its harmonious look of interest, and pity, and protection, she felt to be a mystery far above her in degree, and almost removed beyond her guessing at; ‘may I tell you something, sir?’", "\"다른 말씀을 드리기 전에,\" 리틀 도릿은 희미한 불 앞에 앉아, 관심과 연민과 보호의 빛이 조화롭게 어우러진 그 얼굴 — 자기로서는 도저히 헤아릴 수 없을 만큼 까마득히 높은 곳에 있는 신비처럼 느껴지는 그 얼굴로 다시 눈을 들며 입을 열었다. \"한 가지 여쭙고 싶은 게 있어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Yes, my child.’", "\"그래, 아이야.\""]], [["A slight shade of distress fell upon her, at his so often calling her a child.", "그가 자꾸만 자기를 아이라고 부르는 것에 그녀의 얼굴에 옅은 곤혹의 그림자가 스쳤다."], ["She was surprised that he should see it, or think of such a slight thing; but he said directly:", "그가 그것을 알아채거나 그런 사소한 일을 마음에 둔다는 것이 놀라웠지만, 그는 곧바로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘I wanted a tender word, and could think of no other.", "\"다정한 말을 쓰고 싶었는데, 달리 떠오르는 게 없었단다."], ["As you just now gave yourself the name they give you at my mother’s, and as that is the name by which I always think of you, let me call you Little Dorrit.’", "방금 네가 우리 어머니 댁에서 부르는 이름으로 스스로를 일컬었고, 또 나도 늘 그 이름으로 너를 떠올리니, 그냥 리틀 도릿이라고 부르게 해 다오.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, I should like it better than any name.’", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 어떤 이름보다 그게 더 좋아요.\""]], [["‘Little Dorrit.’", "\"리틀 도릿.\""]], [["‘Little mother,’ Maggy (who had been falling asleep) put in, as a correction.", "\"작은엄마.\" 깜빡 졸고 있던 매기가 고쳐 주듯 끼어들었다."]], [["‘It’s all the same, Maggy,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘all the same.’", "\"다 같은 말이야, 매기.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"다 같은 말이야.\""]], [["‘Is it all the same, mother?’", "\"그게 다 같은 거예요, 엄마?\""]], [["‘Just the same.’", "\"꼭 같은 거야.\""]], [["Maggy laughed, and immediately snored.", "매기는 웃더니 이내 코를 골았다."], ["In Little Dorrit’s eyes and ears, the uncouth figure and the uncouth sound were as pleasant as could be.", "리틀 도릿의 눈과 귀에는 그 투박한 모습도, 그 투박한 소리도 더없이 사랑스러웠다."], ["There was a glow of pride in her big child, overspreading her face, when it again met the eyes of the grave brown gentleman.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 자기 덩치 큰 아이에 대한 자부심의 빛이 번졌고, 그 얼굴이 다시 근엄한 갈색 머리 신사의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["She wondered what he was thinking of, as he looked at Maggy and her.", "그가 매기와 자신을 바라보며 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 그녀는 궁금했다."], ["She thought what a good father he would be.", "그가 얼마나 좋은 아버지가 될까 하고 생각했다."], ["How, with some such look, he would counsel and cherish his daughter.", "저런 눈길로 자기 딸을 가르치고 아껴 주리라고."]], [["‘What I was going to tell you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘is, that my brother is at large.’", "\"제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"제 오빠가 풀려났다는 거예요.\""]], [["Arthur was rejoiced to hear it, and hoped he would do well.", "아서는 그 말을 듣고 기뻐하며, 오빠가 잘 지내기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["‘And what I was going to tell you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit, trembling in all her little figure and in her voice, ‘is, that I am not to know whose generosity released him--am never to ask, and am never to be told, and am never to thank that gentleman with all my grateful heart!’", "\"그리고 제가 말씀드리려던 건요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 작은 몸을 온통 떨며, 목소리마저 떨면서 말했다. \"누구의 너그러움 덕에 오빠가 풀려났는지 저는 알아서는 안 되고, 결코 물어서도 안 되고, 결코 들어서도 안 되고, 이 가슴 가득한 고마움을 담아 그분께 결코 감사를 드려서도 안 된다는 거예요!\""]], [["He would probably need no thanks, Clennam said.", "아마 그분은 감사 따위는 필요로 하지 않을 거라고 클레넘은 말했다."], ["Very likely he would be thankful himself (and with reason), that he had had the means and chance of doing a little service to her, who well deserved a great one.", "도리어 그분은 큰 도움을 받아 마땅한 그녀에게 작은 도움이나마 베풀 수단과 기회가 있었음을 스스로 고맙게 여길 것이라고(또 그럴 만한 이유가 있다고) 했다."]], [["‘And what I was going to say, sir, is,’ said Little Dorrit, trembling more and more, ‘that if I knew him, and I might, I would tell him that he can never, never know how I feel his goodness, and how my good father would feel it. And what I was going to say, sir, is, that if I knew him, and I might--but I don’t know him and I must not--I know that!--I would tell him that I shall never any more lie down to sleep without having prayed to Heaven to bless him and reward him. And if I knew him, and I might, I would go down on my knees to him, and take his hand and kiss it and ask him not to draw it away, but to leave it--O to leave it for a moment--and let my thankful tears fall on it; for I have no other thanks to give him!’", "\"그리고 제가 드리려던 말씀은요, 선생님,\" 리틀 도릿이 점점 더 떨면서 말했다. \"만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면, 저는 그분께 말하고 싶어요. 그분의 자비로움을 제가 어떻게 느끼는지, 또 우리 착하신 아버지가 그것을 어떻게 느끼실지, 그분은 결코, 결코 모르실 거라고요. 그리고 제가 드리려던 말씀은요, 선생님, 만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면 — 하지만 저는 그분을 모르고, 알아서도 안 되지요, 그건 알아요! — 저는 그분께 말하고 싶어요. 그분을 축복하고 보답해 달라고 하늘에 기도드리지 않고는 다시는 잠자리에 들지 않겠다고요. 그리고 만약 제가 그분을 안다면, 그래도 된다면, 저는 그분 앞에 무릎을 꿇고, 그 손을 잡아 입 맞추고, 손을 거두지 말고 그냥 두어 달라고 — 아, 잠깐만이라도 그냥 두어 달라고 — 그래서 제 고마운 눈물이 그 위에 떨어지게 해 달라고 청하고 싶어요. 그분께 드릴 감사라고는 그것밖에 없으니까요!\""]], [["Little Dorrit had put his hand to her lips, and would have kneeled to him, but he gently prevented her, and replaced her in her chair.", "리틀 도릿은 그의 손을 자기 입술로 가져갔고, 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇으려 했으나, 그는 부드럽게 그녀를 만류하여 다시 의자에 앉혔다. 그녀의 눈과 목소리의 떨림은 그녀가 생각하는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 그에게 감사를 전했다."], ["Her eyes, and the tones of her voice, had thanked him far better than she thought.", "그는 평소처럼 침착하게는 말하지 못한 채 입을 열었다. \"자, 리틀 도릿, 자, 자, 자!"], ["He was not able to say, quite as composedly as usual, ‘There, Little Dorrit, there, there, there!", "네가 정말 그분을 안다고 치고, 그 모든 걸 할 수 있다고 치고, 또 그 모든 게 다 이루어졌다고 치자꾸나."], ["We will suppose that you did know this person, and that you might do all this, and that it was all done.", "그러면 이제 말해 보렴. 나는 전혀 다른 사람이니까 — 너에게 자기를 믿어 달라고 청한 친구일 뿐인 나에게 — 어째서 이 한밤중에 나와 있는지, 또 이 늦은 시각에 거리를 그토록 멀리 헤쳐 너를 여기까지 데려온 게 무엇인지를, 가냘프고 여린 나의,\""], ["And now tell me, Who am quite another person--who am nothing more than the friend who begged you to trust him--why you are out at midnight, and what it is that brings you so far through the streets at this late hour, my slight, delicate,’ child was on his lips again, ‘Little Dorrit!’", "다시 아이라는 말이 입가에 맴돌았다. \"리틀 도릿!\""]], [["‘Maggy and I have been to-night,’ she answered, subduing herself with the quiet effort that had long been natural to her, ‘to the theatre where my sister is engaged.’", "\"매기랑 저는 오늘 밤,\" 그녀는 오래도록 몸에 밴 그 조용한 노력으로 자신을 가다듬으며 대답했다. \"언니가 일하는 극장에 갔었어요.\""]], [["‘And oh ain’t it a Ev’nly place,’ suddenly interrupted Maggy, who seemed to have the power of going to sleep and waking up whenever she chose.", "\"아, 거기 참말로 천국 같은 데 아니에요?\" 마음만 먹으면 언제든 잠들었다 깨어나는 재주를 지닌 듯한 매기가 불쑥 끼어들었다."], ["‘Almost as good as a hospital.", "\"병원만큼이나 좋다니까요."], ["Only there ain’t no Chicking in it.’", "다만 거기엔 치킨이 없어요.\""]], [["Here she shook herself, and fell asleep again.", "이렇게 말하고 그녀는 몸을 한 번 떨더니 다시 잠들었다."]], [["‘We went there,’ said Little Dorrit, glancing at her charge, ‘because I like sometimes to know, of my own knowledge, that my sister is doing well; and like to see her there, with my own eyes, when neither she nor Uncle is aware. It is very seldom indeed that I can do that, because when I am not out at work, I am with my father, and even when I am out at work, I hurry home to him. But I pretend to-night that I am at a party.’", "\"우리가 거기 간 건요,\" 리틀 도릿은 자기가 돌보는 아이를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"이따금 언니가 잘 지내고 있는지 제 눈으로 직접 확인하고 싶어서예요. 언니도 삼촌도 모를 때 제 두 눈으로 거기 있는 언니를 보고 싶어서요. 사실 그렇게 할 수 있는 일은 정말 드물어요. 일하러 나가지 않을 때는 아버지 곁에 있고, 일하러 나가서도 아버지께로 서둘러 돌아가니까요. 하지만 오늘 밤만큼은 파티에 와 있는 척하는 거예요.\""]], [["As she made the confession, timidly hesitating, she raised her eyes to the face, and read its expression so plainly that she answered it.", "머뭇거리며 수줍게 그 고백을 하다가, 그녀는 그 얼굴로 눈을 들었고, 거기 떠오른 표정을 너무도 또렷이 읽고는 그에 답했다."]], [["‘Oh no, certainly!", "\"아, 아니에요, 정말로요!"], ["I never was at a party in my life.’", "저는 평생 파티에 가 본 적이 없는걸요.\""]], [["She paused a little under his attentive look, and then said, ‘I hope there is no harm in it.", "그녀는 그의 주의 깊은 눈길 아래 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 다시 말했다. \"이게 나쁜 일은 아니었으면 좋겠어요."], ["I could never have been of any use, if I had not pretended a little.’", "조금이라도 그런 척이라도 하지 않았더라면, 저는 누구에게도 도움이 될 수 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["She feared that he was blaming her in his mind for so devising to contrive for them, think for them, and watch over them, without their knowledge or gratitude; perhaps even with their reproaches for supposed neglect.", "그녀는 자기들 몰래, 또 고마움도 받지 못한 채 — 어쩌면 소홀하다는 책망까지 들어 가며 — 그들을 위해 궁리하고, 그들을 위해 생각하고, 그들을 지켜보려 그렇게 꾸민 것을 그가 마음속으로 나무라고 있을까 봐 두려워했다."], ["But what was really in his mind, was the weak figure with its strong purpose, the thin worn shoes, the insufficient dress, and the pretence of recreation and enjoyment.", "그러나 정작 그의 마음에 있던 것은, 강인한 목적을 지닌 그 여린 모습, 얇게 닳은 신발, 변변찮은 옷차림, 그리고 오락과 즐거움이라는 그 가장(假裝)이었다."], ["He asked where the suppositious party was?", "그는 그 가상의 파티가 어디서 열렸느냐고 물었다."], ["At a place where she worked, answered Little Dorrit, blushing.", "자기가 일하는 곳에서요, 라고 리틀 도릿은 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["She had said very little about it; only a few words to make her father easy.", "그녀는 그 일에 대해 거의 말하지 않았다. 아버지를 안심시킬 몇 마디만 했을 뿐이었다."], ["Her father did not believe it to be a grand party--indeed he might suppose that.", "아버지는 그것을 대단한 파티라고 믿지는 않았다 — 사실 그렇게 짐작했을 법도 했다."], ["And she glanced for an instant at the shawl she wore.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 자기가 두른 숄을 잠깐 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘It is the first night,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that I have ever been away from home. And London looks so large, so barren, and so wild.’ In Little Dorrit’s eyes, its vastness under the black sky was awful; a tremor passed over her as she said the words.", "\"오늘이 처음이에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"집을 떠나 밖에 있어 본 게요. 런던은 어쩌면 이렇게도 크고, 황량하고, 거칠어 보이는지요.\" 리틀 도릿의 눈에는, 검은 하늘 아래 펼쳐진 그 광막함이 무시무시했다. 그 말을 하면서 그녀는 몸을 한 차례 떨었다."]], [["‘But this is not,’ she added, with the quiet effort again, ‘what I have come to trouble you with, sir.", "\"하지만 이게,\" 그녀는 다시 그 조용한 노력으로 덧붙였다."], ["My sister’s having found a friend, a lady she has told me of and made me rather anxious about, was the first cause of my coming away from home.", "\"제가 선생님을 번거롭게 해 드리려고 찾아온 이유는 아니에요, 선생님. 언니가 어떤 친구를, 언니가 제게 말해 주어서 좀 걱정스러워진 어떤 부인을 사귀게 된 것이 제가 집을 나오게 된 첫 번째 까닭이었어요."], ["And being away, and coming (on purpose) round by where you lived and seeing a light in the window--’", "그리고 나온 김에, 일부러 선생님이 사시는 곳을 돌아 지나오다가 창에 불빛이 보여서요—\""]], [["Not for the first time.", "이번이 처음은 아니었다."], ["No, not for the first time.", "아니, 처음이 아니었다."], ["In Little Dorrit’s eyes, the outside of that window had been a distant star on other nights than this.", "리틀 도릿의 눈에는, 그 창의 바깥쪽이 오늘 밤이 아닌 다른 여러 밤에도 멀리 빛나는 별이었다."], ["She had toiled out of her way, tired and troubled, to look up at it, and wonder about the grave, brown gentleman from so far off, who had spoken to her as a friend and protector.", "그녀는 지치고 시름에 잠긴 채로, 가던 길을 애써 벗어나 그것을 올려다보며, 그토록 먼 데서 자기에게 친구로서, 보호자로서 말을 건네 준 그 근엄한 갈색 머리 신사에 대해 생각하곤 했던 것이다."]], [["‘There were three things,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that I thought I would like to say, if you were alone and I might come up-stairs.", "\"세 가지가 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["First, what I have tried to say, but never can--never shall--’", "\"선생님이 혼자 계시고, 제가 올라와도 된다면 말씀드리고 싶은 게요. 첫째는, 말씀드리려 애썼지만 결코 못 하는 — 결코 하지 못할 —\""]], [["‘Hush, hush!", "\"쉿, 쉿!"], ["That is done with, and disposed of.", "그건 이미 끝나서 처리됐단다."], ["Let us pass to the second,’ said Clennam, smiling her agitation away, making the blaze shine upon her, and putting wine and cake and fruit towards her on the table.", "두 번째로 넘어가자꾸나.\" 클레넘은 미소로 그녀의 동요를 가라앉히며, 불빛이 그녀에게 비치도록 하고, 포도주와 케이크와 과일을 탁자 위로 그녀 쪽으로 밀어 주었다."]], [["‘I think,’ said Little Dorrit--‘this is the second thing, sir--I think Mrs Clennam must have found out my secret, and must know where I come from and where I go to. Where I live, I mean.’", "\"제 생각엔요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"이게 두 번째예요, 선생님. 제 생각엔 클레넘 부인께서 제 비밀을 알아내신 것 같아요. 제가 어디서 와서 어디로 가는지 아시는 것 같아요. 제가 어디 사는지 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ returned Clennam quickly.", "\"정말이냐!\" 클레넘이 재빨리 되받았다."], ["He asked her, after short consideration, why she supposed so.", "잠시 생각한 뒤 그는 어째서 그렇게 여기느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I think,’ replied Little Dorrit, ‘that Mr Flintwinch must have watched me.’", "\"제 생각엔요,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다. \"플린트윈치 씨가 저를 감시한 것 같아요.\""]], [["And why, Clennam asked, as he turned his eyes upon the fire, bent his brows, and considered again; why did she suppose that?", "그러면 어째서 그렇게 생각하느냐고, 클레넘은 불로 시선을 돌리고 눈썹을 찌푸린 채 다시 곰곰이 헤아리며 물었다."]], [["‘I have met him twice.", "\"그분을 두 번 마주쳤어요."], ["Both times near home.", "두 번 다 집 근처에서요."], ["Both times at night, when I was going back.", "두 번 다 밤에, 제가 돌아가던 길에서요."], ["Both times I thought (though that may easily be my mistake), that he hardly looked as if he had met me by accident.’", "두 번 다 저는(이건 제 착각일 수도 있지만요) 그분이 우연히 저를 만난 것처럼은 도무지 보이지 않았다고 생각했어요.\""]], [["‘Did he say anything?’", "\"무슨 말이라도 했니?\""]], [["‘No; he only nodded and put his head on one side.’", "\"아니요. 그냥 고개를 끄덕이고는 머리를 한쪽으로 기울였어요.\""]], [["‘The devil take his head!’ mused Clennam, still looking at the fire; ‘it’s always on one side.’", "\"그자의 머리에 악마나 붙으라지!\" 클레넘은 여전히 불을 바라보며 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"늘 한쪽으로 기울어져 있으니.\""]], [["He roused himself to persuade her to put some wine to her lips, and to touch something to eat--it was very difficult, she was so timid and shy--and then said, musing again:", "그는 정신을 차리고 그녀에게 포도주에 입을 좀 대고 뭔가 먹어 보라고 권했다 — 그녀가 워낙 소심하고 수줍어해서 무척 힘든 일이었다 — 그러고는 다시 생각에 잠겨 말했다."]], [["‘Is my mother at all changed to you?’", "\"우리 어머니가 너를 대하는 태도가 조금이라도 달라졌니?\""]], [["‘Oh, not at all.", "\"아, 전혀요."], ["She is just the same.", "꼭 그대로세요."], ["I wondered whether I had better tell her my history.", "제 사정을 말씀드리는 게 나을까 하고 생각해 봤어요."], ["I wondered whether I might--I mean, whether you would like me to tell her.", "그래도 될까 하고요 — 그러니까, 선생님께서 제가 말씀드리길 바라실까 하고요."], ["I wondered,’ said Little Dorrit, looking at him in a suppliant way, and gradually withdrawing her eyes as he looked at her, ‘whether you would advise me what I ought to do.’", "제가 어떻게 해야 할지 선생님께서 조언해 주실 수 있을까 하고요.\" 리틀 도릿은 애원하듯 그를 바라보다가, 그가 자기를 마주 보자 차츰 눈길을 거두며 말했다."]], [["‘Little Dorrit,’ said Clennam; and the phrase had already begun, between these two, to stand for a hundred gentle phrases, according to the varying tone and connection in which it was used; ‘do nothing.", "\"리틀 도릿,\" 클레넘이 말했다 — 이 한마디는 이미 이 두 사람 사이에서, 쓰이는 어조와 맥락에 따라 백 가지 다정한 말을 대신하기 시작하고 있었다."], ["I will have some talk with my old friend, Mrs Affery.", "\"아무것도 하지 마라. 내가 내 오랜 벗 애프리 부인과 얘기를 좀 나눠 보마."], ["Do nothing, Little Dorrit--except refresh yourself with such means as there are here.", "아무것도 하지 마라, 리틀 도릿 — 다만 여기 있는 것들로 기운을 차리는 것만 빼고."], ["I entreat you to do that.’", "부디 그것만은 해 다오.\""]], [["‘Thank you, I am not hungry. Nor,’ said Little Dorrit, as he softly put her glass towards her, ‘nor thirsty.--I think Maggy might like something, perhaps.’", "\"고맙습니다, 배고프지 않아요. 그리고,\" 그가 부드럽게 그녀의 잔을 밀어 주자 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"목마르지도 않고요. — 어쩌면 매기는 뭘 좀 먹고 싶어 할지도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘We will make her find pockets presently for all there is here,’ said Clennam: ‘but before we awake her, there was a third thing to say.’", "\"이따가 여기 있는 걸 죄다 매기 주머니에 넣게 해 주자꾸나.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"하지만 그 애를 깨우기 전에, 세 번째로 할 말이 있다고 했지.\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["You will not be offended, sir?’", "화내지 않으실 거죠, 선생님?\""]], [["‘I promise that, unreservedly.’", "\"전혀 화내지 않겠다고 약속하마.\""]], [["‘It will sound strange.", "\"이상하게 들릴 거예요."], ["I hardly know how to say it.", "어떻게 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["Don’t think it unreasonable or ungrateful in me,’ said Little Dorrit, with returning and increasing agitation.", "제가 분별없거나 고마움을 모른다고 여기지 말아 주세요.\" 리틀 도릿은 다시금 차오르는 동요 속에서 말했다."]], [["‘No, no, no.", "\"아니, 아니, 아니다."], ["I am sure it will be natural and right.", "분명 자연스럽고 옳은 일일 거야."], ["I am not afraid that I shall put a wrong construction on it, whatever it is.’", "그게 무엇이든 내가 그걸 잘못 받아들일까 봐 걱정하지 않는다.\""]], [["‘Thank you.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["You are coming back to see my father again?’", "선생님은 제 아버지를 다시 뵈러 오실 거죠?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"그래.\""]], [["‘You have been so good and thoughtful as to write him a note, saying that you are coming to-morrow?’", "\"내일 가시겠다고 쪽지까지 써 주실 만큼 친절하고 자상하셨고요?\""]], [["‘Oh, that was nothing!", "\"아, 그건 아무것도 아니야!"], ["Yes.’", "그래.\""]], [["‘Can you guess,’ said Little Dorrit, folding her small hands tight in one another, and looking at him with all the earnestness of her soul looking steadily out of her eyes, ‘what I am going to ask you not to do?’", "\"짐작하시겠어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 작은 두 손을 꼭 맞잡고, 두 눈으로 또렷이 내비치는 온 영혼의 진지함을 담아 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 하지 말아 달라고 부탁드리려는 게 무엇인지요?\""]], [["‘I think I can.", "\"알 것 같구나."], ["But I may be wrong.’", "하지만 틀릴 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["‘No, you are not wrong,’ said Little Dorrit, shaking her head.", "\"아니요, 틀리지 않으셨어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘If we should want it so very, very badly that we cannot do without it, let me ask you for it.’", "\"만약 우리가 정말로, 정말로 절박해서 그게 없이는 도저히 안 될 지경이 되거든, 제가 선생님께 부탁드리게 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I Will,--I Will.’", "\"그러마, — 그러마.\""]], [["‘Don’t encourage him to ask.", "\"아버지가 청하도록 부추기지 말아 주세요."], ["Don’t understand him if he does ask.", "아버지가 청하더라도 알아듣지 말아 주세요."], ["Don’t give it to him.", "아버지께 그걸 주지 말아 주세요."], ["Save him and spare him that, and you will be able to think better of him!’", "그것만은 면하게 해 드리고 아껴 주세요. 그러면 아버지를 더 좋게 여기실 수 있을 거예요!\""]], [["Clennam said--not very plainly, seeing those tears glistening in her anxious eyes--that her wish should be sacred with him.", "클레넘은 — 그녀의 근심 어린 눈에 그렁그렁 빛나는 눈물을 보고서 그리 또렷하게는 아니었으나 — 그녀의 바람을 신성히 여기겠노라고 말했다."]], [["‘You don’t know what he is,’ she said; ‘you don’t know what he really is. How can you, seeing him there all at once, dear love, and not gradually, as I have done! You have been so good to us, so delicately and truly good, that I want him to be better in your eyes than in anybody’s. And I cannot bear to think,’ cried Little Dorrit, covering her tears with her hands, ‘I cannot bear to think that you of all the world should see him in his only moments of degradation.’", "\"선생님은 아버지가 어떤 분인지 모르세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아버지가 정말 어떤 분인지 모르세요. 어떻게 아시겠어요, 그렇게 단번에 거기서 아버지를 뵈었을 뿐, 제가 그래 왔듯이 차츰차츰 알아 가지 않으셨는데요, 다정하신 분! 선생님은 우리에게 그토록 잘해 주셨어요, 그토록 섬세하고 진실하게 잘해 주셨어요. 그래서 저는 누구의 눈에보다도 선생님의 눈에 아버지가 더 좋게 비치기를 바라요. 그런데 차마 생각할 수 없어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 두 손으로 눈물을 가리며 외쳤다. \"이 세상 누구도 아닌 바로 선생님께서, 아버지가 영락하는 그 단 한 순간을 보시게 된다는 걸 차마 견딜 수 없어요.\""]], [["‘Pray,’ said Clennam, ‘do not be so distressed.", "\"제발,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Pray, pray, Little Dorrit!", "\"그렇게 괴로워하지 마라. 제발, 제발, 리틀 도릿!"], ["This is quite understood now.’", "이제 그건 충분히 알아들었단다.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님. 고맙습니다!"], ["Thank you!", "이 말씀을 드리지 않으려고 무척이나 애썼어요. 며칠 밤낮을 생각했어요."], ["I have tried very much to keep myself from saying this; I have thought about it, days and nights; but when I knew for certain you were coming again, I made up my mind to speak to you.", "하지만 선생님이 정말로 다시 오신다는 걸 알게 되니, 말씀드리기로 마음먹었어요. 아버지가 부끄러워서가 아니에요,\""], ["Not because I am ashamed of him,’ she dried her tears quickly, ‘but because I know him better than any one does, and love him, and am proud of him.’", "그녀는 재빨리 눈물을 닦았다. \"그게 아니라 제가 누구보다 아버지를 잘 알고, 아버지를 사랑하고, 아버지를 자랑스러워하기 때문이에요.\""]], [["Relieved of this weight, Little Dorrit was nervously anxious to be gone.", "이 무거운 짐을 내려놓자, 리틀 도릿은 어서 가야겠다는 초조한 마음에 안절부절못했다."], ["Maggy being broad awake, and in the act of distantly gloating over the fruit and cakes with chuckles of anticipation, Clennam made the best diversion in his power by pouring her out a glass of wine, which she drank in a series of loud smacks; putting her hand upon her windpipe after every one, and saying, breathless, with her eyes in a prominent state, ‘Oh, ain’t it d’licious!", "매기가 말짱히 깨어나 과일과 케이크를 멀찍이서 군침을 흘리며 기대에 차 킬킬거리고 있자, 클레넘은 할 수 있는 한 가장 좋은 방법으로 분위기를 바꾸어 그녀에게 포도주 한 잔을 따라 주었다. 매기는 그것을 요란하게 쩝쩝 소리를 내며 마셨는데, 한 모금 들이켤 때마다 손을 목에 갖다 대고는, 눈을 툭 튀어나오게 뜬 채 숨을 헐떡이며 \"아, 정말 끝내주게 맛있어!"], ["Ain’t it hospitally!’ When she had finished the wine and these encomiums, he charged her to load her basket (she was never without her basket) with every eatable thing upon the table, and to take especial care to leave no scrap behind.", "정말 병원 같아!\" 하고 말했다."], ["Maggy’s pleasure in doing this and her little mother’s pleasure in seeing Maggy pleased, was as good a turn as circumstances could have given to the late conversation.", "포도주와 이 찬사를 다 마치고 나자, 그는 그녀에게 바구니(그녀는 늘 바구니를 들고 다녔다)에 탁자 위의 먹을 것을 죄다 담되, 부스러기 하나 남기지 않도록 각별히 신경 쓰라고 일렀다. 그것을 하는 매기의 기쁨과, 매기가 기뻐하는 것을 지켜보는 작은엄마의 기쁨은, 정황이 줄 수 있는 한 방금까지의 대화에 더없이 좋은 전환이 되어 주었다."]], [["‘But the gates will have been locked long ago,’ said Clennam, suddenly remembering it.", "\"하지만 문은 이미 한참 전에 잠겼을 텐데.\" 클레넘이 문득 그것을 떠올리고 말했다."], ["‘Where are you going?’", "\"어디로 갈 셈이냐?\""]], [["‘I am going to Maggy’s lodging,’ answered Little Dorrit.", "\"매기 숙소로 갈 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다."], ["‘I shall be quite safe, quite well taken care of.’", "\"거기 가면 아주 안전하고, 잘 보살핌을 받을 거예요.\""]], [["‘I must accompany you there,’ said Clennam, ‘I cannot let you go alone.’", "\"내가 거기까지 바래다줘야겠다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"혼자 가게 둘 수는 없어.\""]], [["‘Yes, pray leave us to go there by ourselves.", "\"아니에요, 부디 저희끼리 가게 해 주세요."], ["Pray do!’ begged Little Dorrit.", "제발요!\" 리틀 도릿이 간청했다."]], [["She was so earnest in the petition, that Clennam felt a delicacy in obtruding himself upon her: the rather, because he could well understand that Maggy’s lodging was of the obscurest sort. ‘Come, Maggy,’ said Little Dorrit cheerily, ‘we shall do very well; we know the way by this time, Maggy?’", "그 청이 어찌나 간절했던지, 클레넘은 그녀에게 자신을 들이미는 것이 결례가 될 듯해 마음이 조심스러웠다. 더구나 매기의 숙소가 더없이 누추한 부류임을 그가 익히 짐작할 수 있었기에 더욱 그러했다. \"가자, 매기.\" 리틀 도릿이 명랑하게 말했다. \"우린 잘 해낼 거야. 이제는 길을 알지, 매기?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, little mother; we know the way,’ chuckled Maggy. And away they went. Little Dorrit turned at the door to say, ‘God bless you!’ She said it very softly, but perhaps she may have been as audible above--who knows!--as a whole cathedral choir.", "\"네, 네, 작은엄마. 길 알아요.\" 매기가 킬킬거렸다. 그렇게 둘은 떠났다. 리틀 도릿은 문가에서 돌아서서 \"하느님의 가호가 함께하시길!\" 하고 말했다. 무척이나 나직이 말했지만, 어쩌면 그 말은 저 위에서는 — 누가 알겠는가! — 대성당의 온 성가대만큼이나 또렷이 들렸을지도 모른다."]], [["Arthur Clennam suffered them to pass the corner of the street before he followed at a distance; not with any idea of encroaching a second time on Little Dorrit’s privacy, but to satisfy his mind by seeing her secure in the neighbourhood to which she was accustomed.", "아서 클레넘은 둘이 길모퉁이를 돌 때까지 두고 보았다가 멀찍이 떨어져 뒤따랐다. 다시 한번 리틀 도릿의 사생활을 침범할 생각에서가 아니라, 그녀가 익숙한 동네에서 무사한 것을 보고 마음을 놓기 위해서였다."], ["So diminutive she looked, so fragile and defenceless against the bleak damp weather, flitting along in the shuffling shadow of her charge, that he felt, in his compassion, and in his habit of considering her a child apart from the rest of the rough world, as if he would have been glad to take her up in his arms and carry her to her journey’s end.", "그녀는 어찌나 자그마하고, 어찌나 가냘프고, 이 음산하고 축축한 날씨 앞에 어찌나 무방비해 보이던지, 발을 질질 끄는 자기 식솔의 그림자를 따라 종종걸음 치는 그 모습에, 그는 연민에 잠겨, 또 그녀를 거친 세상의 나머지와는 동떨어진 한 아이로 여기는 버릇에 잠겨, 그녀를 두 팔로 안아 올려 가는 곳까지 데려다주고 싶을 만큼이었다."]], [["In course of time she came into the leading thoroughfare where the Marshalsea was, and then he saw them slacken their pace, and soon turn down a by-street.", "이윽고 그녀는 마셜시가 있는 큰길로 들어섰고, 그러자 그는 둘이 걸음을 늦추더니 곧 옆길로 접어드는 것을 보았다."], ["He stopped, felt that he had no right to go further, and slowly left them.", "그는 걸음을 멈추고, 더 따라갈 권리가 없음을 느끼며, 천천히 그들을 떠났다."], ["He had no suspicion that they ran any risk of being houseless until morning; had no idea of the truth until long, long afterwards.", "그는 그들이 아침까지 집 없이 떠돌 위험에 처했으리라고는 짐작조차 못했다. 그 진실은 한참, 아주 한참 뒤에야 알게 되었다."]], [["But, said Little Dorrit, when they stopped at a poor dwelling all in darkness, and heard no sound on listening at the door, ‘Now, this is a good lodging for you, Maggy, and we must not give offence.", "그런데 캄캄하게 어둠에 잠긴 어느 초라한 집 앞에 둘이 멈춰 서서, 문에 귀를 대고 들어 보아도 아무 소리도 나지 않자, 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"자, 매기, 여긴 너한테 좋은 숙소니까 폐를 끼치면 안 돼."], ["Consequently, we will only knock twice, and not very loud; and if we cannot wake them so, we must walk about till day.’", "그러니까 두 번만, 그것도 너무 크지 않게 두드리자. 그래도 못 깨우면 날이 밝을 때까지 걸어 다녀야 해.\""]], [["Once, Little Dorrit knocked with a careful hand, and listened.", "한 번, 리틀 도릿은 조심스러운 손길로 두드리고는 귀를 기울였다."], ["Twice, Little Dorrit knocked with a careful hand, and listened.", "두 번, 리틀 도릿은 조심스러운 손길로 두드리고는 귀를 기울였다."], ["All was close and still.", "사방은 닫혀 있고 고요했다."], ["‘Maggy, we must do the best we can, my dear.", "\"매기, 우린 할 수 있는 대로 해야 해, 얘야."], ["We must be patient, and wait for day.’", "참고 날이 밝길 기다리자.\""]], [["It was a chill dark night, with a damp wind blowing, when they came out into the leading street again, and heard the clocks strike half-past one. ‘In only five hours and a half,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘we shall be able to go home.’ To speak of home, and to go and look at it, it being so near, was a natural sequence. They went to the closed gate, and peeped through into the court-yard. ‘I hope he is sound asleep,’ said Little Dorrit, kissing one of the bars, ‘and does not miss me.’", "둘이 다시 큰길로 나왔을 때는, 축축한 바람이 부는 쌀쌀하고 캄캄한 밤이었고, 시계들이 한 시 반을 치는 소리가 들렸다. \"다섯 시간 반만 지나면,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"우린 집에 갈 수 있어.\" 집 이야기를 하고 보니, 또 집이 그토록 가까운지라 가서 그것을 바라보는 것은 자연스러운 수순이었다. 둘은 닫힌 문으로 가서 마당 안을 들여다보았다. \"아버지가 푹 주무시고 계셨으면 좋겠어.\" 리틀 도릿이 쇠창살 하나에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"그리고 내가 없는 걸 모르셨으면.\""]], [["The gate was so familiar, and so like a companion, that they put down Maggy’s basket in a corner to serve for a seat, and keeping close together, rested there for some time.", "그 문이 어찌나 낯익고 또 길동무처럼 정겹던지, 둘은 매기의 바구니를 한쪽 구석에 내려놓아 의자 삼고는, 서로 바싹 붙어 한참을 거기서 쉬었다. 거리가 텅 비고 고요한 동안에는 리틀 도릿도 무섭지 않았다."], ["While the street was empty and silent, Little Dorrit was not afraid; but when she heard a footstep at a distance, or saw a moving shadow among the street lamps, she was startled, and whispered, ‘Maggy, I see some one.", "그러나 멀리서 발소리가 들리거나, 가로등 사이로 움직이는 그림자가 보이면 그녀는 흠칫 놀라 \"매기, 누가 와. 저리 가자!\""], ["Come away!’ Maggy would then wake up more or less fretfully, and they would wander about a little, and come back again.", "하고 속삭였다. 그러면 매기는 짜증을 부리며 어렴풋이 깨어났고, 둘은 잠시 이리저리 헤매다가 다시 돌아오곤 했다."]], [["As long as eating was a novelty and an amusement, Maggy kept up pretty well.", "먹는 것이 신기하고 즐거운 동안에는 매기도 그럭저럭 견뎌 냈다. 그러나 그 시기가 지나자, 그녀는 추위에 대해 칭얼대며 몸을 떨고 훌쩍이기 시작했다."], ["But that period going by, she became querulous about the cold, and shivered and whimpered.", "\"곧 끝날 거야, 얘야.\" 리틀 도릿이 참을성 있게 말했다."], ["‘It will soon be over, dear,’ said Little Dorrit patiently.", "\"아, 작은엄마야 다 괜찮으시겠지.\" 매기가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Oh it’s all very fine for you, little mother,’ returned Maggy, ‘but I’m a poor thing, only ten years old.’ At last, in the dead of the night, when the street was very still indeed, Little Dorrit laid the heavy head upon her bosom, and soothed her to sleep.", "\"하지만 난 불쌍한 애잖아, 겨우 열 살밖에 안 됐는데.\" 마침내, 거리가 정말로 쥐 죽은 듯 고요해진 한밤중에, 리틀 도릿은 그 무거운 머리를 자기 가슴에 뉘어 매기를 다독여 잠재웠다."], ["And thus she sat at the gate, as it were alone; looking up at the stars, and seeing the clouds pass over them in their wild flight--which was the dance at Little Dorrit’s party.", "그렇게 그녀는 혼자인 양 문가에 앉아, 별들을 올려다보며, 그것들 위로 사납게 내달아 지나가는 구름들을 바라보았다 — 그것이 리틀 도릿의 파티에서 벌어지는 춤이었다."]], [["‘If it really was a party!’ she thought once, as she sat there.", "'정말로 파티라면!' 그녀는 거기 앉아 한 번 그렇게 생각했다."], ["‘If it was light and warm and beautiful, and it was our house, and my poor dear was its master, and had never been inside these walls.", "'밝고 따뜻하고 아름다웠다면, 그게 우리 집이고, 우리 가엾고 사랑하는 아버지가 그 집의 주인이고, 이 담장 안에는 한 번도 발을 들이지 않으셨다면."], ["And if Mr Clennam was one of our visitors, and we were dancing to delightful music, and were all as gay and light-hearted as ever we could be!", "그리고 클레넘 씨가 우리 손님 중 한 분이고, 우리가 흥겨운 음악에 맞춰 춤추며, 더없이 즐겁고 마음 가벼울 수 있는 만큼 다 그랬다면!"], ["I wonder--’ Such a vista of wonder opened out before her, that she sat looking up at the stars, quite lost, until Maggy was querulous again, and wanted to get up and walk.", "그러면—' 그토록 아득한 상상의 풍경이 눈앞에 펼쳐져, 그녀는 매기가 다시 칭얼대며 일어나 걷고 싶어 할 때까지, 별들을 올려다보며 넋을 잃고 앉아 있었다."]], [["Three o’clock, and half-past three, and they had passed over London Bridge.", "세 시, 그리고 세 시 반, 그러는 사이 둘은 런던교를 건너 있었다."], ["They had heard the rush of the tide against obstacles; and looked down, awed, through the dark vapour on the river; had seen little spots of lighted water where the bridge lamps were reflected, shining like demon eyes, with a terrible fascination in them for guilt and misery.", "둘은 조류가 장애물에 부딪쳐 콸콸 쏟아지는 소리를 들었고, 강 위에 드리운 어두운 안개 너머를 두려운 마음으로 내려다보았다. 다리의 등불이 비치는 작은 물빛 자국들이, 죄와 비참함에 무서운 매혹을 던지는 악마의 눈처럼 빛나는 것을 보았다."], ["They had shrunk past homeless people, lying coiled up in nooks.", "둘은 구석에 웅크려 누운 집 없는 사람들을 피해 움츠리며 지나쳤다."], ["They had run from drunkards.", "둘은 술 취한 자들에게서 달아났다."], ["They had started from slinking men, whistling and signing to one another at bye corners, or running away at full speed.", "둘은 으슥한 모퉁이에서 서로 휘파람을 불고 신호를 주고받거나 전속력으로 내빼는, 슬그머니 움직이는 사내들을 보고 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["Though everywhere the leader and the guide, Little Dorrit, happy for once in her youthful appearance, feigned to cling to and rely upon Maggy.", "가는 곳마다 앞장서 길잡이 노릇을 하면서도, 리틀 도릿은 한 번만은 자기가 어려 보이는 것을 다행으로 여기며, 매기에게 매달려 의지하는 척했다."], ["And more than once some voice, from among a knot of brawling or prowling figures in their path, had called out to the rest to ‘let the woman and the child go by!’", "그리고 한 번 이상, 둘이 가는 길의 떠들썩하거나 어슬렁거리는 무리 가운데서 어떤 목소리가 나머지 사람들에게 \"저 아낙이랑 애는 그냥 지나가게 둬!\" 하고 외쳤다."]], [["So, the woman and the child had gone by, and gone on, and five had sounded from the steeples.", "그렇게 그 아낙과 아이는 지나쳤고, 계속 나아갔으며, 첨탑들에서 다섯 시를 알리는 소리가 울렸다."], ["They were walking slowly towards the east, already looking for the first pale streak of day, when a woman came after them.", "둘은 이미 동녘에서 처음 비치는 희끄무레한 새벽빛을 찾으며 천천히 동쪽으로 걷고 있었는데, 그때 한 여자가 그들을 뒤쫓아 왔다."]], [["‘What are you doing with the child?’ she said to Maggy.", "\"그 애를 데리고 뭘 하는 거예요?\" 그녀가 매기에게 말했다."]], [["She was young--far too young to be there, Heaven knows!--and neither ugly nor wicked-looking.", "그녀는 젊었다 — 하느님은 아시겠지만, 거기 있기에는 너무도 젊었다! — 그리고 추하지도, 사악해 보이지도 않았다."], ["She spoke coarsely, but with no naturally coarse voice; there was even something musical in its sound.", "거칠게 말했지만 본래 거친 목소리는 아니었다. 그 소리에는 어딘가 음악적인 데까지 있었다."]], [["‘What are you doing with yourself?’ retorted Maggy, for want of a better answer.", "\"댁은 댁 신세로 뭘 하는 건데요?\" 더 나은 대답이 없어 매기가 받아쳤다."]], [["‘Can’t you see, without my telling you?’", "\"내가 말 안 해도 안 보여요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know as I can,’ said Maggy.", "\"보일 것 같진 않은데요.\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["‘Killing myself!", "\"내 신세 망치고 있는 거예요!"], ["Now I have answered you, answer me.", "자, 내가 대답했으니 댁도 대답해요."], ["What are you doing with the child?’", "그 애를 데리고 뭘 하는 거예요?\""]], [["The supposed child kept her head drooped down, and kept her form close at Maggy’s side.", "아이로 여겨진 그녀는 고개를 푹 떨군 채, 매기 곁에 몸을 바싹 붙이고 있었다."]], [["‘Poor thing!’ said the woman.", "\"가엾어라!\" 여자가 말했다."], ["‘Have you no feeling, that you keep her out in the cruel streets at such a time as this?", "\"이런 시각에 이 모진 거리로 애를 데리고 나오다니, 댁은 인정도 없어요?"], ["Have you no eyes, that you don’t see how delicate and slender she is?", "이 애가 얼마나 여리고 가냘픈지 보이지도 않아요, 눈도 없어요?"], ["Have you no sense (you don’t look as if you had much) that you don’t take more pity on this cold and trembling little hand?’", "이 차갑고 떨리는 작은 손을 더 가엾이 여길 분별도 (별로 있어 보이진 않지만) 없어요?\""]], [["She had stepped across to that side, and held the hand between her own two, chafing it.", "그녀는 그쪽으로 건너와, 그 손을 자기 두 손 사이에 쥐고 비벼 주었다. \"가엾고 길 잃은 사람한테 입맞춤해 주렴, 얘야.\""], ["‘Kiss a poor lost creature, dear,’ she said, bending her face, ‘and tell me where’s she taking you.’", "그녀가 얼굴을 숙이며 말했다. \"그리고 이 사람이 널 어디로 데려가는지 말해 다오.\""]], [["Little Dorrit turned towards her.", "리틀 도릿이 그녀를 향해 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["‘Why, my God!’ she said, recoiling, ‘you’re a woman!’", "\"세상에, 맙소사!\" 그녀가 뒷걸음치며 말했다. \"당신은 여자잖아!\""]], [["‘Don’t mind that!’ said Little Dorrit, clasping one of her hands that had suddenly released hers.", "\"그건 신경 쓰지 마세요!\" 리틀 도릿이, 갑자기 자기 손을 놓아 버린 그녀의 한 손을 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["‘I am not afraid of you.’", "\"전 당신이 무섭지 않아요.\""]], [["‘Then you had better be,’ she answered.", "\"그럼 무서워하는 게 좋을걸요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘Have you no mother?’", "\"어머니는 없어요?\""]], [["‘No.’", "\"없어요.\""]], [["‘No father?’", "\"아버지는요?\""]], [["‘Yes, a very dear one.’", "\"있어요, 아주 사랑하는 아버지가요.\""]], [["‘Go home to him, and be afraid of me.", "\"아버지께로 돌아가요, 그리고 날 무서워해요."], ["Let me go.", "손 놓아요."], ["Good night!’", "잘 가요!\""]], [["‘I must thank you first; let me speak to you as if I really were a child.’", "\"먼저 고맙다는 말씀을 드려야겠어요. 제가 정말 아이인 것처럼 당신께 말하게 해 주세요.\""]], [["‘You can’t do it,’ said the woman.", "\"그렇겐 못 해요.\" 여자가 말했다."], ["‘You are kind and innocent; but you can’t look at me out of a child’s eyes.", "\"당신은 다정하고 순수해요. 하지만 아이의 눈으로 날 바라볼 순 없어요."], ["I never should have touched you, but I thought that you were a child.’ And with a strange, wild cry, she went away.", "당신이 아이인 줄 알았으니까 손을 댔지, 안 그랬으면 결코 손대지 않았을 거예요.\" 그러고는 이상하고 격한 외마디 소리를 내지르며 그녀는 가 버렸다."]], [["No day yet in the sky, but there was day in the resounding stones of the streets; in the waggons, carts, and coaches; in the workers going to various occupations; in the opening of early shops; in the traffic at markets; in the stir of the riverside.", "하늘에는 아직 날이 밝지 않았지만, 거리의 울리는 돌바닥에는 날이 밝아 오고 있었다. 짐마차와 손수레와 사륜마차에도, 갖가지 일터로 가는 일꾼들에게도, 이른 가게들이 문 여는 데에도, 시장의 거래에도, 강가의 부산함에도 날이 밝아 오고 있었다."], ["There was coming day in the flaring lights, with a feebler colour in them than they would have had at another time; coming day in the increased sharpness of the air, and the ghastly dying of the night.", "다른 때라면 띠었을 빛깔보다 더 희미해진, 너울거리는 등불들 속에 날이 밝아 오고 있었다. 더 매서워진 공기와, 으스스하게 사위어 가는 밤에도 날이 밝아 오고 있었다."]], [["They went back again to the gate, intending to wait there now until it should be opened; but the air was so raw and cold that Little Dorrit, leading Maggy about in her sleep, kept in motion.", "둘은 이제 문이 열릴 때까지 거기서 기다릴 작정으로 다시 문가로 돌아갔다. 그러나 공기가 어찌나 차고 쌀쌀하던지, 리틀 도릿은 잠결의 매기를 이리저리 데리고 다니며 계속 몸을 움직였다."], ["Going round by the Church, she saw lights there, and the door open; and went up the steps and looked in.", "교회 쪽으로 돌아가다가 그녀는 거기 불이 켜져 있고 문이 열려 있는 것을 보고는, 층계를 올라가 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Who’s that?’ cried a stout old man, who was putting on a nightcap as if he were going to bed in a vault.", "\"누구냐?\" 마치 지하 납골당에서 잠자리에 들기라도 할 양으로 나이트캡을 쓰고 있던 건장한 노인이 소리쳤다."]], [["‘It’s no one particular, sir,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"별것 아닌 사람이에요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Stop!’ cried the man.", "\"멈춰라!\" 노인이 소리쳤다."], ["‘Let’s have a look at you!’", "\"어디 좀 보자!\""]], [["This caused her to turn back again in the act of going out, and to present herself and her charge before him.", "이 말에 그녀는 막 나가려던 참에 다시 돌아서서, 자기와 자기가 돌보는 아이를 노인 앞에 내보였다."]], [["‘I thought so!’ said he.", "\"그럴 줄 알았다!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘I know _you_.’", "\"나는 너를 알아.\""]], [["‘We have often seen each other,’ said Little Dorrit, recognising the sexton, or the beadle, or the verger, or whatever he was, ‘when I have been at church here.’", "\"우린 자주 서로 봤지요.\" 리틀 도릿은 그가 묘지기인지, 교회 관리인인지, 성당지기인지, 아무튼 무엇이었든 그를 알아보고 말했다. \"제가 여기 예배 보러 왔을 때요.\""]], [["‘More than that, we’ve got your birth in our Register, you know; you’re one of our curiosities.’", "\"그뿐인 줄 아느냐, 우리 명부에 네 출생이 올라 있단 말이다. 너는 우리 진귀한 구경거리 중 하나야.\""]], [["‘Indeed!’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"정말요!\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘To be sure.", "\"그렇고말고."], ["As the child of the--by-the-bye, how did you get out so early?’", "그—의 자식으로 말이다. 그건 그렇고, 어쩌다 이렇게 일찍 나와 있느냐?\""]], [["‘We were shut out last night, and are waiting to get in.’", "\"어젯밤에 밖에 갇혀서, 들어가려고 기다리는 중이에요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean it?", "\"설마!"], ["And there’s another hour good yet!", "게다가 아직 꼬박 한 시간이나 남았는데!"], ["Come into the vestry.", "제의실로 들어오너라."], ["You’ll find a fire in the vestry, on account of the painters.", "제의실엔 칠장이들 덕에 불이 있을 게다."], ["I’m waiting for the painters, or I shouldn’t be here, you may depend upon it.", "나도 칠장이들을 기다리는 게 아니었으면 여기 안 있었을 거다, 그건 믿어도 돼."], ["One of our curiosities mustn’t be cold when we have it in our power to warm her up comfortable.", "우리 진귀한 구경거리 하나가, 우리가 따뜻하게 편안히 데워 줄 수 있는 형편인데 추위에 떨게 둘 순 없지."], ["Come along.’", "어서 오너라.\""]], [["He was a very good old fellow, in his familiar way; and having stirred the vestry fire, he looked round the shelves of registers for a particular volume.", "그는 격의 없는 태도에 무척 마음씨 좋은 노인이었다. 제의실 불을 휘저어 돋우고 나서, 그는 명부가 꽂힌 선반들을 둘러보며 특정한 책 한 권을 찾았다."], ["‘Here you are, you see,’ he said, taking it down and turning the leaves.", "\"자, 여기 있다, 봐라.\" 그는 그것을 꺼내 책장을 넘기며 말했다."], ["‘Here you’ll find yourself, as large as life.", "\"여기 네가 똑똑히 적혀 있을 게다."], ["Amy, daughter of William and Fanny Dorrit.", "에이미, 윌리엄과 패니 도릿의 딸."], ["Born, Marshalsea Prison, Parish of St George.", "세인트 조지 교구 마셜시 감옥에서 출생."], ["And we tell people that you have lived there, without so much as a day’s or a night’s absence, ever since.", "그리고 우린 사람들한테 말하지, 네가 그때부터 단 하루, 단 하룻밤도 떨어진 적 없이 거기서 살아왔다고 말이다."], ["Is it true?’", "정말이냐?\""]], [["‘Quite true, till last night.’", "\"정말이에요, 어젯밤까지는요.\""]], [["‘Lord!’ But his surveying her with an admiring gaze suggested Something else to him, to wit: ‘I am sorry to see, though, that you are faint and tired.", "\"이런!\" 그러나 감탄 어린 눈길로 그녀를 살피던 노인의 머리에 다른 생각이 떠올랐으니, 곧 이러했다."], ["Stay a bit.", "\"그런데 네가 기진하고 지쳐 보이는구나, 안됐다."], ["I’ll get some cushions out of the church, and you and your friend shall lie down before the fire.", "잠깐 있거라. 내가 교회에서 방석을 좀 가져올 테니, 너랑 네 친구는 불 앞에 누워 있거라."], ["Don’t be afraid of not going in to join your father when the gate opens.", "문이 열릴 때 아버지께로 못 들어갈까 봐 걱정은 말고."], ["_I’ll_ call you.’", "내가 불러 주마.\""]], [["He soon brought in the cushions, and strewed them on the ground.", "그는 곧 방석을 가져와 바닥에 펼쳐 놓았다."]], [["‘There you are, you see.", "\"자, 여기 있다, 봐라."], ["Again as large as life.", "또 똑똑히 보이는구나."], ["Oh, never mind thanking.", "아, 고맙단 말은 됐다."], ["I’ve daughters of my own.", "나도 딸자식들이 있는 몸이야."], ["And though they weren’t born in the Marshalsea Prison, they might have been, if I had been, in my ways of carrying on, of your father’s breed.", "그 애들이 마셜시 감옥에서 태어나진 않았지만, 내가 사는 꼴이 네 아버지 같은 부류였더라면 그랬을 수도 있었지."], ["Stop a bit.", "잠깐 있거라."], ["I must put something under the cushion for your head.", "네 머리 밑에 뭘 좀 받쳐 줘야겠다."], ["Here’s a burial volume, just the thing!", "여기 매장 명부가 있구나, 딱 좋겠다!"], ["We have got Mrs Bangham in this book.", "이 책엔 뱅엄 부인이 들어 있단다."], ["But what makes these books interesting to most people is--not who’s in ‘em, but who isn’t--who’s coming, you know, and when.", "그런데 사람들 대부분한테 이런 책들이 흥미로운 건 — 누가 들어 있느냐가 아니라, 누가 안 들어 있느냐란 말이지 — 그러니까 누가 들어올 차례냐, 또 언제냐 하는 거야."], ["That’s the interesting question.’", "그게 흥미로운 물음이지.\""]], [["Commendingly looking back at the pillow he had improvised, he left them to their hour’s repose.", "자기가 급조한 베개를 흐뭇하게 돌아보며, 노인은 둘에게 한 시간의 휴식을 남기고 자리를 떴다."], ["Maggy was snoring already, and Little Dorrit was soon fast asleep with her head resting on that sealed book of Fate, untroubled by its mysterious blank leaves.", "매기는 벌써 코를 골고 있었고, 리틀 도릿도 이내 그 운명의 봉인된 책 위에 머리를 뉜 채 깊이 잠들었다 — 그 신비로운 백지의 책장에는 조금도 마음 쓰지 않고서."]], [["This was Little Dorrit’s party.", "이것이 리틀 도릿의 파티였다. 그 거대한 수도의 수치와 버림받음과 비참함과 무방비함."], ["The shame, desertion, wretchedness, and exposure of the great capital; the wet, the cold, the slow hours, and the swift clouds of the dismal night.", "그 음울한 밤의 축축함과 추위와 더디 가는 시간들과 빠르게 흘러가는 구름들. 이것이 리틀 도릿이, 비 내리는 아침의 첫 잿빛 안개 속에서 기진한 채 집으로 돌아간 바로 그 파티였다."], ["This was the party from which Little Dorrit went home, jaded, in the first grey mist of a rainy morning.", "15장. 플린트윈치 부인이 또 한 번 꿈을 꾸다"]], [["# CHAPTER 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream", "# CHAPTER 15. Mrs Flintwinch has another Dream"]], [["The debilitated old house in the city, wrapped in its mantle of soot, and leaning heavily on the crutches that had partaken of its decay and worn out with it, never knew a healthy or a cheerful interval, let what would betide.", "도시 한복판의 그 쇠약한 노후한 집은 검댕의 망토를 두른 채, 제가 쇠락하는 데 함께하다가 함께 닳아 버린 버팀목에 무겁게 기대고서, 무슨 일이 닥치든 건강하거나 명랑한 시간이라곤 결코 알지 못했다."], ["If the sun ever touched it, it was but with a ray, and that was gone in half an hour; if the moonlight ever fell upon it, it was only to put a few patches on its doleful cloak, and make it look more wretched.", "행여 햇살이 닿더라도 그것은 한 줄기에 지나지 않았고, 그마저도 반 시간이면 사라졌다. 행여 달빛이 그 위에 내려앉더라도, 그것은 그 음울한 외투에 헝겊 몇 조각을 덧대어 한층 더 비참해 보이게 할 뿐이었다."], ["The stars, to be sure, coldly watched it when the nights and the smoke were clear enough; and all bad weather stood by it with a rare fidelity.", "물론 별들은, 밤과 연기가 충분히 맑은 때면, 차갑게 그것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 온갖 궂은 날씨는 보기 드문 충직함으로 그 곁을 지켰다."], ["You should alike find rain, hail, frost, and thaw lingering in that dismal enclosure when they had vanished from other places; and as to snow, you should see it there for weeks, long after it had changed from yellow to black, slowly weeping away its grimy life.", "비도, 우박도, 서리도, 해빙도, 다른 곳에서는 이미 자취를 감춘 뒤에도 그 음산한 울안에서는 머뭇거리는 것을 보게 될 것이며, 눈으로 말하자면, 그곳에서는 몇 주씩이나, 노랗던 것이 검게 변한 지 한참이 지나도록, 더러운 제 목숨을 천천히 울며 흘려보내는 것을 보게 될 것이었다."], ["The place had no other adherents.", "그 자리에는 그 밖의 추종자라곤 없었다."], ["As to street noises, the rumbling of wheels in the lane merely rushed in at the gateway in going past, and rushed out again: making the listening Mistress Affery feel as if she were deaf, and recovered the sense of hearing by instantaneous flashes.", "거리의 소음으로 말하자면, 골목을 지나는 바퀴들의 덜컹거림은 그저 대문 안으로 쏟아져 들어왔다가 다시 쏟아져 나갈 뿐이어서, 귀를 기울이던 애프리 부인은 자신이 귀머거리가 되었다가 순간적인 섬광처럼 청력을 되찾는 것 같은 느낌이 들곤 했다."], ["So with whistling, singing, talking, laughing, and all pleasant human sounds.", "휘파람, 노래, 이야기, 웃음, 그리고 온갖 즐거운 인간의 소리도 그러했다."], ["They leaped the gap in a moment, and went upon their way.", "그것들은 한순간에 그 틈새를 뛰어넘어 제 갈 길로 가 버렸다."]], [["The varying light of fire and candle in Mrs Clennam’s room made the greatest change that ever broke the dead monotony of the spot.", "클레넘 부인의 방에서 일렁이는 난롯불과 촛불의 빛은 그 자리의 죽은 듯한 단조로움을 깨뜨리는 가장 큰 변화였다."], ["In her two long narrow windows, the fire shone sullenly all day, and sullenly all night.", "그녀의 좁고 긴 두 창 안에서, 불은 온종일 시무룩하게 빛났고 밤새도록 시무룩하게 빛났다."], ["On rare occasions it flashed up passionately, as she did; but for the most part it was suppressed, like her, and preyed upon itself evenly and slowly.", "드문 경우에는 그녀가 그러하듯 격정적으로 타올랐다. 그러나 대개는 그녀처럼 억눌린 채, 고르고 느리게 제 자신을 갉아먹었다."], ["During many hours of the short winter days, however, when it was dusk there early in the afternoon, changing distortions of herself in her wheeled chair, of Mr Flintwinch with his wry neck, of Mistress Affery coming and going, would be thrown upon the house wall that was over the gateway, and would hover there like shadows from a great magic lantern.", "그러나 짧은 겨울날의 여러 시간 동안, 오후 일찍부터 그곳이 어스레해질 때면, 바퀴 달린 의자에 앉은 그녀의 일그러진 형상이, 비뚤어진 목을 한 플린트윈치 씨의 형상이, 오가는 애프리 부인의 형상이 시시각각 변하며 대문 위의 집 벽에 던져져, 거대한 환등기에서 나온 그림자처럼 거기서 어른거리곤 했다."], ["As the room-ridden invalid settled for the night, these would gradually disappear: Mistress Affery’s magnified shadow always flitting about, last, until it finally glided away into the air, as though she were off upon a witch excursion.", "방에 갇힌 병자가 밤을 맞을 채비를 하면, 이것들은 차츰차츰 사라졌다. 애프리 부인의 확대된 그림자만은 늘 마지막까지 어른거리다가, 마침내 마치 그녀가 마녀 나들이를 떠나기라도 하는 듯 공중으로 미끄러져 사라졌다."], ["Then the solitary light would burn unchangingly, until it burned pale before the dawn, and at last died under the breath of Mrs Affery, as her shadow descended on it from the witch-region of sleep.", "그러면 외로운 불빛이 변함없이 타다가, 동트기 전에 창백하게 타들어, 마침내 애프리 부인의 입김 아래 꺼졌으니, 잠이라는 마녀의 영역에서 그녀의 그림자가 그 위로 내려앉을 때였다."]], [["Strange, if the little sick-room fire were in effect a beacon fire, summoning some one, and that the most unlikely some one in the world, to the spot that _must_ be come to.", "이상하지 않은가, 그 작은 병실의 불이 사실은 봉화 불이어서, 누군가를, 그것도 세상에서 가장 있을 법하지 않은 누군가를, 반드시 와야만 하는 그 자리로 불러들이는 것이라면."], ["Strange, if the little sick-room light were in effect a watch-light, burning in that place every night until an appointed event should be watched out!", "이상하지 않은가, 그 작은 병실의 불빛이 사실은 망보는 불이어서, 정해진 사건이 끝까지 지켜질 때까지 매일 밤 그 자리에서 타고 있는 것이라면!"], ["Which of the vast multitude of travellers, under the sun and the stars, climbing the dusty hills and toiling along the weary plains, journeying by land and journeying by sea, coming and going so strangely, to meet and to act and react on one another; which of the host may, with no suspicion of the journey’s end, be travelling surely hither?", "해와 별 아래 먼지투성이 언덕을 오르고 지친 평원을 따라 애쓰며, 뭍으로 여행하고 바다로 여행하며, 그토록 기이하게 오가며, 만나고 작용하고 서로에게 반작용하는 그 무수한 여행자들 가운데 — 그 무리 가운데 누가, 여로의 끝을 조금도 의심하지 않은 채, 틀림없이 이리로 오고 있는 것일까?"]], [["Time shall show us.", "시간이 우리에게 보여 주리라."], ["The post of honour and the post of shame, the general’s station and the drummer’s, a peer’s statue in Westminster Abbey and a seaman’s hammock in the bosom of the deep, the mitre and the workhouse, the woolsack and the gallows, the throne and the guillotine--the travellers to all are on the great high road, but it has wonderful divergencies, and only Time shall show us whither each traveller is bound.", "영예의 자리와 치욕의 자리, 장군의 자리와 고수의 자리, 웨스트민스터 사원에 선 귀족의 조상과 깊은 바닷속에 잠긴 뱃사람의 해먹, 주교관과 구빈원, 양털 의자와 교수대, 왕좌와 단두대 — 그 모든 곳으로 가는 여행자들이 큰길 위에 있건만, 그 길에는 놀라운 갈림길들이 있어, 오직 시간만이 우리에게 각 여행자가 어디로 향하는지를 보여 주리라."]], [["On a wintry afternoon at twilight, Mrs Flintwinch, having been heavy all day, dreamed this dream:", "어느 겨울날 해 질 녘, 종일 노곤했던 플린트윈치 부인은 이런 꿈을 꾸었다."]], [["She thought she was in the kitchen getting the kettle ready for tea, and was warming herself with her feet upon the fender and the skirt of her gown tucked up, before the collapsed fire in the middle of the grate, bordered on either hand by a deep cold black ravine.", "그녀는 자신이 부엌에서 차를 끓이려고 주전자를 준비하고 있었다고 생각했고, 양옆으로 깊고 차가운 검은 골짜기가 가장자리를 이룬 화덕 한복판에서 사그라진 불 앞에, 발을 난로 받침대에 올리고 치맛자락을 걷어 올린 채 몸을 녹이고 있었다고 생각했다."], ["She thought that as she sat thus, musing upon the question whether life was not for some people a rather dull invention, she was frightened by a sudden noise behind her.", "그렇게 앉아서, 삶이란 어떤 이들에게는 다소 따분한 발명품이 아닌가 하는 문제를 곰곰이 생각하고 있노라니, 등 뒤에서 난 갑작스러운 소리에 놀랐다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["She thought that she had been similarly frightened once last week, and that the noise was of a mysterious kind--a sound of rustling and of three or four quick beats like a rapid step; while a shock or tremble was communicated to her heart, as if the step had shaken the floor, or even as if she had been touched by some awful hand.", "지난주에도 한 번 비슷하게 놀란 적이 있었으며, 그 소리는 알 수 없는 종류의 것 — 바스락거림과 빠른 발걸음 같은 서너 번의 잰 박자 소리였으며, 한편으로 마치 그 발걸음이 바닥을 흔들었거나, 심지어 어떤 무시무시한 손이 그녀를 건드리기라도 한 듯, 가슴에 충격이랄까 떨림이 전해졌다고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["She thought that this revived within her certain old fears of hers that the house was haunted; and that she flew up the kitchen stairs without knowing how she got up, to be nearer company.", "이것이 그녀 안에서, 이 집에 귀신이 들렸다는 그녀의 어떤 오랜 두려움을 되살렸으며, 그래서 좀 더 사람 곁으로 가려고 어떻게 올라갔는지도 모르게 부엌 계단을 날듯이 뛰어올랐다고 그녀는 생각했다."]], [["Mistress Affery thought that on reaching the hall, she saw the door of her liege lord’s office standing open, and the room empty.", "애프리 부인은, 현관에 다다랐을 때 자기 군주 나리의 사무실 문이 열려 있고 방은 비어 있는 것을 보았다고 생각했다."], ["That she went to the ripped-up window in the little room by the street door to connect her palpitating heart, through the glass, with living things beyond and outside the haunted house.", "그래서 거리로 난 문가의 작은 방에 있는, 뜯어 놓은 창문으로 가서, 두근거리는 가슴을 유리 너머로, 귀신 들린 집 바깥의 살아 있는 것들과 이어 보려 했다고."], ["That she then saw, on the wall over the gateway, the shadows of the two clever ones in conversation above.", "그러고는 대문 위 벽에, 저 위에서 대화를 나누는 두 똑똑한 양반의 그림자가 비치는 것을 보았다고."], ["That she then went upstairs with her shoes in her hand, partly to be near the clever ones as a match for most ghosts, and partly to hear what they were talking about.", "그러고는 신발을 손에 든 채 위층으로 올라갔으니, 한편으로는 웬만한 유령쯤은 당해 낼 그 똑똑한 양반들 가까이에 있고 싶어서였고, 한편으로는 그들이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 들으려는 것이었다고."]], [["‘None of your nonsense with me,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"나한테 그런 헛소리는 안 통하오,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I won’t take it from you.’", "\"당신한테서 그런 건 안 받소.\""]], [["Mrs Flintwinch dreamed that she stood behind the door, which was just ajar, and most distinctly heard her husband say these bold words.", "플린트윈치 부인은 약간 열린 문 뒤에 서서, 남편이 이런 대담한 말을 하는 것을 아주 또렷이 들었다고 꿈꾸었다."]], [["‘Flintwinch,’ returned Mrs Clennam, in her usual strong low voice, ‘there is a demon of anger in you.", "\"플린트윈치,\" 클레넘 부인이 평소의 강하고 낮은 목소리로 대꾸했다."], ["Guard against it.’", "\"당신 안에는 분노의 악마가 있어요. 그것을 경계해요.\""]], [["‘I don’t care whether there’s one or a dozen,’ said Mr Flintwinch, forcibly suggesting in his tone that the higher number was nearer the mark.", "\"하나가 있든 열둘이 있든 상관없소,\" 플린트윈치 씨는 그 더 많은 수가 진실에 가깝다고 말투로 강하게 암시하며 말했다."], ["‘If there was fifty, they should all say, None of your nonsense with me, I won’t take it from you--I’d make ‘em say it, whether they liked it or not.’", "\"쉰이 있다 해도, 그것들 모두 이렇게 말해야 할 거요, 나한테 그런 헛소리는 안 통한다, 당신한테서 그런 건 안 받는다고 — 좋든 싫든 그렇게 말하게 만들 거요.\""]], [["‘What have I done, you wrathful man?’ her strong voice asked.", "\"내가 무엇을 했다고 그러오, 이 화 잘 내는 양반아?\" 그녀의 강한 목소리가 물었다."]], [["‘Done?’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"했냐고?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Dropped down upon me.’", "\"나한테 들이덮쳤지.\""]], [["‘If you mean, remonstrated with you--’", "\"내가 당신에게 충고를 했다는 뜻이라면 —\""]], [["‘Don’t put words into my mouth that I don’t mean,’ said Jeremiah, sticking to his figurative expression with tenacious and impenetrable obstinacy: ‘I mean dropped down upon me.’", "\"내가 뜻하지 않은 말을 내 입에 집어넣지 마시오,\" 제러마이아는 끈질기고 빈틈없는 고집으로 자기 비유적 표현을 고수하며 말했다. \"들이덮쳤다는 뜻이오.\""]], [["‘I remonstrated with you,’ she began again, ‘because--’", "\"내가 당신에게 충고를 한 것은,\" 그녀가 다시 입을 열었다. \"그건 —\""]], [["‘I won’t have it!’ cried Jeremiah.", "\"그건 안 받겠소!\" 제러마이아가 소리쳤다."], ["‘You dropped down upon me.’", "\"당신은 나한테 들이덮쳤소.\""]], [["‘I dropped down upon you, then, you ill-conditioned man,’ (Jeremiah chuckled at having forced her to adopt his phrase,) ‘for having been needlessly significant to Arthur that morning.", "\"그럼 내가 당신한테 들이덮쳤다고 해 둡시다, 이 고약한 양반아,\" (제러마이아는 그녀가 자기 표현을 쓰도록 만든 데에 낄낄 웃었다.) \"그날 아침 아서한테 쓸데없이 의미심장하게 굴었으니 말이오."], ["I have a right to complain of it as almost a breach of confidence.", "나는 그것을 거의 신뢰를 저버린 일로서 불평할 권리가 있어요."], ["You did not mean it--’", "당신은 그럴 뜻이 없었지만 —\""]], [["‘I won’t have it!’ interposed the contradictory Jeremiah, flinging back the concession.", "\"그건 안 받겠소!\" 어깃장 놓는 제러마이아가 그 양보를 도로 내던지며 끼어들었다."], ["‘I did mean it.’", "\"나는 그럴 뜻이 있었소.\""]], [["‘I suppose I must leave you to speak in soliloquy if you choose,’ she replied, after a pause that seemed an angry one.", "\"당신이 굳이 그러겠다면 혼잣말을 하도록 내버려 둘 수밖에 없겠군요,\" 그녀는 화가 난 듯한 침묵 끝에 대꾸했다."], ["‘It is useless my addressing myself to a rash and headstrong old man who has a set purpose not to hear me.’", "\"내 말을 듣지 않겠다고 작정한, 경솔하고 고집 센 늙은이에게 내가 말을 거는 것은 헛된 일이지요.\""]], [["‘Now, I won’t take that from you either,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"이봐, 그것도 안 받겠소,\" 제러마이아가 말했다."], ["‘I have no such purpose.", "\"나한테는 그런 작정 따위 없소."], ["I have told you I did mean it.", "내가 그럴 뜻이 있었다고 했잖소."], ["Do you wish to know why I meant it, you rash and headstrong old woman?’", "내가 왜 그럴 뜻이 있었는지 알고 싶소, 이 경솔하고 고집 센 늙은이여?\""]], [["‘After all, you only restore me my own words,’ she said, struggling with her indignation.", "\"결국 당신은 내 말을 그대로 돌려줄 뿐이군요,\" 그녀는 분을 삭이며 말했다."], ["‘Yes.’", "\"그래요.\""]], [["‘This is why, then.", "\"그럼 이게 그 까닭이오."], ["Because you hadn’t cleared his father to him, and you ought to have done it.", "당신이 그 사람한테 그의 아버지에 대해 깨끗이 밝혀 주지 않았으니까, 그리고 마땅히 밝혔어야 했으니까."], ["Because, before you went into any tantrum about yourself, who are--’", "당신이 당신 자신에 대해 짜증을 부리기 전에, 당신은 —\""]], [["‘Hold there, Flintwinch!’ she cried out in a changed voice: ‘you may go a word too far.’", "\"거기서 멈춰요, 플린트윈치!\" 그녀가 달라진 목소리로 외쳤다. \"한마디가 지나칠 수 있으니.\""]], [["The old man seemed to think so.", "노인도 그렇게 생각하는 듯했다."], ["There was another pause, and he had altered his position in the room, when he spoke again more mildly:", "또 한 차례 침묵이 흘렀고, 그가 방 안에서 자리를 옮긴 뒤에야 다시, 좀 더 누그러진 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘I was going to tell you why it was.", "\"내가 왜 그랬는지 말하려던 참이었소."], ["Because, before you took your own part, I thought you ought to have taken the part of Arthur’s father.", "당신이 당신 자신을 편들기 전에, 마땅히 아서 아버지를 편들었어야 했다고 생각했기 때문이오."], ["Arthur’s father!", "아서의 아버지!"], ["I had no particular love for Arthur’s father.", "나는 아서의 아버지를 딱히 사랑하진 않았소."], ["I served Arthur’s father’s uncle, in this house, when Arthur’s father was not much above me--was poorer as far as his pocket went--and when his uncle might as soon have left me his heir as have left him.", "나는 이 집에서 아서 아버지의 삼촌을 섬겼는데, 그때 아서 아버지는 나보다 그리 위에 있지도 않았소 — 주머니 사정으로 치면 더 가난했지 — 게다가 그 삼촌이 그 사람한테 유산을 남기느니 차라리 나한테 남기는 편이 더 그럴 법했을 정도였소."], ["He starved in the parlour, and I starved in the kitchen; that was the principal difference in our positions; there was not much more than a flight of breakneck stairs between us.", "그 사람은 응접실에서 굶주렸고, 나는 부엌에서 굶주렸으니, 그게 우리 처지의 주된 차이였소. 우리 사이엔 목 부러질 만한 가파른 계단 한 줄 말고는 별다른 게 없었던 거요."], ["I never took to him in those times; I don’t know that I ever took to him greatly at any time.", "그 시절 나는 그 사람한테 정이 든 적이 없었고, 어느 때고 그 사람한테 크게 정이 들었던 기억이 없소."], ["He was an undecided, irresolute chap, who had everything but his orphan life scared out of him when he was young.", "그 사람은 우유부단하고 결단력 없는 친구였는데, 젊었을 때 고아의 목숨만 빼고는 다른 모든 게 겁에 질려 빠져나가 버린 사람이었소."], ["And when he brought you home here, the wife his uncle had named for him, I didn’t need to look at you twice (you were a good-looking woman at that time) to know who’d be master.", "그리고 그 사람이 당신을 이리로 데려왔을 때, 삼촌이 그 사람한테 정해 준 아내였던 당신을 데려왔을 때, 나는 누가 주인이 될지 알아보려고 당신을 두 번 볼 필요도 없었소 (그때 당신은 잘생긴 여자였지)."], ["You have stood of your own strength ever since.", "그 후로 당신은 줄곧 제 힘으로 서 왔소."], ["Stand of your own strength now.", "지금도 제 힘으로 서시오."], ["Don’t lean against the dead.’", "죽은 자에게 기대지 마시오.\""]], [["‘I do _not_--as you call it--lean against the dead.’", "\"나는 결코 — 당신 말마따나 — 죽은 자에게 기대지 않아요.\""]], [["‘But you had a mind to do it, if I had submitted,’ growled Jeremiah, ‘and that’s why you drop down upon me.", "\"하지만 내가 굴복했더라면 당신은 그러려는 마음이 있었지,\" 제러마이아가 으르렁거렸다."], ["You can’t forget that I didn’t submit.", "\"그래서 나한테 들이덮친 거요."], ["I suppose you are astonished that I should consider it worth my while to have justice done to Arthur’s father?", "당신은 내가 굴복하지 않았다는 걸 잊지 못하지. 내가 아서 아버지한테 정의를 행할 만한 가치가 있다고 여긴다는 게 놀랍겠지?"], ["Hey?", "어?"], ["It doesn’t matter whether you answer or not, because I know you are, and you know you are.", "당신이 대답하든 말든 상관없소, 당신이 놀란 걸 나는 알고, 당신도 알고 있으니까."], ["Come, then, I’ll tell you how it is.", "자, 그럼, 어찌 된 일인지 말해 주겠소."], ["I may be a bit of an oddity in point of temper, but this is my temper--I can’t let anybody have entirely their own way.", "나는 성미로 치면 좀 별난 데가 있을지 몰라도, 이게 내 성미요 — 나는 아무도 제멋대로 다 하게 내버려 둘 수 없소."], ["You are a determined woman, and a clever woman; and when you see your purpose before you, nothing will turn you from it.", "당신은 결단력 있는 여자고, 똑똑한 여자요. 그리고 당신은 눈앞에 목적을 보면, 무엇으로도 그것에서 돌이켜지지 않소."], ["Who knows that better than I do?’", "그걸 나보다 더 잘 아는 사람이 어디 있겠소?\""]], [["‘Nothing will turn me from it, Flintwinch, when I have justified it to myself.", "\"무엇으로도 나는 돌이켜지지 않아요, 플린트윈치, 내 자신에게 그것을 정당하다고 입증한 다음에는."], ["Add that.’", "그 말을 덧붙여요.\""]], [["‘Justified it to yourself?", "\"당신 자신에게 정당하다고 입증한 다음에라?"], ["I said you were the most determined woman on the face of the earth (or I meant to say so), and if you are determined to justify any object you entertain, of course you’ll do it.’", "나는 당신이 이 지상에서 가장 결단력 있는 여자라고 했고 (혹은 그렇게 말하려 했고), 만일 당신이 품은 어떤 목적을 정당하다고 입증하기로 작정한다면, 당연히 당신은 그리할 거요.\""]], [["‘Man!", "\"이 양반아!"], ["I justify myself by the authority of these Books,’ she cried, with stern emphasis, and appearing from the sound that followed to strike the dead-weight of her arm upon the table.", "나는 이 책들의 권위로 내 자신을 정당화해요,\" 그녀는 준엄한 강세로 외쳤고, 뒤따른 소리로 미루어 죽은 듯 무거운 팔을 탁자에 내리치는 듯했다."]], [["‘Never mind that,’ returned Jeremiah calmly, ‘we won’t enter into that question at present.", "\"그건 그렇다 치고,\" 제러마이아가 침착하게 대꾸했다."], ["However that may be, you carry out your purposes, and you make everything go down before them.", "\"지금은 그 문제는 따지지 맙시다. 그게 어떻든 간에, 당신은 당신의 목적을 이뤄 내고, 그 앞에서 모든 걸 무릎 꿇게 하지."], ["Now, I won’t go down before them.", "자, 나는 그 앞에서 무릎 꿇지 않겠소."], ["I have been faithful to you, and useful to you, and I am attached to you.", "나는 당신에게 충실했고, 당신에게 쓸모 있었고, 당신에게 애착을 갖고 있소."], ["But I can’t consent, and I won’t consent, and I never did consent, and I never will consent to be lost in you.", "하지만 나는 당신 속에 삼켜져 사라지는 데 동의할 수 없고, 동의하지 않겠고, 한 번도 동의한 적 없으며, 앞으로도 결코 동의하지 않겠소."], ["Swallow up everybody else, and welcome.", "다른 사람들은 다 삼켜 버리시오, 얼마든지 좋소."], ["The peculiarity of my temper is, ma’am, that I won’t be swallowed up alive.’", "내 성미의 별난 점은, 부인, 산 채로 삼켜지지는 않겠다는 거요.\""]], [["Perhaps this had originally been the mainspring of the understanding between them.", "어쩌면 이것이 애초에 두 사람 사이 양해의 핵심 동력이었는지도 모른다."], ["Descrying thus much of force of character in Mr Flintwinch, perhaps Mrs Clennam had deemed alliance with him worth her while.", "플린트윈치 씨에게서 이만큼의 강한 성격을 알아보았기에, 어쩌면 클레넘 부인은 그와 손잡는 편이 제게 이롭다고 여겼는지도 모른다."]], [["‘Enough and more than enough of the subject,’ said she gloomily.", "\"이 이야기는 이만하면 됐고도 남았어요,\" 그녀가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["‘Unless you drop down upon me again,’ returned the persistent Flintwinch, ‘and then you must expect to hear of it again.’", "\"당신이 나한테 다시 들이덮치지만 않는다면,\" 끈질긴 플린트윈치가 대꾸했다. \"그렇지만 또 들이덮친다면, 그땐 또 그 소리를 듣게 될 줄 아시오.\""]], [["Mistress Affery dreamed that the figure of her lord here began walking up and down the room, as if to cool his spleen, and that she ran away; but that, as he did not issue forth when she had stood listening and trembling in the shadowy hall a little time, she crept up-stairs again, impelled as before by ghosts and curiosity, and once more cowered outside the door.", "애프리 부인은, 이 대목에서 자기 나리의 형상이 울화를 식히려는 듯 방 안을 오락가락하기 시작했고, 그래서 자신이 달아났다고 꿈꾸었다. 그러나 그녀가 그늘진 현관에 서서 잠시 귀 기울이며 떨고 있는 동안에도 그가 나오지 않자, 전과 마찬가지로 귀신과 호기심에 떠밀려 다시 살금살금 위층으로 올라가, 또 한 번 문밖에서 몸을 웅크렸다고."]], [["‘Please to light the candle, Flintwinch,’ Mrs Clennam was saying, apparently wishing to draw him back into their usual tone.", "\"양초에 불을 켜요, 플린트윈치,\" 클레넘 부인이 평소의 어조로 그를 되돌리고 싶은 듯 말하고 있었다."], ["‘It is nearly time for tea.", "\"차 마실 시간이 다 됐어요."], ["Little Dorrit is coming, and will find me in the dark.’", "리틀 도릿이 올 텐데, 어두운 데 있는 나를 보게 되겠어요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch lighted the candle briskly, and said as he put it down upon the table:", "플린트윈치 씨는 잽싸게 양초에 불을 켜고, 그것을 탁자 위에 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["‘What are you going to do with Little Dorrit?", "\"리틀 도릿을 어쩔 작정이오?"], ["Is she to come to work here for ever?", "영영 여기 와서 일하라는 거요?"], ["To come to tea here for ever?", "영영 여기 와서 차를 마시라는 거요?"], ["To come backwards and forwards here, in the same way, for ever?’", "줄곧 이렇게 영영 여기를 오가라는 거요?\""]], [["‘How can you talk about “for ever” to a maimed creature like me?", "\"나 같은 불구의 인간한테 어떻게 '영영'이라는 말을 할 수가 있어요?"], ["Are we not all cut down like the grass of the field, and was not I shorn by the scythe many years ago: since when I have been lying here, waiting to be gathered into the barn?’", "우리 모두 들판의 풀처럼 베어지는 게 아닌가요, 그리고 나는 여러 해 전에 낫에 베이지 않았던가요. 그 후로 나는 곳간에 거둬들여지기를 기다리며 여기 누워 있는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Ay, ay!", "\"옳소, 옳소!"], ["But since you have been lying here--not near dead--nothing like it--numbers of children and young people, blooming women, strong men, and what not, have been cut down and carried; and still here are you, you see, not much changed after all.", "하지만 당신이 여기 누워 있던 동안에 — 죽을 지경도 아니고 — 그 비슷하지도 않은데 — 수많은 아이들과 젊은이들이, 한창인 여자들이, 건장한 남자들이, 그 밖에 별별 사람들이 다 베여 실려 갔소. 그런데도 당신은 보다시피 여기 이렇게, 결국 그리 변하지도 않고 있잖소."], ["Your time and mine may be a long one yet.", "당신과 내 시간은 아직 길지도 모르오."], ["When I say for ever, I mean (though I am not poetical) through all our time.’ Mr Flintwinch gave this explanation with great calmness, and calmly waited for an answer.", "내가 '영영'이라고 할 때는, (내가 시인은 아니지만) 우리에게 남은 시간 내내라는 뜻이오.\" 플린트윈치 씨는 이 설명을 무척 침착하게 하고는, 침착하게 대답을 기다렸다."]], [["‘So long as Little Dorrit is quiet and industrious, and stands in need of the slight help I can give her, and deserves it; so long, I suppose, unless she withdraws of her own act, she will continue to come here, I being spared.’", "\"리틀 도릿이 얌전하고 부지런하며, 내가 줄 수 있는 작은 도움을 필요로 하고, 또 그럴 만한 자격이 있는 한, 내 생각에는, 그 애가 제 발로 물러가지 않는 한, 내가 살아 있는 동안에는 계속 여기 올 거예요.\""]], [["‘Nothing more than that?’ said Flintwinch, stroking his mouth and chin.", "\"그것뿐이오?\" 플린트윈치가 입과 턱을 쓰다듬으며 말했다."]], [["‘What should there be more than that!", "\"그것 말고 무엇이 더 있겠어요!"], ["What could there be more than that!’ she ejaculated in her sternly wondering way.", "그것 말고 무엇이 더 있을 수 있겠어요!\" 그녀가 준엄하게 의아해하는 투로 내뱉었다."]], [["Mrs Flintwinch dreamed, that, for the space of a minute or two, they remained looking at each other with the candle between them, and that she somehow derived an impression that they looked at each other fixedly.", "플린트윈치 부인은, 일이 분 동안 두 사람이 그 사이에 양초를 두고 서로를 바라보고 있었으며, 어쩐지 그들이 서로를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있다는 인상을 받았다고 꿈꾸었다."]], [["‘Do you happen to know, Mrs Clennam,’ Affery’s liege lord then demanded in a much lower voice, and with an amount of expression that seemed quite out of proportion to the simple purpose of his words, ‘where she lives?’", "\"혹시 알고 있소, 클레넘 부인,\" 애프리의 군주 나리가 이윽고 훨씬 낮은 목소리로, 그리고 단순한 말의 목적에 견주어 영 어울리지 않을 만큼 무게 실린 표정으로 물었다. \"그 애가 어디 사는지?\" \"몰라요.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘Would you--now, would you like to know?’ said Jeremiah with a pounce as if he had sprung upon her.", "\"알고 싶지 않소 — 자, 알고 싶지 않소?\" 제러마이아가 마치 그녀에게 덤벼들기라도 하듯 와락 물었다."]], [["‘If I cared to know, I should know already.", "\"내가 알고 싶었다면, 진작 알았겠지요."], ["Could I not have asked her any day?’", "아무 날에나 그 애한테 물어볼 수 있지 않았겠어요?\""]], [["‘Then you don’t care to know?’", "\"그럼 알고 싶지 않다는 거요?\""]], [["‘I do not.’", "\"그래요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, having expelled a long significant breath said, with his former emphasis, ‘For I have accidentally--mind!--found out.’", "플린트윈치 씨는 길고 의미심장한 숨을 내뱉고는, 앞서와 같은 강세로 말했다. \"내가 우연히 — 명심하시오! — 알아냈거든.\""]], [["‘Wherever she lives,’ said Mrs Clennam, speaking in one unmodulated hard voice, and separating her words as distinctly as if she were reading them off from separate bits of metal that she took up one by one, ‘she has made a secret of it, and she shall always keep her secret from me.’", "\"그 애가 어디 살든,\" 클레넘 부인이 가락 없이 단단한 한 가지 목소리로, 마치 따로따로 떨어진 금속 조각에서 글자를 하나씩 집어 읽어 내듯 또박또박 말을 끊어 가며 말했다. \"그 애는 그것을 비밀로 했고, 앞으로도 줄곧 그것을 나한테 비밀로 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘After all, perhaps you would rather not have known the fact, any how?’ said Jeremiah; and he said it with a twist, as if his words had come out of him in his own wry shape.", "\"결국, 어쨌든 그 사실을 차라리 모르는 편이 나았겠지?\" 제러마이아가 말했고, 그는 마치 제 말이 제 자신의 비뚤어진 모양새로 나오기라도 하는 듯 비틀린 투로 그것을 말했다."]], [["‘Flintwinch,’ said his mistress and partner, flashing into a sudden energy that made Affery start, ‘why do you goad me?", "\"플린트윈치,\" 그의 주인이자 동업자가 애프리를 흠칫 놀라게 할 만큼 갑작스러운 기세로 번뜩이며 말했다."], ["Look round this room.", "\"어째서 나를 들쑤시는 거요?"], ["If it is any compensation for my long confinement within these narrow limits--not that I complain of being afflicted; you know I never complain of that--if it is any compensation to me for long confinement to this room, that while I am shut up from all pleasant change I am also shut up from the knowledge of some things that I may prefer to avoid knowing, why should you, of all men, grudge me that belief?’", "이 방을 둘러봐요. 이 비좁은 한계 안에 오래 갇혀 있는 데 대한 어떤 보상이라도 된다면 — 고통받는 것을 불평하는 건 아니에요, 그건 내가 결코 불평하지 않는다는 걸 당신도 알잖아요 — 이 방에 오래 갇혀 있는 데 대한 어떤 보상이라도 된다면, 즉 내가 온갖 즐거운 변화로부터 차단된 채, 차라리 모르는 편을 택하고 싶을 어떤 것들에 대한 앎으로부터도 차단되어 있다는 것이 보상이 된다면, 하필 당신이, 다른 누구도 아닌 당신이, 어찌하여 내게서 그 믿음을 아까워하는 거요?\""]], [["‘I don’t grudge it to you,’ returned Jeremiah.", "\"나는 그걸 당신한테 아까워하는 게 아니오,\" 제러마이아가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Then say no more. Say no more.", "\"그럼 더는 말하지 말아요."], ["Let Little Dorrit keep her secret from me, and do you keep it from me also.", "더는 말하지 말아요. 리틀 도릿이 그 비밀을 나한테 간직하게 두고, 당신도 그것을 나한테 간직해요."], ["Let her come and go, unobserved and unquestioned.", "그 애가 보이지 않게, 캐묻지 않은 채 오가게 해요."], ["Let me suffer, and let me have what alleviation belongs to my condition.", "내가 고통받게, 내 처지에 어울리는 위안이나마 갖게 해 둬요."], ["Is it so much, that you torment me like an evil spirit?’", "당신이 악령처럼 나를 괴롭힐 만큼 그게 대단한 일이오?\""]], [["‘I asked you a question.", "\"나는 당신한테 질문을 했을 뿐이오."], ["That’s all.’", "그게 다요.\""]], [["‘I have answered it.", "\"나는 그것에 대답했어요."], ["So, say no more.", "그러니 더는 말하지 말아요."], ["Say no more.’ Here the sound of the wheeled chair was heard upon the floor, and Affery’s bell rang with a hasty jerk.", "더는 말하지 말아요.\" 여기서 바퀴 달린 의자가 바닥을 구르는 소리가 들렸고, 애프리의 종이 황급히 홱 잡아당겨져 울렸다."]], [["More afraid of her husband at the moment than of the mysterious sound in the kitchen, Affery crept away as lightly and as quickly as she could, descended the kitchen stairs almost as rapidly as she had ascended them, resumed her seat before the fire, tucked up her skirt again, and finally threw her apron over her head.", "그 순간 부엌의 알 수 없는 소리보다도 남편이 더 무서웠던 애프리는, 할 수 있는 한 가볍고 빠르게 살금살금 물러나, 올라갈 때 못지않게 잽싸게 부엌 계단을 내려가, 불 앞의 제자리에 다시 앉아, 또 치맛자락을 걷어 올리고, 마침내 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어썼다."], ["Then the bell rang once more, and then once more, and then kept on ringing; in despite of which importunate summons, Affery still sat behind her apron, recovering her breath.", "그러자 종이 한 번 더 울리고, 또 한 번 더 울리더니, 계속해서 울려 댔다. 그 끈질긴 부름에도 아랑곳없이, 애프리는 여전히 앞치마 뒤에 앉아 숨을 고르고 있었다."]], [["At last Mr Flintwinch came shuffling down the staircase into the hall, muttering and calling ‘Affery woman!’ all the way.", "마침내 플린트윈치 씨가 줄곧 \"애프리, 이 여편네야!\" 하고 중얼대며 부르면서 계단을 질질 끌고 현관으로 내려왔다."], ["Affery still remaining behind her apron, he came stumbling down the kitchen stairs, candle in hand, sidled up to her, twitched her apron off, and roused her.", "애프리가 여전히 앞치마 뒤에 남아 있자, 그는 양초를 손에 든 채 부엌 계단을 비틀비틀 내려와, 옆걸음으로 그녀에게 다가가, 앞치마를 홱 잡아채고는 그녀를 깨웠다."]], [["‘Oh Jeremiah!’ cried Affery, waking.", "\"오, 제러마이아!\" 애프리가 깨어나며 외쳤다."], ["‘What a start you gave me!’", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 사람을 놀라게 해요!\""]], [["‘What have you been doing, woman?’ inquired Jeremiah.", "\"뭘 하고 있었던 거야, 이 여편네야?\" 제러마이아가 물었다."], ["‘You’ve been rung for fifty times.’", "\"쉰 번도 더 종을 울려 댔는데.\""]], [["‘Oh Jeremiah,’ said Mistress Affery, ‘I have been a-dreaming!’", "\"오, 제러마이아,\" 애프리 부인이 말했다. \"나 꿈을 꾸고 있었어요!\""]], [["Reminded of her former achievement in that way, Mr Flintwinch held the candle to her head, as if he had some idea of lighting her up for the illumination of the kitchen.", "그런 식의 지난번 일을 떠올린 플린트윈치 씨는, 마치 부엌을 밝히려고 그녀에게 불이라도 붙일 생각인 양 양초를 그녀의 머리에 갖다 댔다."]], [["‘Don’t you know it’s her tea-time?’ he demanded with a vicious grin, and giving one of the legs of Mistress Affery’s chair a kick.", "\"마님 차 드실 시간인 거 모르나?\" 그가 심술궂게 이를 드러내며 묻고는, 애프리 부인의 의자 다리 하나를 걷어찼다."]], [["‘Jeremiah?", "\"제러마이아?"], ["Tea-time?", "차 시간?"], ["I don’t know what’s come to me.", "내가 어떻게 된 건지 모르겠어요."], ["But I got such a dreadful turn, Jeremiah, before I went--off a-dreaming, that I think it must be that.’", "그런데 꿈을 — 꿈을 꾸기 전에, 제러마이아, 어찌나 무시무시하게 가슴이 철렁했던지, 그래서 그런가 봐요.\""]], [["‘Yoogh!", "\"으악! 잠꾸러기 같으니!\""], ["Sleepy-Head!’ said Mr Flintwinch, ‘what are you talking about?’", "플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"무슨 소리를 하는 거야?\""]], [["‘Such a strange noise, Jeremiah, and such a curious movement.", "\"어찌나 이상한 소리였는지, 제러마이아, 그리고 어찌나 묘한 움직임이었는지."], ["In the kitchen here--just here.’", "여기 부엌에서 — 바로 여기서.\""]], [["Jeremiah held up his light and looked at the blackened ceiling, held down his light and looked at the damp stone floor, turned round with his light and looked about at the spotted and blotched walls.", "제러마이아는 불을 치켜들어 거뭇해진 천장을 보고, 불을 내려 축축한 돌바닥을 보고, 불을 들고 돌아서서 얼룩지고 거뭇거뭇한 벽들을 둘러보았다."]], [["‘Rats, cats, water, drains,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"쥐, 고양이, 물, 배수구겠지,\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["Mistress Affery negatived each with a shake of her head.", "애프리 부인은 고개를 저으며 그 하나하나를 부정했다."], ["‘No, Jeremiah; I have felt it before.", "\"아니에요, 제러마이아. 전에도 느꼈어요."], ["I have felt it up-stairs, and once on the staircase as I was going from her room to ours in the night--a rustle and a sort of trembling touch behind me.’", "위층에서도 느꼈고, 한 번은 밤에 마님 방에서 우리 방으로 가는 계단에서도 느꼈어요 — 바스락거리는 소리하고, 등 뒤에서 떨리듯 닿는 어떤 손길을요.\""]], [["‘Affery, my woman,’ said Mr Flintwinch grimly, after advancing his nose to that lady’s lips as a test for the detection of spirituous liquors, ‘if you don’t get tea pretty quick, old woman, you’ll become sensible of a rustle and a touch that’ll send you flying to the other end of the kitchen.’", "\"애프리, 이 여편네야,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 술 냄새를 가려내려는 시험 삼아 코를 그 부인의 입술에 갖다 댄 뒤 음산하게 말했다. \"이 늙은 여편네야, 차를 냉큼 내오지 않으면, 자네는 자네를 부엌 저쪽 끝까지 날려 보낼 바스락 소리와 손길을 똑똑히 느끼게 될 줄 알아.\""]], [["This prediction stimulated Mrs Flintwinch to bestir herself, and to hasten up-stairs to Mrs Clennam’s chamber.", "이 예언은 플린트윈치 부인을 부추겨 부지런히 움직이게 했고, 서둘러 위층 클레넘 부인의 방으로 올라가게 했다."], ["But, for all that, she now began to entertain a settled conviction that there was something wrong in the gloomy house.", "그렇지만 그래도 이제 그녀는 그 음울한 집에 무언가 잘못된 것이 있다는 확고한 확신을 품기 시작했다."], ["Henceforth, she was never at peace in it after daylight departed; and never went up or down stairs in the dark without having her apron over her head, lest she should see something.", "그 후로 그녀는 날이 저문 뒤에는 그 집에서 결코 마음이 편치 않았고, 무언가를 보게 될까 봐 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰지 않고서는 어둠 속에서 결코 계단을 오르내리지 않았다."]], [["What with these ghostly apprehensions and her singular dreams, Mrs Flintwinch fell that evening into a haunted state of mind, from which it may be long before this present narrative descries any trace of her recovery.", "이러한 유령에 대한 두려움과 기묘한 꿈들이 겹쳐, 그날 저녁 플린트윈치 부인은 귀신에 들린 듯한 마음 상태에 빠졌는데, 지금 이 이야기가 그녀가 거기서 회복한 흔적을 찾아내기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸릴지도 모른다."], ["In the vagueness and indistinctness of all her new experiences and perceptions, as everything about her was mysterious to herself she began to be mysterious to others: and became as difficult to be made out to anybody’s satisfaction as she found the house and everything in it difficult to make out to her own.", "자신의 모든 새로운 경험과 지각이 어렴풋하고 불분명한 가운데, 자기 주변의 모든 것이 그녀 자신에게 알 수 없는 것이었던 만큼 그녀 역시 남들에게 알 수 없는 사람이 되어 갔다. 그리고 그녀가 그 집과 그 안의 모든 것을 자기 나름으로 알아내기 어려워했듯, 그녀도 누구에게나 만족스럽게 알아내기 어려운 사람이 되었다."]], [["She had not yet finished preparing Mrs Clennam’s tea, when the soft knock came to the door which always announced Little Dorrit.", "그녀가 아직 클레넘 부인의 차 준비를 끝내지도 않았을 때, 늘 리틀 도릿을 알리는 그 부드러운 노크 소리가 문에 울렸다."], ["Mistress Affery looked on at Little Dorrit taking off her homely bonnet in the hall, and at Mr Flintwinch scraping his jaws and contemplating her in silence, as expecting some wonderful consequence to ensue which would frighten her out of her five wits or blow them all three to pieces.", "애프리 부인은 리틀 도릿이 현관에서 그 소박한 보닛을 벗는 것을, 그리고 플린트윈치 씨가 턱을 긁적이며 말없이 그녀를 바라보는 것을, 마치 그녀를 혼비백산하게 만들거나 셋 모두를 산산조각 낼 어떤 놀라운 결과가 뒤따르기를 기다리기라도 하는 듯, 지켜보았다."]], [["After tea there came another knock at the door, announcing Arthur.", "차를 마신 뒤 또 한 번 문에 노크 소리가 나서 아서가 왔음을 알렸다."], ["Mistress Affery went down to let him in, and he said on entering, ‘Affery, I am glad it’s you.", "애프리 부인은 내려가 그를 들였고, 그는 들어서며 말했다. \"애프리, 당신이라 다행이군요."], ["I want to ask you a question.’ Affery immediately replied, ‘For goodness sake don’t ask me nothing, Arthur!", "당신한테 물어볼 게 있어요.\" 애프리는 즉시 대답했다."], ["I am frightened out of one half of my life, and dreamed out of the other.", "\"제발 부탁이니 나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요, 아서! 나는 한쪽 목숨은 무서워 혼이 다 나갔고, 다른 한쪽은 꿈으로 다 빠져나가 버렸어요."], ["Don’t ask me nothing!", "나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요!"], ["I don’t know which is which, or what is what!’--and immediately started away from him, and came near him no more.", "어느 게 어느 건지, 뭐가 뭔지 모르겠다고요!\" 그러고는 곧장 그에게서 달아나, 다시는 그의 곁에 가까이 오지 않았다."]], [["Mistress Affery having no taste for reading, and no sufficient light for needlework in the subdued room, supposing her to have the inclination, now sat every night in the dimness from which she had momentarily emerged on the evening of Arthur Clennam’s return, occupied with crowds of wild speculations and suspicions respecting her mistress and her husband and the noises in the house.", "애프리 부인은 독서에 취미가 없었고, 설령 마음이 있다 한들 그 침침한 방에서는 바느질을 할 만한 빛도 충분치 않았으므로, 이제 그녀는 아서 클레넘이 돌아온 그날 저녁에 잠시 모습을 드러냈던 그 어스름 속에서 매일 밤을 보내며, 제 주인마님과 남편과 집안의 그 소리들에 관한 야릇한 추측과 의심을 잔뜩 떠안고 있었다."], ["When the ferocious devotional exercises were engaged in, these speculations would distract Mistress Affery’s eyes towards the door, as if she expected some dark form to appear at those propitious moments, and make the party one too many.", "그 사나운 신앙 의식이 거행될 때면, 이런 추측들이 애프리 부인의 눈길을 문 쪽으로 흩뜨려 놓곤 했는데, 마치 그 신성한 순간에 어떤 어두운 형상이 나타나 그 모임의 머릿수를 하나 더 채우기라도 할 것처럼 그녀가 기다리는 듯했다."]], [["Otherwise, Affery never said or did anything to attract the attention of the two clever ones towards her in any marked degree, except on certain occasions, generally at about the quiet hour towards bed-time, when she would suddenly dart out of her dim corner, and whisper with a face of terror to Mr Flintwinch, reading the paper near Mrs Clennam’s little table:", "그 밖에는, 애프리는 그 두 똑똑한 양반의 주의를 자기에게 뚜렷이 끌 만한 말이나 행동을 결코 하지 않았는데, 다만 어떤 경우들, 대개 잠자리에 들 무렵의 그 고요한 시각쯤이면, 그녀가 어둑한 구석에서 갑자기 튀어나와, 클레넘 부인의 작은 탁자 곁에서 신문을 읽고 있는 플린트윈치 씨에게 공포에 질린 얼굴로 속삭이곤 했다."]], [["‘There, Jeremiah!", "\"저기, 제러마이아!"], ["Now!", "지금!"], ["What’s that noise?’", "저 소리 뭐예요?\""]], [["Then the noise, if there were any, would have ceased, and Mr Flintwinch would snarl, turning upon her as if she had cut him down that moment against his will, ‘Affery, old woman, you shall have a dose, old woman, such a dose!", "그러면 그 소리는, 만일 있었다 한들, 그쳐 버렸을 것이고, 플린트윈치 씨는 마치 그녀가 방금 그를 제 뜻에 거슬러 베어 넘기기라도 한 듯 그녀에게 홱 돌아서서 으르렁댈 것이었다. \"애프리, 이 늙은 여편네야, 자네 한 사발 먹어야겠어, 이 늙은 여편네야, 아주 톡톡한 한 사발을!"], ["You have been dreaming again!’", "자네 또 꿈을 꾸고 있었구먼!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 16. Nobody’s Weakness", "# CHAPTER 16. Nobody’s Weakness"]], [["The time being come for the renewal of his acquaintance with the Meagles family, Clennam, pursuant to contract made between himself and Mr Meagles within the precincts of Bleeding Heart Yard, turned his face on a certain Saturday towards Twickenham, where Mr Meagles had a cottage-residence of his own.", "미글스 가족과의 교분을 다시 잇기로 한 때가 되자, 클레넘은 블리딩 하트 야드 경내에서 자신과 미글스 씨 사이에 맺은 약속에 따라, 어느 토요일에 트위크넘으로 발길을 돌렸다. 그곳에 미글스 씨는 자기 소유의 별장 한 채를 두고 있었다."], ["The weather being fine and dry, and any English road abounding in interest for him who had been so long away, he sent his valise on by the coach, and set out to walk.", "날씨가 맑고 건조한 데다, 영국의 어느 길이든 그토록 오래 외지에 나가 있던 사람에게는 흥미가 가득했으므로, 그는 여행 가방을 역마차로 부쳐 보내고 걸어서 길을 나섰다."], ["A walk was in itself a new enjoyment to him, and one that had rarely diversified his life afar off.", "걷는다는 것 자체가 그에게는 새로운 즐거움이었고, 멀리 외국에 있던 시절에는 그의 삶에 좀처럼 변화를 주지 못했던 일이었다."]], [["He went by Fulham and Putney, for the pleasure of strolling over the heath.", "그는 황야를 거닐어 보는 즐거움을 위해 풀럼과 퍼트니를 거쳐 갔다."], ["It was bright and shining there; and when he found himself so far on his road to Twickenham, he found himself a long way on his road to a number of airier and less substantial destinations.", "그곳은 환하고 눈부셨다. 트위크넘으로 가는 길에서 그만큼 멀리 와 있음을 깨닫자, 그는 한층 공허하고 덜 실체적인 여러 목적지로 향하는 길에서도 한참이나 와 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["They had risen before him fast, in the healthful exercise and the pleasant road.", "건강에 좋은 운동과 기분 좋은 길 위에서 그런 생각들이 그의 앞에 빠르게 떠올랐다."], ["It is not easy to walk alone in the country without musing upon something.", "시골을 홀로 걷는 동안 무언가를 골똘히 생각하지 않기란 쉽지 않은 일이다."], ["And he had plenty of unsettled subjects to meditate upon, though he had been walking to the Land’s End.", "그리고 그에게는 곱씹어 볼 미정의 문제들이 많아서, 설령 랜즈엔드까지 걸어갔다 해도 다 못 끝냈을 정도였다."]], [["First, there was the subject seldom absent from his mind, the question, what he was to do henceforth in life; to what occupation he should devote himself, and in what direction he had best seek it.", "먼저, 그의 마음에서 좀처럼 떠나지 않는 문제가 있었다. 앞으로 살아가며 무엇을 할 것인가, 어떤 직업에 자신을 바칠 것이며, 어느 방향에서 그것을 찾는 것이 가장 좋을 것인가 하는 물음이었다."], ["He was far from rich, and every day of indecision and inaction made his inheritance a source of greater anxiety to him.", "그는 부자와는 거리가 멀었고, 결단을 내리지 못한 채 아무것도 하지 않는 날이 하루하루 지날수록 물려받은 재산은 그에게 더 큰 불안의 근원이 되었다."], ["As often as he began to consider how to increase this inheritance, or to lay it by, so often his misgiving that there was some one with an unsatisfied claim upon his justice, returned; and that alone was a subject to outlast the longest walk.", "이 유산을 어떻게 늘릴까, 혹은 어떻게 모아 둘까 궁리하기 시작할 때마다, 자신의 정의에 충족되지 못한 권리를 가진 누군가가 있을지 모른다는 불안이 되살아났다. 그리고 그것만으로도 아무리 긴 산책이라도 다 못 끝낼 만한 문제였다."], ["Again, there was the subject of his relations with his mother, which were now upon an equable and peaceful but never confidential footing, and whom he saw several times a week.", "또한 어머니와의 관계라는 문제도 있었는데, 그 관계는 이제 평온하고 안정되었으나 결코 흉금을 터놓는 사이는 아니었고, 그는 일주일에 몇 번씩 어머니를 보았다. 리틀 도릿은 으뜸가는, 또 변함없는 주제였다."], ["Little Dorrit was a leading and a constant subject: for the circumstances of his life, united to those of her own story, presented the little creature to him as the only person between whom and himself there were ties of innocent reliance on one hand, and affectionate protection on the other; ties of compassion, respect, unselfish interest, gratitude, and pity.", "그 자신의 삶의 사정이 그녀 자신의 사연과 한데 엮여, 그 작은 사람은, 한편으로는 순결한 의지의 끈으로, 다른 한편으로는 다정한 보호의 끈으로 자신과 맺어진 유일한 사람으로 그에게 다가왔다. 연민과 존중, 사심 없는 관심, 감사, 그리고 측은함의 끈이었다."], ["Thinking of her, and of the possibility of her father’s release from prison by the unbarring hand of death--the only change of circumstance he could foresee that might enable him to be such a friend to her as he wished to be, by altering her whole manner of life, smoothing her rough road, and giving her a home--he regarded her, in that perspective, as his adopted daughter, his poor child of the Marshalsea hushed to rest.", "그녀를 생각하고, 그녀의 아버지가 죽음의 빗장 푸는 손길로 감옥에서 풀려날 가능성을 생각하면서 — 그것이 그가 내다볼 수 있는 유일한 사정의 변화였으니, 그녀의 삶의 방식 전체를 바꾸고, 그녀의 험한 길을 평탄하게 하며, 그녀에게 보금자리를 주어, 그가 바라던 대로 그녀의 벗이 되어 줄 수 있게 할 변화였다 — 그는 그런 관점에서 그녀를 자신의 양녀로, 잠재워 쉬게 한 마셜시의 가엾은 자식으로 여겼다."], ["If there were a last subject in his thoughts, and it lay towards Twickenham, its form was so indefinite that it was little more than the pervading atmosphere in which these other subjects floated before him.", "만일 그의 생각 속에 마지막 주제가 하나 있어 그것이 트위크넘 쪽을 향해 있었다면, 그 형체가 너무도 뚜렷하지 않아서, 이 다른 주제들이 그의 앞에 떠다니던 그 사방에 깔린 분위기와 별반 다르지 않은 정도였다."]], [["He had crossed the heath and was leaving it behind when he gained upon a figure which had been in advance of him for some time, and which, as he gained upon it, he thought he knew.", "황야를 가로질러 그것을 뒤로 남기고 떠날 무렵, 그는 한동안 앞서 가던 어떤 사람을 따라잡게 되었는데, 다가가면서 그 사람을 아는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["He derived this impression from something in the turn of the head, and in the figure’s action of consideration, as it went on at a sufficiently sturdy walk.", "그 느낌은 머리를 돌리는 모양새와, 제법 다부진 걸음으로 나아가면서 무언가를 곰곰이 따지는 듯한 그 사람의 거동에서 비롯되었다."], ["But when the man--for it was a man’s figure--pushed his hat up at the back of his head, and stopped to consider some object before him, he knew it to be Daniel Doyce.", "그러나 그 남자가 — 남자의 모습이었으므로 — 모자를 머리 뒤로 밀어 올리고는 앞에 놓인 무엇인가를 살피려 멈춰 서자, 그는 그가 대니얼 도이스임을 알아보았다."]], [["‘How do you do, Mr Doyce?’ said Clennam, overtaking him.", "\"안녕하십니까, 도이스 씨?\" 클레넘이 그를 따라잡으며 말했다."], ["‘I am glad to see you again, and in a healthier place than the Circumlocution Office.’", "\"다시 뵙게 되어, 그것도 에두름국보다 훨씬 건강에 좋은 곳에서 뵙게 되어 반갑습니다.\""]], [["‘Ha!", "\"하!"], ["Mr Meagles’s friend!’ exclaimed that public criminal, coming out of some mental combinations he had been making, and offering his hand.", "미글스 씨의 친구분이시군요!\" 그 공공의 죄인은 골똘히 머릿속으로 짜 맞추던 생각에서 벗어나며 외치고는 손을 내밀었다."], ["‘I am glad to see you, sir.", "\"반갑습니다, 선생."], ["Will you excuse me if I forget your name?’", "성함을 잊었더라도 양해해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Readily.", "\"기꺼이요."], ["It’s not a celebrated name.", "유명한 이름은 아니니까요."], ["It’s not Barnacle.’", "배너클은 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘No, no,’ said Daniel, laughing.", "\"아니, 아니지요.\" 대니얼이 웃으며 말했다."], ["‘And now I know what it is.", "\"이제 생각났습니다."], ["It’s Clennam.", "클레넘이지요."], ["How do you do, Mr Clennam?’", "안녕하십니까, 클레넘 씨?\""]], [["‘I have some hope,’ said Arthur, as they walked on together, ‘that we may be going to the same place, Mr Doyce.’", "\"바라건대,\" 아서가 둘이 함께 걸으며 말했다, \"우리가 같은 곳으로 가고 있는 게 아닐까 합니다, 도이스 씨.\""]], [["‘Meaning Twickenham?’ returned Daniel.", "\"트위크넘 말씀이지요?\" 대니얼이 받았다."], ["‘I am glad to hear it.’", "\"그렇다니 반갑군요.\""]], [["They were soon quite intimate, and lightened the way with a variety of conversation.", "둘은 곧 꽤 가까워졌고, 갖가지 대화로 길을 가볍게 했다. 그 재간 있는 죄인은 무척 겸손하고 분별 있는 사람이었다."], ["The ingenious culprit was a man of great modesty and good sense; and, though a plain man, had been too much accustomed to combine what was original and daring in conception with what was patient and minute in execution, to be by any means an ordinary man.", "비록 수수한 사람이었으나, 발상에서 독창적이고 대담한 것을, 실행에서 끈기 있고 세밀한 것과 결합하는 데 너무도 익숙해진 터라, 결코 평범한 사람일 수는 없었다. 처음에는 그가 자기 이야기를 하게 만들기가 어려웠는데, 아서가 그쪽으로 다가가면 그는 슬쩍 인정만 했다."], ["It was at first difficult to lead him to speak about himself, and he put off Arthur’s advances in that direction by admitting slightly, oh yes, he had done this, and he had done that, and such a thing was of his making, and such another thing was his discovery, but it was his trade, you see, his trade; until, as he gradually became assured that his companion had a real interest in his account of himself, he frankly yielded to it.", "아, 그렇지요, 이 일도 했고 저 일도 했으며, 이런 것은 자기가 만든 것이고 저런 것은 자기가 발견한 것이지만, 그게 자기 직업이지 않습니까, 그저 직업일 뿐이라고 하면서 말이다. 그러다가 차차 동행이 자기 이야기에 진정으로 관심을 가졌음을 확신하게 되자, 그는 솔직하게 마음을 열었다."], ["Then it appeared that he was the son of a north-country blacksmith, and had originally been apprenticed by his widowed mother to a lock-maker; that he had ‘struck out a few little things’ at the lock-maker’s, which had led to his being released from his indentures with a present, which present had enabled him to gratify his ardent wish to bind himself to a working engineer, under whom he had laboured hard, learned hard, and lived hard, seven years.", "그러고 보니 그는 북부 지방 대장장이의 아들로, 본디 홀어머니에 의해 자물쇠 장인에게 도제로 보내졌다. 자물쇠 장인 밑에서 '작은 것 몇 가지를 고안해 낸'"], ["His time being out, he had ‘worked in the shop’ at weekly wages seven or eight years more; and had then betaken himself to the banks of the Clyde, where he had studied, and filed, and hammered, and improved his knowledge, theoretical and practical, for six or seven years more.", "덕에 선물 하나와 함께 도제 계약에서 풀려났고, 그 선물 덕분에 어느 현업 기술자에게 자신을 맡기고 싶다는 간절한 소망을 이룰 수 있었다. 그 밑에서 그는 칠 년 동안 고되게 일하고, 고되게 배우고, 고되게 살았다."], ["There he had had an offer to go to Lyons, which he had accepted; and from Lyons had been engaged to go to Germany, and in Germany had had an offer to go to St Petersburg, and there had done very well indeed--never better.", "도제 기간이 끝나자 다시 칠팔 년을 주급을 받으며 '작업장에서 일했고', 그러고는 클라이드 강가로 가서 거기서 다시 육칠 년 동안 공부하고, 줄질하고, 망치질하며, 이론적 지식과 실무적 지식을 두루 갈고닦았다. 그곳에서 리옹으로 와 달라는 제안을 받아 수락했고, 리옹에서는 독일로 갈 일을 얻었으며, 독일에서는 상트페테르부르크로 와 달라는 제안을 받아, 그곳에서는 정말이지 아주 잘 해냈다 — 그 어느 때보다도 잘했다."], ["However, he had naturally felt a preference for his own country, and a wish to gain distinction there, and to do whatever service he could do, there rather than elsewhere. And so he had come home.", "그러나 그는 당연히 자기 나라를 더 좋아하는 마음을, 그곳에서 명성을 얻고, 다른 어디보다도 그곳에서 할 수 있는 봉사를 하고 싶다는 바람을 품었다."], ["And so at home he had established himself in business, and had invented and executed, and worked his way on, until, after a dozen years of constant suit and service, he had been enrolled in the Great British Legion of Honour, the Legion of the Rebuffed of the Circumlocution Office, and had been decorated with the Great British Order of Merit, the Order of the Disorder of the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings.", "그리하여 그는 고국으로 돌아왔다. 그리고 고국에서 사업을 차렸고, 발명하고 실행하며 차근차근 길을 헤쳐 나가다가, 십수 년에 걸친 끊임없는 청원과 봉직 끝에 위대한 영국 명예 군단 — 에두름국에 퇴짜 맞은 자들의 군단에 입적되었고, 위대한 영국 공로 훈장 — 배너클가와 스틸트스토킹가의 난맥 훈장을 수여받았다."]], [["‘It is much to be regretted,’ said Clennam, ‘that you ever turned your thoughts that way, Mr Doyce.’", "\"참으로 안타까운 일입니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"도이스 씨, 당신께서 애초에 그쪽으로 생각을 돌리셨다는 것이 말입니다.\""]], [["‘True, sir, true to a certain extent.", "\"맞는 말씀입니다, 선생, 어느 정도까지는요."], ["But what is a man to do?", "하지만 사람이 어쩌겠습니까?"], ["if he has the misfortune to strike out something serviceable to the nation, he must follow where it leads him.’", "불행히도 나라에 쓸모 있는 무언가를 고안해 냈다면, 그게 이끄는 대로 따라가야 하는 법이지요.\""]], [["‘Hadn’t he better let it go?’ said Clennam.", "\"차라리 그냥 흘려보내는 게 낫지 않겠습니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘He can’t do it,’ said Doyce, shaking his head with a thoughtful smile.", "\"그럴 수가 없지요.\" 도이스가 생각에 잠긴 미소를 지으며 고개를 저었다."], ["‘It’s not put into his head to be buried.", "\"그건 묻어 두라고 머리에 들어온 게 아닙니다."], ["It’s put into his head to be made useful.", "쓸모 있게 만들라고 들어온 거지요."], ["You hold your life on the condition that to the last you shall struggle hard for it.", "사람은 끝까지 목숨을 위해 치열하게 다투어야 한다는 조건으로 목숨을 부지하는 법입니다."], ["Every man holds a discovery on the same terms.’", "발명을 품은 사람도 누구나 같은 조건으로 그것을 품는 거고요.\""]], [["‘That is to say,’ said Arthur, with a growing admiration of his quiet companion, ‘you are not finally discouraged even now?’", "\"그 말씀은,\" 아서가 이 조용한 동행에 대한 존경이 점점 자라나며 말했다, \"지금에 이르러서도 결국 낙담하지는 않으셨다는 거군요?\""]], [["‘I have no right to be, if I am,’ returned the other.", "\"낙담했다 한들, 그럴 자격이 없지요.\" 상대가 받았다."], ["‘The thing is as true as it ever was.’", "\"그 물건은 예나 지금이나 한결같이 옳으니까요.\""]], [["When they had walked a little way in silence, Clennam, at once to change the direct point of their conversation and not to change it too abruptly, asked Mr Doyce if he had any partner in his business to relieve him of a portion of its anxieties?", "잠시 말없이 걷던 끝에, 클레넘은 대화의 직접적인 화제를 바꾸되 너무 급작스럽지 않게 바꾸려고, 도이스 씨에게 사업의 근심을 얼마간 덜어 줄 동업자가 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ he returned, ‘not at present.", "\"아니요,\" 그가 답했다."], ["I had when I first entered on it, and a good man he was.", "\"지금은 없습니다. 처음 사업을 시작했을 때는 있었지요, 좋은 사람이었습니다."], ["But he has been dead some years; and as I could not easily take to the notion of another when I lost him, I bought his share for myself and have gone on by myself ever since.", "하지만 몇 해 전에 죽었고, 그를 잃은 뒤로는 다른 사람을 들인다는 생각에 좀처럼 마음이 가지 않아서, 그의 몫을 내가 사들였고 그 이후로 줄곧 혼자 해 왔습니다. 그리고 이런 것도 있지요.\""], ["And here’s another thing,’ he said, stopping for a moment with a good-humoured laugh in his eyes, and laying his closed right hand, with its peculiar suppleness of thumb, on Clennam’s arm, ‘no inventor can be a man of business, you know.’", "그가 잠시 멈춰 서서 눈에 선량한 웃음을 띠고, 엄지가 유달리 잘 구부러지는 오른손을 쥔 채 클레넘의 팔에 얹으며 말했다. \"발명가는 사업가가 될 수 없는 법이거든요, 아시다시피.\""]], [["‘No?’ said Clennam.", "\"그렇습니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Why, so the men of business say,’ he answered, resuming the walk and laughing outright.", "\"아, 사업가들이 그렇게들 말하지요.\" 그가 다시 걸음을 옮기며 대놓고 웃으며 답했다."], ["‘I don’t know why we unfortunate creatures should be supposed to want common sense, but it is generally taken for granted that we do.", "\"왜 우리같이 불운한 부류가 상식이 없다고들 여기는지 모르겠지만, 으레 그러려니 하는 게 보통이에요."], ["Even the best friend I have in the world, our excellent friend over yonder,’ said Doyce, nodding towards Twickenham, ‘extends a sort of protection to me, don’t you know, as a man not quite able to take care of himself?’", "이 세상에서 내가 가진 가장 좋은 친구, 저쪽에 계신 우리의 훌륭한 친구분조차,\" 도이스가 트위크넘 쪽을 가리키며 끄덕였다, \"나를 제 한 몸 제대로 건사하지 못하는 사람인 양 일종의 보호를 베푸신다니까요, 아시겠어요?\""]], [["Arthur Clennam could not help joining in the good-humoured laugh, for he recognised the truth of the description.", "아서 클레넘은 그 선량한 웃음에 함께 끼어들지 않을 수 없었으니, 그 묘사가 사실임을 알아본 까닭이었다."]], [["‘So I find that I must have a partner who is a man of business and not guilty of any inventions,’ said Daniel Doyce, taking off his hat to pass his hand over his forehead, ‘if it’s only in deference to the current opinion, and to uphold the credit of the Works. I don’t think he’ll find that I have been very remiss or confused in my way of conducting them; but that’s for him to say--whoever he is--not for me.’", "\"그래서 나는 사업가이면서 발명이라곤 일절 저지르지 않는 동업자를 두어야겠다고 보는 겁니다.\" 대니얼 도이스가 모자를 벗어 이마를 손으로 쓸며 말했다. \"그저 세상의 통념을 존중해서라도, 또 공장의 신용을 지키기 위해서라도 말입니다. 내가 공장을 운영해 온 방식이 그리 태만하거나 어수선했다고 그가 보지는 않을 겁니다. 하지만 그건 그가 — 그게 누구든 간에 — 할 말이지, 내가 할 말은 아니지요.\""]], [["‘You have not chosen him yet, then?’", "\"그럼 아직 그 사람을 고르지는 않으셨군요?\""]], [["‘No, sir, no. I have only just come to a decision to take one.", "\"네, 선생, 못 골랐습니다."], ["The fact is, there’s more to do than there used to be, and the Works are enough for me as I grow older.", "그저 한 사람을 들이기로 막 결심했을 따름이에요. 사실 예전보다 할 일이 많아졌고, 나이가 들어가니 공장만으로도 벅찹니다."], ["What with the books and correspondence, and foreign journeys for which a Principal is necessary, I can’t do all.", "장부와 서신, 또 책임자가 꼭 가야 하는 해외 출장 같은 걸 다 하자니 도저히 혼자서는 못 하겠어요."], ["I am going to talk over the best way of negotiating the matter, if I find a spare half-hour between this and Monday morning, with my--my Nurse and protector,’ said Doyce, with laughing eyes again.", "오늘과 월요일 아침 사이에 짬이 반 시간이라도 나면, 그 문제를 어떻게 풀어 가는 게 가장 좋을지 나의 — 나의 보모이자 보호자와 의논해 볼 생각입니다.\" 도이스가 다시 눈에 웃음을 띠고 말했다."], ["‘He is a sagacious man in business, and has had a good apprenticeship to it.’", "\"그분은 사업에 영민한 사람이고, 좋은 수련도 거치셨지요.\""]], [["After this, they conversed on different subjects until they arrived at their journey’s end.", "그 뒤로 둘은 여러 가지 화제로 이야기를 나누다 마침내 여정의 끝에 다다랐다."], ["A composed and unobtrusive self-sustainment was noticeable in Daniel Doyce--a calm knowledge that what was true must remain true, in spite of all the Barnacles in the family ocean, and would be just the truth, and neither more nor less when even that sea had run dry--which had a kind of greatness in it, though not of the official quality.", "대니얼 도이스에게는 차분하면서도 드러내지 않는 자기 지탱의 태도가 엿보였다 — 집안의 대양에 우글거리는 모든 배너클가 사람들에도 불구하고 참된 것은 참된 것으로 남게 마련이며, 설령 그 바다마저 말라 버려도 그저 진실 그대로일 뿐 더도 덜도 아니라는 고요한 앎이었는데, 관청식 종류의 위대함은 아니었으나 그 안에는 일종의 위대함이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["As he knew the house well, he conducted Arthur to it by the way that showed it to the best advantage.", "그는 그 집을 잘 알았으므로, 가장 좋게 보이는 길로 아서를 안내했다."], ["It was a charming place (none the worse for being a little eccentric), on the road by the river, and just what the residence of the Meagles family ought to be.", "그것은 강가 길에 자리한 매력적인 곳이었고(다소 별난 데가 있었지만 그게 흠은 아니었다), 미글스 가족의 거처라면 마땅히 그래야 할 만한 집이었다."], ["It stood in a garden, no doubt as fresh and beautiful in the May of the Year as Pet now was in the May of her life; and it was defended by a goodly show of handsome trees and spreading evergreens, as Pet was by Mr and Mrs Meagles.", "집은 정원 안에 서 있었는데, 그 정원은 한 해의 오월에, 펫이 지금 제 생애의 오월에 그러하듯 싱그럽고 아름다웠음에 틀림없었다. 또 미글스 씨 부부가 펫을 지켜 주듯, 집은 보기 좋은 멋진 나무들과 넓게 우거진 상록수들로 든든히 보호받고 있었다."], ["It was made out of an old brick house, of which a part had been altogether pulled down, and another part had been changed into the present cottage; so there was a hale elderly portion, to represent Mr and Mrs Meagles, and a young picturesque, very pretty portion to represent Pet.", "그것은 옛 벽돌집을 고쳐 만든 것으로, 일부는 아예 헐어 버렸고 다른 일부는 지금의 별장으로 바꾼 것이었다. 그래서 미글스 씨 부부를 대신할 정정한 노년의 부분이 있었고, 펫을 대신할 젊고 그림처럼 어여쁜 부분이 있었다."], ["There was even the later addition of a conservatory sheltering itself against it, uncertain of hue in its deep-stained glass, and in its more transparent portions flashing to the sun’s rays, now like fire and now like harmless water drops; which might have stood for Tattycoram.", "심지어 나중에 덧붙인 온실 하나가 집에 기대어 몸을 숨기고 있었는데, 짙게 물든 유리 탓에 빛깔이 아리송했고, 더 투명한 부분들은 햇살에 번쩍여 어떤 때는 불 같고 어떤 때는 무해한 물방울 같았으니, 태티코럼을 대신할 만했다."], ["Within view was the peaceful river and the ferry-boat, to moralise to all the inmates saying: Young or old, passionate or tranquil, chafing or content, you, thus runs the current always.", "시야에는 평온한 강과 나룻배가 들어왔는데, 그것은 모든 거주자에게 이렇게 타이르는 듯했다. 젊든 늙든, 격정에 찼든 잔잔하든, 안달하든 흡족하든, 그대들이여, 물길은 늘 이렇게 흐른다오."], ["Let the heart swell into what discord it will, thus plays the rippling water on the prow of the ferry-boat ever the same tune.", "가슴이 어떤 불협화음으로 부풀어 오르든, 잔물결 이는 물은 늘 나룻배 뱃머리에서 한결같은 가락을 연주한다오."], ["Year after year, so much allowance for the drifting of the boat, so many miles an hour the flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet, upon this road that steadily runs away; while you, upon your flowing road of time, are so capricious and distracted.", "해마다, 배가 떠밀려 가는 정도는 이만큼, 시냇물 흐르는 속도는 한 시간에 몇 마일, 여기엔 골풀이, 저기엔 백합이, 무엇 하나 불확실하거나 불안한 것 없이, 꾸준히 흘러가는 이 길 위에서는 그러하다오. 한데 그대들은, 흘러가는 그대들의 시간이라는 길 위에서, 이리도 변덕스럽고 산란하구려."]], [["The bell at the gate had scarcely sounded when Mr Meagles came out to receive them.", "문간의 종이 채 울리기도 전에 미글스 씨가 그들을 맞으러 나왔다."], ["Mr Meagles had scarcely come out, when Mrs Meagles came out.", "미글스 씨가 채 나오기도 전에 미글스 부인이 나왔다."], ["Mrs Meagles had scarcely come out, when Pet came out.", "미글스 부인이 채 나오기도 전에 펫이 나왔다."], ["Pet scarcely had come out, when Tattycoram came out.", "펫이 채 나오기도 전에 태티코럼이 나왔다."], ["Never had visitors a more hospitable reception.", "손님이 이보다 더 환대받은 적은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["‘Here we are, you see,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘boxed up, Mr Clennam, within our own home-limits, as if we were never going to expand--that is, travel--again.", "\"보시다시피, 우리는 이렇게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Not like Marseilles, eh?", "\"클레넘 씨, 우리 집의 울타리 안에 틀어박혀 있답니다. 마치 다시는 넓혀 나갈 — 그러니까 여행할 — 생각이 없는 사람처럼 말이지요."], ["No allonging and marshonging here!’", "마르세유와는 다르지요, 안 그래요? 여기선 알롱하고 마르송할 일이 없으니!\""]], [["‘A different kind of beauty, indeed!’ said Clennam, looking about him.", "\"과연 다른 종류의 아름다움이군요!\" 클레넘이 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘But, Lord bless me!’ cried Mr Meagles, rubbing his hands with a relish, ‘it was an uncommonly pleasant thing being in quarantine, wasn’t it?", "\"하지만 이거 참!\" 미글스 씨가 흡족하게 두 손을 비비며 외쳤다."], ["Do you know, I have often wished myself back again?", "\"검역소에 갇혀 있던 것도 유달리 즐거운 일이었지요, 안 그렇습니까? 저, 내가 그때로 돌아갔으면 하고 종종 바란다는 거 아십니까?"], ["We were a capital party.’", "우리 정말 굉장한 일행이었지요.\""]], [["This was Mr Meagles’s invariable habit.", "이것이 미글스 씨의 변함없는 버릇이었다."], ["Always to object to everything while he was travelling, and always to want to get back to it when he was not travelling.", "여행하는 동안에는 늘 모든 것에 트집을 잡고, 여행하지 않을 때는 늘 그리로 돌아가고 싶어 하는 것 말이다."]], [["‘If it was summer-time,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘which I wish it was on your account, and in order that you might see the place at its best, you would hardly be able to hear yourself speak for birds. Being practical people, we never allow anybody to scare the birds; and the birds, being practical people too, come about us in myriads. We are delighted to see you, Clennam (if you’ll allow me, I shall drop the Mister); I heartily assure you, we are delighted.’", "\"지금이 여름이라면,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"당신을 위해서도, 또 당신이 이곳을 가장 좋을 때 보았으면 하는 마음에서도 여름이었으면 싶은데, 그랬다면 새소리 때문에 제 말소리조차 듣기 어려웠을 겁니다. 우린 실용적인 사람들이라 누구도 새를 쫓지 못하게 하지요. 그래서 새들도, 역시 실용적인 녀석들인지라, 떼를 지어 우리 곁으로 몰려든답니다. 당신을 만나 정말 기쁩니다, 클레넘(괜찮으시다면 '씨' 자는 떼겠습니다). 진심으로 장담하건대, 정말 기쁩니다.\""]], [["‘I have not had so pleasant a greeting,’ said Clennam--then he recalled what Little Dorrit had said to him in his own room, and faithfully added ‘except once--since we last walked to and fro, looking down at the Mediterranean.’", "\"이토록 기분 좋은 환대를 받아 본 적이 없습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다 — 그러다 리틀 도릿이 자기 방에서 그에게 했던 말이 떠올라, 정직하게 덧붙였다. \"단 한 번을 빼고는요 — 우리가 지중해를 내려다보며 마지막으로 거닐었던 그때 이후로 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ returned Mr Meagles.", "\"아!\" 미글스 씨가 받았다."], ["‘Something like a look out, _that_ was, wasn’t it?", "\"그게 참 경치 한번 볼만한 곳이었지요, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["I don’t want a military government, but I shouldn’t mind a little allonging and marshonging--just a dash of it--in this neighbourhood sometimes.", "군사 정부야 바라지 않지만, 이 동네에도 가끔은 알롱하고 마르송하는 게 — 그저 살짝이라도 — 있어도 나쁘진 않을 텐데요."], ["It’s Devilish still.’", "여긴 지독하게 고요하거든요.\""]], [["Bestowing this eulogium on the retired character of his retreat with a dubious shake of the head, Mr Meagles led the way into the house.", "이렇게 자기 은신처의 한적한 정취에 미심쩍게 고개를 저으며 찬사를 보내고는, 미글스 씨가 앞장서서 집 안으로 들어갔다."], ["It was just large enough, and no more; was as pretty within as it was without, and was perfectly well-arranged and comfortable.", "집은 꼭 알맞을 만큼 컸지 그 이상은 아니었고, 바깥만큼이나 안도 어여뻤으며, 완벽하게 잘 정돈되어 안락했다."], ["Some traces of the migratory habits of the family were to be observed in the covered frames and furniture, and wrapped-up hangings; but it was easy to see that it was one of Mr Meagles’s whims to have the cottage always kept, in their absence, as if they were always coming back the day after to-morrow.", "가족의 떠돌이 습성의 흔적이 천을 씌운 액자며 가구, 싸 둔 휘장 따위에서 엿보였다. 그러나 그들이 없는 동안에도 별장을 늘, 마치 모레쯤 돌아올 것처럼 그대로 유지해 두는 것이 미글스 씨의 별난 고집 가운데 하나임을 쉬이 알 수 있었다."], ["Of articles collected on his various expeditions, there was such a vast miscellany that it was like the dwelling of an amiable Corsair.", "갖가지 원정에서 그러모은 물건들이 어찌나 잡다하게 많은지, 마치 마음씨 좋은 해적의 거처 같았다."], ["There were antiquities from Central Italy, made by the best modern houses in that department of industry; bits of mummy from Egypt (and perhaps Birmingham); model gondolas from Venice; model villages from Switzerland; morsels of tesselated pavement from Herculaneum and Pompeii, like petrified minced veal; ashes out of tombs, and lava out of Vesuvius; Spanish fans, Spezzian straw hats, Moorish slippers, Tuscan hairpins, Carrara sculpture, Trastaverini scarves, Genoese velvets and filigree, Neapolitan coral, Roman cameos, Geneva jewellery, Arab lanterns, rosaries blest all round by the Pope himself, and an infinite variety of lumber.", "중부 이탈리아에서 온 골동품들이 있었는데, 그 분야에서 가장 솜씨 좋은 현대 업체가 만든 것이었고, 이집트(어쩌면 버밍엄)에서 온 미라 조각들, 베네치아의 곤돌라 모형, 스위스의 마을 모형, 헤르쿨라네움과 폼페이에서 온 모자이크 포석 부스러기들 — 마치 화석이 된 다진 송아지 고기 같았다 — 무덤에서 나온 재와 베수비오에서 나온 용암, 에스파냐 부채, 스페치아 밀짚모자, 무어식 슬리퍼, 토스카나 머리핀, 카라라 조각, 트라스테베레 스카프, 제노바 벨벳과 금세공, 나폴리 산호, 로마 카메오, 제네바 보석, 아랍 등불, 교황이 손수 두루 축복해 준 묵주, 그리고 무한히 다양한 잡동사니가 있었다. 무수한 장소의, 닮기도 하고 닮지 않기도 한 풍경화들이 있었다."], ["There were views, like and unlike, of a multitude of places; and there was one little picture-room devoted to a few of the regular sticky old Saints, with sinews like whipcord, hair like Neptune’s, wrinkles like tattooing, and such coats of varnish that every holy personage served for a fly-trap, and became what is now called in the vulgar tongue a Catch-em-alive O.", "그리고 작은 그림방 하나가 있었는데, 거기엔 여느 끈적끈적한 옛 성인들 몇이 모셔져 있었으니, 채찍 끈 같은 힘줄에, 넵투누스 같은 머리칼에, 문신 같은 주름에, 어찌나 두껍게 광택제를 발랐는지 거룩한 인물 하나하나가 파리잡이 구실을 하여, 요즘 속된 말로 '산 채로 잡아라 오' 따위가 되어 버렸다."], ["Of these pictorial acquisitions Mr Meagles spoke in the usual manner.", "이렇게 사들인 그림들에 대해 미글스 씨는 늘 같은 식으로 말했다."], ["He was no judge, he said, except of what pleased himself; he had picked them up, dirt-cheap, and people _had_ considered them rather fine.", "자기는 그저 제 마음에 드는 것 말고는 볼 줄 모른다고, 헐값에 주워 온 것인데 사람들이 제법 훌륭하다고 여기더라고 했다."], ["One man, who at any rate ought to know something of the subject, had declared that ‘Sage, Reading’ (a specially oily old gentleman in a blanket, with a swan’s-down tippet for a beard, and a web of cracks all over him like rich pie-crust), to be a fine Guercino.", "적어도 이 분야를 좀 안다 할 만한 어느 사람이 단언하기를, '독서하는 현자'(담요를 두르고, 백조 솜털로 수염을 삼고, 진한 파이 껍질처럼 온통 금이 그물처럼 간, 유난히 기름진 노신사)는 훌륭한 게르치노 작품이라고 했다."], ["As for Sebastian del Piombo there, you would judge for yourself; if it were not his later manner, the question was, Who was it?", "저쪽의 세바스티아노 델 피옴보로 말하자면, 직접 판단하시라고 했다. 그의 후기 화풍이 아니라면, 그럼 대체 누구 거란 말인가?"], ["Titian, that might or might not be--perhaps he had only touched it.", "티치아노라면, 맞을 수도 아닐 수도 있지만 — 아마 그가 손만 좀 댔을지도 모른다고 했다."], ["Daniel Doyce said perhaps he hadn’t touched it, but Mr Meagles rather declined to overhear the remark.", "대니얼 도이스가 아마 손도 안 댔을 거라고 하자, 미글스 씨는 그 말을 짐짓 못 들은 척했다."]], [["When he had shown all his spoils, Mr Meagles took them into his own snug room overlooking the lawn, which was fitted up in part like a dressing-room and in part like an office, and in which, upon a kind of counter-desk, were a pair of brass scales for weighing gold, and a scoop for shovelling out money.", "전리품을 죄다 보여 주고 나서, 미글스 씨는 그들을 잔디밭이 내다보이는 자신의 아늑한 방으로 데려갔는데, 그 방은 일부는 화장실처럼, 일부는 사무실처럼 꾸며져 있었고, 그 안의 일종의 카운터 책상 위에는 금의 무게를 다는 놋쇠 저울 한 쌍과, 돈을 퍼내는 삽 하나가 놓여 있었다."]], [["‘Here they are, you see,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"보시다시피, 여기 있습니다.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I stood behind these two articles five-and-thirty years running, when I no more thought of gadding about than I now think of--staying at home.", "\"삼십오 년 내리 나는 이 두 물건 뒤에 서 있었지요. 그땐 지금 집에 눌러앉을 생각 안 하듯 쏘다닐 생각 따윈 안 했답니다."], ["When I left the Bank for good, I asked for them, and brought them away with me.", "은행을 아주 떠날 때, 이것들을 달라고 청해서 가지고 나왔지요."], ["I mention it at once, or you might suppose that I sit in my counting-house (as Pet says I do), like the king in the poem of the four-and-twenty blackbirds, counting out my money.’", "미리 말씀드리는 겁니다. 안 그러면 당신은 내가 (펫이 말하듯) 〈검은새 스물네 마리〉라는 시 속의 임금님처럼 회계실에 앉아 제 돈이나 세고 있는 줄 아실 테니까요.\""]], [["Clennam’s eyes had strayed to a natural picture on the wall, of two pretty little girls with their arms entwined.", "클레넘의 눈은 벽에 걸린 자연스러운 그림 한 점으로 흘러갔는데, 팔짱을 낀 어여쁜 여자아이 둘을 그린 것이었다."], ["‘Yes, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, in a lower voice.", "\"그래요, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 목소리를 낮추어 말했다."], ["‘There they both are.", "\"저기 둘 다 있지요."], ["It was taken some seventeen years ago.", "한 십칠 년쯤 전에 그린 겁니다."], ["As I often say to Mother, they were babies then.’", "어멈한테 종종 말하듯, 그땐 갓난쟁이들이었어요.\""]], [["‘Their names?’ said Arthur.", "\"이름이 어떻게 되지요?\" 아서가 물었다."]], [["‘Ah, to be sure!", "\"아, 그렇지!"], ["You have never heard any name but Pet.", "펫 말고 다른 이름은 들어 본 적이 없으시겠군요."], ["Pet’s name is Minnie; her sister’s Lillie.’", "펫의 이름은 미니랍니다. 언니는 릴리이고요.\""]], [["‘Should you have known, Mr Clennam, that one of them was meant for me?’ asked Pet herself, now standing in the doorway.", "\"클레넘 씨, 저 둘 중 하나가 저라는 걸 아시겠어요?\" 펫 자신이 이제 문간에 서서 물었다."]], [["‘I might have thought that both of them were meant for you, both are still so like you. Indeed,’ said Clennam, glancing from the fair original to the picture and back, ‘I cannot even now say which is not your portrait.’", "\"둘 다 당신이라고 생각했을 법합니다. 둘 다 여전히 그토록 당신을 닮았으니까요. 정말이지,\" 클레넘이 아리따운 실물에서 그림으로, 다시 실물로 눈길을 옮기며 말했다. \"지금도 어느 쪽이 당신 초상이 아니라고는 말씀드릴 수가 없군요.\""]], [["‘D’ye hear that, Mother?’ cried Mr Meagles to his wife, who had followed her daughter.", "\"여보, 그 말 들었소?\" 미글스 씨가 딸을 뒤따라온 아내에게 외쳤다."], ["‘It’s always the same, Clennam; nobody can decide.", "\"늘 이렇다니까요, 클레넘. 아무도 분간을 못 해요."], ["The child to your left is Pet.’", "당신 왼편에 있는 아이가 펫입니다.\""]], [["The picture happened to be near a looking-glass.", "그 그림은 마침 거울 가까이에 있었다."], ["As Arthur looked at it again, he saw, by the reflection of the mirror, Tattycoram stop in passing outside the door, listen to what was going on, and pass away with an angry and contemptuous frown upon her face, that changed its beauty into ugliness.", "아서가 다시 그것을 바라보다가, 거울에 비친 모습으로, 문밖을 지나가던 태티코럼이 멈춰 서서 안에서 오가는 말에 귀를 기울이고는, 얼굴에 노여움과 경멸의 찌푸림을 띤 채 지나가는 것을 보았다. 그 찌푸림이 그녀의 아름다움을 추함으로 바꾸어 놓았다."]], [["‘But come!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"자, 이런!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You have had a long walk, and will be glad to get your boots off.", "\"먼 길을 걸어오셨으니 부츠를 벗고 싶으실 게요."], ["As to Daniel here, I suppose he’d never think of taking _his_ boots off, unless we showed him a boot-jack.’", "여기 대니얼로 말하자면, 부츠 벗기개를 보여 주지 않는 한 자기 부츠를 벗을 생각조차 안 할 양반이지요.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ asked Daniel, with a significant smile at Clennam.", "\"왜 안 한다는 거지요?\" 대니얼이 클레넘에게 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["You have so many things to think about,’ returned Mr Meagles, clapping him on the shoulder, as if his weakness must not be left to itself on any account.", "자넨 생각할 게 너무 많잖나.\" 미글스 씨가 그의 어깨를 두드리며 받았다."], ["‘Figures, and wheels, and cogs, and levers, and screws, and cylinders, and a thousand things.’", "마치 그의 약점을 무슨 일이 있어도 제멋대로 내버려 두어서는 안 된다는 듯이. \"숫자며, 바퀴며, 톱니며, 지렛대며, 나사며, 실린더며, 그밖에 천 가지 것들 말이야.\""]], [["‘In my calling,’ said Daniel, amused, ‘the greater usually includes the less.", "\"내 직업에서는,\" 대니얼이 재미있어하며 말했다."], ["But never mind, never mind!", "\"큰 것이 으레 작은 것을 포함하는 법이라네."], ["Whatever pleases you, pleases me.’", "하지만 괜찮네, 괜찮아! 자네가 좋다면 나도 좋으이.\""]], [["Clennam could not help speculating, as he seated himself in his room by the fire, whether there might be in the breast of this honest, affectionate, and cordial Mr Meagles, any microscopic portion of the mustard-seed that had sprung up into the great tree of the Circumlocution Office.", "클레넘은 제 방에서 벽난로 곁에 앉으며, 이 정직하고 다정하며 정 깊은 미글스 씨의 가슴속에, 에두름국이라는 큰 나무로 자라난 그 겨자씨가 현미경으로나 보일 만큼이라도 들어 있는 것은 아닐까 하고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["His curious sense of a general superiority to Daniel Doyce, which seemed to be founded, not so much on anything in Doyce’s personal character as on the mere fact of his being an originator and a man out of the beaten track of other men, suggested the idea.", "대니얼 도이스보다 자기가 전반적으로 더 우월하다는 그 묘한 감각은, 도이스의 인품에 있는 무엇 때문이라기보다 그저 그가 무언가를 창안하는 사람이요 여느 사람들이 다니는 길에서 벗어난 사람이라는 단순한 사실에 근거를 둔 듯했는데, 바로 그 점이 그런 생각을 떠올리게 했다."], ["It might have occupied him until he went down to dinner an hour afterwards, if he had not had another question to consider, which had been in his mind so long ago as before he was in quarantine at Marseilles, and which had now returned to it, and was very urgent with it.", "한 시간 뒤 저녁 식사를 하러 내려가기까지 그 생각이 그를 사로잡았을지도 모르나, 그에게는 곰곰이 따져 볼 또 다른 물음이 있었으니, 그것은 마르세유에서 검역을 받기도 전부터 그의 마음속에 있었고, 이제 다시 되돌아와 그를 몹시도 다그쳤다."], ["No less a question than this: Whether he should allow himself to fall in love with Pet?", "다름 아닌 이런 물음이었다. 펫과 사랑에 빠지도록 자신을 내버려 두어도 좋을 것인가?"]], [["He was twice her age.", "그는 그녀의 갑절 나이였다."], ["(He changed the leg he had crossed over the other, and tried the calculation again, but could not bring out the total at less.) He was twice her age.", "(그는 한 다리를 다른 다리 위로 바꿔 꼬고 다시 계산해 보았으나, 그 합계를 그보다 적게 낼 수는 없었다.) 그는 그녀의 갑절 나이였다."], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["He was young in appearance, young in health and strength, young in heart.", "그는 겉보기로 젊었고, 건강과 기력으로도 젊었으며, 마음으로도 젊었다."], ["A man was certainly not old at forty; and many men were not in circumstances to marry, or did not marry, until they had attained that time of life.", "마흔에 늙었다고 할 사람은 분명 없으며, 많은 남자들이 그 나이에 이르도록 결혼할 형편이 못 되거나 결혼하지 않기도 했다."], ["On the other hand, the question was, not what he thought of the point, but what she thought of it.", "그러나 다른 한편으로, 문제는 그가 그 점을 어찌 생각하느냐가 아니라 그녀가 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐였다."]], [["He believed that Mr Meagles was disposed to entertain a ripe regard for him, and he knew that he had a sincere regard for Mr Meagles and his good wife.", "그는 미글스 씨가 자기에게 무르익은 호의를 품으려는 듯하다고 믿었고, 자기도 미글스 씨와 그 선량한 부인에게 진심 어린 호의를 품고 있음을 알았다."], ["He could foresee that to relinquish this beautiful only child, of whom they were so fond, to any husband, would be a trial of their love which perhaps they never yet had had the fortitude to contemplate.", "그토록 애지중지하는 이 아름다운 외동딸을 어떤 남편에게든 내어 준다는 것이, 그들의 사랑으로서는 아마 여태껏 차마 헤아려 볼 용기조차 내지 못한 시련이 되리라는 것을 그는 내다볼 수 있었다."], ["But the more beautiful and winning and charming she, the nearer they must always be to the necessity of approaching it.", "그러나 그녀가 아름답고 사랑스럽고 매혹적일수록, 그들은 늘 그 시련에 다가가야 할 필연에 더욱 가까이 놓일 수밖에 없었다."], ["And why not in his favour, as well as in another’s?", "그리고 다른 남자가 아니라 자신에게라고 안 될 까닭이 무엇인가?"]], [["When he had got so far, it came again into his head that the question was, not what they thought of it, but what she thought of it.", "여기까지 이르렀을 때, 문제는 그들이 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐가 아니라 그녀가 그것을 어찌 생각하느냐라는 데에 다시 생각이 미쳤다."]], [["Arthur Clennam was a retiring man, with a sense of many deficiencies; and he so exalted the merits of the beautiful Minnie in his mind, and depressed his own, that when he pinned himself to this point, his hopes began to fail him.", "아서 클레넘은 물러서기 잘하는 사람이었고, 자신에게 부족한 점이 많다는 자각이 있었다. 그리하여 그는 마음속으로 아름다운 미니의 장점은 한껏 추켜올리고 자신의 장점은 깎아내렸으므로, 이 대목에 자신을 못 박아 두자 그의 희망은 시들기 시작했다."], ["He came to the final resolution, as he made himself ready for dinner, that he would not allow himself to fall in love with Pet.", "저녁 식사를 위해 채비를 갖추며, 그는 마침내 결론에 이르렀다. 펫과 사랑에 빠지도록 자신을 내버려 두지 않겠다는 것이었다."]], [["There were only five, at a round table, and it was very pleasant indeed.", "둥근 식탁에 단 다섯 사람뿐이었으나, 분위기는 정말이지 무척 즐거웠다."], ["They had so many places and people to recall, and they were all so easy and cheerful together (Daniel Doyce either sitting out like an amused spectator at cards, or coming in with some shrewd little experiences of his own, when it happened to be to the purpose), that they might have been together twenty times, and not have known so much of one another.", "그들은 떠올릴 만한 장소며 사람들이 어찌나 많았는지, 또 다 함께 어찌나 격의 없고 명랑했는지(대니얼 도이스는 카드놀이를 흥미롭게 구경하는 사람처럼 빠져 있거나, 마침 적절한 때엔 자기 나름의 빈틈없는 자잘한 경험담을 끼워 넣곤 했다), 마치 스무 번은 같이 어울려 본 사이라 해도 서로를 이만큼은 알지 못했으리라 싶을 정도였다."]], [["‘And Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, after they had recalled a number of fellow-travellers.", "\"그런데 웨이드 양은,\" 미글스 씨가 함께 여행했던 여러 사람을 떠올린 끝에 말했다."], ["‘Has anybody seen Miss Wade?’", "\"누구 웨이드 양을 본 사람 있나?\""]], [["‘I have,’ said Tattycoram.", "\"제가 봤어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다."]], [["She had brought a little mantle which her young mistress had sent for, and was bending over her, putting it on, when she lifted up her dark eyes and made this unexpected answer.", "그녀는 어린 주인 아가씨가 가져오라 한 작은 망토를 가지고 와, 아가씨에게 몸을 굽혀 그것을 걸쳐 주던 참이었는데, 그때 검은 눈을 들어 이 뜻밖의 대답을 한 것이었다."]], [["‘Tatty!’ her young mistress exclaimed.", "\"태티!\" 어린 주인 아가씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘You seen Miss Wade?--where?’", "\"네가 웨이드 양을 봤다고? — 어디서?\""]], [["‘Here, miss,’ said Tattycoram.", "\"여기서요, 아가씨.\""], ["‘How?’", "태티코럼이 말했다."]], [["An impatient glance from Tattycoram seemed, as Clennam saw it, to answer ‘With my eyes!’ But her only answer in words was: ‘I met her near the church.’", "\"어떻게?\" 태티코럼의 못마땅한 눈길은, 클레넘이 보기에, '제 눈으로요!'라고 답하는 듯했다. 그러나 그녀가 말로 한 대답은 이것뿐이었다. \"교회 근처에서 그분을 만났어요.\""]], [["‘What was she doing there I wonder!’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"거기서 대체 뭘 하고 있었을까!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Not going to it, I should think.’", "\"교회에 가려던 건 아닐 텐데 말이야.\""]], [["‘She had written to me first,’ said Tattycoram.", "\"먼저 제게 편지를 보내셨어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh, Tatty!’ murmured her mistress, ‘take your hands away.", "\"아, 태티!\" 주인 아가씨가 중얼거렸다."], ["I feel as if some one else was touching me!’", "\"손 좀 치워 줘. 마치 다른 누가 날 만지는 것 같단 말이야!\""]], [["She said it in a quick involuntary way, but half playfully, and not more petulantly or disagreeably than a favourite child might have done, who laughed next moment.", "그녀는 그 말을 빠르게, 저도 모르게 했으나, 반쯤은 장난스러웠고, 다음 순간 웃음을 터뜨리는 응석받이 아이가 했을 법한 것보다 더 짜증스럽거나 거슬리지는 않았다."], ["Tattycoram set her full red lips together, and crossed her arms upon her bosom.", "태티코럼은 도톰한 붉은 입술을 꽉 다물고는, 두 팔을 가슴 위로 모아 꼈다."]], [["‘Did you wish to know, sir,’ she said, looking at Mr Meagles, ‘what Miss Wade wrote to me about?’", "\"선생님, 웨이드 양이 제게 무슨 편지를 했는지 아시고 싶으세요?\" 그녀가 미글스 씨를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["‘Well, Tattycoram,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘since you ask the question, and we are all friends here, perhaps you may as well mention it, if you are so inclined.’", "\"음, 태티코럼.\" 미글스 씨가 받았다. \"네가 물으니 하는 말이다만, 여기 모인 사람들 다 친구 사이니, 정 그러고 싶거든 말해도 괜찮겠지.\""]], [["‘She knew, when we were travelling, where you lived,’ said Tattycoram, ‘and she had seen me not quite--not quite--’", "\"그분은 우리가 여행할 때, 선생님 댁이 어딘지 아셨어요.\" 태티코럼이 말했다. \"그리고 제가 영 — 영 —\""]], [["‘Not quite in a good temper, Tattycoram?’ suggested Mr Meagles, shaking his head at the dark eyes with a quiet caution.", "\"영 기분이 좋지 않은 걸 보셨다는 거냐, 태티코럼?\" 미글스 씨가 그 검은 눈을 향해 고개를 저으며 조용히 조심스레 일러 주었다."], ["‘Take a little time--count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’", "\"잠깐 진정해라 — 스물다섯까지 세어 보렴, 태티코럼.\""]], [["She pressed her lips together again, and took a long deep breath.", "그녀는 다시 입술을 꾹 다물고는 길고 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["‘So she wrote to me to say that if I ever felt myself hurt,’ she looked down at her young mistress, ‘or found myself worried,’ she looked down at her again, ‘I might go to her, and be considerately treated. I was to think of it, and could speak to her by the church. So I went there to thank her.’", "\"그래서 제게 편지로 이러셨어요. 혹시라도 제가 마음을 다치거나,\" 그녀가 어린 주인 아가씨를 내려다보았다. \"괴로움을 겪는다 싶으면,\" 그녀가 다시 아가씨를 내려다보았다. \"그분께 가도 좋고, 사려 깊은 대접을 받을 수 있다고요. 잘 생각해 보고, 교회 옆에서 말을 나누면 된다고요. 그래서 거기 가서 고맙다고 했어요.\""]], [["‘Tatty,’ said her young mistress, putting her hand up over her shoulder that the other might take it, ‘Miss Wade almost frightened me when we parted, and I scarcely like to think of her just now as having been so near me without my knowing it. Tatty dear!’", "\"태티.\" 어린 주인 아가씨가 상대가 잡을 수 있도록 어깨 너머로 손을 올리며 말했다. \"헤어질 때 웨이드 양이 날 거의 무섭게 했었거든. 지금 그분이 내가 알지도 못한 새 그렇게 가까이 와 있었다고 생각하니 영 마음이 안 좋아. 태티, 얘!\""]], [["Tatty stood for a moment, immovable.", "태티는 잠시 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."]], [["‘Hey?’ cried Mr Meagles.", "\"이런?\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Count another five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’", "\"다시 스물다섯까지 세어 봐, 태티코럼.\""]], [["She might have counted a dozen, when she bent and put her lips to the caressing hand.", "그녀가 열둘쯤 세었을까 싶을 때, 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그 어루만지는 손에 입술을 갖다 댔다."], ["It patted her cheek, as it touched the owner’s beautiful curls, and Tattycoram went away.", "그 손이 그녀의 뺨을 토닥였고, 그러면서 손 주인의 어여쁜 곱슬머리를 매만졌다. 그러고는 태티코럼이 물러갔다."]], [["‘Now there,’ said Mr Meagles softly, as he gave a turn to the dumb-waiter on his right hand to twirl the sugar towards himself.", "\"자, 보세요.\" 미글스 씨가 오른편의 자동 회전대를 돌려 설탕을 제 쪽으로 빙그르르 가져오며 부드럽게 말했다."], ["‘There’s a girl who might be lost and ruined, if she wasn’t among practical people.", "\"저 아이는 실용적인 사람들 틈에 있지 않았다면 길을 잃고 신세를 망쳤을 수도 있는 애예요."], ["Mother and I know, solely from being practical, that there are times when that girl’s whole nature seems to roughen itself against seeing us so bound up in Pet.", "어멈과 나는, 오로지 실용적이기에 압니다만, 우리가 펫에게 그토록 푹 빠져 있는 걸 보면 저 아이의 본성 전체가 곤두서는 때가 있다는 걸 알지요."], ["No father and mother were bound up in her, poor soul.", "가엾게도, 저 아이한테는 푹 빠져 줄 아비 어미가 없었으니까요."], ["I don’t like to think of the way in which that unfortunate child, with all that passion and protest in her, feels when she hears the Fifth Commandment on a Sunday.", "저 불행한 아이가, 그 모든 격정과 반항을 품은 채로, 일요일에 십계명 중 제오계를 들을 때 어떤 심정일지 생각하면 영 안쓰럽습니다."], ["I am always inclined to call out, Church, Count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’", "그럴 때마다 나는 이렇게 외치고 싶어진다니까요. 교회야, 스물다섯까지 세어 보렴, 태티코럼.\""]], [["Besides his dumb-waiter, Mr Meagles had two other not dumb waiters in the persons of two parlour-maids with rosy faces and bright eyes, who were a highly ornamental part of the table decoration.", "미글스 씨에게는 자동 회전대 말고도, 말 못 하는 회전대와 달리 말을 하는 시중꾼이 둘 더 있었으니, 발그레한 얼굴에 초롱초롱한 눈을 한 두 하녀였다. 이들은 식탁 장식에서 매우 화려한 한 부분을 이루었다."], ["‘And why not, you see?’ said Mr Meagles on this head.", "\"왜 안 되겠습니까, 그렇잖아요?\" 이 점에 대해 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘As I always say to Mother, why not have something pretty to look at, if you have anything at all?’", "\"내가 늘 어멈한테 말하듯, 기왕 무엇이든 둘 거면, 보기에 예쁜 걸 두면 안 될 까닭이 무어란 말입니까?\""]], [["A certain Mrs Tickit, who was Cook and Housekeeper when the family were at home, and Housekeeper only when the family were away, completed the establishment.", "티킷 부인이라는 어떤 사람이 — 가족이 집에 있을 때는 요리사이자 가정부였고, 가족이 떠나 있을 때는 가정부 노릇만 했다 — 이 살림을 마저 채웠다."], ["Mr Meagles regretted that the nature of the duties in which she was engaged, rendered Mrs Tickit unpresentable at present, but hoped to introduce her to the new visitor to-morrow.", "미글스 씨는 티킷 부인이 맡은 일의 성격상 지금은 손님 앞에 내보일 수 없는 처지라 유감이지만, 내일은 새 손님에게 소개할 수 있기를 바란다고 했다."], ["She was an important part of the Cottage, he said, and all his friends knew her.", "그녀는 이 별장의 중요한 일부라고, 자기 친구들은 다 그녀를 안다고 했다."], ["That was her picture up in the corner.", "저기 구석에 걸린 게 그녀의 초상이라고 했다."], ["When they went away, she always put on the silk-gown and the jet-black row of curls represented in that portrait (her hair was reddish-grey in the kitchen), established herself in the breakfast-room, put her spectacles between two particular leaves of Doctor Buchan’s Domestic Medicine, and sat looking over the blind all day until they came back again.", "가족이 떠나면 그녀는 늘 그 초상에 그려진 대로 비단 가운을 차려입고 새카만 곱슬머리 가발을 쓰고는(부엌에 있을 땐 머리가 불그스름한 잿빛이었다), 아침 식사실에 자리를 잡고, 안경을 부캔 박사의 〈가정 의학〉의 특정한 두 쪽 사이에 끼워 둔 채, 그들이 돌아올 때까지 온종일 블라인드 너머를 내다보며 앉아 있었다."], ["It was supposed that no persuasion could be invented which would induce Mrs Tickit to abandon her post at the blind, however long their absence, or to dispense with the attendance of Dr Buchan; the lucubrations of which learned practitioner, Mr Meagles implicitly believed she had never yet consulted to the extent of one word in her life.", "그들이 아무리 오래 떠나 있어도 티킷 부인을 그 블라인드 앞 자리에서 떠나게 하거나 부캔 박사의 시중을 마다하게 할 만한 어떤 설득도 고안해 낼 수 없으리라고들 했다. 그러나 미글스 씨는, 그 박식한 의사의 노작을 그녀가 평생토록 단 한 마디라도 들춰 본 적이 없으리라고 굳게 믿었다."]], [["In the evening they played an old-fashioned rubber; and Pet sat looking over her father’s hand, or singing to herself by fits and starts at the piano.", "저녁에는 옛날식 러버 카드놀이를 했다. 펫은 아버지의 패를 들여다보며 앉아 있거나, 피아노 앞에서 이따금 혼자 흥얼거리며 노래했다."], ["She was a spoilt child; but how could she be otherwise?", "그녀는 응석받이로 자란 아이였다. 그러나 어찌 안 그럴 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Who could be much with so pliable and beautiful a creature, and not yield to her endearing influence?", "이토록 순순하고 아름다운 사람과 오래 함께하면서, 누가 그 사랑스러운 영향에 굴복하지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Who could pass an evening in the house, and not love her for the grace and charm of her very presence in the room?", "누가 이 집에서 저녁 한때를 보내며, 그 방에 그녀가 있다는 것만으로도 풍기는 그 우아함과 매력에 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["This was Clennam’s reflection, notwithstanding the final conclusion at which he had arrived up-stairs.", "위층에서 그가 이미 내린 최종 결론에도 불구하고, 이것이 클레넘의 생각이었다."]], [["In making it, he revoked.", "그러다 보니 그는 카드 규칙을 어기고 말았다."], ["‘Why, what are you thinking of, my good sir?’ asked the astonished Mr Meagles, who was his partner.", "\"아니, 무슨 생각을 하고 계신 거요, 선생?\" 그의 짝패였던 미글스 씨가 놀라 물었다."], ["‘I beg your pardon.", "\"죄송합니다."], ["Nothing,’ returned Clennam.", "아무것도 아닙니다.\" 클레넘이 받았다."], ["‘Think of something, next time; that’s a dear fellow,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"다음번엔 뭐든 생각을 좀 하세요. 그래야 좋은 양반이지.\""], ["Pet laughingly believed he had been thinking of Miss Wade.", "미글스 씨가 말했다. 펫은 웃으며 그가 웨이드 양 생각을 하고 있었던 거라고 했다."], ["‘Why of Miss Wade, Pet?’ asked her father.", "\"왜 하필 웨이드 양이냐, 펫?\" 아버지가 물었다."], ["‘Why, indeed!’ said Arthur Clennam.", "\"글쎄 말이에요, 왜일까요!\""], ["Pet coloured a little, and went to the piano again.", "아서 클레넘이 말했다. 펫은 살짝 얼굴을 붉히고는 다시 피아노로 갔다."]], [["As they broke up for the night, Arthur overheard Doyce ask his host if he could give him half an hour’s conversation before breakfast in the morning?", "밤이 깊어 자리를 파할 무렵, 아서는 도이스가 주인에게 내일 아침 식사 전에 반 시간쯤 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 묻는 것을 우연히 들었다."], ["The host replying willingly, Arthur lingered behind a moment, having his own word to add to that topic.", "주인이 흔쾌히 응하자, 아서는 그 화제에 자기도 한마디 보탤 것이 있어 잠시 뒤에 남았다."]], [["‘Mr Meagles,’ he said, on their being left alone, ‘do you remember when you advised me to go straight to London?’", "\"미글스 씨,\" 둘만 남게 되자 그가 말했다. \"제게 곧장 런던으로 가라고 권하셨던 때를 기억하십니까?\""]], [["‘Perfectly well.’", "\"아주 잘 기억하지요.\""]], [["‘And when you gave me some other good advice which I needed at that time?’", "\"그리고 그때 제게 필요했던 또 다른 좋은 조언을 해 주셨던 것도요?\""]], [["‘I won’t say what it was worth,’ answered Mr Meagles: ‘but of course I remember our being very pleasant and confidential together.’", "\"그게 얼마나 값진 거였는지야 내가 말하지 않겠습니다만,\" 미글스 씨가 답했다. \"물론 우리가 함께 무척 즐겁고 흉허물 없이 지냈던 건 기억하지요.\""]], [["‘I have acted on your advice; and having disembarrassed myself of an occupation that was painful to me for many reasons, wish to devote myself and what means I have, to another pursuit.’", "\"저는 당신 조언대로 행했습니다. 그리고 여러 까닭으로 제게 괴로웠던 직무를 떨쳐 버린 터라, 이제 저 자신과 제가 가진 얼마간의 재력을 다른 일에 바치고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘Right!", "\"옳은 말씀이오!"], ["You can’t do it too soon,’ said Mr Meagles.", "아무리 일러도 지나치지 않지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Now, as I came down to-day, I found that your friend, Mr Doyce, is looking for a partner in his business--not a partner in his mechanical knowledge, but in the ways and means of turning the business arising from it to the best account.’", "\"한데 오늘 내려오면서 알게 되었는데, 당신 친구분인 도이스 씨가 사업에 동업자를 찾고 계시더군요 — 그분의 기계 지식의 동업자가 아니라, 거기서 생기는 사업을 가장 이롭게 굴려 가는 방도와 수단에서의 동업자 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said Mr Meagles, with his hands in his pockets, and with the old business expression of face that had belonged to the scales and scoop.", "\"바로 그렇소.\" 미글스 씨가 주머니에 손을 찌른 채, 저울과 삽에 어울리던 그 옛 사업가다운 표정을 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Doyce mentioned incidentally, in the course of our conversation, that he was going to take your valuable advice on the subject of finding such a partner.", "\"도이스 씨가 대화 중에 지나가는 말로, 그런 동업자를 찾는 문제로 당신의 귀한 조언을 받으려 한다고 하시더군요."], ["If you should think our views and opportunities at all likely to coincide, perhaps you will let him know my available position.", "만일 우리의 생각과 처지가 조금이라도 맞아떨어질 성싶다 보시거든, 제가 어떤 형편에 있는지 그분께 알려 주시면 좋겠습니다."], ["I speak, of course, in ignorance of the details, and they may be unsuitable on both sides.’", "물론 저는 세부 사정을 모르고 드리는 말씀이라, 양쪽 모두에게 맞지 않을 수도 있습니다.\""]], [["‘No doubt, no doubt,’ said Mr Meagles, with the caution belonging to the scales and scoop.", "\"그렇지요, 그렇고말고요.\" 미글스 씨가 저울과 삽에 어울리는 신중함으로 말했다."]], [["‘But they will be a question of figures and accounts--’", "\"하지만 그건 숫자와 셈의 문제일 테니 —\""]], [["‘Just so, just so,’ said Mr Meagles, with arithmetical solidity belonging to the scales and scoop.", "\"바로 그렇소, 그렇고말고요.\" 미글스 씨가 저울과 삽에 어울리는 산술적 견실함으로 말했다."]], [["‘--And I shall be glad to enter into the subject, provided Mr Doyce responds, and you think well of it.", "\"— 그리고 도이스 씨가 응하시고 당신이 좋게 보신다면, 저는 기꺼이 이 일에 뛰어들겠습니다."], ["If you will at present, therefore, allow me to place it in your hands, you will much oblige me.’", "그러니 지금으로서는 이 일을 당신 손에 맡기도록 허락해 주신다면, 큰 신세를 지는 셈이 되겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Clennam, I accept the trust with readiness,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"클레넘, 그 소임을 기꺼이 맡지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘And without anticipating any of the points which you, as a man of business, have of course reserved, I am free to say to you that I think something may come of this.", "\"그리고 당신이 사업가로서 당연히 유보해 두신 어느 대목도 미리 넘겨짚지 않고, 이 일에서 뭔가가 나올 법하다고 솔직히 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Of one thing you may be perfectly certain.", "한 가지만은 완벽히 확신하셔도 좋습니다."], ["Daniel is an honest man.’", "대니얼은 정직한 사람입니다.\""]], [["‘I am so sure of it that I have promptly made up my mind to speak to you.’", "\"저도 그렇게 확신하기에, 곧장 마음을 정하고 당신께 말씀드린 겁니다.\""]], [["‘You must guide him, you know; you must steer him; you must direct him; he is one of a crotchety sort,’ said Mr Meagles, evidently meaning nothing more than that he did new things and went new ways; ‘but he is as honest as the sun, and so good night!’", "\"당신이 그를 이끌어야 합니다, 아시겠어요. 키를 잡아 주고, 방향을 잡아 줘야 해요. 그는 좀 별난 부류라서요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했는데, 그가 새로운 일을 하고 새로운 길을 간다는 것 이상의 뜻은 분명 아니었다. \"하지만 햇빛만큼이나 정직한 사람이니, 그럼 안녕히 주무세요!\""]], [["Clennam went back to his room, sat down again before his fire, and made up his mind that he was glad he had resolved not to fall in love with Pet.", "클레넘은 제 방으로 돌아가 다시 벽난로 앞에 앉아, 펫과 사랑에 빠지지 않기로 결심한 것이 다행이라고 마음을 굳혔다."], ["She was so beautiful, so amiable, so apt to receive any true impression given to her gentle nature and her innocent heart, and make the man who should be so happy as to communicate it, the most fortunate and enviable of all men, that he was very glad indeed he had come to that conclusion.", "그녀는 너무도 아름답고, 너무도 사랑스러우며, 그 부드러운 천성과 순결한 마음에 주어지는 어떤 참된 인상이든 받아들여, 그것을 전하는 행운을 누릴 남자를 세상 모든 남자 가운데 가장 운 좋고 부러운 사람으로 만들어 줄 만한 사람이었으므로, 그는 그런 결론에 이른 것이 정말이지 다행이라 여겼다."]], [["But, as this might have been a reason for coming to the opposite conclusion, he followed out the theme again a little way in his mind; to justify himself, perhaps.", "그러나 이것은 도리어 정반대의 결론에 이를 만한 이유가 될 수도 있었으므로, 그는 그 주제를 마음속으로 조금 더 따라가 보았다. 어쩌면 자신을 정당화하기 위해서였으리라."]], [["‘Suppose that a man,’ so his thoughts ran, ‘who had been of age some twenty years or so; who was a diffident man, from the circumstances of his youth; who was rather a grave man, from the tenor of his life; who knew himself to be deficient in many little engaging qualities which he admired in others, from having been long in a distant region, with nothing softening near him; who had no kind sisters to present to her; who had no congenial home to make her known in; who was a stranger in the land; who had not a fortune to compensate, in any measure, for these defects; who had nothing in his favour but his honest love and his general wish to do right--suppose such a man were to come to this house, and were to yield to the captivation of this charming girl, and were to persuade himself that he could hope to win her; what a weakness it would be!’", "'어떤 남자가 있다고 치자.' 그의 생각은 이렇게 흘러갔다. '스무 해쯤 전부터 성년이 된 남자, 어린 시절의 사정 탓에 자신감이 부족한 남자, 살아온 행로로 보아 다소 무거운 남자, 자기 곁에 마음을 누그러뜨려 줄 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 먼 지방에 오래 있던 탓에 남들에게서는 감탄하던 그 자잘한 사람을 끄는 자질들이 자기에게는 모자란다는 걸 아는 남자, 그녀에게 소개해 줄 다정한 누이도 없는 남자, 그녀를 알릴 만한 마음 맞는 보금자리도 없는 남자, 이 땅에서는 이방인인 남자, 이런 결함들을 조금이라도 보상할 재산도 없는 남자, 자기에게 유리한 거라곤 정직한 사랑과 옳게 행하려는 일반적인 바람밖에 없는 남자 — 그런 남자가 이 집에 와서, 이 매혹적인 아가씨에게 사로잡히고 말아, 그녀를 차지하기를 바랄 수 있다고 스스로를 설득한다면, 그 얼마나 어리석은 약점이겠는가!'"]], [["He softly opened his window, and looked out upon the serene river.", "그는 가만히 창문을 열고 고요한 강을 내다보았다."], ["Year after year so much allowance for the drifting of the ferry-boat, so many miles an hour the flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet.", "해마다, 나룻배가 떠밀려 가는 정도는 이만큼, 시냇물 흐르는 속도는 한 시간에 몇 마일, 여기엔 골풀이, 저기엔 백합이, 무엇 하나 불확실하거나 불안한 것 없었다."]], [["Why should he be vexed or sore at heart?", "어찌하여 그가 속을 끓이거나 마음 아파해야 한단 말인가?"], ["It was not his weakness that he had imagined.", "그가 그려 본 것은 그 자신의 약점이 아니었다."], ["It was nobody’s, nobody’s within his knowledge; why should it trouble him?", "그것은 누구의 약점도 아니었고, 그가 아는 한 어느 누구의 것도 아니었으니, 어찌하여 그것이 그를 괴롭혀야 한단 말인가?"], ["And yet it did trouble him.", "그런데도 그것은 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["And he thought--who has not thought for a moment, sometimes?--that it might be better to flow away monotonously, like the river, and to compound for its insensibility to happiness with its insensibility to pain.", "그리고 그는 생각했다 — 때로 한순간이나마 그런 생각을 안 해 본 사람이 누가 있으랴? — 차라리 저 강물처럼 단조롭게 흘러가, 행복을 느끼지 못하는 대신 고통도 느끼지 못하는 것으로 셈을 맞추는 편이 나을지도 모른다고."]], [["# CHAPTER 17. Nobody’s Rival", "# CHAPTER 17. Nobody’s Rival"]], [["Before breakfast in the morning, Arthur walked out to look about him.", "아침 식사 전, 아서는 주위를 둘러보려고 밖으로 나가 걸었다."], ["As the morning was fine and he had an hour on his hands, he crossed the river by the ferry, and strolled along a footpath through some meadows.", "아침이 화창하고 한 시간 정도 여유가 있었으므로, 그는 나룻배로 강을 건너 목초지를 가로지르는 오솔길을 따라 거닐었다."], ["When he came back to the towing-path, he found the ferry-boat on the opposite side, and a gentleman hailing it and waiting to be taken over.", "다시 견인로로 돌아왔을 때, 나룻배가 건너편에 있는 것과, 어떤 신사가 그것을 부르며 건너편으로 실려 가기를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["This gentleman looked barely thirty.", "이 신사는 서른이 채 안 되어 보였다."], ["He was well dressed, of a sprightly and gay appearance, a well-knit figure, and a rich dark complexion.", "옷차림이 단정했고, 활기차고 쾌활한 인상에, 다부진 체격과 짙고 풍부한 혈색을 지녔다."], ["As Arthur came over the stile and down to the water’s edge, the lounger glanced at him for a moment, and then resumed his occupation of idly tossing stones into the water with his foot.", "아서가 울타리 디딤대를 넘어 물가로 내려오자, 빈둥거리던 그 사람은 잠시 그를 흘끗 보더니, 다시 발로 돌멩이를 무심히 물속으로 차 넣는 일에 몰두했다."], ["There was something in his way of spurning them out of their places with his heel, and getting them into the required position, that Clennam thought had an air of cruelty in it.", "발뒤꿈치로 돌들을 제자리에서 걷어차 내고, 그것들을 원하는 위치로 몰아넣는 그 방식에는 클레넘이 보기에 어딘가 잔인한 데가 있었다."], ["Most of us have more or less frequently derived a similar impression from a man’s manner of doing some very little thing: plucking a flower, clearing away an obstacle, or even destroying an insentient object.", "우리 대부분은 어떤 사람이 아주 사소한 일을 하는 태도에서 — 꽃을 꺾거나, 장애물을 치우거나, 심지어 감각도 없는 물체를 부수는 것에서 — 다소간 자주 그와 비슷한 인상을 받아 본 적이 있다."]], [["The gentleman’s thoughts were preoccupied, as his face showed, and he took no notice of a fine Newfoundland dog, who watched him attentively, and watched every stone too, in its turn, eager to spring into the river on receiving his master’s sign.", "그 신사의 생각은 그의 얼굴이 보여주듯 다른 데 쏠려 있었고, 그래서 그는 자기를 주의 깊게 지켜보는 멋진 뉴펀들랜드 개도, 또 차례차례 던져지는 돌멩이마다 주인의 신호를 받으면 강물로 뛰어들려고 안달하며 지켜보는 그 개도 알아채지 못했다."], ["The ferry-boat came over, however, without his receiving any sign, and when it grounded his master took him by the collar and walked him into it.", "그러나 나룻배는 그가 아무 신호도 내리지 않은 채로 건너왔고, 배가 모래톱에 닿자 주인은 개의 목덜미를 잡아 배 안으로 데리고 들어갔다."]], [["‘Not this morning,’ he said to the dog.", "\"오늘 아침은 아니야.\" 그가 개에게 말했다."], ["‘You won’t do for ladies’ company, dripping wet.", "\"물에 흠뻑 젖은 채로는 숙녀들 상대로 어울리지 않거든."], ["Lie down.’", "엎드려.\""]], [["Clennam followed the man and the dog into the boat, and took his seat.", "클레넘은 그 사람과 개를 따라 배에 올라 자리에 앉았다."], ["The dog did as he was ordered.", "개는 명령받은 대로 했다."], ["The man remained standing, with his hands in his pockets, and towered between Clennam and the prospect.", "사람은 양손을 주머니에 넣은 채 그대로 서서, 클레넘과 풍경 사이에 우뚝 솟아 있었다."], ["Man and dog both jumped lightly out as soon as they touched the other side, and went away.", "사람도 개도 건너편에 닿자마자 가볍게 뛰어내려 가 버렸다."], ["Clennam was glad to be rid of them.", "클레넘은 그들에게서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤다."]], [["The church clock struck the breakfast hour as he walked up the little lane by which the garden-gate was approached.", "정원 문으로 통하는 작은 골목길을 따라 걸어 올라갈 때, 교회 시계가 아침 식사 시간을 알렸다."], ["The moment he pulled the bell a deep loud barking assailed him from within the wall.", "그가 종을 당기자마자, 담장 안에서 깊고 우렁찬 짖는 소리가 그를 덮쳐 왔다."]], [["‘I heard no dog last night,’ thought Clennam.", "'어젯밤엔 개가 없었는데.' 클레넘은 생각했다."], ["The gate was opened by one of the rosy maids, and on the lawn were the Newfoundland dog and the man.", "혈색 좋은 하녀들 중 하나가 문을 열어 주었고, 잔디밭에는 그 뉴펀들랜드 개와 그 사람이 있었다."]], [["‘Miss Minnie is not down yet, gentlemen,’ said the blushing portress, as they all came together in the garden.", "\"미니 아가씨는 아직 안 내려오셨어요, 신사분들.\" 얼굴을 붉히던 문지기 하녀가 그들이 모두 정원에 함께 모였을 때 말했다."], ["Then she said to the master of the dog, ‘Mr Clennam, sir,’ and tripped away.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 개 주인에게 \"클레넘 씨이십니다, 손님\" 하고는 종종걸음으로 가 버렸다."]], [["‘Odd enough, Mr Clennam, that we should have met just now,’ said the man.", "\"방금 우리가 마주쳤다니 참 묘하군요, 클레넘 씨.\" 그 사람이 말했다."], ["Upon which the dog became mute.", "그러자 개가 잠잠해졌다."], ["‘Allow me to introduce myself--Henry Gowan.", "\"제 소개를 하겠습니다 — 헨리 가우언입니다."], ["A pretty place this, and looks wonderfully well this morning!’", "멋진 곳이지요. 오늘 아침엔 놀랄 만큼 좋아 보입니다!\""]], [["The manner was easy, and the voice agreeable; but still Clennam thought, that if he had not made that decided resolution to avoid falling in love with Pet, he would have taken a dislike to this Henry Gowan.", "태도는 거리낌이 없었고, 목소리는 듣기 좋았다. 그러나 그래도 클레넘은, 자기가 펫에게 사랑에 빠지지 않겠다고 그토록 단호히 결심하지 않았더라면, 이 헨리 가우언에게 반감을 품었으리라고 생각했다."]], [["‘It’s new to you, I believe?’ said this Gowan, when Arthur had extolled the place.", "\"여기는 처음이시겠지요?\" 아서가 그곳을 칭찬하고 나자 이 가우언이 말했다."]], [["‘Quite new.", "\"아주 처음입니다."], ["I made acquaintance with it only yesterday afternoon.’", "어제 오후에야 비로소 인연을 맺었지요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Of course this is not its best aspect.", "물론 이게 가장 좋은 모습은 아닙니다."], ["It used to look charming in the spring, before they went away last time.", "지난번 그분들이 떠나기 전, 봄에는 매혹적이었답니다."], ["I should like you to have seen it then.’", "그때 보셨더라면 좋았을 텐데요.\""]], [["But for that resolution so often recalled, Clennam might have wished him in the crater of Mount Etna, in return for this civility.", "그토록 자주 떠올린 그 결심만 아니었다면, 클레넘은 이 친절에 대한 답례로 그를 에트나 화산 분화구 속에 처넣고 싶었을지도 모른다."]], [["‘I have had the pleasure of seeing it under many circumstances during the last three years, and it’s--a Paradise.’", "\"저는 지난 삼 년 동안 여러 상황에서 이곳을 보는 기쁨을 누렸는데, 이곳은 — 낙원입니다.\""]], [["It was (at least it might have been, always excepting for that wise resolution) like his dexterous impudence to call it a Paradise.", "(적어도, 그 현명한 결심을 늘 예외로 친다면) 이곳을 낙원이라 부르는 것은 그의 능란한 뻔뻔함다웠다."], ["He only called it a Paradise because he first saw her coming, and so made her out within her hearing to be an angel, Confusion to him!", "그가 이곳을 낙원이라 부른 것은 다만 그녀가 다가오는 것을 먼저 보았기 때문이고, 그래서 그녀가 들을 수 있는 거리에서 그녀를 천사로 만들어 버린 것이었다. 그에게 저주를!"]], [["And ah! how beaming she looked, and how glad!", "아, 그녀는 얼마나 환하게 빛나 보였고, 또 얼마나 기뻐 보였던가!"], ["How she caressed the dog, and how the dog knew her!", "그녀가 개를 어떻게 어루만지고, 또 개가 그녀를 어떻게 알아보는지!"], ["How expressive that heightened colour in her face, that fluttered manner, her downcast eyes, her irresolute happiness!", "그녀의 얼굴에 짙어진 그 홍조며, 들떠 떨리는 그 태도며, 내리깐 눈이며, 망설이는 행복이 얼마나 많은 것을 말해 주던가!"], ["When had Clennam seen her look like this?", "클레넘이 그녀가 이런 모습을 보이는 것을 언제 본 적이 있었던가?"], ["Not that there was any reason why he might, could, would, or should have ever seen her look like this, or that he had ever hoped for himself to see her look like this; but still--when had he ever known her do it!", "그가 그녀의 이런 모습을 일찍이 보았거나, 볼 수 있었거나, 보았으리라거나, 보았어야 했을 어떤 이유가 있는 것도 아니고, 그가 일찍이 자기 자신이 그녀의 이런 모습을 보기를 바란 적이 있는 것도 아니었지만 — 그래도 — 그가 언제 그녀가 이러는 것을 알았던 적이 있던가!"]], [["He stood at a little distance from them.", "그는 그들에게서 조금 떨어져 서 있었다."], ["This Gowan when he had talked about a Paradise, had gone up to her and taken her hand.", "이 가우언은 낙원에 관해 이야기하고 나서 그녀에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["The dog had put his great paws on her arm and laid his head against her dear bosom.", "개는 커다란 앞발을 그녀의 팔에 올리고 머리를 그녀의 사랑스러운 가슴에 기댔다."], ["She had laughed and welcomed them, and made far too much of the dog, far, far, too much--that is to say, supposing there had been any third person looking on who loved her.", "그녀는 웃으며 그들을 반겼고, 개에게 지나치게 살갑게 굴었다, 너무, 너무 지나치게 — 다시 말해, 만약 그녀를 사랑하는 어떤 제삼자가 지켜보고 있었더라면 그렇게 보였을 만큼."]], [["She disengaged herself now, and came to Clennam, and put her hand in his and wished him good morning, and gracefully made as if she would take his arm and be escorted into the house.", "이제 그녀는 몸을 빼내어 클레넘에게로 와서, 자기 손을 그의 손에 얹고 그에게 안녕히 주무셨느냐 인사한 뒤, 우아하게 그의 팔을 잡고 집 안으로 안내받으려는 시늉을 했다. 이에 대해 가우언은 아무런 반대도 하지 않았다."], ["To this Gowan had no objection. No, he knew he was too safe.", "그렇다, 그는 자기가 너무도 안전하다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["There was a passing cloud on Mr Meagles’s good-humoured face when they all three (four, counting the dog, and he was the most objectionable but one of the party) came in to breakfast.", "그들 셋이(개까지 세면 넷인데, 그 개는 일행 중에서 가장 거슬리는 자 다음으로 거슬렸다) 아침 식사를 하러 들어왔을 때, 미글스 씨의 쾌활한 얼굴 위에 스쳐 지나가는 구름이 한 자락 끼었다."], ["Neither it, nor the touch of uneasiness on Mrs Meagles as she directed her eyes towards it, was unobserved by Clennam.", "그 구름도, 그리로 눈길을 돌리던 미글스 부인의 얼굴에 어린 한 가닥 불안의 기색도, 클레넘의 눈을 피해 가지 못했다."]], [["‘Well, Gowan,’ said Mr Meagles, even suppressing a sigh; ‘how goes the world with you this morning?’", "\"자, 가우언.\" 미글스 씨가 한숨까지 억누르며 말했다. \"오늘 아침 자네에게 세상은 어떻게 돌아가고 있나?\""]], [["‘Much as usual, sir.", "\"여느 때와 다름없습니다, 어르신. 라이언하고 저는 주간 방문을 조금도 허비하지 않기로 단단히 마음먹고 일찍 나서서, 제가 지금 본거지로 삼은 킹스턴에서 건너왔습니다."], ["Lion and I being determined not to waste anything of our weekly visit, turned out early, and came over from Kingston, my present headquarters, where I am making a sketch or two.’ Then he told how he had met Mr Clennam at the ferry, and they had come over together.", "거기서 스케치를 한두 점 그리고 있거든요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 나루터에서 클레넘 씨를 만나 함께 건너왔다고 이야기했다."]], [["‘Mrs Gowan is well, Henry?’ said Mrs Meagles.", "\"가우언 부인은 안녕하시지요, 헨리?\" 미글스 부인이 말했다."], ["(Clennam became attentive.)", "(클레넘은 귀를 기울이게 되었다.)"]], [["‘My mother is quite well, thank you.’ (Clennam became inattentive.) ‘I have taken the liberty of making an addition to your family dinner-party to-day, which I hope will not be inconvenient to you or to Mr Meagles.", "\"어머니는 아주 건강하십니다, 감사합니다.\" (클레넘은 다시 흥미를 잃었다.) \"오늘 댁의 가족 만찬에 한 사람을 더하는 무례를 범했는데, 어르신이나 미글스 씨께 불편이 되지 않기를 바랍니다."], ["I couldn’t very well get out of it,’ he explained, turning to the latter.", "도무지 거절할 수가 없었거든요.\" 그가 미글스 씨를 향하며 설명했다."], ["‘The young fellow wrote to propose himself to me; and as he is well connected, I thought you would not object to my transferring him here.’", "\"그 젊은 친구가 제게 편지를 보내 자기를 초대해 달라고 청했는데, 연줄이 좋은 집안이라 제가 여기로 데려와도 반대하지 않으시리라 생각했습니다.\""]], [["‘Who _is_ the young fellow?’ asked Mr Meagles with peculiar complacency.", "\"그 젊은 친구가 누구인가?\" 미글스 씨가 유난히 흡족한 기색으로 물었다."]], [["‘He is one of the Barnacles.", "\"배너클 집안 사람 중 하나입니다."], ["Tite Barnacle’s son, Clarence Barnacle, who is in his father’s Department.", "타이트 배너클의 아들, 클래런스 배너클인데, 자기 아버지의 국에 근무하고 있지요."], ["I can at least guarantee that the river shall not suffer from his visit.", "적어도 그가 방문한다고 해서 강물이 화를 입지는 않으리란 건 보장합니다."], ["He won’t set it on fire.’", "강에 불을 지를 위인은 못 되거든요.\""]], [["‘Aye, aye?’ said Meagles.", "\"이런, 이런?\" 미글스가 말했다."], ["‘A Barnacle is he?", "\"배너클이라고?"], ["_We_ know something of that family, eh, Dan?", "우리도 그 집안에 관해 좀 알지, 안 그런가, 댄?"], ["By George, they are at the top of the tree, though!", "아무렴, 그 사람들은 그래도 나무 꼭대기에 앉아 있으니 말일세!"], ["Let me see.", "어디 보자."], ["What relation will this young fellow be to Lord Decimus now?", "이 젊은 친구가 데시머스 경에게는 어떤 관계가 되는 건가?"], ["His Lordship married, in seventeen ninety-seven, Lady Jemima Bilberry, who was the second daughter by the third marriage--no!", "그분께서는 천칠백구십칠 년에 제미마 빌베리 영애와 혼인하셨는데, 그분은 세 번째 결혼에서 난 둘째 따님이고 — 아니야!"], ["There I am wrong!", "거기서 틀렸군!"], ["That was Lady Seraphina--Lady Jemima was the first daughter by the second marriage of the fifteenth Earl of Stiltstalking with the Honourable Clementina Toozellem.", "그분은 세라피나 영애였지 — 제미마 영애는 열다섯 번째 스틸트스토킹 백작이 클레멘티나 투젤럼 영애와 한 두 번째 결혼에서 난 첫째 따님이었어."], ["Very well.", "좋아."], ["Now this young fellow’s father married a Stiltstalking and _his_ father married his cousin who was a Barnacle.", "자, 이 젊은 친구의 아버지는 스틸트스토킹 집안 여자와 혼인했고, 그 아버지는 배너클 집안 여자인 사촌과 혼인했지. 배너클 집안 여자와 혼인한 그 아버지의 아버지는 조들비 집안 여자와 혼인했고."], ["The father of that father who married a Barnacle, married a Joddleby.--I am getting a little too far back, Gowan; I want to make out what relation this young fellow is to Lord Decimus.’", "— 가우언, 내가 좀 너무 거슬러 올라가는군. 나는 이 젊은 친구가 데시머스 경에게 어떤 관계가 되는지 알아내려는 거였는데.\""]], [["‘That’s easily stated.", "\"그건 쉽게 말씀드릴 수 있습니다."], ["His father is nephew to Lord Decimus.’", "그의 아버지가 데시머스 경의 조카입니다.\""]], [["‘Nephew--to--Lord--Decimus,’ Mr Meagles luxuriously repeated with his eyes shut, that he might have nothing to distract him from the full flavour of the genealogical tree.", "\"데시머스 — 경의 — 조카.\" 미글스 씨는 가계도의 그 충만한 향취에서 자기를 흩뜨릴 것이 하나도 없도록 눈을 감은 채 흐뭇하게 되풀이했다."], ["‘By George, you are right, Gowan.", "\"아무렴, 자네 말이 맞아, 가우언."], ["So he is.’", "그렇구먼.\""]], [["‘Consequently, Lord Decimus is his great uncle.’", "\"따라서 데시머스 경은 그의 종조부가 되지요.\""]], [["‘But stop a bit!’ said Mr Meagles, opening his eyes with a fresh discovery.", "\"하지만 잠깐만!\" 미글스 씨가 새로운 발견에 눈을 뜨며 말했다."], ["‘Then on the mother’s side, Lady Stiltstalking is his great aunt.’", "\"그렇다면 어머니 쪽으로는 스틸트스토킹 부인이 그의 종조모가 되는군.\""]], [["‘Of course she is.’", "\"물론 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Aye, aye, aye?’ said Mr Meagles with much interest.", "\"이런, 이런, 이런?\" 미글스 씨가 큰 흥미를 보이며 말했다."], ["‘Indeed, indeed?", "\"정말로, 정말로?"], ["We shall be glad to see him.", "우린 기꺼이 그를 맞이하겠네."], ["We’ll entertain him as well as we can, in our humble way; and we shall not starve him, I hope, at all events.’", "우리 변변찮은 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 잘 대접하지. 그래도 적어도 굶기지는 않을 걸세, 그건 분명해.\""]], [["In the beginning of this dialogue, Clennam had expected some great harmless outburst from Mr Meagles, like that which had made him burst out of the Circumlocution Office, holding Doyce by the collar.", "이 대화가 시작될 무렵, 클레넘은 미글스 씨에게서, 도이스의 목덜미를 붙잡은 채 그가 에두름국에서 뛰쳐나오게 만들었던 것과 같은 어떤 대단하지만 해롭지는 않은 폭발이 터지기를 기대했었다."], ["But his good friend had a weakness which none of us need go into the next street to find, and which no amount of Circumlocution experience could long subdue in him.", "그러나 그의 선량한 친구에게는, 우리 중 누구라도 옆 거리까지 나가지 않아도 찾을 수 있는 약점이, 아무리 많은 에두름국 경험으로도 그 안에서 오래 억누를 수 없는 약점이 하나 있었다."], ["Clennam looked at Doyce; but Doyce knew all about it beforehand, and looked at his plate, and made no sign, and said no word.", "클레넘은 도이스를 바라보았다. 그러나 도이스는 그 모든 것을 미리 다 알고 있었기에, 자기 접시만 바라보며 아무런 내색도 하지 않고 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘I am much obliged to you,’ said Gowan, to conclude the subject.", "\"정말 고맙습니다.\" 가우언이 화제를 마무리 짓느라 말했다."], ["‘Clarence is a great ass, but he is one of the dearest and best fellows that ever lived!’", "\"클래런스는 굉장한 멍청이입니다만, 세상에 둘도 없이 정답고 더없이 좋은 친구지요!\""]], [["It appeared, before the breakfast was over, that everybody whom this Gowan knew was either more or less of an ass, or more or less of a knave; but was, notwithstanding, the most lovable, the most engaging, the simplest, truest, kindest, dearest, best fellow that ever lived. The process by which this unvarying result was attained, whatever the premises, might have been stated by Mr Henry Gowan thus: ‘I claim to be always book-keeping, with a peculiar nicety, in every man’s case, and posting up a careful little account of Good and Evil with him. I do this so conscientiously, that I am happy to tell you I find the most worthless of men to be the dearest old fellow too: and am in a condition to make the gratifying report, that there is much less difference than you are inclined to suppose between an honest man and a scoundrel.’ The effect of this cheering discovery happened to be, that while he seemed to be scrupulously finding good in most men, he did in reality lower it where it was, and set it up where it was not; but that was its only disagreeable or dangerous feature.", "아침 식사가 끝나기 전에, 이 가우언이 아는 사람은 누구나 다소간 멍청이거나 다소간 악당이지만, 그런데도 세상에 둘도 없이 사랑스럽고, 더없이 매력적이며, 가장 순박하고, 가장 진실하고, 가장 친절하고, 가장 정답고, 가장 좋은 사람이라는 것이 드러났다. 전제가 무엇이든 이 한결같은 결론에 이르는 과정을, 헨리 가우언 씨는 아마 이렇게 진술했으리라. \"저는 모든 사람의 경우를 두고 늘, 유난히 꼼꼼하게 회계 장부를 매기고, 그와 더불어 선과 악에 관한 세심한 작은 계산서를 결산해 두는 것을 제 신조로 삼습니다. 어찌나 양심적으로 이 일을 하는지, 저는 인간들 가운데 가장 쓸모없는 자도 알고 보면 더없이 정다운 친구라는 사실을 발견하게 되어 기쁩니다. 그리고 정직한 사람과 악당 사이에는 여러분이 짐작하시는 것보다 훨씬 차이가 적다는 흐뭇한 보고를 드릴 처지에 있습니다.\" 이 유쾌한 발견이 빚어낸 결과는, 그가 대부분의 사람에게서 양심적으로 선을 찾아내는 것처럼 보이는 동안 실은 선이 있는 곳에서는 그것을 끌어내리고 선이 없는 곳에는 그것을 세워 올렸다는 것이다. 그러나 그것이 그 발견의 유일하게 불쾌하거나 위험한 특징이었다."]], [["It scarcely seemed, however, to afford Mr Meagles as much satisfaction as the Barnacle genealogy had done.", "그러나 그것은 배너클 가계도가 안겨 준 만큼의 만족을 미글스 씨에게 주지는 못하는 듯했다."], ["The cloud that Clennam had never seen upon his face before that morning, frequently overcast it again; and there was the same shadow of uneasy observation of him on the comely face of his wife.", "그날 아침까지는 그의 얼굴에서 한 번도 본 적이 없던 그 구름이 여러 차례 다시 그 얼굴을 뒤덮었다. 그리고 그를 불안하게 살피는 그 똑같은 그림자가 그 아내의 고운 얼굴에도 어려 있었다."], ["More than once or twice when Pet caressed the dog, it appeared to Clennam that her father was unhappy in seeing her do it; and, in one particular instance when Gowan stood on the other side of the dog, and bent his head at the same time, Arthur fancied that he saw tears rise to Mr Meagles’s eyes as he hurried out of the room.", "펫이 개를 어루만질 때마다 한두 번이 아니라 여러 차례, 클레넘에게는 그녀의 아버지가 그것을 보며 마음 아파하는 것처럼 보였다. 특히 한번은 가우언이 개의 다른 쪽에 서서 동시에 머리를 숙였을 때, 아서는 미글스 씨가 방을 황급히 나가면서 그의 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 본 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["It was either the fact too, or he fancied further, that Pet herself was not insensible to these little incidents; that she tried, with a more delicate affection than usual, to express to her good father how much she loved him; that it was on this account that she fell behind the rest, both as they went to church and as they returned from it, and took his arm.", "또한 펫 자신도 이런 작은 일들에 무감하지 않다는 것도 사실이거나, 적어도 그가 더 그렇게 느낀 것이거나 했다. 그녀가 평소보다 더 섬세한 애정으로 자기 선량한 아버지에게 그를 얼마나 사랑하는지 표현하려 애쓰는 것, 바로 그 때문에 교회로 갈 때나 돌아올 때나 그녀가 나머지 사람들보다 뒤처져 그의 팔짱을 끼는 것이 그렇게 느껴졌다."], ["He could not have sworn but that as he walked alone in the garden afterwards, he had an instantaneous glimpse of her in her father’s room, clinging to both her parents with the greatest tenderness, and weeping on her father’s shoulder.", "그리고 나중에 그가 정원을 홀로 거닐 때, 그녀가 아버지의 방에서 더없는 다정함으로 부모 두 사람에게 매달려 아버지의 어깨에 기대어 우는 모습을 한순간 언뜻 보았노라고 그는 단언할 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["The latter part of the day turning out wet, they were fain to keep the house, look over Mr Meagles’s collection, and beguile the time with conversation.", "그날 하루의 후반부가 비로 접어들었으므로, 그들은 집 안에 머물며 미글스 씨의 수집품을 둘러보고 대화로 시간을 보낼 수밖에 없었다."], ["This Gowan had plenty to say for himself, and said it in an off-hand and amusing manner.", "이 가우언은 자기에 관해 할 말이 많았고, 그것을 거리낌 없고 재미있는 투로 늘어놓았다."], ["He appeared to be an artist by profession, and to have been at Rome some time; yet he had a slight, careless, amateur way with him--a perceptible limp, both in his devotion to art and his attainments--which Clennam could scarcely understand.", "그는 직업상 화가인 듯했고, 한동안 로마에 있었던 듯했다. 그런데도 그에게는 어딘가 가볍고 무심한 아마추어 같은 분위기가 — 예술에 대한 헌신에서도 그 성취에서도 눈에 띄는 절뚝거림이 — 있었는데, 클레넘으로서는 도무지 이해하기 어려웠다."]], [["He applied to Daniel Doyce for help, as they stood together, looking out of window.", "그는 창밖을 함께 내다보며 서 있던 대니얼 도이스에게 도움을 청했다."]], [["‘You know Mr Gowan?’ he said in a low voice.", "\"가우언 씨를 아십니까?\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I have seen him here.", "\"여기서 본 적이 있지요."], ["Comes here every Sunday when they are at home.’", "저 집안이 집에 있을 땐 일요일마다 옵니다.\""]], [["‘An artist, I infer from what he says?’", "\"하는 말로 미루어 화가인가 보군요?\""]], [["‘A sort of a one,’ said Daniel Doyce, in a surly tone.", "\"일종의 화가지요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 퉁명스러운 어조로 말했다."]], [["‘What sort of a one?’ asked Clennam, with a smile.", "\"어떤 종류의 화가입니까?\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘Why, he has sauntered into the Arts at a leisurely Pall-Mall pace,’ said Doyce, ‘and I doubt if they care to be taken quite so coolly.’", "\"글쎄요, 한가로운 펠멜 거리를 거니는 걸음걸이로 예술 속에 어슬렁어슬렁 들어온 사람이지요.\" 도이스가 말했다. \"그런데 예술이라는 게 그렇게 태평스럽게 다뤄지는 걸 좋아하기나 할지 의문입니다.\""]], [["Pursuing his inquiries, Clennam found that the Gowan family were a very distant ramification of the Barnacles; and that the paternal Gowan, originally attached to a legation abroad, had been pensioned off as a Commissioner of nothing particular somewhere or other, and had died at his post with his drawn salary in his hand, nobly defending it to the last extremity.", "* 펠멜: 런던의 사교 클럽들이 모여 있던 한가로운 거리. 질문을 이어 가던 끝에, 클레넘은 가우언 집안이 배너클 집안에서 아주 멀리 뻗어 나간 한 가지임을, 그리고 가우언의 아버지가 본디 해외 공사관에 소속되어 있다가 어디선가 별 대단치 않은 무엇의 위원으로 연금을 받고 물러났으며, 받아 챙긴 봉급을 손에 쥔 채 그것을 마지막 순간까지 고결하게 지키며 제 자리에서 죽었음을 알게 되었다."], ["In consideration of this eminent public service, the Barnacle then in power had recommended the Crown to bestow a pension of two or three hundred a-year on his widow; to which the next Barnacle in power had added certain shady and sedate apartments in the Palaces at Hampton Court, where the old lady still lived, deploring the degeneracy of the times in company with several other old ladies of both sexes.", "이 탁월한 공직 봉사를 참작하여, 당시 권력을 쥐고 있던 배너클이 왕실에 그의 미망인에게 연 이삼백 파운드의 연금을 내리도록 추천했다. 여기에 다음으로 권력을 쥔 배너클이 햄프턴 코트 궁전 안의 어둑하고 차분한 거처 몇 칸을 더해 주었으니, 그 노부인은 여전히 거기서 살며, 남녀 양쪽의 여러 다른 노인들과 어울려 세태의 타락을 한탄하고 있었다."], ["Her son, Mr Henry Gowan, inheriting from his father, the Commissioner, that very questionable help in life, a very small independence, had been difficult to settle; the rather, as public appointments chanced to be scarce, and his genius, during his earlier manhood, was of that exclusively agricultural character which applies itself to the cultivation of wild oats.", "그녀의 아들 헨리 가우언 씨는, 그 위원이던 아버지에게서 인생의 그 지극히 미심쩍은 밑천, 곧 아주 보잘것없는 독립 재산을 물려받았으니, 자리를 잡기가 어려웠다. 더욱이 공직 자리가 마침 드물었고, 그의 천재성은 젊은 시절 내내 야생 귀리를 가꾸는 데에만 전념하는 그런 오로지 농경적인 성격의 것이었기에 더 그러했다."], ["At last he had declared that he would become a Painter; partly because he had always had an idle knack that way, and partly to grieve the souls of the Barnacles-in-chief who had not provided for him.", "마침내 그는 화가가 되겠다고 선언했다. 한편으로는 그쪽으로 늘 한가한 재주가 있었기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 그를 거두어 주지 않은 배너클 수뇌부의 속을 썩이기 위해서였다."], ["So it had come to pass successively, first, that several distinguished ladies had been frightfully shocked; then, that portfolios of his performances had been handed about o’ nights, and declared with ecstasy to be perfect Claudes, perfect Cuyps, perfect phaenomena; then, that Lord Decimus had bought his picture, and had asked the President and Council to dinner at a blow, and had said, with his own magnificent gravity, ‘Do you know, there appears to me to be really immense merit in that work?’ and, in short, that people of condition had absolutely taken pains to bring him into fashion.", "그리하여 차례차례 이런 일들이 벌어졌다. 우선, 몇몇 명망 있는 부인들이 끔찍하게 충격을 받았고, 다음으로 그의 작품들을 담은 화첩이 밤마다 이리저리 돌려지며 완벽한 클로드요, 완벽한 카위프요, 완벽한 경이라고 황홀하게 선언되었으며, 그다음으로 데시머스 경이 그의 그림을 사들이고는 단번에 미술원 원장과 평의원들을 만찬에 초대하여, 그 특유의 위풍당당한 근엄함으로 \"그거 아시오, 저 작품에는 정말로 막대한 가치가 있는 듯하오만\""], ["But, somehow, it had all failed. The prejudiced public had stood out against it obstinately.", "하고 말했으며, 요컨대 지체 있는 사람들이 그를 유행의 반열에 올려놓으려고 적잖이 애를 썼다."], ["They had determined not to admire Lord Decimus’s picture.", "그러나 어쩐 일인지 그 모든 것이 다 수포로 돌아갔다. 편견에 찬 대중은 완강하게 그것을 거부했다."], ["They had determined to believe that in every service, except their own, a man must qualify himself, by striving early and late, and by working heart and soul, might and main.", "그들은 데시머스 경의 그림을 찬탄하지 않기로 작정했다. 그들은 어떤 직무에서든, 자기네 직무만 빼고는, 사람이란 아침저녁으로 분투하고 온 마음과 영혼을 다해, 있는 힘껏 일함으로써 자격을 갖추어야 한다고 믿기로 작정했다."], ["So now Mr Gowan, like that worn-out old coffin which never was Mahomet’s nor anybody else’s, hung midway between two points: jaundiced and jealous as to the one he had left: jaundiced and jealous as to the other that he couldn’t reach.", "그리하여 이제 가우언 씨는, 마호메트의 것도 그 누구의 것도 아니었던 그 낡아빠진 옛 관처럼, 두 지점 사이 한가운데에 매달려 있게 되었다. 떠나온 한쪽에 대해서는 시샘과 질투로 누렇게 떴고, 닿을 수 없는 다른 한쪽에 대해서도 시샘과 질투로 누렇게 떠 있었다."]], [["Such was the substance of Clennam’s discoveries concerning him, made that rainy Sunday afternoon and afterwards.", "그 비 내리는 일요일 오후와 그 후에 클레넘이 그에 관해 알아낸 것의 요지는 그러했다."]], [["About an hour or so after dinner time, Young Barnacle appeared, attended by his eye-glass; in honour of whose family connections, Mr Meagles had cashiered the pretty parlour-maids for the day, and had placed on duty in their stead two dingy men.", "만찬 시간이 한 시간쯤 지났을 무렵, 젊은 배너클이 외알 안경을 거느린 채 나타났다. 그 집안 연줄에 경의를 표하느라 미글스 씨는 그날 하루 예쁜 응접실 하녀들을 물리고, 그 대신 칙칙한 사내 둘을 근무에 배치해 두었다."], ["Young Barnacle was in the last degree amazed and disconcerted at sight of Arthur, and had murmured involuntarily, ‘Look here!", "젊은 배너클은 아서를 보고 더없이 아연실색하고 당황하여, 정신을 차리기에 앞서 저도 모르게 \"이것 보게!"], ["upon my soul, you know!’ before his presence of mind returned.", "정말이지, 자네도 알다시피, 말이야!\" 하고 중얼거렸다."]], [["Even then, he was obliged to embrace the earliest opportunity of taking his friend into a window, and saying, in a nasal way that was a part of his general debility:", "그러고 나서도 그는 가장 이른 기회를 붙잡아 친구를 창가로 데려가서는, 그의 전반적인 허약함의 일부인 그 콧소리로 이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘I want to speak to you, Gowan.", "\"자네한테 할 말이 있네, 가우언."], ["I say.", "이봐."], ["Look here.", "이것 보게."], ["Who is that fellow?’", "저 친구는 누군가?\""]], [["‘A friend of our host’s.", "\"우리 주인장의 친구일세."], ["None of mine.’", "내 친구는 아니야.\""]], [["‘He’s a most ferocious Radical, you know,’ said Young Barnacle.", "\"저 사람 말이야, 끔찍한 급진파라고, 자네도 알다시피.\" 젊은 배너클이 말했다."]], [["‘Is he?", "\"그래?"], ["How do you know?’", "자넨 그걸 어떻게 아나?\""]], [["‘Ecod, sir, he was Pitching into our people the other day in the most tremendous manner.", "\"이런, 여보게, 저 사람이 며칠 전에 우리 사람들한테 가장 무시무시한 태도로 들이댔단 말일세."], ["Went up to our place and Pitched into my father to that extent that it was necessary to order him out.", "우리 청사로 올라와서는 우리 아버지한테 어찌나 들이대던지 내쫓으라고 명령하는 수밖에 없었지."], ["Came back to our Department, and Pitched into me.", "우리 국으로 다시 와서는 나한테 또 들이댔어."], ["Look here.", "이것 보게."], ["You never saw such a fellow.’", "자넨 그런 친구를 본 적이 없을 걸세.\""]], [["‘What did he want?’", "\"그가 뭘 원했는데?\""]], [["‘Ecod, sir,’ returned Young Barnacle, ‘he said he wanted to know, you know!", "\"이런, 여보게.\" 젊은 배너클이 대답했다."], ["Pervaded our Department--without an appointment--and said he wanted to know!’", "\"저 사람이 자긴 알고 싶다고 했단 말이야, 자네도 알다시피! 약속도 없이 우리 국을 휘젓고 다니면서 — 자긴 알고 싶다고 하더라니까!\""]], [["The stare of indignant wonder with which Young Barnacle accompanied this disclosure, would have strained his eyes injuriously but for the opportune relief of dinner.", "이 폭로에 곁들인, 젊은 배너클의 분개에 찬 경이의 응시는, 마침맞게 찾아온 만찬이라는 구원이 아니었던들 그의 눈을 해칠 만큼 짓눌렀을 것이다."], ["Mr Meagles (who had been extremely solicitous to know how his uncle and aunt were) begged him to conduct Mrs Meagles to the dining-room.", "(삼촌과 숙모의 안부를 몹시도 궁금해하던) 미글스 씨가 그에게 미글스 부인을 식당으로 모셔 달라고 청했다."], ["And when he sat on Mrs Meagles’s right hand, Mr Meagles looked as gratified as if his whole family were there.", "그리고 그가 미글스 부인의 오른편에 앉자, 미글스 씨는 마치 온 집안이 다 거기 모인 양 흐뭇해 보였다."]], [["All the natural charm of the previous day was gone.", "전날의 그 자연스러운 매력은 죄다 사라지고 없었다."], ["The eaters of the dinner, like the dinner itself, were lukewarm, insipid, overdone--and all owing to this poor little dull Young Barnacle.", "만찬을 먹는 사람들도, 만찬 그 자체처럼, 미적지근하고 맛없고 너무 익혀져 있었다 — 그리고 이 모든 것이 이 가엾고 굼뜨고 멍청한 젊은 배너클 탓이었다."], ["Conversationless at any time, he was now the victim of a weakness special to the occasion, and solely referable to Clennam.", "평소에도 화제가 없는 그가, 지금은 이 자리에 특유한 어떤 약점의 희생물이 되어 있었고, 그것은 오직 클레넘에게만 연유한 것이었다."], ["He was under a pressing and continual necessity of looking at that gentleman, which occasioned his eye-glass to get into his soup, into his wine-glass, into Mrs Meagles’s plate, to hang down his back like a bell-rope, and be several times disgracefully restored to his bosom by one of the dingy men.", "그는 절박하고도 끊임없이 그 신사를 바라보지 않을 수 없었고, 그 바람에 그의 외알 안경이 수프 속으로, 포도주 잔 속으로, 미글스 부인의 접시 속으로 빠지고, 종 끈처럼 등 뒤로 늘어지고, 칙칙한 사내들 중 하나의 손에 의해 망신스럽게도 여러 차례 다시 그의 가슴에 걸쳐졌다."], ["Weakened in mind by his frequent losses of this instrument, and its determination not to stick in his eye, and more and more enfeebled in intellect every time he looked at the mysterious Clennam, he applied spoons to his eyes, forks, and other foreign matters connected with the furniture of the dinner-table.", "이 기구를 자꾸 잃어버리는 데다 그것이 그의 눈에 도무지 박혀 있으려 하지 않는 통에 마음이 약해지고, 그 수수께끼 같은 클레넘을 바라볼 때마다 점점 더 지력이 쇠약해진 그는, 숟가락이며 포크며, 식탁의 식기와 관련된 그 밖의 엉뚱한 물건들을 자기 눈에 갖다 댔다."], ["His discovery of these mistakes greatly increased his difficulties, but never released him from the necessity of looking at Clennam.", "이런 실수들을 알아차리는 일은 그의 곤경을 한층 더 키웠지만, 그렇다고 그를 클레넘을 바라보아야 할 필연에서 풀어 주지는 못했다."], ["And whenever Clennam spoke, this ill-starred young man was clearly seized with a dread that he was coming, by some artful device, round to that point of wanting to know, you know.", "그리고 클레넘이 입을 열 때마다, 이 불운한 젊은이는 그가 어떤 교묘한 술책으로 자긴 알고 싶다, 자네도 알다시피, 하는 그 대목으로 돌아오고 있다는 두려움에 또렷이 사로잡혔다."]], [["It may be questioned, therefore, whether any one but Mr Meagles had much enjoyment of the time.", "따라서 미글스 씨 말고는 누구도 그 시간을 그다지 즐겼을지 의문이다."], ["Mr Meagles, however, thoroughly enjoyed Young Barnacle.", "그러나 미글스 씨만큼은 젊은 배너클을 한껏 즐겼다."], ["As a mere flask of the golden water in the tale became a full fountain when it was poured out, so Mr Meagles seemed to feel that this small spice of Barnacle imparted to his table the flavour of the whole family-tree.", "옛이야기 속 황금 물 한 병이 쏟아부어지자 가득한 샘이 된 것처럼, 미글스 씨는 이 작은 배너클 향료 한 줌이 자기 식탁에 온 집안 가계도의 풍미를 부어 준다고 느끼는 듯했다."], ["In its presence, his frank, fine, genuine qualities paled; he was not so easy, he was not so natural, he was striving after something that did not belong to him, he was not himself.", "그 앞에서 그의 솔직하고 훌륭하고 진실한 자질들은 빛이 바랬다. 그는 그토록 편안하지 못했고, 그토록 자연스럽지 못했으며, 자기에게 속하지 않은 무언가를 좇고 있었고, 자기 자신이 아니었다."], ["What a strange peculiarity on the part of Mr Meagles, and where should we find another such case!", "미글스 씨 쪽의 이 무슨 기이한 별남이며, 또 이런 사례를 우리가 달리 어디서 찾을 수 있겠는가!"]], [["At last the wet Sunday wore itself out in a wet night; and Young Barnacle went home in a cab, feebly smoking; and the objectionable Gowan went away on foot, accompanied by the objectionable dog.", "마침내 비 내리는 일요일은 비 내리는 밤 속으로 스러져 갔다. 젊은 배너클은 힘없이 담배를 피우며 삯마차를 타고 집으로 갔고, 거슬리는 가우언은 거슬리는 개를 데리고 걸어서 떠났다."], ["Pet had taken the most amiable pains all day to be friendly with Clennam, but Clennam had been a little reserved since breakfast--that is to say, would have been, if he had loved her.", "펫은 온종일 더없이 상냥하게 애를 쓰며 클레넘에게 다정하게 굴었지만, 클레넘은 아침 식사 이후로 조금 서먹해져 있었다 — 다시 말해, 그가 만약 그녀를 사랑했더라면 그랬으리라는 뜻이다."]], [["When he had gone to his own room, and had again thrown himself into the chair by the fire, Mr Doyce knocked at the door, candle in hand, to ask him how and at what hour he proposed returning on the morrow?", "그가 자기 방으로 가서 다시 난롯가 의자에 몸을 던졌을 때, 도이스 씨가 손에 촛불을 든 채 문을 두드리며, 내일 어떻게, 또 몇 시에 돌아갈 작정이냐고 물었다."], ["After settling this question, he said a word to Mr Doyce about this Gowan--who would have run in his head a good deal, if he had been his rival.", "이 문제를 정한 뒤에, 그는 도이스 씨에게 이 가우언에 관해 한마디 했다 — 만약 그가 자기 연적이었더라면 그의 머릿속을 꽤나 들쑤셔 놓았을 그 가우언에 관해."]], [["‘Those are not good prospects for a painter,’ said Clennam.", "\"화가에게는 그다지 좋은 전망이 못 되는군요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ returned Doyce.", "\"그렇지요.\" 도이스가 대답했다."]], [["Mr Doyce stood, chamber-candlestick in hand, the other hand in his pocket, looking hard at the flame of his candle, with a certain quiet perception in his face that they were going to say something more.", "도이스 씨는 침실용 촛대를 한 손에 들고 다른 손은 주머니에 넣은 채 서서, 자기 촛불의 불꽃을 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 그의 얼굴에는 그들이 무언가 더 이야기하려 한다는 어떤 조용한 알아차림이 어려 있었다."]], [["‘I thought our good friend a little changed, and out of spirits, after he came this morning?’ said Clennam.", "\"우리의 선량한 친구분이 오늘 아침 그 사람이 온 뒤로 조금 달라지고 풀이 죽으신 듯한데요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Doyce.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 도이스가 대답했다."]], [["‘But not his daughter?’ said Clennam.", "\"하지만 따님은 그렇지 않던데요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ said Doyce.", "\"그렇지요.\" 도이스가 말했다."]], [["There was a pause on both sides.", "양쪽 다 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["Mr Doyce, still looking at the flame of his candle, slowly resumed:", "도이스 씨는 여전히 촛불의 불꽃을 바라보며 천천히 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘The truth is, he has twice taken his daughter abroad in the hope of separating her from Mr Gowan.", "\"실은, 그분은 따님을 가우언 씨에게서 떼어 놓을 셈으로 두 차례나 해외로 데리고 나가셨답니다."], ["He rather thinks she is disposed to like him, and he has painful doubts (I quite agree with him, as I dare say you do) of the hopefulness of such a marriage.’", "그분은 따님이 그를 좋아하는 쪽으로 기울었다고 짐작하고 계시고, 또 그런 혼인이 과연 희망적일지에 대해 고통스러운 의구심을 품고 계시지요(저도 그분 생각에 전적으로 동감입니다만, 아마 당신도 그러시리라 봅니다).\""]], [["‘There--’ Clennam choked, and coughed, and stopped.", "\"그 둘 사이에는 —\" 클레넘은 목이 메어 기침을 하고는 멈추었다."]], [["‘Yes, you have taken cold,’ said Daniel Doyce.", "\"네, 감기가 드셨군요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다."], ["But without looking at him.", "그러나 그를 쳐다보지는 않았다."]], [["‘--There is an engagement between them, of course?’ said Clennam airily.", "\"— 물론 둘 사이에 약혼이 되어 있겠지요?\" 클레넘이 짐짓 가벼운 투로 말했다."]], [["‘No. As I am told, certainly not.", "\"아닙니다."], ["It has been solicited on the gentleman’s part, but none has been made.", "제가 듣기로는 분명히 아닙니다. 그쪽 신사 편에서 청혼은 있었지만, 약혼은 이루어지지 않았어요."], ["Since their recent return, our friend has yielded to a weekly visit, but that is the utmost.", "최근에 돌아온 뒤로 우리 친구분이 주 일 회의 방문은 허락하셨지만, 그게 한계지요."], ["Minnie would not deceive her father and mother.", "미니가 부모를 속일 사람은 아니거든요."], ["You have travelled with them, and I believe you know what a bond there is among them, extending even beyond this present life.", "당신도 그들과 함께 여행을 해 보셨으니, 그들 사이에, 이번 생을 넘어서까지 뻗어 있는 그 유대가 어떤 것인지 아시리라 믿습니다."], ["All that there is between Miss Minnie and Mr Gowan, I have no doubt we see.’", "미니 양과 가우언 씨 사이에 있는 것은, 우리가 보고 있는 그게 전부라는 데 의심의 여지가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["We see enough!’ cried Arthur.", "우리가 본 것만으로도 충분합니다!\" 아서가 외쳤다."]], [["Mr Doyce wished him Good Night in the tone of a man who had heard a mournful, not to say despairing, exclamation, and who sought to infuse some encouragement and hope into the mind of the person by whom it had been uttered.", "도이스 씨는, 절망까지는 아니더라도 비통한 외침을 들은 사람이, 그 외침을 토해 낸 이의 마음에 어떤 격려와 희망을 불어넣으려 하는 듯한 어조로 그에게 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다."], ["Such tone was probably a part of his oddity, as one of a crotchety band; for how could he have heard anything of that kind, without Clennam’s hearing it too?", "그런 어조는 아마, 별난 무리의 하나인 그의 괴팍함의 일부였을 것이다. 그게 아니라면, 클레넘도 함께 듣지 않고서야 그가 어떻게 그런 종류의 무언가를 들을 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["The rain fell heavily on the roof, and pattered on the ground, and dripped among the evergreens and the leafless branches of the trees.", "비가 지붕 위로 세차게 떨어지고, 땅바닥을 후두두 두드리고, 상록수들 사이로, 또 잎 떨어진 나뭇가지들 사이로 똑똑 흘러내렸다."], ["The rain fell heavily, drearily.", "비는 세차게, 음울하게 내렸다."], ["It was a night of tears.", "눈물의 밤이었다."]], [["If Clennam had not decided against falling in love with Pet; if he had had the weakness to do it; if he had, little by little, persuaded himself to set all the earnestness of his nature, all the might of his hope, and all the wealth of his matured character, on that cast; if he had done this and found that all was lost; he would have been, that night, unutterably miserable.", "만약 클레넘이 펫에게 사랑에 빠지지 않기로 작정하지 않았더라면, 만약 그가 그럴 만큼 마음이 약했더라면, 만약 그가 조금씩 조금씩 자기 본성의 온 진지함과, 자기 희망의 온 힘과, 무르익은 자기 인격의 온 풍요를 그 한 판의 도박에 걸도록 스스로를 설득했더라면, 만약 그가 그렇게 했는데 모든 것을 잃었음을 알게 되었더라면, 그는 그날 밤 말할 수 없이 비참했으리라."], ["As it was--", "사정이 사정인지라 —"]], [["As it was, the rain fell heavily, drearily.", "사정이 사정인지라, 비는 세차게, 음울하게 내렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover", "# CHAPTER 18. Little Dorrit’s Lover"]], [["Little Dorrit had not attained her twenty-second birthday without finding a lover.", "리틀 도릿은 스물두 번째 생일을 맞기까지 연모하는 이 하나 없이 지내지는 않았다."], ["Even in the shallow Marshalsea, the ever young Archer shot off a few featherless arrows now and then from a mouldy bow, and winged a Collegian or two.", "그 얕디얕은 마셜시에서조차, 영원히 늙지 않는 궁수는 곰팡내 나는 활로 깃 없는 화살 몇 대를 이따금 쏘아 보내며, 수용자 한둘쯤은 명중시키곤 했던 것이다."]], [["Little Dorrit’s lover, however, was not a Collegian.", "그러나 리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 수용자가 아니었다."], ["He was the sentimental son of a turnkey.", "그는 어느 간수의 다감한 아들이었다."], ["His father hoped, in the fulness of time, to leave him the inheritance of an unstained key; and had from his early youth familiarised him with the duties of his office, and with an ambition to retain the prison-lock in the family.", "그의 아버지는 때가 무르익으면 흠 한 점 없는 열쇠를 그에게 물려주기를 바랐고, 어린 시절부터 자기 직무의 의무를, 그리고 그 감옥 자물쇠를 집안에 계속 두고 싶다는 야심을 그에게 익히 가르쳐 두었다."], ["While the succession was yet in abeyance, he assisted his mother in the conduct of a snug tobacco business round the corner of Horsemonger Lane (his father being a non-resident turnkey), which could usually command a neat connection within the College walls.", "그 계승이 아직 보류 상태인 동안, 그는 호스몽거 길 모퉁이를 돌아선 곳에서 어머니가 운영하는 아담한 담배 가게 일을 거들었는데(그의 아버지는 비거주 간수였다), 그 가게는 대개 감옥 담장 안에서 쏠쏠한 단골을 거느릴 수 있었다."]], [["Years agone, when the object of his affections was wont to sit in her little arm-chair by the high Lodge-fender, Young John (family name, Chivery), a year older than herself, had eyed her with admiring wonder.", "여러 해 전, 그의 연정의 대상이 수위실의 높직한 난로 가림막 옆 작은 안락의자에 앉아 있곤 하던 시절, 그녀보다 한 살 많은 영 존(집안 성은 치버리)은 감탄과 경이의 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["When he had played with her in the yard, his favourite game had been to counterfeit locking her up in corners, and to counterfeit letting her out for real kisses.", "마당에서 그녀와 놀 때 그가 가장 좋아하던 놀이는, 그녀를 구석에 가둬 두는 시늉을 하다가 진짜 입맞춤을 받고서 풀어 주는 시늉을 하는 것이었다."], ["When he grew tall enough to peep through the keyhole of the great lock of the main door, he had divers times set down his father’s dinner, or supper, to get on as it might on the outer side thereof, while he stood taking cold in one eye by dint of peeping at her through that airy perspective.", "정문의 큰 자물쇠 열쇠구멍으로 들여다볼 만큼 키가 자라자, 그는 여러 차례 아버지의 점심이나 저녁밥을 문 바깥쪽에 내려놓고 식어 가든 말든 내버려둔 채, 그 바람 통하는 구멍으로 그녀를 들여다보느라 한쪽 눈에 찬 바람을 맞으며 서 있곤 했다."]], [["If Young John had ever slackened in his truth in the less penetrable days of his boyhood, when youth is prone to wear its boots unlaced and is happily unconscious of digestive organs, he had soon strung it up again and screwed it tight.", "소년 시절, 청춘이라는 것이 구두끈을 풀어헤치고 다니기 일쑤이며 다행히도 제 소화기관 따위는 의식하지 못하던 그 둔감한 나날에 영 존의 일편단심이 행여 느슨해진 적이 있었더라도, 그는 이내 그것을 다시 팽팽히 죄어 단단히 조여 두었다. 열아홉 살 때 그의 손은 그녀의 생일을 맞아 그녀 거처를 마주 보는 담벽에 분필로 이렇게 새겼다."], ["At nineteen, his hand had inscribed in chalk on that part of the wall which fronted her lodgings, on the occasion of her birthday, ‘Welcome sweet nursling of the Fairies!’ At twenty-three, the same hand falteringly presented cigars on Sundays to the Father of the Marshalsea, and Father of the queen of his soul.", "\"요정들이 길러 낸 어여쁜 아기를 환영하노라!\" 스물세 살에는 같은 그 손이 일요일마다 떨리는 손길로, 마셜시의 아버지이자 제 영혼의 여왕의 아버지께 시가를 바쳤다."]], [["Young John was small of stature, with rather weak legs and very weak light hair.", "영 존은 키가 작았고, 다리가 다소 부실했으며, 옅은 빛깔의 머리카락은 무척이나 가늘었다."], ["One of his eyes (perhaps the eye that used to peep through the keyhole) was also weak, and looked larger than the other, as if it couldn’t collect itself.", "한쪽 눈도(어쩌면 열쇠구멍으로 들여다보던 그 눈이었으리라) 부실해서, 마치 초점을 그러모으지 못하는 듯 다른 쪽보다 더 크게 보였다."], ["Young John was gentle likewise.", "영 존은 또한 온순했다."], ["But he was great of soul.", "그러나 그 영혼만은 위대했다."], ["Poetical, expansive, faithful.", "시적이고, 도량 넓고, 한결같았다."]], [["Though too humble before the ruler of his heart to be sanguine, Young John had considered the object of his attachment in all its lights and shades.", "제 마음의 지배자 앞에서 너무도 겸손하여 낙관할 수는 없었으나, 영 존은 제 연정의 대상을 온갖 명암 속에서 곰곰이 살펴보았다."], ["Following it out to blissful results, he had descried, without self-commendation, a fitness in it.", "그것을 더없이 행복한 결말까지 따라가 보며, 그는 — 자화자찬 없이 — 거기에 어울림이 있음을 알아보았다."], ["Say things prospered, and they were united.", "일이 잘 풀려 두 사람이 맺어진다고 치자."], ["She, the child of the Marshalsea; he, the lock-keeper.", "그녀는 마셜시의 아이, 그는 자물쇠지기."], ["There was a fitness in that.", "거기에 어울림이 있었다."], ["Say he became a resident turnkey.", "그가 거주 간수가 된다고 치자."], ["She would officially succeed to the chamber she had rented so long.", "그녀는 그토록 오래 세 들어 있던 그 방을 공식적으로 이어받게 되리라."], ["There was a beautiful propriety in that.", "거기에 아름다운 합당함이 있었다."], ["It looked over the wall, if you stood on tip-toe; and, with a trellis-work of scarlet beans and a canary or so, would become a very Arbour.", "그 방은 발끝으로 서면 담장 너머가 보였고, 진홍색 강낭콩 격자 시렁과 카나리아 한두 마리만 있으면 그야말로 정자가 될 터였다."], ["There was a charming idea in that.", "거기에 매혹적인 발상이 있었다."], ["Then, being all in all to one another, there was even an appropriate grace in the lock.", "그리고 서로가 서로의 전부가 되면, 그 자물쇠에마저 어울리는 우아함이 깃들었다."], ["With the world shut out (except that part of it which would be shut in); with its troubles and disturbances only known to them by hearsay, as they would be described by the pilgrims tarrying with them on their way to the Insolvent Shrine; with the Arbour above, and the Lodge below; they would glide down the stream of time, in pastoral domestic happiness.", "세상을 닫아걸고(닫아 가둘 부분만 빼고), 세상의 근심과 소란은 빚 갚는 성지로 향하던 순례자들이 그들 곁에 머물며 들려주는 풍문으로나 전해 듣고, 위로는 정자가 있고 아래로는 수위실이 있으니, 두 사람은 전원풍의 가정적 행복 속에서 세월의 강물을 미끄러져 내려가리라."], ["Young John drew tears from his eyes by finishing the picture with a tombstone in the adjoining churchyard, close against the prison wall, bearing the following touching inscription: ‘Sacred to the Memory Of JOHN CHIVERY, Sixty years Turnkey, and fifty years Head Turnkey, Of the neighbouring Marshalsea, Who departed this life, universally respected, on the thirty-first of December, One thousand eight hundred and eighty-six, Aged eighty-three years.", "영 존은 그 그림을 마무리 짓되, 감옥 담벼락 바로 옆 인접한 교회 묘지에 다음과 같은 가슴 저미는 비문을 새긴 묘비 하나를 더함으로써 제 눈에서 눈물을 자아냈다. \"이웃한 마셜시의 간수로 육십 년, 수석 간수로 오십 년을 봉직하고, 만인의 존경을 받으며 천팔백팔십육년 십이월 삼십일일에 여든세 살의 나이로 이승을 떠난 존 치버리를 기리며."], ["Also of his truly beloved and truly loving wife, AMY, whose maiden name was DORRIT, Who survived his loss not quite forty-eight hours, And who breathed her last in the Marshalsea aforesaid.", "아울러 처녀 적 성이 도릿이었던, 그가 참으로 사랑하고 또 그를 참으로 사랑한 아내 에이미를 기리며. 그녀는 남편을 잃은 지 채 마흔여덟 시간도 못 되어 앞서 말한 마셜시에서 마지막 숨을 거두었다."], ["There she was born, There she lived, There she died.’", "그곳에서 그녀는 태어났고, 그곳에서 살았으며, 그곳에서 죽었다.\""]], [["The Chivery parents were not ignorant of their son’s attachment--indeed it had, on some exceptional occasions, thrown him into a state of mind that had impelled him to conduct himself with irascibility towards the customers, and damage the business--but they, in their turns, had worked it out to desirable conclusions.", "치버리 부부가 아들의 연정을 모르는 바 아니었다 — 실제로 그것은 몇몇 유난한 경우에 그를 손님들에게 성마르게 굴게 하고 장사에 해를 끼칠 만한 심사로 몰아넣기도 했다 — 그러나 그들 역시 나름대로 그것을 바람직한 결론으로 끌어내어 두었다."], ["Mrs Chivery, a prudent woman, had desired her husband to take notice that their John’s prospects of the Lock would certainly be strengthened by an alliance with Miss Dorrit, who had herself a kind of claim upon the College and was much respected there.", "신중한 여인인 치버리 부인은 남편더러 이 점을 새겨 두라 일렀으니, 곧 그들의 존이 자물쇠를 물려받을 전망은 도릿 양과 맺어지면 틀림없이 더 든든해지리라는 것이었다. 그녀 자신이 그 감옥에 대해 일종의 연고를 가졌고 그곳에서 크게 존경받고 있으니 말이다."], ["Mrs Chivery had desired her husband to take notice that if, on the one hand, their John had means and a post of trust, on the other hand, Miss Dorrit had family; and that her (Mrs Chivery’s) sentiment was, that two halves made a whole.", "치버리 부인은 남편더러 또 이 점을 새겨 두라 일렀으니, 한편으로 그들의 존에게는 살림 밑천과 믿음직한 직책이 있고, 다른 한편으로 도릿 양에게는 가문이 있으며, 그녀(치버리 부인)의 견해로는 반쪽 둘이 모여 온전한 하나가 된다는 것이었다."], ["Mrs Chivery, speaking as a mother and not as a diplomatist, had then, from a different point of view, desired her husband to recollect that their John had never been strong, and that his love had fretted and worrited him enough as it was, without his being driven to do himself a mischief, as nobody couldn’t say he wouldn’t be if he was crossed.", "그러고 나서 치버리 부인은 외교가가 아닌 어머니로서, 다른 관점에서 남편더러 이 점을 떠올리라 일렀으니, 곧 그들의 존은 한 번도 튼튼했던 적이 없는데 그 연정이 가뜩이나 그를 충분히 애태우고 졸이고 있으니, 만약 일이 어그러지면 제 몸에 무슨 변고를 일으키지 않으리라고 아무도 장담 못 할 지경까지 그를 몰아붙여서는 안 된다는 것이었다."], ["These arguments had so powerfully influenced the mind of Mr Chivery, who was a man of few words, that he had on sundry Sunday mornings, given his boy what he termed ‘a lucky touch,’ signifying that he considered such commendation of him to Good Fortune, preparatory to his that day declaring his passion and becoming triumphant.", "이런 논거들은 말수 적은 사내인 치버리 씨의 마음을 어찌나 강하게 움직였던지, 그는 여러 일요일 아침마다 아들에게 그가 이른바 \"행운의 손길\"이라 부르는 것을 베풀었으니, 이는 그날 아들이 사랑을 고백하고 의기양양하게 성공하기에 앞서 행운의 여신께 그를 그렇게 천거하는 뜻이라 여겼던 것이다."], ["But Young John had never taken courage to make the declaration; and it was principally on these occasions that he had returned excited to the tobacco shop, and flown at the customers.", "그러나 영 존은 한 번도 그 고백을 할 용기를 내지 못했다. 그가 흥분한 채 담배 가게로 돌아와 손님들에게 달려든 것은 주로 이런 때였다."]], [["In this affair, as in every other, Little Dorrit herself was the last person considered.", "이 일에서도 다른 모든 일에서와 마찬가지로, 정작 리틀 도릿 자신은 맨 마지막으로 고려되는 사람이었다."], ["Her brother and sister were aware of it, and attained a sort of station by making a peg of it on which to air the miserably ragged old fiction of the family gentility.", "그녀의 오빠와 언니는 이 사실을 알고서, 그것을 빌미 삼아 비참하게 너덜너덜해진 가문의 체통이라는 해묵은 허구를 거기에 걸어 펄럭이며 일종의 지위를 누렸다."], ["Her sister asserted the family gentility by flouting the poor swain as he loitered about the prison for glimpses of his dear.", "언니는 그 가엾은 구애자가 사랑하는 이를 한번 보겠다고 감옥 주위를 서성일 때마다 그를 조롱함으로써 가문의 체통을 내세웠다."], ["Tip asserted the family gentility, and his own, by coming out in the character of the aristocratic brother, and loftily swaggering in the little skittle ground respecting seizures by the scruff of the neck, which there were looming probabilities of some gentleman unknown executing on some little puppy not mentioned.", "팁은 귀족적인 오빠 노릇을 하고 나서서, 작은 구주희 마당에서 어느 이름 모를 신사가 거명되지 않은 어떤 강아지 새끼의 목덜미를 움켜잡을 듯 말 듯한 일을 두고 거만하게 으스댐으로써, 가문의 체통과 제 체통을 함께 내세웠다."], ["These were not the only members of the Dorrit family who turned it to account.", "도릿 가문에서 이를 이용한 이가 그들만은 아니었다. 결코, 결코 아니었다."], ["No, no. The Father of the Marshalsea was supposed to know nothing about the matter, of course: his poor dignity could not see so low.", "마셜시의 아버지로 말하자면, 물론 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르는 것으로 되어 있었다."], ["But he took the cigars, on Sundays, and was glad to get them; and sometimes even condescended to walk up and down the yard with the donor (who was proud and hopeful then), and benignantly to smoke one in his society.", "그의 가련한 위엄이 그토록 낮은 데까지 눈길을 줄 리 없었다. 그러나 그는 일요일마다 시가를 받았고, 받아서 기뻐했으며, 때로는 선물한 이(그럴 때면 그는 우쭐하고 희망에 부풀었다)와 함께 마당을 거닐며 그 곁에서 너그럽게 한 대 피워 주기까지 황송하게 했다."], ["With no less readiness and condescension did he receive attentions from Chivery Senior, who always relinquished his arm-chair and newspaper to him, when he came into the Lodge during one of his spells of duty; and who had even mentioned to him, that, if he would like at any time after dusk quietly to step out into the fore-court and take a look at the street, there was not much to prevent him.", "못지않은 흔쾌함과 자상한 너그러움으로 그는 치버리 영감의 시중도 받아들였는데, 영감은 근무 차례 동안 그가 수위실에 들어오면 언제나 제 안락의자와 신문을 그에게 내주었고, 심지어 그에게, 해 진 뒤 어느 때라도 조용히 앞마당으로 나가 거리를 한번 내다보고 싶으시면 그를 막을 만한 것은 별로 없다고 일러 주기까지 했다. 그가 이 후자의 호의를 누리지 않은 것은 다만 그것에 대한 흥미를 잃었기 때문이었다."], ["If he did not avail himself of this latter civility, it was only because he had lost the relish for it; inasmuch as he took everything else he could get, and would say at times, ‘Extremely civil person, Chivery; very attentive man and very respectful.", "그 밖에 받을 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 받았으니 말이다. 그는 이따금 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["Young Chivery, too; really almost with a delicate perception of one’s position here.", "\"치버리는 지극히 예의 바른 사람이지. 무척 자상하고 무척 공손해."], ["A very well conducted family indeed, the Chiveries.", "영 치버리 또한 그렇고. 정말이지 이곳에서의 한 사람의 처지에 대해 거의 섬세한 분별력을 지녔다 할 만하지."], ["Their behaviour gratifies me.’", "치버리네는 참으로 처신이 반듯한 집안이야. 그들의 행실은 나를 흐뭇하게 한다네.\""]], [["The devoted Young John all this time regarded the family with reverence.", "그토록 헌신적인 영 존은 이 내내 그 가족을 경외의 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["He never dreamed of disputing their pretensions, but did homage to the miserable Mumbo jumbo they paraded.", "그는 그들의 허세를 따져 볼 엄두조차 내지 못한 채, 그들이 떠받들고 내세우는 그 비참한 우상에 경배를 올렸다."], ["As to resenting any affront from _her_ brother, he would have felt, even if he had not naturally been of a most pacific disposition, that to wag his tongue or lift his hand against that sacred gentleman would be an unhallowed act.", "그녀의 오빠로부터 어떤 모욕을 당하더라도 원망할 마음으로 말하자면, 그는 설령 천성이 더없이 평화로운 기질이 아니었더라도, 그 신성한 신사에게 혀를 놀리거나 손을 쳐드는 것은 불경한 짓이라 여겼을 것이다."], ["He was sorry that his noble mind should take offence; still, he felt the fact to be not incompatible with its nobility, and sought to propitiate and conciliate that gallant soul.", "그 고결한 마음이 노여움을 사는 것은 안타까웠으나, 그래도 그는 그 사실이 그 고결함과 모순되지 않는다고 느끼며, 그 늠름한 영혼을 달래고 누그러뜨리고자 애썼다."], ["Her father, a gentleman in misfortune--a gentleman of a fine spirit and courtly manners, who always bore with him--he deeply honoured.", "그녀의 아버지는, 불운에 처한 신사 — 고매한 기상과 정중한 풍모를 지니고 늘 그를 너그러이 대해 주던 신사 — 였기에 그는 깊이 존경했다."], ["Her sister he considered somewhat vain and proud, but a young lady of infinite accomplishments, who could not forget the past.", "그녀의 언니는 다소 허영심 있고 거만하다 여겼으나, 지난날을 잊지 못하는, 무한한 재예를 갖춘 아가씨라 보았다."], ["It was an instinctive testimony to Little Dorrit’s worth and difference from all the rest, that the poor young fellow honoured and loved her for being simply what she was.", "그 가엾은 젊은이가 리틀 도릿을 다른 모든 식구와 달리 있는 그대로의 모습 때문에 존경하고 사랑했다는 것은, 그녀의 가치와 남다름에 대한 본능적인 증언이었다."]], [["The tobacco business round the corner of Horsemonger Lane was carried out in a rural establishment one story high, which had the benefit of the air from the yards of Horsemonger Lane jail, and the advantage of a retired walk under the wall of that pleasant establishment.", "호스몽거 길 모퉁이를 돌아선 곳의 담배 가게는 단층짜리 시골풍 건물에서 운영되었는데, 이곳은 호스몽거 길 감옥 마당에서 불어오는 바람의 혜택을 누렸고, 그 쾌적한 시설의 담벼락 아래로 한적하게 거닐 수 있는 이점도 있었다."], ["The business was of too modest a character to support a life-size Highlander, but it maintained a little one on a bracket on the door-post, who looked like a fallen Cherub that had found it necessary to take to a kilt.", "장사 규모가 너무 소박하여 실물 크기의 하일랜드 사람 인형을 내걸 정도는 못 되었으나, 문설주 까치발 위에 작은 것 하나를 두었는데, 그 모습은 마치 킬트를 입지 않을 수 없게 된 추락한 천사 같았다."]], [["From the portal thus decorated, one Sunday after an early dinner of baked viands, Young John issued forth on his usual Sunday errand; not empty-handed, but with his offering of cigars.", "이렇게 장식된 문간에서, 어느 일요일 구운 고기로 이른 점심을 든 뒤, 영 존은 여느 일요일의 심부름에 나섰다. 빈손이 아니라 시가를 봉헌물로 들고서였다."], ["He was neatly attired in a plum-coloured coat, with as large a collar of black velvet as his figure could carry; a silken waistcoat, bedecked with golden sprigs; a chaste neckerchief much in vogue at that day, representing a preserve of lilac pheasants on a buff ground; pantaloons so highly decorated with side-stripes that each leg was a three-stringed lute; and a hat of state very high and hard.", "그는 말끔하게 차려입었으니, 제 체구가 감당할 수 있는 한 큼직한 검정 벨벳 깃이 달린 자두 빛 외투에, 금빛 잔가지 무늬가 박힌 비단 조끼를 받쳐 입었고, 황갈색 바탕에 라일락 빛 꿩들의 사냥터를 그린, 당시 크게 유행하던 점잖은 목도리를 둘렀으며, 옆줄이 어찌나 화려하게 들어갔던지 다리 한 짝이 세 줄 류트처럼 보이는 바지를 입었고, 무척 높고 빳빳한 예장용 모자를 썼다."], ["When the prudent Mrs Chivery perceived that in addition to these adornments her John carried a pair of white kid gloves, and a cane like a little finger-post, surmounted by an ivory hand marshalling him the way that he should go; and when she saw him, in this heavy marching order, turn the corner to the right; she remarked to Mr Chivery, who was at home at the time, that she thought she knew which way the wind blew.", "신중한 치버리 부인이, 이 온갖 치장에 더해 제 아들 존이 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑 한 켤레와, 그가 가야 할 길을 가리키는 상아 손이 꼭대기에 달린 작은 이정표 같은 지팡이까지 들고 있음을 알아채고, 그가 이 거창한 출정 차림으로 오른쪽 모퉁이를 도는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀는 그때 마침 집에 있던 치버리 씨에게 자기는 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 알 것 같다고 한마디 했다."]], [["The Collegians were entertaining a considerable number of visitors that Sunday afternoon, and their Father kept his room for the purpose of receiving presentations.", "그 일요일 오후, 수용자들은 상당수의 방문객을 맞고 있었고, 그들의 아버지는 알현을 받고자 자기 방을 지키고 있었다."], ["After making the tour of the yard, Little Dorrit’s lover with a hurried heart went up-stairs, and knocked with his knuckles at the Father’s door.", "마당을 한 바퀴 돈 뒤, 리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 두근거리는 가슴으로 위층에 올라가 손가락 마디로 아버지의 방문을 두드렸다."]], [["‘Come in, come in!’ said a gracious voice.", "\"들어와요, 들어와!\" 너그러운 목소리가 말했다."], ["The Father’s voice, her father’s, the Marshalsea’s father’s.", "아버지의 목소리, 그녀의 아버지, 마셜시의 아버지의 목소리였다."], ["He was seated in his black velvet cap, with his newspaper, three-and-sixpence accidentally left on the table, and two chairs arranged.", "그는 검정 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 신문을 든 채 앉아 있었고, 탁자에는 우연히 놓인 양 삼 실링 육 펜스가 있었으며, 의자 두 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["Everything prepared for holding his Court.", "알현을 위한 만반의 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["‘Ah, Young John!", "\"아아, 영 존!"], ["How do you do, how do you do!’", "잘 지냈나, 잘 지냈어!\""]], [["‘Pretty well, I thank you, sir.", "\"덕분에 그럭저럭 지냅니다."], ["I hope you are the same.’", "어르신께서도 평안하시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, John Chivery; yes.", "\"그럼, 존 치버리, 그럼."], ["Nothing to complain of.’", "불평할 것 하나 없네.\""]], [["‘I have taken the liberty, sir, of--’", "\"외람되지만 제가, 어르신—\""]], [["‘Eh?’ The Father of the Marshalsea always lifted up his eyebrows at this point, and became amiably distraught and smilingly absent in mind.", "\"응?\" 마셜시의 아버지는 이 대목에서 늘 눈썹을 치켜올렸고, 상냥하게 얼이 빠진 듯 미소를 머금은 채 정신을 딴 데 두는 표정이 되었다."]], [["‘--A few cigars, sir.’", "\"—시가를 몇 대 가져왔습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ (For the moment, excessively surprised.) ‘Thank you, Young John, thank you.", "\"오!\" (한순간, 더없이 놀란 듯이.) \"고맙네, 영 존, 고마워."], ["But really, I am afraid I am too--No?", "그렇지만 정말이지 내가 너무 과한—아니라고?"], ["Well then, I will say no more about it.", "그래, 그렇다면 더는 말하지 않겠네."], ["Put them on the mantelshelf, if you please, Young John.", "영 존, 괜찮다면 벽난로 선반 위에 놔 주게."], ["And sit down, sit down.", "그리고 앉게, 앉아."], ["You are not a stranger, John.’", "자네는 낯선 사람이 아니지 않나, 존.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, I am sure--Miss;’ here Young John turned the great hat round and round upon his left-hand, like a slowly twirling mouse-cage; ‘Miss Amy quite well, sir?’", "\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 정말로—아가씨는;\" 여기서 영 존은 천천히 돌아가는 쥐장처럼 그 큼직한 모자를 왼손 위에서 빙글빙글 돌렸다. \"에이미 아가씨께서는 안녕하신지요, 어르신?\""]], [["‘Yes, John, yes; very well.", "\"그럼, 존, 그럼. 아주 잘 있네."], ["She is out.’\n‘Indeed, sir?’", "지금 외출 중이라네.\""]], [["‘Yes, John.", "\"그렇습니까, 어르신?\""], ["Miss Amy is gone for an airing.", "\"그래, 존. 에이미는 바람을 쐬러 나갔네."], ["My young people all go out a good deal.", "우리 집 아이들은 다들 곧잘 나다닌다네."], ["But at their time of life, it’s natural, John.’", "하지만 그 나이엔 자연스러운 일이지, 존.\""]], [["‘Very much so, I am sure, sir.’", "\"지당하신 말씀입니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘An airing.", "\"바람을 쐬러."], ["An airing.", "바람을 쐬러."], ["Yes.’ He was blandly tapping his fingers on the table, and casting his eyes up at the window.", "그래.\" 그는 탁자 위에 손가락을 부드럽게 두드리며 창문 쪽으로 눈길을 올렸다."], ["‘Amy has gone for an airing on the Iron Bridge.", "\"에이미는 아이언 브리지로 바람을 쐬러 갔네."], ["She has become quite partial to the Iron Bridge of late, and seems to like to walk there better than anywhere.’ He returned to conversation.", "요즘 들어 그 아이는 아이언 브리지를 무척 좋아하게 되어서, 어디보다도 거기를 거니는 걸 좋아하는 듯하다네.\" 그가 다시 대화로 돌아왔다."], ["‘Your father is not on duty at present, I think, John?’", "\"자네 부친은 지금은 근무가 아닌 듯한데, 존?\""]], [["‘No, sir, he comes on later in the afternoon.’ Another twirl of the great hat, and then Young John said, rising, ‘I am afraid I must wish you good day, sir.’", "\"네, 어르신, 아버지는 오후 늦게 나옵니다.\" 큼직한 모자를 또 한 번 빙글 돌리고 나서, 영 존은 일어서며 말했다. \"이만 물러가야 할 것 같습니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘So soon?", "\"벌써?"], ["Good day, Young John.", "잘 가게, 영 존."], ["Nay, nay,’ with the utmost condescension, ‘never mind your glove, John.", "아니, 아니,\" 더없는 너그러움으로, \"장갑은 신경 쓰지 말게, 존."], ["Shake hands with it on.", "낀 채로 악수하세."], ["You are no stranger here, you know.’", "자네는 여기서 낯선 사람이 아니지 않은가.\""]], [["Highly gratified by the kindness of his reception, Young John descended the staircase.", "받아 준 친절에 더없이 흐뭇해하며, 영 존은 계단을 내려갔다."], ["On his way down he met some Collegians bringing up visitors to be presented, and at that moment Mr Dorrit happened to call over the banisters with particular distinctness, ‘Much obliged to you for your little testimonial, John!’", "내려가는 길에 그는 알현시킬 방문객들을 데리고 올라오는 수용자 몇과 마주쳤는데, 바로 그 순간 도릿 씨가 마침 난간 너머로 유난히 또렷하게 외쳤다. \"자네의 자그마한 성의에 참 고맙네, 존!\""]], [["Little Dorrit’s lover very soon laid down his penny on the tollplate of the Iron Bridge, and came upon it looking about him for the well-known and well-beloved figure.", "리틀 도릿을 연모하는 이는 이내 아이언 브리지의 통행료 접시 위에 일 페니를 내려놓고, 그 다리 위에 올라서서 잘 알고 깊이 사랑하는 모습을 찾아 두리번거렸다."], ["At first he feared she was not there; but as he walked on towards the Middlesex side, he saw her standing still, looking at the water.", "처음에는 그녀가 거기 없을까 봐 두려웠으나, 미들섹스 쪽을 향해 걸어가다가 그는 그녀가 가만히 서서 물을 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She was absorbed in thought, and he wondered what she might be thinking about.", "그녀는 생각에 잠겨 있었고, 그는 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까 궁금해했다."], ["There were the piles of city roofs and chimneys, more free from smoke than on week-days; and there were the distant masts and steeples.", "평일보다 매연이 덜한 도시의 지붕과 굴뚝 더미가 있었고, 멀리 돛대와 첨탑들이 있었다."], ["Perhaps she was thinking about them.", "어쩌면 그녀는 그것들을 생각하고 있는지도 몰랐다."]], [["Little Dorrit mused so long, and was so entirely preoccupied, that although her lover stood quiet for what he thought was a long time, and twice or thrice retired and came back again to the former spot, still she did not move.", "리틀 도릿이 어찌나 오래 골똘히 생각에 빠져 온통 정신을 쏟고 있던지, 그녀를 연모하는 이가 제 딴에는 한참이라 여길 만큼 가만히 서 있고, 두세 번 물러났다가 다시 본래 자리로 돌아와도, 그녀는 여전히 움직이지 않았다."], ["So, in the end, he made up his mind to go on, and seem to come upon her casually in passing, and speak to her.", "그래서 그는 결국 마음을 정하고 다가가, 지나는 길에 우연히 마주친 척 그녀에게 말을 걸기로 했다."], ["The place was quiet, and now or never was the time to speak to her.", "그곳은 한적했고, 지금이 아니면 영영 그녀에게 말을 걸 기회가 없을 듯했다."]], [["He walked on, and she did not appear to hear his steps until he was close upon her.", "그가 걸어가는데, 그녀는 그가 바로 곁에 이를 때까지 그의 발소리를 듣지 못한 듯했다."], ["When he said ‘Miss Dorrit!’ she started and fell back from him, with an expression in her face of fright and something like dislike that caused him unutterable dismay.", "그가 \"도릿 양!\" 하고 부르자, 그녀는 흠칫 놀라며 그에게서 물러섰는데, 그 얼굴에는 두려움과 무언가 혐오에 가까운 빛이 어려 있어 그에게 이루 말할 수 없는 낙담을 안겼다."], ["She had often avoided him before--always, indeed, for a long, long while.", "그녀는 전에도 자주 그를 피했었다 — 사실, 아주 오래오래 전부터 늘 그랬다."], ["She had turned away and glided off so often when she had seen him coming toward her, that the unfortunate Young John could not think it accidental.", "그가 자기 쪽으로 오는 것을 보고는 어찌나 자주 돌아서서 미끄러지듯 사라졌던지, 가엾은 영 존도 그것을 우연이라 여길 수가 없었다."], ["But he had hoped that it might be shyness, her retiring character, her foreknowledge of the state of his heart, anything short of aversion.", "그러나 그는 그것이 수줍음이거나, 그녀의 내성적인 성품이거나, 제 마음의 상태를 미리 알아챈 까닭이거나, 아무튼 혐오만은 아닌 무언가이기를 바랐다."], ["Now, that momentary look had said, ‘You, of all people!", "그런데 이제 그 순간의 눈빛이 말하고 있었다. \"하필 당신이!"], ["I would rather have seen any one on earth than you!’", "세상 누구라도 당신보다는 차라리 나았을 텐데!\""]], [["It was but a momentary look, inasmuch as she checked it, and said in her soft little voice, ‘Oh, Mr John!", "그것은 한순간의 눈빛에 지나지 않았으니, 그녀가 이내 그것을 다잡고는 부드럽고 작은 목소리로 말했기 때문이다. \"어머, 존 씨!"], ["Is it you?’ But she felt what it had been, as he felt what it had been; and they stood looking at one another equally confused.", "당신이었군요?\" 그러나 그녀는 그것이 무엇이었는지를 느꼈고, 그도 그것이 무엇이었는지를 느꼈으며, 두 사람은 똑같이 어쩔 줄 모른 채 서로를 마주 보고 서 있었다."]], [["‘Miss Amy, I am afraid I disturbed you by speaking to you.’", "\"에이미 아가씨, 제가 말을 걸어 방해를 한 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, rather.", "\"네, 좀요."], ["I--I came here to be alone, and I thought I was.’", "저—저는 혼자 있으려고 여기 왔고, 혼자인 줄 알았어요.\""]], [["‘Miss Amy, I took the liberty of walking this way, because Mr Dorrit chanced to mention, when I called upon him just now, that you--’", "\"에이미 아가씨, 제가 외람되이 이쪽으로 걸어온 것은, 방금 도릿 어르신을 찾아뵈었을 때 어르신께서 마침 말씀하시기를, 아가씨께서—\""]], [["She caused him more dismay than before by suddenly murmuring, ‘O father, father!’ in a heartrending tone, and turning her face away.", "그녀는 갑자기 가슴 미어지는 어조로 \"아아, 아버지, 아버지!\" 하고 중얼거리며 얼굴을 돌려 버려, 그에게 이전보다 더한 낙담을 안겼다."]], [["‘Miss Amy, I hope I don’t give you any uneasiness by naming Mr Dorrit.", "\"에이미 아가씨, 도릿 어르신을 입에 올려 행여 아가씨를 불편하게 해 드린 것이 아니기를 바랍니다."], ["I assure you I found him very well and in the best of Spirits, and he showed me even more than his usual kindness; being so very kind as to say that I was not a stranger there, and in all ways gratifying me very much.’", "정말이지 어르신께서는 아주 평안하시고 더없이 좋은 기분이셨으며, 평소보다도 더한 친절을 제게 베푸셨습니다. 어찌나 친절하신지 제가 그곳에서 낯선 사람이 아니라고 말씀해 주시고, 여러모로 저를 무척 흐뭇하게 해 주셨답니다.\""]], [["To the inexpressible consternation of her lover, Little Dorrit, with her hands to her averted face, and rocking herself where she stood as if she were in pain, murmured, ‘O father, how can you!", "연모하는 이가 이루 형용할 수 없는 경악에 빠지게도, 리틀 도릿은 돌린 얼굴을 두 손으로 감싸고는 마치 고통에 시달리는 듯 선 자리에서 몸을 흔들며 중얼거렸다. \"아아, 아버지, 어떻게 그러실 수가!"], ["O dear, dear father, how can you, can you, do it!’", "아아, 사랑하는, 사랑하는 아버지, 어떻게, 어떻게 그러실 수가 있어요!\""]], [["The poor fellow stood gazing at her, overflowing with sympathy, but not knowing what to make of this, until, having taken out her handkerchief and put it to her still averted face, she hurried away.", "가엾은 젊은이는 연민이 북받쳐 오른 채 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었으나, 이것을 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 알 수 없었다. 마침내 그녀가 손수건을 꺼내 여전히 돌린 얼굴에 갖다 대고는 황급히 가 버렸다."], ["At first he remained stock still; then hurried after her.", "처음에 그는 못 박힌 듯 서 있었으나, 곧 그녀를 뒤쫓아 달려갔다."]], [["‘Miss Amy, pray!", "\"에이미 아가씨, 제발!"], ["Will you have the goodness to stop a moment?", "잠깐만 멈춰 주시겠습니까?"], ["Miss Amy, if it comes to that, let _me_ go.", "에이미 아가씨, 정 그렇다면, 제가 가겠습니다."], ["I shall go out of my senses, if I have to think that I have driven you away like this.’", "제가 아가씨를 이렇게 내쫓았다고 생각해야 한다면 저는 제정신을 잃고 말 겁니다.\""]], [["His trembling voice and unfeigned earnestness brought Little Dorrit to a stop.", "떨리는 그의 목소리와 꾸밈없는 간절함에 리틀 도릿은 걸음을 멈췄다. \"아아, 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요,\""], ["‘Oh, I don’t know what to do,’ she cried, ‘I don’t know what to do!’", "그녀가 외쳤다. \"어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요!\""]], [["To Young John, who had never seen her bereft of her quiet self-command, who had seen her from her infancy ever so reliable and self-suppressed, there was a shock in her distress, and in having to associate himself with it as its cause, that shook him from his great hat to the pavement.", "그녀가 그 차분한 자제력을 잃은 모습을 한 번도 본 적 없는, 어린 시절부터 늘 그토록 믿음직하고 자신을 억누르는 모습만 보아 온 영 존에게는, 그녀의 비탄이, 그리고 자신이 그 원인으로 거기 얽히게 되었다는 사실이 큰 충격이어서, 그 큼직한 모자에서부터 포장도로까지 그를 뒤흔들어 놓았다."], ["He felt it necessary to explain himself.", "그는 자기 뜻을 해명할 필요를 느꼈다."], ["He might be misunderstood--supposed to mean something, or to have done something, that had never entered into his imagination.", "자기가 오해받을 수도 있었다 — 제 상상에 들어온 적도 없는 무언가를 뜻한 것으로, 혹은 한 것으로 여겨질 수도 있었다."], ["He begged her to hear him explain himself, as the greatest favour she could show him.", "그는 그녀가 베풀 수 있는 가장 큰 호의로서, 자기 해명을 들어 달라고 간청했다."]], [["‘Miss Amy, I know very well that your family is far above mine.", "\"에이미 아가씨, 아가씨 가문이 제 가문보다 훨씬 위라는 것을 저는 잘 압니다."], ["It were vain to conceal it.", "그것을 감추려 해 봐야 헛일이지요."], ["There never was a Chivery a gentleman that ever I heard of, and I will not commit the meanness of making a false representation on a subject so momentous.", "제가 들어 아는 한 치버리 집안에 신사였던 이는 한 번도 없었고, 저는 이토록 중대한 문제에 대해 거짓을 꾸미는 비열한 짓은 하지 않겠습니다."], ["Miss Amy, I know very well that your high-souled brother, and likewise your spirited sister, spurn me from a height.", "에이미 아가씨, 아가씨의 고결하신 오라버니께서, 또한 기개 넘치는 언니께서 높은 데서 저를 멸시하신다는 것도 저는 잘 압니다."], ["What I have to do is to respect them, to wish to be admitted to their friendship, to look up at the eminence on which they are placed from my lowlier station--for, whether viewed as tobacco or viewed as the lock, I well know it is lowly--and ever wish them well and happy.’", "제가 할 일은 그분들을 공경하고, 그분들의 벗으로 받아들여지기를 바라며, 제 낮은 처지에서 그분들이 놓이신 그 높은 자리를 우러러보는 것입니다—담배로 보든 자물쇠로 보든 그것이 낮다는 것을 저는 잘 아니까요—그리고 언제까지나 그분들이 평안하고 행복하시기를 비는 것입니다.\""]], [["There really was a genuineness in the poor fellow, and a contrast between the hardness of his hat and the softness of his heart (albeit, perhaps, of his head, too), that was moving.", "그 가엾은 젊은이에게는 참으로 진실함이 있었고, 그 모자의 딱딱함과 그 마음의 부드러움(어쩌면 그 머리의 부드러움도) 사이의 대비가 사람을 감동시켰다."], ["Little Dorrit entreated him to disparage neither himself nor his station, and, above all things, to divest himself of any idea that she supposed hers to be superior.", "리틀 도릿은 그에게 자신도 자기 처지도 깎아내리지 말라고, 무엇보다도 자기가 제 처지를 더 높다고 여긴다는 생각일랑 떨쳐 버리라고 간청했다."], ["This gave him a little comfort.", "이 말이 그에게 약간의 위안을 주었다."]], [["‘Miss Amy,’ he then stammered, ‘I have had for a long time--ages they seem to me--Revolving ages--a heart-cherished wish to say something to you.", "\"에이미 아가씨,\" 그러더니 그가 더듬거렸다."], ["May I say it?’", "\"저는 오랫동안—제게는 영겁처럼, 돌고 도는 영겁처럼 느껴집니다만—아가씨께 드리고 싶은, 가슴속에 고이 품어 온 소원이 하나 있었습니다. 말씀드려도 될까요?\""]], [["Little Dorrit involuntarily started from his side again, with the faintest shadow of her former look; conquering that, she went on at great speed half across the Bridge without replying!", "리틀 도릿은 자신도 모르게 또다시 그의 곁에서 흠칫 물러섰는데, 그 얼굴에는 아까 그 눈빛의 희미한 그림자가 어렸다. 그것을 가까스로 억누르며, 그녀는 대답도 없이 빠른 걸음으로 다리를 절반 가까이 가로질러 걸어갔다!"]], [["‘May I--Miss Amy, I but ask the question humbly--may I say it?", "\"드려도—에이미 아가씨, 저는 그저 겸손히 여쭐 뿐입니다—말씀드려도 될까요?"], ["I have been so unlucky already in giving you pain without having any such intentions, before the holy Heavens!", "저는 아무 그럴 뜻도 없이 이미 아가씨께 이렇게나 큰 아픔을 드려, 거룩한 하늘에 맹세코!"], ["that there is no fear of my saying it unless I have your leave.", "아가씨의 허락 없이는 결코 말씀드리지 않을 것입니다."], ["I can be miserable alone, I can be cut up by myself, why should I also make miserable and cut up one that I would fling myself off that parapet to give half a moment’s joy to!", "저 혼자 비참해질 수도 있고, 저 혼자 가슴이 찢어질 수도 있는데, 한순간의 기쁨이라도 드릴 수 있다면 저 난간에서 몸을 던지기라도 할 그분을, 어찌하여 함께 비참하게 하고 가슴 찢어지게 하겠습니까!"], ["Not that that’s much to do, for I’d do it for twopence.’", "뭐, 그게 대단한 일도 아니지요. 두 푼만 준대도 그렇게 할 테니까요.\""]], [["The mournfulness of his spirits, and the gorgeousness of his appearance, might have made him ridiculous, but that his delicacy made him respectable.", "그 침울한 심사와 화려한 차림새가 그를 우스꽝스럽게 만들 법도 했으나, 그의 섬세함이 그를 존경할 만한 사람으로 만들어 주었다."], ["Little Dorrit learnt from it what to do.", "리틀 도릿은 거기서 어떻게 해야 할지를 깨달았다."]], [["‘If you please, John Chivery,’ she returned, trembling, but in a quiet way, ‘since you are so considerate as to ask me whether you shall say any more--if you please, no.’", "\"부디, 존 치버리 씨,\" 그녀가 떨면서, 그러나 차분한 투로 대답했다. \"더 말해도 되겠느냐고 이렇게 사려 깊게 물어 주셨으니—부디, 그러지 마세요.\""]], [["‘Never, Miss Amy?’", "\"영영 안 됩니까, 에이미 아가씨?\""]], [["‘No, if you please.", "\"아니요, 제발요. 절대로 안 돼요.\""], ["Never.’\n‘O Lord!’ gasped Young John.", "\"오, 하느님!\" 영 존이 숨을 헐떡였다."]], [["‘But perhaps you will let me, instead, say something to you.", "\"하지만 대신 제가 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있는데, 들어 주시겠어요."], ["I want to say it earnestly, and with as plain a meaning as it is possible to express.", "저는 진심으로, 표현할 수 있는 한 가장 분명한 뜻으로 이 말을 하고 싶어요."], ["When you think of us, John--I mean my brother, and sister, and me--don’t think of us as being any different from the rest; for, whatever we once were (which I hardly know) we ceased to be long ago, and never can be any more.", "우리를 떠올리실 때, 존—오빠와 언니와 저 말이에요—우리를 다른 사람들과 조금이라도 다르게 여기지 말아 주세요. 우리가 한때 어떠했든(저로서는 잘 모르겠지만) 오래전에 그러기를 그쳤고, 다시는 결코 그럴 수 없으니까요."], ["It will be much better for you, and much better for others, if you will do that instead of what you are doing now.’", "지금 하시는 대로 하시는 것보다 그렇게 해 주시는 편이, 당신께도 훨씬 낫고 다른 사람들에게도 훨씬 나을 거예요.\""]], [["Young John dolefully protested that he would try to bear it in mind, and would be heartily glad to do anything she wished.", "영 존은 그것을 마음에 새기도록 애쓰겠노라고, 그녀가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 진심으로 기꺼이 하겠노라고 구슬프게 다짐했다."]], [["‘As to me,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘think as little of me as you can; the less, the better. When you think of me at all, John, let it only be as the child you have seen grow up in the prison with one set of duties always occupying her; as a weak, retired, contented, unprotected girl. I particularly want you to remember, that when I come outside the gate, I am unprotected and solitary.’", "\"저로 말하자면,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"되도록 저를 적게 생각해 주세요. 적을수록 좋아요. 그래도 저를 생각하실 때가 있다면, 존, 그저 감옥에서 늘 한 가지 의무에만 매여 자라는 것을 당신이 보아 온 그 아이로만, 약하고, 내성적이고, 만족할 줄 알고, 보호받지 못하는 한 여자아이로만 여겨 주세요. 특히 이것을 기억해 주셨으면 해요. 제가 그 문 밖으로 나오면, 저는 보호받지 못하는 외로운 신세라는 것을요.\""]], [["He would try to do anything she wished.", "그녀가 바라는 일이라면 무엇이든 애써 보겠다고 그가 말했다."], ["But why did Miss Amy so much want him to remember that?", "그런데 에이미 아가씨는 어찌하여 그토록 그것을 기억하라 하시는 걸까?"]], [["‘Because,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘I know I can then quite trust you not to forget to-day, and not to say any more to me.", "\"왜냐하면,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다."], ["You are so generous that I know I can trust to you for that; and I do and I always will.", "\"그래야 제가 당신을 온전히 믿을 수 있으니까요. 오늘 일을 잊지 않고, 제게 더는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라고요."], ["I am going to show you, at once, that I fully trust you.", "당신은 너그러운 분이라 그 점은 믿을 수 있다는 것을 저는 알아요. 정말 그렇게 믿고, 또 언제까지나 그렇게 믿을 거예요."], ["I like this place where we are speaking better than any place I know;’ her slight colour had faded, but her lover thought he saw it coming back just then; ‘and I may be often here.", "제가 당신을 온전히 믿는다는 것을 지금 당장 보여 드릴게요. 저는 지금 우리가 이야기를 나누는 이곳이 제가 아는 그 어떤 곳보다도 좋아요;\""], ["I know it is only necessary for me to tell you so, to be quite sure that you will never come here again in search of me.", "그녀의 옅은 혈색이 사라져 있었으나, 연모하는 이는 바로 그 순간 그것이 되살아나는 것을 본 듯했다. \"그래서 자주 이곳에 올지도 몰라요."], ["And I am--quite sure!’", "제가 이렇게 말씀드리기만 하면 당신이 결코 저를 찾아 다시는 이곳에 오지 않으리라는 것을, 저는 확신할 수 있다는 걸 알아요. 그리고 저는—정말 확신해요!\""]], [["She might rely upon it, said Young John.", "믿어도 좋다고 영 존이 말했다."], ["He was a miserable wretch, but her word was more than a law for him.", "자기는 비참한 가련뱅이지만, 그녀의 말은 자기에게 법보다 더한 것이라고."]], [["‘And good-bye, John,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그러면 안녕히, 존,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘And I hope you will have a good wife one day, and be a happy man.", "\"그리고 언젠가 당신이 좋은 아내를 얻어 행복한 사람이 되기를 바라요."], ["I am sure you will deserve to be happy, and you will be, John.’", "당신은 분명 행복할 자격이 있고, 또 그렇게 될 거예요, 존.\""]], [["As she held out her hand to him with these words, the heart that was under the waistcoat of sprigs--mere slop-work, if the truth must be known--swelled to the size of the heart of a gentleman; and the poor common little fellow, having no room to hold it, burst into tears.", "이 말과 함께 그녀가 손을 내밀자, 그 잔가지 무늬 조끼 아래에 든 심장이—사실대로 말하자면 그저 싸구려 기성품에 지나지 않았으나—신사의 심장만큼이나 부풀어 올랐고, 그 가엾고 평범하고 보잘것없는 젊은이는 그것을 담아 둘 자리가 없어 그만 울음을 터뜨리고 말았다."]], [["‘Oh, don’t cry,’ said Little Dorrit piteously.", "\"오, 울지 마세요,\" 리틀 도릿이 가엾어하며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t, don’t!", "\"그러지, 그러지 마세요!"], ["Good-bye, John.", "안녕히, 존."], ["God bless you!’", "하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["‘Good-bye, Miss Amy.", "\"안녕히, 에이미 아가씨."], ["Good-bye!’", "안녕히!\""]], [["And so he left her: first observing that she sat down on the corner of a seat, and not only rested her little hand upon the rough wall, but laid her face against it too, as if her head were heavy, and her mind were sad.", "그렇게 그는 그녀를 떠났다. 떠나기 전에, 그녀가 의자 한 귀퉁이에 앉아 작은 손을 거친 담벼락에 기댈 뿐 아니라 그 얼굴마저 거기에 갖다 대는 것을, 마치 머리가 무겁고 마음이 서글픈 듯이 하는 것을 지켜본 다음이었다."]], [["It was an affecting illustration of the fallacy of human projects, to behold her lover, with the great hat pulled over his eyes, the velvet collar turned up as if it rained, the plum-coloured coat buttoned to conceal the silken waistcoat of golden sprigs, and the little direction-post pointing inexorably home, creeping along by the worst back-streets, and composing, as he went, the following new inscription for a tombstone in St George’s Churchyard:", "큼직한 모자를 눈까지 푹 눌러쓰고, 비라도 오는 양 벨벳 깃을 세워 올리고, 금빛 잔가지 무늬 비단 조끼를 감추려 자두 빛 외투의 단추를 채우고, 작은 이정표가 가차 없이 집 쪽을 가리키는 가운데, 더없이 후미진 뒷골목을 따라 살금살금 걸어가며, 가는 길에 세인트 조지 교회 묘지에 세울 묘비의 다음과 같은 새 비문을 짓고 있는 연모하는 이의 모습을 바라보는 것은, 인간의 계획이 얼마나 헛된 것인가를 보여 주는 가슴 저미는 예시였다."]], [["‘Here lie the mortal remains Of JOHN CHIVERY, Never anything worth mentioning, Who died about the end of the year one thousand eight hundred and twenty-six, Of a broken heart, Requesting with his last breath that the word AMY might be inscribed over his ashes, which was accordingly directed to be done, By his afflicted Parents.’", "\"여기 존 치버리의 유해가 잠들다. 이렇다 할 것 하나 없던 이로, 천팔백이십육년 끝 무렵 상심으로 세상을 떠났으니, 마지막 숨으로 제 유골 위에 에이미라는 이름을 새겨 달라 청하매, 비통에 잠긴 그의 부모가 이에 따라 그리하도록 분부하였노라.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations", "# CHAPTER 19. The Father of the Marshalsea in two or three Relations"]], [["The brothers William and Frederick Dorrit, walking up and down the College-yard--of course on the aristocratic or Pump side, for the Father made it a point of his state to be chary of going among his children on the Poor side, except on Sunday mornings, Christmas Days, and other occasions of ceremony, in the observance whereof he was very punctual, and at which times he laid his hand upon the heads of their infants, and blessed those young insolvents with a benignity that was highly edifying--the brothers, walking up and down the College-yard together, were a memorable sight.", "윌리엄과 프레더릭 도릿 형제는 칼리지 마당을 거닐고 있었다 — 물론 귀족적인, 다시 말해 펌프 쪽 마당이었으니, 아버지가 자신의 위신을 위해 일요일 아침이나 성탄절, 그 밖의 의례적인 경우를 제외하고는 가난한 쪽에 있는 자기 자녀들 틈에 끼는 일을 삼가는 것을 하나의 원칙으로 삼았기 때문이다. 그런 의례를 지키는 데에는 그가 무척 시간을 잘 지켰으며, 그런 때에는 자녀들의 어린것들 머리 위에 손을 얹고 그 어린 파산자들에게 자못 교화적인 자비로움으로 축복을 내려 주었다 — 칼리지 마당을 함께 거니는 형제는 인상적인 광경이었다."], ["Frederick the free, was so humbled, bowed, withered, and faded; William the bond, was so courtly, condescending, and benevolently conscious of a position; that in this regard only, if in no other, the brothers were a spectacle to wonder at.", "자유의 몸인 프레더릭은 그토록 풀이 죽고, 허리가 굽고, 시들고, 빛이 바래 있었고, 갇힌 몸인 윌리엄은 그토록 정중하고, 점잖게 굽어보며, 제 처지를 자애롭게 의식하고 있었으니, 다른 무엇으로는 몰라도 적어도 이 점에서만큼은 형제는 경탄할 만한 구경거리였다."]], [["They walked up and down the yard on the evening of Little Dorrit’s Sunday interview with her lover on the Iron Bridge.", "리틀 도릿이 일요일에 아이언 브리지에서 연인과 만난 그날 저녁에도 두 사람은 마당을 거닐었다."], ["The cares of state were over for that day, the Drawing Room had been well attended, several new presentations had taken place, the three-and-sixpence accidentally left on the table had accidentally increased to twelve shillings, and the Father of the Marshalsea refreshed himself with a whiff of cigar.", "그날의 국정은 끝났고, 응접실에는 손님이 잘 들었으며, 새로 인사를 드리러 온 이가 여럿이었고, 우연히 탁자 위에 놓아둔 삼 실링 육 펜스는 우연히 십이 실링으로 불어나 있었으며, 마셜시의 아버지는 한 모금의 시가로 기운을 돋우는 중이었다."], ["As he walked up and down, affably accommodating his step to the shuffle of his brother, not proud in his superiority, but considerate of that poor creature, bearing with him, and breathing toleration of his infirmities in every little puff of smoke that issued from his lips and aspired to get over the spiked wall, he was a sight to wonder at.", "마당을 오르내리며, 자기 우월함에 거만을 떨지 않고 그 가엾은 피조물을 헤아려, 형의 발걸음을 동생의 절뚝거리는 걸음에 상냥하게 맞추고, 그를 너그러이 참아 주며, 입술에서 흘러나와 뾰족한 담장을 넘어가려 애쓰는 작은 연기 한 모금 한 모금마다 그 노쇠함에 대한 관용을 내뿜으니, 그는 경탄할 만한 광경이었다."]], [["His brother Frederick of the dim eye, palsied hand, bent form, and groping mind, submissively shuffled at his side, accepting his patronage as he accepted every incident of the labyrinthian world in which he had got lost.", "흐릿한 눈, 마비된 손, 굽은 몸, 더듬거리는 정신의 동생 프레더릭은 순순히 그의 곁에서 발을 끌었고, 길을 잃고 헤매는 그 미궁 같은 세상의 온갖 일을 받아들이듯 형의 보살핌을 받아들였다."], ["He held the usual screwed bit of whitey-brown paper in his hand, from which he ever and again unscrewed a spare pinch of snuff.", "그는 여느 때처럼 비틀어 만 누르스름한 갈색 종이쪽을 손에 들고서, 거기서 이따금 코담배 한 줌을 비틀어 풀어 꺼냈다."], ["That falteringly taken, he would glance at his brother not unadmiringly, put his hands behind him, and shuffle on so at his side until he took another pinch, or stood still to look about him--perchance suddenly missing his clarionet.", "그것을 떨리는 손으로 집어넣고는, 적잖이 감탄하는 눈빛으로 형을 흘끗 보고, 두 손을 등 뒤로 돌리고서 다시 한 줌을 집을 때까지, 아니면 가만히 서서 주위를 둘러볼 때까지 — 어쩌면 문득 자기 클라리넷이 없는 것을 깨닫고서 — 그렇게 형의 곁에서 발을 끌었다."]], [["The College visitors were melting away as the shades of night drew on, but the yard was still pretty full, the Collegians being mostly out, seeing their friends to the Lodge.", "밤 그림자가 깃들면서 칼리지의 방문객들은 슬슬 빠져나가고 있었으나, 마당은 여전히 꽤 붐볐으니, 수용자들이 대개 나와 친구들을 수위실까지 배웅하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["As the brothers paced the yard, William the bond looked about him to receive salutes, returned them by graciously lifting off his hat, and, with an engaging air, prevented Frederick the free from running against the company, or being jostled against the wall.", "형제가 마당을 거니는 동안, 갇힌 몸인 윌리엄은 인사를 받으려고 주위를 둘러보았고, 우아하게 모자를 들어 답례했으며, 매혹적인 태도로 자유의 몸인 프레더릭이 사람들에게 부딪치거나 담장에 밀려 떠밀리지 않도록 막아 주었다."], ["The Collegians as a body were not easily impressible, but even they, according to their various ways of wondering, appeared to find in the two brothers a sight to wonder at.", "수용자들이란 집단으로 보면 쉽사리 감동하는 이들이 아니었으나, 그들조차 저마다의 방식으로 경탄하며, 이 두 형제에게서 경탄할 만한 광경을 발견하는 듯했다."]], [["‘You are a little low this evening, Frederick,’ said the Father of the Marshalsea.", "\"오늘 저녁엔 좀 처져 보이는구나, 프레더릭.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 말했다."], ["‘Anything the matter?’", "\"무슨 일이라도 있느냐?\""]], [["‘The matter?’ He stared for a moment, and then dropped his head and eyes again.", "\"무슨 일?\" 그는 한순간 멍하니 쳐다보더니, 다시 머리와 눈을 떨구었다."], ["‘No, William, no.", "\"아니, 윌리엄, 아니."], ["Nothing is the matter.’", "아무 일도 없네.\""]], [["‘If you could be persuaded to smarten yourself up a little, Frederick--’", "\"네가 좀 매무새를 가다듬도록 마음을 먹는다면, 프레더릭—\""]], [["‘Aye, aye!’ said the old man hurriedly.", "\"아아, 아아!\" 노인이 다급하게 말했다."], ["‘But I can’t be.", "\"하지만 그럴 수가 없네."], ["I can’t be.", "그럴 수가 없어."], ["Don’t talk so.", "그런 말은 말게."], ["That’s all over.’", "그건 다 끝난 일일세.\""]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea glanced at a passing Collegian with whom he was on friendly terms, as who should say, ‘An enfeebled old man, this; but he is my brother, sir, my brother, and the voice of Nature is potent!’ and steered his brother clear of the handle of the pump by the threadbare sleeve. Nothing would have been wanting to the perfection of his character as a fraternal guide, philosopher and friend, if he had only steered his brother clear of ruin, instead of bringing it upon him.", "마셜시의 아버지는 자기와 친분이 있는 한 수용자가 지나가자 그를 흘끗 보았는데, 마치 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '쇠약한 늙은이지요, 이 사람이. 하지만 내 동생입니다, 선생, 내 동생이에요. 천륜의 목소리란 힘이 센 법이지요!' 그러고는 닳아빠진 소매를 잡아 동생을 펌프 손잡이에서 비켜 가게 했다. 형제간의 길잡이요, 철학자요, 벗으로서 그의 인격이 완벽함에 이르는 데 부족한 것은 오직 하나, 동생에게 파멸을 가져다준 것이 아니라 동생을 파멸에서 비켜 가게 인도했더라면 하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["‘I think, William,’ said the object of his affectionate consideration, ‘that I am tired, and will go home to bed.’", "\"윌리엄, 내 생각엔,\" 그의 다정한 배려의 대상이 말했다. \"내가 피곤한 모양이니, 집에 가서 자야겠네.\""]], [["‘My dear Frederick,’ returned the other, ‘don’t let me detain you; don’t sacrifice your inclination to me.’", "\"이보게 프레더릭,\" 다른 이가 대꾸했다. \"내가 자넬 붙잡지 않겠네. 나 때문에 자네 마음을 희생하지 말게.\""]], [["‘Late hours, and a heated atmosphere, and years, I suppose,’ said Frederick, ‘weaken me.’", "\"늦은 시각하며, 후끈한 공기하며, 그리고 세월 탓이겠지.\" 프레더릭이 말했다. \"그런 게 나를 약하게 만드는군.\""]], [["‘My dear Frederick,’ returned the Father of the Marshalsea, ‘do you think you are sufficiently careful of yourself?", "\"이보게 프레더릭,\" 마셜시의 아버지가 대꾸했다."], ["Do you think your habits are as precise and methodical as--shall I say as mine are?", "\"자넨 자기 몸을 충분히 돌보고 있다고 생각하나? 자네 생활 습관이 — 이를테면 내 습관만큼이나 — 규칙적이고 질서 잡혀 있다고 생각하나?"], ["Not to revert again to that little eccentricity which I mentioned just now, I doubt if you take air and exercise enough, Frederick.", "방금 말한 그 사소한 별난 버릇 이야기를 다시 들추지 않더라도, 나는 자네가 바람과 운동을 충분히 쐬고 하는지 의심스럽네, 프레더릭."], ["Here is the parade, always at your service.", "여기 이 산책로가 있잖나, 언제든 자네가 쓰라고."], ["Why not use it more regularly than you do?’", "어째서 좀 더 규칙적으로 이용하지 않는가?\""]], [["‘Hah!’ sighed the other.", "\"하!\" 다른 이가 한숨지었다."], ["‘Yes, yes, yes, yes.’", "\"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\""]], [["‘But it is of no use saying yes, yes, my dear Frederick,’ the Father of the Marshalsea in his mild wisdom persisted, ‘unless you act on that assent.", "\"하지만 그래, 그래, 한다고 해서 소용이 없네, 이보게 프레더릭.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 온화한 지혜로 고집스레 말을 이었다."], ["Consider my case, Frederick. I am a kind of example.", "\"그렇게 동의해 놓고 행동으로 옮기지 않는다면 말이야."], ["Necessity and time have taught me what to do.", "내 경우를 보게, 프레더릭. 나는 일종의 본보기일세."], ["At certain stated hours of the day, you will find me on the parade, in my room, in the Lodge, reading the paper, receiving company, eating and drinking.", "필요와 세월이 내게 무엇을 해야 하는지 가르쳐 주었지. 하루 중 정해진 시각이면 자넨 산책로에서, 내 방에서, 수위실에서, 신문을 읽거나, 손님을 맞거나, 먹고 마시는 나를 보게 될 걸세."], ["I have impressed upon Amy during many years, that I must have my meals (for instance) punctually.", "나는 에이미에게 여러 해 동안 (이를테면) 식사를 정확히 시간 맞춰 해야 한다는 것을 일러 두었네."], ["Amy has grown up in a sense of the importance of these arrangements, and you know what a good girl she is.’", "에이미는 이런 일들의 중요함을 깨달으며 자랐고, 자네도 그 아이가 얼마나 착한 애인지 알지 않나.\""]], [["The brother only sighed again, as he plodded dreamily along, ‘Hah!", "동생은 꿈결처럼 터덜터덜 걸으며 다시 한숨만 지을 뿐이었다. \"하!"], ["Yes, yes, yes, yes.’", "그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\""]], [["‘My dear fellow,’ said the Father of the Marshalsea, laying his hand upon his shoulder, and mildly rallying him--mildly, because of his weakness, poor dear soul; ‘you said that before, and it does not express much, Frederick, even if it means much. I wish I could rouse you, my good Frederick; you want to be roused.’", "\"이보게,\" 마셜시의 아버지가 동생의 어깨에 손을 얹고 부드럽게 그를 다그치며 말했다 — 부드럽게, 가엾은 사람이 약한 탓이었다. \"방금도 그렇게 말했잖나, 그런 말은 별로 의미가 없네, 프레더릭. 설령 의미가 많다 한들 말이야. 자넬 좀 분발하게 만들 수 있다면 좋으련만, 착한 프레더릭. 자넨 분발할 필요가 있어.\""]], [["‘Yes, William, yes.", "\"그래, 윌리엄, 그래."], ["No doubt,’ returned the other, lifting his dim eyes to his face.", "틀림없이 그렇지.\" 다른 이가 흐릿한 눈을 들어 형의 얼굴을 보며 대꾸했다."], ["‘But I am not like you.’", "\"하지만 난 자네 같지가 않아.\""]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea said, with a shrug of modest self-depreciation, ‘Oh! You might be like me, my dear Frederick; you might be, if you chose!’ and forbore, in the magnanimity of his strength, to press his fallen brother further.", "마셜시의 아버지는 겸손하게 자기를 낮추는 듯 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"오! 자네도 나처럼 될 수 있네, 이보게 프레더릭. 마음만 먹으면 될 수 있어!\" 그러고는 자기 강함의 아량으로, 무너진 동생을 더는 다그치지 않았다."]], [["There was a great deal of leave-taking going on in corners, as was usual on Sunday nights; and here and there in the dark, some poor woman, wife or mother, was weeping with a new Collegian.", "일요일 밤이면 으레 그렇듯, 구석구석에서 작별이 한창이었고, 여기저기 어둠 속에서 어떤 가엾은 여인이, 아내나 어머니가, 새로 들어온 수용자와 함께 울고 있었다."], ["The time had been when the Father himself had wept, in the shades of that yard, as his own poor wife had wept.", "이 아버지 자신도 한때는 그 마당 그늘에서, 자기 가엾은 아내가 울었듯이 울던 시절이 있었다."], ["But it was many years ago; and now he was like a passenger aboard ship in a long voyage, who has recovered from sea-sickness, and is impatient of that weakness in the fresher passengers taken aboard at the last port.", "그러나 그것은 여러 해 전의 일이었고, 이제 그는 긴 항해 길에 오른 배의 승객 같아서, 뱃멀미에서는 이미 회복했건만 마지막 항구에서 새로 올라탄 더 풋풋한 승객들의 그 나약함이 거슬리는 것이었다."], ["He was inclined to remonstrate, and to express his opinion that people who couldn’t get on without crying, had no business there.", "그는 못마땅함을 드러내며, 울지 않고는 못 견디는 사람들이라면 여기 있을 자격이 없다는 견해를 표하고 싶어 했다."], ["In manner, if not in words, he always testified his displeasure at these interruptions of the general harmony; and it was so well understood, that delinquents usually withdrew if they were aware of him.", "말로는 아니더라도 태도로는 늘 이런 전체의 화목을 깨뜨리는 짓에 대한 불쾌함을 내비쳤고, 그것이 워낙 잘 알려져 있어서, 위반자들은 그가 곁에 있는 줄 알면 으레 물러서곤 했다."]], [["On this Sunday evening, he accompanied his brother to the gate with an air of endurance and clemency; being in a bland temper and graciously disposed to overlook the tears.", "이 일요일 저녁, 그는 인내와 관용의 태도로 동생을 문까지 배웅했으니, 기분이 부드러워져 너그러이 그 눈물들을 못 본 척해 주려는 마음이었던 것이다."], ["In the flaring gaslight of the Lodge, several Collegians were basking; some taking leave of visitors, and some who had no visitors, watching the frequent turning of the key, and conversing with one another and with Mr Chivery.", "수위실의 일렁이는 가스등 불빛 아래 여러 수용자들이 몸을 쬐고 있었다. 더러는 방문객을 배웅하는 중이었고, 더러는 방문객이 없어 자물쇠가 자주 돌아가는 것을 지켜보며 자기들끼리, 또 치버리 씨와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["The paternal entrance made a sensation of course; and Mr Chivery, touching his hat (in a short manner though) with his key, hoped he found himself tolerable.", "이 아버지의 등장은 물론 한바탕 소동을 일으켰고, 치버리 씨는 (다소 짧게나마) 열쇠를 든 채 모자에 손을 대며, 그가 그런대로 잘 지내기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["‘Thank you, Chivery, quite well.", "\"고맙네, 치버리, 아주 잘 지내네."], ["And you?’", "자넨 어떤가?\""]], [["Mr Chivery said in a low growl, ‘Oh!", "치버리 씨는 낮게 으르렁대듯 말했다. \"오!"], ["_he_ was all right.’ Which was his general way of acknowledging inquiries after his health when a little sullen.", "저야 멀쩡합니다요.\" 이것이 그가 조금 뚱해 있을 때 안부 인사에 응하는 일반적인 방식이었다."]], [["‘I had a visit from Young John to-day, Chivery.", "\"오늘 영 존이 나를 보러 왔더군, 치버리."], ["And very smart he looked, I assure you.’", "아주 말쑥해 보이더라고, 정말이지.\""]], [["So Mr Chivery had heard.", "그래, 치버리 씨도 들어 알고 있었다."], ["Mr Chivery must confess, however, that his wish was that the boy didn’t lay out so much money upon it.", "다만, 그 녀석이 거기다 그렇게 돈을 많이 들이지 않았으면 하는 게 자기 바람이라고 고백해야겠다고 했다."], ["For what did it bring him in?", "그래 봐야 그 녀석한테 돌아오는 게 뭐냔 말이다."], ["It only brought him in wexation.", "돌아오는 거라곤 속만 끓는 일뿐이었다."], ["And he could get that anywhere for nothing.", "그런 거야 어디서든 거저 얻을 수 있는 것이고."]], [["‘How vexation, Chivery?’ asked the benignant father.", "\"속이 어째서 끓는다는 건가, 치버리?\" 자애로운 아버지가 물었다."]], [["‘No odds,’ returned Mr Chivery.", "\"별것 아닙니다.\" 치버리 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Never mind.", "\"신경 쓰지 마십쇼."], ["Mr Frederick going out?’", "프레더릭 씨 나가십니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, Chivery, my brother is going home to bed.", "\"그래, 치버리, 내 동생이 집에 가서 자려고 하네."], ["He is tired, and not quite well.", "피곤한 데다 몸이 좀 좋지 않아서."], ["Take care, Frederick, take care.", "조심하게, 프레더릭, 조심해."], ["Good night, my dear Frederick!’", "잘 가게, 이보게 프레더릭!\""]], [["Shaking hands with his brother, and touching his greasy hat to the company in the Lodge, Frederick slowly shuffled out of the door which Mr Chivery unlocked for him.", "형과 악수하고, 수위실 안의 사람들에게 기름때 묻은 모자에 손을 대어 인사하고서, 프레더릭은 치버리 씨가 열어 준 문으로 느릿느릿 발을 끌며 나갔다."], ["The Father of the Marshalsea showed the amiable solicitude of a superior being that he should come to no harm.", "마셜시의 아버지는 동생이 아무 일도 당하지 않게 하려는, 우월한 존재다운 다정한 노심초사를 내보였다."]], [["‘Be so kind as to keep the door open a moment, Chivery, that I may see him go along the passage and down the steps.", "\"미안하지만 문을 잠깐 열어 두게나, 치버리, 저 사람이 통로를 지나 계단을 내려가는 것을 좀 봐야겠네."], ["Take care, Frederick!", "조심하게, 프레더릭!"], ["(He is very infirm.) Mind the steps!", "(저 사람은 아주 쇠약하다네.) 계단 조심하게!"], ["(He is so very absent.) Be careful how you cross, Frederick.", "(저 사람은 정신이 워낙 없어서.) 길 건널 때 조심하게, 프레더릭."], ["(I really don’t like the notion of his going wandering at large, he is so extremely liable to be run over.)’", "(저 사람이 마음대로 떠도는 건 정말 마음에 들지가 않아, 차에 치이기가 어찌나 쉬운지 말이야.)\""]], [["With these words, and with a face expressive of many uneasy doubts and much anxious guardianship, he turned his regards upon the assembled company in the Lodge: so plainly indicating that his brother was to be pitied for not being under lock and key, that an opinion to that effect went round among the Collegians assembled.", "이런 말과 함께, 갖은 불안한 의구심과 노심초사의 보호자다운 표정을 얼굴에 띤 채, 그는 수위실에 모인 사람들에게로 시선을 돌렸으니, 자기 동생이 자물쇠 안에 갇혀 지내지 못하는 처지가 가엾다는 뜻을 어찌나 노골적으로 내비쳤던지, 모여 있던 수용자들 사이에 그런 취지의 의견이 한 바퀴 돌았다."]], [["But he did not receive it with unqualified assent; on the contrary, he said, No, gentlemen, no; let them not misunderstand him.", "그러나 그는 그것을 무조건 동의하며 받아들이지는 않았다. 오히려 그는 이렇게 말했다."], ["His brother Frederick was much broken, no doubt, and it might be more comfortable to himself (the Father of the Marshalsea) to know that he was safe within the walls.", "아니올시다, 여러분, 아니에요. 오해는 하지 마시길."], ["Still, it must be remembered that to support an existence there during many years, required a certain combination of qualities--he did not say high qualities, but qualities--moral qualities.", "내 동생 프레더릭이 많이 망가진 건 틀림없고, 나로서는 (마셜시의 아버지로서는) 저 사람이 이 담장 안에서 안전하다고 알고 있는 편이 더 마음 편할 수도 있겠지요. 그래도 잊지 말아야 할 것은, 여기서 여러 해 동안 살아남으려면 어떤 자질의 결합이 필요하다는 점입니다 — 고매한 자질이라고는 하지 않겠으나, 어떤 자질, 어떤 도덕적 자질 말이지요."], ["Now, had his brother Frederick that peculiar union of qualities?", "그렇다면 내 동생 프레더릭에게 그런 독특한 자질의 결합이 있겠습니까?"], ["Gentlemen, he was a most excellent man, a most gentle, tender, and estimable man, with the simplicity of a child; but would he, though unsuited for most other places, do for that place?", "여러분, 저 사람은 더없이 훌륭한 사람이요, 더없이 온화하고 다정하며 존경할 만한 사람으로, 어린아이 같은 천진함을 지녔지요. 하지만 다른 대부분의 자리에는 어울리지 않더라도, 이 자리에는 맞겠습니까?"], ["No; he said confidently, no!", "아니지요. 나는 자신 있게 말합니다, 아니에요!"], ["And, he said, Heaven forbid that Frederick should be there in any other character than in his present voluntary character!", "그리고 말하건대, 프레더릭이 지금 같은 자발적인 처지 말고 다른 어떤 처지로 여기 있게 되는 일은 결코 없기를 빕니다!"], ["Gentlemen, whoever came to that College, to remain there a length of time, must have strength of character to go through a good deal and to come out of a good deal.", "여러분, 이 칼리지에 들어와 오래 머무는 자라면 누구든, 숱한 일을 견뎌 내고 숱한 일에서 헤쳐 나올 만한 성격의 강인함이 있어야 합니다."], ["Was his beloved brother Frederick that man?", "내 사랑하는 동생 프레더릭이 그런 사람입니까? 아니지요."], ["No. They saw him, even as it was, crushed.", "여러분도 보시다시피 이렇게나 짓눌려 있습니다."], ["Misfortune crushed him.", "불운이 저 사람을 짓눌러 버렸지요."], ["He had not power of recoil enough, not elasticity enough, to be a long time in such a place, and yet preserve his self-respect and feel conscious that he was a gentleman.", "저 사람은 이런 곳에 오래 있으면서도 자존감을 지키고 스스로 신사임을 의식할 만한 반발력이, 탄력이 모자랍니다."], ["Frederick had not (if he might use the expression) Power enough to see in any delicate little attentions and--and--Testimonials that he might under such circumstances receive, the goodness of human nature, the fine spirit animating the Collegians as a community, and at the same time no degradation to himself, and no depreciation of his claims as a gentleman.", "프레더릭에게는 (이런 표현을 써도 된다면) 그런 처지에서 받을 법한 세심한 작은 호의와 — 그리고 — 그리고 — 정표들 속에서, 인간 본성의 선함을, 한 공동체로서 수용자들을 살아 움직이게 하는 그 고결한 정신을 알아보면서도, 동시에 그것이 자기를 깎아내리는 것도 아니요 신사로서의 권위를 떨어뜨리는 것도 아님을 알아볼 만한 힘이 없습니다."], ["Gentlemen, God bless you!", "여러분, 신의 가호가 있기를!"]], [["Such was the homily with which he improved and pointed the occasion to the company in the Lodge before turning into the sallow yard again, and going with his own poor shabby dignity past the Collegian in the dressing-gown who had no coat, and past the Collegian in the sea-side slippers who had no shoes, and past the stout greengrocer Collegian in the corduroy knee-breeches who had no cares, and past the lean clerk Collegian in buttonless black who had no hopes, up his own poor shabby staircase to his own poor shabby room.", "이것이 그가 수위실의 사람들에게 그 자리를 교화하고 훈계 삼아 베푼 설교였고, 그러고 나서 그는 다시 누르스름한 마당으로 들어서서, 코트도 없이 실내복만 걸친 수용자를 지나, 신발도 없이 해변용 슬리퍼만 신은 수용자를 지나, 골덴 반바지를 입고서 근심이라곤 없는 뚱뚱한 채소 장수 수용자를 지나, 단추 떨어진 검은 옷차림에 희망이라곤 없는 비쩍 마른 사무원 수용자를 지나, 제 초라한 계단을 올라 제 초라한 방으로 자기만의 가엾고 초라한 위엄을 지니고 돌아갔다."]], [["There, the table was laid for his supper, and his old grey gown was ready for him on his chair-back at the fire.", "그곳에는 그의 저녁상이 차려져 있었고, 낡은 회색 가운이 난롯가 의자 등받이에 걸린 채 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["His daughter put her little prayer-book in her pocket--had she been praying for pity on all prisoners and captives!--and rose to welcome him.", "딸은 작은 기도서를 주머니에 넣고서 — 모든 죄수와 포로에게 자비가 내리기를 기도하던 참이었을까! — 일어나 그를 맞았다."]], [["Uncle had gone home, then?", "그래, 삼촌은 댁에 가셨고요?"], ["she asked him, as she changed his coat and gave him his black velvet cap.", "그녀는 그의 코트를 갈아입히고 검은 벨벳 모자를 건네며 물었다. 그래, 삼촌은 집에 갔지."], ["Yes, uncle had gone home.", "아버지는 산책이 즐거우셨어요?"], ["Had her father enjoyed his walk?", "글쎄, 별로다, 에이미. 별로야."], ["Why, not much, Amy; not much. No!", "아니라고!"], ["Did he not feel quite well?", "어디 편찮으신 거예요?"]], [["As she stood behind him, leaning over his chair so lovingly, he looked with downcast eyes at the fire.", "딸이 그토록 다정하게 그의 의자 위로 몸을 기울인 채 뒤에 서 있는데, 그는 눈을 떨군 채 난롯불을 바라보았다."], ["An uneasiness stole over him that was like a touch of shame; and when he spoke, as he presently did, it was in an unconnected and embarrassed manner.", "부끄러움의 기색과도 같은 불안이 그를 슬그머니 엄습했고, 이윽고 그가 입을 열었을 때 그것은 두서없고 거북한 투였다."]], [["‘Something, I--hem!--I don’t know what, has gone wrong with Chivery. He is not--ha!--not nearly so obliging and attentive as usual to-night. It--hem!--it’s a little thing, but it puts me out, my love. It’s impossible to forget,’ turning his hands over and over and looking closely at them, ‘that--hem!--that in such a life as mine, I am unfortunately dependent on these men for something every hour in the day.’", "\"무언가가, 나는 — 흠! — 무언지는 모르겠다만, 치버리한테 틀어진 일이 있는 모양이야. 오늘 밤엔 — 하! — 평소처럼 그렇게 살갑고 친절하지가 않더구나. 그게 — 흠! — 사소한 일이긴 하다만, 영 마음에 걸리는구나, 얘야. 잊을 수가 없거든,\" 그는 손을 이리저리 뒤집으며 유심히 들여다보았다. \"나 같은 처지로 살자면 — 흠! — 하루에도 매시간 무언가를 이 사람들에게 의지할 수밖에 없다는 것을 말이다, 가엾게도.\""]], [["Her arm was on his shoulder, but she did not look in his face while he spoke.", "딸의 팔은 그의 어깨에 놓여 있었으나, 그가 말하는 동안 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보지 않았다."], ["Bending her head she looked another way.", "고개를 숙인 채 다른 쪽을 보았다."]], [["‘I--hem!--I can’t think, Amy, what has given Chivery offence.", "\"나는 — 흠! — 도무지 모르겠구나, 에이미, 무엇이 치버리의 비위를 거슬렀는지."], ["He is generally so--so very attentive and respectful.", "평소엔 그렇게나 — 그렇게나 친절하고 깍듯하던 사람인데."], ["And to-night he was quite--quite short with me.", "그런데 오늘 밤엔 영 — 영 퉁명스럽더구나, 나한테."], ["Other people there too!", "거기 다른 사람들도 있는 데서 말이야!"], ["Why, good Heaven!", "아니, 맙소사!"], ["if I was to lose the support and recognition of Chivery and his brother officers, I might starve to death here.’ While he spoke, he was opening and shutting his hands like valves; so conscious all the time of that touch of shame, that he shrunk before his own knowledge of his meaning.", "내가 만약 치버리와 그 동료 간수들의 후원과 인정을 잃기라도 하면, 나는 여기서 굶어 죽을 수도 있어.\" 그는 말하는 내내 두 손을 판막처럼 폈다 쥐었다 했는데, 그러는 동안에도 그 부끄러움의 기색을 어찌나 의식했던지, 자기 말뜻을 스스로 아는 데서 움츠러들었다."]], [["‘I--ha!--I can’t think what it’s owing to.", "\"나는 — 하! — 무엇 때문인지 도무지 알 수가 없구나."], ["I am sure I cannot imagine what the cause of it is.", "정말이지 그 원인이 무엇인지 짐작도 못하겠어."], ["There was a certain Jackson here once, a turnkey of the name of Jackson (I don’t think you can remember him, my dear, you were very young), and--hem!--and he had a--brother, and this--young brother paid his addresses to--at least, did not go so far as to pay his addresses to--but admired--respectfully admired--the--not daughter, the sister--of one of us; a rather distinguished Collegian; I may say, very much so.", "한때 여기에 잭슨이라는 자가 있었지, 잭슨이라는 이름의 간수였단다 (네가 그를 기억할 성싶지는 않구나, 얘야, 그땐 네가 아주 어렸으니까), 그런데 — 흠! — 그에게 동생이 하나 있었는데, 이 — 젊은 동생이 우리 가운데 한 사람의 — 적어도 구애까지 한 건 아니고 — 흠모했던 거지 — 정중히 흠모했던 거야 — 어느 꽤 명망 있는 수용자의 — 딸이 아니라 누이를 말이다."], ["His name was Captain Martin; and he consulted me on the question whether it was necessary that his daughter--sister--should hazard offending the turnkey brother by being too--ha!--too plain with the other brother.", "정말이지 대단히 명망 있는 분이었어. 이름은 마틴 대위였단다."], ["Captain Martin was a gentleman and a man of honour, and I put it to him first to give me his--his own opinion.", "그가 나한테 의논을 청하기를, 자기 딸 — 누이가 그 다른 동생한테 너무 — 하! — 너무 솔직하게 굴어서 간수인 형의 비위를 거스를 위험을 무릅쓸 필요가 있겠느냐는 거였지."], ["Captain Martin (highly respected in the army) then unhesitatingly said that it appeared to him that his--hem!--sister was not called upon to understand the young man too distinctly, and that she might lead him on--I am doubtful whether “lead him on”", "마틴 대위는 신사요 명예를 아는 사람이라, 나는 먼저 그에게 그 자신의 — 자기 의견을 말해 보라고 했단다. 그러자 마틴 대위는 (군에서 크게 존경받던 분이지) 망설임 없이 말하기를, 자기 — 흠!"], ["was Captain Martin’s exact expression: indeed I think he said tolerate him--on her father’s--I should say, brother’s--account.", "— 누이가 그 젊은이를 너무 분명하게 이해해 줄 필요는 없을 듯하고, 아버지를 — 아니, 오라비를 — 위해서 그를 — 이끌어 가도 좋겠다고 하더구나 — '이끌어 간다'가 마틴 대위의 정확한 표현이었는지는 잘 모르겠다만, 실은 그가 그를 봐 준다고 했던 것 같구나."], ["I hardly know how I have strayed into this story.", "내가 어쩌다 이 이야기로 빠졌는지 모르겠구나."], ["I suppose it has been through being unable to account for Chivery; but as to the connection between the two, I don’t see--’", "치버리를 도무지 이해할 수가 없어서 그랬나 보다. 하지만 그 둘 사이에 무슨 연관이 있는지는, 나도 모르겠—\""]], [["His voice died away, as if she could not bear the pain of hearing him, and her hand had gradually crept to his lips.", "딸이 그의 말을 듣는 고통을 차마 견딜 수 없는 듯, 그의 목소리는 잦아들었고, 어느새 그녀의 손이 슬그머니 그의 입술로 올라가 있었다."], ["For a little while there was a dead silence and stillness; and he remained shrunk in his chair, and she remained with her arm round his neck and her head bowed down upon his shoulder.", "잠시 동안 죽은 듯한 침묵과 고요가 흘렀다. 그는 의자에 움츠린 채로 있었고, 그녀는 그의 목에 팔을 두르고 그의 어깨에 머리를 묻은 채로 있었다."]], [["His supper was cooking in a saucepan on the fire, and, when she moved, it was to make it ready for him on the table.", "그의 저녁식사는 난로 위 냄비에서 익고 있었고, 그녀가 몸을 움직였을 때 그것은 그것을 식탁 위에 차려 내기 위함이었다."], ["He took his usual seat, she took hers, and he began his meal.", "그는 늘 앉던 자리에 앉았고, 그녀도 제자리에 앉았으며, 그는 식사를 시작했다."], ["They did not, as yet, look at one another.", "두 사람은 아직 서로를 바라보지 않았다."], ["By little and little he began; laying down his knife and fork with a noise, taking things up sharply, biting at his bread as if he were offended with it, and in other similar ways showing that he was out of sorts.", "그는 조금씩 시작했으니, 나이프와 포크를 소리 나게 내려놓고, 물건을 거칠게 집어 들고, 빵에 화풀이라도 하듯 베어 물고, 그 밖에도 그런 식으로 자기가 심사가 틀어졌음을 내보였다."], ["At length he pushed his plate from him, and spoke aloud; with the strangest inconsistency.", "마침내 그는 접시를 밀어내고는, 더없이 기묘하게 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 소리로 큰 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘What does it matter whether I eat or starve?", "\"내가 먹든 굶든 그게 무슨 대수냐?"], ["What does it matter whether such a blighted life as mine comes to an end, now, next week, or next year?", "나처럼 시들어 버린 인생이 지금 끝나든, 다음 주에 끝나든, 내년에 끝나든 그게 무슨 대수란 말이냐?"], ["What am I worth to anyone?", "내가 누구한테 무슨 값어치가 있다고?"], ["A poor prisoner, fed on alms and broken victuals; a squalid, disgraced wretch!’", "적선과 남은 음식이나 얻어먹는 가엾은 죄수, 더럽고 욕된 비렁뱅이일 뿐인데!\""]], [["‘Father, father!’ As he rose she went on her knees to him, and held up her hands to him.", "\"아버지, 아버지!\" 그가 일어서자 그녀는 그의 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그를 향해 두 손을 들어 올렸다."]], [["‘Amy,’ he went on in a suppressed voice, trembling violently, and looking at her as wildly as if he had gone mad.", "\"에이미,\" 그는 격하게 떨면서, 미친 사람처럼 그녀를 사납게 바라보며, 억눌린 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["‘I tell you, if you could see me as your mother saw me, you wouldn’t believe it to be the creature you have only looked at through the bars of this cage.", "\"내 말 좀 들어 보아라, 네 어미가 보았던 나를 네가 볼 수 있었다면, 너는 이 우리 창살 사이로만 봐 온 그 인간이 나라고는 믿지 못할 게다."], ["I was young, I was accomplished, I was good-looking, I was independent--by God I was, child!--and people sought me out, and envied me.", "나는 젊었고, 재주가 있었고, 잘생겼고, 떳떳한 독립된 몸이었어 — 맹세코 그랬다, 얘야! — 사람들이 나를 찾아왔고, 나를 부러워했지."], ["Envied me!’", "나를 부러워했단 말이다!\""]], [["‘Dear father!’ She tried to take down the shaking arm that he flourished in the air, but he resisted, and put her hand away.", "\"가엾은 아버지!\" 그녀는 그가 허공에 휘젓는 떨리는 팔을 끌어내리려 했으나, 그는 뿌리치며 그녀의 손을 밀어냈다."]], [["‘If I had but a picture of myself in those days, though it was ever so ill done, you would be proud of it, you would be proud of it.", "\"그 시절의 내 초상화 한 장만 있었더라면, 아무리 서툴게 그린 것일지라도, 너는 그것을 자랑스러워했을 게다, 그것을 자랑스러워했을 게야. 하지만 그런 건 없구나."], ["But I have no such thing.", "자, 나를 본보기로 삼아라!"], ["Now, let me be a warning!", "어느 누구도,\""], ["Let no man,’ he cried, looking haggardly about, ‘fail to preserve at least that little of the times of his prosperity and respect.", "그는 초췌하게 주위를 둘러보며 외쳤다. \"제가 번성하고 존경받던 시절의 그 한 조각만이라도 간직하는 데 실패하지 말지어다."], ["Let his children have that clue to what he was.", "제 자식들에게 제가 어떤 사람이었는지를 알 단서를 남겨 줄지어다."], ["Unless my face, when I am dead, subsides into the long departed look--they say such things happen, I don’t know--my children will have never seen me.’", "내가 죽었을 때 내 얼굴이 오래전에 떠나간 그 모습으로 가라앉지 않는다면 — 그런 일이 있다고들 하더라만, 나는 모르겠다 — 내 자식들은 나를 한 번도 보지 못한 셈이 되겠지.\""]], [["‘Father, father!’", "\"아버지, 아버지!\""]], [["‘O despise me, despise me!", "\"오, 나를 경멸해라, 나를 경멸해!"], ["Look away from me, don’t listen to me, stop me, blush for me, cry for me--even you, Amy!", "나한테서 눈길을 돌리고, 내 말을 듣지 말고, 나를 막고, 나 때문에 부끄러워하고, 나 때문에 울어라 — 너마저도, 에이미!"], ["Do it, do it!", "그렇게 해, 그렇게 하란 말이야!"], ["I do it to myself!", "나도 나 자신한테 그러는걸!"], ["I am hardened now, I have sunk too low to care long even for that.’", "나는 이제 굳어 버렸어, 너무 깊이 가라앉아서 이젠 그런 것조차 오래 마음에 두지 못한다.\""]], [["‘Dear father, loved father, darling of my heart!’ She was clinging to him with her arms, and she got him to drop into his chair again, and caught at the raised arm, and tried to put it round her neck.", "\"가엾은 아버지, 사랑하는 아버지, 제 마음의 보배!\" 그녀는 두 팔로 그에게 매달려, 그를 다시 의자에 주저앉히고, 들어 올린 그의 팔을 붙잡아 자기 목에 두르려 했다."]], [["‘Let it lie there, father.", "\"여기 그대로 두세요, 아버지."], ["Look at me, father, kiss me, father!", "저를 보세요, 아버지, 입을 맞춰 주세요, 아버지!"], ["Only think of me, father, for one little moment!’", "단 한순간만이라도 제 생각만 해 주세요, 아버지!\""]], [["Still he went on in the same wild way, though it was gradually breaking down into a miserable whining.", "그래도 그는 여전히 그 사나운 투로 말을 이었으나, 그것은 차츰 비참한 흐느낌으로 무너져 갔다."]], [["‘And yet I have some respect here.", "\"그래도 나는 여기서 어느 정도 존경을 받고 있어."], ["I have made some stand against it.", "나는 그것에 어느 만큼 맞서 왔지."], ["I am not quite trodden down.", "나는 아주 짓밟히지는 않았어."], ["Go out and ask who is the chief person in the place.", "나가서 물어봐라, 이곳의 으뜸가는 사람이 누구냐고."], ["They’ll tell you it’s your father.", "네 아버지라고 할 게다."], ["Go out and ask who is never trifled with, and who is always treated with some delicacy.", "나가서 물어봐라, 누가 결코 함부로 대접받지 않고, 누가 늘 어느 정도 정중한 대접을 받느냐고."], ["They’ll say, your father.", "네 아버지라고 할 게다."], ["Go out and ask what funeral here (it must be here, I know it can be nowhere else) will make more talk, and perhaps more grief, than any that has ever gone out at the gate.", "나가서 물어봐라, 여기서 (여기여야지, 다른 데서는 있을 수 없는 일이란 걸 안다) 어떤 장례가 이 문을 나선 어떤 장례보다도 더 많은 이야깃거리가 되고, 어쩌면 더 많은 슬픔을 자아내겠느냐고."], ["They’ll say your father’s.", "네 아버지의 장례라고 할 게다."], ["Well then.", "자, 그렇다면."], ["Amy! Amy!", "에이미!"], ["Is your father so universally despised?", "에이미! 네 아버지가 그렇게 모두에게 경멸받는단 말이냐?"], ["Is there nothing to redeem him?", "그를 건져 줄 것이 아무것도 없단 말이냐?"], ["Will you have nothing to remember him by but his ruin and decay?", "너는 그의 파멸과 쇠락 말고는 그를 기억할 게 아무것도 없겠느냐?"], ["Will you be able to have no affection for him when he is gone, poor castaway, gone?’", "그가 가고 나면, 가엾이 버려진 그가 가고 나면, 너는 그에게 아무런 애정도 품을 수 없겠느냐?\""]], [["He burst into tears of maudlin pity for himself, and at length suffering her to embrace him and take charge of him, let his grey head rest against her cheek, and bewailed his wretchedness.", "그는 자기 자신을 향한 신파조의 연민에 겨워 눈물을 터뜨렸고, 마침내 그녀가 자기를 끌어안고 보살피게 내버려 둔 채, 회색 머리를 그녀의 뺨에 기대고 자기 비참함을 한탄했다."], ["Presently he changed the subject of his lamentations, and clasping his hands about her as she embraced him, cried, O Amy, his motherless, forlorn child!", "이윽고 그는 한탄의 화제를 바꾸어, 그녀가 안아 주는 동안 두 손으로 그녀를 끌어안고 외쳤다. 오, 에이미, 어미 없는 가엾은 내 자식!"], ["O the days that he had seen her careful and laborious for him!", "오, 그녀가 나를 위해 정성을 다하고 고생하는 것을 보아 온 그 세월이여!"], ["Then he reverted to himself, and weakly told her how much better she would have loved him if she had known him in his vanished character, and how he would have married her to a gentleman who should have been proud of her as his daughter, and how (at which he cried again) she should first have ridden at his fatherly side on her own horse, and how the crowd (by which he meant in effect the people who had given him the twelve shillings he then had in his pocket) should have trudged the dusty roads respectfully.", "그러다 그는 다시 자기 이야기로 돌아가, 네가 사라진 옛 시절의 나를 알았더라면 나를 훨씬 더 사랑했을 거라고, 또 너를 자기 딸로서 자랑스러워할 신사에게 시집보냈을 거라고, 또 (이 대목에서 그는 다시 울었다) 네가 네 말을 타고 아비인 내 곁에서 먼저 말을 달렸을 것이고, 또 군중이 (그가 사실상 가리킨 것은 그날 주머니에 든 십이 실링을 자기에게 준 그 사람들이었다) 먼지 이는 길을 정중하게 터덜터덜 걸어갔을 거라고, 힘없이 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Thus, now boasting, now despairing, in either fit a captive with the jail-rot upon him, and the impurity of his prison worn into the grain of his soul, he revealed his degenerate state to his affectionate child.", "이렇게, 이제는 으스대고 이제는 절망하며, 어느 쪽 발작에서나 감옥의 부패가 몸에 밴 죄수요, 감옥의 불결함이 영혼의 결까지 스며든 자가 되어, 그는 자기 타락한 처지를 그 다정한 자식에게 드러내 보였다."], ["No one else ever beheld him in the details of his humiliation.", "그가 굴욕의 세세한 모습을 다른 어느 누구에게도 보인 적은 없었다."], ["Little recked the Collegians who were laughing in their rooms over his late address in the Lodge, what a serious picture they had in their obscure gallery of the Marshalsea that Sunday night.", "그날 일요일 밤, 수위실에서 그가 한 연설을 두고 자기 방에서 웃어 대던 수용자들은, 자기들이 마셜시라는 그 어둑한 화랑 속에 얼마나 처연한 그림 한 점을 가지고 있는지 거의 알지 못했다."]], [["There was a classical daughter once--perhaps--who ministered to her father in his prison as her mother had ministered to her.", "옛적에 한 고전적인 딸이 있어 — 어쩌면 — 제 어미가 자기를 보살폈듯이 감옥에 갇힌 제 아비를 보살폈다고들 한다."], ["Little Dorrit, though of the unheroic modern stock and mere English, did much more, in comforting her father’s wasted heart upon her innocent breast, and turning to it a fountain of love and fidelity that never ran dry or waned through all his years of famine.", "리틀 도릿은, 비록 영웅적이지 않은 현대의 핏줄을 타고난 한낱 영국 처녀였으나, 그보다 훨씬 더한 일을 해냈으니, 아버지의 시든 마음을 제 순결한 가슴에 기대게 하여 위로하고, 그 모진 굶주림의 세월 내내 마르거나 시드는 법 없는 사랑과 정성의 샘물을 그 마음에 흘려보낸 것이었다."]], [["She soothed him; asked him for his forgiveness if she had been, or seemed to have been, undutiful; told him, Heaven knows truly, that she could not honour him more if he were the favourite of Fortune and the whole world acknowledged him.", "그녀는 그를 달랬다. 자기가 불효했거나 불효한 것처럼 보였다면 용서해 달라고 빌었다."], ["When his tears were dried, and he sobbed in his weakness no longer, and was free from that touch of shame, and had recovered his usual bearing, she prepared the remains of his supper afresh, and, sitting by his side, rejoiced to see him eat and drink.", "하늘이 아시듯 진실로, 설령 그가 운명의 총아여서 온 세상이 그를 떠받든다 한들 지금보다 더 그를 우러를 수는 없을 거라고 말했다. 그의 눈물이 마르고, 그가 더는 약하게 흐느끼지 않으며, 그 부끄러움의 기색에서 벗어나 평소의 태도를 되찾자, 그녀는 그가 남긴 저녁식사를 새로 차렸고, 그의 곁에 앉아 그가 먹고 마시는 것을 보며 기뻐했다."], ["For now he sat in his black velvet cap and old grey gown, magnanimous again; and would have comported himself towards any Collegian who might have looked in to ask his advice, like a great moral Lord Chesterfield, or Master of the ethical ceremonies of the Marshalsea.", "이제 그는 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 낡은 회색 가운을 걸친 채 다시금 도량 넓은 모습으로 앉아 있었으니, 누군가 수용자가 조언을 구하러 들렀더라면, 그는 위대한 도덕가 체스터필드 경처럼, 또는 마셜시의 예법을 관장하는 의전관처럼 처신했을 것이다."]], [["To keep his attention engaged, she talked with him about his wardrobe; when he was pleased to say, that Yes, indeed, those shirts she proposed would be exceedingly acceptable, for those he had were worn out, and, being ready-made, had never fitted him.", "그의 주의를 붙들어 두려고 그녀는 그와 옷가지 이야기를 나누었다. 그러자 그는 기꺼이 말하기를, 그래, 정말이지, 네가 말한 그 셔츠들이라면 더없이 반갑겠구나, 지금 것들은 다 닳은 데다 기성품이라 도통 몸에 맞은 적이 없으니 말이다, 했다."], ["Being conversational, and in a reasonable flow of spirits, he then invited her attention to his coat as it hung behind the door: remarking that the Father of the place would set an indifferent example to his children, already disposed to be slovenly, if he went among them out at elbows.", "이야기에 빠져들고 기분도 그런대로 풀린 그는, 이번엔 문 뒤에 걸린 자기 코트로 그녀의 주의를 돌리며 한마디 했으니, 이곳의 아버지가 팔꿈치가 다 해진 차림으로 자식들 틈을 다닌다면, 가뜩이나 추레해지기 쉬운 그 아이들에게 좋지 않은 본보기가 될 거라는 것이었다."], ["He was jocular, too, as to the heeling of his shoes; but became grave on the subject of his cravat, and promised her that, when she could afford it, she should buy him a new one.", "그는 자기 구두 뒤축을 두고도 농을 했으나, 넥타이 이야기에 이르러서는 진지해져서, 그녀에게 형편이 되거든 자기에게 새것을 하나 사 주라고 약속받았다."]], [["While he smoked out his cigar in peace, she made his bed, and put the small room in order for his repose.", "그가 편안히 시가를 다 피우는 동안, 그녀는 잠자리를 펴고 그가 쉴 수 있도록 작은 방을 정돈했다."], ["Being weary then, owing to the advanced hour and his emotions, he came out of his chair to bless her and wish her Good night.", "그러고 나서 늦은 시각과 격한 감정 탓에 지친 그는 의자에서 일어나, 그녀를 축복하고 잘 자라고 인사했다."], ["All this time he had never once thought of _her_ dress, her shoes, her need of anything.", "그러는 내내 그는 그녀의 옷이며, 그녀의 신발이며, 그녀가 무엇 하나 필요한 것이 있는지는 단 한 번도 생각하지 않았다."], ["No other person upon earth, save herself, could have been so unmindful of her wants.", "이 세상 누구도, 그녀 자신을 빼고는, 그토록 그녀의 궁핍에 무심할 수는 없었으리라."]], [["He kissed her many times with ‘Bless you, my love.", "그는 \"잘 자라, 내 사랑. 잘 자거라, 얘야!\""], ["Good night, my dear!’", "하며 여러 번 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["But her gentle breast had been so deeply wounded by what she had seen of him that she was unwilling to leave him alone, lest he should lament and despair again.", "그러나 그녀의 여린 가슴은 그가 보여 준 모습에 너무도 깊이 상처 입어, 그녀는 그를 혼자 두기가 꺼려졌으니, 그가 다시 한탄하고 절망할까 두려웠던 것이다."], ["‘Father, dear, I am not tired; let me come back presently, when you are in bed, and sit by you.’", "\"아버지, 저는 안 피곤해요. 아버지가 자리에 드시면 곧 다시 와서 곁에 앉아 있을게요.\""]], [["He asked her, with an air of protection, if she felt solitary?", "그는 보호자 같은 태도로, 혼자 있기가 적적하냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Yes, father.’", "\"네, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Then come back by all means, my love.’", "\"그러면 꼭 다시 오너라, 얘야.\""]], [["‘I shall be very quiet, father.’", "\"아주 조용히 있을게요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Don’t think of me, my dear,’ he said, giving her his kind permission fully.", "\"내 생각은 말거라, 얘야.\" 그는 너그러이 흔쾌히 허락하며 말했다."], ["‘Come back by all means.’", "\"꼭 다시 오너라.\""]], [["He seemed to be dozing when she returned, and she put the low fire together very softly lest she should awake him.", "그녀가 돌아왔을 때 그는 깜빡 졸고 있는 듯했고, 그녀는 그를 깨우지 않으려고 사위어 가는 불을 아주 조용히 추슬렀다."], ["But he overheard her, and called out who was that?", "그러나 그는 그 소리를 듣고는, 거기 누구냐고 소리쳤다."]], [["‘Only Amy, father.’", "\"에이미일 뿐이에요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Amy, my child, come here.", "\"에이미, 얘야, 이리 오너라."], ["I want to say a word to you.’", "너한테 할 말이 있다.\""]], [["He raised himself a little in his low bed, as she kneeled beside it to bring her face near him; and put his hand between hers. O!", "그녀가 그의 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 얼굴을 가까이 가져가자, 그는 낮은 침상에서 몸을 조금 일으켰고, 자기 손을 그녀의 두 손 사이에 두었다."], ["Both the private father and the Father of the Marshalsea were strong within him then.", "오! 그때 그의 안에서는 한 집안의 아버지와 마셜시의 아버지가 둘 다 강하게 자리하고 있었다."]], [["‘My love, you have had a life of hardship here.", "\"얘야, 너는 여기서 고생스러운 삶을 살아왔구나."], ["No companions, no recreations, many cares I am afraid?’", "벗도 없고, 즐길 거리도 없고, 근심만 많았겠지, 안 그러냐?\""]], [["‘Don’t think of that, dear.", "\"그런 건 생각지 마세요, 아버지."], ["I never do.’", "저는 한 번도 그런 생각 안 해요.\""]], [["‘You know my position, Amy.", "\"내 처지를 너도 알지, 에이미."], ["I have not been able to do much for you; but all I have been able to do, I have done.’", "나는 너를 위해 많은 걸 해 주지 못했다. 하지만 내가 할 수 있었던 건 다 했어.\""]], [["‘Yes, my dear father,’ she rejoined, kissing him.", "\"네, 가엾은 아버지.\" 그녀는 그에게 입맞추며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I know, I know.’", "\"알아요, 알아요.\""]], [["‘I am in the twenty-third year of my life here,’ he said, with a catch in his breath that was not so much a sob as an irrepressible sound of self-approval, the momentary outburst of a noble consciousness.", "\"내가 여기서 산 게 스물세 해째구나.\" 그는 숨을 한 번 멈칫했는데, 그것은 흐느낌이라기보다는 억누를 수 없는 자기만족의 소리, 고결한 의식이 순간적으로 터져 나온 것에 가까웠다."], ["‘It is all I could do for my children--I have done it.", "\"그게 내가 자식들을 위해 할 수 있었던 전부였고 — 나는 그걸 해냈다."], ["Amy, my love, you are by far the best loved of the three; I have had you principally in my mind--whatever I have done for your sake, my dear child, I have done freely and without murmuring.’", "에이미, 얘야, 너는 셋 중에서 단연 가장 사랑받는 아이야. 나는 누구보다 너를 마음에 두어 왔단다 — 너를 위해 내가 한 일이라면 무엇이든, 얘야, 나는 기꺼이, 군말 없이 해 온 거다.\""]], [["Only the wisdom that holds the clue to all hearts and all mysteries, can surely know to what extent a man, especially a man brought down as this man had been, can impose upon himself.", "모든 마음과 모든 신비의 단서를 쥐고 있는 그 지혜만이, 사람이 — 특히 이 사람처럼 영락한 사람이 — 어느 정도까지 자기 자신을 속일 수 있는지 분명히 알 것이다."], ["Enough, for the present place, that he lay down with wet eyelashes, serene, in a manner majestic, after bestowing his life of degradation as a sort of portion on the devoted child upon whom its miseries had fallen so heavily, and whose love alone had saved him to be even what he was.", "지금 이 자리에서는 이것으로 족하다. 그가 굴욕의 삶을, 그 비참함이 그토록 무겁게 떨어졌고 오직 그 사랑만이 그를 지금의 그로나마 살아남게 해 준, 그 헌신적인 자식에게 일종의 유산처럼 물려주고 나서, 젖은 속눈썹으로, 평온하게, 어딘지 위엄 있는 모습으로 자리에 누웠다는 것으로."]], [["That child had no doubts, asked herself no question, for she was but too content to see him with a lustre round his head.", "그 자식은 아무런 의심도 품지 않았고, 스스로에게 아무런 물음도 던지지 않았으니, 그의 머리 둘레에 후광이 어린 모습을 보는 것만으로도 너무나 만족스러웠기 때문이다."], ["Poor dear, good dear, truest, kindest, dearest, were the only words she had for him, as she hushed him to rest.", "가엾은 분, 착한 분, 더없이 진실하고 더없이 자상하고 더없이 사랑스러운 분, 이것이 그녀가 그를 재우며 그에게 건넬 수 있는 유일한 말이었다."]], [["She never left him all that night.", "그날 밤 내내 그녀는 그의 곁을 떠나지 않았다."], ["As if she had done him a wrong which her tenderness could hardly repair, she sat by him in his sleep, at times softly kissing him with suspended breath, and calling him in a whisper by some endearing name.", "마치 자기 다정함으로도 좀처럼 갚을 수 없는 잘못이라도 그에게 저지른 듯, 그녀는 그가 잠든 곁에 앉아, 이따금 숨을 죽인 채 살그머니 그에게 입맞추고, 다정한 애칭으로 속삭이며 그를 불렀다."], ["At times she stood aside so as not to intercept the low fire-light, and, watching him when it fell upon his sleeping face, wondered did he look now at all as he had looked when he was prosperous and happy; as he had so touched her by imagining that he might look once more in that awful time.", "이따금은 사위어 가는 난롯불을 가리지 않으려고 곁으로 비켜서서, 그 불빛이 그의 잠든 얼굴 위로 떨어질 때 그를 지켜보며, 그가 번성하고 행복하던 시절의 모습을, 그가 그 끔찍한 때에 다시 한번 그 모습이 되어 볼지도 모른다고 상상하며 그녀를 그토록 가슴 아프게 했던 그 모습을 지금 조금이라도 닮았는지 궁금해했다."], ["At the thought of that time, she kneeled beside his bed again, and prayed, ‘O spare his life!", "그 끔찍한 때를 생각하며 그녀는 다시 그의 침상 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 기도했다. \"오, 그의 목숨을 지켜 주소서!"], ["O save him to me!", "오, 그를 제 곁에 살려 주소서!"], ["O look down upon my dear, long-suffering, unfortunate, much-changed, dear dear father!’", "오, 사랑하는, 오래 고통받아 온, 불운하고, 너무도 달라져 버린, 사랑하고 또 사랑하는 제 아버지를 굽어살피소서!\""]], [["Not until the morning came to protect him and encourage him, did she give him a last kiss and leave the small room.", "그를 지켜 주고 북돋아 줄 아침이 오고서야 비로소, 그녀는 그에게 마지막 입맞춤을 하고 그 작은 방을 떠났다."], ["When she had stolen down-stairs, and along the empty yard, and had crept up to her own high garret, the smokeless housetops and the distant country hills were discernible over the wall in the clear morning.", "그녀가 살그머니 계단을 내려와 텅 빈 마당을 가로질러, 자기 높다란 다락방으로 살금살금 올라갔을 때, 맑은 아침 담장 너머로 연기 없는 지붕들과 멀리 시골의 언덕들이 보였다."], ["As she gently opened the window, and looked eastward down the prison yard, the spikes upon the wall were tipped with red, then made a sullen purple pattern on the sun as it came flaming up into the heavens.", "그녀가 가만히 창을 열고 동쪽으로 감옥 마당을 내려다보니, 담장 위 쇠꼬챙이들의 끝이 붉게 물들었다가, 이내 태양이 불꽃처럼 하늘로 솟아오를 때 그 위에 음울한 자줏빛 무늬를 드리웠다. 쇠꼬챙이가 그토록 날카롭고 잔인해 보인 적도, 창살이 그토록 무거워 보인 적도, 감옥의 공간이 그토록 음침하고 옹색해 보인 적도 없었다."], ["The spikes had never looked so sharp and cruel, nor the bars so heavy, nor the prison space so gloomy and contracted.", "그녀는 굽이치는 강 위로 떠오르는 해를, 드넓은 바다 위로 떠오르는 해를, 풍요로운 들판 위로 떠오르는 해를, 새들이 깨어나고 나무들이 사각이는 큰 숲 위로 떠오르는 해를 떠올렸다."], ["She thought of the sunrise on rolling rivers, of the sunrise on wide seas, of the sunrise on rich landscapes, of the sunrise on great forests where the birds were waking and the trees were rustling; and she looked down into the living grave on which the sun had risen, with her father in it three-and-twenty years, and said, in a burst of sorrow and compassion, ‘No, no, I have never seen him in my life!’", "그러고는, 자기 아버지가 스물세 해째 갇혀 있는, 해가 떠오른 그 산 무덤을 내려다보며, 슬픔과 연민이 북받쳐 이렇게 말했다. \"아니야, 아니야, 나는 평생 그분을 본 적이 없어!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 20. Moving in Society", "# CHAPTER 20. Moving in Society"]], [["If Young John Chivery had had the inclination and the power to write a satire on family pride, he would have had no need to go for an avenging illustration out of the family of his beloved.", "만약 젊은 존 치버리가 가문의 자긍심을 풍자할 마음과 능력이 있었더라면, 그는 보복의 본보기를 찾으러 사랑하는 여인의 집안 밖으로 나갈 필요가 전혀 없었을 것이다."], ["He would have found it amply in that gallant brother and that dainty sister, so steeped in mean experiences, and so loftily conscious of the family name; so ready to beg or borrow from the poorest, to eat of anybody’s bread, spend anybody’s money, drink from anybody’s cup and break it afterwards.", "그는 그것을 그 늠름한 오라비와 그 도도한 누이에게서 넘치도록 발견했을 테니까. 천박한 경험에 그토록 깊이 절어 있으면서도 가문의 이름만큼은 그토록 거만하게 의식하고, 가장 가난한 이에게서까지 구걸하거나 빌리고, 아무의 빵이든 먹고, 아무의 돈이든 쓰고, 아무의 잔에서든 마시고 나서 깨뜨려 버리는 데 그토록 거리낌이 없는 그들 말이다."], ["To have painted the sordid facts of their lives, and they throughout invoking the death’s head apparition of the family gentility to come and scare their benefactors, would have made Young John a satirist of the first water.", "그들의 삶의 비루한 사실들을 그려 내고, 그러면서도 그들이 내내 가문의 고귀함이라는 해골 유령을 불러내어 자기네 은인들을 겁주게 만드는 모습을 그렸더라면, 젊은 존은 일급의 풍자가가 되었을 것이다."]], [["Tip had turned his liberty to hopeful account by becoming a billiard-marker.", "팁은 당구장 점수원이 됨으로써 제 자유를 유망하게 써먹었다."], ["He had troubled himself so little as to the means of his release, that Clennam scarcely needed to have been at the pains of impressing the mind of Mr Plornish on that subject.", "그는 자신이 어떻게 풀려났는가 하는 문제에 너무도 무관심했으므로, 클레넘이 굳이 플로니시 씨의 머릿속에 그 문제를 새겨 넣느라 애쓸 필요조차 거의 없었다."], ["Whoever had paid him the compliment, he very readily accepted the compliment with _his_ compliments, and there was an end of it.", "누가 그에게 그런 호의를 베풀었든, 그는 그 호의를 자신의 인사와 함께 아주 선뜻 받아들였고, 그것으로 끝이었다."], ["Issuing forth from the gate on these easy terms, he became a billiard-marker; and now occasionally looked in at the little skittle-ground in a green Newmarket coat (second-hand), with a shining collar and bright buttons (new), and drank the beer of the Collegians.", "이렇게 손쉬운 조건으로 문을 나선 그는 당구장 점수원이 되었고, 이제는 이따금 (헌) 초록색 뉴마켓 코트에 (새) 번쩍이는 깃과 환한 단추를 달고서 작은 구주희 마당을 들여다보며, 수감자들의 맥주를 마셨다."]], [["One solid stationary point in the looseness of this gentleman’s character was, that he respected and admired his sister Amy.", "이 신사의 헐거운 성격 가운데 한 가지 단단하고 흔들림 없는 점은, 그가 누이 에이미를 존경하고 흠모한다는 것이었다."], ["The feeling had never induced him to spare her a moment’s uneasiness, or to put himself to any restraint or inconvenience on her account; but with that Marshalsea taint upon his love, he loved her.", "그 감정이 그로 하여금 그녀에게 단 한순간의 불편함이라도 덜어 주게 하거나, 그녀를 위해 무슨 자제나 불편을 감수하게 한 적은 결코 없었다. 그러나 그 사랑에 마셜시의 얼룩이 묻어 있긴 했어도, 그는 그녀를 사랑했다."], ["The same rank Marshalsea flavour was to be recognised in his distinctly perceiving that she sacrificed her life to her father, and in his having no idea that she had done anything for himself.", "그 똑같은 짙은 마셜시의 냄새는, 그가 그녀가 아버지에게 제 삶을 희생하고 있음을 또렷이 알아보면서도, 정작 그녀가 자기 자신을 위해 무언가 해 주었다는 생각은 전혀 하지 못한다는 점에서도 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["When this spirited young man and his sister had begun systematically to produce the family skeleton for the overawing of the College, this narrative cannot precisely state.", "이 기개 넘치는 젊은이와 그의 누이가 수감자들을 주눅 들게 하려고 가문의 해골을 체계적으로 끄집어내기 시작한 것이 언제부터인지, 이 이야기는 정확히 짚어 말할 수 없다."], ["Probably at about the period when they began to dine on the College charity.", "아마도 그들이 수감소의 자선으로 끼니를 때우기 시작한 무렵쯤일 것이다."], ["It is certain that the more reduced and necessitous they were, the more pompously the skeleton emerged from its tomb; and that when there was anything particularly shabby in the wind, the skeleton always came out with the ghastliest flourish.", "확실한 것은, 그들이 더 궁색하고 더 곤궁해질수록 그 해골이 무덤에서 더 거창하게 솟아 나왔다는 것, 그리고 무언가 특별히 초라한 일이 닥쳐올 낌새가 있을 때면 그 해골이 언제나 가장 섬뜩한 위세로 나타났다는 것이다."]], [["Little Dorrit was late on the Monday morning, for her father slept late, and afterwards there was his breakfast to prepare and his room to arrange.", "리틀 도릿은 월요일 아침에 늦었다. 아버지가 늦잠을 잤고, 그 뒤에는 아침 식사를 준비하고 방을 정돈해야 했기 때문이다."], ["She had no engagement to go out to work, however, and therefore stayed with him until, with Maggy’s help, she had put everything right about him, and had seen him off upon his morning walk (of twenty yards or so) to the coffee-house to read the paper.", "그러나 그날은 일하러 나갈 약속이 없었으므로, 매기의 도움을 받아 아버지에 관한 모든 것을 제대로 챙기고, 그가 신문을 읽으러 (스무 걸음쯤 되는) 아침 산책을 나서서 커피점으로 가는 것을 배웅할 때까지 그의 곁에 머물렀다."], ["She then got on her bonnet and went out, having been anxious to get out much sooner.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 보닛을 쓰고 밖으로 나섰는데, 사실은 훨씬 더 일찍 나가고 싶어 안달이 나 있던 터였다."], ["There was, as usual, a cessation of the small-talk in the Lodge as she passed through it; and a Collegian who had come in on Saturday night, received the intimation from the elbow of a more seasoned Collegian, ‘Look out.", "그녀가 수위실을 지날 때면 늘 그렇듯 잡담이 뚝 그쳤고, 토요일 밤에 들어온 어느 수감자는 더 노련한 수감자의 팔꿈치로부터 \"조심해. 그녀가 왔다네!\""], ["Here she is!’", "하는 귀띔을 받았다."]], [["She wanted to see her sister, but when she got round to Mr Cripples’s, she found that both her sister and her uncle had gone to the theatre where they were engaged.", "그녀는 언니를 만나고 싶었지만, 크리플스 씨네 집에 돌아 들어가 보니 언니와 삼촌이 둘 다 일하기로 되어 있는 극장에 가 있었다."], ["Having taken thought of this probability by the way, and having settled that in such case she would follow them, she set off afresh for the theatre, which was on that side of the river, and not very far away.", "오는 길에 이럴 가능성을 미리 생각해 두었고, 그런 경우라면 그들을 따라가겠다고 마음먹어 두었던지라, 그녀는 다시 극장을 향해 길을 나섰다. 극장은 강의 그쪽 편에 있었고 그리 멀지 않았다."]], [["Little Dorrit was almost as ignorant of the ways of theatres as of the ways of gold mines, and when she was directed to a furtive sort of door, with a curious up-all-night air about it, that appeared to be ashamed of itself and to be hiding in an alley, she hesitated to approach it; being further deterred by the sight of some half-dozen close-shaved gentlemen with their hats very strangely on, who were lounging about the door, looking not at all unlike Collegians.", "리틀 도릿은 금광에 관해 모르는 것만큼이나 극장이 돌아가는 방식에 대해서도 무지했다. 그래서 그녀가 어느 은밀한 문 쪽으로 안내받았을 때 — 묘하게도 밤새도록 잠들지 않는 듯한 분위기를 풍기며, 마치 제 모습을 부끄러워하여 골목에 숨어 있는 것처럼 보이는 그 문으로 — 그녀는 다가가기를 망설였다."], ["On her applying to them, reassured by this resemblance, for a direction to Miss Dorrit, they made way for her to enter a dark hall--it was more like a great grim lamp gone out than anything else--where she could hear the distant playing of music and the sound of dancing feet.", "게다가 모자를 아주 이상하게 쓴, 말끔히 면도한 신사 대여섯 명이 그 문 주위를 어슬렁거리고 있는 모습이 그녀를 더욱 머뭇거리게 했는데, 그들은 영락없이 수감자들처럼 보였다. 바로 그 닮은 점에 안심하여 그들에게 도릿 양이 있는 곳을 물었더니, 그들은 길을 비켜 주어 그녀가 어두운 복도로 들어가게 해 주었다."], ["A man so much in want of airing that he had a blue mould upon him, sat watching this dark place from a hole in a corner, like a spider; and he told her that he would send a message up to Miss Dorrit by the first lady or gentleman who went through.", "그곳은 무엇보다도 꺼져 버린 커다랗고 음울한 등불 속 같았는데, 거기서 그녀는 멀리서 들려오는 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리를 들을 수 있었다. 바람을 쐬어야 할 필요가 너무도 절실해 온몸에 푸른곰팡이가 핀 듯한 사내 하나가, 거미처럼 한쪽 구석의 구멍에서 그 어두운 곳을 지켜보고 앉아 있다가, 가장 먼저 지나가는 숙녀나 신사 편에 도릿 양에게 전갈을 올려 보내 주겠다고 말했다."], ["The first lady who went through had a roll of music, half in her muff and half out of it, and was in such a tumbled condition altogether, that it seemed as if it would be an act of kindness to iron her.", "가장 먼저 지나간 숙녀는 악보 두루마리를 토시 안에 반쯤 넣고 반쯤 내놓은 채였는데, 온몸이 어찌나 헝클어진 꼴이던지 그녀를 다림질해 주는 것이 친절한 일이 되겠다 싶을 정도였다. 그러나 그녀는 무척 마음씨가 좋아서 \"이리 와요, 내가 금방 도릿 양을 찾아 줄게요\""], ["But as she was very good-natured, and said, ‘Come with me; I’ll soon find Miss Dorrit for you,’ Miss Dorrit’s sister went with her, drawing nearer and nearer at every step she took in the darkness to the sound of music and the sound of dancing feet.", "하고 말했고, 도릿 양의 동생은 그녀를 따라갔다. 어둠 속에서 한 걸음 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리에 점점 더 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["At last they came into a maze of dust, where a quantity of people were tumbling over one another, and where there was such a confusion of unaccountable shapes of beams, bulkheads, brick walls, ropes, and rollers, and such a mixing of gaslight and daylight, that they seemed to have got on the wrong side of the pattern of the universe.", "마침내 그들은 먼지투성이의 미로 속으로 들어섰다. 그곳에는 수많은 사람들이 서로 부딪치며 넘어질 듯 뒤엉켜 있었고, 들보며 칸막이며 벽돌 벽이며 밧줄이며 도르래며, 도무지 형용할 수 없는 형체들이 어찌나 어지럽게 뒤섞여 있던지, 그리고 가스등 불빛과 햇빛이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽이던지, 마치 우주라는 무늬의 뒤편으로 잘못 들어선 것만 같았다."], ["Little Dorrit, left to herself, and knocked against by somebody every moment, was quite bewildered, when she heard her sister’s voice.", "혼자 남겨진 리틀 도릿은 시시각각 누군가에게 부딪히며 완전히 어리둥절해 있다가, 언니의 목소리를 들었다."]], [["‘Why, good gracious, Amy, what ever brought you here?’", "\"아니, 세상에, 에이미, 대체 무슨 일로 여기까지 온 거야?\""]], [["‘I wanted to see you, Fanny dear; and as I am going out all day to-morrow, and knew you might be engaged all day to-day, I thought--’", "\"언니를 보고 싶었어, 패니 언니. 내일은 하루 종일 일하러 나가야 하고, 언니는 오늘 하루 종일 일에 매여 있을지도 모른다는 걸 알았으니까, 그래서 생각했어 —\""]], [["‘But the idea, Amy, of _you_ coming behind!", "\"그렇지만 네가 무대 뒤로 들어올 생각을 하다니, 에이미! 정말 별일이네!\""], ["I never did!’ As her sister said this in no very cordial tone of welcome, she conducted her to a more open part of the maze, where various golden chairs and tables were heaped together, and where a number of young ladies were sitting on anything they could find, chattering.", "언니가 그다지 살가운 환영의 어조가 아니게 이렇게 말하면서, 그녀를 미로 가운데서도 좀 더 트인 곳으로 데려갔다. 그곳에는 갖가지 금빛 의자와 탁자가 한데 쌓여 있었고, 젊은 아가씨 여럿이 닥치는 대로 아무 데나 걸터앉아 재잘거리고 있었다."], ["All these young ladies wanted ironing, and all had a curious way of looking everywhere while they chattered.", "이 아가씨들은 하나같이 다림질이 필요해 보였고, 재잘거리면서도 하나같이 묘하게도 사방을 두리번거리는 버릇이 있었다."]], [["Just as the sisters arrived here, a monotonous boy in a Scotch cap put his head round a beam on the left, and said, ‘Less noise there, ladies!’ and disappeared.", "자매가 이곳에 막 다다랐을 때, 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 단조로운 표정의 소년 하나가 왼쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조용히 좀 하세요, 숙녀분들!\" 하고는 사라졌다."], ["Immediately after which, a sprightly gentleman with a quantity of long black hair looked round a beam on the right, and said, ‘Less noise there, darlings!’ and also disappeared.", "그 직후에, 긴 검은 머리를 잔뜩 늘어뜨린 활기찬 신사 하나가 오른쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조용히 좀 해요, 그대들!\" 하고는 역시 사라졌다."]], [["‘The notion of you among professionals, Amy, is really the last thing I could have conceived!’ said her sister.", "\"네가 이런 무대 사람들 틈에 끼어 있다는 발상은, 에이미, 정말이지 내가 도무지 상상조차 못 할 일이었어!\" 언니가 말했다."], ["‘Why, how did you ever get here?’", "\"대체 어떻게 여기까지 들어왔니?\""]], [["‘I don’t know.", "\"나도 몰라."], ["The lady who told you I was here, was so good as to bring me in.’", "언니한테 내가 여기 있다고 전해 준 그 숙녀분이 친절하게도 나를 데리고 들어와 줬어.\""]], [["‘Like you quiet little things!", "\"꼭 너희같이 조용하고 자그마한 것들이 그래!"], ["You can make your way anywhere, I believe.", "어디든 길을 찾아 들어가더라니까."], ["_I_ couldn’t have managed it, Amy, though I know so much more of the world.’", "나는 세상 물정을 훨씬 더 잘 아는데도, 에이미, 그렇게는 못 했을 거야.\""]], [["It was the family custom to lay it down as family law, that she was a plain domestic little creature, without the great and sage experience of the rest.", "그녀가 다른 식구들 같은 위대하고 현명한 경험은 없는, 그저 수수하고 살림이나 하는 자그마한 아이라고 못 박아 두는 것은 집안의 관례였고, 그것을 집안의 법도로 삼는 것이었다."], ["This family fiction was the family assertion of itself against her services.", "이 집안의 허구는, 그녀가 해 주는 봉사에 맞서 집안이 제 자존심을 내세우는 방식이었다."], ["Not to make too much of them.", "그 봉사를 너무 대수롭게 여기지 않으려는 것이었다."]], [["‘Well!", "\"자!"], ["And what have you got on your mind, Amy?", "그래 무슨 생각으로 온 거야, 에이미?"], ["Of course you have got something on your mind about me?’ said Fanny.", "당연히 나에 대해 무슨 할 말이 있어서 온 거겠지?\" 패니가 말했다."], ["She spoke as if her sister, between two and three years her junior, were her prejudiced grandmother.", "그녀는 자기보다 두세 살 어린 동생을 마치 편견에 찬 자기 할머니라도 되는 양 대고 말했다."]], [["‘It is not much; but since you told me of the lady who gave you the bracelet, Fanny--’", "\"별건 아니야. 그렇지만 언니가 그 팔찌를 준 숙녀분 얘기를 해 준 뒤로, 패니 언니 —\""]], [["The monotonous boy put his head round the beam on the left, and said, ‘Look out there, ladies!’ and disappeared.", "단조로운 소년이 왼쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조심하세요, 숙녀분들!\" 하고는 사라졌다."], ["The sprightly gentleman with the black hair as suddenly put his head round the beam on the right, and said, ‘Look out there, darlings!’ and also disappeared.", "검은 머리의 활기찬 신사가 그만큼이나 느닷없이 오른쪽 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"거기 조심해요, 그대들!\" 하고는 역시 사라졌다."], ["Thereupon all the young ladies rose and began shaking their skirts out behind.", "그러자 젊은 아가씨들이 모두 일어나 뒤로 치맛자락을 털어 펴기 시작했다."]], [["‘Well, Amy?’ said Fanny, doing as the rest did; ‘what were you going to say?’", "\"그래, 에이미?\" 패니가 다른 이들이 하는 대로 따라 하며 말했다. \"무슨 말을 하려던 거야?\""]], [["‘Since you told me a lady had given you the bracelet you showed me, Fanny, I have not been quite easy on your account, and indeed want to know a little more if you will confide more to me.’", "\"언니가 보여 준 그 팔찌를 어떤 숙녀분이 줬다고 한 뒤로, 패니 언니, 나는 언니 일이 영 마음이 놓이지 않아서, 정말이지 언니가 좀 더 털어놓아 준다면 조금 더 알고 싶어.\""]], [["‘Now, ladies!’ said the boy in the Scotch cap.", "\"자, 숙녀분들!\" 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 소년이 말했다."], ["‘Now, darlings!’ said the gentleman with the black hair.", "\"자, 그대들!\" 검은 머리의 신사가 말했다."], ["They were every one gone in a moment, and the music and the dancing feet were heard again.", "그들은 순식간에 한 사람도 남김없이 가 버렸고, 다시 음악 소리와 춤추는 발소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Little Dorrit sat down in a golden chair, made quite giddy by these rapid interruptions. Her sister and the rest were a long time gone; and during their absence a voice (it appeared to be that of the gentleman with the black hair) was continually calling out through the music, ‘One, two, three, four, five, six--go! One, two, three, four, five, six--go! Steady, darlings! One, two, three, four, five, six--go!’ Ultimately the voice stopped, and they all came back again, more or less out of breath, folding themselves in their shawls, and making ready for the streets. ‘Stop a moment, Amy, and let them get away before us,’ whispered Fanny. They were soon left alone; nothing more important happening, in the meantime, than the boy looking round his old beam, and saying, ‘Everybody at eleven to-morrow, ladies!’ and the gentleman with the black hair looking round his old beam, and saying, ‘Everybody at eleven to-morrow, darlings!’ each in his own accustomed manner.", "리틀 도릿은 금빛 의자에 주저앉았다. 이 잇따른 방해에 머리가 어질어질했다. 언니와 다른 이들은 오랫동안 가 있었다. 그들이 자리를 비운 동안, 한 목소리가 (검은 머리의 신사 목소리인 듯했다) 음악 사이로 끊임없이 외쳐 댔다. \"하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작! 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작! 침착하게, 그대들! 하나, 둘, 셋, 넷, 다섯, 여섯 — 시작!\" 마침내 그 목소리가 멎었고, 그들이 모두 숨이 차다는 듯 돌아와서는 숄을 두르고 거리로 나설 채비를 했다. \"잠깐만 있어, 에이미, 저 사람들이 우리보다 먼저 나가게.\" 패니가 속삭였다. 곧 그들만 남게 되었다. 그동안 더 중요한 일이라곤, 소년이 제 낡은 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"내일 다들 열한 시예요, 숙녀분들!\" 한 것과, 검은 머리의 신사가 제 낡은 들보 너머로 머리를 내밀고 \"내일 다들 열한 시야, 그대들!\" 하고, 저마다 제 버릇대로 말한 것 말고는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."]], [["When they were alone, something was rolled up or by other means got out of the way, and there was a great empty well before them, looking down into the depths of which Fanny said, ‘Now, uncle!’ Little Dorrit, as her eyes became used to the darkness, faintly made him out at the bottom of the well, in an obscure corner by himself, with his instrument in its ragged case under his arm.", "그들만 남게 되자, 무언가가 둘둘 말리거나 다른 방법으로 치워졌고, 그들 앞에 커다란 텅 빈 우물 같은 구덩이가 나타났다. 그 깊은 바닥을 내려다보며 패니가 말했다. \"자, 삼촌!\" 리틀 도릿은 눈이 어둠에 익숙해지면서, 그 우물 바닥 한쪽 어둑한 구석에 혼자 앉아 있는 삼촌의 모습을 어렴풋이 알아보았다. 너덜너덜한 악기 갑에 든 악기를 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다."]], [["The old man looked as if the remote high gallery windows, with their little strip of sky, might have been the point of his better fortunes, from which he had descended, until he had gradually sunk down below there to the bottom.", "노인은 마치 작은 하늘 한 조각이 비치는 저 멀리 높다란 회랑의 창문이 한때 그의 더 나은 운수의 정점이었는데, 거기서부터 차츰차츰 내려와 마침내 저 아래 바닥까지 가라앉아 버린 것만 같은 모습이었다."], ["He had been in that place six nights a week for many years, but had never been observed to raise his eyes above his music-book, and was confidently believed to have never seen a play.", "그는 여러 해 동안 일주일에 엿새씩 그 자리에 있어 왔지만, 악보집 위로 눈을 들어 올리는 모습은 한 번도 목격된 적이 없었고, 연극이라곤 한 번도 본 적이 없으리라고 다들 확신했다."], ["There were legends in the place that he did not so much as know the popular heroes and heroines by sight, and that the low comedian had ‘mugged’ at him in his richest manner fifty nights for a wager, and he had shown no trace of consciousness.", "그곳에는 그가 인기 있는 남녀 주인공의 얼굴조차 알아보지 못한다는 전설이 떠돌았고, 어느 저급 희극 배우가 내기를 걸고 쉰 번의 공연에서 그를 향해 가장 능청스러운 솜씨로 익살을 부려 댔는데도 그가 알아챘다는 기색을 도무지 보이지 않았다는 이야기도 있었다. 목수들 사이에는 그가 죽었으면서도 정작 자기가 죽은 줄 모른다는 농담이 있었고, 일반석 단골들은 그가 밤이고 낮이고, 일요일까지도 평생을 오케스트라석에서 보내는 줄로 여겼다."], ["The carpenters had a joke to the effect that he was dead without being aware of it; and the frequenters of the pit supposed him to pass his whole life, night and day, and Sunday and all, in the orchestra.", "그들은 몇 번인가 난간 너머로 코담배를 한 줌씩 권하며 그를 떠본 적이 있었는데, 그럴 때마다 그는 잠깐 정신이 깨어나는 듯한 태도로 이 관심에 응했고, 거기에는 신사의 창백한 유령 같은 분위기가 어려 있었다."], ["They had tried him a few times with pinches of snuff offered over the rails, and he had always responded to this attention with a momentary waking up of manner that had the pale phantom of a gentleman in it: beyond this he never, on any occasion, had any other part in what was going on than the part written out for the clarionet; in private life, where there was no part for the clarionet, he had no part at all.", "그러나 그 이상으로는, 그는 어떤 경우에도 무대 위에서 벌어지는 일에서 클라리넷을 위해 적혀 있는 부분 말고는 아무런 역할도 맡지 않았다. 사생활에서는, 클라리넷을 위한 역할이 없으니, 그는 아예 아무런 역할도 없었다."], ["Some said he was poor, some said he was a wealthy miser; but he said nothing, never lifted up his bowed head, never varied his shuffling gait by getting his springless foot from the ground.", "어떤 이들은 그가 가난하다 했고, 어떤 이들은 그가 부유한 구두쇠라 했지만, 그 자신은 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 숙인 고개를 들어 올린 적도 없었으며, 용수철 없는 발을 땅에서 떼어 올려 질질 끄는 걸음걸이를 바꾸는 일도 없었다. 지금도 조카에게 불려 가기를 기다리고 있었으면서, 그녀가 서너 번 말을 걸 때까지 듣지 못했다."], ["Though expecting now to be summoned by his niece, he did not hear her until she had spoken to him three or four times; nor was he at all surprised by the presence of two nieces instead of one, but merely said in his tremulous voice, ‘I am coming, I am coming!’ and crept forth by some underground way which emitted a cellarous smell.", "또 하나가 아니라 둘이나 되는 조카딸이 와 있는데도 그는 조금도 놀라지 않고, 그저 떨리는 목소리로 \"가마, 가마!\" 하고는, 지하실 같은 냄새를 풍기는 어떤 지하 통로를 통해 기어 나왔다."]], [["‘And so, Amy,’ said her sister, when the three together passed out at the door that had such a shame-faced consciousness of being different from other doors: the uncle instinctively taking Amy’s arm as the arm to be relied on: ‘so, Amy, you are curious about me?’", "\"그래, 에이미,\" 셋이 함께, 다른 문들과는 다르다는 것을 그토록 부끄럽게 의식하는 듯한 그 문을 나설 때 언니가 말했다. 삼촌은 본능적으로 에이미의 팔을, 믿고 의지할 만한 팔로서 잡았다. \"그래, 에이미, 너 내 일을 궁금해하는구나?\""]], [["She was pretty, and conscious, and rather flaunting; and the condescension with which she put aside the superiority of her charms, and of her worldly experience, and addressed her sister on almost equal terms, had a vast deal of the family in it.", "그녀는 예뻤고, 그것을 의식했으며, 다소 으스대고 있었다. 그리고 자신의 미모와 세상 경험이라는 우월함을 잠시 제쳐 두고 동생을 거의 대등한 사이인 양 대하는 그 겸양 속에는, 집안의 기질이 잔뜩 배어 있었다."]], [["‘I am interested, Fanny, and concerned in anything that concerns you.’", "\"나는 언니에게 관련된 일이라면 무엇에든 관심이 있고 마음이 쓰여, 패니 언니.\""]], [["‘So you are, so you are, and you are the best of Amys.", "\"그래, 그렇겠지, 그렇겠지, 너야말로 에이미 중에서도 제일가는 에이미니까."], ["If I am ever a little provoking, I am sure you’ll consider what a thing it is to occupy my position and feel a consciousness of being superior to it.", "내가 가끔 좀 짜증을 부리더라도, 내 처지에 놓여 있으면서 거기보다 내가 더 나은 사람이라는 의식을 갖는다는 게 어떤 건지 너는 분명 헤아려 줄 거야. 다른 애들이 그렇게 천하지만 않다면,\""], ["I shouldn’t care,’ said the Daughter of the Father of the Marshalsea, ‘if the others were not so common.", "마셜시 아버지의 딸이 말했다. \"나도 신경 쓰지 않을 거야."], ["None of them have come down in the world as we have.", "그 애들 중 누구도 우리처럼 영락한 처지가 아니거든."], ["They are all on their own level.", "그들은 다들 제 본래 위치에 그대로 있는 거지."], ["Common.’", "천한 거야.\""]], [["Little Dorrit mildly looked at the speaker, but did not interrupt her.", "리틀 도릿은 말하는 언니를 온화하게 바라보았지만, 말을 가로막지는 않았다."], ["Fanny took out her handkerchief, and rather angrily wiped her eyes.", "패니는 손수건을 꺼내 다소 화가 난 듯 눈가를 닦았다."], ["‘I was not born where you were, you know, Amy, and perhaps that makes a difference.", "\"나는 네가 태어난 곳에서 태어나지 않았잖아, 에이미, 알다시피. 어쩌면 그게 차이를 만드는 건지도 몰라."], ["My dear child, when we get rid of Uncle, you shall know all about it.", "우리 사랑하는 동생아, 삼촌을 떼어 놓고 나면 그 일을 다 말해 줄게."], ["We’ll drop him at the cook’s shop where he is going to dine.’", "삼촌은 저녁을 먹으러 가는 음식점에 내려 드리자.\""]], [["They walked on with him until they came to a dirty shop window in a dirty street, which was made almost opaque by the steam of hot meats, vegetables, and puddings.", "그들은 삼촌과 함께 걸어, 더러운 거리에 있는 어느 더러운 가게 창문 앞에 이르렀다. 그 창문은 뜨거운 고기와 채소와 푸딩에서 나는 김으로 거의 불투명해져 있었다."], ["But glimpses were to be caught of a roast leg of pork bursting into tears of sage and onion in a metal reservoir full of gravy, of an unctuous piece of roast beef and blisterous Yorkshire pudding, bubbling hot in a similar receptacle, of a stuffed fillet of veal in rapid cut, of a ham in a perspiration with the pace it was going at, of a shallow tank of baked potatoes glued together by their own richness, of a truss or two of boiled greens, and other substantial delicacies.", "그래도 언뜻언뜻 보이는 것들이 있었다. 육즙이 가득한 금속 통 안에서 세이지와 양파의 눈물을 터뜨리는 구운 돼지 다리, 비슷한 그릇 속에서 부글부글 뜨겁게 끓는 기름진 로스트비프와 부풀어 물집투성이가 된 요크셔푸딩, 빠르게 잘려 나가는 송아지고기 속박이 살, 가는 속도가 어찌나 빠른지 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리는 햄, 제 기름기로 서로 들러붙은 구운 감자가 담긴 얕은 통, 한두 단의 삶은 푸성귀, 그리고 그 밖의 든든한 별미들."], ["Within, were a few wooden partitions, behind which such customers as found it more convenient to take away their dinners in stomachs than in their hands, Packed their purchases in solitude.", "안쪽에는 나무 칸막이가 몇 개 있었고, 그 뒤에서는 저녁거리를 손에 들고 가기보다 뱃속에 담아 가는 편이 더 편하다고 여기는 손님들이 호젓하게 자기가 산 것을 챙겨 넣고 있었다."], ["Fanny opening her reticule, as they surveyed these things, produced from that repository a shilling and handed it to Uncle.", "그것들을 둘러보는 동안 패니가 그물 손지갑을 열어 그 안에서 1실링을 꺼내 삼촌에게 건넸다."], ["Uncle, after not looking at it a little while, divined its object, and muttering ‘Dinner?", "삼촌은 한참을 그것을 쳐다보지도 않다가, 그 용도를 알아채고는 \"저녁?"], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Yes, yes, yes!’ slowly vanished from them into the mist.", "그래, 그래, 그래!\" 하고 중얼거리며 천천히 안개 속으로 그들에게서 사라져 갔다."]], [["‘Now, Amy,’ said her sister, ‘come with me, if you are not too tired to walk to Harley Street, Cavendish Square.’", "\"자, 에이미,\" 언니가 말했다. \"캐번디시 광장의 할리 가까지 걸어갈 만큼 지치지 않았다면, 나랑 같이 가자.\""]], [["The air with which she threw off this distinguished address and the toss she gave to her new bonnet (which was more gauzy than serviceable), made her sister wonder; however, she expressed her readiness to go to Harley Street, and thither they directed their steps.", "그녀가 이 고상한 주소를 툭 내던지듯 말하는 태도하며, (실용적이라기보다는 얇디얇은) 새 보닛을 휙 젖히는 몸짓은 동생을 의아하게 만들었다. 그러나 동생은 할리 가에 갈 의향이 있음을 밝혔고, 그들은 그쪽으로 발걸음을 옮겼다."], ["Arrived at that grand destination, Fanny singled out the handsomest house, and knocking at the door, inquired for Mrs Merdle.", "그 으리으리한 목적지에 다다르자, 패니는 가장 멋진 집을 골라내어 문을 두드리고는 머들 부인을 찾았다."], ["The footman who opened the door, although he had powder on his head and was backed up by two other footmen likewise powdered, not only admitted Mrs Merdle to be at home, but asked Fanny to walk in.", "문을 열어 준 하인은 머리에 분을 발랐고, 역시 분을 바른 다른 두 하인의 뒷받침을 받고 있었는데, 머들 부인이 댁에 계시다고 인정했을 뿐 아니라 패니에게 안으로 들어오시라고 청했다. 패니는 동생을 데리고 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Fanny walked in, taking her sister with her; and they went up-stairs with powder going before and powder stopping behind, and were left in a spacious semicircular drawing-room, one of several drawing-rooms, where there was a parrot on the outside of a golden cage holding on by its beak, with its scaly legs in the air, and putting itself into many strange upside-down postures.", "그들은 분을 바른 하인이 앞서고 분을 바른 하인이 뒤에 멈춰 선 가운데 위층으로 올라가, 여러 응접실 가운데 하나인 넓은 반원형 응접실에 안내되어 남겨졌다. 그곳에는 금빛 새장 바깥에 매달린 앵무새 한 마리가 부리로 몸을 지탱한 채 비늘 돋친 다리를 공중으로 쳐들고 거꾸로 매달리는 갖가지 괴상한 자세를 취하고 있었다."], ["This peculiarity has been observed in birds of quite another feather, climbing upon golden wires.", "이런 특이한 버릇은 금빛 철사를 기어오르는, 깃털이 전혀 다른 새들에게서도 목격된 바 있다."]], [["The room was far more splendid than anything Little Dorrit had ever imagined, and would have been splendid and costly in any eyes.", "그 방은 리틀 도릿이 여태껏 상상해 본 그 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 더 화려했고, 누구의 눈에라도 화려하고 값져 보였을 것이다."], ["She looked in amazement at her sister and would have asked a question, but that Fanny with a warning frown pointed to a curtained doorway of communication with another room.", "그녀는 놀라워하며 언니를 바라보았고 무언가 물어보려 했지만, 패니가 경고하듯 눈살을 찌푸리며 다른 방으로 통하는 커튼 친 출입구를 가리켰다."], ["The curtain shook next moment, and a lady, raising it with a heavily ringed hand, dropped it behind her again as she entered.", "다음 순간 커튼이 흔들리더니, 한 부인이 반지를 잔뜩 낀 손으로 그것을 걷어 올리고 들어서면서 다시 등 뒤로 떨구었다."]], [["The lady was not young and fresh from the hand of Nature, but was young and fresh from the hand of her maid.", "그 부인은 자연의 손에서 갓 나온 듯 젊고 싱싱한 것이 아니라, 하녀의 손에서 갓 나온 듯 젊고 싱싱했다."], ["She had large unfeeling handsome eyes, and dark unfeeling handsome hair, and a broad unfeeling handsome bosom, and was made the most of in every particular.", "그녀는 무감각한 아름다운 큰 눈과, 무감각한 아름다운 검은 머리카락과, 무감각한 아름다운 넓은 가슴을 지녔고, 어느 한 군데도 최대한으로 돋보이게 꾸며져 있었다."], ["Either because she had a cold, or because it suited her face, she wore a rich white fillet tied over her head and under her chin.", "감기가 들었기 때문인지, 아니면 그것이 얼굴에 어울리기 때문인지, 그녀는 머리 위로 둘러 턱 밑에서 묶은 화려한 흰 머리띠를 두르고 있었다."], ["And if ever there were an unfeeling handsome chin that looked as if, for certain, it had never been, in familiar parlance, ‘chucked’ by the hand of man, it was the chin curbed up so tight and close by that laced bridle.", "그리고 이 세상에, 흔한 말로 사내의 손에 한 번도 '쓰다듬어진' 적이 없는 게 분명해 보이는 무감각한 아름다운 턱이 있다면, 바로 그 끈 달린 굴레로 그토록 빳빳하고 바투 죄어진 턱이었다."]], [["‘Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny.", "\"머들 부인이세요.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘My sister, ma’am.’", "\"제 동생이에요, 부인.\""]], [["‘I am glad to see your sister, Miss Dorrit.", "\"동생을 만나게 되어 반가워요, 도릿 양."], ["I did not remember that you had a sister.’", "동생이 있는 줄은 몰랐네요.\""]], [["‘I did not mention that I had,’ said Fanny.", "\"있다고 말씀드린 적이 없으니까요.\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ Mrs Merdle curled the little finger of her left hand as who should say, ‘I have caught you. I know you didn’t!’ All her action was usually with her left hand because her hands were not a pair; and left being much the whiter and plumper of the two. Then she added: ‘Sit down,’ and composed herself voluptuously, in a nest of crimson and gold cushions, on an ottoman near the parrot.", "\"아!\" 머들 부인은 마치 \"내가 너를 잡았구나. 네가 말 안 했다는 거 다 안다!\" 하고 말하려는 듯 왼손 새끼손가락을 둥글게 구부렸다. 그녀의 모든 동작은 보통 왼손으로 이루어졌는데, 두 손이 짝이 맞지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 왼손이 둘 중에 훨씬 더 희고 통통했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 \"앉아요\" 하고 덧붙이고는, 앵무새 가까이에 놓인 오토만 위, 진홍빛과 금빛 방석의 둥지 속에 관능적으로 몸을 파묻었다."]], [["‘Also professional?’ said Mrs Merdle, looking at Little Dorrit through an eye-glass.", "\"이쪽도 무대 사람인가요?\" 머들 부인이 외알 안경 너머로 리틀 도릿을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Fanny answered No.", "패니가 아니라고 대답했다."], ["‘No,’ said Mrs Merdle, dropping her glass.", "\"아니로군요.\" 머들 부인이 안경을 떨구며 말했다."], ["‘Has not a professional air.", "\"무대 사람 분위기가 아니네요."], ["Very pleasant; but not professional.’", "아주 상냥해 보여요. 하지만 무대 사람은 아니에요.\""]], [["‘My sister, ma’am,’ said Fanny, in whom there was a singular mixture of deference and hardihood, ‘has been asking me to tell her, as between sisters, how I came to have the honour of knowing you.", "\"제 동생이,\" 묘하게도 공손함과 대담함이 뒤섞인 패니가 말했다."], ["And as I had engaged to call upon you once more, I thought I might take the liberty of bringing her with me, when perhaps you would tell her.", "\"자매지간이니 제가 어떻게 부인을 알게 되는 영광을 얻었는지 말해 달라고 하더군요. 그리고 제가 부인을 한 번 더 찾아뵙기로 약속했던 터라, 외람되게나마 동생을 데려와도 되겠다 싶었어요."], ["I wish her to know, and perhaps you will tell her?’", "어쩌면 부인께서 동생에게 말씀해 주실 수도 있겠다고요. 동생이 알았으면 좋겠는데, 부인께서 말씀해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Do you think, at your sister’s age--’ hinted Mrs Merdle.", "\"동생분 나이에 그런 이야기를 —\" 머들 부인이 넌지시 말을 비쳤다."]], [["‘She is much older than she looks,’ said Fanny; ‘almost as old as I am.’", "\"이 애는 보기보다 훨씬 나이가 많아요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"저랑 거의 동갑이나 마찬가지예요.\""]], [["‘Society,’ said Mrs Merdle, with another curve of her little finger, ‘is so difficult to explain to young persons (indeed is so difficult to explain to most persons), that I am glad to hear that.", "\"사교계라는 건,\" 머들 부인이 새끼손가락을 또 한 번 둥글게 구부리며 말했다."], ["I wish Society was not so arbitrary, I wish it was not so exacting--Bird, be quiet!’", "\"젊은 사람들에게 설명하기가 어찌나 어려운지 (사실 대부분의 사람들에게 설명하기가 어찌나 어려운지), 그 말을 들으니 다행이네요. 사교계가 그렇게 제멋대로가 아니었으면, 그렇게 까다롭지 않았으면 좋으련만 — 새야, 조용히 해!\""]], [["The parrot had given a most piercing shriek, as if its name were Society and it asserted its right to its exactions.", "앵무새가 마치 제 이름이 사교계이기라도 한 듯, 또 제 까다로움을 부릴 권리를 주장하기라도 하듯, 더없이 날카로운 비명을 질러 댔던 것이다."]], [["‘But,’ resumed Mrs Merdle, ‘we must take it as we find it.", "\"하지만,\" 머들 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["We know it is hollow and conventional and worldly and very shocking, but unless we are Savages in the Tropical seas (I should have been charmed to be one myself--most delightful life and perfect climate, I am told), we must consult it.", "\"우리는 그것을 있는 그대로 받아들여야 해요. 그것이 공허하고 인습적이고 속물적이며 무척이나 충격적이라는 걸 우리도 알지만, 우리가 열대 바다의 미개인이 아닌 이상에야 (나 자신은 미개인이 되는 것도 무척 매력적이었겠다 싶어요 — 더없이 즐거운 삶에 완벽한 기후라고들 하더군요), 우리는 그것을 따라야만 해요."], ["It is the common lot.", "그게 다들 짊어진 운명이지요."], ["Mr Merdle is a most extensive merchant, his transactions are on the vastest scale, his wealth and influence are very great, but even he--Bird, be quiet!’", "머들 씨는 더없이 손이 넓은 상인이고, 그분의 거래는 가장 방대한 규모에 이르며, 그분의 재산과 영향력은 대단하지만, 그분조차도 — 새야, 조용히 해!\""]], [["The parrot had shrieked another shriek; and it filled up the sentence so expressively that Mrs Merdle was under no necessity to end it.", "앵무새가 또 한 번 비명을 질렀던 것이다. 그것이 어찌나 함축적으로 문장을 채워 주었던지, 머들 부인은 굳이 말을 맺을 필요가 없었다."]], [["‘Since your sister begs that I would terminate our personal acquaintance,’ she began again, addressing Little Dorrit, ‘by relating the circumstances that are much to her credit, I cannot object to comply with her request, I am sure. I have a son (I was first married extremely young) of two or three-and-twenty.’", "\"동생분이 우리의 사적인 친분을 끝맺기를 청하니,\" 그녀가 리틀 도릿을 향해 다시 입을 열었다. \"동생분에게 무척 명예로운 그 사정을 들려 드림으로써 그렇게 하지요. 그 청에 응하는 것을 마다할 까닭이야 없고말고요. 내게는 스물두셋쯤 된 아들이 하나 있어요 (나는 무척 어린 나이에 첫 결혼을 했거든요).\""]], [["Fanny set her lips, and her eyes looked half triumphantly at her sister.", "패니가 입술을 꼭 다물었고, 그녀의 눈은 반쯤 의기양양한 빛으로 동생을 바라보았다."]], [["‘A son of two or three-and-twenty.", "\"스물두셋쯤 된 아들이지요."], ["He is a little gay, a thing Society is accustomed to in young men, and he is very impressible.", "그 애는 좀 들떠 있는데, 그건 사교계가 젊은이들에게서 흔히 보아 넘기는 거고, 또 그 애는 정에 무척 잘 휩쓸려요."], ["Perhaps he inherits that misfortune.", "어쩌면 그 불운을 물려받은 건지도 모르지요."], ["I am very impressible myself, by nature.", "나 자신이 천성적으로 정에 아주 잘 휩쓸리거든요."], ["The weakest of creatures--my feelings are touched in a moment.’", "가장 여린 사람이라 — 내 감정은 순식간에 흔들려 버려요.\""]], [["She said all this, and everything else, as coldly as a woman of snow; quite forgetting the sisters except at odd times, and apparently addressing some abstraction of Society; for whose behoof, too, she occasionally arranged her dress, or the composition of her figure upon the ottoman.", "그녀는 이 말도, 그 밖의 모든 말도, 눈사람 같은 여인처럼 차갑게 내뱉었다. 이따금씩을 빼고는 자매를 완전히 잊어버린 채, 겉보기에는 어떤 추상적인 사교계에다 대고 말하는 듯했다. 또한 바로 그 사교계를 위하여, 그녀는 가끔씩 옷매무새를 다듬거나 오토만 위에 놓인 제 몸의 자태를 매만지곤 했다."]], [["‘So he is very impressible.", "\"그래서 그 애는 정에 아주 잘 휩쓸려요."], ["Not a misfortune in our natural state I dare say, but we are not in a natural state.", "우리가 자연 그대로의 상태에 있다면 그건 불운이 아니겠지만, 우리는 자연 그대로의 상태에 있지 않으니까요."], ["Much to be lamented, no doubt, particularly by myself, who am a child of nature if I could but show it; but so it is.", "의심할 바 없이 무척 한탄스러운 일이지요, 특히 나처럼 본디 자연의 아이인데도 그것을 드러내 보일 수 없는 사람에게는요. 하지만 사정이 그래요."], ["Society suppresses us and dominates us--Bird, be quiet!’", "사교계가 우리를 억누르고 지배하니까요 — 새야, 조용히 해!\""]], [["The parrot had broken into a violent fit of laughter, after twisting divers bars of his cage with his crooked bill, and licking them with his black tongue.", "앵무새가 구부러진 부리로 새장 창살을 여러 개 비틀어 대고 검은 혀로 핥아 대더니, 격렬한 웃음을 터뜨렸던 것이다."]], [["‘It is quite unnecessary to say to a person of your good sense, wide range of experience, and cultivated feeling,’ said Mrs Merdle from her nest of crimson and gold--and there put up her glass to refresh her memory as to whom she was addressing,--‘that the stage sometimes has a fascination for young men of that class of character.", "\"양식이 풍부하고, 경험의 폭이 넓으며, 감정이 세련된 분께 굳이 말씀드릴 필요도 없겠지만,\" 머들 부인이 진홍빛과 금빛 둥지에서 말하며 — 그러고는 자신이 누구에게 말하고 있는지 기억을 되살리려고 안경을 들어 올렸다."], ["In saying the stage, I mean the people on it of the female sex.", "\"그런 부류의 성격을 가진 젊은이들에게는 무대가 때로 매혹으로 다가오지요. 무대라고 하면, 그 위에 선 여자들을 두고 하는 말이에요."], ["Therefore, when I heard that my son was supposed to be fascinated by a dancer, I knew what that usually meant in Society, and confided in her being a dancer at the Opera, where young men moving in Society are usually fascinated.’", "그러니 내 아들이 어떤 무용수에게 홀렸다는 소문을 들었을 때, 나는 그게 사교계에서 흔히 무엇을 뜻하는지 알았고, 그 여자가 오페라 극장의 무용수이리라 믿었어요. 사교계에서 움직이는 젊은이들이 으레 홀리는 곳이 거기니까요.\""]], [["She passed her white hands over one another, observant of the sisters now; and the rings upon her fingers grated against each other with a hard sound.", "그녀는 이제 자매를 유심히 살피며 하얀 두 손을 맞비볐다. 그러자 손가락의 반지들이 단단한 소리를 내며 서로 부딪혔다."]], [["‘As your sister will tell you, when I found what the theatre was I was much surprised and much distressed.", "\"동생분에게 들으면 알겠지만, 그곳이 무슨 극장인지 알게 되었을 때 나는 무척 놀랐고 무척 괴로웠어요."], ["But when I found that your sister, by rejecting my son’s advances (I must add, in an unexpected manner), had brought him to the point of proposing marriage, my feelings were of the profoundest anguish--acute.’", "그러나 동생분이 (덧붙이자면, 뜻밖의 방식으로) 내 아들의 구애를 물리침으로써 그 애를 청혼하겠다는 지경까지 몰고 갔다는 걸 알게 되자, 내 심정은 더없이 깊은 고뇌에 빠졌지요 — 절절한 고뇌에요.\""]], [["She traced the outline of her left eyebrow, and put it right.", "그녀는 왼쪽 눈썹의 윤곽을 따라 매만지며 매무새를 바로잡았다."]], [["‘In a distracted condition, which only a mother--moving in Society--can be susceptible of, I determined to go myself to the theatre, and represent my state of mind to the dancer.", "\"오로지 사교계에서 움직이는 어머니만이 느낄 수 있는 그 심란한 심정으로, 나는 직접 극장에 가서 그 무용수에게 내 마음 상태를 호소하기로 작정했어요."], ["I made myself known to your sister.", "나는 동생분에게 내 신분을 밝혔지요."], ["I found her, to my surprise, in many respects different from my expectations; and certainly in none more so, than in meeting me with--what shall I say--a sort of family assertion on her own part?’ Mrs Merdle smiled.", "놀랍게도 나는 그분이 여러 면에서 내 예상과 다르다는 걸 알게 되었고, 그중에서도 분명코 가장 다른 점은, 나를 — 뭐라고 해야 할까요 — 자기 나름의 일종의 가문 자긍심으로 맞이했다는 거였어요.\" 머들 부인이 미소 지었다."]], [["‘I told you, ma’am,’ said Fanny, with a heightening colour, ‘that although you found me in that situation, I was so far above the rest, that I considered my family as good as your son’s; and that I had a brother who, knowing the circumstances, would be of the same opinion, and would not consider such a connection any honour.’", "\"제가 말씀드렸잖아요, 부인,\" 패니가 낯빛을 붉히며 말했다. \"비록 부인께서 저를 그런 처지에서 발견하셨지만, 저는 다른 이들보다 훨씬 위에 있는 사람이라, 제 가문이 부인 아드님 가문에 못지않다고 여긴다고요. 그리고 제게는 사정을 알면 같은 생각을 할, 그런 인연을 도무지 명예로 여기지 않을 오라비가 하나 있다고요.\""]], [["‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Mrs Merdle, after frostily looking at her through her glass, ‘precisely what I was on the point of telling your sister, in pursuance of your request. Much obliged to you for recalling it so accurately and anticipating me. I immediately,’ addressing Little Dorrit, ‘(for I am the creature of impulse), took a bracelet from my arm, and begged your sister to let me clasp it on hers, in token of the delight I had in our being able to approach the subject so far on a common footing.’ (This was perfectly true, the lady having bought a cheap and showy article on her way to the interview, with a general eye to bribery.)", "\"도릿 양,\" 머들 부인이 안경 너머로 그녀를 쌀쌀맞게 바라본 뒤 말했다. \"당신 청에 따라 동생분에게 막 이야기하려던 게 바로 그거였어요. 그렇게 정확히 떠올려서 내 말을 앞질러 주니 무척 고맙군요. 나는 즉시 (나는 충동의 존재거든요),\" 리틀 도릿을 향해 말하며, \"내 팔에서 팔찌를 풀어, 우리가 이 문제를 이만큼이나 공통의 발판 위에서 다룰 수 있게 된 데 대한 기쁨의 표시로, 그것을 동생분의 팔에 채우게 해 달라고 청했어요.\" (이것은 더없이 사실이었다. 그 부인은 면담하러 오는 길에, 전반적으로 매수를 염두에 두고서, 값싸고 번지르르한 물건을 하나 사 두었던 것이다.)"]], [["‘And I told you, Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny, ‘that we might be unfortunate, but we are not common.’", "\"그래서 제가 말씀드렸어요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 말했다. \"우리가 불운할 수는 있어도, 천하지는 않다고요.\""]], [["‘I think, the very words, Miss Dorrit,’ assented Mrs Merdle.", "\"바로 그 말이었던 것 같네요, 도릿 양.\" 머들 부인이 동의했다."]], [["‘And I told you, Mrs Merdle,’ said Fanny, ‘that if you spoke to me of the superiority of your son’s standing in Society, it was barely possible that you rather deceived yourself in your suppositions about my origin; and that my father’s standing, even in the Society in which he now moved (what that was, was best known to myself), was eminently superior, and was acknowledged by every one.’", "\"그리고 제가 말씀드렸어요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 말했다. \"부인께서 제게 아드님이 사교계에서 차지하는 지위의 우월함을 들먹이신다면, 부인께서 제 출신에 대한 짐작에서 다소 스스로를 속이고 계실 가능성도 충분하다고요. 그리고 제 아버지의 지위는, 지금 그분이 움직이는 사교계에서조차 (그게 어떤 곳인지는 제가 가장 잘 알지만요) 두드러지게 우월하며, 누구에게나 인정받고 있다고요.\""]], [["‘Quite accurate,’ rejoined Mrs Merdle.", "\"아주 정확해요.\" 머들 부인이 응수했다."], ["‘A most admirable memory.’", "\"더없이 훌륭한 기억력이군요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, ma’am.", "\"고맙습니다, 부인."], ["Perhaps you will be so kind as to tell my sister the rest.’", "나머지 이야기는 부인께서 친절히 제 동생에게 들려주시지요.\""]], [["‘There is very little to tell,’ said Mrs Merdle, reviewing the breadth of bosom which seemed essential to her having room enough to be unfeeling in, ‘but it is to your sister’s credit.", "\"더 들려줄 건 거의 없어요.\" 머들 부인이, 무감각하게 있기에 충분한 공간을 확보하는 데 꼭 필요한 듯 보이는 그 넓은 가슴을 한번 굽어보며 말했다."], ["I pointed out to your sister the plain state of the case; the impossibility of the Society in which we moved recognising the Society in which she moved--though charming, I have no doubt; the immense disadvantage at which she would consequently place the family she had so high an opinion of, upon which we should find ourselves compelled to look down with contempt, and from which (socially speaking) we should feel obliged to recoil with abhorrence.", "\"하지만 그건 동생분에게 명예로운 일이지요. 나는 동생분에게 그 일의 명백한 사정을 짚어 드렸어요."], ["In short, I made an appeal to that laudable pride in your sister.’", "우리가 움직이는 사교계가, 동생분이 움직이는 사교계를 — 의심할 바 없이 매력적이긴 하겠지만 — 인정한다는 것은 불가능하다는 것, 따라서 동생분이 그토록 높이 평가하는 그 가문을, 우리로서는 어쩔 수 없이 경멸하며 내려다볼 수밖에 없고 (사교적으로 말하자면) 혐오감을 품고 물러설 수밖에 없는 그 처지에, 동생분이 엄청나게 불리하게 몰아넣게 되리라는 것을요. 요컨대 나는 동생분의 그 가상한 자긍심에 호소했던 거예요.\""]], [["‘Let my sister know, if you please, Mrs Merdle,’ Fanny pouted, with a toss of her gauzy bonnet, ‘that I had already had the honour of telling your son that I wished to have nothing whatever to say to him.’", "\"부디 제 동생에게 알려 주세요, 머들 부인,\" 패니가 얇은 보닛을 휙 젖히며 입을 삐죽였다. \"저는 이미 아드님께 당신과는 무슨 할 말도 없다고 말씀드리는 영광을 누렸다는 걸요.\""]], [["‘Well, Miss Dorrit,’ assented Mrs Merdle, ‘perhaps I might have mentioned that before.", "\"음, 도릿 양,\" 머들 부인이 동의했다."], ["If I did not think of it, perhaps it was because my mind reverted to the apprehensions I had at the time that he might persevere and you might have something to say to him.", "\"어쩌면 그 얘기를 먼저 했어야 했는지도 모르겠네요. 그걸 떠올리지 못했다면, 아마 그건 그 애가 끈질기게 매달리고 당신은 그 애에게 할 말이 생길지도 모른다는, 당시의 그 우려로 내 마음이 되돌아갔기 때문일 거예요."], ["I also mentioned to your sister--I again address the non-professional Miss Dorrit--that my son would have nothing in the event of such a marriage, and would be an absolute beggar.", "나는 또 동생분에게도 말했지요 — 다시 무대 사람이 아닌 도릿 양께 말씀드리는데 — 그런 결혼을 하게 되면 내 아들은 한 푼도 받지 못하고 완전한 빈털터리가 될 거라고요. (이건 그저 이야기의 일부인 사실로서 말씀드리는 거지, 그것이 동생분에게 영향을 미쳤으리라고 여겨서는 아니에요."], ["(I mention that merely as a fact which is part of the narrative, and not as supposing it to have influenced your sister, except in the prudent and legitimate way in which, constituted as our artificial system is, we must all be influenced by such considerations.) Finally, after some high words and high spirit on the part of your sister, we came to the complete understanding that there was no danger; and your sister was so obliging as to allow me to present her with a mark or two of my appreciation at my dressmaker’s.’", "다만 우리의 인위적인 제도가 짜인 방식상, 우리 모두가 그런 고려에 영향을 받을 수밖에 없는, 그 신중하고 정당한 방식으로라면 모를까요.) 마침내, 동생분 쪽에서 다소 격한 말과 격한 기개를 보인 끝에, 우리는 아무 위험도 없다는 데 완전히 의견을 같이하게 되었어요. 그리고 동생분은 친절하게도 내 양장점에서 내 고마운 마음의 표시를 한두 가지 받아 주었지요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit looked sorry, and glanced at Fanny with a troubled face.", "리틀 도릿은 안쓰러운 표정으로, 괴로운 얼굴을 하고 패니를 흘긋 보았다."]], [["‘Also,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘as to promise to give me the present pleasure of a closing interview, and of parting with her on the best of terms. On which occasion,’ added Mrs Merdle, quitting her nest, and putting something in Fanny’s hand, ‘Miss Dorrit will permit me to say Farewell with best wishes in my own dull manner.’", "\"또한,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"이렇게 마지막으로 만나 더없이 좋은 사이로 작별하는 기쁨을 내게 주기로 약속해 주었지요. 그래서 이 자리에서,\" 머들 부인이 둥지에서 몸을 일으켜 패니의 손에 무언가를 쥐여 주며 덧붙였다. \"도릿 양, 내 무딘 방식대로나마, 더없는 행운을 빌며 작별 인사를 하게 해 주세요.\""]], [["The sisters rose at the same time, and they all stood near the cage of the parrot, as he tore at a claw-full of biscuit and spat it out, seemed to mock them with a pompous dance of his body without moving his feet, and suddenly turned himself upside down and trailed himself all over the outside of his golden cage, with the aid of his cruel beak and black tongue.", "자매는 동시에 일어섰고, 그들 모두 앵무새 새장 가까이에 섰다. 앵무새는 한 발톱 가득 비스킷을 움켜쥐어 물어뜯고는 뱉어 내더니, 발은 움직이지 않은 채 몸뚱이만 거드름 피우며 춤추는 시늉으로 그들을 조롱하는 듯하다가, 별안간 거꾸로 몸을 뒤집어 잔인한 부리와 검은 혀의 도움으로 금빛 새장 바깥을 온통 기어 다녔다."]], [["‘Adieu, Miss Dorrit, with best wishes,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"안녕히, 도릿 양, 행운을 빌어요.\" 머들 부인이 말했다."], ["‘If we could only come to a Millennium, or something of that sort, I for one might have the pleasure of knowing a number of charming and talented persons from whom I am at present excluded.", "\"우리가 천년왕국이나 그 비슷한 무엇에라도 이를 수 있다면, 나로서는 지금은 만날 수 없는 수많은 매력적이고 재능 있는 분들을 알게 되는 기쁨을 누릴 수도 있을 텐데요."], ["A more primitive state of society would be delicious to me.", "좀 더 원시적인 상태의 사회가 내게는 더없이 즐거울 거예요."], ["There used to be a poem when I learnt lessons, something about Lo the poor Indians whose something mind!", "내가 공부할 때 시 한 편이 있었는데, '보라 저 가련한 인디언, 그 무슨 마음을!' 하는 무슨 그런 거였어요."], ["If a few thousand persons moving in Society, could only go and be Indians, I would put my name down directly; but as, moving in Society, we can’t be Indians, unfortunately--Good morning!’", "사교계에서 움직이는 몇천 명이라도 가서 인디언이 될 수만 있다면, 나는 당장에 내 이름을 적어 넣겠어요. 하지만 사교계에서 움직이는 우리로서는, 안타깝게도 인디언이 될 수가 없으니 — 안녕히 가세요!\""]], [["They came down-stairs with powder before them and powder behind, the elder sister haughty and the younger sister humbled, and were shut out into unpowdered Harley Street, Cavendish Square.", "그들은 앞에도 분 바른 하인, 뒤에도 분 바른 하인을 둔 채 아래층으로 내려왔다. 언니는 거만했고 동생은 풀이 죽어 있었다. 그리고 그들은 분 바르지 않은 캐번디시 광장 할리 가로 내쫓기듯 밀려났다."]], [["‘Well?’ said Fanny, when they had gone a little way without speaking.", "\"그래서?\" 한동안 말없이 걷다가 패니가 말했다."], ["‘Have you nothing to say, Amy?’", "\"넌 할 말이 없니, 에이미?\""]], [["‘Oh, I don’t know what to say!’ she answered, distressed.", "\"아, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모르겠어!\" 그녀가 괴로워하며 대답했다."], ["‘You didn’t like this young man, Fanny?’", "\"그 젊은이가 마음에 들지 않았던 거지, 패니 언니?\""]], [["‘Like him?", "\"마음에 드냐고?"], ["He is almost an idiot.’", "그 사람은 거의 바보야.\""]], [["‘I am so sorry--don’t be hurt--but, since you ask me what I have to say, I am so very sorry, Fanny, that you suffered this lady to give you anything.’", "\"정말 안됐어 — 기분 상해하지는 마 — 그렇지만, 언니가 내게 할 말이 뭐냐고 물으니까 하는 말인데, 정말이지 너무 안타까워, 패니 언니, 언니가 그 부인이 주는 걸 받아 버렸다는 게.\""]], [["‘You little Fool!’ returned her sister, shaking her with the sharp pull she gave her arm.", "\"이 어리석은 것아!\" 언니가 그녀의 팔을 홱 잡아채며 흔들고 대꾸했다."], ["‘Have you no spirit at all?", "\"넌 기개라곤 도무지 없니? 하지만 딱 그런 식이지!"], ["But that’s just the way!", "넌 자존심도 없고, 마땅한 자긍심도 없어."], ["You have no self-respect, you have no becoming pride, just as you allow yourself to be followed about by a contemptible little Chivery of a thing,’ with the scornfullest emphasis, ‘you would let your family be trodden on, and never turn.’", "꼭 그 하찮은 치버리 따위가 졸졸 따라다니게 내버려 두는 것처럼,\" 더없이 경멸스러운 어조로 힘주어, \"넌 네 가문이 짓밟혀도, 절대 돌아서지 않을 거야.\""]], [["‘Don’t say that, dear Fanny.", "\"그런 말 하지 마, 패니 언니."], ["I do what I can for them.’", "나는 가족을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 하고 있어.\""]], [["‘You do what you can for them!’ repeated Fanny, walking her on very fast.", "\"가족을 위해 할 수 있는 걸 한다고!\" 패니가 그녀를 아주 빠르게 걷게 몰아붙이며 되뇌었다."], ["‘Would you let a woman like this, whom you could see, if you had any experience of anything, to be as false and insolent as a woman can be--would you let her put her foot upon your family, and thank her for it?’", "\"이런 여자를 — 네가 무슨 일에 대해서라도 경험이 좀 있었다면 여자가 부릴 수 있는 가장 거짓되고 무례한 짓을 부리는 여자라는 걸 알아챘을 그런 여자를 — 네 가문을 발로 짓밟게 내버려 두고, 그것에 대해 고맙다고 한단 말이야?\""]], [["‘No, Fanny, I am sure.’", "\"아니, 패니 언니, 정말 아니야.\""]], [["‘Then make her pay for it, you mean little thing.", "\"그럼 그 값을 치르게 해야지, 이 야무지지 못한 것아."], ["What else can you make her do?", "그 여자한테 달리 뭘 시킬 수 있겠어?"], ["Make her pay for it, you stupid child; and do your family some credit with the money!’", "그 값을 치르게 해, 이 멍청한 애야. 그리고 그 돈으로 네 가문 체면이라도 좀 세워 드려!\""]], [["They spoke no more all the way back to the lodging where Fanny and her uncle lived.", "그들은 패니와 삼촌이 사는 하숙집으로 돌아오는 내내 더는 말이 없었다."], ["When they arrived there, they found the old man practising his clarionet in the dolefullest manner in a corner of the room.", "거기 도착해 보니, 노인은 방 한구석에서 더없이 구슬프게 클라리넷을 연습하고 있었다."], ["Fanny had a composite meal to make, of chops, and porter, and tea; and indignantly pretended to prepare it for herself, though her sister did all that in quiet reality.", "패니는 양고기 토막과 흑맥주와 차로 이것저것 섞인 끼니를 차려야 했는데, 분개한 듯 제 손으로 차리는 척했지만, 사실 그 모든 일은 동생이 조용히 다 했다."], ["When at last Fanny sat down to eat and drink, she threw the table implements about and was angry with her bread, much as her father had been last night.", "마침내 패니가 자리에 앉아 먹고 마시기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 식탁 도구들을 함부로 내던지고 빵에 화풀이를 했다. 어젯밤 자기 아버지가 그랬던 것과 흡사했다."]], [["‘If you despise me,’ she said, bursting into vehement tears, ‘because I am a dancer, why did you put me in the way of being one?", "\"내가 무용수라고 나를 깔본다면,\" 그녀가 격렬한 눈물을 터뜨리며 말했다."], ["It was your doing.", "\"왜 나를 무용수가 되는 길로 들여놓은 거야?"], ["You would have me stoop as low as the ground before this Mrs Merdle, and let her say what she liked and do what she liked, and hold us all in contempt, and tell me so to my face.", "그건 네가 한 일이야. 너는 내가 이 머들 부인 앞에서 땅바닥에 닿도록 굽실거리게 하고, 그 여자가 하고 싶은 말 다 하고 하고 싶은 짓 다 하면서, 우리 모두를 경멸하고, 그걸 내 면전에 대고 말하게 내버려 뒀어."], ["Because I am a dancer!’", "내가 무용수라고!\""]], [["‘O Fanny!’", "\"아, 패니 언니!\""]], [["‘And Tip, too, poor fellow.", "\"그리고 팁도, 가엾은 오라비도."], ["She is to disparage him just as much as she likes, without any check--I suppose because he has been in the law, and the docks, and different things.", "그 여자가 마음껏 그를 깎아내리는데도 아무런 제지도 못 받았어 — 아마 그가 법률 사무소에도, 부두에도, 이런저런 데에도 있었기 때문이겠지."], ["Why, it was your doing, Amy.", "아니, 그것도 네가 한 일이야, 에이미."], ["You might at least approve of his being defended.’", "적어도 그가 변호받는 거라도 찬성해 줄 수는 있잖아.\""]], [["All this time the uncle was dolefully blowing his clarionet in the corner, sometimes taking it an inch or so from his mouth for a moment while he stopped to gaze at them, with a vague impression that somebody had said something.", "이러는 내내 삼촌은 구석에서 구슬프게 클라리넷을 불고 있었는데, 가끔씩은 입에서 1인치쯤 떼어 내고 잠시 멈추어, 누군가 무슨 말을 한 것 같다는 어렴풋한 느낌으로 그들을 멍하니 바라보았다."]], [["‘And your father, your poor father, Amy.", "\"그리고 네 아버지, 가엾은 네 아버지도, 에이미."], ["Because he is not free to show himself and to speak for himself, you would let such people insult him with impunity.", "그분이 자유롭게 나서서 스스로를 변호할 수 없으니까, 너는 그런 사람들이 아무 벌도 받지 않고 그분을 모욕하게 내버려 두는 거야."], ["If you don’t feel for yourself because you go out to work, you might at least feel for him, I should think, knowing what he has undergone so long.’", "네가 일하러 나간다고 해서 네 자신은 챙기지 못한다 쳐도, 그분이 그토록 오래 무슨 일을 겪어 왔는지 알면서, 적어도 그분만큼은 헤아려 드려야 한다고 생각해.\""]], [["Poor Little Dorrit felt the injustice of this taunt rather sharply.", "가엾은 리틀 도릿은 이 비난의 부당함을 다소 날카롭게 느꼈다."], ["The remembrance of last night added a barbed point to it.", "어젯밤의 기억이 거기에 가시 돋친 끝을 더했다."], ["She said nothing in reply, but turned her chair from the table towards the fire.", "그녀는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 식탁에서 의자를 돌려 난롯불 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Uncle, after making one more pause, blew a dismal wail and went on again.", "삼촌은 한 번 더 멈추었다가, 구슬픈 흐느낌 같은 소리를 불고는 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["Fanny was passionate with the tea-cups and the bread as long as her passion lasted, and then protested that she was the wretchedest girl in the world, and she wished she was dead.", "패니는 격정이 가시지 않는 동안 찻잔과 빵에 화풀이를 하더니, 이윽고 자신이 세상에서 가장 비참한 처녀라고, 차라리 죽었으면 좋겠다고 토로했다."], ["After that, her crying became remorseful, and she got up and put her arms round her sister.", "그러고 나서 그녀의 울음은 뉘우치는 울음으로 바뀌었고, 그녀는 일어나 동생을 두 팔로 끌어안았다."], ["Little Dorrit tried to stop her from saying anything, but she answered that she would, she must!", "리틀 도릿은 언니가 무슨 말이라도 하려는 것을 말리려 했지만, 그녀는 말하겠다고, 꼭 말해야 한다고 대답했다."], ["Thereupon she said again, and again, ‘I beg your pardon, Amy,’ and ‘Forgive me, Amy,’ almost as passionately as she had said what she regretted.", "그러고는 거듭거듭 \"미안해, 에이미\"라고, \"용서해 줘, 에이미\"라고, 좀 전에 후회스러운 말을 쏟아 낼 때만큼이나 격렬하게 말했다."]], [["‘But indeed, indeed, Amy,’ she resumed when they were seated in sisterly accord side by side, ‘I hope and I think you would have seen this differently, if you had known a little more of Society.’", "\"하지만 정말, 정말이지, 에이미,\" 그들이 자매답게 화목하게 나란히 앉았을 때 그녀가 다시 말을 이었다. \"네가 사교계를 조금만 더 알았더라면, 이 일을 다르게 보았을 거라고 나는 바라고 또 그렇게 생각해.\""]], [["‘Perhaps I might, Fanny,’ said the mild Little Dorrit.", "\"어쩌면 그랬을지도 모르지, 패니 언니.\" 온화한 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘You see, while you have been domestic and resignedly shut up there, Amy,’ pursued her sister, gradually beginning to patronise, ‘I have been out, moving more in Society, and may have been getting proud and spirited--more than I ought to be, perhaps?’", "\"있잖아, 너는 그곳에 살림하며 체념한 채 갇혀 지내 왔지만, 에이미,\" 언니가 점점 생색을 내기 시작하며 말을 이었다. \"나는 밖으로 나가 사교계에서 더 많이 움직였으니, 어쩌면 내가 거만하고 기개가 세진 건지도 몰라 — 어쩌면 마땅한 정도보다 더 말이야?\""]], [["Little Dorrit answered ‘Yes.", "리틀 도릿이 \"응. 아, 그래!\""], ["O yes!’", "하고 대답했다."]], [["‘And while you have been thinking of the dinner or the clothes, I may have been thinking, you know, of the family.", "\"그리고 너는 저녁거리나 옷을 생각하고 있었지만, 나는, 알다시피, 가문을 생각하고 있었던 건지도 몰라."], ["Now, may it not be so, Amy?’", "자, 그렇지 않겠니, 에이미?\""]], [["Little Dorrit again nodded ‘Yes,’ with a more cheerful face than heart.", "리틀 도릿이 마음보다는 밝은 얼굴로 다시 \"응\" 하고 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘Especially as we know,’ said Fanny, ‘that there certainly is a tone in the place to which you have been so true, which does belong to it, and which does make it different from other aspects of Society. So kiss me once again, Amy dear, and we will agree that we may both be right, and that you are a tranquil, domestic, home-loving, good girl.’", "\"특히 우리도 알다시피,\" 패니가 말했다. \"네가 그토록 충실히 지켜 온 그곳에는 분명 어떤 기풍이 있고, 그건 그곳에 본디 깃든 것이며, 사교계의 다른 면모들과 그곳을 다르게 만들어 주는 거니까. 그러니 한 번 더 내게 입맞춤해 줘, 사랑하는 에이미. 그리고 우리 둘 다 옳을 수도 있다는 데, 또 너는 차분하고 살림 잘하고 집안을 사랑하는 착한 아이라는 데 뜻을 모으자.\""]], [["The clarionet had been lamenting most pathetically during this dialogue, but was cut short now by Fanny’s announcement that it was time to go; which she conveyed to her uncle by shutting up his scrap of music, and taking the clarionet out of his mouth.", "이 대화가 오가는 내내 클라리넷은 더없이 애처롭게 탄식하고 있었지만, 이제 그만 가야 할 시간이라는 패니의 선언으로 뚝 끊겼다. 그녀는 삼촌의 악보 조각을 덮어 버리고 그 입에서 클라리넷을 빼냄으로써 그 뜻을 삼촌에게 전했다."]], [["Little Dorrit parted from them at the door, and hastened back to the Marshalsea.", "리틀 도릿은 문 앞에서 그들과 헤어져 서둘러 마셜시로 돌아갔다."], ["It fell dark there sooner than elsewhere, and going into it that evening was like going into a deep trench.", "그곳은 다른 곳보다 일찍 어둠이 내려, 그날 저녁 그 안으로 들어가는 것은 마치 깊은 참호 속으로 들어가는 것과 같았다."], ["The shadow of the wall was on every object.", "담장의 그림자가 모든 것 위에 드리워 있었다."], ["Not least upon the figure in the old grey gown and the black velvet cap, as it turned towards her when she opened the door of the dim room.", "그녀가 어둑한 방의 문을 열었을 때 그녀를 향해 돌아서던, 낡은 회색 가운에 검은 벨벳 모자를 쓴 그 모습 위에도 그 그림자는 어김없이 드리워 있었다."]], [["‘Why not upon me too!’ thought Little Dorrit, with the door yet in her hand.", "\"어찌하여 나 또한 그렇지 않으랴!\" 리틀 도릿은 아직 문을 손에 쥔 채 생각했다."], ["‘It was not unreasonable in Fanny.’", "\"패니가 그런 건 무리도 아니었어.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint", "# CHAPTER 21. Mr Merdle’s Complaint"]], [["Upon that establishment of state, the Merdle establishment in Harley Street, Cavendish Square, there was the shadow of no more common wall than the fronts of other establishments of state on the opposite side of the street.", "할리 가, 캐번디시 광장에 있는 그 으리으리한 명문가, 곧 머들 가문의 저택에 드리운 그림자라야, 길 건너편에 늘어선 다른 명문가 저택들의 정면이 드리우는 그림자에서 조금도 더할 것 없는 흔해 빠진 담벼락의 그림자에 불과했다."], ["Like unexceptionable Society, the opposing rows of houses in Harley Street were very grim with one another.", "흠잡을 데 없는 상류 사회가 그러하듯, 할리 가에서 마주 보고 늘어선 두 줄의 집들은 서로를 무척이나 음울하게 노려보고 있었다."], ["Indeed, the mansions and their inhabitants were so much alike in that respect, that the people were often to be found drawn up on opposite sides of dinner-tables, in the shade of their own loftiness, staring at the other side of the way with the dullness of the houses.", "실로 그 저택들과 그 거주자들은 이 점에서 어찌나 닮았던지, 사람들은 흔히 만찬 식탁의 양쪽에 갈라 앉아, 제 잘난 척의 그늘 속에서, 길 건너편을 집들만큼이나 멍한 표정으로 바라보고 있는 것이었다."]], [["Everybody knows how like the street the two dinner-rows of people who take their stand by the street will be.", "길가에 자리 잡은 두 줄의 만찬 손님들이 거리와 얼마나 닮았는지는 누구나 안다."], ["The expressionless uniform twenty houses, all to be knocked at and rung at in the same form, all approachable by the same dull steps, all fended off by the same pattern of railing, all with the same impracticable fire-escapes, the same inconvenient fixtures in their heads, and everything without exception to be taken at a high valuation--who has not dined with these?", "표정 없이 똑같은 스무 채의 집들, 모두 똑같은 방식으로 문을 두드리고 초인종을 울려야 하고, 모두 똑같이 칙칙한 계단으로 다가가야 하며, 모두 똑같은 무늬의 난간으로 가로막혀 있고, 모두 똑같이 쓸모없는 화재 비상구를 달고 있으며, 머리 꼭대기엔 똑같이 불편한 부속물들이 붙어 있고, 예외 없이 모든 것에 높은 값을 매겨야 하는 집들 — 이런 집들과 한 식탁에서 식사를 해 본 적 없는 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The house so drearily out of repair, the occasional bow-window, the stuccoed house, the newly-fronted house, the corner house with nothing but angular rooms, the house with the blinds always down, the house with the hatchment always up, the house where the collector has called for one quarter of an Idea, and found nobody at home--who has not dined with these?", "음산하게 손볼 데 투성이인 집, 이따금 보이는 활 모양 창문이 달린 집, 회반죽을 바른 집, 정면을 새로 단장한 집, 죄다 모난 방뿐인 모퉁이 집, 늘 차양이 내려져 있는 집, 늘 문장(紋章) 액자가 걸려 있는 집, 사 분기 분의 어떤 발상을 거두러 수금원이 찾아왔으나 아무도 집에 없던 집 — 이런 집들과 한 식탁에서 식사를 해 본 적 없는 사람이 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The house that nobody will take, and is to be had a bargain--who does not know her?", "아무도 사려 하지 않아 헐값에 나온 집 — 그 여자를 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"], ["The showy house that was taken for life by the disappointed gentleman, and which does not suit him at all--who is unacquainted with that haunted habitation?", "실의에 빠진 신사가 평생 살겠다고 사들였지만 도무지 그에게 맞지 않는 화려한 집 — 그 귀신 들린 거처를 모르는 이가 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["Harley Street, Cavendish Square, was more than aware of Mr and Mrs Merdle.", "할리 가, 캐번디시 광장은 머들 부부의 존재를 진작 알고도 남았다. 할리 가에는 그것이 미처 모르는 침입자들도 있었다."], ["Intruders there were in Harley Street, of whom it was not aware; but Mr and Mrs Merdle it delighted to honour.", "하지만 머들 부부야말로 그것이 기꺼이 떠받드는 이들이었다. 상류 사회는 머들 부부를 알고 있었다."], ["Society was aware of Mr and Mrs Merdle.", "상류 사회는 이렇게 말했던 것이다."], ["Society had said ‘Let us license them; let us know them.’", "\"저들에게 인가를 내려 주자. 저들과 사귀자.\""]], [["Mr Merdle was immensely rich; a man of prodigious enterprise; a Midas without the ears, who turned all he touched to gold.", "머들 씨는 어마어마한 부자였다. 굉장한 사업가였다."], ["He was in everything good, from banking to building.", "당나귀 귀만 없는 미다스로, 손대는 것마다 황금으로 바꾸어 놓았다. 은행업에서 건설업에 이르기까지, 좋은 것이라면 무엇이든 손을 대고 있었다."], ["He was in Parliament, of course.", "물론 의회에도 들어가 있었다."], ["He was in the City, necessarily.", "당연히 시티에도 있었다."], ["He was Chairman of this, Trustee of that, President of the other.", "이것의 회장이요, 저것의 이사요, 또 다른 것의 회장이었다. 가장 비중 있는 인사들이 사업 발기인들에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다."], ["The weightiest of men had said to projectors, ‘Now, what name have you got?", "\"자, 누구를 잡았소? 머들을 잡았소?\""], ["Have you got Merdle?’ And, the reply being in the negative, had said, ‘Then I won’t look at you.’", "그러고는 부정적인 대답이 돌아오면 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"그럼 거들떠보지도 않겠소.\""]], [["This great and fortunate man had provided that extensive bosom which required so much room to be unfeeling enough in, with a nest of crimson and gold some fifteen years before.", "이 위대하고도 운 좋은 사내는, 그 안에서 충분히 무정해지려면 그토록 넓은 공간이 필요했던 그 드넓은 가슴팍에다, 한 십오 년쯤 전에 진홍색과 황금색의 보금자리를 마련해 주었다."], ["It was not a bosom to repose upon, but it was a capital bosom to hang jewels upon.", "그것은 기대어 쉴 만한 가슴팍이 아니라, 보석을 걸어 두기에 더할 나위 없이 좋은 가슴팍이었다."], ["Mr Merdle wanted something to hang jewels upon, and he bought it for the purpose.", "머들 씨는 보석을 걸어 둘 무언가가 필요했고, 바로 그 목적으로 그것을 사들였다."], ["Storr and Mortimer might have married on the same speculation.", "스토어와 모티머도 그와 같은 속셈으로 결혼을 했을 법하다."]], [["Like all his other speculations, it was sound and successful.", "다른 모든 투기와 마찬가지로, 그것 역시 견실하고 성공적이었다."], ["The jewels showed to the richest advantage.", "보석들은 더없이 값지게 돋보였다."], ["The bosom moving in Society with the jewels displayed upon it, attracted general admiration.", "보석을 진열해 단 그 가슴팍이 상류 사회를 누비고 다니자, 뭇사람의 감탄을 자아냈다."], ["Society approving, Mr Merdle was satisfied.", "상류 사회가 흡족해하니, 머들 씨도 만족했다."], ["He was the most disinterested of men,--did everything for Society, and got as little for himself out of all his gain and care, as a man might.", "그는 더없이 사심 없는 사내였다 — 모든 것을 상류 사회를 위해 했고, 그 모든 이득과 노고에서 자기 몫으로 챙긴 것이라곤 한 인간이 챙길 수 있는 가장 적은 정도였다."]], [["That is to say, it may be supposed that he got all he wanted, otherwise with unlimited wealth he would have got it.", "말하자면, 그는 원하는 것은 다 가졌으리라 짐작할 수 있다. 그렇지 않았다면 그 무한한 재산으로 손에 넣었을 테니까."], ["But his desire was to the utmost to satisfy Society (whatever that was), and take up all its drafts upon him for tribute.", "그러나 그의 욕망은 상류 사회(그것이 무엇이든 간에)를 극진히 만족시키고, 그것이 공물로 그에게 발행하는 어음을 죄다 떠맡는 데 있었다. 그는 사람들 틈에서 빛나는 인물이 아니었다."], ["He did not shine in company; he had not very much to say for himself; he was a reserved man, with a broad, overhanging, watchful head, that particular kind of dull red colour in his cheeks which is rather stale than fresh, and a somewhat uneasy expression about his coat-cuffs, as if they were in his confidence, and had reasons for being anxious to hide his hands.", "자기 입으로 할 말도 그리 많지 않았다. 그는 말수 적은 사내로, 머리는 넓고 앞으로 불거진 채 경계하는 듯했으며, 두 뺨에는 싱싱하다기보다는 오히려 시들한, 그 특유의 칙칙한 붉은빛이 감돌았고, 마치 소맷동이 그의 비밀을 알고 있어 그의 두 손을 감추고 싶어 할 이유라도 있다는 듯, 외투 소맷동 언저리에는 어딘가 불안한 기색이 어려 있었다."], ["In the little he said, he was a pleasant man enough; plain, emphatic about public and private confidence, and tenacious of the utmost deference being shown by every one, in all things, to Society.", "그가 더러 하는 적은 말 속에서는 제법 유쾌한 사내였다. 꾸밈이 없었고, 공적·사적 신용에 관해서는 단호했으며, 모든 일에서 모든 이가 상류 사회에 더없는 경의를 표해야 한다는 데는 집요했다."], ["In this same Society (if that were it which came to his dinners, and to Mrs Merdle’s receptions and concerts), he hardly seemed to enjoy himself much, and was mostly to be found against walls and behind doors.", "바로 그 상류 사회(그의 만찬에, 그리고 머들 부인의 연회와 음악회에 오는 것이 그것이라면) 속에서, 그는 그다지 즐거워하는 것 같지 않았고, 대개는 벽에 붙어 있거나 문 뒤에 숨어 있기 일쑤였다."], ["Also when he went out to it, instead of its coming home to him, he seemed a little fatigued, and upon the whole rather more disposed for bed; but he was always cultivating it nevertheless, and always moving in it--and always laying out money on it with the greatest liberality.", "또한 그것이 그를 찾아오는 게 아니라 그가 그것을 찾아 나설 때면, 그는 다소 지쳐 보였고, 대체로 잠자리에 들고 싶어 하는 기색이었다. 그럼에도 그는 늘 그것을 가꾸었고, 늘 그 속을 누볐으며 — 늘 더없이 후하게 그것에 돈을 쏟아부었다."]], [["Mrs Merdle’s first husband had been a colonel, under whose auspices the bosom had entered into competition with the snows of North America, and had come off at little disadvantage in point of whiteness, and at none in point of coldness.", "머들 부인의 첫 남편은 어느 대령이었는데, 그 후원 아래 이 가슴팍은 북아메리카의 흰 눈과 경쟁을 벌인 적이 있었고, 흰빛에서는 거의 손색이 없었으며 차가움에서는 전혀 손색이 없었다."], ["The colonel’s son was Mrs Merdle’s only child.", "그 대령의 아들이 머들 부인의 외아들이었다."], ["He was of a chuckle-headed, high-shouldered make, with a general appearance of being, not so much a young man as a swelled boy.", "그는 멍청해 보이는 머리에 어깨가 높이 솟은 체격이었으며, 전체적인 인상이 청년이라기보다는 몸집만 부풀어 오른 사내아이 같았다."], ["He had given so few signs of reason, that a by-word went among his companions that his brain had been frozen up in a mighty frost which prevailed at St John’s, New Brunswick, at the period of his birth there, and had never thawed from that hour.", "분별의 기미를 어찌나 보이지 않던지, 동료들 사이에서는 그가 태어났던 무렵 뉴브런즈윅 세인트존에 몰아닥친 엄청난 혹한에 두뇌가 얼어붙어 그 시각 이래 한 번도 녹지 않았다는 우스갯소리가 돌았다."], ["Another by-word represented him as having in his infancy, through the negligence of a nurse, fallen out of a high window on his head, which had been heard by responsible witnesses to crack.", "또 다른 우스갯소리는, 그가 갓난아기 시절 유모의 부주의로 높은 창문에서 머리부터 떨어졌는데, 책임 있는 목격자들이 머리뼈가 쩍 갈라지는 소리를 들었다는 것이었다. 이 두 가지 설명은 아마도 사후에 지어낸 것이리라."], ["It is probable that both these representations were of ex post facto origin; the young gentleman (whose expressive name was Sparkler) being monomaniacal in offering marriage to all manner of undesirable young ladies, and in remarking of every successive young lady to whom he tendered a matrimonial proposal that she was ‘a doosed fine gal--well educated too--with no biggodd nonsense about her.’", "이 젊은 신사(그 함축적인 이름은 스파클러였다)는 온갖 탐탁잖은 아가씨들에게 청혼을 해 대는 데 단단히 미쳐 있었고, 청혼한 아가씨마다 두고 \"기차게 멋진 계집애야 — 교양도 있고 — 어쭙잖게 까다로운 구석도 없거든\" 하고 평하는 데 또한 미쳐 있었으니 말이다."]], [["A son-in-law with these limited talents, might have been a clog upon another man; but Mr Merdle did not want a son-in-law for himself; he wanted a son-in-law for Society.", "이렇게 보잘것없는 재능을 지닌 사위라면 다른 사내에게는 짐이 되었을지 모른다. 그러나 머들 씨는 자기 자신을 위해 사위가 필요했던 게 아니었다."], ["Mr Sparkler having been in the Guards, and being in the habit of frequenting all the races, and all the lounges, and all the parties, and being well known, Society was satisfied with its son-in-law.", "상류 사회를 위해 사위가 필요했던 것이다. 스파클러 씨가 근위대에 몸담은 적이 있고, 온갖 경마장과 온갖 사교 휴게실과 온갖 연회에 드나드는 데 익숙하며 널리 알려져 있었으므로, 상류 사회는 그 사위에 만족했다."], ["This happy result Mr Merdle would have considered well attained, though Mr Sparkler had been a more expensive article.", "이 행복한 결과라면, 설령 스파클러 씨가 더 값비싼 물건이었더라도 머들 씨는 잘 손에 넣었다 여겼을 것이다."], ["And he did not get Mr Sparkler by any means cheap for Society, even as it was.", "그리고 사실 그대로라 해도, 그는 결코 헐값에 스파클러 씨를 상류 사회에 들여보낸 게 아니었다."]], [["There was a dinner giving in the Harley Street establishment, while Little Dorrit was stitching at her father’s new shirts by his side that night; and there were magnates from the Court and magnates from the City, magnates from the Commons and magnates from the Lords, magnates from the bench and magnates from the bar, Bishop magnates, Treasury magnates, Horse Guard magnates, Admiralty magnates,--all the magnates that keep us going, and sometimes trip us up.", "그날 밤 리틀 도릿이 아버지 곁에서 그의 새 셔츠를 깁고 있던 바로 그때, 할리 가의 저택에서는 만찬이 베풀어지고 있었다. 궁정의 거물들과 시티의 거물들, 하원의 거물들과 상원의 거물들, 법관석의 거물들과 변호사석의 거물들, 주교 거물들, 재무성 거물들, 근위 기병대 거물들, 해군성 거물들 — 우리를 굴러가게 하는, 그리고 더러는 우리를 넘어뜨리는 온갖 거물들이 모여 있었다."]], [["‘I am told,’ said Bishop magnate to Horse Guards, ‘that Mr Merdle has made another enormous hit.", "\"듣자 하니,\" 주교 거물이 근위 기병대에게 말했다, \"머들 씨가 또 한 번 어마어마한 한탕을 했다더군요."], ["They say a hundred thousand pounds.’", "십만 파운드라던데요.\""]], [["Horse Guards had heard two.", "근위 기병대는 이십만 파운드라고 들었다."]], [["Treasury had heard three.", "재무성은 삼십만 파운드라고 들었다."]], [["Bar, handling his persuasive double eye-glass, was by no means clear but that it might be four.", "변호사는 설득력 있는 양안경을 만지작거리며, 사십만 파운드일지도 모른다는 것을 결코 부정할 수 없다고 했다."], ["It was one of those happy strokes of calculation and combination, the result of which it was difficult to estimate.", "그것은 계산과 결합의 그 절묘한 솜씨 가운데 하나로, 그 결과를 가늠하기란 어려웠다."], ["It was one of those instances of a comprehensive grasp, associated with habitual luck and characteristic boldness, of which an age presented us but few.", "그것은 폭넓은 파악력이 습관적인 행운과 특유의 대담성과 결합된 사례 가운데 하나로, 한 시대가 우리에게 보여 주는 것이 몇 안 되는 것이었다."], ["But here was Brother Bellows, who had been in the great Bank case, and who could probably tell us more.", "한데 마침 여기 그 거대한 은행 사건에 관여했던 벨로스 형제가 있으니, 아마도 우리에게 더 자세히 일러 줄 수 있으리라."], ["What did Brother Bellows put this new success at?", "벨로스 형제는 이 새로운 성공을 얼마로 보는가?"]], [["Brother Bellows was on his way to make his bow to the bosom, and could only tell them in passing that he had heard it stated, with great appearance of truth, as being worth, from first to last, half-a-million of money.", "벨로스 형제는 그 가슴팍에 인사를 올리러 가는 길이었던지라, 지나가며 이렇게 일러 줄 수 있을 뿐이었다. 처음부터 끝까지 따지면 오십만 파운드의 값어치가 있다고, 자못 그럴듯한 모양새로 진술되는 것을 들었노라고."]], [["Admiralty said Mr Merdle was a wonderful man, Treasury said he was a new power in the country, and would be able to buy up the whole House of Commons.", "해군성은 머들 씨가 놀라운 인물이라고 했고, 재무성은 그가 이 나라의 새로운 권력이며 하원 전체를 통째로 사들일 만하다고 했다."], ["Bishop said he was glad to think that this wealth flowed into the coffers of a gentleman who was always disposed to maintain the best interests of Society.", "주교는 이 재물이 언제나 상류 사회의 최선의 이익을 지키려는 신사의 금고로 흘러든다고 생각하니 기쁘다고 했다."]], [["Mr Merdle himself was usually late on these occasions, as a man still detained in the clutch of giant enterprises when other men had shaken off their dwarfs for the day.", "머들 씨 본인은 이런 자리에 으레 늦게 나타나곤 했으니, 다른 이들이 하루치의 난쟁이 같은 걱정을 떨쳐 버린 뒤에도 여전히 거대한 사업의 손아귀에 붙들려 있는 사내였던 까닭이다."], ["On this occasion, he was the last arrival.", "이번에도 그가 가장 늦게 도착했다."], ["Treasury said Merdle’s work punished him a little.", "재무성은 머들의 일이 그를 좀 괴롭히는 모양이라고 했다."], ["Bishop said he was glad to think that this wealth flowed into the coffers of a gentleman who accepted it with meekness.", "주교는 이 재물이 그것을 온순하게 받아들이는 신사의 금고로 흘러든다고 생각하니 기쁘다고 했다."]], [["Powder!", "분가루!"], ["There was so much Powder in waiting, that it flavoured the dinner.", "대기하는 분가루가 어찌나 많던지, 그것이 만찬에 향을 더할 지경이었다."], ["Pulverous particles got into the dishes, and Society’s meats had a seasoning of first-rate footmen.", "분가루 알갱이가 음식에 들어갔고, 상류 사회의 고기에는 일류 하인들의 양념이 배어 있었다."], ["Mr Merdle took down a countess who was secluded somewhere in the core of an immense dress, to which she was in the proportion of the heart to the overgrown cabbage.", "머들 씨는 어느 백작 부인을 식당으로 안내해 내려갔는데, 그녀는 거대한 드레스의 한복판 어딘가에 파묻혀 있어, 마치 웃자란 양배추 속의 심처럼 그 드레스에 비해 작은 비율을 차지했다."], ["If so low a simile may be admitted, the dress went down the staircase like a richly brocaded Jack in the Green, and nobody knew what sort of small person carried it.", "이토록 저속한 비유를 허락받을 수 있다면, 그 드레스는 화려한 비단으로 짠 '잭 인 더 그린'처럼 계단을 내려갔고, 그것을 떠받친 자그마한 인물이 대체 어떤 사람인지는 아무도 몰랐다."]], [["Society had everything it could want, and could not want, for dinner.", "* 잭 인 더 그린: 영국 오월제 행렬에서 나뭇잎과 꽃으로 만든 거대한 원추형 틀을 뒤집어쓰고 행진하던 인물."], ["It had everything to look at, and everything to eat, and everything to drink.", "상류 사회는 만찬을 위해 바랄 수 있는 것은 물론, 바랄 수조차 없는 것까지 죄다 갖추고 있었다."], ["It is to be hoped it enjoyed itself; for Mr Merdle’s own share of the repast might have been paid for with eighteenpence.", "볼 것도 죄다, 먹을 것도 죄다, 마실 것도 죄다 갖추고 있었다."], ["Mrs Merdle was magnificent.", "부디 그것이 즐겁게 지냈기를 바란다."], ["The chief butler was the next magnificent institution of the day.", "머들 씨 자신의 식사 몫이야 십팔 펜스면 치를 수 있었을 테니 말이다. 머들 부인은 장엄했다."], ["He was the stateliest man in the company.", "그날의 그다음으로 장엄한 제도는 집사장이었다."], ["He did nothing, but he looked on as few other men could have done.", "그는 그 자리에서 가장 위풍당당한 사내였다. 그는 아무것도 하지 않았으되, 다른 어떤 사내도 해내지 못할 만큼 위엄 있게 지켜보았다."], ["He was Mr Merdle’s last gift to Society.", "그는 머들 씨가 상류 사회에 바친 가장 최근의 선물이었다."], ["Mr Merdle didn’t want him, and was put out of countenance when the great creature looked at him; but inappeasable Society would have him--and had got him.", "머들 씨는 그를 원치 않았고, 그 위대한 피조물이 자기를 쳐다보면 무안해졌다. 그러나 달랠 길 없는 상류 사회는 그를 가지려 했고 — 결국 가지고 말았다."]], [["The invisible countess carried out the Green at the usual stage of the entertainment, and the file of beauty was closed up by the bosom.", "여흥의 여느 단계에 이르자 보이지 않는 백작 부인이 그 '그린'을 내가고, 미인들의 행렬은 그 가슴팍이 마무리 지었다."], ["Treasury said, Juno.", "재무성은 유노라고 했다."], ["Bishop said, Judith.", "주교는 유디트라고 했다."]], [["Bar fell into discussion with Horse Guards concerning courts-martial.", "변호사는 근위 기병대와 더불어 군법 회의에 관한 토론에 빠져들었다."], ["Brothers Bellows and Bench struck in.", "벨로스 형제와 법관석 형제도 끼어들었다."], ["Other magnates paired off.", "다른 거물들도 짝을 지어 흩어졌다."], ["Mr Merdle sat silent, and looked at the table-cloth.", "머들 씨는 말없이 앉아 식탁보를 들여다보았다."], ["Sometimes a magnate addressed him, to turn the stream of his own particular discussion towards him; but Mr Merdle seldom gave much attention to it, or did more than rouse himself from his calculations and pass the wine.", "이따금 어느 거물이 자기 토론의 물줄기를 그에게 돌리려 그에게 말을 건넸으나, 머들 씨는 좀처럼 거기에 큰 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 자기 계산에서 잠깐 깨어나 포도주를 건네주는 것 이상은 하지 않았다."]], [["When they rose, so many of the magnates had something to say to Mr Merdle individually that he held little levees by the sideboard, and checked them off as they went out at the door.", "자리에서 일어났을 때, 무척 많은 거물들이 머들 씨에게 따로 할 말이 있었던지라, 그는 찬장 옆에서 작은 알현식을 열고, 그들이 문을 나설 때마다 하나씩 처리해 갔다."]], [["Treasury hoped he might venture to congratulate one of England’s world-famed capitalists and merchant-princes (he had turned that original sentiment in the house a few times, and it came easy to him) on a new achievement.", "재무성은 새로운 업적을 두고 영국의 세계적으로 이름난 자본가요 상인 군주 가운데 한 분께 감히 축하를 드려도 되겠느냐고 했다(그는 의사당에서 그 독창적인 정서를 몇 차례 굴려 본 적이 있어, 그 말이 술술 나왔다)."], ["To extend the triumphs of such men was to extend the triumphs and resources of the nation; and Treasury felt--he gave Mr Merdle to understand--patriotic on the subject.", "그러한 인물들의 개가를 넓히는 것은 곧 나라의 개가와 자원을 넓히는 일이니, 재무성은 이 점에 관해 — 그는 머들 씨가 알아듣도록 일렀다 — 애국심이 솟구침을 느낀다고 했다."]], [["‘Thank you, my lord,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘thank you. I accept your congratulations with pride, and I am glad you approve.’", "\"고맙소, 경.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"고맙소. 자랑스럽게 축하를 받겠소. 그대가 흡족해하니 기쁘오.\""]], [["‘Why, I don’t unreservedly approve, my dear Mr Merdle.", "\"이런, 무조건 흡족해하는 건 아니오, 친애하는 머들 씨."], ["Because,’ smiling Treasury turned him by the arm towards the sideboard and spoke banteringly, ‘it never can be worth your while to come among us and help us.’", "왜냐하면,\" 미소 띤 재무성이 그의 팔을 잡아 찬장 쪽으로 돌려세우며 농담조로 말했다, \"그대가 우리 틈에 끼어 우리를 도와 줄 만한 가치라곤 결코 없을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["Mr Merdle felt honoured by the--", "머들 씨는 영광스럽다고 느꼈으니 —"]], [["‘No, no,’ said Treasury, ‘that is not the light in which one so distinguished for practical knowledge and great foresight, can be expected to regard it.", "\"아니, 아니,\" 재무성이 말했다, \"실무 지식과 위대한 선견지명으로 그토록 두드러진 분이 그렇게 여기실 거라 기대할 수야 없지요."], ["If we should ever be happily enabled, by accidentally possessing the control over circumstances, to propose to one so eminent to--to come among us, and give us the weight of his influence, knowledge, and character, we could only propose it to him as a duty.", "만일 우리가 어쩌다 사정을 좌우할 위치를 갖게 되어, 운 좋게도 그토록 탁월한 분께 — 우리 틈에 끼어 그의 영향력과 지식과 인품의 무게를 우리에게 실어 달라고 청할 수 있게 된다면, 우리는 오로지 그것을 의무로서 청할 수 있을 따름이오."], ["In fact, as a duty that he owed to Society.’", "실로, 그가 상류 사회에 진 의무로서 말이오.\""]], [["Mr Merdle intimated that Society was the apple of his eye, and that its claims were paramount to every other consideration.", "머들 씨는 상류 사회야말로 자기 눈동자처럼 소중하며, 그 요구가 다른 어떤 고려보다 우선한다고 넌지시 밝혔다."], ["Treasury moved on, and Bar came up.", "재무성은 자리를 옮겼고, 변호사가 다가왔다."]], [["Bar, with his little insinuating jury droop, and fingering his persuasive double eye-glass, hoped he might be excused if he mentioned to one of the greatest converters of the root of all evil into the root of all good, who had for a long time reflected a shining lustre on the annals even of our commercial country--if he mentioned, disinterestedly, and as, what we lawyers called in our pedantic way, amicus curiae, a fact that had come by accident within his knowledge.", "변호사는 배심원을 구슬릴 때 짓는 그 살짝 고개를 숙이는 버릇과 더불어, 설득력 있는 양안경을 만지작거리며, 만일 자기가 모든 악의 뿌리를 모든 선의 뿌리로 바꾸어 놓는 가장 위대한 전환자 가운데 한 분께 — 우리 상업 국가의 연대기에마저 오랫동안 찬란한 광채를 드리워 온 분께 — 말씀을 드린다면, 그것도 사심 없이, 그리고 우리 법조인들이 현학적인 투로 '법정의 벗'이라 부르는 입장에서, 우연히 그가 알게 된 어떤 사실을 말씀드린다면, 부디 용서해 주십사 했다. 그는 동부의 어느 주에 있는 자못 상당한 규모의 영지의 권리 증서를 검토해 달라는 의뢰를 받은 적이 있었다 — 사실은, 머들 씨도 우리 법조인이 정확함을 즐긴다는 것을 알고 계실 터라 하는 말이지만, 동부의 두 주 경계에 걸쳐 있는 영지였다."], ["He had been required to look over the title of a very considerable estate in one of the eastern counties--lying, in fact, for Mr Merdle knew we lawyers loved to be particular, on the borders of two of the eastern counties.", "그런데 그 권리 증서는 완벽하게 견실했고, 그 영지는 — 돈을(배심원용 고갯짓과 설득력 있는 양안경) — 마음껏 쓸 수 있는 어떤 이가 자못 유리한 조건에 사들이게 되어 있었다."], ["Now, the title was perfectly sound, and the estate was to be purchased by one who had the command of--Money (jury droop and persuasive eye-glass), on remarkably advantageous terms.", "이 사실을 변호사가 알게 된 것은 바로 그날이었고, 문득 이런 생각이 떠올랐다고 했다. '오늘 저녁 나는 존경하는 벗 머들 씨와 만찬을 함께할 영광을 누리니, 우리끼리만 은밀히, 그 기회를 일러 드려야겠다.'"], ["This had come to Bar’s knowledge only that day, and it had occurred to him, ‘I shall have the honour of dining with my esteemed friend Mr Merdle this evening, and, strictly between ourselves, I will mention the opportunity.’ Such a purchase would involve not only a great legitimate political influence, but some half-dozen church presentations of considerable annual value.", "그러한 매입에는 거대하고 정당한 정치적 영향력뿐 아니라, 상당한 연수익이 따르는 대여섯 자리의 성직 추천권까지 딸려 온다고 했다. 그런데 머들 씨로서는 이미 자기 자본을 굴릴 방도며 그 활발하고 왕성한 지력을 십분 부려 먹을 방도를 찾는 데 조금도 막힘이 없음을 변호사는 잘 알고 있었다."], ["Now, that Mr Merdle was already at no loss to discover means of occupying even his capital, and of fully employing even his active and vigorous intellect, Bar well knew: but he would venture to suggest that the question arose in his mind, whether one who had deservedly gained so high a position and so European a reputation did not owe it--we would not say to himself, but we would say to Society, to possess himself of such influences as these; and to exercise them--we would not say for his own, or for his party’s, but we would say for Society’s--benefit.", "그러나 그는 감히 이런 제언을 드리고자 했으니, 곧 그토록 높은 지위와 그토록 유럽적인 명성을 마땅히 얻은 분이라면, 그것은 — 자기 자신에게라고는 하지 않겠고, 상류 사회에게 — 그러한 영향력을 스스로 손에 넣고, 그것을 행사할 빚을 진 것 아니냐는 의문이 마음속에 떠오른다는 것이었다. 그것도 — 그 자신을 위해서라거나 그의 당파를 위해서라고는 하지 않겠고 — 상류 사회의 이익을 위해서 말이다."]], [["Mr Merdle again expressed himself as wholly devoted to that object of his constant consideration, and Bar took his persuasive eye-glass up the grand staircase.", "머들 씨는 다시금, 자기가 늘 마음 쓰는 그 목적에 전적으로 헌신하고 있노라고 밝혔고, 변호사는 자신의 설득력 있는 양안경을 챙겨 대계단을 올라갔다."], ["Bishop then came undesignedly sidling in the direction of the sideboard.", "그러자 주교가 무심한 척 찬장 쪽으로 슬그머니 다가왔다."]], [["Surely the goods of this world, it occurred in an accidental way to Bishop to remark, could scarcely be directed into happier channels than when they accumulated under the magic touch of the wise and sagacious, who, while they knew the just value of riches (Bishop tried here to look as if he were rather poor himself), were aware of their importance, judiciously governed and rightly distributed, to the welfare of our brethren at large.", "이 세상의 재물이란, 부유함의 정당한 가치를 알면서도(여기서 주교는 자기야말로 다소 가난한 사람인 척하려 애썼다) 그것이 슬기롭게 운용되고 올바르게 분배될 때 우리 형제들 전반의 복리에 얼마나 중요한지를 아는 현명하고 사려 깊은 이들의 마법 같은 손길 아래 쌓일 때만큼 행복한 물길로 흘러드는 일은 좀처럼 없을 것이라고, 주교는 우연히 그런 생각이 떠올랐다는 식으로 한마디 했다."]], [["Mr Merdle with humility expressed his conviction that Bishop couldn’t mean him, and with inconsistency expressed his high gratification in Bishop’s good opinion.", "머들 씨는 겸손하게, 주교께서 설마 자기를 두고 하시는 말씀일 리는 없을 거라는 확신을 밝혔고, 앞뒤가 맞지 않게도, 주교의 호평에 더없이 흡족하다는 뜻을 밝혔다."]], [["Bishop then--jauntily stepping out a little with his well-shaped right leg, as though he said to Mr Merdle ‘don’t mind the apron; a mere form!’ put this case to his good friend:", "그러자 주교는 — 마치 머들 씨에게 \"이 앞치마는 신경 쓰지 마시오. 한낱 형식일 뿐이외다!\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯, 맵시 있게 빠진 오른쪽 다리를 경쾌하게 살짝 내딛으며 — 자기 좋은 벗에게 이런 가정을 내놓았다."]], [["Whether it had occurred to his good friend, that Society might not unreasonably hope that one so blest in his undertakings, and whose example on his pedestal was so influential with it, would shed a little money in the direction of a mission or so to Africa?", "그토록 사업에 복을 받고, 그 받침대 위에 선 그 본보기가 상류 사회에 그토록 큰 영향을 끼치는 분이라면, 가령 아프리카 선교 사업이나 그 비슷한 방면에 약간의 돈을 흩뿌려 주리라고 상류 사회가 바란대도 부당하지 않으리라는 생각이, 혹 자기 좋은 벗에게 떠오른 적은 없는지요?"]], [["Mr Merdle signifying that the idea should have his best attention, Bishop put another case:", "머들 씨가 그 생각에 최선의 주의를 기울이겠노라고 표하자, 주교는 또 다른 가정을 내놓았다."]], [["Whether his good friend had at all interested himself in the proceedings of our Combined Additional Endowed Dignitaries Committee, and whether it had occurred to him that to shed a little money in _that_ direction might be a great conception finely executed?", "자기 좋은 벗이 혹 우리 '연합 추가 기부 고위 성직자 위원회'의 활동에 조금이라도 관심을 가져 본 적은 없는지요? 그리고 그 방면에 약간의 돈을 흩뿌리는 것이야말로 위대한 구상을 멋지게 실행하는 일이 되리라는 생각이, 혹 그에게 떠오른 적은 없는지요?"]], [["Mr Merdle made a similar reply, and Bishop explained his reason for inquiring.", "머들 씨가 비슷한 대답을 하자, 주교는 자기가 묻는 까닭을 설명했다."]], [["Society looked to such men as his good friend to do such things.", "상류 사회가 그러한 일을 해 주기를 기대하는 것은 다름 아닌 자기 좋은 벗과 같은 분들이라고."], ["It was not that _he_ looked to them, but that Society looked to them.", "그것을 기대하는 것은 자기가 아니라 상류 사회라고."], ["Just as it was not Our Committee who wanted the Additional Endowed Dignitaries, but it was Society that was in a state of the most agonising uneasiness of mind until it got them.", "마치 추가 기부 고위 성직자를 원한 것이 우리 위원회가 아니라, 그들을 얻기 전까지는 더없이 괴로운 마음의 불안에 빠져 있던 상류 사회였던 것과 마찬가지라고."], ["He begged to assure his good friend that he was extremely sensible of his good friend’s regard on all occasions for the best interests of Society; and he considered that he was at once consulting those interests and expressing the feeling of Society, when he wished him continued prosperity, continued increase of riches, and continued things in general.", "그는 자기 좋은 벗에게, 모든 경우에 상류 사회의 최선의 이익을 헤아려 주는 그 마음 씀씀이를 자기가 극진히 고맙게 여긴다는 것을 단언해 두고자 했다. 그리고 그가 그 벗에게 변함없는 번영과, 변함없는 부의 증대와, 변함없는 만사형통을 빌어 줄 때, 자기는 그 이익을 헤아리는 동시에 상류 사회의 정서를 표하는 것이라 여긴다고 했다."]], [["Bishop then betook himself up-stairs, and the other magnates gradually floated up after him until there was no one left below but Mr Merdle.", "그러고 나서 주교는 위층으로 자리를 옮겼고, 다른 거물들도 차츰 그를 따라 떠올라가, 마침내 아래층에는 머들 씨 말고는 아무도 남지 않았다."], ["That gentleman, after looking at the table-cloth until the soul of the chief butler glowed with a noble resentment, went slowly up after the rest, and became of no account in the stream of people on the grand staircase.", "그 신사는 집사장의 영혼이 고귀한 분노로 이글거릴 만큼 오래도록 식탁보를 들여다보다가, 천천히 나머지 사람들을 따라 올라가서는, 대계단을 메운 인파의 흐름 속에서 아무 존재감도 없는 사람이 되었다."], ["Mrs Merdle was at home, the best of the jewels were hung out to be seen, Society got what it came for, Mr Merdle drank twopennyworth of tea in a corner and got more than he wanted.", "머들 부인은 손님을 맞고 있었고, 가장 좋은 보석들은 사람들 눈에 띄도록 내걸려 있었으며, 상류 사회는 찾아온 목적을 얻었고, 머들 씨는 구석에서 이 펜스어치의 차를 마시고는 원하는 것보다 더 많이 마셨다."]], [["Among the evening magnates was a famous physician, who knew everybody, and whom everybody knew.", "저녁의 거물들 가운데에는 모든 이를 알고 모든 이가 아는, 어느 이름난 의사가 있었다."], ["On entering at the door, he came upon Mr Merdle drinking his tea in a corner, and touched him on the arm.", "그는 문으로 들어서다가 구석에서 차를 마시는 머들 씨와 마주쳐, 그의 팔을 건드렸다."]], [["Mr Merdle started.", "머들 씨가 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["It’s you!’", "자네였군!\""]], [["‘Any better to-day?’", "\"오늘은 좀 나으신가요?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I am no better.’", "\"아니,\" 머들 씨가 말했다, \"나아진 게 없네.\""]], [["‘A pity I didn’t see you this morning.", "\"오늘 아침에 못 뵌 게 안타깝군요."], ["Pray come to me to-morrow, or let me come to you.’", "부디 내일 저를 찾아오시든지, 아니면 제가 가 뵙도록 하지요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ he replied.", "\"음!\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘I will come to-morrow as I drive by.’", "\"마차로 지나는 길에 내일 들르겠네.\""]], [["Bar and Bishop had both been bystanders during this short dialogue, and as Mr Merdle was swept away by the crowd, they made their remarks upon it to the Physician.", "이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안 변호사와 주교가 둘 다 곁에 서 있다가, 머들 씨가 인파에 휩쓸려 떠밀려 가자, 그 일을 두고 의사에게 한마디씩 했다."], ["Bar said, there was a certain point of mental strain beyond which no man could go; that the point varied with various textures of brain and peculiarities of constitution, as he had had occasion to notice in several of his learned brothers; but the point of endurance passed by a line’s breadth, depression and dyspepsia ensued.", "변호사는, 어느 누구도 넘어설 수 없는 정신적 긴장의 어떤 한계점이 있는 법이며, 그 한계점은 뇌의 다양한 결과 체질의 특이함에 따라 달라진다고, 자기가 학식 높은 여러 동료들에게서 여러 차례 보아 온 바라고 했다. 그러나 그 견딤의 한계점을 머리카락 한 올의 너비만큼이라도 지나치면, 곧 우울과 소화 불량이 뒤따른다고 했다."], ["Not to intrude on the sacred mysteries of medicine, he took it, now (with the jury droop and persuasive eye-glass), that this was Merdle’s case?", "의학의 신성한 신비를 침범할 뜻은 없으나, 자기 생각으로는 이제(배심원용 고갯짓과 설득력 있는 양안경과 더불어) 이것이 머들의 경우 아니겠느냐고 했다."], ["Bishop said that when he was a young man, and had fallen for a brief space into the habit of writing sermons on Saturdays, a habit which all young sons of the church should sedulously avoid, he had frequently been sensible of a depression, arising as he supposed from an over-taxed intellect, upon which the yolk of a new-laid egg, beaten up by the good woman in whose house he at that time lodged, with a glass of sound sherry, nutmeg, and powdered sugar acted like a charm.", "주교는, 자기가 젊은 시절 한동안 토요일마다 설교문을 쓰는 버릇 — 교회의 모든 젊은 자제들이 부지런히 멀리해야 할 버릇 — 에 빠진 적이 있었는데, 그때 짐작건대 과로한 지력에서 비롯된 우울을 자주 느꼈으며, 그 우울에는 당시 자기가 세 들어 살던 집의 착한 안주인이 신선한 달걀노른자에 질 좋은 셰리주 한 잔과 육두구, 그리고 가루 설탕을 넣어 휘저어 준 것이 마법처럼 잘 들었다고 했다."], ["Without presuming to offer so simple a remedy to the consideration of so profound a professor of the great healing art, he would venture to inquire whether the strain, being by way of intricate calculations, the spirits might not (humanly speaking) be restored to their tone by a gentle and yet generous stimulant?", "그토록 심오한 위대한 치유술의 대가께 그토록 단순한 처방을 감히 권할 생각은 없으나, 긴장이 복잡한 계산에서 비롯된 것이라면, 부드러우면서도 넉넉한 자극제로 그 기력을 (인간적으로 말하여) 본래의 활기로 되돌릴 수는 없을지 감히 여쭙고 싶다고 했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said the physician, ‘yes, you are both right.", "\"그렇소,\" 의사가 말했다, \"그렇소, 두 분 다 옳소."], ["But I may as well tell you that I can find nothing the matter with Mr Merdle.", "하지만 이 말씀은 드려도 좋겠소. 나는 머들 씨에게서 아무런 이상도 찾아낼 수 없다는 것이오."], ["He has the constitution of a rhinoceros, the digestion of an ostrich, and the concentration of an oyster.", "그는 코뿔소의 체질에, 타조의 소화력에, 굴의 집중력을 지녔소."], ["As to nerves, Mr Merdle is of a cool temperament, and not a sensitive man: is about as invulnerable, I should say, as Achilles.", "신경으로 말하자면, 머들 씨는 냉정한 기질이라 예민한 사람이 아니오. 내 보기엔 아킬레우스만큼이나 끄떡없는 사람이오."], ["How such a man should suppose himself unwell without reason, you may think strange.", "그런 사람이 어찌 까닭도 없이 자기를 병들었다 여기는지, 이상하다 생각하실 게요."], ["But I have found nothing the matter with him.", "하지만 나는 그에게서 아무런 이상도 찾지 못했소."], ["He may have some deep-seated recondite complaint.", "무언가 깊이 자리 잡은 알 수 없는 병이 있을지도 모르지요."], ["I can’t say.", "그건 나도 모르겠소."], ["I only say, that at present I have not found it out.’", "다만 내가 말할 수 있는 건, 지금으로서는 그것을 찾아내지 못했다는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["There was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on the bosom now displaying precious stones in rivalry with many similar superb jewel-stands; there was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on young Sparkler hovering about the rooms, monomaniacally seeking any sufficiently ineligible young lady with no nonsense about her; there was no shadow of Mr Merdle’s complaint on the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings, of whom whole colonies were present; or on any of the company.", "지금 여러 비슷한 화려한 보석 진열대와 겨루며 보석을 자랑하고 있는 그 가슴팍에는, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자가 어려 있지 않았다. 어쭙잖게 까다로운 구석이라곤 없는 충분히 탐탁잖은 아가씨를 단단히 미친 듯이 찾아 헤매며 방들을 누비는 젊은 스파클러에게도, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자는 어려 있지 않았다."], ["Even on himself, its shadow was faint enough as he moved about among the throng, receiving homage.", "통째로 한 무리씩 와 있던 배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가에게도, 그 자리의 다른 누구에게도, 머들 씨의 병의 그림자는 어려 있지 않았다. 인파 속을 누비며 경의를 받는 그 자신에게마저, 그 그림자는 충분히 희미했다."]], [["Mr Merdle’s complaint.", "머들 씨의 병."], ["Society and he had so much to do with one another in all things else, that it is hard to imagine his complaint, if he had one, being solely his own affair.", "그와 상류 사회는 다른 모든 일에서 그토록 깊이 얽혀 있었으니, 만일 그에게 병이 있다 한들 그 병만은 오로지 그 자신만의 문제이리라고는 상상하기 어려운 노릇이었다."], ["Had he that deep-seated recondite complaint, and did any doctor find it out?", "그에게 그 깊이 자리 잡은 알 수 없는 병이 정말 있었던 것일까, 그리고 어느 의사가 그것을 찾아냈을까?"], ["Patience, in the meantime, the shadow of the Marshalsea wall was a real darkening influence, and could be seen on the Dorrit Family at any stage of the sun’s course.", "그러는 사이에, 진득이 기다리자. 마셜시 담벼락의 그림자는 실제로 어둠을 드리우는 힘이었고, 해가 어느 길을 지나든 그 어느 시각에도 도릿 집안 위에 어려 있는 것을 볼 수 있었으니."]], [["# CHAPTER 22. A Puzzle", "# CHAPTER 22. A Puzzle"]], [["Mr Clennam did not increase in favour with the Father of the Marshalsea in the ratio of his increasing visits.", "클레넘 씨는 방문 횟수가 늘어나는 만큼 마셜시의 아버지에게 호감을 얻지는 못했다."], ["His obtuseness on the great Testimonial question was not calculated to awaken admiration in the paternal breast, but had rather a tendency to give offence in that sensitive quarter, and to be regarded as a positive shortcoming in point of gentlemanly feeling.", "그 위대한 사례금 문제에 대한 그의 둔감함은 아버지의 가슴속에 흠모를 불러일으킬 만한 것이 못 되었고, 오히려 그 예민한 영역에 불쾌감을 안기고, 신사다운 정서의 측면에서 명백한 결함으로 여겨지는 경향이 있었다."], ["An impression of disappointment, occasioned by the discovery that Mr Clennam scarcely possessed that delicacy for which, in the confidence of his nature, he had been inclined to give him credit, began to darken the fatherly mind in connection with that gentleman.", "클레넘 씨가, 그 천성을 믿고 그에게 기꺼이 인정해 주려 했던 그 섬세함을 거의 지니지 못했다는 발견에서 비롯된 실망의 인상이, 그 신사와 관련하여 아버지의 마음을 어둡게 물들이기 시작했다."], ["The father went so far as to say, in his private family circle, that he feared Mr Clennam was not a man of high instincts.", "아버지는 사사로운 가족 모임에서, 클레넘 씨가 고결한 본능을 지닌 사람이 아닐까 염려된다고까지 말하기에 이르렀다."], ["He was happy, he observed, in his public capacity as leader and representative of the College, to receive Mr Clennam when he called to pay his respects; but he didn’t find that he got on with him personally.", "그는, 칼리지의 지도자이자 대표라는 공적 지위에서 클레넘 씨가 인사를 차리러 찾아올 때 그를 맞이하는 것은 기쁜 일이라고 했으나, 개인적으로는 그와 잘 통하지가 않는다고 했다."], ["There appeared to be something (he didn’t know what it was) wanting in him.", "그에게는 뭔가가(그게 무엇인지는 모르겠으나) 빠져 있는 것 같다고 했다."], ["Howbeit, the father did not fail in any outward show of politeness, but, on the contrary, honoured him with much attention; perhaps cherishing the hope that, although not a man of a sufficiently brilliant and spontaneous turn of mind to repeat his former testimonial unsolicited, it might still be within the compass of his nature to bear the part of a responsive gentleman, in any correspondence that way tending.", "그렇다고 해도 아버지는 겉으로 드러내는 예의에 있어서는 어긋남이 없었으며, 오히려 그 반대로 그에게 깍듯한 관심을 베풀어 예우했다. 아마도, 비록 청하지도 않은 종전의 사례금을 되풀이할 만큼 충분히 명민하고 자발적인 기질의 사람은 아닐지언정, 그쪽으로 흐르는 어떠한 교신에서든 화답하는 신사의 역할을 감당하는 것 정도는 여전히 그 천성의 범위 안에 있으리라는 희망을 품고 있었을 것이다."]], [["In the threefold capacity, of the gentleman from outside who had been accidentally locked in on the night of his first appearance, of the gentleman from outside who had inquired into the affairs of the Father of the Marshalsea with the stupendous idea of getting him out, and of the gentleman from outside who took an interest in the child of the Marshalsea, Clennam soon became a visitor of mark.", "처음 나타난 날 밤에 우연히 안에 갇혔던 바깥세상의 신사로서, 마셜시의 아버지를 빼내겠다는 엄청난 발상으로 그의 사정을 알아본 바깥세상의 신사로서, 또 마셜시의 아이에게 관심을 둔 바깥세상의 신사로서, 클레넘은 그 삼중의 자격으로 곧 주목할 만한 방문객이 되었다."], ["He was not surprised by the attentions he received from Mr Chivery when that officer was on the lock, for he made little distinction between Mr Chivery’s politeness and that of the other turnkeys.", "그는 치버리 씨가 문지기를 설 때 그로부터 받는 관심에 놀라지 않았는데, 치버리 씨의 예의와 다른 간수들의 예의를 별로 구별하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["It was on one particular afternoon that Mr Chivery surprised him all at once, and stood forth from his companions in bold relief.", "그런데 어느 특별한 오후에, 치버리 씨가 그를 단번에 놀라게 하며 동료들 사이에서 두드러지게 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["Mr Chivery, by some artful exercise of his power of clearing the Lodge, had contrived to rid it of all sauntering Collegians; so that Clennam, coming out of the prison, should find him on duty alone.", "치버리 씨는 문지기 방을 비우는 자신의 재간을 어떤 교묘한 방식으로 발휘하여, 어슬렁거리던 칼리지 사람들을 모두 그곳에서 내보내는 데 성공했다. 그리하여 클레넘이 감옥에서 나올 때 그 혼자만 근무 중인 것을 보게끔 해 놓았다."]], [["‘(Private) I ask your pardon, sir,’ said Mr Chivery in a secret manner; ‘but which way might you be going?’", "\"(은밀히) 실례합니다만, 선생님,\" 치버리 씨가 비밀스러운 태도로 말했다. \"어느 쪽으로 가시는 길입니까?\""]], [["‘I am going over the Bridge.’ He saw in Mr Chivery, with some astonishment, quite an Allegory of Silence, as he stood with his key on his lips.", "\"다리를 건너갈 참이오.\" 그는 적잖이 놀라며, 열쇠를 입술에 대고 선 채 그야말로 '침묵의 알레고리'와도 같은 치버리 씨를 보았다."]], [["‘(Private) I ask your pardon again,’ said Mr Chivery, ‘but could you go round by Horsemonger Lane?", "\"(은밀히) 다시 한번 실례합니다만,\" 치버리 씨가 말했다."], ["Could you by any means find time to look in at that address?’ handing him a little card, printed for circulation among the connection of Chivery and Co., Tobacconists, Importers of pure Havannah Cigars, Bengal Cheroots, and fine-flavoured Cubas, Dealers in Fancy Snuffs, &c.", "\"혹 호스몽거 레인 쪽으로 돌아가 주실 수는 없겠습니까? 어떻게든 짬을 내어 이 주소에 들러 봐 주실 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["&c.", "그러면서 그는 작은 명함 한 장을 건넸는데, 그것은 '치버리 상회, 담배상, 순수 하바나 시가·벵골 셰루트·풍미 좋은 쿠바산 수입상, 고급 코담배 등등 취급'의 거래처들 사이에 돌리려고 인쇄해 둔 것이었다."]], [["‘(Private) It an’t tobacco business,’ said Mr Chivery.", "\"(은밀히) 담배 장사 일이 아닙니다,\" 치버리 씨가 말했다."], ["‘The truth is, it’s my wife.", "\"사실은, 제 아내 일입니다. 아내가 선생님께 한 말씀 드리고 싶어 합니다, 어떤 점에 관해서요—그렇습니다,\""], ["She’s wishful to say a word to you, sir, upon a point respecting--yes,’ said Mr Chivery, answering Clennam’s look of apprehension with a nod, ‘respecting _her_.’", "치버리 씨가 클레넘의 염려스러운 표정에 고개를 끄덕여 답하며 말했다. \"그 애에 관해서요.\""]], [["‘I will make a point of seeing your wife directly.’", "\"부인을 곧장 꼭 뵙도록 하겠소.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 선생님."], ["Much obliged.", "정말 감사합니다."], ["It an’t above ten minutes out of your way.", "가시는 길에서 십 분도 안 벗어납니다."], ["Please to ask for _Mrs_ Chivery!’ These instructions, Mr Chivery, who had already let him out, cautiously called through a little slide in the outer door, which he could draw back from within for the inspection of visitors when it pleased him.", "치버리 부인을 찾으시면 됩니다!\" 이 당부의 말을, 이미 그를 내보낸 치버리 씨는 바깥문에 달린 작은 미닫이 구멍을 통해 조심스레 건넸는데, 그것은 내키면 안에서 끌어당겨 방문객을 살펴볼 수 있는 구멍이었다."]], [["Arthur Clennam, with the card in his hand, betook himself to the address set forth upon it, and speedily arrived there.", "아서 클레넘은 명함을 손에 들고, 거기에 적힌 주소로 발길을 옮겨 곧 그곳에 도착했다."], ["It was a very small establishment, wherein a decent woman sat behind the counter working at her needle.", "그것은 아주 작은 가게였는데, 점잖아 보이는 여자가 계산대 뒤에 앉아 바느질을 하고 있었다."], ["Little jars of tobacco, little boxes of cigars, a little assortment of pipes, a little jar or two of snuff, and a little instrument like a shoeing horn for serving it out, composed the retail stock in trade.", "담배가 든 작은 단지 몇 개, 시가가 든 작은 상자 몇 개, 자그마하게 갖춘 파이프들, 코담배가 든 작은 단지 한두 개, 그리고 그것을 떠 담는 구둣주걱 같은 작은 도구가 그 소매점의 상품 전부였다."]], [["Arthur mentioned his name, and his having promised to call, on the solicitation of Mr Chivery.", "아서는 자기 이름을 대고, 치버리 씨의 부탁으로 들르기로 약속했다고 말했다."], ["About something relating to Miss Dorrit, he believed.", "도릿 양과 관련된 어떤 일 때문인 것으로 안다고 했다."], ["Mrs Chivery at once laid aside her work, rose up from her seat behind the counter, and deploringly shook her head.", "치버리 부인은 곧장 바느질감을 내려놓고 계산대 뒤 자리에서 일어나, 한탄스레 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘You may see him now,’ said she, ‘if you’ll condescend to take a peep.’", "\"이제 그 애를 보실 수 있어요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"황송하지만 한번 들여다봐 주신다면요.\""]], [["With these mysterious words, she preceded the visitor into a little parlour behind the shop, with a little window in it commanding a very little dull back-yard.", "이 알 수 없는 말과 함께, 그녀는 방문객을 가게 뒤편의 작은 거실로 앞장서 안내했는데, 거기에는 아주 작은 음침한 뒷마당이 내다보이는 작은 창문이 하나 있었다."], ["In this yard a wash of sheets and table-cloths tried (in vain, for want of air) to get itself dried on a line or two; and among those flapping articles was sitting in a chair, like the last mariner left alive on the deck of a damp ship without the power of furling the sails, a little woe-begone young man.", "그 마당에서는 빨아 놓은 시트며 식탁보가 (공기가 통하지 않아 헛되이) 빨랫줄 한두 가닥에 걸린 채 마르려 애쓰고 있었다. 그리고 그 펄럭이는 빨랫감 사이에, 마치 돛을 거둘 힘도 없이 축축한 배의 갑판 위에 마지막까지 살아남은 뱃사람처럼, 의기소침한 젊은이 하나가 의자에 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Our John,’ said Mrs Chivery.", "\"우리 존이에요,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다."]], [["Not to be deficient in interest, Clennam asked what he might be doing there?", "관심이 없어 보이지 않으려고, 클레넘은 그가 거기서 무엇을 하고 있는 것이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘It’s the only change he takes,’ said Mrs Chivery, shaking her head afresh.", "\"저게 저 애가 누리는 유일한 기분 전환이랍니다,\" 치버리 부인이 다시 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘He won’t go out, even in the back-yard, when there’s no linen; but when there’s linen to keep the neighbours’ eyes off, he’ll sit there, hours.", "\"빨래가 없을 때는 뒷마당에조차 안 나가려 해요. 그런데 이웃들 눈을 가려 줄 빨래가 널려 있으면, 거기 몇 시간이고 앉아 있지요."], ["Hours he will.", "몇 시간씩요."], ["Says he feels as if it was groves!’ Mrs Chivery shook her head again, put her apron in a motherly way to her eyes, and reconducted her visitor into the regions of the business.", "마치 숲속에 있는 기분이라나요!\" 치버리 부인은 다시 고개를 젓고, 어머니다운 몸짓으로 앞치마를 눈에 갖다 댄 다음, 방문객을 다시 가게 쪽으로 데리고 나왔다."]], [["‘Please to take a seat, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery. ‘Miss Dorrit is the matter with Our John, sir; he’s a breaking his heart for her, and I would wish to take the liberty to ask how it’s to be made good to his parents when bust?’", "\"앉으시지요, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"우리 존을 그렇게 만든 건 도릿 양이에요, 선생님. 그 애가 그 처녀 때문에 가슴이 미어지고 있답니다. 외람되지만 한 말씀 여쭙겠는데, 저 애가 이러다 결딴나면 그게 부모한테 어떻게 보상이 된단 말입니까?\""]], [["Mrs Chivery, who was a comfortable-looking woman much respected about Horsemonger Lane for her feelings and her conversation, uttered this speech with fell composure, and immediately afterwards began again to shake her head and dry her eyes.", "치버리 부인은 호스몽거 레인 일대에서 그 정 깊은 마음씨와 말솜씨로 꽤 존경받는, 푸근해 보이는 여자였는데, 이 말을 무서울 만큼 차분하게 내뱉고는 곧바로 다시 고개를 젓고 눈물을 닦기 시작했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said she in continuation, ‘you are acquainted with the family, and have interested yourself with the family, and are influential with the family.", "\"선생님,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["If you can promote views calculated to make two young people happy, let me, for Our John’s sake, and for both their sakes, implore you so to do!’", "\"선생님은 그 댁을 잘 아시고, 그 댁 일에 마음을 써 주셨고, 그 댁에 영향력이 있으시지요. 두 젊은이를 행복하게 해 줄 만한 길을 도모해 주실 수 있다면, 우리 존을 위해서, 또 그 둘 모두를 위해서, 부디 그렇게 해 주십사 간청드립니다!\""]], [["‘I have been so habituated,’ returned Arthur, at a loss, ‘during the short time I have known her, to consider Little--I have been so habituated to consider Miss Dorrit in a light altogether removed from that in which you present her to me, that you quite take me by surprise.", "\"제가 그분을 알게 된 짧은 동안,\" 아서가 당혹스러워하며 대꾸했다."], ["Does she know your son?’", "\"저는 워낙 익숙해진 터라 리틀—저는 도릿 양을, 부인께서 제게 말씀하시는 것과는 전혀 다른 관점에서 생각하는 데 워낙 익숙해진 터라, 정말 깜짝 놀랐습니다. 그분이 부인의 아드님을 압니까?\""]], [["‘Brought up together, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery.", "\"같이 자랐답니다, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Played together.’", "\"같이 놀았고요.\""]], [["‘Does she know your son as her admirer?’", "\"그분이 아드님을 흠모하는 사람으로 압니까?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["bless you, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, with a sort of triumphant shiver, ‘she never could have seen him on a Sunday without knowing he was that.", "저런, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 일종의 의기양양한 떨림과 함께 말했다."], ["His cane alone would have told it long ago, if nothing else had.", "\"일요일에 그 애를 보고도 그걸 몰랐을 리가 없지요. 다른 건 다 제쳐 두더라도 그 애 지팡이 하나만으로도 진작 알았을 거예요."], ["Young men like John don’t take to ivory hands a pinting, for nothing.", "존 같은 젊은이가 까닭 없이 손가락 모양 상아 장식이 달린 걸 들고 다니진 않거든요."], ["How did I first know it myself?", "저 자신은 처음에 그걸 어떻게 알았냐고요?"], ["Similarly.’", "그렇게 알았지요.\""]], [["‘Perhaps Miss Dorrit may not be so ready as you, you see.’", "\"하지만 도릿 양은 부인만큼 눈치가 빠르지 않을지도 모르지요.\""]], [["‘Then she knows it, sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘by word of mouth.’", "\"그렇다면 그 처녀는 말로 들어서 압니다, 선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Are you sure?’", "\"확실합니까?\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘sure and certain as in this house I am. I see my son go out with my own eyes when in this house I was, and I see my son come in with my own eyes when in this house I was, and I know he done it!’ Mrs Chivery derived a surprising force of emphasis from the foregoing circumstantiality and repetition.", "\"선생님,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다. \"제가 이 집에 있는 것만큼이나 확실하고 분명합니다. 제 두 눈으로 이 집에서 제 아들이 나가는 걸 봤고, 제 두 눈으로 이 집에서 제 아들이 들어오는 걸 봤으니, 그 애가 그렇게 한 걸 압니다!\" 치버리 부인은 이 앞선 상세함과 되풀이에서 놀랄 만한 강조의 힘을 끌어냈다."]], [["‘May I ask you how he came to fall into the desponding state which causes you so much uneasiness?’", "\"그토록 부인을 불안케 하는 그 낙심한 상태에 어떻게 빠지게 되었는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘That,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘took place on that same day when to this house I see that John with these eyes return.", "\"그건,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다."], ["Never been himself in this house since.", "\"바로 그 날, 이 집으로 그 존이 돌아오는 걸 이 눈으로 본 바로 그 날에 일어났습니다. 그 뒤로 이 집에서 한 번도 제정신이었던 적이 없어요."], ["Never was like what he has been since, not from the hour when to this house seven year ago me and his father, as tenants by the quarter, came!’ An effect in the nature of an affidavit was gained from this speech by Mrs Chivery’s peculiar power of construction.", "칠 년 전 저와 그 애 아버지가 분기 단위 세입자로 이 집에 들어온 그 시각 이후로, 그 뒤의 그 애 같은 모습은 한 번도 없었지요!\" 치버리 부인 특유의 문장 구성력 덕분에 이 말에서 진술서와도 같은 효력이 우러났다."]], [["‘May I venture to inquire what is your version of the matter?’", "\"이 일에 대한 부인의 견해는 어떠한지 감히 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘You may,’ said Mrs Chivery, ‘and I will give it to you in honour and in word as true as in this shop I stand.", "\"되고말고요,\" 치버리 부인이 말했다."], ["Our John has every one’s good word and every one’s good wish.", "\"제가 이 가게에 서 있는 게 진실인 만큼, 명예를 걸고 사실 그대로 말씀드리지요. 우리 존은 누구에게나 좋은 말을 듣고 누구에게나 좋은 바람을 받는 아이예요."], ["He played with her as a child when in that yard a child she played.", "그 처녀가 저 마당에서 어린아이로 놀 때 그 애도 어린아이로 같이 놀았답니다. 그때부터 줄곧 그 처녀를 알아 온 거지요."], ["He has known her ever since.", "그 애가 바로 이 거실에서 저녁을 먹고 일요일 오후에 나가서 그 처녀를 만났어요. 약속이 있었는지 없었는지는 제가 뭐라 말씀드리지 않겠습니다."], ["He went out upon the Sunday afternoon when in this very parlour he had dined, and met her, with appointment or without appointment; which, I do not pretend to say.", "그 애가 청혼을 했지요. 그 처녀의 오라비와 언니는 콧대가 높아서 우리 존을 반대해요."], ["He made his offer to her.", "그 처녀의 아버지는 만사 제 잇속만 차려서 그 처녀를 누구에게도 나눠 주려 하지 않고요."], ["Her brother and sister is high in their views, and against Our John.", "그런 사정 아래서 그 처녀가 우리 존에게 대답한 거예요. '안 돼요, 존, 당신을 받아들일 수 없어요."], ["Her father is all for himself in his views and against sharing her with any one.", "저는 어떤 남편도 받아들일 수 없어요. 저는 결코 아내가 될 생각이 없어요."], ["Under which circumstances she has answered Our John, “No, John, I cannot have you, I cannot have any husband, it is not my intentions ever to become a wife, it is my intentions to be always a sacrifice, farewell, find another worthy of you, and forget me!”", "저는 언제까지나 희생물이 될 생각이에요. 잘 가요, 당신에게 어울리는 다른 사람을 찾으세요, 그리고 저를 잊으세요!'"], ["This is the way in which she is doomed to be a constant slave to them that are not worthy that a constant slave she unto them should be.", "이런 식으로 그 처녀는, 한결같은 종이 되어 줄 가치도 없는 자들에게 한결같은 종으로 매여 살게 운명 지어진 거예요. 이런 식으로 우리 존은, 빨래 사이에서 감기에 걸리는 것과, 저 마당에서, 제가 방금 그 마당에서 선생님께 보여 드린 것처럼, 어미 가슴을 후벼 파는 폐허 같은 꼴을 드러내는 것밖에는 아무 즐거움도 찾지 못하게 된 거랍니다!\""], ["This is the way in which Our John has come to find no pleasure but in taking cold among the linen, and in showing in that yard, as in that yard I have myself shown you, a broken-down ruin that goes home to his mother’s heart!’ Here the good woman pointed to the little window, whence her son might be seen sitting disconsolate in the tuneless groves; and again shook her head and wiped her eyes, and besought him, for the united sakes of both the young people, to exercise his influence towards the bright reversal of these dismal events.", "여기서 그 선량한 여인은, 가락 없는 숲속에 시름겹게 앉아 있는 아들이 보이는 그 작은 창문을 가리켰다. 그리고 다시 고개를 젓고 눈물을 닦으며, 두 젊은이 모두를 위해서 이 음울한 사태가 밝게 뒤집히도록 그의 영향력을 발휘해 달라고 그에게 애원했다."]], [["She was so confident in her exposition of the case, and it was so undeniably founded on correct premises in so far as the relative positions of Little Dorrit and her family were concerned, that Clennam could not feel positive on the other side.", "그녀는 자기 사정 설명에 어찌나 확신에 차 있었던지, 또 리틀 도릿과 그 가족의 상대적 위치에 관한 한 그것이 너무도 부인할 수 없는 올바른 전제에 근거하고 있었던지라, 클레넘으로서는 다른 쪽에 대해 확신을 가질 수가 없었다."], ["He had come to attach to Little Dorrit an interest so peculiar--an interest that removed her from, while it grew out of, the common and coarse things surrounding her--that he found it disappointing, disagreeable, almost painful, to suppose her in love with young Mr Chivery in the back-yard, or any such person.", "그는 리틀 도릿에게 어떤 독특한 관심을—그녀를 둘러싼 흔하고 거친 것들에서 비롯되었으면서도 그녀를 그것들로부터 떼어 놓는 그런 관심을—붙이게 된 터라, 그녀가 뒷마당의 젊은 치버리 씨라든가 그런 누군가와 사랑에 빠졌으리라고 추측하는 것이 실망스럽고, 마뜩잖고, 거의 고통스럽기까지 했다."], ["On the other hand, he reasoned with himself that she was just as good and just as true in love with him, as not in love with him; and that to make a kind of domesticated fairy of her, on the penalty of isolation at heart from the only people she knew, would be but a weakness of his own fancy, and not a kind one.", "한편으로 그는, 그녀가 사랑에 빠졌든 빠지지 않았든 똑같이 선하고 똑같이 참되다고, 그리고 그녀를 일종의 길든 요정으로 만들어 그녀가 아는 유일한 사람들로부터 마음속으로 떼어 놓는 대가를 치르게 하는 것은 그저 자기 공상의 나약함일 뿐, 결코 친절한 일이 아니라고 스스로 따져 보았다."], ["Still, her youthful and ethereal appearance, her timid manner, the charm of her sensitive voice and eyes, the very many respects in which she had interested him out of her own individuality, and the strong difference between herself and those about her, were not in unison, and were determined not to be in unison, with this newly presented idea.", "그렇더라도 그녀의 앳되고 청초한 모습, 수줍은 태도, 그 예민한 목소리와 눈의 매력, 그녀가 그 자신의 개성으로써 그의 관심을 끌었던 그 숱한 측면들, 그리고 그녀와 그녀 주위 사람들 사이의 뚜렷한 차이—이 모든 것은 이 새로 제시된 생각과 조화를 이루지 못했고, 결단코 조화를 이루려 하지 않았다."]], [["He told the worthy Mrs Chivery, after turning these things over in his mind--he did that, indeed, while she was yet speaking--that he might be relied upon to do his utmost at all times to promote the happiness of Miss Dorrit, and to further the wishes of her heart if it were in his power to do so, and if he could discover what they were.", "이런 것들을 마음속으로 곱씹은 끝에—사실 그는 그녀가 아직 말하고 있는 동안에도 그러고 있었다—그는 그 갸륵한 치버리 부인에게, 자기가 언제든 도릿 양의 행복을 도모하고, 또 자신의 힘이 닿는 한, 그리고 그녀의 마음속 소망이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있는 한, 그 소망을 이루어 주려고 최선을 다하리라는 것을 믿어도 좋다고 말했다."], ["At the same time he cautioned her against assumptions and appearances; enjoined strict silence and secrecy, lest Miss Dorrit should be made unhappy; and particularly advised her to endeavour to win her son’s confidence and so to make quite sure of the state of the case.", "동시에 그는 그녀에게 섣부른 추측과 겉모습을 경계하라고 일렀고, 도릿 양이 불행해질까 봐 철저한 침묵과 비밀을 당부했으며, 특히 아들의 신뢰를 얻어 사정의 실상을 확실히 알아내도록 애써 보라고 충고했다."], ["Mrs Chivery considered the latter precaution superfluous, but said she would try.", "치버리 부인은 그 마지막 주의는 불필요하다고 여겼으나, 그래도 해 보겠다고 했다."], ["She shook her head as if she had not derived all the comfort she had fondly expected from this interview, but thanked him nevertheless for the trouble he had kindly taken.", "그녀는 이 면담에서 은근히 기대했던 만큼의 위안을 다 얻지 못한 듯 고개를 저었지만, 그래도 친절히 수고를 베풀어 준 데 대해 그에게 고마워했다."], ["They then parted good friends, and Arthur walked away.", "그렇게 두 사람은 좋은 사이로 헤어졌고, 아서는 걸어 나왔다."]], [["The crowd in the street jostling the crowd in his mind, and the two crowds making a confusion, he avoided London Bridge, and turned off in the quieter direction of the Iron Bridge.", "거리의 인파가 그의 마음속 인파와 부딪치고, 그 두 인파가 뒤엉켜 혼란을 빚자, 그는 런던 다리를 피하고 더 한적한 쪽인 철교 방향으로 꺾어 들었다."], ["He had scarcely set foot upon it, when he saw Little Dorrit walking on before him.", "그 다리에 막 발을 들여놓자마자, 그는 리틀 도릿이 자기 앞에서 걸어가고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["It was a pleasant day, with a light breeze blowing, and she seemed to have that minute come there for air.", "가벼운 산들바람이 부는 쾌청한 날이었고, 그녀는 바로 그 순간 바람을 쐬러 거기 나온 듯했다."], ["He had left her in her father’s room within an hour.", "그가 한 시간 안에 아버지의 방에 그녀를 두고 떠나온 터였다."]], [["It was a timely chance, favourable to his wish of observing her face and manner when no one else was by.", "다른 사람이 곁에 없을 때 그녀의 표정과 태도를 살펴보고 싶다는 그의 바람에 알맞은, 때맞춘 기회였다."], ["He quickened his pace; but before he reached her, she turned her head.", "그는 걸음을 재촉했다. 그러나 그녀에게 미처 다가가기도 전에, 그녀가 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["‘Have I startled you?’ he asked.", "\"제가 놀라게 했나요?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["‘I thought I knew the step,’ she answered, hesitating.", "\"발소리를 아는 것 같다고 생각했어요,\" 그녀가 머뭇거리며 대답했다."]], [["‘And did you know it, Little Dorrit?", "\"그래서, 그 소리를 알아챘나요, 리틀 도릿?"], ["You could hardly have expected mine.’", "제 발소리는 도무지 예상하지 못했을 텐데요.\""]], [["‘I did not expect any.", "\"누구의 것도 예상하지 못했어요."], ["But when I heard a step, I thought it--sounded like yours.’", "그렇지만 발소리가 들렸을 때, 그게—당신 것처럼 들린다고 생각했어요.\""]], [["‘Are you going further?’", "\"더 가는 길인가요?\""]], [["‘No, sir, I am only walking here for a little change.’", "\"아니요, 선생님. 그저 기분 전환 삼아 여기서 좀 걷고 있을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["They walked together, and she recovered her confiding manner with him, and looked up in his face as she said, after glancing around:", "두 사람은 함께 걸었고, 그녀는 그에게 마음을 터놓는 태도를 되찾고는, 주위를 한 번 둘러본 뒤 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["‘It is so strange.", "\"참 이상해요."], ["Perhaps you can hardly understand it.", "어쩌면 좀처럼 이해하지 못하실 거예요."], ["I sometimes have a sensation as if it was almost unfeeling to walk here.’", "저는 가끔 여기서 걷는 것이 거의 매정한 짓처럼 느껴질 때가 있어요.\""]], [["‘Unfeeling?’", "\"매정하다고요?\""]], [["‘To see the river, and so much sky, and so many objects, and such change and motion.", "\"강을 보고, 그렇게 넓은 하늘을, 그렇게 많은 것들을, 그렇게 많은 변화와 움직임을 보는 게요."], ["Then to go back, you know, and find him in the same cramped place.’", "그러고는, 아시겠지만, 돌아가서 똑같이 비좁은 곳에 계신 그분을 보게 되는 게요.\""]], [["‘Ah yes!", "\"아 그렇군요!"], ["But going back, you must remember that you take with you the spirit and influence of such things to cheer him.’", "하지만 돌아갈 때는, 그런 것들의 기운과 영향을 당신이 함께 지니고 가서 그분을 북돋아 드리는 거라고 생각해야 해요.\""]], [["‘Do I? I hope I may!", "\"제가 그러나요?"], ["I am afraid you fancy too much, sir, and make me out too powerful.", "그럴 수 있으면 좋겠어요! 선생님은 너무 많이 상상하셔서 저를 너무 대단한 사람으로 만드시는 것 같아 걱정이에요."], ["If you were in prison, could I bring such comfort to you?’", "만일 선생님이 감옥에 계시다면, 제가 선생님께 그런 위안을 가져다드릴 수 있을까요?\""]], [["‘Yes, Little Dorrit, I am sure of it.’", "\"그럼요, 리틀 도릿, 저는 확신합니다.\""]], [["He gathered from a tremor on her lip, and a passing shadow of great agitation on her face, that her mind was with her father.", "그는 그녀의 입술에 어린 떨림과, 얼굴을 스쳐 지나가는 큰 동요의 그림자에서, 그녀의 마음이 아버지에게 가 있음을 알아챘다."], ["He remained silent for a few moments, that she might regain her composure.", "그는 그녀가 평정을 되찾을 수 있도록 잠시 말이 없었다."], ["The Little Dorrit, trembling on his arm, was less in unison than ever with Mrs Chivery’s theory, and yet was not irreconcilable with a new fancy which sprung up within him, that there might be some one else in the hopeless--newer fancy still--in the hopeless unattainable distance.", "그의 팔에 매달린 채 떨고 있는 리틀 도릿은 그 어느 때보다도 치버리 부인의 이론과 어울리지 않았지만, 그렇다고 그의 안에서 새로 솟아난 공상—희망 없는 곳에, 더더욱 새로운 공상으로는, 희망 없고 닿을 수 없는 먼 곳에 누군가 다른 사람이 있을지도 모른다는 공상—과 영 맞지 않는 것도 아니었다."]], [["They turned, and Clennam said, Here was Maggy coming!", "두 사람이 돌아서는데, 클레넘이 저기 매기가 오고 있다고 말했다."], ["Little Dorrit looked up, surprised, and they confronted Maggy, who brought herself at sight of them to a dead stop.", "리틀 도릿이 놀라 고개를 들었고, 그들은 매기와 마주쳤는데, 매기는 그들을 보자마자 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["She had been trotting along, so preoccupied and busy that she had not recognised them until they turned upon her.", "그녀는 어찌나 골똘하고 분주하게 종종걸음을 치고 있었던지, 그들이 그녀 쪽으로 돌아설 때까지 그들을 알아보지 못했던 것이다."], ["She was now in a moment so conscience-stricken that her very basket partook of the change.", "이제 그녀는 한순간에 어찌나 양심의 가책을 받았던지, 그녀의 바구니까지도 그 변화에 한몫 거드는 듯했다."]], [["‘Maggy, you promised me to stop near father.’", "\"매기, 아버지 곁에 있겠다고 나한테 약속했잖아.\""]], [["‘So I would, Little Mother, only he wouldn’t let me.", "\"그러려고 했어요, 작은 엄마, 그런데 그분이 못 있게 했는걸요."], ["If he takes and sends me out I must go.", "그분이 나를 내보내겠다고 하면 나는 가야 하잖아요."], ["If he takes and says, “Maggy, you hurry away and back with that letter, and you shall have a sixpence if the answer’s a good ‘un,”", "그분이 '매기, 이 편지를 가지고 얼른 다녀와라, 답장이 좋은 거면 육 펜스를 주마' 하고 말하면, 나는 받아야지요."], ["I must take it. Lor, Little Mother, what’s a poor thing of ten year old to do?", "어머나, 작은 엄마, 가엾은 열 살짜리가 어쩌겠어요?"], ["And if Mr Tip--if he happens to be a coming in as I come out, and if he says “Where are you going, Maggy?”", "그리고 팁 씨가—내가 나갈 때 마침 그분이 들어오다가, '어디 가니, 매기?'"], ["and if I says, “I’m a going So and So,”", "하고 묻고, 내가 '아무 데 아무 데 가요'"], ["and if he says, “I’ll have a Try too,”", "하고 대답하고, 그분이 '나도 한번 해 봐야겠다'"], ["and if he goes into the George and writes a letter and if he gives it me and says, “Take that one to the same place, and if the answer’s a good ‘un I’ll give you a shilling,”", "하고는 조지 술집에 들어가 편지를 써서 그걸 나한테 주면서 '이건 같은 데로 가져가라, 답장이 좋은 거면 일 실링을 주마'"], ["it ain’t my fault, mother!’", "하면, 그건 내 잘못이 아니잖아요, 엄마!\""]], [["Arthur read, in Little Dorrit’s downcast eyes, to whom she foresaw that the letters were addressed.", "아서는 리틀 도릿의 내리깐 눈에서, 그 편지들이 누구에게 보내질 것을 그녀가 예감하는지 읽었다."]], [["‘I’m a going So and So.", "\"나는 아무 데 아무 데 가요."], ["There!", "거기요!"], ["That’s where I am a going to,’ said Maggy.", "내가 가는 데가 거기예요,\" 매기가 말했다."], ["‘I’m a going So and So.", "\"나는 아무 데 아무 데 가요."], ["It ain’t you, Little Mother, that’s got anything to do with it--it’s you, you know,’ said Maggy, addressing Arthur.", "그건 작은 엄마하고는 상관없는 일이에요—그건 당신이지요, 아시잖아요,\" 매기가 아서를 향해 말했다."], ["‘You’d better come, So and So, and let me take and give ‘em to you.’", "\"당신이 아무 데 아무 데로 와 주면, 내가 그걸 당신한테 건네줄 수 있는데.\""]], [["‘We will not be so particular as that, Maggy.", "\"우리 그렇게까지 까다롭게 굴진 말자꾸나, 매기."], ["Give them me here,’ said Clennam in a low voice.", "여기서 그것들을 내게 다오,\" 클레넘이 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Well, then, come across the road,’ answered Maggy in a very loud whisper.", "\"그럼 길 건너편으로 와요,\" 매기가 아주 큰 소리의 속삭임으로 대답했다."], ["‘Little Mother wasn’t to know nothing of it, and she would never have known nothing of it if you had only gone So and So, instead of bothering and loitering about.", "\"작은 엄마는 이걸 아무것도 몰라야 하는데, 당신이 까탈 부리고 어정거리지 말고 아무 데 아무 데로 가기만 했으면 작은 엄마가 아무것도 몰랐을 거예요."], ["It ain’t my fault.", "그건 내 잘못이 아니에요."], ["I must do what I am told.", "나는 시키는 대로 해야 하는걸요."], ["They ought to be ashamed of themselves for telling me.’", "나한테 그런 걸 시키는 사람들이야말로 부끄러운 줄 알아야지요.\""]], [["Clennam crossed to the other side, and hurriedly opened the letters.", "클레넘은 길 건너편으로 가서 서둘러 편지들을 펴 보았다."], ["That from the father mentioned that most unexpectedly finding himself in the novel position of having been disappointed of a remittance from the City on which he had confidently counted, he took up his pen, being restrained by the unhappy circumstance of his incarceration during three-and-twenty years (doubly underlined), from coming himself, as he would otherwise certainly have done--took up his pen to entreat Mr Clennam to advance him the sum of Three Pounds Ten Shillings upon his I.O.U., which he begged to enclose.", "아버지가 보낸 편지에는, 자기가 굳게 믿고 의지하던 시내로부터의 송금이 어그러지는, 난생처음 겪는 처지에 뜻밖에도 놓이게 되어, 스물세 해에 걸친(이중으로 밑줄이 그어져 있었다) 그 불행한 수감의 사정에 매여 있는 탓에—그렇지 않았다면 분명히 그러했을 터인데도—직접 찾아가지 못하고 펜을 들었노라고, 펜을 들어 동봉한 차용 증서를 담보로 삼 파운드 십 실링을 융통해 주십사 클레넘 씨에게 간청한다고 적혀 있었다."], ["That from the son set forth that Mr Clennam would, he knew, be gratified to hear that he had at length obtained permanent employment of a highly satisfactory nature, accompanied with every prospect of complete success in life; but that the temporary inability of his employer to pay him his arrears of salary to that date (in which condition said employer had appealed to that generous forbearance in which he trusted he should never be wanting towards a fellow-creature), combined with the fraudulent conduct of a false friend and the present high price of provisions, had reduced him to the verge of ruin, unless he could by a quarter before six that evening raise the sum of eight pounds.", "아들이 보낸 편지에는, 자기가 마침내 더없이 만족스러운 성격의 영구적인 일자리를 얻었으며, 인생에서 완전한 성공을 거둘 온갖 전망까지 곁들여졌다는 소식을 클레넘 씨가 들으면 기뻐하실 줄로 안다고 적혀 있었다. 다만 그의 고용주가 현재까지의 밀린 봉급을 지급할 형편이 일시적으로 못 되는 데다(그런 처지에서 그 고용주는, 동료 인간을 향해 결코 모자람이 없기를 바라는 그 너그러운 관용에 호소해 왔다고 했다), 거짓된 벗의 사기 행각과 지금의 높은 물가가 겹쳐, 그날 저녁 여섯 시 십오 분 전까지 팔 파운드를 마련하지 못하면 그는 파멸의 벼랑에 몰리게 된다고 했다."], ["This sum, Mr Clennam would be happy to learn, he had, through the promptitude of several friends who had a lively confidence in his probity, already raised, with the exception of a trifling balance of one pound seventeen and fourpence; the loan of which balance, for the period of one month, would be fraught with the usual beneficent consequences.", "다행히 클레넘 씨가 들으면 기뻐할 일이지만, 그의 정직함을 활기차게 믿어 주는 여러 벗들의 신속한 도움 덕분에, 그는 일 파운드 십칠 실링 사 펜스라는 사소한 잔액만 빼고 이미 그 돈을 마련했는데, 그 잔액을 한 달간만 빌려 주신다면 늘 그러하듯 자애로운 결과가 가득하리라고 했다."]], [["These letters Clennam answered with the aid of his pencil and pocket-book, on the spot; sending the father what he asked for, and excusing himself from compliance with the demand of the son.", "클레넘은 그 자리에서 연필과 수첩의 도움을 받아 이 편지들에 답을 적었다. 아버지에게는 청한 것을 보내고, 아들의 요구에 응하는 것은 사양하는 말을 했다."], ["He then commissioned Maggy to return with his replies, and gave her the shilling of which the failure of her supplemental enterprise would have disappointed her otherwise.", "그러고는 매기에게 그 답신들을 가지고 돌아가도록 시켰고, 그녀의 부수적인 사업이 실패하여 그녀가 받지 못할 뻔했던 일 실링을 주었다."]], [["When he rejoined Little Dorrit, and they had begun walking as before, she said all at once:", "그가 다시 리틀 도릿에게 돌아와 두 사람이 아까처럼 걷기 시작했을 때, 그녀가 느닷없이 말했다."]], [["‘I think I had better go.", "\"저는 가는 게 좋겠어요."], ["I had better go home.’", "집에 가는 게 좋겠어요.\""]], [["‘Don’t be distressed,’ said Clennam, ‘I have answered the letters.", "\"괴로워하지 마세요,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["They were nothing.", "\"편지에는 답을 했어요."], ["You know what they were.", "별것 아니었어요. 어떤 내용인지 아시잖아요."], ["They were nothing.’", "별것 아니었어요.\""]], [["‘But I am afraid,’ she returned, ‘to leave him, I am afraid to leave any of them.", "\"하지만 저는 두려워요,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["When I am gone, they pervert--but they don’t mean it--even Maggy.’", "\"그분 곁을 떠나는 게 두려워요, 그들 중 누구의 곁이든 떠나는 게 두려워요. 제가 없으면 그들은 비뚤어져요—그러려는 건 아니지만—매기까지도요.\""]], [["‘It was a very innocent commission that she undertook, poor thing.", "\"가엾은 것이 떠맡은 건 아주 순진한 심부름이었어요."], ["And in keeping it secret from you, she supposed, no doubt, that she was only saving you uneasiness.’", "그리고 그걸 당신한테 숨긴 건, 분명히 그저 당신의 걱정을 덜어 주려는 것이라고 여겼을 거예요.\""]], [["‘Yes, I hope so, I hope so.", "\"네, 그랬으면 좋겠어요, 그랬으면 좋겠어요."], ["But I had better go home!", "하지만 저는 집에 가는 게 좋겠어요!"], ["It was but the other day that my sister told me I had become so used to the prison that I had its tone and character.", "바로 며칠 전에 언니가 그러더군요, 내가 감옥에 너무 익숙해져서 그곳의 분위기와 성격을 그대로 지니게 되었다고요."], ["It must be so.", "틀림없이 그럴 거예요."], ["I am sure it must be when I see these things.", "이런 것들을 볼 때면 분명 그러리라는 생각이 들어요."], ["My place is there.", "제 자리는 거기예요."], ["I am better there, it is unfeeling in me to be here, when I can do the least thing there.", "저는 거기 있는 편이 나아요. 거기서 아주 작은 일이라도 할 수 있는데 여기 있는 건 매정한 짓이에요."], ["Good-bye.", "안녕히 가세요."], ["I had far better stay at home!’", "저는 집에 있는 편이 훨씬 나아요!\""]], [["The agonised way in which she poured this out, as if it burst of itself from her suppressed heart, made it difficult for Clennam to keep the tears from his eyes as he saw and heard her.", "마치 억눌린 가슴에서 저절로 터져 나오는 듯, 그녀가 이 말을 쏟아 내는 그 괴로운 모양새를 보고 들으면서, 클레넘은 눈에서 눈물이 솟는 것을 참기가 어려웠다."]], [["‘Don’t call it home, my child!’ he entreated.", "\"그곳을 집이라고 부르지 말아요, 내 아이여!\" 그가 간청했다."], ["‘It is always painful to me to hear you call it home.’", "\"당신이 그곳을 집이라고 부르는 걸 듣는 건 언제나 제겐 고통스러워요.\""]], [["‘But it is home!", "\"하지만 거기가 집인걸요!"], ["What else can I call home?", "달리 어디를 집이라 부르겠어요?"], ["Why should I ever forget it for a single moment?’", "어째서 제가 단 한순간이라도 그곳을 잊어야 한단 말인가요?\""]], [["‘You never do, dear Little Dorrit, in any good and true service.’", "\"당신은 결코 잊지 않아요, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿, 그 어떤 선하고 참된 봉사 속에서도요.\""]], [["‘I hope not, O I hope not!", "\"안 잊었으면, 오 안 잊었으면 좋겠어요!"], ["But it is better for me to stay there; much better, much more dutiful, much happier.", "하지만 저는 거기 있는 편이 나아요. 훨씬 낫고, 훨씬 도리에 맞고, 훨씬 행복해요."], ["Please don’t go with me, let me go by myself.", "부디 저와 함께 가지 마세요, 저 혼자 가게 해 주세요."], ["Good-bye, God bless you.", "안녕히 가세요, 신의 가호가 있기를."], ["Thank you, thank you.’", "고마워요, 고마워요.\""]], [["He felt that it was better to respect her entreaty, and did not move while her slight form went quickly away from him.", "그는 그녀의 간청을 존중하는 편이 낫다고 느껴, 그녀의 가냘픈 모습이 그에게서 빠르게 멀어져 가는 동안 움직이지 않았다."], ["When it had fluttered out of sight, he turned his face towards the water and stood thinking.", "그 모습이 팔랑이며 시야에서 사라지자, 그는 얼굴을 물 쪽으로 돌리고 서서 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["She would have been distressed at any time by this discovery of the letters; but so much so, and in that unrestrainable way?", "그녀는 언제든 이 편지들의 발각에 괴로워했을 것이다. 그러나 이토록, 그처럼 걷잡을 수 없는 방식으로 괴로워했을까? 아니다."]], [["No. When she had seen her father begging with his threadbare disguise on, when she had entreated him not to give her father money, she had been distressed, but not like this.", "그녀가 아버지가 해진 가장(假裝)을 두른 채 구걸하는 것을 보았을 때, 자기 아버지에게 돈을 주지 말아 달라고 그에게 애원했을 때, 그녀는 괴로워했으나 이와 같지는 않았다."], ["Something had made her keenly and additionally sensitive just now.", "무언가가 바로 지금 그녀를 예리하게, 한층 더 예민하게 만든 것이다."], ["Now, was there some one in the hopeless unattainable distance?", "그렇다면 희망 없고 닿을 수 없는 먼 곳에 누군가가 있는 것일까?"], ["Or had the suspicion been brought into his mind, by his own associations of the troubled river running beneath the bridge with the same river higher up, its changeless tune upon the prow of the ferry-boat, so many miles an hour the peaceful flowing of the stream, here the rushes, there the lilies, nothing uncertain or unquiet?", "아니면, 다리 아래로 흐르는 그 어지러운 강물과, 그것의 상류에서 똑같이 흐르던 그 강물—나룻배 이물에 부딪치며 변함없는 가락을 내고, 시간당 그토록 여러 마일을 평화로이 흐르며, 여기엔 골풀, 저기엔 백합, 아무것도 불확실하거나 불온하지 않던 그 강물—을 떠올리는 그 자신의 연상 작용이 이런 의혹을 그의 마음에 불러일으킨 것일까?"]], [["He thought of his poor child, Little Dorrit, for a long time there; he thought of her going home; he thought of her in the night; he thought of her when the day came round again. And the poor child Little Dorrit thought of him--too faithfully, ah, too faithfully!--in the shadow of the Marshalsea wall.", "그는 그곳에서 오래도록 그의 가엾은 아이 리틀 도릿을 생각했다. 그는 그녀가 집으로 돌아가는 것을 생각했다. 그는 밤에 그녀를 생각했다. 그는 다시 날이 밝아 올 때 그녀를 생각했다. 그리고 가엾은 아이 리틀 도릿은, 마셜시 담장의 그늘 아래에서 그를 생각했다—너무도 한결같이, 아, 너무도 한결같이!"]], [["# CHAPTER 23. Machinery in Motion", "# CHAPTER 23. Machinery in Motion"]], [["Mr Meagles bestirred himself with such prompt activity in the matter of the negotiation with Daniel Doyce which Clennam had entrusted to him, that he soon brought it into business train, and called on Clennam at nine o’clock one morning to make his report.", "클레넘이 자기에게 맡긴 대니얼 도이스와의 교섭 건에 대해, 미글스 씨는 어찌나 재빠르고 활발하게 움직였던지 이내 그것을 사무적인 궤도에 올려놓았고, 어느 날 아침 아홉 시에 클레넘을 찾아와 보고를 했다."]], [["‘Doyce is highly gratified by your good opinion,’ he opened the business by saying, ‘and desires nothing so much as that you should examine the affairs of the Works for yourself, and entirely understand them.", "\"도이스는 자네의 호의를 무척 고맙게 여기고 있네.\" 그는 이렇게 운을 떼며 본론에 들어갔다."], ["He has handed me the keys of all his books and papers--here they are jingling in this pocket--and the only charge he has given me is “Let Mr Clennam have the means of putting himself on a perfect equality with me as to knowing whatever I know.", "\"그리고 무엇보다도 자네가 직접 공장의 사정을 조사하여 그것을 온전히 파악해 주기를 바라고 있어. 그가 자기 장부와 서류 일체의 열쇠를 내게 넘겨주었는데—여기 이 주머니에서 짤랑거리는 게 그거야—그가 내게 준 단 한 가지 당부는 이거였네."], ["If it should come to nothing after all, he will respect my confidence.", "'클레넘 씨가 내가 아는 것이라면 무엇이든 알 수 있도록, 나와 완전히 동등한 위치에 설 수단을 갖게 해 주시오. 설령 결국 아무 일도 성사되지 않더라도, 그분은 내 신뢰를 존중해 주실 거요."], ["Unless I was sure of that to begin with, I should have nothing to do with him.”", "처음부터 그걸 확신할 수 없었다면, 나는 그분과 아예 관계를 맺지 않았을 테니.' 자, 보게나.\""], ["And there, you see,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘you have Daniel Doyce all over.’", "미글스 씨가 말했다. \"여기에 대니얼 도이스라는 사람이 고스란히 드러나 있지 않은가.\""]], [["‘A very honourable character.’", "\"매우 명예로운 인품이군요.\""]], [["‘Oh, yes, to be sure.", "\"오, 그렇지, 암."], ["Not a doubt of it.", "의심할 여지가 없네."], ["Odd, but very honourable.", "별나지만, 아주 명예로워. 하지만 정말 별나단 말이야."], ["Very odd though.", "그런데 자네 이걸 믿겠나, 클레넘?\""], ["Now, would you believe, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, with a hearty enjoyment of his friend’s eccentricity, ‘that I had a whole morning in What’s-his-name Yard--’", "미글스 씨가 친구의 괴짜다움을 진심으로 즐기며 말했다. \"내가 그—뭐라더라 그 마당에서 꼬박 한나절을—\""]], [["‘Bleeding Heart?’", "\"블리딩 하트 말입니까?\""]], [["‘A whole morning in Bleeding Heart Yard, before I could induce him to pursue the subject at all?’", "\"블리딩 하트 마당에서 꼬박 한나절을 보내고서야 겨우 그 사람더러 그 얘기를 꺼내게 만들었다네.\""]], [["‘How was that?’", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["‘How was that, my friend?", "\"어째서냐고, 이 사람아?"], ["I no sooner mentioned your name in connection with it than he declared off.’", "내가 그 일과 관련해 자네 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 그는 손사래를 치더란 말이야.\""]], [["‘Declared off on my account?’", "\"제 때문에 손사래를 쳤다고요?\""]], [["‘I no sooner mentioned your name, Clennam, than he said, “That will never do!”", "\"내가 자네 이름을 꺼내자마자, 클레넘, 그가 이러더군. '그건 결코 안 될 일이오!'"], ["What did he mean by that?", "그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 내가 물었지. '아무것도 아니오, 미글스."], ["I asked him.", "그저 결코 안 될 일이라는 거요.'"], ["No matter, Meagles; that would never do.", "어째서 결코 안 될 일이냐? 자네 좀처럼 믿기 힘들겠지만, 클레넘,\""], ["Why would it never do?", "미글스 씨가 속으로 웃으며 말했다. \"이런 사연이 드러났어."], ["You’ll hardly believe it, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, laughing within himself, ‘but it came out that it would never do, because you and he, walking down to Twickenham together, had glided into a friendly conversation in the course of which he had referred to his intention of taking a partner, supposing at the time that you were as firmly and finally settled as St Paul’s Cathedral.", "그건 결코 안 될 일이라는 게, 자네와 그 사람이 트위크넘으로 함께 걸어 내려가면서 다정한 대화에 빠져들었는데, 그 도중에 그가 동업자를 들일 작정이라고 언급한 적이 있었기 때문이라는 거야. 그때 그는 자네가 세인트폴 대성당처럼 확고하고 최종적으로 자리 잡은 사람이라고 여겼던 거지."], ["“Whereas,”", "'그런데,'"], ["says he, “Mr Clennam might now believe, if I entertained his proposition, that I had a sinister and designing motive in what was open free speech.", "하고 그가 말하더군. '내가 그분의 제안을 받아들인다면, 클레넘 씨는 이제 와서, 내가 솔직하고 거리낌 없이 한 그 말 속에 음흉하고 계산된 속셈이 있었다고 믿게 될지도 모르오."], ["Which I can’t bear,”", "그건 나로서는 견딜 수가 없소.'"], ["says he, “which I really am too proud to bear.”’", "그가 말했네. '정말이지 나는 그것을 견디기엔 너무 자존심이 강하오.'\""]], [["‘I should as soon suspect--’", "\"저라면 의심하기는커녕—\""]], [["‘Of course you would,’ interrupted Mr Meagles, ‘and so I told him.", "\"물론 그렇겠지.\" 미글스 씨가 가로막았다."], ["But it took a morning to scale that wall; and I doubt if any other man than myself (he likes me of old) could have got his leg over it. Well, Clennam.", "\"나도 그 사람에게 그렇게 말했다네."], ["This business-like obstacle surmounted, he then stipulated that before resuming with you I should look over the books and form my own opinion.", "그렇지만 그 벽을 넘는 데 한나절이 걸렸어. 나 말고 다른 누가(그는 나와 오래 알고 지냈으니 나를 좋아하지) 그 벽을 넘을 수 있었을지 의문이야."], ["I looked over the books, and formed my own opinion.", "자, 클레넘. 이 사무적인 장애물을 넘고 나니, 그는 자네와 다시 이야기를 재개하기 전에 내가 먼저 장부를 살펴보고 나름의 의견을 정해 달라는 조건을 붙였네."], ["“Is it, on the whole, for, or against?”", "그래서 내가 장부를 살펴보고 나름의 의견을 정했지. '전체적으로 보아 찬성이오, 반대요?'"], ["says he. “For,”", "하고 그가 묻더군."], ["says I.", "'찬성이오.'"], ["“Then,”", "내가 답했지."], ["says he, “you may now, my good friend, give Mr Clennam the means of forming his opinion.", "'그러면,' 하고 그가 말하기를, '이제 자네는, 친애하는 벗이여, 클레넘 씨가 스스로 의견을 정할 수단을 그분에게 드려도 좋소."], ["To enable him to do which, without bias and with perfect freedom, I shall go out of town for a week.”", "그분이 어떤 편견도 없이 완전히 자유롭게 그리할 수 있도록, 나는 일주일간 도시를 떠나 있겠소.' 그래서 그는 떠났네.\""], ["And he’s gone,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘that’s the rich conclusion of the thing.’", "미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그게 이 일의 알찬 결말이야.\""]], [["‘Leaving me,’ said Clennam, ‘with a high sense, I must say, of his candour and his--’", "\"그렇다면 저로서는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그분의 솔직함과 그—그 무엇이라 해야 할지, 그것에 대해 깊은 감명을 받았다고 말씀드려야겠군요.\""]], [["‘Oddity,’ Mr Meagles struck in.", "\"별남이지.\" 미글스 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["‘I should think so!’", "\"내가 보기엔 그래!\""]], [["It was not exactly the word on Clennam’s lips, but he forbore to interrupt his good-humoured friend.", "그것이 클레넘의 입에 떠오른 단어와 꼭 같지는 않았으나, 그는 기분 좋은 친구의 말을 가로막지 않았다."]], [["‘And now,’ added Mr Meagles, ‘you can begin to look into matters as soon as you think proper.", "\"자, 그러니 이제,\" 미글스 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["I have undertaken to explain where you may want explanation, but to be strictly impartial, and to do nothing more.’", "\"자네가 적당하다고 생각하는 대로 곧 일에 착수하면 되네. 설명이 필요한 데가 있으면 내가 설명을 맡기로 했네만, 엄정히 공평을 지키고 그 이상은 아무것도 하지 않기로 했어.\""]], [["They began their perquisitions in Bleeding Heart Yard that same forenoon.", "그들은 바로 그날 오전부터 블리딩 하트 마당에서 면밀한 조사를 시작했다."], ["Little peculiarities were easily to be detected by experienced eyes in Mr Doyce’s way of managing his affairs, but they almost always involved some ingenious simplification of a difficulty, and some plain road to the desired end.", "도이스 씨가 자기 사업을 운영하는 방식에는 숙련된 눈으로 보면 자잘한 특이점들이 쉽게 눈에 띄었지만, 그것들은 거의 언제나 난점을 기발하게 단순화하는 무언가와, 원하는 목적에 이르는 명백한 지름길을 담고 있었다."], ["That his papers were in arrear, and that he stood in need of assistance to develop the capacity of his business, was clear enough; but all the results of his undertakings during many years were distinctly set forth, and were ascertainable with ease.", "그의 서류가 밀려 있다는 것, 그리고 사업의 역량을 키우려면 도움이 필요하다는 것은 충분히 분명했다. 그러나 여러 해에 걸친 그의 사업 성과는 모두 또렷이 기록되어 있어 어렵잖게 확인할 수 있었다."], ["Nothing had been done for the purposes of the pending investigation; everything was in its genuine working dress, and in a certain honest rugged order.", "이번 조사를 위해 따로 손본 것은 아무것도 없었다. 모든 것이 진짜 작업복 차림 그대로, 어떤 정직하고 투박한 질서 속에 놓여 있었다."], ["The calculations and entries, in his own hand, of which there were many, were bluntly written, and with no very neat precision; but were always plain and directed straight to the purpose.", "그가 직접 손으로 쓴 계산과 기재가 많았는데, 그것들은 무뚝뚝하게 적혀 있어 그다지 깔끔하거나 정밀하지는 않았지만, 언제나 명료했고 곧장 목적을 향했다."], ["It occurred to Arthur that a far more elaborate and taking show of business--such as the records of the Circumlocution Office made perhaps--might be far less serviceable, as being meant to be far less intelligible.", "아서는 훨씬 정교하고 보기 좋은 사업 외양—이를테면 에두름국의 기록 같은 것—이 오히려 훨씬 덜 쓸모 있으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그것은 훨씬 덜 알아먹게 할 의도로 만들어진 것이기 때문이었다."]], [["Three or four days of steady application tendered him master of all the facts it was essential to become acquainted with.", "사나흘 꾸준히 매달리고 나니 그는 꼭 알아 두어야 할 사실을 모두 익혔다."], ["Mr Meagles was at hand the whole time, always ready to illuminate any dim place with the bright little safety-lamp belonging to the scales and scoop.", "미글스 씨는 그 내내 곁에 있으면서, 저울과 됫박에 속한 그 작고 밝은 안전등으로 어두운 데를 늘 기꺼이 비춰 주었다."], ["Between them they agreed upon the sum it would be fair to offer for the purchase of a half-share in the business, and then Mr Meagles unsealed a paper in which Daniel Doyce had noted the amount at which he valued it; which was even something less.", "두 사람은 사업의 절반 지분을 사들이는 데 적정한 금액을 합의했고, 그러자 미글스 씨가 봉인된 종이 한 장을 열어 보였는데, 거기에는 대니얼 도이스가 그 가치를 매겨 적어 둔 액수가 있었다. 그것은 오히려 조금 더 낮은 금액이었다."], ["Thus, when Daniel came back, he found the affair as good as concluded.", "이리하여 대니얼이 돌아왔을 때, 그는 일이 거의 매듭지어진 것을 발견했다."]], [["‘And I may now avow, Mr Clennam,’ said he, with a cordial shake of the hand, ‘that if I had looked high and low for a partner, I believe I could not have found one more to my mind.’", "\"이제는 털어놓아도 되겠습니다, 클레넘 씨.\" 그는 진심 어린 악수를 나누며 말했다. \"내가 위아래로 샅샅이 뒤져 동업자를 찾았다 한들, 이보다 더 내 마음에 드는 사람을 찾지는 못했을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I say the same,’ said Clennam.", "\"저도 같은 말을 드립니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘And I say of both of you,’ added Mr Meagles, ‘that you are well matched.", "\"나는 두 분 다에 대해 이렇게 말하겠소.\" 미글스 씨가 덧붙였다."], ["You keep him in check, Clennam, with your common sense, and you stick to the Works, Dan, with your--’", "\"두 분은 천생연분이오. 클레넘, 자네는 자네의 상식으로 이 사람을 단속하고, 댄, 자네는 공장에 자네의 그—\""]], [["‘Uncommon sense?’ suggested Daniel, with his quiet smile.", "\"비범한 식견으로 말입니까?\" 대니얼이 조용한 미소를 띠며 거들었다."]], [["‘You may call it so, if you like--and each of you will be a right hand to the other.", "\"원한다면 그렇게 불러도 좋소—그리고 두 사람은 서로에게 오른팔이 되어 줄 거요."], ["Here’s my own right hand upon it, as a practical man, to both of you.’", "자, 실무가로서 내 오른손을 여기 두 분께 내밀어 그 약속을 다집니다.\""]], [["The purchase was completed within a month.", "매입은 한 달 안에 마무리되었다. 그것으로 아서에게는 사적인 재산이라곤 몇백 파운드를 넘지 않는 정도밖에 남지 않았지만, 활동적이고 전도유망한 진로가 그에게 열렸다."], ["It left Arthur in possession of private personal means not exceeding a few hundred pounds; but it opened to him an active and promising career.", "세 친구는 이 경사스러운 날 함께 식사했다. 공장과 공장 식구들, 아내들과 아이들도 휴일을 맞아 함께 식사했다."], ["The three friends dined together on the auspicious occasion; the factory and the factory wives and children made holiday and dined too; even Bleeding Heart Yard dined and was full of meat.", "블리딩 하트 마당까지도 식사를 하며 고기로 가득 찼다. 그러나 채 두 달도 지나지 않아 블리딩 하트 마당은 또다시 모자란 배급에 너무도 익숙해진 나머지 그 잔치를 잊어버렸다."], ["Two months had barely gone by in all, when Bleeding Heart Yard had become so familiar with short-commons again, that the treat was forgotten there; when nothing seemed new in the partnership but the paint of the inscription on the door-posts, DOYCE AND CLENNAM; when it appeared even to Clennam himself, that he had had the affairs of the firm in his mind for years.", "동업에서 새로운 것이라고는 문기둥에 새 페인트로 쓴 글귀, '도이스 앤드 클레넘'밖에 없어 보였다. 클레넘 자신에게조차 자기가 이 회사의 일을 여러 해 동안 머릿속에 담아 온 것처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["The little counting-house reserved for his own occupation, was a room of wood and glass at the end of a long low workshop, filled with benches, and vices, and tools, and straps, and wheels; which, when they were in gear with the steam-engine, went tearing round as though they had a suicidal mission to grind the business to dust and tear the factory to pieces.", "그의 전용으로 따로 둔 작은 사무실은, 길고 나지막한 작업장 끝에 있는 나무와 유리로 된 방이었다. 작업장은 작업대와 죔쇠, 연장, 가죽 띠, 바퀴 들로 가득했는데, 이것들이 증기기관과 맞물려 돌아갈 때면, 마치 사업을 가루로 갈아 버리고 공장을 산산조각 내려는 자살의 사명이라도 띤 듯 미친 듯이 돌아갔다."], ["A communication of great trap-doors in the floor and roof with the workshop above and the workshop below, made a shaft of light in this perspective, which brought to Clennam’s mind the child’s old picture-book, where similar rays were the witnesses of Abel’s murder.", "바닥과 천장에 난 커다란 들창문들이 위층 작업장 및 아래층 작업장과 통해 있어, 이 풍경 속에 한 줄기 빛기둥을 드리웠는데, 그것은 비슷한 빛줄기가 아벨의 살해를 증언하던 어린 시절의 옛 그림책을 클레넘의 마음속에 떠올리게 했다."], ["The noises were sufficiently removed and shut out from the counting-house to blend into a busy hum, interspersed with periodical clinks and thumps.", "소음들은 사무실로부터 충분히 멀리 떨어져 차단되어 있어, 주기적으로 들려오는 쇳소리와 쿵쿵거림 사이로 분주한 윙윙거림으로 뒤섞였다."], ["The patient figures at work were swarthy with the filings of iron and steel that danced on every bench and bubbled up through every chink in the planking.", "묵묵히 일하는 사람들의 모습은 작업대마다 춤추고 널빤지 틈마다 부글거려 올라오는 쇳가루와 강철 부스러기로 거뭇거뭇했다."], ["The workshop was arrived at by a step-ladder from the outer yard below, where it served as a shelter for the large grindstone where tools were sharpened.", "작업장은 아래 바깥마당에서 사다리를 타고 올라가게 되어 있었는데, 그 사다리 밑은 연장을 가는 커다란 숫돌의 가림막 노릇을 했다."], ["The whole had at once a fanciful and practical air in Clennam’s eyes, which was a welcome change; and, as often as he raised them from his first work of getting the array of business documents into perfect order, he glanced at these things with a feeling of pleasure in his pursuit that was new to him.", "그 전체가 클레넘의 눈에는 한꺼번에 환상적이면서도 실용적인 분위기를 띠었고, 그것은 반가운 변화였다. 그래서 그는 사업 서류들을 완벽한 질서로 정돈하는 첫 작업에서 눈을 들 때마다, 자기 일에서 느끼는 새로운 즐거움으로 그것들을 바라보곤 했다."]], [["Raising his eyes thus one day, he was surprised to see a bonnet labouring up the step-ladder.", "그러던 어느 날 이렇게 눈을 들다가, 그는 보닛 하나가 사다리를 힘겹게 올라오는 것을 보고 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["The unusual apparition was followed by another bonnet.", "이 뜻밖의 출현 뒤를 또 다른 보닛이 따랐다."], ["He then perceived that the first bonnet was on the head of Mr F.’s Aunt, and that the second bonnet was on the head of Flora, who seemed to have propelled her legacy up the steep ascent with considerable difficulty.", "그제야 그는 첫 번째 보닛이 F씨의 고모 머리에 얹혀 있고, 두 번째 보닛이 플로라의 머리에 얹혀 있음을 알아보았는데, 플로라는 그 유산을 가파른 비탈 위로 상당한 곤란을 무릅쓰고 떠밀어 올린 듯했다."]], [["Though not altogether enraptured at the sight of these visitors, Clennam lost no time in opening the counting-house door, and extricating them from the workshop; a rescue which was rendered the more necessary by Mr F.’s Aunt already stumbling over some impediment, and menacing steam power as an Institution with a stony reticule she carried.", "이 방문객들을 보고 마냥 황홀해진 것은 아니었으나, 클레넘은 지체 없이 사무실 문을 열고 그들을 작업장에서 빼냈다. 이 구조는 더욱 필요한 것이었으니, F씨의 고모가 벌써 무언가에 걸려 비틀거리며 손에 든 돌처럼 단단한 손가방으로 증기 동력을 하나의 제도로서 위협하고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘Good gracious, Arthur,--I should say Mr Clennam, far more proper--the climb we have had to get up here and how ever to get down again without a fire-escape and Mr F.’s Aunt slipping through the steps and bruised all over and you in the machinery and foundry way too only think, and never told us!’", "\"어머나 세상에, 아서—아니, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 훨씬 적절하겠죠—여기 올라오느라 이 고생이라니, 게다가 비상 사다리도 없이 어떻게 다시 내려가나, F씨의 고모님은 계단 사이로 빠져 온통 멍이 들고, 게다가 당신은 이 기계니 주물이니 하는 일에 들어와 있다니 글쎄 생각 좀 해 봐요, 게다가 우리한테는 한마디도 안 했죠!\""]], [["Thus, Flora, out of breath.", "이렇게 플로라가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["Meanwhile, Mr F.’s Aunt rubbed her esteemed insteps with her umbrella, and vindictively glared.", "그동안 F씨의 고모는 자신의 소중한 발등을 우산으로 문지르며 앙심에 찬 눈으로 노려보았다."]], [["‘Most unkind never to have come back to see us since that day, though naturally it was not to be expected that there should be any attraction at _our_ house and you were much more pleasantly engaged, that’s pretty certain, and is she fair or dark blue eyes or black I wonder, not that I expect that she should be anything but a perfect contrast to me in all particulars for I am a disappointment as I very well know and you are quite right to be devoted no doubt though what I am saying Arthur never mind I hardly know myself Good gracious!’", "\"그날 이후로 우리를 보러 한 번도 돌아오지 않다니 정말 너무 매정해요, 하긴 당연히 우리 집에 무슨 끌릴 만한 게 있을 리 없고 당신은 훨씬 더 즐거운 일에 매여 있었겠죠, 그건 거의 틀림없고, 그런데 그 사람은 금발일까 흑발일까 푸른 눈일까 검은 눈일까 궁금하네요, 하긴 그녀가 모든 면에서 나와 완벽한 대조가 아니리라곤 기대하지 않아요, 나야 나도 잘 알다시피 실망스러운 사람이니까요, 당신이 헌신적인 건 의심할 바 없이 당연한 일이지요, 하긴 내가 무슨 말을 하는 건지 아서 신경 쓰지 말아요 나도 나를 잘 모르겠으니 어머나 세상에!\""]], [["By this time he had placed chairs for them in the counting-house.", "이때쯤 그는 사무실 안에 그들이 앉을 의자를 놓아 두었다."], ["As Flora dropped into hers, she bestowed the old look upon him.", "플로라는 의자에 풀썩 주저앉으며 그에게 옛날 그 눈길을 보냈다."]], [["‘And to think of Doyce and Clennam, and who Doyce can be,’ said Flora; ‘delightful man no doubt and married perhaps or perhaps a daughter, now has he really?", "\"도이스 앤드 클레넘이라니, 게다가 도이스란 또 누구일까요,\" 플로라가 말했다."], ["then one understands the partnership and sees it all, don’t tell me anything about it for I know I have no claim to ask the question the golden chain that once was forged being snapped and very proper.’", "\"틀림없이 유쾌한 사람일 테고 어쩌면 결혼했거나 아니면 딸이 있을지도, 자 정말 그래요? 그렇다면 동업이 이해가 되고 모든 게 환히 보이네요, 아무 말도 하지 말아요 내가 그런 질문을 할 자격이 없다는 걸 잘 아니까요, 한때 벼려졌던 황금 사슬이 끊어졌으니 그게 마땅하지요.\""]], [["Flora put her hand tenderly on his, and gave him another of the youthful glances.", "플로라는 그의 손 위에 자기 손을 다정하게 얹고는, 또 한 번 그 젊디젊은 눈길을 그에게 던졌다."]], [["‘Dear Arthur--force of habit, Mr Clennam every way more delicate and adapted to existing circumstances--I must beg to be excused for taking the liberty of this intrusion but I thought I might so far presume upon old times for ever faded never more to bloom as to call with Mr F.’s Aunt to congratulate and offer best wishes, A great deal superior to China not to be denied and much nearer though higher up!’", "\"친애하는 아서—습관의 힘이란, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 모든 면에서 더 섬세하고 지금 형편에 맞겠죠—이렇게 함부로 끼어드는 무례를 부디 용서해 주세요, 하지만 영영 시들어 다시는 피지 않을 옛 시절을 빌미로 이만한 무람없음은 부려도 되지 않을까 싶어서, F씨의 고모님과 함께 들러 축하하고 행운을 빌어 드리려고 왔어요, 중국보다는 훨씬 우월하다는 건 부인할 수 없고 훨씬 가깝긴 한데 더 높이 올라가야 하긴 했지만요!\""]], [["‘I am very happy to see you,’ said Clennam, ‘and I thank you, Flora, very much for your kind remembrance.’", "\"이렇게 와 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그리고 친절히 저를 기억해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다, 플로라.\""]], [["‘More than I can say myself at any rate,’ returned Flora, ‘for I might have been dead and buried twenty distinct times over and no doubt whatever should have been before you had genuinely remembered Me or anything like it in spite of which one last remark I wish to make, one last explanation I wish to offer--’", "\"적어도 나로서야 차마 입에 담을 수 없는 만큼이죠.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 나는 따로따로 스무 번쯤 죽어 묻혔다 해도 당신이 진정으로 나를, 아니 그 비슷한 무엇이라도 기억하기 전에 그랬을 게 틀림없으니까요, 그럼에도 한 가지 마지막 말을 하고 싶어요, 한 가지 마지막 해명을 드리고 싶어요—\""]], [["‘My dear Mrs Finching,’ Arthur remonstrated in alarm.", "\"이런, 핀칭 부인.\" 아서가 깜짝 놀라 만류했다."]], [["‘Oh not that disagreeable name, say Flora!’", "\"오 그 듣기 싫은 이름은 말고, 플로라라고 불러요!\""]], [["‘Flora, is it worth troubling yourself afresh to enter into explanations?", "\"플로라, 새삼 수고스럽게 또 해명을 하실 만한 가치가 있겠습니까?"], ["I assure you none are needed.", "정말이지 그런 건 전혀 필요 없습니다."], ["I am satisfied--I am perfectly satisfied.’", "저는 만족합니다—더할 나위 없이 만족합니다.\""]], [["A diversion was occasioned here, by Mr F.’s Aunt making the following inexorable and awful statement:", "여기서 F씨의 고모가 다음과 같은 가차 없고 무시무시한 진술을 내놓는 바람에 분위기가 한 차례 흐트러졌다."]], [["‘There’s mile-stones on the Dover road!’", "\"도버 길에는 이정표가 있어!\""]], [["With such mortal hostility towards the human race did she discharge this missile, that Clennam was quite at a loss how to defend himself; the rather as he had been already perplexed in his mind by the honour of a visit from this venerable lady, when it was plain she held him in the utmost abhorrence.", "그녀가 인류 전체를 향한 그 살벌한 적의를 담아 이 포탄을 발사하는 통에, 클레넘은 어떻게 막아야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡을 수 없었다. 더구나 이 노부인이 자기를 극도로 혐오하고 있음이 분명한데도 방문이라는 영예를 베푸는 까닭에 이미 마음이 어리둥절해 있던 터라 더욱 그랬다."], ["He could not but look at her with disconcertment, as she sat breathing bitterness and scorn, and staring leagues away.", "그녀가 신랄함과 경멸을 내뿜으며 까마득히 먼 곳을 노려보고 앉아 있는 동안, 그는 당혹스러운 눈으로 그녀를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다."], ["Flora, however, received the remark as if it had been of a most apposite and agreeable nature; approvingly observing aloud that Mr F.’s Aunt had a great deal of spirit.", "그러나 플로라는 그 말이 마치 더없이 적절하고 유쾌한 것이라도 되는 양 받아들이며, F씨의 고모가 기개가 대단하다고 소리 내어 흐뭇하게 평했다. 이 칭찬에 자극받았는지 아니면 타오르는 분노에 자극받았는지, 그 존엄한 여인은 이어서 덧붙였다."], ["Stimulated either by this compliment, or by her burning indignation, that illustrious woman then added, ‘Let him meet it if he can!’ And, with a rigid movement of her stony reticule (an appendage of great size and of a fossil appearance), indicated that Clennam was the unfortunate person at whom the challenge was hurled.", "\"할 수 있거든 그가 한번 맞서 보라지!\" 그러고는 돌처럼 단단한 손가방(엄청난 크기에 화석 같은 외양을 한 부속물이었다)을 뻣뻣하게 움직여, 그 도전이 던져진 불운한 상대가 바로 클레넘임을 가리켰다."]], [["‘One last remark,’ resumed Flora, ‘I was going to say I wish to make one last explanation I wish to offer, Mr F.’s Aunt and myself would not have intruded on business hours Mr F.", "\"마지막 말을,\" 플로라가 말을 이었다."], ["having been in business and though the wine trade still business is equally business call it what you will and business habits are just the same as witness Mr F. himself who had his slippers always on the mat at ten minutes before six in the afternoon and his boots inside the fender at ten minutes before eight in the morning to the moment in all weathers light or dark--would not therefore have intruded without a motive which being kindly meant it may be hoped will be kindly taken Arthur, Mr Clennam far more proper, even Doyce and Clennam probably more business-like.’", "\"하려던 참이었어요, 한 가지 마지막 해명을 드리고 싶다고요, F씨의 고모님과 나는 영업 시간에 끼어들지 않았을 거예요, F씨도 사업을 했으니까요, 비록 포도주 거래이긴 했지만 그래도 사업은 사업이고 뭐라 부르든 사업이며 사업 습관이란 다 똑같지요, F씨 본인을 봐도 그래요, 그분은 날씨가 밝든 어둡든 어김없이 오후 여섯 시 십 분 전이면 늘 신발을 매트에 올려 두고 아침 여덟 시 십 분 전이면 부츠를 난로 울 안에 넣어 두셨으니까요—그러니 동기 없이는 끼어들지 않았을 거고, 그 동기가 선의에서 나온 것이니 선의로 받아들여 주시기를 바라요 아서, 클레넘 씨라고 해야 훨씬 적절하고, 어쩌면 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 더 사업가다울지도 모르겠네요.\""]], [["‘Pray say nothing in the way of apology,’ Arthur entreated.", "\"제발 사과 같은 말씀은 마세요.\" 아서가 간청했다."], ["‘You are always welcome.’", "\"두 분은 언제나 환영입니다.\""]], [["‘Very polite of you to say so Arthur--cannot remember Mr Clennam until the word is out, such is the habit of times for ever fled, and so true it is that oft in the stilly night ere slumber’s chain has bound people, fond memory brings the light of other days around people--very polite but more polite than true I am afraid, for to go into the machinery business without so much as sending a line or a card to papa--I don’t say me though there was a time but that is past and stern reality has now my gracious never mind--does not look like it you must confess.’", "\"그렇게 말해 주니 참 점잖네요 아서—클레넘 씨란 말은 입 밖에 나오기 전까진 떠오르질 않아요, 영영 사라진 옛 시절의 습관이 그러하니까요, 고요한 밤이면 흔히, 잠의 사슬이 사람을 옭아매기도 전에, 애틋한 기억이 지난날의 빛을 사람 둘레에 데려온다더니 정말 그래요—참 점잖긴 한데 점잖긴 해도 진심보다 더 점잖은 게 아닐까 두려워요, 왜냐하면 아빠한테 편지 한 줄, 명함 한 장 보내지 않고 기계 사업에 뛰어들다니—내 얘긴 아니에요 한때는 그런 시절도 있었지만 그건 지나갔고 이젠 냉엄한 현실이 나의 어머나 세상에 신경 쓰지 말아요—그건 점잖아 보이진 않잖아요, 당신도 인정해야 할걸요.\""]], [["Even Flora’s commas seemed to have fled on this occasion; she was so much more disjointed and voluble than in the preceding interview.", "이번에는 플로라의 쉼표마저 달아나 버린 듯했다. 그만큼 그녀는 앞서 만났을 때보다 훨씬 더 두서없고 말이 많았다."]], [["‘Though indeed,’ she hurried on, ‘nothing else is to be expected and why should it be expected and if it’s not to be expected why should it be, and I am far from blaming you or any one, When your mama and my papa worried us to death and severed the golden bowl--I mean bond but I dare say you know what I mean and if you don’t you don’t lose much and care just as little I will venture to add--when they severed the golden bond that bound us and threw us into fits of crying on the sofa nearly choked at least myself everything was changed and in giving my hand to Mr F.", "\"하긴 정말,\" 그녀가 서둘러 말을 이었다."], ["I know I did so with my eyes open but he was so very unsettled and in such low spirits that he had distractedly alluded to the river if not oil of something from the chemist’s and I did it for the best.’", "\"다른 걸 기대할 것도 없고 왜 기대하겠으며 기대할 게 아니라면 왜 그래야겠어요, 나는 당신이나 누구를 탓할 마음은 조금도 없어요, 당신 어머니와 우리 아빠가 우리를 죽도록 들볶아 그 황금 사발을—아니 결속을 끊었을 때, 하긴 무슨 말인지 아실 테고 모른다 해도 잃을 것도 없고 마찬가지로 신경도 안 쓰리란 건 감히 덧붙여 두지요—우리를 묶고 있던 황금 결속을 끊고 우리를 소파 위에서 울음 발작에 빠뜨려 적어도 나는 거의 숨이 막힐 지경이었을 때, 모든 게 달라졌고 F씨에게 손을 내밀면서 나는 두 눈 똑똑히 뜨고 그리한 줄 알지만 그분이 어찌나 불안정하고 어찌나 의기소침해 있던지 강물을, 아니면 약방에서 파는 무슨 기름이라도, 정신없이 들먹였기에 나는 그게 최선이라 여겨 그리한 거예요.\""]], [["‘My good Flora, we settled that before.", "\"고마운 플로라, 그 얘기는 우리가 전에 매듭지었잖습니까."], ["It was all quite right.’", "다 정말 옳은 일이었어요.\""]], [["‘It’s perfectly clear you think so,’ returned Flora, ‘for you take it very coolly, if I hadn’t known it to be China I should have guessed myself the Polar regions, dear Mr Clennam you are right however and I cannot blame you but as to Doyce and Clennam papa’s property being about here we heard it from Pancks and but for him we never should have heard one word about it I am satisfied.’", "\"당신이 그렇게 생각하는 게 아주 분명하네요.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"왜냐하면 아주 태연하게 받아들이니까요, 중국인 줄 몰랐다면 나는 북극 지방인가 했을 거예요, 친애하는 클레넘 씨 그래도 당신 말이 옳고 나는 당신을 탓할 수 없지만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아빠 소유지 근처에 있다는 것은 우리가 팽크스한테 들었어요, 그 사람이 아니었다면 우리는 그것에 대해 한마디도 못 들었을 거예요 그건 분명해요.\""]], [["‘No, no, don’t say that.’", "\"아니, 아니요, 그런 말씀 마세요.\""]], [["‘What nonsense not to say it Arthur--Doyce and Clennam--easier and less trying to me than Mr Clennam--when I know it and you know it too and can’t deny it.’", "\"그런 말 안 할 까닭이 어디 있어요 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘이—클레넘 씨라고 부르는 것보다 내겐 더 쉽고 덜 거북해요—내가 알고 당신도 알고 부인할 수도 없는데요.\""]], [["‘But I do deny it, Flora.", "\"하지만 저는 부인합니다, 플로라."], ["I should soon have made you a friendly visit.’", "저는 머잖아 친구로서 댁을 찾아뵈었을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Ah!’ said Flora, tossing her head.", "\"어머!\" 플로라가 고개를 까닥하며 말했다."], ["‘I dare say!’ and she gave him another of the old looks.", "\"그러시겠죠!\" 그러고는 또 한 번 그 옛날 눈길을 그에게 던졌다."], ["‘However when Pancks told us I made up my mind that Mr F.’s Aunt and I would come and call because when papa--which was before that--happened to mention her name to me and to say that you were interested in her I said at the moment Good gracious why not have her here then when there’s anything to do instead of putting it out.’", "\"그건 그렇고 팽크스가 우리한테 말해 주었을 때, 나는 F씨의 고모님과 함께 와서 들러야겠다고 마음먹었어요, 왜냐하면 아빠가—그건 그 이전 일인데—마침 내게 그녀의 이름을 언급하며 당신이 그녀에게 관심이 있다고 말했을 때, 나는 그 자리에서 어머나 세상에 그렇다면 일거리가 있을 때 밖에다 맡길 게 아니라 여기서 그녀를 쓰면 어떨까 했거든요.\""]], [["‘When you say Her,’ observed Clennam, by this time pretty well bewildered, ‘do you mean Mr F.’s--’", "\"'그녀'라고 하시는 건,\" 이쯤 되자 어지간히 어리둥절해진 클레넘이 끼어들었다. \"F씨의 고모—\""]], [["‘My goodness, Arthur--Doyce and Clennam really easier to me with old remembrances--who ever heard of Mr F.’s Aunt doing needlework and going out by the day?’", "\"세상에, 아서—옛 추억을 떠올리면 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 정말 더 편하네요—F씨의 고모님이 삯바느질을 하고 날품팔이로 나간단 말은 또 누가 들어 봤대요?\""]], [["‘Going out by the day!", "\"날품팔이로 나간다고요!"], ["Do you speak of Little Dorrit?’", "리틀 도릿을 말하는 겁니까?\""]], [["‘Why yes of course,’ returned Flora; ‘and of all the strangest names I ever heard the strangest, like a place down in the country with a turnpike, or a favourite pony or a puppy or a bird or something from a seed-shop to be put in a garden or a flower-pot and come up speckled.’", "\"네 물론이죠.\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"내가 들어 본 가장 이상한 이름 중에서도 가장 이상한 이름이에요, 통행세 받는 시골 어디 동네 이름 같기도 하고, 좋아하는 조랑말이나 강아지나 새 이름 같기도 하고, 아니면 씨앗 가게에서 사다가 정원이나 화분에 심으면 얼룩덜룩 돋아나는 무슨 풀 이름 같기도 하고요.\""]], [["‘Then, Flora,’ said Arthur, with a sudden interest in the conversation, ‘Mr Casby was so kind as to mention Little Dorrit to you, was he?", "\"그렇다면, 플로라,\" 아서가 갑자기 대화에 흥미를 느끼며 말했다."], ["What did he say?’", "\"캐스비 씨가 친절하게도 댁에게 리틀 도릿 이야기를 하셨단 거군요? 뭐라고 하시던가요?\""]], [["‘Oh you know what papa is,’ rejoined Flora, ‘and how aggravatingly he sits looking beautiful and turning his thumbs over and over one another till he makes one giddy if one keeps one’s eyes upon him, he said when we were talking of you--I don’t know who began the subject Arthur (Doyce and Clennam) but I am sure it wasn’t me, at least I hope not but you really must excuse my confessing more on that point.’", "\"오 우리 아빠가 어떤 사람인지 당신도 알잖아요.\" 플로라가 다시 말했다. \"그리고 어찌나 얄밉게도 아름답게 앉아서 엄지손가락을 빙글빙글 돌리는지 눈을 떼지 않고 보고 있으면 어지러울 지경인데, 우리가 당신 얘기를 하다가—누가 그 화제를 꺼냈는지는 모르겠어요 아서(도이스 앤드 클레넘) 하지만 분명 나는 아니었어요, 적어도 아니길 바라지만 그 점에 대해선 더 이상 고백 못 하는 걸 정말 양해해 줘야 해요.\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Arthur.", "\"물론입니다.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘By all means.’", "\"얼마든지요.\""]], [["‘You are very ready,’ pouted Flora, coming to a sudden stop in a captivating bashfulness, ‘that I must admit, Papa said you had spoken of her in an earnest way and I said what I have told you and that’s all.’", "\"참 선뜻이네요.\" 플로라가, 매혹적인 수줍음 속에 별안간 말을 뚝 멈추고는 입을 삐죽이며 말했다. \"그건 인정해야겠어요, 아빠가 당신이 그녀 얘기를 진지하게 했다고 말했고 나는 당신에게 들려준 그 말을 했어요, 그게 전부예요.\""]], [["‘That’s all?’ said Arthur, a little disappointed.", "\"그게 전부입니까?\" 아서가 조금 실망하여 말했다."]], [["‘Except that when Pancks told us of your having embarked in this business and with difficulty persuaded us that it was really you I said to Mr F.’s Aunt then we would come and ask you if it would be agreeable to all parties that she should be engaged at our house when required for I know she often goes to your mama’s and I know that your mama has a very touchy temper Arthur--Doyce and Clennam--or I never might have married Mr F. and might have been at this hour but I am running into nonsense.’", "\"팽크스가 당신이 이 사업에 뛰어들었다고 말해 주고서 그게 정말 당신이라는 걸 우리한테 겨우 납득시켰을 때, 내가 F씨의 고모님에게 그렇다면 우리가 와서 당신에게 물어봐야겠다고 했어요, 필요할 때 그녀를 우리 집에서 쓰는 게 모두에게 좋을지 말이에요, 왜냐하면 그녀가 당신 어머니 댁에 자주 가는 걸 알고, 또 당신 어머니가 성미가 무척 까다로운 분이란 것도 아니까요 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘—안 그랬다면 나는 F씨와 결혼하지 않았을 수도 있고 지금쯤 이 시각에 다른 처지였을 수도 있겠지만 내가 또 부질없는 소리로 빠지네요.\""]], [["‘It was very kind of you, Flora, to think of this.’", "\"이런 생각을 해 주시다니 정말 친절하시군요, 플로라.\""]], [["Poor Flora rejoined with a plain sincerity which became her better than her youngest glances, that she was glad he thought so.", "가엾은 플로라는, 그녀의 가장 젊은 눈길보다 한결 더 잘 어울리는 꾸밈없는 진심으로, 그가 그렇게 생각해 주니 기쁘다고 대답했다."], ["She said it with so much heart that Clennam would have given a great deal to buy his old character of her on the spot, and throw it and the mermaid away for ever.", "어찌나 진심을 담아 말하던지, 클레넘은 그 자리에서 그녀가 품은 자신의 옛 모습을 큰돈을 치르고서라도 사들여, 그것과 인어 따위를 영영 내던져 버리고 싶을 지경이었다."]], [["‘I think, Flora,’ he said, ‘that the employment you can give Little Dorrit, and the kindness you can show her--’", "\"플로라, 제 생각에는,\" 그가 말했다. \"댁이 리틀 도릿에게 줄 수 있는 일거리와, 그녀에게 베풀 수 있는 친절은—\""]], [["‘Yes and I will,’ said Flora, quickly.", "\"네 그렇게 할게요.\" 플로라가 얼른 말했다."]], [["‘I am sure of it--will be a great assistance and support to her.", "\"그게—그녀에게 큰 도움과 버팀목이 되리라 확신합니다."], ["I do not feel that I have the right to tell you what I know of her, for I acquired the knowledge confidentially, and under circumstances that bind me to silence.", "그녀에 관해 제가 아는 바를 댁에게 말씀드릴 권리가 제게 있다고는 느껴지지 않습니다, 그 앎을 비밀리에, 침묵을 지키게끔 묶어 두는 사정 아래서 얻었으니까요."], ["But I have an interest in the little creature, and a respect for her that I cannot express to you.", "하지만 저는 그 가여운 사람에게 관심이 있고, 또 댁에게 다 표현할 수 없을 만큼의 존경심을 품고 있습니다."], ["Her life has been one of such trial and devotion, and such quiet goodness, as you can scarcely imagine.", "그녀의 삶은 댁이 거의 상상도 못 할 만큼 시련과 헌신으로, 또 조용한 선량함으로 점철된 삶이었습니다."], ["I can hardly think of her, far less speak of her, without feeling moved.", "저는 그녀를 떠올리기만 해도, 말하기는커녕, 마음이 움직이지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["Let that feeling represent what I could tell you, and commit her to your friendliness with my thanks.’", "그 마음이 제가 댁에게 들려줄 수 있는 모든 것을 대신한다 여겨 주시고, 제 감사와 함께 그녀를 댁의 우정에 맡깁니다.\""]], [["Once more he put out his hand frankly to poor Flora; once more poor Flora couldn’t accept it frankly, found it worth nothing openly, must make the old intrigue and mystery of it.", "다시 한번 그는 가엾은 플로라에게 솔직하게 손을 내밀었다. 다시 한번 가엾은 플로라는 그것을 솔직하게 받지 못했고, 드러내 놓고는 그것이 아무 값어치 없다 여겼으며, 거기에 옛날의 그 꿍꿍이와 비밀스러움을 입혀야만 했다."], ["As much to her own enjoyment as to his dismay, she covered it with a corner of her shawl as she took it.", "그를 당황케 하는 만큼이나 자기 자신의 즐거움을 위해, 그녀는 그 손을 잡으면서 숄 자락으로 덮어 가렸다."], ["Then, looking towards the glass front of the counting-house, and seeing two figures approaching, she cried with infinite relish, ‘Papa!", "그러더니 사무실의 유리 정면 쪽을 바라보다 두 사람이 다가오는 것을 보고는, 한껏 신이 나서 외쳤다. \"아빠!"], ["Hush, Arthur, for Mercy’s sake!’ and tottered back to her chair with an amazing imitation of being in danger of swooning, in the dread surprise and maidenly flutter of her spirits.", "쉿, 아서, 제발!\" 그러고는 두려운 놀라움과 처녀다운 마음의 설렘에 못 이겨 기절할 위험에 처한 척, 놀랍도록 그럴듯한 흉내를 내며 비틀비틀 자기 의자로 물러났다."]], [["The Patriarch, meanwhile, came inanely beaming towards the counting-house in the wake of Pancks.", "그러는 사이 가부장은 팽크스의 뒤를 따라 멍하니 환한 얼굴로 사무실 쪽으로 다가왔다."], ["Pancks opened the door for him, towed him in, and retired to his own moorings in a corner.", "팽크스가 그를 위해 문을 열고, 그를 끌어들인 다음, 자기 정박지인 한쪽 구석으로 물러났다."]], [["‘I heard from Flora,’ said the Patriarch with his benevolent smile, ‘that she was coming to call, coming to call.", "\"플로라한테서 들었네.\" 가부장이 인자한 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["And being out, I thought I’d come also, thought I’d come also.’", "\"이 애가 자네를 보러 온다고, 보러 온다고 말이야. 나도 마침 나와 있던 길이라 나도 와 봐야겠다, 나도 와 봐야겠다 싶었지.\""]], [["The benign wisdom he infused into this declaration (not of itself profound), by means of his blue eyes, his shining head, and his long white hair, was most impressive.", "그가 그 자체로는 그리 심오할 것 없는 이 선언에, 그 푸른 눈과 빛나는 머리와 길고 흰 머리카락을 동원하여 불어넣은 자비로운 지혜는 참으로 인상적이었다."], ["It seemed worth putting down among the noblest sentiments enunciated by the best of men.", "그것은 가장 훌륭한 사람들이 토해 낸 가장 고귀한 격언들 가운데 기록해 둘 만한 가치가 있어 보였다."], ["Also, when he said to Clennam, seating himself in the proffered chair, ‘And you are in a new business, Mr Clennam?", "또한 그가 권하는 의자에 앉으며 클레넘에게 \"그래, 새 사업을 하신다지요, 클레넘 씨?"], ["I wish you well, sir, I wish you well!’ he seemed to have done benevolent wonders.", "잘되길 빕니다, 선생, 잘되길 빌어요!\"라고 말했을 때도, 그는 마치 자비로운 기적이라도 베푼 듯이 보였다."]], [["‘Mrs Finching has been telling me, sir,’ said Arthur, after making his acknowledgments; the relict of the late Mr F.", "\"핀칭 부인께서 제게 말씀해 주셨습니다, 선생님.\""], ["meanwhile protesting, with a gesture, against his use of that respectable name; ‘that she hopes occasionally to employ the young needlewoman you recommended to my mother.", "아서가 인사를 마친 뒤 말했다—그동안 고인이 된 F씨의 미망인은 그가 그 점잖은 이름을 쓰는 데 손짓으로 항의했다—\"제가 어머니께 추천해 드렸던 그 젊은 바느질꾼을 이따금 부려 쓰고 싶으시다고요."], ["For which I have been thanking her.’", "그래서 제가 부인께 감사를 드리던 참이었습니다.\""]], [["The Patriarch turning his head in a lumbering way towards Pancks, that assistant put up the note-book in which he had been absorbed, and took him in tow.", "가부장이 둔중하게 고개를 팽크스 쪽으로 돌리자, 그 보좌역은 골몰해 있던 수첩을 치우고는 그를 끌어당겼다."]], [["‘You didn’t recommend her, you know,’ said Pancks; ‘how could you?", "\"어르신은 그녀를 추천한 게 아닙니다, 아시잖아요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["You knew nothing about her, you didn’t.", "\"어떻게 추천하셨겠어요? 그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르셨는데요."], ["The name was mentioned to you, and you passed it on.", "그저 어르신께 이름이 거론되었고 그걸 넘겨주신 거죠."], ["That’s what _you_ did.’", "어르신이 하신 건 그거예요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Clennam.", "\"뭐!\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘As she justifies any recommendation, it is much the same thing.’", "\"그녀가 어떤 추천이든 받을 만한 사람임을 증명해 보였으니, 결국 거의 같은 셈이지요.\""]], [["‘You are glad she turns out well,’ said Pancks, ‘but it wouldn’t have been your fault if she had turned out ill.", "\"그녀가 잘 풀려서 기쁘신 거죠.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["The credit’s not yours as it is, and the blame wouldn’t have been yours as it might have been.", "\"하지만 그녀가 잘못 풀렸다 해도 그게 어르신 잘못은 아니었을 겁니다. 지금 와서 그 공이 어르신 것이 아니듯, 잘못됐다 해도 그 탓이 어르신 것은 아니었을 거예요."], ["You gave no guarantee.", "어르신은 아무 보증도 안 하셨으니까요."], ["You knew nothing about her.’", "그녀에 대해 아무것도 모르셨고요.\""]], [["‘You are not acquainted, then,’ said Arthur, hazarding a random question, ‘with any of her family?’", "\"그러면 당신은,\" 아서가 무심코 한마디 던져 보았다. \"그녀의 가족 중 누구와도 아는 사이가 아니로군요?\""]], [["‘Acquainted with any of her family?’ returned Pancks.", "\"그녀의 가족 중 누구와 아는 사이냐고요?\" 팽크스가 받았다."], ["‘How should you be acquainted with any of her family?", "\"어떻게 그녀의 가족 중 누구와 아는 사이일 수 있겠습니까?"], ["You never heard of ‘em.", "그들에 대해 들어 본 적도 없으신데요."], ["You can’t be acquainted with people you never heard of, can you?", "들어 본 적도 없는 사람들과 아는 사이일 순 없잖습니까, 안 그래요?"], ["You should think not!’", "그럴 리 없겠죠!\""]], [["All this time the Patriarch sat serenely smiling; nodding or shaking his head benevolently, as the case required.", "이 내내 가부장은 평온하게 미소 지으며 앉아 있었고, 그때그때 형편에 따라 자비롭게 고개를 끄덕이거나 가로저었다."]], [["‘As to being a reference,’ said Pancks, ‘you know, in a general way, what being a reference means.", "\"신원 보증인이 된다는 것 말인데요,\""], ["It’s all your eye, that is!", "팽크스가 말했다."], ["Look at your tenants down the Yard here.", "\"어르신도 대체로 그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시잖습니까."], ["They’d all be references for one another, if you’d let ‘em.", "다 헛것입니다, 그게! 여기 이 마당 아래 어르신네 세입자들을 보세요."], ["What would be the good of letting ‘em?", "내버려 두면 다들 서로의 보증인이 되어 줄 겁니다."], ["It’s no satisfaction to be done by two men instead of one.", "그렇게 둬서 뭐가 좋겠어요? 한 사람 대신 두 사람한테 떼인다고 만족스러울 건 없지요."], ["One’s enough.", "한 사람이면 충분합니다."], ["A person who can’t pay, gets another person who can’t pay, to guarantee that he can pay.", "돈 못 갚는 사람이 또 돈 못 갚는 사람을 데려와서 자기가 갚을 수 있다고 보증하게 하는 격이에요."], ["Like a person with two wooden legs getting another person with two wooden legs, to guarantee that he has got two natural legs.", "나무다리 두 짝을 단 사람이 또 나무다리 두 짝을 단 사람을 데려와서 자기한테 진짜 다리 두 짝이 있다고 보증하게 하는 거나 마찬가지죠."], ["It don’t make either of them able to do a walking match.", "그런다고 둘 중 누구도 경보 시합을 할 수 있게 되진 않습니다."], ["And four wooden legs are more troublesome to you than two, when you don’t want any.’ Mr Pancks concluded by blowing off that steam of his.", "그리고 나무다리는, 정작 하나도 필요 없을 때엔, 두 짝보다 네 짝이 더 성가신 법이고요.\" 팽크스 씨는 예의 그 김 빠지는 소리를 한 번 내뿜는 것으로 말을 맺었다."]], [["A momentary silence that ensued was broken by Mr F.’s Aunt, who had been sitting upright in a cataleptic state since her last public remark.", "뒤이은 잠깐의 침묵을 깨뜨린 것은 F씨의 고모였는데, 그녀는 마지막으로 좌중 앞에 한마디를 던진 이래 강직증에 걸린 듯 꼿꼿이 앉아 있던 참이었다."], ["She now underwent a violent twitch, calculated to produce a startling effect on the nerves of the uninitiated, and with the deadliest animosity observed:", "이제 그녀는 격렬한 경련을 일으켜—초심자의 신경에는 소스라칠 효과를 낼 법한 것이었다—더없이 살벌한 적의를 담아 말했다."]], [["‘You can’t make a head and brains out of a brass knob with nothing in it.", "\"속이 텅 빈 놋쇠 손잡이로는 머리와 두뇌를 만들 수 없어."], ["You couldn’t do it when your Uncle George was living; much less when he’s dead.’", "네 조지 삼촌이 살아 있을 때도 못 했는데, 죽고 난 지금이야 더 말할 것도 없지.\""]], [["Mr Pancks was not slow to reply, with his usual calmness, ‘Indeed, ma’am!", "팽크스 씨는 평소의 침착함으로 지체 없이 대꾸했다. \"그렇습니까, 부인!"], ["Bless my soul!", "이런 세상에! 그 말씀을 들으니 놀랍군요.\""], ["I’m surprised to hear it.’ Despite his presence of mind, however, the speech of Mr F.’s Aunt produced a depressing effect on the little assembly; firstly, because it was impossible to disguise that Clennam’s unoffending head was the particular temple of reason depreciated; and secondly, because nobody ever knew on these occasions whose Uncle George was referred to, or what spectral presence might be invoked under that appellation.", "그러나 그의 임기응변에도 불구하고, F씨의 고모의 말은 이 작은 모임에 가라앉는 분위기를 자아냈다. 첫째로, 폄훼당한 그 이성의 신전이 죄 없는 클레넘의 머리라는 것을 감출 도리가 없었기 때문이고, 둘째로, 이런 경우마다 대체 누구의 조지 삼촌을 일컫는 것인지, 또 그 호칭 아래 어떤 유령 같은 존재가 불려 나오는 것인지 아무도 알 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["Therefore Flora said, though still not without a certain boastfulness and triumph in her legacy, that Mr F.’s Aunt was ‘very lively to-day, and she thought they had better go.’ But Mr F.’s Aunt proved so lively as to take the suggestion in unexpected dudgeon and declare that she would not go; adding, with several injurious expressions, that if ‘He’--too evidently meaning Clennam--wanted to get rid of her, ‘let him chuck her out of winder;’ and urgently expressing her desire to see ‘Him’ perform that ceremony.", "그래서 플로라는, 그래도 자기 유산에 대한 어떤 자랑과 득의가 없지는 않은 채로, F씨의 고모가 \"오늘 무척 기운이 넘치니 우리는 그만 가는 게 좋겠다\"고 말했다. 그러나 F씨의 고모는 어찌나 기운이 넘쳤던지 그 제안을 뜻밖의 격분으로 받아들이며 가지 않겠다고 선언했고, 몇 마디 모욕적인 말과 함께 덧붙이기를, 만약 '저자'가—너무도 분명히 클레넘을 가리키는 것이었다—자기를 떼어 내고 싶다면 '저자더러 자기를 창밖으로 내던지라'고 했으며, '저자'가 그 의식을 거행하는 것을 보고 싶다고 다급히 요구했다."]], [["In this dilemma, Mr Pancks, whose resources appeared equal to any emergency in the Patriarchal waters, slipped on his hat, slipped out at the counting-house door, and slipped in again a moment afterwards with an artificial freshness upon him, as if he had been in the country for some weeks.", "이 진퇴양난에서, 가부장의 물길에서라면 어떤 위기에도 대처할 만한 재간을 갖춘 듯한 팽크스 씨가 잽싸게 모자를 쓰고 사무실 문밖으로 슬쩍 빠져나가더니, 잠시 후 마치 몇 주 동안 시골에라도 다녀온 듯한 인위적인 생기를 띠고 다시 슬쩍 들어왔다. \"아니, 이런, 부인!\""], ["‘Why, bless my heart, ma’am!’ said Mr Pancks, rubbing up his hair in great astonishment, ‘is that you?", "팽크스 씨가 머리를 쓱 쓸어 올리며 크게 놀란 척 말했다. \"부인 아니십니까?"], ["How do you _do_, ma’am?", "어떻게 지내십니까, 부인?"], ["You are looking charming to-day!", "오늘 참 매력적이시네요! 뵙게 되어 정말 기쁩니다."], ["I am delighted to see you.", "제게 팔을 좀 내주시지요, 부인."], ["Favour me with your arm, ma’am; we’ll have a little walk together, you and me, if you’ll honour me with your company.’ And so escorted Mr F.’s Aunt down the private staircase of the counting-house with great gallantry and success.", "영광스럽게 함께해 주신다면 우리 둘이 잠깐 산책이나 합시다.\" 그러고는 사무실의 전용 계단으로 F씨의 고모를 큰 정중함과 성공으로 호위하여 내려갔다."], ["The patriarchal Mr Casby then rose with the air of having done it himself, and blandly followed: leaving his daughter, as she followed in her turn, to remark to her former lover in a distracted whisper (which she very much enjoyed), that they had drained the cup of life to the dregs; and further to hint mysteriously that the late Mr F. was at the bottom of it.", "그러자 가부장 캐스비 씨가 마치 자기가 그 일을 해낸 양 일어나 점잖게 뒤따랐고, 그 뒤를 차례로 따르던 딸은 옛 연인에게 정신없는 속삭임으로(그녀는 그것을 무척 즐겼다) 그들이 인생의 잔을 찌꺼기까지 다 마셔 버렸노라고 한마디 했으며, 나아가 고인이 된 F씨가 그 밑바닥에 있다고 알쏭달쏭하게 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["Alone again, Clennam became a prey to his old doubts in reference to his mother and Little Dorrit, and revolved the old thoughts and suspicions.", "다시 혼자가 된 클레넘은 어머니와 리틀 도릿에 관한 예전의 의혹에 또 사로잡혀, 옛 생각과 의심을 곱씹었다."], ["They were all in his mind, blending themselves with the duties he was mechanically discharging, when a shadow on his papers caused him to look up for the cause.", "그가 기계적으로 처리하던 일거리에 그것들이 뒤섞인 채 모두 머릿속을 맴돌고 있을 때, 서류 위에 드리운 그림자에 그는 까닭을 찾아 고개를 들었다."], ["The cause was Mr Pancks.", "그 까닭은 팽크스 씨였다."], ["With his hat thrown back upon his ears as if his wiry prongs of hair had darted up like springs and cast it off, with his jet-black beads of eyes inquisitively sharp, with the fingers of his right hand in his mouth that he might bite the nails, and with the fingers of his left hand in reserve in his pocket for another course, Mr Pancks cast his shadow through the glass upon the books and papers.", "모자는 마치 철사처럼 뻣뻣한 머리카락의 갈래들이 용수철처럼 솟구쳐 벗겨 내기라도 한 듯 귀 뒤로 젖혀져 있었고, 칠흑 같은 구슬 같은 눈은 캐묻듯이 날카로웠으며, 손톱을 물어뜯으려고 오른손 손가락은 입속에 들어가 있고, 왼손 손가락은 다음 차례를 위해 주머니 속에 예비해 둔 채로, 팽크스 씨는 유리를 통해 장부와 서류 위로 자기 그림자를 드리웠다."]], [["Mr Pancks asked, with a little inquiring twist of his head, if he might come in again?", "팽크스 씨가 고개를 살짝 캐묻듯 기울이며, 다시 들어가도 되겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Clennam replied with a nod of his head in the affirmative.", "클레넘은 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕여 답했다."], ["Mr Pancks worked his way in, came alongside the desk, made himself fast by leaning his arms upon it, and started conversation with a puff and a snort.", "팽크스 씨는 비집고 들어와 책상 곁에 와서는, 두 팔을 책상에 괴어 자기 몸을 단단히 붙들고, 한 번 푹 내쉬고 한 번 콧김을 뿜으며 대화를 시작했다."]], [["‘Mr F.’s Aunt is appeased, I hope?’ said Clennam.", "\"F씨의 고모님은 가라앉으셨겠지요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘All right, sir,’ said Pancks.", "\"괜찮습니다, 선생.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘I am so unfortunate as to have awakened a strong animosity in the breast of that lady,’ said Clennam.", "\"제가 운이 없게도 그 부인 가슴속에 강한 적의를 불러일으킨 모양입니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘Do you know why?’", "\"그 까닭을 아십니까?\""]], [["‘Does _she_ know why?’ said Pancks.", "\"그분이야 그 까닭을 아실까요?\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose not.’", "\"모르시겠지요.\""]], [["‘_I_ suppose not,’ said Pancks.", "\"저도 모를 거라고 봅니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["He took out his note-book, opened it, shut it, dropped it into his hat, which was beside him on the desk, and looked in at it as it lay at the bottom of the hat: all with a great appearance of consideration.", "그는 수첩을 꺼내 펼쳤다가 덮었고, 그것을 옆에 책상 위에 놓여 있던 모자 속에 떨어뜨린 다음, 모자 바닥에 놓인 그것을 들여다보았다. 이 모두를 무척 신중을 기하는 모양새로 했다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ he then began, ‘I am in want of information, sir.’", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 그가 이윽고 입을 열었다. \"저는 어떤 정보가 필요합니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Connected with this firm?’ asked Clennam.", "\"이 회사와 관련된 겁니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ said Pancks.", "\"아니요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘With what then, Mr Pancks?", "\"그럼 무엇과 관련된 겁니까, 팽크스 씨?"], ["That is to say, assuming that you want it of me.’", "말하자면, 그것을 제게서 얻고 싶으시다면요.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir; yes, I want it of you,’ said Pancks, ‘if I can persuade you to furnish it.", "\"네, 선생, 그래요, 그걸 선생께 얻고 싶습니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["A, B, C, D. DA, DE, DI, DO.", "\"선생을 설득해 내놓게 할 수 있다면요."], ["Dictionary order.", "가, 나, 다, 라. 다아, 다어, 다이, 다오."], ["Dorrit.", "사전 순서대로."], ["That’s the name, sir?’", "도릿. 그게 그 이름이지요, 선생?\""]], [["Mr Pancks blew off his peculiar noise again, and fell to at his right-hand nails.", "팽크스 씨는 다시 한번 그 특유의 소리를 내뿜고는, 오른손 손톱을 물어뜯기 시작했다."], ["Arthur looked searchingly at him; he returned the look.", "아서가 그를 캐묻듯 바라보자, 그도 그 눈길을 마주 받았다."]], [["‘I don’t understand you, Mr Pancks.’", "\"무슨 말씀인지 모르겠군요, 팽크스 씨.\""]], [["‘That’s the name that I want to know about.’", "\"제가 알고 싶은 게 그 이름입니다.\""]], [["‘And what do you want to know?’", "\"그래서 무엇을 알고 싶으신 겁니까?\""]], [["‘Whatever you can and will tell me.’ This comprehensive summary of his desires was not discharged without some heavy labouring on the part of Mr Pancks’s machinery.", "\"선생께서 말씀해 주실 수 있고 또 해 주실 의향이 있는 것이라면 무엇이든요.\" 자기 욕구를 이렇게 포괄적으로 요약하는 데도 팽크스 씨의 기관은 적잖이 힘겹게 돌아가야 했다."]], [["‘This is a singular visit, Mr Pancks.", "\"이건 별난 방문이군요, 팽크스 씨."], ["It strikes me as rather extraordinary that you should come, with such an object, to me.’", "당신이 그런 목적을 가지고 제게 오셨다는 게 다소 기이하게 느껴집니다.\""]], [["‘It may be all extraordinary together,’ returned Pancks.", "\"통째로 기이할 수도 있겠지요.\" 팽크스가 대답했다."], ["‘It may be out of the ordinary course, and yet be business.", "\"통상적인 절차에서 벗어났을 수도 있지만, 그래도 사업일 수 있습니다."], ["In short, it is business.", "요컨대 그건 사업입니다."], ["I am a man of business.", "저는 사업가예요."], ["What business have I in this present world, except to stick to business?", "사업에 매달리는 것 말고 제가 이 세상에서 무슨 볼일이 있겠습니까?"], ["No business.’", "아무 볼일도 없죠.\""]], [["With his former doubt whether this dry hard personage were quite in earnest, Clennam again turned his eyes attentively upon his face.", "이 무미건조하고 딱딱한 인물이 과연 진지한 것인지 다시금 미심쩍어진 클레넘은, 또 한 번 그의 얼굴을 주의 깊게 살폈다."], ["It was as scrubby and dingy as ever, and as eager and quick as ever, and he could see nothing lurking in it that was at all expressive of a latent mockery that had seemed to strike upon his ear in the voice.", "그것은 여느 때처럼 까칠하고 거뭇거뭇했고, 여느 때처럼 열렬하고 민첩했으며, 아까 목소리에서 귀를 스치는 듯하던 그 잠복한 비웃음을 조금이라도 드러내는 기색은 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Now,’ said Pancks, ‘to put this business on its own footing, it’s not my proprietor’s.’", "\"자,\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"이 일을 그 자체의 입장에서 따져 보자면, 이건 제 고용주의 일이 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Do you refer to Mr Casby as your proprietor?’", "\"고용주라 하시면 캐스비 씨를 말씀하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["Pancks nodded. ‘My proprietor.", "팽크스가 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["Put a case.", "\"제 고용주죠."], ["Say, at my proprietor’s I hear name--name of young person Mr Clennam wants to serve.", "한 경우를 가정해 보지요. 가령, 제 고용주 댁에서 제가 어떤 이름을 듣습니다—클레넘 씨가 돕고 싶어 하는 젊은 사람의 이름을요."], ["Say, name first mentioned to my proprietor by Plornish in the Yard.", "가령, 그 이름이 마당의 플로니시를 통해 제 고용주에게 처음 거론됩니다."], ["Say, I go to Plornish.", "가령, 제가 플로니시를 찾아갑니다."], ["Say, I ask Plornish as a matter of business for information.", "가령, 제가 사업상의 일로 플로니시에게 정보를 청합니다."], ["Say, Plornish, though six weeks in arrear to my proprietor, declines.", "가령, 플로니시는, 제 고용주에게 여섯 주나 집세가 밀렸으면서도 거절합니다."], ["Say, Mrs Plornish declines.", "가령, 플로니시 부인도 거절합니다."], ["Say, both refer to Mr Clennam.", "가령, 두 사람 다 클레넘 씨를 가리킵니다."], ["Put the case.’", "그 경우를 가정해 보세요.\""]], [["‘Well?’", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ returned Pancks, ‘say, I come to him.", "\"그래서, 선생,\" 팽크스가 대답했다."], ["Say, here I am.’", "\"가령, 제가 그분을 찾아옵니다. 가령, 여기 제가 와 있습니다.\""]], [["With those prongs of hair sticking up all over his head, and his breath coming and going very hard and short, the busy Pancks fell back a step (in Tug metaphor, took half a turn astern) as if to show his dingy hull complete, then forged a-head again, and directed his quick glance by turns into his hat where his note-book was, and into Clennam’s face.", "머리 온통에 그 머리카락 갈래들을 곤두세운 채, 숨을 몹시 가쁘고 짧게 들이쉬고 내쉬며, 분주한 팽크스는 한 발짝 물러섰다가(예인선의 비유로 치면 반 바퀴 후진했다가) 마치 자기의 거뭇거뭇한 선체를 온전히 내보이려는 듯하더니, 다시 앞으로 나아가, 수첩이 든 자기 모자 속과 클레넘의 얼굴로 번갈아 그 재빠른 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["‘Mr Pancks, not to trespass on your grounds of mystery, I will be as plain with you as I can.", "\"팽크스 씨, 당신의 비밀스러운 영역을 침범하지 않으면서, 제가 할 수 있는 한 솔직하게 말씀드리겠습니다."], ["Let me ask two questions.", "두 가지를 묻겠습니다."], ["First--’", "첫째로—\""]], [["‘All right!’ said Pancks, holding up his dirty forefinger with his broken nail.", "\"좋습니다!\" 팽크스가 부러진 손톱이 달린 더러운 집게손가락을 치켜들며 말했다."], ["‘I see!", "\"알겠어요!"], ["“What’s your motive?”’", "'당신의 동기가 무엇이냐?'겠지요.\""]], [["‘Exactly.’", "\"바로 그겁니다.\""]], [["‘Motive,’ said Pancks, ‘good.", "\"동기라,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["Nothing to do with my proprietor; not stateable at present, ridiculous to state at present; but good.", "\"좋습니다. 제 고용주와는 아무 상관 없고, 지금은 밝힐 수 없으며, 지금 밝히자면 우스운 노릇이겠지만, 그래도 좋은 겁니다."], ["Desiring to serve young person, name of Dorrit,’ said Pancks, with his forefinger still up as a caution.", "젊은 사람을, 도릿이라는 이름의 사람을 돕고 싶다는 거죠.\" 팽크스가 여전히 집게손가락을 경고하듯 치켜든 채 말했다."], ["‘Better admit motive to be good.’", "\"동기는 좋은 것으로 인정해 두시는 게 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Secondly, and lastly, what do you want to know?’", "\"둘째로, 그리고 마지막으로, 당신은 무엇을 알고 싶으신 겁니까?\""]], [["Mr Pancks fished up his note-book before the question was put, and buttoning it with care in an inner breast-pocket, and looking straight at Clennam all the time, replied with a pause and a puff, ‘I want supplementary information of any sort.’", "팽크스 씨는 질문이 채 떨어지기도 전에 수첩을 낚아 올려 안쪽 가슴 주머니에 조심스레 단추를 채워 넣고는, 그 내내 똑바로 클레넘을 바라보면서, 한 번 뜸을 들이고 한 번 푹 내쉰 뒤 대답했다. \"저는 어떤 종류의 것이든 보충 정보를 원합니다.\""]], [["Clennam could not withhold a smile, as the panting little steam-tug, so useful to that unwieldy ship, the Casby, waited on and watched him as if it were seeking an opportunity of running in and rifling him of all he wanted before he could resist its manoeuvres; though there was that in Mr Pancks’s eagerness, too, which awakened many wondering speculations in his mind.", "클레넘은 미소를 참을 수 없었으니, 저 거추장스러운 배 캐스비 호에 그토록 요긴한 그 헐떡이는 작은 증기 예인선이, 마치 그가 막아 낼 새도 없이 곁에 바싹 붙어 자기가 원하는 것을 죄다 털어 갈 기회라도 노리는 듯, 그를 시중들며 지켜보고 있었기 때문이다. 다만 팽크스 씨의 그 열렬함에는 클레넘의 마음속에 갖가지 궁금증을 자아내는 무언가가 있기도 했다."], ["After a little consideration, he resolved to supply Mr Pancks with such leading information as it was in his power to impart him; well knowing that Mr Pancks, if he failed in his present research, was pretty sure to find other means of getting it.", "잠시 생각한 끝에, 그는 자기가 전해 줄 수 있는 정도의 요긴한 정보를 팽크스 씨에게 제공하기로 마음먹었다. 팽크스 씨가 이번 탐색에 실패하더라도 다른 수단으로 그것을 알아낼 것이 거의 틀림없음을 잘 알았기 때문이다."]], [["He, therefore, first requesting Mr Pancks to remember his voluntary declaration that his proprietor had no part in the disclosure, and that his own intentions were good (two declarations which that coaly little gentleman with the greatest ardour repeated), openly told him that as to the Dorrit lineage or former place of habitation, he had no information to communicate, and that his knowledge of the family did not extend beyond the fact that it appeared to be now reduced to five members; namely, to two brothers, of whom one was single, and one a widower with three children.", "그리하여 그는 먼저 팽크스 씨에게, 이 폭로에 자기 고용주는 아무 관여도 없으며 자신의 의도는 선의에서 나온 것이라던 자발적 선언을 기억해 달라고 청한 뒤(그 석탄가루 같은 작은 신사는 더없이 열렬하게 그 두 선언을 되풀이했다), 도릿 가의 혈통이나 예전 거주지에 대해서는 전해 줄 정보가 없으며, 그 가족에 대한 자신의 지식은 그것이 지금 다섯 식구로 줄어든 듯하다는 사실—즉 두 형제가 있는데 하나는 독신이고 하나는 세 자녀를 둔 홀아비라는 것—이상은 미치지 못한다고 솔직히 말해 주었다."], ["The ages of the whole family he made known to Mr Pancks, as nearly as he could guess at them; and finally he described to him the position of the Father of the Marshalsea, and the course of time and events through which he had become invested with that character.", "온 가족의 나이도 그가 짐작할 수 있는 한 최대한 가깝게 팽크스 씨에게 알려 주었다. 그리고 끝으로 마셜시의 아버지의 처지와, 그가 그러한 신분을 지니게 된 세월과 사건의 경과를 그에게 설명해 주었다."], ["To all this, Mr Pancks, snorting and blowing in a more and more portentous manner as he became more interested, listened with great attention; appearing to derive the most agreeable sensations from the painfullest parts of the narrative, and particularly to be quite charmed by the account of William Dorrit’s long imprisonment.", "이 모든 이야기에, 팽크스 씨는 흥미가 깊어질수록 점점 더 심상치 않은 모양새로 콧김을 뿜고 숨을 몰아쉬며 무척 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. 그는 이야기 중에서도 가장 고통스러운 대목에서 가장 흐뭇한 감흥을 길어 올리는 듯했고, 특히 윌리엄 도릿의 오랜 수감 이야기에는 아주 매료된 듯했다."]], [["‘In conclusion, Mr Pancks,’ said Arthur, ‘I have but to say this.", "\"끝으로 한마디 드리자면, 팽크스 씨,\" 아서가 말했다."], ["I have reasons beyond a personal regard for speaking as little as I can of the Dorrit family, particularly at my mother’s house’ (Mr Pancks nodded), ‘and for knowing as much as I can.", "\"저는 이 말씀만 드리겠습니다. 제게는 단순한 개인적 호의를 넘어서서, 도릿 가에 대해 가능한 한 적게 말하려는—특히 어머니 댁에서는요(팽크스 씨가 고개를 끄덕였다)—그리고 가능한 한 많이 알아 두려는 이유가 있습니다."], ["So devoted a man of business as you are--eh?’", "당신처럼 그토록 사업에 충실한 분이라면—응?\""]], [["For Mr Pancks had suddenly made that blowing effort with unusual force.", "팽크스 씨가 별안간 평소와 다른 세찬 기세로 그 콧김 뿜는 소리를 냈던 것이다."]], [["‘It’s nothing,’ said Pancks.", "\"아무것도 아닙니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘So devoted a man of business as yourself has a perfect understanding of a fair bargain.", "\"당신처럼 그토록 사업에 충실한 분이라면 공정한 거래가 무엇인지 완벽히 아실 겁니다."], ["I wish to make a fair bargain with you, that you shall enlighten me concerning the Dorrit family when you have it in your power, as I have enlightened you.", "저는 당신과 공정한 거래를 하고 싶습니다. 제가 당신을 깨우쳐 드렸듯, 당신도 도릿 가에 관해 알게 되는 것이 생기면 그것을 제게 알려 주시는 거지요."], ["It may not give you a very flattering idea of my business habits, that I failed to make my terms beforehand,’ continued Clennam; ‘but I prefer to make them a point of honour.", "제가 미리 조건을 정해 두지 않은 것이 당신에게 제 사업 습관에 대해 그리 미덥지 못한 인상을 줄지도 모르겠습니다만,\" 클레넘이 말을 이었다."], ["I have seen so much business done on sharp principles that, to tell you the truth, Mr Pancks, I am tired of them.’", "\"저는 그것을 명예의 문제로 삼는 편이 좋습니다. 저는 약삭빠른 원칙으로 처리되는 사업을 하도 많이 봐 와서, 솔직히 말씀드리면, 팽크스 씨, 그런 데 진력이 났습니다.\""]], [["Mr Pancks laughed.", "팽크스 씨가 웃었다."], ["‘It’s a bargain, sir,’ said he.", "\"거래 성립입니다, 선생.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘You shall find me stick to it.’", "\"제가 그것을 지키는 걸 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["After that, he stood a little while looking at Clennam, and biting his ten nails all round; evidently while he fixed in his mind what he had been told, and went over it carefully, before the means of supplying a gap in his memory should be no longer at hand.", "그러고 나서 그는 잠시 서서 클레넘을 바라보며 열 손톱을 빙 둘러 가며 물어뜯었다. 자기가 들은 바를 머릿속에 단단히 새기며, 기억의 빈틈을 메울 수단이 더는 곁에 없게 되기 전에 그것을 꼼꼼히 되짚어 보는 것이 분명했다."], ["‘It’s all right,’ he said at last, ‘and now I’ll wish you good day, as it’s collecting day in the Yard.", "\"다 됐습니다.\" 그가 이윽고 말했다."], ["By-the-bye, though.", "\"그럼 이만 안녕히 계십시오, 마당에서 수금하는 날이라서요."], ["A lame foreigner with a stick.’", "아 참, 그건 그렇고. 지팡이를 짚은 절름발이 외국인 하나가 있는데요.\""]], [["‘Ay, ay.", "\"아, 그래요."], ["You do take a reference sometimes, I see?’ said Clennam.", "보아하니 당신도 가끔은 보증인을 받기도 하는군요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘When he can pay, sir,’ replied Pancks.", "\"그자가 갚을 수 있을 때라면요, 선생.\" 팽크스가 대답했다."], ["‘Take all you can get, and keep back all you can’t be forced to give up. That’s business.", "\"받아 낼 수 있는 건 죄다 받아 내고, 억지로 토해 내게 할 수 없는 건 죄다 붙들어 두는 것, 그게 사업이지요."], ["The lame foreigner with the stick wants a top room down the Yard.", "지팡이를 짚은 그 절름발이 외국인이 마당 아래 꼭대기 방을 원합니다."], ["Is he good for it?’", "그자가 그걸 감당할 만합니까?\""]], [["‘I am,’ said Clennam, ‘and I will answer for him.’", "\"제가 감당하지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"그자를 제가 보증하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s enough.", "\"그거면 됐습니다."], ["What I must have of Bleeding Heart Yard,’ said Pancks, making a note of the case in his book, ‘is my bond.", "블리딩 하트 마당에서 제가 받아 내야 하는 건,\" 팽크스가 그 건을 자기 수첩에 적으며 말했다."], ["I want my bond, you see.", "\"제 채권입니다."], ["Pay up, or produce your property!", "보시다시피 저는 제 채권을 원하지요."], ["That’s the watchword down the Yard.", "갚든지, 아니면 잡힐 물건을 내놓든지!"], ["The lame foreigner with the stick represented that you sent him; but he could represent (as far as that goes) that the Great Mogul sent him.", "그게 마당 아래의 구호입니다. 지팡이를 짚은 그 절름발이 외국인은 선생께서 자기를 보냈다고 했지만, 그자야 (그쯤이야) 무굴 대제가 자기를 보냈다고도 할 수 있는 자입니다."], ["He has been in the hospital, I believe?’", "그자가 병원에 있었다지요, 아마?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["Through having met with an accident.", "사고를 당해서요."], ["He is only just now discharged.’", "이제 막 퇴원했습니다.\""]], [["‘It’s pauperising a man, sir, I have been shown, to let him into a hospital?’ said Pancks.", "\"사람을 병원에 들이는 건 그자를 빈민으로 전락시키는 거라고들 하더군요, 선생, 제가 듣기로는요?\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["And again blew off that remarkable sound.", "그러고는 다시 한번 그 별난 소리를 내뿜었다."]], [["‘I have been shown so too,’ said Clennam, coldly.", "\"저도 그렇게 들었습니다.\" 클레넘이 차갑게 말했다."]], [["Mr Pancks, being by that time quite ready for a start, got under steam in a moment, and, without any other signal or ceremony, was snorting down the step-ladder and working into Bleeding Heart Yard, before he seemed to be well out of the counting-house.", "이때쯤 출발할 채비를 완전히 갖춘 팽크스 씨는, 순식간에 김을 올리더니, 다른 신호도 의례도 없이, 사무실에서 미처 다 빠져나갔다 싶기도 전에 콧김을 뿜으며 사다리를 내려가 블리딩 하트 마당으로 헤집고 들어갔다."]], [["Throughout the remainder of the day, Bleeding Heart Yard was in consternation, as the grim Pancks cruised in it; haranguing the inhabitants on their backslidings in respect of payment, demanding his bond, breathing notices to quit and executions, running down defaulters, sending a swell of terror on before him, and leaving it in his wake.", "그날 남은 시간 내내, 무서운 팽크스가 그 안을 휘젓고 다니는 통에 블리딩 하트 마당은 공포에 휩싸였다. 그는 주민들에게 집세를 미룬 죄를 늘어놓으며 일장 연설을 하고, 자기 채권을 요구하고, 퇴거 통지와 강제 집행을 으르렁대고, 체납자들을 몰아붙이고, 가는 곳마다 앞으로 공포의 물결을 보내고 뒤로도 그것을 남겼다."], ["Knots of people, impelled by a fatal attraction, lurked outside any house in which he was known to be, listening for fragments of his discourses to the inmates; and, when he was rumoured to be coming down the stairs, often could not disperse so quickly but that he would be prematurely in among them, demanding their own arrears, and rooting them to the spot.", "사람들은 무리를 지어, 불길한 끌림에 이끌려, 그가 있는 줄로 알려진 집 밖에 숨어, 그가 거주자들에게 늘어놓는 일장연설의 토막이라도 엿들으려 했다. 그러다 그가 계단을 내려온다는 소문이 돌면, 미처 흩어지기도 전에 그가 느닷없이 그들 한복판에 들이닥쳐 그들 자신의 체납금을 요구하며 그 자리에 못 박아 두는 일이 잦았다."], ["Throughout the remainder of the day, Mr Pancks’s What were they up to?", "그날 남은 시간 내내, \"저들이 무슨 수작이지?\" \"저들이 그걸로 무슨 짓을 하려는 거야?\""], ["and What did they mean by it? sounded all over the Yard.", "하는 팽크스 씨의 소리가 온 마당에 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Mr Pancks wouldn’t hear of excuses, wouldn’t hear of complaints, wouldn’t hear of repairs, wouldn’t hear of anything but unconditional money down.", "팽크스 씨는 변명도 들으려 하지 않고, 불평도 들으려 하지 않고, 수리 요청도 들으려 하지 않고, 무조건 즉석에서 내는 돈 말고는 그 무엇도 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["Perspiring and puffing and darting about in eccentric directions, and becoming hotter and dingier every moment, he lashed the tide of the yard into a most agitated and turbid state.", "땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 콧김을 뿜으며 별난 방향으로 휙휙 내달으면서, 매 순간 더 달아오르고 더 거뭇거뭇해지는 채로, 그는 마당의 물결을 더없이 사납고 탁한 상태로 휘저어 놓았다."], ["It had not settled down into calm water again full two hours after he had been seen fuming away on the horizon at the top of the steps.", "그가 계단 꼭대기 지평선 위에서 씩씩대며 사라지는 것이 목격된 지 꼬박 두 시간이 지나서야 그것은 다시 잔잔한 물로 가라앉았다."]], [["There were several small assemblages of the Bleeding Hearts at the popular points of meeting in the Yard that night, among whom it was universally agreed that Mr Pancks was a hard man to have to do with; and that it was much to be regretted, so it was, that a gentleman like Mr Casby should put his rents in his hands, and never know him in his true light.", "그날 밤 마당의 사람 모이는 명소마다 블리딩 하트 주민들이 몇몇 작은 무리를 이루었는데, 그들 사이에서는 팽크스 씨가 상대하기 까다로운 사람이라는 데, 그리고 캐스비 씨 같은 신사가 자기 집세를 그자의 손에 맡기고서 그자의 본색을 끝내 알지 못하다니 참으로 유감스러운 일이라는 데 이견이 없었다."], ["For (said the Bleeding Hearts), if a gentleman with that head of hair and them eyes took his rents into his own hands, ma’am, there would be none of this worriting and wearing, and things would be very different.", "왜냐하면(블리딩 하트 주민들이 말하기를) 저런 머리카락과 저런 눈을 가진 신사분이 손수 집세를 거두신다면, 부인, 이런 들볶임과 시달림은 하나도 없을 테고 만사가 아주 달라질 거라는 것이었다."]], [["At which identical evening hour and minute, the Patriarch--who had floated serenely through the Yard in the forenoon before the harrying began, with the express design of getting up this trustfulness in his shining bumps and silken locks--at which identical hour and minute, that first-rate humbug of a thousand guns was heavily floundering in the little Dock of his exhausted Tug at home, and was saying, as he turned his thumbs:", "바로 그 저녁 그 시각, 그 분초에—오전에 그 들볶음이 시작되기에 앞서, 자기의 빛나는 혹과 비단 같은 머리카락에 이런 신망을 자아내려는 뚜렷한 의도를 품고 마당을 평온하게 떠다녔던 그 가부장은—바로 그 시각 그 분초에, 천 문의 대포에 맞먹는 그 일류 사기꾼은 집에서 기진맥진한 자기 예인선의 작은 선거 안에 묵직하게 들어앉아 허우적대며, 엄지손가락을 빙글빙글 돌리며 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["‘A very bad day’s work, Pancks, very bad day’s work.", "\"오늘 일은 아주 형편없었네, 팽크스, 아주 형편없는 하루였어."], ["It seems to me, sir, and I must insist on making this observation forcibly in justice to myself, that you ought to have got much more money, much more money.’", "내가 보기엔, 자네, 그리고 나 자신을 위해서라도 이 말은 단호히 해 두어야겠는데, 자네는 돈을 훨씬 더 거둬 왔어야 했어, 훨씬 더 많이 말이야.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 24. Fortune-Telling", "# CHAPTER 24. Fortune-Telling"]], [["Little Dorrit received a call that same evening from Mr Plornish, who, having intimated that he wished to speak to her privately, in a series of coughs so very noticeable as to favour the idea that her father, as regarded her seamstress occupation, was an illustration of the axiom that there are no such stone-blind men as those who will not see, obtained an audience with her on the common staircase outside the door.", "바로 그날 저녁 리틀 도릿은 플로니시 씨의 방문을 받았다. 그는 그녀와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다는 뜻을, 어찌나 두드러진 일련의 헛기침으로 내비쳤던지, 그녀의 바느질 일에 관한 한 그녀의 아버지가 보려 하지 않는 자만큼 눈먼 자는 없다는 격언의 본보기라는 생각에 힘을 실어 줄 정도였는데, 마침내 문밖의 공동 계단에서 그녀와의 면담을 얻어 냈다."]], [["‘There’s been a lady at our place to-day, Miss Dorrit,’ Plornish growled, ‘and another one along with her as is a old wixen if ever I met with such.", "\"오늘 저희 집에 어떤 부인이 한 분 오셨더랬어요, 도릿 양.\" 플로니시가 낮게 으르렁대듯 말했다."], ["The way she snapped a person’s head off, dear me!’", "\"그리고 함께 온 또 한 분은, 제가 여태 본 늙은 살쾡이 중에 그런 살쾡이가 또 없습디다. 어찌나 사람 머리를 댕강 물어뜯듯 쏘아붙이는지, 원, 세상에!\""]], [["The mild Plornish was at first quite unable to get his mind away from Mr F.’s Aunt.", "온순한 플로니시는 처음에는 좀처럼 에프 씨의 고모에게서 마음을 떼어 놓지 못했다. \"글쎄,\""], ["‘For,’ said he, to excuse himself, ‘she is, I do assure you, the winegariest party.’", "그가 변명하듯 말했다. \"장담컨대 그분은 정말이지 식초 같은 분이라니까요.\""]], [["At length, by a great effort, he detached himself from the subject sufficiently to observe:", "마침내 그는 큰 애를 써서 그 화제에서 충분히 벗어나 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘But she’s neither here nor there just at present.", "\"하지만 지금 그분 얘긴 여기 끼어들 게 아니죠."], ["The other lady, she’s Mr Casby’s daughter; and if Mr Casby an’t well off, none better, it an’t through any fault of Pancks.", "다른 부인 말인데요, 그분은 캐스비 씨의 따님이에요. 그리고 캐스비 씨가 형편이 넉넉하다면, 그보다 더 넉넉한 사람이 없을 만큼 넉넉하다면, 그건 결코 팽크스 잘못이 아니에요."], ["For, as to Pancks, he does, he really does, he does indeed!’", "팽크스로 말하자면, 그 사람은 해요, 정말로 해요, 그럼요, 정말 한다니까요!\""]], [["Mr Plornish, after his usual manner, was a little obscure, but conscientiously emphatic.", "플로니시 씨는 늘 그렇듯 다소 모호했으나, 양심껏 힘주어 말했다."]], [["‘And what she come to our place for,’ he pursued, ‘was to leave word that if Miss Dorrit would step up to that card--which it’s Mr Casby’s house that is, and Pancks he has a office at the back, where he really does, beyond belief--she would be glad for to engage her.", "\"그리고 그분이 저희 집에 오신 까닭은요,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["She was a old and a dear friend, she said particular, of Mr Clennam, and hoped for to prove herself a useful friend to _his_ friend.", "\"도릿 양이 그 카드에 적힌 곳으로—그게 캐스비 씨 댁인데요, 거기 뒤편에 팽크스가 사무실을 두고서 정말 믿기지 않을 만큼 일을 한답니다—찾아와 주신다면, 그분을 기꺼이 고용하고 싶다는 전갈을 남기려는 거였어요. 그분은 클레넘 씨의 오래되고 소중한 친구라고 특별히 말씀하시면서, 그분 친구에게 쓸모 있는 친구가 되어 드리고 싶다고 바라셨어요."], ["Them was her words.", "그게 그분 말씀 그대로예요."], ["Wishing to know whether Miss Dorrit could come to-morrow morning, I said I would see you, Miss, and inquire, and look round there to-night, to say yes, or, if you was engaged to-morrow, when?’", "도릿 양이 내일 아침에 올 수 있는지 알고 싶다 하시기에, 제가 도릿 양을 만나 여쭤보고, 오늘 밤에 거기 들러서 그러겠다고, 아니면 내일 약속이 있으시면 언제가 좋을지 전하겠다고 했어요.\""]], [["‘I can go to-morrow, thank you,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"내일 갈 수 있어요, 고마워요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘This is very kind of you, but you are always kind.’", "\"정말 친절하시네요, 하지만 아저씨는 늘 친절하시죠.\""]], [["Mr Plornish, with a modest disavowal of his merits, opened the room door for her readmission, and followed her in with such an exceedingly bald pretence of not having been out at all, that her father might have observed it without being very suspicious.", "플로니시 씨는 자기 공을 겸손하게 부인하며 그녀가 다시 들어갈 수 있도록 방문을 열어 주었고, 자기는 도무지 밖에 나간 적이 없다는 듯 어찌나 빤한 시늉을 하며 뒤따라 들어가던지, 그녀의 아버지가 그리 의심 많은 사람이 아니더라도 알아챘을 법했다. 그러나 그는 상냥한 무심함 속에서 아무것도 눈치채지 못했다."], ["In his affable unconsciousness, however, he took no heed.", "플로니시는 잠시 이야기를 나누었는데, 거기에는 한때 수감자였던 옛 의무감과 지금은 변변찮은 바깥 친구라는 현재의 특권이 뒤섞였고, 다시 그것은 미장이라는 자기의 낮은 처지로 가늠되었다."], ["Plornish, after a little conversation, in which he blended his former duty as a Collegian with his present privilege as a humble outside friend, qualified again by his low estate as a plasterer, took his leave; making the tour of the prison before he left, and looking on at a game of skittles with the mixed feelings of an old inhabitant who had his private reasons for believing that it might be his destiny to come back again.", "그러고는 작별을 고했다. 떠나기 전에 감옥을 한 바퀴 돌고, 스키틀 놀이를 구경하면서, 언젠가 다시 이곳으로 돌아오는 것이 자기 운명일지도 모른다고 믿을 나름의 이유가 있는 옛 거주자의 복잡한 심정으로 바라보았다."]], [["Early in the morning, Little Dorrit, leaving Maggy in high domestic trust, set off for the Patriarchal tent.", "이른 아침, 리틀 도릿은 매기에게 집안일을 단단히 맡겨 두고 가부장의 천막으로 길을 나섰다."], ["She went by the Iron Bridge, though it cost her a penny, and walked more slowly in that part of her journey than in any other.", "그녀는 일 페니가 들었지만 아이언 브리지로 갔고, 여정의 그 구간에서는 다른 어느 곳에서보다 더 천천히 걸었다."], ["At five minutes before eight her hand was on the Patriarchal knocker, which was quite as high as she could reach.", "여덟 시 오 분 전에 그녀의 손은 가부장 댁의 문고리에 닿았는데, 그 문고리는 그녀가 손을 뻗어 닿을 수 있는 꼭 그 높이에 있었다."]], [["She gave Mrs Finching’s card to the young woman who opened the door, and the young woman told her that ‘Miss Flora’--Flora having, on her return to the parental roof, reinvested herself with the title under which she had lived there--was not yet out of her bedroom, but she was to please to walk up into Miss Flora’s sitting-room.", "그녀는 문을 열어 준 젊은 여자에게 핀칭 부인의 카드를 건넸고, 젊은 여자는 \"플로라 아씨\"가—플로라는 친정 지붕 아래로 돌아오면서 그곳에서 살던 시절의 호칭을 다시 두르고 있었다—아직 침실에서 나오지 않으셨지만, 플로라 아씨의 거실로 올라가 계시면 된다고 말했다."], ["She walked up into Miss Flora’s sitting-room, as in duty bound, and there found a breakfast-table comfortably laid for two, with a supplementary tray upon it laid for one.", "그녀는 마땅한 도리대로 플로라 아씨의 거실로 올라갔고, 그곳에서 둘을 위해 안락하게 차려진 아침 식탁을 발견했는데, 그 위에는 한 사람 몫이 따로 차려진 쟁반이 곁들여져 있었다."], ["The young woman, disappearing for a few moments, returned to say that she was to please to take a chair by the fire, and to take off her bonnet and make herself at home.", "젊은 여자는 잠시 사라졌다가 다시 돌아와, 난롯가에 의자를 놓고 앉아 보닛을 벗고 편히 계시라고 전했다."], ["But Little Dorrit, being bashful, and not used to make herself at home on such occasions, felt at a loss how to do it; so she was still sitting near the door with her bonnet on, when Flora came in in a hurry half an hour afterwards.", "그러나 리틀 도릿은 수줍음이 많았고 그런 자리에서 편히 굴어 본 적이 없었으므로 어찌해야 할지 몰라 막막했다. 그래서 삼십 분 뒤 플로라가 부랴부랴 들어왔을 때에도 그녀는 여전히 보닛을 쓴 채 문 가까이에 앉아 있었다."]], [["Flora was so sorry to have kept her waiting, and good gracious why did she sit out there in the cold when she had expected to find her by the fire reading the paper, and hadn’t that heedless girl given her the message then, and had she really been in her bonnet all this time, and pray for goodness sake let Flora take it off!", "플로라는 그녀를 기다리게 해서 너무나 미안하다고, 어머나 세상에 어쩌자고 난롯가에서 신문을 읽으며 계실 줄 알았는데 이 추운 데 나앉아 계셨느냐고, 그 칠칠치 못한 계집애가 전갈을 안 드렸느냐고, 정말로 이 시간 내내 보닛을 쓰고 계셨느냐고, 부디 제발 플로라가 벗겨 드리게 해 달라고 했다! 플로라는 세상에서 가장 너그러운 손길로 보닛을 벗기다가, 드러난 얼굴에 어찌나 마음이 끌렸던지 이렇게 말했다."], ["Flora taking it off in the best-natured manner in the world, was so struck with the face disclosed, that she said, ‘Why, what a good little thing you are, my dear!’ and pressed her face between her hands like the gentlest of women.", "\"어머, 아가씨, 정말 착하고 자그마한 분이네요!\" 그러고는 더없이 다정한 여인답게 두 손으로 그녀의 얼굴을 감싸 쥐었다."]], [["It was the word and the action of a moment.", "그것은 한순간의 말이요 동작이었다."], ["Little Dorrit had hardly time to think how kind it was, when Flora dashed at the breakfast-table full of business, and plunged over head and ears into loquacity.", "리틀 도릿이 그 친절함을 미처 헤아릴 새도 없이, 플로라는 분주하게 아침 식탁으로 달려들어 수다 속으로 머리끝까지 곤두박질쳤다."]], [["‘Really so sorry that I should happen to be late on this morning of all mornings because my intention and my wish was to be ready to meet you when you came in and to say that any one that interested Arthur Clennam half so much must interest me and that I gave you the heartiest welcome and was so glad, instead of which they never called me and there I still am snoring I dare say if the truth was known and if you don’t like either cold fowl or hot boiled ham which many people don’t I dare say besides Jews and theirs are scruples of conscience which we must all respect though I must say I wish they had them equally strong when they sell us false articles for real that certainly ain’t worth the money I shall be quite vexed,’ said Flora.", "\"하고많은 아침 중에 하필 오늘 아침에 늦다니 정말 너무 미안해요 왜냐하면 제 뜻과 바람은 아가씨가 들어오실 때 맞이할 채비를 하고서 아서 클레넘이 그토록 관심을 두는 분이라면 누구든 절반만큼이라도 제게 관심을 끌게 마련이니 진심으로 환영한다고 정말 기쁘다고 말씀드리려던 거였는데 그러기는커녕 아무도 절 깨우지 않았고 사실대로 말하자면 전 아마 아직도 코를 골고 있었을 거예요 그리고 차가운 닭고기든 뜨거운 삶은 햄이든 둘 다 싫으시다면 그야 유대인들 말고도 싫어하는 사람이 많다고 봐요 그건 양심의 가책이니 우리 모두 존중해야 마땅하지만 그래도 정말이지 가짜 물건을 진짜라고 우리한테 팔아넘길 때에도 그 양심이 그만큼 강했으면 좋겠다 싶은데 그 물건들은 분명 값어치도 못하니 그러면 저는 어지간히 속상하겠어요.\" 플로라가 말했다."]], [["Little Dorrit thanked her, and said, shyly, bread-and-butter and tea was all she usually--", "리틀 도릿은 고맙다고 인사하고, 부끄러운 듯 보통은 버터 바른 빵과 차면 충분하다고—"]], [["‘Oh nonsense my dear child I can never hear of that,’ said Flora, turning on the urn in the most reckless manner, and making herself wink by splashing hot water into her eyes as she bent down to look into the teapot.", "\"오 말도 안 돼요 아가씨 그런 말은 도무지 못 들어줘요.\" 플로라가 더없이 무모한 손짓으로 탕관 꼭지를 틀고, 찻주전자 안을 들여다보려 몸을 굽히다가 뜨거운 물을 눈에 튀겨 저절로 눈을 깜박이며 말했다."], ["‘You are coming here on the footing of a friend and companion you know if you will let me take that liberty and I should be ashamed of myself indeed if you could come here upon any other, besides which Arthur Clennam spoke in such terms--you are tired my dear.’", "\"괜찮으시다면 그런 외람된 말을 좀 하자면 아가씨는 친구이자 동무로서 여기 오시는 거예요 다른 어떤 자격으로 오신다면 정말 제가 부끄러울 거예요 게다가 아서 클레넘이 그런 식으로 말씀하셨으니—아가씨 지쳤군요.\""]], [["‘No, ma’am.’", "\"아니에요, 부인.\""]], [["‘You turn so pale you have walked too far before breakfast and I dare say live a great way off and ought to have had a ride,’ said Flora, ‘dear dear is there anything that would do you good?’", "\"안색이 그렇게 창백해지시는 걸 보니 아침도 거른 채 너무 멀리 걸어오셨군요 분명 멀리 사시는데 마차를 타셨어야 했어요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"저런 저런 뭔가 몸에 좋을 만한 게 없을까요?\""]], [["‘Indeed I am quite well, ma’am.", "\"정말 저는 아주 괜찮아요, 부인."], ["I thank you again and again, but I am quite well.’", "거듭거듭 감사드려요, 하지만 정말 괜찮아요.\""]], [["‘Then take your tea at once I beg,’ said Flora, ‘and this wing of fowl and bit of ham, don’t mind me or wait for me, because I always carry in this tray myself to Mr F.’s Aunt who breakfasts in bed and a charming old lady too and very clever, Portrait of Mr F.", "\"그럼 어서 차부터 드세요.\" 플로라가 말했다."], ["behind the door and very like though too much forehead and as to a pillar with a marble pavement and balustrades and a mountain, I never saw him near it nor not likely in the wine trade, excellent man but not at all in that way.’", "\"그리고 이 닭 날개랑 햄 한 점도요, 저는 신경 쓰지 마시고 기다리지도 마세요 저는 늘 이 쟁반을 에프 씨의 고모님께 직접 가져다드리거든요 침대에서 아침을 드시는데 그분도 참 멋진 노부인이고 아주 영리하시지요 문 뒤에 에프 씨의 초상화가 있는데 아주 닮았어요 이마가 좀 지나치게 넓긴 하지만요 그리고 대리석 바닥에 난간이 딸린 기둥이며 산이 그려져 있는데 저는 그이가 그런 데 가까이 가는 걸 본 적도 없고 포도주 장사를 하던 사람이 그럴 리도 없죠 훌륭한 사람이었지만 도무지 그런 쪽은 아니었어요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit glanced at the portrait, very imperfectly following the references to that work of art.", "리틀 도릿은 그 예술 작품에 대한 언급을 아주 어렴풋이 따라가며 초상화를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["‘Mr F.", "\"에프 씨는 제게 어찌나 헌신적이었던지 저를 잠시도 눈앞에서 떼어 놓질 못했어요.\""], ["was so devoted to me that he never could bear me out of his sight,’ said Flora, ‘though of course I am unable to say how long that might have lasted if he hadn’t been cut short while I was a new broom, worthy man but not poetical manly prose but not romance.’", "플로라가 말했다. \"물론 제가 새 빗자루였을 때 그이가 갑자기 세상을 뜨지 않았더라면 그게 얼마나 갔을지야 알 수 없지만요 훌륭한 사람이었지만 시적이진 않았고 사내다운 산문이었지 로맨스는 아니었어요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit glanced at the portrait again.", "리틀 도릿은 다시 초상화를 흘끗 보았다."], ["The artist had given it a head that would have been, in an intellectual point of view, top-heavy for Shakespeare.", "화가는 지적인 관점에서 보자면 셰익스피어에게도 머리가 너무 무거웠을 법한 두상을 그 인물에게 붙여 놓았다."]], [["‘Romance, however,’ Flora went on, busily arranging Mr F.’s Aunt’s toast, ‘as I openly said to Mr F.", "\"하지만 로맨스로 말하자면,\" 플로라는 에프 씨 고모의 토스트를 분주히 차려 놓으며 말을 이었다."], ["when he proposed to me and you will be surprised to hear that he proposed seven times once in a hackney-coach once in a boat once in a pew once on a donkey at Tunbridge Wells and the rest on his knees, Romance was fled with the early days of Arthur Clennam, our parents tore us asunder we became marble and stern reality usurped the throne, Mr F. said very much to his credit that he was perfectly aware of it and even preferred that state of things accordingly the word was spoken the fiat went forth and such is life you see my dear and yet we do not break but bend, pray make a good breakfast while I go in with the tray.’", "\"그이가 제게 청혼했을 때 제가 에프 씨에게 대놓고 말한 그대로인데 그이가 일곱 번이나 청혼했다는 걸 들으면 아가씨도 놀라실 거예요 한 번은 삯마차 안에서 한 번은 보트에서 한 번은 교회 좌석에서 한 번은 턴브리지 웰스에서 당나귀를 탄 채로 나머지는 무릎을 꿇고서 했지요, 로맨스는 아서 클레넘의 그 시절과 함께 사라졌고 우리 부모님이 우리를 갈라놓아 우리는 대리석이 되었으며 냉엄한 현실이 그 왕좌를 차지했어요 에프 씨는 참으로 떳떳하게도 자기가 그걸 완전히 알고 있으며 오히려 그런 상태를 더 바란다고 말했고 그리하여 말이 떨어지고 명령이 내려졌으니 인생이란 게 그런 거예요 아가씨 그래도 우리는 부러지지 않고 휘어질 뿐이지요, 부디 든든히 아침을 드세요 저는 쟁반을 들고 들어갈 테니.\""]], [["She disappeared, leaving Little Dorrit to ponder over the meaning of her scattered words.", "그녀는 사라지면서 리틀 도릿에게 그 흩어진 말들의 의미를 곱씹도록 남겨 두었다."], ["She soon came back again; and at last began to take her own breakfast, talking all the while.", "그녀는 곧 다시 돌아왔고, 마침내 줄곧 이야기하면서 제 아침을 먹기 시작했다."]], [["‘You see, my dear,’ said Flora, measuring out a spoonful or two of some brown liquid that smelt like brandy, and putting it into her tea, ‘I am obliged to be careful to follow the directions of my medical man though the flavour is anything but agreeable being a poor creature and it may be have never recovered the shock received in youth from too much giving way to crying in the next room when separated from Arthur, have you known him long?’", "\"있잖아요 아가씨,\" 플로라가 브랜디 냄새가 나는 갈색 액체를 한두 숟갈 떠서 자기 차에 넣으며 말했다. \"저는 제 주치의가 일러 준 대로 따르도록 조심해야 해요 맛은 영 달갑지 않지만요 저는 가련한 사람이라 어쩌면 아서와 헤어졌을 때 옆방에서 너무 울어 댄 탓에 젊은 날에 받은 충격에서 끝내 회복하지 못했는지도 몰라요, 그분을 안 지 오래되셨나요?\""]], [["As soon as Little Dorrit comprehended that she had been asked this question--for which time was necessary, the galloping pace of her new patroness having left her far behind--she answered that she had known Mr Clennam ever since his return.", "리틀 도릿은 이 질문을 받았다는 것을 알아차리자마자—새 후원자의 질주하는 속도가 그녀를 멀찍이 뒤에 떨어뜨려 놓은 터라 그러는 데에는 시간이 필요했다—클레넘 씨가 돌아오신 뒤로 줄곧 알고 지냈다고 대답했다."]], [["‘To be sure you couldn’t have known him before unless you had been in China or had corresponded neither of which is likely,’ returned Flora, ‘for travelling-people usually get more or less mahogany and you are not at all so and as to corresponding what about?", "\"하긴 아가씨가 중국에 있었거나 편지를 주고받았다면 모를까 그게 둘 다 그럴 법하지 않으니 그 전에 알았을 리는 없죠.\" 플로라가 받아 말했다."], ["that’s very true unless tea, so it was at his mother’s was it really that you knew him first, highly sensible and firm but dreadfully severe--ought to be the mother of the man in the iron mask.’", "\"여행을 다니는 사람들은 대개 살갗이 마호가니처럼 거뭇해지는데 아가씨는 전혀 그렇지 않고 편지로 말하자면 무슨 사연으로요? 그도 그럴 게 차 거래라면 모를까, 그러니 정말 그분을 처음 알게 된 건 그분 어머니 댁에서였나요 무척 분별 있고 굳센 분이지만 끔찍하게 엄하시지요—철가면을 쓴 사내의 어머니로 딱 어울릴 분이에요.\""]], [["‘Mrs Clennam has been kind to me,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"클레넘 부인은 제게 친절하셨어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Really?", "\"정말요?"], ["I am sure I am glad to hear it because as Arthur’s mother it’s naturally pleasant to my feelings to have a better opinion of her than I had before, though what she thinks of me when I run on as I am certain to do and she sits glowering at me like Fate in a go-cart--shocking comparison really--invalid and not her fault--I never know or can imagine.’", "그 말을 들으니 정말 기뻐요 그분이 아서의 어머니이시니 전보다 더 좋게 여기게 되는 게 제 마음에 자연히 흐뭇한 일이거든요 하긴 제가 분명히 그러게 마련인 것처럼 쉴 새 없이 떠들어 댈 때 그분이 손수레에 탄 운명의 여신처럼 저를 노려보며 앉아 계시면—정말 흉한 비유지만요—병약한 분이니 그분 탓은 아니죠—그분이 저를 어떻게 생각하시는지는 도무지 알 수도 짐작할 수도 없어요.\""]], [["‘Shall I find my work anywhere, ma’am?’ asked Little Dorrit, looking timidly about; ‘can I get it?’", "\"제 일감을 어디서 찾을 수 있을까요, 부인?\" 리틀 도릿이 머뭇머뭇 주위를 둘러보며 물었다. \"가져와도 될까요?\""]], [["‘You industrious little fairy,’ returned Flora, taking, in another cup of tea, another of the doses prescribed by her medical man, ‘there’s not the slightest hurry and it’s better that we should begin by being confidential about our mutual friend--too cold a word for me at least I don’t mean that, very proper expression mutual friend--than become through mere formalities not you but me like the Spartan boy with the fox biting him, which I hope you’ll excuse my bringing up for of all the tiresome boys that will go tumbling into every sort of company that boy’s the tiresomest.’", "\"부지런하고 자그마한 요정님,\" 플로라가 또 한 잔의 차에 주치의가 처방한 약을 또 한 번 타며 받아 말했다. \"조금도 서두를 것 없어요 그보다 우리의 공통된 친구에 관해 흉허물 없이 이야기를 시작하는 편이 나아요—적어도 제게는 너무 차가운 말이지만 그런 뜻은 아니에요, 공통된 친구라는 건 아주 적절한 표현이죠—그저 형식만 차리다가 아가씨가 아니라 제가 여우에게 물린 스파르타 소년처럼 되느니 말이에요, 그런데 그 이야기를 꺼내는 걸 양해해 주셔야겠어요 온갖 자리에 끼어드는 진력나는 소년들 중에서도 그 소년이 제일 진력나니까요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit, her face very pale, sat down again to listen.", "리틀 도릿은 얼굴이 몹시 창백해진 채 다시 앉아 귀를 기울였다."], ["‘Hadn’t I better work the while?’ she asked.", "\"그동안 일을 하는 편이 낫지 않을까요?\" 그녀가 물었다."], ["‘I can work and attend too.", "\"저는 일을 하면서도 들을 수 있어요."], ["I would rather, if I may.’", "괜찮으시다면 그러는 편이 좋겠어요.\""]], [["Her earnestness was so expressive of her being uneasy without her work, that Flora answered, ‘Well my dear whatever you like best,’ and produced a basket of white handkerchiefs.", "그녀의 간곡함이 일거리가 없으면 마음이 불편하다는 뜻을 어찌나 잘 드러냈던지, 플로라는 \"그럼 아가씨 가장 좋은 대로 하세요.\" 하고 대답하며 흰 손수건이 든 바구니를 내놓았다."], ["Little Dorrit gladly put it by her side, took out her little pocket-housewife, threaded the needle, and began to hem.", "리틀 도릿은 기쁘게 그것을 곁에 두고, 작은 반짇고리를 꺼내 바늘에 실을 꿰고는 단을 박기 시작했다."]], [["‘What nimble fingers you have,’ said Flora, ‘but are you sure you are well?’", "\"손가락이 어찌나 날쌘지.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그런데 정말 몸은 괜찮으세요?\""]], [["‘Oh yes, indeed!’", "\"오 네, 정말이에요!\""]], [["Flora put her feet upon the fender, and settled herself for a thorough good romantic disclosure.", "플로라는 두 발을 난로 울에 올리고, 한바탕 제대로 된 낭만적인 고백을 위해 자세를 잡았다."], ["She started off at score, tossing her head, sighing in the most demonstrative manner, making a great deal of use of her eyebrows, and occasionally, but not often, glancing at the quiet face that bent over the work.", "그녀는 단숨에 내달리듯 시작했다. 머리를 까딱이고, 더없이 과시하듯 한숨을 쉬고, 눈썹을 한껏 놀려 가며, 이따금, 그러나 자주는 아니게, 일감 위로 수그린 그 고요한 얼굴을 흘끗거렸다."]], [["‘You must know my dear,’ said Flora, ‘but that I have no doubt you know already not only because I have already thrown it out in a general way but because I feel I carry it stamped in burning what’s his names upon my brow that before I was introduced to the late Mr F.", "\"아가씨도 아셔야 해요.\" 플로라가 말했다."], ["I had been engaged to Arthur Clennam--Mr Clennam in public where reserve is necessary Arthur here--we were all in all to one another it was the morning of life it was bliss it was frenzy it was everything else of that sort in the highest degree, when rent asunder we turned to stone in which capacity Arthur went to China and I became the statue bride of the late Mr F.’", "\"하긴 이미 아시리라 믿어 의심치 않지만요 제가 두루뭉술하게나마 이미 흘려 두기도 했고 또 제 이마에 그 뭐라더라 활활 타는 무엇으로 새겨진 채 다니는 게 느껴지기도 하니까요 곧 제가 작고하신 에프 씨에게 소개되기 전에 아서 클레넘과 약혼한 사이였다는 거예요—체면을 차려야 하는 자리에서는 클레넘 씨, 여기서는 아서—우리는 서로에게 전부였고 그것은 인생의 아침이었으며 더없는 행복이자 광란이자 그런 종류의 온갖 것이 극에 달한 상태였는데 갈가리 찢기자 우리는 돌이 되었고 그 처지로 아서는 중국으로 갔으며 저는 작고하신 에프 씨의 석상 신부가 되었지요.\""]], [["Flora, uttering these words in a deep voice, enjoyed herself immensely.", "플로라는 이 말을 굵은 목소리로 읊으며 더없는 즐거움을 만끽했다."]], [["‘To paint,’ said she, ‘the emotions of that morning when all was marble within and Mr F.’s Aunt followed in a glass-coach which it stands to reason must have been in shameful repair or it never could have broken down two streets from the house and Mr F.’s Aunt brought home like the fifth of November in a rush-bottomed chair I will not attempt, suffice it to say that the hollow form of breakfast took place in the dining-room downstairs that papa partaking too freely of pickled salmon was ill for weeks and that Mr F.", "\"속이 온통 대리석이던 그 아침의 심정을 그려 보자면,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["and myself went upon a continental tour to Calais where the people fought for us on the pier until they separated us though not for ever that was not yet to be.’", "\"에프 씨의 고모님은 유리 마차에 타고 뒤따랐는데 그게 분명 형편없이 망가진 마차였을 거예요 안 그랬다면 집에서 두 거리 떨어진 데서 고장 날 리가 없었을 테고 에프 씨의 고모님은 마치 십일월 오일 행사 인형처럼 골풀 방석 의자에 실려 집으로 돌아오셨지요 그 심정을 그려 보려는 시도는 하지 않겠어요, 다만 아래층 식당에서 형식뿐인 아침 식사가 치러졌고 절인 연어를 너무 욕심껏 드신 아빠가 몇 주를 앓으셨으며 에프 씨와 저는 칼레로 대륙 여행을 떠났는데 거기 부두에서 사람들이 우리 짐을 서로 들겠다고 다투다가 결국 우리를 떼어 놓고 말았다는 것만 말씀드리면 충분해요 하긴 영원히는 아니었죠 그건 아직 일어날 일이 아니었으니까요.\""]], [["The statue bride, hardly pausing for breath, went on, with the greatest complacency, in a rambling manner sometimes incidental to flesh and blood.", "석상 신부는 숨 돌릴 새도 거의 없이, 더없이 흡족하게, 때로 살아 있는 사람에게 따라붙곤 하는 두서없는 식으로 말을 이었다."]], [["‘I will draw a veil over that dreamy life, Mr F. was in good spirits his appetite was good he liked the cookery he considered the wine weak but palatable and all was well, we returned to the immediate neighbourhood of Number Thirty Little Gosling Street London Docks and settled down, ere we had yet fully detected the housemaid in selling the feathers out of the spare bed Gout flying upwards soared with Mr F. to another sphere.’", "\"그 꿈결 같은 삶에는 장막을 드리우겠어요, 에프 씨는 기분이 좋았고 식욕이 좋았고 요리를 좋아했고 포도주는 묽지만 마실 만하다고 여겼으니 만사가 평안했어요, 우리는 런던 부두의 리틀 고슬링 가 삼십 번지 바로 근처로 돌아와 정착했고 가정부가 손님용 침대에서 깃털을 빼내 팔아먹는 걸 미처 다 적발하기도 전에 통풍이 위로 솟구쳐 에프 씨와 함께 다른 세계로 날아올라 갔지요.\""]], [["His relict, with a glance at his portrait, shook her head and wiped her eyes.", "그의 미망인은 초상화를 흘끗 보고는 고개를 저으며 눈물을 훔쳤다."]], [["‘I revere the memory of Mr F. as an estimable man and most indulgent husband, only necessary to mention Asparagus and it appeared or to hint at any little delicate thing to drink and it came like magic in a pint bottle it was not ecstasy but it was comfort, I returned to papa’s roof and lived secluded if not happy during some years until one day papa came smoothly blundering in and said that Arthur Clennam awaited me below, I went below and found him ask me not what I found him except that he was still unmarried still unchanged!’", "\"저는 에프 씨를 존경할 만한 분이자 더없이 너그러운 남편으로서 그 기억을 기립니다, 아스파라거스라고 한마디만 하면 그게 나타났고 무슨 자그마한 마실 것을 넌지시 비치기만 해도 마법처럼 일 파인트 병에 담겨 왔으니 황홀경은 아니었지만 안락이었지요, 저는 아빠의 지붕 아래로 돌아와 행복하지는 않을지언정 몇 해를 두문불출하며 지냈어요 그러던 어느 날 아빠가 슬그머니 어물쩍 들어오시더니 아래층에서 아서 클레넘이 저를 기다린다고 하시기에 내려가 보니 그분이 계셨는데 어떤 모습이었는지는 묻지 말아 주세요 다만 그분이 여전히 미혼이고 여전히 변함없더라는 것만은!\""]], [["The dark mystery with which Flora now enshrouded herself might have stopped other fingers than the nimble fingers that worked near her.", "플로라가 이제 제 몸에 두른 어두운 신비는 그녀 곁에서 일하는 날쌘 손가락이 아닌 다른 손가락이었다면 멈추게 했을지도 몰랐다."], ["They worked on without pause, and the busy head bent over them watching the stitches.", "그러나 그 손가락들은 쉼 없이 움직였고, 분주한 머리는 그 위로 수그린 채 한 땀 한 땀을 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Ask me not,’ said Flora, ‘if I love him still or if he still loves me or what the end is to be or when, we are surrounded by watchful eyes and it may be that we are destined to pine asunder it may be never more to be reunited not a word not a breath not a look to betray us all must be secret as the tomb wonder not therefore that even if I should seem comparatively cold to Arthur or Arthur should seem comparatively cold to me we have fatal reasons it is enough if we understand them hush!’", "\"묻지 말아 주세요.\" 플로라가 말했다. \"제가 아직도 그분을 사랑하는지 그분이 아직도 저를 사랑하는지 그 끝이 어떻게 될지 언제일지, 우리는 지켜보는 눈들에 에워싸여 있고 어쩌면 우리는 서로 떨어져 애태우다가 어쩌면 다시는 결코 맺어지지 못할 운명인지도 몰라요 한마디도 한숨도 한 번의 눈길도 우리를 들켜선 안 되고 모든 것이 무덤처럼 비밀이어야 하니 그러므로 제가 아서에게 비교적 차갑게 보이거나 아서가 제게 비교적 차갑게 보인다 해도 놀라지 마세요 우리에게는 숙명적인 사정이 있고 우리가 그것을 알면 그만이에요 쉿!\""]], [["All of which Flora said with so much headlong vehemence as if she really believed it.", "이 모든 것을 플로라는 정말로 그렇게 믿는 양 어찌나 앞뒤 없이 격렬하게 말하던지."], ["There is not much doubt that when she worked herself into full mermaid condition, she did actually believe whatever she said in it.", "그녀가 완전한 인어 상태로 자신을 몰아넣었을 때에는 그 상태에서 하는 말이 무엇이든 실제로 믿었다는 데에 의심의 여지가 별로 없다."]], [["‘Hush!’ repeated Flora, ‘I have now told you all, confidence is established between us hush, for Arthur’s sake I will always be a friend to you my dear girl and in Arthur’s name you may always rely upon me.’", "\"쉿!\" 플로라가 거듭 말했다. \"이제 다 말씀드렸어요 우리 사이에 흉허물 없는 믿음이 생겼으니 쉿, 아서를 위해서라도 저는 언제나 아가씨의 친구가 되어 드리겠어요 사랑스러운 아가씨 그리고 아서의 이름으로 언제든 저를 믿으셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["The nimble fingers laid aside the work, and the little figure rose and kissed her hand.", "날쌘 손가락들이 일감을 내려놓았고, 그 자그마한 사람이 일어나 플로라의 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["‘You are very cold,’ said Flora, changing to her own natural kind-hearted manner, and gaining greatly by the change.", "\"손이 무척 차네요.\" 플로라가 본래의 따뜻하고 다정한 태도로 돌아오며 말했는데, 그 변화로 한결 보기 좋아졌다."], ["‘Don’t work to-day.", "\"오늘은 일하지 마세요."], ["I am sure you are not well I am sure you are not strong.’", "분명 몸이 좋지 않으신 거예요 분명 기운이 없으신 거예요.\""]], [["‘It is only that I feel a little overcome by your kindness, and by Mr Clennam’s kindness in confiding me to one he has known and loved so long.’", "\"그저 부인의 친절과, 그토록 오래 알고 아껴 오신 분께 저를 맡기신 클레넘 씨의 친절에 조금 압도되었을 뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Well really my dear,’ said Flora, who had a decided tendency to be always honest when she gave herself time to think about it, ‘it’s as well to leave that alone now, for I couldn’t undertake to say after all, but it doesn’t signify lie down a little!’", "\"글쎄 정말이지 아가씨,\" 곰곰이 생각할 짬이 생기면 늘 정직해지려는 뚜렷한 성향이 있는 플로라가 말했다. \"그건 이제 그냥 놔두는 편이 낫겠어요 어쨌든 제가 단언할 수는 없으니까요 하지만 그런 건 중요치 않아요 잠깐 누우세요!\""]], [["‘I have always been strong enough to do what I want to do, and I shall be quite well directly,’ returned Little Dorrit, with a faint smile.", "\"저는 늘 하고 싶은 일을 할 만큼은 튼튼했어요, 곧 아주 괜찮아질 거예요.\" 리틀 도릿이 희미한 미소를 띠며 받아 말했다."], ["‘You have overpowered me with gratitude, that’s all.", "\"그저 감사한 마음에 압도되었을 뿐이에요."], ["If I keep near the window for a moment I shall be quite myself.’", "잠깐 창가에 있으면 곧 제정신이 들 거예요.\""]], [["Flora opened a window, sat her in a chair by it, and considerately retired to her former place.", "플로라는 창을 열고 그 옆 의자에 그녀를 앉힌 뒤, 사려 깊게 제자리로 물러났다."], ["It was a windy day, and the air stirring on Little Dorrit’s face soon brightened it.", "바람 부는 날이었고, 리틀 도릿의 얼굴을 스치는 바람이 이내 그 얼굴을 환하게 했다."], ["In a very few minutes she returned to her basket of work, and her nimble fingers were as nimble as ever.", "채 몇 분도 안 되어 그녀는 일감 바구니로 돌아갔고, 날쌘 손가락은 여느 때처럼 날쌨다."]], [["Quietly pursuing her task, she asked Flora if Mr Clennam had told her where she lived?", "그녀는 조용히 일을 이어 가다가, 클레넘 씨가 자기가 어디 사는지 말씀하셨느냐고 플로라에게 물었다."], ["When Flora replied in the negative, Little Dorrit said that she understood why he had been so delicate, but that she felt sure he would approve of her confiding her secret to Flora, and that she would therefore do so now with Flora’s permission.", "플로라가 아니라고 대답하자, 리틀 도릿은 그분이 왜 그토록 세심하게 입을 다무셨는지 이해한다고, 하지만 자기 비밀을 플로라에게 털어놓는 것을 그분이 분명 좋아하시리라 믿는다고, 그러니 플로라가 허락한다면 이제 그러겠다고 말했다."], ["Receiving an encouraging answer, she condensed the narrative of her life into a few scanty words about herself and a glowing eulogy upon her father; and Flora took it all in with a natural tenderness that quite understood it, and in which there was no incoherence.", "격려하는 대답을 듣고 그녀는 제 삶의 이야기를 자신에 관한 몇 마디 짧은 말과 아버지에 대한 빛나는 찬사로 압축했다. 플로라는 그 모두를 그것을 온전히 이해하는 천성의 다정함으로 받아들였는데, 거기에는 두서없는 구석이 조금도 없었다."]], [["When dinner-time came, Flora drew the arm of her new charge through hers, and led her down-stairs, and presented her to the Patriarch and Mr Pancks, who were already in the dining-room waiting to begin.", "저녁 식사 때가 되자 플로라는 새로 맡은 사람의 팔을 제 팔에 끼고 아래층으로 데려가, 이미 식당에서 식사를 시작하려고 기다리고 있던 가부장과 팽크스 씨에게 소개했다."], ["(Mr F.’s Aunt was, for the time, laid up in ordinary in her chamber.) By those gentlemen she was received according to their characters; the Patriarch appearing to do her some inestimable service in saying that he was glad to see her, glad to see her; and Mr Pancks blowing off his favourite sound as a salute.", "(에프 씨의 고모는 그동안 자기 방에 드러누워 출항 준비 중인 배처럼 정박해 있었다.) 그 신사들은 각자의 성격대로 그녀를 맞이했다. 가부장은 만나서 반갑다고, 반갑다고 말하는 것으로 그녀에게 헤아릴 수 없는 은혜라도 베푸는 듯했고, 팽크스 씨는 인사 삼아 자신이 즐겨 내는 소리를 한 번 뿜어냈다."]], [["In that new presence she would have been bashful enough under any circumstances, and particularly under Flora’s insisting on her drinking a glass of wine and eating of the best that was there; but her constraint was greatly increased by Mr Pancks.", "그 새로운 자리에서라면 어떤 상황에서든 그녀는 충분히 수줍어했을 테고, 더욱이 포도주 한 잔을 마시고 거기 있는 가장 좋은 것을 먹으라고 고집하는 플로라 앞에서는 더했을 테지만, 그녀의 거북함을 한층 키운 것은 팽크스 씨였다."], ["The demeanour of that gentleman at first suggested to her mind that he might be a taker of likenesses, so intently did he look at her, and so frequently did he glance at the little note-book by his side.", "그 신사의 거동은 처음에 그녀의 마음속에 그가 초상화가일지도 모른다는 생각을 불러일으켰다. 어찌나 그녀를 뚫어지게 보고, 어찌나 자주 자기 곁의 작은 수첩을 흘끗거리던지."], ["Observing that he made no sketch, however, and that he talked about business only, she began to have suspicions that he represented some creditor of her father’s, the balance due to whom was noted in that pocket volume.", "그러나 그가 아무 그림도 그리지 않고 오로지 사업 이야기만 하는 것을 보고서, 그녀는 그가 자기 아버지의 어떤 채권자를 대리하는 사람이고, 갚아야 할 잔액이 그 수첩에 적혀 있는 게 아닐까 의심하기 시작했다."], ["Regarded from this point of view Mr Pancks’s puffings expressed injury and impatience, and each of his louder snorts became a demand for payment.", "이런 관점에서 보니 팽크스 씨의 씩씩거림은 손해와 조바심을 나타냈고, 그가 더 크게 콧김을 뿜을 때마다 그것은 지불을 요구하는 것이 되었다."]], [["But here again she was undeceived by anomalous and incongruous conduct on the part of Mr Pancks himself.", "그러나 여기서도 그녀는 팽크스 씨 자신의 변칙적이고 모순된 행동에 속았음을 깨달았다."], ["She had left the table half an hour, and was at work alone.", "그녀는 식탁을 떠난 지 삼십 분이 지나 혼자 일하고 있었다."], ["Flora had ‘gone to lie down’ in the next room, concurrently with which retirement a smell of something to drink had broken out in the house.", "플로라는 옆방으로 \"누우러 갔고,\" 그 은퇴와 동시에 집안에 무언가 마실 것의 냄새가 터져 나왔다."], ["The Patriarch was fast asleep, with his philanthropic mouth open under a yellow pocket-handkerchief in the dining-room.", "가부장은 식당에서 박애의 입을 노란 손수건 아래 벌린 채 곤히 잠들어 있었다."], ["At this quiet time, Mr Pancks softly appeared before her, urbanely nodding.", "이 고요한 때에 팽크스 씨가 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 그녀 앞에 슬며시 나타났다."]], [["‘Find it a little dull, Miss Dorrit?’ inquired Pancks in a low voice.", "\"좀 따분하시죠, 도릿 양?\" 팽크스가 낮은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["‘No, thank you, sir,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"아니에요, 고마워요, 선생님.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Busy, I see,’ observed Mr Pancks, stealing into the room by inches.", "\"바쁘시군요, 보아하니.\" 팽크스 씨가 한 치씩 슬그머니 방으로 들어오며 말했다."], ["‘What are those now, Miss Dorrit?’", "\"그게 지금 뭡니까, 도릿 양?\""]], [["‘Handkerchiefs.’", "\"손수건이에요.\""]], [["‘Are they, though!’ said Pancks.", "\"아니 그래요!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I shouldn’t have thought it.’ Not in the least looking at them, but looking at Little Dorrit.", "\"전 그렇게 생각 못 했는데요.\" 손수건은 조금도 보지 않고, 리틀 도릿을 보면서 한 말이었다."], ["‘Perhaps you wonder who I am.", "\"어쩌면 제가 누군지 궁금하시겠죠."], ["Shall I tell you?", "말씀드릴까요?"], ["I am a fortune-teller.’", "저는 점쟁이입니다.\""]], [["Little Dorrit now began to think he was mad.", "리틀 도릿은 이제 그가 미친 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["‘I belong body and soul to my proprietor,’ said Pancks; ‘you saw my proprietor having his dinner below. But I do a little in the other way, sometimes; privately, very privately, Miss Dorrit.’", "\"저는 몸도 마음도 제 주인에게 속해 있습니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"아래층에서 식사하시던 제 주인을 보셨지요. 하지만 가끔은 다른 쪽 일도 조금 합니다. 은밀히, 아주 은밀히요, 도릿 양.\""]], [["Little Dorrit looked at him doubtfully, and not without alarm.", "리틀 도릿은 미심쩍게, 그리고 적잖이 놀란 채 그를 바라보았다."], ["‘I wish you’d show me the palm of your hand,’ said Pancks.", "\"손바닥을 좀 보여 주시면 좋겠는데요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I should like to have a look at it.", "\"한번 들여다보고 싶어서요."], ["Don’t let me be troublesome.’", "폐가 되지 않게 해 주세요.\""]], [["He was so far troublesome that he was not at all wanted there, but she laid her work in her lap for a moment, and held out her left hand with her thimble on it.", "폐로 따지자면 그는 거기 전혀 있을 필요가 없다는 점에서 폐가 되었지만, 그녀는 잠시 일감을 무릎에 내려놓고, 골무를 낀 왼손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘Years of toil, eh?’ said Pancks, softly, touching it with his blunt forefinger.", "\"수고로 보낸 세월이군요, 그렇죠?\" 팽크스가 뭉툭한 집게손가락으로 손을 짚으며 부드럽게 말했다."], ["‘But what else are we made for?", "\"하지만 우리가 그 말고 무엇 때문에 만들어졌겠습니까? 아무것도 아니지요."], ["Nothing.", "이런!\""], ["Hallo!’ looking into the lines.", "손금을 들여다보며 그가 말했다."], ["‘What’s this with bars?", "\"창살이 있는 이건 뭡니까?"], ["It’s a College!", "감옥이로군요!"], ["And what’s this with a grey gown and a black velvet cap?", "그리고 회색 가운에 검은 우단 모자를 쓴 이건 뭡니까?"], ["it’s a father!", "아버지로군요!"], ["And what’s this with a clarionet?", "그리고 클라리넷을 든 이건 뭡니까?"], ["It’s an uncle!", "삼촌이로군요!"], ["And what’s this in dancing-shoes?", "그리고 무도화를 신은 이건 뭡니까?"], ["It’s a sister!", "누이로군요!"], ["And what’s this straggling about in an idle sort of a way?", "그리고 빈둥대듯 어슬렁대는 이건 뭡니까?"], ["It’s a brother!", "오라비로군요!"], ["And what’s this thinking for ‘em all?", "그리고 이 모두를 위해 궁리하는 이건 뭡니까?"], ["Why, this is you, Miss Dorrit!’", "아니, 이건 바로 당신이로군요, 도릿 양!\""]], [["Her eyes met his as she looked up wonderingly into his face, and she thought that although his were sharp eyes, he was a brighter and gentler-looking man than she had supposed at dinner.", "그녀가 의아해하며 그의 얼굴을 올려다보았을 때 그녀의 눈과 그의 눈이 마주쳤고, 그녀는 그의 눈이 비록 날카롭기는 해도 그가 저녁 식사 때 짐작했던 것보다 더 환하고 부드러운 인상의 사람이라고 생각했다."], ["His eyes were on her hand again directly, and her opportunity of confirming or correcting the impression was gone.", "그의 눈은 곧장 다시 그녀의 손으로 향했고, 그 인상을 확인하거나 바로잡을 기회는 사라졌다."]], [["‘Now, the deuce is in it,’ muttered Pancks, tracing out a line in her hand with his clumsy finger, ‘if this isn’t me in the corner here!", "\"이런, 별일이로군.\" 팽크스가 그 투박한 손가락으로 그녀의 손금을 더듬으며 중얼거렸다."], ["What do I want here?", "\"여기 구석에 있는 이게 저라니! 제가 여기서 뭘 바라는 걸까요?"], ["What’s behind me?’", "제 뒤에 있는 건 뭘까요?\""]], [["He carried his finger slowly down to the wrist, and round the wrist, and affected to look at the back of the hand for what was behind him.", "그는 손가락을 천천히 손목까지 내리더니, 손목을 빙 둘러, 자기 뒤에 있는 것을 보려는 양 손등을 살펴보는 시늉을 했다."]], [["‘Is it any harm?’ asked Little Dorrit, smiling.", "\"무슨 해로운 거라도 있나요?\" 리틀 도릿이 미소 지으며 물었다."]], [["‘Deuce a bit!’ said Pancks.", "\"천만에요!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘What do you think it’s worth?’", "\"그 값어치가 얼마나 될 것 같습니까?\""]], [["‘I ought to ask you that.", "\"그건 제가 선생님께 여쭐 말이지요."], ["I am not the fortune-teller.’", "점쟁이는 제가 아니니까요.\""]], [["‘True,’ said Pancks.", "\"그렇군요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘What’s it worth?", "\"그 값어치가 얼마냐고요?"], ["You shall live to see, Miss Dorrit.’", "살아서 직접 보시게 될 겁니다, 도릿 양.\""]], [["Releasing the hand by slow degrees, he drew all his fingers through his prongs of hair, so that they stood up in their most portentous manner; and repeated slowly, ‘Remember what I say, Miss Dorrit.", "그는 천천히 손을 놓아주며, 자기의 뻗친 머리카락 사이로 손가락을 모두 훑어 넣어 그 머리카락이 더없이 불길한 모양으로 곤두서게 했다. 그러고는 천천히 되풀이했다."], ["You shall live to see.’", "\"제 말을 기억하세요, 도릿 양. 살아서 보시게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["She could not help showing that she was much surprised, if it were only by his knowing so much about her.", "그녀는 적어도 그가 자기에 관해 그토록 많이 안다는 사실만으로도 몹시 놀랐다는 것을 드러내지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["That’s it!’ said Pancks, pointing at her.", "바로 그거예요!\" 팽크스가 그녀를 가리키며 말했다."], ["‘Miss Dorrit, not that, ever!’", "\"도릿 양, 절대 그러시면 안 됩니다, 결코요!\""]], [["More surprised than before, and a little more frightened, she looked to him for an explanation of his last words.", "전보다 더 놀라고 조금 더 겁이 난 채 그녀는 그 마지막 말의 설명을 구하려 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Not that,’ said Pancks, making, with great seriousness, an imitation of a surprised look and manner that appeared to be unintentionally grotesque.", "\"그러지 마세요.\" 팽크스가 자못 진지하게, 본의 아니게 기괴해 보이는 놀란 표정과 몸짓을 흉내 내며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t do that.", "\"그러지 마세요."], ["Never on seeing me, no matter when, no matter where.", "저를 보더라도 절대로요, 언제든, 어디서든. 저는 아무것도 아닌 사람입니다."], ["I am nobody. Don’t take on to mind me.", "저를 신경 쓰지 마세요."], ["Don’t mention me.", "저를 입에 올리지 마세요."], ["Take no notice.", "모르는 척하세요."], ["Will you agree, Miss Dorrit?’", "약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\""]], [["‘I hardly know what to say,’ returned Little Dorrit, quite astounded.", "\"뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 어안이 벙벙해져 받아 말했다."], ["‘Why?’", "\"왜요?\""]], [["‘Because I am a fortune-teller.", "\"제가 점쟁이라서요."], ["Pancks the gipsy.", "집시 팽크스라서요."], ["I haven’t told you so much of your fortune yet, Miss Dorrit, as to tell you what’s behind me on that little hand.", "도릿 양, 저는 아직 그 작은 손에서 제 뒤에 무엇이 있는지 말씀드릴 만큼 당신의 운명을 다 말해 드리지 않았어요."], ["I have told you you shall live to see.", "살아서 보시게 될 거라고만 말씀드렸지요."], ["Is it agreed, Miss Dorrit?’", "약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\""]], [["‘Agreed that I--am--to--’", "\"제가—그러니까—\""]], [["‘To take no notice of me away from here, unless I take on first.", "\"여기를 벗어나서는 제가 먼저 아는 척하지 않는 한 저를 모르는 척하시는 거예요."], ["Not to mind me when I come and go.", "제가 오고 갈 때 저를 신경 쓰지 않으시는 거지요."], ["It’s very easy.", "아주 쉬운 일이에요."], ["I am no loss, I am not handsome, I am not good company, I am only my proprietors grubber.", "저는 잃을 게 없는 사람이고, 잘생기지도 않았고, 좋은 말동무도 아니고, 그저 제 주인의 돈 캐는 일꾼일 뿐이니까요."], ["You need do no more than think, “Ah!", "그저 이렇게만 생각하시면 돼요. '아!"], ["Pancks the gipsy at his fortune-telling--he’ll tell the rest of my fortune one day--I shall live to know it.”", "집시 팽크스가 점을 치는구나—언젠가 내 운명의 나머지를 말해 주겠지—나는 살아서 그걸 알게 되겠지.'"], ["Is it agreed, Miss Dorrit?’", "약속하시겠습니까, 도릿 양?\""]], [["‘Ye-es,’ faltered Little Dorrit, whom he greatly confused, ‘I suppose so, while you do no harm.’", "\"네—에.\" 그가 자기를 몹시 혼란스럽게 한 리틀 도릿이 더듬더듬 말했다. \"그래야겠지요, 선생님이 해를 끼치지 않으신다면요.\""]], [["‘Good!’ Mr Pancks glanced at the wall of the adjoining room, and stooped forward.", "\"좋습니다!\" 팽크스 씨는 옆방 벽을 흘끗 보고는 앞으로 몸을 숙였다."], ["‘Honest creature, woman of capital points, but heedless and a loose talker, Miss Dorrit.’ With that he rubbed his hands as if the interview had been very satisfactory to him, panted away to the door, and urbanely nodded himself out again.", "\"정직한 사람이고, 됨됨이는 훌륭하지만, 조심성이 없고 입이 헤픈 분이지요, 도릿 양.\" 그러고는 면담이 무척 흡족했다는 듯 두 손을 비비고, 헐떡이며 문 쪽으로 가더니, 정중하게 고개를 끄덕이며 다시 빠져나갔다."]], [["If Little Dorrit were beyond measure perplexed by this curious conduct on the part of her new acquaintance, and by finding herself involved in this singular treaty, her perplexity was not diminished by ensuing circumstances.", "리틀 도릿이 새로 사귄 사람의 이 묘한 행동에, 그리고 이 별난 약조에 휘말린 자신을 보고서 한없이 어리둥절했다면, 그 어리둥절함은 뒤이어 벌어진 일들로도 줄어들지 않았다."], ["Besides that Mr Pancks took every opportunity afforded him in Mr Casby’s house of significantly glancing at her and snorting at her--which was not much, after what he had done already--he began to pervade her daily life.", "팽크스 씨는 캐스비 씨의 집에서 기회가 주어질 때마다 그녀를 의미심장하게 흘끗거리고 코를 씩씩거렸을 뿐 아니라—이미 한 짓을 생각하면 그쯤이야 별것 아니었지만—그녀의 일상생활 속으로 스며들기 시작했다."], ["She saw him in the street, constantly.", "그녀는 거리에서 줄곧 그를 보았다."], ["When she went to Mr Casby’s, he was always there.", "캐스비 씨 댁에 가면 그는 늘 거기 있었다."], ["When she went to Mrs Clennam’s, he came there on any pretence, as if to keep her in his sight.", "클레넘 부인 댁에 가면 그는 무슨 핑계를 대서라도 그곳에 나타나, 마치 그녀를 시야에 두려는 듯했다."], ["A week had not gone by, when she found him to her astonishment in the Lodge one night, conversing with the turnkey on duty, and to all appearance one of his familiar companions.", "일주일도 채 지나기 전에 그녀는 어느 날 밤 수위실에서 당직 간수와 이야기를 나누는, 그것도 보아하니 그의 막역한 동무 중 하나인 듯한 그를 발견하고 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Her next surprise was to find him equally at his ease within the prison; to hear of his presenting himself among the visitors at her father’s Sunday levee; to see him arm in arm with a Collegiate friend about the yard; to learn, from Fame, that he had greatly distinguished himself one evening at the social club that held its meetings in the Snuggery, by addressing a speech to the members of the institution, singing a song, and treating the company to five gallons of ale--report madly added a bushel of shrimps.", "그다음 놀라움은 그가 감옥 안에서도 똑같이 편안해하는 것을 본 일이었다. 일요일에 아버지가 베푸는 알현 자리에 손님들 틈에 끼어 모습을 드러냈다는 이야기를 들은 일, 마당에서 수감자 친구와 팔짱을 끼고 다니는 것을 본 일, 어느 날 저녁 스너거리에서 모임을 갖는 사교 클럽에서 회원들에게 연설을 하고 노래를 부르고 일동에게 맥주 다섯 갤런을—소문은 미친 듯이 새우 한 부셸까지 보탰다—대접하여 크게 명성을 떨쳤다는 소문을 들은 일이었다."], ["The effect on Mr Plornish of such of these phenomena as he became an eye-witness of in his faithful visits, made an impression on Little Dorrit only second to that produced by the phenomena themselves.", "이러한 현상들 가운데 충실한 방문 중에 직접 목격한 것들이 플로니시 씨에게 미친 영향은, 그 현상들 자체가 자아낸 인상에 버금가는 인상을 리틀 도릿에게 주었다."], ["They seemed to gag and bind him.", "그것들은 그를 재갈 물리고 결박하는 듯했다."], ["He could only stare, and sometimes weakly mutter that it wouldn’t be believed down Bleeding Heart Yard that this was Pancks; but he never said a word more, or made a sign more, even to Little Dorrit.", "그는 그저 멍하니 쳐다볼 뿐이었고, 이따금 이게 팽크스라는 걸 블리딩 하트 야드에서는 믿지 않을 거라고 힘없이 중얼거릴 뿐이었다. 그러나 그는 그 이상 한마디도 더 하지 않았고, 리틀 도릿에게조차 더는 어떤 내색도 하지 않았다."], ["Mr Pancks crowned his mysteries by making himself acquainted with Tip in some unknown manner, and taking a Sunday saunter into the College on that gentleman’s arm.", "팽크스 씨는 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 팁과 친해지고, 일요일에 그 신사의 팔짱을 끼고 감옥으로 어슬렁어슬렁 들어옴으로써 자신의 신비를 절정에 이르게 했다. 그러는 내내 그는 리틀 도릿에게 전혀 아는 척을 하지 않았는데, 다만 한두 번 우연히 그녀 가까이 다가왔고 주위에 사람이 아주 가까이 없을 때만은 예외였다."], ["Throughout he never took any notice of Little Dorrit, save once or twice when he happened to come close to her and there was no one very near; on which occasions, he said in passing, with a friendly look and a puff of encouragement, ‘Pancks the gipsy--fortune-telling.’", "그런 경우에 그는 다정한 눈길과 격려의 콧김을 한 번 뿜으며 지나가는 말로 이렇게 말했다. \"집시 팽크스—점치기.\""]], [["Little Dorrit worked and strove as usual, wondering at all this, but keeping her wonder, as she had from her earliest years kept many heavier loads, in her own breast.", "리틀 도릿은 이 모든 것을 의아해하면서도, 어린 시절부터 더 무거운 짐을 수없이 가슴에 담아 두어 온 것처럼 그 의아함도 가슴속에 간직한 채, 여느 때처럼 일하고 애썼다."], ["A change had stolen, and was stealing yet, over the patient heart.", "그 참을성 많은 마음 위로 어떤 변화가 슬며시 깃들었고, 여전히 깃들어 가고 있었다."], ["Every day found her something more retiring than the day before.", "하루하루가 지날 때마다 그녀는 전날보다 한층 더 움츠러들어 있었다."], ["To pass in and out of the prison unnoticed, and elsewhere to be overlooked and forgotten, were, for herself, her chief desires.", "눈에 띄지 않게 감옥을 드나드는 것, 그리고 다른 곳에서는 간과되고 잊히는 것이 그녀 자신을 위한 가장 큰 바람이었다."]], [["To her own room too, strangely assorted room for her delicate youth and character, she was glad to retreat as often as she could without desertion of any duty.", "그녀의 섬세한 젊음과 성품에는 어울리지 않게 짝지어진 방이긴 했으나, 자기 방으로도 그녀는 어떤 의무도 저버리지 않는 한 되도록 자주 물러나기를 기뻐했다."], ["There were afternoon times when she was unemployed, when visitors dropped in to play a hand at cards with her father, when she could be spared and was better away.", "오후에 일거리가 없을 때, 손님들이 들러 아버지와 카드 한판을 벌일 때, 그녀가 없어도 될 때, 아니 차라리 자리를 비우는 편이 나을 때가 있었다."], ["Then she would flit along the yard, climb the scores of stairs that led to her room, and take her seat at the window.", "그러면 그녀는 마당을 가로질러 사뿐히 가서, 제 방으로 이어지는 수십 개의 계단을 올라, 창가에 앉곤 했다."], ["Many combinations did those spikes upon the wall assume, many light shapes did the strong iron weave itself into, many golden touches fell upon the rust, while Little Dorrit sat there musing.", "리틀 도릿이 거기 앉아 골똘히 생각하는 동안, 담장 위의 그 뾰족한 창들은 숱한 형상을 이루었고, 그 단단한 쇠붙이는 스스로 숱한 가벼운 모양으로 엮였으며, 숱한 황금빛 손길이 녹슨 표면에 내려앉았다."], ["New zig-zags sprung into the cruel pattern sometimes, when she saw it through a burst of tears; but beautified or hardened still, always over it and under it and through it, she was fain to look in her solitude, seeing everything with that ineffaceable brand.", "눈물이 왈칵 솟구쳐 그것을 흐릿하게 바라볼 때면 이따금 그 잔혹한 무늬 속에 새로운 지그재그가 돋아나기도 했다. 그러나 아름다워지든 모질어지든, 그녀는 고독 속에서 그 위로, 그 아래로, 그 사이로 늘 바라볼 수밖에 없었고, 모든 것을 그 지울 수 없는 낙인과 더불어 보았다."]], [["A garret, and a Marshalsea garret without compromise, was Little Dorrit’s room.", "다락방, 그것도 타협이라곤 없는 마셜시의 다락방이 리틀 도릿의 방이었다."], ["Beautifully kept, it was ugly in itself, and had little but cleanliness and air to set it off; for what embellishment she had ever been able to buy, had gone to her father’s room.", "곱게 건사되었으되 그 자체로는 흉했고, 청결함과 바람 말고는 그것을 돋보이게 하는 것이 거의 없었다. 그녀가 이제껏 살 수 있었던 장식거리는 모조리 아버지의 방으로 갔기 때문이다."], ["Howbeit, for this poor place she showed an increasing love; and to sit in it alone became her favourite rest.", "그럼에도 그녀는 이 초라한 곳에 점점 더 깊은 애정을 보였고, 그곳에 홀로 앉아 있는 것이 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 쉼이 되었다."]], [["Insomuch, that on a certain afternoon during the Pancks mysteries, when she was seated at her window, and heard Maggy’s well-known step coming up the stairs, she was very much disturbed by the apprehension of being summoned away.", "그리하여 팽크스의 신비가 한창이던 어느 오후, 그녀가 창가에 앉아 있다가 계단을 올라오는 매기의 낯익은 발소리를 들었을 때, 그녀는 어디론가 불려 가게 되지 않을까 하는 염려에 몹시 마음이 어지러웠다."], ["As Maggy’s step came higher up and nearer, she trembled and faltered; and it was as much as she could do to speak, when Maggy at length appeared.", "매기의 발소리가 점점 위로, 점점 가까이 올라올수록 그녀는 떨고 머뭇거렸다. 마침내 매기가 나타났을 때 그녀는 가까스로 입을 뗄 수 있었다."]], [["‘Please, Little Mother,’ said Maggy, panting for breath, ‘you must come down and see him.", "\"저기요, 작은엄마.\" 매기가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["He’s here.’", "\"내려가서 그 사람 좀 만나 봐요. 그 사람 와 있어요.\""]], [["‘Who, Maggy?’", "\"누가, 매기?\""]], [["‘Who, o’ course Mr Clennam.", "\"누구긴요, 당연히 클레넘 씨죠."], ["He’s in your father’s room, and he says to me, Maggy, will you be so kind and go and say it’s only me.’", "그 사람이 작은엄마 아버지 방에 있는데, 저한테 그러더라고요, 매기, 미안하지만 가서 그냥 나라고 전해 주겠소, 하고요.\""]], [["‘I am not very well, Maggy.", "\"몸이 별로 안 좋아, 매기."], ["I had better not go.", "안 내려가는 게 낫겠어."], ["I am going to lie down.", "좀 누워야겠어."], ["See!", "봐!"], ["I lie down now, to ease my head.", "머리를 식히려고 지금 누울게."], ["Say, with my grateful regard, that you left me so, or I would have come.’", "고맙고 정중한 인사를 전하고, 내가 이렇게 누워 있어서 그렇지 안 그랬으면 내려갔을 거라고 말씀드려.\""]], [["‘Well, it an’t very polite though, Little Mother,’ said the staring Maggy, ‘to turn your face away, neither!’", "\"근데 그것도 별로 예의 바른 건 아니에요, 작은엄마.\" 멍하니 쳐다보던 매기가 말했다. \"얼굴을 그렇게 돌려 버리는 것도요!\""]], [["Maggy was very susceptible to personal slights, and very ingenious in inventing them.", "매기는 자기에게 가해지는 무시에 무척 민감했고, 그런 무시를 지어내는 데에도 무척 재간이 있었다."], ["‘Putting both your hands afore your face too!’ she went on.", "\"게다가 두 손으로 얼굴까지 가리다니요!\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["‘If you can’t bear the looks of a poor thing, it would be better to tell her so at once, and not go and shut her out like that, hurting her feelings and breaking her heart at ten year old, poor thing!’", "\"불쌍한 사람 꼴이 보기 싫으시면 차라리 단번에 그렇다고 말씀하시는 게 낫지, 그렇게 막아 버리고 열 살배기 가엾은 것 마음을 다치게 하고 가슴을 찢어 놓다니요, 불쌍한 것!\""]], [["‘It’s to ease my head, Maggy.’", "\"머리를 식히려는 거야, 매기.\""]], [["‘Well, and if you cry to ease your head, Little Mother, let me cry too. Don’t go and have all the crying to yourself,’ expostulated Maggy, ‘that an’t not being greedy.’ And immediately began to blubber.", "\"그럼, 머리 식히려고 우시는 거면, 작은엄마, 저도 같이 울게요. 혼자서 우는 걸 다 차지하지 마세요.\" 매기가 따지듯 말했다. \"그건 욕심부리지 않는 게 아니잖아요.\" 그러고는 곧장 엉엉 울기 시작했다."]], [["It was with some difficulty that she could be induced to go back with the excuse; but the promise of being told a story--of old her great delight--on condition that she concentrated her faculties upon the errand and left her little mistress to herself for an hour longer, combined with a misgiving on Maggy’s part that she had left her good temper at the bottom of the staircase, prevailed.", "그녀를 그 핑계와 함께 돌려보내도록 구슬리기는 여간 힘든 일이 아니었다. 그러나 옛적부터 더없이 좋아하던 이야기를 들려주겠다는 약속이—심부름에 정신을 모으고 한 시간 더 작은 여주인을 혼자 두는 조건으로—계단 밑에 제 좋은 기분을 두고 온 게 아닐까 하는 매기 자신의 불안과 어우러져 효력을 발휘했다."], ["So away she went, muttering her message all the way to keep it in her mind, and, at the appointed time, came back.", "그리하여 그녀는 잊지 않으려고 가는 내내 전갈을 중얼거리며 떠났고, 정해진 시간에 돌아왔다."]], [["‘He was very sorry, I can tell you,’ she announced, ‘and wanted to send a doctor.", "\"그 사람 무척 안타까워했어요, 정말로요.\" 그녀가 알렸다."], ["And he’s coming again to-morrow he is and I don’t think he’ll have a good sleep to-night along o’ hearing about your head, Little Mother.", "\"의사를 보내려고 했어요. 그리고 내일 또 온대요, 정말로요, 그리고 작은엄마 머리가 아프다는 소릴 들어서 오늘 밤 잠을 푹 못 잘 것 같아요, 작은엄마."], ["Oh my!", "아이고 참!"], ["Ain’t you been a-crying!’", "우셨던 거 아니에요!\""]], [["‘I think I have, a little, Maggy.’", "\"운 것 같긴 해, 조금, 매기.\""]], [["‘A little!", "\"조금이라뇨!"], ["Oh!’", "어머!\""]], [["‘But it’s all over now--all over for good, Maggy.", "\"하지만 이제 다 끝났어—아주 끝났단다, 매기."], ["And my head is much better and cooler, and I am quite comfortable.", "머리도 훨씬 나아지고 시원해져서 아주 편안해."], ["I am very glad I did not go down.’", "안 내려가길 정말 잘했어.\""]], [["Her great staring child tenderly embraced her; and having smoothed her hair, and bathed her forehead and eyes with cold water (offices in which her awkward hands became skilful), hugged her again, exulted in her brighter looks, and stationed her in her chair by the window.", "멍하니 쳐다보던 그녀의 큰 아이가 다정하게 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 머리를 매만져 주고, 이마와 눈을 찬물로 씻겨 준 뒤에(서투른 손이 이런 일에는 능숙해졌다), 다시 한번 꼭 껴안고, 한결 밝아진 그녀의 얼굴빛에 기뻐하며, 창가의 의자에 그녀를 앉혔다."], ["Over against this chair, Maggy, with apoplectic exertions that were not at all required, dragged the box which was her seat on story-telling occasions, sat down upon it, hugged her own knees, and said, with a voracious appetite for stories, and with widely-opened eyes:", "그 의자 맞은편으로 매기는 전혀 필요도 없는 졸도할 듯한 안간힘을 써서, 이야기를 들을 때 자기 자리가 되어 주는 상자를 끌어다가 그 위에 앉았다. 그러고는 제 무릎을 끌어안고, 이야기에 게걸스러운 식욕을 보이며, 눈을 크게 뜨고 말했다."]], [["‘Now, Little Mother, let’s have a good ‘un!’", "\"자, 작은엄마, 멋진 걸로 하나 해 줘요!\""]], [["‘What shall it be about, Maggy?’", "\"무슨 이야기로 할까, 매기?\""]], [["‘Oh, let’s have a princess,’ said Maggy, ‘and let her be a reg’lar one.", "\"오, 공주님 이야기로 해요.\" 매기가 말했다."], ["Beyond all belief, you know!’", "\"그리고 제대로 된 공주님으로요. 믿기지 않을 만큼요, 알죠!\""]], [["Little Dorrit considered for a moment; and with a rather sad smile upon her face, which was flushed by the sunset, began:", "리틀 도릿은 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가, 노을빛에 발그레해진 얼굴에 다소 슬픈 미소를 띤 채 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["‘Maggy, there was once upon a time a fine King, and he had everything he could wish for, and a great deal more.", "\"매기, 옛날 옛적에 훌륭한 임금님이 있었는데, 그분은 바라는 것을 다 가졌고, 그보다 훨씬 더 많이 가졌단다."], ["He had gold and silver, diamonds and rubies, riches of every kind.", "금과 은, 다이아몬드와 루비, 온갖 보화를 가졌지."], ["He had palaces, and he had--’", "궁전도 여럿 있었고, 또—\""]], [["‘Hospitals,’ interposed Maggy, still nursing her knees.", "\"병원이요.\" 매기가 여전히 제 무릎을 안은 채 끼어들었다."], ["‘Let him have hospitals, because they’re so comfortable.", "\"병원도 갖게 해 줘요, 병원은 참 편안하니까요."], ["Hospitals with lots of Chicking.’", "닭고기가 잔뜩 있는 병원이요.\""]], [["‘Yes, he had plenty of them, and he had plenty of everything.’", "\"그래, 병원도 많이 가졌어, 그리고 뭐든 다 많이 가졌지.\""]], [["‘Plenty of baked potatoes, for instance?’ said Maggy.", "\"이를테면 구운 감자도 잔뜩요?\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["‘Plenty of everything.’", "\"뭐든 다 잔뜩.\""]], [["‘Lor!’ chuckled Maggy, giving her knees a hug.", "\"어머나!\" 매기가 제 무릎을 꼭 끌어안으며 킬킬댔다."], ["‘Wasn’t it prime!’", "\"정말 끝내주네요!\""]], [["‘This King had a daughter, who was the wisest and most beautiful Princess that ever was seen.", "\"이 임금님께는 따님이 한 분 있었는데, 여태 본 적 없는 가장 지혜롭고 가장 아름다운 공주님이었어."], ["When she was a child she understood all her lessons before her masters taught them to her; and when she was grown up, she was the wonder of the world.", "어릴 적엔 스승들이 가르치기도 전에 모든 가르침을 다 깨쳤고, 다 자라서는 온 세상의 경이로움이 되었단다."], ["Now, near the Palace where this Princess lived, there was a cottage in which there was a poor little tiny woman, who lived all alone by herself.’", "그런데 이 공주님이 사는 궁전 가까이에 오두막이 하나 있었는데, 거기엔 가난하고 자그마한 여인이 홀로 외따로 살고 있었어.\""]], [["‘An old woman,’ said Maggy, with an unctuous smack of her lips.", "\"늙은 여인이요.\" 매기가 입맛을 쩝쩝 다시며 말했다."]], [["‘No, not an old woman.", "\"아니, 늙은 여인이 아니야."], ["Quite a young one.’", "아주 젊은 여인이었어.\""]], [["‘I wonder she warn’t afraid,’ said Maggy.", "\"안 무서웠나 봐요.\" 매기가 말했다."], ["‘Go on, please.’", "\"계속해 줘요, 제발.\""]], [["‘The Princess passed the cottage nearly every day, and whenever she went by in her beautiful carriage, she saw the poor tiny woman spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the tiny woman, and the tiny woman looked at her.", "\"공주님은 거의 날마다 그 오두막 앞을 지나갔는데, 아름다운 마차를 타고 지나갈 때마다 그 가난하고 자그마한 여인이 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣는 것을 보았어. 그리고 공주님은 그 자그마한 여인을 바라보았고, 자그마한 여인은 공주님을 바라보았지."], ["So, one day she stopped the coachman a little way from the cottage, and got out and walked on and peeped in at the door, and there, as usual, was the tiny woman spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the Princess, and the Princess looked at her.’", "그래서 어느 날 공주님은 오두막에서 조금 떨어진 데서 마부를 멈추게 하고, 마차에서 내려 걸어가 문 안을 들여다보았는데, 거기엔 여느 때처럼 그 자그마한 여인이 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 있었어. 그리고 여인은 공주님을 바라보았고, 공주님은 여인을 바라보았지.\""]], [["‘Like trying to stare one another out,’ said Maggy.", "\"서로 눈싸움이라도 하듯이요.\" 매기가 말했다."], ["‘Please go on, Little Mother.’", "\"계속해 줘요, 작은엄마.\""]], [["‘The Princess was such a wonderful Princess that she had the power of knowing secrets, and she said to the tiny woman, Why do you keep it there?", "\"공주님은 어찌나 놀라운 공주님이었던지 비밀을 알아내는 힘이 있었어. 그래서 자그마한 여인에게 말했단다, 왜 그것을 거기에 간직하고 있나요?"], ["This showed her directly that the Princess knew why she lived all alone by herself spinning at her wheel, and she kneeled down at the Princess’s feet, and asked her never to betray her.", "이 말에 여인은 공주님이 자기가 왜 홀로 외따로 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 사는지 안다는 걸 단번에 알아차렸고, 공주님의 발치에 무릎을 꿇고서 자기를 결코 발설하지 말아 달라고 빌었어."], ["So the Princess said, I never will betray you. Let me see it.", "그러자 공주님이 말했지, 결코 발설하지 않겠어요."], ["So the tiny woman closed the shutter of the cottage window and fastened the door, and trembling from head to foot for fear that any one should suspect her, opened a very secret place and showed the Princess a shadow.’", "그걸 내게 보여 주세요. 그래서 자그마한 여인은 오두막 창의 덧문을 닫고 문을 걸어 잠근 뒤, 누가 자기를 의심할까 봐 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨면서, 아주 은밀한 곳을 열어 공주님에게 그림자 하나를 보여 주었어.\""]], [["‘Lor!’ said Maggy.", "\"어머나!\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["‘It was the shadow of Some one who had gone by long before: of Some one who had gone on far away quite out of reach, never, never to come back.", "\"그것은 오래전에 지나가 버린 어떤 사람의 그림자였어. 손이 닿지 않을 만큼 멀리 떠나가 버려, 다시는, 결코 다시는 돌아오지 않을 어떤 사람의 그림자였지."], ["It was bright to look at; and when the tiny woman showed it to the Princess, she was proud of it with all her heart, as a great, great treasure.", "보기에 환했고, 자그마한 여인이 그것을 공주님에게 보여 줄 때 그녀는 그것을 크나큰, 크나큰 보물처럼 온 마음으로 자랑스러워했어."], ["When the Princess had considered it a little while, she said to the tiny woman, And you keep watch over this every day?", "공주님은 그것을 잠시 바라보고 나서 자그마한 여인에게 말했지, 그래서 당신은 날마다 이것을 지키고 있나요?"], ["And she cast down her eyes, and whispered, Yes.", "여인은 눈을 내리깔고 속삭였어, 네."], ["Then the Princess said, Remind me why.", "그러자 공주님이 말했지, 왜 그러는지 다시 말해 주세요."], ["To which the other replied, that no one so good and kind had ever passed that way, and that was why in the beginning.", "이에 여인이 대답하기를, 그토록 선하고 다정한 이가 그 길로 지나간 적이 없었으니, 그것이 처음의 까닭이었다고 했어."], ["She said, too, that nobody missed it, that nobody was the worse for it, that Some one had gone on, to those who were expecting him--’", "또 말하기를, 아무도 그것을 아쉬워하지 않고, 아무도 그것 때문에 더 나빠지지 않으며, 그 어떤 사람은 자기를 기다리던 이들에게로 떠나갔다고—\""]], [["‘Some one was a man then?’ interposed Maggy.", "\"그럼 그 어떤 사람은 남자였군요?\" 매기가 끼어들었다."]], [["Little Dorrit timidly said Yes, she believed so; and resumed:", "리틀 도릿은 머뭇머뭇 그래, 그런 것 같다고 말하고는 다시 이었다."]], [["‘--Had gone on to those who were expecting him, and that this remembrance was stolen or kept back from nobody.", "\"—자기를 기다리던 이들에게로 떠나갔으며, 이 기억은 그 누구에게서 훔치거나 빼앗은 것이 아니라고 했어."], ["The Princess made answer, Ah!", "공주님이 대답했지, 아!"], ["But when the cottager died it would be discovered there.", "하지만 당신이 죽으면 거기서 그것이 발견될 거예요."], ["The tiny woman told her No; when that time came, it would sink quietly into her own grave, and would never be found.’", "자그마한 여인은 아니라고 했어. 그때가 오면 그것은 조용히 자기 무덤 속으로 가라앉을 것이고, 결코 발견되지 않을 거라고 했지.\""]], [["‘Well, to be sure!’ said Maggy.", "\"이런, 세상에!\" 매기가 말했다."], ["‘Go on, please.’", "\"계속해 줘요, 제발.\""]], [["‘The Princess was very much astonished to hear this, as you may suppose, Maggy.’", "\"공주님은 이 말을 듣고 무척 놀랐단다, 짐작이 가지, 매기.\""]], [["[‘And well she might be,’ said Maggy.)", "(\"그야 놀랄 만하죠.\" 매기가 말했다.)"]], [["‘So she resolved to watch the tiny woman, and see what came of it.", "\"그래서 공주님은 그 자그마한 여인을 지켜보며 어찌 되는지 보기로 마음먹었어."], ["Every day she drove in her beautiful carriage by the cottage-door, and there she saw the tiny woman always alone by herself spinning at her wheel, and she looked at the tiny woman, and the tiny woman looked at her.", "날마다 아름다운 마차를 타고 오두막 문 앞을 지나갔는데, 거기엔 늘 그 자그마한 여인이 홀로 외따로 물레를 돌리며 실을 잣고 있었어. 그리고 공주님은 여인을 바라보았고, 여인은 공주님을 바라보았지."], ["At last one day the wheel was still, and the tiny woman was not to be seen.", "마침내 어느 날 물레가 멈춰 있었고, 자그마한 여인은 보이지 않았어."], ["When the Princess made inquiries why the wheel had stopped, and where the tiny woman was, she was informed that the wheel had stopped because there was nobody to turn it, the tiny woman being dead.’", "공주님이 왜 물레가 멈췄는지, 자그마한 여인은 어디 있는지 물었을 때, 물레가 멈춘 건 그것을 돌릴 사람이 아무도 없어서이고, 자그마한 여인은 죽었다는 대답을 들었단다.\""]], [["[‘They ought to have took her to the Hospital,’ said Maggy, and then she’d have got over it.’)", "(\"그 여자를 병원으로 데려갔어야 하는데요.\" 매기가 말했다. \"그랬으면 나았을 텐데요.\")"]], [["‘The Princess, after crying a very little for the loss of the tiny woman, dried her eyes and got out of her carriage at the place where she had stopped it before, and went to the cottage and peeped in at the door.", "\"공주님은 자그마한 여인을 잃은 슬픔에 아주 조금 운 뒤, 눈물을 닦고 전에 멈추었던 그 자리에서 마차에서 내려, 오두막으로 가 문 안을 들여다보았어. 이제 그녀를 바라볼 이도 없었고, 그녀가 바라볼 이도 없었지."], ["There was nobody to look at her now, and nobody for her to look at, so she went in at once to search for the treasured shadow.", "그래서 그녀는 곧장 안으로 들어가 그 소중히 간직된 그림자를 찾았어. 그러나 그것은 어디서도 흔적조차 찾을 수 없었지."], ["But there was no sign of it to be found anywhere; and then she knew that the tiny woman had told her the truth, and that it would never give anybody any trouble, and that it had sunk quietly into her own grave, and that she and it were at rest together.", "그제야 공주님은 자그마한 여인이 진실을 말했음을, 그것이 결코 누구에게도 폐를 끼치지 않으리라는 것을, 그것이 조용히 그녀 자신의 무덤 속으로 가라앉았음을, 그리고 그녀와 그것이 함께 안식에 들었음을 알았단다."]], [["‘That’s all, Maggy.’", "이게 다야, 매기.\""]], [["The sunset flush was so bright on Little Dorrit’s face when she came thus to the end of her story, that she interposed her hand to shade it.", "리틀 도릿이 이렇게 이야기의 끝에 이르렀을 때 그녀의 얼굴에 노을빛이 어찌나 환하게 비치던지, 그녀는 손을 들어 그것을 가렸다."]], [["‘Had she got to be old?’ Maggy asked.", "\"그 여자는 늙도록 살았어요?\" 매기가 물었다."]], [["‘The tiny woman?’", "\"그 자그마한 여인 말이니?\""]], [["‘Ah!’", "\"네!\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"모르겠어.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘But it would have been just the same if she had been ever so old.’", "\"하지만 아무리 늙도록 살았다 해도 마찬가지였을 거야.\""]], [["‘Would it raly!’ said Maggy.", "\"정말요!\" 매기가 말했다."], ["‘Well, I suppose it would though.’ And sat staring and ruminating.", "\"음, 하긴 그랬겠죠.\" 그러고는 멍하니 쳐다보며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["She sat so long with her eyes wide open, that at length Little Dorrit, to entice her from her box, rose and looked out of window.", "그녀가 어찌나 오래도록 눈을 크게 뜨고 앉아 있던지, 마침내 리틀 도릿은 그녀를 상자에서 일으키려고 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["As she glanced down into the yard, she saw Pancks come in and leer up with the corner of his eye as he went by.", "마당 아래를 흘끗 내려다보았을 때, 그녀는 팽크스가 들어와 지나가면서 곁눈질로 흘끗 올려다보는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Who’s he, Little Mother?’ said Maggy.", "\"저 사람 누구예요, 작은엄마?\" 매기가 말했다."], ["She had joined her at the window and was leaning on her shoulder.", "그녀는 창가에서 그녀와 나란히 서서 그녀의 어깨에 기대고 있었다."], ["‘I see him come in and out often.’", "\"저 사람 들락날락하는 거 자주 봤어요.\""]], [["‘I have heard him called a fortune-teller,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"점쟁이라고 부르는 걸 들었어.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘But I doubt if he could tell many people even their past or present fortunes.’", "\"하지만 저 사람이 사람들의 과거나 현재 운명조차 많이 말해 줄 수 있을지 의문이야.\""]], [["‘Couldn’t have told the Princess hers?’ said Maggy.", "\"공주님 운명도 못 말해 줬을까요?\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["Little Dorrit, looking musingly down into the dark valley of the prison, shook her head.", "리틀 도릿은 어두운 감옥의 골짜기를 골똘히 내려다보며 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘Nor the tiny woman hers?’ said Maggy.", "\"그 자그마한 여인 운명도요?\" 매기가 말했다."]], [["‘No,’ said Little Dorrit, with the sunset very bright upon her.", "\"못 했을 거야.\" 리틀 도릿이 노을빛을 환하게 받으며 말했다."], ["‘But let us come away from the window.’", "\"하지만 이제 창가에서 그만 물러나자.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 25. Conspirators and Others", "# CHAPTER 25. Conspirators and Others"]], [["The private residence of Mr Pancks was in Pentonville, where he lodged on the second-floor of a professional gentleman in an extremely small way, who had an inner-door within the street door, poised on a spring and starting open with a click like a trap; and who wrote up in the fan-light, RUGG, GENERAL AGENT, ACCOUNTANT, DEBTS RECOVERED.", "팽크스 씨의 사저는 펜턴빌에 있었는데, 그는 그곳에서 지극히 영세한 사업을 하는 어느 전문직 신사의 삼층에 세 들어 살았다. 그 신사의 집은 거리로 난 문 안쪽에 또 하나의 문이 있어, 용수철 위에 얹혀 덫처럼 찰칵 소리를 내며 튀어 열렸고, 그는 채광창에 이렇게 써 붙여 두었다. 러그, 종합 대리인, 회계사, 채권 추심."]], [["This scroll, majestic in its severe simplicity, illuminated a little slip of front garden abutting on the thirsty high-road, where a few of the dustiest of leaves hung their dismal heads and led a life of choking.", "엄정한 단순함 속에 위엄을 갖춘 이 명문은, 목마른 큰길에 잇닿은 작은 앞뜰 한 뙈기를 환히 밝혀 주었다. 그곳에서는 가장 먼지투성이인 잎사귀 몇 장이 음울한 고개를 떨군 채 숨 막히는 삶을 이어 가고 있었다."], ["A professor of writing occupied the first-floor, and enlivened the garden railings with glass-cases containing choice examples of what his pupils had been before six lessons and while the whole of his young family shook the table, and what they had become after six lessons when the young family was under restraint.", "이층에는 글씨 선생이 살았는데, 그는 정원 난간을 유리 진열장으로 활기차게 꾸며 놓았다. 그 진열장에는 그의 제자들이 여섯 번 수업을 받기 전에—온 가족이 식탁을 흔들어 대는 와중에—어떠했는지, 그리고 여섯 번 수업을 받은 뒤에—그 어린 가족이 자제되었을 때—어떻게 달라졌는지를 보여 주는 빼어난 본보기들이 담겨 있었다."], ["The tenancy of Mr Pancks was limited to one airy bedroom; he covenanting and agreeing with Mr Rugg his landlord, that in consideration of a certain scale of payments accurately defined, and on certain verbal notice duly given, he should be at liberty to elect to share the Sunday breakfast, dinner, tea, or supper, or each or any or all of those repasts or meals of Mr and Miss Rugg (his daughter) in the back-parlour.", "팽크스 씨의 임차 범위는 통풍 잘되는 침실 하나에 한정되어 있었다. 그는 집주인 러그 씨와 더불어, 정확히 규정된 일정 요금표에 따라, 또 일정한 구두 통지를 정식으로 했을 경우, 뒷방에서 러그 씨와 그의 딸 러그 양의 일요일 아침, 점심, 차, 또는 저녁 식사 가운데 어느 하나, 또는 여럿, 또는 전부를 함께할지 선택할 자유를 가진다고 약정하고 합의했다."]], [["Miss Rugg was a lady of a little property which she had acquired, together with much distinction in the neighbourhood, by having her heart severely lacerated and her feelings mangled by a middle-aged baker resident in the vicinity, against whom she had, by the agency of Mr Rugg, found it necessary to proceed at law to recover damages for a breach of promise of marriage.", "러그 양은 약간의 재산을 가진 숙녀였는데, 그 재산과 더불어 이웃에서 상당한 명성을 얻게 된 사연인즉, 그 동네에 사는 어느 중년 제빵사에게 마음을 심하게 찢기고 감정을 짓이겨져, 러그 씨의 주선으로 혼약 파기에 대한 손해배상을 받아내기 위해 법적 절차를 밟지 않을 수 없었던 데 있었다."], ["The baker having been, by the counsel for Miss Rugg, witheringly denounced on that occasion up to the full amount of twenty guineas, at the rate of about eighteen-pence an epithet, and having been cast in corresponding damages, still suffered occasional persecution from the youth of Pentonville.", "그 제빵사는 그 자리에서 러그 양 측 변호인에게, 욕설 한 마디당 약 십팔 펜스 꼴로 도합 이십 기니에 이르도록 신랄하게 규탄당했고, 그에 상응하는 손해배상 판결을 받았으나, 여전히 펜턴빌의 젊은이들로부터 이따금 박해를 받았다."], ["But Miss Rugg, environed by the majesty of the law, and having her damages invested in the public securities, was regarded with consideration.", "그러나 러그 양은 법의 위엄에 둘러싸인 데다 손해배상금을 공채에 투자해 둔 터라, 사람들의 존중을 받았다."]], [["In the society of Mr Rugg, who had a round white visage, as if all his blushes had been drawn out of him long ago, and who had a ragged yellow head like a worn-out hearth broom; and in the society of Miss Rugg, who had little nankeen spots, like shirt buttons, all over her face, and whose own yellow tresses were rather scrubby than luxuriant; Mr Pancks had usually dined on Sundays for some few years, and had twice a week, or so, enjoyed an evening collation of bread, Dutch cheese, and porter. Mr Pancks was one of the very few marriageable men for whom Miss Rugg had no terrors, the argument with which he reassured himself being twofold; that is to say, firstly, ‘that it wouldn’t do twice,’ and secondly, ‘that he wasn’t worth it.’ Fortified within this double armour, Mr Pancks snorted at Miss Rugg on easy terms.", "러그 씨로 말하자면, 마치 오래전에 그의 혈색이 죄다 뽑혀 나가기라도 한 듯 둥글고 허연 낯에, 닳아빠진 벽난로용 빗자루 같은 누렇고 텁수룩한 머리를 한 사람이었다. 또 러그 양으로 말하자면, 얼굴 곳곳에 셔츠 단추 같은 작은 황갈색 반점이 돋아 있고, 제 누런 머릿단은 풍성하다기보다 차라리 듬성듬성한 여인이었다. 팽크스 씨는 몇 해 동안 일요일이면 으레 이 두 사람과 어울려 식사를 했고, 일주일에 두 번쯤은 빵과 네덜란드 치즈, 그리고 흑맥주로 차린 저녁 간식을 즐겼다. 팽크스 씨는 러그 양에게 아무런 두려움을 주지 않는 극소수의 혼인 적격 남자 가운데 하나였는데, 그가 스스로를 안심시키는 논거는 두 갈래였다. 즉 첫째로 \"두 번씩이나 통할 리는 없다\"는 것이었고, 둘째로 \"자기는 그럴 만한 가치가 없다\"는 것이었다. 이 이중의 갑옷으로 무장한 채, 팽크스 씨는 거리낌 없이 러그 양을 향해 콧방귀를 뀌었다."]], [["Up to this time, Mr Pancks had transacted little or no business at his quarters in Pentonville, except in the sleeping line; but now that he had become a fortune-teller, he was often closeted after midnight with Mr Rugg in his little front-parlour office, and even after those untimely hours, burnt tallow in his bed-room.", "이때까지 팽크스 씨는 펜턴빌의 거처에서 잠자는 것 말고는 이렇다 할 일을 거의 또는 전혀 처리하지 않았다. 그러나 이제 점쟁이 노릇을 하게 된 지금, 그는 자정이 넘어서까지 작은 앞방 사무실에서 러그 씨와 곧잘 밀담을 나누었고, 그런 야심한 시각이 지난 뒤에도 침실에서 수지 양초를 태우곤 했다."], ["Though his duties as his proprietor’s grubber were in no wise lessened; and though that service bore no greater resemblance to a bed of roses than was to be discovered in its many thorns; some new branch of industry made a constant demand upon him.", "집주인의 빚 캐는 일꾼으로서 그의 임무가 조금도 가벼워지지 않았음에도, 또 그 일이 숱한 가시밭이 보여 주는 것 외에는 장미 침대와 닮은 구석이라곤 없었음에도, 어떤 새로운 분야의 일거리가 그에게 끊임없이 손길을 요구했다."], ["When he cast off the Patriarch at night, it was only to take an anonymous craft in tow, and labour away afresh in other waters.", "밤에 가부장을 떨궈 놓는 것은 곧 어느 이름 없는 배를 끌고 다른 물길에서 새로이 노 저어 일하기 위함일 뿐이었다."]], [["The advance from a personal acquaintance with the elder Mr Chivery to an introduction to his amiable wife and disconsolate son, may have been easy; but easy or not, Mr Pancks soon made it.", "부친 치버리 씨와의 개인적 친분에서 그의 상냥한 아내와 시름에 잠긴 아들에게 소개받는 데까지 나아가는 일은 쉬웠을 수도 있다. 쉬웠든 아니든, 팽크스 씨는 그 일을 곧 이루어 냈다."], ["He nestled in the bosom of the tobacco business within a week or two after his first appearance in the College, and particularly addressed himself to the cultivation of a good understanding with Young John.", "그는 그 교도소에 처음 모습을 보인 지 한두 주 만에 담배 가게의 품속에 자리 잡았고, 특히 청년 존과 좋은 사이를 다지는 데 마음을 쏟았다."], ["In this endeavour he so prospered as to lure that pining shepherd forth from the groves, and tempt him to undertake mysterious missions; on which he began to disappear at uncertain intervals for as long a space as two or three days together.", "이 노력에서 그는 크게 성공을 거두어, 그 시들어 가던 목동을 숲에서 꾀어내고, 신비로운 임무를 떠맡도록 부추겼다. 그리하여 청년 존은 일정치 않은 간격으로 길게는 이삼 일씩 잇따라 자취를 감추기 시작했다."], ["The prudent Mrs Chivery, who wondered greatly at this change, would have protested against it as detrimental to the Highland typification on the doorpost but for two forcible reasons; one, that her John was roused to take strong interest in the business which these starts were supposed to advance--and this she held to be good for his drooping spirits; the other, that Mr Pancks confidentially agreed to pay her, for the occupation of her son’s time, at the handsome rate of seven and sixpence per day.", "신중한 치버리 부인은 이 변화를 몹시 의아해하여, 문기둥에 그려진 하일랜드 사람 그림에 해로운 일이라며 항의했을 법도 했으나, 두 가지 강력한 이유 때문에 그러지 않았다. 하나는 그녀의 존이 이런 외출이 진척시키리라고 여겨지는 일에 강한 흥미를 갖게 되어 분발했다는 것이었는데—이 점은 그녀가 풀 죽은 그의 기운에 이롭다고 여겼다—다른 하나는 팽크스 씨가 그녀의 아들이 쓰는 시간에 대한 대가로 하루에 칠 실링 육 펜스라는 후한 금액을 지불하기로 은밀히 합의했다는 것이었다."], ["The proposal originated with himself, and was couched in the pithy terms, ‘If your John is weak enough, ma’am, not to take it, that is no reason why you should be, don’t you see?", "그 제안은 그 자신에게서 비롯된 것으로, 다음과 같이 간명한 말로 표현되었다. \"댁의 존이 그걸 받지 않을 만큼 어수룩하다 해도, 그게 부인까지 그래야 할 이유는 안 되지요, 안 그렇습니까?"], ["So, quite between ourselves, ma’am, business being business, here it is!’", "그러니, 우리끼리만의 얘깁니다만, 부인, 일은 일이니까요, 자 여기 있습니다!\""]], [["What Mr Chivery thought of these things, or how much or how little he knew about them, was never gathered from himself.", "치버리 씨가 이런 일들을 어떻게 생각했는지, 또는 그에 대해 얼마나 알았는지 혹은 몰랐는지는 그 자신에게서 도무지 알아낼 수가 없었다."], ["It has been already remarked that he was a man of few words; and it may be here observed that he had imbibed a professional habit of locking everything up.", "그가 말수가 적은 사람이라는 점은 이미 언급한 바 있다. 그리고 여기서 한 가지 덧붙이자면, 그는 모든 것을 잠가 두는 직업적 습관에 젖어 있었다."], ["He locked himself up as carefully as he locked up the Marshalsea debtors.", "그는 마셜시의 채무자들을 가두듯이 자기 자신도 꼼꼼히 잠가 두었다."], ["Even his custom of bolting his meals may have been a part of an uniform whole; but there is no question, that, as to all other purposes, he kept his mouth as he kept the Marshalsea door.", "식사를 후딱 해치우는 그의 버릇조차 한결같은 전체의 일부였는지도 모른다. 그러나 다른 모든 면에서 그가 마셜시 문을 다루듯 제 입을 다루었다는 데에는 의문의 여지가 없다."], ["He never opened it without occasion.", "그는 까닭 없이는 결코 그것을 열지 않았다."], ["When it was necessary to let anything out, he opened it a little way, held it open just as long as sufficed for the purpose, and locked it again.", "무언가를 내보낼 필요가 있을 때면, 그는 입을 조금 열어 꼭 그 목적에 충분할 만큼만 벌린 채 두었다가 다시 잠갔다."], ["Even as he would be sparing of his trouble at the Marshalsea door, and would keep a visitor who wanted to go out, waiting for a few moments if he saw another visitor coming down the yard, so that one turn of the key should suffice for both, similarly he would often reserve a remark if he perceived another on its way to his lips, and would deliver himself of the two together.", "마치 마셜시 문에서 수고를 아끼느라, 밖으로 나가려는 방문객이 있어도 또 다른 방문객이 마당을 가로질러 오는 것을 보면 열쇠 한 번 돌리는 것으로 둘 다 해결되도록 그를 잠시 기다리게 했듯이, 마찬가지로 그는 또 다른 말이 입가로 올라오는 것을 알아채면 곧잘 한마디를 미뤄 두었다가 둘을 한꺼번에 내놓곤 했다."], ["As to any key to his inner knowledge being to be found in his face, the Marshalsea key was as legible as an index to the individual characters and histories upon which it was turned.", "그의 내심을 들여다볼 열쇠를 그의 얼굴에서 찾는 일로 말하자면, 그 마셜시 열쇠가 돌려지는 대상인 각 인물의 성격과 내력을 보여 주는 색인만큼이나 그의 얼굴은 읽기 어려웠다."]], [["That Mr Pancks should be moved to invite any one to dinner at Pentonville, was an unprecedented fact in his calendar.", "팽크스 씨가 누군가를 펜턴빌의 만찬에 초대하도록 마음이 움직였다는 것은 그의 달력에서 전례 없는 사건이었다."], ["But he invited Young John to dinner, and even brought him within range of the dangerous (because expensive) fascinations of Miss Rugg.", "그러나 그는 청년 존을 만찬에 초대했고, 심지어 그를 (값비싸므로) 위험한 러그 양의 매력의 사정거리 안에까지 끌어들였다."], ["The banquet was appointed for a Sunday, and Miss Rugg with her own hands stuffed a leg of mutton with oysters on the occasion, and sent it to the baker’s--not _the_ baker’s but an opposition establishment.", "잔치는 일요일로 정해졌고, 러그 양은 그날을 위해 손수 양다리 고기에 굴을 채워 넣어 제빵사에게 보냈다—단, 바로 그 제빵사가 아니라 경쟁 상대인 가게로 말이다."], ["Provision of oranges, apples, and nuts was also made.", "오렌지와 사과, 견과류도 마련되었다."], ["And rum was brought home by Mr Pancks on Saturday night, to gladden the visitor’s heart.", "그리고 손님의 마음을 흥겹게 해 줄 럼주는 토요일 밤에 팽크스 씨가 직접 사 들고 왔다."]], [["The store of creature comforts was not the chief part of the visitor’s reception.", "먹고 마실 것의 비축이 손님 접대의 주된 부분은 아니었다."], ["Its special feature was a foregone family confidence and sympathy.", "그 특별한 묘미는 미리부터 마련된 식구다운 신뢰와 공감에 있었다."], ["When Young John appeared at half-past one without the ivory hand and waistcoat of golden sprigs, the sun shorn of his beams by disastrous clouds, Mr Pancks presented him to the yellow-haired Ruggs as the young man he had so often mentioned who loved Miss Dorrit.", "청년 존이 한 시 반에, 상아빛 손잡이 지팡이도 황금빛 잔가지 무늬 조끼도 없이, 마치 재앙 같은 구름에 햇살을 잘린 태양처럼 나타나자, 팽크스 씨는 그를 누런 머리의 러그 부녀에게, 자기가 그토록 자주 입에 올렸던, 도릿 양을 사랑하는 그 청년이라며 소개했다."]], [["‘I am glad,’ said Mr Rugg, challenging him specially in that character, ‘to have the distinguished gratification of making your acquaintance, sir.", "\"선생,\" 러그 씨가 특별히 그 자격으로 그를 맞으며 말했다."], ["Your feelings do you honour.", "\"이렇게 알게 되어 더없는 기쁨이외다. 그대의 그 감정은 그대를 영예롭게 하는 것이지요."], ["You are young; may you never outlive your feelings!", "그대는 젊으니, 부디 그 감정보다 오래 살아남지 않기를! 만약 내가 내 감정보다 오래 살아남게 된다면 말이오, 선생,\""], ["If I was to outlive my own feelings, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, who was a man of many words, and was considered to possess a remarkably good address; ‘if I was to outlive my own feelings, I’d leave fifty pound in my will to the man who would put me out of existence.’", "말수가 많고 화술이 유난히 좋다고 여겨지던 러그 씨가 말했다. \"만약 내가 내 감정보다 오래 살아남게 된다면, 나를 이 세상에서 없애 줄 사람에게 유언으로 오십 파운드를 남기겠소.\""]], [["Miss Rugg heaved a sigh.", "러그 양은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["‘My daughter, sir,’ said Mr Rugg.", "\"내 딸이오, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Anastatia, you are no stranger to the state of this young man’s affections.", "\"애너스테이샤, 너도 이 청년의 애정의 처지에 낯설지 않을 게다."], ["My daughter has had her trials, sir’--Mr Rugg might have used the word more pointedly in the singular number--‘and she can feel for you.’", "내 딸도 시련을 겪었지요, 선생\"—러그 씨로서는 그 말을 더 정확히 단수로 썼어야 했을 테지만—\"그러니 그대의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있을 거외다.\""]], [["Young John, almost overwhelmed by the touching nature of this greeting, professed himself to that effect.", "청년 존은 이 인사의 가슴 뭉클한 정겨움에 거의 압도당하여, 그런 취지로 자기 마음을 밝혔다."]], [["‘What I envy you, sir, is,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘allow me to take your hat--we are rather short of pegs--I’ll put it in the corner, nobody will tread on it there--What I envy you, sir, is the luxury of your own feelings.", "\"내가 그대에게 부러운 점은 말이오, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다."], ["I belong to a profession in which that luxury is sometimes denied us.’", "\"모자를 받아 드리지요—우리 집은 걸이가 좀 부족해서—구석에 둘 테니, 거기선 아무도 밟지 않을 거요—내가 그대에게 부러운 점은, 선생, 자기 감정을 누리는 그 호사외다. 나는 그런 호사가 때로 금지되는 직업에 몸담고 있지요.\""]], [["Young John replied, with acknowledgments, that he only hoped he did what was right, and what showed how entirely he was devoted to Miss Dorrit.", "청년 존은 감사의 뜻과 함께 대답하기를, 자기는 그저 옳은 일을, 그리고 자기가 도릿 양에게 얼마나 온전히 헌신하고 있는지를 보여 주는 일을 하기를 바랄 뿐이라고 했다."], ["He wished to be unselfish; and he hoped he was.", "자기는 사심 없기를 바라며, 또 그러기를 소망한다고 했다."], ["He wished to do anything as laid in his power to serve Miss Dorrit, altogether putting himself out of sight; and he hoped he did.", "자기를 완전히 뒷전으로 물린 채, 도릿 양에게 도움이 될 일이라면 무엇이든 제 힘닿는 데까지 하기를 바라며, 또 그러고 있기를 소망한다고 했다."], ["It was but little that he could do, but he hoped he did it.", "자기가 할 수 있는 일이란 보잘것없지만, 그래도 그 일만은 하고 있기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ said Mr Rugg, taking him by the hand, ‘you are a young man that it does one good to come across.", "\"선생,\" 러그 씨가 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["You are a young man that I should like to put in the witness-box, to humanise the minds of the legal profession.", "\"그대는 마주치는 것만으로도 사람에게 이로운 청년이외다. 그대야말로 내가 증인석에 세워, 법조계 사람들의 마음을 인간답게 만들어 주고 싶은 청년이오."], ["I hope you have brought your appetite with you, and intend to play a good knife and fork?’", "입맛도 챙겨 오셨기를 바라며, 칼과 포크를 야무지게 놀려 주시기를 바라오만?\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ returned Young John, ‘I don’t eat much at present.’", "\"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 청년 존이 대답했다. \"요즈음은 많이 먹지를 못합니다.\""]], [["Mr Rugg drew him a little apart.", "러그 씨는 그를 조금 옆으로 데려갔다."], ["‘My daughter’s case, sir,’ said he, ‘at the time when, in vindication of her outraged feelings and her sex, she became the plaintiff in Rugg and Bawkins.", "\"내 딸의 소송 때 말이오, 선생,\" 그가 말했다."], ["I suppose I could have put it in evidence, Mr Chivery, if I had thought it worth my while, that the amount of solid sustenance my daughter consumed at that period did not exceed ten ounces per week.’", "\"그 애가 짓밟힌 감정과 제 성(性)을 옹호하고자 러그 대 보킨스 사건의 원고가 되었던 그때 말이지요. 만약 그럴 만한 가치가 있다고 여겼더라면, 치버리 씨, 그 시절 내 딸이 먹은 든든한 음식의 양이 일주일에 십 온스를 넘지 않았다는 사실을 증거로 제출할 수도 있었을 거외다.\""]], [["‘I think I go a little beyond that, sir,’ returned the other, hesitating, as if he confessed it with some shame.", "\"저는 그보다는 조금 더 먹는 것 같습니다, 선생,\" 상대방이 마치 다소 부끄러운 일을 고백하듯 머뭇거리며 대답했다."]], [["‘But in your case there’s no fiend in human form,’ said Mr Rugg, with argumentative smile and action of hand.", "\"하지만 그대의 경우엔 사람의 탈을 쓴 악귀가 없잖소,\" 러그 씨가 논증하는 듯한 미소와 손짓을 곁들여 말했다."], ["‘Observe, Mr Chivery!", "\"보시오, 치버리 씨!"], ["No fiend in human form!’", "사람의 탈을 쓴 악귀가 없단 말이오!\""]], [["‘No, sir, certainly,’ Young John added with simplicity, ‘I should be very sorry if there was.’", "\"네, 선생, 정말 그렇습니다,\" 청년 존이 순박하게 덧붙였다. \"만약 있다면 저는 무척 마음 아플 겁니다.\""]], [["‘The sentiment,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘is what I should have expected from your known principles.", "\"그 마음이야말로,\" 러그 씨가 말했다."], ["It would affect my daughter greatly, sir, if she heard it.", "\"그대가 지닌 것으로 알려진 신조에서 마땅히 기대할 만한 것이외다."], ["As I perceive the mutton, I am glad she didn’t hear it.", "만약 내 딸이 그 말을 들었다면 크게 감동했을 거요, 선생. 양고기가 보이니 하는 말인데, 그 애가 듣지 않아 다행이오."], ["Mr Pancks, on this occasion, pray face me.", "팽크스 씨, 이번에는 부디 내 맞은편에 앉아 주시오."], ["My dear, face Mr Chivery.", "얘야, 너는 치버리 씨 맞은편에 앉으렴."], ["For what we are going to receive, may we (and Miss Dorrit) be truly thankful!’", "우리가 이제 받으려 하는 것에 대하여, 우리가 (그리고 도릿 양이) 진심으로 감사할 수 있기를!\""]], [["But for a grave waggishness in Mr Rugg’s manner of delivering this introduction to the feast, it might have appeared that Miss Dorrit was expected to be one of the company.", "이 식사 개회사를 전하는 러그 씨의 태도에 진중한 익살이 깃들어 있지 않았더라면, 마치 도릿 양이 그 자리의 일원으로 와 있기를 기대하는 듯이 보였을지도 몰랐다."], ["Pancks recognised the sally in his usual way, and took in his provender in his usual way.", "팽크스는 늘 하던 식으로 그 농을 알아차렸고, 늘 하던 식으로 음식을 먹어 치웠다."], ["Miss Rugg, perhaps making up some of her arrears, likewise took very kindly to the mutton, and it rapidly diminished to the bone.", "러그 양도, 어쩌면 그동안 밀린 양을 좀 메우려는 듯, 마찬가지로 양고기에 무척 살갑게 달려들어, 고기는 순식간에 뼈만 남도록 줄어들었다."], ["A bread-and-butter pudding entirely disappeared, and a considerable amount of cheese and radishes vanished by the same means.", "빵과 버터로 만든 푸딩이 통째로 사라졌고, 상당량의 치즈와 무도 같은 방식으로 자취를 감추었다."], ["Then came the dessert.", "그러고 나서 후식이 나왔다."]], [["Then also, and before the broaching of the rum and water, came Mr Pancks’s note-book.", "그러고 나서, 럼주에 물을 타기 전에, 또한 팽크스 씨의 수첩이 등장했다."], ["The ensuing business proceedings were brief but curious, and rather in the nature of a conspiracy.", "뒤이은 업무 진행은 짧으면서도 기이했으며, 다분히 음모의 성격을 띠고 있었다."], ["Mr Pancks looked over his note-book, which was now getting full, studiously; and picked out little extracts, which he wrote on separate slips of paper on the table; Mr Rugg, in the meanwhile, looking at him with close attention, and Young John losing his uncollected eye in mists of meditation.", "팽크스 씨는 이제 거의 가득 차 가는 수첩을 꼼꼼히 들여다보며, 짤막한 발췌 사항들을 골라내어 식탁 위 별도의 종이쪽지에 적었다. 그동안 러그 씨는 그를 면밀히 주시했고, 청년 존은 갈피 못 잡는 그 눈을 사색의 안개 속에 잃고 있었다."], ["When Mr Pancks, who supported the character of chief conspirator, had completed his extracts, he looked them over, corrected them, put up his note-book, and held them like a hand at cards.", "우두머리 모의자 역할을 맡은 팽크스 씨가 발췌를 마치자, 그는 그것들을 한 번 훑어보고, 손질하고, 수첩을 거둬들인 뒤, 그것들을 카드 패처럼 손에 쥐었다."]], [["‘Now, there’s a churchyard in Bedfordshire,’ said Pancks.", "\"자, 베드퍼드셔에 묘지가 하나 있소,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Who takes it?’", "\"누가 맡겠소?\""]], [["‘I’ll take it, sir,’ returned Mr Rugg, ‘if no one bids.’", "\"아무도 부르지 않거든, 선생, 제가 맡지요,\" 러그 씨가 대답했다."]], [["Mr Pancks dealt him his card, and looked at his hand again.", "팽크스 씨는 그에게 카드 한 장을 내준 뒤, 다시 제 패를 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Now, there’s an Enquiry in York,’ said Pancks.", "\"자, 요크에 조회 건이 하나 있소,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Who takes it?’", "\"누가 맡겠소?\""]], [["‘I’m not good for York,’ said Mr Rugg.", "\"나는 요크는 영 자신이 없소,\" 러그 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Then perhaps,’ pursued Pancks, ‘you’ll be so obliging, John Chivery?’", "\"그렇다면 아마도,\" 팽크스가 말을 이었다. \"자네가 좀 수고해 주겠나, 존 치버리?\""]], [["Young John assenting, Pancks dealt him his card, and consulted his hand again.", "청년 존이 응낙하자, 팽크스는 그에게 카드를 내주고 다시 제 패를 살폈다."]], [["‘There’s a Church in London; I may as well take that.", "\"런던에 교회가 하나 있군. 이건 내가 맡지."], ["And a Family Bible; I may as well take that, too.", "그리고 가족용 성경책이 하나. 이것도 내가 맡고."], ["That’s two to me.", "그러면 내가 둘이군."], ["Two to me,’ repeated Pancks, breathing hard over his cards.", "내가 둘,\" 카드 위로 거칠게 숨을 내쉬며 팽크스가 되뇌었다."], ["‘Here’s a Clerk at Durham for you, John, and an old seafaring gentleman at Dunstable for you, Mr Rugg.", "\"여기 더럼의 사무원 건은 자네 거다, 존. 그리고 던스터블의 늙은 뱃사람 신사 건은 당신 거외다, 러그 씨."], ["Two to me, was it?", "내가 둘이라고 했던가?"], ["Yes, two to me.", "그래, 내가 둘."], ["Here’s a Stone; three to me.", "여기 비석이 하나. 이러면 내가 셋."], ["And a Still-born Baby; four to me.", "그리고 사산아가 하나. 이러면 내가 넷."], ["And all, for the present, told.’", "그리고 당장은 이게 전부요.\""]], [["When he had thus disposed of his cards, all being done very quietly and in a suppressed tone, Mr Pancks puffed his way into his own breast-pocket and tugged out a canvas bag; from which, with a sparing hand, he told forth money for travelling expenses in two little portions. ‘Cash goes out fast,’ he said anxiously, as he pushed a portion to each of his male companions, ‘very fast.’", "이렇게 카드를 다 나누고 나자—모든 일이 아주 조용히, 낮춘 목소리로 이루어졌다—팽크스 씨는 푸푸 숨을 몰아쉬며 제 가슴주머니 속을 더듬어 즈크 자루 하나를 끄집어냈다. 그러고는 인색한 손길로 여비를 두 몫으로 헤아려 냈다. \"현금이 빠르게 나가는군,\" 그가 두 남자 동료에게 각각 한 몫씩 밀어 주며 근심스레 말했다. \"아주 빠르게 말이야.\""]], [["‘I can only assure you, Mr Pancks,’ said Young John, ‘that I deeply regret my circumstances being such that I can’t afford to pay my own charges, or that it’s not advisable to allow me the time necessary for my doing the distances on foot; because nothing would give me greater satisfaction than to walk myself off my legs without fee or reward.’", "\"제가 드릴 말씀은 이것뿐입니다, 팽크스 씨,\" 청년 존이 말했다. \"제 형편이 제 경비조차 댈 수 없을 만큼인 게, 또 거리를 도보로 가는 데 필요한 시간을 제게 허락하는 게 바람직하지 못한 게 참으로 유감스럽습니다. 사례도 보수도 없이 두 다리가 닳도록 걷는 것보다 더 큰 만족을 줄 일은 제겐 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["This young man’s disinterestedness appeared so very ludicrous in the eyes of Miss Rugg, that she was obliged to effect a precipitate retirement from the company, and to sit upon the stairs until she had had her laugh out.", "이 청년의 사심 없음이 러그 양의 눈에는 어찌나 우스꽝스러워 보였던지, 그녀는 황급히 자리를 빠져나가 웃음이 다할 때까지 계단에 앉아 있어야 했다."], ["Meanwhile Mr Pancks, looking, not without some pity, at Young John, slowly and thoughtfully twisted up his canvas bag as if he were wringing its neck.", "그동안 팽크스 씨는 청년 존을 적잖은 연민을 담아 바라보며, 마치 즈크 자루의 목을 비틀어 짜기라도 하듯 천천히, 생각에 잠겨 그것을 비틀어 말았다."], ["The lady, returning as he restored it to his pocket, mixed rum and water for the party, not forgetting her fair self, and handed to every one his glass.", "숙녀는 그가 그것을 다시 주머니에 넣을 즈음 돌아와, 일행을 위해 제 고운 몫도 잊지 않고 럼주에 물을 타서 저마다에게 잔을 건넸다."], ["When all were supplied, Mr Rugg rose, and silently holding out his glass at arm’s length above the centre of the table, by that gesture invited the other three to add theirs, and to unite in a general conspiratorial clink.", "모두에게 잔이 돌아가자, 러그 씨가 일어서서 식탁 한가운데 위로 팔을 쭉 뻗어 잔을 말없이 내밀었고, 그 몸짓으로 나머지 셋에게 잔을 보태어 다 함께 모의자다운 건배를 울리자고 청했다."], ["The ceremony was effective up to a certain point, and would have been wholly so throughout, if Miss Rugg, as she raised her glass to her lips in completion of it, had not happened to look at Young John; when she was again so overcome by the contemptible comicality of his disinterestedness as to splutter some ambrosial drops of rum and water around, and withdraw in confusion.", "그 의식은 어느 지점까지는 효과적이었고, 만약 러그 양이 마무리로 잔을 입술로 가져가다가 마침 청년 존을 쳐다보지만 않았던들 끝까지 온전히 그러했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 사심 없음의 가소로운 우스꽝스러움에 또다시 어찌나 사로잡혔던지, 럼주 물의 천상 같은 몇 방울을 사방에 뿜고는 당황한 채 물러나 버렸다."]], [["Such was the dinner without precedent, given by Pancks at Pentonville; and such was the busy and strange life Pancks led.", "이것이 팽크스가 펜턴빌에서 베푼 전례 없는 만찬이었다. 또 이것이 팽크스가 영위한 바쁘고도 기이한 삶이었다."], ["The only waking moments at which he appeared to relax from his cares, and to recreate himself by going anywhere or saying anything without a pervading object, were when he showed a dawning interest in the lame foreigner with the stick, down Bleeding Heart Yard.", "깨어 있는 시간 가운데 그가 근심에서 풀려나, 두루 깔린 목적 없이 어딘가로 가거나 무언가를 말하며 스스로를 즐기는 듯 보이는 유일한 순간은, 블리딩 하트 야드 아래쪽의 지팡이 짚은 절름발이 외국인에게 어렴풋한 관심을 보일 때였다."]], [["The foreigner, by name John Baptist Cavalletto--they called him Mr Baptist in the Yard--was such a chirping, easy, hopeful little fellow, that his attraction for Pancks was probably in the force of contrast.", "그 외국인은—이름은 존 밥티스트 카발레토였으나 야드에서는 그를 밥티스트 씨라고 불렀다—재잘대고 느긋하며 희망에 찬 자그마한 사내여서, 그가 팽크스의 마음을 끈 것은 아마도 대조의 힘에서였으리라."], ["Solitary, weak, and scantily acquainted with the most necessary words of the only language in which he could communicate with the people about him, he went with the stream of his fortunes, in a brisk way that was new in those parts.", "외로이, 연약하게, 주변 사람들과 의사를 나눌 수 있는 유일한 언어의 가장 필요한 낱말조차 변변히 알지 못한 채로, 그는 그 일대에서는 새로운, 활기찬 태도로 제 운명의 흐름을 따라갔다."], ["With little to eat, and less to drink, and nothing to wear but what he wore upon him, or had brought tied up in one of the smallest bundles that ever were seen, he put as bright a face upon it as if he were in the most flourishing circumstances when he first hobbled up and down the Yard, humbly propitiating the general good-will with his white teeth.", "먹을 것은 적고 마실 것은 더 적었으며, 몸에 걸친 것이나 여태껏 본 가장 작은 봇짐 가운데 하나에 꾸려 온 것 말고는 입을 옷도 없었으나, 그는 처음 야드를 절뚝거리며 오르내릴 때 하얀 이를 드러내며 겸손하게 사람들의 호의를 구할 적에도, 마치 더없이 융성한 처지에 있기라도 한 양 환한 낯빛을 지어 보였다."]], [["It was uphill work for a foreigner, lame or sound, to make his way with the Bleeding Hearts.", "절름발이든 멀쩡하든, 외국인이 블리딩 하트 사람들과 사이좋게 지내기란 힘겨운 일이었다."], ["In the first place, they were vaguely persuaded that every foreigner had a knife about him; in the second, they held it to be a sound constitutional national axiom that he ought to go home to his own country.", "첫째로, 그들은 외국인이라면 누구나 칼을 한 자루 지니고 다닌다고 막연히 믿었다. 둘째로, 외국인이라면 마땅히 제 나라로 돌아가야 한다는 것을 건전한 국민적 헌정의 공리로 여겼다."], ["They never thought of inquiring how many of their own countrymen would be returned upon their hands from divers parts of the world, if the principle were generally recognised; they considered it particularly and peculiarly British.", "그들은 만약 그 원칙이 두루 인정된다면 세계 각지에서 얼마나 많은 자기 동포가 자기들 손으로 되돌아오게 될지는 도무지 따져 볼 생각을 하지 않았고, 그것을 유독 영국 특유의 것으로만 여겼다."], ["In the third place, they had a notion that it was a sort of Divine visitation upon a foreigner that he was not an Englishman, and that all kinds of calamities happened to his country because it did things that England did not, and did not do things that England did.", "셋째로, 그들은 외국인이 영국인이 아니라는 것 자체가 그에게 내려진 일종의 천벌이며, 그의 나라가 영국이 하지 않는 일을 하고 영국이 하는 일을 하지 않기에 온갖 재앙이 그 나라에 닥치는 것이라는 관념을 품고 있었다."], ["In this belief, to be sure, they had long been carefully trained by the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings, who were always proclaiming to them, officially, that no country which failed to submit itself to those two large families could possibly hope to be under the protection of Providence; and who, when they believed it, disparaged them in private as the most prejudiced people under the sun.", "물론 이 믿음에 있어서 그들은 오래도록 배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가에게서 면밀히 길들여져 왔는데, 이들은 공식적으로 그들에게, 그 두 거대 가문에 복종하기를 거부하는 나라는 결코 신의 섭리의 보호 아래 있기를 바랄 수 없노라고 늘 선포하면서도, 정작 그들이 그것을 곧이듣자 뒤에서는 그들을 해 아래 가장 편견에 찬 족속이라고 헐뜯었다."]], [["This, therefore, might be called a political position of the Bleeding Hearts; but they entertained other objections to having foreigners in the Yard.", "그러므로 이것은 블리딩 하트 사람들의 정치적 입장이라 부를 만했다. 그러나 그들은 야드에 외국인을 들이는 것에 대해 또 다른 반대 이유들도 품고 있었다."], ["They believed that foreigners were always badly off; and though they were as ill off themselves as they could desire to be, that did not diminish the force of the objection.", "그들은 외국인이란 늘 형편이 어렵다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기들 역시 더 바랄 나위 없을 만큼 어려운 형편이었음에도, 그 사실이 반대의 힘을 누그러뜨리지는 못했다."], ["They believed that foreigners were dragooned and bayoneted; and though they certainly got their own skulls promptly fractured if they showed any ill-humour, still it was with a blunt instrument, and that didn’t count.", "그들은 외국인이란 군홧발에 짓밟히고 총검에 찔린다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기들도 조금이라도 심사가 사나운 기색을 보이면 곧장 두개골이 박살나곤 했음에도, 그것은 어디까지나 무딘 연장에 의한 것이니 셈에 들지 않았다."], ["They believed that foreigners were always immoral; and though they had an occasional assize at home, and now and then a divorce case or so, that had nothing to do with it.", "그들은 외국인이란 늘 부도덕하다고 믿었다. 그리고 자기네 나라에도 이따금 순회재판이 열리고 가끔 이혼 소송 따위가 있었음에도, 그것은 이 문제와는 아무 상관이 없었다."], ["They believed that foreigners had no independent spirit, as never being escorted to the poll in droves by Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle, with colours flying and the tune of Rule Britannia playing.", "그들은 외국인이란, 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경에게 깃발을 펄럭이고 '룰 브리타니아' 곡조를 울리며 떼 지어 투표소까지 호위받아 가 본 적이 없으니, 독립적 기개라곤 없다고 믿었다."], ["Not to be tedious, they had many other beliefs of a similar kind.", "장황해지지 않기 위해 줄이거니와, 그들은 이와 비슷한 부류의 다른 믿음도 많이 품고 있었다."]], [["Against these obstacles, the lame foreigner with the stick had to make head as well as he could; not absolutely single-handed, because Mr Arthur Clennam had recommended him to the Plornishes (he lived at the top of the same house), but still at heavy odds.", "이런 장애물들에 맞서, 지팡이 짚은 절름발이 외국인은 제 깜냥껏 헤쳐 나가야 했다. 아주 외돌토리는 아니었으니, 아서 클레넘 씨가 그를 플로니시 부부에게 추천해 둔 덕이었으나(그는 같은 집의 꼭대기 층에 살았다), 그래도 형세는 몹시 불리했다."], ["However, the Bleeding Hearts were kind hearts; and when they saw the little fellow cheerily limping about with a good-humoured face, doing no harm, drawing no knives, committing no outrageous immoralities, living chiefly on farinaceous and milk diet, and playing with Mrs Plornish’s children of an evening, they began to think that although he could never hope to be an Englishman, still it would be hard to visit that affliction on his head.", "그러나 블리딩 하트 사람들은 마음씨가 고운 이들이었다. 그래서 그들은 그 자그마한 사내가 명랑하게 절뚝이며 돌아다니는 것을, 사람 좋은 낯빛으로, 해코지 하나 하지 않고, 칼 한 번 빼 들지 않고, 차마 못 봐줄 부도덕을 저지르는 일도 없이, 주로 곡물과 우유로 끼니를 때우고, 저녁이면 플로니시 부인의 아이들과 놀아 주는 것을 보자, 그가 영국인이 되기를 결코 바랄 수는 없을지언정 그 불운을 그의 머리 위에 들씌우는 것은 가혹한 일이라고 여기기 시작했다."], ["They began to accommodate themselves to his level, calling him ‘Mr Baptist,’ but treating him like a baby, and laughing immoderately at his lively gestures and his childish English--more, because he didn’t mind it, and laughed too.", "그들은 그의 수준에 자기들을 맞추어, 그를 '밥티스트 씨'라고 부르면서도 갓난아기 다루듯 대했고, 그의 생기 넘치는 몸짓과 어린애 같은 영어에 한도 끝도 없이 웃어 댔는데—그가 그것을 개의치 않고 함께 웃었기에 더욱 그러했다. 그들은 그가 귀머거리라도 되는 양 아주 큰 목소리로 그에게 말했다."], ["They spoke to him in very loud voices as if he were stone deaf.", "그들은 순수한 형태의 언어를 가르친답시고, 미개인들이 쿡 선장에게, 혹은 프라이데이가 로빈슨 크루소에게 건넸음 직한 문장을 지어냈다. 플로니시 부인은 특히 이 재주에 뛰어나, \"내 바람 댁 다리 곧 다 낫기\"라는 말로 어찌나 명성을 떨쳤던지, 야드에서는 그것이 이탈리아어를 하는 것과 정말 한 걸음밖에 차이 나지 않는다고들 여겼다."], ["They constructed sentences, by way of teaching him the language in its purity, such as were addressed by the savages to Captain Cook, or by Friday to Robinson Crusoe.", "플로니시 부인 자신마저 자기에게 그 언어에 대한 타고난 소명이 있다고 여기기 시작했다. 그가 더 인기를 끌게 되자, 그에게 풍성한 어휘를 가르치고자 집 안 살림살이까지 동원되었다."], ["Mrs Plornish was particularly ingenious in this art; and attained so much celebrity for saying ‘Me ope you leg well soon,’ that it was considered in the Yard but a very short remove indeed from speaking Italian.", "그래서 그가 야드에 나타날 때면 부인들이 저마다 문간으로 뛰쳐나와 외쳐 댔다. \"밥티스트 씨—찻주전자!\""], ["Even Mrs Plornish herself began to think that she had a natural call towards that language.", "\"밥티스트 씨—쓰레받기!\" \"밥티스트 씨—밀가루 체!\""], ["As he became more popular, household objects were brought into requisition for his instruction in a copious vocabulary; and whenever he appeared in the Yard ladies would fly out at their doors crying ‘Mr Baptist--tea-pot!’ ‘Mr Baptist--dust-pan!’ ‘Mr Baptist--flour-dredger!’ ‘Mr Baptist--coffee-biggin!’ At the same time exhibiting those articles, and penetrating him with a sense of the appalling difficulties of the Anglo-Saxon tongue.", "\"밥티스트 씨—커피 끓이개!\" 그러면서 그 물건들을 내보이며, 앵글로색슨 말의 끔찍한 어려움을 그에게 절감시켰다."]], [["It was in this stage of his progress, and in about the third week of his occupation, that Mr Pancks’s fancy became attracted by the little man.", "밥티스트 씨가 이런 진척의 단계에 이르렀을 무렵, 또 그가 그곳에 들어와 산 지 세 주째쯤 되었을 때, 팽크스 씨의 호기심이 그 자그마한 사내에게 끌리게 되었다."], ["Mounting to his attic, attended by Mrs Plornish as interpreter, he found Mr Baptist with no furniture but his bed on the ground, a table, and a chair, carving with the aid of a few simple tools, in the blithest way possible.", "통역으로 따라온 플로니시 부인을 데리고 그의 다락방에 올라가 보니, 밥티스트 씨는 바닥에 놓인 침상과 탁자 하나, 의자 하나 말고는 가구라곤 없는 곳에서, 몇 가지 간단한 연장의 도움을 받아 더없이 흥겨운 태도로 무언가를 깎고 있었다."]], [["‘Now, old chap,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘pay up!’", "\"자, 영감님,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"셈을 치르시오!\""]], [["He had his money ready, folded in a scrap of paper, and laughingly handed it in; then with a free action, threw out as many fingers of his right hand as there were shillings, and made a cut crosswise in the air for an odd sixpence.", "그는 종잇조각에 접어 넣은 돈을 미리 준비해 두었다가, 웃으며 그것을 건넸다. 그러고는 거침없는 동작으로, 실링 액수만큼 오른손 손가락을 펴 보이고, 나머지 육 펜스를 나타내려고 허공에 가로로 한 번 그었다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Mr Pancks, watching him, wonderingly.", "\"오!\" 팽크스 씨가 그를 신기한 듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘That’s it, is it?", "\"그런 거였군, 그래?"], ["You’re a quick customer.", "빠릿빠릿한 양반이로구만."], ["It’s all right.", "됐소."], ["I didn’t expect to receive it, though.’", "하긴 받으리라곤 기대도 안 했지만.\""]], [["Mrs Plornish here interposed with great condescension, and explained to Mr Baptist.", "여기서 플로니시 부인이 자못 너그러운 태도로 끼어들어 밥티스트 씨에게 설명했다."], ["‘E please.", "\"이분 좋아해."], ["E glad get money.’", "이분 돈 받아 기뻐.\""]], [["The little man smiled and nodded.", "자그마한 사내는 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["His bright face seemed uncommonly attractive to Mr Pancks.", "그 환한 얼굴이 팽크스 씨에게는 유난히 매력적으로 보이는 듯했다."], ["‘How’s he getting on in his limb?’ he asked Mrs Plornish.", "\"다리는 좀 어떻소?\" 그가 플로니시 부인에게 물었다."]], [["‘Oh, he’s a deal better, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish. ‘We expect next week he’ll be able to leave off his stick entirely.’ (The opportunity being too favourable to be lost, Mrs Plornish displayed her great accomplishment by explaining with pardonable pride to Mr Baptist, ‘E ope you leg well soon.’)", "\"오, 한결 나아졌답니다, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"다음 주쯤이면 지팡이를 아주 놓을 수 있을 것 같아요.\" (이 기회가 놓치기엔 너무 좋았던지라, 플로니시 부인은 봐줄 만한 자부심을 담아 밥티스트 씨에게 \"내 바람 댁 다리 곧 다 낫기\" 하고 설명함으로써 자신의 그 대단한 재주를 펼쳐 보였다.)"]], [["‘He’s a merry fellow, too,’ said Mr Pancks, admiring him as if he were a mechanical toy.", "\"게다가 명랑한 친구로군,\" 팽크스 씨가 마치 태엽 장난감이라도 되는 양 그에게 감탄하며 말했다."], ["‘How does he live?’", "\"어떻게 먹고삽니까?\""]], [["‘Why, sir,’ rejoined Mrs Plornish, ‘he turns out to have quite a power of carving them flowers that you see him at now.’ (Mr Baptist, watching their faces as they spoke, held up his work. Mrs Plornish interpreted in her Italian manner, on behalf of Mr Pancks, ‘E please. Double good!’)", "\"그게 말이죠, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 대답했다. \"지금 보시다시피 저 꽃들을 깎는 데 제법 솜씨가 있다는 게 드러났답니다.\" (밥티스트 씨는 그들이 말하는 동안 그 얼굴들을 살피다가 제 작품을 들어 보였다. 플로니시 부인이 팽크스 씨를 위해 자신의 이탈리아식으로 통역했다. \"이분 좋아해. 갑절로 좋아!\")"]], [["‘Can he live by that?’ asked Mr Pancks.", "\"저걸로 먹고살 수 있답니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘He can live on very little, sir, and it is expected as he will be able, in time, to make a very good living.", "\"아주 적게 들여서도 살 수 있고요, 선생, 머지않아 꽤 괜찮은 벌이를 할 수 있으리라 기대된답니다."], ["Mr Clennam got it him to do, and gives him odd jobs besides in at the Works next door--makes ‘em for him, in short, when he knows he wants ‘em.’", "클레넘 씨가 저 일을 얻어다 주셨고, 그 밖에도 바로 옆집 공장에서 자질구레한 일거리를 주신답니다—말하자면, 저 사람이 필요로 하는 걸 아실 때면 일부러 만들어 주시는 거죠.\""]], [["‘And what does he do with himself, now, when he ain’t hard at it?’ said Mr Pancks.", "\"그럼 일에 매달려 있지 않을 때는, 저 친구 뭘 하고 지냅니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Why, not much as yet, sir, on accounts I suppose of not being able to walk much; but he goes about the Yard, and he chats without particular understanding or being understood, and he plays with the children, and he sits in the sun--he’ll sit down anywhere, as if it was an arm-chair--and he’ll sing, and he’ll laugh!’", "\"글쎄요, 아직은 별로 하는 게 없어요, 선생, 아무래도 많이 걷지를 못해서 그렇겠지요. 그래도 야드를 돌아다니고, 딱히 알아듣거나 알아듣게 하지도 못하면서 잡담을 나누고, 아이들과 놀고, 햇볕 아래 앉아 있고요—마치 안락의자라도 되는 양 아무 데나 털썩 앉지요—노래도 부르고, 웃기도 한답니다!\""]], [["‘Laugh!’ echoed Mr Pancks.", "\"웃는다고요!\" 팽크스 씨가 따라 말했다."], ["‘He looks to me as if every tooth in his head was always laughing.’", "\"내 보기엔 저 친구 머릿속 이빨이 죄다 늘 웃고 있는 것 같던데.\""]], [["‘But whenever he gets to the top of the steps at t’other end of the Yard,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘he’ll peep out in the curiousest way!", "\"하지만 야드 저쪽 끝 계단 꼭대기에 올라설 때마다요, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."], ["So that some of us thinks he’s peeping out towards where his own country is, and some of us thinks he’s looking for somebody he don’t want to see, and some of us don’t know what to think.’", "\"어찌나 묘하게 밖을 내다보는지! 그래서 우리 중에는 저 사람이 제 나라가 있는 쪽을 내다본다고 여기는 이도 있고, 보고 싶지 않은 누군가를 찾는 거라고 여기는 이도 있고, 또 뭐라고 생각해야 할지 모르겠다는 이도 있답니다.\""]], [["Mr Baptist seemed to have a general understanding of what she said; or perhaps his quickness caught and applied her slight action of peeping.", "밥티스트 씨는 그녀의 말뜻을 대강 알아들은 듯했다. 아니면 그 영민함으로 그녀가 내다보는 시늉을 한 가벼운 몸짓을 포착해 적용했는지도 모른다."], ["In any case he closed his eyes and tossed his head with the air of a man who had sufficient reasons for what he did, and said in his own tongue, it didn’t matter.", "어느 쪽이든 그는 눈을 감고 고개를 젖히며, 자기가 한 일에 충분한 이유가 있는 사람의 태도로, 제 나라 말로 그건 아무것도 아니라고 말했다."], ["Altro!", "알트로!"]], [["‘What’s Altro?’ said Pancks.", "\"알트로가 뭡니까?\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘Hem!", "\"흠!"], ["It’s a sort of a general kind of expression, sir,’ said Mrs Plornish.", "그건 일종의 두루뭉술한 표현이랍니다, 선생,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Is it?’ said Pancks.", "\"그래요?\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Why, then Altro to you, old chap.", "\"그렇다면, 영감님, 당신에게도 알트로요."], ["Good afternoon.", "그럼 이만."], ["Altro!’", "알트로!\""]], [["Mr Baptist in his vivacious way repeating the word several times, Mr Pancks in his duller way gave it him back once. From that time it became a frequent custom with Pancks the gipsy, as he went home jaded at night, to pass round by Bleeding Heart Yard, go quietly up the stairs, look in at Mr Baptist’s door, and, finding him in his room, to say, ‘Hallo, old chap! Altro!’ To which Mr Baptist would reply with innumerable bright nods and smiles, ‘Altro, signore, altro, altro, altro!’ After this highly condensed conversation, Mr Pancks would go his way with an appearance of being lightened and refreshed.", "밥티스트 씨가 제 활기찬 투로 그 말을 여러 번 되뇌자, 팽크스 씨는 제 좀 더 굼뜬 투로 그것을 한 번 되돌려 주었다. 그때부터, 집시 같은 팽크스가 밤에 녹초가 되어 집으로 돌아가는 길에 블리딩 하트 야드를 빙 둘러, 조용히 계단을 올라가 밥티스트 씨의 방문 안을 들여다보고, 그가 방에 있는 것을 보면 \"어이, 영감님! 알트로!\" 하고 인사하는 것이 잦은 버릇이 되었다. 그러면 밥티스트 씨는 한도 없이 환하게 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며 \"알트로, 시뇨레, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로!\" 하고 화답하곤 했다. 이 극도로 압축된 대화를 나눈 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 짐을 던 듯 산뜻해진 모습으로 제 갈 길을 가곤 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 26. Nobody’s State of Mind", "# CHAPTER 26. Nobody’s State of Mind"]], [["If Arthur Clennam had not arrived at that wise decision firmly to restrain himself from loving Pet, he would have lived on in a state of much perplexity, involving difficult struggles with his own heart.", "만약 아서 클레넘이 펫을 사랑하는 일을 단호히 자제하겠다는 그 현명한 결심에 이르지 못했더라면, 그는 자기 마음과의 어려운 싸움을 동반한 숱한 혼란 속에서 살아갔을 것이다."], ["Not the least of these would have been a contention, always waging within it, between a tendency to dislike Mr Henry Gowan, if not to regard him with positive repugnance, and a whisper that the inclination was unworthy.", "그중에서도 가장 작지 않은 것은, 헨리 가우언 씨를, 노골적인 혐오로까지는 아니더라도 싫어하려는 경향과, 그러한 마음이 떳떳하지 못하다는 속삭임 사이에서 그의 마음속에서 끊임없이 벌어지는 다툼이었으리라."], ["A generous nature is not prone to strong aversions, and is slow to admit them even dispassionately; but when it finds ill-will gaining upon it, and can discern between-whiles that its origin is not dispassionate, such a nature becomes distressed.", "너그러운 천성은 강한 반감에 쉽게 빠지지 않으며, 냉정하게라도 그것을 받아들이는 데 더디다. 그러나 악감정이 자기 안에서 자라나는 것을 발견하고, 때때로 그 기원이 냉정하지 못함을 알아챌 수 있을 때, 그런 천성은 괴로워지게 마련이다."]], [["Therefore Mr Henry Gowan would have clouded Clennam’s mind, and would have been far oftener present to it than more agreeable persons and subjects but for the great prudence of his decision aforesaid.", "그러므로 앞서 말한 그 큰 신중함의 결심이 아니었더라면, 헨리 가우언 씨는 클레넘의 마음을 흐려 놓았을 것이며, 더 유쾌한 사람들이나 화제들보다 훨씬 자주 그의 마음에 떠올랐을 것이다."], ["As it was, Mr Gowan seemed transferred to Daniel Doyce’s mind; at all events, it so happened that it usually fell to Mr Doyce’s turn, rather than to Clennam’s, to speak of him in the friendly conversations they held together.", "실상은, 가우언 씨가 대니얼 도이스의 마음으로 옮겨 간 듯했다. 어쨌든 둘이 함께 나누는 다정한 대화에서 그를 입에 올리는 일은 대개 클레넘보다는 도이스 씨의 차지가 되곤 했다."], ["These were of frequent occurrence now; as the two partners shared a portion of a roomy house in one of the grave old-fashioned City streets, lying not far from the Bank of England, by London Wall.", "이런 대화는 이제 자주 있는 일이었는데, 두 동업자가 런던 월 가까이, 잉글랜드 은행에서 멀지 않은 어느 점잖고 고풍스러운 시티 거리에 자리한 널찍한 집의 일부를 함께 쓰고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Mr Doyce had been to Twickenham to pass the day.", "도이스 씨는 트위크넘에 가서 하루를 보내고 온 참이었다."], ["Clennam had excused himself.", "클레넘은 사양했었다."], ["Mr Doyce was just come home.", "도이스 씨는 막 집에 돌아왔다."], ["He put in his head at the door of Clennam’s sitting-room to say Good night.", "그는 클레넘의 거실 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀며 안녕히 주무시라고 인사했다."]], [["‘Come in, come in!’ said Clennam.", "\"들어와요, 들어와요!\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘I saw you were reading,’ returned Doyce, as he entered, ‘and thought you might not care to be disturbed.’", "\"책을 읽고 계신 걸 보고,\" 도이스가 들어서며 대꾸했다. \"방해받기 싫으실까 봐서요.\""]], [["But for the notable resolution he had made, Clennam really might not have known what he had been reading; really might not have had his eyes upon the book for an hour past, though it lay open before him.", "그가 세운 그 주목할 만한 결심이 아니었더라면, 클레넘은 자기가 무엇을 읽고 있었는지조차 정말로 알지 못했을 것이다. 책이 그의 앞에 펼쳐져 있긴 했어도, 정말로 한 시간 내내 책에 눈길을 두지 않고 있었을 것이다."], ["He shut it up, rather quickly.", "그는 책을 꽤 빠르게 덮어 버렸다."]], [["‘Are they well?’ he asked.", "\"다들 잘 지내십니까?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Doyce; ‘they are well.", "\"네.\" 도이스가 말했다."], ["They are all well.’", "\"다들 잘 지냅니다. 모두 평안해요.\""]], [["Daniel had an old workmanlike habit of carrying his pocket-handkerchief in his hat.", "대니얼에게는 손수건을 모자 안에 넣어 다니는 오래된 장인다운 버릇이 있었다."], ["He took it out and wiped his forehead with it, slowly repeating, ‘They are all well.", "그는 그것을 꺼내 이마를 닦으며 천천히 되풀이했다. \"모두 평안해요."], ["Miss Minnie looking particularly well, I thought.’", "미니 양은 특히 안색이 좋아 보이던데요.\""]], [["‘Any company at the cottage?’", "\"오두막에 손님이라도 있었습니까?\""]], [["‘No, no company.’", "\"아니요, 손님은 없었습니다.\""]], [["‘And how did you get on, you four?’ asked Clennam gaily.", "\"그래서 네 분이 어떻게 지내셨습니까?\" 클레넘이 명랑하게 물었다."]], [["‘There were five of us,’ returned his partner.", "\"다섯이었습니다.\" 그의 동업자가 대꾸했다."], ["‘There was What’s-his-name.", "\"그 뭐냐 하는 사람이 있었지요."], ["He was there.’", "그 사람이 거기 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘Who is he?’ said Clennam.", "\"그게 누굽니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Henry Gowan.’", "\"헨리 가우언 씨요.\""]], [["‘Ah, to be sure!’ cried Clennam with unusual vivacity, ‘Yes!--I forgot him.’", "\"아, 그렇지!\" 클레넘이 평소와 달리 활기차게 외쳤다. \"그렇네요!—깜빡했습니다.\""]], [["‘As I mentioned, you may remember,’ said Daniel Doyce, ‘he is always there on Sunday.’", "\"전에 말씀드렸을 텐데요, 기억하실지.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다. \"그 사람은 일요일이면 늘 거기 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes,’ returned Clennam; ‘I remember now.’", "\"네, 네.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"이제 기억나는군요.\""]], [["Daniel Doyce, still wiping his forehead, ploddingly repeated.", "대니얼 도이스는 여전히 이마를 닦으며 끈덕지게 되풀이했다."], ["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["He was there, he was there.", "그 사람이 거기 있었지요, 거기 있었습니다."], ["Oh yes, he was there.", "아, 그래요, 거기 있었어요."], ["And his dog.", "그리고 그 사람의 개도요."], ["_He_ was there too.’", "그놈도 거기 있었습니다.\""]], [["‘Miss Meagles is quite attached to--the--dog,’ observed Clennam.", "\"미글스 양이 그—그 개를—무척 좋아하지요.\" 클레넘이 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Quite so,’ assented his partner.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 동업자가 동의했다."], ["‘More attached to the dog than I am to the man.’", "\"제가 그 사람을 좋아하는 것보다 그분이 그 개를 더 좋아하지요.\""]], [["‘You mean Mr--?’", "\"말씀하시는 게 그—?\""]], [["‘I mean Mr Gowan, most decidedly,’ said Daniel Doyce.", "\"가우언 씨 말입니다, 아주 분명히요.\" 대니얼 도이스가 말했다."]], [["There was a gap in the conversation, which Clennam devoted to winding up his watch.", "대화에 빈틈이 생겼고, 클레넘은 그것을 시계 태엽을 감는 데 썼다."]], [["‘Perhaps you are a little hasty in your judgment,’ he said.", "\"어쩌면 판단이 좀 성급하신지도 모르겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Our judgments--I am supposing a general case--’", "\"우리의 판단이라는 게—일반적인 경우를 두고 하는 말입니다만—\""]], [["‘Of course,’ said Doyce.", "\"물론이지요.\" 도이스가 말했다."]], [["‘Are so liable to be influenced by many considerations, which, almost without our knowing it, are unfair, that it is necessary to keep a guard upon them.", "\"우리도 거의 모르는 사이에 공정하지 못한 여러 고려에 휩쓸리기가 쉬워서, 거기에 경계를 두어야 할 필요가 있습니다."], ["For instance, Mr--’", "이를테면, 그—씨는—\""]], [["‘Gowan,’ quietly said Doyce, upon whom the utterance of the name almost always devolved.", "\"가우언이오.\" 도이스가 조용히 말했다. 그 이름을 입에 올리는 일은 거의 늘 그의 몫이었다."]], [["‘Is young and handsome, easy and quick, has talent, and has seen a good deal of various kinds of life.", "\"젊고 잘생겼고, 느긋하고 재빠르며, 재능이 있고, 다양한 종류의 삶을 꽤 많이 보아 온 사람입니다."], ["It might be difficult to give an unselfish reason for being prepossessed against him.’", "그에게 편견을 품을 만한 사심 없는 이유를 대기란 어려울지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["‘Not difficult for me, I think, Clennam,’ returned his partner.", "\"나로서는 어렵지 않은 것 같소, 클레넘.\" 동업자가 대꾸했다."], ["‘I see him bringing present anxiety, and, I fear, future sorrow, into my old friend’s house.", "\"나는 그가 내 오랜 친구의 집에 지금의 근심을, 그리고 내가 두려워하건대 앞으로의 슬픔을 가져오는 것을 봅니다."], ["I see him wearing deeper lines into my old friend’s face, the nearer he draws to, and the oftener he looks at, the face of his daughter.", "그가 딸의 얼굴에 가까이 다가가고 더 자주 그 얼굴을 바라볼수록, 내 오랜 친구의 얼굴에 더 깊은 주름을 새겨 넣는 것을 봅니다."], ["In short, I see him with a net about the pretty and affectionate creature whom he will never make happy.’", "한마디로, 나는 그가 결코 행복하게 해 주지 못할 그 예쁘고 다정한 사람에게 그물을 던지고 있는 것을 봅니다.\""]], [["‘We don’t know,’ said Clennam, almost in the tone of a man in pain, ‘that he will not make her happy.’", "\"우리가 어떻게 압니까,\" 클레넘이 거의 고통스러운 사람의 어조로 말했다. \"그가 그녀를 행복하게 해 주지 못하리라는 것을요.\""]], [["‘We don’t know,’ returned his partner, ‘that the earth will last another hundred years, but we think it highly probable.’", "\"우리가 어떻게 알겠소,\" 동업자가 대꾸했다. \"이 땅이 앞으로 백 년을 더 버틸지 말이오. 하지만 그럴 가능성이 매우 높다고 우리는 생각하지요.\""]], [["‘Well, well!’ said Clennam, ‘we must be hopeful, and we must at least try to be, if not generous (which, in this case, we have no opportunity of being), just. We will not disparage this gentleman, because he is successful in his addresses to the beautiful object of his ambition; and we will not question her natural right to bestow her love on one whom she finds worthy of it.’", "\"자, 자!\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"우리는 희망을 가져야 하고, 적어도 너그럽지는 못할지언정—이 경우에는 너그러울 기회가 우리에게 없으니까요—공정하려고 애써야 합니다. 이 신사가 야망의 아름다운 대상에게 구애하여 성공을 거두었다고 해서 그를 깎아내리지 맙시다. 그리고 그녀가 자기 사랑을 받을 만하다고 여기는 사람에게 그 사랑을 베풀 타고난 권리에 시비를 걸지도 맙시다.\""]], [["‘Maybe, my friend,’ said Doyce.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요, 친구.\""], ["‘Maybe also, that she is too young and petted, too confiding and inexperienced, to discriminate well.’", "도이스가 말했다. \"또한 그녀가 너무 어리고 응석받이로 자라, 너무 잘 믿고 경험이 없어서 제대로 분별하지 못하는지도 모르고요.\""]], [["‘That,’ said Clennam, ‘would be far beyond our power of correction.’", "\"그건,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"우리가 바로잡을 수 있는 능력을 훨씬 넘어서는 일이지요.\""]], [["Daniel Doyce shook his head gravely, and rejoined, ‘I fear so.’", "대니얼 도이스는 무겁게 고개를 가로젓고는 응했다. \"그런 것 같소.\""]], [["‘Therefore, in a word,’ said Clennam, ‘we should make up our minds that it is not worthy of us to say any ill of Mr Gowan.", "\"그러니 한마디로,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["It would be a poor thing to gratify a prejudice against him.", "\"우리는 가우언 씨에 관해 어떤 험담도 하지 않는 것이 우리답다고 마음을 정해야 합니다. 그에 대한 편견을 만족시키는 것은 옹졸한 짓일 테니까요."], ["And I resolve, for my part, not to depreciate him.’", "그래서 저는, 제 몫으로는, 그를 폄하하지 않기로 결심합니다.\""]], [["‘I am not quite so sure of myself, and therefore I reserve my privilege of objecting to him,’ returned the other. ‘But, if I am not sure of myself, I am sure of you, Clennam, and I know what an upright man you are, and how much to be respected. Good night, _my_ friend and partner!’ He shook his hand in saying this, as if there had been something serious at the bottom of their conversation; and they separated.", "\"나는 나 자신에 대해 그렇게까지 확신이 서지 않으니, 그에게 반대할 권리는 남겨 두겠소.\" 상대가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 내가 나 자신에 대해서는 확신이 없어도, 당신에 대해서는 확신이 있소, 클레넘. 나는 당신이 얼마나 올곧은 사람인지, 얼마나 존경받을 만한지 알지요. 안녕히 주무시오, 나의 친구이자 동업자여!\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 손을 맞잡았는데, 마치 그들의 대화 밑바닥에 무언가 심각한 것이 있었다는 듯했다. 그러고 두 사람은 헤어졌다."]], [["By this time they had visited the family on several occasions, and had always observed that even a passing allusion to Mr Henry Gowan when he was not among them, brought back the cloud which had obscured Mr Meagles’s sunshine on the morning of the chance encounter at the Ferry.", "이 무렵 그들은 여러 차례 그 가족을 방문했고, 헨리 가우언 씨가 그들 가운데 없을 때라도 그에 대한 지나가는 언급 한마디가 그 나루터에서의 우연한 만남이 있던 아침 미글스 씨의 햇살을 가렸던 그 구름을 다시 불러온다는 것을 늘 알아챘다."], ["If Clennam had ever admitted the forbidden passion into his breast, this period might have been a period of real trial; under the actual circumstances, doubtless it was nothing--nothing.", "클레넘이 그 금지된 열정을 가슴에 들인 적이 있었더라면, 이 시기는 진정한 시련의 시기였을 것이다. 실제 상황에서는, 의심할 바 없이 아무것도 아니었다—아무것도."]], [["Equally, if his heart had given entertainment to that prohibited guest, his silent fighting of his way through the mental condition of this period might have been a little meritorious.", "마찬가지로, 그의 마음이 그 금지된 손님을 맞아들였더라면, 이 시기의 정신 상태를 묵묵히 헤쳐 나간 그의 싸움은 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["In the constant effort not to be betrayed into a new phase of the besetting sin of his experience, the pursuit of selfish objects by low and small means, and to hold instead to some high principle of honour and generosity, there might have been a little merit.", "그의 경험에서 늘 따라다니던 죄악—저열하고 작은 수단으로 이기적인 목적을 좇는 일—의 새로운 국면으로 배신당하지 않으려 끊임없이 애쓰고, 그 대신 명예와 관대함의 어떤 높은 원칙을 붙드는 데에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["In the resolution not even to avoid Mr Meagles’s house, lest, in the selfish sparing of himself, he should bring any slight distress upon the daughter through making her the cause of an estrangement which he believed the father would regret, there might have been a little merit.", "자신을 이기적으로 아끼느라 딸을, 아버지가 후회하리라 그가 믿는 그 소원해짐의 원인으로 만들어 그녀에게 조금이라도 괴로움을 안길까 봐, 미글스 씨의 집을 피하지조차 않기로 한 결심에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["In the modest truthfulness of always keeping in view the greater equality of Mr Gowan’s years and the greater attractions of his person and manner, there might have been a little merit.", "가우언 씨가 나이가 더 많아 더 대등하다는 점과, 그의 용모와 태도가 더 매력적이라는 점을 늘 염두에 두는 그 겸허한 진실함에 약간의 공이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["In doing all this and much more, in a perfectly unaffected way and with a manful and composed constancy, while the pain within him (peculiar as his life and history) was very sharp, there might have been some quiet strength of character.", "그의 삶과 내력만큼이나 특이한 그 안의 고통이 몹시 날카로웠음에도, 이 모든 것을, 그리고 그보다 훨씬 많은 것을 조금도 꾸밈없이, 사내답고 차분한 한결같음으로 해내는 데에 어떤 조용한 인격의 힘이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["But, after the resolution he had made, of course he could have no such merits as these; and such a state of mind was nobody’s--nobody’s.", "그러나 그가 세운 결심 이후로는, 물론 그에게 이런 공이 있을 수 없었다. 그리고 그러한 정신 상태는 그 누구의 것도 아니었다—그 누구의 것도."]], [["Mr Gowan made it no concern of his whether it was nobody’s or somebody’s.", "가우언 씨는 그것이 그 누구의 것이든 누군가의 것이든 자기와는 상관없는 일로 여겼다."], ["He preserved his perfect serenity of manner on all occasions, as if the possibility of Clennam’s presuming to have debated the great question were too distant and ridiculous to be imagined.", "그는 어떤 경우에도 완벽하게 평온한 태도를 유지했는데, 마치 클레넘이 감히 그 중대한 문제를 논했으리라는 가능성이란 너무도 동떨어지고 우스꽝스러워 상상조차 할 수 없다는 듯했다."], ["He had always an affability to bestow on Clennam and an ease to treat him with, which might of itself (in the supposititious case of his not having taken that sagacious course) have been a very uncomfortable element in his state of mind.", "그는 늘 클레넘에게 베풀 상냥함과 그를 대할 여유를 지니고 있었으니, 그것만으로도 (만약 그가 그 현명한 길을 택하지 않았다는 가정 하에서) 그의 정신 상태에 매우 불편한 요소가 되었을 것이다."]], [["‘I quite regret you were not with us yesterday,’ said Mr Henry Gowan, calling on Clennam the next morning.", "\"어제 함께하지 못하셔서 정말 아쉬웠습니다.\" 헨리 가우언 씨가 이튿날 아침 클레넘을 찾아와 말했다."], ["‘We had an agreeable day up the river there.’", "\"거기 강 상류에서 유쾌한 하루를 보냈지요.\""]], [["So he had heard, Arthur said.", "그렇게 들었습니다, 하고 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘From your partner?’ returned Henry Gowan.", "\"동업자분한테서요?\" 헨리 가우언이 대꾸했다."], ["‘What a dear old fellow he is!’", "\"참으로 사랑스러운 노인장이시죠!\""]], [["‘I have a great regard for him.’", "\"제가 그분을 무척 존경합니다.\""]], [["‘By Jove, he is the finest creature!’ said Gowan.", "\"맙소사, 더없이 훌륭한 분이세요!\" 가우언이 말했다."], ["‘So fresh, so green, trusts in such wonderful things!’", "\"어쩜 그리 싱싱하고, 그리 푸르고, 그렇게 놀라운 것들을 믿으시는지요!\""]], [["Here was one of the many little rough points that had a tendency to grate on Clennam’s hearing.", "여기에 클레넘의 귀에 거슬리는 경향이 있는 그 숱한 작고 거친 가시 중 하나가 있었다."], ["He put it aside by merely repeating that he had a high regard for Mr Doyce.", "그는 도이스 씨를 깊이 존경한다고 그저 되풀이하며 그것을 흘려 넘겼다."]], [["‘He is charming!", "\"매력적인 분이세요!"], ["To see him mooning along to that time of life, laying down nothing by the way and picking up nothing by the way, is delightful.", "그 연세에 이르도록 그렇게 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오시면서, 길에서 아무것도 내려놓지 않고 길에서 아무것도 주워 담지 않는 걸 보면 얼마나 즐거운지요. 사람 마음을 따뜻하게 합니다."], ["It warms a man. So unspoilt, so simple, such a good soul!", "어찌나 때 묻지 않고, 어찌나 소박하며, 어찌나 좋은 영혼이신지요!"], ["Upon my life Mr Clennam, one feels desperately worldly and wicked in comparison with such an innocent creature.", "정말이지 클레넘 씨, 그런 순진한 분에 비하면 사람은 자기가 지독히 세속적이고 사악하게 느껴지지요."], ["I speak for myself, let me add, without including you.", "덧붙이자면, 이건 제 얘기일 뿐 당신을 포함하는 건 아닙니다."], ["You are genuine also.’", "당신도 진실한 분이시니까요.\""]], [["‘Thank you for the compliment,’ said Clennam, ill at ease; ‘you are too, I hope?’", "\"칭찬 고맙습니다.\" 클레넘이 거북하게 말했다. \"당신도 그러시길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘So so,’ rejoined the other.", "\"그저 그렇지요.\" 상대가 응했다."], ["‘To be candid with you, tolerably.", "\"솔직히 말씀드리자면, 웬만큼은요."], ["I am not a great impostor.", "저는 대단한 사기꾼은 아닙니다."], ["Buy one of my pictures, and I assure you, in confidence, it will not be worth the money.", "제 그림을 한 점 사 보세요, 그러면 단언컨대, 은밀히 말씀드리는데, 값어치만큼은 못 될 겁니다."], ["Buy one of another man’s--any great professor who beats me hollow--and the chances are that the more you give him, the more he’ll impose upon you.", "다른 사람 그림을 사 보세요—저를 완전히 능가하는 어떤 대가의 것이라도요—그러면 십중팔구 그에게 더 많이 줄수록, 그가 당신을 더 많이 속여 넘길 겁니다."], ["They all do it.’", "다들 그렇게 합니다.\""]], [["‘All painters?’", "\"화가들이 다요?\""]], [["‘Painters, writers, patriots, all the rest who have stands in the market.", "\"화가, 작가, 애국자, 시장에 좌판을 벌인 나머지 모두가요."], ["Give almost any man I know ten pounds, and he will impose upon you to a corresponding extent; a thousand pounds--to a corresponding extent; ten thousand pounds--to a corresponding extent.", "제가 아는 거의 누구에게든 십 파운드를 주어 보세요, 그러면 그는 그에 상응하는 만큼 당신을 속일 겁니다. 천 파운드면—그에 상응하는 만큼."], ["So great the success, so great the imposition.", "만 파운드면—그에 상응하는 만큼이요. 성공이 클수록, 사기도 큰 법이지요."], ["But what a capital world it is!’ cried Gowan with warm enthusiasm.", "하지만 이 얼마나 멋진 세상입니까!\" 가우언이 따뜻한 열정으로 외쳤다."], ["‘What a jolly, excellent, lovable world it is!’", "\"이 얼마나 유쾌하고, 훌륭하고, 사랑스러운 세상인지요!\""]], [["‘I had rather thought,’ said Clennam, ‘that the principle you mention was chiefly acted on by--’", "\"저는 오히려 생각했습니다만,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"당신이 말씀하시는 그 원리는 주로 다음과 같은 자들이 따르는 것이라고요—\""]], [["‘By the Barnacles?’ interrupted Gowan, laughing.", "\"배너클 일가가요?\" 가우언이 웃으며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘By the political gentlemen who condescend to keep the Circumlocution Office.’", "\"황송하게도 에두름국을 운영하시는 그 정치하는 신사분들이요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Don’t be hard upon the Barnacles,’ said Gowan, laughing afresh, ‘they are darling fellows!", "배너클 일가에게 너무 모질게 굴지 마세요.\" 가우언이 다시 웃으며 말했다."], ["Even poor little Clarence, the born idiot of the family, is the most agreeable and most endearing blockhead!", "\"그들은 사랑스러운 친구들입니다! 집안에서 타고난 바보인 가엾은 클래런스조차도 더없이 유쾌하고 더없이 사랑스러운 멍청이지요!"], ["And by Jupiter, with a kind of cleverness in him too that would astonish you!’", "게다가, 맙소사, 그에게는 당신을 깜짝 놀라게 할 일종의 영리함까지 있답니다!\""]], [["‘It would.", "\"그렇겠지요."], ["Very much,’ said Clennam, drily.", "무척이나요.\" 클레넘이 무뚝뚝하게 말했다."]], [["‘And after all,’ cried Gowan, with that characteristic balancing of his which reduced everything in the wide world to the same light weight, ‘though I can’t deny that the Circumlocution Office may ultimately shipwreck everybody and everything, still, that will probably not be in our time--and it’s a school for gentlemen.’", "\"게다가 결국,\" 가우언이, 이 너른 세상의 모든 것을 똑같이 가벼운 무게로 줄여 버리는 그 특유의 균형 잡기로 외쳤다. \"에두름국이 끝내 모든 사람과 모든 것을 난파시킬지 모른다는 건 부정할 수 없지만, 그래도 그게 우리 시대에 일어나지는 않을 테고—게다가 그건 신사를 위한 학교거든요.\""]], [["‘It’s a very dangerous, unsatisfactory, and expensive school to the people who pay to keep the pupils there, I am afraid,’ said Clennam, shaking his head.", "\"그건 학생들을 거기 두느라 돈을 내는 사람들에게는 무척 위험하고, 만족스럽지 못하고, 비싼 학교일까 봐 걱정입니다.\" 클레넘이 고개를 가로저으며 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You are a terrible fellow,’ returned Gowan, airily.", "당신은 무서운 분이로군요.\" 가우언이 가볍게 대꾸했다."], ["‘I can understand how you have frightened that little donkey, Clarence, the most estimable of moon-calves (I really love him) nearly out of his wits.", "\"당신이 그 작은 당나귀 클래런스를, 그 더없이 존경할 만한 얼간이를(전 그를 정말 사랑합니다) 거의 넋이 나가도록 어떻게 겁주었는지 알 만합니다."], ["But enough of him, and of all the rest of them.", "하지만 그 사람 이야기도, 나머지 모든 사람 이야기도 그만하지요."], ["I want to present you to my mother, Mr Clennam.", "저희 어머니께 당신을 소개하고 싶습니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["Pray do me the favour to give me the opportunity.’", "부디 그 기회를 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주세요.\""]], [["In nobody’s state of mind, there was nothing Clennam would have desired less, or would have been more at a loss how to avoid.", "그 누구의 것도 아닌 정신 상태에서, 클레넘이 그보다 덜 바라거나, 그것을 어떻게 피할지 더 막막해할 일은 없었다."]], [["‘My mother lives in a most primitive manner down in that dreary red-brick dungeon at Hampton Court,’ said Gowan.", "\"저희 어머니는 햄프턴 코트의 저 음산한 붉은 벽돌 감옥 같은 곳에서 더없이 원시적인 방식으로 사십니다.\""], ["‘If you would make your own appointment, suggest your own day for permitting me to take you there to dinner, you would be bored and she would be charmed.", "가우언이 말했다. \"당신이 직접 약속을 정하시고, 제가 당신을 그곳으로 저녁식사에 모셔갈 날을 직접 골라 주신다면, 당신은 지루해하시겠지만 어머니는 기뻐하실 겁니다."], ["Really that’s the state of the case.’", "정말이지 그게 사정입니다.\""]], [["What could Clennam say after this?", "이런 말을 듣고서 클레넘이 무어라 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["His retiring character included a great deal that was simple in the best sense, because unpractised and unused; and in his simplicity and modesty, he could only say that he was happy to place himself at Mr Gowan’s disposal.", "그의 내성적인 성격에는 가장 좋은 의미에서 소박한 면이 많았는데, 그것은 단련되지 않고 익숙하지 않은 데서 온 것이었다. 그리고 그 소박함과 겸손함 속에서 그는 가우언 씨의 뜻에 자신을 맡기게 되어 기쁘다고밖에 말할 수 없었다."], ["Accordingly he said it, and the day was fixed.", "그래서 그는 그렇게 말했고, 날짜가 정해졌다."], ["And a dreaded day it was on his part, and a very unwelcome day when it came and they went down to Hampton Court together.", "그리고 그것은 그로서는 두려운 날이었으며, 막상 그날이 와서 둘이 함께 햄프턴 코트로 내려갈 때는 더없이 달갑지 않은 날이었다."]], [["The venerable inhabitants of that venerable pile seemed, in those times, to be encamped there like a sort of civilised gipsies.", "그 유서 깊은 건물군의 유서 깊은 거주자들은 그 무렵 마치 일종의 문명화된 집시처럼 거기에 야영하고 있는 듯 보였다."], ["There was a temporary air about their establishments, as if they were going away the moment they could get anything better; there was also a dissatisfied air about themselves, as if they took it very ill that they had not already got something much better.", "그들의 거처에는 임시변통의 분위기가 감돌았는데, 마치 무언가 더 나은 것을 얻을 수 있는 순간 곧장 떠나갈 것 같았다. 또한 그들 자신에게는 불만에 찬 분위기가 있었는데, 마치 이미 훨씬 더 나은 무언가를 얻지 못한 것을 몹시 못마땅해하는 듯했다."], ["Genteel blinds and makeshifts were more or less observable as soon as their doors were opened; screens not half high enough, which made dining-rooms out of arched passages, and warded off obscure corners where footboys slept at nights with their heads among the knives and forks; curtains which called upon you to believe that they didn’t hide anything; panes of glass which requested you not to see them; many objects of various forms, feigning to have no connection with their guilty secret, a bed; disguised traps in walls, which were clearly coal-cellars; affectations of no thoroughfares, which were evidently doors to little kitchens.", "점잖은 가림막과 임시변통들이 문이 열리자마자 어느 정도 눈에 띄었다. 절반도 채 안 되게 낮아서 아치형 통로를 식당으로 만들어 버리는 칸막이며, 시동(侍童)들이 밤에 나이프와 포크 사이에 머리를 처박고 잠드는 어둑한 구석을 가리는 가림막이며, 아무것도 감추지 않는다고 믿어 달라고 청하는 커튼이며, 자기를 보지 말아 달라고 부탁하는 유리창이며, 여러 형태로 자신들의 떳떳치 못한 비밀, 곧 침대와는 아무 관련이 없는 척하는 갖가지 물건들이며, 분명 석탄 창고인데 벽에 위장한 함정 문이며, 명백히 작은 부엌으로 통하는 문인데 막다른 길인 척하는 것들이 그것이었다."], ["Mental reservations and artful mysteries grew out of these things.", "마음속 유보와 교묘한 비밀들이 이런 것들에서 자라났다."], ["Callers looking steadily into the eyes of their receivers, pretended not to smell cooking three feet off; people, confronting closets accidentally left open, pretended not to see bottles; visitors with their heads against a partition of thin canvas, and a page and a young female at high words on the other side, made believe to be sitting in a primeval silence.", "방문객들은 맞이하는 이의 눈을 빤히 바라보며 석 자 떨어진 곳에서 나는 요리 냄새를 못 맡는 척했고, 우연히 열려 있는 벽장과 마주친 사람들은 술병을 못 본 척했으며, 얇은 천 칸막이에 머리를 기대고 있고 그 너머에서 시동과 젊은 여자가 큰소리로 다투고 있는 방문객들은 태곳적의 침묵 속에 앉아 있는 척했다."], ["There was no end to the small social accommodation-bills of this nature which the gipsies of gentility were constantly drawing upon, and accepting for, one another.", "이런 종류의, 점잖은 집시들이 서로에게 끊임없이 발행하고 또 받아 주는 사소한 사교적 융통어음에는 끝이 없었다."]], [["Some of these Bohemians were of an irritable temperament, as constantly soured and vexed by two mental trials: the first, the consciousness that they had never got enough out of the public; the second, the consciousness that the public were admitted into the building.", "이 보헤미안들 가운데 일부는 짜증을 잘 내는 기질이었는데, 두 가지 정신적 시련으로 끊임없이 마음이 시고 짜증이 나 있었다. 첫째는 대중에게서 결코 충분히 뜯어내지 못했다는 자각이었고, 둘째는 대중이 이 건물 안으로 들여보내진다는 자각이었다."], ["Under the latter great wrong, a few suffered dreadfully--particularly on Sundays, when they had for some time expected the earth to open and swallow the public up; but which desirable event had not yet occurred, in consequence of some reprehensible laxity in the arrangements of the Universe.", "후자의 큰 부당함 아래서 몇몇은 끔찍하게 괴로워했다—특히 일요일이면, 그들은 한동안 땅이 갈라져 대중을 삼켜 버리기를 기대해 왔는데, 우주의 운영에 있어 어떤 비난받아 마땅한 해이함 탓에 그 바람직한 사건은 아직 일어나지 않았던 것이다."]], [["Mrs Gowan’s door was attended by a family servant of several years’ standing, who had his own crow to pluck with the public concerning a situation in the Post-Office which he had been for some time expecting, and to which he was not yet appointed.", "가우언 부인의 집 문은 여러 해 근무한 집안 하인이 맡고 있었는데, 그는 한동안 고대해 온 우체국 자리를 두고 대중에게 따질 자기 나름의 원한이 있었으나 아직 그 자리에 임명되지 못했다."], ["He perfectly knew that the public could never have got him in, but he grimly gratified himself with the idea that the public kept him out.", "그는 대중이 결코 자기를 거기 넣어 줄 수 없었으리라는 것을 완벽히 알고 있었지만, 대중이 자기를 막고 있다는 생각으로 음울하게 스스로를 만족시켰다."], ["Under the influence of this injury (and perhaps of some little straitness and irregularity in the matter of wages), he had grown neglectful of his person and morose in mind; and now beholding in Clennam one of the degraded body of his oppressors, received him with ignominy.", "이 억울함의 영향(그리고 어쩌면 임금 문제에서의 약간의 궁핍과 불규칙함의 영향)으로, 그는 몸가짐에 무심해지고 마음이 뚱해졌다. 그리고 이제 클레넘에게서 자기를 억압하는 그 천한 무리 중 하나를 알아보고는 그를 모욕적으로 맞이했다."]], [["Mrs Gowan, however, received him with condescension. He found her a courtly old lady, formerly a Beauty, and still sufficiently well-favoured to have dispensed with the powder on her nose and a certain impossible bloom under each eye. She was a little lofty with him; so was another old lady, dark-browed and high-nosed, and who must have had something real about her or she could not have existed, but it was certainly not her hair or her teeth or her figure or her complexion; so was a grey old gentleman of dignified and sullen appearance; both of whom had come to dinner. But, as they had all been in the British Embassy way in sundry parts of the earth, and as a British Embassy cannot better establish a character with the Circumlocution Office than by treating its compatriots with illimitable contempt (else it would become like the Embassies of other countries), Clennam felt that on the whole they let him off lightly.", "그러나 가우언 부인은 그를 거들먹거리며 맞이했다. 그가 보기에 그녀는 한때 미인이었던 궁정풍의 노부인으로, 콧등의 분과 두 눈 밑의 어떤 있을 수 없는 홍조를 생략해도 될 만큼 여전히 충분히 용모가 고왔다. 그녀는 그에게 약간 거만했다. 짙은 눈썹에 코가 높은 또 다른 노부인도 그러했는데, 그녀에게는 무언가 진짜인 것이 있었음이 분명했다. 안 그랬다면 존재할 수도 없었을 테니까. 하지만 그것은 분명 그녀의 머리카락도, 치아도, 몸매도, 안색도 아니었다. 위엄 있고 뚱한 외모의 백발 노신사도 그러했다. 두 사람 다 만찬에 와 있었다. 그러나 그들 모두 지구의 여러 곳에서 영국 대사관 일을 해 왔고, 영국 대사관이란 자기 동포를 한없는 경멸로 대하는 것보다 더 확실히 에두름국에서 그 평판을 세우는 길이 없으므로(안 그러면 다른 나라 대사관들처럼 되어 버릴 테니까), 클레넘은 대체로 그들이 자기를 가볍게 봐주었다고 느꼈다."]], [["The dignified old gentleman turned out to be Lord Lancaster Stiltstalking, who had been maintained by the Circumlocution Office for many years as a representative of the Britannic Majesty abroad.", "그 위엄 있는 노신사는 랭커스터 스틸트스토킹 경으로 밝혀졌는데, 그는 여러 해 동안 에두름국의 비용으로 해외에서 영국 폐하의 대리인 노릇을 해 온 사람이었다."], ["This noble Refrigerator had iced several European courts in his time, and had done it with such complete success that the very name of Englishman yet struck cold to the stomachs of foreigners who had the distinguished honour of remembering him at a distance of a quarter of a century.", "이 고귀한 냉장고는 당대에 여러 유럽 궁정을 얼려 놓았고, 어찌나 완벽한 성공을 거두었던지, 사반세기의 거리를 두고 그를 기억하는 영광을 누린 외국인들의 위장에 영국인이라는 이름 자체가 여전히 냉기를 끼얹을 정도였다."]], [["He was now in retirement, and hence (in a ponderous white cravat, like a stiff snow-drift) was so obliging as to shade the dinner.", "그는 이제 은퇴해 있었고, 따라서 (뻣뻣한 눈더미 같은 묵직한 흰 크라바트를 매고) 친절하게도 만찬에 그늘을 드리워 주었다."], ["There was a whisper of the pervading Bohemian character in the nomadic nature of the service and its curious races of plates and dishes; but the noble Refrigerator, infinitely better than plate or porcelain, made it superb.", "유랑하는 듯한 시중과 그 묘하게 잡다한 종류의 접시며 그릇에는 만연한 보헤미안적 기질의 기미가 속삭이고 있었다. 그러나 은쟁반이나 도자기보다 한없이 나은 그 고귀한 냉장고가 그것을 더없이 훌륭하게 만들어 주었다."], ["He shaded the dinner, cooled the wines, chilled the gravy, and blighted the vegetables.", "그는 만찬에 그늘을 드리우고, 포도주를 식히고, 그레이비를 차갑게 만들고, 채소를 시들게 했다."]], [["There was only one other person in the room: a microscopically small footboy, who waited on the malevolent man who hadn’t got into the Post-Office.", "방 안에는 단 한 사람이 더 있었다. 현미경으로 봐야 할 만큼 작은 시동이었는데, 우체국에 들어가지 못한 그 악의에 찬 사내의 시중을 들고 있었다."], ["Even this youth, if his jacket could have been unbuttoned and his heart laid bare, would have been seen, as a distant adherent of the Barnacle family, already to aspire to a situation under Government.", "이 젊은이조차도, 그의 윗도리 단추를 풀고 그 마음을 드러내 보일 수 있었다면, 배너클 가문의 먼 추종자로서, 이미 정부 산하의 자리를 열망하고 있음이 드러났을 것이다."]], [["Mrs Gowan with a gentle melancholy upon her, occasioned by her son’s being reduced to court the swinish public as a follower of the low Arts, instead of asserting his birthright and putting a ring through its nose as an acknowledged Barnacle, headed the conversation at dinner on the evil days.", "가우언 부인은, 아들이 인정받는 배너클로서 그 타고난 권리를 주장하며 대중의 코에 코뚜레를 꿰는 대신, 천한 예술의 추종자로서 그 돼지 같은 대중에게 구애하는 신세로 전락한 데서 비롯된 부드러운 우수를 띤 채, 만찬 자리에서 이 험한 시절에 관한 대화를 이끌었다."], ["It was then that Clennam learned for the first time what little pivots this great world goes round upon.", "클레넘이 이 거대한 세상이 얼마나 작은 굴대들 위에서 돌아가는지를 처음으로 알게 된 것이 바로 그때였다."]], [["‘If John Barnacle,’ said Mrs Gowan, after the degeneracy of the times had been fully ascertained, ‘if John Barnacle had but abandoned his most unfortunate idea of conciliating the mob, all would have been well, and I think the country would have been preserved.’", "\"존 배너클이,\" 시대의 타락이 충분히 확인된 뒤 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"존 배너클이 군중을 회유하겠다는 그 더없이 불운한 생각만 버렸더라면, 만사가 잘되었을 것이고, 나라는 보전되었으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["The old lady with the high nose assented; but added that if Augustus Stiltstalking had in a general way ordered the cavalry out with instructions to charge, she thought the country would have been preserved.", "코가 높은 노부인이 동의했다. 그러나 만약 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹이 일반적으로 기병대에 돌격 명령을 내려 출동시켰더라면, 나라가 보전되었으리라 생각한다고 덧붙였다."]], [["The noble Refrigerator assented; but added that if William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, when they came over to one another and formed their ever-memorable coalition, had boldly muzzled the newspapers, and rendered it penal for any Editor-person to presume to discuss the conduct of any appointed authority abroad or at home, he thought the country would have been preserved.", "고귀한 냉장고가 동의했다. 그러나 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹이 서로에게 넘어가 그 길이 기억될 연합을 결성했을 때, 대담하게 신문에 재갈을 물리고, 어떤 편집자라는 자가 감히 국내외 임명된 권력의 행위를 논하는 것을 처벌 대상으로 삼았더라면, 나라가 보전되었으리라 생각한다고 덧붙였다."]], [["It was agreed that the country (another word for the Barnacles and Stiltstalkings) wanted preserving, but how it came to want preserving was not so clear.", "나라(배너클 일가와 스틸트스토킹 일가를 가리키는 또 다른 말)가 보전될 필요가 있다는 데는 의견이 일치했으나, 어쩌다 보전이 필요하게 되었는지는 그리 분명치 않았다."], ["It was only clear that the question was all about John Barnacle, Augustus Stiltstalking, William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, Tom, Dick, or Harry Barnacle or Stiltstalking, because there was nobody else but mob.", "다만 분명한 것은, 그 문제가 온통 존 배너클, 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹, 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹, 톰, 딕, 또는 해리 배너클이나 스틸트스토킹에 관한 것일 뿐이라는 점이었다. 왜냐하면 그 밖에는 군중 말고 아무도 없었으니까."], ["And this was the feature of the conversation which impressed Clennam, as a man not used to it, very disagreeably: making him doubt if it were quite right to sit there, silently hearing a great nation narrowed to such little bounds.", "그리고 이것이 그 대화에서, 이런 데 익숙지 않은 사람인 클레넘에게 매우 거북하게 와닿은 특징이었다. 거기 앉아 거대한 한 나라가 그토록 작은 테두리로 좁혀지는 것을 잠자코 듣고 있는 것이 과연 옳은 일인지 의심스럽게 만들었던 것이다."], ["Remembering, however, that in the Parliamentary debates, whether on the life of that nation’s body or the life of its soul, the question was usually all about and between John Barnacle, Augustus Stiltstalking, William Barnacle and Tudor Stiltstalking, Tom, Dick, or Harry Barnacle or Stiltstalking, and nobody else; he said nothing on the part of mob, bethinking himself that mob was used to it.", "그러나 의회의 토론에서도, 그것이 그 나라 몸의 생명에 관한 것이든 영혼의 생명에 관한 것이든, 그 문제가 대개 온통 존 배너클, 오거스터스 스틸트스토킹, 윌리엄 배너클과 튜더 스틸트스토킹, 톰, 딕, 또는 해리 배너클이나 스틸트스토킹에 관한 것이고 그들 사이의 일일 뿐 아무도 없었음을 떠올리며, 그는 군중을 대변하여 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 군중은 이런 데 익숙하다고 스스로 생각하면서."]], [["Mr Henry Gowan seemed to have a malicious pleasure in playing off the three talkers against each other, and in seeing Clennam startled by what they said.", "헨리 가우언 씨는 그 세 명의 화자를 서로 맞붙여 놓고, 그들이 하는 말에 클레넘이 놀라는 것을 보는 데서 짓궂은 즐거움을 느끼는 듯했다."], ["Having as supreme a contempt for the class that had thrown him off as for the class that had not taken him on, he had no personal disquiet in anything that passed.", "그는 자기를 내쳐 버린 계급에 대해서도, 자기를 받아들이지 않은 계급에 대해서도 한결같이 지극한 경멸을 품고 있었으므로, 오가는 어떤 말에도 개인적으로 동요가 없었다."], ["His healthy state of mind appeared even to derive a gratification from Clennam’s position of embarrassment and isolation among the good company; and if Clennam had been in that condition with which Nobody was incessantly contending, he would have suspected it, and would have struggled with the suspicion as a meanness, even while he sat at the table.", "그의 건강한 정신 상태는, 그 훌륭한 사람들 가운데서 클레넘이 처한 당혹과 고립의 처지에서 오히려 만족을 얻는 듯하기까지 했다. 만약 클레넘이 '그 누구'가 끊임없이 싸우고 있던 그 상태에 있었더라면, 그는 그것을 눈치챘을 것이고, 식탁에 앉아 있는 동안에도 그러한 의심을 비열한 것으로 여겨 그와 씨름했을 것이다."]], [["In the course of a couple of hours the noble Refrigerator, at no time less than a hundred years behind the period, got about five centuries in arrears, and delivered solemn political oracles appropriate to that epoch.", "두어 시간이 지나는 동안 그 고귀한 냉장고는, 언제나 그 시대보다 백 년은 뒤처져 있던 사람이, 어느덧 오백 년쯤 뒤처지게 되어, 그 시대에 어울리는 엄숙한 정치적 신탁을 늘어놓았다."], ["He finished by freezing a cup of tea for his own drinking, and retiring at his lowest temperature.", "그는 마침내 자기가 마실 차 한 잔을 얼려 놓고는, 가장 낮은 온도로 물러갔다."]], [["Then Mrs Gowan, who had been accustomed in her days of a vacant arm-chair beside her to which to summon state to retain her devoted slaves, one by one, for short audiences as marks of her especial favour, invited Clennam with a turn of her fan to approach the presence.", "그러자, 자기 곁에 비어 있는 안락의자를 두고 거기에 위엄을 불러 앉히던 시절에, 헌신적인 노예들을 하나씩 짧은 알현으로 불러들여 특별한 총애의 표시로 삼는 데 익숙해 있던 가우언 부인이, 부채를 한 번 돌려 클레넘에게 어전(御前)으로 다가오라고 청했다."], ["He obeyed, and took the tripod recently vacated by Lord Lancaster Stiltstalking.", "그는 따랐고, 방금 랭커스터 스틸트스토킹 경이 비운 그 삼각의자에 앉았다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘apart from the happiness I have in becoming known to you, though in this odiously inconvenient place--a mere barrack--there is a subject on which I am dying to speak to you.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["It is the subject in connection with which my son first had, I believe, the pleasure of cultivating your acquaintance.’", "\"이 가증스럽도록 불편한 곳—그저 막사일 뿐인 데서나마—당신을 알게 되어 기쁜 마음과는 별개로, 제가 당신께 몹시 이야기하고 싶은 주제가 하나 있습니다. 제 아들이 처음으로 당신과 교분을 쌓는 즐거움을 누리게 된 것도, 제 생각엔 바로 그 주제와 관련해서였지요.\""]], [["Clennam inclined his head, as a generally suitable reply to what he did not yet quite understand.", "클레넘은, 아직 완전히 이해하지 못한 것에 대한 대체로 적절한 대답으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘First,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘now, is she really pretty?’", "\"우선,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"그래, 그 애가 정말 예쁜가요?\""]], [["In nobody’s difficulties, he would have found it very difficult to answer; very difficult indeed to smile, and say ‘Who?’", "그 누구의 곤경에 처해서도 그는 대답하기가 무척 어려웠을 것이다. 미소를 지으며 \"누구 말씀이십니까?\" 하고 말하기란 정말이지 무척 어려웠을 것이다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You know!’ she returned.", "아시잖아요!\" 그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["‘This flame of Henry’s.", "\"헨리의 그 불꽃 말이에요."], ["This unfortunate fancy.", "그 불운한 연정 말입니다."], ["There!", "거 봐요!"], ["If it is a point of honour that I should originate the name--Miss Mickles--Miggles.’", "제가 그 이름을 먼저 꺼내는 것이 명예가 걸린 문제라면—미클스 양—미글스요.\""]], [["‘Miss Meagles,’ said Clennam, ‘is very beautiful.’", "\"미글스 양은,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"매우 아름답습니다.\""]], [["‘Men are so often mistaken on those points,’ returned Mrs Gowan, shaking her head, ‘that I candidly confess to you I feel anything but sure of it, even now; though it is something to have Henry corroborated with so much gravity and emphasis. He picked the people up at Rome, I think?’", "\"남자들은 그런 점에서 어찌나 자주 잘못 보는지요.\" 가우언 부인이 고개를 가로저으며 대꾸했다. \"그래서 솔직히 당신께 고백하건대, 저는 지금도 도무지 확신이 서질 않습니다. 헨리가 그토록 진지하고 힘주어 뒷받침해 주는 것만으로도 다행이긴 합니다만. 그 애가 로마에서 그 사람들을 주워 온 거지요, 아마?\""]], [["The phrase would have given nobody mortal offence.", "그 표현은 어느 누구에게도 끔찍한 모욕이 되지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Clennam replied, ‘Excuse me, I doubt if I understand your expression.’", "클레넘이 대답했다. \"죄송합니다만, 그 표현을 제가 제대로 이해했는지 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Picked the people up,’ said Mrs Gowan, tapping the sticks of her closed fan (a large green one, which she used as a hand-screen) on her little table.", "\"그 사람들을 주워 왔다고요.\" 가우언 부인이 (손가리개로 쓰는 커다란 초록 부채인) 접은 부채의 살을 자기 작은 탁자에 톡톡 두드리며 말했다."], ["‘Came upon them.", "\"마주쳤다는 거지요."], ["Found them out.", "찾아냈다고요."], ["Stumbled against them.’", "우연히 부딪쳤다는 거예요.\""]], [["‘The people?’", "\"그 사람들이요?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["The Miggles people.’", "그 미글스네 사람들 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I really cannot say,’ said Clennam, ‘where my friend Mr Meagles first presented Mr Henry Gowan to his daughter.’", "\"제 친구 미글스 씨가 어디서 처음으로 헨리 가우언 씨를 자기 딸에게 소개했는지는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"정말 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘I am pretty sure he picked her up at Rome; but never mind where--somewhere.", "\"틀림없이 로마에서 그 애를 주워 왔을 거예요. 하지만 어디든 상관없지요—어딘가에서겠죠."], ["Now (this is entirely between ourselves), is she very plebeian?’", "자 그럼(이건 전적으로 우리끼리만의 얘깁니다), 그 애가 아주 천민 출신인가요?\""]], [["‘Really, ma’am,’ returned Clennam, ‘I am so undoubtedly plebeian myself, that I do not feel qualified to judge.’", "\"정말이지, 부인.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"저 자신이 의심할 바 없이 천민이라서, 그것을 판단할 자격이 있다고 느껴지지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Very neat!’ said Mrs Gowan, coolly unfurling her screen.", "\"아주 깔끔하군요!\" 가우언 부인이 가리개를 차갑게 펼치며 말했다."], ["‘Very happy!", "\"아주 능란해요!"], ["From which I infer that you secretly think her manner equal to her looks?’", "그 말에서 저는, 당신이 속으로는 그 애의 태도가 그 외모에 걸맞다고 생각한다고 추론합니다만?\""]], [["Clennam, after a moment’s stiffness, bowed.", "클레넘은 한순간 뻣뻣해진 뒤 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘That’s comforting, and I hope you may be right.", "\"그것 참 위안이 되는군요. 당신 말이 맞기를 바랍니다."], ["Did Henry tell me you had travelled with them?’", "헨리가 당신더러 그 사람들과 함께 여행했다고 하던가요?\""]], [["‘I travelled with my friend Mr Meagles, and his wife and daughter, during some months.’ (Nobody’s heart might have been wrung by the remembrance.)", "\"제 친구 미글스 씨와, 그분 부인과 따님과 함께 몇 달 동안 여행했습니다.\" (그 누구의 가슴도 그 기억에 쥐어짜였을지 모른다.)"]], [["‘Really comforting, because you must have had a large experience of them.", "\"정말 위안이 되네요. 그렇다면 당신은 그들에 대해 폭넓은 경험을 했겠군요."], ["You see, Mr Clennam, this thing has been going on for a long time, and I find no improvement in it.", "보세요, 클레넘 씨, 이 일이 오래도록 계속되고 있는데, 저는 거기서 아무런 진전도 보지 못하겠어요."], ["Therefore to have the opportunity of speaking to one so well informed about it as yourself, is an immense relief to me.", "그러니 그 일에 관해 당신처럼 잘 아는 분께 이야기할 기회를 얻는 것은 제게 엄청난 위안입니다."], ["Quite a boon.", "정말 큰 은혜예요."], ["Quite a blessing, I am sure.’", "정말 축복이고말고요.\""]], [["‘Pardon me,’ returned Clennam, ‘but I am not in Mr Henry Gowan’s confidence.", "\"용서하십시오.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다."], ["I am far from being so well informed as you suppose me to be.", "\"하지만 저는 헨리 가우언 씨의 속내를 아는 사람이 아닙니다."], ["Your mistake makes my position a very delicate one.", "부인이 생각하시는 만큼 잘 알고 있는 것과는 거리가 멉니다."], ["No word on this topic has ever passed between Mr Henry Gowan and myself.’", "부인의 오해가 제 처지를 매우 곤란하게 만드는군요. 이 주제에 관해 헨리 가우언 씨와 저 사이에 단 한마디도 오간 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["Mrs Gowan glanced at the other end of the room, where her son was playing ecarte on a sofa, with the old lady who was for a charge of cavalry.", "가우언 부인은 방 저쪽 끝을 흘끗 보았다. 거기서는 그녀의 아들이 소파에 앉아, 기병대 돌격을 주장하던 그 노부인과 에카르테 놀이를 하고 있었다."]], [["‘Not in his confidence?", "\"그 애의 속내를 모른다고요? 그렇죠.\""], ["No,’ said Mrs Gowan.", "가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["‘No word has passed between you?", "\"두 분 사이에 한마디도 오가지 않았다고요? 그렇겠죠."], ["No.", "그건 짐작이 갑니다."], ["That I can imagine.", "하지만 말로 표현되지 않는 속내라는 것도 있지요, 클레넘 씨. 그리고 당신이 그 사람들 틈에서 그토록 친밀하게 지내 왔으니, 지금 이 경우에 그런 종류의 속내가 존재한다는 것을 저는 의심할 수가 없어요."], ["But there are unexpressed confidences, Mr Clennam; and as you have been together intimately among these people, I cannot doubt that a confidence of that sort exists in the present case.", "어쩌면 들으셨을지도 모르겠지만, 저는 헨리가 어떤 일에 손을 댔다는 것에—저런!\" 어깨를 으쓱하며."], ["Perhaps you have heard that I have suffered the keenest distress of mind from Henry’s having taken to a pursuit which--well!’ shrugging her shoulders, ‘a very respectable pursuit, I dare say, and some artists are, as artists, quite superior persons; still, we never yet in our family have gone beyond an Amateur, and it is a pardonable weakness to feel a little--’", "\"꽤 점잖은 일이긴 하겠지요, 그리고 어떤 예술가들은 예술가로서 꽤 우월한 사람들이긴 합니다. 그래도 우리 집안에서는 아직껏 아마추어 이상으로 나아간 적이 없으니, 조금쯤 마음 쓰이는 것도 용서받을 만한 약점이지요—\""]], [["As Mrs Gowan broke off to heave a sigh, Clennam, however resolute to be magnanimous, could not keep down the thought that there was mighty little danger of the family’s ever going beyond an Amateur, even as it was.", "가우언 부인이 한숨을 쉬느라 말을 끊는 동안, 클레넘은 아무리 너그러워지려고 굳게 마음먹고 있어도, 이 집안이 지금 이대로라도 아마추어 이상으로 나아갈 위험은 지극히 적다는 생각을 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["‘Henry,’ the mother resumed, ‘is self-willed and resolute; and as these people naturally strain every nerve to catch him, I can entertain very little hope, Mr Clennam, that the thing will be broken off. I apprehend the girl’s fortune will be very small; Henry might have done much better; there is scarcely anything to compensate for the connection: still, he acts for himself; and if I find no improvement within a short time, I see no other course than to resign myself and make the best of these people. I am infinitely obliged to you for what you have told me.’", "\"헨리는,\" 어머니가 다시 말을 이었다. \"고집스럽고 단호하답니다. 그리고 이 사람들이 자연히 온갖 수를 다 써서 그 애를 낚으려 드니, 클레넘 씨, 저는 이 일이 깨어지리라는 희망을 거의 품을 수가 없어요. 그 처녀의 재산은 매우 적을 듯합니다. 헨리라면 훨씬 더 나은 혼처를 구할 수 있었을 텐데요. 그런 연줄을 보상해 줄 만한 것이 거의 없습니다. 그래도 그 애는 제 뜻대로 하고 있으니, 머지않아 아무 진전이 없다면, 저로서는 체념하고 이 사람들을 받아들여 최선을 다하는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없겠지요. 당신이 제게 말씀해 주신 것에 대해 무한히 감사드립니다.\""]], [["As she shrugged her shoulders, Clennam stiffly bowed again.", "그녀가 어깨를 으쓱하자, 클레넘은 다시 뻣뻣하게 고개를 숙였다."], ["With an uneasy flush upon his face, and hesitation in his manner, he then said in a still lower tone than he had adopted yet:", "그러고는 얼굴에 거북한 홍조를 띠고 머뭇거리는 태도로, 지금까지 취해 온 것보다 한층 더 낮은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘Mrs Gowan, I scarcely know how to acquit myself of what I feel to be a duty, and yet I must ask you for your kind consideration in attempting to discharge it.", "\"가우언 부인, 제가 의무라고 느끼는 일을 어떻게 다해야 할지 도무지 모르겠습니다. 그래도 그것을 이행하려 애쓰는 데 부인의 너그러운 헤아림을 청하지 않을 수 없습니다."], ["A misconception on your part, a very great misconception if I may venture to call it so, seems to require setting right.", "부인 편에서의 어떤 오해가, 감히 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 매우 큰 오해가 바로잡힐 필요가 있는 듯합니다."], ["You have supposed Mr Meagles and his family to strain every nerve, I think you said--’", "부인은 미글스 씨와 그 가족이 온갖 수를 다 쓴다고, 그렇게 말씀하신 것 같은데요—\""]], [["‘Every nerve,’ repeated Mrs Gowan, looking at him in calm obstinacy, with her green fan between her face and the fire.", "\"온갖 수를요.\" 가우언 부인이, 얼굴과 난롯불 사이에 초록 부채를 든 채, 차분한 고집으로 그를 바라보며 되풀이했다."]], [["‘To secure Mr Henry Gowan?’", "\"헨리 가우언 씨를 차지하려고요?\""]], [["The lady placidly assented.", "부인이 차분하게 동의했다."]], [["‘Now that is so far,’ said Arthur, ‘from being the case, that I know Mr Meagles to be unhappy in this matter; and to have interposed all reasonable obstacles with the hope of putting an end to it.’", "\"그런데 그건 결코 사실이 아닙니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"오히려 저는 미글스 씨가 이 일로 불행해하고 있으며, 이를 끝내고자 하는 바람에서 온갖 합당한 장애물을 끼워 놓았다는 것을 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["Mrs Gowan shut up her great green fan, tapped him on the arm with it, and tapped her smiling lips.", "가우언 부인은 자기 커다란 초록 부채를 접어, 그것으로 그의 팔을 톡 치고는, 미소 짓는 자기 입술을 톡 쳤다."], ["‘Why, of course,’ said she.", "\"그야, 물론이지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Just what I mean.’", "\"바로 제 말이 그거예요.\""]], [["Arthur watched her face for some explanation of what she did mean.", "아서는 그녀가 무슨 말을 하려는 건지 설명을 들으려고 그녀의 얼굴을 살폈다."]], [["‘Are you really serious, Mr Clennam?", "\"정말 진심이세요, 클레넘 씨?"], ["Don’t you see?’", "모르시겠어요?\""]], [["Arthur did not see; and said so.", "아서는 모르겠다고, 그렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Why, don’t I know my son, and don’t I know that this is exactly the way to hold him?’ said Mrs Gowan, contemptuously; ‘and do not these Miggles people know it, at least as well as I?", "\"그야, 제가 제 아들을 모르겠어요? 그리고 그게 바로 그 애를 붙들어 두는 방법이라는 걸 제가 모르겠어요?\""], ["Oh, shrewd people, Mr Clennam: evidently people of business!", "가우언 부인이 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그리고 이 미글스네 사람들이 그걸, 적어도 저만큼은 모르겠어요?"], ["I believe Miggles belonged to a Bank.", "오, 약삭빠른 사람들이지요, 클레넘 씨. 보아하니 장사꾼들이에요!"], ["It ought to have been a very profitable Bank, if he had much to do with its management.", "미글스는 어느 은행에 몸담았던 것 같던데요. 그가 그 경영에 많이 관여했더라면, 무척 이문이 많은 은행이었겠지요."], ["This is very well done, indeed.’", "이거 정말이지 아주 잘 꾸민 일이에요.\""]], [["‘I beg and entreat you, ma’am--’ Arthur interposed.", "\"제발, 간청드립니다, 부인—\" 아서가 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Oh, Mr Clennam, can you really be so credulous?’", "\"오, 클레넘 씨, 정말 그렇게 잘 속아 넘어가실 수가 있나요?\""]], [["It made such a painful impression upon him to hear her talking in this haughty tone, and to see her patting her contemptuous lips with her fan, that he said very earnestly, ‘Believe me, ma’am, this is unjust, a perfectly groundless suspicion.’", "그녀가 이런 거만한 어조로 말하는 것을 듣고, 경멸 어린 입술을 부채로 톡톡 두드리는 것을 보는 일이 어찌나 고통스러운 인상을 주었던지, 그는 매우 간곡하게 말했다. \"믿어 주십시오, 부인, 이건 부당한, 완전히 근거 없는 의심입니다.\""]], [["‘Suspicion?’ repeated Mrs Gowan.", "\"의심이라고요?\" 가우언 부인이 되풀이했다."], ["‘Not suspicion, Mr Clennam, Certainty.", "\"의심이 아니에요, 클레넘 씨, 확신입니다. 정말이지 아주 영리하게 꾸민 일이고, 당신을 완전히 속여 넘긴 모양이네요.\""], ["It is very knowingly done indeed, and seems to have taken _you_ in completely.’ She laughed; and again sat tapping her lips with her fan, and tossing her head, as if she added, ‘Don’t tell me.", "그녀는 웃었다. 그러고는 다시 부채로 입술을 톡톡 두드리며 고개를 까딱이고 앉아 있었는데, 마치 이렇게 덧붙이는 듯했다."], ["I know such people will do anything for the honour of such an alliance.’", "\"내게 그런 말 마세요. 그런 사람들은 그런 혼인의 명예를 위해서라면 무슨 짓이든 하리라는 걸 나는 압니다.\""]], [["At this opportune moment, the cards were thrown up, and Mr Henry Gowan came across the room saying, ‘Mother, if you can spare Mr Clennam for this time, we have a long way to go, and it’s getting late.’ Mr Clennam thereupon rose, as he had no choice but to do; and Mrs Gowan showed him, to the last, the same look and the same tapped contemptuous lips.", "이 알맞은 순간에 카드가 던져졌고, 헨리 가우언 씨가 방을 가로질러 오며 말했다. \"어머니, 이번엔 클레넘 씨를 놓아주실 수 있으시면, 저희가 갈 길이 멀고 시간도 늦어 가고 있어서요.\" 그러자 클레넘은 달리 도리가 없었으므로 일어섰다. 그리고 가우언 부인은 끝까지 그에게 똑같은 눈빛과, 똑같이 톡톡 두드린 경멸 어린 입술을 보여 주었다."]], [["‘You have had a portentously long audience of my mother,’ said Gowan, as the door closed upon them.", "\"어머니와 어마어마하게 긴 알현을 하셨군요.\" 문이 그들 뒤에서 닫히자 가우언이 말했다."], ["‘I fervently hope she has not bored you?’", "\"부디 어머니가 당신을 지루하게 하지 않으셨기를 간절히 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Clennam.", "\"전혀요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["They had a little open phaeton for the journey, and were soon in it on the road home. Gowan, driving, lighted a cigar; Clennam declined one. Do what he would, he fell into such a mood of abstraction that Gowan said again, ‘I am very much afraid my mother has bored you?’ To which he roused himself to answer, ‘Not at all!’ and soon relapsed again.", "여행을 위해 그들에게는 작은 무개 사륜마차가 있었고, 곧 그것에 올라 집으로 가는 길에 올랐다. 가우언은 마차를 몰면서 시가에 불을 붙였다. 클레넘은 한 대 권하는 것을 사양했다. 그는 아무리 애를 써도 그런 골똘한 사색의 기분에 빠져들어, 가우언이 다시 말했다. \"아무래도 어머니가 당신을 지루하게 하신 게 무척 걱정스럽군요?\" 이에 그는 정신을 차리고 대답했다. \"전혀 아닙니다!\" 그러고는 곧 다시 사색에 빠져들었다."]], [["In that state of mind which rendered nobody uneasy, his thoughtfulness would have turned principally on the man at his side.", "그 누구도 불안하게 하지 않는 그 정신 상태에서라면, 그의 골똘한 생각은 주로 자기 곁에 있는 사내에게로 향했을 것이다. 그는 그가 발뒤꿈치로 돌을 파헤치던 것을 처음 본 그 아침을 떠올렸을 것이고, 스스로에게 물었을 것이다."], ["He would have thought of the morning when he first saw him rooting out the stones with his heel, and would have asked himself, ‘Does he jerk me out of the path in the same careless, cruel way?’ He would have thought, had this introduction to his mother been brought about by him because he knew what she would say, and that he could thus place his position before a rival and loftily warn him off, without himself reposing a word of confidence in him?", "\"저 사람이 나도 저렇게 무심하고 잔인하게 길에서 걷어차 내치는 것일까?\" 그는 생각했을 것이다."], ["He would have thought, even if there were no such design as that, had he brought him there to play with his repressed emotions, and torment him?", "그가 어머니가 무슨 말을 할지 알았기에, 그리하여 자기는 한마디 속내도 털어놓지 않은 채 연적 앞에 자기 처지를 펼쳐 보이고 거만하게 물러나라고 경고할 수 있었기에, 어머니와의 이 만남을 일부러 주선한 것은 아닐까 하고. 그는 생각했을 것이다."], ["The current of these meditations would have been stayed sometimes by a rush of shame, bearing a remonstrance to himself from his own open nature, representing that to shelter such suspicions, even for the passing moment, was not to hold the high, unenvious course he had resolved to keep.", "설령 그런 의도까지는 아니었다 하더라도, 자기 억눌린 감정을 가지고 놀고 자기를 괴롭히려고 그를 여기로 데려온 것은 아닐까 하고. 이런 명상의 흐름은 때때로 한바탕 밀려드는 부끄러움에 가로막혔을 것이다."], ["At those times, the striving within him would have been hardest; and looking up and catching Gowan’s eyes, he would have started as if he had done him an injury.", "그 부끄러움은 그 자신의 솔직한 천성에서 비롯된 항변을 그에게 전해 주며, 그런 의심을, 스쳐 가는 한순간일지라도 품는 것은, 그가 지키기로 결심한 그 높고 시샘 없는 길을 가는 것이 아니라고 일러 주었을 것이다. 그럴 때면 그의 안에서 벌어지는 분투가 가장 격렬했을 것이고, 고개를 들어 가우언의 눈과 마주치면, 마치 그에게 잘못이라도 저지른 듯 흠칫 놀랐을 것이다."]], [["Then, looking at the dark road and its uncertain objects, he would have gradually trailed off again into thinking, ‘Where are we driving, he and I, I wonder, on the darker road of life? How will it be with us, and with her, in the obscure distance?’ Thinking of her, he would have been troubled anew with a reproachful misgiving that it was not even loyal to her to dislike him, and that in being so easily prejudiced against him he was less deserving of her than at first.", "그러다 어두운 길과 그 위의 어렴풋한 사물들을 바라보며, 그는 차츰 다시 생각에 잠겨 들었을 것이다. \"이 사람과 나는, 인생이라는 더 어두운 길 위에서 대체 어디로 마차를 몰아가고 있는 걸까? 그 흐릿한 먼 곳에서, 우리는, 그리고 그녀는 어떻게 될까?\" 그녀를 생각하면, 그는 새삼 자책 어린 불안에 시달렸을 것이다. 그를 싫어하는 것은 그녀에게조차 떳떳하지 못한 일이며, 그를 향해 그토록 쉽게 편견을 품는 것은 자기가 처음보다 그녀에게 덜 합당한 사람임을 뜻한다는 불안에."]], [["‘You are evidently out of spirits,’ said Gowan; ‘I am very much afraid my mother must have bored you dreadfully.’", "\"확실히 기분이 가라앉으셨군요.\" 가우언이 말했다. \"아무래도 어머니가 당신을 끔찍이도 지루하게 하신 게 틀림없어 무척 걱정입니다.\""]], [["‘Believe me, not at all,’ said Clennam.", "\"믿어 주세요, 전혀 아닙니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘It’s nothing--nothing!’", "\"아무것도 아닙니다—아무것도요!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 27. Five-and-Twenty", "# CHAPTER 27. Five-and-Twenty"]], [["A frequently recurring doubt, whether Mr Pancks’s desire to collect information relative to the Dorrit family could have any possible bearing on the misgivings he had imparted to his mother on his return from his long exile, caused Arthur Clennam much uneasiness at this period.", "팬크스 씨가 도릿 가족에 관한 정보를 수집하려는 욕망이, 그가 오랜 유배에서 돌아온 뒤 어머니에게 털어놓았던 불안과 어떤 식으로든 연관될 수 있지 않을까 하는 의심이 자꾸만 되살아나면서, 이 무렵 아서 클레넘은 적잖은 불편을 느꼈다."], ["What Mr Pancks already knew about the Dorrit family, what more he really wanted to find out, and why he should trouble his busy head about them at all, were questions that often perplexed him.", "팬크스 씨가 도릿 가족에 대해 이미 알고 있는 것이 무엇인지, 더 알아내고자 하는 것이 정말로 무엇인지, 그리고 대체 무엇 때문에 그 바쁜 머리를 그들 일로 괴롭히는지 하는 의문들이 그를 자주 당혹스럽게 했다."], ["Mr Pancks was not a man to waste his time and trouble in researches prompted by idle curiosity.", "팬크스 씨는 한가한 호기심에서 비롯된 탐색에 자기 시간과 수고를 허비할 사람이 아니었다."], ["That he had a specific object Clennam could not doubt.", "그에게 특정한 목적이 있다는 것을 클레넘은 의심할 수 없었다."], ["And whether the attainment of that object by Mr Pancks’s industry might bring to light, in some untimely way, secret reasons which had induced his mother to take Little Dorrit by the hand, was a serious speculation.", "그리고 팬크스 씨가 그 부지런함으로 그 목적을 달성하는 과정에서, 어머니로 하여금 리틀 도릿에게 손을 내밀게 만든 비밀스러운 이유가 어느 때 아닌 때에 드러나게 되지는 않을까 하는 것은 심각한 추측거리였다."]], [["Not that he ever wavered either in his desire or his determination to repair a wrong that had been done in his father’s time, should a wrong come to light, and be reparable.", "그렇다고 해서, 만일 잘못이 드러나고 그것을 바로잡을 수 있다면 아버지 시절에 저질러진 잘못을 바로잡겠다는 그의 바람이나 결심이 흔들렸던 것은 결코 아니었다."], ["The shadow of a supposed act of injustice, which had hung over him since his father’s death, was so vague and formless that it might be the result of a reality widely remote from his idea of it.", "아버지가 죽은 이래로 그의 머리 위에 드리워 있던, 부당한 행위가 있었으리라는 그 추정의 그림자는 너무도 어렴풋하고 형체가 없어서, 그가 상상하는 것과는 한참 동떨어진 실제 사실의 결과일 수도 있었다."], ["But, if his apprehensions should prove to be well founded, he was ready at any moment to lay down all he had, and begin the world anew.", "그러나 만일 그의 우려가 근거 있는 것으로 밝혀진다면, 그는 언제라도 자신이 가진 모든 것을 내려놓고 세상을 새로 시작할 각오가 되어 있었다."], ["As the fierce dark teaching of his childhood had never sunk into his heart, so that first article in his code of morals was, that he must begin, in practical humility, with looking well to his feet on Earth, and that he could never mount on wings of words to Heaven.", "어린 시절의 그 사납고 어두운 가르침이 결코 그의 가슴에 스며들지 않았던 것과 마찬가지로, 그의 도덕률의 첫째 조항은, 그가 실천적인 겸손함으로 시작해야 한다는 것, 곧 이 땅 위에서 제 발밑을 잘 살피는 일에서 시작해야 하며, 결코 말의 날개를 타고 천국으로 날아오를 수는 없다는 것이었다."], ["Duty on earth, restitution on earth, action on earth; these first, as the first steep steps upward.", "이 땅에서의 의무, 이 땅에서의 보상, 이 땅에서의 행동, 이것들이 먼저였다. 위로 오르는 그 가파른 첫 계단들이었다."], ["Strait was the gate and narrow was the way; far straiter and narrower than the broad high road paved with vain professions and vain repetitions, motes from other men’s eyes and liberal delivery of others to the judgment--all cheap materials costing absolutely nothing.", "그 문은 좁고 그 길은 협착하였다. 헛된 신앙고백과 헛된 반복, 남의 눈 속의 티끌과 남을 심판에 내어주는 너그러움으로 포장된 그 넓고 큰 길보다 훨씬 더 좁고 더 협착하였으니, 그 길은 죄다 값이라곤 전혀 들지 않는 싸구려 재료로 만들어진 것이었다."]], [["No.", "아니었다."], ["It was not a selfish fear or hesitation that rendered him uneasy, but a mistrust lest Pancks might not observe his part of the understanding between them, and, making any discovery, might take some course upon it without imparting it to him.", "그를 불안하게 만든 것은 이기적인 두려움이나 망설임이 아니라, 팬크스가 둘 사이의 합의에서 자기 몫을 지키지 않고, 무언가를 알아낸 끝에 그것을 자기에게 알리지도 않은 채 어떤 행동을 취하지나 않을까 하는 불신이었다."], ["On the other hand, when he recalled his conversation with Pancks, and the little reason he had to suppose that there was any likelihood of that strange personage being on that track at all, there were times when he wondered that he made so much of it.", "한편으로, 팬크스와 나눈 대화를 떠올리고, 그 기묘한 인물이 도대체 그 자취를 좇고 있을 가능성이 있다고 짐작할 만한 근거가 거의 없음을 생각하면, 자기가 이 일을 너무 크게 부풀리고 있는 것이 아닌가 싶은 때도 있었다."], ["Labouring in this sea, as all barks labour in cross seas, he tossed about and came to no haven.", "모든 배가 거센 맞물결 속에서 그러하듯 이 바다에서 안간힘을 쓰며, 그는 이리저리 흔들리기만 할 뿐 어떤 항구에도 닿지 못했다."]], [["The removal of Little Dorrit herself from their customary association, did not mend the matter.", "리틀 도릿 자신이 그들의 익숙한 교제에서 멀어진 것도 사정을 나아지게 하지는 않았다."], ["She was so much out, and so much in her own room, that he began to miss her and to find a blank in her place.", "그녀는 어찌나 자주 밖에 나가 있고, 또 어찌나 자주 제 방에만 틀어박혀 있던지, 그는 그녀가 그리워지기 시작했고 그녀의 자리에 빈 구멍이 느껴졌다."], ["He had written to her to inquire if she were better, and she had written back, very gratefully and earnestly telling him not to be uneasy on her behalf, for she was quite well; but he had not seen her, for what, in their intercourse, was a long time.", "그는 그녀에게 좀 나아졌는지 묻는 편지를 썼고, 그녀는 자기는 아주 잘 지내니 자기 때문에 마음 쓰지 말라고 무척 고맙고도 간곡하게 답장을 보내왔다. 그러나 그는, 그들의 교류로 치자면 오랜 동안, 그녀를 보지 못했다."]], [["He returned home one evening from an interview with her father, who had mentioned that she was out visiting--which was what he always said when she was hard at work to buy his supper--and found Mr Meagles in an excited state walking up and down his room.", "어느 날 저녁 그는 그녀의 아버지를 만나고 집으로 돌아왔는데, 그 아버지는 그녀가 방문을 나갔다고 했다. 그녀가 아버지의 저녁거리를 마련하느라 고되게 일하고 있을 때면 그가 늘 하는 말이었다."], ["On his opening the door, Mr Meagles stopped, faced round, and said:", "그런데 돌아와 보니 미글스 씨가 흥분한 상태로 그의 방 안을 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 그가 문을 열자 미글스 씨는 걸음을 멈추고 휙 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["‘Clennam!--Tattycoram!’", "\"클레넘!—태티코럼이!\""]], [["‘What’s the matter?’", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["‘Lost!’", "\"사라졌소!\""]], [["‘Why, bless my heart alive!’ cried Clennam in amazement.", "\"아니, 세상에나!\" 클레넘이 놀라서 외쳤다."], ["‘What do you mean?’", "\"그게 무슨 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["‘Wouldn’t count five-and-twenty, sir; couldn’t be got to do it; stopped at eight, and took herself off.’", "\"스물다섯을 세려고 하지 않았소, 여보게. 도무지 시키질 못했지. 여덟에서 멈추더니, 떠나 버렸소.\""]], [["‘Left your house?’", "\"댁을 나갔다고요?\""]], [["‘Never to come back,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head.", "\"다시는 돌아오지 않을 작정으로 말이오.\" 미글스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘You don’t know that girl’s passionate and proud character.", "\"자네는 그 아이의 격정적이고 자존심 강한 성격을 모를 거요."], ["A team of horses couldn’t draw her back now; the bolts and bars of the old Bastille couldn’t keep her.’", "이젠 말 한 무리로도 그 애를 끌고 올 수 없을 거고, 옛 바스티유의 빗장과 쇠창살로도 그 애를 붙들어 둘 수 없을 거요.\""]], [["‘How did it happen?", "\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐습니까?"], ["Pray sit down and tell me.’", "부디 앉으셔서 말씀해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘As to how it happened, it’s not so easy to relate: because you must have the unfortunate temperament of the poor impetuous girl herself, before you can fully understand it.", "\"어쩌다 그렇게 됐는지를 말로 하기란 그리 쉬운 일이 아니라오. 가엾고 충동적인 그 아이의 그 불행한 기질을 자네가 먼저 헤아려야만 온전히 이해할 수 있을 테니까."], ["But it came about in this way.", "하지만 일은 이런 식으로 벌어졌소."], ["Pet and Mother and I have been having a good deal of talk together of late.", "펫과 어멈과 나는 요즘 들어 셋이 꽤나 많은 이야기를 나누고 있었소."], ["I’ll not disguise from you, Clennam, that those conversations have not been of as bright a kind as I could wish; they have referred to our going away again.", "클레넘, 그 대화들이 내가 바랄 만큼 밝은 종류는 아니었음을 자네에게 숨기지 않으리다. 다시 떠나는 문제에 관한 이야기였으니까."], ["In proposing to do which, I have had, in fact, an object.’", "그렇게 하자고 제안한 데에는, 사실, 내게 한 가지 목적이 있었소.\""]], [["Nobody’s heart beat quickly.", "누구의 가슴도 빠르게 뛰지 않았다."]], [["‘An object,’ said Mr Meagles, after a moment’s pause, ‘that I will not disguise from you, either, Clennam.", "\"한 가지 목적이오.\" 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["There’s an inclination on the part of my dear child which I am sorry for.", "\"클레넘, 그것 역시 자네에게 숨기지 않으리다. 내 사랑하는 아이 쪽에 마음이 기우는 데가 있는데, 나는 그게 안타깝다오."], ["Perhaps you guess the person.", "아마 그 사람이 누군지 짐작이 갈 거요."], ["Henry Gowan.’", "헨리 가우언이오.\""]], [["‘I was not unprepared to hear it.’", "\"저도 그 말씀을 들을 마음의 준비가 없지는 않았습니다.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr Meagles, with a heavy sigh, ‘I wish to God you had never had to hear it.", "\"허!\" 미글스 씨가 무거운 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["However, so it is.", "\"자네가 그런 말을 들을 일이 아예 없었더라면 얼마나 좋았겠나."], ["Mother and I have done all we could to get the better of it, Clennam.", "하지만 사정이 그렇소. 어멈과 나는 그걸 이겨 내려고 할 수 있는 건 다 해 봤소, 클레넘."], ["We have tried tender advice, we have tried time, we have tried absence.", "다정한 충고도 해 봤고, 시간에도 맡겨 봤고, 떼어 놓아도 봤지."], ["As yet, of no use.", "아직껏 아무 소용이 없소."], ["Our late conversations have been upon the subject of going away for another year at least, in order that there might be an entire separation and breaking off for that term.", "요즈음 나눈 대화는, 적어도 한 해는 더 멀리 떠나 있자는 이야기였소. 그 기간만큼은 완전히 떼어 놓고 인연을 끊어 보자는 거였지."], ["Upon that question, Pet has been unhappy, and therefore Mother and I have been unhappy.’", "그 문제로 펫이 괴로워했고, 그러니 어멈과 나도 괴로웠소.\""]], [["Clennam said that he could easily believe it.", "클레넘은 충분히 믿을 만한 일이라고 말했다."]], [["‘Well!’ continued Mr Meagles in an apologetic way, ‘I admit as a practical man, and I am sure Mother would admit as a practical woman, that we do, in families, magnify our troubles and make mountains of our molehills in a way that is calculated to be rather trying to people who look on--to mere outsiders, you know, Clennam.", "\"허!\" 미글스 씨가 변명하는 투로 말을 이었다."], ["Still, Pet’s happiness or unhappiness is quite a life or death question with us; and we may be excused, I hope, for making much of it.", "\"현실적인 사람으로서 나는 인정하겠소, 어멈도 현실적인 여인으로서 인정할 게 분명한데, 우리네 식구들이란 자기네 골칫거리를 부풀려서 두더지가 파 놓은 흙더미를 산으로 만들어 버리는 법이오. 곁에서 지켜보는 사람들에게는 — 그러니까 한낱 바깥 사람들에게는 말일세, 클레넘 — 꽤나 진력나게 굴 정도로 말이오."], ["At all events, it might have been borne by Tattycoram.", "그래도 펫의 행복이냐 불행이냐 하는 건 우리에게 그야말로 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제라, 우리가 그걸 크게 여기는 건 부디 너그러이 봐주었으면 하오. 어쨌든 그쯤은 태티코럼이 견딜 수 있었어야 했소."], ["Now, don’t you think so?’", "자, 자네도 그렇게 생각하지 않나?\""]], [["‘I do indeed think so,’ returned Clennam, in most emphatic recognition of this very moderate expectation.", "\"정말 그렇게 생각합니다.\" 클레넘이 그 지극히 온당한 기대에 강하게 동의하며 대답했다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head ruefully.", "\"아니었소, 여보게.\" 미글스 씨가 서글피 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘She couldn’t stand it.", "\"그 애는 그걸 견디지 못했소."], ["The chafing and firing of that girl, the wearing and tearing of that girl within her own breast, has been such that I have softly said to her again and again in passing her, “Five-and-twenty, Tattycoram, five-and-twenty!”", "그 아이가 제 가슴속에서 부글거리고 타오르며, 닳고 찢기던 그 모습이 어찌나 심했던지, 나는 지나치면서 몇 번이고 가만히 일러 주곤 했소. '스물다섯이다, 태티코럼, 스물다섯!'"], ["I heartily wish she could have gone on counting five-and-twenty day and night, and then it wouldn’t have happened.’", "그 애가 밤이고 낮이고 스물다섯을 계속 셀 수 있었더라면 진심으로 좋았을 것을. 그랬다면 이런 일은 없었을 텐데.\""]], [["Mr Meagles with a despondent countenance in which the goodness of his heart was even more expressed than in his times of cheerfulness and gaiety, stroked his face down from his forehead to his chin, and shook his head again.", "미글스 씨는, 그 흥겹고 유쾌하던 때보다 오히려 마음씨의 선함이 더욱 잘 드러나는 풀 죽은 얼굴로, 이마에서 턱까지 손으로 쓸어내리고는 다시 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘I said to Mother (not that it was necessary, for she would have thought it all for herself), we are practical people, my dear, and we know her story; we see in this unhappy girl some reflection of what was raging in her mother’s heart before ever such a creature as this poor thing was in the world; we’ll gloss her temper over, Mother, we won’t notice it at present, my dear, we’ll take advantage of some better disposition in her another time. So we said nothing. But, do what we would, it seems as if it was to be; she broke out violently one night.’", "\"내가 어멈에게 말했소(굳이 그럴 필요도 없었지, 어멈 혼자서도 다 그렇게 생각했을 테니). 여보, 우리는 현실적인 사람들이고, 그 애의 내력을 알고 있잖소. 우리는 이 불행한 아이에게서, 이 가엾은 것이 이 세상에 나오기도 전에 그 애 어미의 가슴속에서 들끓던 무언가의 반영을 보는 거요. 우리는 그 애의 성미를 슬쩍 덮어 주고, 여보, 지금은 그걸 모른 척합시다, 언젠가 그 애가 좀 더 나은 기분일 때를 틈타 봅시다 하고. 그래서 우린 아무 말도 하지 않았소. 하지만 우리가 어떻게 하든, 일어날 일은 일어나고야 마는 것 같았소. 그 애가 어느 날 밤 사납게 터져 버렸지.\""]], [["‘How, and why?’", "\"어떻게, 또 왜요?\""]], [["‘If you ask me Why,’ said Mr Meagles, a little disturbed by the question, for he was far more intent on softening her case than the family’s, ‘I can only refer you to what I have just repeated as having been pretty near my words to Mother. As to How, we had said Good night to Pet in her presence (very affectionately, I must allow), and she had attended Pet up-stairs--you remember she was her maid. Perhaps Pet, having been out of sorts, may have been a little more inconsiderate than usual in requiring services of her: but I don’t know that I have any right to say so; she was always thoughtful and gentle.’", "\"자네가 만일 '왜'냐고 묻는다면,\" 미글스 씨가 그 질문에 조금 마음이 흔들려서 말했다. 그는 식구들의 처지보다 그 애의 처지를 누그러뜨리는 데 훨씬 더 마음을 쏟고 있었기 때문이다. \"내가 방금 어멈에게 한 거나 다름없는 말이라고 되풀이한 것밖에는 달리 들려줄 게 없소. '어떻게'냐고 하면, 우리는 그 애가 있는 자리에서 펫에게 잘 자라고 인사를 했고(꽤 다정하게, 그건 인정해야겠지), 그 애가 펫을 데리고 위층으로 올라갔소 — 그 애가 펫의 몸종이었던 거 기억하지? 어쩌면 펫이 몸이 좋지 않았던 터라, 그 애에게 시중을 청하면서 평소보다 조금 더 배려가 없었을지도 모르지. 하지만 내가 그렇게 말할 자격이 있는지는 모르겠소. 그 애는 늘 사려 깊고 다정했으니까.\""]], [["‘The gentlest mistress in the world.’", "\"세상에서 가장 다정한 여주인이지요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking him by the hand; ‘you have often seen them together.", "\"고맙소, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 그의 손을 잡고 흔들며 말했다."], ["Well! We presently heard this unfortunate Tattycoram loud and angry, and before we could ask what was the matter, Pet came back in a tremble, saying she was frightened of her.", "\"자네는 그 둘이 함께 있는 걸 자주 봤지."], ["Close after her came Tattycoram in a flaming rage.", "그런데! 이내 우리는 그 불행한 태티코럼이 큰 소리로 화내는 걸 들었고, 무슨 일인지 미처 물어볼 새도 없이 펫이 부들부들 떨며 돌아와서는, 그 애가 무섭다고 했소."], ["“I hate you all three,”", "펫 바로 뒤에 태티코럼이 활활 성을 내며 따라 들어왔소."], ["says she, stamping her foot at us.", "'난 세 분 다 미워요.' 그 애가 우리를 향해 발을 구르며 말하더군."], ["“I am bursting with hate of the whole house.”’", "'이 집 전체가 미워서 터질 것 같아요.'\""]], [["‘Upon which you--?’", "\"그래서 어르신께서는—?\""]], [["‘I?’ said Mr Meagles, with a plain good faith that might have commanded the belief of Mrs Gowan herself.", "\"내가?\" 미글스 씨가, 가우언 부인 자신마저도 믿게 만들 만한 그 꾸밈없는 진심으로 말했다."], ["‘I said, count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’", "\"스물다섯을 세거라, 태티코럼, 하고 말했소.\""]], [["Mr Meagles again stroked his face and shook his head, with an air of profound regret.", "미글스 씨는 깊은 뉘우침의 기색으로 다시 얼굴을 쓸어내리고 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘She was so used to do it, Clennam, that even then, such a picture of passion as you never saw, she stopped short, looked me full in the face, and counted (as I made out) to eight.", "\"그 애는 그게 어찌나 몸에 뱄던지, 클레넘, 그렇게나 격정에 휩싸인 모습은 자네도 본 적이 없을 정도였는데, 바로 그 순간에도 딱 멈추고는 내 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며 (내가 알아듣기로는) 여덟까지 세더군."], ["But she couldn’t control herself to go any further.", "하지만 더는 자제할 수가 없었던 거요."], ["There she broke down, poor thing, and gave the other seventeen to the four winds.", "거기서 무너져 버렸소, 가엾은 것이. 나머지 열일곱은 사방으로 흩어 버렸지."], ["Then it all burst out.", "그러고는 모든 게 터져 나왔소."], ["She detested us, she was miserable with us, she couldn’t bear it, she wouldn’t bear it, she was determined to go away.", "우리가 밉다, 우리와 있으면 비참하다, 견딜 수 없다, 견디지 않겠다, 떠나기로 마음먹었다고."], ["She was younger than her young mistress, and would she remain to see her always held up as the only creature who was young and interesting, and to be cherished and loved? No.", "자기는 어린 여주인보다 어린데, 늘 그 여주인만이 젊고 흥미로운 유일한 존재라며 아끼고 사랑받는 걸 두고만 봐야 하느냐고."], ["She wouldn’t, she wouldn’t, she wouldn’t!", "싫다고. 그러지 않겠다, 않겠다, 않겠다고!"], ["What did we think she, Tattycoram, might have been if she had been caressed and cared for in her childhood, like her young mistress?", "우리가 어린 여주인처럼 자기를 어린 시절에 어루만지고 보살펴 주었다면, 자기 태티코럼은 어떤 사람이 되었을 것 같으냐고."], ["As good as her?", "그 여주인만큼은 됐을 거라고?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Perhaps fifty times as good.", "어쩌면 쉰 배는 더 나은 사람이 됐을 거라고."], ["When we pretended to be so fond of one another, we exulted over her; that was what we did; we exulted over her and shamed her.", "우리가 서로 그토록 아끼는 척할 때, 우리는 자기를 두고 우쭐댄 거라고. 그게 우리가 한 짓이라고."], ["And all in the house did the same.", "우리가 자기를 두고 우쭐대며 부끄럽게 만들었다고."], ["They talked about their fathers and mothers, and brothers and sisters; they liked to drag them up before her face.", "그리고 집안 사람들이 다 똑같이 그랬다고. 다들 자기네 아비 어미, 형제자매 얘기를 떠들어 댔고, 자기 앞에서 일부러 그들을 끄집어내는 걸 좋아했다고."], ["There was Mrs Tickit, only yesterday, when her little grandchild was with her, had been amused by the child’s trying to call her (Tattycoram) by the wretched name we gave her; and had laughed at the name.", "바로 어제도 티킷 부인이, 어린 손주가 와 있을 때, 그 아이가 우리가 자기한테 붙여 준 그 형편없는 이름으로 자기(태티코럼)를 부르려고 애쓰는 걸 재미있어하며 그 이름을 두고 웃었다고."], ["Why, who didn’t; and who were we that we should have a right to name her like a dog or a cat?", "아니, 안 웃은 사람이 누가 있느냐고. 우리가 대체 뭐기에 자기를 개나 고양이처럼 이름 붙일 권리가 있느냐고."], ["But she didn’t care.", "하지만 자기는 상관없다고."], ["She would take no more benefits from us; she would fling us her name back again, and she would go.", "더는 우리한테서 아무 은혜도 받지 않겠다고. 그 이름을 우리한테 도로 내던지고 떠나겠다고."], ["She would leave us that minute, nobody should stop her, and we should never hear of her again.’", "바로 그 순간 우리를 떠나겠다, 아무도 못 막는다, 다시는 자기 소식을 듣지 못할 거라고 했소.\""]], [["Mr Meagles had recited all this with such a vivid remembrance of his original, that he was almost as flushed and hot by this time as he described her to have been.", "미글스 씨는 이 모든 것을 그 원래 광경을 어찌나 생생히 떠올리며 읊었던지, 이쯤 되자 자기가 묘사한 그 애만큼이나 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아올라 있었다."]], [["‘Ah, well!’ he said, wiping his face.", "\"아, 허!\" 그가 얼굴을 닦으며 말했다."], ["‘It was of no use trying reason then, with that vehement panting creature (Heaven knows what her mother’s story must have been); so I quietly told her that she should not go at that late hour of night, and I gave her my hand and took her to her room, and locked the house doors.", "\"그 격하게 헐떡이는 아이를 두고(그 애 어미의 사연이 어떠했을지는 하늘만이 알 일이지) 그때 이치를 따져 봐야 아무 소용이 없었소. 그래서 나는 그 늦은 밤중에 떠나선 안 된다고 조용히 일러 주고, 손을 내밀어 그 애를 방으로 데려다준 다음 집 문들을 잠갔소."], ["But she was gone this morning.’", "그런데 오늘 아침에 보니 가 버린 거요.\""]], [["‘And you know no more of her?’", "\"그래서 그 애에 대해 더는 아는 게 없으신 거고요?\""]], [["‘No more,’ returned Mr Meagles.", "\"더는 없소.\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘I have been hunting about all day.", "\"온종일 사방을 뒤지고 다녔소."], ["She must have gone very early and very silently.", "아주 이른 시각에 아주 소리 없이 떠났을 거요."], ["I have found no trace of her down about us.’", "우리 근방에서는 그 애 자취를 하나도 찾지 못했소.\""]], [["‘Stay!", "\"잠깐만요! 어르신께서는,\""], ["You want,’ said Clennam, after a moment’s reflection, ‘to see her?", "클레넘이 잠시 생각한 뒤에 말했다. \"그 애를 만나고 싶으신 거지요?"], ["I assume that?’", "그렇게 보아도 될까요?\""]], [["‘Yes, assuredly; I want to give her another chance; Mother and Pet want to give her another chance; come! You yourself,’ said Mr Meagles, persuasively, as if the provocation to be angry were not his own at all, ‘want to give the poor passionate girl another chance, I know, Clennam.’", "\"그렇소, 물론이오. 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶소. 어멈도 펫도 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶어 하오. 자!\" 미글스 씨가, 화가 날 만한 도발이 자기 쪽 일은 전혀 아니라는 듯 설득하는 투로 말했다. \"자네 자신도 그 가엾고 격정적인 아이에게 한 번 더 기회를 주고 싶을 거요. 나는 다 아오, 클레넘.\""]], [["‘It would be strange and hard indeed if I did not,’ said Clennam, ‘when you are all so forgiving.", "\"여러분이 그토록 너그러운데 제가 그러지 않는다면 정말 이상하고 모진 일이겠지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["What I was going to ask you was, have you thought of that Miss Wade?’", "\"제가 여쭤보려던 건, 그 웨이드 양은 생각해 보셨는지 하는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘I have.", "\"생각해 봤소."], ["I did not think of her until I had pervaded the whole of our neighbourhood, and I don’t know that I should have done so then but for finding Mother and Pet, when I went home, full of the idea that Tattycoram must have gone to her.", "우리 동네를 온통 훑고 다니기 전까지는 그 사람 생각을 못 했지. 집에 돌아가 보니 어멈과 펫이 태티코럼이 분명 그 사람에게 갔을 거라는 생각에 사로잡혀 있는 걸 보지 않았더라면, 그때도 그런 생각을 했을지 모르겠소."], ["Then, of course, I recalled what she said that day at dinner when you were first with us.’", "그러고 나니 물론, 자네가 우리와 처음 함께 있던 날 만찬 자리에서 그 사람이 했던 말이 떠올랐소.\""]], [["‘Have you any idea where Miss Wade is to be found?’", "\"웨이드 양을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 짐작 가는 데가 있으십니까?\""]], [["‘To tell you the truth,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘it’s because I have an addled jumble of a notion on that subject that you found me waiting here. There is one of those odd impressions in my house, which do mysteriously get into houses sometimes, which nobody seems to have picked up in a distinct form from anybody, and yet which everybody seems to have got hold of loosely from somebody and let go again, that she lives, or was living, thereabouts.’ Mr Meagles handed him a slip of paper, on which was written the name of one of the dull by-streets in the Grosvenor region, near Park Lane.", "\"솔직히 말하자면,\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다. \"그 일에 대해 머릿속이 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 짐작이 있어서, 자네가 여기서 나를 기다리고 있는 걸 보게 된 거요. 우리 집에는, 가끔 집안에 신비롭게 스며드는 그런 묘한 인상 중 하나가 있소. 아무도 누구한테서 또렷한 형태로 주워들은 것 같지는 않은데, 그러면서도 다들 누군가한테서 어렴풋이 얻어 듣고는 다시 흘려버린 것 같은 그런 인상 말이오. 그 사람이 이 근처에 산다, 혹은 살고 있었다는 거요.\" 미글스 씨가 그에게 종잇조각 하나를 건넸는데, 거기에는 파크 레인 근처 그로스베너 구역의 칙칙한 뒷골목 중 하나의 이름이 적혀 있었다."]], [["‘Here is no number,’ said Arthur looking over it.", "\"번지가 없군요.\" 아서가 그것을 훑어보며 말했다."]], [["‘No number, my dear Clennam?’ returned his friend.", "\"번지가 없다고, 클레넘?\" 그의 친구가 대답했다."], ["‘No anything!", "\"아무것도 없소! 거리 이름조차도 어쩌면 그냥 허공에 떠다니던 것일지 모르오."], ["The very name of the street may have been floating in the air; for, as I tell you, none of my people can say where they got it from.", "내가 말했듯이, 우리 집 사람들 가운데 누구도 어디서 그걸 들었는지 말하지 못하니까. 하지만 한번 물어볼 가치는 있소."], ["However, it’s worth an inquiry; and as I would rather make it in company than alone, and as you too were a fellow-traveller of that immovable woman’s, I thought perhaps--’ Clennam finished the sentence for him by taking up his hat again, and saying he was ready.", "그리고 나는 혼자보다는 누군가와 함께 알아보고 싶은데, 자네도 그 꿈쩍 않는 여인과 함께 여행한 길동무였으니, 혹시 자네가—\" 클레넘이 다시 모자를 집어 들고 준비되었다고 말하며 그 문장을 대신 마무리해 주었다."]], [["It was now summer-time; a grey, hot, dusty evening.", "이제 한여름이었다. 잿빛에, 무덥고, 먼지 자욱한 저녁이었다."], ["They rode to the top of Oxford Street, and there alighting, dived in among the great streets of melancholy stateliness, and the little streets that try to be as stately and succeed in being more melancholy, of which there is a labyrinth near Park Lane.", "그들은 옥스퍼드 거리 끝까지 마차로 갔다가 거기서 내려, 파크 레인 근처에 미로처럼 얽혀 있는, 우울하리만치 위엄을 부리는 큰 거리들과, 그만큼 위엄을 부리려 애쓰지만 결국 더 우울해지고만 작은 거리들 사이로 파고들었다."], ["Wildernesses of corner houses, with barbarous old porticoes and appurtenances; horrors that came into existence under some wrong-headed person in some wrong-headed time, still demanding the blind admiration of all ensuing generations and determined to do so until they tumbled down; frowned upon the twilight.", "야만스럽게 낡은 주랑과 부속물을 단 모퉁이 집들의 황무지. 어느 그릇된 시대에 어느 그릇된 사람의 손으로 생겨난 그 흉물들은, 무너져 내릴 때까지 그러기로 작정한 듯, 뒤따르는 모든 세대의 맹목적인 찬탄을 여전히 요구하며 황혼을 노려보고 있었다."], ["Parasite little tenements, with the cramp in their whole frame, from the dwarf hall-door on the giant model of His Grace’s in the Square to the squeezed window of the boudoir commanding the dunghills in the Mews, made the evening doleful.", "광장에 있는 공작 각하 저택을 거대하게 본뜬 난쟁이 같은 현관문에서부터, 마구간 뒷골목의 거름더미가 내다보이는 규방의 찌부러진 창문에 이르기까지, 온통 비좁아 쥐가 난 듯한 기생충 같은 작은 셋집들이 저녁을 구슬프게 만들었다."], ["Rickety dwellings of undoubted fashion, but of a capacity to hold nothing comfortably except a dismal smell, looked like the last result of the great mansions’ breeding in-and-in; and, where their little supplementary bows and balconies were supported on thin iron columns, seemed to be scrofulously resting upon crutches.", "의심할 바 없이 유행을 따랐으되 음울한 냄새 말고는 무엇 하나 편안히 담아 둘 만한 용량이 없는 그 허청거리는 집들은, 큰 저택들이 근친끼리 거듭 번식한 끝에 나온 마지막 결과물처럼 보였다. 그 작은 부속 퇴창과 발코니가 가느다란 쇠기둥에 받쳐져 있는 곳에서는, 마치 연주창에라도 걸려 목발에 기대 쉬고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["Here and there a Hatchment, with the whole science of Heraldry in it, loomed down upon the street, like an Archbishop discoursing on Vanity.", "여기저기 문장학의 온갖 지식이 담긴 상장(喪章)이, 마치 허영에 대해 설교하는 대주교처럼 거리를 굽어보고 있었다."], ["The shops, few in number, made no show; for popular opinion was as nothing to them.", "몇 안 되는 상점들은 아무런 진열도 하지 않았으니, 그들에게 대중의 평판 따위는 아무것도 아니었기 때문이다."], ["The pastrycook knew who was on his books, and in that knowledge could be calm, with a few glass cylinders of dowager peppermint-drops in his window, and half-a-dozen ancient specimens of currant-jelly.", "제과점 주인은 누가 자기 외상 장부에 올라 있는지 알았고, 그 앎에 기대어 태연할 수 있었기에, 진열창에는 유리 원통에 담긴 노부인용 박하사탕 몇 통과 골동품 같은 산딸기 젤리 대여섯 개뿐이었다."], ["A few oranges formed the greengrocer’s whole concession to the vulgar mind.", "오렌지 몇 알이 청과상이 천박한 대중에게 베푼 유일한 양보였다."], ["A single basket made of moss, once containing plovers’ eggs, held all that the poulterer had to say to the rabble.", "한때 물떼새 알이 담겼던 이끼로 엮은 바구니 하나가, 가금 장수가 무지렁이들에게 할 말의 전부를 담고 있었다."], ["Everybody in those streets seemed (which is always the case at that hour and season) to be gone out to dinner, and nobody seemed to be giving the dinners they had gone to.", "그 거리의 모든 사람이(이 시각과 이 계절에는 늘 그러하듯) 만찬에 나가 버린 듯했고, 정작 그들이 찾아간 그 만찬을 베푸는 사람은 아무도 없는 것 같았다."], ["On the doorsteps there were lounging footmen with bright parti-coloured plumage and white polls, like an extinct race of monstrous birds; and butlers, solitary men of recluse demeanour, each of whom appeared distrustful of all other butlers.", "문간 계단에는 밝은 잡색 깃털 같은 제복에 머리에는 흰 분을 바른 종복들이, 멸종한 괴상한 새들의 종족처럼 한가로이 늘어서 있었고, 집사장들은, 다른 모든 집사장을 못 미더워하는 듯한, 은둔자 같은 태도의 고독한 사내들이었다."], ["The roll of carriages in the Park was done for the day; the street lamps were lighting; and wicked little grooms in the tightest fitting garments, with twists in their legs answering to the twists in their minds, hung about in pairs, chewing straws and exchanging fraudulent secrets.", "공원의 마차 행렬은 하루치가 끝나 있었고, 거리의 가로등에는 불이 켜지고 있었으며, 머릿속에 든 비틀림에 걸맞게 다리도 비틀린, 몸에 꼭 끼는 옷차림의 짓궂은 어린 마부들이 둘씩 짝을 지어 지푸라기를 씹으며 음흉한 비밀을 주고받고 있었다."], ["The spotted dogs who went out with the carriages, and who were so associated with splendid equipages that it looked like a condescension in those animals to come out without them, accompanied helpers to and fro on messages.", "마차를 따라 나서곤 하던, 그래서 화려한 마차 행렬과 어찌나 한 짝으로 여겨지던지 그것들 없이 나오는 게 마치 그 짐승들 쪽에서 격을 낮춰 주는 일처럼 보이던 점박이 개들이, 심부름하는 일꾼들을 따라 이리저리 오갔다."], ["Here and there was a retiring public-house which did not require to be supported on the shoulders of the people, and where gentlemen out of livery were not much wanted.", "여기저기, 사람들의 어깨에 받쳐질 필요가 없는, 또 제복 벗은 신사들이 그다지 필요치 않은, 한적한 선술집이 한 채씩 있었다."]], [["This last discovery was made by the two friends in pursuing their inquiries.", "이 마지막 사실은 두 친구가 수소문을 이어 가던 중에 알아낸 것이었다."], ["Nothing was there, or anywhere, known of such a person as Miss Wade, in connection with the street they sought.", "그들이 찾는 거리와 관련해서, 거기서든 어디서든 웨이드 양이라는 인물에 대해 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["It was one of the parasite streets; long, regular, narrow, dull and gloomy; like a brick and mortar funeral.", "그곳은 그 기생충 같은 거리들 가운데 하나였다. 길고, 반듯하고, 좁고, 칙칙하고, 음울한 것이, 벽돌과 회반죽으로 치른 장례 행렬 같았다."], ["They inquired at several little area gates, where a dejected youth stood spiking his chin on the summit of a precipitous little shoot of wooden steps, but could gain no information.", "그들은 여러 작은 지하 출입구 문에서 물어보았는데, 그곳에는 풀이 죽은 젊은이가 가파른 나무 계단의 꼭대기에 턱을 괴고 서 있었지만, 아무 정보도 얻지 못했다."], ["They walked up the street on one side of the way, and down it on the other, what time two vociferous news-sellers, announcing an extraordinary event that had never happened and never would happen, pitched their hoarse voices into the secret chambers; but nothing came of it.", "그들은 길 한쪽으로는 거리를 거슬러 올라갔다가 다른 쪽으로는 내려왔는데, 그러는 동안 두 명의 떠들썩한 신문팔이가, 결코 일어난 적도 없고 결코 일어나지도 않을 굉장한 사건을 외쳐 대며 그 은밀한 방들 안으로 쉰 목소리를 내질렀지만, 거기서도 아무것도 나오지 않았다."], ["At length they stood at the corner from which they had begun, and it had fallen quite dark, and they were no wiser.", "마침내 그들은 출발했던 그 모퉁이에 다시 섰는데, 날은 완전히 캄캄해져 있었고, 그들은 아무것도 더 알아내지 못한 채였다."]], [["It happened that in the street they had several times passed a dingy house, apparently empty, with bills in the windows, announcing that it was to let.", "마침 그 거리에서 그들은 칙칙한 집 한 채를 여러 번 지나쳤는데, 창문마다 세놓는다는 광고지가 붙어 있는, 겉보기엔 빈집이었다."], ["The bills, as a variety in the funeral procession, almost amounted to a decoration.", "그 광고지들은 장례 행렬 속의 별난 변화로서, 거의 장식이라 할 만했다."], ["Perhaps because they kept the house separated in his mind, or perhaps because Mr Meagles and himself had twice agreed in passing, ‘It is clear she don’t live there,’ Clennam now proposed that they should go back and try that house before finally going away.", "어쩌면 그 집을 마음속에 따로 떼어 두고 있었기 때문인지, 아니면 미글스 씨와 그가 지나치며 두 번이나 \"저기 사는 게 아닌 건 분명하군\" 하고 의견을 같이했기 때문인지, 클레넘은 이제, 아주 떠나기 전에 돌아가서 그 집을 한번 해 보자고 제안했다."], ["Mr Meagles agreed, and back they went.", "미글스 씨가 동의했고, 그들은 되돌아갔다."]], [["They knocked once, and they rang once, without any response.", "그들은 한 번 문을 두드렸고, 한 번 초인종을 울렸지만, 아무 응답이 없었다."], ["‘Empty,’ said Mr Meagles, listening.", "\"비었군.\" 미글스 씨가 귀를 기울이며 말했다."], ["‘Once more,’ said Clennam, and knocked again.", "\"한 번 더.\" 클레넘이 말하며 다시 두드렸다."], ["After that knock they heard a movement below, and somebody shuffling up towards the door.", "그렇게 두드린 뒤에 그들은 아래쪽에서 무언가 움직이는 소리와, 누군가가 문 쪽으로 발을 끌며 다가오는 소리를 들었다."]], [["The confined entrance was so dark that it was impossible to make out distinctly what kind of person opened the door; but it appeared to be an old woman.", "비좁은 입구가 너무 어두워서, 문을 연 사람이 어떤 부류인지 또렷이 알아보기란 불가능했지만, 노파인 듯했다. \"성가시게 해서 미안합니다.\""], ["‘Excuse our troubling you,’ said Clennam.", "클레넘이 말했다. \"혹 웨이드 양이 어디 사는지 알려 주실 수 있을까요?\""], ["‘Pray can you tell us where Miss Wade lives?’ The voice in the darkness unexpectedly replied, ‘Lives here.’", "어둠 속의 목소리가 뜻밖에도 대답했다. \"여기 사오.\""]], [["‘Is she at home?’", "\"지금 댁에 계십니까?\""]], [["No answer coming, Mr Meagles asked again.", "아무 대답이 없자 미글스 씨가 다시 물었다."], ["‘Pray is she at home?’", "\"혹시 댁에 계신가요?\""]], [["After another delay, ‘I suppose she is,’ said the voice abruptly; ‘you had better come in, and I’ll ask.’", "또 한 차례 뜸을 들인 뒤, \"있을 거요.\" 그 목소리가 퉁명스레 말했다. \"들어오는 게 낫겠소, 내가 물어보리다.\""]], [["They were summarily shut into the close black house; and the figure rustling away, and speaking from a higher level, said, ‘Come up, if you please; you can’t tumble over anything.’ They groped their way up-stairs towards a faint light, which proved to be the light of the street shining through a window; and the figure left them shut in an airless room.", "그들은 즉시 그 답답하고 컴컴한 집 안으로 들여보내졌다. 그러더니 그 형체가 부스럭부스럭 멀어지며, 더 높은 데서 말소리를 내어, \"올라오시오, 괜찮다면. 발에 걸려 넘어질 건 아무것도 없으니까\" 하고 말했다. 그들은 희미한 빛을 향해 더듬더듬 위층으로 올라갔는데, 그 빛은 알고 보니 창문을 통해 비쳐 드는 거리의 불빛이었다. 그러고는 그 형체가 그들을 공기도 통하지 않는 방에 가두어 놓고 가 버렸다."]], [["‘This is odd, Clennam,’ said Mr Meagles, softly.", "\"이거 묘하군, 클레넘.\" 미글스 씨가 나직이 말했다."]], [["‘Odd enough,’ assented Clennam in the same tone, ‘but we have succeeded; that’s the main point.", "\"꽤 묘하긴 합니다.\" 클레넘도 같은 어조로 동의했다."], ["Here’s a light coming!’", "\"하지만 어쨌든 찾아냈으니, 그게 중요한 점이지요. 불이 옵니다!\""]], [["The light was a lamp, and the bearer was an old woman: very dirty, very wrinkled and dry. ‘She’s at home,’ she said (and the voice was the same that had spoken before); ‘she’ll come directly.’ Having set the lamp down on the table, the old woman dusted her hands on her apron, which she might have done for ever without cleaning them, looked at the visitors with a dim pair of eyes, and backed out.", "그 불빛은 등잔이었고, 그것을 든 이는 노파였다. 무척 더럽고, 무척 주름지고, 메마른 노파였다. \"있다오.\" 그녀가 말했다(아까 말했던 그 목소리였다). \"곧 나올 거요.\" 등잔을 탁자 위에 내려놓고 나서, 노파는 앞치마에 손을 닦았는데, 그래 봐야 영원히 닦은들 깨끗해지지 않을 손이었다. 그러고는 흐릿한 두 눈으로 손님들을 보고는 뒷걸음질로 나갔다."]], [["The lady whom they had come to see, if she were the present occupant of the house, appeared to have taken up her quarters there as she might have established herself in an Eastern caravanserai.", "그들이 만나러 온 그 여인이, 만일 그녀가 이 집의 현재 거주자라면, 마치 동방의 대상 숙소에 자리를 잡듯 이곳에 거처를 마련한 것 같았다."], ["A small square of carpet in the middle of the room, a few articles of furniture that evidently did not belong to the room, and a disorder of trunks and travelling articles, formed the whole of her surroundings.", "방 한가운데 깔린 작은 네모 양탄자 한 장, 분명 그 방에 딸린 것이 아닌 가구 몇 점, 그리고 어지러이 널린 트렁크와 여행용 물품들이 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것이었다."], ["Under some former regular inhabitant, the stifling little apartment had broken out into a pier-glass and a gilt table; but the gilding was as faded as last year’s flowers, and the glass was so clouded that it seemed to hold in magic preservation all the fogs and bad weather it had ever reflected.", "이전의 어느 제대로 된 거주자 시절에는, 이 숨 막히는 작은 방에서도 체경(體鏡)과 금박을 입힌 탁자가 피어났던 모양이지만, 금박은 지난해 꽃처럼 빛이 바랬고, 거울은 어찌나 흐려졌던지 그동안 비춰 온 온갖 안개와 궂은 날씨를 마법으로 보존해 두고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["The visitors had had a minute or two to look about them, when the door opened and Miss Wade came in.", "손님들이 한두 분쯤 주위를 둘러볼 시간이 있었을 때, 문이 열리고 웨이드 양이 들어왔다."]], [["She was exactly the same as when they had parted, just as handsome, just as scornful, just as repressed.", "그녀는 그들이 헤어졌을 때와 똑같았다. 똑같이 아름답고, 똑같이 경멸에 차 있고, 똑같이 억눌려 있었다."], ["She manifested no surprise in seeing them, nor any other emotion.", "그녀는 그들을 보고도 놀라는 기색이나 다른 어떤 감정도 드러내지 않았다."], ["She requested them to be seated; and declining to take a seat herself, at once anticipated any introduction of their business.", "그녀는 그들에게 앉기를 청했고, 자신은 자리에 앉기를 마다하며, 그들이 용건을 꺼내기도 전에 곧장 가로챘다."]], [["‘I apprehend,’ she said, ‘that I know the cause of your favouring me with this visit.", "\"제가 짐작하기로는,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["We may come to it at once.’", "\"두 분이 이렇게 찾아 주신 까닭을 알 것 같군요. 곧장 본론으로 들어가도 되겠지요.\""]], [["‘The cause then, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘is Tattycoram.’", "\"그럼 그 까닭이란, 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"태티코럼입니다.\""]], [["‘So I supposed.’", "\"그러리라 생각했어요.\""]], [["‘Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘will you be so kind as to say whether you know anything of her?’", "\"웨이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"부디 그 애에 대해 무언가 아시는 게 있는지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Surely.", "\"물론이죠."], ["I know she is here with me.’", "그 애가 여기 저와 함께 있다는 걸 알고 있어요.\""]], [["‘Then, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘allow me to make known to you that I shall be happy to have her back, and that my wife and daughter will be happy to have her back. She has been with us a long time: we don’t forget her claims upon us, and I hope we know how to make allowances.’", "\"그렇다면, 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"제가 그 애를 기꺼이 데려가고 싶다는 것, 그리고 제 아내와 딸도 그 애를 기꺼이 다시 맞고 싶어 한다는 것을 알려 드리게 해 주십시오. 그 애는 우리와 오랫동안 함께 지냈습니다. 우리는 그 애가 우리에게 가진 권리를 잊지 않으며, 우리도 사정을 헤아릴 줄 안다고 믿습니다.\""]], [["‘You hope to know how to make allowances?’ she returned, in a level, measured voice.", "\"사정을 헤아릴 줄 안다고 믿으신다고요?\" 그녀가 차분하고 절제된 목소리로 되받았다."], ["‘For what?’", "\"무엇을 헤아린다는 거죠?\""]], [["‘I think my friend would say, Miss Wade,’ Arthur Clennam interposed, seeing Mr Meagles rather at a loss, ‘for the passionate sense that sometimes comes upon the poor girl, of being at a disadvantage.", "\"제 친구가 하려는 말씀은, 웨이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 다소 말문이 막히는 것을 보고 아서 클레넘이 끼어들었다."], ["Which occasionally gets the better of better remembrances.’", "\"그 가엾은 아이에게 이따금 덮쳐 오는, 자기가 불리한 처지에 있다는 그 격정적인 감각을 헤아린다는 뜻일 겁니다. 그것이 가끔 더 나은 기억들을 이겨 버리곤 하니까요.\""]], [["The lady broke into a smile as she turned her eyes upon him.", "그 여인은 그에게 눈길을 돌리며 빙긋 웃음을 지었다. \"그래요?\""], ["‘Indeed?’ was all she answered.", "그것이 그녀의 대답 전부였다."]], [["She stood by the table so perfectly composed and still after this acknowledgment of his remark that Mr Meagles stared at her under a sort of fascination, and could not even look to Clennam to make another move.", "그녀는 그의 말을 그렇게 받아넘긴 뒤로, 어찌나 완벽하게 침착하고 고요하게 탁자 곁에 서 있던지, 미글스 씨는 일종의 홀린 듯한 기분으로 그녀를 빤히 바라보았고, 다음 수를 두라고 클레넘 쪽을 쳐다볼 엄두조차 내지 못했다."], ["After waiting, awkwardly enough, for some moments, Arthur said:", "꽤나 어색하게 한동안 기다린 끝에, 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Perhaps it would be well if Mr Meagles could see her, Miss Wade?’", "\"미글스 씨가 그 애를 만날 수 있게 해 주시면 좋겠는데요, 웨이드 양?\""]], [["‘That is easily done,’ said she.", "\"그쯤은 쉬운 일이죠.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Come here, child.’ She had opened a door while saying this, and now led the girl in by the hand.", "\"이리 오너라, 얘야.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 문을 열어, 이제 그 아이의 손을 잡고 안으로 데리고 들어왔다."], ["It was very curious to see them standing together: the girl with her disengaged fingers plaiting the bosom of her dress, half irresolutely, half passionately; Miss Wade with her composed face attentively regarding her, and suggesting to an observer, with extraordinary force, in her composure itself (as a veil will suggest the form it covers), the unquenchable passion of her own nature.", "그 둘이 나란히 서 있는 모습은 무척 기이했다. 아이는 한가한 손가락으로 옷 앞자락을 반쯤은 우유부단하게, 반쯤은 격정적으로 주름잡고 있었고, 웨이드 양은 침착한 얼굴로 그 아이를 유심히 지켜보면서, 그 침착함 자체로(베일이 그것이 가린 형체를 떠올리게 하듯) 자기 본성 속의 꺼지지 않는 격정을 보는 이에게 유난히 강하게 암시하고 있었다."]], [["‘See here,’ she said, in the same level way as before.", "\"보세요.\" 그녀가 아까와 똑같이 차분한 어조로 말했다."], ["‘Here is your patron, your master.", "\"여기 네 후견인, 네 주인이 오셨다."], ["He is willing to take you back, my dear, if you are sensible of the favour and choose to go.", "네가 그 은혜를 알고 가겠다고 한다면, 이분은 너를 기꺼이 도로 데려가실 거란다, 얘야."], ["You can be, again, a foil to his pretty daughter, a slave to her pleasant wilfulness, and a toy in the house showing the goodness of the family.", "너는 다시 그분의 예쁜 딸을 돋보이게 하는 들러리가 되고, 그 딸의 사랑스러운 변덕에 매인 종이 되고, 집안의 선량함을 보여 주는 장난감이 될 수 있어."], ["You can have your droll name again, playfully pointing you out and setting you apart, as it is right that you should be pointed out and set apart.", "너는 다시 그 우스꽝스러운 이름을 가질 수 있지. 너를 장난스레 가리키며 따로 떼어 놓는, 그래 마땅히 너를 가리키며 따로 떼어 놓아야 할 그 이름을 말이야.(네 출생, 알지?"], ["(Your birth, you know; you must not forget your birth.) You can again be shown to this gentleman’s daughter, Harriet, and kept before her, as a living reminder of her own superiority and her gracious condescension.", "네 출생을 잊으면 안 돼.) 너는 다시 이 신사분의 따님 해리엇 앞에 내보여져, 그 딸 앞에 서서 그 딸 자신의 우월함과 너그러운 겸양을 생생히 일깨워 주는 표지로 머물 수 있어."], ["You can recover all these advantages and many more of the same kind which I dare say start up in your memory while I speak, and which you lose in taking refuge with me--you can recover them all by telling these gentlemen how humbled and penitent you are, and by going back to them to be forgiven.", "너는 이 모든 이점을, 그리고 같은 종류의 다른 많은 것들을 — 내가 말하는 동안에도 네 기억 속에서 솟아오르고 있을, 또 네가 나에게로 피신함으로써 잃게 될 그것들을 — 도로 되찾을 수 있단다. 네가 얼마나 굴복하고 뉘우치는지 이 신사분들께 말씀드리고, 용서받으러 그분들에게 돌아감으로써 모두 되찾을 수 있어."], ["What do you say, Harriet?", "어떠니, 해리엇?"], ["Will you go?’", "가겠니?\""]], [["The girl who, under the influence of these words, had gradually risen in anger and heightened in colour, answered, raising her lustrous black eyes for the moment, and clenching her hand upon the folds it had been puckering up, ‘I’d die sooner!’", "이 말들의 영향으로 점점 분노가 차오르고 얼굴빛이 짙어졌던 그 아이는, 한순간 그 윤기 흐르는 검은 눈을 치켜뜨고, 줄곧 주름잡고 있던 옷자락을 손으로 꽉 움켜쥐며 대답했다. \"차라리 죽고 말겠어요!\""]], [["Miss Wade, still standing at her side holding her hand, looked quietly round and said with a smile, ‘Gentlemen!", "웨이드 양은 여전히 그 곁에 서서 그 아이의 손을 잡은 채, 조용히 둘러보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다. \"신사분들!"], ["What do you do upon that?’", "이러시면 어떻게 하시겠어요?\""]], [["Poor Mr Meagles’s inexpressible consternation in hearing his motives and actions so perverted, had prevented him from interposing any word until now; but now he regained the power of speech.", "가엾은 미글스 씨는 자기의 동기와 행동이 그토록 비틀려 풀이되는 것을 들으며 형언할 수 없는 경악에 빠져 있던 터라 지금까지 한마디도 끼어들지 못했지만, 이제 말할 힘을 되찾았다."]], [["‘Tattycoram,’ said he, ‘for I’ll call you by that name still, my good girl, conscious that I meant nothing but kindness when I gave it to you, and conscious that you know it--’", "\"태티코럼.\" 그가 말했다. \"이 이름으로 너를 계속 부르마, 착한 아이야. 내가 이 이름을 너에게 지어 줄 때 친절 말고는 다른 뜻이 없었음을 나는 알고, 또 너도 그걸 안다는 걸 나는 아니까—\""]], [["‘I don’t!’ said she, looking up again, and almost rending herself with the same busy hand.", "\"몰라요!\" 그 아이가 다시 고개를 들며, 그 분주한 손으로 제 몸을 거의 쥐어뜯다시피 하며 말했다."]], [["‘No, not now, perhaps,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘not with that lady’s eyes so intent upon you, Tattycoram,’ she glanced at them for a moment, ‘and that power over you, which we see she exercises; not now, perhaps, but at another time. Tattycoram, I’ll not ask that lady whether she believes what she has said, even in the anger and ill blood in which I and my friend here equally know she has spoken, though she subdues herself, with a determination that any one who has once seen her is not likely to forget. I’ll not ask you, with your remembrance of my house and all belonging to it, whether you believe it. I’ll only say that you have no profession to make to me or mine, and no forgiveness to entreat; and that all in the world that I ask you to do, is, to count five-and-twenty, Tattycoram.’", "\"그래, 지금은 모를지도 모르지.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"저 부인의 눈이 너를 그토록 뚫어지게 보고 있는 동안에는, 태티코럼.\" 그 아이가 잠시 그 눈을 흘끗 보았다. \"또 저분이 너에게 휘두르는 게 우리 눈에도 보이는 저 힘이 너를 사로잡고 있는 동안에는 말이다. 지금은 모를지 모르지만, 다른 때에는 알게 될 거다. 태티코럼, 나는 저 부인께, 저분이 한 말을 정말 믿으시는지 여쭙지 않으마. 비록 나와 여기 내 친구가 똑같이 알고 있듯, 저분이 분노와 악감정 속에서 그 말을 하시면서도, 한 번이라도 본 사람이라면 잊기 어려울 그 결연함으로 자신을 억누르고 계신다 해도 말이다. 너에게도, 우리 집과 거기 딸린 모든 것에 대한 네 기억을 두고, 그 말을 믿느냐고 묻지 않으마. 다만 나는 이 한 가지만 말하겠다. 너는 나나 내 식구에게 굴복을 고백할 것도, 용서를 빌 것도 없다는 것을. 그리고 이 세상에서 내가 너에게 바라는 단 한 가지는, 스물다섯을 세는 것, 그뿐이라는 것을, 태티코럼.\""]], [["She looked at him for an instant, and then said frowningly, ‘I won’t.", "그 아이는 한순간 그를 바라보다가, 이내 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"싫어요."], ["Miss Wade, take me away, please.’", "웨이드 양, 제발 저를 데려가 주세요.\""]], [["The contention that raged within her had no softening in it now; it was wholly between passionate defiance and stubborn defiance.", "이제 그 아이 안에서 들끓는 갈등에는 누그러짐이라곤 없었다. 그것은 온전히 격정적인 반항과 완고한 반항 사이의 다툼이었다."], ["Her rich colour, her quick blood, her rapid breath, were all setting themselves against the opportunity of retracing their steps.", "짙은 얼굴빛도, 빠르게 도는 피도, 가쁜 숨도, 모두 걸음을 되돌릴 그 기회에 맞서 버티고 있었다."], ["‘I won’t.", "\"싫어요."], ["I won’t.", "싫어요."], ["I won’t!’ she repeated in a low, thick voice.", "싫어요!\" 그 아이가 낮고 잠긴 목소리로 되풀이했다."], ["‘I’d be torn to pieces first.", "\"차라리 갈가리 찢기는 게 나아요."], ["I’d tear myself to pieces first!’", "차라리 제 손으로 저를 갈가리 찢어 버리겠어요!\""]], [["Miss Wade, who had released her hold, laid her hand protectingly on the girl’s neck for a moment, and then said, looking round with her former smile and speaking exactly in her former tone, ‘Gentlemen!", "붙잡고 있던 손을 놓았던 웨이드 양은, 한순간 그 아이의 목덜미에 보호하듯 손을 얹었다가, 이내 아까처럼 미소를 띠며 둘러보고, 정확히 아까와 똑같은 어조로 말했다. \"신사분들!"], ["What do you do upon that?’", "이러시면 어떻게 하시겠어요?\""]], [["‘Oh, Tattycoram, Tattycoram!’ cried Mr Meagles, adjuring her besides with an earnest hand.", "\"오, 태티코럼, 태티코럼!\" 미글스 씨가 외치며, 거기에 더해 간절한 손짓으로 그 아이에게 호소했다."], ["‘Hear that lady’s voice, look at that lady’s face, consider what is in that lady’s heart, and think what a future lies before you.", "\"저 부인의 목소리를 들어 보고, 저 부인의 얼굴을 보고, 저 부인의 가슴속에 무엇이 들었는지 헤아려 보고, 네 앞에 어떤 미래가 놓여 있는지 생각해 보아라."], ["My child, whatever you may think, that lady’s influence over you--astonishing to us, and I should hardly go too far in saying terrible to us to see--is founded in passion fiercer than yours, and temper more violent than yours.", "얘야, 네가 어떻게 생각하든, 저 부인이 너에게 미치는 영향력은 — 우리에게도 놀랍고, 보고 있자니 끔찍하다고 해도 지나치지 않을 그 영향력은 — 너의 것보다 더 사나운 격정, 너의 것보다 더 격렬한 성미에 뿌리를 두고 있단다."], ["What can you two be together?", "너희 둘이 함께 무엇이 될 수 있겠니?"], ["What can come of it?’", "거기서 무엇이 나올 수 있겠어?\""]], [["‘I am alone here, gentlemen,’ observed Miss Wade, with no change of voice or manner.", "\"저는 이곳에서 혼자랍니다, 신사분들.\" 웨이드 양이 목소리나 태도에 아무 변화 없이 말했다."], ["‘Say anything you will.’", "\"하고 싶은 말씀은 무엇이든 하세요.\""]], [["‘Politeness must yield to this misguided girl, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘at her present pass; though I hope not altogether to dismiss it, even with the injury you do her so strongly before me.", "\"이 그릇 인도된 아이를 위해서는 예의를 접어 두는 수밖에 없겠습니다, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Excuse me for reminding you in her hearing--I must say it--that you were a mystery to all of us, and had nothing in common with any of us when she unfortunately fell in your way.", "\"지금 이 막다른 처지에서는 말입니다. 그렇다 해도, 당신이 내 눈앞에서 이토록 노골적으로 이 아이를 해치고 있음에도, 예의를 아주 내던지고 싶지는 않습니다만."], ["I don’t know what you are, but you don’t hide, can’t hide, what a dark spirit you have within you.", "이 애가 듣는 데서 일깨워 드리는 걸 용서하십시오 — 이 말은 꼭 해야겠습니다 — 이 애가 불행히도 당신과 마주쳤을 때, 당신은 우리 모두에게 수수께끼였고, 우리 누구와도 통하는 데가 없었습니다."], ["If it should happen that you are a woman, who, from whatever cause, has a perverted delight in making a sister-woman as wretched as she is (I am old enough to have heard of such), I warn her against you, and I warn you against yourself.’", "나는 당신이 어떤 사람인지 모르지만, 당신은 자기 안에 얼마나 어두운 영혼을 품고 있는지를 숨기지 못하고, 숨길 수도 없습니다. 만일 당신이, 어떤 까닭에서든, 같은 여인을 자기만큼이나 비참하게 만드는 데서 비뚤어진 기쁨을 느끼는 여자라면(나는 그런 이야기를 들을 만큼은 나이를 먹었습니다), 나는 이 애에게 당신을 경계하라 경고하고, 또 당신에게도 당신 자신을 경계하라 경고하는 바입니다.\""]], [["‘Gentlemen!’ said Miss Wade, calmly.", "\"신사분들!\" 웨이드 양이 차분하게 말했다."], ["‘When you have concluded--Mr Clennam, perhaps you will induce your friend--’", "\"이야기가 끝나셨거든 — 클레넘 씨, 부디 당신 친구분을 좀—\""]], [["‘Not without another effort,’ said Mr Meagles, stoutly.", "\"한 번 더 애써 보지 않고서는 안 됩니다.\" 미글스 씨가 꿋꿋이 말했다."], ["‘Tattycoram, my poor dear girl, count five-and-twenty.’", "\"태티코럼, 가엾은 내 아가야, 스물다섯을 세거라.\""]], [["‘Do not reject the hope, the certainty, this kind man offers you,’ said Clennam in a low emphatic voice.", "\"이 친절한 분이 너에게 주시는 희망을, 그 확실함을 물리치지 말거라.\" 클레넘이 나직하지만 힘 있는 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘Turn to the friends you have not forgotten.", "\"네가 잊지 않은 친구들에게로 돌아오너라."], ["Think once more!’", "한 번만 더 생각해!\""]], [["‘I won’t!", "\"싫어요! 웨이드 양,\""], ["Miss Wade,’ said the girl, with her bosom swelling high, and speaking with her hand held to her throat, ‘take me away!’", "그 아이가 가슴을 한껏 부풀리며, 손을 목에 갖다 댄 채 말했다. \"저를 데려가 주세요!\""]], [["‘Tattycoram,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"태티코럼.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Once more yet!", "\"다시 한번만 더!"], ["The only thing I ask of you in the world, my child!", "이 세상에서 내가 너에게 바라는 단 한 가지다, 얘야!"], ["Count five-and-twenty!’", "스물다섯을 세거라!\""]], [["She put her hands tightly over her ears, confusedly tumbling down her bright black hair in the vehemence of the action, and turned her face resolutely to the wall.", "그 아이는 두 손으로 귀를 꽉 막았는데, 그 격렬한 동작에 윤기 흐르는 검은 머리채가 어지러이 흘러내렸다. 그러고는 단호히 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["Miss Wade, who had watched her under this final appeal with that strange attentive smile, and that repressing hand upon her own bosom with which she had watched her in her struggle at Marseilles, then put her arm about her waist as if she took possession of her for evermore.", "웨이드 양은, 마르세유에서 그 아이가 몸부림칠 때 지켜보던 그 기묘하고도 주의 깊은 미소를 띠고, 또 그때 자기 가슴에 얹어 억누르던 그 손짓 그대로, 이 마지막 호소 아래 그 아이를 지켜보고 있다가, 마치 그 아이를 영영 자기 소유로 삼는다는 듯, 그 허리에 팔을 둘렀다."]], [["And there was a visible triumph in her face when she turned it to dismiss the visitors.", "그리고 그녀가 손님들을 내보내려고 얼굴을 돌렸을 때, 그 얼굴에는 뚜렷한 승리의 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["‘As it is the last time I shall have the honour,’ she said, ‘and as you have spoken of not knowing what I am, and also of the foundation of my influence here, you may now know that it is founded in a common cause.", "\"이번이 그 영광을 누릴 마지막일 테니,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["What your broken plaything is as to birth, I am.", "\"또 두 분이 내가 어떤 사람인지 모르겠다고, 그리고 여기서 내 영향력이 어디에 뿌리를 두고 있느냐고 말씀하셨으니, 이제 그것이 공통의 사연에 뿌리를 두고 있음을 아셔도 되겠지요. 당신이 부숴 버린 그 장난감이 출생에 있어 어떠한지, 나도 똑같습니다."], ["She has no name, I have no name.", "그 애에게 이름이 없듯, 나도 이름이 없어요."], ["Her wrong is my wrong.", "그 애가 받은 부당함이 곧 내가 받은 부당함입니다."], ["I have nothing more to say to you.’", "나는 두 분께 더 드릴 말씀이 없군요.\""]], [["This was addressed to Mr Meagles, who sorrowfully went out.", "이 말은 미글스 씨를 향한 것이었고, 그는 비탄에 잠겨 밖으로 나갔다."], ["As Clennam followed, she said to him, with the same external composure and in the same level voice, but with a smile that is only seen on cruel faces: a very faint smile, lifting the nostril, scarcely touching the lips, and not breaking away gradually, but instantly dismissed when done with:", "클레넘이 뒤따르자, 그녀는 똑같이 겉으로는 침착한 태도로, 똑같이 차분한 목소리로, 그러나 잔인한 얼굴에서만 볼 수 있는 미소를 띠고 그에게 말했다. 콧방울을 살짝 들어 올리고, 입술은 거의 건드리지도 않으며, 차츰 스러지는 것이 아니라 할 일이 끝나자마자 즉시 거두어지는, 아주 희미한 미소였다."]], [["‘I hope the wife of your dear friend Mr Gowan, may be happy in the contrast of her extraction to this girl’s and mine, and in the high good fortune that awaits her.’", "\"당신의 그 소중한 친구 가우언 씨의 부인이, 자기 혈통과 이 아이와 나의 혈통이 빚어내는 그 대비에서, 그리고 자기를 기다리고 있는 그 드높은 행운에서, 부디 행복하기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER 28. Nobody’s Disappearance", "# CHAPTER 28. Nobody’s Disappearance"]], [["Not resting satisfied with the endeavours he had made to recover his lost charge, Mr Meagles addressed a letter of remonstrance, breathing nothing but goodwill, not only to her, but to Miss Wade too.", "잃어버린 피보호자를 되찾으려 기울인 노력에 만족하지 못한 미글스 씨는, 오로지 선의로만 가득 찬 충고의 편지를, 그녀에게뿐 아니라 웨이드 양에게도 써 보냈다."], ["No answer coming to these epistles, or to another written to the stubborn girl by the hand of her late young mistress, which might have melted her if anything could (all three letters were returned weeks afterwards as having been refused at the house-door), he deputed Mrs Meagles to make the experiment of a personal interview.", "이 서한들에도, 또 옛 젊은 여주인의 손으로 그 고집 센 처녀에게 쓴 또 한 통의 편지에도—만약 무엇이라도 그녀의 마음을 누그러뜨릴 수 있었다면 그 편지가 그러했을 텐데—아무런 답장이 오지 않자(세 통의 편지는 몇 주 뒤 모두 집 문간에서 수취가 거절되었다는 표시와 함께 되돌아왔다), 그는 미글스 부인에게 직접 면담을 시도하는 임무를 맡겼다."], ["That worthy lady being unable to obtain one, and being steadfastly denied admission, Mr Meagles besought Arthur to essay once more what he could do.", "그 갸륵한 부인이 면담을 얻지 못하고 입장을 한사코 거절당하자, 미글스 씨는 아서에게 다시 한번 할 수 있는 일을 해 달라고 간청했다."], ["All that came of his compliance was, his discovery that the empty house was left in charge of the old woman, that Miss Wade was gone, that the waifs and strays of furniture were gone, and that the old woman would accept any number of half-crowns and thank the donor kindly, but had no information whatever to exchange for those coins, beyond constantly offering for perusal a memorandum relative to fixtures, which the house-agent’s young man had left in the hall.", "그가 응한 결과 알게 된 것이라곤, 텅 빈 집이 한 노파의 관리에 맡겨져 있다는 것, 웨이드 양은 떠나 버렸다는 것, 떠돌이 같던 가구붙이들도 사라졌다는 것, 그리고 그 노파는 반 크라운짜리 동전을 몇 개든 받고 주는 이에게 친절히 고맙다고 인사하면서도, 그 동전과 맞바꿀 만한 정보라곤 전혀 없이, 다만 집 중개인의 젊은 사내가 현관에 두고 간 비품에 관한 비망록을 끊임없이 들여다보라고 내밀 뿐이라는 것이었다."]], [["Unwilling, even under this discomfiture, to resign the ingrate and leave her hopeless, in case of her better dispositions obtaining the mastery over the darker side of her character, Mr Meagles, for six successive days, published a discreetly covert advertisement in the morning papers, to the effect that if a certain young person who had lately left home without reflection, would at any time apply to his address at Twickenham, everything would be as it had been before, and no reproaches need be apprehended.", "이런 좌절 속에서도, 그 배은망덕한 처녀의 더 나은 성품이 그 성격의 어두운 면을 이겨 낼 경우를 대비하여, 그녀를 단념하고 절망에 빠뜨려 두기를 꺼린 미글스 씨는, 엿새 동안 연이어 조간신문에 신중하게 에둘러 쓴 광고를 실었다. 그 내용인즉, 최근 깊이 생각지 않고 집을 떠난 어느 젊은 사람이 언제든 트위크넘에 있는 자기 주소로 찾아온다면 모든 것이 예전 그대로 돌아갈 것이며 아무런 책망도 걱정할 필요가 없다는 것이었다."], ["The unexpected consequences of this notification suggested to the dismayed Mr Meagles for the first time that some hundreds of young persons must be leaving their homes without reflection every day; for shoals of wrong young people came down to Twickenham, who, not finding themselves received with enthusiasm, generally demanded compensation by way of damages, in addition to coach-hire there and back.", "이 통지가 불러온 뜻밖의 결과로, 당황한 미글스 씨는 비로소 깊이 생각지 않고 집을 떠나는 젊은 사람이 날마다 수백 명은 되겠구나 하고 처음 깨달았다. 엉뚱한 젊은이들이 떼를 지어 트위크넘으로 몰려왔는데, 열렬한 환대를 받지 못하자 대개는 오가는 마차 삯에 더해 손해배상 조로 보상을 요구했기 때문이다."], ["Nor were these the only uninvited clients whom the advertisement produced.", "그리고 이런 자들만이 그 광고가 불러낸 청하지 않은 손님은 아니었다."], ["The swarm of begging-letter writers, who would seem to be always watching eagerly for any hook, however small, to hang a letter upon, wrote to say that having seen the advertisement, they were induced to apply with confidence for various sums, ranging from ten shillings to fifty pounds: not because they knew anything about the young person, but because they felt that to part with those donations would greatly relieve the advertiser’s mind.", "아무리 작은 갈고리라도 거기에 편지 한 통을 걸 만한 구실이라면 늘 눈에 불을 켜고 노리는 듯한 구걸 편지꾼들이 떼로 달려들어, 광고를 보고 자신 있게 청하게 되었다며 십 실링에서 오십 파운드에 이르는 갖가지 액수를 요구했다. 그 젊은 사람에 관해 무엇을 알아서가 아니라, 그런 기부금을 내놓으면 광고주의 마음이 크게 가벼워지리라 느꼈기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["Several projectors, likewise, availed themselves of the same opportunity to correspond with Mr Meagles; as, for example, to apprise him that their attention having been called to the advertisement by a friend, they begged to state that if they should ever hear anything of the young person, they would not fail to make it known to him immediately, and that in the meantime if he would oblige them with the funds necessary for bringing to perfection a certain entirely novel description of Pump, the happiest results would ensue to mankind.", "마찬가지로 여러 사업가들도 이 기회를 틈타 미글스 씨와 서신을 주고받았다. 이를테면, 친구가 그 광고를 알려 주어 주의를 기울이게 되었다며, 만일 그 젊은 사람에 대해 무엇이든 듣게 되면 즉시 알려 드리기를 게을리하지 않겠고, 그동안 어떤 전혀 새로운 종류의 양수기를 완성하는 데 필요한 자금을 베풀어 주신다면 인류에게 더없이 행복한 결과가 따를 것이라고 알려 오는 식이었다."]], [["Mr Meagles and his family, under these combined discouragements, had begun reluctantly to give up Tattycoram as irrecoverable, when the new and active firm of Doyce and Clennam, in their private capacities, went down on a Saturday to stay at the cottage until Monday.", "미글스 씨와 그의 식구들은 이런 갖가지 낙담이 겹친 끝에 마지못해 태티코람을 영영 되찾을 수 없는 사람으로 단념하기 시작했는데, 그 무렵 새로 의욕에 차서 일하던 도이스 앤드 클레넘 상회의 두 사람이 개인 자격으로 토요일에 그 별장에 내려와 월요일까지 머물게 되었다."], ["The senior partner took the coach, and the junior partner took his walking-stick.", "선임 동업자는 마차를 탔고, 후임 동업자는 지팡이를 짚었다."]], [["A tranquil summer sunset shone upon him as he approached the end of his walk, and passed through the meadows by the river side.", "산책의 끝에 다다라 강가의 풀밭을 가로질러 갈 무렵, 고요한 여름 석양이 그를 비추었다."], ["He had that sense of peace, and of being lightened of a weight of care, which country quiet awakens in the breasts of dwellers in towns.", "그에게는 도시에 사는 이들의 가슴에 시골의 정적이 불러일으키는 그 평화로움이, 근심의 무게에서 벗어난 듯한 그 느낌이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Everything within his view was lovely and placid.", "그의 눈에 들어오는 모든 것이 사랑스럽고 잔잔했다."], ["The rich foliage of the trees, the luxuriant grass diversified with wild flowers, the little green islands in the river, the beds of rushes, the water-lilies floating on the surface of the stream, the distant voices in boats borne musically towards him on the ripple of the water and the evening air, were all expressive of rest.", "나무들의 풍성한 잎새, 들꽃이 어우러져 무성한 풀, 강 위의 작은 초록 섬들, 골풀이 우거진 곳, 물줄기 표면에 떠 있는 수련, 잔물결과 저녁 공기에 실려 음악처럼 그에게 다가오는 보트 위의 먼 목소리들, 이 모두가 안식을 말하고 있었다."], ["In the occasional leap of a fish, or dip of an oar, or twittering of a bird not yet at roost, or distant barking of a dog, or lowing of a cow--in all such sounds, there was the prevailing breath of rest, which seemed to encompass him in every scent that sweetened the fragrant air.", "이따금 물고기가 튀어 오르는 소리, 노가 물에 잠기는 소리, 아직 둥지에 들지 않은 새의 지저귐, 멀리서 들리는 개 짖는 소리, 소가 우는 소리—이 모든 소리에는 안식의 기운이 가득히 배어 있었고, 그것은 향긋한 공기를 달콤하게 하는 온갖 냄새 속에서 그를 감싸는 듯했다."], ["The long lines of red and gold in the sky, and the glorious track of the descending sun, were all divinely calm.", "하늘에 길게 뻗은 붉고 금빛인 띠들, 그리고 지는 해가 그려 놓은 찬란한 자취가 모두 거룩하리만치 고요했다."], ["Upon the purple tree-tops far away, and on the green height near at hand up which the shades were slowly creeping, there was an equal hush.", "멀리 보랏빛으로 물든 나무 우듬지 위에도, 어스름이 천천히 기어오르는 가까운 초록 언덕 위에도, 한결같은 정적이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Between the real landscape and its shadow in the water, there was no division; both were so untroubled and clear, and, while so fraught with solemn mystery of life and death, so hopefully reassuring to the gazer’s soothed heart, because so tenderly and mercifully beautiful.", "실재하는 풍경과 물에 비친 그 그림자 사이에는 아무런 경계가 없었다. 둘 다 그토록 잔잔하고 맑았으며, 삶과 죽음의 엄숙한 신비로 가득 차 있으면서도, 그토록 부드럽고 자비롭게 아름다웠기에 바라보는 이의 누그러진 마음에 그토록 희망차게 위안을 주었다."]], [["Clennam had stopped, not for the first time by many times, to look about him and suffer what he saw to sink into his soul, as the shadows, looked at, seemed to sink deeper and deeper into the water.", "클레넘은 한두 번이 아니라 여러 번이나 걸음을 멈추고 주위를 둘러보며, 바라보고 있는 그림자가 물속으로 점점 더 깊이 가라앉는 듯이, 자기가 본 것이 영혼 속으로 가라앉도록 내버려 두곤 했다."], ["He was slowly resuming his way, when he saw a figure in the path before him which he had, perhaps, already associated with the evening and its impressions.", "그가 천천히 다시 길을 가려는데, 앞쪽 오솔길에 한 사람의 모습이 보였다. 어쩌면 그가 이미 이 저녁과 그 정취에 결부시켜 떠올렸을지도 모를 모습이었다."]], [["Minnie was there, alone.", "미니가 거기, 혼자 있었다."], ["She had some roses in her hand, and seemed to have stood still on seeing him, waiting for him.", "손에 장미 몇 송이를 들고서, 그를 보자 가만히 멈추어 서서 그를 기다린 듯했다."], ["Her face was towards him, and she appeared to have been coming from the opposite direction.", "그녀는 그를 향해 얼굴을 돌리고 있었는데, 반대편에서 오던 모양이었다."], ["There was a flutter in her manner, which Clennam had never seen in it before; and as he came near her, it entered his mind all at once that she was there of a set purpose to speak to him.", "그녀의 거동에는 클레넘이 여태껏 본 적 없는 떨림이 어려 있었다. 그리고 그가 가까이 다가가자, 그녀가 작정하고 자기에게 말을 건네려고 거기 와 있다는 생각이 문득 그의 마음에 떠올랐다."]], [["She gave him her hand, and said, ‘You wonder to see me here by myself?", "그녀가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"제가 여기 혼자 있는 게 의아하시죠?"], ["But the evening is so lovely, I have strolled further than I meant at first.", "하지만 저녁이 너무 고와서, 처음 생각했던 것보다 멀리까지 거닐어 왔어요."], ["I thought it likely I might meet you, and that made me more confident.", "어쩌면 당신을 만날지도 모른다고 생각했고, 그러니 더 마음이 놓였답니다."], ["You always come this way, do you not?’", "늘 이 길로 오시잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["As Clennam said that it was his favourite way, he felt her hand falter on his arm, and saw the roses shake.", "이 길을 제일 좋아한다고 클레넘이 말할 때, 그는 그녀의 손이 자기 팔 위에서 흔들리는 것을 느꼈고 장미가 떨리는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Will you let me give you one, Mr Clennam?", "\"한 송이 드려도 될까요, 클레넘 씨?"], ["I gathered them as I came out of the garden.", "정원에서 나오면서 꺾은 거예요."], ["Indeed, I almost gathered them for you, thinking it so likely I might meet you.", "사실은, 당신을 만날 것 같다는 생각에 거의 당신을 위해 꺾은 거나 다름없어요."], ["Mr Doyce arrived more than an hour ago, and told us you were walking down.’", "도이스 씨는 한 시간도 더 전에 도착하셔서, 당신이 걸어서 내려오고 있다고 말씀해 주셨거든요.\""]], [["His own hand shook, as he accepted a rose or two from hers and thanked her.", "그녀의 손에서 장미 한두 송이를 받아 고맙다고 말할 때, 그 자신의 손도 떨렸다."], ["They were now by an avenue of trees.", "두 사람은 어느새 나무가 늘어선 가로수길에 이르렀다."], ["Whether they turned into it on his movement or on hers matters little.", "그의 움직임으로 그리로 들어섰는지 그녀의 움직임으로 그랬는지는 대수롭지 않다."], ["He never knew how that was.", "그는 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지 끝내 알지 못했다."]], [["‘It is very grave here,’ said Clennam, ‘but very pleasant at this hour.", "\"여기는 무척 엄숙하군요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Passing along this deep shade, and out at that arch of light at the other end, we come upon the ferry and the cottage by the best approach, I think.’", "\"하지만 이 시각에는 무척 정겹습니다. 이 깊은 그늘을 지나 저쪽 끝의 저 빛의 아치로 빠져나가면, 가장 좋은 길로 나루터와 별장에 닿게 되지요, 그렇게 생각합니다.\""]], [["In her simple garden-hat and her light summer dress, with her rich brown hair naturally clustering about her, and her wonderful eyes raised to his for a moment with a look in which regard for him and trustfulness in him were strikingly blended with a kind of timid sorrow for him, she was so beautiful that it was well for his peace--or ill for his peace, he did not quite know which--that he had made that vigorous resolution he had so often thought about.", "소박한 정원 모자를 쓰고 가벼운 여름 옷을 입은 채, 풍성한 갈색 머리칼을 자연스레 얼굴 둘레에 드리우고서, 그 놀라운 두 눈을 잠시 그에게로 들어 올렸을 때—그 눈빛에는 그에 대한 존중과 그를 향한 신뢰가, 그를 향한 일종의 수줍은 슬픔과 두드러지게 어우러져 있었다—그녀는 너무도 아름다워서, 그가 그토록 자주 곱씹어 온 그 굳은 결심을 세워 두었던 것이 그의 마음의 평안을 위해 다행이었다—아니, 어쩌면 불행이었는지도, 그는 어느 쪽인지 확실히 알 수 없었다."]], [["She broke a momentary silence by inquiring if he knew that papa had been thinking of another tour abroad?", "그녀가 잠시의 침묵을 깨고, 아빠가 또 한 번 외국 여행을 생각하고 계신 걸 아느냐고 물었다."], ["He said he had heard it mentioned.", "그는 그런 얘기를 들었다고 말했다."], ["She broke another momentary silence by adding, with some hesitation, that papa had abandoned the idea.", "그녀가 또 잠시의 침묵을 깨고, 약간 머뭇거리며, 아빠가 그 생각을 접으셨다고 덧붙였다."]], [["At this, he thought directly, ‘they are to be married.’", "이 말에 그는 곧장 생각했다. '저들이 결혼하려는 거구나.'"]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ she said, hesitating more timidly yet, and speaking so low that he bent his head to hear her.", "\"클레넘 씨.\" 그녀가 한층 더 수줍게 머뭇거리며, 너무도 낮은 목소리로 말해서 그가 들으려고 고개를 숙였다."], ["‘I should very much like to give you my confidence, if you would not mind having the goodness to receive it.", "\"정말이지 제 속마음을 당신께 털어놓고 싶어요, 부디 받아 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주신다면요."], ["I should have very much liked to have given it to you long ago, because--I felt that you were becoming so much our friend.’", "오래전부터 그러고 싶었어요. 왜냐하면—당신이 우리에게 그토록 좋은 친구가 되어 가고 계신다고 느꼈으니까요.\""]], [["‘How can I be otherwise than proud of it at any time!", "\"언제든 그것을 자랑스러워하지 않을 도리가 어디 있겠습니까!"], ["Pray give it to me.", "부디 제게 털어놓으세요."], ["Pray trust me.’", "부디 저를 믿으세요.\""]], [["‘I could never have been afraid of trusting you,’ she returned, raising her eyes frankly to his face.", "\"당신을 믿기를 두려워한 적은 한 번도 없었어요.\" 그녀가 솔직한 눈길을 그의 얼굴로 들어 올리며 대답했다."], ["‘I think I would have done so some time ago, if I had known how.", "\"어떻게 하는지만 알았다면 얼마 전에라도 그렇게 했을 거예요."], ["But I scarcely know how, even now.’", "하지만 지금도 어떻게 해야 할지 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Mr Gowan,’ said Arthur Clennam, ‘has reason to be very happy.", "\"가우언 씨는,\" 아서 클레넘이 말했다."], ["God bless his wife and him!’", "\"무척 행복할 만한 사람입니다. 그의 아내와 그에게 하느님의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["She wept, as she tried to thank him.", "그녀는 그에게 고맙다고 말하려 애쓰면서 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["He reassured her, took her hand as it lay with the trembling roses in it on his arm, took the remaining roses from it, and put it to his lips.", "그는 그녀를 안심시키며, 떨리는 장미와 함께 그의 팔 위에 놓여 있던 그녀의 손을 잡고, 거기 남은 장미를 마저 빼낸 뒤, 그 손을 입술에 가져갔다."], ["At that time, it seemed to him, he first finally resigned the dying hope that had flickered in nobody’s heart so much to its pain and trouble; and from that time he became in his own eyes, as to any similar hope or prospect, a very much older man who had done with that part of life.", "그때, 그에게는 누구의 가슴속에선가 그토록 큰 고통과 괴로움으로 깜박이던 그 죽어 가는 희망을 마침내 처음으로 내려놓은 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그리고 그때부터 그는 그와 비슷한 희망이나 전망에 관한 한, 제 눈에 스스로가 그런 삶의 한 부분을 끝내 버린, 훨씬 나이 든 사람이 되었다."]], [["He put the roses in his breast and they walked on for a little while, slowly and silently, under the umbrageous trees.", "그는 장미를 가슴에 꽂았고, 두 사람은 그늘진 나무들 아래를 잠시 동안 천천히, 말없이 걸었다."], ["Then he asked her, in a voice of cheerful kindness, was there anything else that she would say to him as her friend and her father’s friend, many years older than herself; was there any trust she would repose in him, any service she would ask of him, any little aid to her happiness that she could give him the lasting gratification of believing it was in his power to render?", "이윽고 그는 명랑하고 다정한 목소리로 그녀에게 물었다. 그녀의 친구이자 그녀의 아버지의 친구로서, 자기보다 여러 해 나이가 많은 사람으로서—그에게 더 들려줄 말은 없는지, 그에게 맡겨 둘 어떤 믿음은 없는지, 부탁할 어떤 도움은 없는지, 그녀의 행복에 보탬이 될 어떤 작은 일이라도, 그것이 자기 힘으로 해 줄 수 있는 일이라고 믿는 영원한 기쁨을 자기에게 줄 만한 것은 없는지."]], [["She was going to answer, when she was so touched by some little hidden sorrow or sympathy--what could it have been?--that she said, bursting into tears again: ‘O Mr Clennam!", "그녀가 막 대답하려는데, 어떤 작은, 숨겨진 슬픔인지 연민인지에—그것이 무엇이었을까?—너무도 마음이 동해서, 다시 눈물을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"오, 클레넘 씨!"], ["Good, generous, Mr Clennam, pray tell me you do not blame me.’", "선량하고 너그러우신 클레넘 씨, 제발 저를 탓하지 않는다고 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["‘I blame you?’ said Clennam.", "\"제가 당신을 탓한다고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘My dearest girl!", "\"사랑하는 아가씨!"], ["I blame you?", "제가 당신을 탓해요?"], ["No!’", "아니요!\""]], [["After clasping both her hands upon his arm, and looking confidentially up into his face, with some hurried words to the effect that she thanked him from her heart (as she did, if it be the source of earnestness), she gradually composed herself, with now and then a word of encouragement from him, as they walked on slowly and almost silently under the darkening trees.", "그녀는 두 손으로 그의 팔을 꼭 붙잡고, 미덥게 그의 얼굴을 올려다보며, 진심으로 그에게 감사한다는 뜻의 다급한 몇 마디를 더듬더듬 내뱉고는(만약 진심이 그런 간절함의 원천이라면, 그녀는 정말로 그러했다), 어두워져 가는 나무들 아래를 천천히, 거의 말없이 걸어가는 동안, 이따금 그가 건네는 격려의 한마디에 힘입어 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다."]], [["‘And, now, Minnie Gowan,’ at length said Clennam, smiling; ‘will you ask me nothing?’", "\"자, 그럼, 미니 가우언.\" 마침내 클레넘이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"제게 부탁할 게 아무것도 없나요?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I have very much to ask of you.’", "부탁드릴 게 무척 많아요.\""]], [["‘That’s well!", "\"그거 잘됐군요!"], ["I hope so; I am not disappointed.’", "그러길 바랐답니다. 실망하지 않았어요.\""]], [["‘You know how I am loved at home, and how I love home. You can hardly think it perhaps, dear Mr Clennam,’ she spoke with great agitation, ‘seeing me going from it of my own free will and choice, but I do so dearly love it!’", "\"제가 집에서 얼마나 사랑받는지, 또 제가 집을 얼마나 사랑하는지 아시잖아요. 어쩌면 잘 믿기지 않으실지도 몰라요, 사랑하는 클레넘 씨.\" 그녀가 몹시 동요하며 말했다. \"제가 제 자유의지와 선택으로 집을 떠나는 걸 보시면서요. 하지만 저는 그토록 진심으로 집을 사랑한답니다!\""]], [["‘I am sure of that,’ said Clennam.", "\"그건 저도 확신합니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘Can you suppose I doubt it?’", "\"제가 그걸 의심한다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["‘No, no.", "\"아니요, 아니요."], ["But it is strange, even to me, that loving it so much and being so much beloved in it, I can bear to cast it away.", "하지만 제 자신에게도 이상하게 여겨져요. 그토록 집을 사랑하고 그 안에서 그토록 사랑받으면서도, 그걸 내던져 버릴 수 있다니."], ["It seems so neglectful of it, so unthankful.’", "너무도 집을 소홀히 하는 것 같고, 너무도 배은망덕한 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘My dear girl,’ said Clennam, ‘it is in the natural progress and change of time.", "\"사랑하는 아가씨.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["All homes are left so.’", "\"그건 세월의 자연스러운 흐름이고 변화입니다. 모든 가정이 다 그렇게 떠나가는 법이죠.\""]], [["‘Yes, I know; but all homes are not left with such a blank in them as there will be in mine when I am gone.", "\"네, 알아요. 하지만 모든 가정이 다, 제가 떠난 뒤 우리 집에 생길 만큼 큰 빈자리를 남기며 떠나는 건 아니에요."], ["Not that there is any scarcity of far better and more endearing and more accomplished girls than I am; not that I am much, but that they have made so much of me!’", "저보다 훨씬 더 낫고, 훨씬 더 사랑스럽고, 훨씬 더 재능 있는 처녀들이 부족해서가 아니라, 제가 대단해서가 아니라, 식구들이 저를 그토록 대단하게 여겨 주었기 때문이에요!\""]], [["Pet’s affectionate heart was overcharged, and she sobbed while she pictured what would happen.", "펫의 다정한 마음은 벅차올랐고, 그녀는 앞으로 벌어질 일을 그려 보며 흐느꼈다."]], [["‘I know what a change papa will feel at first, and I know that at first I cannot be to him anything like what I have been these many years.", "\"아빠가 처음에 어떤 변화를 느끼실지 저는 알아요. 그리고 처음에는 제가 이 여러 해 동안 그래 왔던 만큼 아빠에게 무엇이 되어 드릴 수 없으리란 것도 알아요."], ["And it is then, Mr Clennam, then more than at any time, that I beg and entreat you to remember him, and sometimes to keep him company when you can spare a little while; and to tell him that you know I was fonder of him when I left him, than I ever was in all my life.", "바로 그때예요, 클레넘 씨, 다른 어느 때보다도 바로 그때, 제가 당신께 빌고 또 간청드려요. 아빠를 기억해 주시고, 짬을 좀 낼 수 있으실 때 이따금 아빠와 함께해 주시기를요."], ["For there is nobody--he told me so himself when he talked to me this very day--there is nobody he likes so well as you, or trusts so much.’", "그리고 제가 아빠를 떠날 때 평생 어느 때보다도 아빠를 더 사랑했다는 걸 당신이 안다고 아빠께 말씀드려 주세요. 왜냐하면 아무도 없으니까요—바로 오늘 저와 이야기하실 때 아빠가 직접 제게 그렇게 말씀하셨어요—아빠가 당신만큼 좋아하는 사람도, 그토록 믿는 사람도 없으니까요.\""]], [["A clue to what had passed between the father and daughter dropped like a heavy stone into the well of Clennam’s heart, and swelled the water to his eyes.", "아버지와 딸 사이에 오간 일에 대한 실마리가 무거운 돌처럼 클레넘의 가슴속 우물에 떨어져, 그 물을 눈가에까지 차오르게 했다."], ["He said, cheerily, but not quite so cheerily as he tried to say, that it should be done--that he gave her his faithful promise.", "그는 명랑하게, 그러나 그러려 애쓴 만큼 썩 명랑하지는 못하게, 그렇게 하겠노라고—성심껏 약속하겠노라고 말했다."]], [["‘If I do not speak of mama,’ said Pet, more moved by, and more pretty in, her innocent grief, than Clennam could trust himself even to consider--for which reason he counted the trees between them and the fading light as they slowly diminished in number--‘it is because mama will understand me better in this action, and will feel my loss in a different way, and will look forward in a different manner. But you know what a dear, devoted mother she is, and you will remember her too; will you not?’", "\"제가 엄마 얘기를 하지 않는 건요,\" 펫이 말했다. 그 천진한 슬픔에, 클레넘이 차마 곰곰이 생각해 볼 엄두조차 못 낼 만큼 마음이 동했고, 또 그만큼 그녀가 어여뻤기에—바로 그 때문에 그는 두 사람과 스러져 가는 빛 사이에 놓인 나무들을, 천천히 그 수가 줄어들수록 하나하나 세었다—\"엄마는 이 일을 더 잘 이해해 주실 거고, 저를 잃는 걸 다른 식으로 느끼실 거고, 다른 방식으로 앞날을 내다보실 테니까예요. 하지만 엄마가 얼마나 다정하고 헌신적인 어머니인지 당신도 아시잖아요. 엄마도 기억해 주실 거죠? 그러실 거죠?\""]], [["Let Minnie trust him, Clennam said, let Minnie trust him to do all she wished.", "미니가 자기를 믿어 달라고, 미니가 바라는 일을 다 하도록 자기를 믿어 달라고 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘And, dear Mr Clennam,’ said Minnie, ‘because papa and one whom I need not name, do not fully appreciate and understand one another yet, as they will by-and-by; and because it will be the duty, and the pride, and pleasure of my new life, to draw them to a better knowledge of one another, and to be a happiness to one another, and to be proud of one another, and to love one another, both loving me so dearly; oh, as you are a kind, true man! when I am first separated from home (I am going a long distance away), try to reconcile papa to him a little more, and use your great influence to keep him before papa’s mind free from prejudice and in his real form. Will you do this for me, as you are a noble-hearted friend?’", "\"그리고, 사랑하는 클레넘 씨.\" 미니가 말했다. \"아빠와, 굳이 이름을 대지 않아도 될 한 사람이 아직은 서로를 충분히 알아주고 이해하지 못하니까요. 머지않아 그렇게 되겠지만요. 그리고 두 사람이 서로를 더 잘 알도록 이끄는 것, 서로에게 행복이 되어 주는 것, 서로를 자랑스러워하게 하는 것, 서로를 사랑하게 하는 것—두 사람 다 저를 그토록 깊이 사랑하니까요—그것이 제 새로운 삶의 의무이자 긍지이자 기쁨이 될 테니까요. 오, 당신은 친절하고 진실한 분이니! 제가 집과 처음으로 떨어지게 될 때(저는 아주 먼 곳으로 가요), 아빠가 그 사람과 조금이라도 더 화해하도록 애써 주세요. 그리고 당신의 큰 영향력을 발휘하여, 아빠의 마음속에서 그 사람을 편견 없이, 그 사람의 참모습 그대로 남아 있게 해 주세요. 당신은 고결한 마음을 지닌 친구이니, 저를 위해 이렇게 해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["Poor Pet!", "가엾은 펫!"], ["Self-deceived, mistaken child!", "스스로를 속이는, 잘못 생각하는 아이! 사람들 사이의 타고난 관계에 그런 변화가 언제 이루어진 적이 있었던가."], ["When were such changes ever made in men’s natural relations to one another: when was such reconcilement of ingrain differences ever effected!", "뿌리 깊은 차이의 그런 화해가 언제 이루어진 적이 있었던가! 다른 딸들도 그것을 여러 번 시도했단다, 미니야."], ["It has been tried many times by other daughters, Minnie; it has never succeeded; nothing has ever come of it but failure.", "그러나 한 번도 성공한 적이 없었지. 거기서 나온 것이라곤 실패뿐이었다."]], [["So Clennam thought.", "클레넘은 그렇게 생각했다."], ["So he did not say; it was too late.", "그러나 그렇게 말하지는 않았다. 너무 늦은 일이었다."], ["He bound himself to do all she asked, and she knew full well that he would do it.", "그는 그녀가 부탁하는 모든 것을 하겠노라고 자신을 묶었고, 그녀는 그가 그렇게 하리라는 것을 잘 알았다."]], [["They were now at the last tree in the avenue.", "이제 두 사람은 가로수길의 마지막 나무에 이르렀다."], ["She stopped, and withdrew her arm.", "그녀가 걸음을 멈추고 팔을 거두었다."], ["Speaking to him with her eyes lifted up to his, and with the hand that had lately rested on his sleeve trembling by touching one of the roses in his breast as an additional appeal to him, she said:", "그녀는 두 눈을 들어 그를 올려다보며, 조금 전까지 그의 소매 위에 얹혀 있던 손으로 그의 가슴에 꽂힌 장미 한 송이를 만지며—그에게 거듭 호소하듯—떨면서 그에게 말했다."]], [["‘Dear Mr Clennam, in my happiness--for I am happy, though you have seen me crying--I cannot bear to leave any cloud between us.", "\"사랑하는 클레넘 씨, 제 행복 속에서도—제가 우는 걸 보시긴 했지만 저는 정말 행복하답니다—저는 우리 사이에 어떤 구름이라도 남겨 두고 떠날 수가 없어요."], ["If you have anything to forgive me (not anything that I have wilfully done, but any trouble I may have caused you without meaning it, or having it in my power to help it), forgive me to-night out of your noble heart!’", "만약 제가 당신께 용서를 구할 일이 있다면(제가 일부러 한 일이 아니라, 의도치 않게, 또 막을 도리도 없이 제가 끼쳐 드렸을지 모를 어떤 괴로움이라도요), 오늘 밤 당신의 고결한 마음으로 저를 용서해 주세요!\""]], [["He stooped to meet the guileless face that met his without shrinking.", "그는 위축됨 없이 자기를 마주하는 그 꾸밈없는 얼굴을 향해 고개를 숙였다."], ["He kissed it, and answered, Heaven knew that he had nothing to forgive.", "그는 그 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 자기에게는 용서할 것이 아무것도 없음을 하늘이 안다고 대답했다."], ["As he stooped to meet the innocent face once again, she whispered, ‘Good-bye!’ and he repeated it.", "그가 그 순결한 얼굴을 다시 한번 마주하려 고개를 숙였을 때, 그녀가 속삭였다. \"안녕히!\""], ["It was taking leave of all his old hopes--all nobody’s old restless doubts.", "그도 그 말을 되풀이했다. 그것은 그의 모든 옛 희망에—누구의 것인지 모를 그 모든 옛 안절부절못하던 의심들에 작별을 고하는 것이었다."], ["They came out of the avenue next moment, arm-in-arm as they had entered it: and the trees seemed to close up behind them in the darkness, like their own perspective of the past.", "다음 순간 두 사람은, 들어설 때와 똑같이 팔짱을 끼고 가로수길에서 나왔다. 그리고 나무들은 그들 뒤로 어둠 속에서, 마치 그들 자신의 지나간 날의 원근법처럼 닫혀 드는 듯했다."]], [["The voices of Mr and Mrs Meagles and Doyce were audible directly, speaking near the garden gate.", "미글스 씨와 미글스 부인, 그리고 도이스가 정원 문 가까이에서 이야기하는 목소리가 곧바로 들려왔다. 그들 사이에서 펫의 이름이 들리자, 클레넘이 외쳤다."], ["Hearing Pet’s name among them, Clennam called out, ‘She is here, with me.’ There was some little wondering and laughing until they came up; but as soon as they had all come together, it ceased, and Pet glided away.", "\"여기 저와 함께 있습니다.\" 그들이 다가올 때까지 약간의 의아함과 웃음이 오갔지만, 모두가 한자리에 모이자 그것은 그쳤고, 펫은 슬며시 자리를 떴다."]], [["Mr Meagles, Doyce, and Clennam, without speaking, walked up and down on the brink of the river, in the light of the rising moon, for a few minutes; and then Doyce lingered behind, and went into the house.", "미글스 씨와 도이스와 클레넘은 떠오르는 달빛 속에서 말없이 강가를 몇 분 동안 거닐었다. 그러다가 도이스가 뒤에 처져 머뭇거리더니 집 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Mr Meagles and Clennam walked up and down together for a few minutes more without speaking, until at length the former broke silence.", "미글스 씨와 클레넘은 몇 분을 더 말없이 함께 거닐었고, 이윽고 앞사람이 침묵을 깼다."]], [["‘Arthur,’ said he, using that familiar address for the first time in their communication, ‘do you remember my telling you, as we walked up and down one hot morning, looking over the harbour at Marseilles, that Pet’s baby sister who was dead seemed to Mother and me to have grown as she had grown, and changed as she had changed?’", "\"아서.\" 그들이 서로 이야기를 나누어 온 이래 처음으로 그 친근한 호칭을 쓰며 그가 말했다. \"어느 더운 아침에 우리가 마르세유 항구를 내려다보며 거닐 때, 죽은 펫의 아기 누이가 아내와 내게는 마치 펫이 자란 만큼 자라고, 펫이 변한 만큼 변한 것처럼 여겨진다고 자네에게 말했던 걸 기억하나?\""]], [["‘Very well.’", "\"잘 기억합니다.\""]], [["‘You remember my saying that our thoughts had never been able to separate those twin sisters, and that, in our fancy, whatever Pet was, the other was?’", "\"우리 생각이 그 쌍둥이 자매를 도무지 갈라놓을 수 없었다고, 그리고 우리 상상 속에서는 펫이 어떤 모습이든 다른 아이도 꼭 그렇다고 내가 말한 것을 기억하나?\""]], [["‘Yes, very well.’", "\"네, 잘 기억합니다.\""]], [["‘Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, much subdued, ‘I carry that fancy further to-night.", "\"아서.\" 미글스 씨가 한껏 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다."], ["I feel to-night, my dear fellow, as if you had loved my dead child very tenderly, and had lost her when she was like what Pet is now.’", "\"오늘 밤 나는 그 상상을 더 멀리 끌고 가 보네. 오늘 밤 나는, 여보게, 마치 자네가 내 죽은 아이를 무척이나 다정하게 사랑했고, 그 아이가 지금의 펫과 같은 모습이었을 때 그 아이를 잃은 것만 같이 느껴진다네.\""]], [["‘Thank you!’ murmured Clennam, ‘thank you!’ And pressed his hand.", "\"고맙습니다!\" 클레넘이 나직이 말했다. \"고맙습니다!\" 그러고는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["‘Will you come in?’ said Mr Meagles, presently.", "\"들어오겠나?\" 이윽고 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘In a little while.’", "\"조금 이따가요.\""]], [["Mr Meagles fell away, and he was left alone.", "미글스 씨가 멀어지고, 그는 홀로 남았다."], ["When he had walked on the river’s brink in the peaceful moonlight for some half an hour, he put his hand in his breast and tenderly took out the handful of roses.", "평화로운 달빛 속에서 강가를 반 시간쯤 거닌 뒤, 그는 가슴에 손을 넣어 한 줌의 장미를 다정하게 꺼냈다."], ["Perhaps he put them to his heart, perhaps he put them to his lips, but certainly he bent down on the shore and gently launched them on the flowing river.", "어쩌면 그것을 가슴에 가져다 댔는지도, 어쩌면 입술에 가져다 댔는지도 모른다. 그러나 그가 강기슭에 몸을 굽혀 흐르는 강물 위에 그것을 가만히 띄워 보낸 것만은 분명했다."], ["Pale and unreal in the moonlight, the river floated them away.", "달빛 속에서 창백하고 비현실적인 그 강물이 장미를 실어 떠내려 보냈다."]], [["The lights were bright within doors when he entered, and the faces on which they shone, his own face not excepted, were soon quietly cheerful.", "그가 들어섰을 때 집 안의 불빛은 환했고, 그 불빛이 비추는 얼굴들은—그 자신의 얼굴도 예외 없이—이내 조용히 명랑해졌다."], ["They talked of many subjects (his partner never had had such a ready store to draw upon for the beguiling of the time), and so to bed, and to sleep.", "그들은 여러 가지 이야기를 나누었고(그의 동업자는 시간을 즐겁게 보내는 데 끌어다 쓸 화젯거리를 그토록 풍성하게 갖추고 있은 적이 없었다), 그렇게 잠자리에 들어 잠이 들었다."], ["While the flowers, pale and unreal in the moonlight, floated away upon the river; and thus do greater things that once were in our breasts, and near our hearts, flow from us to the eternal seas.", "그동안 그 꽃들은, 달빛 속에서 창백하고 비현실적인 채로, 강물 위를 떠내려갔다. 그리고 한때 우리 가슴속에 있었고 우리 마음 가까이 있었던 더 큰 것들도, 이처럼 우리에게서 흘러나가 영원한 바다로 흘러드는 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming", "# CHAPTER 29. Mrs Flintwinch goes on Dreaming"]], [["The house in the city preserved its heavy dulness through all these transactions, and the invalid within it turned the same unvarying round of life.", "도심의 그 집은 이 모든 거래가 오가는 동안에도 그 무거운 둔중함을 고스란히 지켰고, 그 안의 병자는 변함없는 삶의 똑같은 순환을 되풀이했다."], ["Morning, noon, and night, morning, noon, and night, each recurring with its accompanying monotony, always the same reluctant return of the same sequences of machinery, like a dragging piece of clockwork.", "아침, 정오, 밤, 다시 아침, 정오, 밤, 저마다 따라붙는 단조로움과 함께 되돌아오고, 언제나 똑같은 기계 장치의 똑같은 순서가 마지못해 되풀이되는 것이, 마치 질질 끌리는 시계태엽 같았다."]], [["The wheeled chair had its associated remembrances and reveries, one may suppose, as every place that is made the station of a human being has.", "바퀴 달린 의자에도, 인간 존재의 거처가 된 모든 장소가 그러하듯, 거기에 얽힌 추억과 몽상이 있었으리라 짐작할 만했다."], ["Pictures of demolished streets and altered houses, as they formerly were when the occupant of the chair was familiar with them, images of people as they too used to be, with little or no allowance made for the lapse of time since they were seen; of these, there must have been many in the long routine of gloomy days.", "헐려 버린 거리와 달라진 집들이, 그 의자의 주인이 한때 그것들과 친숙하던 시절의 모습 그대로 그려지고, 사람들의 모습도 옛 그 모습 그대로, 그들을 본 이래 흘러간 세월에 대한 참작은 거의 또는 전혀 없이 떠올랐다. 침울한 나날의 그 기나긴 일과 속에 그런 것들이 분명 많았으리라."], ["To stop the clock of busy existence at the hour when we were personally sequestered from it, to suppose mankind stricken motionless when we were brought to a stand-still, to be unable to measure the changes beyond our view by any larger standard than the shrunken one of our own uniform and contracted existence, is the infirmity of many invalids, and the mental unhealthiness of almost all recluses.", "우리가 몸소 그 흐름에서 격리된 그 시각에 분주한 삶의 시계를 멈춰 세우는 것, 우리가 멈춰 서게 되었을 때 인류도 함께 꼼짝없이 멈췄으리라 여기는 것, 우리 시야 너머의 변화를 우리 자신의 한결같고 옹색한 삶이라는 그 쪼그라든 잣대보다 더 큰 잣대로는 가늠하지 못하는 것, 이는 많은 병자의 병약함이요, 거의 모든 은둔자의 정신적 불건강함이다."]], [["What scenes and actors the stern woman most reviewed, as she sat from season to season in her one dark room, none knew but herself.", "그 엄격한 여인이 철 따라 어두운 단칸방에 앉아 어떤 장면과 어떤 인물들을 가장 자주 되새겼는지는 그녀 자신밖에 알지 못했다."], ["Mr Flintwinch, with his wry presence brought to bear upon her daily like some eccentric mechanical force, would perhaps have screwed it out of her, if there had been less resistance in her; but she was too strong for him.", "플린트윈치 씨가, 마치 별난 기계적 힘처럼 매일같이 그 비뚤어진 존재감을 그녀에게 들이대며, 그녀의 저항이 좀 덜했다면 어쩌면 그것을 나사 돌리듯 비틀어 끄집어냈을지도 모른다. 그러나 그녀는 그에게 맞서기에 너무 강했다."], ["So far as Mistress Affery was concerned, to regard her liege-lord and her disabled mistress with a face of blank wonder, to go about the house after dark with her apron over her head, always to listen for the strange noises and sometimes to hear them, and never to emerge from her ghostly, dreamy, sleep-waking state, was occupation enough for her.", "애프리 부인으로 말하자면, 자기 주군과 불구가 된 안주인을 멍한 경이의 얼굴로 바라보는 것, 어두워진 뒤에 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰고 집 안을 돌아다니는 것, 늘 그 이상한 소리들에 귀 기울이고 때로 그것을 듣는 것, 그리고 결코 자신의 그 유령 같고 꿈결 같고 잠과 깸의 경계에 있는 상태에서 벗어나지 못하는 것, 그것만으로도 그녀에게는 충분한 일거리였다."]], [["There was a fair stroke of business doing, as Mistress Affery made out, for her husband had abundant occupation in his little office, and saw more people than had been used to come there for some years.", "애프리 부인이 알아챈 바로는 제법 짭짤한 사업이 진행되고 있었으니, 남편이 그 작은 사무실에서 일거리가 넘쳐났고, 몇 해 동안 그곳에 드나들던 것보다 훨씬 많은 사람을 만났기 때문이다."], ["This might easily be, the house having been long deserted; but he did receive letters, and comers, and keep books, and correspond.", "집이 오래 비어 있었으니 그럴 만도 했다. 그래도 그는 편지를 받고 손님을 받고 장부를 적고 서신을 주고받았다."], ["Moreover, he went about to other counting-houses, and to wharves, and docks, and to the Custom House, and to Garraway’s Coffee House, and the Jerusalem Coffee House, and on ‘Change; so that he was much in and out.", "게다가 그는 다른 회계 사무소며 부두며 선창이며 세관이며 개러웨이 커피점이며 예루살렘 커피점이며 거래소에도 드나들었으니, 들고나는 일이 잦았다."], ["He began, too, sometimes of an evening, when Mrs Clennam expressed no particular wish for his society, to resort to a tavern in the neighbourhood to look at the shipping news and closing prices in the evening paper, and even to exchange small socialities with mercantile Sea Captains who frequented that establishment.", "또한 그는 클레넘 부인이 자기 곁을 굳이 원하지 않을 때면 저녁녘에 가끔씩 근처 선술집을 찾아 석간신문의 해운 소식과 마감 시세를 들여다보고, 그 가게를 자주 드나드는 상선 선장들과 자잘한 사교를 나누기까지 했다."], ["At some period of every day, he and Mrs Clennam held a council on matters of business; and it appeared to Affery, who was always groping about, listening and watching, that the two clever ones were making money.", "매일 어느 때엔가 그와 클레넘 부인은 사업 문제를 두고 회의를 열었다. 늘 더듬듯 돌아다니며 엿듣고 살피던 애프리에게는, 그 약삭빠른 두 사람이 돈을 벌고 있는 것으로 보였다."]], [["The state of mind into which Mr Flintwinch’s dazed lady had fallen, had now begun to be so expressed in all her looks and actions that she was held in very low account by the two clever ones, as a person, never of strong intellect, who was becoming foolish.", "플린트윈치 씨의 얼이 빠진 아내가 빠져든 정신 상태가 이제는 그녀의 모든 표정과 행동에 워낙 드러나기 시작한지라, 그 약삭빠른 두 사람은 그녀를 본디 지력이 강했던 적도 없는 데다 점점 어리석어져 가는 사람으로 여겨 아주 낮잡아 보았다."], ["Perhaps because her appearance was not of a commercial cast, or perhaps because it occurred to him that his having taken her to wife might expose his judgment to doubt in the minds of customers, Mr Flintwinch laid his commands upon her that she should hold her peace on the subject of her conjugal relations, and should no longer call him Jeremiah out of the domestic trio.", "어쩌면 그녀의 외양이 장사꾼다운 데가 없어서였을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그녀를 아내로 삼은 것이 손님들의 마음속에 자신의 판단력을 의심받게 할지 모른다는 생각이 들어서였는지도 모르나, 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀에게 부부 관계라는 화제에 대해 입을 다물 것이며, 식구 셋이 모인 자리에서 더는 자신을 제러마이아라고 부르지 말 것을 명령으로 내렸다."], ["Her frequent forgetfulness of this admonition intensified her startled manner, since Mr Flintwinch’s habit of avenging himself on her remissness by making springs after her on the staircase, and shaking her, occasioned her to be always nervously uncertain when she might be thus waylaid next.", "이 훈계를 그녀가 자주 잊는 통에 그녀의 깜짝깜짝 놀라는 태도는 한층 심해졌으니, 그녀의 태만을 응징하는 플린트윈치 씨의 버릇이 계단에서 그녀에게 달려들어 흔들어 대는 것이었던 탓에, 그녀는 다음번에 또 어느 때 이렇게 매복에 걸려들지 늘 신경이 곤두선 채 짐작도 못 하고 지냈다."]], [["Little Dorrit had finished a long day’s work in Mrs Clennam’s room, and was neatly gathering up her shreds and odds and ends before going home.", "리틀 도릿은 클레넘 부인의 방에서 긴 하루의 일을 마치고, 집으로 가기 전에 자신의 자투리와 토막들을 깔끔하게 거두어 모으던 참이었다."], ["Mr Pancks, whom Affery had just shown in, was addressing an inquiry to Mrs Clennam on the subject of her health, coupled with the remark that, ‘happening to find himself in that direction,’ he had looked in to inquire, on behalf of his proprietor, how she found herself.", "애프리가 방금 들여보낸 팽크스 씨가 클레넘 부인에게 안부를 묻고 있었는데, '마침 그쪽 방향으로 오게 되어' 자기 고용주를 대신해 그녀가 어떻게 지내는지 여쭤보러 들렀다는 말을 덧붙였다."], ["Mrs Clennam, with a deep contraction of her brows, was looking at him.", "클레넘 부인은 미간을 깊이 찌푸린 채 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘Mr Casby knows,’ said she, ‘that I am not subject to changes.", "\"내가 변하는 법이 없는 사람이라는 걸 캐스비 씨도 알지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["The change that I await here is the great change.’", "\"내가 여기서 기다리는 변화란 그 큰 변화입니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed, ma’am?’ returned Mr Pancks, with a wandering eye towards the figure of the little seamstress on her knee picking threads and fraying of her work from the carpet.", "\"그렇군요, 부인?\" 팽크스 씨는 무릎을 꿇고 양탄자에서 실밥과 일감의 보풀을 집어내는 그 작은 바느질꾼의 모습 쪽으로 눈길을 흘리며 대답했다."], ["‘You look nicely, ma’am.’", "\"안색이 좋아 보이십니다, 부인.\""]], [["‘I bear what I have to bear,’ she answered.", "\"나는 내가 견뎌야 할 것을 견디지요.\" 그녀가 대답했다."], ["‘Do you what you have to do.’", "\"당신은 당신이 해야 할 일을 하세요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, ma’am,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘such is my endeavour.’", "\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다. \"그러려고 애쓰고 있지요.\""]], [["‘You are often in this direction, are you not?’ asked Mrs Clennam.", "\"이쪽 방향으로 자주 오시지요, 그렇지 않은가요?\" 클레넘 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘Why, yes, ma’am,’ said Pancks, ‘rather so lately; I have lately been round this way a good deal, owing to one thing and another.’", "\"네, 그렇습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"요즘 들어 좀 그렇지요. 요사이엔 이런저런 일로 이 근방을 꽤 많이 돌아다녔습니다.\""]], [["‘Beg Mr Casby and his daughter not to trouble themselves, by deputy, about me.", "\"캐스비 씨와 그 따님에게, 대리를 시켜 나를 두고 수고하지 말라고 전해 주세요."], ["When they wish to see me, they know I am here to see them.", "나를 보고 싶으면, 여기 와서 나를 만날 수 있다는 걸 그들도 압니다."], ["They have no need to trouble themselves to send.", "굳이 사람을 보내느라 애쓸 필요가 없어요."], ["You have no need to trouble yourself to come.’", "당신도 굳이 오느라 수고할 필요가 없고요.\""]], [["‘Not the least trouble, ma’am,’ said Mr Pancks.", "\"조금도 수고랄 게 없습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You really are looking uncommonly nicely, ma’am.’", "\"참으로 유달리 좋아 보이십니다, 부인.\""]], [["‘Thank you.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["Good evening.’", "안녕히 가세요.\""]], [["The dismissal, and its accompanying finger pointed straight at the door, was so curt and direct that Mr Pancks did not see his way to prolong his visit.", "물러가라는 그 말과, 그에 곁들여 곧장 문을 가리킨 손가락이 어찌나 퉁명스럽고 직설적이던지, 팽크스 씨로서는 방문을 더 끌어갈 도리가 없었다."], ["He stirred up his hair with his sprightliest expression, glanced at the little figure again, said ‘Good evening, ma ‘am; don’t come down, Mrs Affery, I know the road to the door,’ and steamed out.", "그는 더없이 발랄한 표정으로 머리카락을 휘저어 헝클고, 그 작은 모습을 다시 한번 흘긋 보고는 \"안녕히 계십시오, 부인. 내려오지 마세요, 애프리 부인, 문 가는 길은 제가 압니다\""], ["Mrs Clennam, her chin resting on her hand, followed him with attentive and darkly distrustful eyes; and Affery stood looking at her as if she were spell-bound.", "하고 말하더니 증기 뿜듯 빠져나갔다. 클레넘 부인은 턱을 손에 괴고서 주의 깊고도 어둡게 의심하는 눈으로 그를 좇았고, 애프리는 무언가에 홀린 듯 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["Slowly and thoughtfully, Mrs Clennam’s eyes turned from the door by which Pancks had gone out, to Little Dorrit, rising from the carpet.", "천천히, 또 생각에 잠긴 채, 클레넘 부인의 눈길이 팽크스가 나간 문에서 양탄자에서 일어서는 리틀 도릿에게로 돌아갔다."], ["With her chin drooping more heavily on her hand, and her eyes vigilant and lowering, the sick woman sat looking at her until she attracted her attention.", "턱을 손에 더욱 무겁게 떨군 채, 경계하듯 내리깐 눈으로, 그 병든 여인은 리틀 도릿이 자신의 시선을 알아챌 때까지 앉아서 바라보았다."], ["Little Dorrit coloured under such a gaze, and looked down.", "리틀 도릿은 그런 눈길을 받고 얼굴을 붉히며 눈을 내리깔았다."], ["Mrs Clennam still sat intent.", "클레넘 부인은 여전히 골똘히 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Little Dorrit,’ she said, when she at last broke silence, ‘what do you know of that man?’", "\"리틀 도릿.\" 마침내 그녀가 침묵을 깨고 말했다. \"넌 저 사람에 대해 무얼 아느냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t know anything of him, ma’am, except that I have seen him about, and that he has spoken to me.’", "\"저는 저분에 대해 아무것도 모릅니다, 부인. 그저 이리저리 다니는 걸 본 적이 있고, 저한테 말을 건 적이 있다는 것밖에는요.\""]], [["‘What has he said to you?’", "\"너에게 무슨 말을 하더냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t understand what he has said, he is so strange.", "\"무슨 말을 하는지 잘 모르겠어요, 워낙 이상하셔서요."], ["But nothing rough or disagreeable.’", "하지만 거칠거나 불쾌한 말은 하나도 없었어요.\""]], [["‘Why does he come here to see you?’", "\"왜 너를 보러 여기 오는 거지?\""]], [["‘I don’t know, ma’am,’ said Little Dorrit, with perfect frankness.", "\"모르겠습니다, 부인.\" 리틀 도릿이 더없이 솔직하게 말했다."]], [["‘You know that he does come here to see you?’", "\"저 사람이 너를 보러 여기 오는 줄은 알고 있지?\""]], [["‘I have fancied so,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그런가 보다 하고 짐작은 했어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘But why he should come here or anywhere for that, ma’am, I can’t think.’", "\"하지만 무엇 때문에 저분이 여기든 어디든 그런 일로 오는지는, 부인, 도무지 생각이 안 나요.\""]], [["Mrs Clennam cast her eyes towards the ground, and with her strong, set face, as intent upon a subject in her mind as it had lately been upon the form that seemed to pass out of her view, sat absorbed.", "클레넘 부인은 눈을 땅으로 떨구고는, 강하고 굳은 얼굴로, 조금 전 시야에서 사라지는 듯한 그 형체에 쏠려 있던 것만큼이나 마음속 어떤 문제에 골똘히 쏠린 채, 빠져들어 앉아 있었다."], ["Some minutes elapsed before she came out of this thoughtfulness, and resumed her hard composure.", "몇 분이 흐른 뒤에야 그녀는 이 깊은 생각에서 빠져나와 그 딱딱한 평정을 되찾았다."]], [["Little Dorrit in the meanwhile had been waiting to go, but afraid to disturb her by moving.", "그동안 리틀 도릿은 떠나려고 기다리고 있었으나, 움직여서 그녀를 방해할까 두려웠다."], ["She now ventured to leave the spot where she had been standing since she had risen, and to pass gently round by the wheeled chair.", "그녀는 이제 일어선 뒤로 줄곧 서 있던 그 자리를 떠나, 바퀴 달린 의자 곁으로 가만히 돌아 지나갈 엄두를 냈다."], ["She stopped at its side to say ‘Good night, ma’am.’", "그 곁에 멈춰 서서 \"안녕히 주무세요, 부인\" 하고 말했다."]], [["Mrs Clennam put out her hand, and laid it on her arm.", "클레넘 부인이 손을 내밀어 그녀의 팔에 얹었다."], ["Little Dorrit, confused under the touch, stood faltering.", "리틀 도릿은 그 손길에 당황하여 머뭇거리며 섰다."], ["Perhaps some momentary recollection of the story of the Princess may have been in her mind.", "어쩌면 그 공주 이야기에 대한 어떤 순간적인 기억이 그녀의 마음에 스쳤는지도 모른다."]], [["‘Tell me, Little Dorrit,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘have you many friends now?’", "\"말해 보아라, 리틀 도릿.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"넌 이제 친구가 많으냐?\""]], [["‘Very few, ma’am.", "\"아주 적어요, 부인."], ["Besides you, only Miss Flora and--one more.’", "부인 말고는 플로라 아가씨뿐이고요—그리고 한 사람 더요.\""]], [["‘Meaning,’ said Mrs Clennam, with her unbent finger again pointing to the door, ‘that man?’", "\"그 말은,\" 클레넘 부인이 펴진 손가락으로 다시 문을 가리키며 말했다. \"저 사람을 두고 하는 말이냐?\""]], [["‘Oh no, ma’am!’", "\"아, 아니에요, 부인!\""]], [["‘Some friend of his, perhaps?’", "\"그럼 그 사람의 친구쯤 되는 사람이냐?\""]], [["‘No ma’am.’ Little Dorrit earnestly shook her head.", "\"아니에요, 부인.\" 리틀 도릿은 간절하게 고개를 저었다."], ["‘Oh no!", "\"아, 아니에요!"], ["No one at all like him, or belonging to him.’", "그분과 닮았거나 그분과 얽힌 사람은 전혀 아니에요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Mrs Clennam, almost smiling.", "\"그래!\" 클레넘 부인은 거의 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["‘It is no affair of mine.", "\"내가 상관할 바는 아니지."], ["I ask, because I take an interest in you; and because I believe I was your friend when you had no other who could serve you.", "내가 묻는 건, 네게 관심이 있어서다. 그리고 너를 도와줄 다른 사람이 아무도 없을 때 내가 네 친구였다고 믿기 때문이다."], ["Is that so?’", "그렇지 않으냐?\""]], [["‘Yes, ma’am; indeed it is.", "\"네, 부인. 정말 그래요."], ["I have been here many a time when, but for you and the work you gave me, we should have wanted everything.’", "부인과 부인이 주신 일감이 아니었다면 우리가 모든 것을 다 아쉬워했을 그런 때에, 저는 여러 번 이곳에 왔었어요.\""]], [["‘We,’ repeated Mrs Clennam, looking towards the watch, once her dead husband’s, which always lay upon her table.", "\"우리라.\" 클레넘 부인은 죽은 남편의 것이었던, 늘 그녀의 탁자 위에 놓여 있는 그 회중시계 쪽을 바라보며 되뇌었다."], ["‘Are there many of you?’", "\"너희가 식구가 많으냐?\""]], [["‘Only father and I, now.", "\"이제는 아버지와 저뿐이에요."], ["I mean, only father and I to keep regularly out of what we get.’", "그러니까, 버는 돈으로 늘 꾸준히 살아가야 하는 건 아버지와 저뿐이라는 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Have you undergone many privations?", "\"너희는 궁핍을 많이 겪었느냐?"], ["You and your father and who else there may be of you?’ asked Mrs Clennam, speaking deliberately, and meditatively turning the watch over and over.", "너와 네 아버지와, 또 너희 중에 누가 더 있든 말이다.\" 클레넘 부인이 신중하게 말하며, 생각에 잠긴 듯 시계를 자꾸만 뒤집어 보면서 물었다."]], [["‘Sometimes it has been rather hard to live,’ said Little Dorrit, in her soft voice, and timid uncomplaining way; ‘but I think not harder--as to that--than many people find it.’", "\"이따금 사는 게 꽤 힘들 때도 있었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 그 부드러운 목소리로, 소심하고 불평 없는 태도로 말했다. \"하지만 그 점에 대해서는—많은 사람들이 겪는 것보다 더 힘들었다고는 생각하지 않아요.\""]], [["‘That’s well said!’ Mrs Clennam quickly returned.", "\"잘 말했다!\" 클레넘 부인이 얼른 받았다."], ["‘That’s the truth!", "\"그게 진실이야!"], ["You are a good, thoughtful girl.", "넌 착하고 사려 깊은 아이야."], ["You are a grateful girl too, or I much mistake you.’", "게다가 고마움을 아는 아이지. 내가 너를 크게 잘못 본 게 아니라면 말이다.\""]], [["‘It is only natural to be that.", "\"그러는 게 당연한걸요."], ["There is no merit in being that,’ said Little Dorrit.", "그러는 데에 무슨 갸륵함이 있는 건 아니에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘I am indeed.’", "\"저는 정말로 고맙게 생각하고 있어요.\""]], [["Mrs Clennam, with a gentleness of which the dreaming Affery had never dreamed her to be capable, drew down the face of her little seamstress, and kissed her on the forehead.", "클레넘 부인은, 꿈꾸는 애프리가 그녀에게 그럴 능력이 있으리라고는 꿈에도 생각지 못했을 그런 다정함으로, 자신의 작은 바느질꾼의 얼굴을 끌어내려 그 이마에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘Now go, Little Dorrit,’ said she, ‘or you will be late, poor child!’", "\"자, 이제 가거라, 리틀 도릿.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"안 그러면 늦겠다, 가엾은 것아!\""]], [["In all the dreams Mistress Affery had been piling up since she first became devoted to the pursuit, she had dreamed nothing more astonishing than this.", "애프리 부인이 그 일에 빠져든 이래 차곡차곡 쌓아 올린 온갖 꿈들 가운데에도, 그녀는 이보다 더 놀라운 것을 꿈꾼 적이 없었다."], ["Her head ached with the idea that she would find the other clever one kissing Little Dorrit next, and then the two clever ones embracing each other and dissolving into tears of tenderness for all mankind.", "다음번엔 또 그 약삭빠른 다른 한 사람이 리틀 도릿에게 입을 맞추고, 그러고 나면 그 약삭빠른 두 사람이 서로를 끌어안고서 온 인류를 향한 애틋함의 눈물에 녹아드는 광경을 보게 되는 게 아닐까 하는 생각에 그녀는 머리가 다 지끈거렸다."], ["The idea quite stunned her, as she attended the light footsteps down the stairs, that the house door might be safely shut.", "집 대문을 안심하고 닫을 수 있도록 그 가벼운 발소리를 따라 계단 아래까지 배웅하는 동안, 그 생각이 그녀를 완전히 멍하게 만들었다."]], [["On opening it to let Little Dorrit out, she found Mr Pancks, instead of having gone his way, as in any less wonderful place and among less wonderful phenomena he might have been reasonably expected to do, fluttering up and down the court outside the house.", "리틀 도릿을 내보내려고 문을 열자, 그녀는 팽크스 씨가, 덜 신기한 곳에서 덜 신기한 현상들 가운데에 있었더라면 응당 그러리라 기대됨 직했던 대로 제 갈 길을 가지 않고, 집 바깥 마당을 이리저리 서성이고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The moment he saw Little Dorrit, he passed her briskly, said with his finger to his nose (as Mrs Affery distinctly heard), ‘Pancks the gipsy, fortune-telling,’ and went away.", "그는 리틀 도릿을 보자마자 그녀 곁을 잰걸음으로 지나치며, 손가락을 코에 갖다 댄 채 (애프리 부인이 또렷이 들은 바로는) \"집시 팽크스, 점치기\" 하고 말하고는 가 버렸다."], ["‘Lord save us, here’s a gipsy and a fortune-teller in it now!’ cried Mistress Affery.", "\"맙소사, 이제는 집시에다 점쟁이까지 끼어들었네!\" 애프리 부인이 부르짖었다."], ["‘What next!’", "\"이다음엔 또 뭐람!\""]], [["She stood at the open door, staggering herself with this enigma, on a rainy, thundery evening.", "그녀는 비 내리고 천둥 치는 저녁에, 이 수수께끼에 휘청대며 열린 문가에 서 있었다."], ["The clouds were flying fast, and the wind was coming up in gusts, banging some neighbouring shutters that had broken loose, twirling the rusty chimney-cowls and weather-cocks, and rushing round and round a confined adjacent churchyard as if it had a mind to blow the dead citizens out of their graves.", "구름은 빠르게 흘러갔고, 바람은 돌풍으로 불어닥쳐, 풀려 덜렁대는 이웃집 덧문 몇 짝을 쾅쾅 두드리고, 녹슨 굴뚝 갓과 풍향계를 빙글빙글 돌리고, 인접한 비좁은 교회 묘지를 빙빙 휩쓸어 돌며, 마치 죽은 시민들을 무덤에서 불어 날려 버릴 작정이라도 한 듯했다."], ["The low thunder, muttering in all quarters of the sky at once, seemed to threaten vengeance for this attempted desecration, and to mutter, ‘Let them rest!", "하늘 사방에서 한꺼번에 낮게 웅얼대는 천둥은, 이 모독의 시도에 보복이라도 하겠다는 듯 으르렁대며 \"그들을 쉬게 두라! 그들을 쉬게 두라!\""], ["Let them rest!’", "하고 중얼거리는 것 같았다."]], [["Mistress Affery, whose fear of thunder and lightning was only to be equalled by her dread of the haunted house with a premature and preternatural darkness in it, stood undecided whether to go in or not, until the question was settled for her by the door blowing upon her in a violent gust of wind and shutting her out.", "천둥과 번개에 대한 두려움이 그 안에 때 이르고 으스스한 어둠이 깃든 유령 들린 집에 대한 무서움과 맞먹을 뿐이었던 애프리 부인은, 들어가야 할지 말지 정하지 못한 채 서 있었는데, 마침 사나운 돌풍에 문이 그녀를 향해 휙 닫히며 그녀를 바깥에 가둬 버림으로써 그 문제가 저절로 결판났다. \"이제 어쩐담, 이제 어쩐담!\""], ["‘What’s to be done now, what’s to be done now!’ cried Mistress Affery, wringing her hands in this last uneasy dream of all; ‘when she’s all alone by herself inside, and can no more come down to open it than the churchyard dead themselves!’", "애프리 부인이 그 모든 것 중에서도 가장 마지막의 불안한 꿈속에서 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 부르짖었다. \"안에서 마님이 홀로 외따로 계신데, 교회 묘지의 죽은 이들과 마찬가지로 내려와서 문을 열어 줄 수도 없으니!\""]], [["In this dilemma, Mistress Affery, with her apron as a hood to keep the rain off, ran crying up and down the solitary paved enclosure several times.", "이 진퇴양난에 빠진 애프리 부인은, 비를 막으려고 앞치마를 두건 삼아 쓴 채, 그 외딴 포석 깔린 마당을 울며 몇 차례 오르내렸다."], ["Why she should then stoop down and look in at the keyhole of the door as if an eye would open it, it would be difficult to say; but it is none the less what most people would have done in the same situation, and it is what she did.", "그러고서 왜 그녀가 몸을 굽혀, 마치 눈 하나가 문을 열어 주기라도 할 것처럼 문 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보았는지는 설명하기 어렵겠으나, 그래도 그것은 같은 처지에 놓인 대부분의 사람들이 했을 법한 일이고, 또 그녀가 한 일이었다."]], [["From this posture she started up suddenly, with a half scream, feeling something on her shoulder.", "이 자세로 있다가 그녀는 어깨에 무언가가 닿는 것을 느끼고는, 반쯤 비명을 지르며 갑자기 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["It was the touch of a hand; of a man’s hand.", "그것은 손의 닿음이었으니, 어떤 남자의 손이었다."]], [["The man was dressed like a traveller, in a foraging cap with fur about it, and a heap of cloak.", "그 남자는 여행자 차림으로, 털이 둘린 약모를 쓰고 망토를 한 무더기 걸치고 있었다."], ["He looked like a foreigner.", "외국인처럼 보였다."], ["He had a quantity of hair and moustache--jet black, except at the shaggy ends, where it had a tinge of red--and a high hook nose.", "그는 머리털과 콧수염이 숱이 많았는데—새카만 빛깔에, 더부룩한 끝부분만은 불그스름한 기가 돌았다—매부리코가 우뚝했다."], ["He laughed at Mistress Affery’s start and cry; and as he laughed, his moustache went up under his nose, and his nose came down over his moustache.", "그는 애프리 부인이 깜짝 놀라 지른 비명에 웃었는데, 그가 웃자 콧수염은 코밑으로 치켜올라가고 코는 콧수염 위로 내려앉았다."]], [["‘What’s the matter?’ he asked in plain English.", "\"무슨 일이오?\" 그가 또렷한 영어로 물었다."], ["‘What are you frightened at?’", "\"뭘 그리 무서워하는 거요?\""]], [["‘At you,’ panted Affery.", "\"당신이요.\""], ["‘Me, madam?’", "애프리가 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["‘And the dismal evening, and--and everything,’ said Affery.", "\"나 말이오, 부인?\" \"그리고 음산한 저녁이며—그리고—그리고 죄다요.\""], ["‘And here!", "애프리가 말했다."], ["The wind has been and blown the door to, and I can’t get in.’", "\"그리고 이것 좀 봐요! 바람이 불어 문을 닫아 버려서, 내가 들어갈 수가 없어요.\""]], [["‘Hah!’ said the gentleman, who took that very coolly.", "\"하!\" 그 신사는 그 일을 아주 태연하게 받아들이며 말했다."], ["‘Indeed!", "\"그렇군!"], ["Do you know such a name as Clennam about here?’", "이 근방에 클레넘이라는 이름을 아시오?\""]], [["‘Lord bless us, I should think I did, I should think I did!’ cried Affery, exasperated into a new wringing of hands by the inquiry.", "\"맙소사, 알다마다요, 알다마다요!\" 그 물음에 애프리는 발끈하여 또다시 두 손을 비틀어 짜며 부르짖었다."]], [["‘Where about here?’", "\"이 근처 어디 말이오?\""]], [["‘Where!’ cried Affery, goaded into another inspection of the keyhole.", "\"어디라뇨!\" 애프리는 부추겨져 또 한 번 열쇠 구멍을 살피며 부르짖었다."], ["‘Where but here in this house?", "\"여기, 바로 이 집 말고 어디겠어요? 마님은 방에 홀로 계신데, 팔다리를 쓰지 못해 당신 몸은커녕 나도 도우려고 꼼짝할 수가 없고, 약삭빠른 그 양반은 나가 있고, 아이고 하느님 용서하소서!\""], ["And she’s all alone in her room, and lost the use of her limbs and can’t stir to help herself or me, and t’other clever one’s out, and Lord forgive me!’ cried Affery, driven into a frantic dance by these accumulated considerations, ‘if I ain’t a-going headlong out of my mind!’", "애프리는 이렇게 쌓여 가는 걱정거리에 떼밀려 미친 듯 춤을 추다시피 하며 부르짖었다. \"내가 정신줄을 놓고 곤두박질치는 게 아니라면 말이오!\""]], [["Taking a warmer view of the matter now that it concerned himself, the gentleman stepped back to glance at the house, and his eye soon rested on the long narrow window of the little room near the hall-door.", "이 일이 자기와도 관계되자 한층 적극적인 견지에서 보게 된 그 신사는, 한 걸음 물러서서 그 집을 흘긋 살펴보았고, 그의 눈은 이내 현관 옆 작은 방의 길고 좁은 창에 머물렀다."]], [["‘Where may the lady be who has lost the use of her limbs, madam?’ he inquired, with that peculiar smile which Mistress Affery could not choose but keep her eyes upon.", "\"팔다리를 쓰지 못한다는 그 부인이 어디 계시오, 부인?\" 애프리 부인이 도무지 눈을 떼지 못하던 그 독특한 미소를 띠며 그가 물었다."]], [["‘Up there!’ said Affery.", "\"저 위요!\" 애프리가 말했다."], ["‘Them two windows.’", "\"저기 저 창문 둘이요.\""]], [["‘Hah!", "\"하!"], ["I am of a fair size, but could not have the honour of presenting myself in that room without a ladder.", "내 몸집이 제법 됩니다만, 사다리 없이는 저 방에 들어가 나를 소개하는 영광을 누릴 수가 없겠군."], ["Now, madam, frankly--frankness is a part of my character--shall I open the door for you?’", "자, 부인, 솔직히 말하자면—솔직함은 내 성격의 일부지요—내가 당신을 위해 문을 열어 드릴까요?\""]], [["‘Yes, bless you, sir, for a dear creetur, and do it at once,’ cried Affery, ‘for she may be a-calling to me at this very present minute, or may be setting herself a fire and burning herself to death, or there’s no knowing what may be happening to her, and me a-going out of my mind at thinking of it!’", "\"네, 복받으세요, 나리, 참 고마운 양반이로구려, 어서 그렇게 해 주세요.\" 애프리가 부르짖었다. \"지금 이 순간에도 마님이 나를 부르고 계실지 모르고, 아니면 불을 지펴 타 죽고 계실지도 모르고, 아니 마님께 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알 수가 없는데, 그 생각만 하면 내가 정신을 놓아 버릴 지경이라오!\""]], [["‘Stay, my good madam!’ He restrained her impatience with a smooth white hand.", "\"잠깐만요, 부인!\" 그가 매끄러운 흰 손으로 그녀의 조바심을 억눌렀다."], ["‘Business-hours, I apprehend, are over for the day?’", "\"오늘 영업시간은 끝난 모양이지요?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, yes,’ cried Affery.", "\"네, 네, 네.\" 애프리가 부르짖었다."], ["‘Long ago.’", "\"벌써 한참 전에요.\""]], [["‘Let me make, then, a fair proposal.", "\"그럼 내가 공정한 제안을 하나 하리다. 공정함은 내 성격의 일부지요."], ["Fairness is a part of my character.", "보다시피 나는 우편선에서 막 내린 참이라오.\""], ["I am just landed from the packet-boat, as you may see.’ He showed her that his cloak was very wet, and that his boots were saturated with water; she had previously observed that he was dishevelled and sallow, as if from a rough voyage, and so chilled that he could not keep his teeth from chattering.", "그는 그녀에게 자기 망토가 흠뻑 젖었고 장화가 물에 절었음을 보여 주었다. 그녀는 이미, 그가 거친 항해를 한 듯 부스스하고 누렇게 떠 있으며, 어찌나 몸이 얼었는지 이가 딱딱 부딪는 것을 막지 못하는 것을 눈여겨본 터였다."], ["‘I am just landed from the packet-boat, madam, and have been delayed by the weather: the infernal weather!", "\"나는 우편선에서 막 내린 참이오, 부인, 그런데 날씨 때문에 발이 묶였지요. 그 빌어먹을 날씨 말이오!"], ["In consequence of this, madam, some necessary business that I should otherwise have transacted here within the regular hours (necessary business because money-business), still remains to be done.", "이 때문에, 부인, 아니었으면 정규 시간 안에 이곳에서 처리했을 어떤 긴요한 용무가 (긴요한 용무라 함은 돈에 관한 일이기 때문인데) 아직 처리되지 못한 채 남아 있소이다."], ["Now, if you will fetch any authorised neighbouring somebody to do it in return for my opening the door, I’ll open the door.", "자, 만약 당신이 그 일을 처리할 만한 권한 있는 이웃 사람을 누구든 데려와 준다면, 그 대가로 내가 문을 열어 드리리다."], ["If this arrangement should be objectionable, I’ll--’ and with the same smile he made a significant feint of backing away.", "이 제안이 마음에 들지 않는다면, 나는—\" 그러더니 똑같은 그 미소를 띠고서 물러가는 시늉을 의미심장하게 해 보였다."]], [["Mistress Affery, heartily glad to effect the proposed compromise, gave in her willing adhesion to it.", "애프리 부인은 그가 제안한 그 타협을 이루게 된 것이 진심으로 반가워, 기꺼이 거기에 따르겠노라 동의했다."], ["The gentleman at once requested her to do him the favour of holding his cloak, took a short run at the narrow window, made a leap at the sill, clung his way up the bricks, and in a moment had his hand at the sash, raising it.", "그 신사는 즉시 그녀에게 자기 망토를 들어 주는 호의를 베풀어 달라 청하고는, 좁은 창문 쪽으로 짧게 달려가 창턱으로 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 벽돌을 붙잡고 기어올라가더니, 순식간에 한 손을 창틀에 대고 그것을 들어 올렸다."], ["His eyes looked so very sinister, as he put his leg into the room and glanced round at Mistress Affery, that she thought with a sudden coldness, if he were to go straight up-stairs to murder the invalid, what could she do to prevent him?", "그가 한쪽 다리를 방 안으로 들여놓고 애프리 부인 쪽을 돌아볼 때 그의 눈이 어찌나 음흉해 보이던지, 그녀는 만약 그가 곧장 위층으로 올라가 그 병자를 살해하려 든다면 자기가 무슨 수로 그것을 막을 수 있을까 하는 생각에 별안간 오싹해졌다."]], [["Happily he had no such purpose; for he reappeared, in a moment, at the house door.", "다행히도 그에게는 그런 속셈이 없었으니, 그는 잠깐 사이에 집 대문 앞에 다시 나타났다. \"자, 부인.\""], ["‘Now, my dear madam,’ he said, as he took back his cloak and threw it on, ‘if you have the goodness to--what the Devil’s that!’", "그가 망토를 되받아 둘러 걸치며 말했다. \"친절을 베풀어 이제—저게 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 소리야!\""]], [["The strangest of sounds.", "더없이 기이한 소리였다."], ["Evidently close at hand from the peculiar shock it communicated to the air, yet subdued as if it were far off.", "공기에 전해지는 그 독특한 진동으로 미루어 분명 바로 가까이에서 나는데도, 멀리 떨어진 데서 나는 듯 잦아든 소리였다."], ["A tremble, a rumble, and a fall of some light dry matter.", "떨림 한 번, 우르릉거림 한 번, 그리고 무언가 가볍고 메마른 것이 무너져 내리는 소리."]], [["‘What the Devil is it?’", "\"저게 대체 무슨 빌어먹을 소리야?\""]], [["‘I don’t know what it is, but I’ve heard the like of it over and over again,’ said Affery, who had caught his arm.", "\"그게 뭔지는 모르지만, 나는 그 비슷한 소리를 몇 번이고 거듭 들었다오.\" 그의 팔을 붙든 애프리가 말했다."]], [["He could hardly be a very brave man, even she thought in her dreamy start and fright, for his trembling lips had turned colourless.", "그녀가 꿈결 같은 놀람과 두려움 속에서 보기에도, 그는 그리 용감한 사람일 수는 없었으니, 그의 떨리는 입술이 핏기를 잃었기 때문이다."], ["After listening a few moments, he made light of it.", "잠깐 귀를 기울이고 나서 그는 그 일을 대수롭지 않게 넘겼다."]], [["‘Bah!", "\"흥!"], ["Nothing!", "아무것도 아니오!"], ["Now, my dear madam, I think you spoke of some clever personage.", "자, 부인, 무슨 약삭빠른 인물에 대해 말씀하셨던 것 같은데."], ["Will you be so good as to confront me with that genius?’ He held the door in his hand, as though he were quite ready to shut her out again if she failed.", "그 잘난 양반과 나를 대면시켜 주시겠소?\" 그는, 그녀가 못 해내면 다시 그녀를 바깥에 가둬 버릴 채비라도 한 듯, 문을 손에 쥔 채로 있었다."]], [["‘Don’t you say anything about the door and me, then,’ whispered Affery.", "\"그럼 문에 대한 거랑 나에 대한 거는 아무 말도 하지 말아 줘요.\" 애프리가 속삭였다."]], [["‘Not a word.’", "\"한 마디도 안 하리다.\""]], [["‘And don’t you stir from here, or speak if she calls, while I run round the corner.’", "\"그리고 내가 모퉁이를 돌아 다녀오는 동안, 여기서 꼼짝도 말고, 마님이 부르셔도 말하지 말아요.\""]], [["‘Madam, I am a statue.’", "\"부인, 나는 석상이외다.\""]], [["Affery had so vivid a fear of his going stealthily up-stairs the moment her back was turned, that after hurrying out of sight, she returned to the gateway to peep at him.", "애프리는 그가 자기 등을 돌리는 순간 살그머니 위층으로 올라가지나 않을까 하는 두려움이 어찌나 생생하던지, 보이지 않는 데까지 서둘러 갔다가 다시 대문께로 돌아와 그를 엿보았다."], ["Seeing him still on the threshold, more out of the house than in it, as if he had no love for darkness and no desire to probe its mysteries, she flew into the next street, and sent a message into the tavern to Mr Flintwinch, who came out directly.", "그가 여전히 그 자리, 마치 어둠을 좋아하지도 않고 그 비밀을 캐고 싶어 하지도 않는 듯, 집 안에 든 것보다 바깥으로 나와 있는 채 문턱에 서 있는 것을 보고서야, 그녀는 옆 거리로 날듯이 달려가 선술집 안의 플린트윈치 씨에게 전갈을 넣었고, 그는 곧바로 나왔다."], ["The two returning together--the lady in advance, and Mr Flintwinch coming up briskly behind, animated with the hope of shaking her before she could get housed--saw the gentleman standing in the same place in the dark, and heard the strong voice of Mrs Clennam calling from her room, ‘Who is it?", "두 사람이 함께 돌아오는데—아내가 앞서고, 플린트윈치 씨는 그녀가 집으로 들기 전에 한바탕 흔들어 주리라는 기대에 들떠 뒤에서 잰걸음으로 따라붙으며—그 신사가 어둠 속 똑같은 자리에 서 있는 것을 보았고, 클레넘 부인이 자기 방에서 부르는 그 우렁찬 목소리를 들었다. \"누구냐?"], ["What is it?", "무슨 일이냐?"], ["Why does no one answer?", "왜 아무도 대답을 않느냐?"], ["Who _is_ that, down there?’", "저 아래 그 사람은 누구냐?\""]], [["# CHAPTER 30. The Word of a Gentleman", "# CHAPTER 30. The Word of a Gentleman"]], [["When Mr and Mrs Flintwinch panted up to the door of the old house in the twilight, Jeremiah within a second of Affery, the stranger started back.", "플린트윈치 부부가 황혼 속 그 낡은 집 문간으로 헐떡이며 올라왔을 때, 제러마이아는 애프리보다 일 초쯤 늦었는데, 낯선 사내가 흠칫 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["‘Death of my soul!’ he exclaimed.", "\"내 영혼이 죽을 일이로군!\" 그가 외쳤다."], ["‘Why, how did you get here?’", "\"아니, 당신이 어떻게 여기 와 있단 말이오?\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, to whom these words were spoken, repaid the stranger’s wonder in full.", "이 말을 들은 플린트윈치 씨는 낯선 사내의 놀라움을 고스란히 되갚았다. 그는 멍한 경악에 휩싸여 사내를 바라보았다."], ["He gazed at him with blank astonishment; he looked over his own shoulder, as expecting to see some one he had not been aware of standing behind him; he gazed at the stranger again, speechlessly, at a loss to know what he meant; he looked to his wife for explanation; receiving none, he pounced upon her, and shook her with such heartiness that he shook her cap off her head, saying between his teeth, with grim raillery, as he did it, ‘Affery, my woman, you must have a dose, my woman!", "자신이 미처 알아채지 못한 누군가가 뒤에 서 있을까 싶어 어깨너머를 돌아보았다. 그러고는 무슨 뜻인지 도무지 알 수 없어 말도 못 한 채 다시 낯선 사내를 바라보았다."], ["This is some of your tricks!", "설명을 구하려 아내를 쳐다보았다."], ["You have been dreaming again, mistress.", "아무 대답도 얻지 못하자, 그는 아내에게 달려들어 모자가 머리에서 벗겨질 만큼 격렬하게 흔들어대며, 그러는 동안 이를 악물고 음울한 야유를 내뱉었다. \"애프리, 이 사람아, 약 한 첩 먹어야겠구먼, 이 사람아!"], ["What’s it about? Who is it?", "또 무슨 수작이야!"], ["What does it mean!", "또 꿈을 꾸고 있었구먼, 마누라. 무슨 꿈이야?"], ["Speak out or be choked!", "이게 누구냐고? 무슨 뜻이냐고!"], ["It’s the only choice I’ll give you.’", "어서 말해, 안 그러면 목을 졸라 버릴 테다! 내가 줄 수 있는 선택은 그것뿐이야.\""]], [["Supposing Mistress Affery to have any power of election at the moment, her choice was decidedly to be choked; for she answered not a syllable to this adjuration, but, with her bare head wagging violently backwards and forwards, resigned herself to her punishment.", "이 추궁의 순간 애프리 부인에게 무언가를 고를 힘이 있었다고 친다면, 그녀의 선택은 단연 목 졸리는 쪽이었다. 이 호령에 한 음절도 답하지 않은 채, 모자도 없는 머리를 앞뒤로 사납게 흔들며 그저 제 벌을 받아들이고 있었으니 말이다."], ["The stranger, however, picking up her cap with an air of gallantry, interposed.", "그러나 낯선 사내가 정중한 태도로 그녀의 모자를 집어 들며 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Permit me,’ said he, laying his hand on the shoulder of Jeremiah, who stopped and released his victim.", "\"제게 맡기시지요.\" 그가 제러마이아의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 말하자, 제러마이아는 멈추고 제 희생양을 놓아주었다."], ["‘Thank you.", "\"고맙습니다."], ["Excuse me.", "실례했군요."], ["Husband and wife I know, from this playfulness.", "이 장난기를 보니 부부 사이임을 알겠습니다."], ["Haha!", "하하!"], ["Always agreeable to see that relation playfully maintained. Listen!", "그 관계가 장난스럽게 유지되는 걸 보는 건 언제나 흐뭇한 일이지요."], ["May I suggest that somebody up-stairs, in the dark, is becoming energetically curious to know what is going on here?’", "들어 보십시오! 위층 어둠 속의 누군가가 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 무척이나 궁금해하고 있는 것 같은데요?\""]], [["This reference to Mrs Clennam’s voice reminded Mr Flintwinch to step into the hall and call up the staircase. ‘It’s all right, I am here, Affery is coming with your light.’ Then he said to the latter flustered woman, who was putting her cap on, ‘Get out with you, and get up-stairs!’ and then turned to the stranger and said to him, ‘Now, sir, what might you please to want?’", "클레넘 부인의 목소리에 대한 이 언급에 플린트윈치 씨는 정신이 들어 현관으로 들어가 계단 위쪽을 향해 소리쳤다. \"다 괜찮습니다, 제가 여기 있어요, 애프리가 등불을 가지고 올라갈 겁니다.\" 그러고는 모자를 다시 쓰고 있는, 허둥대던 그 여인에게 말했다. \"어서 나가, 위층으로 올라가!\" 그런 다음 낯선 사내에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"자, 선생, 무슨 용건이신지요?\""]], [["‘I am afraid,’ said the stranger, ‘I must be so troublesome as to propose a candle.’", "\"송구스럽게도,\" 낯선 사내가 말했다. \"촛불을 하나 청해야 할 만큼 폐를 끼쳐야겠군요.\""]], [["‘True,’ assented Jeremiah.", "\"그렇군요.\" 제러마이아가 수긍했다."], ["‘I was going to do so.", "\"막 그러려던 참이었습니다."], ["Please to stand where you are while I get one.’", "제가 가져올 동안 거기 그대로 서 계시지요.\""]], [["The visitor was standing in the doorway, but turned a little into the gloom of the house as Mr Flintwinch turned, and pursued him with his eyes into the little room, where he groped about for a phosphorus box.", "손님은 문간에 서 있었으나, 플린트윈치 씨가 몸을 돌리자 집 안 어둠 속으로 조금 몸을 틀어, 그가 인광 성냥갑을 더듬어 찾는 작은 방까지 눈으로 그를 좇았다."], ["When he found it, it was damp, or otherwise out of order; and match after match that he struck into it lighted sufficiently to throw a dull glare about his groping face, and to sprinkle his hands with pale little spots of fire, but not sufficiently to light the candle.", "마침내 성냥갑을 찾았을 때 그것은 눅눅했거나 그도 아니면 망가진 상태였다. 그어 대는 성냥마다 더듬거리는 그의 얼굴 둘레로 흐릿한 불빛을 던지고 그의 손에 작고 창백한 불꽃 점들을 흩뿌릴 만큼만 타오를 뿐, 촛불을 붙일 만큼은 타지 않았다."], ["The stranger, taking advantage of this fitful illumination of his visage, looked intently and wonderingly at him.", "낯선 사내는 그의 얼굴을 단속적으로 비추는 이 빛을 틈타 그를 골똘하고 의아한 눈으로 바라보았다."], ["Jeremiah, when he at last lighted the candle, knew he had been doing this, by seeing the last shade of a lowering watchfulness clear away from his face, as it broke into the doubtful smile that was a large ingredient in its expression.", "제러마이아는 마침내 촛불을 붙였을 때, 자신의 표정에서 큰 비중을 차지하는 그 미심쩍은 미소로 얼굴이 펴지면서, 음험하게 경계하던 마지막 그늘이 사라지는 것을 보고서, 사내가 그러고 있었음을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Be so good,’ said Jeremiah, closing the house door, and taking a pretty sharp survey of the smiling visitor in his turn, ‘as to step into my counting-house.--It’s all right, I tell you!’ petulantly breaking off to answer the voice up-stairs, still unsatisfied, though Affery was there, speaking in persuasive tones.", "\"수고스럽겠지만,\" 제러마이아가 집 문을 닫고 이번엔 자기 차례로 미소 짓는 손님을 꽤 날카롭게 훑어보며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t I tell you it’s all right?", "\"제 사무실로 들어가시지요.— 다 괜찮대도요!\" 애프리가 거기서 어르는 목소리로 달래고 있는데도 여전히 진정되지 않은 위층의 목소리에 답하느라 그는 짜증스레 말을 끊었다."], ["Preserve the woman, has she no reason at all in her!’", "\"다 괜찮다고 하지 않았소? 맙소사, 저 여편네는 분별이라곤 도무지 없단 말이야!\""]], [["‘Timorous,’ remarked the stranger.", "\"겁이 많으신가 봅니다.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘Timorous?’ said Mr Flintwinch, turning his head to retort, as he went before with the candle.", "\"겁이 많다고요?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 촛불을 들고 앞서 가면서 받아치려 고개를 돌리며 말했다."], ["‘More courageous than ninety men in a hundred, sir, let me tell you.’", "\"단언컨대, 백 명 중 아흔 명보다 더 용감한 분입니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘Though an invalid?’", "\"병약하신데도요?\""]], [["‘Many years an invalid.", "\"여러 해 병약하셨지요."], ["Mrs Clennam.", "클레넘 부인."], ["The only one of that name left in the House now.", "이제 이 상회에 그 이름으로 남은 유일한 분이지요."], ["My partner.’", "제 동업자입니다.\""]], [["Saying something apologetically as he crossed the hall, to the effect that at that time of night they were not in the habit of receiving any one, and were always shut up, Mr Flintwinch led the way into his own office, which presented a sufficiently business-like appearance.", "플린트윈치 씨는 현관을 가로지르며, 이 시각엔 본래 손님을 들이는 법이 없이 늘 문을 닫아 두는 터라는 식으로 무언가 변명조의 말을 하면서, 충분히 사무적인 모양새를 갖춘 자기 사무실로 앞장서 들어갔다."], ["Here he put the light on his desk, and said to the stranger, with his wryest twist upon him, ‘Your commands.’", "그는 책상에 불을 내려놓고, 입을 한껏 비틀며 낯선 사내에게 말했다. \"분부하시지요.\""]], [["‘My name is Blandois.’", "\"제 이름은 블랑두아입니다.\""]], [["‘Blandois.", "\"블랑두아."], ["I don’t know it,’ said Jeremiah.", "모르는 이름이군요.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["‘I thought it possible,’ resumed the other, ‘that you might have been advised from Paris--’", "\"혹시\" 상대가 말을 이었다. \"파리에서 통지를 받으셨을지도 모른다고 생각했는데—\""]], [["‘We have had no advice from Paris respecting anybody of the name of Blandois,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"블랑두아라는 이름을 가진 그 누구에 관해서도 파리에서 받은 통지는 없습니다.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["‘No?’\n‘No.’", "\"없다고요?\""]], [["Jeremiah stood in his favourite attitude.", "\"없습니다.\" 제러마이아는 좋아하는 자세로 서 있었다."], ["The smiling Mr Blandois, opening his cloak to get his hand to a breast-pocket, paused to say, with a laugh in his glittering eyes, which it occurred to Mr Flintwinch were too near together:", "미소 짓던 블랑두아 씨는 가슴 주머니에 손을 넣으려고 외투를 열다가 잠시 멈추고, 플린트윈치 씨가 보기에 너무 가까이 붙어 있는 듯한 그 반짝이는 눈에 웃음을 띤 채 말했다."]], [["‘You are so like a friend of mine!", "\"당신은 내 친구 하나와 어찌나 닮았는지! 정말로 어스름 속에서 잠시나마 당신을 그 사람으로 착각했을 만큼 똑같지는 않습니다만 — 그 점은 사과드려야겠군요."], ["Not so identically the same as I supposed when I really did for the moment take you to be the same in the dusk--for which I ought to apologise; permit me to do so; a readiness to confess my errors is, I hope, a part of the frankness of my character--still, however, uncommonly like.’", "사과드리게 해 주십시오. 제 잘못을 기꺼이 인정하는 것이, 부디 바라건대, 제 성품의 솔직함의 한 부분이지요 — 그래도 여전히, 비상하게 닮았습니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed?’ said Jeremiah, perversely.", "\"그렇습니까?\" 제러마이아가 심술궂게 말했다."], ["‘But I have not received any letter of advice from anywhere respecting anybody of the name of Blandois.’", "\"하지만 저는 블랑두아라는 이름을 가진 그 누구에 관해서도 어디서든 통지 편지를 받은 바가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said the stranger.", "\"바로 그렇지요.\" 낯선 사내가 말했다."]], [["‘_Just_ so,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["Mr Blandois, not at all put out by this omission on the part of the correspondents of the house of Clennam and Co., took his pocket-book from his breast-pocket, selected a letter from that receptacle, and handed it to Mr Flintwinch.", "클레넘 상회의 거래처들이 보인 이 누락에도 블랑두아 씨는 조금도 당황하지 않고, 가슴 주머니에서 수첩을 꺼내어 그 안에서 편지 한 통을 골라 플린트윈치 씨에게 건넸다."], ["‘No doubt you are well acquainted with the writing.", "\"필체는 잘 아실 겁니다."], ["Perhaps the letter speaks for itself, and requires no advice.", "아마 편지가 스스로 말해 줄 테니 따로 통지가 필요 없겠지요."], ["You are a far more competent judge of such affairs than I am.", "이런 일에 대해선 저보다 당신이 훨씬 유능한 판단가시지요."], ["It is my misfortune to be, not so much a man of business, as what the world calls (arbitrarily) a gentleman.’", "제가 사업가라기보다 세상에서 (제멋대로) 신사라 부르는 부류인 것이 제 불운입니다.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch took the letter, and read, under date of Paris, ‘We have to present to you, on behalf of a highly esteemed correspondent of our Firm, M. Blandois, of this city,’ &c.", "플린트윈치 씨는 편지를 받아 읽었다."], ["&c. ‘Such facilities as he may require and such attentions as may lie in your power,’ &c.", "파리 날짜로, \"당 상사가 깊이 존경하는 거래처를 대신하여, 본 시의 블랑두아 씨를 귀하께 소개합니다.\""], ["&c. ‘Also have to add that if you will honour M.", "운운, \"그가 필요로 할 만한 편의와 귀하의 능력이 닿는 한의 배려를\""], ["Blandois’ drafts at sight to the extent of, say Fifty Pounds sterling (50_l_.),’ &c.", "운운, \"또한 덧붙이건대, 블랑두아 씨의 어음을 일람 즉시, 가령 오십 파운드(50파운드)까지 응해 주신다면\""], ["&c.", "운운."]], [["‘Very good, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"매우 좋습니다, 선생.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Take a chair.", "\"의자에 앉으시지요."], ["To the extent of anything that our House can do--we are in a retired, old-fashioned, steady way of business, sir--we shall be happy to render you our best assistance.", "우리 상회가 할 수 있는 한 무엇이든 — 우리는 한적하고 구식이며 견실한 방식으로 사업을 하는 곳입니다, 선생 — 기꺼이 최선의 도움을 드리겠습니다."], ["I observe, from the date of this, that we could not yet be advised of it.", "이 날짜로 보아, 아직 통지를 받을 수 없었던 게로군요."], ["Probably you came over with the delayed mail that brings the advice.’", "아마 통지를 전하는 지연된 우편물과 함께 건너오신 모양입니다.\""]], [["‘That I came over with the delayed mail, sir,’ returned Mr Blandois, passing his white hand down his high-hooked nose, ‘I know to the cost of my head and stomach: the detestable and intolerable weather having racked them both. You see me in the plight in which I came out of the packet within this half-hour. I ought to have been here hours ago, and then I should not have to apologise--permit me to apologise--for presenting myself so unreasonably, and frightening--no, by-the-bye, you said not frightening; permit me to apologise again--the esteemed lady, Mrs Clennam, in her invalid chamber above stairs.’", "\"제가 지연된 우편물과 함께 건너온 것은, 선생,\" 블랑두아 씨가 우뚝 갈고리 진 코를 따라 하얀 손을 쓸어내리며 답했다. \"제 머리와 위장의 대가로 알고 있지요. 그 진저리 나고 견딜 수 없는 날씨가 둘 다 들쑤셔 놓았으니까요. 반 시간 전 배에서 내렸을 때의 그 몰골 그대로의 저를 보고 계신 겁니다. 몇 시간 전에는 도착했어야 했고, 그랬다면 이렇게 터무니없는 시간에 불쑥 찾아와 — 사과드리게 해 주십시오 — 위층 병실에 계신 존경하는 부인, 클레넘 부인을 놀라게 — 아니, 그러고 보니 놀라게 한 건 아니라 하셨지요. 다시 사과드리게 해 주십시오 — 그런 일은 없었겠지요.\""]], [["Swagger and an air of authorised condescension do so much, that Mr Flintwinch had already begun to think this a highly gentlemanly personage.", "허세와 으스대는 겸양이라는 게 워낙 위력이 커서, 플린트윈치 씨는 벌써 이 사람을 대단히 신사다운 인물로 여기기 시작했다."], ["Not the less unyielding with him on that account, he scraped his chin and said, what could he have the honour of doing for Mr Blandois to-night, out of business hours?", "그렇다고 그에게 조금도 누그러지지 않은 채, 그는 턱을 긁적이며 이 영업 시간 외에 오늘 밤 블랑두아 씨를 위해 무엇을 해 드리는 영광을 누릴 수 있겠느냐고 말했다."]], [["‘Faith!’ returned that gentleman, shrugging his cloaked shoulders, ‘I must change, and eat and drink, and be lodged somewhere.", "\"이런!\" 그 신사가 외투 두른 어깨를 으쓱하며 답했다."], ["Have the kindness to advise me, a total stranger, where, and money is a matter of perfect indifference until to-morrow.", "\"옷을 갈아입고, 먹고 마시고, 어디든 묵어야지요. 부디 친절을 베푸셔서, 일면식도 없는 제게 어디가 좋을지 일러 주십시오."], ["The nearer the place, the better.", "돈은 내일까지 전혀 문제 될 게 없습니다. 가까울수록 좋고요."], ["Next door, if that’s all.’", "바로 옆집이라면 더 바랄 게 없지요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch was slowly beginning, ‘For a gentleman of your habits, there is not in this immediate neighbourhood any hotel--’ when Mr Blandois took him up.", "플린트윈치 씨가 느릿느릿 입을 떼었다. \"당신 같은 습성을 지닌 신사분께는, 이 근방 가까이에 호텔이라곤—\" 그때 블랑두아 씨가 말을 가로챘다."]], [["‘So much for my habits!", "\"제 습성이라니, 무슨 말씀을!"], ["my dear sir,’ snapping his fingers.", "이보십시오,\" 그가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 말했다."], ["‘A citizen of the world has no habits.", "\"세계의 시민에게는 습성이라는 게 없습니다."], ["That I am, in my poor way, a gentleman, by Heaven!", "제가 보잘것없는 나름대로 신사인 것은, 맹세코!"], ["I will not deny, but I have no unaccommodating prejudiced habits.", "부인하지 않겠습니다만, 까다롭고 편협한 습성 따위는 없습니다."], ["A clean room, a hot dish for dinner, and a bottle of not absolutely poisonous wine, are all I want tonight.", "깨끗한 방, 저녁에 따뜻한 요리 한 접시, 그리고 아주 독약은 아닌 포도주 한 병이면 오늘 밤 제가 원하는 전부입니다."], ["But I want that much without the trouble of going one unnecessary inch to get it.’", "다만 그걸 얻겠다고 불필요하게 한 치라도 더 걸어 다니는 수고는 하고 싶지 않을 뿐이지요.\""]], [["‘There is,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with more than his usual deliberation, as he met, for a moment, Mr Blandois’ shining eyes, which were restless; ‘there is a coffee-house and tavern close here, which, so far, I can recommend; but there’s no style about it.’", "\"있긴 합니다.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 잠시 블랑두아 씨의 잠시도 가만있지 않는 번뜩이는 눈과 마주치며 평소보다 더 신중하게 말했다. \"여기서 아주 가까운 곳에 커피점 겸 선술집이 하나 있는데, 그 정도라면 추천할 만합니다. 다만 격식이라곤 없는 곳이지요.\""]], [["‘I dispense with style!’ said Mr Blandois, waving his hand.", "\"격식 따위 사양합니다!\" 블랑두아 씨가 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["‘Do me the honour to show me the house, and introduce me there (if I am not too troublesome), and I shall be infinitely obliged.’", "\"그 집을 보여 주시고 (제가 너무 폐가 되지 않는다면) 거기 소개해 주시는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 무한히 감사하겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, upon this, looked up his hat, and lighted Mr Blandois across the hall again.", "이 말에 플린트윈치 씨는 모자를 찾아 들고, 다시 블랑두아 씨를 위해 현관을 가로질러 불을 밝혔다."], ["As he put the candle on a bracket, where the dark old panelling almost served as an extinguisher for it, he bethought himself of going up to tell the invalid that he would not be absent five minutes.", "어두운 낡은 벽판이 거의 촛불 끄개 노릇이라도 하는 벽 받침대에 촛불을 올려놓다가, 그는 위층으로 올라가 환자에게 오 분도 안 되어 돌아오겠다고 일러 두어야겠다는 생각이 들었다."]], [["‘Oblige me,’ said the visitor, on his saying so, ‘by presenting my card of visit. Do me the favour to add that I shall be happy to wait on Mrs Clennam, to offer my personal compliments, and to apologise for having occasioned any agitation in this tranquil corner, if it should suit her convenience to endure the presence of a stranger for a few minutes, after he shall have changed his wet clothes and fortified himself with something to eat and drink.’", "\"부탁드리건대,\" 손님이 그 말에 응해 말했다. \"제 방문 명함을 전해 주십시오. 더불어 이렇게 전해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주십시오. 클레넘 부인을 찾아뵙고 직접 인사 올리며, 이 고요한 구석에 소동을 일으킨 데 대해 사과드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠다고요. 다만 제가 젖은 옷을 갈아입고 무언가 먹고 마셔 기운을 차린 뒤에, 부인께서 잠시 낯선 자의 존재를 견뎌 주시는 게 편의에 맞으신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["Jeremiah made all despatch, and said, on his return, ‘She’ll be glad to see you, sir; but, being conscious that her sick room has no attractions, wishes me to say that she won’t hold you to your offer, in case you should think better of it.’", "제러마이아는 서둘러 다녀와서 돌아오자 말했다. \"부인께서 기꺼이 뵙겠다 하십니다, 선생. 다만 당신의 병실에 끌릴 만한 데가 없음을 잘 아시는 터라, 혹 마음이 바뀌시거든 그 제안에 매이지 않으셔도 된다고 전하라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["‘To think better of it,’ returned the gallant Blandois, ‘would be to slight a lady; to slight a lady would be to be deficient in chivalry towards the sex; and chivalry towards the sex is a part of my character!’ Thus expressing himself, he threw the draggled skirt of his cloak over his shoulder, and accompanied Mr Flintwinch to the tavern; taking up on the road a porter who was waiting with his portmanteau on the outer side of the gateway.", "\"마음이 바뀐다는 건,\" 정중한 블랑두아가 답했다. \"숙녀를 가벼이 여기는 일이요, 숙녀를 가벼이 여기는 것은 여성에 대한 기사도가 모자란 것이며, 여성에 대한 기사도는 제 성품의 한 부분입니다!\" 이렇게 자기 뜻을 밝힌 그는 흠뻑 젖어 처진 외투 자락을 어깨 너머로 휙 넘기고 플린트윈치 씨를 따라 선술집으로 향했다. 가는 길에 대문 바깥쪽에서 그의 여행 가방을 들고 기다리던 짐꾼 하나를 데리고 갔다."]], [["The house was kept in a homely manner, and the condescension of Mr Blandois was infinite.", "그 집은 수수하게 꾸려지고 있었고, 블랑두아 씨의 겸양은 끝이 없었다."], ["It seemed to fill to inconvenience the little bar in which the widow landlady and her two daughters received him; it was much too big for the narrow wainscoted room with a bagatelle-board in it, that was first proposed for his reception; it perfectly swamped the little private holiday sitting-room of the family, which was finally given up to him.", "과부인 안주인과 두 딸이 그를 맞이한 작은 바는 그로 인해 불편하리만치 가득 차 보였고, 처음 그를 들이려 했던, 베가텔 놀이판이 놓인 비좁은 판벽 방은 그에게 한참 비좁았으며, 끝내 그에게 내준 가족의 작은 명절용 사실(私室)도 그가 들어서니 완전히 압도되어 버렸다."], ["Here, in dry clothes and scented linen, with sleeked hair, a great ring on each forefinger and a massive show of watch-chain, Mr Blandois waiting for his dinner, lolling on a window-seat with his knees drawn up, looked (for all the difference in the setting of the jewel) fearfully and wonderfully like a certain Monsieur Rigaud who had once so waited for his breakfast, lying on the stone ledge of the iron grating of a cell in a villainous dungeon at Marseilles.", "여기, 마른 옷에 향내 나는 속옷 차림으로, 머리는 매끈하게 빗어 넘기고 두 집게손가락마다 큼직한 반지를 끼고 묵직한 시곗줄을 드러낸 채, 블랑두아 씨는 저녁 식사를 기다리며 무릎을 끌어당기고 창가 자리에 비스듬히 기대 있었는데, (장식한 보석의 차림새가 아무리 다르다 한들) 한때 마르세유의 흉악한 지하 감방에서 쇠창살 돌 턱에 누워 아침을 그렇게 기다리던 어느 리고 씨와 무섭도록, 또 기이하리만치 닮아 보였다."]], [["His greed at dinner, too, was closely in keeping with the greed of Monsieur Rigaud at breakfast.", "저녁 식사에서의 그의 탐욕 또한 아침 식사에서 보였던 리고 씨의 탐욕과 빼닮아 있었다."], ["His avaricious manner of collecting all the eatables about him, and devouring some with his eyes while devouring others with his jaws, was the same manner.", "먹을 것을 죄다 제 주위로 그러모으고는, 어떤 것은 턱으로 게걸스레 먹으면서 다른 것은 눈으로 게걸스레 먹어 치우는 그 인색한 태도가 똑같았다."], ["His utter disregard of other people, as shown in his way of tossing the little womanly toys of furniture about, flinging favourite cushions under his boots for a softer rest, and crushing delicate coverings with his big body and his great black head, had the same brute selfishness at the bottom of it.", "여자 취향의 작고 앙증맞은 가구를 함부로 내던지고, 좋아하는 방석을 발밑에 깔아 더 푹신하게 쉬려 하며, 그 큼직한 몸뚱이와 커다란 검은 머리로 섬세한 덮개를 짓이기는 데서 드러난, 타인에 대한 그 철저한 무시도 그 밑바닥엔 똑같은 짐승 같은 이기심이 깔려 있었다."], ["The softly moving hands that were so busy among the dishes had the old wicked facility of the hands that had clung to the bars.", "접시들 사이를 그토록 분주히 오가던 그 부드럽게 움직이는 손은, 쇠창살을 움켜쥐던 그 손이 지녔던 옛 사악한 능란함을 그대로 지니고 있었다."], ["And when he could eat no more, and sat sucking his delicate fingers one by one and wiping them on a cloth, there wanted nothing but the substitution of vine-leaves to finish the picture.", "그리고 더는 먹을 수 없게 되어 섬세한 손가락을 하나씩 빨고는 천에 닦으며 앉아 있을 때, 그 그림을 완성하는 데 모자란 것이라곤 포도나무 잎사귀를 갖다 놓는 것뿐이었다."]], [["On this man, with his moustache going up and his nose coming down in that most evil of smiles, and with his surface eyes looking as if they belonged to his dyed hair, and had had their natural power of reflecting light stopped by some similar process, Nature, always true, and never working in vain, had set the mark, Beware!", "위로 치켜 올라간 콧수염과 아래로 내려온 코로 그 더없이 사악한 미소를 짓고, 마치 염색한 머리에 딸린 듯이, 빛을 반사하는 타고난 능력마저 비슷한 어떤 처리로 멎어 버린 듯한 그 겉만 번드르르한 눈을 지닌 이 사내에게, 언제나 진실하고 결코 헛되이 일하지 않는 자연은, 조심하라! 하는 표지를 새겨 두었다."], ["It was not her fault, if the warning were fruitless.", "그 경고가 헛되이 그쳤다 한들 그것은 자연의 잘못이 아니다."], ["She is never to blame in any such instance.", "자연은 그런 어떤 경우에도 결코 탓할 수 없다."]], [["Mr Blandois, having finished his repast and cleaned his fingers, took a cigar from his pocket, and, lying on the window-seat again, smoked it out at his leisure, occasionally apostrophising the smoke as it parted from his thin lips in a thin stream:", "블랑두아 씨는 식사를 마치고 손가락을 닦은 뒤 주머니에서 시가를 꺼내, 다시 창가 자리에 누워 느긋하게 끝까지 피웠다. 가느다란 입술에서 가느다란 줄기로 갈라져 나가는 연기에 이따금 말을 건네면서."]], [["‘Blandois, you shall turn the tables on society, my little child.", "\"블랑두아, 너는 세상에 한 방 먹여 줄 거야, 내 어린 아이야."], ["Haha!", "하하!"], ["Holy blue, you have begun well, Blandois!", "거룩한 푸른 하늘이여, 시작이 좋구나, 블랑두아!"], ["At a pinch, an excellent master in English or French; a man for the bosom of families!", "막판에 가서는, 영어든 프랑스어든 가르치는 훌륭한 선생이요, 어느 집안의 품에서든 환영받을 사내지!"], ["You have a quick perception, you have humour, you have ease, you have insinuating manners, you have a good appearance; in effect, you are a gentleman!", "너는 눈치가 빠르고, 유머가 있고, 여유가 있고, 사람을 파고드는 매너가 있고, 외모가 좋다. 요컨대 너는 신사야!"], ["A gentleman you shall live, my small boy, and a gentleman you shall die.", "너는 신사로 살 거야, 내 꼬마야, 그리고 신사로 죽을 거야."], ["You shall win, however the game goes.", "판이 어찌 돌아가든 너는 이길 거야."], ["They shall all confess your merit, Blandois.", "그들 모두가 네 진가를 인정할 거야, 블랑두아."], ["You shall subdue the society which has grievously wronged you, to your own high spirit.", "너를 모질게 짓밟은 그 사회를 네 드높은 기개 앞에 굴복시킬 거야. 내 영혼이 죽을 일이로다!"], ["Death of my soul! You are high spirited by right and by nature, my Blandois!’", "너는 권리로도, 천성으로도 기개가 높은 사람이야, 나의 블랑두아!\""]], [["To such soothing murmurs did this gentleman smoke out his cigar and drink out his bottle of wine.", "이런 자위의 중얼거림과 함께 이 신사는 시가를 끝까지 피우고 포도주 한 병을 다 비웠다."], ["Both being finished, he shook himself into a sitting attitude; and with the concluding serious apostrophe, ‘Hold, then!", "둘 다 끝나자 그는 몸을 흔들어 앉은 자세로 고쳐 앉고는, 마무리 짓는 진지한 한마디로, \"자, 그럼!"], ["Blandois, you ingenious one, have all your wits about you!’ arose and went back to the house of Clennam and Co.", "블랑두아, 이 영리한 사람아, 정신 바짝 차려라!\" 하고는 일어나 클레넘 상회의 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["He was received at the door by Mistress Affery, who, under instructions from her lord, had lighted up two candles in the hall and a third on the staircase, and who conducted him to Mrs Clennam’s room.", "문간에서 그를 맞은 것은 애프리 부인이었는데, 그녀는 주인의 지시대로 현관에 촛불 두 개를, 계단에 세 번째 촛불을 켜 두었고, 그를 클레넘 부인의 방으로 안내했다."], ["Tea was prepared there, and such little company arrangements had been made as usually attended the reception of expected visitors.", "그곳에는 차가 준비되어 있었고, 예고된 손님을 맞이할 때 으레 따르던 약간의 손님맞이 차림이 갖추어져 있었다."], ["They were slight on the greatest occasion, never extending beyond the production of the China tea-service, and the covering of the bed with a sober and sad drapery.", "그것은 아무리 큰 일이라 해도 변변찮은 것이어서, 중국제 다기를 내오고 침대를 수수하고 침울한 휘장으로 덮는 정도를 넘지 않았다."], ["For the rest, there was the bier-like sofa with the block upon it, and the figure in the widow’s dress, as if attired for execution; the fire topped by the mound of damped ashes; the grate with its second little mound of ashes; the kettle and the smell of black dye; all as they had been for fifteen years.", "그 밖에는 받침대를 올려놓은 관(棺) 같은 소파와, 마치 처형을 위해 차려입기라도 한 듯 미망인 옷을 입은 인물이 있었고, 눅눅한 잿더미를 얹은 난로와, 또 한 무더기의 작은 잿더미가 쌓인 쇠살대와, 주전자, 그리고 검은 염료 냄새가 있었다. 모든 것이 십오 년 동안 그래 왔던 그대로였다."]], [["Mr Flintwinch presented the gentleman commended to the consideration of Clennam and Co.", "플린트윈치 씨는 클레넘 상회의 배려를 부탁받은 그 신사를 소개했다."], ["Mrs Clennam, who had the letter lying before her, bent her head and requested him to sit.", "편지를 앞에 펼쳐 놓고 있던 클레넘 부인은 고개를 숙이며 그에게 앉기를 청했다."], ["They looked very closely at one another.", "두 사람은 서로를 아주 유심히 바라보았다."], ["That was but natural curiosity.", "그것은 그저 자연스러운 호기심이었다."]], [["‘I thank you, sir, for thinking of a disabled woman like me.", "\"저 같은 불구의 여인을 떠올려 주셔서 감사합니다, 선생."], ["Few who come here on business have any remembrance to bestow on one so removed from observation.", "사업차 이곳을 찾는 이들 가운데 이토록 남의 눈에서 멀어진 사람을 기억해 주는 이는 거의 없지요."], ["It would be idle to expect that they should have.", "그들에게 그러기를 기대하는 것 자체가 부질없는 일입니다."], ["Out of sight, out of mind.", "눈에서 멀어지면 마음에서도 멀어지는 법이니까요."], ["While I am grateful for the exception, I don’t complain of the rule.’", "저는 그 예외에 감사하면서도, 그 통례를 탓하지는 않습니다.\""]], [["Mr Blandois, in his most gentlemanly manner, was afraid he had disturbed her by unhappily presenting himself at such an unconscionable time.", "블랑두아 씨는 더없이 신사다운 태도로, 이런 무례한 시각에 불운하게도 불쑥 찾아와 부인을 어지럽힌 게 아닐까 두렵다고 했다."], ["For which he had already offered his best apologies to Mr--he begged pardon--but by name had not the distinguished honour--", "그 점에 대해선 이미 — 죄송합니다만 — 이름은 영광스럽게도 아직 알지 못하는 그분께 최선의 사과를 올렸노라고 했다."]], [["‘Mr Flintwinch has been connected with the House many years.’", "\"플린트윈치 씨는 여러 해 동안 이 상회와 관계해 온 분입니다.\""]], [["Mr Blandois was Mr Flintwinch’s most obedient humble servant.", "블랑두아 씨는 플린트윈치 씨의 더없이 순종적이고 겸손한 종이었다."], ["He entreated Mr Flintwinch to receive the assurance of his profoundest consideration.", "그는 플린트윈치 씨에게 자신의 가장 깊은 경의의 표시를 받아 달라고 간청했다."]], [["‘My husband being dead,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘and my son preferring another pursuit, our old House has no other representative in these days than Mr Flintwinch.’", "\"제 남편이 세상을 떠나고,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다. \"제 아들이 다른 길을 택한 까닭에, 우리 옛 상회를 대표하는 사람은 이제 플린트윈치 씨뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘What do you call yourself?’ was the surly demand of that gentleman.", "\"그러면 당신은 자신을 뭐라 부릅니까?\" 그 신사가 퉁명스레 따져 물었다."], ["‘You have the head of two men.’", "\"당신은 두 사람 몫의 머리를 가졌는데.\""]], [["‘My sex disqualifies me,’ she proceeded with merely a slight turn of her eyes in Jeremiah’s direction, ‘from taking a responsible part in the business, even if I had the ability; and therefore Mr Flintwinch combines my interest with his own, and conducts it.", "\"제 성별이 자격을 박탈하지요,\" 그녀는 제러마이아 쪽으로 눈을 살짝 돌리며 말을 이었다."], ["It is not what it used to be; but some of our old friends (principally the writers of this letter) have the kindness not to forget us, and we retain the power of doing what they entrust to us as efficiently as we ever did.", "\"설령 능력이 있다 한들, 사업에서 책임 있는 역할을 맡는 데 말입니다. 그래서 플린트윈치 씨가 제 몫을 자기 몫과 합쳐 이끌어 나가지요."], ["This however is not interesting to you.", "예전 같지는 않습니다만, 옛 친구들 가운데 더러는 (주로 이 편지를 쓴 분들이지요) 친절히도 우리를 잊지 않으시고, 우리는 그분들이 맡기는 일을 예전 못지않게 능률적으로 해낼 힘을 지니고 있습니다. 하지만 이런 건 당신께 흥미로운 일이 아니겠지요."], ["You are English, sir?’", "영국분이십니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Faith, madam, no; I am neither born nor bred in England. In effect, I am of no country,’ said Mr Blandois, stretching out his leg and smiting it: ‘I descend from half-a-dozen countries.’", "\"이런, 부인, 아닙니다. 저는 영국에서 나지도, 자라지도 않았습니다. 사실 저는 어느 나라 사람도 아니지요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 다리를 쭉 뻗어 탁 치며 말했다. \"저는 예닐곱 나라의 피를 이어받았습니다.\""]], [["‘You have been much about the world?’", "\"세상을 두루 떠돌아 다니셨군요?\""]], [["‘It is true.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["By Heaven, madam, I have been here and there and everywhere!’", "맹세코, 부인, 저는 여기도 저기도 어디든 가 보았습니다!\""]], [["‘You have no ties, probably.", "\"아마 매인 데가 없으시겠군요."], ["Are not married?’", "결혼은 안 하셨나요?\""]], [["‘Madam,’ said Mr Blandois, with an ugly fall of his eyebrows, ‘I adore your sex, but I am not married--never was.’", "\"부인,\" 블랑두아 씨가 눈썹을 흉하게 늘어뜨리며 말했다. \"저는 여성을 숭배합니다만, 결혼은 안 했습니다 — 한 적도 없지요.\""]], [["Mistress Affery, who stood at the table near him, pouring out the tea, happened in her dreamy state to look at him as he said these words, and to fancy that she caught an expression in his eyes which attracted her own eyes so that she could not get them away.", "그의 옆 탁자에 서서 차를 따르고 있던 애프리 부인은, 몽롱한 상태에서 마침 그가 이 말을 할 때 그를 쳐다보다가, 그의 눈에서 어떤 표정을 잡아챈 듯한 느낌이 들었는데, 그 표정이 제 눈을 끌어당겨 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없었다."], ["The effect of this fancy was to keep her staring at him with the tea-pot in her hand, not only to her own great uneasiness, but manifestly to his, too; and, through them both, to Mrs Clennam’s and Mr Flintwinch’s.", "이 느낌의 결과로 그녀는 찻주전자를 손에 든 채 그를 빤히 쳐다보게 되었고, 이는 자신에게도 몹시 거북했을 뿐 아니라 명백히 그에게도 그러했으며, 두 사람을 통해 클레넘 부인과 플린트윈치 씨에게까지 그러했다."], ["Thus a few ghostly moments supervened, when they were all confusedly staring without knowing why.", "그리하여 모두가 까닭도 모른 채 어지러이 쳐다보는 으스스한 몇 순간이 이어졌다."]], [["‘Affery,’ her mistress was the first to say, ‘what is the matter with you?’", "\"애프리,\" 마침내 그녀의 안주인이 먼저 입을 열었다. \"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Mistress Affery, with her disengaged left hand extended towards the visitor.", "\"모르겠어요.\" 애프리 부인이 빈 왼손을 손님 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했다."], ["‘It ain’t me.", "\"제가 아니에요."], ["It’s him!’", "저 사람이에요!\""]], [["‘What does this good woman mean?’ cried Mr Blandois, turning white, hot, and slowly rising with a look of such deadly wrath that it contrasted surprisingly with the slight force of his words.", "\"이 선량한 여인이 무슨 말을 하는 거요?\" 블랑두아 씨가 새파랗게, 또 시뻘겋게 질려 외치며, 그 말의 가벼운 어조와는 놀라우리만치 대조되는 무시무시한 분노의 표정으로 천천히 일어섰다."], ["‘How is it possible to understand this good creature?’", "\"이 선량한 사람의 말을 어찌 알아들을 수 있단 말입니까?\""]], [["‘It’s _not_ possible,’ said Mr Flintwinch, screwing himself rapidly in that direction.", "\"알아들을 수 없지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 그쪽으로 잽싸게 몸을 비틀며 말했다."], ["‘She don’t know what she means.", "\"자기가 무슨 소릴 하는지도 몰라요."], ["She’s an idiot, a wanderer in her mind.", "머리가 좀 모자란 데다 정신이 헤매는 여편네입니다."], ["She shall have a dose, she shall have such a dose!", "약을 먹여야지, 한 첩 톡톡히 먹여야겠어! 어서 나가, 이 사람아.\""], ["Get along with you, my woman,’ he added in her ear, ‘get along with you, while you know you’re Affery, and before you’re shaken to yeast.’", "그가 그녀의 귀에 대고 덧붙였다. \"당신이 애프리인 줄 알 때, 효모 반죽처럼 흔들려 버리기 전에 어서 나가라고.\""]], [["Mistress Affery, sensible of the danger in which her identity stood, relinquished the tea-pot as her husband seized it, put her apron over her head, and in a twinkling vanished.", "애프리 부인은 자기 정체가 처한 위험을 알아채고, 남편이 찻주전자를 낚아채자 그것을 내주고는, 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓰고 눈 깜짝할 새에 사라져 버렸다."], ["The visitor gradually broke into a smile, and sat down again.", "손님은 차츰 미소를 띠더니 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["‘You’ll excuse her, Mr Blandois,’ said Jeremiah, pouring out the tea himself, ‘she’s failing and breaking up; that’s what she’s about. Do you take sugar, sir?’", "\"저 사람을 용서하십시오, 블랑두아 씨.\" 제러마이아가 직접 차를 따르며 말했다. \"쇠약해져서 무너져 가고 있어요. 그게 저 사람의 형편입니다. 설탕은 넣으십니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Thank you, no tea for me.--Pardon my observing it, but that’s a very remarkable watch!’", "\"고맙습니다만, 저는 차는 됐습니다.— 이런 말씀 드려 무례합니다만, 그건 참으로 진귀한 시계로군요!\""]], [["The tea-table was drawn up near the sofa, with a small interval between it and Mrs Clennam’s own particular table.", "찻상은 소파 가까이로 끌어다 놓여, 클레넘 부인의 전용 탁자와는 약간의 간격을 두고 있었다."], ["Mr Blandois in his gallantry had risen to hand that lady her tea (her dish of toast was already there), and it was in placing the cup conveniently within her reach that the watch, lying before her as it always did, attracted his attention.", "블랑두아 씨는 정중하게도 그 부인에게 차를 건네려 일어섰고 (그녀의 토스트 접시는 이미 거기 있었다), 그녀의 손 닿기 좋은 곳에 찻잔을 놓다가, 늘 그렇듯 그녀 앞에 놓여 있던 그 시계가 그의 눈길을 끌었다."], ["Mrs Clennam looked suddenly up at him.", "클레넘 부인이 불쑥 그를 올려다보았다."]], [["‘May I be permitted?", "\"좀 봐도 될까요?"], ["Thank you.", "감사합니다."], ["A fine old-fashioned watch,’ he said, taking it in his hand.", "멋진 구식 시계로군요.\" 그가 그것을 손에 들며 말했다."], ["‘Heavy for use, but massive and genuine.", "\"쓰기엔 무겁지만, 묵직하고 진짜배기입니다."], ["I have a partiality for everything genuine.", "저는 진짜인 것은 무엇이든 좋아하지요."], ["Such as I am, I am genuine myself.", "이런 저 자신도 진짜배기입니다."], ["Hah!", "하!"], ["A gentleman’s watch with two cases in the old fashion.", "옛날식으로 케이스가 두 겹인 신사용 시계로군요."], ["May I remove it from the outer case?", "바깥 케이스에서 빼내도 될까요?"], ["Thank you.", "감사합니다."], ["Aye?", "어라?"], ["An old silk watch-lining, worked with beads!", "구슬로 수놓은 낡은 비단 시계 안감이로군요!"], ["I have often seen these among old Dutch people and Belgians.", "옛 네덜란드 사람들과 벨기에 사람들 사이에서 이런 걸 자주 보았습니다."], ["Quaint things!’", "진기한 물건이지요!\""]], [["‘They are old-fashioned, too,’ said Mrs Clennam.", "\"그것도 구식이지요.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Very.", "\"무척이나요."], ["But this is not so old as the watch, I think?’", "그런데 이건 시계만큼 오래되진 않은 것 같은데요?\""]], [["‘I think not.’", "\"그렇겠지요.\""]], [["‘Extraordinary how they used to complicate these cyphers!’ remarked Mr Blandois, glancing up with his own smile again.", "\"이런 머리글자들을 어쩌면 이리도 복잡하게 만들어 놓았던지!\" 블랑두아 씨가 다시 자기 특유의 미소를 띠며 올려다보고 말했다."], ["‘Now is this D. N.", "\"자, 이게 D."], ["F.?", "N."], ["It might be almost anything.’", "F.인가요? 거의 무엇이든 될 수 있겠는데요.\""]], [["‘Those are the letters.’", "\"그 글자들이 맞습니다.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, who had been observantly pausing all this time with a cup of tea in his hand, and his mouth open ready to swallow the contents, began to do so: always entirely filling his mouth before he emptied it at a gulp; and always deliberating again before he refilled it.", "이 내내 찻잔을 손에 들고 안의 내용물을 삼킬 준비로 입을 벌린 채 유심히 멈춰 있던 플린트윈치 씨가 마침내 마시기 시작했다. 그는 늘 입을 가득 채운 다음에야 한 모금에 비워 냈고, 다시 채우기 전에는 늘 한참 뜸을 들였다."]], [["‘D. N.", "\"D."], ["F.", "N."], ["was some tender, lovely, fascinating fair-creature, I make no doubt,’ observed Mr Blandois, as he snapped on the case again.", "F.는 분명 어느 다정하고 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 미인이었겠지요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 케이스를 도로 탁 닫으며 말했다."], ["‘I adore her memory on the assumption.", "\"그렇다 치고 저는 그 추억을 숭배합니다."], ["Unfortunately for my peace of mind, I adore but too readily.", "제 마음의 평안에는 안된 일이지만, 저는 너무도 쉽게 숭배에 빠지지요."], ["It may be a vice, it may be a virtue, but adoration of female beauty and merit constitutes three parts of my character, madam.’", "그것이 악덕일 수도, 미덕일 수도 있겠으나, 여성의 아름다움과 가치에 대한 숭배가 제 성품의 네 부분 가운데 셋을 이룬답니다, 부인.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch had by this time poured himself out another cup of tea, which he was swallowing in gulps as before, with his eyes directed to the invalid.", "플린트윈치 씨는 이때쯤 또 한 잔의 차를 따라, 환자에게 눈길을 둔 채 아까처럼 모금모금 삼키고 있었다."]], [["‘You may be heart-free here, sir,’ she returned to Mr Blandois.", "\"여기서라면 당신의 마음은 자유로워도 되겠군요, 선생.\" 그녀가 블랑두아 씨에게 답했다."], ["‘Those letters are not intended, I believe, for the initials of any name.’", "\"제 생각엔, 그 글자들은 어떤 이름의 머리글자를 뜻하는 게 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Of a motto, perhaps,’ said Mr Blandois, casually.", "\"그렇다면 좌우명의 머리글자겠군요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 무심코 말했다."]], [["‘Of a sentence.", "\"한 문장이지요."], ["They have always stood, I believe, for Do Not Forget!’", "제가 알기로 그것은 늘 '잊지 마라!'를 뜻해 왔습니다.\""]], [["‘And naturally,’ said Mr Blandois, replacing the watch and stepping backward to his former chair, ‘you do _not_ forget.’", "\"그러면 당연히,\" 블랑두아 씨가 시계를 도로 놓고 본래 앉았던 의자로 물러서며 말했다. \"당신은 잊지 않으시겠군요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, finishing his tea, not only took a longer gulp than he had taken yet, but made his succeeding pause under new circumstances: that is to say, with his head thrown back and his cup held still at his lips, while his eyes were still directed at the invalid.", "차를 다 마신 플린트윈치 씨는 여태껏보다 더 긴 한 모금을 들이켰을 뿐 아니라, 그다음의 뜸을 새로운 자세로 들였다. 다시 말해, 고개를 뒤로 젖히고 잔을 입에 댄 채 그대로 두고서 눈은 여전히 환자를 향한 것이다."], ["She had that force of face, and that concentrated air of collecting her firmness or obstinacy, which represented in her case what would have been gesture and action in another, as she replied with her deliberate strength of speech:", "그녀는 신중한 어조의 힘으로 답하면서, 그녀의 경우에 다른 사람이라면 몸짓과 행동으로 드러냈을 것을 대신하는, 그 강인한 얼굴과 굳건함 혹은 완고함을 그러모으는 집중된 기색을 보였다."]], [["‘No, sir, I do not forget.", "\"아니요, 선생, 저는 잊지 않습니다."], ["To lead a life as monotonous as mine has been during many years, is not the way to forget.", "여러 해 동안 저처럼 단조로운 삶을 사는 것은 잊는 길이 아니지요."], ["To lead a life of self-correction is not the way to forget.", "자기 교정의 삶을 사는 것은 잊는 길이 아닙니다."], ["To be sensible of having (as we all have, every one of us, all the children of Adam!) offences to expiate and peace to make, does not justify the desire to forget.", "(우리 모두가, 우리 한 사람 한 사람, 아담의 모든 자손이 그러하듯이!) 속죄할 죄와 이루어야 할 화해가 있음을 자각하는 것은, 잊고자 하는 마음을 정당화해 주지 않습니다."], ["Therefore I have long dismissed it, and I neither forget nor wish to forget.’", "그러므로 저는 오래전에 그 마음을 떨쳐 버렸고, 저는 잊지도 않거니와 잊기를 바라지도 않습니다.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, who had latterly been shaking the sediment at the bottom of his tea-cup, round and round, here gulped it down, and putting the cup in the tea-tray, as done with, turned his eyes upon Mr Blandois as if to ask him what he thought of that?", "근래 찻잔 바닥의 찌꺼기를 빙빙 돌리고 있던 플린트윈치 씨는 여기서 그것을 꿀꺽 삼키고는, 다 마쳤다는 듯 잔을 찻쟁반에 내려놓고, 마치 그 점을 어찌 생각하느냐고 묻기라도 하듯 블랑두아 씨에게 눈을 돌렸다."]], [["‘All expressed, madam,’ said Mr Blandois, with his smoothest bow and his white hand on his breast, ‘by the word “naturally,”", "\"전부 다 담겨 있었지요, 부인.\" 블랑두아 씨가 더없이 매끄러운 절과 함께 하얀 손을 가슴에 얹으며 말했다."], ["which I am proud to have had sufficient apprehension and appreciation (but without appreciation I could not be Blandois) to employ.’", "\"제가 영광스럽게도 충분한 이해력과 안목을 (안목이 없다면 저는 블랑두아일 수 없지요) 갖추어 쓴 그 '당연히'라는 말 한마디에 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Pardon me, sir,’ she returned, ‘if I doubt the likelihood of a gentleman of pleasure, and change, and politeness, accustomed to court and to be courted--’", "\"용서하십시오, 선생.\" 그녀가 답했다. \"쾌락과 변화와 예절을 좇으며, 떠받들고 또 떠받들어지는 데 익숙한 신사분이—\""]], [["‘Oh madam!", "\"오, 부인!"], ["By Heaven!’", "맹세코!\""]], [["‘--If I doubt the likelihood of such a character quite comprehending what belongs to mine in my circumstances. Not to obtrude doctrine upon you,’ she looked at the rigid pile of hard pale books before her, ‘(for you go your own way, and the consequences are on your own head), I will say this much: that I shape my course by pilots, strictly by proved and tried pilots, under whom I cannot be shipwrecked--can not be--and that if I were unmindful of the admonition conveyed in those three letters, I should not be half as chastened as I am.’", "\"—그런 분이 제 처지에서 제게 속한 것을 온전히 이해할 가능성을 제가 의심한다 해서 말입니다. 당신께 교리를 강요하려는 건 아닙니다만,\" 그녀는 앞에 놓인 단단하고 빛바랜 책들의 뻣뻣한 더미를 바라보았다. \"(당신은 당신의 길을 가시고, 그 결과는 당신 자신의 머리 위에 있으니까요) 이 정도는 말씀드리겠습니다. 저는 인도자들을 따라, 엄격히 검증되고 시험된 인도자들을 따라 제 항로를 잡아 나가는데, 그분들 아래서라면 저는 결코 난파할 수 없고 — 결코 그럴 수 없으며 — 만일 제가 그 세 글자에 담긴 훈계를 마음에 두지 않았다면, 저는 지금의 절반만큼도 단련되지 못했을 거라고요.\""]], [["It was curious how she seized the occasion to argue with some invisible opponent.", "그녀가 그 기회를 붙잡아 보이지 않는 어떤 적수와 논쟁을 벌이는 모습은 묘했다."], ["Perhaps with her own better sense, always turning upon herself and her own deception.", "어쩌면 늘 자기 자신과 자신의 기만으로 향하는, 자기 안의 더 나은 분별과의 논쟁이었는지도 모른다."]], [["‘If I forgot my ignorances in my life of health and freedom, I might complain of the life to which I am now condemned.", "\"만일 건강하고 자유롭던 시절의 제 무지함들을 제가 잊는다면, 지금 제가 처한 이 삶을 두고 불평할 수도 있겠지요."], ["I never do; I never have done.", "저는 결코 그러지 않습니다."], ["If I forgot that this scene, the Earth, is expressly meant to be a scene of gloom, and hardship, and dark trial, for the creatures who are made out of its dust, I might have some tenderness for its vanities.", "결코 그래 본 적이 없습니다. 만일 이 무대, 곧 이 땅이, 그 흙으로 빚어진 피조물들에게 음울과 고난과 어두운 시련의 무대가 되도록 분명히 정해졌음을 제가 잊는다면, 그 헛된 것들에 얼마간의 애정을 품을 수도 있겠지요."], ["But I have no such tenderness.", "하지만 저는 그런 애정이 없습니다."], ["If I did not know that we are, every one, the subject (most justly the subject) of a wrath that must be satisfied, and against which mere actions are nothing, I might repine at the difference between me, imprisoned here, and the people who pass that gateway yonder.", "만일 우리가, 우리 모두가, 반드시 채워져야 하고 한낱 행위 따위로는 어찌할 수 없는 어떤 진노의 (지극히 정당하게도 그 진노의) 대상임을 제가 모른다면, 여기 갇힌 저와 저기 저 대문을 지나가는 사람들 사이의 차이를 두고 불평할지도 모르지요."], ["But I take it as a grace and favour to be elected to make the satisfaction I am making here, to know what I know for certain here, and to work out what I have worked out here.", "하지만 저는 여기서 제가 치르고 있는 이 보속을 치르도록, 여기서 제가 확실히 아는 것을 알도록, 여기서 제가 풀어낸 것을 풀어내도록 택함받은 것을 은총이요 은혜로 받아들입니다."], ["My affliction might otherwise have had no meaning to me.", "그렇지 않았다면 제 고난은 제게 아무 뜻도 없었을지 모릅니다."], ["Hence I would forget, and I do forget, nothing.", "그러므로 저는 아무것도 잊지 않으려 하고, 또 잊지 않습니다."], ["Hence I am contented, and say it is better with me than with millions.’", "그러므로 저는 만족하며, 제 처지가 수백만의 처지보다 낫다고 말합니다.\""]], [["As she spoke these words, she put her hand upon the watch, and restored it to the precise spot on her little table which it always occupied.", "이 말을 하면서 그녀는 시계에 손을 얹어, 작은 탁자 위 그것이 늘 차지하던 바로 그 자리에 도로 놓았다."], ["With her touch lingering upon it, she sat for some moments afterwards, looking at it steadily and half-defiantly.", "손길을 그 위에 머무른 채, 그녀는 그 뒤로 한동안 앉아서, 시계를 한결같이, 또 반쯤 도전하듯이 바라보았다."]], [["Mr Blandois, during this exposition, had been strictly attentive, keeping his eyes fastened on the lady, and thoughtfully stroking his moustache with his two hands.", "이 설명이 이어지는 동안 블랑두아 씨는 두 손으로 골똘히 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며, 부인에게 눈을 박은 채 한껏 주의를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Mr Flintwinch had been a little fidgety, and now struck in.", "플린트윈치 씨는 다소 안절부절못하다가, 이제 끼어들었다."]], [["‘There, there, there!’ said he.", "\"자, 자, 자!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘That is quite understood, Mrs Clennam, and you have spoken piously and well.", "\"그건 잘 알았습니다, 클레넘 부인, 경건하고 훌륭하게 말씀하셨어요."], ["Mr Blandois, I suspect, is not of a pious cast.’", "블랑두아 씨는 제가 보기에 경건한 부류는 아닌 듯합니다만.\""]], [["‘On the contrary, sir!’ that gentleman protested, snapping his fingers.", "\"천만에요, 선생!\" 그 신사가 손가락을 딱 튕기며 항변했다."], ["‘Your pardon!", "\"용서하십시오!"], ["It’s a part of my character.", "그건 제 성품의 한 부분입니다."], ["I am sensitive, ardent, conscientious, and imaginative.", "저는 감수성이 풍부하고, 열정적이며, 양심적이고, 상상력이 풍부하지요."], ["A sensitive, ardent, conscientious, and imaginative man, Mr Flintwinch, must be that, or nothing!’", "감수성 풍부하고 열정적이며 양심적이고 상상력 풍부한 사람이라면, 플린트윈치 씨, 경건한 사람이거나, 아니면 아무것도 아닌 거지요!\""]], [["There was an inkling of suspicion in Mr Flintwinch’s face that he might be nothing, as he swaggered out of his chair (it was characteristic of this man, as it is of all men similarly marked, that whatever he did, he overdid, though it were sometimes by only a hairsbreadth), and approached to take his leave of Mrs Clennam.", "그가 의자에서 으스대며 일어나 (이 사내는, 그와 비슷하게 특징지어진 모든 사내가 그러하듯, 무슨 짓을 하든 도가 지나친 것이 특징이었으니, 때로는 그것이 머리카락 한 올 차이일 뿐이라 해도 그러했다) 클레넘 부인에게 작별 인사를 하러 다가갈 때, 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에는 그가 아무것도 아닐지도 모른다는 어렴풋한 의심이 어렸다."]], [["‘With what will appear to you the egotism of a sick old woman, sir,’ she then said, ‘though really through your accidental allusion, I have been led away into the subject of myself and my infirmities.", "\"병든 늙은 여자의 이기심으로 보이실 테지만, 선생,\" 그러자 그녀가 말했다."], ["Being so considerate as to visit me, I hope you will be likewise so considerate as to overlook that.", "\"실은 당신의 우연한 언급 탓에, 저는 저 자신과 제 병약함이라는 주제로 끌려 들어가고 말았습니다. 이토록 사려 깊게 저를 찾아 주셨으니, 부디 그 점도 사려 깊게 눈감아 주시기 바랍니다."], ["Don’t compliment me, if you please.’ For he was evidently going to do it.", "제발 저를 추켜세우지는 마십시오.\" 그가 분명 그러려는 참이었기 때문이다."], ["‘Mr Flintwinch will be happy to render you any service, and I hope your stay in this city may prove agreeable.’", "\"플린트윈치 씨가 기꺼이 어떤 도움이든 드릴 것이며, 이 도시에서의 체류가 즐거운 것이 되기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["Mr Blandois thanked her, and kissed his hand several times.", "블랑두아 씨는 그녀에게 감사하며 여러 번 손에 입을 맞추었다. \"낡은 방이로군요.\""], ["‘This is an old room,’ he remarked, with a sudden sprightliness of manner, looking round when he got near the door, ‘I have been so interested that I have not observed it.", "그가 문 가까이에 이르러 둘러보면서, 별안간 쾌활해진 태도로 말했다. \"너무 흥미로워서 미처 방을 살펴보지 못했습니다."], ["But it’s a genuine old room.’", "하지만 진짜배기 낡은 방이군요.\""]], [["‘It is a genuine old house,’ said Mrs Clennam, with her frozen smile.", "\"진짜배기 낡은 집이지요.\" 클레넘 부인이 얼어붙은 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["‘A place of no pretensions, but a piece of antiquity.’", "\"내세울 것 없는 곳이지만, 오래된 골동품 같은 데지요.\""]], [["‘Faith!’ cried the visitor.", "\"이런!\" 손님이 외쳤다."], ["‘If Mr Flintwinch would do me the favour to take me through the rooms on my way out, he could hardly oblige me more.", "\"플린트윈치 씨께서 나가는 길에 방들을 두루 안내해 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주신다면, 더없이 기쁘겠습니다."], ["An old house is a weakness with me.", "낡은 집이라면 제가 사족을 못 씁니다."], ["I have many weaknesses, but none greater.", "약점이야 많습니다만, 이보다 더한 건 없지요."], ["I love and study the picturesque in all its varieties.", "저는 그림처럼 아름다운 것을 그 온갖 형태로 사랑하고 또 탐구합니다."], ["I have been called picturesque myself.", "저 자신이 그림 같다는 소리도 들어 보았지요."], ["It is no merit to be picturesque--I have greater merits, perhaps--but I may be, by an accident.", "그림 같다는 게 무슨 미덕은 아닙니다만 — 어쩌면 제겐 더 큰 미덕들이 있겠지요 — 그래도 어쩌다 보니 그럴 수도 있으니까요."], ["Sympathy, sympathy!’", "공감, 공감이지요!\""]], [["‘I tell you beforehand, Mr Blandois, that you’ll find it very dingy and very bare,’ said Jeremiah, taking up the candle. ‘It’s not worth your looking at.’But Mr Blandois, smiting him in a friendly manner on the back, only laughed; so the said Blandois kissed his hand again to Mrs Clennam, and they went out of the room together.", "\"미리 말씀드립니다만, 블랑두아 씨, 무척 칙칙하고 무척 휑하다는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다.\" 제러마이아가 촛불을 집어 들며 말했다. \"보실 가치가 없는 곳이에요.\" 그러나 블랑두아 씨는 그의 등을 다정스레 탁 치며 그저 웃기만 했다. 그리하여 그 블랑두아는 클레넘 부인에게 다시 한번 손에 입을 맞추고는, 두 사람은 함께 방을 나섰다."]], [["‘You don’t care to go up-stairs?’ said Jeremiah, on the landing.", "\"위층은 보고 싶지 않으시지요?\" 층계참에서 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["‘On the contrary, Mr Flintwinch; if not tiresome to you, I shall be ravished!’", "\"천만에요, 플린트윈치 씨. 번거롭지 않으시다면, 황홀할 따름이겠습니다!\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, therefore, wormed himself up the staircase, and Mr Blandois followed close.", "그리하여 플린트윈치 씨는 몸을 비틀며 계단을 올라갔고, 블랑두아 씨가 바짝 뒤따랐다."], ["They ascended to the great garret bed-room which Arthur had occupied on the night of his return.", "그들은 아서가 돌아온 밤에 묵었던 커다란 다락 침실까지 올라갔다. \"자, 블랑두아 씨!\""], ["‘There, Mr Blandois!’ said Jeremiah, showing it, ‘I hope you may think that worth coming so high to see.", "제러마이아가 그곳을 보여 주며 말했다. \"이렇게 높이 올라와 볼 가치가 있다고 여기실지 모르겠군요."], ["I confess I don’t.’", "솔직히 저는 아닙니다만.\""]], [["Mr Blandois being enraptured, they walked through other garrets and passages, and came down the staircase again.", "블랑두아 씨가 황홀해하기에, 그들은 다른 다락방들과 복도들을 두루 거닐고는 다시 계단을 내려왔다."], ["By this time Mr Flintwinch had remarked that he never found the visitor looking at any room, after throwing one quick glance around, but always found the visitor looking at him, Mr Flintwinch.", "이즈음 플린트윈치 씨는, 손님이 어느 방을 보든 한 차례 휙 둘러본 뒤로는 방을 보고 있는 법이 없고 늘 자신을, 곧 플린트윈치 씨를 보고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["With this discovery in his thoughts, he turned about on the staircase for another experiment.", "이 발견을 머릿속에 둔 채, 그는 또 한 번 시험해 보려고 계단에서 몸을 돌렸다."], ["He met his eyes directly; and on the instant of their fixing one another, the visitor, with that ugly play of nose and moustache, laughed (as he had done at every similar moment since they left Mrs Clennam’s chamber) a diabolically silent laugh.", "그는 곧장 손님의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그리고 두 사람이 서로 눈을 붙박는 그 순간, 손님은 그 흉한 코와 콧수염의 놀림과 함께, (클레넘 부인의 방을 나선 뒤로 그런 비슷한 순간마다 그래 왔듯이) 악마처럼 소리 없는 웃음을 지었다."]], [["As a much shorter man than the visitor, Mr Flintwinch was at the physical disadvantage of being thus disagreeably leered at from a height; and as he went first down the staircase, and was usually a step or two lower than the other, this disadvantage was at the time increased.", "플린트윈치 씨는 손님보다 훨씬 키가 작은 터라, 이렇게 높은 데서 거북스럽게 내려다보며 곁눈질당하는 신체적 불리함을 안고 있었다. 게다가 그가 앞장서서 계단을 내려가느라 대개 상대보다 한두 칸 아래에 있었으니, 이 불리함은 당시 더욱 커졌다."], ["He postponed looking at Mr Blandois again until this accidental inequality was removed by their having entered the late Mr Clennam’s room.", "그는 두 사람이 고(故) 클레넘 씨의 방으로 들어서서 이 우연한 높이 차가 사라질 때까지 블랑두아 씨를 다시 보는 것을 미루었다."], ["But, then twisting himself suddenly round upon him, he found his look unchanged.", "그러나 그때 별안간 그에게로 몸을 홱 틀어 돌아보니, 그의 눈빛은 변함이 없었다."]], [["‘A most admirable old house,’ smiled Mr Blandois.", "\"참으로 감탄스러운 낡은 집이로군요.\" 블랑두아 씨가 미소 지었다."], ["‘So mysterious.", "\"어찌나 신비로운지."], ["Do you never hear any haunted noises here?’", "여기서 유령이 내는 소리 같은 건 들어 본 적이 없으십니까?\""]], [["‘Noises,’ returned Mr Flintwinch.", "\"소리라니요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했다."], ["‘No.’", "\"없습니다.\""]], [["‘Nor see any devils?’", "\"악마를 본 적도 없고요?\""]], [["‘Not,’ said Mr Flintwinch, grimly screwing himself at his questioner, ‘not any that introduce themselves under that name and in that capacity.’", "\"없지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 질문자에게로 험상궂게 몸을 비틀며 말했다. \"적어도 그런 이름과 그런 자격으로 자기를 소개하는 놈은 없었습니다.\""]], [["‘Haha!", "\"하하!"], ["A portrait here, I see.’", "여기 초상화가 하나 있군요.\""]], [["(Still looking at Mr Flintwinch, as if he were the portrait.)", "(여전히 플린트윈치 씨를, 마치 그가 초상화이기라도 한 듯 바라보면서.)"]], [["‘It’s a portrait, sir, as you observe.’", "\"보시다시피 초상화지요, 선생.\""]], [["‘May I ask the subject, Mr Flintwinch?’", "\"누구인지 여쭤도 되겠습니까, 플린트윈치 씨?\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam, deceased.", "\"고(故) 클레넘 씨입니다."], ["Her husband.’", "부인의 남편이지요.\""]], [["‘Former owner of the remarkable watch, perhaps?’ said the visitor.", "\"그 진귀한 시계의 옛 주인이겠군요?\" 손님이 말했다."]], [["Mr Flintwinch, who had cast his eyes towards the portrait, twisted himself about again, and again found himself the subject of the same look and smile.", "초상화 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸던 플린트윈치 씨가 다시 몸을 비틀어 돌아보니, 또다시 그 똑같은 눈빛과 미소의 대상이 된 자신을 발견했다."], ["‘Yes, Mr Blandois,’ he replied tartly.", "\"그렇습니다, 블랑두아 씨.\" 그가 톡 쏘듯 답했다."], ["‘It was his, and his uncle’s before him, and Lord knows who before him; and that’s all I can tell you of its pedigree.’", "\"그분 것이었고, 그 전에는 그분 삼촌 것이었고, 그 전에는 또 누구 것이었는지는 하느님이나 아시겠지요. 그 내력에 대해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 건 그게 전부입니다.\""]], [["‘That’s a strongly marked character, Mr Flintwinch, our friend up-stairs.’", "\"위층의 우리 친구분은, 플린트윈치 씨, 개성이 뚜렷한 분이로군요.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ said Jeremiah, twisting himself at the visitor again, as he did during the whole of this dialogue, like some screw-machine that fell short of its grip; for the other never changed, and he always felt obliged to retreat a little.", "\"그렇지요, 선생.\" 제러마이아가, 이 대화 내내 그러했듯, 마치 무는 힘이 모자란 나사 기계처럼 또다시 손님에게로 몸을 비틀며 말했다."], ["‘She is a remarkable woman.", "상대는 결코 변하지 않았고, 그는 늘 조금씩 물러서지 않을 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["Great fortitude--great strength of mind.’", "\"비범한 여인이지요. 대단한 불굴의 정신 — 대단한 마음의 강인함을 지니셨습니다.\""]], [["‘They must have been very happy,’ said Blandois.", "\"두 분은 무척 행복하셨겠군요.\" 블랑두아가 말했다."]], [["‘Who?’ demanded Mr Flintwinch, with another screw at him.", "\"누가요?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 또 한 번 그에게 몸을 비틀며 따져 물었다."]], [["Mr Blandois shook his right forefinger towards the sick room, and his left forefinger towards the portrait, and then, putting his arms akimbo and striding his legs wide apart, stood smiling down at Mr Flintwinch with the advancing nose and the retreating moustache.", "블랑두아 씨는 오른 집게손가락을 병실 쪽으로, 왼 집게손가락을 초상화 쪽으로 까딱이고는, 두 팔을 양 허리에 척 짚고 다리를 넓게 벌리고 서서, 나아오는 코와 물러나는 콧수염으로 플린트윈치 씨를 내려다보며 미소 지었다."]], [["‘As happy as most other married people, I suppose,’ returned Mr Flintwinch.", "\"여느 부부들 대개만큼은 행복했겠지요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했다."], ["‘I can’t say. I don’t know.", "\"저야 말할 수 없습니다."], ["There are secrets in all families.’", "모르지요. 어느 집안에나 비밀은 있는 법이니까요.\""]], [["‘Secrets!’ cried Mr Blandois, quickly.", "\"비밀이라!\" 블랑두아 씨가 잽싸게 외쳤다."], ["‘Say it again, my son.’", "\"한 번 더 말해 보시오, 이 사람아.\""]], [["‘I say,’ replied Mr Flintwinch, upon whom he had swelled himself so suddenly that Mr Flintwinch found his face almost brushed by the dilated chest.", "\"제 말은,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 답했는데, 그가 어찌나 별안간 몸을 부풀렸던지 플린트윈치 씨는 자기 얼굴이 그 부푼 가슴팍에 거의 스칠 지경이었다."], ["‘I say there are secrets in all families.’", "\"어느 집안에나 비밀은 있다는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘So there are,’ cried the other, clapping him on both shoulders, and rolling him backwards and forwards.", "\"그렇고말고요.\" 상대가 그의 양 어깨를 탁 치며, 그를 앞뒤로 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["‘Haha!", "\"하하!"], ["you are right.", "당신 말이 옳소."], ["So there are!", "그렇고말고! 비밀이라니!"], ["Secrets! Holy Blue!", "거룩한 푸른 하늘이여!"], ["There are the devil’s own secrets in some families, Mr Flintwinch!’ With that, after clapping Mr Flintwinch on both shoulders several times, as if in a friendly and humorous way he were rallying him on a joke he had made, he threw up his arms, threw back his head, hooked his hands together behind it, and burst into a roar of laughter.", "어떤 집안에는 악마의 비밀이라 할 만한 것들이 있지요, 플린트윈치 씨!\" 그러면서 그는, 마치 자기가 한 농담을 두고 다정하고 익살스럽게 놀려 대기라도 하듯 플린트윈치 씨의 양 어깨를 여러 차례 탁탁 치고는, 두 팔을 치켜들고 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 양손을 그 뒤에서 깍지 끼고 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["It was in vain for Mr Flintwinch to try another screw at him.", "플린트윈치 씨가 그에게 또 한 번 몸을 비틀어 봐야 소용없었다."], ["He had his laugh out.", "그는 실컷 웃어 댔다."]], [["‘But, favour me with the candle a moment,’ he said, when he had done.", "\"하지만, 잠깐 촛불을 좀 빌려 주시지요.\" 웃음을 그치고 그가 말했다."], ["‘Let us have a look at the husband of the remarkable lady.", "\"그 비범한 부인의 남편을 한번 봅시다."], ["Hah!’ holding up the light at arm’s length.", "하!\" 그가 팔을 쭉 뻗어 불빛을 치켜들었다."], ["‘A decided expression of face here too, though not of the same character.", "\"여기도 얼굴에 단호한 표정이 있군요, 비록 같은 종류는 아닙니다만."], ["Looks as if he were saying, what is it--Do Not Forget--does he not, Mr Flintwinch?", "마치 이렇게 말하는 것 같지 않습니까, 그게 뭐였더라 — 잊지 마라 — 라고요, 플린트윈치 씨?"], ["By Heaven, sir, he does!’", "맹세코, 선생, 정말 그렇군요!\""]], [["As he returned the candle, he looked at him once more; and then, leisurely strolling out with him into the hall, declared it to be a charming old house indeed, and one which had so greatly pleased him that he would not have missed inspecting it for a hundred pounds.", "촛불을 돌려주며 그는 그를 다시 한번 바라보았다. 그러고는 느긋하게 그와 함께 현관으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나오면서, 참으로 매력적인 낡은 집이라고, 자기 마음에 어찌나 쏙 들었던지 백 파운드를 준다 해도 둘러보지 않을 수 없었을 거라고 단언했다."]], [["Throughout these singular freedoms on the part of Mr Blandois, which involved a general alteration in his demeanour, making it much coarser and rougher, much more violent and audacious than before, Mr Flintwinch, whose leathern face was not liable to many changes, preserved its immobility intact.", "블랑두아 씨 쪽의 이 기묘한 무람없음이 이어지는 내내 — 그것은 그의 태도 전반을 바꾸어 전보다 훨씬 거칠고 험악하게, 훨씬 난폭하고 대담하게 만들었는데 — 변화가 그리 많을 수 없는 가죽 같은 얼굴의 플린트윈치 씨는 그 부동(不動)함을 고스란히 지켰다."], ["Beyond now appearing perhaps, to have been left hanging a trifle too long before that friendly operation of cutting down, he outwardly maintained an equable composure.", "다만 그 다정한 끌어 내림의 작업 전에 살짝 너무 오래 매달려 있었던 것처럼 이제 보일 뿐, 겉으로는 한결같은 침착함을 유지했다."], ["They had brought their survey to a close in the little room at the side of the hall, and he stood there, eyeing Mr Blandois.", "그들은 현관 옆 작은 방에서 둘러보기를 마쳤고, 그는 거기 서서 블랑두아 씨를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["‘I am glad you are so well satisfied, sir,’ was his calm remark.", "\"그토록 만족하시니 기쁩니다, 선생.\" 그의 차분한 말이었다."], ["‘I didn’t expect it.", "\"기대치 못했던 일이군요."], ["You seem to be quite in good spirits.’", "무척 기분이 좋아 보이십니다.\""]], [["‘In admirable spirits,’ returned Blandois.", "\"감탄스러우리만치 좋지요.\" 블랑두아가 답했다."], ["‘Word of honour!", "\"맹세코!"], ["never more refreshed in spirits.", "이보다 더 기분이 상쾌했던 적이 없습니다."], ["Do you ever have presentiments, Mr Flintwinch?’", "당신은 예감이라는 걸 가져 보신 적이 있습니까, 플린트윈치 씨?\""]], [["‘I am not sure that I know what you mean by the term, sir,’ replied that gentleman.", "\"그 말로 무엇을 뜻하시는지 제가 안다고 장담하긴 어렵군요, 선생.\" 그 신사가 답했다."]], [["‘Say, in this case, Mr Flintwinch, undefined anticipations of pleasure to come.’", "\"이 경우라면, 플린트윈치 씨, 앞으로 다가올 기쁨에 대한 형체 없는 예상이라고 해 두지요.\""]], [["‘I can’t say I’m sensible of such a sensation at present,’ returned Mr Flintwinch with the utmost gravity.", "\"지금으로선 그런 느낌을 느끼고 있다고는 말할 수 없군요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 더없이 진지하게 답했다."], ["‘If I should find it coming on, I’ll mention it.’", "\"그게 다가오는 걸 느끼게 되면, 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["‘Now I,’ said Blandois, ‘I, my son, have a presentiment to-night that we shall be well acquainted.", "\"그런데 나는,\" 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["Do you find it coming on?’", "\"나는, 이 사람아, 오늘 밤 우리가 서로 잘 알게 되리라는 예감이 든단 말이오. 그게 다가오는 게 느껴지지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘N-no,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, deliberately inquiring of himself.", "\"아—아니요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 신중하게 자신에게 물어보며 답했다."], ["‘I can’t say I do.’", "\"느껴진다고는 말 못 하겠군요.\""]], [["‘I have a strong presentiment that we shall become intimately acquainted.--You have no feeling of that sort yet?’", "\"나는 우리가 아주 가까운 사이가 되리라는 강한 예감이 드는데. — 당신은 아직 그런 느낌이 없습니까?\""]], [["‘Not yet,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"아직은요.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr Blandois, taking him by both shoulders again, rolled him about a little in his former merry way, then drew his arm through his own, and invited him to come off and drink a bottle of wine like a dear deep old dog as he was.", "블랑두아 씨는 또다시 그의 양 어깨를 붙잡고 아까의 그 유쾌한 투로 그를 조금 흔들어 대더니, 자기 팔을 그의 팔에 꿰고는, 점잖고 속 깊은 늙은 친구답게 나가서 포도주나 한 병 마시자고 청했다."]], [["Without a moment’s indecision, Mr Flintwinch accepted the invitation, and they went out to the quarters where the traveller was lodged, through a heavy rain which had rattled on the windows, roofs, and pavements, ever since nightfall.", "플린트윈치 씨는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그 청을 받아들였고, 두 사람은 해 질 녘부터 줄곧 창과 지붕과 포도(鋪道)를 두드려 대던 거센 빗속을 뚫고, 그 나그네가 묵는 거처로 나갔다."], ["The thunder and lightning had long ago passed over, but the rain was furious.", "천둥과 번개는 오래전에 지나갔으나, 비는 사납게 퍼부었다."], ["On their arrival at Mr Blandois’ room, a bottle of port wine was ordered by that gallant gentleman; who (crushing every pretty thing he could collect, in the soft disposition of his dainty figure) coiled himself upon the window-seat, while Mr Flintwinch took a chair opposite to him, with the table between them.", "블랑두아 씨의 방에 이르자, 그 정중한 신사는 포트와인 한 병을 시켰다. 그러고는 (제 맵시 있는 몸뚱이의 부드러운 처분으로, 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 앙증맞은 물건을 죄다 짓이기며) 창가 자리에 몸을 둘둘 말고 앉았고, 플린트윈치 씨는 그 맞은편에 탁자를 사이에 두고 의자를 차지했다."], ["Mr Blandois proposed having the largest glasses in the house, to which Mr Flintwinch assented.", "블랑두아 씨가 그 집에서 가장 큰 잔을 가져오자고 제안하자 플린트윈치 씨도 동의했다."], ["The bumpers filled, Mr Blandois, with a roystering gaiety, clinked the top of his glass against the bottom of Mr Flintwinch’s, and the bottom of his glass against the top of Mr Flintwinch’s, and drank to the intimate acquaintance he foresaw.", "잔이 가득 채워지자, 블랑두아 씨는 떠들썩한 흥겨움으로 제 잔의 윗부분을 플린트윈치 씨 잔의 밑부분에, 또 제 잔의 밑부분을 플린트윈치 씨 잔의 윗부분에 쨍그랑 부딪치고는, 자신이 예견하는 가까운 사이를 위해 건배했다."], ["Mr Flintwinch gravely pledged him, and drank all the wine he could get, and said nothing.", "플린트윈치 씨는 진지하게 화답하여, 손에 넣을 수 있는 포도주는 모조리 마셨으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["As often as Mr Blandois clinked glasses (which was at every replenishment), Mr Flintwinch stolidly did his part of the clinking, and would have stolidly done his companion’s part of the wine as well as his own: being, except in the article of palate, a mere cask.", "블랑두아 씨가 잔을 부딪칠 때마다 (그것은 채워질 때마다였다), 플린트윈치 씨는 무덤덤하게 제 몫의 부딪침을 해냈고, 입맛이라는 점만 빼면 그저 한 통의 술통이나 다름없어서, 제 몫의 포도주뿐 아니라 동행의 몫까지도 무덤덤하게 다 마셔 치울 기세였다."]], [["In short, Mr Blandois found that to pour port wine into the reticent Flintwinch was, not to open him but to shut him up.", "요컨대 블랑두아 씨는, 과묵한 플린트윈치에게 포트와인을 부어 넣는 일이 그를 여는 게 아니라 도리어 닫아 버리는 일임을 알게 되었다."], ["Moreover, he had the appearance of a perfect ability to go on all night; or, if occasion were, all next day and all next night; whereas Mr Blandois soon grew indistinctly conscious of swaggering too fiercely and boastfully.", "게다가 그는 밤새도록이든, 필요하다면 다음 날 내내, 또 다음 날 밤 내내든 계속할 수 있는 완벽한 능력을 갖춘 듯한 기색이었다. 반면 블랑두아 씨는 이내 자신이 지나치게 거세고 허풍스럽게 으스대고 있음을 어렴풋이 깨닫게 되었다."], ["He therefore terminated the entertainment at the end of the third bottle.", "그리하여 그는 세 번째 병이 끝나자 그 자리를 마무리 지었다."]], [["‘You will draw upon us to-morrow, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch, with a business-like face at parting.", "\"내일 우리 앞으로 어음을 끊으시지요, 선생.\" 헤어지면서 플린트윈치 씨가 사무적인 얼굴로 말했다."]], [["‘My Cabbage,’ returned the other, taking him by the collar with both hands, ‘I’ll draw upon you; have no fear. Adieu, my Flintwinch. Receive at parting;’ here he gave him a southern embrace, and kissed him soundly on both cheeks; ‘the word of a gentleman! By a thousand Thunders, you shall see me again!’", "\"내 양배추 같은 양반,\" 상대가 두 손으로 그의 멱살을 잡으며 답했다. \"당신 앞으로 끊고말고. 걱정 마시오. 안녕히, 나의 플린트윈치. 헤어지며 받아 두시오.\" 여기서 그는 남국식으로 그를 끌어안고 양 볼에 쩍쩍 입을 맞추었다. \"신사의 약속을! 천 번의 천둥을 걸고, 당신은 나를 다시 보게 될 거요!\""]], [["He did not present himself next day, though the letter of advice came duly to hand.", "그는 통지 편지는 제때 도착했음에도 이튿날에는 모습을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Inquiring after him at night, Mr Flintwinch found, with surprise, that he had paid his bill and gone back to the Continent by way of Calais.", "밤에 그의 소식을 수소문하던 플린트윈치 씨는, 그가 셈을 치르고 칼레를 거쳐 대륙으로 돌아가 버렸음을 놀라움 속에 알게 되었다."], ["Nevertheless, Jeremiah scraped out of his cogitating face a lively conviction that Mr Blandois would keep his word on this occasion, and would be seen again.", "그럼에도 제러마이아는 곰곰이 따져 보는 제 얼굴에서, 블랑두아 씨가 이번만큼은 약속을 지켜 다시 나타나리라는 생생한 확신을 긁어내었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 31. Spirit", "# CHAPTER 31. Spirit"]], [["Anybody may pass, any day, in the thronged thoroughfares of the metropolis, some meagre, wrinkled, yellow old man (who might be supposed to have dropped from the stars, if there were any star in the Heavens dull enough to be suspected of casting off so feeble a spark), creeping along with a scared air, as though bewildered and a little frightened by the noise and bustle.", "누구든 어느 날엔가, 사람들로 붐비는 대도시의 큰길에서, 어떤 비쩍 마르고 주름지고 누런 늙은이를 스쳐 지나갈 수 있다(별들에서 떨어져 내린 것이라고 짐작될 만한 자인데, 하늘에 그토록 미약한 불티를 흘려보냈으리라 의심받을 만큼 흐릿한 별이 있다면 말이다). 그 늙은이는 겁먹은 기색으로, 마치 그 소음과 부산함에 어리둥절하고 조금은 놀란 듯 엉금엉금 길을 따라간다."], ["This old man is always a little old man.", "이 늙은이는 언제나 자그마한 늙은이다."], ["If he were ever a big old man, he has shrunk into a little old man; if he were always a little old man, he has dwindled into a less old man.", "한때 큰 늙은이였다 해도 자그마한 늙은이로 오그라들었고, 늘 자그마한 늙은이였다면 더더욱 작은 늙은이로 쪼그라들었다."], ["His coat is a colour, and cut, that never was the mode anywhere, at any period.", "그의 외투는 그 빛깔도 마름질도, 어느 곳 어느 시절에도 유행이었던 적이 없는 것이다."], ["Clearly, it was not made for him, or for any individual mortal.", "분명 그를 위해 지은 것도, 어떤 개별의 인간을 위해 지은 것도 아니다."], ["Some wholesale contractor measured Fate for five thousand coats of such quality, and Fate has lent this old coat to this old man, as one of a long unfinished line of many old men.", "어떤 도매업자가 그런 품질의 외투 오천 벌을 두고 운명에게 치수를 재었고, 운명은 이 낡은 외투를, 끝나지 않을 긴 행렬을 이룬 수많은 늙은이들 가운데 하나에게 빌려주듯 이 늙은이에게 빌려준 것이다."], ["It has always large dull metal buttons, similar to no other buttons.", "거기엔 언제나, 다른 어떤 단추와도 닮지 않은 크고 칙칙한 쇠단추가 달려 있다."], ["This old man wears a hat, a thumbed and napless and yet an obdurate hat, which has never adapted itself to the shape of his poor head.", "이 늙은이는 모자를 쓰고 있는데, 손때가 묻고 보풀이 다 닳았으면서도 고집스러운 모자라, 그의 가엾은 머리 모양에 한 번도 맞춰 준 적이 없다."], ["His coarse shirt and his coarse neckcloth have no more individuality than his coat and hat; they have the same character of not being his--of not being anybody’s.", "그의 거친 셔츠와 거친 목수건도 그의 외투나 모자보다 더 개성이 없다. 그것들은 모두 그의 것이 아니라는—그 누구의 것도 아니라는 똑같은 성격을 지녔다."], ["Yet this old man wears these clothes with a certain unaccustomed air of being dressed and elaborated for the public ways; as though he passed the greater part of his time in a nightcap and gown.", "그런데도 이 늙은이는 이 옷들을, 마치 평소엔 잠자리 모자와 가운 차림으로 대부분의 시간을 보내다가 큰길에 나서느라 차려입고 꾸민 양, 어딘가 익숙지 않은 분위기로 걸치고 있다."], ["And so, like the country mouse in the second year of a famine, come to see the town mouse, and timidly threading his way to the town-mouse’s lodging through a city of cats, this old man passes in the streets.", "그리하여, 흉년 두 해째에 시골 쥐가 도시 쥐를 찾아와 고양이들의 도시를 가로질러 도시 쥐의 거처로 겁먹은 채 길을 더듬어 가듯, 이 늙은이는 거리를 지나간다."]], [["Sometimes, on holidays towards evening, he will be seen to walk with a slightly increased infirmity, and his old eyes will glimmer with a moist and marshy light.", "가끔 휴일 저녁 무렵이면, 그가 약간 더 비척거리며 걷는 모습을 볼 수 있고, 그의 늙은 두 눈은 축축하고 늪 같은 빛으로 어슴푸레 빛날 것이다."], ["Then the little old man is drunk.", "그럴 때면 그 자그마한 늙은이는 취해 있다."], ["A very small measure will overset him; he may be bowled off his unsteady legs with a half-pint pot.", "아주 적은 양으로도 그는 쓰러진다. 반 파인트짜리 잔 하나면 그 불안한 다리가 휘청 무너질 수 있다."], ["Some pitying acquaintance--chance acquaintance very often--has warmed up his weakness with a treat of beer, and the consequence will be the lapse of a longer time than usual before he shall pass again.", "어떤 가엾이 여기는 지인이—흔히는 우연히 만난 지인이—맥주 한턱으로 그의 허약함을 데워 준 것이고, 그 결과 그가 다시 지나가기까지 평소보다 더 긴 시간이 걸리게 된다."], ["For the little old man is going home to the Workhouse; and on his good behaviour they do not let him out often (though methinks they might, considering the few years he has before him to go out in, under the sun); and on his bad behaviour they shut him up closer than ever in a grove of two score and nineteen more old men, every one of whom smells of all the others.", "왜냐하면 그 자그마한 늙은이는 구빈원으로 돌아가는 길이고, 행실이 좋을 때면 그들은 그를 자주 내보내 주지 않으며(내 생각엔, 햇빛 아래 나가 볼 날이 그에게 얼마 남지 않았음을 헤아린다면 좀 더 내보내 줄 법도 한데), 행실이 나쁠 때면 마흔아홉 명의 다른 늙은이들이 모인 숲속에 그 어느 때보다 단단히 가두어 버리는데, 그 숲의 늙은이들은 저마다 다른 모두의 냄새를 풍긴다."]], [["Mrs Plornish’s father,--a poor little reedy piping old gentleman, like a worn-out bird; who had been in what he called the music-binding business, and met with great misfortunes, and who had seldom been able to make his way, or to see it or to pay it, or to do anything at all with it but find it no thoroughfare,--had retired of his own accord to the Workhouse which was appointed by law to be the Good Samaritan of his district (without the twopence, which was bad political economy), on the settlement of that execution which had carried Mr Plornish to the Marshalsea College.", "플로니시 부인의 아버지는—닳고 닳은 새처럼, 가냘프고 피리 소리 같은 목소리를 내는 가엾은 늙은 신사로, 그가 음악 제본업이라 부르던 일에 종사하다가 큰 불운을 겪었으며, 그 일로 좀처럼 형편을 펼 줄도, 길을 볼 줄도, 갚을 줄도 몰랐고, 그저 막다른 길임을 깨닫는 것 외엔 그 일로 아무것도 할 줄 몰랐던 사람이었다—플로니시 씨를 마셜시 학교로 데려간 그 차압이 매듭지어지자, 제 발로 구빈원으로 물러났다. 그곳은 법에 따라 그가 사는 구역의 선한 사마리아인 노릇을 하도록 정해진 곳이었다(다만 그 두 푼은 없었으니, 그것은 나쁜 정치경제학이었던 것이다)."], ["Previous to his son-in-law’s difficulties coming to that head, Old Nandy (he was always so called in his legal Retreat, but he was Old Mr Nandy among the Bleeding Hearts) had sat in a corner of the Plornish fireside, and taken his bite and sup out of the Plornish cupboard.", "사위의 곤경이 그 지경에 이르기 전, 노인 낸디는(법적인 은거처에서는 늘 그렇게 불렸으나, 블리딩 하트 사람들 사이에서는 노인 낸디 씨였다) 플로니시 집안 화롯가 한구석에 앉아 플로니시 집안 찬장에서 한 입 베어 먹고 한 모금 마시곤 했다."], ["He still hoped to resume that domestic position when Fortune should smile upon his son-in-law; in the meantime, while she preserved an immovable countenance, he was, and resolved to remain, one of these little old men in a grove of little old men with a community of flavour.", "그는 운수가 사위에게 미소 지을 때 그 가정 내 자리를 다시 차지하길 여전히 바라고 있었다. 그러는 동안, 운수가 꼼짝 않는 낯을 짓고 있는 한, 그는 한데 어우러진 풍미를 풍기는 자그마한 늙은이들의 숲속에 든 그런 자그마한 늙은이 가운데 하나였으며, 또 그대로 남아 있기로 마음먹고 있었다."]], [["But no poverty in him, and no coat on him that never was the mode, and no Old Men’s Ward for his dwelling-place, could quench his daughter’s admiration.", "그러나 그에게 깃든 어떤 가난도, 어느 곳 어느 시절에도 유행이었던 적 없는 그의 외투도, 그가 거처로 삼은 그 노인 병동도, 딸의 흠모를 꺼뜨릴 수는 없었다. 플로니시 부인은 아버지의 재능을, 설령 그 재능이 그를 대법관으로 만들어 주었다 해도 그보다 더할 수 없을 만큼 자랑스러워했다."], ["Mrs Plornish was as proud of her father’s talents as she could possibly have been if they had made him Lord Chancellor.", "그녀는 아버지의 태도가 지닌 다정함과 품위를, 설령 그가 시종장이었다 해도 그보다 더 굳게 믿을 수 없을 만큼 굳게 믿었다."], ["She had as firm a belief in the sweetness and propriety of his manners as she could possibly have had if he had been Lord Chamberlain.", "그 가엾은 자그마한 늙은이는, 클로에며 필리스며, 비너스의 아들에게 상처 입은 스트레폰을 노래한, 오래전에 유행이 지난 빛바래고 김빠진 짧은 노래 몇 곡을 알고 있었다."], ["The poor little old man knew some pale and vapid little songs, long out of date, about Chloe, and Phyllis, and Strephon being wounded by the son of Venus; and for Mrs Plornish there was no such music at the Opera as the small internal flutterings and chirpings wherein he would discharge himself of these ditties, like a weak, little, broken barrel-organ, ground by a baby.", "그리고 플로니시 부인에게는, 아기가 돌리는 약하고 작고 망가진 손풍금처럼 그가 그 가락들을 작게 속에서 떨고 지저귀며 풀어낼 때의 그 소리만큼 오페라에서도 들을 수 없는 음악은 없었다. '나들이 가는 날'이면—다듬어 친 늙은이들의 그 단조로운 풍경 속에 박힌 그 빛 조각들이면—그가 고기를 먹어 기운이 나고 반 페니어치 흑맥주를 가득 들이켰을 때, \"노래 한 곡 해 주세요, 아버지\""], ["On his ‘days out,’ those flecks of light in his flat vista of pollard old men,’ it was at once Mrs Plornish’s delight and sorrow, when he was strong with meat, and had taken his full halfpenny-worth of porter, to say, ‘Sing us a song, Father.’ Then he would give them Chloe, and if he were in pretty good spirits, Phyllis also--Strephon he had hardly been up to since he went into retirement--and then would Mrs Plornish declare she did believe there never was such a singer as Father, and wipe her eyes.", "하고 말하는 것이 플로니시 부인의 기쁨이자 슬픔이었다. 그러면 그는 그들에게 클로에를 들려주었고, 기분이 제법 좋을 때면 필리스도 들려주었으며—스트레폰은 은거에 든 뒤로 좀처럼 부를 형편이 못 되었다—그러면 플로니시 부인은 아버지만 한 가수는 정말로 세상에 없었다고 단언하며 눈물을 닦았다."]], [["If he had come from Court on these occasions, nay, if he had been the noble Refrigerator come home triumphantly from a foreign court to be presented and promoted on his last tremendous failure, Mrs Plornish could not have handed him with greater elevation about Bleeding Heart Yard.", "이런 자리에서 그가 궁정에서 돌아왔다 한들, 아니, 그가 그 고귀한 냉장고 각하라서, 마지막 끔찍한 실패 끝에 외국 궁정에서 의기양양하게 돌아와 알현하고 승진하게 되었다 한들, 플로니시 부인이 그를 블리딩 하트 야드 일대로 끌고 다니며 더 우쭐대게 모실 수는 없었을 것이다. \"여기 우리 아버지예요.\""], ["‘Here’s Father,’ she would say, presenting him to a neighbour.", "이웃에게 그를 소개하며 그녀는 말하곤 했다. \"아버지가 이제 곧 우리 집에 아주 돌아오신답니다."], ["‘Father will soon be home with us for good, now.", "아버지 안색이 좋아 보이지 않아요?"], ["Ain’t Father looking well?", "아버지는 그 어느 때보다 노래를 더 달콤하게 부르신답니다."], ["Father’s a sweeter singer than ever; you’d never have forgotten it, if you’d aheard him just now.’ As to Mr Plornish, he had married these articles of belief in marrying Mr Nandy’s daughter, and only wondered how it was that so gifted an old gentleman had not made a fortune.", "방금 한 곡 들으셨다면 잊지 못하셨을 거예요.\" 플로니시 씨로 말하자면, 낸디 씨의 딸과 결혼하면서 이런 신조들도 함께 떠맡은 셈이라, 그토록 재능 있는 늙은 신사가 어찌하여 큰돈을 벌지 못했는지 그저 의아해할 따름이었다."], ["This he attributed, after much reflection, to his musical genius not having been scientifically developed in his youth.", "그는 오래 곱씹은 끝에, 그것을 그의 음악적 천재가 젊은 시절에 학문적으로 계발되지 못한 탓으로 돌렸다. \"그러니까 말이야,\""], ["‘For why,’ argued Mr Plornish, ‘why go a-binding music when you’ve got it in yourself?", "플로니시 씨는 논했다. \"제 안에 음악을 갖고 있는데 뭐 하러 음악을 제본하러 다니냐는 거지."], ["That’s where it is, I consider.’", "내가 보기엔 거기에 문제가 있어.\""]], [["Old Nandy had a patron: one patron.", "노인 낸디에게는 후원자가 있었다."], ["He had a patron who in a certain sumptuous way--an apologetic way, as if he constantly took an admiring audience to witness that he really could not help being more free with this old fellow than they might have expected, on account of his simplicity and poverty--was mightily good to him.", "후원자 한 사람. 그에게는 어떤 호화로운 방식으로—변명하는 듯한 방식으로, 마치 자신이 이 늙은이의 단순함과 가난을 헤아려, 사람들이 기대했을 법한 것보다 더 스스럼없이 대할 수밖에 없음을 흠모하는 청중을 끊임없이 증인으로 세우려는 양—그에게 무척 잘해 주는 후원자가 있었다."], ["Old Nandy had been several times to the Marshalsea College, communicating with his son-in-law during his short durance there; and had happily acquired to himself, and had by degrees and in course of time much improved, the patronage of the Father of that national institution.", "노인 낸디는 사위가 마셜시 학교에 짧게 갇혀 있는 동안 그와 연락하느라 여러 차례 그곳에 드나들었고, 그 국가적 기관의 아버지의 후원을 다행히도 손에 넣었으며, 차차 시간이 흐르면서 그 후원을 크게 두텁게 만들어 왔다."]], [["Mr Dorrit was in the habit of receiving this old man as if the old man held of him in vassalage under some feudal tenure.", "도릿 씨는 이 늙은이를, 마치 늙은이가 어떤 봉건적 차지 관계 아래 자신에게 신하로 속해 있기라도 한 것처럼 맞이하는 버릇이 있었다."], ["He made little treats and teas for him, as if he came in with his homage from some outlying district where the tenantry were in a primitive state.", "그는 마치 늙은이가 소작인들이 원시적 상태에 있는 어느 변두리 지방에서 충성의 표시를 들고 찾아오기라도 한 양 그를 위해 소소한 대접과 차를 베풀었다."], ["It seemed as if there were moments when he could by no means have sworn but that the old man was an ancient retainer of his, who had been meritoriously faithful.", "어떤 순간에는, 이 늙은이가 공로를 세워 충실했던 자신의 오랜 가신이 아니라고는 결코 맹세할 수 없을 것 같은 때가 있는 듯했다."], ["When he mentioned him, he spoke of him casually as his old pensioner.", "그는 늙은이를 언급할 때 무심히 자신의 오랜 연금 수급자라 일컬었다."], ["He had a wonderful satisfaction in seeing him, and in commenting on his decayed condition after he was gone.", "그는 그를 보는 데에서, 그리고 그가 떠난 뒤 그의 쇠락한 처지를 두고 논평하는 데에서 놀라운 만족을 얻었다."], ["It appeared to him amazing that he could hold up his head at all, poor creature.", "저 가엾은 자가 그래도 고개를 들고 다닐 수 있다는 것이 그에게는 신기하게만 보였다."], ["‘In the Workhouse, sir, the Union; no privacy, no visitors, no station, no respect, no speciality.", "\"구빈원이라니, 선생, 연합 구빈원 말이오. 사생활도 없고, 방문객도 없고, 신분도 없고, 존중도 없고, 특별함이라곤 없지."], ["Most deplorable!’", "참으로 통탄할 일이오!\""]], [["It was Old Nandy’s birthday, and they let him out.", "노인 낸디의 생일이라, 그들은 그를 내보내 주었다."], ["He said nothing about its being his birthday, or they might have kept him in; for such old men should not be born.", "그는 생일이라는 말은 한마디도 하지 않았는데, 그랬다간 그들이 그를 가두어 두었을지도 모르기 때문이다."], ["He passed along the streets as usual to Bleeding Heart Yard, and had his dinner with his daughter and son-in-law, and gave them Phyllis.", "그런 늙은이들은 태어나지 말았어야 했으니까. 그는 평소처럼 거리를 따라 블리딩 하트 야드로 가서 딸과 사위와 함께 저녁을 먹고, 그들에게 필리스를 들려주었다."], ["He had hardly concluded, when Little Dorrit looked in to see how they all were.", "그가 노래를 막 마쳤을 때, 리틀 도릿이 다들 어찌 지내는지 보러 들렀다."]], [["‘Miss Dorrit,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘here’s Father!", "\"도릿 양.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다."], ["Ain’t he looking nice?", "\"여기 우리 아버지예요! 멋져 보이시지 않아요?"], ["And such voice he’s in!’", "게다가 목소리가 어찌나 좋으신지!\""]], [["Little Dorrit gave him her hand, and smilingly said she had not seen him this long time.", "리틀 도릿이 그에게 손을 내밀며, 그를 오랫동안 못 뵈었다고 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["‘No, they’re rather hard on poor Father,’ said Mrs Plornish with a lengthening face, ‘and don’t let him have half as much change and fresh air as would benefit him.", "\"네, 그들이 가엾은 아버지에게 좀 야박하게 굴어요.\" 플로니시 부인이 얼굴을 길게 늘이며 말했다."], ["But he’ll soon be home for good, now.", "\"아버지께 도움이 될 만큼 바람 쐬고 기분 전환할 시간을 절반도 주질 않는답니다. 하지만 이제 곧 아주 집에 돌아오실 거예요."], ["Won’t you, Father?’", "그렇죠, 아버지?\""]], [["‘Yes, my dear, I hope so.", "\"그래, 얘야, 그러길 바란다."], ["In good time, please God.’", "하느님 뜻이라면, 때가 되면 그리되겠지.\""]], [["Here Mr Plornish delivered himself of an oration which he invariably made, word for word the same, on all such opportunities.", "여기서 플로니시 씨는 그런 기회마다 한 자도 틀림없이 똑같이 늘어놓는 연설을 토해 냈다."], ["It was couched in the following terms:", "그것은 다음과 같은 말로 이루어졌다."]], [["‘John Edward Nandy.", "\"존 에드워드 낸디."], ["Sir.", "어르신."], ["While there’s a ounce of wittles or drink of any sort in this present roof, you’re fully welcome to your share on it.", "지금 이 지붕 아래 먹을 것이든 마실 것이든 무엇이든 한 온스라도 있는 한, 어르신은 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 온전히 있습니다."], ["While there’s a handful of fire or a mouthful of bed in this present roof, you’re fully welcome to your share on it.", "지금 이 지붕 아래 불씨 한 줌이든 잠자리 한 입이든 있는 한, 어르신은 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 온전히 있습니다."], ["If so be as there should be nothing in this present roof, you should be as welcome to your share on it as if it was something, much or little.", "만에 하나 지금 이 지붕 아래 아무것도 없게 된다 해도, 어르신은 그것이 많든 적든 무언가가 있을 때와 똑같이 그 몫을 받으실 자격이 있습니다."], ["And this is what I mean and so I don’t deceive you, and consequently which is to stand out is to entreat of you, and therefore why not do it?’", "이것이 제 뜻이고 그러니 거짓으로 드리는 말씀이 아닙니다, 따라서 결국 남는 결론은 어르신께 간청드리는 것이니, 그러니 어찌 그러지 않으시겠습니까?\""]], [["To this lucid address, which Mr Plornish always delivered as if he had composed it (as no doubt he had) with enormous labour, Mrs Plornish’s father pipingly replied:", "이 명료한 연설에—플로니시 씨는 늘 자신이 그것을 엄청난 노고를 들여 지어낸 양(틀림없이 그렇긴 했다) 늘어놓았다—플로니시 부인의 아버지는 피리 같은 소리로 대답했다."]], [["‘I thank you kindly, Thomas, and I know your intentions well, which is the same I thank you kindly for.", "\"고맙네, 토머스, 자네 뜻은 잘 아네, 그러니 또한 고맙다는 말일세. 하지만 안 되네, 토머스."], ["But no, Thomas. Until such times as it’s not to take it out of your children’s mouths, which take it is, and call it by what name you will it do remain and equally deprive, though may they come, and too soon they can not come, no Thomas, no!’", "그것이 자네 아이들 입에서 빼앗는 일이 아니게 될 때까지는—빼앗는 일이지, 무어라 이름 붙이든 그건 그대로 남아 똑같이 앗아 가는 일이야, 비록 그날이 오긴 하겠지만, 아무리 빨리 와도 모자랄 그날이 오긴 하겠지만, 안 되네, 토머스, 안 돼!\""]], [["Mrs Plornish, who had been turning her face a little away with a corner of her apron in her hand, brought herself back to the conversation again by telling Miss Dorrit that Father was going over the water to pay his respects, unless she knew of any reason why it might not be agreeable.", "앞치마 한 귀퉁이를 손에 쥐고 얼굴을 조금 돌리고 있던 플로니시 부인이, 도릿 양에게 아버지가 강 건너로 문안을 드리러 가실 참인데 혹시 그게 좋지 않을 무슨 이유라도 아시느냐고 물으며 다시 대화로 돌아왔다."]], [["Her answer was, ‘I am going straight home, and if he will come with me I shall be so glad to take care of him--so glad,’ said Little Dorrit, always thoughtful of the feelings of the weak, ‘of his company.’", "그녀의 대답은 이러했다. \"저는 곧장 집으로 가는 길이에요. 그러니 같이 가 주신다면 제가 모시고 가게 되어 정말 기쁘겠어요—그분과 함께 가는 게 정말 기뻐요.\" 늘 약한 이들의 마음을 헤아리는 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘There, Father!’ cried Mrs Plornish.", "\"보세요, 아버지!\" 플로니시 부인이 외쳤다."], ["‘Ain’t you a gay young man to be going for a walk along with Miss Dorrit!", "\"도릿 양과 함께 산책을 나가시다니, 아버지도 참 멋쟁이 젊은이시네요!"], ["Let me tie your neck-handkerchief into a regular good bow, for you’re a regular beau yourself, Father, if ever there was one.’", "목수건을 제대로 멋진 매듭으로 매 드릴게요, 정말 멋쟁이라는 게 있다면 바로 아버지시니까요.\""]], [["With this filial joke his daughter smartened him up, and gave him a loving hug, and stood at the door with her weak child in her arms, and her strong child tumbling down the steps, looking after her little old father as he toddled away with his arm under Little Dorrit’s.", "이런 효성 어린 농담과 함께 딸은 그를 말끔하게 단장해 주고는, 사랑스럽게 꼭 끌어안고, 약한 아이를 품에 안고 튼튼한 아이는 층계를 굴러 내려가는 가운데 문간에 서서, 자그마한 늙은 아버지가 리틀 도릿의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고 아장아장 걸어가는 뒷모습을 바라보았다."]], [["They walked at a slow pace, and Little Dorrit took him by the Iron Bridge and sat him down there for a rest, and they looked over at the water and talked about the shipping, and the old man mentioned what he would do if he had a ship full of gold coming home to him (his plan was to take a noble lodging for the Plornishes and himself at a Tea Gardens, and live there all the rest of their lives, attended on by the waiter), and it was a special birthday of the old man.", "그들은 천천히 걸었고, 리틀 도릿은 그를 아이언 브리지로 데려가 거기에 앉혀 쉬게 했으며, 두 사람은 강물을 내려다보며 배들에 관해 이야기를 나누었다. 늙은이는 만약 자기에게 금화를 가득 실은 배가 돌아온다면 무엇을 하겠다고 말했는데(그의 계획은 어느 다과 정원에 플로니시 가족과 자신을 위해 근사한 거처를 얻어, 시중드는 종업원의 시중을 받으며 남은 평생을 그곳에서 사는 것이었다), 그날은 늙은이의 특별한 생일이었다."], ["They were within five minutes of their destination, when, at the corner of her own street, they came upon Fanny in her new bonnet bound for the same port.", "목적지까지 오 분 거리도 안 남았을 때, 자기 집 거리 모퉁이에서 두 사람은 새 보닛을 쓰고 같은 항구로 향하던 패니와 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Why, good gracious me, Amy!’ cried that young lady starting.", "\"아니, 어쩜 이럴 수가, 에이미!\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다."], ["‘You never mean it!’", "\"설마 그럴 리가!\""]], [["‘Mean what, Fanny dear?’", "\"무얼 말이야, 패니 언니?\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"흥! 너에 대해선 어지간한 건 다 믿을 수 있었지만,\""], ["I could have believed a great deal of you,’ returned the young lady with burning indignation, ‘but I don’t think even I could have believed this, of even you!’", "그 젊은 아가씨가 활활 타오르는 분노로 응수했다. \"이건, 아무리 너라도, 나조차 믿을 수 없었을 거야!\""]], [["‘Fanny!’ cried Little Dorrit, wounded and astonished.", "\"패니 언니!\" 리틀 도릿이 상처받고 놀라 외쳤다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아유!"], ["Don’t Fanny me, you mean little thing, don’t!", "패니 언니 소리 하지 마, 이 못된 것아, 하지 마!"], ["The idea of coming along the open streets, in the broad light of day, with a Pauper!’ (firing off the last word as if it were a ball from an air-gun).", "환한 대낮에, 훤히 트인 거리에서, 빈민하고 같이 걸어오다니!\"(마지막 단어를 마치 공기총에서 쏘아 보낸 총알인 양 발사했다.)\n\"아, 패니 언니!\""]], [["‘O Fanny!’\n‘I tell you not to Fanny me, for I’ll not submit to it!", "\"패니 언니 소리 하지 말라니까, 그런 건 참을 수 없으니까!"], ["I never knew such a thing.", "이런 일은 본 적이 없어."], ["The way in which you are resolved and determined to disgrace us on all occasions, is really infamous.", "기회만 있으면 우리를 망신시키겠다고 작정하고 마음을 다잡는 그 꼴이라니, 정말 파렴치하기 짝이 없어."], ["You bad little thing!’", "이 못된 것!\""]], [["‘Does it disgrace anybody,’ said Little Dorrit, very gently, ‘to take care of this poor old man?’", "\"이 가엾은 늙은 분을 돌봐 드리는 게,\" 리틀 도릿이 아주 부드럽게 말했다. \"누구한테 망신이 되나요?\""]], [["‘Yes, miss,’ returned her sister, ‘and you ought to know it does.", "\"그래, 망신이야, 아가씨.\""], ["And you do know it does, and you do it because you know it does.", "언니가 응수했다. \"너도 그게 망신인 줄 알아야 마땅하고."], ["The principal pleasure of your life is to remind your family of their misfortunes.", "너도 그게 망신인 줄 알고 있어, 그게 망신인 줄 아니까 하는 거지."], ["And the next great pleasure of your existence is to keep low company.", "네 인생의 으뜸가는 즐거움은 가족에게 그 불운을 일깨워 주는 거잖아."], ["But, however, if you have no sense of decency, I have.", "그리고 그다음으로 큰 낙은 천한 사람들과 어울리는 거고."], ["You’ll please to allow me to go on the other side of the way, unmolested.’", "하지만 어쨌든, 네게 체면이라는 게 없다 해도, 내겐 있어. 나는 길 건너편으로 가게 좀 내버려 둬, 방해받지 않고.\""]], [["With this, she bounced across to the opposite pavement.", "이렇게 말하며 그녀는 펄쩍 뛰어 맞은편 보도로 건너갔다."], ["The old disgrace, who had been deferentially bowing a pace or two off (for Little Dorrit had let his arm go in her wonder, when Fanny began), and who had been hustled and cursed by impatient passengers for stopping the way, rejoined his companion, rather giddy, and said, ‘I hope nothing’s wrong with your honoured father, Miss?", "그 늙은 망신거리는—패니가 입을 열었을 때 리틀 도릿이 놀라 그의 팔을 놓아 버려서—한두 걸음 떨어져 공손히 절을 하고 있었고, 길을 막는다고 성급한 행인들에게 떠밀리고 욕을 먹던 차에, 좀 어지러운 채로 자기 길동무에게 다시 다가와 말했다. \"댁의 존경하는 아버님께 무슨 일이라도 있으신지요, 아가씨?"], ["I hope there’s nothing the matter in the honoured family?’", "그 존경하는 가족에게 무슨 탈이라도 난 건 아니길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘No, no,’ returned Little Dorrit.", "\"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 응했다."], ["‘No, thank you.", "\"아니에요, 고마워요."], ["Give me your arm again, Mr Nandy.", "다시 제 팔을 잡으세요, 낸디 씨."], ["We shall soon be there now.’", "이제 곧 도착할 거예요.\""]], [["So she talked to him as she had talked before, and they came to the Lodge and found Mr Chivery on the lock, and went in.", "그래서 그녀는 전처럼 그에게 이야기를 건넸고, 두 사람은 수위실에 이르러 자물쇠 옆에 있는 치버리 씨를 만나 안으로 들어갔다."], ["Now, it happened that the Father of the Marshalsea was sauntering towards the Lodge at the moment when they were coming out of it, entering the prison arm in arm.", "그런데 마침, 마셜시의 아버지가 수위실 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오던 그 순간, 두 사람은 팔짱을 끼고 감옥 안으로 들어서며 그곳에서 나오는 길이었다."], ["As the spectacle of their approach met his view, he displayed the utmost agitation and despondency of mind; and--altogether regardless of Old Nandy, who, making his reverence, stood with his hat in his hand, as he always did in that gracious presence--turned about, and hurried in at his own doorway and up the staircase.", "그들이 다가오는 광경이 눈에 들어오자, 그는 더할 수 없는 마음의 동요와 낙담을 드러냈으며—모자를 손에 들고 절을 하며 서 있던 노인 낸디는 그 인자한 분 앞에서 늘 그러듯이 서 있었으나 그를 전혀 아랑곳하지 않고—몸을 돌려 제 집 문간으로 급히 들어가 층계를 올라가 버렸다."]], [["Leaving the old unfortunate, whom in an evil hour she had taken under her protection, with a hurried promise to return to him directly, Little Dorrit hastened after her father, and, on the staircase, found Fanny following her, and flouncing up with offended dignity.", "불길한 시각에 자기 보호 아래 거두었던 그 늙은 불행한 이를, 곧 돌아오겠다는 다급한 약속과 함께 남겨 둔 채, 리틀 도릿은 아버지를 뒤쫓아 서둘러 갔고, 층계에서 패니가 모욕당한 위엄으로 발끈하며 뒤따라 올라오는 것을 보았다."], ["The three came into the room almost together; and the Father sat down in his chair, buried his face in his hands, and uttered a groan.", "세 사람은 거의 동시에 방으로 들어섰다. 아버지는 의자에 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 신음을 토했다."]], [["‘Of course,’ said Fanny.", "\"그러게 말이에요.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘Very proper.", "\"지당하시죠."], ["Poor, afflicted Pa!", "가엾고 괴로우신 아버지!"], ["Now, I hope you believe me, Miss?’", "자, 이제 제 말을 믿으시겠죠, 아가씨?\""]], [["‘What is it, father?’ cried Little Dorrit, bending over him.", "\"무슨 일이에요, 아버지?\" 리틀 도릿이 그에게 몸을 숙이며 외쳤다."], ["‘Have I made you unhappy, father?", "\"제가 아버지를 슬프게 해 드렸나요, 아버지?"], ["Not I, I hope!’", "설마 제가 그랬을 리가요!\""]], [["‘You hope, indeed!", "\"그래, 설마라니! 말 다 했다!"], ["I dare say!", "아유, 너—\""], ["Oh, you’--Fanny paused for a sufficiently strong expression--‘you Common-minded little Amy!", "패니는 충분히 강한 표현을 찾느라 말을 멈추었다. \"너 천박한 마음을 가진 자그마한 에이미!"], ["You complete prison-child!’", "완전히 감옥에서 자란 아이!\""]], [["He stopped these angry reproaches with a wave of his hand, and sobbed out, raising his face and shaking his melancholy head at his younger daughter, ‘Amy, I know that you are innocent in intention.", "그는 손을 한 번 내저어 이 분노에 찬 비난들을 막고는, 얼굴을 들어 작은딸을 향해 우울한 고개를 가로저으며 흐느껴 말했다. \"에이미, 네 의도가 순수하다는 건 안다."], ["But you have cut me to the soul.’", "하지만 너는 내 영혼을 도려냈어.\""]], [["‘Innocent in intention!’ the implacable Fanny struck in.", "\"의도가 순수하다고요!\" 가차 없는 패니가 끼어들었다."], ["‘Stuff in intention!", "\"의도라니 헛소리예요!"], ["Low in intention!", "의도가 천한 거죠!"], ["Lowering of the family in intention!’", "의도가 가문을 깎아내리는 거예요!\""]], [["‘Father!’ cried Little Dorrit, pale and trembling.", "\"아버지!\" 리틀 도릿이 창백하게 떨며 외쳤다."], ["‘I am very sorry.", "\"정말 죄송해요."], ["Pray forgive me.", "부디 용서해 주세요."], ["Tell me how it is, that I may not do it again!’", "무슨 일인지 말씀해 주세요, 다시는 그러지 않게요!\""]], [["‘How it is, you prevaricating little piece of goods!’ cried Fanny.", "\"무슨 일이냐고, 이 발뺌하는 어린 것아!\" 패니가 외쳤다."], ["‘You know how it is.", "\"무슨 일인지 너도 알면서."], ["I have told you already, so don’t fly in the face of Providence by attempting to deny it!’", "내가 이미 말해 줬잖아, 그러니 그걸 부정하려 들어서 하늘의 섭리에 거역하지 마!\""]], [["‘Hush!", "\"쉿! 에이미.\""], ["Amy,’ said the father, passing his pocket-handkerchief several times across his face, and then grasping it convulsively in the hand that dropped across his knee, ‘I have done what I could to keep you select here; I have done what I could to retain you a position here.", "아버지가 손수건으로 얼굴을 여러 번 문지른 다음, 무릎 위로 떨어뜨린 손에 그것을 경련하듯 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"나는 너를 이곳에서 고상하게 지키려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다."], ["I may have succeeded; I may not.", "너에게 이곳에서 어떤 지위를 유지시켜 주려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했어."], ["You may know it; you may not.", "성공했을 수도 있고, 못 했을 수도 있지."], ["I give no opinion.", "너는 그걸 알 수도 있고, 모를 수도 있고."], ["I have endured everything here but humiliation.", "나는 아무 의견도 내지 않으마. 나는 이곳에서 굴욕만 빼고는 모든 것을 견뎌 왔다."], ["That I have happily been spared--until this day.’", "그 굴욕만은 다행히도 면해 왔어—오늘 이날까지는 말이다.\""]], [["Here his convulsive grasp unclosed itself, and he put his pocket-handkerchief to his eyes again.", "여기서 경련하듯 움켜쥔 그의 손이 풀리더니, 그는 다시 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."], ["Little Dorrit, on the ground beside him, with her imploring hand upon his arm, watched him remorsefully.", "그의 곁 바닥에 앉아 애원하는 손을 그의 팔에 얹은 리틀 도릿은, 자책하며 그를 지켜보았다."], ["Coming out of his fit of grief, he clenched his pocket-handkerchief once more.", "슬픔의 발작에서 빠져나오며 그는 다시 한번 손수건을 꽉 움켜쥐었다."]], [["‘Humiliation I have happily been spared until this day.", "\"굴욕만은 오늘 이날까지 다행히 면해 왔다."], ["Through all my troubles there has been that--Spirit in myself, and that--that submission to it, if I may use the term, in those about me, which has spared me--ha--humiliation.", "나의 온갖 고난을 통틀어, 내 안에는 그—기개라는 것이 있었고, 그리고 내 주위 사람들에게는—그 말을 써도 된다면—그 기개에 대한 순응이 있었기에, 그것이 나를—허—굴욕에서 지켜 주었다."], ["But this day, this minute, I have keenly felt it.’", "그러나 오늘 이날, 이 순간, 나는 그 굴욕을 뼈저리게 느꼈어.\""]], [["‘Of course!", "\"그야 당연하죠!"], ["How could it be otherwise?’ exclaimed the irrepressible Fanny.", "어찌 안 그러겠어요?\" 억누를 수 없는 패니가 외쳤다."], ["‘Careering and prancing about with a Pauper!’ (air-gun again).", "\"빈민하고 같이 휘젓고 으스대며 돌아다녔으니!\"(또 공기총이었다.)"]], [["‘But, dear father,’ cried Little Dorrit, ‘I don’t justify myself for having wounded your dear heart--no!", "\"하지만 사랑하는 아버지,\" 리틀 도릿이 외쳤다."], ["Heaven knows I don’t!’ She clasped her hands in quite an agony of distress.", "\"아버지의 소중한 마음을 아프게 한 것을 변명하려는 게 아니에요—아니에요! 하늘이 알듯이 그렇지 않아요!\""], ["‘I do nothing but beg and pray you to be comforted and overlook it.", "그녀는 그야말로 비탄의 고통 속에 두 손을 맞잡았다. \"저는 그저 마음을 푸시고 너그러이 봐 달라고 빌고 또 비는 것밖에 못 해요."], ["But if I had not known that you were kind to the old man yourself, and took much notice of him, and were always glad to see him, I would not have come here with him, father, I would not, indeed.", "하지만 아버지께서 직접 그 늙은 분께 친절하시고, 그분을 무척 마음에 두시고, 늘 그분을 보면 기뻐하셨다는 걸 몰랐다면, 저는 그분과 함께 이곳에 오지 않았을 거예요, 아버지, 정말 그러지 않았을 거예요. 제가 이토록 불행하게도 저지른 일은, 잘못 알고 한 일이에요."], ["What I have been so unhappy as to do, I have done in mistake.", "저는 절대로 일부러 아버지의 눈에 눈물 한 방울 맺히게 하지 않을 거예요, 사랑하는 아버지!\""], ["I would not wilfully bring a tear to your eyes, dear love!’ said Little Dorrit, her heart well-nigh broken, ‘for anything the world could give me, or anything it could take away.’", "거의 가슴이 미어진 채 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"세상이 제게 줄 수 있는 그 무엇을 위해서도, 세상이 제게서 앗아 갈 수 있는 그 무엇을 위해서도 그러지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["Fanny, with a partly angry and partly repentant sob, began to cry herself, and to say--as this young lady always said when she was half in passion and half out of it, half spiteful with herself and half spiteful with everybody else--that she wished she were dead.", "패니는 반은 화가 나고 반은 뉘우치는 흐느낌과 함께 자신도 울기 시작하더니, 자기가 차라리 죽었으면 좋겠다고 말했다—이 젊은 아가씨는 반은 격정에 차고 반은 격정에서 빠져나와, 반은 자신에게 심술이 나고 반은 다른 모든 이에게 심술이 날 때면 늘 그렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea in the meantime took his younger daughter to his breast, and patted her head.", "그러는 동안 마셜시의 아버지는 작은딸을 가슴에 끌어안고 그녀의 머리를 토닥였다."]], [["‘There, there!", "\"자, 자!"], ["Say no more, Amy, say no more, my child.", "더는 말하지 마라, 에이미, 더는 말하지 마, 얘야."], ["I will forget it as soon as I can.", "되도록 빨리 잊으마. 나는,\""], ["I,’ with hysterical cheerfulness, ‘I--shall soon be able to dismiss it.", "발작적인 쾌활함을 띠며, \"나는—곧 이 일을 떨쳐 낼 수 있을 거야."], ["It is perfectly true, my dear, that I am always glad to see my old pensioner--as such, as such--and that I do--ha--extend as much protection and kindness to the--hum--the bruised reed--I trust I may so call him without impropriety--as in my circumstances, I can.", "내가 나의 오랜 연금 수급자를 보는 게 언제나 기쁜 건—그런 자로서, 그런 자로서—틀림없는 사실이고, 또 내가—허—그 상한 갈대에게—그를 그렇게 불러도 결례가 아니길 바란다만—내 형편이 닿는 한 보호와 친절을 최대한 베푸는 것도—흠—틀림없는 사실이야."], ["It is quite true that this is the case, my dear child.", "그것이 사실이라는 건 정말 그래, 사랑하는 내 딸아."], ["At the same time, I preserve in doing this, if I may--ha--if I may use the expression--Spirit.", "그러면서도 나는 그렇게 하는 가운데, 말하자면—허—이 표현을 써도 된다면—기개를 지켜 왔다. 격에 맞는 기개를 말이야."], ["Becoming Spirit.", "그리고 그 기개와는,\""], ["And there are some things which are,’ he stopped to sob, ‘irreconcilable with that, and wound that--wound it deeply.", "그는 흐느끼느라 말을 멈추었다. \"도무지 어울릴 수 없는, 그것에 상처를 주는—그것에 깊이 상처를 주는 일들이 있어."], ["It is not that I have seen my good Amy attentive, and--ha--condescending to my old pensioner--it is not _that_ that hurts me.", "나의 착한 에이미가 내 오랜 연금 수급자에게 신경을 쓰고—허—몸을 낮춰 대하는 것을 본 게 아니야—나를 아프게 한 건 그게 아니다."], ["It is, if I am to close the painful subject by being explicit, that I have seen my child, my own child, my own daughter, coming into this College out of the public streets--smiling!", "이 괴로운 이야기를 분명히 말하고 끝내야 한다면, 그건, 내 아이가, 내 친자식이, 내 친딸이, 공공의 거리에서 이 학교로 들어오는 모습을—웃으면서!"], ["smiling!--arm in arm with--O my God, a livery!’", "웃으면서!—팔짱을 끼고서—아 하느님, 하인 제복하고 말이다!\""]], [["This reference to the coat of no cut and no time, the unfortunate gentleman gasped forth, in a scarcely audible voice, and with his clenched pocket-handkerchief raised in the air.", "마름질도 시절도 없는 그 외투를 두고 한 이 말을, 그 불행한 신사는 거의 들리지 않는 목소리로, 움켜쥔 손수건을 허공에 치켜든 채 헐떡이며 토해 냈다."], ["His excited feelings might have found some further painful utterance, but for a knock at the door, which had been already twice repeated, and to which Fanny (still wishing herself dead, and indeed now going so far as to add, buried) cried ‘Come in!’", "그의 격앙된 감정은 어쩌면 좀 더 괴로운 토로로 이어졌을지도 모르나, 이미 두 번이나 되풀이된 문 두드리는 소리가 그것을 막았고, 그 소리에 패니가(여전히 자기가 죽었으면 좋겠다고, 아니 이제는 한술 더 떠 땅에 묻혔으면 좋겠다고까지 하면서) \"들어오세요!\" 하고 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah, Young John!’ said the Father, in an altered and calmed voice.", "\"아, 젊은 존이구나!\" 아버지가 달라지고 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다."], ["‘What is it, Young John?’", "\"무슨 일이냐, 젊은 존?\""]], [["‘A letter for you, sir, being left in the Lodge just this minute, and a message with it, I thought, happening to be there myself, sir, I would bring it to your room.’ The speaker’s attention was much distracted by the piteous spectacle of Little Dorrit at her father’s feet, with her head turned away.", "\"어르신께 온 편지인데, 방금 막 수위실에 맡겨졌고 전갈도 함께 왔습니다, 마침 제가 그곳에 있던 터라, 어르신 방으로 가져다 드리는 게 좋겠다 싶어서요.\" 말하는 이의 주의는 아버지 발치에서 고개를 돌린 채 있는 리틀 도릿의 가련한 광경에 몹시 흐트러져 있었다."]], [["‘Indeed, John?", "\"그렇구나, 존?"], ["Thank you.’", "고맙다.\""]], [["‘The letter is from Mr Clennam, sir--it’s the answer--and the message was, sir, that Mr Clennam also sent his compliments, and word that he would do himself the pleasure of calling this afternoon, hoping to see you, and likewise,’ attention more distracted than before, ‘Miss Amy.’", "\"편지는 클레남 씨한테서 온 것입니다, 어르신—답장이고요—전갈은, 어르신, 클레남 씨가 안부를 전하면서, 오늘 오후에 찾아뵙는 기쁨을 누리고 싶다고, 어르신을 뵙기를 바라고, 또한,\" 아까보다 더 흐트러진 주의로, \"에이미 양도 뵙기를 바란다고 했습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ As the Father glanced into the letter (there was a bank-note in it), he reddened a little, and patted Amy on the head afresh.", "\"오!\" 아버지가 편지를 흘끗 들여다보니(그 안에는 은행권 한 장이 들어 있었다) 조금 얼굴을 붉히며 다시 에이미의 머리를 토닥였다."], ["‘Thank you, Young John.", "\"고맙다, 젊은 존."], ["Quite right.", "아주 잘했어."], ["Much obliged to you for your attention.", "마음 써 줘서 정말 고맙구나."], ["No one waiting?’", "기다리는 사람은 없느냐?\""]], [["‘No, sir, no one waiting.’", "\"없습니다, 어르신, 기다리는 사람은 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Thank you, John.", "\"고맙다, 존."], ["How is your mother, Young John?’", "어머니는 어떠시냐, 젊은 존?\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, she’s not quite as well as we could wish--in fact, we none of us are, except father--but she’s pretty well, sir.’", "\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 저희가 바라는 만큼 좋으시진 않습니다—사실 아버지만 빼고 저희 모두가 그렇습니다만—그래도 그런대로 잘 지내십니다, 어르신.\""]], [["‘Say we sent our remembrances, will you?", "\"우리가 안부 전하더라고 말씀드려 주겠니?"], ["Say kind remembrances, if you please, Young John.’", "부디, 따뜻한 안부 전하더라고 말이다, 젊은 존.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, I will.’ And Mr Chivery junior went his way, having spontaneously composed on the spot an entirely new epitaph for himself, to the effect that Here lay the body of John Chivery, Who, Having at such a date, Beheld the idol of his life, In grief and tears, And feeling unable to bear the harrowing spectacle, Immediately repaired to the abode of his inconsolable parents, And terminated his existence by his own rash act.", "\"고맙습니다, 어르신, 그러겠습니다.\" 그리고 치버리 주니어 씨는 제 갈 길을 갔는데, 그 자리에서 즉흥적으로 자신을 위한 완전히 새로운 묘비명을 지어 냈으니, 그 내용은 이러했다. 여기 존 치버리의 몸이 누웠노라, 모년 모월 모일에, 제 생의 우상이 슬픔과 눈물에 잠긴 모습을 보고, 그 가슴 미어지는 광경을 차마 견딜 수 없어, 즉시 위로할 길 없는 부모의 거처로 돌아가, 제 무모한 손으로 제 목숨을 끝낸 자라."]], [["‘There, there, Amy!’ said the Father, when Young John had closed the door, ‘let us say no more about it.’ The last few minutes had improved his spirits remarkably, and he was quite lightsome.", "\"자, 자, 에이미!\" 젊은 존이 문을 닫자 아버지가 말했다."], ["‘Where is my old pensioner all this while?", "\"이 일에 관해선 더는 말하지 말자.\" 지난 몇 분 사이에 그의 기분이 눈에 띄게 나아져, 그는 무척 가벼워졌다."], ["We must not leave him by himself any longer, or he will begin to suppose he is not welcome, and that would pain me.", "\"그동안 내 오랜 연금 수급자는 어디 있느냐? 그를 더는 혼자 두어서는 안 돼, 안 그러면 자기가 환영받지 못한다고 여기기 시작할 테고, 그건 내 마음을 아프게 할 테니."], ["Will you fetch him, my child, or shall I?’", "네가 그를 데려오겠느냐, 아니면 내가 가랴?\""]], [["‘If you wouldn’t mind, father,’ said Little Dorrit, trying to bring her sobbing to a close.", "\"괜찮으시다면, 아버지.\" 흐느낌을 그치려 애쓰며 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Certainly I will go, my dear.", "\"물론 내가 가마, 얘야."], ["I forgot; your eyes are rather red.", "깜빡했구나. 네 눈이 좀 빨갛구나."], ["There!", "자!"], ["Cheer up, Amy.", "기운 내라, 에이미."], ["Don’t be uneasy about me.", "나는 걱정 말고."], ["I am quite myself again, my love, quite myself.", "나는 이제 완전히 괜찮아졌단다, 얘야, 완전히 괜찮아."], ["Go to your room, Amy, and make yourself look comfortable and pleasant to receive Mr Clennam.’", "네 방으로 가서, 클레남 씨를 맞이할 수 있게 편안하고 보기 좋게 단장하렴.\""]], [["‘I would rather stay in my own room, Father,’ returned Little Dorrit, finding it more difficult than before to regain her composure.", "\"저는 제 방에 그냥 있는 게 좋겠어요, 아버지.\" 아까보다 더 평정을 되찾기 어려워하며 리틀 도릿이 응했다."], ["‘I would far rather not see Mr Clennam.’", "\"클레남 씨를 뵙지 않는 편이 훨씬 좋겠어요.\""]], [["‘Oh, fie, fie, my dear, that’s folly.", "\"아니, 저런, 저런, 얘야, 그건 어리석은 소리다."], ["Mr Clennam is a very gentlemanly man--very gentlemanly.", "클레남 씨는 아주 신사다운 분이야—아주 신사답지."], ["A little reserved at times; but I will say extremely gentlemanly.", "가끔은 좀 말수가 적지만, 그래도 더없이 신사답다고 나는 말하마."], ["I couldn’t think of your not being here to receive Mr Clennam, my dear, especially this afternoon.", "네가 여기서 클레남 씨를 맞이하지 않는다는 건 생각할 수도 없다, 얘야, 특히 오늘 오후엔 말이야."], ["So go and freshen yourself up, Amy; go and freshen yourself up, like a good girl.’", "그러니 가서 산뜻하게 단장하렴, 에이미. 가서 산뜻하게 단장하렴, 착한 아이답게.\""]], [["Thus directed, Little Dorrit dutifully rose and obeyed: only pausing for a moment as she went out of the room, to give her sister a kiss of reconciliation.", "이렇게 지시받자, 리틀 도릿은 순순히 일어나 따랐다. 다만 방을 나서며 잠시 멈추어, 화해의 입맞춤을 언니에게 건넸을 뿐이다."], ["Upon which, that young lady, feeling much harassed in her mind, and having for the time worn out the wish with which she generally relieved it, conceived and executed the brilliant idea of wishing Old Nandy dead, rather than that he should come bothering there like a disgusting, tiresome, wicked wretch, and making mischief between two sisters.", "그러자 그 젊은 아가씨는 마음이 몹시 어지럽고, 평소 마음을 달래던 그 소원도 당분간 다 써 버린 터라, 노인 낸디가 그런 역겹고 성가시고 고약한 비렁뱅이처럼 와서 귀찮게 굴며 자매 사이에 불화를 일으키느니 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다는 기발한 생각을 떠올리고 실행에 옮겼다."]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea, even humming a tune, and wearing his black velvet cap a little on one side, so much improved were his spirits, went down into the yard, and found his old pensioner standing there hat in hand just within the gate, as he had stood all this time. ‘Come, Nandy!’ said he, with great suavity. ‘Come up-stairs, Nandy; you know the way; why don’t you come up-stairs?’ He went the length, on this occasion, of giving him his hand and saying, ‘How are you, Nandy? Are you pretty well?’ To which that vocalist returned, ‘I thank you, honoured sir, I am all the better for seeing your honour.’ As they went along the yard, the Father of the Marshalsea presented him to a Collegian of recent date. ‘An old acquaintance of mine, sir, an old pensioner.’ And then said, ‘Be covered, my good Nandy; put your hat on,’ with great consideration.", "마셜시의 아버지는, 기분이 어찌나 좋아졌는지 콧노래까지 흥얼거리고 검은 벨벳 모자를 한쪽으로 살짝 비스듬히 쓴 채 마당으로 내려갔고, 거기서 그동안 내내 그러고 서 있던 대로 문 바로 안쪽에 모자를 손에 들고 서 있는 그의 오랜 연금 수급자를 발견했다. \"이리 오게, 낸디!\" 그가 무척 부드럽게 말했다. \"위층으로 올라오게, 낸디. 길은 알지 않나. 어째서 올라오지 않고 있나?\" 이번에는 그가 손까지 내밀며 \"잘 지내나, 낸디? 그런대로 괜찮은가?\" 하고 말하는 데까지 나아갔다. 그러자 그 노래꾼은 \"고맙습니다, 존경하는 어르신, 어르신을 뵈니 한결 좋아졌습니다\" 하고 응했다. 마당을 지나가며 마셜시의 아버지는 그를 최근에 들어온 한 재소자에게 소개했다. \"내 오랜 지인이라오, 선생, 오랜 연금 수급자지요.\" 그러고는 무척 배려 깊게 \"모자를 쓰게, 착한 낸디. 모자를 쓰게나\" 하고 말했다."]], [["His patronage did not stop here; for he charged Maggy to get the tea ready, and instructed her to buy certain tea-cakes, fresh butter, eggs, cold ham, and shrimps: to purchase which collation he gave her a bank-note for ten pounds, laying strict injunctions on her to be careful of the change.", "그의 후원은 여기서 그치지 않았으니, 그는 매기에게 차를 준비하라 이르고, 차에 곁들일 빵 몇 가지와 신선한 버터, 달걀, 차가운 햄, 새우를 사 오라고 지시했다. 그 음식들을 사 오라며 그는 매기에게 십 파운드짜리 은행권을 건네면서, 거스름돈을 잘 챙기라고 엄히 당부했다."], ["These preparations were in an advanced stage of progress, and his daughter Amy had come back with her work, when Clennam presented himself; whom he most graciously received, and besought to join their meal.", "이 준비가 한창 진행되고, 딸 에이미가 바느질거리를 들고 돌아왔을 무렵 클레남이 모습을 드러냈는데, 그는 클레남을 더없이 정중히 맞으며 식사에 함께해 달라고 청했다."]], [["‘Amy, my love, you know Mr Clennam even better than I have the happiness of doing.", "\"에이미, 얘야, 너는 내가 누리는 영광보다도 더 클레남 씨를 잘 알지. 패니, 얘야, 너도 클레남 씨와는 아는 사이고.\""], ["Fanny, my dear, you are acquainted with Mr Clennam.’ Fanny acknowledged him haughtily; the position she tacitly took up in all such cases being that there was a vast conspiracy to insult the family by not understanding it, or sufficiently deferring to it, and here was one of the conspirators.", "패니는 거만하게 그를 아는 체했다. 이런 경우마다 그녀가 말없이 취하는 입장은, 가문을 이해하지 못하거나 충분히 떠받들지 않음으로써 가문을 모욕하려는 거대한 음모가 있고 여기 그 음모자 중 하나가 있다는 것이었다."], ["‘This, Mr Clennam, you must know, is an old pensioner of mine, Old Nandy, a very faithful old man.’ (He always spoke of him as an object of great antiquity, but he was two or three years younger than himself.) ‘Let me see.", "\"이 사람은, 클레남 씨, 아셔야 하는데, 내 오랜 연금 수급자인 노인 낸디라오, 아주 충실한 늙은이지요.\"(그는 늘 그를 아주 까마득한 옛 인물인 양 말했으나, 그는 자신보다 두세 살 아래였다.) \"어디 보자."], ["You know Plornish, I think?", "당신은 플로니시를 아시지요?"], ["I think my daughter Amy has mentioned to me that you know poor Plornish?’", "내 딸 에이미가 당신이 그 가엾은 플로니시를 안다고 내게 말한 것 같은데?\""]], [["‘O yes!’ said Arthur Clennam.", "\"아, 그럼요!\" 아서 클레남이 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir, this is Mrs Plornish’s father.’", "\"그렇다면, 선생, 이 사람이 바로 플로니시 부인의 아버지라오.\""]], [["‘Indeed?", "\"그렇습니까?"], ["I am glad to see him.’", "만나 뵙게 되어 기쁩니다.\""]], [["‘You would be more glad if you knew his many good qualities, Mr Clennam.’", "\"그의 많은 미덕을 아신다면 더욱 기쁘실 겁니다, 클레남 씨.\""]], [["‘I hope I shall come to know them through knowing him,’ said Arthur, secretly pitying the bowed and submissive figure.", "\"그를 알아 가면서 그 미덕들도 알게 되길 바랍니다.\" 아서가 그 굽은 채 순종하는 모습을 속으로 가엾이 여기며 말했다."]], [["‘It is a holiday with him, and he comes to see his old friends, who are always glad to see him,’ observed the Father of the Marshalsea.", "\"이 사람에겐 오늘이 휴일이라, 그를 보면 늘 반가워하는 오랜 친구들을 보러 온 것이라오.\" 마셜시의 아버지가 말했다."], ["Then he added behind his hand, [‘Union, poor old fellow.", "그러고는 손으로 입을 가리며 덧붙였다. (\"연합 구빈원 신세지요, 가엾은 늙은이."], ["Out for the day.’)", "하루 외출 나온 거랍니다.\")"]], [["By this time Maggy, quietly assisted by her Little Mother, had spread the board, and the repast was ready.", "이쯤 되어 매기는, 자기의 리틀 마더의 조용한 도움을 받아 상을 차렸고, 식사가 준비되었다."], ["It being hot weather and the prison very close, the window was as wide open as it could be pushed.", "더운 날씨에 감옥 안이 무척 답답해, 창문은 밀어 열 수 있는 데까지 활짝 열려 있었다. \"매기가 창턱에 저 신문지를 펴 주면, 얘야,\""], ["‘If Maggy will spread that newspaper on the window-sill, my dear,’ remarked the Father complacently and in a half whisper to Little Dorrit, ‘my old pensioner can have his tea there, while we are having ours.’", "아버지가 흐뭇하게 리틀 도릿에게 반쯤 속삭이며 말했다. \"내 오랜 연금 수급자는 우리가 차를 마시는 동안 거기서 차를 마실 수 있겠구나.\""]], [["So, with a gulf between him and the good company of about a foot in width, standard measure, Mrs Plornish’s father was handsomely regaled.", "그리하여, 정식 척도로 약 한 자 너비의 만(灣)을 사이에 두고 그 훌륭한 일행과 떨어진 채, 플로니시 부인의 아버지는 융숭한 대접을 받았다."], ["Clennam had never seen anything like his magnanimous protection by that other Father, he of the Marshalsea; and was lost in the contemplation of its many wonders.", "클레남은 저 다른 아버지, 곧 마셜시의 아버지에게서 베풀어지는 그 너그러운 비호 같은 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없었으며, 그 숱한 기이함을 바라보는 데 정신이 팔렸다."]], [["The most striking of these was perhaps the relishing manner in which he remarked on the pensioner’s infirmities and failings, as if he were a gracious Keeper making a running commentary on the decline of the harmless animal he exhibited.", "그 가운데 가장 인상적인 것은 아마도, 그가 그 연금 수급자의 노쇠함과 결함을 두고, 마치 자신이 전시하는 그 해롭잖은 짐승의 쇠락에 관해 실황 해설을 늘어놓는 인자한 사육사라도 된 양, 음미하듯 논평하는 태도였을 것이다."]], [["‘Not ready for more ham yet, Nandy?", "\"아직 햄을 더 들 준비가 안 됐나, 낸디? 아니, 어찌 이리 굼뜬가!"], ["Why, how slow you are!", "(저 친구 마지막 남은 이가,\""], ["(His last teeth,’ he explained to the company, ‘are going, poor old boy.’)", "그가 일행에게 설명했다. \"다 빠져 가고 있다오, 가엾은 늙은이.)\""]], [["At another time, he said, ‘No shrimps, Nandy?’ and on his not instantly replying, observed, [‘His hearing is becoming very defective.", "또 어느 때엔 \"새우는 안 드나, 낸디?\" 하고 말했다가, 그가 곧바로 대답하지 않자 이렇게 말했다."], ["He’ll be deaf directly.’)", "(\"저 친구 청력이 아주 나빠지고 있어요. 곧 귀머거리가 될 겁니다.)\""]], [["At another time he asked him, ‘Do you walk much, Nandy, about the yard within the walls of that place of yours?’", "또 어느 때엔 그에게 이렇게 물었다. \"그곳 담장 안 마당에서 산책은 많이 하나, 낸디?\""]], [["‘No, sir; no.", "\"아닙니다, 어르신."], ["I haven’t any great liking for that.’", "안 합니다. 그건 그리 좋아하지 않아서요.\""]], [["‘No, to be sure,’ he assented. ‘Very natural.’ Then he privately informed the circle [‘Legs going.’)", "\"그래, 그렇겠지.\" 그가 맞장구쳤다. \"지극히 당연하지.\" 그러고는 좌중에 가만히 알려 주었다. (\"다리가 쇠하고 있어요.)\""]], [["Once he asked the pensioner, in that general clemency which asked him anything to keep him afloat, how old his younger grandchild was?", "한번은 그를 띄워 주려고 무엇이든 묻고 보는 그 두루뭉술한 관대함으로, 연금 수급자에게 막내 손주가 몇 살이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘John Edward,’ said the pensioner, slowly laying down his knife and fork to consider.", "\"존 에드워드 말입니까.\" 연금 수급자가 생각하느라 천천히 나이프와 포크를 내려놓으며 말했다."], ["‘How old, sir?", "\"몇 살이냐고요, 어르신?"], ["Let me think now.’", "어디 가만 보자.\""]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea tapped his forehead [‘Memory weak.’)", "마셜시의 아버지는 자기 이마를 톡톡 두드렸다. (\"기억력이 약해요.)\""]], [["‘John Edward, sir?", "\"존 에드워드 말입니까, 어르신?"], ["Well, I really forget.", "글쎄, 정말 잊었습니다."], ["I couldn’t say at this minute, sir, whether it’s two and two months, or whether it’s two and five months.", "지금 당장은 말씀드릴 수가 없네요, 어르신, 그게 두 살 두 달인지, 아니면 두 살 다섯 달인지."], ["It’s one or the other.’", "둘 중 하납니다.\""]], [["‘Don’t distress yourself by worrying your mind about it,’ he returned, with infinite forbearance.", "\"그것 때문에 마음을 졸이며 애쓰지 말게.\" 그가 한없는 너그러움으로 응했다."], ["[‘Faculties evidently decaying--old man rusts in the life he leads!’)", "(\"기능이 확연히 쇠하고 있어요—이런 삶을 사니 늙은이가 녹이 슬지요!)\""]], [["The more of these discoveries that he persuaded himself he made in the pensioner, the better he appeared to like him; and when he got out of his chair after tea to bid the pensioner good-bye, on his intimating that he feared, honoured sir, his time was running out, he made himself look as erect and strong as possible.", "연금 수급자에게서 그런 발견을 했다고 스스로 믿게 될수록, 그는 그를 더욱 마음에 들어 하는 듯했다. 그리고 차를 마신 뒤, 연금 수급자가 죄송하오나 어르신, 시간이 다 되어 가는 것 같다고 넌지시 비치자, 그에게 작별을 고하려고 의자에서 일어서면서, 그는 되도록 꼿꼿하고 튼튼해 보이도록 자세를 가다듬었다."]], [["‘We don’t call this a shilling, Nandy, you know,’ he said, putting one in his hand.", "\"이건 우리끼린 일 실링이라고 부르지 않는 거 알지, 낸디.\" 그가 그의 손에 한 닢을 쥐여 주며 말했다."], ["‘We call it tobacco.’", "\"이건 담배라고 부르는 거야.\""]], [["‘Honoured sir, I thank you.", "\"존경하는 어르신, 감사합니다."], ["It shall buy tobacco.", "이걸로 담배를 사겠습니다."], ["My thanks and duty to Miss Amy and Miss Fanny.", "에이미 양과 패니 양께도 감사와 문안의 말씀을."], ["I wish you good night, Mr Clennam.’", "안녕히 주무십시오, 클레남 씨.\""]], [["‘And mind you don’t forget us, you know, Nandy,’ said the Father.", "\"그리고 명심하게, 우리를 잊으면 안 되네, 낸디.\" 아버지가 말했다."], ["‘You must come again, mind, whenever you have an afternoon.", "\"또 와야 하네, 명심하고, 오후가 빌 때마다 말이야. 우리를 보지 않고 그냥 나가면 안 돼, 안 그러면 우리가 서운해할 테니."], ["You must not come out without seeing us, or we shall be jealous.", "잘 가게, 낸디. 층계를 내려갈 땐 아주 조심하게, 낸디."], ["Good night, Nandy.", "좀 고르지 못하고 닳았으니까.\""], ["Be very careful how you descend the stairs, Nandy; they are rather uneven and worn.’ With that he stood on the landing, watching the old man down: and when he came into the room again, said, with a solemn satisfaction on him, ‘A melancholy sight that, Mr Clennam, though one has the consolation of knowing that he doesn’t feel it himself.", "그러고서 그는 층계참에 서서 늙은이가 내려가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그리고 다시 방으로 들어와, 엄숙한 만족감을 띤 채 말했다."], ["The poor old fellow is a dismal wreck.", "\"참 서글픈 광경이지요, 클레남 씨, 그래도 본인은 그걸 느끼지 못한다는 걸 아는 게 위안이긴 합니다만."], ["Spirit broken and gone--pulverised--crushed out of him, sir, completely!’", "저 가엾은 늙은이는 처량한 폐인이라오. 기개가 꺾이고 사라져—가루가 되고—완전히 으스러져 버렸지요, 선생!\""]], [["As Clennam had a purpose in remaining, he said what he could responsive to these sentiments, and stood at the window with their enunciator, while Maggy and her Little Mother washed the tea-service and cleared it away.", "클레남은 남아 있을 목적이 있었으므로, 이런 소회에 응하여 할 수 있는 말을 했고, 매기와 그녀의 리틀 마더가 다기를 닦아 치우는 동안 그것을 토로하는 사람과 함께 창가에 서 있었다."], ["He noticed that his companion stood at the window with the air of an affable and accessible Sovereign, and that, when any of his people in the yard below looked up, his recognition of their salutes just stopped short of a blessing.", "그는 자기 길동무가 상냥하고 가까이하기 쉬운 군주 같은 분위기로 창가에 서 있는 것을, 그리고 아래 마당에 있는 자기 백성 가운데 누군가가 올려다볼 때면, 그 인사에 답하는 것이 축복을 내리는 것에 못 미칠 따름임을 알아챘다."]], [["When Little Dorrit had her work on the table, and Maggy hers on the bedstead, Fanny fell to tying her bonnet as a preliminary to her departure.", "리틀 도릿이 자기 바느질거리를 탁자 위에 올려놓고, 매기가 자기 것을 침상에 올려놓았을 때, 패니는 떠날 채비로 보닛 끈을 매기 시작했다."], ["Arthur, still having his purpose, still remained.", "아서는 여전히 목적이 있었으므로 여전히 남아 있었다."], ["At this time the door opened, without any notice, and Mr Tip came in.", "이때 문이 아무런 기별도 없이 열리더니 팁 씨가 들어왔다."], ["He kissed Amy as she started up to meet him, nodded to Fanny, nodded to his father, gloomed on the visitor without further recognition, and sat down.", "그는 자기를 맞으려 벌떡 일어선 에이미에게 입을 맞추고, 패니에게 고개를 끄덕이고, 아버지에게 고개를 끄덕이고, 그 이상의 아는 체는 없이 손님을 침울하게 흘겨보고는 자리에 앉았다."]], [["‘Tip, dear,’ said Little Dorrit, mildly, shocked by this, ‘don’t you see--’", "\"팁 오빠,\" 리틀 도릿이 이에 충격을 받아 부드럽게 말했다. \"안 보여요—\""]], [["‘Yes, I see, Amy.", "\"보여, 에이미. 네가 여기 와 있는 손님의 존재를 두고 하는 말이라면—내 말은, 네가 그걸 두고 하는 말이라면,\""], ["If you refer to the presence of any visitor you have here--I say, if you refer to that,’ answered Tip, jerking his head with emphasis towards his shoulder nearest Clennam, ‘I see!’", "클레남 쪽에 가까운 어깨 쪽으로 힘주어 고개를 홱 젖히며 팁이 대답했다. \"보인다고!\""]], [["‘Is that all you say?’", "\"그게 다예요?\""]], [["‘That’s all I say.", "\"그게 다야. 그리고 내 짐작에,\""], ["And I suppose,’ added the lofty young man, after a moment’s pause, ‘that visitor will understand me, when I say that’s all I say.", "그 거만한 젊은이가 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 덧붙였다. \"저 손님도 내가 그게 다라고 할 때 무슨 말인지 알아들을 거야."], ["In short, I suppose the visitor will understand that he hasn’t used me like a gentleman.’", "한마디로, 저 손님도 자기가 나를 신사답게 대하지 않았다는 걸 알아들을 거라고.\""]], [["‘I do not understand that,’ observed the obnoxious personage referred to with tranquillity.", "\"나는 그렇게 알아듣지 못하겠는데요.\" 거명된 그 거슬리는 인물이 차분하게 말했다."]], [["‘No?", "\"못 알아들어요?"], ["Why, then, to make it clearer to you, sir, I beg to let you know that when I address what I call a properly-worded appeal, and an urgent appeal, and a delicate appeal, to an individual, for a small temporary accommodation, easily within his power--easily within his power, mind!--and when that individual writes back word to me that he begs to be excused, I consider that he doesn’t treat me like a gentleman.’", "그렇다면, 더 분명히 해 드리지요, 선생, 알려 드리는데, 내가 누군가에게 점잖게 말을 갖춘 부탁, 절박한 부탁, 조심스러운 부탁을, 그 사람 능력으로 손쉽게 들어줄 수 있는—손쉽게 들어줄 수 있는, 명심하시오!—작은 일시적 융통을 청했을 때, 그리고 그 사람이 내게 죄송하지만 사양하겠노라 답장을 써 보냈을 때, 나는 그자가 나를 신사답게 대하지 않은 것으로 칩니다.\""]], [["The Father of the Marshalsea, who had surveyed his son in silence, no sooner heard this sentiment, than he began in angry voice:--", "아들을 말없이 살피고 있던 마셜시의 아버지는, 이 말을 듣자마자 노여운 목소리로 입을 열었다."]], [["‘How dare you--’ But his son stopped him.", "\"어찌 감히 네가—\" 그러나 아들이 그를 막았다."]], [["‘Now, don’t ask me how I dare, father, because that’s bosh.", "\"자, 어찌 감히냐고는 묻지 마세요, 아버지, 그건 다 헛소리니까."], ["As to the fact of the line of conduct I choose to adopt towards the individual present, you ought to be proud of my showing a proper spirit.’", "내가 여기 있는 저 인물에게 취하기로 한 처신의 사실로 말하자면, 아버지는 내가 마땅한 기개를 보이는 걸 자랑스러워하셔야 마땅하다고요.\""]], [["‘I should think so!’ cried Fanny.", "\"그렇고말고!\" 패니가 외쳤다."]], [["‘A proper spirit?’ said the Father.", "\"마땅한 기개라고?\" 아버지가 말했다."], ["‘Yes, a proper spirit; a becoming spirit.", "\"그래, 마땅한 기개, 격에 맞는 기개라."], ["Is it come to this that my son teaches me--_me_--spirit!’", "이제 내 아들이 나에게—나에게—기개를 가르치는 지경에 이른 것이냐!\""]], [["‘Now, don’t let us bother about it, father, or have any row on the subject.", "\"자, 이 일로 골치 썩지도 말고, 시끄럽게 굴지도 맙시다, 아버지."], ["I have fully made up my mind that the individual present has not treated me like a gentleman.", "나는 여기 있는 저 인물이 나를 신사답게 대하지 않았다고 완전히 마음을 굳혔어요."], ["And there’s an end of it.’", "그걸로 끝입니다.\""]], [["‘But there is not an end of it, sir,’ returned the Father.", "\"하지만 그걸로 끝이 아니다, 이놈아.\" 아버지가 응수했다."], ["‘But there shall not be an end of it.", "\"그걸로 끝나서는 안 돼."], ["You have made up your mind?", "네가 마음을 굳혔다고?"], ["You have made up your mind?’", "마음을 굳혔다고?\""]], [["‘Yes, _I_ have.", "\"네, 내가 굳혔어요."], ["What’s the good of keeping on like that?’", "그렇게 계속 들볶아서 좋을 게 뭡니까?\""]], [["‘Because,’ returned the Father, in a great heat, ‘you had no right to make up your mind to what is monstrous, to what is--ha--immoral, to what is--hum--parricidal.", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["No, Mr Clennam, I beg, sir.", "아버지가 잔뜩 열을 내며 응수했다."], ["Don’t ask me to desist; there is a--hum--a general principle involved here, which rises even above considerations of--ha--hospitality.", "\"너에겐 그 끔찍한 것에, 그—허—부도덕한 것에, 그—흠—애비를 죽이는 짓 같은 것에 마음을 굳힐 권리가 없기 때문이다. 아니요, 클레남 씨, 부탁이오, 선생."], ["I object to the assertion made by my son.", "나더러 그만두라 하지 마시오. 여기에는—흠—일반적인 원칙이 걸려 있고, 그것은—허—환대에 대한 고려보다도 더 위에 있는 것이오."], ["I--ha--I personally repel it.’", "나는 내 아들이 한 그 주장에 반대하오. 나는—허—나 개인으로서 그것을 물리치오.\""]], [["‘Why, what is it to you, father?’ returned the son, over his shoulder.", "\"아니, 그게 아버지하고 무슨 상관이에요?\" 아들이 어깨 너머로 응수했다."]], [["‘What is it to me, sir?", "\"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이냐고, 이놈아?"], ["I have a--hum--a spirit, sir, that will not endure it.", "나에겐—흠—기개가 있다, 이놈아, 그걸 참아 넘기지 않을 기개 말이다."], ["I,’ he took out his pocket-handkerchief again and dabbed his face.", "나는,\" 그는 다시 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 톡톡 눌렀다."], ["‘I am outraged and insulted by it.", "\"그것 때문에 모욕당하고 능멸당했다."], ["Let me suppose the case that I myself may at a certain time--ha--or times, have made a--hum--an appeal, and a properly-worded appeal, and a delicate appeal, and an urgent appeal to some individual for a small temporary accommodation.", "이런 경우를 한번 가정해 보자, 나 자신이 어느 한때—허—혹은 여러 때, 어떤 사람에게 작은 일시적 융통을 청하는—흠—부탁을, 그것도 점잖게 말을 갖춘 부탁, 조심스러운 부탁, 절박한 부탁을 했다고 치자."], ["Let me suppose that that accommodation could have been easily extended, and was not extended, and that that individual informed me that he begged to be excused.", "그 융통을 손쉽게 베풀 수 있었는데도 베풀어 주지 않고, 그 사람이 내게 죄송하지만 사양하겠노라 알려 왔다고 치자."], ["Am I to be told by my own son, that I therefore received treatment not due to a gentleman, and that I--ha--I submitted to it?’", "그렇다고 해서 내가 신사로서 받아야 할 대접을 받지 못한 것이고, 그래서 내가—허—그것을 감수했다고, 내 친아들에게서 들어야 하는 것이냐?\""]], [["His daughter Amy gently tried to calm him, but he would not on any account be calmed.", "딸 에이미가 부드럽게 그를 진정시키려 했으나, 그는 무슨 일이 있어도 진정하려 들지 않았다."], ["He said his spirit was up, and wouldn’t endure this.", "그는 자기 기개가 솟구쳤으니 이것을 견디지 못하겠노라고 말했다."]], [["Was he to be told that, he wished to know again, by his own son on his own hearth, to his own face?", "자기 친아들에게서, 자기 집 화롯가에서, 자기 면전에 대고 그런 말을 들어야 하느냐고, 그는 다시 알고 싶어 했다."], ["Was that humiliation to be put upon him by his own blood?", "자기 핏줄에게서 그런 굴욕을 당해야 하느냐고?"]], [["‘You are putting it on yourself, father, and getting into all this injury of your own accord!’ said the young gentleman morosely.", "\"그건 아버지가 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 거예요, 아버지, 그 모든 상처를 스스로 자초하시는 거라고요!\" 그 젊은 신사가 뚱하게 말했다."], ["‘What I have made up my mind about has nothing to do with you.", "\"내가 마음을 굳힌 건 아버지와는 아무 상관 없어요."], ["What I said had nothing to do with you.", "내가 한 말도 아버지와는 아무 상관 없었고요."], ["Why need you go trying on other people’s hats?’", "왜 굳이 남의 모자를 써 보려고 하세요?\""]], [["‘I reply it has everything to do with me,’ returned the Father.", "\"내 대답은, 그것이 나하고 모든 면에서 상관이 있다는 것이다.\""], ["‘I point out to you, sir, with indignation, that--hum--the--ha--delicacy and peculiarity of your father’s position should strike you dumb, sir, if nothing else should, in laying down such--ha--such unnatural principles.", "아버지가 응수했다. \"분개하며 네게 지적하건대, 이놈아—흠—네 아비가 처한 위치의—허—그 미묘함과 특수함이, 다른 무엇으로도 그러지 못한다면, 적어도 너로 하여금 그런—허—그런 천륜에 어긋나는 원칙을 늘어놓을 때 입을 다물게 해야 마땅하다, 이놈아."], ["Besides; if you are not filial, sir, if you discard that duty, you are at least--hum--not a Christian?", "게다가, 네가 자식 된 도리를 모른다면, 그 본분을 저버린다면, 너는 적어도—흠—기독교인은 아니란 말이냐?"], ["Are you--ha--an Atheist?", "너는—허—무신론자냐?"], ["And is it Christian, let me ask you, to stigmatise and denounce an individual for begging to be excused this time, when the same individual may--ha--respond with the required accommodation next time?", "묻건대, 어떤 사람이 이번에 사양하겠다고 청했다고 해서—그 같은 사람이—허—다음번에는 청한 융통에 응할 수도 있는데—그 사람을 낙인찍고 규탄하는 것이 기독교인다운 일이냐?"], ["Is it the part of a Christian not to--hum--not to try him again?’ He had worked himself into quite a religious glow and fervour.", "그를 다시—흠—다시 시험해 보지 않는 것이 기독교인의 도리냐?\" 그는 어느새 자못 종교적인 열기와 열정에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["‘I see precious well,’ said Mr Tip, rising, ‘that I shall get no sensible or fair argument here to-night, and so the best thing I can do is to cut.", "\"오늘 밤 여기서는 분별 있거나 공정한 의논을 얻지 못하겠다는 걸 아주 잘 알겠네요.\" 팁 씨가 일어서며 말했다."], ["Good night, Amy.", "\"그러니 내가 할 수 있는 최선은 가 버리는 겁니다."], ["Don’t be vexed.", "잘 있어, 에이미. 속상해하지 마."], ["I am very sorry it happens here, and you here, upon my soul I am; but I can’t altogether part with my spirit, even for your sake, old girl.’", "하필 여기서, 그리고 네가 있는 데서 이런 일이 벌어져서 정말 미안하다, 정말이야. 하지만 너를 봐서라도 내 기개를 아주 내려놓을 수는 없어, 누이야.\""]], [["With those words he put on his hat and went out, accompanied by Miss Fanny; who did not consider it spirited on her part to take leave of Clennam with any less opposing demonstration than a stare, importing that she had always known him for one of the large body of conspirators.", "이 말과 함께 그는 모자를 쓰고 나가 버렸고, 패니 양이 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그녀는 클레남이 늘 알고 지내던 그 거대한 음모자 무리의 하나임을 내비치는 한 차례의 노려봄, 그보다 덜한 적대의 시위로 그에게 작별하는 것은 자기 기개에 어울리지 않는다고 여겼다."]], [["When they were gone, the Father of the Marshalsea was at first inclined to sink into despondency again, and would have done so, but that a gentleman opportunely came up within a minute or two to attend him to the Snuggery.", "그들이 가고 나자, 마셜시의 아버지는 처음엔 다시 낙담에 빠지려는 기색이었고, 실제로 그리되었을 테지만, 마침 한두 분 뒤에 한 신사가 그를 스너거리로 모시러 올라왔다."], ["It was the gentleman Clennam had seen on the night of his own accidental detention there, who had that impalpable grievance about the misappropriated Fund on which the Marshal was supposed to batten.", "그는 클레남이 우연히 이곳에 갇혔던 그날 밤 보았던 신사로, 마셜이 살을 찌우는 것으로 여겨지는 그 유용된 기금에 관해 손에 잡히지 않는 불만을 품고 있던 자였다."], ["He presented himself as deputation to escort the Father to the Chair, it being an occasion on which he had promised to preside over the assembled Collegians in the enjoyment of a little Harmony.", "그는 아버지를 의장석으로 모시는 대표단으로 나선 것이었으니, 그날은 그가 약간의 음악적 화합을 즐기려고 모인 재소자들 위에 좌장으로 앉기로 약속한 자리였다."]], [["‘Such, you see, Mr Clennam,’ said the Father, ‘are the incongruities of my position here.", "\"보시다시피, 클레남 씨,\" 아버지가 말했다."], ["But a public duty!", "\"이런 것이 여기서의 내 처지가 지닌 부조화라오."], ["No man, I am sure, would more readily recognise a public duty than yourself.’", "하지만 공적인 의무이니! 내 확신컨대, 누구도 당신 자신보다 더 기꺼이 공적인 의무를 인정하지는 못할 것이오.\""]], [["Clennam besought him not to delay a moment.", "클레남은 그에게 잠시도 지체하지 말라고 간청했다."]], [["‘Amy, my dear, if you can persuade Mr Clennam to stay longer, I can leave the honours of our poor apology for an establishment with confidence in your hands, and perhaps you may do something towards erasing from Mr Clennam’s mind the--ha--untoward and unpleasant circumstance which has occurred since tea-time.’", "\"에이미, 얘야, 네가 클레남 씨를 더 머물게 설득할 수 있다면, 우리의 변변찮은 살림이나마 그 손님 대접의 영예를 네 손에 안심하고 맡길 수 있겠구나. 그리고 어쩌면 너는 차 마신 뒤로 일어난—허—그 거북하고 불쾌한 일을 클레남 씨의 마음에서 지우는 데 무언가 보탬이 될지도 모르고.\""]], [["Clennam assured him that it had made no impression on his mind, and therefore required no erasure.", "클레남은 그것이 자기 마음에 아무런 인상도 남기지 않았으므로 지울 필요도 없다고 그를 안심시켰다."]], [["‘My dear sir,’ said the Father, with a removal of his black cap and a grasp of Clennam’s hand, combining to express the safe receipt of his note and enclosure that afternoon, ‘Heaven ever bless you!’", "\"이보시오,\" 검은 모자를 벗고 클레남의 손을 잡으며—이는 그날 오후 받은 그의 편지와 동봉물을 무사히 받았음을 함께 나타내는 것이었다—아버지가 말했다. \"하늘이 늘 당신을 축복하시길!\""]], [["So, at last, Clennam’s purpose in remaining was attained, and he could speak to Little Dorrit with nobody by.", "그리하여 마침내, 남아 있던 클레남의 목적이 이루어졌고, 그는 곁에 아무도 없이 리틀 도릿에게 말을 건넬 수 있게 되었다."], ["Maggy counted as nobody, and she was by.", "매기는 아무도 아닌 것으로 쳤고, 그녀는 곁에 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 32. More Fortune-Telling", "# CHAPTER 32. More Fortune-Telling"]], [["Maggy sat at her work in her great white cap with its quantity of opaque frilling hiding what profile she had (she had none to spare), and her serviceable eye brought to bear upon her occupation, on the window side of the room.", "매기는 창가 쪽에 앉아 일을 하고 있었다. 머리에는 커다란 흰색 모자를 썼는데, 불투명한 주름 장식이 잔뜩 달려 있어 그나마 있던 옆얼굴 윤곽을(여분이라 할 만한 게 없었다) 가려 버렸고, 쓸 만한 한쪽 눈만은 일감에 집중하고 있었다."], ["What with her flapping cap, and what with her unserviceable eye, she was quite partitioned off from her Little Mother, whose seat was opposite the window.", "펄럭이는 모자와 못 쓰는 한쪽 눈 때문에 그녀는 창문 맞은편에 앉은 작은 어머니와 완전히 칸막이로 갈라진 듯했다."], ["The tread and shuffle of feet on the pavement of the yard had much diminished since the taking of the Chair, the tide of Collegians having set strongly in the direction of Harmony.", "마당 포석을 밟는 발소리와 끄는 소리는 의장석에 누군가 앉은 뒤로 한결 잦아들었으니, 재학생들의 물결이 화합을 향해 거세게 밀려간 까닭이었다."], ["Some few who had no music in their souls, or no money in their pockets, dawdled about; and the old spectacle of the visitor-wife and the depressed unseasoned prisoner still lingered in corners, as broken cobwebs and such unsightly discomforts draggle in corners of other places.", "영혼에 음악이 없거나 주머니에 돈이 없는 몇몇은 어슬렁거렸고, 면회 온 아내와 풀 죽은 채 아직 익숙해지지 못한 죄수가 어우러진 그 오래된 풍경은, 마치 다른 곳들의 구석마다 찢어진 거미줄이며 흉한 너저분함이 늘어져 있듯, 여전히 구석에 머물러 있었다."], ["It was the quietest time the College knew, saving the night hours when the Collegians took the benefit of the act of sleep.", "재학생들이 잠이라는 법령의 혜택을 누리던 밤 시간을 빼면, 이때가 학교에서 가장 조용한 시간이었다."], ["The occasional rattle of applause upon the tables of the Snuggery, denoted the successful termination of a morsel of Harmony; or the responsive acceptance, by the united children, of some toast or sentiment offered to them by their Father.", "이따금 아늑한 방의 탁자를 두드리는 박수 소리가 들려와 화합의 한 토막이 성공적으로 끝났음을, 또는 아버지가 그들에게 내건 어떤 건배사나 좋은 말을 한데 모인 자식들이 호응하며 받아들였음을 알렸다."], ["Occasionally, a vocal strain more sonorous than the generality informed the listener that some boastful bass was in blue water, or in the hunting field, or with the reindeer, or on the mountain, or among the heather; but the Marshal of the Marshalsea knew better, and had got him hard and fast.", "가끔은 보통보다 더 우렁찬 노랫가락이 듣는 이에게 어떤 허풍스러운 베이스가 푸른 바다 위에 있다거나, 사냥터에 있다거나, 순록과 함께 있다거나, 산 위에 있다거나, 히스 풀밭 사이에 있다고 일러 주었으나, 마셜시의 간수장은 더 잘 알았으니, 그자를 단단히 붙들어 두고 있었던 것이다."]], [["As Arthur Clennam moved to sit down by the side of Little Dorrit, she trembled so that she had much ado to hold her needle.", "아서 클레넘이 리틀 도릿 곁에 앉으려고 다가가자, 그녀는 어찌나 떨었던지 바늘을 쥐고 있기조차 버거워했다."], ["Clennam gently put his hand upon her work, and said, ‘Dear Little Dorrit, let me lay it down.’", "클레넘은 그녀의 일감 위에 가만히 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 이건 내려놓도록 합시다.\""]], [["She yielded it to him, and he put it aside.", "그녀는 그것을 그에게 내주었고, 그는 한쪽으로 치워 두었다."], ["Her hands were then nervously clasping together, but he took one of them.", "그러자 그녀의 두 손은 초조하게 맞잡혔는데, 그가 그중 한 손을 잡았다."]], [["‘How seldom I have seen you lately, Little Dorrit!’", "\"요즘 당신을 정말 드물게밖에 못 보았군요, 리틀 도릿!\""]], [["‘I have been busy, sir.’", "\"제가 바빴어요, 선생님.\""]], [["‘But I heard only to-day,’ said Clennam, ‘by mere accident, of your having been with those good people close by me.", "\"그런데 오늘에서야, 그것도 순전히 우연으로 들었답니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Why not come to me, then?’", "\"당신이 내 바로 가까이 사는 그 선량한 사람들과 함께 있었다는 걸 말입니다. 그럼 어째서 나를 찾아오지 않았어요?\""]], [["‘I--I don’t know.", "\"저는—저는 모르겠어요."], ["Or rather, I thought you might be busy too.", "아니, 그보다도, 선생님도 바쁘시리라 생각했어요."], ["You generally are now, are you not?’", "요즘 대개 그러시잖아요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["He saw her trembling little form and her downcast face, and the eyes that drooped the moment they were raised to his--he saw them almost with as much concern as tenderness.", "그는 떨고 있는 그녀의 작은 몸과 떨군 얼굴을, 그리고 그를 향해 들렸다가 그 순간 다시 처지는 눈을 보았다—그는 그것을 다정함 못지않은 염려로 바라보았다."]], [["‘My child, your manner is so changed!’", "\"얘야, 네 태도가 너무도 달라졌구나!\""]], [["The trembling was now quite beyond her control.", "이제 떨림은 그녀가 도저히 어쩌지 못할 지경이 되었다."], ["Softly withdrawing her hand, and laying it in her other hand, she sat before him with her head bent and her whole form trembling.", "그녀는 가만히 손을 빼내어 다른 손 위에 포개고는, 고개를 숙이고 온몸을 떨면서 그의 앞에 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘My own Little Dorrit,’ said Clennam, compassionately.", "\"내 소중한 리틀 도릿.\" 클레넘이 측은히 여기며 말했다."]], [["She burst into tears.", "그녀는 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Maggy looked round of a sudden, and stared for at least a minute; but did not interpose.", "매기가 갑자기 고개를 돌리더니 적어도 일 분은 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그러나 끼어들지는 않았다."], ["Clennam waited some little while before he spoke again.", "클레넘은 잠시 기다렸다가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["‘I cannot bear,’ he said then, ‘to see you weep; but I hope this is a relief to an overcharged heart.’", "\"나는 당신이 우는 걸 보고 있을 수가 없군요.\" 그때 그가 말했다. \"하지만 이것이 너무 벅찬 가슴을 덜어 주는 거라면 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Yes it is, sir.", "\"네, 그래요, 선생님."], ["Nothing but that.’", "그것뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Well, well!", "\"자, 자!"], ["I feared you would think too much of what passed here just now.", "나는 당신이 방금 여기서 있었던 일을 너무 깊이 생각하지나 않을까 걱정했어요."], ["It is of no moment; not the least.", "그건 아무것도 아닙니다, 조금도요."], ["I am only unfortunate to have come in the way.", "다만 내가 그 자리에 끼어든 것이 불운이었을 뿐이지요."], ["Let it go by with these tears.", "이 눈물과 함께 흘려보냅시다."], ["It is not worth one of them. One of them?", "그 일은 눈물 한 방울만큼의 가치도 없어요."], ["Such an idle thing should be repeated, with my glad consent, fifty times a day, to save you a moment’s heart-ache, Little Dorrit.’", "눈물 한 방울이라니? 그런 하찮은 일이라면, 당신의 한순간의 가슴앓이를 덜어 줄 수만 있다면 나는 기꺼이 동의하며 하루에 쉰 번이라도 되풀이하겠습니다, 리틀 도릿.\""]], [["She had taken courage now, and answered, far more in her usual manner, ‘You are so good!", "이제 그녀는 용기를 내어, 한결 평소의 태도에 가깝게 대답했다. \"선생님은 정말 좋으신 분이에요!"], ["But even if there was nothing else in it to be sorry for and ashamed of, it is such a bad return to you--’", "하지만 거기에 슬퍼하고 부끄러워할 다른 무엇이 없다 하더라도, 이건 선생님께 너무도 잘못된 보답이에요—\""]], [["‘Hush!’ said Clennam, smiling and touching her lips with his hand.", "\"쉿!\" 클레넘이 미소 지으며 그녀의 입술에 손을 대고 말했다."], ["‘Forgetfulness in you who remember so many and so much, would be new indeed.", "\"그토록 많은 것과 많은 사람을 기억하는 당신에게 망각이라니, 그거야말로 정말 새로운 일이겠지요."], ["Shall I remind you that I am not, and that I never was, anything but the friend whom you agreed to trust?", "내가 당신이 믿기로 한 그 친구일 뿐이고, 여태껏 그 이상의 무엇이었던 적도 없다는 걸 일깨워 드릴까요? 아니죠."], ["No. You remember it, don’t you?’", "당신은 기억하고 있지요, 그렇지요?\""]], [["‘I try to do so, or I should have broken the promise just now, when my mistaken brother was here. You will consider his bringing-up in this place, and will not judge him hardly, poor fellow, I know!’ In raising her eyes with these words, she observed his face more nearly than she had done yet, and said, with a quick change of tone, ‘You have not been ill, Mr Clennam?’", "\"기억하려고 애써요. 그러지 않았다면 방금, 제 오해 많은 오빠가 여기 있었을 때 그 약속을 깨뜨렸을 거예요. 그가 이런 곳에서 자랐다는 걸 헤아려 주시고, 그를 모질게 판단하지 않으시리라는 걸, 가엾은 사람을, 저는 알아요!\" 이 말과 함께 눈을 들면서 그녀는 여태껏보다 더 가까이 그의 얼굴을 살피더니, 빠르게 어조를 바꾸어 말했다. \"어디 편찮으셨던 건 아니에요, 클레넘 씨?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘Nor tried?", "\"무슨 시련을 겪으신 것도?"], ["Nor hurt?’ she asked him, anxiously.", "다치신 것도 아니고요?\" 그녀가 걱정스레 물었다."]], [["It fell to Clennam now, to be not quite certain how to answer.", "이제 어떻게 대답해야 할지 썩 분명치 않게 된 쪽은 클레넘이었다."], ["He said in reply:", "그는 대답으로 말했다."]], [["‘To speak the truth, I have been a little troubled, but it is over.", "\"사실대로 말하자면, 조금 마음고생을 했답니다. 하지만 이젠 다 끝났어요."], ["Do I show it so plainly?", "내가 그렇게 빤히 드러나 보였나요?"], ["I ought to have more fortitude and self-command than that.", "나는 그보다 더한 꿋꿋함과 자제력을 지녀야 마땅한데."], ["I thought I had.", "그런 줄로 알았는데 말입니다."], ["I must learn them of you.", "당신에게서 그것들을 배워야겠군요."], ["Who could teach me better!’", "당신보다 더 잘 가르쳐 줄 사람이 어디 있겠어요!\""]], [["He never thought that she saw in him what no one else could see.", "그는 그녀가 그에게서 다른 누구도 보지 못하는 것을 본다는 사실을 전혀 생각하지 못했다."], ["He never thought that in the whole world there were no other eyes that looked upon him with the same light and strength as hers.", "온 세상에 그녀의 눈과 같은 빛과 힘으로 그를 바라보는 다른 눈은 없다는 것을 그는 전혀 생각하지 못했다."]], [["‘But it brings me to something that I wish to say,’ he continued, ‘and therefore I will not quarrel even with my own face for telling tales and being unfaithful to me.", "\"하지만 그 때문에 내가 하고 싶은 이야기로 들어서게 되었군요.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["Besides, it is a privilege and pleasure to confide in my Little Dorrit.", "\"그러니 비밀을 들키고 나를 배신했다고 해서 내 얼굴과 다투지는 않으렵니다."], ["Let me confess then, that, forgetting how grave I was, and how old I was, and how the time for such things had gone by me with the many years of sameness and little happiness that made up my long life far away, without marking it--that, forgetting all this, I fancied I loved some one.’", "게다가 내 리틀 도릿에게 속을 털어놓는 건 특권이자 즐거움이지요. 그럼 고백하건대, 내가 얼마나 진지하고, 얼마나 나이 들었으며, 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 그것을 의식조차 못 한 채 한결같음과 자잘한 행복으로 채워진 오랜 세월과 더불어 그런 일들의 때가 이미 나를 지나가 버렸는지를 잊은 채로—이 모든 걸 잊은 채로, 나는 누군가를 사랑한다고 착각했답니다.\""]], [["‘Do I know her, sir?’ asked Little Dorrit.", "\"제가 그분을 아나요, 선생님?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다."]], [["‘No, my child.’", "\"아니, 얘야.\""]], [["‘Not the lady who has been kind to me for your sake?’", "\"선생님 때문에 제게 친절히 대해 주신 그 부인은 아니고요?\""]], [["‘Flora.", "\"플로라요."], ["No, no.", "아니, 아니에요."], ["Do you think--’", "당신은 설마—\""]], [["‘I never quite thought so,’ said Little Dorrit, more to herself than him.", "\"그렇게 생각한 적은 한 번도 없었어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 그에게라기보다는 거의 혼잣말처럼 말했다."], ["‘I did wonder at it a little.’", "\"조금 의아하긴 했지만요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Clennam, abiding by the feeling that had fallen on him in the avenue on the night of the roses, the feeling that he was an older man, who had done with that tender part of life, ‘I found out my mistake, and I thought about it a little--in short, a good deal--and got wiser.", "\"자!\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Being wiser, I counted up my years and considered what I am, and looked back, and looked forward, and found that I should soon be grey.", "장미가 핀 그 밤에 가로수 길에서 자신에게 내려앉았던 그 느낌, 곧 자신은 인생의 그 다정한 부분과는 이미 끝낸 더 나이 든 사람이라는 그 느낌을 붙든 채로. \"나는 내 착각을 알아차렸고, 그것에 대해 조금—요컨대 꽤 많이—생각했으며, 그래서 더 현명해졌지요."], ["I found that I had climbed the hill, and passed the level ground upon the top, and was descending quickly.’", "더 현명해진 김에 내 나이를 헤아려 보고, 내가 어떤 사람인지 따져 보고, 뒤를 돌아보고 앞을 내다보았더니, 머지않아 백발이 되리라는 걸 알게 되었습니다. 나는 그 언덕을 다 올라, 꼭대기의 평평한 땅을 지나, 빠르게 내려가고 있다는 걸 알았어요.\""]], [["If he had known the sharpness of the pain he caused the patient heart, in speaking thus!", "이렇게 말하면서 그가 그 인내심 깊은 가슴에 얼마나 날카로운 아픔을 주고 있는지 알았더라면!"], ["While doing it, too, with the purpose of easing and serving her.", "더구나 그녀를 위로하고 도우려는 마음으로 그러고 있었으니."]], [["‘I found that the day when any such thing would have been graceful in me, or good in me, or hopeful or happy for me or any one in connection with me, was gone, and would never shine again.’", "\"그런 일이 내게 우아하거나, 내게 어울리거나, 나에게든 나와 얽힌 누구에게든 희망차거나 행복할 수 있었을 그런 날은 이미 가 버렸고, 다시는 빛나지 않으리라는 걸 알았습니다.\""]], [["O!", "오!"], ["If he had known, if he had known!", "그가 알았더라면, 그가 알았더라면!"], ["If he could have seen the dagger in his hand, and the cruel wounds it struck in the faithful bleeding breast of his Little Dorrit!", "자기 손에 쥔 단검을, 그리고 그것이 자신의 리틀 도릿의 충실하게 피 흘리는 가슴에 새기는 그 잔인한 상처들을 그가 볼 수 있었더라면!"]], [["‘All that is over, and I have turned my face from it.", "\"그 모든 게 다 끝났고, 나는 거기서 얼굴을 돌렸습니다."], ["Why do I speak of this to Little Dorrit?", "그런데 어째서 나는 이걸 리틀 도릿에게 이야기하고 있는 걸까요?"], ["Why do I show you, my child, the space of years that there is between us, and recall to you that I have passed, by the amount of your whole life, the time that is present to you?’", "어째서, 얘야, 우리 사이에 놓인 그 세월의 간격을 당신에게 보여 주고, 내가 당신 인생 전체만큼의 시간을, 당신에게는 지금 현재인 그 시간을, 이미 지나 버렸다는 걸 떠올리게 하는 걸까요?\""]], [["‘Because you trust me, I hope.", "\"저를 믿으시기 때문이겠죠, 그러길 바라요."], ["Because you know that nothing can touch you without touching me; that nothing can make you happy or unhappy, but it must make me, who am so grateful to you, the same.’", "저를 건드리지 않고는 선생님께 닿는 것이 아무것도 없다는 걸, 선생님께 그토록 감사하는 저를 똑같이 만들지 않고는 선생님을 행복하게도 불행하게도 할 수 있는 것이 아무것도 없다는 걸 아시기 때문이겠죠.\""]], [["He heard the thrill in her voice, he saw her earnest face, he saw her clear true eyes, he saw the quickened bosom that would have joyfully thrown itself before him to receive a mortal wound directed at his breast, with the dying cry, ‘I love him!’ and the remotest suspicion of the truth never dawned upon his mind.", "그는 그녀의 목소리에 어린 떨림을 들었고, 그녀의 진지한 얼굴을 보았으며, 그녀의 맑고 참된 눈을 보았고, \"나는 그를 사랑해!\"라는 죽어 가는 외침과 함께 그의 가슴을 겨눈 치명적인 일격을 받으려고 기꺼이 그 앞에 몸을 던졌을 그 빠르게 두근거리는 가슴을 보았다—그러나 그 진실에 대한 아주 희미한 의심조차 그의 마음에는 떠오르지 않았다. 그렇다."], ["No.", "그가 본 것은 닳아빠진 신발을 신고, 수수한 옷차림에, 감옥인 자기 집에 있는, 헌신적인 작은 존재였다."], ["He saw the devoted little creature with her worn shoes, in her common dress, in her jail-home; a slender child in body, a strong heroine in soul; and the light of her domestic story made all else dark to him.", "몸은 가냘픈 아이였으나 영혼은 강인한 여걸이었다. 그리고 그녀의 그 가정사의 빛이 그에게는 다른 모든 것을 어둡게 만들었다."]], [["‘For those reasons assuredly, Little Dorrit, but for another too.", "\"분명 그런 이유들 때문이지요, 리틀 도릿. 하지만 또 다른 이유 때문이기도 합니다."], ["So far removed, so different, and so much older, I am the better fitted for your friend and adviser.", "그토록 멀리 떨어져 있고, 그토록 다르며, 그토록 나이가 많으니, 나는 당신의 친구이자 조언자로 더 잘 어울려요."], ["I mean, I am the more easily to be trusted; and any little constraint that you might feel with another, may vanish before me.", "내 말은, 더 쉽게 믿을 수 있는 상대라는 거지요. 다른 사람한테라면 느낄 법한 자그마한 어색함도 내 앞에서는 사라질 수 있을 겁니다."], ["Why have you kept so retired from me?", "어째서 나에게서 그토록 멀찍이 물러나 있었던 거지요?"], ["Tell me.’", "말해 줘요.\""]], [["‘I am better here.", "\"저는 여기가 더 나아요."], ["My place and use are here.", "제 자리와 쓸모는 여기 있어요."], ["I am much better here,’ said Little Dorrit, faintly.", "저는 여기가 훨씬 나아요.\" 리틀 도릿이 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["‘So you said that day upon the bridge.", "\"그날 다리 위에서도 그렇게 말했지요."], ["I thought of it much afterwards.", "그 뒤로 그 말을 많이 생각했답니다."], ["Have you no secret you could entrust to me, with hope and comfort, if you would!’", "마음만 있다면, 희망과 위안을 가지고 나에게 맡길 수 있는 비밀이 하나도 없나요!\""]], [["‘Secret?", "\"비밀이요?"], ["No, I have no secret,’ said Little Dorrit in some trouble.", "아니에요, 저는 비밀이 없어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 곤란해하며 말했다."]], [["They had been speaking in low voices; more because it was natural to what they said to adopt that tone, than with any care to reserve it from Maggy at her work.", "그들은 낮은 목소리로 이야기하고 있었다. 일하고 있는 매기에게 감추려는 어떤 조심스러움 때문이라기보다는, 그들이 나누는 이야기에 그런 어조가 자연스러웠던 까닭이었다."], ["All of a sudden Maggy stared again, and this time spoke:", "그런데 별안간 매기가 다시 빤히 쳐다보더니, 이번에는 입을 열었다."]], [["‘I say!", "\"저기요!"], ["Little Mother!’", "작은 어머니!\""]], [["‘Yes, Maggy.’", "\"왜, 매기.\""]], [["‘If you an’t got no secret of your own to tell him, tell him that about the Princess.", "\"작은 어머니가 직접 그분께 들려줄 비밀이 없으면, 그 공주님 얘기를 들려드려요."], ["_She_ had a secret, you know.’", "그 공주님은 비밀이 있었잖아요, 그쵸.\""]], [["‘The Princess had a secret?’ said Clennam, in some surprise.", "\"공주님이 비밀이 있었다고요?\" 클레넘이 다소 놀라며 말했다."], ["‘What Princess was that, Maggy?’", "\"그게 무슨 공주냐, 매기?\""]], [["‘Lor!", "\"아유!"], ["How you do go and bother a gal of ten,’ said Maggy, ‘catching the poor thing up in that way.", "어쩜 열 살짜리 계집애를 그렇게 들들 볶으세요.\" 매기가 말했다."], ["Whoever said the Princess had a secret?", "\"가엾은 애를 그런 식으로 붙잡고서. 누가 공주님이 비밀이 있었다고 했어요?"], ["_I_ never said so.’", "나는 그런 말 한 적 없어요.\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon.", "\"미안하구나."], ["I thought you did.’", "네가 그런 줄 알았다.\""]], [["‘No, I didn’t.", "\"아뇨, 안 그랬어요."], ["How could I, when it was her as wanted to find it out?", "어떻게 그래요, 비밀을 찾아내고 싶어 한 게 바로 그 공주님인데?"], ["It was the little woman as had the secret, and she was always a spinning at her wheel.", "비밀을 가진 건 그 작은 여자였고, 그 여자는 늘 물레질을 하고 있었어요."], ["And so she says to her, why do you keep it there? And so the t’other one says to her, no I don’t; and so the t’other one says to her, yes you do; and then they both goes to the cupboard, and there it is.", "그래서 공주님이 그 여자한테, 왜 그걸 거기다 두느냐고 물었더니, 그 여자는, 아뇨 안 둬요, 했고, 공주님은, 아니 두잖아요, 했고, 그래서 둘이 다 같이 찬장으로 가니까, 거기 그게 있었어요."], ["And she wouldn’t go into the Hospital, and so she died.", "그리고 그 여자는 병원에 들어가려고 하지 않았고, 그래서 죽었어요."], ["_You_ know, Little Mother; tell him that.", "작은 어머니는 알잖아요, 그분께 그 얘기 해 드려요."], ["For it was a reg’lar good secret, that was!’ cried Maggy, hugging herself.", "그건 정말이지 진짜 멋진 비밀이었으니까요!\" 매기가 제 몸을 끌어안으며 외쳤다."]], [["Arthur looked at Little Dorrit for help to comprehend this, and was struck by seeing her so timid and red.", "아서는 이 말을 이해하는 데 도움을 받으려고 리틀 도릿을 바라보았는데, 그녀가 그토록 수줍어하며 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보고 흠칫했다."], ["But, when she told him that it was only a Fairy Tale she had one day made up for Maggy, and that there was nothing in it which she wouldn’t be ashamed to tell again to anybody else, even if she could remember it, he left the subject where it was.", "그러나 그것이 자기가 언젠가 매기에게 지어 준 동화일 뿐이고, 설령 기억해 낼 수 있다 하더라도 누구에게든 다시 들려주기 부끄럽지 않을 만한 내용은 하나도 없다고 그녀가 말하자, 그는 그 화제를 그대로 두었다."]], [["However, he returned to his own subject by first entreating her to see him oftener, and to remember that it was impossible to have a stronger interest in her welfare than he had, or to be more set upon promoting it than he was.", "그러나 그는 자기 화제로 돌아와, 우선 자기를 더 자주 만나 달라고, 그리고 자신만큼 그녀의 안녕에 더 깊은 관심을 가질 사람도, 자신만큼 그것을 도모하는 데 더 마음을 쏟는 사람도 없다는 걸 기억해 달라고 간청했다."], ["When she answered fervently, she well knew that, she never forgot it, he touched upon his second and more delicate point--the suspicion he had formed.", "그녀가 열렬히, 자기도 그걸 잘 알며 결코 잊지 않는다고 대답하자, 그는 두 번째이자 더 미묘한 점—그가 품게 된 짐작을 건드렸다."]], [["‘Little Dorrit,’ he said, taking her hand again, and speaking lower than he had spoken yet, so that even Maggy in the small room could not hear him, ‘another word.", "\"리틀 도릿.\" 그가 다시 그녀의 손을 잡고, 여태껏보다 더 낮은 목소리로, 좁은 방 안의 매기조차 듣지 못하도록 말했다."], ["I have wanted very much to say this to you; I have tried for opportunities.", "\"한마디만 더. 나는 이 말을 당신에게 무척이나 하고 싶었어요."], ["Don’t mind me, who, for the matter of years, might be your father or your uncle.", "기회를 노려 왔지요. 햇수로 따지면 당신의 아버지뻘이나 삼촌뻘일 수도 있는 나를 신경 쓰지 말아요."], ["Always think of me as quite an old man.", "나를 그저 늙은이로만 여겨 줘요."], ["I know that all your devotion centres in this room, and that nothing to the last will ever tempt you away from the duties you discharge here.", "나는 당신의 헌신이 모두 이 방에 모여 있다는 걸, 그리고 끝까지 어떤 것도 당신을 이곳에서 다하는 의무로부터 떼어 놓도록 유혹하지 못하리라는 걸 압니다."], ["If I were not sure of it, I should, before now, have implored you, and implored your father, to let me make some provision for you in a more suitable place.", "만약 그걸 확신하지 못했다면, 나는 진작에 당신에게도, 당신의 아버지에게도, 좀 더 어울리는 곳에서 당신을 위해 무언가 마련하게 해 달라고 애원했을 거예요."], ["But you may have an interest--I will not say, now, though even that might be--may have, at another time, an interest in some one else; an interest not incompatible with your affection here.’", "하지만 당신에게는 어떤 마음이 있을지도—지금이라고는 말하지 않겠어요, 비록 그것조차 있을 수 있겠지만—언젠가, 다른 누군가에 대한 어떤 마음이 있을지도 모르지요. 이곳을 향한 당신의 애정과 양립하지 못할 게 없는 그런 마음 말입니다.\""]], [["She was very, very pale, and silently shook her head.", "그녀는 몹시, 몹시 창백해진 채 말없이 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘It may be, dear Little Dorrit.’", "\"그럴 수도 있어요, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿.\""]], [["‘No. No.", "\"아니에요."], ["No.’ She shook her head, after each slow repetition of the word, with an air of quiet desolation that he remembered long afterwards.", "아니에요. 아니에요.\""], ["The time came when he remembered it well, long afterwards, within those prison walls; within that very room.", "그 말을 천천히 한 번씩 되풀이할 때마다 그녀는 고개를 저었는데, 그가 오랜 뒤까지 기억하게 될 그 조용한 황량함의 기색이 어려 있었다. 그가 그것을 또렷이 기억하게 되는 때가 왔으니, 오랜 세월이 지난 뒤 저 감옥의 담장 안, 바로 그 방 안에서였다."]], [["‘But, if it ever should be, tell me so, my dear child.", "\"하지만 혹시라도 언젠가 그렇게 된다면, 내게 말해 줘요, 사랑하는 얘야."], ["Entrust the truth to me, point out the object of such an interest to me, and I will try with all the zeal, and honour, and friendship and respect that I feel for you, good Little Dorrit of my heart, to do you a lasting service.’", "그 진실을 내게 맡기고, 그런 마음의 대상을 내게 가리켜 줘요. 그러면 내가 당신에게 느끼는 온갖 열의와 명예와 우정과 존경을 다해, 내 마음속 착한 리틀 도릿, 당신에게 변치 않을 도움을 베풀려고 애써 보겠어요.\""]], [["‘O thank you, thank you!", "\"오, 고맙습니다, 고맙습니다!"], ["But, O no, O no, O no!’ She said this, looking at him with her work-worn hands folded together, and in the same resigned accents as before.", "하지만, 오 아니에요, 오 아니에요, 오 아니에요!\" 그녀는 일에 닳은 두 손을 맞잡은 채 그를 바라보며, 앞서와 똑같이 체념한 어조로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘I press for no confidence now.", "\"지금 당장 털어놓으라고 채근하는 게 아니에요."], ["I only ask you to repose unhesitating trust in me.’", "나는 다만 망설임 없이 나를 믿어 달라고 부탁하는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Can I do less than that, when you are so good!’", "\"선생님이 그토록 좋으신데, 제가 그만한 일도 못 하겠어요!\""]], [["‘Then you will trust me fully?", "\"그럼 나를 온전히 믿어 줄 거지요?"], ["Will have no secret unhappiness, or anxiety, concealed from me?’", "어떤 남모를 슬픔이나 근심도 내게 숨기지 않을 거지요?\""]], [["‘Almost none.’", "\"거의 없을 거예요.\""]], [["‘And you have none now?’", "\"그리고 지금은 하나도 없고요?\""]], [["She shook her head.", "그녀는 고개를 저었다."], ["But she was very pale.", "그러나 몹시 창백했다."]], [["‘When I lie down to-night, and my thoughts come back--as they will, for they do every night, even when I have not seen you--to this sad place, I may believe that there is no grief beyond this room, now, and its usual occupants, which preys on Little Dorrit’s mind?’", "\"오늘 밤 자리에 누워, 내 생각이 다시—그러겠지요, 당신을 보지 못한 날에도 매일 밤 그러니까—이 슬픈 곳으로 돌아올 때, 지금 이 방과 늘 이곳에 사는 사람들 너머에는 리틀 도릿의 마음을 갉아먹는 어떤 슬픔도 없다고 믿어도 될까요?\""]], [["She seemed to catch at these words--that he remembered, too, long afterwards--and said, more brightly, ‘Yes, Mr Clennam; yes, you may!’", "그녀는 이 말을 붙잡으려는 듯했고—그것 또한 그가 오랜 뒤까지 기억했다—한결 밝아진 목소리로 말했다. \"네, 클레넘 씨. 네, 그러셔도 돼요!\""]], [["The crazy staircase, usually not slow to give notice when any one was coming up or down, here creaked under a quick tread, and a further sound was heard upon it, as if a little steam-engine with more steam than it knew what to do with, were working towards the room.", "평소에는 누가 오르내릴 때 알려 주기를 게을리하지 않던 그 삐걱대는 계단이, 이번에는 빠른 발걸음 아래에서 삐걱거렸고, 그 위에서 또 다른 소리가 들려왔으니, 마치 주체 못 할 만큼 김이 가득 찬 작은 증기 기관 하나가 방을 향해 일하며 다가오는 듯했다."], ["As it approached, which it did very rapidly, it laboured with increased energy; and, after knocking at the door, it sounded as if it were stooping down and snorting in at the keyhole.", "그것이 다가오면서—아주 빠르게 다가왔다—한층 거센 기세로 힘을 쓰더니, 문을 두드린 뒤에는 마치 몸을 숙이고 열쇠 구멍에 대고 콧김을 뿜는 것 같은 소리가 났다."]], [["Before Maggy could open the door, Mr Pancks, opening it from without, stood without a hat and with his bare head in the wildest condition, looking at Clennam and Little Dorrit, over her shoulder.", "매기가 문을 열기도 전에, 팽크스 씨가 바깥에서 문을 열고는, 모자도 없이 맨머리를 더없이 산발한 채로 서서, 리틀 도릿의 어깨 너머로 그녀와 클레넘을 바라보았다."], ["He had a lighted cigar in his hand, and brought with him airs of ale and tobacco smoke.", "손에는 불붙은 시가가 들려 있었고, 그는 맥주와 담배 연기 냄새를 풍기며 들어섰다."]], [["‘Pancks the gipsy,’ he observed out of breath, ‘fortune-telling.’", "\"점쟁이 팽크스올시다.\" 그가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"운수를 봐 드리지요.\""]], [["He stood dingily smiling, and breathing hard at them, with a most curious air; as if, instead of being his proprietor’s grubber, he were the triumphant proprietor of the Marshalsea, the Marshal, all the turnkeys, and all the Collegians.", "그는 거뭇하게 미소 지으며 그들을 향해 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬었는데, 더없이 묘한 분위기였다. 마치 자기가 고용주의 돈벌이꾼이 아니라, 마셜시와 간수장과 모든 옥졸과 모든 재학생을 거느린 의기양양한 주인이라도 되는 것 같았다."], ["In his great self-satisfaction he put his cigar to his lips (being evidently no smoker), and took such a pull at it, with his right eye shut up tight for the purpose, that he underwent a convulsion of shuddering and choking.", "그는 한껏 흡족해진 나머지 시가를 입에 물고는(분명 담배를 피우는 사람이 아니었다), 그러느라 오른쪽 눈을 질끈 감은 채 어찌나 깊이 빨아들였던지, 부들부들 떨고 캑캑거리는 발작을 겪었다."], ["But even in the midst of that paroxysm, he still essayed to repeat his favourite introduction of himself, ‘Pa-ancks the gi-ipsy, fortune-telling.’", "그러나 그 발작이 한창인 와중에도 그는 자기를 소개하는 그 애용하는 인사말을 또 한 번 되뇌려 했다. \"저엄쟁이 팽크스올시다, 운수를 봐 드리지요.\""]], [["‘I am spending the evening with the rest of ‘em,’ said Pancks.", "\"다들이랑 같이 저녁 시간을 보내고 있었답니다.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I’ve been singing.", "\"노래를 불렀지요."], ["I’ve been taking a part in White sand and grey sand.", "'흰 모래 회색 모래'에서 한 파트를 맡았어요."], ["_I_ don’t know anything about it.", "나야 그런 거 아무것도 몰라요."], ["Never mind.", "상관없어요."], ["I’ll take any part in anything.", "뭐든 어느 파트든 맡지요."], ["It’s all the same, if you’re loud enough.’", "다 똑같아요, 목소리만 크면.\""]], [["At first Clennam supposed him to be intoxicated.", "처음에 클레넘은 그가 취한 줄 알았다."], ["But he soon perceived that though he might be a little the worse (or better) for ale, the staple of his excitement was not brewed from malt, or distilled from any grain or berry.", "그러나 곧 그가 비록 맥주 탓에 조금 나빠졌(거나 좋아졌)을지는 몰라도, 그 흥분의 주성분은 엿기름으로 빚거나 어떤 곡물이나 열매로 증류한 것이 아님을 알아차렸다."]], [["‘How d’ye do, Miss Dorrit?’ said Pancks.", "\"안녕하세요, 도릿 양?\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I thought you wouldn’t mind my running round, and looking in for a moment.", "\"제가 잠깐 달려와 들여다봐도 신경 안 쓰실 줄 알았어요."], ["Mr Clennam I heard was here, from Mr Dorrit.", "클레넘 씨가 여기 계시다는 건 도릿 씨한테 들었지요."], ["How are you, Sir?’", "안녕하십니까, 선생님?\""]], [["Clennam thanked him, and said he was glad to see him so gay.", "클레넘은 고맙다고 하며, 그가 그토록 들떠 있는 걸 보니 기쁘다고 말했다."]], [["‘Gay!’ said Pancks.", "\"들떠요!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I’m in wonderful feather, sir.", "\"기분이 끝내주게 좋답니다, 선생님."], ["I can’t stop a minute, or I shall be missed, and I don’t want ‘em to miss me.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "일 분도 못 머물러요, 안 그러면 자리를 비운 게 들통날 텐데, 그러긴 싫거든요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["He seemed to have an insatiate delight in appealing to her and looking at her; excitedly sticking his hair up at the same moment, like a dark species of cockatoo.", "그는 그녀에게 말을 걸고 그녀를 바라보는 데 만족할 줄 모르는 기쁨을 느끼는 듯했다. 동시에 흥분해서 머리카락을 곤두세웠는데, 마치 거뭇한 종류의 앵무새 같았다."]], [["‘I haven’t been here half an hour.", "\"여기 온 지 반 시간도 안 됐어요."], ["I knew Mr Dorrit was in the chair, and I said, “I’ll go and support him!”", "도릿 씨가 의장석에 앉아 계신 걸 알고, '내가 가서 떠받쳐 드려야지!' 했지요."], ["I ought to be down in Bleeding Heart Yard by rights; but I can worry them to-morrow.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "사실 따지면 블리딩 하트 야드에 내려가 있어야 마땅하지만, 그 사람들이야 내일 들들 볶으면 되니까요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["His little black eyes sparkled electrically.", "그의 작은 검은 눈이 전기를 띤 듯 반짝였다."], ["His very hair seemed to sparkle as he roughened it.", "머리카락을 헝클어뜨리니 그 머리카락마저 반짝이는 것 같았다."], ["He was in that highly-charged state that one might have expected to draw sparks and snaps from him by presenting a knuckle to any part of his figure.", "그는 어찌나 충전된 상태였던지, 그의 몸 어느 부위에든 손가락 마디를 갖다 대면 불꽃과 탁탁 소리가 튀어나올 법했다."]], [["‘Capital company here,’ said Pancks.--‘Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "\"여기 사람들 정말 멋지네요.\" 팽크스가 말했다.—\"그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["She was half afraid of him, and irresolute what to say.", "그녀는 그가 반쯤 무서워 무어라 말해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["He laughed, with a nod towards Clennam.", "그는 클레넘을 향해 고갯짓하며 웃었다."]], [["‘Don’t mind him, Miss Dorrit. He’s one of us.", "\"저 사람 신경 쓰지 마세요, 도릿 양."], ["We agreed that you shouldn’t take on to mind me before people, but we didn’t mean Mr Clennam.", "저분도 우리 편이니까요. 사람들 앞에서는 나를 아는 척하지 않기로 약속했지만, 클레넘 씨는 예외였지요."], ["He’s one of us.", "저분도 우리 편이에요."], ["He’s in it.", "한패예요."], ["An’t you, Mr Clennam?--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "안 그렇습니까, 클레넘 씨?—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["The excitement of this strange creature was fast communicating itself to Clennam.", "이 기이한 인물의 흥분이 빠르게 클레넘에게 옮아가고 있었다."], ["Little Dorrit with amazement, saw this, and observed that they exchanged quick looks.", "리틀 도릿은 놀라움 속에 이것을 보았고, 두 사람이 재빠른 눈짓을 주고받는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["‘I was making a remark,’ said Pancks, ‘but I declare I forget what it was.", "\"내가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는데,\""], ["Oh, I know!", "팽크스가 말했다."], ["Capital company here.", "\"정말이지 뭐였는지 까먹었네. 아, 알겠다!"], ["I’ve been treating ‘em all round.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "여기 사람들 정말 멋지다고요. 다들한테 한턱 쏘고 있었거든요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["‘Very generous of you,’ she returned, noticing another of the quick looks between the two.", "\"정말 후하시네요.\" 그녀가 대꾸하며, 두 사람 사이에 오가는 또 한 번의 재빠른 눈짓을 알아챘다."]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Pancks.", "\"천만에요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t mention it.", "\"그런 말씀 마세요."], ["I’m coming into my property, that’s the fact.", "사실 내가 재산을 물려받게 됐거든요."], ["I can afford to be liberal.", "그러니 한턱 낼 형편이 되지요."], ["I think I’ll give ‘em a treat here.", "여기서 사람들한테 잔치를 한번 베풀까 봐요."], ["Tables laid in the yard.", "마당에 식탁을 차리고."], ["Bread in stacks.", "빵을 산더미로."], ["Pipes in faggots.", "파이프를 다발로."], ["Tobacco in hayloads.", "담배를 수레 가득."], ["Roast beef and plum-pudding for every one.", "누구에게나 구운 소고기와 자두 푸딩."], ["Quart of double stout a head.", "한 사람당 진한 흑맥주 한 쿼트씩."], ["Pint of wine too, if they like it, and the authorities give permission.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "좋다면 포도주도 한 파인트씩, 당국이 허가만 한다면요.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["She was thrown into such a confusion by his manner, or rather by Clennam’s growing understanding of his manner (for she looked to him after every fresh appeal and cockatoo demonstration on the part of Mr Pancks), that she only moved her lips in answer, without forming any word.", "그의 태도에, 아니 그보다도 그 태도를 점점 알아채 가는 클레넘 때문에(그녀는 팽크스 씨가 또 한 번 새로 말을 걸고 앵무새 같은 시늉을 할 때마다 그를 바라보았으므로) 그녀는 어찌나 혼란에 빠졌던지, 대답이랍시고 입술만 움직일 뿐 아무 말도 이루지 못했다."]], [["‘And oh, by-the-bye!’ said Pancks, ‘you were to live to know what was behind us on that little hand of yours.", "\"아, 그러고 보니!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["And so you shall, you shall, my darling.--Eh, Miss Dorrit?’", "\"당신은 그 작은 손 위에 우리 뒤에 무엇이 있었는지를 살아서 알게 되리라 했지요. 그러니 알게 될 거예요, 알게 되고말고요, 내 사랑.—그렇죠, 도릿 양?\""]], [["He had suddenly checked himself.", "그는 갑자기 자제했다."], ["Where he got all the additional black prongs from, that now flew up all over his head like the myriads of points that break out in the large change of a great firework, was a wonderful mystery.", "큰 불꽃놀이의 큰 변화에서 무수한 점들이 터져 나오듯, 이제 그의 머리 온통 사방으로 솟구쳐 오른 그 추가된 검은 갈래들을 어디서 다 가져왔는지는 놀라운 수수께끼였다."]], [["‘But I shall be missed;’ he came back to that; ‘and I don’t want ‘em to miss me.", "\"하지만 자리를 비운 게 들통나겠어요.\" 그는 다시 그 말로 돌아갔다."], ["Mr Clennam, you and I made a bargain.", "\"그러긴 싫거든요."], ["I said you should find me stick to it.", "클레넘 씨, 우리 둘이 거래를 했지요."], ["You shall find me stick to it now, sir, if you’ll step out of the room a moment.", "내가 약속을 지키는 사람이란 걸 알게 되실 거라고 했고요. 이제 그걸 보여 드리지요, 선생님, 잠깐 방 밖으로 나와 주신다면요."], ["Miss Dorrit, I wish you good night.", "도릿 양, 안녕히 주무세요."], ["Miss Dorrit, I wish you good fortune.’", "도릿 양, 행운을 빕니다.\""]], [["He rapidly shook her by both hands, and puffed down stairs.", "그는 그녀의 두 손을 빠르게 흔들고는 콧김을 뿜으며 계단을 내려갔다."], ["Arthur followed him with such a hurried step, that he had very nearly tumbled over him on the last landing, and rolled him down into the yard.", "아서가 어찌나 급한 걸음으로 그를 뒤따랐던지, 마지막 층계참에서 하마터면 그를 넘어뜨려 마당으로 굴려 떨어뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["‘What is it, for Heaven’s sake!’ Arthur demanded, when they burst out there both together.", "\"대체 무슨 일이오, 제발!\" 두 사람이 함께 마당으로 뛰쳐나오자 아서가 다그쳐 물었다."]], [["‘Stop a moment, sir.", "\"잠깐만요, 선생님."], ["Mr Rugg.", "러그 씨예요."], ["Let me introduce him.’", "소개해 드리지요.\""]], [["With those words he presented another man without a hat, and also with a cigar, and also surrounded with a halo of ale and tobacco smoke, which man, though not so excited as himself, was in a state which would have been akin to lunacy but for its fading into sober method when compared with the rampancy of Mr Pancks.", "그 말과 함께 그는 역시 모자도 없고, 역시 시가를 든 데다, 역시 맥주와 담배 연기의 후광에 둘러싸인 또 한 사람을 내보였다. 그 사람은 팽크스 씨만큼 흥분하지는 않았으나, 팽크스 씨의 광란에 견주어 보면 차분한 절도로 옅어졌기에 망정이지 그러지 않았다면 거의 실성한 것과 다름없을 상태였다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, Mr Rugg,’ said Pancks.", "\"클레넘 씨, 이쪽은 러그 씨예요.\""], ["‘Stop a moment.", "팽크스가 말했다."], ["Come to the pump.’", "\"잠깐만요. 펌프로 갑시다.\""]], [["They adjourned to the pump.", "그들은 펌프로 자리를 옮겼다."], ["Mr Pancks, instantly putting his head under the spout, requested Mr Rugg to take a good strong turn at the handle.", "팽크스 씨는 곧장 주둥이 아래에 머리를 들이밀고는, 러그 씨에게 손잡이를 힘껏 한 번 세게 돌려 달라고 청했다."], ["Mr Rugg complying to the letter, Mr Pancks came forth snorting and blowing to some purpose, and dried himself on his handkerchief.", "러그 씨가 글자 그대로 따라 주자, 팽크스 씨는 콧김을 뿜고 푸푸거리며 시원하게 나와서는 손수건으로 몸을 닦았다."]], [["‘I am the clearer for that,’ he gasped to Clennam standing astonished.", "\"덕분에 좀 개운해졌습니다.\" 그가 놀라서 서 있는 클레넘에게 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["‘But upon my soul, to hear her father making speeches in that chair, knowing what we know, and to see her up in that room in that dress, knowing what we know, is enough to--give me a back, Mr Rugg--a little higher, sir,--that’ll do!’", "\"하지만 정말이지, 우리가 아는 걸 알면서, 그녀의 아버지가 저 의장석에서 연설하는 걸 듣고 있자니, 우리가 아는 걸 알면서, 그녀가 저 방에서 저런 옷차림으로 있는 걸 보고 있자니, 그건 그만—등 좀 내어 주세요, 러그 씨—조금만 더 높게요, 선생님,—됐어요!\""]], [["Then and there, on that Marshalsea pavement, in the shades of evening, did Mr Pancks, of all mankind, fly over the head and shoulders of Mr Rugg of Pentonville, General Agent, Accountant, and Recoverer of Debts.", "그러고는 바로 그때 그 자리에서, 마셜시의 그 포석 위에서, 저물어 가는 저녁 어스름 속에서, 하고많은 사람 중에 하필 팽크스 씨가, 펜턴빌의 종합 대리인이자 회계사이자 채권 추심인인 러그 씨의 머리와 어깨 위를 훌쩍 뛰어넘었다."], ["Alighting on his feet, he took Clennam by the button-hole, led him behind the pump, and pantingly produced from his pocket a bundle of papers.", "두 발로 내려선 그는 클레넘의 단춧구멍을 붙잡고 펌프 뒤로 데려가더니, 헐떡이며 주머니에서 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다."]], [["Mr Rugg, also, pantingly produced from his pocket a bundle of papers.", "러그 씨도 헐떡이며 주머니에서 서류 한 묶음을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘Stay!’ said Clennam in a whisper.’You have made a discovery.’", "\"잠깐!\" 클레넘이 속삭이며 말했다. \"당신, 무언가를 알아냈군요.\""]], [["Mr Pancks answered, with an unction which there is no language to convey, ‘We rather think so.’", "팽크스 씨는 말로는 도무지 전할 수 없는 감격에 젖어 대답했다. \"아무래도 그런 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Does it implicate any one?’", "\"누군가가 연루됩니까?\""]], [["‘How implicate, sir?’", "\"연루라니, 무슨 말씀이세요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘In any suppression or wrong dealing of any kind?’", "\"어떤 은폐나 부정한 거래 같은 것에 말이오.\""]], [["‘Not a bit of it.’", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["‘Thank God!’ said Clennam to himself.", "\"고맙기도 하셔라!\" 클레넘이 속으로 말했다."], ["‘Now show me.’", "\"자, 보여 줘요.\""]], [["‘You are to understand’--snorted Pancks, feverishly unfolding papers, and speaking in short high-pressure blasts of sentences, ‘Where’s the Pedigree?", "\"이걸 아셔야 합니다.\" 팽크스가 콧김을 뿜으며 열에 들떠 서류를 펼치고, 짧게 고압으로 터져 나오는 문장들로 말했다."], ["Where’s Schedule number four, Mr Rugg?", "\"혈통서가 어디 있지? 명세표 제4호는 어디 있어요, 러그 씨?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["all right!", "됐다!"], ["Here we are.--You are to understand that we are this very day virtually complete.", "여기 있군.—이걸 아셔야 해요, 우리가 바로 오늘로 사실상 다 마쳤다는 걸 말입니다."], ["We shan’t be legally for a day or two.", "법적으로야 하루 이틀은 더 걸리겠지만요."], ["Call it at the outside a week.", "길게 잡아도 일주일이라고 해 두지요."], ["We’ve been at it night and day for I don’t know how long.", "우리는 얼마나 됐는지도 모를 만큼 밤낮으로 이 일에 매달렸어요."], ["Mr Rugg, you know how long?", "러그 씨, 얼마나 됐는지 아시죠?"], ["Never mind.", "아니, 됐어요."], ["Don’t say.", "말하지 마세요."], ["You’ll only confuse me.", "나만 헷갈리게 할 테니까."], ["You shall tell her, Mr Clennam.", "그녀에게는 당신이 알려 주시는 겁니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["Not till we give you leave.", "우리가 허락하기 전엔 안 돼요."], ["Where’s that rough total, Mr Rugg?", "그 대략적인 합계가 어디 있지요, 러그 씨?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["Here we are!", "여기 있군!"], ["There sir!", "여기요, 선생님!"], ["That’s what you’ll have to break to her.", "이게 당신이 그녀에게 깨우쳐 줘야 할 내용입니다."], ["That man’s your Father of the Marshalsea!’", "저 사람이 당신의 마셜시 아버지요!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint", "# CHAPTER 33. Mrs Merdle’s Complaint"]], [["Resigning herself to inevitable fate by making the best of those people, the Miggleses, and submitting her philosophy to the draught upon it, of which she had foreseen the likelihood in her interview with Arthur, Mrs Gowan handsomely resolved not to oppose her son’s marriage.", "필연적인 운명에 순응하여 미글스 일가, 즉 그 사람들을 최대한 좋게 받아들이고, 아서와의 면담에서 그럴 가능성을 미리 내다보았던바 자신의 철학에 가해질 부담에 머리를 숙이며, 가우언 부인은 아들의 결혼에 반대하지 않기로 너그러이 결심했다."], ["In her progress to, and happy arrival at, this resolution, she was possibly influenced, not only by her maternal affections but by three politic considerations.", "이 결심에 이르고 또 흡족하게 다다르는 과정에서, 그녀는 아마도 모성애뿐 아니라 세 가지 타산적 고려에도 영향을 받았을 것이다."]], [["Of these, the first may have been that her son had never signified the smallest intention to ask her consent, or any mistrust of his ability to dispense with it; the second, that the pension bestowed upon her by a grateful country (and a Barnacle) would be freed from any little filial inroads, when her Henry should be married to the darling only child of a man in very easy circumstances; the third, that Henry’s debts must clearly be paid down upon the altar-railing by his father-in-law.", "그중 첫째는, 아들이 한 번도 그녀의 동의를 구할 의향을 조금도 내비친 적이 없었고, 동의 없이도 해낼 자기 능력에 대해 어떠한 불신도 보인 적이 없었다는 점이었을 테고, 둘째는, 감사할 줄 아는 조국(그리고 어느 배너클)이 그녀에게 베푼 연금이, 자기 헨리가 형편이 매우 넉넉한 사람의 외동 귀염둥이 딸과 결혼하게 되면 자식이 그리로 조금씩 손을 뻗는 일에서 풀려나리라는 점이었을 테고, 셋째는, 헨리의 빚이 명백히 장인 될 사람에 의해 제단 난간 앞에서 곧장 청산되리라는 점이었을 것이다."], ["When, to these three-fold points of prudence there is added the fact that Mrs Gowan yielded her consent the moment she knew of Mr Meagles having yielded his, and that Mr Meagles’s objection to the marriage had been the sole obstacle in its way all along, it becomes the height of probability that the relict of the deceased Commissioner of nothing particular, turned these ideas in her sagacious mind.", "이 세 갈래 신중함의 요점에다, 가우언 부인이 미글스 씨가 동의했다는 사실을 안 바로 그 순간에 자기 동의를 내주었다는 점과, 미글스 씨의 반대가 처음부터 줄곧 이 결혼의 유일한 장애였다는 점까지 더해 놓고 보면, 별다른 것도 아닌 어느 작고한 위원의 미망인이 그 명민한 머릿속에서 이런 생각들을 굴렸다는 것은 더없이 그럴듯한 일이 된다."]], [["Among her connections and acquaintances, however, she maintained her individual dignity and the dignity of the blood of the Barnacles, by diligently nursing the pretence that it was a most unfortunate business; that she was sadly cut up by it; that this was a perfect fascination under which Henry laboured; that she had opposed it for a long time, but what could a mother do; and the like.", "그러나 친지와 지인들 사이에서 그녀는, 이것이 무척이나 불운한 일이며, 그 일로 자신이 몹시 상심하고 있으며, 헨리가 빠져 있는 것은 완벽한 마법 같은 홀림이며, 자신이 오랫동안 그것을 반대해 왔으나 어미가 무엇을 할 수 있겠느냐는 둥의 가식을 부지런히 키워 가며 자신의 개인적 위엄과 배너클 가문 혈통의 위엄을 지켰다."], ["She had already called Arthur Clennam to bear witness to this fable, as a friend of the Meagles family; and she followed up the move by now impounding the family itself for the same purpose.", "그녀는 이미 미글스 가의 친구로서 아서 클레넘을 불러다 이 꾸민 이야기의 증인으로 세운 바 있었고, 이번에는 같은 목적으로 그 가족 자체를 옭아매는 수순으로 이어 갔다."], ["In the first interview she accorded to Mr Meagles, she slided herself into the position of disconsolately but gracefully yielding to irresistible pressure.", "미글스 씨에게 허락한 첫 면담에서, 그녀는 거역할 수 없는 압력에 비통하지만 우아하게 굴복하는 자리로 슬그머니 자신을 밀어 넣었다."], ["With the utmost politeness and good-breeding, she feigned that it was she--not he--who had made the difficulty, and who at length gave way; and that the sacrifice was hers--not his.", "극도의 정중함과 좋은 가정교육으로, 그녀는 어려움을 만든 사람이 그가 아니라 자기였고, 결국 양보한 것도 자기였으며, 희생은 그의 것이 아니라 자기의 것이라는 양 꾸며 보였다. 같은 가장을, 같은 정중한 솜씨로, 그녀는 미글스 부인에게도 떠넘겼는데, 마치 마술사가 그 순진한 부인에게 카드를 강요하듯 했다."], ["The same feint, with the same polite dexterity, she foisted on Mrs Meagles, as a conjuror might have forced a card on that innocent lady; and, when her future daughter-in-law was presented to her by her son, she said on embracing her, ‘My dear, what have you done to Henry that has bewitched him so!’ at the same time allowing a few tears to carry before them, in little pills, the cosmetic powder on her nose; as a delicate but touching signal that she suffered much inwardly for the show of composure with which she bore her misfortune.", "그리고 장차 며느리가 될 사람이 아들의 손에 이끌려 그녀 앞에 소개되자, 그녀는 그녀를 끌어안으며 \"얘야, 도대체 헨리에게 무슨 짓을 했길래 그 애를 이토록 홀려 놨니!\" 하고 말했고, 동시에 몇 방울의 눈물이 작은 알약처럼 그녀의 코에 발린 화장 분을 밀어내려 흐르도록 두었으니, 이는 자신이 그 불운을 견디며 보이는 침착함의 이면에서 속으로 얼마나 괴로워하고 있는지를 알리는 섬세하나 가슴 저린 신호였다."]], [["Among the friends of Mrs Gowan (who piqued herself at once on being Society, and on maintaining intimate and easy relations with that Power), Mrs Merdle occupied a front row.", "가우언 부인의 친구들(그녀는 자신이 곧 사교계라는 점과, 그 권력과 친밀하고 격의 없는 관계를 유지한다는 점을 동시에 자랑으로 삼았다) 가운데, 머들 부인은 맨 앞줄을 차지했다."], ["True, the Hampton Court Bohemians, without exception, turned up their noses at Merdle as an upstart; but they turned them down again, by falling flat on their faces to worship his wealth.", "사실 햄프턴 코트의 보헤미안들은 예외 없이 머들을 벼락출세한 자라며 코를 치켜들었지만, 그의 부를 숭배하느라 납작 엎드림으로써 다시 그 코를 내렸다."], ["In which compensating adjustment of their noses, they were pretty much like Treasury, Bar, and Bishop, and all the rest of them.", "이렇게 코를 올렸다 내렸다 보정하는 점에서, 그들은 재무부며 법조계며 주교며 그 밖의 모든 자들과 꽤나 비슷했다."]], [["To Mrs Merdle, Mrs Gowan repaired on a visit of self-condolence, after having given the gracious consent aforesaid.", "앞서 말한 자비로운 동의를 내준 뒤, 가우언 부인은 자기 위로의 방문 길에 머들 부인을 찾아갔다."], ["She drove into town for the purpose in a one-horse carriage irreverently called at that period of English history, a pill-box.", "그녀는 이 목적을 위해, 영국 역사의 그 시기에 불경스럽게도 알약통이라 불리던 외마차를 타고 도시로 들어갔다."], ["It belonged to a job-master in a small way, who drove it himself, and who jobbed it by the day, or hour, to most of the old ladies in Hampton Court Palace; but it was a point of ceremony, in that encampment, that the whole equipage should be tacitly regarded as the private property of the jobber for the time being, and that the job-master should betray personal knowledge of nobody but the jobber in possession.", "그것은 영세한 마차 대여업자의 소유였는데, 그가 직접 몰았고, 햄프턴 코트 궁의 노부인들 대다수에게 하루 단위나 시간 단위로 빌려주었다. 다만 그 거처에서는 그 마차 한 벌 전체를, 그때그때 빌린 사람의 개인 소유물로 암묵적으로 여기고, 마차 대여업자는 현재 빌린 사람 외에는 아무도 개인적으로 아는 척하지 않는다는 것이 하나의 예법이었다."], ["So the Circumlocution Barnacles, who were the largest job-masters in the universe, always pretended to know of no other job but the job immediately in hand.", "그러니 우주에서 가장 큰 마차 대여업자라 할 에두름국의 배너클 일가도, 늘 당장 손에 쥔 일거리 외에는 어떤 일거리도 모르는 척하곤 했다."]], [["Mrs Merdle was at home, and was in her nest of crimson and gold, with the parrot on a neighbouring stem watching her with his head on one side, as if he took her for another splendid parrot of a larger species.", "머들 부인은 집에 있었고, 진홍과 금빛으로 꾸민 자신의 둥지에 들어 있었으며, 곁의 횃대에 앉은 앵무새가 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채, 마치 그녀를 더 큰 종류의 또 다른 화려한 앵무새로 여기기라도 하듯 그녀를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["To whom entered Mrs Gowan, with her favourite green fan, which softened the light on the spots of bloom.", "그곳으로 가우언 부인이, 발그레한 반점들 위에 떨어지는 빛을 부드럽게 누그러뜨리는 좋아하는 초록 부채를 들고 들어섰다."]], [["‘My dear soul,’ said Mrs Gowan, tapping the back of her friend’s hand with this fan after a little indifferent conversation, ‘you are my only comfort.", "\"여보, 내 소중한 이여,\" 가우언 부인은 시시한 잡담을 조금 나눈 뒤 이 부채로 친구의 손등을 톡톡 두드리며 말했다."], ["That affair of Henry’s that I told you of, is to take place.", "\"당신은 내 유일한 위안이에요. 내가 말했던 헨리의 그 일이 결국 성사될 모양이에요."], ["Now, how does it strike you?", "자, 당신은 어떻게 생각하나요?"], ["I am dying to know, because you represent and express Society so well.’", "나는 정말 죽도록 알고 싶어요. 당신만큼 사교계를 잘 대표하고 표현하는 사람이 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mrs Merdle reviewed the bosom which Society was accustomed to review; and having ascertained that show-window of Mr Merdle’s and the London jewellers’ to be in good order, replied:", "머들 부인은 사교계가 늘 살펴보곤 하던 그 가슴께를 점검하고는, 머들 씨와 런던 보석상들의 그 진열창이 잘 정돈되어 있음을 확인한 뒤 대답했다."]], [["‘As to marriage on the part of a man, my dear, Society requires that he should retrieve his fortunes by marriage.", "\"남자 쪽의 결혼이라면 말이에요, 여보, 사교계는 그가 결혼으로 재산을 만회하길 요구하지요."], ["Society requires that he should gain by marriage.", "사교계는 그가 결혼으로 득을 보길 요구해요."], ["Society requires that he should found a handsome establishment by marriage.", "사교계는 그가 결혼으로 번듯한 살림을 차리길 요구하고요."], ["Society does not see, otherwise, what he has to do with marriage.", "그렇지 않다면 사교계는 그가 결혼과 무슨 상관이 있는지 알 수가 없답니다."], ["Bird, be quiet!’", "새야, 조용히 해!\""]], [["For the parrot on his cage above them, presiding over the conference as if he were a judge (and indeed he looked rather like one), had wound up the exposition with a shriek.", "머리 위 새장의 앵무새가, 마치 자기가 판사라도 되는 양(실제로 꽤 판사처럼 보이기도 했다) 이 회담을 주재하다가, 그 설명을 한 번의 비명으로 마무리 지었기 때문이었다."]], [["‘Cases there are,’ said Mrs Merdle, delicately crooking the little finger of her favourite hand, and making her remarks neater by that neat action; ‘cases there are where a man is not young or elegant, and is rich, and has a handsome establishment already.", "\"경우가 있긴 해요,\" 머들 부인은 좋아하는 손의 새끼손가락을 우아하게 구부려, 그 깔끔한 동작으로 자기 말을 한층 깔끔하게 다듬으며 말했다."], ["Those are of a different kind.", "\"남자가 젊지도 우아하지도 않으나 부유하고 이미 번듯한 살림을 갖춘 경우가 있지요. 그건 다른 종류예요."], ["In such cases--’", "그런 경우엔—\""]], [["Mrs Merdle shrugged her snowy shoulders and put her hand upon the jewel-stand, checking a little cough, as though to add, ‘why, a man looks out for this sort of thing, my dear.’ Then the parrot shrieked again, and she put up her glass to look at him, and said, ‘Bird! Do be quiet!’", "머들 부인은 눈처럼 흰 어깨를 으쓱하고는 보석 받침대 위에 손을 얹으며, 작은 기침을 누르는 시늉을 했는데, 마치 \"그야, 남자가 그런 부류를 노리는 거지요, 여보\" 하고 덧붙이려는 듯했다. 그러자 앵무새가 또 비명을 질렀고, 그녀는 안경을 들어 새를 바라보며 말했다. \"새야! 좀 조용히 해!\""]], [["‘But, young men,’ resumed Mrs Merdle, ‘and by young men you know what I mean, my love--I mean people’s sons who have the world before them--they must place themselves in a better position towards Society by marriage, or Society really will not have any patience with their making fools of themselves. Dreadfully worldly all this sounds,’ said Mrs Merdle, leaning back in her nest and putting up her glass again, ‘does it not?’", "\"하지만 젊은 남자들은,\" 머들 부인이 다시 말을 이었다. \"여보, 젊은 남자라 하면 내가 무슨 뜻인지 알 거예요—나는 앞날이 창창한 남의 집 아들들을 말하는 거예요—그들은 결혼으로 사교계에서 더 나은 위치에 자신을 두어야 해요, 그러지 않으면 사교계는 그들이 바보짓 하는 걸 정말이지 참아 주지 않을 거예요. 이게 다 끔찍이도 속물스럽게 들리지요,\" 머들 부인은 자기 둥지에 등을 기대며 다시 안경을 들어 올리고 말했다. \"그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["‘But it is true,’ said Mrs Gowan, with a highly moral air.", "\"하지만 사실이잖아요,\" 가우언 부인이 무척 도덕적인 태도로 말했다."]], [["‘My dear, it is not to be disputed for a moment,’ returned Mrs Merdle; ‘because Society has made up its mind on the subject, and there is nothing more to be said.", "\"여보, 한순간도 다툴 여지가 없는 얘기죠,\" 머들 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["If we were in a more primitive state, if we lived under roofs of leaves, and kept cows and sheep and creatures instead of banker’s accounts (which would be delicious; my dear, I am pastoral to a degree, by nature), well and good.", "\"사교계가 그 문제에 대해 마음을 정해 버렸으니, 더 할 말이 없는 거예요. 우리가 좀 더 원시적인 상태에 있다면, 우리가 나뭇잎 지붕 아래 살면서 은행 계좌 대신 소며 양이며 짐승을 친다면(그건 참 근사할 거예요."], ["But we don’t live under leaves, and keep cows and sheep and creatures.", "여보, 나는 천성이 어지간히 목가적인 사람이거든요), 그러면 좋고 좋겠지요."], ["I perfectly exhaust myself sometimes, in pointing out the distinction to Edmund Sparkler.’", "하지만 우리는 나뭇잎 아래 살지도, 소며 양이며 짐승을 치지도 않잖아요. 나는 가끔 에드먼드 스파클러에게 그 구별을 일깨워 주느라 완전히 진을 빼곤 한답니다.\""]], [["Mrs Gowan, looking over her green fan when this young gentleman’s name was mentioned, replied as follows:", "이 젊은 신사의 이름이 언급되자, 가우언 부인은 초록 부채 너머로 바라보며 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["‘My love, you know the wretched state of the country--those unfortunate concessions of John Barnacle’s!--and you therefore know the reasons for my being as poor as Thingummy.’", "\"여보, 당신도 이 나라의 비참한 상태를 알잖아요—존 배너클의 그 불행한 양보들 말이에요!—그러니 내가 어쩌고저쩌고만큼이나 가난한 이유도 알 테고요.\""]], [["‘A church mouse?’ Mrs Merdle suggested with a smile.", "\"교회 생쥐만큼?\" 머들 부인이 미소 지으며 거들었다."]], [["‘I was thinking of the other proverbial church person--Job,’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"나는 속담에 나오는 또 다른 교회 인물, 욥을 떠올리고 있었어요,\""], ["‘Either will do.", "가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["It would be idle to disguise, consequently, that there is a wide difference between the position of your son and mine.", "\"어느 쪽이든 좋아요. 따라서 당신 아들의 처지와 내 아들의 처지 사이에 큰 차이가 있다는 걸 숨겨 봐야 부질없지요."], ["I may add, too, that Henry has talent--’", "게다가 덧붙이자면, 헨리에겐 재능이 있어요—\""]], [["‘Which Edmund certainly has not,’ said Mrs Merdle, with the greatest suavity.", "\"에드먼드에겐 분명코 없는 그것 말이지요,\" 머들 부인이 더없이 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘--and that his talent, combined with disappointment,’ Mrs Gowan went on, ‘has led him into a pursuit which--ah dear me!", "\"—그리고 그 재능이 좌절과 맞물려,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["You know, my dear.", "\"그 애를 어떤 추구로 이끌었는데, 그게—아아, 저런!"], ["Such being Henry’s different position, the question is what is the most inferior class of marriage to which I can reconcile myself.’", "당신도 알잖아요, 여보. 헨리의 처지가 이렇게 다르니, 문제는 내가 받아들일 수 있는 가장 격이 낮은 결혼이 무엇이냐 하는 거예요.\""]], [["Mrs Merdle was so much engaged with the contemplation of her arms (beautiful-formed arms, and the very thing for bracelets), that she omitted to reply for a while.", "머들 부인은 자기 팔(아름답게 빚어진 팔로, 팔찌에 딱 어울리는 그런 팔이었다)을 가만히 들여다보는 데 몰두한 나머지, 한동안 대답하기를 잊었다."], ["Roused at length by the silence, she folded the arms, and with admirable presence of mind looked her friend full in the face, and said interrogatively, ‘Ye-es?", "마침내 그 침묵에 정신이 든 그녀는, 그 팔을 모아 끼고는 감탄스러운 침착함으로 친구의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 묻듯이 말했다. \"그—래요?"], ["And then?’", "그래서요?\""]], [["‘And then, my dear,’ said Mrs Gowan not quite so sweetly as before, ‘I should be glad to hear what you have to say to it.’", "\"그래서요, 여보,\" 가우언 부인이 아까만큼 살갑지는 않은 투로 말했다. \"당신이 그것에 대해 무슨 말을 해 줄지 듣고 싶어요.\""]], [["Here the parrot, who had been standing on one leg since he screamed last, burst into a fit of laughter, bobbed himself derisively up and down on both legs, and finished by standing on one leg again, and pausing for a reply, with his head as much awry as he could possibly twist it.", "이때 마지막으로 비명을 지른 뒤 한쪽 다리로 서 있던 앵무새가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨리더니, 두 다리로 비웃듯 위아래로 까닥거리다가, 결국 다시 한쪽 다리로 서서, 머리를 비틀 수 있는 한 최대로 갸웃한 채 대답을 기다리며 멈췄다."]], [["‘Sounds mercenary to ask what the gentleman is to get with the lady,’ said Mrs Merdle; ‘but Society is perhaps a little mercenary, you know, my dear.’", "\"신사가 그 숙녀와 함께 무엇을 얻게 되느냐고 묻는 게 돈만 밝히는 소리처럼 들리긴 하네요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"하지만 사교계가 아마 돈을 좀 밝히긴 하잖아요, 여보, 알다시피.\""]], [["‘From what I can make out,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘I believe I may say that Henry will be relieved from debt--’", "\"내가 알아낼 수 있는 바로는,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"헨리가 빚에서 풀려나리라고 말해도 될 것 같아요—\""]], [["‘Much in debt?’ asked Mrs Merdle through her eyeglass.", "\"빚이 많아요?\" 머들 부인이 안경 너머로 물었다."]], [["‘Why tolerably, I should think,’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"글쎄, 어지간하긴 할 거예요,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Meaning the usual thing; I understand; just so,’ Mrs Merdle observed in a comfortable sort of way.", "\"흔한 정도란 뜻이군요. 알겠어요. 바로 그거죠,\" 머들 부인이 편안한 투로 말했다."]], [["‘And that the father will make them an allowance of three hundred a-year, or perhaps altogether something more, which, in Italy-’", "\"그리고 그 애 아버지가 그들에게 연 삼백 파운드, 어쩌면 다 합쳐 그보다 좀 더 되는 생활비를 대 주리라는 거예요. 그게 이탈리아에서라면—\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["Going to Italy?’ said Mrs Merdle.", "이탈리아에 가요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘For Henry to study.", "\"헨리가 공부를 하러요."], ["You need be at no loss to guess why, my dear.", "왜인지는 굳이 짐작하느라 애쓸 것 없어요, 여보."], ["That dreadful Art--’", "그 끔찍한 예술 말이에요—\""]], [["True.", "과연 그랬다."], ["Mrs Merdle hastened to spare the feelings of her afflicted friend.", "머들 부인은 상심한 친구의 감정을 아껴 주려고 서둘렀다. 알겠어요."], ["She understood. Say no more!", "더 말하지 말아요!"]], [["‘And that,’ said Mrs Gowan, shaking her despondent head, ‘that’s all.", "\"그리고,\" 가우언 부인이 낙담한 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["That,’ repeated Mrs Gowan, furling her green fan for the moment, and tapping her chin with it (it was on the way to being a double chin; might be called a chin and a half at present), ‘that’s all!", "\"그게 다예요. 그게,\""], ["On the death of the old people, I suppose there will be more to come; but how it may be restricted or locked up, I don’t know.", "가우언 부인이 잠시 초록 부채를 접어 그것으로 턱을 톡톡 치며 거듭 말했다(그 턱은 이중 턱이 되어 가는 중이었으니, 지금은 한 턱 반쯤이라 할 만했다). \"그게 다예요!"], ["And as to that, they may live for ever.", "노인들이 죽고 나면, 더 나올 것이 있겠지요. 하지만 그게 어떻게 제한되거나 묶여 있을지는 모르겠어요."], ["My dear, they are just the kind of people to do it.’", "게다가 그 점으로 말하면, 그들은 영영 살지도 몰라요. 여보, 그들은 딱 그렇게 할 부류의 사람들이거든요.\""]], [["Now, Mrs Merdle, who really knew her friend Society pretty well, and who knew what Society’s mothers were, and what Society’s daughters were, and what Society’s matrimonial market was, and how prices ruled in it, and what scheming and counter-scheming took place for the high buyers, and what bargaining and huckstering went on, thought in the depths of her capacious bosom that this was a sufficiently good catch.", "이제 친구인 사교계를 정말로 꽤나 잘 알고, 사교계의 어머니들이 어떤지, 사교계의 딸들이 어떤지, 사교계의 혼인 시장이 어떤지, 그 안에서 시세가 어떻게 매겨지는지, 큰손 매수자들을 두고 어떤 책략과 맞책략이 펼쳐지는지, 어떤 흥정과 행상질이 오가는지를 두루 아는 머들 부인은, 그 넉넉한 가슴 깊은 곳에서 이만하면 꽤 괜찮은 건수라고 생각했다."], ["Knowing, however, what was expected of her, and perceiving the exact nature of the fiction to be nursed, she took it delicately in her arms, and put her required contribution of gloss upon it.", "그러나 자신에게 기대되는 바가 무엇인지 알고, 키워 줘야 할 허구의 정확한 성질을 알아차린 그녀는, 그것을 섬세하게 두 팔에 안아, 자기 몫으로 요구되는 윤기를 덧칠해 주었다."]], [["‘And that is all, my dear?’ said she, heaving a friendly sigh.", "\"그래서 그게 다라는 거죠, 여보?\" 그녀가 다정한 한숨을 내쉬며 말했다."], ["‘Well, well!", "\"저런, 저런!"], ["The fault is not yours.", "잘못은 당신에게 없어요."], ["You have nothing to reproach yourself with.", "당신이 자책할 일은 하나도 없고요."], ["You must exercise the strength of mind for which you are renowned, and make the best of it.’", "당신은 그 유명한 굳센 마음을 발휘해서, 최선을 다해 받아들여야 해요.\""]], [["‘The girl’s family have made,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘of course, the most strenuous endeavours to--as the lawyers say--to have and to hold Henry.’", "\"그 처녀의 가족은,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"물론 헨리를—법률가들 말마따나—'차지하고 붙들어 두려고' 더없이 안간힘을 썼지요.\""]], [["‘Of course they have, my dear,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"물론 그랬겠죠, 여보,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘I have persisted in every possible objection, and have worried myself morning, noon, and night, for means to detach Henry from the connection.’", "\"나는 가능한 모든 반대를 고집했고, 헨리를 그 인연에서 떼어 놓을 방도를 찾느라 아침이고 한낮이고 밤이고 애를 태웠어요.\""]], [["‘No doubt you have, my dear,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"틀림없이 그러셨겠죠, 여보,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘And all of no use.", "\"그런데 다 소용없었어요."], ["All has broken down beneath me.", "모든 게 내 밑에서 무너져 버렸지요."], ["Now tell me, my love.", "자, 말해 줘요, 여보."], ["Am I justified in at last yielding my most reluctant consent to Henry’s marrying among people not in Society; or, have I acted with inexcusable weakness?’", "결국 헨리가 사교계에 속하지 않은 사람들 사이에서 결혼하는 데 더없이 마지못한 동의를 내준 것이, 내가 정당화될 일인가요? 아니면 내가 변명할 수 없는 나약함으로 행동한 건가요?\""]], [["In answer to this direct appeal, Mrs Merdle assured Mrs Gowan (speaking as a Priestess of Society) that she was highly to be commended, that she was much to be sympathised with, that she had taken the highest of parts, and had come out of the furnace refined.", "이 직접적인 호소에 답하여, 머들 부인은(사교계의 여사제로서 말하건대) 가우언 부인을 향해, 그녀는 크게 칭찬받아 마땅하며, 깊이 동정받아 마땅하며, 가장 고귀한 역할을 맡았으며, 시련의 용광로에서 한층 정련되어 나왔노라고 단언했다."], ["And Mrs Gowan, who of course saw through her own threadbare blind perfectly, and who knew that Mrs Merdle saw through it perfectly, and who knew that Society would see through it perfectly, came out of this form, notwithstanding, as she had gone into it, with immense complacency and gravity.", "그리고 물론 자기 자신의 닳아빠진 눈가림을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보고, 머들 부인도 그것을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 본다는 것을 알며, 사교계도 그것을 완벽하게 꿰뚫어 보리라는 것을 아는 가우언 부인은, 그럼에도 불구하고, 들어갈 때와 마찬가지로, 엄청난 만족감과 진중함을 안고 이 의식에서 걸어 나왔다."]], [["The conference was held at four or five o’clock in the afternoon, when all the region of Harley Street, Cavendish Square, was resonant of carriage-wheels and double-knocks.", "이 회담은 오후 네다섯 시쯤에 열렸는데, 그 무렵 캐번디시 광장의 할리 가 일대는 온통 마차 바퀴 소리와 두 번 두드리는 노크 소리로 울려 퍼졌다."], ["It had reached this point when Mr Merdle came home from his daily occupation of causing the British name to be more and more respected in all parts of the civilised globe capable of the appreciation of world-wide commercial enterprise and gigantic combinations of skill and capital.", "머들 씨가 매일의 업무, 즉 세계적인 상업 진취성과 기술과 자본의 거대한 결합을 감상할 줄 아는 문명 세계의 방방곡곡에서 영국이라는 이름을 점점 더 떠받들게 만드는 일을 마치고 집에 돌아온 것이 바로 그 시점이었다."], ["For, though nobody knew with the least precision what Mr Merdle’s business was, except that it was to coin money, these were the terms in which everybody defined it on all ceremonious occasions, and which it was the last new polite reading of the parable of the camel and the needle’s eye to accept without inquiry.", "머들 씨의 사업이 돈을 찍어 내는 것이라는 점 말고는, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지 조금이라도 아는 사람이 아무도 없었지만, 모든 격식 차린 자리에서 누구나 그의 일을 이런 식으로 정의했고, 낙타와 바늘귀의 비유에 대한 가장 최근의 점잖은 풀이는 그것을 따져 묻지 않고 받아들이는 것이었다."]], [["For a gentleman who had this splendid work cut out for him, Mr Merdle looked a little common, and rather as if, in the course of his vast transactions, he had accidentally made an interchange of heads with some inferior spirit.", "이처럼 화려한 일거리가 맡겨진 신사치고, 머들 씨는 조금 평범해 보였고, 오히려 그 막대한 거래를 진행하는 와중에 어쩌다 어느 열등한 영혼과 머리를 맞바꾸기라도 한 사람 같았다."], ["He presented himself before the two ladies in the course of a dismal stroll through his mansion, which had no apparent object but escape from the presence of the chief butler.", "그는 자기 저택을 음울하게 거닐던 중에 두 부인 앞에 나타났는데, 그 거닒이라는 것에는 집사장의 면전을 피하는 것 말고는 별다른 목적이 없어 보였다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon,’ he said, stopping short in confusion; ‘I didn’t know there was anybody here but the parrot.’", "\"실례했습니다,\" 그가 당황하여 우뚝 멈춰 서며 말했다. \"여기 앵무새 말고 다른 사람이 있는 줄 몰랐어요.\""]], [["However, as Mrs Merdle said, ‘You can come in!’ and as Mrs Gowan said she was just going, and had already risen to take her leave, he came in, and stood looking out at a distant window, with his hands crossed under his uneasy coat-cuffs, clasping his wrists as if he were taking himself into custody.", "그러나 머들 부인이 \"들어와도 돼요!\" 하고 말하고, 가우언 부인이 막 가려던 참이라며 이미 일어나 작별 인사를 하려 했으므로, 그는 들어와서 멀찍이 떨어진 창문 밖을 내다보며 섰는데, 불편한 외투 소맷부리 아래로 두 손을 엇갈려 손목을 움켜쥔 것이 마치 자기 자신을 구금하기라도 하는 듯했다."], ["In this attitude he fell directly into a reverie from which he was only aroused by his wife’s calling to him from her ottoman, when they had been for some quarter of an hour alone.", "이런 자세로 그는 곧장 깊은 상념에 빠졌고, 둘만 십오 분쯤 남게 되자 오토만에 앉은 아내가 그를 부르고 나서야 비로소 거기서 깨어났다."]], [["‘Eh?", "\"응?"], ["Yes?’ said Mr Merdle, turning towards her.", "뭐라고?\" 머들 씨가 그녀 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘What is it?’", "\"무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘What is it?’ repeated Mrs Merdle.", "\"무슨 일이냐고요?\" 머들 부인이 되받았다."], ["‘It is, I suppose, that you have not heard a word of my complaint.’", "\"내가 보기엔, 당신이 내 불평을 한 마디도 듣지 못했다는 게 무슨 일이지요.\""]], [["‘Your complaint, Mrs Merdle?’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"당신 불평이라니, 머들 부인?\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I didn’t know that you were suffering from a complaint.", "\"당신이 무슨 병으로 고생하는 줄은 몰랐는데."], ["What complaint?’", "무슨 병이오?\""]], [["‘A complaint of you,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"당신에 대한 불평이에요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["A complaint of me,’ said Mr Merdle.", "나에 대한 불평이라,\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What is the--what have I--what may you have to complain of in me, Mrs Merdle?’", "\"도대체 무엇이—내가 무엇을—나한테 무엇을 불평할 게 있다는 거요, 머들 부인?\""]], [["In his withdrawing, abstracted, pondering way, it took him some time to shape this question.", "물러서고 멍하고 곱씹는 그 특유의 태도로, 그가 이 질문을 형태로 빚어내는 데에는 어느 정도 시간이 걸렸다."], ["As a kind of faint attempt to convince himself that he was the master of the house, he concluded by presenting his forefinger to the parrot, who expressed his opinion on that subject by instantly driving his bill into it.", "자기가 이 집의 주인임을 스스로 납득시켜 보려는 일종의 희미한 시도로, 그는 결국 앵무새에게 집게손가락을 내밀었는데, 앵무새는 그 화제에 관한 제 의견을, 즉각 그 손가락에 부리를 박아 넣음으로써 표명했다."]], [["‘You were saying, Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, with his wounded finger in his mouth, ‘that you had a complaint against me?’", "\"방금 말하길, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 상처 난 손가락을 입에 문 채 말했다. \"나한테 불평이 있다고 했지요?\""]], [["‘A complaint which I could scarcely show the justice of more emphatically, than by having to repeat it,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"되풀이해 말해야만 한다는 사실보다 더 절실하게 그 정당함을 보여 줄 수 있는 불평은 없을 거예요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."], ["‘I might as well have stated it to the wall.", "\"차라리 벽에다 대고 말하는 편이 나았겠어요."], ["I had far better have stated it to the bird.", "새한테 말하는 게 훨씬 나았을 거예요."], ["He would at least have screamed.’", "적어도 새는 비명이라도 질렀을 테니까.\""]], [["‘You don’t want me to scream, Mrs Merdle, I suppose,’ said Mr Merdle, taking a chair.", "\"내가 비명을 지르길 바라는 건 아니겠지요, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 의자를 끌어다 앉으며 말했다."]], [["‘Indeed I don’t know,’ retorted Mrs Merdle, ‘but that you had better do that, than be so moody and distraught.", "\"글쎄, 정말 모르겠네요,\" 머들 부인이 쏘아붙였다."], ["One would at least know that you were sensible of what was going on around you.’", "\"그렇게 침울하고 정신 나간 듯이 있느니, 차라리 그러는 편이 낫지 않을까 싶어요. 적어도 당신이 주변에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지는 알고 있다는 걸 사람들이 알 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["‘A man might scream, and yet not be that, Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, heavily.", "\"사람이 비명을 지르고도 그런 상태일 수 있지 않소, 머들 부인,\" 머들 씨가 무겁게 말했다."]], [["‘And might be dogged, as you are at present, without screaming,’ returned Mrs Merdle.", "\"그리고 지금 당신처럼 비명 한번 안 지르고도 음울하게 굴 수 있고요,\""], ["‘That’s very true.", "머들 부인이 되받았다."], ["If you wish to know the complaint I make against you, it is, in so many plain words, that you really ought not to go into Society unless you can accommodate yourself to Society.’", "\"그건 정말 맞는 말이에요. 내가 당신에게 하는 불평이 무엇인지 알고 싶다면, 아주 평범한 몇 마디로 말해서, 당신이 사교계에 자신을 맞추지 못할 거면 정말이지 사교계에 나가지 말아야 한다는 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Merdle, so twisting his hands into what hair he had upon his head that he seemed to lift himself up by it as he started out of his chair, cried:", "머들 씨는 머리에 남은 머리카락에 두 손을 비비 꼬아 넣어, 마치 그것으로 자기 몸을 들어 올리듯이 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Why, in the name of all the infernal powers, Mrs Merdle, who does more for Society than I do?", "\"아니, 온갖 지옥의 권능을 두고 말하건대, 머들 부인, 나보다 사교계를 위해 더 많은 일을 하는 사람이 어디 있소?"], ["Do you see these premises, Mrs Merdle?", "이 집을 보시오, 머들 부인?"], ["Do you see this furniture, Mrs Merdle?", "이 가구들을 보시오, 머들 부인?"], ["Do you look in the glass and see yourself, Mrs Merdle?", "거울을 들여다보고 당신 자신을 보시오, 머들 부인?"], ["Do you know the cost of all this, and who it’s all provided for?", "이 모든 것에 든 비용을, 또 이게 다 누구를 위해 마련된 것인지를 당신이 안단 말이오?"], ["And yet will you tell me that I oughtn’t to go into Society?", "그런데도 내가 사교계에 나가선 안 된다고 말하겠소?"], ["I, who shower money upon it in this way?", "이런 식으로 사교계에 돈을 쏟아붓는 나한테?"], ["I, who might always be said--to--to--to harness myself to a watering-cart full of money, and go about saturating Society every day of my life.’", "늘 말할 수 있을 법한 나한테—저—저—돈을 가득 실은 살수차에 나 자신을 매고서, 평생 날마다 돌아다니며 사교계를 흠뻑 적셔 주고 있다고 말이오.\""]], [["‘Pray, don’t be violent, Mr Merdle,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"제발 흥분하지 말아요, 머들 씨,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Violent?’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"흥분이라고?\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You are enough to make me desperate.", "\"당신은 사람을 절망하게 만드는구려."], ["You don’t know half of what I do to accommodate Society.", "내가 사교계에 맞추려고 하는 일의 절반도 당신은 모르오."], ["You don’t know anything of the sacrifices I make for it.’", "내가 그것을 위해 치르는 희생에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고.\""]], [["‘I know,’ returned Mrs Merdle, ‘that you receive the best in the land.", "\"내가 아는 건,\" 머들 부인이 되받았다."], ["I know that you move in the whole Society of the country.", "\"당신이 이 나라 최고의 사람들을 맞이한다는 거예요. 당신이 이 나라 사교계 전체 안에서 움직인다는 것도 알고요."], ["And I believe I know (indeed, not to make any ridiculous pretence about it, I know I know) who sustains you in it, Mr Merdle.’", "그리고 내가 알기로는(아니, 그것에 대해 우스꽝스러운 가식을 부리지 않고 말하자면, 나는 내가 안다는 걸 알아요), 그 안에서 당신을 떠받치는 게 누구인지도 알지요, 머들 씨.\""]], [["‘Mrs Merdle,’ retorted that gentleman, wiping his dull red and yellow face, ‘I know that as well as you do.", "\"머들 부인,\" 그 신사는 칙칙한 적황색 얼굴을 닦으며 되받았다."], ["If you were not an ornament to Society, and if I was not a benefactor to Society, you and I would never have come together.", "\"그건 나도 당신만큼 잘 알고 있소. 당신이 사교계의 장식품이 아니었고, 내가 사교계의 후원자가 아니었다면, 당신과 나는 결코 맺어지지 않았을 거요."], ["When I say a benefactor to it, I mean a person who provides it with all sorts of expensive things to eat and drink and look at.", "후원자라 함은, 사교계에 먹고 마시고 구경할 온갖 값비싼 것들을 대 주는 사람을 두고 하는 말이오. 그런데, 내가 그것을 위해 그토록 많은 일을 하고도—그토록 많은 일을 하고도,\""], ["But, to tell me that I am not fit for it after all I have done for it--after all I have done for it,’ repeated Mr Merdle, with a wild emphasis that made his wife lift up her eyelids, ‘after all--all!--to tell me I have no right to mix with it after all, is a pretty reward.’", "머들 씨는 아내가 눈꺼풀을 치켜뜨게 만들 만큼 격한 어조로 거듭 말했다. \"그 모든 것을 하고도—전부 다!—그러고도 내가 그것과 어울릴 자격이 없다고 말하다니, 참 대단한 보답이로구려.\""]], [["‘I say,’ answered Mrs Merdle composedly, ‘that you ought to make yourself fit for it by being more degage, and less preoccupied.", "\"내 말은,\" 머들 부인이 차분하게 대답했다."], ["There is a positive vulgarity in carrying your business affairs about with you as you do.’", "\"당신이 좀 더 느긋하고 덜 골몰함으로써 거기에 어울리게 처신해야 한다는 거예요. 당신처럼 사업 일거리를 그렇게 끼고 다니는 데에는 확실히 천박한 데가 있어요.\""]], [["‘How do I carry them about, Mrs Merdle?’ asked Mr Merdle.", "\"내가 그걸 어떻게 끼고 다닌다는 거요, 머들 부인?\" 머들 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘How do you carry them about?’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"그걸 어떻게 끼고 다니느냐고요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Look at yourself in the glass.’", "\"거울에 비친 당신 자신을 보세요.\""]], [["Mr Merdle involuntarily turned his eyes in the direction of the nearest mirror, and asked, with a slow determination of his turbid blood to his temples, whether a man was to be called to account for his digestion?", "머들 씨는 저도 모르게 가장 가까운 거울 쪽으로 눈을 돌렸고, 탁한 피가 천천히 관자놀이로 몰려드는 채로, 사람이 제 소화 작용을 두고 책임 추궁을 당해야 하는 거냐고 물었다."]], [["‘You have a physician,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"당신에겐 의사가 있잖아요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘He does me no good,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"그자는 나한테 아무 도움이 안 되오,\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mrs Merdle changed her ground.", "머들 부인은 발판을 바꾸었다."]], [["‘Besides,’ said she, ‘your digestion is nonsense.", "\"게다가,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["I don’t speak of your digestion.", "\"당신 소화 작용은 말도 안 되는 소리고요. 나는 당신 소화 작용을 말하는 게 아니에요."], ["I speak of your manner.’", "나는 당신 태도를 말하는 거예요.\""]], [["‘Mrs Merdle,’ returned her husband, ‘I look to you for that.", "\"머들 부인,\" 남편이 되받았다."], ["You supply manner, and I supply money.’", "\"그건 당신한테 기대하는 바요. 태도는 당신이 대고, 돈은 내가 대는 거지.\""]], [["‘I don’t expect you,’ said Mrs Merdle, reposing easily among her cushions, ‘to captivate people.", "\"나는 당신이,\" 머들 부인이 방석 사이에 편안히 기대며 말했다."], ["I don’t want you to take any trouble upon yourself, or to try to be fascinating.", "\"사람들을 매혹하기를 바라지 않아요. 당신이 수고를 떠맡거나 매력적으로 보이려 애쓰기를 바라지도 않고요."], ["I simply request you to care about nothing--or seem to care about nothing--as everybody else does.’", "나는 그저, 다른 모두가 그러듯이, 당신도 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 말기를—아니, 아무것에도 마음 쓰지 않는 것처럼 보이기를 청할 뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Do I ever say I care about anything?’ asked Mr Merdle.", "\"내가 무언가에 마음 쓴다고 말한 적이라도 있소?\" 머들 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘Say? No!", "\"말이라뇨?"], ["Nobody would attend to you if you did.", "천만에요! 당신이 그런 말을 한들 아무도 귀 기울이지 않을 거예요."], ["But you show it.’", "하지만 당신은 그걸 드러내잖아요.\""]], [["‘Show what?", "\"무얼 드러내?"], ["What do I show?’ demanded Mr Merdle hurriedly.", "내가 무얼 드러낸다는 거요?\" 머들 씨가 다급히 캐물었다."]], [["‘I have already told you.", "\"이미 말했잖아요."], ["You show that you carry your business cares an projects about, instead of leaving them in the City, or wherever else they belong to,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "당신은 사업 걱정거리며 계획들을, 그것들이 속한 시티나 그 어디에든 놔두지 않고 끼고 다니는 걸 드러내요,\""], ["‘Or seeming to.", "머들 부인이 말했다."], ["Seeming would be quite enough: I ask no more.", "\"아니면 적어도 그래 보이기라도 하든가요. 그래 보이는 것만으로도 충분해요."], ["Whereas you couldn’t be more occupied with your day’s calculations and combinations than you habitually show yourself to be, if you were a carpenter.’", "나는 그 이상은 바라지도 않아요. 그런데 당신은, 설령 당신이 목수였다 한들 그날의 계산과 궁리에 그보다 더 골몰해 보이지는 못했을 만큼, 늘 그렇게 골몰한 모습을 드러내잖아요.\""]], [["‘A carpenter!’ repeated Mr Merdle, checking something like a groan.", "\"목수라!\" 머들 씨가 신음 비슷한 것을 억누르며 되뇌었다."], ["‘I shouldn’t so much mind being a carpenter, Mrs Merdle.’", "\"목수가 되는 거라면 나는 그리 싫지 않을 것 같소, 머들 부인.\""]], [["‘And my complaint is,’ pursued the lady, disregarding the low remark, ‘that it is not the tone of Society, and that you ought to correct it, Mr Merdle.", "\"그리고 내 불평은,\" 부인이 그 낮은 중얼거림을 무시한 채 말을 이었다."], ["If you have any doubt of my judgment, ask even Edmund Sparkler.’ The door of the room had opened, and Mrs Merdle now surveyed the head of her son through her glass.", "\"그게 사교계의 격조가 아니라는 거고, 당신이 그걸 고쳐야 한다는 거예요, 머들 씨. 내 판단이 못 미덥거든, 에드먼드 스파클러한테라도 물어보세요.\""], ["‘Edmund; we want you here.’", "방문이 열려 있었고, 머들 부인은 이제 안경 너머로 아들의 머리를 살폈다. \"에드먼드, 여기 좀 와 줄래.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler, who had merely put in his head and looked round the room without entering (as if he were searching the house for that young lady with no nonsense about her), upon this followed up his head with his body, and stood before them.", "스파클러 씨는 안에 들어오지는 않고 그저 머리만 들이밀어 방 안을 둘러보고 있었는데(마치 잘난 척이라곤 없는 그 젊은 아가씨를 찾아 온 집안을 뒤지기라도 하듯이), 이 말에 머리 뒤로 몸을 들이밀어 그들 앞에 섰다."], ["To whom, in a few easy words adapted to his capacity, Mrs Merdle stated the question at issue.", "머들 부인은 그의 이해력에 맞춘 쉬운 몇 마디로 그에게 쟁점을 설명했다."]], [["The young gentleman, after anxiously feeling his shirt-collar as if it were his pulse and he were hypochondriacal, observed, ‘That he had heard it noticed by fellers.’", "그 젊은 신사는, 마치 셔츠 깃이 제 맥박이고 자기가 건강 염려증 환자이기라도 한 양 그 깃을 근심스레 짚어 보더니, 이렇게 말했다. \"친구들이 그런 얘길 하는 걸 들은 적이 있어요.\""]], [["‘Edmund Sparkler has heard it noticed,’ said Mrs Merdle, with languid triumph.", "\"에드먼드 스파클러가 그런 얘길 들은 적이 있대요,\" 머들 부인이 나른한 승리감으로 말했다."], ["‘Why, no doubt everybody has heard it noticed!’ Which in truth was no unreasonable inference; seeing that Mr Sparkler would probably be the last person, in any assemblage of the human species, to receive an impression from anything that passed in his presence.", "\"그것 봐요, 분명 누구나 그런 얘길 들었을 거예요!\" 사실 이는 그리 무리한 추론도 아니었으니, 스파클러 씨야말로 인간이라는 종이 모인 어떤 자리에서든, 자기 면전에서 벌어진 일로부터 무언가 인상을 받는 일이 가장 늦을 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["‘And Edmund Sparkler will tell you, I dare say,’ said Mrs Merdle, waving her favourite hand towards her husband, ‘how he has heard it noticed.’", "\"그리고 에드먼드 스파클러가, 그걸 어떻게 들었는지 당신한테 말해 줄 거예요, 아마,\" 머들 부인이 좋아하는 손을 남편 쪽으로 흔들며 말했다."]], [["‘I couldn’t,’ said Mr Sparkler, after feeling his pulse as before, ‘couldn’t undertake to say what led to it--‘cause memory desperate loose.", "\"제가,\" 스파클러 씨가 아까처럼 맥을 짚어 보고 나서 말했다."], ["But being in company with the brother of a doosed fine gal--well educated too--with no biggodd nonsense about her--at the period alluded to--’", "\"무엇 때문에 그 얘기가 나왔는지는 말씀드릴 수가 없네요—기억력이 형편없이 헐거워서요. 근데 거시기한 멋진 아가씨의—교육도 잘 받고—잘난 척이라곤 눈곱만큼도 없는 그 아가씨의 오라비랑 자리를 같이했을 때—말씀하신 그 무렵에—\""]], [["‘There!", "\"됐다!"], ["Never mind the sister,’ remarked Mrs Merdle, a little impatiently.", "누이 얘긴 신경 쓰지 마라,\" 머들 부인이 조금 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["‘What did the brother say?’", "\"그 오라비가 뭐라고 했니?\""]], [["‘Didn’t say a word, ma’am,’ answered Mr Sparkler.", "\"한마디도 안 했어요, 어머니,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘As silent a feller as myself.", "\"저만큼이나 말없는 친구죠."], ["Equally hard up for a remark.’", "할 말이 궁한 것도 저랑 똑같고요.\""]], [["‘Somebody said something,’ returned Mrs Merdle.", "\"누군가가 무슨 말을 했을 거 아니냐,\" 머들 부인이 되받았다."], ["‘Never mind who it was.’", "\"누구였는지는 신경 쓰지 말고.\""]], [["[‘Assure you I don’t in the least,’ said Mr Sparkler.)", "[\"정말이지 저도 전혀 신경 안 써요,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다.]"]], [["‘But tell us what it was.’", "\"그게 뭐였는지나 말해 봐라.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler referred to his pulse again, and put himself through some severe mental discipline before he replied:", "스파클러 씨는 다시 한번 제 맥을 짚어 보고, 대답하기에 앞서 몇 차례 호된 정신 수련을 거쳤다."]], [["‘Fellers referring to my Governor--expression not my own--occasionally compliment my Governor in a very handsome way on being immensely rich and knowing--perfect phenomenon of Buyer and Banker and that--but say the Shop sits heavily on him.", "\"친구들이 저희 영감을 두고—표현은 제가 지은 게 아니에요—이따금 저희 영감을 아주 근사하게 추켜세워요, 엄청 부자고 박식하다고—매수자며 은행가며 뭐며 완벽한 경이라고요—근데 가게가 그분을 무겁게 짓누른다고 해요."], ["Say he carried the Shop about, on his back rather--like Jew clothesmen with too much business.’", "그분이 가게를 짊어지고 다닌다고요, 등에다 지다시피—일감이 너무 많은 유대인 헌옷 장수들처럼요.\""]], [["‘Which,’ said Mrs Merdle, rising, with her floating drapery about her, ‘is exactly my complaint.", "\"바로 그게,\" 머들 부인이 너울거리는 휘장을 두른 채 일어서며 말했다."], ["Edmund, give me your arm up-stairs.’", "\"정확히 내 불평이에요. 에드먼드, 위층까지 팔 좀 빌려 다오.\""]], [["Mr Merdle, left alone to meditate on a better conformation of himself to Society, looked out of nine windows in succession, and appeared to see nine wastes of space.", "홀로 남아 사교계에 자신을 더 잘 맞출 방도를 궁리하게 된 머들 씨는, 창문 아홉 개를 연달아 내다보았고, 텅 빈 공허 아홉 자락을 보는 듯했다."], ["When he had thus entertained himself he went down-stairs, and looked intently at all the carpets on the ground-floor; and then came up-stairs again, and looked intently at all the carpets on the first-floor; as if they were gloomy depths, in unison with his oppressed soul.", "이렇게 자신을 즐겁게 한 뒤 그는 아래층으로 내려가, 일층의 모든 양탄자를 골똘히 들여다보았다. 그러고는 다시 위층으로 올라가, 이층의 모든 양탄자를 골똘히 들여다보았으니, 마치 그것들이 짓눌린 자기 영혼과 어울리는 음울한 심연이기라도 한 듯했다."], ["Through all the rooms he wandered, as he always did, like the last person on earth who had any business to approach them.", "그는 언제나 그러듯 모든 방을 돌아다녔는데, 마치 그 방들에 다가갈 자격이 있는 이 세상 최후의 사람이라도 된 듯한 모습이었다."], ["Let Mrs Merdle announce, with all her might, that she was at Home ever so many nights in a season, she could not announce more widely and unmistakably than Mr Merdle did that he was never at home.", "머들 부인이 한 철에 그토록 여러 밤 집에서 손님을 맞는다고 아무리 목청껏 알린다 한들, 머들 씨가 결코 집에 없다고 알리는 것보다 더 널리, 더 명백히 알릴 수는 없었다."]], [["At last he met the chief butler, the sight of which splendid retainer always finished him.", "마침내 그는 집사장과 마주쳤는데, 그 화려한 하인의 모습은 늘 그를 끝장내곤 했다."], ["Extinguished by this great creature, he sneaked to his dressing-room, and there remained shut up until he rode out to dinner, with Mrs Merdle, in her own handsome chariot.", "이 위대한 피조물에게 기가 꺾인 그는, 슬그머니 자기 화장실로 들어가 거기 틀어박혀 있다가, 머들 부인과 함께 그녀의 그 멋진 마차를 타고 만찬에 나갈 때까지 그러고 있었다."], ["At dinner, he was envied and flattered as a being of might, was Treasuried, Barred, and Bishoped, as much as he would; and an hour after midnight came home alone, and being instantly put out again in his own hall, like a rushlight, by the chief butler, went sighing to bed.", "만찬에서 그는 권세 있는 존재로 부러움과 아첨을 받았고, 재무부 대접, 법조계 대접, 주교 대접을 실컷 받았다. 그리고 자정이 한 시간쯤 지난 뒤 혼자 집에 돌아와, 제 집 현관에서 집사장에 의해 골풀 양초처럼 또다시 꺼져 버린 채, 한숨을 쉬며 잠자리로 향했다."]], [["# CHAPTER 34. A Shoal of Barnacles", "# CHAPTER 34. A Shoal of Barnacles"]], [["Mr Henry Gowan and the dog were established frequenters of the cottage, and the day was fixed for the wedding.", "헨리 가우언 씨와 그 개는 그 별장의 단골 출입객으로 자리를 굳혔고, 결혼식 날짜도 정해졌다."], ["There was to be a convocation of Barnacles on the occasion, in order that that very high and very large family might shed as much lustre on the marriage as so dim an event was capable of receiving.", "이 자리를 위해 배너클 일가의 모임이 열릴 예정이었는데, 이는 그토록 지체 높고 그토록 거대한 가문이, 이처럼 보잘것없는 행사가 받아들일 수 있는 한껏 광채를 그 결혼식에 드리워 주기 위함이었다."]], [["To have got the whole Barnacle family together would have been impossible for two reasons.", "배너클 일가 전체를 한자리에 모으는 일은 두 가지 이유에서 불가능했을 것이다."], ["Firstly, because no building could have held all the members and connections of that illustrious house.", "첫째, 그 명문가의 모든 구성원과 인척을 다 수용할 수 있는 건물이란 존재하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Secondly, because wherever there was a square yard of ground in British occupation under the sun or moon, with a public post upon it, sticking to that post was a Barnacle.", "둘째, 해와 달 아래 영국이 점유한 땅에서 공직의 말뚝 하나가 박혀 있는 곳이라면 어디든, 그 말뚝에 들러붙어 있는 것이 바로 배너클이었기 때문이다."], ["No intrepid navigator could plant a flag-staff upon any spot of earth, and take possession of it in the British name, but to that spot of earth, so soon as the discovery was known, the Circumlocution Office sent out a Barnacle and a despatch-box.", "어떤 담대한 항해자가 지상 어느 한 지점에 깃대를 꽂고 영국의 이름으로 그곳을 차지한다 해도, 그 발견이 알려지기가 무섭게 에두름국은 그 땅으로 배너클 한 명과 공문서함 하나를 내보냈다."], ["Thus the Barnacles were all over the world, in every direction--despatch-boxing the compass.", "그리하여 배너클들은 온 세계, 사방팔방에 퍼져 있었다 — 공문서함으로 나침반의 방위를 일일이 짚어 가면서."]], [["But, while the so-potent art of Prospero himself would have failed in summoning the Barnacles from every speck of ocean and dry land on which there was nothing (except mischief) to be done and anything to be pocketed, it was perfectly feasible to assemble a good many Barnacles.", "그러나 아무런 할 일도(못된 짓 빼고는) 없으면서 챙길 것은 무엇이든 있는 대양과 육지의 점점이 흩어진 모든 자리로부터 배너클들을 불러 모으는 일은, 저 막강한 프로스페로의 마법으로도 실패했을 터이지만, 상당히 많은 수의 배너클을 모으는 것만큼은 충분히 실현 가능했다."], ["This Mrs Gowan applied herself to do; calling on Mr Meagles frequently with new additions to the list, and holding conferences with that gentleman when he was not engaged (as he generally was at this period) in examining and paying the debts of his future son-in-law, in the apartment of scales and scoop.", "가우언 부인은 바로 이 일에 매달려, 미글스 씨를 자주 찾아와 명단에 새 이름을 보태었고, 그가 (이 시기에 흔히 그러했듯이) 저울과 됫박이 놓인 방에서 장차 사위가 될 사람의 빚을 따지고 갚아 주는 일에 매여 있지 않을 때면 그 신사와 회담을 가졌다."]], [["One marriage guest there was, in reference to whose presence Mr Meagles felt a nearer interest and concern than in the attendance of the most elevated Barnacle expected; though he was far from insensible of the honour of having such company.", "미글스 씨가 가장 지체 높으리라 기대되는 배너클의 참석보다도 더 가깝게 흥미와 관심을 느끼는 결혼식 손님이 한 사람 있었다. 물론 그런 일행을 모시는 영예에 그가 무감각했던 것은 결코 아니지만 말이다."], ["This guest was Clennam.", "이 손님은 클레넘이었다."], ["But Clennam had made a promise he held sacred, among the trees that summer night, and, in the chivalry of his heart, regarded it as binding him to many implied obligations.", "그러나 클레넘은 그 여름밤 나무들 사이에서 스스로 신성하게 여기는 약속을 했고, 마음속 기사도에 따라 그 약속이 자신을 여러 암묵적 의무에 묶어 둔다고 여겼다."], ["In forgetfulness of himself, and delicate service to her on all occasions, he was never to fail; to begin it, he answered Mr Meagles cheerfully, ‘I shall come, of course.’", "자기 자신은 잊은 채 어떤 경우에도 그녀에게 섬세히 봉사하는 일에서 결코 어긋남이 없으리라 다짐했으니, 그 첫걸음으로 그는 미글스 씨에게 명랑하게 대답했다. \"물론 가야지요.\""]], [["His partner, Daniel Doyce, was something of a stumbling-block in Mr Meagles’s way, the worthy gentleman being not at all clear in his own anxious mind but that the mingling of Daniel with official Barnacleism might produce some explosive combination, even at a marriage breakfast. The national offender, however, lightened him of his uneasiness by coming down to Twickenham to represent that he begged, with the freedom of an old friend, and as a favour to one, that he might not be invited. ‘For,’ said he, ‘as my business with this set of gentlemen was to do a public duty and a public service, and as their business with me was to prevent it by wearing my soul out, I think we had better not eat and drink together with a show of being of one mind.’ Mr Meagles was much amused by his friend’s oddity; and patronised him with a more protecting air of allowance than usual, when he rejoined: ‘Well, well, Dan, you shall have your own crotchety way.’", "그의 동업자 대니얼 도이스는 미글스 씨에게 다소 걸림돌이었다. 그 훌륭한 신사는 근심 어린 마음속으로, 대니얼이 관청의 배너클주의와 한데 섞이면 결혼 축하연 자리에서조차 무언가 폭발성 화합물을 만들어 내지 않을까 영 마음을 놓지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이 나라의 죄인은 직접 트위커넘으로 내려와, 옛 친구로서의 허물없음으로, 또한 한 친구에게 베푸는 호의로서, 자신을 초대하지 말아 달라고 청함으로써 그의 불안을 덜어 주었다. \"이보게,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 신사분들과 내가 얽힌 일이라는 게, 내 쪽에서는 공공의 의무와 공공의 봉사를 하자는 것이었고, 그분들 쪽에서는 내 영혼을 닳아 없어지게 만들어 그걸 막자는 것이었으니, 우리가 한마음인 척하며 함께 먹고 마시지 않는 편이 나을 듯하네.\" 미글스 씨는 친구의 별난 성미에 무척 즐거워했고, 평소보다 한층 더 감싸 주는 너그러움의 태도로 그를 두둔하며 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"그래, 그래, 댄, 자네 그 괴팍한 고집대로 하게나.\""]], [["To Mr Henry Gowan, as the time approached, Clennam tried to convey by all quiet and unpretending means, that he was frankly and disinterestedly desirous of tendering him any friendship he would accept.", "식이 다가오자 클레넘은 헨리 가우언 씨에게, 온갖 조용하고 꾸밈없는 방식으로, 그가 받아들이려는 어떤 우정이든 솔직하고 사심 없이 건네고 싶다는 뜻을 전하려 애썼다."], ["Mr Gowan treated him in return with his usual ease, and with his usual show of confidence, which was no confidence at all.", "가우언 씨는 그 답례로 그를 평소의 편안함으로, 또 평소처럼 신뢰를 내보이는 태도로 대했는데, 그것은 사실 전혀 신뢰가 아니었다."]], [["‘You see, Clennam,’ he happened to remark in the course of conversation one day, when they were walking near the Cottage within a week of the marriage, ‘I am a disappointed man.", "\"있잖나, 클레넘,\" 결혼식을 일주일 앞둔 어느 날, 두 사람이 별장 근처를 거닐던 중에 그가 마침 이런 말을 꺼냈다."], ["That you know already.’", "\"나는 실의에 빠진 사람일세. 그건 자네도 이미 알고 있지.\""]], [["‘Upon my word,’ said Clennam, a little embarrassed, ‘I scarcely know how.’", "\"솔직히 말해,\" 클레넘이 다소 거북해하며 말했다. \"어째서 그런지는 도무지 잘 모르겠는데.\""]], [["‘Why,’ returned Gowan, ‘I belong to a clan, or a clique, or a family, or a connection, or whatever you like to call it, that might have provided for me in any one of fifty ways, and that took it into its head not to do it at all.", "\"왜냐하면 말이야,\" 가우언이 대꾸했다."], ["So here I am, a poor devil of an artist.’", "\"나는 어떤 씨족이랄까, 패거리랄까, 가문이랄까, 인척이랄까, 뭐라 부르든 좋네만, 그런 집안에 속해 있고, 그 집안은 쉰 가지 방식 중 어느 하나로든 나를 자리 잡아 줄 수 있었는데도, 아예 그러지 않기로 작정해 버렸거든. 그래서 이렇게, 나는 가난뱅이 화가 신세가 된 거지.\""]], [["Clennam was beginning, ‘But on the other hand--’ when Gowan took him up.", "클레넘이 \"하지만 다른 한편으로는—\" 하고 말을 꺼내려는데 가우언이 가로챘다."]], [["‘Yes, yes, I know.", "\"그래, 그래, 알아."], ["I have the good fortune of being beloved by a beautiful and charming girl whom I love with all my heart.’", "나는 아름답고 매력적인 아가씨에게 사랑받는 행운을 누리고 있고, 나 또한 온 마음을 다해 그녀를 사랑하지.\""]], [["[‘Is there much of it?’ Clennam thought.", "('그 마음이라는 게 그리 많기는 한가?'"], ["And as he thought it, felt ashamed of himself.)", "클레넘은 생각했다. 그리고 그렇게 생각하면서 스스로 부끄러워졌다.)"]], [["‘And of finding a father-in-law who is a capital fellow and a liberal good old boy.", "\"게다가 더없이 훌륭한 사람이자 너그럽고 마음씨 좋은 노신사인 장인까지 얻었지. 그래도, 어릴 적 누군가가 내 머리를 감기고 빗겨 줄 때, 그 어린 머릿속에는 다른 전망들이 같이 감기고 빗겨져 들어왔단 말이야."], ["Still, I had other prospects washed and combed into my childish head when it was washed and combed for me, and I took them to a public school when I washed and combed it for myself, and I am here without them, and thus I am a disappointed man.’", "그래서 내가 스스로 머리를 감고 빗게 되었을 때 그것들을 사립학교까지 들고 갔는데, 지금은 그것들 없이 여기 이렇게 있단 말이지. 그래서 나는 실의에 빠진 사람인 거야.\""]], [["Clennam thought (and as he thought it, again felt ashamed of himself), was this notion of being disappointed in life, an assertion of station which the bridegroom brought into the family as his property, having already carried it detrimentally into his pursuit?", "클레넘은 생각했다(그리고 그렇게 생각하면서 또다시 스스로 부끄러워졌다). 이 신랑이 자기 재산처럼 처가에 들고 들어오는, 인생에서 실의에 빠졌다는 이 관념은, 이미 자기 본업에까지 해롭게 끌어들였던 어떤 신분 의식의 주장이 아닐까?"], ["And was it a hopeful or a promising thing anywhere?", "그리고 그것이 어디에서든 희망적이거나 장래성 있는 일일 수 있을까?"]], [["‘Not bitterly disappointed, I think,’ he said aloud.", "\"그래도 쓰라리게 실의에 빠진 건 아니겠지,\" 그가 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["‘Hang it, no; not bitterly,’ laughed Gowan.", "\"제기랄, 그럼, 쓰라리게는 아니지,\" 가우언이 웃었다."], ["‘My people are not worth that--though they are charming fellows, and I have the greatest affection for them.", "\"내 식구들이 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 사람들도 아니거든 — 매력적인 친구들이고 나도 그들을 무척 아끼긴 하지만."], ["Besides, it’s pleasant to show them that I can do without them, and that they may all go to the Devil.", "게다가 내가 그들 없이도 잘 지낼 수 있다는 걸, 그들 모두 지옥에나 가도 좋다는 걸 보여 주는 게 즐겁기도 하고."], ["And besides, again, most men are disappointed in life, somehow or other, and influenced by their disappointment.", "그리고 또, 사람이란 대개 이런저런 식으로 인생에서 실의를 겪고, 그 실의에 휘둘리며 살아가는 법이지."], ["But it’s a dear good world, and I love it!’", "그래도 이건 참 사랑스러운 좋은 세상이고, 나는 이 세상이 좋아!\""]], [["‘It lies fair before you now,’ said Arthur.", "\"이제 자네 앞에 그 세상이 환히 펼쳐져 있군,\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Fair as this summer river,’ cried the other, with enthusiasm, ‘and by Jove I glow with admiration of it, and with ardour to run a race in it.", "\"이 여름 강물처럼 환하게 말이지,\" 상대가 열을 올리며 외쳤다."], ["It’s the best of old worlds!", "\"그리고 맹세코, 나는 이 세상에 대한 찬탄으로, 이 세상에서 한판 경주를 펼치겠다는 열정으로 달아오른다네."], ["And my calling!", "더없이 좋은 옛 세상이지!"], ["The best of old callings, isn’t it?’", "그리고 내 직업은 또 어떻고! 더없이 좋은 옛 직업 아닌가, 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["‘Full of interest and ambition, I conceive,’ said Clennam.", "\"흥미와 야심으로 가득하다고 봅니다,\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘And imposition,’ added Gowan, laughing; ‘we won’t leave out the imposition.", "\"또 사기로도 가득하지,\" 가우언이 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["I hope I may not break down in that; but there, my being a disappointed man may show itself.", "\"사기를 빼놓을 순 없지. 나도 거기서 무너지지 않기를 바라네만, 글쎄, 바로 그 대목에서 내가 실의에 빠진 사람이라는 게 드러날지도 몰라."], ["I may not be able to face it out gravely enough.", "충분히 근엄하게 그걸 밀고 나갈 수가 없을지도 모르거든."], ["Between you and me, I think there is some danger of my being just enough soured not to be able to do that.’", "우리끼리 얘기지만, 내가 딱 그걸 못 할 만큼 적당히 비뚤어져 있는 게 아닌가 싶은 위험이 좀 있단 말이야.\""]], [["‘To do what?’ asked Clennam.", "\"무엇을 못 한다는 거죠?\" 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘To keep it up.", "\"그 짓을 계속 유지하는 거 말일세."], ["To help myself in my turn, as the man before me helps himself in his, and pass the bottle of smoke.", "내 앞 사람이 제 차례에 제 잇속을 챙기듯 내 차례에 내 잇속을 챙기고, 연기 든 병을 다음 사람에게 넘기는 거지."], ["To keep up the pretence as to labour, and study, and patience, and being devoted to my art, and giving up many solitary days to it, and abandoning many pleasures for it, and living in it, and all the rest of it--in short, to pass the bottle of smoke according to rule.’", "노동이니, 연구니, 인내니, 내 예술에 헌신한다느니, 그것에 수많은 고독한 나날을 바친다느니, 그것을 위해 숱한 즐거움을 포기한다느니, 그 속에서 산다느니, 그 밖에 이러쿵저러쿵하는 척을 계속 유지하는 것 — 한마디로, 규칙에 따라 연기 든 병을 넘기는 일 말이지.\""]], [["‘But it is well for a man to respect his own vocation, whatever it is; and to think himself bound to uphold it, and to claim for it the respect it deserves; is it not?’ Arthur reasoned.", "\"하지만 사람이 제 직업이 무엇이든 그것을 존중하고, 그것을 떠받칠 의무가 있다고 여기며, 그것이 마땅히 받아야 할 존중을 요구하는 건 좋은 일 아닙니까? 그렇지 않나요?\""], ["‘And your vocation, Gowan, may really demand this suit and service.", "아서가 따져 물었다. \"그리고 가우언, 자네 직업이야말로 정말로 그런 헌신과 봉사를 요구할지 모르지."], ["I confess I should have thought that all Art did.’", "솔직히 나는 모든 예술이 다 그렇다고 생각해 왔는데.\""]], [["‘What a good fellow you are, Clennam!’ exclaimed the other, stopping to look at him, as if with irrepressible admiration.", "\"자네 참 좋은 사람이로군, 클레넘!\" 상대가 걸음을 멈추고, 억누를 수 없는 찬탄이라도 담은 듯 그를 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["‘What a capital fellow!", "\"참 훌륭한 친구야!"], ["_You_ have never been disappointed.", "자네는 한 번도 실의에 빠져 본 적이 없지."], ["That’s easy to see.’", "그건 보면 알 수 있어.\""]], [["It would have been so cruel if he had meant it, that Clennam firmly resolved to believe he did not mean it.", "만약 그가 정말 그런 뜻으로 한 말이라면 너무도 잔인한 일이었을 터라, 클레넘은 그가 그런 뜻이 아니라고 굳게 믿기로 마음먹었다."], ["Gowan, without pausing, laid his hand upon his shoulder, and laughingly and lightly went on:", "가우언은 멈추지 않고 그의 어깨에 손을 얹은 채, 웃으며 가볍게 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Clennam, I don’t like to dispel your generous visions, and I would give any money (if I had any), to live in such a rose-coloured mist.", "\"클레넘, 나는 자네의 그 너그러운 환상을 깨뜨리고 싶지 않고, (돈이 있다면) 어떤 돈을 주고서라도 그런 장밋빛 안개 속에 살고 싶다네."], ["But what I do in my trade, I do to sell.", "하지만 내가 내 일에서 하는 짓은 팔기 위해서 하는 거야."], ["What all we fellows do, we do to sell.", "우리 같은 자들이 하는 일은 죄다 팔려고 하는 짓이지."], ["If we didn’t want to sell it for the most we can get for it, we shouldn’t do it.", "우리가 거기서 받아 낼 수 있는 최대치를 받고 팔 생각이 없다면, 애초에 그 짓을 하지도 않을걸."], ["Being work, it has to be done; but it’s easily enough done.", "일이니까 해야 하는 거지만, 사실 그건 쉽게 해치울 수 있어."], ["All the rest is hocus-pocus.", "나머지는 죄다 눈속임이고."], ["Now here’s one of the advantages, or disadvantages, of knowing a disappointed man.", "자, 이게 바로 실의에 빠진 사람을 사귈 때의 이점이라면 이점, 단점이라면 단점이지."], ["You hear the truth.’", "진실을 듣게 된다는 것.\""]], [["Whatever he had heard, and whether it deserved that name or another, it sank into Clennam’s mind.", "그가 무슨 말을 들었든, 그리고 그것이 진실이라는 이름에 값하는 것이든 다른 이름에 값하는 것이든, 그 말은 클레넘의 마음속에 가라앉았다."], ["It so took root there, that he began to fear Henry Gowan would always be a trouble to him, and that so far he had gained little or nothing from the dismissal of Nobody, with all his inconsistencies, anxieties, and contradictions.", "그것은 그 안에 깊이 뿌리를 내려, 그는 헨리 가우언이 자신에게 언제까지나 골칫거리가 되리라는 두려움이 들기 시작했고, 또 자신이 그 온갖 모순과 불안과 자가당착을 지닌 '아무도 아닌 자'를 떨쳐 내고서도 여태 얻은 것이 거의, 아니 전혀 없다는 생각마저 들었다."], ["He found a contest still always going on in his breast between his promise to keep Gowan in none but good aspects before the mind of Mr Meagles, and his enforced observation of Gowan in aspects that had no good in them.", "그는 자기 가슴속에서 여전히 끊임없는 싸움이 벌어지고 있음을 깨달았다. 한쪽에는 미글스 씨의 마음 앞에서 가우언을 오직 좋은 면으로만 비춰 주겠다는 약속이 있었고, 다른 쪽에는 좋은 구석이라곤 없는 가우언의 여러 면모를 어쩔 수 없이 목격하게 되는 일이 있었다."], ["Nor could he quite support his own conscientious nature against misgivings that he distorted and discoloured himself, by reminding himself that he never sought those discoveries, and that he would have avoided them with willingness and great relief.", "또한 그는, 자신이 그런 발견들을 결코 찾아 나선 적이 없으며 기꺼이, 그것도 크게 안도하며 피했을 것이라고 스스로를 일깨워 보았지만, 자신이 가우언을 일그러뜨리고 나쁘게 색칠하고 있는 것은 아닌가 하는 의구심 앞에서 양심적인 본성을 온전히 지탱하지는 못했다."], ["For he never could forget what he had been; and he knew that he had once disliked Gowan for no better reason than that he had come in his way.", "자신이 한때 어떤 사람이었는지 결코 잊을 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 그는, 가우언이 자기 앞길에 끼어들었다는 것 말고는 더 나은 이유도 없이 한때 그를 싫어했었다는 사실을 알고 있었다."]], [["Harassed by these thoughts, he now began to wish the marriage over, Gowan and his young wife gone, and himself left to fulfil his promise, and discharge the generous function he had accepted.", "이런 생각들에 시달리던 그는, 이제 결혼식이 어서 끝나, 가우언과 그의 젊은 아내가 떠나고, 자신은 남아 약속을 지키며 자신이 받아들인 너그러운 소임을 다하게 되기를 바라기 시작했다."], ["This last week was, in truth, an uneasy interval for the whole house.", "사실 이 마지막 한 주는 온 집안에 불안한 시간이었다."], ["Before Pet, or before Gowan, Mr Meagles was radiant; but Clennam had more than once found him alone, with his view of the scales and scoop much blurred, and had often seen him look after the lovers, in the garden or elsewhere when he was not seen by them, with the old clouded face on which Gowan had fallen like a shadow.", "미글스 씨는 펫 앞에서나 가우언 앞에서는 환하게 빛났다. 그러나 클레넘은 그가 저울과 됫박에 관한 자기 생각이 잔뜩 흐려진 채 홀로 있는 모습을 여러 번 보았고, 또 그가 두 연인에게 보이지 않는 곳에서, 정원이나 다른 데서 그들을 눈으로 좇으며, 가우언이 그림자처럼 내려앉은 그 예의 흐려진 얼굴을 짓고 있는 것을 자주 보았다."], ["In the arrangement of the house for the great occasion, many little reminders of the old travels of the father and mother and daughter had to be disturbed and passed from hand to hand; and sometimes, in the midst of these mute witnesses, to the life they had had together, even Pet herself would yield to lamenting and weeping.", "이 큰 행사를 위해 집을 정돈하느라, 아버지와 어머니와 딸이 함께한 옛 여행들을 떠올리게 하는 자잘한 물건들이 여럿 흐트러지고 이 손 저 손으로 옮겨져야 했다. 그러다 보면 함께 보낸 삶에 대한 이 말 없는 증인들 한가운데에서, 때로는 펫 자신조차도 비탄과 눈물에 무너지곤 했다."], ["Mrs Meagles, the blithest and busiest of mothers, went about singing and cheering everybody; but she, honest soul, had her flights into store rooms, where she would cry until her eyes were red, and would then come out, attributing that appearance to pickled onions and pepper, and singing clearer than ever.", "어머니들 가운데 가장 쾌활하고 분주한 미글스 부인은 노래를 부르며 모두를 북돋우고 다녔다. 그러나 그 정직한 사람도 이따금 광으로 숨어들어, 눈이 빨개질 때까지 울다가 나오면서는 그 모습을 절인 양파와 후추 탓으로 돌리고는 어느 때보다 더 맑게 노래를 불렀다."], ["Mrs Tickit, finding no balsam for a wounded mind in Buchan’s Domestic Medicine, suffered greatly from low spirits, and from moving recollections of Minnie’s infancy.", "티킷 부인은 뷰캔의 「가정 의학」에서도 상처받은 마음을 달랠 향유를 찾지 못해 깊은 우울에 시달렸고, 미니의 어린 시절에 대한 가슴 저미는 추억으로 괴로워했다."], ["When the latter was powerful with her, she usually sent up secret messages importing that she was not in parlour condition as to her attire, and that she solicited a sight of ‘her child’ in the kitchen; there, she would bless her child’s face, and bless her child’s heart, and hug her child, in a medley of tears and congratulations, chopping-boards, rolling-pins, and pie-crust, with the tenderness of an old attached servant, which is a very pretty tenderness indeed.", "그 추억이 그녀를 강하게 사로잡을 때면, 그녀는 보통 은밀한 전갈을 위로 올려, 자기 차림새가 응접실에 나설 만한 상태가 아니니 부엌에서 '내 아이'를 한번 보게 해 달라고 간청하곤 했다. 거기서 그녀는 도마와 밀방망이와 파이 반죽 사이에서, 눈물과 축하가 뒤섞인 가운데, 아이의 얼굴에 축복을, 아이의 마음에 축복을 빌며, 오랜 세월 정든 하인의 그 다정함으로 아이를 끌어안았으니, 그것은 참으로 어여쁜 다정함이었다."]], [["But all days come that are to be; and the marriage-day was to be, and it came; and with it came all the Barnacles who were bidden to the feast.", "그러나 와야 할 모든 날은 오고야 마는 법, 결혼식 날도 와야 했고, 마침내 왔다. 그리고 그날과 함께 잔치에 초대받은 배너클들이 모두 도착했다."]], [["There was Mr Tite Barnacle, from the Circumlocution Office, and Mews Street, Grosvenor Square, with the expensive Mrs Tite Barnacle _nee_ Stiltstalking, who made the Quarter Days so long in coming, and the three expensive Miss Tite Barnacles, double-loaded with accomplishments and ready to go off, and yet not going off with the sharpness of flash and bang that might have been expected, but rather hanging fire.", "에두름국에서, 그로스브너 광장 뮤스 거리에서 타이트 배너클 씨가 왔는데, 분기 결산일을 그토록 더디게 오는 날로 만들어 버리는, 결혼 전 성이 스틸트스토킹이던 사치스러운 타이트 배너클 부인과, 갖가지 재예를 이중으로 가득 채워 넣고 시집갈 만반의 채비를 갖추었으면서도, 기대됨직한 그 번쩍이고 펑 터지는 날카로움으로 시집가지는 못하고 오히려 불발탄처럼 늑장을 부리고 있는 사치스러운 타이트 배너클 양 셋도 함께였다."], ["There was Barnacle junior, also from the Circumlocution Office, leaving the Tonnage of the country, which he was somehow supposed to take under his protection, to look after itself, and, sooth to say, not at all impairing the efficiency of its protection by leaving it alone.", "역시 에두름국 소속인 배너클 주니어도 왔는데, 그는 어쩐 영문인지 자기 보호 아래 두기로 되어 있는 이 나라의 선박 톤수 업무를 알아서 굴러가도록 내버려둔 채였으며, 사실대로 말하자면 그렇게 내버려둔다고 해서 그 보호의 효율이 조금도 떨어지지는 않았다."], ["There was the engaging Young Barnacle, deriving from the sprightly side of the family, also from the Circumlocution Office, gaily and agreeably helping the occasion along, and treating it, in his sparkling way, as one of the official forms and fees of the Church Department of How not to do it.", "가문의 발랄한 갈래에서 나온, 역시 에두름국 소속의 매력적인 젊은 배너클도 왔는데, 그는 쾌활하고 상냥하게 이 자리를 거들며, 특유의 반짝이는 태도로 그것을 '안 되게 하는 법' 교회국이 치르는 의례적인 절차와 수수료의 하나쯤으로 취급했다."], ["There were three other Young Barnacles from three other offices, insipid to all the senses, and terribly in want of seasoning, doing the marriage as they would have ‘done’ the Nile, Old Rome, the new singer, or Jerusalem.", "다른 세 관청에서 온 또 다른 젊은 배너클 셋도 있었는데, 모든 감각에 밋밋하기 짝이 없고 도무지 양념이 절실히 필요한 자들로, 그들이 나일강이나 고대 로마나 새로 나온 가수나 예루살렘을 '해치우듯' 이 결혼식을 해치우고 있었다."]], [["But there was greater game than this.", "그러나 이보다 더 큰 사냥감도 있었다."], ["There was Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle himself, in the odour of Circumlocution--with the very smell of Despatch-Boxes upon him.", "에두름의 향내를 풍기며 — 그야말로 공문서함 냄새를 몸에 두른 채 — 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경 본인이 와 있었던 것이다. 그렇다, 단 하나의 분노에 찬 발상의 날개를 타고 관직의 정상에 오른 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경이 와 있었다."], ["Yes, there was Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle, who had risen to official heights on the wings of one indignant idea, and that was, My Lords, that I am yet to be told that it behoves a Minister of this free country to set bounds to the philanthropy, to cramp the charity, to fetter the public spirit, to contract the enterprise, to damp the independent self-reliance, of its people.", "그 발상이란 이러했다. 의원 여러분, 이 자유로운 나라의 한 장관으로서, 그 국민의 박애에 한계를 긋고, 그 자선을 옥죄며, 그 공공 정신을 옭아매고, 그 진취성을 위축시키며, 그 독립적 자조 정신에 찬물을 끼얹는 일이 마땅한 도리라는 말을, 나는 여태 들어 본 바가 없습니다."], ["That was, in other words, that this great statesman was always yet to be told that it behoved the Pilot of the ship to do anything but prosper in the private loaf and fish trade ashore, the crew being able, by dint of hard pumping, to keep the ship above water without him.", "이를 달리 말하자면, 이 위대한 정치가는, 배의 수로 안내인 된 자가 마땅히 해야 할 일이란, 선원들이 죽어라 펌프질을 해서 그가 없이도 배를 물 위에 띄워 둘 수 있는 동안, 육지에서 빵과 물고기를 사사로이 거래하며 잘 먹고사는 것 말고 다른 무엇이라는 말을 여태 들어 본 바가 없다는 뜻이었다."], ["On this sublime discovery in the great art How not to do it, Lord Decimus had long sustained the highest glory of the Barnacle family; and let any ill-advised member of either House but try How to do it by bringing in a Bill to do it, that Bill was as good as dead and buried when Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle rose up in his place and solemnly said, soaring into indignant majesty as the Circumlocution cheering soared around him, that he was yet to be told, My Lords, that it behoved him as the Minister of this free country, to set bounds to the philanthropy, to cramp the charity, to fetter the public spirit, to contract the enterprise, to damp the independent self-reliance, of its people.", "'안 되게 하는 법'이라는 위대한 기예에서 이뤄 낸 이 숭고한 발견 위에서, 데시머스 경은 오랫동안 배너클 가문 최고의 영광을 떠받쳐 왔다. 그리하여 양원 중 어느 쪽이든 분별없는 의원 하나가 무언가를 하기 위한 법안을 제출함으로써 '되게 하는 법'을 시도하기만 하면, 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경이 자기 자리에서 일어나 엄숙하게, 에두름파의 환호가 그를 에워싸고 솟구쳐 오르는 가운데 자신도 분노에 찬 위엄 속으로 솟구쳐 오르며, 의원 여러분, 이 자유로운 나라의 한 장관으로서 그 국민의 박애에 한계를 긋고, 그 자선을 옥죄며, 그 공공 정신을 옭아매고, 그 진취성을 위축시키며, 그 독립적 자조 정신에 찬물을 끼얹는 일이 마땅한 도리라는 말을 여태 들어 본 바가 없다고 말하는 순간, 그 법안은 이미 죽어 묻힌 거나 다름없었다."], ["The discovery of this Behoving Machine was the discovery of the political perpetual motion.", "이 '마땅한 도리 기계'의 발견은 곧 정치적 영구 기관의 발견이었다."], ["It never wore out, though it was always going round and round in all the State Departments.", "그것은 모든 국가 부처 안에서 끊임없이 빙글빙글 돌면서도 결코 닳는 법이 없었다."]], [["And there, with his noble friend and relative Lord Decimus, was William Barnacle, who had made the ever-famous coalition with Tudor Stiltstalking, and who always kept ready his own particular recipe for How not to do it; sometimes tapping the Speaker, and drawing it fresh out of him, with a ‘First, I will beg you, sir, to inform the House what Precedent we have for the course into which the honourable gentleman would precipitate us;’ sometimes asking the honourable gentleman to favour him with his own version of the Precedent; sometimes telling the honourable gentleman that he (William Barnacle) would search for a Precedent; and oftentimes crushing the honourable gentleman flat on the spot by telling him there was no Precedent.", "그리고 그곳에는, 그의 고귀한 벗이자 친척인 데시머스 경과 함께, 일찍이 튜더 스틸트스토킹과 저 영원히 유명한 연립을 이뤄 낸 윌리엄 배너클이 있었다. 그는 언제나 자기만의 독특한 '안 되게 하는 법'"], ["But Precedent and Precipitate were, under all circumstances, the well-matched pair of battle-horses of this able Circumlocutionist.", "비방을 준비해 두고 있었다. 때로는 의장을 두드려 그에게서 갓 우려낸 것을 끄집어내며, \"먼저, 의장님, 존경하는 의원께서 우리를 황급히 몰아넣으려는 그 방침에 어떤 선례가 있는지 본 의회에 알려 주시기를 청합니다\""], ["No matter that the unhappy honourable gentleman had been trying in vain, for twenty-five years, to precipitate William Barnacle into this--William Barnacle still put it to the House, and (at second-hand or so) to the country, whether he was to be precipitated into this.", "하였고, 때로는 그 존경하는 의원에게 선례에 대한 본인의 해석을 들려 달라고 부탁했으며, 때로는 그 존경하는 의원에게 자신(윌리엄 배너클)이 선례를 찾아보겠노라고 말했고, 그리고 더 자주는, 그 존경하는 의원에게 선례 따위는 없다고 말함으로써 그 자리에서 그를 납작하게 으스러뜨렸다. 그러나 선례와 황급함이라는 이 한 쌍의 잘 어울리는 전마는, 이 유능한 에두름가의 어떤 상황에서도 변함없는 무기였다."], ["No matter that it was utterly irreconcilable with the nature of things and course of events that the wretched honourable gentleman could possibly produce a Precedent for this--William Barnacle would nevertheless thank the honourable gentleman for that ironical cheer, and would close with him upon that issue, and would tell him to his teeth that there Was NO Precedent for this.", "가련하게도 그 존경하는 의원이 이십오 년 동안 헛되이 윌리엄 배너클을 이 일로 황급히 몰아넣으려 애써 왔다 한들 상관없었다 — 윌리엄 배너클은 여전히 본 의회에, 그리고 (한 다리 건너서나마) 이 나라에, 자신이 이 일로 황급히 몰려야 마땅하냐고 되물었다. 사물의 본성과 사태의 흐름에 비추어, 그 비참한 존경하는 의원이 이 일에 대한 선례를 도저히 제시할 수 없다는 것이 전혀 타협 불가능한 사실이라 한들 상관없었다 — 그럼에도 윌리엄 배너클은 그 비아냥대는 환호에 대해 존경하는 의원에게 감사를 표하고, 바로 그 쟁점을 두고 그와 맞붙어, 그 일에 대한 선례 따위는 없다고 그의 면전에 대고 말하곤 했다."], ["It might perhaps have been objected that the William Barnacle wisdom was not high wisdom or the earth it bamboozled would never have been made, or, if made in a rash mistake, would have remained blank mud.", "어쩌면 윌리엄 배너클의 지혜가 그리 높은 지혜는 아니었다고, 만약 그랬다면 그가 우롱한 이 땅은 애초에 만들어지지도 않았거나, 혹은 성급한 착오로 만들어졌다 한들 텅 빈 진흙으로 남았으리라고 반박할 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["But Precedent and Precipitate together frightened all objection out of most people.", "그러나 선례와 황급함이 한데 어우러져 거의 모든 사람에게서 온갖 반박을 겁박해 쫓아냈다."]], [["And there, too, was another Barnacle, a lively one, who had leaped through twenty places in quick succession, and was always in two or three at once, and who was the much-respected inventor of an art which he practised with great success and admiration in all Barnacle Governments.", "그리고 그곳에는 또 다른 배너클, 활기 넘치는 자도 있었다. 그는 스무 개의 자리를 잇달아 껑충껑충 뛰어다녔고 늘 두세 자리를 동시에 차지하고 있었으며, 모든 배너클 정부에서 큰 성공과 찬탄 속에 몸소 펼쳐 보인 어떤 기예의 매우 존경받는 발명가였다."], ["This was, when he was asked a Parliamentary question on any one topic, to return an answer on any other.", "그것인즉, 어느 한 주제에 관한 의회의 질문을 받으면, 전혀 다른 주제에 관한 답변을 내놓는 것이었다."], ["It had done immense service, and brought him into high esteem with the Circumlocution Office.", "이 기예는 막대한 기여를 했고, 그를 에두름국에서 높은 평판에 올려놓았다."]], [["And there, too, was a sprinkling of less distinguished Parliamentary Barnacles, who had not as yet got anything snug, and were going through their probation to prove their worthiness. These Barnacles perched upon staircases and hid in passages, waiting their orders to make houses or not to make houses; and they did all their hearing, and ohing, and cheering, and barking, under directions from the heads of the family; and they put dummy motions on the paper in the way of other men’s motions; and they stalled disagreeable subjects off until late in the night and late in the session, and then with virtuous patriotism cried out that it was too late; and they went down into the country, whenever they were sent, and swore that Lord Decimus had revived trade from a swoon, and commerce from a fit, and had doubled the harvest of corn, quadrupled the harvest of hay, and prevented no end of gold from flying out of the Bank. Also these Barnacles were dealt, by the heads of the family, like so many cards below the court-cards, to public meetings and dinners; where they bore testimony to all sorts of services on the part of their noble and honourable relatives, and buttered the Barnacles on all sorts of toasts. And they stood, under similar orders, at all sorts of elections; and they turned out of their own seats, on the shortest notice and the most unreasonable terms, to let in other men; and they fetched and carried, and toadied and jobbed, and corrupted, and ate heaps of dirt, and were indefatigable in the public service. And there was not a list, in all the Circumlocution Office, of places that might fall vacant anywhere within half a century, from a lord of the Treasury to a Chinese consul, and up again to a governor-general of India, but as applicants for such places, the names of some or of every one of these hungry and adhesive Barnacles were down.", "그리고 그곳에는 또, 덜 저명한 의회 배너클들도 몇몇 흩뿌려져 있었는데, 이들은 아직 아늑한 자리 하나 차지하지 못한 채 자신의 가치를 입증하려고 수습 기간을 거치는 중이었다. 이 배너클들은 계단에 올라앉거나 복도에 숨어, 정족수를 채우라는 명령이나 채우지 말라는 명령을 기다렸다. 그들은 가문 우두머리들의 지시에 따라, 듣는 일도, 감탄하는 일도, 환호하는 일도, 짖어 대는 일도 죄다 해냈다. 그들은 다른 사람들의 동의안을 가로막을 요량으로 허수아비 동의안을 의사일정에 올려놓았다. 또 그들은 달갑지 않은 안건들을 밤늦은 시각, 회기 막바지까지 미적미적 미뤄 두었다가, 그제야 갸륵한 애국심으로 이미 너무 늦었다고 외쳐 댔다. 그들은 파견될 때마다 지방으로 내려가, 데시머스 경이 실신했던 무역을 되살리고 발작을 일으켰던 상업을 회복시켰으며, 곡물 수확량을 두 배로, 건초 수확량을 네 배로 늘렸고, 끝도 없는 황금이 은행에서 빠져나가는 것을 막았노라고 맹세했다. 또한 이 배너클들은 가문 우두머리들의 손에서, 그림 패 아래의 잔챙이 패처럼, 공개 집회와 만찬 자리에 패로 뿌려졌다. 거기서 그들은 자기네 고귀하고 존경스러운 친척들이 베푼 온갖 봉사를 증언하고, 갖가지 축배 자리에서 배너클들에게 알랑방귀를 뀌어 댔다. 그들은 비슷한 명령에 따라 온갖 선거에 출마했으며, 다른 사람을 들이기 위해서라면 가장 촉박한 통보와 가장 부당한 조건으로도 자기 의석을 내놓았다. 그들은 심부름을 도맡고, 아첨하고, 자리를 사고팔며, 부패하고, 산더미 같은 치욕을 삼켜 가면서도, 공공 봉사에 지칠 줄을 몰랐다. 그리고 에두름국 전체에 걸쳐, 향후 반세기 안에 어디서든 비게 될지 모를 자리들의 명단이라면, 재무부 위원에서 중국 영사에 이르기까지, 또 거기서 다시 인도 총독에 이르기까지, 그 모든 자리의 지원자로 굶주리고 들러붙는 이 배너클들 가운데 몇몇 혹은 전부의 이름이 올라 있지 않은 명단이란 없었다."]], [["It was necessarily but a sprinkling of any class of Barnacles that attended the marriage, for there were not two score in all, and what is that subtracted from Legion!", "결혼식에 참석한 것은 어떤 부류의 배너클이든 어쩔 수 없이 그 일부의 흩뿌림에 지나지 않았으니, 다 합쳐도 마흔이 채 안 되었고, 그것이 그 무리 전체에서 떼어 낸들 무엇이겠는가!"], ["But the sprinkling was a swarm in the Twickenham cottage, and filled it.", "그러나 그 흩뿌림조차도 트위커넘의 별장 안에서는 떼를 이루어 집을 가득 메웠다."], ["A Barnacle (assisted by a Barnacle) married the happy pair, and it behoved Lord Decimus Tite Barnacle himself to conduct Mrs Meagles to breakfast.", "한 배너클이(다른 배너클의 도움을 받아) 행복한 한 쌍의 결혼식을 주례했고, 미글스 부인을 축하연으로 안내하는 것은 데시머스 타이트 배너클 경 본인의 마땅한 도리였다."]], [["The entertainment was not as agreeable and natural as it might have been.", "연회는 본디 그래야 했을 만큼 유쾌하지도, 자연스럽지도 못했다."], ["Mr Meagles, hove down by his good company while he highly appreciated it, was not himself.", "미글스 씨는 그 고귀한 일행을 무척 높이 평가하면서도 그들에게 짓눌려 제정신이 아니었다."], ["Mrs Gowan was herself, and that did not improve him.", "가우언 부인은 평소의 자기 모습 그대로였는데, 그것이 그를 나아지게 하지는 못했다."], ["The fiction that it was not Mr Meagles who had stood in the way, but that it was the Family greatness, and that the Family greatness had made a concession, and there was now a soothing unanimity, pervaded the affair, though it was never openly expressed.", "길을 막아섰던 것은 미글스 씨가 아니라 가문의 위세였으며, 그 가문의 위세가 양보를 했고, 그래서 이제는 마음을 누그러뜨리는 일치가 이루어졌다는 식의 허구가, 공공연히 입 밖에 내어진 적은 결코 없었으나 행사 전체에 스며들어 있었다. 그러다 보니 배너클들은 이 생색내는 자리만 끝나면 자기네 쪽에서는 미글스 일가와 인연을 끊으리라 여겼고, 미글스 일가도 자기네 쪽에서 똑같이 여겼다."], ["Then the Barnacles felt that they for their parts would have done with the Meagleses when the present patronising occasion was over; and the Meagleses felt the same for their parts.", "그러다 가우언은, 가문에 원한을 품은, 또 어쩌면 자기 어머니가 다른 어떤 자비로운 목적에서라기보다 그들에게 다소간 불쾌감을 주리라는 기대에서 그들을 그곳에 불러 모으도록 내버려두었던, 실의에 빠진 사람으로서의 권리를 내세우며, 자신의 화필과 가난을 그들 앞에서 보란 듯이 드러냈다. 그는 머지않아 아내에게 빵 한 조각과 치즈 한 덩이 정도는 마련해 줄 수 있기를 바란다고, 또 그들 가운데 (자기보다 운이 좋아서) 무슨 좋은 자리라도 차지하여 그림 한 점쯤 살 형편이 되는 이가 있거든, 부디 이 가난한 화가를 기억해 달라고 청했다."], ["Then Gowan asserting his rights as a disappointed man who had his grudge against the family, and who, perhaps, had allowed his mother to have them there, as much in the hope it might give them some annoyance as with any other benevolent object, aired his pencil and his poverty ostentatiously before them, and told them he hoped in time to settle a crust of bread and cheese on his wife, and that he begged such of them as (more fortunate than himself) came in for any good thing, and could buy a picture, to please to remember the poor painter.", "그러다 데시머스 경은, 제 의회의 단상 위에서는 경이로운 존재이건만, 이 자리에서는 가장 허풍선이 같은 인물로 드러나고 말았다. 그는 진실한 추종자요 신봉자라면 누구든 머리카락이 곤두설 만한 진부한 말들을 줄줄이 늘어놓으며 신랑 신부에게 행복을 빌었고, 자신이 큰길로 착각한 듯한, 울부짖는 미궁 같은 문장들 사이를, 백치 같은 코끼리의 흐뭇함을 띤 채 터덜터덜 누비며, 거기서 빠져나오고 싶다는 생각조차 도통 하지 않았다."], ["Then Lord Decimus, who was a wonder on his own Parliamentary pedestal, turned out to be the windiest creature here: proposing happiness to the bride and bridegroom in a series of platitudes that would have made the hair of any sincere disciple and believer stand on end; and trotting, with the complacency of an idiotic elephant, among howling labyrinths of sentences which he seemed to take for high roads, and never so much as wanted to get out of.", "그러다 타이트 배너클 씨는, 만약 그런 방해가 가능했더라면, 완전한 공직자의 풍모로 토머스 로런스 경 앞에 평생토록 앉아 초상을 그리게 하던 그 자세를 흐트러뜨렸을 인물이 이 자리에 끼어 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 한편 배너클 주니어는 자기 친척인 두 명의 맥빠진 신사에게 분개하며 이렇게 일러바쳤다."], ["Then Mr Tite Barnacle could not but feel that there was a person in company, who would have disturbed his life-long sitting to Sir Thomas Lawrence in full official character, if such disturbance had been possible: while Barnacle junior did, with indignation, communicate to two vapid gentlemen, his relatives, that there was a feller here, look here, who had come to our Department without an appointment and said he wanted to know, you know; and that, look here, if he was to break out now, as he might you know (for you never could tell what an ungentlemanly Radical of that sort would be up to next), and was to say, look here, that he wanted to know this moment, you know, that would be jolly; wouldn’t it?", "여기 어떤 작자가 하나 있는데, 이보게나, 글쎄 임명장도 없이 우리 국에 찾아와서는, 알다시피, 알고 싶은 게 있다는 거야. 그런데 말이지, 이보게나, 만약 저자가 지금 당장 발끈해서는 — 워낙에 그런 무례한 급진파 자식이 다음 순간에 무슨 짓을 할지는 도무지 알 수 없는 노릇이니 — 이보게나, 알다시피 바로 이 순간 그걸 알고 싶다고 떠들어 댄다면, 그거 정말 가관이지 않겠나, 안 그래?"]], [["The pleasantest part of the occasion by far, to Clennam, was the painfullest.", "클레넘에게는, 이 자리에서 단연코 가장 흐뭇한 부분이 곧 가장 고통스러운 부분이었다."], ["When Mr and Mrs Meagles at last hung about Pet in the room with the two pictures (where the company were not), before going with her to the threshold which she could never recross to be the old Pet and the old delight, nothing could be more natural and simple than the three were.", "마침내 미글스 씨 부부가, 두 점의 그림이 걸린 방에서(일행은 그 방에 없었다), 펫을 둘러싸고 한참 어우러졌을 때, 곧 그녀가 옛 펫, 옛 기쁨으로서는 다시는 되넘어 돌아올 수 없는 그 문턱으로 함께 데려가기 전에, 세 사람보다 더 자연스럽고 더 꾸밈없는 모습은 없었다."], ["Gowan himself was touched, and answered Mr Meagles’s ‘O Gowan, take care of her, take care of her!’ with an earnest ‘Don’t be so broken-hearted, sir.", "가우언 자신도 마음이 움직여, 미글스 씨의 \"오, 가우언, 저 아이를 잘 돌봐 주게, 잘 돌봐 줘!\"라는 말에 진심 어린 목소리로 답했다. \"그렇게 상심하지 마십시오, 어르신."], ["By Heaven I will!’", "하늘에 맹세코 그러겠습니다!\""]], [["And so, with the last sobs and last loving words, and a last look to Clennam of confidence in his promise, Pet fell back in the carriage, and her husband waved his hand, and they were away for Dover; though not until the faithful Mrs Tickit, in her silk gown and jet black curls, had rushed out from some hiding-place, and thrown both her shoes after the carriage: an apparition which occasioned great surprise to the distinguished company at the windows.", "그리하여 마지막 흐느낌과 마지막 사랑의 말들과 함께, 또 자신의 약속을 믿어 달라는 클레넘을 향한 마지막 눈길과 함께, 펫은 마차에 몸을 기댔고, 그녀의 남편은 손을 흔들었으며, 두 사람은 도버를 향해 떠나갔다. 다만, 충직한 티킷 부인이 비단 가운에 새까만 곱슬머리 차림으로 어느 숨어 있던 곳에서 뛰쳐나와 마차 뒤로 제 신발 두 짝을 다 던지고 난 다음에야 비로소 그러했으니, 그 광경은 창가의 저명한 일행에게 큰 놀라움을 안겨 주었다."]], [["The said company being now relieved from further attendance, and the chief Barnacles being rather hurried (for they had it in hand just then to send a mail or two which was in danger of going straight to its destination, beating about the seas like the Flying Dutchman, and to arrange with complexity for the stoppage of a good deal of important business otherwise in peril of being done), went their several ways; with all affability conveying to Mr and Mrs Meagles that general assurance that what they had been doing there, they had been doing at a sacrifice for Mr and Mrs Meagles’s good, which they always conveyed to Mr John Bull in their official condescension to that most unfortunate creature.", "이제 그 일행은 더 머물러 있을 의무에서 벗어났고, 으뜸가는 배너클들은 적이 분주해진 터라(바로 그때 그들은, 플라잉 더치맨처럼 바다를 헤매는 일 없이 곧장 목적지로 직행할 위험에 처한 우편선 한두 척을 띄워 보낼 일과, 그러지 않으면 처리될 위험에 처한 중대한 업무를 한 무더기 가로막을 복잡한 방책을 마련할 일을 손에 쥐고 있었던 것이다) 저마다의 길로 흩어졌다. 그러면서 그들은 미글스 씨 부부에게, 자신들이 그곳에서 했던 일은 미글스 씨 부부의 이익을 위해 희생을 무릅쓰고 한 것이라는 그 일반적인 확언을, 더없이 상냥하게 건넸으니, 그것은 그들이 그 가장 불운한 피조물인 존 불 씨에게 관직의 생색을 베풀 때 늘 건네던 바로 그 확언이었다."]], [["A miserable blank remained in the house and in the hearts of the father and mother and Clennam.", "집 안에도, 또 그 아버지와 어머니와 클레넘의 가슴속에도 비참한 공백이 남았다."], ["Mr Meagles called only one remembrance to his aid, that really did him good.", "미글스 씨는 단 하나의 기억만을 자기 위안으로 불러냈는데, 그것만큼은 정말로 그에게 도움이 되었다."]], [["‘It’s very gratifying, Arthur,’ he said, ‘after all, to look back upon.’", "\"그래도, 아서,\" 그가 말했다. \"결국, 이렇게 돌이켜보면 참 흐뭇한 일이야.\""]], [["‘The past?’ said Clennam.", "\"지난 일 말씀입니까?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes--but I mean the company.’", "\"응 — 그런데 내가 말하는 건 그 일행 말일세.\""]], [["It had made him much more low and unhappy at the time, but now it really did him good.", "그것은 당시에는 그를 한층 더 풀 죽고 불행하게 만들었건만, 이제는 정말로 그에게 도움이 되었다."], ["‘It’s very gratifying,’ he said, often repeating the remark in the course of the evening.", "\"참 흐뭇한 일이야,\" 그가 그날 저녁 내내 그 말을 거듭 되뇌며 말했다."], ["‘Such high company!’", "\"그토록 지체 높은 일행이라니!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand", "# CHAPTER 35. What was behind Mr Pancks on Little Dorrit’s Hand"]], [["It was at this time that Mr Pancks, in discharge of his compact with Clennam, revealed to him the whole of his gipsy story, and told him Little Dorrit’s fortune.", "팽크스 씨가 클레넘과 맺은 약속을 이행하느라 자신의 집시 같은 이야기를 통째로 그에게 털어놓고, 리틀 도릿의 운명을 일러 준 것이 바로 이 무렵이었다."], ["Her father was heir-at-law to a great estate that had long lain unknown of, unclaimed, and accumulating.", "그녀의 아버지는, 오랫동안 알려지지 않은 채 임자 없이 쌓이기만 하던 큰 재산의 적법한 상속인이었다."], ["His right was now clear, nothing interposed in his way, the Marshalsea gates stood open, the Marshalsea walls were down, a few flourishes of his pen, and he was extremely rich.", "그의 권리는 이제 명백했고, 가로막는 것이 아무것도 없었으며, 마셜시의 대문은 열려 있고 마셜시의 담은 무너져 있었으니, 그가 펜을 몇 번 휘갈기기만 하면 그는 엄청난 부자가 될 터였다."]], [["In his tracking out of the claim to its complete establishment, Mr Pancks had shown a sagacity that nothing could baffle, and a patience and secrecy that nothing could tire.", "그 권리를 추적하여 완전히 확립하기까지, 팽크스 씨는 그 무엇으로도 당황시킬 수 없는 명민함과, 그 무엇으로도 지치게 할 수 없는 인내와 비밀스러움을 보였다."], ["‘I little thought, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘when you and I crossed Smithfield that night, and I told you what sort of a Collector I was, that this would come of it.", "\"그날 밤 당신과 제가 스미스필드를 가로질러 가면서, 제가 어떤 부류의 수금원인지 당신께 말했을 때,\" 팽크스가 말했다, \"그게 이런 결과를 낳으리라고는 정말이지 생각조차 못 했습니다."], ["I little thought, sir, when I told you you were not of the Clennams of Cornwall, that I was ever going to tell you who were of the Dorrits of Dorsetshire.’ He then went on to detail.", "당신이 콘월의 클레넘 가문 사람이 아니라고 말씀드렸을 때, 제가 도싯셔의 도릿 가문 사람이 누구인지를 장차 당신께 일러 주게 되리라고는 정말이지 생각조차 못 했고요.\" 그러고 나서 그는 상세히 설명해 나갔다."], ["How, having that name recorded in his note-book, he was first attracted by the name alone.", "그 이름을 자기 수첩에 적어 두었던 까닭에, 처음에는 그 이름 하나에 끌렸다는 것을."], ["How, having often found two exactly similar names, even belonging to the same place, to involve no traceable consanguinity, near or distant, he did not at first give much heed to this, except in the way of speculation as to what a surprising change would be made in the condition of a little seamstress, if she could be shown to have any interest in so large a property.", "똑같은 두 이름이, 심지어 같은 지방에 속하면서도, 가깝건 멀건 추적할 만한 어떠한 혈연관계도 수반하지 않는 경우를 자주 보아 왔던 터라, 처음에는 이것에 별로 주의를 기울이지 않았고, 다만 만약 그 가난한 어린 바느질꾼이 그토록 큰 재산에 어떤 권리가 있음을 밝혀낼 수 있다면 그녀의 처지에 얼마나 놀라운 변화가 일어날까 하는 막연한 추측의 차원에서만 그러했다는 것을."], ["How he rather supposed himself to have pursued the idea into its next degree, because there was something uncommon in the quiet little seamstress, which pleased him and provoked his curiosity.", "그 조용한 어린 바느질꾼에게는 그를 즐겁게 하고 호기심을 자극하는 무언가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있었던 까닭에, 그 생각을 다음 단계로까지 추적해 들어간 셈이 되었다고 스스로 짐작한다는 것을."], ["How he had felt his way inch by inch, and ‘Moled it out, sir’ (that was Mr Pancks’s expression), grain by grain.", "어떻게 그가 한 치씩 한 치씩 더듬어 나가며, 한 알 한 알씩 \"두더지처럼 파냈는지를\"(이것이 팽크스 씨의 표현이었다)."], ["How, in the beginning of the labour described by this new verb, and to render which the more expressive Mr Pancks shut his eyes in pronouncing it and shook his hair over them, he had alternated from sudden lights and hopes to sudden darkness and no hopes, and back again, and back again.", "이 새 동사로 묘사되는 노고의 초입에서—그것을 더 실감 나게 전하려고 팽크스 씨는 그 말을 발음하면서 눈을 감고 머리카락을 그 위로 흔들어 내렸다—갑작스러운 빛과 희망에서 갑작스러운 어둠과 절망으로, 또다시 돌아오고 또다시 돌아오며 오갔는지를. 어떻게 그가, 다른 모든 드나드는 이들처럼 그곳을 드나들 수 있도록 일부러 감옥 안에서 아는 사람들을 사귀었는지를."], ["How he had made acquaintances in the Prison, expressly that he might come and go there as all other comers and goers did; and how his first ray of light was unconsciously given him by Mr Dorrit himself and by his son; to both of whom he easily became known; with both of whom he talked much, casually [‘but always Moleing you’ll observe,’ said Mr Pancks): and from whom he derived, without being at all suspected, two or three little points of family history which, as he began to hold clues of his own, suggested others.", "그리고 어떻게 그의 첫 한 줄기 빛이 도릿 씨 본인과 그의 아들에게서 자기도 모르게 주어졌는지를—그는 두 사람 모두와 쉽게 가까워졌고, 두 사람 모두와 무심한 척하며 많은 이야기를 나누었다(\"하지만 늘 두더지처럼 파고 있었다는 걸 알아주십시오,\" 하고 팽크스 씨가 말했다)—그리고 그들로부터 조금도 의심받지 않으면서, 두세 가지 자그마한 집안 내력을 얻어 냈으며, 그것이 그가 자기 나름의 단서들을 쥐기 시작하자 또 다른 단서들을 시사해 주었다는 것을."], ["How it had at length become plain to Mr Pancks that he had made a real discovery of the heir-at-law to a great fortune, and that his discovery had but to be ripened to legal fulness and perfection.", "어떻게 마침내 팽크스 씨에게, 자신이 큰 재산의 적법한 상속인을 진짜로 발견해 냈으며, 그 발견을 법적으로 충분하고 완전한 상태로 무르익히기만 하면 된다는 것이 분명해졌는지를."], ["How he had, thereupon, sworn his landlord, Mr Rugg, to secrecy in a solemn manner, and taken him into Moleing partnership.", "그러고 나서 어떻게 그가 자기 집주인인 럭 씨에게 엄숙한 방식으로 비밀을 지키겠다는 맹세를 시키고, 그를 두더지 작업의 동업자로 끌어들였는지를."], ["How they had employed John Chivery as their sole clerk and agent, seeing to whom he was devoted.", "어떻게 그들이 존 치버리가 누구에게 헌신적인지를 알아보고서 그를 그들의 유일한 서기 겸 대리인으로 부렸는지를."], ["And how, until the present hour, when authorities mighty in the Bank and learned in the law declared their successful labours ended, they had confided in no other human being.", "그리고 어떻게, 은행에서 막강하고 법에 정통한 권위자들이 그들의 성공한 노고가 끝났음을 선언한 지금 이 시각에 이르기까지, 그들이 다른 어떤 사람에게도 털어놓지 않았는지를."]], [["‘So if the whole thing had broken down, sir,’ concluded Pancks, ‘at the very last, say the day before the other day when I showed you our papers in the Prison yard, or say that very day, nobody but ourselves would have been cruelly disappointed, or a penny the worse.’", "\"그러니 만약 이 모든 일이 와르르 무너졌다 하더라도, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 결론지었다, \"맨 마지막 순간에, 가령 그저께, 제가 감옥 마당에서 우리 서류를 당신께 보여 드린 그 전날에든, 혹은 바로 그날에든 말입니다, 우리 자신 말고는 아무도 잔인하게 실망할 일도 없었고, 한 푼이라도 손해 볼 일도 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["Clennam, who had been almost incessantly shaking hands with him throughout the narrative, was reminded by this to say, in an amazement which even the preparation he had had for the main disclosure smoothed down, ‘My dear Mr Pancks, this must have cost you a great sum of money.’", "이야기 내내 거의 쉴 새 없이 그와 악수를 나누고 있던 클레넘은, 이 말에 정신이 들어, 핵심 사실에 대한 마음의 준비조차도 다 누그러뜨리지 못한 놀라움에 잠겨 말했다. \"이보게 팽크스 씨, 이건 자네에게 큰돈이 들었겠군.\""]], [["‘Pretty well, sir,’ said the triumphant Pancks.", "\"꽤 들었지요, 선생님,\" 의기양양한 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘No trifle, though we did it as cheap as it could be done.", "\"할 수 있는 한 가장 싸게 했는데도 적은 돈은 아니었습니다."], ["And the outlay was a difficulty, let me tell you.’", "그리고 그 지출이 골칫거리였다는 점은 말씀드려 둬야겠습니다.\""]], [["‘A difficulty!’ repeated Clennam.", "\"골칫거리라!\" 클레넘이 되풀이했다."], ["‘But the difficulties you have so wonderfully conquered in the whole business!’ shaking his hand again.", "\"하지만 자네가 이 모든 일에서 그토록 놀랍게 정복해 낸 그 어려움들이라니!\" 하며 다시 그의 손을 잡고 흔들었다."]], [["‘I’ll tell you how I did it,’ said the delighted Pancks, putting his hair into a condition as elevated as himself.", "\"제가 어떻게 했는지 말씀드리지요,\" 기뻐하는 팽크스가, 머리카락을 자기 자신만큼이나 한껏 곤두선 상태로 세우며 말했다."], ["‘First, I spent all I had of my own.", "\"먼저, 제가 가진 제 돈을 다 썼습니다."], ["That wasn’t much.’", "많지는 않았지요.\""]], [["‘I am sorry for it,’ said Clennam: ‘not that it matters now, though.", "\"그것 참 안됐군,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Then, what did you do?’", "\"물론 이제는 상관없는 일이지만. 그래서, 그다음엔 어떻게 했나?\""]], [["‘Then,’ answered Pancks, ‘I borrowed a sum of my proprietor.’", "\"그다음엔,\" 팽크스가 대답했다, \"제 고용주에게서 얼마간을 빌렸습니다.\""]], [["‘Of Mr Casby?’ said Clennam.", "\"캐스비 씨에게서?\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘He’s a fine old fellow.’", "\"그분은 훌륭한 노인이지.\""]], [["‘Noble old boy; an’t he?’ said Mr Pancks, entering on a series of the dryest snorts.", "\"고결하신 노인이지요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 더없이 메마른 콧방귀를 잇달아 뀌기 시작하며 말했다."], ["‘Generous old buck.", "\"관대하신 노양반."], ["Confiding old boy.", "사람을 믿어 주시는 노인."], ["Philanthropic old buck.", "박애로우신 노양반."], ["Benevolent old boy!", "자비로우신 노인!"], ["Twenty per cent. I engaged to pay him, sir.", "제가 그분께 이십 퍼센트를 치르기로 약속했습니다, 선생님."], ["But we never do business for less at our shop.’", "하지만 우리 가게에서는 그보다 싸게 거래하는 일이 결코 없지요.\""]], [["Arthur felt an awkward consciousness of having, in his exultant condition, been a little premature.", "아서는, 우쭐해진 상태에서 자기가 조금 성급했다는 어색한 자각이 들었다."]], [["‘I said to that boiling-over old Christian,’ Mr Pancks pursued, appearing greatly to relish this descriptive epithet, ‘that I had got a little project on hand; a hopeful one; I told him a hopeful one; which wanted a certain small capital.", "\"제가 그 펄펄 끓는 노기독교인에게 말씀드렸지요,\" 이 묘사적인 호칭을 무척 즐기는 듯한 기색으로 팽크스 씨가 이어 갔다, \"제 손에 작은 사업 하나가 걸려 있는데, 가망 있는 일이라고요—가망 있는 일이라고 말씀드렸습니다—거기에 일정한 소액의 자본이 필요하다고요."], ["I proposed to him to lend me the money on my note.", "제 어음을 담보로 돈을 빌려 달라고 청했지요."], ["Which he did, at twenty; sticking the twenty on in a business-like way, and putting it into the note, to look like a part of the principal.", "그분은 그렇게 해 주셨습니다, 이십 퍼센트에요. 그 이십을 사업가답게 떡하니 붙여서, 마치 원금의 일부인 것처럼 보이도록 어음에 끼워 넣어서 말입니다."], ["If I had broken down after that, I should have been his grubber for the next seven years at half wages and double grind.", "그 후에 제가 망했더라면, 저는 앞으로 칠 년 동안 절반의 임금에 두 배의 노역으로 그분 밑에서 땅이나 파는 신세가 됐을 겁니다."], ["But he’s a perfect Patriarch; and it would do a man good to serve him on such terms--on any terms.’", "하지만 그분은 완벽한 가부장이시지요. 그런 조건으로—어떤 조건으로든 그분을 섬기는 건 사람에게 이로운 일일 겁니다.\""]], [["Arthur for his life could not have said with confidence whether Pancks really thought so or not.", "아서로서는, 죽었다 깨어나도, 팽크스가 정말로 그렇게 생각하는지 아닌지를 자신 있게 말할 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["‘When that was gone, sir,’ resumed Pancks, ‘and it did go, though I dribbled it out like so much blood, I had taken Mr Rugg into the secret.", "\"그게 다 떨어지자, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 다시 말을 이었다, \"정말이지 피처럼 한 방울 한 방울 흘리듯 썼는데도 그것이 다 떨어지자, 저는 럭 씨를 비밀에 끌어들였습니다."], ["I proposed to borrow of Mr Rugg (or of Miss Rugg; it’s the same thing; she made a little money by a speculation in the Common Pleas once).", "럭 씨에게서(혹은 럭 양에게서, 같은 얘기지요. 그 여자는 언젠가 민사 법원에서 한 건 투기로 돈을 좀 벌었거든요) 빌리자고 제안했지요."], ["He lent it at ten, and thought that pretty high.", "그분은 십 퍼센트에 빌려주면서도 그게 꽤 높다고 생각하시더군요."], ["But Mr Rugg’s a red-haired man, sir, and gets his hair cut.", "하지만 럭 씨는 빨강 머리 사내라서, 선생님, 머리를 깎고 다니십니다."], ["And as to the crown of his hat, it’s high.", "모자의 우뚝한 부분으로 말하자면, 높다란데요."], ["And as to the brim of his hat, it’s narrow.", "모자의 챙으로 말하자면, 좁고요."], ["And there’s no more benevolence bubbling out of him, than out of a ninepin.’", "그리고 그분에게서 부글부글 솟아나는 자비라곤, 볼링 핀에서 솟아나는 것만큼도 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Your own recompense for all this, Mr Pancks,’ said Clennam, ‘ought to be a large one.’", "\"이 모든 일에 대한 자네 자신의 보수는, 팽크스 씨,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당연히 후해야겠군.\""]], [["‘I don’t mistrust getting it, sir,’ said Pancks.", "\"그걸 못 받으리라곤 걱정하지 않습니다, 선생님,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I have made no bargain.", "\"흥정 같은 건 안 했어요."], ["I owed you one on that score; now I have paid it.", "그 점에서 저는 당신께 빚이 하나 있었는데, 이제 그걸 갚은 겁니다."], ["Money out of pocket made good, time fairly allowed for, and Mr Rugg’s bill settled, a thousand pounds would be a fortune to me.", "주머니에서 나간 돈을 메우고, 들인 시간을 정당하게 쳐 주고, 럭 씨의 청구서를 정산하고 나면, 천 파운드면 제겐 큰 재산이 될 겁니다."], ["That matter I place in your hands.", "그 문제는 당신 손에 맡기겠습니다."], ["I authorize you now to break all this to the family in any way you think best.", "이제 저는 당신께, 당신이 가장 좋다고 여기는 어떤 방식으로든 이 모든 것을 가족에게 알릴 권한을 드립니다."], ["Miss Amy Dorrit will be with Mrs Finching this morning.", "에이미 도릿 양은 오늘 아침 핀칭 부인 댁에 있을 겁니다."], ["The sooner done the better.", "빠를수록 좋아요."], ["Can’t be done too soon.’", "아무리 빨라도 지나치지 않습니다.\""]], [["This conversation took place in Clennam’s bed-room, while he was yet in bed.", "이 대화는 클레넘의 침실에서, 그가 아직 자리에 누워 있을 때 오갔다."], ["For Mr Pancks had knocked up the house and made his way in, very early in the morning; and, without once sitting down or standing still, had delivered himself of the whole of his details (illustrated with a variety of documents) at the bedside.", "팽크스 씨가 이른 아침에 집안을 깨워 들어왔던 것이고, 한 번도 앉거나 가만히 서 있지도 않은 채, 침대 곁에서 자신의 모든 세부 사항을(갖가지 서류로 뒷받침하며) 늘어놓았던 것이다."], ["He now said he would ‘go and look up Mr Rugg’, from whom his excited state of mind appeared to require another back; and bundling up his papers, and exchanging one more hearty shake of the hand with Clennam, he went at full speed down-stairs, and steamed off.", "이제 그는 \"가서 럭 씨를 찾아봐야겠다\"고 말했는데, 흥분한 그의 마음 상태는 또 다른 후원자를 필요로 하는 듯했다. 그러고는 서류를 챙겨 묶고, 클레넘과 한 번 더 힘찬 악수를 나눈 뒤, 전속력으로 계단을 내려가 증기를 내뿜듯 떠나갔다."]], [["Clennam, of course, resolved to go direct to Mr Casby’s.", "클레넘은 물론 곧장 캐스비 씨 댁으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["He dressed and got out so quickly that he found himself at the corner of the patriarchal street nearly an hour before her time; but he was not sorry to have the opportunity of calming himself with a leisurely walk.", "그는 어찌나 빨리 옷을 입고 나섰던지, 그 가부장의 거리 모퉁이에 그녀가 올 시간보다 거의 한 시간이나 일찍 도착해 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그러나 한가로이 거닐며 마음을 가라앉힐 기회가 생긴 것이 싫지는 않았다."]], [["When he returned to the street, and had knocked at the bright brass knocker, he was informed that she had come, and was shown up-stairs to Flora’s breakfast-room.", "그가 그 거리로 돌아와 번쩍이는 놋쇠 문고리를 두드리자, 그녀가 와 있다는 말을 들었고, 위층 플로라의 아침 식사 방으로 안내되었다."], ["Little Dorrit was not there herself, but Flora was, and testified the greatest amazement at seeing him.", "리틀 도릿 본인은 거기 없었지만 플로라는 있었고, 그를 보고 더없이 큰 놀라움을 드러냈다."]], [["‘Good gracious, Arthur--Doyce and Clennam!’ cried that lady, ‘who would have ever thought of seeing such a sight as this and pray excuse a wrapper for upon my word I really never and a faded check too which is worse but our little friend is making me, not that I need mind mentioning it to you for you must know that there are such things a skirt, and having arranged that a trying on should take place after breakfast is the reason though I wish not so badly starched.’", "\"어머나 세상에, 아서—도이스 앤드 클레넘!\" 그 부인이 외쳤다, \"이런 광경을 보게 되리라고 누가 상상이나 했을까요 그리고 부디 이 실내복을 양해해 주세요 정말이지 제가 결코 게다가 빛바랜 체크무늬라 그게 더 나쁜데 하지만 우리 작은 친구가 저한테 만들어 주고 있어요, 당신께 이런 걸 언급할 필요까지야 없지만 당신도 아셔야 하니까 그런 게 있잖아요 스커트라는 거 말이에요, 그리고 아침 식사 후에 가봉을 하기로 정해 둔 게 그 이유인데 그래도 풀이 이렇게 심하게 먹지 않았으면 싶어요.\""]], [["‘I ought to make an apology,’ said Arthur, ‘for so early and abrupt a visit; but you will excuse it when I tell you the cause.’", "\"제가 사과를 드려야겠습니다,\" 아서가 말했다, \"이렇게 이르고 불쑥한 방문에 대해서요. 하지만 그 까닭을 말씀드리면 양해해 주실 겁니다.\""]], [["‘In times for ever fled Arthur,’ returned Mrs Finching, ‘pray excuse me Doyce and Clennam infinitely more correct and though unquestionably distant still ‘tis distance lends enchantment to the view, at least I don’t mean that and if I did I suppose it would depend considerably on the nature of the view, but I’m running on again and you put it all out of my head.’", "\"영영 가 버린 시절에는 아서,\" 핀칭 부인이 대꾸했다, \"부디 양해해 주세요 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 훨씬 더 옳지요 그리고 의심할 바 없이 거리가 멀긴 해도 여전히 멀어지면 풍경이 매혹적으로 보이는 법이지요, 적어도 그런 뜻은 아니었고 설령 그렇다 해도 그건 풍경의 성질에 상당히 달려 있을 것 같은데, 하지만 제가 또 주절거리고 있네요 게다가 당신이 제 머릿속을 온통 헝클어 놓으셔서요.\""]], [["She glanced at him tenderly, and resumed:", "그녀는 다정하게 그를 흘끗 보고는 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["‘In times for ever fled I was going to say it would have sounded strange indeed for Arthur Clennam--Doyce and Clennam naturally quite different--to make apologies for coming here at any time, but that is past and what is past can never be recalled except in his own case as poor Mr F. said when he was in spirits Cucumber and therefore never ate it.’", "\"영영 가 버린 시절에는 이런 말을 하려던 거였어요, 아서 클레넘이—도이스 앤드 클레넘은 물론 사뭇 다르지만—아무 때나 여기 오는 것에 대해 사과를 한다는 게 정말 이상하게 들렸겠지만, 하지만 그건 지난 일이고 지난 일은 결코 되돌릴 수 없지요, 가엾은 F 씨가 기분이 좋을 때 오이는 그렇다고 말씀하시고는 그래서 결코 먹지 않으셨던 그 경우만 빼고요.\""]], [["She was making the tea when Arthur came in, and now hastily finished that operation.", "아서가 들어왔을 때 그녀는 차를 우리고 있던 참이었고, 이제 서둘러 그 일을 끝냈다."]], [["‘Papa,’ she said, all mystery and whisper, as she shut down the tea-pot lid, ‘is sitting prosingly breaking his new laid egg in the back parlour over the City article exactly like the Woodpecker Tapping and need never know that you are here, and our little friend you are well aware may be fully trusted when she comes down from cutting out on the large table overhead.’", "\"아빠는요,\" 그녀가 찻주전자 뚜껑을 탁 덮으며, 온통 비밀스럽고 속삭이는 투로 말했다, \"뒷방에서 시티 기사를 읽으며 새로 낳은 달걀을 깨고 계세요 꼭 딱따구리가 톡톡 쪼는 것처럼요 그러니 당신이 여기 있는 걸 결코 아실 필요가 없고, 우리 작은 친구는 당신도 잘 아시다시피 위층 큰 탁자에서 재단을 마치고 내려오면 완전히 믿어도 되는 사람이지요.\""]], [["Arthur then told her, in the fewest words, that it was their little friend he came to see; and what he had to announce to their little friend.", "그러고 나서 아서는, 더없이 적은 말로 그녀에게, 자신이 만나러 온 것이 바로 그들의 작은 친구라는 것과, 그 작은 친구에게 알려야 할 것이 무엇인지를 일러 주었다."], ["At which astounding intelligence, Flora clasped her hands, fell into a tremble, and shed tears of sympathy and pleasure, like the good-natured creature she really was.", "그 놀라운 소식에, 플로라는 두 손을 맞잡고 부르르 떨더니, 정말로 본바탕이 착한 사람답게 동정과 기쁨의 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["‘For gracious sake let me get out of the way first,’ said Flora, putting her hands to her ears and moving towards the door, ‘or I know I shall go off dead and screaming and make everybody worse, and the dear little thing only this morning looking so nice and neat and good and yet so poor and now a fortune is she really and deserves it too!", "\"제발 부탁이니 제가 먼저 자리를 피하게 해 주세요,\" 플로라가 두 손을 귀에 갖다 대고 문 쪽으로 움직이며 말했다, \"안 그러면 분명 저는 죽은 듯이 비명을 지르며 쓰러져서 모두를 더 곤란하게 만들 거예요, 그리고 그 사랑스러운 어린것이 바로 오늘 아침만 해도 그렇게 곱고 단정하고 착해 보였는데 그러면서도 그토록 가난했는데 이제 재산이 생긴다니 정말로요 게다가 받을 자격도 충분하고요!"], ["and might I mention it to Mr F.’s Aunt Arthur not Doyce and Clennam for this once or if objectionable not on any account.’", "그리고 이번 한 번만 F 씨의 고모님께 제가 이걸 말씀드려도 될까요 아서 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 아니면 곤란하다면 무슨 일이 있어도 안 하고요.\""]], [["Arthur nodded his free permission, since Flora shut out all verbal communication.", "아서는 흔쾌히 허락한다는 뜻으로 고개를 끄덕였는데, 플로라가 말로 하는 의사소통을 죄다 차단해 버렸기 때문이었다."], ["Flora nodded in return to thank him, and hurried out of the room.", "플로라는 답례로 고개를 끄덕여 고마움을 표하고는 서둘러 방을 나갔다."]], [["Little Dorrit’s step was already on the stairs, and in another moment she was at the door.", "리틀 도릿의 발소리는 이미 계단에 들려 왔고, 다음 순간 그녀가 문 앞에 와 있었다."], ["Do what he could to compose his face, he could not convey so much of an ordinary expression into it, but that the moment she saw it she dropped her work, and cried, ‘Mr Clennam!", "그가 아무리 얼굴을 차분히 하려 해도, 그 표정에 평범한 기색을 충분히 담아 넣을 수는 없었던지라, 그녀는 그 얼굴을 보는 순간 하던 일을 떨어뜨리며 외쳤다. \"클레넘 씨!"], ["What’s the matter?’", "무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["‘Nothing, nothing.", "\"아무 일도, 아무 일도 아닙니다."], ["That is, no misfortune has happened.", "그러니까, 불행한 일이 일어난 건 아니에요."], ["I have come to tell you something, but it is a piece of great good-fortune.’", "무언가 말씀드리러 왔습니다만, 그건 큰 행운에 관한 것입니다.\""]], [["‘Good-fortune?’", "\"행운이라고요?\""]], [["‘Wonderful fortune!’", "\"놀라운 행운이지요!\""]], [["They stood in a window, and her eyes, full of light, were fixed upon his face.", "그들은 창가에 서 있었고, 빛으로 가득한 그녀의 두 눈은 그의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["He put an arm about her, seeing her likely to sink down.", "그는 그녀가 주저앉을 것 같아 한 팔로 그녀를 감쌌다."], ["She put a hand upon that arm, partly to rest upon it, and partly so to preserve their relative positions as that her intent look at him should be shaken by no change of attitude in either of them.", "그녀는 한 손을 그 팔에 얹었는데, 한편으로는 그 팔에 기대기 위해서였고, 한편으로는 둘 중 누구의 자세도 바뀌어 그를 향한 자신의 골똘한 시선이 흔들리는 일이 없도록 둘의 상대적 위치를 그대로 지키기 위해서였다. 그녀의 입술이 \"놀라운 행운이라고요?\""], ["Her lips seemed to repeat ‘Wonderful fortune?’ He repeated it again, aloud.", "하고 되뇌는 듯했다. 그는 그것을 다시, 소리 내어 되풀이했다."]], [["‘Dear Little Dorrit!", "\"사랑하는 리틀 도릿!"], ["Your father.’", "당신 아버님 말입니다.\""]], [["The ice of the pale face broke at the word, and little lights and shoots of expression passed all over it.", "그 말에 창백한 얼굴의 얼음이 깨지며, 자잘한 빛과 표정의 떨림이 얼굴 전체에 번져 갔다."], ["They were all expressions of pain.", "그것들은 모두 고통의 표정이었다."], ["Her breath was faint and hurried.", "그녀의 숨결은 희미하고 가빴다."], ["Her heart beat fast.", "그녀의 심장은 빠르게 뛰었다."], ["He would have clasped the little figure closer, but he saw that the eyes appealed to him not to be moved.", "그는 그 작은 몸을 더 가까이 끌어안고 싶었지만, 그 두 눈이 자기에게, 움직이지 말아 달라고 호소하는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘Your father can be free within this week.", "\"당신 아버님은 이번 주 안에 자유로워지실 수 있습니다."], ["He does not know it; we must go to him from here, to tell him of it.", "그분은 그걸 모르세요. 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 합니다."], ["Your father will be free within a few days.", "당신 아버님은 며칠 안에 자유로워지실 겁니다."], ["Your father will be free within a few hours.", "당신 아버님은 몇 시간 안에 자유로워지실 겁니다."], ["Remember we must go to him from here, to tell him of it!’", "기억하세요, 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 한다는 걸요!\""]], [["That brought her back.", "그 말이 그녀를 정신 들게 했다."], ["Her eyes were closing, but they opened again.", "그녀의 눈이 감기려 했지만, 다시 떠졌다."]], [["‘This is not all the good-fortune.", "\"이게 다가 아닙니다."], ["This is not all the wonderful good-fortune, my dear Little Dorrit.", "이 놀라운 행운이 이게 다가 아니에요, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿."], ["Shall I tell you more?’", "더 말씀드릴까요?\""]], [["Her lips shaped ‘Yes.’", "그녀의 입술이 \"네\"라는 모양을 지었다."]], [["‘Your father will be no beggar when he is free.", "\"당신 아버님은 자유로워지셔도 거지가 되지 않으실 겁니다."], ["He will want for nothing.", "아무것도 부족함이 없으실 거예요."], ["Shall I tell you more?", "더 말씀드릴까요?"], ["Remember!", "기억하세요!"], ["He knows nothing of it; we must go to him, from here, to tell him of it!’", "그분은 이 일을 전혀 모르세요. 우리가 여기서 그분께 가서 알려 드려야 합니다!\""]], [["She seemed to entreat him for a little time.", "그녀는 그에게 잠시만 시간을 달라고 간청하는 듯했다."], ["He held her in his arm, and, after a pause, bent down his ear to listen.", "그는 한 팔로 그녀를 안고 있다가, 잠시 후 귀를 기울이려 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘Did you ask me to go on?’", "\"계속하라고 하셨나요?\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘He will be a rich man.", "\"그분은 부자가 되실 겁니다."], ["He is a rich man.", "이미 부자이시고요."], ["A great sum of money is waiting to be paid over to him as his inheritance; you are all henceforth very wealthy.", "상속분으로 그분께 지급되기를 기다리고 있는 큰돈이 있습니다. 당신들은 이제부터 모두 대단한 부자예요."], ["Bravest and best of children, I thank Heaven that you are rewarded!’", "가장 용감하고 가장 착한 자식이여, 당신이 보상을 받게 되어 하늘에 감사합니다!\""]], [["As he kissed her, she turned her head towards his shoulder, and raised her arm towards his neck; cried out ‘Father!", "그가 그녀에게 입을 맞추자, 그녀는 머리를 그의 어깨 쪽으로 돌리고 팔을 그의 목 쪽으로 들어 올리며 \"아버지! 아버지!"], ["Father!", "아버지!\""], ["Father!’ and swooned away.", "하고 외치고는 정신을 잃고 쓰러졌다."]], [["Upon which Flora returned to take care of her, and hovered about her on a sofa, intermingling kind offices and incoherent scraps of conversation in a manner so confounding, that whether she pressed the Marshalsea to take a spoonful of unclaimed dividends, for it would do her good; or whether she congratulated Little Dorrit’s father on coming into possession of a hundred thousand smelling-bottles; or whether she explained that she put seventy-five thousand drops of spirits of lavender on fifty thousand pounds of lump sugar, and that she entreated Little Dorrit to take that gentle restorative; or whether she bathed the foreheads of Doyce and Clennam in vinegar, and gave the late Mr F.", "이에 플로라가 그녀를 돌보러 돌아와, 소파에 누인 그녀 곁을 맴돌며, 친절한 보살핌과 두서없는 말 조각들을 어찌나 헷갈리게 뒤섞던지, 그녀가 마셜시더러 임자 없는 배당금을 한 숟갈 떠먹으라고 권하는 것인지—그게 그녀에게 이로울 테니—아니면 리틀 도릿의 아버지가 십만 개의 냄새 맡는 약병을 손에 넣게 된 것을 축하하는 것인지, 아니면 자기가 라벤더 정기 칠만 오천 방울을 오만 파운드의 각설탕에 떨어뜨렸다고, 그러니 리틀 도릿더러 그 순한 강장제를 들라고 간청하는 것인지, 아니면 도이스 앤드 클레넘의 이마를 식초로 적시며 고인이 된 F 씨에게 바람을 더 넣어 주는 것인지, 어느 정도라도 책임감이 있는 사람이라면 누구도 감히 판가름하려 들 수 없었을 것이다."], ["more air; no one with any sense of responsibility could have undertaken to decide.", "게다가 옆방에서도 혼란의 지류 한 줄기가 흘러들었는데, 그 방에서는 F 씨의 고모가 목소리로 미루어 보아, 아침 식사를 기다리며 수평으로 누운 자세를 취하고 있는 듯했다."], ["A tributary stream of confusion, moreover, poured in from an adjoining bedroom, where Mr F.’s Aunt appeared, from the sound of her voice, to be in a horizontal posture, awaiting her breakfast; and from which bower that inexorable lady snapped off short taunts, whenever she could get a hearing, as, ‘Don’t believe it’s his doing!’ and ‘He needn’t take no credit to himself for it!’ and ‘It’ll be long enough, I expect, afore he’ll give up any of his own money!’ all designed to disparage Clennam’s share in the discovery, and to relieve those inveterate feelings with which Mr F.’s Aunt regarded him.", "그리고 그 규방에서 그 가차 없는 부인은 자기 말이 들릴 만하면 언제든 짤막한 조롱을 톡톡 쏘아붙였으니, 가령 \"저자가 한 일이라고 믿지 마!\"라든가 \"저자가 그 일로 공치사 받을 거 없어!\"라든가 \"내 짐작엔, 저자가 제 돈을 한 푼이라도 내놓을 때까진 어지간히도 오래 걸릴걸!\" 따위였고, 이 모든 것은 그 발견에서 클레넘이 한 몫을 깎아내리고, F 씨의 고모가 그를 향해 품은 그 뿌리 깊은 감정을 풀어내려는 것이었다."]], [["But Little Dorrit’s solicitude to get to her father, and to carry the joyful tidings to him, and not to leave him in his jail a moment with this happiness in store for him and still unknown to him, did more for her speedy restoration than all the skill and attention on earth could have done.", "그러나 아버지에게 가고 싶고, 그 기쁜 소식을 그에게 전하고 싶고, 그를 위해 마련된 이 행복을 그가 아직 모른 채로 감옥 안에 단 한순간도 두고 싶지 않은 리틀 도릿의 절박한 마음은, 세상의 그 어떤 솜씨와 보살핌도 해낼 수 없을 만큼 그녀를 빠르게 회복시켰다."], ["‘Come with me to my dear father.", "\"저랑 같이 제 사랑하는 아버지께 가요."], ["Pray come and tell my dear father!’ were the first words she said.", "부디 가서 제 사랑하는 아버지께 말씀드려 주세요!\" 이것이 그녀가 한 첫마디였다."], ["Her father, her father.", "아버지, 아버지."], ["She spoke of nothing but him, thought of nothing but him.", "그녀는 그분 말고는 아무것도 말하지 않았고, 그분 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다."], ["Kneeling down and pouring out her thankfulness with uplifted hands, her thanks were for her father.", "무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜든 채 감사의 마음을 쏟아 내는데, 그 감사는 아버지를 위한 것이었다."]], [["Flora’s tenderness was quite overcome by this, and she launched out among the cups and saucers into a wonderful flow of tears and speech.", "플로라의 다정한 마음은 이에 완전히 무너져, 그녀는 찻잔과 받침 접시들 사이에서 놀라운 눈물과 말의 홍수 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["‘I declare,’ she sobbed, ‘I never was so cut up since your mama and my papa not Doyce and Clennam for this once but give the precious little thing a cup of tea and make her put it to her lips at least pray Arthur do, not even Mr F.’s last illness for that was of another kind and gout is not a child’s affection though very painful for all parties and Mr F.", "\"정말이지,\""], ["a martyr with his leg upon a rest and the wine trade in itself inflammatory for they will do it more or less among themselves and who can wonder, it seems like a dream I am sure to think of nothing at all this morning and now Mines of money is it really, but you must know my darling love because you never will be strong enough to tell him all about it upon teaspoons, mightn’t it be even best to try the directions of my own medical man for though the flavour is anything but agreeable still I force myself to do it as a prescription and find the benefit, you’d rather not why no my dear I’d rather not but still I do it as a duty, everybody will congratulate you some in earnest and some not and many will congratulate you with all their hearts but none more so I do assure you from the bottom of my own I do myself though sensible of blundering and being stupid, and will be judged by Arthur not Doyce and Clennam for this once so good-bye darling and God bless you and may you be very happy and excuse the liberty, vowing that the dress shall never be finished by anybody else but shall be laid by for a keepsake just as it is and called Little Dorrit though why that strangest of denominations at any time I never did myself and now I never shall!’", "그녀가 흐느꼈다, \"당신 엄마랑 우리 아빠 이후로 이렇게 가슴이 미어진 적이 없어요 이번 한 번만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 하지만 이 귀한 어린것한테 차 한 잔 주고 적어도 입술에라도 대게 해 줘요 부디 아서 그래 줘요, F 씨의 마지막 병환 때조차 이렇진 않았는데 그건 종류가 다른 것이었고 통풍은 어린아이의 병이 아니지만 모두에게 무척 고통스러운 거예요 그리고 F 씨는 다리를 받침대에 올려놓고 순교자였지요 게다가 포도주 거래라는 게 그 자체로 염증을 일으키는 일이라 자기들끼리 어느 정도는 마시기 마련이고 누가 그걸 의아해하겠어요, 오늘 아침 아무것도 아닌 일을 생각하니 정말 꿈만 같은데 이제 돈더미라니 정말로요, 하지만 당신은 알아야 해요 내 사랑하는 이여 왜냐하면 당신은 결코 그분께 찻숟갈로 떠먹이듯 그 모든 걸 다 말씀드릴 만큼 튼튼하지 못할 테니까요, 차라리 우리 주치의 선생님의 처방을 시험해 보는 게 제일 나을지도 모르겠어요 비록 그 맛이 도무지 좋지 않지만 그래도 저는 처방으로 알고 억지로 마셔서 효험을 보거든요, 안 마시고 싶다고요 아니 그래요 내 사랑 저도 마시고 싶진 않지만 그래도 의무로 알고 마셔요, 다들 당신을 축하해 줄 거예요 어떤 이는 진심으로 어떤 이는 진심 아니게 그리고 많은 이가 마음을 다해 축하해 줄 테지만 제 마음 밑바닥에서 우러나오는 저보다 더한 사람은 없을 거예요 정말이지요 비록 서툴고 어리석은 걸 알지만요, 그리고 이번 한 번만 도이스 앤드 클레넘이 아니라 아서가 판단해 줄 거예요 그러니 잘 가요 내 사랑 하느님의 축복이 있기를 그리고 부디 아주 행복하기를 그리고 이 무례를 용서해 줘요, 맹세컨대 이 옷은 다른 누구도 결코 완성하지 못하게 하고 지금 이대로 기념품으로 간직해서 리틀 도릿이라고 부를 거예요 비록 어째서 그 이상한 호칭인지는 그동안 저도 통 알 수 없었고 이제 영영 모르겠지만요!\""]], [["Thus Flora, in taking leave of her favourite.", "이렇게 플로라는 자신이 아끼는 아이와 작별했다."], ["Little Dorrit thanked her, and embraced her, over and over again; and finally came out of the house with Clennam, and took coach for the Marshalsea.", "리틀 도릿은 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고, 몇 번이고 거듭 그녀를 끌어안았다. 그러고는 마침내 클레넘과 함께 집을 나서, 마셜시로 가는 마차에 올랐다."]], [["It was a strangely unreal ride through the old squalid streets, with a sensation of being raised out of them into an airy world of wealth and grandeur.", "낡고 누추한 거리들을 지나는 그 마차길은 묘하게 비현실적이었으니, 마치 그 거리들 위로 들어 올려져 부와 영화의 공중 세계로 떠오르는 듯한 감각이 들었다."], ["When Arthur told her that she would soon ride in her own carriage through very different scenes, when all the familiar experiences would have vanished away, she looked frightened.", "아서가 그녀에게, 머지않아 자신의 마차를 타고 사뭇 다른 풍경 속을 지나게 될 것이며 그때면 익숙한 경험들이 모조리 사라져 버릴 거라고 말하자, 그녀는 겁에 질린 표정을 지었다."], ["But when he substituted her father for herself, and told her how he would ride in his carriage, and how great and grand he would be, her tears of joy and innocent pride fell fast.", "그러나 그가 그녀 대신 아버지를 주어로 바꾸어, 아버지가 마차를 타고 가시는 모습이며, 그분이 얼마나 위대하고 당당하실지를 일러 주자, 기쁨과 순진한 자부심의 눈물이 그녀의 뺨을 빠르게 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["Seeing that the happiness her mind could realise was all shining upon him, Arthur kept that single figure before her; and so they rode brightly through the poor streets in the prison neighbourhood to carry him the great news.", "그녀의 마음이 실감할 수 있는 행복이라곤 죄다 그분께만 비치고 있음을 본 아서는, 그 한 사람의 모습만을 그녀 앞에 계속 두었다. 그리하여 그들은 감옥 인근의 가난한 거리들을 환한 마음으로 달려, 그에게 그 큰 소식을 전하러 갔다."]], [["When Mr Chivery, who was on duty, admitted them into the Lodge, he saw something in their faces which filled him with astonishment.", "당번을 서던 치버리 씨가 그들을 문지기 방으로 들여보냈을 때, 그는 그들의 얼굴에서 자신을 놀라움으로 가득 채우는 무언가를 보았다."], ["He stood looking after them, when they hurried into the prison, as though he perceived that they had come back accompanied by a ghost a-piece.", "그들이 서둘러 감옥 안으로 들어가는 동안, 그는 마치 그들이 각자 유령을 하나씩 데리고 돌아온 것을 알아챈 것처럼 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Two or three Collegians whom they passed, looked after them too, and presently joining Mr Chivery, formed a little group on the Lodge steps, in the midst of which there spontaneously originated a whisper that the Father was going to get his discharge.", "그들이 지나친 두세 명의 수감자들도 그들의 뒷모습을 바라보더니, 곧 치버리 씨에게 합류해 문지기 방 계단에 작은 무리를 이루었고, 그 한가운데서 아버지가 석방을 받게 된다는 속삭임이 저절로 생겨났다."], ["Within a few minutes, it was heard in the remotest room in the College.", "몇 분 안에 그 소식은 그 수용소의 가장 외진 방에서까지 들려왔다."]], [["Little Dorrit opened the door from without, and they both entered.", "리틀 도릿이 밖에서 문을 열어, 두 사람은 함께 안으로 들어갔다."], ["He was sitting in his old grey gown and his old black cap, in the sunlight by the window, reading his newspaper.", "그는 낡은 회색 가운에 낡은 검은 모자 차림으로, 창가의 햇살 속에 앉아 신문을 읽고 있었다."], ["His glasses were in his hand, and he had just looked round; surprised at first, no doubt, by her step upon the stairs, not expecting her until night; surprised again, by seeing Arthur Clennam in her company.", "그의 안경은 손에 들려 있었고, 그는 막 둘러본 참이었다. 분명 처음에는 밤이 되어서야 그녀가 오리라 여겼던 터라 계단의 그녀 발소리에 놀랐을 것이고, 다시 한번 그녀가 아서 클레넘과 함께 온 것을 보고 놀랐을 것이다."], ["As they came in, the same unwonted look in both of them which had already caught attention in the yard below, struck him.", "그들이 들어서자, 이미 아래층 마당에서 주목을 끌었던 두 사람 모두의 그 예사롭지 않은 기색이 그를 사로잡았다."], ["He did not rise or speak, but laid down his glasses and his newspaper on the table beside him, and looked at them with his mouth a little open and his lips trembling.", "그는 일어서지도 입을 열지도 않은 채, 안경과 신문을 곁의 탁자에 내려놓고는, 입을 조금 벌리고 입술을 떨며 그들을 바라보았다."], ["When Arthur put out his hand, he touched it, but not with his usual state; and then he turned to his daughter, who had sat down close beside him with her hands upon his shoulder, and looked attentively in her face.", "아서가 손을 내밀자 그는 그 손을 만졌지만, 평소의 위엄 있는 태도로는 아니었다. 그러고 나서 그는 자기 곁에 바싹 앉아 두 손을 그의 어깨에 얹은 딸 쪽으로 돌아서서, 그녀의 얼굴을 골똘히 들여다보았다."]], [["‘Father!", "\"아버지!"], ["I have been made so happy this morning!’", "저는 오늘 아침 너무도 행복해졌어요!\""]], [["‘You have been made so happy, my dear?’", "\"네가 그토록 행복해졌다고, 얘야?\""]], [["‘By Mr Clennam, father.", "\"클레넘 씨 덕분이에요, 아버지."], ["He brought me such joyful and wonderful intelligence about you!", "그분이 아버지에 관해서 그토록 기쁘고 놀라운 소식을 가져다주셨어요!"], ["If he had not with his great kindness and gentleness, prepared me for it, father--prepared me for it, father--I think I could not have borne it.’", "그분이 그 큰 친절과 다정함으로 제가 그걸 받아들일 마음의 준비를 시켜 주지 않으셨다면, 아버지—제가 그걸 받아들일 준비를 시켜 주지 않으셨다면, 아버지—저는 그걸 견뎌 내지 못했을 것 같아요.\""]], [["Her agitation was exceedingly great, and the tears rolled down her face.", "그녀의 동요는 더없이 컸고, 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흘러내렸다."], ["He put his hand suddenly to his heart, and looked at Clennam.", "그는 불쑥 손을 가슴에 갖다 대고 클레넘을 바라보았다."]], [["‘Compose yourself, sir,’ said Clennam, ‘and take a little time to think.", "\"마음을 가라앉히십시오, 선생님,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"그리고 잠시 시간을 두고 생각하세요."], ["To think of the brightest and most fortunate accidents of life.", "인생에서 가장 밝고 가장 운 좋은 우연들을 생각해 보십시오."], ["We have all heard of great surprises of joy.", "우리는 모두 기쁨의 큰 놀라움에 관해 들어 본 적이 있지요."], ["They are not at an end, sir.", "그런 일은 끝난 게 아닙니다, 선생님."], ["They are rare, but not at an end.’", "드물기는 해도, 끝난 게 아니에요.\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam?", "\"클레넘 씨?"], ["Not at an end?", "끝난 게 아니라고요?"], ["Not at an end for--’ He touched himself upon the breast, instead of saying ‘me.’", "끝난 게 아니라니 누구한테—\" 그는 \"나\"라고 말하는 대신 자기 가슴을 짚었다."]], [["‘No,’ returned Clennam.", "\"그렇습니다,\" 클레넘이 대답했다."]], [["‘What surprise,’ he asked, keeping his left hand over his heart, and there stopping in his speech, while with his right hand he put his glasses exactly level on the table: ‘what such surprise can be in store for me?’", "\"무슨 놀라움이,\" 그가 왼손을 가슴에 댄 채 거기서 말을 멈추고, 오른손으로는 안경을 탁자 위에 정확히 수평으로 놓으며 물었다, \"무슨 그런 놀라움이 나를 위해 마련되어 있을 수 있다는 말이오?\""]], [["‘Let me answer with another question.", "\"제가 또 다른 질문으로 답하게 해 주십시오."], ["Tell me, Mr Dorrit, what surprise would be the most unlooked for and the most acceptable to you.", "말씀해 보세요, 도릿 씨, 무슨 놀라움이 당신께 가장 뜻밖이고 가장 반가운 것이겠습니까."], ["Do not be afraid to imagine it, or to say what it would be.’", "그것을 상상해 보거나, 그게 무엇일지 말하기를 두려워하지 마십시오.\""]], [["He looked steadfastly at Clennam, and, so looking at him, seemed to change into a very old haggard man.", "그는 클레넘을 뚫어지게 바라보았고, 그렇게 그를 바라보는 사이 몹시 늙고 수척한 사람으로 변해 가는 듯했다."], ["The sun was bright upon the wall beyond the window, and on the spikes at top.", "햇살이 창 너머 담벼락 위에, 그리고 그 꼭대기의 쇠창살 위에 환했다."], ["He slowly stretched out the hand that had been upon his heart, and pointed at the wall.", "그는 가슴에 얹고 있던 손을 천천히 뻗어 그 담을 가리켰다."]], [["‘It is down,’ said Clennam.", "\"그건 무너졌습니다,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘Gone!’", "\"사라졌어요!\""]], [["He remained in the same attitude, looking steadfastly at him.", "그는 같은 자세를 유지한 채, 그를 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘And in its place,’ said Clennam, slowly and distinctly, ‘are the means to possess and enjoy the utmost that they have so long shut out.", "\"그리고 그 자리에는,\" 클레넘이 천천히 또렷하게 말했다, \"그것이 그토록 오래 가로막아 온 모든 것을 손에 넣고 누릴 수단이 마련되어 있습니다."], ["Mr Dorrit, there is not the smallest doubt that within a few days you will be free, and highly prosperous.", "도릿 씨, 며칠 안에 당신이 자유로워지고 크게 번창하게 되리라는 것은 손톱만큼의 의심도 없는 일입니다."], ["I congratulate you with all my soul on this change of fortune, and on the happy future into which you are soon to carry the treasure you have been blest with here--the best of all the riches you can have elsewhere--the treasure at your side.’", "이 운명의 변화에 대해, 그리고 당신이 여기서 축복받은 그 보물을—다른 어디서 가질 수 있는 그 모든 부 가운데 으뜸가는 보물을, 곧 당신 곁의 그 보물을—머지않아 안고 들어서게 될 그 행복한 미래에 대해, 저는 온 영혼을 다해 축하드립니다.\""]], [["With those words, he pressed his hand and released it; and his daughter, laying her face against his, encircled him in the hour of his prosperity with her arms, as she had in the long years of his adversity encircled him with her love and toil and truth; and poured out her full heart in gratitude, hope, joy, blissful ecstasy, and all for him.", "그 말과 함께 그는 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다가 놓아 주었다. 그러자 그의 딸은 자기 얼굴을 아버지의 얼굴에 갖다 대고는, 역경의 긴 세월 동안 사랑과 수고와 진심으로 그를 감싸 안았던 것처럼, 번영의 시간에 두 팔로 그를 감싸 안았다. 그리고 감사와 희망과 기쁨과 더없는 황홀로 가득 찬 마음을 쏟아 냈으니, 그 모든 것이 그를 위한 것이었다."]], [["‘I shall see him as I never saw him yet.", "\"이제껏 한 번도 본 적 없는 모습으로 아버지를 뵐 거예요."], ["I shall see my dear love, with the dark cloud cleared away.", "검은 먹구름이 걷힌 제 사랑하는 분을 뵐 거예요."], ["I shall see him, as my poor mother saw him long ago.", "오래전 제 가엾은 어머니가 아버지를 보았던 그 모습으로 뵐 거예요."], ["O my dear, my dear!", "오 사랑하는 분, 사랑하는 분!"], ["O father, father!", "오 아버지, 아버지!"], ["O thank God, thank God!’", "오 하느님 감사합니다, 하느님 감사합니다!\""]], [["He yielded himself to her kisses and caresses, but did not return them, except that he put an arm about her.", "그는 그녀의 입맞춤과 어루만짐에 몸을 맡겼지만, 그것에 답하지는 않았다. 다만 한 팔로 그녀를 감쌌을 뿐이었다."], ["Neither did he say one word.", "또한 그는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["His steadfast look was now divided between her and Clennam, and he began to shake as if he were very cold.", "그의 뚫어질 듯한 시선은 이제 그녀와 클레넘 사이를 오갔고, 그는 마치 몹시 추운 것처럼 떨기 시작했다."], ["Explaining to Little Dorrit that he would run to the coffee-house for a bottle of wine, Arthur fetched it with all the haste he could use.", "아서는 리틀 도릿에게 자기가 커피점으로 달려가 포도주 한 병을 가져오겠다고 설명하고는, 쓸 수 있는 모든 속력을 다해 그것을 가져왔다."], ["While it was being brought from the cellar to the bar, a number of excited people asked him what had happened; when he hurriedly informed them that Mr Dorrit had succeeded to a fortune.", "술이 지하 저장고에서 카운터로 옮겨지는 동안, 흥분한 여러 사람이 그에게 무슨 일이 있었느냐고 물었고, 그는 서둘러 그들에게 도릿 씨가 재산을 상속받게 되었다고 알렸다."]], [["On coming back with the wine in his hand, he found that she had placed her father in his easy chair, and had loosened his shirt and neckcloth.", "손에 포도주를 들고 돌아와 보니, 그녀는 아버지를 안락의자에 앉히고 셔츠와 목수건을 풀어 놓은 참이었다."], ["They filled a tumbler with wine, and held it to his lips.", "그들은 큰 잔에 포도주를 채워 그의 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["When he had swallowed a little, he took the glass himself and emptied it.", "그가 조금 삼키고 나자, 그는 잔을 직접 받아 들어 비웠다."], ["Soon after that, he leaned back in his chair and cried, with his handkerchief before his face.", "그 직후 그는 의자에 등을 기대고, 손수건으로 얼굴을 가린 채 울었다."]], [["After this had lasted a while Clennam thought it a good season for diverting his attention from the main surprise, by relating its details.", "이것이 한참 이어진 후에, 클레넘은 그 세부 사항을 이야기해 줌으로써 그의 주의를 핵심적인 놀라움에서 다른 데로 돌리기에 좋은 때라고 생각했다."], ["Slowly, therefore, and in a quiet tone of voice, he explained them as best he could, and enlarged on the nature of Pancks’s service.", "그리하여 천천히, 조용한 목소리로, 그는 할 수 있는 한 잘 그것들을 설명하고, 팽크스가 한 일의 성격에 관해 자세히 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘He shall be--ha--he shall be handsomely recompensed, sir,’ said the Father, starting up and moving hurriedly about the room.", "\"그 사람은—하—그 사람은 후하게 보상받아야 하오, 선생,\" 아버지가 벌떡 일어나 서둘러 방 안을 돌아다니며 말했다."], ["‘Assure yourself, Mr Clennam, that everybody concerned shall be--ha--shall be nobly rewarded.", "\"안심하시오, 클레넘 씨, 관련된 모든 이가—하—고귀하게 보답받을 것이오."], ["No one, my dear sir, shall say that he has an unsatisfied claim against me.", "나를 상대로 채워지지 못한 청구권이 있다고 말할 자는 아무도 없을 거요, 선생."], ["I shall repay the--hum--the advances I have had from you, sir, with peculiar pleasure.", "당신께 받은—흠—선불금은 각별한 기쁨으로 갚겠소, 선생."], ["I beg to be informed at your earliest convenience, what advances you have made my son.’", "가장 빠른 편하실 때에, 당신이 내 아들에게 얼마를 빌려주었는지 알려 주시기를 청하오.\""]], [["He had no purpose in going about the room, but he was not still a moment.", "그가 방 안을 돌아다니는 데에 무슨 목적이 있는 것은 아니었지만, 그는 한순간도 가만있지 못했다."]], [["‘Everybody,’ he said, ‘shall be remembered.", "\"모두가,\" 그가 말했다, \"기억될 것이오."], ["I will not go away from here in anybody’s debt.", "나는 누구에게도 빚을 진 채로 여기서 떠나지 않겠소."], ["All the people who have been--ha--well behaved towards myself and my family, shall be rewarded.", "나와 내 가족에게—하—잘 처신해 준 모든 사람이 보답받을 것이오."], ["Chivery shall be rewarded.", "치버리도 보답받을 거요."], ["Young John shall be rewarded.", "젊은 존도 보답받을 거요."], ["I particularly wish, and intend, to act munificently, Mr Clennam.’", "나는 특별히, 후하게 처신하기를 바라고 또 그럴 작정이오, 클레넘 씨.\""]], [["‘Will you allow me,’ said Arthur, laying his purse on the table, ‘to supply any present contingencies, Mr Dorrit?", "\"제가,\" 아서가 지갑을 탁자에 내려놓으며 말했다, \"당장 필요하실 일들에 대비할 돈을 마련해 드려도 되겠습니까, 도릿 씨?"], ["I thought it best to bring a sum of money for the purpose.’", "그러려고 얼마간의 돈을 가져오는 게 가장 좋겠다고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, thank you.", "\"고맙소, 선생, 고맙소."], ["I accept with readiness, at the present moment, what I could not an hour ago have conscientiously taken.", "한 시간 전이라면 양심상 받을 수 없었을 것을, 지금 이 순간에는 기꺼이 받겠소."], ["I am obliged to you for the temporary accommodation.", "임시변통에 신세를 지는구려."], ["Exceedingly temporary, but well timed--well timed.’ His hand had closed upon the money, and he carried it about with him.", "지극히 임시변통이지만, 때를 잘 맞췄소—때를 잘 맞췄어.\" 그의 손이 그 돈을 움켜쥐었고, 그는 그것을 들고 돌아다녔다."], ["‘Be so kind, sir, as to add the amount to those former advances to which I have already referred; being careful, if you please, not to omit advances made to my son.", "\"선생, 부디 그 액수를, 내가 이미 말한 그 이전의 선불금에 더해 주시오. 부탁하건대 내 아들에게 빌려준 것도 빠뜨리지 않도록 유의해 주시고."], ["A mere verbal statement of the gross amount is all I shall--ha--all I shall require.’", "총액을 그저 말로 일러 주는 것이면 다 되오—하—그것이면 다 되오.\""]], [["His eye fell upon his daughter at this point, and he stopped for a moment to kiss her, and to pat her head.", "이때 그의 시선이 딸에게 닿았고, 그는 잠시 멈춰 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 머리를 토닥였다."]], [["‘It will be necessary to find a milliner, my love, and to make a speedy and complete change in your very plain dress.", "\"양재사를 구하고, 네 그 너무도 수수한 옷을 빠르고 완전히 바꿔야겠구나, 얘야."], ["Something must be done with Maggy too, who at present is--ha--barely respectable, barely respectable.", "매기에 대해서도 무언가 해야겠다, 그 애는 지금—하—겨우 봐줄 만하지, 겨우 봐줄 만해."], ["And your sister, Amy, and your brother.", "그리고 네 언니, 에이미, 그리고 네 오빠도."], ["And _my_ brother, your uncle--poor soul, I trust this will rouse him--messengers must be despatched to fetch them.", "그리고 내 동생, 네 삼촌도—가엾은 사람, 이 일이 그를 분발시켰으면 좋겠구나—그들을 데려오라고 심부름꾼을 보내야겠어."], ["They must be informed of this.", "그들에게 이 일을 알려야지."], ["We must break it to them cautiously, but they must be informed directly.", "조심스럽게 알려 줘야겠지만, 곧바로 알려야 한다."], ["We owe it as a duty to them and to ourselves, from this moment, not to let them--hum--not to let them do anything.’", "이 순간부터 그들과 우리 자신에 대한 도리로서, 그들이—흠—그들이 무슨 일도 하지 않게 하는 게 우리의 의무야.\""]], [["This was the first intimation he had ever given, that he was privy to the fact that they did something for a livelihood.", "이것은 그가, 그들이 생계를 위해 무슨 일을 하고 있다는 사실을 자기가 알고 있었음을 처음으로 내비친 것이었다."]], [["He was still jogging about the room, with the purse clutched in his hand, when a great cheering arose in the yard.", "그가 지갑을 손에 움켜쥐고 여전히 방 안을 종종거리며 돌아다니고 있을 때, 마당에서 커다란 환호 소리가 일었다."], ["‘The news has spread already,’ said Clennam, looking down from the window.", "\"소식이 벌써 퍼졌군요,\" 클레넘이 창밖을 내려다보며 말했다."], ["‘Will you show yourself to them, Mr Dorrit?", "\"저들에게 모습을 보여 주시겠습니까, 도릿 씨?"], ["They are very earnest, and they evidently wish it.’", "무척 간절하고, 분명히 그러기를 바라는 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘I--hum--ha--I confess I could have desired, Amy my dear,’ he said, jogging about in a more feverish flutter than before, ‘to have made some change in my dress first, and to have bought a--hum--a watch and chain.", "\"나는—흠—하—솔직히 바라기는, 에이미 얘야,\" 그가 아까보다 더 열에 들뜬 떨림 속에 종종거리며 말했다, \"먼저 옷을 좀 갈아입고, 그리고—흠—시계와 줄을 하나 샀으면 했단다."], ["But if it must be done as it is, it--ha--it must be done.", "하지만 이대로 해야 한다면, 그것은—하—그렇게 해야겠지."], ["Fasten the collar of my shirt, my dear.", "내 셔츠 깃을 채워 다오, 얘야."], ["Mr Clennam, would you oblige me--hum--with a blue neckcloth you will find in that drawer at your elbow.", "클레넘 씨, 부탁드리는데—흠—그 팔꿈치 옆 서랍에 파란 목수건이 있을 거요."], ["Button my coat across at the chest, my love.", "가슴께에서 내 외투를 채워 다오, 얘야."], ["It looks--ha--it looks broader, buttoned.’", "단추를 채우면—하—단추를 채우면 더 넓어 보이는구나.\""]], [["With his trembling hand he pushed his grey hair up, and then, taking Clennam and his daughter for supporters, appeared at the window leaning on an arm of each.", "그는 떨리는 손으로 자신의 회색 머리카락을 위로 쓸어 올린 다음, 클레넘과 딸을 부축꾼으로 삼아 그들의 한쪽 팔에 각각 기댄 채 창가에 모습을 드러냈다."], ["The Collegians cheered him very heartily, and he kissed his hand to them with great urbanity and protection.", "수감자들은 그에게 무척 진심으로 환호했고, 그는 더없이 정중하고 너그러운 태도로 그들에게 손에 입을 맞춰 답례했다."], ["When he withdrew into the room again, he said ‘Poor creatures!’ in a tone of much pity for their miserable condition.", "다시 방 안으로 물러나며 그는 \"가엾은 것들!\" 하고, 그들의 비참한 처지를 몹시 측은히 여기는 어조로 말했다."]], [["Little Dorrit was deeply anxious that he should lie down to compose himself.", "리틀 도릿은 그가 자리에 누워 마음을 가라앉혔으면 하고 몹시 애가 탔다."], ["On Arthur’s speaking to her of his going to inform Pancks that he might now appear as soon as he would, and pursue the joyful business to its close, she entreated him in a whisper to stay with her until her father should be quite calm and at rest.", "아서가 그녀에게, 이제 팽크스가 원하는 대로 곧 나타나서 이 기쁜 일을 마무리 지어도 좋다는 걸 알리러 가겠노라 말하자, 그녀는 아버지가 완전히 차분해져 안정을 찾을 때까지 곁에 있어 달라고 속삭이며 간청했다."], ["He needed no second entreaty; and she prepared her father’s bed, and begged him to lie down.", "그는 두 번 청할 필요도 없었다. 그녀는 아버지의 잠자리를 마련하고는 누우시라고 권했다."], ["For another half-hour or more he would be persuaded to do nothing but go about the room, discussing with himself the probabilities for and against the Marshal’s allowing the whole of the prisoners to go to the windows of the official residence which commanded the street, to see himself and family depart for ever in a carriage--which, he said, he thought would be a Sight for them.", "그러나 그는 또 반 시간 남짓 동안, 방 안을 돌아다니며, 행정관이 모든 수감자에게 거리를 내다볼 수 있는 관사의 창문으로 가서 자신과 가족이 마차를 타고 영영 떠나가는 모습을 보도록 허락할지 안 할지의 가능성을 혼자 따져 보는 것 말고는 무엇도 하려 들지 않았다—그것이 저들에게는 볼만한 구경거리가 될 것 같다고 그는 말했다."], ["But gradually he began to droop and tire, and at last stretched himself upon the bed.", "그러나 점차 그는 기운이 빠지고 지치기 시작하더니, 마침내 침대에 몸을 뉘었다."]], [["She took her faithful place beside him, fanning him and cooling his forehead; and he seemed to be falling asleep (always with the money in his hand), when he unexpectedly sat up and said:", "그녀는 그의 곁 충실한 자리를 차지하고서 부채질을 해 주고 그의 이마를 식혀 주었다. 그가 (여전히 손에 돈을 쥔 채) 잠이 드는 듯하더니, 뜻밖에도 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, I beg your pardon.", "\"클레넘 씨, 실례하오."], ["Am I to understand, my dear sir, that I could--ha--could pass through the Lodge at this moment, and--hum--take a walk?’", "내가 이해하기로는, 선생, 내가—하—지금 이 순간 문지기 방을 통과해서—흠—산책을 좀 할 수 있다는 거요?\""]], [["‘I think not, Mr Dorrit,’ was the unwilling reply.", "\"그건 아닐 듯합니다, 도릿 씨,\" 마지못한 대답이었다."], ["‘There are certain forms to be completed; and although your detention here is now in itself a form, I fear it is one that for a little longer has to be observed too.’", "\"마쳐야 할 어떤 절차들이 있습니다. 그리고 여기 당신의 구금이 이제 그 자체로 하나의 형식에 불과하긴 합니다만, 조금만 더 지켜져야 하는 형식이기도 한 것 같아 우려됩니다.\""]], [["At this he shed tears again.", "이 말에 그는 다시 눈물을 흘렸다."]], [["‘It is but a few hours, sir,’ Clennam cheerfully urged upon him.", "\"불과 몇 시간입니다, 선생님,\" 클레넘이 그에게 명랑하게 다독였다."]], [["‘A few hours, sir,’ he returned in a sudden passion.", "\"몇 시간이라, 선생,\" 그가 불쑥 격해져 대꾸했다."], ["‘You talk very easily of hours, sir!", "\"당신은 시간을 무척 쉽게 말하는구려, 선생!"], ["How long do you suppose, sir, that an hour is to a man who is choking for want of air?’", "공기가 없어 숨이 막혀 가는 사람에게 한 시간이 얼마나 긴 것인지 짐작이나 하시오, 선생?\""]], [["It was his last demonstration for that time; as, after shedding some more tears and querulously complaining that he couldn’t breathe, he slowly fell into a slumber.", "이것이 그때로서는 그의 마지막 격앙이었다. 눈물을 좀 더 흘리고는 숨을 쉴 수가 없다고 푸념하듯 투덜대더니, 그는 서서히 선잠에 빠져들었다."], ["Clennam had abundant occupation for his thoughts, as he sat in the quiet room watching the father on his bed, and the daughter fanning his face.", "클레넘은 조용한 방에 앉아 침대 위의 아버지와, 그 얼굴에 부채질하는 딸을 지켜보며, 생각에 잠길 거리가 넘쳐 났다."]], [["Little Dorrit had been thinking too.", "리틀 도릿도 생각에 잠겨 있었다."], ["After softly putting his grey hair aside, and touching his forehead with her lips, she looked towards Arthur, who came nearer to her, and pursued in a low whisper the subject of her thoughts.", "그의 회색 머리카락을 살며시 옆으로 쓸어 넘기고 그의 이마에 입술을 갖다 댄 뒤, 그녀는 아서 쪽을 바라보았고, 그가 그녀에게 더 가까이 다가오자 낮은 속삭임으로 자기 생각의 갈래를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, will he pay all his debts before he leaves here?’", "\"클레넘 씨, 아버지는 여기를 떠나시기 전에 빚을 다 갚으실까요?\""]], [["‘No doubt.", "\"틀림없이요."], ["All.’", "전부 다요.\""]], [["‘All the debts for which he had been imprisoned here, all my life and longer?’", "\"여기 갇히게 만든 그 모든 빚을, 제 평생 내내, 아니 그보다 더 오래된 그 빚을 다요?\""]], [["‘No doubt.’", "\"틀림없이요.\""]], [["There was something of uncertainty and remonstrance in her look; something that was not all satisfaction.", "그녀의 표정에는 무언가 미심쩍어하고 항변하는 듯한 것이, 온전한 만족만은 아닌 무언가가 어려 있었다."], ["He wondered to detect it, and said:", "그는 그것을 알아채고 의아해하며 말했다."]], [["‘You are glad that he should do so?’", "\"아버님이 그러시는 게 기쁘지 않으신가요?\""]], [["‘Are you?’ asked Little Dorrit, wistfully.", "\"당신은요?\" 리틀 도릿이 아쉬운 듯 물었다."]], [["‘Am I?", "\"제가요?"], ["Most heartily glad!’", "더없이 진심으로 기쁩니다!\""]], [["‘Then I know I ought to be.’", "\"그렇다면 저도 기뻐해야 마땅한 거겠지요.\""]], [["‘And are you not?’", "\"기쁘지 않으세요?\""]], [["‘It seems to me hard,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘that he should have lost so many years and suffered so much, and at last pay all the debts as well.", "\"제게는 가혹하게 느껴져요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다, \"아버지가 그토록 많은 세월을 잃고 그토록 많은 고통을 겪으셨는데, 끝에 가서 그 빚까지 다 갚으셔야 한다는 게요."], ["It seems to me hard that he should pay in life and money both.’", "인생과 돈을 둘 다 치르셔야 한다는 게 제게는 가혹하게 느껴져요.\""]], [["‘My dear child--’ Clennam was beginning.", "\"얘야—\" 클레넘이 말을 꺼내려 했다."]], [["‘Yes, I know I am wrong,’ she pleaded timidly, ‘don’t think any worse of me; it has grown up with me here.’", "\"네, 제가 잘못 생각하는 걸 알아요,\" 그녀가 소심하게 변명했다, \"저를 너무 나쁘게 여기진 마세요. 여기서 저와 함께 자라난 생각이라서요.\""]], [["The prison, which could spoil so many things, had tainted Little Dorrit’s mind no more than this.", "그토록 많은 것을 망가뜨릴 수 있는 감옥도, 리틀 도릿의 마음을 더럽힌 것이라곤 이것뿐이었다."], ["Engendered as the confusion was, in compassion for the poor prisoner, her father, it was the first speck Clennam had ever seen, it was the last speck Clennam ever saw, of the prison atmosphere upon her.", "그 혼란이라는 것도 가엾은 죄수인 아버지에 대한 연민에서 생겨난 것이었으니, 그것은 클레넘이 그녀에게서 본 첫 번째 얼룩이었고, 그가 그녀에게서 본 마지막 얼룩이기도 했으니, 감옥의 공기가 그녀에게 남긴 자국이라곤 그뿐이었다."]], [["He thought this, and forbore to say another word.", "그는 그렇게 생각하고는 더 말하기를 삼갔다."], ["With the thought, her purity and goodness came before him in their brightest light.", "그 생각과 함께, 그녀의 순수함과 선함이 가장 밝은 빛 속에서 그의 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["The little spot made them the more beautiful.", "그 작은 얼룩이 그것들을 한층 더 아름답게 만들었다."]], [["Worn out with her own emotions, and yielding to the silence of the room, her hand slowly slackened and failed in its fanning movement, and her head dropped down on the pillow at her father’s side.", "자기 자신의 감정에 지쳐, 방 안의 고요에 몸을 맡기면서, 그녀의 손은 부채질하던 움직임이 서서히 느려지다가 멈추었고, 그녀의 머리는 아버지 곁의 베개 위로 떨어졌다."], ["Clennam rose softly, opened and closed the door without a sound, and passed from the prison, carrying the quiet with him into the turbulent streets.", "클레넘은 살며시 일어나, 소리 하나 내지 않고 문을 열었다 닫고는, 감옥을 빠져나와, 소란스러운 거리로 그 고요를 안고 나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan", "# CHAPTER 36. The Marshalsea becomes an Orphan"]], [["And now the day arrived when Mr Dorrit and his family were to leave the prison for ever, and the stones of its much-trodden pavement were to know them no more.", "이제 도릿 씨와 그의 가족이 영원히 감옥을 떠나, 그토록 많이 밟힌 그곳 포석이 더는 그들을 알지 못하게 될 날이 다가왔다."]], [["The interval had been short, but he had greatly complained of its length, and had been imperious with Mr Rugg touching the delay.", "그 사이의 기간은 짧았으나, 그는 그것이 길다고 몹시 불평했고, 지체되는 일을 두고 러그 씨에게 위압적으로 굴었다."], ["He had been high with Mr Rugg, and had threatened to employ some one else.", "그는 러그 씨에게 고압적으로 나왔고, 다른 사람을 쓰겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."], ["He had requested Mr Rugg not to presume upon the place in which he found him, but to do his duty, sir, and to do it with promptitude.", "그는 러그 씨에게, 자신이 어디에서 그를 발견했는지를 빌미로 주제넘게 굴지 말고, 자네의 본분을 다하게, 그것도 신속하게 다하라고 요구했다."], ["He had told Mr Rugg that he knew what lawyers and agents were, and that he would not submit to imposition.", "그는 러그 씨에게, 변호사나 대리인이 어떤 자들인지 자기는 잘 알고 있으며, 농간에 넘어가지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["On that gentleman’s humbly representing that he exerted himself to the utmost, Miss Fanny was very short with him; desiring to know what less he could do, when he had been told a dozen times that money was no object, and expressing her suspicion that he forgot whom he talked to.", "그 신사가 자기는 최선을 다해 애쓰고 있다고 겸손하게 변명하자, 패니 양이 그에게 아주 쌀쌀맞게 굴었다. 돈은 문제가 아니라는 말을 수십 번이나 들었으면서 그보다 더 적게 할 일이 무엇이 있겠느냐며 따져 물었고, 자기가 누구와 이야기하고 있는지를 그가 잊은 모양이라는 의심을 드러냈다."]], [["Towards the Marshal, who was a Marshal of many years’ standing, and with whom he had never had any previous difference, Mr Dorrit comported himself with severity.", "여러 해 동안 자리를 지켜 온, 그리고 전에 한 번도 다툰 적이 없던 간수장에게도 도릿 씨는 엄격하게 처신했다."], ["That officer, on personally tendering his congratulations, offered the free use of two rooms in his house for Mr Dorrit’s occupation until his departure.", "그 관리는 몸소 축하 인사를 건네면서, 떠날 때까지 도릿 씨가 쓰도록 자기 집의 방 두 개를 무료로 내어 주겠다고 제안했다."], ["Mr Dorrit thanked him at the moment, and replied that he would think of it; but the Marshal was no sooner gone than he sat down and wrote him a cutting note, in which he remarked that he had never on any former occasion had the honour of receiving his congratulations (which was true, though indeed there had not been anything particular to congratulate him upon), and that he begged, on behalf of himself and family, to repudiate the Marshal’s offer, with all those thanks which its disinterested character and its perfect independence of all worldly considerations demanded.", "도릿 씨는 그 자리에서는 고맙다며, 생각해 보겠다고 대답했다. 그러나 간수장이 떠나기가 무섭게 그는 앉아서 신랄한 쪽지를 써 보냈는데, 거기에서 그는 이전에 어떤 경우에도 그의 축하를 받는 영광을 누린 적이 없었다고(이것은 사실이었으니, 실은 그를 축하할 만한 특별한 일이 따로 있었던 것도 아니었다) 지적하고, 자신과 가족을 대신하여 간수장의 제안을 거절하노라고, 그 사심 없는 성격과 온갖 세속적 고려로부터의 완전한 초연함이 요구하는 모든 감사와 함께 거절한다고 적었다."]], [["Although his brother showed so dim a glimmering of interest in their altered fortunes that it was very doubtful whether he understood them, Mr Dorrit caused him to be measured for new raiment by the hosiers, tailors, hatters, and bootmakers whom he called in for himself; and ordered that his old clothes should be taken from him and burned.", "그의 형은 바뀐 처지에 대해 너무도 흐릿한 관심의 기미밖에 보이지 않아 과연 그가 그것을 이해는 하는지조차 매우 의심스러웠지만, 도릿 씨는 자기를 위해 불러들인 양품점 주인과 재단사, 모자 장수, 구두 장수를 시켜 형의 새 옷 치수를 재게 했고, 형의 헌 옷은 거두어 태워 버리라고 지시했다."], ["Miss Fanny and Mr Tip required no direction in making an appearance of great fashion and elegance; and the three passed this interval together at the best hotel in the neighbourhood--though truly, as Miss Fanny said, the best was very indifferent.", "패니 양과 팁 씨는 대단히 멋스럽고 우아한 차림을 하는 데 아무 지시도 필요치 않았다. 세 사람은 이 기간을 인근에서 가장 좋은 호텔에서 함께 보냈다."], ["In connection with that establishment, Mr Tip hired a cabriolet, horse, and groom, a very neat turn out, which was usually to be observed for two or three hours at a time gracing the Borough High Street, outside the Marshalsea court-yard.", "비록 패니 양의 말처럼 가장 좋은 곳이라야 매우 형편없었지만 말이다. 그 호텔과 관련하여, 팁 씨는 이륜마차와 말과 마부를 빌렸는데, 아주 말끔한 차림새여서, 보통 한 번에 두세 시간씩 마셜시 안마당 바깥의 버러 하이 스트리트를 장식하며 서 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["A modest little hired chariot and pair was also frequently to be seen there; in alighting from and entering which vehicle, Miss Fanny fluttered the Marshal’s daughters by the display of inaccessible bonnets.", "거기에는 또 수수하고 작은 임대 사두마차 한 대도 자주 보였는데, 패니 양은 그 마차에서 내리고 타면서 손에 넣을 수 없는 보닛을 과시하여 간수장의 딸들을 들뜨게 했다."]], [["A great deal of business was transacted in this short period.", "이 짧은 기간에 수많은 일이 처리되었다."], ["Among other items, Messrs Peddle and Pool, solicitors, of Monument Yard, were instructed by their client Edward Dorrit, Esquire, to address a letter to Mr Arthur Clennam, enclosing the sum of twenty-four pounds nine shillings and eightpence, being the amount of principal and interest computed at the rate of five per cent. per annum, in which their client believed himself to be indebted to Mr Clennam.", "그 가운데 하나로, 모뉴먼트 야드의 사무 변호사 페들 앤드 풀 양반들은 그들의 의뢰인 에드워드 도릿 귀하의 지시를 받아 아서 클레넘 씨에게 편지를 보내며, 거기에 24파운드 9실링 8펜스의 금액을 동봉했는데, 이는 그들의 의뢰인이 클레넘 씨에게 빚졌다고 여기는 원금에, 연 5퍼센트의 이율로 계산한 이자를 더한 액수였다."], ["In making this communication and remittance, Messrs Peddle and Pool were further instructed by their client to remind Mr Clennam that the favour of the advance now repaid (including gate-fees) had not been asked of him, and to inform him that it would not have been accepted if it had been openly proffered in his name.", "이 통지와 송금을 하면서, 페들 앤드 풀 양반들은 그들의 의뢰인으로부터 또한 지시를 받아, 이제 갚는 그 선급금(출입문 수수료를 포함하여)의 호의를 클레넘 씨에게 청한 적이 없었음을 그에게 일깨우고, 만일 그것이 그의 이름으로 공공연히 제안되었더라면 받지 않았을 것이라는 점을 알렸다."], ["With which they requested a stamped receipt, and remained his obedient servants.", "이와 함께 그들은 인지를 붙인 영수증을 청하며, 그의 충실한 종으로 남는다고 적었다."], ["A great deal of business had likewise to be done, within the so-soon-to-be-orphaned Marshalsea, by Mr Dorrit so long its Father, chiefly arising out of applications made to him by Collegians for small sums of money.", "곧 고아가 될 마셜시 안에서도, 그토록 오래 그곳의 아버지였던 도릿 씨가 처리해야 할 일이 많았는데, 주로 수감자들이 그에게 소액의 돈을 청하는 데서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["To these he responded with the greatest liberality, and with no lack of formality; always first writing to appoint a time at which the applicant might wait upon him in his room, and then receiving him in the midst of a vast accumulation of documents, and accompanying his donation (for he said in every such case, ‘it is a donation, not a loan’) with a great deal of good counsel: to the effect that he, the expiring Father of the Marshalsea, hoped to be long remembered, as an example that a man might preserve his own and the general respect even there.", "이에 그는 더없이 후하게, 그러면서도 격식을 빠뜨리지 않고 응했으니, 언제나 먼저 청한 자가 자기 방으로 찾아올 시간을 정해 편지를 써 보낸 다음, 산더미처럼 쌓인 서류 한가운데에서 그를 맞아, 자신의 기부에(그는 그런 경우마다 \"이것은 기부지 대여가 아니오\"라고 말했으므로) 숱한 좋은 조언을 곁들였다. 그 요지인즉, 곧 사라질 마셜시의 아버지인 자신이, 사람은 그런 곳에서조차 자기 자신과 모두의 존경을 지킬 수 있다는 본보기로서 오래도록 기억되기를 바란다는 것이었다."]], [["The Collegians were not envious.", "수감자들은 시기하지 않았다."], ["Besides that they had a personal and traditional regard for a Collegian of so many years’ standing, the event was creditable to the College, and made it famous in the newspapers.", "그토록 여러 해 동안 자리를 지킨 수감자에게 개인적이고 전통적인 경의를 품고 있었던 데다가, 이 사건은 그 학교의 명예가 되는 일이었고 신문에까지 그곳을 유명하게 만들었으니 말이다."], ["Perhaps more of them thought, too, than were quite aware of it, that the thing might in the lottery of chances have happened to themselves, or that something of the sort might yet happen to themselves some day or other.", "게다가 아마도 그들 가운데 스스로 자각하는 것보다 더 많은 이들이, 운수의 추첨에서 같은 일이 자기에게도 일어날 수 있었으리라고, 혹은 언젠가 그 비슷한 일이 자기에게도 일어날지 모른다고 생각했을 것이다."], ["They took it very well.", "그들은 그것을 아주 잘 받아들였다."], ["A few were low at the thought of being left behind, and being left poor; but even these did not grudge the family their brilliant reverse.", "몇몇은 자기들만 뒤에 남겨지고, 가난한 채로 남겨지리라는 생각에 풀이 죽었으나, 그런 이들조차 그 가족의 찬란한 운명의 역전을 시기하지는 않았다."], ["There might have been much more envy in politer places.", "좀 더 점잖은 곳이었다면 훨씬 더 많은 시기가 있었을지도 모른다."], ["It seems probable that mediocrity of fortune would have been disposed to be less magnanimous than the Collegians, who lived from hand to mouth--from the pawnbroker’s hand to the day’s dinner.", "전당포 주인의 손에서 그날의 저녁밥으로—그날그날 손에서 입으로 살아가던 수감자들보다는, 어중간한 형편이 오히려 덜 너그러웠으리라는 것이 그럴듯한 일이다."]], [["They got up an address to him, which they presented in a neat frame and glass (though it was not afterwards displayed in the family mansion or preserved among the family papers); and to which he returned a gracious answer.", "그들은 그에게 보내는 헌사를 작성하여, 깔끔한 액자와 유리에 끼워 그에게 바쳤고(다만 그것이 그 뒤로 그 가문의 저택에 걸리거나 가문의 문서로 보존되지는 않았다), 그는 거기에 우아한 답을 보냈다."], ["In that document he assured them, in a Royal manner, that he received the profession of their attachment with a full conviction of its sincerity; and again generally exhorted them to follow his example--which, at least in so far as coming into a great property was concerned, there is no doubt they would have gladly imitated.", "그 문서에서 그는, 임금이라도 된 듯한 태도로, 자기들에 대한 그들의 애착의 고백을 그 진실함에 대한 충분한 확신을 가지고 받아들인다고 그들에게 장담했다. 그리고 다시금 두루 자기를 본받으라고 권했는데—적어도 막대한 재산을 물려받는 일에 관한 한, 그들이 그것을 기꺼이 흉내 냈으리라는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없었다."], ["He took the same occasion of inviting them to a comprehensive entertainment, to be given to the whole College in the yard, and at which he signified he would have the honour of taking a parting glass to the health and happiness of all those whom he was about to leave behind.", "그는 같은 기회를 빌려, 학교 전체를 위해 안마당에서 베풀 성대한 연회에 그들을 초대했고, 자기가 곧 뒤에 남겨 두고 갈 모든 이들의 건강과 행복을 위해 이별의 술잔을 드는 영광을 누리겠노라고 알렸다."]], [["He did not in person dine at this public repast (it took place at two in the afternoon, and his dinners now came in from the hotel at six), but his son was so good as to take the head of the principal table, and to be very free and engaging.", "그는 이 공개 연회에서 직접 식사하지는 않았다(연회는 오후 두 시에 열렸는데, 이제 그의 식사는 여섯 시에 호텔에서 들어왔다). 그러나 그의 아들이 친절하게도 주빈석의 상석에 앉아 매우 격의 없고 다정하게 굴었다."], ["He himself went about among the company, and took notice of individuals, and saw that the viands were of the quality he had ordered, and that all were served.", "그는 손수 손님들 사이를 돌아다니며 개개인에게 눈길을 주었고, 음식이 자기가 주문한 품질대로인지, 모두에게 빠짐없이 차려졌는지를 살폈다."], ["On the whole, he was like a baron of the olden time in a rare good humour.", "전체적으로 그는 보기 드물게 기분이 좋은 옛 시대의 영주 같았다."], ["At the conclusion of the repast, he pledged his guests in a bumper of old Madeira; and told them that he hoped they had enjoyed themselves, and what was more, that they would enjoy themselves for the rest of the evening; that he wished them well; and that he bade them welcome.", "연회가 끝날 무렵, 그는 손님들과 함께 오래된 마데이라 포도주를 가득 채운 잔으로 건배했고, 그들이 즐거운 시간을 보냈기를, 그리고 더 나아가 그 저녁의 남은 시간도 즐겁게 보내기를 바란다고 말했다. 그들이 잘되기를 빌며, 그들을 환영한다고도 했다."], ["His health being drunk with acclamations, he was not so baronial after all but that in trying to return thanks he broke down, in the manner of a mere serf with a heart in his breast, and wept before them all.", "환호 속에 그의 건강을 위한 축배가 들리자, 그는 결국 그렇게까지 영주답지는 못했으니, 답례의 인사를 하려다가 가슴속에 마음을 지닌 한낱 농노처럼 무너져 내려, 모든 이들 앞에서 눈물을 흘리고 말았다."], ["After this great success, which he supposed to be a failure, he gave them ‘Mr Chivery and his brother officers;’ whom he had beforehand presented with ten pounds each, and who were all in attendance.", "자기로서는 실패라고 여긴 이 대성공 뒤에, 그는 \"치버리 씨와 그의 동료 관리들\"을 위해 건배를 제의했는데, 그는 미리 그들에게 각각 10파운드씩을 주었고, 그들은 모두 그 자리에 와 있었다."], ["Mr Chivery spoke to the toast, saying, What you undertake to lock up, lock up; but remember that you are, in the words of the fettered African, a man and a brother ever.", "치버리 씨가 그 건배에 답하여 말하기를, 가두라고 맡겨진 것은 가두되, 다만 그대도, 족쇄 찬 아프리카인의 말처럼, 언제나 한 사람의 인간이요 형제임을 기억하라고 했다."], ["The list of toasts disposed of, Mr Dorrit urbanely went through the motions of playing a game of skittles with the Collegian who was the next oldest inhabitant to himself; and left the tenantry to their diversions.", "건배의 목록이 다 처리되자, 도릿 씨는 자기 다음으로 가장 오래 머문 수감자와 함께 스키틀 놀이를 하는 시늉을 점잖게 해 보였고, 입주민들은 저마다 즐기도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["But all these occurrences preceded the final day.", "그러나 이 모든 일은 마지막 날에 앞선 것이었다."], ["And now the day arrived when he and his family were to leave the prison for ever, and when the stones of its much-trodden pavement were to know them no more.", "그리고 이제 그와 그의 가족이 영원히 감옥을 떠나, 그토록 많이 밟힌 그곳 포석이 더는 그들을 알지 못하게 될 날이 다가왔다."]], [["Noon was the hour appointed for the departure.", "정오가 출발 시각으로 정해졌다."], ["As it approached, there was not a Collegian within doors, nor a turnkey absent.", "그 시각이 다가오자, 안에 있는 수감자는 하나도 없었고, 자리를 비운 간수도 하나 없었다."], ["The latter class of gentlemen appeared in their Sunday clothes, and the greater part of the Collegians were brightened up as much as circumstances allowed.", "뒤의 신사들은 나들이옷 차림으로 나타났고, 수감자들 대부분은 형편이 허락하는 한 한껏 차려입었다."], ["Two or three flags were even displayed, and the children put on odds and ends of ribbon.", "깃발도 두세 개 내걸렸고, 아이들은 자투리 리본을 둘렀다."], ["Mr Dorrit himself, at this trying time, preserved a serious but graceful dignity.", "이 고비의 순간에도 도릿 씨 자신은 엄숙하지만 기품 있는 위엄을 잃지 않았다."], ["Much of his great attention was given to his brother, as to whose bearing on the great occasion he felt anxious.", "그의 크나큰 주의는 형에게 많이 쏠려 있었으니, 그 중대한 자리에서 형의 처신이 어떨지 걱정스러웠던 것이다."]], [["‘My dear Frederick,’ said he, ‘if you will give me your arm we will pass among our friends together.", "\"형님,\" 그가 말했다."], ["I think it is right that we should go out arm in arm, my dear Frederick.’", "\"팔을 좀 빌려주시면 우리 함께 친구들 사이를 지나가지요. 우리가 팔짱을 끼고 나가는 것이 옳다고 생각합니다, 형님.\""]], [["‘Hah!’ said Frederick.", "\"하!\" 프레더릭이 말했다."], ["‘Yes, yes, yes, yes.’", "\"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래.\""]], [["‘And if, my dear Frederick--if you could, without putting any great constraint upon yourself, throw a little (pray excuse me, Frederick), a little polish into your usual demeanour--’", "\"그리고 형님—형님이 너무 무리하지 않고서, 평소의 거동에 조금만(부디 양해해 주세요, 형님), 조금만 광을 더해 주실 수 있다면—\""]], [["‘William, William,’ said the other, shaking his head, ‘it’s for you to do all that.", "\"윌리엄, 윌리엄,\" 형이 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["I don’t know how.", "\"그런 건 다 네가 할 일이다."], ["All forgotten, forgotten!’", "나는 어떻게 하는지 모른다. 다 잊었어, 다 잊었어!\""]], [["‘But, my dear fellow,’ returned William, ‘for that very reason, if for no other, you must positively try to rouse yourself.", "\"하지만 형님,\" 윌리엄이 대답했다."], ["What you have forgotten you must now begin to recall, my dear Frederick.", "\"바로 그 이유 때문에라도, 달리 이유가 없더라도, 형님은 반드시 정신을 차리려고 애쓰셔야 합니다. 잊으신 것을 이제 다시 떠올리기 시작하셔야 해요, 형님."], ["Your position--’", "형님의 처지는—\""]], [["‘Eh?’ said Frederick.", "\"응?\" 프레더릭이 말했다."]], [["‘Your position, my dear Frederick.’", "\"형님의 처지 말입니다, 형님.\""]], [["‘Mine?’ He looked first at his own figure, and then at his brother’s, and then, drawing a long breath, cried, ‘Hah, to be sure!", "\"나의?\" 그는 먼저 자기 모습을 보고, 다음에 동생의 모습을 보고는, 긴 한숨을 내쉬며 외쳤다."], ["Yes, yes, yes.’", "\"하, 그렇지! 그래, 그래, 그래.\""]], [["‘Your position, my dear Frederick, is now a fine one.", "\"형님의 처지는, 이제 훌륭한 것입니다."], ["Your position, as my brother, is a very fine one.", "내 형으로서의 형님의 처지는 아주 훌륭한 것이지요."], ["And I know that it belongs to your conscientious nature to try to become worthy of it, my dear Frederick, and to try to adorn it.", "그리고 나는 형님의 양심적인 천성이라면 그에 걸맞은 사람이 되려고, 그것을 빛내려고 애쓰리라는 걸 압니다, 형님."], ["To be no discredit to it, but to adorn it.’", "그것에 누가 되지 않도록, 오히려 그것을 빛내도록 말이지요.\""]], [["‘William,’ said the other weakly, and with a sigh, ‘I will do anything you wish, my brother, provided it lies in my power.", "\"윌리엄,\" 형이 힘없이, 한숨과 함께 말했다."], ["Pray be so kind as to recollect what a limited power mine is.", "\"네가 바라는 건 무엇이든 하마, 동생아, 내 힘이 닿는 한은. 부디 내 힘이 얼마나 보잘것없는지를 기억해 다오."], ["What would you wish me to do to-day, brother?", "오늘 내가 무엇을 하기를 바라느냐, 동생아?"], ["Say what it is, only say what it is.’", "그것이 무엇인지 말해 다오, 그저 무엇인지만 말해 다오.\""]], [["‘My dearest Frederick, nothing.", "\"형님, 아무것도 없어요."], ["It is not worth troubling so good a heart as yours with.’", "형님처럼 그토록 착한 마음을 번거롭게 할 만한 일이 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Pray trouble it,’ returned the other.", "\"부디 번거롭게 해 다오,\" 형이 대답했다."], ["‘It finds it no trouble, William, to do anything it can for you.’", "\"그 마음으로서는, 윌리엄, 너를 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 번거롭지가 않으니까.\""]], [["William passed his hand across his eyes, and murmured with august satisfaction, ‘Blessings on your attachment, my poor dear fellow!’ Then he said aloud, ‘Well, my dear Frederick, if you will only try, as we walk out, to show that you are alive to the occasion--that you think about it--’", "윌리엄은 손으로 눈을 훔치며, 위엄에 찬 흡족함으로 중얼거렸다. \"형님의 그 애정에 축복이 있기를, 가엾고 가여운 형님!\" 그러고는 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그럼, 형님, 우리가 걸어 나갈 때 그저 이 자리를 의식하고 있다는 것만—그것을 마음에 두고 있다는 것만 보여 주시려고 애써 주시면—\""]], [["‘What would you advise me to think about it?’ returned his submissive brother.", "\"내가 무엇을 마음에 두기를 권하겠느냐?\" 순종적인 형이 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["my dear Frederick, how can I answer you?", "형님, 제가 어찌 대답해 드리겠어요?"], ["I can only say what, in leaving these good people, I think myself.’", "다만 이 착한 사람들을 떠나면서 제 자신이 무슨 생각을 하는지를 말씀드릴 수밖에요.\""]], [["‘That’s it!’ cried his brother.", "\"바로 그거다!\" 형이 외쳤다."], ["‘That will help me.’", "\"그게 나를 도와줄 게다.\""]], [["‘I find that I think, my dear Frederick, and with mixed emotions in which a softened compassion predominates, What will they do without me!’", "\"제가 생각하는 건요, 형님, 그것도 누그러진 연민이 가장 큰 자리를 차지하는 뒤섞인 감정으로 생각하는 건, '내가 없으면 저들은 어떻게 할까!' 하는 것입니다.\""]], [["‘True,’ returned his brother.", "\"옳다,\" 형이 대답했다."], ["‘Yes, yes, yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래, 그래, 그래."], ["I’ll think that as we go, What will they do without my brother!", "걸어가면서 그 생각을 하마, '내 동생이 없으면 저들은 어떻게 할까!"], ["Poor things!", "가엾은 것들!"], ["What will they do without him!’", "저 사람이 없으면 어떻게 할까!' 하고.\""]], [["Twelve o’clock having just struck, and the carriage being reported ready in the outer court-yard, the brothers proceeded down-stairs arm-in-arm.", "열두 시가 막 울리고, 바깥뜰에 마차가 준비되었다는 전갈이 오자, 형제는 팔짱을 끼고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Edward Dorrit, Esquire (once Tip), and his sister Fanny followed, also arm-in-arm; Mr Plornish and Maggy, to whom had been entrusted the removal of such of the family effects as were considered worth removing, followed, bearing bundles and burdens to be packed in a cart.", "에드워드 도릿 귀하(한때의 팁)와 그의 누이 패니가 역시 팔짱을 끼고 뒤따랐다. 가져갈 만하다고 여겨진 가재도구를 옮기는 일을 맡은 플로니시 씨와 매기가, 수레에 실을 보따리와 짐을 들고 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["In the yard, were the Collegians and turnkeys.", "마당에는 수감자들과 간수들이 있었다."], ["In the yard, were Mr Pancks and Mr Rugg, come to see the last touch given to their work.", "마당에는, 자기들이 한 일에 마지막 손길이 닿는 것을 보러 온 팽크스 씨와 러그 씨가 있었다."], ["In the yard, was Young John making a new epitaph for himself, on the occasion of his dying of a broken heart.", "마당에는, 상심하여 죽는 마당에 자신을 위한 새 묘비명을 짓고 있는 젊은 존이 있었다."], ["In the yard, was the Patriarchal Casby, looking so tremendously benevolent that many enthusiastic Collegians grasped him fervently by the hand, and the wives and female relatives of many more Collegians kissed his hand, nothing doubting that he had done it all.", "마당에는, 어찌나 어마어마하게 자비로워 보이던지 열성적인 수감자들 다수가 그의 손을 뜨겁게 부여잡고, 더 많은 수감자들의 아내와 여자 친척들이 그가 이 모든 일을 해냈다고 추호도 의심하지 않으며 그의 손에 입을 맞추던, 가부장다운 캐스비가 있었다."], ["In the yard, was the man with the shadowy grievance respecting the Fund which the Marshal embezzled, who had got up at five in the morning to complete the copying of a perfectly unintelligible history of that transaction, which he had committed to Mr Dorrit’s care, as a document of the last importance, calculated to stun the Government and effect the Marshal’s downfall.", "마당에는, 간수장이 횡령했다는 그 자금에 관한 어렴풋한 불만을 품은 사내가 있었는데, 그는 그 거래에 관한 도무지 알아먹을 수 없는 내력을 베껴 끝내려고 새벽 다섯 시에 일어났고, 그것을 정부를 깜짝 놀라게 하고 간수장의 몰락을 가져올 더없이 중요한 문서로서 도릿 씨의 손에 맡긴 터였다."], ["In the yard, was the insolvent whose utmost energies were always set on getting into debt, who broke into prison with as much pains as other men have broken out of it, and who was always being cleared and complimented; while the insolvent at his elbow--a mere little, snivelling, striving tradesman, half dead of anxious efforts to keep out of debt--found it a hard matter, indeed, to get a Commissioner to release him with much reproof and reproach.", "마당에는, 그 온갖 정력을 늘 빚을 지는 데에 쏟던 파산자가 있었는데, 그는 남들이 감옥에서 빠져나오느라 들이는 만큼의 공을 들여 감옥으로 비집고 들어왔고, 늘 빚을 청산받으며 칭송을 들었다. 반면 그의 곁에 있던 파산자는—그저 자그맣고 훌쩍거리며 안간힘 쓰는 장사꾼으로, 빚을 지지 않으려는 노심초사로 반쯤 죽어 가던 자였는데—갖은 질책과 비난과 함께라도 위원에게서 풀려나기가 참으로 어려운 노릇이었다."], ["In the yard, was the man of many children and many burdens, whose failure astonished everybody; in the yard, was the man of no children and large resources, whose failure astonished nobody.", "마당에는, 자식도 많고 짐도 많은데도 그 파산이 모두를 놀라게 한 사내가 있었다. 마당에는, 자식도 없고 재산도 많은데도 그 파산이 아무도 놀라게 하지 않은 사내가 있었다."], ["There, were the people who were always going out to-morrow, and always putting it off; there, were the people who had come in yesterday, and who were much more jealous and resentful of this freak of fortune than the seasoned birds.", "거기에는, 늘 내일 나가겠다고 하면서 늘 미루는 사람들이 있었고, 거기에는, 어제 들어와서는 그 운수의 변덕을 산전수전 다 겪은 새들보다 훨씬 더 시샘하고 분해하는 사람들이 있었다."], ["There, were some who, in pure meanness of spirit, cringed and bowed before the enriched Collegian and his family; there, were others who did so really because their eyes, accustomed to the gloom of their imprisonment and poverty, could not support the light of such bright sunshine.", "거기에는, 순전히 비루한 마음에서 부유해진 수감자와 그 가족 앞에 굽실거리며 절하는 이들이 있었고, 거기에는, 감금과 가난의 어둠에 익은 눈이 그처럼 밝은 햇빛을 견디지 못해 정말로 그렇게 하는 이들도 있었다."], ["There, were many whose shillings had gone into his pocket to buy him meat and drink; but none who were now obtrusively Hail fellow well met!", "거기에는, 그에게 고기와 술을 사 주려고 자기 실링을 그의 주머니에 넣어 준 이들이 많았으나, 이제 와서 그 도움을 빌미로 주제넘게 \"어이, 친구, 잘 만났네!\""], ["with him, on the strength of that assistance.", "하는 이는 하나도 없었다."], ["It was rather to be remarked of the caged birds, that they were a little shy of the bird about to be so grandly free, and that they had a tendency to withdraw themselves towards the bars, and seem a little fluttered as he passed.", "오히려 새장에 갇힌 새들에게서 눈여겨볼 만한 점은, 그들이 곧 그토록 당당하게 자유로워질 그 새를 조금 어려워하며, 창살 쪽으로 물러나는 경향을 보이고, 그가 지나갈 때 조금 동요하는 듯 보였다는 것이었다."]], [["Through these spectators the little procession, headed by the two brothers, moved slowly to the gate.", "이 구경꾼들 사이로, 두 형제를 앞세운 작은 행렬이 정문을 향해 천천히 움직였다. 도릿 씨는, 자기가 없으면 저 가엾은 자들이 어떻게 살아갈까 하는 그 막대한 상념에 잠겨, 위대하면서도 슬펐으나, 거기에 온통 빠져 있지는 않았다."], ["Mr Dorrit, yielding to the vast speculation how the poor creatures were to get on without him, was great, and sad, but not absorbed.", "그는 교회에 가는 로저 드 커벌리 경처럼 아이들의 머리를 쓰다듬었고, 뒤편에 있는 사람들에게 세례명으로 말을 건넸으며, 그 자리의 모두에게 너그러이 몸을 낮추었고, 그들의 위안을 위해 황금빛 글자로 쓰인 \"위로받으라, 나의 백성이여! 견디라!\""], ["He patted children on the head like Sir Roger de Coverley going to church, he spoke to people in the background by their Christian names, he condescended to all present, and seemed for their consolation to walk encircled by the legend in golden characters, ‘Be comforted, my people! Bear it!’", "하는 구절에 에워싸여 걷는 듯이 보였다."]], [["At last three honest cheers announced that he had passed the gate, and that the Marshalsea was an orphan.", "마침내 정직한 세 번의 환호가, 그가 정문을 지나갔으며 마셜시가 고아가 되었음을 알렸다."], ["Before they had ceased to ring in the echoes of the prison walls, the family had got into their carriage, and the attendant had the steps in his hand.", "그 소리가 감옥 담장의 메아리 속에서 채 그치기도 전에, 가족은 마차에 올라탔고, 수행원이 발판을 손에 쥐고 있었다."]], [["Then, and not before, ‘Good Gracious!’ cried Miss Fanny all at once, ‘Where’s Amy!’", "그제야, 그 전이 아니라 그제야, \"어머나 세상에!\" 하고 패니 양이 별안간 외쳤다. \"에이미는 어디 있어요!\""]], [["Her father had thought she was with her sister.", "아버지는 그애가 언니와 함께 있는 줄 알았다. 언니는 그애가 \"어딘가에\""], ["Her sister had thought she was ‘somewhere or other.’ They had all trusted to finding her, as they had always done, quietly in the right place at the right moment.", "있는 줄 알았다. 그들은 모두, 늘 그래 왔듯이, 그애가 알맞은 때에 알맞은 자리에 얌전히 있는 것을 발견하리라 믿어 의심치 않았다."], ["This going away was perhaps the very first action of their joint lives that they had got through without her.", "이번 떠남이야말로, 그애 없이 치러 낸, 그들 공동의 삶에서 어쩌면 맨 첫 번째 일이었다."]], [["A minute might have been consumed in the ascertaining of these points, when Miss Fanny, who, from her seat in the carriage, commanded the long narrow passage leading to the Lodge, flushed indignantly.", "이 점들을 확인하는 데 일 분쯤 걸렸을 무렵, 마차의 자기 자리에서 수위실로 통하는 길고 좁은 통로를 내려다볼 수 있던 패니 양이, 분개하여 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["‘Now I do say, Pa,’ cried she, ‘that this is disgraceful!’", "\"이건 정말이지, 아버지,\" 그녀가 외쳤다. \"수치스러운 일이에요!\""]], [["‘What is disgraceful, Fanny?’", "\"무엇이 수치스럽단 말이냐, 패니?\""]], [["‘I do say,’ she repeated, ‘this is perfectly infamous!", "\"제 말은요,\" 그녀가 거듭 말했다."], ["Really almost enough, even at such a time as this, to make one wish one was dead!", "\"이건 정말 더없이 망신스러운 일이라고요! 정말 이런 때에조차, 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 싶을 만큼이라니까요!"], ["Here is that child Amy, in her ugly old shabby dress, which she was so obstinate about, Pa, which I over and over again begged and prayed her to change, and which she over and over again objected to, and promised to change to-day, saying she wished to wear it as long as ever she remained in there with you--which was absolutely romantic nonsense of the lowest kind--here is that child Amy disgracing us to the last moment and at the last moment, by being carried out in that dress after all.", "저기 저 에이미라는 애가, 그토록 고집을 부리던 그 보기 흉한 낡은 누더기 옷을 입고서, 아버지, 제가 몇 번이고 빌고 사정하며 갈아입으라고 했는데도, 몇 번이고 마다하다가 오늘은 갈아입겠다고 약속해 놓고는, 아버지와 함께 저 안에 머무는 동안만큼은 그걸 계속 입고 싶다던—그건 정말이지 더없이 천한 부류의 낭만적 헛소리였는데—저기 저 에이미라는 애가, 결국 그 옷차림 그대로 들려 나오면서, 마지막 순간까지, 그것도 마지막 순간에 우리를 망신시키고 있다고요."], ["And by that Mr Clennam too!’", "게다가 저 클레넘 씨 손에 들려서요!\""]], [["The offence was proved, as she delivered the indictment.", "그녀가 그 고발장을 늘어놓는 동안, 그 죄목이 증명되었다."], ["Clennam appeared at the carriage-door, bearing the little insensible figure in his arms.", "클레넘이 그 작고 정신 잃은 모습을 품에 안고 마차 문 앞에 나타났던 것이다."]], [["‘She has been forgotten,’ he said, in a tone of pity not free from reproach.", "\"이 아이가 잊혀 있었더군요.\" 그가 질책이 섞이지 않은 것은 아닌 연민의 어조로 말했다."], ["‘I ran up to her room (which Mr Chivery showed me) and found the door open, and that she had fainted on the floor, dear child.", "\"제가 이 아이의 방으로(치버리 씨가 가르쳐 주었습니다) 뛰어 올라가 보니 문이 열려 있었고, 이 가엾은 아이가 바닥에 쓰러져 있었습니다."], ["She appeared to have gone to change her dress, and to have sunk down overpowered.", "옷을 갈아입으러 갔다가, 그만 기력이 다해 주저앉은 모양입니다."], ["It may have been the cheering, or it may have happened sooner.", "환호 소리 때문이었을 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그보다 더 일찍 일어난 일일 수도 있겠지요."], ["Take care of this poor cold hand, Miss Dorrit.", "이 가엾고 차가운 손을 조심하세요, 도릿 양."], ["Don’t let it fall.’", "떨어뜨리지 마시고요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ returned Miss Dorrit, bursting into tears.", "\"고마워요, 선생님.\" 도릿 양이 울음을 터뜨리며 대답했다."], ["‘I believe I know what to do, if you will give me leave.", "\"허락만 해 주시면 어찌해야 할지는 제가 알아요."], ["Dear Amy, open your eyes, that’s a love!", "에이미, 눈을 떠, 착하지!"], ["Oh, Amy, Amy, I really am so vexed and ashamed!", "오, 에이미, 에이미, 나 정말 너무 속상하고 부끄러워!"], ["Do rouse yourself, darling!", "제발 정신 차려, 얘야!"], ["Oh, why are they not driving on!", "오, 왜 어서 가지 않는 거야!"], ["Pray, Pa, do drive on!’", "부디, 아버지, 어서 가요!\""]], [["The attendant, getting between Clennam and the carriage-door, with a sharp ‘By your leave, sir!’ bundled up the steps, and they drove away.", "수행원이 클레넘과 마차 문 사이로 끼어들며, 날카롭게 \"실례합니다, 선생님!\" 하고는 발판을 부랴부랴 접어 올렸고, 마차는 떠나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 1. Fellow Travellers", "# CHAPTER 1. Fellow Travellers"]], [["In the autumn of the year, Darkness and Night were creeping up to the highest ridges of the Alps.", "가을이 깊어 가던 무렵, 어둠과 밤이 알프스의 가장 높은 능선들을 향해 슬그머니 기어오르고 있었다."]], [["It was vintage time in the valleys on the Swiss side of the Pass of the Great Saint Bernard, and along the banks of the Lake of Geneva.", "그랑생베르나르 고개의 스위스 쪽 골짜기들과 제네바 호반을 따라서는 포도 수확철이었다."], ["The air there was charged with the scent of gathered grapes.", "그곳의 공기는 거두어들인 포도 향으로 가득했다."], ["Baskets, troughs, and tubs of grapes stood in the dim village doorways, stopped the steep and narrow village streets, and had been carrying all day along the roads and lanes.", "포도가 담긴 바구니와 구유와 통이 어둑한 마을 문간마다 놓여 있고, 가파르고 좁은 마을 길을 가로막았으며, 하루 종일 길과 골목을 따라 실려 다녔다."], ["Grapes, split and crushed under foot, lay about everywhere.", "발에 밟혀 터지고 으깨진 포도가 사방에 널려 있었다."], ["The child carried in a sling by the laden peasant woman toiling home, was quieted with picked-up grapes; the idiot sunning his big goitre under the leaves of the wooden chalet by the way to the Waterfall, sat munching grapes; the breath of the cows and goats was redolent of leaves and stalks of grapes; the company in every little cabaret were eating, drinking, talking grapes.", "짐을 진 채 집으로 터벅터벅 돌아가는 농촌 아낙이 멜빵에 안고 가는 아이는 주워 든 포도를 받아 들고 잠잠해졌고, 폭포로 가는 길가 나무 산장의 잎사귀 아래에서 커다란 갑상선종을 햇볕에 쬐던 백치는 앉아서 포도를 우물거렸으며, 소와 염소의 숨결은 포도 잎과 줄기 냄새로 향긋했고, 작은 선술집마다 모인 사람들은 포도를 먹고 마시며 포도 이야기를 했다."], ["A pity that no ripe touch of this generous abundance could be given to the thin, hard, stony wine, which after all was made from the grapes!", "이 후한 풍요로움의 무르익은 손길이, 어쨌거나 그 포도로 만들어진 그 묽고 거칠고 떫은 포도주에는 조금도 깃들 수 없다는 것이 안타까운 일이었다!"]], [["The air had been warm and transparent through the whole of the bright day.", "밝은 한낮 내내 공기는 따뜻하고 투명했다."], ["Shining metal spires and church-roofs, distant and rarely seen, had sparkled in the view; and the snowy mountain-tops had been so clear that unaccustomed eyes, cancelling the intervening country, and slighting their rugged heights for something fabulous, would have measured them as within a few hours easy reach.", "멀리 좀처럼 보이지 않던 번쩍이는 금속 첨탑과 교회 지붕이 시야 속에서 반짝였고, 눈 덮인 산봉우리들은 어찌나 또렷한지 익숙지 않은 눈으로 보면 그 사이에 놓인 들판을 지워 버리고, 험준한 높이를 우습게 보아 무언가 전설 같은 것으로 여기며, 몇 시간이면 쉬이 닿을 거리쯤으로 가늠했을 법했다."], ["Mountain-peaks of great celebrity in the valleys, whence no trace of their existence was visible sometimes for months together, had been since morning plain and near in the blue sky.", "골짜기에서는 이름 높으면서도 때로는 몇 달 내내 그 존재의 흔적조차 보이지 않던 산봉우리들이, 아침부터 푸른 하늘 속에 또렷하고 가깝게 모습을 드러내고 있었다."], ["And now, when it was dark below, though they seemed solemnly to recede, like spectres who were going to vanish, as the red dye of the sunset faded out of them and left them coldly white, they were yet distinctly defined in their loneliness above the mists and shadows.", "그리고 이제 아래쪽이 어두워지자, 그 봉우리들은 노을의 붉은 물감이 빠져나가 차갑게 흰빛만 남으면서, 마치 사라지려는 유령처럼 엄숙히 물러서는 듯 보였으나, 안개와 그림자 위 저 외로움 속에서는 여전히 그 윤곽이 또렷했다."]], [["Seen from these solitudes, and from the Pass of the Great Saint Bernard, which was one of them, the ascending Night came up the mountain like a rising water.", "이 적막한 곳들, 그 가운데 하나인 그랑생베르나르 고개에서 바라보면, 솟아오르는 밤은 차오르는 물처럼 산을 타고 올라왔다."], ["When it at last rose to the walls of the convent of the Great Saint Bernard, it was as if that weather-beaten structure were another Ark, and floated on the shadowy waves.", "마침내 밤이 그랑생베르나르 수도원의 담장에까지 차오르자, 풍상에 시달린 그 건물은 또 하나의 방주가 되어 그림자의 물결 위에 떠 있는 듯했다."]], [["Darkness, outstripping some visitors on mules, had risen thus to the rough convent walls, when those travellers were yet climbing the mountain.", "노새를 탄 몇몇 길손을 앞질러, 어둠은 이렇듯 거친 수도원 담장에까지 차올랐고, 그때 그 길손들은 아직 산을 오르는 중이었다."], ["As the heat of the glowing day when they had stopped to drink at the streams of melted ice and snow, was changed to the searching cold of the frosty rarefied night air at a great height, so the fresh beauty of the lower journey had yielded to barrenness and desolation.", "녹은 얼음과 눈의 물줄기에서 목을 축이려 멈춰 섰던 한낮의 타는 더위가 높은 곳의 서리 내린 희박한 밤공기의 사무치는 추위로 바뀐 것처럼, 낮은 곳 여정의 싱그러운 아름다움도 황량함과 적막함에 자리를 내주었다."], ["A craggy track, up which the mules in single file scrambled and turned from block to block, as though they were ascending the broken staircase of a gigantic ruin, was their way now.", "노새들이 한 줄로 바위 덩어리에서 덩어리로 기어오르고 돌아가며, 마치 거대한 폐허의 부서진 계단을 오르는 듯한 험한 바윗길이 이제 그들의 길이었다."], ["No trees were to be seen, nor any vegetable growth save a poor brown scrubby moss, freezing in the chinks of rock.", "나무 한 그루 보이지 않았고, 바위 틈에서 얼어붙은 초라한 갈색 덤불 이끼 말고는 어떤 식물도 자라지 않았다."], ["Blackened skeleton arms of wood by the wayside pointed upward to the convent as if the ghosts of former travellers overwhelmed by the snow haunted the scene of their distress.", "길가에 시커멓게 탄 앙상한 나무 팔뚝들이 수도원 쪽을 위로 가리키고 있었는데, 마치 눈에 파묻혀 죽은 옛 길손들의 유령이 제 고통의 자리를 떠도는 것 같았다."], ["Icicle-hung caves and cellars built for refuges from sudden storms, were like so many whispers of the perils of the place; never-resting wreaths and mazes of mist wandered about, hunted by a moaning wind; and snow, the besetting danger of the mountain, against which all its defences were taken, drifted sharply down.", "고드름이 매달린 동굴과, 갑작스러운 폭풍을 피하려고 지은 대피소들은 이곳의 위험을 속삭이는 무수한 입처럼 보였다. 쉴 새 없이 떠도는 안개의 화환과 미로가 신음하는 바람에 쫓겨 떠돌았고, 산에서 가장 끈질긴 위험이자 그 모든 방비가 맞서고 있는 눈은, 날카롭게 휘날려 내렸다."]], [["The file of mules, jaded by their day’s work, turned and wound slowly up the deep ascent; the foremost led by a guide on foot, in his broad-brimmed hat and round jacket, carrying a mountain staff or two upon his shoulder, with whom another guide conversed.", "하루의 고된 일에 지친 노새의 행렬이 깊은 비탈을 천천히 돌고 굽이쳐 올라갔다. 맨 앞에서는 챙 넓은 모자에 짧은 윗도리를 걸치고 어깨에 산악 지팡이 한두 개를 멘 안내인이 걸어서 길을 이끌었고, 또 다른 안내인이 그와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["There was no speaking among the string of riders.", "줄지어 탄 사람들 사이에는 말이 없었다."], ["The sharp cold, the fatigue of the journey, and a new sensation of a catching in the breath, partly as if they had just emerged from very clear crisp water, and partly as if they had been sobbing, kept them silent.", "매서운 추위와 여정의 피로, 그리고 마치 방금 아주 맑고 차가운 물에서 빠져나온 것 같기도 하고 한바탕 흐느낀 것 같기도 한, 숨이 막히는 새로운 감각이 그들을 잠잠하게 했다."]], [["At length, a light on the summit of the rocky staircase gleamed through the snow and mist.", "마침내 바위 계단의 꼭대기에 켜진 불빛이 눈과 안개를 뚫고 어렴풋이 빛났다."], ["The guides called to the mules, the mules pricked up their drooping heads, the travellers’ tongues were loosened, and in a sudden burst of slipping, climbing, jingling, clinking, and talking, they arrived at the convent door.", "안내인들이 노새를 부르자 노새들은 늘어뜨렸던 머리를 쫑긋 세웠고, 길손들의 혀도 풀려, 미끄러지고 기어오르고 딸랑거리고 짤랑거리고 떠드는 소리가 한꺼번에 터져 나오는 가운데, 그들은 수도원 문 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["Other mules had arrived not long before, some with peasant riders and some with goods, and had trodden the snow about the door into a pool of mud.", "다른 노새들도 얼마 전에 도착해 있었는데, 더러는 농부를 태웠고 더러는 짐을 실은 채, 문 앞의 눈을 짓밟아 진창 웅덩이로 만들어 놓았다."], ["Riding-saddles and bridles, pack-saddles and strings of bells, mules and men, lanterns, torches, sacks, provender, barrels, cheeses, kegs of honey and butter, straw bundles and packages of many shapes, were crowded confusedly together in this thawed quagmire and about the steps.", "안장과 굴레, 짐안장과 줄줄이 달린 방울, 노새와 사람, 등불, 횃불, 자루, 사료, 술통, 치즈, 꿀과 버터가 든 작은 통, 짚단, 갖가지 모양의 꾸러미들이 이 녹아내린 수렁과 계단 언저리에 뒤죽박죽 뒤엉켜 있었다."], ["Up here in the clouds, everything was seen through cloud, and seemed dissolving into cloud.", "이 구름 위 높은 곳에서는 모든 것이 구름을 통해 보였고, 모든 것이 구름 속으로 녹아드는 듯했다."], ["The breath of the men was cloud, the breath of the mules was cloud, the lights were encircled by cloud, speakers close at hand were not seen for cloud, though their voices and all other sounds were surprisingly clear.", "사람들의 숨도 구름, 노새들의 숨도 구름이었고, 불빛은 구름에 둘러싸였으며, 바로 가까이에 있는 사람도 구름 때문에 보이지 않았으나 그 목소리와 다른 모든 소리는 놀랄 만큼 또렷했다."], ["Of the cloudy line of mules hastily tied to rings in the wall, one would bite another, or kick another, and then the whole mist would be disturbed: with men diving into it, and cries of men and beasts coming out of it, and no bystander discerning what was wrong.", "담장 고리에 황급히 매어 놓은 그 흐릿한 노새 행렬 중 하나가 다른 놈을 물거나 발로 차면, 안개 전체가 술렁였다. 사람들이 그 속으로 뛰어들고, 사람과 짐승의 외침이 그 속에서 흘러나왔지만, 곁에 선 누구도 무엇이 잘못되었는지 분간하지 못했다."], ["In the midst of this, the great stable of the convent, occupying the basement story and entered by the basement door, outside which all the disorder was, poured forth its contribution of cloud, as if the whole rugged edifice were filled with nothing else, and would collapse as soon as it had emptied itself, leaving the snow to fall upon the bare mountain summit.", "이 한가운데에서, 지하층 전부를 차지하고 지하 문으로 드나드는 수도원의 커다란 마구간은 — 그 모든 소란이 벌어지는 곳이 바로 그 문밖이었다 — 마치 이 험상궂은 건물 전체가 구름 말고는 아무것도 들어 있지 않아서, 제 안을 비우자마자 무너져 내려 헐벗은 산꼭대기에 눈만 내리게 할 것처럼, 제 몫의 구름을 토해 내고 있었다."]], [["While all this noise and hurry were rife among the living travellers, there, too, silently assembled in a grated house half-a-dozen paces removed, with the same cloud enfolding them and the same snow flakes drifting in upon them, were the dead travellers found upon the mountain.", "이 모든 소음과 부산함이 살아 있는 길손들 사이에 무성하던 그때, 거기에서 여남은 걸음 떨어진 쇠창살 친 집 안에도, 같은 구름에 싸이고 같은 눈송이가 흩날려 들어오는 가운데, 산에서 발견된 죽은 길손들이 말없이 모여 있었다."], ["The mother, storm-belated many winters ago, still standing in the corner with her baby at her breast; the man who had frozen with his arm raised to his mouth in fear or hunger, still pressing it with his dry lips after years and years.", "여러 겨울 전 폭풍에 발이 묶인 어머니는 가슴에 아기를 안은 채 아직도 구석에 서 있었고, 두려움 혹은 굶주림에 팔을 입가로 들어 올린 채 얼어 죽은 사내는 여러 해가 지난 지금도 그 마른 입술로 여전히 팔을 누르고 있었다."], ["An awful company, mysteriously come together!", "무시무시한 무리가, 알 수 없는 인연으로 함께 모인 것이다!"], ["A wild destiny for that mother to have foreseen!", "그 어머니가 내다보았을 법한 거친 운명이여!"], ["‘Surrounded by so many and such companions upon whom I never looked, and never shall look, I and my child will dwell together inseparable, on the Great Saint Bernard, outlasting generations who will come to see us, and will never know our name, or one word of our story but the end.’", "\"내가 한 번도 본 적 없고 앞으로도 보지 못할 그토록 많은, 그런 동무들에게 둘러싸여, 나와 내 아이는 그랑생베르나르에서 떨어질 수 없이 함께 머무르며, 우리를 보러 올 세대들을 넘겨 살아남으리라. 그러나 그들은 결코 우리의 이름도, 우리 이야기의 단 한 마디도, 그 끝 말고는 알지 못하리라.\""]], [["The living travellers thought little or nothing of the dead just then.", "살아 있는 길손들은 그때 죽은 자들을 거의, 아니 전혀 떠올리지 않았다."], ["They thought much more of alighting at the convent door, and warming themselves at the convent fire.", "그들은 수도원 문에서 내려 수도원 불가에서 몸을 녹이는 일을 훨씬 더 많이 생각했다."], ["Disengaged from the turmoil, which was already calming down as the crowd of mules began to be bestowed in the stable, they hurried shivering up the steps and into the building.", "노새 떼가 마구간에 들어가기 시작하면서 이미 가라앉아 가던 소동에서 벗어나, 그들은 덜덜 떨며 서둘러 계단을 올라 건물 안으로 들어갔다."], ["There was a smell within, coming up from the floor, of tethered beasts, like the smell of a menagerie of wild animals.", "안에는 매어 둔 짐승들에게서 풍기는, 바닥에서 올라오는 냄새가 있었는데, 야생 동물 우리의 냄새 같았다."], ["There were strong arched galleries within, huge stone piers, great staircases, and thick walls pierced with small sunken windows--fortifications against the mountain storms, as if they had been human enemies.", "안에는 튼튼한 아치형 회랑과 거대한 돌기둥, 큰 계단들이 있었고, 두꺼운 벽에는 깊숙이 박힌 작은 창들이 뚫려 있었다 — 산의 폭풍을 마치 인간 적군이기라도 한 듯 막아 내는 요새인 셈이었다."], ["There were gloomy vaulted sleeping-rooms within, intensely cold, but clean and hospitably prepared for guests.", "안에는 음울한 둥근 천장의 침실들이 있었는데, 몹시 추웠으나 깨끗하고 손님을 맞을 채비가 갖추어져 있었다."], ["Finally, there was a parlour for guests to sit in and sup in, where a table was already laid, and where a blazing fire shone red and high.", "끝으로, 손님들이 앉아 저녁을 먹을 응접실이 하나 있었는데, 그곳에는 이미 식탁이 차려져 있었고, 활활 타는 불이 붉고 높게 빛나고 있었다."]], [["In this room, after having had their quarters for the night allotted to them by two young Fathers, the travellers presently drew round the hearth.", "두 젊은 수사가 길손들에게 하룻밤 묵을 방을 배정해 준 뒤, 이 방에서 그들은 이윽고 난롯가에 둘러앉았다."], ["They were in three parties; of whom the first, as the most numerous and important, was the slowest, and had been overtaken by one of the others on the way up.", "그들은 세 무리였는데, 그중 첫째 무리는 가장 인원이 많고 비중이 큰 만큼 가장 느려서, 올라오는 길에 다른 한 무리에게 따라잡혔다."], ["It consisted of an elderly lady, two grey-haired gentlemen, two young ladies, and their brother.", "이 무리는 나이 지긋한 부인 한 명, 백발의 신사 두 명, 젊은 숙녀 두 명, 그리고 그들의 오라비로 이루어져 있었다."], ["These were attended (not to mention four guides), by a courier, two footmen, and two waiting-maids: which strong body of inconvenience was accommodated elsewhere under the same roof.", "이들에게는 (안내인 네 명은 말할 것도 없고) 안내원 한 명과 하인 두 명, 시중드는 하녀 두 명이 딸려 있었는데, 이 거추장스러운 큰 무리는 같은 지붕 아래 다른 곳에 묵었다."], ["The party that had overtaken them, and followed in their train, consisted of only three members: one lady and two gentlemen.", "이들을 따라잡아 그 뒤를 따라온 무리는 단 세 사람으로, 숙녀 한 명과 신사 두 명이었다."], ["The third party, which had ascended from the valley on the Italian side of the Pass, and had arrived first, were four in number: a plethoric, hungry, and silent German tutor in spectacles, on a tour with three young men, his pupils, all plethoric, hungry, and silent, and all in spectacles.", "셋째 무리는 고개의 이탈리아 쪽 골짜기에서 올라와 가장 먼저 도착했는데, 모두 네 사람이었다. 안경을 낀 다혈질에 굶주리고 말 없는 독일인 가정교사 한 명이, 역시 다혈질에 굶주리고 말 없으며 모두 안경을 낀 세 청년 제자와 함께 여행하는 길이었다."]], [["These three groups sat round the fire eyeing each other drily, and waiting for supper.", "이 세 무리는 불가에 둘러앉아 서로를 무뚝뚝하게 곁눈질하며 저녁을 기다렸다."], ["Only one among them, one of the gentlemen belonging to the party of three, made advances towards conversation.", "그들 가운데 단 한 사람, 세 사람 무리에 속한 신사 한 명만이 대화를 트려고 나섰다."], ["Throwing out his lines for the Chief of the important tribe, while addressing himself to his own companions, he remarked, in a tone of voice which included all the company if they chose to be included, that it had been a long day, and that he felt for the ladies.", "자기 일행에게 말을 건네는 척하면서 그 비중 큰 무리의 우두머리를 겨냥해 낚싯줄을 던지듯, 그는 좌중 누구든 끼고 싶으면 끼어들 수 있는 어조로, 긴 하루였다고, 숙녀분들이 안쓰럽다고 말했다."], ["That he feared one of the young ladies was not a strong or accustomed traveller, and had been over-fatigued two or three hours ago.", "젊은 숙녀 한 분이 튼튼하거나 여행에 익숙한 분 같지 않아 두세 시간 전쯤 지나치게 지쳐 보이지 않았나 걱정스러웠다고."], ["That he had observed, from his station in the rear, that she sat her mule as if she were exhausted.", "자기는 뒤쪽에 있던 자리에서, 그분이 기진맥진한 듯 노새에 앉아 계신 것을 보았노라고."], ["That he had, twice or thrice afterwards, done himself the honour of inquiring of one of the guides, when he fell behind, how the lady did.", "그 뒤로 두세 번, 안내인 한 명이 뒤로 처졌을 때 그분이 어떠신지 물어보는 영광을 누렸다고."], ["That he had been enchanted to learn that she had recovered her spirits, and that it had been but a passing discomfort.", "그분이 기운을 되찾으셨고 그저 잠시 지나가는 불편이었을 뿐이라는 말을 듣고 무척 기뻤다고."], ["That he trusted (by this time he had secured the eyes of the Chief, and addressed him) he might be permitted to express his hope that she was now none the worse, and that she would not regret having made the journey.", "그분이 이제 조금도 나빠지지 않으시고, 이 여정을 떠나 오신 것을 후회하지 않으시기를 바란다는 마음을 표해도 좋을지 (이쯤에서 그는 우두머리의 눈길을 사로잡았고, 그를 향해 말했다) 믿어 본다고 했다."]], [["‘My daughter, I am obliged to you, sir,’ returned the Chief, ‘is quite restored, and has been greatly interested.’", "\"내 딸은 말이오, 고맙소이다, 선생,\" 우두머리가 답했다. \"완전히 회복했고, 무척 흥미로워하고 있소.\""]], [["‘New to mountains, perhaps?’ said the insinuating traveller.", "\"산이 처음이신 모양이지요?\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다."]], [["‘New to--ha--to mountains,’ said the Chief.", "\"처음이오 — 허 — 산이 말이오,\" 우두머리가 말했다."]], [["‘But you are familiar with them, sir?’ the insinuating traveller assumed.", "\"하지만 선생께서는 산에 익숙하시지요?\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 넘겨짚었다."]], [["‘I am--hum--tolerably familiar.", "\"나는 — 흠 — 그럭저럭 익숙하오."], ["Not of late years.", "근래에는 아니지만."], ["Not of late years,’ replied the Chief, with a flourish of his hand.", "근래에는 아니오,\" 우두머리가 손을 한 번 휘저으며 답했다."]], [["The insinuating traveller, acknowledging the flourish with an inclination of his head, passed from the Chief to the second young lady, who had not yet been referred to otherwise than as one of the ladies in whose behalf he felt so sensitive an interest.", "넌지시 파고드는 길손은 고개를 숙여 그 손짓에 화답하고는, 우두머리에게서 둘째 젊은 숙녀에게로 옮겨 갔다. 그 숙녀는 그가 그토록 예민한 관심을 느낀다던 숙녀들 가운데 한 명으로만 언급되었을 뿐, 그밖에 따로 언급된 적은 없었다."]], [["He hoped she was not incommoded by the fatigues of the day.", "그는 그분이 오늘의 피로로 불편을 겪지 않으셨기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["‘Incommoded, certainly,’ returned the young lady, ‘but not tired.’", "\"불편이야 분명 겪었지요,\" 젊은 숙녀가 답했다. \"하지만 지치지는 않았어요.\""]], [["The insinuating traveller complimented her on the justice of the distinction.", "넌지시 파고드는 길손은 그 구분이 정확하다며 그녀를 칭송했다."], ["It was what he had meant to say.", "자기가 하려던 말이 바로 그것이라고."], ["Every lady must doubtless be incommoded by having to do with that proverbially unaccommodating animal, the mule.", "어떤 숙녀라도 노새라는, 속담으로도 다루기 까다롭기로 이름난 그 짐승을 상대하노라면 틀림없이 불편을 겪을 수밖에 없다고."]], [["‘We have had, of course,’ said the young lady, who was rather reserved and haughty, ‘to leave the carriages and fourgon at Martigny.", "\"우리야 물론,\" 다소 새침하고 도도한 젊은 숙녀가 말했다, \"마르티니에서 마차며 짐수레를 두고 와야 했지요."], ["And the impossibility of bringing anything that one wants to this inaccessible place, and the necessity of leaving every comfort behind, is not convenient.’", "게다가 이런 닿기 어려운 곳에는 원하는 것을 무엇 하나 가져올 수 없고, 온갖 안락함을 다 두고 와야 하니, 편할 리가 없지요.\""]], [["‘A savage place indeed,’ said the insinuating traveller.", "\"참으로 야만스러운 곳이지요,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다."]], [["The elderly lady, who was a model of accurate dressing, and whose manner was perfect, considered as a piece of machinery, here interposed a remark in a low soft voice.", "차림새가 정확함의 본보기요, 기계 장치로 친다면 그 태도가 완벽한 나이 지긋한 부인이, 여기에서 낮고 부드러운 목소리로 한마디 끼어들었다."]], [["‘But, like other inconvenient places,’ she observed, ‘it must be seen.", "\"하지만 다른 불편한 곳들과 마찬가지로,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"한 번은 봐 두어야 하는 곳이지요."], ["As a place much spoken of, it is necessary to see it.’", "사람들 입에 많이 오르내리는 곳이니, 봐 둘 필요가 있어요.\""]], [["‘O!", "\"오!"], ["I have not the least objection to seeing it, I assure you, Mrs General,’ returned the other, carelessly.", "봐 두는 거야 저는 조금도 마다하지 않아요, 정말이에요, 제너럴 부인,\" 다른 숙녀가 무심하게 답했다."]], [["‘You, madam,’ said the insinuating traveller, ‘have visited this spot before?’", "\"부인께서는,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다, \"이곳에 전에도 와 보셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Mrs General.", "\"네,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다."], ["‘I have been here before.", "\"전에 와 본 적이 있어요."], ["Let me commend you, my dear,’ to the former young lady, ‘to shade your face from the hot wood, after exposure to the mountain air and snow.", "얘야, 한마디 일러 두마,\" 앞의 젊은 숙녀에게 하는 말이었다, \"산 공기와 눈을 쐰 뒤이니, 뜨거운 장작불에서 얼굴을 가리도록 하렴."], ["You, too, my dear,’ to the other and younger lady, who immediately did so; while the former merely said, ‘Thank you, Mrs General, I am Perfectly comfortable, and prefer remaining as I am.’", "너도, 얘야,\" 다른, 더 어린 숙녀에게 하는 말에 그 숙녀는 곧바로 그렇게 했으나, 앞의 숙녀는 그저 \"고맙습니다, 제너럴 부인, 저는 아주 편안하니 이대로 있는 게 좋아요\"라고만 했다."]], [["The brother, who had left his chair to open a piano that stood in the room, and who had whistled into it and shut it up again, now came strolling back to the fire with his glass in his eye.", "오라비는, 방 안에 놓인 피아노를 열려고 의자를 떠나 그 안에 대고 휘파람을 불어 보고는 다시 닫아 버린 뒤, 한쪽 눈에 외알 안경을 낀 채 어슬렁어슬렁 불가로 돌아왔다."], ["He was dressed in the very fullest and completest travelling trim.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 완벽하게 갖춘 여행 차림이었다."], ["The world seemed hardly large enough to yield him an amount of travel proportionate to his equipment.", "그의 채비에 걸맞은 분량의 여행거리를 내어 주기에는 세상이 도무지 충분치 않아 보일 정도였다."]], [["‘These fellows are an immense time with supper,’ he drawled.", "\"저 친구들, 저녁 차리는 데 엄청 굼뜨군,\" 그가 느릿느릿 말했다."], ["‘I wonder what they’ll give us!", "\"뭘 내올지 궁금하군."], ["Has anybody any idea?’", "누구 짐작 가는 사람 있나?\""]], [["‘Not roast man, I believe,’ replied the voice of the second gentleman of the party of three.", "\"사람 구이는 아닐 것 같은데,\" 세 사람 무리 가운데 둘째 신사의 목소리가 답했다."]], [["‘I suppose not.", "\"안 그렇겠지요."], ["What d’ye mean?’ he inquired.", "무슨 뜻이오?\" 그가 물었다."]], [["‘That, as you are not to be served for the general supper, perhaps you will do us the favour of not cooking yourself at the general fire,’ returned the other.", "\"당신은 다 같이 먹을 저녁상에 오를 분이 아니니, 다 같이 쬐는 불에 당신 몸을 구워 대지 말아 주시는 호의를 베풀어 주십사 하는 뜻이지요,\" 상대가 답했다."]], [["The young gentleman who was standing in an easy attitude on the hearth, cocking his glass at the company, with his back to the blaze and his coat tucked under his arms, something as if he were Of the Poultry species and were trussed for roasting, lost countenance at this reply; he seemed about to demand further explanation, when it was discovered--through all eyes turning on the speaker--that the lady with him, who was young and beautiful, had not heard what had passed through having fainted with her head upon his shoulder.", "난롯가에 편안한 자세로 서서, 불길을 등지고 윗도리 자락을 양팔 밑에 끼운 채 좌중을 향해 외알 안경을 치켜올린 그 젊은 신사는, 마치 가금류에 속하는 짐승이 구이용으로 묶여 있는 것 같은 모양새였는데, 이 대꾸에 낯을 잃었다. 그가 막 더 자세한 설명을 요구하려는 참에 — 모두의 눈길이 말한 이에게 쏠리면서 — 그와 함께 온, 젊고 아름다운 부인이 그의 어깨에 머리를 묻고 까무러쳐서 방금 오간 말을 듣지 못했다는 사실이 드러났다."]], [["‘I think,’ said the gentleman in a subdued tone, ‘I had best carry her straight to her room.", "\"아무래도,\" 신사가 가라앉은 목소리로 말했다, \"곧장 방으로 데려가는 게 낫겠소."], ["Will you call to some one to bring a light?’ addressing his companion, ‘and to show the way?", "누구든 불러서 등불을 가져오게 해 주겠소?\" 일행에게 하는 말이었다, \"그리고 길을 안내하게 해 주오?"], ["In this strange rambling place I don’t know that I could find it.’", "이 낯설고 어수선한 곳에서는 길을 찾을 수 있을지 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘Pray, let me call my maid,’ cried the taller of the young ladies.", "\"제 하녀를 부르게 해 주세요,\" 두 젊은 숙녀 가운데 키가 더 큰 쪽이 외쳤다."]], [["‘Pray, let me put this water to her lips,’ said the shorter, who had not spoken yet.", "\"이 물을 입에 좀 대어 드리게 해 주세요,\" 아직 말이 없던 키가 더 작은 쪽이 말했다."]], [["Each doing what she suggested, there was no want of assistance.", "저마다 제안한 대로 하니 도움이 부족할 리 없었다."], ["Indeed, when the two maids came in (escorted by the courier, lest any one should strike them dumb by addressing a foreign language to them on the road), there was a prospect of too much assistance.", "사실, 두 하녀가 (길에서 누군가 외국어로 말을 걸어 그들을 벙어리로 만들까 봐 안내원의 호위를 받으며) 들어오자, 도움이 너무 넘칠 지경이었다."], ["Seeing this, and saying as much in a few words to the slighter and younger of the two ladies, the gentleman put his wife’s arm over his shoulder, lifted her up, and carried her away.", "이를 보고, 두 숙녀 가운데 더 가냘프고 어린 쪽에게 몇 마디로 그렇게 일러 준 뒤, 신사는 아내의 팔을 자기 어깨에 두르고 그녀를 안아 들어 데리고 나갔다."]], [["His friend, being left alone with the other visitors, walked slowly up and down the room without coming to the fire again, pulling his black moustache in a contemplative manner, as if he felt himself committed to the late retort.", "홀로 다른 손님들과 남게 된 그의 친구는, 다시 불가로 오지 않은 채 천천히 방 안을 오르내리며, 마치 아까의 응수에 자기가 책임이 있다고 느끼기라도 하는 양 생각에 잠겨 검은 콧수염을 잡아당겼다."], ["While the subject of it was breathing injury in a corner, the Chief loftily addressed this gentleman.", "그 응수의 대상이 된 사람이 한구석에서 분을 씩씩 내뿜고 있는 동안, 우두머리가 거만하게 이 신사에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘Your friend, sir,’ said he, ‘is--ha--is a little impatient; and, in his impatience, is not perhaps fully sensible of what he owes to--hum--to--but we will waive that, we will waive that.", "\"선생 친구분은 말이오, 선생,\" 그가 말했다, \"그— 허 — 좀 성마른 분이구려."], ["Your friend is a little impatient, sir.’", "그리고 그 성급함 탓에, 자기가 — 흠 — 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 도리를 온전히 헤아리지 못하는 듯한데 — 아니, 그 이야기는 접어 둡시다, 접어 둡시다. 선생 친구분은 좀 성마른 분이오, 선생.\""]], [["‘It may be so, sir,’ returned the other.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요, 선생,\" 상대가 답했다."], ["‘But having had the honour of making that gentleman’s acquaintance at the hotel at Geneva, where we and much good company met some time ago, and having had the honour of exchanging company and conversation with that gentleman on several subsequent excursions, I can hear nothing--no, not even from one of your appearance and station, sir--detrimental to that gentleman.’", "\"하지만 얼마 전 제네바의 어느 호텔에서, 우리를 비롯해 좋은 분들이 여럿 모였던 그곳에서 저 신사분과 알게 되는 영광을 누렸고, 그 뒤로도 여러 차례 함께 길을 다니며 같이 지내고 이야기를 나누는 영광을 누린 사람으로서, 저는 저 신사분에 대해 해로운 말은 어떤 것도 — 아니, 선생 같은 풍채와 지위를 지닌 분에게서라 해도 — 들을 수 없습니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘You are in no danger, sir, of hearing any such thing from me.", "\"내게서 그런 말을 들으실 일은 없을 것이오, 선생."], ["In remarking that your friend has shown impatience, I say no such thing.", "선생 친구분이 성급함을 보였다고 말한 것이지, 그런 말을 한 것이 아니오."], ["I make that remark, because it is not to be doubted that my son, being by birth and by--ha--by education a--hum--a gentleman, would have readily adapted himself to any obligingly expressed wish on the subject of the fire being equally accessible to the whole of the present circle.", "내가 그 말을 한 것은, 내 아들이 태생으로도 그리고 — 허 — 교육으로도 — 흠 — 신사인 까닭에, 불을 이 자리의 모두가 똑같이 쬘 수 있게 하자는, 정중하게 표한 바람이 있다면 두말없이 거기에 맞추었으리라는 것이 의심할 여지가 없기 때문이오."], ["Which, in principle, I--ha--for all are--hum--equal on these occasions--I consider right.’", "이는 원칙적으로 보아도 나는 — 허 — 이런 경우에는 모두가 — 흠 — 평등하니 — 옳다고 여기는 바이오.\""]], [["‘Good,’ was the reply.", "\"좋습니다,\" 그 대답이었다."], ["‘And there it ends!", "\"그것으로 끝입니다!"], ["I am your son’s obedient servant.", "저는 아드님의 충직한 종입니다."], ["I beg your son to receive the assurance of my profound consideration.", "아드님께 깊은 경의를 받아 주십사 청합니다."], ["And now, sir, I may admit, freely admit, that my friend is sometimes of a sarcastic temper.’", "그리고 이제, 선생, 제 친구가 때로 비꼬는 기질이 있다는 점은 기꺼이, 솔직히 인정하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘The lady is your friend’s wife, sir?’", "\"그 부인이 선생 친구분의 아내이시오, 선생?\""]], [["‘The lady is my friend’s wife, sir.’", "\"그 부인은 제 친구의 아내입니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘She is very handsome.’", "\"매우 아름다우시더이다.\""]], [["‘Sir, she is peerless.", "\"선생, 견줄 데가 없는 분입니다."], ["They are still in the first year of their marriage.", "두 분은 아직 결혼 첫해이지요."], ["They are still partly on a marriage, and partly on an artistic, tour.’", "두 분은 한편으로는 신혼여행, 한편으로는 예술 기행 중입니다.\""]], [["‘Your friend is an artist, sir?’", "\"선생 친구분이 예술가시오, 선생?\""]], [["The gentleman replied by kissing the fingers of his right hand, and wafting the kiss the length of his arm towards Heaven.", "신사는 오른손 손가락에 입을 맞추고는 그 입맞춤을 팔 길이만큼 하늘을 향해 흩날려 보내는 것으로 답했다."], ["As who should say, I devote him to the celestial Powers as an immortal artist!", "마치, 나는 그를 불멸의 예술가로서 천상의 신들께 바치노라, 하고 말하는 듯이."]], [["‘But he is a man of family,’ he added.", "\"하지만 그분은 명문가 출신입니다,\" 그가 덧붙였다."], ["‘His connections are of the best.", "\"혈연 인맥이 더없이 훌륭하지요."], ["He is more than an artist: he is highly connected.", "그분은 예술가 이상입니다. 가문이 대단한 분입니다."], ["He may, in effect, have repudiated his connections, proudly, impatiently, sarcastically (I make the concession of both words); but he has them.", "사실, 그분이 자기 인맥을 거만하게, 성마르게, 비꼬듯이 (두 단어 다 양보하겠습니다) 내쳤을지는 몰라도, 그 인맥은 엄연히 있습니다."], ["Sparks that have been struck out during our intercourse have shown me this.’", "우리가 어울리는 동안 튀어나온 불꽃들이 제게 이를 보여 주었지요.\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"그렇소! 그 부인의 탈이,\""], ["I hope,’ said the lofty gentleman, with the air of finally disposing of the subject, ‘that the lady’s indisposition may be only temporary.’", "거만한 신사가 그 화제를 마침내 정리해 버리는 투로 말했다, \"일시적인 것에 그치기를 바라오.\" \"선생, 저도 그러기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Mere fatigue, I dare say.’", "\"그저 피로 탓이겠지요, 아마.\""]], [["‘Not altogether mere fatigue, sir, for her mule stumbled to-day, and she fell from the saddle.", "\"순전히 피로 탓만은 아닙니다, 선생. 오늘 그분의 노새가 발을 헛디뎌 안장에서 떨어지셨거든요."], ["She fell lightly, and was up again without assistance, and rode from us laughing; but she complained towards evening of a slight bruise in the side.", "가볍게 떨어지셔서 부축 없이 곧장 일어나셨고, 웃으며 우리 곁을 떠나 말을 모셨지요. 하지만 저녁 무렵 옆구리에 가벼운 멍이 들었다고 호소하셨습니다."], ["She spoke of it more than once, as we followed your party up the mountain.’", "우리가 선생 일행을 따라 산을 오르는 동안 그분은 그 이야기를 한 번 이상 하셨습니다.\""]], [["The head of the large retinue, who was gracious but not familiar, appeared by this time to think that he had condescended more than enough.", "그 많은 수행원의 우두머리는, 정중하긴 해도 허물없지는 않은 사람이라, 이쯤 되자 자기가 분에 넘치도록 자세를 낮추었다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["He said no more, and there was silence for some quarter of an hour until supper appeared.", "그는 더는 말이 없었고, 저녁상이 나오기까지 십오 분쯤 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["With the supper came one of the young Fathers (there seemed to be no old Fathers) to take the head of the table.", "저녁상과 함께 젊은 수사 한 명이 (나이 든 수사는 없는 듯했다) 식탁 상석에 앉으러 들어왔다."], ["It was like the supper of an ordinary Swiss hotel, and good red wine grown by the convent in more genial air was not wanting.", "그것은 여느 스위스 호텔의 저녁상과 같았고, 더 온화한 기후에서 수도원이 직접 빚은 좋은 적포도주도 빠지지 않았다."], ["The artist traveller calmly came and took his place at table when the rest sat down, with no apparent sense upon him of his late skirmish with the completely dressed traveller.", "예술가 길손은 다른 이들이 자리에 앉자 태연히 와서 자기 자리를 차지했는데, 아까 말끔히 차려입은 길손과 벌인 옥신각신에 대한 기색은 조금도 없어 보였다."]], [["‘Pray,’ he inquired of the host, over his soup, ‘has your convent many of its famous dogs now?’", "\"실례지만,\" 그가 수프를 먹으며 주인 격인 수사에게 물었다, \"이 수도원에는 그 유명한 개가 지금 여러 마리 있나요?\""]], [["‘Monsieur, it has three.’", "\"무슈, 세 마리 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I saw three in the gallery below.", "\"아래 회랑에서 세 마리를 보았소."], ["Doubtless the three in question.’", "분명 바로 그 세 마리겠군요.\""]], [["The host, a slender, bright-eyed, dark young man of polite manners, whose garment was a black gown with strips of white crossed over it like braces, and who no more resembled the conventional breed of Saint Bernard monks than he resembled the conventional breed of Saint Bernard dogs, replied, doubtless those were the three in question.", "호리호리하고 눈이 반짝이는, 거무스름한 얼굴의 정중한 젊은 주인은, 멜빵처럼 흰 띠가 가로질러 덧대어진 검은 가운을 걸쳤는데, 그가 흔히들 떠올리는 생베르나르 수사의 전형을 닮지 않은 것은 흔히들 떠올리는 생베르나르 개의 전형을 닮지 않은 것과 매한가지였다. 그는 그렇다, 바로 그 세 마리라고 답했다."]], [["‘And I think,’ said the artist traveller, ‘I have seen one of them before.’", "\"그런데 내 생각엔,\" 예술가 길손이 말했다, \"그중 한 마리는 전에 본 적이 있는 것 같소.\""]], [["It was possible.", "그럴 법한 일이었다."], ["He was a dog sufficiently well known.", "그 개는 꽤 널리 알려진 놈이었다."], ["Monsieur might have easily seen him in the valley or somewhere on the lake, when he (the dog) had gone down with one of the order to solicit aid for the convent.", "무슈는 그 개가 수도회의 한 수사와 함께 수도원을 위한 도움을 청하러 내려갔을 때, 골짜기나 호수 어딘가에서 그 개를 쉬이 보았을 수 있었다."]], [["‘Which is done in its regular season of the year, I think?’", "\"그런 일은 해마다 정해진 철에 한다는 거지요, 아마?\""]], [["Monsieur was right.", "무슈의 말이 옳았다."]], [["‘And never without a dog.", "\"그리고 늘 개를 데리고 말이지요."], ["The dog is very important.’", "그 개가 아주 중요한 모양이오.\""]], [["Again Monsieur was right.", "다시 무슈의 말이 옳았다."], ["The dog was very important.", "그 개는 매우 중요했다."], ["People were justly interested in the dog.", "사람들이 그 개에게 관심을 두는 것은 마땅했다."], ["As one of the dogs celebrated everywhere, Ma’amselle would observe.", "어디서나 이름난 그 개들 가운데 한 마리이니, 마드무아젤께서도 보셨을 거라고."]], [["Ma’amselle was a little slow to observe it, as though she were not yet well accustomed to the French tongue.", "마드무아젤은, 아직 프랑스어에 익숙지 않은 듯, 그 말을 알아채는 데 조금 더뎠다."], ["Mrs General, however, observed it for her.", "하지만 제너럴 부인이 그녀를 대신해 알아챘다."]], [["‘Ask him if he has saved many lives?’ said, in his native English, the young man who had been put out of countenance.", "\"그동안 많은 목숨을 구했는지 물어봐 주세요,\" 아까 낯을 잃었던 젊은이가 모국어인 영어로 말했다."]], [["The host needed no translation of the question.", "주인은 그 물음을 통역해 줄 필요가 없었다."], ["He promptly replied in French, ‘No.", "그는 곧바로 프랑스어로 답했다. \"아니요."], ["Not this one.’", "이 녀석은 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ the same gentleman asked.", "\"왜 아니죠?\" 같은 신사가 물었다."]], [["‘Pardon,’ returned the host composedly, ‘give him the opportunity and he will do it without doubt.", "\"실례지만,\" 주인이 차분하게 답했다, \"기회만 주어진다면 이 녀석은 틀림없이 그렇게 할 겁니다."], ["For example, I am well convinced,’ smiling sedately, as he cut up the dish of veal to be handed round, on the young man who had been put out of countenance, ‘that if you, Monsieur, would give him the opportunity, he would hasten with great ardour to fulfil his duty.’", "이를테면, 저는 확신합니다만,\" 송아지 고기 요리를 잘라 돌리려고 하면서, 낯을 잃었던 젊은이를 향해 점잖게 미소 지으며 말했다, \"무슈께서 이 녀석에게 기회를 주신다면, 녀석은 자기 소임을 다하려고 무척이나 열렬히 달려갈 겁니다.\""]], [["The artist traveller laughed.", "예술가 길손이 웃었다."], ["The insinuating traveller (who evinced a provident anxiety to get his full share of the supper), wiping some drops of wine from his moustache with a piece of bread, joined the conversation.", "넌지시 파고드는 길손은 (저녁을 제 몫만큼 빠짐없이 챙기려는 빈틈없는 조바심을 드러내며) 빵 한 조각으로 콧수염에 묻은 포도주 방울을 닦아 내고는 대화에 끼어들었다."]], [["‘It is becoming late in the year, my Father,’ said he, ‘for tourist-travellers, is it not?’", "\"신부님, 관광객들이 다니기에는 철이 늦어 가고 있지 않습니까?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes, it is late.", "\"그렇습니다, 늦었지요."], ["Yet two or three weeks, at most, and we shall be left to the winter snows.’", "그래도 길어야 두세 주만 지나면, 우리는 겨울눈에 갇히게 됩니다.\""]], [["‘And then,’ said the insinuating traveller, ‘for the scratching dogs and the buried children, according to the pictures!’", "\"그러고 나면,\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했다, \"그림에서 보던 대로, 발로 눈을 파헤치는 개들과 파묻힌 아이들의 차례가 오겠군요!\""]], [["‘Pardon,’ said the host, not quite understanding the allusion.", "\"실례지만,\" 주인이 그 비유를 잘 알아듣지 못한 채 말했다."], ["‘How, then the scratching dogs and the buried children according to the pictures?’", "\"어째서, 그림에서 보던 대로, 눈을 파헤치는 개들과 파묻힌 아이들이라는 거죠?\""]], [["The artist traveller struck in again before an answer could be given.", "대답이 나오기 전에 예술가 길손이 다시 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Don’t you know,’ he coldly inquired across the table of his companion, ‘that none but smugglers come this way in the winter or can have any possible business this way?’", "\"자네는 모르나,\" 그가 식탁 너머로 일행에게 냉랭하게 물었다, \"겨울에 이 길로 다니는 건 밀수꾼뿐이고, 이 길로 무슨 볼일이 있을 만한 자도 밀수꾼뿐이라는 걸?\""]], [["‘Holy blue!", "\"맙소사! 모르는데."], ["No; never heard of it.’", "그런 얘긴 들어 본 적이 없어.\""]], [["‘So it is, I believe.", "\"그렇다더군."], ["And as they know the signs of the weather tolerably well, they don’t give much employment to the dogs--who have consequently died out rather--though this house of entertainment is conveniently situated for themselves.", "그리고 그자들은 날씨의 징후를 제법 잘 아니까, 개들을 별로 부려 먹지 않지 — 그래서 개들도 차츰 죽어 나간 셈이야 — 정작 이 숙소는 그자들 자신에게는 자리가 편리하긴 하지만 말이야."], ["Their young families, I am told, they usually leave at home.", "듣자 하니 그자들은 어린 자식들은 보통 집에 두고 온다더군."], ["But it’s a grand idea!’ cried the artist traveller, unexpectedly rising into a tone of enthusiasm.", "하지만 거 참 멋진 발상이야!\" 예술가 길손이 느닷없이 열띤 어조로 목소리를 높이며 외쳤다."], ["‘It’s a sublime idea.", "\"숭고한 발상이지."], ["It’s the finest idea in the world, and brings tears into a man’s eyes, by Jupiter!’ He then went on eating his veal with great composure.", "세상에서 가장 근사한 발상이고, 맹세코, 사람 눈에 눈물이 다 나게 하는 발상이야!\" 그러고는 더없이 태연하게 송아지 고기를 마저 먹어 갔다."]], [["There was enough of mocking inconsistency at the bottom of this speech to make it rather discordant, though the manner was refined and the person well-favoured, and though the depreciatory part of it was so skilfully thrown off as to be very difficult for one not perfectly acquainted with the English language to understand, or, even understanding, to take offence at: so simple and dispassionate was its tone.", "이 말의 바닥에는 조롱 섞인 모순이 충분히 깔려 있어서 다소 거슬리는 데가 있었으나, 태도가 세련되고 사람됨이 인물 좋은 데다, 깎아내리는 부분은 어찌나 솜씨 좋게 흘려 던졌는지, 영어에 완전히 익숙하지 않은 사람으로서는 알아듣기조차 어렵고, 설령 알아듣는다 해도 그 어조가 너무 담담하고 격이 없어 노여워하기조차 어려울 정도였다."], ["After finishing his veal in the midst of silence, the speaker again addressed his friend.", "침묵 속에서 송아지 고기를 다 먹고 나서, 말한 이는 다시 친구에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘Look,’ said he, in his former tone, ‘at this gentleman our host, not yet in the prime of life, who in so graceful a way and with such courtly urbanity and modesty presides over us!", "\"보게,\""], ["Manners fit for a crown!", "그가 아까의 어조로 말했다, \"여기 우리 주인이신 이 신사분을 말이야."], ["Dine with the Lord Mayor of London (if you can get an invitation) and observe the contrast.", "아직 한창때도 안 되었는데, 저토록 우아하게, 저토록 궁정풍의 정중함과 겸손함으로 우리를 보살펴 주시잖나! 왕관에나 어울릴 예법이지!"], ["This dear fellow, with the finest cut face I ever saw, a face in perfect drawing, leaves some laborious life and comes up here I don’t know how many feet above the level of the sea, for no other purpose on earth (except enjoying himself, I hope, in a capital refectory) than to keep an hotel for idle poor devils like you and me, and leave the bill to our consciences!", "런던 시장과 만찬을 해 보고 (초대받을 수 있다면 말이지) 그 차이를 살펴보게나. 내가 여태 본 가장 잘생긴 얼굴을 지닌 이 친구는, 흠 하나 없이 잘 그려진 그 얼굴로, 어디 고된 삶을 버리고 이 위로 — 해발 몇 피트나 되는지도 모를 이 높은 곳으로 — 올라와서는, 자네나 나 같은 한가로운 불쌍한 인간들을 위해 숙소를 지키고 그 계산은 우리 양심에 맡기는 것 말고는 (좋은 식당에서 직접 즐기는 것도 있겠지만, 그러길 바라네) 세상에 다른 목적이라곤 없잖나!"], ["Why, isn’t it a beautiful sacrifice?", "자, 이거 참 아름다운 희생 아닌가?"], ["What do we want more to touch us?", "우리를 감동시키는 데 이보다 더 무엇이 필요한가?"], ["Because rescued people of interesting appearance are not, for eight or nine months out of every twelve, holding on here round the necks of the most sagacious of dogs carrying wooden bottles, shall we disparage the place?", "열두 달 중 여덟아홉 달은, 구조된 사람들이 — 그것도 흥미로운 풍채를 한 이들이 — 나무통을 매단 가장 영리한 개의 목에 매달려 여기 들러붙어 있지 않는다고 해서, 이곳을 깎아내려야 하나? 아니지!"], ["No! Bless the place.", "이곳에 축복을."], ["It’s a great place, a glorious place!’", "위대한 곳이야, 영광스러운 곳이지!\""]], [["The chest of the grey-haired gentleman who was the Chief of the important party, had swelled as if with a protest against his being numbered among poor devils.", "비중 큰 무리의 우두머리인 백발 신사의 가슴은, 자기가 불쌍한 인간들 축에 끼는 데 항의라도 하듯 부풀어 올랐다."], ["No sooner had the artist traveller ceased speaking than he himself spoke with great dignity, as having it incumbent on him to take the lead in most places, and having deserted that duty for a little while.", "예술가 길손이 말을 마치자마자 그는, 어느 자리에서나 앞장서는 것이 자기 소임인데 잠시 그 소임을 저버리고 있었다는 듯, 자못 위엄 있게 입을 열었다."]], [["He weightily communicated his opinion to their host, that his life must be a very dreary life here in the winter.", "그는 주인에게, 겨울철 이곳의 삶이 무척 따분한 삶일 것이 틀림없다는 견해를 묵직하게 전했다."]], [["The host allowed to Monsieur that it was a little monotonous.", "주인은 무슈에게, 다소 단조롭기는 하다고 인정했다."], ["The air was difficult to breathe for a length of time consecutively.", "공기는 오래 잇따라 들이쉬기에 버거웠다."], ["The cold was very severe.", "추위는 매우 혹독했다."], ["One needed youth and strength to bear it.", "그것을 견디려면 젊음과 힘이 필요했다."], ["However, having them and the blessing of Heaven--", "그러나 그것을 갖추고, 또한 하늘의 축복이 있으면 —"]], [["Yes, that was very good.", "그렇소, 참으로 좋은 말이오."], ["‘But the confinement,’ said the grey-haired gentleman.", "\"하지만 그 갇혀 지냄 말이오,\" 백발 신사가 말했다."]], [["There were many days, even in bad weather, when it was possible to walk about outside.", "날씨가 궂은 날에도, 바깥을 거닐 수 있는 날이 많았다."], ["It was the custom to beat a little track, and take exercise there.", "작은 길을 다져 놓고 거기에서 운동을 하는 것이 관례였다."]], [["‘But the space,’ urged the grey-haired gentleman.", "\"하지만 그 공간이,\" 백발 신사가 따지듯 말했다."], ["‘So small.", "\"너무 비좁지 않소."], ["So--ha--very limited.’", "그— 허 — 매우 한정되어 있지 않소.\""]], [["Monsieur would recall to himself that there were the refuges to visit, and that tracks had to be made to them also.", "무슈께서는, 찾아가 볼 대피소들이 있고, 그곳들로 향하는 길도 다져 놓아야 한다는 점을 떠올려 보시라고."]], [["Monsieur still urged, on the other hand, that the space was so--ha--hum--so very contracted.", "무슈는 그래도, 그 공간이 그— 허 — 흠 — 너무나 옹색하다고 거듭 따졌다."], ["More than that, it was always the same, always the same.", "게다가 늘 똑같지 않으냐고, 언제나 똑같지 않으냐고."]], [["With a deprecating smile, the host gently raised and gently lowered his shoulders.", "주인은 송구하다는 미소를 띠며 어깨를 가만히 올렸다가 가만히 내렸다."], ["That was true, he remarked, but permit him to say that almost all objects had their various points of view.", "그것은 사실이라고 그는 말했다. 하지만 거의 모든 사물에는 저마다 다른 관점이 있다는 점을 말씀드리도록 해 달라고."], ["Monsieur and he did not see this poor life of his from the same point of view.", "무슈와 자기는 자기의 이 가난한 삶을 같은 관점에서 보고 있지 않다고."], ["Monsieur was not used to confinement.", "무슈는 갇혀 지냄에 익숙지 않으시니까."]], [["‘I--ha--yes, very true,’ said the grey-haired gentleman.", "\"나는 — 허 — 그렇소, 참으로 옳은 말이오,\""], ["He seemed to receive quite a shock from the force of the argument.", "백발 신사가 말했다. 그는 그 논리의 힘에 적잖이 충격을 받은 듯했다."]], [["Monsieur, as an English traveller, surrounded by all means of travelling pleasantly; doubtless possessing fortune, carriages, and servants--", "무슈는, 영국인 길손으로서, 즐겁게 여행할 온갖 수단에 둘러싸여 있고, 틀림없이 재산과 마차와 하인을 거느린 분이시니 —"]], [["‘Perfectly, perfectly.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소."], ["Without doubt,’ said the gentleman.", "의심할 여지 없이,\" 신사가 말했다."]], [["Monsieur could not easily place himself in the position of a person who had not the power to choose, I will go here to-morrow, or there next day; I will pass these barriers, I will enlarge those bounds.", "무슈로서는, 내일 여기로 가야지, 모레 저기로 가야지, 하고 고를 힘이 없는 사람, 이 장벽들을 넘어가야지, 저 경계들을 넓혀야지 하고 마음대로 할 수 없는 사람의 처지에 자신을 놓아 보기가 쉽지 않으실 거라고."], ["Monsieur could not realise, perhaps, how the mind accommodated itself in such things to the force of necessity.", "무슈는, 그런 일들에서 사람의 마음이 어떻게 필요의 힘에 스스로를 맞추어 가는지를, 아마 실감하지 못하실 거라고."]], [["‘It is true,’ said Monsieur.", "\"그건 사실이오,\" 무슈가 말했다."], ["‘We will--ha--not pursue the subject.", "\"우리는 — 허 — 이 화제를 더 끌지 맙시다."], ["You are--hum--quite accurate, I have no doubt.", "선생 말이 — 흠 — 더없이 옳소, 의심치 않소."], ["We will say no more.’", "더는 말하지 맙시다.\""]], [["The supper having come to a close, he drew his chair away as he spoke, and moved back to his former place by the fire.", "저녁이 끝나자 그는 이렇게 말하며 의자를 뒤로 빼고, 불가의 아까 그 자리로 물러갔다."], ["As it was very cold at the greater part of the table, the other guests also resumed their former seats by the fire, designing to toast themselves well before going to bed.", "식탁의 대부분 자리가 몹시 추웠으므로, 다른 손님들도 잠자리에 들기 전에 몸을 충분히 데우려고 불가의 아까 그 자리로 돌아갔다."], ["The host, when they rose from the table, bowed to all present, wished them good night, and withdrew.", "주인은 그들이 식탁에서 일어서자 좌중에 모두에게 절하고 안녕히 주무시라 인사한 뒤 물러갔다."], ["But first the insinuating traveller had asked him if they could have some wine made hot; and as he had answered Yes, and had presently afterwards sent it in, that traveller, seated in the centre of the group, and in the full heat of the fire, was soon engaged in serving it out to the rest.", "다만 그 전에 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 그에게 포도주를 데워 줄 수 있는지 물었고, 그가 그렇다고 답한 뒤 곧 그것을 들여보내 주었으므로, 그 길손은 무리 한가운데, 불기운을 온전히 받는 자리에 앉아 이내 나머지 사람들에게 그것을 따라 돌리는 일에 분주했다."]], [["At this time, the younger of the two young ladies, who had been silently attentive in her dark corner (the fire-light was the chief light in the sombre room, the lamp being smoky and dull) to what had been said of the absent lady, glided out.", "이 무렵, 두 젊은 숙녀 가운데 더 어린 쪽이 — 어둑한 구석에서 (등불은 그을음이 끼고 흐릿했으므로, 그 음울한 방의 주된 빛은 난롯불이었다) 그 자리에 없는 부인에 관해 오간 말을 말없이 귀 기울여 듣고 있던 그녀가 — 슬며시 방을 빠져나갔다."], ["She was at a loss which way to turn when she had softly closed the door; but, after a little hesitation among the sounding passages and the many ways, came to a room in a corner of the main gallery, where the servants were at their supper.", "문을 살그머니 닫고 나자 어느 쪽으로 가야 할지 막막했으나, 울리는 복도들과 갈래길 사이에서 잠시 망설이다가, 본 회랑 한구석에 있는, 하인들이 저녁을 먹고 있는 방에 이르렀다."], ["From these she obtained a lamp, and a direction to the lady’s room.", "그들에게서 그녀는 등불 하나와 그 부인의 방으로 가는 길을 얻었다."]], [["It was up the great staircase on the story above.", "그것은 큰 계단을 올라간 위층에 있었다."], ["Here and there, the bare white walls were broken by an iron grate, and she thought as she went along that the place was something like a prison.", "군데군데 헐벗은 흰 벽이 쇠창살로 끊겨 있어, 걸어가면서 그녀는 이곳이 어쩐지 감옥 같다고 생각했다."], ["The arched door of the lady’s room, or cell, was not quite shut.", "그 부인의 방, 아니 독방의 아치형 문은 완전히 닫혀 있지 않았다."], ["After knocking at it two or three times without receiving an answer, she pushed it gently open, and looked in.", "두세 번 두드려도 대답이 없자, 그녀는 문을 가만히 밀어 열고 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["The lady lay with closed eyes on the outside of the bed, protected from the cold by the blankets and wrappers with which she had been covered when she revived from her fainting fit.", "부인은 눈을 감은 채 침대 위에 누워 있었는데, 까무러쳤다 깨어났을 때 덮어 주었던 담요와 두르개로 추위를 막고 있었다."], ["A dull light placed in the deep recess of the window, made little impression on the arched room.", "창의 깊숙이 들어간 자리에 놓인 흐릿한 불빛은 아치형 방에 거의 빛을 비추지 못했다."], ["The visitor timidly stepped to the bed, and said, in a soft whisper, ‘Are you better?’", "찾아온 이는 머뭇머뭇 침대로 다가가, 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다. \"좀 나으세요?\""]], [["The lady had fallen into a slumber, and the whisper was too low to awake her.", "부인은 선잠에 빠져 있었고, 그 속삭임은 너무 낮아 그녀를 깨우지 못했다."], ["Her visitor, standing quite still, looked at her attentively.", "찾아온 이는 가만히 선 채 그녀를 유심히 바라보았다."]], [["‘She is very pretty,’ she said to herself.", "\"참 예쁜 분이야,\" 그녀가 혼잣말을 했다."], ["‘I never saw so beautiful a face.", "\"이렇게 아름다운 얼굴은 본 적이 없어."], ["O how unlike me!’", "오, 나와는 어쩌면 이리도 다를까!\""]], [["It was a curious thing to say, but it had some hidden meaning, for it filled her eyes with tears.", "이상한 말이었지만, 거기엔 어떤 숨은 뜻이 담겨 있었으니, 그 말에 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["‘I know I must be right.", "\"내 짐작이 틀림없어."], ["I know he spoke of her that evening.", "그날 저녁 그분이 이분 이야기를 했던 게 분명해."], ["I could very easily be wrong on any other subject, but not on this, not on this!’", "다른 일이라면 얼마든지 틀릴 수 있어도, 이건 아니야, 이것만은 아니야!\""]], [["With a quiet and tender hand she put aside a straying fold of the sleeper’s hair, and then touched the hand that lay outside the covering.", "그녀는 조용하고 다정한 손길로 잠든 이의 흐트러진 머리카락 한 가닥을 옆으로 넘겨 주고는, 이불 밖으로 나와 있는 손을 가만히 만졌다."]], [["‘I like to look at her,’ she breathed to herself.", "\"이분을 바라보는 게 좋아,\" 그녀가 속으로 숨죽여 말했다."], ["‘I like to see what has affected him so much.’", "\"그분의 마음을 그토록 흔들어 놓은 게 무엇인지 보는 게 좋아.\""]], [["She had not withdrawn her hand, when the sleeper opened her eyes and started.", "그녀가 미처 손을 거두지 못했을 때, 잠든 이가 눈을 뜨며 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["‘Pray don’t be alarmed.", "\"놀라지 마세요."], ["I am only one of the travellers from down-stairs.", "저는 아래층에서 온 길손 중 한 명일 뿐이에요."], ["I came to ask if you were better, and if I could do anything for you.’", "좀 나으셨는지, 제가 도와드릴 일은 없는지 여쭈러 왔어요.\""]], [["‘I think you have already been so kind as to send your servants to my assistance?’", "\"이미 친절하게도 하녀를 보내 저를 도와주신 것 같던데요?\""]], [["‘No, not I; that was my sister.", "\"아니요, 제가 아니에요. 그건 제 언니였어요."], ["Are you better?’", "좀 나으세요?\""]], [["‘Much better.", "\"훨씬 나아요."], ["It is only a slight bruise, and has been well looked to, and is almost easy now.", "그저 가벼운 멍이고, 잘 돌봐 주어서 이제 거의 괜찮아졌어요."], ["It made me giddy and faint in a moment.", "그게 한순간에 어지럽고 정신이 아득해지더라고요."], ["It had hurt me before; but at last it overpowered me all at once.’", "전부터 아프긴 했는데, 마지막에 한꺼번에 저를 덮쳤어요.\""]], [["‘May I stay with you until some one comes?", "\"누가 올 때까지 곁에 있어도 될까요?"], ["Would you like it?’", "그러는 게 좋으시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I should like it, for it is lonely here; but I am afraid you will feel the cold too much.’", "\"그래 주면 좋겠어요. 여기 혼자 있으니 외로워서요. 하지만 당신이 너무 추울까 봐 걱정이에요.\""]], [["‘I don’t mind cold.", "\"저는 추위는 괜찮아요."], ["I am not delicate, if I look so.’ She quickly moved one of the two rough chairs to the bedside, and sat down.", "이래 보여도 약한 사람이 아니거든요.\" 그녀는 두 개의 투박한 의자 가운데 하나를 재빨리 침대 곁으로 옮겨 와 앉았다."], ["The other as quickly moved a part of some travelling wrapper from herself, and drew it over her, so that her arm, in keeping it about her, rested on her shoulder.", "상대도 그만큼 재빨리 자기 여행용 두르개의 한 자락을 풀어 그녀에게 덮어 주었는데, 그것을 여미느라 그녀의 팔이 자기 어깨에 얹히게 되었다."]], [["‘You have so much the air of a kind nurse,’ said the lady, smiling on her, ‘that you seem as if you had come to me from home.’", "\"당신은 어찌나 다정한 간병인 같은지,\" 부인이 그녀에게 미소 지으며 말했다, \"꼭 우리 집에서 나를 찾아와 준 사람 같아요.\""]], [["‘I am very glad of it.’", "\"그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["‘I was dreaming of home when I woke just now.", "\"방금 깨어났을 때 집 꿈을 꾸고 있었어요."], ["Of my old home, I mean, before I was married.’", "옛집 말이에요, 결혼하기 전의.\""]], [["‘And before you were so far away from it.’", "\"그리고 그곳에서 이토록 멀리 떠나오기 전의 집이군요.\""]], [["‘I have been much farther away from it than this; but then I took the best part of it with me, and missed nothing.", "\"이보다 훨씬 더 멀리 떠나 본 적도 있어요. 하지만 그땐 그 집의 가장 좋은 것을 함께 가지고 가서, 아쉬울 게 없었지요."], ["I felt solitary as I dropped asleep here, and, missing it a little, wandered back to it.’", "여기서 잠이 들 때 외로웠고, 그게 조금 그리워서, 마음이 그 집으로 거슬러 가더군요.\""]], [["There was a sorrowfully affectionate and regretful sound in her voice, which made her visitor refrain from looking at her for the moment.", "그녀의 목소리에는 슬프도록 애틋하고 안타까운 울림이 깃들어 있어서, 찾아온 이는 그 순간 그녀를 차마 바라보지 못했다."]], [["‘It is a curious chance which at last brings us together, under this covering in which you have wrapped me,’ said the visitor after a pause; ‘for do you know, I think I have been looking for you some time.’", "\"당신이 나를 감싸 준 이 두르개 아래에서 우리가 마침내 이렇게 만나게 되다니, 묘한 인연이에요,\" 잠시 뒤 찾아온 이가 말했다. \"실은 말이에요, 제가 얼마 전부터 당신을 찾고 있었거든요.\""]], [["‘Looking for me?’", "\"저를 찾고 있었다고요?\""]], [["‘I believe I have a little note here, which I was to give to you whenever I found you.", "\"여기 작은 쪽지를 하나 가지고 있는 것 같아요. 당신을 찾게 되면 전해 드리라던 거예요."], ["This is it.", "바로 이거예요."], ["Unless I greatly mistake, it is addressed to you?", "제가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 당신 앞으로 된 것이지요?"], ["Is it not?’", "아니에요?\""]], [["The lady took it, and said yes, and read it.", "부인은 그것을 받아 들고 그렇다고 말한 뒤 읽었다."], ["Her visitor watched her as she did so.", "찾아온 이는 그녀가 읽는 동안 그녀를 지켜보았다."], ["It was very short.", "쪽지는 아주 짧았다."], ["She flushed a little as she put her lips to her visitor’s cheek, and pressed her hand.", "그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 찾아온 이의 뺨에 입을 맞추고 그 손을 꼭 잡았다."]], [["‘The dear young friend to whom he presents me, may be a comfort to me at some time, he says.", "\"그분이 나에게 소개해 주신 그 다정한 젊은 벗이, 언젠가 나에게 위로가 되어 줄지 모른다고 여기 적혀 있네요."], ["She is truly a comfort to me the first time I see her.’", "그런데 그 벗은, 내가 처음 보는 이 순간부터 이미 진정한 위로가 되어 주고 있어요.\""]], [["‘Perhaps you don’t,’ said the visitor, hesitating--‘perhaps you don’t know my story?", "\"어쩌면 당신은,\" 찾아온 이가 머뭇거리며 말했다, \"어쩌면 당신은 제 사연을 모르시겠지요?"], ["Perhaps he never told you my story?’", "어쩌면 그분이 제 사연을 한 번도 말씀하지 않으셨겠지요?\""]], [["‘No.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘Oh no, why should he!", "\"오, 아니, 무엇하러 그러셨겠어요!"], ["I have scarcely the right to tell it myself at present, because I have been entreated not to do so.", "저도 지금은 그걸 직접 말씀드릴 처지가 거의 못 돼요. 그러지 말라고 부탁을 받았거든요."], ["There is not much in it, but it might account to you for my asking you not to say anything about the letter here.", "별것 아닌 사연이지만, 어쩌면 그 때문에 제가 여기서는 이 편지에 대해 아무 말도 말아 달라고 부탁드리는 까닭을 짐작하실 수 있을 거예요."], ["You saw my family with me, perhaps?", "아마 저와 함께 온 제 가족을 보셨겠지요?"], ["Some of them--I only say this to you--are a little proud, a little prejudiced.’", "그 가운데 몇몇은 — 이 말은 당신께만 드리는 거예요 — 조금 자존심이 세고, 조금 편견이 있어요.\""]], [["‘You shall take it back again,’ said the other; ‘and then my husband is sure not to see it.", "\"이건 다시 당신이 가져가는 게 좋겠어요,\" 상대가 말했다."], ["He might see it and speak of it, otherwise, by some accident.", "\"그러면 제 남편이 절대 보지 못할 테니까요. 안 그러면 어쩌다 그가 이걸 보고 입에 올릴 수도 있잖아요."], ["Will you put it in your bosom again, to be certain?’", "확실히 해 두게, 다시 품속에 넣어 두시겠어요?\""]], [["She did so with great care.", "그녀는 그것을 매우 조심스럽게 그렇게 했다."], ["Her small, slight hand was still upon the letter, when they heard some one in the gallery outside.", "작고 가냘픈 그녀의 손이 아직 그 편지 위에 놓여 있을 때, 그들은 바깥 회랑에서 누군가의 인기척을 들었다."]], [["‘I promised,’ said the visitor, rising, ‘that I would write to him after seeing you (I could hardly fail to see you sooner or later), and tell him if you were well and happy.", "\"제가 약속했거든요,\" 찾아온 이가 일어서며 말했다, \"당신을 만난 뒤에 (조만간 당신을 만나게 될 게 거의 틀림없었으니까요) 그분께 편지를 써서, 당신이 잘 지내고 행복한지 알려 드리겠다고요."], ["I had better say you were well and happy.’", "잘 지내고 행복하시다고 적는 게 좋겠어요.\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, yes!", "\"네, 네, 네!"], ["Say I was very well and very happy.", "아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복하다고 적어 주세요."], ["And that I thanked him affectionately, and would never forget him.’", "그리고 그분께 애정 어린 감사를 드리며, 결코 그분을 잊지 않겠다고요.\""]], [["‘I shall see you in the morning.", "\"아침에 또 뵐게요."], ["After that we are sure to meet again before very long.", "그다음에도 머지않아 분명 다시 만나게 될 거예요."], ["Good night!’", "안녕히 주무세요!\""]], [["‘Good night.", "\"안녕히 주무세요."], ["Thank you, thank you.", "고마워요, 고마워요."], ["Good night, my dear!’", "잘 자요, 그대!\""]], [["Both of them were hurried and fluttered as they exchanged this parting, and as the visitor came out of the door.", "이 작별 인사를 주고받을 때도, 찾아온 이가 문밖으로 나설 때도, 두 사람 다 마음이 들떠 떨리고 있었다."], ["She had expected to meet the lady’s husband approaching it; but the person in the gallery was not he: it was the traveller who had wiped the wine-drops from his moustache with the piece of bread.", "그녀는 그 부인의 남편이 문 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 마주칠 줄 알았으나, 회랑에 있는 사람은 그가 아니었다. 빵 조각으로 콧수염에 묻은 포도주 방울을 닦아 내던 그 길손이었다."], ["When he heard the step behind him, he turned round--for he was walking away in the dark.", "등 뒤의 발소리를 듣고 그는 돌아섰다 — 어둠 속에서 저쪽으로 걸어가던 참이었다."]], [["His politeness, which was extreme, would not allow of the young lady’s lighting herself down-stairs, or going down alone.", "지극히 깍듯한 그의 예의는, 젊은 숙녀가 손수 불을 밝혀 가며 아래층으로 내려가거나 혼자 내려가도록 두고 보지 못했다."], ["He took her lamp, held it so as to throw the best light on the stone steps, and followed her all the way to the supper-room.", "그는 그녀의 등불을 받아 들어 돌계단에 가장 밝게 빛이 비치도록 들고, 저녁 식사를 한 방까지 줄곧 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["She went down, not easily hiding how much she was inclined to shrink and tremble; for the appearance of this traveller was particularly disagreeable to her.", "그녀는 자기가 얼마나 움츠러들고 떨고 싶은지를 가까스로 감추며 내려갔다. 이 길손의 모습이 그녀에게는 유독 거북했기 때문이다."], ["She had sat in her quiet corner before supper imagining what he would have been in the scenes and places within her experience, until he inspired her with an aversion that made him little less than terrific.", "저녁 식사 전 조용한 구석에 앉아, 그녀는 그가 자기 경험 속의 장면과 장소들에 놓이면 어떤 모습일지 상상하다가, 마침내 그에게서 거의 끔찍하다 할 만한 혐오감을 느끼게 되었던 것이다."]], [["He followed her down with his smiling politeness, followed her in, and resumed his seat in the best place in the hearth.", "그는 미소 띤 정중함으로 그녀를 따라 내려갔고, 그녀를 따라 방으로 들어와, 난롯가의 가장 좋은 자리에 다시 앉았다."], ["There with the wood-fire, which was beginning to burn low, rising and falling upon him in the dark room, he sat with his legs thrust out to warm, drinking the hot wine down to the lees, with a monstrous shadow imitating him on the wall and ceiling.", "사그라들기 시작한 장작불이 어두운 방 안에서 그의 위로 너울대며 오르내리는 가운데, 그는 두 다리를 쭉 뻗어 녹이며, 벽과 천장에 그를 흉내 내는 괴물 같은 그림자를 드리운 채, 데운 포도주를 찌꺼기까지 들이켜며 앉아 있었다."]], [["The tired company had broken up, and all the rest were gone to bed except the young lady’s father, who dozed in his chair by the fire.", "지친 일행은 흩어졌고, 불가의 의자에서 졸고 있는 젊은 숙녀의 아버지를 빼고는 나머지 모두가 잠자리에 들었다."], ["The traveller had been at the pains of going a long way up-stairs to his sleeping-room to fetch his pocket-flask of brandy.", "길손은 일부러 한참이나 위층 자기 침실까지 올라가 호주머니에 넣어 두던 브랜디 병을 가져왔다."], ["He told them so, as he poured its contents into what was left of the wine, and drank with a new relish.", "그는 남은 포도주에 그 내용물을 부으며 그들에게 그 이야기를 했고, 새로운 맛에 흡족해하며 마셨다."]], [["‘May I ask, sir, if you are on your way to Italy?’", "\"실례지만, 선생, 이탈리아로 가시는 길입니까?\""]], [["The grey-haired gentleman had roused himself, and was preparing to withdraw.", "백발 신사가 몸을 추스르고 물러갈 채비를 하던 참이었다."], ["He answered in the affirmative.", "그는 그렇다고 답했다."]], [["‘I also!’ said the traveller.", "\"저도 그렇습니다!\""], ["‘I shall hope to have the honour of offering my compliments in fairer scenes, and under softer circumstances, than on this dismal mountain.’", "길손이 말했다. \"이 음울한 산보다 더 아름다운 풍경 속에서, 더 부드러운 상황 아래에서 다시 인사 올리는 영광을 누리기를 바라겠습니다.\""]], [["The gentleman bowed, distantly enough, and said he was obliged to him.", "신사는 제법 데면데면하게 절하고는, 고맙다고 말했다."]], [["‘We poor gentlemen, sir,’ said the traveller, pulling his moustache dry with his hand, for he had dipped it in the wine and brandy; ‘we poor gentlemen do not travel like princes, but the courtesies and graces of life are precious to us.", "\"우리 같은 가난한 신사들은 말입니다, 선생,\" 길손이 손으로 콧수염을 훔쳐 말렸는데, 포도주와 브랜디에 담갔던 탓이었다, \"우리 가난한 신사들은 왕족처럼 여행하지는 못해도, 삶의 예의와 품위만은 우리에게 소중하지요."], ["To your health, sir!’", "선생의 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["‘Sir, I thank you.’", "\"선생, 고맙소.\""]], [["‘To the health of your distinguished family--of the fair ladies, your daughters!’", "\"선생의 명망 높은 가문의 건강을 위하여 — 아리따운 숙녀분들, 따님들의 건강을 위하여!\""]], [["‘Sir, I thank you again, I wish you good night.", "\"선생, 다시 한번 고맙소. 안녕히 주무시기를."], ["My dear, are our--ha--our people in attendance?’", "얘야, 우리 — 허 — 우리 사람들이 시중들러 와 있느냐?\""]], [["‘They are close by, father.’", "\"바로 곁에 있어요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Permit me!’ said the traveller, rising and holding the door open, as the gentleman crossed the room towards it with his arm drawn through his daughter’s.", "\"제가 열어 드리지요!\" 길손이 일어나 문을 잡아 주며 말했는데, 신사가 딸의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼고 방을 가로질러 문 쪽으로 가던 참이었다."], ["‘Good repose!", "\"편히 쉬시기를!"], ["To the pleasure of seeing you once more!", "다시 한번 뵙게 될 기쁨을 위하여!"], ["To to-morrow!’", "내일을 위하여!\""]], [["As he kissed his hand, with his best manner and his daintiest smile, the young lady drew a little nearer to her father, and passed him with a dread of touching him.", "그가 더없이 우아한 태도와 가장 멋스러운 미소로 손에 입을 맞추는 동안, 젊은 숙녀는 아버지 쪽으로 조금 더 바짝 다가서서, 그에게 닿을까 두려워하며 그의 곁을 지나쳤다."]], [["‘Humph!’ said the insinuating traveller, whose manner shrunk, and whose voice dropped when he was left alone.", "\"흥!\" 넌지시 파고드는 길손이 말했는데, 혼자 남게 되자 그의 태도는 움츠러들고 목소리는 가라앉았다."], ["‘If they all go to bed, why I must go.", "\"다들 잠자리에 든다면, 나도 가야겠군. 거 더럽게도 서두르는군."], ["They are in a devil of a hurry.", "이 얼어붙은 적막과 고독 속에서라면, 두 시간 뒤에 잠자리에 든다 해도 밤이 충분히 길 텐데 말이야.\" 잔을 비우느라 고개를 뒤로 젖히던 그는, 피아노 위에 펜과 잉크가 곁에 놓인 채 펼쳐져 있는 길손 명부에 눈길을 던졌다."], ["One would think the night would be long enough, in this freezing silence and solitude, if one went to bed two hours hence.’", "마치 그가 자리에 없는 사이에 오늘 밤 이름들이 적혀 있는 듯했다. 그것을 손에 들고 그는 이 기록들을 읽었다."]], [["Throwing back his head in emptying his glass, he cast his eyes upon the travellers’ book, which lay on the piano, open, with pens and ink beside it, as if the night’s names had been registered when he was absent. Taking it in his hand, he read these entries.", "윌리엄 도릿 귀하\n프레더릭 도릿 귀하"]], [["William Dorrit, Esquire Frederick Dorrit, Esquire Edward Dorrit, Esquire Miss Dorrit Miss Amy Dorrit Mrs General and Suite. From France to Italy.", "에드워드 도릿 귀하\n도릿 양"]], [["Mr and Mrs Henry Gowan. From France to Italy.", "에이미 도릿 양\n제너럴 부인"]], [["To which he added, in a small complicated hand, ending with a long lean flourish, not unlike a lasso thrown at all the rest of the names:", "그리고 수행원 일동. 프랑스에서 이탈리아로."]], [["Blandois. Paris.", "헨리 가우언 부부."], ["From France to Italy.", "프랑스에서 이탈리아로."]], [["And then, with his nose coming down over his moustache and his moustache going up and under his nose, repaired to his allotted cell.", "거기에 그는, 마치 나머지 이름들 전부를 향해 던진 올가미처럼 길고 가느다란 장식 획으로 끝맺는, 작고 복잡한 필체로 이렇게 덧붙였다. 블랑두아. 파리."]], [["# CHAPTER 2. Mrs General", "# CHAPTER 2. Mrs General"]], [["It is indispensable to present the accomplished lady who was of sufficient importance in the suite of the Dorrit Family to have a line to herself in the Travellers’ Book.", "프랑스에서 이탈리아로. 그러고는, 콧날을 콧수염 위로 내리고 콧수염을 콧날 밑으로 치켜올린 채, 자기에게 배정된 독방으로 물러갔다."]], [["Mrs General was the daughter of a clerical dignitary in a cathedral town, where she had led the fashion until she was as near forty-five as a single lady can be.", "도릿 일가의 수행원 가운데, 여행자 명부에 자기 이름 한 줄을 차지할 만큼 충분히 중요한 인물이었던 그 교양 있는 부인을 소개하는 일은 빼놓을 수 없다."], ["A stiff commissariat officer of sixty, famous as a martinet, had then become enamoured of the gravity with which she drove the proprieties four-in-hand through the cathedral town society, and had solicited to be taken beside her on the box of the cool coach of ceremony to which that team was harnessed.", "제너럴 부인은 어느 대성당 도시에 사는 성직 고위 인사의 딸로, 미혼의 부인이 다다를 수 있는 마흔다섯 살에 거의 근접할 때까지 그곳에서 유행을 선도해 왔다. 예순 살의 한 깐깐한 병참 장교는, 군기에 엄격한 사람으로 이름난 자였는데, 그녀가 대성당 도시의 사교계 한복판으로 예법이라는 사두마차를 몰아 가던 그 위엄에 반하고 말았다."], ["His proposal of marriage being accepted by the lady, the commissary took his seat behind the proprieties with great decorum, and Mrs General drove until the commissary died.", "그래서 그 사두마차가 매여 있는 의례라는 냉랭한 마차의 마부석에서 그녀 곁에 앉게 해 달라고 청했다. 그의 청혼이 부인에게 받아들여지자, 병참 장교는 더없이 점잖게 예법 뒤편에 자기 자리를 잡았고, 제너럴 부인은 병참 장교가 죽을 때까지 마차를 몰았다."], ["In the course of their united journey, they ran over several people who came in the way of the proprieties; but always in a high style and with composure.", "두 사람이 함께한 여정에서 그들은 예법의 길목에 끼어든 여러 사람을 깔아뭉갰지만, 언제나 고상한 품새로, 침착하게 그렇게 했다."]], [["The commissary having been buried with all the decorations suitable to the service (the whole team of proprieties were harnessed to his hearse, and they all had feathers and black velvet housings with his coat of arms in the corner), Mrs General began to inquire what quantity of dust and ashes was deposited at the bankers’.", "병참 장교가 그 직무에 어울리는 온갖 장식을 갖추고 묻히고 나자(예법이라는 사두마차 전체가 그의 영구차에 매였고, 모두 깃털 장식과 검은 벨벳 마의를 둘렀으며 그 한구석에는 그의 문장이 박혀 있었다), 제너럴 부인은 은행에 먼지와 재가 얼마나 예치되어 있는지 알아보기 시작했다."], ["It then transpired that the commissary had so far stolen a march on Mrs General as to have bought himself an annuity some years before his marriage, and to have reserved that circumstance in mentioning, at the period of his proposal, that his income was derived from the interest of his money.", "그러자 병참 장교가 결혼 몇 해 전에 종신연금을 사 두고도, 청혼 당시 자기 수입이 재산의 이자에서 나온다고만 말하면서 그 사정을 슬쩍 감춤으로써 제너럴 부인에게 한발 앞섰다는 사실이 드러났다."], ["Mrs General consequently found her means so much diminished, that, but for the perfect regulation of her mind, she might have felt disposed to question the accuracy of that portion of the late service which had declared that the commissary could take nothing away with him.", "그 결과 제너럴 부인은 자기 재산이 너무도 줄어든 것을 알게 되어, 만약 그 정신이 완벽하게 다스려지지 않았더라면, 병참 장교가 아무것도 가지고 갈 수 없다고 선언했던 장례 의식의 그 대목이 정확한지 의심하고 싶은 마음이 들었을지도 모른다."]], [["In this state of affairs it occurred to Mrs General, that she might ‘form the mind,’ and eke the manners of some young lady of distinction.", "이런 형편에서 제너럴 부인은, 어느 명문가 젊은 아가씨의 '정신을 형성하고' 아울러 그 몸가짐도 가다듬어 줄 수 있겠다는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["Or, that she might harness the proprieties to the carriage of some rich young heiress or widow, and become at once the driver and guard of such vehicle through the social mazes.", "또는 어느 부유한 젊은 상속녀나 미망인의 마차에 예법을 매어, 그런 탈것을 몰고 사교계의 미로를 헤쳐 나가는 마부이자 호위병이 단번에 될 수 있겠다는 생각도 들었다."], ["Mrs General’s communication of this idea to her clerical and commissariat connection was so warmly applauded that, but for the lady’s undoubted merit, it might have appeared as though they wanted to get rid of her.", "제너럴 부인이 이 착상을 성직과 병참 쪽 친지들에게 전하자 어찌나 열렬한 박수를 받았던지, 부인의 의심할 바 없는 미덕이 아니었다면 마치 그들이 그녀를 떼어 내고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보였을 정도였다."], ["Testimonials representing Mrs General as a prodigy of piety, learning, virtue, and gentility, were lavishly contributed from influential quarters; and one venerable archdeacon even shed tears in recording his testimony to her perfections (described to him by persons on whom he could rely), though he had never had the honour and moral gratification of setting eyes on Mrs General in all his life.", "제너럴 부인을 신앙심과 학식, 덕성과 품위의 경이라고 일컫는 추천장들이 유력한 곳들로부터 아낌없이 쏟아져 들어왔다. 어느 존경받는 부주교는 평생 단 한 번도 제너럴 부인을 직접 본 영광이나 도덕적 만족을 누린 적이 없으면서도, (믿을 만한 사람들이 그에게 묘사해 준) 그녀의 완벽함을 증언하며 기록할 때 눈물까지 흘렸다."]], [["Thus delegated on her mission, as it were by Church and State, Mrs General, who had always occupied high ground, felt in a condition to keep it, and began by putting herself up at a very high figure.", "이렇게 이를테면 교회와 국가로부터 사명을 위임받은 제너럴 부인은, 늘 높은 자리를 차지해 왔던 터라 그 자리를 지킬 만한 처지라 여겼고, 자기 몸값을 아주 높게 매기는 것으로 일을 시작했다."], ["An interval of some duration elapsed, in which there was no bid for Mrs General.", "한동안 시간이 흐르도록 제너럴 부인에게 응찰하는 이가 없었다."], ["At length a county-widower, with a daughter of fourteen, opened negotiations with the lady; and as it was a part either of the native dignity or of the artificial policy of Mrs General (but certainly one or the other) to comport herself as if she were much more sought than seeking, the widower pursued Mrs General until he prevailed upon her to form his daughter’s mind and manners.", "마침내 열네 살 난 딸을 둔 어느 시골의 홀아비가 부인과 협상을 트게 되었다. 그런데 자기가 구하는 것이 아니라 오히려 훨씬 더 구해지는 사람인 양 처신하는 것이 제너럴 부인의 타고난 위엄에서 비롯된 것이든 인위적인 처세술에서 비롯된 것이든(어쨌든 둘 중 하나임은 분명했다) 그 일부였던 까닭에, 홀아비는 부인이 자기 딸의 정신과 몸가짐을 가다듬어 주도록 승낙할 때까지 줄곧 그녀를 좇았다."]], [["The execution of this trust occupied Mrs General about seven years, in the course of which time she made the tour of Europe, and saw most of that extensive miscellany of objects which it is essential that all persons of polite cultivation should see with other people’s eyes, and never with their own.", "이 임무를 수행하는 데 제너럴 부인은 약 칠 년을 들였고, 그동안 유럽을 두루 돌며, 세련된 교양을 갖춘 사람이라면 누구나 제 눈이 아니라 남의 눈으로 보아야 마땅한 그 방대하고 잡다한 볼거리의 대부분을 구경했다."], ["When her charge was at length formed, the marriage, not only of the young lady, but likewise of her father, the widower, was resolved on.", "마침내 맡은 아가씨의 정신이 다 형성되자, 그 젊은 아가씨뿐 아니라 그녀의 아버지인 홀아비의 결혼까지도 결정되었다."], ["The widower then finding Mrs General both inconvenient and expensive, became of a sudden almost as much affected by her merits as the archdeacon had been, and circulated such praises of her surpassing worth, in all quarters where he thought an opportunity might arise of transferring the blessing to somebody else, that Mrs General was a name more honourable than ever.", "그러자 홀아비는 제너럴 부인이 거추장스럽고 돈이 많이 든다는 것을 깨닫고는, 별안간 부주교 못지않게 그녀의 미덕에 감복한 듯 굴면서, 이 축복을 다른 누군가에게 넘길 기회가 생길 법한 곳이면 어디서든 그녀의 빼어난 가치를 칭송하고 다녔다. 그리하여 제너럴 부인의 이름은 그 어느 때보다 더 명예로워졌다."]], [["The phoenix was to let, on this elevated perch, when Mr Dorrit, who had lately succeeded to his property, mentioned to his bankers that he wished to discover a lady, well-bred, accomplished, well connected, well accustomed to good society, who was qualified at once to complete the education of his daughters, and to be their matron or chaperon.", "이 불사조가 그 드높은 횃대에 세 들 곳을 찾고 있을 무렵, 최근에 재산을 물려받은 도릿 씨가 자기 거래 은행에 일러두기를, 좋은 가문 출신에 교양 있고 인맥이 좋으며 상류 사회에 익숙하여, 자기 딸들의 교육을 마무리해 주는 동시에 그들의 보호자 겸 동반자가 될 자격을 갖춘 부인을 찾고 싶다고 했다."], ["Mr Dorrit’s bankers, as bankers of the county-widower, instantly said, ‘Mrs General.’", "도릿 씨의 거래 은행은, 그 시골 홀아비의 거래 은행이기도 했던 터라, 즉각 \"제너럴 부인\"이라고 말했다."]], [["Pursuing the light so fortunately hit upon, and finding the concurrent testimony of the whole of Mrs General’s acquaintance to be of the pathetic nature already recorded, Mr Dorrit took the trouble of going down to the county of the county-widower to see Mrs General, in whom he found a lady of a quality superior to his highest expectations.", "이토록 다행스럽게 비친 그 빛줄기를 좇아, 제너럴 부인을 아는 사람들 전부의 한결같은 증언이 앞서 기록한 그 애절한 성격을 띠고 있음을 확인한 도릿 씨는, 수고를 마다하지 않고 그 시골 홀아비의 고장까지 내려가 제너럴 부인을 만나 보았는데, 그녀에게서 자신의 가장 높은 기대마저 뛰어넘는 품격을 갖춘 부인을 발견했다."]], [["‘Might I be excused,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘if I inquired--ha--what remune--’", "\"실례가 안 된다면,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"한 가지 여쭤도—하—보수가 얼마나—\""]], [["‘Why, indeed,’ returned Mrs General, stopping the word, ‘it is a subject on which I prefer to avoid entering.", "\"실은요,\" 제너럴 부인이 그 말을 끊으며 답했다."], ["I have never entered on it with my friends here; and I cannot overcome the delicacy, Mr Dorrit, with which I have always regarded it.", "\"그건 제가 들어가기를 꺼리는 주제랍니다. 여기 제 벗들과도 그 이야기에 들어간 적이 없거니와, 도릿 씨, 저는 늘 그 문제를 대해 온 그 섬세한 마음을 도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요."], ["I am not, as I hope you are aware, a governess--’", "아시겠지만 저는 가정교사가 아니랍니다—\""]], [["‘O dear no!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"아, 천만에요!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Pray, madam, do not imagine for a moment that I think so.’ He really blushed to be suspected of it.", "\"부디, 부인, 제가 한순간이라도 그렇게 여긴다고는 생각지 말아 주십시오.\" 그는 정말로 그런 의심을 받은 것에 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["Mrs General gravely inclined her head.", "제너럴 부인은 점잖게 고개를 숙였다."], ["‘I cannot, therefore, put a price upon services which it is a pleasure to me to render if I can render them spontaneously, but which I could not render in mere return for any consideration.", "\"그러니 저는, 자발적으로 베풀 수만 있다면 베푸는 것이 제게 기쁨이 되는 봉사에, 그리고 어떤 대가를 바라고서는 결코 베풀 수 없는 봉사에 값을 매길 수가 없답니다."], ["Neither do I know how, or where, to find a case parallel to my own.", "또한 저는 제 처지와 견줄 만한 사례를 어디서, 어떻게 찾아야 할지도 모르겠어요."], ["It is peculiar.’", "제 처지는 특이하니까요.\""]], [["No doubt.", "물론 그렇다."], ["But how then (Mr Dorrit not unnaturally hinted) could the subject be approached?", "그렇다면 (도릿 씨가 자연스럽게 넌지시 비추기를) 이 문제에 어떻게 접근하면 좋겠는가?"]], [["‘I cannot object,’ said Mrs General--‘though even that is disagreeable to me--to Mr Dorrit’s inquiring, in confidence of my friends here, what amount they have been accustomed, at quarterly intervals, to pay to my credit at my bankers’.’", "\"저는 반대하지 않겠어요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"비록 그것조차 제게는 거북한 일이긴 합니다만—도릿 씨가, 여기 제 벗들에게 은밀히 여쭈어, 그들이 분기마다 제 거래 은행의 제 계좌로 얼마를 치러 왔는지 알아보시는 것 말이에요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit bowed his acknowledgements.", "도릿 씨는 고개를 숙여 감사를 표했다."]], [["‘Permit me to add,’ said Mrs General, ‘that beyond this, I can never resume the topic.", "\"한마디 덧붙이게 해 주세요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다."], ["Also that I can accept no second or inferior position.", "\"이 이상은 제가 결코 그 화제를 다시 꺼낼 수 없다는 것을요."], ["If the honour were proposed to me of becoming known to Mr Dorrit’s family--I think two daughters were mentioned?--’", "또한 저는 두 번째 자리나 그보다 못한 자리는 받아들일 수 없습니다. 만약 제가 도릿 씨 가족과 알고 지내는 영광을 제안받는다면—따님이 두 분이라고 하셨던가요?—\""]], [["‘Two daughters.’", "\"두 딸입니다.\""]], [["‘I could only accept it on terms of perfect equality, as a companion, protector, Mentor, and friend.’", "\"저는 그저 완벽히 대등한 조건으로만, 동반자이자 보호자이자 멘토이자 벗으로서만 그것을 받아들일 수 있어요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit, in spite of his sense of his importance, felt as if it would be quite a kindness in her to accept it on any conditions.", "도릿 씨는 자신의 위세에 대한 자부심에도 불구하고, 그녀가 어떤 조건으로든 그것을 받아들여 준다면 그야말로 큰 호의라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["He almost said as much.", "그는 거의 그렇게 말할 뻔했다."]], [["‘I think,’ repeated Mrs General, ‘two daughters were mentioned?’", "\"제 생각에는,\" 제너럴 부인이 거듭 말했다. \"따님이 두 분이라고 하셨지요?\""]], [["‘Two daughters,’ said Mr Dorrit again.", "\"두 딸입니다.\" 도릿 씨가 다시 말했다."]], [["‘It would therefore,’ said Mrs General, ‘be necessary to add a third more to the payment (whatever its amount may prove to be), which my friends here have been accustomed to make to my bankers’.’", "\"그러니,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"여기 제 벗들이 제 거래 은행에 치러 오던 그 금액에(그게 얼마로 드러나든) 삼분의 일을 더 보태야 할 필요가 있겠어요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit lost no time in referring the delicate question to the county-widower, and finding that he had been accustomed to pay three hundred pounds a-year to the credit of Mrs General, arrived, without any severe strain on his arithmetic, at the conclusion that he himself must pay four.", "도릿 씨는 지체 없이 그 미묘한 문제를 시골 홀아비에게 알아보았고, 그가 제너럴 부인의 계좌로 해마다 삼백 파운드를 치러 왔다는 것을 알게 되자, 자신의 산수에 별 무리를 주지 않고도 자기는 사백 파운드를 치러야 한다는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["Mrs General being an article of that lustrous surface which suggests that it is worth any money, he made a formal proposal to be allowed to have the honour and pleasure of regarding her as a member of his family.", "제너럴 부인이 어떤 값을 치러도 아깝지 않을 만큼 광택이 도는 물품이었던 까닭에, 그는 그녀를 자기 가족의 일원으로 여기는 영광과 기쁨을 누리게 해 달라고 정식으로 청을 넣었다."], ["Mrs General conceded that high privilege, and here she was.", "제너럴 부인은 그 높은 특권을 허락했고, 그리하여 그녀가 여기 있게 된 것이다."]], [["In person, Mrs General, including her skirts which had much to do with it, was of a dignified and imposing appearance; ample, rustling, gravely voluminous; always upright behind the proprieties.", "생김새로 말하자면, 제너럴 부인은—거기에 큰 몫을 한 치맛자락까지 포함하여—위엄 있고 당당한 풍채를 지녔다. 풍성하고, 바스락거리며, 묵직하게 부피 있는 모습으로, 늘 예법 뒤편에서 꼿꼿이 서 있었다."], ["She might have been taken--had been taken--to the top of the Alps and the bottom of Herculaneum, without disarranging a fold in her dress, or displacing a pin.", "그녀는 알프스 꼭대기에든 헤르쿨라네움 바닥에든 데려가도—실제로 데려가진 적도 있었는데—옷의 주름 하나 흐트러지거나 핀 하나 어긋나지 않았을 것이다."], ["If her countenance and hair had rather a floury appearance, as though from living in some transcendently genteel Mill, it was rather because she was a chalky creation altogether, than because she mended her complexion with violet powder, or had turned grey.", "그녀의 얼굴과 머리카락이 마치 더없이 우아한 어느 방앗간에 사는 것처럼 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 듯 보였다면, 그것은 그녀가 보랏빛 분으로 안색을 손질했거나 머리가 세었기 때문이라기보다, 통째로 백악 같은 피조물이었기 때문이었다."], ["If her eyes had no expression, it was probably because they had nothing to express.", "그녀의 눈에 아무 표정이 없었다면, 그것은 아마 표정으로 드러낼 것이 아무것도 없었기 때문이리라."], ["If she had few wrinkles, it was because her mind had never traced its name or any other inscription on her face.", "그녀에게 주름이 거의 없었다면, 그것은 그녀의 정신이 자기 이름이든 다른 어떤 글귀든 그 얼굴에 새긴 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이었다."], ["A cool, waxy, blown-out woman, who had never lighted well.", "차갑고, 밀랍 같고, 부풀어 오른 여인, 한 번도 제대로 불이 켜진 적 없는 여인이었다."]], [["Mrs General had no opinions.", "제너럴 부인에게는 의견이라는 것이 없었다."], ["Her way of forming a mind was to prevent it from forming opinions.", "그녀가 정신을 형성하는 방식이란 그 정신이 의견을 형성하지 못하게 막는 것이었다."], ["She had a little circular set of mental grooves or rails on which she started little trains of other people’s opinions, which never overtook one another, and never got anywhere.", "그녀에게는 작은 원형의 정신적 홈, 곧 궤도가 한 벌 있어서, 그 위로 남들의 의견을 실은 작은 열차들을 출발시켰는데, 그 열차들은 결코 서로를 따라잡지도, 어디에도 다다르지도 않았다."], ["Even her propriety could not dispute that there was impropriety in the world; but Mrs General’s way of getting rid of it was to put it out of sight, and make believe that there was no such thing.", "그녀의 예법조차도 이 세상에 무례함이 존재한다는 것은 부정할 수 없었지만, 제너럴 부인이 그것을 처리하는 방식이란 그것을 눈에 보이지 않게 치워 버리고, 그런 것은 없는 척하는 것이었다."], ["This was another of her ways of forming a mind--to cram all articles of difficulty into cupboards, lock them up, and say they had no existence.", "이것이 그녀가 정신을 형성하는 또 다른 방식이었다—까다로운 물건이란 물건은 모조리 찬장에 쑤셔 넣고 자물쇠를 채운 뒤, 그런 것은 존재하지 않는다고 말하는 것."], ["It was the easiest way, and, beyond all comparison, the properest.", "그것이 가장 쉬운 방법이자, 비할 데 없이 가장 예법에 맞는 방법이었다."]], [["Mrs General was not to be told of anything shocking.", "제너럴 부인에게는 충격적인 일이라곤 일러 줄 수 없었다."], ["Accidents, miseries, and offences, were never to be mentioned before her.", "사고나 비참한 일, 잘못된 일은 그녀 앞에서 결코 입에 올려서는 안 되었다."], ["Passion was to go to sleep in the presence of Mrs General, and blood was to change to milk and water.", "격정은 제너럴 부인 앞에서는 잠들어야 했고, 피는 우유와 물로 변해야 했다."], ["The little that was left in the world, when all these deductions were made, it was Mrs General’s province to varnish.", "이 모든 것을 덜어 내고 세상에 남은 그 자그마한 것에 광택을 입히는 것이 제너럴 부인의 소임이었다."], ["In that formation process of hers, she dipped the smallest of brushes into the largest of pots, and varnished the surface of every object that came under consideration.", "그 형성 작업에서 그녀는 가장 작은 붓을 가장 큰 통에 담갔다가, 고려 대상이 되는 모든 사물의 표면에 광택을 칠했다."], ["The more cracked it was, the more Mrs General varnished it.", "금이 가 있을수록 제너럴 부인은 더욱 그것에 광택을 입혔다."]], [["There was varnish in Mrs General’s voice, varnish in Mrs General’s touch, an atmosphere of varnish round Mrs General’s figure.", "제너럴 부인의 목소리에는 광택이 있었고, 제너럴 부인의 손길에도 광택이 있었으며, 제너럴 부인의 자태 둘레에는 광택의 기운이 감돌았다."], ["Mrs General’s dreams ought to have been varnished--if she had any--lying asleep in the arms of the good Saint Bernard, with the feathery snow falling on his house-top.", "제너럴 부인의 꿈에도—만약 꿈이라는 것을 꾸었다면—광택이 입혀져 있었어야 마땅했으리라. 깃털 같은 눈이 지붕 위로 내리는 가운데, 선량한 성 베르나르의 품에 안겨 잠들어 있는 그 꿈에 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 3. On the Road", "# CHAPTER 3. On the Road"]], [["The bright morning sun dazzled the eyes, the snow had ceased, the mists had vanished, the mountain air was so clear and light that the new sensation of breathing it was like the having entered on a new existence.", "눈부신 아침 햇살이 두 눈을 어지럽혔고, 눈은 그쳤고, 안개는 사라졌으며, 산 공기가 어찌나 맑고 가벼운지 그것을 들이마시는 새삼스러운 감각이 마치 새로운 존재로 들어선 것 같았다."], ["To help the delusion, the solid ground itself seemed gone, and the mountain, a shining waste of immense white heaps and masses, to be a region of cloud floating between the blue sky above and the earth far below.", "그 착각을 거들기라도 하듯, 단단한 땅 자체가 사라진 듯했고, 산은—거대한 흰 무더기와 덩어리가 빛나는 황무지였는데—위로는 푸른 하늘, 저 아래로는 땅 사이를 떠도는 구름의 영역인 듯 보였다."]], [["Some dark specks in the snow, like knots upon a little thread, beginning at the convent door and winding away down the descent in broken lengths which were not yet pieced together, showed where the Brethren were at work in several places clearing the track.", "눈 위의 거뭇한 점들이, 작은 실에 맺힌 매듭처럼, 수도원 문에서 시작되어 내리막을 따라 끊긴 토막토막으로 굽이쳐 내려갔는데, 아직 이어지지 않은 그 자국들은 여러 곳에서 수도사 형제들이 길을 트는 작업을 하고 있음을 보여 주었다."], ["Already the snow had begun to be foot-thawed again about the door.", "벌써 문 주위의 눈은 다시 발에 밟혀 녹기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Mules were busily brought out, tied to the rings in the wall, and laden; strings of bells were buckled on, burdens were adjusted, the voices of drivers and riders sounded musically.", "노새들이 분주히 끌려 나와 벽의 고리에 매이고 짐을 실었다. 방울 줄이 채워지고, 짐이 가지런히 매여지고, 몰이꾼과 탄 사람들의 목소리가 음악처럼 울렸다."], ["Some of the earliest had even already resumed their journey; and, both on the level summit by the dark water near the convent, and on the downward way of yesterday’s ascent, little moving figures of men and mules, reduced to miniatures by the immensity around, went with a clear tinkling of bells and a pleasant harmony of tongues.", "가장 일찍 떠나는 이들 가운데 몇몇은 이미 여정을 다시 시작하기까지 했다. 수도원 근처 검은 물가의 평평한 정상에서도, 어제 올라온 내리막길에서도, 사람과 노새의 작게 움직이는 형상들이, 주위의 광대함에 눌려 축소된 채로, 방울 소리의 맑은 딸랑임과 즐거운 말소리의 화음을 내며 나아갔다."]], [["In the supper-room of last night, a new fire, piled upon the feathery ashes of the old one, shone upon a homely breakfast of loaves, butter, and milk.", "어젯밤의 그 저녁 식사 방에서는, 지난밤 불의 깃털 같은 잿더미 위에 새로 쌓아 올린 불이, 빵 덩이와 버터와 우유로 차린 소박한 아침상을 비추었다."], ["It also shone on the courier of the Dorrit family, making tea for his party from a supply he had brought up with him, together with several other small stores which were chiefly laid in for the use of the strong body of inconvenience.", "그 불은 또한 도릿 일가의 안내인을 비추었는데, 그는 자기가 함께 들고 올라온 비축품—편의를 위한 든든한 짐 속에 주로 챙겨 둔 여러 자잘한 물품과 더불어—으로 일행을 위해 차를 끓이고 있었다."], ["Mr Gowan and Blandois of Paris had already breakfasted, and were walking up and down by the lake, smoking their cigars.", "가우언 씨와 파리의 블랑두아는 이미 아침을 마치고, 호숫가를 오르내리며 시가를 피우고 있었다."]], [["‘Gowan, eh?’ muttered Tip, otherwise Edward Dorrit, Esquire, turning over the leaves of the book, when the courier had left them to breakfast.", "\"가우언이라, 응?\" 별칭이 팁이요 본명은 에드워드 도릿 도련님인 자가, 안내인이 그들에게 아침을 맡기고 자리를 뜬 뒤, 그 명부의 책장을 넘기며 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Then Gowan is the name of a puppy, that’s all I have got to say!", "\"그럼 가우언이라는 건 어느 풋내기의 이름이로군, 내가 할 말은 그게 다야!"], ["If it was worth my while, I’d pull his nose.", "그럴 만한 값어치가 있다면 코를 비틀어 줬을 텐데."], ["But it isn’t worth my while--fortunately for him.", "하지만 그럴 값어치가 없어—그자한테는 다행이지."], ["How’s his wife, Amy? I suppose you know.", "그자 아내는 좀 어떠냐, 에이미?"], ["You generally know things of that sort.’", "너는 알겠지. 그런 일은 대개 네가 아니까.\""]], [["‘She is better, Edward.", "\"좀 나아졌어, 에드워드."], ["But they are not going to-day.’", "하지만 오늘은 안 떠난대.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["They are not going to-day!", "오늘은 안 떠난다고! 그 친구한테도 다행이군.\""], ["Fortunately for that fellow too,’ said Tip, ‘or he and I might have come into collision.’", "팁이 말했다. \"안 그랬으면 그자랑 내가 부딪칠 뻔했으니까.\""]], [["‘It is thought better here that she should lie quiet to-day, and not be fatigued and shaken by the ride down until to-morrow.’", "\"오늘은 가만히 누워 있는 편이 낫겠다고 여기서들 그러더라. 내일까지는 내려가는 길의 흔들림과 피로를 겪지 않는 게 좋다고.\""]], [["‘With all my heart.", "\"그거야 좋고말고."], ["But you talk as if you had been nursing her.", "그런데 너 말하는 게 꼭 네가 그 여자를 간호하기라도 한 것 같구나."], ["You haven’t been relapsing into (Mrs General is not here) into old habits, have you, Amy?’", "너 (제너럴 부인은 여기 안 계시니 하는 말이다만) 옛 버릇으로 도로 빠져든 건 아니지, 에이미?\""]], [["He asked her the question with a sly glance of observation at Miss Fanny, and at his father too.", "그는 패니 양을 슬쩍, 그리고 아버지까지 곁눈질로 살피며 그 질문을 던졌다."]], [["‘I have only been in to ask her if I could do anything for her, Tip,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그저 들어가서 뭐 해 드릴 게 없냐고 물어봤을 뿐이야, 팁.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘You needn’t call me Tip, Amy child,’ returned that young gentleman with a frown; ‘because that’s an old habit, and one you may as well lay aside.’", "\"나를 팁이라고 부를 거 없다, 에이미야.\" 그 젊은 신사가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 대꾸했다. \"그게 옛날 버릇이니까, 너도 좀 버리는 게 좋아.\""]], [["‘I didn’t mean to say so, Edward dear.", "\"그럴 뜻은 아니었어, 에드워드 오빠."], ["I forgot.", "깜빡했어."], ["It was so natural once, that it seemed at the moment the right word.’", "한때는 너무 자연스러웠던 거라, 순간적으로 그게 맞는 말처럼 느껴졌나 봐.\""]], [["‘Oh yes!’ Miss Fanny struck in.", "\"오, 그래!\" 패니 양이 끼어들었다."], ["‘Natural, and right word, and once, and all the rest of it!", "\"자연스럽고, 맞는 말이고, 한때고, 그 밖에 다 그렇지!"], ["Nonsense, you little thing!", "헛소리 마, 이 조그만 것아!"], ["I know perfectly well why you have been taking such an interest in this Mrs Gowan.", "네가 왜 이 가우언 부인이라는 여자한테 그렇게 관심을 쏟는지 나는 아주 잘 알아."], ["You can’t blind _me_.’", "나는 못 속여.\""]], [["‘I will not try to, Fanny.", "\"속이려는 게 아니야, 패니."], ["Don’t be angry.’", "화내지 마.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["angry!’ returned that young lady with a flounce.", "화라니!\" 그 젊은 숙녀가 몸을 홱 흔들며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I have no patience’ (which indeed was the truth).", "\"참을성이 다 떨어졌어\"(이는 사실이었다)."]], [["‘Pray, Fanny,’ said Mr Dorrit, raising his eyebrows, ‘what do you mean?", "\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다."], ["Explain yourself.’", "\"대체 무슨 소리냐? 분명히 말해 봐라.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Never mind, Pa,’ replied Miss Fanny, ‘it’s no great matter.", "신경 쓰지 마세요, 아빠.\" 패니 양이 대답했다."], ["Amy will understand me.", "\"별일 아니에요."], ["She knew, or knew of, this Mrs Gowan before yesterday, and she may as well admit that she did.’", "에이미는 제 말을 알아들을 테니까요. 쟤는 어제 이전부터 이 가우언 부인을 알았거나, 적어도 들어서 알고 있었으니, 그랬다고 인정하는 게 좋을 거예요.\""]], [["‘My child,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning to his younger daughter, ‘has your sister--any--ha--authority for this curious statement?’", "\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 작은딸에게 돌아서며 말했다. \"네 언니의—이—허—이 묘한 주장에 무슨—근거라도 있느냐?\""]], [["‘However meek we are,’ Miss Fanny struck in before she could answer, ‘we don’t go creeping into people’s rooms on the tops of cold mountains, and sitting perishing in the frost with people, unless we know something about them beforehand.", "\"우리가 아무리 얌전해도,\" 에이미가 대답하기도 전에 패니 양이 끼어들었다."], ["It’s not very hard to divine whose friend Mrs Gowan is.’", "\"차가운 산꼭대기에서 남의 방으로 슬그머니 기어들어가, 미리 그 사람들에 대해 뭔가 알지 않고서야, 서리 속에 사람들과 함께 얼어 죽도록 앉아 있지는 않잖아요. 가우언 부인이 누구의 친구인지 짐작하기란 그리 어렵지 않죠.\""]], [["‘Whose friend?’ inquired her father.", "\"누구의 친구라는 거냐?\" 아버지가 물었다."]], [["‘Pa, I am sorry to say,’ returned Miss Fanny, who had by this time succeeded in goading herself into a state of much ill-usage and grievance, which she was often at great pains to do: ‘that I believe her to be a friend of that very objectionable and unpleasant person, who, with a total absence of all delicacy, which our experience might have led us to expect from him, insulted us and outraged our feelings in so public and wilful a manner on an occasion to which it is understood among us that we will not more pointedly allude.’", "\"아빠, 말씀드리기 유감스럽지만,\" 이때쯤 자기 스스로를 부추겨 몹시 학대받고 억울한 상태로 몰아넣는 데—그녀가 곧잘 큰 애를 들여 하던 일이었다—성공한 패니 양이 대답했다. \"저는 그 여자가 바로 그 아주 못마땅하고 불쾌한 인물의 친구라고 믿어요. 우리 경험으로 미루어 으레 예상할 수 있었던 모든 섬세함이 통째로 결여된 채, 우리끼리는 더는 콕 집어 언급하지 않기로 한 그 자리에서, 그토록 공공연하고 고의적인 방식으로 우리를 모욕하고 우리 감정을 짓밟은 그 사람 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Amy, my child,’ said Mr Dorrit, tempering a bland severity with a dignified affection, ‘is this the case?’", "\"에이미야,\" 도릿 씨가 점잖은 엄격함을 위엄 있는 애정으로 누그러뜨리며 말했다. \"그게 사실이냐?\""]], [["Little Dorrit mildly answered, yes it was.", "리틀 도릿은 온순하게, 네, 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Yes it is!’ cried Miss Fanny.", "\"그래, 그렇다잖아요!\""], ["‘Of course!", "패니 양이 외쳤다."], ["I said so!", "\"당연하죠!"], ["And now, Pa, I do declare once for all’--this young lady was in the habit of declaring the same thing once for all every day of her life, and even several times in a day--‘that this is shameful!", "제가 그랬잖아요! 그리고 이제, 아빠, 저는 단번에 못 박아 선언하겠어요\"—이 젊은 숙녀는 매일같이, 심지어 하루에도 몇 번씩 같은 것을 단번에 못 박아 선언하는 버릇이 있었다—\"이건 수치스러운 일이라고요!"], ["I do declare once for all that it ought to be put a stop to.", "저는 단번에 못 박아 선언해요, 이건 그만두게 해야 한다고요."], ["Is it not enough that we have gone through what is only known to ourselves, but are we to have it thrown in our faces, perseveringly and systematically, by the very person who should spare our feelings most?", "우리끼리만 아는 일들을 다 겪은 것으로도 모자라, 우리 감정을 가장 아껴 줘야 할 바로 그 사람한테서 끈질기고 조직적으로 그 일을 얼굴에 들이밀려야 하나요?"], ["Are we to be exposed to this unnatural conduct every moment of our lives?", "우리는 평생 매 순간 이 부자연스러운 행동에 노출되어야 하는 건가요?"], ["Are we never to be permitted to forget?", "우리는 영영 잊는 것조차 허락받지 못하는 건가요?"], ["I say again, it is absolutely infamous!’", "다시 말하지만, 이건 정말이지 파렴치한 일이에요!\""]], [["‘Well, Amy,’ observed her brother, shaking his head, ‘you know I stand by you whenever I can, and on most occasions. But I must say, that, upon my soul, I do consider it rather an unaccountable mode of showing your sisterly affection, that you should back up a man who treated me in the most ungentlemanly way in which one man can treat another. And who,’ he added convincingly, ‘must be a low-minded thief, you know, or he never could have conducted himself as he did.’", "\"그런데, 에이미,\" 그녀의 오빠가 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"내가 가능할 때면 언제나, 거의 모든 경우에 네 편을 들어 주는 건 너도 알지. 하지만 한마디 안 할 수 없구나. 정말이지, 한 사내가 다른 사내를 대할 수 있는 가장 신사답지 못한 방식으로 나를 대한 자를 네가 두둔하다니, 이건 자매로서의 애정을 보이는 방식치고는 꽤나 이해할 수 없는 거라고 나는 생각한다. 게다가 그자는,\" 그가 확신에 차 덧붙였다. \"비열한 좀도둑임에 틀림없어, 알겠니, 안 그러고서야 그런 식으로 행동했을 리가 없으니까.\""]], [["‘And see,’ said Miss Fanny, ‘see what is involved in this!", "\"그리고 보세요,\" 패니 양이 말했다."], ["Can we ever hope to be respected by our servants?", "\"여기에 뭐가 걸려 있는지 보시라고요! 우리가 하인들한테 존경받기를 바랄 수나 있겠어요?"], ["Never.", "절대로 못하죠."], ["Here are our two women, and Pa’s valet, and a footman, and a courier, and all sorts of dependents, and yet in the midst of these, we are to have one of ourselves rushing about with tumblers of cold water, like a menial!", "여기 우리 하녀 둘에, 아빠 시종에, 하인에, 안내인에, 온갖 딸린 사람들이 있는데, 그런 가운데서 우리 식구 중 하나가 하인처럼 찬물 컵을 들고 이리저리 뛰어다녀야 한다니! 아니, 길거리에서 거지가 발작을 일으키면 순경이,\""], ["Why, a policeman,’ said Miss Fanny, ‘if a beggar had a fit in the street, could but go plunging about with tumblers, as this very Amy did in this very room before our very eyes last night!’", "패니 양이 말했다. \"물컵을 들고 허둥지둥 뛰어다닐 수밖에 없겠지요, 어젯밤 바로 이 방에서 우리 눈앞에서 바로 이 에이미가 그랬던 것처럼요!\""]], [["‘I don’t so much mind that, once in a way,’ remarked Mr Edward; ‘but your Clennam, as he thinks proper to call himself, is another thing.’", "\"그건 어쩌다 한 번이라면 나도 그렇게 신경 쓰지 않아.\" 에드워드 도련님이 말했다. \"하지만 자기가 클레넘이라고 부르는 게 적절하다고 여기는 그 작자는 다른 문제지.\""]], [["‘He is part of the same thing,’ returned Miss Fanny, ‘and of a piece with all the rest.", "\"그자도 같은 일의 일부예요.\" 패니 양이 대꾸했다."], ["He obtruded himself upon us in the first instance.", "\"나머지 모든 것과 한통속이죠. 그자는 애당초 우리한테 제멋대로 끼어들었어요."], ["We never wanted him.", "우리는 그자를 전혀 원하지 않았고요."], ["I always showed him, for one, that I could have dispensed with his company with the greatest pleasure.", "적어도 저로 말하자면, 그자가 곁에 없어도 더없이 기꺼이 견딜 수 있다는 걸 늘 보여 줬어요. 그러더니 그자는 우리 감정을 그토록 심하게 짓밟는 짓을 저질렀죠."], ["He then commits that gross outrage upon our feelings, which he never could or would have committed but for the delight he took in exposing us; and then we are to be demeaned for the service of his friends!", "우리를 망신 주는 데서 느끼는 기쁨이 아니었다면 결코 저지를 수도, 저지르려고도 하지 않았을 짓을요. 그러고 나서 우리는 그자의 친구들 시중을 들도록 깎아내려져야 한다니요!"], ["Why, I don’t wonder at this Mr Gowan’s conduct towards you.", "아니, 저는 이 가우언 씨가 오빠한테 한 행동도 놀랍지 않아요."], ["What else was to be expected when he was enjoying our past misfortunes--gloating over them at the moment!’", "그자가 우리의 지난 불행을 즐기며—그 순간 그걸 고소해하고 있었는데—달리 뭘 기대할 수 있었겠어요!\""]], [["‘Father--Edward--no indeed!’ pleaded Little Dorrit.", "\"아버지—오빠—정말 아니에요!\" 리틀 도릿이 호소했다."], ["‘Neither Mr nor Mrs Gowan had ever heard our name.", "\"가우언 씨도 가우언 부인도 우리 이름을 들어 본 적이 없어요."], ["They were, and they are, quite ignorant of our history.’", "그분들은 우리 내력을 전혀, 지금도 전혀 모르세요.\""]], [["‘So much the worse,’ retorted Fanny, determined not to admit anything in extenuation, ‘for then you have no excuse.", "\"그러면 더 나쁘지.\" 정상참작이 될 만한 건 아무것도 인정하지 않기로 작정한 패니가 되받았다."], ["If they had known about us, you might have felt yourself called upon to conciliate them.", "\"그러면 너한테는 변명거리도 없는 거니까. 그분들이 우리에 대해 알았다면, 네가 그분들을 달래야 한다는 의무감을 느꼈을 수도 있겠지."], ["That would have been a weak and ridiculous mistake, but I can respect a mistake, whereas I can’t respect a wilful and deliberate abasing of those who should be nearest and dearest to us.", "그건 나약하고 우스꽝스러운 실수겠지만, 실수라면 나도 봐줄 수 있어. 하지만 우리한테 가장 가깝고 소중해야 할 사람들을 고의로 일부러 깎아내리는 건 봐줄 수 없어."], ["No.", "안 돼."], ["I can’t respect that.", "그건 봐줄 수 없어."], ["I can do nothing but denounce that.’", "나는 그걸 규탄할 수밖에 없어.\""]], [["‘I never offend you wilfully, Fanny,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘though you are so hard with me.’", "\"나는 너한테 일부러 마음 상하게 한 적 없어, 패니.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"네가 나한테 그렇게 모질게 굴어도 말이야.\""]], [["‘Then you should be more careful, Amy,’ returned her sister.", "\"그렇다면 좀 더 조심해야지, 에이미.\" 언니가 대꾸했다."], ["‘If you do such things by accident, you should be more careful.", "\"네가 그런 일을 어쩌다 저지른다면, 좀 더 조심해야 한다고."], ["If I happened to have been born in a peculiar place, and under peculiar circumstances that blunted my knowledge of propriety, I fancy I should think myself bound to consider at every step, “Am I going, ignorantly, to compromise any near and dear relations?”", "만약 내가 어쩌다 별난 곳에서, 예법에 대한 분별을 무디게 만드는 별난 처지에서 태어났다면, 나는 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 이렇게 생각하는 게 마땅하다고 여길 것 같아. '내가 무지해서, 가깝고 소중한 친척들을 곤란하게 만들고 있는 건 아닐까?'"], ["That is what I fancy _I_ should do, if it was _my_ case.’", "만약 내 경우라면 나는 그렇게 할 것 같다는 거야.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit now interposed, at once to stop these painful subjects by his authority, and to point their moral by his wisdom.", "이제 도릿 씨가, 자신의 권위로 이 고통스러운 화제를 끊는 동시에 자신의 지혜로 그 교훈을 짚어 주려고 끼어들었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said he to his younger daughter, ‘I beg you to--ha--to say no more.", "\"얘야,\" 그가 작은딸에게 말했다."], ["Your sister Fanny expresses herself strongly, but not without considerable reason.", "\"부탁이니—허—더는 말하지 말아라. 네 언니 패니가 격하게 말하기는 했지만, 상당한 근거가 없는 건 아니다."], ["You have now a--hum--a great position to support.", "너는 이제—흠—지켜야 할 큰 지위가 있다."], ["That great position is not occupied by yourself alone, but by--ha--by me, and--ha hum--by us. Us.", "그 큰 지위는 너 혼자 차지하는 게 아니라—허—나와, 그리고—허 흠—우리가 차지하고 있는 거야."], ["Now, it is incumbent upon all people in an exalted position, but it is particularly so on this family, for reasons which I--ha--will not dwell upon, to make themselves respected.", "우리 말이다. 자, 높은 지위에 있는 모든 사람에게 그것이 마땅한 일이지만, 특히 이 집안에는—내가—허—굳이 자세히 말하지 않을 이유들로 해서—존경받도록 처신하는 것이 마땅하다."], ["To be vigilant in making themselves respected.", "존경받도록 부단히 경계하는 것 말이다."], ["Dependants, to respect us, must be--ha--kept at a distance and--hum--kept down. Down.", "아랫사람들이 우리를 존경하게 하려면, 그들을—허—거리를 두고—흠—눌러 두어야 한다."], ["Therefore, your not exposing yourself to the remarks of our attendants by appearing to have at any time dispensed with their services and performed them for yourself, is--ha--highly important.’", "눌러서. 그러니, 네가 언제든 하인들의 시중을 마다하고 그 일을 스스로 한 것처럼 보여 그들의 입길에 오르내리지 않도록 하는 것이—허—대단히 중요하다.\""]], [["‘Why, who can doubt it?’ cried Miss Fanny.", "\"아니, 누가 그걸 의심하겠어요?\" 패니 양이 외쳤다."], ["‘It’s the essence of everything.’", "\"그게 모든 것의 핵심인걸요.\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned her father, grandiloquently, ‘give me leave, my dear.", "\"패니,\" 그녀의 아버지가 거창하게 대꾸했다."], ["We then come to--ha--to Mr Clennam.", "\"잠깐 양보해 다오, 얘야."], ["I am free to say that I do not, Amy, share your sister’s sentiments--that is to say altogether--hum-- altogether--in reference to Mr Clennam.", "그러면 우리는—허—클레넘 씨 문제로 넘어가게 되는구나. 솔직히 말해, 나는, 에이미야, 클레넘 씨에 관해서는 네 언니의 감정에—그러니까 전적으로—흠—전적으로 동의하지는 않는다."], ["I am content to regard that individual in the light of--ha--generally--a well-behaved person.", "나는 그 사람을—허—대체로—품행이 단정한 사람으로 보는 것으로 만족한다."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["A well-behaved person.", "품행이 단정한 사람으로."], ["Nor will I inquire whether Mr Clennam did, at any time, obtrude himself on--ha--my society.", "또한 나는 클레넘 씨가 언제든—허—내 교제에 제멋대로 끼어들었는지 어떤지도 따지지 않겠다."], ["He knew my society to be--hum--sought, and his plea might be that he regarded me in the light of a public character.", "그는 내 교제가—흠—사람들이 구하는 것임을 알고 있었으니, 자기는 나를 공적인 인물로 여겼다고 변명할 수도 있겠지. 하지만 내가—허—클레넘 씨를 약간 알게 된 데에는(아주 약간이었다만) 사정들이 따라붙어 있어서,\""], ["But there were circumstances attending my--ha--slight knowledge of Mr Clennam (it was very slight), which,’ here Mr Dorrit became extremely grave and impressive, ‘would render it highly indelicate in Mr Clennam to--ha--to seek to renew communication with me or with any member of my family under existing circumstances.", "여기서 도릿 씨는 지극히 진지하고 인상 깊은 태도가 되었다. \"그것은 현재의 사정 아래에서 클레넘 씨가—허—나와, 혹은 내 집안의 누구와도 교류를 다시 트려 한다면 대단히 무례한 일이 되게 만들 것이다."], ["If Mr Clennam has sufficient delicacy to perceive the impropriety of any such attempt, I am bound as a responsible gentleman to--ha--defer to that delicacy on his part.", "만약 클레넘 씨가 그런 시도가 부적절하다는 것을 알아챌 만큼 섬세함을 갖췄다면, 책임 있는 신사로서 나는—허—그의 그 섬세함에 따라 줄 의무가 있다."], ["If, on the other hand, Mr Clennam has not that delicacy, I cannot for a moment--ha--hold any correspondence with so--hum--coarse a mind.", "반대로 클레넘 씨가 그런 섬세함을 갖추지 못했다면, 나는 한순간도—허—그토록—흠—거친 정신과는 어떤 교류도 가질 수 없다."], ["In either case, it would appear that Mr Clennam is put altogether out of the question, and that we have nothing to do with him or he with us.", "어느 쪽이든, 클레넘 씨는 아예 논의 밖에 놓이는 것으로 보이며, 우리는 그와 아무 상관이 없고 그도 우리와 아무 상관이 없는 것이다."], ["Ha--Mrs General!’", "허—제너럴 부인!\""]], [["The entrance of the lady whom he announced, to take her place at the breakfast-table, terminated the discussion.", "그가 알린 그 부인이 아침 식탁의 자리를 차지하러 들어오면서 이 논의는 끝이 났다."], ["Shortly afterwards, the courier announced that the valet, and the footman, and the two maids, and the four guides, and the fourteen mules, were in readiness; so the breakfast party went out to the convent door to join the cavalcade.", "곧이어 안내인이 시종과 마부와 두 하녀와 네 안내꾼과 열네 마리의 노새가 채비되었다고 알렸다. 그리하여 아침을 먹던 일행은 행렬에 합류하러 수도원 문으로 나갔다."]], [["Mr Gowan stood aloof with his cigar and pencil, but Mr Blandois was on the spot to pay his respects to the ladies.", "가우언 씨는 시가와 연필을 들고 멀찍이 떨어져 있었으나, 블랑두아 씨는 그 자리에서 숙녀들에게 인사를 올렸다."], ["When he gallantly pulled off his slouched hat to Little Dorrit, she thought he had even a more sinister look, standing swart and cloaked in the snow, than he had in the fire-light over-night.", "그가 챙 늘어진 모자를 벗어 정중하게 리틀 도릿에게 인사할 때, 그녀는 눈 속에 거뭇하고 망토를 두른 채 서 있는 그가 지난밤 불빛 아래에서보다 한층 더 음험한 인상이라고 생각했다."], ["But, as both her father and her sister received his homage with some favour, she refrained from expressing any distrust of him, lest it should prove to be a new blemish derived from her prison birth.", "하지만 아버지도 언니도 그의 경의를 어느 정도 호의로 받아들이는지라, 그녀는 그에 대한 어떤 불신도 입에 올리지 않았다. 그것이 또다시 자신의 감옥 태생에서 비롯된 흠으로 드러날까 두려웠기 때문이다."]], [["Nevertheless, as they wound down the rugged way while the convent was yet in sight, she more than once looked round, and descried Mr Blandois, backed by the convent smoke which rose straight and high from the chimneys in a golden film, always standing on one jutting point looking down after them.", "그럼에도 험한 길을 굽이굽이 내려가는 동안, 수도원이 아직 보이는 사이에 그녀는 한 번 이상 뒤를 돌아보았고, 굴뚝에서 황금빛 막처럼 곧고 높게 피어오르는 수도원의 연기를 등진 채, 블랑두아 씨가 늘 어느 튀어나온 바위 끝에 서서 그들을 내려다보고 있는 것을 알아보았다."], ["Long after he was a mere black stick in the snow, she felt as though she could yet see that smile of his, that high nose, and those eyes that were too near it.", "그가 눈 속의 한낱 검은 막대기에 지나지 않게 된 뒤로도 한참 동안, 그녀는 여전히 그 미소를, 그 높은 코를, 그리고 코에 너무 가까이 붙은 그 두 눈을 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다."], ["And even after that, when the convent was gone and some light morning clouds veiled the pass below it, the ghastly skeleton arms by the wayside seemed to be all pointing up at him.", "그리고 그것마저 사라진 뒤, 수도원이 자취를 감추고 그 아래 고개를 가벼운 아침 구름이 가렸을 때에도, 길가의 섬뜩한 해골 같은 팔들이 모두 그를 가리키며 위를 향하고 있는 듯했다."]], [["More treacherous than snow, perhaps, colder at heart, and harder to melt, Blandois of Paris by degrees passed out of her mind, as they came down into the softer regions.", "어쩌면 눈보다 더 음흉하고, 가슴은 더 차갑고, 녹이기는 더 어려웠을 파리의 블랑두아는, 그들이 한결 부드러운 지역으로 내려옴에 따라 차츰 그녀의 마음에서 사라져 갔다."], ["Again the sun was warm, again the streams descending from glaciers and snowy caverns were refreshing to drink at, again they came among the pine-trees, the rocky rivulets, the verdant heights and dales, the wooden chalets and rough zigzag fences of Swiss country.", "다시 햇살은 따뜻했고, 빙하와 눈 동굴에서 흘러내리는 물줄기는 마시기에 상쾌했으며, 그들은 다시 소나무 숲과 바위 사이 시냇물과 푸른 언덕과 골짜기, 스위스 시골의 나무 산막과 지그재그로 거칠게 엮은 울타리 사이로 들어섰다."], ["Sometimes the way so widened that she and her father could ride abreast.", "때로 길이 충분히 넓어져 그녀와 아버지가 나란히 말을 탈 수 있었다."], ["And then to look at him, handsomely clothed in his fur and broadcloths, rich, free, numerously served and attended, his eyes roving far away among the glories of the landscape, no miserable screen before them to darken his sight and cast its shadow on him, was enough.", "그럴 때 그를 바라보는 것—모피와 고급 모직을 멋지게 차려입고, 부유하고, 자유롭고, 수많은 사람의 시중을 받으며, 그의 두 눈은 풍경의 영광 속을 멀리 떠돌고, 그 앞에는 시야를 어둡게 하고 그에게 그늘을 드리울 비참한 가림막 하나 없는 그 모습을 바라보는 것—만으로도 충분했다."]], [["Her uncle was so far rescued from that shadow of old, that he wore the clothes they gave him, and performed some ablutions as a sacrifice to the family credit, and went where he was taken, with a certain patient animal enjoyment, which seemed to express that the air and change did him good.", "그녀의 삼촌은 옛 그늘에서 어찌나 벗어났는지, 사람들이 주는 옷을 입었고, 집안의 체면을 위한 제물로 몸을 좀 씻기도 했으며, 데려가는 대로 따라갔는데, 어떤 참을성 있는 동물적 만족감을 보여, 마치 공기와 변화가 그에게 이롭다는 것을 드러내는 듯했다."], ["In all other respects, save one, he shone with no light but such as was reflected from his brother.", "그 한 가지를 빼면 다른 모든 면에서 그는 자기 형에게서 비친 빛 말고는 아무런 빛도 내지 않았다."], ["His brother’s greatness, wealth, freedom, and grandeur, pleased him without any reference to himself.", "형의 위대함, 부, 자유, 그리고 위풍당당함이 그 자신과는 아무 상관없이 그를 기쁘게 했다."], ["Silent and retiring, he had no use for speech when he could hear his brother speak; no desire to be waited on, so that the servants devoted themselves to his brother.", "말이 없고 물러나 있는 그는, 형이 말하는 것을 들을 수 있는 한 자기가 말할 필요가 없었고, 하인들이 형에게 헌신하는 한 자기가 시중받기를 바라지도 않았다."], ["The only noticeable change he originated in himself, was an alteration in his manner to his younger niece.", "그 자신에게서 비롯된 유일하게 눈에 띄는 변화는 작은 조카딸을 대하는 태도의 변화였다."], ["Every day it refined more and more into a marked respect, very rarely shown by age to youth, and still more rarely susceptible, one would have said, of the fitness with which he invested it.", "날마다 그것은 점점 더 두드러진 존경으로 다듬어졌는데, 그것은 노인이 젊은이에게 좀처럼 보이지 않는 것이었고, 또 그가 거기에 부여한 그 어울림을 그가 감당해 낼 수 있으리라고는 더더욱 보기 어려운 종류의 것이었다."], ["On those occasions when Miss Fanny did declare once for all, he would take the next opportunity of baring his grey head before his younger niece, and of helping her to alight, or handing her to the carriage, or showing her any other attention, with the profoundest deference.", "패니 양이 단번에 못 박아 선언하는 그런 경우가 있을 때면, 그는 다음 기회를 잡아 작은 조카딸 앞에서 자신의 백발 머리를 드러내거나, 그녀가 말에서 내리는 것을 거들거나, 마차에 오르도록 손을 내밀거나, 그 밖의 온갖 시중을 더없이 깊은 경의를 담아 들곤 했다."], ["Yet it never appeared misplaced or forced, being always heartily simple, spontaneous, and genuine.", "그러면서도 그것이 부적절하거나 억지스러워 보인 적은 결코 없었으니, 늘 진심으로 소박하고, 자연스럽고, 진실했기 때문이다."], ["Neither would he ever consent, even at his brother’s request, to be helped to any place before her, or to take precedence of her in anything.", "또한 그는 형의 청에도 결코 응하지 않았으니, 어느 자리에서든 그녀보다 먼저 안내받거나, 어떤 일에서든 그녀를 앞서려 하지 않았다."], ["So jealous was he of her being respected, that, on this very journey down from the Great Saint Bernard, he took sudden and violent umbrage at the footman’s being remiss to hold her stirrup, though standing near when she dismounted; and unspeakably astonished the whole retinue by charging at him on a hard-headed mule, riding him into a corner, and threatening to trample him to death.", "그녀가 존경받는 것에 어찌나 마음을 쓰는지, 바로 이번 생 베르나르 고개에서 내려오는 여정에서, 그녀가 말에서 내릴 때 마부가 가까이 서 있었으면서도 등자를 잡아 주는 데 소홀하자, 그는 별안간 격렬하게 화를 내며, 고집 센 노새를 타고 그에게 돌진하여 구석으로 몰아붙이고는 짓밟아 죽이겠다고 위협하여 수행원 전부를 이루 말할 수 없이 놀라게 했다."]], [["They were a goodly company, and the Innkeepers all but worshipped them.", "그들은 훌륭한 일행이었고, 여관 주인들은 거의 그들을 떠받들었다."], ["Wherever they went, their importance preceded them in the person of the courier riding before, to see that the rooms of state were ready.", "그들이 어디를 가든 그들의 위세는, 귀빈실이 준비되었는지 확인하러 앞서 말을 달리는 안내인의 모습으로 그들을 앞질러 갔다."], ["He was the herald of the family procession.", "그는 이 집안 행렬의 전령이었다."], ["The great travelling-carriage came next: containing, inside, Mr Dorrit, Miss Dorrit, Miss Amy Dorrit, and Mrs General; outside, some of the retainers, and (in fine weather) Edward Dorrit, Esquire, for whom the box was reserved.", "그다음으로 큰 여행용 마차가 왔는데, 안에는 도릿 씨, 도릿 양, 에이미 도릿 양, 그리고 제너럴 부인이 탔고, 바깥에는 하인 몇 명과 (날이 좋을 때면) 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 탔으니, 마부석이 그를 위해 비워져 있었다."], ["Then came the chariot containing Frederick Dorrit, Esquire, and an empty place occupied by Edward Dorrit, Esquire, in wet weather.", "그다음으로 사륜마차가 왔는데, 거기엔 프레더릭 도릿 도련님이 탔고, 비 오는 날엔 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 차지하는 빈자리가 하나 있었다."], ["Then came the fourgon with the rest of the retainers, the heavy baggage, and as much as it could carry of the mud and dust which the other vehicles left behind.", "그다음으로 짐마차가 왔는데, 거기엔 나머지 하인들과 무거운 짐, 그리고 앞서가는 다른 차량들이 남긴 진흙과 먼지를 실을 수 있는 만큼 잔뜩 실려 있었다."]], [["These equipages adorned the yard of the hotel at Martigny, on the return of the family from their mountain excursion.", "이 마차들이 마르티니의 호텔 마당을 장식하고 있었으니, 일가가 산행에서 돌아왔을 때였다."], ["Other vehicles were there, much company being on the road, from the patched Italian Vettura--like the body of a swing from an English fair put upon a wooden tray on wheels, and having another wooden tray without wheels put atop of it--to the trim English carriage.", "다른 차량들도 거기 있었는데, 길에는 많은 일행이 오가고 있었던 까닭이다. 기운 천을 댄 이탈리아 베투라—영국 장터의 그네 좌석을 바퀴 달린 나무 쟁반에 얹고, 그 위에 바퀴 없는 또 하나의 나무 쟁반을 올려놓은 듯한—에서부터 말끔한 영국 마차에 이르기까지."], ["But there was another adornment of the hotel which Mr Dorrit had not bargained for.", "그런데 도릿 씨가 예상하지 못한 또 하나의 장식이 호텔에 있었다."], ["Two strange travellers embellished one of his rooms.", "낯선 여행객 둘이 그의 방 가운데 하나를 차지하고 있었던 것이다."]], [["The Innkeeper, hat in hand in the yard, swore to the courier that he was blighted, that he was desolated, that he was profoundly afflicted, that he was the most miserable and unfortunate of beasts, that he had the head of a wooden pig.", "여관 주인은 마당에서 모자를 손에 든 채, 자기는 망했다고, 자기는 비탄에 빠졌다고, 자기는 깊이 괴롭다고, 자기는 짐승 중에서도 가장 비참하고 운 없는 놈이라고, 자기 머리는 나무로 만든 돼지 대가리라고 안내인에게 맹세했다."], ["He ought never to have made the concession, he said, but the very genteel lady had so passionately prayed him for the accommodation of that room to dine in, only for a little half-hour, that he had been vanquished.", "결코 그 양보를 하지 말았어야 했다고 그는 말했지만, 그 아주 점잖은 부인이 단지 작은 반 시간만 그 방에서 식사하게 해 달라고 어찌나 간곡히 빌었던지 자기가 그만 굴복하고 말았다는 것이었다."], ["The little half-hour was expired, the lady and gentleman were taking their little dessert and half-cup of coffee, the note was paid, the horses were ordered, they would depart immediately; but, owing to an unhappy destiny and the curse of Heaven, they were not yet gone.", "그 작은 반 시간은 이미 지났고, 부인과 신사는 가벼운 후식과 커피 반 잔을 들고 있으며, 셈은 치러졌고, 말도 대기시켰으니, 곧 떠날 것이라고 했다. 그러나 불행한 운명과 하늘의 저주 탓에 그들이 아직 떠나지 않았다는 것이었다."]], [["Nothing could exceed Mr Dorrit’s indignation, as he turned at the foot of the staircase on hearing these apologies.", "이 사과를 들으며 계단 발치에서 돌아선 도릿 씨의 분개를 능가할 것은 없었다."], ["He felt that the family dignity was struck at by an assassin’s hand.", "그는 집안의 위엄이 자객의 손에 찔렸다고 느꼈다."], ["He had a sense of his dignity, which was of the most exquisite nature.", "그에게는 더없이 예민한 종류의 위엄 감각이 있었다."], ["He could detect a design upon it when nobody else had any perception of the fact.", "그는 다른 누구도 그 사실을 알아채지 못할 때에도 그것을 노린 음모를 감지해 낼 수 있었다."], ["His life was made an agony by the number of fine scalpels that he felt to be incessantly engaged in dissecting his dignity.", "자기 위엄을 끊임없이 해부하는 데 매달려 있다고 느껴지는 수많은 정교한 메스 때문에 그의 삶은 하나의 고통이 되어 있었다."]], [["‘Is it possible, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, reddening excessively, ‘that you have--ha--had the audacity to place one of my rooms at the disposition of any other person?’", "\"이게 가능한 일이오, 선생,\" 도릿 씨가 지나치게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"당신이—허—내 방 가운데 하나를 다른 사람 마음대로 쓰게 할 만큼 뻔뻔했단 말이오?\""]], [["Thousands of pardons!", "천만번 용서를!"], ["It was the host’s profound misfortune to have been overcome by that too genteel lady.", "그 너무도 점잖은 부인에게 압도당한 것은 주인의 깊은 불운이었다."], ["He besought Monseigneur not to enrage himself.", "그는 나리께서 노여워하지 마시기를 간청했다."], ["He threw himself on Monseigneur for clemency.", "그는 나리의 자비에 몸을 던졌다."], ["If Monseigneur would have the distinguished goodness to occupy the other salon especially reserved for him, for but five minutes, all would go well.", "나리께서 특별히 그를 위해 마련해 둔 다른 응접실을 단 오 분만이라도 너그러이 쓰셔 주신다면 만사가 잘 풀릴 것이라고 했다."]], [["‘No, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"안 되오, 선생.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I will not occupy any salon.", "\"나는 어떤 응접실도 쓰지 않겠소."], ["I will leave your house without eating or drinking, or setting foot in it.", "나는 당신 집에서 먹지도 마시지도 않고, 발도 들이지 않고 떠나겠소."], ["How do you dare to act like this?", "어떻게 감히 이런 식으로 군단 말이오?"], ["Who am I that you--ha--separate me from other gentlemen?’", "당신이—허—나를 다른 신사들과 갈라놓다니, 내가 대체 누구란 말이오?\""]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["The host called all the universe to witness that Monseigneur was the most amiable of the whole body of nobility, the most important, the most estimable, the most honoured.", "주인은 나리가 귀족 전체 가운데 가장 상냥하고, 가장 중요하고, 가장 존경할 만하며, 가장 영예로운 분이심을 온 우주가 증언해 달라 외쳤다."], ["If he separated Monseigneur from others, it was only because he was more distinguished, more cherished, more generous, more renowned.", "그가 나리를 다른 이들과 갈라놓았다면, 그것은 오로지 나리가 더 뛰어나고, 더 소중하고, 더 너그럽고, 더 명망 높으시기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["‘Don’t tell me so, sir,’ returned Mr Dorrit, in a mighty heat.", "\"그런 소리 마시오, 선생.\" 도릿 씨가 엄청난 열을 내며 되받았다."], ["‘You have affronted me.", "\"당신은 나를 모욕했소."], ["You have heaped insults upon me.", "당신은 내게 모욕을 산더미처럼 쌓아 올렸소."], ["How dare you?", "어떻게 감히?"], ["Explain yourself.’", "분명히 말해 보시오.\""]], [["Ah, just Heaven, then, how could the host explain himself when he had nothing more to explain; when he had only to apologise, and confide himself to the so well-known magnanimity of Monseigneur!", "아, 그렇다면, 의로우신 하늘이여, 더는 설명할 것이 없을 때, 그저 사과하고 그 너무도 잘 알려진 나리의 아량에 자신을 맡길 수밖에 없을 때, 주인이 어찌 자신을 설명할 수 있단 말인가!"]], [["‘I tell you, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, panting with anger, ‘that you separate me--ha--from other gentlemen; that you make distinctions between me and other gentlemen of fortune and station.", "\"내 말하건대, 선생,\" 도릿 씨가 분노로 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["I demand of you, why?", "\"당신은 나를—허—다른 신사들과 갈라놓고 있소. 당신은 나와 다른 재산과 지위를 갖춘 신사들 사이에 차별을 두고 있단 말이오."], ["I wish to know on--ha--what authority, on whose authority.", "나는 당신에게 묻겠소, 왜요? 나는 알고 싶소—허—무슨 권한으로, 누구의 권한으로 그러는지."], ["Reply sir.", "대답하시오, 선생."], ["Explain.", "분명히 말하시오."], ["Answer why.’", "왜 그러는지 답하시오.\""]], [["Permit the landlord humbly to submit to Monsieur the Courier then, that Monseigneur, ordinarily so gracious, enraged himself without cause.", "그렇다면 주인이 안내인 나리께 황송하게 아뢰는 것을 허락해 주십사 했다. 평소 그토록 너그러우신 나리께서 까닭 없이 노여워하고 계신다고."], ["There was no why.", "까닭이란 없었다."], ["Monsieur the Courier would represent to Monseigneur, that he deceived himself in suspecting that there was any why, but the why his devoted servant had already had the honour to present to him.", "안내인 나리께서 나리께 말씀드려 주시기를, 무슨 까닭이 있다고 의심하시는 것은 스스로를 속이시는 일이며, 까닭이란 그의 충실한 종이 이미 영광스럽게 아뢴 것뿐이라고."], ["The very genteel lady--", "그 아주 점잖은 부인이—"]], [["‘Silence!’ cried Mr Dorrit.", "\"닥치시오!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Hold your tongue!", "\"입 다무시오!"], ["I will hear no more of the very genteel lady; I will hear no more of you.", "나는 그 아주 점잖은 부인 이야기를 더는 듣지 않겠소. 나는 당신 이야기를 더는 듣지 않겠소."], ["Look at this family--my family--a family more genteel than any lady.", "이 집안을 보시오—내 집안을—그 어떤 부인보다도 점잖은 집안이오."], ["You have treated this family with disrespect; you have been insolent to this family.", "당신은 이 집안을 무례하게 대했소. 당신은 이 집안에 오만하게 굴었소."], ["I’ll ruin you.", "내 당신을 파멸시키겠소."], ["Ha--send for the horses, pack the carriages, I’ll not set foot in this man’s house again!’", "허—말을 부르고, 마차에 짐을 실으시오. 나는 다시는 이자의 집에 발을 들이지 않겠소!\""]], [["No one had interfered in the dispute, which was beyond the French colloquial powers of Edward Dorrit, Esquire, and scarcely within the province of the ladies.", "이 논쟁에 아무도 끼어들지 않았으니, 그것은 에드워드 도릿 도련님의 프랑스어 회화 실력을 넘어서는 것이었고, 숙녀들의 영역에도 거의 들지 않는 것이었다."], ["Miss Fanny, however, now supported her father with great bitterness; declaring, in her native tongue, that it was quite clear there was something special in this man’s impertinence; and that she considered it important that he should be, by some means, forced to give up his authority for making distinctions between that family and other wealthy families.", "그러나 이제 패니 양이 몹시 신랄하게 아버지를 거들었다. 그녀는 모국어로, 이 사내의 무례함에는 분명 뭔가 특별한 것이 있으며, 이 집안과 다른 부유한 집안들 사이에 차별을 두는 그의 권한을 어떻게든 강제로 포기하게 만드는 것이 중요하다고 여긴다고 선언했다."], ["What the reasons of his presumption could be, she was at a loss to imagine; but reasons he must have, and they ought to be torn from him.", "그의 주제넘은 짓의 이유가 무엇일지 그녀는 도무지 짐작할 수 없었지만, 이유가 있을 것이 분명하니, 그것을 그에게서 뜯어내야 한다고 했다."]], [["All the guides, mule-drivers, and idlers in the yard, had made themselves parties to the angry conference, and were much impressed by the courier’s now bestirring himself to get the carriages out.", "마당의 안내꾼들과 노새 몰이꾼들과 빈둥거리던 자들 모두가 이 성난 논쟁의 당사자가 되었고, 이제 안내인이 마차를 끌어내려 부산을 떠는 데 큰 감명을 받았다."], ["With the aid of some dozen people to each wheel, this was done at a great cost of noise; and then the loading was proceeded with, pending the arrival of the horses from the post-house.", "바퀴마다 십여 명이 달라붙어 거든 끝에, 엄청난 소음을 대가로 그 일을 해냈다. 그러고는 역참에서 말이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안 짐 싣기가 진행되었다."]], [["But the very genteel lady’s English chariot being already horsed and at the inn-door, the landlord had slipped up-stairs to represent his hard case.", "그런데 그 아주 점잖은 부인의 영국 마차는 이미 말이 매여져 여관 문 앞에 와 있었으므로, 주인은 자신의 딱한 처지를 호소하러 슬며시 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["This was notified to the yard by his now coming down the staircase in attendance on the gentleman and the lady, and by his pointing out the offended majesty of Mr Dorrit to them with a significant motion of his hand.", "그가 신사와 부인을 모시고 계단을 내려오면서, 도릿 씨의 상한 위엄을 의미심장한 손짓으로 그들에게 가리켜 보임으로써 이 사실이 마당에 알려졌다."]], [["‘Beg your pardon,’ said the gentleman, detaching himself from the lady, and coming forward.", "\"실례합니다.\" 신사가 부인에게서 떨어져 앞으로 나서며 말했다."], ["‘I am a man of few words and a bad hand at an explanation--but lady here is extremely anxious that there should be no Row.", "\"저는 말수가 적고 설명에는 서툰 사람입니다만—여기 이 부인께서 소동이 일어나지 않기를 몹시 바라십니다."], ["Lady--a mother of mine, in point of fact--wishes me to say that she hopes no Row.’", "부인께서는—사실은 제 어머니이신데—소동이 없기를 바라신다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit, still panting under his injury, saluted the gentleman, and saluted the lady, in a distant, final, and invincible manner.", "도릿 씨는 여전히 자신의 상처에 헐떡이면서, 신사에게 인사하고 부인에게 인사했는데, 거리를 둔, 단호한, 굽힐 수 없는 태도였다."]], [["‘No, but really--here, old feller; you!’ This was the gentleman’s way of appealing to Edward Dorrit, Esquire, on whom he pounced as a great and providential relief.", "\"아니, 근데 정말로—이봐요, 영감님, 당신!\" 이것은 신사가 에드워드 도릿 도련님에게 호소하는 방식이었으니, 그는 그를 크고도 천우신조 같은 구원으로 여겨 덥석 붙들었다."], ["‘Let you and I try to make this all right.", "\"당신하고 나하고 이걸 다 잘 풀어 봅시다."], ["Lady so very much wishes no Row.’", "부인께서 소동이 없기를 정말 너무도 바라시니까.\""]], [["Edward Dorrit, Esquire, led a little apart by the button, assumed a diplomatic expression of countenance in replying, ‘Why you must confess, that when you bespeak a lot of rooms beforehand, and they belong to you, it’s not pleasant to find other people in ‘em.’", "에드워드 도릿 도련님은 단추를 잡혀 한쪽으로 약간 끌려가서는, 외교적인 표정을 지으며 대답했다. \"아니, 인정하셔야 할 게, 방을 여러 개 미리 예약해 두고 그게 자기 것인데 거기서 다른 사람들이 있는 걸 발견하면, 그게 유쾌한 일은 아니잖소.\""]], [["‘No,’ said the other, ‘I know it isn’t.", "\"그렇소.\""], ["I admit it.", "상대가 말했다."], ["Still, let you and I try to make it all right, and avoid Row.", "\"그게 유쾌하지 않다는 거 압니다. 인정해요."], ["The fault is not this chap’s at all, but my mother’s.", "그래도 당신하고 나하고 이걸 다 잘 풀어서 소동을 피해 봅시다."], ["Being a remarkably fine woman with no bigodd nonsense about her--well educated, too--she was too many for this chap.", "잘못은 이 친구한테 있는 게 전혀 아니라 우리 어머니한테 있어요. 워낙 빼어나게 훌륭한 분이라 잘난 척하는 허튼소리라곤 없으신 데다—교육도 잘 받으셔서—이 친구가 당해낼 수가 없었던 거죠."], ["Regularly pocketed him.’", "아주 손바닥 안에 넣고 주무르신 거예요.\""]], [["‘If that’s the case--’ Edward Dorrit, Esquire, began.", "\"그런 사정이라면—\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 입을 떼었다."]], [["‘Assure you ‘pon my soul ‘tis the case.", "\"내 영혼을 걸고 장담하건대 정말 그런 사정입니다. 따라서,\""], ["Consequently,’ said the other gentleman, retiring on his main position, ‘why Row?’", "상대 신사가 자신의 주된 입장으로 물러서며 말했다. \"왜 소동을 벌입니까?\""]], [["‘Edmund,’ said the lady from the doorway, ‘I hope you have explained, or are explaining, to the satisfaction of this gentleman and his family that the civil landlord is not to blame?’", "\"에드먼드,\" 부인이 문간에서 말했다. \"친절한 여관 주인에게는 잘못이 없다는 걸 이 신사와 그 가족분들이 납득하시도록 설명했거나 설명하고 있는 중이겠지?\""]], [["‘Assure you, ma’am,’ returned Edmund, ‘perfectly paralysing myself with trying it on.’ He then looked steadfastly at Edward Dorrit, Esquire, for some seconds, and suddenly added, in a burst of confidence, ‘Old feller! _Is_ it all right?’", "\"장담합니다, 어머니.\" 에드먼드가 대답했다. \"그걸 해 보느라 완전히 진이 다 빠져 버릴 지경입니다.\" 그러더니 그는 몇 초간 에드워드 도릿 도련님을 한결같이 바라보다가, 별안간 속내를 털어놓듯 덧붙였다. \"영감님! 이제 다 괜찮은 거 맞소?\""]], [["‘I don’t know, after all,’ said the lady, gracefully advancing a step or two towards Mr Dorrit, ‘but that I had better say myself, at once, that I assured this good man I took all the consequences on myself of occupying one of a stranger’s suite of rooms during his absence, for just as much (or as little) time as I could dine in. I had no idea the rightful owner would come back so soon, nor had I any idea that he had come back, or I should have hastened to make restoration of my ill-gotten chamber, and to have offered my explanation and apology. I trust in saying this--’", "\"그래도 결국,\" 부인이 우아하게 도릿 씨 쪽으로 한두 걸음 다가서며 말했다. \"제가 직접 곧바로 말씀드리는 게 낫겠군요. 이 선량한 분께, 낯선 분의 방 가운데 하나를 그분이 안 계신 동안 식사할 만큼만(혹은 그만큼이나 짧게) 차지하는 데 따르는 모든 책임을 제가 다 지겠다고 제가 장담했답니다. 정당한 주인께서 이렇게 빨리 돌아오시리라곤 전혀 생각지 못했고, 이미 돌아오셨다는 것도 전혀 알지 못했어요. 그렇지 않았다면 부당하게 차지한 이 방을 서둘러 돌려드리고, 제 사정을 설명드리며 사과드렸을 텐데요. 이렇게 말씀드리는 것으로 저는—\""]], [["For a moment the lady, with a glass at her eye, stood transfixed and speechless before the two Miss Dorrits.", "한순간 그 부인은, 한쪽 눈에 안경을 댄 채로, 두 도릿 양 앞에서 못 박힌 듯 말문이 막혀 섰다."], ["At the same moment, Miss Fanny, in the foreground of a grand pictorial composition, formed by the family, the family equipages, and the family servants, held her sister tight under one arm to detain her on the spot, and with the other arm fanned herself with a distinguished air, and negligently surveyed the lady from head to foot.", "같은 순간, 패니 양은, 일가와 일가의 마차들과 일가의 하인들이 이룬 웅장한 회화적 구도의 전경에서, 동생을 한쪽 팔로 꽉 붙들어 그 자리에 잡아 두고는, 다른 한 팔로는 기품 있는 태도로 부채질을 하면서 부인을 머리끝부터 발끝까지 무심하게 훑어보았다."]], [["The lady, recovering herself quickly--for it was Mrs Merdle and she was not easily dashed--went on to add that she trusted in saying this, she apologised for her boldness, and restored this well-behaved landlord to the favour that was so very valuable to him.", "부인은—그녀는 머들 부인이었고 쉽사리 기죽는 사람이 아니었으므로—재빨리 정신을 가다듬고는, 이렇게 말함으로써 자신의 무람없음을 사과하며 이 품행 단정한 여관 주인을, 그에게 그토록 소중한 그 호의에 되돌려 놓는 바라고 덧붙였다."], ["Mr Dorrit, on the altar of whose dignity all this was incense, made a gracious reply; and said that his people should--ha--countermand his horses, and he would--hum--overlook what he had at first supposed to be an affront, but now regarded as an honour.", "도릿 씨는, 그의 위엄의 제단에 이 모든 것이 향처럼 피워 올려진 셈인지라, 너그러이 대답하고는, 자기 사람들에게—허—말을 도로 물리라 이르겠으며, 자기는—흠—처음엔 모욕으로 여겼으나 이제는 영광으로 여기게 된 일을 눈감아 주겠노라고 말했다."], ["Upon this the bosom bent to him; and its owner, with a wonderful command of feature, addressed a winning smile of adieu to the two sisters, as young ladies of fortune in whose favour she was much prepossessed, and whom she had never had the gratification of seeing before.", "이에 그 가슴이 그를 향해 굽혔다. 그 가슴의 주인은 놀라우리만치 표정을 다스리며, 두 자매에게 마음을 끄는 작별의 미소를 보냈으니, 자신이 무척 호감을 품은, 그러나 전에는 만나는 기쁨을 한 번도 누리지 못한 재산 있는 젊은 숙녀들을 대하듯 한 것이었다."]], [["Not so, however, Mr Sparkler.", "그러나 스파클러 씨는 그렇지 않았다."], ["This gentleman, becoming transfixed at the same moment as his lady-mother, could not by any means unfix himself again, but stood stiffly staring at the whole composition with Miss Fanny in the Foreground.", "이 신사는 자기 모친과 같은 순간에 못 박혔다가, 도무지 다시 풀려나지를 못하고, 패니 양이 전경에 있는 그 구도 전체를 뻣뻣하게 응시하며 서 있었다."], ["On his mother saying, ‘Edmund, we are quite ready; will you give me your arm?’ he seemed, by the motion of his lips, to reply with some remark comprehending the form of words in which his shining talents found the most frequent utterance, but he relaxed no muscle.", "그의 어머니가 \"에드먼드, 우리는 다 채비됐다. 팔을 내주겠니?\"라고 말하자, 그는 입술을 움직여, 그의 빛나는 재능이 가장 자주 발화되곤 하던 그 말투의 형식을 담은 무언가로 대답하는 듯했지만, 근육 하나 풀지 않았다."], ["So fixed was his figure, that it would have been matter of some difficulty to bend him sufficiently to get him in the carriage-door, if he had not received the timely assistance of a maternal pull from within.", "그의 자세가 어찌나 고정되었던지, 안에서 모친이 알맞은 때에 당겨 주는 도움을 받지 못했더라면, 그를 마차 문에 들어갈 만큼 충분히 굽히는 것은 다소 어려운 일이었을 것이다."], ["He was no sooner within than the pad of the little window in the back of the chariot disappeared, and his eye usurped its place.", "그가 안에 들어서기가 무섭게 마차 뒤편 작은 창의 덧대개가 사라지고 그 자리를 그의 눈이 차지했다."], ["There it remained as long as so small an object was discernible, and probably much longer, staring (as though something inexpressibly surprising should happen to a codfish) like an ill-executed eye in a large locket.", "그 눈은 그토록 작은 물체가 분간될 수 있는 한 그곳에 머물렀으며, 아마 그보다 훨씬 더 오래, (마치 대구에게 뭔가 말할 수 없이 놀라운 일이 벌어지기라도 할 듯이) 커다란 로켓에 서툴게 그려 넣은 눈처럼 응시하고 있었다."]], [["This encounter was so highly agreeable to Miss Fanny, and gave her so much to think of with triumph afterwards, that it softened her asperities exceedingly.", "이 만남은 패니 양에게 지극히 흡족했고, 그 뒤로 두고두고 의기양양하게 곱씹을 거리를 어찌나 많이 안겨 주었는지, 그녀의 가시 돋친 성미를 무척이나 누그러뜨렸다."], ["When the procession was again in motion next day, she occupied her place in it with a new gaiety; and showed such a flow of spirits indeed, that Mrs General looked rather surprised.", "이튿날 행렬이 다시 움직이기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 새로운 명랑함으로 자기 자리를 차지했고, 실로 어찌나 흥이 넘쳐흐르는지 제너럴 부인이 다소 놀란 표정을 지을 정도였다."]], [["Little Dorrit was glad to be found no fault with, and to see that Fanny was pleased; but her part in the procession was a musing part, and a quiet one.", "리틀 도릿은 아무 흠도 잡히지 않은 것이, 그리고 패니가 기뻐하는 것을 보는 것이 다행스러웠다. 그러나 행렬 속에서 그녀의 몫은 골똘히 생각에 잠긴, 조용한 몫이었다."], ["Sitting opposite her father in the travelling-carriage, and recalling the old Marshalsea room, her present existence was a dream.", "여행용 마차에서 아버지와 마주 앉아 옛 마셜시의 방을 떠올리노라면, 지금의 그녀의 존재는 한낱 꿈이었다."], ["All that she saw was new and wonderful, but it was not real; it seemed to her as if those visions of mountains and picturesque countries might melt away at any moment, and the carriage, turning some abrupt corner, bring up with a jolt at the old Marshalsea gate.", "그녀가 보는 모든 것이 새롭고 경이로웠지만, 그것은 현실이 아니었다. 그녀에게는 마치 그 산들과 그림 같은 시골의 환영이 언제라도 녹아 사라지고, 마차가 어느 가파른 모퉁이를 돌다가 덜컹하며 옛 마셜시 정문 앞에 와 멈출 것만 같았다."]], [["To have no work to do was strange, but not half so strange as having glided into a corner where she had no one to think for, nothing to plan and contrive, no cares of others to load herself with.", "할 일이 없다는 것은 낯설었으나, 누구를 대신해 생각해 줄 사람도, 짜내고 궁리할 일도, 짊어질 남의 근심도 없는 구석으로 미끄러져 들어온 것에 비하면 그 절반도 낯설지 않았다."], ["Strange as that was, it was far stranger yet to find a space between herself and her father, where others occupied themselves in taking care of him, and where she was never expected to be.", "그것도 낯설었지만, 자기와 아버지 사이에 어떤 틈이 생겨, 거기서 다른 이들이 그를 보살피는 일에 매달리고, 자기는 결코 거기 있으리라 기대되지 않는다는 것을 깨닫는 것은 훨씬 더 낯설었다."], ["At first, this was so much more unlike her old experience than even the mountains themselves, that she had been unable to resign herself to it, and had tried to retain her old place about him.", "처음에 이것은 산들 그 자체보다도 더 그녀의 옛 경험과 동떨어진 일이어서, 그녀는 거기에 자신을 내맡기지 못하고 그의 곁의 옛 자리를 지키려 애썼다."], ["But he had spoken to her alone, and had said that people--ha--people in an exalted position, my dear, must scrupulously exact respect from their dependents; and that for her, his daughter, Miss Amy Dorrit, of the sole remaining branch of the Dorrits of Dorsetshire, to be known to--hum--to occupy herself in fulfilling the functions of--ha hum--a valet, would be incompatible with that respect.", "그러나 그가 그녀에게 따로 이야기를 하여, 사람들이란—허—높은 지위에 있는 사람들이란, 얘야, 아랫사람들에게서 엄격하게 존경을 받아내야 하며, 그의 딸이요 도싯셔 도릿가의 유일하게 남은 가문의 에이미 도릿 양인 그녀가—흠—시종의 직무를 수행하는 일에 몸을 쓴다고—허 흠—알려진다면 그 존경과 양립할 수 없으리라고 말했다."], ["Therefore, my dear, he--ha--he laid his parental injunctions upon her, to remember that she was a lady, who had now to conduct herself with--hum--a proper pride, and to preserve the rank of a lady; and consequently he requested her to abstain from doing what would occasion--ha--unpleasant and derogatory remarks.", "그러니, 얘야, 그는—허—그녀에게 부모로서 명하건대, 너는 이제—흠—온당한 자긍심을 지니고 처신해야 하고 숙녀의 신분을 지켜야 할 숙녀임을 기억하라고 했다. 따라서 그는 그녀에게—허—불쾌하고 격을 떨어뜨리는 입길을 초래할 만한 일은 삼가 달라고 당부했다."], ["She had obeyed without a murmur.", "그녀는 군말 없이 따랐다."], ["Thus it had been brought about that she now sat in her corner of the luxurious carriage with her little patient hands folded before her, quite displaced even from the last point of the old standing ground in life on which her feet had lingered.", "그리하여 이제 그녀가 그 호화로운 마차의 자기 구석에, 작고 참을성 있는 두 손을 앞에 모은 채, 그녀의 두 발이 머뭇거리고 있던 옛 삶의 마지막 발판마저 완전히 잃은 채 앉아 있게 된 것이었다."]], [["It was from this position that all she saw appeared unreal; the more surprising the scenes, the more they resembled the unreality of her own inner life as she went through its vacant places all day long.", "그녀가 보는 모든 것이 비현실적으로 보이는 것은 바로 이 처지에서였다. 풍경이 놀라우면 놀라울수록, 그것은 그녀가 하루 종일 텅 빈 자리를 지나며 겪는 자기 내면의 삶의 비현실성을 더욱 닮아 갔다."], ["The gorges of the Simplon, its enormous depths and thundering waterfalls, the wonderful road, the points of danger where a loose wheel or a faltering horse would have been destruction, the descent into Italy, the opening of that beautiful land as the rugged mountain-chasm widened and let them out from a gloomy and dark imprisonment--all a dream--only the old mean Marshalsea a reality.", "심플론의 협곡, 그 어마어마한 깊이와 천둥 치는 폭포들, 그 경이로운 길, 바퀴가 빠지거나 말이 비틀거리면 곧 파멸이었을 위험한 지점들, 이탈리아로의 하강, 험한 산의 틈이 넓어지며 음울하고 어두운 감금에서 그들을 풀어 놓을 때 그 아름다운 땅이 펼쳐지던 것—이 모두가 꿈이었고—오직 옛 초라한 마셜시만이 현실이었다."], ["Nay, even the old mean Marshalsea was shaken to its foundations when she pictured it without her father.", "아니, 그 옛 초라한 마셜시조차도 그녀가 아버지 없이 그곳을 그려 보면 그 토대까지 흔들렸다."], ["She could scarcely believe that the prisoners were still lingering in the close yard, that the mean rooms were still every one tenanted, and that the turnkey still stood in the Lodge letting people in and out, all just as she well knew it to be.", "죄수들이 여전히 그 비좁은 마당에서 어슬렁거리고, 그 초라한 방들이 여전히 하나하나 세 들어 있으며, 간수가 여전히 수위실에 서서 사람들을 들이고 내보내고 있다는 것을, 그 모든 것이 그녀가 잘 아는 그대로라는 것을, 그녀는 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었다."]], [["With a remembrance of her father’s old life in prison hanging about her like the burden of a sorrowful tune, Little Dorrit would wake from a dream of her birth-place into a whole day’s dream.", "감옥에서의 아버지의 옛 삶에 대한 기억이 마치 어느 슬픈 곡조의 후렴구처럼 그녀에게 매달려 있는 채로, 리틀 도릿은 자기 출생지의 꿈에서 깨어나 하루 종일 이어지는 또 하나의 꿈으로 들어가곤 했다."], ["The painted room in which she awoke, often a humbled state-chamber in a dilapidated palace, would begin it; with its wild red autumnal vine-leaves overhanging the glass, its orange-trees on the cracked white terrace outside the window, a group of monks and peasants in the little street below, misery and magnificence wrestling with each other upon every rood of ground in the prospect, no matter how widely diversified, and misery throwing magnificence with the strength of fate.", "그녀가 잠에서 깨어나는 채색된 방—흔히 황폐해진 궁전의 격이 떨어진 귀빈실이었다—이 그 하루의 꿈을 시작했다. 거친 붉은빛 가을 포도 덩굴 잎이 유리창 위로 드리워지고, 창밖 금 간 흰 테라스에는 오렌지나무들이 서 있고, 아래쪽 작은 거리에는 수도사와 농부 무리가 있고, 아무리 넓고 다채롭게 펼쳐진들 그 전망의 한 뼘 땅마다 비참함과 장려함이 서로 맞붙어 씨름하며, 비참함이 운명의 힘으로 장려함을 메다꽂는 그런 방이었다."], ["To this would succeed a labyrinth of bare passages and pillared galleries, with the family procession already preparing in the quadrangle below, through the carriages and luggage being brought together by the servants for the day’s journey.", "여기에 휑한 복도들과 기둥 늘어선 회랑들의 미로가 뒤따랐는데, 아래쪽 안뜰에서는 하인들이 그날의 여정을 위해 마차와 짐을 한데 모으는 가운데 일가의 행렬이 이미 채비되고 있었다."], ["Then breakfast in another painted chamber, damp-stained and of desolate proportions; and then the departure, which, to her timidity and sense of not being grand enough for her place in the ceremonies, was always an uneasy thing.", "그다음에는 또 하나의 채색된 방—습기로 얼룩지고 황량할 만큼 큰—에서의 아침 식사, 그다음에는 출발이었는데, 이는 그녀의 소심함과 그 의식에서 자기 자리를 차지하기엔 충분히 위엄 있지 못하다는 느낌으로 인해 늘 불편한 일이었다."], ["For then the courier (who himself would have been a foreign gentleman of high mark in the Marshalsea) would present himself to report that all was ready; and then her father’s valet would pompously induct him into his travelling-cloak; and then Fanny’s maid, and her own maid (who was a weight on Little Dorrit’s mind--absolutely made her cry at first, she knew so little what to do with her), would be in attendance; and then her brother’s man would complete his master’s equipment; and then her father would give his arm to Mrs General, and her uncle would give his to her, and, escorted by the landlord and Inn servants, they would swoop down-stairs.", "출발 때면 안내인이(그 자신은 마셜시에서라면 지체 높은 외국인 신사였을 것이다) 나서서 만사가 준비되었다고 보고하고, 그다음엔 아버지의 시종이 거드름을 피우며 그에게 여행용 망토를 둘러 입혔고, 그다음엔 패니의 하녀와 그녀 자신의 하녀가(이 하녀는 리틀 도릿의 마음에 부담이었으니—처음엔 그녀를 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 몰라 정말이지 울음이 나올 지경이었다) 시중을 들었고, 그다음엔 오빠의 하인이 주인의 차림새를 완성했고, 그다음엔 아버지가 제너럴 부인에게 팔을 내밀고 삼촌이 그녀에게 팔을 내밀어, 여관 주인과 여관 하인들의 호위를 받으며 그들은 계단을 휩쓸듯 내려가곤 했다."], ["There, a crowd would be collected to see them enter their carriages, which, amidst much bowing, and begging, and prancing, and lashing, and clattering, they would do; and so they would be driven madly through narrow unsavoury streets, and jerked out at the town gate.", "거기서는 군중이 모여들어 그들이 마차에 오르는 것을 구경했고, 그들은 숱한 절과 구걸과 날뛰는 말과 채찍질과 덜컹거림 한가운데서 마차에 올랐다. 그러고는 그들은 좁고 고약한 냄새가 나는 거리들을 미친 듯이 내달려, 성문 밖으로 덜컹 튕겨져 나가곤 했다."]], [["Among the day’s unrealities would be roads where the bright red vines were looped and garlanded together on trees for many miles; woods of olives; white villages and towns on hill-sides, lovely without, but frightful in their dirt and poverty within; crosses by the way; deep blue lakes with fairy islands, and clustering boats with awnings of bright colours and sails of beautiful forms; vast piles of building mouldering to dust; hanging-gardens where the weeds had grown so strong that their stems, like wedges driven home, had split the arch and rent the wall; stone-terraced lanes, with the lizards running into and out of every chink; beggars of all sorts everywhere: pitiful, picturesque, hungry, merry; children beggars and aged beggars.", "그날의 비현실적인 것들 가운데에는, 선명한 붉은 포도 덩굴이 여러 마일에 걸쳐 나무에 고리지고 화환처럼 엮인 길들이 있었고, 올리브 숲이 있었고, 산비탈의 흰 마을과 도시들이 있었으니—겉은 사랑스러웠으나 안의 더러움과 가난은 끔찍했다—길가의 십자가들이 있었고, 요정 같은 섬들이 떠 있고 화려한 색의 차양과 아름다운 모양의 돛을 단 배들이 무리 지은 짙푸른 호수들이 있었고, 먼지로 허물어져 가는 거대한 건물 더미들이 있었고, 잡초가 어찌나 억세게 자랐던지 그 줄기가 박아 넣은 쐐기처럼 아치를 쪼개고 벽을 갈라놓은 공중정원들이 있었고, 돌로 단을 쌓은 골목들에는 도마뱀이 온갖 틈으로 들고 났고, 도처에 온갖 부류의 거지들이 있었으니—가엾고, 그림 같고, 굶주리고, 명랑한, 어린 거지와 늙은 거지들이었다."], ["Often at posting-houses and other halting places, these miserable creatures would appear to her the only realities of the day; and many a time, when the money she had brought to give them was all given away, she would sit with her folded hands, thoughtfully looking after some diminutive girl leading her grey father, as if the sight reminded her of something in the days that were gone.", "역참이나 다른 멈추는 곳에서 이 비참한 존재들은 그녀에게 그날의 유일한 현실로 보이곤 했다. 그리고 여러 번, 그들에게 주려고 가져온 돈을 다 나눠 주고 나면, 그녀는 두 손을 모은 채 앉아, 백발의 아버지를 이끄는 어느 자그마한 소녀를 깊은 생각에 잠겨 바라보곤 했으니, 마치 그 광경이 지나간 날들의 무언가를 떠올리게 하기라도 하는 듯했다."]], [["Again, there would be places where they stayed the week together in splendid rooms, had banquets every day, rode out among heaps of wonders, walked through miles of palaces, and rested in dark corners of great churches; where there were winking lamps of gold and silver among pillars and arches, kneeling figures dotted about at confessionals and on the pavements; where there was the mist and scent of incense; where there were pictures, fantastic images, gaudy altars, great heights and distances, all softly lighted through stained glass, and the massive curtains that hung in the doorways.", "또한 그들이 화려한 방에서 한 주씩 함께 머무르며, 날마다 연회를 열고, 무수한 경이 사이로 말을 타고 나가고, 몇 마일에 걸친 궁전들을 거닐고, 큰 성당들의 어두운 구석에서 쉬는 곳들도 있었다. 거기서는 기둥과 아치 사이에서 금빛 은빛 등불들이 깜빡였고, 고해소와 포석 위 여기저기에 무릎 꿇은 형상들이 점점이 있었고, 향의 안개와 향내가 자욱했고, 그림들과 환상적인 형상들과 현란한 제단들과 거대한 높이와 거리들이 있었으니, 모두가 스테인드글라스와 문간에 드리운 묵직한 휘장을 통해 부드럽게 비추어졌다."], ["From these cities they would go on again, by the roads of vines and olives, through squalid villages, where there was not a hovel without a gap in its filthy walls, not a window with a whole inch of glass or paper; where there seemed to be nothing to support life, nothing to eat, nothing to make, nothing to grow, nothing to hope, nothing to do but die.", "이런 도시들에서 그들은 다시 포도와 올리브의 길들을 따라, 더러운 마을들을 지나 나아갔으니, 그곳에는 그 추한 벽에 구멍이 나지 않은 오두막이 하나도 없었고, 온전한 한 치의 유리나 종이가 붙은 창이 하나도 없었다. 거기에는 생명을 부지할 것이라곤 아무것도 없는 듯했고, 먹을 것도, 만들 것도, 기를 것도, 바랄 것도 없는 듯했으며, 죽는 것 말고는 할 일이 아무것도 없는 듯했다."]], [["Again they would come to whole towns of palaces, whose proper inmates were all banished, and which were all changed into barracks: troops of idle soldiers leaning out of the state windows, where their accoutrements hung drying on the marble architecture, and showing to the mind like hosts of rats who were (happily) eating away the props of the edifices that supported them, and must soon, with them, be smashed on the heads of the other swarms of soldiers and the swarms of priests, and the swarms of spies, who were all the ill-looking population left to be ruined, in the streets below.", "또한 그들은 궁전들로만 이루어진 도시 전체에 이르곤 했는데, 그 마땅한 거주자들은 모두 쫓겨났고, 모든 궁전이 병영으로 바뀌어 있었다. 빈둥거리는 병사 무리가 귀빈실 창밖으로 몸을 내밀고, 그들의 장구는 대리석 건축물에 걸려 마르고 있었으니, 그 광경은 마음에 이런 것을 떠올리게 했다—그들을 떠받치던 건물의 받침을 (다행스럽게도) 갉아먹고 있는 쥐 떼의 무리이며, 머잖아 그 건물과 더불어, 아래쪽 거리에 남겨져 파멸할 다른 병사 떼와 사제 떼와 첩자 떼—그 험상궂은 주민 전부—의 머리 위로 와르르 무너지고 말 것이라는 것을."]], [["Through such scenes, the family procession moved on to Venice.", "이런 풍경들을 지나 일가의 행렬은 베네치아로 나아갔다."], ["And here it dispersed for a time, as they were to live in Venice some few months in a palace (itself six times as big as the whole Marshalsea) on the Grand Canal.", "그리고 여기서 그것은 한동안 흩어졌으니, 그들이 대운하변의 한 궁전(그 자체가 마셜시 전체보다 여섯 배는 컸다)에서 몇 달간 베네치아에 살기로 했기 때문이다."]], [["In this crowning unreality, where all the streets were paved with water, and where the deathlike stillness of the days and nights was broken by no sound but the softened ringing of church-bells, the rippling of the current, and the cry of the gondoliers turning the corners of the flowing streets, Little Dorrit, quite lost by her task being done, sat down to muse.", "모든 거리가 물로 포장되어 있고, 낮과 밤의 죽음 같은 고요가 교회 종소리의 누그러진 울림과 물살의 찰랑거림과 흐르는 거리의 모퉁이를 도는 곤돌라 사공의 외침 말고는 아무 소리로도 깨어지지 않는 이 절정의 비현실 속에서, 리틀 도릿은 자기 할 일이 끝나 완전히 길을 잃은 채로 앉아 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The family began a gay life, went here and there, and turned night into day; but she was timid of joining in their gaieties, and only asked leave to be left alone.", "일가는 흥겨운 생활을 시작하여 이곳저곳을 다니고 밤을 낮으로 삼았으나, 그녀는 그들의 흥겨움에 끼는 것이 겁이 나서, 그저 혼자 있게 해 달라고 청할 뿐이었다."]], [["Sometimes she would step into one of the gondolas that were always kept in waiting, moored to painted posts at the door--when she could escape from the attendance of that oppressive maid, who was her mistress, and a very hard one--and would be taken all over the strange city.", "이따금 그녀는, 자신의 안주인이자 몹시 모진 그 짓누르는 하녀의 시중에서 벗어날 수 있을 때면, 문 앞 채색된 기둥에 매여 늘 대기하고 있는 곤돌라 가운데 하나에 올라, 그 낯선 도시를 두루 다니곤 했다."], ["Social people in other gondolas began to ask each other who the little solitary girl was whom they passed, sitting in her boat with folded hands, looking so pensively and wonderingly about her.", "다른 곤돌라에 탄 사교적인 사람들은 자기들이 스쳐 지나가는, 두 손을 모은 채 배에 앉아 그토록 시름에 잠겨 의아한 듯 주위를 둘러보는 그 외로운 작은 소녀가 누구인지 서로 묻기 시작했다."], ["Never thinking that it would be worth anybody’s while to notice her or her doings, Little Dorrit, in her quiet, scared, lost manner, went about the city none the less.", "자기나 자기 행동을 누가 눈여겨볼 만한 가치가 있으리라곤 결코 생각지 않은 채, 리틀 도릿은 그래도 여전히, 그 조용하고 겁먹은, 길 잃은 태도로 도시를 돌아다녔다."]], [["But her favourite station was the balcony of her own room, overhanging the canal, with other balconies below, and none above.", "그러나 그녀가 가장 좋아하는 자리는 운하 위로 돌출된 자기 방의 발코니였으니, 아래로는 다른 발코니들이 있었으나 위로는 하나도 없었다."], ["It was of massive stone darkened by ages, built in a wild fancy which came from the East to that collection of wild fancies; and Little Dorrit was little indeed, leaning on the broad-cushioned ledge, and looking over.", "그것은 세월에 거뭇해진 묵직한 돌로 되어 있었고, 동방에서 비롯되어 그 무수한 기이한 발상들의 집합소로 흘러든 어떤 분방한 발상으로 지어진 것이었다. 그리고 넓은 쿠션을 댄 난간에 기대어 밖을 내다보는 리틀 도릿은 실로 작디작았다."], ["As she liked no place of an evening half so well, she soon began to be watched for, and many eyes in passing gondolas were raised, and many people said, There was the little figure of the English girl who was always alone.", "저녁이면 그녀가 그곳의 절반만큼이라도 좋아하는 자리가 달리 없었으므로, 곧 사람들이 그녀를 눈여겨 찾기 시작했고, 지나가는 곤돌라의 수많은 눈이 위를 올려다보았으며, 많은 사람이 저기 늘 혼자인 영국 소녀의 작은 모습이 있다고 말했다."]], [["Such people were not realities to the little figure of the English girl; such people were all unknown to her.", "그런 사람들은 그 영국 소녀의 작은 모습에게는 현실이 아니었다. 그런 사람들은 모두 그녀에게는 낯선 이들이었다."], ["She would watch the sunset, in its long low lines of purple and red, and its burning flush high up into the sky: so glowing on the buildings, and so lightening their structure, that it made them look as if their strong walls were transparent, and they shone from within.", "그녀는 해 지는 것을 바라보곤 했으니, 길고 나직한 자줏빛과 붉은빛의 띠를 이루며, 그 타오르는 홍조가 하늘 높이 솟구쳐, 건물들 위로 어찌나 이글거리며 그 구조를 환히 밝히는지, 마치 그 단단한 벽이 투명해져 안에서부터 빛나는 듯 보일 정도였다."], ["She would watch those glories expire; and then, after looking at the black gondolas underneath, taking guests to music and dancing, would raise her eyes to the shining stars.", "그녀는 그 영광들이 스러지는 것을 바라보곤 했고, 그러고는 아래쪽에서 손님들을 음악과 춤으로 실어 나르는 검은 곤돌라들을 바라본 뒤, 빛나는 별들로 눈을 들어 올리곤 했다."], ["Was there no party of her own, in other times, on which the stars had shone?", "옛날 어느 때엔가, 별들이 비추어 주던 그녀만의 어떤 모임도 있지 않았던가?"], ["To think of that old gate now!", "이제 그 옛 정문을 떠올리다니!"]], [["She would think of that old gate, and of herself sitting at it in the dead of the night, pillowing Maggy’s head; and of other places and of other scenes associated with those different times.", "그녀는 그 옛 정문을 떠올리고, 한밤중에 그 문가에 앉아 매기의 머리를 무릎에 누이던 자신을 떠올리고, 그 다른 시절들과 결부된 다른 장소들과 다른 광경들을 떠올리곤 했다."], ["And then she would lean upon her balcony, and look over at the water, as though they all lay underneath it.", "그러고는 발코니에 기대어, 마치 그 모든 것이 물 아래에 잠겨 있기라도 한 듯, 물 위를 굽어보곤 했다."], ["When she got to that, she would musingly watch its running, as if, in the general vision, it might run dry, and show her the prison again, and herself, and the old room, and the old inmates, and the old visitors: all lasting realities that had never changed.", "거기에 이르면 그녀는 물이 흐르는 것을, 그 전체의 환영 속에서 그것이 말라 버려 다시금 감옥을, 그리고 자신을, 그 옛 방을, 그 옛 거주자들을, 그 옛 방문객들을—한 번도 변한 적 없는 그 영속하는 현실들 모두를—그녀에게 드러내 보여 주기라도 할 듯, 생각에 잠겨 지켜보곤 했다."]], [["Dear Mr Clennam,", "친애하는 클레넘 씨,"]], [["I write to you from my own room at Venice, thinking you will be glad to hear from me.", "저는 지금 베네치아에 있는 제 방에서 당신께 편지를 씁니다. 제 소식을 들으시면 기뻐하시리라 생각하기 때문이에요."], ["But I know you cannot be so glad to hear from me as I am to write to you; for everything about you is as you have been accustomed to see it, and you miss nothing--unless it should be me, which can only be for a very little while together and very seldom--while everything in my life is so strange, and I miss so much.", "하지만 당신께서 제 소식을 들으시는 기쁨이, 제가 당신께 편지를 쓰는 기쁨만큼 클 수는 없으리란 걸 저는 압니다. 당신을 둘러싼 모든 것은 당신께서 늘 보아 오시던 그대로이고, 당신께선 무엇 하나 아쉬울 게 없으시니까요—혹 저를 그리워하신다면 모르겠지만, 그것도 아주 잠깐, 아주 드물게나 그러시겠지요—그런데 제 삶의 모든 것은 어찌나 낯선지, 저는 아쉬운 것이 너무도 많답니다."]], [["When we were in Switzerland, which appears to have been years ago, though it was only weeks, I met young Mrs Gowan, who was on a mountain excursion like ourselves.", "스위스에 있을 때—몇 주 전 일인데도 마치 몇 해 전 일처럼 느껴져요—저는 젊은 가우언 부인을 만났어요. 우리처럼 산으로 소풍을 나오셨더군요."], ["She told me she was very well and very happy.", "부인은 자기가 아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복하다고 하셨어요."], ["She sent you the message, by me, that she thanked you affectionately and would never forget you.", "저를 통해 당신께 전갈을 보내셨는데, 당신께 다정하게 고마워하며 결코 잊지 않으시겠다고 하셨어요."], ["She was quite confiding with me, and I loved her almost as soon as I spoke to her.", "부인은 제게 무척이나 마음을 터놓으셨고, 저도 말을 나누자마자 거의 그 즉시 그분을 사랑하게 되었지요."], ["But there is nothing singular in that; who could help loving so beautiful and winning a creature!", "하지만 거기에 이상할 것은 하나도 없어요. 그토록 아름답고 사람을 사로잡는 분을 누군들 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어요!"], ["I could not wonder at any one loving her.", "저는 누구라도 그분을 사랑하는 것을 조금도 이상히 여길 수 없어요."], ["No indeed.", "정말이지 그렇답니다."]], [["It will not make you uneasy on Mrs Gowan’s account, I hope--for I remember that you said you had the interest of a true friend in her--if I tell you that I wish she could have married some one better suited to her.", "가우언 부인에 관해 이런 말씀을 드린다고 마음 불편해하지는 않으시길 바라요—당신께서 그분에게 진실한 벗으로서 관심을 두고 계신다고 말씀하셨던 것을 기억하니까요—그분이 좀 더 잘 어울리는 분과 결혼하셨더라면 좋았을 텐데 하는 생각이 들었다는 말씀을 드려도요."], ["Mr Gowan seems fond of her, and of course she is very fond of him, but I thought he was not earnest enough--I don’t mean in that respect--I mean in anything.", "가우언 씨는 부인을 아끼는 것 같고, 물론 부인도 그분을 무척 아끼시지만, 제가 보기엔 그분이 그다지 진지하지 못한 듯했어요—그 점을 두고 하는 말은 아니에요—무슨 일에서든 그렇다는 말이에요."], ["I could not keep it out of my mind that if I was Mrs Gowan (what a change that would be, and how I must alter to become like her!) I should feel that I was rather lonely and lost, for the want of some one who was steadfast and firm in purpose.", "만약 제가 가우언 부인이라면(그게 얼마나 큰 변화일지, 또 그분처럼 되려면 제가 얼마나 달라져야 할지요!) 한결같고 목표가 굳건한 누군가가 곁에 없어서 어쩐지 외롭고 길을 잃은 듯한 기분이 들 거라는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없었어요. 심지어 저는 그분도 그런 부족함을, 거의 자기도 모르는 사이에 조금은 느끼고 계신다는 생각마저 들었답니다."], ["I even thought she felt this want a little, almost without knowing it.", "하지만 이 일로 마음 불편해하지는 마세요. 그분은 '아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복'하시니까요."], ["But mind you are not made uneasy by this, for she was ‘very well and very happy.’ And she looked most beautiful.", "그리고 더없이 아름다워 보이셨어요."]], [["I expect to meet her again before long, and indeed have been expecting for some days past to see her here.", "머지않아 그분을 다시 만나게 되리라 기대하고 있고, 사실 며칠 전부터 이곳에서 그분을 뵙기를 기다리고 있어요."], ["I will ever be as good a friend to her as I can for your sake.", "당신을 위해서라도 저는 언제까지나 그분에게 할 수 있는 한 좋은 벗이 되어 드릴 거예요."], ["Dear Mr Clennam, I dare say you think little of having been a friend to me when I had no other (not that I have any other now, for I have made no new friends), but I think much of it, and I never can forget it.", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, 제가 다른 벗이 하나도 없을 때(지금도 다른 벗은 없어요, 새로운 벗을 사귀지 않았으니까요) 당신께서 제 벗이 되어 주셨던 일을 당신께선 대수롭지 않게 여기실지도 모르지만, 저는 그 일을 무척 소중히 여기고, 결코 잊을 수가 없답니다."]], [["I wish I knew--but it is best for no one to write to me--how Mr and Mrs Plornish prosper in the business which my dear father bought for them, and that old Mr Nandy lives happily with them and his two grandchildren, and sings all his songs over and over again.", "저는 알고 싶어요—그래도 아무도 제게 편지를 쓰지 않는 편이 가장 좋겠지요—제 사랑하는 아버지께서 그분들을 위해 사 주신 그 가게에서 플로니시 씨 부부가 잘 꾸려 나가고 계신지, 그리고 늙은 낸디 씨가 그분들과 두 손주와 함께 행복하게 지내며 자기 노래들을 몇 번이고 거듭 부르고 계신지를요."], ["I cannot quite keep back the tears from my eyes when I think of my poor Maggy, and of the blank she must have felt at first, however kind they all are to her, without her Little Mother.", "가엾은 우리 매기를 생각하면, 또 모두가 아무리 그 애에게 잘해 준다 해도 처음에 자기 작은 엄마 없이 그 애가 느꼈을 허전함을 생각하면, 저는 눈물이 솟는 것을 도무지 참을 수가 없어요."], ["Will you go and tell her, as a strict secret, with my love, that she never can have regretted our separation more than I have regretted it?", "그 애에게 가셔서, 제 사랑을 전하며, 엄히 비밀로 하시고서, 우리가 헤어진 것을 그 애가 아쉬워한 것 못지않게 저도 아쉬워했노라고 전해 주시겠어요?"], ["And will you tell them all that I have thought of them every day, and that my heart is faithful to them everywhere?", "그리고 그분들 모두에게, 제가 날마다 그분들을 생각했고, 어디에 있든 제 마음은 그분들에게 변함없다고 전해 주시겠어요?"], ["O, if you could know how faithful, you would almost pity me for being so far away and being so grand!", "아, 그 마음이 얼마나 한결같은지 당신께서 아신다면, 제가 이토록 멀리 떨어져, 이토록 거창하게 지내는 것을 거의 가엾게 여기실 거예요!"]], [["You will be glad, I am sure, to know that my dear father is very well in health, and that all these changes are highly beneficial to him, and that he is very different indeed from what he used to be when you used to see him.", "분명 기뻐하실 거예요, 제 사랑하는 아버지께서 건강이 아주 좋으시고, 이 모든 변화가 그분께 더없이 이로우며, 당신께서 뵙곤 하시던 예전 모습과는 정말이지 아주 달라지셨다는 소식을 들으시면요. 큰아버지께도 나아진 점이 있는 것 같아요."], ["There is an improvement in my uncle too, I think, though he never complained of old, and never exults now.", "비록 그분은 예전에도 결코 불평하지 않으셨고, 지금도 결코 우쭐대지 않으시지만요."], ["Fanny is very graceful, quick, and clever.", "패니는 무척 우아하고, 재빠르고, 영리해요."], ["It is natural to her to be a lady; she has adapted herself to our new fortunes with wonderful ease.", "그 애에게 귀부인이 되는 것은 자연스러운 일이고, 우리의 새로운 형편에 놀라우리만치 수월하게 적응했답니다."]], [["This reminds me that I have not been able to do so, and that I sometimes almost despair of ever being able to do so.", "이 말을 하다 보니, 저는 그렇게 하지 못했고, 때로는 영영 그렇게 하지 못할 것 같아 거의 절망스러워질 때가 있다는 게 떠올라요."], ["I find that I cannot learn.", "저는 도무지 배울 수가 없는 것 같아요."], ["Mrs General is always with us, and we speak French and speak Italian, and she takes pains to form us in many ways.", "제너럴 부인이 늘 우리와 함께 계시고, 우리는 프랑스어로 말하고 이탈리아어로 말하며, 그분은 여러 모로 우리를 다듬으시려 애쓰세요."], ["When I say we speak French and Italian, I mean they do.", "우리가 프랑스어와 이탈리아어로 말한다고 했지만, 사실은 그들이 그런다는 뜻이에요."], ["As for me, I am so slow that I scarcely get on at all.", "저로 말하자면, 어찌나 더딘지 거의 나아가질 못한답니다."], ["As soon as I begin to plan, and think, and try, all my planning, thinking, and trying go in old directions, and I begin to feel careful again about the expenses of the day, and about my dear father, and about my work, and then I remember with a start that there are no such cares left, and that in itself is so new and improbable that it sets me wandering again.", "제가 계획하고, 생각하고, 애써 보려 하자마자, 제 모든 계획과 생각과 애씀이 옛날 방향으로 흘러가 버려서, 다시 그날그날의 비용이며, 사랑하는 아버지며, 제 일감에 대해 걱정하기 시작하다가, 문득 화들짝 놀라 이제 그런 걱정거리는 하나도 남지 않았다는 걸 떠올려요. 그런데 그 사실 자체가 어찌나 새롭고 믿기지 않는 일인지, 저는 또 정신이 헤매기 시작한답니다."], ["I should not have the courage to mention this to any one but you.", "당신이 아닌 다른 누구에게라면 이런 말을 꺼낼 용기조차 없었을 거예요."]], [["It is the same with all these new countries and wonderful sights.", "이 모든 새로운 나라들과 놀라운 풍경들에 대해서도 마찬가지예요."], ["They are very beautiful, and they astonish me, but I am not collected enough--not familiar enough with myself, if you can quite understand what I mean--to have all the pleasure in them that I might have.", "그것들은 무척 아름답고 저를 놀라게 하지만, 저는 충분히 차분하지 못해서—제 말뜻을 온전히 이해하신다면, 제 자신과 충분히 친숙하지 못해서—그것들에서 누릴 수 있었을 즐거움을 다 누리지 못해요."], ["What I knew before them, blends with them, too, so curiously.", "게다가 그것들 이전에 제가 알던 것들이 어찌나 묘하게 그것들과 뒤섞이는지요."], ["For instance, when we were among the mountains, I often felt (I hesitate to tell such an idle thing, dear Mr Clennam, even to you) as if the Marshalsea must be behind that great rock; or as if Mrs Clennam’s room where I have worked so many days, and where I first saw you, must be just beyond that snow.", "이를테면 산속에 있을 때, 저는 종종(이런 부질없는 것을 말씀드리기가 망설여져요, 친애하는 클레넘 씨, 당신께라도요) 저 거대한 바위 뒤에 마셜시가 있을 것만 같은, 혹은 제가 그토록 여러 날 일했고 당신을 처음 뵈었던 클레넘 부인의 방이 저 눈 너머에 있을 것만 같은 기분이 들었어요."], ["Do you remember one night when I came with Maggy to your lodging in Covent Garden?", "제가 매기와 함께 코번트 가든의 당신 숙소로 찾아갔던 그 밤을 기억하세요?"], ["That room I have often and often fancied I have seen before me, travelling along for miles by the side of our carriage, when I have looked out of the carriage-window after dark.", "그 방을, 날이 저문 뒤 마차 창밖을 내다볼 때면, 우리 마차 곁을 따라 몇 마일이고 함께 달려오는 모습으로 제가 자주, 또 자주 떠올렸답니다."], ["We were shut out that night, and sat at the iron gate, and walked about till morning.", "그날 밤 우리는 문이 닫혀 들어가지 못하고, 철문 앞에 앉아 있다가 아침까지 이리저리 거닐었지요."], ["I often look up at the stars, even from the balcony of this room, and believe that I am in the street again, shut out with Maggy.", "저는 이 방의 발코니에서조차 종종 별들을 올려다보며, 제가 다시 그 거리에 매기와 함께 갇혀 있다고 믿는답니다."], ["It is the same with people that I left in England.", "영국에 두고 온 사람들에 대해서도 마찬가지예요."]], [["When I go about here in a gondola, I surprise myself looking into other gondolas as if I hoped to see them.", "이곳에서 곤돌라를 타고 다닐 때면, 저는 마치 그들을 보기를 바라기라도 하듯 다른 곤돌라들을 들여다보고 있는 제 모습에 스스로 놀라요."], ["It would overcome me with joy to see them, but I don’t think it would surprise me much, at first.", "그들을 본다면 기쁨에 겨워 어쩔 줄 모르겠지만, 처음 순간엔 그리 놀라지 않을 것 같아요."], ["In my fanciful times, I fancy that they might be anywhere; and I almost expect to see their dear faces on the bridges or the quays.", "공상에 잠길 때면 저는 그들이 어디에든 있을 수 있다고 상상하고, 다리 위에서나 부두에서 그 정겨운 얼굴들을 보게 될 것만 같은 기분마저 든답니다."]], [["Another difficulty that I have will seem very strange to you.", "제게 있는 또 다른 어려움은 당신께 무척 이상하게 여겨질 거예요."], ["It must seem very strange to any one but me, and does even to me: I often feel the old sad pity for--I need not write the word--for him.", "저 말고는 누구에게라도 무척 이상하게 보일 것이 분명하고, 저 자신에게조차 그래요. 저는 종종 그 옛날의 슬픈 연민을 느껴요—그 말을 굳이 쓸 필요는 없겠지요—그분에 대해서요."], ["Changed as he is, and inexpressibly blest and thankful as I always am to know it, the old sorrowful feeling of compassion comes upon me sometimes with such strength that I want to put my arms round his neck, tell him how I love him, and cry a little on his breast.", "그분이 그토록 달라지셨고, 그 사실을 알게 된 것을 제가 언제나 이루 말할 수 없이 다행으로, 감사히 여기는데도, 그 옛날의 서글픈 연민의 감정이 이따금 어찌나 거세게 밀려오는지, 저는 그분의 목을 두 팔로 끌어안고, 제가 얼마나 그분을 사랑하는지 말씀드리며, 그분 가슴에 안겨 조금 울고 싶어져요."], ["I should be glad after that, and proud and happy.", "그러고 나면 저는 기쁘고, 자랑스럽고, 행복할 거예요."], ["But I know that I must not do this; that he would not like it, that Fanny would be angry, that Mrs General would be amazed; and so I quiet myself.", "하지만 저는 그래선 안 된다는 걸 알아요. 그분이 좋아하지 않으시리란 것을, 패니가 화를 내리란 것을, 제너럴 부인이 깜짝 놀라시리란 것을요."], ["Yet in doing so, I struggle with the feeling that I have come to be at a distance from him; and that even in the midst of all the servants and attendants, he is deserted, and in want of me.", "그래서 저는 마음을 가라앉힌답니다. 그러나 그렇게 하면서도, 제가 그분에게서 멀어지고 말았다는 느낌과, 그 모든 하인과 시중드는 이들에 둘러싸여 있으면서도 그분이 버림받은 채 저를 필요로 하고 계신다는 느낌과 싸운답니다."]], [["Dear Mr Clennam, I have written a great deal about myself, but I must write a little more still, or what I wanted most of all to say in this weak letter would be left out of it.", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, 저는 제 자신에 관해 무척 많은 것을 썼지만, 그래도 조금 더 써야겠어요. 그러지 않으면 이 가냘픈 편지에서 무엇보다도 가장 하고 싶었던 말이 빠지고 말 테니까요."], ["In all these foolish thoughts of mine, which I have been so hardy as to confess to you because I know you will understand me if anybody can, and will make more allowance for me than anybody else would if you cannot--in all these thoughts, there is one thought scarcely ever--never--out of my memory, and that is that I hope you sometimes, in a quiet moment, have a thought for me.", "제가 이토록 대담하게 당신께 털어놓은 이 모든 어리석은 생각들 속에는—누군가 저를 이해해 줄 수 있다면 그건 당신이시리란 걸 알고, 설령 이해하지 못하신다 해도 다른 누구보다 저를 너그러이 봐주시리란 걸 알기에 털어놓는 것이지만—이 모든 생각들 속에는, 제 기억에서 좀처럼—아니, 결코—떠나지 않는 한 가지 생각이 있어요. 그건 바로 당신께서 이따금 고요한 순간에 저를 한 번쯤 생각해 주시기를 바란다는 거예요."], ["I must tell you that as to this, I have felt, ever since I have been away, an anxiety which I am very anxious to relieve.", "이 점에 관해 말씀드려야겠어요. 저는 떠나온 이래로 줄곧, 무척이나 풀고 싶은 어떤 불안을 느껴 왔어요."], ["I have been afraid that you may think of me in a new light, or a new character.", "당신께서 저를 새로운 시선으로, 혹은 새로운 모습으로 생각하실까 봐 두려웠답니다."], ["Don’t do that, I could not bear that--it would make me more unhappy than you can suppose.", "그러지 마세요, 저는 그것을 견딜 수 없어요—그것은 당신께서 짐작하시는 것보다 저를 더 불행하게 만들 거예요."], ["It would break my heart to believe that you thought of me in any way that would make me stranger to you than I was when you were so good to me.", "당신께서 제게 그토록 잘해 주셨던 그때보다 저를 당신께 더 낯선 사람으로 만드는 어떤 식으로든 저를 생각하신다고 믿게 된다면, 제 가슴은 미어질 거예요."], ["What I have to pray and entreat of you is, that you will never think of me as the daughter of a rich person; that you will never think of me as dressing any better, or living any better, than when you first knew me.", "제가 당신께 빌고 간청해야 할 것은, 부디 저를 부유한 사람의 딸로 결코 생각하지 마시고, 저를 당신께서 처음 아셨을 때보다 조금이라도 더 좋은 옷을 입거나 조금이라도 더 나은 형편으로 사는 사람으로 결코 생각하지 말아 주시라는 거예요."], ["That you will remember me only as the little shabby girl you protected with so much tenderness, from whose threadbare dress you have kept away the rain, and whose wet feet you have dried at your fire.", "부디 저를 당신께서 그토록 다정하게 보살펴 주셨던 그 작고 초라한 소녀로만, 당신께서 그 닳아빠진 옷에 비가 들이치지 않게 막아 주셨고, 그 젖은 발을 당신 난롯불에 말려 주셨던 그 소녀로만 기억해 주세요."], ["That you will think of me (when you think of me at all), and of my true affection and devoted gratitude, always without change, as of", "부디 저를(저를 생각하실 때면 언제든), 그리고 저의 진실한 애정과 헌신적인 감사를, 언제나 변함없이 이러한 사람으로 생각해 주세요—\n당신의 가엾은 아이,"]], [["Your poor child,", "리틀 도릿 올림."]], [["# LITTLE DORRIT.", "# LITTLE DORRIT."]], [["P.S.--Particularly remember that you are not to be uneasy about Mrs Gowan.", "추신.—특히 가우언 부인에 관해서는 마음 불편해하지 마셔야 한다는 걸 기억해 주세요."], ["Her words were, ‘Very well and very happy.’ And she looked most beautiful.", "그분의 말씀은 '아주 잘 지내고 아주 행복'하다는 것이었어요. 그리고 더없이 아름다워 보이셨답니다."]], [["# CHAPTER 5. Something Wrong Somewhere", "# CHAPTER 5. Something Wrong Somewhere"]], [["The family had been a month or two at Venice, when Mr Dorrit, who was much among Counts and Marquises, and had but scant leisure, set an hour of one day apart, beforehand, for the purpose of holding some conference with Mrs General.", "가족이 베네치아에 머문 지 한두 달이 지났을 무렵, 백작과 후작들 사이에 자주 어울리느라 여가가 거의 없던 도릿 씨는 어느 날 하루 가운데 한 시간을 미리 따로 떼어 두어, 제너럴 부인과 모종의 회담을 갖기로 했다."]], [["The time he had reserved in his mind arriving, he sent Mr Tinkler, his valet, to Mrs General’s apartment (which would have absorbed about a third of the area of the Marshalsea), to present his compliments to that lady, and represent him as desiring the favour of an interview.", "마음속에 정해 둔 시각이 다가오자, 그는 시종 팅클러 씨를 제너럴 부인의 처소로(그곳은 마셜시 면적의 삼분의 일쯤은 너끈히 차지할 만한 넓이였다) 보내, 그 부인에게 안부 인사를 전하고 면담의 영광을 청한다는 자신의 뜻을 알리게 했다."], ["It being that period of the forenoon when the various members of the family had coffee in their own chambers, some couple of hours before assembling at breakfast in a faded hall which had once been sumptuous, but was now the prey of watery vapours and a settled melancholy, Mrs General was accessible to the valet.", "그때는 가족의 여러 구성원이 각자의 방에서 커피를 마시는 오전 시간으로, 한때는 호화로웠으나 이제는 축축한 물안개와 깊이 자리 잡은 우수의 먹잇감이 되어 버린 빛바랜 홀에서 아침 식사를 위해 모이기 두어 시간쯤 전이었다. 그래서 제너럴 부인은 시종을 들일 수 있는 상태였다."], ["That envoy found her on a little square of carpet, so extremely diminutive in reference to the size of her stone and marble floor that she looked as if she might have had it spread for the trying on of a ready-made pair of shoes; or as if she had come into possession of the enchanted piece of carpet, bought for forty purses by one of the three princes in the Arabian Nights, and had that moment been transported on it, at a wish, into a palatial saloon with which it had no connection.", "그 사절은 부인이 작은 사각 양탄자 위에 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그 양탄자는 돌과 대리석으로 된 바닥의 크기에 비하면 어찌나 작던지, 마치 기성품 구두 한 켤레를 신어 보려고 펼쳐 놓은 것 같기도 했고, 혹은 아라비안나이트에 나오는 세 왕자 가운데 하나가 마흔 자루의 돈을 주고 산 그 마법의 양탄자를 손에 넣어, 방금 소원을 빌어 그것을 타고 아무런 연고도 없는 궁전 같은 응접실로 옮겨져 온 것 같기도 했다."]], [["Mrs General, replying to the envoy, as she set down her empty coffee-cup, that she was willing at once to proceed to Mr Dorrit’s apartment, and spare him the trouble of coming to her (which, in his gallantry, he had proposed), the envoy threw open the door, and escorted Mrs General to the presence.", "제너럴 부인은 빈 커피잔을 내려놓으며, 도릿 씨가 자신에게로 오는 수고를 덜도록(그는 신사다운 정중함으로 그렇게 하겠노라 제안했었다) 자기가 곧장 그의 처소로 가겠노라고 사절에게 답했다. 그러자 사절은 문을 활짝 열고 제너럴 부인을 그 앞으로 모셔 갔다."], ["It was quite a walk, by mysterious staircases and corridors, from Mrs General’s apartment,--hoodwinked by a narrow side street with a low gloomy bridge in it, and dungeon-like opposite tenements, their walls besmeared with a thousand downward stains and streaks, as if every crazy aperture in them had been weeping tears of rust into the Adriatic for centuries--to Mr Dorrit’s apartment: with a whole English house-front of window, a prospect of beautiful church-domes rising into the blue sky sheer out of the water which reflected them, and a hushed murmur of the Grand Canal laving the doorways below, where his gondolas and gondoliers attended his pleasure, drowsily swinging in a little forest of piles.", "제너럴 부인의 처소에서—좁은 옆골목에 가려진 그곳은, 그 골목에 낮고 음울한 다리가 걸려 있고, 맞은편엔 토굴 같은 셋집들이 늘어서 있었으며, 그 벽들은 수천 갈래의 흘러내린 얼룩과 줄무늬로 더럽혀져 있어, 마치 그 미친 듯한 틈새들 하나하나가 수백 년 동안 아드리아 해를 향해 녹물의 눈물을 흘려 온 것만 같았다—도릿 씨의 처소까지는, 신비로운 계단과 복도를 지나 제법 걸어야 하는 거리였다. 그의 처소는 영국식 집 정면을 통째로 옮겨 놓은 듯한 창이 나 있고, 푸른 하늘로 곧장 솟아오른 아름다운 교회 둥근 지붕들이 그것을 비추는 물 위에서 마주 보이며, 아래 문턱을 씻는 대운하의 잔잔한 속삭임이 들려왔고, 그곳엔 그의 곤돌라와 사공들이 작은 말뚝 숲 속에서 졸린 듯 흔들리며 그의 분부를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Mr Dorrit, in a resplendent dressing-gown and cap--the dormant grub that had so long bided its time among the Collegians had burst into a rare butterfly--rose to receive Mrs General.", "도릿 씨는 화려한 실내복과 모자를 걸친 채로—그토록 오랜 세월 수감자들 사이에서 때를 기다리며 잠자던 굼벵이가 마침내 진귀한 나비로 부화한 것이었다—제너럴 부인을 맞으러 일어섰다."], ["A chair to Mrs General.", "제너럴 부인에게 의자를."], ["An easier chair, sir; what are you doing, what are you about, what do you mean?", "더 편한 의자를, 여보게. 자네 지금 뭘 하는 건가, 무얼 하고 있는 거야, 무슨 생각인가?"], ["Now, leave us!", "자, 우리만 있게 물러가게!"]], [["‘Mrs General,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I took the liberty--’", "\"제너럴 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 입을 열었다, \"외람되이—\""]], [["‘By no means,’ Mrs General interposed.", "\"천만에요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말을 받았다."], ["‘I was quite at your disposition.", "\"저는 얼마든지 응할 수 있었답니다."], ["I had had my coffee.’", "커피도 이미 마셨고요.\""]], [["‘--I took the liberty,’ said Mr Dorrit again, with the magnificent placidity of one who was above correction, ‘to solicit the favour of a little private conversation with you, because I feel rather worried respecting my--ha--my younger daughter.", "\"—외람되이,\" 도릿 씨가 정정 따위는 초월한 사람다운 당당한 평정으로 다시 말했다, \"당신과 잠시 사사로운 대화를 나누는 영광을 청하였습니다."], ["You will have observed a great difference of temperament, madam, between my two daughters?’", "제가—허—저의 작은딸 문제로 적잖이 마음을 쓰고 있는 까닭입니다. 부인께서도 제 두 딸 사이에 기질의 큰 차이가 있음을 보셨겠지요?\""]], [["Said Mrs General in response, crossing her gloved hands (she was never without gloves, and they never creased and always fitted), ‘There is a great difference.’", "제너럴 부인이 이에 답하여, 장갑 낀 두 손을 모아 포개며(그녀는 결코 장갑을 벗는 법이 없었고, 그 장갑은 결코 구겨지지 않았으며 언제나 꼭 맞았다) 말했다. \"큰 차이가 있지요.\""]], [["‘May I ask to be favoured with your view of it?’ said Mr Dorrit, with a deference not incompatible with majestic serenity.", "\"그에 대한 부인의 견해를 들려주시기를 청해도 되겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 위엄 있는 평온함과 어긋나지 않는 정중함으로 말했다."]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned Mrs General, ‘has force of character and self-reliance.", "\"패니는,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"성격에 강단이 있고 자립심이 있습니다."], ["Amy, none.’", "에이미는 전혀 없고요.\""]], [["None?", "전혀 없다고?"], ["O Mrs General, ask the Marshalsea stones and bars.", "오 제너럴 부인, 마셜시의 돌과 쇠창살에게 물어보시오."], ["O Mrs General, ask the milliner who taught her to work, and the dancing-master who taught her sister to dance.", "오 제너럴 부인, 그 아이에게 바느질을 가르친 모자 만드는 여인에게, 그 아이의 언니에게 춤을 가르친 무용 선생에게 물어보시오."], ["O Mrs General, Mrs General, ask me, her father, what I owe her; and hear my testimony touching the life of this slighted little creature from her childhood up!", "오 제너럴 부인, 제너럴 부인, 그 아이의 아비인 나에게, 내가 그 아이에게 무엇을 빚졌는지 물어보시오. 그리고 그 어린 시절부터 줄곧 푸대접받아 온 이 작은 생명의 삶에 대한 나의 증언을 들어 보시오!"]], [["No such adjuration entered Mr. Dorrit’s head.", "그런 절절한 호소는 도릿 씨의 머릿속에 들어오지 않았다."], ["He looked at Mrs General, seated in her usual erect attitude on her coach-box behind the proprieties, and he said in a thoughtful manner, ‘True, madam.’", "그는 예법이라는 마부석 위에 늘 그렇듯 꼿꼿한 자세로 앉아 있는 제너럴 부인을 바라보며, 사려 깊은 태도로 말했다. \"지당하신 말씀입니다, 부인.\""]], [["‘I would not,’ said Mrs General, ‘be understood to say, observe, that there is nothing to improve in Fanny.", "\"제 말을,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"패니에게 고칠 점이 전혀 없다는 뜻으로 받아들이지는 말아 주세요."], ["But there is material there--perhaps, indeed, a little too much.’", "다만 그 아이에게는 다듬을 재료가 있습니다—사실, 어쩌면 조금 지나치게 많을 정도지요.\""]], [["‘Will you be kind enough, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘to be--ha--more explicit?", "\"부인, 조금 더—허—분명히 말씀해 주실 수 있겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["I do not quite understand my elder daughter’s having--hum--too much material.", "\"제 큰딸이—흠—재료가 지나치게 많다는 게 잘 이해되지 않는군요."], ["What material?’", "무슨 재료 말씀입니까?\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned Mrs General, ‘at present forms too many opinions.", "\"패니는,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"지금으로서는 의견을 지나치게 많이 형성합니다."], ["Perfect breeding forms none, and is never demonstrative.’", "완벽한 교양은 의견을 형성하지 않으며, 결코 감정을 드러내지 않지요.\""]], [["Lest he himself should be found deficient in perfect breeding, Mr Dorrit hastened to reply, ‘Unquestionably, madam, you are right.’ Mrs General returned, in her emotionless and expressionless manner, ‘I believe so.’", "자기 자신이 완벽한 교양에 모자란 사람으로 비칠까 저어하여, 도릿 씨는 황급히 답했다. \"두말할 나위 없이 옳으신 말씀입니다, 부인.\" 제너럴 부인은 감정도 표정도 없는 투로 답했다. \"저도 그렇게 믿습니다.\""]], [["‘But you are aware, my dear madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘that my daughters had the misfortune to lose their lamented mother when they were very young; and that, in consequence of my not having been until lately the recognised heir to my property, they have lived with me as a comparatively poor, though always proud, gentleman, in--ha hum--retirement!’", "\"하지만 부인께서도 아시다시피,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제 딸들은 아주 어린 나이에 애통하게도 그 어미를 잃는 불행을 겪었습니다. 그리고 제가 최근까지는 제 재산의 정식 상속인으로 인정받지 못한 탓에, 그 아이들은 저와 함께, 비교적 가난하나 언제나 자긍심만은 잃지 않은 신사의 곁에서, —허 흠—은거하며 살아왔지요!\""]], [["‘I do not,’ said Mrs General, ‘lose sight of the circumstance.’", "\"저는,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"그 사정을 놓치지 않고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, ‘of my daughter Fanny, under her present guidance and with such an example constantly before her--’", "\"부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다, \"제 딸 패니로 말하면, 지금의 지도 아래, 그리고 그토록 한결같은 본보기를 늘 곁에 두고 있으니—\""]], [["(Mrs General shut her eyes.)", "(제너럴 부인이 눈을 감았다.)"]], [["--‘I have no misgivings.", "\"—저는 걱정이 없습니다."], ["There is adaptability of character in Fanny.", "패니에게는 성격의 적응력이 있어요."], ["But my younger daughter, Mrs General, rather worries and vexes my thoughts.", "하지만 제 작은딸은, 제너럴 부인, 제 마음을 적잖이 졸이게 하고 괴롭힙니다."], ["I must inform you that she has always been my favourite.’", "말씀드려야겠습니다만, 그 아이는 늘 제가 가장 아끼는 자식이었습니다.\""]], [["‘There is no accounting,’ said Mrs General, ‘for these partialities.’", "\"이런 편애란,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"설명할 수가 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["‘Ha--no,’ assented Mr Dorrit.", "\"허—그렇습니다,\" 도릿 씨가 수긍했다."], ["‘No.", "\"그렇지요."], ["Now, madam, I am troubled by noticing that Amy is not, so to speak, one of ourselves.", "그런데 부인, 저는 에이미가 말하자면 우리네 사람들 중 하나가 아니라는 걸 알아채고 마음이 쓰입니다."], ["She does not care to go about with us; she is lost in the society we have here; our tastes are evidently not her tastes.", "그 아이는 우리와 함께 다니는 걸 내켜 하지 않고, 우리가 여기서 어울리는 사교계에서 갈피를 못 잡습니다. 우리의 취향이 분명 그 아이의 취향이 아니에요."], ["Which,’ said Mr Dorrit, summing up with judicial gravity, ‘is to say, in other words, that there is something wrong in--ha--Amy.’", "다시 말해,\" 도릿 씨가 재판관 같은 근엄함으로 요약하며 말했다, \"—허—에이미에게 무언가 잘못된 점이 있다는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘May we incline to the supposition,’ said Mrs General, with a little touch of varnish, ‘that something is referable to the novelty of the position?’", "\"혹 이렇게 짐작해 볼 수도 있지 않을까요,\" 제너럴 부인이 약간의 윤을 더해 가며 말했다, \"그 무언가가 처지의 새로움에서 비롯된 것이라고요?\""]], [["‘Excuse me, madam,’ observed Mr Dorrit, rather quickly.", "\"실례지만 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 다소 빠르게 받았다."], ["‘The daughter of a gentleman, though--ha--himself at one time comparatively far from affluent--comparatively--and herself reared in--hum--retirement, need not of necessity find this position so very novel.’\n‘True,’ said Mrs General, ‘true.’", "\"한 신사의 딸이라면—그 자신이—허—한때 비교적 부유함과는 거리가 멀었다 하더라도—비교적—그리고 그 딸 또한—흠—은거하며 자랐다 하더라도, 이 처지를 그렇게까지 새롭게 느낄 필요는 없지요.\""]], [["‘Therefore, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I took the liberty’ (he laid an emphasis on the phrase and repeated it, as though he stipulated, with urbane firmness, that he must not be contradicted again), ‘I took the liberty of requesting this interview, in order that I might mention the topic to you, and inquire how you would advise me?’", "\"옳으신 말씀입니다,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다, \"옳으신 말씀이에요.\" \"그러니 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제가 외람되이\"(그는 이 표현에 힘을 주어 되풀이했는데, 다시는 반박당하지 않겠노라고 정중하나 단호하게 못 박는 듯했다) \"이 면담을 청한 것은, 이 화제를 부인께 말씀드리고, 제게 어떻게 권하실지 여쭙기 위함이었습니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, ‘I have conversed with Amy several times since we have been residing here, on the general subject of the formation of a demeanour.", "\"도릿 씨,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다, \"저는 우리가 이곳에 머무는 동안 여러 차례 에이미와 대화를 나누었습니다, 거동을 갖추는 일이라는 일반적인 주제에 관하여서요."], ["She has expressed herself to me as wondering exceedingly at Venice.", "그 아이는 제게 베네치아가 몹시 경이롭다고 토로하더군요."], ["I have mentioned to her that it is better not to wonder.", "저는 경이로워하지 않는 편이 낫다고 일러 주었습니다."], ["I have pointed out to her that the celebrated Mr Eustace, the classical tourist, did not think much of it; and that he compared the Rialto, greatly to its disadvantage, with Westminster and Blackfriars Bridges.", "고전 여행기로 유명한 유스터스 씨가 이곳을 그리 높이 치지 않았으며, 리알토 다리를 웨스트민스터 다리며 블랙프라이어스 다리와 견주어 크게 깎아내렸다는 점도 지적해 주었지요."], ["I need not add, after what you have said, that I have not yet found my arguments successful.", "도릿 씨께서 하신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 제 논변이 아직 성공을 거두지 못했다는 건 굳이 덧붙일 것도 없겠군요."], ["You do me the honour to ask me what to advise.", "도릿 씨께서 제게 무엇을 권할지 물어 주시니 영광입니다."], ["It always appears to me (if this should prove to be a baseless assumption, I shall be pardoned), that Mr Dorrit has been accustomed to exercise influence over the minds of others.’", "제겐 늘 이렇게 여겨집니다만(이것이 근거 없는 짐작으로 드러난다면 용서해 주시기를), 도릿 씨께서는 다른 이들의 마음에 영향력을 행사하는 데 익숙하셨던 듯합니다.\""]], [["‘Hum--madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I have been at the head of--ha of a considerable community.", "\"흠—부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"저는—허—상당한 규모의 공동체의 수장 자리에 있었습니다."], ["You are right in supposing that I am not unaccustomed to--an influential position.’", "제가—영향력 있는 지위에—익숙하지 않은 사람이 아니라고 짐작하신 건 옳습니다.\""]], [["‘I am happy,’ returned Mrs General, ‘to be so corroborated.", "\"이렇게 확인해 주시니 기쁩니다,\" 제너럴 부인이 답했다."], ["I would therefore the more confidently recommend that Mr Dorrit should speak to Amy himself, and make his observations and wishes known to her.", "\"그러니 저는 더욱 자신 있게 권하겠습니다, 도릿 씨께서 직접 에이미에게 말씀하시어, 당신의 의향과 바람을 그 아이에게 알리시라고요."], ["Being his favourite, besides, and no doubt attached to him, she is all the more likely to yield to his influence.’", "게다가 그 아이는 당신이 가장 아끼는 딸이고, 분명 당신을 따르고 있으니, 당신의 영향력에 더더욱 잘 따를 것입니다.\""]], [["‘I had anticipated your suggestion, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘but--ha--was not sure that I might--hum--not encroach on--’", "\"저도 부인의 제안을 미리 짐작하고 있었습니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"다만—허—제가—흠—부인의 영역을 침범하는 것은 아닐지 확신이 서지 않아서—\""]], [["‘On my province, Mr Dorrit?’ said Mrs General, graciously.", "\"제 영역을 말씀이신가요, 도릿 씨?\" 제너럴 부인이 너그러이 말했다."], ["‘Do not mention it.’", "\"그런 말씀은 마세요.\""]], [["‘Then, with your leave, madam,’ resumed Mr Dorrit, ringing his little bell to summon his valet, ‘I will send for her at once.’", "\"그렇다면, 부인의 허락을 얻어,\" 도릿 씨가 시종을 부르려 작은 종을 울리며 다시 말했다, \"그 아이를 지금 곧 부르겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Does Mr Dorrit wish me to remain?’", "\"제가 남아 있기를 바라십니까, 도릿 씨?\""]], [["‘Perhaps, if you have no other engagement, you would not object for a minute or two--’", "\"혹시 다른 약속이 없으시다면, 일이 분 정도 함께해 주셔도 괜찮으시다면—\""]], [["‘Not at all.’", "\"전혀 상관없습니다.\""]], [["So, Tinkler the valet was instructed to find Miss Amy’s maid, and to request that subordinate to inform Miss Amy that Mr Dorrit wished to see her in his own room.", "그리하여 시종 팅클러는 에이미 아가씨의 하녀를 찾아, 그 아랫사람에게 도릿 씨가 자기 방에서 에이미 아가씨를 보고 싶어 한다고 전하라는 지시를 받았다."], ["In delivering this charge to Tinkler, Mr Dorrit looked severely at him, and also kept a jealous eye upon him until he went out at the door, mistrusting that he might have something in his mind prejudicial to the family dignity; that he might have even got wind of some Collegiate joke before he came into the service, and might be derisively reviving its remembrance at the present moment.", "이 임무를 팅클러에게 맡기면서 도릿 씨는 그를 매섭게 노려보았고, 또한 그가 문밖으로 나갈 때까지 질투 어린 눈으로 감시했는데, 혹여 그자가 가문의 위엄을 해칠 무언가를 마음에 품고 있는 건 아닌지, 심지어 이 집에 들어오기 전에 그 수감 시절의 농담 같은 것을 어디서 주워듣고는 지금 이 순간 비웃듯 그 기억을 되살리고 있는 건 아닌지 의심한 것이었다."], ["If Tinkler had happened to smile, however faintly and innocently, nothing would have persuaded Mr Dorrit, to the hour of his death, but that this was the case.", "만약 팅클러가, 아무리 희미하고 순진하게라도 웃기라도 했다면, 도릿 씨는 죽는 날까지 그것이 사실이라고 믿어 의심치 않았을 것이다."], ["As Tinkler happened, however, very fortunately for himself, to be of a serious and composed countenance, he escaped the secret danger that threatened him.", "그러나 자기 자신에게는 매우 다행스럽게도, 팅클러는 진지하고 차분한 얼굴을 하고 있던 덕분에 그를 위협하던 그 은밀한 위기를 모면했다."], ["And as on his return--when Mr Dorrit eyed him again--he announced Miss Amy as if she had come to a funeral, he left a vague impression on Mr Dorrit’s mind that he was a well-conducted young fellow, who had been brought up in the study of his Catechism by a widowed mother.", "그리고 돌아왔을 때—도릿 씨가 다시 그를 노려보았을 때—마치 장례식에라도 온 듯한 투로 에이미 아가씨가 왔음을 알린 덕분에, 그는 도릿 씨의 마음속에 막연하나마, 홀어머니 밑에서 교리문답을 익히며 자란 행실 바른 젊은이라는 인상을 남겼다."]], [["‘Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘you have just now been the subject of some conversation between myself and Mrs General.", "\"에이미,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"방금 너에 관하여 나와 제너럴 부인이 잠시 대화를 나누었다."], ["We agree that you scarcely seem at home here.", "우리는 네가 이곳에서 좀처럼 편안해 보이지 않는다는 데 의견을 같이했단다."], ["Ha--how is this?’", "허—어찌 된 일이냐?\""]], [["A pause.", "침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["‘I think, father, I require a little time.’", "\"아버지, 제겐 시간이 조금 필요할 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Papa is a preferable mode of address,’ observed Mrs General.", "\"파파라고 부르는 편이 더 낫단다,\" 제너럴 부인이 지적했다."], ["‘Father is rather vulgar, my dear.", "\"아버지란 말은 다소 천박하지요, 얘야."], ["The word Papa, besides, gives a pretty form to the lips.", "게다가 파파라는 말은 입술 모양을 예쁘게 만들어 준답니다."], ["Papa, potatoes, poultry, prunes, and prism are all very good words for the lips: especially prunes and prism.", "파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬, 그리고 프리즘은 모두 입술에 아주 좋은 말이에요. 특히 프룬과 프리즘이 그렇지요."], ["You will find it serviceable, in the formation of a demeanour, if you sometimes say to yourself in company--on entering a room, for instance--Papa, potatoes, poultry, prunes and prism, prunes and prism.’", "거동을 갖추는 데 도움이 될 거예요, 사람들과 함께 있을 때—이를테면 방에 들어설 때—이따금 속으로 이렇게 되뇌면요. 파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬과 프리즘, 프룬과 프리즘.\""]], [["‘Pray, my child,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘attend to the--hum--precepts of Mrs General.’", "\"부디, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"제너럴 부인의—흠—가르침에 따르려무나.\""]], [["Poor Little Dorrit, with a rather forlorn glance at that eminent varnisher, promised to try.", "가엾은 리틀 도릿은 그 탁월한 윤내기 장인을 다소 막막한 눈길로 바라보며, 노력해 보겠노라 약속했다."]], [["‘You say, Amy,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, ‘that you think you require time.", "\"네 말이,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다, \"에이미, 시간이 필요할 것 같다고 했지."], ["Time for what?’", "무엇을 위한 시간 말이냐?\""]], [["Another pause.", "또 한 번의 침묵."]], [["‘To become accustomed to the novelty of my life, was all I meant,’ said Little Dorrit, with her loving eyes upon her father; whom she had very nearly addressed as poultry, if not prunes and prism too, in her desire to submit herself to Mrs General and please him.", "\"제 생활의 새로움에 익숙해질 시간이라는 뜻이었어요,\" 리틀 도릿이 아버지를 향한 다정한 눈길로 말했다. 제너럴 부인에게 순종하고 아버지를 기쁘게 하려는 마음에서, 그녀는 하마터면 아버지를 폴트리라고, 어쩌면 프룬과 프리즘이라고까지 부를 뻔했다."]], [["Mr Dorrit frowned, and looked anything but pleased.", "도릿 씨는 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 도무지 흡족하지 않은 표정이었다."], ["‘Amy,’ he returned, ‘it appears to me, I must say, that you have had abundance of time for that.", "\"에이미,\" 그가 답했다, \"내 말해야겠다만, 너는 그러기에 충분한 시간을 가졌던 것으로 보이는구나."], ["Ha--you surprise me.", "허—너는 나를 놀라게 하는구나."], ["You disappoint me.", "너는 나를 실망시켜."], ["Fanny has conquered any such little difficulties, and--hum--why not you?’", "패니는 그런 사소한 어려움 따위는 다 극복했는데—흠—어찌 너는 못 한단 말이냐?\""]], [["‘I hope I shall do better soon,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"머지않아 더 잘하게 되기를 바라요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘I hope so,’ returned her father.", "\"나도 그러길 바란다,\" 아버지가 답했다."], ["‘I--ha--I most devoutly hope so, Amy.", "\"나는—허—진심으로 그러길 바라, 에이미."], ["I sent for you, in order that I might say--hum--impressively say, in the presence of Mrs General, to whom we are all so much indebted for obligingly being present among us, on--ha--on this or any other occasion,’ Mrs General shut her eyes, ‘that I--ha hum--am not pleased with you.", "내가 너를 부른 것은—흠—분명히 말하기 위해서다, 우리 모두가 이런—허—이런 자리든 다른 어떤 자리든 너그러이 함께해 주시어 그토록 큰 은혜를 입고 있는 제너럴 부인 앞에서,\" 제너럴 부인이 눈을 감았다, \"내가—허 흠—너에게 흡족하지 않다는 것을."], ["You make Mrs General’s a thankless task.", "너는 제너럴 부인의 노고를 보람 없게 만들고 있어."], ["You--ha--embarrass me very much.", "너는—허—나를 몹시 난처하게 한다."], ["You have always (as I have informed Mrs General) been my favourite child; I have always made you a--hum--a friend and companion; in return, I beg--I--ha--I _do_ beg, that you accommodate yourself better to--hum--circumstances, and dutifully do what becomes your--your station.’", "너는 늘(내가 제너럴 부인께 말씀드렸듯이) 내가 가장 아끼는 자식이었고, 나는 늘 너를—흠—벗이자 동반자로 여겨 왔다. 그 보답으로 내가 청하건대—나는—허—진심으로 청하건대, 네가—흠—형편에 좀 더 잘 맞추어, 네—네 신분에 걸맞은 바를 충실히 행하기를 바란다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit was even a little more fragmentary than usual, being excited on the subject and anxious to make himself particularly emphatic.", "도릿 씨는 이 주제에 흥분한 데다 유난히 힘주어 말하고 싶은 마음에, 평소보다도 더 토막토막 끊어 말했다."]], [["‘I do beg,’ he repeated, ‘that this may be attended to, and that you will seriously take pains and try to conduct yourself in a manner both becoming your position as--ha--Miss Amy Dorrit, and satisfactory to myself and Mrs General.’", "\"내가 진심으로 청하건대,\" 그가 되풀이했다, \"이 일에 유념하여, 진지하게 애를 쓰고, —허—에이미 도릿 아가씨라는 네 신분에 걸맞으면서 동시에 나와 제너럴 부인을 흡족하게 할 방식으로 처신하도록 노력해 다오.\""]], [["That lady shut her eyes again, on being again referred to; then, slowly opening them and rising, added these words:", "다시 자기가 거론되자 제너럴 부인은 또 한 번 눈을 감았다. 그러더니 천천히 눈을 뜨고 일어서며 이런 말을 덧붙였다."]], [["‘If Miss Amy Dorrit will direct her own attention to, and will accept of my poor assistance in, the formation of a surface, Mr. Dorrit will have no further cause of anxiety.", "\"만약 에이미 도릿 아가씨가 표면을 갖추는 일에 스스로 주의를 기울이고 저의 변변찮은 도움을 받아 주신다면, 도릿 씨께서는 더 이상 근심하실 일이 없을 거예요."], ["May I take this opportunity of remarking, as an instance in point, that it is scarcely delicate to look at vagrants with the attention which I have seen bestowed upon them by a very dear young friend of mine?", "이 기회를 빌려, 적절한 예로서 한 말씀 드리자면, 제가 아주 아끼는 어느 젊은 벗에게서 본 바와 같은 그런 주의를 기울여 부랑자들을 바라보는 것은 좀처럼 우아한 일이 못 됩니다."], ["They should not be looked at.", "그런 이들은 바라보아서는 안 됩니다."], ["Nothing disagreeable should ever be looked at.", "불쾌한 것은 무엇이든 결코 바라보아서는 안 되지요."], ["Apart from such a habit standing in the way of that graceful equanimity of surface which is so expressive of good breeding, it hardly seems compatible with refinement of mind.", "그런 습관은 좋은 교양을 그토록 잘 드러내 주는 그 우아한 표면의 평정을 가로막을 뿐 아니라, 정신의 세련됨과도 거의 양립하기 어렵습니다."], ["A truly refined mind will seem to be ignorant of the existence of anything that is not perfectly proper, placid, and pleasant.’ Having delivered this exalted sentiment, Mrs General made a sweeping obeisance, and retired with an expression of mouth indicative of Prunes and Prism.", "참으로 세련된 정신은 완벽하게 적절하고 평온하며 유쾌하지 않은 것이라면 그 존재 자체를 모르는 듯 보이는 법이지요.\" 이 고상한 견해를 늘어놓은 뒤, 제너럴 부인은 위엄 있게 깊이 절하고는, 프룬과 프리즘을 머금은 듯한 입 모양을 한 채 물러났다."]], [["Little Dorrit, whether speaking or silent, had preserved her quiet earnestness and her loving look.", "리틀 도릿은 말을 하든 잠자코 있든, 그 차분한 진지함과 다정한 눈빛을 잃지 않았다."], ["It had not been clouded, except for a passing moment, until now.", "그것은 지금까지, 한순간 스쳐 간 때를 빼고는 흐려진 적이 없었다."], ["But now that she was left alone with him the fingers of her lightly folded hands were agitated, and there was repressed emotion in her face.", "그러나 이제 아버지와 단둘이 남게 되자, 가볍게 맞잡고 있던 그녀의 손가락들이 떨렸고, 얼굴에는 억눌린 감정이 어렸다."]], [["Not for herself.", "자기 자신을 위해서가 아니었다."], ["She might feel a little wounded, but her care was not for herself.", "그녀는 어쩌면 조금 상처를 받았을지도 모르나, 그녀의 근심은 자기 자신을 향한 것이 아니었다."], ["Her thoughts still turned, as they always had turned, to him.", "그녀의 생각은 늘 그래 왔듯 여전히 아버지를 향해 있었다."], ["A faint misgiving, which had hung about her since their accession to fortune, that even now she could never see him as he used to be before the prison days, had gradually begun to assume form in her mind.", "부를 얻은 이후로 그녀에게 줄곧 매달려 있던 어렴풋한 불안—이제 와서도 그녀는 결코 아버지를 감옥 시절 이전의 모습으로는 볼 수 없으리라는 불안—이 차츰 그녀의 마음속에서 형태를 갖추기 시작했다."], ["She felt that, in what he had just now said to her and in his whole bearing towards her, there was the well-known shadow of the Marshalsea wall.", "그녀는 느꼈다, 아버지가 방금 그녀에게 한 말에서도, 그녀를 대하는 그의 태도 전체에서도, 그 익히 아는 마셜시 담장의 그림자가 드리워 있음을."], ["It took a new shape, but it was the old sad shadow.", "그것은 새로운 형태를 띠었으나, 그 옛날의 슬픈 그림자였다."], ["She began with sorrowful unwillingness to acknowledge to herself that she was not strong enough to keep off the fear that no space in the life of man could overcome that quarter of a century behind the prison bars.", "그녀는 슬프고도 내키지 않는 마음으로, 인간의 생애 그 어떤 세월로도 쇠창살 뒤의 그 사반세기를 이겨낼 수 없으리라는 두려움을 막아 낼 만큼 자신이 강하지 못함을 스스로 인정하기 시작했다."], ["She had no blame to bestow upon him, therefore: nothing to reproach him with, no emotions in her faithful heart but great compassion and unbounded tenderness.", "그러므로 그녀에게는 아버지를 탓할 거리가 없었다. 그를 책망할 것도, 그 충실한 마음속에 어떤 감정도 없었으니, 다만 깊은 연민과 한없는 다정함뿐이었다."]], [["This is why it was, that, even as he sat before her on his sofa, in the brilliant light of a bright Italian day, the wonderful city without and the splendours of an old palace within, she saw him at the moment in the long-familiar gloom of his Marshalsea lodging, and wished to take her seat beside him, and comfort him, and be again full of confidence with him, and of usefulness to him.", "이것이 바로, 환한 이탈리아의 하루의 눈부신 빛 속에서, 밖에는 그 경이로운 도시가, 안에는 오래된 궁전의 화려함이 펼쳐진 가운데, 아버지가 소파에 앉아 그녀 앞에 있는 그 순간에도, 그녀가 오랜 세월 익숙했던 마셜시 거처의 우중충함 속에 있는 그를 보았던 까닭이며, 그의 곁에 다시 앉아 그를 위로하고, 그와 다시 마음을 터놓고, 그에게 다시 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶어 했던 까닭이다."], ["If he divined what was in her thoughts, his own were not in tune with it.", "만약 그가 그녀의 마음속에 든 것을 헤아렸다 하더라도, 그 자신의 마음은 그것과 가락이 맞지 않았다."], ["After some uneasy moving in his seat, he got up and walked about, looking very much dissatisfied.", "자리에서 한동안 불편하게 들썩이더니, 그는 일어나 이리저리 거닐었고, 몹시 못마땅한 기색이었다."]], [["‘Is there anything else you wish to say to me, dear father?’", "\"제게 더 하실 말씀이 있으신가요, 아버지?\""]], [["‘No, no.", "\"아니, 아니다."], ["Nothing else.’", "더는 없다.\""]], [["‘I am sorry you have not been pleased with me, dear.", "\"제가 아버지를 흡족하게 해 드리지 못해 죄송해요."], ["I hope you will not think of me with displeasure now.", "부디 지금 저를 언짢게 여기지 말아 주세요."], ["I am going to try, more than ever, to adapt myself as you wish to what surrounds me--for indeed I have tried all along, though I have failed, I know.’", "저는 그 어느 때보다 더 애써, 아버지의 바람대로 제 주위 것들에 저를 맞추어 보겠어요—정말이지 저는 그동안 줄곧 애써 왔답니다, 비록 실패한 줄은 저도 알지만요.\""]], [["‘Amy,’ he returned, turning short upon her.", "\"에이미,\" 그가 그녀에게 홱 돌아서며 답했다."], ["‘You--ha--habitually hurt me.’\n‘Hurt you, father! I!’", "\"너는—허—번번이 나에게 상처를 주는구나.\""]], [["‘There is a--hum--a topic,’ said Mr Dorrit, looking all about the ceiling of the room, and never at the attentive, uncomplainingly shocked face, ‘a painful topic, a series of events which I wish--ha--altogether to obliterate.", "\"제가 상처를 드린다고요, 아버지! 제가요!\""], ["This is understood by your sister, who has already remonstrated with you in my presence; it is understood by your brother; it is understood by--ha hum--by every one of delicacy and sensitiveness except yourself--ha--I am sorry to say, except yourself.", "\"한 가지—흠—화제가 있다,\" 도릿 씨가 방 천장을 이리저리 둘러볼 뿐, 그 가만히 귀 기울이는, 군말 없이 충격받은 얼굴은 한 번도 보지 않으며 말했다, \"괴로운 화제, 내가—허—온전히 지워 버리고 싶은 일련의 사건들 말이다."], ["You, Amy--hum--you alone and only you--constantly revive the topic, though not in words.’", "이는 네 언니도 알고 있어서, 이미 내 앞에서 너를 타이른 바 있고, 네 오라비도 알고 있으며, —허 흠—섬세함과 감수성을 지닌 이라면 누구나 알고 있다, 너 하나만 빼고—허—유감스럽게도 너 하나만 빼고 말이다. 너는, 에이미—흠—오직 너만이, 오직 너 혼자만이, 끊임없이 그 화제를 되살린다, 말로는 아닐지언정.\""]], [["She laid her hand on his arm.", "그녀는 그의 팔에 손을 얹었다."], ["She did nothing more.", "그뿐이었다."], ["She gently touched him.", "그저 가만히 그를 어루만졌다."], ["The trembling hand may have said, with some expression, ‘Think of me, think how I have worked, think of my many cares!’ But she said not a syllable herself.", "그 떨리는 손은 어쩌면 어떤 표정을 담아 이렇게 말했는지도 모른다, \"저를 생각해 주세요, 제가 어떻게 일해 왔는지, 제 그 많은 근심들을 생각해 주세요!\" 그러나 그녀 자신은 단 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["There was a reproach in the touch so addressed to him that she had not foreseen, or she would have withheld her hand.", "그렇게 그에게 건넨 그 손길 속에는 그녀가 미처 예상치 못한 책망이 깃들어 있었다—예상했더라면 그녀는 손을 거두었을 것이다."], ["He began to justify himself in a heated, stumbling, angry manner, which made nothing of it.", "그는 발끈하여, 더듬거리며, 성난 태도로 자기를 변호하기 시작했는데, 그 손길의 의미 따위는 안중에도 없었다."]], [["‘I was there all those years.", "\"나는 그 모든 세월 동안 거기 있었다."], ["I was--ha--universally acknowledged as the head of the place.", "나는—허—누구나 인정하는 그곳의 수장이었어."], ["I--hum--I caused you to be respected there, Amy.", "나는—흠—내가 거기서 너를 존경받게 했다, 에이미."], ["I--ha hum--I gave my family a position there.", "나는—허 흠—내가 거기서 내 가족에게 지위를 마련해 주었어."], ["I deserve a return.", "나는 보답을 받을 자격이 있다."], ["I claim a return.", "나는 보답을 요구한다."], ["I say, sweep it off the face of the earth and begin afresh.", "그것을 지상에서 깨끗이 쓸어 없애고 새로 시작하자는 거다."], ["Is that much?", "그게 그리 큰 요구냐?"], ["I ask, is _that_ much?’", "묻겠다만, 그게 그리 큰 요구냐?\""]], [["He did not once look at her, as he rambled on in this way; but gesticulated at, and appealed to, the empty air.", "이렇게 두서없이 떠들어 대는 동안, 그는 그녀를 한 번도 보지 않았다. 다만 허공을 향해 손짓하고, 허공에 대고 호소할 뿐이었다."]], [["‘I have suffered.", "\"나는 고통받았다."], ["Probably I know how much I have suffered better than any one--ha--I say than any one!", "아마 내가 얼마나 고통받았는지는 그 누구보다도 내가 더 잘 알 게다—허—내 말하건대 그 누구보다도!"], ["If _I_ can put that aside, if _I_ can eradicate the marks of what I have endured, and can emerge before the world--a--ha--gentleman unspoiled, unspotted--is it a great deal to expect--I say again, is it a great deal to expect--that my children should--hum--do the same and sweep that accursed experience off the face of the earth?’", "만약 내가 그것을 제쳐 둘 수 있다면, 만약 내가 견뎌 온 것의 자국들을 지워 없애고, 세상 앞에 나설 수 있다면—허—더럽혀지지 않고 얼룩지지 않은 신사로서 말이다—그게 그리 큰 바람이냐—다시 말하건대, 그게 그리 큰 바람이냔 말이다—내 자식들이—흠—나처럼 똑같이 하여 그 저주받은 경험을 지상에서 쓸어 없애기를 바라는 것이?\""]], [["In spite of his flustered state, he made all these exclamations in a carefully suppressed voice, lest the valet should overhear anything.", "이렇게 흥분한 상태에서도, 그는 시종이 무엇이라도 엿들을까 저어하여 이 모든 외침을 조심스레 죽인 목소리로 토해 냈다."]], [["‘Accordingly, they do it.", "\"그래서, 다들 그렇게 한다."], ["Your sister does it.", "네 언니도 그렇게 해."], ["Your brother does it.", "네 오라비도 그렇게 한다."], ["You alone, my favourite child, whom I made the friend and companion of my life when you were a mere--hum--Baby, do not do it.", "오직 너만이, 네가 그저—흠—갓난아기였을 적부터 내가 내 인생의 벗이자 동반자로 삼아 온 내 가장 아끼는 자식인 너만이, 그렇게 하지 않아."], ["You alone say you can’t do it.", "오직 너만이 그렇게 할 수 없다고 말하지."], ["I provide you with valuable assistance to do it.", "나는 네가 그것을 할 수 있도록 값진 조력을 마련해 주었다."], ["I attach an accomplished and highly bred lady--ha--Mrs General, to you, for the purpose of doing it.", "나는 그 일을 위해 교양 있고 지체 높은 부인을—허—제너럴 부인을 네게 붙여 주었어."], ["Is it surprising that I should be displeased?", "내가 언짢아하는 것이 그리 놀랄 일이냐?"], ["Is it necessary that I should defend myself for expressing my displeasure?", "내가 언짢음을 드러내는 데 변명을 늘어놓아야 할 까닭이 있느냔 말이다?"], ["No!’", "없다!\""]], [["Notwithstanding which, he continued to defend himself, without any abatement of his flushed mood.", "그럼에도 그는 그 달아오른 기분을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채 계속해서 자기를 변호했다."]], [["‘I am careful to appeal to that lady for confirmation, before I express any displeasure at all.", "\"나는 조금이라도 언짢음을 드러내기에 앞서, 그 부인에게 확인을 구하는 데에까지 신경을 썼다."], ["I--hum--I necessarily make that appeal within limited bounds, or I--ha--should render legible, by that lady, what I desire to be blotted out.", "나는—흠—그 확인을 구할 때 어쩔 수 없이 그 한계를 두었어, 그러지 않으면 나는—허—내가 지워 없애고 싶은 것을 그 부인이 읽어 낼 수 있게 만들 테니까."], ["Am I selfish?", "내가 이기적이냐?"], ["Do I complain for my own sake?", "내가 나 자신을 위해 불평하는 것이냐?"], ["No. No.", "아니다."], ["Principally for--ha hum--your sake, Amy.’", "아니야. 무엇보다도—허 흠—너를 위해서다, 에이미.\""]], [["This last consideration plainly appeared, from his manner of pursuing it, to have just that instant come into his head.", "이 마지막 고려는, 그가 그것을 이어 가는 태도로 보아, 바로 그 순간에야 머릿속에 떠오른 것이 분명했다."]], [["‘I said I was hurt. So I am.", "\"내가 상처받았다고 했다."], ["So I--ha--am determined to be, whatever is advanced to the contrary.", "정말로 그렇다. 그러니 나는—허—무슨 말로 반박하든 상처받기로 작정했어."], ["I am hurt that my daughter, seated in the--hum--lap of fortune, should mope and retire and proclaim herself unequal to her destiny.", "부의—흠—품에 안긴 내 딸이, 우울에 잠겨 물러나 앉아 제 운명을 감당할 수 없노라 공언하는 것이 나는 상처가 된다."], ["I am hurt that she should--ha--systematically reproduce what the rest of us blot out; and seem--hum--I had almost said positively anxious--to announce to wealthy and distinguished society that she was born and bred in--ha hum--a place that I myself decline to name.", "그 아이가—허—나머지 우리가 지워 없애려는 것을 줄기차게 되살리고, 부유하고 명망 높은 사교계를 향해, 자기가—허 흠—나 자신은 그 이름을 입에 담기조차 거부하는 어떤 곳에서 태어나고 자랐노라고—흠—거의 안달하다시피 알리려는 듯 보이는 것이 나는 상처가 돼."], ["But there is no inconsistency--ha--not the least, in my feeling hurt, and yet complaining principally for your sake, Amy.", "그러나 거기에는 모순이 없다—허—조금도 없어, 내가 상처받았다고 느끼면서, 그러면서도 무엇보다 너를 위해 불평하는 데에는 말이다, 에이미."], ["I do; I say again, I do.", "그래, 다시 말하건대, 정말로 그렇다."], ["It is for your sake that I wish you, under the auspices of Mrs General, to form a--hum--a surface.", "내가 제너럴 부인의 후원 아래 네가—흠—표면을 갖추기를 바라는 것은 너를 위해서다."], ["It is for your sake that I wish you to have a--ha--truly refined mind, and (in the striking words of Mrs General) to be ignorant of everything that is not perfectly proper, placid, and pleasant.’", "내가 네게—허—참으로 세련된 정신이 깃들기를, 그리고 (제너럴 부인의 인상적인 말씀대로) 완벽하게 적절하고 평온하며 유쾌하지 않은 것이라면 무엇이든 모르는 사람이 되기를 바라는 것은 너를 위해서야.\""]], [["He had been running down by jerks, during his last speech, like a sort of ill-adjusted alarum.", "이 마지막 일장 연설을 하는 동안, 그는 잘못 맞춰진 자명종처럼 띄엄띄엄 기운이 다해 가고 있었다."], ["The touch was still upon his arm.", "그 손길은 여전히 그의 팔 위에 있었다."], ["He fell silent; and after looking about the ceiling again for a little while, looked down at her.", "그는 입을 다물었고, 잠시 다시 천장을 둘러보더니, 그녀를 내려다보았다."], ["Her head drooped, and he could not see her face; but her touch was tender and quiet, and in the expression of her dejected figure there was no blame--nothing but love.", "그녀의 고개는 숙여져 있어 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으나, 그녀의 손길은 부드럽고 고요했으며, 그 풀 죽은 모습의 표정 속에는 책망이라곤 없었다—오직 사랑뿐이었다."], ["He began to whimper, just as he had done that night in the prison when she afterwards sat at his bedside till morning; exclaimed that he was a poor ruin and a poor wretch in the midst of his wealth; and clasped her in his arms.", "그는 흐느끼기 시작했다, 꼭 그 옛날 감옥에서 그 밤에 그랬듯이—그날 밤 그녀는 아침까지 그의 머리맡에 앉아 있었다. 그는 부유함 한가운데서 자기가 가엾은 폐허요 가엾은 비참한 인간이라고 부르짖으며, 그녀를 두 팔로 끌어안았다."], ["‘Hush, hush, my own dear!", "\"쉿, 쉿, 내 사랑하는 아이야!"], ["Kiss me!’ was all she said to him.", "입맞춰 다오!\" 그녀가 그에게 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."], ["His tears were soon dried, much sooner than on the former occasion; and he was presently afterwards very high with his valet, as a way of righting himself for having shed any.", "그의 눈물은 곧 말랐는데, 이전 때보다 훨씬 빨리 말랐다. 그러더니 그는 곧이어 시종에게 자못 거만하게 굴었으니, 눈물을 흘린 일에 대해 스스로를 추스르는 한 방편이었다."]], [["With one remarkable exception, to be recorded in its place, this was the only time, in his life of freedom and fortune, when he spoke to his daughter Amy of the old days.", "뒤에 제자리에서 기록될 한 가지 주목할 만한 예외를 빼면, 이것이 그의 자유롭고 부유한 생애에서 딸 에이미에게 옛 시절을 이야기한 유일한 때였다."]], [["But, now, the breakfast hour arrived; and with it Miss Fanny from her apartment, and Mr Edward from his apartment.", "그러나 이제 아침 식사 시간이 다가왔고, 그와 더불어 패니 아가씨가 제 처소에서, 에드워드 도련님이 제 처소에서 나타났다."], ["Both these young persons of distinction were something the worse for late hours.", "이 지체 높은 두 젊은이는 둘 다 늦은 시각까지 깨어 있던 탓에 다소 상해 있었다."], ["As to Miss Fanny, she had become the victim of an insatiate mania for what she called ‘going into society;’ and would have gone into it head-foremost fifty times between sunset and sunrise, if so many opportunities had been at her disposal.", "패니 아가씨로 말하면, 그녀는 자칭 '사교계 출입'이라는 것에 대한 끝없는 광증의 희생물이 되어 있어서, 기회만 마음껏 주어졌더라면 해 질 녘부터 해 뜰 녘까지 쉰 번이라도 곤두박질치듯 그 속으로 뛰어들었을 것이다."], ["As to Mr Edward, he, too, had a large acquaintance, and was generally engaged (for the most part, in diceing circles, or others of a kindred nature), during the greater part of every night.", "에드워드 도련님으로 말하면, 그 역시 폭넓은 교우 관계를 지녀, 밤마다 거의 대부분의 시간 동안(주로 노름판이나 그와 비슷한 부류의 자리에서) 분주했다."], ["For this gentleman, when his fortunes changed, had stood at the great advantage of being already prepared for the highest associates, and having little to learn: so much was he indebted to the happy accidents which had made him acquainted with horse-dealing and billiard-marking.", "이 신사로 말하자면, 그는 운수가 바뀌었을 때 이미 가장 지체 높은 부류와 어울릴 채비가 갖추어져 있고 새로 배울 것도 거의 없다는 큰 이점을 누리고 있었으니, 그가 말 거래며 당구장 점수 기록과 친숙해지게 만들어 준 그 다행한 우연들에 그만큼 큰 빚을 진 셈이었다."]], [["At breakfast, Mr Frederick Dorrit likewise appeared.", "아침 식사에는 프레더릭 도릿 씨도 모습을 보였다."], ["As the old gentleman inhabited the highest story of the palace, where he might have practised pistol-shooting without much chance of discovery by the other inmates, his younger niece had taken courage to propose the restoration to him of his clarionet, which Mr Dorrit had ordered to be confiscated, but which she had ventured to preserve.", "그 노신사는 궁전의 맨 위층에 거처하고 있었는데, 그곳에서라면 다른 거주자들에게 들킬 염려 없이 권총 사격 연습을 해도 무방했을 것이다. 그래서 작은 조카딸은 용기를 내어, 도릿 씨가 압수하라 명했으나 그녀가 감히 간직해 둔 그의 클라리넷을 그에게 돌려주자고 제안했다."], ["Notwithstanding some objections from Miss Fanny, that it was a low instrument, and that she detested the sound of it, the concession had been made.", "그것은 천한 악기인 데다 그 소리가 질색이라는 패니 아가씨의 몇몇 반대에도 불구하고, 그 양보는 받아들여졌다."], ["But it was then discovered that he had had enough of it, and never played it, now that it was no longer his means of getting bread.", "그러나 막상 돌려주고 보니, 이제 그것이 더는 그의 밥벌이 수단이 아니게 되자 그는 그것에 싫증이 났는지, 통 불지 않는 것이었다."], ["He had insensibly acquired a new habit of shuffling into the picture-galleries, always with his twisted paper of snuff in his hand (much to the indignation of Miss Fanny, who had proposed the purchase of a gold box for him that the family might not be discredited, which he had absolutely refused to carry when it was bought); and of passing hours and hours before the portraits of renowned Venetians.", "그는 어느새 손에 비틀어 만 코담배 종이를 늘 든 채로 미술관을 어슬렁어슬렁 드나드는 새 습관을 들였고(가문이 망신당하지 않도록 그에게 금 코담뱃갑을 사 주자고 제안했던 패니 아가씨로서는 분통 터지는 일이었으니, 그는 막상 그것을 사 주자 들고 다니기를 한사코 거부했다), 이름난 베네치아인들의 초상화 앞에서 몇 시간이고 보내곤 했다."], ["It was never made out what his dazed eyes saw in them; whether he had an interest in them merely as pictures, or whether he confusedly identified them with a glory that was departed, like the strength of his own mind.", "그 멍한 두 눈이 그 그림들 속에서 무엇을 보았는지는 끝내 알 수 없었으니, 그저 그림으로서 흥미를 느꼈던 것인지, 아니면 그것들을 자기 정신의 기력처럼 이미 떠나 버린 어떤 영광과 어렴풋이 동일시했던 것인지 알 길이 없었다."], ["But he paid his court to them with great exactness, and clearly derived pleasure from the pursuit.", "그러나 그는 더없이 정확하게 그것들에게 경의를 표했고, 그 일에서 분명 즐거움을 얻었다."], ["After the first few days, Little Dorrit happened one morning to assist at these attentions.", "처음 며칠이 지난 어느 아침, 리틀 도릿은 우연히 이 경의의 자리에 함께하게 되었다."], ["It so evidently heightened his gratification that she often accompanied him afterwards, and the greatest delight of which the old man had shown himself susceptible since his ruin, arose out of these excursions, when he would carry a chair about for her from picture to picture, and stand behind it, in spite of all her remonstrances, silently presenting her to the noble Venetians.", "그것이 그의 기쁨을 어찌나 뚜렷이 돋우어 주던지, 그 뒤로 그녀는 자주 그와 동행했고, 노인이 몰락 이후로 느낄 수 있었던 가장 큰 즐거움이 바로 이 나들이에서 비롯되었으니, 그럴 때면 그는 그녀를 위해 의자를 이 그림 저 그림으로 들고 다니며, 그녀의 온갖 만류에도 아랑곳없이 그 뒤에 서서, 말없이 고귀한 베네치아인들에게 그녀를 소개하는 것이었다."]], [["It fell out that, at this family breakfast, he referred to their having seen in a gallery, on the previous day, the lady and gentleman whom they had encountered on the Great Saint Bernard, ‘I forget the name,’ said he.", "마침 이 가족 아침 식사 자리에서, 그가 전날 어느 미술관에서 그들이 그레이트 세인트버나드에서 마주쳤던 그 부인과 신사를 보았던 일을 언급했다. \"이름은 잊어버렸구나,\""], ["‘I dare say you remember them, William?", "그가 말했다. \"넌 기억하겠지, 윌리엄?"], ["I dare say you do, Edward?’", "넌 기억하지, 에드워드?\""]], [["‘_I_ remember ‘em well enough,’ said the latter.", "\"저야 잘 기억하지요,\" 후자가 말했다."]], [["‘I should think so,’ observed Miss Fanny, with a toss of her head and a glance at her sister.", "\"그러시겠죠,\" 패니 아가씨가 고개를 한 번 까딱하고 동생을 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["‘But they would not have been recalled to our remembrance, I suspect, if Uncle hadn’t tumbled over the subject.’", "\"하지만 큰아버지가 그 일에 발이 걸려 넘어지지만 않으셨어도, 우리 기억 속에 다시 떠오를 일은 없었을 거예요.\""]], [["‘My dear, what a curious phrase,’ said Mrs General.", "\"얘야, 참 묘한 표현이구나,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Would not inadvertently lighted upon, or accidentally referred to, be better?’", "\"무심코 마주치게 되었다거나 우연히 거론되었다고 하는 편이 낫지 않겠니?\""]], [["‘Thank you very much, Mrs General,’ returned the young lady, ‘no, I think not.", "\"정말 감사합니다, 제너럴 부인,\" 그 젊은 아가씨가 답했다, \"하지만 아뇨, 그렇지 않은 것 같아요."], ["On the whole I prefer my own expression.’", "전반적으로 저는 제 표현이 더 좋네요.\""]], [["This was always Miss Fanny’s way of receiving a suggestion from Mrs General.", "이것이 제너럴 부인에게서 받은 제안을 대하는 패니 아가씨의 한결같은 방식이었다."], ["But she always stored it up in her mind, and adopted it at another time.", "그러나 그녀는 늘 그것을 마음에 갈무리해 두었다가 다른 때에 써먹었다."]], [["‘I should have mentioned our having met Mr and Mrs Gowan, Fanny,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘even if Uncle had not.", "\"큰아버지가 아니더라도 제가 가우언 부부를 만난 이야기를 했을 거예요, 패니 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["I have scarcely seen you since, you know.", "\"그 뒤로 언니 얼굴을 거의 못 봤잖아요."], ["I meant to have spoken of it at breakfast; because I should like to pay a visit to Mrs Gowan, and to become better acquainted with her, if Papa and Mrs General do not object.’", "저는 아침 식사 때 그 얘기를 하려고 했어요. 가우언 부인을 찾아뵙고 더 가까이 알고 지내고 싶거든요, 아버지와 제너럴 부인께서 반대만 안 하신다면요.\""]], [["‘Well, Amy,’ said Fanny, ‘I am sure I am glad to find you at last expressing a wish to become better acquainted with anybody in Venice.", "\"그래, 에이미,\" 패니가 말했다, \"네가 마침내 베네치아에서 누구든 더 가까이 알고 지내고 싶다는 바람을 입에 올리다니 정말 기쁘구나."], ["Though whether Mr and Mrs Gowan are desirable acquaintances, remains to be determined.’", "다만 가우언 부부가 사귈 만한 사람들인지는 두고 봐야 할 일이지만.\""]], [["‘Mrs Gowan I spoke of, dear.’", "\"제가 말한 건 가우언 부인이에요, 언니.\""]], [["‘No doubt,’ said Fanny.", "\"물론 그렇겠지,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘But you can’t separate her from her husband, I believe, without an Act of Parliament.’", "\"하지만 의회 법령이라도 통과시키지 않는 한 그 여자를 그 남편한테서 떼어 놓을 수는 없을걸.\""]], [["‘Do you think, Papa,’ inquired Little Dorrit, with diffidence and hesitation, ‘there is any objection to my making this visit?’", "\"아버지는 어떻게 생각하세요,\" 리틀 도릿이 조심스럽고도 머뭇거리며 물었다, \"제가 이 방문을 하는 데 무슨 반대가 있으실까요?\""]], [["‘Really,’ he replied, ‘I--ha--what is Mrs General’s view?’", "\"글쎄다,\" 그가 답했다, \"나는—허—제너럴 부인의 견해는 어떠신지요?\""]], [["Mrs General’s view was, that not having the honour of any acquaintance with the lady and gentleman referred to, she was not in a position to varnish the present article.", "제너럴 부인의 견해는, 거론된 그 부인과 신사와 아무런 친분의 영광이 없는 터라, 이번 물건에는 윤을 입혀 드릴 처지가 못 된다는 것이었다."], ["She could only remark, as a general principle observed in the varnishing trade, that much depended on the quarter from which the lady under consideration was accredited to a family so conspicuously niched in the social temple as the family of Dorrit.", "다만 윤내기 업계에서 통하는 일반 원칙으로서, 도릿 가문처럼 사교의 전당에 그토록 두드러진 자리를 차지한 가문에, 문제의 그 부인이 어느 방면으로부터 천거받아 들어오느냐에 많은 것이 달려 있다는 점만은 짚어 둘 수 있다고 했다."]], [["At this remark the face of Mr Dorrit gloomed considerably.", "이 말에 도릿 씨의 얼굴이 적잖이 어두워졌다."], ["He was about (connecting the accrediting with an obtrusive person of the name of Clennam, whom he imperfectly remembered in some former state of existence) to black-ball the name of Gowan finally, when Edward Dorrit, Esquire, came into the conversation, with his glass in his eye, and the preliminary remark of ‘I say--you there!", "그는(이 천거라는 것을, 자기가 어느 전생에서인가 어렴풋이 기억하는 클레넘이라는 이름의 주제넘은 인물과 결부시키면서) 가우언이라는 이름에 끝내 반대표를 던지려던 참이었는데, 마침 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 눈에 외알 안경을 낀 채로 대화에 끼어들었으니, 그 서두는 \"이봐—거기 자네들!"], ["Go out, will you!’--which was addressed to a couple of men who were handing the dishes round, as a courteous intimation that their services could be temporarily dispensed with.", "좀 나가 주겠나!\"라는 말이었다—이는 접시를 돌리고 있던 두 하인을 향한 것으로, 그들의 시중을 잠시 사양하겠다는 정중한 통고였다."]], [["Those menials having obeyed the mandate, Edward Dorrit, Esquire, proceeded.", "그 하인들이 명에 따라 물러가자, 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말을 이었다."]], [["‘Perhaps it’s a matter of policy to let you all know that these Gowans--in whose favour, or at least the gentleman’s, I can’t be supposed to be much prepossessed myself--are known to people of importance, if that makes any difference.’", "\"여러분 모두에게 알려 두는 게 상책일지도 모르겠는데, 이 가우언 부부는—제 자신이 그들에게, 적어도 그 신사 쪽에 그리 호감을 품고 있다고 볼 수는 없습니다만—지체 높은 사람들과 알고 지내는 사이입니다, 그게 무슨 차이라도 만든다면 말이죠.\""]], [["‘That, I would say,’ observed the fair varnisher, ‘Makes the greatest difference.", "\"그렇다면,\" 그 어여쁜 윤내기 장인이 말했다, \"그것은 가장 큰 차이를 만들지요."], ["The connection in question, being really people of importance and consideration--’", "문제의 그 인맥이 정말로 지체 높고 명망 있는 사람들이라면—\""]], [["‘As to that,’ said Edward Dorrit, Esquire, ‘I’ll give you the means of judging for yourself.", "\"그 점이라면,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말했다, \"직접 판단하실 거리를 드리지요."], ["You are acquainted, perhaps, with the famous name of Merdle?’", "혹시 그 유명한 머들이라는 이름을 알고 계십니까?\""]], [["‘The great Merdle!’ exclaimed Mrs General.", "\"위대한 머들 말씀이군요!\" 제너럴 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["‘_The_ Merdle,’ said Edward Dorrit, Esquire.", "\"바로 그 머들입니다,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 말했다."], ["‘They are known to him.", "\"그들은 그와 아는 사이예요."], ["Mrs Gowan--I mean the dowager, my polite friend’s mother--is intimate with Mrs Merdle, and I know these two to be on their visiting list.’", "가우언 부인이—제 말은 그 노부인, 제 정중한 친구의 어머니 말입니다만—머들 부인과 가깝고, 이 두 집안이 서로 왕래하는 사이라는 걸 제가 알고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘If so, a more undeniable guarantee could not be given,’ said Mrs General to Mr Dorrit, raising her gloves and bowing her head, as if she were doing homage to some visible graven image.", "\"그렇다면, 그보다 더 부인할 수 없는 보증은 없겠군요,\" 제너럴 부인이 도릿 씨에게 말하며, 마치 눈에 보이는 어떤 우상에게 경배라도 드리듯 장갑 낀 손을 들어 올리고 고개를 숙였다."]], [["‘I beg to ask my son, from motives of--ah--curiosity,’ Mr Dorrit observed, with a decided change in his manner, ‘how he becomes possessed of this--hum--timely information?’", "\"내—아—호기심에서, 아들에게 묻고 싶구나,\" 도릿 씨가 태도를 확연히 바꾸어 말했다, \"이—흠—때맞은 정보를 어찌 알게 되었는지 말이다.\""]], [["‘It’s not a long story, sir,’ returned Edward Dorrit, Esquire, ‘and you shall have it out of hand.", "\"긴 얘기는 아닙니다, 아버지,\" 에드워드 도릿 도련님이 답했다, \"당장 들려드리지요."], ["To begin with, Mrs Merdle is the lady you had the parley with at what’s-his-name place.’", "우선, 머들 부인은 아버지가 그 뭐라더라 하는 곳에서 담소를 나누셨던 바로 그 부인이에요.\""]], [["‘Martigny,’ interposed Miss Fanny with an air of infinite languor.", "\"마르티니였죠,\" 패니 아가씨가 한없이 나른한 태도로 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Martigny,’ assented her brother, with a slight nod and a slight wink; in acknowledgment of which, Miss Fanny looked surprised, and laughed and reddened.", "\"마르티니,\" 그녀의 오라비가 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이고 슬쩍 눈을 찡긋하며 맞장구쳤다. 그것을 알아챈 패니 아가씨는 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 웃음을 터뜨리며 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["‘How can that be, Edward?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"그게 어찌 그럴 수 있느냐, 에드워드?\""], ["‘You informed me that the name of the gentleman with whom you conferred was--ha--Sparkler.", "도릿 씨가 말했다. \"네가 나에게 알려 주기로는, 네가 이야기를 나눈 그 신사의 이름이—허—스파클러라 했다."], ["Indeed, you showed me his card.", "실은 네가 내게 그의 명함도 보여 주었지."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Sparkler.’", "스파클러라고.\""]], [["‘No doubt of it, father; but it doesn’t follow that his mother’s name must be the same.", "\"틀림없습니다, 아버지. 하지만 그렇다고 그 어머니의 이름까지 같아야 하는 건 아니죠."], ["Mrs Merdle was married before, and he is her son.", "머들 부인은 재혼한 분이고, 그는 그 부인의 아들입니다."], ["She is in Rome now; where probably we shall know more of her, as you decide to winter there.", "그분은 지금 로마에 계세요. 아버지가 그곳에서 겨울을 나기로 정하셨으니, 거기서 아마 그분에 대해 더 알게 되겠지요."], ["Sparkler is just come here.", "스파클러는 막 이곳에 도착했습니다."], ["I passed last evening in company with Sparkler.", "어젯밤을 저는 스파클러와 함께 보냈어요."], ["Sparkler is a very good fellow on the whole, though rather a bore on one subject, in consequence of being tremendously smitten with a certain young lady.’ Here Edward Dorrit, Esquire, eyed Miss Fanny through his glass across the table.", "스파클러는 대체로 아주 괜찮은 친구입니다, 어떤 한 가지 주제에서는 좀 지겨운 구석이 있긴 하지만요, 어떤 젊은 아가씨한테 엄청나게 반해 버린 탓에 말이죠.\" 여기서 에드워드 도릿 도련님은 외알 안경 너머로 식탁 건너편의 패니 아가씨를 바라보았다."], ["‘We happened last night to compare notes about our travels, and I had the information I have given you from Sparkler himself.’ Here he ceased; continuing to eye Miss Fanny through his glass, with a face much twisted, and not ornamentally so, in part by the action of keeping his glass in his eye, and in part by the great subtlety of his smile.", "\"어젯밤에 우리는 마침 여행담을 주고받게 되었고, 제가 여러분께 들려드린 정보는 스파클러 본인에게서 직접 들은 거예요.\" 여기서 그는 말을 멈추었으나, 외알 안경 너머로 계속 패니 아가씨를 바라보았으니, 그의 얼굴은 한편으로는 안경을 눈에 고정해 두느라, 또 한편으로는 그 야릇하기 짝이 없는 미소 탓에, 보기 좋지 않게 잔뜩 일그러져 있었다."]], [["‘Under these circumstances,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I believe I express the sentiments of--ha--Mrs General, no less than my own, when I say that there is no objection, but--ha hum--quite the contrary--to your gratifying your desire, Amy.", "\"이런 사정이라면,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다, \"내가—허—제너럴 부인의 심정도, 나 자신의 심정 못지않게 대변하는 것이라 믿는다만, 네 바람을 들어주는 데 아무 반대가 없다, 아니—허 흠—오히려 정반대다, 에이미."], ["I trust I may--ha--hail--this desire,’ said Mr Dorrit, in an encouraging and forgiving manner, ‘as an auspicious omen.", "나는—허—이 바람을 길조로—\" 도릿 씨가 격려하고 너그러이 용서하는 투로 말했다, \"맞이할 수 있기를 바란다."], ["It is quite right to know these people.", "그런 사람들과 알고 지내는 것은 지극히 옳은 일이다."], ["It is a very proper thing.", "매우 마땅한 일이지."], ["Mr Merdle’s is a name of--ha--world-wide repute.", "머들 씨로 말하면 그 이름이—허—세계적인 명성을 떨치는 분이다."], ["Mr Merdle’s undertakings are immense.", "머들 씨의 사업은 어마어마하지."], ["They bring him in such vast sums of money that they are regarded as--hum--national benefits.", "그 사업들이 그에게 막대한 돈을 벌어다 주어, 사람들이 그것을—흠—국익으로 여길 정도다."], ["Mr Merdle is the man of this time.", "머들 씨야말로 이 시대의 인물이지."], ["The name of Merdle is the name of the age.", "머들이라는 이름은 이 시대의 이름이야."], ["Pray do everything on my behalf that is civil to Mr and Mrs Gowan, for we will--ha--we will certainly notice them.’", "부디 나를 대신해 가우언 부부에게 갖출 예의는 다 갖추어라, 우리가—허—우리가 반드시 그들을 알아줄 테니.\""]], [["This magnificent accordance of Mr Dorrit’s recognition settled the matter.", "도릿 씨가 이토록 거창하게 인정해 주자 그 일은 결판이 났다."], ["It was not observed that Uncle had pushed away his plate, and forgotten his breakfast; but he was not much observed at any time, except by Little Dorrit.", "큰아버지가 접시를 밀쳐 놓고 아침 식사를 잊어버린 것은 아무도 눈여겨보지 못했으나, 그는 어느 때든 리틀 도릿 말고는 누구의 눈길도 거의 받지 못하는 사람이었다."], ["The servants were recalled, and the meal proceeded to its conclusion.", "하인들이 다시 불려 왔고, 식사는 마무리를 향해 진행되었다."], ["Mrs General rose and left the table.", "제너럴 부인이 일어나 식탁을 떴다."], ["Little Dorrit rose and left the table.", "리틀 도릿이 일어나 식탁을 떴다."], ["When Edward and Fanny remained whispering together across it, and when Mr Dorrit remained eating figs and reading a French newspaper, Uncle suddenly fixed the attention of all three by rising out of his chair, striking his hand upon the table, and saying, ‘Brother!", "에드워드와 패니가 식탁을 사이에 두고 소곤대며 남아 있고, 도릿 씨가 무화과를 먹으며 프랑스 신문을 읽고 있을 때, 큰아버지가 느닷없이 의자에서 일어나 식탁을 손으로 내리치며 이렇게 말하여 세 사람의 주의를 한꺼번에 사로잡았다. \"아우야!"], ["I protest against it!’", "나는 이 일에 반대한다!\""]], [["If he had made a proclamation in an unknown tongue, and given up the ghost immediately afterwards, he could not have astounded his audience more.", "설령 그가 알 수 없는 언어로 포고를 내리고는 곧바로 숨을 거두었다 한들, 그 자리의 청중을 이보다 더 경악시키지는 못했을 것이다."], ["The paper fell from Mr Dorrit’s hand, and he sat petrified, with a fig half way to his mouth.", "도릿 씨의 손에서 신문이 떨어졌고, 그는 무화과를 입으로 가져가다 만 채 굳어 버려 그대로 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Brother!’ said the old man, conveying a surprising energy into his trembling voice, ‘I protest against it!", "\"아우야!\" 노인이 떨리는 목소리에 놀라운 기운을 실어 말했다, \"나는 이 일에 반대한다!"], ["I love you; you know I love you dearly.", "나는 너를 사랑한다. 내가 너를 끔찍이 사랑하는 줄은 너도 알 게다."], ["In these many years I have never been untrue to you in a single thought.", "이 오랜 세월 동안 나는 단 한 번도 마음으로조차 네게 등을 돌린 적이 없어."], ["Weak as I am, I would at any time have struck any man who spoke ill of you.", "이렇게 약한 나라도, 너를 헐뜯는 자가 있으면 그게 누구든 언제라도 후려쳤을 게다."], ["But, brother, brother, brother, I protest against it!’", "하지만 아우야, 아우야, 아우야, 나는 이 일에 반대한다!\""]], [["It was extraordinary to see of what a burst of earnestness such a decrepit man was capable.", "그토록 노쇠한 사람이 어떻게 그런 진정의 분출을 해낼 수 있는지 놀라웠다."], ["His eyes became bright, his grey hair rose on his head, markings of purpose on his brow and face which had faded from them for five-and-twenty years, started out again, and there was an energy in his hand that made its action nervous once more.", "그의 두 눈이 형형해지고, 백발이 머리 위에서 곤두서고, 이십오 년 동안 사그라들어 있던 의지의 결기가 그의 이마와 얼굴에 다시 또렷이 솟아났으며, 그 손에는 다시금 동작을 떨리게 만드는 기운이 깃들었다."]], [["‘My dear Frederick!’ exclaimed Mr Dorrit faintly.", "\"내 사랑하는 프레더릭 형님!\" 도릿 씨가 힘없이 외쳤다."], ["‘What is wrong?", "\"무엇이 잘못됐습니까?"], ["What is the matter?’", "무슨 일입니까?\""]], [["‘How dare you,’ said the old man, turning round on Fanny, ‘how dare you do it?", "\"네가 어찌 감히,\" 노인이 패니에게로 홱 돌아서며 말했다, \"어찌 감히 그럴 수 있느냐?"], ["Have you no memory?", "너는 기억이라곤 없느냐?"], ["Have you no heart?’", "너는 마음이라곤 없느냐?\""]], [["‘Uncle?’ cried Fanny, affrighted and bursting into tears, ‘why do you attack me in this cruel manner?", "\"큰아버지?\" 패니가 겁에 질려 울음을 터뜨리며 외쳤다, \"어찌 이리 잔인하게 저를 몰아세우세요?"], ["What have I done?’", "제가 무얼 했다고요?\""]], [["‘Done?’ returned the old man, pointing to her sister’s place, ‘where’s your affectionate invaluable friend?", "\"무얼 했느냐고?\" 노인이 그녀 동생의 자리를 가리키며 답했다, \"네 다정하고 더없이 소중한 벗은 어디 있느냐?"], ["Where’s your devoted guardian?", "네 헌신적인 보호자는 어디 있어?"], ["Where’s your more than mother?", "네게 어미보다 더한 그 아이는 어디 있느냔 말이다?"], ["How dare you set up superiorities against all these characters combined in your sister?", "네 동생 하나에게 한데 모인 그 모든 됨됨이에 맞서 어찌 감히 잘난 체를 한단 말이냐?"], ["For shame, you false girl, for shame!’", "부끄러운 줄 알아라, 이 그릇된 계집애야, 부끄러운 줄 알아!\""]], [["‘I love Amy,’ cried Miss Fanny, sobbing and weeping, ‘as well as I love my life--better than I love my life.", "\"저는 에이미를 사랑해요,\" 패니 아가씨가 흐느껴 울며 외쳤다, \"제 목숨만큼이나—제 목숨보다 더요."], ["I don’t deserve to be so treated.", "저는 이렇게 대접받을 까닭이 없어요."], ["I am as grateful to Amy, and as fond of Amy, as it’s possible for any human being to be.", "저는 에이미에게 고마워하고, 사람이 그럴 수 있는 한껏 에이미를 아껴요. 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠어요."], ["I wish I was dead. I never was so wickedly wronged.", "이토록 부당하게 누명을 쓴 적이 없어요."], ["And only because I am anxious for the family credit.’", "그저 제가 가문의 체면을 염려한 것뿐인데요.\""]], [["‘To the winds with the family credit!’ cried the old man, with great scorn and indignation.", "\"가문의 체면 따위는 바람에 날려 버려라!\" 노인이 크나큰 경멸과 분노로 외쳤다."], ["‘Brother, I protest against pride.", "\"아우야, 나는 자만심에 반대한다."], ["I protest against ingratitude.", "나는 배은망덕에 반대해."], ["I protest against any one of us here who have known what we have known, and have seen what we have seen, setting up any pretension that puts Amy at a moment’s disadvantage, or to the cost of a moment’s pain.", "나는 우리 가운데, 우리가 겪어 본 것을 겪고 우리가 본 것을 본 사람이라면 그 누구든, 에이미를 한순간이라도 불리하게 하거나 한순간의 고통이라도 치르게 하는 그 어떤 잘난 척에도 반대한다."], ["We may know that it’s a base pretension by its having that effect.", "그런 잘난 척이 그런 효과를 낸다는 것만으로도 우리는 그것이 비열한 잘난 척임을 알 수 있어."], ["It ought to bring a judgment on us.", "그것은 마땅히 우리에게 심판을 불러와야 한다."], ["Brother, I protest against it in the sight of God!’", "아우야, 나는 하느님 앞에서 이 일에 반대한다!\""]], [["As his hand went up above his head and came down on the table, it might have been a blacksmith’s.", "그의 손이 머리 위로 올라갔다가 식탁 위로 내리꽂힐 때, 그것은 대장장이의 손이라 해도 좋았을 것이다. 잠깐의 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 그 손은 평소의 힘없는 상태로 풀어져 있었다."], ["After a few moments’ silence, it had relaxed into its usual weak condition.", "그는 평소의 그 끄는 걸음으로 아우에게 다가가, 그 손을 아우의 어깨에 얹고는, 누그러진 목소리로 말했다, \"윌리엄, 아우야, 나는 이 말을 해야만 했다."], ["He went round to his brother with his ordinary shuffling step, put the hand on his shoulder, and said, in a softened voice, ‘William, my dear, I felt obliged to say it; forgive me, for I felt obliged to say it!’ and then went, in his bowed way, out of the palace hall, just as he might have gone out of the Marshalsea room.", "용서해 다오, 나는 이 말을 하지 않을 수 없었어!\" 그러고는 구부정한 모습으로 궁전의 홀을 나섰으니, 꼭 마셜시의 방을 나서던 그 모습 그대로였다."]], [["All this time Fanny had been sobbing and crying, and still continued to do so.", "이 모든 동안 패니는 줄곧 흐느끼며 울고 있었고, 여전히 그러고 있었다."], ["Edward, beyond opening his mouth in amazement, had not opened his lips, and had done nothing but stare.", "에드워드는 놀라움에 입을 벌렸을 뿐 한마디도 떼지 못하고, 멍하니 바라보기만 했다."], ["Mr Dorrit also had been utterly discomfited, and quite unable to assert himself in any way.", "도릿 씨 또한 완전히 당황하여, 어떤 식으로도 위신을 세우지 못했다."], ["Fanny was now the first to speak.", "이제 먼저 입을 연 것은 패니였다."]], [["‘I never, never, never was so used!’ she sobbed.", "\"이렇게, 이렇게, 이렇게 당해 본 적은 한 번도 없어!\""], ["‘There never was anything so harsh and unjustifiable, so disgracefully violent and cruel!", "그녀가 흐느꼈다. \"이토록 모질고 부당한 일은, 이토록 망신스럽게 난폭하고 잔인한 일은 세상에 없었어!"], ["Dear, kind, quiet little Amy, too, what would she feel if she could know that she had been innocently the means of exposing me to such treatment!", "게다가 다정하고 착하고 얌전한 우리 에이미가, 자기가 영문도 모른 채 나를 이런 꼴을 당하게 만든 빌미였다는 걸 알게 되면 어떤 심정이겠어!"], ["But I’ll never tell her!", "하지만 절대 그 애한테는 말 안 할 거야!"], ["No, good darling, I’ll never tell her!’", "그래, 착한 우리 아가, 절대 말하지 않을 거야!\""]], [["This helped Mr Dorrit to break his silence.", "이 말이 도릿 씨가 침묵을 깨는 데 도움이 되었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said he, ‘I--ha--approve of your resolution.", "\"얘야,\" 그가 말했다, \"나는—허—네 결심에 찬성한다."], ["It will be--ha hum--much better not to speak of this to Amy.", "이 일을 에이미에게 말하지 않는 편이—허 흠—훨씬 나을 게다."], ["It might--hum--it might distress her.", "그건—흠—그 아이를 괴롭게 할지도 모른다."], ["Ha.", "허."], ["No doubt it would distress her greatly.", "분명 그 아이를 몹시 괴롭게 할 게다."], ["It is considerate and right to avoid doing so.", "그러지 않는 것이 사려 깊고 옳은 일이지."], ["We will--ha--keep this to ourselves.’", "우리는—허—이 일을 우리끼리만 묻어 두자꾸나.\""]], [["‘But the cruelty of Uncle!’ cried Miss Fanny.", "\"하지만 큰아버지의 그 잔인함이라니!\" 패니 아가씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘O, I never can forgive the wanton cruelty of Uncle!’", "\"오, 저는 큰아버지의 그 까닭 없는 잔인함을 결코 용서할 수 없어요!\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, recovering his tone, though he remained unusually pale, ‘I must request you not to say so.", "\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 평소답지 않게 창백한 낯빛은 여전했으나 어조만은 되찾으며 말했다, \"그런 말은 하지 말아 다오."], ["You must remember that your uncle is--ha--not what he formerly was.", "네 큰아버지는—허—예전의 그분이 아니심을 기억해야 한다."], ["You must remember that your uncle’s state requires--hum--great forbearance from us, great forbearance.’", "네 큰아버지의 상태는—흠—우리에게 큰 인내를, 큰 인내를 요한다는 걸 명심해야 해.\""]], [["‘I am sure,’ cried Fanny, piteously, ‘it is only charitable to suppose that there must be something wrong in him somewhere, or he never could have so attacked Me, of all the people in the world.’", "\"정말이지,\" 패니가 가엾게 외쳤다, \"그분 어딘가에 무언가 잘못된 데가 있다고 너그러이 여기는 수밖에 없겠어요, 안 그러고서야 세상 모든 사람 중에 하필 저를 그렇게 몰아세우셨을 리가 없잖아요.\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit in a deeply fraternal tone, ‘you know, with his innumerable good points, what a--hum--wreck your uncle is; and, I entreat you by the fondness that I have for him, and by the fidelity that you know I have always shown him, to--ha--to draw your own conclusions, and to spare my brotherly feelings.’", "\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 깊은 형제애 어린 어조로 답했다, \"너도 알다시피, 그 무수한 좋은 점에도 불구하고 네 큰아버지가 얼마나—흠—폐인이 되셨는지를. 그러니 내가 그분에게 품은 정으로써, 그리고 내가 그분에게 늘 보여 온 줄을 너도 아는 그 한결같음으로써 간청하건대—허—네 스스로 결론을 내리고, 형제로서의 내 심정을 배려해 다오.\""]], [["This ended the scene; Edward Dorrit, Esquire, saying nothing throughout, but looking, to the last, perplexed and doubtful.", "이로써 그 소동은 끝났다. 에드워드 도릿 도련님은 처음부터 끝까지 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 마지막까지 어리둥절하고 미심쩍은 표정이었다."], ["Miss Fanny awakened much affectionate uneasiness in her sister’s mind that day by passing the greater part of it in violent fits of embracing her, and in alternately giving her brooches, and wishing herself dead.", "패니 아가씨는 그날 하루 대부분을 격렬하게 동생을 끌어안기를 발작처럼 거듭하며, 또 번갈아 가며 동생에게 브로치를 안겨 주기도 하고 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다고 하기도 하며 보냈으니, 그 통에 동생의 마음속에 적잖은 애틋한 불안을 불러일으켰다."]], [["# CHAPTER 6. Something Right Somewhere", "# CHAPTER 6. Something Right Somewhere"]], [["To be in the halting state of Mr Henry Gowan; to have left one of two powers in disgust; to want the necessary qualifications for finding promotion with another, and to be loitering moodily about on neutral ground, cursing both; is to be in a situation unwholesome for the mind, which time is not likely to improve.", "헨리 가우언 씨처럼 어정쩡한 처지에 놓인다는 것 — 두 권력 가운데 하나를 넌더리를 내며 떠나 버리고, 다른 하나에서 출세하는 데 필요한 자격은 갖추지 못한 채, 어느 편에도 속하지 않은 중립지대를 침울하게 어슬렁거리며 양쪽 모두를 저주한다는 것 — 그것은 정신에 해로운 처지이며, 세월이 흐른들 나아질 가망이 없는 처지이다."], ["The worst class of sum worked in the every-day world is cyphered by the diseased arithmeticians who are always in the rule of Subtraction as to the merits and successes of others, and never in Addition as to their own.", "일상의 세계에서 풀리는 가장 나쁜 부류의 셈은, 남의 공적과 성공에 관해서는 언제나 뺄셈의 법칙에 매달리면서 제 것에 관해서는 결코 덧셈을 하지 않는 병든 산술가들에 의해 계산된다."]], [["The habit, too, of seeking some sort of recompense in the discontented boast of being disappointed, is a habit fraught with degeneracy.", "또한 실망했노라는 불평 어린 자랑 속에서 일종의 보상을 구하려는 버릇은 타락이 깃든 버릇이다."], ["A certain idle carelessness and recklessness of consistency soon comes of it.", "이내 게으른 무신경과 일관성에 대한 무책임함이 거기서 자라난다."], ["To bring deserving things down by setting undeserving things up is one of its perverted delights; and there is no playing fast and loose with the truth, in any game, without growing the worse for it.", "받을 자격 없는 것을 치켜세움으로써 받을 자격 있는 것을 끌어내리는 일은 그 비뚤어진 즐거움 가운데 하나다. 그리고 어떤 놀음에서든 진실을 가지고 줄타기를 하면서 그 때문에 더 나빠지지 않는 법은 없다."]], [["In his expressed opinions of all performances in the Art of painting that were completely destitute of merit, Gowan was the most liberal fellow on earth.", "회화 예술의 작품들 가운데 도무지 아무런 가치도 없는 것들에 관해 의견을 밝힐 때, 가우언은 세상에서 가장 너그러운 사람이었다."], ["He would declare such a man to have more power in his little finger (provided he had none), than such another had (provided he had much) in his whole mind and body.", "그는 어떤 작자가 (실은 아무것도 없는데도) 그 새끼손가락 하나에, 다른 어떤 작자가 (실은 많은 것을 지녔는데도) 온 정신과 온몸에 지닌 것보다 더 큰 역량을 지녔다고 단언하곤 했다."], ["If the objection were taken that the thing commended was trash, he would reply, on behalf of his art, ‘My good fellow, what do we all turn out but trash?", "칭찬받은 그 물건이 쓰레기라는 이의가 제기되면, 그는 자신의 예술을 변호하여 이렇게 대답하곤 했다. \"이보게, 우리가 만들어 내는 게 쓰레기 말고 뭐가 있나?"], ["_I_ turn out nothing else, and I make you a present of the confession.’", "나야말로 쓰레기밖에 못 만들어 내지, 그 고백을 자네에게 선물로 주겠네.\""]], [["To make a vaunt of being poor was another of the incidents of his splenetic state, though this may have had the design in it of showing that he ought to be rich; just as he would publicly laud and decry the Barnacles, lest it should be forgotten that he belonged to the family.", "가난을 떠벌리는 것 또한 그 신경질적인 상태에서 비롯된 한 가지 버릇이었는데, 다만 거기에는 자신이 마땅히 부유해야 한다는 점을 드러내려는 의도가 담겨 있었는지도 모른다. 마치 자신이 그 가문에 속해 있다는 사실이 잊히지 않도록 공공연히 배너클 일가를 추켜세웠다가 깎아내리곤 했던 것처럼."], ["Howbeit, these two subjects were very often on his lips; and he managed them so well that he might have praised himself by the month together, and not have made himself out half so important a man as he did by his light disparagement of his claims on anybody’s consideration.", "어쨌든 이 두 가지 화제는 그의 입에 무척 자주 올랐고, 그는 그것을 워낙 잘 다루어서, 몇 달 내내 줄곧 자기 자랑을 늘어놓았다 해도, 남의 호의를 받을 자격이 자기에게 있다는 점을 가볍게 깎아내림으로써 스스로를 내세운 만큼 절반도 중요한 사람으로 만들어 내지는 못했을 것이다."]], [["Out of this same airy talk of his, it always soon came to be understood, wherever he and his wife went, that he had married against the wishes of his exalted relations, and had had much ado to prevail on them to countenance her.", "그가 늘어놓는 바로 이 허황된 이야기에서, 그와 그의 아내가 가는 곳마다 이내 사람들이 이해하게 되는 것이 있었다. 곧 그가 지체 높은 친척들의 뜻을 거슬러 결혼했으며, 그들이 그녀를 인정하도록 설득하느라 무진 애를 먹었다는 것이었다."], ["He never made the representation, on the contrary seemed to laugh the idea to scorn; but it did happen that, with all his pains to depreciate himself, he was always in the superior position.", "그는 결코 그런 말을 입에 올리지 않았고, 도리어 그런 생각을 비웃어 일소에 부치는 듯했다. 하지만 자신을 깎아내리려는 그 온갖 노력에도 불구하고, 그는 언제나 우월한 위치에 있게 되는 것이 사실이었다."], ["From the days of their honeymoon, Minnie Gowan felt sensible of being usually regarded as the wife of a man who had made a descent in marrying her, but whose chivalrous love for her had cancelled that inequality.", "신혼 시절부터 미니 가우언은, 자신이 으레 어떤 남자의 아내로, 곧 그녀와 결혼함으로써 신분을 낮춘 남자이되 그녀를 향한 기사도적인 사랑으로 그 불평등을 상쇄한 남자의 아내로 여겨진다는 것을 느끼고 있었다."]], [["To Venice they had been accompanied by Monsieur Blandois of Paris, and at Venice Monsieur Blandois of Paris was very much in the society of Gowan.", "베네치아까지 그들은 파리의 블랑두아 씨와 동행했고, 베네치아에서 파리의 블랑두아 씨는 가우언과 무척이나 어울려 지냈다."], ["When they had first met this gallant gentleman at Geneva, Gowan had been undecided whether to kick him or encourage him; and had remained for about four-and-twenty hours, so troubled to settle the point to his satisfaction, that he had thought of tossing up a five-franc piece on the terms, ‘Tails, kick; heads, encourage,’ and abiding by the voice of the oracle.", "제네바에서 이 늠름한 신사를 처음 만났을 때, 가우언은 그를 걷어차 버릴지 부추겨 줄지 결정을 내리지 못했다. 그리고 그 점을 만족스럽게 매듭짓느라 어찌나 골머리를 앓았던지 꼬박 스물네 시간 동안 그 상태로 머물렀으며, 결국 \"뒷면이면 걷어차고, 앞면이면 부추긴다\"는 조건으로 오 프랑짜리 동전을 던져 그 신탁의 목소리에 따를까 하는 생각까지 했다."], ["It chanced, however, that his wife expressed a dislike to the engaging Blandois, and that the balance of feeling in the hotel was against him.", "그러나 마침 그의 아내가 그 매력적인 블랑두아를 싫어한다고 말했고, 호텔 안의 감정의 저울추도 그에게 불리하게 기울어 있었다."], ["Upon it, Gowan resolved to encourage him.", "그러자 가우언은 그를 부추기기로 작정했다."]], [["Why this perversity, if it were not in a generous fit?--which it was not.", "이 비뚤어진 처사가 너그러운 변덕에서 나온 것이 아니라면 — 실제로 그렇지 않았으므로 — 대체 무슨 까닭이었을까?"], ["Why should Gowan, very much the superior of Blandois of Paris, and very well able to pull that prepossessing gentleman to pieces and find out the stuff he was made of, take up with such a man?", "파리의 블랑두아보다 훨씬 우월하고, 그 그럴싸한 신사를 산산조각 내어 그가 무엇으로 빚어진 인간인지 능히 알아낼 수 있는 가우언이, 어째서 그런 자와 어울렸을까?"], ["In the first place, he opposed the first separate wish he observed in his wife, because her father had paid his debts and it was desirable to take an early opportunity of asserting his independence.", "첫째로, 그는 아내에게서 처음으로 관찰된 독립된 소망에 반대했는데, 그녀의 아버지가 자신의 빚을 갚아 준 터라 일찌감치 기회를 잡아 자신의 독립성을 내세우는 것이 바람직했기 때문이다."], ["In the second place, he opposed the prevalent feeling, because with many capacities of being otherwise, he was an ill-conditioned man.", "둘째로, 그는 그 우세한 감정에 반대했는데, 달리 될 수 있는 여러 자질을 지녔으면서도 그가 심성이 비뚤어진 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["He found a pleasure in declaring that a courtier with the refined manners of Blandois ought to rise to the greatest distinction in any polished country.", "그는 블랑두아처럼 세련된 예법을 갖춘 정신(廷臣)이라면 어느 교양 있는 나라에서든 마땅히 가장 높은 영예에까지 올라야 한다고 선언하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다."], ["He found a pleasure in setting up Blandois as the type of elegance, and making him a satire upon others who piqued themselves on personal graces.", "그는 블랑두아를 우아함의 전형으로 떠받들고, 그를 빌미로 자기 몸가짐의 기품을 뽐내는 다른 자들을 풍자하는 데서 즐거움을 찾았다."], ["He seriously protested that the bow of Blandois was perfect, that the address of Blandois was irresistible, and that the picturesque ease of Blandois would be cheaply purchased (if it were not a gift, and unpurchasable) for a hundred thousand francs.", "그는 블랑두아의 인사가 완벽하다고, 블랑두아의 말솜씨가 거역할 수 없을 만큼 매혹적이라고, 그리고 블랑두아의 그림 같은 여유로움이라면 (그것이 타고난 천품이어서 값을 매길 수 없는 것이 아니라면) 십만 프랑에 사들여도 싼 값이라고 진지하게 주장했다."], ["That exaggeration in the manner of the man which has been noticed as appertaining to him and to every such man, whatever his original breeding, as certainly as the sun belongs to this system, was acceptable to Gowan as a caricature, which he found it a humorous resource to have at hand for the ridiculing of numbers of people who necessarily did more or less of what Blandois overdid.", "본래의 가정교육이 어떠하든, 태양이 이 태양계에 속하는 것만큼이나 틀림없이 블랑두아와 그런 부류의 모든 사람에게 들러붙어 있다고 앞서 언급된 그 몸짓의 과장됨을, 가우언은 하나의 희화(戲畵)로서 기꺼이 받아들였다. 그는 그것을, 블랑두아가 도를 넘겨 하는 짓을 정도의 차이는 있을지언정 어쩔 수 없이 따라 하는 숱한 사람들을 비웃을 때 곁에 두고 쓸 수 있는, 익살스러운 밑천으로 여겼던 것이다."], ["Thus he had taken up with him; and thus, negligently strengthening these inclinations with habit, and idly deriving some amusement from his talk, he had glided into a way of having him for a companion.", "이렇게 해서 그는 그와 어울리게 되었다. 그리고 이렇게 무심코 이런 성향을 습관으로 굳혀 가며 그의 이야기에서 빈둥빈둥 얼마간 재미를 끌어내다가, 그는 어느새 그를 동무로 삼는 생활로 미끄러져 들어가 있었다."], ["This, though he supposed him to live by his wits at play-tables and the like; though he suspected him to be a coward, while he himself was daring and courageous; though he thoroughly knew him to be disliked by Minnie; and though he cared so little for him, after all, that if he had given her any tangible personal cause to regard him with aversion, he would have had no compunction whatever in flinging him out of the highest window in Venice into the deepest water of the city.", "그는 그가 도박판 따위에서 잔꾀로 먹고산다고 짐작했으면서도, 자신은 대담하고 용감한 반면에 그는 겁쟁이라고 의심했으면서도, 그가 미니에게 미움받고 있음을 익히 알았으면서도, 그리고 결국 그를 워낙 대수롭지 않게 여긴 나머지 만약 그가 아내에게 그를 혐오할 만한 손에 잡히는 개인적 빌미라도 주었더라면 베네치아에서 가장 높은 창문 밖, 도시의 가장 깊은 물속으로 그를 내던지는 데 일말의 거리낌도 없었을 것이면서도, 이렇게 그와 어울렸던 것이다."]], [["Little Dorrit would have been glad to make her visit to Mrs Gowan, alone; but as Fanny, who had not yet recovered from her Uncle’s protest, though it was four-and-twenty hours of age, pressingly offered her company, the two sisters stepped together into one of the gondolas under Mr Dorrit’s window, and, with the courier in attendance, were taken in high state to Mrs Gowan’s lodging.", "리틀 도릿은 가우언 부인을 혼자 찾아뵙고 싶었다. 하지만 패니가 — 비록 그 일이 스물네 시간이나 묵은 것이긴 했어도 아직 삼촌의 항의에서 헤어나지 못한 채 — 함께 가겠다고 끈질기게 나서는 바람에, 두 자매는 도릿 씨의 창문 아래 매여 있던 곤돌라 가운데 하나에 나란히 올라타, 안내인을 거느린 채 한껏 위세를 부리며 가우언 부인의 거처로 실려 갔다."], ["In truth, their state was rather too high for the lodging, which was, as Fanny complained, ‘fearfully out of the way,’ and which took them through a complexity of narrow streets of water, which the same lady disparaged as ‘mere ditches.’", "사실 그들의 위세는 그 거처에 비해 다소 지나치게 거창했다. 패니가 투덜댄 대로 그곳은 \"끔찍하게 외진 곳\"에 있었고, 같은 숙녀가 \"한낱 도랑\"이라고 깎아내린 좁은 물길의 복잡한 미로를 지나야 다다를 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["The house, on a little desert island, looked as if it had broken away from somewhere else, and had floated by chance into its present anchorage in company with a vine almost as much in want of training as the poor wretches who were lying under its leaves. The features of the surrounding picture were, a church with hoarding and scaffolding about it, which had been under suppositious repair so long that the means of repair looked a hundred years old, and had themselves fallen into decay; a quantity of washed linen, spread to dry in the sun; a number of houses at odds with one another and grotesquely out of the perpendicular, like rotten pre-Adamite cheeses cut into fantastic shapes and full of mites; and a feverish bewilderment of windows, with their lattice-blinds all hanging askew, and something draggled and dirty dangling out of most of them.", "작은 무인도 위에 선 그 집은, 어딘가 다른 곳에서 떨어져 나와 우연히 지금의 정박지로 흘러든 것처럼 보였는데, 그 잎사귀 밑에 드러누워 있는 가엾은 자들 못지않게 손질이 필요한 포도덩굴 한 그루를 동무 삼아 흘러온 듯했다. 주위 풍경의 특징을 이루는 것은 다음과 같았다. 둘레에 널빤지 가림막과 비계를 둘러친 교회 하나 — 보수 공사를 한다는 명목으로 어찌나 오래도록 방치되었는지 그 보수의 수단들이 백 년은 묵은 듯 보이고 그 자체가 도리어 썩어 들어가 있었다. 햇볕에 말리려고 널어 둔 수북한 빨래. 서로 어긋나 기괴하게 수직에서 벗어나 기울어진 집들 — 마치 기상천외한 모양으로 잘려 좀벌레가 들끓는, 아담 이전 시대의 썩은 치즈 같았다. 그리고 격자 덧창이 죄다 비뚜름하게 매달려 있고 그 대부분에서 무언가 너저분하고 더러운 것이 늘어뜨려진, 열에 들뜬 듯 어지러운 창문들이었다."]], [["On the first-floor of the house was a Bank--a surprising experience for any gentleman of commercial pursuits bringing laws for all mankind from a British city--where two spare clerks, like dried dragoons, in green velvet caps adorned with golden tassels, stood, bearded, behind a small counter in a small room, containing no other visible objects than an empty iron-safe with the door open, a jug of water, and a papering of garland of roses; but who, on lawful requisition, by merely dipping their hands out of sight, could produce exhaustless mounds of five-franc pieces.", "그 집 이층에는 은행이 있었는데 — 인류 전체에게 통할 법규를 영국의 어느 상업 도시에서 가져온, 장사를 업으로 삼는 신사라면 누구든 놀랄 만한 경험이었다 — 그곳에서는 말라빠진 용기병(龍騎兵) 같은 비쩍 마른 점원 둘이, 금빛 술로 장식된 초록빛 벨벳 모자를 쓰고 수염을 기른 채, 작은 방 안의 작은 계산대 뒤에 서 있었다. 그 방에는 문이 열린 텅 빈 무쇠 금고 하나, 물 주전자 하나, 장미 화환 무늬 벽지 말고는 눈에 띄는 물건이라곤 없었다."], ["Below the Bank was a suite of three or four rooms with barred windows, which had the appearance of a jail for criminal rats.", "그러나 합당한 요구가 있으면 이들은 그저 손을 보이지 않는 곳에 슬쩍 담그기만 해도 오 프랑짜리 동전을 끝없이 무더기로 꺼내 놓을 수 있었다. 은행 아래에는 창살을 친 창문이 달린 서너 칸짜리 방들이 늘어서 있었는데, 마치 범죄자 쥐들을 가두는 감옥 같은 꼴이었다."], ["Above the Bank was Mrs Gowan’s residence.", "은행 위가 가우언 부인의 거처였다."]], [["Notwithstanding that its walls were blotched, as if missionary maps were bursting out of them to impart geographical knowledge; notwithstanding that its weird furniture was forlornly faded and musty, and that the prevailing Venetian odour of bilge water and an ebb tide on a weedy shore was very strong; the place was better within, than it promised.", "벽에는 마치 지리 지식을 전하려는 선교회의 지도가 거기서 터져 나오기라도 한 듯 얼룩이 번져 있었음에도, 기괴한 가구들이 처량하게 빛바래고 곰팡내가 났음에도, 그리고 배 밑바닥 고인 물과 해초 무성한 기슭의 썰물에서 풍기는 베네치아 특유의 냄새가 무척 짙었음에도 불구하고, 그 안은 겉모습이 예고한 것보다는 나았다."], ["The door was opened by a smiling man like a reformed assassin--a temporary servant--who ushered them into the room where Mrs Gowan sat, with the announcement that two beautiful English ladies were come to see the mistress.", "개과천선한 자객 같은 미소 띤 사내가 — 임시로 부리는 하인이었다 — 문을 열어 주며, 아름다운 영국 숙녀 두 분이 마님을 뵈러 오셨다고 알리고는 가우언 부인이 앉아 있는 방으로 그들을 안내했다."]], [["Mrs Gowan, who was engaged in needlework, put her work aside in a covered basket, and rose, a little hurriedly.", "바느질에 매달려 있던 가우언 부인은 일감을 뚜껑 달린 바구니에 치워 두고 다소 황급히 일어섰다."], ["Miss Fanny was excessively courteous to her, and said the usual nothings with the skill of a veteran.", "패니 양은 그녀에게 더없이 정중하게 굴며, 노련한 사람다운 능숙함으로 으레 하는 빈말들을 늘어놓았다."]], [["‘Papa was extremely sorry,’ proceeded Fanny, ‘to be engaged to-day (he is so much engaged here, our acquaintance being so wretchedly large!); and particularly requested me to bring his card for Mr Gowan.", "\"아빠께서는 오늘 일이 있으셔서 무척 안타까워하셨어요.\" 패니가 말을 이었다."], ["That I may be sure to acquit myself of a commission which he impressed upon me at least a dozen times, allow me to relieve my conscience by placing it on the table at once.’", "\"(여기서 어찌나 일이 많으신지 몰라요, 아는 사람이 끔찍하게도 많은 탓이지요!) 그래서 가우언 씨께 당신의 명함을 꼭 전해 드리라고 제게 각별히 당부하셨답니다. 아빠께서 적어도 열두 번은 다짐을 두신 그 심부름을 틀림없이 해내려면, 어디 양심의 짐을 덜 겸 지금 당장 이 탁자 위에 놓아 두는 걸 허락해 주세요.\""]], [["Which she did with veteran ease.", "그러고는 노련한 사람다운 여유로움으로 그렇게 했다."]], [["‘We have been,’ said Fanny, ‘charmed to understand that you know the Merdles.", "\"저희는,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["We hope it may be another means of bringing us together.’", "\"댁에서 머들 부부와 아는 사이라는 걸 알고 무척 반가웠어요. 그게 저희를 또 한번 가깝게 이어 줄 인연이 되었으면 해요.\""]], [["‘They are friends,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘of Mr Gowan’s family.", "\"그분들은,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["I have not yet had the pleasure of a personal introduction to Mrs Merdle, but I suppose I shall be presented to her at Rome.’", "\"가우언 씨 집안의 친구들이세요. 저는 아직 머들 부인과 직접 인사를 나누는 기쁨은 누리지 못했지만, 로마에 가면 그분께 소개받게 될 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘Indeed?’ returned Fanny, with an appearance of amiably quenching her own superiority.", "\"그러세요?\" 패니가, 자기 쪽의 우월함을 상냥하게 누그러뜨리는 듯한 기색으로 대답했다."], ["‘I think you’ll like her.’", "\"마음에 드실 거예요.\""]], [["‘You know her very well?’", "\"그분을 아주 잘 아시나요?\""]], [["‘Why, you see,’ said Fanny, with a frank action of her pretty shoulders, ‘in London one knows every one.", "\"글쎄요, 그러니까,\" 패니가 예쁜 어깨를 솔직하게 한번 으쓱이며 말했다."], ["We met her on our way here, and, to say the truth, papa was at first rather cross with her for taking one of the rooms that our people had ordered for us.", "\"런던에서는 누구나 다 알고 지내거든요. 저희는 이리로 오는 길에 그분을 만났는데, 솔직히 말하자면 아빠께서는 처음엔 그분이 저희 일행이 잡아 둔 방 하나를 차지하셨다고 좀 언짢아하셨어요."], ["However, of course, that soon blew over, and we were all good friends again.’", "하지만 물론 그것도 곧 풀려서, 다시 다들 좋은 친구가 되었지요.\""]], [["Although the visit had as yet given Little Dorrit no opportunity of conversing with Mrs Gowan, there was a silent understanding between them, which did as well.", "이 방문이 아직 리틀 도릿에게 가우언 부인과 이야기를 나눌 기회를 주지는 못했으나, 두 사람 사이에는 말없는 이해가 흘렀고, 그것으로도 충분했다. 리틀 도릿은 날카롭고도 식지 않는 관심으로 가우언 부인을 바라보았다."], ["She looked at Mrs Gowan with keen and unabated interest; the sound of her voice was thrilling to her; nothing that was near her, or about her, or at all concerned her, escaped Little Dorrit.", "그녀의 목소리는 리틀 도릿의 가슴을 떨리게 했다. 그녀 곁에 있는 것, 그녀와 관련된 것, 그녀에게 조금이라도 얽힌 것은 무엇 하나 리틀 도릿의 눈을 벗어나지 못했다."], ["She was quicker to perceive the slightest matter here, than in any other case--but one.", "그녀는 다른 어떤 경우에서보다 — 단 하나의 경우만을 빼고는 — 여기서 가장 사소한 일까지도 더 재빨리 알아챘다."]], [["‘You have been quite well,’ she now said, ‘since that night?’", "\"그날 밤 이후로 줄곧 잘 지내셨나요?\" 이제 그녀가 물었다."]], [["‘Quite, my dear.", "\"아주 잘 지냈어요, 얘."], ["And you?’", "당신은요?\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아!"], ["I am always well,’ said Little Dorrit, timidly.", "저는 늘 건강해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 수줍게 말했다."], ["‘I--yes, thank you.’", "\"저는 — 네, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["There was no reason for her faltering and breaking off, other than that Mrs Gowan had touched her hand in speaking to her, and their looks had met.", "그녀가 말을 더듬다 끊은 데에는, 가우언 부인이 말을 건네며 그녀의 손을 스쳤고 두 사람의 눈길이 마주쳤다는 것 말고는 아무 까닭도 없었다."], ["Something thoughtfully apprehensive in the large, soft eyes, had checked Little Dorrit in an instant.", "그 크고 부드러운 눈동자에 어린 무언가 생각에 잠긴 듯한 불안이 리틀 도릿을 한순간에 멈칫하게 했던 것이다."]], [["‘You don’t know that you are a favourite of my husband’s, and that I am almost bound to be jealous of you?’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"당신이 제 남편이 아끼는 사람이라는 걸, 그래서 제가 당신을 거의 질투하지 않을 수 없다는 걸 모르시죠?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."]], [["Little Dorrit, blushing, shook her head.", "리틀 도릿은 얼굴을 붉히며 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["‘He will tell you, if he tells you what he tells me, that you are quieter and quicker of resource than any one he ever saw.’", "\"그이가 저한테 하는 말을 당신한테도 한다면, 당신더러 자기가 여태껏 본 누구보다도 차분하고 임기응변에 빠른 사람이라고 할 거예요.\""]], [["‘He speaks far too well of me,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"제 칭찬을 너무 지나치게 하시네요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘I doubt that; but I don’t at all doubt that I must tell him you are here.", "\"그건 잘 모르겠지만, 당신이 여기 와 있다고 그이에게 알려야 한다는 건 조금도 의심하지 않아요."], ["I should never be forgiven, if I were to let you--and Miss Dorrit--go, without doing so. May I?", "그러지 않고 당신과 — 도릿 양을 — 그냥 보내 드린다면 결코 용서받지 못할 거예요."], ["You can excuse the disorder and discomfort of a painter’s studio?’", "그래도 될까요? 화가의 화실이 어지럽고 누추하더라도 양해해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["The inquiries were addressed to Miss Fanny, who graciously replied that she would be beyond anything interested and enchanted.", "이 물음들은 패니 양을 향한 것이었는데, 패니는 무엇보다도 흥미롭고 황홀할 거라고 우아하게 대답했다. 가우언 부인은 어느 문으로 가서 그 너머를 들여다보고는 돌아왔다."], ["Mrs Gowan went to a door, looked in beyond it, and came back.", "\"헨리한테 들어와 달라고 청해도 되겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘Do Henry the favour to come in,’ said she, ‘I knew he would be pleased!’", "\"그이가 좋아할 줄 알았어요!\""]], [["The first object that confronted Little Dorrit, entering first, was Blandois of Paris in a great cloak and a furtive slouched hat, standing on a throne platform in a corner, as he had stood on the Great Saint Bernard, when the warning arms seemed to be all pointing up at him.", "먼저 들어선 리틀 도릿과 맨 처음 맞닥뜨린 것은, 커다란 망토를 두르고 음험하게 푹 눌러쓴 모자를 쓴 채 한구석의 높은 단(壇) 위에 서 있는 파리의 블랑두아였다. 그것은 그가 그랑 생베르나르에서, 경고하듯 뻗은 표지판의 팔들이 죄다 그를 가리키는 듯하던 그곳에 서 있던 모습 그대로였다."], ["She recoiled from this figure, as it smiled at her.", "그 형상이 그녀를 향해 미소를 짓자, 그녀는 흠칫 물러섰다."]], [["‘Don’t be alarmed,’ said Gowan, coming from his easel behind the door.", "\"놀라지 마십시오.\" 문 뒤 화가(畵架)에서 나오며 가우언이 말했다."], ["‘It’s only Blandois.", "\"블랑두아일 뿐입니다."], ["He is doing duty as a model to-day.", "오늘은 모델 노릇을 하고 있어요."], ["I am making a study of him.", "그를 습작하는 중이지요."], ["It saves me money to turn him to some use.", "그를 어디든 써먹으면 돈이 절약되니까요."], ["We poor painters have none to spare.’", "우리 가난한 화가들은 남아도는 돈이 없거든요.\""]], [["Blandois of Paris pulled off his slouched hat, and saluted the ladies without coming out of his corner.", "파리의 블랑두아는 푹 눌러쓴 모자를 벗어, 제 구석에서 나오지는 않은 채 숙녀들에게 인사했다."]], [["‘A thousand pardons!’ said he.", "\"천만번 용서를 빕니다!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘But the Professore here is so inexorable with me, that I am afraid to stir.’", "\"하지만 여기 이 화백님께서 어찌나 저를 가차 없이 다루시는지, 움직이기가 겁이 나서요.\""]], [["‘Don’t stir, then,’ said Gowan coolly, as the sisters approached the easel.", "\"그럼 움직이지 마.\" 자매가 화가에게 다가서자 가우언이 태연하게 말했다."], ["‘Let the ladies at least see the original of the daub, that they may know what it’s meant for.", "\"이 숙녀분들께서 적어도 이 졸작의 원본은 보셔야 무엇을 그린 건지 아실 게 아닌가."], ["There he stands, you see.", "자, 보십시오, 저기 그가 서 있습니다."], ["A bravo waiting for his prey, a distinguished noble waiting to save his country, the common enemy waiting to do somebody a bad turn, an angelic messenger waiting to do somebody a good turn--whatever you think he looks most like!’", "제물을 노리는 자객, 조국을 구하려고 기다리는 고매한 귀족, 누군가에게 못된 짓을 하려고 도사린 만인의 적, 누군가에게 좋은 일을 베풀려고 기다리는 천사의 사자(使者) — 무엇이든 여러분이 보시기에 그와 가장 닮았다 싶은 것이지요!\""]], [["‘Say, Professore Mio, a poor gentleman waiting to do homage to elegance and beauty,’ remarked Blandois.", "\"이렇게 말씀하시지요, 화백님, 우아함과 아름다움에 경의를 표하려고 기다리는 가난한 신사라고요.\" 블랑두아가 한마디 했다."]], [["‘Or say, Cattivo Soggetto Mio,’ returned Gowan, touching the painted face with his brush in the part where the real face had moved, ‘a murderer after the fact.", "\"아니면 이렇게 말하든가, 못된 인간 양반,\" 실제 얼굴이 움직인 부분에 붓을 대며 가우언이 응수했다."], ["Show that white hand of yours, Blandois.", "\"범행을 저지른 뒤의 살인자라고. 그 흰 손을 보여 봐, 블랑두아."], ["Put it outside the cloak.", "망토 밖으로 내놔."], ["Keep it still.’", "가만히 둬.\""]], [["Blandois’ hand was unsteady; but he laughed, and that would naturally shake it.", "블랑두아의 손은 떨렸다. 하지만 그가 웃었으니, 그 손이 떨리는 것도 당연했다."]], [["‘He was formerly in some scuffle with another murderer, or with a victim, you observe,’ said Gowan, putting in the markings of the hand with a quick, impatient, unskilful touch, ‘and these are the tokens of it. Outside the cloak, man!--Corpo di San Marco, what are you thinking of?’", "\"이자는 예전에 다른 살인자, 혹은 어떤 희생자와 한바탕 격투를 벌인 적이 있는 모양입니다.\" 빠르고 성마르며 서투른 붓질로 손의 흔적을 그려 넣으며 가우언이 말했다. \"이게 그 자국들이지요. 망토 밖으로 내라니까, 이 사람아! — 성 마르코의 몸에 맹세코, 대체 무슨 생각을 하는 거야?\""]], [["Blandois of Paris shook with a laugh again, so that his hand shook more; now he raised it to twist his moustache, which had a damp appearance; and now he stood in the required position, with a little new swagger.", "파리의 블랑두아가 다시 웃음으로 몸을 흔들어 대는 바람에 그의 손은 더욱 떨렸다. 이번에는 그 손을 들어 올려, 축축해 보이는 콧수염을 비비 꼬았다. 그러고는 새삼스레 조금 거들먹거리며 요구받은 자세로 섰다."]], [["His face was so directed in reference to the spot where Little Dorrit stood by the easel, that throughout he looked at her.", "그의 얼굴은 리틀 도릿이 화가 곁에 서 있는 자리를 향하도록 놓여 있어, 그는 내내 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Once attracted by his peculiar eyes, she could not remove her own, and they had looked at each other all the time.", "한번 그의 기묘한 두 눈에 사로잡히자 그녀는 자신의 눈을 거둘 수가 없었고, 그래서 두 사람은 줄곧 서로를 마주 보고 있었다. 그녀가 이제 몸을 떨었다."], ["She trembled now; Gowan, feeling it, and supposing her to be alarmed by the large dog beside him, whose head she caressed in her hand, and who had just uttered a low growl, glanced at her to say, ‘He won’t hurt you, Miss Dorrit.’", "가우언은 그것을 느끼고는, 그녀가 손으로 머리를 쓰다듬어 주고 있던, 곁에 있던 커다란 개가 방금 낮게 으르렁댄 탓에 그녀가 놀란 줄로 짐작하고, 그녀를 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"이 녀석은 해치지 않습니다, 도릿 양.\""]], [["‘I am not afraid of him,’ she returned in the same breath; ‘but will you look at him?’", "\"이 개가 무섭진 않아요.\" 그녀가 같은 숨결로 대답했다. \"그런데 이 개를 좀 봐 주시겠어요?\""]], [["In a moment Gowan had thrown down his brush, and seized the dog with both hands by the collar.", "순식간에 가우언은 붓을 내던지고 두 손으로 개의 목걸이를 움켜잡았다."]], [["‘Blandois!", "\"블랑두아!"], ["How can you be such a fool as to provoke him!", "어쩌자고 이 녀석을 약 올리는 그런 바보짓을 하나!"], ["By Heaven, and the other place too, he’ll tear you to bits!", "맹세코, 그리고 지옥에 두고서도 맹세코, 이 녀석이 자넬 갈가리 찢어 놓을 걸세!"], ["Lie down!", "엎드려!"], ["Lion!", "라이언!"], ["Do you hear my voice, you rebel!’", "내 목소리가 안 들리느냐, 이 반항하는 놈아!\""]], [["The great dog, regardless of being half-choked by his collar, was obdurately pulling with his dead weight against his master, resolved to get across the room.", "그 커다란 개는 목걸이에 반쯤 목이 졸리는 것도 아랑곳없이, 죽은 듯한 제 무게를 실어 주인에게 한사코 버티며, 기어이 방을 가로질러 가겠다는 듯이 끌어 댔다."], ["He had been crouching for a spring at the moment when his master caught him.", "주인이 붙잡은 그 순간에 녀석은 뛰어오르려고 잔뜩 웅크리고 있던 참이었다."]], [["‘Lion!", "\"라이언!"], ["Lion!’ He was up on his hind legs, and it was a wrestle between master and dog.", "라이언!\" 녀석이 뒷다리로 곧추서니, 주인과 개의 씨름이 벌어졌다."], ["‘Get back!", "\"물러서!"], ["Down, Lion!", "엎드려, 라이언!"], ["Get out of his sight, Blandois!", "이 녀석 눈에 안 띄게 비키게, 블랑두아!"], ["What devil have you conjured into the dog?’", "대체 이 개한테 무슨 악마를 불러들인 건가?\""]], [["‘I have done nothing to him.’", "\"난 녀석한테 아무 짓도 안 했소.\""]], [["‘Get out of his sight or I can’t hold the wild beast!", "\"이 녀석 눈에 안 띄게 비키게, 안 그러면 이 사나운 짐승을 붙들 수가 없네!"], ["Get out of the room!", "방에서 나가게!"], ["By my soul, he’ll kill you!’", "내 영혼에 걸고, 녀석이 자넬 죽이고 말 걸세!\""]], [["The dog, with a ferocious bark, made one other struggle as Blandois vanished; then, in the moment of the dog’s submission, the master, little less angry than the dog, felled him with a blow on the head, and standing over him, struck him many times severely with the heel of his boot, so that his mouth was presently bloody.", "블랑두아가 사라지자 개는 사나운 짖음과 함께 다시 한 번 발버둥을 쳤다. 그러다 개가 굴복하는 순간, 개 못지않게 화가 난 주인은 머리를 한 대 후려쳐 녀석을 쓰러뜨리고는, 그 위에 우뚝 서서 부츠 뒤축으로 여러 차례 모질게 내리쳤다. 그래서 이내 녀석의 주둥이에서 피가 났다."]], [["‘Now get you into that corner and lie down,’ said Gowan, ‘or I’ll take you out and shoot you.’", "\"자, 저 구석으로 가서 엎드려 있어.\" 가우언이 말했다. \"안 그러면 끌고 나가 쏘아 죽일 테다.\""]], [["Lion did as he was ordered, and lay down licking his mouth and chest.", "라이언은 명령대로 따라, 엎드려서 제 주둥이와 가슴을 핥았다."], ["Lion’s master stopped for a moment to take breath, and then, recovering his usual coolness of manner, turned to speak to his frightened wife and her visitors.", "라이언의 주인은 잠시 숨을 고르려고 멈추었다가, 평소의 냉정한 태도를 되찾고는, 겁에 질린 아내와 그 방문객들에게 말을 건네려 돌아섰다."], ["Probably the whole occurrence had not occupied two minutes.", "아마 이 모든 소동은 채 이 분도 걸리지 않았을 것이다."]], [["‘Come, come, Minnie!", "\"자자, 미니!"], ["You know he is always good-humoured and tractable.", "이 녀석이 언제나 온순하고 말 잘 듣는다는 걸 당신도 알잖소."], ["Blandois must have irritated him,--made faces at him.", "블랑두아가 녀석을 자극한 게 분명해 — 얼굴을 찡그려 약을 올렸겠지."], ["The dog has his likings and dislikings, and Blandois is no great favourite of his; but I am sure you will give him a character, Minnie, for never having been like this before.’", "이 개도 좋고 싫음이 있는데, 블랑두아는 녀석이 그리 좋아하는 상대가 아니거든. 하지만 미니, 당신은 분명 이 녀석이 전엔 한 번도 이런 적이 없었다고 증언해 줄 거요.\""]], [["Minnie was too much disturbed to say anything connected in reply; Little Dorrit was already occupied in soothing her; Fanny, who had cried out twice or thrice, held Gowan’s arm for protection; Lion, deeply ashamed of having caused them this alarm, came trailing himself along the ground to the feet of his mistress.", "미니는 너무 동요한 나머지 조리 있는 대답을 하지 못했다. 리틀 도릿은 벌써 그녀를 달래는 데 마음을 쏟고 있었다. 두세 번 비명을 질렀던 패니는 보호를 받으려는 듯 가우언의 팔을 붙들고 있었다. 라이언은 이런 소동을 일으킨 것이 몹시 부끄럽다는 듯 몸을 바닥에 질질 끌며 여주인의 발치로 다가왔다."]], [["‘You furious brute,’ said Gowan, striking him with his foot again.", "\"이 사나운 짐승 같으니.\" 가우언이 다시 발로 녀석을 차며 말했다."], ["‘You shall do penance for this.’ And he struck him again, and yet again.", "\"이 일을 두고 벌을 받아야 해.\" 그러고는 또 한 번, 그리고 또 한 번 녀석을 찼다."]], [["‘O, pray don’t punish him any more,’ cried Little Dorrit.", "\"오, 제발 더는 벌하지 마세요.\" 리틀 도릿이 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t hurt him.", "\"이 녀석을 아프게 하지 마세요. 보세요, 얼마나 얌전한지!\""], ["See how gentle he is!’ At her entreaty, Gowan spared him; and he deserved her intercession, for truly he was as submissive, and as sorry, and as wretched as a dog could be.", "그녀의 간청에 가우언은 녀석을 봐주었다. 그리고 녀석은 그녀의 두둔을 받을 만했으니, 정말이지 개가 그럴 수 있는 한 더없이 순종적이고, 더없이 미안해하며, 더없이 비참한 꼴이었다."]], [["It was not easy to recover this shock and make the visit unrestrained, even though Fanny had not been, under the best of circumstances, the least trifle in the way.", "설령 패니가 아무리 좋은 상황에서라도 손톱만큼도 거치적거리지 않았다 한들, 이 충격에서 벗어나 거리낌 없이 방문을 이어 가기란 쉽지 않았을 것이다."], ["In such further communication as passed among them before the sisters took their departure, Little Dorrit fancied it was revealed to her that Mr Gowan treated his wife, even in his very fondness, too much like a beautiful child.", "자매가 떠나기 전에 그들 사이에 오간 더 이상의 대화에서, 리틀 도릿은 가우언 씨가 아내를 — 그토록 다정한 순간에조차 — 너무 지나치게 예쁜 아이 대하듯 한다는 것을 깨닫게 된 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["He seemed so unsuspicious of the depths of feeling which she knew must lie below that surface, that she doubted if there could be any such depths in himself.", "그는 그 겉모습 아래 틀림없이 깊은 감정이 자리하고 있을 것이라는 점을 도무지 짐작조차 못 하는 것 같았다. 그래서 그녀는 그 자신에게 과연 그런 깊이가 있을까 의심스러웠다."], ["She wondered whether his want of earnestness might be the natural result of his want of such qualities, and whether it was with people as with ships, that, in too shallow and rocky waters, their anchors had no hold, and they drifted anywhere.", "그의 진지함의 결여가 그런 자질의 결여에서 비롯된 자연스러운 결과는 아닐까, 그리고 사람도 배와 같아서 너무 얕고 바위투성이인 물에서는 닻이 걸릴 데가 없어 정처 없이 떠돌게 되는 것은 아닐까 하고 그녀는 생각했다."]], [["He attended them down the staircase, jocosely apologising for the poor quarters to which such poor fellows as himself were limited, and remarking that when the high and mighty Barnacles, his relatives, who would be dreadfully ashamed of them, presented him with better, he would live in better to oblige them.", "그는 자매를 계단 아래까지 배웅하며, 자기 같은 가난뱅이에게나 어울리는 누추한 거처라고 익살스레 변명을 늘어놓았다. 그러고는, 자기를 몹시 부끄럽게 여기는 그 잘나고 거만한 친척 배너클 일가가 더 나은 거처를 마련해 준다면, 그들의 비위를 맞추느라 더 나은 곳에서 살아 주겠노라고 덧붙였다."], ["At the water’s edge they were saluted by Blandois, who looked white enough after his late adventure, but who made very light of it notwithstanding,--laughing at the mention of Lion.", "물가에서 그들은 블랑두아의 인사를 받았는데, 그는 방금 겪은 모험 탓에 얼굴이 제법 창백했으나 그럼에도 그 일을 무척 대수롭지 않게 여기며 — 라이언 이야기가 나오자 웃어넘겼다."]], [["Leaving the two together under the scrap of vine upon the causeway, Gowan idly scattering the leaves from it into the water, and Blandois lighting a cigarette, the sisters were paddled away in state as they had come.", "둑길 위 한 조각 포도덩굴 아래 두 사람을 — 가우언은 덩굴에서 잎사귀를 따 무심히 물 위에 흩뿌리고 있었고 블랑두아는 담배에 불을 붙이고 있었다 — 남겨 둔 채, 자매는 올 때처럼 위세를 부리며 노 저어 떠나갔다."], ["They had not glided on for many minutes, when Little Dorrit became aware that Fanny was more showy in manner than the occasion appeared to require, and, looking about for the cause through the window and through the open door, saw another gondola evidently in waiting on them.", "미끄러져 나아간 지 몇 분 되지 않아, 리틀 도릿은 패니가 상황이 요구하는 것보다 더 요란하게 굴고 있음을 알아챘다. 그래서 그 까닭을 찾아 창문 너머로, 또 열린 문 너머로 둘러보다가, 또 다른 곤돌라 한 척이 분명 그들을 따라붙고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["As this gondola attended their progress in various artful ways; sometimes shooting on a-head, and stopping to let them pass; sometimes, when the way was broad enough, skimming along side by side with them; and sometimes following close astern; and as Fanny gradually made no disguise that she was playing off graces upon somebody within it, of whom she at the same time feigned to be unconscious; Little Dorrit at length asked who it was?", "이 곤돌라가 갖은 교묘한 방식으로 그들의 행로를 따라붙는 동안 — 때로는 앞으로 쏜살같이 나아갔다가 그들을 지나가도록 멈추어 서고, 때로는 물길이 넉넉히 넓으면 나란히 미끄러져 가고, 또 때로는 바짝 뒤를 따르고 — 그리고 패니가 그 안에 탄 누군가를 향해 우아한 자태를 부리고 있으면서도 그 사실을 모르는 체하는 것을 차츰 숨기지 않게 되자, 리틀 도릿은 마침내 그가 누구냐고 물었다."]], [["To which Fanny made the short answer, ‘That gaby.’", "이에 패니는 짤막하게 대답했다. \"저 멍청이 말이야.\""]], [["‘Who?’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"누구?\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘My dear child,’ returned Fanny (in a tone suggesting that before her Uncle’s protest she might have said, You little fool, instead), ‘how slow you are!", "\"얘야,\" 패니가 (삼촌의 항의가 있기 전이었다면 '이 바보 같은 것아'라고 했을 법한 어조로) 대꾸했다."], ["Young Sparkler.’", "\"넌 어쩜 그리 굼뜨니! 젊은 스파클러 말이야.\""]], [["She lowered the window on her side, and, leaning back and resting her elbow on it negligently, fanned herself with a rich Spanish fan of black and gold.", "그녀는 제 쪽 창문을 내리고, 몸을 뒤로 기대 팔꿈치를 무심히 창턱에 올려놓은 채, 검은빛과 금빛이 어우러진 화려한 스페인 부채로 부채질을 했다."], ["The attendant gondola, having skimmed forward again, with some swift trace of an eye in the window, Fanny laughed coquettishly and said, ‘Did you ever see such a fool, my love?’", "뒤따르던 곤돌라가, 창 안에서 재빠르게 한 줄기 눈길을 흘리며 다시 앞으로 미끄러져 나아가자, 패니는 교태롭게 웃으며 말했다. \"얘, 저렇게 바보 같은 사람 본 적 있니?\""]], [["‘Do you think he means to follow you all the way?’ asked Little Dorrit.", "\"저 사람이 끝까지 언니를 따라올 작정일까?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다."]], [["‘My precious child,’ returned Fanny, ‘I can’t possibly answer for what an idiot in a state of desperation may do, but I should think it highly probable.", "\"내 귀여운 것아,\" 패니가 대꾸했다."], ["It’s not such an enormous distance.", "\"절망에 빠진 멍청이가 무슨 짓을 할지야 내가 도무지 장담할 수 없지만, 그럴 가능성이 아주 높다고 봐."], ["All Venice would scarcely be that, I imagine, if he’s dying for a glimpse of me.’", "그렇게 엄청난 거리도 아니잖니. 베네치아를 통틀어도 그만한 거리는 거의 안 될 거야, 저 사람이 날 한 번 보겠다고 죽기 살기로 매달린다면 말이지.\""]], [["‘And is he?’ asked Little Dorrit in perfect simplicity.", "\"정말 그런가요?\" 리틀 도릿이 더없이 순진하게 물었다."]], [["‘Well, my love, that really is an awkward question for me to answer,’ said her sister.", "\"글쎄, 얘, 그건 정말이지 내가 대답하기 곤란한 질문이구나.\""], ["‘I believe he is.", "그녀의 언니가 말했다."], ["You had better ask Edward.", "\"그런 것 같긴 해."], ["He tells Edward he is, I believe.", "에드워드한테 물어보는 게 낫겠다."], ["I understand he makes a perfect spectacle of himself at the Casino, and that sort of places, by going on about me.", "저 사람이 에드워드한테는 그렇다고 말하는 모양이야, 아마. 듣자 하니 카지노 같은 데서 내 얘기를 늘어놓으며 아주 꼴불견을 한다더라."], ["But you had better ask Edward if you want to know.’", "하지만 알고 싶으면 에드워드한테 물어보는 게 나아.\""]], [["‘I wonder he doesn’t call,’ said Little Dorrit after thinking a moment.", "\"그런데 왜 찾아오지 않을까요?\" 리틀 도릿이 잠시 생각하다 말했다."]], [["‘My dear Amy, your wonder will soon cease, if I am rightly informed.", "\"얘, 에이미, 내가 제대로 들은 거라면 네 궁금증은 곧 풀릴 거야."], ["I should not be at all surprised if he called to-day.", "저 사람이 오늘 찾아온대도 난 조금도 놀라지 않을걸."], ["The creature has only been waiting to get his courage up, I suspect.’", "아무래도 저 작자는 그저 용기를 그러모으느라 여태 기다린 것 같아.\""]], [["‘Will you see him?’", "\"만나 줄 거예요?\""]], [["‘Indeed, my darling,’ said Fanny, ‘that’s just as it may happen.", "\"글쎄다, 얘,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["Here he is again.", "\"그건 그때 가 봐야 알지."], ["Look at him.", "저기 또 오는구나."], ["O, you simpleton!’", "좀 봐. 오, 이 얼간이 같으니!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler had, undeniably, a weak appearance; with his eye in the window like a knot in the glass, and no reason on earth for stopping his bark suddenly, except the real reason.", "스파클러 씨는, 부인할 수 없이, 모자라 보이는 행색이었다. 유리에 박힌 옹이처럼 창에 눈을 댄 채, 갑자기 제 배(舟)를 멈춰 세울 이유라곤 세상에 그 진짜 이유 말고는 도무지 없었으니 말이다."]], [["‘When you asked me if I will see him, my dear,’ said Fanny, almost as well composed in the graceful indifference of her attitude as Mrs Merdle herself, ‘what do you mean?’", "\"내가 그를 만나 줄 거냐고 물었는데, 얘,\" 패니가, 그 우아하게 무심한 자태에서만큼은 머들 부인 자신에 거의 못지않게 침착한 태도로 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻이니?\""]], [["‘I mean,’ said Little Dorrit--‘I think I rather mean what do you mean, dear Fanny?’", "\"제 말은,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"언니야말로 무슨 뜻인지 묻고 싶었다는 거예요, 패니 언니.\""]], [["Fanny laughed again, in a manner at once condescending, arch, and affable; and said, putting her arm round her sister in a playfully affectionate way:", "패니는 다시, 짐짓 너그러우면서도 짓궂고 상냥한 태도로 웃었다. 그러고는 장난스레 다정하게 동생의 허리에 팔을 두르며 말했다."]], [["‘Now tell me, my little pet.", "\"자, 내 귀여운 동생아, 말해 보렴."], ["When we saw that woman at Martigny, how did you think she carried it off?", "우리가 마르티니에서 그 여자를 봤을 때, 그 여자가 어떻게 그 일을 받아넘기던지 넌 어떻게 봤니?"], ["Did you see what she decided on in a moment?’\n‘No, Fanny.’", "그 여자가 한순간에 무얼 작정했는지 봤니?\""]], [["‘Then I’ll tell you, Amy.", "\"아니요, 패니 언니.\" \"그럼 내가 말해 주마, 에이미."], ["She settled with herself, now I’ll never refer to that meeting under such different circumstances, and I’ll never pretend to have any idea that these are the same girls.", "그 여자는 속으로 이렇게 정한 거야. '이렇게 처지가 다른데 그때 만난 일은 결코 입에 올리지 않겠어, 그리고 이 애들이 그때 그 애들이라는 건 결코 아는 척하지 않겠어.'"], ["That’s _her_ way out of a difficulty.", "그게 그 여자가 곤경을 벗어나는 방식이지."], ["What did I tell you when we came away from Harley Street that time?", "우리가 그때 할리 가에서 나오면서 내가 너한테 뭐라고 했었니?"], ["She is as insolent and false as any woman in the world.", "그 여자는 세상 어느 여자 못지않게 오만하고 거짓되다고."], ["But in the first capacity, my love, she may find people who can match her.’", "하지만 그 첫 번째 자질에 관해서라면, 얘야, 그 여자도 자기 맞수가 될 사람을 만나게 될 거야.\""]], [["A significant turn of the Spanish fan towards Fanny’s bosom, indicated with great expression where one of these people was to be found.", "스페인 부채를 패니의 가슴 쪽으로 의미심장하게 돌린 것이, 그런 사람 가운데 하나가 어디서 발견될지를 자못 또렷하게 가리켜 보였다."]], [["‘Not only that,’ pursued Fanny, ‘but she gives the same charge to Young Sparkler; and doesn’t let him come after me until she has got it thoroughly into his most ridiculous of all ridiculous noddles (for one really can’t call it a head), that he is to pretend to have been first struck with me in that Inn Yard.’", "\"그뿐 아니라,\" 패니가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자는 젊은 스파클러한테도 똑같은 지시를 내려서는, 그자의 그 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없는 머리통 속에 (정말이지 그걸 머리라고 부를 수도 없지만) 그자가 그 여관 마당에서 처음으로 내게 반한 척해야 한다는 걸 단단히 박아 넣기 전까지는 날 따라오지 못하게 한 거야.\""]], [["‘Why?’ asked Little Dorrit.", "\"왜요?\" 리틀 도릿이 물었다."]], [["‘Why?", "\"왜라니?"], ["Good gracious, my love!’ (again very much in the tone of You stupid little creature) ‘how can you ask?", "어머나, 얘!\" (또다시 '이 멍청한 것아'"], ["Don’t you see that I may have become a rather desirable match for a noddle?", "하는 어조로) \"어떻게 그런 걸 묻니? 내가 그 머리통한테 꽤 탐나는 혼처가 되었을지도 모른다는 걸 모르겠어?"], ["And don’t you see that she puts the deception upon us, and makes a pretence, while she shifts it from her own shoulders (very good shoulders they are too, I must say),’ observed Miss Fanny, glancing complacently at herself, ‘of considering our feelings?’", "그리고 그 여자가 자기 어깨에서 (정말이지 무척 고운 어깨이긴 하지만) 그 짐을 떨쳐 내면서, 우리 기분을 헤아려 주는 척 시늉을 하며 우리한테 그 속임수를 떠넘긴다는 걸 모르겠니?\" 패니 양이 흐뭇하게 제 모습을 흘긋 보며 말했다."]], [["‘But we can always go back to the plain truth.’", "\"하지만 우리는 언제든 있는 그대로의 진실로 돌아갈 수 있잖아요.\""]], [["‘Yes, but if you please we won’t,’ retorted Fanny.", "\"그래, 하지만 미안하지만 우린 그러지 않을 거야.\" 패니가 받아쳤다."], ["‘No; I am not going to have that done, Amy.", "\"안 돼. 난 그렇게 되도록 두지 않을 거야, 에이미."], ["The pretext is none of mine; it’s hers, and she shall have enough of it.’", "그 핑계는 내 게 아니라 그 여자 거니까, 그 여자가 실컷 그 맛을 봐야지.\""]], [["In the triumphant exaltation of her feelings, Miss Fanny, using her Spanish fan with one hand, squeezed her sister’s waist with the other, as if she were crushing Mrs Merdle.", "의기양양하게 들뜬 기분에 패니 양은 한 손으로는 스페인 부채를 부치면서, 다른 손으로는 마치 머들 부인을 짓이기기라도 하는 듯 동생의 허리를 꽉 끌어안았다."]], [["‘No,’ repeated Fanny.", "\"안 돼.\" 패니가 되풀이했다."], ["‘She shall find me go her way.", "\"그 여자는 내가 자기 방식대로 나가는 걸 보게 될 거야."], ["She took it, and I’ll follow it.", "그 여자가 택한 길이니, 나도 그걸 따라가겠어."], ["And, with the blessing of fate and fortune, I’ll go on improving that woman’s acquaintance until I have given her maid, before her eyes, things from my dressmaker’s ten times as handsome and expensive as she once gave me from hers!’", "그리고 운명과 행운의 가호가 있다면, 난 그 여자와의 교분을 계속 다져 나가서, 마침내 그 여자가 보는 앞에서, 언젠가 그 여자가 자기 양재사한테서 받은 걸 내게 준 것보다 열 배는 더 곱고 값비싼 물건들을 내 양재사한테서 받아 그 여자 하녀한테 줄 거야!\""]], [["Little Dorrit was silent; sensible that she was not to be heard on any question affecting the family dignity, and unwilling to lose to no purpose her sister’s newly and unexpectedly restored favour.", "리틀 도릿은 잠자코 있었다. 가문의 체면에 관한 어떤 문제에서도 자기 말은 받아들여지지 않으리라는 것을 알았고, 새로이, 뜻밖에 되찾은 언니의 호의를 공연히 잃고 싶지 않았기 때문이다."], ["She could not concur, but she was silent.", "그녀는 동의할 수 없었으나, 잠자코 있었다."], ["Fanny well knew what she was thinking of; so well, that she soon asked her.", "패니는 동생이 무슨 생각을 하는지 잘 알았다. 어찌나 잘 알았던지 이내 동생에게 물어보았다."]], [["Her reply was, ‘Do you mean to encourage Mr Sparkler, Fanny?’", "동생의 대답은 이러했다. \"언니, 스파클러 씨를 부추길 작정이에요?\""]], [["‘Encourage him, my dear?’ said her sister, smiling contemptuously, ‘that depends upon what you call encourage.", "\"그를 부추긴다고, 얘?\" 언니가 경멸하듯 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["No, I don’t mean to encourage him.", "\"그야 네가 부추긴다는 걸 어떻게 보느냐에 달렸지. 아니, 난 그를 부추길 작정은 없어."], ["But I’ll make a slave of him.’", "하지만 그를 내 종으로 만들어 버리겠어.\""]], [["Little Dorrit glanced seriously and doubtfully in her face, but Fanny was not to be so brought to a check.", "리틀 도릿이 진지하고도 미심쩍은 눈으로 언니의 얼굴을 흘긋 보았으나, 패니는 그쯤으로 멈출 사람이 아니었다."], ["She furled her fan of black and gold, and used it to tap her sister’s nose; with the air of a proud beauty and a great spirit, who toyed with and playfully instructed a homely companion.", "그녀는 검은빛과 금빛 부채를 접어, 그것으로 동생의 코를 톡톡 두드렸다. 마치 도도한 미인이자 기개 넘치는 여인이, 수수한 동무를 가지고 놀며 장난스레 가르치기라도 하는 듯한 태도였다."]], [["‘I shall make him fetch and carry, my dear, and I shall make him subject to me.", "\"난 그가 이리저리 심부름을 하게 만들 거야, 얘, 그리고 그를 내게 복종하게 만들 거야."], ["And if I don’t make his mother subject to me, too, it shall not be my fault.’", "그리고 그의 어머니까지 내게 복종하게 만들지 못한다면, 그건 내 탓이 아닐 테고.\""]], [["‘Do you think--dear Fanny, don’t be offended, we are so comfortable together now--that you can quite see the end of that course?’", "\"패니 언니 — 부디 언짢아하진 마요, 우린 지금 이렇게 사이좋게 지내고 있으니까 — 그 길의 끝이 어떻게 될지 언니는 다 내다볼 수 있다고 생각해요?\""]], [["‘I can’t say I have so much as looked for it yet, my dear,’ answered Fanny, with supreme indifference; ‘all in good time.", "\"아직 그것까지 내다본 적은 없다고 해야겠구나, 얘.\" 패니가 더없이 무심하게 대답했다."], ["Such are my intentions.", "\"다 때가 되면 알게 되겠지."], ["And really they have taken me so long to develop, that here we are at home.", "내 작정은 그렇다는 거야. 그런데 정말이지 이 얘기를 풀어놓느라 어찌나 오래 걸렸는지, 어느새 집에 다 왔네."], ["And Young Sparkler at the door, inquiring who is within.", "그리고 젊은 스파클러가 문간에서 안에 누가 있느냐고 묻고 있고."], ["By the merest accident, of course!’", "물론 순전히 우연으로 말이지!\""]], [["In effect, the swain was standing up in his gondola, card-case in hand, affecting to put the question to a servant.", "과연 그 구애자는 명함갑을 손에 든 채 곤돌라 안에 일어서서, 어느 하인에게 그 질문을 하는 시늉을 하고 있었다. 이런 정황이 한데 어우러져, 그는 곧이어 젊은 숙녀들 앞에 어떤 자세로 나타나게 되었는데, 옛날 같았으면 그의 구애에 길조로 여겨지지는 않았을 자세였다."], ["This conjunction of circumstances led to his immediately afterwards presenting himself before the young ladies in a posture, which in ancient times would not have been considered one of favourable augury for his suit; since the gondoliers of the young ladies, having been put to some inconvenience by the chase, so neatly brought their own boat in the gentlest collision with the bark of Mr Sparkler, as to tip that gentleman over like a larger species of ninepin, and cause him to exhibit the soles of his shoes to the object of his dearest wishes: while the nobler portions of his anatomy struggled at the bottom of his boat in the arms of one of his men.", "추격에 다소 애를 먹은 젊은 숙녀들의 곤돌라 사공들이, 자기네 배를 어찌나 깔끔하게 몰아 스파클러 씨의 배에 더없이 부드럽게 부딪쳐 놓았는지, 그 신사를 큼직한 볼링 핀처럼 발랑 자빠뜨려, 사모해 마지않는 대상에게 제 구둣발 밑창을 드러내 보이게 만들었던 것이다. 한편 그의 더 고귀한 부위들은 하인 가운데 하나의 팔에 안긴 채 배 바닥에서 허우적거리고 있었다."]], [["However, as Miss Fanny called out with much concern, Was the gentleman hurt, Mr Sparkler rose more restored than might have been expected, and stammered for himself with blushes, ‘Not at all so.’ Miss Fanny had no recollection of having ever seen him before, and was passing on, with a distant inclination of her head, when he announced himself by name.", "그러나 패니 양이 자못 걱정스러운 목소리로 그 신사가 다치지는 않았느냐고 외치자, 스파클러 씨는 예상보다 멀쩡한 모습으로 몸을 일으켜, 얼굴을 붉히며 더듬더듬 제 입으로 \"조금도 안 다쳤습니다\"라고 말했다. 패니 양은 전에 그를 본 기억이 도무지 없는 듯, 멀찍이 고개만 까딱 숙이고 지나치려다가, 그가 제 이름을 밝히자 멈추었다."], ["Even then she was in a difficulty from being unable to call it to mind, until he explained that he had had the honour of seeing her at Martigny. Then she remembered him, and hoped his lady-mother was well.", "그러고도 그녀는 그 이름을 떠올리지 못해 난처해하다가, 그가 마르티니에서 그녀를 뵙는 영광을 누렸노라고 설명하자 비로소 그를 기억해 냈고, 그의 어머님께서 안녕하시냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Thank you,’ stammered Mr Sparkler, ‘she’s uncommonly well--at least, poorly.’", "\"고맙습니다,\" 스파클러 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"어머니는 유난히 잘 지내십니다 — 적어도, 편찮으시지요.\""]], [["‘In Venice?’ said Miss Fanny.", "\"베네치아에 계신가요?\" 패니 양이 말했다."]], [["‘In Rome,’ Mr Sparkler answered.", "\"로마에 계십니다.\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘I am here by myself, myself.", "\"저는 여기 혼자, 저 혼자 와 있습니다."], ["I came to call upon Mr Edward Dorrit myself.", "에드워드 도릿 씨를 뵈러 제가 직접 찾아온 겁니다."], ["Indeed, upon Mr Dorrit likewise.", "사실, 도릿 씨도 마찬가지로요."], ["In fact, upon the family.’", "요컨대, 온 가족을 뵈러요.\""]], [["Turning graciously to the attendants, Miss Fanny inquired whether her papa or brother was within?", "패니 양은 우아하게 시중꾼들을 돌아보며, 자기 아빠나 오라버니가 안에 계시냐고 물었다."], ["The reply being that they were both within, Mr Sparkler humbly offered his arm.", "두 분 다 안에 계신다는 대답이 돌아오자, 스파클러 씨가 공손히 제 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Miss Fanny accepting it, was squired up the great staircase by Mr Sparkler, who, if he still believed (which there is not any reason to doubt) that she had no nonsense about her, rather deceived himself.", "패니 양은 그것을 받아들여, 스파클러 씨의 시중을 받으며 그 큰 계단을 올라갔다. 스파클러 씨는 그녀에게 허튼 구석이라곤 없다고 (그가 그렇게 여겼음을 의심할 까닭은 전혀 없다) 여전히 믿고 있었다면, 자기 자신을 적잖이 속이고 있는 셈이었다."]], [["Arrived in a mouldering reception-room, where the faded hangings, of a sad sea-green, had worn and withered until they looked as if they might have claimed kindred with the waifs of seaweed drifting under the windows, or clinging to the walls and weeping for their imprisoned relations, Miss Fanny despatched emissaries for her father and brother.", "빛바랜 휘장이 — 서글픈 바다 빛 초록색의 — 어찌나 닳고 시들었던지 창문 아래로 떠도는 부유물 같은 해초붙이와 친척뻘이라고 주장하거나, 혹은 벽에 들러붙어 갇혀 있는 친척들을 위해 흐느끼는 듯이 보이는, 그 곰팡이 핀 응접실에 다다르자, 패니 양은 사람을 보내 아버지와 오라버니를 모셔 오게 했다."], ["Pending whose appearance, she showed to great advantage on a sofa, completing Mr Sparkler’s conquest with some remarks upon Dante--known to that gentleman as an eccentric man in the nature of an Old File, who used to put leaves round his head, and sit upon a stool for some unaccountable purpose, outside the cathedral at Florence.", "그들이 나타나기를 기다리는 동안, 그녀는 소파에 앉아 한껏 자태를 뽐내며, 단테에 관한 몇 마디로 스파클러 씨의 함락을 완성했다. 그 신사가 알기로 단테란, 머리에 잎사귀를 둘러 쓰고 알 수 없는 까닭으로 피렌체 대성당 밖 걸상에 앉아 있곤 했다는, 골동품 같은 부류의 괴짜였다."]], [["Mr Dorrit welcomed the visitor with the highest urbanity, and most courtly manners.", "도릿 씨는 더없이 정중하고 가장 고상한 예법으로 손님을 맞이했다."], ["He inquired particularly after Mrs Merdle.", "그는 머들 부인의 안부를 각별히 물었다."], ["He inquired particularly after Mr Merdle.", "머들 씨의 안부도 각별히 물었다."], ["Mr Sparkler said, or rather twitched out of himself in small pieces by the shirt-collar, that Mrs Merdle having completely used up her place in the country, and also her house at Brighton, and being, of course, unable, don’t you see, to remain in London when there wasn’t a soul there, and not feeling herself this year quite up to visiting about at people’s places, had resolved to have a touch at Rome, where a woman like herself, with a proverbially fine appearance, and with no nonsense about her, couldn’t fail to be a great acquisition.", "스파클러 씨는, 아니 차라리 셔츠 깃을 잡아당기며 자기 자신을 잘게 토막 내듯 띄엄띄엄 말하기를, 머들 부인이 시골 별장에서 지낼 만큼 지냈고 브라이턴 저택에서도 지낼 만큼 지냈는데, 보시다시피 런던에 사람이라곤 한 명도 없을 때 거기 머물 수가 없는 데다, 올해는 이 사람 저 사람 별장을 돌아다닐 만큼 기력도 그리 좋지 않으셔서, 로마에 한번 가 보기로 작정하셨다고 했다. 자기 어머니 같은 여인은, 익히 소문난 고운 자태에 허튼 구석이라곤 없으니, 로마에서 틀림없이 큰 보탬이 될 것이 분명하다는 것이었다."], ["As to Mr Merdle, he was so much wanted by the men in the City and the rest of those places, and was such a doosed extraordinary phenomenon in Buying and Banking and that, that Mr Sparkler doubted if the monetary system of the country would be able to spare him; though that his work was occasionally one too many for him, and that he would be all the better for a temporary shy at an entirely new scene and climate, Mr Sparkler did not conceal.", "머들 씨로 말하자면, 그분은 시티며 그 밖의 여러 곳의 사람들이 어찌나 찾는지, 또 매입이며 은행업이며 그런 일에 어찌나 엄청나게 비범한 현상이신지, 스파클러 씨가 의심하기로는 이 나라의 화폐 제도가 그분 없이 굴러갈 수 있을지 모를 지경이라고 했다. 다만 그분의 일이 이따금 그분에게 벅찰 때가 있어, 전혀 새로운 풍광과 기후로 잠시 바람을 쐬는 편이 그분께 두루 이로울 것이라는 점만큼은 스파클러 씨도 숨기지 않았다."], ["As to himself, Mr Sparkler conveyed to the Dorrit family that he was going, on rather particular business, wherever they were going.", "정작 자기 자신으로 말하자면, 스파클러 씨는 도릿 일가가 어디로 가든 자기도 다소 특별한 용무로 그리로 갈 작정이라는 뜻을 전했다."]], [["This immense conversational achievement required time, but was effected.", "이 어마어마한 대화의 위업에는 시간이 필요했으나, 어쨌든 해냈다."], ["Being effected, Mr Dorrit expressed his hope that Mr Sparkler would shortly dine with them.", "그것이 이루어지자 도릿 씨는 스파클러 씨가 조만간 함께 만찬을 들기를 바란다고 했다."], ["Mr Sparkler received the idea so kindly that Mr Dorrit asked what he was going to do that day, for instance?", "스파클러 씨가 그 제안을 어찌나 반갑게 받아들였던지, 도릿 씨는 이를테면 오늘은 무엇을 할 작정이냐고 물었다."], ["As he was going to do nothing that day (his usual occupation, and one for which he was particularly qualified), he was secured without postponement; being further bound over to accompany the ladies to the Opera in the evening.", "그날 그는 아무것도 할 일이 없었으므로 (그것은 그의 평소 일과였고, 그가 특히 적격인 일이었다), 그는 미룰 것도 없이 붙들렸다. 게다가 저녁에는 숙녀들을 오페라에 모시고 가기로 단단히 약속까지 받게 되었다."]], [["At dinner-time Mr Sparkler rose out of the sea, like Venus’s son taking after his mother, and made a splendid appearance ascending the great staircase.", "만찬 시각이 되자 스파클러 씨는, 어머니를 빼닮은 비너스의 아들인 양 바다에서 솟아올라, 그 큰 계단을 오르며 훌륭한 모습을 선보였다."], ["If Fanny had been charming in the morning, she was now thrice charming, very becomingly dressed in her most suitable colours, and with an air of negligence upon her that doubled Mr Sparkler’s fetters, and riveted them.", "아침에 패니가 매혹적이었다면, 이제 그녀는 세 곱절 매혹적이었다. 더없이 잘 어울리는 색으로 가장 알맞게 차려입고, 거기에 무심한 듯한 분위기까지 두르고 있어 스파클러 씨의 족쇄를 두 배로 채워 단단히 박아 넣었던 것이다."]], [["‘I hear you are acquainted, Mr Sparkler,’ said his host at dinner, ‘with--ha--Mr Gowan.", "\"스파클러 씨, 듣자 하니 — 하 — 가우언 씨와 아는 사이라더군요. 헨리 가우언 씨 말이오.\""], ["Mr Henry Gowan?’", "만찬 자리에서 주인이 말했다."]], [["‘Perfectly, sir,’ returned Mr Sparkler.", "\"잘 알지요, 어르신.\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘His mother and my mother are cronies in fact.’", "\"사실 그의 어머니와 제 어머니가 막역한 사이거든요.\""]], [["‘If I had thought of it, Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a patronage as magnificent as that of Lord Decimus himself, ‘you should have despatched a note to them, asking them to dine to-day.", "\"내가 그 생각을 했더라면, 에이미,\" 데시머스 경 자신 못지않게 거창한 생색을 내며 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["Some of our people could have--ha--fetched them, and taken them home.", "\"그분들께 오늘 만찬에 오시라는 쪽지를 보내라고 너에게 일렀을 텐데. 우리 사람들 가운데 누가 — 하 — 그분들을 모셔 오고, 또 댁까지 모셔다 드릴 수도 있었을 게다."], ["We could have spared a--hum--gondola for that purpose.", "그 일에 — 흠 — 곤돌라 한 척쯤은 내줄 수 있었을 테고."], ["I am sorry to have forgotten this.", "이걸 잊은 게 유감이구나."], ["Pray remind me of them to-morrow.’", "부디 내일 그분들을 일깨워 다오.\""]], [["Little Dorrit was not without doubts how Mr Henry Gowan might take their patronage; but she promised not to fail in the reminder.", "리틀 도릿은 헨리 가우언 씨가 그들의 생색을 어떻게 받아들일지 의심스럽지 않은 것은 아니었으나, 잊지 않고 일깨우겠다고 약속했다."]], [["‘Pray, does Mr Henry Gowan paint--ha--Portraits?’ inquired Mr Dorrit.", "\"한데 헨리 가우언 씨는 — 하 — 초상화를 그리오?\" 도릿 씨가 물었다."]], [["Mr Sparkler opined that he painted anything, if he could get the job.", "스파클러 씨는, 일감만 얻을 수 있다면 그는 무엇이든 그린다고 보았다."]], [["‘He has no particular walk?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"특정한 분야는 없소?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["Mr Sparkler, stimulated by Love to brilliancy, replied that for a particular walk a man ought to have a particular pair of shoes; as, for example, shooting, shooting-shoes; cricket, cricket-shoes.", "사랑에 자극받아 재기가 번득인 스파클러 씨는, 특정한 분야를 가지려면 사람은 특정한 신발 한 켤레가 있어야 한다고 대답했다. 이를테면 사냥에는 사냥 신발, 크리켓에는 크리켓 신발 하는 식으로 말이다."], ["Whereas, he believed that Henry Gowan had no particular pair of shoes.", "그런데 그가 알기로 헨리 가우언에게는 특정한 신발 한 켤레가 없다는 것이었다."]], [["‘No speciality?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"전문 분야가 없소?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["This being a very long word for Mr Sparkler, and his mind being exhausted by his late effort, he replied, ‘No, thank you.", "이것은 스파클러 씨에게 무척 긴 단어인 데다 방금 전의 분발로 머리가 바닥난 터라, 그는 이렇게 대답했다. \"아뇨, 괜찮습니다."], ["I seldom take it.’", "저는 그건 좀처럼 안 합니다.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"음!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It would be very agreeable to me to present a gentleman so connected, with some--ha--Testimonial of my desire to further his interests, and develop the--hum--germs of his genius.", "\"그토록 좋은 연줄을 둔 신사께, 그의 이익을 도모하고 그의 — 흠 — 천재성의 싹을 틔워 주고픈 내 마음의 — 하 — 증표가 될 무언가를 선사할 수 있다면 무척 기쁘겠소."], ["I think I must engage Mr Gowan to paint my picture.", "아무래도 내 초상을 그리도록 가우언 씨에게 맡겨야겠소."], ["If the result should be--ha--mutually satisfactory, I might afterwards engage him to try his hand upon my family.’", "그 결과가 — 하 — 서로 만족스럽다면, 그 뒤엔 내 가족에게도 그의 솜씨를 한번 발휘해 보라고 맡길 수도 있겠지.\""]], [["The exquisitely bold and original thought presented itself to Mr Sparkler, that there was an opening here for saying there were some of the family (emphasising ‘some’ in a marked manner) to whom no painter could render justice.", "스파클러 씨에게는, 가족 가운데 어떤 화가도 제대로 그려 낼 수 없는 분들이 ('어떤'에 자못 힘을 주어) 더러 있다고 말할 틈이 여기 있다는, 더없이 대담하고 독창적인 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["But, for want of a form of words in which to express the idea, it returned to the skies.", "그러나 그 생각을 담아낼 말의 형태가 없어, 그것은 다시 하늘로 돌아가 버렸다."]], [["This was the more to be regretted as Miss Fanny greatly applauded the notion of the portrait, and urged her papa to act upon it.", "그것이 더욱 안타까운 까닭은, 패니 양이 그 초상화라는 발상에 크게 박수를 보내며 아빠에게 어서 실행하라고 부추겼기 때문이다."], ["She surmised, she said, that Mr Gowan had lost better and higher opportunities by marrying his pretty wife; and Love in a cottage, painting pictures for dinner, was so delightfully interesting, that she begged her papa to give him the commission whether he could paint a likeness or not: though indeed both she and Amy knew he could, from having seen a speaking likeness on his easel that day, and having had the opportunity of comparing it with the original.", "그녀가 짐작하기로 가우언 씨는 그 예쁜 아내와 결혼함으로써 더 낫고 더 높은 기회들을 잃었으며, 끼니를 위해 그림을 그리는 오두막 속 사랑이란 너무도 황홀하게 흥미로운 것이라, 그가 초상을 그릴 줄 알든 모르든 아빠께서 그에게 일을 맡겨 주십사 청한다고 했다. 하기야 그녀도 에이미도, 그날 그의 화가 위에서 살아 있는 듯한 초상을 보고 또 그것을 원본과 견주어 볼 기회를 가진 터라, 그가 그릴 줄 안다는 것은 알고 있었지만 말이다."], ["These remarks made Mr Sparkler (as perhaps they were intended to do) nearly distracted; for while on the one hand they expressed Miss Fanny’s susceptibility of the tender passion, she herself showed such an innocent unconsciousness of his admiration that his eyes goggled in his head with jealousy of an unknown rival.", "이런 말들은 (어쩌면 그러려고 의도된 것이었을 테지만) 스파클러 씨를 거의 미칠 지경으로 만들었다. 한편으로 그 말들은 패니 양이 애틋한 연정에 마음이 끌리기 쉽다는 것을 드러내면서도, 정작 그녀 자신은 그의 흠모를 그토록 천진하게 모르는 체하는 바람에, 그는 알지 못할 연적에 대한 질투로 두 눈이 부릅떠진 채 튀어나올 지경이었던 것이다."]], [["Descending into the sea again after dinner, and ascending out of it at the Opera staircase, preceded by one of their gondoliers, like an attendant Merman, with a great linen lantern, they entered their box, and Mr Sparkler entered on an evening of agony.", "만찬 뒤에 다시 바다로 내려갔다가 오페라 극장 계단에서 다시 솟아오른 그들은, 사공 가운데 하나가 시중드는 인어처럼 커다란 천 등롱을 들고 앞장선 가운데 자기네 칸막이 좌석으로 들어섰고, 스파클러 씨는 고뇌의 저녁으로 들어섰다."], ["The theatre being dark, and the box light, several visitors lounged in during the representation; in whom Fanny was so interested, and in conversation with whom she fell into such charming attitudes, as she had little confidences with them, and little disputes concerning the identity of people in distant boxes, that the wretched Sparkler hated all mankind.", "극장 안은 어둡고 좌석은 환했으므로, 공연 도중에 여러 방문객이 어슬렁어슬렁 들어왔다. 패니는 그들에게 어찌나 관심을 보이고, 또 그들과 이야기를 나누며 어찌나 매혹적인 자태를 짓던지 — 그들과 은밀한 속닥임을 주고받고, 먼 좌석에 있는 사람들이 누구인가를 두고 자잘한 입씨름을 벌이는 동안 — 가엾은 스파클러는 온 인류를 증오하게 되었다."], ["But he had two consolations at the close of the performance.", "그러나 공연이 끝날 무렵 그에게는 두 가지 위안이 있었다."], ["She gave him her fan to hold while she adjusted her cloak, and it was his blessed privilege to give her his arm down-stairs again.", "그녀가 망토를 여미는 동안 자기 부채를 그에게 들고 있게 한 것, 그리고 다시 계단을 내려가며 그에게 자기 팔을 잡게 해 준 그 복된 특권이었다."], ["These crumbs of encouragement, Mr Sparkler thought, would just keep him going; and it is not impossible that Miss Dorrit thought so too.", "이 격려의 부스러기들이면 그럭저럭 버틸 수 있겠다고 스파클러 씨는 생각했고, 도릿 양 역시 그렇게 생각했을 가능성도 없지 않았다."]], [["The Merman with his light was ready at the box-door, and other Mermen with other lights were ready at many of the doors.", "등불을 든 인어가 좌석 문간에 대기하고 있었고, 다른 등불을 든 다른 인어들도 여러 문간에 대기하고 있었다."], ["The Dorrit Merman held his lantern low, to show the steps, and Mr Sparkler put on another heavy set of fetters over his former set, as he watched her radiant feet twinkling down the stairs beside him.", "도릿네 인어는 계단을 비추려고 등롱을 낮게 들었고, 스파클러 씨는 그녀의 빛나는 발이 제 곁에서 계단을 깡총깡총 내려가는 것을 바라보며, 먼젓번 족쇄 위에 또 한 벌의 묵직한 족쇄를 덧채웠다."], ["Among the loiterers here, was Blandois of Paris.", "이곳에서 어슬렁거리던 자들 가운데 파리의 블랑두아가 있었다."], ["He spoke, and moved forward beside Fanny.", "그는 말을 건네며 패니 곁으로 다가왔다."]], [["Little Dorrit was in front with her brother and Mrs General (Mr Dorrit had remained at home), but on the brink of the quay they all came together.", "리틀 도릿은 오라버니, 제너럴 부인과 함께 앞쪽에 있었으나 (도릿 씨는 집에 남아 있었다), 부두 가장자리에서 그들 모두가 한데 모이게 되었다."], ["She started again to find Blandois close to her, handing Fanny into the boat.", "그녀는 블랑두아가 패니를 배에 태워 주며 바로 자기 곁에 와 있는 것을 보고 다시 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["‘Gowan has had a loss,’ he said, ‘since he was made happy to-day by a visit from fair ladies.’", "\"가우언이 오늘 고운 숙녀분들의 방문으로 기뻐했었는데, 그 뒤에 그만 한 가지 잃은 게 있답니다.\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘A loss?’ repeated Fanny, relinquished by the bereaved Sparkler, and taking her seat.", "\"잃다니요?\" 사별한 듯 풀이 죽은 스파클러에게서 풀려나 자리에 앉으며 패니가 되물었다."]], [["‘A loss,’ said Blandois.", "\"잃었지요.\" 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["‘His dog Lion.’", "\"그의 개 라이언 말입니다.\""]], [["Little Dorrit’s hand was in his, as he spoke.", "그가 그렇게 말할 때, 리틀 도릿의 손은 그의 손 안에 있었다."]], [["‘He is dead,’ said Blandois.", "\"녀석이 죽었습니다.\" 블랑두아가 말했다."]], [["‘Dead?’ echoed Little Dorrit.", "\"죽었다고요?\" 리틀 도릿이 따라 말했다."], ["‘That noble dog?’", "\"그 의젓한 개가요?\""]], [["‘Faith, dear ladies!’ said Blandois, smiling and shrugging his shoulders, ‘somebody has poisoned that noble dog.", "\"이런, 고운 숙녀분들!\" 블랑두아가 미소 지으며 어깨를 으쓱하고 말했다."], ["He is as dead as the Doges!’", "\"누군가가 그 의젓한 개를 독살했지 뭡니까. 도제(總督)들만큼이나 죽어 버렸답니다!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism", "# CHAPTER 7. Mostly, Prunes and Prism"]], [["Mrs General, always on her coach-box keeping the proprieties well together, took pains to form a surface on her very dear young friend, and Mrs General’s very dear young friend tried hard to receive it.", "제너럴 부인은 언제나 자기 마부석에 앉아 예의범절을 잘 단속하면서, 그 매우 사랑하는 젊은 벗에게 매끄러운 겉면을 입히려 애를 썼고, 제너럴 부인의 그 매우 사랑하는 젊은 벗은 그것을 받아들이려 무던히 애를 썼다."], ["Hard as she had tried in her laborious life to attain many ends, she had never tried harder than she did now, to be varnished by Mrs General.", "고된 인생을 살며 수많은 목표를 이루려 애써 왔지만, 그녀는 제너럴 부인의 손에 윤이 발리도록 하는 이 일만큼 열심히 애써 본 적이 없었다."], ["It made her anxious and ill at ease to be operated upon by that smoothing hand, it is true; but she submitted herself to the family want in its greatness as she had submitted herself to the family want in its littleness, and yielded to her own inclinations in this thing no more than she had yielded to her hunger itself, in the days when she had saved her dinner that her father might have his supper.", "그 매끄럽게 다듬는 손에 다루어지는 것이 그녀를 불안하고 거북하게 만든 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 그녀는 식구들의 자잘한 요구에 자신을 내맡겨 왔듯이, 식구들의 큰 요구에도 그 큰 그대로 자신을 내맡겼고, 아버지가 저녁을 들 수 있도록 자기 점심을 아껴 두던 시절에 자기 허기 그 자체에 굴복하지 않았던 것 이상으로 이 일에서 자기 자신의 마음에 굴복하지 않았다."]], [["One comfort that she had under the Ordeal by General was more sustaining to her, and made her more grateful than to a less devoted and affectionate spirit, not habituated to her struggles and sacrifices, might appear quite reasonable; and, indeed, it may often be observed in life, that spirits like Little Dorrit do not appear to reason half as carefully as the folks who get the better of them.", "제너럴 부인에 의한 시련 속에서 그녀가 누린 한 가지 위안은, 그녀의 분투와 희생에 익숙하지 않은, 덜 헌신적이고 덜 다정한 사람에게는 자못 합당해 보일 것 이상으로, 그녀를 더 든든히 지탱해 주었고 더 고맙게 느끼게 해 주었다. 그리고 실제로 인생에서 자주 보게 되는 일이지만, 리틀 도릿 같은 사람들은 자기를 누르고 이기는 이들의 절반만큼도 신중히 따져 보지 않는 것처럼 보인다."], ["The continued kindness of her sister was this comfort to Little Dorrit.", "언니가 계속 베풀어 주는 친절, 이것이 리틀 도릿에게는 그 위안이었다."], ["It was nothing to her that the kindness took the form of tolerant patronage; she was used to that.", "그 친절이 너그러운 후견의 형태를 띤다는 것은 그녀에게 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 거기에 익숙했다."], ["It was nothing to her that it kept her in a tributary position, and showed her in attendance on the flaming car in which Miss Fanny sat on an elevated seat, exacting homage; she sought no better place.", "그것이 그녀를 종속된 자리에 두고, 패니 양이 높은 자리에 앉아 경의를 강요하는 그 불타는 수레를 시중드는 처지에 보이게 한다는 것도 그녀에게는 아무것도 아니었다. 그녀는 더 나은 자리를 바라지 않았다."], ["Always admiring Fanny’s beauty, and grace, and readiness, and not now asking herself how much of her disposition to be strongly attached to Fanny was due to her own heart, and how much to Fanny’s, she gave her all the sisterly fondness her great heart contained.", "언제나 패니의 아름다움과 우아함과 재치를 흠모하면서, 그리고 패니에게 강하게 매여 있고 싶어 하는 자기 성향이 얼마만큼은 제 마음에서 나온 것이고 얼마만큼은 패니의 마음에서 나온 것인지 이제는 스스로에게 묻지도 않으면서, 그녀는 자신의 너른 가슴에 담긴 자매로서의 온갖 애정을 언니에게 다 주었다."]], [["The wholesale amount of Prunes and Prism which Mrs General infused into the family life, combined with the perpetual plunges made by Fanny into society, left but a very small residue of any natural deposit at the bottom of the mixture.", "제너럴 부인이 가정생활에 들이부은 자두와 프리즘의 막대한 양은, 패니가 사교계로 끊임없이 뛰어드는 일과 어우러져, 그 혼합물의 바닥에 자연스럽게 가라앉는 침전물이라곤 아주 적은 찌꺼기밖에 남기지 않았다."], ["This rendered confidences with Fanny doubly precious to Little Dorrit, and heightened the relief they afforded her.", "이 때문에 패니와 나누는 속내 이야기가 리틀 도릿에게는 갑절로 소중했고, 그것이 그녀에게 주는 안도감이 한층 커졌다."]], [["‘Amy,’ said Fanny to her one night when they were alone, after a day so tiring that Little Dorrit was quite worn out, though Fanny would have taken another dip into society with the greatest pleasure in life, ‘I am going to put something into your little head. You won’t guess what it is, I suspect.’", "\"에이미,\" 어느 날 밤 둘만 있을 때 패니가 그녀에게 말했다. 그날은 어찌나 고단했던지 리틀 도릿은 완전히 녹초가 되었으나, 패니는 세상 무엇보다도 기꺼이 사교계에 한 번 더 발을 담그고 싶어 했을 정도였다. \"네 그 작은 머릿속에 뭘 좀 넣어 줄까 해. 그게 뭔지 넌 짐작도 못 할걸, 내 짐작에는.\""]], [["‘I don’t think that’s likely, dear,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그럴 것 같진 않아요, 언니.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Come, I’ll give you a clue, child,’ said Fanny.", "\"자, 단서를 하나 줄게, 얘.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘Mrs General.’", "\"제너럴 부인.\""]], [["Prunes and Prism, in a thousand combinations, having been wearily in the ascendant all day--everything having been surface and varnish and show without substance--Little Dorrit looked as if she had hoped that Mrs General was safely tucked up in bed for some hours.", "자두와 프리즘이 천 가지 조합으로 종일 지겹도록 위세를 떨치고 있었던 터라—모든 것이 알맹이 없는 겉면이요 윤칠이요 겉치레였던 터라—리틀 도릿은 제너럴 부인이 몇 시간째 침대에 안전히 파묻혀 있기를 바랐던 사람 같은 얼굴을 했다."]], [["‘_Now_, can you guess, Amy?’ said Fanny.", "\"이제 짐작이 가, 에이미?\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘No, dear.", "\"아니요, 언니."], ["Unless I have done anything,’ said Little Dorrit, rather alarmed, and meaning anything calculated to crack varnish and ruffle surface.", "내가 뭔가 한 게 아니라면요.\" 리틀 도릿이 다소 놀라며 말했는데, 윤칠을 갈라지게 하고 겉면을 흐트러뜨릴 만한 무언가를 했다는 뜻이었다."]], [["Fanny was so very much amused by the misgiving, that she took up her favourite fan (being then seated at her dressing-table with her armoury of cruel instruments about her, most of them reeking from the heart of Sparkler), and tapped her sister frequently on the nose with it, laughing all the time.", "패니는 그 걱정스러운 표정이 어찌나 재미있던지, 자기가 가장 아끼는 부채를 집어 들고서(그때 그녀는 화장대 앞에 앉아 잔혹한 무기들을 늘어놓고 있었는데, 그 대부분이 스파클러의 심장에서 갓 뽑혀 김이 나는 듯했다) 그것으로 동생의 코를 연신 톡톡 치며 줄곧 웃었다."]], [["‘Oh, our Amy, our Amy!’ said Fanny.", "\"오, 우리 에이미, 우리 에이미!\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘What a timid little goose our Amy is!", "\"우리 에이미는 어쩜 이리 겁 많은 작은 거위람!"], ["But this is nothing to laugh at.", "하지만 이건 웃을 일이 아니야."], ["On the contrary, I am very cross, my dear.’", "오히려 난 무척 화가 나, 얘.\""]], [["‘As it is not with me, Fanny, I don’t mind,’ returned her sister, smiling.", "\"나한테 난 게 아니라면, 언니, 난 상관없어요.\" 동생이 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But I do mind,’ said Fanny, ‘and so will you, Pet, when I enlighten you.", "하지만 난 상관있어.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["Amy, has it never struck you that somebody is monstrously polite to Mrs General?’", "\"그리고 너도 그럴 거야, 얘, 내가 알려 주면. 에이미, 누군가가 제너럴 부인에게 어이없을 만큼 깍듯이 군다는 생각, 한 번도 안 들었어?\""]], [["‘Everybody is polite to Mrs General,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"누구나 제너럴 부인에게는 깍듯해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘Because--’", "\"왜냐하면—\""]], [["‘Because she freezes them into it?’ interrupted Fanny.", "\"그 부인이 사람들을 얼려서 그렇게 만드니까?\" 패니가 끼어들었다."], ["‘I don’t mean that; quite different from that.", "\"내 말은 그게 아니야. 그것과는 영 다른 얘기야."], ["Come!", "자!"], ["Has it never struck you, Amy, that Pa is monstrously polite to Mrs General.’", "에이미, 아빠가 제너럴 부인에게 어이없을 만큼 깍듯이 군다는 생각, 한 번도 안 들었느냐고.\""]], [["Amy, murmuring ‘No,’ looked quite confounded.", "에이미는 \"아니요\" 하고 중얼거리며 어리둥절한 얼굴을 했다."]], [["‘No; I dare say not.", "\"그래, 안 들었겠지."], ["But he is,’ said Fanny.", "하지만 그러셔.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘He is, Amy.", "\"그러신다니까, 에이미."], ["And remember my words.", "그리고 내 말 잘 기억해 둬."], ["Mrs General has designs on Pa!’", "제너럴 부인은 아빠한테 흑심을 품고 있어!\""]], [["‘Dear Fanny, do you think it possible that Mrs General has designs on any one?’", "\"패니 언니, 제너럴 부인이 누구한테든 흑심을 품을 수 있다고 생각해요?\""]], [["‘Do I think it possible?’ retorted Fanny.", "\"그게 가능하다고 생각하느냐고?\""], ["‘My love, I know it.", "패니가 받아쳤다."], ["I tell you she has designs on Pa.", "\"얘, 난 확실히 알아."], ["And more than that, I tell you Pa considers her such a wonder, such a paragon of accomplishment, and such an acquisition to our family, that he is ready to get himself into a state of perfect infatuation with her at any moment.", "내 말하잖니, 그 여자는 아빠한테 흑심이 있다고. 그뿐 아니라, 아빠는 그 여자를 어찌나 대단한 사람으로, 교양의 표본으로, 우리 집안에 어찌나 큰 보탬으로 여기시는지, 언제라도 그 여자한테 홀딱 빠져 버릴 태세야."], ["And that opens a pretty picture of things, I hope?", "그러면 참 보기 좋은 그림이 펼쳐지겠지, 안 그래?"], ["Think of me with Mrs General for a Mama!’", "제너럴 부인을 엄마로 둔 나를 생각해 봐!\""]], [["Little Dorrit did not reply, ‘Think of me with Mrs General for a Mama;’ but she looked anxious, and seriously inquired what had led Fanny to these conclusions.", "리틀 도릿은 \"제너럴 부인을 엄마로 둔 나를 생각해 봐\"라고 대꾸하지는 않았다. 그러나 그녀는 근심스러운 얼굴로, 무엇 때문에 패니가 이런 결론에 이르렀는지 진지하게 물었다."]], [["‘Lord, my darling,’ said Fanny, tartly.", "\"세상에, 얘.\" 패니가 톡 쏘며 말했다."], ["‘You might as well ask me how I know when a man is struck with myself!", "\"그럼 넌 어떤 남자가 나한테 반했을 때 내가 그걸 어떻게 아느냐고도 묻겠구나!"], ["But, of course I do know.", "하지만 난 당연히 알지."], ["It happens pretty often: but I always know it.", "꽤 자주 있는 일이거든. 하지만 난 늘 알아챈다고."], ["I know this in much the same way, I suppose.", "이번 일도 거의 그런 식으로 알아챈 거야, 아마."], ["At all events, I know it.’", "어쨌든 난 알아.\""]], [["‘You never heard Papa say anything?’", "\"아빠가 무슨 말씀하시는 걸 들은 적은 없죠?\""]], [["‘Say anything?’ repeated Fanny.", "\"무슨 말을 해?\" 패니가 되뇌었다."], ["‘My dearest, darling child, what necessity has he had, yet awhile, to say anything?’", "\"내 가장 소중한 동생아, 아직까지 아빠가 무슨 말을 할 필요가 어디 있었겠니?\""]], [["‘And you have never heard Mrs General say anything?’", "\"그럼 제너럴 부인이 무슨 말 하는 것도 들은 적이 없고요?\""]], [["‘My goodness me, Amy,’ returned Fanny, ‘is she the sort of woman to say anything?", "\"세상에나, 에이미.\" 패니가 대꾸했다."], ["Isn’t it perfectly plain and clear that she has nothing to do at present but to hold herself upright, keep her aggravating gloves on, and go sweeping about?", "\"그 여자가 무슨 말을 할 그런 부류로 보여? 지금 그 여자가 할 일이라곤 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고, 그 거슬리는 장갑을 낀 채로, 우아하게 휩쓸고 다니는 것밖에 없다는 게 너무 빤하고 분명하지 않아?"], ["Say anything!", "무슨 말을 해!"], ["If she had the ace of trumps in her hand at whist, she wouldn’t say anything, child.", "휘스트에서 으뜸패의 에이스를 손에 쥐고 있어도, 그 여자는 한마디도 안 할걸, 얘."], ["It would come out when she played it.’", "그건 패를 낼 때 비로소 드러나는 법이지.\""]], [["‘At least, you may be mistaken, Fanny.", "\"적어도 언니가 잘못 짚은 걸 수도 있잖아요, 패니."], ["Now, may you not?’", "자, 그럴 수도 있지 않아요?\""]], [["‘O yes, I _may_ be,’ said Fanny, ‘but I am not.", "\"오 그래, 그럴 수도 있지.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["However, I am glad you can contemplate such an escape, my dear, and I am glad that you can take this for the present with sufficient coolness to think of such a chance.", "\"하지만 난 틀리지 않았어. 그래도 네가 그런 탈출구를 떠올릴 수 있다니 기쁘구나, 얘."], ["It makes me hope that you may be able to bear the connection.", "그리고 네가 지금 이 일을 충분히 냉정하게 받아들이고 그런 가능성을 생각할 수 있다니 기뻐."], ["I should not be able to bear it, and I should not try.", "그걸 보니 네가 이 인연을 견딜 수 있을지도 모르겠다는 희망이 생기네. 나라면 못 견뎌, 견디려고 하지도 않을 거고."], ["I’d marry young Sparkler first.’", "차라리 저 젊은 스파클러랑 결혼하고 말지.\""]], [["‘O, you would never marry him, Fanny, under any circumstances.’", "\"오, 언니는 어떤 상황에서도 그 사람과 결혼하지 않을 거잖아요, 패니.\""]], [["‘Upon my word, my dear,’ rejoined that young lady with exceeding indifference, ‘I wouldn’t positively answer even for that.", "\"정말이지, 얘,\" 그 젊은 숙녀가 지극히 무심하게 받아쳤다."], ["There’s no knowing what might happen.", "\"그것조차 단언하진 못하겠어."], ["Especially as I should have many opportunities, afterwards, of treating that woman, his mother, in her own style.", "무슨 일이 일어날지 누가 알겠니. 게다가 나중에 그 여자, 그 사람 어머니를 그 여자 식대로 갚아 줄 기회가 숱하게 생길 테고."], ["Which I most decidedly should not be slow to avail myself of, Amy.’", "그 기회를 난 결코 굼뜨게 흘려보내지 않을 거야, 에이미.\""]], [["No more passed between the sisters then; but what had passed gave the two subjects of Mrs General and Mr Sparkler great prominence in Little Dorrit’s mind, and thenceforth she thought very much of both.", "그때 자매 사이에 더 오간 말은 없었다. 그러나 오간 그 말들 덕에 제너럴 부인과 스파클러 씨라는 두 화제가 리틀 도릿의 마음속에서 크게 부각되었고, 그때부터 그녀는 그 둘을 무척 많이 생각하게 되었다."]], [["Mrs General, having long ago formed her own surface to such perfection that it hid whatever was below it (if anything), no observation was to be made in that quarter.", "제너럴 부인은 오래전부터 자기 겉면을 어찌나 완벽하게 다듬어 두었던지 그 아래에 무엇이 있든(있기나 하다면) 다 가려 버렸으므로, 그쪽에서는 아무것도 관찰할 수가 없었다."], ["Mr Dorrit was undeniably very polite to her and had a high opinion of her; but Fanny, impetuous at most times, might easily be wrong for all that.", "도릿 씨가 그녀에게 매우 깍듯하고 그녀를 높이 평가한다는 것은 부인할 수 없었다. 그러나 대개는 충동적인 패니인지라, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고 쉽사리 틀릴 수 있었다."], ["Whereas, the Sparkler question was on the different footing that any one could see what was going on there, and Little Dorrit saw it and pondered on it with many doubts and wonderings.", "반면에 스파클러 문제는 사정이 달라서, 거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지는 누구든 알아볼 수 있었고, 리틀 도릿도 그것을 보며 숱한 의문과 궁금증을 품고 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["The devotion of Mr Sparkler was only to be equalled by the caprice and cruelty of his enslaver. Sometimes she would prefer him to such distinction of notice, that he would chuckle aloud with joy; next day, or next hour, she would overlook him so completely, and drop him into such an abyss of obscurity, that he would groan under a weak pretence of coughing. The constancy of his attendance never touched Fanny: though he was so inseparable from Edward, that, when that gentleman wished for a change of society, he was under the irksome necessity of gliding out like a conspirator in disguised boats and by secret doors and back ways; though he was so solicitous to know how Mr Dorrit was, that he called every other day to inquire, as if Mr Dorrit were the prey of an intermittent fever; though he was so constantly being paddled up and down before the principal windows, that he might have been supposed to have made a wager for a large stake to be paddled a thousand miles in a thousand hours; though whenever the gondola of his mistress left the gate, the gondola of Mr Sparkler shot out from some watery ambush and gave chase, as if she were a fair smuggler and he a custom-house officer. It was probably owing to this fortification of the natural strength of his constitution with so much exposure to the air, and the salt sea, that Mr Sparkler did not pine outwardly; but, whatever the cause, he was so far from having any prospect of moving his mistress by a languishing state of health, that he grew bluffer every day, and that peculiarity in his appearance of seeming rather a swelled boy than a young man, became developed to an extraordinary degree of ruddy puffiness.", "스파클러 씨의 헌신은 그를 노예로 부리는 여인의 변덕과 잔인함에나 견줄 만했다. 어떤 때는 그녀가 그를 어찌나 두드러지게 챙겨 주는지 그가 기뻐서 큰 소리로 낄낄거렸다가도, 이튿날이나 다음 순간이면 그를 어찌나 완벽하게 못 본 척하고 캄캄한 무명의 심연으로 떨어뜨리는지 그가 기침하는 척 어설피 가장하며 끙끙댔다. 그의 한결같은 시중도 패니의 마음을 결코 움직이지 못했다. 그는 에드워드와 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사이여서, 그 신사가 다른 부류의 사람들과 어울리고 싶을 때면 변장한 음모자처럼 위장한 곤돌라를 타고 비밀의 문과 뒷길로 몰래 빠져나가야 하는 거북한 처지에 놓였는데도 그러했다. 그는 도릿 씨의 안부를 어찌나 걱정스레 궁금해했던지, 마치 도릿 씨가 간헐열의 먹이라도 되는 양 하루걸러 찾아와 안부를 물었는데도 그러했다. 그는 주요 창문들 앞을 어찌나 끊임없이 노 저어 오르내렸던지, 천 시간에 천 마일을 노 저어 가는 큰 내기라도 건 사람처럼 보일 지경이었는데도 그러했다. 그가 섬기는 여인의 곤돌라가 대문을 떠날 때면, 마치 그녀가 어여쁜 밀수꾼이고 그가 세관 관리이기라도 한 듯, 스파클러 씨의 곤돌라가 어느 물가의 매복처에서 튀어나와 뒤를 쫓았는데도 그러했다. 스파클러 씨가 겉보기에 야위지 않은 것은 아마도 이렇게 바깥 공기와 짠 바닷바람에 그리도 자주 노출되어 타고난 체질의 힘이 한층 다져진 덕이었을 것이다. 그러나 원인이 무엇이든, 그는 시들시들 병약한 모습으로 섬기는 여인의 마음을 움직일 가망이라곤 전혀 없을뿐더러, 날이 갈수록 더 혈색 좋고 우람해졌으며, 청년이라기보다 퉁퉁 부은 소년 같아 보이던 그 특유의 인상이 불그레하게 부풀어 오른 비범한 정도로까지 발달했다."]], [["Blandois calling to pay his respects, Mr Dorrit received him with affability as the friend of Mr Gowan, and mentioned to him his idea of commissioning Mr Gowan to transmit him to posterity.", "블랑두아가 인사차 찾아오자, 도릿 씨는 그를 가우언 씨의 친구로서 상냥하게 맞이했고, 가우언 씨에게 자신의 모습을 후세에 전하도록 의뢰하려는 생각을 그에게 말했다."], ["Blandois highly extolling it, it occurred to Mr Dorrit that it might be agreeable to Blandois to communicate to his friend the great opportunity reserved for him.", "블랑두아가 그 생각을 한껏 칭송하자, 그 친구에게 마련된 이 큰 기회를 블랑두아가 직접 전해 주면 좋아하겠다는 생각이 도릿 씨에게 떠올랐다."], ["Blandois accepted the commission with his own free elegance of manner, and swore he would discharge it before he was an hour older.", "블랑두아는 특유의 자유롭고 우아한 태도로 그 임무를 받아들이며, 한 시간이 채 지나기 전에 그것을 완수하겠노라 맹세했다."], ["On his imparting the news to Gowan, that Master gave Mr Dorrit to the Devil with great liberality some round dozen of times (for he resented patronage almost as much as he resented the want of it), and was inclined to quarrel with his friend for bringing him the message.", "그가 가우언에게 그 소식을 전하자, 그 거장은 매우 너그럽게도 도릿 씨를 열 몇 번이나 악마에게 내던졌고(그는 후견을 받는 것을 후견이 없는 것 못지않게 분개했으므로), 그 전갈을 가져왔다고 친구와 다투려 들었다."]], [["‘It may be a defect in my mental vision, Blandois,’ said he, ‘but may I die if I see what you have to do with this.’", "\"내 정신의 시야에 결함이 있는 건지도 모르겠네만, 블랑두아,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일이 자네와 무슨 상관인지 내가 안다면 차라리 죽어 버리지.\""]], [["‘Death of my life,’ replied Blandois, ‘nor I neither, except that I thought I was serving my friend.’", "\"내 목숨을 걸고 말하건대,\" 블랑두아가 대답했다. \"나도 모르겠네, 그저 내 친구를 도와주는 줄 알았다는 것 말고는.\""]], [["‘By putting an upstart’s hire in his pocket?’ said Gowan, frowning.", "\"벼락출세한 자의 품삯을 내 호주머니에 찔러 넣어서?\" 가우언이 인상을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["‘Do you mean that?", "\"그런 뜻인가?"], ["Tell your other friend to get his head painted for the sign of some public-house, and to get it done by a sign-painter.", "자네 그 다른 친구한테 가서, 무슨 선술집 간판으로 쓸 얼굴이나 그리라고 하게, 그것도 간판장이를 시켜서 말이야."], ["Who am I, and who is he?’", "내가 누군데, 또 그자가 누군데?\""]], [["‘Professore,’ returned the ambassador, ‘and who is Blandois?’", "\"프로페소레,\" 그 사절이 대꾸했다. \"그럼 블랑두아는 누구지?\""]], [["Without appearing at all interested in the latter question, Gowan angrily whistled Mr Dorrit away.", "가우언은 후자의 질문에는 조금도 관심을 보이지 않은 채, 화가 나서 휘파람으로 도릿 씨를 쫓아 보내듯 했다."], ["But, next day, he resumed the subject by saying in his off-hand manner and with a slighting laugh, ‘Well, Blandois, when shall we go to this Maecenas of yours?", "그러나 이튿날 그는 그 화제를 다시 꺼내, 특유의 무심한 태도로 깔보는 웃음을 지으며 말했다. \"그래, 블랑두아, 자네 그 마에케나스님께는 언제 가지?"], ["We journeymen must take jobs when we can get them.", "우리 같은 품팔이꾼들은 일감이 생길 때 잡아야 하는 법이거든."], ["When shall we go and look after this job?’", "이 일감을 보러 언제 가 볼까?\""]], [["‘When you will,’ said the injured Blandois, ‘as you please.", "\"자네가 원할 때,\" 마음 상한 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["What have I to do with it?", "\"자네 좋을 대로. 그게 나랑 무슨 상관인가?"], ["What is it to me?’", "나한테 뭐가 어떻다고?\""]], [["‘I can tell you what it is to me,’ said Gowan.", "\"나한테 뭐가 어떤지는 내가 말해 주지,\" 가우언이 말했다."], ["‘Bread and cheese.", "\"빵하고 치즈일세."], ["One must eat!", "사람은 먹어야 하지 않나!"], ["So come along, my Blandois.’", "그러니 가세, 내 친구 블랑두아.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit received them in the presence of his daughters and of Mr Sparkler, who happened, by some surprising accident, to be calling there.", "도릿 씨는 자기 딸들과, 어떤 놀라운 우연으로 마침 그곳에 찾아와 있던 스파클러 씨가 있는 자리에서 두 사람을 맞이했다."], ["‘How are you, Sparkler?’ said Gowan carelessly.", "\"잘 지내나, 스파클러?\" 가우언이 무심하게 말했다."], ["‘When you have to live by your mother wit, old boy, I hope you may get on better than I do.’", "\"자네가 타고난 머리로 먹고살아야 할 때가 오거든, 부디 나보다는 잘 헤쳐 나가기를 비네.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit then mentioned his proposal.", "이윽고 도릿 씨가 자신의 제안을 꺼냈다. \"선생,\""], ["‘Sir,’ said Gowan, laughing, after receiving it gracefully enough, ‘I am new to the trade, and not expert at its mysteries.", "가우언이 그 제안을 제법 우아하게 받아들인 뒤 웃으며 말했다. \"저는 이 일에 풋내기여서 그 비법에 능숙하질 못합니다."], ["I believe I ought to look at you in various lights, tell you you are a capital subject, and consider when I shall be sufficiently disengaged to devote myself with the necessary enthusiasm to the fine picture I mean to make of you.", "마땅히 저는 선생을 여러 각도에서 살펴보고, 선생이 더없이 훌륭한 소재라고 말씀드리고, 제가 그리고자 하는 그 멋진 그림에 필요한 만큼의 열정을 다 쏟아부을 수 있을 만큼 한가해질 때가 언제일지 가늠해 봐야 하겠지요. 정말이지,\""], ["I assure you,’ and he laughed again, ‘I feel quite a traitor in the camp of those dear, gifted, good, noble fellows, my brother artists, by not doing the hocus-pocus better.", "그가 또 웃었다. \"저는 그 사랑스럽고 재능 있고 선량하고 고결한 친구들, 제 동료 화가들의 진영에서, 이 눈속임을 더 그럴듯하게 하지 못하니 영락없는 배신자 같은 기분이 듭니다."], ["But I have not been brought up to it, and it’s too late to learn it.", "그러나 저는 그렇게 자라지 못했고, 이제 와 배우기에는 너무 늦었지요."], ["Now, the fact is, I am a very bad painter, but not much worse than the generality.", "그런데 사실인즉, 저는 형편없는 화가입니다, 다만 보통내기들보다 그리 많이 나쁘지는 않을 뿐이지요."], ["If you are going to throw away a hundred guineas or so, I am as poor as a poor relation of great people usually is, and I shall be very much obliged to you, if you’ll throw them away upon me.", "선생께서 백 기니쯤 내던지실 작정이라면, 저야 으레 지체 높은 분들의 가난한 친척이 그렇듯 가난뱅이니, 그것을 저한테 내던져 주신다면 무척 감사하겠습니다."], ["I’ll do the best I can for the money; and if the best should be bad, why even then, you may probably have a bad picture with a small name to it, instead of a bad picture with a large name to it.’", "그 돈값을 하려 최선을 다하지요. 그리고 그 최선이 형편없다 한들, 그럼 그때는 큰 이름이 붙은 형편없는 그림 대신 작은 이름이 붙은 형편없는 그림을 손에 넣으시게 될 테니까요.\""]], [["This tone, though not what he had expected, on the whole suited Mr Dorrit remarkably well.", "이런 어조는 그가 예상했던 바는 아니었으나, 대체로 도릿 씨에게는 놀라우리만치 잘 맞았다."], ["It showed that the gentleman, highly connected, and not a mere workman, would be under an obligation to him.", "그것은 이 신사가, 지체 높은 집안과 연이 닿아 있고 한낱 직공이 아닌 사람이, 자기에게 신세를 지게 되리라는 것을 보여 주었다."], ["He expressed his satisfaction in placing himself in Mr Gowan’s hands, and trusted that he would have the pleasure, in their characters of private gentlemen, of improving his acquaintance.", "그는 가우언 씨의 손에 자신을 맡기게 되어 만족스럽다고 말했고, 두 사람이 사사로운 신사로서 교분을 더 두텁게 할 즐거움을 누리기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["‘You are very good,’ said Gowan. ‘I have not forsworn society since I joined the brotherhood of the brush (the most delightful fellows on the face of the earth), and am glad enough to smell the old fine gunpowder now and then, though it did blow me into mid-air and my present calling. You’ll not think, Mr Dorrit,’ and here he laughed again in the easiest way, ‘that I am lapsing into the freemasonry of the craft--for it’s not so; upon my life I can’t help betraying it wherever I go, though, by Jupiter, I love and honour the craft with all my might--if I propose a stipulation as to time and place?’", "\"참으로 너그러우십니다,\" 가우언이 말했다. \"저는 이 붓의 형제단(이 지상에서 가장 유쾌한 친구들이지요)에 들어온 뒤로도 사교를 끊은 적이 없고, 이따금 그 좋은 옛 화약 냄새를 맡는 것이 꽤나 반갑습니다—그 화약이 저를 공중으로 날려 지금의 이 직업에 떨어뜨려 놓긴 했지만요. 그런데 도릿 씨, 제가 시간과 장소에 대해 한 가지 조건을 내건다 해서,\" 여기서 그가 또 더없이 편하게 웃었다. \"제가 이 업계의 비밀 결사 같은 것에 빠져들었다고 여기진 마십시오—그런 건 아니거든요. 맹세코, 저는 어딜 가든 그것을 드러내지 않고는 못 배기지만요, 비록 주피터께 맹세코 제 온 힘을 다해 이 업을 사랑하고 존경합니다만 말입니다.\""]], [["Ha!", "하!"], ["Mr Dorrit could erect no--hum--suspicion of that kind on Mr Gowan’s frankness.", "도릿 씨로서는 가우언 씨의 솔직함에 그런—흠—의심 따위는 도무지 품을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Again you are very good,’ said Gowan.", "\"또다시 참으로 너그러우십니다,\" 가우언이 말했다."], ["‘Mr Dorrit, I hear you are going to Rome.", "\"도릿 씨, 듣자 하니 로마로 가신다더군요."], ["I am going to Rome, having friends there.", "저도 로마로 갑니다, 거기에 친구들이 있거든요."], ["Let me begin to do you the injustice I have conspired to do you, there--not here.", "제가 선생께 끼치기로 작정한 그 무례를, 여기가 아니라 거기서 시작하게 해 주십시오."], ["We shall all be hurried during the rest of our stay here; and though there’s not a poorer man with whole elbows in Venice, than myself, I have not quite got all the Amateur out of me yet--comprising the trade again, you see!--and can’t fall on to order, in a hurry, for the mere sake of the sixpences.’", "여기 머무는 동안에는 우리 모두 정신없이 바쁠 테니까요. 그리고 베네치아에 저만큼 팔꿈치 성한 가난뱅이는 없습니다만, 저는 아직 제 안의 아마추어 기질을 다 빼내지 못해서요—보시다시피 또 업계 얘기로군요!—몇 푼 안 되는 돈만 보고 서둘러 주문대로 작업에 들러붙진 못합니다.\""]], [["These remarks were not less favourably received by Mr Dorrit than their predecessors.", "이 말들도 앞의 말들 못지않게 도릿 씨에게 호의적으로 받아들여졌다."], ["They were the prelude to the first reception of Mr and Mrs Gowan at dinner, and they skilfully placed Gowan on his usual ground in the new family.", "그것은 가우언 부부를 처음으로 만찬에 맞이하는 일의 서곡이었고, 그 말들은 가우언을 새 집안에서 그의 늘 그러한 위치에 능란하게 자리 잡게 해 주었다."]], [["His wife, too, they placed on her usual ground.", "그의 아내 또한 그 말들이 그녀의 늘 그러한 위치에 자리 잡게 했다."], ["Miss Fanny understood, with particular distinctness, that Mrs Gowan’s good looks had cost her husband very dear; that there had been a great disturbance about her in the Barnacle family; and that the Dowager Mrs Gowan, nearly heart-broken, had resolutely set her face against the marriage until overpowered by her maternal feelings.", "패니 양은 특히 또렷하게 이해했으니, 가우언 부인의 고운 용모가 남편에게 무척 비싼 대가를 치르게 했다는 것, 그녀를 두고 배너클 집안에 큰 소동이 있었다는 것, 그리고 미망인 가우언 부인이 거의 가슴이 찢어진 채로 모성의 정에 눌려 굴복하기 전까지는 그 결혼에 단호히 반대했다는 것이었다."], ["Mrs General likewise clearly understood that the attachment had occasioned much family grief and dissension.", "제너럴 부인 역시 그 애정이 집안에 많은 슬픔과 불화를 일으켰다는 것을 분명히 이해했다."], ["Of honest Mr Meagles no mention was made; except that it was natural enough that a person of that sort should wish to raise his daughter out of his own obscurity, and that no one could blame him for trying his best to do so.", "정직한 미글스 씨에 대해서는 아무런 언급도 없었다. 다만 그런 부류의 사람이 제 딸을 자신의 미천함에서 끌어올리고 싶어 하는 것은 지극히 당연한 일이며, 그러려고 최선을 다한다 해서 아무도 그를 탓할 수 없으리라는 정도였다."]], [["Little Dorrit’s interest in the fair subject of this easily accepted belief was too earnest and watchful to fail in accurate observation.", "이렇게 손쉽게 받아들여진 믿음의 그 고운 대상에 대한 리틀 도릿의 관심은 너무도 진실하고 세심해서 정확한 관찰에서 어긋나지 않았다."], ["She could see that it had its part in throwing upon Mrs Gowan the touch of a shadow under which she lived, and she even had an instinctive knowledge that there was not the least truth in it.", "그녀는 그것이 가우언 부인이 그 아래에서 살아가는 그 그림자의 기운을 그녀에게 드리우는 데 한몫한다는 것을 볼 수 있었고, 심지어 그 안에 진실이라곤 털끝만큼도 없다는 것을 본능적으로 알았다."], ["But it had an influence in placing obstacles in the way of her association with Mrs Gowan by making the Prunes and Prism school excessively polite to her, but not very intimate with her; and Little Dorrit, as an enforced sizar of that college, was obliged to submit herself humbly to its ordinances.", "그러나 그것은 자두와 프리즘 학파 사람들로 하여금 가우언 부인에게 지나치게 깍듯하되 그리 가깝지는 않게 만듦으로써, 그녀가 가우언 부인과 가까워지는 길에 장애물을 놓는 영향을 끼쳤다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 그 학교의 떠밀려 들어간 사이저로서, 그 규율에 겸허히 자신을 내맡겨야만 했다."]], [["Nevertheless, there was a sympathetic understanding already established between the two, which would have carried them over greater difficulties, and made a friendship out of a more restricted intercourse.", "* 사이저: 옛 케임브리지 대학에서 잡일을 하며 학비를 면제받던 가난한 학생. 그럼에도 두 사람 사이에는 이미 공감의 이해가 자리 잡고 있어서, 더 큰 어려움도 넘어서게 해 주고 더 제약된 교류에서도 우정을 빚어낼 만했다."], ["As though accidents were determined to be favourable to it, they had a new assurance of congeniality in the aversion which each perceived that the other felt towards Blandois of Paris; an aversion amounting to the repugnance and horror of a natural antipathy towards an odious creature of the reptile kind.", "마치 우연들이 그 우정에 우호적이기로 작정이라도 한 듯, 두 사람은 서로가 파리의 블랑두아에게 품고 있음을 알아챈 그 혐오감에서 새로운 동질감의 확신을 얻었으니, 그것은 파충류 부류의 역겨운 생물에 대한 타고난 반감의 거부감과 공포에 이르는 혐오였다."]], [["And there was a passive congeniality between them, besides this active one.", "그리고 이 능동적인 동질감 말고도 두 사람 사이에는 수동적인 동질감이 있었다."], ["To both of them, Blandois behaved in exactly the same manner; and to both of them his manner had uniformly something in it, which they both knew to be different from his bearing towards others.", "블랑두아는 두 사람에게 똑같은 방식으로 행동했고, 두 사람을 대하는 그의 태도에는 한결같이 무언가가 깃들어 있었는데, 그것이 다른 이들을 대하는 그의 거동과는 다르다는 것을 둘 다 알았다."], ["The difference was too minute in its expression to be perceived by others, but they knew it to be there.", "그 차이는 표현이 너무도 미묘하여 다른 이들은 알아챌 수 없었으나, 두 사람은 그것이 거기 있음을 알았다."], ["A mere trick of his evil eyes, a mere turn of his smooth white hand, a mere hair’s-breadth of addition to the fall of his nose and the rise of the moustache in the most frequent movement of his face, conveyed to both of them, equally, a swagger personal to themselves.", "그의 사악한 눈의 단순한 한 가닥 버릇, 매끄러운 흰 손의 단순한 한 번 놀림, 그의 얼굴이 가장 자주 짓는 움직임에서 코끝이 처지고 콧수염이 들리는 그 머리카락 한 올만큼의 단순한 변화가, 두 사람에게 똑같이, 그들 자신을 겨냥한 어떤 으스댐을 전했다."], ["It was as if he had said, ‘I have a secret power in this quarter.", "그것은 마치 그가 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"내겐 이쪽으로 은밀한 힘이 있지."], ["I know what I know.’", "나는 내가 아는 것을 알고 있어.\""]], [["This had never been felt by them both in so great a degree, and never by each so perfectly to the knowledge of the other, as on a day when he came to Mr Dorrit’s to take his leave before quitting Venice.", "이 점을 둘이 그토록 크게 느낀 적도, 또 서로 상대가 그것을 느낀다는 것을 그토록 완벽하게 안 적도, 그가 베네치아를 떠나기 전 작별 인사를 하러 도릿 씨의 집에 온 그날만큼 일찍이 없었다."], ["Mrs Gowan was herself there for the same purpose, and he came upon the two together; the rest of the family being out.", "가우언 부인도 마침 같은 목적으로 거기 와 있었고, 그는 둘이 함께 있는 자리에 들이닥쳤다. 나머지 식구들은 외출 중이었다."], ["The two had not been together five minutes, and the peculiar manner seemed to convey to them, ‘You were going to talk about me.", "두 사람이 함께 있은 지 채 오 분도 되지 않았는데, 그 특유의 태도가 그들에게 이렇게 전하는 듯했다. \"당신들 내 얘기를 하려던 참이었지."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["Behold me here to prevent it!’", "그걸 막으려고 내가 여기 떡하니 와 있는 거야!\""]], [["‘Gowan is coming here?’ said Blandois, with a smile.", "\"가우언이 여기로 옵니까?\" 블랑두아가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Mrs Gowan replied he was not coming.", "가우언 부인은 그가 오지 않는다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Not coming!’ said Blandois.", "\"오지 않는다고요!\" 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["‘Permit your devoted servant, when you leave here, to escort you home.’", "\"그럼 여기를 나가실 때, 댁의 충직한 종이 댁까지 모시게 허락해 주시지요.\""]], [["‘Thank you: I am not going home.’", "\"고맙지만, 저는 집으로 가지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Not going home!’ said Blandois.", "\"집으로 가지 않으신다고요!\" 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["‘Then I am forlorn.’", "\"그럼 저는 가엾이 버려진 신세로군요.\""]], [["That he might be; but he was not so forlorn as to roam away and leave them together.", "그럴지도 몰랐다. 그러나 그는 자리를 떠 두 사람을 함께 남겨 둘 만큼 가엾이 버려진 신세는 아니었다."], ["He sat entertaining them with his finest compliments, and his choicest conversation; but he conveyed to them, all the time, ‘No, no, no, dear ladies.", "그는 자기의 가장 멋들어진 찬사와 가장 빼어난 화술로 두 사람을 즐겁게 해 주려 앉아 있었으나, 그러면서도 줄곧 이렇게 전했다. \"안 되지, 안 돼, 안 돼, 사랑스러운 숙녀분들."], ["Behold me here expressly to prevent it!’", "내가 여기 있는 건 바로 그걸 막기 위해서요!\""]], [["He conveyed it to them with so much meaning, and he had such a diabolical persistency in him, that at length, Mrs Gowan rose to depart.", "그가 어찌나 의미심장하게 그것을 전하던지, 또 그에게 어찌나 악마 같은 끈질김이 있던지, 마침내 가우언 부인이 떠나려고 일어섰다."], ["On his offering his hand to Mrs Gowan to lead her down the staircase, she retained Little Dorrit’s hand in hers, with a cautious pressure, and said, ‘No, thank you.", "그가 가우언 부인을 계단 아래로 인도하려 손을 내밀자, 그녀는 리틀 도릿의 손을 자기 손에 조심스레 꼭 쥔 채로 말했다. \"아니요, 괜찮습니다."], ["But, if you will please to see if my boatman is there, I shall be obliged to you.’", "다만 제 뱃사공이 와 있는지 좀 봐 주시면 고맙겠어요.\""]], [["It left him no choice but to go down before them.", "이로써 그는 두 사람보다 먼저 내려가는 수밖에 없게 되었다."], ["As he did so, hat in hand, Mrs Gowan whispered:", "그가 모자를 손에 들고 그렇게 내려가는 사이, 가우언 부인이 속삭였다."]], [["‘He killed the dog.’", "\"저 사람이 그 개를 죽였어요.\""]], [["‘Does Mr Gowan know it?’ Little Dorrit whispered.", "\"가우언 씨도 그걸 아나요?\" 리틀 도릿이 속삭였다."]], [["‘No one knows it.", "\"아무도 몰라요."], ["Don’t look towards me; look towards him.", "나를 보지 말아요, 그를 봐요."], ["He will turn his face in a moment.", "곧 얼굴을 돌릴 테니까."], ["No one knows it, but I am sure he did.", "아무도 모르지만, 나는 그가 그랬다고 확신해요."], ["You are?’", "당신도 그렇죠?\""]], [["‘I--I think so,’ Little Dorrit answered.", "\"저—저도 그렇게 생각해요.\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다."]], [["‘Henry likes him, and he will not think ill of him; he is so generous and open himself.", "\"헨리는 그를 좋아해서 그를 나쁘게 보려 하지 않아요. 그이 자신이 워낙 너그럽고 솔직하거든요."], ["But you and I feel sure that we think of him as he deserves.", "하지만 당신과 나는 그를 그가 마땅히 받을 만큼으로 본다고 확신하죠."], ["He argued with Henry that the dog had been already poisoned when he changed so, and sprang at him.", "그는 헨리에게, 그 개가 그렇게 돌변해 자기에게 달려들었을 때 이미 독에 당한 상태였다고 우겼어요."], ["Henry believes it, but we do not.", "헨리는 그걸 믿지만, 우리는 안 믿어요."], ["I see he is listening, but can’t hear.", "보니 그가 귀를 기울이고 있긴 한데, 들리진 않는 모양이에요."], ["Good-bye, my love!", "잘 가요, 내 사랑!"], ["Good-bye!’", "잘 가요!\""]], [["The last words were spoken aloud, as the vigilant Blandois stopped, turned his head, and looked at them from the bottom of the staircase.", "마지막 말들은 큰 소리로 했는데, 빈틈없는 블랑두아가 걸음을 멈추고 고개를 돌려 계단 밑에서 두 사람을 올려다본 때였다."], ["Assuredly he did look then, though he looked his politest, as if any real philanthropist could have desired no better employment than to lash a great stone to his neck, and drop him into the water flowing beyond the dark arched gateway in which he stood.", "분명 그때 그는, 비록 더없이 정중한 표정을 짓고 있긴 했으나, 마치 어떤 진정한 박애주의자라도 그의 목에 커다란 돌을 묶어, 그가 서 있는 그 어두운 아치형 통로 너머로 흐르는 물속에 그를 떨어뜨리는 것보다 더 좋은 소일거리를 바랄 수 없을 만한 그런 모습이었다."], ["No such benefactor to mankind being on the spot, he handed Mrs Gowan to her boat, and stood there until it had shot out of the narrow view; when he handed himself into his own boat and followed.", "인류를 위한 그런 은인이 그 자리에 없었으므로, 그는 가우언 부인을 그녀의 배에 태워 드린 뒤, 그 배가 좁은 시야 밖으로 쏜살같이 사라질 때까지 거기 서 있었다. 그러고는 자기 배에 몸을 실어 뒤따랐다."]], [["Little Dorrit had sometimes thought, and now thought again as she retraced her steps up the staircase, that he had made his way too easily into her father’s house.", "리틀 도릿은 이따금, 그리고 지금 계단을 되짚어 올라가면서 다시, 그가 아버지의 집에 너무 쉽사리 발을 들였다고 생각했다."], ["But so many and such varieties of people did the same, through Mr Dorrit’s participation in his elder daughter’s society mania, that it was hardly an exceptional case.", "그러나 도릿 씨가 큰딸의 사교계 열병에 가담한 탓에 그토록 많고 갖가지인 사람들이 똑같이 그러했으므로, 그것은 딱히 예외적인 경우라 할 수 없었다."], ["A perfect fury for making acquaintances on whom to impress their riches and importance, had seized the House of Dorrit.", "자신들의 부와 권세를 각인시킬 상대를 사귀려는 완전한 광기가 도릿 가를 사로잡고 있었다."]], [["It appeared on the whole, to Little Dorrit herself, that this same society in which they lived, greatly resembled a superior sort of Marshalsea.", "대체로 리틀 도릿 자신이 보기에, 그들이 사는 바로 이 사교계는 한층 고급스러운 종류의 마셜시와 무척이나 닮아 있었다."], ["Numbers of people seemed to come abroad, pretty much as people had come into the prison; through debt, through idleness, relationship, curiosity, and general unfitness for getting on at home.", "수많은 사람들이 외국으로 나오는 모양새가, 사람들이 그 감옥으로 들어오던 것과 거의 다를 바 없어 보였다. 빚 때문에, 게으름 때문에, 인척 관계 때문에, 호기심 때문에, 그리고 본국에서 도무지 잘 살아가지 못하는 전반적인 부적응 때문에 말이다."], ["They were brought into these foreign towns in the custody of couriers and local followers, just as the debtors had been brought into the prison.", "채무자들이 감옥으로 끌려 들어왔던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그들도 안내인들과 현지의 추종자들에게 호송되어 이 외국 도시들로 끌려 들어왔다. 그들은 그 옛날의 음울한 감옥 마당에서나 다름없는 태도로 교회며 미술관을 어슬렁거렸다."], ["They prowled about the churches and picture-galleries, much in the old, dreary, prison-yard manner.", "그들은 으레 내일이나 다음 주면 다시 떠날 참이었고, 좀처럼 제 마음을 알지 못했으며, 하겠다던 일을 하는 일도, 가겠다던 곳으로 가는 일도 드물었다."], ["They were usually going away again to-morrow or next week, and rarely knew their own minds, and seldom did what they said they would do, or went where they said they would go: in all this again, very like the prison debtors.", "이 모든 점에서 또한 감옥의 채무자들과 무척이나 닮아 있었다. 그들은 변변찮은 숙소에 비싼 값을 치르면서도, 어느 곳을 좋아하는 척하면서 그곳을 헐뜯었다."], ["They paid high for poor accommodation, and disparaged a place while they pretended to like it: which was exactly the Marshalsea custom.", "이는 영락없는 마셜시의 풍습이었다. 그들이 떠날 때면, 떠나기 싫지 않은 척하며 뒤에 남는 이들이 그들을 부러워했다."], ["They were envied when they went away by people left behind, feigning not to want to go: and that again was the Marshalsea habit invariably.", "이 또한 한결같은 마셜시의 습성이었다. 대학과 스너거리가 그 감옥에 속하던 것만큼이나 여행객들에게 속한 일정한 어휘와 어구가 늘 그들의 입에 붙어 있었다."], ["A certain set of words and phrases, as much belonging to tourists as the College and the Snuggery belonged to the jail, was always in their mouths.", "그들은 죄수들이 그러했던 것과 똑같이 무엇 하나에도 진득하게 자리 잡지 못하는 무능을 보였다. 그들은 죄수들이 그러했듯이 서로를 오히려 망가뜨렸다."], ["They had precisely the same incapacity for settling down to anything, as the prisoners used to have; they rather deteriorated one another, as the prisoners used to do; and they wore untidy dresses, and fell into a slouching way of life: still, always like the people in the Marshalsea.", "그리고 그들은 단정치 못한 옷차림을 하고 흐트러진 생활 방식에 빠져들었다. 여전히, 한결같이 마셜시 사람들과 닮은 채로."]], [["The period of the family’s stay at Venice came, in its course, to an end, and they moved, with their retinue, to Rome.", "그 집안이 베네치아에 머무르던 기간도 어느덧 끝에 이르렀고, 그들은 수행원들을 거느린 채 로마로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Through a repetition of the former Italian scenes, growing more dirty and more haggard as they went on, and bringing them at length to where the very air was diseased, they passed to their destination.", "앞서 거쳐 온 이탈리아 풍경의 반복을 지나, 갈수록 더 지저분하고 더 황량해지는 그 길을 통해, 마침내 공기 그 자체가 병들어 있는 곳에까지 이르러, 그들은 목적지로 나아갔다."], ["A fine residence had been taken for them on the Corso, and there they took up their abode, in a city where everything seemed to be trying to stand still for ever on the ruins of something else--except the water, which, following eternal laws, tumbled and rolled from its glorious multitude of fountains.", "그들을 위해 코르소 가에 멋진 거처가 마련되어 있어, 그들은 거기에 거처를 잡았다. 그 도시에서는, 영원한 법칙을 따라 그 찬란히 무수한 분수에서 쏟아져 흐르고 굽이치는 물을 빼고는, 모든 것이 다른 무언가의 폐허 위에서 영원히 멈춰 서 있으려 애쓰는 듯 보였다."]], [["Here it seemed to Little Dorrit that a change came over the Marshalsea spirit of their society, and that Prunes and Prism got the upper hand.", "여기서 리틀 도릿이 보기에, 그들 사교계의 마셜시 기질에 변화가 찾아왔고, 자두와 프리즘이 우위를 점하게 되었다."], ["Everybody was walking about St Peter’s and the Vatican on somebody else’s cork legs, and straining every visible object through somebody else’s sieve.", "모두가 남의 코르크 의족을 짚고 성 베드로 대성당과 바티칸을 거닐었고, 눈에 보이는 모든 것을 남의 체로 걸러 냈다."], ["Nobody said what anything was, but everybody said what the Mrs Generals, Mr Eustace, or somebody else said it was.", "누구도 무엇이 어떻다고 스스로 말하지 않았고, 다들 제너럴 부인들이나 유스터스 씨나 그 밖의 누군가가 그것을 어떻다고 했다더라고만 말했다."], ["The whole body of travellers seemed to be a collection of voluntary human sacrifices, bound hand and foot, and delivered over to Mr Eustace and his attendants, to have the entrails of their intellects arranged according to the taste of that sacred priesthood.", "여행객의 무리 전체가 손발이 묶인 채 자진해 바쳐진 인간 제물들의 집합 같았으니, 유스터스 씨와 그 시종들에게 넘겨져, 그 신성한 사제단의 취향에 맞게 자기 지성의 내장을 정리당하는 처지였다."], ["Through the rugged remains of temples and tombs and palaces and senate halls and theatres and amphitheatres of ancient days, hosts of tongue-tied and blindfolded moderns were carefully feeling their way, incessantly repeating Prunes and Prism in the endeavour to set their lips according to the received form.", "옛 시절의 신전과 무덤과 궁전과 원로원과 극장과 원형 경기장의 울퉁불퉁한 잔해 사이를, 혀가 묶이고 눈이 가려진 현대인들의 무리가 조심조심 더듬어 길을 찾아가며, 정해진 형식에 맞게 입술을 모으려 애쓰느라 끊임없이 자두와 프리즘을 되뇌고 있었다. 제너럴 부인은 자기의 순수한 본령에 들어선 셈이었다."], ["Mrs General was in her pure element.", "누구도 자기 의견이라곤 없었다."], ["Nobody had an opinion. There was a formation of surface going on around her on an amazing scale, and it had not a flaw of courage or honest free speech in it.", "그녀 둘레로 놀라운 규모의 겉면 형성이 진행 중이었고, 거기에는 용기나 정직한 자유 발언이라곤 한 점의 흠집도 없었다."]], [["Another modification of Prunes and Prism insinuated itself on Little Dorrit’s notice very shortly after their arrival.", "자두와 프리즘의 또 다른 변형이 그들이 도착한 직후 곧 리틀 도릿의 눈에 슬며시 들어왔다."], ["They received an early visit from Mrs Merdle, who led that extensive department of life in the Eternal City that winter; and the skilful manner in which she and Fanny fenced with one another on the occasion, almost made her quiet sister wink, like the glittering of small-swords.", "그들은 머들 부인의 이른 방문을 받았는데, 그 부인은 그해 겨울 이 영원의 도시에서 그 광대한 사교 영역을 주름잡고 있었다. 그리고 그 자리에서 그녀와 패니가 서로 능란하게 칼을 맞대고 견주는 모습은, 그 조용한 동생의 눈을 거의 깜박이게 할 지경이었으니, 마치 작은 검들이 번뜩이는 듯했다."]], [["‘So delighted,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘to resume an acquaintance so inauspiciously begun at Martigny.’", "\"이렇게 기쁠 데가요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"마르티니에서 그토록 불길하게 시작된 인연을 다시 잇게 되다니요.\""]], [["‘At Martigny, of course,’ said Fanny.", "\"마르티니, 그렇고말고요,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘Charmed, I am sure!’", "\"정말이지, 황홀하답니다!\""]], [["‘I understand,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘from my son Edmund Sparkler, that he has already improved that chance occasion.", "\"제 아들 에드먼드 스파클러에게서 듣자니,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."], ["He has returned quite transported with Venice.’", "\"그 애가 그 우연한 기회를 벌써 잘 살렸다더군요. 베네치아에 아주 푹 빠져서 돌아왔답니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed?’ returned the careless Fanny.", "\"그래요?\" 무심한 패니가 받았다."], ["‘Was he there long?’", "\"오래 거기 있었나요?\""]], [["‘I might refer that question to Mr Dorrit,’ said Mrs Merdle, turning the bosom towards that gentleman; ‘Edmund having been so much indebted to him for rendering his stay agreeable.’", "\"그 질문은 도릿 씨께 돌려야 할까 보네요,\" 머들 부인이 그 가슴을 도릿 씨 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다. \"에드먼드가 그곳 체류를 즐겁게 보내도록 그분께 어찌나 큰 신세를 졌는지요.\""]], [["‘Oh, pray don’t speak of it,’ returned Fanny.", "\"오, 부디 그런 말씀 마십시오,\" 패니가 대꾸했다."], ["‘I believe Papa had the pleasure of inviting Mr Sparkler twice or thrice,--but it was nothing.", "\"아빠가 스파클러 씨를 두어 번 초대하는 기쁨을 누리신 것 같긴 하지만요—하지만 그건 아무것도 아니었어요."], ["We had so many people about us, and kept such open house, that if he had that pleasure, it was less than nothing.’", "저희 주변에 사람이 어찌나 많았고 손님을 어찌나 자유로이 맞았던지, 설령 아빠가 그런 기쁨을 누리셨다 한들, 그건 아무것도 아닌 것만도 못한 일이었답니다.\""]], [["‘Except, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘except--ha--as it afforded me unusual gratification to--hum--show by any means, however slight and worthless, the--ha, hum--high estimation in which, in--ha--common with the rest of the world, I hold so distinguished and princely a character as Mr Merdle’s.’", "\"다만, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"다만—하—머들 씨처럼 그토록 출중하고 제왕다운 인물에 대해—하, 흠—이 세상 나머지 사람들과—하—다름없이 내가 품고 있는 그 높은 평가를, 아무리 사소하고 보잘것없는 수단으로나마—흠—드러낼 수 있었다는 것이 나에게 유다른 기쁨을 안겨 주었던 만큼은 예외다만.\""]], [["The bosom received this tribute in its most engaging manner.", "그 가슴은 이 찬사를 더없이 매혹적인 태도로 받아들였다. \"머들 씨는 말이죠,\""], ["‘Mr Merdle,’ observed Fanny, as a means of dismissing Mr Sparkler into the background, ‘is quite a theme of Papa’s, you must know, Mrs Merdle.’", "패니가 스파클러 씨를 뒷전으로 물려 보내려는 수단으로 말했다. \"아셔야 할 게, 아빠가 늘 입에 올리시는 화제랍니다, 머들 부인.\""]], [["‘I have been--ha--disappointed, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘to understand from Mr Sparkler that there is no great--hum--probability of Mr Merdle’s coming abroad.’", "\"저는—하—실망했습니다, 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"스파클러 씨에게서 듣자니 머들 씨께서 외국으로 나오실—흠—가망이 그리 크지 않다고 하니 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Why, indeed,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘he is so much engaged and in such request, that I fear not.", "\"그러게나요, 정말,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."], ["He has not been able to get abroad for years.", "\"그이는 워낙 일에 매여 있고 찾는 데가 많아서, 안 될까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["You, Miss Dorrit, I believe have been almost continually abroad for a long time.’", "그이는 몇 해째 외국에 나오질 못했답니다. 도릿 양은, 제가 알기로, 오랫동안 거의 줄곧 외국에 계셨다지요.\""]], [["‘Oh dear yes,’ drawled Fanny, with the greatest hardihood.", "\"오 그럼요,\" 패니가 더없이 뻔뻔하게 늘어뜨려 말했다."], ["‘An immense number of years.’", "\"엄청나게 오랜 세월을요.\""]], [["‘So I should have inferred,’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"그러리라 짐작했어요,\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Exactly,’ said Fanny.", "\"그렇답니다,\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘I trust, however,’ resumed Mr Dorrit, ‘that if I have not the--hum--great advantage of becoming known to Mr Merdle on this side of the Alps or Mediterranean, I shall have that honour on returning to England.", "\"하지만 저는 바라건대,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["It is an honour I particularly desire and shall particularly esteem.’", "\"이쪽 알프스나 지중해 이편에서 머들 씨와 알고 지내는—흠—큰 영광을 누리지 못한다 하더라도, 영국으로 돌아가면 그 영광을 누리게 되기를요. 그것은 제가 각별히 바라고 또 각별히 귀히 여길 영광입니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Merdle,’ said Mrs Merdle, who had been looking admiringly at Fanny through her eye-glass, ‘will esteem it, I am sure, no less.’", "\"머들 씨도,\" 안경 너머로 패니를 흠모하듯 바라보고 있던 머들 부인이 말했다. \"분명 그에 못지않게 귀히 여길 거예요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit, still habitually thoughtful and solitary though no longer alone, at first supposed this to be mere Prunes and Prism.", "리틀 도릿은, 이제는 혼자가 아니었으나 여전히 버릇처럼 생각에 잠겨 외로이 지내던 터라, 처음에는 이것을 그저 자두와 프리즘이려니 여겼다."], ["But as her father when they had been to a brilliant reception at Mrs Merdle’s, harped at their own family breakfast-table on his wish to know Mr Merdle, with the contingent view of benefiting by the advice of that wonderful man in the disposal of his fortune, she began to think it had a real meaning, and to entertain a curiosity on her own part to see the shining light of the time.", "그러나 머들 부인 댁의 화려한 연회에 다녀온 뒤, 아버지가 제 집안의 아침 식탁에서까지 머들 씨와 알고 지내고 싶다는 바람을 거듭 읊으며, 그 비범한 인물의 조언을 빌려 자기 재산을 운용하는 데 덕을 볼지도 모른다는 속셈을 내비치자, 그녀는 거기에 실제 의미가 담겨 있다고 여기기 시작했고, 자기 나름으로도 이 시대의 그 빛나는 인물을 보고 싶다는 호기심을 품게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’", "# CHAPTER 8. The Dowager Mrs Gowan is reminded that ‘It Never Does’"]], [["While the waters of Venice and the ruins of Rome were sunning themselves for the pleasure of the Dorrit family, and were daily being sketched out of all earthly proportion, lineament, and likeness, by travelling pencils innumerable, the firm of Doyce and Clennam hammered away in Bleeding Heart Yard, and the vigorous clink of iron upon iron was heard there through the working hours.", "베네치아의 물결과 로마의 폐허가 도릿 일가의 즐거움을 위해 햇볕을 쬐고 있었고, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 여행자의 연필에 의해 날마다 온갖 지상의 비례와 윤곽과 닮음을 벗어난 모습으로 사생되고 있는 동안, 도이스 클레넘 상회는 블리딩 하트 야드에서 쉴 새 없이 망치질을 했고, 쇠와 쇠가 부딪는 힘찬 쟁그랑 소리가 작업 시간 내내 그곳에서 들려왔다."]], [["The younger partner had, by this time, brought the business into sound trim; and the elder, left free to follow his own ingenious devices, had done much to enhance the character of the factory.", "이 무렵 젊은 동업자는 사업을 견실한 궤도에 올려놓았고, 자기만의 독창적인 궁리를 자유로이 좇게 된 손위 동업자는 공장의 명성을 드높이는 데 크게 이바지했다."], ["As an ingenious man, he had necessarily to encounter every discouragement that the ruling powers for a length of time had been able by any means to put in the way of this class of culprits; but that was only reasonable self-defence in the powers, since How to do it must obviously be regarded as the natural and mortal enemy of How not to do it.", "독창적인 사람으로서 그는, 오랜 세월 동안 지배 권력이 어떤 수단으로든 이런 부류의 죄인들 앞길에 놓을 수 있었던 온갖 낙담거리를 마주할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 그것은 권력 측의 합당한 자기방어에 지나지 않았으니, 일을 하는 법은 일을 하지 않는 법의 자연스럽고도 불구대천의 적으로 여겨질 수밖에 없었기 때문이다."], ["In this was to be found the basis of the wise system, by tooth and nail upheld by the Circumlocution Office, of warning every ingenious British subject to be ingenious at his peril: of harassing him, obstructing him, inviting robbers (by making his remedy uncertain, and expensive) to plunder him, and at the best of confiscating his property after a short term of enjoyment, as though invention were on a par with felony.", "바로 여기에, 에두름국이 이를 악물고 손톱이 닳도록 떠받든 그 현명한 제도의 토대가 있었다 — 독창적인 영국 신민이라면 누구든 제 목숨을 걸고나 독창적이 되라고 경고하고, 그를 괴롭히고 방해하고, (그의 구제책을 불확실하고 비싸게 만듦으로써) 강도들을 불러들여 그를 약탈하게 하고, 잘해 봐야 짧은 향유 기간이 지난 뒤 그의 재산을 몰수하는 — 마치 발명이 중죄와 동급이라도 되는 양 말이다."], ["The system had uniformly found great favour with the Barnacles, and that was only reasonable, too; for one who worthily invents must be in earnest, and the Barnacles abhorred and dreaded nothing half so much.", "이 제도는 한결같이 배너클 일가의 큰 호의를 누렸고, 그 또한 합당한 일에 지나지 않았다. 가치 있게 발명하는 자는 반드시 진지하기 마련인데, 배너클 일가가 진지함만큼 혐오하고 두려워하는 것은 달리 없었기 때문이다."], ["That again was very reasonable; since in a country suffering under the affliction of a great amount of earnestness, there might, in an exceeding short space of time, be not a single Barnacle left sticking to a post.", "그것 역시 매우 합당했다. 막대한 양의 진지함이라는 재앙에 시달리는 나라에서라면, 머지않아 어떤 자리에든 들러붙어 있는 배너클이 단 하나도 남지 않게 될지도 모르는 일이니까."]], [["Daniel Doyce faced his condition with its pains and penalties attached to it, and soberly worked on for the work’s sake.", "대니얼 도이스는 거기에 딸린 고통과 형벌을 모두 떠안은 채 자신의 처지를 마주했고, 일 그 자체를 위해 묵묵히 일을 계속했다."], ["Clennam cheering him with a hearty co-operation, was a moral support to him, besides doing good service in his business relation.", "클레넘은 진심 어린 협력으로 그를 격려하며 정신적 지주가 되어 주었고, 그 밖에도 사업상의 관계에서 훌륭한 역할을 해냈다."], ["The concern prospered, and the partners were fast friends.", "사업은 번창했고, 두 동업자는 둘도 없는 벗이 되었다."]], [["But Daniel could not forget the old design of so many years.", "그러나 대니얼은 그토록 오랜 세월 품어 온 그 옛 설계를 잊을 수 없었다."], ["It was not in reason to be expected that he should; if he could have lightly forgotten it, he could never have conceived it, or had the patience and perseverance to work it out.", "그가 그것을 잊으리라 기대하는 것은 이치에 맞지 않았다. 만약 그가 그것을 가벼이 잊을 수 있는 사람이었다면, 애초에 그것을 착안하지도, 그것을 완성해 낼 인내와 끈기를 지니지도 못했을 것이다."], ["So Clennam thought, when he sometimes observed him of an evening looking over the models and drawings, and consoling himself by muttering with a sigh as he put them away again, that the thing was as true as it ever was.", "클레넘은 이따금 어느 저녁에 그가 모형과 도면을 들여다보다가, 다시 그것들을 치우며 한숨과 함께 그 물건이 예나 지금이나 변함없이 옳다고 중얼거리며 스스로를 위로하는 모습을 볼 때면 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["To show no sympathy with so much endeavour, and so much disappointment, would have been to fail in what Clennam regarded as among the implied obligations of his partnership.", "그토록 많은 노력과 그토록 큰 실망에 아무런 공감도 보이지 않는 것은, 클레넘이 동업 관계의 암묵적 의무 가운데 하나로 여기던 바를 저버리는 일이 될 터였다."], ["A revival of the passing interest in the subject which had been by chance awakened at the door of the Circumlocution Office, originated in this feeling.", "에두름국 문 앞에서 우연히 일깨워졌던 그 주제에 대한 일시적 관심이 되살아난 것은 이런 마음에서 비롯되었다."], ["He asked his partner to explain the invention to him; ‘having a lenient consideration,’ he stipulated, ‘for my being no workman, Doyce.’", "그는 동업자에게 그 발명을 설명해 달라고 청하면서, \"내가 직공이 아니라는 점을 너그러이 헤아려 주시오, 도이스\" 하고 단서를 달았다."]], [["‘No workman?’ said Doyce.", "\"직공이 아니라니요?\""], ["‘You would have been a thorough workman if you had given yourself to it.", "도이스가 말했다. \"당신이 마음만 먹었다면 완벽한 직공이 되었을 겁니다."], ["You have as good a head for understanding such things as I have met with.’", "이런 것들을 이해하는 머리로 말하자면, 내가 만나 본 누구 못지않게 좋은 머리를 가지셨소.\""]], [["‘A totally uneducated one, I am sorry to add,’ said Clennam.", "\"안타깝게도 전혀 교육받지 못한 머리지요.\" 클레넘이 덧붙였다."]], [["‘I don’t know that,’ returned Doyce, ‘and I wouldn’t have you say that.", "\"그건 모를 일이오.\" 도이스가 대답했다."], ["No man of sense who has been generally improved, and has improved himself, can be called quite uneducated as to anything.", "\"그리고 나는 당신이 그런 말을 하지 않았으면 합니다. 두루 향상되고 스스로를 향상시킨 분별 있는 사람이라면, 어떤 것에 대해서도 전혀 교육받지 못했다고 말할 수는 없는 법이지요."], ["I don’t particularly favour mysteries.", "나는 비밀스러운 것을 특별히 좋아하지 않소."], ["I would as soon, on a fair and clear explanation, be judged by one class of man as another, provided he had the qualification I have named.’", "공정하고 명료한 설명만 주어진다면, 내가 말한 자질을 갖춘 사람이기만 하다면, 어느 부류의 사람에게 판단받든 마찬가지요.\""]], [["‘At all events,’ said Clennam--‘this sounds as if we were exchanging compliments, but we know we are not--I shall have the advantage of as plain an explanation as can be given.’", "\"어쨌든,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"이건 마치 서로 치사를 주고받는 것처럼 들리지만, 우리 둘 다 그렇지 않다는 걸 알지요. 어쨌든 나는 가능한 한 평이한 설명을 듣는 이점을 누리게 되겠군요.\""]], [["‘Well!’ said Daniel, in his steady even way, ‘I’ll try to make it so.’", "\"그럼!\" 대니얼이 그 특유의 차분하고 한결같은 어조로 말했다. \"그렇게 되도록 애써 보겠소.\""]], [["He had the power, often to be found in union with such a character, of explaining what he himself perceived, and meant, with the direct force and distinctness with which it struck his own mind.", "그는 그런 성격의 사람에게서 흔히 발견되는 능력, 곧 자기 자신이 인식하고 의도한 바를, 그것이 자기 마음에 와닿은 그 직접적인 힘과 또렷함 그대로 설명해 내는 능력을 지니고 있었다."], ["His manner of demonstration was so orderly and neat and simple, that it was not easy to mistake him.", "그의 시연 방식은 어찌나 정연하고 깔끔하고 단순한지, 그를 오해하기란 쉽지 않았다."], ["There was something almost ludicrous in the complete irreconcilability of a vague conventional notion that he must be a visionary man, with the precise, sagacious travelling of his eye and thumb over the plans, their patient stoppages at particular points, their careful returns to other points whence little channels of explanation had to be traced up, and his steady manner of making everything good and everything sound at each important stage, before taking his hearer on a line’s-breadth further.", "그가 몽상가일 것이라는 막연하고 통념적인 관념과, 도면 위를 옮겨 다니는 그의 눈과 엄지의 정확하고 명민한 움직임, 특정 지점에서의 끈기 있는 멈춤, 설명의 작은 물줄기들을 거슬러 짚어 가야 할 다른 지점들로의 신중한 되돌아감, 그리고 중요한 단계마다 듣는 이를 한 줄 너비라도 더 앞으로 데려가기에 앞서 모든 것을 옳고 견실하게 다져 놓는 그 한결같은 태도 — 이 둘의 완전한 양립 불가능함에는 거의 우스꽝스러운 데가 있었다. 그가 자신의 설명에서 스스로를 지워 버리는 태도 또한 그에 못지않게 주목할 만했다."], ["His dismissal of himself from his description, was hardly less remarkable.", "그는 결코 내가 이런 응용을 발견했다거나 저런 조합을 발명했다고 말하지 않았다."], ["He never said, I discovered this adaptation or invented that combination; but showed the whole thing as if the Divine artificer had made it, and he had happened to find it; so modest he was about it, such a pleasant touch of respect was mingled with his quiet admiration of it, and so calmly convinced he was that it was established on irrefragable laws.", "오히려 그것을 마치 신성한 장인이 만들어 놓은 것을 자기가 우연히 찾아낸 것처럼 통째로 보여 주었다. 그만큼 그는 그것에 관해 겸손했고, 그것을 향한 조용한 감탄에는 그토록 흐뭇한 경의의 빛이 어려 있었으며, 그것이 반박할 수 없는 법칙 위에 세워졌다고 그토록 차분히 확신하고 있었다."]], [["Not only that evening, but for several succeeding evenings, Clennam was quite charmed by this investigation.", "그날 저녁뿐만 아니라 그 뒤로 며칠 저녁 동안, 클레넘은 이 탐구에 완전히 매료되었다."], ["The more he pursued it, and the oftener he glanced at the grey head bending over it, and the shrewd eye kindling with pleasure in it and love of it--instrument for probing his heart though it had been made for twelve long years--the less he could reconcile it to his younger energy to let it go without one effort more.", "그것을 좇으면 좇을수록, 그 위로 숙인 잿빛 머리와 그 일에 대한 기쁨과 애정으로 빛나는 그 명민한 눈 — 비록 그것은 열두 해라는 긴 세월 동안 그의 마음을 후벼 파는 도구가 되어 왔지만 — 을 자주 흘끗거릴수록, 한 번 더 애써 보지도 않고 그것을 놓아 버리는 일을 자신의 젊은 혈기와 화해시키기가 점점 어려워졌다."], ["At length he said:", "마침내 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Doyce, it came to this at last--that the business was to be sunk with Heaven knows how many more wrecks, or begun all over again?’", "\"도이스, 결국 이렇게 된 거로군요 — 그 사업을, 하늘만이 아는 숱한 난파선들과 함께 가라앉히거나, 아니면 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작하거나?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ returned Doyce, ‘that’s what the noblemen and gentlemen made of it after a dozen years.’", "\"그렇소.\" 도이스가 대답했다. \"그게 바로 그 귀족 나리들과 신사 양반들이 열두 해 만에 만들어 낸 결론이었지요.\""]], [["‘And pretty fellows too!’ said Clennam, bitterly.", "\"꽤나 잘난 양반들이로군요!\" 클레넘이 쓰라리게 말했다."]], [["‘The usual thing!’ observed Doyce.", "\"흔한 일이지요!\" 도이스가 말했다."], ["‘I must not make a martyr of myself, when I am one of so large a company.’", "\"이렇게 많은 무리 가운데 하나인 처지에, 내 스스로를 순교자로 만들 수야 없지요.\""]], [["‘Relinquish it, or begin it all over again?’ mused Clennam.", "\"포기하거나, 아니면 모든 걸 처음부터 다시 시작하거나라.\" 클레넘이 곰곰이 되뇌었다."]], [["‘That was exactly the long and the short of it,’ said Doyce.", "\"바로 그게 요점이고 전부였소.\" 도이스가 말했다."]], [["‘Then, my friend,’ cried Clennam, starting up and taking his work-roughened hand, ‘it shall be begun all over again!’", "\"그렇다면, 친구여,\" 클레넘이 벌떡 일어나 그의 노동으로 거칠어진 손을 잡으며 외쳤다. \"처음부터 다시 시작합시다!\""]], [["Doyce looked alarmed, and replied in a hurry--for him, ‘No, no.", "도이스는 깜짝 놀란 표정을 짓더니, 그로서는 보기 드물게 황급히 대답했다."], ["Better put it by.", "\"아니, 아니오."], ["Far better put it by.", "그냥 치워 두는 게 낫소."], ["It will be heard of, one day.", "훨씬 낫지요. 언젠가는 세상이 알아줄 거요."], ["I can put it by.", "나는 그걸 치워 둘 수 있소."], ["You forget, my good Clennam; I _have_ put it by.", "잊으셨군요, 친애하는 클레넘. 나는 이미 그걸 치워 두었소."], ["It’s all at an end.’", "다 끝난 일이오.\""]], [["‘Yes, Doyce,’ returned Clennam, ‘at an end as far as your efforts and rebuffs are concerned, I admit, but not as far as mine are.", "\"그렇소, 도이스.\""], ["I am younger than you: I have only once set foot in that precious office, and I am fresh game for them.", "클레넘이 대답했다. \"당신의 노력과 퇴짜에 관한 한 끝난 일이라는 건 나도 인정하지요."], ["Come!", "하지만 내 노력에 관해선 아니오."], ["I’ll try them.", "나는 당신보다 젊고, 그 잘난 관청에 발을 들인 게 딱 한 번뿐이라 그들에게는 신선한 먹잇감이지요. 자!"], ["You shall do exactly as you have been doing since we have been together.", "내가 그들을 한번 시험해 보겠소. 당신은 우리가 함께한 이래 해 오던 그대로만 하시오."], ["I will add (as I easily can) to what I have been doing, the attempt to get public justice done to you; and, unless I have some success to report, you shall hear no more of it.’", "나는 (얼마든지 할 수 있는 일이니) 지금까지 내가 해 오던 일에다, 당신을 위해 공공의 정의를 받아 내려는 시도를 보태겠소. 그래서 보고할 만한 성과가 없거든, 다시는 이 이야기를 꺼내지 않으리다.\""]], [["Daniel Doyce was still reluctant to consent, and again and again urged that they had better put it by.", "대니얼 도이스는 여전히 동의하기를 꺼렸고, 그냥 치워 두는 편이 낫다고 거듭거듭 우겼다."], ["But it was natural that he should gradually allow himself to be over-persuaded by Clennam, and should yield.", "그러나 그가 점차 클레넘의 설득에 못 이겨 양보하게 된 것은 자연스러운 일이었다. 그는 끝내 양보했다."], ["Yield he did. So Arthur resumed the long and hopeless labour of striving to make way with the Circumlocution Office.", "그리하여 아서는 에두름국을 상대로 길을 뚫어 보려는, 그 길고도 가망 없는 노고를 다시 시작했다."]], [["The waiting-rooms of that Department soon began to be familiar with his presence, and he was generally ushered into them by its janitors much as a pickpocket might be shown into a police-office; the principal difference being that the object of the latter class of public business is to keep the pickpocket, while the Circumlocution object was to get rid of Clennam.", "그 부서의 대기실들은 곧 그의 출입에 익숙해지기 시작했고, 그는 대개 그곳 수위들에 의해 마치 소매치기가 경찰서로 끌려가듯 그 안으로 안내되었다. 다만 주된 차이라면, 후자의 공무는 소매치기를 붙잡아 두는 것이 목적인 데 반해, 에두름국의 목적은 클레넘을 떼어 내는 것이었다는 점이다."], ["However, he was resolved to stick to the Great Department; and so the work of form-filling, corresponding, minuting, memorandum-making, signing, counter-signing, counter-counter-signing, referring backwards and forwards, and referring sideways, crosswise, and zig-zag, recommenced.", "그러나 그는 그 위대한 부서에 끈질기게 들러붙기로 작심했다. 그리하여 양식 작성, 서신 왕래, 의사록 작성, 비망록 작성, 서명, 부서명, 부부서명, 앞뒤로의 회부, 그리고 옆으로, 가로로, 지그재그로의 회부 작업이 다시 시작되었다."]], [["Here arises a feature of the Circumlocution Office, not previously mentioned in the present record.", "여기서 에두름국의 한 특징이 떠오르는데, 이 기록에서 아직 언급되지 않은 것이다."], ["When that admirable Department got into trouble, and was, by some infuriated members of Parliament whom the smaller Barnacles almost suspected of labouring under diabolic possession, attacked on the merits of no individual case, but as an Institution wholly abominable and Bedlamite; then the noble or right honourable Barnacle who represented it in the House, would smite that member and cleave him asunder, with a statement of the quantity of business (for the prevention of business) done by the Circumlocution Office.", "그 감탄스러운 부서가 곤경에 처했을 때, 그러니까 작은 배너클들이 거의 악마에 들린 게 아닌가 의심할 지경인 어느 격분한 국회의원들에게, 개별 사건의 시비를 두고서가 아니라 통째로 가증스럽고 정신병원 같은 기관이라며 공격당했을 때, 그러면 하원에서 그 부서를 대표하는 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클이 그 의원을 후려쳐 두 동강 내곤 했으니, 에두름국이 (업무 방지를 위해) 처리한 업무량의 진술로써 그렇게 했다."], ["Then would that noble or right honourable Barnacle hold in his hand a paper containing a few figures, to which, with the permission of the House, he would entreat its attention.", "그러면 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 몇 개의 수치가 적힌 종이 한 장을 손에 들고, 하원의 허락을 얻어 그것에 주목해 줄 것을 간청하곤 했다. 그러면 하급 배너클들이 명령에 따라 \"옳소, 옳소, 옳소!\""], ["Then would the inferior Barnacles exclaim, obeying orders, ‘Hear, Hear, Hear!’ and ‘Read!’ Then would the noble or right honourable Barnacle perceive, sir, from this little document, which he thought might carry conviction even to the perversest mind (Derisive laughter and cheering from the Barnacle fry), that within the short compass of the last financial half-year, this much-maligned Department (Cheers) had written and received fifteen thousand letters (Loud cheers), had written twenty-four thousand minutes (Louder cheers), and thirty-two thousand five hundred and seventeen memoranda (Vehement cheering).", "그리고 \"읽으시오!\"라고 외쳐 댔다. 그러면 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 이렇게 인지하곤 했으니, 의장 각하, 가장 비뚤어진 정신에까지도 확신을 안겨 주리라 여겨지는 이 작은 문서로부터 (배너클 송사리들로부터 조롱 섞인 폭소와 환호) 알 수 있는바, 지난 회계 반년이라는 짧은 기간 안에 이 숱한 비방을 받아 온 부서가 (환호) 일만 오천 통의 편지를 쓰고 받았으며 (큰 환호), 이만 사천 건의 의사록을 작성했고 (더 큰 환호), 삼만 이천 오백 십칠 건의 비망록을 작성했다는 (격렬한 환호) 것이었다."], ["Nay, an ingenious gentleman connected with the Department, and himself a valuable public servant, had done him the favour to make a curious calculation of the amount of stationery consumed in it during the same period.", "아니, 이 부서에 관계된 어느 독창적인 신사이자 그 자신이 귀중한 공복이기도 한 분이, 같은 기간 그 안에서 소비된 문구류의 양을 흥미롭게 계산해 주는 호의를 베풀었다."], ["It formed a part of this same short document; and he derived from it the remarkable fact that the sheets of foolscap paper it had devoted to the public service would pave the footways on both sides of Oxford Street from end to end, and leave nearly a quarter of a mile to spare for the park (Immense cheering and laughter); while of tape--red tape--it had used enough to stretch, in graceful festoons, from Hyde Park Corner to the General Post Office.", "그것 또한 바로 이 작은 문서의 일부였는데, 그로부터 그는 다음과 같은 주목할 만한 사실을 끌어냈다 — 이 부서가 공무에 바친 대판 양지의 낱장들을 깔면 옥스퍼드가 양쪽 보도를 끝에서 끝까지 포장하고도 거의 사분의 일 마일이 공원 몫으로 남으리라는 것이며 (엄청난 환호와 폭소), 끈으로 말하자면 — 붉은 끈으로 말하자면 — 우아한 꽃 줄 모양으로 하이드 파크 코너에서 중앙우체국까지 늘일 만큼 충분히 썼다는 것이었다."], ["Then, amidst a burst of official exultation, would the noble or right honourable Barnacle sit down, leaving the mutilated fragments of the Member on the field.", "그런 다음, 관변의 환희가 터져 나오는 가운데, 그 고귀하신 혹은 영예로우신 배너클은 그 의원의 토막 난 잔해를 전장에 남겨 둔 채 자리에 앉곤 했다."], ["No one, after that exemplary demolition of him, would have the hardihood to hint that the more the Circumlocution Office did, the less was done, and that the greatest blessing it could confer on an unhappy public would be to do nothing.", "그토록 모범적인 박살을 당한 뒤로는, 에두름국이 일을 많이 하면 할수록 정작 이루어지는 일은 적어진다든가, 불행한 대중에게 그것이 베풀 수 있는 최대의 축복은 아무 일도 하지 않는 것이라든가 하는 암시를 감히 내비칠 만한 배짱을 가진 자가 아무도 없었다."]], [["With sufficient occupation on his hands, now that he had this additional task--such a task had many and many a serviceable man died of before his day--Arthur Clennam led a life of slight variety.", "이제 이 추가 임무까지 떠안아 손에 일이 넉넉해진 — 그런 임무로 말하자면, 그의 시대 이전에 숱하게 많은 유능한 사람들이 그 때문에 목숨을 잃었던 그런 임무였다 — 아서 클레넘은 별다른 변화 없는 삶을 살았다."], ["Regular visits to his mother’s dull sick room, and visits scarcely less regular to Mr Meagles at Twickenham, were its only changes during many months.", "어머니의 음울한 병실로의 정기적인 방문과, 트위크넘의 미글스 씨를 찾는 그에 못지않게 정기적인 방문이, 여러 달 동안 그 삶의 유일한 변화였다."]], [["He sadly and sorely missed Little Dorrit.", "그는 슬프고도 사무치게 리틀 도릿을 그리워했다."], ["He had been prepared to miss her very much, but not so much.", "그녀가 무척 그리울 것이라고 각오는 하고 있었으나, 이토록 그리울 줄은 몰랐다."], ["He knew to the full extent only through experience, what a large place in his life was left blank when her familiar little figure went out of it.", "그녀의 정겹고 자그마한 모습이 그의 삶에서 사라졌을 때 얼마나 큰 자리가 텅 비게 되는지를, 그는 오직 겪어 봄으로써 비로소 온전히 알게 되었다."], ["He felt, too, that he must relinquish the hope of its return, understanding the family character sufficiently well to be assured that he and she were divided by a broad ground of separation.", "또한 그는 그녀가 돌아오리라는 희망을 단념해야 한다고 느꼈으니, 그 가족의 성격을 충분히 잘 이해한 터라 그와 그녀가 넓은 단절의 들판으로 갈라져 있음을 확신했기 때문이다."], ["The old interest he had had in her, and her old trusting reliance on him, were tinged with melancholy in his mind: so soon had change stolen over them, and so soon had they glided into the past with other secret tendernesses.", "그가 그녀에게 품었던 옛 관심과, 그를 향한 그녀의 옛 신뢰는 그의 마음속에서 우울한 빛으로 물들어 있었다. 그토록 빨리 변화가 그것들 위로 몰래 깃들었고, 그토록 빨리 그것들은 다른 은밀한 애틋함과 더불어 과거 속으로 미끄러져 갔던 것이다."]], [["When he received her letter he was greatly moved, but did not the less sensibly feel that she was far divided from him by more than distance.", "그녀의 편지를 받았을 때 그는 크게 감동했으나, 그렇다고 그녀가 거리 이상의 무언가로 자기와 멀리 갈라져 있다는 것을 덜 절실히 느낀 것은 아니었다."], ["It helped him to a clearer and keener perception of the place assigned him by the family.", "그 편지는 그 가족이 자기에게 할당한 자리를 더 또렷하고 예리하게 깨닫게 해 주었다."], ["He saw that he was cherished in her grateful remembrance secretly, and that they resented him with the jail and the rest of its belongings.", "그는 자신이 그녀의 감사 어린 기억 속에 은밀히 소중히 간직되어 있다는 것, 그리고 그들이 그 감옥과 거기에 딸린 나머지 모든 것과 함께 자기에게 원한을 품고 있다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Through all these meditations which every day of his life crowded about her, he thought of her otherwise in the old way.", "그의 삶의 하루하루마다 그녀를 둘러싸고 몰려드는 이 모든 사색을 거치면서도, 그는 여전히 옛 방식대로 그녀를 다르게 생각했다."], ["She was his innocent friend, his delicate child, his dear Little Dorrit.", "그녀는 그의 순결한 벗이요, 그의 가냘픈 아이요, 그의 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿이었다."], ["This very change of circumstances fitted curiously in with the habit, begun on the night when the roses floated away, of considering himself as a much older man than his years really made him.", "바로 이 처지의 변화가, 장미꽃이 떠내려가던 그날 밤 시작된 습관 — 자신을 실제 나이보다 훨씬 더 늙은 사람으로 여기는 습관 — 과 묘하게 맞아떨어졌다."], ["He regarded her from a point of view which in its remoteness, tender as it was, he little thought would have been unspeakable agony to her.", "그는 어떤 시점에서 그녀를 바라보았는데, 그 시점은 비록 다정하긴 했으나 그 아득함으로 말미암아 그녀에게는 이루 말할 수 없는 고통이 되리라는 것을 그는 거의 생각하지 못했다."], ["He speculated about her future destiny, and about the husband she might have, with an affection for her which would have drained her heart of its dearest drop of hope, and broken it.", "그는 그녀의 미래의 운명에 관하여, 그리고 그녀가 맞이할 남편에 관하여 헤아려 보았는데, 그것은 그녀를 향한 애정에서 비롯된 것이었으되 만일 그녀가 그것을 알았더라면 그녀의 마음에서 희망의 가장 소중한 한 방울까지 말려 버리고 그 마음을 부수어 버렸을 그런 애정이었다."]], [["Everything about him tended to confirm him in the custom of looking on himself as an elderly man, from whom such aspirations as he had combated in the case of Minnie Gowan (though that was not so long ago either, reckoning by months and seasons), were finally departed.", "그를 둘러싼 모든 것이, 그가 자신을 노년의 남자로 — 미니 가우언의 경우에 그가 떨쳐 내려 애썼던 그런 갈망 따위는 (그 일도 달과 계절로 따지자면 그리 오래된 것은 아니었지만) 이미 영영 떠나 버린 그런 남자로 — 바라보는 습관을 굳히는 쪽으로 작용했다."], ["His relations with her father and mother were like those on which a widower son-in-law might have stood.", "그녀의 아버지, 어머니와 맺은 관계는 마치 사위였다가 홀아비가 된 사람이 섰을 법한 그런 관계와도 같았다."], ["If the twin sister who was dead had lived to pass away in the bloom of womanhood, and he had been her husband, the nature of his intercourse with Mr and Mrs Meagles would probably have been just what it was.", "만일 죽은 쌍둥이 자매가 살아남아 여인의 한창때에 세상을 떠났고 그가 그녀의 남편이었더라면, 미글스 씨 부부와의 교제의 성격은 아마 지금 그대로였을 것이다."], ["This imperceptibly helped to render habitual the impression within him, that he had done with, and dismissed that part of life.", "이것이 알게 모르게, 자신은 인생의 그 부분과는 끝을 냈고 떨쳐 버렸다는 그의 내면의 인상을 습관으로 만드는 데 일조했다."]], [["He invariably heard of Minnie from them, as telling them in her letters how happy she was, and how she loved her husband; but inseparable from that subject, he invariably saw the old cloud on Mr Meagles’s face.", "그는 그들에게서 변함없이 미니의 소식을 들었으니, 그녀가 편지에 자신이 얼마나 행복한지, 남편을 얼마나 사랑하는지를 적어 보낸다는 것이었다. 그러나 그 화제와 떼려야 뗄 수 없이, 그는 변함없이 미글스 씨의 얼굴에 어린 그 옛 그늘을 보았다."], ["Mr Meagles had never been quite so radiant since the marriage as before.", "미글스 씨는 결혼 이후로 예전만큼 환한 적이 없었다."], ["He had never quite recovered the separation from Pet.", "펫과의 이별에서 끝내 완전히 회복되지 못했던 것이다."], ["He was the same good-humoured, open creature; but as if his face, from being much turned towards the pictures of his two children which could show him only one look, unconsciously adopted a characteristic from them, it always had now, through all its changes of expression, a look of loss in it.", "그는 여전히 한결같이 쾌활하고 솔직한 사람이었다. 그러나 마치 그의 얼굴이, 오직 한 가지 표정밖에 보여 줄 수 없는 두 아이의 초상을 하도 자주 향하다 보니 무의식중에 거기서 한 가지 특징을 따 온 것처럼, 그 얼굴에는 이제 온갖 표정의 변화를 거치면서도 늘 상실의 빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["One wintry Saturday when Clennam was at the cottage, the Dowager Mrs Gowan drove up, in the Hampton Court equipage which pretended to be the exclusive equipage of so many individual proprietors.", "어느 겨울날 토요일, 클레넘이 그 별장에 있을 때, 미망인 가우언 부인이 마차를 타고 당도했는데, 그것은 햄프턴 코트의 그토록 많은 개별 소유자들이 저마다 자기 전용 마차라고 우기는 그 마차였다."], ["She descended, in her shady ambuscade of green fan, to favour Mr and Mrs Meagles with a call.", "그녀는 초록 부채의 그늘진 매복 속에서 내려와, 미글스 씨 부부에게 방문의 은혜를 베풀었다."]], [["‘And how do you both do, Papa and Mama Meagles?’ said she, encouraging her humble connections.", "\"그래, 두 분 다 어떻게 지내셨나요, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님?\" 그녀가 자신의 미천한 인척들을 격려하듯 말했다."], ["‘And when did you last hear from or about my poor fellow?’", "\"그래, 내 가엾은 아이의 소식이나 그 애에 관한 소식을 마지막으로 들으신 게 언제인가요?\""]], [["My poor fellow was her son; and this mode of speaking of him politely kept alive, without any offence in the world, the pretence that he had fallen a victim to the Meagles’ wiles.", "내 가엾은 아이란 그녀의 아들이었고, 이렇게 그를 점잖게 일컫는 방식은, 그가 미글스 일가의 술수에 희생되었다는 가식을, 세상 어디에도 거슬릴 데 없이 살려 두는 것이었다."]], [["‘And the dear pretty one?’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"그리고 그 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아이는요?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Have you later news of her than I have?’", "\"내가 아는 것보다 더 최근 소식을 가지고 계신가요?\""]], [["Which also delicately implied that her son had been captured by mere beauty, and under its fascination had forgone all sorts of worldly advantages.", "이 또한 미묘하게, 자기 아들이 한낱 미모에 사로잡혀, 그 매혹 아래 온갖 세속적 이득을 다 포기했다는 것을 넌지시 비추는 것이었다."]], [["‘I am sure,’ said Mrs Gowan, without straining her attention on the answers she received, ‘it’s an unspeakable comfort to know they continue happy.", "\"정말이지,\" 가우언 부인이, 자기가 받는 대답에는 그다지 주의를 기울이지도 않으면서 말했다."], ["My poor fellow is of such a restless disposition, and has been so used to roving about, and to being inconstant and popular among all manner of people, that it’s the greatest comfort in life.", "\"그 애들이 여전히 행복하다는 걸 아는 건 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이에요. 내 가엾은 아이는 워낙 들썩이는 성미인 데다, 여기저기 떠돌아다니고 온갖 부류의 사람들 사이에서 변덕스럽고 인기 있는 데 익숙했던 터라, 이건 인생 최대의 위안이지요."], ["I suppose they’re as poor as mice, Papa Meagles?’", "그 애들은 생쥐만큼이나 가난하겠지요, 미글스 아버님?\""]], [["Mr Meagles, fidgety under the question, replied, ‘I hope not, ma’am.", "미글스 씨는 그 질문에 안절부절못하며 대답했다. \"그러지 않기를 바랍니다, 부인."], ["I hope they will manage their little income.’", "그 애들이 자기네 적은 수입을 잘 꾸려 가기를 바라지요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 내 가장 친애하는 미글스 씨!\""], ["my dearest Meagles!’ returned the lady, tapping him on the arm with the green fan and then adroitly interposing it between a yawn and the company, ‘how can you, as a man of the world and one of the most business-like of human beings--for you know you are business-like, and a great deal too much for us who are not--’", "부인이 초록 부채로 그의 팔을 톡톡 치고는, 능란하게 그것을 하품과 좌중 사이에 끼워 넣으며 대꾸했다. \"세상 물정에 밝으신 분이자 인간들 중에서도 가장 사업가다운 분이신 당신이 — 당신은 사업가다우시잖아요, 게다가 사업가답지 못한 우리에게는 너무 과할 만큼 그러시니까요 —\""]], [["(Which went to the former purpose, by making Mr Meagles out to be an artful schemer.)", "(이는 미글스 씨를 교활한 책략가로 만들어 버림으로써 앞서의 의도를 다시 좇은 것이었다.)"]], [["‘--How can you talk about their managing their little means?", "\"— 어떻게 그 애들이 그 알량한 살림을 꾸려 간다는 말씀을 하실 수가 있어요?"], ["My poor dear fellow!", "내 가엾고 사랑스러운 아이라니!"], ["The idea of his managing hundreds!", "그 애가 수백 파운드를 꾸려 간다는 생각이라니!"], ["And the sweet pretty creature too.", "게다가 그 사랑스럽고 예쁜 아이까지요."], ["The notion of her managing!", "그 애가 살림을 꾸린다는 발상이라니!"], ["Papa Meagles!", "미글스 아버님!"], ["Don’t!’", "그러지 마세요!\""]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, gravely, ‘I am sorry to admit, then, that Henry certainly does anticipate his means.’", "\"그렇군요, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그렇다면 유감스럽지만 헨리가 분명 제 수입을 미리 당겨 쓰고 있다는 점은 인정해야겠군요.\""]], [["‘My dear good man--I use no ceremony with you, because we are a kind of relations;--positively, Mama Meagles,’ exclaimed Mrs Gowan cheerfully, as if the absurd coincidence then flashed upon her for the first time, ‘a kind of relations! My dear good man, in this world none of us can have _everything_ our own way.’", "\"내 친애하고 선량한 양반 — 우리는 일종의 친척이니 격식은 차리지 않겠어요. 정말이지, 미글스 어머님,\" 가우언 부인이, 마치 그 어처구니없는 우연이 그제야 처음으로 머릿속에 번뜩 떠오르기라도 한 듯 명랑하게 외쳤다. \"일종의 친척이라니까요! 내 친애하고 선량한 양반, 이 세상에서는 우리 누구도 모든 일을 제 뜻대로 가질 수는 없는 법이지요.\""]], [["This again went to the former point, and showed Mr Meagles with all good breeding that, so far, he had been brilliantly successful in his deep designs.", "이 또한 앞서의 요점을 다시 좇은 것으로, 미글스 씨가 거기까지는 그 깊은 책략으로 눈부신 성공을 거두었음을, 더없이 점잖게 그에게 일러 주는 것이었다."], ["Mrs Gowan thought the hit so good a one, that she dwelt upon it; repeating ‘Not _everything_.", "가우언 부인은 그 한 방이 어찌나 잘 들어맞았다고 여겼는지 거기에 머물러 곱씹었으니, \"모든 일은 아니지요. 아니고말고요."], ["No, no; in this world we must not expect _everything_, Papa Meagles.’", "이 세상에서 우리는 모든 일을 기대해서는 안 되지요, 미글스 아버님\" 하고 되뇌었다."]], [["‘And may I ask, ma’am,’ retorted Mr Meagles, a little heightened in colour, ‘who does expect everything?’", "\"여쭤봐도 되겠습니까, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 얼굴빛이 약간 붉어진 채 받아쳤다. \"대체 누가 모든 일을 기대한다는 겁니까?\""]], [["‘Oh, nobody, nobody!’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"오, 아무도, 아무도 안 하지요!\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["‘I was going to say--but you put me out.", "\"내가 말하려던 건 — 그런데 당신이 내 말을 끊으셨군요."], ["You interrupting Papa, what was I going to say?’", "아버님이 끼어드시는 바람에, 내가 무슨 말을 하려고 했더라?\""]], [["Drooping her large green fan, she looked musingly at Mr Meagles while she thought about it; a performance not tending to the cooling of that gentleman’s rather heated spirits.", "그녀는 그 커다란 초록 부채를 늘어뜨린 채, 그것을 곰곰이 생각하며 미글스 씨를 골똘히 바라보았는데, 그 행동은 다소 달아오른 그 신사의 심기를 식히는 데 도움이 되지는 않았다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Yes, to be sure!’ said Mrs Gowan.", "그래요, 그렇지!\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["‘You must remember that my poor fellow has always been accustomed to expectations.", "\"내 가엾은 아이가 늘 기대 속에서 살아 온 데 익숙하다는 걸 당신은 기억하셔야 해요."], ["They may have been realised, or they may not have been realised--’", "그 기대가 실현되었을 수도 있고, 실현되지 않았을 수도 있고 —\""]], [["‘Let us say, then, may not have been realised,’ observed Mr Meagles.", "\"그렇다면, 실현되지 않았을 수도 있다고 해 두지요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["The Dowager for a moment gave him an angry look; but tossed it off with her head and her fan, and pursued the tenor of her way in her former manner.", "미망인은 한순간 그에게 성난 눈길을 던졌으나, 머리와 부채를 흔들어 그것을 떨쳐 버리고는, 앞서의 방식대로 자기 이야기의 가락을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘It makes no difference.", "\"그건 아무 차이도 없어요."], ["My poor fellow has been accustomed to that sort of thing, and of course you knew it, and were prepared for the consequences.", "내 가엾은 아이는 그런 부류의 것에 익숙했고, 물론 당신도 그걸 알고 그 결과에 대비하고 계셨겠지요."], ["I myself always clearly foresaw the consequences, and am not surprised.", "나 자신은 늘 그 결과를 분명히 내다보았고, 그래서 놀라지 않아요."], ["And you must not be surprised.", "그러니 당신도 놀라셔서는 안 돼요."], ["In fact, can’t be surprised.", "사실, 놀라실 수가 없지요."], ["Must have been prepared for it.’", "분명 그에 대비하고 계셨을 테니까요.\""]], [["Mr Meagles looked at his wife and at Clennam; bit his lip; and coughed.", "미글스 씨는 아내를, 그리고 클레넘을 바라보더니, 입술을 깨물고는 헛기침을 했다."]], [["‘And now here’s my poor fellow,’ Mrs Gowan pursued, ‘receiving notice that he is to hold himself in expectation of a baby, and all the expenses attendant on such an addition to his family!", "\"그런데 이제 내 가엾은 아이가,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["Poor Henry!", "\"아기를 기대해야 한다는 통지를, 그리고 식구가 그렇게 하나 늘어나는 데 따르는 온갖 비용을 떠안아야 한다는 통지를 받았다는군요! 가엾은 헨리!"], ["But it can’t be helped now; it’s too late to help it now.", "하지만 이제는 어쩔 수가 없어요. 이제 와서는 손쓰기엔 너무 늦었지요."], ["Only don’t talk of anticipating means, Papa Meagles, as a discovery; because that would be too much.’", "다만 수입을 미리 당겨 쓴다는 이야기를 무슨 대단한 발견인 양 하지는 마세요, 미글스 아버님. 그건 너무 과한 일일 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Too much, ma’am?’ said Mr Meagles, as seeking an explanation.", "\"과하다니요, 부인?\" 미글스 씨가 설명을 구하듯 말했다."]], [["‘There, there!’ said Mrs Gowan, putting him in his inferior place with an expressive action of her hand.", "\"자, 자!\" 가우언 부인이 손을 의미심장하게 놀려 그를 자기보다 낮은 자리에 앉히며 말했다."], ["‘Too much for my poor fellow’s mother to bear at this time of day.", "\"내 가엾은 아이의 어미가 이 나이에 견디기엔 너무 과한 일이지요."], ["They are fast married, and can’t be unmarried.", "그 애들은 단단히 결혼했고, 결혼을 무를 수도 없어요."], ["There, there!", "자, 자!"], ["I know that!", "그건 나도 알아요!"], ["You needn’t tell me that, Papa Meagles.", "그걸 굳이 일러 주실 필요 없어요, 미글스 아버님."], ["I know it very well.", "나도 아주 잘 알고 있으니까요."], ["What was it I said just now?", "내가 방금 뭐라고 했지요?"], ["That it was a great comfort they continued happy.", "그 애들이 여전히 행복하다는 게 큰 위안이라고 했지요."], ["It is to be hoped they will still continue happy.", "부디 그 애들이 앞으로도 계속 행복하기를 바랄 뿐이에요."], ["It is to be hoped Pretty One will do everything she can to make my poor fellow happy, and keep him contented.", "부디 그 예쁜 아이가 내 가엾은 아이를 행복하게 해 주고 만족하게 해 주려고 할 수 있는 모든 걸 다 하기를 바라요."], ["Papa and Mama Meagles, we had better say no more about it.", "미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님, 우리 이 이야기는 더 하지 않는 게 낫겠어요."], ["We never did look at this subject from the same side, and we never shall.", "우리는 이 문제를 같은 편에서 바라본 적이 한 번도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 그러지 못할 테니까요."], ["There, there!", "자, 자!"], ["Now I am good.’", "이제 나는 착해졌어요.\""]], [["Truly, having by this time said everything she could say in maintenance of her wonderfully mythical position, and in admonition to Mr Meagles that he must not expect to bear his honours of alliance too cheaply, Mrs Gowan was disposed to forgo the rest.", "참으로, 이로써 자신의 그 놀랍도록 신화적인 입장을 지키기 위해, 또 미글스 씨에게 동맹의 영예를 너무 헐값에 누리려 해서는 안 된다고 훈계하기 위해 할 수 있는 말은 다 한 터라, 가우언 부인은 나머지는 그만둘 마음이 있었다."], ["If Mr Meagles had submitted to a glance of entreaty from Mrs Meagles, and an expressive gesture from Clennam, he would have left her in the undisturbed enjoyment of this state of mind.", "만약 미글스 씨가 미글스 부인의 간청 어린 눈길과 클레넘의 의미심장한 몸짓에 따랐더라면, 그는 그녀를 이런 마음 상태에 흐트러짐 없이 만족하도록 내버려 두었을 것이다."], ["But Pet was the darling and pride of his heart; and if he could ever have championed her more devotedly, or loved her better, than in the days when she was the sunlight of his house, it would have been now, when, as its daily grace and delight, she was lost to it.", "그러나 펫은 그의 마음의 사랑이자 자랑이었다. 그래서 만일 그가 그녀를, 그녀가 집안의 햇살이었던 시절보다 더 헌신적으로 옹호하거나 더 깊이 사랑할 수 있는 때가 있었다면, 그것은 바로 지금, 곧 집안의 나날의 우아함이요 기쁨이던 그녀를 잃어버린 지금이었을 것이다."]], [["‘Mrs Gowan, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I have been a plain man all my life.", "\"가우언 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["If I was to try--no matter whether on myself, on somebody else, or both--any genteel mystifications, I should probably not succeed in them.’", "\"저는 평생 솔직한 사람으로 살아왔습니다. 제가 — 제 자신에게든, 다른 누구에게든, 아니면 둘 다에게든 그건 상관없습니다만 — 무슨 점잖은 눈속임을 부려 보려 한다면, 저는 아마 거기서 성공하지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Papa Meagles,’ returned the Dowager, with an affable smile, but with the bloom on her cheeks standing out a little more vividly than usual as the neighbouring surface became paler, ‘probably not.’", "\"미글스 아버님,\" 미망인이 상냥한 미소를 띠며 대꾸했는데, 다만 주변 살갗이 더 창백해지면서 두 뺨의 홍조는 평소보다 좀 더 또렷이 도드라져 보였다. \"아마 성공하지 못하시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Therefore, my good madam,’ said Mr Meagles, at great pains to restrain himself, ‘I hope I may, without offence, ask to have no such mystification played off upon me.’", "\"그러니, 선량하신 부인,\" 미글스 씨가 자신을 억누르려 무진 애를 쓰며 말했다. \"부디 거슬리지 않으시기를 바라며 청하건대, 제게 그런 눈속임을 부리지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["‘Mama Meagles,’ observed Mrs Gowan, ‘your good man is incomprehensible.’", "\"미글스 어머님,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"당신네 선량한 양반은 도무지 이해할 수가 없네요.\""]], [["Her turning to that worthy lady was an artifice to bring her into the discussion, quarrel with her, and vanquish her.", "그녀가 그 점잖은 부인에게로 화살을 돌린 것은, 그 부인을 논쟁 속으로 끌어들여 시비를 걸고 굴복시키려는 술수였다."], ["Mr Meagles interposed to prevent that consummation.", "미글스 씨는 그런 결말을 막으려 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Mother,’ said he, ‘you are inexpert, my dear, and it is not a fair match.", "\"여보,\" 그가 말했다."], ["Let me beg of you to remain quiet.", "\"당신은 서투른 사람이오, 여보. 이건 공정한 싸움이 못 되오."], ["Come, Mrs Gowan, come!", "부디 잠자코 있어 주시오. 자, 가우언 부인, 자!"], ["Let us try to be sensible; let us try to be good-natured; let us try to be fair.", "우리 분별 있게 굴어 봅시다. 호의를 가져 봅시다."], ["Don’t you pity Henry, and I won’t pity Pet.", "공정해 봅시다. 부인께서 헨리를 가엾이 여기지 않으신다면, 저도 펫을 가엾이 여기지 않겠습니다."], ["And don’t be one-sided, my dear madam; it’s not considerate, it’s not kind.", "그리고 한쪽으로만 치우치지 마십시오, 부인. 그건 사려 깊지 못하고 친절하지도 못합니다."], ["Don’t let us say that we hope Pet will make Henry happy, or even that we hope Henry will make Pet happy,’ (Mr Meagles himself did not look happy as he spoke the words,) ‘but let us hope they will make each other happy.’", "펫이 헨리를 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다거나, 심지어 헨리가 펫을 행복하게 해 주기를 바란다거나 하는 말은 하지 맙시다.\" (이 말을 하는 미글스 씨 자신도 행복해 보이지는 않았다.) \"다만 그 애들이 서로를 행복하게 해 주기를 바라도록 합시다.\""]], [["‘Yes, sure, and there leave it, father,’ said Mrs Meagles the kind-hearted and comfortable.", "\"그래요, 정말이지, 거기서 그만 두세요, 여보.\" 마음씨 곱고 푸근한 미글스 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Why, mother, no,’ returned Mr Meagles, ‘not exactly there.", "\"아니, 여보, 안 되오.\" 미글스 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["I can’t quite leave it there; I must say just half-a-dozen words more.", "\"거기서 딱 끝낼 수는 없소. 거기서 영 끝낼 수가 없어요."], ["Mrs Gowan, I hope I am not over-sensitive.", "꼭 대여섯 마디만 더 해야겠소. 가우언 부인, 저는 제가 너무 예민한 사람은 아니기를 바랍니다."], ["I believe I don’t look it.’", "그렇게 보이지는 않을 거라고 믿습니다만.\""]], [["‘Indeed you do not,’ said Mrs Gowan, shaking her head and the great green fan together, for emphasis.", "\"정말 그렇게 보이지 않으세요.\" 가우언 부인이, 강조를 위해 머리와 그 커다란 초록 부채를 함께 흔들며 말했다."]], [["‘Thank you, ma’am; that’s well.", "\"고맙습니다, 부인. 그것 참 다행이군요."], ["Notwithstanding which, I feel a little--I don’t want to use a strong word--now shall I say hurt?’ asked Mr Meagles at once with frankness and moderation, and with a conciliatory appeal in his tone.", "그럼에도 불구하고, 저는 좀 — 강한 말을 쓰고 싶지는 않습니다만 — 자, 상처받았다고 해 둘까요?\" 미글스 씨가, 솔직하면서도 절제된 태도로, 화해를 청하는 어조를 담아 단번에 물었다."]], [["‘Say what you like,’ answered Mrs Gowan.", "\"하고 싶은 대로 말씀하세요.\" 가우언 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘It is perfectly indifferent to me.’", "\"저로서는 전혀 상관없는 일이니까요.\""]], [["‘No, no, don’t say that,’ urged Mr Meagles, ‘because that’s not responding amiably.", "\"아니, 아니, 그런 말씀 마십시오.\" 미글스 씨가 강권했다."], ["I feel a little hurt when I hear references made to consequences having been foreseen, and to its being too late now, and so forth.’", "\"그건 다정하게 응하시는 게 아니니까요. 결과가 이미 예견되었다느니, 이제는 너무 늦었다느니 하는 따위의 언급을 들으면 저는 좀 상처를 받습니다.\""]], [["‘_Do_ you, Papa Meagles?’ said Mrs Gowan.", "\"그러세요, 미글스 아버님?\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["‘I am not surprised.’", "\"놀랍지도 않네요.\""]], [["‘Well, ma’am,’ reasoned Mr Meagles, ‘I was in hopes you would have been at least surprised, because to hurt me wilfully on so tender a subject is surely not generous.’", "\"이런, 부인.\" 미글스 씨가 따지듯 말했다. \"저는 적어도 부인께서 놀라시기라도 했으면 했습니다. 이토록 민감한 문제로 저를 고의로 상처 입히는 건 분명 너그러운 일이 못 되니까요.\""]], [["‘I am not responsible,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘for your conscience, you know.’", "\"저는 당신의 양심에 대해서는,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다. \"책임이 없답니다, 아시잖아요.\""]], [["Poor Mr Meagles looked aghast with astonishment.", "가엾은 미글스 씨는 경악하여 어안이 벙벙해진 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘If I am unluckily obliged to carry a cap about with me, which is yours and fits you,’ pursued Mrs Gowan, ‘don’t blame me for its pattern, Papa Meagles, I beg!’", "\"제가 불운하게도, 당신 것이며 당신께 꼭 맞는 모자를 하나 지니고 다녀야 하는 처지라 해도,\" 가우언 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그 무늬를 두고 저를 탓하지는 마세요, 미글스 아버님, 부디!\""]], [["‘Why, good Lord, ma’am!’ Mr Meagles broke out, ‘that’s as much as to state--’", "\"이런, 맙소사, 부인!\" 미글스 씨가 버럭 내뱉었다. \"그건 곧 이렇게 말씀하시는 거나 다름없군요 —\""]], [["‘Now, Papa Meagles, Papa Meagles,’ said Mrs Gowan, who became extremely deliberate and prepossessing in manner whenever that gentleman became at all warm, ‘perhaps to prevent confusion, I had better speak for myself than trouble your kindness to speak for me.", "\"자, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 아버님.\" 가우언 부인이, 그 신사가 조금이라도 달아오를 때면 으레 더없이 신중하고 사람을 끄는 태도가 되어 말했다."], ["It’s as much as to state, you begin.", "\"혼란을 막기 위해, 당신의 친절을 빌려 제 말을 대신하시게 하느니, 차라리 제가 직접 말하는 편이 낫겠네요."], ["If you please, I will finish the sentence.", "'그건 곧 이렇게 말하는 거나 다름없다'고 당신이 시작하셨지요."], ["It is as much as to state--not that I wish to press it or even recall it, for it is of no use now, and my only wish is to make the best of existing circumstances--that from the first to the last I always objected to this match of yours, and at a very late period yielded a most unwilling consent to it.’", "괜찮으시다면, 제가 그 문장을 끝맺지요. 그건 곧 이렇게 말하는 거나 다름없어요 — 제가 그걸 강조하거나 다시 들추려는 게 아니에요, 이제 와선 아무 소용도 없고, 제 유일한 바람은 그저 현재의 상황에서 최선을 다하는 것뿐이니까요 — 처음부터 끝까지 저는 늘 당신네의 이 혼사에 반대했고, 아주 뒤늦게야 더없이 마지못해 동의를 내주었다는 것 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Mother!’ cried Mr Meagles.", "\"여보!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Do you hear this!", "\"이 말을 들었소!"], ["Arthur!", "아서!"], ["Do you hear this!’", "자네도 이 말을 들었나!\""]], [["‘The room being of a convenient size,’ said Mrs Gowan, looking about as she fanned herself, ‘and quite charmingly adapted in all respects to conversation, I should imagine I am audible in any part of it.’", "\"방이 적당히 넓고,\" 가우언 부인이 부채질을 하며 둘러보면서 말했다. \"또 모든 면에서 대화에 아주 매력적으로 잘 맞으니, 방 어느 구석에서든 제 말이 들릴 거라고 생각합니다만.\""]], [["Some moments passed in silence, before Mr Meagles could hold himself in his chair with sufficient security to prevent his breaking out of it at the next word he spoke.", "미글스 씨가 다음에 입을 열 한마디에 자리를 박차고 일어나지 않을 만큼 충분히 의자에 자신을 붙들어 매기까지, 얼마간 침묵의 시간이 흘렀다. 마침내 그가 말했다."], ["At last he said: ‘Ma’am, I am very unwilling to revive them, but I must remind you what my opinions and my course were, all along, on that unfortunate subject.’", "\"부인, 저는 그 일들을 다시 들추고 싶은 마음이 정말 없습니다만, 그 불행한 문제에 관해 줄곧 제 견해와 제 처신이 어떠했는지를 부인께 일깨워 드려야겠군요.\""]], [["‘O, my dear sir!’ said Mrs Gowan, smiling and shaking her head with accusatory intelligence, ‘they were well understood by me, I assure you.’", "\"오, 친애하는 선생!\" 가우언 부인이 미소 지으며, 비난 어린 통찰을 담아 머리를 가로저으며 말했다. \"그건 제가 잘 이해하고 있었답니다, 정말이에요.\""]], [["‘I never, ma’am,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘knew unhappiness before that time, I never knew anxiety before that time. It was a time of such distress to me that--’ That Mr Meagles could really say no more about it, in short, but passed his handkerchief before his face.", "\"부인, 저는,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다. \"그때 이전에는 불행이란 걸 알지 못했고, 그때 이전에는 근심이란 걸 알지 못했습니다. 그건 제게 어찌나 고통스러운 시간이었던지 —\" 요컨대 미글스 씨는 더 이상 그 일에 관해 정말로 아무 말도 할 수 없어, 손수건을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다."]], [["‘I understood the whole affair,’ said Mrs Gowan, composedly looking over her fan.", "\"저는 그 모든 일을 다 이해했어요.\" 가우언 부인이 부채 너머로 침착하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["‘As you have appealed to Mr Clennam, I may appeal to Mr Clennam, too.", "\"당신이 클레넘 씨께 호소하셨으니, 저 역시 클레넘 씨께 호소해도 되겠지요."], ["He knows whether I did or not.’", "제가 그랬는지 안 그랬는지는 저분이 아시니까요.\""]], [["‘I am very unwilling,’ said Clennam, looked to by all parties, ‘to take any share in this discussion, more especially because I wish to preserve the best understanding and the clearest relations with Mr Henry Gowan.", "\"저는 이 논쟁에 어떤 식으로든 끼어드는 것이 정말 내키지 않습니다.\" 좌중 모두의 시선을 받으며 클레넘이 말했다."], ["I have very strong reasons indeed, for entertaining that wish.", "\"더욱이 저는 헨리 가우언 씨와 더없이 좋은 양해와 더없이 분명한 관계를 지켜 가고자 하기 때문입니다."], ["Mrs Gowan attributed certain views of furthering the marriage to my friend here, in conversation with me before it took place; and I endeavoured to undeceive her.", "저는 정말이지 그런 바람을 품을 아주 강한 이유들이 있습니다. 가우언 부인께서는 그 결혼이 이루어지기 전에 저와 나눈 대화에서, 이 결혼을 추진하려는 어떤 속셈을 여기 계신 제 친구분의 것으로 돌리셨고, 저는 그 오해를 풀어 드리려 애썼습니다."], ["I represented that I knew him (as I did and do) to be strenuously opposed to it, both in opinion and action.’", "저는 그가 (제가 알았던 대로, 또 지금도 아는 대로) 견해로나 행동으로나 그 결혼에 한사코 반대했다는 것을 분명히 밝혔습니다.\""]], [["‘You see?’ said Mrs Gowan, turning the palms of her hands towards Mr Meagles, as if she were Justice herself, representing to him that he had better confess, for he had not a leg to stand on.", "\"보셨지요?\" 가우언 부인이, 마치 자신이 정의의 여신이라도 되는 양 미글스 씨를 향해 두 손바닥을 펼쳐 보이며, 그가 발붙일 데가 없으니 차라리 자백하는 편이 낫겠다고 일러 주듯 말했다."], ["‘You see?", "\"보셨지요?"], ["Very good!", "아주 좋아요!"], ["Now Papa and Mama Meagles both!’ here she rose; ‘allow me to take the liberty of putting an end to this rather formidable controversy.", "자, 미글스 아버님, 미글스 어머님, 두 분 다!\" 여기서 그녀는 자리에서 일어났다."], ["I will not say another word upon its merits.", "\"외람되지만 이 자못 만만찮은 논쟁에 종지부를 찍는 것을 허락해 주세요. 저는 이 일의 시비에 관해서는 더 이상 한마디도 하지 않겠어요."], ["I will only say that it is an additional proof of what one knows from all experience; that this kind of thing never answers--as my poor fellow himself would say, that it never pays--in one word, that it never does.’", "다만, 온갖 경험에서 익히 아는 바를 다시금 입증해 주는 사례라는 것만 말씀드리지요. 이런 부류의 일은 결코 잘되는 법이 없다는 것 — 제 가엾은 아이라면 이렇게 말하겠지만, 결코 수지가 맞는 법이 없다는 것 — 한마디로, 결코 잘 풀리는 법이 없다는 것 말이에요.\""]], [["Mr Meagles asked, What kind of thing?", "미글스 씨가 어떤 부류의 일을 말하는 거냐고 물었다."]], [["‘It is in vain,’ said Mrs Gowan, ‘for people to attempt to get on together who have such extremely different antecedents; who are jumbled against each other in this accidental, matrimonial sort of way; and who cannot look at the untoward circumstance which has shaken them together in the same light.", "\"그렇게도 극단적으로 다른 내력을 지닌 사람들이,\" 가우언 부인이 말했다."], ["It never does.’", "\"이런 우연하고 혼인이랍시고 하는 식으로 서로 뒤섞여 부대끼게 된 사람들이, 그리고 자기들을 한데 흔들어 놓은 이 고약한 사정을 같은 시각으로 바라볼 수 없는 사람들이, 함께 잘 살아 보려고 애쓰는 건 헛된 일이에요. 그건 결코 잘되는 법이 없어요.\""]], [["Mr Meagles was beginning, ‘Permit me to say, ma’am--’", "미글스 씨가 막 입을 열려 했다. \"부인, 한 말씀 드리도록 허락해 주시면 —\""]], [["‘No, don’t,’ returned Mrs Gowan.", "\"아니요, 그러지 마세요.\" 가우언 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Why should you!", "\"왜 그러시겠어요!"], ["It is an ascertained fact. It never does.", "그건 이미 확인된 사실이니까요."], ["I will therefore, if you please, go my way, leaving you to yours.", "결코 잘되는 법이 없어요. 그러니 괜찮으시다면, 저는 제 길을 가겠어요."], ["I shall at all times be happy to receive my poor fellow’s pretty wife, and I shall always make a point of being on the most affectionate terms with her.", "당신들도 당신들 길을 가시도록 두고요. 저는 언제든 제 가엾은 아이의 예쁜 아내를 기꺼이 맞이할 것이고, 늘 그 애와 더없이 다정한 사이로 지내기를 잊지 않을 거예요."], ["But as to these terms, semi-family and semi-stranger, semi-goring and semi-boring, they form a state of things quite amusing in its impracticability.", "하지만 이런 식의 관계로 말하자면, 반쯤 가족이고 반쯤 남남이며, 반쯤 들이받고 반쯤 지루하게 하는 이런 관계는, 그 실행 불가능함에서 자못 우스꽝스러운 상태를 이루지요."], ["I assure you it never does.’", "정말이지, 결코 잘되는 법이 없어요.\""]], [["The Dowager here made a smiling obeisance, rather to the room than to any one in it, and therewith took a final farewell of Papa and Mama Meagles. Clennam stepped forward to hand her to the Pill-Box which was at the service of all the Pills in Hampton Court Palace; and she got into that vehicle with distinguished serenity, and was driven away.", "여기서 미망인은 방 안의 누구에게랄 것도 없이 차라리 방을 향해 미소 띤 채 절을 하고는, 그것으로 미글스 아버님과 미글스 어머님에게 마지막 작별을 고했다. 클레넘이 앞으로 나서서, 햄프턴 코트 궁의 온갖 알약들이 마음껏 쓰는 그 알약통 같은 마차로 그녀를 안내했다. 그녀는 그 마차에 더없이 의젓하고 평온하게 올라타 떠나갔다. * 알약통(Pill-Box): 작고 답답한 마차를 빗댄 말장난. 햄프턴 코트 궁에 기거하던 노부인들('알약들')이 공동으로 쓰던 마차를 가리킨다."]], [["Thenceforth the Dowager, with a light and careless humour, often recounted to her particular acquaintance how, after a hard trial, she had found it impossible to know those people who belonged to Henry’s wife, and who had made that desperate set to catch him.", "그날 이후로 미망인은 가볍고 천연덕스러운 익살로, 자신의 절친한 지인들에게 곧잘 이런 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 자신이 모진 시험을 거친 끝에, 헨리의 아내에게 딸린 그 사람들, 그를 낚으려고 그토록 필사적으로 덤벼들었던 그 사람들과 알고 지낸다는 것이 불가능함을 알게 되었노라고."], ["Whether she had come to the conclusion beforehand, that to get rid of them would give her favourite pretence a better air, might save her some occasional inconvenience, and could risk no loss (the pretty creature being fast married, and her father devoted to her), was best known to herself.", "그들을 떼어 내는 것이 자신이 애지중지하는 가식에 더 그럴싸한 분위기를 입혀 줄 것이며, 이따금의 불편을 덜어 줄 수 있고, 또 아무런 손실의 위험도 없으리라는 (그 예쁜 아이는 단단히 결혼했고 그 아비는 그 아이에게 헌신하고 있으니) 결론에, 그녀가 미리 도달해 있었는지 어떤지는 그녀 자신만이 가장 잘 알 일이었다."], ["Though this history has its opinion on that point too, and decidedly in the affirmative.", "비록 이 이야기는 그 점에 관해서도 나름의 견해를 지니고 있으니, 단연코 그렇다는 쪽이긴 하지만 말이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 9. Appearance and Disappearance", "# CHAPTER 9. Appearance and Disappearance"]], [["‘Arthur, my dear boy,’ said Mr Meagles, on the evening of the following day, ‘Mother and I have been talking this over, and we don’t feel comfortable in remaining as we are.", "\"아서, 이 친구야,\" 다음 날 저녁 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["That elegant connection of ours--that dear lady who was here yesterday--’", "\"어머니하고 내가 이 일을 곰곰이 의논해 봤는데, 우리는 지금 이대로 있기가 영 마음이 편치 않네그려. 우리의 그 고상한 인척 말이야 — 어제 여기 왔던 그 친애하는 부인 말일세 —\""]], [["‘I understand,’ said Arthur.", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠습니다.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Even that affable and condescending ornament of society,’ pursued Mr Meagles, ‘may misrepresent us, we are afraid. We could bear a great deal, Arthur, for her sake; but we think we would rather not bear that, if it was all the same to her.’", "\"그 상냥하고 겸허한 사교계의 장식품께서도 말이야,\" 미글스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"우리를 잘못 전할까 봐 걱정이라네. 그분을 위해서라면 우리가 많은 걸 견딜 수도 있지, 아서. 하지만 우리는 그건 견디지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어. 그분에게도 매한가지라면 말이야.\""]], [["‘Good,’ said Arthur.", "\"좋습니다.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘Go on.’", "\"계속하시지요.\""]], [["‘You see,’ proceeded Mr Meagles ‘it might put us wrong with our son-in-law, it might even put us wrong with our daughter, and it might lead to a great deal of domestic trouble.", "\"보게나,\" 미글스 씨가 이어 갔다."], ["You see, don’t you?’", "\"그게 우리를 사위와 사이가 틀어지게 만들지도 모르고, 심지어 딸아이와도 틀어지게 만들지도 모르고, 또 집안에 큰 분란을 불러올지도 모른단 말일세. 알겠지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["‘Yes, indeed,’ returned Arthur, ‘there is much reason in what you say.’ He had glanced at Mrs Meagles, who was always on the good and sensible side; and a petition had shone out of her honest face that he would support Mr Meagles in his present inclinings.", "\"네, 정말 그렇습니다.\" 아서가 대답했다. \"말씀하신 데에는 일리가 많습니다.\" 그는 미글스 부인을 흘끗 보았는데, 그녀는 언제나 선량하고 분별 있는 편에 서 있었다. 그리고 그녀의 정직한 얼굴에서는, 지금 미글스 씨가 기울이는 마음을 아서가 지지해 주기를 바라는 간청이 환히 비쳐 나오고 있었다."]], [["‘So we are very much disposed, are Mother and I,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘to pack up bags and baggage and go among the Allongers and Marshongers once more.", "\"그래서 어머니하고 나는 말이야,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["I mean, we are very much disposed to be off, strike right through France into Italy, and see our Pet.’", "\"짐이고 보따리고 다 꾸려서, 다시 한번 '알롱제'와 '마르숑제'들 틈으로 떠나 볼까 하는 마음이 아주 굴뚝같다네. 그러니까 내 말은, 훌훌 떠나서 프랑스를 곧장 가로질러 이탈리아로 들어가, 우리 펫을 보고 오자는 거지.\""]], [["‘And I don’t think,’ replied Arthur, touched by the motherly anticipation in the bright face of Mrs Meagles (she must have been very like her daughter, once), ‘that you could do better.", "\"제 생각에는,\" 미글스 부인의 밝은 얼굴에 어린 어머니다운 기대(그녀는 한때 딸과 무척 닮았음이 틀림없었다)에 마음이 움직여 아서가 대답했다."], ["And if you ask me for my advice, it is that you set off to-morrow.’", "\"그보다 더 좋은 일을 하실 수는 없을 겁니다. 그리고 제게 조언을 구하신다면, 내일 당장 떠나시라는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Is it really, though?’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"정말 그런가, 진짜로?\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Mother, this is being backed in an idea!’", "\"여보, 이거 우리 생각에 든든한 지지를 얻은 셈이구려!\""]], [["Mother, with a look which thanked Clennam in a manner very agreeable to him, answered that it was indeed.", "어머니는, 클레넘에게 그가 무척 흐뭇하게 느낄 만한 방식으로 고마움을 전하는 눈빛으로, 정말 그렇다고 대답했다."]], [["‘The fact is, besides, Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, the old cloud coming over his face, ‘that my son-in-law is already in debt again, and that I suppose I must clear him again.", "\"게다가 사실은 말일세, 아서,\" 미글스 씨가 말했고, 그 옛 먹구름이 그의 얼굴 위로 다시 드리워졌다."], ["It may be as well, even on this account, that I should step over there, and look him up in a friendly way.", "\"내 사위가 또 빚을 졌단 말이지. 그러니 내가 또 그걸 갚아 줘야 할 모양이야."], ["Then again, here’s Mother foolishly anxious (and yet naturally too) about Pet’s state of health, and that she should not be left to feel lonesome at the present time.", "이 점만 봐도, 내가 거기 한번 건너가서 친구로서 그를 살펴보는 게 좋을 것 같네. 그리고 또, 여기 어머니가 펫의 건강 상태를 두고 어리석을 만큼(그러면서도 자연스럽게도) 안절부절못하고 있다네."], ["It’s undeniably a long way off, Arthur, and a strange place for the poor love under all the circumstances.", "게다가 이런 시기에 그 애가 외로움을 느끼도록 내버려 둬서는 안 된다는 거지. 부인할 수 없는 일이야, 아서, 거긴 너무 멀고, 이 모든 사정을 생각하면 가엾은 우리 딸에게는 낯선 곳이라네."], ["Let her be as well cared for as any lady in that land, still it is a long way off.", "그곳에서 어떤 귀부인 못지않게 잘 보살핌을 받는다 해도, 그래도 너무 멀어. 집이 아무리 초라해도 집은 집인 것처럼, 그러니까 자네도 알겠지만,\""], ["just as Home is Home though it’s never so Homely, why you see,’ said Mr Meagles, adding a new version to the proverb, ‘Rome is Rome, though it’s never so Romely.’", "미글스 씨가 그 속담에 새 변주를 덧붙이며 말했다. \"로마가 아무리 로마답지 않아도, 로마는 로마인 거야.\""]], [["‘All perfectly true,’ observed Arthur, ‘and all sufficient reasons for going.’", "\"전부 지당하신 말씀입니다.\" 아서가 말했다. \"그리고 전부 떠나실 충분한 이유가 되고요.\""]], [["‘I am glad you think so; it decides me.", "\"자네가 그렇게 생각해 주니 기쁘군. 그걸로 마음이 정해졌네."], ["Mother, my dear, you may get ready.", "여보, 준비를 시작하구려. 우리는 그 솜씨 좋은 통역사를 잃었지(그 애는 외국어 세 가지를 아름답게 했다네, 아서."], ["We have lost our pleasant interpreter (she spoke three foreign languages beautifully, Arthur; you have heard her many a time), and you must pull me through it, Mother, as well as you can.", "자네도 여러 번 들었지). 그러니 당신이 나를 잘 끌고 가 줘야 해, 여보, 할 수 있는 만큼은."], ["I require a deal of pulling through, Arthur,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head, ‘a deal of pulling through.", "나는 끌어 줄 데가 많은 사람이야, 아서,\" 미글스 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["I stick at everything beyond a noun-substantive--and I stick at him, if he’s at all a tight one.’", "\"끌어 줄 데가 아주 많지. 보통명사만 넘어가면 죄다 막혀 버린다네 — 그리고 상대가 조금이라도 까다로운 자라면, 그 사람한테도 막혀 버려.\""]], [["‘Now I think of it,’ returned Clennam, ‘there’s Cavalletto.", "\"그러고 보니 말입니다,\" 클레넘이 대답했다."], ["He shall go with you, if you like.", "\"카발레토가 있지 않습니까. 원하신다면 그를 함께 보내 드리지요."], ["I could not afford to lose him, but you will bring him safe back.’", "저로서는 그를 잃으면 곤란하지만, 두 분께서 무사히 데려와 주실 테니까요.\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"이런!"], ["I am much obliged to you, my boy,’ said Mr Meagles, turning it over, ‘but I think not.", "정말 고맙네, 친구.\" 미글스 씨가 그 제안을 곰곰이 따져 보며 말했다."], ["No, I think I’ll be pulled through by Mother.", "\"하지만 그러지 않는 게 좋겠어."], ["Cavallooro (I stick at his very name to start with, and it sounds like the chorus to a comic song) is so necessary to you, that I don’t like the thought of taking him away.", "아니, 나는 어머니한테 끌려가는 편이 낫겠네. 카발루로(우선 그 이름부터가 막혀 버리는데다, 무슨 희극 노래 후렴구처럼 들린단 말이야)는 자네한테 그렇게 꼭 필요한 사람이니, 데려간다는 생각이 영 내키질 않아."], ["More than that, there’s no saying when we may come home again; and it would never do to take him away for an indefinite time.", "게다가 우리가 언제 다시 집에 돌아올지도 알 수 없는 노릇이고. 정해지지 않은 기간 동안 그를 데려간다는 건 도저히 안 될 일이지."], ["The cottage is not what it was.", "그 오두막은 예전 같지가 않아."], ["It only holds two little people less than it ever did, Pet, and her poor unfortunate maid Tattycoram; but it seems empty now.", "늘 살던 것보다 단지 두 사람이 줄었을 뿐인데 말이야 — 펫과, 그 가엾고 불운한 하녀 태티코럼이지 — 그런데도 이제는 텅 빈 것만 같다네."], ["Once out of it, there’s no knowing when we may come back to it.", "한번 거길 떠나면, 언제 다시 돌아올 수 있을지 알 수가 없어."], ["No, Arthur, I’ll be pulled through by Mother.’", "아니야, 아서, 나는 어머니한테 끌려가겠네.\""]], [["They would do best by themselves perhaps, after all, Clennam thought; therefore did not press his proposal.", "어쩌면 결국 두 분끼리 가시는 게 가장 좋을 거라고 클레넘은 생각했다. 그래서 그는 자기 제안을 밀어붙이지 않았다."]], [["‘If you would come down and stay here for a change, when it wouldn’t trouble you,’ Mr Meagles resumed, ‘I should be glad to think--and so would Mother too, I know--that you were brightening up the old place with a bit of life it was used to when it was full, and that the Babies on the wall there had a kind eye upon them sometimes. You so belong to the spot, and to them, Arthur, and we should every one of us have been so happy if it had fallen out--but, let us see--how’s the weather for travelling now?’ Mr Meagles broke off, cleared his throat, and got up to look out of the window.", "\"성가시지 않을 때 가끔 내려와서 머물러 준다면,\" 미글스 씨가 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"나는 — 그리고 어머니도 그럴 거라고 분명히 아네만 — 자네가 이 옛집을, 사람들로 북적이던 시절에 익숙했던 그 약간의 생기로 환하게 밝혀 주고 있다고 생각하면 기쁠 거야. 그리고 저 벽에 걸린 아기들이 가끔이라도 다정한 눈길을 받는다고 생각하면 말이지. 자네는 이 자리와, 또 저 아이들에게 그토록 속한 사람이라네, 아서. 그리고 만약 일이 그렇게 풀렸다면 우리 모두 무척 행복했을 텐데 — 하지만, 어디 보자 — 지금 여행하기에 날씨는 어떤가?\" 미글스 씨가 말을 뚝 그치고, 목청을 가다듬더니, 일어나서 창밖을 내다보았다."]], [["They agreed that the weather was of high promise; and Clennam kept the talk in that safe direction until it had become easy again, when he gently diverted it to Henry Gowan and his quick sense and agreeable qualities when he was delicately dealt with; he likewise dwelt on the indisputable affection he entertained for his wife. Clennam did not fail of his effect upon good Mr Meagles, whom these commendations greatly cheered; and who took Mother to witness that the single and cordial desire of his heart in reference to their daughter’s husband, was harmoniously to exchange friendship for friendship, and confidence for confidence. Within a few hours the cottage furniture began to be wrapped up for preservation in the family absence--or, as Mr Meagles expressed it, the house began to put its hair in papers--and within a few days Father and Mother were gone, Mrs Tickit and Dr Buchan were posted, as of yore, behind the parlour blind, and Arthur’s solitary feet were rustling among the dry fallen leaves in the garden walks.", "날씨가 자못 기대해 볼 만하다는 데에 그들은 의견을 모았다. 그리고 클레넘은 그 안전한 방향으로 대화를 이어 가다가, 이야기가 다시 편안해지자 슬며시 화제를 헨리 가우언에게로 돌렸다. 섬세하게 대해 주기만 하면 그가 보이는 재빠른 감각과 호감 가는 자질들에 관해서였다. 그는 마찬가지로 가우언이 아내에게 품은 의심할 여지 없는 애정에 대해서도 자세히 이야기했다. 클레넘은 선량한 미글스 씨에게 그 효과를 거두는 데 실패하지 않았다. 이런 칭찬들은 그를 크게 기쁘게 했고, 그는 어머니를 증인 삼아, 딸의 남편에 관하여 자기 마음의 유일하고도 진심 어린 바람은 우정에는 우정으로, 신뢰에는 신뢰로 화목하게 화답하는 것뿐이라고 했다. 몇 시간 안에 오두막의 가구들이 가족이 비운 동안 보존하기 위해 싸이기 시작했고 — 미글스 씨의 표현을 빌리면, 집이 머리를 종이로 마는 일에 들어갔고 — 며칠 안에 아버지와 어머니는 떠났으며, 틱킷 부인과 부캔 의사는 예전처럼 거실 블라인드 뒤에 자리 잡았고, 정원 산책로의 마른 낙엽들 사이로 아서의 외로운 발소리가 바스락거렸다."]], [["As he had a liking for the spot, he seldom let a week pass without paying a visit.", "그는 이곳을 좋아했으므로, 한 주가 그냥 지나가도록 방문하지 않는 일이 드물었다."], ["Sometimes, he went down alone from Saturday to Monday; sometimes his partner accompanied him; sometimes, he merely strolled for an hour or two about the house and garden, saw that all was right, and returned to London again.", "어떤 때는 토요일부터 월요일까지 혼자 내려가기도 했고, 어떤 때는 동업자가 함께 가기도 했으며, 어떤 때는 그저 한두 시간 집과 정원을 거닐며 모든 게 제대로 되어 있는지 확인하고는 다시 런던으로 돌아가기도 했다."], ["At all times, and under all circumstances, Mrs Tickit, with her dark row of curls, and Dr Buchan, sat in the parlour window, looking out for the family return.", "어느 때든, 어떤 상황에서든, 검은 곱슬머리를 늘어뜨린 틱킷 부인과 부캔 의사는 거실 창가에 앉아 가족이 돌아오기를 내다보고 있었다."]], [["On one of his visits Mrs Tickit received him with the words, ‘I have something to tell you, Mr Clennam, that will surprise you.’ So surprising was the something in question, that it actually brought Mrs Tickit out of the parlour window and produced her in the garden walk, when Clennam went in at the gate on its being opened for him.", "그가 방문한 어느 날, 틱킷 부인은 이런 말로 그를 맞이했다. \"클레넘 씨, 깜짝 놀라실 일을 말씀드릴 게 있어요.\" 문제의 그 일은 어찌나 놀라운 것이었던지, 그것은 실제로 틱킷 부인을 거실 창가에서 끌어내어 정원 산책로에 나타나게 만들었고, 그리하여 클레넘이 열린 대문으로 들어설 때 그녀가 거기 있었다."]], [["‘What is it, Mrs Tickit?’ said he.", "\"무슨 일입니까, 틱킷 부인?\" 그가 말했다."]], [["‘Sir,’ returned that faithful housekeeper, having taken him into the parlour and closed the door; ‘if ever I saw the led away and deluded child in my life, I saw her identically in the dusk of yesterday evening.’", "\"나리,\" 그 충직한 가정부가 그를 거실로 데리고 들어가 문을 닫고는 대답했다. \"제 평생 그 꾐에 넘어가 정신이 홀린 아이를 본 적이 있다면, 바로 어제 저녁 어스름 속에서 영락없이 그 애를 봤다니까요.\""]], [["‘You don’t mean Tatty--’", "\"설마 태티를 말하는 건 —\""]], [["‘Coram yes I do!’ quoth Mrs Tickit, clearing the disclosure at a leap.", "\"코럼이요, 그래요, 그 애를 말하는 거예요!\" 틱킷 부인이 단숨에 그 폭로를 건너뛰며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Where?’", "\"어디서요?\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ returned Mrs Tickit, ‘I was a little heavy in my eyes, being that I was waiting longer than customary for my cup of tea which was then preparing by Mary Jane.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 틱킷 부인이 대답했다."], ["I was not sleeping, nor what a person would term correctly, dozing.", "\"제가 눈이 좀 무거웠더랬어요. 그때 메리 제인이 차를 끓이고 있었는데, 평소보다 차 한 잔을 더 오래 기다리고 있었거든요."], ["I was more what a person would strictly call watching with my eyes closed.’", "잠을 자고 있던 것도 아니고, 사람들이 흔히 정확히 말하는 졸고 있던 것도 아니에요. 그보다는, 사람들이 엄밀히 말하자면 눈을 감고 망을 보고 있었다고나 할까요.\""]], [["Without entering upon an inquiry into this curious abnormal condition, Clennam said, ‘Exactly.", "이 기묘하고 비정상적인 상태에 대한 탐구로 들어가는 일 없이, 클레넘이 말했다. \"그렇군요."], ["Well?’", "그래서요?\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ proceeded Mrs Tickit, ‘I was thinking of one thing and thinking of another, just as you yourself might. Just as anybody might.’", "\"그래서요, 나리,\" 틱킷 부인이 이어 갔다. \"저는 이 생각도 했다가 저 생각도 했다가 했지요. 나리께서도 그러시듯이요. 누구든 그러듯이 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Precisely so,’ said Clennam.", "\"바로 그렇지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘Well?’", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["‘And when I do think of one thing and do think of another,’ pursued Mrs Tickit, ‘I hardly need to tell you, Mr Clennam, that I think of the family. Because, dear me! a person’s thoughts,’ Mrs Tickit said this with an argumentative and philosophic air, ‘however they may stray, will go more or less on what is uppermost in their minds. They _will_ do it, sir, and a person can’t prevent them.’", "\"그리고 제가 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 할 때면,\" 틱킷 부인이 계속했다. \"클레넘 씨, 굳이 말씀드릴 것도 없지만, 저는 가족분들 생각을 한답니다. 왜냐하면, 어머나! 사람의 생각이란,\" 틱킷 부인은 이 말을 논쟁적이고 철학적인 태도로 했다. \"아무리 여기저기 헤매더라도, 마음속에 가장 위에 떠 있는 것으로 다소간 가게 마련이거든요. 정말 그렇답니다, 나리. 그건 사람이 막을 수가 없는 노릇이에요.\""]], [["Arthur subscribed to this discovery with a nod.", "아서는 이 발견에 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다."]], [["‘You find it so yourself, sir, I’ll be bold to say,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘and we all find it so. It an’t our stations in life that changes us, Mr Clennam; thoughts is free!--As I was saying, I was thinking of one thing and thinking of another, and thinking very much of the family. Not of the family in the present times only, but in the past times too. For when a person does begin thinking of one thing and thinking of another in that manner, as it’s getting dark, what I say is, that all times seem to be present, and a person must get out of that state and consider before they can say which is which.’", "\"나리께서도 직접 그러시다는 걸 아실 거예요, 제가 감히 말씀드리는데요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"우리 모두가 그렇다는 걸요. 우리를 바꾸는 건 살아가는 신분 따위가 아니에요, 클레넘 씨. 생각은 자유랍니다! — 말씀드렸다시피, 저는 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 하면서, 가족분들 생각을 아주 많이 했어요. 지금 시절의 가족분들만이 아니라, 지난 시절의 가족분들까지요. 왜냐하면 어두워질 무렵에 사람이 그런 식으로 이 생각도 하고 저 생각도 하기 시작하면, 제가 드리는 말씀은, 모든 시절이 다 지금인 것처럼 느껴진다는 거예요. 그래서 그 상태에서 빠져나와 곰곰이 따져 봐야만 어느 게 어느 건지 말할 수 있게 되거든요.\""]], [["He nodded again; afraid to utter a word, lest it should present any new opening to Mrs Tickit’s conversational powers.", "그는 다시 고개를 끄덕였다. 틱킷 부인의 화술에 새로운 물꼬를 터 줄까 봐 한마디도 입에 올리기가 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["‘In consequence of which,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘when I quivered my eyes and saw her actual form and figure looking in at the gate, I let them close again without so much as starting, for that actual form and figure came so pat to the time when it belonged to the house as much as mine or your own, that I never thought at the moment of its having gone away. But, sir, when I quivered my eyes again, and saw that it wasn’t there, then it all flooded upon me with a fright, and I jumped up.’", "\"그 결과로요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"제가 눈을 깜빡여 떠 보니 그 애의 실제 모습과 형상이 대문으로 들여다보고 있더란 말이에요. 저는 깜짝 놀라기는커녕 그냥 다시 눈을 감아 버렸어요. 그 실제 모습과 형상이라는 게, 그 애가 제 것이나 나리 것이나 다름없이 이 집에 속해 있던 그 시절과 어찌나 딱 들어맞던지, 그 순간에는 그 애가 떠났다는 생각이 도무지 들지를 않았거든요. 하지만 나리, 제가 다시 눈을 깜빡여 떠 보니 거기에 없는 거예요. 그제야 무서운 마음이 와락 밀려들어서, 저는 벌떡 일어났지요.\""]], [["‘You ran out directly?’ said Clennam.", "\"곧장 뛰어나가셨고요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘I ran out,’ assented Mrs Tickit, ‘as fast as ever my feet would carry me; and if you’ll credit it, Mr Clennam, there wasn’t in the whole shining Heavens, no not so much as a finger of that young woman.’", "\"뛰어나갔지요,\" 틱킷 부인이 수긍했다. \"발이 닿는 한 빨리요. 그런데 믿으시겠어요, 클레넘 씨, 저 빛나는 온 하늘을 통틀어, 그 젊은 여자의 손가락 하나조차 없더라니까요.\""]], [["Passing over the absence from the firmament of this novel constellation, Arthur inquired of Mrs Tickit if she herself went beyond the gate?", "이 새로운 별자리가 창공에서 사라진 일은 그냥 넘기고, 아서는 틱킷 부인에게 본인이 대문 밖까지는 나가 보았느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Went to and fro, and high and low,’ said Mrs Tickit, ‘and saw no sign of her!’", "\"이리저리, 높은 데 낮은 데 다 가 봤지요,\" 틱킷 부인이 말했다. \"그런데 그 애 그림자도 못 봤어요!\""]], [["He then asked Mrs Tickit how long a space of time she supposed there might have been between the two sets of ocular quiverings she had experienced?", "그러자 그는 틱킷 부인에게, 그녀가 경험한 두 차례의 눈 깜빡임 사이에 시간 간격이 얼마쯤 있었으리라 짐작하느냐고 물었다."], ["Mrs Tickit, though minutely circumstantial in her reply, had no settled opinion between five seconds and ten minutes.", "틱킷 부인은 대답이 자질구레하게 상세하기는 했지만, 5초에서 10분 사이에서 확실한 의견을 정하지 못했다."], ["She was so plainly at sea on this part of the case, and had so clearly been startled out of slumber, that Clennam was much disposed to regard the appearance as a dream.", "그녀는 이 부분에 관해서는 너무도 분명히 갈피를 못 잡았고, 또 분명히 선잠에서 깜짝 놀라 깬 것이었으므로, 클레넘은 그 출현을 한낱 꿈으로 여기는 쪽으로 기우는 마음이 컸다."], ["Without hurting Mrs Tickit’s feelings with that infidel solution of her mystery, he took it away from the cottage with him; and probably would have retained it ever afterwards if a circumstance had not soon happened to change his opinion.", "그는 그 신앙심 없는 풀이로 그녀의 수수께끼를 깨뜨려 틱킷 부인의 마음을 상하게 하지는 않으면서, 그 결론을 오두막에서 가지고 나왔다. 그리고 만약 곧 그의 의견을 바꿔 놓을 사건이 일어나지 않았더라면, 아마 그는 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 생각을 간직했을 것이다."]], [["He was passing at nightfall along the Strand, and the lamp-lighter was going on before him, under whose hand the street-lamps, blurred by the foggy air, burst out one after another, like so many blazing sunflowers coming into full-blow all at once,--when a stoppage on the pavement, caused by a train of coal-waggons toiling up from the wharves at the river-side, brought him to a stand-still.", "그가 해 질 무렵 스트랜드 거리를 따라 지나가고 있었는데, 점등부가 그보다 앞서 걸어가고 있었다. 그의 손길이 닿는 대로 안개 낀 공기에 흐릿해진 가로등들이 하나씩 차례로 확 피어났는데, 마치 활짝 만개하는 수많은 이글거리는 해바라기들이 한꺼번에 피어나는 것 같았다."], ["He had been walking quickly, and going with some current of thought, and the sudden check given to both operations caused him to look freshly about him, as people under such circumstances usually do.", "— 그때 강가 부두에서 힘겹게 올라오는 석탄 마차 행렬 때문에 보도가 막히면서, 그는 걸음을 멈추게 되었다. 그는 빠르게 걷고 있었고, 어떤 생각의 흐름에 빠져 있었는데, 두 작용이 갑자기 멈춰 버리자, 그런 상황에 처한 사람들이 흔히 그러듯 새삼스레 주위를 둘러보게 되었다."]], [["Immediately, he saw in advance--a few people intervening, but still so near to him that he could have touched them by stretching out his arm--Tattycoram and a strange man of a remarkable appearance: a swaggering man, with a high nose, and a black moustache as false in its colour as his eyes were false in their expression, who wore his heavy cloak with the air of a foreigner.", "그 순간 그는 앞쪽에서 — 몇 사람이 사이에 끼어 있었지만, 그래도 팔을 뻗으면 닿을 만큼 가까운 곳에서 — 태티코럼과, 눈에 띄는 행색을 한 낯선 사내를 보았다. 거들먹거리는 사내로, 코는 높았고, 검은 콧수염은 그 빛깔이 가짜였으니, 그 눈빛이 표정에서 가짜인 것과 매한가지였다."], ["His dress and general appearance were those of a man on travel, and he seemed to have very recently joined the girl.", "그는 외국인 특유의 분위기로 두툼한 망토를 걸치고 있었다. 그의 옷차림과 전반적인 행색은 여행 중인 사람의 것이었고, 그는 아주 최근에야 그 처녀와 합류한 듯했다."], ["In bending down (being much taller than she was), listening to whatever she said to him, he looked over his shoulder with the suspicious glance of one who was not unused to be mistrustful that his footsteps might be dogged.", "(그는 처녀보다 훨씬 키가 컸으므로) 몸을 숙여 그녀가 하는 말이 무엇이든 귀 기울이면서, 그는 어깨 너머로, 자기 발자취가 미행당할까 봐 의심하는 데 익숙한 사람의 미심쩍은 눈길을 던졌다. 바로 그때 클레넘은 그의 얼굴을 보았다."], ["It was then that Clennam saw his face; as his eyes lowered on the people behind him in the aggregate, without particularly resting upon Clennam’s face or any other.", "그의 눈이 자기 뒤에 있는 사람들을 한 덩어리로 내리훑었을 뿐, 클레넘의 얼굴이나 다른 어느 누구의 얼굴에 특별히 머무르지는 않았던 것이다."]], [["He had scarcely turned his head about again, and it was still bent down, listening to the girl, when the stoppage ceased, and the obstructed stream of people flowed on.", "그가 고개를 채 다시 돌리기도 전에, 그러니까 아직 처녀의 말에 귀 기울이느라 고개를 숙인 채로 있을 때, 길막힘이 풀리면서 가로막혔던 사람들의 물결이 흘러갔다."], ["Still bending his head and listening to the girl, he went on at her side, and Clennam followed them, resolved to play this unexpected play out, and see where they went.", "여전히 고개를 숙이고 처녀의 말에 귀 기울이며, 그는 그녀 곁에서 걸어갔고, 클레넘은 이 뜻밖의 연극을 끝까지 지켜보아 그들이 어디로 가는지 보기로 마음먹고 그들을 뒤따랐다."]], [["He had hardly made the determination (though he was not long about it), when he was again as suddenly brought up as he had been by the stoppage.", "그가 그 결심을 채 굳히기도 전에(그렇다고 오래 걸린 것은 아니지만), 그는 아까 길이 막혔을 때처럼 또다시 별안간 걸음을 멈추게 되었다."], ["They turned short into the Adelphi,--the girl evidently leading,--and went straight on, as if they were going to the Terrace which overhangs the river.", "그들이 아델파이로 홱 꺾어 들어간 것이다 — 처녀가 분명히 앞장서고 있었다 — 그러고는 마치 강 위로 비스듬히 솟은 테라스로 가려는 듯 곧장 나아갔다."]], [["There is always, to this day, a sudden pause in that place to the roar of the great thoroughfare.", "오늘날까지도 그곳에는 큰길의 굉음이 갑자기 잦아드는 지점이 늘 있다."], ["The many sounds become so deadened that the change is like putting cotton in the ears, or having the head thickly muffled.", "온갖 소음이 어찌나 죽어 버리는지, 그 변화는 마치 귀에 솜을 틀어막거나 머리를 두툼하게 싸맨 것 같다."], ["At that time the contrast was far greater; there being no small steam-boats on the river, no landing places but slippery wooden stairs and foot-causeways, no railroad on the opposite bank, no hanging bridge or fish-market near at hand, no traffic on the nearest bridge of stone, nothing moving on the stream but watermen’s wherries and coal-lighters.", "그 시절에는 그 대조가 훨씬 더 컸다. 강에는 작은 증기선들도 없었고, 미끄러운 나무 계단과 보행용 둑길 말고는 선착장도 없었으며, 맞은편 강둑에 철도도 없었고, 가까이에 매달린 다리나 어시장도 없었으며, 가장 가까운 돌다리에도 통행이 없었고, 강물 위에서 움직이는 것이라고는 뱃사공의 거룻배와 석탄 운반선뿐이었다."], ["Long and broad black tiers of the latter, moored fast in the mud as if they were never to move again, made the shore funereal and silent after dark; and kept what little water-movement there was, far out towards mid-stream.", "후자, 곧 석탄 운반선들의 길고 넓은 검은 무리가, 마치 다시는 움직이지 않을 것처럼 진흙 속에 단단히 매여 있어, 어둠이 내린 뒤의 강기슭을 음산하고 적막하게 만들었으며, 그나마 얼마 안 되는 물의 움직임을 강 한복판 쪽 멀리로 밀어내고 있었다."], ["At any hour later than sunset, and not least at that hour when most of the people who have anything to eat at home are going home to eat it, and when most of those who have nothing have hardly yet slunk out to beg or steal, it was a deserted place and looked on a deserted scene.", "해가 진 뒤 어느 시각이든, 그리고 집에 먹을 것이 있는 대다수 사람들이 그것을 먹으러 집으로 돌아가고 있고, 먹을 것이 아무것도 없는 대다수 사람들이 아직 구걸이나 도둑질을 하러 슬그머니 기어 나오지도 않은 그 시각에는 특히 더, 그곳은 인적이 끊긴 장소였고 인적이 끊긴 풍경을 굽어보고 있었다."]], [["Such was the hour when Clennam stopped at the corner, observing the girl and the strange man as they went down the street.", "클레넘이 모퉁이에 멈춰 서서 처녀와 낯선 사내가 거리를 따라 내려가는 것을 지켜본 것이 바로 그런 시각이었다."], ["The man’s footsteps were so noisy on the echoing stones that he was unwilling to add the sound of his own.", "사내의 발소리는 울리는 돌바닥 위에서 어찌나 요란했던지, 클레넘은 거기에 자기 발소리까지 보태고 싶지 않았다."], ["But when they had passed the turning and were in the darkness of the dark corner leading to the terrace, he made after them with such indifferent appearance of being a casual passenger on his way, as he could assume.", "그러나 그들이 모퉁이를 돌아 테라스로 이어지는 어두운 모퉁이의 어둠 속으로 들어서자, 그는 자기가 우연히 제 갈 길을 가는 행인인 양 짐짓 무심한 기색을 한껏 꾸미며 그들을 뒤쫓았다."]], [["When he rounded the dark corner, they were walking along the terrace towards a figure which was coming towards them.", "그가 어두운 모퉁이를 돌아 나왔을 때, 그들은 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 어떤 형체를 향해 테라스를 따라 걷고 있었다."], ["If he had seen it by itself, under such conditions of gas-lamp, mist, and distance, he might not have known it at first sight, but with the figure of the girl to prompt him, he at once recognised Miss Wade.", "만약 그가 그 형체만을, 그런 가스등과 안개와 거리라는 조건 아래에서 보았더라면, 첫눈에 알아보지 못했을지도 모른다. 그러나 처녀의 형체가 그에게 단서를 주었으므로, 그는 단번에 그것이 웨이드 양임을 알아보았다."]], [["He stopped at the corner, seeming to look back expectantly up the street as if he had made an appointment with some one to meet him there; but he kept a careful eye on the three.", "그는 모퉁이에 멈춰 서서, 마치 누군가와 거기서 만나기로 약속이라도 한 듯 거리 위쪽을 기대에 차 돌아보는 척했다. 그러나 그는 그 세 사람을 조심스레 주시했다."], ["When they came together, the man took off his hat, and made Miss Wade a bow.", "그들이 한자리에 모이자, 사내는 모자를 벗어 웨이드 양에게 인사를 했다."], ["The girl appeared to say a few words as though she presented him, or accounted for his being late, or early, or what not; and then fell a pace or so behind, by herself.", "처녀는 마치 그를 소개하거나, 그가 늦었거나 빨랐거나 하는 사정을 설명하는 듯 몇 마디 하는 것 같더니, 한 걸음쯤 뒤로 물러나 혼자 떨어져 섰다."], ["Miss Wade and the man then began to walk up and down; the man having the appearance of being extremely courteous and complimentary in manner; Miss Wade having the appearance of being extremely haughty.", "그러자 웨이드 양과 사내는 거닐기 시작했다. 사내는 태도가 극도로 정중하고 아첨하는 듯한 행색이었고, 웨이드 양은 극도로 오만한 행색이었다."]], [["When they came down to the corner and turned, she was saying, ‘If I pinch myself for it, sir, that is my business.", "그들이 모퉁이까지 내려와 몸을 돌렸을 때, 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"내가 그걸 위해 허리띠를 졸라매든 말든, 그건 내 일이에요."], ["Confine yourself to yours, and ask me no question.’", "당신은 당신 일에나 신경 쓰고, 나한테 아무것도 묻지 말아요.\""]], [["‘By Heaven, ma’am!’ he replied, making her another bow.", "\"맙소사, 부인!\" 그가 그녀에게 또 한 번 인사를 하며 대답했다."], ["‘It was my profound respect for the strength of your character, and my admiration of your beauty.’", "\"그건 당신 성품의 강인함에 대한 제 깊은 존경과, 당신의 아름다움에 대한 제 찬탄이었을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘I want neither the one nor the other from any one,’ said she, ‘and certainly not from you of all creatures.", "\"나는 누구한테서도 그 둘 중 어느 것도 원하지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Go on with your report.’", "\"더구나 하고많은 사람 중에서도 당신한테서는 확실히 아니고요. 보고나 계속하세요.\""]], [["‘Am I pardoned?’ he asked, with an air of half abashed gallantry.", "\"용서받은 건가요?\" 그가 반쯤 무안해진 정중함을 띠며 물었다."]], [["‘You are paid,’ she said, ‘and that is all you want.’", "\"당신은 돈을 받잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그게 당신이 원하는 전부고요.\""]], [["Whether the girl hung behind because she was not to hear the business, or as already knowing enough about it, Clennam could not determine.", "처녀가 뒤로 처져 있는 것이, 그 일을 들어서는 안 되기 때문인지, 아니면 이미 그에 관해 충분히 알고 있기 때문인지, 클레넘은 단정할 수 없었다."], ["They turned and she turned.", "그들이 몸을 돌리자 그녀도 몸을 돌렸다."], ["She looked away at the river, as she walked with her hands folded before her; and that was all he could make of her without showing his face.", "그녀는 두 손을 앞으로 모은 채 걸으면서 강 쪽을 멀리 바라보았다. 자기 얼굴을 드러내지 않고서 그가 그녀에 관해 알아낼 수 있는 건 그게 다였다."], ["There happened, by good fortune, to be a lounger really waiting for some one; and he sometimes looked over the railing at the water, and sometimes came to the dark corner and looked up the street, rendering Arthur less conspicuous.", "다행스럽게도, 정말로 누군가를 기다리며 어슬렁거리는 사람이 하나 있었다. 그는 때로는 난간 너머로 강물을 내려다보고, 때로는 어두운 모퉁이로 와서 거리 위쪽을 올려다보았으므로, 아서를 덜 눈에 띄게 해 주었다."]], [["When Miss Wade and the man came back again, she was saying, ‘You must wait until to-morrow.’", "웨이드 양과 사내가 다시 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"내일까지 기다려야 해요.\""]], [["‘A thousand pardons?’ he returned.", "\"천 번 용서를 빕니다.\" 그가 대답했다."], ["‘My faith!", "\"이런!"], ["Then it’s not convenient to-night?’", "그러면 오늘 밤은 곤란하다는 거군요?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"네."], ["I tell you I must get it before I can give it to you.’", "말했잖아요, 내가 그걸 먼저 구해야 당신한테 줄 수 있다고요.\""]], [["She stopped in the roadway, as if to put an end to the conference.", "그녀는 마치 이 회담을 끝내려는 듯 차도 위에 멈춰 섰다."], ["He of course stopped too.", "사내도 물론 멈췄다."], ["And the girl stopped.", "그리고 처녀도 멈췄다."]], [["‘It’s a little inconvenient,’ said the man.", "\"좀 곤란하군요.\" 사내가 말했다."], ["‘A little.", "\"좀요."], ["But, Holy Blue!", "하지만, 거룩한 성모님!"], ["that’s nothing in such a service.", "이런 임무를 하는 마당에 그건 아무것도 아니지요."], ["I am without money to-night, by chance.", "제가 오늘 밤은 공교롭게도 돈이 없어서요."], ["I have a good banker in this city, but I would not wish to draw upon the house until the time when I shall draw for a round sum.’", "이 도시에 든든한 거래 은행이 있긴 합니다만, 큰 액수를 한꺼번에 찾을 그때까지는 그 은행에서 돈을 빼고 싶지 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Harriet,’ said Miss Wade, ‘arrange with him--this gentleman here--for sending him some money to-morrow.’ She said it with a slur of the word gentleman which was more contemptuous than any emphasis, and walked slowly on.", "\"해리엇,\" 웨이드 양이 말했다. \"이분과 — 여기 이 신사분과 — 내일 돈을 좀 보내 드리는 걸로 처리해 다오.\" 그녀는 신사라는 말을 어떤 강조보다도 더 경멸스럽게 끌듯이 내뱉으며 말하고는, 천천히 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The man bent his head again, and the girl spoke to him as they both followed her.", "사내는 다시 고개를 숙였고, 둘 다 그녀를 뒤따라가며 처녀가 그에게 말을 걸었다."], ["Clennam ventured to look at the girl as they moved away.", "그들이 멀어져 갈 때 클레넘은 큰맘 먹고 처녀를 바라보았다."], ["He could note that her rich black eyes were fastened upon the man with a scrutinising expression, and that she kept at a little distance from him, as they walked side by side to the further end of the terrace.", "그는 그녀의 그윽한 검은 눈이 무언가를 살피는 표정으로 사내에게 고정되어 있는 것, 그리고 그들이 나란히 테라스 저쪽 끝으로 걸어가는 동안 그녀가 그에게서 약간의 거리를 두고 있는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["A loud and altered clank upon the pavement warned him, before he could discern what was passing there, that the man was coming back alone.", "거기서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 채 알아보기도 전에, 보도 위에서 크고도 달라진 쩔걱거리는 발소리가 그에게 사내 혼자 돌아오고 있음을 일러 주었다."], ["Clennam lounged into the road, towards the railing; and the man passed at a quick swing, with the end of his cloak thrown over his shoulder, singing a scrap of a French song.", "클레넘은 어슬렁어슬렁 차도로 나가 난간 쪽으로 갔다. 사내는 망토 자락을 어깨 위로 휙 걸친 채 프랑스 노래 한 토막을 부르며 빠른 걸음으로 휙 지나갔다."]], [["The whole vista had no one in it now but himself.", "이제 그 전망 전체에는 그 자신 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["The lounger had lounged out of view, and Miss Wade and Tattycoram were gone.", "어슬렁거리던 사람은 어슬렁어슬렁 시야에서 사라졌고, 웨이드 양과 태티코럼도 가 버렸다."], ["More than ever bent on seeing what became of them, and on having some information to give his good friend, Mr Meagles, he went out at the further end of the terrace, looking cautiously about him.", "그들이 어떻게 되는지 보고, 또 자기의 선량한 친구 미글스 씨에게 전해 줄 무언가를 얻으려는 마음이 그 어느 때보다 간절해진 그는, 조심스레 주위를 둘러보며 테라스 저쪽 끝으로 나갔다."], ["He rightly judged that, at first at all events, they would go in a contrary direction from their late companion.", "그는 적어도 처음에는 그들이 방금 헤어진 동행과는 반대 방향으로 가리라고 옳게 판단했다."], ["He soon saw them in a neighbouring bye-street, which was not a thoroughfare, evidently allowing time for the man to get well out of their way.", "그는 곧 그들을 이웃한 골목길에서 발견했는데, 그곳은 한길로 통하지 않는 막다른 길이어서, 분명히 사내가 자기들 길에서 충분히 멀어질 시간을 벌어 주고 있는 것이었다."], ["They walked leisurely arm-in-arm down one side of the street, and returned on the opposite side.", "그들은 거리 한쪽을 따라 느긋하게 팔짱을 끼고 내려갔다가 맞은편으로 되돌아왔다."], ["When they came back to the street-corner, they changed their pace for the pace of people with an object and a distance before them, and walked steadily away.", "그들이 거리 모퉁이로 다시 왔을 때, 그들은 걸음걸이를 바꾸어, 목적지와 갈 길이 정해진 사람들의 걸음으로 꾸준히 걸어갔다."], ["Clennam, no less steadily, kept them in sight.", "클레넘은 못지않게 꾸준히 그들을 시야에 두었다."]], [["They crossed the Strand, and passed through Covent Garden (under the windows of his old lodging where dear Little Dorrit had come that night), and slanted away north-east, until they passed the great building whence Tattycoram derived her name, and turned into the Gray’s Inn Road.", "그들은 스트랜드 거리를 건너, 코번트 가든을 지나(그 옛 하숙집 창문 아래를 지났는데, 바로 그날 밤 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿이 거기 찾아왔었다), 북동쪽으로 비스듬히 나아가더니, 태티코럼이 자기 이름을 따온 그 큰 건물을 지나 그레이스 인 로드로 접어들었다."], ["Clennam was quite at home here, in right of Flora, not to mention the Patriarch and Pancks, and kept them in view with ease.", "클레넘은 플로라 덕분에 — 가장과 팽크스는 말할 것도 없고 — 이곳을 제집처럼 잘 알았으므로, 그들을 손쉽게 시야에 두었다."], ["He was beginning to wonder where they might be going next, when that wonder was lost in the greater wonder with which he saw them turn into the Patriarchal street.", "그들이 다음에는 어디로 갈까 궁금해하기 시작할 무렵, 그 궁금증은 그들이 가장의 거리로 접어드는 것을 본 더 큰 궁금증에 묻혀 버렸다."], ["That wonder was in its turn swallowed up on the greater wonder with which he saw them stop at the Patriarchal door.", "그리고 그 궁금증은 또다시, 그들이 가장의 집 문 앞에 멈춰 서는 것을 본 더 큰 궁금증에 삼켜져 버렸다."], ["A low double knock at the bright brass knocker, a gleam of light into the road from the opened door, a brief pause for inquiry and answer and the door was shut, and they were housed.", "반들거리는 놋쇠 문고리를 낮게 두 번 두드리는 소리, 열린 문에서 거리로 새어 나오는 한 줄기 불빛, 묻고 답하는 짧은 사이 — 그러고는 문이 닫혔고, 그들은 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["After looking at the surrounding objects for assurance that he was not in an odd dream, and after pacing a little while before the house, Arthur knocked at the door.", "자기가 이상한 꿈속에 있는 게 아니라는 확신을 얻으려고 주위의 사물들을 둘러보고, 또 집 앞을 잠시 서성인 뒤에, 아서는 문을 두드렸다."], ["It was opened by the usual maid-servant, and she showed him up at once, with her usual alacrity, to Flora’s sitting-room.", "문은 늘 보던 하녀가 열어 주었고, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 민첩하게 그를 곧장 플로라의 거실로 안내했다."]], [["There was no one with Flora but Mr F.’s Aunt, which respectable gentlewoman, basking in a balmy atmosphere of tea and toast, was ensconced in an easy-chair by the fireside, with a little table at her elbow, and a clean white handkerchief spread over her lap on which two pieces of toast at that moment awaited consumption. Bending over a steaming vessel of tea, and looking through the steam, and breathing forth the steam, like a malignant Chinese enchantress engaged in the performance of unholy rites, Mr F.’s Aunt put down her great teacup and exclaimed, ‘Drat him, if he an’t come back again!’", "플로라 곁에는 'F 씨의 아주머니' 말고는 아무도 없었다. 이 점잖은 노부인은 차와 토스트가 풍기는 향긋한 공기를 쬐며 난롯가의 안락의자에 폭 파묻혀 있었고, 팔꿈치 옆에는 작은 탁자가, 무릎 위에는 깨끗한 흰 손수건이 펼쳐져 있었으며, 그 위에는 바로 그 순간 먹히기를 기다리는 토스트 두 조각이 놓여 있었다. 김이 모락모락 나는 찻잔 위로 몸을 숙여, 그 김을 통해 내다보며, 그 김을 토해 내는 모습이, 마치 부정한 의식을 거행하는 사악한 중국 여자 마법사 같았는데, 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 자기의 커다란 찻잔을 내려놓고 외쳤다. \"빌어먹을, 저자가 또 돌아왔잖아!\""]], [["It would seem from the foregoing exclamation that this uncompromising relative of the lamented Mr F., measuring time by the acuteness of her sensations and not by the clock, supposed Clennam to have lately gone away; whereas at least a quarter of a year had elapsed since he had had the temerity to present himself before her.", "앞의 외침으로 미루어 보건대, 작고하신 'F 씨'의 이 타협을 모르는 친척은 시간을 시계가 아니라 자기 감정의 격렬함으로 재는 모양이어서, 클레넘이 최근에 떠났다고 여기는 것 같았다. 그러나 그가 감히 그녀 앞에 나타난 지는 적어도 한 분기는 지나 있었다."]], [["‘My goodness Arthur!’ cried Flora, rising to give him a cordial reception, ‘Doyce and Clennam what a start and a surprise for though not far from the machinery and foundry business and surely might be taken sometimes if at no other time about mid-day when a glass of sherry and a humble sandwich of whatever cold meat in the larder might not come amiss nor taste the worse for being friendly for you know you buy it somewhere and wherever bought a profit must be made or they would never keep the place it stands to reason without a motive still never seen and learnt now not to be expected, for as Mr F.", "\"세상에 아서!\" 플로라가 그를 다정하게 맞으려고 일어서며 외쳤다."], ["himself said if seeing is believing not seeing is believing too and when you don’t see you may fully believe you’re not remembered not that I expect you Arthur Doyce and Clennam to remember me why should I for the days are gone but bring another teacup here directly and tell her fresh toast and pray sit near the fire.’", "\"도이스 앤드 클레넘 깜짝이야 놀라워라 비록 기계니 주물 공장 일이니 하는 데서 그리 멀지 않으니 다른 때는 몰라도 한낮쯤이면 셰리주 한 잔에 식료품실에 있는 찬 고기가 뭐든 간에 그걸로 만든 변변찮은 샌드위치 하나쯤은 곁들여도 나쁘지 않을 테고 친하다고 해서 맛이 더 나빠질 것도 없는데 왜냐하면 그건 어딘가에서 사 오는 거고 어디서 사 오든 이윤이 남아야지 안 그러면 가게를 유지할 수가 없으니 이치상 동기 없이는 안 되는 일이지만 그래도 통 못 뵈었으니 이제 기대도 안 한다는 걸 배웠어요 왜냐하면 F 씨가 직접 말씀하셨듯이 본다는 게 믿는 거라면 안 보는 것도 믿는 거고 안 보이면 잊혔다고 충분히 믿어도 되는데 그렇다고 내가 당신을 아서 도이스 앤드 클레넘 나를 기억해 주기를 바라는 건 아니에요 내가 왜 그러겠어요 그 시절은 다 갔는걸요 하지만 여기 찻잔 하나 더 가져오고 저 애한테 토스트 새로 굽고 그리고 제발 불 가까이 앉아요.\""]], [["Arthur was in the greatest anxiety to explain the object of his visit; but was put off for the moment, in spite of himself, by what he understood of the reproachful purport of these words, and by the genuine pleasure she testified in seeing him.", "아서는 자기가 찾아온 목적을 설명하고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았으나, 이 말들에서 그가 알아들은 책망의 취지와, 그를 보고 그녀가 드러낸 진심 어린 기쁨 때문에, 본의 아니게 잠시 미뤄질 수밖에 없었다."]], [["‘And now pray tell me something all you know,’ said Flora, drawing her chair near to his, ‘about the good dear quiet little thing and all the changes of her fortunes carriage people now no doubt and horses without number most romantic, a coat of arms of course and wild beasts on their hind legs showing it as if it was a copy they had done with mouths from ear to ear good gracious, and has she her health which is the first consideration after all for what is wealth without it Mr F.", "\"그리고 이제 제발 그 다정하고 착한 조용한 그 애에 관해서,\" 플로라가 자기 의자를 그의 의자 가까이 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["himself so often saying when his twinges came that sixpence a day and find yourself and no gout so much preferable, not that he could have lived on anything like it being the last man or that the previous little thing though far too familiar an expression now had any tendency of that sort much too slight and small but looked so fragile bless her?’", "\"그리고 그 애 운명의 온갖 변화에 관해서 아는 걸 전부 좀 들려줘요 이제 틀림없이 마차 타는 사람들이고 셀 수 없이 많은 말들이고 더없이 낭만적이지 물론 가문 문장도 있고 짐승들이 뒷다리로 서서 그걸 떠받치고 있을 텐데 마치 무슨 본보기라도 베껴 그린 것처럼 입이 귀밑까지 찢어진 채로 어머나 세상에 그리고 그 애는 건강한가요 그게 결국은 무엇보다 먼저 챙길 일이지 그게 없으면 재물이 다 무슨 소용이겠어요 F 씨가 직접 통증이 도질 때마다 그렇게 자주 말씀하셨지 하루 육 펜스에 제 입은 제가 벌어 먹고 통풍이 없는 게 훨씬 낫다고 그렇다고 그분이 그런 걸로 살 수 있었던 건 아니에요 그럴 사람이 못 되셨으니까 아니면 그 예전의 작은 그 애가 지금은 너무 허물없는 표현이지만 그런 쪽 기미가 있었던 것도 아니고 너무 가냘프고 작긴 했지만 그래도 어찌나 연약해 보이던지 가엾기도 하지?\""]], [["Mr F.’s Aunt, who had eaten a piece of toast down to the crust, here solemnly handed the crust to Flora, who ate it for her as a matter of business.", "토스트 한 조각을 가장자리 빵껍질만 남기고 다 먹은 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 여기서 그 빵껍질을 엄숙하게 플로라에게 건넸고, 플로라는 으레 하는 일이라는 듯 그것을 대신 먹어 주었다."], ["Mr F.’s Aunt then moistened her ten fingers in slow succession at her lips, and wiped them in exactly the same order on the white handkerchief; then took the other piece of toast, and fell to work upon it.", "그러고 나서 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 열 손가락을 입술에 대고 차례차례 천천히 적시더니, 정확히 똑같은 순서로 그것들을 흰 손수건에 닦았다. 그런 다음 다른 토스트 한 조각을 집어 들고 거기에 달려들었다."], ["While pursuing this routine, she looked at Clennam with an expression of such intense severity that he felt obliged to look at her in return, against his personal inclinations.", "이 일과를 수행하는 동안, 그녀는 어찌나 매서운 표정으로 클레넘을 바라보았던지, 그는 본인의 마음과는 달리 마지못해 그녀를 마주 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘She is in Italy, with all her family, Flora,’ he said, when the dreaded lady was occupied again.", "\"그 애는 식구들 모두와 함께 이탈리아에 있어요, 플로라.\" 그 무시무시한 부인이 다시 일에 몰두하자 그가 말했다."]], [["‘In Italy is she really?’ said Flora, ‘with the grapes growing everywhere and lava necklaces and bracelets too that land of poetry with burning mountains picturesque beyond belief though if the organ-boys come away from the neighbourhood not to be scorched nobody can wonder being so young and bringing their white mice with them most humane, and is she really in that favoured land with nothing but blue about her and dying gladiators and Belvederes though Mr F.", "\"이탈리아에 정말요?\" 플로라가 말했다."], ["himself did not believe for his objection when in spirits was that the images could not be true there being no medium between expensive quantities of linen badly got up and all in creases and none whatever, which certainly does not seem probable though perhaps in consequence of the extremes of rich and poor which may account for it.’", "\"어디에나 포도가 자라고 용암으로 만든 목걸이랑 팔찌도 있고 그 시의 나라 불타는 산들이 있는 믿기지 않을 만큼 그림 같은 곳이지 비록 손풍금 켜는 소년들이 그 동네에서 데어 죽지 않으려고 떠나온다 해도 누가 뭐라겠어요 그렇게 어린데다 흰 생쥐까지 데리고 오니 참 인정도 많지 그런데 그 애가 정말 그 축복받은 땅에 있는 거예요 둘레에는 온통 파란 하늘뿐이고 죽어 가는 검투사들이랑 벨베데레들이 있는 데 말이에요 비록 F 씨는 직접 그걸 안 믿으셨지만요 기분 좋으실 때 그분 반론은 그 조각상들이 진짜일 리 없다는 거였어요 왜냐하면 비싼 양의 천을 형편없이 손질해서 온통 구김투성이로 두르거나 아니면 아예 한 오라기도 안 걸치거나 그 중간이 없다는 거였지 사실 그럴 법하진 않지만 어쩌면 빈부의 극단 때문에 그런 건지도 모르지 그걸로 설명이 될 수도 있고요.\""]], [["Arthur tried to edge a word in, but Flora hurried on again.", "아서는 한마디 끼워 넣어 보려 했으나, 플로라가 또다시 줄달음쳤다."]], [["‘Venice Preserved too,’ said she, ‘I think you have been there is it well or ill preserved for people differ so and Maccaroni if they really eat it like the conjurors why not cut it shorter, you are acquainted Arthur--dear Doyce and Clennam at least not dear and most assuredly not Doyce for I have not the pleasure but pray excuse me--acquainted I believe with Mantua what _has_ it got to do with Mantua-making for I never have been able to conceive?’", "\"구원받은 베네치아 말인데요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"거기 가 보신 적 있는 것 같은데 잘 구원받았어요 아니면 잘못 구원받았어요 사람마다 의견이 어찌나 다른지 그리고 마카로니 말인데 그걸 정말 곡예사들처럼 먹는다면 왜 좀 더 짧게 자르지 않는 거예요 당신은 아시죠 아서 — 친애하는 도이스 앤드 클레넘 적어도 친애하는 것도 아니고 도이스는 더더욱 아니지 그분과는 알고 지내는 기쁨이 없으니까요 하지만 부디 용서해요 — 만토바를 아실 거예요 그게 망토 짓는 일하고 무슨 상관이 있는지 나는 도무지 알 수가 없는데 말이에요?\""]], [["‘I believe there is no connection, Flora, between the two,’ Arthur was beginning, when she caught him up again.", "\"그 둘 사이에는 아무 관련도 없을 겁니다, 플로라.\" 아서가 막 말을 꺼내는데, 그녀가 다시 그를 가로챘다."]], [["‘Upon your word no isn’t there I never did but that’s like me I run away with an idea and having none to spare I keep it, alas there was a time dear Arthur that is to say decidedly not dear nor Arthur neither but you understand me when one bright idea gilded the what’s-his-name horizon of et cetera but it is darkly clouded now and all is over.’", "\"맙소사 정말 없어요 그런가요 난 한 번도 안 그랬는데 하지만 그게 나다워요 어떤 생각이 떠오르면 거기 휩쓸려서 여분도 없으니 그냥 붙들고 있거든요 아아 한때는 있었지요 친애하는 아서 그러니까 분명히 친애하는 것도 아니고 아서도 아니지만 무슨 말인지 아시겠죠 어떤 빛나는 생각 하나가 무어라더라 그 지평선이며 기타 등등을 금빛으로 물들이던 때가 하지만 이제는 어둡게 구름이 끼었고 다 끝나 버렸어요.\""]], [["Arthur’s increasing wish to speak of something very different was by this time so plainly written on his face, that Flora stopped in a tender look, and asked him what it was?", "뭔가 아주 다른 이야기를 하고 싶은 아서의 점점 커져 가는 바람이 이제는 그의 얼굴에 너무도 또렷이 쓰여 있었으므로, 플로라는 다정한 눈길로 말을 멈추고 그게 무엇이냐고 물었다."]], [["‘I have the greatest desire, Flora, to speak to some one who is now in this house--with Mr Casby no doubt.", "\"플로라, 저는 지금 이 집에 와 있는 누군가와 이야기를 나누고 싶은 마음이 간절합니다 — 틀림없이 캐스비 씨와 함께 있겠지요."], ["Some one whom I saw come in, and who, in a misguided and deplorable way, has deserted the house of a friend of mine.’", "제가 들어오는 걸 봤던 어떤 사람인데, 그릇되고 개탄스러운 방식으로 제 친구의 집을 버리고 떠난 사람입니다.\""]], [["‘Papa sees so many and such odd people,’ said Flora, rising, ‘that I shouldn’t venture to go down for any one but you Arthur but for you I would willingly go down in a diving-bell much more a dining-room and will come back directly if you’ll mind and at the same time not mind Mr F.’s Aunt while I’m gone.’", "\"아빠는 워낙 많은 사람을, 그것도 별별 이상한 사람들을 만나서,\" 플로라가 일어서며 말했다. \"당신 말고는 누구를 위해서도 감히 내려갈 엄두를 못 내겠지만 아서 당신을 위해서라면 잠수종 속이라도 기꺼이 내려가겠어요 하물며 식당쯤이야 그리고 곧 돌아올 테니 내가 없는 동안 부디 F 씨의 아주머니를 신경 써 주고 동시에 신경 쓰지 마세요.\""]], [["With those words and a parting glance, Flora bustled out, leaving Clennam under dreadful apprehension of this terrible charge.", "그 말과 함께 작별의 눈길을 남기고, 플로라는 부산스레 나가 버려, 이 무시무시한 책무를 떠안은 클레넘을 끔찍한 불안 속에 남겨 두었다."]], [["The first variation which manifested itself in Mr F.’s Aunt’s demeanour when she had finished her piece of toast, was a loud and prolonged sniff.", "'F 씨의 아주머니'가 토스트 한 조각을 다 먹고 나서 그녀의 거동에 나타난 첫 번째 변화는, 크고도 길게 끄는 코 훌쩍임이었다."], ["Finding it impossible to avoid construing this demonstration into a defiance of himself, its gloomy significance being unmistakable, Clennam looked plaintively at the excellent though prejudiced lady from whom it emanated, in the hope that she might be disarmed by a meek submission.", "그 음울한 의미가 오해의 여지 없이 분명한 터라, 이 시위를 자기에 대한 도전으로 해석하지 않기란 불가능하다는 걸 깨닫고, 클레넘은 그 시위가 흘러나온 그 훌륭하지만 편견에 찬 부인을 애처로이 바라보았다. 온순하게 복종하면 그녀의 마음이 누그러지지 않을까 하는 바람에서였다."]], [["‘None of your eyes at me,’ said Mr F.’s Aunt, shivering with hostility.", "\"나한테 그 눈깔 부라리지 마.\" 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 적의에 부르르 떨며 말했다."], ["‘Take that.’", "\"이거나 받아.\""]], [["‘That’ was the crust of the piece of toast.", "'이거'란 토스트 조각의 빵껍질이었다."], ["Clennam accepted the boon with a look of gratitude, and held it in his hand under the pressure of a little embarrassment, which was not relieved when Mr F.’s Aunt, elevating her voice into a cry of considerable power, exclaimed, ‘He has a proud stomach, this chap!", "클레넘은 감사하는 표정으로 그 선물을 받아 들고, 약간의 당혹감에 눌린 채 그것을 손에 쥐고 있었는데, 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 목청을 상당한 위력의 고함으로 끌어올려 \"이 녀석은 배알이 뒤틀린 놈이라니까!"], ["He’s too proud a chap to eat it!’ and, coming out of her chair, shook her venerable fist so very close to his nose as to tickle the surface.", "그걸 먹기엔 너무 잘난 척하는 놈이야!\"라고 외치며 의자에서 일어나, 그 점잖은 주먹을 그의 코끝에 표면을 간질일 만큼 바싹 들이대며 휘둘렀을 때, 그 당혹감은 가시기는커녕 더해졌다."], ["But for the timely return of Flora, to find him in this difficult situation, further consequences might have ensued.", "플로라가 때맞춰 돌아와 그가 이 곤란한 처지에 빠진 것을 발견하지 않았더라면, 더한 일이 벌어졌을지도 몰랐다."], ["Flora, without the least discomposure or surprise, but congratulating the old lady in an approving manner on being ‘very lively to-night’, handed her back to her chair.", "플로라는 조금도 당황하거나 놀라는 기색 없이, 오히려 노부인에게 \"오늘 밤 아주 정정하시네요\"라고 칭찬조로 추어올리며 그녀를 의자로 도로 데려다 앉혔다."]], [["‘He has a proud stomach, this chap,’ said Mr F.’s relation, on being reseated.", "\"이 녀석은 배알이 뒤틀린 놈이야.\" 'F 씨'의 친척이 다시 자리에 앉으며 말했다."], ["‘Give him a meal of chaff!’", "\"여물이나 한 끼 먹여!\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"어머!"], ["I don’t think he would like that, aunt,’ returned Flora.", "그건 별로 좋아하지 않을 거예요, 아주머니.\" 플로라가 대답했다."]], [["‘Give him a meal of chaff, I tell you,’ said Mr F.’s Aunt, glaring round Flora on her enemy.", "\"여물이나 한 끼 먹이라니까.\" 'F 씨의 아주머니'가 플로라 너머로 자기 원수를 노려보며 말했다."], ["‘It’s the only thing for a proud stomach.", "\"배알이 뒤틀린 놈한테는 그게 약이야."], ["Let him eat up every morsel.", "한 부스러기도 남김없이 다 먹게 해."], ["Drat him, give him a meal of chaff!’", "빌어먹을 놈, 여물이나 한 끼 먹여!\""]], [["Under a general pretence of helping him to this refreshment, Flora got him out on the staircase; Mr F.’s Aunt even then constantly reiterating, with inexpressible bitterness, that he was ‘a chap,’ and had a ‘proud stomach,’ and over and over again insisting on that equine provision being made for him which she had already so strongly prescribed.", "이 다과를 그에게 갖다주는 척하는 일반적인 핑계로, 플로라는 그를 계단으로 데리고 나갔다. 'F 씨의 아주머니'는 그때까지도 형언할 수 없는 신랄함으로 그가 '녀석'이며 '배알이 뒤틀렸다'는 말을 끊임없이 되뇌었고, 자기가 이미 그토록 강하게 처방한 그 말[馬]에게나 줄 사료를 그에게 마련해 주라고 거듭거듭 우겨 댔다."]], [["‘Such an inconvenient staircase and so many corner-stairs Arthur,’ whispered Flora, ‘would you object to putting your arm round me under my pelerine?’", "\"계단이 어찌나 불편하고 모퉁이 계단이 또 어찌나 많은지요 아서,\" 플로라가 속삭였다. \"제 망토 밑으로 팔을 둘러 주셔도 괜찮을까요?\""]], [["With a sense of going down-stairs in a highly-ridiculous manner, Clennam descended in the required attitude, and only released his fair burden at the dining-room door; indeed, even there she was rather difficult to be got rid of, remaining in his embrace to murmur, ‘Arthur, for mercy’s sake, don’t breathe it to papa!’", "지극히 우스꽝스러운 꼴로 계단을 내려가고 있다는 느낌과 함께, 클레넘은 요구받은 자세로 내려갔고, 식당 문 앞에 이르러서야 그 아리따운 짐을 내려놓았다. 사실 거기서조차 그녀를 떨쳐 내기는 다소 어려웠으니, 그녀는 그의 품에 남아 \"아서, 제발 부탁이에요, 아빠한테는 한마디도 흘리지 말아요!\"라고 속삭였던 것이다."]], [["She accompanied Arthur into the room, where the Patriarch sat alone, with his list shoes on the fender, twirling his thumbs as if he had never left off.", "그녀는 아서와 함께 방으로 들어갔고, 그 안에는 가장이 홀로 앉아, 펠트 슬리퍼를 신은 발을 난로 망에 올린 채, 마치 한 번도 멈춘 적이 없다는 듯 엄지손가락을 빙빙 돌리고 있었다."], ["The youthful Patriarch, aged ten, looked out of his picture-frame above him with no calmer air than he.", "그의 머리 위 그림 액자 속에서 내다보는 열 살배기의 젊은 가장도 그에 못지않게 차분한 기색이었다."], ["Both smooth heads were alike beaming, blundering, and bumpy.", "두 매끈한 머리는 똑같이 환하게 빛나고, 어수룩하고, 울룩불룩했다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, I am glad to see you.", "\"클레넘 군, 만나서 반갑네."], ["I hope you are well, sir, I hope you are well.", "건강하길 바라네, 군, 건강하길 바라네."], ["Please to sit down, please to sit down.’", "부디 앉게, 부디 앉게.\""]], [["‘I had hoped, sir,’ said Clennam, doing so, and looking round with a face of blank disappointment, ‘not to find you alone.’", "\"저는 바랐습니다만, 어르신,\" 클레넘이 자리에 앉아 멍한 실망의 표정으로 둘러보며 말했다. \"어르신께서 혼자 계신 게 아니기를요.\""]], [["‘Ah, indeed?’ said the Patriarch, sweetly.", "\"아아, 그런가?\" 가장이 상냥하게 말했다."], ["‘Ah, indeed?’", "\"아아, 그런가?\""]], [["‘I told you so you know papa,’ cried Flora.", "\"제가 그렇게 말씀드렸잖아요 아빠.\" 플로라가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Ah, to be sure!’ returned the Patriarch.", "\"아아, 그렇지!\" 가장이 대꾸했다."], ["‘Yes, just so.", "\"그래, 바로 그래."], ["Ah, to be sure!’", "아아, 그렇지!\""]], [["‘Pray, sir,’ demanded Clennam, anxiously, ‘is Miss Wade gone?’", "\"여쭙건대, 어르신,\" 클레넘이 애타게 물었다. \"웨이드 양은 갔습니까?\""]], [["‘Miss--?", "\"양?"], ["Oh, you call her Wade,’ returned Mr Casby.", "— 오, 자네는 그녀를 웨이드라고 부르는군.\" 캐스비 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Highly proper.’", "\"지극히 온당한 일이지.\""]], [["Arthur quickly returned, ‘What do you call her?’", "아서가 재빨리 되물었다. \"어르신은 그녀를 무어라 부르십니까?\""]], [["‘Wade,’ said Mr Casby.", "\"웨이드.\" 캐스비 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Oh, always Wade.’", "\"오, 늘 웨이드지.\""]], [["After looking at the philanthropic visage and the long silky white hair for a few seconds, during which Mr Casby twirled his thumbs, and smiled at the fire as if he were benevolently wishing it to burn him that he might forgive it, Arthur began:", "그 박애주의자다운 얼굴과 길고 비단결 같은 백발을 몇 초간 바라보는 동안, 캐스비 씨는 엄지손가락을 빙빙 돌리며, 마치 불에게 자기를 태워 주기를 자비롭게 바라기라도 하는 듯, 그래야 그 불을 용서해 줄 수 있다는 듯 불을 향해 미소 짓고 있었다. 그러다 아서가 입을 열었다."]], [["‘I beg your pardon, Mr Casby--’", "\"실례합니다만, 캐스비 어르신 —\""]], [["‘Not so, not so,’ said the Patriarch, ‘not so.’", "\"천만에, 천만에.\" 가장이 말했다. \"천만에.\""]], [["‘--But, Miss Wade had an attendant with her--a young woman brought up by friends of mine, over whom her influence is not considered very salutary, and to whom I should be glad to have the opportunity of giving the assurance that she has not yet forfeited the interest of those protectors.’", "\"— 하지만, 웨이드 양에게는 시중드는 이가 하나 함께 있었습니다 — 제 친구분들이 키운 젊은 여자인데, 웨이드 양이 그 아이에게 미치는 영향이 그리 좋지 않다고 여겨집니다. 그리고 저는 그 아이에게, 아직 그 보호자들의 관심을 잃은 것이 아니라는 확언을 전할 기회를 얻을 수 있다면 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Really, really?’ returned the Patriarch.", "\"그래요, 그래요?\" 가장이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘Will you therefore be so good as to give me the address of Miss Wade?’", "\"그러니 부디 호의를 베푸시어 웨이드 양의 주소를 제게 알려 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Dear, dear, dear!’ said the Patriarch, ‘how very unfortunate!", "\"이런, 이런, 이런!\" 가장이 말했다."], ["If you had only sent in to me when they were here!", "\"참으로 안타깝구먼! 그들이 여기 있을 때 내게 사람을 들여보내기만 했어도!"], ["I observed the young woman, Mr Clennam.", "나도 그 젊은 여자를 보았다네, 클레넘 군."], ["A fine full-coloured young woman, Mr Clennam, with very dark hair and very dark eyes.", "혈색 좋고 풍만한 젊은 여자였지, 클레넘 군. 머리도 아주 검고 눈도 아주 검었고."], ["If I mistake not, if I mistake not?’", "내가 잘못 본 게 아니라면, 잘못 본 게 아니라면?\""]], [["Arthur assented, and said once more with new expression, ‘If you would be so good as to give me the address.’", "아서는 그렇다고 수긍하고, 새삼스러운 어조로 다시 한번 말했다. \"부디 호의를 베푸시어 그 주소를 알려 주신다면.\""]], [["‘Dear, dear, dear!’ exclaimed the Patriarch in sweet regret.", "\"이런, 이런, 이런!\" 가장이 달콤한 유감을 담아 외쳤다."], ["‘Tut, tut, tut!", "\"쯧, 쯧, 쯧!"], ["what a pity, what a pity!", "참으로 애석하네, 참으로 애석해!"], ["I have no address, sir.", "나는 주소가 없다네, 군."], ["Miss Wade mostly lives abroad, Mr Clennam.", "웨이드 양은 대개 외국에서 산다네, 클레넘 군."], ["She has done so for some years, and she is (if I may say so of a fellow-creature and a lady) fitful and uncertain to a fault, Mr Clennam.", "몇 해째 그래 왔지. 그리고 그녀는(같은 사람이자 숙녀를 두고 이렇게 말해도 된다면) 흠이라 할 만큼 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수가 없네, 클레넘 군."], ["I may not see her again for a long, long time.", "나도 오래오래 그녀를 다시 못 볼지도 모르지."], ["I may never see her again.", "영영 다시 못 볼지도 모르고."], ["What a pity, what a pity!’", "참으로 애석해, 참으로 애석해!\""]], [["Clennam saw now, that he had as much hope of getting assistance out of the Portrait as out of the Patriarch; but he said nevertheless:", "이제 클레넘은 그 초상화에서 도움을 얻을 가망이나 이 가장에게서 도움을 얻을 가망이나 매한가지임을 알았다. 그래도 그는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Casby, could you, for the satisfaction of the friends I have mentioned, and under any obligation of secrecy that you may consider it your duty to impose, give me any information at all touching Miss Wade?", "\"캐스비 어르신, 제가 말씀드린 친구분들의 안심을 위하여, 그리고 어르신께서 부과하는 것이 자기 의무라 여기시는 어떠한 비밀 엄수의 조건 아래에서라도, 웨이드 양에 관하여 무엇이든 알려 주실 수는 없겠습니까?"], ["I have seen her abroad, and I have seen her at home, but I know nothing of her.", "저는 그녀를 외국에서도 보았고 이 나라에서도 보았습니다만, 그녀에 관해 아는 것이 아무것도 없습니다."], ["Could you give me any account of her whatever?’", "무엇이든 그녀에 관한 이야기를 좀 들려주실 수 없겠습니까?\""]], [["‘None,’ returned the Patriarch, shaking his big head with his utmost benevolence.", "\"전혀 없네.\" 가장이 한껏 자비로운 태도로 그 큰 머리를 흔들며 대꾸했다."], ["‘None at all.", "\"전혀 없어."], ["Dear, dear, dear!", "이런, 이런, 이런!"], ["What a real pity that she stayed so short a time, and you delayed!", "그녀가 그토록 잠깐만 머물렀고 자네는 늑장을 부렸으니 참으로 애석한 노릇이야!"], ["As confidential agency business, agency business, I have occasionally paid this lady money; but what satisfaction is it to you, sir, to know that?’", "비밀스러운 대리 업무로서, 대리 업무로서, 나는 이따금 이 부인에게 돈을 지급한 적이 있네. 하지만 그걸 안다 한들 자네한테 무슨 만족이 되겠나, 군?\""]], [["‘Truly, none at all,’ said Clennam.", "\"정말이지, 아무 만족도 안 됩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Truly,’ assented the Patriarch, with a shining face as he philanthropically smiled at the fire, ‘none at all, sir.", "\"정말로,\" 가장이 박애주의자답게 불을 향해 미소 지으며 환한 얼굴로 수긍했다."], ["You hit the wise answer, Mr Clennam.", "\"아무 만족도 안 되지, 군. 자네가 현명한 답을 짚었네, 클레넘 군."], ["Truly, none at all, sir.’", "정말로, 아무 만족도 안 되지, 군.\""]], [["His turning of his smooth thumbs over one another as he sat there, was so typical to Clennam of the way in which he would make the subject revolve if it were pursued, never showing any new part of it nor allowing it to make the smallest advance, that it did much to help to convince him of his labour having been in vain.", "그가 거기 앉아 매끈한 두 엄지손가락을 서로 빙빙 돌리는 모양은, 만약 이 화제를 계속 파고든다면 그가 그것을 어떤 식으로 빙빙 돌리기만 할 뿐, 그 새로운 면이라고는 결코 보여 주지 않고 조금의 진전도 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 클레넘에게 너무도 전형적으로 보여 주는 것이어서, 그것은 자기의 수고가 헛되었음을 그에게 확신시키는 데 큰 몫을 했다."], ["He might have taken any time to think about it, for Mr Casby, well accustomed to get on anywhere by leaving everything to his bumps and his white hair, knew his strength to lie in silence.", "그는 얼마든지 시간을 들여 그 일을 생각해 보아도 좋았을 것이다. 모든 것을 자기의 울룩불룩한 머리와 백발에 맡겨 두고도 어디서든 일을 풀어 나가는 데 익숙한 캐스비 씨는, 자기의 힘이 침묵에 있음을 알고 있었으니까."], ["So there Casby sat, twirling and twirling, and making his polished head and forehead look largely benevolent in every knob.", "그리하여 캐스비는 거기 앉아 빙빙, 또 빙빙 돌리며, 자기의 윤이 나는 머리와 이마를 혹마다 더없이 자비로워 보이게 만들고 있었다."]], [["With this spectacle before him, Arthur had risen to go, when from the inner Dock where the good ship Pancks was hove down when out in no cruising ground, the noise was heard of that steamer labouring towards him.", "이 광경을 눈앞에 두고 아서가 가려고 막 일어섰을 때, 순항할 만한 곳이 못 되면 끌어 올려져 놓이는 그 훌륭한 배 '팽크스 호'가 정박해 있는 안쪽 선거(船渠)에서, 그 증기선이 그를 향해 힘겹게 다가오는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["It struck Arthur that the noise began demonstratively far off, as though Mr Pancks sought to impress on any one who might happen to think about it, that he was working on from out of hearing.", "그 소리가 마치 누구든 그 일에 대해 생각할 사람에게, 자기는 들리지 않는 곳에서부터 일하며 오고 있노라고 각인시키려는 듯, 떠들썩하게 멀찍이서부터 시작된다는 느낌이 아서에게 들었다."]], [["Mr Pancks and he shook hands, and the former brought his employer a letter or two to sign.", "팽크스 씨와 그는 악수를 했고, 팽크스 씨는 고용주에게 서명할 편지 한두 통을 가져왔다."], ["Mr Pancks in shaking hands merely scratched his eyebrow with his left forefinger and snorted once, but Clennam, who understood him better now than of old, comprehended that he had almost done for the evening and wished to say a word to him outside.", "팽크스 씨는 악수를 하면서 그저 왼쪽 집게손가락으로 자기 눈썹을 긁고 한 번 콧방귀를 뀌었을 뿐이지만, 이제는 예전보다 그를 더 잘 이해하게 된 클레넘은, 그가 오늘 저녁 일을 거의 끝냈으며 밖에서 그에게 한마디 하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Therefore, when he had taken his leave of Mr Casby, and (which was a more difficult process) of Flora, he sauntered in the neighbourhood on Mr Pancks’s line of road.", "그래서 그는 캐스비 씨에게, 그리고 (그보다 더 까다로운 절차였던) 플로라에게 작별을 고한 뒤, 팽크스 씨가 다니는 길목 근처를 어슬렁거렸다."]], [["He had waited but a short time when Mr Pancks appeared.", "그가 잠깐 기다리자 곧 팽크스 씨가 나타났다."], ["Mr Pancks shaking hands again with another expressive snort, and taking off his hat to put his hair up, Arthur thought he received his cue to speak to him as one who knew pretty well what had just now passed.", "팽크스 씨가 또 한 번 의미심장한 콧방귀와 함께 다시 악수를 하고는, 머리를 매만지려고 모자를 벗자, 아서는 방금 일어난 일을 꽤 잘 아는 사람으로서 그에게 말을 걸어도 좋다는 신호를 받은 셈이라고 생각했다."], ["Therefore he said, without any preface:", "그래서 그는 서두 없이 말했다."]], [["‘I suppose they were really gone, Pancks?’", "\"정말로 그들은 가 버렸겠지요, 팽크스?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ replied Pancks.", "\"네.\" 팽크스가 대답했다."], ["‘They were really gone.’", "\"정말로 가 버렸습니다.\""]], [["‘Does he know where to find that lady?’", "\"저분은 그 부인을 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 압니까?\""]], [["‘Can’t say.", "\"뭐라 말씀 못 드리겠습니다."], ["I should think so.’", "알 거라고 봅니다만.\""]], [["Mr Pancks did not?", "팽크스 씨는 모르는가?"], ["No, Mr Pancks did not.", "아니, 팽크스 씨는 몰랐다."], ["Did Mr Pancks know anything about her?", "팽크스 씨는 그녀에 관해 무어라도 아는가?"]], [["‘I expect,’ rejoined that worthy, ‘I know as much about her as she knows about herself.", "\"제 짐작에는,\" 그 훌륭한 사내가 대꾸했다."], ["She is somebody’s child--anybody’s, nobody’s.", "\"그녀가 자기 자신에 관해 아는 만큼은 저도 그녀에 관해 압니다."], ["Put her in a room in London here with any six people old enough to be her parents, and her parents may be there for anything she knows.", "그녀는 누군가의 자식이지요 — 아무의 자식이기도 하고, 누구의 자식도 아니기도 합니다. 여기 런던의 어느 방에 그녀를 부모뻘 되는 나이 든 사람 여섯 명과 함께 넣어 두면, 그 부모가 거기 있다 한들 그녀로서는 알 길이 없습니다."], ["They may be in any house she sees, they may be in any churchyard she passes, she may run against ‘em in any street, she may make chance acquaintance of ‘em at any time; and never know it.", "그들은 그녀가 보는 어느 집에 있을 수도 있고, 그녀가 지나치는 어느 묘지에 있을 수도 있고, 그녀가 어느 거리에서든 부딪칠 수도 있고, 그녀가 언제든 우연히 알게 될 수도 있는데 — 그러고도 결코 모르지요."], ["She knows nothing about ‘em.", "그녀는 그들에 관해 아무것도 모릅니다."], ["She knows nothing about any relative whatever.", "그 어떤 친척에 관해서도 아무것도 몰라요. 안 적도 없고요."], ["Never did. Never will.’", "앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Casby could enlighten her, perhaps?’", "\"캐스비 씨가 어쩌면 그녀를 깨우쳐 줄 수도 있겠군요?\""]], [["‘May be,’ said Pancks.", "\"그럴 수도 있지요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I expect so, but don’t know.", "\"제 짐작엔 그렇습니다만, 잘은 모릅니다."], ["He has long had money (not overmuch as I make out) in trust to dole out to her when she can’t do without it.", "그분은 오래전부터 돈을(제가 알기로 그리 많지는 않습니다만) 위탁받아서, 그녀가 도저히 없이는 못 지낼 때 조금씩 나눠 주고 있지요."], ["Sometimes she’s proud and won’t touch it for a length of time; sometimes she’s so poor that she must have it.", "어떤 때는 자존심이 세서 한참 동안 손도 안 대고, 어떤 때는 너무 궁해서 안 받고는 못 배기지요."], ["She writhes under her life.", "그녀는 제 인생에 몸부림치며 괴로워합니다."], ["A woman more angry, passionate, reckless, and revengeful never lived.", "그녀보다 더 화 많고, 격정적이고, 무모하고, 복수심에 찬 여자는 살았던 적이 없을 겁니다."], ["She came for money to-night.", "오늘 밤에는 돈 때문에 왔지요."], ["Said she had peculiar occasion for it.’", "그게 각별히 필요한 사정이 있다고 했습니다.\""]], [["‘I think,’ observed Clennam musing, ‘I by chance know what occasion--I mean into whose pocket the money is to go.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 클레넘이 골똘히 생각하며 말했다. \"그 사정이 무엇인지 제가 우연히 아는 것 같습니다 — 그러니까, 그 돈이 누구 주머니로 들어갈지 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Indeed?’ said Pancks.", "\"그래요?\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘If it’s a compact, I recommend that party to be exact in it.", "\"만약 그게 무슨 계약이라면, 저는 그 당사자에게 정확히 이행하라고 권하겠습니다. 만약 제가 그 여자한테 잘못을 저질렀다면, 그녀가 아무리 젊고 잘생겼다 해도 저는 그녀를 믿고 제 몸을 맡기지 않겠어요."], ["I wouldn’t trust myself to that woman, young and handsome as she is, if I had wronged her; no, not for twice my proprietor’s money!", "아니, 제 고용주 돈의 두 배를 준다 해도요! 다만,\""], ["Unless,’ Pancks added as a saving clause, ‘I had a lingering illness on me, and wanted to get it over.’", "팽크스가 단서를 달았다. \"제게 오래 끄는 병이 있어서 그걸 어서 끝내고 싶은 게 아니라면 말입니다.\""]], [["Arthur, hurriedly reviewing his own observation of her, found it to tally pretty nearly with Mr Pancks’s view.", "아서는 자기가 그녀를 관찰한 바를 서둘러 되짚어 보고는, 그것이 팽크스 씨의 견해와 상당히 가깝게 들어맞는다는 것을 알았다."]], [["‘The wonder is to me,’ pursued Pancks, ‘that she has never done for my proprietor, as the only person connected with her story she can lay hold of.", "\"제가 놀라운 건 말입니다,\" 팽크스가 이어 갔다."], ["Mentioning that, I may tell you, between ourselves, that I am sometimes tempted to do for him myself.’", "\"그녀가 자기 사연과 연관된 유일하게 붙들 수 있는 사람인 제 고용주를 여태 끝장내지 않았다는 거예요. 그 말이 나온 김에, 우리끼리 얘기지만, 저 자신도 이따금 그분을 끝장내고 싶은 유혹을 느낀다는 걸 말씀드리지요.\""]], [["Arthur started and said, ‘Dear me, Pancks, don’t say that!’", "아서는 흠칫 놀라며 말했다. \"이런, 팽크스, 그런 말 하지 마세요!\""]], [["‘Understand me,’ said Pancks, extending five cropped coaly finger-nails on Arthur’s arm; ‘I don’t mean, cut his throat.", "\"제 말을 알아들으세요.\" 팽크스가 석탄 가루로 시커메진 짤막한 손톱 다섯 개를 아서의 팔에 갖다 대며 말했다."], ["But by all that’s precious, if he goes too far, I’ll cut his hair!’", "\"그분 목을 따겠다는 게 아닙니다. 하지만 세상 모든 소중한 것을 두고 맹세컨대, 그분이 너무 지나치면, 저는 그분 머리카락을 잘라 버릴 겁니다!\""]], [["Having exhibited himself in the new light of enunciating this tremendous threat, Mr Pancks, with a countenance of grave import, snorted several times and steamed away.", "이 어마어마한 협박을 토해 내는 새로운 면모로 자신을 드러내 보인 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 자못 심각한 표정으로 몇 차례 콧방귀를 뀌고는 증기를 내뿜으며 떠나갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken", "# CHAPTER 10. The Dreams of Mrs Flintwinch thicken"]], [["The shady waiting-rooms of the Circumlocution Office, where he passed a good deal of time in company with various troublesome Convicts who were under sentence to be broken alive on that wheel, had afforded Arthur Clennam ample leisure, in three or four successive days, to exhaust the subject of his late glimpse of Miss Wade and Tattycoram.", "그가 살아 있는 채로 그 형틀에 매달려 부서질 판결을 받은 온갖 골치 아픈 죄수들과 어울려 적잖은 시간을 보낸 에두름국의 그늘진 대기실들은, 사나흘 연이은 날 동안 아서 클레넘에게 미스 웨이드와 태티코럼을 얼핏 본 일에 관한 생각을 다 짜내고도 남을 한가한 시간을 안겨 주었다."], ["He had been able to make no more of it and no less of it, and in this unsatisfactory condition he was fain to leave it.", "그는 거기서 더 많은 것도 더 적은 것도 끌어낼 수 없었고, 이 미진한 상태로 그 일을 내버려 둘 수밖에 없었다."]], [["During this space he had not been to his mother’s dismal old house.", "이 기간 동안 그는 어머니의 음울한 옛집에 가지 않았다."], ["One of his customary evenings for repairing thither now coming round, he left his dwelling and his partner at nearly nine o’clock, and slowly walked in the direction of that grim home of his youth.", "그곳을 찾아가는 평소 저녁 가운데 하루가 다시 돌아오자, 그는 거의 아홉 시 무렵에 자기 거처와 동업자를 떠나, 젊은 시절의 그 음산한 집을 향해 천천히 걸어갔다."]], [["It always affected his imagination as wrathful, mysterious, and sad; and his imagination was sufficiently impressible to see the whole neighbourhood under some tinge of its dark shadow. As he went along, upon a dreary night, the dim streets by which he went, seemed all depositories of oppressive secrets. The deserted counting-houses, with their secrets of books and papers locked up in chests and safes; the banking-houses, with their secrets of strong rooms and wells, the keys of which were in a very few secret pockets and a very few secret breasts; the secrets of all the dispersed grinders in the vast mill, among whom there were doubtless plunderers, forgers, and trust-betrayers of many sorts, whom the light of any day that dawned might reveal; he could have fancied that these things, in hiding, imparted a heaviness to the air. The shadow thickening and thickening as he approached its source, he thought of the secrets of the lonely church-vaults, where the people who had hoarded and secreted in iron coffers were in their turn similarly hoarded, not yet at rest from doing harm; and then of the secrets of the river, as it rolled its turbid tide between two frowning wildernesses of secrets, extending, thick and dense, for many miles, and warding off the free air and the free country swept by winds and wings of birds.", "그 집은 언제나 그의 상상 속에서 분노에 차 있고, 신비롭고, 슬픈 것으로 다가왔다. 그리고 그의 상상은 충분히 예민해서, 그 일대 전체를 그 어두운 그림자의 어떤 빛깔로 물든 채 바라보았다. 음울한 밤, 그가 지나가는 동안 그가 걷는 어슴푸레한 거리들은 죄다 짓누르는 비밀을 간직한 저장소처럼 보였다. 책과 서류의 비밀을 궤짝과 금고에 잠가 둔 채 텅 빈 계산소들, 견고한 방과 금고실의 비밀을 간직한 은행들 — 그 열쇠는 극소수의 비밀 주머니와 극소수의 비밀스러운 가슴속에 들어 있었다. 그 거대한 방앗간 속에 흩어진 모든 분쇄자들의 비밀들 — 그들 가운데에는 의심할 바 없이 온갖 종류의 약탈자, 위조범, 신의를 저버린 자들이 있었고, 동이 트는 어느 날의 빛이 그들을 드러낼 수도 있었다. 그는 이런 것들이 숨어서 공기에 무거움을 더한다고 상상할 수도 있었다. 그 근원에 다가갈수록 그림자가 짙어지고 또 짙어지자, 그는 외로운 교회 지하 납골당의 비밀들을 떠올렸다. 쇠궤짝 속에 돈을 쌓고 감춰 두었던 사람들이 이제 그들 차례가 되어 비슷하게 쌓여 있되, 아직 해악을 끼치는 일에서 벗어나 안식하지 못하고 있는 그곳을. 그러고는 강의 비밀들을 떠올렸다 — 강은 두 갈래의 험상궂은 비밀의 황야 사이로 탁한 물결을 굴리며, 두텁고 빽빽하게 여러 마일에 걸쳐 뻗어, 바람과 새의 날개가 휩쓸고 가는 자유로운 공기와 자유로운 들판을 가로막았다."]], [["The shadow still darkening as he drew near the house, the melancholy room which his father had once occupied, haunted by the appealing face he had himself seen fade away with him when there was no other watcher by the bed, arose before his mind.", "그가 집에 가까워질수록 그림자가 여전히 짙어지자, 한때 아버지가 머물렀던 그 우울한 방이 그의 마음속에 떠올랐다. 침상 곁에 다른 지켜보는 이가 없을 때 아버지와 함께 스러져 가는 것을 그 자신이 보았던 그 애원하는 얼굴이 그 방에 떠돌고 있었다."], ["Its close air was secret.", "그 방의 답답한 공기가 비밀스러웠다."], ["The gloom, and must, and dust of the whole tenement, were secret.", "그 집 전체의 음울함과 곰팡내와 먼지가 비밀스러웠다."], ["At the heart of it his mother presided, inflexible of face, indomitable of will, firmly holding all the secrets of her own and his father’s life, and austerely opposing herself, front to front, to the great final secret of all life.", "그 중심에 그의 어머니가 군림하고 있었다 — 얼굴은 흔들림이 없고, 의지는 굽힐 줄 모르며, 자신과 남편 생애의 모든 비밀을 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 모든 생의 그 거대한 마지막 비밀에 맞서 정면으로, 준엄하게 자신을 버티고 서 있었다."]], [["He had turned into the narrow and steep street from which the court of enclosure wherein the house stood opened, when another footstep turned into it behind him, and so close upon his own that he was jostled to the wall.", "그가 집이 서 있는 둘러막힌 안마당으로 통하는, 좁고 가파른 거리로 막 접어들었을 때, 또 다른 발소리가 그의 뒤에서 그 거리로 접어들었다 — 그것은 그의 발소리에 너무도 바짝 붙어 있어서 그는 벽 쪽으로 떠밀렸다."], ["As his mind was teeming with these thoughts, the encounter took him altogether unprepared, so that the other passenger had had time to say, boisterously, ‘Pardon!", "그의 마음이 이런 생각들로 가득 차 있던 터라, 이 마주침은 그를 전혀 무방비로 덮쳤고, 그래서 다른 행인은 호기롭게 \"실례! 내 잘못이 아니오!\""], ["Not my fault!’ and to pass on before the instant had elapsed which was requisite to his recovery of the realities about him.", "하고 말하고는, 그가 자기 주위의 현실을 되찾는 데 필요한 그 순간이 채 지나기도 전에 앞서 지나가 버렸다."]], [["When that moment had flashed away, he saw that the man striding on before him was the man who had been so much in his mind during the last few days.", "그 순간이 번쩍 스쳐 지나가자, 그는 자기 앞에서 성큼성큼 걸어가는 사내가 지난 며칠간 그토록 그의 마음을 차지하고 있던 바로 그 사내임을 알아보았다."], ["It was no casual resemblance, helped out by the force of the impression the man made upon him.", "그것은 그 사내가 그에게 남긴 인상의 힘에 떠받쳐진 우연한 닮음이 아니었다."], ["It was the man; the man he had followed in company with the girl, and whom he had overheard talking to Miss Wade.", "바로 그 사내였다. 그가 그 처녀와 함께 뒤따랐고, 미스 웨이드에게 말하는 것을 엿들었던 그 사내였다."]], [["The street was a sharp descent and was crooked too, and the man (who although not drunk had the air of being flushed with some strong drink) went down it so fast that Clennam lost him as he looked at him.", "거리는 가파른 내리막인 데다 구불구불하기도 했는데, 그 사내는(취하지는 않았으나 무슨 독한 술기운으로 얼굴이 붉어진 기색이었다) 어찌나 빨리 내려가는지, 클레넘은 보고 있는 사이에 그를 놓쳐 버렸다."], ["With no defined intention of following him, but with an impulse to keep the figure in view a little longer, Clennam quickened his pace to pass the twist in the street which hid him from his sight.", "그를 따라가겠다는 뚜렷한 의도는 없었으나, 그 모습을 조금만 더 시야에 두고 싶은 충동에서, 클레넘은 걸음을 재촉해 그를 시야에서 가렸던 거리의 굽이를 돌아섰다."], ["On turning it, he saw the man no more.", "그 굽이를 돌자, 그는 그 사내를 더는 볼 수 없었다."]], [["Standing now, close to the gateway of his mother’s house, he looked down the street: but it was empty.", "이제 어머니의 집 대문 가까이 서서 그는 거리 아래쪽을 내려다보았다."], ["There was no projecting shadow large enough to obscure the man; there was no turning near that he could have taken; nor had there been any audible sound of the opening and closing of a door.", "그러나 거리는 텅 비어 있었다. 그 사내를 가릴 만큼 큰 돌출된 그늘도 없었고, 그가 접어들 만한 가까운 모퉁이도 없었으며, 문이 열리고 닫히는 소리도 들리지 않았다."], ["Nevertheless, he concluded that the man must have had a key in his hand, and must have opened one of the many house-doors and gone in.", "그럼에도 그는, 그 사내가 손에 열쇠를 쥐고 있었음에 틀림없고, 그 수많은 집 문 가운데 하나를 열고 안으로 들어갔음에 틀림없다고 결론지었다."]], [["Ruminating on this strange chance and strange glimpse, he turned into the court-yard.", "이 기이한 우연과 기이한 일별을 곱씹으며, 그는 안마당으로 접어들었다."], ["As he looked, by mere habit, towards the feebly lighted windows of his mother’s room, his eyes encountered the figure he had just lost, standing against the iron railings of the little waste enclosure looking up at those windows and laughing to himself.", "그저 습관처럼 어머니 방의 희미하게 불 켜진 창문 쪽으로 시선을 던지는데, 그의 눈이 방금 놓쳤던 그 모습과 마주쳤다. 그 사내는 작은 황량한 빈터의 쇠난간에 기대어 서서 그 창문들을 올려다보며 혼자 웃고 있었다."], ["Some of the many vagrant cats who were always prowling about there by night, and who had taken fright at him, appeared to have stopped when he had stopped, and were looking at him with eyes by no means unlike his own from tops of walls and porches, and other safe points of pause.", "밤이면 늘 그 주위를 어슬렁거리던 수많은 떠돌이 고양이들 가운데 더러는, 그에게 겁을 먹고 그가 멈춘 자리에 함께 멈춰 선 듯, 담장 꼭대기와 현관 차양과 안전하게 쉴 만한 다른 지점들에서 그 사내 자신의 눈과 조금도 다르지 않은 눈으로 그를 바라보고 있었다."], ["He had only halted for a moment to entertain himself thus; he immediately went forward, throwing the end of his cloak off his shoulder as he went, ascended the unevenly sunken steps, and knocked a sounding knock at the door.", "그는 이렇게 혼자 즐기느라 잠깐 멈춰 섰을 뿐, 곧장 앞으로 나아가, 가면서 외투 자락을 어깨에서 휙 젖히고, 고르지 않게 내려앉은 계단을 올라, 문을 쾅쾅 울리도록 두드렸다."]], [["Clennam’s surprise was not so absorbing but that he took his resolution without any incertitude.", "클레넘의 놀라움이 아무리 컸어도 그가 한 점 망설임 없이 결심을 굳히지 못할 만큼은 아니었다."], ["He went up to the door too, and ascended the steps too.", "그 역시 문으로 다가가, 그 역시 계단을 올랐다."], ["His friend looked at him with a braggart air, and sang to himself.", "그의 그 친구는 허세 부리는 태도로 그를 바라보며 혼자 노래를 흥얼거렸다."]], [["‘Who passes by this road so late?", "\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Compagnon de la Majolaine; Who passes by this road so late?", "마졸렌의 길동무여, 이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Always gay!’", "언제나 즐거운 자!\""]], [["After which he knocked again.", "그러고는 다시 문을 두드렸다."]], [["‘You are impatient, sir,’ said Arthur.", "\"성미가 급하시군요, 선생.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘I am, sir.", "\"그렇소이다, 선생. 목숨을 걸고서라도, 선생.\""], ["Death of my life, sir,’ returned the stranger, ‘it’s my character to be impatient!’", "낯선 사내가 받았다. \"성미가 급한 건 내 천성이오!\""]], [["The sound of Mistress Affery cautiously chaining the door before she opened it, caused them both to look that way.", "애프리 부인이 문을 열기 전에 조심스럽게 사슬을 거는 소리에 두 사람 다 그쪽을 바라보았다."], ["Affery opened it a very little, with a flaring candle in her hands and asked who was that, at that time of night, with that knock!", "애프리는 손에 너울거리는 촛불을 들고 문을 아주 조금만 열고는, 이 야밤에 그런 식으로 문을 두드리는 게 누구냐고 물었다. \"어머, 아서 도련님!\""], ["‘Why, Arthur!’ she added with astonishment, seeing him first.", "그를 먼저 알아보고 그녀가 놀라며 덧붙였다. \"정말 도련님이 아니에요?"], ["‘Not you sure?", "아이고, 맙소사!"], ["Ah, Lord save us!", "아니야,\""], ["No,’ she cried out, seeing the other.", "다른 사내를 보고 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["‘Him again!’", "\"또 저 사람이야!\""]], [["‘It’s true!", "\"맞소!"], ["Him again, dear Mrs Flintwinch,’ cried the stranger.", "또 나요, 사랑스러운 플린트윈치 부인.\" 낯선 사내가 외쳤다."], ["‘Open the door, and let me take my dear friend Jeremiah to my arms!", "\"문을 여시오, 내 친애하는 벗 제러마이어를 품에 안게 해 주시오!"], ["Open the door, and let me hasten myself to embrace my Flintwinch!’", "문을 여시오, 어서 가서 내 플린트윈치를 껴안게 해 주시오!\""]], [["‘He’s not at home,’ cried Affery.", "\"그이는 집에 없어요.\" 애프리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘Fetch him!’ cried the stranger.", "\"데려오시오!\" 낯선 사내가 외쳤다."], ["‘Fetch my Flintwinch!", "\"내 플린트윈치를 데려오시오!"], ["Tell him that it is his old Blandois, who comes from arriving in England; tell him that it is his little boy who is here, his cabbage, his well-beloved!", "잉글랜드에 막 도착한 그의 옛 블랑두아가 왔다고 전하시오. 여기 그의 어린 소년이, 그의 양배추가, 그의 더없이 사랑받는 이가 와 있다고 전하시오!"], ["Open the door, beautiful Mrs Flintwinch, and in the meantime let me to pass upstairs, to present my compliments--homage of Blandois--to my lady!", "문을 여시오, 아름다운 플린트윈치 부인, 그러는 동안 나는 위층으로 올라가, 마님께 인사를 — 블랑두아의 경의를 — 올리게 해 주시오! 마님께서는 여전히 살아 계시오?"], ["My lady lives always?", "잘됐군."], ["It is well. Open then!’", "자, 어서 여시오!\""]], [["To Arthur’s increased surprise, Mistress Affery, stretching her eyes wide at himself, as if in warning that this was not a gentleman for him to interfere with, drew back the chain, and opened the door.", "아서의 놀라움을 더욱 키운 것은, 애프리 부인이 이 사람은 도련님이 끼어들 만한 신사가 아니라고 경고하듯 그를 향해 눈을 휘둥그레 뜬 채, 사슬을 풀고 문을 열었다는 것이었다."], ["The stranger, without ceremony, walked into the hall, leaving Arthur to follow him.", "낯선 사내는 격식도 없이 현관으로 걸어 들어와, 아서더러 자기를 따라오게 내버려 두었다."]], [["‘Despatch then!", "\"그러니 서두르시오!"], ["Achieve then!", "어서 해치우시오!"], ["Bring my Flintwinch!", "내 플린트윈치를 데려오시오!"], ["Announce me to my lady!’ cried the stranger, clanking about the stone floor.", "마님께 내가 왔다고 아뢰시오!\" 낯선 사내가 돌바닥을 쩌렁쩌렁 울리며 외쳤다."]], [["‘Pray tell me, Affery,’ said Arthur aloud and sternly, as he surveyed him from head to foot with indignation; ‘who is this gentleman?’", "\"애프리, 말 좀 해 주오.\" 아서가 분노에 차 그를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보며 크고 엄한 목소리로 말했다. \"이 신사가 누구요?\""]], [["‘Pray tell me, Affery,’ the stranger repeated in his turn, ‘who--ha, ha, ha!--who is this gentleman?’", "\"애프리, 말 좀 해 주오.\" 낯선 사내가 이번엔 자기가 따라 했다. \"누구냐고 — 하, 하, 하! — 이 신사가 누구냐고?\""]], [["The voice of Mrs Clennam opportunely called from her chamber above, ‘Affery, let them both come up.", "마침맞게 위층 방에서 클레넘 부인의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"애프리, 둘 다 올라오게 해라."], ["Arthur, come straight to me!’", "아서, 곧장 내게로 오너라!\""]], [["‘Arthur?’ exclaimed Blandois, taking off his hat at arm’s length, and bringing his heels together from a great stride in making him a flourishing bow.", "\"아서?\" 블랑두아가 팔을 쭉 뻗어 모자를 벗고는, 성큼 내디딘 자세에서 두 발꿈치를 모으며 그에게 거창한 절을 올리며 외쳤다."], ["‘The son of my lady?", "\"마님의 아드님이시군요?"], ["I am the all-devoted of the son of my lady!’", "나는 마님 아드님께 온 마음을 다 바치는 종복이올시다!\""]], [["Arthur looked at him again in no more flattering manner than before, and, turning on his heel without acknowledgment, went up-stairs.", "아서는 그를 앞서와 조금도 다르지 않은 호의 없는 태도로 다시 한번 바라보고는, 아무 응대도 없이 발꿈치를 돌려 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The visitor followed him up-stairs.", "손님이 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["Mistress Affery took the key from behind the door, and deftly slipped out to fetch her lord.", "애프리 부인은 문 뒤에서 열쇠를 집어 들고는, 자기 주인을 데려오려고 날렵하게 빠져나갔다."]], [["A bystander, informed of the previous appearance of Monsieur Blandois in that room, would have observed a difference in Mrs Clennam’s present reception of him.", "블랑두아 씨가 앞서 그 방에 나타났던 일을 알고 있는 구경꾼이라면, 클레넘 부인이 이번에 그를 맞이하는 데서 어떤 차이를 알아챘을 것이다."], ["Her face was not one to betray it; and her suppressed manner, and her set voice, were equally under her control.", "그녀의 얼굴은 그것을 드러낼 만한 얼굴이 아니었고, 억눌린 태도와 가라앉은 목소리도 똑같이 그녀의 통제 아래 있었다."], ["It wholly consisted in her never taking her eyes off his face from the moment of his entrance, and in her twice or thrice, when he was becoming noisy, swaying herself a very little forward in the chair in which she sat upright, with her hands immovable upon its elbows; as if she gave him the assurance that he should be presently heard at any length he would.", "그 차이란 오로지, 그가 들어선 순간부터 그녀가 그의 얼굴에서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다는 것, 그리고 그가 시끄러워질 무렵이면 두세 차례, 똑바로 앉은 의자에서 두 손을 팔걸이 위에 미동 없이 둔 채 몸을 아주 조금 앞으로 기울였다는 것에 있었다 — 마치 그가 하고 싶은 만큼 얼마든지 길게 말해도 곧 들어주겠다는 보장을 그에게 주는 듯이."], ["Arthur did not fail to observe this; though the difference between the present occasion and the former was not within his power of observation.", "아서도 이것을 놓치지 않고 보았다. 비록 이번 경우와 지난번 사이의 차이는 그가 알아챌 수 있는 범위 밖이었지만."]], [["‘Madame,’ said Blandois, ‘do me the honour to present me to Monsieur, your son.", "\"부인,\" 블랑두아가 말했다."], ["It appears to me, madame, that Monsieur, your son, is disposed to complain of me.", "\"영광스럽게도 저를 아드님이신 무슈께 소개해 주십시오. 제가 보기에 부인, 아드님이신 무슈께서는 저를 두고 불평하실 작정인 듯합니다."], ["He is not polite.’", "그분은 정중하지가 않으십니다.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Arthur, striking in expeditiously, ‘whoever you are, and however you come to be here, if I were the master of this house I would lose no time in placing you on the outside of it.’", "\"선생,\" 아서가 재빨리 끼어들며 말했다. \"당신이 누구든, 또 어떻게 여기 오게 되었든, 만약 내가 이 집의 주인이라면 나는 지체 없이 당신을 이 집 바깥으로 내보낼 것이오.\""]], [["‘But you are not,’ said his mother, without looking at him.", "\"하지만 너는 주인이 아니다.\" 어머니가 그를 보지도 않은 채 말했다."], ["‘Unfortunately for the gratification of your unreasonable temper, you are not the master, Arthur.’", "\"네 그 분별없는 성미를 풀어 줄 만큼 운이 따르지 않게도, 너는 주인이 아니다, 아서.\""]], [["‘I make no claim to be, mother.", "\"제가 주인이라고 주장하는 게 아닙니다, 어머니."], ["If I object to this person’s manner of conducting himself here, and object to it so much, that if I had any authority here I certainly would not suffer him to remain a minute, I object on your account.’", "제가 이 사람이 여기서 처신하는 방식에 반대한다면 — 그것도 어찌나 반대하는지, 만약 제게 이곳에서 어떤 권한이라도 있다면 단 일 분도 그가 머무는 것을 결코 용납하지 않을 만큼 반대한다면 — 그것은 어머니를 위해 반대하는 것입니다.\""]], [["‘In the case of objection being necessary,’ she returned, ‘I could object for myself.", "\"반대가 필요한 경우라면,\" 그녀가 받았다."], ["And of course I should.’", "\"나는 나 스스로 반대할 수 있다. 그리고 당연히 그리할 것이다.\""]], [["The subject of their dispute, who had seated himself, laughed aloud, and rapped his legs with his hand.", "이들 다툼의 대상은, 자리에 앉은 채로, 소리 내어 웃으며 손으로 자기 다리를 탁탁 쳤다."]], [["‘You have no right,’ said Mrs Clennam, always intent on Blandois, however directly she addressed her son, ‘to speak to the prejudice of any gentleman (least of all a gentleman from another country), because he does not conform to your standard, or square his behaviour by your rules.", "\"너에게는 권리가 없다.\" 클레넘 부인이, 아무리 아들에게 곧장 말을 건네더라도 시선만은 늘 블랑두아에게 고정한 채로 말했다."], ["It is possible that the gentleman may, on similar grounds, object to you.’", "\"어떤 신사든(더구나 다른 나라에서 온 신사라면 더욱) 그가 네 기준에 맞추지 않는다고, 또는 네 규칙에 자기 행동을 맞추지 않는다고 해서, 그를 헐뜯어 말할 권리는 없다. 어쩌면 그 신사도 비슷한 이유로 너를 못마땅해할지도 모른다.\""]], [["‘I hope so,’ returned Arthur.", "\"그러기를 바랍니다.\" 아서가 받았다."]], [["‘The gentleman,’ pursued Mrs Clennam, ‘on a former occasion brought a letter of recommendation to us from highly esteemed and responsible correspondents.", "\"그 신사는,\" 클레넘 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["I am perfectly unacquainted with the gentleman’s object in coming here at present.", "\"전번에 우리에게 대단히 존경받고 신뢰할 만한 거래처들로부터 추천장을 가져왔다. 나는 그 신사가 지금 여기 온 목적을 전혀 알지 못한다."], ["I am entirely ignorant of it, and cannot be supposed likely to be able to form the remotest guess at its nature;’ her habitual frown became stronger, as she very slowly and weightily emphasised those words; ‘but, when the gentleman proceeds to explain his object, as I shall beg him to have the goodness to do to myself and Flintwinch, when Flintwinch returns, it will prove, no doubt, to be one more or less in the usual way of our business, which it will be both our business and our pleasure to advance.", "나는 그것을 도무지 모르며, 그 성격을 조금이라도 짐작할 수 있으리라 여겨질 리도 없다.\" 그녀가 그 말들을 아주 천천히 무게 있게 힘주어 말하자, 늘 짓던 찌푸림이 한층 짙어졌다."], ["It can be nothing else.’", "\"하지만 그 신사가 그 목적을 설명하기에 이르면 — 플린트윈치가 돌아오면 나와 플린트윈치에게 그리 친절을 베풀어 달라고 청할 참이다만 — 그것은 틀림없이, 어느 정도는 우리 사업의 일상적인 방식에 속하는 일로 드러날 테고, 그것을 진척시키는 것은 우리의 일이자 기쁨이 될 것이다. 그 외의 다른 것일 리가 없다.\""]], [["‘We shall see, madame!’ said the man of business.", "\"두고 보지요, 부인!\" 그 사업가가 말했다."]], [["‘We shall see,’ she assented.", "\"두고 보자꾸나.\" 그녀가 동의했다."], ["‘The gentleman is acquainted with Flintwinch; and when the gentleman was in London last, I remember to have heard that he and Flintwinch had some entertainment or good-fellowship together.", "\"그 신사는 플린트윈치와 아는 사이다. 그리고 그 신사가 지난번 런던에 있었을 때, 나는 그와 플린트윈치가 함께 무슨 흥겨운 자리나 친목을 가졌다는 말을 들은 기억이 있다."], ["I am not in the way of knowing much that passes outside this room, and the jingle of little worldly things beyond it does not much interest me; but I remember to have heard that.’", "나는 이 방 바깥에서 일어나는 일을 많이 알 처지가 못 되고, 이 방 너머의 자질구레한 세속사의 소란에는 별 관심이 없다만, 그것만은 들은 기억이 있다.\""]], [["‘Right, madame.", "\"옳습니다, 부인."], ["It is true.’ He laughed again, and whistled the burden of the tune he had sung at the door.", "사실이고말고요.\" 그가 다시 웃고는, 문 앞에서 불렀던 곡조의 후렴을 휘파람으로 불었다."]], [["‘Therefore, Arthur,’ said his mother, ‘the gentleman comes here as an acquaintance, and no stranger; and it is much to be regretted that your unreasonable temper should have found offence in him.", "\"그러니 아서,\" 그의 어머니가 말했다."], ["I regret it.", "\"그 신사는 아는 사람으로서 여기에 온 것이지, 낯선 이로서 온 것이 아니다."], ["I say so to the gentleman.", "네 그 분별없는 성미가 그분에게서 거슬리는 점을 찾아냈다니 심히 유감스러운 일이다. 나는 그것을 유감으로 여긴다."], ["You will not say so, I know; therefore I say it for myself and Flintwinch, since with us two the gentleman’s business lies.’", "그 신사께 그렇다고 말씀드린다. 너는 그리 말하지 않으리라는 걸 알고 있으니, 나와 플린트윈치를 대신해 내가 말하는 것이다 — 그 신사의 용무는 우리 두 사람과 얽힌 것이므로.\""]], [["The key of the door below was now heard in the lock, and the door was heard to open and close.", "이제 아래층 문의 열쇠가 자물쇠에 꽂히는 소리가 들리고, 문이 열렸다가 닫히는 소리가 들렸다."], ["In due sequence Mr Flintwinch appeared; on whose entrance the visitor rose from his chair, laughing loud, and folded him in a close embrace.", "차례대로 플린트윈치 씨가 나타났다. 그가 들어서자 손님이 의자에서 일어나 큰 소리로 웃으며 그를 꼭 껴안았다."]], [["‘How goes it, my cherished friend!’ said he.", "\"어떻게 지내시오, 내 소중한 벗이여!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘How goes the world, my Flintwinch?", "\"세상살이는 어떻소, 내 플린트윈치?"], ["Rose-coloured?", "장밋빛이오?"], ["So much the better, so much the better!", "그렇다면 더없이 좋소, 더없이 좋소!"], ["Ah, but you look charming!", "아, 그런데 보기 좋구려!"], ["Ah, but you look young and fresh as the flowers of Spring!", "아, 그런데 봄꽃처럼 젊고 싱그러워 보이는구려!"], ["Ah, good little boy!", "아, 착한 어린 소년!"], ["Brave child, brave child!’", "용감한 아이, 용감한 아이!\""]], [["While heaping these compliments on Mr Flintwinch, he rolled him about with a hand on each of his shoulders, until the staggerings of that gentleman, who under the circumstances was dryer and more twisted than ever, were like those of a teetotum nearly spent.", "플린트윈치 씨에게 이런 찬사를 잔뜩 퍼붓는 동안, 그는 두 손을 그의 양어깨에 얹은 채 그를 이리저리 흔들어 댔다. 그러자 이런 형편에 그 어느 때보다 더 메마르고 더 비틀린 그 신사의 비틀거림은, 거의 힘이 다한 팽이의 그것과 흡사해졌다."]], [["‘I had a presentiment, last time, that we should be better and more intimately acquainted.", "\"지난번에 나는 우리가 더 친해지고 더 가깝게 알고 지내게 되리라는 예감이 들었소."], ["Is it coming on you, Flintwinch?", "그게 당신에게 다가오고 있소, 플린트윈치?"], ["Is it yet coming on?’", "아직 다가오고 있소?\""]], [["‘Why, no, sir,’ retorted Mr Flintwinch.", "\"글쎄올시다, 선생.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 되받았다."], ["‘Not unusually.", "\"별달리는 아니올시다."], ["Hadn’t you better be seated?", "앉으시는 게 낫지 않겠소?"], ["You have been calling for some more of that port, sir, I guess?’", "그 포도주를 좀 더 청하셨던 것 같습니다만, 선생, 그렇지요?\""]], [["‘Ah, Little joker!", "\"아, 귀여운 익살꾼!"], ["Little pig!’ cried the visitor.", "귀여운 새끼 돼지!\" 손님이 외쳤다."], ["‘Ha ha ha ha!’ And throwing Mr Flintwinch away, as a closing piece of raillery, he sat down again.", "\"하 하 하 하!\" 그러고는 마무리 삼아 한바탕 놀리듯 플린트윈치 씨를 휙 내던지고 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["The amazement, suspicion, resentment, and shame, with which Arthur looked on at all this, struck him dumb.", "이 모든 광경을 바라보는 아서를 사로잡은 놀라움, 의혹, 분노, 수치심이 그를 말문이 막히게 했다."], ["Mr Flintwinch, who had spun backward some two or three yards under the impetus last given to him, brought himself up with a face completely unchanged in its stolidity except as it was affected by shortness of breath, and looked hard at Arthur.", "마지막으로 받은 그 힘에 떠밀려 두세 야드쯤 뒤로 빙그르르 밀려났던 플린트윈치 씨는, 숨이 가빠진 것 말고는 그 둔감한 표정이 조금도 변하지 않은 얼굴로 몸을 바로 세우고는, 아서를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Not a whit less reticent and wooden was Mr Flintwinch outwardly, than in the usual course of things: the only perceptible difference in him being that the knot of cravat which was generally under his ear, had worked round to the back of his head: where it formed an ornamental appendage not unlike a bagwig, and gave him something of a courtly appearance.", "겉보기에 플린트윈치 씨는 여느 때와 다름없이 입을 다물고 나무토막 같았다. 그에게서 알아챌 수 있는 단 하나의 차이라면, 평소 귀 밑에 있던 넥타이 매듭이 머리 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 마치 가발 끄트머리 같은 장식물을 이루어, 그에게 어딘가 궁정 신하다운 풍모를 더해 주고 있다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["As Mrs Clennam never removed her eyes from Blandois (on whom they had some effect, as a steady look has on a lower sort of dog), so Jeremiah never removed his from Arthur.", "클레넘 부인이 블랑두아에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았듯이(그것은 마치 낮은 종류의 개에게 던지는 한결같은 눈길처럼 그에게 어떤 영향을 끼쳤다), 제러마이어도 아서에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["It was as if they had tacitly agreed to take their different provinces.", "마치 그들이 각자의 관할을 나누기로 암묵적으로 합의한 것 같았다."], ["Thus, in the ensuing silence, Jeremiah stood scraping his chin and looking at Arthur as though he were trying to screw his thoughts out of him with an instrument.", "그리하여 뒤이은 침묵 속에서, 제러마이어는 턱을 쓱쓱 문지르며, 마치 어떤 연장으로 아서에게서 그 속마음을 비틀어 빼내려는 듯 그를 바라보며 서 있었다."]], [["After a little, the visitor, as if he felt the silence irksome, rose, and impatiently put himself with his back to the sacred fire which had burned through so many years.", "잠시 뒤, 손님은 그 침묵이 거북스러운 듯, 일어나서 그토록 오랜 세월 타올라 온 그 신성한 난롯불에 등을 대고 못마땅하게 자리 잡았다."], ["Thereupon Mrs Clennam said, moving one of her hands for the first time, and moving it very slightly with an action of dismissal:", "그러자 클레넘 부인이, 처음으로 한 손을 움직이며, 그것도 물러가라는 동작으로 아주 살짝 움직이며 말했다."]], [["‘Please to leave us to our business, Arthur.’", "\"우리 일은 우리에게 맡기고 자리를 비켜 주렴, 아서.\""]], [["‘Mother, I do so with reluctance.’", "\"어머니, 저는 내키지 않는 마음으로 그리합니다.\""]], [["‘Never mind with what,’ she returned, ‘or with what not.", "\"무엇이 내키는지,\" 그녀가 받았다."], ["Please to leave us.", "\"혹은 무엇이 내키지 않는지는 상관없다."], ["Come back at any other time when you may consider it a duty to bury half an hour wearily here.", "자리를 비켜 주렴. 여기서 반 시간을 따분하게 묻어 두는 것을 의무라 여길 만한 다른 어느 때든 다시 오너라."], ["Good night.’", "잘 자거라.\""]], [["She held up her muffled fingers that he might touch them with his, according to their usual custom, and he stood over her wheeled chair to touch her face with his lips.", "그녀는 그가 평소 습관대로 자기 손가락에 그의 손가락을 갖다 댈 수 있도록 천에 감싼 손가락을 들어 올렸고, 그는 입술로 그녀의 얼굴을 만지려 바퀴 달린 의자 위로 몸을 숙였다."], ["He thought, then, that her cheek was more strained than usual, and that it was colder.", "그때 그는 그녀의 뺨이 여느 때보다 더 팽팽하고, 더 차갑다고 느꼈다."], ["As he followed the direction of her eyes, in rising again, towards Mr Flintwinch’s good friend, Mr Blandois, Mr Blandois snapped his finger and thumb with one loud contemptuous snap.", "다시 몸을 일으키며 그녀의 눈길이 향하는 쪽을 따라 플린트윈치 씨의 좋은 벗 블랑두아 씨 쪽을 바라보자, 블랑두아 씨가 엄지와 검지로 경멸에 찬 소리를 한 번 크게 딱 튕겼다."]], [["‘I leave your--your business acquaintance in my mother’s room, Mr Flintwinch,’ said Clennam, ‘with a great deal of surprise and a great deal of unwillingness.’", "\"플린트윈치 씨, 나는 어머니의 방에 당신의 — 당신의 사업상 아는 분을 남겨 두고 갑니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"참으로 큰 놀라움과 참으로 큰 내키지 않음을 안고서 말입니다.\""]], [["The person referred to snapped his finger and thumb again.", "방금 언급된 그 사람이 다시 엄지와 검지를 딱 튕겼다."]], [["‘Good night, mother.’", "\"안녕히 주무세요, 어머니.\""]], [["‘Good night.’", "\"잘 자거라.\""]], [["‘I had a friend once, my good comrade Flintwinch,’ said Blandois, standing astride before the fire, and so evidently saying it to arrest Clennam’s retreating steps, that he lingered near the door; ‘I had a friend once, who had heard so much of the dark side of this city and its ways, that he wouldn’t have confided himself alone by night with two people who had an interest in getting him under the ground--my faith!", "\"내게도 한때 친구가 하나 있었소, 나의 선량한 동무 플린트윈치.\" 블랑두아가 난롯불 앞에 두 다리를 떡 벌리고 서서 말했는데, 그것이 너무도 분명히 클레넘의 물러가는 발걸음을 붙들려고 하는 말이어서, 그는 문 가까이에서 머뭇거렸다."], ["not even in a respectable house like this--unless he was bodily too strong for them.", "\"내게도 한때 친구가 하나 있었소. 그는 이 도시의 어두운 면과 그 수법들에 관해 어찌나 많이 들었던지, 자기를 땅속에 묻어 버리는 데 이해관계가 걸린 두 사람과는 밤에 단둘이 자기 몸을 맡기려 하지 않았소 — 정말이지!"], ["Bah!", "이처럼 점잖은 집에서조차 말이오 — 자기가 그들보다 몸으로 너무나 강하지 않은 한은 말이오."], ["What a poltroon, my Flintwinch!", "흥! 얼마나 겁쟁이요, 내 플린트윈치!"], ["Eh?’\n‘A cur, sir.’", "응?\""]], [["‘Agreed! A cur.", "\"개 같은 놈이지요, 선생.\""], ["But he wouldn’t have done it, my Flintwinch, unless he had known them to have the will to silence him, without the power.", "\"동감이오! 개 같은 놈."], ["He wouldn’t have drunk from a glass of water under such circumstances--not even in a respectable house like this, my Flintwinch--unless he had seen one of them drink first, and swallow too!’", "하지만 그 친구는 말이오, 내 플린트윈치, 그들이 자기를 침묵시킬 의지는 있어도 힘은 없다는 걸 알지 못하는 한, 그런 짓은 하지 않았을 거요. 그런 형편에서는 물 한 잔도 마시지 않았을 거요 — 이처럼 점잖은 집에서조차도, 내 플린트윈치 — 그들 중 하나가 먼저 마시고, 그것도 삼키는 걸 보지 않는 한은 말이오!\""]], [["Disdaining to speak, and indeed not very well able, for he was half-choking, Clennam only glanced at the visitor as he passed out.", "말을 하는 것조차 경멸스럽다는 듯, 실은 반쯤 숨이 막혀 제대로 말할 수도 없었던 클레넘은, 빠져나가면서 그저 손님을 한 번 흘끗 보았을 뿐이다."], ["The visitor saluted him with another parting snap, and his nose came down over his moustache and his moustache went up under his nose, in an ominous and ugly smile.", "손님은 작별 인사로 또 한 번 손가락을 딱 튕겨 그에게 답례했고, 그의 코는 콧수염 위로 내려오고 콧수염은 코 밑으로 올라가며, 불길하고 흉한 미소를 지었다."]], [["‘For Heaven’s sake, Affery,’ whispered Clennam, as she opened the door for him in the dark hall, and he groped his way to the sight of the night-sky, ‘what is going on here?’", "\"제발 부탁이오, 애프리.\" 그녀가 어두운 현관에서 그를 위해 문을 열어 주고 그가 더듬더듬 밤하늘이 보이는 쪽으로 길을 찾아 나가는 동안, 클레넘이 속삭였다. \"여기서 대체 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는 거요?\""]], [["Her own appearance was sufficiently ghastly, standing in the dark with her apron thrown over her head, and speaking behind it in a low, deadened voice.", "앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 채 어둠 속에 서서, 그 뒤에서 낮고 죽은 듯한 목소리로 말하는 그녀 자신의 모습도 충분히 섬뜩했다."]], [["‘Don’t ask me anything, Arthur.", "\"내게 아무것도 묻지 마세요, 아서."], ["I’ve been in a dream for ever so long.", "나는 한참 전부터 꿈속에 있었으니까요."], ["Go away!’", "어서 가세요!\""]], [["He went out, and she shut the door upon him.", "그가 나가자 그녀는 그의 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["He looked up at the windows of his mother’s room, and the dim light, deadened by the yellow blinds, seemed to say a response after Affery, and to mutter, ‘Don’t ask me anything.", "그가 어머니 방의 창문들을 올려다보니, 노란 차양에 가려 죽은 듯이 흐려진 희미한 불빛이 애프리의 말에 화답하기라도 하듯, 이렇게 중얼거리는 것 같았다. \"내게 아무것도 묻지 마세요."], ["Go away!’", "어서 가세요!\""]], [["Dear Mr Clennam,", "친애하는 클레넘 씨께,"]], [["As I said in my last that it was best for nobody to write to me, and as my sending you another little letter can therefore give you no other trouble than the trouble of reading it (perhaps you may not find leisure for even that, though I hope you will some day), I am now going to devote an hour to writing to you again.", "지난번 편지에서 아무도 제게 편지를 쓰지 않는 편이 가장 좋겠다고 말씀드렸으니, 제가 또 이렇게 짧은 편지를 보낸다 해도 읽으시는 수고(어쩌면 그럴 짬조차 내지 못하실지도 모르지만, 언젠가는 그러시리라 바랍니다) 말고는 다른 폐를 끼치지 않을 테지요. 그래서 저는 이제 한 시간을 들여 다시 한번 당신께 편지를 쓰려고 합니다."], ["This time, I write from Rome.", "이번에는 로마에서 씁니다."]], [["We left Venice before Mr and Mrs Gowan did, but they were not so long upon the road as we were, and did not travel by the same way, and so when we arrived we found them in a lodging here, in a place called the Via Gregoriana.", "우리는 가우언 부부보다 먼저 베네치아를 떠났지만, 그들은 우리만큼 길에서 오래 걸리지 않았고, 같은 길로 오지도 않았습니다. 그래서 우리가 도착했을 때 그들은 이곳, 비아 그레고리아나라는 곳의 셋집에 이미 들어 있었습니다."], ["I dare say you know it.", "아마 당신께서도 그곳을 아시리라 짐작합니다."]], [["Now I am going to tell you all I can about them, because I know that is what you most want to hear.", "이제 그들에 관해 제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 모든 것을 들려 드리려 합니다."], ["Theirs is not a very comfortable lodging, but perhaps I thought it less so when I first saw it than you would have done, because you have been in many different countries and have seen many different customs.", "당신께서 가장 듣고 싶어 하시는 것이 바로 그것임을 제가 알기 때문입니다. 그들의 셋집은 그리 편안한 곳이 못 됩니다만, 처음 보았을 때 제가 당신보다 더 못한 곳으로 여겼을지도 모르겠습니다."], ["Of course it is a far, far better place--millions of times--than any I have ever been used to until lately; and I fancy I don’t look at it with my own eyes, but with hers.", "당신께서는 여러 나라를 다니셨고 여러 풍습을 보아 오셨으니까요. 물론 그곳은, 제가 근래까지 익숙했던 그 어떤 곳보다도 — 수백만 배나 — 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나은 곳입니다."], ["For it would be easy to see that she has always been brought up in a tender and happy home, even if she had not told me so with great love for it.", "그래서 제가 그곳을 제 눈이 아니라 그분의 눈으로 보고 있는 것 같다는 생각이 듭니다. 그분이 큰 애정을 담아 제게 말씀하시지 않았더라도, 늘 다정하고 행복한 집안에서 자라났다는 것을 알아보기란 쉬웠을 테니까요."]], [["Well, it is a rather bare lodging up a rather dark common staircase, and it is nearly all a large dull room, where Mr Gowan paints.", "자, 그곳은 다소 어두컴컴한 공용 계단을 올라가 닿는 다소 휑한 셋집인데, 거의 전부가 가우언 씨가 그림을 그리는 크고 칙칙한 방 하나입니다."], ["The windows are blocked up where any one could look out, and the walls have been all drawn over with chalk and charcoal by others who have lived there before--oh,--I should think, for years!", "밖을 내다볼 만한 곳의 창문은 죄다 막혀 있고, 벽이란 벽은 앞서 그곳에 살던 이들이 분필과 목탄으로 온통 끼적여 놓았는데 — 아, 짐작건대 — 여러 해에 걸쳐 그랬을 것 같습니다!"], ["There is a curtain more dust-coloured than red, which divides it, and the part behind the curtain makes the private sitting-room.", "붉은빛이라기보다는 먼지 빛깔에 가까운 휘장이 방을 나누고 있고, 그 휘장 뒤의 부분이 사사로운 거실 노릇을 합니다."], ["When I first saw her there she was alone, and her work had fallen out of her hand, and she was looking up at the sky shining through the tops of the windows.", "제가 처음 그곳에서 그분을 보았을 때, 그분은 혼자였고, 손에서 일감이 떨어져 있었으며, 창문 윗부분으로 비쳐 드는 하늘을 올려다보고 있었습니다."], ["Pray do not be uneasy when I tell you, but it was not quite so airy, nor so bright, nor so cheerful, nor so happy and youthful altogether as I should have liked it to be.", "이런 말씀을 드려도 부디 마음 졸이지는 마시되, 그곳은 제가 바랐던 만큼 바람이 잘 통하지도, 환하지도, 명랑하지도, 또 전체적으로 행복하고 젊은 기운이 감돌지도 않았습니다."]], [["On account of Mr Gowan’s painting Papa’s picture (which I am not quite convinced I should have known from the likeness if I had not seen him doing it), I have had more opportunities of being with her since then than I might have had without this fortunate chance.", "가우언 씨가 아버지의 초상화를 그리고 계신 덕분에(그가 그리는 모습을 직접 보지 못했더라면 그 닮음새만으로 아버지인 줄 알아보았을지 제 자신도 썩 자신이 없습니다만), 그 일이 없었더라면 좀처럼 갖지 못했을 그분과 함께 있을 기회를 그 다행스러운 우연 덕에 그 뒤로 더 많이 가질 수 있었습니다."], ["She is very much alone.", "그분은 무척 외롭게 지냅니다."], ["Very much alone indeed.", "정말이지 무척이나 외롭게요."]], [["Shall I tell you about the second time I saw her?", "제가 그분을 두 번째로 만났을 때의 이야기를 들려 드릴까요?"], ["I went one day, when it happened that I could run round by myself, at four or five o’clock in the afternoon.", "어느 날 마침 혼자 잠깐 들를 수 있게 되어, 오후 네다섯 시쯤 찾아갔습니다."], ["She was then dining alone, and her solitary dinner had been brought in from somewhere, over a kind of brazier with a fire in it, and she had no company or prospect of company, that I could see, but the old man who had brought it.", "그때 그분은 혼자 식사를 하고 있었는데, 외로운 그 식사는 불을 담은 일종의 화로 위에 얹혀 어딘가에서 날라져 온 것이었고, 제가 보기에 그것을 가져온 노인 말고는 함께할 사람도, 함께할 기미도 없었습니다."], ["He was telling her a long story (of robbers outside the walls being taken up by a stone statue of a Saint), to entertain her--as he said to me when I came out, ‘because he had a daughter of his own, though she was not so pretty.’", "그 노인은 그분을 즐겁게 해 드리려고 긴 이야기를(성벽 밖의 도적들이 어느 성인의 돌 조각상에게 붙들려 갔다는 이야기를) 들려주고 있었는데 — 제가 나올 때 제게 한 말로는, \"자기에게도 딸이 하나 있는데, 비록 이 아가씨만큼 예쁘지는 않지만\" 그래서라더군요."]], [["I ought now to mention Mr Gowan, before I say what little more I have to say about her.", "그분에 관해 더 드릴 말씀이 얼마 남지 않았으니, 이제는 그 전에 가우언 씨 이야기를 해야겠습니다. 그는 분명 그분의 아름다움에 감탄할 테고, 그분을 자랑스러워할 것입니다."], ["He must admire her beauty, and he must be proud of her, for everybody praises it, and he must be fond of her, and I do not doubt that he is--but in his way.", "모두가 그 아름다움을 칭찬하니까요. 또 그분을 아낄 것이 틀림없고, 저도 그가 그러하리라 의심치 않습니다 — 다만 그 나름의 방식으로요."], ["You know his way, and if it appears as careless and discontented in your eyes as it does in mine, I am not wrong in thinking that it might be better suited to her.", "당신께서는 그 방식을 아시지요. 그것이 제 눈에 비치는 것처럼 당신의 눈에도 무심하고 불만스러워 보인다면, 그것이 그분께는 차라리 더 잘 어울렸으리라고 제가 생각하는 것도 그릇된 일은 아닐 것입니다."], ["If it does not seem so to you, I am quite sure I am wholly mistaken; for your unchanged poor child confides in your knowledge and goodness more than she could ever tell you if she was to try.", "만일 당신께는 그렇게 보이지 않는다면, 제가 아주 단단히 잘못 생각하고 있는 것이 분명합니다. 당신의 변함없는 가엾은 아이는, 애써 말로 다 표현할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더, 당신의 식견과 선량함을 믿기 때문입니다."], ["But don’t be frightened, I am not going to try.", "하지만 놀라지 마세요, 저는 그것을 말로 다 표현해 보려는 것이 아니니까요."]], [["Owing (as I think, if you think so too) to Mr Gowan’s unsettled and dissatisfied way, he applies himself to his profession very little.", "(당신께서도 그렇게 생각하신다면, 제 생각으로는) 가우언 씨의 안정되지 못하고 만족할 줄 모르는 태도 때문에, 그는 자신의 일에 거의 마음을 쏟지 않습니다."], ["He does nothing steadily or patiently; but equally takes things up and throws them down, and does them, or leaves them undone, without caring about them.", "그는 무엇이든 꾸준하거나 끈기 있게 하지 않고, 똑같이 무언가를 집어 들었다가는 내던지고, 마음에 두는 법 없이 하다가도 내버려 둡니다."], ["When I have heard him talking to Papa during the sittings for the picture, I have sat wondering whether it could be that he has no belief in anybody else, because he has no belief in himself.", "초상화를 그리는 동안 그가 아버지와 이야기하는 것을 들으며, 저는 그가 자기 자신을 믿지 못하기에 다른 누구도 믿지 못하는 것은 아닐까 하고 앉아서 생각하곤 했습니다. 정말 그런 것일까요?"], ["Is it so? I wonder what you will say when you come to this!", "당신께서 이 대목에 이르면 무어라 말씀하실지 궁금합니다!"], ["I know how you will look, and I can almost hear the voice in which you would tell me on the Iron Bridge.", "당신께서 어떤 표정을 지으실지 알 것 같고, 아이언 브리지 위에서라면 그 일을 제게 들려주실 그 목소리가 거의 들리는 듯합니다."]], [["Mr Gowan goes out a good deal among what is considered the best company here--though he does not look as if he enjoyed it or liked it when he is with it--and she sometimes accompanies him, but lately she has gone out very little.", "가우언 씨는 이곳에서 가장 좋은 사교계라 여겨지는 곳에 자주 드나듭니다 — 정작 그 안에 있을 때면 그것을 즐기거나 좋아하는 것처럼 보이지 않습니다만 — 그분도 가끔 그를 따라가지만, 요즘 들어서는 거의 나가지 않습니다."], ["I think I have noticed that they have an inconsistent way of speaking about her, as if she had made some great self-interested success in marrying Mr Gowan, though, at the same time, the very same people, would not have dreamed of taking him for themselves or their daughters.", "사람들이 그분에 관해 이야기하는 데에 앞뒤가 맞지 않는 구석이 있음을 제가 눈여겨본 것 같습니다. 마치 그분이 가우언 씨와 결혼함으로써 무슨 대단한 잇속을 챙긴 성공을 거두기라도 한 것처럼 말하면서도, 정작 바로 그 똑같은 사람들은 자기 자신이나 자기 딸이 그를 남편으로 삼는다는 것은 꿈에도 생각하지 않았을 테니까요."], ["Then he goes into the country besides, to think about making sketches; and in all places where there are visitors, he has a large acquaintance and is very well known.", "그러고는 그는 또 시골로 나가 스케치를 할 궁리를 합니다. 그리고 방문객이 있는 곳이라면 어디서든 아는 이가 많고 아주 널리 알려져 있습니다."], ["Besides all this, he has a friend who is much in his society both at home and away from home, though he treats this friend very coolly and is very uncertain in his behaviour to him.", "이 모든 것에 더해, 집에서나 밖에서나 그와 자주 어울리는 친구가 하나 있는데, 그는 이 친구를 아주 쌀쌀맞게 대하고, 그를 대하는 태도가 종잡을 수 없습니다."], ["I am quite sure (because she has told me so), that she does not like this friend.", "저는 (그분이 제게 그렇게 말씀하셨으니) 그분이 이 친구를 좋아하지 않는다는 것을 분명히 압니다."], ["He is so revolting to me, too, that his being away from here, at present, is quite a relief to my mind.", "그는 제게도 어찌나 역겨운지, 그가 지금 이곳을 떠나 있다는 것만으로도 제 마음이 한결 놓입니다."], ["How much more to hers!", "하물며 그분의 마음은 얼마나 더 그렇겠습니까!"]], [["But what I particularly want you to know, and why I have resolved to tell you so much while I am afraid it may make you a little uncomfortable without occasion, is this.", "하지만 제가 특별히 당신께 알려 드리고 싶은 것, 그리고 공연히 당신을 조금 불편하게 해 드릴까 두려우면서도 이토록 많은 것을 말씀드리기로 결심한 까닭은 이것입니다."], ["She is so true and so devoted, and knows so completely that all her love and duty are his for ever, that you may be certain she will love him, admire him, praise him, and conceal all his faults, until she dies.", "그분은 어찌나 진실하고 헌신적인지, 또 자신의 모든 사랑과 의무가 영원히 그의 것임을 어찌나 온전히 알고 계신지, 그분이 죽는 날까지 그를 사랑하고, 그에게 감탄하고, 그를 칭송하며, 그의 온갖 결점을 감싸 안으리라는 것을 당신께서는 확실히 믿으셔도 좋습니다."], ["I believe she conceals them, and always will conceal them, even from herself.", "저는 그분이 그 결점들을 감추고 있고, 앞으로도 늘 감출 것이라고 믿습니다, 심지어 자기 자신에게서까지도요."], ["She has given him a heart that can never be taken back; and however much he may try it, he will never wear out its affection.", "그분은 그에게 결코 거두어들일 수 없는 마음을 주었습니다. 그가 아무리 그것을 시험하더라도, 그 애정을 결코 닳아 없어지게 하지는 못할 것입니다."], ["You know the truth of this, as you know everything, far far better than I; but I cannot help telling you what a nature she shows, and that you can never think too well of her.", "당신께서는 모든 것을 아시듯이 이것의 참됨도 저보다 훨씬, 훨씬 더 잘 아시겠지요. 하지만 그분이 어떤 천성을 지녔는지, 또 그분을 아무리 좋게 생각하셔도 지나치지 않다는 것을, 저는 당신께 말씀드리지 않을 수가 없습니다."]], [["I have not yet called her by her name in this letter, but we are such friends now that I do so when we are quietly together, and she speaks to me by my name--I mean, not my Christian name, but the name you gave me.", "이 편지에서 저는 아직 그분의 이름을 부르지 않았습니다만, 우리는 이제 무척 가까운 사이가 되어, 둘이 조용히 함께 있을 때면 저도 이름을 부르고, 그분도 제 이름으로 저를 부릅니다 — 제 세례명이 아니라, 당신께서 제게 지어 주신 이름으로요."], ["When she began to call me Amy, I told her my short story, and that you had always called me Little Dorrit.", "그분이 저를 에이미라고 부르기 시작했을 때, 저는 제 짧은 사연을 들려 드리며 당신께서 늘 저를 리틀 도릿이라 부르셨다고 말씀드렸습니다."], ["I told her that the name was much dearer to me than any other, and so she calls me Little Dorrit too.", "그 이름이 다른 어떤 이름보다도 제게 훨씬 더 소중하다고 했더니, 그분도 저를 리틀 도릿이라 부르신답니다."]], [["Perhaps you have not heard from her father or mother yet, and may not know that she has a baby son.", "아마 당신께서는 아직 그분의 아버지나 어머니로부터 소식을 듣지 못하셨을 테고, 그래서 그분에게 사내아이가 태어났다는 것을 모르실 듯합니다."], ["He was born only two days ago, and just a week after they came.", "아이는 불과 이틀 전에, 그들이 이곳에 온 지 꼭 일주일 만에 태어났습니다."], ["It has made them very happy.", "그 일로 그들은 무척 행복해합니다."], ["However, I must tell you, as I am to tell you all, that I fancy they are under a constraint with Mr Gowan, and that they feel as if his mocking way with them was sometimes a slight given to their love for her.", "그렇지만 모든 것을 말씀드리기로 했으니 이 말씀도 드려야겠습니다. 제 생각에 그들은 가우언 씨 앞에서 어떤 거북함을 느끼는 것 같고, 그가 그들을 대하며 비웃는 듯한 태도를 보일 때면 그것이 그분을 향한 그들의 사랑에 대한 모욕처럼 느껴지는 모양입니다."], ["It was but yesterday, when I was there, that I saw Mr Meagles change colour, and get up and go out, as if he was afraid that he might say so, unless he prevented himself by that means.", "바로 어제, 제가 그곳에 있을 때에도, 미글스 씨가 낯빛이 변하더니 자리에서 일어나 밖으로 나가는 것을 보았는데, 마치 그렇게라도 자신을 다잡지 않으면 그 말을 입 밖에 낼까 봐 두려워하는 것 같았습니다."], ["Yet I am sure they are both so considerate, good-humoured, and reasonable, that he might spare them.", "그렇지만 저는 그 두 분이 어찌나 사려 깊고 너그럽고 분별 있는지를 잘 알기에, 그가 그들을 좀 너그러이 대해도 좋으련만 싶습니다."], ["It is hard in him not to think of them a little more.", "그가 그들을 조금이라도 더 헤아려 주지 않는 것은 모진 일입니다."]], [["I stopped at the last full stop to read all this over.", "저는 마지막 마침표에서 멈추고 이 모든 것을 다시 읽어 보았습니다."], ["It looked at first as if I was taking on myself to understand and explain so much, that I was half inclined not to send it.", "처음에는 제가 이토록 많은 것을 제멋대로 이해하고 설명하려 든 것처럼 보여, 이 편지를 부치지 말까 하는 마음이 반쯤 들었습니다."], ["But when I thought it over a little, I felt more hopeful for your knowing at once that I had only been watchful for you, and had only noticed what I think I have noticed, because I was quickened by your interest in it.", "그러나 조금 곰곰이 생각해 보니, 제가 그저 당신을 위해 주의 깊게 살폈을 뿐이며, 제가 눈여겨보았다고 여기는 것들을 눈여겨본 까닭도 오로지 당신께서 그 일에 관심을 두신 덕에 제 마음이 움직였기 때문임을, 당신께서 단번에 알아주시리라는 희망이 더 솟아났습니다."], ["Indeed, you may be sure that is the truth.", "정말이지, 그것이 진실임을 믿으셔도 좋습니다."]], [["And now I have done with the subject in the present letter, and have little left to say.", "자, 이제 이번 편지에서는 그 이야기를 마치겠습니다. 남은 이야기는 별로 없습니다."]], [["We are all quite well, and Fanny improves every day.", "우리는 모두 아주 잘 지내고 있고, 패니는 날마다 나아지고 있습니다."], ["You can hardly think how kind she is to me, and what pains she takes with me.", "그 언니가 제게 얼마나 다정한지, 또 저를 위해 얼마나 애를 쓰는지 당신께서는 좀처럼 짐작하지 못하실 것입니다."], ["She has a lover, who has followed her, first all the way from Switzerland, and then all the way from Venice, and who has just confided to me that he means to follow her everywhere.", "언니에게는 구애하는 이가 하나 있는데, 처음에는 스위스에서부터 줄곧 따라왔고, 그다음에는 베네치아에서부터 줄곧 따라왔습니다. 그리고 방금 제게 어디든 언니를 따라다닐 작정이라고 털어놓았습니다."], ["I was much confused by his speaking to me about it, but he would.", "그가 그 일을 제게 이야기하는 바람에 저는 무척 당황했지만, 그는 굳이 그러더군요."], ["I did not know what to say, but at last I told him that I thought he had better not.", "저는 무어라 해야 할지 몰랐다가, 마침내 그러지 않는 편이 낫겠다고 생각한다고 말했습니다."], ["For Fanny (but I did not tell him this) is much too spirited and clever to suit him.", "패니는(이 말은 그에게 하지 않았습니다만) 그에게 어울리기에는 너무나 기개가 넘치고 영리하기 때문입니다."], ["Still, he said he would, all the same.", "그래도 그는 그래도 따라다닐 것이라고 했습니다."], ["I have no lover, of course.", "저는 물론 구애하는 이가 없습니다."]], [["If you should ever get so far as this in this long letter, you will perhaps say, Surely Little Dorrit will not leave off without telling me something about her travels, and surely it is time she did.", "이 긴 편지에서 여기까지 읽으셨다면, 어쩌면 이렇게 말씀하실지도 모르겠습니다. \"리틀 도릿이 자기 여행 이야기를 조금도 들려주지 않고 끝맺지는 않겠지, 이제 들려줄 때도 되었고.\""], ["I think it is indeed, but I don’t know what to tell you.", "정말 그럴 때가 되었다고 저도 생각합니다만, 무엇을 말씀드려야 할지 모르겠습니다."], ["Since we left Venice we have been in a great many wonderful places, Genoa and Florence among them, and have seen so many wonderful sights, that I am almost giddy when I think what a crowd they make.", "베네치아를 떠난 뒤로 우리는 제노바와 피렌체를 비롯해 무척 경이로운 곳을 숱하게 거쳤고, 어찌나 경이로운 광경을 많이 보았는지, 그것들이 한데 모여 이루는 무리를 떠올리면 거의 어지러울 지경입니다."], ["But you can tell me so much more about them than I can tell you, that why should I tire you with my accounts and descriptions?", "하지만 그것들에 관해서라면 제가 당신께 들려 드릴 수 있는 것보다 당신께서 제게 들려주실 수 있는 것이 훨씬 더 많으실 텐데, 어찌하여 제 이야기며 묘사로 당신을 지치게 해 드리겠습니까?"]], [["Dear Mr Clennam, as I had the courage to tell you what the familiar difficulties in my travelling mind were before, I will not be a coward now.", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, 예전에 여행하는 제 마음속에 늘 자리하던 어려움이 무엇인지 용기 내어 말씀드렸으니, 이제 와서 비겁해지지는 않겠습니다."], ["One of my frequent thoughts is this:--Old as these cities are, their age itself is hardly so curious, to my reflections, as that they should have been in their places all through those days when I did not even know of the existence of more than two or three of them, and when I scarcely knew of anything outside our old walls.", "제가 자주 하는 생각 가운데 하나는 이것입니다. — 이 도시들이 아무리 오래되었다 한들, 제 사색에는 그 나이 자체보다도, 제가 그중 두셋의 존재조차 알지 못하던 그 시절, 우리의 그 낡은 담장 바깥의 무엇도 거의 알지 못하던 그 시절 내내, 이 도시들이 제자리에 있었다는 사실이 더 신기하게 다가옵니다."], ["There is something melancholy in it, and I don’t know why.", "그 안에는 어딘가 서글픈 무언가가 있는데, 그 까닭은 저도 모르겠습니다."], ["When we went to see the famous leaning tower at Pisa, it was a bright sunny day, and it and the buildings near it looked so old, and the earth and the sky looked so young, and its shadow on the ground was so soft and retired!", "피사의 그 유명한 기울어진 탑을 보러 갔을 때, 그날은 환하고 볕 좋은 날이었고, 그 탑과 가까이 있는 건물들은 무척 오래되어 보였으며, 땅과 하늘은 무척 젊어 보였고, 땅 위에 드리운 그 그림자는 어찌나 부드럽고 조용했는지요!"], ["I could not at first think how beautiful it was, or how curious, but I thought, ‘O how many times when the shadow of the wall was falling on our room, and when that weary tread of feet was going up and down the yard--O how many times this place was just as quiet and lovely as it is to-day!’ It quite overpowered me.", "처음에는 그것이 얼마나 아름다운지, 얼마나 신기한지 미처 헤아리지 못했지만, 이런 생각이 들었습니다. \"아, 담장의 그림자가 우리 방에 드리우고, 그 지친 발걸음 소리가 마당을 오르내리던 그 시절에도 — 아, 얼마나 여러 번 이곳이 오늘처럼 이토록 고요하고 사랑스러웠을까!\""], ["My heart was so full that tears burst out of my eyes, though I did what I could to restrain them.", "그 생각이 저를 온통 사로잡았습니다. 가슴이 어찌나 벅차오르던지, 애써 참아 보려 했지만 눈에서 눈물이 터져 나왔습니다."], ["And I have the same feeling often--often.", "그리고 저는 종종 — 자주 — 같은 감정을 느낍니다."]], [["Do you know that since the change in our fortunes, though I appear to myself to have dreamed more than before, I have always dreamed of myself as very young indeed!", "아세요? 우리 형편이 달라진 뒤로, 제가 보기에는 전보다 꿈을 더 많이 꾸게 된 것 같은데, 꿈속의 저는 늘 정말이지 무척 어린 모습이랍니다!"], ["I am not very old, you may say. No, but that is not what I mean.", "제가 그리 나이를 먹지 않았다고 말씀하실지도 모르겠습니다."], ["I have always dreamed of myself as a child learning to do needlework.", "그래요, 하지만 제가 말씀드리려는 것은 그게 아닙니다. 꿈속에서 저는 늘 바느질을 배우는 어린아이입니다."], ["I have often dreamed of myself as back there, seeing faces in the yard little known, and which I should have thought I had quite forgotten; but, as often as not, I have been abroad here--in Switzerland, or France, or Italy--somewhere where we have been--yet always as that little child.", "거의 잊은 줄로만 알았던, 마당에서 보던 그리 낯익지 않은 얼굴들을 다시 보며, 그 옛 자리로 돌아가 있는 꿈을 자주 꾸었습니다. 하지만 더러는, 이곳 이국땅에 — 스위스든 프랑스든 이탈리아든, 우리가 다녀온 어딘가에 — 와 있는 꿈도 꾸는데, 그래도 늘 그 어린아이의 모습으로요."], ["I have dreamed of going down to Mrs General, with the patches on my clothes in which I can first remember myself.", "제가 맨 처음 기억하는 제 모습 그대로, 옷에 헝겊을 덧댄 채로 제너럴 부인께 내려가는 꿈을 꾸기도 했습니다."], ["I have over and over again dreamed of taking my place at dinner at Venice when we have had a large company, in the mourning for my poor mother which I wore when I was eight years old, and wore long after it was threadbare and would mend no more.", "또 베네치아에서 손님이 많이 모인 자리에 앉아 식사를 하는 꿈을 거듭거듭 꾸었는데, 그때 저는 여덟 살 적에 입던, 가엾은 어머니를 여의고 입던 상복 차림이었습니다. 그 상복은 올이 다 닳고 더는 기울 수도 없게 된 뒤까지도 오래도록 입던 것이었지요."], ["It has been a great distress to me to think how irreconcilable the company would consider it with my father’s wealth, and how I should displease and disgrace him and Fanny and Edward by so plainly disclosing what they wished to keep secret.", "그 손님들이 그 차림새를 아버지의 재산과는 도무지 어울리지 않는 것으로 여길 테고, 또 식구들이 비밀로 감추고 싶어 하는 것을 제가 이토록 노골적으로 드러냄으로써 아버지와 패니와 에드워드를 얼마나 언짢게 하고 욕보일까 하는 생각에 저는 무척 괴로웠습니다."], ["But I have not grown out of the little child in thinking of it; and at the self-same moment I have dreamed that I have sat with the heart-ache at table, calculating the expenses of the dinner, and quite distracting myself with thinking how they were ever to be made good.", "하지만 그것을 생각하는 동안에도 저는 그 어린아이의 모습에서 벗어나지 못했고, 바로 그 같은 순간에, 식탁에 앉아 가슴 아파하며 그 식사 비용을 셈해 보고, 그 비용을 대체 어떻게 메워야 할지 골몰하느라 정신이 다 산란해지는 꿈을 꾸었습니다."], ["I have never dreamed of the change in our fortunes itself; I have never dreamed of your coming back with me that memorable morning to break it; I have never even dreamed of you.", "정작 우리 형편이 달라진 그 일 자체는 한 번도 꿈에 본 적이 없고, 그 잊지 못할 아침에 당신께서 저와 함께 돌아와 그 소식을 전해 주시던 일도 꿈에 본 적이 없으며, 심지어 당신을 꿈에 본 적조차 없습니다."]], [["Dear Mr Clennam, it is possible that I have thought of you--and others--so much by day, that I have no thoughts left to wander round you by night.", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, 어쩌면 제가 낮 동안 당신을 — 그리고 다른 이들을 — 너무도 많이 생각한 나머지, 밤에 당신 곁을 맴돌 생각이 더는 남아 있지 않은 것인지도 모르겠습니다."], ["For I must now confess to you that I suffer from home-sickness--that I long so ardently and earnestly for home, as sometimes, when no one sees me, to pine for it.", "이제 당신께 고백해야겠는데, 저는 향수병을 앓고 있습니다 — 집을 어찌나 간절하고 애틋하게 그리워하는지, 때로는 아무도 보지 않을 때면 그리움에 야위어 갈 지경입니다."], ["I cannot bear to turn my face further away from it.", "집에서 더 멀리 얼굴을 돌리는 것을 저는 견딜 수가 없습니다."], ["My heart is a little lightened when we turn towards it, even for a few miles, and with the knowledge that we are soon to turn away again.", "비록 몇 마일에 지나지 않더라도, 또 머잖아 다시 등을 돌리게 되리라는 것을 알면서도, 집 쪽으로 향할 때면 제 마음은 조금 가벼워집니다."], ["So dearly do I love the scene of my poverty and your kindness.", "저는 제 가난과 당신의 친절이 깃든 그 풍경을 그토록 애틋이 사랑합니다."], ["O so dearly, O so dearly!", "아, 그토록 애틋이, 아, 그토록 애틋이요!"]], [["Heaven knows when your poor child will see England again.", "당신의 가엾은 아이가 언제 다시 영국을 보게 될지는 하늘만이 아십니다."], ["We are all fond of the life here (except me), and there are no plans for our return.", "우리는 모두 이곳의 생활을 좋아하고(저만 빼고요), 돌아갈 계획은 전혀 없습니다."], ["My dear father talks of a visit to London late in this next spring, on some affairs connected with the property, but I have no hope that he will bring me with him.", "사랑하는 아버지께서는 재산과 관련된 무슨 일로 내년 늦봄에 런던을 방문할 이야기를 하시지만, 저를 함께 데려가시리라는 희망은 없습니다."]], [["I have tried to get on a little better under Mrs General’s instruction, and I hope I am not quite so dull as I used to be.", "저는 제너럴 부인의 가르침 아래 조금이라도 더 나아지려 애써 왔고, 예전만큼 그렇게 둔하지는 않게 된 것 같기를 바랍니다."], ["I have begun to speak and understand, almost easily, the hard languages I told you about.", "전에 말씀드렸던 그 어려운 언어들을 이제 거의 어려움 없이 말하고 알아듣기 시작했습니다."], ["I did not remember, at the moment when I wrote last, that you knew them both; but I remembered it afterwards, and it helped me on.", "지난번 편지를 쓸 때에는 당신께서 그 두 언어를 다 아신다는 것을 미처 떠올리지 못했지만, 나중에 그 생각이 나서 큰 도움이 되었습니다."], ["God bless you, dear Mr Clennam.", "하느님의 가호가 있으시기를, 친애하는 클레넘 씨."], ["Do not forget", "부디 잊지 마세요,"]], [["Your ever grateful and affectionate", "언제까지나 감사하고 정겨운 마음을 담아"]], [["# LITTLE DORRIT.", "# LITTLE DORRIT."]], [["P.S.--Particularly remember that Minnie Gowan deserves the best remembrance in which you can hold her.", "리틀 도릿 올림. 추신 — 미니 가우언이 당신께서 품으실 수 있는 가장 좋은 기억을 받을 자격이 있는 사람임을 특히 기억해 주세요."], ["You cannot think too generously or too highly of her.", "그분을 아무리 너그럽게, 아무리 높이 생각하셔도 지나치지 않습니다."], ["I forgot Mr Pancks last time.", "지난번에는 팽크스 씨를 깜박했습니다."], ["Please, if you should see him, give him your Little Dorrit’s kind regard.", "부디 그분을 만나시거든 당신의 리틀 도릿이 안부를 전하더라고 일러 주세요."], ["He was very good to Little D.", "그분은 리틀 D에게 무척 잘 대해 주셨답니다."]], [["# CHAPTER 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden", "# CHAPTER 12. In which a Great Patriotic Conference is holden"]], [["The famous name of Merdle became, every day, more famous in the land.", "머들이라는 그 이름난 이름은 날이 갈수록 이 나라에서 더욱 이름을 떨쳤다."], ["Nobody knew that the Merdle of such high renown had ever done any good to any one, alive or dead, or to any earthly thing; nobody knew that he had any capacity or utterance of any sort in him, which had ever thrown, for any creature, the feeblest farthing-candle ray of light on any path of duty or diversion, pain or pleasure, toil or rest, fact or fancy, among the multiplicity of paths in the labyrinth trodden by the sons of Adam; nobody had the smallest reason for supposing the clay of which this object of worship was made, to be other than the commonest clay, with as clogged a wick smouldering inside of it as ever kept an image of humanity from tumbling to pieces.", "그토록 드높은 명성을 누리는 머들이 살아 있는 사람에게든 죽은 사람에게든, 아니 이 세상 그 어떤 것에든 무슨 좋은 일을 한 적이 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다. 그가 무슨 능력이나 어떤 견식을 지니고 있어, 아담의 아들들이 밟아 온 미궁 같은 그 수많은 길 가운데, 의무든 오락이든, 고통이든 기쁨이든, 고역이든 휴식이든, 사실이든 공상이든, 그 어떤 길 위에 누구를 위해서든 가장 희미한 동전 한 닢짜리 양초 불빛만큼이라도 빛을 비춰 준 적이 있는지 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["All people knew (or thought they knew) that he had made himself immensely rich; and, for that reason alone, prostrated themselves before him, more degradedly and less excusably than the darkest savage creeps out of his hole in the ground to propitiate, in some log or reptile, the Deity of his benighted soul.", "이 숭배의 대상이 빚어진 진흙이, 인간의 형상이 산산이 부서지지 않도록 붙들어 두는, 속에서 그을음만 피우는 막힌 심지를 품은 그 가장 흔해 빠진 진흙과 다를 것이라고 짐작할 만한 근거를 가진 자는 아무도 없었다. 모든 사람이 그가 스스로 어마어마한 부자가 되었다는 것을 알았고(혹은 안다고 여겼고), 오직 그 이유 하나만으로 그 앞에 엎드렸으니, 가장 미개한 야만인이 제 무지몽매한 영혼의 신을 통나무나 파충류 속에서 달래려 땅속 굴에서 기어 나오는 것보다 더 비굴하고 덜 변명할 만한 방식이었다."]], [["Nay, the high priests of this worship had the man before them as a protest against their meanness.", "아니, 이 숭배의 대제사장들은 자기네의 비열함에 대한 항변으로서 그 사람을 바로 눈앞에 두고 있었다."], ["The multitude worshipped on trust--though always distinctly knowing why--but the officiators at the altar had the man habitually in their view.", "군중은 — 비록 언제나 그 까닭을 똑똑히 알고 있긴 했으나 — 믿음만으로 숭배했지만, 제단에서 의식을 집전하는 자들은 그 사람을 늘 눈앞에 두고 있었다."], ["They sat at his feasts, and he sat at theirs.", "그들은 그의 연회에 앉았고, 그도 그들의 연회에 앉았다."], ["There was a spectre always attendant on him, saying to these high priests, ‘Are such the signs you trust, and love to honour; this head, these eyes, this mode of speech, the tone and manner of this man?", "늘 그를 따라다니는 유령이 하나 있어, 이 대제사장들에게 이렇게 말했다. \"그대들이 신뢰하고 기꺼이 떠받드는 징표가 이런 것들이오?"], ["You are the levers of the Circumlocution Office, and the rulers of men.", "이 머리, 이 두 눈, 이 말투, 이 사람의 어조와 태도가? 그대들은 에두름국의 지렛대이며 인간들의 통치자요."], ["When half-a-dozen of you fall out by the ears, it seems that mother earth can give birth to no other rulers.", "그대들 가운데 대여섯이 서로 멱살을 잡고 싸우면, 어머니 대지는 다른 통치자를 도무지 낳지 못할 것만 같소."], ["Does your qualification lie in the superior knowledge of men which accepts, courts, and puffs this man?", "이 사람을 받아들이고, 떠받들고, 부풀려 주는 그 빼어난 사람 보는 안목에 그대들의 자격이 있는 것이오?"], ["Or, if you are competent to judge aright the signs I never fail to show you when he appears among you, is your superior honesty your qualification?’ Two rather ugly questions these, always going about town with Mr Merdle; and there was a tacit agreement that they must be stifled.", "아니면, 그가 그대들 사이에 나타날 때마다 내가 어김없이 보여 주는 징표들을 옳게 판단할 능력이 그대들에게 있다면, 그대들의 빼어난 정직함이 곧 그대들의 자격인 것이오?\" 머들 씨를 따라 늘 시내를 돌아다니던 자못 추한 두 가지 질문이었으니, 그것들은 반드시 입막음되어야 한다는 암묵의 합의가 있었다."]], [["In Mrs Merdle’s absence abroad, Mr Merdle still kept the great house open for the passage through it of a stream Of visitors.", "머들 부인이 외국으로 떠나 자리를 비운 동안에도, 머들 씨는 방문객들의 물결이 지나다닐 수 있도록 그 큰 저택을 여전히 열어 두었다."], ["A few of these took affable possession of the establishment.", "그들 가운데 몇몇은 그 집을 제집처럼 스스럼없이 차지했다."], ["Three or four ladies of distinction and liveliness used to say to one another, ‘Let us dine at our dear Merdle’s next Thursday.", "지체 높고 활달한 부인 서넛이 서로 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"다음 목요일에는 우리 사랑하는 머들 댁에서 식사합시다."], ["Whom shall we have?’ Our dear Merdle would then receive his instructions; and would sit heavily among the company at table and wander lumpishly about his drawing-rooms afterwards, only remarkable for appearing to have nothing to do with the entertainment beyond being in its way.", "누구를 부를까요?\" 그러면 우리 사랑하는 머들은 지시를 받았고, 식탁의 손님들 사이에 묵직하게 앉아 있다가, 그 후에는 응접실들을 굼뜨게 어슬렁거리며 돌아다닐 뿐이었으니, 그 연회와는 거리에 거치적거린다는 것 말고는 아무 상관도 없어 보이는 점에서만 눈에 띄었다."]], [["The Chief Butler, the Avenging Spirit of this great man’s life, relaxed nothing of his severity.", "이 대단한 사람의 인생을 따라다니는 복수의 정령인 집사장은 자기 엄격함을 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않았다."], ["He looked on at these dinners when the bosom was not there, as he looked on at other dinners when the bosom was there; and his eye was a basilisk to Mr Merdle.", "그는 그 가슴이 있을 때 다른 만찬들을 지켜보던 것과 똑같이, 그 가슴이 없는 이런 만찬들도 지켜보았으며, 그의 눈길은 머들 씨에게 바실리스크와도 같았다."], ["He was a hard man, and would never bate an ounce of plate or a bottle of wine.", "그는 깐깐한 사람이어서 은식기 한 온스도, 포도주 한 병도 결코 깎으려 하지 않았다."], ["He would not allow a dinner to be given, unless it was up to his mark.", "자기 기준에 미치지 못하면 만찬을 베푸는 것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["He set forth the table for his own dignity.", "그는 자기 자신의 위엄을 위하여 식탁을 차렸다."], ["If the guests chose to partake of what was served, he saw no objection; but it was served for the maintenance of his rank.", "손님들이 차려진 음식을 들고 싶어 한다면 그로서는 반대할 까닭이 없었으나, 그것은 자기 지위를 유지하기 위하여 차려진 것이었다. 그가 찬장 옆에 서 있을 때면 이렇게 선언하는 듯했다."], ["As he stood by the sideboard he seemed to announce, ‘I have accepted office to look at this which is now before me, and to look at nothing less than this.’ If he missed the presiding bosom, it was as a part of his own state of which he was, from unavoidable circumstances, temporarily deprived, just as he might have missed a centre-piece, or a choice wine-cooler, which had been sent to the Banker’s.", "\"나는 지금 내 앞에 놓인 이것을 보기 위하여, 그리고 결코 이만 못한 것은 보지 않기 위하여 이 직책을 맡았노라.\" 좌중을 주재하는 그 가슴이 없으면, 그는 그것을 마치 자기 위엄의 한 부분으로 여겨, 불가피한 사정으로 잠시 빼앗긴 것처럼 아쉬워했으니, 마치 식탁 중앙 장식이나 골라 둔 포도주 냉각기가 은행가에게 보내져 자리를 비운 것을 아쉬워하듯 했다."]], [["Mr Merdle issued invitations for a Barnacle dinner.", "머들 씨는 배너클 일가를 위한 만찬에 초대장을 보냈다."], ["Lord Decimus was to be there, Mr Tite Barnacle was to be there, the pleasant young Barnacle was to be there; and the Chorus of Parliamentary Barnacles who went about the provinces when the House was up, warbling the praises of their Chief, were to be represented there.", "데시머스 경이 올 예정이었고, 타이트 배너클 씨가 올 예정이었으며, 그 호감 가는 젊은 배너클이 올 예정이었다. 그리고 의회가 휴회하면 지방을 돌아다니며 그 우두머리의 칭송을 지저귀던 의회 배너클 합창단도 그 자리에 대표를 보낼 예정이었다."], ["It was understood to be a great occasion.", "그것은 대단한 행사로 여겨졌다."], ["Mr Merdle was going to take up the Barnacles.", "머들 씨가 배너클 일가를 후원하려는 참이었다."], ["Some delicate little negotiations had occurred between him and the noble Decimus--the young Barnacle of engaging manners acting as negotiator--and Mr Merdle had decided to cast the weight of his great probity and great riches into the Barnacle scale.", "그와 고귀한 데시머스 사이에 — 그 매력적인 태도를 지닌 젊은 배너클이 교섭자 노릇을 하여 — 미묘한 작은 협상들이 오갔고, 머들 씨는 자신의 위대한 청렴과 위대한 재력의 무게를 배너클 쪽 저울에 실어 주기로 마음먹었다."], ["Jobbery was suspected by the malicious; perhaps because it was indisputable that if the adherence of the immortal Enemy of Mankind could have been secured by a job, the Barnacles would have jobbed him--for the good of the country, for the good of the country.", "악의 있는 자들은 부정한 거래를 의심했으니, 어쩌면 그것은, 인류의 불멸의 원수*의 협력을 부정한 거래로 확보할 수 있었더라면 배너클 일가가 그자에게도 부정한 거래를 했으리라는 — 나라를 위하여, 나라를 위하여 — 것이 부인할 수 없는 사실이었기 때문이리라."]], [["Mrs Merdle had written to this magnificent spouse of hers, whom it was heresy to regard as anything less than all the British Merchants since the days of Whittington rolled into one, and gilded three feet deep all over--had written to this spouse of hers, several letters from Rome, in quick succession, urging upon him with importunity that now or never was the time to provide for Edmund Sparkler.", "* 인류의 불멸의 원수: 악마를 가리킴. 머들 부인은 — 그를 휘팅턴 시절 이래의 모든 영국 상인을 하나로 뭉뚱그려 온통 세 자 두께로 도금한 존재보다 못한 무엇으로 여기는 것은 이단이었거니와 — 이 으리으리한 남편에게 로마에서 잇따라 여러 통의 편지를 보내, 에드먼드 스파클러의 앞날을 마련해 줄 때는 바로 지금이며 지금이 아니면 영영 안 된다고 줄기차게 다그쳤다."], ["Mrs Merdle had shown him that the case of Edmund was urgent, and that infinite advantages might result from his having some good thing directly.", "머들 부인은 에드먼드의 처지가 급하다는 것을, 그리고 그에게 당장 무슨 좋은 자리가 생기면 무한한 이득이 따를 수 있다는 것을 그에게 일러 주었다."], ["In the grammar of Mrs Merdle’s verbs on this momentous subject, there was only one mood, the Imperative; and that Mood had only one Tense, the Present.", "이 막중한 주제에 관한 머들 부인의 동사 문법에는 오직 하나의 법(法), 곧 명령법만이 있었고, 그 법에는 오직 하나의 시제, 곧 현재시제만이 있었다."], ["Mrs Merdle’s verbs were so pressingly presented to Mr Merdle to conjugate, that his sluggish blood and his long coat-cuffs became quite agitated.", "머들 부인의 동사들은 어찌나 다급하게 머들 씨에게 들이밀어져 활용하라고 강요되었던지, 그의 굼뜬 피와 그의 긴 소맷부리까지도 자못 동요했다."]], [["In which state of agitation, Mr Merdle, evasively rolling his eyes round the Chief Butler’s shoes without raising them to the index of that stupendous creature’s thoughts, had signified to him his intention of giving a special dinner: not a very large dinner, but a very special dinner.", "그렇게 동요한 상태에서 머들 씨는, 그 어마어마한 인물의 생각이 가리키는 표지까지 눈을 들어 올리지는 못한 채 집사장의 구두 둘레로 슬그머니 눈알을 굴리면서, 특별 만찬을 베풀 작정임을 그에게 알렸다. 아주 큰 만찬은 아니고, 아주 특별한 만찬이라고."], ["The Chief Butler had signified, in return, that he had no objection to look on at the most expensive thing in that way that could be done; and the day of the dinner was now come.", "집사장은 그에 답하여, 그런 식으로 할 수 있는 가장 값비싼 것을 지켜보는 데는 자기로서 반대가 없다고 알려 주었다. 그리하여 이제 만찬의 날이 다가왔다."]], [["Mr Merdle stood in one of his drawing-rooms, with his back to the fire, waiting for the arrival of his important guests.", "머들 씨는 응접실 하나에 서서 난로를 등진 채, 중요한 손님들이 도착하기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He seldom or never took the liberty of standing with his back to the fire unless he was quite alone.", "그는 완전히 혼자 있지 않은 한 난로를 등지고 서는 무례를 좀처럼, 아니 결코 부리지 않았다."], ["In the presence of the Chief Butler, he could not have done such a deed.", "집사장이 있는 자리에서라면 그런 짓은 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["He would have clasped himself by the wrists in that constabulary manner of his, and have paced up and down the hearthrug, or gone creeping about among the rich objects of furniture, if his oppressive retainer had appeared in the room at that very moment.", "만약 그 위압적인 하인이 바로 그 순간에 방에 나타났더라면, 그는 그 순경 같은 태도로 두 손목을 마주 잡고 난롯가 깔개 위를 왔다 갔다 하거나, 호화로운 가구들 사이를 슬금슬금 기어다녔을 것이다."], ["The sly shadows which seemed to dart out of hiding when the fire rose, and to dart back into it when the fire fell, were sufficient witnesses of his making himself so easy.", "불길이 솟아오를 때면 숨어 있다가 튀어나오고 불길이 사그라들 때면 다시 숨어드는 듯한 그 교활한 그림자들이, 그가 그렇게 편안히 구는 것을 지켜보는 충분한 증인이었다."], ["They were even more than sufficient, if his uncomfortable glances at them might be taken to mean anything.", "그가 그 그림자들을 향해 던지는 불편한 눈길에 무슨 뜻이 담겨 있다고 본다면, 그 증인들은 충분하고도 남았다."]], [["Mr Merdle’s right hand was filled with the evening paper, and the evening paper was full of Mr Merdle.", "머들 씨의 오른손에는 석간신문이 가득 쥐여 있었고, 석간신문에는 머들 씨가 가득했다."], ["His wonderful enterprise, his wonderful wealth, his wonderful Bank, were the fattening food of the evening paper that night.", "그의 놀라운 사업, 그의 놀라운 재산, 그의 놀라운 은행이 그날 밤 석간신문을 살찌우는 양식이었다."], ["The wonderful Bank, of which he was the chief projector, establisher, and manager, was the latest of the many Merdle wonders.", "그가 주된 발기인이자 설립자이자 경영자인 그 놀라운 은행은 수많은 머들의 경이 가운데 가장 최근의 것이었다."], ["So modest was Mr Merdle withal, in the midst of these splendid achievements, that he looked far more like a man in possession of his house under a distraint, than a commercial Colossus bestriding his own hearthrug, while the little ships were sailing into dinner.", "그토록 찬란한 업적들 한가운데서도 머들 씨가 어찌나 겸손해 보였던지, 작은 배들이 만찬을 향해 들어오는 동안 자기 난롯가 깔개 위에 떡 버티고 선 상업의 거인이라기보다는, 압류당한 채 자기 집에 들어앉아 있는 사람처럼 훨씬 더 보였다."]], [["Behold the vessels coming into port!", "보라, 배들이 항구로 들어온다!"], ["The engaging young Barnacle was the first arrival; but Bar overtook him on the staircase.", "매력적인 젊은 배너클이 가장 먼저 도착했으나, 바가 계단에서 그를 앞질렀다. 여느 때처럼 이중 안경과 그 잔뜩 처진 배심원용 표정으로 무장한 바는 매력적인 젊은 배너클을 보고 무척이나 반가워했다."], ["Bar, strengthened as usual with his double eye-glass and his little jury droop, was overjoyed to see the engaging young Barnacle; and opined that we were going to sit in Banco, as we lawyers called it, to take a special argument?", "그러고는 우리가, 우리 법조인들이 말하는 식으로, 특별 변론을 듣기 위해 전원합의부*로 앉을 모양이냐고 물었다. * 전원합의부(in Banco): 법관 전원이 함께 앉아 심리하는 법정을 가리키는 라틴어 법률 용어."]], [["‘Indeed,’ said the sprightly young Barnacle, whose name was Ferdinand; ‘how so?’", "\"그래요,\" 페르디난드라는 이름의 그 발랄한 젊은 배너클이 말했다. \"어째서 그렇소?\""]], [["‘Nay,’ smiled Bar.", "\"아니,\" 바가 미소 지었다."], ["‘If you don’t know, how can I know?", "\"그대가 모른다면 내가 어찌 알겠소?"], ["You are in the innermost sanctuary of the temple; I am one of the admiring concourse on the plain without.’", "그대는 신전의 가장 깊은 지성소 안에 있고, 나는 바깥 들판에서 우러러보는 군중 가운데 하나일 뿐이오.\""]], [["Bar could be light in hand, or heavy in hand, according to the customer he had to deal with.", "바는 상대해야 할 손님에 따라 가볍게 다루기도, 무겁게 다루기도 했다."], ["With Ferdinand Barnacle he was gossamer.", "페르디난드 배너클에게는 거미줄처럼 가뿐했다."], ["Bar was likewise always modest and self-depreciatory--in his way.", "또한 바는 늘 — 제 나름의 방식으로 — 겸손하고 자기를 낮추었다."], ["Bar was a man of great variety; but one leading thread ran through the woof of all his patterns.", "바는 변화무쌍한 사람이었으나, 그 모든 무늬의 씨실을 관통하는 하나의 주된 실이 있었다."], ["Every man with whom he had to do was in his eyes a jury-man; and he must get that jury-man over, if he could.", "그가 상대해야 하는 모든 사람은 그의 눈에 배심원이었고, 그는 할 수 있는 한 그 배심원을 자기편으로 넘어오게 만들어야 했다."]], [["‘Our illustrious host and friend,’ said Bar; ‘our shining mercantile star;--going into politics?’", "\"우리의 저 빛나는 주인이자 벗,\" 바가 말했다. \"우리의 저 찬란한 상업의 별이 — 정치에 발을 들이려는 거요?\""]], [["‘Going?", "\"들이다니요?"], ["He has been in Parliament some time, you know,’ returned the engaging young Barnacle.", "알다시피 그분은 이미 한동안 의회에 계셨는걸요,\" 매력적인 젊은 배너클이 대꾸했다."]], [["‘True,’ said Bar, with his light-comedy laugh for special jury-men, which was a very different thing from his low-comedy laugh for comic tradesmen on common juries: ‘he has been in Parliament for some time.", "\"맞소,\" 특별 배심원을 위한 그 경희극풍 웃음을 띠며 바가 말했다 — 이것은 일반 배심에 앉은 우스꽝스러운 장사치들을 위한 그의 저급희극풍 웃음과는 사뭇 다른 것이었다."], ["Yet hitherto our star has been a vacillating and wavering star?", "\"그분은 한동안 의회에 계셨소. 하지만 지금까지 우리의 그 별은 흔들리고 어른거리는 별이었지요?"], ["Humph?’", "흠?\""]], [["An average witness would have been seduced by the Humph?", "평범한 증인이라면 그 \"흠?\"에 넘어가 긍정의 대답을 했을 것이다."], ["into an affirmative answer, But Ferdinand Barnacle looked knowingly at Bar as he strolled up-stairs, and gave him no answer at all.", "그러나 페르디난드 배너클은 계단을 어슬렁어슬렁 올라가며 바를 의미심장하게 쳐다보고는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["‘Just so, just so,’ said Bar, nodding his head, for he was not to be put off in that way, ‘and therefore I spoke of our sitting _in Banco_ to take a special argument--meaning this to be a high and solemn occasion, when, as Captain Macheath says, “the judges are met: a terrible show!” We lawyers are sufficiently liberal, you see, to quote the Captain, though the Captain is severe upon us. Nevertheless, I think I could put in evidence an admission of the Captain’s,’ said Bar, with a little jocose roll of his head; for, in his legal current of speech, he always assumed the air of rallying himself with the best grace in the world; ‘an admission of the Captain’s that Law, in the gross, is at least intended to be impartial. For what says the Captain, if I quote him correctly--and if not,’ with a light-comedy touch of his double eye-glass on his companion’s shoulder, ‘my learned friend will set me right:", "\"바로 그렇지, 바로 그래,\" 그런 식으로 물러설 사람이 아닌지라 바는 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"그래서 내가 특별 변론을 듣기 위해 전원합의부로 앉는다고 말한 것이오 — 이것이 높고 엄숙한 자리라는 뜻으로 말이오. 매키스 대위가 말하듯, '판관들이 모였으니, 무시무시한 광경이로다!' 하는 그런 자리 말이오. 보다시피 우리 법조인들은 그 대위를 인용할 만큼 너그럽다오, 비록 그 대위가 우리를 가혹하게 다루기는 하지만. 그렇긴 해도, 나는 그 대위의 한 가지 인정을 증거로 제출할 수 있을 것 같소,\" 바가 익살스럽게 고개를 살짝 까딱이며 말했다. 법률적인 말투의 흐름을 탈 때면, 그는 늘 세상에서 가장 우아한 태도로 제 자신을 놀리는 듯한 분위기를 풍겼던 것이다. \"법이, 대체로, 적어도 공정하게 의도되어 있다는 그 대위의 인정 말이오. 내가 그를 옳게 인용한다면 — 만약 그렇지 않다면,\" 이중 안경으로 동행의 어깨를 경희극풍으로 가볍게 톡 치며, \"여기 내 박식한 벗이 바로잡아 줄 터이지만 — 그 대위가 뭐라 했던가 하니:\n'법이란 만인을 위해 만들어진 것이라,"]], [["“Since laws were made for every degree, To curb vice in others as well as in me, I wonder we ha’n’t better company Upon Tyburn Tree!”’", "남들의 악덕도 내 악덕도 다스리려 함이니,\n타이번 교수목* 위에 더 나은 동무가 없음을"]], [["These words brought them to the drawing-room, where Mr Merdle stood before the fire.", "나는 의아해하노라!'\""], ["So immensely astounded was Mr Merdle by the entrance of Bar with such a reference in his mouth, that Bar explained himself to have been quoting Gay. ‘Assuredly not one of our Westminster Hall authorities,’ said he, ‘but still no despicable one to a man possessing the largely-practical Mr Merdle’s knowledge of the world.’", "* 타이번 교수목: 옛 런던의 공개 교수형장."]], [["Mr Merdle looked as if he thought he would say something, but subsequently looked as if he thought he wouldn’t. The interval afforded time for Bishop to be announced.", "이 말들과 함께 그들은 머들 씨가 난로 앞에 서 있는 응접실에 이르렀다. 그런 인용을 입에 담은 바가 들어서는 것을 보고 머들 씨가 어찌나 어마어마하게 놀랐던지, 바는 자기가 게이\\를 인용한 것이라고 해명했다. \"확실히 우리 웨스트민스터 홀\\의 권위자 가운데 하나는 아니지요,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 폭넓은 실무 지식을 갖춘 머들 씨처럼 세상 물정에 밝은 분께는 결코 얕볼 수 없는 권위자랍니다.\" * 게이: 「거지 오페라」를 쓴 극작가 존 게이. * 웨스트민스터 홀: 영국 법정이 열리던 곳."]], [["Bishop came in with meekness, and yet with a strong and rapid step as if he wanted to get his seven-league dress-shoes on, and go round the world to see that everybody was in a satisfactory state.", "머들 씨는 무슨 말을 할까 생각하는 듯이 보이더니, 이내 하지 않는 게 좋겠다고 생각하는 듯이 보였다."], ["Bishop had no idea that there was anything significant in the occasion.", "그 사이에 비숍*이 당도했음이 알려졌다."], ["That was the most remarkable trait in his demeanour. He was crisp, fresh, cheerful, affable, bland; but so surprisingly innocent.", "* 비숍: 주교를 가리키는 호칭이자 별명으로 쓰임."]], [["Bar sidled up to prefer his politest inquiries in reference to the health of Mrs Bishop. Mrs Bishop had been a little unfortunate in the article of taking cold at a Confirmation, but otherwise was well. Young Mr Bishop was also well. He was down, with his young wife and little family, at his Cure of Souls.", "비숍은 온순하게 들어왔으나, 그러면서도 칠 리그 장화를 신은 정장 구두라도 신고서 온 세상을 한 바퀴 돌며 모든 이가 흡족한 상태인지 둘러보고 싶어 하는 듯한, 강하고 빠른 걸음걸이였다. 비숍은 이 자리에 무슨 의미심장한 일이 있다는 것을 전혀 알지 못했다. 그것이 그의 거동에서 가장 두드러진 특징이었다. 그는 산뜻하고, 생기 있고, 쾌활하고, 상냥하고, 부드러웠다. 그러면서도 놀라우리만치 순진했다. 바는 슬그머니 다가가 비숍 부인의 건강에 관해 더없이 정중한 안부를 여쭈었다. 비숍 부인은 견진성사 때 감기에 걸린 점에서 조금 운이 나빴으나, 그 밖에는 평안하다고 했다. 젊은 비숍 씨도 평안했다. 그는 어린 아내와 어린 자식들과 함께 자기 사목지*에 내려가 있다고 했다."]], [["The representatives of the Barnacle Chorus dropped in next, and Mr Merdle’s physician dropped in next. Bar, who had a bit of one eye and a bit of his double eye-glass for every one who came in at the door, no matter with whom he was conversing or what he was talking about, got among them all by some skilful means, without being seen to get at them, and touched each individual gentleman of the jury on his own individual favourite spot. With some of the Chorus, he laughed about the sleepy member who had gone out into the lobby the other night, and voted the wrong way: with others, he deplored that innovating spirit in the time which could not even be prevented from taking an unnatural interest in the public service and the public money: with the physician he had a word to say about the general health; he had also a little information to ask him for, concerning a professional man of unquestioned erudition and polished manners--but those credentials in their highest development he believed were the possession of other professors of the healing art (jury droop)--whom he had happened to have in the witness-box the day before yesterday, and from whom he had elicited in cross-examination that he claimed to be one of the exponents of this new mode of treatment which appeared to Bar to--eh?--well, Bar thought so; Bar had thought, and hoped, Physician would tell him so. Without presuming to decide where doctors disagreed, it did appear to Bar, viewing it as a question of common sense and not of so-called legal penetration, that this new system was--might be, in the presence of so great an authority--say, Humbug? Ah! Fortified by such encouragement, he could venture to say Humbug; and now Bar’s mind was relieved.", "* 사목지(Cure of Souls): 성직자가 영혼을 돌보는 교구. 다음으로 배너클 합창단의 대표들이 들어왔고, 그다음으로 머들 씨의 주치의가 들어왔다. 누구와 이야기를 나누든, 무슨 이야기를 하든, 문으로 들어오는 사람마다 한쪽 눈의 일부와 이중 안경의 일부를 늘 보내던 바는, 무슨 능숙한 수단을 써서, 그들에게 다가가는 것이 눈에 띄지 않으면서도 그들 모두 사이로 끼어들었고, 배심원 한 사람 한 사람의 저마다 좋아하는 급소를 건드렸다. 합창단 가운데 몇몇과는, 며칠 전 밤에 졸다가 로비로 나가 엉뚱한 쪽에 표를 던진 그 의원에 관해 함께 웃었다. 또 다른 몇몇과는, 공공의 직무와 공공의 돈에 대해 부자연스러운 관심을 갖는 것조차 막을 수 없는 그 시대의 혁신적 기풍을 함께 개탄했다. 주치의와는 전반적인 건강에 관해 한마디 나누었다. 또한 그에게 작은 정보를 하나 구하려 했으니, 어느 직업인에 관한 것으로 — 학식은 의심할 바 없고 태도는 세련되었으되, 그 두 자질이 가장 높은 경지로 발휘되는 것은 다른 치료술 종사자들의 소유라고 그가 믿는 인물(배심원용 처진 표정) — 그저께 그가 우연히 증인석에 세웠던 사람이며, 반대신문에서 그자가 스스로를 바로 이 새로운 치료법의 주창자 가운데 하나라 자처한다는 사실을 끌어냈는데, 바가 보기에 그 치료법은 — 어떻소? — 글쎄, 바는 그렇게 생각했다. 바는 주치의가 자기에게 그렇게 말해 주리라 생각했고, 또 그러기를 바랐다. 의사들끼리 의견이 갈리는 데서 감히 판단을 내리려는 것은 아니나, 이른바 법률적 통찰이 아니라 상식의 문제로 보건대, 이 새로운 체계는 — 이토록 위대한 권위자 앞에서 말하건대 — 말하자면, 엉터리*가 아니겠소? 아! 그런 격려에 힘입어 그는 감히 엉터리라고 말할 수 있었다. 그리하여 이제 바의 마음은 후련해졌다."]], [["Mr Tite Barnacle, who, like Dr Johnson’s celebrated acquaintance, had only one idea in his head and that was a wrong one, had appeared by this time.", "* 엉터리(Humbug): 사기, 허튼소리를 뜻함. 존슨 박사의 그 유명한 지인처럼, 머릿속에 단 하나의 생각밖에 없고 그마저도 틀린 생각이었던 타이트 배너클 씨가, 이때쯤 모습을 드러냈다."], ["This eminent gentleman and Mr Merdle, seated diverse ways and with ruminating aspects on a yellow ottoman in the light of the fire, holding no verbal communication with each other, bore a strong general resemblance to the two cows in the Cuyp picture over against them.", "이 저명한 신사와 머들 씨는, 난롯불 빛 속에서 노란 오토만 위에 서로 다른 방향을 향해 골똘히 되새기는 표정으로 앉아, 아무런 말도 주고받지 않은 채, 자기들 맞은편에 걸린 카위프*의 그림 속 두 마리 소와 전반적으로 강한 닮음을 보였다."]], [["But now, Lord Decimus arrived.", "* 카위프: 17세기 네덜란드 풍경화가 알베르트 카위프."], ["The Chief Butler, who up to this time had limited himself to a branch of his usual function by looking at the company as they entered (and that, with more of defiance than favour), put himself so far out of his way as to come up-stairs with him and announce him.", "그런데 이제 데시머스 경이 도착했다. 그때까지 들어오는 손님들을 (호의보다는 적의를 담아) 바라보는 것으로 자기 평소 직무의 한 갈래에 머물러 있던 집사장이, 그를 위해서는 분에 넘치게 몸소 계단을 함께 올라와 그의 당도를 알렸다."], ["Lord Decimus being an overpowering peer, a bashful young member of the Lower House who was the last fish but one caught by the Barnacles, and who had been invited on this occasion to commemorate his capture, shut his eyes when his Lordship came in.", "데시머스 경이 위압적인 귀족인지라, 배너클 일가가 낚은 마지막에서 두 번째 물고기이자 이 자리에 자기 포획을 기념하려 초대된, 수줍은 젊은 하원의원은, 그 경이 들어서자 두 눈을 감아 버렸다."]], [["Lord Decimus, nevertheless, was glad to see the Member.", "그렇지만 데시머스 경은 그 의원을 보고 기뻐했다."], ["He was also glad to see Mr Merdle, glad to see Bishop, glad to see Bar, glad to see Physician, glad to see Tite Barnacle, glad to see Chorus, glad to see Ferdinand his private secretary.", "머들 씨를 보고도 기뻐했고, 비숍을 보고도 기뻐했고, 바를 보고도 기뻐했고, 주치의를 보고도 기뻐했고, 타이트 배너클을 보고도 기뻐했고, 합창단을 보고도 기뻐했고, 자기 개인비서인 페르디난드를 보고도 기뻐했다."], ["Lord Decimus, though one of the greatest of the earth, was not remarkable for ingratiatory manners, and Ferdinand had coached him up to the point of noticing all the fellows he might find there, and saying he was glad to see them.", "데시머스 경은 세상에서 가장 위대한 인물 가운데 하나이긴 했으나 사람의 환심을 사는 태도로는 그리 두드러지지 못했는데, 페르디난드가 그곳에서 만날 만한 모든 친구를 알아보고 만나서 기쁘다고 말하는 데까지 그를 미리 일러 두었던 것이다."], ["When he had achieved this rush of vivacity and condescension, his Lordship composed himself into the picture after Cuyp, and made a third cow in the group.", "이 활기와 겸양의 발작을 다 해내고 나자, 그 경은 카위프풍 그림 속으로 가라앉아, 그 무리에 세 번째 소가 되었다."]], [["Bar, who felt that he had got all the rest of the jury and must now lay hold of the Foreman, soon came sidling up, double eye-glass in hand.", "배심원의 나머지를 다 확보했으니 이제는 배심장을 붙잡아야겠다고 느낀 바는, 이중 안경을 손에 들고 곧 슬그머니 다가왔다."], ["Bar tendered the weather, as a subject neatly aloof from official reserve, for the Foreman’s consideration.", "바는 공직의 신중함과는 얌전히 동떨어진 화제로서 날씨를 배심장의 고려에 올렸다."], ["Bar said that he was told (as everybody always is told, though who tells them, and why, will ever remain a mystery), that there was to be no wall-fruit this year.", "바는 (누구나 늘 듣게 되는 말이지만, 누가 무엇 때문에 그런 말을 해 주는지는 영영 수수께끼인) 올해는 담장 과실이 없을 거라는 이야기를 들었노라고 했다."], ["Lord Decimus had not heard anything amiss of his peaches, but rather believed, if his people were correct, he was to have no apples.", "데시머스 경은 자기 복숭아에 대해서는 무슨 나쁜 소식을 듣지 못했으나, 자기 집 사람들의 말이 옳다면 사과는 없을 듯하다고 했다."], ["No apples?", "사과가 없다고요?"], ["Bar was lost in astonishment and concern.", "바는 놀라움과 걱정에 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["It would have been all one to him, in reality, if there had not been a pippin on the surface of the earth, but his show of interest in this apple question was positively painful.", "사실 지상에 사과 한 알이 없다 한들 그에게는 매한가지였겠으나, 이 사과 문제에 대한 그의 관심 어린 시늉은 그야말로 고통스러울 지경이었다."], ["Now, to what, Lord Decimus--for we troublesome lawyers loved to gather information, and could never tell how useful it might prove to us--to what, Lord Decimus, was this to be attributed?", "그런데, 데시머스 경, 이것을 어디에 — 귀찮은 우리 법조인들은 정보를 모으기를 좋아하고, 그것이 우리에게 언제 얼마나 쓸모 있을지 도무지 알 수 없으니까요 — 데시머스 경, 이것을 어디에 돌려야 하겠습니까?"], ["Lord Decimus could not undertake to propound any theory about it.", "데시머스 경은 그에 관해 어떤 이론도 내놓겠다고 나설 수 없었다."], ["This might have stopped another man; but Bar, sticking to him fresh as ever, said, ‘As to pears, now?’", "다른 사람이라면 여기서 멈췄을 법도 했으나, 바는 여느 때처럼 싱싱하게 그에게 들러붙으며 말했다. \"그러면 배는 어떻습니까, 자?\""]], [["Long after Bar got made Attorney-General, this was told of him as a master-stroke.", "바가 검찰총장이 되고 한참 뒤까지도 이 일은 그의 묘수로 회자되었다. 데시머스 경에게는 옛날 이튼 시절 사감 집 뒤뜰 근처 정원에서 자라던 배나무 한 그루에 얽힌 추억이 있었으니, 그 배나무 위에서 그의 평생 유일한 농담이 해마다 꽃을 피웠다."], ["Lord Decimus had a reminiscence about a pear-tree formerly growing in a garden near the back of his dame’s house at Eton, upon which pear-tree the only joke of his life perennially bloomed.", "그것은 짜임새 있고 간편한 종류의 농담으로, 이튼 배(pears)와 의회의 짝짓기 표결(pairs)* 사이의 차이를 가지고 노는 것이었다. 그러나 그것은, 그 나무와 속속들이 친밀하게 사귀지 않고서는 그 정묘한 묘미를 도무지 맛볼 수 없다고 데시머스 경에게는 여겨지는 듯한 농담이었다."], ["It was a joke of a compact and portable nature, turning on the difference between Eton pears and Parliamentary pairs; but it was a joke, a refined relish of which would seem to have appeared to Lord Decimus impossible to be had without a thorough and intimate acquaintance with the tree.", "그러므로 그 이야기는 처음에는 그런 나무가 있다는 생각조차 없었다가, 차차 겨울 속에서 그것을 찾아내고, 변하는 계절을 거쳐 그것을 끌고 나가며, 그것이 움트는 것을 보고, 꽃피는 것을 보고, 열매 맺는 것을 보고, 열매가 익는 것을 보았다. 요컨대, 침실 창문을 빠져나가 그 열매를 훔치러 가기에 앞서 어찌나 부지런하고 세세하게 그 나무를 가꾸었던지, 그 나무가 데시머스 경 시대 이전에 심어지고 접붙여졌다는 데 대해, 늦게까지 붙들려 듣던 이들의 감사가 숱하게 바쳐졌을 정도였다."], ["Therefore, the story at first had no idea of such a tree, sir, then gradually found it in winter, carried it through the changing season, saw it bud, saw it blossom, saw it bear fruit, saw the fruit ripen; in short, cultivated the tree in that diligent and minute manner before it got out of the bed-room window to steal the fruit, that many thanks had been offered up by belated listeners for the trees having been planted and grafted prior to Lord Decimus’s time.", "사과에 대한 바의 관심은, 데시머스 경이 \"배 이야기를 하시니 어느 배나무 한 그루가 생각나는구려\" 하고 엄숙하게 운을 떼는 순간부터 \"그리하여 우리는 인생의 갖가지 변천을 거쳐, 이튼의 배에서 의회의 짝짓기 표결로 넘어가는 것이라오\""], ["Bar’s interest in apples was so overtopped by the wrapt suspense in which he pursued the changes of these pears, from the moment when Lord Decimus solemnly opened with ‘Your mentioning pears recalls to my remembrance a pear-tree,’ down to the rich conclusion, ‘And so we pass, through the various changes of life, from Eton pears to Parliamentary pairs,’ that he had to go down-stairs with Lord Decimus, and even then to be seated next to him at table in order that he might hear the anecdote out.", "하는 풍성한 결말에 이르기까지, 이 배들의 변천을 좇으며 빠져든 넋 나간 긴장에 어찌나 압도되었던지, 그는 데시머스 경과 함께 아래층으로 내려가야 했고, 그러고도 이 일화를 끝까지 듣기 위해 식탁에서 그의 옆자리에 앉아야 했다. 그쯤 되자 바는 배심장을 확보했다고 느꼈고, 좋은 식욕으로 만찬에 임할 수 있었다."], ["By that time, Bar felt that he had secured the Foreman, and might go to dinner with a good appetite.", "* 배(pears)와 짝짓기 표결(pairs): 발음이 같음을 이용한 말장난. 의회의 'pair'는 표결 때 입장이 반대인 두 의원이 함께 기권하기로 짝을 짓는 관행."]], [["It was a dinner to provoke an appetite, though he had not had one.", "비록 식욕이 없었더라도 식욕을 돋울 만한 만찬이었다."], ["The rarest dishes, sumptuously cooked and sumptuously served; the choicest fruits; the most exquisite wines; marvels of workmanship in gold and silver, china and glass; innumerable things delicious to the senses of taste, smell, and sight, were insinuated into its composition.", "더없이 진귀한 요리들이 호사스럽게 조리되어 호사스럽게 차려졌고, 가장 고른 과일들, 가장 빼어난 포도주들, 금과 은, 도자기와 유리로 된 솜씨의 경이들, 미각과 후각과 시각을 만족시키는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 것들이 그 구성 속에 슬며시 짜여 들었다."], ["O, what a wonderful man this Merdle, what a great man, what a master man, how blessedly and enviably endowed--in one word, what a rich man!", "오, 이 머들이라는 자는 얼마나 놀라운 사람인가, 얼마나 위대한 사람인가, 얼마나 능란한 사람인가, 얼마나 복되고 부럽게 타고났는가 — 한마디로, 얼마나 부유한 사람인가!"]], [["He took his usual poor eighteenpennyworth of food in his usual indigestive way, and had as little to say for himself as ever a wonderful man had.", "그는 여느 때처럼 소화도 안 되는 식으로 평소의 그 빈약한 일 실링 육 페니어치 음식을 들었고, 놀라운 사람치고 자기에 관해 할 말이 그토록 적은 사람도 없었다."], ["Fortunately Lord Decimus was one of those sublimities who have no occasion to be talked to, for they can be at any time sufficiently occupied with the contemplation of their own greatness.", "다행히도 데시머스 경은, 말을 걸어 줄 필요가 전혀 없는 그런 숭고한 인물 가운데 하나였으니, 언제든 제 자신의 위대함을 관조하는 것만으로 충분히 시간을 보낼 수 있는 까닭이었다."], ["This enabled the bashful young Member to keep his eyes open long enough at a time to see his dinner.", "덕분에 그 수줍은 젊은 의원은 한 번에 충분히 오래 눈을 떠서 자기 식사를 볼 수 있었다."], ["But, whenever Lord Decimus spoke, he shut them again.", "그러나 데시머스 경이 입을 열 때마다, 그는 다시 눈을 감았다."]], [["The agreeable young Barnacle, and Bar, were the talkers of the party.", "그 호감 가는 젊은 배너클과 바가 좌중의 이야기꾼이었다."], ["Bishop would have been exceedingly agreeable also, but that his innocence stood in his way.", "비숍도 무척이나 호감 가는 이야기꾼이 되었을 테지만, 그의 순진함이 걸림돌이었다."], ["He was so soon left behind.", "그는 너무도 쉽게 뒤처졌다."], ["When there was any little hint of anything being in the wind, he got lost directly.", "무슨 일이 진행되고 있다는 작은 낌새라도 있으면, 그는 곧장 길을 잃었다."], ["Worldly affairs were too much for him; he couldn’t make them out at all.", "세상사는 그에게 벅찼다. 그는 그것을 도무지 알아채지 못했다."]], [["This was observable when Bar said, incidentally, that he was happy to have heard that we were soon to have the advantage of enlisting on the good side, the sound and plain sagacity--not demonstrative or ostentatious, but thoroughly sound and practical--of our friend Mr Sparkler.", "이 점은 바가 지나가는 말로, 우리가 머지않아 우리 벗 스파클러 씨의 — 과시하거나 떠벌리는 것이 아니라 철저히 건전하고 실무적인 — 그 건전하고 평이한 분별력을 좋은 편에 끌어들이는 이점을 누리게 되리라는 이야기를 들어 기쁘다고 했을 때 드러났다."]], [["Ferdinand Barnacle laughed, and said oh yes, he believed so.", "페르디난드 배너클은 웃으며, 오 그렇소, 그런 줄로 안다고 했다."], ["A vote was a vote, and always acceptable.", "한 표는 한 표이고, 언제나 환영받는 법이니까."]], [["Bar was sorry to miss our good friend Mr Sparkler to-day, Mr Merdle.", "바는 오늘 우리의 좋은 벗 스파클러 씨를 만나지 못해 아쉽다고 했다, 머들 씨."]], [["‘He is away with Mrs Merdle,’ returned that gentleman, slowly coming out of a long abstraction, in the course of which he had been fitting a tablespoon up his sleeve.", "\"그 애는 머들 부인과 함께 떠나 있소,\" 그 신사가, 줄곧 식탁용 큰 숟가락을 자기 소매에 끼워 넣고 있던 긴 멍한 상태에서 천천히 깨어나며 대답했다."], ["‘It is not indispensable for him to be on the spot.’", "\"그 애가 꼭 이 자리에 있어야 하는 건 아니니까.\""]], [["‘The magic name of Merdle,’ said Bar, with the jury droop, ‘no doubt will suffice for all.’", "\"머들이라는 그 마법의 이름이면,\" 바가 배심원용 처진 표정으로 말했다. \"틀림없이 모든 게 다 될 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Why--yes--I believe so,’ assented Mr Merdle, putting the spoon aside, and clumsily hiding each of his hands in the coat-cuff of the other hand.", "\"글쎄 — 그렇소 — 그런 줄로 아오,\" 머들 씨는 숟가락을 옆에 내려놓고 두 손을 어색하게 서로 상대 손의 소맷부리에 감추며 수긍했다."], ["‘I believe the people in my interest down there will not make any difficulty.’", "\"거기 내 영향권 안에 있는 사람들이 별 까다롭게 굴지는 않으리라 보오.\""]], [["‘Model people!’ said Bar.", "\"본받을 만한 사람들이군요!\" 바가 말했다."]], [["‘I am glad you approve of them,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"그들을 그리 좋게 봐 주시니 기쁘오,\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘And the people of those other two places, now,’ pursued Bar, with a bright twinkle in his keen eye, as it slightly turned in the direction of his magnificent neighbour; ‘we lawyers are always curious, always inquisitive, always picking up odds and ends for our patchwork minds, since there is no knowing when and where they may fit into some corner;--the people of those other two places now? Do they yield so laudably to the vast and cumulative influence of such enterprise and such renown; do those little rills become absorbed so quietly and easily, and, as it were by the influence of natural laws, so beautifully, in the swoop of the majestic stream as it flows upon its wondrous way enriching the surrounding lands; that their course is perfectly to be calculated, and distinctly to be predicated?’", "\"그러면 그 다른 두 곳의 사람들은요, 자,\" 바가 자기 으리으리한 옆 사람 쪽으로 살짝 눈길을 돌리며, 날카로운 눈에 환한 반짝임을 띠고 말을 이었다. \"우리 법조인들은 늘 호기심이 많고, 늘 캐묻기를 좋아하며, 우리 누덕이 같은 머릿속에 자투리들을 늘 주워 모으지요, 그것이 언제 어디서 어느 구석에 들어맞을지 알 수 없으니까요 — 그러면 그 다른 두 곳의 사람들은요? 그들도 이토록 가상하게, 그런 사업과 그런 명성의 광대하고 누적된 영향에 순순히 따르는지요? 그 작은 시냇물들도, 주위의 땅을 비옥하게 하며 경이로운 길을 따라 흐르는 그 장엄한 강물의 휩쓸림 속으로, 그토록 조용하고 수월하게, 말하자면 자연법칙의 작용에 의해 그토록 아름답게 흡수되어, 그 물길이 완벽하게 계산되고 또렷이 예측될 수 있는지요?\""]], [["Mr Merdle, a little troubled by Bar’s eloquence, looked fitfully about the nearest salt-cellar for some moments, and then said hesitating:", "바의 능변에 다소 곤혹스러워진 머들 씨는, 잠깐 동안 가장 가까운 소금통 둘레를 두서없이 바라보더니, 이윽고 머뭇거리며 말했다."]], [["‘They are perfectly aware, sir, of their duty to Society.", "\"그들은, 선생, 사회에 대한 자기네 의무를 완벽하게 알고 있소."], ["They will return anybody I send to them for that purpose.’", "내가 그 목적으로 보내는 사람이면 누구든 당선시켜 줄 거요.\""]], [["‘Cheering to know,’ said Bar.", "\"기쁜 소식이군요,\" 바가 말했다."], ["‘Cheering to know.’", "\"기쁜 소식입니다.\""]], [["The three places in question were three little rotten holes in this Island, containing three little ignorant, drunken, guzzling, dirty, out-of-the-way constituencies, that had reeled into Mr Merdle’s pocket.", "문제의 그 세 곳은 이 섬나라의 자그마한 썩은 구멍 셋으로, 무지하고 술 취하고 게걸스럽고 지저분하며 외진 작은 선거구 셋을 품고 있었는데, 그것들이 비틀거리며 머들 씨의 호주머니 속으로 굴러떨어진 것이었다."], ["Ferdinand Barnacle laughed in his easy way, and airily said they were a nice set of fellows.", "페르디난드 배너클은 그 느긋한 투로 웃으며, 참 괜찮은 친구들이라고 가볍게 말했다."], ["Bishop, mentally perambulating among paths of peace, was altogether swallowed up in absence of mind.", "평화의 길을 거닐며 마음을 헤매던 비숍은 멍한 상태에 온통 삼켜져 있었다."]], [["‘Pray,’ asked Lord Decimus, casting his eyes around the table, ‘what is this story I have heard of a gentleman long confined in a debtors’ prison proving to be of a wealthy family, and having come into the inheritance of a large sum of money?", "\"한데,\" 데시머스 경이 식탁을 빙 둘러보며 물었다."], ["I have met with a variety of allusions to it.", "\"채무자 감옥에 오래 갇혀 있던 어느 신사가 알고 보니 부유한 집안 출신이고, 막대한 돈의 유산을 물려받게 되었다는, 내가 들은 그 이야기는 대체 무엇이오? 그에 관해 이런저런 언급을 여러 번 접했소."], ["Do you know anything of it, Ferdinand?’", "자네는 그에 관해 뭔가 아는 바가 있는가, 페르디난드?\""]], [["‘I only know this much,’ said Ferdinand, ‘that he has given the Department with which I have the honour to be associated;’ this sparkling young Barnacle threw off the phrase sportively, as who should say, We know all about these forms of speech, but we must keep it up, we must keep the game alive; ‘no end of trouble, and has put us into innumerable fixes.’", "\"저는 이 정도밖에 모릅니다,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"그자가, 제가 몸담는 영광을 누리는 부처에\" — 이 반짝이는 젊은 배너클은 마치 '우리야 이런 말투를 빤히 알지만, 그래도 계속 써 줘야지, 이 놀음을 살려 두어야지' 하는 듯이 그 표현을 장난스럽게 내던졌다 — \"끝없는 골칫거리를 안겼고, 우리를 숱한 곤경에 빠뜨렸다는 것 정도지요.\""]], [["‘Fixes?’ repeated Lord Decimus, with a majestic pausing and pondering on the word that made the bashful Member shut his eyes quite tight.", "\"곤경이라?\" 데시머스 경이, 그 수줍은 의원으로 하여금 두 눈을 꼭 감게 만든, 위엄 있는 멈춤과 곱씹음으로 그 단어를 되뇌었다."], ["‘Fixes?’", "\"곤경이라?\""]], [["‘A very perplexing business indeed,’ observed Mr Tite Barnacle, with an air of grave resentment.", "\"실로 무척이나 골치 아픈 일이었습니다,\" 타이트 배너클 씨가 점잖은 분개의 기색으로 말했다."]], [["‘What,’ said Lord Decimus, ‘was the character of his business; what was the nature of these--a--Fixes, Ferdinand?’", "\"그래,\" 데시머스 경이 말했다. \"그자의 일이란 어떤 성격의 것이었는가? 그 — 어 — 곤경이라는 것의 본질이 무엇이었나, 페르디난드?\""]], [["‘Oh, it’s a good story, as a story,’ returned that gentleman; ‘as good a thing of its kind as need be.", "\"오, 이야기로 치자면 괜찮은 이야기입니다,\" 그 신사가 대답했다."], ["This Mr Dorrit (his name is Dorrit) had incurred a responsibility to us, ages before the fairy came out of the Bank and gave him his fortune, under a bond he had signed for the performance of a contract which was not at all performed.", "\"그런 종류로는 더 바랄 게 없는 이야기지요. 이 도릿 씨라는 자가(이름이 도릿입니다) 어떤 계약의 이행을 위해 서명한 증서에 따라 우리에게 책임을 지고 있었는데, 그 계약이 전혀 이행되지 않았던 거지요."], ["He was a partner in a house in some large way--spirits, or buttons, or wine, or blacking, or oatmeal, or woollen, or pork, or hooks and eyes, or iron, or treacle, or shoes, or something or other that was wanted for troops, or seamen, or somebody--and the house burst, and we being among the creditors, detainees were lodged on the part of the Crown in a scientific manner, and all the rest of it.", "그 요정이 은행에서 나와 그에게 재산을 안겨 주기 오래전 일입니다. 그자는 무슨 큰 사업을 하는 상사의 동업자였는데 — 증류주든, 단추든, 포도주든, 구두약이든, 귀리든, 모직물이든, 돼지고기든, 갈고리단추든, 쇠붙이든, 당밀이든, 신발이든, 군대나 선원이나 누군가에게 필요한 그 무엇이든 — 아무튼 그 상사가 파산했고, 우리도 채권자 가운데 있던지라, 왕실 측에서 학술적인 방식으로 구금 신청을 걸고, 그 밖에 온갖 절차를 다 밟았지요."], ["When the fairy had appeared and he wanted to pay us off, Egad we had got into such an exemplary state of checking and counter-checking, signing and counter-signing, that it was six months before we knew how to take the money, or how to give a receipt for it.", "요정이 나타나 그가 우리에게 빚을 갚으려 했을 때, 어럽쇼, 우리는 점검과 재점검, 서명과 부서(副署)의 그 본보기 같은 상태에 빠져 있어서, 그 돈을 어떻게 받아야 할지, 어떻게 영수증을 써 줘야 할지 알아내는 데 여섯 달이 걸렸습니다. 그것은 공무의 개가였지요,\""], ["It was a triumph of public business,’ said this handsome young Barnacle, laughing heartily, ‘You never saw such a lot of forms in your life.", "이 잘생긴 젊은 배너클이 진심으로 껄껄 웃으며 말했다. \"평생 그렇게 많은 서식은 본 적이 없으실 겁니다."], ["“Why,”", "'아니'"], ["the attorney said to me one day, “if I wanted this office to give me two or three thousand pounds instead of take it, I couldn’t have more trouble about it.”", "하고 어느 날 그 변호사가 제게 말하더군요. '내가 이 부처더러 돈을 받으라는 게 아니라 나한테 이삼천 파운드를 내놓으라고 했어도, 이보다 더 애를 먹지는 않았겠소.'"], ["“You are right, old fellow,”", "'맞는 말이오, 친구,' 제가 말했지요."], ["I told him, “and in future you’ll know that we have something to do here.”’ The pleasant young Barnacle finished by once more laughing heartily.", "'앞으로는 우리가 여기서 뭔가 하는 일이 있다는 걸 알게 될 거요.'\" 그 유쾌한 젊은 배너클은 다시 한 번 진심으로 껄껄 웃으며 말을 맺었다."], ["He was a very easy, pleasant fellow indeed, and his manners were exceedingly winning.", "그는 실로 무척이나 느긋하고 유쾌한 친구였고, 그 태도는 더없이 사람을 끌어당겼다."]], [["Mr Tite Barnacle’s view of the business was of a less airy character.", "이 일에 대한 타이트 배너클 씨의 견해는 덜 가뿐한 성격의 것이었다."], ["He took it ill that Mr Dorrit had troubled the Department by wanting to pay the money, and considered it a grossly informal thing to do after so many years.", "그는 도릿 씨가 돈을 갚으려 들어 부처를 성가시게 한 것을 못마땅하게 여겼고, 그토록 여러 해가 지난 뒤에 그런 짓을 한 것을 지극히 비정상적인 일로 여겼다."], ["But Mr Tite Barnacle was a buttoned-up man, and consequently a weighty one.", "그러나 타이트 배너클 씨는 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람이었고, 따라서 묵직한 사람이었다."], ["All buttoned-up men are weighty.", "단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람은 다들 묵직하다."], ["All buttoned-up men are believed in.", "단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람은 다들 신뢰받는다."], ["Whether or no the reserved and never-exercised power of unbuttoning, fascinates mankind; whether or no wisdom is supposed to condense and augment when buttoned up, and to evaporate when unbuttoned; it is certain that the man to whom importance is accorded is the buttoned-up man.", "단추를 끄르는, 그러나 결코 행사되지 않는 비축된 권능이 인류를 매혹하는 것인지, 아니면 지혜란 단추를 채우면 응축되고 부풀며 단추를 끄르면 증발한다고 여겨지는 것인지는 모르겠으나, 중요성을 부여받는 사람이 단추를 꼭꼭 채운 사람이라는 것만은 확실하다."], ["Mr Tite Barnacle never would have passed for half his current value, unless his coat had been always buttoned-up to his white cravat.", "타이트 배너클 씨는, 만약 그 외투가 늘 흰 크라바트까지 단추가 꼭꼭 채워져 있지 않았더라면, 결코 그 통용되는 가치의 절반만큼도 행세하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["‘May I ask,’ said Lord Decimus, ‘if Mr Darrit--or Dorrit--has any family?’", "\"여쭤도 되겠소,\" 데시머스 경이 말했다. \"그 대릿 씨가 — 아니 도릿 씨가 — 가족이 있소?\""]], [["Nobody else replying, the host said, ‘He has two daughters, my lord.’", "아무도 대답하지 않자, 주인이 말했다. \"딸이 둘 있습니다, 각하.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["you are acquainted with him?’ asked Lord Decimus.", "그대가 그자를 아는 거요?\" 데시머스 경이 물었다."]], [["‘Mrs Merdle is.", "\"머들 부인이 압니다."], ["Mr Sparkler is, too.", "스파클러 씨도 압니다. 실은,\""], ["In fact,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I rather believe that one of the young ladies has made an impression on Edmund Sparkler.", "머들 씨가 말했다. \"그 젊은 숙녀 중 하나가 에드먼드 스파클러에게 인상을 남긴 게 아닌가 싶기도 합니다."], ["He is susceptible, and--I--think--the conquest--’ Here Mr Merdle stopped, and looked at the table-cloth, as he usually did when he found himself observed or listened to.", "그 애가 마음이 잘 흔들리는 데다 — 제 — 생각엔 — 그 정복이 —\" 여기서 머들 씨는 말을 멈추고, 자기가 주목받거나 경청되고 있음을 깨달았을 때 늘 그러듯 식탁보를 내려다보았다."]], [["Bar was uncommonly pleased to find that the Merdle family, and this family, had already been brought into contact.", "바는 머들 집안과 이 집안이 이미 접촉했다는 사실을 알고 유난히 기뻐했다."], ["He submitted, in a low voice across the table to Bishop, that it was a kind of analogical illustration of those physical laws, in virtue of which Like flies to Like.", "그는 식탁 너머로 비숍에게 낮은 목소리로, 그것은 유유상종이라는 그 물리법칙의 일종의 유비적 예증이라고 했다."], ["He regarded this power of attraction in wealth to draw wealth to it, as something remarkably interesting and curious--something indefinably allied to the loadstone and gravitation.", "그는 부가 부를 제게로 끌어당기는 이 인력을 무언가 자못 흥미롭고 신기한 것으로 — 자석이나 만유인력과 어딘지 모르게 닮은 무엇으로 — 여겼다."], ["Bishop, who had ambled back to earth again when the present theme was broached, acquiesced.", "지금의 주제가 꺼내질 때 다시 땅 위로 어슬렁어슬렁 돌아왔던 비숍은 이에 동의했다."], ["He said it was indeed highly important to Society that one in the trying situation of unexpectedly finding himself invested with a power for good or for evil in Society, should become, as it were, merged in the superior power of a more legitimate and more gigantic growth, the influence of which (as in the case of our friend at whose board we sat) was habitually exercised in harmony with the best interests of Society.", "그는 말하기를, 뜻하지 않게 자기가 사회에서 선이든 악이든 행할 권능을 떠안게 되었음을 알게 된 그 곤란한 처지에 놓인 자가, 더 정당하고 더 거대하게 자라난 우월한 권능 — 그 영향력이 (우리가 그 식탁에 앉아 있는 우리 벗의 경우처럼) 사회의 최선의 이익과 조화롭게 늘 행사되는 그 권능 — 속으로 말하자면 흡수되어 들어가는 것은 실로 사회에 대단히 중요한 일이라고 했다."], ["Thus, instead of two rival and contending flames, a larger and a lesser, each burning with a lurid and uncertain glare, we had a blended and a softened light whose genial ray diffused an equable warmth throughout the land.", "그리하여, 저마다 음산하고 불확실한 불빛으로 타오르는, 크고 작은 두 개의 경쟁하고 다투는 불꽃 대신, 우리는 섞이고 누그러진 하나의 빛을 갖게 되며, 그 따스한 빛살이 온 나라에 고른 온기를 퍼뜨린다고 했다."], ["Bishop seemed to like his own way of putting the case very much, and rather dwelt upon it; Bar, meanwhile (not to throw away a jury-man), making a show of sitting at his feet and feeding on his precepts.", "비숍은 그 비유를 자기 식으로 풀어놓은 것이 무척 마음에 드는 듯 그것을 자못 곱씹었고, 그동안 바는 (배심원 하나도 놓치지 않으려고) 그의 발치에 앉아 그 가르침을 받아먹는 시늉을 했다."]], [["The dinner and dessert being three hours long, the bashful Member cooled in the shadow of Lord Decimus faster than he warmed with food and drink, and had but a chilly time of it.", "만찬과 후식이 세 시간이나 이어지는 동안, 수줍은 의원은 음식과 술로 데워지는 것보다 더 빠르게 데시머스 경의 그늘 속에서 차게 식어, 으슬으슬한 시간을 보냈다."], ["Lord Decimus, like a tall tower in a flat country, seemed to project himself across the table-cloth, hide the light from the honourable Member, cool the honourable Member’s marrow, and give him a woeful idea of distance.", "데시머스 경은 평평한 들판에 우뚝 선 높은 탑처럼 식탁보를 가로질러 제 몸을 내미는 듯했고, 그 명예로운 의원에게서 빛을 가렸으며, 그 명예로운 의원의 골수까지 식혔고, 그에게 거리에 대한 비참한 관념을 안겨 주었다."], ["When he asked this unfortunate traveller to take wine, he encompassed his faltering steps with the gloomiest of shades; and when he said, ‘Your health sir!’ all around him was barrenness and desolation.", "그가 이 불운한 길손에게 술을 들라 권할 때면, 그 비틀거리는 발걸음을 가장 음울한 그늘로 에워쌌고, \"당신의 건강을 위하여, 선생!\" 하고 말하면, 그 둘레는 온통 황량하고 적막했다."]], [["At length Lord Decimus, with a coffee-cup in his hand, began to hover about among the pictures, and to cause an interesting speculation to arise in all minds as to the probabilities of his ceasing to hover, and enabling the smaller birds to flutter up-stairs; which could not be done until he had urged his noble pinions in that direction.", "마침내 데시머스 경은 커피잔을 손에 들고 그림들 사이를 맴돌기 시작하더니, 그가 맴돌기를 그쳐 더 작은 새들이 위층으로 푸드덕 올라갈 수 있게 해 줄 가능성이 얼마나 되는가를 두고 모든 이의 마음에 흥미로운 추측이 일게 만들었으니, 그가 자기 고귀한 깃을 그쪽으로 펼치기 전에는 그렇게 될 수 없는 일이었다."], ["After some delay, and several stretches of his wings which came to nothing, he soared to the drawing-rooms.", "얼마간 미적거리고, 별 소득 없이 몇 차례 날개를 펴 보인 끝에, 그는 응접실들로 솟구쳐 올랐다."]], [["And here a difficulty arose, which always does arise when two people are specially brought together at a dinner to confer with one another.", "그리고 여기서, 만찬에서 두 사람이 서로 의논하라고 특별히 한자리에 모일 때면 늘 생기는 난처함이 일어났다."], ["Everybody (except Bishop, who had no suspicion of it) knew perfectly well that this dinner had been eaten and drunk, specifically to the end that Lord Decimus and Mr Merdle should have five minutes’ conversation together.", "모두가 (그런 낌새를 전혀 눈치채지 못한 비숍만 빼고) 이 만찬이, 데시머스 경과 머들 씨가 단둘이 오 분간 대화를 나누게 한다는 바로 그 목적을 위해 먹고 마셔진 것임을 완벽하게 알고 있었다."], ["The opportunity so elaborately prepared was now arrived, and it seemed from that moment that no mere human ingenuity could so much as get the two chieftains into the same room.", "그토록 공들여 마련된 기회가 이제 다가왔건만, 그 순간부터 인간의 그 어떤 묘책으로도 이 두 우두머리를 같은 방 안에 들여놓는 일조차 해낼 수 없을 것만 같았다."], ["Mr Merdle and his noble guest persisted in prowling about at opposite ends of the perspective.", "머들 씨와 그 고귀한 손님은 원근법 풍경의 양 끝에서 어슬렁거리기를 고집했다."], ["It was in vain for the engaging Ferdinand to bring Lord Decimus to look at the bronze horses near Mr Merdle.", "매력적인 페르디난드가 데시머스 경을 머들 씨 곁의 청동 말들을 보러 데려오려 해도 헛수고였다."], ["Then Mr Merdle evaded, and wandered away.", "그러면 머들 씨가 슬쩍 비켜 다른 데로 가 버렸다."], ["It was in vain for him to bring Mr Merdle to Lord Decimus to tell him the history of the unique Dresden vases.", "머들 씨를 데시머스 경에게 데려와 그 진귀한 드레스덴 화병들의 내력을 들려주게 하려 해도 헛수고였다."], ["Then Lord Decimus evaded and wandered away, while he was getting his man up to the mark.", "그가 자기 사람을 그 자리까지 끌어올려 놓으면, 이번엔 데시머스 경이 슬쩍 비켜 다른 데로 가 버렸다."]], [["‘Did you ever see such a thing as this?’ said Ferdinand to Bar when he had been baffled twenty times.", "\"이런 일 본 적 있소?\" 스무 번이나 좌절한 페르디난드가 바에게 말했다."]], [["‘Often,’ returned Bar.", "\"자주 봤지,\" 바가 대답했다."]], [["‘Unless I butt one of them into an appointed corner, and you butt the other,’ said Ferdinand, ‘it will not come off after all.’", "\"내가 한쪽을 정해 둔 구석으로 들이받아 몰고, 당신이 다른 쪽을 들이받아 몰지 않는 한,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"결국 성사되지 않을 거요.\""]], [["‘Very good,’ said Bar.", "\"좋소,\" 바가 말했다."], ["‘I’ll butt Merdle, if you like; but not my lord.’", "\"원한다면 내가 머들을 들이받지요. 하지만 각하는 안 되오.\""]], [["Ferdinand laughed, in the midst of his vexation.", "페르디난드는 짜증이 난 와중에도 웃었다."], ["‘Confound them both!’ said he, looking at his watch.", "\"둘 다 빌어먹을!\" 그가 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["‘I want to get away.", "\"나는 자리를 뜨고 싶소."], ["Why the deuce can’t they come together!", "대체 어째서 둘이 한자리에 오지 못한단 말이오!"], ["They both know what they want and mean to do.", "둘 다 자기가 원하는 게 뭔지, 무얼 하려는지 알고 있건만."], ["Look at them!’", "저 꼴을 좀 보시오!\""]], [["They were still looming at opposite ends of the perspective, each with an absurd pretence of not having the other on his mind, which could not have been more transparently ridiculous though his real mind had been chalked on his back.", "그들은 여전히 원근법 풍경의 양 끝에서 어렴풋이 버티고 서서, 저마다 상대를 마음에 두지 않은 척 터무니없이 굴고 있었으니, 자기의 진짜 속마음이 등에 분필로 적혀 있었다 한들 그보다 더 빤히 우스꽝스러웠을 리 없었다."], ["Bishop, who had just now made a third with Bar and Ferdinand, but whose innocence had again cut him out of the subject and washed him in sweet oil, was seen to approach Lord Decimus and glide into conversation.", "방금 바와 페르디난드와 어울려 셋이 되었으나, 그 순진함이 다시 그를 화제에서 도려내어 향유로 씻어내 버린 비숍은, 데시머스 경에게 다가가 슬며시 대화 속으로 미끄러져 드는 것이 보였다."]], [["‘I must get Merdle’s doctor to catch and secure him, I suppose,’ said Ferdinand; ‘and then I must lay hold of my illustrious kinsman, and decoy him if I can--drag him if I can’t--to the conference.’", "\"머들의 주치의를 시켜 그를 붙잡아 묶어 두게 해야겠소,\" 페르디난드가 말했다. \"그러고 나서 내가 저 빛나는 친척 양반을 붙들어, 할 수 있으면 꾀어서 — 안 되면 끌고서라도 — 회담으로 데려가야지.\""]], [["‘Since you do me the honour,’ said Bar, with his slyest smile, to ask for my poor aid, it shall be yours with the greatest pleasure.", "\"이 보잘것없는 나의 도움을 청하는 영광을 베푸시니,\" 바가 그 가장 능청스러운 미소를 띠고 말했다."], ["I don’t think this is to be done by one man.", "\"더없이 기쁘게 그대 것이 되어 드리지요."], ["But if you will undertake to pen my lord into that furthest drawing-room where he is now so profoundly engaged, I will undertake to bring our dear Merdle into the presence, without the possibility of getting away.’", "이건 한 사람으로 될 일이 아닌 듯하오. 하지만 그대가 각하를 지금 그토록 깊이 빠져 있는 저 가장 안쪽 응접실에 가둬 두기로 한다면, 나는 우리 사랑하는 머들을, 빠져나갈 가망 없이 그 면전으로 데려오기로 하지요.\""]], [["‘Done!’ said Ferdinand.", "\"좋소!\" 페르디난드가 말했다."], ["‘Done!’ said Bar.", "\"좋소!\" 바가 말했다."]], [["Bar was a sight wondrous to behold, and full of matter, when, jauntily waving his double eye-glass by its ribbon, and jauntily drooping to an Universe of Jurymen, he, in the most accidental manner ever seen, found himself at Mr Merdle’s shoulder, and embraced that opportunity of mentioning a little point to him, on which he particularly wished to be guided by the light of his practical knowledge.", "이중 안경을 그 끈으로 멋들어지게 흔들고, 온 우주의 배심원들에게 멋들어지게 처진 표정을 지으며, 바가, 일찍이 본 적 없는 더없이 우연한 모양새로 머들 씨의 어깨 곁에 와 있는 모습은, 가히 볼 만한 진풍경이었고 또 속으로 꿍꿍이가 가득했다. 그러고는 그 기회를 빌려, 머들 씨의 실무 지식의 빛으로 특별히 인도받고 싶은 작은 문제 하나를 그에게 꺼냈다."], ["(Here he took Mr Merdle’s arm and walked him gently away.) A banker, whom we would call A.", "(여기서 그는 머들 씨의 팔을 붙들고 슬며시 데려갔다.) 우리가 A."], ["B., advanced a considerable sum of money, which we would call fifteen thousand pounds, to a client or customer of his, whom he would call P.", "B.라 부를 어느 은행가가, 우리가 P. Q.라 부를 자기 고객 혹은 거래처에게, 우리가 만오천 파운드라 부를 상당한 금액을 빌려주었다고 합시다."], ["Q. (Here, as they were getting towards Lord Decimus, he held Mr Merdle tight.) As a security for the repayment of this advance to P.", "(여기서, 그들이 데시머스 경 쪽으로 다가가고 있었으므로, 그는 머들 씨를 꽉 붙들었다.) 우리가 미망인이라 부를 그 P."], ["Q. whom we would call a widow lady, there were placed in A.", "Q.에게 이 대출금을 상환받기 위한 담보로, 우리가 블링키터 도들즈라 부를 어느 자유보유지의 권리증서가 A."], ["B.’s hands the title-deeds of a freehold estate, which we would call Blinkiter Doddles.", "B.의 손에 맡겨졌다고 합시다."], ["Now, the point was this.", "그런데, 요점은 이렇소."], ["A limited right of felling and lopping in the woods of Blinkiter Doddles, lay in the son of P.", "블링키터 도들즈의 숲에서 나무를 베고 가지를 칠 제한된 권리가, 그때 이미 성년을 넘긴, 우리가 X. Y.라 부를 P."], ["Q. then past his majority, and whom we would call X.", "Q.의 아들에게 있었는데 — 아니, 이건 정말 너무하군요!"], ["Y.--but really this was too bad! In the presence of Lord Decimus, to detain the host with chopping our dry chaff of law, was really too bad!", "데시머스 경이 계신 자리에서, 우리의 메마른 법률 검불이나 썰어 주인을 붙들어 두다니, 정말 너무합니다!"], ["Another time!", "다음 기회에!"], ["Bar was truly repentant, and would not say another syllable.", "바는 진심으로 뉘우치며, 다시는 한마디도 하지 않으리라 했다."], ["Would Bishop favour him with half-a-dozen words?", "비숍이 자기에게 대여섯 마디만 베풀어 주시겠소?"], ["(He had now set Mr Merdle down on a couch, side by side with Lord Decimus, and to it they must go, now or never.)", "(이제 그는 머들 씨를 데시머스 경과 나란히 긴 의자에 앉혀 놓았으니, 지금이 아니면 영영 안 될 일이라 둘은 본론으로 들어가야 했다.)"]], [["And now the rest of the company, highly excited and interested, always excepting Bishop, who had not the slightest idea that anything was going on, formed in one group round the fire in the next drawing-room, and pretended to be chatting easily on the infinite variety of small topics, while everybody’s thoughts and eyes were secretly straying towards the secluded pair.", "그리고 이제 나머지 좌중은, 무슨 일이 진행되고 있다는 것을 털끝만큼도 짐작하지 못한 비숍은 언제나처럼 예외로 하고, 몹시 들떠 흥미진진한 채로 옆 응접실 난로 둘레에 한 무리로 모여, 끝없이 다양한 자질구레한 화제로 스스럼없이 잡담하는 척했으나, 모두의 생각과 눈길은 은밀히 그 외딴 한 쌍을 향해 가고 있었다."], ["The Chorus were excessively nervous, perhaps as labouring under the dreadful apprehension that some good thing was going to be diverted from them!", "합창단은 지나치게 초조해했으니, 어쩌면 무슨 좋은 자리가 자기들에게서 빼돌려지려는 것이 아닌가 하는 무시무시한 불안에 시달리고 있었기 때문이리라!"], ["Bishop alone talked steadily and evenly.", "오직 비숍만이 꾸준하고 차분하게 이야기했다."], ["He conversed with the great Physician on that relaxation of the throat with which young curates were too frequently afflicted, and on the means of lessening the great prevalence of that disorder in the church.", "그는 그 위대한 주치의와 더불어, 젊은 부목사들이 너무도 빈번히 시달리는 그 목쉼 증세에 관해, 그리고 교회에 그토록 만연한 그 병을 줄이는 방도에 관해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Physician, as a general rule, was of opinion that the best way to avoid it was to know how to read, before you made a profession of reading.", "주치의는 일반론으로서, 그것을 피하는 가장 좋은 방법은 낭독을 직업으로 삼기 전에 낭독하는 법을 아는 것이라는 의견이었다."], ["Bishop said dubiously, did he really think so?", "비숍은 미심쩍은 듯, 정말 그렇게 생각하시오, 하고 물었다."], ["And Physician said, decidedly, yes he did.", "주치의는 단호히, 그렇소, 그렇게 생각하오, 하고 답했다."]], [["Ferdinand, meanwhile, was the only one of the party who skirmished on the outside of the circle; he kept about mid-way between it and the two, as if some sort of surgical operation were being performed by Lord Decimus on Mr Merdle, or by Mr Merdle on Lord Decimus, and his services might at any moment be required as Dresser. In fact, within a quarter of an hour Lord Decimus called to him ‘Ferdinand!’ and he went, and took his place in the conference for some five minutes more. Then a half-suppressed gasp broke out among the Chorus; for Lord Decimus rose to take his leave. Again coached up by Ferdinand to the point of making himself popular, he shook hands in the most brilliant manner with the whole company, and even said to Bar, ‘I hope you were not bored by my pears?’ To which Bar retorted, ‘Eton, my lord, or Parliamentary?’ neatly showing that he had mastered the joke, and delicately insinuating that he could never forget it while his life remained.", "한편 페르디난드는, 그 무리 바깥에서 어슬렁거리는 유일한 사람이었다. 그는 그 무리와 두 사람 사이의 한가운데쯤에 서서, 마치 데시머스 경이 머들 씨에게, 또는 머들 씨가 데시머스 경에게 무슨 외과 수술이라도 하고 있어, 자기 도움이 언제 보조의로 필요할지 모른다는 듯이 있었다. 과연 십오 분도 채 못 되어 데시머스 경이 그를 향해 \"페르디난드!\" 하고 불렀고, 그는 가서 다시 오 분쯤 그 회담에 자리를 차지했다. 그러자 합창단 사이로 반쯤 억눌린 헉 소리가 터져 나왔으니, 데시머스 경이 작별을 고하려 일어섰기 때문이었다. 또 한 번 페르디난드에게 인기를 끄는 데까지 미리 일러진 그는, 더없이 눈부신 솜씨로 좌중 모두와 악수를 나누었고, 심지어 바에게는 \"내 배 이야기에 지루하시지 않았소?\" 하고까지 말했다. 이에 바는 \"이튼의 것이었습니까, 각하, 아니면 의회의 것이었습니까?\" 하고 응수하여, 그 농담을 완벽히 익혔음을 깔끔하게 내보이며, 자기 목숨이 붙어 있는 한 결코 그것을 잊을 수 없으리라는 것을 은근히 내비쳤다."]], [["All the grave importance that was buttoned up in Mr Tite Barnacle, took itself away next; and Ferdinand took himself away next, to the opera.", "타이트 배너클 씨에게 단추로 꼭꼭 채워져 있던 그 온갖 점잖은 중요성이 다음으로 자리를 떴고, 그다음으로 페르디난드가 오페라를 보러 자리를 떴다."], ["Some of the rest lingered a little, marrying golden liqueur glasses to Buhl tables with sticky rings; on the desperate chance of Mr Merdle’s saying something.", "나머지 몇몇은 잠시 머무적거리며, 머들 씨가 무슨 말이라도 하지 않을까 하는 가망 없는 기대에, 황금빛 리큐어 잔을 끈적한 고리 자국으로 불 세공 탁자에 맞붙여 놓았다."], ["But Merdle, as usual, oozed sluggishly and muddily about his drawing-room, saying never a word.", "그러나 머들은 여느 때처럼 자기 응접실을 굼뜨고 흐릿하게 스며 돌아다닐 뿐, 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["In a day or two it was announced to all the town, that Edmund Sparkler, Esquire, son-in-law of the eminent Mr Merdle of worldwide renown, was made one of the Lords of the Circumlocution Office; and proclamation was issued, to all true believers, that this admirable appointment was to be hailed as a graceful and gracious mark of homage, rendered by the graceful and gracious Decimus, to that commercial interest which must ever in a great commercial country--and all the rest of it, with blast of trumpet.", "하루 이틀 사이에 온 도시에, 세계적 명성을 떨치는 저명한 머들 씨의 사위인 에드먼드 스파클러 귀하가 에두름국의 위원 가운데 하나로 임명되었다는 소식이 알려졌다. 그리고 모든 참된 신자에게, 이 훌륭한 임명은 우아하고 자비로운 데시머스가 그 상업적 이익에 — 위대한 상업 국가에서 마땅히 언제나 그러해야 할 — 그 어쩌고저쩌고에 바친 우아하고 자비로운 경의의 표시로 환영받아야 한다는 포고가, 나팔 소리와 함께 발해졌다."], ["So, bolstered by this mark of Government homage, the wonderful Bank and all the other wonderful undertakings went on and went up; and gapers came to Harley Street, Cavendish Square, only to look at the house where the golden wonder lived.", "그리하여 이 정부의 경의 표시에 떠받쳐져, 그 놀라운 은행과 그 밖의 온갖 놀라운 사업들은 계속되고 또 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그리고 구경꾼들은 캐번디시 광장 할리 가로 몰려와, 그저 그 황금의 경이가 사는 집을 구경하려 했다."]], [["And when they saw the Chief Butler looking out at the hall-door in his moments of condescension, the gapers said how rich he looked, and wondered how much money he had in the wonderful Bank.", "그리고 집사장이 너그러운 순간에 현관문 밖을 내다보는 것을 보면, 구경꾼들은 그가 얼마나 부유해 보이는지 모르겠다고 했고, 그가 그 놀라운 은행에 돈을 얼마나 넣어 두었을까 궁금해했다."], ["But, if they had known that respectable Nemesis better, they would not have wondered about it, and might have stated the amount with the utmost precision.", "그러나 그들이 그 존경스러운 응보의 여신을 좀 더 잘 알았더라면, 그것을 두고 궁금해하지 않았을 것이며, 그 액수를 더없이 정확하게 댈 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 13. The Progress of an Epidemic", "# CHAPTER 13. The Progress of an Epidemic"]], [["That it is at least as difficult to stay a moral infection as a physical one; that such a disease will spread with the malignity and rapidity of the Plague; that the contagion, when it has once made head, will spare no pursuit or condition, but will lay hold on people in the soundest health, and become developed in the most unlikely constitutions: is a fact as firmly established by experience as that we human creatures breathe an atmosphere.", "도덕적 감염을 막는 일이 신체적 감염을 막는 일 못지않게 적어도 어렵다는 것, 그러한 병이 흑사병의 악성과 신속함으로 퍼져 나가리라는 것, 그 전염이 일단 기세를 얻으면 어떤 직업도 신분도 가리지 않고, 가장 건강한 사람마저 붙들어 가장 있을 법하지 않은 체질에서까지 발현되리라는 것 — 이것은 우리 인간이라는 존재가 대기를 들이마신다는 사실만큼이나 경험으로 단단히 굳어진 사실이다."], ["A blessing beyond appreciation would be conferred upon mankind, if the tainted, in whose weakness or wickedness these virulent disorders are bred, could be instantly seized and placed in close confinement (not to say summarily smothered) before the poison is communicable.", "이 독성 강한 병이 그 약함이나 사악함 속에서 자라나는 오염된 자들을, 독이 옮겨지기 전에 즉각 붙잡아 엄중히 감금할 수 있다면(아예 즉결로 질식시킨다고는 하지 않겠다) 인류에게 헤아릴 수 없는 축복이 내려질 것이다."]], [["As a vast fire will fill the air to a great distance with its roar, so the sacred flame which the mighty Barnacles had fanned caused the air to resound more and more with the name of Merdle.", "거대한 불길이 멀리까지 그 포효로 공기를 가득 채우듯, 막강한 배너클 일가가 부채질한 그 신성한 불꽃은 공기를 점점 더 머들의 이름으로 울려 퍼지게 했다."], ["It was deposited on every lip, and carried into every ear.", "그 이름은 모든 입술에 얹혀 모든 귀로 실려 들어갔다."], ["There never was, there never had been, there never again should be, such a man as Mr Merdle.", "머들 씨 같은 사람은 일찍이 없었고, 있었던 적도 없었으며, 다시는 있지 않을 것이었다."], ["Nobody, as aforesaid, knew what he had done; but everybody knew him to be the greatest that had appeared.", "앞서 말했듯이 아무도 그가 무엇을 했는지는 몰랐다. 그러나 누구나 그가 여태껏 나타난 가장 위대한 인물임은 알았다."]], [["Down in Bleeding Heart Yard, where there was not one unappropriated halfpenny, as lively an interest was taken in this paragon of men as on the Stock Exchange.", "임자 없는 반 페니 한 닢조차 없는 저 아래 '피 흘리는 심장의 마당'에서도, 이 인간 중의 표본에 대한 관심은 증권 거래소에서만큼이나 생생했다."], ["Mrs Plornish, now established in the small grocery and general trade in a snug little shop at the crack end of the Yard, at the top of the steps, with her little old father and Maggy acting as assistants, habitually held forth about him over the counter in conversation with her customers.", "플로니시 부인은 이제 마당의 그 위쪽 끝, 계단 꼭대기에 자리한 아늑한 작은 가게에서 소규모 식료품 잡화상을 차려 놓고, 자그마한 노부친과 매기를 점원으로 부리며, 계산대 너머로 손님들과 대화를 나누면서 으레 그에 대해 떠벌리곤 했다."], ["Mr Plornish, who had a small share in a small builder’s business in the neighbourhood, said, trowel in hand, on the tops of scaffolds and on the tiles of houses, that people did tell him as Mr Merdle was _the_ one, mind you, to put us all to rights in respects of that which all on us looked to, and to bring us all safe home as much as we needed, mind you, fur toe be brought.", "인근의 작은 건축업체에 작은 지분을 가진 플로니시 씨는, 흙손을 쥐고 비계 꼭대기에서, 또 집의 기왓장 위에서 말하기를, 사람들이 자기한테 그러는데, 머들 씨야말로, 알겠수, 우리 모두가 바라는 그것에 관해서 우리를 죄다 바로잡아 줄 바로 그 사람이고, 우리 모두가 필요한 만큼, 알겠수, 우리를 죄다 무사히 데려다줄 그런 사람이라고 했다."], ["Mr Baptist, sole lodger of Mr and Mrs Plornish was reputed in whispers to lay by the savings which were the result of his simple and moderate life, for investment in one of Mr Merdle’s certain enterprises.", "플로니시 부부의 유일한 하숙인인 밥티스트 씨는, 소박하고 절제된 삶의 결실로 모은 저축을 머들 씨의 확실한 사업 가운데 하나에 투자하려고 따로 챙겨 둔다는 소문이 수군수군 돌았다."], ["The female Bleeding Hearts, when they came for ounces of tea, and hundredweights of talk, gave Mrs Plornish to understand, That how, ma’am, they had heard from their cousin Mary Anne, which worked in the line, that his lady’s dresses would fill three waggons.", "'피 흘리는 심장'의 여인들은 차 몇 온스와 수다 몇 백 무게를 사러 올 때면 플로니시 부인에게 이렇게 일러 주었다. 글쎄, 부인, 그쪽 일을 하는 사촌 메리 앤한테서 들었는데, 그 양반 부인의 드레스만 해도 짐마차 세 대를 채운다지 뭐예요."], ["That how she was as handsome a lady, ma’am, as lived, no matter wheres, and a busk like marble itself.", "그 부인이 어디 사는 사람이든 둘째가라면 서러울 미인이고, 가슴받이는 대리석 그 자체 같다지 뭐예요."], ["That how, according to what they was told, ma’am, it was her son by a former husband as was took into the Government; and a General he had been, and armies he had marched again and victory crowned, if all you heard was to be believed.", "그리고 들은 바로는, 부인, 정부에 들어간 건 그 부인이 전 남편한테서 낳은 아들이라는데, 그 아들은 장군이었고, 들은 게 다 사실이라면 군대를 거느리고 진군해서 승리로 영광을 얻었다지 뭐예요."], ["That how it was reported that Mr Merdle’s words had been, that if they could have made it worth his while to take the whole Government he would have took it without a profit, but that take it he could not and stand a loss.", "또 소문에 따르면 머들 씨가 한 말이, 자기한테 그럴 만한 값어치만 해 준다면 정부 전체를 떠맡되 이문 한 푼 안 남기고 떠맡을 텐데, 다만 떠맡고서 손해까지 보고 있을 수는 없다고 했다지 뭐예요."], ["That how it was not to be expected, ma’am, that he should lose by it, his ways being, as you might say and utter no falsehood, paved with gold; but that how it was much to be regretted that something handsome hadn’t been got up to make it worth his while; for it was such and only such that knowed the heighth to which the bread and butchers’ meat had rose, and it was such and only such that both could and would bring that heighth down.", "그리고 부인, 그분이 손해를 보길 바랄 수야 없는 노릇이지요, 그분이 가는 길은, 거짓 한마디 안 보태고 말하자면, 황금으로 깔려 있다니까요. 다만 그분에게 그럴 값어치를 만들어 줄 만큼 뭔가 번듯한 게 마련되지 못한 건 참으로 안타까운 일이에요, 빵값과 푸줏간 고깃값이 얼마나 치솟았는지 아는 건 오직 그런 분뿐이고, 그 값을 끌어내릴 수 있고 또 끌어내릴 분도 오직 그런 분뿐이니까요."]], [["So rife and potent was the fever in Bleeding Heart Yard, that Mr Pancks’s rent-days caused no interval in the patients.", "'피 흘리는 심장의 마당'에서 이 열병이 어찌나 만연하고 강력했던지, 팽크스 씨의 집세 받는 날조차 환자 발생을 멈추게 하지 못했다."], ["The disease took the singular form, on those occasions, of causing the infected to find an unfathomable excuse and consolation in allusions to the magic name.", "그런 날이면 병은 기이한 형태를 띠어, 감염된 자들로 하여금 그 마법 같은 이름에 빗대어 헤아릴 수 없는 핑계와 위안을 찾게 만들었다."]], [["‘Now, then!’ Mr Pancks would say, to a defaulting lodger.", "\"자, 어서!\" 팽크스 씨는 집세를 밀린 하숙인에게 말하곤 했다."], ["‘Pay up!", "\"내시오!"], ["Come on!’", "어서!\""]], [["‘I haven’t got it, Mr Pancks,’ Defaulter would reply.", "\"가진 게 없어요, 팽크스 씨.\" 체납자는 대답하곤 했다."], ["‘I tell you the truth, sir, when I say I haven’t got so much as a single sixpence of it to bless myself with.’", "\"사실대로 말씀드리는 겁니다, 나리. 내 한 몸 위로할 단돈 육 펜스도 없다니까요.\""]], [["‘This won’t do, you know,’ Mr Pancks would retort.", "\"이래선 안 되는 거, 알잖소.\" 팽크스 씨가 받아치곤 했다."], ["‘You don’t expect it _will_ do; do you?’", "\"이래도 된다고 생각하는 건 아니겠지, 응?\""]], [["Defaulter would admit, with a low-spirited ‘No, sir,’ having no such expectation.", "체납자는 풀 죽은 \"안 됩니다, 나리\" 한마디로, 그런 기대는 없다고 인정하곤 했다."]], [["‘My proprietor isn’t going to stand this, you know,’ Mr Pancks would proceed.", "\"우리 집주인이 이걸 그냥 둘 사람이 아니라는 거, 알잖소.\" 팽크스 씨는 이어 가곤 했다."], ["‘He don’t send me here for this.", "\"이러라고 날 여기 보낸 게 아니란 말이오."], ["Pay up!", "내시오!"], ["Come!’", "어서!\""]], [["The Defaulter would make answer, ‘Ah, Mr Pancks.", "체납자는 이렇게 대답하곤 했다. \"아아, 팽크스 씨."], ["If I was the rich gentleman whose name is in everybody’s mouth--if my name was Merdle, sir--I’d soon pay up, and be glad to do it.’", "내가 만약 누구나 입에 올리는 그 부자 양반이라면 — 내 이름이 머들이라면, 나리 — 당장 내고말고요, 기꺼이 그러겠어요.\""]], [["Dialogues on the rent-question usually took place at the house-doors or in the entries, and in the presence of several deeply interested Bleeding Hearts.", "집세 문제에 관한 이런 대화는 대개 집 문간이나 출입구에서, 깊은 관심을 가진 '피 흘리는 심장'들 여럿이 보는 앞에서 벌어졌다."], ["They always received a reference of this kind with a low murmur of response, as if it were convincing; and the Defaulter, however black and discomfited before, always cheered up a little in making it.", "그들은 이런 식의 비유가 나올 때면 늘 그것이 설득력 있다는 듯 낮은 웅성거림으로 호응했다. 그리고 체납자는 그 전까지 아무리 침울하고 풀이 죽어 있었어도, 그 말을 하는 동안만큼은 늘 조금 기운을 차렸다."]], [["‘If I was Mr Merdle, sir, you wouldn’t have cause to complain of me then.", "\"내가 만약 머들 씨라면, 나리, 당신은 나에 대해 불평할 일이 없을 거예요."], ["No, believe me!’ the Defaulter would proceed with a shake of the head.", "정말이라니까요!\" 체납자는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 이어 가곤 했다."], ["‘I’d pay up so quick then, Mr Pancks, that you shouldn’t have to ask me.’", "\"그러면 정말 후딱 내드릴 테니, 팽크스 씨, 당신이 달라고 청할 필요도 없을 거라고요.\""]], [["The response would be heard again here, implying that it was impossible to say anything fairer, and that this was the next thing to paying the money down.", "여기서 다시 호응이 들려오곤 했는데, 그보다 더 공정하게 말할 수는 없으며 이것이야말로 돈을 현장에서 내는 것 바로 다음가는 일이라는 뜻이었다."]], [["Mr Pancks would be now reduced to saying as he booked the case, ‘Well!", "이제 팽크스 씨는 그 건을 장부에 적으며 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없게 되곤 했다. \"좋소!"], ["You’ll have the broker in, and be turned out; that’s what’ll happen to you.", "당신은 압류 집행인을 맞아들이고 쫓겨나게 될 거요. 당신한테 닥칠 일이 그거란 말이오."], ["It’s no use talking to me about Mr Merdle.", "머들 씨 이야기를 나한테 해 봤자 소용없소."], ["You are not Mr Merdle, any more than I am.’", "당신은 머들 씨가 아니오, 내가 아닌 것이나 마찬가지지.\""]], [["‘No, sir,’ the Defaulter would reply.", "\"맞아요, 나리.\" 체납자는 대답하곤 했다."], ["‘I only wish you _were_ him, sir.’", "\"그저 나리가 그분이었으면 싶을 뿐이라고요.\""]], [["The response would take this up quickly; replying with great feeling, ‘Only wish you _were_ him, sir.’", "여기서 호응이 재빨리 그 말을 받아, 깊은 감정을 담아 이렇게 답하곤 했다. \"그저 나리가 그분이었으면 싶을 뿐이라고요.\""]], [["‘You’d be easier with us if you were Mr Merdle, sir,’ the Defaulter would go on with rising spirits, ‘and it would be better for all parties.", "\"나리가 만약 머들 씨라면 우리한테 더 너그러우실 거예요, 나리.\" 체납자는 기운이 솟아 이어 가곤 했다."], ["Better for our sakes, and better for yours, too.", "\"그러면 모두한테 더 좋을 거고요. 우리한테도 좋고, 나리한테도 좋고요."], ["You wouldn’t have to worry no one, then, sir.", "그러면 나리는 아무도 들들 볶을 필요가 없을 거예요."], ["You wouldn’t have to worry us, and you wouldn’t have to worry yourself.", "우리를 볶을 필요도 없고, 나리 자신을 볶을 필요도 없겠죠."], ["You’d be easier in your own mind, sir, and you’d leave others easier, too, you would, if you were Mr Merdle.’", "마음이 더 편하실 거고, 다른 사람들도 더 편하게 두실 거예요, 나리가 머들 씨라면 정말 그러실 거라고요.\""]], [["Mr Pancks, in whom these impersonal compliments produced an irresistible sheepishness, never rallied after such a charge.", "이런 사람을 빗댄 칭찬이 팽크스 씨에게는 떨쳐 낼 수 없는 머쓱함을 자아냈던지라, 그는 그런 공격을 받고 나면 결코 기를 회복하지 못했다."], ["He could only bite his nails and puff away to the next Defaulter.", "그저 손톱을 깨물며 씩씩거리고는 다음 체납자에게로 옮겨 가는 수밖에 없었다."], ["The responsive Bleeding Hearts would then gather round the Defaulter whom he had just abandoned, and the most extravagant rumours would circulate among them, to their great comfort, touching the amount of Mr Merdle’s ready money.", "그러면 호응하던 '피 흘리는 심장'들이 그가 방금 포기하고 간 체납자 주위로 모여들었고, 그들 사이에서는 머들 씨의 현금이 얼마나 되는지에 관해 더없이 터무니없는 소문들이 떠돌며 큰 위안을 안겨 주곤 했다."]], [["From one of the many such defeats of one of many rent-days, Mr Pancks, having finished his day’s collection, repaired with his note-book under his arm to Mrs Plornish’s corner.", "수많은 집세 받는 날 가운데 어느 하루, 수많은 그런 패배 중 하나를 맛본 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 그날의 수금을 마치고 수첩을 옆구리에 낀 채 플로니시 부인의 모퉁이로 향했다."], ["Mr Pancks’s object was not professional, but social.", "팽크스 씨의 목적은 일이 아니라 친목이었다."], ["He had had a trying day, and wanted a little brightening.", "그는 고된 하루를 보낸 터라 기분을 좀 밝게 풀고 싶었다."], ["By this time he was on friendly terms with the Plornish family, having often looked in upon them at similar seasons, and borne his part in recollections of Miss Dorrit.", "이즈음 그는 플로니시 가족과 친한 사이가 되어 있었으니, 비슷한 때에 자주 그들을 들여다보며 도릿 양에 관한 추억담에 한몫을 거들곤 했기 때문이다."]], [["Mrs Plornish’s shop-parlour had been decorated under her own eye, and presented, on the side towards the shop, a little fiction in which Mrs Plornish unspeakably rejoiced.", "플로니시 부인의 가게 안방은 그녀 자신의 안목 아래 꾸며진 것으로, 가게 쪽을 향한 면에는 그녀가 이루 말할 수 없이 기뻐하는 작은 허구 하나가 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["This poetical heightening of the parlour consisted in the wall being painted to represent the exterior of a thatched cottage; the artist having introduced (in as effective a manner as he found compatible with their highly disproportionate dimensions) the real door and window.", "안방을 이렇게 시적으로 한껏 돋운 것은, 벽을 초가집의 외관으로 그려 놓은 데 있었다. 화가는 (그것들의 심히 어긋난 크기와 양립할 수 있다고 본 한도 내에서 최대한 효과적으로) 실제 문과 창문을 그림 안에 끌어들여 놓았다."], ["The modest sunflower and hollyhock were depicted as flourishing with great luxuriance on this rustic dwelling, while a quantity of dense smoke issuing from the chimney indicated good cheer within, and also, perhaps, that it had not been lately swept.", "소박한 해바라기와 접시꽃이 이 시골집 위에서 무성하게 만발한 것으로 그려졌고, 굴뚝에서 짙은 연기가 잔뜩 피어오르는 것은 안에서의 단란한 정경을 가리켰으며, 어쩌면 굴뚝을 근래 청소하지 않았음을 가리키기도 했다."], ["A faithful dog was represented as flying at the legs of the friendly visitor, from the threshold; and a circular pigeon-house, enveloped in a cloud of pigeons, arose from behind the garden-paling.", "충직한 개 한 마리가 문지방에서 다정한 방문객의 다리를 향해 달려드는 것으로 그려졌고, 비둘기 떼 구름에 감싸인 둥근 비둘기장이 정원 울타리 뒤에서 솟아 있었다."], ["On the door (when it was shut), appeared the semblance of a brass-plate, presenting the inscription, Happy Cottage, T.", "문에는 (그 문이 닫혀 있을 때) 놋쇠 명패 비슷한 것이 나타났는데, 거기에는 '행복한 오두막, T."], ["and M.", "& M."], ["Plornish; the partnership expressing man and wife.", "플로니시'라는 글귀가 적혀 있었으니, 그 동업 관계는 부부를 뜻했다."], ["No Poetry and no Art ever charmed the imagination more than the union of the two in this counterfeit cottage charmed Mrs Plornish.", "그 어떤 시도, 그 어떤 예술도 이 가짜 오두막 안에서 둘이 하나로 어우러진 모습이 플로니시 부인의 상상을 매혹한 것만큼 누군가의 상상을 매혹한 적은 없었다."], ["It was nothing to her that Plornish had a habit of leaning against it as he smoked his pipe after work, when his hat blotted out the pigeon-house and all the pigeons, when his back swallowed up the dwelling, when his hands in his pockets uprooted the blooming garden and laid waste the adjacent country.", "플로니시가 일을 마친 뒤 파이프 담배를 피우며 그 벽에 기대는 버릇이 있어서, 그의 모자가 비둘기장과 비둘기를 죄다 지워 버리고, 그의 등이 그 집을 통째로 삼켜 버리며, 주머니에 찔러 넣은 그의 두 손이 만발한 정원을 뿌리째 뽑고 인근 들판을 황폐하게 만든다 한들, 그녀에게는 아무래도 상관없었다."], ["To Mrs Plornish, it was still a most beautiful cottage, a most wonderful deception; and it made no difference that Mr Plornish’s eye was some inches above the level of the gable bed-room in the thatch.", "플로니시 부인에게 그것은 여전히 더없이 아름다운 오두막이요, 더없이 놀라운 눈속임이었다. 그리고 플로니시 씨의 눈이 초가지붕 박공 침실보다 몇 인치 위에 있다 한들 아무 차이가 없었다."], ["To come out into the shop after it was shut, and hear her father sing a song inside this cottage, was a perfect Pastoral to Mrs Plornish, the Golden Age revived.", "가게 문을 닫은 뒤 가게로 나와, 이 오두막 안에서 아버지가 노래 부르는 것을 듣는 일은 플로니시 부인에게 완벽한 전원시였으니, 황금시대가 되살아난 것이었다."], ["And truly if that famous period had been revived, or had ever been at all, it may be doubted whether it would have produced many more heartily admiring daughters than the poor woman.", "그리고 정말로 그 유명한 시대가 되살아났거나 애당초 존재한 적이 있었다 한들, 그 시대가 이 가난한 여인보다 더 진심으로 흠모하는 딸들을 그리 많이 낳았을지는 의심스러운 일이다."]], [["Warned of a visitor by the tinkling bell at the shop-door, Mrs Plornish came out of Happy Cottage to see who it might be.", "가게 문에 달린 종이 딸랑거려 손님이 왔음을 알리자, 플로니시 부인은 누구인지 보려고 '행복한 오두막'에서 나왔다."], ["‘I guessed it was you, Mr Pancks,’ said she, ‘for it’s quite your regular night; ain’t it?", "\"팽크스 씨일 거라고 짐작했어요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Here’s father, you see, come out to serve at the sound of the bell, like a brisk young shopman.", "\"딱 당신이 오는 날이잖아요, 그렇죠? 보세요, 아버지가 종소리에 손님 맞으러 나오셨어요, 부지런한 젊은 점원처럼요."], ["Ain’t he looking well?", "정정해 보이시죠?"], ["Father’s more pleased to see you than if you was a customer, for he dearly loves a gossip; and when it turns upon Miss Dorrit, he loves it all the more.", "아버지는 당신을 손님으로 맞는 것보다 더 반가워하세요, 수다 떠는 걸 무척 좋아하시거든요. 그리고 이야기가 도릿 양 쪽으로 흐르면 더더욱 좋아하시고요."], ["You never heard father in such voice as he is at present,’ said Mrs Plornish, her own voice quavering, she was so proud and pleased.", "요즘처럼 아버지 목소리가 좋을 때를 들어 보신 적 없을 거예요.\" 플로니시 부인은 자랑스럽고 흐뭇한 나머지 제 목소리마저 떨렸다."], ["‘He gave us Strephon last night to that degree that Plornish gets up and makes him this speech across the table.", "\"어젯밤엔 스트레폰을 어찌나 잘 불러 주셨는지, 플로니시가 자리에서 일어나 식탁 너머로 이런 말을 다 했다니까요."], ["“John Edward Nandy,”", "'존 에드워드 낸디.'"], ["says Plornish to father, “I never heard you come the warbles as I have heard you come the warbles this night.”", "플로니시가 아버지한테 그러더라고요. '제가 오늘 밤 당신 트릴 노래를 들은 것만큼 트릴 노래를 잘 부르시는 걸 들어 본 적이 없어요.'"], ["An’t it gratifying, Mr Pancks, though; really?’", "정말 흐뭇한 일 아니에요, 팽크스 씨, 안 그래요?\""]], [["Mr Pancks, who had snorted at the old man in his friendliest manner, replied in the affirmative, and casually asked whether that lively Altro chap had come in yet?", "팽크스 씨는 더없이 다정한 태도로 노인을 향해 콧방귀를 한번 뀐 뒤 그렇다고 대답하고는, 그 활기찬 알트로 녀석이 아직 안 들어왔느냐고 무심코 물었다."], ["Mrs Plornish answered no, not yet, though he had gone to the West-End with some work, and had said he should be back by tea-time.", "플로니시 부인은 아니라고, 아직이라고, 일거리를 들고 웨스트엔드에 갔는데 차 마실 시간쯤이면 돌아오겠다고 했더라고 답했다."], ["Mr Pancks was then hospitably pressed into Happy Cottage, where he encountered the elder Master Plornish just come home from school.", "그러자 팽크스 씨는 융숭한 권유에 밀려 '행복한 오두막'으로 들어갔고, 거기서 학교에서 막 돌아온 큰 플로니시 도련님과 마주쳤다."], ["Examining that young student, lightly, on the educational proceedings of the day, he found that the more advanced pupils who were in the large text and the letter M, had been set the copy ‘Merdle, Millions.’", "그는 그 어린 학생에게 그날의 학업 진척에 대해 가볍게 물어보다가, 큰 글씨와 알파벳 M을 배우는 윗반 학생들에게 '머들, 백만장자'라는 베껴 쓰기 본보기가 주어졌음을 알게 되었다."]], [["‘And how are _you_ getting on, Mrs Plornish,’ said Pancks, ‘since we’re mentioning millions?’", "\"그래, 부인은 어떻게 지내십니까.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"백만장자 얘기가 나온 김에 말입니다.\""]], [["‘Very steady, indeed, sir,’ returned Mrs Plornish.", "\"아주 꾸준하답니다, 정말로요, 나리.\" 플로니시 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘Father, dear, would you go into the shop and tidy the window a little bit before tea, your taste being so beautiful?’", "\"아버지, 저, 차 마시기 전에 가게에 가서 진열창을 좀 정돈해 주시겠어요? 아버지 안목이 워낙 빼어나시잖아요.\""]], [["John Edward Nandy trotted away, much gratified, to comply with his daughter’s request.", "존 에드워드 낸디는 딸의 부탁을 들어주려고 흐뭇해하며 종종걸음으로 갔다."], ["Mrs Plornish, who was always in mortal terror of mentioning pecuniary affairs before the old gentleman, lest any disclosure she made might rouse his spirit and induce him to run away to the workhouse, was thus left free to be confidential with Mr Pancks.", "플로니시 부인은 노인 앞에서 금전 문제를 입에 올리는 것을 늘 죽기보다 두려워했으니, 자기가 무슨 말이라도 흘렸다가 노인의 자존심을 건드려 구빈원으로 달아나게 만들까 봐서였다. 이렇게 해서 그녀는 자유로이 팽크스 씨와 은밀한 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 되었다."]], [["‘It’s quite true that the business is very steady indeed,’ said Mrs Plornish, lowering her voice; ‘and has a excellent connection.", "\"장사가 정말이지 아주 꾸준한 건 사실이에요.\" 플로니시 부인이 목소리를 낮추며 말했다."], ["The only thing that stands in its way, sir, is the Credit.’", "\"거래처도 훌륭하고요. 단 한 가지 걸림돌은요, 나리, 바로 외상이에요.\""]], [["This drawback, rather severely felt by most people who engaged in commercial transactions with the inhabitants of Bleeding Heart Yard, was a large stumbling-block in Mrs Plornish’s trade.", "이 결함은, '피 흘리는 심장의 마당' 주민들과 상거래에 발을 들인 대부분의 사람들이 꽤나 호되게 느끼는 것으로, 플로니시 부인의 장사에도 큰 걸림돌이었다."], ["When Mr Dorrit had established her in the business, the Bleeding Hearts had shown an amount of emotion and a determination to support her in it, that did honour to human nature.", "도릿 씨가 그녀에게 이 장사를 차려 주었을 때, '피 흘리는 심장'들은 인간 본성에 영광을 돌릴 만한 감격과, 그녀를 떠받쳐 주겠다는 결의를 보였다. 그들의 공동체에 오래 몸담아 온 일원으로서 그들의 너그러운 정에 호소할 자격이 있음을 인정하여, 그들은 깊은 감정에 차서 무슨 일이 있어도 플로니시 부인하고만 거래하고 다른 어떤 가게에도 단골이 되어 주지 않겠노라 맹세했다."], ["Recognising her claim upon their generous feelings as one who had long been a member of their community, they pledged themselves, with great feeling, to deal with Mrs Plornish, come what would and bestow their patronage on no other establishment.", "이 고귀한 정서에 감화된 나머지, 그들은 평소 익숙하지 않던 식료품이나 버터 같은 자그마한 사치품까지 일부러 사들이기도 했다. 서로에게 이르기를, 비록 좀 무리하더라도 그게 이웃이자 친구를 위해서가 아니냐, 이런 사람을 위해서가 아니라면 누구를 위해 무리를 하겠느냐고 했다."], ["Influenced by these noble sentiments, they had even gone out of their way to purchase little luxuries in the grocery and butter line to which they were unaccustomed; saying to one another, that if they did stretch a point, was it not for a neighbour and a friend, and for whom ought a point to be stretched if not for such?", "이렇게 부추겨진 덕에 장사는 대단히 활기를 띠었고, 재고품은 더없이 빠르게 빠져나갔다. 요컨대, '피 흘리는 심장'들이 돈을 내기만 했다면 이 사업은 완전한 성공을 거두었을 것이다."], ["So stimulated, the business was extremely brisk, and the articles in stock went off with the greatest celerity. In short, if the", "그러나 그들이 외상 지는 일에만 오로지 매달린 탓에, 실제로 실현된 이윤은 아직 장부에 모습을 드러내기 시작조차 하지 않았다."]], [["Bleeding Hearts had but paid, the undertaking would have been a complete success; whereas, by reason of their exclusively confining themselves to owing, the profits actually realised had not yet begun to appear in the books.", "팽크스 씨가 이 회계 상태를 곰곰이 들여다보며 머리카락을 곤두세워 영락없는 호저 꼴이 되어 가고 있을 때, 노인 낸디 씨가 수상쩍은 기색으로 오두막에 다시 들어와, 밥티스트 씨의 이상한 거동을 와서 좀 보라고 그들에게 청했다."], ["Mr Pancks was making a very porcupine of himself by sticking his hair up in the contemplation of this state of accounts, when old Mr Nandy, re-entering the cottage with an air of mystery, entreated them to come and look at the strange behaviour of Mr Baptist, who seemed to have met with something that had scared him.", "무언가 그를 겁먹게 한 일을 만난 것 같다는 것이었다. 셋이 모두 가게로 들어가 창문 너머로 지켜보니, 창백하고 동요한 밥티스트 씨가 다음과 같은 기이한 행동을 펼치고 있었다."], ["All three going into the shop, and watching through the window, then saw Mr Baptist, pale and agitated, go through the following extraordinary performances.", "먼저 그는 마당으로 내려가는 계단 꼭대기에 몸을 숨긴 채, 가게 문 옆에 바짝 붙어 조심스레 고개를 내밀고는 거리를 위아래로 살피는 모습이었다."], ["First, he was observed hiding at the top of the steps leading down into the Yard, and peeping up and down the street with his head cautiously thrust out close to the side of the shop-door.", "무척이나 불안하게 살핀 끝에 그는 은신처에서 나와, 아주 떠나가기라도 하는 듯 거리를 따라 잰걸음으로 내려갔다."], ["After very anxious scrutiny, he came out of his retreat, and went briskly down the street as if he were going away altogether; then, suddenly turned about, and went, at the same pace, and with the same feint, up the street.", "그러다 갑자기 돌아서서는, 똑같은 걸음으로, 똑같은 위장을 하며 거리를 따라 올라갔다. 그는 내려갔던 만큼만 올라가고는 길을 가로질러 사라졌다."], ["He had gone no further up the street than he had gone down, when he crossed the road and disappeared. The object of this last manoeuvre was only apparent, when his entering the shop with a sudden twist, from the steps again, explained that he had made a wide and obscure circuit round to the other, or Doyce and Clennam, end of the Yard, and had come through the Yard and bolted in.", "이 마지막 술책의 목적은, 그가 계단 쪽에서 다시 불쑥 몸을 비틀며 가게로 들어왔을 때에야 비로소 드러났으니, 그가 마당의 반대쪽 끝, 곧 도이스앤클레넘 끝까지 멀고 은밀하게 빙 돌아, 마당을 가로질러 안으로 뛰어들었다는 것이었다."], ["He was out of breath by that time, as he might well be, and his heart seemed to jerk faster than the little shop-bell, as it quivered and jingled behind him with his hasty shutting of the door.", "그때쯤 그는 그럴 만도 하게 숨이 차 있었고, 그가 황급히 문을 닫는 바람에 뒤에서 떨리며 딸랑거리는 작은 가게 종보다도 그의 심장이 더 빠르게 두근거리는 듯했다."]], [["‘Hallo, old chap!’ said Mr Pancks.", "\"이봐, 친구!\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Altro, old boy!", "\"알트로, 이 사람아!"], ["What’s the matter?’", "무슨 일인가?\""]], [["Mr Baptist, or Signor Cavalletto, understood English now almost as well as Mr Pancks himself, and could speak it very well too.", "밥티스트 씨, 곧 카발레토 씨는 이제 영어를 팽크스 씨 자신만큼이나 잘 알아들었고, 말도 아주 잘했다."], ["Nevertheless, Mrs Plornish, with a pardonable vanity in that accomplishment of hers which made her all but Italian, stepped in as interpreter.", "그럼에도 플로니시 부인은 자신을 거의 이탈리아 사람으로 만들어 주는 그 재주에 대한 가히 용서받을 만한 허영심에서, 통역으로 나섰다."]], [["‘E ask know,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘what go wrong?’", "\"이이가 알고 싶대요.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"무어가 잘모대써?\""]], [["‘Come into the happy little cottage, Padrona,’ returned Mr Baptist, imparting great stealthiness to his flurried back-handed shake of his right forefinger.", "\"행복한 작은 오두막으로 들어갑시다, 파드로나.\" 밥티스트 씨가 당황하여 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 쪽으로 흔들면서, 그 동작에 큰 은밀함을 담아 대답했다."], ["‘Come there!’", "\"저기로요!\""]], [["Mrs Plornish was proud of the title Padrona, which she regarded as signifying: not so much Mistress of the house, as Mistress of the Italian tongue.", "플로니시 부인은 파드로나라는 호칭이 자랑스러웠으니, 그것이 집의 안주인이라기보다 이탈리아 말의 안주인을 뜻한다고 여겼던 것이다."], ["She immediately complied with Mr Baptist’s request, and they all went into the cottage.", "그녀는 즉시 밥티스트 씨의 청을 따랐고, 모두 오두막으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘E ope you no fright,’ said Mrs Plornish then, interpreting Mr Pancks in a new way with her usual fertility of resource.", "\"이이가 바라기를 다미 안 노라긔 바래요.\" 그제야 플로니시 부인이 늘 그렇듯 풍부한 임기응변으로 팽크스를 새로운 방식으로 통역하며 말했다."], ["‘What appen?", "\"무순 이리야?"], ["Peaka Padrona!’", "마래봐 파드로나!\""]], [["‘I have seen some one,’ returned Baptist.", "\"누군가를 봤어요.\" 밥티스트가 대답했다."], ["‘I have rincontrato him.’", "\"그 사람을 린콘트라토했어요.\""]], [["‘Im?", "\"그 사람?"], ["Oo him?’ asked Mrs Plornish.", "누구요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘A bad man.", "\"나쁜 사람."], ["A baddest man.", "제일 나쁜 사람."], ["I have hoped that I should never see him again.’", "다시는 그를 안 보길 바랐는데요.\""]], [["‘Ow you know him bad?’ asked Mrs Plornish.", "\"그 사라미 나뿐 거 어떠케 아라요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘It does not matter, Padrona.", "\"그건 중요하지 않아요, 파드로나."], ["I know it too well.’", "너무도 잘 알아요.\""]], [["‘E see you?’ asked Mrs Plornish.", "\"그 사라미 다실 봐써요?\" 플로니시 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘No.", "\"아니요."], ["I hope not.", "안 봤기를 바라요."], ["I believe not.’", "안 봤다고 믿어요.\""]], [["‘He says,’ Mrs Plornish then interpreted, addressing her father and Pancks with mild condescension, ‘that he has met a bad man, but he hopes the bad man didn’t see him--Why,’ inquired Mrs Plornish, reverting to the Italian language, ‘why ope bad man no see?’", "\"이이 말이,\" 그러자 플로니시 부인이 온화한 겸양으로 아버지와 팽크스를 향해 통역했다. \"나쁜 사람을 만났는데, 그 나쁜 사람이 자기를 못 봤기를 바란대요. 그런데 — 어째서,\" 플로니시 부인이 이탈리아 말로 되돌아가 물었다. \"어째서 나뿐 사라미 안 보기 바래요?\""]], [["‘Padrona, dearest,’ returned the little foreigner whom she so considerately protected, ‘do not ask, I pray.", "\"파드로나, 사랑하는 분.\" 그녀가 그토록 다정하게 보호해 주는 그 작은 외국인이 대답했다."], ["Once again I say it matters not.", "\"묻지 말아 주세요, 부탁이에요."], ["I have fear of this man.", "다시 한번 말하지만 그건 중요하지 않아요."], ["I do not wish to see him, I do not wish to be known of him--never again!", "나는 이 사람이 두려워요. 그를 보고 싶지 않고, 그에게 알려지고 싶지 않아요 — 다시는요!"], ["Enough, most beautiful.", "그걸로 충분해요, 더없이 아름다운 분."], ["Leave it.’", "그냥 두세요.\""]], [["The topic was so disagreeable to him, and so put his usual liveliness to the rout, that Mrs Plornish forbore to press him further: the rather as the tea had been drawing for some time on the hob.", "이 화제가 그에게 어찌나 거북했던지, 또 그의 평소 활기를 어찌나 무너뜨렸던지, 플로니시 부인은 더 캐묻기를 삼갔다. 차가 화덕 선반 위에서 한참 우러나고 있던 터라 더욱 그러했다."], ["But she was not the less surprised and curious for asking no more questions; neither was Mr Pancks, whose expressive breathing had been labouring hard since the entrance of the little man, like a locomotive engine with a great load getting up a steep incline.", "그러나 더 묻지 않았다고 해서 놀라움과 호기심이 덜한 것은 아니었으며, 팽크스 씨도 마찬가지였으니, 그 작은 사내가 들어온 뒤로 그의 표정 풍부한 숨소리는, 무거운 짐을 끌고 가파른 비탈을 오르는 기관차처럼 힘겹게 헐떡이고 있었다."], ["Maggy, now better dressed than of yore, though still faithful to the monstrous character of her cap, had been in the background from the first with open mouth and eyes, which staring and gaping features were not diminished in breadth by the untimely suppression of the subject.", "매기는 이제 예전보다 옷차림이 나아졌으되 여전히 그 괴상한 모자의 됨됨이에는 충실했는데, 처음부터 입을 떡 벌리고 눈을 휘둥그렇게 뜬 채 뒷전에 있었으니, 화제가 때아니게 억눌렸다고 해서 그 멍하니 벌어진 이목구비의 너비가 줄어들지는 않았다."], ["However, no more was said about it, though much appeared to be thought on all sides: by no means excepting the two young Plornishes, who partook of the evening meal as if their eating the bread and butter were rendered almost superfluous by the painful probability of the worst of men shortly presenting himself for the purpose of eating them.", "어쨌든 그 일에 관해 더 이상 아무 말도 나오지 않았으나, 모두가 속으로는 많은 생각을 하는 듯했다. 두 어린 플로니시도 결코 예외가 아니었으니, 그들은 마치 사람 중에 가장 못된 자가 곧 그들을 잡아먹으려고 들이닥치리라는 괴로운 가능성 탓에 빵과 버터를 먹는 일이 거의 부질없게 된 것처럼 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Mr Baptist, by degrees began to chirp a little; but never stirred from the seat he had taken behind the door and close to the window, though it was not his usual place.", "밥티스트 씨는 차츰 조금씩 재잘거리기 시작했으나, 평소 자기 자리도 아니건만 문 뒤, 창문 가까이 잡은 자리에서는 결코 움직이지 않았다."], ["As often as the little bell rang, he started and peeped out secretly, with the end of the little curtain in his hand and the rest before his face; evidently not at all satisfied but that the man he dreaded had tracked him through all his doublings and turnings, with the certainty of a terrible bloodhound.", "작은 종이 울릴 때마다 그는 흠칫 놀라, 작은 커튼 자락을 손에 쥐고 나머지로 얼굴을 가린 채 몰래 밖을 엿보았으니, 자기가 두려워하는 그 사람이 무서운 사냥개의 확실함으로 자기의 온갖 우회와 방향 전환을 죄다 추적해 왔으리라는 의심을 도무지 떨치지 못하는 게 분명했다."]], [["The entrance, at various times, of two or three customers and of Mr Plornish, gave Mr Baptist just enough of this employment to keep the attention of the company fixed upon him.", "이따금 손님 두셋과 플로니시 씨가 들어오는 통에, 밥티스트 씨는 꼭 그만큼 이 일에 매달려 좌중의 주의를 자기에게 붙들어 두기에 충분했다."], ["Tea was over, and the children were abed, and Mrs Plornish was feeling her way to the dutiful proposal that her father should favour them with Chloe, when the bell rang again, and Mr Clennam came in.", "차도 다 끝나고 아이들도 잠자리에 든 뒤, 플로니시 부인이 아버지더러 '클로이'를 한 곡 들려주시는 게 자식 된 도리로 마땅하지 않겠느냐는 제안을 슬슬 꺼내려던 참에, 종이 다시 울리며 클레넘 씨가 들어왔다."]], [["Clennam had been poring late over his books and letters; for the waiting-rooms of the Circumlocution Office ravaged his time sorely.", "클레넘은 늦도록 장부와 편지를 들여다보고 있었으니, 에두름국의 대기실들이 그의 시간을 모질게 갉아먹은 탓이었다."], ["Over and above that, he was depressed and made uneasy by the late occurrence at his mother’s.", "그뿐 아니라 그는 근래 어머니 댁에서 있었던 일로 침울하고 불안한 상태였다."], ["He looked worn and solitary.", "그는 지치고 외로워 보였다."], ["He felt so, too; but, nevertheless, was returning home from his counting-house by that end of the Yard to give them the intelligence that he had received another letter from Miss Dorrit.", "실제로 그렇게 느끼기도 했다. 그럼에도 그는 도릿 양에게서 또 한 통의 편지를 받았다는 소식을 그들에게 전하려고, 사무소에서 집으로 돌아가는 길에 마당의 그 끝을 택해 들른 것이었다."]], [["The news made a sensation in the cottage which drew off the general attention from Mr Baptist.", "그 소식은 오두막에 일대 파문을 일으켜 좌중의 관심을 밥티스트 씨에게서 거두어 갔다."], ["Maggy, who pushed her way into the foreground immediately, would have seemed to draw in the tidings of her Little Mother equally at her ears, nose, mouth, and eyes, but that the last were obstructed by tears.", "즉시 앞쪽으로 비집고 나온 매기는, 마치 제 작은 어머니의 소식을 귀로, 코로, 입으로, 눈으로 한꺼번에 빨아들이는 듯 보였을 테지만, 마지막 것은 눈물에 가로막혀 있었다."], ["She was particularly delighted when Clennam assured her that there were hospitals, and very kindly conducted hospitals, in Rome.", "로마에도 병원이, 그것도 무척 친절하게 운영되는 병원이 있다고 클레넘이 그녀를 안심시키자, 그녀는 유난히 기뻐했다."], ["Mr Pancks rose into new distinction in virtue of being specially remembered in the letter.", "팽크스 씨는 편지에 특별히 그 이름이 기억되어 있는 덕에 새로운 명예에 올랐다."], ["Everybody was pleased and interested, and Clennam was well repaid for his trouble.", "모두가 기뻐하고 흥미로워했으니, 클레넘은 수고에 대한 충분한 보답을 받은 셈이었다."]], [["‘But you are tired, sir. Let me make you a cup of tea,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘if you’d condescend to take such a thing in the cottage; and many thanks to you, too, I am sure, for bearing us in mind so kindly.’", "\"하지만 지치셨네요, 나리. 제가 차 한 잔 타 드릴게요.\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다. \"이 오두막에서 그런 걸 드시는 걸 마다하지 않으신다면요. 그리고 저희를 이렇게 다정하게 마음에 담아 주셔서 정말 감사드려요.\""]], [["Mr Plornish deeming it incumbent on him, as host, to add his personal acknowledgments, tendered them in the form which always expressed his highest ideal of a combination of ceremony with sincerity.", "플로니시 씨는 집주인으로서 자기 나름의 사례를 보태는 것이 도리라 여겨, 격식과 진심의 결합에 관한 자신의 최고 이상을 늘 표현해 온 그 형식으로 사례를 올렸다."]], [["‘John Edward Nandy,’ said Mr Plornish, addressing the old gentleman.", "\"존 에드워드 낸디.\" 플로니시 씨가 노신사를 향해 말했다."], ["‘Sir.", "\"나리."], ["It’s not too often that you see unpretending actions without a spark of pride, and therefore when you see them give grateful honour unto the same, being that if you don’t, and live to want ‘em, it follows serve you right.’", "거만함이라곤 티끌만큼도 없는, 꾸밈없는 행동을 보는 일이 그리 흔치가 않으니, 그러므로 그런 걸 보거든 거기에 감사한 영예를 돌려야 하는 법이지요. 안 그러고서 그런 걸 아쉬워할 때까지 살게 된다면, 그건 자업자득이 따르는 거니까요.\""]], [["To which Mr Nandy replied:", "이에 낸디 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘I am heartily of your opinion, Thomas, and which your opinion is the same as mine, and therefore no more words and not being backwards with that opinion, which opinion giving it as yes, Thomas, yes, is the opinion in which yourself and me must ever be unanimously jined by all, and where there is not difference of opinion there can be none but one opinion, which fully no, Thomas, Thomas, no!’", "\"진심으로 자네 의견에 동감일세, 토머스, 그리고 자네 의견이 곧 내 의견인즉, 그러므로 더 말이 필요 없고, 그 의견을 내놓기를 주저하지 않으니, 그 의견을 그렇다고 내놓는바, 토머스, 그렇다고 내놓는바, 그건 자네와 나가 모든 이와 더불어 언제나 만장일치로 한데 묶일 수밖에 없는 그 의견이고, 의견의 차이가 없는 곳에는 오직 하나의 의견밖엔 있을 수 없으니, 그것은 완전히 아니로다, 토머스, 토머스, 아니로다!\""]], [["Arthur, with less formality, expressed himself gratified by their high appreciation of so very slight an attention on his part; and explained as to the tea that he had not yet dined, and was going straight home to refresh after a long day’s labour, or he would have readily accepted the hospitable offer.", "아서는 그보다 덜 격식을 차려, 그토록 사소한 자신의 관심을 그들이 높이 평가해 주니 흐뭇하다고 표하고는, 차에 관해서는 아직 저녁을 들지 않은 데다 긴 하루의 노동 끝에 곧장 집으로 가서 기운을 차리려던 참이라고, 그렇지 않았다면 기꺼이 그 융숭한 권유를 받아들였을 거라고 설명했다."], ["As Mr Pancks was somewhat noisily getting his steam up for departure, he concluded by asking that gentleman if he would walk with him?", "팽크스 씨가 떠날 채비로 다소 요란하게 증기를 올리고 있던지라, 아서는 끝으로 그에게 함께 걸어가겠느냐고 물었다."], ["Mr Pancks said he desired no better engagement, and the two took leave of Happy Cottage.", "팽크스 씨는 그보다 더 바라는 일이 없다고 했고, 두 사람은 '행복한 오두막'에 작별을 고했다."]], [["‘If you will come home with me, Pancks,’ said Arthur, when they got into the street, ‘and will share what dinner or supper there is, it will be next door to an act of charity; for I am weary and out of sorts to-night.’", "\"우리 집에 같이 가서, 팽크스.\" 거리로 나서자 아서가 말했다. \"있는 대로 저녁이든 야참이든 나눠 들면, 그건 자선 행위나 다름없을 걸세. 오늘 밤 내가 지치고 기분이 영 좋지 않거든.\""]], [["‘Ask me to do a greater thing than that,’ said Pancks, ‘when you want it done, and I’ll do it.’", "\"그보다 더한 일을 시키시구려.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"필요할 때 시키시면 해 드리리다.\""]], [["Between this eccentric personage and Clennam, a tacit understanding and accord had been always improving since Mr Pancks flew over Mr Rugg’s back in the Marshalsea Yard.", "이 별난 인물과 클레넘 사이에는, 팽크스 씨가 마셜시 마당에서 러그 씨의 등 너머로 펄쩍 뛰어오른 그날 이후로 줄곧 무언의 이해와 교감이 깊어져 왔다."], ["When the carriage drove away on the memorable day of the family’s departure, these two had looked after it together, and had walked slowly away together.", "가족이 떠나던 그 잊지 못할 날, 마차가 달려가 버렸을 때 이 두 사람은 함께 그 뒤를 바라보았고, 함께 천천히 걸어 나왔던 것이다."], ["When the first letter came from little Dorrit, nobody was more interested in hearing of her than Mr Pancks.", "리틀 도릿에게서 첫 편지가 왔을 때, 그녀의 소식을 듣는 일에 팽크스 씨보다 더 관심을 가진 사람은 없었다."], ["The second letter, at that moment in Clennam’s breast-pocket, particularly remembered him by name.", "지금 클레넘의 가슴 주머니에 든 두 번째 편지는 특별히 그의 이름을 기억해 주고 있었다."], ["Though he had never before made any profession or protestation to Clennam, and though what he had just said was little enough as to the words in which it was expressed, Clennam had long had a growing belief that Mr Pancks, in his own odd way, was becoming attached to him.", "그가 전에는 클레넘에게 어떤 고백이나 다짐도 한 적이 없었고, 방금 한 말도 그 표현된 어구로만 보자면 미미하기 짝이 없었으나, 클레넘은 오래전부터 팽크스 씨가 제 나름의 별난 방식으로 자신에게 정이 들고 있다는 믿음이 점점 자라나던 터였다."], ["All these strings intertwining made Pancks a very cable of anchorage that night.", "이 모든 끈이 한데 얽혀, 그날 밤 팽크스는 그야말로 닻줄 같은 존재가 되어 주었다."]], [["‘I am quite alone,’ Arthur explained as they walked on.", "\"나는 완전히 혼자라네.\" 걸어가며 아서가 설명했다."], ["‘My partner is away, busily engaged at a distance on his branch of our business, and you shall do just as you like.’", "\"동업자는 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 우리 사업의 자기 부문 일로 분주히 매달려 있거든. 그러니 자넨 좋을 대로 하게나.\""]], [["‘Thank you. You didn’t take particular notice of little Altro just now; did you?’ said Pancks.", "\"고맙소. 헌데 아까 어린 알트로를 눈여겨보지 않으셨지요?\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그래. 왜 그러나?\""]], [["‘No. Why?’\n‘He’s a bright fellow, and I like him,’ said Pancks.", "\"명민한 친구라 내가 좋아하는데.\""], ["‘Something has gone amiss with him to-day.", "팽크스가 말했다. \"오늘 뭔가 그에게 잘못된 일이 있었구려."], ["Have you any idea of any cause that can have overset him?’", "그를 그렇게 뒤흔들어 놓을 만한 무슨 까닭이 있는지 짚이는 데가 있소?\""]], [["‘You surprise me!", "\"자네가 날 놀라게 하는군!"], ["None whatever.’", "전혀 모르겠는데.\""]], [["Mr Pancks gave his reasons for the inquiry.", "팽크스 씨는 그렇게 물은 까닭을 댔다."], ["Arthur was quite unprepared for them, and quite unable to suggest an explanation of them.", "아서는 그에 전혀 마음의 준비가 되어 있지 않았고, 그에 대한 설명도 도무지 내놓을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Perhaps you’ll ask him,’ said Pancks, ‘as he’s a stranger?’", "\"그자한테 한번 물어보시구려.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"외국 사람이니까 말이오.\""]], [["‘Ask him what?’ returned Clennam.", "\"무엇을 물어보란 말인가?\" 클레넘이 되물었다."]], [["‘What he has on his mind.’", "\"무슨 근심이 있는지를요.\""]], [["‘I ought first to see for myself that he has something on his mind, I think,’ said Clennam.", "\"내 생각엔, 먼저 그에게 정말 무슨 근심이 있는지를 내 눈으로 확인해야 할 것 같네.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘I have found him in every way so diligent, so grateful (for little enough), and so trustworthy, that it might look like suspecting him.", "\"나는 그가 무슨 일에서나 그토록 부지런하고, (그 적은 것에도) 그토록 고마워하고, 그토록 믿음직한 사람임을 알게 되었으니, 그러면 그를 의심하는 것처럼 보일 거야."], ["And that would be very unjust.’", "그건 무척 부당한 일이겠지.\""]], [["‘True,’ said Pancks.", "\"맞소.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘But, I say!", "\"허나, 그게 말이오!"], ["You oughtn’t to be anybody’s proprietor, Mr Clennam.", "클레넘 씨, 당신은 누구의 집주인 노릇도 해선 안 될 사람이오."], ["You’re much too delicate.’", "너무 마음이 여리거든.\""]], [["‘For the matter of that,’ returned Clennam laughing, ‘I have not a large proprietary share in Cavalletto.", "\"그 점으로 말하자면,\" 클레넘이 웃으며 대답했다."], ["His carving is his livelihood.", "\"나는 카발레토에 대해 큰 소유주 지분이 있는 게 아닐세. 그의 조각이 그의 생계지."], ["He keeps the keys of the Factory, watches it every alternate night, and acts as a sort of housekeeper to it generally; but we have little work in the way of his ingenuity, though we give him what we have.", "그는 공장 열쇠를 맡아 두고, 격일로 밤마다 그곳을 지키고, 대체로 일종의 관리인 노릇을 하지. 다만 우리한테는 그의 솜씨를 쓸 일감이 거의 없어."], ["No! I am rather his adviser than his proprietor.", "있는 만큼은 그에게 주지만 말이야."], ["To call me his standing counsel and his banker would be nearer the fact.", "아니야! 나는 그의 소유주라기보다 차라리 그의 조언자에 가깝지."], ["Speaking of being his banker, is it not curious, Pancks, that the ventures which run just now in so many people’s heads, should run even in little Cavalletto’s?’", "그의 고문이자 은행 노릇을 한다고 하는 편이 사실에 더 가까울 걸세. 그의 은행 노릇 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데, 팽크스, 요즘 그토록 많은 사람들 머릿속에 도는 그 투기가 어린 카발레토의 머릿속에까지 돈다는 게 묘하지 않은가?\""]], [["‘Ventures?’ retorted Pancks, with a snort.", "\"투기요?\" 팽크스가 콧방귀를 뀌며 받아쳤다."], ["‘What ventures?’", "\"무슨 투기 말이오?\""]], [["‘These Merdle enterprises.’", "\"이 머들의 사업들 말일세.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"아아!"], ["Investments,’ said Pancks.", "투자 말이군요.\""], ["‘Ay, ay!", "팽크스가 말했다."], ["I didn’t know you were speaking of investments.’", "\"그렇지, 그렇지! 당신이 투자 얘기를 하는 줄 몰랐소.\""]], [["His quick way of replying caused Clennam to look at him, with a doubt whether he meant more than he said.", "그가 재빨리 대답하는 품에, 클레넘은 그가 말 이상의 무언가를 뜻하는 게 아닌가 하는 의심으로 그를 쳐다보았다."], ["As it was accompanied, however, with a quickening of his pace and a corresponding increase in the labouring of his machinery, Arthur did not pursue the matter, and they soon arrived at his house.", "그러나 그 대답에 발걸음이 빨라지고 그에 걸맞게 기관 가동이 더 힘차지는 것이 따라붙는지라, 아서는 그 문제를 더 캐지 않았고, 두 사람은 곧 그의 집에 다다랐다."]], [["A dinner of soup and a pigeon-pie, served on a little round table before the fire, and flavoured with a bottle of good wine, oiled Mr Pancks’s works in a highly effective manner; so that when Clennam produced his Eastern pipe, and handed Mr Pancks another Eastern pipe, the latter gentleman was perfectly comfortable.", "수프 한 그릇과 비둘기 파이로 차린 저녁이 난롯불 앞 작은 둥근 탁자에 올라, 좋은 포도주 한 병으로 풍미를 더하니, 팽크스 씨의 작동부에 더없이 효과적으로 기름칠을 해 주었다. 그리하여 클레넘이 자신의 동방산 파이프를 꺼내고 팽크스 씨에게도 다른 동방산 파이프를 건넸을 때, 후자의 신사는 더없이 편안한 상태였다."]], [["They puffed for a while in silence, Mr Pancks like a steam-vessel with wind, tide, calm water, and all other sea-going conditions in her favour.", "두 사람은 한동안 말없이 담배를 피웠다. 팽크스 씨는 바람도, 조류도, 잔잔한 물도, 그 밖의 모든 항해 조건도 죄다 자기편인 증기선 같았다."], ["He was the first to speak, and he spoke thus:", "먼저 입을 연 것은 그였고, 그는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래요."], ["Investments is the word.’", "투자가 맞는 말이오.\""]], [["Clennam, with his former look, said ‘Ah!’", "클레넘은 아까와 같은 표정으로 \"아!\" 하고 말했다."]], [["‘I am going back to it, you see,’ said Pancks.", "\"보다시피 그 얘기로 되돌아가는 거요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래, 자네가 그 얘기로 되돌아가는 게 보이네.\""], ["I see you are going back to it,’ returned Clennam, wondering why.", "클레넘이 왜 그러나 궁금해하며 대답했다."]], [["‘Wasn’t it a curious thing that they should run in little Altro’s head?", "\"그게 어린 알트로 머릿속에까지 돈다는 게 묘한 일 아니오, 응?\""], ["Eh?’ said Pancks as he smoked.", "팽크스가 담배를 피우며 말했다."], ["‘Wasn’t that how you put it?’", "\"당신이 그렇게 말하지 않았소?\""]], [["‘That was what I said.’", "\"내가 한 말이 그거지.\""]], [["‘Ay!", "\"그렇소!"], ["But think of the whole Yard having got it.", "헌데 마당 전체가 그걸 손에 넣었다고 생각해 보시오."], ["Think of their all meeting me with it, on my collecting days, here and there and everywhere.", "그자들이 죄다 그걸로 날 맞이한다고 생각해 보란 말이오, 수금하는 날이면, 여기서도 저기서도 사방에서."], ["Whether they pay, or whether they don’t pay.", "집세를 내든 안 내든 말이오."], ["Merdle, Merdle, Merdle.", "머들, 머들, 머들."], ["Always Merdle.’", "노상 머들이오.\""]], [["‘Very strange how these runs on an infatuation prevail,’ said Arthur.", "\"이런 열광이 번지는 것이 참 이상도 하지.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘An’t it?’ returned Pancks.", "\"그렇잖소?\" 팽크스가 대꾸했다."], ["After smoking for a minute or so, more drily than comported with his recent oiling, he added: ‘Because you see these people don’t understand the subject.’", "방금 받은 기름칠에 어울리지 않게 다소 메마르게 잠시 담배를 피우고 나서, 그가 덧붙였다. \"왜냐면 보시오, 그 사람들은 이 문제를 이해하지 못하거든.\""]], [["‘Not a bit,’ assented Clennam.", "\"조금도 못 하지.\" 클레넘이 맞장구쳤다."]], [["‘Not a bit,’ cried Pancks.", "\"조금도요.\" 팽크스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Know nothing of figures.", "\"숫자라곤 아무것도 모르고."], ["Know nothing of money questions.", "돈 문제라곤 아무것도 모르고."], ["Never made a calculation.", "계산이라곤 해 본 적이 없고."], ["Never worked it, sir!’", "따져 본 적이 없단 말이오, 나리!\""]], [["‘If they had--’ Clennam was going on to say; when Mr Pancks, without change of countenance, produced a sound so far surpassing all his usual efforts, nasal or bronchial, that he stopped.", "\"만약 그들이 해 봤다면—\" 클레넘이 말을 이어 가려는데, 팽크스 씨가 표정 하나 바꾸지 않고서, 평소 콧소리나 기관지에서 내던 그 어떤 노력도 까마득히 능가하는 소리를 내는 바람에, 클레넘은 말을 멈췄다."]], [["‘If they had?’ repeated Pancks in an inquiring tone.", "\"만약 그들이 해 봤다면?\" 팽크스가 묻는 투로 되풀이했다."]], [["‘I thought you--spoke,’ said Arthur, hesitating what name to give the interruption.", "\"자네가—말을 하는 줄 알았네.\" 아서는 그 방해음을 무어라 불러야 할지 망설이며 말했다."]], [["‘Not at all,’ said Pancks.", "\"천만에요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Not yet.", "\"아직은 아니오."], ["I may in a minute.", "잠시 후엔 할지 모르지."], ["If they had?’", "그들이 해 봤다면?\""]], [["‘If they had,’ observed Clennam, who was a little at a loss how to take his friend, ‘why, I suppose they would have known better.’", "\"그들이 해 봤다면,\" 친구를 어떻게 받아들여야 할지 다소 갈피를 못 잡으며 클레넘이 말했다. \"글쎄, 더 잘 알게 됐겠지.\""]], [["‘How so, Mr Clennam?’ Pancks asked quickly, and with an odd effect of having been from the commencement of the conversation loaded with the heavy charge he now fired off.", "\"어째서요, 클레넘 씨?\" 팽크스가 재빨리, 그리고 대화가 시작될 때부터 묵직한 장약을 잔뜩 장전하고 있다가 이제야 발사하는 듯한 묘한 효과를 풍기며 물었다."], ["‘They’re right, you know.", "\"그 사람들이 옳단 말이오, 알겠소."], ["They don’t mean to be, but they’re right.’", "그럴 작정은 아니지만, 그들이 옳단 말이오.\""]], [["‘Right in sharing Cavalletto’s inclination to speculate with Mr Merdle?’", "\"머들 씨에게 투기하려는 카발레토의 마음에 동조하는 게 옳다고?\""]], [["‘Per-fectly, sir,’ said Pancks.", "\"완—벽하게 옳소, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘I’ve gone into it.", "\"내가 파고들어 봤소."], ["I’ve made the calculations.", "계산을 해 봤단 말이오."], ["I’ve worked it.", "따져 봤소."], ["They’re safe and genuine.’ Relieved by having got to this, Mr Pancks took as long a pull as his lungs would permit at his Eastern pipe, and looked sagaciously and steadily at Clennam while inhaling and exhaling too.", "안전하고 진짜요.\" 여기까지 말하고 나니 후련해진 팽크스 씨는, 동방산 파이프를 폐가 허락하는 한 길게 한 모금 빨았고, 들이쉬고 또 내쉬는 동안에도 슬기롭고 한결같은 눈으로 클레넘을 바라보았다."]], [["In those moments, Mr Pancks began to give out the dangerous infection with which he was laden.", "그 순간, 팽크스 씨는 자기가 잔뜩 짊어진 그 위험한 전염병을 내뿜기 시작했다."], ["It is the manner of communicating these diseases; it is the subtle way in which they go about.", "이런 병이란 그렇게 옮는 것이며, 그렇게 교묘한 방식으로 퍼져 다니는 것이다."]], [["‘Do you mean, my good Pancks,’ asked Clennam emphatically, ‘that you would put that thousand pounds of yours, let us say, for instance, out at this kind of interest?’", "\"여보게 팽크스, 자네 말은,\" 클레넘이 힘주어 물었다. \"자네라면, 가령 예를 들어 그 천 파운드를 이런 종류의 이자에 내놓겠다는 건가?\""]], [["‘Certainly,’ said Pancks.", "\"물론이오.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Already done it, sir.’", "\"벌써 했소, 나리.\""]], [["Mr Pancks took another long inhalation, another long exhalation, another long sagacious look at Clennam.", "팽크스 씨는 다시 한번 길게 들이쉬고, 다시 한번 길게 내쉬고, 다시 한번 길게 슬기로운 눈으로 클레넘을 바라보았다."]], [["‘I tell you, Mr Clennam, I’ve gone into it,’ said Pancks.", "\"클레넘 씨, 내가 말하잖소, 내가 파고들어 봤다고.\""], ["‘He’s a man of immense resources--enormous capital--government influence.", "팽크스가 말했다. \"그분은 막대한 자원을 가진 사람이오—엄청난 자본—정부의 영향력."], ["They’re the best schemes afloat.", "떠도는 사업 중에 최고요."], ["They’re safe.", "안전하오."], ["They’re certain.’", "확실하오.\""]], [["‘Well!’ returned Clennam, looking first at him gravely and then at the fire gravely.", "\"흠!\" 클레넘이 먼저 그를 진지하게 보고는 다음으로 불을 진지하게 바라보며 대답했다."], ["‘You surprise me!’", "\"자네가 날 놀라게 하는군!\""]], [["‘Bah!’ Pancks retorted.", "\"쳇!\" 팽크스가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Don’t say that, sir.", "\"그런 말 마시오, 나리."], ["It’s what you ought to do yourself!", "당신 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일이란 말이오!"], ["Why don’t you do as I do?’", "왜 나처럼 하지 않는 거요?\""]], [["Of whom Mr Pancks had taken the prevalent disease, he could no more have told than if he had unconsciously taken a fever.", "팽크스 씨가 이 만연한 병을 누구한테서 옮았는지는, 그가 무의식중에 열병에 걸렸다 한들 알 수 없었을 것과 마찬가지로 알 길이 없었다."], ["Bred at first, as many physical diseases are, in the wickedness of men, and then disseminated in their ignorance, these epidemics, after a period, get communicated to many sufferers who are neither ignorant nor wicked.", "많은 신체적 질병이 그러하듯 처음에는 인간의 사악함 속에서 자라나, 그다음엔 그들의 무지 속에서 퍼져 나가는 이런 유행병은, 한동안 시간이 지나면 무지하지도 사악하지도 않은 수많은 사람에게까지 옮겨 간다."], ["Mr Pancks might, or might not, have caught the illness himself from a subject of this class; but in this category he appeared before Clennam, and the infection he threw off was all the more virulent.", "팽크스 씨가 이 부류에 속한 자에게서 그 병을 옮았는지 아닌지는 모를 일이나, 그는 그런 부류로서 클레넘 앞에 나타났고, 그가 내뿜는 전염은 그만큼 더 독성이 강했다."]], [["‘And you have really invested,’ Clennam had already passed to that word, ‘your thousand pounds, Pancks?’", "\"그래서 자네는 정말로, 자네 그 천 파운드를 투자했다는 건가, 팽크스?\" 클레넘은 이미 그 단어로 넘어가 있었다."]], [["‘To be sure, sir!’ replied Pancks boldly, with a puff of smoke.", "\"틀림없소, 나리!\" 팽크스가 담배 연기를 한 모금 내뿜으며 대담하게 대답했다."], ["‘And only wish it ten!’", "\"차라리 만 파운드였으면 싶소!\""]], [["Now, Clennam had two subjects lying heavy on his lonely mind that night; the one, his partner’s long-deferred hope; the other, what he had seen and heard at his mother’s.", "자, 그날 밤 클레넘의 외로운 마음을 무겁게 누르는 화제가 둘 있었다. 하나는 그토록 오래 미뤄진 동업자의 희망이요, 다른 하나는 그가 어머니 댁에서 보고 들은 것이었다."], ["In the relief of having this companion, and of feeling that he could trust him, he passed on to both, and both brought him round again, with an increase and acceleration of force, to his point of departure.", "이렇게 길동무가 생기고, 그를 믿을 수 있다고 느끼는 안도감에서 그는 두 화제로 옮겨 갔으나, 둘 다 그를 다시금, 더 큰 힘으로 더 빠르게, 출발점으로 데려다 놓았다."]], [["It came about in the simplest manner.", "그것은 더없이 단순하게 일어났다."], ["Quitting the investment subject, after an interval of silent looking at the fire through the smoke of his pipe, he told Pancks how and why he was occupied with the great National Department.", "파이프 연기 너머로 불을 말없이 바라보던 막간 끝에, 그는 투자 이야기를 떠나, 자신이 어쩌다 어떤 까닭으로 그 거대한 국가 부서에 얽매여 있는지를 팽크스에게 들려주었다."], ["‘A hard case it has been, and a hard case it is on Doyce,’ he finished by saying, with all the honest feeling the topic roused in him.", "\"힘든 일이었고, 도이스에게는 지금도 힘든 일일세.\" 그는 그 화제가 그의 안에 불러일으킨 온갖 정직한 감정을 담아, 이렇게 끝맺었다."]], [["‘Hard indeed,’ Pancks acquiesced.", "\"정말 힘들겠소.\" 팽크스가 동의했다."], ["‘But you manage for him, Mr Clennam?’", "\"허나 당신이 그분을 위해 일을 봐주는 거요, 클레넘 씨?\""]], [["‘How do you mean?’", "\"그게 무슨 말인가?\""]], [["‘Manage the money part of the business?’", "\"사업의 돈 쪽을 관리한단 말이오?\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그렇지."], ["As well as I can.’", "내가 할 수 있는 한은.\""]], [["‘Manage it better, sir,’ said Pancks.", "\"더 잘 관리하시오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Recompense him for his toils and disappointments.", "\"그분의 노고와 실망을 보상해 드리시오."], ["Give him the chances of the time.", "이 시대의 기회를 잡아 드리란 말이오."], ["He’ll never benefit himself in that way, patient and preoccupied workman.", "그분은 그런 식으로는 결코 제 잇속을 챙기지 못할 거요, 끈기 있고 한 가지에만 골몰하는 일꾼이니까."], ["He looks to you, sir.’", "그분은 당신만 바라보고 있소, 나리.\""]], [["‘I do my best, Pancks,’ returned Clennam, uneasily.", "\"나도 최선을 다하네, 팽크스.\" 클레넘이 거북하게 대답했다."], ["‘As to duly weighing and considering these new enterprises of which I have had no experience, I doubt if I am fit for it, I am growing old.’", "\"다만 내가 겪어 본 적 없는 이런 새로운 사업들을 제대로 따져 보고 가늠하는 일이라면, 내가 거기 적합한지 의심스럽네. 나도 나이를 먹어 가고 있으니.\""]], [["‘Growing old?’ cried Pancks.", "\"나이를 먹어 간다고요?\" 팽크스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Ha, ha!’", "\"하, 하!\""]], [["There was something so indubitably genuine in the wonderful laugh, and series of snorts and puffs, engendered in Mr Pancks’s astonishment at, and utter rejection of, the idea, that his being quite in earnest could not be questioned.", "그 생각에 대한 팽크스 씨의 경악과 전적인 거부에서 우러난 그 놀라운 웃음, 그리고 일련의 콧방귀와 뿜어 대는 소리에는 의심할 여지 없이 진실한 무언가가 담겨 있어서, 그가 정말로 진지하다는 것은 의심할 수 없었다."]], [["‘Growing old?’ cried Pancks.", "\"나이를 먹어 간다고요?\" 팽크스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Hear, hear, hear!", "\"들어 보소, 들어 보소, 들어 보소!"], ["Old?", "늙었다고?"], ["Hear him, hear him!’", "저 양반 말 좀 들어 보소, 들어 보소!\""]], [["The positive refusal expressed in Mr Pancks’s continued snorts, no less than in these exclamations, to entertain the sentiment for a single instant, drove Arthur away from it.", "그 생각을 단 한 순간도 받아들이지 않겠다는, 이 외침들 못지않게 팽크스 씨의 계속되는 콧방귀에 담긴 단호한 거부는, 아서를 그 생각에서 멀리 떼어 놓았다."], ["Indeed, he was fearful of something happening to Mr Pancks in the violent conflict that took place between the breath he jerked out of himself and the smoke he jerked into himself.", "사실 그는 팽크스 씨가 제 몸에서 끌어내듯 토해 내는 숨과, 제 몸으로 빨아들이듯 들이켜는 연기 사이에 벌어지는 격렬한 다툼 속에서 그에게 무슨 일이 생기지나 않을까 걱정스러웠다."], ["This abandonment of the second topic threw him on the third.", "이렇게 두 번째 화제를 접게 되면서 그는 세 번째 화제로 떠밀렸다."]], [["‘Young, old, or middle-aged, Pancks,’ he said, when there was a favourable pause, ‘I am in a very anxious and uncertain state; a state that even leads me to doubt whether anything now seeming to belong to me, may be really mine.", "\"젊었든, 늙었든, 중년이든, 팽크스.\" 알맞게 말이 끊긴 틈에 그가 말했다."], ["Shall I tell you how this is?", "\"나는 무척 불안하고 불확실한 상태에 있다네. 지금 내 것처럼 보이는 것 가운데 무엇이든 정말 내 것일까 의심하게까지 만드는 그런 상태일세."], ["Shall I put a great trust in you?’", "이게 어찌 된 일인지 자네한테 말해도 될까? 자네를 크게 믿어도 될까?\""]], [["‘You shall, sir,’ said Pancks, ‘if you believe me worthy of it.’", "\"믿어도 되오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"내가 그럴 만하다고 여기신다면 말이오.\""]], [["‘I do.’", "\"여기네.\""]], [["‘You may!’ Mr Pancks’s short and sharp rejoinder, confirmed by the sudden outstretching of his coaly hand, was most expressive and convincing.", "\"믿으셔도 되오!\" 팽크스 씨의 짧고 날카로운 응수는, 석탄검댕이 묻은 손을 불쑥 내밀어 굳히니, 더없이 표정 풍부하고 설득력 있었다."], ["Arthur shook the hand warmly.", "아서는 그 손을 따뜻하게 흔들었다."]], [["He then, softening the nature of his old apprehensions as much as was possible consistently with their being made intelligible and never alluding to his mother by name, but speaking vaguely of a relation of his, confided to Mr Pancks a broad outline of the misgivings he entertained, and of the interview he had witnessed.", "그러고서 그는, 자신의 오랜 우려를 알아들을 수 있게 하는 한도 내에서 최대한 그 성격을 누그러뜨리고, 어머니를 이름으로 언급하는 일은 결코 없이, 다만 자기 친척이라고 막연히 일컬으며, 자신이 품은 불길한 예감의 큰 윤곽과 자신이 목격한 면담을 팽크스 씨에게 털어놓았다."], ["Mr Pancks listened with such interest that, regardless of the charms of the Eastern pipe, he put it in the grate among the fire-irons, and occupied his hands during the whole recital in so erecting the loops and hooks of hair all over his head, that he looked, when it came to a conclusion, like a journeyman Hamlet in conversation with his father’s spirit.", "팽크스 씨는 어찌나 흥미진진하게 들었던지, 동방산 파이프의 매력도 아랑곳없이 그것을 부삽 사이 화격자에 처박아 두고는, 이야기가 진행되는 내내 두 손으로 머리 위 온통 머리카락의 고리와 갈고리를 곤두세우는 데 매달려, 이야기가 끝났을 무렵엔 마치 아버지의 혼령과 대화를 나누는 풋내기 햄릿처럼 보였다."]], [["‘Brings me back, sir,’ was his exclamation then, with a startling touch on Clennam’s knee, ‘brings me back, sir, to the Investments!", "\"그게 날 도로 데려가는구려, 나리.\" 그제야 그가 클레넘의 무릎을 깜짝 놀라게 탁 치며 외쳤다."], ["I don’t say anything of your making yourself poor to repair a wrong you never committed.", "\"그게 날 도로 투자로 데려가는구려, 나리! 당신이 저지르지도 않은 잘못을 바로잡겠다고 스스로를 가난뱅이로 만드는 일에 대해선 아무 말 않겠소."], ["That’s you.", "그게 당신이지."], ["A man must be himself.", "사람은 제 자신이어야 하니까."], ["But I say this, fearing you may want money to save your own blood from exposure and disgrace--make as much as you can!’", "허나 이건 말하겠소, 당신이 제 핏줄을 폭로와 치욕에서 구하기 위해 돈이 필요할까 봐 하는 말이오만—할 수 있는 한 많이 버시오!\""]], [["Arthur shook his head, but looked at him thoughtfully too.", "아서는 고개를 가로저었으나, 또한 그를 생각에 잠겨 바라보았다."]], [["‘Be as rich as you can, sir,’ Pancks adjured him with a powerful concentration of all his energies on the advice.", "\"할 수 있는 한 부자가 되시오, 나리.\" 팽크스가 그 충고에 자신의 온 기력을 강력히 모아 그에게 간곡히 권했다."], ["‘Be as rich as you honestly can.", "\"정직하게 할 수 있는 한 부자가 되란 말이오."], ["It’s your duty.", "그게 당신의 의무요."], ["Not for your sake, but for the sake of others.", "당신 자신을 위해서가 아니라 남들을 위해서."], ["Take time by the forelock.", "시간의 앞머리 갈기를 붙잡으시오."], ["Poor Mr Doyce (who really _is_ growing old) depends upon you.", "가엾은 도이스 씨는(이분이야말로 정말 나이를 먹어 가고 있소) 당신만 의지하고 있소."], ["Your relative depends upon you.", "당신 친척도 당신만 의지하고 있고."], ["You don’t know what depends upon you.’", "당신은 얼마나 많은 것이 당신한테 달려 있는지 모른단 말이오.\""]], [["‘Well, well, well!’ returned Arthur.", "\"알았네, 알았어, 알았어!\" 아서가 대답했다."], ["‘Enough for to-night.’", "\"오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐네.\""]], [["‘One word more, Mr Clennam,’ retorted Pancks, ‘and then enough for to-night.", "\"한마디만 더 하겠소, 클레넘 씨.\" 팽크스가 받아쳤다."], ["Why should you leave all the gains to the gluttons, knaves, and impostors?", "\"그러고 나면 오늘 밤은 이만하면 됐소. 어째서 그 모든 이득을 탐욕꾼, 악당, 사기꾼들한테 죄다 넘겨주려는 거요?"], ["Why should you leave all the gains that are to be got to my proprietor and the like of him?", "어째서 손에 넣을 수 있는 그 모든 이득을 내 집주인이나 그자 같은 부류한테 죄다 넘겨주려는 거요?"], ["Yet you’re always doing it.", "헌데 당신은 늘 그 짓을 하고 있단 말이오."], ["When I say you, I mean such men as you.", "내가 당신이라고 할 때, 나는 당신 같은 사람들을 뜻하는 거요."], ["You know you are.", "당신도 그런 줄 알잖소."], ["Why, I see it every day of my life.", "그야, 나는 그걸 내 삶의 매일같이 본단 말이오."], ["I see nothing else.", "그것밖엔 안 보여요."], ["It’s my business to see it.", "그걸 보는 게 내 직업이거든. 그러니 내 말은,\""], ["Therefore I say,’ urged Pancks, ‘Go in and win!’", "팽크스가 채근했다. \"뛰어들어서 이기란 말이오!\""]], [["‘But what of Go in and lose?’ said Arthur.", "\"허나 뛰어들어서 잃으면 어쩌나?\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Can’t be done, sir,’ returned Pancks.", "\"그럴 리 없소, 나리.\""], ["‘I have looked into it.", "팽크스가 대답했다."], ["Name up everywhere--immense resources--enormous capital--great position--high connection--government influence.", "\"내가 들여다봤소. 이름은 사방에 자자하고—막대한 자원—엄청난 자본—대단한 지위—드높은 연줄—정부의 영향력."], ["Can’t be done!’", "그럴 리 없단 말이오!\""]], [["Gradually, after this closing exposition, Mr Pancks subsided; allowed his hair to droop as much as it ever would droop on the utmost persuasion; reclaimed the pipe from the fire-irons, filled it anew, and smoked it out.", "이 마무리 설파를 끝으로 팽크스 씨는 차츰 가라앉았다. 머리카락은 아무리 구슬려도 늘어질 수 있는 한껏 늘어지게 내버려 두었고, 화격자에서 파이프를 도로 거두어 새로 채워 끝까지 피웠다."], ["They said little more; but were company to one another in silently pursuing the same subjects, and did not part until midnight.", "두 사람은 그 뒤로 별말이 없었으나, 같은 화제를 말없이 곱씹으며 서로에게 길동무가 되어 주었고, 자정이 되어서야 헤어졌다."], ["On taking his leave, Mr Pancks, when he had shaken hands with Clennam, worked completely round him before he steamed out at the door.", "작별 인사를 하면서 팽크스 씨는 클레넘과 악수를 나눈 뒤, 문밖으로 증기를 뿜으며 나가기 전에 그를 완전히 한 바퀴 빙 돌았다."], ["This, Arthur received as an assurance that he might implicitly rely on Pancks, if he ever should come to need assistance; either in any of the matters of which they had spoken that night, or any other subject that could in any way affect himself.", "아서는 이것을, 혹여 자기가 도움이 필요한 처지에 이르게 된다면—그날 밤 그들이 이야기한 어떤 일에서든, 아니면 자신에게 어떤 식으로든 영향을 미칠 수 있는 다른 어떤 일에서든—팽크스를 전적으로 믿어도 된다는 보증으로 받아들였다."]], [["At intervals all next day, and even while his attention was fixed on other things, he thought of Mr Pancks’s investment of his thousand pounds, and of his having ‘looked into it.’ He thought of Mr Pancks’s being so sanguine in this matter, and of his not being usually of a sanguine character.", "이튿날 종일토록 틈틈이, 심지어 다른 일에 정신이 쏠려 있을 때조차, 그는 팽크스 씨가 자기 천 파운드를 투자한 일과 그가 그걸 '들여다봤다'고 한 일을 생각했다. 그는 팽크스 씨가 이 일에 그토록 낙관적이라는 것을, 그리고 그가 평소엔 낙관적인 성격이 아니라는 것을 생각했다."], ["He thought of the great National Department, and of the delight it would be to him to see Doyce better off.", "그는 그 거대한 국가 부서를 생각했고, 도이스가 형편이 나아지는 것을 보면 자기가 얼마나 기쁠지를 생각했다."], ["He thought of the darkly threatening place that went by the name of Home in his remembrance, and of the gathering shadows which made it yet more darkly threatening than of old.", "그는 자신의 기억 속에서 집이라는 이름으로 통하는 그 음울하게 위협적인 곳을, 그리고 그곳을 옛날보다 한층 더 음울하게 위협적으로 만드는, 몰려드는 그림자들을 생각했다."], ["He observed anew that wherever he went, he saw, or heard, or touched, the celebrated name of Merdle; he found it difficult even to remain at his desk a couple of hours, without having it presented to one of his bodily senses through some agency or other.", "그는 어디를 가든 그 유명한 머들의 이름을 보거나 듣거나 만지게 된다는 것을 새삼 깨달았다. 어떤 경로로든 자신의 몸의 감각 중 하나에 그 이름이 들이밀어지지 않고서는 책상에 두어 시간 앉아 있기조차 어려웠다."], ["He began to think it was curious too that it should be everywhere, and that nobody but he should seem to have any mistrust of it.", "그는 그 이름이 어디에나 있다는 것도, 그리고 자기 말고는 아무도 그것을 미덥지 않게 여기지 않는 듯하다는 것도 이상하다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["Though indeed he began to remember, when he got to this, even _he_ did not mistrust it; he had only happened to keep aloof from it.", "하기야 여기까지 생각이 미치자, 그는 자기 자신마저도 그것을 미덥지 않게 여기는 건 아니었음을 떠올리기 시작했다. 다만 그것에서 멀찍이 떨어져 있었을 뿐이었다."]], [["Such symptoms, when a disease of the kind is rife, are usually the signs of sickening.", "이런 종류의 병이 만연할 때 이러한 증상들은, 대개 병이 들기 시작했다는 징후인 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 14. Taking Advice", "# CHAPTER 14. Taking Advice"]], [["When it became known to the Britons on the shore of the yellow Tiber that their intelligent compatriot, Mr Sparkler, was made one of the Lords of their Circumlocution Office, they took it as a piece of news with which they had no nearer concern than with any other piece of news--any other Accident or Offence--in the English papers.", "누런 테베레 강가의 영국인들 사이에, 자기들의 영민한 동포인 스파클러 씨가 에두름국의 장관 중 한 사람이 되었다는 소식이 알려지자, 그들은 그것을 영국 신문에 실린 다른 어떤 소식보다 — 다른 어떤 사고나 비행보다 — 더 가까울 것 없는 한낱 소식거리로 받아들였다."], ["Some laughed; some said, by way of complete excuse, that the post was virtually a sinecure, and any fool who could spell his name was good enough for it; some, and these the more solemn political oracles, said that Decimus did wisely to strengthen himself, and that the sole constitutional purpose of all places within the gift of Decimus, was, that Decimus _should_ strengthen himself.", "더러는 웃었고, 더러는 완벽한 변명이랍시고 그 자리가 사실상 명목뿐인 한직이라, 제 이름 철자나 쓸 줄 아는 바보면 누구든 그 자리에 충분하다고 말했으며, 더러는 — 이쪽이 더 엄숙한 정치 신탁가들이었는데 — 데시무스가 제 세력을 굳히는 것은 현명한 처사이며, 데시무스의 임명권 안에 든 모든 자리의 유일한 헌법적 목적은 데시무스가 제 세력을 굳히는 것이라고 말했다."], ["A few bilious Britons there were who would not subscribe to this article of faith; but their objection was purely theoretical.", "이 신조 조항에 동의하지 않으려는 까다로운 영국인도 몇몇 있었지만, 그들의 반대는 순전히 이론적인 것이었다."], ["In a practical point of view, they listlessly abandoned the matter, as being the business of some other Britons unknown, somewhere, or nowhere.", "실제적 관점에서 그들은 그 일을, 어딘가 혹은 아무 데도 아닌 곳에 있는, 이름 모를 다른 영국인들의 일이라 여기며 무심히 내버려 두었다."], ["In like manner, at home, great numbers of Britons maintained, for as long as four-and-twenty consecutive hours, that those invisible and anonymous Britons ‘ought to take it up;’ and that if they quietly acquiesced in it, they deserved it.", "같은 식으로, 본국에서도 수많은 영국인들이 무려 스물네 시간 연속으로, 그 보이지 않고 이름 없는 영국인들이 '이 문제를 떠맡아야 한다'고, 그리고 그들이 잠자코 묵인한다면 그런 꼴을 당해도 싸다고 주장했다."], ["But of what class the remiss Britons were composed, and where the unlucky creatures hid themselves, and why they hid themselves, and how it constantly happened that they neglected their interests, when so many other Britons were quite at a loss to account for their not looking after those interests, was not, either upon the shore of the yellow Tiber or the shore of the black Thames, made apparent to men.", "그러나 그 태만한 영국인들이 어떤 부류로 이루어져 있는지, 그 불운한 자들이 어디에 숨었는지, 왜 숨었는지, 또 그토록 많은 다른 영국인들은 그들이 자기 이익을 돌보지 않는 까닭을 도무지 알 수 없어 하는 마당에, 어찌하여 그들은 늘 자기 이익을 등한히 하게 되는지는, 누런 테베레 강가에서도 검은 템스 강가에서도 사람들에게 밝혀지지 않았다."]], [["Mrs Merdle circulated the news, as she received congratulations on it, with a careless grace that displayed it to advantage, as the setting displays the jewel.", "머들 부인은 이 소식에 대한 축하를 받으면서, 그것을 무심한 우아함으로 퍼뜨렸는데, 그 무심함이 보석을 돋보이게 하는 받침대처럼 소식을 더없이 돋보이게 했다."], ["Yes, she said, Edmund had taken the place.", "그래요, 에드먼드가 그 자리를 맡았답니다, 하고 그녀는 말했다."], ["Mr Merdle wished him to take it, and he had taken it.", "머들 씨가 그 애더러 맡으라 했고, 그래서 맡았지요."], ["She hoped Edmund might like it, but really she didn’t know.", "에드먼드가 그 일을 좋아하면 좋겠지만, 정말 모르겠어요."], ["It would keep him in town a good deal, and he preferred the country.", "그러면 도시에 꽤 매여 있어야 할 텐데, 그 애는 시골을 더 좋아하거든요."], ["Still, it was not a disagreeable position--and it was a position.", "그래도 못마땅한 자리는 아니지요 — 어쨌든 자리니까요."], ["There was no denying that the thing was a compliment to Mr Merdle, and was not a bad thing for Edmund if he liked it.", "그 일이 머들 씨에게 하나의 영예라는 것은 부인할 수 없고, 에드먼드가 좋아한다면 그 애에게도 나쁘지 않은 일이지요."], ["It was just as well that he should have something to do, and it was just as well that he should have something for doing it.", "그 애가 할 일이 생긴 것도 잘된 일이고, 그 일을 한 대가로 무언가를 받게 된 것도 잘된 일이고요."], ["Whether it would be more agreeable to Edmund than the army, remained to be seen.", "그것이 에드먼드에게 군 생활보다 더 마음에 들지 어떨지는 두고 봐야겠지요."]], [["Thus the Bosom; accomplished in the art of seeming to make things of small account, and really enhancing them in the process.", "가슴은 그렇게 말했다 — 무언가를 대수롭지 않게 보이도록 만드는 기술에 능숙하면서, 실은 그 과정에서 그것을 한껏 추켜올리는 것이다."], ["While Henry Gowan, whom Decimus had thrown away, went through the whole round of his acquaintance between the Gate of the People and the town of Albano, vowing, almost (but not quite) with tears in his eyes, that Sparkler was the sweetest-tempered, simplest-hearted, altogether most lovable jackass that ever grazed on the public common; and that only one circumstance could have delighted him (Gowan) more, than his (the beloved jackass’s) getting this post, and that would have been his (Gowan’s) getting it himself.", "한편, 데시무스가 내버린 헨리 가우언은 인민의 문에서 알바노 마을에 이르는 사이의 온갖 지인들을 두루 찾아다니며, 거의 (그러나 완전히는 아니게) 눈에 눈물을 글썽이며 맹세하기를, 스파클러야말로 공유지에서 풀을 뜯어 본 그 어떤 멍청이보다도 성품이 가장 곱고 마음이 가장 순박하며 도무지 사랑스럽기 그지없는 얼간이라고 했다. 그리고 자기(가우언)를 그자(사랑스러운 얼간이)가 이 자리를 얻은 일보다 더 기쁘게 할 수 있었던 사정은 단 하나뿐인데, 그것은 자기(가우언)가 그 자리를 직접 얻는 것이었으리라고 했다."], ["He said it was the very thing for Sparkler.", "그는 그 자리가 스파클러에게 안성맞춤이라고 했다."], ["There was nothing to do, and he would do it charmingly; there was a handsome salary to draw, and he would draw it charmingly; it was a delightful, appropriate, capital appointment; and he almost forgave the donor his slight of himself, in his joy that the dear donkey for whom he had so great an affection was so admirably stabled.", "할 일이 없으니 그는 그 일을 멋들어지게 해낼 것이고, 두둑한 봉급이 나오니 그는 그 봉급을 멋들어지게 받아 챙길 것이며, 그것은 유쾌하고 적절하고 더할 나위 없는 임명이라고 했다. 그래서 그는, 그토록 깊은 애정을 품은 그 사랑스러운 당나귀가 그토록 훌륭한 마구간에 들어앉은 것이 기뻐, 자기를 푸대접한 그 시혜자를 거의 용서할 지경이 되었다."], ["Nor did his benevolence stop here.", "그의 자비심은 거기서 그치지 않았다."], ["He took pains, on all social occasions, to draw Mr Sparkler out, and make him conspicuous before the company; and, although the considerate action always resulted in that young gentleman’s making a dreary and forlorn mental spectacle of himself, the friendly intention was not to be doubted.", "그는 온갖 사교 자리마다 애써 스파클러 씨의 말문을 끌어내어 좌중 앞에서 그를 두드러지게 만들었는데, 그 사려 깊은 행동은 늘 그 젊은 신사가 스스로 음울하고 처량한 정신적 구경거리가 되는 결과로 이어졌지만, 그 우정 어린 의도만큼은 의심할 여지가 없었다."]], [["Unless, indeed, it chanced to be doubted by the object of Mr Sparkler’s affections.", "물론, 스파클러 씨가 애정을 바치는 대상이 그것을 의심하는 경우는 예외였지만 말이다."], ["Miss Fanny was now in the difficult situation of being universally known in that light, and of not having dismissed Mr Sparkler, however capriciously she used him.", "패니 양은 이제, 세상에 그런 의미로 널리 알려져 있으면서도, 아무리 변덕스럽게 스파클러 씨를 부려먹어 봤자 그를 내쫓은 것은 아닌, 난처한 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["Hence, she was sufficiently identified with the gentleman to feel compromised by his being more than usually ridiculous; and hence, being by no means deficient in quickness, she sometimes came to his rescue against Gowan, and did him very good service.", "그리하여 그녀는 그 신사와 충분히 한데 엮여 있는 셈이어서, 그가 평소보다 더 우스꽝스러워질 때면 자기 체면이 깎인다고 느꼈다. 또한 그녀는 결코 재치가 모자란 사람이 아니었던지라, 때때로 가우언에 맞서 그를 구해 주며 그에게 큰 도움을 베풀기도 했다."], ["But, while doing this, she was ashamed of him, undetermined whether to get rid of him or more decidedly encourage him, distracted with apprehensions that she was every day becoming more and more immeshed in her uncertainties, and tortured by misgivings that Mrs Merdle triumphed in her distress.", "그러나 그러면서도 그녀는 그가 부끄러웠고, 그를 떨쳐 버릴지 아니면 더 단호하게 부추길지 마음을 정하지 못했으며, 날마다 점점 더 자신의 불확실함 속으로 옭매여 든다는 불안에 마음이 산란했고, 머들 부인이 자기 곤경을 보며 의기양양해하리라는 불길한 예감에 시달렸다."], ["With this tumult in her mind, it is no subject for surprise that Miss Fanny came home one night in a state of agitation from a concert and ball at Mrs Merdle’s house, and on her sister affectionately trying to soothe her, pushed that sister away from the toilette-table at which she sat angrily trying to cry, and declared with a heaving bosom that she detested everybody, and she wished she was dead.", "마음속에 이런 소용돌이가 있었으니, 어느 날 밤 패니 양이 머들 부인 댁의 음악회와 무도회에서 흥분한 상태로 집에 돌아와, 동생이 다정하게 달래려 하자 화장대 앞에 앉아 성난 채로 울려고 애쓰던 그 화장대에서 동생을 밀쳐 내고, 가슴을 들썩이며 모두가 다 밉고 차라리 죽어 버렸으면 좋겠다고 선언한 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다."]], [["‘Dear Fanny, what is the matter?", "\"패니 언니, 무슨 일이야?"], ["Tell me.’", "말해 봐.\""]], [["‘Matter, you little Mole,’ said Fanny.", "\"무슨 일이냐고, 이 작은 두더지야,\""], ["‘If you were not the blindest of the blind, you would have no occasion to ask me.", "패니가 말했다. \"네가 눈먼 자들 중에서도 가장 눈먼 애가 아니라면, 나한테 물어볼 까닭도 없을 거야."], ["The idea of daring to pretend to assert that you have eyes in your head, and yet ask me what’s the matter!’", "감히 머릿속에 눈이 박혀 있다고 우겨 대면서, 그러고도 무슨 일이냐고 나한테 묻다니!\""]], [["‘Is it Mr Sparkler, dear?’", "\"스파클러 씨 일이야, 언니?\""]], [["‘Mis-ter Spark-ler!’ repeated Fanny, with unbounded scorn, as if he were the last subject in the Solar system that could possibly be near her mind.", "\"스파아클러 씨라니!\" 패니는 마치 그가 태양계에서 자기 마음에 가까이 다가올 수 있는 가장 동떨어진 주제라도 되는 양, 한없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다."], ["‘No, Miss Bat, it is not.’", "\"아니야, 박쥐 아가씨, 그게 아니란다.\""]], [["Immediately afterwards, she became remorseful for having called her sister names; declaring with sobs that she knew she made herself hateful, but that everybody drove her to it.", "곧이어 그녀는 동생에게 별명을 붙여 부른 것을 뉘우치며, 흐느끼는 소리로, 자기가 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만드는 줄 알지만 다들 자기를 그렇게 몰아간다고 했다."]], [["‘I don’t think you are well to-night, dear Fanny.’", "\"오늘 밤 언니 몸이 좀 안 좋은 것 같아, 패니 언니.\""]], [["‘Stuff and nonsense!’ replied the young lady, turning angry again; ‘I am as well as you are.", "\"쓸데없는 소리!\" 젊은 아가씨가 다시 발끈하며 대꾸했다."], ["Perhaps I might say better, and yet make no boast of it.’", "\"나는 너만큼 멀쩡해. 어쩌면 더 멀쩡하다고 할 수도 있겠지만, 그렇다고 그걸 자랑할 생각은 없어.\""]], [["Poor Little Dorrit, not seeing her way to the offering of any soothing words that would escape repudiation, deemed it best to remain quiet.", "가엾은 리틀 도릿은 거부당하지 않을 만한 달래는 말을 무엇 하나 찾아낼 길이 보이지 않아, 잠자코 있는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다."], ["At first, Fanny took this ill, too; protesting to her looking-glass, that of all the trying sisters a girl could have, she did think the most trying sister was a flat sister.", "처음에는 패니가 이것마저 못마땅해했다. 거울을 향해 항변하기를, 한 여자아이가 가질 수 있는 온갖 성가신 언니 중에서도 가장 성가신 언니는 맹탕인 동생을 둔 언니일 거라고 했다."], ["That she knew she was at times a wretched temper; that she knew she made herself hateful; that when she made herself hateful, nothing would do her half the good as being told so; but that, being afflicted with a flat sister, she never _was_ told so, and the consequence resulted that she was absolutely tempted and goaded into making herself disagreeable.", "자기가 때때로 형편없는 성미를 부리는 줄 안다고, 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만드는 줄 안다고, 그리고 스스로를 밉살스럽게 만들 때면 그렇다고 일러 주는 것만큼 자기에게 약이 되는 것이 없는데, 맹탕인 동생을 둔 탓에 도무지 그런 말을 듣지 못하고, 그 결과 자기가 못되게 굴도록 그야말로 부추김당하고 내몰린다고 했다."], ["Besides (she angrily told her looking-glass), she didn’t want to be forgiven.", "게다가 (그녀는 성난 채로 거울에게 말했다) 자기는 용서받고 싶지도 않다고 했다."], ["It was not a right example, that she should be constantly stooping to be forgiven by a younger sister.", "동생보다 윗사람이면서 늘 굽실거리며 동생에게 용서받는 것은 옳은 본보기가 못 된다고 했다."], ["And this was the Art of it--that she was always being placed in the position of being forgiven, whether she liked it or not.", "그리고 바로 이게 그 수법이라고 — 자기가 좋든 싫든 늘 용서받는 처지에 놓이게 된다는 것이었다."], ["Finally she burst into violent weeping, and, when her sister came and sat close at her side to comfort her, said, ‘Amy, you’re an Angel!’", "마침내 그녀는 격렬하게 울음을 터뜨렸고, 동생이 다가와 곁에 바짝 앉아 위로하자 이렇게 말했다. \"에이미, 너는 천사야!\""]], [["‘But, I tell you what, my Pet,’ said Fanny, when her sister’s gentleness had calmed her, ‘it now comes to this; that things cannot and shall not go on as they are at present going on, and that there must be an end of this, one way or another.’", "\"하지만 내 말 좀 들어 봐, 우리 귀염둥이,\" 동생의 다정함에 마음이 가라앉자 패니가 말했다. \"이제 결론은 이거야. 일이 지금 흘러가는 대로 흘러가서는 안 되고 그래서도 안 된다는 것, 그리고 이런 상태에 이런 식으로든 저런 식으로든 끝장을 봐야 한다는 거지.\""]], [["As the announcement was vague, though very peremptory, Little Dorrit returned, ‘Let us talk about it.’", "그 선언이 매우 단호하기는 했어도 막연했으므로, 리틀 도릿은 이렇게 받았다. \"그 얘기를 해 보자.\""]], [["‘Quite so, my dear,’ assented Fanny, as she dried her eyes.", "\"바로 그거야, 얘,\" 패니가 눈물을 닦으며 동의했다."], ["‘Let us talk about it.", "\"그 얘기를 해 보자."], ["I am rational again now, and you shall advise me.", "이제 다시 차분해졌으니, 네가 나한테 조언을 해 주렴."], ["_Will_ you advise me, my sweet child?’", "나한테 조언해 주겠니, 우리 귀여운 아가?\""]], [["Even Amy smiled at this notion, but she said, ‘I will, Fanny, as well as I can.’", "에이미조차 이 생각에는 미소를 지었지만, 이렇게 말했다. \"그럴게, 패니 언니, 내가 할 수 있는 만큼.\""]], [["‘Thank you, dearest Amy,’ returned Fanny, kissing her.", "\"고맙다, 사랑하는 에이미,\" 패니가 동생에게 입을 맞추며 대꾸했다."], ["‘You are my anchor.’", "\"너는 내 닻이야.\""]], [["Having embraced her Anchor with great affection, Fanny took a bottle of sweet toilette water from the table, and called to her maid for a fine handkerchief.", "자기 닻을 무척 다정하게 끌어안은 다음, 패니는 탁자에서 향긋한 화장수 병을 집어 들고 하녀를 불러 고운 손수건을 가져오게 했다."], ["She then dismissed that attendant for the night, and went on to be advised; dabbing her eyes and forehead from time to time to cool them.", "그러고는 그 시중꾼을 그날 밤 일에서 물러가게 하고, 이따금 눈과 이마를 톡톡 두드려 식히면서 조언을 받기 시작했다."]], [["‘My love,’ Fanny began, ‘our characters and points of view are sufficiently different (kiss me again, my darling), to make it very probable that I shall surprise you by what I am going to say.", "\"얘,\" 패니가 입을 열었다."], ["What I am going to say, my dear, is, that notwithstanding our property, we labour, socially speaking, under disadvantages.", "\"우리 둘의 성격이며 보는 눈은 (다시 한번 입 맞춰 주렴, 우리 예쁜이) 제법 다르단다. 그래서 내가 이제 하려는 말에 네가 놀랄 가능성이 아주 크지."], ["You don’t quite understand what I mean, Amy?’", "내가 하려는 말은 말이야, 우리가 재산은 있어도, 사교적으로 말하자면 불리한 처지에서 애를 쓰고 있다는 거야. 내 말이 잘 이해가 안 가지, 에이미?\""]], [["‘I have no doubt I shall,’ said Amy, mildly, ‘after a few words more.’", "\"몇 마디만 더 들으면,\" 에이미가 부드럽게 말했다. \"분명 이해할 수 있을 거야.\""]], [["‘Well, my dear, what I mean is, that we are, after all, newcomers into fashionable life.’", "\"그래, 얘, 내 말은, 어쨌든 우리는 상류 생활에 새로 들어온 사람들이라는 거야.\""]], [["‘I am sure, Fanny,’ Little Dorrit interposed in her zealous admiration, ‘no one need find that out in you.’", "\"그래도 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 열렬한 감탄을 담아 끼어들었다. \"언니한테서 그걸 알아챌 사람은 아무도 없을 거야.\""]], [["‘Well, my dear child, perhaps not,’ said Fanny, ‘though it’s most kind and most affectionate in you, you precious girl, to say so.’ Here she dabbed her sister’s forehead, and blew upon it a little.", "\"글쎄, 얘, 아마 그럴지도 모르지,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘But you are,’ resumed Fanny, ‘as is well known, the dearest little thing that ever was!", "\"비록 우리 귀한 아가야, 네가 그렇게 말해 주는 건 더없이 친절하고 더없이 다정한 일이지만 말이야.\" 여기서 그녀는 동생의 이마를 톡톡 두드리고 그 위에 입김을 살짝 불었다."], ["To resume, my child.", "\"하지만 너는,\""], ["Pa is extremely gentlemanly and extremely well informed, but he is, in some trifling respects, a little different from other gentlemen of his fortune: partly on account of what he has gone through, poor dear: partly, I fancy, on account of its often running in his mind that other people are thinking about that, while he is talking to them.", "패니가 말을 이었다. \"잘 알려진 대로, 세상에 둘도 없는 사랑스러운 아이란다!"], ["Uncle, my love, is altogether unpresentable.", "다시 본론으로 돌아가자, 얘."], ["Though a dear creature to whom I am tenderly attached, he is, socially speaking, shocking.", "아빠는 더없이 신사답고 더없이 박식하시지만, 몇몇 사소한 점에서 같은 재산을 가진 다른 신사들과 조금 다르셔. 한편으로는 가엾게도 겪으신 일 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 내 생각에, 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 동안에도 저들이 그 일을 떠올리고 있으리라는 생각이 자꾸 머릿속에 맴돌기 때문이지."], ["Edward is frightfully expensive and dissipated.", "삼촌은, 얘, 도무지 남 앞에 내세울 수가 없는 분이야. 내가 애틋하게 따르는 사랑스러운 분이긴 하지만, 사교적으로 말하면 차마 못 봐줄 지경이지."], ["I don’t mean that there is anything ungenteel in that itself--far from it--but I do mean that he doesn’t do it well, and that he doesn’t, if I may so express myself, get the money’s-worth in the sort of dissipated reputation that attaches to him.’", "에드워드는 끔찍하게 돈을 헤프게 쓰고 방탕해. 그렇다고 그 자체가 천박하다는 말은 아니야 — 천만의 말씀이지 — 하지만 내 말은, 그 애가 그걸 제대로 못 한다는 거고, 이렇게 표현해도 된다면, 자기한테 따라붙는 그 방탕한 평판에서 돈값을 못 뽑아낸다는 거야.\""]], [["‘Poor Edward!’ sighed Little Dorrit, with the whole family history in the sigh.", "\"가엾은 에드워드!\" 리틀 도릿이 그 한숨 속에 온 집안의 내력을 담아 한숨지었다."]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["And poor you and me, too,’ returned Fanny, rather sharply.", "그리고 가엾은 너와 나도 마찬가지지,\" 패니가 다소 날카롭게 받았다."], ["‘Very true!", "\"딱 맞는 말이야!"], ["Then, my dear, we have no mother, and we have a Mrs General.", "그리고 얘, 우리한테는 어머니가 없고, 대신 제너럴 부인이 있어."], ["And I tell you again, darling, that Mrs General, if I may reverse a common proverb and adapt it to her, is a cat in gloves who _will_ catch mice.", "다시 말하지만, 얘, 제너럴 부인은 흔한 속담을 뒤집어 그녀에게 맞춰 보자면, 쥐를 기어이 잡고 마는 장갑 낀 고양이야."], ["That woman, I am quite sure and confident, will be our mother-in-law.’", "그 여자는, 나는 아주 확신하고 단언컨대, 우리 시어머니가 될 사람이야.\""]], [["‘I can hardly think, Fanny--’ Fanny stopped her.", "\"그렇게는 생각하기 어려운데, 언니—\" 패니가 동생의 말을 막았다."]], [["‘Now, don’t argue with me about it, Amy,’ said she, ‘because I know better.’ Feeling that she had been sharp again, she dabbed her sister’s forehead again, and blew upon it again. ‘To resume once more, my dear. It then becomes a question with me (I am proud and spirited, Amy, as you very well know: too much so, I dare say) whether I shall make up my mind to take it upon myself to carry the family through.’", "\"이 일을 두고 나하고 따지지 마, 에이미,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 더 잘 아니까.\" 또 날카로워졌다는 것을 느끼고, 그녀는 동생의 이마를 다시 톡톡 두드리고 그 위에 다시 입김을 불었다. \"다시 한번 본론으로 돌아가자, 얘. 그러면 나한테 이런 문제가 생기는 거야 (나는 자존심이 세고 기개가 있지, 에이미, 너도 잘 알다시피. 아마 지나치게 그럴 테고) — 내가 마음을 다잡고 이 집안을 끌고 나갈 책임을 떠맡을지 말지 하는 문제 말이야.\""]], [["‘How?’ asked her sister, anxiously.", "\"어떻게?\" 동생이 불안하게 물었다."]], [["‘I will not,’ said Fanny, without answering the question, ‘submit to be mother-in-lawed by Mrs General; and I will not submit to be, in any respect whatever, either patronised or tormented by Mrs Merdle.’", "\"나는,\" 패니가 그 물음에는 대답하지 않고 말했다. \"제너럴 부인한테 시어머니 노릇을 당하며 굴복하지 않을 거야. 그리고 머들 부인한테, 그 어떤 점에서든, 생색을 받거나 시달림을 받으며 굴복하지도 않을 거야.\""]], [["Little Dorrit laid her hand upon the hand that held the bottle of sweet water, with a still more anxious look.", "리틀 도릿은 향긋한 화장수 병을 든 그 손 위에, 한층 더 불안한 눈빛으로 자기 손을 얹었다."], ["Fanny, quite punishing her own forehead with the vehement dabs she now began to give it, fitfully went on.", "패니는 이제 자기 이마를 격렬하게 톡톡 두드려 그야말로 벌하다시피 하면서, 변덕스럽게 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["‘That he has somehow or other, and how is of no consequence, attained a very good position, no one can deny.", "\"그가 어떻게든, 그 방법이야 어떻든 상관없지만, 아주 좋은 지위에 올랐다는 건 아무도 부인 못 해."], ["That it is a very good connection, no one can deny.", "그게 아주 좋은 혼처라는 것도 아무도 부인 못 하고."], ["And as to the question of clever or not clever, I doubt very much whether a clever husband would be suitable to me.", "그리고 영리하냐 아니냐의 문제로 말하자면, 영리한 남편이 나한테 어울릴지는 무척 의심스러워."], ["I cannot submit.", "나는 굴복 못 하니까."], ["I should not be able to defer to him enough.’", "그한테 충분히 고분고분 따를 수가 없을 테니까.\""]], [["‘O, my dear Fanny!’ expostulated Little Dorrit, upon whom a kind of terror had been stealing as she perceived what her sister meant.", "\"오, 패니 언니!\" 언니가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 알아채면서 일종의 공포가 스며들던 리틀 도릿이 항변했다."], ["‘If you loved any one, all this feeling would change.", "\"언니가 누군가를 사랑한다면, 이 모든 마음이 달라질 거야."], ["If you loved any one, you would no more be yourself, but you would quite lose and forget yourself in your devotion to him.", "언니가 누군가를 사랑한다면, 더는 언니 자신이 아니라, 그에 대한 헌신 속에서 자신을 완전히 잃고 잊어버리게 될 거야."], ["If you loved him, Fanny--’ Fanny had stopped the dabbing hand, and was looking at her fixedly.", "언니가 그를 사랑한다면, 패니 언니—\" 패니는 톡톡 두드리던 손을 멈추고, 동생을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었다."]], [["‘O, indeed!’ cried Fanny.", "\"오, 정말이지!\" 패니가 외쳤다."], ["‘Really?", "\"그래?"], ["Bless me, how much some people know of some subjects!", "어머나, 어떤 주제에 대해선 어떤 사람들이 어찌 그리 많이 아는지!"], ["They say every one has a subject, and I certainly seem to have hit upon yours, Amy.", "다들 사람마다 잘 아는 주제가 하나씩 있다고들 하던데, 내가 정말로 네 주제를 콕 짚어낸 모양이로구나, 에이미. 자, 이 작은 것아, 농담이었어,\""], ["There, you little thing, I was only in fun,’ dabbing her sister’s forehead; ‘but don’t you be a silly puss, and don’t you think flightily and eloquently about degenerate impossibilities.", "동생의 이마를 톡톡 두드리며 말했다. \"하지만 너는 어리석은 새침데기가 되지 말고, 이루어질 수도 없는 한심한 것들을 두고 들떠서 그럴듯하게 늘어놓지도 마."], ["There!", "자!"], ["Now, I’ll go back to myself.’", "이제 다시 내 얘기로 돌아갈게.\""]], [["‘Dear Fanny, let me say first, that I would far rather we worked for a scanty living again than I would see you rich and married to Mr Sparkler.’", "\"패니 언니, 먼저 이 말만 하게 해 줘. 언니가 부자가 되어 스파클러 씨와 결혼하는 걸 보느니, 차라리 우리가 다시 빠듯하게 입에 풀칠하며 일하는 게 나는 훨씬 더 좋아.\""]], [["‘_Let_ you say, my dear?’ retorted Fanny.", "\"하게 해 달라고, 얘?\" 패니가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Why, of course, I will _let_ you say anything.", "\"아니, 당연히, 너는 무슨 말이든 하게 해 줄게."], ["There is no constraint upon you, I hope.", "너한테 무슨 강제가 있는 건 아니길 바라."], ["We are together to talk it over.", "우리는 함께 이 일을 의논하는 중이잖니."], ["And as to marrying Mr Sparkler, I have not the slightest intention of doing so to-night, my dear, or to-morrow morning either.’", "그리고 스파클러 씨와 결혼하는 일로 말하자면, 나는 오늘 밤에 그럴 생각이 조금도 없어, 얘, 내일 아침에도 마찬가지고.\""]], [["‘But at some time?’", "\"그래도 언젠가는?\""]], [["‘At no time, for anything I know at present,’ answered Fanny, with indifference.", "\"지금 내가 아는 한, 언제고 절대 아니야,\" 패니가 무심하게 대답했다."], ["Then, suddenly changing her indifference into a burning restlessness, she added, ‘You talk about the clever men, you little thing!", "그러더니 갑자기 그 무심함을 타는 듯한 안절부절못함으로 바꾸며 덧붙였다. \"너는 영리한 남자 얘기를 하는구나, 이 작은 것아!"], ["It’s all very fine and easy to talk about the clever men; but where are they?", "영리한 남자 얘기를 하는 건 다 좋고 쉽지. 그런데 그런 남자들이 어디 있어?"], ["_I_ don’t see them anywhere near _me_!’", "내 주변 어디에도 그런 남자는 안 보인다고!\""]], [["‘My dear Fanny, so short a time--’", "\"패니 언니, 이렇게 짧은 시간에는—\""]], [["‘Short time or long time,’ interrupted Fanny.", "\"짧은 시간이든 긴 시간이든,\" 패니가 말을 끊었다."], ["‘I am impatient of our situation.", "\"나는 우리 처지가 못 견디게 갑갑해."], ["I don’t like our situation, and very little would induce me to change it.", "우리 처지가 마음에 안 들고, 그걸 바꾸는 데는 아주 사소한 계기만으로도 충분해."], ["Other girls, differently reared and differently circumstanced altogether, might wonder at what I say or may do.", "다르게 자라고 처지가 완전히 다른 다른 여자아이들은 내가 하는 말이나 하려는 일에 의아해할지도 모르지."], ["Let them.", "그러라지."], ["They are driven by their lives and characters; I am driven by mine.’", "그 애들은 제 삶과 성격에 떠밀려 가는 거고, 나는 내 것에 떠밀려 가는 거니까.\""]], [["‘Fanny, my dear Fanny, you know that you have qualities to make you the wife of one very superior to Mr Sparkler.’", "\"패니, 패니 언니, 언니는 스파클러 씨보다 훨씬 뛰어난 사람의 아내가 될 만한 자질이 있다는 걸 알잖아.\""]], [["‘Amy, my dear Amy,’ retorted Fanny, parodying her words, ‘I know that I wish to have a more defined and distinct position, in which I can assert myself with greater effect against that insolent woman.’", "\"에이미, 에이미야,\" 패니가 동생의 말을 흉내 내며 받아쳤다. \"나는 그 무례한 여자에게 더 큰 효과로 나 자신을 내세울 수 있는, 더 명확하고 확고한 지위를 갖고 싶다는 걸 안단다.\""]], [["‘Would you therefore--forgive my asking, Fanny--therefore marry her son?’", "\"그래서 — 이런 걸 물어 미안하지만, 패니 언니 — 그래서 그 여자의 아들과 결혼하겠다는 거야?\""]], [["‘Why, perhaps,’ said Fanny, with a triumphant smile.", "\"글쎄, 어쩌면,\" 패니가 의기양양한 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["‘There may be many less promising ways of arriving at an end than that, my dear.", "\"그 목적에 이르는 데는 그보다 가망 없는 길이 얼마든지 있을 수 있어, 얘."], ["That piece of insolence may think, now, that it would be a great success to get her son off upon me, and shelve me.", "그 무례한 여자는 지금, 자기 아들을 나한테 떠넘기고 나를 한쪽으로 치워 버리면 큰 성공이라고 여길지도 모르지."], ["But, perhaps, she little thinks how I would retort upon her if I married her son.", "하지만, 어쩌면, 내가 그 아들과 결혼하면 그 여자한테 어떻게 앙갚음할지 그녀는 짐작도 못 할걸."], ["I would oppose her in everything, and compete with her.", "나는 모든 일에서 그 여자에게 맞서고, 그 여자와 겨룰 거야."], ["I would make it the business of my life.’", "그걸 내 평생의 과업으로 삼을 거야.\""]], [["Fanny set down the bottle when she came to this, and walked about the room; always stopping and standing still while she spoke.", "여기에 이르자 패니는 병을 내려놓고 방 안을 거닐었다. 말을 할 때면 늘 멈춰 서서 가만히 있었다."]], [["‘One thing I could certainly do, my child: I could make her older.", "\"한 가지는 확실히 할 수 있어, 얘. 나는 그 여자를 더 늙어 보이게 만들 수 있어."], ["And I would!’", "그리고 그렇게 할 거야!\""]], [["This was followed by another walk.", "이 말에 이어 또 한 차례 거닐었다."]], [["‘I would talk of her as an old woman.", "\"나는 그 여자를 노파라고 떠들어 댈 거야."], ["I would pretend to know--if I didn’t, but I should from her son--all about her age.", "그 여자 나이에 대해 다 아는 척할 거야 — 비록 몰라도, 그 아들한테서 알아낼 테니까."], ["And she should hear me say, Amy: affectionately, quite dutifully and affectionately: how well she looked, considering her time of life.", "그리고 에이미, 그 여자가 내 말을 듣게 만들 거야. 다정하게, 아주 효성스럽고 다정하게, 그 연세를 생각하면 참 정정해 보이신다고 말이야."], ["I could make her seem older at once, by being myself so much younger.", "나는 내가 그렇게나 훨씬 젊으니까, 그 여자를 단번에 더 늙어 보이게 만들 수 있어."], ["I may not be as handsome as she is; I am not a fair judge of that question, I suppose; but I know I am handsome enough to be a thorn in her side.", "내가 그 여자만큼 예쁘진 않을지도 모르지. 그 문제에 대해선 내가 공정한 판관은 못 되겠지만, 그 여자의 옆구리에 박힌 가시 노릇은 충분히 할 만큼 예쁘다는 건 알아."], ["And I would be!’", "그리고 그렇게 할 거야!\""]], [["‘My dear sister, would you condemn yourself to an unhappy life for this?’", "\"언니, 이런 일 때문에 스스로를 불행한 삶에 옭아매겠다는 거야?\""]], [["‘It wouldn’t be an unhappy life, Amy.", "\"불행한 삶은 아닐 거야, 에이미."], ["It would be the life I am fitted for.", "그건 내가 어울리는 삶일 거야."], ["Whether by disposition, or whether by circumstances, is no matter; I am better fitted for such a life than for almost any other.’", "타고난 기질 탓이든 처지 탓이든 그건 상관없어. 나는 그런 삶에 다른 거의 어떤 삶보다 더 잘 맞으니까.\""]], [["There was something of a desolate tone in those words; but, with a short proud laugh she took another walk, and after passing a great looking-glass came to another stop.", "그 말에는 어딘가 황량한 어조가 깃들어 있었지만, 그녀는 짤막하게 거만한 웃음을 짓고 다시 한번 거닐다가, 커다란 거울 앞을 지나치고는 또 한 번 멈춰 섰다."]], [["‘Figure!", "\"몸매!"], ["Figure, Amy!", "몸매라, 에이미!"], ["Well.", "그래."], ["The woman has a good figure.", "그 여자는 몸매가 좋지."], ["I will give her her due, and not deny it.", "마땅한 건 인정해 주고 부인하지 않을게."], ["But is it so far beyond all others that it is altogether unapproachable?", "그렇지만 그게 다른 모든 사람을 멀찍이 뛰어넘어 도무지 따라잡을 수 없을 정도일까?"], ["Upon my word, I am not so sure of it.", "정말이지, 나는 그렇게 확신하지 않아."], ["Give some much younger woman the latitude as to dress that she has, being married; and we would see about that, my dear!’", "그 여자가 결혼한 몸이라 누리는 만큼의 옷차림 여유를, 훨씬 젊은 여자한테 주어 봐. 그러면 그 문제는 두고 보자고, 얘!\""]], [["Something in the thought that was agreeable and flattering, brought her back to her seat in a gayer temper.", "그 생각 속에 무언가 흐뭇하고 우쭐한 것이 있어, 그녀는 한결 명랑한 기분으로 자리로 돌아왔다."], ["She took her sister’s hands in hers, and clapped all four hands above her head as she looked in her sister’s face laughing:", "그녀는 동생의 두 손을 자기 손에 쥐고, 동생의 얼굴을 들여다보며 웃으면서 네 손을 모두 머리 위로 마주쳤다."]], [["‘And the dancer, Amy, that she has quite forgotten--the dancer who bore no sort of resemblance to me, and of whom I never remind her, oh dear no!--should dance through her life, and dance in her way, to such a tune as would disturb her insolent placidity a little.", "\"그리고 에이미, 그 여자가 까맣게 잊어버린 그 무희 말이야 — 나하고는 전혀 닮은 데가 없고, 내가 결코 그 여자한테 떠올려 주지 않는 그 무희 — 오, 천만에! — 그 무희가 그 여자의 삶을 가로지르며 춤을 추는 거야."], ["Just a little, my dear Amy, just a little!’", "그 여자의 길 위에서, 그 거만한 평온을 조금쯤 흔들어 놓을 만한 곡조에 맞춰서. 딱 조금만, 에이미, 딱 조금만!\""]], [["Meeting an earnest and imploring look in Amy’s face, she brought the four hands down, and laid only one on Amy’s lips.", "에이미의 얼굴에서 진지하고 애원하는 듯한 눈빛을 마주하자, 그녀는 네 손을 내리고 그중 한 손만을 에이미의 입술 위에 얹었다."]], [["‘Now, don’t argue with me, child,’ she said in a sterner way, ‘because it is of no use.", "\"자, 이 일로 나하고 따지지 마, 얘,\" 그녀가 한층 엄한 투로 말했다."], ["I understand these subjects much better than you do.", "\"아무 소용 없으니까. 나는 이런 주제들을 너보다 훨씬 더 잘 알아."], ["I have not nearly made up my mind, but it may be.", "아직 마음을 거의 정하진 못했지만, 어쩌면 그렇게 될지도 몰라."], ["Now we have talked this over comfortably, and may go to bed.", "이제 이 얘기를 편하게 나누었으니, 자러 가도 되겠어."], ["You best and dearest little mouse, Good night!’ With those words Fanny weighed her Anchor, and--having taken so much advice--left off being advised for that occasion.", "너야말로 더없이 착하고 더없이 사랑스러운 작은 생쥐야, 잘 자거라!\" 그 말과 함께 패니는 자기 닻을 끌어올렸고 — 그토록 많은 조언을 받았으니 — 그날만큼은 조언받기를 그만두었다."]], [["Thenceforward, Amy observed Mr Sparkler’s treatment by his enslaver, with new reasons for attaching importance to all that passed between them.", "그때부터 에이미는, 스파클러 씨가 그를 사로잡은 여자에게 어떻게 다루어지는지를, 두 사람 사이에 오가는 모든 일에 새삼 무게를 둘 새로운 이유를 안고 지켜보았다."], ["There were times when Fanny appeared quite unable to endure his mental feebleness, and when she became so sharply impatient of it that she would all but dismiss him for good.", "패니가 그의 정신적 둔함을 도무지 견딜 수 없어 하는 듯한 때가 있었고, 그것에 어찌나 날카롭게 짜증을 내던지 하마터면 그를 영영 내쫓아 버릴 뻔한 때도 있었다."], ["There were other times when she got on much better with him; when he amused her, and when her sense of superiority seemed to counterbalance that opposite side of the scale.", "또 그녀가 그와 훨씬 잘 지내는 때도 있었으니, 그가 그녀를 즐겁게 해 주고, 그녀의 우월감이 저울의 반대편 접시와 균형을 이루는 듯한 때였다."], ["If Mr Sparkler had been other than the faithfullest and most submissive of swains, he was sufficiently hard pressed to have fled from the scene of his trials, and have set at least the whole distance from Rome to London between himself and his enchantress.", "만약 스파클러 씨가 더없이 충직하고 더없이 순종적인 연모자가 아니었더라면, 그는 그 시련의 무대에서 달아나, 적어도 로마에서 런던에 이르는 그 온 거리만큼이나 자기와 자기 마법사 사이를 벌려 놓을 만큼 호되게 시달렸을 것이다."], ["But he had no greater will of his own than a boat has when it is towed by a steam-ship; and he followed his cruel mistress through rough and smooth, on equally strong compulsion.", "그러나 그는 증기선에 끌려가는 거룻배만큼도 제 의지가 없었으므로, 거친 데서나 잔잔한 데서나 한결같이 강한 강압에 떠밀려 자기의 잔혹한 여주인을 따라다녔다."]], [["Mrs Merdle, during these passages, said little to Fanny, but said more about her.", "머들 부인은 이 무렵 패니에게는 거의 말을 하지 않았으나, 그녀에 관한 말은 더 많이 했다."], ["She was, as it were, forced to look at her through her eye-glass, and in general conversation to allow commendations of her beauty to be wrung from her by its irresistible demands.", "그녀는, 말하자면, 외알 안경 너머로 패니를 바라볼 수밖에 없었고, 일반적인 대화에서는 그 거역할 수 없는 요구에 떠밀려 패니의 미모에 대한 찬사를 어쩔 수 없이 내뱉곤 했다. 패니가 이런 칭송을 들을 때면 (대개 듣게 마련이었는데) 그녀의 얼굴에 떠오르는 도전적인 기색은, 그 공평무사한 가슴에 양보하는 표정은 결코 아니었다."], ["The defiant character it assumed when Fanny heard these extollings (as it generally happened that she did), was not expressive of concessions to the impartial bosom; but the utmost revenge the bosom took was, to say audibly, ‘A spoilt beauty--but with that face and shape, who could wonder?’", "그러나 그 가슴이 취하는 최대의 복수란, 들으라는 듯이 이렇게 말하는 것이었다. \"버릇없이 자란 미인이지요 — 하지만 저런 얼굴과 몸매라면, 누가 놀라겠어요?\""]], [["It might have been about a month or six weeks after the night of the new advice, when Little Dorrit began to think she detected some new understanding between Mr Sparkler and Fanny.", "그 새로운 조언이 있던 밤으로부터 한 달이나 여섯 주쯤 지났을 무렵, 리틀 도릿은 스파클러 씨와 패니 사이에 무언가 새로운 양해가 생긴 것을 눈치챘다고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["Mr Sparkler, as if in attendance to some compact, scarcely ever spoke without first looking towards Fanny for leave.", "스파클러 씨는 무슨 약조라도 지키는 듯, 먼저 패니 쪽을 바라보며 허락을 구하지 않고는 거의 입을 떼는 법이 없었다."], ["That young lady was too discreet ever to look back again; but, if Mr Sparkler had permission to speak, she remained silent; if he had not, she herself spoke.", "그 젊은 아가씨는 너무도 신중해 결코 마주 보아 답하는 법이 없었지만, 스파클러 씨가 말해도 좋다는 허락을 받으면 그녀는 잠자코 있었고, 허락받지 못하면 그녀 자신이 말했다."], ["Moreover, it became plain whenever Henry Gowan attempted to perform the friendly office of drawing him out, that he was not to be drawn.", "게다가, 헨리 가우언이 그의 말문을 끌어내는 그 우정 어린 일을 해 보려 할 때마다, 그가 도무지 끌려 나오지 않으리라는 것이 분명해졌다."], ["And not only that, but Fanny would presently, without any pointed application in the world, chance to say something with such a sting in it that Gowan would draw back as if he had put his hand into a bee-hive.", "그뿐 아니라, 패니는 곧, 세상에 콕 집어 누구를 겨냥한 것도 아니면서, 어쩌다 가시가 잔뜩 박힌 한마디를 던지곤 했는데, 그러면 가우언은 마치 벌집 속에 손을 넣었다가 뺀 사람처럼 움찔 물러서곤 했다."]], [["There was yet another circumstance which went a long way to confirm Little Dorrit in her fears, though it was not a great circumstance in itself.", "리틀 도릿이 자기 두려움을 굳히는 데 큰 몫을 한 사정이 또 하나 있었으니, 그 자체로 대단한 일은 아니었다."], ["Mr Sparkler’s demeanour towards herself changed.", "스파클러 씨가 그녀를 대하는 태도가 달라진 것이다."], ["It became fraternal.", "그것이 오누이처럼 되었다."], ["Sometimes, when she was in the outer circle of assemblies--at their own residence, at Mrs Merdle’s, or elsewhere--she would find herself stealthily supported round the waist by Mr Sparkler’s arm.", "때때로 그녀가 모임의 바깥쪽 가장자리에 있을 때면 — 자기들 거처에서든, 머들 부인 댁에서든, 다른 어디에서든 — 어느새 스파클러 씨의 팔이 그녀의 허리를 슬그머니 감싸 받치고 있는 것을 알아채곤 했다."], ["Mr Sparkler never offered the slightest explanation of this attention; but merely smiled with an air of blundering, contented, good-natured proprietorship, which, in so heavy a gentleman, was ominously expressive.", "스파클러 씨는 이 살가운 행동에 대해 조금도 설명하는 법이 없었고, 다만 어설프고 흡족하며 사람 좋은 소유주 같은 태도로 미소 지을 뿐이었는데, 그토록 둔한 신사에게서 나오니 그것이 불길하게도 의미심장했다."]], [["Little Dorrit was at home one day, thinking about Fanny with a heavy heart.", "리틀 도릿은 어느 날 집에서, 무거운 마음으로 패니를 생각하고 있었다."], ["They had a room at one end of their drawing-room suite, nearly all irregular bay-window, projecting over the street, and commanding all the picturesque life and variety of the Corso, both up and down.", "그들의 응접실 한쪽 끝에는, 거의 전체가 불규칙한 퇴창으로 이루어져 거리 위로 돌출해 있고, 코르소 거리의 그 그림 같은 활기와 다채로움을 위아래로 죄다 굽어볼 수 있는 방이 하나 있었다."], ["At three or four o’clock in the afternoon, English time, the view from this window was very bright and peculiar; and Little Dorrit used to sit and muse here, much as she had been used to while away the time in her balcony at Venice.", "영국 시각으로 오후 서너 시쯤이면, 이 창에서 내다보이는 풍경은 무척 환하고 독특했다. 그래서 리틀 도릿은 베네치아에서 발코니에 앉아 시간을 흘려보내곤 하던 것과 흡사하게, 여기 앉아 곧잘 생각에 잠기곤 했다."], ["Seated thus one day, she was softly touched on the shoulder, and Fanny said, ‘Well, Amy dear,’ and took her seat at her side.", "어느 날 이렇게 앉아 있는데, 누군가 부드럽게 그녀의 어깨를 건드렸고, 패니가 \"에이미, 잘 있었니,\" 하고는 그녀 곁에 자리를 잡았다."], ["Their seat was a part of the window; when there was anything in the way of a procession going on, they used to have bright draperies hung out of the window, and used to kneel or sit on this seat, and look out at it, leaning on the brilliant colour.", "그들이 앉은 자리는 그 창의 일부였다. 무슨 행렬 같은 것이 지나갈 때면, 그들은 창밖으로 화사한 휘장을 늘어뜨리고, 이 자리에 무릎을 꿇거나 앉아서, 그 찬란한 빛깔에 기대어 행렬을 내다보곤 했다."], ["But there was no procession that day, and Little Dorrit was rather surprised by Fanny’s being at home at that hour, as she was generally out on horseback then.", "그러나 그날은 행렬이 없었고, 리틀 도릿은 평소 그 시각이면 으레 말을 타고 나가 있던 패니가 그 시간에 집에 있는 것이 다소 의아했다."]], [["‘Well, Amy,’ said Fanny, ‘what are you thinking of, little one?’", "\"그래, 에이미,\" 패니가 말했다. \"무슨 생각을 하고 있었니, 꼬마야?\""]], [["‘I was thinking of you, Fanny.’", "\"언니 생각을 하고 있었어, 패니.\""]], [["‘No?", "\"설마?"], ["What a coincidence!", "이런 우연이 다 있나!"], ["I declare here’s some one else.", "어머, 여기 또 다른 누가 왔네."], ["You were not thinking of this some one else too; were you, Amy?’", "너 이 다른 누군가 생각도 하고 있었던 건 아니지, 그렇지, 에이미?\""]], [["Amy _had_ been thinking of this some one else too; for it was Mr Sparkler.", "에이미는 사실 이 다른 누군가에 대해서도 생각하고 있었다. 그것은 바로 스파클러 씨였으니까."], ["She did not say so, however, as she gave him her hand.", "그러나 그녀는 그렇게 말하지 않고 그에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Mr Sparkler came and sat down on the other side of her, and she felt the fraternal railing come behind her, and apparently stretch on to include Fanny.", "스파클러 씨는 다가와 그녀의 반대편에 앉았고, 그녀는 그 오누이 같은 난간이 자기 등 뒤로 둘러지더니, 패니까지 아우르려는 듯 죽 뻗는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["‘Well, my little sister,’ said Fanny with a sigh, ‘I suppose you know what this means?’", "\"그래, 내 어린 동생,\" 패니가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인지 짐작하겠지?\""]], [["‘She’s as beautiful as she’s doated on,’ stammered Mr Sparkler--‘and there’s no nonsense about her--it’s arranged--’", "\"이분은 사랑받는 만큼이나 아름다우시고,\" 스파클러 씨가 더듬거렸다. \"게다가 도무지 허튼 데가 없는 분이지요 — 이제 정해졌습니다—\""]], [["‘You needn’t explain, Edmund,’ said Fanny.", "\"굳이 설명할 것 없어요, 에드먼드,\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘No, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"그래요, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘In short, pet,’ proceeded Fanny, ‘on the whole, we are engaged.", "\"한마디로, 얘,\" 패니가 말을 이었다."], ["We must tell papa about it either to-night or to-morrow, according to the opportunities.", "\"결국, 우리는 약혼했단다. 기회를 봐서 오늘 밤이나 내일, 아빠한테 이 얘기를 해야 해."], ["Then it’s done, and very little more need be said.’", "그러면 끝난 거고, 더 할 말도 거의 없는 거지.\""]], [["‘My dear Fanny,’ said Mr Sparkler, with deference, ‘I should like to say a word to Amy.’", "\"패니,\" 스파클러 씨가 공손하게 말했다. \"에이미한테 한마디 하고 싶은데요.\""]], [["‘Well, well!", "\"그래요, 그래요!"], ["Say it for goodness’ sake,’ returned the young lady.", "제발 말해 봐요,\" 젊은 아가씨가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘I am convinced, my dear Amy,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘that if ever there was a girl, next to your highly endowed and beautiful sister, who had no nonsense about her--’", "\"나는 확신합니다, 에이미,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"당신의 더없이 자질이 빼어나고 아름다운 언니 다음으로, 만약 세상에 도무지 허튼 데가 없는 아가씨가 있었다면 그건—\""]], [["‘We know all about that, Edmund,’ interposed Miss Fanny.", "\"그건 우리도 다 알아요, 에드먼드,\" 패니 양이 끼어들었다."], ["‘Never mind that.", "\"그 얘긴 됐어요."], ["Pray go on to something else besides our having no nonsense about us.’", "제발 우리한테 허튼 데가 없다는 것 말고 다른 얘기로 넘어가요.\""]], [["‘Yes, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"네, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘And I assure you, Amy, that nothing can be a greater happiness to myself, myself--next to the happiness of being so highly honoured with the choice of a glorious girl who hasn’t an atom of--’", "\"그리고 단언하건대, 에이미, 내게 이보다 더 큰 행복은 없을 겁니다, 내게는요 — 티끌만큼도 허튼 데가 없는, 그 빛나는 아가씨의 선택을 받는 더없는 영광의 행복 다음으로 말입니다—\""]], [["‘Pray, Edmund, pray!’ interrupted Fanny, with a slight pat of her pretty foot upon the floor.", "\"제발요, 에드먼드, 제발!\" 패니가 그 예쁜 발로 마룻바닥을 가볍게 톡 치며 말을 끊었다."]], [["‘My love, you’re quite right,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘and I know I have a habit of it. What I wished to declare was, that nothing can be a greater happiness to myself, myself-next to the happiness of being united to pre-eminently the most glorious of girls--than to have the happiness of cultivating the affectionate acquaintance of Amy. I may not myself,’ said Mr Sparkler manfully, ‘be up to the mark on some other subjects at a short notice, and I am aware that if you were to poll Society the general opinion would be that I am not; but on the subject of Amy I AM up to the mark!’", "\"내 사랑, 당신 말이 옳소,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"내게 그런 버릇이 있다는 거 압니다. 내가 하고 싶었던 말은, 더없이 빼어나게 빛나는 아가씨와 맺어지는 행복 다음으로, 내게 이보다 더 큰 행복은 없으리라는 것 — 내게는요 — 곧 에이미와 다정한 교분을 쌓아 가는 행복 말입니다. 다른 몇몇 주제에 대해선 갑작스레 닥치면 내가 수준에 못 미칠지도 모르고,\" 스파클러 씨가 사내답게 말했다. \"또 사교계에 여론을 물으면 다들 내가 못 미친다고 할 거라는 것도 압니다. 하지만 에이미라는 주제에 대해선 나도 수준에 충분히 미칩니다!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler kissed her, in witness thereof.", "스파클러 씨는 그 증거로 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘A knife and fork and an apartment,’ proceeded Mr Sparkler, growing, in comparison with his oratorical antecedents, quite diffuse, ‘will ever be at Amy’s disposal. My Governor, I am sure, will always be proud to entertain one whom I so much esteem. And regarding my mother,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘who is a remarkably fine woman, with--’", "\"나이프와 포크 한 벌, 그리고 방 한 칸은,\" 스파클러 씨가, 자기의 지난 연설 솜씨에 비하면 제법 술술 풀려 나오며 말을 이었다. \"언제든 에이미가 마음대로 쓸 수 있도록 늘 마련될 겁니다. 우리 영감님께서도, 분명, 내가 그토록 존경하는 사람을 손님으로 맞는 걸 늘 자랑스러워하실 겁니다. 그리고 우리 어머니로 말하자면,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다. \"참으로 빼어난 여성이신데, 게다가—\""]], [["‘Edmund, Edmund!’ cried Miss Fanny, as before.", "\"에드먼드, 에드먼드!\" 패니 양이 또다시 외쳤다."]], [["‘With submission, my soul,’ pleaded Mr Sparkler.", "\"이 말만은 봐주오, 내 영혼,\" 스파클러 씨가 사정했다."], ["‘I know I have a habit of it, and I thank you very much, my adorable girl, for taking the trouble to correct it; but my mother is admitted on all sides to be a remarkably fine woman, and she really hasn’t any.’", "\"내게 그런 버릇이 있다는 거 알고, 그걸 바로잡아 주려 애써 주는 당신께, 나의 사랑스러운 아가씨, 진심으로 고맙소. 하지만 우리 어머니는 누구나 인정하다시피 참으로 빼어난 여성이신데, 정말이지 조금도 허튼—\""]], [["‘That may be, or may not be,’ returned Fanny, ‘but pray don’t mention it any more.’", "\"그럴 수도 있고 아닐 수도 있겠죠,\" 패니가 받았다. \"하지만 제발 그 얘긴 더 이상 꺼내지 말아요.\""]], [["‘I will not, my love,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"안 하겠소, 내 사랑,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Then, in fact, you have nothing more to say, Edmund; have you?’ inquired Fanny.", "\"그러면, 사실상, 더 할 말은 없는 거죠, 에드먼드, 그렇죠?\" 패니가 물었다."]], [["‘So far from it, my adorable girl,’ answered Mr Sparkler, ‘I apologise for having said so much.’", "\"전혀 없고말고요, 나의 사랑스러운 아가씨,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"오히려 이렇게 말을 많이 한 걸 사과드립니다.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler perceived, by a kind of inspiration, that the question implied had he not better go?", "스파클러 씨는 일종의 영감으로, 그 물음이 이제 그만 가는 게 낫지 않겠느냐는 뜻을 담고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["He therefore withdrew the fraternal railing, and neatly said that he thought he would, with submission, take his leave.", "그래서 그는 오누이 같은 난간을 거두고는, 송구하지만 이만 물러가는 게 좋겠다고 정중히 말했다."], ["He did not go without being congratulated by Amy, as well as she could discharge that office in the flutter and distress of her spirits.", "그는 에이미에게서, 그녀가 들뜨고 괴로운 심정 속에서나마 그 임무를 다해 할 수 있는 만큼의 축하를 받고서야 떠났다."]], [["When he was gone, she said, ‘O Fanny, Fanny!’ and turned to her sister in the bright window, and fell upon her bosom and cried there.", "그가 가고 나자, 그녀는 \"오, 패니, 패니!\" 하고 환한 창가에 있는 언니에게로 돌아서더니, 그 가슴에 안겨 거기서 울었다."], ["Fanny laughed at first; but soon laid her face against her sister’s and cried too--a little.", "패니는 처음엔 웃었지만, 곧 자기 얼굴을 동생의 얼굴에 맞대고 그녀도 울었다 — 조금."], ["It was the last time Fanny ever showed that there was any hidden, suppressed, or conquered feeling in her on the matter.", "패니가 이 일에 관하여 자기 안에 숨겨졌거나 억눌렸거나 정복당한 어떤 감정이 있음을 내비친 것은 그때가 마지막이었다."], ["From that hour the way she had chosen lay before her, and she trod it with her own imperious self-willed step.", "그 시각부터, 그녀가 택한 길이 그녀 앞에 놓였고, 그녀는 그 길을 제 거만하고 고집스러운 걸음으로 밟아 나갔다."]], [["CHAPTER 15. No just Cause or Impediment why these Two Persons should not be joined together\nMr Dorrit, on being informed by his elder daughter that she had accepted matrimonial overtures from Mr Sparkler, to whom she had plighted her troth, received the communication at once with great dignity and with a large display of parental pride; his dignity dilating with the widened prospect of advantageous ground from which to make acquaintances, and his parental pride being developed by Miss Fanny’s ready sympathy with that great object of his existence.", "장녀로부터, 자신이 스파클러 씨의 청혼을 받아들였으며 그에게 백년가약을 맺었노라는 통보를 받자, 도릿 씨는 그 소식을 일변 크나큰 위엄으로, 일변 부성적 자긍심을 한껏 펼쳐 보이며 받아들였다."], ["He gave her to understand that her noble ambition found harmonious echoes in his heart; and bestowed his blessing on her, as a child brimful of duty and good principle, self-devoted to the aggrandisement of the family name.", "유리한 처지에서 교제를 넓혀 갈 전망이 한층 넓어지자 그의 위엄은 부풀어 올랐고, 그의 부성적 자긍심은 자기 생애의 그 위대한 목적에 패니 양이 선뜻 공명해 준 덕에 발현되었다. 그는 그녀의 고결한 야심이 자기 가슴속에서 조화로운 메아리를 찾았음을 알려 주었으며, 의무감과 올바른 신조로 가득 차서 집안의 명성을 드높이는 데 스스로를 바친 자식에게 하듯 그녀에게 축복을 내렸다."]], [["To Mr Sparkler, when Miss Fanny permitted him to appear, Mr Dorrit said, he would not disguise that the alliance Mr Sparkler did him the honour to propose was highly congenial to his feelings; both as being in unison with the spontaneous affections of his daughter Fanny, and as opening a family connection of a gratifying nature with Mr Merdle, the master spirit of the age.", "패니 양이 그가 모습을 드러내도록 허락했을 때, 도릿 씨는 스파클러 씨에게 이렇게 말했다. 스파클러 씨가 영광스럽게도 제의해 온 이 결연이 자기 심정에 더없이 흡족하다는 사실을 굳이 감추지 않겠노라고."], ["Mrs Merdle also, as a leading lady rich in distinction, elegance, grace, and beauty, he mentioned in very laudatory terms.", "그것은 딸 패니의 자발적인 애정과 부합하는 동시에, 당대의 거두인 머들 씨와 흐뭇한 성격의 인척 관계를 열어 주기 때문이라고. 그는 또한 머들 부인에 대해서도, 품격과 우아함과 기품과 미모가 넘치는 일류 부인이라며 무척 칭송하는 어조로 언급했다."], ["He felt it his duty to remark (he was sure a gentleman of Mr Sparkler’s fine sense would interpret him with all delicacy), that he could not consider this proposal definitely determined on, until he should have had the privilege of holding some correspondence with Mr Merdle; and of ascertaining it to be so far accordant with the views of that eminent gentleman as that his (Mr Dorrit’s) daughter would be received on that footing which her station in life and her dowry and expectations warranted him in requiring that she should maintain in what he trusted he might be allowed, without the appearance of being mercenary, to call the Eye of the Great World.", "그는 자신의 의무로서 한마디 일러둘 일이 있다고 했다(스파클러 씨처럼 고매한 분별을 갖춘 신사라면 그 말을 더없이 섬세하게 헤아려 주리라 믿는다고 했다). 곧, 머들 씨와 몇 차례 서신을 주고받는 특권을 누리고, 그 저명한 신사의 견해와도 어느 정도 부합하는지를 확인하기 전까지는, 이 제의가 확정되었다고 볼 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["While saying this, which his character as a gentleman of some little station, and his character as a father, equally demanded of him, he would not be so diplomatic as to conceal that the proposal remained in hopeful abeyance and under conditional acceptance, and that he thanked Mr Sparkler for the compliment rendered to himself and to his family.", "다시 말해, 자기 딸이 마땅히 누려야 할 지위 — 그녀의 인생에서의 신분과 그녀의 지참금과 장래의 기대가 보장하는, 그리고 그가 딸에게 요구할 권리가 있다고 믿는 그 지위 — 곧, 돈에 집착하는 것처럼 비치지 않으면서 부른다면, 이른바 대(大)사교계의 눈길 속에서 그녀가 유지해야 할 그 발판 위에서 받아들여질 것인지를 확인하기 전까지는 그렇다는 말이었다. 어느 정도 지위 있는 신사로서의 입장과 아버지로서의 입장이 똑같이 요구하는 이 말을 하면서도, 그는 외교적으로 굴어 이 제의가 희망적인 보류 상태에서 조건부로 수락되었음을 감추지는 않겠노라고 했고, 또 자신과 자기 집안에 베풀어진 영예에 대해 스파클러 씨에게 감사한다고 했다."], ["He concluded with some further and more general observations on the--ha--character of an independent gentleman, and the--hum--character of a possibly too partial and admiring parent.", "그는 — 하 — 독립적 신사의 성품과, — 흠 — 아마도 지나치게 편애하고 찬탄하기 쉬운 부모의 성품에 관해 몇 마디 더 일반적인 소회를 덧붙이며 말을 맺었다."], ["To sum the whole up shortly, he received Mr Sparkler’s offer very much as he would have received three or four half-crowns from him in the days that were gone.", "요컨대 짧게 간추리자면, 그는 스파클러 씨의 청혼을, 지난 시절에 그에게서 반 크라운짜리 동전 서너 닢을 받았을 때와 거의 다름없는 태도로 받아들였다."]], [["Mr Sparkler, finding himself stunned by the words thus heaped upon his inoffensive head, made a brief though pertinent rejoinder; the same being neither more nor less than that he had long perceived Miss Fanny to have no nonsense about her, and that he had no doubt of its being all right with his Governor.", "이렇게 자신의 무해한 머리 위로 쏟아 부어진 말들에 어리벙벙해진 스파클러 씨는, 짧지만 적절한 응수를 했다. 그것은 다름 아니라, 그는 패니 양에게 시건방진 구석이 없다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있었으며, 자기 부친께서도 다 흔쾌히 여기시리라 의심치 않는다는 말이었다."], ["At that point the object of his affections shut him up like a box with a spring lid, and sent him away.", "그 대목에서 그의 애정의 대상은 용수철 뚜껑 달린 상자처럼 그를 탁 닫아 버리고는 돌려보냈다."]], [["Proceeding shortly afterwards to pay his respects to the Bosom, Mr Dorrit was received by it with great consideration.", "얼마 지나지 않아 도릿 씨는 그 '가슴'에게 경의를 표하러 갔고, 그 '가슴'은 그를 크나큰 호의로 맞이했다."], ["Mrs Merdle had heard of this affair from Edmund.", "머들 부인은 에드먼드에게서 이 일에 관해 들은 터였다."], ["She had been surprised at first, because she had not thought Edmund a marrying man.", "처음에는 놀랐다고 했는데, 에드먼드가 결혼할 사람이라고는 생각지 않았기 때문이라 했다. 사교계도 에드먼드를 결혼할 사람이라고 여기지 않았다고."], ["Society had not thought Edmund a marrying man.", "그렇지만 물론 자기는 여자로서 진작에 알아챘다고 했다(우리 여자들은 본능적으로 그런 것들을 알아보는 법이지요, 도릿 씨!)."], ["Still, of course she had seen, as a woman (we women did instinctively see these things, Mr Dorrit!), that Edmund had been immensely captivated by Miss Dorrit, and she had openly said that Mr Dorrit had much to answer for in bringing so charming a girl abroad to turn the heads of his countrymen.", "에드먼드가 도릿 양에게 홀딱 사로잡혀 있었다는 것을 말이다. 그래서 자기는, 그토록 매력적인 처녀를 외국에 데려와 자기 나라 사내들의 넋을 빼놓다니 도릿 씨께서 책임지실 일이 많다고 대놓고 말한 적이 있다고 했다."]], [["‘Have I the honour to conclude, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘that the direction which Mr Sparkler’s affections have taken, is--ha-approved of by you?’", "\"부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그렇다면 스파클러 씨의 애정이 향한 그 방향을 — 하 — 부인께서 승인하시는 것으로 결론 내려도 영광이겠습니까?\""]], [["‘I assure you, Mr Dorrit,’ returned the lady, ‘that, personally, I am charmed.’", "\"분명히 말씀드리지만, 도릿 씨,\" 부인이 대답했다. \"저 개인적으로는 매료되었답니다.\""]], [["That was very gratifying to Mr Dorrit.", "도릿 씨에게는 그 말이 무척 흐뭇했다."]], [["‘Personally,’ repeated Mrs Merdle, ‘charmed.’", "\"개인적으로는,\" 머들 부인이 거듭 말했다. \"매료되었지요.\""]], [["This casual repetition of the word ‘personally,’ moved Mr Dorrit to express his hope that Mr Merdle’s approval, too, would not be wanting?", "이렇게 무심코 되풀이된 '개인적으로'라는 말에 도릿 씨는, 머들 씨의 승인 또한 빠지지 않기를 바란다는 뜻을 표하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["‘I cannot,’ said Mrs Merdle, ‘take upon myself to answer positively for Mr Merdle; gentlemen, especially gentlemen who are what Society calls capitalists, having their own ideas of these matters. But I should think--merely giving an opinion, Mr Dorrit--I should think Mr Merdle would be upon the whole,’ here she held a review of herself before adding at her leisure, ‘quite charmed.’", "\"제가,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"머들 씨를 대신해 단정적으로 답해 드릴 수는 없습니다. 신사분들이란, 특히 사교계에서 자본가라 부르는 신사분들이란, 이런 문제에 대해 나름의 생각을 갖고 계시니까요. 하지만 제 생각으로는 — 어디까지나 한낱 의견을 말씀드리는 것입니다만, 도릿 씨 — 제 생각으로는 머들 씨도 대체로,\" 여기서 그녀는 잠시 자기 자신을 점검하고는 느긋하게 덧붙였다. \"꽤나 매료되시리라 봅니다.\""]], [["At the mention of gentlemen whom Society called capitalists, Mr Dorrit had coughed, as if some internal demur were breaking out of him.", "사교계에서 자본가라 부르는 신사분들이라는 말이 나오자, 도릿 씨는 마치 속에서 무슨 이의가 터져 나오기라도 하는 듯 헛기침을 했다."], ["Mrs Merdle had observed it, and went on to take up the cue.", "머들 부인은 그것을 알아채고는, 그 신호를 이어받아 말을 계속했다."]], [["‘Though, indeed, Mr Dorrit, it is scarcely necessary for me to make that remark, except in the mere openness of saying what is uppermost to one whom I so highly regard, and with whom I hope I may have the pleasure of being brought into still more agreeable relations. For one cannot but see the great probability of your considering such things from Mr Merdle’s own point of view, except indeed that circumstances have made it Mr Merdle’s accidental fortune, or misfortune, to be engaged in business transactions, and that they, however vast, may a little cramp his horizons. I am a very child as to having any notion of business,’ said Mrs Merdle; ‘but I am afraid, Mr Dorrit, it may have that tendency.’", "\"하기야, 도릿 씨, 굳이 그런 말씀을 드릴 필요는 없겠지요. 다만 제가 그토록 높이 우러르는 분께, 더욱 흐뭇한 관계로 맺어지는 기쁨을 누릴 수 있기를 바라는 그런 분께, 마음속에 떠오르는 바를 솔직히 털어놓는다는 의미에서 드리는 말씀입니다. 어찌하든, 선생님께서 이런 일을 머들 씨 자신의 관점에서 헤아리실 공산이 크다는 것은 누구나 알 수 있는 노릇이니까요. 다만 여러 사정이 머들 씨로 하여금 우연히도 — 그게 행운인지 불운인지 — 사업 거래에 종사하게 만들었고, 그 거래가 아무리 방대하다 한들 그분의 시야를 다소 옥죌 수도 있다는 점만은 다르겠지요. 저는 사업에 관한 한 갓난아이나 다름없습니다만,\" 머들 부인이 말했다. \"도릿 씨, 사업이란 게 그런 경향을 띠지나 않을까 염려스럽습니다.\""]], [["This skilful see-saw of Mr Dorrit and Mrs Merdle, so that each of them sent the other up, and each of them sent the other down, and neither had the advantage, acted as a sedative on Mr Dorrit’s cough.", "도릿 씨와 머들 부인이 이렇게 능숙하게 시소를 타듯, 서로를 한 번씩 띄워 올리고 한 번씩 내려놓으며, 어느 쪽도 우위를 점하지 않는 그 솜씨는 도릿 씨의 기침에 진정제처럼 작용했다."], ["He remarked with his utmost politeness, that he must beg to protest against its being supposed, even by Mrs Merdle, the accomplished and graceful (to which compliment she bent herself), that such enterprises as Mr Merdle’s, apart as they were from the puny undertakings of the rest of men, had any lower tendency than to enlarge and expand the genius in which they were conceived.", "그는 더없이 정중하게, 머들 씨의 사업 같은 사업은 — 나머지 인간들의 하찮은 사업과는 동떨어진 것으로서 — 그것을 잉태한 천재를 더 넓히고 확장하는 것 말고는 어떤 저급한 경향도 가질 수 없다고, 박학하고 우아한 머들 부인조차도(그녀는 이 찬사에 몸을 숙였다) 그렇게 여기는 일에는 부디 반대하게 해 달라고 항변했다. \"선생님은 관대함 그 자체시군요,\""], ["‘You are generosity itself,’ said Mrs Merdle in return, smiling her best smile; ‘let us hope so.", "머들 부인이 더없이 환한 미소를 지으며 응답했다. \"그러기를 바라야지요."], ["But I confess I am almost superstitious in my ideas about business.’", "하지만 고백하건대 저는 사업에 관해서는 거의 미신적일 만큼 생각이 어둡답니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit threw in another compliment here, to the effect that business, like the time which was precious in it, was made for slaves; and that it was not for Mrs Merdle, who ruled all hearts at her supreme pleasure, to have anything to do with it.", "도릿 씨는 여기서 또 한마디 찬사를 던져 넣었으니, 사업이란, 그 안에서 귀중한 시간과 마찬가지로, 노예들을 위해 생겨난 것이며, 더없는 흥에 따라 만인의 마음을 다스리는 머들 부인 같은 분이 그런 것과 얽힐 까닭이 없다는 취지였다."], ["Mrs Merdle laughed, and conveyed to Mr Dorrit an idea that the Bosom flushed--which was one of her best effects.", "머들 부인은 웃었고, 도릿 씨에게 그 '가슴'이 발그레해진 듯한 인상을 전했다 — 이는 그녀의 가장 빼어난 효과 중 하나였다."]], [["‘I say so much,’ she then explained, ‘merely because Mr Merdle has always taken the greatest interest in Edmund, and has always expressed the strongest desire to advance his prospects.", "\"제가 이렇게 길게 말씀드리는 것은,\" 그녀가 이어서 설명했다."], ["Edmund’s public position, I think you know.", "\"다만 머들 씨가 늘 에드먼드에게 지대한 관심을 보여 오셨고, 늘 그 아이의 앞길을 열어 주고 싶다는 강한 바람을 표하셨기 때문입니다."], ["His private position rests solely with Mr Merdle.", "에드먼드의 공적인 처지는 선생님께서도 아시리라 봅니다."], ["In my foolish incapacity for business, I assure you I know no more.’", "그 아이의 사적인 처지는 오로지 머들 씨께 달려 있고요. 사업에 어두운 제 어리석은 무능 탓에, 정말이지 저는 그 이상은 아무것도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit again expressed, in his own way, the sentiment that business was below the ken of enslavers and enchantresses.", "도릿 씨는 다시금, 사업이란 사람을 사로잡는 이들과 마법을 거는 여인들의 안목 밑에 놓인 것이라는 취지의 말을 자기 식으로 표했다."], ["He then mentioned his intention, as a gentleman and a parent, of writing to Mr Merdle.", "그러고는 신사로서, 또 아버지로서 머들 씨에게 편지를 쓰겠다는 뜻을 밝혔다."], ["Mrs Merdle concurred with all her heart--or with all her art, which was exactly the same thing--and herself despatched a preparatory letter by the next post to the eighth wonder of the world.", "머들 부인은 진심을 다해 — 아니, 그녀의 온갖 기교를 다해, 그것은 그녀에게는 똑같은 말이었다 — 그 뜻에 찬동했고, 다음 우편으로 세계 제8대 불가사의에게 미리 알리는 편지를 손수 띄웠다."]], [["In his epistolary communication, as in his dialogues and discourses on the great question to which it related, Mr Dorrit surrounded the subject with flourishes, as writing-masters embellish copy-books and ciphering-books: where the titles of the elementary rules of arithmetic diverge into swans, eagles, griffins, and other calligraphic recreations, and where the capital letters go out of their minds and bodies into ecstasies of pen and ink.", "이 일에 관련된 큰 문제를 두고 나눈 대화나 담론에서와 마찬가지로, 도릿 씨는 서신에서도 그 주제를 갖은 장식으로 둘러쌌다. 마치 습자 선생들이 습자책과 셈본을 꾸미는 것과 같았으니, 거기서는 산술 기초 규칙의 표제들이 백조, 독수리, 그리핀, 그 밖의 갖가지 서법적 유희로 갈라져 나가고, 대문자들이 정신과 육체를 잃고서 펜과 잉크의 황홀경으로 빠져드는 것이었다."], ["Nevertheless, he did render the purport of his letter sufficiently clear, to enable Mr Merdle to make a decent pretence of having learnt it from that source.", "그렇지만 그는 편지의 요지만은 충분히 분명하게 전달했으니, 머들 씨가 그 출처에서 그 내용을 알게 되었노라고 그럴듯하게 시치미 떼기에 부족함이 없었다."], ["Mr Merdle replied to it accordingly.", "머들 씨도 그에 맞춰 답장을 보냈다."], ["Mr Dorrit replied to Mr Merdle; Mr Merdle replied to Mr Dorrit; and it was soon announced that the corresponding powers had come to a satisfactory understanding.", "도릿 씨가 머들 씨에게 답하고, 머들 씨가 도릿 씨에게 답하고, 도릿 씨가 다시 머들 씨에게 답했다. 그리고 머지않아 서신을 주고받던 두 세력이 만족스러운 합의에 이르렀다는 발표가 나왔다."]], [["Now, and not before, Miss Fanny burst upon the scene, completely arrayed for her new part.", "이제야, 그 전에는 결코 아니었으나, 패니 양이 자신의 새 배역을 위해 완벽하게 차려입고 무대 위로 활짝 등장했다."], ["Now and not before, she wholly absorbed Mr Sparkler in her light, and shone for both, and twenty more.", "이제야, 그 전에는 결코 아니었으나, 그녀는 스파클러 씨를 자신의 광채 속에 온통 흡수해 버렸고, 두 사람 몫으로, 아니 그 이상의 스무 사람 몫으로 빛났다."], ["No longer feeling that want of a defined place and character which had caused her so much trouble, this fair ship began to steer steadily on a shaped course, and to swim with a weight and balance that developed her sailing qualities.", "그토록 그녀를 괴롭혀 온, 정해진 자리와 정체성의 결여를 더는 느끼지 않게 되자, 이 아름다운 배는 정해진 항로를 따라 꾸준히 키를 잡고 나아가기 시작했고, 자신의 항해 능력을 발휘시키는 무게와 균형으로 헤엄쳐 나가기 시작했다."]], [["‘The preliminaries being so satisfactorily arranged, I think I will now, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘announce--ha--formally, to Mrs General--’", "\"예비 절차가 그토록 만족스럽게 매듭지어졌으니, 이제 나는, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인에게 — 하 — 정식으로 알려야겠다고 — \""]], [["‘Papa,’ returned Fanny, taking him up short upon that name, ‘I don’t see what Mrs General has got to do with it.’", "\"아빠,\" 패니가 그 이름이 나오자 말을 뚝 끊으며 대꾸했다. \"제너럴 부인이 이 일과 무슨 상관인지 저는 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘it will be an act of courtesy to--hum--a lady, well bred and refined--’", "\"얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"그것은 — 흠 — 교양 있고 세련된 부인에 대한 예의의 행동이 될 거다 — \""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I am sick of Mrs General’s good breeding and refinement, papa,’ said Fanny.", "저는 제너럴 부인의 교양이니 세련이니 하는 것에 신물이 났어요, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘I am tired of Mrs General.’", "\"제너럴 부인이라면 진력이 나요.\""]], [["‘Tired,’ repeated Mr Dorrit in reproachful astonishment, ‘of--ha--Mrs General.’", "\"진력이 난다고,\" 도릿 씨가 나무라듯 놀라며 되뇌었다. \"— 하 — 제너럴 부인이 말이냐.\""]], [["‘Quite disgusted with her, papa,’ said Fanny.", "\"정말이지 그분이라면 넌더리가 나요, 아빠,\""], ["‘I really don’t see what she has to do with my marriage.", "패니가 말했다. \"저는 그분이 제 결혼과 무슨 상관인지 정말 모르겠어요."], ["Let her keep to her own matrimonial projects--if she has any.’", "그분은 그분 자신의 혼사 계획에나 매달리라죠 — 그런 게 있다면 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit, with a grave and weighty slowness upon him, contrasting strongly with his daughter’s levity: ‘I beg the favour of your explaining--ha--what it is you mean.’", "\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했는데, 그 위에는 딸의 경박함과 뚜렷이 대비되는 진중하고 묵직한 느릿함이 깔려 있었다. \"부디 — 하 — 네 말이 무슨 뜻인지를 설명해 주는 호의를 베풀어 다오.\""]], [["‘I mean, papa,’ said Fanny, ‘that if Mrs General should happen to have any matrimonial projects of her own, I dare say they are quite enough to occupy her spare time. And that if she has not, so much the better; but still I don’t wish to have the honour of making announcements to her.’", "\"제 말은요, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 혹시 자기 자신의 혼사 계획을 갖고 있다면, 그것만으로도 그분의 여가 시간을 채우기에 충분하고도 남으리라는 거예요. 그리고 그런 게 없다면, 차라리 더 잘된 일이고요. 하지만 어쨌든 저는 그분께 무슨 발표를 하는 영광 따위는 누리고 싶지 않아요.\""]], [["‘Permit me to ask you, Fanny,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘why not?’", "\"패니, 한 가지 물어보자,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"어째서 그러느냐?\""]], [["‘Because she can find my engagement out for herself, papa,’ retorted Fanny.", "\"왜냐하면 그분은 제 약혼을 혼자 알아낼 수 있을 테니까요, 아빠,\" 패니가 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘She is watchful enough, I dare say.", "\"그분은 충분히 주의 깊으시잖아요, 정말이지."], ["I think I have seen her so.", "제가 그런 걸 본 것 같아요."], ["Let her find it out for herself.", "혼자 알아내라죠."], ["If she should not find it out for herself, she will know it when I am married.", "만약 혼자 알아내지 못한다 해도, 제가 결혼하면 알게 될 테고요."], ["And I hope you will not consider me wanting in affection for you, papa, if I say it strikes me that will be quite enough for Mrs General.’", "그리고 아빠, 제너럴 부인께는 그 정도면 아주 충분하다는 생각이 든다고 제가 말씀드린다고 해서, 제가 아빠에 대한 애정이 부족하다고 여기지는 말아 주세요.\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ returned Mr Dorrit, ‘I am amazed, I am displeased by this--hum--this capricious and unintelligible display of animosity towards--ha--Mrs General.’", "\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다. \"나는 놀랍구나, 나는 불쾌하다, 이런 — 흠 — 이런 변덕스럽고 영문 모를, — 하 — 제너럴 부인에 대한 적의의 표출에 말이다.\""]], [["‘Do not, if you please, papa,’ urged Fanny, ‘call it animosity, because I assure you I do not consider Mrs General worth my animosity.’", "\"제발, 아빠,\" 패니가 다그쳤다. \"그걸 적의라고 부르지는 말아 주세요. 분명히 말씀드리지만, 저는 제너럴 부인이 제 적의를 살 만한 가치가 있다고는 생각지 않으니까요.\""]], [["At this, Mr Dorrit rose from his chair with a fixed look of severe reproof, and remained standing in his dignity before his daughter.", "이 말에 도릿 씨는 의자에서 일어나, 준엄한 질책의 표정을 고정한 채로 딸 앞에 위엄 있게 우뚝 섰다."], ["His daughter, turning the bracelet on her arm, and now looking at him, and now looking from him, said, ‘Very well, papa.", "딸은 팔에 찬 팔찌를 돌리며, 아버지를 쳐다보았다가 또 그에게서 시선을 돌렸다가 하면서 말했다. \"좋아요, 아빠."], ["I am truly sorry if you don’t like it; but I can’t help it.", "아빠가 마음에 안 들어 하신다니 정말 죄송해요. 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["I am not a child, and I am not Amy, and I must speak.’", "저는 어린애가 아니고, 에이미도 아니니까, 말을 해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘Fanny,’ gasped Mr Dorrit, after a majestic silence, ‘if I request you to remain here, while I formally announce to Mrs General, as an exemplary lady, who is--hum--a trusted member of this family, the--ha--the change that is contemplated among us; if I--ha--not only request it, but--hum--insist upon it--’", "\"패니,\" 도릿 씨가 위엄 있는 침묵 끝에 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"내가 너에게, 여기 남아 있어 달라고 청하면서, 그동안 제너럴 부인께 — 흠 — 이 집안에서 신임받는 일원인 그 모범적인 부인께, — 하 — 우리 사이에 고려되고 있는 변화를 정식으로 알린다면, 만약 내가 — 하 — 그것을 청할 뿐 아니라 — 흠 — 그것을 고집한다면 — \""]], [["‘Oh, papa,’ Fanny broke in with pointed significance, ‘if you make so much of it as that, I have in duty nothing to do but comply. I hope I may have my thoughts upon the subject, however, for I really cannot help it under the circumstances.’ So, Fanny sat down with a meekness which, in the junction of extremes, became defiance; and her father, either not deigning to answer, or not knowing what to answer, summoned Mr Tinkler into his presence.", "\"오, 아빠,\" 패니가 날카로운 의미를 담아 말을 끊었다. \"그 일을 그렇게까지 대단하게 여기신다면, 저는 도리상 따르는 것 말고는 할 일이 없겠지요. 다만 이 일에 대한 제 생각만은 간직하게 해 주세요. 이런 사정에서야 정말 어쩔 수가 없으니까요.\" 그리하여 패니는, 극과 극이 맞닿은 지점에서 도리어 반항이 되어 버린 그런 순종의 태도로 자리에 앉았다. 그리고 그녀의 아버지는, 대답할 가치를 두지 않았거나 무어라 대답해야 할지를 몰랐거나, 어느 쪽이든 간에, 팅클러 씨를 불러들였다."]], [["‘Mrs General.’", "\"제너럴 부인을.\""]], [["Mr Tinkler, unused to receive such short orders in connection with the fair varnisher, paused.", "그 고운 광택칠하는 여인과 관련해 그토록 짧은 명령을 받는 데 익숙하지 않은 팅클러 씨는 멈칫했다."], ["Mr Dorrit, seeing the whole Marshalsea and all its testimonials in the pause, instantly flew at him with, ‘How dare you, sir?", "도릿 씨는 그 멈칫함 속에서 마셜시 감옥 전체와 그곳의 온갖 증표를 보고는, 즉시 그에게 달려들 듯 호통쳤다. \"어찌 감히 그러느냐, 이놈?"], ["What do you mean?’", "무슨 뜻이냐?\""]], [["‘I beg your pardon, sir,’ pleaded Mr Tinkler, ‘I was wishful to know--’", "\"용서하십시오, 나리,\" 팅클러 씨가 변명했다. \"저는 알고 싶었을 뿐 — \""]], [["‘You wished to know nothing, sir,’ cried Mr Dorrit, highly flushed.", "\"너는 아무것도 알고 싶지 않았다, 이놈,\" 도릿 씨가 시뻘게진 얼굴로 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t tell me you did.", "\"알고 싶었다고 내게 말하지 마라."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["You didn’t.", "너는 그렇지 않았어."], ["You are guilty of mockery, sir.’", "너는 조롱의 죄를 지었다, 이놈.\""]], [["‘I assure you, sir--’ Mr Tinkler began.", "\"분명히 말씀드리지만, 나리 — \" 팅클러 씨가 입을 떼었다."]], [["‘Don’t assure me!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"내게 분명히 말하지 마라!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I will not be assured by a domestic.", "\"나는 하인에게서 보증 따위 받지 않겠다."], ["You are guilty of mockery.", "너는 조롱의 죄를 지었어."], ["You shall leave me--hum--the whole establishment shall leave me.", "너는 내 곁을 떠나야 한다 — 흠 — 이 집안 식솔 전부가 나를 떠나야 한다."], ["What are you waiting for?’", "무얼 기다리고 있느냐?\""]], [["‘Only for my orders, sir.’", "\"그저 분부만 기다리고 있습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘It’s false,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘you have your orders.", "\"거짓말이다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["Ha--hum.", "\"너는 이미 분부를 받았다."], ["My compliments to Mrs General, and I beg the favour of her coming to me, if quite convenient, for a few minutes.", "하 — 흠. 제너럴 부인께 안부를 전하고, 괜찮으시다면 잠시 내게 와 주시는 호의를 청한다고 여쭈어라."], ["Those are your orders.’", "그것이 네가 받은 분부다.\""]], [["In his execution of this mission, Mr Tinkler perhaps expressed that Mr Dorrit was in a raging fume.", "이 심부름을 수행하면서 팅클러 씨는 어쩌면 도릿 씨가 노발대발하고 있다는 사정을 전했는지도 모른다."], ["However that was, Mrs General’s skirts were very speedily heard outside, coming along--one might almost have said bouncing along--with unusual expedition.", "어찌 되었든, 곧이어 제너럴 부인의 치맛자락이 바깥에서 다가오는 소리가 — 거의 통통 튀어 오며 다가온다고 해도 좋을 만큼 — 평소답지 않게 서둘러 들려왔다."], ["Albeit, they settled down at the door and swept into the room with their customary coolness.", "그렇긴 해도, 그 치맛자락은 문간에서 진정되어, 여느 때의 침착함으로 방 안을 쓸 듯이 들어왔다."]], [["‘Mrs General,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘take a chair.’", "\"제너럴 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"의자에 앉으시지요.\""]], [["Mrs General, with a graceful curve of acknowledgment, descended into the chair which Mr Dorrit offered.", "제너럴 부인은 우아한 곡선을 그리는 답례로, 도릿 씨가 권한 의자에 내려앉았다."]], [["‘Madam,’ pursued that gentleman, ‘as you have had the kindness to undertake the--hum--formation of my daughters, and as I am persuaded that nothing nearly affecting them can--ha--be indifferent to you--’", "\"부인,\" 그 신사가 말을 이었다. \"부인께서 친절하게도 제 딸들의 — 흠 — 도야를 맡아 주셨고, 또 그 아이들과 가까이 관련된 일이라면 무엇이든 — 하 — 부인께 무관한 일일 수 없으리라고 제가 확신하는 까닭에 — \""]], [["‘Wholly impossible,’ said Mrs General in the calmest of ways.", "\"전혀 그럴 수 없지요,\" 제너럴 부인이 더없이 차분한 태도로 말했다."]], [["‘--I therefore wish to announce to you, madam, that my daughter now present--’", "\" — 그러므로 부인께 알려 드리고자 합니다. 지금 여기 있는 제 딸이 — \""]], [["Mrs General made a slight inclination of her head to Fanny, who made a very low inclination of her head to Mrs General, and came loftily upright again.", "제너럴 부인은 패니에게 머리를 살짝 숙여 보였고, 패니는 제너럴 부인에게 머리를 아주 낮게 숙였다가 다시 거만하게 꼿꼿이 세웠다."]], [["‘--That my daughter Fanny is--ha--contracted to be married to Mr Sparkler, with whom you are acquainted.", "\" — 제 딸 패니가, — 하 — 부인께서도 아시는 스파클러 씨와 혼약을 맺게 되었습니다."], ["Hence, madam, you will be relieved of half your difficult charge--ha--difficult charge.’ Mr Dorrit repeated it with his angry eye on Fanny.", "그러니, 부인, 부인께서는 힘겨운 책무의 절반을 — 하 — 힘겨운 책무를 덜게 되시는 것입니다.\" 도릿 씨는 성난 눈으로 패니를 보며 그 말을 되풀이했다."], ["‘But not, I hope, to the--hum--diminution of any other portion, direct or indirect, of the footing you have at present the kindness to occupy in my family.’", "\"하지만 부인께서 지금 친절하게도 우리 집안에서 차지하고 계신 그 발판의, 직접적이든 간접적이든 다른 어떤 부분도, — 흠 — 줄어드는 일이 없기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, with her gloved hands resting on one another in exemplary repose, ‘is ever considerate, and ever but too appreciative of my friendly services.’", "\"도릿 씨께서는,\" 제너럴 부인이 장갑 낀 두 손을 모범적인 평온함으로 포개어 놓은 채 대답했다. \"언제나 사려 깊으시고, 또 언제나 제 친선의 봉사를 너무도 높이 평가해 주시는군요.\""]], [["(Miss Fanny coughed, as much as to say, ‘You are right.’)", "(패니 양은, '맞는 말이군요'라고 하기라도 하듯 헛기침을 했다.)"]], [["‘Miss Dorrit has no doubt exercised the soundest discretion of which the circumstances admitted, and I trust will allow me to offer her my sincere congratulations.", "\"도릿 양은 분명 사정이 허락하는 한 가장 건전한 분별력을 발휘하셨으리라 봅니다. 그리고 부디 제가 도릿 양께 진심 어린 축하를 드리도록 허락해 주시기를 바랍니다."], ["When free from the trammels of passion,’ Mrs General closed her eyes at the word, as if she could not utter it, and see anybody; ‘when occurring with the approbation of near relatives; and when cementing the proud structure of a family edifice; these are usually auspicious events.", "정념의 굴레에서 벗어나 이루어질 때,\" 제너럴 부인은 그 단어를 입에 담으면서 — 그 말을 입에 올리고도 누군가의 얼굴을 마주 볼 수는 없다는 듯 — 눈을 감았다."], ["I trust Miss Dorrit will allow me to offer her my best congratulations.’", "\"가까운 친척의 승인을 얻어 이루어질 때, 그리고 한 가문이라는 당당한 건축물의 구조를 굳건히 다질 때, 이런 일들은 대개 상서로운 사건이지요. 부디 도릿 양께 제 진심 어린 축하를 드리도록 허락해 주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["Here Mrs General stopped, and added internally, for the setting of her face, ‘Papa, potatoes, poultry, Prunes, and prism.’", "여기서 제너럴 부인은 멈추고는, 표정을 가다듬기 위해 속으로 이렇게 덧붙였다. '파파, 포테이토, 포울트리, 프룬, 프리즘.'"]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ she superadded aloud, ‘is ever most obliging; and for the attention, and I will add distinction, of having this confidence imparted to me by himself and Miss Dorrit at this early time, I beg to offer the tribute of my thanks. My thanks, and my congratulations, are equally the meed of Mr Dorrit and of Miss Dorrit.’", "\"도릿 씨께서는,\" 그녀가 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"언제나 더없이 친절하시지요. 그리고 이 신뢰를 이토록 이른 시기에 선생님과 도릿 양께서 손수 제게 전해 주신 그 배려와, 감히 덧붙이자면 영예에 대하여, 저는 감사의 헌사를 올리고자 합니다. 제 감사와 제 축하는, 도릿 씨와 도릿 양 두 분께 똑같이 마땅한 보답입니다.\""]], [["‘To me,’ observed Miss Fanny, ‘they are excessively gratifying--inexpressibly so. The relief of finding that you have no objection to make, Mrs General, quite takes a load off my mind, I am sure. I hardly know what I should have done,’ said Fanny, ‘if you had interposed any objection, Mrs General.’", "\"제게는,\" 패니 양이 한마디 했다. \"그 말씀이 더없이 흐뭇하군요 — 이루 말할 수 없이요. 제너럴 부인, 부인께서 아무런 이의도 없으시다는 걸 알게 되니 정말이지 마음의 짐이 한결 덜어지는걸요. 만약 부인께서 무슨 이의라도 제기하셨더라면,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제가 어찌해야 했을지 도무지 모르겠어요, 제너럴 부인.\""]], [["Mrs General changed her gloves, as to the right glove being uppermost and the left undermost, with a Prunes and Prism smile.", "제너럴 부인은 오른쪽 장갑이 위로, 왼쪽 장갑이 아래로 가도록, 프룬과 프리즘의 미소를 띤 채 두 손을 바꾸어 포갰다."]], [["‘To preserve your approbation, Mrs General,’ said Fanny, returning the smile with one in which there was no trace of those ingredients, ‘will of course be the highest object of my married life; to lose it, would of course be perfect wretchedness.", "\"부인의 승인을 지켜 가는 것이,\" 패니가, 그 어떤 재료의 흔적도 없는 미소로 부인의 미소에 답하며 말했다."], ["I am sure your great kindness will not object, and I hope papa will not object, to my correcting a small mistake you have made, however.", "\"물론 제 결혼 생활의 가장 높은 목표가 될 거예요. 그것을 잃는다면, 물론 더없는 비참이겠지요."], ["The best of us are so liable to mistakes, that even you, Mrs General, have fallen into a little error.", "부인의 크나큰 친절이 마다하지 않으시리라 믿고, 또 아빠께서도 마다하지 않으시리라 믿으며, 한 가지 부인께서 저지르신 작은 착오를 바로잡을까 합니다."], ["The attention and distinction you have so impressively mentioned, Mrs General, as attaching to this confidence, are, I have no doubt, of the most complimentary and gratifying description; but they don’t at all proceed from me.", "우리 가운데 가장 훌륭한 사람조차 착오에 빠지기 쉬운 법이라, 부인 같으신 분조차 작은 오류에 빠지셨군요, 제너럴 부인. 부인께서 이 신뢰에 결부된 것으로 그토록 인상 깊게 말씀하신 그 배려와 영예는, 의심할 바 없이 더없이 찬사에 차고 흐뭇한 성격의 것이긴 합니다만, 그것은 결코 저에게서 비롯된 것이 아니랍니다."], ["The merit of having consulted you on the subject would have been so great in me, that I feel I must not lay claim to it when it really is not mine. It is wholly papa’s.", "이 일을 두고 부인께 의논을 드렸다는 그 공로는 제게 너무도 큰 것이라, 정작 그것이 제 것이 아닌 이상 그 공을 가로챌 수는 없다는 생각이 들어요."], ["I am deeply obliged to you for your encouragement and patronage, but it was papa who asked for it.", "그것은 온전히 아빠의 것이지요. 부인의 격려와 후원에는 깊이 감사드립니다만, 그것을 청한 것은 아빠셨답니다."], ["I have to thank you, Mrs General, for relieving my breast of a great weight by so handsomely giving your consent to my engagement, but you have really nothing to thank me for.", "제 약혼에 그토록 너그러이 승낙해 주셔서 제 가슴에 얹힌 큰 짐을 덜어 주신 점은 제가 부인께 감사드려야 할 일이지만, 부인께서 정작 제게 감사하실 일은 전혀 없는 거예요."], ["I hope you will always approve of my proceedings after I have left home and that my sister also may long remain the favoured object of your condescension, Mrs General.’", "제가 집을 떠난 뒤에도 부인께서 늘 제 행실을 인정해 주시기를, 그리고 제 동생 또한 부인의 황송하신 호의를 받는 사랑스러운 대상으로 오래도록 남기를 바랍니다, 제너럴 부인.\""]], [["With this address, which was delivered in her politest manner, Fanny left the room with an elegant and cheerful air--to tear up-stairs with a flushed face as soon as she was out of hearing, pounce in upon her sister, call her a little Dormouse, shake her for the better opening of her eyes, tell her what had passed below, and ask her what she thought of Pa now?", "이 인사를, 더없이 정중한 태도로 늘어놓고 나서, 패니는 우아하고 명랑한 기색으로 방을 나섰다 — 그러고는 남의 귀에 들리지 않을 데까지 가자마자 상기된 얼굴로 위층으로 내달려, 동생에게 불쑥 뛰어들어, 그 애를 자그마한 겨울잠쥐라고 부르고, 눈이 좀 번쩍 뜨이도록 흔들어 대고, 아래층에서 벌어진 일을 일러 주고, 이제 아빠를 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물었다."]], [["Towards Mrs Merdle, the young lady comported herself with great independence and self-possession; but not as yet with any more decided opening of hostilities.", "머들 부인을 대하는 이 젊은 숙녀의 태도는 무척 독립적이고 자신만만했지만, 아직 그 이상으로 뚜렷하게 적대 행위의 포문을 여는 정도는 아니었다. 이따금 둘은 가벼운 접전을 벌였으니, 이를테면 패니가 그 부인에게 등을 두드려지는 느낌을 받았을 때라든지, 머들 부인이 유난히 젊고 고와 보였을 때가 그러했다."], ["Occasionally they had a slight skirmish, as when Fanny considered herself patted on the back by that lady, or as when Mrs Merdle looked particularly young and well; but Mrs Merdle always soon terminated those passages of arms by sinking among her cushions with the gracefullest indifference, and finding her attention otherwise engaged.", "그러나 머들 부인은 언제나, 더없이 우아한 무심함으로 방석들 사이에 묻혀 들어가 다른 데로 주의를 돌려 버림으로써 그런 무력 충돌을 곧 끝내곤 했다. 사교계는(그 신비로운 피조물은 일곱 언덕 위에도 자리 잡고 있었으니) 패니 양이 약혼으로 무척 나아졌다고 보았다."], ["Society (for that mysterious creature sat upon the Seven Hills too) found Miss Fanny vastly improved by her engagement.", "그녀는 훨씬 더 가까이하기 쉽고, 훨씬 더 거리낌 없고 사람을 끄는 데가 있고, 훨씬 덜 까다로워졌다. 그리하여 이제 그녀는 수많은 추종자와 찬미자를 거느리게 되었으니, 시집보낼 딸을 둔 부인들의 쓰라린 분개를 샀다."], ["She was much more accessible, much more free and engaging, much less exacting; insomuch that she now entertained a host of followers and admirers, to the bitter indignation of ladies with daughters to marry, who were to be regarded as Having revolted from Society on the Miss Dorrit grievance, and erected a rebellious standard.", "그 부인들은 도릿 양 사태를 두고 사교계에 반기를 들고 반란의 깃발을 세운 자들로 여겨질 만했다. 자신이 일으킨 동요를 즐기면서, 도릿 양은 그 한가운데를 자기 한 몸으로 거만하게 누비고 다녔을 뿐 아니라, 스파클러 씨까지도 거만하게, 아니 보란 듯이 그 속으로 끌고 다녔다."], ["Enjoying the flutter she caused.", "그것은 마치 그들 모두에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."], ["Miss Dorrit not only haughtily moved through it in her own proper person, but haughtily, even Ostentatiously, led Mr Sparkler through it too: seeming to say to them all, ‘If I think proper to march among you in triumphal procession attended by this weak captive in bonds, rather than a stronger one, that is my business.", "'내가 더 강한 포로가 아니라 이 사슬에 묶인 나약한 포로를 거느리고 너희들 사이를 개선 행렬처럼 행진하는 것이 옳다고 여긴다면, 그건 내 일이다. 내가 그러기로 택했다는 것으로 충분하다!'"], ["Enough that I choose to do it!’ Mr Sparkler for his part, questioned nothing; but went wherever he was taken, did whatever he was told, felt that for his bride-elect to be distinguished was for him to be distinguished on the easiest terms, and was truly grateful for being so openly acknowledged.", "한편 스파클러 씨로 말하자면, 그는 아무것도 따지지 않았다. 그저 데려가는 곳이면 어디든 가고, 시키는 일이면 무엇이든 하고, 자신의 약혼녀가 두드러지는 것이 곧 가장 손쉬운 조건으로 자기가 두드러지는 일이라 여기며, 이토록 공공연히 인정받는 데 진심으로 감사했다."]], [["The winter passing on towards the spring while this condition of affairs prevailed, it became necessary for Mr Sparkler to repair to England, and take his appointed part in the expression and direction of its genius, learning, commerce, spirit, and sense.", "이런 형국이 이어지는 가운데 겨울이 봄을 향해 흘러가자, 스파클러 씨가 영국으로 건너가, 그 나라의 천재성과 학문, 상업, 기상, 그리고 분별을 표현하고 이끄는 데서 자신에게 맡겨진 역할을 떠맡아야 할 필요가 생겼다."], ["The land of Shakespeare, Milton, Bacon, Newton, Watt, the land of a host of past and present abstract philosophers, natural philosophers, and subduers of Nature and Art in their myriad forms, called to Mr Sparkler to come and take care of it, lest it should perish.", "셰익스피어와 밀턴, 베이컨, 뉴턴, 와트의 나라, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 과거와 현재의 추상철학자, 자연철학자, 그리고 무수한 형태로 자연과 기예를 정복한 이들의 나라가, 스파클러 씨에게 와서 자기를 돌보아 달라고, 그러지 않으면 멸망하고 말리라고 외쳐 불렀다."], ["Mr Sparkler, unable to resist the agonised cry from the depths of his country’s soul, declared that he must go.", "조국의 영혼 깊은 곳에서 터져 나오는 그 고뇌의 외침을 차마 외면할 수 없었던 스파클러 씨는, 자기가 가야만 한다고 선언했다."]], [["It followed that the question was rendered pressing when, where, and how Mr Sparkler should be married to the foremost girl in all this world with no nonsense about her.", "그리하여, 스파클러 씨가 이 온 세상에서 시건방진 데라곤 없는 으뜸가는 처녀와 언제, 어디서, 어떻게 결혼해야 하는가 하는 문제가 시급해졌다."], ["Its solution, after some little mystery and secrecy, Miss Fanny herself announced to her sister.", "약간의 비밀과 은밀함을 거친 끝에, 패니 양 자신이 그 해결책을 동생에게 알려 주었다."]], [["‘Now, my child,’ said she, seeking her out one day, ‘I am going to tell you something. It is only this moment broached; and naturally I hurry to you the moment it _is_ broached.’", "\"자, 얘야,\" 어느 날 동생을 찾아내고는 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 너한테 할 얘기가 있어. 바로 지금 막 꺼내진 일이야. 그래서 당연히 꺼내지자마자 너한테 달려온 거고.\""]], [["‘Your marriage, Fanny?’", "\"언니 결혼 말이야, 패니?\""]], [["‘My precious child,’ said Fanny, ‘don’t anticipate me.", "\"내 소중한 아가,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["Let me impart my confidence to you, you flurried little thing, in my own way.", "\"내 말을 앞질러 가지 마. 이 안달복달하는 꼬마야, 내 비밀을 내 방식대로 너한테 털어놓게 해 줘."], ["As to your guess, if I answered it literally, I should answer no.", "네 짐작으로 말하자면, 곧이곧대로 답하자면 아니라고 해야겠지."], ["For really it is not my marriage that is in question, half as much as it is Edmund’s.’", "사실 지금 문제가 되는 건 내 결혼이라기보다는, 그 절반쯤이나 될까, 에드먼드의 결혼이거든.\""]], [["Little Dorrit looked, and perhaps not altogether without cause, somewhat at a loss to understand this fine distinction.", "리틀 도릿은, 어쩌면 전혀 까닭이 없는 것도 아니게, 이 미묘한 구별을 알아듣기에 다소 곤혹스러운 표정을 지었다."]], [["‘I am in no difficulty,’ exclaimed Fanny, ‘and in no hurry.", "\"내가 곤란할 것도 없고,\" 패니가 외쳤다."], ["I am not wanted at any public office, or to give any vote anywhere else.", "\"서두를 것도 없어. 나는 무슨 관청에서 필요로 하는 것도 아니고, 어디 가서 표를 던져야 하는 것도 아니니까."], ["But Edmund is.", "하지만 에드먼드는 그래."], ["And Edmund is deeply dejected at the idea of going away by himself, and, indeed, I don’t like that he should be trusted by himself.", "그리고 에드먼드는 혼자 떠난다는 생각에 몹시 풀이 죽어 있어. 게다가 정말이지 나도 그이를 혼자 내버려두는 게 못 미더워."], ["For, if it’s possible--and it generally is--to do a foolish thing, he is sure to do it.’", "어리석은 짓을 저지를 여지가 있으면 — 대개는 있게 마련이지만 — 그이는 영락없이 그 짓을 저지르고 마니까.\""]], [["As she concluded this impartial summary of the reliance that might be safely placed upon her future husband, she took off, with an air of business, the bonnet she wore, and dangled it by its strings upon the ground.", "장차 남편 될 사람에게 얼마나 안심하고 의지할 수 있는지에 관한 이 공정한 총평을 마무리하면서, 그녀는 사무적인 태도로 쓰고 있던 보닛을 벗어, 끈을 쥐고 바닥에 대롱대롱 흔들었다."]], [["‘It is far more Edmund’s question, therefore, than mine.", "\"그러니 이건 내 문제라기보다 훨씬 더 에드먼드의 문제인 거야."], ["However, we need say no more about that.", "어쨌든 그 얘긴 더 할 필요 없어."], ["That is self-evident on the face of it.", "그건 보기에도 자명하니까."], ["Well, my dearest Amy!", "자, 사랑하는 에이미야!"], ["The point arising, is he to go by himself, or is he not to go by himself, this other point arises, are we to be married here and shortly, or are we to be married at home months hence?’", "그이가 혼자 갈 것이냐, 혼자 가지 않을 것이냐 하는 문제가 생기니, 거기서 또 다른 문제가 생기는 거지. 우리가 여기서 곧 결혼할 것이냐, 아니면 몇 달 뒤에 본국에 가서 결혼할 것이냐 하는 문제 말이야.\""]], [["‘I see I am going to lose you, Fanny.’", "\"내가 언니를 잃게 되는 거구나, 패니.\""]], [["‘What a little thing you are,’ cried Fanny, half tolerant and half impatient, ‘for anticipating one!", "\"넌 어쩜 그렇게,\" 패니가 반쯤은 너그럽게, 반쯤은 못 참겠다는 듯 외쳤다."], ["Pray, my darling, hear me out.", "\"남의 말을 앞질러 가는 꼬마람!"], ["That woman,’ she spoke of Mrs Merdle, of course, ‘remains here until after Easter; so, in the case of my being married here and going to London with Edmund, I should have the start of her.", "제발, 얘야, 내 말을 끝까지 들어 봐. 그 여자는,\""], ["That is something. Further, Amy.", "물론 머들 부인을 두고 하는 말이었다."], ["That woman being out of the way, I don’t know that I greatly object to Mr Merdle’s proposal to Pa that Edmund and I should take up our abode in that house--_you_ know--where you once went with a dancer, my dear, until our own house can be chosen and fitted up.", "\"부활절이 지나서까지 여기 머물 거야. 그러니 내가 여기서 결혼해서 에드먼드와 런던으로 간다면, 그 여자보다 앞서 가는 셈이지."], ["Further still, Amy.", "그게 뭐라도 되는 일이야. 게다가, 에이미."], ["Papa having always intended to go to town himself, in the spring,--you see, if Edmund and I were married here, we might go off to Florence, where papa might join us, and we might all three travel home together.", "그 여자만 비켜나 있다면, 우리 집이 정해지고 꾸며질 때까지 에드먼드와 내가 그 집에 — 너도 알지, 네가 언젠가 어떤 무용수와 함께 갔던 그 집 말이야 — 들어가 살라는 머들 씨의 제안을 아빠께 한 것도, 내가 딱히 크게 반대할 일은 아니지 싶어. 더 나아가서, 에이미."], ["Mr Merdle has entreated Pa to stay with him in that same mansion I have mentioned, and I suppose he will.", "아빠는 늘 봄에 직접 런던으로 가실 작정이셨으니 — 봐, 에드먼드와 내가 여기서 결혼한다면, 우리는 피렌체로 떠나 거기서 아빠와 합류해서, 우리 셋이 함께 본국으로 여행해 갈 수 있는 거야. 머들 씨가 아빠께, 내가 말한 바로 그 저택에 함께 머물러 달라고 간청했으니, 아마 그러실 거야."], ["But he is master of his own actions; and upon that point (which is not at all material) I can’t speak positively.’", "하지만 아빠는 당신 행동의 주인이시니까. 그 점에 대해선(그건 전혀 중요한 게 아니지만) 나도 단정적으로는 말 못 하겠어.\""]], [["The difference between papa’s being master of his own actions and Mr Sparkler’s being nothing of the sort, was forcibly expressed by Fanny in her manner of stating the case.", "아빠는 당신 행동의 주인이고 스파클러 씨는 전혀 그렇지 못하다는 그 차이가, 패니가 이 사정을 늘어놓는 태도 속에 강렬하게 드러났다."], ["Not that her sister noticed it; for she was divided between regret at the coming separation, and a lingering wish that she had been included in the plans for visiting England.", "동생이 그것을 눈치챈 것은 아니었으니, 그녀는 다가올 이별에 대한 아쉬움과, 자기도 영국 방문 계획에 끼었으면 하는 미련 사이에서 마음이 갈라져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["‘And these are the arrangements, Fanny dear?’", "\"그게 그 계획이구나, 패니 언니?\""]], [["‘Arrangements!’ repeated Fanny.", "\"계획이라니!\" 패니가 되받았다."], ["‘Now, really, child, you are a little trying.", "\"정말이지, 얘야, 너 좀 사람을 힘들게 하는구나."], ["You know I particularly guarded myself against laying my words open to any such construction.", "알다시피 나는 내 말이 그런 식으로 해석될 여지가 없도록 특별히 조심했잖아."], ["What I said was, that certain questions present themselves; and these are the questions.’", "내가 한 말은, 어떤 문제들이 제기된다는 거였고, 그게 바로 그 문제들이라는 거야.\""]], [["Little Dorrit’s thoughtful eyes met hers, tenderly and quietly.", "리틀 도릿의 사려 깊은 눈이, 다정하고 고요하게 언니의 눈과 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Now, my own sweet girl,’ said Fanny, weighing her bonnet by the strings with considerable impatience, ‘it’s no use staring.", "\"자, 내 사랑스러운 아가,\" 패니가 끈을 쥐고 보닛의 무게를 가늠하며 자못 못 참겠다는 듯 말했다."], ["A little owl could stare.", "\"그렇게 빤히 쳐다봐야 소용없어."], ["I look to you for advice, Amy.", "새끼 올빼미라도 그쯤은 쳐다보겠다. 나는 너한테 조언을 구하는 거야, 에이미."], ["What do you advise me to do?’", "나더러 어떻게 하라고 권하겠니?\""]], [["‘Do you think,’ asked Little Dorrit, persuasively, after a short hesitation, ‘do you think, Fanny, that if you were to put it off for a few months, it might be, considering all things, best?’", "\"있잖아,\" 리틀 도릿이 잠시 망설인 끝에 설득하듯 물었다. \"패니 언니, 있잖아, 만약 언니가 그걸 몇 달 미룬다면, 여러 가지를 생각할 때, 그게 제일 낫지 않을까?\""]], [["‘No, little Tortoise,’ retorted Fanny, with exceeding sharpness.", "\"아니, 이 꼬마 거북아,\" 패니가 자못 매섭게 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘I don’t think anything of the kind.’", "\"나는 그런 건 조금도 그렇게 생각 안 해.\""]], [["Here, she threw her bonnet from her altogether, and flounced into a chair.", "여기서 그녀는 보닛을 아예 멀찍이 내던지고, 의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."], ["But, becoming affectionate almost immediately, she flounced out of it again, and kneeled down on the floor to take her sister, chair and all, in her arms.", "그러나 거의 곧바로 다정해져서, 다시 휙 일어나더니, 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 의자째로 동생을 끌어안았다."]], [["‘Don’t suppose I am hasty or unkind, darling, because I really am not.", "\"내가 성급하다거나 매정하다고 여기지 마, 얘야. 정말로 그런 게 아니니까."], ["But you are such a little oddity!", "하지만 넌 어쩜 그렇게 별난 꼬마람!"], ["You make one bite your head off, when one wants to be soothing beyond everything.", "누가 더없이 달래 주려고 하는데도 네가 사람 화를 돋우잖니."], ["Didn’t I tell you, you dearest baby, that Edmund can’t be trusted by himself?", "내가 말했지, 이 귀여운 아가야, 에드먼드는 혼자 내버려둘 수가 없다고?"], ["And don’t you know that he can’t?’", "그리고 너도 그걸 알잖아?\""]], [["‘Yes, yes, Fanny.", "\"응, 응, 패니 언니."], ["You said so, I know.’", "언니가 그렇게 말했어, 알아.\""]], [["‘And you know it, I know,’ retorted Fanny.", "\"그리고 너도 알고 있잖아, 나도 안다고,\" 패니가 되받았다."], ["‘Well, my precious child!", "\"그래, 내 소중한 아가야!"], ["If he is not to be trusted by himself, it follows, I suppose, that I should go with him?’", "그이를 혼자 내버려둘 수 없다면, 그럼 내가 그이와 함께 가야 한다는 게 당연한 결론 아니겠니?\""]], [["‘It--seems so, love,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그런 — 것 같아, 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."]], [["‘Therefore, having heard the arrangements that are feasible to carry out that object, am I to understand, dearest Amy, that on the whole you advise me to make them?’", "\"그러니, 그 목적을 이루기에 실행 가능한 그 계획들을 다 들은 마당에, 사랑하는 에이미야, 결국 네가 나더러 그 계획들을 세우라고 권하는 것으로 알아들으면 되겠니?\""]], [["‘It--seems so, love,’ said Little Dorrit again.", "\"그런 — 것 같아, 언니,\" 리틀 도릿이 다시 말했다."]], [["‘Very well,’ cried Fanny with an air of resignation, ‘then I suppose it must be done!", "\"좋아,\" 패니가 체념하는 기색으로 외쳤다."], ["I came to you, my sweet, the moment I saw the doubt, and the necessity of deciding.", "\"그럼 그렇게 해야겠지! 나는, 얘야, 그 의구심과 결정을 내려야 할 필요를 본 순간 너한테 온 거야."], ["I have now decided.", "이제 결정을 내렸어."], ["So let it be.’", "그러니 그렇게 하기로 하자.\""]], [["After yielding herself up, in this pattern manner, to sisterly advice and the force of circumstances, Fanny became quite benignant: as one who had laid her own inclinations at the feet of her dearest friend, and felt a glow of conscience in having made the sacrifice. ‘After all, my Amy,’ she said to her sister, ‘you are the best of small creatures, and full of good sense; and I don’t know what I shall ever do without you!’", "이렇게 모범적으로, 자매의 조언과 사정의 힘에 자신을 내맡기고 나자, 패니는 무척이나 인자해졌다. 마치 자기 자신의 뜻을 더없이 친한 벗의 발치에 내려놓고는, 그 희생을 치렀다는 데서 양심의 뿌듯한 빛을 느끼는 사람 같았다. \"어쨌든, 내 에이미야,\" 그녀가 동생에게 말했다. \"넌 작은 피조물들 가운데 으뜸가는 아이고, 양식으로 가득 차 있어. 너 없이 내가 대체 어떻게 살아갈지 모르겠구나!\""]], [["With which words she folded her in a closer embrace, and a really fond one.", "이 말과 함께 그녀는 동생을 한층 꼭, 정말로 애틋하게 끌어안았다."]], [["‘Not that I contemplate doing without You, Amy, by any means, for I hope we shall ever be next to inseparable.", "\"그렇다고 내가 너 없이 지낼 작정이라는 건 결코 아니야, 에이미. 우리는 앞으로도 늘 떨어질 수 없을 만큼 가까이 지낼 거라고 바라니까."], ["And now, my pet, I am going to give you a word of advice.", "자, 우리 귀염둥이야, 내가 한마디 조언을 해 줄게."], ["When you are left alone here with Mrs General--’", "네가 여기 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 되면 — \""]], [["‘I am to be left alone here with Mrs General?’ said Little Dorrit, quietly.", "\"내가 여기서 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 된다고?\" 리틀 도릿이 조용히 말했다."]], [["‘Why, of course, my precious, till papa comes back!", "\"아니, 당연하지, 얘야, 아빠가 돌아오실 때까지는 말이야!"], ["Unless you call Edward company, which he certainly is not, even when he is here, and still more certainly is not when he is away at Naples or in Sicily.", "에드워드를 동무라고 친다면 모를까, 그 애는 분명 동무가 못 되지, 여기 있을 때조차도 그렇고, 나폴리나 시칠리아에 가 있을 때야 더더욱 그렇잖아."], ["I was going to say--but you are such a beloved little Marplot for putting one out--when you are left alone here with Mrs General, Amy, don’t you let her slide into any sort of artful understanding with you that she is looking after Pa, or that Pa is looking after her.", "내가 말하려던 건 — 하지만 넌 어쩜 그렇게 사람 말을 끊는 사랑스러운 훼방꾼이람 — 네가 여기서 제너럴 부인과 단둘이 남게 되면, 에이미, 그 여자가 너랑 무슨 교묘한 묵계 같은 걸 슬그머니 맺어서, 자기가 아빠를 마음에 두고 있다거나 아빠가 자기를 마음에 두고 있다거나 하는 식으로 흘러가게 두면 안 돼. 할 수만 있다면 그 여자는 그렇게 할 테니까."], ["She will if she can. I know her sly manner of feeling her way with those gloves of hers.", "나는 그 여자가 장갑 낀 손으로 살금살금 떠보는 그 음흉한 태도를 알아."], ["But don’t you comprehend her on any account.", "하지만 무슨 일이 있어도 그 여자 말을 알아들은 척하지 마."], ["And if Pa should tell you when he comes back, that he has it in contemplation to make Mrs General your mama (which is not the less likely because I am going away), my advice to you is, that you say at once, “Papa, I beg to object most strongly.", "그리고 만약 아빠가 돌아오셔서, 제너럴 부인을 네 엄마로 삼을 생각을 품고 계신다고 너한테 말씀하시거든(내가 떠나고 나면 그럴 가능성이 더 커지면 커졌지 작지는 않을 테니), 너한테 권하는 건, 곧바로 이렇게 말하라는 거야. '아빠, 저는 가장 단호히 반대한다고 말씀드려요."], ["Fanny cautioned me about this, and she objected, and I object.”", "패니가 이 일을 미리 일러 줬고, 그 애도 반대했고, 저도 반대해요.'"], ["I don’t mean to say that any objection from you, Amy, is likely to be of the smallest effect, or that I think you likely to make it with any degree of firmness.", "에이미, 네가 무슨 반대를 한다고 해서 그게 조금이라도 효력이 있으리라는 말은 아니고, 또 네가 그걸 어느 정도라도 단호하게 해낼 거라고 생각하는 것도 아니야."], ["But there is a principle involved--a filial principle--and I implore you not to submit to be mother-in-lawed by Mrs General, without asserting it in making every one about you as uncomfortable as possible.", "하지만 거기엔 한 가지 원칙이 걸려 있어 — 자식 된 자의 원칙이 — 그러니 부탁이건대, 네 주위 모두를 가능한 한 불편하게 만들면서라도 그 원칙을 주장하지 않은 채로 제너럴 부인에게 새엄마 노릇을 당하지는 말아 줘."], ["I don’t expect you to stand by it--indeed, I know you won’t, Pa being concerned--but I wish to rouse you to a sense of duty.", "네가 그걸 끝까지 밀고 나가리라고 기대하지는 않아 — 사실 너는 그러지 못할 거야, 아빠가 걸린 일이니 — 그래도 나는 네게 의무감을 일깨워 주고 싶은 거야."], ["As to any help from me, or as to any opposition that I can offer to such a match, you shall not be left in the lurch, my love.", "내가 도울 수 있는 부분이라든지, 그런 혼사에 내가 맞설 수 있는 부분에 관해서라면, 너를 곤경에 내버려두지는 않을 거야, 얘야."], ["Whatever weight I may derive from my position as a married girl not wholly devoid of attractions--used, as that position always shall be, to oppose that woman--I will bring to bear, you May depend upon it, on the head and false hair (for I am confident it’s not all real, ugly as it is and unlikely as it appears that any One in their Senses would go to the expense of buying it) of Mrs General!’", "적잖이 매력이 없지도 않은 기혼 여성이라는 내 위치에서 끌어낼 수 있는 무게라면 무엇이든 — 그 위치는 늘 그 여자에게 맞서는 데 쓰일 테니까 — 분명히 말하지만, 제너럴 부인의 머리와 그 가짜 머리카락(그게 다 진짜가 아니라고 나는 확신해. 보기 흉하기 짝이 없고, 제정신인 사람이라면 그걸 돈 주고 살 리도 없어 보이긴 하지만)을 향해 죄다 들이댈 거야!\""]], [["Little Dorrit received this counsel without venturing to oppose it but without giving Fanny any reason to believe that she intended to act upon it.", "리틀 도릿은 감히 거기에 반대하지도 않았지만, 그렇다고 자기가 그대로 행동할 작정이라고 패니가 믿을 만한 어떤 빌미도 주지 않은 채, 이 충고를 들었다."], ["Having now, as it were, formally wound up her single life and arranged her worldly affairs, Fanny proceeded with characteristic ardour to prepare for the serious change in her condition.", "이제 말하자면 자신의 독신 생활을 정식으로 청산하고 세속의 일들을 정리해 둔 패니는, 특유의 열정으로 자기 처지의 그 중대한 변화를 준비하는 일에 착수했다."]], [["The preparation consisted in the despatch of her maid to Paris under the protection of the Courier, for the purchase of that outfit for a bride on which it would be extremely low, in the present narrative, to bestow an English name, but to which (on a vulgar principle it observes of adhering to the language in which it professes to be written) it declines to give a French one.", "그 준비란, 신부를 위한 그 일습을 사들이기 위해, 자기 하녀를 그 길잡이의 보호하에 파리로 보내는 일을 두고 하는 말이었다 — 그 일습에 영어 이름을 붙이는 것은 이 이야기에서 지극히 천박한 노릇일 테고, 그렇다고(이 이야기가 쓰였다고 표방하는 그 언어를 고수한다는 속된 원칙을 지키느라) 프랑스어 이름을 붙이는 것도 사양하는 바이다. 이 대리인들이 사들인 그 호화롭고 아름다운 혼수는, 몇 주에 걸쳐, 세관들이 빽빽이 들어선 사이의 땅을 가로질러 나아갔다."], ["The rich and beautiful wardrobe purchased by these agents, in the course of a few weeks made its way through the intervening country, bristling with custom-houses, garrisoned by an immense army of shabby mendicants in uniform who incessantly repeated the Beggar’s Petition over it, as if every individual warrior among them were the ancient Belisarius: and of whom there were so many Legions, that unless the Courier had expended just one bushel and a half of silver money relieving their distresses, they would have worn the wardrobe out before it got to Rome, by turning it over and over.", "그곳은 제복 차림의 초라한 거지들로 이루어진 거대한 군대가 지키고 있었는데, 그들은 마치 자기들 병사 하나하나가 저 옛날의 벨리사리우스라도 되는 양, 그 혼수 위에다 끊임없이 거지의 탄원을 되뇌어 댔다. 그런 군단이 어찌나 많던지, 길잡이가 그들의 궁핍을 달래는 데 은화 꼭 한 부셸 반을 쏟아붓지 않았더라면, 그들은 그 혼수가 로마에 닿기도 전에 이리저리 뒤집어 가며 죄다 닳게 했을 것이다."], ["Through all such dangers, however, it was triumphantly brought, inch by inch, and arrived at its journey’s end in fine condition.", "그러나 그 모든 위험을 뚫고, 그 혼수는 한 치 한 치 의기양양하게 옮겨져, 멀쩡한 상태로 여정의 끝에 다다랐다."]], [["There it was exhibited to select companies of female viewers, in whose gentle bosoms it awakened implacable feelings.", "거기서 그것은, 가려 뽑힌 여성 관람객 무리에게 전시되었고, 그 점잖은 가슴속에 누그러질 줄 모르는 감정을 일깨웠다."], ["Concurrently, active preparations were made for the day on which some of its treasures were to be publicly displayed.", "그와 동시에, 그 보물들 가운데 일부가 공공연히 진열될 그 날을 위해 활발한 준비가 이루어졌다."], ["Cards of breakfast-invitation were sent out to half the English in the city of Romulus; the other half made arrangements to be under arms, as criticising volunteers, at various outer points of the solemnity.", "로물루스의 도시에 사는 영국인의 절반에게 아침 식사 초대장이 발송되었고, 나머지 절반은 그 엄숙한 행사의 바깥 여러 지점에서, 비평하는 의용병으로서 무장 태세를 갖추기로 작정했다."], ["The most high and illustrious English Signor Edgardo Dorrit, came post through the deep mud and ruts (from forming a surface under the improving Neapolitan nobility), to grace the occasion.", "더없이 고귀하고 저명한 영국인, 에드가르도 도릿 시뇨르께서, (날로 발전하는 나폴리 귀족들이 그 위에 표면을 깔아 놓은) 깊은 진창과 바큇자국을 뚫고 역마차로 달려와, 그 행사를 빛내 주었다."], ["The best hotel and all its culinary myrmidons, were set to work to prepare the feast.", "가장 좋은 호텔과 그곳의 온갖 주방 병졸들이 그 잔치를 준비하느라 동원되었다."], ["The drafts of Mr Dorrit almost constituted a run on the Torlonia Bank.", "도릿 씨가 발행한 어음들은 거의 토를로니아 은행에 대한 예금 인출 사태나 다름없었다."], ["The British Consul hadn’t had such a marriage in the whole of his Consularity.", "영국 영사는 그의 영사 재임 전 기간을 통틀어 이런 결혼식을 치러 본 적이 없었다."]], [["The day came, and the She-Wolf in the Capitol might have snarled with envy to see how the Island Savages contrived these things now-a-days.", "마침내 그 날이 왔고, 카피톨리노의 암늑대도, 섬나라 야만인들이 요즘 들어 이런 일들을 어찌 꾸려 내는지를 보았더라면 시샘에 으르렁댔을 법했다. 조각가들이 그 흉악스러운 추함을 아무리 아첨으로도 덜어 주지 못했던, 군대 출신 사악한 황제들의 살벌한 머리통을 한 입상들이, 받침대에서 내려와 신부를 채 가려 했을 법했다."], ["The murderous-headed statues of the wicked Emperors of the Soldiery, whom sculptors had not been able to flatter out of their villainous hideousness, might have come off their pedestals to run away with the Bride.", "옛적에 검투사들이 몸을 씻던, 막혀 버린 그 늙은 분수가 다시 생기를 얻어 솟구쳐 그 예식을 기리려 했을 법했다. 베스타 신전이 그 폐허에서 새로이 솟아올라, 오로지 그 행사에 위신을 보태려 했을 법했다."], ["The choked old fountain, where erst the gladiators washed, might have leaped into life again to honour the ceremony.", "그랬을 법은 했으나, 그러지 않았다. 감각 있는 존재들처럼 — 더러는 만물의 영장인 귀족 나리와 귀부인들조차 그러하듯 — 많은 것을 할 법은 했으나, 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["The Temple of Vesta might have sprung up anew from its ruins, expressly to lend its countenance to the occasion.", "그 식은 감탄스러운 장엄함 속에 치러졌다. 검은 옷, 흰 옷, 적갈색 옷을 입은 수도사들이 걸음을 멈추고 마차들을 바라보았다."], ["Might have done; but did not.", "양털을 두른 떠돌이 농민들이 집집의 창문 아래에서 구걸하며 피리를 불었다. 영국인 의용병들이 줄지어 행진했다."], ["Like sentient things--even like the lords and ladies of creation sometimes--might have done much, but did nothing.", "날은 저녁 기도 시각으로 기울어 갔다. 축제는 사위어 갔다."], ["The celebration went off with admirable pomp; monks in black robes, white robes, and russet robes stopped to look after the carriages; wandering peasants in fleeces of sheep, begged and piped under the house-windows; the English volunteers defiled; the day wore on to the hour of vespers; the festival wore away; the thousand churches rang their bells without any reference to it; and St Peter denied that he had anything to do with it.", "천 개의 교회들이 그 일과는 아무 상관 없이 종을 울렸다. 그리고 성 베드로는 자기가 그 일과 무슨 관련이 있다는 것을 부인했다."]], [["But by that time the Bride was near the end of the first day’s journey towards Florence.", "그러나 그 무렵 신부는 피렌체를 향한 첫째 날 여정의 끝에 거의 다다라 있었다."], ["It was the peculiarity of the nuptials that they were all Bride.", "이 혼례의 특이한 점은, 온통 신부뿐이었다는 것이다."], ["Nobody noticed the Bridegroom.", "신랑에게는 아무도 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["Nobody noticed the first Bridesmaid.", "첫째 들러리에게도 아무도 눈길을 주지 않았다."], ["Few could have seen Little Dorrit (who held that post) for the glare, even supposing many to have sought her.", "설령 많은 이가 그녀를 찾았다 한들, 그 눈부신 광채 때문에 리틀 도릿을(그 자리를 맡은 사람이었다) 알아본 이는 거의 없었으리라."], ["So, the Bride had mounted into her handsome chariot, incidentally accompanied by the Bridegroom; and after rolling for a few minutes smoothly over a fair pavement, had begun to jolt through a Slough of Despond, and through a long, long avenue of wrack and ruin.", "그리하여 신부는 멋들어진 사두마차에 올라탔고, 어쩌다 보니 신랑이 함께 탔다. 그러고는 평탄한 포장도로 위를 몇 분 매끄럽게 굴러간 뒤, 절망의 수렁을 지나며, 또 길고 긴 파멸과 황폐의 가로수길을 지나며 덜컹대기 시작했다."], ["Other nuptial carriages are said to have gone the same road, before and since.", "다른 혼례 마차들도 그 전이나 후에 같은 길을 갔다고들 한다."]], [["If Little Dorrit found herself left a little lonely and a little low that night, nothing would have done so much against her feeling of depression as the being able to sit at work by her father, as in the old time, and help him to his supper and his rest.", "만약 그날 밤 리틀 도릿이 다소 외롭고 다소 쓸쓸한 처지에 놓였다면, 그 우울한 기분을 떨치는 데, 옛날처럼 아버지 곁에 앉아 바느질하며 그분의 저녁상과 휴식을 거들어 드릴 수 있는 일만큼 효험 있는 것은 없었을 것이다."], ["But that was not to be thought of now, when they sat in the state-equipage with Mrs General on the coach-box.", "그러나 마부석에 제너럴 부인을 앉히고 의전용 마차에 앉아 있는 지금에 와서는, 그런 일은 생각조차 할 수 없었다."], ["And as to supper!", "더구나 저녁상이라니!"], ["If Mr Dorrit had wanted supper, there was an Italian cook and there was a Swiss confectioner, who must have put on caps as high as the Pope’s Mitre, and have performed the mysteries of Alchemists in a copper-saucepaned laboratory below, before he could have got it.", "만약 도릿 씨가 저녁상을 원했다면, 이탈리아인 요리사가 있고 스위스인 제과사가 있어서, 그들이 교황의 삼중관만큼이나 높은 모자를 쓰고서, 아래층의 구리 냄비를 갖춘 실험실에서 연금술사의 신비를 행하고 난 뒤라야 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["He was sententious and didactic that night.", "그날 밤 그는 격언조에 훈계조였다."], ["If he had been simply loving, he would have done Little Dorrit more good; but she accepted him as he was--when had she not accepted him as he was!--and made the most and best of him.", "그저 다정하기만 했더라면 리틀 도릿에게 더 큰 위안이 되었으련만, 그녀는 그를 있는 그대로 받아들였다 — 그녀가 그를 있는 그대로 받아들이지 않은 적이 언제 있었던가! — 그리하여 그에게서 가장 많은 것을, 가장 좋은 것을 끌어냈다."], ["Mrs General at length retired.", "마침내 제너럴 부인이 물러갔다."], ["Her retirement for the night was always her frostiest ceremony, as if she felt it necessary that the human imagination should be chilled into stone to prevent its following her.", "밤이 되어 물러가는 일은 그녀에게 늘 가장 쌀쌀맞은 의식이었으니, 마치 인간의 상상력이 자기를 뒤따르지 못하도록 그것을 돌처럼 얼려 버려야 한다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["When she had gone through her rigid preliminaries, amounting to a sort of genteel platoon-exercise, she withdrew.", "일종의 점잖은 소대 훈련에 맞먹는 그 뻣뻣한 예비 절차를 다 마치고 나서야, 그녀는 물러갔다."], ["Little Dorrit then put her arm round her father’s neck, to bid him good night.", "그러고 나서 리틀 도릿은 아버지의 목에 팔을 둘러 밤 인사를 했다."]], [["‘Amy, my dear,’ said Mr Dorrit, taking her by the hand, ‘this is the close of a day, that has--ha--greatly impressed and gratified me.’", "\"에이미, 얘야,\" 도릿 씨가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"오늘은, — 하 — 나를 크게 감동시키고 흐뭇하게 한 하루의 마무리로구나.\""]], [["‘A little tired you, dear, too?’", "\"그리고 좀 피곤하시기도 하시지요, 아버지?\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘no: I am not sensible of fatigue when it arises from an occasion so--hum--replete with gratification of the purest kind.’", "\"아니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"아니야. 이렇게 — 흠 — 더없이 순수한 종류의 흐뭇함으로 가득 찬 행사에서 비롯된 것이라면, 나는 피로를 느끼지 않는단다.\""]], [["Little Dorrit was glad to find him in such heart, and smiled from her own heart.", "리틀 도릿은 그가 그토록 기운찬 것을 보고 기뻤고, 자기 마음에서 우러나 미소 지었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ he continued, ‘this is an occasion--ha--teeming with a good example.", "\"얘야,\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["With a good example, my favourite and attached child--hum--to you.’", "\"이건 — 하 — 좋은 본보기로 가득 찬 행사다. 좋은 본보기로 말이다, 내가 아끼고 정든 자식아, — 흠 — 너에게 말이다.\""]], [["Little Dorrit, fluttered by his words, did not know what to say, though he stopped as if he expected her to say something.", "리틀 도릿은 그의 말에 가슴이 두근거려 무어라 해야 할지 몰랐다. 그가 마치 그녀가 무슨 말이라도 하기를 기대하는 듯 말을 멈췄는데도 그러했다."]], [["‘Amy,’ he resumed; ‘your dear sister, our Fanny, has contracted ha hum--a marriage, eminently calculated to extend the basis of our--ha--connection, and to--hum--consolidate our social relations.", "\"에이미,\" 그가 다시 말했다."], ["My love, I trust that the time is not far distant when some--ha--eligible partner may be found for you.’", "\"네 사랑하는 언니, 우리 패니가 — 하 흠 — 혼약을 맺었으니, 그것은 우리의 — 하 — 연줄의 기반을 넓히고, 우리의 — 흠 — 사회적 관계를 공고히 하는 데 더없이 알맞은 것이다. 내 사랑하는 아이야, 나는 머지않아 너에게도 — 하 — 어울리는 짝이 나타날 날이 멀지 않기를 바란다.\""]], [["‘Oh no!", "\"오, 아니에요!"], ["Let me stay with you.", "저는 아버지 곁에 있게 해 주세요."], ["I beg and pray that I may stay with you!", "부디 간청하건대, 아버지 곁에 머물게 해 주세요!"], ["I want nothing but to stay and take care of you!’", "저는 그저 곁에 남아 아버지를 보살펴 드리는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않아요!\""]], [["She said it like one in sudden alarm.", "그녀는 갑작스러운 두려움에 사로잡힌 사람처럼 그렇게 말했다."]], [["‘Nay, Amy, Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"아니, 에이미, 에이미야,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘This is weak and foolish, weak and foolish.", "\"그건 나약하고 어리석은 말이다, 나약하고 어리석어."], ["You have a--ha--responsibility imposed upon you by your position.", "너에게는 너의 처지가 지운 — 하 — 책무가 있다. 그것은 그 처지를 발전시키고, 그 처지에 — 흠 — 어울리는 사람이 되는 일이다."], ["It is to develop that position, and be--hum--worthy of that position.", "나를 보살피는 일로 말하자면, 나는 — 하 — 내 한 몸은 내가 건사할 수 있다."], ["As to taking care of me; I can--ha--take care of myself.", "아니,\" 그는 잠시 후 덧붙였다."], ["Or,’ he added after a moment, ‘if I should need to be taken care of, I--hum--can, with the--ha--blessing of Providence, be taken care of, I--ha hum--I cannot, my dear child, think of engrossing, and--ha--as it were, sacrificing you.’", "\"혹여 내가 누군가의 보살핌을 받아야 할 처지가 된다면, 나는 — 흠 — 섭리의 — 하 — 축복으로 보살핌을 받을 수 있을 거다. 나는 — 하 흠 — 내 사랑하는 아이야, 너를 독차지하고, — 하 — 말하자면, 너를 희생시키는 일은 생각할 수 없구나.\""]], [["O what a time of day at which to begin that profession of self-denial; at which to make it, with an air of taking credit for it; at which to believe it, if such a thing could be!", "오, 저런 자기희생의 선언을 꺼내기에 어찌 이리도 늦은 시각이며, 그것을 마치 그 공을 자기 것으로 치는 듯한 태도로 늘어놓기에는, 또 만약 그런 일이 가능하기라도 하다면 그것을 정말로 믿기에는!"]], [["‘Don’t speak, Amy.", "\"말하지 마라, 에이미."], ["I positively say I cannot do it.", "나는 단언컨대 그럴 수 없다."], ["I--ha--must not do it.", "나는 — 하 — 그래서는 안 된다."], ["My--hum--conscience would not allow it.", "내 — 흠 — 양심이 그것을 허락지 않을 거다."], ["I therefore, my love, take the opportunity afforded by this gratifying and impressive occasion of--ha--solemnly remarking, that it is now a cherished wish and purpose of mine to see you--ha--eligibly (I repeat eligibly) married.’", "그러므로, 내 사랑하는 아이야, 나는 이 흐뭇하고 인상 깊은 행사가 마련해 준 기회를 빌려 — 하 — 엄숙히 말해 두는 바다. 너를 — 하 — 어울리게(거듭 말하건대 어울리게) 시집보내는 일이, 이제 내가 소중히 품은 소망이요 목적이라고 말이다.\""]], [["‘Oh no, dear!", "\"오, 안 돼요, 아버지!"], ["Pray!’", "제발요!\""]], [["‘Amy,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I am well persuaded that if the topic were referred to any person of superior social knowledge, of superior delicacy and sense--let us say, for instance, to--ha--Mrs General--that there would not be two opinions as to the--hum--affectionate character and propriety of my sentiments.", "\"에이미,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["But, as I know your loving and dutiful nature from--hum--from experience, I am quite satisfied that it is necessary to say no more.", "\"이 화제를, 더 높은 사회적 식견과, 더 높은 섬세함과 분별을 갖춘 누군가에게 — 이를테면, — 하 — 제너럴 부인 같은 분께 — 가져간다면, 내 심정의 — 흠 — 애정 어린 성격과 그 온당함에 대해 두 가지 의견이 나올 리 없으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다. 그러나 나는 너의 사랑스럽고 효성스러운 천성을 — 흠 — 경험으로 알기에, 더는 말할 필요가 없다는 데 아주 흡족하구나."], ["I have--hum--no husband to propose at present, my dear: I have not even one in view.", "나는 — 흠 — 지금 당장 제안할 남편감이 있는 것도 아니다, 얘야. 점찍어 둔 사람조차 없어."], ["I merely wish that we should--ha--understand each other.", "나는 다만 우리가 — 하 — 서로를 이해했으면 할 뿐이다."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Good night, my dear and sole remaining daughter.", "잘 자거라, 내게 남은 단 하나의 사랑하는 딸아."], ["Good night.", "잘 자거라."], ["God bless you!’", "신의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["If the thought ever entered Little Dorrit’s head that night, that he could give her up lightly now in his prosperity, and when he had it in his mind to replace her with a second wife, she drove it away.", "그날 밤 만약 리틀 도릿의 머릿속에, 아버지가 이제 형편이 펴진 마당에, 게다가 자기를 두 번째 아내로 대신할 마음을 품고서, 자기를 가벼이 내칠 수 있겠구나 하는 생각이 떠올랐다 한들, 그녀는 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["Faithful to him still, as in the worst times through which she had borne him single-handed, she drove the thought away; and entertained no harder reflection, in her tearful unrest, than that he now saw everything through their wealth, and through the care he always had upon him that they should continue rich, and grow richer.", "그를 홀로 떠받쳐 온 그 가장 험난한 시절에 그랬듯이 여전히 그에게 충실하게, 그녀는 그 생각을 떨쳐 버렸다. 그리고 눈물 어린 안타까움 속에서도, 그가 이제 모든 것을 그들의 재물을 통해, 그리고 그들이 계속 부유하기를, 더욱 부유해지기를 바라는 늘 마음에 품은 그 근심을 통해 바라보게 되었구나, 하는 것 이상으로 모진 생각은 품지 않았다."]], [["They sat in their equipage of state, with Mrs General on the box, for three weeks longer, and then he started for Florence to join Fanny.", "그들은 마부석에 제너럴 부인을 앉힌 채 의전용 마차에 앉아 삼 주를 더 보냈고, 그러고 나서 그는 패니와 합류하러 피렌체로 떠났다."], ["Little Dorrit would have been glad to bear him company so far, only for the sake of her own love, and then to have turned back alone, thinking of dear England.", "리틀 도릿은 오로지 자신의 애정을 위해서라도 거기까지 그와 동행했다가, 사랑하는 영국을 생각하며 혼자 되돌아오고 싶었으리라."], ["But, though the Courier had gone on with the Bride, the Valet was next in the line; and the succession would not have come to her, as long as any one could be got for money.", "그러나 길잡이는 신부와 함께 떠나 버렸고, 그다음 순번은 시종이었으니, 돈으로 부릴 수 있는 사람이 하나라도 남아 있는 한 그 차례가 그녀에게까지 돌아올 리 없었다."]], [["Mrs General took life easily--as easily, that is, as she could take anything--when the Roman establishment remained in their sole occupation; and Little Dorrit would often ride out in a hired carriage that was left them, and alight alone and wander among the ruins of old Rome.", "로마의 살림이 그들 두 사람만의 차지로 남게 되자, 제너럴 부인은 마음 편히 — 즉, 무슨 일이든 그녀가 마음 편히 받아들일 수 있는 그만큼 마음 편히 — 지냈다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 그들에게 남겨진 세낸 마차를 타고 자주 나가서는, 혼자 내려 고대 로마의 폐허 사이를 거닐곤 했다."], ["The ruins of the vast old Amphitheatre, of the old Temples, of the old commemorative Arches, of the old trodden highways, of the old tombs, besides being what they were, to her were ruins of the old Marshalsea--ruins of her own old life--ruins of the faces and forms that of old peopled it--ruins of its loves, hopes, cares, and joys.", "그 광대한 옛 원형경기장의 폐허, 옛 신전들의 폐허, 옛 기념 개선문들의 폐허, 옛적에 사람들이 밟고 다니던 큰길들의 폐허, 옛 무덤들의 폐허는, 그것들 본연의 모습인 동시에, 그녀에게는 옛 마셜시의 폐허이기도 했다 — 그녀 자신의 옛 삶의 폐허, 옛적에 그곳을 채웠던 얼굴들과 모습들의 폐허, 그곳의 사랑과 희망과 근심과 기쁨의 폐허이기도 했다."], ["Two ruined spheres of action and suffering were before the solitary girl often sitting on some broken fragment; and in the lonely places, under the blue sky, she saw them both together.", "무너진 어느 파편 위에 자주 홀로 앉아 있는 그 외로운 처녀 앞에는, 행위와 고난의 두 폐허가 놓여 있었다. 그리고 그 적막한 곳에서, 푸른 하늘 아래에서, 그녀는 그 둘을 함께 보았다."]], [["Up, then, would come Mrs General; taking all the colour out of everything, as Nature and Art had taken it out of herself; writing Prunes and Prism, in Mr Eustace’s text, wherever she could lay a hand; looking everywhere for Mr Eustace and company, and seeing nothing else; scratching up the driest little bones of antiquity, and bolting them whole without any human visitings--like a Ghoule in gloves.", "그러면 어김없이 제너럴 부인이 나타났다. 자연과 기예가 자기 자신에게서 온갖 색채를 다 빼내 버렸듯이, 그녀는 모든 것에서 색채를 빼내 버리면서. 손을 댈 수 있는 데라면 어디든 유스터스 씨의 본문대로 프룬과 프리즘을 적어 넣으면서. 어디서나 유스터스 씨와 그 일행을 찾으며 그 밖에는 아무것도 보지 못하면서. 고대의 가장 메마른 잔뼈들을 긁어모아, 인간다운 감흥이라곤 하나 없이 통째로 삼키면서 — 마치 장갑 낀 식시귀(食屍鬼)처럼."]], [["# CHAPTER 16. Getting on", "# CHAPTER 16. Getting on"]], [["The newly married pair, on their arrival in Harley Street, Cavendish Square, London, were received by the Chief Butler.", "할리 가, 캐번디시 광장, 런던에 막 도착한 신혼부부는 집사장의 영접을 받았다."], ["That great man was not interested in them, but on the whole endured them.", "그 위대한 인물은 그들에게 관심이 없었으나, 대체로는 참아 주었다."], ["People must continue to be married and given in marriage, or Chief Butlers would not be wanted.", "사람들이 결혼하고 시집 장가 가는 일이 계속되어야 집사장도 필요해지는 법이다."], ["As nations are made to be taxed, so families are made to be butlered.", "국가가 세금을 매기기 위해 만들어지듯, 가문은 집사를 두기 위해 만들어진다."], ["The Chief Butler, no doubt, reflected that the course of nature required the wealthy population to be kept up, on his account.", "집사장은 의심할 바 없이, 자연의 이치란 자신을 위해서라도 부유한 인구가 유지되기를 요구한다고 곰곰이 생각하고 있었을 것이다."]], [["He therefore condescended to look at the carriage from the Hall-door without frowning at it, and said, in a very handsome way, to one of his men, ‘Thomas, help with the luggage.’ He even escorted the Bride up-stairs into Mr Merdle’s presence; but this must be considered as an act of homage to the sex (of which he was an admirer, being notoriously captivated by the charms of a certain Duchess), and not as a committal of himself with the family.", "그리하여 그는 현관에서 마차를 내려다보되 눈살을 찌푸리지 않을 만큼은 너그러이 굴었고, 매우 점잖은 태도로 부하 하나에게 일렀다. \"토머스, 짐 옮기는 걸 거들어라.\" 그는 신부를 위층의 머들 씨 앞으로까지 안내하기도 했지만, 이는 그가 가문과 자신을 결부시키는 행위로서가 아니라, 여성에 대한 경의의 표시로 보아야 마땅하다(그는 여성의 숭배자였으니, 어느 공작 부인의 매력에 사로잡혀 있다는 사실은 익히 알려진 바였다)."]], [["Mr Merdle was slinking about the hearthrug, waiting to welcome Mrs Sparkler.", "머들 씨는 벽난로 앞 깔개 주위를 어슬렁거리며 스파클러 부인을 맞이하려고 기다리고 있었다."], ["His hand seemed to retreat up his sleeve as he advanced to do so, and he gave her such a superfluity of coat-cuff that it was like being received by the popular conception of Guy Fawkes.", "그가 그러려고 다가서는데 그의 손은 소맷자락 속으로 움츠러드는 듯했고, 외투 소맷부리를 어찌나 잔뜩 내미는지 마치 사람들이 흔히 떠올리는 가이 포크스의 형상에게 영접받는 것 같았다."], ["When he put his lips to hers, besides, he took himself into custody by the wrists, and backed himself among the ottomans and chairs and tables as if he were his own Police officer, saying to himself, ‘Now, none of that!", "게다가 그가 그녀의 입술에 제 입술을 갖다 댈 때는, 마치 제 손목을 스스로 붙잡아 구금하듯 하더니, 자기 자신의 경찰관이라도 된 양 오토만과 의자와 탁자 사이로 뒷걸음질 치며 속으로 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다. \"자, 허튼짓은 안 돼!"], ["Come!", "가자!"], ["I’ve got you, you know, and you go quietly along with me!’", "내가 널 붙잡았으니, 얌전히 따라와야지!\""]], [["Mrs Sparkler, installed in the rooms of state--the innermost sanctuary of down, silk, chintz, and fine linen--felt that so far her triumph was good, and her way made, step by step.", "귀빈실에 들여진 스파클러 부인은—솜털과 비단과 친츠와 고운 아마포의 가장 깊숙한 성소에서—여기까지 자신의 개선이 훌륭했으며, 한 걸음 한 걸음 자기 길을 닦아 왔다고 느꼈다."], ["On the day before her marriage, she had bestowed on Mrs Merdle’s maid with an air of gracious indifference, in Mrs Merdle’s presence, a trifling little keepsake (bracelet, bonnet, and two dresses, all new) about four times as valuable as the present formerly made by Mrs Merdle to her.", "결혼 전날, 그녀는 머들 부인이 보는 앞에서 머들 부인의 하녀에게 우아한 무심함을 가장하며 소소한 기념품(팔찌와 보닛과 새 옷 두 벌, 모두 새것)을 건넸는데, 그것은 예전에 머들 부인이 그녀에게 주었던 선물의 네 배쯤 값나가는 것이었다."], ["She was now established in Mrs Merdle’s own rooms, to which some extra touches had been given to render them more worthy of her occupation.", "이제 그녀는 머들 부인 자신의 방에 자리 잡았는데, 그 방은 그녀의 거처로 더욱 걸맞도록 몇 가지 손질이 더해진 터였다."], ["In her mind’s eye, as she lounged there, surrounded by every luxurious accessory that wealth could obtain or invention devise, she saw the fair bosom that beat in unison with the exultation of her thoughts, competing with the bosom that had been famous so long, outshining it, and deposing it.", "마음의 눈으로, 부가 살 수 있고 창의가 고안할 수 있는 온갖 호사스러운 장식에 둘러싸여 거기 비스듬히 기대 있노라니, 그녀는 자기 생각의 환희와 한 박자로 고동치는 그 어여쁜 가슴이, 오래도록 명성을 떨쳐 온 그 가슴과 겨루어, 그것을 능가하고, 그것을 폐위시키는 광경을 보았다. 행복했느냐고?"], ["Happy? Fanny must have been happy.", "패니는 분명 행복했으리라."], ["No more wishing one’s self dead now.", "이제 더는 차라리 죽었으면 하고 바라는 일 따위는 없었다."]], [["The Courier had not approved of Mr Dorrit’s staying in the house of a friend, and had preferred to take him to an hotel in Brook Street, Grosvenor Square.", "쿠리어는 도릿 씨가 친구의 집에 머무르는 것을 탐탁지 않게 여겨, 그를 그로스버너 광장 브룩 가의 어느 호텔로 모시는 편을 택했다."], ["Mr Merdle ordered his carriage to be ready early in the morning that he might wait upon Mr Dorrit immediately after breakfast.", "머들 씨는 아침을 들자마자 곧바로 도릿 씨를 찾아뵐 수 있도록 마차를 이른 아침에 대기시키라 명했다."]], [["Bright the carriage looked, sleek the horses looked, gleaming the harness looked, luscious and lasting the liveries looked.", "마차는 번쩍번쩍 빛났고, 말들은 윤기가 흘렀으며, 마구는 반들거렸고, 제복은 화사하면서도 오래갈 듯 보였다."], ["A rich, responsible turn-out.", "호화롭고 묵직한 위세의 행차였다."], ["An equipage for a Merdle.", "머들에게 어울리는 의장이었다."], ["Early people looked after it as it rattled along the streets, and said, with awe in their breath, ‘There he goes!’", "아침 일찍 나온 사람들은 그것이 거리를 덜컹덜컹 달려갈 때 그 뒤를 바라보며, 숨결에 경외를 담아 이렇게 말했다. \"저기 그분이 가신다!\""]], [["There he went, until Brook Street stopped him.", "거기 그분이 가셨다, 브룩 가가 그를 멈춰 세울 때까지."], ["Then, forth from its magnificent case came the jewel; not lustrous in itself, but quite the contrary.", "그러자 그 으리으리한 함에서 보석이 나왔으니, 그 자체로는 광채라곤 없었고, 오히려 정반대였다."]], [["Commotion in the office of the hotel. Merdle!", "호텔 사무실에 한바탕 소동이 일었다."], ["The landlord, though a gentleman of a haughty spirit who had just driven a pair of thorough-bred horses into town, turned out to show him up-stairs.", "머들이라니! 막 순종마 한 쌍을 도시로 몰고 들어온 오만한 기질의 신사인 호텔 주인이, 몸소 나와 그를 위층으로 안내했다."], ["The clerks and servants cut him off by back-passages, and were found accidentally hovering in doorways and angles, that they might look upon him.", "점원들과 하인들은 뒤쪽 복도로 질러 가서, 그를 한 번 보려고 우연인 척 문간이며 모퉁이에 서성였다. 머들이라니!"], ["Merdle! O ye sun, moon, and stars, the great man!", "오, 해여, 달이여, 별들이여, 저 위대한 인물!"], ["The rich man, who had in a manner revised the New Testament, and already entered into the kingdom of Heaven.", "어떤 의미로는 신약 성서를 개정하고, 이미 천국에 들어선 부자."], ["The man who could have any one he chose to dine with him, and who had made the money!", "누구든 마음에 드는 이를 골라 함께 식사할 수 있는 사람, 그 돈을 벌어들인 사람!"], ["As he went up the stairs, people were already posted on the lower stairs, that his shadow might fall upon them when he came down.", "그가 계단을 올라가는 동안, 사람들은 벌써 아래쪽 계단에 자리를 잡고 서서, 그가 내려올 때 그의 그림자라도 자기들 위에 드리워지기를 바랐다."], ["So were the sick brought out and laid in the track of the Apostle--who had _not_ got into the good society, and had _not_ made the money.", "마치 병자들을 데려다가 사도가 지나는 길목에 누이던 것과 같았으니—다만 그 사도는 상류 사회에 들지도 못했고, 그 돈을 벌어들이지도 못했지만 말이다."]], [["Mr Dorrit, dressing-gowned and newspapered, was at his breakfast.", "도릿 씨는 실내복 차림으로 신문을 들고 아침을 들고 있었다."], ["The Courier, with agitation in his voice, announced ‘Miss Mairdale!’ Mr Dorrit’s overwrought heart bounded as he leaped up.", "쿠리어가 떨리는 목소리로 \"메어들 씨!\"라고 아뢰었다. 도릿 씨는 긴장으로 한껏 부풀어 오른 가슴을 안고 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["‘Mr Merdle, this is--ha--indeed an honour.", "\"머들 씨, 이건—하—참으로 영광입니다."], ["Permit me to express the--hum--sense, the high sense, I entertain of this--ha hum--highly gratifying act of attention.", "부디 제가 이—흠—이토록—하 흠—더없이 흐뭇한 배려에 대해 품고 있는 마음을, 그 깊은 마음을 표하도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["I am well aware, sir, of the many demands upon your time, and its--ha--enormous value,’ Mr Dorrit could not say enormous roundly enough for his own satisfaction.", "저는, 선생님, 선생님의 시간에 얼마나 많은 요구가 쏟아지는지, 또 그 시간이—하—얼마나 막대한 가치를 지니는지 잘 알고 있습니다.\" 도릿 씨는 자기 만족에 차도록 '막대한'이라는 말을 충분히 둥글게 발음하지 못했다."], ["‘That you should--ha--at this early hour, bestow any of your priceless time upon me, is--ha--a compliment that I acknowledge with the greatest esteem.’ Mr Dorrit positively trembled in addressing the great man.", "\"그런데도 선생님께서—하—이렇게 이른 시각에 그 값을 매길 수 없는 시간을 제게 베풀어 주신다는 것은—하—제가 더없는 존경으로 받들어 인정하는 영예입니다.\" 도릿 씨는 그 위대한 인물에게 말을 건네며 그야말로 떨고 있었다."]], [["Mr Merdle uttered, in his subdued, inward, hesitating voice, a few sounds that were to no purpose whatever; and finally said, ‘I am glad to see you, sir.’", "머들 씨는 낮고 안으로 잦아드는 머뭇거리는 목소리로, 아무 뜻도 없는 소리를 몇 마디 내뱉더니, 마침내 이렇게 말했다. \"뵙게 되어 기쁩니다, 선생.\""]], [["‘You are very kind,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"참으로 친절하십니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Truly kind.’ By this time the visitor was seated, and was passing his great hand over his exhausted forehead.", "\"진실로 친절하십니다.\" 이때쯤 손님은 자리에 앉아, 지친 이마 위로 그 큼직한 손을 쓸어내리고 있었다."], ["‘You are well, I hope, Mr Merdle?’", "\"강녕하시지요, 머들 씨?\""]], [["‘I am as well as I--yes, I am as well as I usually am,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"저야 뭐—그렇습니다, 평소만큼은 괜찮습니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Your occupations must be immense.’", "\"하시는 일이 어마어마하시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Tolerably so.", "\"그럭저럭 그렇지요."], ["But--Oh dear no, there’s not much the matter with _me_,’ said Mr Merdle, looking round the room.", "하지만—아니, 천만에, 저야 별로 문제될 게 없습니다.\" 머들 씨가 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["‘A little dyspeptic?’ Mr Dorrit hinted.", "\"소화가 좀 안 되시는지요?\" 도릿 씨가 넌지시 물었다."]], [["‘Very likely.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요."], ["But I--Oh, I am well enough,’ said Mr Merdle.", "하지만 저는—아, 저는 멀쩡합니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["There were black traces on his lips where they met, as if a little train of gunpowder had been fired there; and he looked like a man who, if his natural temperament had been quicker, would have been very feverish that morning.", "그의 입술이 맞닿는 자리에는 마치 작은 화약 도화선이라도 터진 듯 거뭇한 자국이 나 있었다. 그리고 그는, 타고난 기질이 좀 더 빨랐더라면 그날 아침 몹시 신열에 시달렸을 법한 사람처럼 보였다."], ["This, and his heavy way of passing his hand over his forehead, had prompted Mr Dorrit’s solicitous inquiries.", "이것과, 그가 이마 위로 손을 무겁게 쓸어내리는 모양이 도릿 씨의 근심 어린 물음을 자아낸 것이었다."]], [["‘Mrs Merdle,’ Mr Dorrit insinuatingly pursued, ‘I left, as you will be prepared to hear, the--ha--observed of all observers, the--hum--admired of all admirers, the leading fascination and charm of Society in Rome.", "\"머들 부인은요,\" 도릿 씨가 알랑거리듯 말을 이었다."], ["She was looking wonderfully well when I quitted it.’", "\"선생님께서도 짐작하시겠지만, 제가 떠나올 적에—하—모든 이가 주목하는 분이요—흠—모든 이가 흠모하는 분이요, 로마 사교계의 으뜸가는 매혹이자 매력이셨습니다. 제가 그곳을 떠날 때 부인께서는 놀랍도록 좋아 보이셨습니다.\""]], [["‘Mrs Merdle,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘is generally considered a very attractive woman.", "\"머들 부인은,\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["And she is, no doubt.", "\"대개 매우 매력적인 여인으로 여겨지지요."], ["I am sensible of her being so.’", "그리고 분명 그렇습니다. 나도 그 점은 잘 알고 있고요.\""]], [["‘Who can be otherwise?’ responded Mr Dorrit.", "\"그렇지 않을 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?\" 도릿 씨가 응수했다."]], [["Mr Merdle turned his tongue in his closed mouth--it seemed rather a stiff and unmanageable tongue--moistened his lips, passed his hand over his forehead again, and looked all round the room again, principally under the chairs.", "머들 씨는 다문 입속에서 혀를 굴렸는데—꽤나 뻣뻣하고 다루기 힘든 혀 같았다—입술을 축이고, 다시 이마 위로 손을 쓸고, 또다시 방 안을 죽 둘러보았으니, 주로 의자 밑을 살폈다."]], [["‘But,’ he said, looking Mr Dorrit in the face for the first time, and immediately afterwards dropping his eyes to the buttons of Mr Dorrit’s waistcoat; ‘if we speak of attractions, your daughter ought to be the subject of our conversation.", "\"하지만,\" 그가 처음으로 도릿 씨의 얼굴을 바라보고는 곧바로 도릿 씨의 조끼 단추로 눈을 떨구며 말했다."], ["She is extremely beautiful.", "\"매력을 말하자면, 우리 대화의 주제가 되어야 할 분은 따님이지요."], ["Both in face and figure, she is quite uncommon.", "그분은 더없이 아름답습니다. 얼굴이며 자태가 참으로 비범하시지요."], ["When the young people arrived last night, I was really surprised to see such charms.’", "어젯밤 젊은이들이 도착했을 때, 나는 그런 매력을 보고 정말이지 놀랐습니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit’s gratification was such that he said--ha--he could not refrain from telling Mr Merdle verbally, as he had already done by letter, what honour and happiness he felt in this union of their families.", "도릿 씨의 흡족함이란 어찌나 컸던지, 그는—하—이미 편지로 전한 바를 머들 씨에게 직접 말씀드리지 않을 수 없다며, 두 가문의 이 결합으로 자기가 느끼는 영예와 행복을 토로했다."], ["And he offered his hand.", "그리고 손을 내밀었다."], ["Mr Merdle looked at the hand for a little while, took it on his for a moment as if his were a yellow salver or fish-slice, and then returned it to Mr Dorrit.", "머들 씨는 잠시 그 손을 바라보더니, 마치 자기 손이 노란 쟁반이나 생선용 칼이라도 되는 양 한순간 그 위에 얹었다가, 도릿 씨에게 도로 돌려주었다."]], [["‘I thought I would drive round the first thing,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘to offer my services, in case I can do anything for you; and to say that I hope you will at least do me the honour of dining with me to-day, and every day when you are not better engaged during your stay in town.’", "\"무엇보다 먼저 마차를 몰고 들러야겠다고 생각했습니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"혹시 제가 도와드릴 일이 있을지 모르니 제 도움을 드리고자 했고, 또 부디 오늘 저와 식사하는 영광을 베풀어 주십사, 그리고 도시에 머무시는 동안 더 나은 약속이 없는 날이면 언제든 그래 주십사 청하려고요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit was enraptured by these attentions.", "도릿 씨는 이러한 배려에 황홀해했다."]], [["‘Do you stay long, sir?’", "\"오래 머무십니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘I have not at present the intention,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘of--ha--exceeding a fortnight.’", "\"지금으로서는,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"보름을—하—넘길 생각은 없습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s a very short stay, after so long a journey,’ returned Mr Merdle.", "\"그토록 긴 여정 끝에 그건 너무 짧은 체류로군요.\" 머들 씨가 응했다."]], [["‘Hum.", "\"흠."], ["Yes,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "그렇지요.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘But the truth is--ha--my dear Mr Merdle, that I find a foreign life so well suited to my health and taste, that I--hum--have but two objects in my present visit to London.", "\"하지만 사실은—하—친애하는 머들 씨, 제가 외국 생활이 제 건강과 취향에 어찌나 잘 맞는지를 깨달아서, 저는—흠—이번 런던 방문에 두 가지 목적밖에 없습니다."], ["First, the--ha--the distinguished happiness and--ha--privilege which I now enjoy and appreciate; secondly, the arrangement--hum--the laying out, that is to say, in the best way, of--ha, hum--my money.’", "첫째는—하—제가 지금 누리며 감사히 여기는 이—하—각별한 행복과—하—특권이요, 둘째는 제—하, 흠—돈을 가장 좋은 방식으로 관리하는—흠—말하자면 운용하는 일입니다.\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ said Mr Merdle, after turning his tongue again, ‘if I can be of any use to you in that respect, you may command me.’", "\"그러시군요, 선생.\" 머들 씨가 다시 혀를 굴린 뒤 말했다. \"그 점에 대해 제가 무슨 쓸모가 있다면, 얼마든지 분부만 내리시지요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit’s speech had had more hesitation in it than usual, as he approached the ticklish topic, for he was not perfectly clear how so exalted a potentate might take it.", "도릿 씨의 말투는 이 까다로운 화제에 다가설수록 평소보다 더 많은 머뭇거림을 담고 있었으니, 그토록 드높은 권세가가 이를 어찌 받아들일지 도무지 분명치 않았던 까닭이다."], ["He had doubts whether reference to any individual capital, or fortune, might not seem a wretchedly retail affair to so wholesale a dealer.", "그는 어느 한 개인의 자본이나 재산을 들먹이는 일이, 그토록 도매로 거래하는 이에게는 한심하리만치 소매 같은 일로 비치지 않을까 미심쩍어했다."], ["Greatly relieved by Mr Merdle’s affable offer of assistance, he caught at it directly, and heaped acknowledgments upon him.", "머들 씨가 상냥하게 도움을 자청하자 크게 안도한 그는, 곧장 그 제안에 매달리며 감사를 산더미처럼 쏟아냈다."]], [["‘I scarcely--ha--dared,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘I assure you, to hope for so--hum--vast an advantage as your direct advice and assistance.", "\"저는 감히—하—꿈도 꾸지 못했습니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["Though of course I should, under any circumstances, like the--ha, hum--rest of the civilised world, have followed in Mr Merdle’s train.’", "\"정말이지, 선생님의 직접적인 조언과 도움이라는—흠—그토록 막대한 이득을 바랄 엄두를요. 물론 저야 어떤 상황에서든 문명 세계의 나머지 모든 이들처럼—하, 흠—머들 씨의 행렬을 뒤따랐겠지만요.\""]], [["‘You know we may almost say we are related, sir,’ said Mr Merdle, curiously interested in the pattern of the carpet, ‘and, therefore, you may consider me at your service.’", "\"우리는 거의 친척이라 할 수도 있지 않습니까, 선생.\" 머들 씨가 양탄자 무늬에 묘하게 관심을 두며 말했다. \"그러니 저를 마음껏 부리셔도 좋습니다.\""]], [["‘Ha.", "\"하."], ["Very handsome, indeed!’ cried Mr Dorrit.", "참으로 점잖으십니다!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Ha.", "\"하."], ["Most handsome!’", "더없이 점잖으십니다!\""]], [["‘It would not,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘be at the present moment easy for what I may call a mere outsider to come into any of the good things--of course I speak of my own good things--’", "\"이를테면 한낱 외부인이라 할 만한 사람이 지금 이 시점에 그 좋은 거리들 가운데—물론 제 거리들을 말하는 겁니다만—그 어느 하나에라도 끼어들기란 쉬운 일이 아닐 겁니다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Of course, of course!’ cried Mr Dorrit, in a tone implying that there were no other good things.", "\"물론이지요, 물론입니다!\" 도릿 씨가 다른 좋은 거리란 따로 없다는 듯한 어조로 외쳤다."]], [["‘--Unless at a high price.", "\"—아주 높은 값을 치르지 않고서는 말이지요."], ["At what we are accustomed to term a very long figure.’", "우리가 흔히 매우 긴 숫자라 부르는 그것 말입니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit laughed in the buoyancy of his spirit.", "도릿 씨는 들뜬 기분에 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["Long figure.", "긴 숫자라."], ["Good.", "좋군요."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Very expressive to be sure!", "참으로 적절한 표현이고말고요!"]], [["‘However,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I do generally retain in my own hands the power of exercising some preference--people in general would be pleased to call it favour--as a sort of compliment for my care and trouble.’", "\"하지만,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"저는 대개 어느 정도의 우대를 행사할 권한을—사람들은 흔히 그것을 호의라 부르고 싶어 하지요—제 손안에 쥐고 있습니다. 제 수고와 노고에 대한 일종의 사례 같은 것으로요.\""]], [["‘And public spirit and genius,’ Mr Dorrit suggested.", "\"그리고 공익심과 천재성에 대한 사례로요.\" 도릿 씨가 거들었다."]], [["Mr Merdle, with a dry, swallowing action, seemed to dispose of those qualities like a bolus; then added, ‘As a sort of return for it.", "머들 씨는 무언가를 삼키는 듯 메마른 동작으로, 그 자질들을 알약처럼 꿀꺽 처리해 버리는 것 같더니, 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"그에 대한 일종의 보답으로요."], ["I will see, if you please, how I can exert this limited power (for people are jealous, and it is limited), to your advantage.’", "괜찮으시다면, 제가 이 제한된 권한을(사람들이 시샘하는 터라 제한되어 있지요) 어떻게 선생께 유리하게 발휘할 수 있을지 살펴보겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You are very good,’ replied Mr Dorrit.", "\"참으로 친절하십니다.\" 도릿 씨가 답했다."], ["‘You are _very_ good.’", "\"정말로 친절하십니다.\""]], [["‘Of course,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘there must be the strictest integrity and uprightness in these transactions; there must be the purest faith between man and man; there must be unimpeached and unimpeachable confidence; or business could not be carried on.’", "\"물론,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"이런 거래에는 더없이 엄격한 청렴과 정직이 있어야 합니다. 사람과 사람 사이에 더없이 순수한 신의가 있어야 하고요. 흠 잡을 데 없고 흠 잡힐 데 없는 신뢰가 있어야 합니다. 그러지 않고서는 사업이란 굴러갈 수 없으니까요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit hailed these generous sentiments with fervour.", "도릿 씨는 이 너그러운 소회를 열렬히 반겼다."]], [["‘Therefore,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I can only give you a preference to a certain extent.’", "\"그러므로,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"저는 어느 한도까지만 선생께 우대를 드릴 수 있습니다.\""]], [["‘I perceive.", "\"알겠습니다."], ["To a defined extent,’ observed Mr Dorrit.", "정해진 한도까지 말씀이시군요.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Defined extent.", "\"정해진 한도까지요."], ["And perfectly above-board.", "그리고 완전히 떳떳하게요. 하지만 제 조언에 관해서라면,\""], ["As to my advice, however,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘that is another matter.", "머들 씨가 말했다. \"그건 별개의 문제입니다."], ["That, such as it is--’", "그것은, 변변찮은 것이나마—\""]], [["Oh!", "오!"], ["Such as it was!", "변변찮다니요!"], ["(Mr Dorrit could not bear the faintest appearance of its being depreciated, even by Mr Merdle himself.)", "(도릿 씨는, 설령 머들 씨 자신이 하는 말일지언정, 그것이 깎아내려지는 기미라곤 조금도 견딜 수 없었다.)"]], [["‘--That, there is nothing in the bonds of spotless honour between myself and my fellow-man to prevent my parting with, if I choose.", "\"—그것은, 제가 원한다면 나와 동포 사이의 티 없이 깨끗한 명예의 결속에 비추어 보아도 나누어 드리지 못할 까닭이 없는 것이지요. 그리고 그것은,\""], ["And that,’ said Mr Merdle, now deeply intent upon a dust-cart that was passing the windows, ‘shall be at your command whenever you think proper.’", "머들 씨가 이제 창밖을 지나가는 쓰레기 수레에 골똘히 빠져들며 말했다. \"선생께서 적절하다 여기실 때면 언제든 분부대로 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["New acknowledgments from Mr Dorrit.", "도릿 씨의 새로운 감사."], ["New passages of Mr Merdle’s hand over his forehead.", "머들 씨가 이마 위로 손을 쓸어내리는 새로운 동작."], ["Calm and silence.", "고요와 침묵."], ["Contemplation of Mr Dorrit’s waistcoat buttons by Mr Merdle.", "머들 씨가 도릿 씨의 조끼 단추를 응시함."]], [["‘My time being rather precious,’ said Mr Merdle, suddenly getting up, as if he had been waiting in the interval for his legs and they had just come, ‘I must be moving towards the City.", "\"제 시간이 다소 귀한지라,\" 머들 씨가, 마치 그동안 제 다리가 오기를 기다리다가 이제 막 그것이 도착하기라도 한 듯 불쑥 일어서며 말했다."], ["Can I take you anywhere, sir?", "\"이제 시티 쪽으로 움직여야겠습니다."], ["I shall be happy to set you down, or send you on.", "어디든 모셔다 드릴까요, 선생? 기꺼이 내려 드리거나 보내 드리겠습니다."], ["My carriage is at your disposal.’", "제 마차를 마음껏 쓰시지요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit bethought himself that he had business at his banker’s.", "도릿 씨는 자기 은행에 볼일이 있다는 데 생각이 미쳤다."], ["His banker’s was in the City.", "그의 은행은 시티에 있었다."], ["That was fortunate; Mr Merdle would take him into the City.", "잘된 일이었으니, 머들 씨가 그를 시티로 데려다 줄 터였다."], ["But, surely, he might not detain Mr Merdle while he assumed his coat?", "하지만 정말이지, 자기가 외투를 걸치는 동안 머들 씨를 붙들어 둘 수야 없지 않은가?"], ["Yes, he might and must; Mr Merdle insisted on it.", "아니, 그래도 되고말고, 그래야만 한다고 머들 씨가 우겼다."], ["So Mr Dorrit, retiring into the next room, put himself under the hands of his valet, and in five minutes came back glorious.", "그리하여 도릿 씨는 옆방으로 물러가 하인의 손에 몸을 맡겼고, 오 분 만에 의기양양하게 돌아왔다."]], [["Then said Mr Merdle, ‘Allow me, sir.", "그러자 머들 씨가 말했다. \"선생, 제게 기대시지요."], ["Take my arm!’ Then leaning on Mr Merdle’s arm, did Mr Dorrit descend the staircase, seeing the worshippers on the steps, and feeling that the light of Mr Merdle shone by reflection in himself.", "제 팔을 잡으십시오!\" 그리하여 도릿 씨는 머들 씨의 팔에 기대어 계단을 내려가며, 계단에 모인 숭배자들을 보았고, 머들 씨의 광채가 자기 안에서도 반사되어 빛나는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Then the carriage, and the ride into the City; and the people who looked at them; and the hats that flew off grey heads; and the general bowing and crouching before this wonderful mortal the like of which prostration of spirit was not to be seen--no, by high Heaven, no!", "이어서 마차, 그리고 시티로의 행차. 그들을 바라보는 사람들."], ["It may be worth thinking of by Fawners of all denominations--in Westminster Abbey and Saint Paul’s Cathedral put together, on any Sunday in the year.", "희끗희끗한 머리에서 벗겨져 날아오르는 모자들. 이 경이로운 인간 앞에서 모두가 굽실거리고 움츠리는 광경이라니, 그와 같은 영혼의 부복은—아니, 드높은 하늘에 맹세코, 어디서도 볼 수 없었으니!—온갖 종파의 아첨꾼들이 한 번쯤 곱씹어 볼 만한 일이거니와, 일 년 중 어느 일요일에 웨스트민스터 사원과 세인트 폴 대성당을 합쳐 놓는다 해도 그만한 광경은 볼 수 없으리라."], ["It was a rapturous dream to Mr Dorrit to find himself set aloft in this public car of triumph, making a magnificent progress to that befitting destination, the golden Street of the Lombards.", "이 공개된 개선의 수레 위에 높이 떠받들려, 그에 걸맞은 목적지인 황금빛 롬바르드 거리를 향해 으리으리한 행진을 펼치는 자신을 발견한 것은 도릿 씨에게 황홀한 꿈이었다."]], [["There Mr Merdle insisted on alighting and going his way a-foot, and leaving his poor equipage at Mr Dorrit’s disposition.", "거기서 머들 씨는 굳이 내려서 걸어가겠다고 우기며, 자신의 변변찮은 마차를 도릿 씨가 마음대로 쓰도록 남겨 두었다."], ["So the dream increased in rapture when Mr Dorrit came out of the bank alone, and people looked at _him_ in default of Mr Merdle, and when, with the ears of his mind, he heard the frequent exclamation as he rolled glibly along, ‘A wonderful man to be Mr Merdle’s friend!’", "그리하여 도릿 씨가 홀로 은행에서 나오고, 사람들이 머들 씨 대신 그를 바라보고, 그가 매끄럽게 굴러가는 동안 마음의 귀로 \"머들 씨의 친구라니 대단한 인물이로군!\" 하는 외침을 자꾸만 들을 때, 그 꿈은 더욱 황홀해졌다."]], [["At dinner that day, although the occasion was not foreseen and provided for, a brilliant company of such as are not made of the dust of the earth, but of some superior article for the present unknown, shed their lustrous benediction upon Mr Dorrit’s daughter’s marriage.", "그날 만찬에서는, 비록 미리 예견되거나 마련된 자리가 아니었음에도, 땅의 흙으로 빚어진 것이 아니라 아직은 알려지지 않은 어떤 우월한 재료로 빚어진 듯한 이들의 눈부신 무리가, 도릿 씨 딸의 결혼에 그 광채로운 축복을 내려 주었다."], ["And Mr Dorrit’s daughter that day began, in earnest, her competition with that woman not present; and began it so well that Mr Dorrit could all but have taken his affidavit, if required, that Mrs Sparkler had all her life been lying at full length in the lap of luxury, and had never heard of such a rough word in the English tongue as Marshalsea.", "그리고 그날 도릿 씨의 딸은 그 자리에 없는 그 여인과의 경쟁을 본격적으로 시작했으니, 어찌나 잘 시작했는지, 도릿 씨는 요구만 받는다면, 스파클러 부인이 평생토록 호사의 무릎에 길게 누워 지냈으며 영어에 마셜시 같은 험한 단어가 있다는 것조차 들어 본 적이 없노라고 거의 선서라도 할 수 있을 지경이었다."]], [["Next day, and the day after, and every day, all graced by more dinner company, cards descended on Mr Dorrit like theatrical snow.", "이튿날도, 그다음 날도, 그리고 날마다, 더 많은 만찬 손님으로 빛나는 가운데, 도릿 씨에게는 무대 위의 가짜 눈처럼 명함이 쏟아져 내렸다."], ["As the friend and relative by marriage of the illustrious Merdle, Bar, Bishop, Treasury, Chorus, Everybody, wanted to make or improve Mr Dorrit’s acquaintance.", "저명한 머들의 친구이자 혼인으로 맺어진 친척으로서, 법조계도, 주교도, 재무성도, 합창단도, 너 나 할 것 없이 모두가 도릿 씨와 사귀거나 친분을 두텁게 하고자 했다."], ["In Mr Merdle’s heap of offices in the City, when Mr Dorrit appeared at any of them on his business taking him Eastward (which it frequently did, for it throve amazingly), the name of Dorrit was always a passport to the great presence of Merdle.", "시티에 산더미처럼 늘어선 머들 씨의 사무실들 가운데 어디든, 도릿 씨가 자기 볼일로 동쪽으로 향할 때면(그런 일이 잦았으니, 사업이 놀랍도록 번창했던 까닭이다) 도릿이라는 이름은 언제나 머들이라는 위대한 존재 앞으로 통하는 통행증이 되었다."], ["So the dream increased in rapture every hour, as Mr Dorrit felt increasingly sensible that this connection had brought him forward indeed.", "그리하여 이 인연이 자기를 정말이지 출세시켜 주었다는 것을 도릿 씨가 점점 더 절감할수록, 그 꿈은 시시각각 더욱 황홀해졌다."]], [["Only one thing sat otherwise than auriferously, and at the same time lightly, on Mr Dorrit’s mind.", "오직 한 가지만이 도릿 씨의 마음에 황금빛으로도, 동시에 가볍게도 얹히지 않았다."], ["It was the Chief Butler.", "바로 집사장이었다."], ["That stupendous character looked at him, in the course of his official looking at the dinners, in a manner that Mr Dorrit considered questionable.", "그 어마어마한 인물은 만찬을 공식적으로 살피는 과정에서 도릿 씨를 바라보았는데, 그 방식이 도릿 씨가 보기에 미심쩍었다."], ["He looked at him, as he passed through the hall and up the staircase, going to dinner, with a glazed fixedness that Mr Dorrit did not like.", "그는 도릿 씨가 만찬에 가려고 홀을 가로질러 계단을 올라갈 때, 도릿 씨가 영 마뜩잖게 여기는 그 유리알처럼 굳은 응시로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Seated at table in the act of drinking, Mr Dorrit still saw him through his wine-glass, regarding him with a cold and ghostly eye.", "식탁에 앉아 술을 마시는 와중에도, 도릿 씨는 자기 포도주 잔 너머로 여전히 그를 보았으니, 그는 차갑고 유령 같은 눈으로 그를 살피고 있었다."], ["It misgave him that the Chief Butler must have known a Collegian, and must have seen him in the College--perhaps had been presented to him.", "도릿 씨는 집사장이 어느 수감자를 알고 있었던 게 아닐까, 그를 그 감옥에서 본 적이 있는 게 아닐까—어쩌면 그에게 소개받은 적이 있는 게 아닐까 하는 불안에 사로잡혔다."], ["He looked as closely at the Chief Butler as such a man could be looked at, and yet he did not recall that he had ever seen him elsewhere.", "그는 그런 인물을 살필 수 있는 만큼 바짝 집사장을 살폈지만, 그를 다른 어디선가 본 적이 있다는 기억은 떠오르지 않았다."], ["Ultimately he was inclined to think that there was no reverence in the man, no sentiment in the great creature.", "끝내 그는, 그 사내에게는 경외심이라곤 없으며, 그 위대한 피조물에게는 어떤 감정도 없다고 여기는 쪽으로 기울었다."], ["But he was not relieved by that; for, let him think what he would, the Chief Butler had him in his supercilious eye, even when that eye was on the plate and other table-garniture; and he never let him out of it.", "그러나 그렇다고 마음이 놓이지는 않았으니, 그가 무슨 생각을 하든 집사장은 거만한 눈으로 그를 붙들어 두고 있었고, 심지어 그 눈이 접시며 다른 식탁 장식물에 가 있을 때조차도 결코 그를 그 눈에서 놓아주지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["To hint to him that this confinement in his eye was disagreeable, or to ask him what he meant, was an act too daring to venture upon; his severity with his employers and their visitors being terrific, and he never permitting himself to be approached with the slightest liberty.", "그 사내에게 자기를 이렇게 눈 속에 가두는 것이 불쾌하다고 넌지시 이르거나, 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻는 일은 감히 무릅쓰기에 너무 대담한 짓이었으니, 그가 고용주들과 그 손님들을 대하는 위엄이 가공할 만했고, 자기에게 조금이라도 허물없이 다가오는 것을 결코 용납하지 않았던 까닭이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 17. Missing", "# CHAPTER 17. Missing"]], [["The term of Mr Dorrit’s visit was within two days of being out, and he was about to dress for another inspection by the Chief Butler (whose victims were always dressed expressly for him), when one of the servants of the hotel presented himself bearing a card.", "도릿 씨의 방문 기한은 이틀밖에 남지 않은 시점이었고, 그는 다시 한번 집사장의 검열을 받기 위해 — 그 희생자들은 언제나 집사장만을 위하여 차려입었다 — 옷을 갈아입으려던 참이었다. 그때 호텔 하인 하나가 명함 한 장을 들고 나타났다."], ["Mr Dorrit, taking it, read:", "도릿 씨는 그것을 받아 들고 읽었다."]], [["‘Mrs Finching.’", "'핀칭 부인.'"]], [["The servant waited in speechless deference.", "하인은 말없이 공손히 기다렸다."]], [["‘Man, man,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning upon him with grievous indignation, ‘explain your motive in bringing me this ridiculous name.", "\"이보게, 자네,\" 도릿 씨가 비통한 분노에 차서 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["I am wholly unacquainted with it.", "\"이런 우스꽝스러운 이름을 내게 가져온 이유를 설명해 보게. 나는 이 이름을 전혀 모르네."], ["Finching, sir?’ said Mr Dorrit, perhaps avenging himself on the Chief Butler by Substitute.", "핀칭이라니, 자네?\" 도릿 씨는 어쩌면 집사장에게 받은 분풀이를 대리로 갚고 있는지도 몰랐다."], ["‘Ha!", "\"허!"], ["What do you mean by Finching?’", "핀칭이라니, 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["The man, man, seemed to mean Flinching as much as anything else, for he backed away from Mr Dorrit’s severe regard, as he replied, ‘A lady, sir.’", "이보게, 그 자네는 어쩌면 무엇보다도 움찔(플린칭)한다는 뜻인 듯했다. 도릿 씨의 준엄한 눈초리에서 슬금슬금 물러서며 대답했으니 말이다. \"어느 부인입니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘I know no such lady, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"나는 그런 부인을 모르네, 자네,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Take this card away.", "\"이 명함을 치우게."], ["I know no Finching of either sex.’", "나는 남자든 여자든 핀칭이란 사람을 모르네.\""]], [["‘Ask your pardon, sir.", "\"죄송합니다, 나리."], ["The lady said she was aware she might be unknown by name.", "그 부인께서 이름으로는 모르실 줄 안다고 하셨습니다."], ["But she begged me to say, sir, that she had formerly the honour of being acquainted with Miss Dorrit.", "다만, 나리, 예전에 도릿 양과 알고 지내는 영광을 누렸다고 전해 달라 부탁하셨습니다."], ["The lady said, sir, the youngest Miss Dorrit.’", "나리, 막내 도릿 양이라 하셨습니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit knitted his brows and rejoined, after a moment or two, ‘Inform Mrs Finching, sir,’ emphasising the name as if the innocent man were solely responsible for it, ‘that she can come up.’", "도릿 씨는 이맛살을 찌푸리고는 한두 순간 뒤에 대꾸했다. \"핀칭 부인께 전하게, 자네,\" 마치 그 무고한 사내가 그 이름에 전적으로 책임이 있다는 듯 이름을 힘주어 말하며, \"올라오셔도 좋다고.\""]], [["He had reflected, in his momentary pause, that unless she were admitted she might leave some message, or might say something below, having a disgraceful reference to that former state of existence.", "그는 그 잠깐의 멈춤 동안, 만일 들이지 않으면 그녀가 무슨 전언을 남기거나, 아래층에서 그 옛 시절의 처지에 관한 수치스러운 무언가를 말할지도 모른다고 생각했던 것이다."], ["Hence the concession, and hence the appearance of Flora, piloted in by the man, man.", "그래서 양보가 나왔고, 그래서 플로라가 그 이보게 자네의 안내를 받으며 모습을 드러내게 되었다."]], [["‘I have not the pleasure,’ said Mr Dorrit, standing with the card in his hand, and with an air which imported that it would scarcely have been a first-class pleasure if he had had it, ‘of knowing either this name, or yourself, madam.", "\"이 이름도, 부인 자신도 알게 되는,\" 도릿 씨가 명함을 손에 들고 서서, 설령 그런 영광을 누린다 한들 결코 일류의 영광은 못 되리라는 기색을 풍기며 말했다."], ["Place a chair, sir.’", "\"그 기쁨을 저는 누리지 못하고 있습니다, 부인. 의자를 놓게, 자네.\""]], [["The responsible man, with a start, obeyed, and went out on tiptoe.", "책임을 진 그 사내가 흠칫하더니 시키는 대로 하고는 발끝걸음으로 나갔다."], ["Flora, putting aside her veil with a bashful tremor upon her, proceeded to introduce herself.", "플로라는 수줍은 떨림에 휩싸인 채 베일을 걷어 올리고 자기소개를 시작했다."], ["At the same time a singular combination of perfumes was diffused through the room, as if some brandy had been put by mistake in a lavender-water bottle, or as if some lavender-water had been put by mistake in a brandy-bottle.", "그와 동시에 묘한 향기의 조합이 방 안에 퍼졌다. 마치 라벤더 물병에 실수로 브랜디를 넣었거나, 혹은 브랜디 병에 실수로 라벤더 물을 넣은 것 같았다."]], [["‘I beg Mr Dorrit to offer a thousand apologies and indeed they would be far too few for such an intrusion which I know must appear extremely bold in a lady and alone too, but I thought it best upon the whole however difficult and even apparently improper though Mr F.’s Aunt would have willingly accompanied me and as a character of great force and spirit would probably have struck one possessed of such a knowledge of life as no doubt with so many changes must have been acquired, for Mr F. himself said frequently that although well educated in the neighbourhood of Blackheath at as high as eighty guineas which is a good deal for parents and the plate kept back too on going away but that is more a meanness than its value that he had learnt more in his first years as a commercial traveller with a large commission on the sale of an article that nobody would hear of much less buy which preceded the wine trade a long time than in the whole six years in that academy conducted by a college Bachelor, though why a Bachelor more clever than a married man I do not see and never did but pray excuse me that is not the point.’", "\"도릿 씨께 천 번이나 사과를 드리고 싶고 사실 이런 무례에는 그것도 턱없이 부족하겠지만 숙녀가 그것도 혼자서 이러는 것이 몹시 대담해 보일 줄은 저도 잘 알지만 그래도 전체적으로 이것이 최선이라 여겼는데 아무리 어렵고 게다가 부적절해 보일지라도 F 씨의 고모님이 기꺼이 동행해 주셨을 테고 또 대단히 강하고 기개 있는 분이라 그토록 숱한 변화를 겪으며 틀림없이 익혔을 그런 세상 물정에 밝은 분께는 깊은 인상을 주셨을 테지만 F 씨 본인이 자주 말씀하시기를 비록 블랙히스 인근에서 팔십 기니나 들여 잘 교육받았는데 이는 부모로서 큰돈이고 게다가 떠날 때 식기까지 돌려주지 않았으니 그건 값어치라기보다 인색함이지만 그래도 와인 사업에 한참 앞서 아무도 들어 주지 않을뿐더러 사기는커녕 거들떠도 안 보는 물건을 큰 수수료를 받고 파는 외판원으로 보낸 첫 몇 해에 그 학사 출신이 운영하던 학원에서 보낸 육 년 내내보다 더 많이 배웠다고 하셨는데 다만 어째서 학사가 기혼남보다 더 영리한지는 저는 모르겠고 알았던 적도 없지만 부디 용서하세요 그건 요점이 아니에요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit stood rooted to the carpet, a statue of mystification.", "도릿 씨는 어리둥절함의 석상처럼 양탄자에 못 박힌 채 서 있었다."]], [["‘I must openly admit that I have no pretensions,’ said Flora, ‘but having known the dear little thing which under altered circumstances appears a liberty but is not so intended and Goodness knows there was no favour in half-a-crown a-day to such a needle as herself but quite the other way and as to anything lowering in it far from it the labourer is worthy of his hire and I am sure I only wish he got it oftener and more animal food and less rheumatism in the back and legs poor soul.’", "\"제가 무슨 자격이 있다고 내세울 수 없음은 솔직히 인정하지만,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨를 알고 지냈던 터라 처지가 달라진 지금 이러는 게 주제넘어 보이겠지만 그런 뜻은 아니고 하느님께서 아시듯 그런 바느질 솜씨를 가진 분에게 하루 반 크라운을 준 건 결코 은혜가 아니라 오히려 그 반대였고 거기에 무슨 격을 떨어뜨리는 것이 있다면 천만의 말씀 일꾼이 제 삯을 받는 것은 마땅하고 정말이지 저는 그분이 그걸 더 자주 받고 동물성 음식을 더 많이 먹고 등과 다리에 류머티즘을 덜 앓았으면 했어요 가엾은 분.\""]], [["‘Madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, recovering his breath by a great effort, as the relict of the late Mr Finching stopped to take hers; ‘madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, very red in the face, ‘if I understand you to refer to--ha--to anything in the antecedents of--hum--a daughter of mine, involving--ha hum--daily compensation, madam, I beg to observe that the--ha--fact, assuming it--ha--to be fact, never was within my knowledge.", "\"부인,\" 고인이 된 핀칭 씨의 미망인이 숨을 돌리려 잠시 멈추자 도릿 씨가 큰 노력 끝에 숨을 되찾으며 말했다."], ["Hum.", "\"부인,\""], ["I should not have permitted it.", "얼굴이 새빨개진 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"제가 부인의 말씀을 — 허 — 제 딸의 — 음 — 지난 일 가운데 — 허 음 — 날삯을 받는 일과 관련된 무언가를 가리키시는 것으로 이해한다면, 부인, 저는 그 — 허 — 사실이, 설령 그것이 — 허 — 사실이라 한들, 결코 제가 아는 바가 아니었음을 분명히 말씀드리고 싶군요."], ["Ha.", "음."], ["Never!", "저라면 그것을 허락하지 않았을 겁니다. 허."], ["Never!’", "결코! 결코!\""]], [["‘Unnecessary to pursue the subject,’ returned Flora, ‘and would not have mentioned it on any account except as supposing it a favourable and only letter of introduction but as to being fact no doubt whatever and you may set your mind at rest for the very dress I have on now can prove it and sweetly made though there is no denying that it would tell better on a better figure for my own is much too fat though how to bring it down I know not, pray excuse me I am roving off again.’", "\"그 이야기를 더 끌 필요는 없고요,\" 플로라가 대꾸했다. \"어떤 경우든 그저 호의적이고 유일한 소개의 구실로 여겨 꺼냈을 뿐 결코 다른 의도는 없었지만 그것이 사실인 점에는 추호의 의심도 없고 마음 푹 놓으셔도 좋아요 지금 제가 입고 있는 바로 이 옷이 그걸 증명할 수 있으니까요 곱게도 지었지만 더 나은 몸매에 더 잘 어울릴 거라는 건 부인할 수 없지만 제 몸은 너무 살이 쪄서 어떻게 줄여야 할지 모르겠어요 부디 용서하세요 또 옆길로 새고 있네요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit backed to his chair in a stony way, and seated himself, as Flora gave him a softening look and played with her parasol.", "도릿 씨는 돌처럼 굳은 채 자기 의자로 물러나 앉았고, 플로라는 그에게 누그러뜨리는 듯한 눈길을 던지며 양산을 만지작거렸다."]], [["‘The dear little thing,’ said Flora, ‘having gone off perfectly limp and white and cold in my own house or at least papa’s for though not a freehold still a long lease at a peppercorn on the morning when Arthur--foolish habit of our youthful days and Mr Clennam far more adapted to existing circumstances particularly addressing a stranger and that stranger a gentleman in an elevated station--communicated the glad tidings imparted by a person of name of Pancks emboldens me.’", "\"그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨가,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"아서가 — 우리 어린 시절의 어리석은 버릇이고 클레넘 씨라 하는 편이 지금 형편에 훨씬 어울리는데 특히 낯선 사람을 그것도 높은 지위의 신사를 부를 때는 더 그렇지요 — 팽크스라는 사람이 전해 준 기쁜 소식을 알려 준 그날 아침 제 집에서, 아니 적어도 아빠 집에서, 자유보유권은 아니지만 그래도 명목상의 임대료로 빌린 긴 임대인데, 완전히 축 늘어져 하얗고 차갑게 졸도해 버렸던 일이 제게 용기를 줍니다.\""]], [["At the mention of these two names, Mr Dorrit frowned, stared, frowned again, hesitated with his fingers at his lips, as he had hesitated long ago, and said, ‘Do me the favour to--ha--state your pleasure, madam.’", "이 두 이름이 언급되자 도릿 씨는 이맛살을 찌푸리고, 빤히 쳐다보고, 다시 찌푸리더니, 오래전에 그러했듯 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 망설이다가 말했다. \"부디 — 허 — 용건을 말씀해 주시는 친절을 베풀어 주십시오, 부인.\""]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ said Flora, ‘you are very kind in giving me permission and highly natural it seems to me that you should be kind for though more stately I perceive a likeness filled out of course but a likeness still, the object of my intruding is my own without the slightest consultation with any human being and most decidedly not with Arthur--pray excuse me Doyce and Clennam I don’t know what I am saying Mr Clennam solus--for to put that individual linked by a golden chain to a purple time when all was ethereal out of any anxiety would be worth to me the ransom of a monarch not that I have the least idea how much that would come to but using it as the total of all I have in the world and more.’", "\"도릿 씨,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"허락해 주시니 참 친절하시고 친절하신 게 제겐 지극히 자연스러워 보이는 게 더 위엄 있어지셨지만 닮은 점이 보여요 물론 살은 좀 붙으셨지만 그래도 닮은 점이요, 제가 이렇게 끼어드는 목적은 어떤 사람과도 조금도 의논하지 않은 순전히 제 뜻이고 더더구나 아서와는 결코 의논하지 않았는데 — 부디 용서하세요 도이스 앤드 클레넘 제가 무슨 말을 하는지 모르겠네요 클레넘 씨 혼자서요 — 모든 것이 천상의 빛으로 가득하던 그 보랏빛 시절에 황금 사슬로 매여 있던 그분을 어떤 근심에서 벗어나게 해 드린다면 제겐 한 군주의 몸값만큼이나 값진 일이지만 그게 얼마나 될지는 도무지 짐작도 못 하면서 다만 제가 이 세상에 가진 전부, 아니 그 이상의 총합으로 그것을 쓰는 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit, without greatly regarding the earnestness of these latter words, repeated, ‘State your pleasure, madam.’", "도릿 씨는 이 뒷말의 진심에는 크게 마음 쓰지 않고 되풀이했다. \"용건을 말씀해 주십시오, 부인.\""]], [["‘It’s not likely I well know,’ said Flora, ‘but it’s possible and being possible when I had the gratification of reading in the papers that you had arrived from Italy and were going back I made up my mind to try it for you might come across him or hear something of him and if so what a blessing and relief to all!’", "\"가망이 없는 줄은 저도 잘 알지만,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"그래도 가능은 하고 가능한 일이니 신문에서 당신이 이탈리아에서 오셨다가 돌아가신다는 글을 읽고 흐뭇해하던 차에 시도해 보기로 마음먹었어요 그분과 마주치시거나 그분 소식을 들으실지도 모르고 만약 그러면 모두에게 얼마나 큰 축복이자 안도가 되겠어요!\""]], [["‘Allow me to ask, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, with his ideas in wild confusion, ‘to whom--ha--TO WHOM,’ he repeated it with a raised voice in mere desperation, ‘you at present allude?’", "\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 머릿속이 마구 뒤엉킨 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"누구를 — 허 — 누구를,\" 그는 순전한 절망감에 목소리를 높여 그것을 되풀이했다. \"지금 가리키시는 겁니까?\""]], [["‘To the foreigner from Italy who disappeared in the City as no doubt you have read in the papers equally with myself,’ said Flora, ‘not referring to private sources by the name of Pancks from which one gathers what dreadfully ill-natured things some people are wicked enough to whisper most likely judging others by themselves and what the uneasiness and indignation of Arthur--quite unable to overcome it Doyce and Clennam--cannot fail to be.’", "\"이탈리아에서 온 그 외국인이요 저와 마찬가지로 당신도 틀림없이 신문에서 읽으셨을 텐데 그가 시티에서 사라진 일 말이에요,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"팽크스라는 이름의 사사로운 출처를 두고 하는 말은 아니지만 거기서 어떤 사람들이 사악하게도 얼마나 끔찍하게 심술궂은 말을 수군거리는지 짐작할 수 있고 아마 남을 제 잣대로 재는 거겠지요 그리고 아서의 — 도이스 앤드 클레넘 도무지 그 버릇을 못 고치겠네요 — 불안과 분노가 어떠할지는 뻔하잖아요.\""]], [["It happened, fortunately for the elucidation of any intelligible result, that Mr Dorrit had heard or read nothing about the matter.", "알아들을 만한 결론을 끌어내는 데에는 다행스럽게도, 도릿 씨가 그 일에 관해 들은 것도 읽은 것도 전혀 없었다."], ["This caused Mrs Finching, with many apologies for being in great practical difficulties as to finding the way to her pocket among the stripes of her dress at length to produce a police handbill, setting forth that a foreign gentleman of the name of Blandois, last from Venice, had unaccountably disappeared on such a night in such a part of the city of London; that he was known to have entered such a house, at such an hour; that he was stated by the inmates of that house to have left it, about so many minutes before midnight; and that he had never been beheld since.", "그 덕분에 핀칭 부인은, 옷의 줄무늬 사이에서 주머니를 찾는 데 실질적으로 큰 어려움을 겪은 데 대해 거듭 사과한 끝에, 마침내 경찰 전단을 한 장 꺼내 들었다. 거기에는 마지막으로 베네치아에서 온 블랑두아라는 이름의 외국 신사가 모월 모일 밤 런던 시의 모처에서 까닭 모르게 사라졌다는 것, 그가 모 시각에 모 집에 들어간 것으로 알려졌다는 것, 그 집 거주자들의 진술에 따르면 자정 몇 분 전쯤 그곳을 떠났다는 것, 그리고 그 후로 다시는 그를 본 사람이 없다는 것이 적혀 있었다."], ["This, with exact particulars of time and locality, and with a good detailed description of the foreign gentleman who had so mysteriously vanished, Mr Dorrit read at large.", "시각과 장소의 정확한 세부 사항과 함께, 그토록 불가사의하게 사라진 그 외국 신사에 대한 상세한 묘사가 잘 곁들여진 이 전단을, 도릿 씨는 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다."]], [["‘Blandois!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"블랑두아!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Venice!", "\"베네치아라!"], ["And this description!", "그리고 이 인상착의!"], ["I know this gentleman.", "나는 이 신사를 압니다."], ["He has been in my house.", "그가 우리 집에 온 적이 있어요."], ["He is intimately acquainted with a gentleman of good family (but in indifferent circumstances), of whom I am a--hum--patron.’", "그는 좋은 가문 출신이지만 형편은 변변치 못한 어느 신사와 절친한 사이인데, 내가 그 신사의 — 음 — 후원자입니다.\""]], [["‘Then my humble and pressing entreaty is the more,’ said Flora, ‘that in travelling back you will have the kindness to look for this foreign gentleman along all the roads and up and down all the turnings and to make inquiries for him at all the hotels and orange-trees and vineyards and volcanoes and places for he must be somewhere and why doesn’t he come forward and say he’s there and clear all parties up?’", "\"그렇다면 제 변변찮으면서도 간절한 부탁은 더욱 절실해지는데요,\" 플로라가 말했다. \"돌아가시는 길에 부디 모든 길을 따라가며 모든 모퉁이를 오르내리며 이 외국 신사를 찾아봐 주시고 모든 호텔이며 오렌지 나무며 포도밭이며 화산이며 갖은 곳에서 그분을 수소문해 주세요 어딘가에는 분명 있을 텐데 어째서 나서서 자기가 여기 있다고 말하고 모두의 의혹을 풀어 주지 않는 걸까요?\""]], [["‘Pray, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, referring to the handbill again, ‘who is Clennam and Co.?", "\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 도릿 씨가 다시 전단을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["Ha. I see the name mentioned here, in connection with the occupation of the house which Monsieur Blandois was seen to enter: who is Clennam and Co.?", "\"클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니란 누구입니까?"], ["Is it the individual of whom I had formerly--hum--some--ha--slight transitory knowledge, and to whom I believe you have referred?", "허. 여기 블랑두아 씨가 들어가는 것이 목격된 그 집의 사용과 관련해 이 이름이 적혀 있는데, 클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니란 누구입니까?"], ["Is it--ha--that person?’", "예전에 제가 — 음 — 어떤 — 허 — 잠깐 스치듯 알았던, 그리고 부인이 가리키신 것으로 짐작되는 그 사람입니까? 그 — 허 — 그 사람입니까?\""]], [["‘It’s a very different person indeed,’ replied Flora, ‘with no limbs and wheels instead and the grimmest of women though his mother.’", "\"정말이지 아주 다른 사람이에요,\" 플로라가 대답했다. \"팔다리가 없고 대신 바퀴가 달렸고 어머니이긴 해도 그 어머니가 세상에서 가장 무서운 여자거든요.\""]], [["‘Clennam and Co.", "\"클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니가 — 음 — 어머니라고요!\""], ["a--hum--a mother!’ exclaimed Mr Dorrit.", "도릿 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘And an old man besides,’ said Flora.", "\"게다가 노인도 한 분 있고요,\" 플로라가 말했다."]], [["Mr Dorrit looked as if he must immediately be driven out of his mind by this account.", "도릿 씨는 이 설명에 당장 정신이 나가 버릴 것 같은 표정이었다."], ["Neither was it rendered more favourable to sanity by Flora’s dashing into a rapid analysis of Mr Flintwinch’s cravat, and describing him, without the lightest boundary line of separation between his identity and Mrs Clennam’s, as a rusty screw in gaiters.", "게다가 플로라가 느닷없이 플린트윈치 씨의 목도리를 재빠르게 분석하며, 그의 정체와 클레넘 부인의 정체 사이에 일말의 경계선도 긋지 않은 채 그를 각반 찬 녹슨 나사라고 묘사하는 바람에, 제정신을 유지하기에는 더더욱 불리해졌다."], ["Which compound of man and woman, no limbs, wheels, rusty screw, grimness, and gaiters, so completely stupefied Mr Dorrit, that he was a spectacle to be pitied.", "남자와 여자, 팔다리 없음, 바퀴, 녹슨 나사, 무서움, 그리고 각반이라는 이 혼합물이 도릿 씨를 어찌나 완전히 멍하게 만들었던지, 그는 측은히 여길 만한 구경거리가 되어 있었다."]], [["‘But I would not detain you one moment longer,’ said Flora, upon whom his condition wrought its effect, though she was quite unconscious of having produced it, ‘if you would have the goodness to give your promise as a gentleman that both in going back to Italy and in Italy too you would look for this Mr Blandois high and low and if you found or heard of him make him come forward for the clearing of all parties.’", "\"하지만 더는 한순간도 붙들지 않겠어요,\" 그의 그런 상태에 영향을 받았으면서도 자기가 그 상태를 만들었다는 것은 전혀 의식하지 못한 플로라가 말했다. \"다만 신사로서 약속해 주시는 친절을 베푸시어 이탈리아로 돌아가시는 길에서나 이탈리아에서나 이 블랑두아 씨를 샅샅이 찾아봐 주시고 만약 찾거나 소식을 들으시면 모두의 의혹을 풀도록 그분을 나서게 해 주신다면요.\""]], [["By that time Mr Dorrit had so far recovered from his bewilderment, as to be able to say, in a tolerably connected manner, that he should consider that his duty.", "그 무렵 도릿 씨는 당혹감에서 어지간히 회복되어, 그것을 자신의 의무로 여기겠노라고 제법 조리 있게 말할 수 있게 되었다."], ["Flora was delighted with her success, and rose to take her leave.", "플로라는 자신의 성공에 기뻐하며 작별을 고하려 일어섰다."]], [["‘With a million thanks,’ said she, ‘and my address upon my card in case of anything to be communicated personally, I will not send my love to the dear little thing for it might not be acceptable, and indeed there is no dear little thing left in the transformation so why do it but both myself and Mr F.’s Aunt ever wish her well and lay no claim to any favour on our side you may be sure of that but quite the other way for what she undertook to do she did and that is more than a great many of us do, not to say anything of her doing it as well as it could be done and I myself am one of them for I have said ever since I began to recover the blow of Mr F’s death that I would learn the Organ of which I am extremely fond but of which I am ashamed to say I do not yet know a note, good evening!’", "\"백만 번 감사드리고요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"직접 전할 일이 생길 경우를 위해 제 명함에 주소를 적어 두었어요 그 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨에게는 안부를 전하지 않겠어요 반갑지 않을지도 모르니까요 게다가 그렇게 변신한 마당에 사랑스러운 작은 아가씨는 이제 남아 있지도 않으니 왜 그러겠어요 하지만 저도 F 씨의 고모님도 늘 그분이 잘되기를 바라고 우리 쪽에서 무슨 은혜를 베풀었다는 주장은 추호도 하지 않으니 그건 안심하셔도 좋고 오히려 그 반대예요 그분은 하기로 한 일을 해냈고 그건 우리 중 숱한 사람이 하지 못하는 일이며 그것도 더할 나위 없이 잘 해냈으니 말할 것도 없고 저 자신도 그 못 하는 사람 중 하나인 게 F 씨의 죽음이라는 충격에서 벗어나기 시작한 이래로 줄곧 제가 무척 좋아하는 오르간을 배우겠다고 말해 왔으면서도 부끄럽게도 아직 음표 하나 모르거든요, 안녕히 계세요!\""]], [["When Mr Dorrit, who attended her to the room-door, had had a little time to collect his senses, he found that the interview had summoned back discarded reminiscences which jarred with the Merdle dinner-table.", "방문까지 그녀를 배웅한 도릿 씨가 잠깐 정신을 추스를 시간을 갖고 보니, 그 면담이 머들 만찬 식탁과는 어울리지 않는, 내버려 두었던 옛 기억들을 불러내 놓았음을 알게 되었다. 그는 그날은 사양한다는 짤막한 편지를 써서 보내고, 곧바로 호텔의 자기 방에서 저녁 식사를 준비하게 했다."], ["He wrote and sent off a brief note excusing himself for that day, and ordered dinner presently in his own rooms at the hotel.", "여기에는 또 다른 이유가 있었다. 런던에서의 그의 시간은 거의 다 끝나 가고 있었고 약속들이 그 시간을 앞당겨 잡아먹고 있었다."], ["He had another reason for this.", "돌아갈 계획은 이미 짜여 있었다."], ["His time in London was very nearly out, and was anticipated by engagements; his plans were made for returning; and he thought it behoved his importance to pursue some direct inquiry into the Blandois disappearance, and be in a condition to carry back to Mr Henry Gowan the result of his own personal investigation.", "그리고 그는 자신의 중요한 위치를 보아서라도 블랑두아 실종 사건을 직접 조사하여, 헨리 가우언 씨에게 자신의 친히 한 조사 결과를 가지고 돌아갈 수 있어야 마땅하다고 여겼다."], ["He therefore resolved that he would take advantage of that evening’s freedom to go down to Clennam and Co.’s, easily to be found by the direction set forth in the handbill; and see the place, and ask a question or two there himself.", "그래서 그는 그날 저녁의 한가함을 이용해, 전단에 적힌 안내대로라면 쉽게 찾을 수 있을 클레넘 앤드 컴퍼니로 내려가서 그 장소를 보고 거기서 직접 한두 가지를 물어보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Having dined as plainly as the establishment and the Courier would let him, and having taken a short sleep by the fire for his better recovery from Mrs Finching, he set out in a hackney-cabriolet alone.", "호텔과 수행원이 허락하는 한 더없이 검소하게 저녁을 먹고, 핀칭 부인의 후유증에서 좀 더 잘 회복하려 난롯가에서 짧게 한숨 자고 난 그는, 삯마차를 잡아타고 혼자 길을 나섰다."], ["The deep bell of St Paul’s was striking nine as he passed under the shadow of Temple Bar, headless and forlorn in these degenerate days.", "그가 이 타락한 시절에 머리도 없이 쓸쓸히 서 있는 템플 바의 그림자 밑을 지날 때 세인트폴 대성당의 묵직한 종이 아홉 시를 치고 있었다."]], [["As he approached his destination through the by-streets and water-side ways, that part of London seemed to him an uglier spot at such an hour than he had ever supposed it to be.", "뒷골목과 강가 길을 지나 목적지에 다가갈수록, 런던의 그 일대는 이런 시각에 그가 여태껏 짐작했던 것보다 더 흉한 곳으로 보였다."], ["Many long years had passed since he had seen it; he had never known much of it; and it wore a mysterious and dismal aspect in his eyes.", "그가 그곳을 본 지 여러 해가 흘렀고, 본디 그곳을 잘 알지도 못했으니, 그의 눈에는 그곳이 음산하고 으스스한 모습을 띠고 있었다."], ["So powerfully was his imagination impressed by it, that when his driver stopped, after having asked the way more than once, and said to the best of his belief this was the gateway they wanted, Mr Dorrit stood hesitating, with the coach-door in his hand, half afraid of the dark look of the place.", "그것이 그의 상상력에 어찌나 강하게 인상을 남겼던지, 마부가 길을 한 번 이상 물어본 끝에 멈춰 서서 자기가 믿기로는 이곳이 찾던 그 문간이라고 말했을 때, 도릿 씨는 마차 문을 손에 쥔 채, 그 장소의 어두운 모습이 반쯤 두려워 머뭇거리며 서 있었다."]], [["Truly, it looked as gloomy that night as even it had ever looked.", "과연 그곳은 그날 밤, 일찍이 보였던 그 어느 때 못지않게 음울해 보였다."], ["Two of the handbills were posted on the entrance wall, one on either side, and as the lamp flickered in the night air, shadows passed over them, not unlike the shadows of fingers following the lines.", "전단 두 장이 입구 벽에 한쪽씩 나뉘어 붙어 있었고, 등불이 밤공기에 흔들릴 때마다 그 위로 그림자가 스쳐 갔으니, 마치 글줄을 따라가는 손가락의 그림자 같았다."], ["A watch was evidently kept upon the place.", "분명 그곳에 감시가 붙어 있었다."], ["As Mr Dorrit paused, a man passed in from over the way, and another man passed out from some dark corner within; and both looked at him in passing, and both remained standing about.", "도릿 씨가 멈춰 서 있는 동안 한 사내가 길 건너편에서 안으로 들어오고, 또 한 사내가 안쪽의 어느 어두운 구석에서 밖으로 나왔다. 둘 다 지나가며 그를 쳐다보았고, 둘 다 그 근처에 우두커니 서 있었다."]], [["As there was only one house in the enclosure, there was no room for uncertainty, so he went up the steps of that house and knocked.", "그 울안에는 집이 하나뿐이었으므로 헷갈릴 여지가 없었다. 그래서 그는 그 집의 계단을 올라가 문을 두드렸다."], ["There was a dim light in two windows on the first-floor.", "이층의 창문 둘에 흐릿한 불빛이 있었다."], ["The door gave back a dreary, vacant sound, as though the house were empty; but it was not, for a light was visible, and a step was audible, almost directly.", "문은 마치 집이 빈 듯 황량하고 텅 빈 소리를 되울렸다. 그러나 빈 것은 아니었으니, 거의 곧바로 불빛이 보이고 발소리가 들렸기 때문이다."], ["They both came to the door, and a chain grated, and a woman with her apron thrown over her face and head stood in the aperture.", "그 둘이 함께 문으로 다가왔고, 사슬이 삐걱거리더니, 앞치마를 얼굴과 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 여자가 그 틈에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Who is it?’ said the woman.", "\"누구세요?\" 여자가 말했다."]], [["Mr Dorrit, much amazed by this appearance, replied that he was from Italy, and that he wished to ask a question relative to the missing person, whom he knew.", "이 모습에 몹시 놀란 도릿 씨는, 자기가 이탈리아에서 왔고 자기가 아는 그 실종된 사람에 관해 한 가지 물어보고 싶다고 대답했다."]], [["‘Hi!’ cried the woman, raising a cracked voice.", "\"여보!\" 여자가 갈라진 목소리를 높여 외쳤다."], ["‘Jeremiah!’", "\"제러마이아!\""]], [["Upon this, a dry old man appeared, whom Mr Dorrit thought he identified by his gaiters, as the rusty screw.", "이에 마른 노인 하나가 나타났는데, 도릿 씨는 그의 각반을 보고 그가 바로 그 녹슨 나사라고 알아본 듯했다."], ["The woman was under apprehensions of the dry old man, for she whisked her apron away as he approached, and disclosed a pale affrighted face.", "여자는 그 마른 노인을 두려워하고 있었으니, 그가 다가오자 앞치마를 휙 걷어 내려 핼쑥하니 겁먹은 얼굴을 드러냈기 때문이다. \"문을 열어, 이 멍청아,\""], ["‘Open the door, you fool,’ said the old man; ‘and let the gentleman in.’", "노인이 말했다. \"신사분을 들이라고.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit, not without a glance over his shoulder towards his driver and the cabriolet, walked into the dim hall. ‘Now, sir,’ said Mr Flintwinch, ‘you can ask anything here you think proper; there are no secrets here, sir.’", "도릿 씨는 어깨 너머로 자기 마부와 삯마차 쪽을 한 번 흘끗 보고 나서, 흐릿한 현관으로 걸어 들어갔다. \"자, 나리,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다. \"여기서 적당하다 여기시는 건 무엇이든 물으셔도 됩니다. 여기엔 비밀이 없습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Before a reply could be made, a strong stern voice, though a woman’s, called from above, ‘Who is it?’", "대답이 나오기도 전에, 여자의 목소리이긴 하나 강하고 준엄한 목소리가 위에서 불렀다. \"누구냐?\""]], [["‘Who is it?’ returned Jeremiah.", "\"누구냐고요?\" 제러마이아가 되받았다."], ["‘More inquiries.", "\"또 무슨 조사랍니다."], ["A gentleman from Italy.’", "이탈리아에서 온 신사랍니다.\""]], [["‘Bring him up here!’", "\"이리로 모셔 올려라!\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch muttered, as if he deemed that unnecessary; but, turning to Mr Dorrit, said, ‘Mrs Clennam.", "플린트윈치 씨는 그럴 필요 없다고 여기는 듯 투덜거렸지만, 도릿 씨에게 돌아서서 말했다. \"클레넘 부인입니다."], ["She _will_ do as she likes.", "저분은 하고 싶은 대로 하시지요. 제가 길을 안내하겠습니다.\""], ["I’ll show you the way.’ He then preceded Mr Dorrit up the blackened staircase; that gentleman, not unnaturally looking behind him on the road, saw the woman following, with her apron thrown over her head again in her former ghastly manner.", "그러고는 도릿 씨를 앞장세워 시커멓게 그을린 계단을 올라갔다. 도릿 씨가, 그도 그럴 것이, 가는 길에 뒤를 돌아보니, 아까처럼 그 섬뜩한 모양으로 다시 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어쓴 그 여자가 따라오고 있었다."]], [["Mrs Clennam had her books open on her little table.", "클레넘 부인은 작은 탁자 위에 장부를 펼쳐 놓고 있었다."], ["‘Oh!’ said she abruptly, as she eyed her visitor with a steady look.", "\"오!\" 그녀가 흔들림 없는 눈길로 손님을 바라보며 불쑥 말했다."], ["‘You are from Italy, sir, are you.", "\"이탈리아에서 오셨다고요."], ["Well?’", "그래서요?\""]], [["Mr Dorrit was at a loss for any more distinct rejoinder at the moment than ‘Ha--well?’", "도릿 씨는 그 순간 \"허 — 그래서요?\"보다 더 또렷한 대꾸를 찾지 못해 쩔쩔맸다."]], [["‘Where is this missing man?", "\"이 실종된 사람이 어디 있나요?"], ["Have you come to give us information where he is?", "그가 어디 있는지 알려 주러 오셨나요?"], ["I hope you have?’", "그러셨으면 좋겠는데요?\""]], [["‘So far from it, I--hum--have come to seek information.’", "\"그러기는커녕, 저는 — 음 — 정보를 구하러 왔습니다.\""]], [["‘Unfortunately for us, there is none to be got here.", "\"우리로서는 안된 일이지만, 여기서 얻을 정보는 없어요."], ["Flintwinch, show the gentleman the handbill.", "플린트윈치, 신사분께 전단을 보여 드려요."], ["Give him several to take away.", "가져가시게 몇 장 드리고요."], ["Hold the light for him to read it.’", "읽으시게 등불을 비춰 드려요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch did as he was directed, and Mr Dorrit read it through, as if he had not previously seen it; glad enough of the opportunity of collecting his presence of mind, which the air of the house and of the people in it had a little disturbed.", "플린트윈치 씨가 시키는 대로 했고, 도릿 씨는 마치 전에 본 적이 없는 듯 그것을 끝까지 읽었다. 이 집과 그 안에 있는 사람들의 분위기에 다소 흐트러진 그의 침착함을 가다듬을 기회가 생겨 충분히 다행스러웠다."], ["While his eyes were on the paper, he felt that the eyes of Mr Flintwinch and of Mrs Clennam were on him.", "종이에 눈을 두고 있는 동안 그는 플린트윈치 씨와 클레넘 부인의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He found, when he looked up, that this sensation was not a fanciful one.", "고개를 들어 보니, 그 느낌이 공연한 것이 아니었음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["‘Now you know as much,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘as we know, sir.", "\"이제 당신도 우리가 아는 만큼은 알게 되셨어요, 나리,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."], ["Is Mr Blandois a friend of yours?’", "\"블랑두아 씨가 당신의 친구인가요?\""]], [["‘No--a--hum--an acquaintance,’ answered Mr Dorrit.", "\"아니요 — 그 — 음 — 아는 사이입니다,\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다."]], [["‘You have no commission from him, perhaps?’", "\"혹시 그에게서 무슨 의뢰를 받은 건 아니고요?\""]], [["‘I? Ha.", "\"제가요?"], ["Certainly not.’", "허. 결단코 아닙니다.\""]], [["The searching look turned gradually to the floor, after taking Mr Flintwinch’s face in its way.", "그 캐묻는 눈길은 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴을 거쳐 가더니 차츰 바닥으로 향했다."], ["Mr Dorrit, discomfited by finding that he was the questioned instead of the questioner, applied himself to the reversal of that unexpected order of things.", "묻는 사람이 아니라 묻는 대상이 되어 있음을 알고 당황한 도릿 씨는, 그 뜻밖에 뒤바뀐 상황을 되돌리는 데 매달렸다."]], [["‘I am--ha--a gentleman of property, at present residing in Italy with my family, my servants, and--hum--my rather large establishment.", "\"저는 — 허 — 재산이 있는 신사로, 지금은 가족과 하인들과 — 음 — 제법 큰 살림을 거느리고 이탈리아에 살고 있습니다."], ["Being in London for a short time on affairs connected with--ha--my estate, and hearing of this strange disappearance, I wished to make myself acquainted with the circumstances at first-hand, because there is--ha hum--an English gentleman in Italy whom I shall no doubt see on my return, who has been in habits of close and daily intimacy with Monsieur Blandois.", "— 허 — 제 영지와 관련된 일로 잠시 런던에 와 있다가 이 기이한 실종 소식을 듣고, 저로서는 직접 그 정황을 알고 싶었습니다. 왜냐하면 — 허 음 — 이탈리아에 한 영국 신사가 있는데, 돌아가면 분명 만날 사람이고, 그가 블랑두아 씨와 가까이 날마다 친밀하게 지내 왔거든요."], ["Mr Henry Gowan.", "헨리 가우언 씨입니다."], ["You may know the name.’", "그 이름을 아실지도 모르겠군요.\""]], [["‘Never heard of it.’", "\"들어 본 적 없어요.\""]], [["Mrs Clennam said it, and Mr Flintwinch echoed it.", "클레넘 부인이 그렇게 말했고, 플린트윈치 씨가 그 말을 따라 했다."]], [["‘Wishing to--ha--make the narrative coherent and consecutive to him,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘may I ask--say, three questions?’", "\"그에게 — 허 — 이야기를 조리 있고 차근차근 들려주고 싶어서요,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"여쭤도 될까요 — 가령, 세 가지를?\""]], [["‘Thirty, if you choose.’", "\"원하신다면 서른 가지라도.\""]], [["‘Have you known Monsieur Blandois long?’", "\"블랑두아 씨를 안 지 오래되셨습니까?\""]], [["‘Not a twelvemonth.", "\"채 일 년도 안 됐어요."], ["Mr Flintwinch here, will refer to the books and tell you when, and by whom at Paris he was introduced to us.", "여기 플린트윈치 씨가 장부를 찾아보고 언제, 누구에 의해 파리에서 그가 우리에게 소개되었는지 알려 드릴 거예요. 만약 그것이,\""], ["If that,’ Mrs Clennam added, ‘should be any satisfaction to you.", "클레넘 부인이 덧붙였다. \"당신께 무슨 만족이 된다면요."], ["It is poor satisfaction to us.’", "우리에겐 변변찮은 만족이지만.\""]], [["‘Have you seen him often?’", "\"그를 자주 보셨습니까?\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아뇨."], ["Twice.", "두 번이요."], ["Once before, and--’", "한 번은 전에, 그리고 —\""]], [["‘That once,’ suggested Mr Flintwinch.", "\"그 한 번 말이지요,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 거들었다."]], [["‘And that once.’", "\"그리고 그 한 번이요.\""]], [["‘Pray, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a growing fancy upon him as he recovered his importance, that he was in some superior way in the Commission of the Peace; ‘pray, madam, may I inquire, for the greater satisfaction of the gentleman whom I have the honour to--ha--retain, or protect or let me say to--hum--know--to know--Was Monsieur Blandois here on business on the night indicated in this present sheet?’", "\"부인, 여쭙겠습니다만,\" 도릿 씨가 자신의 중요한 위치를 되찾으면서 자기가 어떤 상급의 자격으로 치안판사직을 맡고 있는 듯한 망상이 점점 커지는 가운데 말했다. \"부인, 제가 — 허 — 거느리는, 아니 보호하는, 아니 말하자면 — 음 — 아는, 아는 그 신사의 더 큰 만족을 위해 여쭙겠습니다만 — 블랑두아 씨가 이 종이에 적힌 그날 밤 여기에 볼일이 있어 온 것입니까?\""]], [["‘On what he called business,’ returned Mrs Clennam.", "\"본인 말로는 볼일이라더군요,\" 클레넘 부인이 대답했다."]], [["‘Is--ha--excuse me--is its nature to be communicated?’\n‘No.’", "\"그 — 허 — 실례지만 — 그 볼일의 성격을 알려 주실 수 있습니까?\""]], [["It was evidently impracticable to pass the barrier of that reply.", "\"아니요.\" 그 대답의 장벽을 넘기란 분명 불가능했다."]], [["‘The question has been asked before,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘and the answer has been, No.", "\"그 질문은 전에도 받았어요,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."], ["We don’t choose to publish our transactions, however unimportant, to all the town.", "\"그리고 대답은 아니요였지요. 우리는 거래를, 아무리 사소한 것이라도, 온 도시에 떠벌리고 싶지 않아요."], ["We say, No.’", "우리는 아니요라고 말합니다.\""]], [["‘I mean, he took away no money with him, for example,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"제 말은, 가령 그가 돈을 가지고 가지는 않았느냐는 겁니다,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘He took away none of ours, sir, and got none here.’", "\"그는 우리 돈은 한 푼도 가져가지 않았고, 여기서 한 푼도 얻지 못했어요, 나리.\""]], [["‘I suppose,’ observed Mr Dorrit, glancing from Mrs Clennam to Mr Flintwinch, and from Mr Flintwinch to Mrs Clennam, ‘you have no way of accounting to yourself for this mystery?’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 도릿 씨가 클레넘 부인에게서 플린트윈치 씨에게로, 플린트윈치 씨에게서 클레넘 부인에게로 시선을 옮기며 말했다. \"두 분은 이 수수께끼를 스스로 설명할 길이 없으신 것 같은데요?\""]], [["‘Why do you suppose so?’ rejoined Mrs Clennam.", "\"왜 그렇게 생각하세요?\" 클레넘 부인이 되받았다."]], [["Disconcerted by the cold and hard inquiry, Mr Dorrit was unable to assign any reason for his supposing so.", "그 차갑고 매정한 물음에 당황한 도릿 씨는, 자기가 왜 그렇게 생각하는지 아무런 이유도 대지 못했다."]], [["‘I account for it, sir,’ she pursued after an awkward silence on Mr Dorrit’s part, ‘by having no doubt that he is travelling somewhere, or hiding somewhere.’", "\"저는 이렇게 설명해요, 나리,\" 도릿 씨 쪽의 어색한 침묵이 흐른 뒤 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그가 어딘가로 여행을 다니거나 어딘가에 숨어 있다는 데에 의심이 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘Do you know--ha--why he should hide anywhere?’\n‘No.’", "\"그가 — 허 — 어딘가에 숨을 이유가 있는지 아십니까?\""]], [["It was exactly the same No as before, and put another barrier up.", "\"아니요.\" 그것은 앞서와 똑같은 아니요였고, 또 하나의 장벽을 세웠다."]], [["‘You asked me if I accounted for the disappearance to myself,’ Mrs Clennam sternly reminded him, ‘not if I accounted for it to you.", "\"당신은 제가 그 실종을 제 스스로에게 설명하느냐고 물으셨지,\" 클레넘 부인이 준엄하게 일깨웠다."], ["I do not pretend to account for it to you, sir.", "\"당신께 설명하느냐고 물으신 게 아니에요."], ["I understand it to be no more my business to do that, than it is yours to require that.’", "저는 당신께 그것을 설명하려 들지 않아요, 나리. 그것이 제 일이 아닌 것은, 그것을 요구하는 것이 당신 일이 아닌 것과 마찬가지라고 생각하니까요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit answered with an apologetic bend of his head.", "도릿 씨는 사과하듯 고개를 숙여 대답했다."], ["As he stepped back, preparatory to saying he had no more to ask, he could not but observe how gloomily and fixedly she sat with her eyes fastened on the ground, and a certain air upon her of resolute waiting; also, how exactly the self-same expression was reflected in Mr Flintwinch, standing at a little distance from her chair, with his eyes also on the ground, and his right hand softly rubbing his chin.", "더 물을 것이 없다고 말하려 뒤로 물러서면서, 그는 그녀가 얼마나 음울하게, 시선을 바닥에 못 박은 채, 무언가를 단호히 기다리는 듯한 기색으로 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있는지 보지 않을 수 없었다. 또한 그녀의 의자에서 조금 떨어져 선 플린트윈치 씨에게도 똑같은 표정이 고스란히 비치고 있음을, 그 역시 시선을 바닥에 두고 오른손으로 턱을 가만히 문지르고 있음을 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["At that moment, Mistress Affery (of course, the woman with the apron) dropped the candlestick she held, and cried out, ‘There!", "바로 그 순간, 애프리 부인이 (물론 그 앞치마를 쓴 여자였다) 손에 들고 있던 촛대를 떨어뜨리며 소리쳤다. \"저기!"], ["O good Lord!", "오 맙소사!"], ["there it is again.", "또 저거예요."], ["Hark, Jeremiah!", "들어 봐요, 제러마이아!"], ["Now!’", "지금요!\""]], [["If there were any sound at all, it was so slight that she must have fallen into a confirmed habit of listening for sounds; but Mr Dorrit believed he did hear a something, like the falling of dry leaves.", "소리가 조금이라도 있었다 한들 너무나 희미했으니, 그녀가 소리에 귀 기울이는 버릇이 단단히 들었음에 틀림없었다. 그러나 도릿 씨는 마른 잎이 떨어지는 것 같은 무언가를 듣긴 들었다고 믿었다."], ["The woman’s terror, for a very short space, seemed to touch the three; and they all listened.", "그 여자의 공포는, 아주 잠깐 동안, 세 사람을 다 건드리는 듯했다. 그리고 그들 모두 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Mr Flintwinch was the first to stir.", "먼저 움직인 것은 플린트윈치 씨였다."], ["‘Affery, my woman,’ said he, sidling at her with his fists clenched, and his elbows quivering with impatience to shake her, ‘you are at your old tricks.", "\"애프리, 이 사람아,\" 그가 주먹을 불끈 쥐고 그녀를 흔들어 대고 싶어 안달이 난 팔꿈치를 부들부들 떨며 그녀에게 슬그머니 다가서서 말했다."], ["You’ll be walking in your sleep next, my woman, and playing the whole round of your distempered antics.", "\"또 옛 버릇이 도졌군. 다음엔 잠결에 돌아다니면서 네 그 정신 나간 꼴을 한바탕 부리겠지, 이 사람아."], ["You must have some physic.", "약을 좀 먹어야겠어."], ["When I have shown this gentleman out, I’ll make you up such a comfortable dose, my woman; such a comfortable dose!’", "이 신사분을 배웅하고 나서, 내가 아주 편안한 약을 한 첩 지어 주지, 이 사람아. 아주 편안한 약으로!\""]], [["It did not appear altogether comfortable in expectation to Mistress Affery; but Jeremiah, without further reference to his healing medicine, took another candle from Mrs Clennam’s table, and said, ‘Now, sir; shall I light you down?’", "애프리 부인에게는 그것이 기대 속에서 그리 편안하게 다가오지 않는 듯했다. 그러나 제러마이아는 자신의 치료약은 더 언급하지 않고, 클레넘 부인의 탁자에서 다른 초 하나를 집어 들며 말했다. \"자, 나리, 아래까지 불을 밝혀 드릴까요?\""]], [["Mr Dorrit professed himself obliged, and went down.", "도릿 씨는 고맙다고 하고는 아래로 내려갔다."], ["Mr Flintwinch shut him out, and chained him out, without a moment’s loss of time.", "플린트윈치 씨는 한순간도 지체 없이 그를 내보내고 사슬로 잠가 버렸다."], ["He was again passed by the two men, one going out and the other coming in; got into the vehicle he had left waiting, and was driven away.", "그는 다시 그 두 사내를 지나쳤는데, 하나는 나가고 하나는 들어오는 중이었다. 그는 기다리게 해 둔 마차에 올라타고 떠났다."]], [["Before he had gone far, the driver stopped to let him know that he had given his name, number, and address to the two men, on their joint requisition; and also the address at which he had taken Mr Dorrit up, the hour at which he had been called from his stand and the way by which he had come.", "멀리 가기도 전에 마부가 마차를 세우더니, 그 두 사내가 함께 요구하기에 자기 이름과 마차 번호와 주소를 그들에게 알려 주었으며, 도릿 씨를 태운 곳의 주소며 마차 대기소에서 불려 온 시각이며 거쳐 온 길까지 다 알려 주었다고 일렀다."], ["This did not make the night’s adventure run any less hotly in Mr Dorrit’s mind, either when he sat down by his fire again, or when he went to bed.", "이 일로 그날 밤의 모험은, 도릿 씨가 다시 난롯가에 앉았을 때나 잠자리에 들었을 때나, 그의 마음속에서 조금도 덜 뜨겁게 들끓지 않았다."], ["All night he haunted the dismal house, saw the two people resolutely waiting, heard the woman with her apron over her face cry out about the noise, and found the body of the missing Blandois, now buried in the cellar, and now bricked up in a wall.", "밤새도록 그는 그 음산한 집을 떠돌았고, 두 사람이 단호히 기다리는 것을 보았고, 앞치마로 얼굴을 가린 여자가 그 소리에 대해 외치는 것을 들었으며, 실종된 블랑두아의 시체를 발견했으니, 그것은 어떤 때는 지하실에 묻혀 있었고 어떤 때는 벽 속에 벽돌로 봉해져 있었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 18. A Castle in the Air", "# CHAPTER 18. A Castle in the Air"]], [["Manifold are the cares of wealth and state.", "부귀와 지위에는 갖가지 근심이 따르는 법이다."], ["Mr Dorrit’s satisfaction in remembering that it had not been necessary for him to announce himself to Clennam and Co., or to make an allusion to his having had any knowledge of the intrusive person of that name, had been damped over-night, while it was still fresh, by a debate that arose within him whether or no he should take the Marshalsea in his way back, and look at the old gate.", "도릿 씨는 자신이 클레넘 상회에 신분을 밝히거나, 그 클레넘이라는 주제넘은 자를 알고 지냈다는 사실을 내비칠 필요가 없었다는 점을 떠올리며 흡족해했으나, 그 만족은 아직 신선할 때 간밤에 한풀 꺾이고 말았다. 돌아오는 길에 마셜시를 들러 옛 문을 한번 들여다보아야 할지 말아야 할지를 두고 그의 마음속에서 논쟁이 일었던 것이다."], ["He had decided not to do so; and had astonished the coachman by being very fierce with him for proposing to go over London Bridge and recross the river by Waterloo Bridge--a course which would have taken him almost within sight of his old quarters.", "그는 그러지 않기로 결정했다. 그리고 런던 다리를 건너 워털루 다리로 강을 다시 건너자고 — 그렇게 가면 옛 거처가 거의 눈에 들어올 만큼 가까운 길이었으므로 — 제안한 마부에게 몹시 사납게 굴어 그를 놀라게 했다."], ["Still, for all that, the question had raised a conflict in his breast; and, for some odd reason or no reason, he was vaguely dissatisfied.", "그래도, 그럼에도 불구하고, 그 문제는 그의 가슴속에 갈등을 불러일으켰고, 무슨 묘한 이유에서인지 아니면 아무 이유도 없이, 그는 막연히 불만스러웠다."], ["Even at the Merdle dinner-table next day, he was so out of sorts about it that he continued at intervals to turn it over and over, in a manner frightfully inconsistent with the good society surrounding him.", "이튿날 머들의 만찬 식탁에서조차 그는 그 일로 어찌나 심기가 불편했던지, 자신을 둘러싼 상류 사회와 무섭도록 어울리지 않는 모양새로 틈만 나면 그 일을 거듭 곱씹었다."], ["It made him hot to think what the Chief Butler’s opinion of him would have been, if that illustrious personage could have plumbed with that heavy eye of his the stream of his meditations.", "그 무거운 눈으로 집사장이 그의 명상의 흐름을 가늠할 수 있었다면 자신을 어떻게 보았을까 생각하니 그는 얼굴이 화끈거렸다."]], [["The farewell banquet was of a gorgeous nature, and wound up his visit in a most brilliant manner.", "작별 만찬은 화려하기 그지없었고, 그의 방문을 더없이 빛나는 방식으로 마무리지었다."], ["Fanny combined with the attractions of her youth and beauty, a certain weight of self-sustainment as if she had been married twenty years.", "패니는 젊음과 미모의 매력에 더하여, 마치 결혼한 지 스무 해는 된 듯한 어떤 진중한 자기 안정감을 갖추고 있었다."], ["He felt that he could leave her with a quiet mind to tread the paths of distinction, and wished--but without abatement of patronage, and without prejudice to the retiring virtues of his favourite child--that he had such another daughter.", "그는 마음 편히 그녀를 두고 떠나 그녀가 명예로운 길을 걷도록 맡길 수 있겠다고 느꼈고, 또 자신이 총애하는 막내딸의 조신한 미덕을 깎아내리지 않고, 또 후견인다운 너그러움을 거두지도 않으면서, 자신에게 그런 딸이 하나 더 있었으면 좋겠다고 바랐다."]], [["‘My dear,’ he told her at parting, ‘our family looks to you to--ha--assert its dignity and--hum--maintain its importance.", "\"얘야,\" 헤어질 때 그가 그녀에게 말했다."], ["I know you will never disappoint it.’", "\"우리 집안은 네가 — 하 — 그 위엄을 떨치고 — 험 — 그 중요함을 지켜 주기를 바란단다. 네가 결코 그것을 저버리지 않으리라 믿는다.\""]], [["‘No, papa,’ said Fanny, ‘you may rely upon that, I think.", "\"네, 아빠,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["My best love to dearest Amy, and I will write to her very soon.’", "\"그건 믿으셔도 될 거예요. 사랑하는 에이미에게 제 깊은 사랑을 전해 주시고, 저도 곧 편지를 쓰겠어요.\""]], [["‘Shall I convey any message to--ha--anybody else?’ asked Mr Dorrit, in an insinuating manner.", "\"내가 — 하 — 다른 누구에게 전할 말은 없느냐?\" 도릿 씨가 슬며시 떠보는 투로 물었다."]], [["‘Papa,’ said Fanny, before whom Mrs General instantly loomed, ‘no, I thank you.", "\"아빠,\" 패니가 말했다."], ["You are very kind, Pa, but I must beg to be excused.", "그 앞에 즉각 제너럴 부인의 모습이 어른거렸다. \"아뇨, 됐어요."], ["There is no other message to send, I thank you, dear papa, that it would be at all agreeable to you to take.’", "친절하시네요, 아빠, 하지만 사양하겠어요. 아빠께서 전해 주실 만한, 아빠 마음에 드실 다른 전언은 없어요, 고마워요, 사랑하는 아빠.\""]], [["They parted in an outer drawing-room, where only Mr Sparkler waited on his lady, and dutifully bided his time for shaking hands.", "그들은 바깥쪽 응접실에서 헤어졌는데, 거기서는 스파클러 씨만이 자기 숙녀의 시중을 들며 의무를 다해 악수할 차례를 기다리고 있었다."], ["When Mr Sparkler was admitted to this closing audience, Mr Merdle came creeping in with not much more appearance of arms in his sleeves than if he had been the twin brother of Miss Biffin, and insisted on escorting Mr Dorrit down-stairs.", "스파클러 씨가 이 마지막 알현에 들게 되자, 머들 씨가 슬그머니 들어왔는데, 소맷자락에 팔이 들어 있는 기색이라곤 비핀 양의 쌍둥이 형제라고 해도 좋을 만큼 거의 없는 모습으로, 도릿 씨를 아래층까지 배웅하겠노라 고집했다."], ["All Mr Dorrit’s protestations being in vain, he enjoyed the honour of being accompanied to the hall-door by this distinguished man, who (as Mr Dorrit told him in shaking hands on the step) had really overwhelmed him with attentions and services during this memorable visit.", "도릿 씨의 만류가 모두 헛되어, 그는 이 저명한 인물이 현관문까지 동행해 주는 영예를 누렸으며, 그 인물은 (도릿 씨가 계단에서 악수하며 그에게 말했듯이) 이 기억할 만한 방문 동안 그야말로 온갖 배려와 호의로 그를 압도해 주었다."], ["Thus they parted; Mr Dorrit entering his carriage with a swelling breast, not at all sorry that his Courier, who had come to take leave in the lower regions, should have an opportunity of beholding the grandeur of his departure.", "그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다. 도릿 씨는 벅찬 가슴을 안고 마차에 올랐으며, 아래층 영역에 작별 인사를 하러 와 있던 자신의 안내원이 그의 위풍당당한 출발을 지켜볼 기회를 가졌다는 사실에 조금도 서운하지 않았다."]], [["The aforesaid grandeur was yet full upon Mr Dorrit when he alighted at his hotel.", "도릿 씨가 호텔에서 내릴 때까지도 앞서 말한 그 위풍당당함은 여전히 그를 가득 채우고 있었다."], ["Helped out by the Courier and some half-dozen of the hotel servants, he was passing through the hall with a serene magnificence, when lo!", "안내원과 호텔 하인 대여섯의 부축을 받으며 그는 차분한 위엄을 풍기며 현관을 지나고 있었는데, 보라!"], ["a sight presented itself that struck him dumb and motionless.", "그를 벙어리처럼 굳어 버리게 만든 광경이 눈앞에 나타났다."], ["John Chivery, in his best clothes, with his tall hat under his arm, his ivory-handled cane genteelly embarrassing his deportment, and a bundle of cigars in his hand!", "존 치버리가, 가장 좋은 옷차림에, 높다란 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고, 상아 손잡이 지팡이를 점잖게 들었으나 그 때문에 거동이 어색해진 채로, 손에 시가 한 다발을 들고 서 있는 것이 아닌가!"]], [["‘Now, young man,’ said the porter.", "\"자, 젊은이,\" 짐꾼이 말했다."], ["‘This is the gentleman.", "\"이분이 그 신사분이라네."], ["This young man has persisted in waiting, sir, saying you would be glad to see him.’", "이 젊은이가 한사코 기다리겠다고 했습니다, 나리. 나리께서 반가워하실 거라면서요.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit glared on the young man, choked, and said, in the mildest of tones, ‘Ah!", "도릿 씨는 그 젊은이를 노려보며 목이 멘 듯하더니, 더없이 부드러운 어조로 말했다. \"아!"], ["Young John!", "영존이로구나!"], ["It is Young John, I think; is it not?’", "영존이지, 아마? 그렇지 않니?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ returned Young John.", "\"네, 나리,\" 영존이 대답했다."]], [["‘I--ha--thought it was Young John!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"내가 — 하 — 영존이라고 생각했지!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘The young man may come up,’ turning to the attendants, as he passed on: ‘oh yes, he may come up.", "\"저 젊은이는 올라와도 좋다.\" 그는 지나가며 시중꾼들을 돌아보았다."], ["Let Young John follow.", "\"오 그래, 올라와도 돼."], ["I will speak to him above.’", "영존을 따라오게 하라. 위에서 그와 이야기하겠다.\""]], [["Young John followed, smiling and much gratified.", "영존은 미소 지으며, 무척 흐뭇해하며 따라갔다."], ["Mr Dorrit’s rooms were reached.", "도릿 씨의 방에 이르렀다."], ["Candles were lighted.", "촛불이 켜졌다."], ["The attendants withdrew.", "시중꾼들이 물러갔다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ said Mr Dorrit, turning round upon him and seizing him by the collar when they were safely alone.", "\"자, 이보게,\" 도릿 씨는 안전하게 둘만 남게 되자 그를 향해 돌아서며 멱살을 움켜쥐고 말했다."], ["‘What do you mean by this?’", "\"이게 무슨 짓인가?\""]], [["The amazement and horror depicted in the unfortunate John’s face--for he had rather expected to be embraced next--were of that powerfully expressive nature that Mr Dorrit withdrew his hand and merely glared at him.", "가엾은 존의 얼굴에 떠오른 경악과 공포는 — 그는 차라리 다음 순간에 포옹을 받으리라 기대했던 터였다 — 어찌나 강렬하게 드러나는 것이었던지, 도릿 씨는 손을 거두고 그저 그를 노려보기만 했다."]], [["‘How dare you do this?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"감히 이런 짓을 해?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘How do you presume to come here?", "\"어떻게 여기 올 생각을 했지?"], ["How dare you insult me?’", "감히 나를 모욕해?\""]], [["‘I insult you, sir?’ cried Young John.", "\"제가 나리를 모욕한다고요?\" 영존이 외쳤다."], ["‘Oh!’", "\"오!\""]], [["‘Yes, sir,’ returned Mr Dorrit.", "\"그래, 이 사람아,\""], ["‘Insult me.", "도릿 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["Your coming here is an affront, an impertinence, an audacity.", "\"나를 모욕하는 거지. 네가 여기 온 것은 모욕이고, 무례이며, 뻔뻔함이다."], ["You are not wanted here.", "여기서 너를 원하는 사람은 없어."], ["Who sent you here?", "누가 너를 여기로 보냈나?"], ["What--ha--the Devil do you do here?’", "도대체 — 하 — 무슨 짓을 하러 여기 온 거야?\""]], [["‘I thought, sir,’ said Young John, with as pale and shocked a face as ever had been turned to Mr Dorrit’s in his life--even in his College life: ‘I thought, sir, you mightn’t object to have the goodness to accept a bundle--’", "\"저는 생각하기를, 나리,\" 영존이, 평생 — 칼리지 시절을 통틀어서도 — 도릿 씨를 향해 보인 적 없는 만큼 창백하고 충격받은 얼굴로 말했다. \"저는 생각하기를, 나리, 나리께서 다발 하나를 받아 주시는 친절을 베푸시는 데 반대하지 않으실 거라고 —\""]], [["‘Damn your bundle, sir!’ cried Mr Dorrit, in irrepressible rage.", "\"그놈의 다발은 집어치워, 이 사람아!\" 도릿 씨가 걷잡을 수 없는 분노로 외쳤다."], ["‘I--hum--don’t smoke.’", "\"나는 — 험 — 담배 안 피워.\""]], [["‘I humbly beg your pardon, sir.", "\"황공하게도 용서를 빕니다, 나리."], ["You used to.’", "예전엔 피우셨지요.\""]], [["‘Tell me that again,’ cried Mr Dorrit, quite beside himself, ‘and I’ll take the poker to you!’", "\"그 말을 다시 해 봐,\" 도릿 씨가 완전히 제정신을 잃고 외쳤다. \"그러면 부지깽이를 들어 네게 휘두를 테니!\""]], [["John Chivery backed to the door.", "존 치버리는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."]], [["‘Stop, sir!’ cried Mr Dorrit.", "\"멈춰, 이 사람아!\" 도릿 씨가 외쳤다."], ["‘Stop!", "\"멈춰!"], ["Sit down.", "앉게."], ["Confound you sit down!’", "빌어먹을, 앉으라니까!\""]], [["John Chivery dropped into the chair nearest the door, and Mr Dorrit walked up and down the room; rapidly at first; then, more slowly.", "존 치버리는 문에서 가장 가까운 의자에 털썩 주저앉았고, 도릿 씨는 방 안을 오갔다. 처음엔 빠르게, 그러다가 점점 느리게."], ["Once, he went to the window, and stood there with his forehead against the glass.", "한번은 창가로 가서 이마를 유리에 대고 서 있었다."], ["All of a sudden, he turned and said:", "그러다 별안간 그가 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["‘What else did you come for, Sir?’", "\"또 무슨 일로 온 건가, 이 사람아?\""]], [["‘Nothing else in the world, sir.", "\"세상에 다른 일은 전혀 없습니다, 나리."], ["Oh dear me!", "오 저런!"], ["Only to say, Sir, that I hoped you was well, and only to ask if Miss Amy was Well?’", "그저, 나리, 나리께서 안녕하시기를 바란다는 말씀을 드리려고, 그리고 에이미 양께서 잘 지내시는지 여쭈려고요.\""]], [["‘What’s that to you, sir?’ retorted Mr Dorrit.", "\"그게 자네와 무슨 상관인가?\" 도릿 씨가 쏘아붙였다."]], [["‘It’s nothing to me, sir, by rights.", "\"마땅히 따지면 저와는 아무 상관 없지요, 나리."], ["I never thought of lessening the distance betwixt us, I am sure.", "우리 사이의 거리를 좁히려는 생각은 결코 한 적 없습니다, 정말로요."], ["I know it’s a liberty, sir, but I never thought you’d have taken it ill.", "주제넘은 일인 줄 압니다, 나리, 하지만 나리께서 이를 언짢게 여기시리라고는 생각도 못 했습니다. 제 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 나리,\""], ["Upon my word and honour, sir,’ said Young John, with emotion, ‘in my poor way, I am too proud to have come, I assure you, if I had thought so.’", "영존이 감정에 북받쳐 말했다. \"저는 비록 보잘것없는 처지라도, 만일 그런 줄 알았다면 너무 자존심이 강해서 오지 않았을 겁니다, 정말입니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit was ashamed.", "도릿 씨는 부끄러웠다."], ["He went back to the window, and leaned his forehead against the glass for some time.", "그는 다시 창가로 가서 한동안 이마를 유리에 기댔다."], ["When he turned, he had his handkerchief in his hand, and he had been wiping his eyes with it, and he looked tired and ill.", "돌아섰을 때 그는 손수건을 손에 들고 있었고, 그것으로 눈물을 닦은 뒤였으며, 지치고 병든 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["‘Young John, I am very sorry to have been hasty with you, but--ha--some remembrances are not happy remembrances, and--hum--you shouldn’t have come.’", "\"영존, 자네에게 성급하게 굴어 무척 미안하네, 하지만 — 하 — 어떤 기억들은 행복한 기억이 아니라서, 그리고 — 험 — 자네는 오지 말았어야 했어.\""]], [["‘I feel that now, sir,’ returned John Chivery; ‘but I didn’t before, and Heaven knows I meant no harm, sir.’", "\"이제 저도 그렇게 느낍니다, 나리,\" 존 치버리가 대답했다. \"하지만 전엔 몰랐고, 하늘에 맹세코 나쁜 뜻은 없었습니다, 나리.\""]], [["‘No.", "\"아니야."], ["No,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "아니지,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I am--hum--sure of that.", "\"나는 — 험 — 그건 확신하네."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Give me your hand, Young John, give me your hand.’", "손을 주게, 영존, 손을 줘.\""]], [["Young John gave it; but Mr Dorrit had driven his heart out of it, and nothing could change his face now, from its white, shocked look.", "영존은 손을 내밀었으나, 도릿 씨가 이미 그 손에서 마음을 몰아낸 뒤였고, 이제 무엇으로도 그 창백하고 충격받은 표정에서 그의 얼굴을 바꿔 놓을 수는 없었다."]], [["‘There!’ said Mr Dorrit, slowly shaking hands with him.", "\"자!\" 도릿 씨가 천천히 그와 악수하며 말했다."], ["‘Sit down again, Young John.’", "\"다시 앉게, 영존.\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir--but I’d rather stand.’", "\"고맙습니다, 나리 — 하지만 그냥 서 있는 게 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit sat down instead.", "대신 도릿 씨가 앉았다."], ["After painfully holding his head a little while, he turned it to his visitor, and said, with an effort to be easy:", "한동안 괴로이 머리를 받치고 있다가, 그는 손님 쪽으로 머리를 돌리며, 애써 편안한 척하며 말했다."]], [["‘And how is your father, Young John?", "\"그래, 자네 부친께선 어떠신가, 영존?"], ["How--ha--how are they all, Young John?’", "어떤가 — 하 — 다들 어떻게 지내나, 영존?\""]], [["‘Thank you, sir, They’re all pretty well, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 나리, 다들 그럭저럭 잘 지냅니다, 나리."], ["They’re not any ways complaining.’", "별로 불평할 것 없이요.\""]], [["‘Hum.", "\"흠."], ["You are in your--ha--old business I see, John?’ said Mr Dorrit, with a glance at the offending bundle he had anathematised.", "자네는 — 하 — 옛날 그 일을 하고 있나 보군, 존?\" 도릿 씨가 자신이 저주했던 그 거슬리는 다발을 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["‘Partly, sir.", "\"일부는요, 나리. 저는 또한 제—\""], ["I am in my’--John hesitated a little--‘father’s business likewise.’", "존은 조금 망설였다. \"아버지의 일도 하고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh indeed!’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"오 그렇군!\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Do you--ha hum--go upon the ha--’", "\"자네는 — 하 험 — 그 — 하 —\""]], [["‘Lock, sir?", "\"문지기 일 말씀이십니까, 나리?"], ["Yes, sir.’\n‘Much to do, John?’", "네, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Yes, sir; we’re pretty heavy at present.", "\"할 일이 많은가, 존?\" \"네, 나리."], ["I don’t know how it is, but we generally _are_ pretty heavy.’", "요즘은 꽤 바쁩니다. 어찌 된 영문인지 모르겠지만, 저희는 대체로 꽤 바쁩니다.\""]], [["‘At this time of the year, Young John?’", "\"이맘때라서 그런가, 영존?\""]], [["‘Mostly at all times of the year, sir.", "\"거의 일 년 내내 그렇습니다, 나리."], ["I don’t know the time that makes much difference to us.", "저희한테 별 차이를 만드는 철이라는 게 있는 줄 모르겠어요."], ["I wish you good night, sir.’", "그럼 안녕히 주무십시오, 나리.\""]], [["‘Stay a moment, John--ha--stay a moment.", "\"잠깐만 있게, 존 — 하 — 잠깐만."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Leave me the cigars, John, I--ha--beg.’", "시가는 두고 가게, 존, 내가 — 하 — 부탁하네.\""]], [["‘Certainly, sir.’ John put them, with a trembling hand, on the table.", "\"물론입니다, 나리.\" 존은 떨리는 손으로 그것을 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["‘Stay a moment, Young John; stay another moment.", "\"잠깐만 있게, 영존. 잠시만 더."], ["It would be a--ha--a gratification to me to send a little--hum--Testimonial, by such a trusty messenger, to be divided among--ha hum--them--_them_--according to their wants.", "자네 같은 믿음직한 전령을 통해 약간의 — 험 — 성의를 보내는 것이 내게는 — 하 — 기쁨이 되겠네. 그들의 — 하 험 — 그들에게 — 그들에게 — 형편에 따라 나누어 주도록 말이야."], ["Would you object to take it, John?’", "그걸 가져가는 게 싫지는 않겠지, 존?\""]], [["‘Not in any ways, sir.", "\"전혀 싫지 않습니다, 나리."], ["There’s many of them, I’m sure, that would be the better for it.’", "그것으로 형편이 나아질 사람이 그곳에 많을 겁니다, 정말로요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, John.", "\"고맙네, 존."], ["I--ha--I’ll write it, John.’", "내가 — 하 — 그것을 적어 주겠네, 존.\""]], [["His hand shook so that he was a long time writing it, and wrote it in a tremulous scrawl at last.", "그의 손이 어찌나 떨렸던지 그것을 쓰는 데 한참이 걸렸고, 결국 떨리는 휘갈김으로 써냈다."], ["It was a cheque for one hundred pounds.", "그것은 백 파운드짜리 수표였다."], ["He folded it up, put it in Young John’s hand, and pressed the hand in his.", "그는 그것을 접어 영존의 손에 쥐여 주고, 자기 손으로 그 손을 꼭 눌렀다."]], [["‘I hope you’ll--ha--overlook--hum--what has passed, John.’", "\"지난 일은 — 하 — 부디 — 험 — 너그러이 보아 주기를 바라네, 존.\""]], [["‘Don’t speak of it, sir, on any accounts.", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 나리, 절대로요."], ["I don’t in any ways bear malice, I’m sure.’", "저는 결코 앙심 따위 품지 않습니다, 정말입니다.\""]], [["But nothing while John was there could change John’s face to its natural colour and expression, or restore John’s natural manner.", "그러나 존이 거기 있는 동안에는 어떤 것으로도 존의 얼굴을 본래의 혈색과 표정으로 되돌리거나, 존의 본래 태도를 회복시킬 수 없었다."]], [["‘And, John,’ said Mr Dorrit, giving his hand a final pressure, and releasing it, ‘I hope we--ha--agree that we have spoken together in confidence; and that you will abstain, in going out, from saying anything to any one that might--hum--suggest that--ha--once I--’", "\"그리고, 존,\" 도릿 씨가 그의 손을 마지막으로 한번 꼭 누른 뒤 놓아주며 말했다. \"우리가 — 하 — 비밀로 이야기를 나누었다는 데 의견이 일치하길 바라네. 그리고 자네가 나가는 길에, 한때 내가 — 하 — 그러니까 — 그런 것을 — 험 — 누구에게든 암시할 만한 말은 일절 삼가 주기를 바라네.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오! 안심하십시오, 나리,\""], ["I assure you, sir,’ returned John Chivery, ‘in my poor humble way, sir, I’m too proud and honourable to do it, sir.’", "존 치버리가 대답했다. \"제 비천하고 보잘것없는 처지에서도, 나리, 저는 그런 짓을 하기엔 너무 자존심이 세고 명예를 압니다, 나리.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit was not too proud and honourable to listen at the door that he might ascertain for himself whether John really went straight out, or lingered to have any talk with any one.", "도릿 씨는 존이 정말로 곧장 나가는지, 아니면 누군가와 이야기를 나누려고 머뭇거리는지를 직접 확인하려고 문에 귀를 대고 엿듣는 일에 대해서만큼은 너무 자존심이 세거나 명예를 따지지 않았다."], ["There was no doubt that he went direct out at the door, and away down the street with a quick step.", "존이 곧장 문으로 나가, 빠른 걸음으로 거리를 따라 사라진 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["After remaining alone for an hour, Mr Dorrit rang for the Courier, who found him with his chair on the hearth-rug, sitting with his back towards him and his face to the fire.", "한 시간 동안 홀로 있은 뒤, 도릿 씨는 안내원을 불렀는데, 안내원은 그가 의자를 벽난로 깔개 위에 놓고 등을 자기 쪽으로, 얼굴을 불 쪽으로 둔 채 앉아 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["‘You can take that bundle of cigars to smoke on the journey, if you like,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a careless wave of his hand.", "\"원한다면 저 시가 다발을 여행 중에 피우게 가져가게,\" 도릿 씨가 무심히 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["‘Ha--brought by--hum--little offering from--ha--son of old tenant of mine.’", "\"하 — 내 옛 — 험 — 소작인의 아들이 — 하 — 가져온 작은 선물이라네.\""]], [["Next morning’s sun saw Mr Dorrit’s equipage upon the Dover road, where every red-jacketed postilion was the sign of a cruel house, established for the unmerciful plundering of travellers.", "이튿날 아침 해는 도버 가도 위의 도릿 씨의 마차를 비추었는데, 그곳에서는 붉은 윗도리를 입은 역마차 마부 하나하나가, 여행자를 무자비하게 약탈하기 위해 세워진 잔혹한 집의 표시였다."], ["The whole business of the human race, between London and Dover, being spoliation, Mr Dorrit was waylaid at Dartford, pillaged at Gravesend, rifled at Rochester, fleeced at Sittingbourne, and sacked at Canterbury.", "런던과 도버 사이 인류의 온갖 일거리란 곧 강탈이어서, 도릿 씨는 다트퍼드에서 매복을 당하고, 그레이브젠드에서 노략질당하고, 로체스터에서 털리고, 시팅본에서 등쳐 먹히고, 캔터베리에서 약탈당했다."], ["However, it being the Courier’s business to get him out of the hands of the banditti, the Courier brought him off at every stage; and so the red-jackets went gleaming merrily along the spring landscape, rising and falling to a regular measure, between Mr Dorrit in his snug corner and the next chalky rise in the dusty highway.", "그러나 그를 그 도적 떼의 손아귀에서 빼내는 것이 안내원의 임무였으므로, 안내원은 매 역참마다 그를 구해 냈다. 그리하여 붉은 윗도리들은 봄날의 풍경을 따라 즐겁게 번뜩이며, 일정한 박자로 오르락내리락하면서, 아늑한 구석에 앉은 도릿 씨와, 먼지 자욱한 큰길의 다음번 백악질 언덕 사이를 달려갔다."]], [["Another day’s sun saw him at Calais.", "또 하루의 해는 칼레에 있는 그를 비추었다."], ["And having now got the Channel between himself and John Chivery, he began to feel safe, and to find that the foreign air was lighter to breathe than the air of England.", "이제 자신과 존 치버리 사이에 해협을 두게 되자, 그는 안전하다고 느끼기 시작했고, 이국의 공기가 영국의 공기보다 숨쉬기에 한결 가볍다는 것을 깨닫기 시작했다."]], [["On again by the heavy French roads for Paris.", "다시 파리를 향해 험한 프랑스 길을 달려갔다."], ["Having now quite recovered his equanimity, Mr Dorrit, in his snug corner, fell to castle-building as he rode along.", "이제 평정을 완전히 되찾은 도릿 씨는 아늑한 구석에 앉아, 마차를 타고 가는 동안 공중누각을 짓기 시작했다."], ["It was evident that he had a very large castle in hand.", "그가 매우 큰 누각을 짓고 있다는 것이 분명했다."], ["All day long he was running towers up, taking towers down, adding a wing here, putting on a battlement there, looking to the walls, strengthening the defences, giving ornamental touches to the interior, making in all respects a superb castle of it.", "온종일 그는 탑을 세웠다가 허물고, 여기에 별채를 더하고, 저기에 흉벽을 얹고, 성벽을 살피고, 방어 시설을 보강하고, 실내에 장식적인 손질을 가하면서, 모든 면에서 그것을 굉장한 누각으로 만들어 갔다."], ["His preoccupied face so clearly denoted the pursuit in which he was engaged, that every cripple at the post-houses, not blind, who shoved his little battered tin-box in at the carriage window for Charity in the name of Heaven, Charity in the name of our Lady, Charity in the name of all the Saints, knew as well what work he was at, as their countryman Le Brun could have known it himself, though he had made that English traveller the subject of a special physiognomical treatise.", "그의 골몰한 얼굴은 그가 무슨 일에 몰두해 있는지를 어찌나 분명히 드러냈던지, 역참마다 눈멀지 않은 불구자라면 누구나, 하늘의 이름으로, 성모의 이름으로, 모든 성인의 이름으로 자선을 구하며 찌그러진 작은 양철통을 마차 창 안으로 들이밀던 그 누구나, 그가 무슨 일을 하고 있는지를 그들의 동포 르 브룅 자신이 알았을 만큼 잘 알아챘다 — 비록 르 브룅이 그 영국인 여행자를 특별한 관상학 논문의 주제로 삼았더라도 말이다."]], [["Arrived at Paris, and resting there three days, Mr Dorrit strolled much about the streets alone, looking in at the shop-windows, and particularly the jewellers’ windows.", "파리에 도착하여 사흘을 머무는 동안, 도릿 씨는 홀로 거리를 많이 거닐며 상점 진열창을, 특히 보석상의 진열창을 들여다보았다."], ["Ultimately, he went into the most famous jeweller’s, and said he wanted to buy a little gift for a lady.", "마침내 그는 가장 유명한 보석상에 들어가, 한 숙녀에게 줄 작은 선물을 사고 싶다고 말했다."]], [["It was a charming little woman to whom he said it--a sprightly little woman, dressed in perfect taste, who came out of a green velvet bower to attend upon him, from posting up some dainty little books of account which one could hardly suppose to be ruled for the entry of any articles more commercial than kisses, at a dainty little shining desk which looked in itself like a sweetmeat.", "그가 그렇게 말한 상대는 매력적인 작은 여인이었다 — 더없이 세련되게 차려입은 발랄한 작은 여인으로, 그녀는 그를 응대하기 위해 초록색 벨벳 정자에서 나왔는데, 그곳에서 그녀는 그 자체가 마치 사탕과자처럼 보이는 앙증맞고 반짝이는 작은 책상에 앉아, 입맞춤보다 더 상업적인 어떤 품목을 기입하기 위해 줄을 친 것이라곤 도무지 상상하기 어려운 앙증맞은 작은 장부를 적고 있던 참이었다."]], [["For example, then, said the little woman, what species of gift did Monsieur desire?", "자, 예를 들어, 작은 여인이 말했다, 무슈께서는 어떤 종류의 선물을 원하시는지요?"], ["A love-gift?", "사랑의 선물인가요?"]], [["Mr Dorrit smiled, and said, Eh, well!", "도릿 씨는 미소 지으며 말했다. 에, 글쎄요!"], ["Perhaps.", "아마도요."], ["What did he know?", "누가 알겠소?"], ["It was always possible; the sex being so charming.", "늘 가능한 일이지요. 여인이라는 존재가 그토록 매력적이니."], ["Would she show him some?", "몇 가지 좀 보여 주겠소?"]], [["Most willingly, said the little woman.", "기꺼이요, 작은 여인이 말했다."], ["Flattered and enchanted to show him many. But pardon!", "여러 가지를 보여 드리게 되어 영광이고 황홀합니다."], ["To begin with, he would have the great goodness to observe that there were love-gifts, and there were nuptial gifts.", "하지만 죄송합니다! 우선, 사랑의 선물이 있고 혼인의 선물이 있다는 것을 부디 유념해 주시겠어요."], ["For example, these ravishing ear-rings and this necklace so superb to correspond, were what one called a love-gift.", "예를 들어, 이 황홀한 귀걸이와 그에 어울리는 이 더없이 멋진 목걸이는 사랑의 선물이라 부르는 것입니다."], ["These brooches and these rings, of a beauty so gracious and celestial, were what one called, with the permission of Monsieur, nuptial gifts.", "이 브로치들과 이 반지들은, 그토록 우아하고 천상적인 아름다움을 지닌 것으로, 무슈의 허락을 얻어 말씀드리자면, 혼인의 선물이라 부르는 것이지요."]], [["Perhaps it would be a good arrangement, Mr Dorrit hinted, smiling, to purchase both, and to present the love-gift first, and to finish with the nuptial offering?", "어쩌면 좋은 방법은, 도릿 씨가 미소 지으며 넌지시 말했다, 둘 다 사서, 사랑의 선물을 먼저 주고, 혼인의 예물로 마무리하는 것이겠군요?"]], [["Ah Heaven!", "아 저런!"], ["said the little woman, laying the tips of the fingers of her two little hands against each other, that would be generous indeed, that would be a special gallantry!", "작은 여인이 두 작은 손의 손끝을 마주 대며 말했다, 그것이야말로 참으로 후하신 일이고, 각별한 멋이겠습니다!"], ["And without doubt the lady so crushed with gifts would find them irresistible.", "그토록 선물 세례를 받은 숙녀라면 틀림없이 그것을 거역하지 못할 것입니다."]], [["Mr Dorrit was not sure of that.", "도릿 씨는 그 점이 확실치는 않았다."], ["But, for example, the sprightly little woman was very sure of it, she said.", "하지만, 예를 들어, 그 발랄한 작은 여인은 그것을 매우 확신한다고 말했다."], ["So Mr Dorrit bought a gift of each sort, and paid handsomely for it.", "그리하여 도릿 씨는 각 종류의 선물을 하나씩 사고, 그 값을 후하게 치렀다."], ["As he strolled back to his hotel afterwards, he carried his head high: having plainly got up his castle now to a much loftier altitude than the two square towers of Notre Dame.", "나중에 호텔로 걸어 돌아갈 때, 그는 고개를 높이 쳐들고 있었다. 이제 그의 누각을 노트르담의 두 네모난 탑보다 훨씬 더 높은 곳까지 끌어올린 것이 분명했다."]], [["Building away with all his might, but reserving the plans of his castle exclusively for his own eye, Mr Dorrit posted away for Marseilles.", "온 힘을 다해 짓되, 자기 누각의 설계도는 오직 자기 눈에만 간직한 채, 도릿 씨는 마르세유를 향해 마차를 몰아갔다."], ["Building on, building on, busily, busily, from morning to night.", "짓고 또 짓고, 바삐, 바삐, 아침부터 밤까지."], ["Falling asleep, and leaving great blocks of building materials dangling in the air; waking again, to resume work and get them into their places.", "잠이 들어 거대한 건축 자재 덩어리들을 허공에 매달아 둔 채로 두었다가, 다시 깨어나 작업을 이어 그것들을 제자리에 앉히고."], ["What time the Courier in the rumble, smoking Young John’s best cigars, left a little thread of thin light smoke behind--perhaps as _he_ built a castle or two with stray pieces of Mr Dorrit’s money.", "그동안 마차 뒷자리의 안내원은 영존의 가장 좋은 시가를 피우며 가느다란 한 줄기 옅은 연기를 뒤에 남겼는데 — 어쩌면 그 역시 도릿 씨의 흩어진 돈 몇 푼으로 누각을 한두 채 짓고 있었는지도 모른다."]], [["Not a fortified town that they passed in all their journey was as strong, not a Cathedral summit was as high, as Mr Dorrit’s castle.", "그들이 온 여정에서 지나친 어떤 요새 도시도 도릿 씨의 누각만큼 견고하지 않았고, 어떤 대성당 첨탑도 그만큼 높지 않았다."], ["Neither the Saone nor the Rhone sped with the swiftness of that peerless building; nor was the Mediterranean deeper than its foundations; nor were the distant landscapes on the Cornice road, nor the hills and bay of Genoa the Superb, more beautiful.", "손강도 론강도 그 비할 데 없는 건축물의 빠르기로 흐르지 못했고, 지중해도 그 토대보다 깊지 않았으며, 코르니체 가도에서 보이는 먼 풍경도, 장엄한 제노바의 언덕과 만도 그보다 더 아름답지 않았다."], ["Mr Dorrit and his matchless castle were disembarked among the dirty white houses and dirtier felons of Civita Vecchia, and thence scrambled on to Rome as they could, through the filth that festered on the way.", "도릿 씨와 그의 비할 데 없는 누각은 치비타베키아의 더러운 흰 집들과 더 더러운 죄인들 사이에 내려졌고, 거기서부터 길에 곪아 있는 오물을 헤치며 할 수 있는 대로 로마까지 기어가듯 나아갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air", "# CHAPTER 19. The Storming of the Castle in the Air"]], [["The sun had gone down full four hours, and it was later than most travellers would like it to be for finding themselves outside the walls of Rome, when Mr Dorrit’s carriage, still on its last wearisome stage, rattled over the solitary Campagna.", "해가 진 지 족히 네 시간은 지났고, 도릿 씨의 마차가 여전히 마지막 고단한 여정을 달리며 적막한 캄파냐 위를 덜컹덜컹 가로지를 무렵은, 어지간한 여행자라면 로마 성벽 바깥에 있고 싶지 않을 만큼 늦은 시각이었다."], ["The savage herdsmen and the fierce-looking peasants who had chequered the way while the light lasted, had all gone down with the sun, and left the wilderness blank.", "빛이 남아 있는 동안 길을 군데군데 채우던 사나운 목동들과 험상궂은 농부들은 모두 해와 함께 자취를 감추었고, 황야를 텅 비워 두었다."], ["At some turns of the road, a pale flare on the horizon, like an exhalation from the ruin-sown land, showed that the city was yet far off; but this poor relief was rare and short-lived.", "길이 굽이지는 어느 모퉁이들에서는, 폐허가 흩뿌려진 땅에서 피어오르는 김처럼 지평선 위에 희미한 불빛이 어른거려 도시가 아직 멀었음을 알려 주었다. 그러나 이 가련한 위안은 드물고도 덧없었다."], ["The carriage dipped down again into a hollow of the black dry sea, and for a long time there was nothing visible save its petrified swell and the gloomy sky.", "마차는 다시 검고 메마른 바다의 우묵한 곳으로 내려갔고, 오랫동안 보이는 것이라곤 돌처럼 굳어 버린 그 너울과 음울한 하늘뿐이었다."]], [["Mr Dorrit, though he had his castle-building to engage his mind, could not be quite easy in that desolate place.", "도릿 씨는 마음을 붙들어 둘 공중누각 짓기가 있었음에도, 그 황량한 곳에서 영 마음을 놓을 수가 없었다."], ["He was far more curious, in every swerve of the carriage, and every cry of the postilions, than he had been since he quitted London.", "그는 런던을 떠난 이래 그 어느 때보다도, 마차가 흔들릴 때마다, 마부들이 소리칠 때마다 훨씬 더 신경을 곤두세웠다."], ["The valet on the box evidently quaked.", "마부석에 앉은 시종은 분명 벌벌 떨고 있었다."], ["The Courier in the rumble was not altogether comfortable in his mind.", "뒷자리에 앉은 안내인도 마음이 썩 편치 않았다."], ["As often as Mr Dorrit let down the glass and looked back at him (which was very often), he saw him smoking John Chivery out, it is true, but still generally standing up the while and looking about him, like a man who had his suspicions, and kept upon his guard.", "도릿 씨가 유리창을 내리고 그를 돌아볼 때마다(그는 매우 자주 그랬다), 안내인이 존 치버리식 담배 연기를 뿜어내고 있는 것은 사실이었으나, 그러면서도 대개는 그러는 내내 일어서서 주위를 두리번거리는 것이, 무언가 의심을 품고 경계를 늦추지 않는 사람 같았다."], ["Then would Mr Dorrit, pulling up the glass again, reflect that those postilions were cut-throat looking fellows, and that he would have done better to have slept at Civita Vecchia, and have started betimes in the morning.", "그러면 도릿 씨는 다시 유리창을 올리며, 저 마부들이 살인자처럼 험악하게 생긴 놈들이라는 것, 그리고 차라리 치비타베키아에서 자고 아침 일찍 출발하는 편이 나았으리라는 것을 곱씹곤 했다."], ["But, for all this, he worked at his castle in the intervals.", "그러면서도 그는 그 사이사이에 자신의 누각을 짓는 일에 매달렸다."]], [["And now, fragments of ruinous enclosure, yawning window-gap and crazy wall, deserted houses, leaking wells, broken water-tanks, spectral cypress-trees, patches of tangled vine, and the changing of the track to a long, irregular, disordered lane where everything was crumbling away, from the unsightly buildings to the jolting road--now, these objects showed that they were nearing Rome.", "이제 허물어진 울타리의 잔해, 입을 떡 벌린 창문 구멍과 금이 간 담, 버려진 집들, 물이 새는 우물들, 부서진 물탱크들, 유령 같은 사이프러스 나무들, 뒤엉킨 포도 덩굴 무더기들, 그리고 보기 흉한 건물들에서부터 덜컹거리는 길에 이르기까지 모든 것이 허물어져 가는, 길고 불규칙하고 어수선한 골목길로 바뀌어 가는 것 — 이제 이런 것들이 그들이 로마에 가까워지고 있음을 보여 주었다. 그러더니 마차가 별안간 휙 돌며 멈춰 서자, 도릿 씨는 도적이 자기를 도랑에 처박고 털어 갈 순간이 왔다는 불안에 사로잡혔다."], ["And now, a sudden twist and stoppage of the carriage inspired Mr Dorrit with the mistrust that the brigand moment was come for twisting him into a ditch and robbing him; until, letting down the glass again and looking out, he perceived himself assailed by nothing worse than a funeral procession, which came mechanically chaunting by, with an indistinct show of dirty vestments, lurid torches, swinging censers, and a great cross borne before a priest.", "그러나 다시 유리창을 내리고 내다보니, 자기를 덮친 것은 다름 아닌 장례 행렬일 뿐이었다. 더러운 제의(祭衣)와 음산한 횃불과 흔들리는 향로가 어렴풋이 보이고, 사제 앞에는 커다란 십자가가 들려 있는 그 행렬은 기계적으로 성가를 읊조리며 지나갔다."], ["He was an ugly priest by torchlight; of a lowering aspect, with an overhanging brow; and as his eyes met those of Mr Dorrit, looking bareheaded out of the carriage, his lips, moving as they chaunted, seemed to threaten that important traveller; likewise the action of his hand, which was in fact his manner of returning the traveller’s salutation, seemed to come in aid of that menace.", "횃불에 비친 그 사제는 못생긴 사제였다. 침울한 인상에 이마가 툭 튀어나와 있었다."], ["So thought Mr Dorrit, made fanciful by the weariness of building and travelling, as the priest drifted past him, and the procession straggled away, taking its dead along with it.", "마차에서 모자도 벗은 채 내다보던 도릿 씨와 그의 눈이 마주쳤을 때, 성가를 읊느라 움직이는 그의 입술은 이 중요한 여행자를 위협하는 듯했고, 사실은 여행자의 인사에 답하는 동작이었던 그의 손짓도 그 위협에 가세하는 것만 같았다. 누각을 짓고 여행을 하느라 지쳐 공상에 빠진 도릿 씨는 그렇게 생각했고, 그러는 사이 사제는 그의 곁을 떠밀려 가듯 지나갔으며, 행렬은 죽은 자를 데리고 흩어지듯 멀어져 갔다."], ["Upon their so-different way went Mr Dorrit’s company too; and soon, with their coach load of luxuries from the two great capitals of Europe, they were (like the Goths reversed) beating at the gates of Rome.", "도릿 씨 일행 역시 그토록 다른 자신들의 길을 갔다. 그리하여 곧, 유럽의 두 위대한 수도에서 사들인 사치품을 가득 실은 마차와 더불어, 그들은 (거꾸로 된 고트족처럼) 로마의 성문을 두드리고 있었다."]], [["Mr Dorrit was not expected by his own people that night.", "그날 밤 도릿 씨의 집안 사람들은 그를 기다리고 있지 않았다."], ["He had been; but they had given him up until to-morrow, not doubting that it was later than he would care, in those parts, to be out.", "기다리긴 했으나, 그가 그런 지역에서 이렇게 늦은 시각까지 바깥에 있고 싶어 할 리 없다고 여겨 내일까지는 오지 않으리라 단념해 둔 터였다."], ["Thus, when his equipage stopped at his own gate, no one but the porter appeared to receive him.", "그래서 그의 마차가 제 집 대문 앞에 섰을 때, 그를 맞으러 나온 것은 문지기뿐이었다. 도릿 양은 외출했느냐고 그가 물었다."], ["Was Miss Dorrit from home?", "아닙니다. 안에 계십니다."], ["he asked. No.", "좋다, 하고 도릿 씨는 모여드는 하인들에게 말했다."], ["She was within.", "다들 있던 자리에 있거라."], ["Good, said Mr Dorrit to the assembling servants; let them keep where they were; let them help to unload the carriage; he would find Miss Dorrit for himself.", "마차에서 짐 부리는 것이나 거들어라. 도릿 양은 내가 직접 찾겠다."]], [["So he went up his grand staircase, slowly, and tired, and looked into various chambers which were empty, until he saw a light in a small ante-room.", "그리하여 그는 자신의 으리으리한 계단을 천천히, 지친 걸음으로 올라갔고, 여러 빈 방들을 들여다보다가, 마침내 어느 작은 대기실에서 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았다."], ["It was a curtained nook, like a tent, within two other rooms; and it looked warm and bright in colour, as he approached it through the dark avenue they made.", "그곳은 다른 두 방 사이에 텐트처럼 커튼이 드리워진 아늑한 구석방이었는데, 그 방들이 만들어 내는 어두운 통로를 지나 다가갈수록 따뜻하고 환한 빛으로 보였다."]], [["There was a draped doorway, but no door; and as he stopped here, looking in unseen, he felt a pang.", "휘장이 드리운 문간은 있었으나 문은 없었다. 그가 그곳에 멈춰 서서 들키지 않은 채 안을 들여다보다가, 그는 가슴이 아릿함을 느꼈다."], ["Surely not like jealousy?", "설마 질투 같은 것은 아니겠지?"], ["For why like jealousy?", "어째서 질투 같은 것이겠는가?"], ["There was only his daughter and his brother there: he, with his chair drawn to the hearth, enjoying the warmth of the evening wood fire; she seated at a little table, busied with some embroidery work.", "그곳에는 그의 딸과 그의 아우뿐이었다. 아우는 의자를 화롯가로 끌어당겨 저녁의 장작불 온기를 즐기고 있었고, 딸은 작은 탁자 앞에 앉아 무슨 자수 일에 골몰해 있었다."], ["Allowing for the great difference in the still-life of the picture, the figures were much the same as of old; his brother being sufficiently like himself to represent himself, for a moment, in the composition.", "그림 속 정물(靜物)의 큰 차이를 감안한다면, 인물들은 옛날과 거의 같았다. 그의 아우는 한순간 그 구도 속에서 그 자신을 대신할 만큼 그와 충분히 닮아 있었다."], ["So had he sat many a night, over a coal fire far away; so had she sat, devoted to him.", "그렇게 그는 저 멀리 떨어진 곳에서, 석탄불 앞에 앉아 숱한 밤을 보냈었다. 그렇게 딸은 그에게 헌신하며 앉아 있었었다."], ["Yet surely there was nothing to be jealous of in the old miserable poverty.", "그러나 분명 그 옛날의 비참한 가난 속에 질투할 만한 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["Whence, then, the pang in his heart?", "그렇다면 그의 가슴속 아릿함은 어디서 온 것인가?"]], [["‘Do you know, uncle, I think you are growing young again?’", "\"아세요, 작은아버지, 제 생각엔 작은아버지가 다시 젊어지고 계신 것 같아요.\""]], [["Her uncle shook his head and said, ‘Since when, my dear; since when?’", "작은아버지는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"언제부터, 얘야. 언제부터 말이냐?\""]], [["‘I think,’ returned Little Dorrit, plying her needle, ‘that you have been growing younger for weeks past.", "\"제 생각엔,\" 리틀 도릿이 바늘을 놀리며 대답했다."], ["So cheerful, uncle, and so ready, and so interested.’", "\"몇 주 전부터 작은아버지가 점점 젊어지고 계세요. 그렇게 명랑하시고, 그렇게 선뜻 나서시고, 그렇게 무엇에든 관심을 보이시고요.\""]], [["‘My dear child--all you.’", "\"얘야, 그게 다 너 덕분이란다.\""]], [["‘All me, uncle!’", "\"다 저 덕분이라뇨, 작은아버지!\""]], [["‘Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["You have done me a world of good.", "너는 내게 더없는 좋은 일을 해 주었어."], ["You have been so considerate of me, and so tender with me, and so delicate in trying to hide your attentions from me, that I--well, well, well!", "나를 그토록 헤아려 주고, 그토록 다정하게 대해 주고, 또 그 마음 씀씀이를 내게 숨기려고 그토록 섬세하게 애써 주어서, 나는 — 글쎄다, 글쎄다, 글쎄다!"], ["It’s treasured up, my darling, treasured up.’", "다 마음에 간직해 두었단다, 얘야, 간직해 두었어.\""]], [["‘There is nothing in it but your own fresh fancy, uncle,’ said Little Dorrit, cheerfully.", "\"그건 다 작은아버지의 새로운 공상일 뿐이에요.\" 리틀 도릿이 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["‘Well, well, well!’ murmured the old man.", "\"글쎄다, 글쎄다, 글쎄다!\" 노인이 중얼거렸다."], ["‘Thank God!’", "\"하느님 감사합니다!\""]], [["She paused for an instant in her work to look at him, and her look revived that former pain in her father’s breast; in his poor weak breast, so full of contradictions, vacillations, inconsistencies, the little peevish perplexities of this ignorant life, mists which the morning without a night only can clear away.", "그녀는 일손을 잠시 멈추고 그를 바라보았는데, 그 눈길이 그녀 아버지의 가슴속에 그 앞서의 아픔을 되살렸다. 모순과 동요와 일관성 없음으로, 이 무지한 삶의 자질구레하고 까다로운 당혹감으로, 밤 없는 아침만이 걷어 낼 수 있는 안개로 가득 찬 그 가련하고 약한 가슴속에."]], [["‘I have been freer with you, you see, my dove,’ said the old man, ‘since we have been alone.", "\"있잖니, 얘야, 우리 둘만 남고 나서는 내가 너한테 좀 더 허물없이 굴었지.\" 노인이 말했다."], ["I say, alone, for I don’t count Mrs General; I don’t care for her; she has nothing to do with me.", "\"둘만 남았다고 한 건, 제너럴 부인은 치지 않으니 하는 말이다. 나는 그 사람한테는 관심이 없어."], ["But I know Fanny was impatient of me.", "그 사람은 나와 상관없는 사람이지. 하지만 패니가 나를 못 견뎌 한 건 안다."], ["And I don’t wonder at it, or complain of it, for I am sensible that I must be in the way, though I try to keep out of it as well as I can.", "그렇다고 그걸 이상하게 여기거나 탓하지는 않아. 나도 내가 거치적거리는 존재라는 걸 잘 아니까."], ["I know I am not fit company for our company.", "되도록 비키려고 애는 쓰지만 말이다. 나는 우리 집안 손님들에게 어울리는 사람이 못 돼."], ["My brother William,’ said the old man admiringly, ‘is fit company for monarchs; but not so your uncle, my dear.", "내 형 윌리엄은,\" 노인이 감탄하듯 말했다."], ["Frederick Dorrit is no credit to William Dorrit, and he knows it quite well.", "\"임금님들에게도 어울릴 사람이지. 하지만 네 작은아버지는 그렇지 못해, 얘야."], ["Ah! Why, here’s your father, Amy!", "프레더릭 도릿은 윌리엄 도릿의 얼굴에 먹칠이나 하는 존재고, 자기도 그걸 아주 잘 안단다."], ["My dear William, welcome back!", "아! 아니, 얘야 에이미, 네 아버지가 오셨구나!"], ["My beloved brother, I am rejoiced to see you!’", "사랑하는 형님 윌리엄, 돌아온 걸 환영하오! 내 사랑하는 형님, 형님을 뵈니 기쁘기 그지없소!\""]], [["(Turning his head in speaking, he had caught sight of him as he stood in the doorway.)", "(말하면서 고개를 돌린 그는 문간에 서 있는 형을 보았던 것이다.)"]], [["Little Dorrit with a cry of pleasure put her arms about her father’s neck, and kissed him again and again.", "리틀 도릿은 기쁨의 탄성을 지르며 아버지의 목을 끌어안고 몇 번이고 입을 맞추었다."], ["Her father was a little impatient, and a little querulous.", "아버지는 조금 짜증스럽고 조금 투덜대는 기색이었다."], ["‘I am glad to find you at last, Amy,’ he said.", "\"마침내 너를 찾아서 기쁘구나, 에이미.\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘Ha.", "\"하."], ["Really I am glad to find--hum--any one to receive me at last.", "정말이지 마침내 — 흠 — 나를 맞아 줄 누군가를 찾게 되어 기쁘다."], ["I appear to have been--ha--so little expected, that upon my word I began--ha hum--to think it might be right to offer an apology for--ha--taking the liberty of coming back at all.’", "아무래도 나는 — 하 — 어찌나 안 기다려졌던지, 정말이지 나는 — 하 흠 — 애초에 돌아오는 — 하 — 결례를 무릅쓴 데 대해 사과라도 드려야 하지 않을까 생각이 들기 시작하더구나.\""]], [["‘It was so late, my dear William,’ said his brother, ‘that we had given you up for to-night.’", "\"너무 늦으셔서요, 사랑하는 형님.\" 아우가 말했다. \"오늘 밤은 안 오시려니 단념하고 있었답니다.\""]], [["‘I am stronger than you, dear Frederick,’ returned his brother with an elaboration of fraternity in which there was severity; ‘and I hope I can travel without detriment at--ha--any hour I choose.’", "\"나는 너보다 튼튼해, 프레더릭.\" 형이 형제애를 한껏 강조하면서도 그 안에 준엄함이 서린 말투로 대꾸했다. \"그리고 나는 — 하 — 내가 택하는 어느 시각에든 탈 없이 다닐 수 있기를 바라네.\""]], [["‘Surely, surely,’ returned the other, with a misgiving that he had given offence.", "\"물론이지요, 물론.\" 상대가, 자기가 비위를 거슬렀나 하는 불안에 사로잡혀 대꾸했다."], ["‘Surely, William.’", "\"물론입니다, 윌리엄.\""]], [["‘Thank you, Amy,’ pursued Mr Dorrit, as she helped him to put off his wrappers.", "\"고맙다, 에이미.\" 도릿 씨는 딸이 외투를 벗는 것을 거들자 말을 이었다."], ["‘I can do it without assistance.", "\"혼자서도 할 수 있어."], ["I--ha--need not trouble you, Amy.", "나는 — 하 — 너를 번거롭게 할 필요가 없다, 에이미."], ["Could I have a morsel of bread and a glass of wine, or--hum--would it cause too much inconvenience?’", "빵 한 조각하고 포도주 한 잔 좀 들 수 있겠느냐, 아니면 — 흠 — 너무 폐가 되겠느냐?\""]], [["‘Dear father, you shall have supper in a very few minutes.’", "\"아버지, 곧 저녁 식사를 차려 드릴게요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, my love,’ said Mr Dorrit, with a reproachful frost upon him; ‘I--ha--am afraid I am causing inconvenience.", "\"고맙구나, 얘야.\" 도릿 씨는 책망하는 듯한 서늘한 기색을 띠며 말했다."], ["Hum.", "\"나는 — 하 — 폐를 끼치는 게 아닌가 싶구나."], ["Mrs General pretty well?’", "흠. 제너럴 부인은 잘 지내느냐?\""]], [["‘Mrs General complained of a headache, and of being fatigued; and so, when we gave you up, she went to bed, dear.’", "\"제너럴 부인은 두통이 있다고, 또 피곤하다고 하셔서요. 그래서 아버지가 안 오시려니 단념했을 때 잠자리에 드셨어요.\""]], [["Perhaps Mr Dorrit thought that Mrs General had done well in being overcome by the disappointment of his not arriving.", "도릿 씨는 어쩌면 제너럴 부인이 자기가 도착하지 못한 실망감에 그렇게 압도된 것이 잘한 일이라고 생각했는지도 모른다."], ["At any rate, his face relaxed, and he said with obvious satisfaction, ‘Extremely sorry to hear that Mrs General is not well.’", "어쨌든 그의 얼굴은 누그러졌고, 그는 역력히 흡족한 기색으로 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 편찮으시다니 참으로 안됐구나.\""]], [["During this short dialogue, his daughter had been observant of him, with something more than her usual interest.", "이 짧은 대화가 오가는 동안, 그의 딸은 여느 때보다 더한 관심을 가지고 그를 살펴보고 있었다."], ["It would seem as though he had a changed or worn appearance in her eyes, and he perceived and resented it; for he said with renewed peevishness, when he had divested himself of his travelling-cloak, and had come to the fire:", "그녀의 눈에는 그가 어딘가 달라졌거나 수척해 보이는 듯했고, 그는 그것을 알아채고는 못마땅해했다. 여행용 망토를 벗고 화롯가로 다가왔을 때, 그가 새삼 짜증스럽게 말했기 때문이다."]], [["‘Amy, what are you looking at?", "\"에이미, 무얼 그리 보느냐?"], ["What do you see in me that causes you to--ha--concentrate your solicitude on me in that--hum--very particular manner?’", "내게서 무얼 보았기에 그렇게 — 하 — 그렇게 — 흠 — 유난스러운 식으로 내게 걱정을 쏟는 게냐?\""]], [["‘I did not know it, father; I beg your pardon.", "\"몰랐어요, 아버지. 죄송해요."], ["It gladdens my eyes to see you again; that’s all.’", "아버지를 다시 뵈니 눈이 즐거워서 그래요. 그뿐이에요.\""]], [["‘Don’t say that’s all, because--ha--that’s not all.", "\"그뿐이라고 하지 마라, 왜냐하면 — 하 — 그뿐이 아니니까. 너는 — 흠 — 너는,\""], ["You--hum--you think,’ said Mr Dorrit, with an accusatory emphasis, ‘that I am not looking well.’", "도릿 씨가 따지듯 힘주어 말했다. \"내 안색이 안 좋다고 생각하는 거지.\""]], [["‘I thought you looked a little tired, love.’", "\"좀 피곤해 보이셔서요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Then you are mistaken,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"그렇다면 네가 잘못 본 거다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Ha, I am _not_ tired.", "\"하, 나는 피곤하지 않아."], ["Ha, hum.", "하, 흠."], ["I am very much fresher than I was when I went away.’", "나는 떠날 때보다 훨씬 더 생기가 넘친다.\""]], [["He was so inclined to be angry that she said nothing more in her justification, but remained quietly beside him embracing his arm.", "그가 어찌나 화를 내려는 기색인지, 그녀는 더 이상 변명하지 않고 그저 그의 곁에 조용히 서서 그의 팔을 끌어안았다."], ["As he stood thus, with his brother on the other side, he fell into a heavy doze, of not a minute’s duration, and awoke with a start.", "그가 그렇게 한쪽에는 아우를 두고 서 있는 동안, 그는 일 분도 채 안 되는 묵직한 졸음에 빠졌다가 흠칫 깨어났다."]], [["‘Frederick,’ he said, turning to his brother: ‘I recommend you to go to bed immediately.’", "\"프레더릭,\" 그가 아우를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"당장 잠자리에 들기를 권하네.\""]], [["‘No, William.", "\"아니에요, 윌리엄."], ["I’ll wait and see you sup.’", "형님 저녁 드시는 걸 보고 기다릴게요.\""]], [["‘Frederick,’ he retorted, ‘I beg you to go to bed.", "\"프레더릭,\" 그가 받아쳤다."], ["I--ha--make it a personal request that you go to bed.", "\"잠자리에 들기를 청하네. 나는 — 하 — 자네가 잠자리에 들기를 개인적인 부탁으로 하는 걸세."], ["You ought to have been in bed long ago.", "자네는 진작 잠자리에 들었어야 해."], ["You are very feeble.’", "자네는 아주 쇠약해.\""]], [["‘Hah!’ said the old man, who had no wish but to please him.", "\"하!\" 그를 기쁘게 해 주는 것밖에 바라지 않는 노인이 말했다."], ["‘Well, well, well!", "\"글쎄, 글쎄, 글쎄!"], ["I dare say I am.’", "그런가 보군.\""]], [["‘My dear Frederick,’ returned Mr Dorrit, with an astonishing superiority to his brother’s failing powers, ‘there can be no doubt of it.", "\"내 사랑하는 프레더릭.\" 도릿 씨는 아우의 쇠해 가는 기력에 견주어 놀랄 만큼 우월한 태도로 대꾸했다."], ["It is painful to me to see you so weak.", "\"그건 의심할 여지가 없어. 자네가 그렇게 약해진 걸 보니 내가 다 괴롭네."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["It distresses me.", "마음이 아파."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["I don’t find you looking at all well.", "안색이 영 안 좋아 보여."], ["You are not fit for this sort of thing.", "자네는 이런 일을 감당할 몸이 아냐."], ["You should be more careful, you should be very careful.’", "더 조심해야 하네, 아주 조심해야 해.\""]], [["‘Shall I go to bed?’ asked Frederick.", "\"잠자리에 들까요?\" 프레더릭이 물었다."]], [["‘Dear Frederick,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘do, I adjure you!", "\"사랑하는 프레더릭,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["Good night, brother.", "\"그러게, 간청하네!"], ["I hope you will be stronger to-morrow.", "잘 자게, 아우님. 내일은 좀 더 튼튼해지기를 바라네."], ["I am not at all pleased with your looks.", "자네 안색이 영 마음에 들지 않아. 잘 자게, 사랑하는 사람아.\""], ["Good night, dear fellow.’ After dismissing his brother in this gracious way, he fell into a doze again before the old man was well out of the room: and he would have stumbled forward upon the logs, but for his daughter’s restraining hold.", "이렇게 정중한 방식으로 아우를 물린 뒤, 그는 노인이 미처 방을 다 나서기도 전에 다시 졸음에 빠져들었다. 딸이 붙들어 주지 않았더라면 그대로 장작 더미 위로 고꾸라질 뻔했다."]], [["‘Your uncle wanders very much, Amy,’ he said, when he was thus roused.", "\"네 작은아버지는 정신이 무척 오락가락하는구나, 에이미.\" 그렇게 깨어났을 때 그가 말했다."], ["‘He is less--ha--coherent, and his conversation is more--hum--broken, than I have--ha, hum--ever known.", "\"예전보다 말이 — 하 — 조리가 없고, 이야기가 더 — 흠 — 끊어진다, 내가 — 하, 흠 — 여태껏 본 것보다 말이다."], ["Has he had any illness since I have been gone?’", "내가 떠나 있는 동안 무슨 병이라도 앓았느냐?\""]], [["‘No, father.’", "\"아니에요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘You--ha--see a great change in him, Amy?’", "\"너는 — 하 — 그가 많이 변했다고 보느냐, 에이미?\""]], [["‘I have not observed it, dear.’", "\"저는 못 느꼈어요, 아버지.\""]], [["‘Greatly broken,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"많이 쇠했어.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Greatly broken.", "\"많이 쇠했다."], ["My poor, affectionate, failing Frederick!", "가엾고 다정한, 기력이 다해 가는 내 프레더릭!"], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Even taking into account what he was before, he is--hum--sadly broken!’", "예전 모습을 감안하더라도, 그는 — 흠 — 안타깝게도 많이 쇠했어!\""]], [["His supper, which was brought to him there, and spread upon the little table where he had seen her working, diverted his attention.", "그곳으로 가져와 그가 딸이 일하던 모습을 보았던 그 작은 탁자 위에 차려진 저녁 식사가 그의 주의를 돌려놓았다."], ["She sat at his side as in the days that were gone, for the first time since those days ended.", "그녀는 지나간 날들에서처럼 그의 곁에 앉았는데, 그 날들이 끝난 이래 처음 있는 일이었다."], ["They were alone, and she helped him to his meat and poured out his drink for him, as she had been used to do in the prison.", "그들은 단둘이었고, 그녀는 감옥에서 늘 그러던 대로 그에게 고기를 덜어 주고 마실 것을 따라 주었다."], ["All this happened now, for the first time since their accession to wealth.", "이 모든 일이, 그들이 부유해진 이래 처음으로 지금 일어나고 있었다."], ["She was afraid to look at him much, after the offence he had taken; but she noticed two occasions in the course of his meal, when he all of a sudden looked at her, and looked about him, as if the association were so strong that he needed assurance from his sense of sight that they were not in the old prison-room.", "그가 화를 낸 뒤라 그녀는 그를 자주 바라보기가 두려웠다. 그러나 그녀는 식사하는 동안 두 번, 그가 별안간 그녀를 보고는 주위를 둘러보는 것을 알아챘는데, 마치 그 연상이 너무도 강렬해 자기들이 옛 감옥 방에 있지 않다는 것을 눈으로 직접 확인할 필요가 있다는 듯했다."], ["Both times, he put his hand to his head as if he missed his old black cap--though it had been ignominiously given away in the Marshalsea, and had never got free to that hour, but still hovered about the yards on the head of his successor.", "두 번 다 그는 손을 머리로 가져갔는데, 마치 옛날의 검은 모자를 찾는 것 같았다 — 그 모자는 마셜시에서 수치스럽게 남에게 넘어간 뒤 그 시각까지도 풀려나지 못한 채, 여전히 후임자의 머리 위에서 그 마당들을 맴돌고 있었건만."]], [["He took very little supper, but was a long time over it, and often reverted to his brother’s declining state.", "그는 저녁을 아주 조금밖에 들지 않았으나 오래도록 식탁에 앉아 있었고, 자주 아우의 쇠약해진 상태로 화제를 돌렸다."], ["Though he expressed the greatest pity for him, he was almost bitter upon him.", "그는 아우를 더없이 가엾어하는 듯하면서도 거의 신랄하게 굴었다."], ["He said that poor Frederick--ha hum--drivelled.", "그는 가엾은 프레더릭이 — 하 흠 — 노망이 들었다고 했다."], ["There was no other word to express it; drivelled.", "달리 표현할 말이 없다는 것이었다. 노망이라고."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["It was melancholy to reflect what Amy must have undergone from the excessive tediousness of his Society--wandering and babbling on, poor dear estimable creature, wandering and babbling on--if it had not been for the relief she had had in Mrs General.", "만일 제너럴 부인이라는 위안이 없었더라면, 에이미가 그토록 지루하기 짝이 없는 그의 말동무 노릇을 하느라 — 정신을 놓고 횡설수설하며, 가엾고 사랑스럽고 존경할 만한 사람이지만, 정신을 놓고 횡설수설하며 — 얼마나 시달렸을지 생각하면 우울하다고 했다."], ["Extremely sorry, he then repeated with his former satisfaction, that that--ha--superior woman was poorly.", "그러더니 그는 그 — 하 — 빼어난 여성이 편찮으시다니 참으로 안됐다고, 앞서의 흡족한 기색으로 되풀이했다."]], [["Little Dorrit, in her watchful love, would have remembered the lightest thing he said or did that night, though she had had no subsequent reason to recall that night.", "리틀 도릿은 그 살뜰한 사랑으로, 그날 밤을 나중에 돌이켜 볼 까닭이 없었더라도, 그가 그날 밤 한 가장 사소한 말이나 행동까지 기억했을 것이다. 그녀는 늘 기억했다."], ["She always remembered that, when he looked about him under the strong influence of the old association, he tried to keep it out of her mind, and perhaps out of his own too, by immediately expatiating on the great riches and great company that had encompassed him in his absence, and on the lofty position he and his family had to sustain.", "그가 옛 연상의 강한 영향 아래 주위를 둘러볼 때면, 그것을 그녀의 마음에서, 어쩌면 자기 자신의 마음에서도 몰아내려고, 곧바로 자기가 떠나 있는 동안 자기를 에워쌌던 대단한 부와 대단한 사람들에 대해, 또 그와 그의 가족이 지탱해야 할 드높은 지위에 대해 장황하게 늘어놓았던 것을. 그녀는 또한 잊지 않고 기억했다."], ["Nor did she fail to recall that there were two under-currents, side by side, pervading all his discourse and all his manner; one showing her how well he had got on without her, and how independent he was of her; the other, in a fitful and unintelligible way almost complaining of her, as if it had been possible that she had neglected him while he was away.", "그의 모든 말과 모든 태도에는 두 가지 저류(底流)가 나란히 흐르고 있었다는 것을. 하나는 그가 그녀 없이도 얼마나 잘 지냈는지, 또 자기가 그녀에게서 얼마나 독립해 있는지를 그녀에게 보여 주는 것이었고, 다른 하나는 변덕스럽고 알 수 없는 방식으로, 마치 그가 떠나 있는 동안 그녀가 자기를 소홀히 했을 리라도 있다는 듯이 그녀를 거의 원망하는 것이었다."]], [["His telling her of the glorious state that Mr Merdle kept, and of the court that bowed before him, naturally brought him to Mrs Merdle.", "머들 씨가 누리는 그 영화로운 형편에 대해, 또 그 앞에 머리를 조아리는 사람들에 대해 그녀에게 이야기하다 보니, 그는 자연스럽게 머들 부인에게로 넘어갔다."], ["So naturally indeed, that although there was an unusual want of sequence in the greater part of his remarks, he passed to her at once, and asked how she was.", "어찌나 자연스러웠던지, 그의 이야기 대부분이 유달리 앞뒤가 맞지 않았음에도 그는 단번에 그녀에게로 넘어가 그녀가 어떻게 지내는지 물었다."]], [["‘She is very well.", "\"아주 잘 지내세요."], ["She is going away next week.’", "다음 주에 떠나신대요.\""]], [["‘Home?’ asked Mr Dorrit.", "\"본국으로?\" 도릿 씨가 물었다."]], [["‘After a few weeks’ stay upon the road.’", "\"도중에 몇 주 머물고 나서요.\""]], [["‘She will be a vast loss here,’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"여기로서는 큰 손실이겠구나.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."], ["‘A vast--ha--acquisition at home.", "\"본국으로서는 큰 — 하 — 소득이겠고."], ["To Fanny, and to--hum--the rest of the--ha--great world.’", "패니에게도, 그리고 — 흠 — 그 밖의 — 하 — 상류 사회에도 말이다.\""]], [["Little Dorrit thought of the competition that was to be entered upon, and assented very softly.", "리틀 도릿은 앞으로 벌어질 경쟁을 떠올리며 아주 나직이 수긍했다."]], [["‘Mrs Merdle is going to have a great farewell Assembly, dear, and a dinner before it.", "\"머들 부인이 성대한 송별 모임을 여신대요, 아버지. 그 전에 만찬도 있고요."], ["She has been expressing her anxiety that you should return in time.", "아버지가 제때 돌아오셔야 한다고 노심초사하셨어요."], ["She has invited both you and me to her dinner.’", "만찬에 아버지와 저를 둘 다 초대하셨어요.\""]], [["‘She is--ha--very kind.", "\"그분은 — 하 — 참 친절하구나."], ["When is the day?’", "그날이 언제냐?\""]], [["‘The day after to-morrow.’", "\"모레예요.\""]], [["‘Write round in the morning, and say that I have returned, and shall--hum--be delighted.’", "\"아침에 회답을 보내, 내가 돌아왔고 — 흠 — 기쁘게 가겠다고 전해라.\""]], [["‘May I walk with you up the stairs to your room, dear?’", "\"방까지 모셔다 드릴까요, 아버지?\""]], [["‘No!’ he answered, looking angrily round; for he was moving away, as if forgetful of leave-taking.", "\"아니!\" 그가 화난 듯 주위를 돌아보며 대답했다."], ["‘You may not, Amy.", "작별 인사도 잊은 듯 자리를 뜨려던 참이었다. \"그럴 것 없다, 에이미."], ["I want no help.", "도움 따위 필요 없어."], ["I am your father, not your infirm uncle!’ He checked himself, as abruptly as he had broken into this reply, and said, ‘You have not kissed me, Amy.", "나는 네 아비지, 병약한 네 작은아비가 아냐!\" 그는 이렇게 불쑥 받아치던 말을 그만큼 불쑥 멈추고는 말했다."], ["Good night, my dear!", "\"에이미, 네가 내게 입을 안 맞췄구나. 잘 자거라, 얘야!"], ["We must marry--ha--we must marry _you_, now.’ With that he went, more slowly and more tired, up the staircase to his rooms, and, almost as soon as he got there, dismissed his valet.", "이제 우리는 — 하 — 이제 너를 시집보내야겠다.\" 그러고서 그는 한층 느리고 한층 지친 걸음으로 계단을 올라 자기 방으로 갔고, 거기 들어서자마자 시종을 물렸다."], ["His next care was to look about him for his Paris purchases, and, after opening their cases and carefully surveying them, to put them away under lock and key.", "다음으로 그가 한 일은 파리에서 사 온 물건들을 둘러보는 것이었는데, 상자들을 열어 꼼꼼히 살펴본 뒤 자물쇠를 채워 치워 두었다."], ["After that, what with dozing and what with castle-building, he lost himself for a long time, so that there was a touch of morning on the eastward rim of the desolate Campagna when he crept to bed.", "그러고 나서는 졸기도 하고 누각을 짓기도 하며 오래도록 자신을 잊고 있었으니, 그가 살금살금 잠자리에 들 무렵에는 적막한 캄파냐의 동쪽 가장자리에 아침의 기미가 어려 있었다."]], [["Mrs General sent up her compliments in good time next day, and hoped he had rested well after this fatiguing journey.", "이튿날 제너럴 부인은 때맞춰 안부를 올려, 그 고된 여정을 마치고 잘 쉬셨기를 바란다고 전했다."], ["He sent down his compliments, and begged to inform Mrs General that he had rested very well indeed, and was in high condition.", "그는 안부를 내려보내며, 제너럴 부인에게 자기는 정말이지 아주 잘 쉬었으며 더없이 좋은 상태라고 알려 드린다고 했다."], ["Nevertheless, he did not come forth from his own rooms until late in the afternoon; and, although he then caused himself to be magnificently arrayed for a drive with Mrs General and his daughter, his appearance was scarcely up to his description of himself.", "그럼에도 그는 늦은 오후가 되어서야 자기 방에서 나왔다. 그러고 나서 제너럴 부인과 딸을 데리고 마차를 타러 나가기 위해 자신을 으리으리하게 차려입게 했음에도, 그의 행색은 스스로 묘사한 바에는 좀처럼 미치지 못했다."]], [["As the family had no visitors that day, its four members dined alone together.", "그날은 집안에 손님이 없었으므로 네 식구가 단출하게 함께 식사했다."], ["He conducted Mrs General to the seat at his right hand with immense ceremony; and Little Dorrit could not but notice as she followed with her uncle, both that he was again elaborately dressed, and that his manner towards Mrs General was very particular.", "그는 자기 오른편 자리로 제너럴 부인을 더없이 격식을 차려 이끌었다. 리틀 도릿은 작은아버지와 함께 뒤따르며 알아채지 않을 수 없었으니, 아버지가 다시금 공들여 차려입었다는 것, 그리고 제너럴 부인을 대하는 태도가 무척 각별하다는 것이었다."], ["The perfect formation of that accomplished lady’s surface rendered it difficult to displace an atom of its genteel glaze, but Little Dorrit thought she descried a slight thaw of triumph in a corner of her frosty eye.", "그 교양 있는 부인의 완벽하게 다듬어진 겉면은 그 점잖은 광택에서 티끌 하나조차 떼어 내기 어렵게 만들었으나, 리틀 도릿은 그 서릿발 같은 눈 한구석에서 의기양양함이 살짝 녹아내리는 기미를 본 것 같았다."]], [["Notwithstanding what may be called in these pages the Pruney and Prismatic nature of the family banquet, Mr Dorrit several times fell asleep while it was in progress.", "이 지면에서 자두와 프리즘식이라 부를 만한 그 가족 만찬의 성격에도 불구하고, 도릿 씨는 식사가 진행되는 동안 여러 번 잠이 들었다. 졸음 발작은 간밤처럼 불쑥 찾아왔고, 그만큼 짧고 깊었다."], ["His fits of dozing were as sudden as they had been overnight, and were as short and profound.", "처음으로 이 졸음이 그를 덮쳤을 때 제너럴 부인은 거의 어안이 벙벙한 얼굴이었다."], ["When the first of these slumberings seized him, Mrs General looked almost amazed: but, on each recurrence of the symptoms, she told her polite beads, Papa, Potatoes, Poultry, Prunes, and Prism; and, by dint of going through that infallible performance very slowly, appeared to finish her rosary at about the same time as Mr Dorrit started from his sleep.", "그러나 그 증상이 되풀이될 때마다 그녀는 자신의 정중한 묵주를 굴렸으니, 파파, 포테이토, 폴트리, 프룬스, 프리즘이었다. 그리고 그 어김없는 의식을 아주 천천히 끝까지 외우는 솜씨로, 도릿 씨가 잠에서 깨어나는 것과 거의 같은 때에 묵주 기도를 마치는 듯했다."]], [["He was again painfully aware of a somnolent tendency in Frederick (which had no existence out of his own imagination), and after dinner, when Frederick had withdrawn, privately apologised to Mrs General for the poor man.", "그는 또다시 프레더릭에게서 졸음 기운이 있다는 것을(그것은 그 자신의 상상 속에만 존재하는 것이었다) 괴롭게 의식했고, 식사 후 프레더릭이 물러가자 제너럴 부인에게 그 가엾은 사람에 대해 은밀히 양해를 구했다. \"더없이 존경스럽고 다정한 아우입니다.\""], ["‘The most estimable and affectionate of brothers,’ he said, ‘but--ha, hum--broken up altogether.", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 — 하, 흠 — 완전히 무너져 버렸지요."], ["Unhappily, declining fast.’", "안타깝게도 빠르게 쇠해 가고 있습니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Frederick, sir,’ quoth Mrs General, ‘is habitually absent and drooping, but let us hope it is not so bad as that.’", "\"프레더릭 씨는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다. \"늘 정신이 딴 데 가 계시고 기운이 없으시지요. 하지만 그렇게까지 나쁘지는 않기를 바랍시다.\""]], [["Mr Dorrit, however, was determined not to let him off.", "그러나 도릿 씨는 그를 그냥 넘길 작정이 아니었다."], ["‘Fast declining, madam.", "\"빠르게 쇠해 가고 있습니다, 부인."], ["A wreck. A ruin.", "폐선(廢船)이지요."], ["Mouldering away before our eyes.", "폐허입니다. 우리 눈앞에서 허물어져 가고 있어요."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Good Frederick!’", "착한 프레더릭!\""]], [["‘You left Mrs Sparkler quite well and happy, I trust?’ said Mrs General, after heaving a cool sigh for Frederick.", "\"스파클러 부인은 아주 잘 지내고 행복하게 두고 오셨겠지요?\" 제너럴 부인이 프레더릭을 위해 차가운 한숨을 한 차례 내쉰 뒤 말했다."]], [["‘Surrounded,’ replied Mr Dorrit, ‘by--ha--all that can charm the taste, and--hum--elevate the mind.", "\"둘러싸여 있지요.\" 도릿 씨가 대답했다."], ["Happy, my dear madam, in a--hum--husband.’", "\"취향을 매혹할 — 하 — 모든 것에, 그리고 — 흠 — 마음을 드높일 모든 것에 둘러싸여서요. 행복합니다, 친애하는 부인, — 흠 — 남편을 두고서요.\""]], [["Mrs General was a little fluttered; seeming delicately to put the word away with her gloves, as if there were no knowing what it might lead to.", "제너럴 부인은 조금 동요했다. 마치 그 말이 어디로 이어질지 알 수 없다는 듯, 자기 장갑으로 그 단어를 점잖게 밀어내려는 듯이 보였다."]], [["‘Fanny,’ Mr Dorrit continued.", "\"패니는요,\" 도릿 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["‘Fanny, Mrs General, has high qualities.", "\"패니는, 제너럴 부인, 빼어난 자질을 지녔습니다."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Ambition--hum--purpose, consciousness of--ha--position, determination to support that position--ha, hum--grace, beauty, and native nobility.’", "야심 — 흠 — 목적의식, — 하 — 지위에 대한 자각, 그 지위를 지키려는 결의 — 하, 흠 — 우아함과 미모와 타고난 고귀함을요.\""]], [["‘No doubt,’ said Mrs General (with a little extra stiffness).", "\"의심할 바 없지요.\" 제너럴 부인이 (다소 한층 뻣뻣하게) 말했다."]], [["‘Combined with these qualities, madam,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘Fanny has--ha--manifested one blemish which has made me--hum--made me uneasy, and--ha--I must add, angry; but which I trust may now be considered at an end, even as to herself, and which is undoubtedly at an end as to--ha--others.’", "\"이런 자질들과 더불어, 부인,\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"패니는 — 하 — 한 가지 흠을 드러내어 나를 — 흠 — 나를 불편하게, 그리고 — 하 — 덧붙이건대, 화나게 만들었습니다. 그러나 이제는 그것이 끝났다고 여겨도 좋으리라 믿습니다, 그 애 자신에 대해서까지도요. 그리고 — 하 — 다른 사람들에 대해서는 의심할 바 없이 끝났습니다.\""]], [["‘To what, Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, with her gloves again somewhat excited, ‘can you allude?", "\"무엇을 두고, 도릿 씨,\" 제너럴 부인이 또다시 다소 들뜬 장갑을 매만지며 대꾸했다."], ["I am at a loss to--’", "\"말씀하시는 건지요? 저는 도무지 — \""]], [["‘Do not say that, my dear madam,’ interrupted Mr Dorrit.", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 친애하는 부인.\" 도릿 씨가 가로막았다."]], [["Mrs General’s voice, as it died away, pronounced the words, ‘at a loss to imagine.’", "제너럴 부인의 목소리는 잦아들며 \"도무지 짐작이 안 갑니다.\"라는 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["After which Mr Dorrit was seized with a doze for about a minute, out of which he sprang with spasmodic nimbleness.", "그 뒤 도릿 씨는 일 분쯤 졸음에 사로잡혔다가, 경련하듯 날쌘 몸짓으로 벌떡 깨어났다."]], [["‘I refer, Mrs General, to that--ha--strong spirit of opposition, or--hum--I might say--ha--jealousy in Fanny, which has occasionally risen against the--ha--sense I entertain of--hum--the claims of--ha--the lady with whom I have now the honour of communing.’", "\"내가 말하는 것은, 제너럴 부인, 그 — 하 — 강한 반발심, 또는 — 흠 — 말하자면 — 하 — 패니의 질투심입니다. 그것이 이따금 고개를 들어 — 하 — 내가 지금 더불어 이야기 나누는 영광을 누리고 있는 — 흠 — 그 숙녀분의 — 하 — 자격에 대해 내가 품은 — 흠 — 인식에 맞서곤 했지요.\""]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ returned Mrs General, ‘is ever but too obliging, ever but too appreciative.", "\"도릿 씨는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["If there have been moments when I have imagined that Miss Dorrit has indeed resented the favourable opinion Mr Dorrit has formed of my services, I have found, in that only too high opinion, my consolation and recompense.’", "\"언제나 너무도 친절하시고, 언제나 너무도 후하게 평가해 주십니다. 도릿 씨가 제 봉사에 대해 형성하신 호의적인 의견에 도릿 양이 정말로 반감을 품은 것은 아닐까 상상했던 순간들이 있었다면, 저는 바로 그 너무도 높은 의견에서 위안과 보답을 찾았답니다.\""]], [["‘Opinion of your services, madam?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"부인의 봉사에 대한 의견이라고요?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Of,’ Mrs General repeated, in an elegantly impressive manner, ‘my services.’", "\"그,\" 제너럴 부인이 우아하면서도 인상적인 태도로 되풀이했다. \"제 봉사에 대한 의견 말이지요.\""]], [["‘Of your services alone, dear madam?’ said Mr Dorrit.", "\"부인의 봉사에만 대한 것이겠습니까, 친애하는 부인?\" 도릿 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I presume,’ retorted Mrs General, in her former impressive manner, ‘of my services alone. For, to what else,’ said Mrs General, with a slightly interrogative action of her gloves, ‘could I impute--’", "\"제 짐작에는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 앞서의 인상적인 태도로 받아쳤다. \"제 봉사에만 대한 것이겠지요. 그렇지 않다면, 달리 무엇에,\" 제너럴 부인이 장갑으로 살짝 묻는 듯한 동작을 하며 말했다. \"제가 그것을 돌릴 수 있겠습니까 — \""]], [["‘To--ha--yourself, Mrs General.", "\"부인 — 하 — 자신에게요, 제너럴 부인."], ["Ha, hum.", "하, 흠."], ["To yourself and your merits,’ was Mr Dorrit’s rejoinder.", "부인 자신과 부인의 미덕에 말입니다.\" 이것이 도릿 씨의 응수였다."]], [["‘Mr Dorrit will pardon me,’ said Mrs General, ‘if I remark that this is not a time or place for the pursuit of the present conversation.", "\"도릿 씨께서 용서해 주시겠지요.\" 제너럴 부인이 말했다."], ["Mr Dorrit will excuse me if I remind him that Miss Dorrit is in the adjoining room, and is visible to myself while I utter her name.", "\"지금이 이 대화를 이어 갈 때나 장소가 아니라고 제가 말씀드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 양해해 주시겠지요."], ["Mr Dorrit will forgive me if I observe that I am agitated, and that I find there are moments when weaknesses I supposed myself to have subdued, return with redoubled power.", "도릿 양이 옆방에 있고, 제가 그 이름을 입에 올리는 동안 그분이 제 눈에 보인다는 것을 일깨워 드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 너그러이 봐주시겠지요."], ["Mr Dorrit will allow me to withdraw.’", "제가 마음이 흔들리고 있다는 것, 그리고 제가 다스렸다고 여겼던 약함이 갑절의 힘으로 되살아나는 순간들이 있음을 알게 되었다고 말씀드려도요. 도릿 씨께서 제가 물러가도록 허락해 주시겠지요.\""]], [["‘Hum. Perhaps we may resume this--ha--interesting conversation,’ said Mr Dorrit, ‘at another time; unless it should be, what I hope it is not--hum--in any way disagreeable to--ah--Mrs General.’", "\"흠. 어쩌면 이 — 하 — 흥미로운 대화를 다시 이어 갈 수 있을지도 모르겠습니다.\" 도릿 씨가 말했다. \"다른 때에 말이지요. 다만 그것이, 제가 그러지 않기를 바라는 바이지만 — 흠 — 어떤 식으로든 — 아 — 제너럴 부인께 불쾌한 일이 아니라면요.\""]], [["‘Mr Dorrit,’ said Mrs General, casting down her eyes as she rose with a bend, ‘must ever claim my homage and obedience.’", "\"도릿 씨께서는요,\" 제너럴 부인이 몸을 숙여 일어서며 눈을 내리깔고 말했다. \"언제까지나 제 경의와 순종을 받으실 분이십니다.\""]], [["Mrs General then took herself off in a stately way, and not with that amount of trepidation upon her which might have been expected in a less remarkable woman.", "그러고 나서 제너럴 부인은 위엄 있게 자리를 떴는데, 그보다 덜 비범한 여인에게서라면 응당 예상되었을 만큼의 동요는 보이지 않았다."], ["Mr Dorrit, who had conducted his part of the dialogue with a certain majestic and admiring condescension--much as some people may be seen to conduct themselves in Church, and to perform their part in the service--appeared, on the whole, very well satisfied with himself and with Mrs General too.", "도릿 씨는 자기 몫의 대화를 어떤 위풍당당하고도 흐뭇한 겸양의 태도로 — 흡사 어떤 사람들이 교회에서 처신하며 예배에서 자기 몫을 수행할 때 보이는 모습으로 — 이끌어 왔던 터라, 대체로 자기 자신에 대해서도 또 제너럴 부인에 대해서도 무척 만족스러워 보였다."], ["On the return of that lady to tea, she had touched herself up with a little powder and pomatum, and was not without moral enchantment likewise: the latter showing itself in much sweet patronage of manner towards Miss Dorrit, and in an air of as tender interest in Mr Dorrit as was consistent with rigid propriety.", "그 부인이 차를 마시러 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 분과 포마드를 약간 발라 단장했고, 또한 정신적 매혹도 없지 않았으니, 그 매혹은 도릿 양을 대하는 더없이 다정스러운 후견인의 태도로, 또 엄격한 예법에 어긋나지 않는 한도 안에서 도릿 씨에게 보이는 다정한 관심의 분위기로 드러났다."], ["At the close of the evening, when she rose to retire, Mr Dorrit took her by the hand as if he were going to lead her out into the Piazza of the people to walk a minuet by moonlight, and with great solemnity conducted her to the room door, where he raised her knuckles to his lips.", "저녁이 끝나 갈 무렵 그녀가 물러가려고 일어섰을 때, 도릿 씨는 마치 그녀를 광장으로 이끌고 나가 달빛 아래 미뉴에트라도 출 듯이 그녀의 손을 잡았고, 더없이 엄숙하게 방문까지 그녀를 안내하여 그곳에서 그녀의 손마디를 입술로 가져갔다."], ["Having parted from her with what may be conjectured to have been a rather bony kiss of a cosmetic flavour, he gave his daughter his blessing, graciously.", "화장품 향이 나는 다소 뼈가 만져지는 입맞춤이었으리라 짐작되는 것으로 그녀와 작별한 뒤, 그는 우아하게 딸에게 축복을 내렸다."], ["And having thus hinted that there was something remarkable in the wind, he again went to bed.", "그리고 이렇게 무언가 심상치 않은 일이 일어나고 있음을 넌지시 비친 뒤, 그는 다시 잠자리에 들었다."]], [["He remained in the seclusion of his own chamber next morning; but, early in the afternoon, sent down his best compliments to Mrs General, by Mr Tinkler, and begged she would accompany Miss Dorrit on an airing without him.", "이튿날 아침 그는 자기 방에 틀어박혀 있었다. 그러나 이른 오후에 그는 팅클러 씨를 시켜 제너럴 부인에게 정중한 안부를 내려보내며, 자기 없이 도릿 양과 함께 바람을 쐬러 다녀오시기를 청했다."], ["His daughter was dressed for Mrs Merdle’s dinner before he appeared.", "그가 모습을 드러내기 전에 그의 딸은 머들 부인의 만찬에 갈 채비를 마쳤다."], ["He then presented himself in a refulgent condition as to his attire, but looking indefinably shrunken and old.", "이윽고 그가 나타났는데, 차림새로 보면 눈부신 상태였으나, 어딘지 모르게 쪼그라들고 늙어 보였다."], ["However, as he was plainly determined to be angry with her if she so much as asked him how he was, she only ventured to kiss his cheek, before accompanying him to Mrs Merdle’s with an anxious heart.", "그러나 그녀가 안색이 어떠시냐고 묻기만 해도 화를 낼 작정인 것이 분명했으므로, 그녀는 감히 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추기만 하고, 불안한 마음으로 그와 함께 머들 부인 댁으로 갔다."]], [["The distance that they had to go was very short, but he was at his building work again before the carriage had half traversed it.", "가야 할 거리는 무척 짧았으나, 마차가 그 길을 절반도 가기 전에 그는 다시 누각 짓는 일에 빠져 있었다. 머들 부인은 그를 대단히 깍듯하게 맞이했다."], ["Mrs Merdle received him with great distinction; the bosom was in admirable preservation, and on the best terms with itself; the dinner was very choice; and the company was very select.", "그 가슴은 감탄스러우리만치 잘 보존되어 있었고 더없이 흡족한 상태였다. 만찬은 무척 고급스러웠고 손님들은 무척 엄선되어 있었다."]], [["It was principally English; saving that it comprised the usual French Count and the usual Italian Marchese--decorative social milestones, always to be found in certain places, and varying very little in appearance. The table was long, and the dinner was long; and Little Dorrit, overshadowed by a large pair of black whiskers and a large white cravat, lost sight of her father altogether, until a servant put a scrap of paper in her hand, with a whispered request from Mrs Merdle that she would read it directly. Mrs Merdle had written on it in pencil, ‘Pray come and speak to Mr Dorrit, I doubt if he is well.’", "손님들은 주로 영국인이었는데, 다만 으레 빠지지 않는 프랑스인 백작과 으레 빠지지 않는 이탈리아인 후작이 끼어 있었다. 어떤 자리에서는 으레 발견되며 외양이 거의 변하지 않는, 사교계의 장식용 이정표 같은 이들이었다. 식탁은 길었고 만찬도 길었다. 리틀 도릿은 커다란 검은 구레나룻과 커다란 흰 크라바트에 가려, 한 하인이 그녀의 손에 종잇조각을 쥐어 주며 그것을 곧바로 읽어 달라는 머들 부인의 속삭이는 부탁을 전할 때까지, 아버지를 아예 시야에서 놓치고 있었다. 머들 부인은 그 위에 연필로 이렇게 써 두었다. \"도릿 씨에게 가서 말씀 좀 나눠 주세요. 편찮으신 게 아닌가 싶어요.\""]], [["She was hurrying to him, unobserved, when he got up out of his chair, and leaning over the table called to her, supposing her to be still in her place:", "그녀가 남의 눈에 띄지 않게 그에게 서둘러 가려던 참에, 그가 의자에서 일어나 식탁 위로 몸을 기울이고는, 그녀가 아직 제자리에 있는 줄 알고 그녀를 불렀다."]], [["‘Amy, Amy, my child!’", "\"에이미, 에이미, 얘야!\""]], [["The action was so unusual, to say nothing of his strange eager appearance and strange eager voice, that it instantaneously caused a profound silence.", "그 행동이 어찌나 예사롭지 않은지, 그의 야릇하게 다급한 모습과 야릇하게 다급한 목소리는 말할 것도 없이, 그 즉시 깊은 정적이 내려앉았다."]], [["‘Amy, my dear,’ he repeated.", "\"에이미, 얘야.\" 그가 되풀이했다."], ["‘Will you go and see if Bob is on the lock?’", "\"가서 밥이 자물쇠를 보고 있는지 좀 봐 주겠니?\""]], [["She was at his side, and touching him, but he still perversely supposed her to be in her seat, and called out, still leaning over the table, ‘Amy, Amy.", "그녀는 그의 곁에 와서 그를 만지고 있었으나, 그는 여전히 엉뚱하게도 그녀가 제자리에 있는 줄 알고, 여전히 식탁 위로 몸을 기울인 채 외쳤다. \"에이미, 에이미."], ["I don’t feel quite myself.", "나는 영 내 정신이 아니구나."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["I don’t know what’s the matter with me.", "내가 왜 이러는지 모르겠다."], ["I particularly wish to see Bob.", "나는 밥을 꼭 만나야겠어."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Of all the turnkeys, he’s as much my friend as yours.", "모든 간수들 중에서도 그는 너만큼이나 내 벗이지."], ["See if Bob is in the lodge, and beg him to come to me.’", "밥이 수위실에 있는지 보고, 내게 와 달라고 청해라.\""]], [["All the guests were now in consternation, and everybody rose.", "이제 손님들은 모두 경악했고, 다들 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["‘Dear father, I am not there; I am here, by you.’", "\"아버지, 저는 거기 있지 않아요. 저 여기, 아버지 곁에 있어요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You are here, Amy!", "너 여기 있구나, 에이미!"], ["Good.", "좋다."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Good.", "좋아."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Call Bob.", "밥을 불러라."], ["If he has been relieved, and is not on the lock, tell Mrs Bangham to go and fetch him.’", "그가 교대해서 자물쇠를 보고 있지 않거든, 뱅엄 부인더러 가서 그를 데려오라고 해라.\""]], [["She was gently trying to get him away; but he resisted, and would not go.", "그녀는 그를 살며시 데려가려 했으나, 그는 버티며 가려 하지 않았다."]], [["‘I tell you, child,’ he said petulantly, ‘I can’t be got up the narrow stairs without Bob.", "\"내가 말하잖니, 얘야.\" 그가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["Ha. Send for Bob.", "\"나는 밥 없이는 그 좁은 계단을 올라갈 수가 없어."], ["Hum.", "하. 밥을 부르러 보내라."], ["Send for Bob--best of all the turnkeys--send for Bob!’", "흠. 밥을 부르러 보내 — 모든 간수들 중에 으뜸가는 밥을 — 밥을 부르러 보내라!\""]], [["He looked confusedly about him, and, becoming conscious of the number of faces by which he was surrounded, addressed them:", "그는 어리둥절하게 주위를 둘러보다가, 자기를 에워싼 수많은 얼굴들을 의식하게 되자, 그들에게 말했다."]], [["‘Ladies and gentlemen, the duty--ha--devolves upon me of--hum--welcoming you to the Marshalsea!", "\"신사 숙녀 여러분, 여러분을 — 하 — 마셜시에 맞이하는 — 흠 — 소임이 제게 떨어졌습니다!"], ["Welcome to the Marshalsea!", "마셜시에 오신 것을 환영합니다!"], ["The space is--ha--limited--limited--the parade might be wider; but you will find it apparently grow larger after a time--a time, ladies and gentlemen--and the air is, all things considered, very good.", "공간이 — 하 — 비좁습니다 — 비좁아요 — 산책로가 좀 더 넓었으면 좋으련만요. 하지만 얼마쯤 지나고 나면 — 얼마쯤 지나고 나면, 신사 숙녀 여러분 — 겉보기에 더 넓어지는 걸 알게 되실 겁니다."], ["It blows over the--ha--Surrey hills.", "그리고 공기는, 이것저것 다 따져 보면, 아주 좋습니다. — 하 — 서리 구릉 너머에서 불어오지요."], ["Blows over the Surrey hills.", "서리 구릉 너머에서 불어옵니다."], ["This is the Snuggery.", "이곳이 스너거리입니다."], ["Hum.", "흠."], ["Supported by a small subscription of the--ha--Collegiate body.", "— 하 — 수용소 일원들의 소액 갹출로 운영되지요."], ["In return for which--hot water--general kitchen--and little domestic advantages.", "그 대가로 — 더운물 — 공동 취사장 — 그리고 자잘한 살림의 편의를 누립니다."], ["Those who are habituated to the--ha--Marshalsea, are pleased to call me its father.", "— 하 — 마셜시에 익숙해진 분들은 저를 그곳의 아버지라 부르기를 좋아하십니다."], ["I am accustomed to be complimented by strangers as the--ha--Father of the Marshalsea.", "저는 외지에서 오신 분들로부터 — 하 — 마셜시의 아버지라 칭송받는 데 익숙합니다."], ["Certainly, if years of residence may establish a claim to so--ha--honourable a title, I may accept the--hum--conferred distinction.", "분명, 여러 해의 거주가 그토록 — 하 — 명예로운 칭호에 대한 자격을 세워 줄 수 있다면, 저는 그 — 흠 — 부여된 영예를 받아들여도 좋겠지요."], ["My child, ladies and gentlemen.", "제 아이입니다, 신사 숙녀 여러분."], ["My daughter.", "제 딸입니다."], ["Born here!’", "여기서 태어났지요!\""]], [["She was not ashamed of it, or ashamed of him.", "그녀는 그것을 부끄러워하지도, 그를 부끄러워하지도 않았다."], ["She was pale and frightened; but she had no other care than to soothe him and get him away, for his own dear sake.", "그녀는 창백하고 겁에 질려 있었으나, 그를 달래어 사랑하는 그 자신을 위해 그 자리에서 데리고 나가는 것 말고는 다른 마음이 없었다."], ["She was between him and the wondering faces, turned round upon his breast with her own face raised to his.", "그녀는 그와 의아해하는 얼굴들 사이에 서서, 그의 가슴께로 돌아서 자기 얼굴을 그의 얼굴로 들어 올렸다."], ["He held her clasped in his left arm, and between whiles her low voice was heard tenderly imploring him to go away with her.", "그는 그녀를 왼팔에 꼭 끌어안았고, 사이사이 그와 함께 자리를 떠나자고 다정하게 애원하는 그녀의 나직한 목소리가 들렸다."]], [["‘Born here,’ he repeated, shedding tears.", "\"여기서 태어났습니다.\" 그가 눈물을 흘리며 되풀이했다."], ["‘Bred here.", "\"여기서 자랐지요."], ["Ladies and gentlemen, my daughter.", "신사 숙녀 여러분, 제 딸입니다."], ["Child of an unfortunate father, but--ha--always a gentleman.", "불운한 아비의 자식이지만, — 하 — 언제나 신사였던 아비의 자식이지요."], ["Poor, no doubt, but--hum--proud. Always proud.", "가난한 것은 틀림없으나, — 흠 — 자존심이 있습니다."], ["It has become a--hum--not infrequent custom for my--ha--personal admirers--personal admirers solely--to be pleased to express their desire to acknowledge my semi-official position here, by offering--ha--little tributes, which usually take the form of--ha--voluntary recognitions of my humble endeavours to--hum--to uphold a Tone here--a Tone--I beg it to be understood that I do not consider myself compromised.", "언제나 자존심이 있지요. — 흠 — 드물지 않은 관습이 되었습니다만, 제 — 하 — 개인적 흠모자들이 — 오로지 개인적 흠모자들이 — 이곳에서의 제 반(半) 공식적인 지위를 인정하고 싶다는 뜻을 기꺼이 표하시며, — 하 — 자그마한 헌사를 바치시는데, 그것은 대개 — 하 — 이곳의 품격을 — 하나의 품격을 — 지탱하려는 제 미천한 노력에 대한 자발적인 답례의 형태를 띱니다 — 제가 결코 처지를 떨어뜨렸다고 여기지 않음을 알아주시기 바랍니다."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Not compromised.", "처지를 떨어뜨린 것이 아닙니다."], ["Ha.", "하."], ["Not a beggar.", "거지가 아니에요."], ["No; I repudiate the title!", "아닙니다. 저는 그 호칭을 거부합니다!"], ["At the same time far be it from me to--hum--to put upon the fine feelings by which my partial friends are actuated, the slight of scrupling to admit that those offerings are--hum--highly acceptable.", "동시에, 제 너그러운 벗들을 움직이는 그 고운 마음씨에 대해 — 흠 — 그 헌사들이 — 흠 — 더없이 고마운 것임을 인정하기를 주저하는 무례를 범하는 일은 결단코 제게서 멀리 있습니다."], ["On the contrary, they are most acceptable.", "오히려, 그것들은 더없이 고마운 것입니다."], ["In my child’s name, if not in my own, I make the admission in the fullest manner, at the same time reserving--ha--shall I say my personal dignity?", "제 이름으로는 아니더라도 제 아이의 이름으로, 저는 그것을 더없이 충실하게 인정하는 바입니다. 동시에 — 하 — 제 개인적 존엄이라 해야 할까요?"], ["Ladies and gentlemen, God bless you all!’", "— 그것은 간직하면서 말입니다. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 모두에게 하느님의 가호가 있기를!\""]], [["By this time, the exceeding mortification undergone by the Bosom had occasioned the withdrawal of the greater part of the company into other rooms.", "이 무렵, 그 가슴께가 겪은 지나친 굴욕감 때문에 손님들 대부분은 다른 방들로 물러간 뒤였다."], ["The few who had lingered thus long followed the rest, and Little Dorrit and her father were left to the servants and themselves.", "그토록 오래 머뭇거리던 몇몇도 나머지를 따라갔고, 리틀 도릿과 그녀의 아버지는 하인들과 그들 둘만 남게 되었다."], ["Dearest and most precious to her, he would come with her now, would he not?", "그녀에게 가장 사랑스럽고 가장 소중한 그가, 이제 그녀와 함께 가 주겠지, 그렇지 않겠는가?"], ["He replied to her fervid entreaties, that he would never be able to get up the narrow stairs without Bob; where was Bob, would nobody fetch Bob?", "그는 그녀의 간절한 애원에, 밥 없이는 그 좁은 계단을 결코 올라갈 수 없을 거라고, 밥이 어디 있느냐고, 아무도 밥을 데려오지 않느냐고 대답했다."], ["Under pretence of looking for Bob, she got him out against the stream of gay company now pouring in for the evening assembly, and got him into a coach that had just set down its load, and got him home.", "밥을 찾는다는 핑계로, 그녀는 이제 저녁 모임을 위해 밀려드는 화사한 손님들의 물결을 거슬러 그를 빼냈고, 마침 손님을 막 내려놓은 마차에 그를 태워, 그를 집으로 데려갔다."]], [["The broad stairs of his Roman palace were contracted in his failing sight to the narrow stairs of his London prison; and he would suffer no one but her to touch him, his brother excepted.", "그의 로마 저택의 널따란 계단은 흐려져 가는 그의 눈에는 런던 감옥의 그 좁은 계단으로 오그라들었다. 그리고 그는 그녀와 아우 말고는 아무도 자기를 건드리지 못하게 했다."], ["They got him up to his room without help, and laid him down on his bed.", "그들은 도움 없이 그를 방으로 올려, 침대에 눕혔다."], ["And from that hour his poor maimed spirit, only remembering the place where it had broken its wings, cancelled the dream through which it had since groped, and knew of nothing beyond the Marshalsea.", "그리고 그 시각부터 그 가엾고 상처 입은 영혼은, 자기 날개가 부러진 그 장소만을 기억하며, 그 뒤로 더듬어 헤쳐 온 꿈을 지워 버리고, 마셜시 너머의 어떤 것도 알지 못했다."], ["When he heard footsteps in the street, he took them for the old weary tread in the yards.", "거리에서 발소리가 들리면, 그는 그것을 마당의 그 지치고 낡은 발소리로 여겼다."], ["When the hour came for locking up, he supposed all strangers to be excluded for the night.", "문을 잠그는 시각이 오면, 그는 외지 사람들이 모두 밤을 위해 차단된 줄로 알았다."], ["When the time for opening came again, he was so anxious to see Bob, that they were fain to patch up a narrative how that Bob--many a year dead then, gentle turnkey--had taken cold, but hoped to be out to-morrow, or the next day, or the next at furthest.", "문을 여는 시각이 다시 오면, 그는 밥을 너무도 보고 싶어 해서, 사람들은 그 밥이 — 그 무렵엔 죽은 지 여러 해 된, 그 점잖은 간수가 — 감기에 걸렸지만 내일이나 모레, 늦어도 그다음 날에는 나오기를 바라고 있다는 이야기를 짜맞춰야 했다."]], [["He fell away into a weakness so extreme that he could not raise his hand.", "그는 손조차 들지 못할 만큼 극도로 쇠약해졌다."], ["But he still protected his brother according to his long usage; and would say with some complacency, fifty times a day, when he saw him standing by his bed, ‘My good Frederick, sit down.", "그러나 그는 오랜 습관대로 여전히 아우를 보살폈고, 아우가 자기 침대 곁에 서 있는 것을 보면 하루에도 쉰 번씩 다소 흐뭇한 듯이 말하곤 했다. \"착한 프레더릭, 앉게."], ["You are very feeble indeed.’", "자네는 정말이지 아주 쇠약해.\""]], [["They tried him with Mrs General, but he had not the faintest knowledge of her.", "사람들이 제너럴 부인을 그에게 데려와 보았으나, 그는 그녀를 조금도 알아보지 못했다."], ["Some injurious suspicion lodged itself in his brain, that she wanted to supplant Mrs Bangham, and that she was given to drinking.", "그녀가 뱅엄 부인을 밀어내려 하며 술을 즐긴다는 어떤 해로운 의심이 그의 머릿속에 자리 잡았다."], ["He charged her with it in no measured terms; and was so urgent with his daughter to go round to the Marshal and entreat him to turn her out, that she was never reproduced after the first failure.", "그는 거리낌 없는 말로 그녀를 몰아세웠고, 딸에게 어찌나 다급하게 마셜 댁에 가서 그녀를 내쫓아 달라고 간청하라고 졸랐던지, 그녀는 그 첫 실패 뒤로 다시는 그 앞에 나타나지 못했다."]], [["Saving that he once asked ‘if Tip had gone outside?’ the remembrance of his two children not present seemed to have departed from him.", "그가 한 번 \"팁이 바깥에 나갔느냐?\"고 물은 것을 빼면, 곁에 없는 두 자식에 대한 기억은 그에게서 떠나 버린 듯했다."], ["But the child who had done so much for him and had been so poorly repaid, was never out of his mind.", "그러나 그를 위해 그토록 많은 일을 했으면서도 그토록 박하게 보답받은 그 아이는, 한 번도 그의 마음에서 떠난 적이 없었다."], ["Not that he spared her, or was fearful of her being spent by watching and fatigue; he was not more troubled on that score than he had usually been.", "그렇다고 그가 그녀를 아낀 것도, 그녀가 간병과 피로로 지쳐 버릴까 염려한 것도 아니었다. 그 점에 대해서는 평소보다 더 마음 쓰지 않았다."], ["No; he loved her in his old way.", "아니었다. 그는 옛 방식으로 그녀를 사랑했다."], ["They were in the jail again, and she tended him, and he had constant need of her, and could not turn without her; and he even told her, sometimes, that he was content to have undergone a great deal for her sake.", "그들은 다시 감옥에 있었고, 그녀는 그를 보살폈으며, 그는 끊임없이 그녀를 필요로 하여 그녀 없이는 몸을 돌리지도 못했다. 그는 심지어 이따금 그녀에게, 그녀를 위해 많은 것을 견뎌 온 것이 흡족하다고 말하기도 했다."], ["As to her, she bent over his bed with her quiet face against his, and would have laid down her own life to restore him.", "그녀로 말하자면, 그녀는 조용한 얼굴을 그의 얼굴에 댄 채 그의 침대 위로 몸을 숙였고, 그를 되살릴 수만 있다면 제 목숨이라도 내놓았으리라."]], [["When he had been sinking in this painless way for two or three days, she observed him to be troubled by the ticking of his watch--a pompous gold watch that made as great a to-do about its going as if nothing else went but itself and Time.", "그가 이렇게 고통 없이 가라앉기를 이삼 일 했을 무렵, 그녀는 그가 회중시계의 똑딱거리는 소리에 시달리는 것을 알아챘다 — 마치 저 자신과 시간 말고는 가는 것이 아무것도 없다는 듯이 제가 가는 데 대해 그토록 야단스러운 소리를 내는, 거창한 금시계였다."], ["She suffered it to run down; but he was still uneasy, and showed that was not what he wanted.", "그녀는 시계가 멎도록 두었으나, 그는 여전히 불안해했고, 그것이 자기가 원하는 바가 아님을 드러냈다."], ["At length he roused himself to explain that he wanted money to be raised on this watch.", "마침내 그는 정신을 추슬러, 이 시계를 잡혀 돈을 마련하기를 바란다고 설명했다."], ["He was quite pleased when she pretended to take it away for the purpose, and afterwards had a relish for his little tastes of wine and jelly, that he had not had before.", "그녀가 그러려고 시계를 가져가는 척하자 그는 무척 기뻐했고, 그 후로는 전에 없던 입맛으로 포도주와 젤리를 조금씩 즐겼다."]], [["He soon made it plain that this was so; for, in another day or two he sent off his sleeve-buttons and finger-rings.", "그가 그렇다는 것을 그는 곧 분명히 드러냈으니, 하루 이틀 더 지나자 그는 소맷부리 단추와 반지들을 내보냈던 것이다."], ["He had an amazing satisfaction in entrusting her with these errands, and appeared to consider it equivalent to making the most methodical and provident arrangements.", "그는 그녀에게 이런 심부름을 맡기는 데서 놀라운 만족을 느꼈고, 그것을 더없이 빈틈없고 신중한 채비를 하는 것과 마찬가지로 여기는 듯했다."], ["After his trinkets, or such of them as he had been able to see about him, were gone, his clothes engaged his attention; and it is as likely as not that he was kept alive for some days by the satisfaction of sending them, piece by piece, to an imaginary pawnbroker’s.", "그의 패물들이, 혹은 그가 주위에서 챙길 수 있었던 것들이 다 나가자, 그의 옷가지가 그의 관심을 끌었다. 그리고 그가 며칠을 더 살아 있었던 것은, 그 옷가지를 한 점씩 상상 속의 전당포로 보내는 만족감 덕분이었을 공산이 크다."]], [["Thus for ten days Little Dorrit bent over his pillow, laying her cheek against his.", "이렇게 열흘 동안 리틀 도릿은 그의 베개 위로 몸을 숙인 채, 자기 뺨을 그의 뺨에 댔다."], ["Sometimes she was so worn out that for a few minutes they would slumber together.", "때로는 그녀가 너무도 지친 나머지 몇 분간 둘이 함께 잠들기도 했다."], ["Then she would awake; to recollect with fast-flowing silent tears what it was that touched her face, and to see, stealing over the cherished face upon the pillow, a deeper shadow than the shadow of the Marshalsea Wall.", "그러다 그녀는 깨어나, 자기 얼굴에 닿는 것이 무엇인지를 소리 없이 줄줄 흐르는 눈물과 함께 떠올리고, 베개 위 그 사랑하는 얼굴 위로, 마셜시 담장의 그림자보다 더 짙은 그림자가 살며시 번져 가는 것을 보곤 했다."]], [["Quietly, quietly, all the lines of the plan of the great Castle melted one after another.", "조용히, 조용히, 그 거대한 누각의 설계도를 이루는 모든 선들이 하나씩 하나씩 녹아 사라졌다."], ["Quietly, quietly, the ruled and cross-ruled countenance on which they were traced, became fair and blank.", "조용히, 조용히, 그 선들이 그어졌던, 줄과 엇줄이 가득했던 그 얼굴이 맑고 텅 비어 갔다."], ["Quietly, quietly, the reflected marks of the prison bars and of the zig-zag iron on the wall-top, faded away.", "조용히, 조용히, 감옥 창살과 담장 꼭대기의 지그재그 철책이 비쳐 새겨졌던 자국들이 흐려져 갔다."], ["Quietly, quietly, the face subsided into a far younger likeness of her own than she had ever seen under the grey hair, and sank to rest.", "조용히, 조용히, 그 얼굴은 그녀가 그 백발 아래에서 여태 본 적 없는, 그녀 자신을 훨씬 더 젊게 닮은 모습으로 가라앉았고, 영원한 안식에 잠겼다."]], [["At first her uncle was stark distracted.", "처음에 그녀의 작은아버지는 완전히 넋을 잃었다."], ["‘O my brother!", "\"오 내 형님!"], ["O William, William!", "오 윌리엄, 윌리엄!"], ["You to go before me; you to go alone; you to go, and I to remain!", "형님이 나보다 먼저 가다니, 형님 혼자 가다니, 형님이 가고 내가 남다니!"], ["You, so far superior, so distinguished, so noble; I, a poor useless creature fit for nothing, and whom no one would have missed!’", "그토록 뛰어나고, 그토록 훌륭하고, 그토록 고귀하던 형님이, 아무짝에도 쓸모없고 아무도 아쉬워하지 않았을 가엾고 무용한 인간인 내가 남다니!\""]], [["It did her, for the time, the good of having him to think of and to succour.", "그것은 한동안, 그가 마음 쓰고 보살필 대상을 가지게 해 주는 좋은 일을 그녀에게 해 주었다."]], [["‘Uncle, dear uncle, spare yourself, spare me!’", "\"작은아버지, 사랑하는 작은아버지, 부디 몸을 아끼세요, 저를 아껴 주세요!\""]], [["The old man was not deaf to the last words.", "노인은 그 마지막 말에 귀를 닫지 않았다."], ["When he did begin to restrain himself, it was that he might spare her.", "그가 스스로를 억누르기 시작한 것은, 그녀를 아끼기 위해서였다."], ["He had no care for himself; but, with all the remaining power of the honest heart, stunned so long and now awaking to be broken, he honoured and blessed her.", "그는 자기 자신은 돌보지 않았다. 다만 그토록 오래 멍하니 잠들어 있다가 이제 깨어나 부서지려 하는 그 정직한 마음의 남은 온 힘을 다해, 그는 그녀를 떠받들고 축복했다."]], [["‘O God,’ he cried, before they left the room, with his wrinkled hands clasped over her.", "\"오 하느님.\" 방을 나서기 전에 그가 주름진 두 손을 그녀 위로 맞잡고 외쳤다."], ["‘Thou seest this daughter of my dear dead brother!", "\"당신께서는 제 사랑하는 죽은 형님의 이 딸을 보고 계십니다!"], ["All that I have looked upon, with my half-blind and sinful eyes, Thou hast discerned clearly, brightly.", "제가 반쯤 멀고 죄 많은 눈으로 바라본 모든 것을, 당신께서는 또렷이, 환하게 분별하셨습니다."], ["Not a hair of her head shall be harmed before Thee.", "당신 앞에서 이 아이의 머리카락 한 올도 상하지 않을 것입니다."], ["Thou wilt uphold her here to her last hour.", "당신께서 이 아이를 그 마지막 시각까지 이곳에서 떠받쳐 주실 것입니다."], ["And I know Thou wilt reward her hereafter!’", "그리고 저는 압니다, 당신께서 이 아이를 이후에 보상해 주시리라는 것을!\""]], [["They remained in a dim room near, until it was almost midnight, quiet and sad together.", "그들은 거의 자정이 될 때까지, 가까운 어느 어둑한 방에 함께 조용하고 슬프게 머물렀다."], ["At times his grief would seek relief in a burst like that in which it had found its earliest expression; but, besides that his little strength would soon have been unequal to such strains, he never failed to recall her words, and to reproach himself and calm himself.", "때로 그의 슬픔은 처음 터져 나왔던 것과 같은 폭발 속에서 위안을 찾으려 했다. 그러나 그의 작은 기력으로는 그런 격정을 곧 감당할 수 없었을 뿐만 아니라, 그는 어김없이 그녀의 말을 떠올리고는 스스로를 책망하며 진정시켰다."], ["The only utterance with which he indulged his sorrow, was the frequent exclamation that his brother was gone, alone; that they had been together in the outset of their lives, that they had fallen into misfortune together, that they had kept together through their many years of poverty, that they had remained together to that day; and that his brother was gone alone, alone!", "그가 자신의 슬픔에 빠져드는 유일한 발설은, 형님이 혼자 가셨다는, 둘이 인생의 출발점에서 함께였고, 함께 불운에 빠졌으며, 그 숱한 가난의 세월 동안 함께 견뎠고, 그날까지 함께 남아 있었건만, 형님이 혼자, 혼자 가셨다는 잦은 탄식뿐이었다."]], [["They parted, heavy and sorrowful.", "그들은 무겁고 슬프게 헤어졌다."], ["She would not consent to leave him anywhere but in his own room, and she saw him lie down in his clothes upon his bed, and covered him with her own hands.", "그녀는 그를 그 자신의 방 말고는 어디에도 두려 하지 않았고, 그가 옷을 입은 채 침대에 눕는 것을 지켜보며 제 손으로 그를 덮어 주었다."], ["Then she sank upon her own bed, and fell into a deep sleep: the sleep of exhaustion and rest, though not of complete release from a pervading consciousness of affliction.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 자기 침대에 몸을 던지고는 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다. 비탄이 온통 배어든 의식에서 완전히 풀려난 것은 아니었으나, 지칠 대로 지쳐 쉬는 잠이었다."], ["Sleep, good Little Dorrit.", "잠들거라, 착한 리틀 도릿."], ["Sleep through the night!", "밤새 잠들거라!"]], [["It was a moonlight night; but the moon rose late, being long past the full.", "달이 뜬 밤이었다. 그러나 보름이 한참 지난 터라 달은 늦게 떠올랐다."], ["When it was high in the peaceful firmament, it shone through half-closed lattice blinds into the solemn room where the stumblings and wanderings of a life had so lately ended.", "달이 평온한 창공에 높이 떴을 때, 그것은 반쯤 닫힌 격자 덧문 사이로, 한 생애의 비틀거림과 방황이 그토록 얼마 전에 끝난 그 엄숙한 방 안으로 비쳐 들었다."], ["Two quiet figures were within the room; two figures, equally still and impassive, equally removed by an untraversable distance from the teeming earth and all that it contains, though soon to lie in it.", "방 안에는 두 고요한 모습이 있었다. 똑같이 미동도 없고 무심한, 곧 그 속에 누우게 될 터이건만 들끓는 이 대지와 그 안의 모든 것으로부터 똑같이 건널 수 없는 거리만큼 떨어진 두 모습이었다."]], [["One figure reposed upon the bed.", "한 모습은 침대 위에 누워 있었다."], ["The other, kneeling on the floor, drooped over it; the arms easily and peacefully resting on the coverlet; the face bowed down, so that the lips touched the hand over which with its last breath it had bent.", "다른 모습은 마룻바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그 위로 수그러져, 두 팔은 편안하고 평화롭게 침대보 위에 얹혀 있었고, 얼굴은 깊이 숙여, 마지막 숨과 함께 그 위로 굽혔던 그 손에 입술이 닿아 있었다."], ["The two brothers were before their Father; far beyond the twilight judgment of this world; high above its mists and obscurities.", "두 형제는 그들의 아버지 앞에 있었다. 이 세상의 어스름한 심판을 아득히 넘어선 곳에, 그 안개와 어둠을 까마득히 위에서 굽어보는 곳에."]], [["# CHAPTER 20. Introduces the next", "# CHAPTER 20. Introduces the next"]], [["The passengers were landing from the packet on the pier at Calais.", "승객들이 칼레 부두에서 우편선을 내리고 있었다."], ["A low-lying place and a low-spirited place Calais was, with the tide ebbing out towards low water-mark.", "칼레는 낮게 가라앉은 곳, 그리고 가라앉은 기분의 곳이었으니, 조수가 저조선을 향해 빠져나가는 중이었다."], ["There had been no more water on the bar than had sufficed to float the packet in; and now the bar itself, with a shallow break of sea over it, looked like a lazy marine monster just risen to the surface, whose form was indistinctly shown as it lay asleep.", "모래톱 위에는 우편선을 띄워 들이기에 겨우 족할 만큼의 물밖에 없었고, 이제 그 모래톱 자체가 그 위로 얕은 파도가 부서지는 채, 막 수면 위로 떠오른 게으른 바다 괴물처럼, 잠든 채 누워 그 형체를 흐릿하게 드러내고 있었다."], ["The meagre lighthouse all in white, haunting the seaboard as if it were the ghost of an edifice that had once had colour and rotundity, dropped melancholy tears after its late buffeting by the waves.", "온통 하얀 그 빈약한 등대는, 한때 색채와 풍만함을 지녔던 어느 건물의 유령이기라도 한 듯 바닷가를 떠돌며, 방금 파도에 두들겨 맞고서 우울한 눈물을 떨구고 있었다."], ["The long rows of gaunt black piles, slimy and wet and weather-worn, with funeral garlands of seaweed twisted about them by the late tide, might have represented an unsightly marine cemetery.", "끈적거리고 축축하며 비바람에 닳은, 길게 늘어선 앙상한 검은 말뚝들은, 방금 지나간 조수가 그 둘레에 해초의 장례 화환을 비비 꼬아 두른 채로, 보기 흉한 바다의 묘지를 나타내는 것이라 해도 좋았을 것이다."], ["Every wave-dashed, storm-beaten object, was so low and so little, under the broad grey sky, in the noise of the wind and sea, and before the curling lines of surf, making at it ferociously, that the wonder was there was any Calais left, and that its low gates and low wall and low roofs and low ditches and low sand-hills and low ramparts and flat streets, had not yielded long ago to the undermining and besieging sea, like the fortifications children make on the sea-shore.", "파도에 부딪히고 폭풍에 두들겨 맞은 모든 것이, 그 넓은 잿빛 하늘 아래, 바람과 바다의 소음 속에서, 그리고 사납게 달려드는 굽이치는 너울의 선들 앞에서 어찌나 낮고 어찌나 작던지, 도리어 칼레가 조금이라도 남아 있다는 것이, 그리고 그 낮은 성문과 낮은 성벽과 낮은 지붕과 낮은 도랑과 낮은 모래언덕과 낮은 성루와 평평한 거리들이, 마치 아이들이 바닷가에 쌓는 요새처럼, 침식하고 포위해 오는 바다에 진작 굴복해 버리지 않았다는 것이 놀라울 지경이었다."]], [["After slipping among oozy piles and planks, stumbling up wet steps and encountering many salt difficulties, the passengers entered on their comfortless peregrination along the pier; where all the French vagabonds and English outlaws in the town (half the population) attended to prevent their recovery from bewilderment.", "미끈거리는 말뚝과 널빤지 사이를 미끄러지듯 지나고, 젖은 계단을 비틀거리며 오르고, 짠내 나는 갖은 곤경과 맞닥뜨린 끝에, 승객들은 부두를 따라 이어지는 그 안락함이라곤 없는 행로에 들어섰다. 그곳에는 이 도시의 프랑스인 부랑자와 영국인 무법자들이(주민의 절반이었다) 죄다 나와, 그들이 어리둥절한 상태에서 헤어나지 못하도록 막아섰다."], ["After being minutely inspected by all the English, and claimed and reclaimed and counter-claimed as prizes by all the French in a hand-to-hand scuffle three quarters of a mile long, they were at last free to enter the streets, and to make off in their various directions, hotly pursued.", "모든 영국인에게 샅샅이 살펴지고, 길이가 사분의 삼 마일에 이르는 육박전 속에서 모든 프랑스인에게 전리품으로 주장되고 또 주장되고 맞주장되고 난 끝에, 그들은 마침내 거리로 들어서서 저마다의 방향으로, 맹렬한 추격을 받으며 빠져나갈 수 있게 되었다."]], [["Clennam, harassed by more anxieties than one, was among this devoted band.", "한 가지가 아닌 여러 근심에 시달리던 클레넘도 이 가엾은 무리에 끼여 있었다."], ["Having rescued the most defenceless of his compatriots from situations of great extremity, he now went his way alone, or as nearly alone as he could be, with a native gentleman in a suit of grease and a cap of the same material, giving chase at a distance of some fifty yards, and continually calling after him, ‘Hi!", "동포들 가운데 가장 무방비한 자들을 큰 곤경에서 구해 낸 뒤, 그는 이제 홀로, 아니 홀로일 수 있는 한 거의 홀로 길을 갔는데, 기름때 묻은 양복에 같은 재질의 모자를 쓴 토박이 신사 하나가 오십 야드쯤 거리를 두고 뒤쫓으며 끊임없이 그를 향해 외쳐 댔다. \"이!"], ["Ice-say!", "아이세이!"], ["You!", "당신!"], ["Seer!", "시어!"], ["Ice-say!", "아이세이!"], ["Nice Oatel!’", "좋은 오텔!\""]], [["Even this hospitable person, however, was left behind at last, and Clennam pursued his way, unmolested.", "그러나 이 친절한 자조차도 마침내 뒤처졌고, 클레넘은 방해받지 않고 제 갈 길을 갔다."], ["There was a tranquil air in the town after the turbulence of the Channel and the beach, and its dulness in that comparison was agreeable.", "해협과 바닷가의 소란이 지나간 뒤라 도시에는 고요한 기운이 감돌았고, 그에 견주어 보면 그 따분함마저 도리어 반가웠다."], ["He met new groups of his countrymen, who had all a straggling air of having at one time overblown themselves, like certain uncomfortable kinds of flowers, and of being now mere weeds.", "그는 동포들의 새로운 무리들과 마주쳤는데, 그들은 하나같이 한때 어떤 불편한 종류의 꽃들처럼 제 멋에 활짝 피어 버렸다가 이제는 한낱 잡초가 되어 버린 듯한, 어딘가 산만한 분위기를 풍기고 있었다."], ["They had all an air, too, of lounging out a limited round, day after day, which strongly reminded him of the Marshalsea.", "그들은 또한 하나같이 날마다 한정된 동선 안을 어슬렁거리며 빈둥대는 분위기를 풍겼는데, 그것이 그에게 마셜시를 강하게 떠올리게 했다."], ["But, taking no further note of them than was sufficient to give birth to the reflection, he sought out a certain street and number which he kept in his mind.", "그러나 그는 그 생각이 떠오를 만큼만 그들에게 주의를 두고는 더 이상 마음에 두지 않은 채, 머릿속에 새겨 두었던 어느 거리와 번지를 찾아 나섰다."]], [["‘So Pancks said,’ he murmured to himself, as he stopped before a dull house answering to the address.", "\"팽크스가 그렇게 말했으니,\" 그는 그 주소에 들어맞는 칙칙한 집 앞에서 멈춰 서며 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["‘I suppose his information to be correct and his discovery, among Mr Casby’s loose papers, indisputable; but, without it, I should hardly have supposed this to be a likely place.’", "\"그가 알아낸 정보가 정확하고, 캐스비 씨의 어수선한 서류들 틈에서 그가 찾아낸 것이 의심의 여지가 없으리라 짐작은 하네만, 그게 없었더라면 나로서는 여기가 그럴 법한 곳이라고는 좀처럼 생각지 못했을 게야.\""]], [["A dead sort of house, with a dead wall over the way and a dead gateway at the side, where a pendant bell-handle produced two dead tinkles, and a knocker produced a dead, flat, surface-tapping, that seemed not to have depth enough in it to penetrate even the cracked door.", "생기 없는 종류의 집이었으니, 길 건너로는 생기 없는 담이 있고 옆으로는 생기 없는 대문이 있어, 거기 늘어진 종 손잡이를 잡아당기자 생기 없는 딸랑 소리가 두 번 났고, 문고리는 갈라진 문조차 뚫고 들어갈 만한 깊이가 없는 듯 생기 없고 밋밋한, 표면만 두드리는 소리를 냈다."], ["However, the door jarred open on a dead sort of spring; and he closed it behind him as he entered a dull yard, soon brought to a close by another dead wall, where an attempt had been made to train some creeping shrubs, which were dead; and to make a little fountain in a grotto, which was dry; and to decorate that with a little statue, which was gone.", "그래도 문은 생기 없는 종류의 용수철에 삐걱이며 열렸고, 그는 들어서며 등 뒤로 문을 닫고는 칙칙한 안마당으로 들어섰는데, 그 마당은 또 하나의 생기 없는 담에 이내 가로막혀 끝났다. 그곳에는 무언가 기어오르는 관목을 길러 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 죽어 있었고, 동굴에 작은 분수를 만들어 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 말라 있었으며, 그 분수를 작은 조상으로 꾸며 보려 한 흔적이 있었으나 그것은 사라지고 없었다."]], [["The entry to the house was on the left, and it was garnished as the outer gateway was, with two printed bills in French and English, announcing Furnished Apartments to let, with immediate possession.", "집으로 드는 입구는 왼편에 있었고, 바깥 대문이 그러했듯 프랑스어와 영어로 된 인쇄 광고지 두 장이 붙어 가구 딸린 셋방을 즉시 입주 가능 조건으로 내놓는다고 알리고 있었다."], ["A strong cheerful peasant woman, all stocking, petticoat, white cap, and ear-ring, stood here in a dark doorway, and said with a pleasant show of teeth, ‘Ice-say!", "온통 양말과 페티코트와 흰 모자와 귀고리뿐인, 기운차고 명랑한 시골 아낙 하나가 어두운 문간에 서서 보기 좋게 이를 드러내며 말했다. \"아이세이!"], ["Seer!", "시어!"], ["Who?’", "누구를?\""]], [["Clennam, replying in French, said the English lady; he wished to see the English lady.", "클레넘은 프랑스어로 답하여 영국 부인이라고 했다. 그는 그 영국 부인을 만나고 싶다고 했다."], ["‘Enter then and ascend, if you please,’ returned the peasant woman, in French likewise.", "\"그러면 들어와서, 괜찮으시다면 올라오세요.\" 아낙도 마찬가지로 프랑스어로 대꾸했다."], ["He did both, and followed her up a dark bare staircase to a back room on the first-floor.", "그는 들어가 올라갔고, 어둡고 휑한 계단을 따라 그녀를 좇아 이층 뒤편의 한 방으로 갔다."], ["Hence, there was a gloomy view of the yard that was dull, and of the shrubs that were dead, and of the fountain that was dry, and of the pedestal of the statue that was gone.", "거기서는 칙칙한 마당과, 죽은 관목과, 마른 분수와, 사라지고 없는 조상의 받침대가 음울하게 내려다보였다."]], [["‘Monsieur Blandois,’ said Clennam.", "\"블랑두아 씨를.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘With pleasure, Monsieur.’", "\"기꺼이요, 무슈.\""]], [["Thereupon the woman withdrew and left him to look at the room.", "그러고서 아낙은 물러가 그가 방을 둘러보도록 내버려 두었다."], ["It was the pattern of room always to be found in such a house.", "그것은 그런 집에서 늘 보게 되는 본보기 같은 방이었다."], ["Cool, dull, and dark.", "서늘하고, 칙칙하고, 어두웠다."], ["Waxed floor very slippery.", "밀랍 칠을 한 바닥은 몹시 미끄러웠다."], ["A room not large enough to skate in; nor adapted to the easy pursuit of any other occupation.", "스케이트를 탈 만큼 크지도 않았고, 다른 어떤 소일거리를 편히 즐기기에도 알맞지 않았다."], ["Red and white curtained windows, little straw mat, little round table with a tumultuous assemblage of legs underneath, clumsy rush-bottomed chairs, two great red velvet arm-chairs affording plenty of space to be uncomfortable in, bureau, chimney-glass in several pieces pretending to be in one piece, pair of gaudy vases of very artificial flowers; between them a Greek warrior with his helmet off, sacrificing a clock to the Genius of France.", "빨강과 흰색 커튼을 친 창들, 작은 짚 매트, 아래로는 다리들이 어수선하게 모여 있는 작은 둥근 탁자, 투박한 골풀 방석의 의자들, 불편하게 앉아 있을 공간만은 넉넉히 내어 주는 커다란 빨간 벨벳 안락의자 둘, 책상 하나, 여러 조각이면서 한 장인 척하는 벽난로 거울, 지극히 조화로 만든 꽃을 꽂은 요란한 꽃병 한 쌍, 그 사이로는 투구를 벗은 그리스 전사가 프랑스의 수호신에게 시계를 제물로 바치고 있었다."]], [["After some pause, a door of communication with another room was opened, and a lady entered.", "얼마쯤 뜸을 들인 뒤, 다른 방으로 통하는 문이 열리고 한 부인이 들어왔다."], ["She manifested great surprise on seeing Clennam, and her glance went round the room in search of some one else.", "그녀는 클레넘을 보고 크게 놀란 기색을 드러내며, 또 다른 누군가를 찾는 듯 방 안을 둘러보았다."]], [["‘Pardon me, Miss Wade.", "\"용서하십시오, 웨이드 양."], ["I am alone.’", "저 혼자입니다.\""]], [["‘It was not your name that was brought to me.’", "\"제게 전해진 것은 당신 이름이 아니었는데요.\""]], [["‘No; I know that.", "\"네, 알고 있습니다."], ["Excuse me.", "죄송합니다."], ["I have already had experience that my name does not predispose you to an interview; and I ventured to mention the name of one I am in search of.’", "제 이름으로는 당신이 면담에 응할 마음이 들지 않으리란 것을 이미 겪어 보았기에, 제가 찾는 어떤 사람의 이름을 감히 댔습니다.\""]], [["‘Pray,’ she returned, motioning him to a chair so coldly that he remained standing, ‘what name was it that you gave?’", "\"그래서,\" 그녀가 어찌나 차갑게 의자를 권하던지 그가 도리어 선 채로 있게 만들며 말했다. \"당신이 댄 이름이 무엇이었나요?\""]], [["‘I mentioned the name of Blandois.’", "\"블랑두아라는 이름을 댔습니다.\""]], [["‘Blandois?’", "\"블랑두아라고요?\""]], [["‘A name you are acquainted with.’", "\"당신이 아는 이름이지요.\""]], [["‘It is strange,’ she said, frowning, ‘that you should still press an undesired interest in me and my acquaintances, in me and my affairs, Mr Clennam.", "\"이상하군요,\" 그녀가 이맛살을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["I don’t know what you mean.’", "\"당신은 여전히 저에게, 저의 지인들에게, 저와 저의 일에, 청하지도 않은 관심을 들이대시는군요, 클레넘 씨. 무슨 말씀인지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["‘Pardon me.", "\"용서하십시오."], ["You know the name?’", "그 이름을 아시지요?\""]], [["‘What can you have to do with the name?", "\"당신이 그 이름과 무슨 상관이 있나요?"], ["What can I have to do with the name?", "제가 그 이름과 무슨 상관이 있고요?"], ["What can you have to do with my knowing or not knowing any name?", "제가 어떤 이름을 알든 모르든 당신이 무슨 상관이 있나요?"], ["I know many names and I have forgotten many more.", "저는 숱한 이름을 알고, 그보다 더 많은 이름을 잊었어요."], ["This may be in the one class, or it may be in the other, or I may never have heard it.", "그 이름은 아는 쪽일 수도, 잊은 쪽일 수도, 아예 들어 본 적 없는 것일 수도 있지요."], ["I am acquainted with no reason for examining myself, or for being examined, about it.’", "저는 그것에 관하여 제 자신을 따져 볼 까닭도, 따짐을 당할 까닭도 알지 못해요.\""]], [["‘If you will allow me,’ said Clennam, ‘I will tell you my reason for pressing the subject.", "\"허락하신다면,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["I admit that I do press it, and I must beg you to forgive me if I do so, very earnestly.", "\"이 일을 거듭 여쭙는 제 까닭을 말씀드리겠습니다. 제가 거듭 여쭙고 있음은 인정하며, 그러는 것을 부디 너그러이 봐 주시길 진심으로 간청합니다."], ["The reason is all mine, I do not insinuate that it is in any way yours.’", "그 까닭은 온전히 제 것이지, 결코 당신의 것이라고 넌지시 비치는 게 아닙니다.\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ she returned, repeating a little less haughtily than before her former invitation to him to be seated: to which he now deferred, as she seated herself.", "\"좋아요, 선생님.\" 그녀가 아까보다는 조금 덜 거만하게, 앉으라던 앞서의 권유를 되풀이하며 대꾸했다."], ["‘I am at least glad to know that this is not another bondswoman of some friend of yours, who is bereft of free choice, and whom I have spirited away.", "그러더니 그녀가 자리에 앉자 이번에는 그도 따랐다. \"적어도 이것이, 자유로운 선택권을 빼앗긴 채 제가 빼돌렸다는 당신 친구의 또 다른 종속녀가 아니라는 걸 알게 되어 다행이군요."], ["I will hear your reason, if you please.’", "괜찮으시다면 당신 까닭을 들어 보지요.\""]], [["‘First, to identify the person of whom we speak,’ said Clennam, ‘let me observe that it is the person you met in London some time back.", "\"먼저, 우리가 말하는 자가 누구인지 확실히 하기 위해,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["You will remember meeting him near the river--in the Adelphi!’", "\"그가 얼마 전 당신이 런던에서 만난 사람임을 일러 두겠습니다. 강 근처에서 그를 만나셨던 일을 기억하실 겁니다—아델파이에서요!\""]], [["‘You mix yourself most unaccountably with my business,’ she replied, looking full at him with stern displeasure.", "\"당신은 도무지 까닭 모르게 제 일에 끼어드는군요.\" 그녀가 엄한 불쾌함을 띤 채 그를 똑바로 바라보며 대꾸했다."], ["‘How do you know that?’", "\"그걸 어떻게 아시지요?\""]], [["‘I entreat you not to take it ill.", "\"부디 언짢게 여기지 마십시오."], ["By mere accident.’", "순전히 우연이었습니다.\""]], [["‘What accident?’", "\"무슨 우연이요?\""]], [["‘Solely the accident of coming upon you in the street and seeing the meeting.’", "\"오로지 거리에서 당신과 마주쳐 그 만남을 보게 된 우연일 뿐입니다.\""]], [["‘Do you speak of yourself, or of some one else?’\n‘Of myself. I saw it.’", "\"당신 자신을 두고 하는 말인가요, 아니면 다른 누군가를 두고 하는 말인가요?\""]], [["‘To be sure it was in the open street,’ she observed, after a few moments of less and less angry reflection.", "\"제 자신입니다. 제가 보았습니다.\""], ["‘Fifty people might have seen it.", "\"하긴 그건 트인 거리에서였지요.\" 점점 누그러지는 노여움 속에 잠깐 생각하더니 그녀가 말했다."], ["It would have signified nothing if they had.’", "\"오십 명쯤이 그걸 보았을 수도 있겠네요. 보았다 한들 아무 의미도 없었을 테고요.\""]], [["‘Nor do I make my having seen it of any moment, nor (otherwise than as an explanation of my coming here) do I connect my visit with it or the favour that I have to ask.’", "\"저 또한 그걸 본 일을 무슨 대수로 삼지 않으며, (제가 여기 온 데 대한 해명이 아니고서는) 제 방문을 그 일이나 제가 청하려는 부탁과 결부시키지도 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["You have to ask a favour!", "부탁이 있으시군요!"], ["It occurred to me,’ and the handsome face looked bitterly at him, ‘that your manner was softened, Mr Clennam.’", "클레넘 씨, 당신 태도가 부드러워졌다 싶었지요.\" 그 아름다운 얼굴이 씁쓸하게 그를 쳐다보며 말했다."]], [["He was content to protest against this by a slight action without contesting it in words.", "그는 말로 다투지는 않은 채, 가벼운 몸짓으로 그것에 항의하는 데 그쳤다."], ["He then referred to Blandois’ disappearance, of which it was probable she had heard?", "그러고 나서 그는 블랑두아의 실종을 언급하며, 아마 그녀도 들었으리라고 했다."], ["However probable it was to him, she had heard of no such thing.", "그에게는 아무리 그럴듯해 보여도, 그녀는 그런 일을 들은 바 없었다."], ["Let him look round him (she said) and judge for himself what general intelligence was likely to reach the ears of a woman who had been shut up there while it was rife, devouring her own heart.", "둘러보고서(라고 그녀는 말했다) 스스로 판단해 보라고, 그 소문이 자자할 적에 이곳에 갇혀 제 마음을 갉아먹고 있던 여자의 귀에 어떤 세상 소식이 닿을 법했겠느냐고 했다."], ["When she had uttered this denial, which he believed to be true, she asked him what he meant by disappearance?", "그가 사실이라 믿은 이 부인의 말을 마치고 나서, 그녀는 실종이란 무슨 뜻이냐고 그에게 물었다."], ["That led to his narrating the circumstances in detail, and expressing something of his anxiety to discover what had really become of the man, and to repel the dark suspicions that clouded about his mother’s house.", "그 때문에 그는 정황을 소상히 이야기하게 되었고, 그자가 정말로 어찌 되었는지를 알아내어 어머니의 집을 에워싼 어두운 의혹을 떨쳐 내고 싶은 자신의 근심을 어느 정도 드러냈다."], ["She heard him with evident surprise, and with more marks of suppressed interest than he had seen in her; still they did not overcome her distant, proud, and self-secluded manner.", "그녀는 역력한 놀라움을 띠고, 그가 그녀에게서 본 적 있는 것보다 더 많은 억눌린 관심의 기색을 띤 채 그의 말을 들었다. 그래도 그것들이 그녀의 거리감 있고 도도하며 스스로를 가두는 태도를 이겨 내지는 못했다."], ["When he had finished, she said nothing but these words:", "그가 말을 마치자 그녀는 이 말밖에 하지 않았다."]], [["‘You have not yet told me, sir, what I have to do with it, or what the favour is?", "\"선생님, 아직 그게 저와 무슨 상관인지, 그 부탁이 무엇인지는 말씀하지 않으셨는데요."], ["Will you be so good as come to that?’", "그 얘기로 좀 넘어가 주시겠어요?\""]], [["‘I assume,’ said Arthur, persevering, in his endeavour to soften her scornful demeanour, ‘that being in communication--may I say, confidential communication?--with this person--’", "\"제 짐작입니다만,\" 그녀의 경멸하는 태도를 누그러뜨리려 애쓰며 아서가 끈질기게 말했다. \"이 사람과 연락을—말하자면, 은밀한 연락을—주고받는 사이이시니—\""]], [["‘You may say, of course, whatever you like,’ she remarked; ‘but I do not subscribe to your assumptions, Mr Clennam, or to any one’s.’", "\"물론 당신이야 무슨 말이든 마음대로 하시면 되지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 클레넘 씨, 저는 당신의 짐작에도, 그 누구의 짐작에도 동의하지 않아요.\""]], [["‘--that being, at least in personal communication with him,’ said Clennam, changing the form of his position in the hope of making it unobjectionable, ‘you can tell me something of his antecedents, pursuits, habits, usual place of residence.", "\"—적어도 그와 직접 연락을 주고받는 사이이시니,\" 클레넘은 그 표현이 거슬리지 않게 되기를 바라며 말투를 바꾸어 말했다."], ["Can give me some little clue by which to seek him out in the likeliest manner, and either produce him, or establish what has become of him.", "\"그자의 내력, 일거리, 습관, 평소 거처에 관해 제게 무언가 일러 주실 수 있을 겁니다. 가장 가망 있는 방식으로 그를 찾아낼 작은 실마리라도 주시어, 그를 데려오게 하시든가, 그가 어찌 되었는지를 밝혀 주실 수 있을 겁니다."], ["This is the favour I ask, and I ask it in a distress of mind for which I hope you will feel some consideration.", "이것이 제가 청하는 부탁이며, 부디 당신이 좀 헤아려 주시기를 바라는 마음의 괴로움 속에서 청합니다."], ["If you should have any reason for imposing conditions upon me, I will respect it without asking what it is.’", "제게 어떤 조건을 다실 까닭이 있으시다면, 그것이 무엇인지 묻지 않고 존중하겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You chanced to see me in the street with the man,’ she observed, after being, to his mortification, evidently more occupied with her own reflections on the matter than with his appeal.", "\"당신은 우연히 거리에서 저와 그자를 함께 보았다는 거지요.\" 그가 분하게도, 그녀는 그의 호소보다 그 일에 대한 자기 생각에 더 몰두한 게 분명한 채로 말했다."], ["‘Then you knew the man before?’", "\"그렇다면 그 전부터 그자를 알았던 건가요?\""]], [["‘Not before; afterwards.", "\"전부터는 아니고, 그 뒤에요."], ["I never saw him before, but I saw him again on this very night of his disappearance.", "그를 전에 본 적은 없습니다만, 바로 그가 실종된 그날 밤에 다시 보았습니다."], ["In my mother’s room, in fact.", "사실은 어머니의 방에서요."], ["I left him there.", "거기 그를 두고 나왔습니다."], ["You will read in this paper all that is known of him.’", "그에 관해 알려진 전부를 이 종이에서 읽으실 수 있습니다.\""]], [["He handed her one of the printed bills, which she read with a steady and attentive face.", "그가 인쇄된 광고지 한 장을 그녀에게 건네자, 그녀는 차분하고 주의 깊은 얼굴로 그것을 읽었다."]], [["‘This is more than _I_ knew of him,’ she said, giving it back.", "\"이건 저도 그자에 대해 알던 것보다 더한 내용이네요.\" 그녀가 그것을 돌려주며 말했다."], ["Clennam’s looks expressed his heavy disappointment, perhaps his incredulity; for she added in the same unsympathetic tone: ‘You don’t believe it.", "클레넘의 표정에는 깊은 실망이, 어쩌면 불신이 어렸다. 그러자 그녀가 똑같이 냉담한 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["Still, it is so.", "\"당신은 믿지 않으시는군요."], ["As to personal communication: it seems that there was personal communication between him and your mother.", "그래도 사실이에요. 직접 연락에 관해서라면, 그자와 당신 어머니 사이에는 직접 연락이 있었던 모양이지요."], ["And yet you say you believe _her_ declaration that she knows no more of him!’", "그런데도 당신은, 그자에 대해 더 아는 게 없다는 어머니의 말은 믿는다고 하시는군요!\""]], [["A sufficiently expressive hint of suspicion was conveyed in these words, and in the smile by which they were accompanied, to bring the blood into Clennam’s cheeks.", "이 말 속에, 그리고 그 말에 곁들여진 미소 속에는, 클레넘의 두 뺨에 피가 오르게 하기에 충분할 만큼 의혹의 암시가 또렷이 담겨 있었다."]], [["‘Come, sir,’ she said, with a cruel pleasure in repeating the stab, ‘I will be as open with you as you can desire.", "\"자, 선생님.\" 그녀가 그 일격을 되풀이하는 데서 잔인한 즐거움을 느끼며 말했다."], ["I will confess that if I cared for my credit (which I do not), or had a good name to preserve (which I have not, for I am utterly indifferent to its being considered good or bad), I should regard myself as heavily compromised by having had anything to do with this fellow.", "\"당신이 바라는 만큼 제가 솔직해지지요. 만일 제가 제 평판을 아낀다면(저는 아끼지 않습니다만), 또는 지킬 만한 좋은 이름이 제게 있다면(없습니다, 저는 그것이 좋게 여겨지든 나쁘게 여겨지든 전혀 개의치 않으니까요), 저는 그런 자와 무슨 관계든 가졌다는 것만으로도 제가 심하게 누를 끼친 셈이라 여길 거라고 고백하지요."], ["Yet he never passed in at _my_ door--never sat in colloquy with _me_ until midnight.’", "하지만 그자는 제 문으로 들어온 적도 없고, 한밤중까지 저와 마주 앉아 이야기를 나눈 적도 없어요.\""]], [["She took her revenge for her old grudge in thus turning his subject against him.", "그녀는 이렇게 그의 화제를 그에게 되돌려 줌으로써 오랜 원한에 대한 복수를 했다."], ["Hers was not the nature to spare him, and she had no compunction.", "그녀의 천성은 그를 봐줄 만한 것이 아니었고, 그녀에게는 가책이라곤 없었다."]], [["‘That he is a low, mercenary wretch; that I first saw him prowling about Italy (where I was, not long ago), and that I hired him there, as the suitable instrument of a purpose I happened to have; I have no objection to tell you.", "\"그자가 비천하고 돈에 매수되는 가련한 자라는 것, 제가 이탈리아에서(저는 얼마 전까지 거기 있었어요) 그자가 어슬렁거리는 것을 처음 보고 거기서 마침 제게 있던 어떤 목적에 알맞은 도구로 그를 고용했다는 것, 저는 당신에게 말하기를 꺼리지 않아요."], ["In short, it was worth my while, for my own pleasure--the gratification of a strong feeling--to pay a spy who would fetch and carry for money.", "요컨대, 제 자신의 만족을 위해—어떤 강렬한 감정을 충족시키기 위해—돈을 받고 심부름을 해 줄 첩자에게 보수를 줄 만한 가치가 제게 있었던 거지요."], ["I paid this creature.", "저는 이 작자에게 돈을 주었어요."], ["And I dare say that if I had wanted to make such a bargain, and if I could have paid him enough, and if he could have done it in the dark, free from all risk, he would have taken any life with as little scruple as he took my money.", "그리고 단언컨대, 제가 그런 거래를 원했고, 그에게 충분히 줄 수 있었으며, 그가 어둠 속에서 아무 위험 없이 그 일을 해낼 수 있었다면, 그는 제 돈을 받았던 것만큼이나 거리낌 없이 누구의 목숨이든 거두었을 거예요."], ["That, at least, is my opinion of him; and I see it is not very far removed from yours.", "적어도 그게 그자에 대한 제 생각이고, 보아하니 당신 생각과 그리 멀지 않군요."], ["Your mother’s opinion of him, I am to assume (following your example of assuming this and that), was vastly different.’", "당신 어머니의 그자에 대한 생각은(이것저것 짐작하는 당신 본을 따르자면) 사뭇 달랐다고 봐야겠지요.\""]], [["‘My mother, let me remind you,’ said Clennam, ‘was first brought into communication with him in the unlucky course of business.’", "\"한 가지 일러 두겠는데,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"제 어머니는 불운한 사업의 과정에서 처음으로 그와 연락을 트게 된 것입니다.\""]], [["‘It appears to have been an unlucky course of business that last brought her into communication with him,’ returned Miss Wade; ‘and business hours on that occasion were late.’", "\"마지막으로 그를 어머니와 연락이 닿게 한 것도 불운한 사업의 과정이었던 모양이네요.\" 웨이드 양이 대꾸했다. \"그리고 그때의 영업 시간은 늦은 시각이었고요.\""]], [["‘You imply,’ said Arthur, smarting under these cool-handed thrusts, of which he had deeply felt the force already, ‘that there was something--’", "\"무슨 말씀이신지,\" 이미 그 무게를 깊이 느낀 바 있는 이 냉정한 일격들에 쓰라려하며 아서가 말했다. \"무언가가 있었다는 뜻으로—\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ she composedly interrupted, ‘recollect that I do not speak by implication about the man.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 그녀가 침착하게 말을 가로막았다."], ["He is, I say again without disguise, a low mercenary wretch.", "\"제가 그자에 관해 넌지시 말하는 게 아님을 잊지 마세요."], ["I suppose such a creature goes where there is occasion for him.", "거듭, 숨김없이 말하건대 그는 비천하고 돈에 매수되는 가련한 자예요."], ["If I had not had occasion for him, you would not have seen him and me together.’", "그런 자는 자기가 필요한 곳에 간다고 봐요. 제가 그를 필요로 하지 않았다면 당신은 그와 저를 함께 보지 못했을 거고요.\""]], [["Wrung by her persistence in keeping that dark side of the case before him, of which there was a half-hidden shadow in his own breast, Clennam was silent.", "자기 가슴속에도 반쯤 숨은 그림자가 있는 그 어두운 면을 그녀가 끈질기게 눈앞에 들이미는 데 괴로워하며, 클레넘은 입을 다물었다."]], [["‘I have spoken of him as still living,’ she added, ‘but he may have been put out of the way for anything I know.", "\"제가 그자를 아직 살아 있는 것처럼 말했지만,\" 그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["For anything I care, also.", "\"어쩌면 제가 모르는 사이에 누가 그를 처치해 버렸을지도 모르지요."], ["I have no further occasion for him.’", "그리고 제가 아랑곳할 바도 아니고요. 저는 더 이상 그를 필요로 하지 않으니까요.\""]], [["With a heavy sigh and a despondent air, Arthur Clennam slowly rose.", "무거운 한숨과 낙담한 기색으로 아서 클레넘은 천천히 일어섰다."], ["She did not rise also, but said, having looked at him in the meanwhile with a fixed look of suspicion, and lips angrily compressed:", "그녀는 함께 일어서지 않은 채, 그러는 동안 의혹의 시선을 그에게 못 박고 입술을 성나게 앙다물고 있다가 말했다."]], [["‘He was the chosen associate of your dear friend, Mr Gowan, was he not?", "\"그자는 당신의 그 친애하는 친구 가우언 씨가 골라 사귄 동무 아니었나요?"], ["Why don’t you ask your dear friend to help you?’", "어째서 그 친애하는 친구분께 도와 달라 하지 않으시고요?\""]], [["The denial that he was a dear friend rose to Arthur’s lips; but he repressed it, remembering his old struggles and resolutions, and said:", "그가 친애하는 친구가 아니라는 부정의 말이 아서의 입가에 떠올랐으나, 그는 지난날의 다툼과 결심을 떠올리며 그것을 억누르고 말했다."]], [["‘Further than that he has never seen Blandois since Blandois set out for England, Mr Gowan knows nothing additional about him.", "\"블랑두아가 영국으로 떠난 뒤로 그를 본 적이 없다는 것 외에, 가우언 씨가 그에 관해 따로 아는 것은 없습니다."], ["He was a chance acquaintance, made abroad.’", "그는 외국에서 우연히 사귄 사람이었지요.\""]], [["‘A chance acquaintance made abroad!’ she repeated.", "\"외국에서 우연히 사귄 사람이라!\" 그녀가 되풀이했다."], ["‘Yes.", "\"그렇겠지요."], ["Your dear friend has need to divert himself with all the acquaintances he can make, seeing what a wife he has.", "당신의 친애하는 친구분은 그런 아내를 두었으니 사귈 수 있는 온갖 사람들로 기분을 풀어야겠지요."], ["I hate his wife, sir.’", "저는 그 아내가 미워요, 선생님.\""]], [["The anger with which she said it, the more remarkable for being so much under her restraint, fixed Clennam’s attention, and kept him on the spot.", "그렇게 말하는 그녀의 노여움은, 그토록 억눌려 있어 더욱 두드러진 까닭에 클레넘의 주의를 붙들어 그를 그 자리에 머물게 했다."], ["It flashed out of her dark eyes as they regarded him, quivered in her nostrils, and fired the very breath she exhaled; but her face was otherwise composed into a disdainful serenity; and her attitude was as calmly and haughtily graceful as if she had been in a mood of complete indifference.", "그것은 그를 바라보는 그녀의 검은 눈에서 번쩍였고, 콧방울에서 떨렸으며, 그녀가 내쉬는 숨결마저 달구었다. 그러나 그 밖으로는 그녀의 얼굴이 경멸 어린 평온함으로 다잡혀 있었고, 그 자태는 마치 완전한 무관심의 기분에 잠긴 듯 차분하고 도도하게 우아했다."]], [["‘All I will say is, Miss Wade,’ he remarked, ‘that you can have received no provocation to a feeling in which I believe you have no sharer.’", "\"한 가지만 말씀드리지요, 웨이드 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 품으신 그 감정에는, 제가 믿건대 함께할 이가 아무도 없을뿐더러, 그 감정을 자아낼 만한 어떤 도발도 당신이 받으신 적이 없을 거라는 겁니다.\""]], [["‘You may ask your dear friend, if you choose,’ she returned, ‘for his opinion upon that subject.’", "\"원하신다면 당신의 친애하는 친구분께,\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그 문제에 관한 의견을 물어보셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["‘I am scarcely on those intimate terms with my dear friend,’ said Arthur, in spite of his resolutions, ‘that would render my approaching the subject very probable, Miss Wade.’", "\"제가 그 친애하는 친구와는,\" 결심에도 불구하고 아서가 말했다. \"그 화제를 꺼낼 법한 그런 가까운 사이가 거의 못 됩니다, 웨이드 양.\""]], [["‘I hate him,’ she returned.", "\"저는 그가 미워요.\" 그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Worse than his wife, because I was once dupe enough, and false enough to myself, almost to love him.", "\"그의 아내보다 더 미워요. 제가 한때는 어리석게도, 또 제 자신에게 진실되지 못하게도 그를 거의 사랑할 뻔했으니까요."], ["You have seen me, sir, only on common-place occasions, when I dare say you have thought me a common-place woman, a little more self-willed than the generality.", "선생님, 당신은 그저 평범한 상황에서만 저를 보셨고, 그래서 저를 보통 사람보다 좀 더 고집 센 평범한 여자쯤으로 여기셨겠지요."], ["You don’t know what I mean by hating, if you know me no better than that; you can’t know, without knowing with what care I have studied myself and people about me.", "저를 그 정도로만 아신다면, 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 당신은 모르세요. 제가 얼마나 세심하게 제 자신과 제 주위 사람들을 살펴 왔는지를 모르고서는 아실 수 없어요."], ["For this reason I have for some time inclined to tell you what my life has been--not to propitiate your opinion, for I set no value on it; but that you may comprehend, when you think of your dear friend and his dear wife, what I mean by hating.", "그래서 저는 얼마 전부터 제 삶이 어떠했는지를 당신에게 들려줄까 마음이 기울었어요—당신의 호의를 사려는 게 아니라, 그건 제가 아무 값어치도 두지 않으니까요. 다만 당신이 그 친애하는 친구와 그 친애하는 아내를 떠올릴 때, 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지를 헤아릴 수 있도록요."], ["Shall I give you something I have written and put by for your perusal, or shall I hold my hand?’", "제가 써서 당신더러 읽으라고 따로 간직해 둔 것을 드릴까요, 아니면 그만둘까요?\""]], [["Arthur begged her to give it to him.", "아서는 그것을 자기에게 달라고 청했다."], ["She went to the bureau, unlocked it, and took from an inner drawer a few folded sheets of paper.", "그녀는 책상으로 가서 자물쇠를 풀고, 안쪽 서랍에서 접힌 종이 몇 장을 꺼냈다."], ["Without any conciliation of him, scarcely addressing him, rather speaking as if she were speaking to her own looking-glass for the justification of her own stubbornness, she said, as she gave them to him:", "그를 누그러뜨리려 들지도 않고, 그에게 말을 거는 둥 마는 둥, 차라리 제 고집을 정당화하느라 제 거울에다 대고 말하는 듯이, 그녀는 그것을 그에게 건네며 말했다."]], [["‘Now you may know what I mean by hating!", "\"이제 제가 미워한다고 할 때 그게 무슨 뜻인지 아시게 될 거예요!"], ["No more of that.", "그 얘긴 그만하지요."], ["Sir, whether you find me temporarily and cheaply lodging in an empty London house, or in a Calais apartment, you find Harriet with me.", "선생님, 당신이 저를 텅 빈 런던 집에서 임시로 값싸게 셋방을 들어 사는 것으로 보든, 칼레의 한 셋방에서 보든, 제 곁에는 해리엇이 있습니다."], ["You may like to see her before you leave.", "떠나시기 전에 그 애를 보고 싶으실지도 모르겠네요."], ["Harriet, come in!’ She called Harriet again.", "해리엇, 들어와요!\" 그녀는 다시 해리엇을 불렀다."], ["The second call produced Harriet, once Tattycoram.", "두 번째 부름에 해리엇이, 한때 태티코럼이었던 그 아이가 나타났다."]], [["‘Here is Mr Clennam,’ said Miss Wade; ‘not come for you; he has given you up,--I suppose you have, by this time?’", "\"여기 클레넘 씨가 오셨어.\" 웨이드 양이 말했다. \"너를 데리러 온 게 아니야. 이분은 너를 단념하셨거든—이제쯤은 그러셨지요?\""]], [["‘Having no authority, or influence--yes,’ assented Clennam.", "\"권한도 영향력도 없으니—그렇습니다.\" 클레넘이 수긍했다."]], [["‘Not come in search of you, you see; but still seeking some one.", "\"보다시피 너를 찾으러 온 게 아니라, 그래도 어떤 사람을 찾고 계셔."], ["He wants that Blandois man.’", "그 블랑두아라는 자를 찾으셔.\""]], [["‘With whom I saw you in the Strand in London,’ hinted Arthur.", "\"런던 스트랜드에서 너와 함께 있는 것을 내가 본 그 사람이지.\" 아서가 일러 주었다."]], [["‘If you know anything of him, Harriet, except that he came from Venice--which we all know--tell it to Mr Clennam freely.’", "\"해리엇, 그자가 베네치아에서 왔다는 것 외에—그건 우리 모두 아는 거고—그에 대해 아는 게 있다면, 클레넘 씨께 거리낌 없이 말씀드려라.\""]], [["‘I know nothing more about him,’ said the girl.", "\"그에 대해 더 아는 건 없어요.\" 아이가 말했다."]], [["‘Are you satisfied?’ Miss Wade inquired of Arthur.", "\"만족하셨나요?\" 웨이드 양이 아서에게 물었다."]], [["He had no reason to disbelieve them; the girl’s manner being so natural as to be almost convincing, if he had had any previous doubts.", "그는 그들을 의심할 이유가 없었다. 아이의 태도가 어찌나 자연스럽던지, 그가 미리 어떤 의심을 품었다 한들 거의 납득될 정도였다."], ["He replied, ‘I must seek for intelligence elsewhere.’", "그가 대답했다. \"다른 데서 소식을 찾아봐야겠군요.\""]], [["He was not going in the same breath; but he had risen before the girl entered, and she evidently thought he was.", "그는 그 말끝에 곧장 떠날 참은 아니었으나, 아이가 들어오기 전에 이미 일어서 있었던 터라 아이는 그가 떠나려는 줄로 안 게 분명했다."], ["She looked quickly at him, and said:", "아이가 그를 얼른 쳐다보며 말했다."]], [["‘Are they well, sir?’\n‘Who?’", "\"그분들 안녕하세요, 선생님?\""]], [["She stopped herself in saying what would have been ‘all of them;’ glanced at Miss Wade; and said ‘Mr and Mrs Meagles.’", "\"누구 말이냐?\" 아이는 \"그분들 모두\"라고 할 뻔한 말을 스스로 그치고는 웨이드 양을 흘끗 보더니 말했다. \"미글스 씨와 미글스 부인이요.\""]], [["‘They were, when I last heard of them.", "\"내가 마지막으로 소식을 들었을 때는 안녕하셨다."], ["They are not at home.", "지금은 집에 안 계신단다."], ["By the way, let me ask you.", "그러고 보니, 한 가지 물어보자."], ["Is it true that you were seen there?’", "네가 거기서 목격됐다는 게 사실이냐?\""]], [["‘Where?", "\"어디서요?"], ["Where does any one say I was seen?’ returned the girl, sullenly casting down her eyes.", "누가 제가 어디서 목격됐다고 하던가요?\" 아이가 침울하게 눈을 내리깔며 되물었다."]], [["‘Looking in at the garden gate of the cottage.’", "\"그 별장 정원 문 안을 들여다보고 있었다더구나.\""]], [["‘No,’ said Miss Wade.", "\"아니에요.\" 웨이드 양이 말했다."], ["‘She has never been near it.’", "\"그 애는 그 근처에 간 적이 없어요.\""]], [["‘You are wrong, then,’ said the girl.", "\"그게 아니에요.\" 아이가 말했다."], ["‘I went down there the last time we were in London.", "\"우리가 마지막으로 런던에 있을 때 저는 거기 갔어요."], ["I went one afternoon when you left me alone.", "어느 날 오후 당신이 저를 혼자 두었을 때 갔어요."], ["And I did look in.’", "그리고 정말로 들여다봤고요.\""]], [["‘You poor-spirited girl,’ returned Miss Wade with infinite contempt; ‘does all our companionship, do all our conversations, do all your old complainings, tell for so little as that?’", "\"이 비굴한 것아.\" 웨이드 양이 한없는 경멸을 담아 대꾸했다. \"우리가 함께한 그 모든 시간이, 우리가 나눈 그 모든 이야기가, 네가 늘어놓던 그 모든 옛 푸념이 고작 그것밖에 안 된다는 거니?\""]], [["‘There was no harm in looking in at the gate for an instant,’ said the girl.", "\"문 안을 잠깐 들여다보는 게 무슨 잘못이에요.\" 아이가 말했다."], ["‘I saw by the windows that the family were not there.’", "\"창문을 보고서 식구들이 거기 없다는 걸 알았는걸요.\""]], [["‘Why should you go near the place?’", "\"왜 그 근처에 갔던 거니?\""]], [["‘Because I wanted to see it.", "\"보고 싶었으니까요."], ["Because I felt that I should like to look at it again.’", "한 번 더 보고 싶다는 마음이 들었으니까요.\""]], [["As each of the two handsome faces looked at the other, Clennam felt how each of the two natures must be constantly tearing the other to pieces.", "그 두 아름다운 얼굴이 서로를 마주 보는 동안, 클레넘은 그 두 천성이 끊임없이 서로를 갈가리 찢어 놓고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Miss Wade, coldly subduing and removing her glance; ‘if you had any desire to see the place where you led the life from which I rescued you because you had found out what it was, that is another thing.", "\"오!\" 웨이드 양이 차갑게 시선을 거두고 가라앉히며 말했다."], ["But is that your truth to me? Is that your fidelity to me?", "\"네가, 그게 어떤 것인지 알아챘기에 내가 너를 구해 낸 그 삶을 살던 곳을 다시 보고 싶은 바람이 있었다면, 그건 또 다른 얘기지."], ["Is that the common cause I make with you?", "하지만 그게 나에 대한 너의 진심이니?"], ["You are not worth the confidence I have placed in you.", "그게 나에 대한 너의 충실함이니? 그게 내가 너와 함께 도모하는 공동의 대의야?"], ["You are not worth the favour I have shown you.", "너는 내가 너에게 둔 신뢰를 받을 자격이 없어."], ["You are no higher than a spaniel, and had better go back to the people who did worse than whip you.’", "너는 내가 너에게 베푼 호의를 받을 자격이 없어. 너는 스패니얼만도 못한 것이니, 차라리 채찍질보다 더 못된 짓을 한 그 사람들에게 돌아가는 게 낫겠다.\""]], [["‘If you speak so of them with any one else by to hear, you’ll provoke me to take their part,’ said the girl.", "\"누가 듣는 데서 그분들을 그렇게 말하면, 제가 그분들 편을 들도록 부추기시는 거예요.\" 아이가 말했다."]], [["‘Go back to them,’ Miss Wade retorted.", "\"그들에게 돌아가.\" 웨이드 양이 쏘아붙였다."], ["‘Go back to them.’", "\"그들에게 돌아가라고.\""]], [["‘You know very well,’ retorted Harriet in her turn, ‘that I won’t go back to them.", "\"잘 아시잖아요.\" 이번에는 해리엇이 쏘아붙였다."], ["You know very well that I have thrown them off, and never can, never shall, never will, go back to them.", "\"저는 그분들에게 돌아가지 않을 거예요. 제가 그분들을 떨쳐 냈고, 다시는 돌아갈 수 없고, 돌아가지 않을 거고, 결코 돌아가지 않으리란 걸 잘 아시잖아요."], ["Let them alone, then, Miss Wade.’", "그러니 그분들은 그냥 내버려 두세요, 웨이드 양.\""]], [["‘You prefer their plenty to your less fat living here,’ she rejoined.", "\"여기서의 덜 기름진 살림보다 그들의 풍족함을 더 좋아하는 거지.\" 그녀가 되받았다."], ["‘You exalt them, and slight me.", "\"너는 그들을 치켜세우고 나를 깔보는구나."], ["What else should I have expected?", "내가 달리 무엇을 기대했겠어?"], ["I ought to have known it.’", "진작 알았어야 했는데.\""]], [["‘It’s not so,’ said the girl, flushing high, ‘and you don’t say what you mean.", "\"그게 아니에요.\""], ["I know what you mean.", "아이가 벌겋게 달아올라 말했다."], ["You are reproaching me, underhanded, with having nobody but you to look to.", "\"그리고 당신은 속마음을 말하고 있지 않아요. 저는 당신이 무슨 뜻인지 알아요."], ["And because I have nobody but you to look to, you think you are to make me do, or not do, everything you please, and are to put any affront upon me.", "당신은 제가 의지할 데라곤 당신밖에 없다는 걸로 저를 음흉하게 나무라고 있는 거예요. 그리고 제가 의지할 데라곤 당신밖에 없으니, 당신 마음대로 저를 부리거나 못 하게 하거나 마음대로 할 수 있고, 무슨 모욕이든 저에게 안길 수 있다고 여기시는 거고요."], ["You are as bad as they were, every bit.", "당신은 그분들만큼이나 나빠요, 하나도 빠짐없이."], ["But I will not be quite tamed, and made submissive.", "하지만 저는 그렇게 고분고분 길들여져 순종하지는 않을 거예요."], ["I will say again that I went to look at the house, because I had often thought that I should like to see it once more.", "다시 말하지만 저는 그 집을 보러 갔어요, 한 번 더 보고 싶다고 종종 생각해 왔으니까요. 다시 묻겠어요, 그분들 안녕하세요."], ["I will ask again how they are, because I once liked them and at times thought they were kind to me.’", "저는 한때 그분들을 좋아했고, 때로는 그분들이 저에게 다정했다고 생각했으니까요.\""]], [["Hereupon Clennam said that he was sure they would still receive her kindly, if she should ever desire to return.", "이에 클레넘은, 만약 그녀가 언제든 돌아가고 싶어진다면 그들이 여전히 다정하게 맞아 줄 것이 분명하다고 말했다."]], [["‘Never!’ said the girl passionately.", "\"절대로요!\" 아이가 격하게 말했다."], ["‘I shall never do that.", "\"절대 그러지 않을 거예요."], ["Nobody knows that better than Miss Wade, though she taunts me because she has made me her dependent.", "웨이드 양보다 그걸 더 잘 아는 사람은 없지요, 비록 저를 자기에게 매인 신세로 만들어 놓고 저를 비웃지만요. 그리고 제가 그런 신세란 걸 저도 알아요."], ["And I know I am so; and I know she is overjoyed when she can bring it to my mind.’", "또 그녀는 그걸 제 마음에 떠올리게 할 수 있을 때면 더없이 기뻐한다는 것도 알고요.\""]], [["‘A good pretence!’ said Miss Wade, with no less anger, haughtiness, and bitterness; ‘but too threadbare to cover what I plainly see in this.", "\"그럴듯한 핑계로구나!\" 웨이드 양이 못지않은 노여움과 거만함과 쓰라림을 담아 말했다."], ["My poverty will not bear competition with their money.", "\"하지만 내가 이 일에서 빤히 보는 것을 가리기엔 너무 닳아빠진 핑계지."], ["Better go back at once, better go back at once, and have done with it!’", "내 가난이 그들의 돈과는 겨룰 수 없다는 거잖아. 차라리 당장 돌아가, 차라리 당장 돌아가서 끝을 봐라!\""]], [["Arthur Clennam looked at them, standing a little distance asunder in the dull confined room, each proudly cherishing her own anger; each, with a fixed determination, torturing her own breast, and torturing the other’s.", "아서 클레넘은 그 칙칙하고 좁은 방 안에서 조금 떨어져 선 채, 저마다 제 노여움을 오만하게 품고, 저마다 한결같은 결의로 제 가슴을 괴롭히고 또 상대의 가슴을 괴롭히는 그들을 바라보았다."], ["He said a word or two of leave-taking; but Miss Wade barely inclined her head, and Harriet, with the assumed humiliation of an abject dependent and serf (but not without defiance for all that), made as if she were too low to notice or to be noticed.", "그는 작별의 말을 한두 마디 했으나, 웨이드 양은 겨우 고개만 까딱했고, 해리엇은 비굴한 식객이자 종의 가장된 굴종으로(그러면서도 그 와중에 반항이 없지 않은 채), 제가 너무 미천하여 누구를 알아보지도 누구의 눈에 띄지도 않는다는 양 행세했다."]], [["He came down the dark winding stairs into the yard with an increased sense upon him of the gloom of the wall that was dead, and of the shrubs that were dead, and of the fountain that was dry, and of the statue that was gone.", "그는 어둡고 구불구불한 계단을 내려와 마당으로 나섰는데, 죽은 담의 음울함과, 죽은 관목의 음울함과, 마른 분수의 음울함과, 사라지고 없는 조상의 음울함이 한층 더 짙게 그를 짓눌렀다."], ["Pondering much on what he had seen and heard in that house, as well as on the failure of all his efforts to trace the suspicious character who was lost, he returned to London and to England by the packet that had taken him over.", "그 집에서 보고 들은 것을, 그리고 행방불명된 그 수상한 인물을 좇으려던 자신의 온갖 노력이 모조리 수포로 돌아간 것을 깊이 곱씹으면서, 그는 자기를 건네주었던 그 우편선을 타고 런던으로, 영국으로 돌아갔다."], ["On the way he unfolded the sheets of paper, and read in them what is reproduced in the next chapter.", "가는 길에 그는 그 종이들을 펼쳐, 다음 장에 옮겨 적은 것을 그 안에서 읽었다."]], [["# CHAPTER 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor", "# CHAPTER 21. The History of a Self-Tormentor"]], [["I have the misfortune of not being a fool.", "나는 바보가 아니라는 불행을 안고 있다."], ["From a very early age I have detected what those about me thought they hid from me.", "아주 어릴 적부터 나는 내 주위 사람들이 내게 숨기고 있다고 여기는 것들을 간파해 왔다."], ["If I could have been habitually imposed upon, instead of habitually discerning the truth, I might have lived as smoothly as most fools do.", "만약 내가 늘 진실을 꿰뚫어 보는 대신 늘 속아넘어갈 수 있는 사람이었다면, 나도 여느 바보들처럼 순탄하게 살았을지 모른다."]], [["My childhood was passed with a grandmother; that is to say, with a lady who represented that relative to me, and who took that title on herself.", "내 어린 시절은 할머니와 함께 보냈다. 다시 말해, 내게 그 친척 노릇을 하며 스스로 그 칭호를 떠맡은 어느 부인과 함께 보냈다."], ["She had no claim to it, but I--being to that extent a little fool--had no suspicion of her.", "그 부인에게는 그럴 자격이 없었지만, 나는—그만큼은 어린 바보였던지라—그녀를 의심하지 않았다."], ["She had some children of her own family in her house, and some children of other people.", "그녀의 집에는 자기 집안의 아이들도 몇 있었고, 남의 집 아이들도 몇 있었다."], ["All girls; ten in number, including me.", "모두 여자아이였고, 나를 포함해 열 명이었다."], ["We all lived together and were educated together.", "우리는 다 함께 살며 다 함께 교육을 받았다."]], [["I must have been about twelve years old when I began to see how determinedly those girls patronised me.", "그 여자아이들이 얼마나 작정하고 나를 보살펴 주는 척하는지 알아채기 시작했을 때, 나는 열두 살쯤이었던 것 같다."], ["I was told I was an orphan.", "사람들은 내가 고아라고 했다."], ["There was no other orphan among us; and I perceived (here was the first disadvantage of not being a fool) that they conciliated me in an insolent pity, and in a sense of superiority.", "우리 가운데 다른 고아는 없었다. 그리고 나는 (여기서 바보가 아니라는 데서 오는 첫 번째 불리함이 드러났다) 그들이 무례한 연민으로, 또 우월감으로 내 비위를 맞추고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["I did not set this down as a discovery, rashly.", "나는 이것을 경솔하게 발견이라고 단정하지 않았다."], ["I tried them often.", "나는 그들을 자주 시험해 보았다."], ["I could hardly make them quarrel with me.", "그들이 나와 다투게 만들기란 좀처럼 쉽지 않았다."], ["When I succeeded with any of them, they were sure to come after an hour or two, and begin a reconciliation.", "누군가와 다투는 데 성공해도, 그들은 반드시 한두 시간 뒤에 찾아와 화해를 시작하곤 했다."], ["I tried them over and over again, and I never knew them wait for me to begin.", "나는 그들을 거듭거듭 시험해 보았지만, 그들이 내가 먼저 다가오기를 기다리는 일은 한 번도 본 적이 없다."], ["They were always forgiving me, in their vanity and condescension.", "그들은 늘 자기들의 허영과 겸손한 척하는 마음으로 나를 용서하고 있었다."], ["Little images of grown people!", "다 큰 어른들의 작은 축소판이었다!"]], [["One of them was my chosen friend.", "그들 가운데 한 아이가 내가 택한 친구였다."], ["I loved that stupid mite in a passionate way that she could no more deserve than I can remember without feeling ashamed of, though I was but a child.", "나는 그 어리석은 꼬마를 격정적으로 사랑했는데, 그 아이는 그런 사랑을 받을 자격이 없었고, 나 또한 비록 어린아이에 불과했다지만 부끄러움 없이는 그 일을 떠올릴 수 없다."], ["She had what they called an amiable temper, an affectionate temper.", "그 아이에게는 사람들이 상냥한 성품, 다정한 성품이라 부르는 것이 있었다."], ["She could distribute, and did distribute pretty looks and smiles to every one among them.", "그 아이는 누구에게나 어여쁜 눈길과 미소를 나누어 줄 수 있었고, 또 실제로 나누어 주었다."], ["I believe there was not a soul in the place, except myself, who knew that she did it purposely to wound and gall me!", "그 아이가 나를 상처 입히고 괴롭히려고 일부러 그런다는 것을 아는 사람은, 나 말고는 그곳에 한 사람도 없었으리라 믿는다!"]], [["Nevertheless, I so loved that unworthy girl that my life was made stormy by my fondness for her.", "그럼에도 나는 그 못난 아이를 어찌나 사랑했던지, 그 아이를 향한 애정 때문에 내 삶이 폭풍에 휩싸였다."], ["I was constantly lectured and disgraced for what was called ‘trying her;’ in other words charging her with her little perfidy and throwing her into tears by showing her that I read her heart.", "나는 '그 아이를 시험한다'는 일로—다시 말해, 그 아이의 자잘한 배신을 들이대고, 내가 그 마음을 읽고 있음을 보여 주어 눈물 흘리게 만든 일로—끊임없이 훈계를 듣고 망신을 당했다."], ["However, I loved her faithfully; and one time I went home with her for the holidays.", "그래도 나는 그 아이를 한결같이 사랑했다. 그리고 한번은 방학 때 그 아이와 함께 그 집에 갔다."]], [["She was worse at home than she had been at school.", "그 아이는 학교에서보다 집에서 더 심했다."], ["She had a crowd of cousins and acquaintances, and we had dances at her house, and went out to dances at other houses, and, both at home and out, she tormented my love beyond endurance.", "사촌이며 친지들이 한 무리나 있었고, 우리는 그 집에서 춤을 추기도 하고 다른 집으로 춤을 추러 나가기도 했는데, 집에서나 밖에서나 그 아이는 내 사랑을 견딜 수 없을 만큼 괴롭혔다. 그 아이의 수법은 모두가 자기를 좋아하게 만들어—그렇게 나를 질투로 미쳐 버리게 하는 것이었다."], ["Her plan was, to make them all fond of her--and so drive me wild with jealousy.", "모두와 허물없이 다정하게 굴어—그렇게 그들을 부러워하다 못해 내가 정신을 잃게 하는 것이었다."], ["To be familiar and endearing with them all--and so make me mad with envying them.", "밤에 우리 둘만 침실에 남으면, 나는 그 아이의 비열함을 내가 완벽히 알고 있다고 책망하곤 했다."], ["When we were left alone in our bedroom at night, I would reproach her with my perfect knowledge of her baseness; and then she would cry and cry and say I was cruel, and then I would hold her in my arms till morning: loving her as much as ever, and often feeling as if, rather than suffer so, I could so hold her in my arms and plunge to the bottom of a river--where I would still hold her after we were both dead.", "그러면 그 아이는 울고 또 울면서 내가 잔인하다고 했고, 그러면 나는 아침까지 그 아이를 품에 안고 있었다. 변함없이 사랑하면서, 또 흔히 이렇게 괴로워하느니 차라리 이대로 그 아이를 품에 안고 강 밑바닥까지 가라앉아 버리는 편이—우리 둘 다 죽은 뒤에도 그곳에서 여전히 그 아이를 안고 있는 편이 낫겠다는 심정으로."]], [["It came to an end, and I was relieved.", "그것도 끝이 났고, 나는 안도했다."], ["In the family there was an aunt who was not fond of me.", "그 집안에는 나를 좋아하지 않는 숙모가 있었다."], ["I doubt if any of the family liked me much; but I never wanted them to like me, being altogether bound up in the one girl.", "그 집안의 누구라도 나를 그리 좋아했을지 의문이지만, 나는 그 한 아이에게 온통 매여 있었기에 그들이 나를 좋아하기를 바란 적이 없었다."], ["The aunt was a young woman, and she had a serious way with her eyes of watching me.", "그 숙모는 젊은 여자였는데, 진지한 눈빛으로 나를 지켜보는 버릇이 있었다."], ["She was an audacious woman, and openly looked compassionately at me.", "그녀는 대담한 여자였고, 드러내 놓고 나를 가엾다는 듯 바라보았다."], ["After one of the nights that I have spoken of, I came down into a greenhouse before breakfast.", "내가 앞서 말한 그런 밤들 가운데 어느 하루가 지난 뒤, 나는 아침 식사 전에 온실로 내려갔다."], ["Charlotte (the name of my false young friend) had gone down before me, and I heard this aunt speaking to her about me as I entered.", "샬럿(내 거짓된 어린 친구의 이름이다)이 나보다 먼저 내려가 있었고, 내가 들어설 때 그 숙모가 나에 관해 그 아이에게 말하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["I stopped where I was, among the leaves, and listened.", "나는 잎사귀들 사이 그 자리에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였다."]], [["The aunt said, ‘Charlotte, Miss Wade is wearing you to death, and this must not continue.’ I repeat the very words I heard.", "숙모가 말했다. \"샬럿, 웨이드 양이 너를 기진맥진하게 만들고 있구나. 이대로 계속되어선 안 돼.\" 내가 들은 바로 그 말을 그대로 옮긴다."]], [["Now, what did she answer? Did she say, ‘It is I who am wearing her to death, I who am keeping her on a rack and am the executioner, yet she tells me every night that she loves me devotedly, though she knows what I make her undergo?’ No; my first memorable experience was true to what I knew her to be, and to all my experience. She began sobbing and weeping (to secure the aunt’s sympathy to herself), and said, ‘Dear aunt, she has an unhappy temper; other girls at school, besides I, try hard to make it better; we all try hard.’", "그러면, 그 아이가 무어라 답했을까? \"그 애를 기진맥진하게 만드는 건 저예요. 그 애를 고문대에 매달아 두고 형 집행인 노릇을 하는 건 저라고요. 그런데도 그 애는 제가 자기에게 무얼 견디게 하는지 알면서도, 매일 밤 저를 헌신적으로 사랑한다고 말한답니다\"라고 했을까? 아니다. 내 첫 번째 잊지 못할 경험은 내가 알던 그 아이의 됨됨이에, 그리고 내 모든 경험에 충실했다. 그 아이는 (숙모의 동정을 제 편으로 끌어들이려고) 흐느끼며 울기 시작하더니 이렇게 말했다. \"숙모, 저 애는 불행한 성미를 가졌어요. 저 말고도 학교의 다른 여자아이들이 그걸 고쳐 주려고 무던히 애쓰고 있어요. 우리 모두 무던히 애쓰고 있다고요.\""]], [["Upon that the aunt fondled her, as if she had said something noble instead of despicable and false, and kept up the infamous pretence by replying, ‘But there are reasonable limits, my dear love, to everything, and I see that this poor miserable girl causes you more constant and useless distress than even so good an effort justifies.’", "그 말에 숙모는 마치 그 아이가 비열하고 거짓된 말이 아니라 무언가 고귀한 말이라도 한 듯 그 아이를 어루만지며, 그 파렴치한 가식을 이어 받아 이렇게 답했다. \"하지만 무슨 일에든 합당한 한도라는 게 있는 거란다, 얘야. 내가 보기에 그 가엾고 비참한 아이는, 그토록 갸륵한 노력으로도 정당화될 수 없을 만큼, 네게 끊임없고 부질없는 괴로움을 끼치고 있어.\""]], [["The poor miserable girl came out of her concealment, as you may be prepared to hear, and said, ‘Send me home.’ I never said another word to either of them, or to any of them, but ‘Send me home, or I will walk home alone, night and day!’ When I got home, I told my supposed grandmother that, unless I was sent away to finish my education somewhere else before that girl came back, or before any one of them came back, I would burn my sight away by throwing myself into the fire, rather than I would endure to look at their plotting faces.", "그 가엾고 비참한 아이는, 짐작하셨겠지만, 숨어 있던 곳에서 나와 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 집으로 보내 주세요.\" 나는 두 사람에게든, 그 집 누구에게든, \"저를 집으로 보내 주세요, 안 그러면 밤이고 낮이고 혼자서라도 걸어서 가겠어요!\"라는 말 외에는 다른 말을 하지 않았다. 집에 돌아와서 나는 할머니라는 그 사람에게, 저 아이가 돌아오기 전에, 아니 저들 가운데 누구라도 돌아오기 전에 나를 어딘가 다른 곳으로 보내 학업을 마치게 해 주지 않는다면, 저들의 음모를 꾸미는 얼굴을 보고 견디느니 차라리 불 속에 몸을 던져 두 눈을 태워 버리겠다고 말했다."]], [["I went among young women next, and I found them no better.", "다음으로 나는 젊은 여자들 사이로 들어갔는데, 그들도 조금도 나을 것이 없었다."], ["Fair words and fair pretences; but I penetrated below those assertions of themselves and depreciations of me, and they were no better.", "고운 말과 고운 척. 그러나 나는 그 자화자찬과 나에 대한 폄하 아래까지 꿰뚫어 보았고, 그들도 조금도 나을 것이 없었다."], ["Before I left them, I learned that I had no grandmother and no recognised relation.", "그들을 떠나기 전에, 나는 내게 할머니도 없고 인정된 친척도 없다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["I carried the light of that information both into my past and into my future.", "나는 그 소식의 빛을 내 과거에도, 내 미래에도 비추어 보았다."], ["It showed me many new occasions on which people triumphed over me, when they made a pretence of treating me with consideration, or doing me a service.", "그것은 사람들이 나를 배려해 대하는 척하거나 내게 호의를 베푸는 척할 때, 실은 나를 짓밟고 우쭐대던 수많은 새로운 경우들을 내게 보여 주었다."]], [["A man of business had a small property in trust for me.", "어느 사업가가 나를 위해 적은 재산을 신탁으로 맡아 두고 있었다. 나는 가정교사가 되기로 되어 있었고, 가정교사가 되었다."], ["I was to be a governess; I became a governess; and went into the family of a poor nobleman, where there were two daughters--little children, but the parents wished them to grow up, if possible, under one instructress.", "그리고 어느 가난한 귀족 집안으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에는 두 딸이 있었다. 아직 어린아이들이었지만, 부모는 가능하다면 한 교사 밑에서 자라기를 바랐다."], ["The mother was young and pretty.", "어머니는 젊고 예뻤다."], ["From the first, she made a show of behaving to me with great delicacy.", "처음부터 그녀는 나를 무척 세심하게 대하는 듯 꾸몄다."], ["I kept my resentment to myself; but I knew very well that it was her way of petting the knowledge that she was my Mistress, and might have behaved differently to her servant if it had been her fancy.", "나는 분개함을 속으로 삭였지만, 그것이 자기가 내 주인이라는 사실을 어루만지며 즐기는 그녀만의 방식임을, 또 마음만 내켰다면 하인을 대하듯 달리 굴 수도 있었으리라는 것을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["I say I did not resent it, nor did I; but I showed her, by not gratifying her, that I understood her.", "분개하지 않았다고 말은 하지만, 실제로 분개하지는 않았다. 다만 나는 그녀의 기분을 채워 주지 않음으로써, 내가 그녀를 꿰뚫어 보고 있음을 보여 주었다."], ["When she pressed me to take wine, I took water.", "그녀가 포도주를 권하면 나는 물을 마셨다."], ["If there happened to be anything choice at table, she always sent it to me: but I always declined it, and ate of the rejected dishes.", "식탁에 귀한 음식이 있으면 그녀는 늘 그것을 내게 보냈지만, 나는 늘 그것을 사양하고 물려진 접시의 음식을 먹었다."], ["These disappointments of her patronage were a sharp retort, and made me feel independent.", "그녀의 후원을 이렇게 좌절시키는 것은 날카로운 반격이었고, 나로 하여금 독립된 사람이라는 느낌이 들게 했다."]], [["I liked the children.", "아이들은 마음에 들었다."], ["They were timid, but on the whole disposed to attach themselves to me.", "소심했지만, 대체로 나를 따르려는 성향이었다."], ["There was a nurse, however, in the house, a rosy-faced woman always making an obtrusive pretence of being gay and good-humoured, who had nursed them both, and who had secured their affections before I saw them.", "그러나 그 집에는 유모가 하나 있었는데, 늘 명랑하고 쾌활한 척 요란하게 꾸며 대는 발그레한 얼굴의 여자였다. 그 여자는 두 아이를 다 길렀고, 내가 보기 전부터 아이들의 애정을 차지하고 있었다."], ["I could almost have settled down to my fate but for this woman.", "이 여자만 없었더라면 나는 거의 내 운명에 안주할 수 있었을지도 모른다."], ["Her artful devices for keeping herself before the children in constant competition with me, might have blinded many in my place; but I saw through them from the first.", "나와 끊임없이 경쟁하며 아이들 앞에 자기를 내세우려는 그녀의 교묘한 술책에는, 내 처지에 놓인 많은 사람이라면 속아 넘어갔을 테지만, 나는 처음부터 그것을 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["On the pretext of arranging my rooms and waiting on me and taking care of my wardrobe (all of which she did busily), she was never absent.", "내 방을 정돈하고 시중을 들고 내 옷을 챙긴다는 (실제로 그 모든 일을 분주히 하긴 했다) 핑계로, 그녀는 한시도 자리를 비우지 않았다. 그녀의 그 많은 교묘한 수작 가운데 가장 약삭빠른 것은, 아이들이 나를 더 좋아하게 만들려 애쓰는 척하는 것이었다."], ["The most crafty of her many subtleties was her feint of seeking to make the children fonder of me.", "그녀는 아이들을 내게 데려와 내게로 어르며 보냈다. \"착한 웨이드 양에게 가렴, 다정한 웨이드 양에게 가렴, 예쁜 웨이드 양에게 가렴."], ["She would lead them to me and coax them to me.", "웨이드 양은 너희를 무척 사랑한단다."], ["‘Come to good Miss Wade, come to dear Miss Wade, come to pretty Miss Wade. She loves you very much.", "웨이드 양은 영리한 분이라 책을 산더미처럼 읽으셨고, 내가 아는 것보다 훨씬 더 멋지고 재미난 이야기를 들려줄 수 있단다."], ["Miss Wade is a clever lady, who has read heaps of books, and can tell you far better and more interesting stories than I know.", "가서 웨이드 양 이야기를 들으렴!\" 이 무지한 술책에 내 가슴이 분노로 타오르는데, 어떻게 내가 아이들의 관심을 끌 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Come and hear Miss Wade!’ How could I engage their attentions, when my heart was burning against these ignorant designs?", "아이들의 천진한 얼굴이 움츠러들며, 그 팔이 내 목이 아니라 그녀의 목을 휘감는 것을 보았으니, 어찌 이상하다 할 수 있었겠는가? 그러면 그녀는 아이들의 곱슬머리를 제 얼굴에서 떨쳐 내며 나를 올려다보고 말하곤 했다."], ["How could I wonder, when I saw their innocent faces shrinking away, and their arms twining round her neck, instead of mine?", "\"아이들이 곧 정이 들 거예요, 웨이드 양. 아주 순수하고 다정한 아이들이랍니다, 마님."], ["Then she would look up at me, shaking their curls from her face, and say, ‘They’ll come round soon, Miss Wade; they’re very simple and loving, ma’am; don’t be at all cast down about it, ma’am’--exulting over me!", "그 일로 조금도 낙심하지 마세요, 마님.\" 나를 두고 의기양양해하며!"]], [["There was another thing the woman did.", "그 여자가 한 짓이 또 하나 있었다."], ["At times, when she saw that she had safely plunged me into a black despondent brooding by these means, she would call the attention of the children to it, and would show them the difference between herself and me.", "이따금 그런 수법으로 나를 무사히 어두운 낙담의 골똘함 속으로 몰아넣었다 싶으면, 그녀는 아이들의 주의를 그리로 돌려 자기와 나의 차이를 보여 주곤 했다."], ["‘Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["Poor Miss Wade is not well.", "가엾은 웨이드 양이 편찮으시단다."], ["Don’t make a noise, my dears, her head aches.", "떠들지 마, 얘들아, 머리가 아프시대."], ["Come and comfort her.", "가서 위로해 드리렴."], ["Come and ask her if she is better; come and ask her to lie down.", "가서 좀 나아지셨는지 여쭤 봐. 가서 누워 계시라고 말씀드려."], ["I hope you have nothing on your mind, ma’am.", "마음에 걸리는 일이 없으셨으면 좋겠네요, 마님."], ["Don’t take on, ma’am, and be sorry!’", "너무 상심하지 마세요, 마님, 슬퍼하지도 마시고요!\""]], [["It became intolerable.", "견딜 수 없는 지경이 되었다."], ["Her ladyship, my Mistress, coming in one day when I was alone, and at the height of feeling that I could support it no longer, I told her I must go.", "내가 혼자 있던 어느 날, 더는 견딜 수 없다는 심정이 극에 달했을 때, 마님이라는 그분이 들어왔고, 나는 떠나야겠다고 말했다."], ["I could not bear the presence of that woman Dawes.", "그 도즈라는 여자가 곁에 있는 것을 견딜 수 없다고."]], [["‘Miss Wade!", "\"웨이드 양!"], ["Poor Dawes is devoted to you; would do anything for you!’", "가엾은 도즈는 당신에게 헌신적이에요. 당신을 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 거라고요!\""]], [["I knew beforehand she would say so; I was quite prepared for it; I only answered, it was not for me to contradict my Mistress; I must go.", "나는 그녀가 그렇게 말하리라는 것을 미리 알고 있었고, 그에 대한 대비도 단단히 되어 있었다. 나는 그저, 주인의 말을 반박하는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니며, 떠나야겠다고만 답했다."]], [["‘I hope, Miss Wade,’ she returned, instantly assuming the tone of superiority she had always so thinly concealed, ‘that nothing I have ever said or done since we have been together, has justified your use of that disagreeable word, “Mistress.”", "\"웨이드 양.\" 그녀는 늘 그토록 얄팍하게 감추어 오던 우월의 어조를 즉각 취하며 받았다."], ["It must have been wholly inadvertent on my part.", "\"우리가 함께 지내는 동안 내가 한 말이나 행동 가운데, 당신이 '주인'이라는 그 거슬리는 말을 쓸 만한 빌미를 준 것이 결코 없기를 바라요. 만약 있었다면 전적으로 내 부주의였겠지요."], ["Pray tell me what it is.’", "부디 무엇이 문제인지 말해 줘요.\""]], [["I replied that I had no complaint to make, either of my Mistress or to my Mistress; but I must go.", "나는 주인에 관해서든 주인에게든 불평할 것은 없으나, 떠나야겠다고 답했다."]], [["She hesitated a moment, and then sat down beside me, and laid her hand on mine.", "그녀는 잠시 망설이더니, 내 곁에 앉아 자기 손을 내 손 위에 얹었다."], ["As if that honour would obliterate any remembrance!", "마치 그 영광이 모든 기억을 지워 주기라도 할 것처럼!"]], [["‘Miss Wade, I fear you are unhappy, through causes over which I have no influence.’", "\"웨이드 양, 당신이 불행한 것 같아 걱정이에요. 내 힘으로는 어쩔 수 없는 어떤 원인들 때문에요.\""]], [["I smiled, thinking of the experience the word awakened, and said, ‘I have an unhappy temper, I suppose.’", "나는 그 말이 일깨운 경험을 떠올리며 미소 짓고는 말했다. \"저는 불행한 성미를 타고난 모양이지요.\""]], [["‘I did not say that.’", "\"나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요.\""]], [["‘It is an easy way of accounting for anything,’ said I.", "\"무슨 일이든 설명하기에는 손쉬운 방법이지요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘It may be; but I did not say so.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요."], ["What I wish to approach is something very different.", "하지만 나는 그렇게 말하지 않았어요. 내가 다가가려는 건 아주 다른 것이에요."], ["My husband and I have exchanged some remarks upon the subject, when we have observed with pain that you have not been easy with us.’", "당신이 우리와 함께 마음 편히 지내지 못하는 것을 우리가 가슴 아프게 지켜보며, 내 남편과 나는 그 일에 관해 몇 마디 말을 나누었답니다.\""]], [["‘Easy?", "\"편하게요?"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["You are such great people, my lady,’ said I.", "마님, 당신들은 워낙 대단한 분들이시잖아요.\" 내가 말했다."]], [["‘I am unfortunate in using a word which may convey a meaning--and evidently does--quite opposite to my intention.’ (She had not expected my reply, and it shamed her.) ‘I only mean, not happy with us.", "\"내 의도와는 정반대의 뜻을 전할 수도 있는—그리고 분명히 그렇게 된—말을 쓰다니 운이 나빴네요.\" (그녀는 내 대답을 예상하지 못했고, 그 대답에 무안해했다.) \"내 말은 그저, 우리와 함께 행복하지 못하다는 뜻이에요."], ["It is a difficult topic to enter on; but, from one young woman to another, perhaps--in short, we have been apprehensive that you may allow some family circumstances of which no one can be more innocent than yourself, to prey upon your spirits.", "꺼내기 어려운 화제이긴 하지만, 한 젊은 여자가 다른 젊은 여자에게 하는 말로서, 아마도—요컨대, 우리는 당신 자신이 누구보다도 결백한 어떤 집안 사정을, 당신이 마음에 두고 시달리도록 내버려 두는 게 아닐까 염려해 왔어요."], ["If so, let us entreat you not to make them a cause of grief.", "만약 그렇다면, 부디 그것을 슬픔의 원인으로 삼지 말기를 간청해요."], ["My husband himself, as is well known, formerly had a very dear sister who was not in law his sister, but who was universally beloved and respected--’", "내 남편 자신도, 잘 알려진 대로, 예전에 법적으로는 누이가 아니었지만 모두에게 사랑받고 존경받던 무척 소중한 누이가 한 분 있었는데—\""]], [["I saw directly that they had taken me in for the sake of the dead woman, whoever she was, and to have that boast of me and advantage of me; I saw, in the nurse’s knowledge of it, an encouragement to goad me as she had done; and I saw, in the children’s shrinking away, a vague impression, that I was not like other people.", "나는 그들이 그 죽은 여인이 누구였든 그 여인을 생각해서 나를 받아들였다는 것을, 그리고 나를 두고 그것을 자랑거리로 삼고 나를 이용하려 했다는 것을 곧바로 알아차렸다. 유모가 그 사실을 알고 있다는 데서, 그녀가 그토록 나를 들쑤셔 댄 까닭이 부추겨졌음을 보았고, 아이들이 움츠러드는 데서, 내가 다른 사람들과 다르다는 어렴풋한 인상이 있었음을 보았다."], ["I left that house that night.", "나는 그날 밤 그 집을 떠났다."]], [["After one or two short and very similar experiences, which are not to the present purpose, I entered another family where I had but one pupil: a girl of fifteen, who was the only daughter.", "지금 이야기와는 상관없는, 한두 차례의 짧고 아주 비슷한 경험을 거친 뒤, 나는 또 다른 집안으로 들어갔는데, 그곳에서 내가 가르칠 학생은 단 한 명, 외동딸인 열다섯 살 소녀였다."], ["The parents here were elderly people: people of station, and rich.", "그 집 부모는 나이 지긋한 사람들로, 지체 있고 부유한 이들이었다."], ["A nephew whom they had brought up was a frequent visitor at the house, among many other visitors; and he began to pay me attention.", "그들이 길러 온 조카 하나가 많은 다른 손님들과 더불어 그 집에 자주 드나들었는데, 그가 내게 관심을 두기 시작했다. 나는 단호히 그를 물리쳤다."], ["I was resolute in repulsing him; for I had determined when I went there, that no one should pity me or condescend to me.", "그곳에 들어갈 때, 누구도 나를 가엾이 여기거나 내게 겸양을 베푸는 일이 없게 하겠다고 작정했기 때문이다."], ["But he wrote me a letter.", "그러나 그가 내게 편지를 보냈다."], ["It led to our being engaged to be married.", "그것이 우리가 약혼하기에 이르게 했다."]], [["He was a year younger than I, and young-looking even when that allowance was made.", "그는 나보다 한 살 아래였고, 그 점을 감안하더라도 어려 보였다."], ["He was on absence from India, where he had a post that was soon to grow into a very good one.", "그는 인도에서 휴가를 받아 와 있었는데, 그곳에서 곧 아주 좋은 자리로 자라날 직책을 맡고 있었다."], ["In six months we were to be married, and were to go to India.", "여섯 달 뒤에 우리는 결혼해 인도로 갈 예정이었다."], ["I was to stay in the house, and was to be married from the house.", "나는 그 집에 머물다 그 집에서 결혼식을 올리기로 되어 있었다."], ["Nobody objected to any part of the plan.", "그 계획의 어느 부분에도 아무도 이의를 달지 않았다."]], [["I cannot avoid saying he admired me; but, if I could, I would.", "그가 나를 흠모했다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없는데, 할 수만 있다면 하지 않으련다."], ["Vanity has nothing to do with the declaration, for his admiration worried me.", "이 고백에 허영심은 조금도 관계가 없으니, 그의 흠모는 나를 괴롭혔기 때문이다."], ["He took no pains to hide it; and caused me to feel among the rich people as if he had bought me for my looks, and made a show of his purchase to justify himself.", "그는 그것을 감추려는 수고를 조금도 하지 않았고, 그 부유한 사람들 사이에서 나로 하여금, 그가 내 외모를 보고 나를 사 들였으며 제 행동을 정당화하려고 그 구매물을 과시하는 것 같은 느낌이 들게 했다."], ["They appraised me in their own minds, I saw, and were curious to ascertain what my full value was.", "나는 그들이 마음속으로 나를 값매기며, 내 온전한 값어치가 얼마인지 알아내려 호기심을 품는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["I resolved that they should not know.", "나는 그들이 결코 알지 못하게 하리라 결심했다."], ["I was immovable and silent before them; and would have suffered any one of them to kill me sooner than I would have laid myself out to bespeak their approval.", "나는 그들 앞에서 꼼짝도 하지 않았고 침묵했다. 그들의 인정을 사려고 나 자신을 내보이느니, 차라리 그들 가운데 누구라도 나를 죽이게 내버려 두었을 것이다."]], [["He told me I did not do myself justice.", "그는 내가 나 자신을 제대로 대접하지 않는다고 했다."], ["I told him I did, and it was because I did and meant to do so to the last, that I would not stoop to propitiate any of them.", "나는 제대로 대접하고 있으며, 또 끝까지 그러려 하기에 그들 가운데 누구의 비위도 맞추려 굽히지 않는 것이라고 했다."], ["He was concerned and even shocked, when I added that I wished he would not parade his attachment before them; but he said he would sacrifice even the honest impulses of his affection to my peace.", "내가 그들 앞에서 당신의 애정을 과시하지 않았으면 좋겠다고 덧붙이자, 그는 걱정하다 못해 충격까지 받았다. 그러나 그는 내 마음의 평안을 위해서라면 자기 애정의 진솔한 충동마저 희생하겠다고 말했다."]], [["Under that pretence he began to retort upon me.", "그는 그 핑계로 내게 앙갚음을 시작했다."], ["By the hour together, he would keep at a distance from me, talking to any one rather than to me.", "몇 시간이고 그는 나와 거리를 둔 채, 나 아닌 다른 누구와든 이야기를 나누곤 했다."], ["I have sat alone and unnoticed, half an evening, while he conversed with his young cousin, my pupil.", "나는 저녁의 절반을 혼자 아무도 거들떠보지 않는 채로 앉아 있었고, 그동안 그는 자기 어린 사촌, 즉 내 학생과 담소했다."], ["I have seen all the while, in people’s eyes, that they thought the two looked nearer on an equality than he and I.", "나는 그 내내 사람들의 눈빛에서, 저 두 사람이 그와 나보다 더 어울리는 한 쌍으로 보인다고 여기는 것을 알아챘다."], ["I have sat, divining their thoughts, until I have felt that his young appearance made me ridiculous, and have raged against myself for ever loving him.", "나는 그들의 생각을 헤아리며 앉아 있다가, 그의 젊어 보이는 모습이 나를 우스꽝스럽게 만든다고 느끼기에 이르렀고, 그를 사랑하게 된 나 자신에게 격분했다."]], [["For I did love him once.", "한때 나는 정말로 그를 사랑했으니까."], ["Undeserving as he was, and little as he thought of all these agonies that it cost me--agonies which should have made him wholly and gratefully mine to his life’s end--I loved him.", "그가 그럴 자격이 없었고, 그를 위해 내가 치른 그 모든 고뇌를—그를 평생토록 온전히, 고마운 마음으로 내 사람이 되게 했어야 마땅한 그 고뇌를—그가 거의 헤아리지 않았음에도, 나는 그를 사랑했다."], ["I bore with his cousin’s praising him to my face, and with her pretending to think that it pleased me, but full well knowing that it rankled in my breast; for his sake.", "나는 그의 사촌이 내 면전에서 그를 칭찬하는 것을, 또 그 아이가 그것이 나를 기쁘게 한다고 여기는 척하는 것을—그러면서도 그것이 내 가슴에 사무친다는 것을 너무도 잘 알면서—그를 위해 견뎌 냈다."], ["While I have sat in his presence recalling all my slights and wrongs, and deliberating whether I should not fly from the house at once and never see him again--I have loved him.", "그가 있는 자리에 앉아 내가 받은 온갖 멸시와 부당함을 떠올리며, 당장 그 집을 뛰쳐나가 다시는 그를 보지 말아야 하지 않을까 곰곰이 따지는 동안에도—나는 그를 사랑했다."]], [["His aunt (my Mistress you will please to remember) deliberately, wilfully, added to my trials and vexations.", "그의 숙모(부디 내 주인이었음을 기억해 두시기를)는 신중하게, 고의로 내 시련과 괴로움을 더해 주었다."], ["It was her delight to expatiate on the style in which we were to live in India, and on the establishment we should keep, and the company we should entertain when he got his advancement.", "그가 승진하면 우리가 인도에서 어떤 식으로 살게 될지, 어떤 살림을 꾸리게 될지, 어떤 손님들을 대접하게 될지를 늘어놓는 것이 그녀의 낙이었다."], ["My pride rose against this barefaced way of pointing out the contrast my married life was to present to my then dependent and inferior position.", "내 자존심은 내 결혼 생활이 당시의 종속적이고 열등한 처지와 어떤 대조를 이룰지를 그토록 뻔뻔스럽게 들이대는 그 방식에 맞서 곤두섰다."], ["I suppressed my indignation; but I showed her that her intention was not lost upon me, and I repaid her annoyance by affecting humility.", "나는 분노를 억눌렀지만, 그녀의 의도가 내게 놓치지 않고 닿았음을 보여 주었고, 겸손한 척함으로써 그녀의 약을 올린 데 되갚았다."], ["What she described would surely be a great deal too much honour for me, I would tell her.", "나는 그녀에게, 당신이 묘사한 것은 분명 내게는 너무도 과분한 영광일 것이라 말하곤 했다."], ["I was afraid I might not be able to support so great a change.", "그토록 큰 변화를 감당하지 못할까 두렵다고."], ["Think of a mere governess, her daughter’s governess, coming to that high distinction!", "한낱 가정교사가, 그것도 당신 딸의 가정교사가, 그런 드높은 영예에 이르다니요!"], ["It made her uneasy, and made them all uneasy, when I answered in this way.", "내가 이런 식으로 대답하면 그녀는 불편해했고, 그들 모두 불편해했다."], ["They knew that I fully understood her.", "그들은 내가 그녀의 속을 훤히 꿰뚫고 있음을 알았다."]], [["It was at the time when my troubles were at their highest, and when I was most incensed against my lover for his ingratitude in caring as little as he did for the innumerable distresses and mortifications I underwent on his account, that your dear friend, Mr Gowan, appeared at the house.", "내 괴로움이 절정에 달했을 때, 그리고 그 때문에 내가 겪은 헤아릴 수 없는 고통과 굴욕을 그토록 하찮게 여기는 그의 배은망덕에 내 연인에게 가장 격분해 있을 때, 당신의 소중한 친구인 가우언 씨가 그 집에 나타났다."], ["He had been intimate there for a long time, but had been abroad.", "그는 오래전부터 그 집과 가까운 사이였지만, 외국에 나가 있었다."], ["He understood the state of things at a glance, and he understood me.", "그는 한눈에 사정을 알아챘고, 또 나를 알아챘다."]], [["He was the first person I had ever seen in my life who had understood me.", "그는 내 평생 나를 이해한 첫 번째 사람이었다."], ["He was not in the house three times before I knew that he accompanied every movement of my mind.", "그가 그 집에 세 번 드나들기도 전에, 나는 그가 내 마음의 모든 움직임을 따라온다는 것을 알았다."], ["In his coldly easy way with all of them, and with me, and with the whole subject, I saw it clearly.", "그들 모두에게, 그리고 내게, 또 그 모든 일에 대한 그의 차갑고 태연한 태도 속에서, 나는 그것을 분명히 보았다."], ["In his light protestations of admiration of my future husband, in his enthusiasm regarding our engagement and our prospects, in his hopeful congratulations on our future wealth and his despondent references to his own poverty--all equally hollow, and jesting, and full of mockery--I saw it clearly.", "내 미래의 남편에 대한 그의 가벼운 흠모의 말 속에서, 우리의 약혼과 우리의 앞날에 대한 그의 열의 속에서, 우리의 장래의 부에 대한 희망에 찬 축하와 자기 자신의 가난에 대한 낙담 어린 언급 속에서—모두 똑같이 공허하고, 농담조이고, 조롱으로 가득한 그 말들 속에서—나는 그것을 분명히 보았다."], ["He made me feel more and more resentful, and more and more contemptible, by always presenting to me everything that surrounded me with some new hateful light upon it, while he pretended to exhibit it in its best aspect for my admiration and his own.", "그는 내 주위의 모든 것을 늘 어떤 새롭고 가증스러운 빛 아래 내게 내보이면서도, 그것을 내 흠모와 자기 자신의 흠모를 위해 가장 좋은 면으로 드러내 보이는 척했고, 그렇게 나를 점점 더 분개하게, 점점 더 비참하게 만들었다."], ["He was like the dressed-up Death in the Dutch series; whatever figure he took upon his arm, whether it was youth or age, beauty or ugliness, whether he danced with it, sang with it, played with it, or prayed with it, he made it ghastly.", "그는 네덜란드 연작화 속의 차려입은 죽음 같았다. 그가 팔에 끼는 형상이 무엇이든—젊음이든 늙음이든, 아름다움이든 추함이든—그것과 춤을 추든, 노래를 부르든, 놀이를 하든, 기도를 하든, 그는 그것을 섬뜩하게 만들었다."]], [["You will understand, then, that when your dear friend complimented me, he really condoled with me; that when he soothed me under my vexations, he laid bare every smarting wound I had; that when he declared my ‘faithful swain’ to be ‘the most loving young fellow in the world, with the tenderest heart that ever beat,’ he touched my old misgiving that I was made ridiculous.", "그러니 당신도 이해하시리라. 당신의 소중한 친구가 내게 찬사를 보낼 때 그는 실은 나를 조위(弔慰)했고, 그가 내 괴로움을 어루만질 때 그는 내가 가진 쓰라린 상처를 하나하나 드러냈으며, 그가 내 '충실한 연인'을 두고 '세상에서 가장 사랑스러운 청년, 일찍이 뛰어 본 적 없는 더없이 다정한 심장의 소유자'라 선언할 때 그는 내가 우스꽝스럽게 되었다는 오랜 불안을 건드렸음을."], ["These were not great services, you may say.", "대단한 봉사는 아니었다고 말할지도 모르겠다."], ["They were acceptable to me, because they echoed my own mind, and confirmed my own knowledge.", "그러나 그것들은 내 마음을 그대로 메아리치고 내 인식을 확증해 주었기에 내게 반가운 것이었다."], ["I soon began to like the society of your dear friend better than any other.", "나는 곧 당신의 소중한 친구와 어울리는 것을 다른 무엇보다 좋아하게 되었다."]], [["When I perceived (which I did, almost as soon) that jealousy was growing out of this, I liked this society still better.", "(거의 그와 동시에 알아차린 것이지만) 여기서 질투가 자라나는 것을 알아채자, 나는 이 어울림이 더욱더 좋아졌다."], ["Had I not been subject to jealousy, and were the endurances to be all mine?", "나는 질투에 시달려 오지 않았던가, 또 견뎌야 할 것은 모조리 내 몫이어야만 했던가? 아니다."], ["No. Let him know what it was!", "그도 그것이 무엇인지 알게 하라!"], ["I was delighted that he should know it; I was delighted that he should feel keenly, and I hoped he did.", "나는 그가 그것을 알게 되어 기뻤고, 그가 사무치게 느끼게 되어 기뻤으며, 또 그가 그러기를 바랐다."], ["More than that.", "그뿐만이 아니었다."], ["He was tame in comparison with Mr Gowan, who knew how to address me on equal terms, and how to anatomise the wretched people around us.", "그는 가우언 씨에 비하면 시시했다. 가우언 씨는 나를 대등한 사람으로 대하는 법을, 또 우리 주위의 가련한 사람들을 낱낱이 해부하는 법을 알았다."]], [["This went on, until the aunt, my Mistress, took it upon herself to speak to me.", "이런 일이 이어지다가, 마침내 숙모, 곧 내 주인이 내게 한마디 하기로 마음먹었다. 거론할 가치도 거의 없는 일이며, 내게 아무 뜻도 없음을 자기도 안다고 했다."], ["It was scarcely worth alluding to; she knew I meant nothing; but she suggested from herself, knowing it was only necessary to suggest, that it might be better if I were a little less companionable with Mr Gowan.", "그러나 그녀는, 넌지시 일러 주기만 하면 된다는 것을 알기에, 제 딴에 넌지시 일렀다. 내가 가우언 씨와 좀 덜 친하게 지내는 편이 나을지도 모르겠다고."]], [["I asked her how she could answer for what I meant?", "나는 그녀에게, 어떻게 내게 아무 뜻도 없다고 장담할 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["She could always answer, she replied, for my meaning nothing wrong.", "그녀는 내가 그릇된 뜻을 품지 않았다는 것이라면 언제든 장담할 수 있다고 답했다."], ["I thanked her, but said I would prefer to answer for myself and to myself.", "나는 고맙다고 했지만, 나 자신에 관해서는 나 스스로에게 장담하는 편을 택하겠다고 했다."], ["Her other servants would probably be grateful for good characters, but I wanted none.", "그녀의 다른 하인들은 좋은 평판을 고마워할지 모르나, 나는 그런 것을 원하지 않는다고."]], [["Other conversation followed, and induced me to ask her how she knew that it was only necessary for her to make a suggestion to me, to have it obeyed?", "다른 대화가 이어졌고, 나는 그녀에게, 내게 넌지시 일러 주기만 하면 그대로 따르리라는 것을 어떻게 아느냐고 묻게 되었다."], ["Did she presume on my birth, or on my hire? I was not bought, body and soul.", "그녀는 내 출신을 빌미로 삼는가, 아니면 나를 고용한 것을 빌미로 삼는가?"], ["She seemed to think that her distinguished nephew had gone into a slave-market and purchased a wife.", "나는 몸도 영혼도 팔린 사람이 아니다. 그녀는 자기의 고귀한 조카가 노예 시장에 들어가 아내를 하나 사 왔다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["It would probably have come, sooner or later, to the end to which it did come, but she brought it to its issue at once.", "조만간 결국 그렇게 끝났을 일이지만, 그녀가 단번에 결말로 몰아갔다."], ["She told me, with assumed commiseration, that I had an unhappy temper.", "그녀는 짐짓 동정하는 척하며, 내가 불행한 성미를 타고났다고 말했다."], ["On this repetition of the old wicked injury, I withheld no longer, but exposed to her all I had known of her and seen in her, and all I had undergone within myself since I had occupied the despicable position of being engaged to her nephew.", "그 오래된 사악한 상처를 되풀이하자, 나는 더는 참지 않고, 내가 그녀에 관해 알고 그녀에게서 본 모든 것을, 또 그녀의 조카와 약혼한 그 비천한 처지에 놓인 이래 내 안에서 겪은 모든 것을 그녀에게 낱낱이 폭로했다."], ["I told her that Mr Gowan was the only relief I had had in my degradation; that I had borne it too long, and that I shook it off too late; but that I would see none of them more.", "나는 가우언 씨가 그 굴욕 속에서 내게 유일한 위안이었다고, 그것을 너무 오래 견뎠고 너무 늦게 떨쳐 냈다고, 그러나 이제 저들 가운데 누구도 다시는 보지 않겠다고 말했다."], ["And I never did.", "그리고 정말로 다시는 보지 않았다."]], [["Your dear friend followed me to my retreat, and was very droll on the severance of the connection; though he was sorry, too, for the excellent people (in their way the best he had ever met), and deplored the necessity of breaking mere house-flies on the wheel.", "당신의 소중한 친구는 내 은신처까지 나를 따라와, 그 관계가 끊어진 일을 두고 매우 익살을 부렸다. 그러면서도 그 훌륭한 사람들(그 나름으로는 그가 여태 만난 가장 훌륭한 이들)에 대해 안타까워했고, 한낱 집파리를 형거(刑車)에 매달아 부수어야 하는 처지를 개탄했다."], ["He protested before long, and far more truly than I then supposed, that he was not worth acceptance by a woman of such endowments, and such power of character; but--well, well--!", "그는 머지않아, 그리고 그때 내가 짐작한 것보다 훨씬 더 진실하게, 자기는 그런 자질과 그런 인격의 힘을 지닌 여자에게 받아들여질 만한 가치가 없는 사람이라고 강변했다. 하지만—뭐, 글쎄—!"]], [["Your dear friend amused me and amused himself as long as it suited his inclinations; and then reminded me that we were both people of the world, that we both understood mankind, that we both knew there was no such thing as romance, that we were both prepared for going different ways to seek our fortunes like people of sense, and that we both foresaw that whenever we encountered one another again we should meet as the best friends on earth.", "당신의 소중한 친구는 자기 마음에 드는 동안만큼 나를 즐겁게 했고 자기 자신도 즐겼다. 그러고는 우리 둘 다 세상 물정에 밝은 사람이며, 둘 다 인간을 이해하고, 둘 다 낭만 따위는 없다는 것을 알며, 둘 다 분별 있는 사람들답게 제각기 다른 길로 운을 찾아 나설 준비가 되어 있고, 또 언제든 다시 마주치면 세상에서 가장 좋은 벗으로 만나리라는 것을 둘 다 내다보고 있노라고 내게 일깨웠다."], ["So he said, and I did not contradict him.", "그가 그렇게 말했고, 나는 반박하지 않았다."]], [["It was not very long before I found that he was courting his present wife, and that she had been taken away to be out of his reach.", "오래지 않아 나는 그가 지금의 아내에게 구애하고 있으며, 그 여자가 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 끌려갔다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["I hated her then, quite as much as I hate her now; and naturally, therefore, could desire nothing better than that she should marry him.", "나는 그때 그 여자를, 지금 미워하는 것과 꼭 같은 만큼 미워했다. 그러니 당연히, 그 여자가 그와 결혼하는 것보다 더 바람직한 일은 없었다."], ["But I was restlessly curious to look at her--so curious that I felt it to be one of the few sources of entertainment left to me.", "그러나 나는 그 여자를 한번 보고 싶어 안절부절못할 만큼 호기심이 일었다—어찌나 호기심이 일었던지, 그것이 내게 남은 몇 안 되는 즐길 거리 가운데 하나라고 느낄 정도였다. 나는 조금 여행을 했다."], ["I travelled a little: travelled until I found myself in her society, and in yours.", "그러다 그 여자의 일행 속에, 그리고 당신의 일행 속에 내가 끼어 있는 것을 발견하기에 이르렀다."], ["Your dear friend, I think, was not known to you then, and had not given you any of those signal marks of his friendship which he has bestowed upon you.", "그때는 당신의 소중한 친구가 당신에게 알려져 있지 않았던 것 같고, 그가 당신에게 베푼 그 두드러진 우정의 표시들도 아직 당신에게 주지 않았던 것 같다."]], [["In that company I found a girl, in various circumstances of whose position there was a singular likeness to my own, and in whose character I was interested and pleased to see much of the rising against swollen patronage and selfishness, calling themselves kindness, protection, benevolence, and other fine names, which I have described as inherent in my nature.", "그 무리 속에서 나는 한 소녀를 발견했는데, 그 처지의 여러 정황이 내 처지와 묘하게 닮아 있었고, 그 성격 속에서 나는, 친절이니 보호니 자비니 하는 그 밖의 그럴듯한 이름을 내세우는 비대한 후원과 이기심에 맞서 일어나는—내가 내 천성에 내재한 것이라 묘사했던 그 기질을 많이 보고 흥미와 기쁨을 느꼈다."], ["I often heard it said, too, that she had ‘an unhappy temper.’ Well understanding what was meant by the convenient phrase, and wanting a companion with a knowledge of what I knew, I thought I would try to release the girl from her bondage and sense of injustice.", "또한 나는 그 소녀가 '불행한 성미를 타고났다'는 말도 자주 들었다. 그 편리한 문구가 뜻하는 바를 잘 이해하고 있었고, 내가 아는 것을 아는 동무를 원하던 터라, 나는 그 소녀를 속박과 부당함의 인식에서 풀어 주려 해 보기로 마음먹었다."], ["I have no occasion to relate that I succeeded.", "내가 성공했다는 것은 굳이 이야기할 필요가 없겠다."]], [["We have been together ever since, sharing my small means.", "우리는 그 이후로 줄곧 함께 지내며, 내 얼마 안 되는 재산을 나누어 쓰고 있다."]], [["# CHAPTER 22. Who passes by this Road so late?", "# CHAPTER 22. Who passes by this Road so late?"]], [["Arthur Clennam had made his unavailing expedition to Calais in the midst of a great pressure of business.", "아서 클레넘은 대단히 일이 몰린 와중에 칼레로 갔다가 헛걸음을 하고 돌아왔다."], ["A certain barbaric Power with valuable possessions on the map of the world, had occasion for the services of one or two engineers, quick in invention and determined in execution: practical men, who could make the men and means their ingenuity perceived to be wanted out of the best materials they could find at hand; and who were as bold and fertile in the adaptation of such materials to their purpose, as in the conception of their purpose itself.", "세계 지도 위에서 값진 영토를 거느린 어느 미개한 권력이, 착상이 빠르고 실행이 단호한 기술자 한둘의 도움을 필요로 하게 되었던 것이다. 손닿는 곳에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 재료로, 자신의 재간이 필요하다고 판단한 사람과 수단을 만들어 낼 수 있는 실무가들, 그리고 그 목적 자체를 구상하는 데서만큼이나 그러한 재료를 목적에 맞게 응용하는 데서도 대담하고 풍부한 발상을 지닌 자들 말이다."], ["This Power, being a barbaric one, had no idea of stowing away a great national object in a Circumlocution Office, as strong wine is hidden from the light in a cellar until its fire and youth are gone, and the labourers who worked in the vineyard and pressed the grapes are dust.", "이 권력은 미개한 것이었던 까닭에, 위대한 국가적 목표를 에두름국 안에 처박아 두려는 생각이 전혀 없었다. 마치 독한 포도주가, 그 불기운과 젊음이 다 가시고 포도밭에서 일하며 포도를 짜던 일꾼들이 흙으로 돌아갈 때까지 지하실에서 빛을 피해 숨겨지듯이 말이다."], ["With characteristic ignorance, it acted on the most decided and energetic notions of How to do it; and never showed the least respect for, or gave any quarter to, the great political science, How not to do it.", "그 권력은 특유의 무지함으로, 어떻게 해낼 것인가에 관한 가장 단호하고 정력적인 관념에 따라 움직였다. 그리고 어떻게 안 할 것인가라는 저 위대한 정치 과학에 대해서는 조금도 경의를 표하지 않았으며 한 치의 봐줌도 베풀지 않았다."], ["Indeed it had a barbarous way of striking the latter art and mystery dead, in the person of any enlightened subject who practised it.", "실로 그 권력은, 그 기술을 실천하는 깨인 신민이 있으면 그 사람을 통해 이 후자의 기예와 신비를 단숨에 때려죽이는 야만적인 방식을 지니고 있었다."]], [["Accordingly, the men who were wanted were sought out and found; which was in itself a most uncivilised and irregular way of proceeding.", "그리하여 필요한 사람들을 수소문해 찾아냈는데, 이는 그 자체로 가장 미개하고 변칙적인 일 처리 방식이었다."], ["Being found, they were treated with great confidence and honour (which again showed dense political ignorance), and were invited to come at once and do what they had to do.", "찾아낸 다음에는 그들을 크나큰 신임과 명예로 대했고(이 또한 정치적으로 빽빽한 무지를 드러내는 것이었다), 당장 와서 해야 할 일을 해 달라고 청했다."], ["In short, they were regarded as men who meant to do it, engaging with other men who meant it to be done.", "요컨대 그들은, 그 일이 이루어지기를 바라는 다른 사람들과 손잡고 그 일을 해내려는 사람들로 여겨졌던 것이다."]], [["Daniel Doyce was one of the chosen.", "대니얼 도이스가 그 선택된 이들 가운데 하나였다."], ["There was no foreseeing at that time whether he would be absent months or years.", "그때로서는 그가 몇 달을 떠나 있게 될지 몇 해를 떠나 있게 될지 내다볼 수가 없었다."], ["The preparations for his departure, and the conscientious arrangement for him of all the details and results of their joint business, had necessitated labour within a short compass of time, which had occupied Clennam day and night.", "그의 출발 준비, 그리고 두 사람이 함께 벌인 사업의 온갖 세부와 결과를 그를 위해 양심껏 정리하는 일은 짧은 기간 안에 막대한 수고를 요구했고, 그 일이 클레넘을 밤낮으로 붙잡아 두었다."], ["He had slipped across the water in his first leisure, and had slipped as quickly back again for his farewell interview with Doyce.", "그는 첫 짬이 나자 바다 건너로 슬쩍 다녀왔고, 도이스와의 작별 면담을 위해 또 그만큼 빨리 슬쩍 돌아왔다."]], [["Him Arthur now showed, with pains and care, the state of their gains and losses, responsibilities and prospects.", "아서는 이제 도이스에게, 두 사람의 손익과 부담과 전망의 상태를 정성껏 공들여 보여 주었다."], ["Daniel went through it all in his patient manner, and admired it all exceedingly.", "대니얼은 그 특유의 참을성 있는 태도로 모든 것을 살펴보고는 모조리 더할 나위 없이 감탄했다."], ["He audited the accounts, as if they were a far more ingenious piece of mechanism than he had ever constructed, and afterwards stood looking at them, weighing his hat over his head by the brims, as if he were absorbed in the contemplation of some wonderful engine.", "그는 그 회계 장부를, 자신이 여태껏 만들어 본 어떤 것보다도 훨씬 더 정교한 기계 장치라도 되는 양 검토했고, 그 뒤에는 모자챙을 두 손으로 잡고 머리 위로 무게를 가늠하듯 들었다 놓았다 하면서, 무슨 경이로운 기관을 골똘히 들여다보기라도 하는 양 그것을 바라보고 서 있었다."]], [["‘It’s all beautiful, Clennam, in its regularity and order.", "\"전부 아름답군요, 클레넘. 그 규칙성과 질서가 말입니다."], ["Nothing can be plainer.", "이보다 더 명료할 수가 없어요."], ["Nothing can be better.’", "이보다 더 나을 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["‘I am glad you approve, Doyce.", "\"마음에 드신다니 기쁩니다, 도이스."], ["Now, as to the management of your capital while you are away, and as to the conversion of so much of it as the business may need from time to time--’ His partner stopped him.", "자, 그럼 당신이 떠나 계신 동안 자본을 운용하는 일에 관해서, 그리고 사업이 그때그때 필요로 하는 만큼의 자본을 현금화하는 일에 관해서는—\" 그의 동업자가 그를 막았다."]], [["‘As to that, and as to everything else of that kind, all rests with you.", "\"그 일이며, 그런 종류의 다른 모든 일이며, 전부 당신에게 맡깁니다."], ["You will continue in all such matters to act for both of us, as you have done hitherto, and to lighten my mind of a load it is much relieved from.’", "당신은 그런 일이라면 지금까지 해 오신 대로 우리 둘을 위해 계속 처리해 주시고, 내가 많이 덜어 냈던 그 짐을 내 마음에서 더 가볍게 해 주시면 됩니다.\""]], [["‘Though, as I often tell you,’ returned Clennam, ‘you unreasonably depreciate your business qualities.’", "\"하지만 제가 자주 말씀드리듯이,\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"당신은 까닭 없이 자신의 사업 수완을 깎아내리시는군요.\""]], [["‘Perhaps so,’ said Doyce, smiling.", "\"아마 그럴지도요.\" 도이스가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["‘And perhaps not.", "\"아닐지도 모르고요."], ["Anyhow, I have a calling that I have studied more than such matters, and that I am better fitted for.", "어쨌든 나는 그런 일들보다 더 많이 연구해 온, 그리고 더 잘 어울리는 천직이 있습니다."], ["I have perfect confidence in my partner, and I am satisfied that he will do what is best.", "나는 내 동업자를 완전히 믿고, 그가 가장 좋은 일을 하리라고 만족하고 있어요."], ["If I have a prejudice connected with money and money figures,’ continued Doyce, laying that plastic workman’s thumb of his on the lapel of his partner’s coat, ‘it is against speculating.", "돈과 돈의 셈에 관해서 내게 하나의 편견이 있다면,\" 도이스는 장인 특유의 그 잘 굽는 엄지손가락을 동업자의 외투 깃에 얹으며 말을 이었다."], ["I don’t think I have any other.", "\"그건 투기에 대한 반감입니다."], ["I dare say I entertain that prejudice, only because I have never given my mind fully to the subject.’", "그 밖에 다른 편견은 없는 것 같아요. 아마 나는, 이 주제에 한 번도 마음을 온전히 쏟아 본 적이 없는 까닭에 그런 편견을 품게 된 것이겠지요.\""]], [["‘But you shouldn’t call it a prejudice,’ said Clennam.", "\"하지만 그걸 편견이라 부르시면 안 됩니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘My dear Doyce, it is the soundest sense.’", "\"이보세요, 도이스, 그건 가장 건전한 분별이에요.\""]], [["‘I am glad you think so,’ returned Doyce, with his grey eye looking kind and bright.", "\"그렇게 생각해 주시니 기쁘군요.\" 도이스가 회색 눈을 다정하고 환하게 빛내며 대답했다."]], [["‘It so happens,’ said Clennam, ‘that just now, not half an hour before you came down, I was saying the same thing to Pancks, who looked in here.", "\"마침,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["We both agreed that to travel out of safe investments is one of the most dangerous, as it is one of the most common, of those follies which often deserve the name of vices.’", "\"바로 조금 전, 당신이 내려오시기 채 반시간도 전에, 여기 들렀던 팽크스에게도 똑같은 말을 하던 참이었습니다. 우리 둘 다, 안전한 투자에서 벗어나는 것은 흔히 악덕이라 불러도 마땅한 어리석음들 가운데 가장 흔한 것인 동시에 가장 위험한 것 중 하나라는 데 의견이 일치했지요.\""]], [["‘Pancks?’ said Doyce, tilting up his hat at the back, and nodding with an air of confidence.", "\"팽크스라고요?\" 도이스가 모자를 뒤로 젖혀 올리며, 신뢰가 가득한 기색으로 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."], ["‘Aye, aye, aye!", "\"그렇지, 그렇고말고요!"], ["That’s a cautious fellow.’", "그 사람은 신중한 친구예요.\""]], [["‘He is a very cautious fellow indeed,’ returned Arthur.", "\"정말이지 무척 신중한 친구지요.\" 아서가 대답했다."], ["‘Quite a specimen of caution.’", "\"신중함의 표본이라 할 만합니다.\""]], [["They both appeared to derive a larger amount of satisfaction from the cautious character of Mr Pancks, than was quite intelligible, judged by the surface of their conversation.", "두 사람은 팽크스 씨의 신중한 성품에서, 대화의 표면만으로 판단해서는 도무지 이해되지 않을 만큼이나 큰 만족을 끌어내는 듯이 보였다."]], [["‘And now,’ said Daniel, looking at his watch, ‘as time and tide wait for no man, my trusty partner, and as I am ready for starting, bag and baggage, at the gate below, let me say a last word.", "\"자, 그럼,\" 대니얼이 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["I want you to grant a request of mine.’", "\"세월과 물때는 사람을 기다려 주지 않는 법이고, 나의 믿음직한 동업자여, 나는 봇짐이며 짐가방이며 아래 대문에서 떠날 채비가 다 되었으니, 마지막으로 한마디 하게 해 주십시오. 당신이 내 부탁 하나를 들어주셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["‘Any request you can make--Except,’ Clennam was quick with his exception, for his partner’s face was quick in suggesting it, ‘except that I will abandon your invention.’", "\"당신이 하실 수 있는 부탁이라면 무엇이든—다만,\" 클레넘은 재빨리 예외를 달았는데, 동업자의 얼굴이 그 예외를 재빨리 암시하고 있었던 까닭이다. \"당신의 발명을 포기하라는 것만은 빼고요.\""]], [["‘That’s the request, and you know it is,’ said Doyce.", "\"그게 바로 그 부탁입니다. 당신도 알고 있지요.\" 도이스가 말했다."]], [["‘I say, No, then.", "\"그렇다면 안 됩니다."], ["I say positively, No.", "단연코 안 된다고 말하겠습니다."], ["Now that I have begun, I will have some definite reason, some responsible statement, something in the nature of a real answer, from those people.’", "일단 시작한 이상, 저는 그자들에게서 어떤 분명한 이유, 책임 있는 진술, 진짜 답변이라 할 만한 무언가를 받아 낼 작정입니다.\""]], [["‘You will not,’ returned Doyce, shaking his head.", "\"그러지 못할 겁니다.\" 도이스가 고개를 저으며 대꾸했다."], ["‘Take my word for it, you never will.’", "\"내 말을 믿으세요, 결코 못 할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘At least, I’ll try,’ said Clennam.", "\"적어도 시도는 해 보겠습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘It will do me no harm to try.’", "\"시도한다고 제게 해될 건 없으니까요.\""]], [["‘I am not certain of that,’ rejoined Doyce, laying his hand persuasively on his shoulder.", "\"그건 나도 장담 못 합니다.\" 도이스가 설득하듯 그의 어깨에 손을 얹으며 응수했다."], ["‘It has done me harm, my friend.", "\"내겐 해가 되었어요, 친구."], ["It has aged me, tired me, vexed me, disappointed me.", "그건 나를 늙게 했고, 지치게 했고, 속을 끓게 했고, 실망시켰습니다."], ["It does no man any good to have his patience worn out, and to think himself ill-used.", "인내가 닳아 없어지고 자신이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 느끼는 건 누구에게도 좋을 게 없어요."], ["I fancy, even already, that unavailing attendance on delays and evasions has made you something less elastic than you used to be.’", "내가 보기엔, 벌써부터도, 지연과 회피에 헛되이 매달리느라 당신은 예전만큼 활기 있는 사람이 못 된 것 같습니다.\""]], [["‘Private anxieties may have done that for the moment,’ said Clennam, ‘but not official harrying.", "\"사사로운 근심이 잠시 그렇게 만들었을지는 몰라도,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Not yet.", "\"관청에 시달려서 그런 건 아닙니다."], ["I am not hurt yet.’", "아직은요. 저는 아직 상하지 않았어요.\""]], [["‘Then you won’t grant my request?’", "\"그러면 내 부탁을 들어주지 않으시겠다는 거군요?\""]], [["‘Decidedly, No,’ said Clennam.", "\"단연코, 안 됩니다.\""], ["‘I should be ashamed if I submitted to be so soon driven out of the field, where a much older and a much more sensitively interested man contended with fortitude so long.’", "클레넘이 말했다. \"훨씬 나이 많고 훨씬 더 예민하게 이해관계가 걸린 분이 그토록 오래 꿋꿋이 버텨 온 그 싸움터에서, 제가 이렇게 빨리 쫓겨나는 데 굴복한다면 부끄러울 겁니다.\""]], [["As there was no moving him, Daniel Doyce returned the grasp of his hand, and, casting a farewell look round the counting-house, went down-stairs with him.", "그를 움직일 수 없음을 알자, 대니얼 도이스는 그의 손을 마주 잡고, 사무실 안을 한 바퀴 작별의 눈길로 둘러본 뒤, 그와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Doyce was to go to Southampton to join the small staff of his fellow-travellers; and a coach was at the gate, well furnished and packed, and ready to take him there.", "도이스는 사우샘프턴으로 가서 함께 여행할 소규모 인원에 합류하기로 되어 있었고, 짐을 그득 싣고 잘 꾸려진 마차가 그를 그곳까지 데려가려고 대문에 와 있었다. 일꾼들이 그를 배웅하러 대문에 나와 있었고, 그를 무척이나 자랑스러워했다."], ["The workmen were at the gate to see him off, and were mightily proud of him.", "\"행운을 빕니다, 도이스 씨!\" 그 무리 가운데 한 사람이 말했다."], ["‘Good luck to you, Mr Doyce!’ said one of the number.", "\"어딜 가시든, 사람들은 자기네 가운데 진짜 사내가 하나 끼었다는 걸 알게 될 거예요."], ["‘Wherever you go, they’ll find as they’ve got a man among ‘em, a man as knows his tools and as his tools knows, a man as is willing and a man as is able, and if that’s not a man, where is a man!’ This oration from a gruff volunteer in the back-ground, not previously suspected of any powers in that way, was received with three loud cheers; and the speaker became a distinguished character for ever afterwards.", "제 연장을 알고 제 연장도 그를 아는 사내, 의지가 있고 능력도 있는 사내, 이런 사람이 사내가 아니라면 대체 사내가 어디 있단 말입니까!\" 뒤편에 서 있던, 그런 방면에 무슨 재주가 있으리라고는 전혀 의심받지 않던 어느 무뚝뚝한 자원자의 이 일장 연설은 우렁찬 만세 삼창으로 환영받았고, 그 연설가는 그 뒤로 영원히 명사가 되었다."], ["In the midst of the three loud cheers, Daniel gave them all a hearty ‘Good Bye, Men!’ and the coach disappeared from sight, as if the concussion of the air had blown it out of Bleeding Heart Yard.", "우렁찬 만세 삼창이 한창인 가운데, 대니얼은 그들 모두에게 진심 어린 \"잘들 있게, 친구들!\"을 건넸고, 마차는, 마치 공기의 충격에 떠밀려 블리딩 하트 야드 밖으로 날아간 듯이, 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["Mr Baptist, as a grateful little fellow in a position of trust, was among the workmen, and had done as much towards the cheering as a mere foreigner could.", "밥티스트 씨는 신임받는 자리에 있는 고마운 작은 사내로서 일꾼들 틈에 끼어, 한낱 외국인이 할 수 있는 만큼 환호에 힘을 보탰다."], ["In truth, no men on earth can cheer like Englishmen, who do so rally one another’s blood and spirit when they cheer in earnest, that the stir is like the rush of their whole history, with all its standards waving at once, from Saxon Alfred’s downwards.", "사실, 영국인만큼 환호할 줄 아는 사람은 이 세상에 없으니, 그들이 진심으로 환호할 때면 서로의 피와 기운을 어찌나 북돋우는지, 그 술렁임은 색슨족 앨프레드 이래로 모든 깃발이 일제히 나부끼는 가운데 그들의 온 역사가 밀려드는 것만 같았다."], ["Mr Baptist had been in a manner whirled away before the onset, and was taking his breath in quite a scared condition when Clennam beckoned him to follow up-stairs, and return the books and papers to their places.", "밥티스트 씨는 그 기세에 어쩐지 휩쓸려 갔다가, 클레넘이 위층으로 따라 올라와 책과 서류를 제자리에 돌려놓으라고 손짓했을 때에야 잔뜩 겁먹은 모습으로 숨을 고르고 있었다."]], [["In the lull consequent on the departure--in that first vacuity which ensues on every separation, foreshadowing the great separation that is always overhanging all mankind--Arthur stood at his desk, looking dreamily out at a gleam of sun. But his liberated attention soon reverted to the theme that was foremost in his thoughts, and began, for the hundredth time, to dwell upon every circumstance that had impressed itself upon his mind on the mysterious night when he had seen the man at his mother’s.", "\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Again the man jostled him in the crooked street, again he followed the man and lost him, again he came upon the man in the court-yard looking at the house, again he followed the man and stood beside him on the door-steps.", "마졸렌의 길동무여,"]], [["‘Who passes by this road so late? Compagnon de la Majolaine; Who passes by this road so late?", "이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Always gay!’", "언제나 명랑하게!\""]], [["It was not the first time, by many, that he had recalled the song of the child’s game, of which the fellow had hummed this verse while they stood side by side; but he was so unconscious of having repeated it audibly, that he started to hear the next verse.", "두 사람이 나란히 서 있던 동안 그 사내가 흥얼거리던, 어린아이들 놀이 노래의 이 구절을 그가 떠올린 것은, 결코 처음이 아니라 여러 번째였다. 그러나 그는 자신이 그것을 소리 내어 읊조렸다는 것을 너무도 의식하지 못한 터라, 다음 구절이 들려오자 흠칫 놀랐다. \"임금의 기사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 꽃이라네,"]], [["‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine; Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’", "마졸렌의 길동무여,\n임금의 기사들 중에서도 으뜸가는 꽃이라네,"]], [["Cavalletto had deferentially suggested the words and tune, supposing him to have stopped short for want of more.", "언제나 명랑하게!\" 카발레토는 그가 더 떠오르지 않아 말이 막힌 줄로 알고, 공손하게 그 가사와 곡조를 일러 주었다."]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["You know the song, Cavalletto?’", "자네도 그 노래를 아는가, 카발레토?\""]], [["‘By Bacchus, yes, sir!", "\"바쿠스를 두고 맹세컨대, 압니다, 나리!"], ["They all know it in France.", "프랑스에서는 다들 알아요."], ["I have heard it many times, sung by the little children.", "어린아이들이 부르는 걸 저는 여러 번 들었습니다. 마지막으로 그걸 제가 들었던 때는,\""], ["The last time when it I have heard,’ said Mr Baptist, formerly Cavalletto, who usually went back to his native construction of sentences when his memory went near home, ‘is from a sweet little voice.", "본디 카발레토였던 밥티스트 씨는, 기억이 고향 가까이로 갈 때면 으레 모국식 문장 구성으로 되돌아가곤 하면서 말했다. \"어느 어여쁜 작은 목소리에게서였지요."], ["A little voice, very pretty, very innocent.", "작은 목소리, 아주 곱고, 아주 순진한."], ["Altro!’", "알트로!\""]], [["‘The last time I heard it,’ returned Arthur, ‘was in a voice quite the reverse of pretty, and quite the reverse of innocent.’ He said it more to himself than to his companion, and added to himself, repeating the man’s next words. ‘Death of my life, sir, it’s my character to be impatient!’", "\"내가 그걸 마지막으로 들었던 건,\" 아서가 대꾸했다. \"곱다는 것과는 정반대요, 순진하다는 것과도 정반대인 목소리에서였네.\" 그는 동행에게라기보다 자기 자신에게 그렇게 말했고, 그 사내가 뒤이어 했던 말을 되뇌며 혼잣말로 덧붙였다. \"맙소사, 나리, 참을성이 없는 게 제 천성이올시다!\""]], [["‘EH!’ cried Cavalletto, astounded, and with all his colour gone in a moment.", "\"엇!\" 카발레토가 소스라치게 놀라 외쳤고, 순식간에 얼굴빛이 싹 가셨다."]], [["‘What is the matter?’", "\"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘Sir!", "\"나리!"], ["You know where I have heard that song the last time?’", "제가 그 노래를 마지막으로 어디서 들었는지 아십니까?\""]], [["With his rapid native action, his hands made the outline of a high hook nose, pushed his eyes near together, dishevelled his hair, puffed out his upper lip to represent a thick moustache, and threw the heavy end of an ideal cloak over his shoulder.", "특유의 민첩한 몸짓으로, 그는 두 손으로 높다란 갈고리 코의 윤곽을 그렸고, 두 눈을 바짝 좁혀 모았으며, 머리카락을 헝클어뜨렸고, 윗입술을 부풀려 두툼한 콧수염을 흉내 냈으며, 가상의 망토 자락 무거운 끝을 어깨 너머로 휙 둘러 넘겼다."], ["While doing this, with a swiftness incredible to one who has not watched an Italian peasant, he indicated a very remarkable and sinister smile.", "이탈리아 농부를 지켜본 적 없는 사람으로서는 믿기 힘들 만큼 잽싸게 이 모든 동작을 해내면서, 그는 아주 기묘하고 음흉한 미소를 내비쳤다."], ["The whole change passed over him like a flash of light, and he stood in the same instant, pale and astonished, before his patron.", "그 모든 변화가 한 줄기 빛처럼 그의 위로 스쳐 지나갔고, 바로 그 찰나에 그는 다시, 창백하게 놀란 얼굴로 자신의 후원자 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["‘In the name of Fate and wonder,’ said Clennam, ‘what do you mean?", "\"운명과 경악의 이름으로,\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["Do you know a man of the name of Blandois?’", "\"대체 무슨 소린가? 자네 블랑두아라는 이름의 사내를 아는가?\""]], [["‘No!’ said Mr Baptist, shaking his head.", "\"아니요!\" 밥티스트 씨가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["‘You have just now described a man who was by when you heard that song; have you not?’", "\"방금 자네는 그 노래를 들었을 때 곁에 있던 사내를 묘사한 것 아닌가? 그렇지 않나?\""]], [["‘Yes!’ said Mr Baptist, nodding fifty times.", "\"그렇습니다!\" 밥티스트 씨가 쉰 번이나 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["‘And was he not called Blandois?’", "\"그런데 그자가 블랑두아라고 불리지 않았다고?\""]], [["‘No!’ said Mr Baptist.", "\"아닙니다!\" 밥티스트 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Altro, Altro, Altro, Altro!’ He could not reject the name sufficiently, with his head and his right forefinger going at once.", "\"알트로, 알트로, 알트로, 알트로!\" 그는 고개와 오른쪽 집게손가락을 동시에 내저으면서, 그 이름을 거부하고 또 거부해도 모자란 듯했다."]], [["‘Stay!’ cried Clennam, spreading out the handbill on his desk.", "\"잠깐!\" 클레넘이 책상 위에 그 전단지를 펼쳐 놓으며 외쳤다."], ["‘Was this the man?", "\"이 사내였나?"], ["You can understand what I read aloud?’", "내가 소리 내어 읽는 걸 알아들을 수 있나?\""]], [["‘Altogether.", "\"전부요."], ["Perfectly.’", "완벽하게요.\""]], [["‘But look at it, too.", "\"하지만 자네도 보게나."], ["Come here and look over me, while I read.’", "이리 와서 내 어깨 너머로 보아, 내가 읽는 동안.\""]], [["Mr Baptist approached, followed every word with his quick eyes, saw and heard it all out with the greatest impatience, then clapped his two hands flat upon the bill as if he had fiercely caught some noxious creature, and cried, looking eagerly at Clennam, ‘It is the man!", "밥티스트 씨가 다가와, 한 마디 한 마디를 그 재빠른 눈으로 좇으며, 더할 나위 없는 조바심으로 그 모든 것을 보고 들었다. 그러더니 무슨 해로운 짐승이라도 사납게 움켜잡은 듯 두 손바닥을 전단지 위에 철썩 내리치고는, 클레넘을 간절히 바라보며 외쳤다."], ["Behold him!’", "\"그 사내예요! 보세요, 이자라고요!\""]], [["‘This is of far greater moment to me’ said Clennam, in great agitation, ‘than you can imagine.", "\"이건 내게는 자네가 상상하는 것보다 훨씬 더 중대한 일이라네.\" 클레넘이 몹시 동요하며 말했다."], ["Tell me where you knew the man.’", "\"이 사내를 자네가 어디서 알게 되었는지 말해 주게.\""]], [["Mr Baptist, releasing the paper very slowly and with much discomfiture, and drawing himself back two or three paces, and making as though he dusted his hands, returned, very much against his will:", "밥티스트 씨는 아주 천천히, 그리고 몹시 거북해하며 종이를 놓고, 두세 걸음 뒤로 물러서서, 손을 터는 시늉을 하더니, 무척 내키지 않는 기색으로 대답했다."]], [["‘At Marsiglia--Marseilles.’", "\"마르실리아에서요—마르세유에서요.\""]], [["‘What was he?’", "\"그자는 뭐였나?\""]], [["‘A prisoner, and--Altro! I believe yes!--an,’ Mr Baptist crept closer again to whisper it, ‘Assassin!’", "\"죄수였습니다. 그리고—알트로! 그래, 그런 것 같아요!—한 사람의,\" 밥티스트 씨는 다시 가까이 다가와 속삭였다. \"살인자였지요!\""]], [["Clennam fell back as if the word had struck him a blow: so terrible did it make his mother’s communication with the man appear.", "그 말이 그에게 일격을 가하기라도 한 듯 클레넘은 뒤로 물러섰다. 어머니가 그 사내와 거래했다는 사실이 그토록 끔찍해 보였던 것이다."], ["Cavalletto dropped on one knee, and implored him, with a redundancy of gesticulation, to hear what had brought himself into such foul company.", "카발레토는 한쪽 무릎을 꿇고, 자기가 어쩌다 그런 추악한 무리에 끼게 되었는지 들어 달라고, 넘쳐나는 몸짓을 곁들여 그에게 애원했다."]], [["He told with perfect truth how it had come of a little contraband trading, and how he had in time been released from prison, and how he had gone away from those antecedents.", "그는 그것이 어떤 자그마한 밀무역에서 비롯되었는지, 자기가 때가 되어 어떻게 감옥에서 풀려났는지, 그리고 그 전력에서 어떻게 떠나왔는지를 한 점 거짓 없이 이야기했다."], ["How, at the house of entertainment called the Break of Day at Chalons on the Saone, he had been awakened in his bed at night by the same assassin, then assuming the name of Lagnier, though his name had formerly been Rigaud; how the assassin had proposed that they should join their fortunes together; how he held the assassin in such dread and aversion that he had fled from him at daylight, and how he had ever since been haunted by the fear of seeing the assassin again and being claimed by him as an acquaintance.", "손강 변 샬롱에 있는 '동틀 녘'이라는 여인숙에서, 어떻게 밤에 침대에 누워 있다가 바로 그 살인자에게 깨워졌는지—그자는 본디 이름이 리고였으나 그때는 라니에라는 이름을 쓰고 있었다—, 그 살인자가 어떻게 둘이서 운명을 함께 묶자고 제안했는지, 그가 그 살인자를 어찌나 두려워하고 혐오했던지 날이 밝자 그에게서 도망쳐 나왔는지, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 그 살인자를 다시 보게 되어 아는 사이라고 붙들리지나 않을까 하는 두려움에 어떻게 시달려 왔는지를."], ["When he had related this, with an emphasis and poise on the word, ‘assassin,’ peculiarly belonging to his own language, and which did not serve to render it less terrible to Clennam, he suddenly sprang to his feet, pounced upon the bill again, and with a vehemence that would have been absolute madness in any man of Northern origin, cried ‘Behold the same assassin!", "그가 자기네 언어 특유의, 그리고 클레넘에게 그 말을 조금도 덜 끔찍하게 들리게 하지 않는, 강조와 무게를 '살인자'라는 낱말에 실어 이 이야기를 다 마치고는, 별안간 벌떡 일어나 다시 그 전단지를 덮치더니, 북방 태생의 사람에게서라면 완전한 광기로 여겨졌을 격렬함으로 외쳤다. \"바로 그 살인자를 보세요!"], ["Here he is!’", "여기 있잖습니까!\""]], [["In his passionate raptures, he at first forgot the fact that he had lately seen the assassin in London.", "격정에 휩싸인 황홀경 속에서, 그는 처음에는 자기가 최근 런던에서 그 살인자를 보았다는 사실을 잊고 있었다."], ["On his remembering it, it suggested hope to Clennam that the recognition might be of later date than the night of the visit at his mother’s; but Cavalletto was too exact and clear about time and place, to leave any opening for doubt that it had preceded that occasion.", "그가 그것을 기억해 내자, 클레넘에게는 그 알아봄이 어머니 집을 방문한 그날 밤보다 나중의 일이었으면 하는 희망이 떠올랐다. 그러나 카발레토는 시간과 장소에 관해 너무도 정확하고 분명해서, 그 일이 그날 밤보다 앞섰다는 데 의심의 여지를 조금도 남기지 않았다."]], [["‘Listen,’ said Arthur, very seriously.", "\"잘 듣게.\" 아서가 아주 진지하게 말했다."], ["‘This man, as we have read here, has wholly disappeared.’", "\"이 사내는, 우리가 여기서 읽었다시피, 완전히 자취를 감추었네.\""]], [["‘Of it I am well content!’ said Cavalletto, raising his eyes piously.", "\"그 점에 대해서는 저도 무척 만족합니다!\" 카발레토가 경건하게 눈을 치뜨며 말했다."], ["‘A thousand thanks to Heaven!", "\"하늘에 천 번 감사를!"], ["Accursed assassin!’", "저주받을 살인자!\""]], [["‘Not so,’ returned Clennam; ‘for until something more is heard of him, I can never know an hour’s peace.’", "\"그렇지 않아.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다. \"그자에 대해 무언가 더 들리기 전까지는, 나는 한시도 마음 편할 수가 없으니 말일세.\""]], [["‘Enough, Benefactor; that is quite another thing.", "\"알겠습니다, 은인이여. 그건 전혀 다른 문제군요."], ["A million of excuses!’", "백만 번 용서를 빕니다!\""]], [["‘Now, Cavalletto,’ said Clennam, gently turning him by the arm, so that they looked into each other’s eyes.", "\"자, 카발레토.\" 클레넘이 그의 팔을 가만히 돌려 서로 눈을 마주 보게 하며 말했다."], ["‘I am certain that for the little I have been able to do for you, you are the most sincerely grateful of men.’", "\"내가 자네에게 해 줄 수 있었던 그 보잘것없는 일에 대해서도, 자네가 세상 누구보다 진심으로 고마워하는 사람이라는 걸 나는 확신하네.\""]], [["‘I swear it!’ cried the other.", "\"맹세합니다!\" 상대가 외쳤다."]], [["‘I know it.", "\"나도 알아."], ["If you could find this man, or discover what has become of him, or gain any later intelligence whatever of him, you would render me a service above any other service I could receive in the world, and would make me (with far greater reason) as grateful to you as you are to me.’", "만약 자네가 이 사내를 찾아내거나, 그가 어찌 되었는지 알아내거나, 그에 관한 그 어떤 최근 소식이라도 알아낼 수 있다면, 자네는 내가 이 세상에서 받을 수 있는 그 어떤 도움보다도 큰 도움을 내게 베푸는 것이고, 나를 (훨씬 더 마땅한 이유로) 자네가 내게 고마워하는 만큼 자네에게 고마워하는 사람으로 만들어 주는 걸세.\""]], [["‘I know not where to look,’ cried the little man, kissing Arthur’s hand in a transport.", "\"어디를 찾아봐야 할지 모르겠습니다.\" 그 작은 사내가 황홀경에 빠져 아서의 손에 입 맞추며 외쳤다."], ["‘I know not where to begin.", "\"어디서 시작해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["I know not where to go.", "어디로 가야 할지도 모르겠고요."], ["But, courage!", "하지만, 용기를!"], ["Enough!", "됐어요!"], ["It matters not!", "상관없습니다!"], ["I go, in this instant of time!’", "저는 갑니다, 바로 이 순간에!\""]], [["‘Not a word to any one but me, Cavalletto.’", "\"나 말고는 누구에게도 한마디도 말게, 카발레토.\""]], [["‘Al-tro!’ cried Cavalletto.", "\"알-트로!\" 카발레토가 외쳤다."], ["And was gone with great speed.", "그러고는 쏜살같이 사라졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams", "# CHAPTER 23. Mistress Affery makes a Conditional Promise, respecting her Dreams"]], [["Left alone, with the expressive looks and gestures of Mr Baptist, otherwise Giovanni Baptista Cavalletto, vividly before him, Clennam entered on a weary day.", "홀로 남겨진 클레넘은, 침례교도 밥티스트, 다시 말해 조반니 밥티스타 카발레토 씨의 표정과 몸짓이 또렷이 눈앞에 어른거리는 가운데, 고단한 하루를 맞이했다."], ["It was in vain that he tried to control his attention by directing it to any business occupation or train of thought; it rode at anchor by the haunting topic, and would hold to no other idea.", "어떤 업무에든, 어떤 사고의 흐름에든 주의를 돌려 다잡으려 했으나 헛수고였다. 그의 생각은 그 떠나지 않는 화제에 닻을 내린 채, 다른 어떤 생각에도 붙들리려 하지 않았다."], ["As though a criminal should be chained in a stationary boat on a deep clear river, condemned, whatever countless leagues of water flowed past him, always to see the body of the fellow-creature he had drowned lying at the bottom, immovable, and unchangeable, except as the eddies made it broad or long, now expanding, now contracting its terrible lineaments; so Arthur, below the shifting current of transparent thoughts and fancies which were gone and succeeded by others as soon as come, saw, steady and dark, and not to be stirred from its place, the one subject that he endeavoured with all his might to rid himself of, and that he could not fly from.", "마치 어느 죄인이 맑고 깊은 강 위에 멈춰 선 배에 사슬로 묶여, 수없이 많은 리그의 물이 곁을 흘러가도, 자신이 익사시킨 동료의 시신이 강바닥에 가라앉아, 소용돌이가 그것을 넓히거나 길게 늘이며 그 끔찍한 윤곽을 이제 부풀렸다가 이제 오므렸다 하는 것 말고는 꼼짝도 않고 변함도 없이 누워 있는 모습을, 영영 바라보도록 선고받은 것과도 같았다. 그렇게 아서도, 떠올랐다가 떠오르자마자 다른 것에 자리를 내주며 흘러가는 투명한 생각과 공상의 변덕스러운 물결 아래에, 한자리에서 흔들리지 않고 묵직하고 어둡게 가라앉아 있는, 온 힘을 다해 떨쳐 버리려 했으나 도무지 달아날 수 없는 그 하나의 주제를 보았다."]], [["The assurance he now had, that Blandois, whatever his right name, was one of the worst of characters, greatly augmented the burden of his anxieties.", "블랑두아가, 본명이 무엇이든, 가장 못된 인간 가운데 하나라는 사실을 이제 확신하게 되니, 불안의 짐이 한층 무거워졌다."], ["Though the disappearance should be accounted for to-morrow, the fact that his mother had been in communication with such a man, would remain unalterable.", "설령 내일 그의 실종이 해명된다 하더라도, 자신의 어머니가 그런 사내와 연락을 주고받았다는 사실만은 바뀌지 않을 터였다."], ["That the communication had been of a secret kind, and that she had been submissive to him and afraid of him, he hoped might be known to no one beyond himself; yet, knowing it, how could he separate it from his old vague fears, and how believe that there was nothing evil in such relations?", "그 연락이 은밀한 성질의 것이었다는 점, 그리고 어머니가 그에게 순종했고 그를 두려워했다는 점은, 자신 말고는 누구도 알지 못하기를 바랐다. 그러나 그것을 알고 있는 이상, 어떻게 그것을 자신의 오래되고 막연한 두려움과 떼어 놓을 수 있으며, 그런 관계에 사악한 구석이 전혀 없다고 어떻게 믿을 수 있겠는가?"]], [["Her resolution not to enter on the question with him, and his knowledge of her indomitable character, enhanced his sense of helplessness.", "그 문제를 자신과 더불어 따져 보지 않겠다는 어머니의 결심, 그리고 그 굽힐 줄 모르는 성격을 잘 아는 까닭에, 그의 무력감은 더욱 깊어졌다."], ["It was like the oppression of a dream to believe that shame and exposure were impending over her and his father’s memory, and to be shut out, as by a brazen wall, from the possibility of coming to their aid.", "어머니와 아버지의 기억 위로 수치와 폭로가 임박해 있는데도, 마치 놋쇠 벽에 막힌 듯 그들을 도울 길에서 차단되어 있다고 믿는 것은, 꿈에 짓눌리는 것과도 같은 압박이었다."], ["The purpose he had brought home to his native country, and had ever since kept in view, was, with her greatest determination, defeated by his mother herself, at the time of all others when he feared that it pressed most.", "그가 고국으로 가져온 목적, 그 뒤로 줄곧 마음에 품어 온 목적은, 다른 어느 때보다도 절박하게 다가왔다고 느껴지던 바로 그 시점에, 어머니 자신의 더없이 단호한 결의로써 좌절되고 말았다."], ["His advice, energy, activity, money, credit, all his resources whatsoever, were all made useless.", "그의 충고도, 정력도, 활동도, 돈도, 신용도, 그가 가진 온갖 수단이 모조리 쓸모없게 되었다."], ["If she had been possessed of the old fabled influence, and had turned those who looked upon her into stone, she could not have rendered him more completely powerless (so it seemed to him in his distress of mind) than she did, when she turned her unyielding face to his in her gloomy room.", "어머니가 옛 전설 속의 힘을 지녀, 자신을 바라보는 자들을 돌로 만들었다 한들, 음울한 방에서 굽힐 줄 모르는 얼굴을 그에게 돌렸을 때보다 그를 더 철저히 무력하게 만들 수는 없었을 것이다(괴로운 마음에 그에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다)."]], [["But the light of that day’s discovery, shining on these considerations, roused him to take a more decided course of action.", "그러나 그날의 발견이 던진 빛이 이런 생각들 위를 비추자, 그는 분기하여 한층 결연한 행동의 길로 나아가게 되었다."], ["Confident in the rectitude of his purpose, and impelled by a sense of overhanging danger closing in around, he resolved, if his mother would still admit of no approach, to make a desperate appeal to Affery.", "자기 목적의 정당함을 확신하고, 사방에서 좁혀 드는 임박한 위험을 느끼며, 그는 만일 어머니가 여전히 어떤 접근도 허락하지 않는다면 애프리에게 필사적으로 호소해 보기로 결심했다."], ["If she could be brought to become communicative, and to do what lay in her to break the spell of secrecy that enshrouded the house, he might shake off the paralysis of which every hour that passed over his head made him more acutely sensible.", "그녀를 입을 열게 만들 수 있고, 이 집을 뒤덮은 비밀의 주술을 깨뜨리는 데 제 몫을 하게 할 수 있다면, 한 시간 한 시간 지날수록 더욱 절실히 느껴지는 그 마비 상태를 떨쳐 낼 수 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["This was the result of his day’s anxiety, and this was the decision he put in practice when the day closed in.", "이것이 그날 하루 불안의 결실이었고, 날이 저물자 그가 실행에 옮긴 결정이었다."]], [["His first disappointment, on arriving at the house, was to find the door open, and Mr Flintwinch smoking a pipe on the steps.", "집에 다다랐을 때 그가 처음 맞닥뜨린 실망은, 문이 열려 있고 플린트윈치 씨가 계단에 앉아 파이프를 피우고 있는 것을 본 일이었다."], ["If circumstances had been commonly favourable, Mistress Affery would have opened the door to his knock.", "형편이 그저 그만큼만 순조로웠더라면, 애프리 부인이 그의 노크에 문을 열어 주었을 것이다."], ["Circumstances being uncommonly unfavourable, the door stood open, and Mr Flintwinch was smoking his pipe on the steps.", "형편이 유달리 어긋난 탓에, 문은 열려 있었고 플린트윈치 씨가 계단에서 파이프를 피우고 있었다."]], [["‘Good evening,’ said Arthur.", "\"안녕하시오.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Good evening,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"안녕하시오.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."]], [["The smoke came crookedly out of Mr Flintwinch’s mouth, as if it circulated through the whole of his wry figure and came back by his wry throat, before coming forth to mingle with the smoke from the crooked chimneys and the mists from the crooked river.", "연기는 플린트윈치 씨의 입에서 비뚤어진 채 흘러나왔다. 마치 그 비뚤어진 몸뚱이 전체를 한 바퀴 돌고 비뚤어진 목구멍을 거쳐 다시 돌아와서야 밖으로 나와, 비뚤어진 굴뚝에서 나는 연기와, 비뚤어진 강에서 피어오르는 안개와 뒤섞이려는 듯했다."]], [["‘Have you any news?’ said Arthur.", "\"무슨 소식이라도 있소?\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘We have no news,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"소식이라곤 없소.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["‘I mean of the foreign man,’ Arthur explained.", "\"그 외국인 말이오.\" 아서가 설명했다."]], [["‘_I_ mean of the foreign man,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"나도 그 외국인 말이오.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."]], [["He looked so grim, as he stood askew, with the knot of his cravat under his ear, that the thought passed into Clennam’s mind, and not for the first time by many, could Flintwinch for a purpose of his own have got rid of Blandois?", "목에 두른 크라바트 매듭이 귀밑으로 비뚜름히 가 있는 채, 그가 몸을 기울이고 선 모습이 어찌나 험상궂던지, 클레넘의 머릿속에는 한 가지 생각이 스쳤다 — 그것도 결코 처음이 아니었는데, 플린트윈치가 제 나름의 속셈으로 블랑두아를 없애 버린 것은 아닐까?"], ["Could it have been his secret, and his safety, that were at issue?", "위태로운 것은 그의 비밀과 그의 안위가 아니었을까?"], ["He was small and bent, and perhaps not actively strong; yet he was as tough as an old yew-tree, and as crusty as an old jackdaw.", "그는 작고 굽었으며 어쩌면 기운차게 힘이 세지는 않을지도 몰랐다. 그러나 오래된 주목나무처럼 질겼고, 늙은 갈까마귀처럼 심술궂었다."], ["Such a man, coming behind a much younger and more vigorous man, and having the will to put an end to him and no relenting, might do it pretty surely in that solitary place at a late hour.", "그런 사내가, 훨씬 젊고 정력적인 사내 뒤로 다가가, 그를 끝장낼 의지를 품고 망설임 없이 임한다면, 늦은 시각의 그 외진 곳에서라면 꽤 확실하게 해낼 법도 했다."]], [["While, in the morbid condition of his thoughts, these thoughts drifted over the main one that was always in Clennam’s mind, Mr Flintwinch, regarding the opposite house over the gateway with his neck twisted and one eye shut up, stood smoking with a vicious expression upon him; more as if he were trying to bite off the stem of his pipe, than as if he were enjoying it.", "병적인 사고의 상태에서 이런 생각들이, 클레넘의 마음속에 늘 자리한 그 주된 생각 위로 떠다니는 동안, 플린트윈치 씨는 목을 비틀고 한쪽 눈을 감은 채 대문 너머 맞은편 집을 바라보며, 악의에 찬 표정으로 담배를 피우고 서 있었다. 즐기는 것이라기보다는 차라리 파이프 자루를 물어뜯으려는 것처럼 보였다."], ["Yet he was enjoying it in his own way.", "그러면서도 그는 제 나름의 방식으로 그것을 즐기고 있었다."]], [["‘You’ll be able to take my likeness, the next time you call, Arthur, I should think,’ said Mr Flintwinch, drily, as he stooped to knock the ashes out.", "\"다음번에 들를 때는 내 초상화를 그릴 수 있겠구먼, 아서, 내 생각엔 말이야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 무뚝뚝하게 말하며, 몸을 굽혀 재를 털어 냈다."]], [["Rather conscious and confused, Arthur asked his pardon, if he had stared at him unpolitely.", "다소 멋쩍고 당황한 아서는, 무례하게 빤히 쳐다보았다면 용서를 구한다고 했다. \"다만 내 마음이 이 일에 너무 쏠려 있어서,\""], ["‘But my mind runs so much upon this matter,’ he said, ‘that I lose myself.’", "그가 말했다. \"정신을 놓아 버립니다.\""]], [["‘Hah!", "\"하! 그래도 난 모르겠는걸,\""], ["Yet I don’t see,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, quite at his leisure, ‘why it should trouble _you_, Arthur.’\n‘No?’", "플린트윈치 씨가 아주 느긋하게 받았다. \"어째서 그게 자네를 괴롭혀야 하는지 말이야, 아서.\""]], [["‘No,’ said Mr Flintwinch, very shortly and decidedly: much as if he were of the canine race, and snapped at Arthur’s hand.", "\"모르시겠다고요?\" \"모르겠어.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 아주 짧고 단호하게 말했다. 마치 개과의 짐승이라도 되는 양, 아서의 손을 덥석 무는 듯한 투였다."]], [["‘Is it nothing to see those placards about?", "\"저 벽보들이 나붙은 걸 보는 것이 아무 일도 아니란 말입니까?"], ["Is it nothing to me to see my mother’s name and residence hawked up and down in such an association?’", "어머니의 이름과 거처가 그런 일과 엮여 여기저기 떠벌려지는 것을 보는 것이, 제게 아무 일도 아니란 말입니까?\""]], [["‘I don’t see,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, scraping his horny cheek, ‘that it need signify much to you. But I’ll tell you what I do see, Arthur,’ glancing up at the windows; ‘I see the light of fire and candle in your mother’s room!’", "\"내가 보기엔,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 가죽처럼 거친 뺨을 긁으며 받았다. \"그게 자네한테 그리 큰 의미가 있을 것 같진 않은데. 하지만 내가 무얼 보는지 말해 주지, 아서,\" 그가 위쪽 창들을 흘끗 올려다보며 말했다. \"자네 어머니 방에 불빛이랑 촛불 빛이 보이는구먼!\""]], [["‘And what has that to do with it?’", "\"그게 이 일과 무슨 상관입니까?\""]], [["‘Why, sir, I read by it,’ said Mr Flintwinch, screwing himself at him, ‘that if it’s advisable (as the proverb says it is) to let sleeping dogs lie, it’s just as advisable, perhaps, to let missing dogs lie.", "\"이런, 여보게, 난 그걸로 이렇게 읽는단 말이야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 그를 향해 몸을 비틀며 말했다."], ["Let ‘em be.", "\"잠자는 개는 그냥 자게 두는 게 (속담에서 이르듯) 상책이라면, 사라진 개 역시 그냥 두는 게 똑같이 상책일지도 모른다는 거지."], ["They generally turn up soon enough.’", "내버려 둬. 그런 것들은 대개 머지않아 제풀에 나타나는 법이거든.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch turned short round when he had made this remark, and went into the dark hall.", "플린트윈치 씨는 이 말을 마치고 홱 돌아서더니 어두운 현관으로 들어갔다."], ["Clennam stood there, following him with his eyes, as he dipped for a light in the phosphorus-box in the little room at the side, got one after three or four dips, and lighted the dim lamp against the wall.", "클레넘은 그 자리에 서서 눈으로 그를 좇았다. 그가 옆방의 작은 인광 성냥갑에 손을 넣어 불을 더듬어 찾고, 서너 번 더듬은 끝에 하나를 얻어, 벽에 걸린 흐릿한 등에 불을 붙이는 동안 줄곧."], ["All the while, Clennam was pursuing the probabilities--rather as if they were being shown to him by an invisible hand than as if he himself were conjuring them up--of Mr Flintwinch’s ways and means of doing that darker deed, and removing its traces by any of the black avenues of shadow that lay around them.", "그러는 내내 클레넘은 — 자기 스스로 그것을 떠올린다기보다는, 보이지 않는 손이 그에게 보여 주기라도 하듯 — 플린트윈치 씨가 그 더 음험한 짓을 저지를 만한 방법과 수단, 그리고 주위에 깔린 어둠의 그 검은 샛길 가운데 어느 하나로 그 흔적을 지워 버릴 가능성을 따라가고 있었다."]], [["‘Now, sir,’ said the testy Jeremiah; ‘will it be agreeable to walk up-stairs?’", "\"자, 여보게,\" 신경질적인 제러마이아가 말했다. \"이제 위층으로 올라가시는 게 좋겠나?\""]], [["‘My mother is alone, I suppose?’", "\"어머니는 혼자 계시겠지요?\""]], [["‘Not alone,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"혼자가 아니야.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Mr Casby and his daughter are with her.", "\"캐스비 씨와 그 딸이 함께 있어."], ["They came in while I was smoking, and I stayed behind to have my smoke out.’", "내가 담배를 피우는 동안 들어왔길래, 나는 남아서 마저 피운 거지.\""]], [["This was the second disappointment.", "이것이 두 번째 실망이었다. 아서는 그에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않고 어머니의 방으로 향했다."], ["Arthur made no remark upon it, and repaired to his mother’s room, where Mr Casby and Flora had been taking tea, anchovy paste, and hot buttered toast.", "그곳에서는 캐스비 씨와 플로라가 차와 멸치 페이스트, 그리고 뜨겁게 버터를 바른 토스트를 들고 있던 참이었다. 그 진미들의 잔해는 식탁에서도, 애프리의 그을린 얼굴에서도 아직 치워지지 않았다."], ["The relics of those delicacies were not yet removed, either from the table or from the scorched countenance of Affery, who, with the kitchen toasting-fork still in her hand, looked like a sort of allegorical personage; except that she had a considerable advantage over the general run of such personages in point of significant emblematical purpose.", "그녀는 부엌에서 쓰는 토스트용 포크를 여태 손에 든 채, 무슨 우의적인 인물상처럼 보였다. 다만 그런 인물상들에서 흔히 보이는 것에 비해, 의미심장한 상징적 목적이라는 점에서 그녀가 상당한 우위를 차지하고 있다는 점만은 달랐다."]], [["Flora had spread her bonnet and shawl upon the bed, with a care indicative of an intention to stay some time.", "플로라는 한동안 머무를 작정임을 드러내는 정성으로 보닛과 숄을 침대 위에 펼쳐 두었다."], ["Mr Casby, too, was beaming near the hob, with his benevolent knobs shining as if the warm butter of the toast were exuding through the patriarchal skull, and with his face as ruddy as if the colouring matter of the anchovy paste were mantling in the patriarchal visage.", "캐스비 씨 역시 난로 선반 가까이에서 환히 빛을 발하고 있었다. 그의 자애로운 머리 혹들은 토스트의 따뜻한 버터가 그 가부장의 두개골을 뚫고 배어 나오기라도 하는 듯 번들거렸고, 그의 얼굴은 멸치 페이스트의 색소가 그 가부장의 면상에 번지기라도 하는 듯 불그레했다."], ["Seeing this, as he exchanged the usual salutations, Clennam decided to speak to his mother without postponement.", "으레 하는 인사를 주고받으며 이 광경을 본 클레넘은, 미루지 않고 어머니에게 말을 건네기로 마음먹었다."]], [["It had long been customary, as she never changed her room, for those who had anything to say to her apart, to wheel her to her desk; where she sat, usually with the back of her chair turned towards the rest of the room, and the person who talked with her seated in a corner, on a stool which was always set in that place for that purpose.", "어머니는 결코 방을 옮기는 일이 없었으므로, 그녀에게 따로 할 말이 있는 사람은 그녀를 책상 쪽으로 밀어 가는 것이 오랜 관례였다. 거기서 그녀는 대개 의자 등을 방 안 나머지 사람들 쪽으로 돌린 채 앉았고, 그녀와 이야기하는 사람은 한쪽 구석에, 늘 그 목적을 위해 그 자리에 놓아 두는 걸상에 앉았다."], ["Except that it was long since the mother and son had spoken together without the intervention of a third person, it was an ordinary matter of course within the experience of visitors for Mrs Clennam to be asked, with a word of apology for the interruption, if she could be spoken with on a matter of business, and, on her replying in the affirmative, to be wheeled into the position described.", "모자와 아들이 제삼자의 개입 없이 단둘이 이야기를 나눈 지 오래되었다는 점만 빼면, 클레넘 부인에게 방해해서 미안하다는 한마디와 함께 사무적인 일로 말을 나눌 수 있겠느냐고 묻고, 그녀가 그렇다고 답하면 앞서 말한 자리로 밀고 가는 것은, 방문객들이 으레 겪는 예사로운 일이었다."]], [["Therefore, when Arthur now made such an apology, and such a request, and moved her to her desk and seated himself on the stool, Mrs Finching merely began to talk louder and faster, as a delicate hint that she could overhear nothing, and Mr Casby stroked his long white locks with sleepy calmness.", "그러므로 이제 아서가 그렇게 양해를 구하고, 그렇게 청하고, 그녀를 책상 쪽으로 밀고 가서 걸상에 앉자, 핀칭 부인은 그저 더 크고 빠르게 떠들기 시작했다. 자기는 아무것도 엿들을 수 없다는 점을 은근히 일러 주려는 것이었고, 캐스비 씨는 졸린 듯 차분하게 길고 흰 머리타래를 쓰다듬었다."]], [["‘Mother, I have heard something to-day which I feel persuaded you don’t know, and which I think you should know, of the antecedents of that man I saw here.’", "\"어머니, 오늘 어떤 이야기를 들었는데, 어머니는 모르고 계신 일이라고 확신합니다. 또 어머니가 마땅히 아셔야 한다고 생각하는 일이고요. 여기서 제가 본 그 사내의 지난 행적에 관한 것입니다.\""]], [["‘I know nothing of the antecedents of the man you saw here, Arthur.’", "\"네가 여기서 본 그 사내의 지난 행적이라면 나는 아무것도 모른다, 아서.\""]], [["She spoke aloud.", "그녀는 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["He had lowered his own voice; but she rejected that advance towards confidence as she rejected every other, and spoke in her usual key and in her usual stern voice.", "그는 제 목소리를 낮추었으나, 그녀는 다른 모든 친밀함의 시도를 거부하듯 이 시도도 거부하고, 늘 쓰는 음조로, 늘 쓰는 준엄한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I have received it on no circuitous information; it has come to me direct.’", "\"빙 둘러 들은 이야기가 아닙니다. 제게 곧장 전해진 이야기입니다.\""]], [["She asked him, exactly as before, if he were there to tell her what it was?", "그녀는 아까와 똑같이, 네가 그것을 내게 말하려고 거기 있는 것이냐고 그에게 물었다."]], [["‘I thought it right that you should know it.’", "\"어머니가 아셔야 옳다고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["‘And what is it?’", "\"그래서 그게 무엇이냐?\""]], [["‘He has been a prisoner in a French gaol.’", "\"그자는 프랑스의 어느 감옥에 갇혔던 죄수였습니다.\""]], [["She answered with composure, ‘I should think that very likely.’", "그녀는 침착하게 대답했다. \"그럴 법도 하겠구나.\""]], [["‘But in a gaol for criminals, mother.", "\"하지만 그건 죄인들을 가두는 감옥이었습니다, 어머니."], ["On an accusation of murder.’", "살인 혐의로 말입니다.\""]], [["She started at the word, and her looks expressed her natural horror.", "그 말에 그녀는 흠칫 놀랐고, 표정에는 자연스러운 공포가 떠올랐다."], ["Yet she still spoke aloud, when she demanded:--", "그래도 그녀는 여전히 큰 소리로 따져 물었다 —"]], [["‘Who told you so?’", "\"누가 네게 그런 말을 하더냐?\""]], [["‘A man who was his fellow-prisoner.’", "\"그자와 함께 갇혀 있던 사내입니다.\""]], [["‘That man’s antecedents, I suppose, were not known to you, before he told you?’", "\"그 사내의 지난 행적은, 그가 네게 말하기 전까지는 너도 몰랐겠지?\" \"몰랐습니다.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘Though the man himself was?’", "\"그 사내 자체는 알았으면서도?\""]], [["‘My case and Flintwinch’s, in respect of this other man!", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"이 다른 사내를 두고 보자면, 나와 플린트윈치의 처지로구나!"], ["I dare say the resemblance is not so exact, though, as that your informant became known to you through a letter from a correspondent with whom he had deposited money?", "하긴 그 닮음이 그렇게까지 꼭 맞지는 않겠지. 네게 그 일을 전한 자가, 돈을 맡겨 둔 거래 상대에게서 온 편지를 통해 네게 알려진 것은 아닐 테니까?"], ["How does that part of the parallel stand?’", "그 대목의 비교는 어떻게 되는 게냐?\""]], [["Arthur had no choice but to say that his informant had not become known to him through the agency of any such credentials, or indeed of any credentials at all.", "아서로서는, 자기에게 그 일을 전한 자가 그런 신원 증명서를 통해, 아니 실은 어떤 신원 증명서를 통해서도 자기에게 알려진 것이 아니라고 말할 수밖에 없었다."], ["Mrs Clennam’s attentive frown expanded by degrees into a severe look of triumph, and she retorted with emphasis, ‘Take care how you judge others, then.", "클레넘 부인의 주의 깊게 찌푸려진 얼굴은 차츰 준엄한 승리의 표정으로 펴졌고, 그녀는 힘주어 반박했다. \"그렇다면 남을 어떻게 판단할지 조심해라."], ["I say to you, Arthur, for your good, take care how you judge!’", "너 잘되라고 하는 말이다, 아서, 남을 어떻게 판단할지 조심해라!\""]], [["Her emphasis had been derived from her eyes quite as much as from the stress she laid upon her words.", "그녀가 실은 말에 둔 힘 못지않게, 그 힘은 그녀의 두 눈에서도 나왔다. 그녀는 계속 그를 바라보았다."], ["She continued to look at him; and if, when he entered the house, he had had any latent hope of prevailing in the least with her, she now looked it out of his heart.", "그가 이 집에 들어설 때 조금이라도 그녀를 이겨 보려는 어떤 은밀한 희망을 품었더라면, 그녀는 이제 그것을 그의 가슴에서 노려보아 몰아내고 있었다."]], [["‘Mother, shall I do nothing to assist you?’", "\"어머니, 어머니를 돕기 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없습니까?\""]], [["‘Nothing.’", "\"없다.\""]], [["‘Will you entrust me with no confidence, no charge, no explanation?", "\"제게 어떤 신뢰도, 어떤 임무도, 어떤 해명도 맡기지 않으시렵니까?"], ["Will you take no counsel with me?", "저와 의논하지 않으시렵니까?"], ["Will you not let me come near you?’", "제가 가까이 가는 것도 허락하지 않으시렵니까?\""]], [["‘How can you ask me?", "\"어떻게 그런 걸 내게 묻느냐?"], ["You separated yourself from my affairs.", "너는 내 일에서 스스로 손을 뗐다."], ["It was not my act; it was yours.", "내가 그런 게 아니라 네가 한 일이다."], ["How can you consistently ask me such a question?", "어떻게 앞뒤 안 맞게 그런 질문을 내게 하느냐?"], ["You know that you left me to Flintwinch, and that he occupies your place.’", "너는 나를 플린트윈치에게 맡겼고, 그가 네 자리를 차지하고 있다는 걸 알지 않느냐.\""]], [["Glancing at Jeremiah, Clennam saw in his very gaiters that his attention was closely directed to them, though he stood leaning against the wall scraping his jaw, and pretended to listen to Flora as she held forth in a most distracting manner on a chaos of subjects, in which mackerel, and Mr F.’s Aunt in a swing, had become entangled with cockchafers and the wine trade.", "제러마이아를 흘끗 본 클레넘은, 그가 벽에 기대어 턱을 긁으며, 정신을 쏙 빼놓을 만큼 어수선한 방식으로 온갖 주제를 — 거기서는 고등어며, 그네에 탄 에프 씨의 고모며, 풍뎅이며, 포도주 무역이 한데 뒤엉켜 버렸다 — 늘어놓는 플로라의 말에 귀 기울이는 척하고 있었지만, 그 각반을 보아도, 그의 주의가 자기들에게 바짝 쏠려 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["‘A prisoner, in a French gaol, on an accusation of murder,’ repeated Mrs Clennam, steadily going over what her son had said.", "\"프랑스 감옥에, 살인 혐의로 갇혔던 죄수라.\" 클레넘 부인이 아들이 한 말을 차근차근 짚어 가며 되뇌었다."], ["‘That is all you know of him from the fellow-prisoner?’", "\"함께 갇혀 있던 그 사내한테서 들은 게 그게 전부냐?\""]], [["‘In substance, all.’", "\"요컨대, 전부입니다.\""]], [["‘And was the fellow-prisoner his accomplice and a murderer, too?", "\"그럼 함께 갇혀 있던 그 사내는 그자의 공범이자 역시 살인자였단 말이냐?"], ["But, of course, he gives a better account of himself than of his friend; it is needless to ask.", "하긴, 그자야 제 친구보다는 제 이야기를 더 그럴듯하게 했겠지. 물을 것도 없다."], ["This will supply the rest of them here with something new to talk about.", "이건 여기 나머지 사람들에게도 떠들 새 이야깃거리가 되겠구나."], ["Casby, Arthur tells me--’", "캐스비, 아서 말이—\""]], [["‘Stay, mother!", "\"멈추세요, 어머니! 멈추세요, 멈추세요!\""], ["Stay, stay!’ He interrupted her hastily, for it had not entered his imagination that she would openly proclaim what he had told her.", "그가 황급히 가로막았다. 어머니가 자기가 한 말을 공공연히 떠벌리리라고는 미처 상상도 못 했던 것이다."]], [["‘What now?’ she said with displeasure.", "\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 못마땅하게 말했다."], ["‘What more?’", "\"또 무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["‘I beg you to excuse me, Mr Casby--and you, too, Mrs Finching--for one other moment with my mother--’", "\"용서해 주십시오, 캐스비 씨 — 그리고 핀칭 부인, 당신도요 — 어머니와 잠깐만 더 이야기를 나누어야겠습니다 —\""]], [["He had laid his hand upon her chair, or she would otherwise have wheeled it round with the touch of her foot upon the ground.", "그가 그녀의 의자에 손을 얹었다. 안 그랬으면 그녀가 발로 바닥을 디뎌 의자를 홱 돌려 버렸을 것이다."], ["They were still face to face.", "두 사람은 여전히 얼굴을 마주하고 있었다."], ["She looked at him, as he ran over the possibilities of some result he had not intended, and could not foresee, being influenced by Cavalletto’s disclosure becoming a matter of notoriety, and hurriedly arrived at the conclusion that it had best not be talked about; though perhaps he was guided by no more distinct reason than that he had taken it for granted that his mother would reserve it to herself and her partner.", "그녀는 그를 바라보았고, 그는 카발레토의 폭로가 세상에 알려진 일이 됨으로써 자기가 의도하지도, 예견하지도 못한 어떤 결과가 빚어질 가능성들을 머릿속에서 빠르게 짚어 보다가, 그 일은 입에 올리지 않는 편이 낫겠다는 결론에 서둘러 이르렀다. 다만 어쩌면 그를 이끈 것은, 어머니가 그것을 자기와 그 동업자만의 비밀로 묻어 두리라 당연히 여겼다는 것 이상의 뚜렷한 이유는 아니었을지도 몰랐다."]], [["‘What now?’ she said again, impatiently.", "\"이번엔 또 뭐냐?\" 그녀가 다시 조바심을 내며 말했다."], ["‘What is it?’", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["‘I did not mean, mother, that you should repeat what I have communicated.", "\"어머니, 제가 전한 말을 되풀이하시라는 뜻은 아니었습니다."], ["I think you had better not repeat it.’", "그건 되풀이하지 않으시는 편이 낫겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Do you make that a condition with me?’", "\"그걸 내게 조건으로 다는 게냐?\""]], [["‘Well!", "\"음!"], ["Yes.’", "그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘Observe, then!", "\"그럼 똑똑히 봐라!"], ["It is you who make this a secret,’ said she, holding up her hand, ‘and not I.", "이걸 비밀로 만드는 건 너지,\" 그녀가 한 손을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["It is you, Arthur, who bring here doubts and suspicions and entreaties for explanations, and it is you, Arthur, who bring secrets here.", "\"내가 아니다. 의심과 혐의와 해명해 달라는 간청을 여기 가져오는 건 너다, 아서, 비밀을 여기 가져오는 것도 너다, 아서."], ["What is it to me, do you think, where the man has been, or what he has been?", "그 사내가 어디 있었든, 무엇이었든, 그게 나와 무슨 상관이라고 생각하느냐?"], ["What can it be to me?", "그게 나와 무슨 상관일 수 있겠느냐?"], ["The whole world may know it, if they care to know it; it is nothing to me.", "온 세상이 알고 싶으면 다 알아도 좋다. 내겐 아무것도 아니다."], ["Now, let me go.’", "자, 이제 나를 놓아 다오.\""]], [["He yielded to her imperious but elated look, and turned her chair back to the place from which he had wheeled it.", "그는 위압적이면서도 의기양양한 그녀의 표정에 굴복하여, 의자를 밀어 왔던 자리로 도로 돌려놓았다."], ["In doing so he saw elation in the face of Mr Flintwinch, which most assuredly was not inspired by Flora.", "그러면서 그는 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에 떠오른 의기양양함을 보았는데, 그것이 결코 플로라가 자아낸 것이 아님은 분명했다."], ["This turning of his intelligence and of his whole attempt and design against himself, did even more than his mother’s fixedness and firmness to convince him that his efforts with her were idle.", "자기의 정보와 시도와 계획 전체가 이렇게 자기에게 도리어 불리하게 돌아간 일은, 어머니의 단호함과 굳건함보다도 더, 그녀를 상대로 한 자신의 노력이 헛되다는 것을 그에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["Nothing remained but the appeal to his old friend Affery.", "이제 남은 것은 옛 벗 애프리에게 호소하는 일뿐이었다."]], [["But even to get the very doubtful and preliminary stage of making the appeal, seemed one of the least promising of human undertakings.", "그러나 그 호소를 하는, 매우 미심쩍은 첫 단계에 이르는 것조차도, 인간이 떠맡을 만한 일 가운데 가장 가망 없는 축에 드는 것처럼 보였다."], ["She was so completely under the thrall of the two clever ones, was so systematically kept in sight by one or other of them, and was so afraid to go about the house besides, that every opportunity of speaking to her alone appeared to be forestalled.", "그녀는 두 영리한 자의 손아귀에 너무도 완전히 쥐여 있었고, 둘 중 하나에게 늘 빈틈없이 감시당했으며, 게다가 집 안을 돌아다니는 것조차 너무 두려워했으므로, 그녀에게 단둘이 말을 걸 기회는 모조리 차단되어 있는 듯했다."], ["Over and above that, Mistress Affery, by some means (it was not very difficult to guess, through the sharp arguments of her liege lord), had acquired such a lively conviction of the hazard of saying anything under any circumstances, that she had remained all this time in a corner guarding herself from approach with that symbolical instrument of hers; so that, when a word or two had been addressed to her by Flora, or even by the bottle-green patriarch himself, she had warded off conversation with the toasting-fork like a dumb woman.", "그뿐 아니라 애프리 부인은, 어떤 수단으로(짐작하기란 그리 어렵지 않았으니, 자기 주인 나리의 날카로운 논리를 통해서였다) 어떤 상황에서든 무어라도 입에 올리는 일이 위험하다는 생생한 확신을 얻은 터라, 줄곧 한구석에서 그 상징적인 도구로 접근을 막으며 버티고 있었다. 그리하여 플로라가, 아니 심지어 그 병녹색 가부장 본인이 한두 마디 건넸을 때조차도, 그녀는 마치 벙어리 여자처럼 토스트용 포크로 대화를 막아 냈다."]], [["After several abortive attempts to get Affery to look at him while she cleared the table and washed the tea-service, Arthur thought of an expedient which Flora might originate.", "애프리가 식탁을 치우고 다기를 씻는 동안 그녀의 눈길을 끌어 보려 여러 차례 헛수고를 한 끝에, 아서는 플로라가 발단을 맡아 줄 만한 한 가지 방편을 떠올렸다."], ["To whom he therefore whispered, ‘Could you say you would like to go through the house?’", "그리하여 그는 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"집을 한번 둘러보고 싶다고 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Now, poor Flora, being always in fluctuating expectation of the time when Clennam would renew his boyhood and be madly in love with her again, received the whisper with the utmost delight; not only as rendered precious by its mysterious character, but as preparing the way for a tender interview in which he would declare the state of his affections.", "그런데 가엾은 플로라는, 클레넘이 소년 시절로 되돌아가 다시 자기에게 미친 듯이 빠져들 그 순간을 늘 들떠 기대하고 있던 터라, 그 속삭임을 더없는 기쁨으로 받아들였다. 그 은밀한 성격 때문에 귀하게 여겨졌을 뿐 아니라, 그가 제 마음의 상태를 고백할 다정한 밀회의 길을 닦아 주는 것으로 여겼던 것이다."], ["She immediately began to work out the hint.", "그녀는 곧장 그 암시를 풀어내기 시작했다."]], [["‘Ah dear me the poor old room,’ said Flora, glancing round, ‘looks just as ever Mrs Clennam I am touched to see except for being smokier which was to be expected with time and which we must all expect and reconcile ourselves to being whether we like it or not as I am sure I have had to do myself if not exactly smokier dreadfully stouter which is the same or worse, to think of the days when papa used to bring me here the least of girls a perfect mass of chilblains to be stuck upon a chair with my feet on the rails and stare at Arthur--pray excuse me--Mr Clennam--the least of boys in the frightfullest of frills and jackets ere yet Mr F.", "\"아 이런 이 가엾고 낡은 방,\" 플로라가 둘레를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["appeared a misty shadow on the horizon paying attentions like the well-known spectre of some place in Germany beginning with a B is a moral lesson inculcating that all the paths in life are similar to the paths down in the North of England where they get the coals and make the iron and things gravelled with ashes!’", "\"예나 지금이나 똑같아 보이는군요 클레넘 부인 보고 있자니 가슴이 뭉클해요 다만 더 그을린 것만 빼면요 그야 세월이 가면 당연한 일이고 좋든 싫든 우리 모두가 예상하고 받아들여야 할 일이지요 저 자신도 분명 그래야 했으니까요 꼭 더 그을렸다기보다는 끔찍하게 더 살이 쪘는데 그게 그거거나 더 나쁜 일이지요 아빠가 저를 이리로 데려오시던 시절을 생각하면요 동상투성이 자그마한 계집애였던 제가 의자에 붙들려 앉아 발을 가로대에 올린 채 아서를 빤히 쳐다보던 — 부디 용서하세요 — 클레넘 씨 말이에요 — 더없이 끔찍한 주름 깃과 재킷 차림의 자그마한 사내아이였던 그를요 에프 씨가 아직 지평선 위 흐릿한 그림자로도 나타나기 전에 그 독일 어느 지방의 비(B)로 시작하는 그 유명한 유령처럼 추근거리던 일을 말이에요 그건 인생의 모든 길이 잉글랜드 북부의 그 길들과 똑같다는 도덕적 교훈을 가르쳐 주지요 거기선 석탄을 캐고 쇠며 뭐며를 만들고 길에 재를 깔잖아요!\""]], [["Having paid the tribute of a sigh to the instability of human existence, Flora hurried on with her purpose.", "인간 존재의 덧없음에 한숨이라는 헌사를 바친 뒤, 플로라는 서둘러 제 목적을 향해 나아갔다."]], [["‘Not that at any time,’ she proceeded, ‘its worst enemy could have said it was a cheerful house for that it was never made to be but always highly impressive, fond memory recalls an occasion in youth ere yet the judgment was mature when Arthur--confirmed habit--Mr Clennam--took me down into an unused kitchen eminent for mouldiness and proposed to secrete me there for life and feed me on what he could hide from his meals when he was not at home for the holidays and on dry bread in disgrace which at that halcyon period too frequently occurred, would it be inconvenient or asking too much to beg to be permitted to revive those scenes and walk through the house?’", "\"하긴 어느 시절에도,\" 그녀가 이어 갔다. \"이 집의 최악의 적이라 한들 이곳이 명랑한 집이라고는 못 했을 거예요 본디 그렇게 지어진 게 아니라 늘 무척 인상 깊은 집이었으니까요 그리운 기억이 떠올려 주는 건 젊은 시절의 어느 일인데 아직 분별이 무르익기 전에 아서가 — 굳어진 버릇이라 — 클레넘 씨가 곰팡내로 이름난 어느 안 쓰는 부엌으로 저를 데려가서는 거기다 평생 저를 숨겨 두고 휴일이라 집에 없을 때면 식사에서 몰래 빼돌릴 수 있는 걸로 먹이고 또 그 행복하던 시절에 너무도 자주 일어났던 일이지만 야단맞아 신세지면 마른 빵으로 먹이겠다고 한 일이에요 그 장면들을 되살려 보고 집 안을 거니는 걸 허락해 달라고 청하는 게 폐가 되거나 너무 지나친 부탁일까요?\""]], [["Mrs Clennam, who responded with a constrained grace to Mrs Finching’s good nature in being there at all, though her visit (before Arthur’s unexpected arrival) was undoubtedly an act of pure good nature and no self-gratification, intimated that all the house was open to her.", "클레넘 부인은, 핀칭 부인이 거기 와 있어 준 그 호의 자체에 — 아서가 뜻밖에 닥치기 전까지만 해도 그녀의 방문은 의심할 바 없이 순전한 호의의 행위였지 자기만족이 아니었다 — 마지못한 우아함으로 응하며, 집 전체가 그녀에게 열려 있다고 일러 주었다. 플로라는 일어서서 동행을 청하듯 아서를 바라보았다."], ["Flora rose and looked to Arthur for his escort.", "\"물론이지요.\" 그가 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["‘Certainly,’ said he, aloud; ‘and Affery will light us, I dare say.’", "\"애프리가 우리를 위해 불을 밝혀 줄 겁니다, 아마도.\""]], [["Affery was excusing herself with ‘Don’t ask nothing of me, Arthur!’ when Mr Flintwinch stopped her with ‘Why not?", "애프리가 \"내겐 아무것도 청하지 마요, 아서!\" 하며 손사래를 치는데, 플린트윈치 씨가 \"왜 안 돼?"], ["Affery, what’s the matter with you, woman?", "애프리, 이 여편네야, 대체 뭐가 문제야? 왜 안 되냐고, 이 칠칠치 못한 것아!\""], ["Why not, jade!’ Thus expostulated with, she came unwillingly out of her corner, resigned the toasting-fork into one of her husband’s hands, and took the candlestick he offered from the other.", "하고 막아섰다. 이렇게 호통을 듣자, 그녀는 마지못해 구석에서 나와, 토스트용 포크를 남편의 한 손에 넘겨주고, 그가 다른 손으로 내민 촛대를 받아 들었다."]], [["‘Go before, you fool!’ said Jeremiah.", "\"앞장서, 이 멍청아!\" 제러마이아가 말했다."], ["‘Are you going up, or down, Mrs Finching?’", "\"위로 갈 거요, 아래로 갈 거요, 핀칭 부인?\""]], [["Flora answered, ‘Down.’", "플로라가 대답했다. \"아래로요.\""]], [["‘Then go before, and down, you Affery,’ said Jeremiah.", "\"그럼 앞장서서 아래로 가, 이 애프리야.\" 제러마이아가 말했다."], ["‘And do it properly, or I’ll come rolling down the banisters, and tumbling over you!’", "\"제대로 해, 안 그러면 내가 난간을 타고 굴러 내려가서 네 위로 자빠져 버릴 테니까!\""]], [["Affery headed the exploring party; Jeremiah closed it.", "애프리가 탐험대의 선두에 섰고, 제러마이아가 후미를 막았다. 그는 그들을 떠날 마음이 전혀 없었다."], ["He had no intention of leaving them.", "클레넘은 뒤를 돌아보다가, 그가 더없이 태연하고 더없이 빈틈없는 태도로 세 계단 뒤에서 따라오는 것을 보고는 낮은 목소리로 외쳤다. \"저자를 떼어 놓을 방법은 없는 겁니까!\""], ["Clennam looking back, and seeing him following three stairs behind, in the coolest and most methodical manner exclaimed in a low voice, ‘Is there no getting rid of him!’ Flora reassured his mind by replying promptly, ‘Why though not exactly proper Arthur and a thing I couldn’t think of before a younger man or a stranger still I don’t mind him if you so particularly wish it and provided you’ll have the goodness not to take me too tight.’", "플로라가 얼른 대답하여 그의 마음을 안심시켰다. \"글쎄 딱히 예의에 맞진 않고 더 젊은 남자나 낯선 사람 앞이라면 생각도 못 할 일이지만 그래도 당신이 그토록 간곡히 바라신다면 저는 저 사람은 개의치 않겠어요 다만 부디 저를 너무 꽉 끌어안지는 말아 주세요.\""]], [["Wanting the heart to explain that this was not at all what he meant, Arthur extended his supporting arm round Flora’s figure.", "그것이 자기가 뜻한 바가 전혀 아니라고 차마 설명할 용기가 나지 않아, 아서는 플로라의 몸을 받쳐 주는 팔을 둘렀다."], ["‘Oh my goodness me,’ said she.", "\"어머나 세상에.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘You are very obedient indeed really and it’s extremely honourable and gentlemanly in you I am sure but still at the same time if you would like to be a little tighter than that I shouldn’t consider it intruding.’", "\"당신 정말 말을 잘 들으시는군요 정말로 그건 더없이 명예롭고 신사다운 일이에요 분명히요 하지만 그래도 동시에 그것보다 좀 더 꽉 안고 싶으시다면 저는 그걸 주제넘은 일이라고 여기진 않겠어요.\""]], [["In this preposterous attitude, unspeakably at variance with his anxious mind, Clennam descended to the basement of the house; finding that wherever it became darker than elsewhere, Flora became heavier, and that when the house was lightest she was too.", "그 어처구니없는 자세로, 그의 불안한 마음과는 이루 말할 수 없이 어긋난 채로, 클레넘은 집의 지하층으로 내려갔다. 그러면서 그는, 어디든 다른 데보다 더 어두워지는 곳에서는 플로라가 더 무거워지고, 집 안이 가장 환할 때도 그녀가 역시 무겁다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Returning from the dismal kitchen regions, which were as dreary as they could be, Mistress Affery passed with the light into his father’s old room, and then into the old dining-room; always passing on before like a phantom that was not to be overtaken, and neither turning nor answering when he whispered, ‘Affery!", "더없이 음산하기 짝이 없는 그 우울한 부엌 구역에서 돌아 나오며, 애프리 부인은 등불을 들고 그의 아버지의 옛 방으로, 이어 옛 식당으로 옮겨 갔다. 그녀는 늘 따라잡을 수 없는 유령처럼 앞서 지나가기만 했고, 그가 \"애프리!"], ["I want to speak to you!’", "당신에게 할 말이 있어요!\" 하고 속삭여도 돌아보지도, 대답하지도 않았다."]], [["In the dining-room, a sentimental desire came over Flora to look into the dragon closet which had so often swallowed Arthur in the days of his boyhood--not improbably because, as a very dark closet, it was a likely place to be heavy in.", "식당에서, 플로라는 소년 시절의 아서를 그토록 자주 삼켜 버리곤 했던 그 용(龍)의 벽장을 들여다보고 싶다는 감상에 사로잡혔다 — 그곳이 무척 어두운 벽장이라 무겁게 굴기에 알맞은 자리였으니 아마 그래서였으리라."], ["Arthur, fast subsiding into despair, had opened it, when a knock was heard at the outer door.", "절망에 빠르게 가라앉고 있던 아서가 그 벽장을 막 열었을 때, 바깥문에서 노크 소리가 들렸다."]], [["Mistress Affery, with a suppressed cry, threw her apron over her head.", "애프리 부인은 비명을 억누르며 앞치마를 머리 위로 뒤집어썼다."]], [["‘What?", "\"뭐야?"], ["You want another dose!’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "한 차례 더 맞고 싶은 게로군!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘You shall have it, my woman, you shall have a good one!", "\"그래, 받게 해 주지, 이 여편네야, 호되게 한 방 받게 해 주겠어!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["You shall have a sneezer, you shall have a teaser!’", "코가 뻥 뚫리게, 정신이 번쩍 들게 해 주마!\""]], [["‘In the meantime is anybody going to the door?’ said Arthur.", "\"그 사이에 누가 문을 열러 가긴 합니까?\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘In the meantime, _I_ am going to the door, sir,’ returned the old man so savagely, as to render it clear that in a choice of difficulties he felt he must go, though he would have preferred not to go.", "\"그 사이에, 내가 문을 열러 가지, 여보게.\" 노인이 어찌나 사납게 받던지, 이러지도 저러지도 못하는 형편에서 가고 싶지 않은데도 자기가 가야 한다고 느끼고 있음이 분명했다."], ["‘Stay here the while, all!", "\"다들 그동안 여기 있어!"], ["Affery, my woman, move an inch, or speak a word in your foolishness, and I’ll treble your dose!’", "애프리, 이 여편네야, 어리석게 한 치라도 움직이거나 한마디라도 지껄였다간, 받을 매를 세 배로 늘려 주마!\""]], [["The moment he was gone, Arthur released Mrs Finching: with some difficulty, by reason of that lady misunderstanding his intentions, and making arrangements with a view to tightening instead of slackening.", "그가 사라지는 순간 아서는 핀칭 부인을 풀어 주었다. 그 부인이 그의 의도를 오해하고, 느슨하게 하기는커녕 더 꽉 죄려고 채비를 갖추는 바람에, 다소 애를 먹어야 했다."]], [["‘Affery, speak to me now!’", "\"애프리, 이제 내게 말해 줘요!\""]], [["‘Don’t touch me, Arthur!’ she cried, shrinking from him.", "\"내게 손대지 마요, 아서!\" 그녀가 그에게서 움츠러들며 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t come near me.", "\"가까이 오지 마요."], ["He’ll see you.", "그가 당신을 볼 거예요."], ["Jeremiah will.", "제러마이아가요."], ["Don’t.’", "그러지 마요.\""]], [["‘He can’t see me,’ returned Arthur, suiting the action to the word, ‘if I blow the candle out.’", "\"그가 나를 볼 수는 없어요.\" 아서가 말에 맞춰 행동하며 받았다. \"내가 촛불을 끄면요.\""]], [["‘He’ll hear you,’ cried Affery.", "\"그가 당신 소리를 들을 거예요.\" 애프리가 외쳤다."]], [["‘He can’t hear me,’ returned Arthur, suiting the action to the words again, ‘if I draw you into this black closet, and speak here.", "\"그가 내 소리를 들을 수는 없어요.\" 아서가 다시 말에 맞춰 행동하며 받았다."], ["Why do you hide your face?’", "\"내가 당신을 이 검은 벽장 안으로 끌어들여 여기서 말하면요. 왜 얼굴을 가리는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Because I am afraid of seeing something.’", "\"무언가를 보게 될까 봐 두려워서요.\""]], [["‘You can’t be afraid of seeing anything in this darkness, Affery.’", "\"이 어둠 속에서는 아무것도 보게 될 리 없어요, 애프리.\""]], [["‘Yes I am.", "\"아니에요, 두려워요."], ["Much more than if it was light.’", "밝을 때보다 훨씬 더요.\""]], [["‘Why are you afraid?’", "\"왜 두려운데요?\""]], [["‘Because the house is full of mysteries and secrets; because it’s full of whisperings and counsellings; because it’s full of noises.", "\"이 집은 수수께끼와 비밀로 가득하니까요. 속삭임과 모의로 가득하니까요."], ["There never was such a house for noises.", "온갖 소리로 가득하니까요. 이런 소리투성이 집은 또 없을 거예요."], ["I shall die of ‘em, if Jeremiah don’t strangle me first.", "제러마이아가 먼저 날 목 졸라 죽이지 않는다면, 난 그 소리들 때문에 죽고 말 거예요."], ["As I expect he will.’", "그가 그럴 것 같지만요.\""]], [["‘I have never heard any noises here, worth speaking of.’", "\"여기서 그럴듯한 소리는 한 번도 들어 본 적이 없는데요.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But you would, though, if you lived in the house, and was obliged to go about it as I am,’ said Affery; ‘and you’d feel that they was so well worth speaking of, that you’d feel you was nigh bursting through not being allowed to speak of ‘em.", "하지만 당신도 이 집에 살면서 나처럼 집 안을 돌아다녀야 한다면 들을걸요.\" 애프리가 말했다."], ["Here’s Jeremiah!", "\"그리고 그 소리들이 어찌나 입에 올릴 만한지, 입에 올리는 게 허락되지 않아서 그 때문에 터져 버릴 것만 같다고 느낄걸요."], ["You’ll get me killed.’", "제러마이아가 와요! 당신 때문에 내가 죽고 말 거예요.\""]], [["‘My good Affery, I solemnly declare to you that I can see the light of the open door on the pavement of the hall, and so could you if you would uncover your face and look.’", "\"이봐요, 애프리, 내가 엄숙히 맹세하건대, 나는 현관 바닥에 열린 문에서 들어오는 불빛이 보여요. 당신도 얼굴을 가린 걸 벗고 보면 보일 거예요.\""]], [["‘I durstn’t do it,’ said Affery, ‘I durstn’t never, Arthur. I’m always blind-folded when Jeremiah an’t a looking, and sometimes even when he is.’", "\"난 그럴 엄두가 안 나요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"절대 못 해요, 아서. 제러마이아가 안 볼 때면 난 늘 눈을 가리고 있어요. 가끔은 그가 볼 때조차도요.\""]], [["‘He cannot shut the door without my seeing him,’ said Arthur.", "\"내가 보지 않고서는 그가 문을 닫을 수 없어요.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘You are as safe with me as if he was fifty miles away.’", "\"그가 오십 마일 떨어진 데 있는 것이나 마찬가지로 당신은 나와 함께 있으면 안전해요.\""]], [["[‘I wish he was!’ cried Affery.)", "[\"그가 그랬으면 좋겠어요!\" 애프리가 외쳤다.]"]], [["‘Affery, I want to know what is amiss here; I want some light thrown on the secrets of this house.’", "\"애프리, 나는 여기서 무엇이 잘못되어 있는지 알고 싶어요. 이 집의 비밀에 약간의 빛을 비추고 싶어요.\""]], [["‘I tell you, Arthur,’ she interrupted, ‘noises is the secrets, rustlings and stealings about, tremblings, treads overhead and treads underneath.’", "\"말했잖아요, 아서,\" 그녀가 가로막았다. \"소리들이 바로 그 비밀이에요. 바스락거리고 살금살금 돌아다니는 소리, 부르르 떠는 소리, 머리 위에서 나는 발소리, 발밑에서 나는 발소리요.\""]], [["‘But those are not all the secrets.’", "\"하지만 그게 비밀의 전부는 아닐 텐데요.\""]], [["‘I don’t know,’ said Affery.", "\"난 몰라요.\" 애프리가 말했다."], ["‘Don’t ask me no more.", "\"더는 내게 묻지 마요."], ["Your old sweetheart an’t far off, and she’s a blabber.’", "당신 옛 연인이 멀지 않은 데 있어요, 그 사람은 입이 가볍거든요.\""]], [["His old sweetheart, being in fact so near at hand that she was then reclining against him in a flutter, a very substantial angle of forty-five degrees, here interposed to assure Mistress Affery with greater earnestness than directness of asseveration, that what she heard should go no further, but should be kept inviolate, ‘if on no other account on Arthur’s--sensible of intruding in being too familiar Doyce and Clennam’s.’", "그의 옛 연인은 사실 손 닿을 만큼 가까이, 그때 마흔다섯 도쯤의 아주 실팍한 각도로 그에게 기댄 채 안절부절못하고 있었는데, 여기서 끼어들어 애프리 부인에게, 단호함보다는 간곡함이 앞서는 단언으로 확언했다. 자기가 듣는 것은 결코 더 멀리 새어 나가지 않고 어김없이 비밀로 지켜질 것이라고, \"다른 무엇 때문이 아니라도 아서 — 너무 허물없이 군 점은 주제넘은 줄 알지만 — 도이스 앤드 클레넘 상사를 봐서라도요.\""]], [["‘I make an imploring appeal to you, Affery, to you, one of the few agreeable early remembrances I have, for my mother’s sake, for your husband’s sake, for my own, for all our sakes.", "\"애프리, 당신에게 간곡히 호소합니다. 내게 몇 안 되는 즐거운 옛 기억 가운데 하나인 당신에게요."], ["I am sure you can tell me something connected with the coming here of this man, if you will.’", "내 어머니를 위해서, 당신 남편을 위해서, 나 자신을 위해서, 우리 모두를 위해서요. 마음만 먹으면 당신은 이 사내가 여기 온 것과 관련된 무언가를 내게 말해 줄 수 있을 거라고 확신해요.\""]], [["‘Why, then I’ll tell you, Arthur,’ returned Affery--‘Jeremiah’s coming!’", "\"그럼, 말해 주죠, 아서,\" 애프리가 받았다. \"제러마이아가 와요!\""]], [["‘No, indeed he is not.", "\"아니, 정말로 오지 않아요."], ["The door is open, and he is standing outside, talking.’", "문이 열려 있고, 그는 밖에 서서 이야기를 나누고 있어요.\""]], [["‘I’ll tell you then,’ said Affery, after listening, ‘that the first time he ever come he heard the noises his own self.", "\"그럼 말해 주죠,\" 애프리가 귀를 기울인 뒤 말했다."], ["“What’s that?” he said to me.", "\"그가 처음 왔을 때 그 소리들을 자기 귀로 직접 들었어요."], ["“I don’t know what it is,”", "'저게 뭐야?' 하고 그가 내게 물었어요."], ["I says to him, catching hold of him, “but I have heard it over and over again.”", "'저게 뭔지 나도 몰라요,' 하고 내가 그를 붙들며 말했어요, '하지만 몇 번이고 들었어요.'"], ["While I says it, he stands a looking at me, all of a shake, he do.’", "내가 그 말을 하는 동안 그는 온통 부들부들 떨면서 나를 빤히 쳐다보고 서 있었어요, 정말로요.\""]], [["‘Has he been here often?’", "\"그가 여기 자주 왔나요?\""]], [["‘Only that night, and the last night.’", "\"그날 밤과 마지막 밤뿐이었어요.\""]], [["‘What did you see of him on the last night, after I was gone?’", "\"마지막 밤에, 내가 가고 난 뒤 그에게서 무얼 보았나요?\""]], [["‘Them two clever ones had him all alone to themselves.", "\"그 영리한 두 사람이 그를 자기들끼리만 데리고 있었어요."], ["Jeremiah come a dancing at me sideways, after I had let you out (he always comes a dancing at me sideways when he’s going to hurt me), and he said to me, “Now, Affery,”", "내가 당신을 내보낸 뒤, 제러마이아가 옆걸음으로 내게 춤추듯 다가왔어요(나를 해치려 할 때면 그는 늘 옆걸음으로 춤추듯 다가오거든요). 그러고는 내게 말했어요, '자, 애프리,'"], ["he said, “I am a coming behind you, my woman, and a going to run you up.”", "하고요, '네 뒤로 다가가서, 이 여편네야, 너를 위로 몰아붙여 주마.'"], ["So he took and squeezed the back of my neck in his hand, till it made me open my mouth, and then he pushed me before him to bed, squeezing all the way.", "그러더니 손으로 내 목덜미를 움켜쥐고 짓눌러서, 내가 입을 떡 벌릴 때까지 그러더니, 내내 짓누르면서 나를 앞세워 침대로 떠밀었어요."], ["That’s what he calls running me up, he do.", "그게 그가 말하는 '위로 몰아붙인다'는 거예요, 정말로요."], ["Oh, he’s a wicked one!’", "오, 그는 사악한 인간이에요!\""]], [["‘And did you hear or see no more, Affery?’", "\"그리고 더는 듣지도 보지도 못했나요, 애프리?\""]], [["‘Don’t I tell you I was sent to bed, Arthur!", "\"내가 침대로 보내졌다고 하잖아요, 아서!"], ["Here he is!’", "그가 와요!\""]], [["‘I assure you he is still at the door.", "\"분명히 말하지만 그는 아직 문가에 있어요."], ["Those whisperings and counsellings, Affery, that you have spoken of.", "당신이 말한 그 속삭임과 모의 말이에요, 애프리."], ["What are they?’", "그게 다 뭐예요?\""]], [["‘How should I know?", "\"내가 어떻게 알겠어요?"], ["Don’t ask me nothing about ‘em, Arthur.", "그것들에 대해선 아무것도 묻지 마요, 아서."], ["Get away!’", "저리 가요!\""]], [["‘But my dear Affery; unless I can gain some insight into these hidden things, in spite of your husband and in spite of my mother, ruin will come of it.’", "\"하지만 애프리, 내가 당신 남편을 무릅쓰고 또 내 어머니를 무릅쓰고 이 숨겨진 일들을 얼마간이라도 들여다보지 못한다면, 거기서 파멸이 올 거예요.\""]], [["‘Don’t ask me nothing,’ repeated Affery.", "\"내게 아무것도 묻지 마요.\" 애프리가 되풀이했다."], ["‘I have been in a dream for ever so long.", "\"난 아주 오랫동안 꿈속에 있었어요."], ["Go away, go away!’", "저리 가요, 저리 가요!\""]], [["‘You said that before,’ returned Arthur.", "\"아까도 그렇게 말했어요.\" 아서가 받았다."], ["‘You used the same expression that night, at the door, when I asked you what was going on here.", "\"그날 밤 문가에서, 내가 여기서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있느냐고 물었을 때도 똑같은 표현을 썼지요."], ["What do you mean by being in a dream?’", "꿈속에 있다는 게 무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["‘I an’t a going to tell you.", "\"말 안 할 거예요."], ["Get away!", "저리 가요!"], ["I shouldn’t tell you, if you was by yourself; much less with your old sweetheart here.’", "당신 혼자였어도 말 안 할 텐데, 하물며 당신 옛 연인이 여기 있는데요.\""]], [["It was equally vain for Arthur to entreat, and for Flora to protest.", "아서가 애원하는 것도, 플로라가 항변하는 것도 똑같이 헛수고였다."], ["Affery, who had been trembling and struggling the whole time, turned a deaf ear to all adjuration, and was bent on forcing herself out of the closet.", "애프리는 내내 부들부들 떨며 몸부림치다가, 모든 간청에 귀를 막고, 기어이 벽장 밖으로 몸을 빼내려고 작정했다."]], [["‘I’d sooner scream to Jeremiah than say another word!", "\"한마디라도 더 하느니 차라리 제러마이아한테 비명을 지르겠어요!"], ["I’ll call out to him, Arthur, if you don’t give over speaking to me.", "그를 부를 거예요, 아서, 당신이 내게 말 거는 걸 그만두지 않으면요."], ["Now here’s the very last word I’ll say afore I call to him--If ever you begin to get the better of them two clever ones your own self (you ought to it, as I told you when you first come home, for you haven’t been a living here long years, to be made afeared of your life as I have), then do you get the better of ‘em afore my face; and then do you say to me, Affery tell your dreams!", "자, 이제 그를 부르기 전에 정말로 마지막으로 할 말이 있어요 — 만일 당신이 그 영리한 두 사람을 당신 손으로 직접 이기기 시작한다면(당신이 처음 집에 돌아왔을 때 내가 말했듯이 그래야 마땅해요, 당신은 나처럼 오랜 세월 여기 살면서 목숨이 두렵도록 만들어진 게 아니니까요), 그럼 내 눈앞에서 그들을 이기세요. 그러고 나서 내게 말해 줘요, 애프리, 네 꿈들을 이야기해 봐, 하고요!"], ["Maybe, then I’ll tell ‘em!’", "그럼, 어쩌면 그때 이야기해 줄지도 몰라요!\""]], [["The shutting of the door stopped Arthur from replying.", "문이 닫히는 소리에 아서는 대답을 멈췄다."], ["They glided into the places where Jeremiah had left them; and Clennam, stepping forward as that old gentleman returned, informed him that he had accidentally extinguished the candle.", "그들은 제러마이아가 두고 떠난 자리로 미끄러져 돌아갔고, 클레넘은 그 노인이 돌아오자 앞으로 나서며, 자기가 실수로 촛불을 꺼뜨렸노라고 일러 주었다."], ["Mr Flintwinch looked on as he re-lighted it at the lamp in the hall, and preserved a profound taciturnity respecting the person who had been holding him in conversation.", "플린트윈치 씨는 그가 현관의 등에 다시 불을 붙이는 것을 지켜보았고, 자기와 이야기를 나누고 있던 사람에 관해서는 깊은 침묵을 지켰다."], ["Perhaps his irascibility demanded compensation for some tediousness that the visitor had expended on him; however that was, he took such umbrage at seeing his wife with her apron over her head, that he charged at her, and taking her veiled nose between his thumb and finger, appeared to throw the whole screw-power of his person into the wring he gave it.", "어쩌면 그의 노여움은, 그 방문객이 그에게 늘어놓은 어떤 지루한 잔소리에 대한 보상을 요구하고 있었는지도 몰랐다. 어찌 됐든, 그는 아내가 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓰고 있는 것을 보고 어찌나 화가 났던지, 그녀에게 달려들어, 천에 덮인 코를 엄지와 손가락 사이에 잡고는, 제 온몸의 비트는 힘을 다 쏟아 비틀어 대는 듯했다."]], [["Flora, now permanently heavy, did not release Arthur from the survey of the house, until it had extended even to his old garret bedchamber.", "이제 줄곧 무거워진 플로라는, 그 둘러봄이 마침내 그의 옛 다락방 침실에까지 미칠 때까지 아서를 집 구경에서 풀어 주지 않았다."], ["His thoughts were otherwise occupied than with the tour of inspection; yet he took particular notice at the time, as he afterwards had occasion to remember, of the airlessness and closeness of the house; that they left the track of their footsteps in the dust on the upper floors; and that there was a resistance to the opening of one room door, which occasioned Affery to cry out that somebody was hiding inside, and to continue to believe so, though somebody was sought and not discovered.", "그의 생각은 그 시찰 행차가 아닌 다른 데 쏠려 있었다. 그래도 그는 그때, 나중에 떠올릴 일이 생기게 되거니와, 집 안의 환기 안 되는 답답함과 갑갑함을, 그들이 위층 바닥들의 먼지 위에 발자국 자취를 남긴 것을, 그리고 어느 방문은 여는 데 저항이 있어서 애프리가 안에 누가 숨어 있다고 외쳐 댔고, 누군가를 찾았으나 발견되지 않았는데도 여전히 그렇게 믿고 있었던 것을, 유독 눈여겨보았다."], ["When they at last returned to his mother’s room, they found her shading her face with her muffled hand, and talking in a low voice to the Patriarch as he stood before the fire, whose blue eyes, polished head, and silken locks, turning towards them as they came in, imparted an inestimable value and inexhaustible love of his species to his remark:", "마침내 그들이 그의 어머니의 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 그녀는 천에 감싼 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채, 난로 앞에 선 가부장과 낮은 목소리로 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 그들이 들어서자 가부장의 파란 눈과 반들거리는 머리와 비단결 같은 머리타래가 그들 쪽으로 돌려졌고, 그것이 그의 말에 헤아릴 수 없는 가치와 동족에 대한 마르지 않는 사랑을 부여했다."]], [["‘So you have been seeing the premises, seeing the premises--premises-- seeing the premises!’", "\"그래, 집을 둘러보고 오셨군요, 집을 둘러보고 — 집을 — 집을 둘러보고 오셨군요!\""]], [["It was not in itself a jewel of benevolence or wisdom, yet he made it an exemplar of both that one would have liked to have a copy of.", "그 자체로는 자비의 보석도 지혜의 보석도 아니었으나, 그는 그것을 둘 다의 본보기로 만들어, 누구라도 한 부 베껴 두고 싶어질 만한 것으로 빚어냈다."]], [["# CHAPTER 24. The Evening of a Long Day", "# CHAPTER 24. The Evening of a Long Day"]], [["That illustrious man and great national ornament, Mr Merdle, continued his shining course.", "저 명망 높은 인물이자 위대한 국가적 장식물인 머들 씨는 빛나는 행로를 이어 갔다."], ["It began to be widely understood that one who had done society the admirable service of making so much money out of it, could not be suffered to remain a commoner.", "사회에서 그토록 많은 돈을 벌어 줌으로써 사회에 그토록 훌륭한 봉사를 한 사람을 평민으로 남겨 둘 수는 없다는 인식이 널리 퍼지기 시작했다."], ["A baronetcy was spoken of with confidence; a peerage was frequently mentioned.", "준남작 작위가 자신 있게 거론되었고, 귀족 작위도 자주 입에 오르내렸다."], ["Rumour had it that Mr Merdle had set his golden face against a baronetcy; that he had plainly intimated to Lord Decimus that a baronetcy was not enough for him; that he had said, ‘No--a Peerage, or plain Merdle.’ This was reported to have plunged Lord Decimus as nigh to his noble chin in a slough of doubts as so lofty a person could be sunk.", "소문에 따르면 머들 씨는 준남작 작위에 대해 그 황금빛 얼굴을 굳혔으며, 데시머스 경에게 준남작 작위로는 자신에게 충분치 않다고 분명히 비쳤고, \"아니오 — 귀족 작위든가, 아니면 그냥 머들이든가\" 하고 말했다는 것이었다."], ["For the Barnacles, as a group of themselves in creation, had an idea that such distinctions belonged to them; and that when a soldier, sailor, or lawyer became ennobled, they let him in, as it were, by an act of condescension, at the family door, and immediately shut it again.", "이 말은 그토록 고귀한 인물이 가라앉을 수 있는 한 가장 깊이, 그 고귀한 턱밑까지 데시머스 경을 의혹의 수렁에 빠뜨렸다고 전해졌다. 왜냐하면 배너클 일가는 그 자체로 창조의 한 무리로서, 그러한 영예가 자기들에게 속한다는 생각을 품고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Not only (said Rumour) had the troubled Decimus his own hereditary part in this impression, but he also knew of several Barnacle claims already on the file, which came into collision with that of the master spirit.", "군인이나 선원이나 법률가가 작위를 받게 되면, 그들은 말하자면 겸양의 행위로써 그 사람을 가문의 문턱으로 들여보냈다가 곧 다시 문을 닫아 버린다는 식이었다. (소문에 의하면) 곤혹스러운 데시머스에게는 이 인식에 대한 자신의 세습적 몫이 있을 뿐 아니라, 그는 이미 명부에 올라 있는 몇몇 배너클 가문의 청구가 그 거장의 청구와 충돌한다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Right or wrong, Rumour was very busy; and Lord Decimus, while he was, or was supposed to be, in stately excogitation of the difficulty, lent her some countenance by taking, on several public occasions, one of those elephantine trots of his through a jungle of overgrown sentences, waving Mr Merdle about on his trunk as Gigantic Enterprise, The Wealth of England, Elasticity, Credit, Capital, Prosperity, and all manner of blessings.", "옳든 그르든, 소문은 무척 바빴다. 그리고 데시머스 경은 그 난제를 위풍당당하게 궁리하는 중이거나 그러하리라 여겨지는 동안, 여러 공식 석상에서 무성하게 자라난 문장들의 정글을 헤치며 코끼리처럼 육중하게 종종걸음을 치고, 머들 씨를 그 코로 거대한 사업, 영국의 부, 탄력성, 신용, 자본, 번영, 그리고 온갖 종류의 축복으로 흔들어 보임으로써 소문에 다소 신빙성을 더해 주었다."]], [["So quietly did the mowing of the old scythe go on, that fully three months had passed unnoticed since the two English brothers had been laid in one tomb in the strangers’ cemetery at Rome.", "낡은 큰 낫의 베어 넘김은 어찌나 조용히 진행되었던지, 두 영국인 형제가 로마의 외국인 묘지 한 무덤에 나란히 묻힌 이래로 꼬박 석 달이 알아채이지 않은 채 흘러갔다."], ["Mr and Mrs Sparkler were established in their own house: a little mansion, rather of the Tite Barnacle class, quite a triumph of inconvenience, with a perpetual smell in it of the day before yesterday’s soup and coach-horses, but extremely dear, as being exactly in the centre of the habitable globe.", "스파클러 부부는 자기들 집에 자리를 잡았다. 그것은 자그마한 저택으로, 다분히 타이트 배너클 부류에 속하는, 불편함의 완벽한 승리이자, 그제 끓인 수프와 마차용 말 냄새가 영영 가시지 않는 곳이었으나, 사람이 살 만한 지구의 정중앙에 위치한다는 이유로 무척이나 비쌌다."], ["In this enviable abode (and envied it really was by many people), Mrs Sparkler had intended to proceed at once to the demolition of the Bosom, when active hostilities had been suspended by the arrival of the Courier with his tidings of death.", "이 부러움 살 만한 거처에서(실제로도 많은 사람이 부러워했다) 스파클러 부인은 즉시 그 가슴을 허물어뜨리는 일에 착수할 작정이었으나, 그 활발한 적대 행위는 부고를 전하는 급사가 도착하는 바람에 중단되었다."], ["Mrs Sparkler, who was not unfeeling, had received them with a violent burst of grief, which had lasted twelve hours; after which, she had arisen to see about her mourning, and to take every precaution that could ensure its being as becoming as Mrs Merdle’s.", "무정한 사람은 아니었던 스파클러 부인은 그 소식을 격렬한 슬픔의 폭발로 받아들였고, 그것은 열두 시간 동안 지속되었다. 그 후 그녀는 일어나 상복을 챙기고, 그것이 머들 부인의 것만큼이나 잘 어울리도록 보장할 수 있는 온갖 예방 조치를 취했다."], ["A gloom was then cast over more than one distinguished family (according to the politest sources of intelligence), and the Courier went back again.", "그리하여(가장 점잖은 소식통에 따르면) 한 명 이상의 저명한 가문에 우울이 드리워졌고, 급사는 다시 돌아갔다."]], [["Mr and Mrs Sparkler had been dining alone, with their gloom cast over them, and Mrs Sparkler reclined on a drawing-room sofa.", "스파클러 부부는 자기들에게 드리운 우울 속에서 단둘이 만찬을 마쳤고, 스파클러 부인은 응접실 소파에 기대어 누워 있었다."], ["It was a hot summer Sunday evening.", "무더운 여름 일요일 저녁이었다."], ["The residence in the centre of the habitable globe, at all times stuffed and close as if it had an incurable cold in its head, was that evening particularly stifling.", "사람이 살 만한 지구의 정중앙에 자리한 그 거처는, 머리에 고칠 수 없는 감기라도 든 양 늘 답답하고 막혀 있었지만, 그날 저녁은 유독 숨이 막혔다."], ["The bells of the churches had done their worst in the way of clanging among the unmelodious echoes of the streets, and the lighted windows of the churches had ceased to be yellow in the grey dusk, and had died out opaque black.", "교회들의 종은 거리의 가락 없는 메아리 속에서 땡땡거리며 그 최악의 짓을 다 해 놓았고, 교회들의 불 밝힌 창들은 잿빛 어스름 속에서 노란빛을 잃고는 불투명한 검정으로 사그라들었다."], ["Mrs Sparkler, lying on her sofa, looking through an open window at the opposite side of a narrow street over boxes of mignonette and flowers, was tired of the view.", "스파클러 부인은 소파에 누워, 열린 창 너머로 좁은 길 맞은편을, 목서초와 꽃들이 담긴 상자들 위로 바라보다가 그 풍경에 싫증이 났다."], ["Mrs Sparkler, looking at another window where her husband stood in the balcony, was tired of that view.", "스파클러 부인은 남편이 발코니에 서 있는 다른 창을 바라보다가 그 풍경에도 싫증이 났다."], ["Mrs Sparkler, looking at herself in her mourning, was even tired of that view: though, naturally, not so tired of that as of the other two.", "스파클러 부인은 상복 차림의 자기 자신을 바라보다가 그 풍경에마저 싫증이 났다. 물론 그 풍경에는 앞의 두 가지만큼 싫증이 나지는 않았지만 말이다."]], [["‘It’s like lying in a well,’ said Mrs Sparkler, changing her position fretfully.", "\"우물 속에 누워 있는 것 같아.\" 스파클러 부인이 짜증스레 자세를 바꾸며 말했다."], ["‘Dear me, Edmund, if you have anything to say, why don’t you say it?’", "\"어머나, 에드먼드, 할 말이 있으면 왜 하지 않는 거예요?\""]], [["Mr Sparkler might have replied with ingenuousness, ‘My life, I have nothing to say.’ But, as the repartee did not occur to him, he contented himself with coming in from the balcony and standing at the side of his wife’s couch.", "스파클러 씨는 솔직하게 \"여보, 나는 할 말이 없소\" 하고 대꾸할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 재치 있는 말이 떠오르지 않았으므로, 그는 발코니에서 들어와 아내의 침상 옆에 서는 것으로 만족했다."]], [["‘Good gracious, Edmund!’ said Mrs Sparkler more fretfully still, ‘you are absolutely putting mignonette up your nose!", "\"세상에, 에드먼드!\" 스파클러 부인이 더욱 짜증스레 말했다."], ["Pray don’t!’", "\"당신, 지금 목서초를 코에 처박고 있잖아요! 제발 그러지 마요!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler, in absence of mind--perhaps in a more literal absence of mind than is usually understood by the phrase--had smelt so hard at a sprig in his hand as to be on the verge of the offence in question.", "스파클러 씨는 정신이 딴 데 가 있던 차에 — 어쩌면 흔히 그 표현으로 이해되는 것보다 더 문자 그대로 정신이 나간 채로 — 손에 든 작은 가지를 너무 세게 냄새 맡다가 그만 문제의 그 무례 직전에 이르렀던 것이다."], ["He smiled, said, ‘I ask your pardon, my dear,’ and threw it out of window.", "그는 미소 지으며 \"미안하오, 여보\" 하고는 그것을 창밖으로 던졌다."]], [["‘You make my head ache by remaining in that position, Edmund,’ said Mrs Sparkler, raising her eyes to him after another minute; ‘you look so aggravatingly large by this light.", "\"당신이 그 자세로 서 있으면 머리가 아파요, 에드먼드.\" 스파클러 부인이 또 일 분쯤 지난 뒤 그를 올려다보며 말했다."], ["Do sit down.’", "\"이 불빛 아래서 당신은 정말이지 거슬리게 커 보여요. 좀 앉아요.\""]], [["‘Certainly, my dear,’ said Mr Sparkler, and took a chair on the same spot.", "\"그러지, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 말하며 바로 그 자리에 의자를 가져다 앉았다."]], [["‘If I didn’t know that the longest day was past,’ said Fanny, yawning in a dreary manner, ‘I should have felt certain this was the longest day.", "\"가장 긴 날이 이미 지났다는 걸 몰랐다면,\" 패니가 음울하게 하품하며 말했다."], ["I never did experience such a day.’", "\"오늘이 분명 가장 긴 날이라고 확신했을 거예요. 이런 날은 한 번도 겪어 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["‘Is that your fan, my love?’ asked Mr Sparkler, picking up one and presenting it.", "\"이게 당신 부채요, 여보?\" 스파클러 씨가 부채 하나를 집어 건네며 물었다."]], [["‘Edmund,’ returned his wife, more wearily yet, ‘don’t ask weak questions, I entreat you not.", "\"에드먼드,\" 그의 아내가 한층 더 지친 어조로 대꾸했다."], ["Whose can it be but mine?’", "\"제발 부탁이니 어리석은 질문은 하지 말아요. 내 것 말고 누구 것이겠어요?\""]], [["‘Yes, I thought it was yours,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"그래, 당신 것인 줄 알았소.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Then you shouldn’t ask,’ retorted Fanny. After a little while she turned on her sofa and exclaimed, ‘Dear me, dear me, there never was such a long day as this!’ After another little while, she got up slowly, walked about, and came back again.", "\"그러면 묻지 말았어야지요.\" 패니가 쏘아붙였다. 잠시 후 그녀는 소파에서 몸을 돌리며 외쳤다. \"어머나, 어머나, 이렇게 긴 날은 또 없었어!\" 또 잠시 후 그녀는 천천히 일어나 방 안을 거닐다가 다시 돌아왔다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr Sparkler, flashing with an original conception, ‘I think you must have got the fidgets.’", "\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 기발한 착상이 번뜩이는 표정으로 말했다. \"당신, 좀 안절부절못하는 것 같소.\""]], [["‘Oh, Fidgets!’ repeated Mrs Sparkler.", "\"어머, 안절부절!\" 스파클러 부인이 되받았다."], ["‘Don’t.’", "\"그러지 마요.\""]], [["‘My adorable girl,’ urged Mr Sparkler, ‘try your aromatic vinegar.", "\"내 사랑스러운 여인,\" 스파클러 씨가 권했다."], ["I have often seen my mother try it, and it seemingly refreshed her.", "\"방향초 식초를 좀 써 봐요. 내 어머니가 그걸 쓰는 걸 자주 봤는데, 보아하니 기운을 회복하시더군."], ["And she is, as I believe you are aware, a remarkably fine woman, with no non--’", "그리고 당신도 알다시피, 어머니는 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없는 참으로—\""]], [["‘Good Gracious!’ exclaimed Fanny, starting up again.", "\"맙소사!\" 패니가 또다시 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."], ["‘It’s beyond all patience!", "\"더는 못 참겠어!"], ["This is the most wearisome day that ever did dawn upon the world, I am certain.’", "이날은 세상에 동튼 날 중에서 가장 지긋지긋한 날이에요, 분명히.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler looked meekly after her as she lounged about the room, and he appeared to be a little frightened.", "스파클러 씨는 아내가 방 안을 어슬렁거리는 것을 온순하게 지켜보았고, 다소 겁먹은 듯이 보였다."], ["When she had tossed a few trifles about, and had looked down into the darkening street out of all the three windows, she returned to her sofa, and threw herself among its pillows.", "그녀가 자질구레한 물건 몇 개를 이리저리 던지고, 세 창문 모두로 어두워져 가는 거리를 내려다본 뒤, 그녀는 소파로 돌아와 쿠션들 사이로 몸을 던졌다."]], [["‘Now Edmund, come here!", "\"자 에드먼드, 이리 와요!"], ["Come a little nearer, because I want to be able to touch you with my fan, that I may impress you very much with what I am going to say.", "좀 더 가까이 와요. 부채로 당신을 건드릴 수 있어야 내가 하려는 말을 당신 머리에 단단히 박아 넣을 테니까."], ["That will do.", "됐어요. 아주 가깝군요."], ["Quite close enough. Oh, you _do_ look so big!’", "어머, 당신은 정말 어쩌면 그렇게 커 보일까!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler apologised for the circumstance, pleaded that he couldn’t help it, and said that ‘our fellows,’ without more particularly indicating whose fellows, used to call him by the name of Quinbus Flestrin, Junior, or the Young Man Mountain.", "스파클러 씨는 그 점에 대해 사과하며, 어쩔 도리가 없다고 변명하고는, \"우리 패거리\"가 — 누구의 패거리인지는 더 구체적으로 밝히지 않은 채 — 자기를 퀸버스 플레스트린 주니어, 또는 젊은 인간 산이라는 이름으로 부르곤 했다고 말했다."]], [["‘You ought to have told me so before,’ Fanny complained.", "\"진작 그 얘기를 했어야지요.\" 패니가 불평했다."]], [["‘My dear,’ returned Mr Sparkler, rather gratified, ‘I didn’t know It would interest you, or I would have made a point of telling you.’", "\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 다소 흐뭇해하며 대꾸했다. \"그게 당신 흥미를 끌 줄 몰랐소. 알았더라면 꼭 얘기했을 텐데.\""]], [["‘There!", "\"됐어요!"], ["For goodness sake, don’t talk,’ said Fanny; ‘I want to talk, myself.", "제발이지, 말하지 마요.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["Edmund, we must not be alone any more.", "\"내가 직접 말하고 싶으니까."], ["I must take such precautions as will prevent my being ever again reduced to the state of dreadful depression in which I am this evening.’", "에드먼드, 우리 더는 단둘이 있어선 안 돼요. 오늘 저녁 내가 빠진 이 끔찍한 우울의 상태로 다시는 떨어지지 않도록 그런 예방 조치를 취해야겠어요.\""]], [["‘My dear,’ answered Mr Sparkler; ‘being as you are well known to be, a remarkably fine woman with no--’", "\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 대답했다. \"당신은 잘 알려졌다시피, 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없는 참으로 훌륭한 여인이—\""]], [["‘Oh, good GRACIOUS!’ cried Fanny.", "\"아이, 맙소사!\" 패니가 소리쳤다."]], [["Mr Sparkler was so discomposed by the energy of this exclamation, accompanied with a flouncing up from the sofa and a flouncing down again, that a minute or two elapsed before he felt himself equal to saying in explanation:", "스파클러 씨는 이 외침의 기세에, 그리고 그것에 따라온 소파에서 펄쩍 일어났다 펄쩍 다시 앉는 동작에 어찌나 당황했던지, 일이 분이 지나서야 비로소 변명조로 이렇게 말할 수 있을 만한 상태가 되었다."]], [["‘I mean, my dear, that everybody knows you are calculated to shine in society.’", "\"내 말은, 여보, 당신이 사교계에서 빛나도록 타고난 사람이라는 걸 누구나 안다는 거요.\""]], [["‘Calculated to shine in society,’ retorted Fanny with great irritability; ‘yes, indeed!", "\"사교계에서 빛나도록 타고났다고요.\" 패니가 몹시 신경질적으로 되받았다."], ["And then what happens?", "\"그래요, 정말이지! 그런데 그래서 어떻게 되었나요?"], ["I no sooner recover, in a visiting point of view, the shock of poor dear papa’s death, and my poor uncle’s--though I do not disguise from myself that the last was a happy release, for, if you are not presentable you had much better die--’", "불쌍한 아버지의 죽음과 가엾은 삼촌의 죽음이 준 충격에서 방문 다닐 만큼 막 회복하자마자 — 물론 마지막 건 다행스러운 해방이었다는 걸 스스로 부정하지는 않지만요. 남 앞에 내놓을 만하지 못하다면 차라리 죽는 편이 훨씬 나으니까—\""]], [["‘You are not referring to me, my love, I hope?’ Mr Sparkler humbly interrupted.", "\"여보, 설마 나를 두고 하는 말은 아니겠지요?\" 스파클러 씨가 겸손하게 끼어들었다."]], [["‘Edmund, Edmund, you would wear out a Saint.", "\"에드먼드, 에드먼드, 당신은 성인이라도 진을 빼겠어요."], ["Am I not expressly speaking of my poor uncle?’", "내가 가엾은 삼촌 얘기를 하고 있다고 분명히 말하지 않았어요?\""]], [["‘You looked with so much expression at myself, my dear girl,’ said Mr Sparkler, ‘that I felt a little uncomfortable.", "\"당신이 나를 어찌나 표정 가득 쳐다보던지, 여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["Thank you, my love.’", "\"좀 불편했소. 고맙소, 여보.\""]], [["‘Now you have put me out,’ observed Fanny with a resigned toss of her fan, ‘and I had better go to bed.’", "\"이제 당신이 내 말을 끊어 놨네요.\" 패니가 단념한 듯 부채를 휙 내저으며 말했다. \"차라리 자러 가는 게 낫겠어요.\""]], [["‘Don’t do that, my love,’ urged Mr Sparkler.", "\"그러지 마오, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 권했다."], ["‘Take time.’", "\"천천히 해요.\""]], [["Fanny took a good deal of time: lying back with her eyes shut, and her eyebrows raised with a hopeless expression as if she had utterly given up all terrestrial affairs.", "패니는 한참을 들였다. 눈을 감고 뒤로 기댄 채, 마치 이 세상사를 완전히 포기해 버린 듯 절망적인 표정으로 눈썹을 치켜올리고 있었다."], ["At length, without the slightest notice, she opened her eyes again, and recommenced in a short, sharp manner:", "마침내 아무런 예고도 없이 그녀는 다시 눈을 뜨고는 짧고 날카로운 어조로 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["‘What happens then, I ask! What happens?", "\"그래서 어떻게 되었느냐고요, 묻겠어요!"], ["Why, I find myself at the very period when I might shine most in society, and should most like for very momentous reasons to shine in society--I find myself in a situation which to a certain extent disqualifies me for going into society.", "어떻게 되었나요? 글쎄, 내가 사교계에서 가장 빛날 수 있을 바로 그 시기에, 그리고 무척 중대한 이유로 사교계에서 가장 빛나고 싶을 바로 그때에 — 어느 정도까지는 사교계에 나갈 자격을 잃게 만드는 처지에 놓인 나를 발견하게 되었지요."], ["It’s too bad, really!’", "정말 너무하잖아요!\""]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"여보,\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I don’t think it need keep you at home.’", "\"그것 때문에 집에만 있어야 한다고는 생각하지 않소.\""]], [["‘Edmund, you ridiculous creature,’ returned Fanny, with great indignation; ‘do you suppose that a woman in the bloom of youth and not wholly devoid of personal attractions, can put herself, at such a time, in competition as to figure with a woman in every other way her inferior?", "\"에드먼드, 우스꽝스러운 양반 같으니라고.\" 패니가 큰 분노를 담아 대꾸했다."], ["If you do suppose such a thing, your folly is boundless.’", "\"한창 젊음의 꽃을 피우고 있고 개인적인 매력도 전혀 없지 않은 여자가, 이런 때에, 모든 면에서 자기보다 못한 여자와 자태를 두고 경쟁에 나설 수 있다고 생각하는 거예요? 정말 그렇게 생각한다면, 당신의 어리석음은 끝이 없군요.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler submitted that he had thought ‘it might be got over.’", "스파클러 씨는 \"그건 넘어갈 수 있을지도\" 모른다고 생각했다고 조심스럽게 내놓았다."]], [["‘Got over!’ repeated Fanny, with immeasurable scorn.", "\"넘어가요!\" 패니가 헤아릴 수 없는 경멸을 담아 되풀이했다."]], [["‘For a time,’ Mr Sparkler submitted.", "\"한동안만 말이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 조심스레 덧붙였다."]], [["Honouring the last feeble suggestion with no notice, Mrs Sparkler declared with bitterness that it really was too bad, and that positively it was enough to make one wish one was dead!", "이 마지막 힘없는 제안에는 아무런 주의도 기울이지 않은 채, 스파클러 부인은 쓰디쓴 어조로 정말이지 너무한 일이며, 차라리 죽었으면 싶을 만큼 지긋지긋한 일이라고 단언했다!"]], [["‘However,’ she said, when she had in some measure recovered from her sense of personal ill-usage; ‘provoking as it is, and cruel as it seems, I suppose it must be submitted to.’", "\"하지만,\" 그녀는 개인적으로 부당한 대우를 받았다는 느낌에서 어느 정도 회복되자 말했다. \"분통이 터지고 잔인하게 여겨지긴 해도, 이건 받아들일 수밖에 없겠지요.\""]], [["‘Especially as it was to be expected,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"더구나 예상했던 일이고 말이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Edmund,’ returned his wife, ‘if you have nothing more becoming to do than to attempt to insult the woman who has honoured you with her hand, when she finds herself in adversity, I think _you_ had better go to bed!’", "\"에드먼드,\" 그의 아내가 대꾸했다. \"역경에 처한 자기 손으로 당신을 영광스럽게 해 준 여인을 모욕하려 드는 것 말고는 더 어울리는 할 일이 없다면, 차라리 당신이 자러 가는 게 낫겠어요!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler was much afflicted by the charge, and offered a most tender and earnest apology.", "스파클러 씨는 그 비난에 크게 괴로워하며, 더없이 다정하고 진지한 사과를 올렸다."], ["His apology was accepted; but Mrs Sparkler requested him to go round to the other side of the sofa and sit in the window-curtain, to tone himself down.", "그의 사과는 받아들여졌으나, 스파클러 부인은 그에게 소파 반대편으로 돌아가 창문 커튼 안에 앉아 기세를 좀 가라앉히라고 청했다."]], [["‘Now, Edmund,’ she said, stretching out her fan, and touching him with it at arm’s length, ‘what I was going to say to you when you began as usual to prose and worry, is, that I shall guard against our being alone any more, and that when circumstances prevent my going out to my own satisfaction, I must arrange to have some people or other always here; for I really cannot, and will not, have another such day as this has been.’", "\"자, 에드먼드,\" 그녀가 부채를 뻗어 팔 길이만큼 떨어진 채 그를 건드리며 말했다. \"당신이 늘 그렇듯 지루하게 늘어놓으며 사람을 괴롭히기 시작했을 때 내가 하려던 말은, 우리가 더는 단둘이 있지 않도록 내가 단속하겠다는 것, 그리고 사정상 내가 만족스럽게 외출할 수 없을 때에는 누군가가 항상 여기 와 있도록 조처하겠다는 거예요. 정말이지 오늘 같은 날을 또 겪는 건 못 견디겠고, 견디지 않을 거니까요.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler’s sentiments as to the plan were, in brief, that it had no nonsense about it.", "그 계획에 대한 스파클러 씨의 의견은, 한마디로 그것이 어디 하나 빠지는 데 없다는 것이었다."], ["He added, ‘And besides, you know it’s likely that you’ll soon have your sister--’", "그는 덧붙였다. \"게다가 말이오, 머잖아 당신 여동생이 올 것 같지 않소—\""]], [["‘Dearest Amy, yes!’ cried Mrs Sparkler with a sigh of affection.", "\"사랑스러운 에이미, 그래요!\" 스파클러 부인이 애정 어린 한숨을 쉬며 외쳤다."], ["‘Darling little thing!", "\"귀여운 우리 아가!"], ["Not, however, that Amy would do here alone.’", "하지만, 에이미 혼자로는 여기서 어림없지요.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler was going to say ‘No?’ interrogatively, but he saw his danger and said it assentingly, ‘No, Oh dear no; she wouldn’t do here alone.’", "스파클러 씨는 의문조로 \"아니라고?\" 하고 말하려 했으나, 위험을 감지하고는 동의조로 말했다. \"그래, 아 그렇고말고, 그 애 혼자로는 여기서 어림없지.\""]], [["‘No, Edmund.", "\"그래요, 에드먼드."], ["For not only are the virtues of the precious child of that still character that they require a contrast--require life and movement around them to bring them out in their right colours and make one love them of all things; but she will require to be roused, on more accounts than one.’", "그 소중한 아이의 미덕은, 그토록 차분한 성품이라서 대조가 필요하거든요 — 그 미덕을 제 빛깔로 드러내고 무엇보다 사랑스럽게 만들려면 주위에 생기와 움직임이 필요하지요. 게다가 그 애는 여러 가지 이유로 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요.\""]], [["‘That’s it,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"바로 그거요.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Roused.’", "\"분발시킨다.\""]], [["‘Pray don’t, Edmund!", "\"제발 그러지 마요, 에드먼드!"], ["Your habit of interrupting without having the least thing in the world to say, distracts one.", "세상에 할 말이라곤 조금도 없으면서 끼어드는 당신 버릇은 사람을 산만하게 해요."], ["You must be broken of it.", "그 버릇을 고쳐야 해요."], ["Speaking of Amy;--my poor little pet was devotedly attached to poor papa, and no doubt will have lamented his loss exceedingly, and grieved very much.", "에이미 얘기로 돌아가자면 — 가엾은 내 어린 동생은 불쌍한 아버지께 헌신적으로 매여 있었으니, 분명 그분을 잃고 몹시 비탄에 잠겼을 테고 무척 슬퍼했을 거예요. 나도 그랬어요."], ["I have done so myself.", "끔찍하게 느꼈지요."], ["I have felt it dreadfully.", "하지만 에이미는 분명 더 절절히 느꼈을 거예요."], ["But Amy will no doubt have felt it even more, from having been on the spot the whole time, and having been with poor dear papa at the last; which I unhappily was not.’", "그 애는 내내 그곳에 있었고, 불쌍한 아버지의 임종을 지켰으니까요. 안타깝게도 나는 그러지 못했고요.\""]], [["Here Fanny stopped to weep, and to say, ‘Dear, dear, beloved papa!", "여기서 패니는 멈춰 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. \"사랑하고 사랑하는, 그리운 아버지!"], ["How truly gentlemanly he was!", "그분은 얼마나 진정으로 신사다우셨던지!"], ["What a contrast to poor uncle!’", "가엾은 삼촌과는 얼마나 대조적이었던지!\""]], [["‘From the effects of that trying time,’ she pursued, ‘my good little Mouse will have to be roused.", "\"그 힘든 시기의 여파로,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["Also, from the effects of this long attendance upon Edward in his illness; an attendance which is not yet over, which may even go on for some time longer, and which in the meanwhile unsettles us all by keeping poor dear papa’s affairs from being wound up.", "\"착한 내 어린 생쥐는 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요. 또한 에드워드의 병환을 오래 간호해 온 여파로도 그래요."], ["Fortunately, however, the papers with his agents here being all sealed up and locked up, as he left them when he providentially came to England, the affairs are in that state of order that they can wait until my brother Edward recovers his health in Sicily, sufficiently to come over, and administer, or execute, or whatever it may be that will have to be done.’", "그 간호는 아직 끝나지도 않았고, 어쩌면 더 오래 이어질지도 모르며, 그러는 사이에 불쌍한 아버지의 재산 정리가 늦춰져 우리 모두를 불안하게 만들고 있어요. 그래도 다행히, 아버지가 섭리에 따라 영국에 오셨을 때 두고 가신 그대로 이곳 대리인들에게 있는 서류들이 모두 봉인되고 잠겨 있어서, 그 일들은 오빠 에드워드가 시칠리아에서 건강을 회복해 건너와 관리하든 집행하든, 해야 할 일이 무엇이든 그것을 할 수 있을 때까지 기다릴 수 있는 정돈된 상태에 있지요.\""]], [["‘He couldn’t have a better nurse to bring him round,’ Mr Sparkler made bold to opine.", "\"오빠를 일으켜 세우는 데 그 애보다 더 나은 간병인은 없을 거요.\" 스파클러 씨가 대담하게 의견을 내놓았다."]], [["‘For a wonder, I can agree with you,’ returned his wife, languidly turning her eyelids a little in his direction (she held forth, in general, as if to the drawing-room furniture), ‘and can adopt your words.", "\"웬일로 당신 말에 동의할 수 있겠네요.\" 그의 아내가 나른하게 눈꺼풀을 그쪽으로 조금 돌리며(그녀는 대개 응접실 가구를 향해 말하듯 했다) 대꾸했다."], ["He couldn’t have a better nurse to bring him round.", "\"당신 말을 그대로 받아들일 수 있겠어요."], ["There are times when my dear child is a little wearing to an active mind; but, as a nurse, she is Perfection.", "오빠를 일으켜 세우는 데 그 애보다 더 나은 간병인은 없을 거예요. 활동적인 정신을 가진 사람에게 내 사랑스러운 동생이 좀 지치게 굴 때도 있긴 하지만, 간병인으로서는 그 애는 완벽함 그 자체예요."], ["Best of Amys!’", "에이미들 중의 최고지!\""]], [["Mr Sparkler, growing rash on his late success, observed that Edward had had, biggodd, a long bout of it, my dear girl.", "스파클러 씨는 최근의 성공에 우쭐해져서는, 맙소사, 에드워드가 한참을 호되게 앓았지 않소, 여보, 하고 말했다."]], [["‘If Bout, Edmund,’ returned Mrs Sparkler, ‘is the slang term for indisposition, he has.", "\"호되게 앓는다는 게, 에드먼드,\" 스파클러 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["If it is not, I am unable to give an opinion on the barbarous language you address to Edward’s sister.", "\"몸져눕는 것을 가리키는 속어라면, 그래요 그랬지요. 그게 아니라면, 당신이 에드워드의 누이에게 건네는 그 야만적인 언사에 대해선 나는 뭐라 의견을 낼 수가 없네요."], ["That he contracted Malaria Fever somewhere, either by travelling day and night to Rome, where, after all, he arrived too late to see poor dear papa before his death--or under some other unwholesome circumstances--is indubitable, if that is what you mean.", "오빠가 어딘가에서, 밤낮으로 달려 로마에 갔다가 — 그래 봤자 불쌍한 아버지가 돌아가시기 전에 뵙기엔 너무 늦게 도착했지만요 — 아니면 다른 어떤 해로운 상황에서 말라리아 열병에 걸렸다는 건, 당신이 말하려는 게 그거라면, 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["Likewise that his extremely careless life has made him a very bad subject for it indeed.’", "마찬가지로 오빠의 지극히 무절제한 생활이 그 병에 정말이지 아주 취약한 체질로 만들었다는 것도요.\""]], [["Mr Sparkler considered it a parallel case to that of some of our fellows in the West Indies with Yellow Jack.", "스파클러 씨는 그것이 서인도제도에서 황열병에 걸린 우리 패거리 몇몇의 경우와 비슷한 사례라고 여겼다."], ["Mrs Sparkler closed her eyes again, and refused to have any consciousness of our fellows of the West Indies, or of Yellow Jack.", "스파클러 부인은 다시 눈을 감고는, 서인도제도의 우리 패거리든 황열병이든 일절 의식하기를 거부했다."]], [["‘So, Amy,’ she pursued, when she reopened her eyelids, ‘will require to be roused from the effects of many tedious and anxious weeks.", "\"그러니 에이미는,\" 그녀가 눈꺼풀을 다시 열며 말을 이었다."], ["And lastly, she will require to be roused from a low tendency which I know very well to be at the bottom of her heart.", "\"수많은 지루하고 불안했던 주들의 여파에서 분발시켜 줄 필요가 있어요. 그리고 마지막으로, 그 애 마음 깊은 곳에 자리 잡고 있다는 걸 내가 아주 잘 아는 어떤 침울한 경향에서도 분발시켜 줘야 해요."], ["Don’t ask me what it is, Edmund, because I must decline to tell you.’", "그게 뭐냐고는 묻지 마요, 에드먼드. 당신한테 말하기는 거절할 테니까.\""]], [["‘I am not going to, my dear,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"안 물을 거요, 여보.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I shall thus have much improvement to effect in my sweet child,’ Mrs Sparkler continued, ‘and cannot have her near me too soon.", "\"그러니 내 사랑스러운 동생을 두고 손볼 일이 많을 거예요.\""], ["Amiable and dear little Twoshoes!", "스파클러 부인이 말을 이었다."], ["As to the settlement of poor papa’s affairs, my interest in that is not very selfish.", "\"그러니 그 애를 한시라도 빨리 곁에 두지 않을 수 없지요. 사랑스럽고 정겨운 우리 꼬마 신발 한 켤레!"], ["Papa behaved very generously to me when I was married, and I have little or nothing to expect.", "불쌍한 아버지의 재산 정리에 대해 말하자면, 내 관심은 그리 이기적인 게 아니에요."], ["Provided he had made no will that can come into force, leaving a legacy to Mrs General, I am contented.", "아버지는 내가 결혼할 때 무척 후하게 대해 주셨으니, 내가 기대할 게 거의 없어요. 효력을 발휘할 유언장을 만들어 제너럴 부인에게 유산을 남기는 일만 없었다면, 나는 만족해요."], ["Dear papa, dear papa.’", "그리운 아버지, 그리운 아버지.\""]], [["She wept again, but Mrs General was the best of restoratives.", "그녀는 다시 눈물을 흘렸으나, 제너럴 부인은 최고의 회복제였다."], ["The name soon stimulated her to dry her eyes and say:", "그 이름은 곧 그녀를 자극해 눈물을 닦고 이렇게 말하게 했다."]], [["‘It is a highly encouraging circumstance in Edward’s illness, I am thankful to think, and gives one the greatest confidence in his sense not being impaired, or his proper spirit weakened--down to the time of poor dear papa’s death at all events--that he paid off Mrs General instantly, and sent her out of the house.", "\"오빠의 병환에서 무척 고무적인 점은, 생각하면 감사한 일이지만, 오빠의 분별력이 손상되지 않았고 마땅한 기개도 약해지지 않았다는 — 적어도 불쌍한 아버지가 돌아가실 무렵까지는요 — 그것에 큰 확신을 준다는 거예요. 오빠가 제너럴 부인을 즉시 청산하고 집에서 내쫓았다는 것 말이에요."], ["I applaud him for it.", "나는 그 일로 오빠를 칭찬해요."], ["I could forgive him a great deal for doing, with such promptitude, so exactly what I would have done myself!’", "그렇게 신속하게, 내가 직접 했을 법한 바로 그 일을 해 준 것에 대해서라면 많은 걸 용서해 줄 수 있어요!\""]], [["Mrs Sparkler was in the full glow of her gratification, when a double knock was heard at the door.", "스파클러 부인이 만족감에 한껏 달아올라 있을 때, 문에서 노크 소리가 두 번 들려왔다."], ["A very odd knock.", "무척 기묘한 노크였다."], ["Low, as if to avoid making a noise and attracting attention.", "소리를 내거나 주목을 끌지 않으려는 듯 낮았다."], ["Long, as if the person knocking were preoccupied in mind, and forgot to leave off.", "노크하는 사람이 무언가에 골몰해 멈추는 것을 잊은 듯 길었다."]], [["‘Halloa!’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"이런!\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Who’s this?’", "\"누구지?\""]], [["‘Not Amy and Edward without notice and without a carriage!’ said Mrs Sparkler.", "\"통기도 없이 마차도 없이 에이미와 에드워드일 리는 없는데!\" 스파클러 부인이 말했다."], ["‘Look out.’", "\"내다봐요.\""]], [["The room was dark, but the street was lighter, because of its lamps.", "방 안은 어두웠으나 거리는 가로등 덕분에 더 환했다."], ["Mr Sparkler’s head peeping over the balcony looked so very bulky and heavy that it seemed on the point of overbalancing him and flattening the unknown below.", "발코니 너머로 내다보는 스파클러 씨의 머리가 어찌나 부피 크고 묵직해 보이던지, 금방이라도 균형을 잃고 아래에 있는 미지의 인물을 납작하게 짓눌러 버릴 것만 같았다."]], [["‘It’s one fellow,’ said Mr Sparkler.", "\"한 사람이오.\" 스파클러 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I can’t see who--stop though!’", "\"누군지 못 알아보겠—아니, 잠깐!\""]], [["On this second thought he went out into the balcony again and had another look.", "이 두 번째 생각에 그는 다시 발코니로 나가 한 번 더 보았다."], ["He came back as the door was opened, and announced that he believed he had identified ‘his governor’s tile.’ He was not mistaken, for his governor, with his tile in his hand, was introduced immediately afterwards.", "문이 열릴 즈음 그는 돌아와, \"우리 영감님의 모자\"를 알아본 것 같다고 알렸다. 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았으니, 곧이어 그의 영감님이 모자를 손에 든 채 안으로 안내되었다."]], [["‘Candles!’ said Mrs Sparkler, with a word of excuse for the darkness.", "\"촛불을!\" 스파클러 부인이 어둠에 대해 양해의 말을 건네며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s light enough for me,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"나한텐 충분히 밝소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["When the candles were brought in, Mr Merdle was discovered standing behind the door, picking his lips.", "촛불이 들어오자, 머들 씨가 문 뒤에 서서 입술을 매만지고 있는 것이 드러났다."], ["‘I thought I’d give you a call,’ he said.", "\"한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\""], ["‘I am rather particularly occupied just now; and, as I happened to be out for a stroll, I thought I’d give you a call.’", "그가 말했다. \"지금 좀 유난히 바쁘긴 한데, 마침 산책 삼아 나온 김에 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\""]], [["As he was in dinner dress, Fanny asked him where he had been dining?", "그가 만찬 차림이었으므로, 패니는 어디서 식사를 하고 왔느냐고 물었다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I haven’t been dining anywhere, particularly.’", "\"글쎄,\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"딱히 어디서 식사를 하진 않았소.\""]], [["‘Of course you have dined?’ said Fanny.", "\"물론 식사는 하셨겠죠?\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘Why--no, I haven’t exactly dined,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"그게 — 아니, 정확히 식사를 하진 않았소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["He had passed his hand over his yellow forehead and considered, as if he were not sure about it.", "그는 누런 이마를 손으로 쓸며, 그 점에 대해 확신이 없는 듯 곰곰이 생각했다. 무언가 먹을 것을 권했다."], ["Something to eat was proposed.", "\"고맙지만 됐소.\""], ["‘No, thank you,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I don’t feel inclined for it.", "머들 씨가 말했다. \"그럴 마음이 안 드는구려."], ["I was to have dined out along with Mrs Merdle.", "원래 머들 부인과 함께 밖에서 식사할 예정이었소."], ["But as I didn’t feel inclined for dinner, I let Mrs Merdle go by herself just as we were getting into the carriage, and thought I’d take a stroll instead.’", "그런데 식사할 마음이 안 들어서, 마차에 막 올라타려던 참에 머들 부인 혼자 가게 두고, 나는 대신 산책이나 해야겠다 싶었소.\""]], [["Would he have tea or coffee?", "차나 커피를 들겠느냐고 물었다."], ["‘No, thank you,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"고맙지만 됐소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I looked in at the Club, and got a bottle of wine.’", "\"클럽에 들러서 포도주 한 병 마셨소.\""]], [["At this period of his visit, Mr Merdle took the chair which Edmund Sparkler had offered him, and which he had hitherto been pushing slowly about before him, like a dull man with a pair of skates on for the first time, who could not make up his mind to start.", "방문이 이 대목에 이르자, 머들 씨는 에드먼드 스파클러가 권했던 의자에 앉았다. 그때까지 그는 그 의자를 마치 스케이트를 처음 신어 보고도 출발할 결심을 못 하는 둔한 사람처럼 앞으로 천천히 밀고 다녔던 것이다."], ["He now put his hat upon another chair beside him, and, looking down into it as if it were some twenty feet deep, said again: ‘You see I thought I’d give you a call.’", "그는 이제 모자를 옆의 다른 의자에 올려놓고, 마치 그것이 스무 자쯤 되는 깊이라도 되는 양 그 안을 들여다보며 다시 말했다. \"보다시피 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었소.\""]], [["‘Flattering to us,’ said Fanny, ‘for you are not a calling man.’", "\"우리한텐 영광이네요.\" 패니가 말했다. \"방문 다니시는 분이 아니니까요.\""]], [["‘No--no,’ returned Mr Merdle, who was by this time taking himself into custody under both coat-sleeves.", "\"그렇소, 그렇소.\" 이즈음 두 소맷자락 아래로 자신을 가두고 있던 머들 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘No, I am not a calling man.’", "\"그렇소, 나는 방문 다니는 사람이 아니오.\""]], [["‘You have too much to do for that,’ said Fanny.", "\"그러기엔 하실 일이 너무 많으시죠.\" 패니가 말했다."], ["‘Having so much to do, Mr Merdle, loss of appetite is a serious thing with you, and you must have it seen to.", "\"하실 일이 그렇게 많으시니, 머들 씨, 식욕을 잃는 건 당신한테 심각한 일이에요. 살펴보셔야 해요."], ["You must not be ill.’", "편찮으시면 안 돼요.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I am very well,’ replied Mr Merdle, after deliberating about it.", "나는 아주 멀쩡하오.\" 머들 씨가 그 점을 곰곰이 생각한 뒤 대답했다."], ["‘I am as well as I usually am.", "\"평소만큼 멀쩡하오."], ["I am well enough.", "충분히 멀쩡하오."], ["I am as well as I want to be.’", "내가 원하는 만큼 멀쩡하다오.\""]], [["The master-mind of the age, true to its characteristic of being at all times a mind that had as little as possible to say for itself and great difficulty in saying it, became mute again.", "언제나 자기에 대해 할 말이 가능한 한 적고 그것을 말하는 데에도 큰 어려움을 겪는다는 특징에 충실한, 이 시대의 거장 정신은 다시 입을 다물었다."], ["Mrs Sparkler began to wonder how long the master-mind meant to stay.", "스파클러 부인은 그 거장 정신이 얼마나 오래 머물 작정인지 궁금해지기 시작했다."]], [["‘I was speaking of poor papa when you came in, sir.’", "\"당신이 들어왔을 때 나는 불쌍한 아버지 얘기를 하고 있었어요, 어르신.\""]], [["‘Aye!", "\"아!"], ["Quite a coincidence,’ said Mr Merdle.", "참으로 공교롭군.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["Fanny did not see that; but felt it incumbent on her to continue talking.", "패니는 어디가 공교롭다는 건지 알 수 없었지만, 말을 계속해야 할 의무를 느꼈다. \"내 말은,\""], ["‘I was saying,’ she pursued, ‘that my brother’s illness has occasioned a delay in examining and arranging papa’s property.’", "그녀가 이어 갔다. \"오빠의 병환 때문에 아버지의 재산을 조사하고 정리하는 일이 미뤄지고 있다는 거였어요.\""]], [["‘Yes,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘yes.", "\"그렇소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["There has been a delay.’", "\"그렇소. 미뤄졌지.\""]], [["‘Not that it is of consequence,’ said Fanny.", "\"그게 중요하다는 건 아니지만요.\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘Not,’ assented Mr Merdle, after having examined the cornice of all that part of the room which was within his range: ‘not that it is of any consequence.’", "\"중요하다는,\" 머들 씨가 자기 시야에 들어오는 방의 그 부분 처마 장식을 죄다 살펴본 뒤 동의했다. \"건 아니지요.\""]], [["‘My only anxiety is,’ said Fanny, ‘that Mrs General should not get anything.’", "\"내 유일한 걱정은,\" 패니가 말했다. \"제너럴 부인이 아무것도 받지 못하게 하는 거예요.\""]], [["‘_She_ won’t get anything,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"그 여자는 아무것도 못 받을 거요.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["Fanny was delighted to hear him express the opinion.", "패니는 그가 그런 의견을 내놓는 것을 듣고 기뻐했다."], ["Mr Merdle, after taking another gaze into the depths of his hat as if he thought he saw something at the bottom, rubbed his hair and slowly appended to his last remark the confirmatory words, ‘Oh dear no.", "머들 씨는 마치 모자 바닥에 뭔가 보이기라도 하는 듯 다시 그 깊은 곳을 들여다본 뒤, 머리카락을 비비며 자신의 마지막 말에 확언의 말을 천천히 덧붙였다. \"오 그렇고말고."], ["No. Not she.", "못 받지."], ["Not likely.’", "그 여자는 못 받소. 어림없는 일이오.\""]], [["As the topic seemed exhausted, and Mr Merdle too, Fanny inquired if he were going to take up Mrs Merdle and the carriage in his way home?", "화제가 다한 듯하고 머들 씨도 그러한 듯하자, 패니는 그가 돌아가는 길에 머들 부인과 마차를 데리러 들를 거냐고 물었다."]], [["‘No,’ he answered; ‘I shall go by the shortest way, and leave Mrs Merdle to--’ here he looked all over the palms of both his hands as if he were telling his own fortune--‘to take care of herself. I dare say she’ll manage to do it.’", "\"아니오.\" 그가 대답했다. \"나는 가장 가까운 길로 갈 테고, 머들 부인은 — \" 여기서 그는 마치 자기 운명을 점치기라도 하는 듯 두 손바닥을 죄다 들여다보았다. \"알아서 하도록 둘 거요. 자기가 알아서 잘 처리하겠지.\""]], [["‘Probably,’ said Fanny.", "\"아마도요.\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["There was then a long silence; during which, Mrs Sparkler, lying back on her sofa again, shut her eyes and raised her eyebrows in her former retirement from mundane affairs.", "그러고 나서 긴 침묵이 이어졌다. 그동안 스파클러 부인은 다시 소파에 기대어 누워, 눈을 감고 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 앞서처럼 세상사에서 물러나 있었다."]], [["‘But, however,’ said Mr Merdle, ‘I am equally detaining you and myself.", "\"하지만, 어쨌든,\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["I thought I’d give you a call, you know.’", "\"내가 당신을 붙들고 나도 붙드는 셈이군. 한번 들러 봐야겠다 싶었던 거요, 알다시피.\""]], [["‘Charmed, I am sure,’ said Fanny.", "\"정말 매력적이세요.\" 패니가 말했다."]], [["‘So I am off,’ added Mr Merdle, getting up.", "\"그럼 이만 가겠소.\" 머들 씨가 일어나며 덧붙였다."], ["‘Could you lend me a penknife?’", "\"주머니칼 하나 빌려줄 수 있겠소?\""]], [["It was an odd thing, Fanny smilingly observed, for her who could seldom prevail upon herself even to write a letter, to lend to a man of such vast business as Mr Merdle.", "편지 한 통 쓰는 것조차 좀처럼 마음먹지 못하는 자기가, 머들 씨처럼 그토록 사업이 방대한 사람에게 빌려준다는 건 묘한 일이네요, 하고 패니가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그렇지 않소?\""], ["‘Isn’t it?’ Mr Merdle acquiesced; ‘but I want one; and I know you have got several little wedding keepsakes about, with scissors and tweezers and such things in them.", "머들 씨가 수긍했다. \"하지만 나는 하나 필요하오."], ["You shall have it back to-morrow.’", "그리고 당신에게 가위니 족집게니 그런 것들이 든 자그마한 결혼 기념품이 여럿 있다는 걸 아오. 내일 돌려주리다.\""]], [["‘Edmund,’ said Mrs Sparkler, ‘open (now, very carefully, I beg and beseech, for you are so very awkward) the mother of pearl box on my little table there, and give Mr Merdle the mother of pearl penknife.’", "\"에드먼드,\" 스파클러 부인이 말했다. \"저기 내 작은 탁자 위에 있는 자개 상자를 열어서(자, 아주 조심해서요, 제발 부탁이에요, 당신은 워낙 서투르니까) 머들 씨에게 자개 주머니칼을 드려요.\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘but if you have got one with a darker handle, I think I should prefer one with a darker handle.’", "\"고맙소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다. \"하지만 손잡이가 더 어두운 게 있으면, 손잡이가 더 어두운 쪽이 좋을 것 같소.\""]], [["‘Tortoise-shell?’", "\"거북 등딱지요?\""]], [["‘Thank you,’ said Mr Merdle; ‘yes.", "\"고맙소.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."], ["I think I should prefer tortoise-shell.’", "\"그래요. 거북 등딱지가 좋을 것 같소.\""]], [["Edmund accordingly received instructions to open the tortoise-shell box, and give Mr Merdle the tortoise-shell knife.", "그리하여 에드먼드는 거북 등딱지 상자를 열어 머들 씨에게 거북 등딱지 칼을 드리라는 지시를 받았다."], ["On his doing so, his wife said to the master-spirit graciously:", "그가 그렇게 하자, 그의 아내가 그 거장에게 너그러이 말했다."]], [["‘I will forgive you, if you ink it.’", "\"잉크만 묻히지 않으면 용서해 드릴게요.\""]], [["‘I’ll undertake not to ink it,’ said Mr Merdle.", "\"잉크는 묻히지 않겠다고 약속하리다.\" 머들 씨가 말했다."]], [["The illustrious visitor then put out his coat-cuff, and for a moment entombed Mrs Sparkler’s hand: wrist, bracelet, and all.", "그러고서 그 명망 높은 방문객은 소맷부리를 내밀어, 한순간 스파클러 부인의 손을 — 손목과 팔찌까지 죄다 — 묻어 버렸다."], ["Where his own hand had shrunk to, was not made manifest, but it was as remote from Mrs Sparkler’s sense of touch as if he had been a highly meritorious Chelsea Veteran or Greenwich Pensioner.", "그 자신의 손이 어디로 움츠러들었는지는 드러나지 않았으나, 마치 그가 더없이 공로 많은 첼시의 노병이나 그리니치 연금 수급자라도 되는 양 스파클러 부인의 촉각으로부터 아득히 멀리 떨어져 있었다."]], [["Thoroughly convinced, as he went out of the room, that it was the longest day that ever did come to an end at last, and that there never was a woman, not wholly devoid of personal attractions, so worn out by idiotic and lumpish people, Fanny passed into the balcony for a breath of air.", "방을 나서면서, 마침내 끝장을 본 날 중에서 가장 긴 날이었으며, 개인적인 매력이 전혀 없지 않은 여자가 멍청하고 둔한 사람들에게 그토록 지쳐 버린 일은 결코 없었다고 철저히 확신한 채, 패니는 바람을 좀 쐬러 발코니로 나갔다."], ["Waters of vexation filled her eyes; and they had the effect of making the famous Mr Merdle, in going down the street, appear to leap, and waltz, and gyrate, as if he were possessed of several Devils.", "분한 마음의 눈물이 그녀의 눈을 채웠고, 그 눈물은 거리를 내려가는 그 유명한 머들 씨가 마치 여러 악마에라도 들린 듯 펄쩍 뛰고 왈츠를 추고 빙빙 도는 것처럼 보이게 하는 효과를 냈다."]], [["# CHAPTER 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office", "# CHAPTER 25. The Chief Butler Resigns the Seals of Office"]], [["The dinner-party was at the great Physician’s.", "만찬 모임은 저명한 의사의 집에서 열렸다."], ["Bar was there, and in full force.", "법조계 인사도 참석했고, 그것도 한껏 위세를 떨치고 있었다."], ["Ferdinand Barnacle was there, and in his most engaging state.", "퍼디낸드 배너클도 참석했는데, 더없이 사람을 끌어당기는 태도였다."], ["Few ways of life were hidden from Physician, and he was oftener in its darkest places than even Bishop.", "의사에게는 인생의 어떤 면도 좀처럼 가려져 있지 않았으니, 그는 주교보다도 더 자주 인생의 가장 어두운 곳에 발을 들였다."], ["There were brilliant ladies about London who perfectly doted on him, my dear, as the most charming creature and the most delightful person, who would have been shocked to find themselves so close to him if they could have known on what sights those thoughtful eyes of his had rested within an hour or two, and near to whose beds, and under what roofs, his composed figure had stood.", "런던에는 그를 더없이 매력적인 사람이자 더없이 유쾌한 인물이라며 자기야, 사족을 못 쓰는 화려한 귀부인들이 있었는데, 그 사려 깊은 눈이 한두 시간 전에 어떤 광경에 머물렀는지를, 또 그의 차분한 모습이 누구의 침상 곁에, 어떤 지붕 아래에 서 있었는지를 알았더라면, 그렇게 가까이 앉아 있다는 사실에 그녀들은 소스라쳤을 것이다."], ["But Physician was a composed man, who performed neither on his own trumpet, nor on the trumpets of other people.", "그러나 의사는 차분한 사람이어서, 제 나팔도 남의 나팔도 불지 않았다."], ["Many wonderful things did he see and hear, and much irreconcilable moral contradiction did he pass his life among; yet his equality of compassion was no more disturbed than the Divine Master’s of all healing was.", "그는 숱한 놀라운 일들을 보고 들었으며, 화해될 수 없는 도덕적 모순들 한가운데에서 일생을 보냈다. 그럼에도 그의 한결같은 연민은, 모든 치유의 신성한 주님의 그것이 그러하듯, 조금도 흐트러지지 않았다."], ["He went, like the rain, among the just and unjust, doing all the good he could, and neither proclaiming it in the synagogues nor at the corner of streets.", "그는 비처럼 의로운 자와 불의한 자 사이를 두루 다니며 할 수 있는 모든 선을 행했으되, 그것을 회당에서도 거리 모퉁이에서도 떠벌리지 않았다."]], [["As no man of large experience of humanity, however quietly carried it may be, can fail to be invested with an interest peculiar to the possession of such knowledge, Physician was an attractive man.", "인간에 대한 폭넓은 경험을 지닌 사람이라면, 그것을 아무리 조용히 지니고 있더라도, 그런 앎의 소유에서 비롯되는 독특한 흥미를 풍기지 않을 수 없는 법이니, 의사는 매력적인 사람이었다."], ["Even the daintier gentlemen and ladies who had no idea of his secret, and who would have been startled out of more wits than they had, by the monstrous impropriety of his proposing to them ‘Come and see what I see!’ confessed his attraction.", "그의 비밀을 짐작조차 못 하고, 만약 그가 그들에게 \"와서 내가 보는 것을 보시오!\"라고 제안한다면 그 끔찍한 무례함에 가진 정신을 죄다 놓아 버렸을 더 까다로운 신사 숙녀들조차도 그의 매력만은 인정했다."], ["Where he was, something real was.", "그가 있는 곳에는 무언가 진실한 것이 있었다."], ["And half a grain of reality, like the smallest portion of some other scarce natural productions, will flavour an enormous quantity of diluent.", "그리고 진실의 반 톨은, 다른 희귀한 천연물의 가장 작은 조각이 그러하듯, 어마어마한 양의 희석제에 풍미를 더한다."]], [["It came to pass, therefore, that Physician’s little dinners always presented people in their least conventional lights. The guests said to themselves, whether they were conscious of it or no, ‘Here is a man who really has an acquaintance with us as we are, who is admitted to some of us every day with our wigs and paint off, who hears the wanderings of our minds, and sees the undisguised expression of our faces, when both are past our control; we may as well make an approach to reality with him, for the man has got the better of us and is too strong for us.’ Therefore, Physician’s guests came out so surprisingly at his round table that they were almost natural.", "그리하여 의사의 자그마한 만찬에서는 사람들이 늘 가장 덜 인습적인 모습으로 드러나기 마련이었다. 손님들은 스스로 의식하든 못 하든 이렇게 중얼거렸다. \"여기 우리를 있는 그대로 정말로 아는 사람이 있다. 우리 가운데 더러는 가발과 분칠을 벗은 채로 날마다 그에게 받아들여지고, 둘 다 우리 통제를 벗어났을 때 그는 우리 마음의 방황을 듣고 가식 없는 얼굴 표정을 본다. 그러니 그 앞에서는 진실에 한 발짝 다가가는 편이 낫겠다. 이 사람은 우리를 이겨 냈고, 우리에게는 너무 강하니까.\" 그래서 의사의 손님들은 그 둥근 식탁에서 어찌나 놀랍도록 본모습을 드러내던지, 거의 자연스러울 정도였다."]], [["Bar’s knowledge of that agglomeration of jurymen which is called humanity was as sharp as a razor; yet a razor is not a generally convenient instrument, and Physician’s plain bright scalpel, though far less keen, was adaptable to far wider purposes.", "배심원들의 그 집합체, 곧 인류라 불리는 것에 관한 법조계 인사의 지식은 면도날처럼 예리했다. 그러나 면도날이라는 게 두루 쓰기에 편리한 도구는 아니어서, 의사의 소박하고 빛나는 메스는 훨씬 덜 날카롭긴 해도 훨씬 더 넓은 용도에 들어맞았다."], ["Bar knew all about the gullibility and knavery of people; but Physician could have given him a better insight into their tendernesses and affections, in one week of his rounds, than Westminster Hall and all the circuits put together, in threescore years and ten.", "법조계 인사는 사람들의 잘 속는 성질과 교활함에 대해서라면 죄다 꿰고 있었다. 그러나 의사라면 자기 왕진 한 주만으로도, 웨스트민스터 홀과 모든 순회 재판을 합쳐 칠십 년 동안 얻을 수 있는 것보다 그들의 다정함과 애정에 대해 더 나은 통찰을 그에게 줄 수 있었을 것이다."], ["Bar always had a suspicion of this, and perhaps was glad to encourage it (for, if the world were really a great Law Court, one would think that the last day of Term could not too soon arrive); and so he liked and respected Physician quite as much as any other kind of man did.", "법조계 인사는 늘 이를 어렴풋이 짐작했고, 어쩌면 그 짐작을 기꺼이 부추겼을 것이다(만약 세상이 정말로 거대한 법정이라면, 종기일의 마지막 날이 아무리 빨리 와도 이르지 않으리라 여겨질 테니). 그래서 그는 다른 어떤 부류의 사람 못지않게 의사를 좋아하고 존경했다."]], [["Mr Merdle’s default left a Banquo’s chair at the table; but, if he had been there, he would have merely made the difference of Banquo in it, and consequently he was no loss.", "머들 씨가 빠진 탓에 식탁에는 뱅코의 빈자리가 생겼다. 그러나 그가 거기 있었더라도 그저 거기 앉은 뱅코만큼의 차이밖에 못 냈을 테니, 그의 부재는 손실이 아니었다."], ["Bar, who picked up all sorts of odds and ends about Westminster Hall, much as a raven would have done if he had passed as much of his time there, had been picking up a great many straws lately and tossing them about, to try which way the Merdle wind blew.", "만약 까마귀가 웨스트민스터 홀에서 그만큼 시간을 보냈다면 그랬을 법한 모양으로, 그곳의 온갖 잡동사니를 주워 모으던 법조계 인사는 요즈음 지푸라기를 잔뜩 주워 이리저리 던져 보며 머들의 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지 가늠하고 있었다."], ["He now had a little talk on the subject with Mrs Merdle herself; sidling up to that lady, of course, with his double eye-glass and his jury droop.", "그는 이제 그 문제를 두고 머들 부인 본인과 잠시 이야기를 나누었다. 물론 두 겹 안경에 배심원을 다룰 때의 고갯짓을 곁들이며 그 부인 곁으로 슬며시 다가가서였다."]], [["‘A certain bird,’ said Bar; and he looked as if it could have been no other bird than a magpie; ‘has been whispering among us lawyers lately, that there is to be an addition to the titled personages of this realm.’", "\"어떤 새 한 마리가,\" 법조계 인사가 말했는데, 그 새가 까치 말고 다른 새일 리는 없다는 표정이었다, \"요즘 우리 법조인들 사이에서 속삭이기를, 이 나라의 작위 가진 분들 명단에 한 분이 더해질 거라더군요.\""]], [["‘Really?’ said Mrs Merdle.", "\"정말요?\" 머들 부인이 말했다."]], [["‘Yes,’ said Bar.", "\"그렇습니다.\" 법조계 인사가 말했다."], ["‘Has not the bird been whispering in very different ears from ours--in lovely ears?’ He looked expressively at Mrs Merdle’s nearest ear-ring.", "\"그 새가 우리와는 사뭇 다른 귀에다가도—어여쁜 귀에다가도 속삭이지 않았던가요?\" 그는 머들 부인의 가장 가까운 귀고리를 의미심장하게 바라보았다."]], [["‘Do you mean mine?’ asked Mrs Merdle.", "\"제 귀를 말씀하시는 건가요?\" 머들 부인이 물었다."]], [["‘When I say lovely,’ said Bar, ‘I always mean you.’", "\"제가 어여쁘다고 할 때는,\" 법조계 인사가 말했다, \"언제나 부인을 뜻합니다.\""]], [["‘You never mean anything, I think,’ returned Mrs Merdle (not displeased).", "\"제 생각엔 당신은 늘 아무 뜻도 없으신 것 같은데요.\" 머들 부인이 (기분이 상하지는 않은 채로) 받았다."]], [["‘Oh, cruelly unjust!’ said Bar.", "\"오, 너무도 매정한 누명이로군요!\" 법조계 인사가 말했다."], ["‘But, the bird.’", "\"한데, 그 새 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I am the last person in the world to hear news,’ observed Mrs Merdle, carelessly arranging her stronghold.", "\"저야 세상에서 소식을 가장 늦게 듣는 사람이지요.\" 머들 부인이 무심히 제 요새를 매만지며 말했다."], ["‘Who is it?’", "\"그게 누군가요?\""]], [["‘What an admirable witness you would make!’ said Bar. ‘No jury (unless we could empanel one of blind men) could resist you, if you were ever so bad a one; but you would be such a good one!’", "\"부인이라면 얼마나 훌륭한 증인이 되실는지요!\" 법조계 인사가 말했다. \"어떤 배심원도(눈먼 자들로만 배심을 꾸리지 않는 한) 부인을 당해 내지 못할 겁니다. 설령 부인이 형편없는 증인이더라도 말이지요. 한데 부인은 정말 훌륭한 증인이 되실 테니까요!\""]], [["‘Why, you ridiculous man?’ asked Mrs Merdle, laughing.", "\"어머나, 이 우스꽝스러운 양반아, 어째서요?\" 머들 부인이 웃으며 물었다."]], [["Bar waved his double eye-glass three or four times between himself and the Bosom, as a rallying answer, and inquired in his most insinuating accents:", "법조계 인사는 자기와 그 가슴팍 사이에서 두 겹 안경을 서너 번 흔들어 응수를 대신하더니, 더없이 알랑거리는 어조로 물었다."]], [["‘What am I to call the most elegant, accomplished and charming of women, a few weeks, or it may be a few days, hence?’", "\"몇 주 뒤, 어쩌면 며칠 뒤에, 저는 여인들 가운데 가장 우아하고 가장 교양 있고 가장 매혹적인 분을 무어라 불러야 하는지요?\""]], [["‘Didn’t your bird tell you what to call her?’ answered Mrs Merdle.", "\"당신네 새가 그분을 무어라 부르라고는 안 일러 주던가요?\" 머들 부인이 받았다."], ["‘Do ask it to-morrow, and tell me the next time you see me what it says.’", "\"내일 한번 물어보시고, 다음에 저를 보실 때 새가 뭐라 하는지 일러 주세요.\""]], [["This led to further passages of similar pleasantry between the two; but Bar, with all his sharpness, got nothing out of them.", "이리하여 둘 사이에 비슷한 농담이 더 오갔다. 그러나 법조계 인사는 그 온갖 예리함에도 거기서 아무것도 얻어 내지 못했다."], ["Physician, on the other hand, taking Mrs Merdle down to her carriage and attending on her as she put on her cloak, inquired into the symptoms with his usual calm directness.", "반면에 의사는 머들 부인을 마차까지 바래다주며 그녀가 망토를 걸치는 것을 거들면서, 늘 그렇듯 차분하고 직설적으로 증상을 캐물었다."]], [["‘May I ask,’ he said, ‘is this true about Merdle?’", "\"여쭤도 될지요,\" 그가 말했다, \"머들에 관한 그 이야기가 사실입니까?\""]], [["‘My dear doctor,’ she returned, ‘you ask me the very question that I was half disposed to ask you.’", "\"선생님,\" 그녀가 받았다, \"제가 선생님께 여쭐까 반쯤 마음먹었던 바로 그 질문을 하시는군요.\""]], [["‘To ask me!", "\"제게 묻겠다고요!"], ["Why me?’", "어째서 제게요?\""]], [["‘Upon my honour, I think Mr Merdle reposes greater confidence in you than in any one.’", "\"정말이지, 머들 씨가 누구보다도 선생님께 더 큰 신뢰를 두고 있다고 생각하거든요.\""]], [["‘On the contrary, he tells me absolutely nothing, even professionally.", "\"천만에요, 그분은 제게 아무것도, 진료에 관해서조차 말하지 않습니다."], ["You have heard the talk, of course?’", "부인도 물론 그 소문을 들으셨겠지요?\""]], [["‘Of course I have.", "\"물론 들었지요."], ["But you know what Mr Merdle is; you know how taciturn and reserved he is.", "하지만 머들 씨가 어떤 사람인지 아시잖아요. 얼마나 말수가 없고 속을 안 드러내는지요."], ["I assure you I have no idea what foundation for it there may be.", "정말이지 저는 그 소문에 무슨 근거가 있는지 짐작도 못 하겠어요."], ["I should like it to be true; why should I deny that to you?", "사실이었으면 싶긴 해요. 선생님 앞에서 그걸 왜 감추겠어요?"], ["You would know better, if I did!’", "제가 감춘다면 선생님이 더 잘 아실 텐데요!\""]], [["‘Just so,’ said Physician.", "\"바로 그렇지요.\" 의사가 말했다."]], [["‘But whether it is all true, or partly true, or entirely false, I am wholly unable to say.", "\"하지만 그게 죄다 사실인지, 일부만 사실인지, 아니면 전부 거짓인지는 도무지 말씀드릴 수가 없네요."], ["It is a most provoking situation, a most absurd situation; but you know Mr Merdle, and are not surprised.’", "참으로 약 오르는 처지예요, 참으로 어처구니없는 처지지요. 하지만 선생님은 머들 씨를 아시니, 놀라지도 않으시겠죠.\""]], [["Physician was not surprised, handed her into her carriage, and bade her Good Night.", "의사는 놀라지 않은 채 그녀를 마차에 태우고 안녕히 가시라 인사했다."], ["He stood for a moment at his own hall door, looking sedately at the elegant equipage as it rattled away.", "그는 자기 집 현관에 잠시 서서, 그 우아한 마차가 덜커덕거리며 멀어져 가는 것을 차분히 바라보았다."], ["On his return up-stairs, the rest of the guests soon dispersed, and he was left alone.", "위층으로 돌아오니 나머지 손님들도 곧 흩어졌고, 그는 홀로 남았다."], ["Being a great reader of all kinds of literature (and never at all apologetic for that weakness), he sat down comfortably to read.", "온갖 종류의 책을 즐겨 읽는 사람이었기에(그리고 그 약점에 대해 결코 변명하지 않았기에), 그는 편안하게 자리를 잡고 책을 읽었다."]], [["The clock upon his study table pointed to a few minutes short of twelve, when his attention was called to it by a ringing at the door bell.", "서재 탁자 위 시계가 자정에서 몇 분 모자라는 시각을 가리킬 무렵, 현관 초인종 소리가 그의 주의를 그쪽으로 돌렸다."], ["A man of plain habits, he had sent his servants to bed and must needs go down to open the door.", "검소한 습관의 사람인지라 그는 하인들을 잠자리에 들여보낸 터여서 손수 내려가 문을 열어야 했다."], ["He went down, and there found a man without hat or coat, whose shirt sleeves were rolled up tight to his shoulders.", "그가 내려가 보니 모자도 외투도 걸치지 않은 한 사내가 있었는데, 셔츠 소매를 어깨까지 단단히 걷어 올린 차림이었다."], ["For a moment, he thought the man had been fighting: the rather, as he was much agitated and out of breath.", "그는 한순간 그 사내가 싸움이라도 한 줄 알았다. 사내가 몹시 흥분해 있고 숨이 차 있었기 때문에 더욱 그랬다."], ["A second look, however, showed him that the man was particularly clean, and not otherwise discomposed as to his dress than as it answered this description.", "그러나 다시 보니 그 사내는 유난히 깨끗했고, 차림새에 관한 한 이 묘사에 들어맞는 것 말고는 흐트러진 데가 없었다."]], [["‘I come from the warm-baths, sir, round in the neighbouring street.’", "\"근처 거리에 있는 온천 목욕탕에서 왔습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘And what is the matter at the warm-baths?’", "\"한데 온천 목욕탕에 무슨 일이오?\""]], [["‘Would you please to come directly, sir.", "\"부디 곧장 와 주십시오, 선생님."], ["We found that, lying on the table.’", "탁자 위에 이게 놓여 있었습니다.\""]], [["He put into the physician’s hand a scrap of paper.", "그는 의사의 손에 종잇조각 하나를 쥐여 주었다."], ["Physician looked at it, and read his own name and address written in pencil; nothing more.", "의사가 들여다보니 연필로 적힌 제 이름과 주소가 보였다. 그뿐이었다."], ["He looked closer at the writing, looked at the man, took his hat from its peg, put the key of his door in his pocket, and they hurried away together.", "그는 그 필체를 더 가까이 들여다보고, 사내를 보고, 못에 걸린 모자를 집어 들고, 문 열쇠를 주머니에 넣고는, 둘이서 함께 서둘러 떠났다."]], [["When they came to the warm-baths, all the other people belonging to that establishment were looking out for them at the door, and running up and down the passages. ‘Request everybody else to keep back, if you please,’ said the physician aloud to the master; ‘and do you take me straight to the place, my friend,’ to the messenger.", "온천 목욕탕에 다다르니, 그곳에 딸린 다른 사람들이 모두 문간에서 그들을 내다보며 통로를 오르내리고 있었다. \"다들 부디 물러서 있으라 일러 주시오.\" 의사가 주인에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그리고 자네는 나를 곧장 그 자리로 데려가게, 친구.\" 심부름꾼에게는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["The messenger hurried before him, along a grove of little rooms, and turning into one at the end of the grove, looked round the door.", "심부름꾼이 작은 방들이 늘어선 줄을 따라 그보다 앞서 서둘러 가더니, 그 줄 끝의 한 방으로 돌아 들어가 문 안을 둘러보았다."], ["Physician was close upon him, and looked round the door too.", "의사도 바짝 뒤따라가 마찬가지로 문 안을 둘러보았다."]], [["There was a bath in that corner, from which the water had been hastily drained off.", "그 구석에 욕조가 하나 있었는데, 물은 황급히 빼낸 뒤였다."], ["Lying in it, as in a grave or sarcophagus, with a hurried drapery of sheet and blanket thrown across it, was the body of a heavily-made man, with an obtuse head, and coarse, mean, common features.", "무덤이나 석관 속에 누인 듯 그 안에 누워 있는 것은, 시트와 담요가 황급히 덮인 채로, 육중하게 생긴 한 사내의 시신이었다. 둔한 머리에, 거칠고 천하고 평범한 이목구비였다."], ["A sky-light had been opened to release the steam with which the room had been filled; but it hung, condensed into water-drops, heavily upon the walls, and heavily upon the face and figure in the bath.", "방을 가득 채웠던 증기를 빼내려고 천창을 열어 둔 터였으나, 증기는 물방울로 응결되어 벽에도 무겁게, 욕조 속 얼굴과 몸에도 무겁게 매달려 있었다."], ["The room was still hot, and the marble of the bath still warm; but the face and figure were clammy to the touch.", "방은 아직 후끈했고 욕조의 대리석도 아직 따뜻했다. 그러나 얼굴과 몸은 손에 닿는 감촉이 끈적하고 차가웠다."], ["The white marble at the bottom of the bath was veined with a dreadful red.", "욕조 바닥의 흰 대리석은 무시무시한 붉은빛으로 줄무늬져 있었다."], ["On the ledge at the side, were an empty laudanum-bottle and a tortoise-shell handled penknife--soiled, but not with ink.", "옆쪽 선반 위에는 빈 아편팅크 병 하나와 거북등껍질 손잡이가 달린 주머니칼 하나가 있었는데, 칼은 더럽혀져 있었으되 잉크로 더럽혀진 것은 아니었다."]], [["‘Separation of jugular vein--death rapid--been dead at least half an hour.’ This echo of the physician’s words ran through the passages and little rooms, and through the house while he was yet straightening himself from having bent down to reach to the bottom of the bath, and while he was yet dabbling his hands in water; redly veining it as the marble was veined, before it mingled into one tint.", "\"경정맥 절단—급사—적어도 삼십 분 전에 사망.\" 의사의 이 말이 메아리쳐 통로와 작은 방들을 지나, 온 집 안으로 퍼져 나갔다. 그가 아직 욕조 바닥에 손을 뻗으려 굽혔던 몸을 펴는 중이었고, 아직 두 손을 물에 적시는 중이었으며, 그 물이 대리석이 줄무늬졌던 것처럼 붉게 줄무늬지다가 이내 한 빛깔로 뒤섞이기 전이었다."]], [["He turned his eyes to the dress upon the sofa, and to the watch, money, and pocket-book on the table.", "그는 소파 위의 옷가지로, 또 탁자 위의 시계와 돈과 수첩으로 눈을 돌렸다. 수첩에 반쯤 끼워지고 반쯤 비어져 나온 접힌 쪽지 하나가 그의 살피는 눈길을 사로잡았다."], ["A folded note half buckled up in the pocket-book, and half protruding from it, caught his observant glance.", "그는 그것을 바라보고, 만져 보고, 책장 사이에서 조금 더 끌어내며 조용히 말했다."], ["He looked at it, touched it, pulled it a little further out from among the leaves, said quietly, ‘This is addressed to me,’ and opened and read it.", "\"이건 내게 보낸 것이군.\" 그러고는 그것을 펴서 읽었다."]], [["There were no directions for him to give.", "그가 내려야 할 지시 사항은 없었다."], ["The people of the house knew what to do; the proper authorities were soon brought; and they took an equable business-like possession of the deceased, and of what had been his property, with no greater disturbance of manner or countenance than usually attends the winding-up of a clock.", "그 집 사람들은 무엇을 해야 하는지 알고 있었다. 관계 당국이 곧 불려 왔고, 그들은 시계의 태엽을 마저 감을 때 흔히 따르는 정도의 동요밖에는 태도에도 안색에도 드러내지 않은 채, 고인과 그가 소유했던 것들을 차분하고 사무적으로 인수했다."], ["Physician was glad to walk out into the night air--was even glad, in spite of his great experience, to sit down upon a door-step for a little while: feeling sick and faint.", "의사는 밤공기 속으로 걸어 나오게 되어 다행이었다—그 풍부한 경험에도 불구하고, 잠시 문간 계단에 앉아 있게 되어 다행이기까지 했다. 속이 메스껍고 어지러웠던 것이다."]], [["Bar was a near neighbour of his, and, when he came to the house, he saw a light in the room where he knew his friend often sat late getting up his work.", "법조계 인사는 그의 가까운 이웃이었는데, 그 집에 이르렀을 때 의사는 친구가 종종 늦도록 앉아 일을 준비하던 방에 불이 켜져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["As the light was never there when Bar was not, it gave him assurance that Bar was not yet in bed.", "법조계 인사가 없을 때는 그 방에 불이 켜진 적이 없었으니, 그것은 그가 아직 잠자리에 들지 않았다는 확신을 주었다."], ["In fact, this busy bee had a verdict to get to-morrow, against evidence, and was improving the shining hours in setting snares for the gentlemen of the jury.", "사실 이 부지런한 꿀벌은 증거에 맞서 내일 평결을 얻어 내야 했고, 배심원 여러분에게 덫을 놓느라 빛나는 시간을 알차게 보내는 중이었다."]], [["Physician’s knock astonished Bar; but, as he immediately suspected that somebody had come to tell him that somebody else was robbing him, or otherwise trying to get the better of him, he came down promptly and softly.", "의사의 노크 소리에 법조계 인사는 놀랐다. 그러나 그는 누군가 자기 물건을 훔치고 있다거나, 아니면 어떻게든 자기를 이기려 든다는 것을 알리러 온 사람이 있다고 곧장 의심했기에, 잽싸고 살그머니 내려왔다."], ["He had been clearing his head with a lotion of cold water, as a good preparative to providing hot water for the heads of the jury, and had been reading with the neck of his shirt thrown wide open that he might the more freely choke the opposite witnesses.", "그는 배심원들의 머리에 끼얹을 뜨거운 물을 마련하기 좋은 준비로 찬물 세정제로 제 머리를 식히던 참이었고, 맞은편 증인들의 목을 더 자유로이 졸라매고자 셔츠 깃을 활짝 풀어헤친 채 책을 읽던 참이었다."], ["In consequence, he came down, looking rather wild.", "그래서 내려오는 그의 모습은 자못 사나워 보였다."], ["Seeing Physician, the least expected of men, he looked wilder and said, ‘What’s the matter?’", "가장 예상치 못한 사람인 의사를 보자 그는 더욱 사나운 표정으로 말했다. \"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘You asked me once what Merdle’s complaint was.’", "\"언젠가 자네가 내게 머들의 병이 무엇이냐고 물었지.\""]], [["‘Extraordinary answer!", "\"별난 대답이로군!"], ["I know I did.’", "그랬지, 그래.\""]], [["‘I told you I had not found out.’", "\"못 알아냈다고 내가 말했고.\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"그래."], ["I know you did.’", "그랬다는 거 알아.\""]], [["‘I have found it out.’", "\"그걸 알아냈네.\""]], [["‘My God!’ said Bar, starting back, and clapping his hand upon the other’s breast.", "\"맙소사!\" 법조계 인사가 화들짝 물러서며 상대의 가슴팍에 손을 탁 짚으며 말했다."], ["‘And so have I!", "\"나도 알아냈어!"], ["I see it in your face.’", "자네 얼굴에 쓰여 있군.\""]], [["They went into the nearest room, where Physician gave him the letter to read.", "그들은 가장 가까운 방으로 들어갔고, 거기서 의사가 그에게 편지를 건네 읽게 했다."], ["He read it through half-a-dozen times.", "그는 그것을 대여섯 번 읽었다."], ["There was not much in it as to quantity; but it made a great demand on his close and continuous attention.", "분량으로 치면 별것 아니었으나, 그의 면밀하고 지속적인 주의를 크게 요했다."], ["He could not sufficiently give utterance to his regret that he had not himself found a clue to this.", "그는 이 일의 단서를 제 손으로 찾아내지 못한 데 대한 후회를 이루 다 말로 표현하지 못했다."], ["The smallest clue, he said, would have made him master of the case, and what a case it would have been to have got to the bottom of!", "아주 작은 단서만 있었어도 그 사건을 손에 넣을 수 있었을 거라고, 또 바닥까지 파헤쳐 보았다면 얼마나 대단한 사건이었겠느냐고 그는 말했다."]], [["Physician had engaged to break the intelligence in Harley Street.", "의사는 할리 가에 그 소식을 전하기로 되어 있었다."], ["Bar could not at once return to his inveiglements of the most enlightened and remarkable jury he had ever seen in that box, with whom, he could tell his learned friend, no shallow sophistry would go down, and no unhappily abused professional tact and skill prevail (this was the way he meant to begin with them); so he said he would go too, and would loiter to and fro near the house while his friend was inside.", "법조계 인사는 자기가 그 자리에서 일찍이 그 배심석에서 본 가장 계몽되고 비범한 배심원들을 꾀어 들이는 일—학식 높은 친구에게 말할 수 있거니와, 어떤 얄팍한 궤변도 그들에게는 통하지 않으며, 어떤 가련하게 오용된 직업적 재치와 솜씨도 먹히지 않을 그 배심원들(이것이 그가 그들에게 운을 떼려던 방식이었다)—로 당장 돌아갈 수는 없었다. 그래서 자기도 함께 가서, 친구가 집 안에 있는 동안 근처를 오락가락하며 어슬렁대겠다고 말했다."], ["They walked there, the better to recover self-possession in the air; and the wings of day were fluttering the night when Physician knocked at the door.", "둘은 바깥 공기 속에서 평정을 더 잘 되찾고자 그곳까지 걸어갔다. 의사가 문을 두드릴 무렵에는 날의 날개가 밤을 펄럭이게 하고 있었다."]], [["A footman of rainbow hues, in the public eye, was sitting up for his master--that is to say, was fast asleep in the kitchen over a couple of candles and a newspaper, demonstrating the great accumulation of mathematical odds against the probabilities of a house being set on fire by accident When this serving man was roused, Physician had still to await the rousing of the Chief Butler.", "세상의 눈에 무지갯빛으로 비치는 하인 하나가 주인을 기다리며 깨어 있었다—말하자면, 양초 두 자루와 신문 한 부를 앞에 두고 부엌에서 곯아떨어져 있었으니, 집이 우연히 불타게 될 개연성을 거스르는 수학적 확률의 막대한 누적을 몸소 증명하는 셈이었다. 이 하인을 깨우고 나서도 의사는 집사장이 깨어나기를 또 기다려야 했다."], ["At last that noble creature came into the dining-room in a flannel gown and list shoes; but with his cravat on, and a Chief Butler all over.", "마침내 그 고귀한 존재가 플란넬 가운에 펠트 신을 신은 채 식당으로 들어왔다. 그러나 넥타이는 매고 있었으며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 영락없는 집사장이었다."], ["It was morning now.", "이제 아침이었다."], ["Physician had opened the shutters of one window while waiting, that he might see the light.", "의사는 기다리는 동안 빛을 보려고 창 하나의 덧문을 열어 두었다."]], [["‘Mrs Merdle’s maid must be called, and told to get Mrs Merdle up, and prepare her as gently as she can to see me.", "\"머들 부인의 시녀를 불러서, 머들 부인을 깨우고, 나를 만날 수 있도록 가능한 한 조심스레 마음의 준비를 시키라고 일러 주시오."], ["I have dreadful news to break to her.’", "부인께 전해야 할 끔찍한 소식이 있소.\""]], [["Thus Physician to the Chief Butler.", "의사가 집사장에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["The latter, who had a candle in his hand, called his man to take it away.", "손에 양초를 들고 있던 집사장은 자기 부하를 불러 그것을 치우게 했다."], ["Then he approached the window with dignity; looking on at Physician’s news exactly as he had looked on at the dinners in that very room.", "그러고는 위엄 있게 창가로 다가가더니, 바로 그 방에서 만찬을 지켜보던 것과 똑같은 태도로 의사의 소식을 지켜보았다."]], [["‘Mr Merdle is dead.’", "\"머들 씨가 돌아가셨소.\""]], [["‘I should wish,’ said the Chief Butler, ‘to give a month’s notice.’", "\"한 달 전에 미리 사직 통보를 드리고 싶습니다.\" 집사장이 말했다."]], [["‘Mr Merdle has destroyed himself.’", "\"머들 씨가 스스로 목숨을 끊었소.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said the Chief Butler, ‘that is very unpleasant to the feelings of one in my position, as calculated to awaken prejudice; and I should wish to leave immediately.’", "\"선생님,\" 집사장이 말했다, \"그건 제 위치에 있는 사람의 감정에 매우 불쾌한 일이며, 편견을 불러일으킬 만한 일입니다. 그러니 저는 즉시 떠나고 싶습니다.\""]], [["‘If you are not shocked, are you not surprised, man?’ demanded the Physician, warmly.", "\"충격을 받지 않았다 치더라도, 자네는 놀랍지도 않은가, 이 사람아?\" 의사가 발끈하여 따져 물었다."]], [["The Chief Butler, erect and calm, replied in these memorable words.", "집사장은 꼿꼿하고 침착하게 다음과 같은 기억할 만한 말로 대답했다."], ["‘Sir, Mr Merdle never was the gentleman, and no ungentlemanly act on Mr Merdle’s part would surprise me.", "\"선생님, 머들 씨는 애당초 신사가 아니었습니다. 그러니 머들 씨가 어떤 신사답지 못한 행동을 했더라도 저는 놀라지 않습니다."], ["Is there anybody else I can send to you, or any other directions I can give before I leave, respecting what you would wish to be done?’", "제가 떠나기 전에 선생님께 더 보내 드릴 사람이 있을지요, 아니면 선생님이 처리되기를 바라시는 일에 관해 제가 남길 다른 지시가 있을지요?\""]], [["When Physician, after discharging himself of his trust up-stairs, rejoined Bar in the street, he said no more of his interview with Mrs Merdle than that he had not yet told her all, but that what he had told her she had borne pretty well.", "의사는 위층에서 자기 소임을 다하고 나서 거리에서 법조계 인사와 다시 만났을 때, 머들 부인과의 면담에 대해서는 그저 아직 그녀에게 다 말하지는 못했지만 자기가 전한 것만큼은 그녀가 제법 잘 견뎌 냈다고만 말했다. 법조계 인사는 거리에서의 여가를 배심원 전원을 단번에 잡아들일 더없이 교묘한 덫을 고안하는 데 바쳤다."], ["Bar had devoted his leisure in the street to the construction of a most ingenious man-trap for catching the whole of his jury at a blow; having got that matter settled in his mind, it was lucid on the late catastrophe, and they walked home slowly, discussing it in every bearing.", "그 문제를 마음속으로 정리해 두고 나니 머릿속이 그 최근의 참사 쪽으로 명료해져서, 둘은 그것을 온갖 측면에서 논하며 천천히 집으로 걸었다. 의사의 집 문 앞에서 헤어지기 전, 둘은 햇살 비치는 아침 하늘을 올려다보았는데, 그곳으로 몇몇 이른 불의 연기와 몇몇 이른 기상자들의 입김과 목소리가 평화로이 피어오르고 있었다."], ["Before parting at the Physician’s door, they both looked up at the sunny morning sky, into which the smoke of a few early fires and the breath and voices of a few early stirrers were peacefully rising, and then looked round upon the immense city, and said, if all those hundreds and thousands of beggared people who were yet asleep could only know, as they two spoke, the ruin that impended over them, what a fearful cry against one miserable soul would go up to Heaven!", "그러고 나서 둘은 그 거대한 도시를 둘러보며 이렇게 말했다. 아직 잠들어 있는 저 수백 수천의 빈털터리가 된 사람들이, 우리 둘이 이야기하는 지금 자기네 위에 닥쳐오는 파멸을 알 수만 있다면, 한 가련한 영혼을 향한 얼마나 무시무시한 외침이 하늘로 치솟겠는가!"]], [["The report that the great man was dead, got about with astonishing rapidity.", "그 위대한 인물이 죽었다는 소식은 놀라운 속도로 퍼졌다."], ["At first, he was dead of all the diseases that ever were known, and of several bran-new maladies invented with the speed of Light to meet the demand of the occasion.", "처음에 그는 일찍이 알려진 온갖 병으로, 그리고 그 상황의 수요에 맞춰 빛의 속도로 발명된 몇 가지 신종 질환으로 죽은 셈이 되었다."], ["He had concealed a dropsy from infancy, he had inherited a large estate of water on the chest from his grandfather, he had had an operation performed upon him every morning of his life for eighteen years, he had been subject to the explosion of important veins in his body after the manner of fireworks, he had had something the matter with his lungs, he had had something the matter with his heart, he had had something the matter with his brain.", "그는 갓난아기 적부터 수종을 감춰 왔고, 할아버지에게서 가슴에 물이 차는 거대한 유산을 물려받았으며, 평생 십팔 년 동안 매일 아침 수술을 받았고, 폭죽처럼 몸속 중요한 혈관들이 터지는 일을 겪어 왔으며, 폐에 무슨 탈이 있었고, 심장에 무슨 탈이 있었고, 뇌에 무슨 탈이 있었다."], ["Five hundred people who sat down to breakfast entirely uninformed on the whole subject, believed before they had done breakfast, that they privately and personally knew Physician to have said to Mr Merdle, ‘You must expect to go out, some day, like the snuff of a candle;’ and that they knew Mr Merdle to have said to Physician, ‘A man can die but once.’ By about eleven o’clock in the forenoon, something the matter with the brain, became the favourite theory against the field; and by twelve the something had been distinctly ascertained to be ‘Pressure.’", "이 문제에 대해 전혀 아는 바 없이 아침 식사에 앉은 오백 명의 사람들이, 식사를 마치기도 전에, 의사가 머들 씨에게 \"언젠가 촛불 심지처럼 꺼져 버릴 각오를 하셔야 합니다\"라고 말하는 것을 자기네가 개인적으로 직접 들어 알고 있으며, 또 머들 씨가 의사에게 \"사람은 한 번만 죽는 법이지요\"라고 말하는 것을 자기네가 알고 있다고 믿게 되었다. 오전 열한 시쯤에는 뇌에 무슨 탈이 있었다는 설이 다른 모든 설을 제치고 가장 인기 있는 학설이 되었고, 열두 시에는 그 무슨 탈이라는 게 '압박'이라고 분명히 확정되었다."]], [["Pressure was so entirely satisfactory to the public mind, and seemed to make everybody so comfortable, that it might have lasted all day but for Bar’s having taken the real state of the case into Court at half-past nine.", "압박은 대중의 마음에 더없이 흡족했고 모두를 더없이 편안하게 해 주는 듯해서, 법조계 인사가 아홉 시 반에 그 사건의 진상을 법정으로 가져가지만 않았더라면 하루 종일 갔을지도 모른다."], ["This led to its beginning to be currently whispered all over London by about one, that Mr Merdle had killed himself.", "이로 인해 한 시쯤에는 머들 씨가 자살했다는 소문이 온 런던에 두루 속삭여지기 시작했다."], ["Pressure, however, so far from being overthrown by the discovery, became a greater favourite than ever.", "그러나 압박은 그 발견으로 뒤집히기는커녕 그 어느 때보다 더 인기를 끌게 되었다."], ["There was a general moralising upon Pressure, in every street.", "거리마다 압박에 대한 일반적인 교훈이 늘어놓아졌다."], ["All the people who had tried to make money and had not been able to do it, said, There you were!", "돈을 벌어 보려다 그러지 못한 사람들은 죄다 이렇게 말했다, 거 봐!"], ["You no sooner began to devote yourself to the pursuit of wealth than you got Pressure.", "부의 추구에 몸 바치기 시작하자마자 압박이 오는 거라고."], ["The idle people improved the occasion in a similar manner.", "게으른 사람들도 비슷한 식으로 그 기회를 활용했다."], ["See, said they, what you brought yourself to by work, work, work!", "봐, 그들은 말했다, 일하고 또 일하고 또 일해서 네 꼴이 어찌 됐는지 보라고!"], ["You persisted in working, you overdid it.", "너는 끈질기게 일했고, 도를 넘게 했어."], ["Pressure came on, and you were done for!", "압박이 닥쳤고, 너는 끝장난 거야!"], ["This consideration was very potent in many quarters, but nowhere more so than among the young clerks and partners who had never been in the slightest danger of overdoing it.", "이런 생각은 여러 방면에서 매우 강력했으나, 도를 넘게 일할 위험이라고는 털끝만큼도 없었던 젊은 서기들과 동업자들 사이에서보다 더 강력한 데는 없었다."], ["These, one and all, declared, quite piously, that they hoped they would never forget the warning as long as they lived, and that their conduct might be so regulated as to keep off Pressure, and preserve them, a comfort to their friends, for many years.", "이들은 너 나 할 것 없이, 자못 경건하게, 살아 있는 한 그 경고를 결코 잊지 않기를 바라며, 자기네 처신을 잘 단속하여 압박을 막고 오랜 세월 벗들에게 위안이 되도록 제 몸을 지키기를 바란다고 선언했다."]], [["But, at about the time of High ‘Change, Pressure began to wane, and appalling whispers to circulate, east, west, north, and south.", "그러나 거래소가 가장 붐비는 시각쯤 되자 압박은 시들기 시작했고, 동서남북으로 소름 끼치는 속삭임이 돌기 시작했다."], ["At first they were faint, and went no further than a doubt whether Mr Merdle’s wealth would be found to be as vast as had been supposed; whether there might not be a temporary difficulty in ‘realising’ it; whether there might not even be a temporary suspension (say a month or so), on the part of the wonderful Bank.", "처음에 그것은 희미하여, 머들 씨의 재산이 짐작했던 만큼 막대한 것으로 드러날지, 그것을 '현금화'하는 데 일시적인 어려움이 있지는 않을지, 심지어 그 놀라운 은행 쪽에서 일시적인 지급 정지(이를테면 한 달쯤)가 있지는 않을지 하는 의심에 그쳤다."], ["As the whispers became louder, which they did from that time every minute, they became more threatening.", "그 속삭임은, 그때부터 매 순간 그러했듯이, 점점 커지면서 점점 더 위협적이 되어 갔다."], ["He had sprung from nothing, by no natural growth or process that any one could account for; he had been, after all, a low, ignorant fellow; he had been a down-looking man, and no one had ever been able to catch his eye; he had been taken up by all sorts of people in quite an unaccountable manner; he had never had any money of his own, his ventures had been utterly reckless, and his expenditure had been most enormous.", "그는 누구도 설명할 수 없는 어떤 자연스러운 성장이나 과정도 없이 무에서 솟아난 자였고, 결국은 비천하고 무지한 작자였으며, 늘 눈을 내리깔던 사내여서 누구도 그의 눈을 마주칠 수 없었고, 도무지 까닭 모를 방식으로 온갖 부류의 사람들에게 떠받들어진 자였으며, 제 돈이라고는 가져 본 적이 없고, 그의 투기는 더없이 무모했으며, 그의 지출은 더없이 어마어마했다."], ["In steady progression, as the day declined, the talk rose in sound and purpose.", "날이 저물어 감에 따라 꾸준히, 그 입소문은 소리와 목적에서 점점 높아졌다."], ["He had left a letter at the Baths addressed to his physician, and his physician had got the letter, and the letter would be produced at the Inquest on the morrow, and it would fall like a thunderbolt upon the multitude he had deluded.", "그는 목욕탕에 자기 주치의 앞으로 편지를 한 통 남겼고, 그 주치의가 편지를 받았으며, 그 편지가 내일 검시 심문에서 공개될 텐데, 그것은 그가 속여 온 군중에게 벼락처럼 떨어질 것이라고 했다."], ["Numbers of men in every profession and trade would be blighted by his insolvency; old people who had been in easy circumstances all their lives would have no place of repentance for their trust in him but the workhouse; legions of women and children would have their whole future desolated by the hand of this mighty scoundrel.", "온갖 직업과 업종의 수많은 사람들이 그의 파산으로 짓밟힐 것이며, 평생 넉넉히 살아온 노인들은 그를 믿었던 것을 뉘우칠 곳이라곤 구빈원밖에 없을 것이며, 무수한 여자와 아이들이 이 거대한 악당의 손에 자기네 온 앞날을 황폐하게 당할 것이었다."], ["Every partaker of his magnificent feasts would be seen to have been a sharer in the plunder of innumerable homes; every servile worshipper of riches who had helped to set him on his pedestal, would have done better to worship the Devil point-blank.", "그의 호화로운 잔치에 참석했던 자는 누구나 무수한 가정을 약탈한 데 한몫한 자로 드러날 것이며, 그를 받침대 위에 올려놓는 데 거든, 부를 비굴하게 숭배하던 자는 누구나 차라리 악마를 정면으로 숭배하는 편이 나았을 것이었다."], ["So, the talk, lashed louder and higher by confirmation on confirmation, and by edition after edition of the evening papers, swelled into such a roar when night came, as might have brought one to believe that a solitary watcher on the gallery above the Dome of St Paul’s would have perceived the night air to be laden with a heavy muttering of the name of Merdle, coupled with every form of execration.", "그리하여 그 입소문은 확인에 확인이 거듭되고 석간신문이 판을 거듭 찍어 냄에 따라 점점 더 크고 높이 채찍질당해, 밤이 오자 어찌나 큰 포효로 부풀어 올랐던지, 세인트 폴 대성당 돔 위 회랑에서 홀로 지켜보는 자가 있었다면 밤공기에 머들이라는 이름이 온갖 형태의 저주와 짝지어 무겁게 웅얼대며 실려 있음을 느꼈으리라 믿게 할 만했다."]], [["For by that time it was known that the late Mr Merdle’s complaint had been simply Forgery and Robbery.", "이는 그때쯤 고 머들 씨의 병이 다름 아닌 위조와 도둑질이었음이 알려졌기 때문이다."], ["He, the uncouth object of such wide-spread adulation, the sitter at great men’s feasts, the roc’s egg of great ladies’ assemblies, the subduer of exclusiveness, the leveller of pride, the patron of patrons, the bargain-driver with a Minister for Lordships of the Circumlocution Office, the recipient of more acknowledgment within some ten or fifteen years, at most, than had been bestowed in England upon all peaceful public benefactors, and upon all the leaders of all the Arts and Sciences, with all their works to testify for them, during two centuries at least--he, the shining wonder, the new constellation to be followed by the wise men bringing gifts, until it stopped over a certain carrion at the bottom of a bath and disappeared--was simply the greatest Forger and the greatest Thief that ever cheated the gallows.", "그토록 널리 퍼진 아첨의 투박한 대상이요, 위인들의 잔치에 앉던 자요, 귀부인들 모임의 로크 새 알이요, 배타성을 굴복시킨 자요, 오만을 평탄케 한 자요, 후원자들의 후원자요, 에두름국의 작위들을 두고 장관과 흥정을 벌이던 자요, 길어야 한 십 년이나 십오 년 안에, 적어도 두 세기 동안 영국에서 모든 평화로운 공공 후원자들과 그들의 업적을 증언할 모든 작품을 지닌 모든 예술과 과학의 지도자 전부에게 베풀어진 것보다 더 많은 찬사를 받은 자요, 빛나는 경이요, 선물을 든 현인들이 따라가던 새로운 별자리—욕조 바닥의 어떤 썩은 고깃덩이 위에서 멈춰 사라질 때까지 그들이 따라가던 그 별자리—였던 그가, 그는 다름 아니라 일찍이 교수대를 속여 넘긴 가장 위대한 위조범이요 가장 위대한 도둑이었던 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER 26. Reaping the Whirlwind", "# CHAPTER 26. Reaping the Whirlwind"]], [["With a precursory sound of hurried breath and hurried feet, Mr Pancks rushed into Arthur Clennam’s Counting-house.", "그 회오리바람을 거두어들이며\n가쁜 숨소리와 다급한 발소리가 미리 들려오더니, 팽크스 씨가 아서 클레넘의 사무실로 달려들었다."], ["The Inquest was over, the letter was public, the Bank was broken, the other model structures of straw had taken fire and were turned to smoke.", "심문은 끝났고, 그 편지는 세상에 공개되었으며, 은행은 무너졌고, 짚으로 지어진 다른 모범적 구조물들도 불이 붙어 연기가 되어 버렸다."], ["The admired piratical ship had blown up, in the midst of a vast fleet of ships of all rates, and boats of all sizes; and on the deep was nothing but ruin; nothing but burning hulls, bursting magazines, great guns self-exploded tearing friends and neighbours to pieces, drowning men clinging to unseaworthy spars and going down every minute, spent swimmers, floating dead, and sharks.", "모두가 우러르던 그 해적선은 온갖 등급의 함선들과 온갖 크기의 작은 배들로 이루어진 거대한 함대 한가운데서 폭발했고, 깊은 바다에는 파멸 말고는 아무것도 없었다. 불타는 선체들, 터지는 화약고들, 저절로 폭발하여 벗과 이웃을 갈가리 찢어 놓는 대포들, 부서진 돛대 조각에 매달려 매 순간 가라앉는 익사자들, 기진한 헤엄꾼들, 떠다니는 시신들, 그리고 상어들 말고는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["The usual diligence and order of the Counting-house at the Works were overthrown.", "공장 사무실의 여느 부지런함과 질서는 무너져 있었다."], ["Unopened letters and unsorted papers lay strewn about the desk.", "뜯지 않은 편지들과 정리되지 않은 서류들이 책상 위에 어지러이 흩어져 있었다."], ["In the midst of these tokens of prostrated energy and dismissed hope, the master of the Counting-house stood idle in his usual place, with his arms crossed on the desk, and his head bowed down upon them.", "꺾여 버린 기력과 사라진 희망의 이 표지들 한가운데에, 사무실의 주인은 여느 때 서 있던 자리에 멍하니 서서, 두 팔을 책상 위에 포개고 그 위에 머리를 떨군 채였다."]], [["Mr Pancks rushed in and saw him, and stood still.", "팽크스 씨는 달려들어와 그를 보고는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["In another minute, Mr Pancks’s arms were on the desk, and Mr Pancks’s head was bowed down upon them; and for some time they remained in these attitudes, idle and silent, with the width of the little room between them.", "잠시 뒤, 팽크스 씨의 두 팔도 책상 위에 놓였고, 팽크스 씨의 머리도 그 위에 떨궈졌다. 한동안 그들은 이런 자세로, 작은 방의 너비만큼 떨어진 채, 멍하니 말없이 그대로 있었다."]], [["Mr Pancks was the first to lift up his head and speak.", "먼저 머리를 들고 입을 연 것은 팽크스 씨였다."]], [["‘I persuaded you to it, Mr Clennam.", "\"제가 권한 거였습니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["I know it.", "압니다."], ["Say what you will.", "무슨 말씀이든 하십시오."], ["You can’t say more to me than I say to myself.", "제가 스스로에게 하는 말보다 더 심하게는 못 하실 겁니다."], ["You can’t say more than I deserve.’", "제가 받아 마땅한 것보다 더 심하게는 못 하실 거예요.\""]], [["‘O, Pancks, Pancks!’ returned Clennam, ‘don’t speak of deserving.", "\"오, 팽크스, 팽크스!\" 클레넘이 대답했다."], ["What do I myself deserve!’", "\"받아 마땅하다는 말은 하지 마시오. 그렇다면 나 자신은 무엇을 받아 마땅하단 말이오!\""]], [["‘Better luck,’ said Pancks.", "\"더 나은 운이겠지요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘I,’ pursued Clennam, without attending to him, ‘who have ruined my partner! Pancks, Pancks, I have ruined Doyce! The honest, self-helpful, indefatigable old man who has worked his way all through his life; the man who has contended against so much disappointment, and who has brought out of it such a good and hopeful nature; the man I have felt so much for, and meant to be so true and useful to; I have ruined him--brought him to shame and disgrace--ruined him, ruined him!’", "\"나는 말이오,\" 클레넘이 그의 말은 듣는 둥 마는 둥 이어 갔다. \"내 동업자를 망쳐 놓은 사람이오! 팽크스, 팽크스, 나는 도이스를 망쳤소! 정직하고, 제 힘으로 일어서고, 지칠 줄 모르고 평생 길을 헤쳐 온 그 노인을. 그토록 많은 좌절에 맞서 싸우면서도, 그 속에서 그토록 선하고 희망에 찬 천성을 길어 올린 사람을. 내가 그토록 마음을 쓰고, 그토록 참되고 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고자 했던 그 사람을. 나는 그를 망쳤소—수치와 불명예로 끌어내렸소—그를 망쳤어, 망쳤단 말이오!\""]], [["The agony into which the reflection wrought his mind was so distressing to see, that Mr Pancks took hold of himself by the hair of his head, and tore it in desperation at the spectacle.", "이 생각이 그의 마음을 몰아넣은 고통이 보기에 너무도 괴로워서, 팽크스 씨는 그 광경에 절망하며 제 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 쥐어뜯었다."]], [["‘Reproach me!’ cried Pancks.", "\"저를 나무라십시오!\" 팽크스가 외쳤다."], ["‘Reproach me, sir, or I’ll do myself an injury.", "\"저를 나무라세요, 선생님. 안 그러면 제가 제 몸에 상처라도 내겠습니다."], ["Say,--You fool, you villain.", "말씀하세요—이 멍청아, 이 악당아."], ["Say,--Ass, how could you do it; Beast, what did you mean by it!", "말씀하세요—이 얼간아, 어떻게 그럴 수 있었느냐. 이 짐승아, 무슨 속셈이었느냐!"], ["Catch hold of me somewhere.", "제 어디라도 붙잡으십시오."], ["Say something abusive to me!’ All the time, Mr Pancks was tearing at his tough hair in a most pitiless and cruel manner.", "저한테 욕설을 퍼부어 주세요!\" 그러는 내내 팽크스 씨는 더없이 무자비하고 잔인하게 제 억센 머리카락을 쥐어뜯고 있었다."]], [["‘If you had never yielded to this fatal mania, Pancks,’ said Clennam, more in commiseration than retaliation, ‘it would have been how much better for you, and how much better for me!’", "\"당신이 이 끔찍한 광기에 굴복하지만 않았더라도, 팽크스,\" 클레넘이 앙갚음이라기보다는 가엾이 여기는 마음으로 말했다. \"당신에게도 얼마나 더 나았겠으며, 내게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소!\""]], [["‘At me again, sir!’ cried Pancks, grinding his teeth in remorse.", "\"또 저를 나무라세요, 선생님!\" 팽크스가 회한에 이를 갈며 외쳤다."], ["‘At me again!’", "\"또 나무라세요!\""]], [["‘If you had never gone into those accursed calculations, and brought out your results with such abominable clearness,’ groaned Clennam, ‘it would have been how much better for you, Pancks, and how much better for me!’", "\"당신이 그 저주받을 계산에 들어가지만 않았더라도, 그 가증스러우리만치 명확한 결과를 끄집어내지만 않았더라도,\" 클레넘이 신음했다. \"당신에게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소, 팽크스. 내게도 얼마나 더 나았겠소!\""]], [["‘At me again, sir!’ exclaimed Pancks, loosening his hold of his hair; ‘at me again, and again!’", "\"또 저를 나무라세요, 선생님!\" 팽크스가 머리카락 쥔 손을 풀며 외쳤다. \"또, 또 나무라세요!\""]], [["Clennam, however, finding him already beginning to be pacified, had said all he wanted to say, and more.", "그러나 클레넘은 그가 벌써 진정되기 시작하는 것을 보고는, 하고 싶었던 말, 아니 그 이상을 다 한 셈이었다. 그는 그저 그의 손을 꽉 쥐며 덧붙였다."], ["He wrung his hand, only adding, ‘Blind leaders of the blind, Pancks!", "\"눈먼 자가 눈먼 자를 이끌었구려, 팽크스! 눈먼 자가 눈먼 자를 이끌었어!"], ["Blind leaders of the blind!", "하지만 도이스, 도이스, 도이스."], ["But Doyce, Doyce, Doyce; my injured partner!’ That brought his head down on the desk once more.", "내가 해를 끼친 동업자!\" 그 말과 함께 그의 머리는 다시 한 번 책상 위로 떨궈졌다."]], [["Their former attitudes and their former silence were once more first encroached upon by Pancks.", "그들의 아까 그 자세와 아까 그 침묵을, 또 먼저 깬 것은 팽크스였다."]], [["‘Not been to bed, sir, since it began to get about.", "\"이 일이 새어 나가기 시작한 뒤로 잠자리에 들지 못했습니다, 선생님."], ["Been high and low, on the chance of finding some hope of saving any cinders from the fire.", "불길 속에서 타다 남은 재 한 줌이라도 건질 가망이 있을까 싶어 여기저기 뛰어다녔어요."], ["All in vain.", "다 헛수고였습니다."], ["All gone.", "다 사라졌어요."], ["All vanished.’", "다 없어졌습니다.\""]], [["‘I know it,’ returned Clennam, ‘too well.’", "\"나도 알고 있소,\" 클레넘이 대답했다. \"너무도 잘 알고 있어요.\""]], [["Mr Pancks filled up a pause with a groan that came out of the very depths of his soul.", "팽크스 씨는 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳에서 우러나온 신음으로 그 침묵을 메웠다."]], [["‘Only yesterday, Pancks,’ said Arthur; ‘only yesterday, Monday, I had the fixed intention of selling, realising, and making an end of it.’", "\"바로 어제 말이오, 팽크스,\" 아서가 말했다. \"바로 어제, 월요일에, 나는 팔아치우고, 현금으로 바꾸고, 이 일에서 손을 떼겠다고 마음을 굳혔었소.\""]], [["‘I can’t say as much for myself, sir,’ returned Pancks.", "\"저로서는 그런 말씀도 못 드리겠습니다, 선생님.\" 팽크스가 대답했다."], ["‘Though it’s wonderful how many people I’ve heard of, who were going to realise yesterday, of all days in the three hundred and sixty-five, if it hadn’t been too late!’", "\"하지만 일 년 삼백예순닷새 중에 하필 어제 현금화하려 했었다는 사람을 제가 얼마나 많이 들었는지 놀라울 따름입니다. 너무 늦지만 않았더라면 말이지요!\""]], [["His steam-like breathings, usually droll in their effect, were more tragic than so many groans: while from head to foot, he was in that begrimed, besmeared, neglected state, that he might have been an authentic portrait of Misfortune which could scarcely be discerned through its want of cleaning.", "증기처럼 내뿜는 그의 숨소리는, 평소에는 우스꽝스러운 효과를 내곤 했으나, 지금은 그 어떤 신음보다도 더 비극적이었다. 머리끝부터 발끝까지 그가 때 묻고 얼룩지고 돌보지 않은 꼴이어서, 마치 너무 더러워 알아보기조차 어려운, 불운을 그린 진품 초상화라 해도 될 지경이었다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, had you laid out--everything?’ He got over the break before the last word, and also brought out the last word itself with great difficulty.", "\"클레넘 씨, 전부 다… 쏟아부으셨습니까?\" 그는 마지막 낱말 앞에서 한 번 말을 끊었다가 가까스로 넘겼고, 그 마지막 낱말 자체도 무척 힘겹게 내뱉었다."]], [["‘Everything.’", "\"전부 다요.\""]], [["Mr Pancks took hold of his tough hair again, and gave it such a wrench that he pulled out several prongs of it.", "팽크스 씨는 다시 제 억센 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 어찌나 세게 잡아당겼는지 몇 가닥이 뽑혀 나왔다."], ["After looking at these with an eye of wild hatred, he put them in his pocket.", "사나운 증오의 눈으로 그것을 바라보더니, 그는 그것을 주머니에 집어넣었다."]], [["‘My course,’ said Clennam, brushing away some tears that had been silently dropping down his face, ‘must be taken at once.", "\"내가 갈 길은,\" 클레넘이 얼굴에 소리 없이 흘러내리던 눈물 몇 방울을 닦으며 말했다."], ["What wretched amends I can make must be made.", "\"당장 정해야겠소."], ["I must clear my unfortunate partner’s reputation.", "내가 할 수 있는 비참한 보상이나마, 해야만 하오."], ["I must retain nothing for myself.", "나는 불운한 내 동업자의 명예를 깨끗이 해야 하오."], ["I must resign to our creditors the power of management I have so much abused, and I must work out as much of my fault--or crime--as is susceptible of being worked out in the rest of my days.’", "나 자신을 위해서는 아무것도 남겨 두어서는 안 되오. 내가 그토록 함부로 휘둘렀던 경영권을 채권자들에게 넘겨주어야 하며, 내 잘못—아니, 죄—을, 남은 평생 동안 갚아낼 수 있는 만큼은 갚아내야 하오.\""]], [["‘Is it impossible, sir, to tide over the present?’", "\"지금 이 고비를 넘기는 것은 불가능합니까, 선생님?\""]], [["‘Out of the question.", "\"말도 안 되오."], ["Nothing can be tided over now, Pancks.", "지금은 그 무엇도 넘길 수 없소, 팽크스."], ["The sooner the business can pass out of my hands, the better for it.", "이 사업이 내 손에서 빨리 떠날수록 사업에는 더 나은 거요."], ["There are engagements to be met, this week, which would bring the catastrophe before many days were over, even if I would postpone it for a single day by going on for that space, secretly knowing what I know.", "이번 주에 갚아야 할 약정들이 있어서, 내가 아는 바를 남몰래 알면서도 단 하루라도 이어 가며 그만큼 미루려 한다 해도, 며칠 지나지 않아 파국이 닥칠 거요. 어젯밤 내내 나는 무엇을 해야 할지 생각했소."], ["All last night I thought of what I would do; what remains is to do it.’", "이제 남은 건 그것을 실행하는 일이오.\""]], [["‘Not entirely of yourself?’ said Pancks, whose face was as damp as if his steam were turning into water as fast as he dismally blew it off.", "\"혼자서 다 하시려는 건 아니겠지요?\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Have some legal help.’", "그의 얼굴은, 침울하게 증기를 내뿜는 속도만큼이나 빨리 그 증기가 물로 변하기라도 하듯 축축했다. \"법률적인 도움을 좀 받으십시오.\""]], [["‘Perhaps I had better.’", "\"그게 낫겠지요.\""]], [["‘Have Rugg.’", "\"러그를 부르십시오.\""]], [["‘There is not much to do.", "\"할 일이 많지는 않소."], ["He will do it as well as another.’", "그가 다른 사람만큼은 해줄 거요.\""]], [["‘Shall I fetch Rugg, Mr Clennam?’", "\"러그를 데려올까요, 클레넘 씨?\""]], [["‘If you could spare the time, I should be much obliged to you.’", "\"시간을 내줄 수 있다면, 정말 고맙겠소.\""]], [["Mr Pancks put on his hat that moment, and steamed away to Pentonville.", "팽크스 씨는 그 즉시 모자를 쓰고는 펜턴빌을 향해 증기처럼 내달렸다."], ["While he was gone Arthur never raised his head from the desk, but remained in that one position.", "그가 가 있는 동안 아서는 책상에서 머리를 한 번도 들지 않고, 그 한 가지 자세 그대로 있었다."]], [["Mr Pancks brought his friend and professional adviser, Mr Rugg, back with him.", "팽크스 씨는 자기 벗이자 직업상의 조언자인 러그 씨를 데리고 돌아왔다."], ["Mr Rugg had had such ample experience, on the road, of Mr Pancks’s being at that present in an irrational state of mind, that he opened his professional mediation by requesting that gentleman to take himself out of the way.", "러그 씨는 오는 길 내내 팽크스 씨가 지금 이성을 잃은 상태라는 것을 충분히 겪은 터라, 그 신사에게 비켜 있어 달라고 청하면서 직업적 중재를 시작했다."], ["Mr Pancks, crushed and submissive, obeyed.", "짓눌리고 고분고분해진 팽크스 씨는 그 말에 따랐다."]], [["‘He is not unlike what my daughter was, sir, when we began the Breach of Promise action of Rugg and Bawkins, in which she was Plaintiff,’ said Mr Rugg.", "\"저 친구는, 우리가 러그 대 보킨스의 혼인 약속 위반 소송을 시작했을 때의 제 딸과 비슷합니다, 선생님. 그 소송에서 딸이 원고였지요.\""], ["‘He takes too strong and direct an interest in the case.", "러그 씨가 말했다. \"사건에 너무 강하고 직접적으로 마음을 쏟거든요."], ["His feelings are worked upon.", "감정이 휘둘리는 겁니다."], ["There is no getting on, in our profession, with feelings worked upon, sir.’", "우리 같은 직업에서는, 감정이 휘둘려서는 일이 되질 않습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["As he pulled off his gloves and put them in his hat, he saw, in a side glance or two, that a great change had come over his client.", "장갑을 벗어 모자 안에 넣으면서, 그는 곁눈질 한두 번에 자기 의뢰인에게 큰 변화가 닥쳤음을 알아챘다."]], [["‘I am sorry to perceive, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘that you have been allowing your own feelings to be worked upon.", "\"이거 안타깝습니다, 선생님.\" 러그 씨가 말했다."], ["Now, pray don’t, pray don’t.", "\"선생님께서 스스로 감정에 휘둘리도록 내버려 두셨군요. 자, 부디 그러지 마십시오, 부디."], ["These losses are much to be deplored, sir, but we must look ‘em in the face.’", "이 손실들은 참으로 통탄할 일입니다, 선생님. 하지만 우리는 그것을 똑바로 마주 보아야 합니다.\""]], [["‘If the money I have sacrificed had been all my own, Mr Rugg,’ sighed Mr Clennam, ‘I should have cared far less.’", "\"내가 날려 버린 그 돈이 전부 내 것이었다면, 러그 씨,\" 클레넘 씨가 한숨지었다. \"훨씬 덜 마음이 쓰였을 거요.\""]], [["‘Indeed, sir?’ said Mr Rugg, rubbing his hands with a cheerful air.", "\"정말이십니까, 선생님?\" 러그 씨가 유쾌한 기색으로 두 손을 비비며 말했다."], ["‘You surprise me.", "\"놀랍습니다."], ["That’s singular, sir.", "그건 별난 일이군요, 선생님."], ["I have generally found, in my experience, that it’s their own money people are most particular about.", "제 경험으로는, 사람들이 가장 까다롭게 구는 건 대개 자기 돈이거든요."], ["I have seen people get rid of a good deal of other people’s money, and bear it very well: very well indeed.’", "남의 돈이라면 꽤 많이 날려 놓고도 아주 잘 견뎌 내는 사람들을 저는 봐 왔습니다. 정말이지 아주 잘 견디더군요.\""]], [["With these comforting remarks, Mr Rugg seated himself on an office-stool at the desk and proceeded to business.", "이런 위로의 말들과 함께 러그 씨는 책상 앞 사무용 걸상에 앉아 본론으로 들어갔다."]], [["‘Now, Mr Clennam, by your leave, let us go into the matter.", "\"자, 클레넘 씨, 괜찮으시다면 사안을 짚어 봅시다."], ["Let us see the state of the case.", "사건의 상태를 봅시다."], ["The question is simple.", "문제는 간단합니다."], ["The question is the usual plain, straightforward, common-sense question.", "늘 그렇듯 명료하고 단도직입적이고 상식적인 문제예요. 우리가 우리 자신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐?"], ["What can we do for ourself? What can we do for ourself?’", "우리가 우리 자신을 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐?\""]], [["‘This is not the question with me, Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur.", "\"그건 나에게는 문제가 아니오, 러그 씨.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘You mistake it in the beginning.", "\"당신은 처음부터 잘못 짚고 있소."], ["It is, what can I do for my partner, how can I best make reparation to him?’", "문제는, 내가 내 동업자를 위해 무엇을 할 수 있느냐, 어떻게 그에게 가장 잘 배상할 수 있느냐 하는 거요.\""]], [["‘I am afraid, sir, do you know,’ argued Mr Rugg persuasively, ‘that you are still allowing your feeling to be worked upon.", "\"이거 송구합니다만, 선생님, 아십니까,\" 러그 씨가 구슬리듯 따졌다."], ["I _don’t_ like the term “reparation,”", "\"선생님께서는 여전히 감정에 휘둘리고 계십니다."], ["sir, except as a lever in the hands of counsel.", "저는 '배상'이라는 말을 좋아하지 않습니다, 선생님."], ["Will you excuse my saying that I feel it my duty to offer you the caution, that you really must not allow your feelings to be worked upon?’", "변호인의 손에 쥐어진 지렛대로 쓰일 때라면 모를까요. 외람되지만, 정말로 감정에 휘둘리셔서는 안 된다는 점을 일러 드리는 게 제 의무라고 느낍니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Rugg,’ said Clennam, nerving himself to go through with what he had resolved upon, and surprising that gentleman by appearing, in his despondency, to have a settled determination of purpose; ‘you give me the impression that you will not be much disposed to adopt the course I have made up my mind to take.", "\"러그 씨,\" 클레넘이 결심한 일을 끝까지 해내려고 마음을 다잡으며 말했는데, 그 의기소침함 속에서도 확고한 목적의식을 드러내어 그 신사를 놀라게 했다."], ["If your disapproval of it should render you unwilling to discharge such business as it necessitates, I am sorry for it, and must seek other aid.", "\"당신은 내가 마음먹은 길을 그다지 따르고 싶어하지 않으리라는 인상을 주는구려. 그것을 못마땅히 여겨 거기에 필요한 일을 맡기를 꺼린다면, 안타까운 일이지만 나는 다른 도움을 구해야 할 거요."], ["But I will represent to you at once, that to argue against it with me is useless.’", "그러나 분명히 말해 두겠소. 그 일을 두고 나와 따져 봐야 소용없다는 것을.\""]], [["‘Good, sir,’ answered Mr Rugg, shrugging his shoulders.", "\"좋습니다, 선생님.\" 러그 씨가 어깨를 으쓱하며 대답했다."], ["‘Good, sir.", "\"좋습니다, 선생님."], ["Since the business is to be done by some hands, let it be done by mine.", "누군가의 손으로 해야 할 일이라면, 제 손으로 하지요."], ["Such was my principle in the case of Rugg and Bawkins.", "러그 대 보킨스 사건에서도 그게 제 원칙이었습니다."], ["Such is my principle in most cases.’", "대개의 사건에서 그게 제 원칙이고요.\""]], [["Clennam then proceeded to state to Mr Rugg his fixed resolution.", "이어 클레넘은 러그 씨에게 자기의 확고한 결심을 설명했다."], ["He told Mr Rugg that his partner was a man of great simplicity and integrity, and that in all he meant to do, he was guided above all things by a knowledge of his partner’s character, and a respect for his feelings.", "그는 러그 씨에게, 자기 동업자가 더없이 소박하고 청렴한 사람이라는 것, 그리고 자기가 하려는 모든 일에서 무엇보다도 그 동업자의 성품에 대한 이해와 그 감정에 대한 존중을 지침으로 삼고 있다는 것을 말했다."], ["He explained that his partner was then absent on an enterprise of importance, and that it particularly behoved himself publicly to accept the blame of what he had rashly done, and publicly to exonerate his partner from all participation in the responsibility of it, lest the successful conduct of that enterprise should be endangered by the slightest suspicion wrongly attaching to his partner’s honour and credit in another country.", "그는, 동업자가 지금 중요한 사업차 멀리 나가 있다는 것, 그리고 자기가 경솔하게 저지른 일에 대한 책임을 공개적으로 받아들이고, 동업자가 그 책임에 조금도 가담하지 않았음을 공개적으로 밝혀 주는 것이 특히 자기에게 마땅한 도리라는 것을 설명했다. 다른 나라에 있는 동업자의 명예와 신용에 부당하게 조금이라도 의심이 따라붙어, 그 사업의 성공적인 수행이 위태로워지는 일이 없도록 말이다."], ["He told Mr Rugg that to clear his partner morally, to the fullest extent, and publicly and unreservedly to declare that he, Arthur Clennam, of that Firm, had of his own sole act, and even expressly against his partner’s caution, embarked its resources in the swindles that had lately perished, was the only real atonement within his power; was a better atonement to the particular man than it would be to many men; and was therefore the atonement he had first to make.", "그는 러그 씨에게, 동업자의 도덕적 결백을 더없이 온전하게 밝혀 주는 것, 그리고 그 상사(商社)의 일원인 자기 아서 클레넘이 오로지 단독으로, 그것도 동업자의 만류를 명백히 거스르면서까지, 최근에 파산한 그 사기 사업에 사업 자금을 쏟아부었음을 공개적이고 거리낌 없이 선언하는 것이야말로, 자기 힘이 닿는 유일한 진정한 속죄라고 말했다. 그것은 다른 여러 사람에게 하는 것보다 그 특정한 사람에게는 더 나은 속죄이며, 그렇기에 자기가 가장 먼저 해야 할 속죄라는 것이었다."], ["With this view, his intention was to print a declaration to the foregoing effect, which he had already drawn up; and, besides circulating it among all who had dealings with the House, to advertise it in the public papers.", "이런 취지에서 그의 뜻은, 앞서 말한 내용을 담아 이미 작성해 둔 선언문을 인쇄하여, 그 상사와 거래하던 모든 이에게 돌리는 것은 물론, 신문에 광고로도 싣겠다는 것이었다."], ["Concurrently with this measure (the description of which cost Mr Rugg innumerable wry faces and great uneasiness in his limbs), he would address a letter to all the creditors, exonerating his partner in a solemn manner, informing them of the stoppage of the House until their pleasure could be known and his partner communicated with, and humbly submitting himself to their direction.", "이 조치와 더불어(이를 설명하는 동안 러그 씨는 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 찡그린 표정을 짓고 사지를 크게 들썩였다), 그는 모든 채권자에게 편지를 보내, 엄숙한 방식으로 동업자의 결백을 밝히고, 그들의 뜻을 알 수 있고 동업자와 연락이 닿을 때까지 상사의 영업을 중단함을 알리며, 겸허히 자기 자신을 그들의 처분에 맡기려 했다. 만일 그들이 동업자의 결백을 헤아려, 사업을 다시 수익성 있게 재개하고 지금의 몰락을 극복할 수 있을 만한 궤도에 일이 오르게 된다면, 그때는 자기 몫을 동업자에게 돌려주겠다는 것이었다."], ["If, through their consideration for his partner’s innocence, the affairs could ever be got into such train as that the business could be profitably resumed, and its present downfall overcome, then his own share in it should revert to his partner, as the only reparation he could make to him in money value for the distress and loss he had unhappily brought upon him, and he himself, at as small a salary as he could live upon, would ask to be allowed to serve the business as a faithful clerk.", "자기가 불행히도 그에게 안긴 고통과 손실에 대해 금전적 가치로 갚을 수 있는 유일한 배상이 그것이기 때문이었다. 그리고 자기 자신은, 먹고살 수 있는 한도에서 가장 적은 봉급을 받으며, 충실한 사무원으로서 그 사업에 봉사하도록 허락받기를 청하겠다고 했다."]], [["Though Mr Rugg saw plainly there was no preventing this from being done, still the wryness of his face and the uneasiness of his limbs so sorely required the propitiation of a Protest, that he made one. ‘I offer no objection, sir,’ said he, ‘I argue no point with you. I will carry out your views, sir; but, under protest.’ Mr Rugg then stated, not without prolixity, the heads of his protest. These were, in effect, because the whole town, or he might say the whole country, was in the first madness of the late discovery, and the resentment against the victims would be very strong: those who had not been deluded being certain to wax exceedingly wroth with them for not having been as wise as they were: and those who had been deluded being certain to find excuses and reasons for themselves, of which they were equally certain to see that other sufferers were wholly devoid: not to mention the great probability of every individual sufferer persuading himself, to his violent indignation, that but for the example of all the other sufferers he never would have put himself in the way of suffering. Because such a declaration as Clennam’s, made at such a time, would certainly draw down upon him a storm of animosity, rendering it impossible to calculate on forbearance in the creditors, or on unanimity among them; and exposing him a solitary target to a straggling cross-fire, which might bring him down from half-a-dozen quarters at once.", "러그 씨는 이 일이 실행되는 것을 막을 수 없음을 빤히 알았으나, 그래도 찡그린 그의 얼굴과 들썩이는 사지가 항의 한마디로 달래지기를 어찌나 절실히 요구하는지, 그는 항의를 하나 내놓았다. \"저는 반대하지 않습니다, 선생님.\" 그가 말했다. \"한 가지도 따지지 않겠습니다. 선생님의 뜻을 받들겠습니다, 선생님. 다만, 항의는 해 두겠습니다.\" 이어 러그 씨는 장황하지 않다 할 수 없게 자기 항의의 요점들을 늘어놓았다. 그 요점들은, 요컨대 이런 것이었다. 온 도시가, 아니 온 나라가 이번 사건의 발각으로 처음의 광란에 빠져 있고, 피해자들에 대한 분노가 몹시 강할 것이다. 속아 넘어가지 않은 자들은 피해자들이 자기들처럼 영리하지 못했다며 틀림없이 길길이 화를 낼 것이고, 속아 넘어간 자들은 자기 자신에게는 변명과 이유를 찾아내면서도, 다른 피해자들에게는 그런 것이 전혀 없다고 똑같이 확신할 것이 틀림없으니 말이다. 게다가 피해자 한 사람 한 사람이, 다른 모든 피해자가 본을 보이지만 않았던들 자기는 결코 고통받을 처지에 빠지지 않았으리라고 스스로를 격렬한 분개 속에 설득할 공산이 크다는 것은 말할 것도 없었다. 또한, 이런 때에 내놓는 클레넘의 그런 선언은 틀림없이 그에게 적의의 폭풍을 불러내려, 채권자들 사이에서 관용을 기대하거나 그들의 의견 일치를 기대하기란 불가능해지고, 그를 외로운 표적으로 만들어, 산발적인 십자포화 속에 놓아, 한꺼번에 대여섯 방향에서 그를 쓰러뜨릴지도 모른다는 것이었다."]], [["To all this Clennam merely replied that, granting the whole protest, nothing in it lessened the force, or could lessen the force, of the voluntary and public exoneration of his partner.", "이 모든 것에 클레넘은 그저, 항의 전체를 인정한다 해도, 그 안의 그 무엇도 동업자를 자발적이고 공개적으로 면죄해 주는 일의 효력을 줄이지 못하며 줄일 수도 없다고만 대답했다."], ["He therefore, once and for all, requested Mr Rugg’s immediate aid in getting the business despatched.", "그러므로 그는 단번에 영원히, 일이 신속히 처리되도록 러그 씨가 곧바로 도와줄 것을 청했다."], ["Upon that, Mr Rugg fell to work; and Arthur, retaining no property to himself but his clothes and books, and a little loose money, placed his small private banker’s-account with the papers of the business.", "그러자 러그 씨는 일에 착수했다. 그리고 아서는 자기 옷가지와 책들, 그리고 약간의 잔돈 말고는 아무 재산도 자기 몫으로 남기지 않은 채, 자기의 보잘것없는 개인 은행 계좌를 사업 서류들과 함께 내놓았다."]], [["The disclosure was made, and the storm raged fearfully.", "진상이 공개되었고, 폭풍은 무섭게 휘몰아쳤다."], ["Thousands of people were wildly staring about for somebody alive to heap reproaches on; and this notable case, courting publicity, set the living somebody so much wanted, on a scaffold.", "수천 명의 사람들이 비난을 퍼부을 산 사람 하나를 찾아 미친 듯이 두리번거리고 있었다. 그리고 이 세상의 이목을 끄는 두드러진 사건은, 그토록 절실히 찾던 그 산 사람 하나를 처형대 위에 세워 놓았다."], ["When people who had nothing to do with the case were so sensible of its flagrancy, people who lost money by it could scarcely be expected to deal mildly with it.", "이 사건과 아무 상관도 없는 사람들조차 그 파렴치함을 이토록 절감했으니, 이 일로 돈을 잃은 사람들이 그것을 너그럽게 다루기를 기대하기란 어려운 일이었다."], ["Letters of reproach and invective showered in from the creditors; and Mr Rugg, who sat upon the high stool every day and read them all, informed his client within a week that he feared there were writs out.", "채권자들로부터 비난과 욕설의 편지가 빗발쳤다. 그리고 날마다 그 높은 걸상에 앉아 그것을 모조리 읽던 러그 씨는, 일주일 안에 의뢰인에게 영장이 발부된 듯하다고 알려 왔다."]], [["‘I must take the consequences of what I have done,’ said Clennam.", "\"내가 한 일의 결과는 내가 감당해야지요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘The writs will find me here.’", "\"영장은 여기서 나를 찾아낼 거요.\""]], [["On the very next morning, as he was turning in Bleeding Heart Yard by Mrs Plornish’s corner, Mrs Plornish stood at the door waiting for him, and mysteriously besought him to step into Happy Cottage.", "바로 그다음 날 아침, 그가 블리딩 하트 야드로 들어서면서 플로니시 부인의 모퉁이를 돌 때, 플로니시 부인이 문가에 서서 그를 기다리고 있다가, 비밀스럽게 해피 코티지로 들어와 달라고 청했다."], ["There he found Mr Rugg.", "그곳에서 그는 러그 씨를 만났다."]], [["‘I thought I’d wait for you here.", "\"여기서 선생님을 기다리는 게 낫겠다 싶었습니다."], ["I wouldn’t go on to the Counting-house this morning if I was you, sir.’", "제가 선생님이라면 오늘 아침 사무실에는 안 나가겠습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["‘Why not, Mr Rugg?’", "\"왜 그렇소, 러그 씨?\""]], [["‘There are as many as five out, to my knowledge.’", "\"제가 아는 것만 해도 다섯 건이나 나와 있습니다.\""]], [["‘It cannot be too soon over,’ said Clennam.", "\"빠를수록 좋소.\" 클레넘이 말했다."], ["‘Let them take me at once.’", "\"당장 나를 잡아가게 합시다.\""]], [["‘Yes, but,’ said Mr Rugg, getting between him and the door, ‘hear reason, hear reason.", "\"네, 하지만요,\" 러그 씨가 그와 문 사이를 가로막으며 말했다."], ["They’ll take you soon enough, Mr Clennam, I don’t doubt; but, hear reason.", "\"이치를 들어 보십시오, 이치를 들어 보세요. 그들은 곧 선생님을 잡아갈 겁니다, 클레넘 씨, 그건 의심치 않습니다."], ["It almost always happens, in these cases, that some insignificant matter pushes itself in front and makes much of itself.", "하지만 이치를 들어 보세요. 이런 경우에는 거의 늘, 별것 아닌 사소한 건이 앞으로 밀고 나와 제딴에 으스대게 마련입니다."], ["Now, I find there’s a little one out--a mere Palace Court jurisdiction--and I have reason to believe that a caption may be made upon that.", "그런데 보니 작은 게 하나 나와 있어요—한낱 궁정재판소 관할의 것이지요—그런데 제가 보기엔 그걸로 체포가 이루어질 듯합니다."], ["I wouldn’t be taken upon that.’", "저라면 그걸로는 잡혀가지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Why not?’ asked Clennam.", "\"왜 그렇소?\" 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘I’d be taken on a full-grown one, sir,’ said Mr Rugg.", "\"저라면 제대로 격을 갖춘 큰 건으로 잡혀가겠습니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘It’s as well to keep up appearances.", "러그 씨가 말했다. \"체면은 차리는 게 좋지요."], ["As your professional adviser, I should prefer your being taken on a writ from one of the Superior Courts, if you have no objection to do me that favour.", "선생님의 직업적 조언자로서, 외람되나마 저를 봐서라도, 상급 법원들 가운데 하나에서 나온 영장으로 잡혀가시는 편이 좋겠습니다."], ["It looks better.’", "그게 보기에 더 낫습니다.\""]], [["‘Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur, in his dejection, ‘my only wish is, that it should be over.", "\"러그 씨,\" 아서가 의기소침하게 말했다."], ["I will go on, and take my chance.’", "\"내 유일한 바람은, 이 일이 끝나는 것뿐이오. 나는 그냥 가서, 운에 맡기겠소.\""]], [["‘Another word of reason, sir!’ cried Mr Rugg.", "\"이치를 한마디만 더 들어 보십시오, 선생님!\""], ["‘Now, this _is_ reason.", "러그 씨가 외쳤다."], ["The other may be taste; but this is reason.", "\"자, 이건 이치입니다. 아까 그건 취향일 수도 있겠지요."], ["If you should be taken on a little one, sir, you would go to the Marshalsea.", "하지만 이건 이치입니다. 작은 건으로 잡혀가시면, 선생님은 마셜시로 가게 됩니다."], ["Now, you know what the Marshalsea is.", "자, 마셜시가 어떤 곳인지 아시지요."], ["Very close.", "아주 좁습니다."], ["Excessively confined.", "지나치게 비좁아요."], ["Whereas in the King’s Bench--’ Mr Rugg waved his right hand freely, as expressing abundance of space.", "그에 비해 왕좌재판소라면—\" 러그 씨는 널찍한 공간을 나타내듯 오른손을 시원하게 내저었다."]], [["‘I would rather,’ said Clennam, ‘be taken to the Marshalsea than to any other prison.’", "\"나는,\" 클레넘이 말했다. \"다른 어떤 감옥보다도 마셜시로 잡혀가는 편이 낫소.\""]], [["‘Do you say so indeed, sir?’ returned Mr Rugg.", "\"정말로 그러십니까, 선생님?\" 러그 씨가 대꾸했다."], ["‘Then this is taste, too, and we may be walking.’", "\"그렇다면 그것도 취향이로군요. 그럼 가 보도록 하지요.\""]], [["He was a little offended at first, but he soon overlooked it.", "그는 처음엔 다소 언짢아했으나, 곧 그 일을 넘겨 버렸다."], ["They walked through the Yard to the other end.", "그들은 야드를 가로질러 반대편 끝으로 걸어갔다."], ["The Bleeding Hearts were more interested in Arthur since his reverses than formerly; now regarding him as one who was true to the place and had taken up his freedom.", "블리딩 하트의 주민들은 아서가 몰락을 겪은 뒤로 전보다 그에게 더 큰 관심을 보였다. 이제 그를 이 동네에 충실하여 그 자유권을 얻은 사람쯤으로 여기는 것이었다."], ["Many of them came out to look after him, and to observe to one another, with great unctuousness, that he was ‘pulled down by it.’ Mrs Plornish and her father stood at the top of the steps at their own end, much depressed and shaking their heads.", "그들 가운데 많은 이가 나와서 그를 지켜보았고, 그가 \"이 일로 풀이 죽었다\"고 서로에게 자못 능청스럽게 속삭였다. 플로니시 부인과 그녀의 아버지는 자기들 쪽 끝 층계 꼭대기에 서서, 몹시 침울하게 고개를 절레절레 젓고 있었다."]], [["There was nobody visibly in waiting when Arthur and Mr Rugg arrived at the Counting-house.", "아서와 러그 씨가 사무실에 이르렀을 때 기다리는 사람은 보이지 않았다."], ["But an elderly member of the Jewish persuasion, preserved in rum, followed them close, and looked in at the glass before Mr Rugg had opened one of the day’s letters.", "그러나 럼주에 절여진 유대교 신앙의 한 노인이 그들을 바짝 뒤따라와, 러그 씨가 그날 온 편지 하나를 채 뜯기도 전에 유리창 너머로 안을 들여다보았다. \"오!\""], ["‘Oh!’ said Mr Rugg, looking up.", "러그 씨가 고개를 들며 말했다."], ["‘How do you do?", "\"안녕하시오?"], ["Step in--Mr Clennam, I think this is the gentleman I was mentioning.’", "들어오시오—클레넘 씨, 제가 말씀드렸던 그 신사인 것 같습니다.\""]], [["This gentleman explained the object of his visit to be ‘a tyfling madder ob bithznithz,’ and executed his legal function.", "이 신사는 자기가 찾아온 목적이 \"쪼끄만 일거리\"라고 설명하고는, 자기 법적 직무를 수행했다. * 럼주에 절여진: 술에 절어 사는 모습을 익살스럽게 표현한 말."]], [["‘Shall I accompany you, Mr Clennam?’ asked Mr Rugg politely, rubbing his hands.", "\"제가 동행할까요, 클레넘 씨?\" 러그 씨가 두 손을 비비며 정중히 물었다."]], [["‘I would rather go alone, thank you.", "\"혼자 가는 편이 낫겠소, 고맙소."], ["Be so good as send me my clothes.’ Mr Rugg in a light airy way replied in the affirmative, and shook hands with him.", "내 옷가지나 보내 주시면 고맙겠소.\" 러그 씨는 가벼운 어조로 그러겠다고 대답하고는, 그와 악수했다."], ["He and his attendant then went down-stairs, got into the first conveyance they found, and drove to the old gates.", "그러고 나서 그와 그의 동행자는 아래층으로 내려가, 처음 눈에 띈 탈것에 올라타고는, 그 오래된 문들을 향해 마차를 몰았다."]], [["‘Where I little thought, Heaven forgive me,’ said Clennam to himself, ‘that I should ever enter thus!’", "\"하느님, 용서하소서. 내가 이런 식으로 이곳에 들어오게 되리라고는,\" 클레넘이 혼잣말을 했다. \"꿈에도 생각지 못했건만!\""]], [["Mr Chivery was on the Lock, and Young John was in the Lodge: either newly released from it, or waiting to take his own spell of duty.", "치버리 씨가 자물쇠를 맡고 있었고, 젊은 존은 수위실에 있었다. 막 근무를 마치고 나온 참이거나, 아니면 제 차례의 당번을 맡으려고 기다리는 중이었다."], ["Both were more astonished on seeing who the prisoner was, than one might have thought turnkeys would have been.", "두 사람은 죄수가 누구인지 알아보고는, 간수라면 그러려니 짐작할 정도보다 훨씬 더 놀랐다. 나이 든 치버리 씨는 면구스러운 듯한 태도로 그와 악수하며 말했다."], ["The elder Mr Chivery shook hands with him in a shame-faced kind of way, and said, ‘I don’t call to mind, sir, as I was ever less glad to see you.’ The younger Mr Chivery, more distant, did not shake hands with him at all; he stood looking at him in a state of indecision so observable that it even came within the observation of Clennam with his heavy eyes and heavy heart.", "\"선생님, 선생님을 뵙고 이토록 반갑지 않았던 적은 없는 것 같습니다.\" 더 서먹한 젊은 치버리 씨는 그와 악수도 전혀 하지 않았다."], ["Presently afterwards, Young John disappeared into the jail.", "그는 망설이는 기색이 어찌나 뚜렷했던지, 무거운 눈과 무거운 마음을 한 클레넘의 눈에까지 들어올 정도로, 그를 바라보며 서 있었다. 잠시 뒤, 젊은 존은 감옥 안으로 사라졌다."]], [["As Clennam knew enough of the place to know that he was required to remain in the Lodge a certain time, he took a seat in a corner, and feigned to be occupied with the perusal of letters from his pocket.", "클레넘은 이곳을 충분히 알고 있어서, 자기가 수위실에 일정 시간 머물러 있어야 한다는 것을 알았으므로, 한쪽 구석에 자리를 잡고, 주머니에서 꺼낸 편지들을 읽는 척했다."], ["They did not so engross his attention, but that he saw, with gratitude, how the elder Mr Chivery kept the Lodge clear of prisoners; how he signed to some, with his keys, not to come in, how he nudged others with his elbows to go out, and how he made his misery as easy to him as he could.", "그러나 그 편지들이 그의 주의를 다 사로잡지는 못했기에, 그는 나이 든 치버리 씨가 죄수들이 수위실에 들어오지 못하게 하는 모습을 고맙게 지켜보았다. 어떤 이들에게는 열쇠로 들어오지 말라는 신호를 보내고, 또 어떤 이들은 팔꿈치로 슬쩍 밀어 내보내며, 그가 겪는 비참함을 되도록 덜어 주려 애쓰는 모습을 말이다."]], [["Arthur was sitting with his eyes fixed on the floor, recalling the past, brooding over the present, and not attending to either, when he felt himself touched upon the shoulder.", "아서는 눈을 바닥에 고정한 채, 과거를 떠올리고 현재를 곱씹으면서도 둘 중 어느 쪽에도 마음을 두지 못한 채 앉아 있었는데, 그때 누군가 자기 어깨를 건드리는 것을 느꼈다. 젊은 존이었다."], ["It was by Young John; and he said, ‘You can come now.’", "그가 말했다. \"이제 들어오셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["He got up and followed Young John.", "그는 일어나 젊은 존을 따라갔다."], ["When they had gone a step or two within the inner iron-gate, Young John turned and said to him:", "안쪽 철문을 한두 걸음 들어선 뒤, 젊은 존이 돌아서서 그에게 말했다."]], [["‘You want a room.", "\"방이 필요하시겠지요."], ["I have got you one.’", "하나 마련해 두었습니다.\""]], [["‘I thank you heartily.’", "\"진심으로 고맙소.\""]], [["Young John turned again, and took him in at the old doorway, up the old staircase, into the old room.", "젊은 존은 다시 돌아서서, 그를 그 옛 문간으로 들여, 그 옛 층계를 올라, 그 옛 방으로 데려갔다."], ["Arthur stretched out his hand.", "아서가 손을 내밀었다."], ["Young John looked at it, looked at him--sternly--swelled, choked, and said:", "젊은 존은 그 손을 보고, 그를 보더니—매섭게—울컥하여, 목이 메어, 말했다."]], [["‘I don’t know as I can.", "\"악수는 못 하겠습니다."], ["No, I find I can’t.", "아니요, 못 하겠어요."], ["But I thought you’d like the room, and here it is for you.’", "하지만 이 방을 마음에 들어 하실 것 같아서요. 그러니 여기, 선생님 방입니다.\""]], [["Surprise at this inconsistent behaviour yielded when he was gone (he went away directly) to the feelings which the empty room awakened in Clennam’s wounded breast, and to the crowding associations with the one good and gentle creature who had sanctified it.", "이 모순된 행동에 대한 놀라움은, 그가 가 버리자(그는 곧장 떠났다), 텅 빈 방이 클레넘의 상처 입은 가슴에 불러일으킨 감정들에, 그리고 이 방을 거룩하게 했던 그 선하고 다정한 한 사람과 얽힌 밀려드는 회상들에 자리를 내주었다. 처지가 뒤바뀐 지금 그녀가 없다는 것이, 이 방을, 그리고 그 안의 그를, 너무도 황량하게, 그리고 사랑과 진실이 어린 그런 얼굴을 너무도 절실히 필요로 하게 만들어서, 그는 벽을 향해 돌아서서 흐느꼈다."], ["Her absence in his altered fortunes made it, and him in it, so very desolate and so much in need of such a face of love and truth, that he turned against the wall to weep, sobbing out, as his heart relieved itself, ‘O my Little Dorrit!’", "마음이 풀려나면서, 흐느끼며 그는 토해 냈다. \"오, 나의 리틀 도릿!\""]], [["# CHAPTER 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea", "# CHAPTER 27. The Pupil of the Marshalsea"]], [["The day was sunny, and the Marshalsea, with the hot noon striking upon it, was unwontedly quiet.", "날은 화창했고, 뜨거운 한낮의 볕이 내리쬐는 마셜시는 평소와 달리 고요했다."], ["Arthur Clennam dropped into a solitary arm-chair, itself as faded as any debtor in the jail, and yielded himself to his thoughts.", "아서 클레넘은 이 감옥의 어느 채무자 못지않게 빛바랜 외딴 안락의자에 몸을 묻고서, 자신의 상념에 몸을 내맡겼다."]], [["In the unnatural peace of having gone through the dreaded arrest, and got there,--the first change of feeling which the prison most commonly induced, and from which dangerous resting-place so many men had slipped down to the depths of degradation and disgrace by so many ways,--he could think of some passages in his life, almost as if he were removed from them into another state of existence.", "그토록 두려워하던 체포를 다 겪고 마침내 이곳에 들어와 버린 데서 오는 부자연스러운 평온 속에서—이는 감옥이 가장 흔히 불러일으키는 첫 번째 감정의 변화로, 그토록 많은 사람이 그 위험한 안식처에서 그토록 많은 길을 거쳐 타락과 치욕의 나락으로 미끄러져 내려간 그 평온이었는데—그는 자신이 마치 그 일들에서 떨어져 나와 또 다른 존재의 상태로 옮겨 간 것처럼, 제 인생의 몇몇 대목을 떠올릴 수 있었다."], ["Taking into account where he was, the interest that had first brought him there when he had been free to keep away, and the gentle presence that was equally inseparable from the walls and bars about him and from the impalpable remembrances of his later life which no walls or bars could imprison, it was not remarkable that everything his memory turned upon should bring him round again to Little Dorrit.", "그가 지금 있는 곳, 자유로이 멀리할 수 있었음에도 처음 그를 이곳으로 이끌었던 관심, 그리고 그를 둘러싼 벽과 창살에서도, 또 어떤 벽이나 창살로도 가둘 수 없는 만년의 손에 잡히지 않는 추억들에서도 똑같이 떼어 놓을 수 없던 그 다정한 존재—이 모든 것을 헤아려 보면, 그의 기억이 어디로 향하든 끝내 다시 리틀 도릿에게로 돌아오는 것은 놀랄 일이 아니었다. 그렇지만 그에게는 그것이 놀라웠다."], ["Yet it was remarkable to him; not because of the fact itself, but because of the reminder it brought with it, how much the dear little creature had influenced his better resolutions.", "그 사실 자체 때문이 아니라, 그 사실이 함께 가져다주는 일깨움 때문이었다—그 사랑스러운 작은 존재가 그의 더 나은 결심들에 얼마나 큰 영향을 끼쳤는지를."]], [["None of us clearly know to whom or to what we are indebted in this wise, until some marked stop in the whirling wheel of life brings the right perception with it.", "우리 가운데 누구도, 인생이라는 빙글빙글 도는 수레바퀴에 어떤 뚜렷한 멈춤이 와서 올바른 깨달음을 함께 가져다주기 전까지는, 이런 식으로 우리가 누구에게 또는 무엇에 빚지고 있는지 분명히 알지 못한다."], ["It comes with sickness, it comes with sorrow, it comes with the loss of the dearly loved, it is one of the most frequent uses of adversity.", "그 깨달음은 병과 함께 오고, 슬픔과 함께 오며, 끔찍이 사랑하던 이를 잃을 때 오니, 역경이 주는 가장 흔한 쓸모 가운데 하나다."], ["It came to Clennam in his adversity, strongly and tenderly.", "그것은 역경에 처한 클레넘에게 강렬하면서도 부드럽게 찾아왔다."], ["‘When I first gathered myself together,’ he thought, ‘and set something like purpose before my jaded eyes, whom had I before me, toiling on, for a good object’s sake, without encouragement, without notice, against ignoble obstacles that would have turned an army of received heroes and heroines?", "'내가 처음으로 정신을 다잡고서,' 그는 생각했다, '지친 두 눈앞에 무언가 목적 비슷한 것을 세웠을 때, 내 앞에는 누가 있었던가, 격려도 없고 주목도 없이, 받들어 모셔진 영웅과 영웅호걸들의 군대조차 등 돌리게 만들었을 그 비천한 장애들에 맞서, 선한 목적을 위해 묵묵히 애쓰던 이가?"], ["One weak girl!", "연약한 처녀 하나였다!"], ["When I tried to conquer my misplaced love, and to be generous to the man who was more fortunate than I, though he should never know it or repay me with a gracious word, in whom had I watched patience, self-denial, self-subdual, charitable construction, the noblest generosity of the affections?", "내가 잘못 둔 사랑을 이겨 내고, 나보다 더 행복한 그 사내에게—그가 결코 알지도 못하고 다정한 말 한마디로 갚지도 않을 텐데도—너그럽게 대하려 애썼을 때, 인내와 자기 부정, 자기 억제, 자비로운 헤아림, 애정의 가장 고귀한 너그러움을 내가 지켜본 이는 누구였던가?"], ["In the same poor girl!", "바로 그 가엾은 처녀였다!"], ["If I, a man, with a man’s advantages and means and energies, had slighted the whisper in my heart, that if my father had erred, it was my first duty to conceal the fault and to repair it, what youthful figure with tender feet going almost bare on the damp ground, with spare hands ever working, with its slight shape but half protected from the sharp weather, would have stood before me to put me to shame?", "만일 나, 한 사내로서 사내가 지닌 이점과 수단과 기력을 갖춘 내가, 아버지가 잘못을 저질렀다면 그 허물을 감추고 바로잡는 것이 나의 첫째 의무라고 가슴속에서 속삭이는 그 소리를 무시했더라면, 축축한 땅 위를 거의 맨발로 디디며, 야윈 두 손으로 쉼 없이 일하고, 가냘픈 몸을 매서운 날씨로부터 절반밖에 가리지 못한 채, 그 어떤 젊은 모습이 내 앞에 서서 나를 부끄럽게 했을 것인가? 바로 리틀 도릿의 모습이었으리라.'"], ["Little Dorrit’s.’ So always as he sat alone in the faded chair, thinking.", "빛바랜 의자에 홀로 앉아 그는 늘 이렇게 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Always, Little Dorrit.", "언제나, 리틀 도릿이었다."], ["Until it seemed to him as if he met the reward of having wandered away from her, and suffered anything to pass between him and his remembrance of her virtues.", "마침내 그에게는, 그녀에게서 멀리 떠돌아다니며 그녀의 덕에 대한 자신의 기억과 자기 사이에 무엇이든 끼어들도록 내버려 둔 데 대한 응보를 만나는 것처럼 여겨질 지경이었다."]], [["His door was opened, and the head of the elder Chivery was put in a very little way, without being turned towards him.", "그의 방문이 열리더니, 늙은 치버리의 머리가 그를 향해 돌아보지도 않은 채 아주 조금만 안으로 들이밀어졌다."]], [["‘I am off the Lock, Mr Clennam, and going out.", "\"저는 이제 문지기 당번에서 빠져서 나가는 길입니다, 클레넘 씨."], ["Can I do anything for you?’", "뭐 해 드릴 일이라도 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Many thanks.", "\"고맙소만."], ["Nothing.’", "없소이다.\""]], [["‘You’ll excuse me opening the door,’ said Mr Chivery; ‘but I couldn’t make you hear.’", "\"문을 열어서 죄송합니다.\" 치버리 씨가 말했다. \"그런데 아무리 불러도 못 들으시더군요.\""]], [["‘Did you knock?’ ‘Half-a-dozen times.’", "\"노크를 했소?\" \"여섯 번쯤 했습니다.\""]], [["Rousing himself, Clennam observed that the prison had awakened from its noontide doze, that the inmates were loitering about the shady yard, and that it was late in the afternoon.", "정신을 차리고 보니, 클레넘은 감옥이 한낮의 졸음에서 깨어났고, 수감자들이 그늘진 마당을 어슬렁거리고 있으며, 늦은 오후가 되었음을 알아차렸다."], ["He had been thinking for hours.", "그는 몇 시간을 줄곧 생각에 잠겨 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘Your things is come,’ said Mr Chivery, ‘and my son is going to carry ‘em up.", "\"짐이 도착했습니다.\" 치버리 씨가 말했다."], ["I should have sent ‘em up but for his wishing to carry ‘em himself.", "\"제 아들놈이 들고 올라갈 겁니다. 제가 올려보냈을 텐데, 녀석이 제 손으로 가져가겠다고 해서요."], ["Indeed he would have ‘em himself, and so I couldn’t send ‘em up.", "정말이지 제가 직접 하겠다고 우기는 통에, 제가 올려보낼 수가 없었습니다."], ["Mr Clennam, could I say a word to you?’", "클레넘 씨, 한 말씀 드려도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["‘Pray come in,’ said Arthur; for Mr Chivery’s head was still put in at the door a very little way, and Mr Chivery had but one ear upon him, instead of both eyes. This was native delicacy in Mr Chivery--true politeness; though his exterior had very much of a turnkey about it, and not the least of a gentleman.", "\"부디 들어오시오.\" 아서가 말했다. 치버리 씨의 머리는 여전히 문틈으로 아주 조금만 들이밀어져 있었고, 두 눈을 다 그에게 두는 대신 한쪽 귀만 그에게 향하고 있었기 때문이다. 이는 치버리 씨 특유의 섬세함이었다—참된 예의 바름이었다. 비록 그 외양은 간수의 티가 다분하고 신사다운 구석이라곤 조금도 없었지만 말이다."]], [["‘Thank you, sir,’ said Mr Chivery, without advancing; ‘it’s no odds me coming in.", "\"감사합니다, 선생님.\" 치버리 씨가 안으로 들어오지 않은 채 말했다."], ["Mr Clennam, don’t you take no notice of my son (if you’ll be so good) in case you find him cut up anyways difficult.", "\"제가 들어가고 자시고 할 것 없습니다. 클레넘 씨, 제 아들놈이 어떻게든 까칠하게 굴더라도 (괜찮으시다면) 너무 마음에 두지 말아 주십시오."], ["My son has a ‘art, and my son’s ‘art is in the right place.", "제 아들놈한테는 마음이 있고, 그 녀석 마음은 제자리에 똑바로 박혀 있습니다."], ["Me and his mother knows where to find it, and we find it sitiwated correct.’", "저랑 그 어미는 그게 어디 있는지 알고 있고, 또 제대로 자리 잡고 있는 걸 확인했습죠.\""]], [["With this mysterious speech, Mr Chivery took his ear away and shut the door.", "이 알쏭달쏭한 말을 남기고서, 치버리 씨는 귀를 거두고 문을 닫았다."], ["He might have been gone ten minutes, when his son succeeded him.", "그가 떠난 지 십 분쯤 되었을 무렵, 그의 아들이 뒤를 이었다."]], [["‘Here’s your portmanteau,’ he said to Arthur, putting it carefully down.", "\"여기 선생님 여행 가방입니다.\" 그가 아서에게 말하며 조심스레 가방을 내려놓았다."]], [["‘It’s very kind of you.", "\"정말 친절하시군요."], ["I am ashamed that you should have the trouble.’", "이런 수고를 끼쳐 부끄럽습니다.\""]], [["He was gone before it came to that; but soon returned, saying exactly as before, ‘Here’s your black box:’ which he also put down with care.", "그 말이 끝나기도 전에 그는 나가 버렸지만, 곧 돌아와 아까와 똑같은 투로 말했다. \"여기 선생님 검은 상자입니다.\" 그러고는 이것 또한 조심스레 내려놓았다."]], [["‘I am very sensible of this attention.", "\"이렇게 신경 써 주시니 깊이 고맙소."], ["I hope we may shake hands now, Mr John.’", "이제 우리 악수라도 합시다, 존 군.\""]], [["Young John, however, drew back, turning his right wrist in a socket made of his left thumb and middle-finger and said as he had said at first, ‘I don’t know as I can.", "그러나 젊은 존은 뒤로 물러서며, 왼손 엄지와 가운뎃손가락으로 만든 고리에 오른쪽 손목을 끼워 돌리더니, 처음에 했던 것과 똑같이 말했다. \"그럴 수 있을지 모르겠습니다."], ["No; I find I can’t!’ He then stood regarding the prisoner sternly, though with a swelling humour in his eyes that looked like pity.", "아니, 못 하겠는데요!\" 그러고는 죄수를 엄하게 바라보며 서 있었는데, 그 두 눈에는 연민처럼 보이는 벅찬 감정이 차오르고 있었다."]], [["‘Why are you angry with me,’ said Clennam, ‘and yet so ready to do me these kind services?", "\"어찌하여 내게 화가 나 있으면서도,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"이토록 기꺼이 이런 친절을 베푸는 거요?"], ["There must be some mistake between us.", "우리 사이에 무슨 오해가 있는 모양이오."], ["If I have done anything to occasion it I am sorry.’", "내가 그럴 만한 일을 했다면 미안하오.\""]], [["‘No mistake, sir,’ returned John, turning the wrist backwards and forwards in the socket, for which it was rather tight.", "\"오해는 없습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 손목을 고리 속에서 앞뒤로 돌리며 대꾸했다."], ["‘No mistake, sir, in the feelings with which my eyes behold you at the present moment!", "고리가 다소 빡빡했다. \"지금 이 순간 제 두 눈이 선생님을 바라보는 그 심정에는 오해가 없습니다!"], ["If I was at all fairly equal to your weight, Mr Clennam--which I am not; and if you weren’t under a cloud--which you are; and if it wasn’t against all rules of the Marshalsea--which it is; those feelings are such, that they would stimulate me, more to having it out with you in a Round on the present spot than to anything else I could name.’", "제가 선생님 체급에 조금이라도 걸맞기만 하다면—그렇지 못합니다만—그리고 선생님이 곤경에 처해 있지만 않다면—처해 있습니다만—그리고 그게 마셜시의 온갖 규칙에 어긋나지만 않는다면—어긋납니다만—그 심정이란 게 어찌나 들끓는지, 제가 떠올릴 수 있는 그 어떤 일보다도 지금 바로 이 자리에서 선생님과 한판 붙고 싶게 만든답니다.\""]], [["Arthur looked at him for a moment in some wonder, and some little anger.", "아서는 잠시 다소 의아하고 또 조금 노여운 마음으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["‘Well, well!’ he said.", "\"원, 이런!\" 그가 말했다."], ["‘A mistake, a mistake!’ Turning away, he sat down with a heavy sigh in the faded chair again.", "\"오해요, 오해라니까!\" 그는 몸을 돌려, 무거운 한숨과 함께 다시 빛바랜 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["Young John followed him with his eyes, and, after a short pause, cried out, ‘I beg your pardon!’", "젊은 존은 눈으로 그를 좇다가, 잠깐 사이를 두고 외쳤다. \"죄송합니다!\""]], [["‘Freely granted,’ said Clennam, waving his hand without raising his sunken head.", "\"흔쾌히 받아들이리다.\" 클레넘이 수그린 고개를 들지도 않은 채 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["‘Say no more.", "\"더 말하지 마시오."], ["I am not worth it.’", "나는 그럴 값어치도 없는 사람이오.\""]], [["‘This furniture, sir,’ said Young John in a voice of mild and soft explanation, ‘belongs to me.", "\"이 가구는요, 선생님,\" 젊은 존이 온화하고 부드럽게 설명하는 어조로 말했다, \"제 것입니다."], ["I am in the habit of letting it out to parties without furniture, that have the room.", "저는 방은 있지만 가구가 없는 분들한테 이걸 빌려드리곤 합니다."], ["It an’t much, but it’s at your service.", "대단한 건 아닙니다만, 선생님 마음대로 쓰십시오."], ["Free, I mean.", "공짜로 말입니다."], ["I could not think of letting you have it on any other terms.", "다른 조건으로 빌려드릴 생각은 도저히 못 하겠습니다."], ["You’re welcome to it for nothing.’", "거저 쓰셔도 좋습니다.\""]], [["Arthur raised his head again to thank him, and to say he could not accept the favour.", "아서는 다시 고개를 들어 고마움을 표하고는, 그 호의를 받을 수 없다고 말하려 했다."], ["John was still turning his wrist, and still contending with himself in his former divided manner.", "존은 여전히 손목을 돌리고 있었고, 여전히 아까처럼 마음이 둘로 갈린 채 자신과 씨름하고 있었다."]], [["‘What is the matter between us?’ said Arthur.", "\"우리 사이에 대체 무슨 문제가 있는 거요?\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘I decline to name it, sir,’ returned Young John, suddenly turning loud and sharp.", "\"그건 입에 올리지 않겠습니다, 선생님.\" 젊은 존이 갑자기 크고 날카로운 목소리로 대꾸했다."], ["‘Nothing’s the matter.’", "\"아무 문제 없습니다.\""]], [["Arthur looked at him again, in vain, for an explanation of his behaviour.", "아서는 그의 행동을 설명해 줄 무언가를 찾으려 다시 그를 바라보았으나 허사였다."], ["After a while, Arthur turned away his head again.", "잠시 뒤 아서는 다시 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Young John said, presently afterwards, with the utmost mildness:", "곧이어 젊은 존이 더없이 부드러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘The little round table, sir, that’s nigh your elbow, was--you know whose--I needn’t mention him--he died a great gentleman.", "\"선생님 팔꿈치 가까이에 있는 저 작고 둥근 탁자는요—그분 것이었습니다—누구인지 굳이 말씀드리지 않아도—그분은 훌륭한 신사로 돌아가셨지요."], ["I bought it of an individual that he gave it to, and that lived here after him.", "저는 그분이 그걸 준 어떤 사람한테서 샀습니다. 그분 뒤에 여기 살던 사람이었지요."], ["But the individual wasn’t any ways equal to him.", "그렇지만 그 사람은 도무지 그분에 견줄 만한 사람이 아니었습니다."], ["Most individuals would find it hard to come up to his level.’", "웬만한 사람은 그분 수준에 이르기가 어려울 겁니다.\""]], [["Arthur drew the little table nearer, rested his arm upon it, and kept it there.", "아서는 그 작은 탁자를 더 가까이 끌어당겨, 그 위에 팔을 얹고는 그대로 두었다."]], [["‘Perhaps you may not be aware, sir,’ said Young John, ‘that I intruded upon him when he was over here in London.", "\"아마 모르고 계실지도 모르겠습니다만, 선생님,\" 젊은 존이 말했다, \"그분이 여기 런던에 와 계실 때 제가 찾아뵌 적이 있습니다."], ["On the whole he was of opinion that it _was_ an intrusion, though he was so good as to ask me to sit down and to inquire after father and all other old friends.", "따지고 보면 그분은 그게 주제넘은 짓이라고 여기시는 눈치였습니다만, 그래도 친절하게 앉으라 하시고 아버지며 다른 옛 친구분들 안부를 물어 주셨지요."], ["Leastways humblest acquaintances.", "하기야 가장 미천한 지인들이라고 해야겠습니다만."], ["He looked, to me, a good deal changed, and I said so when I came back.", "제가 보기엔 그분은 꽤나 달라지신 듯해서, 돌아와서 그렇게 말했습니다."], ["I asked him if Miss Amy was well--’\n‘And she was?’", "제가 에이미 양은 잘 지내시느냐고 여쭸더니—\""]], [["‘I should have thought you would have known without putting the question to such as me,’ returned Young John, after appearing to take a large invisible pill.", "\"그래서, 잘 지내고 있었소?\" \"저 같은 사람한테 묻지 않으셔도 선생님은 알고 계시리라 생각했습니다만.\""], ["‘Since you do put me the question, I am sorry I can’t answer it.", "젊은 존이 커다란 보이지 않는 알약을 삼키는 듯한 표정을 짓고 나서 대꾸했다. \"기어이 제게 물으시니, 답해 드릴 수 없어 죄송합니다."], ["But the truth is, he looked upon the inquiry as a liberty, and said, “What was that to me?”", "사실인즉슨, 그분은 그 물음을 무엄한 짓으로 여기시고는 '그게 자네랑 무슨 상관인가?' 하셨거든요."], ["It was then I became quite aware I was intruding: of which I had been fearful before.", "그제야 저는 제가 주제넘은 짓을 하고 있다는 걸 똑똑히 깨달았습니다. 그 전부터 그럴까 봐 두려워하긴 했습니다만."], ["However, he spoke very handsome afterwards; very handsome.’", "그렇지만 그 뒤로는 아주 점잖게 말씀해 주셨습니다. 아주 점잖게요.\""]], [["They were both silent for several minutes: except that Young John remarked, at about the middle of the pause, ‘He both spoke and acted very handsome.’", "두 사람 다 몇 분 동안 말이 없었다. 다만 젊은 존이 그 침묵의 한중간쯤에서 한마디 덧붙였을 뿐이다. \"말씀도 점잖으셨고, 행동도 아주 점잖으셨습니다.\""]], [["It was again Young John who broke the silence by inquiring:", "침묵을 다시 깬 것도 젊은 존이었으니, 그가 물었다."]], [["‘If it’s not a liberty, how long may it be your intentions, sir, to go without eating and drinking?’", "\"외람된 말씀이 아니라면, 선생님, 대체 언제까지 아무것도 먹지도 마시지도 않으실 작정입니까?\""]], [["‘I have not felt the want of anything yet,’ returned Clennam.", "\"아직 아무것도 아쉽지가 않소.\" 클레넘이 대꾸했다."], ["‘I have no appetite just now.’", "\"지금은 입맛이 없소이다.\""]], [["‘The more reason why you should take some support, sir,’ urged Young John.", "\"그러니까 더더욱 뭐라도 좀 드셔야 합니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘If you find yourself going on sitting here for hours and hours partaking of no refreshment because you have no appetite, why then you should and must partake of refreshment without an appetite.", "젊은 존이 권했다. \"입맛이 없다고 해서 아무것도 안 드시고 여기 몇 시간이고 앉아 계실 작정이시라면, 입맛이 없더라도 뭐라도 드셔야 하고 또 드셔야만 합니다."], ["I’m going to have tea in my own apartment.", "저는 제 방에서 차를 마시려던 참입니다."], ["If it’s not a liberty, please to come and take a cup.", "외람되지 않다면, 오셔서 한 잔 드시지요."], ["Or I can bring a tray here in two minutes.’", "아니면 제가 이 분 안에 쟁반을 여기로 가져오겠습니다.\""]], [["Feeling that Young John would impose that trouble on himself if he refused, and also feeling anxious to show that he bore in mind both the elder Mr Chivery’s entreaty, and the younger Mr Chivery’s apology, Arthur rose and expressed his willingness to take a cup of tea in Mr John’s apartment.", "거절하면 젊은 존이 그 수고를 제 손으로 떠맡으리라는 것을 느꼈고, 또 늙은 치버리 씨의 간청과 젊은 치버리 씨의 사과를 둘 다 마음에 새기고 있음을 보여 주고 싶기도 하여, 아서는 자리에서 일어나 존 군의 방에서 차 한 잔을 들겠노라고 흔쾌히 응했다."], ["Young John locked his door for him as they went out, slided the key into his pocket with great dexterity, and led the way to his own residence.", "젊은 존은 나가면서 그를 위해 문을 잠그고는, 아주 능숙한 솜씨로 열쇠를 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣고, 자기 거처로 앞장서 갔다."]], [["It was at the top of the house nearest to the gateway.", "그 방은 정문에서 가장 가까운, 건물 맨 꼭대기에 있었다."], ["It was the room to which Clennam had hurried on the day when the enriched family had left the prison for ever, and where he had lifted her insensible from the floor.", "부유해진 가족이 영영 감옥을 떠나던 날 클레넘이 황급히 달려갔던 바로 그 방, 그가 정신을 잃은 그녀를 마룻바닥에서 안아 올렸던 그 방이었다."], ["He foresaw where they were going as soon as their feet touched the staircase.", "두 사람의 발이 계단에 닿자마자 그는 어디로 가는지를 미리 알아챘다."], ["The room was so far changed that it was papered now, and had been repainted, and was far more comfortably furnished; but he could recall it just as he had seen it in that single glance, when he raised her from the ground and carried her down to the carriage.", "방은 이제 벽지가 발리고 새로 페인트칠이 되었으며 훨씬 안락하게 꾸며져 있어 많이 달라져 있었다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 바닥에서 안아 올려 마차로 데려가던 그 한 번의 눈길에서 본 그대로 그 방을 떠올릴 수 있었다."]], [["Young John looked hard at him, biting his fingers.", "젊은 존은 손가락을 깨물며 그를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["‘I see you recollect the room, Mr Clennam?’", "\"이 방을 기억하시는군요, 클레넘 씨?\""]], [["‘I recollect it well, Heaven bless her!’", "\"잘 기억하오. 그녀에게 하늘의 축복이 있기를!\""]], [["Oblivious of the tea, Young John continued to bite his fingers and to look at his visitor, as long as his visitor continued to glance about the room.", "차는 까맣게 잊은 채, 젊은 존은 손님이 방 안을 두리번거리는 내내 손가락을 깨물며 그를 바라보았다."], ["Finally, he made a start at the teapot, gustily rattled a quantity of tea into it from a canister, and set off for the common kitchen to fill it with hot water.", "마침내 그는 찻주전자를 와락 집어 들더니, 통에서 차를 한 움큼 요란하게 쏟아 넣고는, 뜨거운 물을 채우러 공동 부엌으로 향했다."]], [["The room was so eloquent to Clennam in the changed circumstances of his return to the miserable Marshalsea; it spoke to him so mournfully of her, and of his loss of her; that it would have gone hard with him to resist it, even though he had not been alone.", "비참한 마셜시로 돌아온 그 달라진 처지에서, 그 방은 클레넘에게 어찌나 많은 것을 말해 주던지. 그 방은 어찌나 애잔하게 그녀를, 또 그녀를 잃은 일을 그에게 들려주던지."], ["Alone, he did not try.", "혼자가 아니었더라도 그것을 견뎌 내기는 힘들었으리라."], ["He had his hand on the insensible wall as tenderly as if it had been herself that he touched, and pronounced her name in a low voice.", "혼자였기에, 그는 견디려 들지도 않았다. 그는 마치 그녀를 만지듯 다정하게 무정한 벽에 손을 얹고는, 낮은 목소리로 그녀의 이름을 불렀다."], ["He stood at the window, looking over the prison-parapet with its grim spiked border, and breathed a benediction through the summer haze towards the distant land where she was rich and prosperous.", "그는 창가에 서서, 음산한 대못이 박힌 가장자리가 둘린 감옥의 흉벽 너머를 바라보며, 여름 안개 사이로, 그녀가 부유하고 번창하게 지내는 저 머나먼 땅을 향해 축복의 기도를 불어 보냈다."]], [["Young John was some time absent, and, when he came back, showed that he had been outside by bringing with him fresh butter in a cabbage leaf, some thin slices of boiled ham in another cabbage leaf, and a little basket of water-cresses and salad herbs.", "젊은 존은 한참을 나가 있었는데, 돌아왔을 때 그가 밖에 다녀왔음을 보여 주었으니, 양배추 잎에 싼 신선한 버터와 또 다른 양배추 잎에 싼 얇게 저민 삶은 햄 몇 점, 그리고 물냉이와 샐러드용 풀이 담긴 작은 바구니를 들고 온 것이었다."], ["When these were arranged upon the table to his satisfaction, they sat down to tea.", "이것들을 흡족하게 탁자에 차려 놓은 뒤, 두 사람은 차를 마시려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Clennam tried to do honour to the meal, but unavailingly.", "클레넘은 그 식사를 잘 받들어 보려 애썼으나 소용이 없었다."], ["The ham sickened him, the bread seemed to turn to sand in his mouth.", "햄은 속을 메스껍게 했고, 빵은 입안에서 모래로 변하는 듯했다."], ["He could force nothing upon himself but a cup of tea.", "그가 억지로 넘길 수 있는 것이라곤 차 한 잔뿐이었다."]], [["‘Try a little something green,’ said Young John, handing him the basket.", "\"푸성귀라도 좀 드셔 보세요.\" 젊은 존이 바구니를 건네며 말했다."]], [["He took a sprig or so of water-cress, and tried again; but the bread turned to a heavier sand than before, and the ham (though it was good enough of itself) seemed to blow a faint simoom of ham through the whole Marshalsea.", "그는 물냉이를 한두 줄기 집어 다시 먹어 보려 했으나, 빵은 전보다 더 무거운 모래로 변했고, 햄은(그 자체로는 충분히 괜찮았는데도) 마셜시 전체에 햄의 희미한 모래바람을 불어 보내는 듯했다."]], [["‘Try a little more something green, sir,’ said Young John; and again handed the basket.", "\"푸성귀를 좀 더 드셔 보세요, 선생님.\" 젊은 존이 말하며 다시 바구니를 건넸다."]], [["It was so like handing green meat into the cage of a dull imprisoned bird, and John had so evidently brought the little basket as a handful of fresh relief from the stale hot paving-stones and bricks of the jail, that Clennam said, with a smile, ‘It was very kind of you to think of putting this between the wires; but I cannot even get this down to-day.’", "그것은 마치 갇혀 풀이 죽은 새의 새장 안으로 푸른 먹이를 넣어 주는 것과 어찌나 비슷하던지, 또 존이 그 작은 바구니를 감옥의 케케묵고 뜨거운 포석과 벽돌로부터 한 줌의 신선한 위안으로 가져온 것이 어찌나 분명하던지, 클레넘은 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"이걸 창살 사이로 넣어 줄 생각을 하다니 참 친절하시오. 하지만 오늘은 이것조차 넘기질 못하겠소.\""]], [["As if the difficulty were contagious, Young John soon pushed away his own plate, and fell to folding the cabbage-leaf that had contained the ham.", "마치 그 곤란함이 옮기라도 하는 듯, 젊은 존도 곧 제 접시를 밀쳐 두고, 햄을 쌌던 양배추 잎을 접기 시작했다."], ["When he had folded it into a number of layers, one over another, so that it was small in the palm of his hand, he began to flatten it between both his hands, and to eye Clennam attentively.", "그것을 여러 겹 차곡차곡 접어 손바닥에 들어올 만큼 작게 만든 뒤, 두 손 사이에 끼워 납작하게 누르며 클레넘을 유심히 바라보았다."]], [["‘I wonder,’ he at length said, compressing his green packet with some force, ‘that if it’s not worth your while to take care of yourself for your own sake, it’s not worth doing for some one else’s.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 그가 마침내 그 푸른 꾸러미를 다소 힘주어 누르며 말했다, \"선생님 자신을 위해서는 몸을 돌볼 가치가 없다 하더라도, 다른 누군가를 위해서라면 그럴 가치가 있지 않을까 싶습니다만.\""]], [["‘Truly,’ returned Arthur, with a sigh and a smile, ‘I don’t know for whose.’", "\"솔직히,\" 아서가 한숨과 미소를 섞어 대꾸했다, \"그게 누굴 위해서인지 나는 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam,’ said John, warmly, ‘I am surprised that a gentleman who is capable of the straightforwardness that you are capable of, should be capable of the mean action of making me such an answer.", "\"클레넘 씨,\" 존이 발끈하여 말했다, \"선생님 같은 솔직함을 갖추신 신사분이 제게 그런 답변을 하는 옹졸한 짓을 하실 수 있다니 놀랍습니다."], ["Mr Clennam, I am surprised that a gentleman who is capable of having a heart of his own, should be capable of the heartlessness of treating mine in that way.", "클레넘 씨, 자기 가슴을 지니신 신사분이 그렇게 남의 가슴을 짓밟는 매정한 짓을 하실 수 있다니 놀랍습니다."], ["I am astonished at it, sir.", "저는 그게 놀랍습니다, 선생님."], ["Really and truly I am astonished!’", "정말로 진심으로 놀랍습니다!\""]], [["Having got upon his feet to emphasise his concluding words, Young John sat down again, and fell to rolling his green packet on his right leg; never taking his eyes off Clennam, but surveying him with a fixed look of indignant reproach.", "끝맺는 말에 힘을 싣느라 자리에서 벌떡 일어났던 젊은 존은 다시 앉아, 그 푸른 꾸러미를 자기 오른쪽 다리에 대고 굴리기 시작했다. 클레넘에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 분개에 찬 비난의 시선으로 그를 줄곧 살펴보았다."]], [["‘I had got over it, sir,’ said John.", "\"저는 그걸 다 이겨 냈었습니다, 선생님.\""], ["‘I had conquered it, knowing that it _must_ be conquered, and had come to the resolution to think no more about it.", "존이 말했다. \"반드시 이겨 내야만 한다는 걸 알기에 그걸 정복하고, 두 번 다시 그 일을 생각하지 않기로 결심까지 했었습니다."], ["I shouldn’t have given my mind to it again, I hope, if to this prison you had not been brought, and in an hour unfortunate for me, this day!’ (In his agitation Young John adopted his mother’s powerful construction of sentences.) ‘When you first came upon me, sir, in the Lodge, this day, more as if a Upas tree had been made a capture of than a private defendant, such mingled streams of feelings broke loose again within me, that everything was for the first few minutes swept away before them, and I was going round and round in a vortex.", "만일 선생님이 이 감옥으로 끌려오시지만 않았더라면, 그것도 제게 하필이면 불운한 시각에, 오늘 같은 날에 말입니다, 저는 다시 그 일에 마음을 두지 않았을 겁니다!\" (격앙된 나머지 젊은 존은 어머니 특유의 위풍당당한 문장 구조를 빌려 쓰고 있었다.) \"오늘 선생님이 처음 수위실에서 제 앞에 나타나셨을 때, 마치 일개 사사로운 피고가 아니라 한 그루 우파스 나무가 사로잡혀 온 것 같았는데, 제 안에서 그 뒤섞인 감정의 물줄기가 다시 터져 나와, 처음 몇 분간은 그 앞에서 모든 게 휩쓸려 가 버리고, 저는 소용돌이 속을 빙글빙글 돌았습니다."], ["I got out of it.", "저는 거기서 빠져나왔습니다."], ["I struggled, and got out of it.", "안간힘을 써서 빠져나왔습니다."], ["If it was the last word I had to speak, against that vortex with my utmost powers I strove, and out of it I came.", "이게 제가 남길 마지막 말이라 해도, 저는 온 힘을 다해 그 소용돌이에 맞서 싸웠고, 거기서 빠져나왔습니다."], ["I argued that if I had been rude, apologies was due, and those apologies without a question of demeaning, I did make.", "제가 무례했다면 사과가 마땅하다고 스스로 따져, 비굴해질 것도 없이 그 사과를 했습니다."], ["And now, when I’ve been so wishful to show that one thought is next to being a holy one with me and goes before all others--now, after all, you dodge me when I ever so gently hint at it, and throw me back upon myself.", "그런데 이제, 한 가지 생각이 제게는 거의 거룩한 것이나 다름없고 다른 무엇보다 앞선다는 걸 그토록 보여 드리고 싶었건만—이제, 결국에 가서, 제가 그걸 아무리 점잖게 넌지시 비쳐도 선생님은 슬쩍 피해 버리시고, 저를 도로 제 안으로 내동댕이치시는군요. 그러니, 선생님,\""], ["For, do not, sir,’ said Young John, ‘do not be so base as to deny that dodge you do, and thrown me back upon myself you have!’", "젊은 존이 말했다, \"그렇게 피하셨다는 걸, 또 저를 도로 제 안으로 내던지셨다는 걸 부인하는 비열한 짓일랑 하지 말아 주십시오!\""]], [["All amazement, Arthur gazed at him like one lost, only saying, ‘What is it?", "온통 어리둥절해진 아서는 마치 길을 잃은 사람처럼 그를 응시하며, 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"대체 무슨 말이오?"], ["What do you mean, John?’ But, John, being in that state of mind in which nothing would seem to be more impossible to a certain class of people than the giving of an answer, went ahead blindly.", "무슨 뜻이오, 존?\" 그러나 존은, 어떤 부류의 사람들에게는 답을 내놓는 것보다 더 불가능해 보이는 일이 없을 그런 심정에 빠져 있었기에, 앞뒤 가리지 않고 내달렸다."]], [["‘I hadn’t,’ John declared, ‘no, I hadn’t, and I never had the audaciousness to think, I am sure, that all was anything but lost.", "\"저는,\" 존이 단언했다, \"아니, 저는 그러지 않았고, 모든 게 끝장났을 뿐이라는 것 말고 다른 무언가를 감히 생각해 본 적이 결코 없습니다, 정말입니다."], ["I hadn’t, no, why should I say I hadn’t if I ever had, any hope that it was possible to be so blest, not after the words that passed, not even if barriers insurmountable had not been raised!", "그런 복을 누릴 수 있으리라는 희망 따위는 저는 품지 않았습니다—아니, 품은 적이 없는데 어찌 품었다고 하겠습니까—그런 말이 오간 뒤에는, 넘을 수 없는 장벽이 세워지지 않았더라도 말입니다!"], ["But is that a reason why I am to have no memory, why I am to have no thoughts, why I am to have no sacred spots, nor anything?’", "그렇지만 그렇다고 해서 제가 추억도 가지면 안 되고, 생각도 가지면 안 되고, 거룩한 자리 하나, 그 무엇도 가지면 안 된다는 이유가 됩니까?\""]], [["‘What can you mean?’ cried Arthur.", "\"대체 무슨 뜻이오?\" 아서가 외쳤다."]], [["‘It’s all very well to trample on it, sir,’ John went on, scouring a very prairie of wild words, ‘if a person can make up his mind to be guilty of the action.", "\"그걸 짓밟는 거야 얼마든지 좋습니다, 선생님.\" 존이 미친 듯한 말의 광야를 내달리며 계속했다, \"그런 짓을 저지르기로 마음먹을 수 있는 사람이라면 말입니다."], ["It’s all very well to trample on it, but it’s there.", "그걸 짓밟는 거야 얼마든지 좋습니다만, 그건 거기 있습니다."], ["It may be that it couldn’t be trampled upon if it wasn’t there.", "거기 있지 않다면 짓밟힐 수도 없을지 모르지요."], ["But that doesn’t make it gentlemanly, that doesn’t make it honourable, that doesn’t justify throwing a person back upon himself after he has struggled and strived out of himself like a butterfly.", "그러나 그렇다고 그게 신사다운 짓이 되는 것도 아니고, 명예로운 짓이 되는 것도 아니며, 나비처럼 제 껍데기에서 안간힘으로 빠져나온 사람을 도로 제 안으로 내던지는 짓이 정당화되는 것도 아닙니다."], ["The world may sneer at a turnkey, but he’s a man--when he isn’t a woman, which among female criminals he’s expected to be.’", "세상은 간수를 비웃을지 모르나, 그래도 그는 사내입니다—여죄수들 사이에서 여자 노릇을 하길 요구받을 때 빼고는 말이지요.\""]], [["Ridiculous as the incoherence of his talk was, there was yet a truthfulness in Young John’s simple, sentimental character, and a sense of being wounded in some very tender respect, expressed in his burning face and in the agitation of his voice and manner, which Arthur must have been cruel to disregard.", "그의 횡설수설이 우스꽝스럽기는 했어도, 젊은 존의 소박하고 감상적인 성품에는 진실함이 깃들어 있었고, 어떤 몹시 여린 구석을 다친 듯한 느낌이 그의 달아오른 얼굴과 떨리는 목소리와 몸짓에 드러나 있어, 아서가 그것을 무시했더라면 무정한 사람이 되었으리라."], ["He turned his thoughts back to the starting-point of this unknown injury; and in the meantime Young John, having rolled his green packet pretty round, cut it carefully into three pieces, and laid it on a plate as if it were some particular delicacy.", "그는 이 영문 모를 상처의 출발점으로 생각을 되돌렸다. 그러는 동안 젊은 존은 그 푸른 꾸러미를 제법 동그랗게 굴려 놓고는, 그것을 조심스레 세 조각으로 잘라, 마치 무슨 특별한 진미라도 되는 양 접시에 담았다."]], [["‘It seems to me just possible,’ said Arthur, when he had retraced the conversation to the water-cresses and back again, ‘that you have made some reference to Miss Dorrit.’", "\"내 생각에 어쩌면,\" 아서가 물냉이까지 거슬러 올라갔다가 다시 더듬어 내려온 끝에 말했다, \"당신이 도릿 양에 관해 무언가 언급한 듯하오만.\""]], [["‘It is just possible, sir,’ returned John Chivery.", "\"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르지요, 선생님.\" 존 치버리가 대꾸했다."]], [["‘I don’t understand it.", "\"나는 도무지 모르겠소."], ["I hope I may not be so unlucky as to make you think I mean to offend you again, for I never have meant to offend you yet, when I say I don’t understand it.’", "또 당신의 기분을 상하게 할 생각이라고 여겨지는 불운만은 없기를 바라오—나는 아직껏 당신의 기분을 상하게 할 생각 따윈 한 번도 없었으니 말이오—이렇게 말한다 해서 말이오, 나는 정말 모르겠소.\""]], [["‘Sir,’ said Young John, ‘will you have the perfidy to deny that you know and long have known that I felt towards Miss Dorrit, call it not the presumption of love, but adoration and sacrifice?’", "\"선생님,\" 젊은 존이 말했다, \"제가 도릿 양을 향해—사랑이라는 주제넘은 말은 쓰지 않겠습니다만, 흠모와 헌신을 품어 왔다는 걸, 그리고 그걸 오래전부터 알고 계셨다는 걸 부인하는 배신을 저지르시렵니까?\""]], [["‘Indeed, John, I will not have any perfidy if I know it; why you should suspect me of it I am at a loss to think.", "\"진정이오, 존, 내가 아는 한 나는 어떤 배신도 저지르지 않겠소. 어찌하여 당신이 나를 그런 사람으로 의심하는지 나는 도무지 모르겠소."], ["Did you ever hear from Mrs Chivery, your mother, that I went to see her once?’", "당신 어머니 치버리 부인한테서, 내가 한번 그분을 찾아갔다는 얘기를 들은 적이 있소?\""]], [["‘No, sir,’ returned John, shortly.", "\"아니요, 선생님.\" 존이 퉁명스레 대꾸했다."], ["‘Never heard of such a thing.’", "\"그런 얘긴 들은 적 없습니다.\""]], [["‘But I did.", "\"하지만 나는 찾아갔소."], ["Can you imagine why?’", "왜 그랬는지 짐작이 가오?\""]], [["‘No, sir,’ returned John, shortly.", "\"아니요, 선생님.\" 존이 퉁명스레 대꾸했다."], ["‘I can’t imagine why.’", "\"왜 그러셨는지 짐작이 안 갑니다.\""]], [["‘I will tell you.", "\"내가 말해 주리다."], ["I was solicitous to promote Miss Dorrit’s happiness; and if I could have supposed that Miss Dorrit returned your affection--’", "나는 도릿 양의 행복을 도모하고 싶었소. 그래서 만일 도릿 양이 당신의 마음에 화답하고 있다고 짐작할 수만 있었더라면—\""]], [["Poor John Chivery turned crimson to the tips of his ears.", "가엾은 존 치버리는 귀 끝까지 새빨개졌다. \"도릿 양은 한 번도 그러지 않았습니다, 선생님."], ["‘Miss Dorrit never did, sir.", "저는 제 미천한 처지에서나마 할 수 있는 한 명예롭고 진실하고자 합니다."], ["I wish to be honourable and true, so far as in my humble way I can, and I would scorn to pretend for a moment that she ever did, or that she ever led me to believe she did; no, nor even that it was ever to be expected in any cool reason that she would or could.", "그분이 한 번이라도 그랬다거나, 한 번이라도 제게 그렇게 믿게 만드셨다고 꾸며 대는 짓일랑 한순간도 경멸합니다. 아니, 차분히 이성으로 따지자면 그분이 그러시리라거나 그러실 수 있으리라 기대할 만한 구석조차 결코 없었습니다."], ["She was far above me in all respects at all times.", "그분은 언제나 모든 면에서 저보다 한참 높은 분이셨습니다."], ["As likewise,’ added John, ‘similarly was her gen-teel family.’", "마찬가지로,\" 존이 덧붙였다, \"그분의 점잖은 집안도 그러했고요.\""]], [["His chivalrous feeling towards all that belonged to her made him so very respectable, in spite of his small stature and his rather weak legs, and his very weak hair, and his poetical temperament, that a Goliath might have sat in his place demanding less consideration at Arthur’s hands.", "그녀에게 속한 모든 것을 향한 그 기사도적인 마음씨가 그를 어찌나 떳떳해 보이게 했던지, 그 자그마한 키와 다소 부실한 다리, 몹시 가는 머리칼, 시인 기질에도 불구하고, 골리앗이 그 자리에 앉아 있었더라도 아서에게서 그만한 존중을 끌어내지는 못했으리라."]], [["‘You speak, John,’ he said, with cordial admiration, ‘like a Man.’", "\"당신은, 존,\" 그가 진심 어린 감탄을 담아 말했다, \"사내답게 말하는구려.\""]], [["‘Well, sir,’ returned John, brushing his hand across his eyes, ‘then I wish you’d do the same.’", "\"그렇다면요, 선생님,\" 존이 손등으로 두 눈을 훔치며 대꾸했다, \"선생님께서도 그렇게 해 주셨으면 합니다.\""]], [["He was quick with this unexpected retort, and it again made Arthur regard him with a wondering expression of face.", "이 뜻밖의 응수가 어찌나 재빨랐던지, 그것은 또 한 번 아서로 하여금 의아한 표정으로 그를 바라보게 만들었다."]], [["‘Leastways,’ said John, stretching his hand across the tea-tray, ‘if too strong a remark, withdrawn!", "\"하기야,\" 존이 찻쟁반 너머로 손을 뻗으며 말했다, \"너무 지나친 말이라면 거두겠습니다!"], ["But, why not, why not?", "하지만 왜 안 됩니까, 왜 안 되느냐는 말입니다?"], ["When I say to you, Mr Clennam, take care of yourself for some one else’s sake, why not be open, though a turnkey?", "클레넘 씨, 제가 선생님께 다른 누군가를 위해서라도 몸을 돌보시라고 말씀드릴 때, 비록 간수일지언정, 어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하십니까?"], ["Why did I get you the room which I knew you’d like best?", "어찌하여 제가 선생님이 가장 마음에 들어 하실 줄 아는 방을 마련해 드렸겠습니까?"], ["Why did I carry up your things?", "어찌하여 제가 선생님 짐을 들어 올렸겠습니까?"], ["Not that I found ‘em heavy; I don’t mention ‘em on that accounts; far from it.", "짐이 무거워서 그러는 게 아닙니다. 그런 이유로 들먹이는 게 아니에요."], ["Why have I cultivated you in the manner I have done since the morning?", "천만의 말씀입니다. 어찌하여 제가 오늘 아침부터 그토록 선생님을 정성껏 모셨겠습니까?"], ["On the ground of your own merits?", "선생님 자신의 됨됨이 때문에요? 아닙니다."], ["No. They’re very great, I’ve no doubt at all; but not on the ground of them.", "그 됨됨이가 매우 훌륭하다는 건 저도 조금도 의심치 않습니다."], ["Another’s merits have had their weight, and have had far more weight with Me.", "그렇지만 그 때문은 아닙니다. 다른 분의 됨됨이가 무게를 지녔고, 제게는 그게 훨씬 더 무겁게 다가왔던 겁니다."], ["Then why not speak free?’", "그러니 어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하느냐는 겁니다?\""]], [["‘Unaffectedly, John,’ said Clennam, ‘you are so good a fellow and I have so true a respect for your character, that if I have appeared to be less sensible than I really am of the fact that the kind services you have rendered me to-day are attributable to my having been trusted by Miss Dorrit as her friend--I confess it to be a fault, and I ask your forgiveness.’", "\"꾸밈없이 말하리다, 존,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당신은 참으로 좋은 사람이고 나는 당신의 인품을 진실로 존경하고 있소. 그러니 오늘 당신이 내게 베푼 친절한 봉사가 도릿 양에게 그녀의 벗으로 신뢰받은 데서 비롯되었다는 사실을, 내가 실제로 느끼는 것보다 덜 절감하는 것처럼 비쳤다면—나는 그것을 잘못으로 인정하며, 당신의 용서를 구하오.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["why not,’ John repeated with returning scorn, ‘why not speak free!’", "어찌하여,\" 존이 다시 경멸을 되살려 되풀이했다, \"어찌하여 터놓고 말하지 못하느냐는 말입니다!\""]], [["‘I declare to you,’ returned Arthur, ‘that I do not understand you.", "\"단언컨대,\" 아서가 대꾸했다, \"나는 당신 말을 못 알아듣겠소."], ["Look at me.", "나를 보시오."], ["Consider the trouble I have been in.", "내가 처해 온 곤경을 헤아려 보시오."], ["Is it likely that I would wilfully add to my other self-reproaches, that of being ungrateful or treacherous to you.", "내가 다른 자책들에다 일부러, 당신에게 은혜를 저버렸다거나 배신했다는 자책까지 더 보태려 들 것 같소?"], ["I do not understand you.’", "나는 당신 말을 못 알아듣겠소.\""]], [["John’s incredulous face slowly softened into a face of doubt.", "존의 못 믿겠다는 얼굴이 서서히 누그러져 미심쩍어하는 얼굴로 바뀌었다."], ["He rose, backed into the garret-window of the room, beckoned Arthur to come there, and stood looking at him thoughtfully.", "그는 일어나, 방의 다락 창문 쪽으로 물러서더니, 아서에게 그리로 오라 손짓하고는, 생각에 잠긴 채 그를 바라보며 섰다."]], [["‘Mr Clennam, do you mean to say that you don’t know?’", "\"클레넘 씨, 선생님은 정녕 모르신다는 말씀이십니까?\""]], [["‘What, John?’", "\"무얼 말이오, 존?\""]], [["‘Lord,’ said Young John, appealing with a gasp to the spikes on the wall.", "\"맙소사,\" 젊은 존이 숨을 헐떡이며 담장의 대못을 향해 호소하듯 말했다."], ["‘He says, What!’", "\"이분이 '무얼'이라시네.\""]], [["Clennam looked at the spikes, and looked at John; and looked at the spikes, and looked at John.", "클레넘은 대못을 보고, 또 존을 보고, 또 대못을 보고, 또 존을 보았다."]], [["‘He says What!", "\"'무얼'이라시는군요!"], ["And what is more,’ exclaimed Young John, surveying him in a doleful maze, ‘he appears to mean it!", "게다가 더 기막힌 건,\" 젊은 존이 비통한 어리둥절함 속에 그를 훑어보며 외쳤다, \"정말로 진심으로 하시는 말씀 같다는 겁니다!"], ["Do you see this window, sir?’", "이 창문이 보이십니까, 선생님?\""]], [["‘Of course I see this window.’", "\"물론 이 창문이 보이오.\""]], [["‘See this room?’", "\"이 방이 보이십니까?\""]], [["‘Why, of course I see this room.’", "\"아니, 물론 이 방이 보이오.\""]], [["‘That wall opposite, and that yard down below?", "\"맞은편 저 벽, 그리고 저 아래 마당이 보이십니까?"], ["They have all been witnesses of it, from day to day, from night to night, from week to week, from month to month.", "저것들이 죄다 그 증인이었습니다, 날이면 날마다, 밤이면 밤마다, 주가 가고 주가 가도록, 달이 가고 달이 가도록 말입니다."], ["For how often have I seen Miss Dorrit here when she has not seen me!’", "도릿 양이 저를 보지 못할 때 제가 여기서 그분을 본 게 몇 번이던지요!\""]], [["‘Witnesses of what?’ said Clennam.", "\"무엇의 증인이란 말이오?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Of Miss Dorrit’s love.’", "\"도릿 양의 사랑의 증인입니다.\""]], [["‘For whom?’", "\"누구를 향한?\""]], [["‘You,’ said John.", "\"선생님을요.\""], ["And touched him with the back of his hand upon the breast, and backed to his chair, and sat down on it with a pale face, holding the arms, and shaking his head at him.", "존이 말했다. 그러고는 손등으로 그의 가슴을 툭 건드리고는, 의자로 물러나 거기 앉았는데, 얼굴이 창백한 채 두 팔걸이를 붙들고 그를 향해 고개를 절레절레 흔들었다."]], [["If he had dealt Clennam a heavy blow, instead of laying that light touch upon him, its effect could not have been to shake him more.", "그 가벼운 손길을 얹는 대신 클레넘에게 묵직한 일격을 가했더라도, 그를 이보다 더 뒤흔들어 놓지는 못했으리라."], ["He stood amazed; his eyes looking at John; his lips parted, and seeming now and then to form the word ‘Me!’ without uttering it; his hands dropped at his sides; his whole appearance that of a man who has been awakened from sleep, and stupefied by intelligence beyond his full comprehension.", "그는 얼이 빠져 서 있었다. 두 눈은 존을 바라보고, 입술은 벌어진 채 이따금 '나를!'이라는 말을 소리 내지 않으며 빚어내는 듯했고, 두 손은 옆구리로 축 늘어졌으며, 그 온 모습은 잠에서 깨어났으되 자신이 온전히 헤아릴 수 없는 소식에 멍해진 사람의 그것이었다."]], [["‘Me!’ he at length said aloud.", "\"나를!\" 그가 마침내 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["‘Ah!’ groaned Young John.", "\"아!\" 젊은 존이 신음했다."], ["‘You!’", "\"선생님을요!\""]], [["He did what he could to muster a smile, and returned, ‘Your fancy.", "그는 애써 미소를 끌어모으며 대꾸했다. \"당신의 착각이오."], ["You are completely mistaken.’", "완전히 잘못 짚었소.\""]], [["‘I mistaken, sir!’ said Young John.", "\"제가 착각이라고요, 선생님!\" 젊은 존이 말했다."], ["‘_I_ completely mistaken on that subject!", "\"제가 그 일에 대해 완전히 착각을 했다고요!"], ["No, Mr Clennam, don’t tell me so.", "아니요, 클레넘 씨, 제게 그런 말씀 마십시오."], ["On any other, if you like, for I don’t set up to be a penetrating character, and am well aware of my own deficiencies.", "다른 일이라면 얼마든지 좋습니다. 저는 제가 통찰력 있는 인간이라고 내세우지도 않고, 제 부족함을 잘 알고 있으니까요."], ["But, _I_ mistaken on a point that has caused me more smart in my breast than a flight of savages’ arrows could have done!", "하지만, 제가, 야만인들이 쏘아 댄 화살 떼보다도 더 제 가슴을 쓰라리게 한 그 일에 대해 착각이라니요!"], ["_I_ mistaken on a point that almost sent me into my grave, as I sometimes wished it would, if the grave could only have been made compatible with the tobacco-business and father and mother’s feelings!", "무덤이 담배 장사며 아버지 어머니 마음과만 맞아떨어질 수 있었다면 이따금 차라리 그러기를 바랐을 만큼, 저를 거의 무덤으로 몰아넣은 그 일에 대해 제가 착각이라니요!"], ["I mistaken on a point that, even at the present moment, makes me take out my pocket-handkerchief like a great girl, as people say: though I am sure I don’t know why a great girl should be a term of reproach, for every rightly constituted male mind loves ‘em great and small.", "사람들 말마따나 다 큰 계집애처럼 지금 이 순간조차 손수건을 꺼내 들게 만드는 그 일에 대해 제가 착각이라니요. 하기야 어찌하여 다 큰 계집애가 흉이 되는 말이어야 하는지 저는 도무지 모르겠습니다만, 제대로 된 사내 마음이라면 누구나 크든 작든 그들을 사랑하는 법이니까요."], ["Don’t tell me so, don’t tell me so!’", "그런 말씀 마십시오, 그런 말씀 마시라고요!\""]], [["Still highly respectable at bottom, though absurd enough upon the surface, Young John took out his pocket-handkerchief with a genuine absence both of display and concealment, which is only to be seen in a man with a great deal of good in him, when he takes out his pocket-handkerchief for the purpose of wiping his eyes.", "겉으로는 충분히 우스꽝스러웠으나 속으로는 여전히 떳떳했던 젊은 존은, 과시도 감춤도 없이 진정으로 손수건을 꺼내 들었는데, 이는 두 눈을 닦으려고 손수건을 꺼내 드는, 속에 선량함을 가득 지닌 사람한테서만 볼 수 있는 모습이었다."], ["Having dried them, and indulged in the harmless luxury of a sob and a sniff, he put it up again.", "두 눈을 닦고는, 흐느낌 한 번과 코를 한 번 훌쩍이는 무해한 호사를 누린 뒤, 그는 그것을 도로 집어넣었다."]], [["The touch was still in its influence so like a blow that Arthur could not get many words together to close the subject with.", "그 손길은 여전히 일격이나 다름없는 위력으로 남아 있어, 아서는 그 화제를 매듭지을 만한 말을 몇 마디 모으지도 못했다."], ["He assured John Chivery when he had returned his handkerchief to his pocket, that he did all honour to his disinterestedness and to the fidelity of his remembrance of Miss Dorrit.", "존 치버리가 손수건을 도로 주머니에 넣자, 아서는 그에게, 그의 사심 없음과 도릿 양에 대한 한결같은 추억을 더없이 높이 산다고 다짐해 주었다."], ["As to the impression on his mind, of which he had just relieved it--here John interposed, and said, ‘No impression!", "방금 떨쳐 낸 그 마음속 인상에 관해서는—여기서 존이 끼어들어 \"인상이 아닙니다!"], ["Certainty!’--as to that, they might perhaps speak of it at another time, but would say no more now.", "확신입니다!\"라고 말했다—그 일에 관해서는 다음에 이야기할 수 있을지언정, 지금은 더 말하지 않겠다고 했다."], ["Feeling low-spirited and weary, he would go back to his room, with John’s leave, and come out no more that night.", "마음이 가라앉고 지친 그는 존이 허락한다면 제 방으로 돌아가, 그날 밤은 다시 나오지 않겠노라고 했다."], ["John assented, and he crept back in the shadow of the wall to his own lodging.", "존이 응낙하자, 그는 담장의 그늘을 따라 슬그머니 제 거처로 돌아갔다."]], [["The feeling of the blow was still so strong upon him that, when the dirty old woman was gone whom he found sitting on the stairs outside his door, waiting to make his bed, and who gave him to understand while doing it, that she had received her instructions from Mr Chivery, ‘not the old ‘un but the young ‘un,’ he sat down in the faded arm-chair, pressing his head between his hands, as if he had been stunned.", "그 일격의 느낌이 여전히 어찌나 강하게 그를 짓누르던지, 그가 방문 밖 계단에 앉아 잠자리를 봐 주려고 기다리던—그러면서 자기는 치버리 씨, '늙은 양반이 아니라 젊은 양반'한테 지시를 받았다고 알려 주던—그 지저분한 노파가 떠나고 나서도, 그는 마치 얻어맞은 사람처럼 두 손으로 머리를 감싸 쥔 채 빛바랜 안락의자에 주저앉았다. 리틀 도릿이 그를 사랑한다니!"], ["Little Dorrit love him! More bewildering to him than his misery, far.", "그에게는 자신의 비참함보다도 한층 더, 훨씬 더 얼떨떨한 일이었다."]], [["Consider the improbability.", "그 있을 법하지 않음을 헤아려 보라."], ["He had been accustomed to call her his child, and his dear child, and to invite her confidence by dwelling upon the difference in their respective ages, and to speak of himself as one who was turning old.", "그는 그녀를 자기 아이라고, 사랑하는 아이라고 부르는 데 익숙했고, 둘 사이의 나이 차를 늘 들먹이며 그녀가 마음을 털어놓도록 청했으며, 자신을 늙어 가는 사람이라 일컫곤 했다."], ["Yet she might not have thought him old.", "그렇지만 그녀는 그를 늙었다고 여기지 않았을지도 모른다."], ["Something reminded him that he had not thought himself so, until the roses had floated away upon the river.", "그 장미꽃들이 강물 위로 떠내려가 버리기 전까지는 그 자신도 스스로를 늙었다고 여기지 않았음을, 무언가가 그에게 일깨워 주었다."]], [["He had her two letters among other papers in his box, and he took them out and read them.", "그는 상자 속 다른 서류들 틈에 그녀의 편지 두 통을 간직하고 있었는데, 그것을 꺼내어 읽어 보았다."], ["There seemed to be a sound in them like the sound of her sweet voice.", "그 안에는 그녀의 감미로운 목소리 같은 울림이 깃들어 있는 듯했다."], ["It fell upon his ear with many tones of tenderness, that were not insusceptible of the new meaning.", "그것은 다정함의 온갖 가락으로 그의 귓가에 내려앉았고, 그 가락은 그 새로운 의미를 받아들이지 못할 것도 아니었다."], ["Now it was that the quiet desolation of her answer, ‘No, No, No,’ made to him that night in that very room--that night when he had been shown the dawn of her altered fortune, and when other words had passed between them which he had been destined to remember in humiliation and a prisoner, rushed into his mind.", "바로 그제야, 그날 밤 바로 그 방에서—그녀의 달라진 운명의 여명을 그가 처음 알게 된 그날 밤, 그리고 그가 굴욕 속에 갇힌 몸이 되어 떠올리도록 운명 지어진 다른 말들이 두 사람 사이에 오갔던 그날 밤—그에게 한 그녀의 대답, \"아니요, 아니요, 아니요\"라는 그 조용한 적막감이 그의 마음속으로 와락 밀려들었다."]], [["Consider the improbability.", "그 있을 법하지 않음을 헤아려 보라."]], [["But it had a preponderating tendency, when considered, to become fainter.", "그러나 그것은, 헤아려 볼수록 점점 더 희미해지는 쪽으로 기울어 갔다."], ["There was another and a curious inquiry of his own heart’s that concurrently became stronger.", "그와 더불어 점점 더 강해지는, 제 가슴속의 또 다른 묘한 물음이 있었다."], ["In the reluctance he had felt to believe that she loved any one; in his desire to set that question at rest; in a half-formed consciousness he had had that there would be a kind of nobleness in his helping her love for any one, was there no suppressed something on his own side that he had hushed as it arose?", "그녀가 누군가를 사랑한다고 믿기를 그가 꺼렸던 그 마음속에, 그 물음을 잠재우고 싶어 했던 그 마음속에, 그녀가 누군가를 사랑하도록 자신이 돕는 데에 일종의 고결함이 있으리라고 어렴풋이 의식했던 그 마음속에, 떠오르자마자 그가 눌러 잠재운 무언가가 제 쪽에는 없었던가?"], ["Had he ever whispered to himself that he must not think of such a thing as her loving him, that he must not take advantage of her gratitude, that he must keep his experience in remembrance as a warning and reproof; that he must regard such youthful hopes as having passed away, as his friend’s dead daughter had passed away; that he must be steady in saying to himself that the time had gone by him, and he was too saddened and old?", "그녀가 자기를 사랑한다는 따위는 생각해서는 안 된다고, 그녀의 감사를 이용해서는 안 된다고, 자신의 경험을 경고이자 책망으로 기억에 새겨 두어야 한다고, 그런 젊은 날의 희망들은 자기 벗의 죽은 딸이 가 버렸듯 가 버린 것으로 여겨야 한다고, 자기에게는 그 시절이 이미 지나가 버렸으며 자기는 너무 슬픔에 절고 너무 늙었다고 스스로에게 흔들림 없이 되뇌어야 한다고—그가 자신에게 속삭인 적이 있지 않았던가?"]], [["He had kissed her when he raised her from the ground on the day when she had been so consistently and expressively forgotten.", "그토록 한결같이, 또 그토록 또렷이 잊힌 그날, 그녀를 바닥에서 안아 올리며 그는 그녀에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["Quite as he might have kissed her, if she had been conscious?", "그녀가 의식이 있었더라도 그가 했음 직한 바로 그런 입맞춤을?"], ["No difference?", "아무 차이가 없었던가?"]], [["The darkness found him occupied with these thoughts.", "어둠이 내릴 무렵에도 그는 이런 생각들에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["The darkness also found Mr and Mrs Plornish knocking at his door.", "어둠은 또한 플로니시 부부가 그의 방문을 두드리는 것을 맞이했다."], ["They brought with them a basket, filled with choice selections from that stock in trade which met with such a quick sale and produced such a slow return.", "그들은 그토록 빨리 팔려 나가면서도 그토록 더디게 본전을 돌려주던 그 재고품 가운데서 골라 담은 것들로 가득 찬 바구니를 들고 왔다."], ["Mrs Plornish was affected to tears.", "플로니시 부인은 눈물을 글썽였다."], ["Mr Plornish amiably growled, in his philosophical but not lucid manner, that there was ups you see, and there was downs.", "플로니시 씨는 자기 특유의 철학적이되 명료하지는 않은 투로, 세상에는 오르막이 있고 또 내리막이 있는 법이라고 다정하게 투덜거렸다."], ["It was in vain to ask why ups, why downs; there they was, you know.", "어찌하여 오르막이고 어찌하여 내리막이냐고 물어 봐야 소용없으니, 그저 거기 그렇게 있는 거지요."], ["He had heerd it given for a truth that accordin’ as the world went round, which round it did rewolve undoubted, even the best of gentlemen must take his turn of standing with his ed upside down and all his air a flying the wrong way into what you might call Space.", "세상이 빙글빙글 돈다는데, 의심할 바 없이 돌고 도는 거니까요, 그러다 보면 아무리 훌륭한 신사라도 머리를 거꾸로 처박고 머리털이 죄다 엉뚱한 쪽으로, 말하자면 허공 속으로 휘날리는 차례를 한 번은 맞아야 한다는 걸, 그는 진실로 들어 알고 있다는 거였다. 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면."], ["Wery well then. What Mr Plornish said was, wery well then.", "플로니시 씨가 한 말은, 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면, 이었다."], ["That gentleman’s ed would come up-ards when his turn come, that gentleman’s air would be a pleasure to look upon being all smooth again, and wery well then!", "그 신사의 머리는 차례가 오면 다시 위로 솟을 것이고, 그 신사의 머리털은 다시 죄다 매끈하게 가지런해져 바라보기에 흐뭇할 것이니, 그러니까 좋습니다 그렇다면!"]], [["It has been already stated that Mrs Plornish, not being philosophical, wept.", "이미 말한 바와 같이, 플로니시 부인은 철학적이지 못한지라 울었다."], ["It further happened that Mrs Plornish, not being philosophical, was intelligible.", "게다가 플로니시 부인은 철학적이지 못한지라 알아듣게 말했다."], ["It may have arisen out of her softened state of mind, out of her sex’s wit, out of a woman’s quick association of ideas, or out of a woman’s no association of ideas, but it further happened somehow that Mrs Plornish’s intelligibility displayed itself upon the very subject of Arthur’s meditations.", "그것은 누그러진 그녀의 심정에서 비롯되었는지도, 여자 특유의 재치에서 비롯되었는지도, 여자다운 빠른 연상에서 비롯되었는지도, 아니면 여자다운 무연상에서 비롯되었는지도 모른다. 어쨌든 어찌어찌하여 플로니시 부인의 알아들을 만한 그 말이 하필 아서가 골똘히 생각하던 바로 그 화제 위에서 펼쳐졌다."]], [["‘The way father has been talking about you, Mr Clennam,’ said Mrs Plornish, ‘you hardly would believe.", "\"아버지가 클레넘 씨 얘기를 어찌나 하시는지요,\" 플로니시 부인이 말했다, \"도무지 못 믿으실 거예요."], ["It’s made him quite poorly.", "그 일로 영 풀이 죽으셨답니다."], ["As to his voice, this misfortune has took it away.", "목소리로 말하자면, 이 불운이 그걸 앗아가 버렸어요."], ["You know what a sweet singer father is; but he couldn’t get a note out for the children at tea, if you’ll credit what I tell you.’", "아버지가 얼마나 노래를 곱게 부르는 분인지 아시잖아요. 그런데 차 마실 때 아이들한테 한 가락도 못 뽑아내셨다니까요, 제 말 믿으세요.\""]], [["While speaking, Mrs Plornish shook her head, and wiped her eyes, and looked retrospectively about the room.", "말하는 동안 플로니시 부인은 고개를 절레절레 흔들고, 두 눈을 훔치며, 방 안을 회상에 잠겨 둘러보았다."]], [["‘As to Mr Baptist,’ pursued Mrs Plornish, ‘whatever he’ll do when he comes to know of it, I can’t conceive nor yet imagine.", "\"밥티스트 씨로 말하자면요,\" 플로니시 부인이 이어 말했다, \"이 일을 알게 되면 어쩔는지, 저는 짐작도 못 하겠고 상상도 못 하겠어요."], ["He’d have been here before now, you may be sure, but that he’s away on confidential business of your own.", "진작 여기 와 있었을 게 분명한데, 선생님 일로 은밀한 볼일을 보느라 나가 있거든요."], ["The persevering manner in which he follows up that business, and gives himself no rest from it--it really do,’ said Mrs Plornish, winding up in the Italian manner, ‘as I say to him, Mooshattonisha padrona.’", "그 일을 어찌나 끈질기게 좇으며 도무지 쉬지를 않는지—정말이지,\" 플로니시 부인이 이탈리아어 투로 마무리하며 말했다, \"제가 그한테 늘 말하지요, 무샤토니샤 파드로나라고요.\""]], [["Though not conceited, Mrs Plornish felt that she had turned this Tuscan sentence with peculiar elegance.", "거만한 사람은 아니었지만, 플로니시 부인은 이 토스카나 문장을 유난히 우아하게 굴려 냈다고 느꼈다."], ["Mr Plornish could not conceal his exultation in her accomplishments as a linguist.", "플로니시 씨는 언어학자로서의 아내의 재주에 대한 자랑스러움을 감추지 못했다."]], [["‘But what I say is, Mr Clennam,’ the good woman went on, ‘there’s always something to be thankful for, as I am sure you will yourself admit.", "\"그렇지만 제가 드리고 싶은 말씀은요, 클레넘 씨,\" 그 선량한 여인이 계속했다, \"언제나 감사할 일이 한 가지는 있는 법이고, 그건 선생님도 분명 인정하시리라는 거예요."], ["Speaking in this room, it’s not hard to think what the present something is.", "이 방에서 말하자면, 지금 그 한 가지가 무엇인지 헤아리기란 어렵지 않지요."], ["It’s a thing to be thankful for, indeed, that Miss Dorrit is not here to know it.’", "도릿 양이 여기 없어서 이 일을 모른다는 것, 그게 정말이지 감사할 일이랍니다.\""]], [["Arthur thought she looked at him with particular expression.", "아서는 그녀가 유난스러운 표정으로 자기를 바라보는 것 같았다."]], [["‘It’s a thing,’ reiterated Mrs Plornish, ‘to be thankful for, indeed, that Miss Dorrit is far away.", "\"정말이지,\" 플로니시 부인이 되풀이했다, \"도릿 양이 멀리 떨어져 있다는 게 감사할 일이에요."], ["It’s to be hoped she is not likely to hear of it.", "부디 그분이 이 일을 들을 일이 없기를 바라요."], ["If she had been here to see it, sir, it’s not to be doubted that the sight of you,’ Mrs Plornish repeated those words--‘not to be doubted, that the sight of you--in misfortune and trouble, would have been almost too much for her affectionate heart.", "만일 그분이 여기서 이걸 보았더라면요, 선생님, 선생님이,\" 플로니시 부인이 그 말을 되풀이했다—\"의심할 바 없이, 선생님이—불운과 곤경에 빠지신 모습을 보는 게 그분의 정 많은 가슴엔 거의 견디기 힘든 일이었으리라는 건 의심할 여지가 없지요."], ["There’s nothing I can think of, that would have touched Miss Dorrit so bad as that.’", "그것만큼 도릿 양을 모질게 아프게 했을 일은 달리 생각나는 게 없어요.\""]], [["Of a certainty Mrs Plornish did look at him now, with a sort of quivering defiance in her friendly emotion.", "확실히 플로니시 부인은 이제 정겨운 감정 속에 일종의 떨리는 도전기를 담아 그를 바라보았다."]], [["‘Yes!’ said she.", "\"맞아요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["‘And it shows what notice father takes, though at his time of life, that he says to me this afternoon, which Happy Cottage knows I neither make it up nor any ways enlarge, “Mary, it’s much to be rejoiced in that Miss Dorrit is not on the spot to behold it.”", "\"게다가 아버지가, 그 연세에도, 얼마나 살피시는지 이걸 보면 알 수 있어요, 오늘 오후에 제게 이러시지 뭐예요—이 '행복한 오두막'이 증인인데 제가 지어내는 것도 아니고 어떤 식으로 부풀리는 것도 아니에요—'메리야, 도릿 양이 이 자리에서 이걸 안 보는 게 크게 기뻐할 일이로구나.'"], ["Those were father’s words.", "그게 아버지 말씀이었어요."], ["Father’s own words was, “Much to be rejoiced in, Mary, that Miss Dorrit is not on the spot to behold it.”", "아버지가 직접 하신 말씀이 '크게 기뻐할 일이야, 메리야, 도릿 양이 이 자리에서 이걸 안 보는 게' 였다니까요."], ["I says to father then, I says to him, “Father, you are right!”", "그래서 제가 아버지께 그랬죠, 이랬어요, '아버지, 옳으신 말씀이에요!'"], ["That,’ Mrs Plornish concluded, with the air of a very precise legal witness, ‘is what passed betwixt father and me.", "그게요,\" 플로니시 부인이 아주 꼼꼼한 법정 증인 같은 태도로 끝맺었다, \"아버지와 저 사이에 오간 얘기랍니다."], ["And I tell you nothing but what did pass betwixt me and father.’", "그리고 저는 저와 아버지 사이에 실제로 오간 것 말고는 아무것도 말씀드리지 않은 거예요.\""]], [["Mr Plornish, as being of a more laconic temperament, embraced this opportunity of interposing with the suggestion that she should now leave Mr Clennam to himself.", "플로니시 씨는 좀 더 과묵한 기질이었던지라, 이 틈을 타 이제 클레넘 씨를 혼자 있게 해 드리는 게 어떻겠느냐고 끼어들었다. \"이봐, 자네도 알다시피,\""], ["‘For, you see,’ said Mr Plornish, gravely, ‘I know what it is, old gal;’ repeating that valuable remark several times, as if it appeared to him to include some great moral secret.", "플로니시 씨가 근엄하게 말했다, \"내가 어떤 건지 다 알거든, 마누라.\" 그러면서 그는 마치 그 말이 무슨 위대한 도덕적 비밀이라도 담고 있다는 듯, 그 값진 한마디를 여러 번 되풀이했다."], ["Finally, the worthy couple went away arm in arm.", "마침내 그 갸륵한 부부는 팔짱을 끼고 떠나갔다."]], [["Little Dorrit, Little Dorrit.", "리틀 도릿, 리틀 도릿."], ["Again, for hours.", "또다시, 몇 시간 동안."], ["Always Little Dorrit!", "언제나 리틀 도릿!"]], [["Happily, if it ever had been so, it was over, and better over.", "다행히도, 설령 한때 그러했다 한들, 이제는 다 지나간 일이었고, 지나간 게 차라리 나았다."], ["Granted that she had loved him, and he had known it and had suffered himself to love her, what a road to have led her away upon--the road that would have brought her back to this miserable place!", "그녀가 그를 사랑했고 그도 그것을 알아 스스로 그녀를 사랑하도록 내버려 두었다 치자, 그 길은 그녀를 어디로 이끄는 길이었겠는가—그녀를 이 비참한 곳으로 되돌려 데려올 길이 아니었겠는가!"], ["He ought to be much comforted by the reflection that she was quit of it forever; that she was, or would soon be, married (vague rumours of her father’s projects in that direction had reached Bleeding Heart Yard, with the news of her sister’s marriage); and that the Marshalsea gate had shut for ever on all those perplexed possibilities of a time that was gone.", "그녀가 이곳을 영영 벗어났다는 것, 그녀가 이미 결혼했거나 곧 결혼하리라는 것(여동생의 결혼 소식과 함께, 그 방면을 향한 그녀 아버지의 계획에 관한 어렴풋한 소문이 '피 흘리는 심장 마당'까지 닿아 있었다), 그리고 지나가 버린 시절의 그 모든 어지러운 가능성 위로 마셜시의 문이 영영 닫혀 버렸다는 것을 떠올리며, 그는 크게 위안을 얻어야 마땅했다."]], [["Dear Little Dorrit.", "사랑스러운 리틀 도릿."]], [["Looking back upon his own poor story, she was its vanishing-point.", "그 자신의 가련한 이야기를 돌이켜 보면, 그녀는 그 이야기의 소실점이었다."], ["Every thing in its perspective led to her innocent figure.", "그 원근법 속의 모든 것이 그녀의 순결한 모습으로 이어졌다."], ["He had travelled thousands of miles towards it; previous unquiet hopes and doubts had worked themselves out before it; it was the centre of the interest of his life; it was the termination of everything that was good and pleasant in it; beyond, there was nothing but mere waste and darkened sky.", "그는 그것을 향해 수천 마일을 여행해 왔고, 이전의 불안한 희망과 의혹들도 그 앞에서 스스로 풀려 나갔으며, 그것은 그의 인생에서 관심의 중심이었고, 그의 인생 안에 있던 선하고 즐거운 모든 것의 종착점이었다. 그 너머에는 그저 황량함과 어두워진 하늘 말고는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["As ill at ease as on the first night of his lying down to sleep within those dreary walls, he wore the night out with such thoughts.", "그 음울한 벽 안에 처음 누워 잠을 청하던 밤만큼이나 마음이 편치 않은 채, 그는 그런 생각들로 밤을 지새웠다."], ["What time Young John lay wrapt in peaceful slumber, after composing and arranging the following monumental inscription on his pillow--", "한편 젊은 존은 제 베개 위에 다음과 같은 묘비명을 지어 가다듬은 뒤, 평온한 잠에 폭 싸여 누워 있었다—"]], [["STRANGER!", "나그네여!"], ["RESPECT THE TOMB OF JOHN CHIVERY, JUNIOR, WHO DIED AT AN ADVANCED AGE NOT NECESSARY TO MENTION.", "존 치버리 2세의 무덤에 경의를 표하라. 그는 굳이 밝힐 필요 없는 노령에 이르러 세상을 떠났노라."], ["HE ENCOUNTERED HIS RIVAL IN A DISTRESSED STATE, AND FELT INCLINED TO HAVE A ROUND WITH HIM; BUT, FOR THE SAKE OF THE LOVED ONE, CONQUERED THOSE FEELINGS OF BITTERNESS, AND BECAME MAGNANIMOUS.", "그는 곤경에 처한 자신의 연적과 마주쳤고, 그와 한판 붙고 싶은 마음이 일었으나, 사랑하는 이를 위하여, 그 쓰라린 감정을 정복하고, 너그러운 사람이 되었노라."]], [["# CHAPTER 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea", "# CHAPTER 28. An Appearance in the Marshalsea"]], [["The opinion of the community outside the prison gates bore hard on Clennam as time went on, and he made no friends among the community within.", "감옥 문밖 사회의 여론은 시간이 흐를수록 클레넘에게 가혹해져 갔고, 그는 감옥 안의 무리 가운데서도 친구를 사귀지 못했다."], ["Too depressed to associate with the herd in the yard, who got together to forget their cares; too retiring and too unhappy to join in the poor socialities of the tavern; he kept his own room, and was held in distrust.", "마당에 모여 근심을 잊으려는 무리와 어울리기에는 너무 의기소침했고, 술집의 초라한 사교에 끼기에는 너무 내성적이고 너무 불행했던 그는 제 방에 틀어박혀 지냈으며, 그 때문에 불신을 샀다."], ["Some said he was proud; some objected that he was sullen and reserved; some were contemptuous of him, for that he was a poor-spirited dog who pined under his debts.", "어떤 이들은 그가 거만하다 했고, 어떤 이들은 음울하고 속을 드러내지 않는다고 트집을 잡았으며, 어떤 이들은 빚에 짓눌려 야위어 가는 기개 없는 개라며 그를 경멸했다."], ["The whole population were shy of him on these various counts of indictment, but especially the last, which involved a species of domestic treason; and he soon became so confirmed in his seclusion, that his only time for walking up and down was when the evening Club were assembled at their songs and toasts and sentiments, and when the yard was nearly left to the women and children.", "감옥의 주민 전부가 이런저런 죄목을 들어 그를 멀리했는데, 특히 마지막 죄목—일종의 가정 내 반역에 해당하는—이 컸다. 그리하여 그는 곧 칩거에 더욱 굳어져, 그가 이리저리 거닐 수 있는 유일한 시간이라곤 저녁의 클럽이 노래와 건배와 감상에 젖어 모여 있을 때, 그리하여 마당이 거의 여자들과 아이들에게만 남겨졌을 때뿐이었다."]], [["Imprisonment began to tell upon him.", "투옥은 그에게 영향을 미치기 시작했다."], ["He knew that he idled and moped.", "그는 자신이 빈둥거리고 풀이 죽어 있음을 알았다."], ["After what he had known of the influences of imprisonment within the four small walls of the very room he occupied, this consciousness made him afraid of himself.", "자신이 차지하고 있는 바로 그 작은 방의 네 벽 안에서 투옥이 어떤 영향을 끼치는지 익히 보아 온 터라, 이 자각은 그로 하여금 스스로를 두려워하게 만들었다."], ["Shrinking from the observation of other men, and shrinking from his own, he began to change very sensibly.", "다른 사람들의 시선에서 움츠러들고, 또 제 자신의 시선에서도 움츠러들면서 그는 매우 뚜렷하게 변해 가기 시작했다."], ["Anybody might see that the shadow of the wall was dark upon him.", "담장의 그늘이 그의 위에 어둡게 드리워 있음을 누구라도 알아볼 수 있었다."]], [["One day when he might have been some ten or twelve weeks in jail, and when he had been trying to read and had not been able to release even the imaginary people of the book from the Marshalsea, a footstep stopped at his door, and a hand tapped at it.", "투옥된 지 한 열두 주쯤 되었을 무렵의 어느 날, 책을 읽으려 애쓰면서도 그 책 속의 상상 속 인물들조차 마셜시에서 풀려나게 하지 못하고 있을 때, 발소리 하나가 그의 문 앞에 멈추더니 손 하나가 문을 두드렸다."], ["He arose and opened it, and an agreeable voice accosted him with ‘How do you do, Mr Clennam?", "그가 일어나 문을 열자, 듣기 좋은 목소리가 그에게 인사를 건넸다. \"안녕하십니까, 클레넘 씨?"], ["I hope I am not unwelcome in calling to see you.’", "제가 찾아뵙는 것이 달갑잖은 일은 아니길 바랍니다.\""]], [["It was the sprightly young Barnacle, Ferdinand.", "쾌활한 젊은 배너클, 퍼디낸드였다."], ["He looked very good-natured and prepossessing, though overpoweringly gay and free, in contrast with the squalid prison.", "그는 누추한 감옥과 대조되어 압도적으로 명랑하고 거리낌이 없었지만, 매우 선량하고 호감 가는 인상이었다."]], [["‘You are surprised to see me, Mr Clennam,’ he said, taking the seat which Clennam offered him.", "\"제가 찾아오니 놀라셨겠군요, 클레넘 씨.\" 그는 클레넘이 권한 자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["‘I must confess to being much surprised.’", "\"무척 놀랐다고 고백해야겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Not disagreeably, I hope?’", "\"불쾌하실 정도는 아니길 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘By no means.’", "\"천만에요.\""]], [["‘Thank you.", "\"감사합니다. 솔직히 말씀드리면,\""], ["Frankly,’ said the engaging young Barnacle, ‘I have been excessively sorry to hear that you were under the necessity of a temporary retirement here, and I hope (of course as between two private gentlemen) that our place has had nothing to do with it?’", "호감 가는 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"여기서 잠시 은거하실 처지가 되셨다는 소식을 듣고 무척이나 안타까웠습니다. 그리고 (물론 두 사사로운 신사 사이의 일로) 저희 관청이 그 일과 아무 상관이 없기를 바랍니다만?\""]], [["‘Your office?’", "\"댁의 관청 말입니까?\""]], [["‘Our Circumlocution place.’", "\"저희 에두름국 말입니다.\""]], [["‘I cannot charge any part of my reverses upon that remarkable establishment.’", "\"제 불운의 어느 한 부분이라도 그 주목할 만한 기관 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Upon my life,’ said the vivacious young Barnacle, ‘I am heartily glad to know it.", "\"맹세코,\" 활기찬 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"그 말씀을 들으니 진심으로 기쁩니다."], ["It is quite a relief to me to hear you say it.", "그렇게 말씀해 주시니 정말 마음이 놓입니다."], ["I should have so exceedingly regretted our place having had anything to do with your difficulties.’", "저희 관청이 선생의 곤경과 무슨 관련이라도 있었다면 저는 더없이 후회했을 것입니다.\""]], [["Clennam again assured him that he absolved it of the responsibility.", "클레넘은 그 책임에서 관청을 면제해 준다고 다시 한번 확언했다."]], [["‘That’s right,’ said Ferdinand.", "\"잘됐습니다.\" 퍼디낸드가 말했다."], ["‘I am very happy to hear it.", "\"그 말씀을 들으니 정말 기쁩니다."], ["I was rather afraid in my own mind that we might have helped to floor you, because there is no doubt that it is our misfortune to do that kind of thing now and then.", "사실 제 마음 한구석에선 저희가 선생을 쓰러뜨리는 데 일조하지 않았을까 다소 걱정했었지요. 저희가 이따금 그런 류의 일을 하고 마는 것이 저희의 불운임은 분명하니까요."], ["We don’t want to do it; but if men will be gravelled, why--we can’t help it.’", "일부러 그러려는 건 아닙니다만, 사람들이 곤경에 빠지기를 자처한다면야—어쩌겠습니까.\""]], [["‘Without giving an unqualified assent to what you say,’ returned Arthur, gloomily, ‘I am much obliged to you for your interest in me.’", "\"당신 말씀에 전적으로 동의하는 것은 아니나,\" 아서가 침울하게 응수했다, \"저를 염려해 주시니 무척 고맙습니다.\""]], [["‘No, but really!", "\"아니, 정말입니다!"], ["Our place is,’ said the easy young Barnacle, ‘the most inoffensive place possible.", "저희 관청은,\" 느긋한 젊은 배너클이 말했다, \"있을 수 있는 한 가장 무해한 곳입니다."], ["You’ll say we are a humbug.", "저희가 사기꾼이라고 하시겠지요."], ["I won’t say we are not; but all that sort of thing is intended to be, and must be.", "아니라고는 하지 않겠습니다. 하지만 그런 류의 일은 다 그렇게 의도된 것이고, 또 그래야만 하는 것입니다."], ["Don’t you see?’", "아시겠습니까?\""]], [["‘I do not,’ said Clennam.", "\"모르겠습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘You don’t regard it from the right point of view.", "\"올바른 관점에서 보시질 않는군요."], ["It is the point of view that is the essential thing.", "관점이야말로 핵심입니다."], ["Regard our place from the point of view that we only ask you to leave us alone, and we are as capital a Department as you’ll find anywhere.’", "저희 관청을, 그저 저희를 가만 내버려 두기만 바란다는 관점에서 보십시오. 그러면 저희는 어디서도 찾기 힘들 만큼 훌륭한 부처입니다.\""]], [["‘Is your place there to be left alone?’ asked Clennam.", "\"댁의 관청이 가만 내버려 두라고 있는 곳입니까?\" 클레넘이 물었다."]], [["‘You exactly hit it,’ returned Ferdinand.", "\"정확히 짚으셨습니다.\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다."], ["‘It is there with the express intention that everything shall be left alone.", "\"모든 일이 가만 내버려지도록 하겠다는 명확한 의도로 거기 있는 것이지요."], ["That is what it means.", "그게 그 뜻입니다."], ["That is what it’s for.", "그게 그 용도입니다."], ["No doubt there’s a certain form to be kept up that it’s for something else, but it’s only a form.", "물론 다른 무언가를 위한 것인 양 일정한 격식을 갖추기는 해야 하지만, 그건 그저 격식일 뿐입니다."], ["Why, good Heaven, we are nothing but forms!", "아니, 맙소사, 저희는 격식 그 자체일 뿐이지요!"], ["Think what a lot of our forms you have gone through.", "선생께서 저희의 격식을 얼마나 많이 거치셨는지 생각해 보십시오."], ["And you have never got any nearer to an end?’", "그러고도 끝에는 조금도 더 가까이 다가가지 못하셨지요?\""]], [["‘Never,’ said Clennam.", "\"한 번도요.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Look at it from the right point of view, and there you have us--official and effectual.", "\"올바른 관점에서 보시면, 거기 저희가 있습니다—공식적이고 효과적으로요."], ["It’s like a limited game of cricket.", "그건 일종의 제한된 크리켓 경기 같은 겁니다."], ["A field of outsiders are always going in to bowl at the Public Service, and we block the balls.’", "한 무리의 외부인들이 늘 들어와 공무에다 공을 던지려 들고, 저희는 그 공을 막아 냅니다.\""]], [["Clennam asked what became of the bowlers?", "클레넘은 그 투수들은 어떻게 되느냐고 물었다."], ["The airy young Barnacle replied that they grew tired, got dead beat, got lamed, got their backs broken, died off, gave it up, went in for other games.", "가벼운 젊은 배너클은, 그들이 지쳐서 녹초가 되고, 절름발이가 되고, 허리가 부러지고, 하나둘 죽어 나가고, 포기하고, 다른 경기로 옮겨 간다고 대답했다."]], [["‘And this occasions me to congratulate myself again,’ he pursued, ‘on the circumstance that our place has had nothing to do with your temporary retirement.", "\"바로 이 점이 다시금 제게 자축할 거리를 줍니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"저희 관청이 선생의 일시적인 은거와 아무 관련이 없다는 사정 말입니다."], ["It very easily might have had a hand in it; because it is undeniable that we are sometimes a most unlucky place, in our effects upon people who will not leave us alone.", "충분히 한몫했을 수도 있었지요. 저희를 가만 내버려 두지 않는 사람들에게 미치는 영향에서, 저희가 때때로 더없이 불운한 곳이라는 것은 부정할 수 없으니까요."], ["Mr Clennam, I am quite unreserved with you.", "클레넘 씨, 저는 선생께 아주 거리낌이 없습니다."], ["As between yourself and myself, I know I may be.", "선생과 저 사이라면 그래도 된다는 걸 알지요."], ["I was so, when I first saw you making the mistake of not leaving us alone; because I perceived that you were inexperienced and sanguine, and had--I hope you’ll not object to my saying--some simplicity?’", "처음 선생께서 저희를 가만 내버려 두지 않는 실수를 저지르시는 걸 보았을 때부터 저는 그랬습니다. 선생께서 경험이 없고 낙관적이며, 그리고—이런 말 해도 언짢지 않으시길 바랍니다만—다소 순진하신 데가 있다고 느꼈으니까요?\""]], [["‘Not at all.’", "\"전혀요.\""]], [["‘Some simplicity.", "\"다소 순진하신 데가요."], ["Therefore I felt what a pity it was, and I went out of my way to hint to you (which really was not official, but I never am official when I can help it) something to the effect that if I were you, I wouldn’t bother myself.", "그래서 저는 참 안타깝다고 느꼈고, 일부러 시간을 내어 선생께 넌지시 일러 드린 것입니다(사실 그건 공무가 아니었지요. 저는 가능한 한 공무적으로 굴지 않으니까요)."], ["However, you did bother yourself, and you have since bothered yourself.", "제가 선생이라면 굳이 골머리를 앓지 않겠노라는 취지로 말입니다. 그런데도 선생께선 골머리를 앓으셨고, 그 후로도 계속 골머리를 앓으셨지요."], ["Now, don’t do it any more.’", "자, 이제 더는 그러지 마십시오.\""]], [["‘I am not likely to have the opportunity,’ said Clennam.", "\"그럴 기회조차 없을 것 같습니다.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Oh yes, you are!", "\"아니, 있고말고요!"], ["You’ll leave here.", "여기서 나가시게 될 겁니다."], ["Everybody leaves here.", "누구나 여기서 나갑니다."], ["There are no ends of ways of leaving here.", "여기서 나가는 길은 끝도 없지요."], ["Now, don’t come back to us.", "자, 다시 저희에게로 돌아오지 마십시오."], ["That entreaty is the second object of my call.", "그 간청이 제가 찾아온 두 번째 목적입니다."], ["Pray, don’t come back to us.", "부디 저희에게 돌아오지 마십시오."], ["Upon my honour,’ said Ferdinand in a very friendly and confiding way, ‘I shall be greatly vexed if you don’t take warning by the past and keep away from us.’", "명예를 걸고,\" 퍼디낸드가 매우 다정하고 흉허물 없는 투로 말했다, \"지난 일을 교훈 삼아 저희를 멀리하지 않으신다면, 저는 몹시 속이 상할 겁니다.\""]], [["‘And the invention?’ said Clennam.", "\"그럼 그 발명은요?\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘My good fellow,’ returned Ferdinand, ‘if you’ll excuse the freedom of that form of address, nobody wants to know of the invention, and nobody cares twopence-halfpenny about it.’", "\"이보십시오,\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다, \"이런 식의 허물없는 호칭을 용서해 주신다면, 그 발명에 대해 알고자 하는 사람은 아무도 없고, 그 따위에 한 푼 반어치라도 관심 두는 사람도 없습니다.\""]], [["‘Nobody in the Office, that is to say?’", "\"즉, 관청 안에는 아무도 없다는 말씀이지요?\""]], [["‘Nor out of it.", "\"바깥에도 없습니다."], ["Everybody is ready to dislike and ridicule any invention.", "누구나 어떤 발명이든 싫어하고 비웃을 채비가 되어 있지요."], ["You have no idea how many people want to be left alone.", "가만 내버려지기를 바라는 사람이 얼마나 많은지 선생께선 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["You have no idea how the Genius of the country (overlook the Parliamentary nature of the phrase, and don’t be bored by it) tends to being left alone.", "이 나라의 천재성이라는 것이(이 말의 의회적인 성격은 눈감아 주시고, 지루해하지는 마십시오) 얼마나 가만 내버려지는 쪽으로 기우는지 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다. 제 말을 믿으십시오, 클레넘 씨,\""], ["Believe me, Mr Clennam,’ said the sprightly young Barnacle in his pleasantest manner, ‘our place is not a wicked Giant to be charged at full tilt; but only a windmill showing you, as it grinds immense quantities of chaff, which way the country wind blows.’", "쾌활한 젊은 배너클이 더없이 유쾌한 태도로 말했다, \"저희 관청은 전속력으로 돌진해 칠 사악한 거인이 아닙니다. 그저 어마어마한 양의 겨를 갈아 내며 이 나라의 바람이 어느 쪽으로 부는지를 보여 주는 풍차일 뿐이지요.\""]], [["‘If I could believe that,’ said Clennam, ‘it would be a dismal prospect for all of us.’", "\"그 말을 믿을 수 있다면,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"그것은 우리 모두에게 암담한 전망이겠습니다.\""]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Don’t say so!’ returned Ferdinand.", "그런 말씀 마십시오!\" 퍼디낸드가 응수했다."], ["‘It’s all right.", "\"다 괜찮습니다."], ["We must have humbug, we all like humbug, we couldn’t get on without humbug.", "우리에겐 사기가 있어야 하고, 우리 모두 사기를 좋아하며, 사기 없이는 살아갈 수 없습니다."], ["A little humbug, and a groove, and everything goes on admirably, if you leave it alone.’", "약간의 사기와, 한 줄기 정해진 홈, 그리고 가만 내버려 두기만 하면, 만사가 훌륭하게 굴러가지요.\""]], [["With this hopeful confession of his faith as the head of the rising Barnacles who were born of woman, to be followed under a variety of watchwords which they utterly repudiated and disbelieved, Ferdinand rose.", "여자의 몸에서 태어나, 그들이 철저히 부인하고 믿지 않는 갖가지 구호 아래 뒤를 이을 신진 배너클 일가의 우두머리로서, 이런 희망찬 신앙고백을 마치고 퍼디낸드는 일어섰다."], ["Nothing could be more agreeable than his frank and courteous bearing, or adapted with a more gentlemanly instinct to the circumstances of his visit.", "그의 솔직하고 정중한 태도보다, 또 신사다운 본능으로 이 방문의 정황에 그토록 잘 맞춰진 태도보다 더 유쾌한 것은 없었다."]], [["‘Is it fair to ask,’ he said, as Clennam gave him his hand with a real feeling of thankfulness for his candour and good-humour, ‘whether it is true that our late lamented Merdle is the cause of this passing inconvenience?’", "\"여쭤 봐도 될는지요,\" 클레넘이 그의 솔직함과 유쾌함에 진심 어린 고마움을 느끼며 손을 내밀자 그가 말했다, \"고인이 되신 우리의 애석한 머들 씨가 이 지나가는 불편의 원인이라는 것이 사실인지요?\""]], [["‘I am one of the many he has ruined.", "\"제가 그에게 파멸당한 수많은 사람 가운데 하나입니다."], ["Yes.’", "그렇습니다.\""]], [["‘He must have been an exceedingly clever fellow,’ said Ferdinand Barnacle.", "\"대단히 영리한 작자였음에 틀림없군요.\" 퍼디낸드 배너클이 말했다."]], [["Arthur, not being in the mood to extol the memory of the deceased, was silent.", "고인의 기억을 칭송할 기분이 아니었던 아서는 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘A consummate rascal, of course,’ said Ferdinand, ‘but remarkably clever!", "\"물론 더할 나위 없는 악당이었지요.\" 퍼디낸드가 말했다, \"그러나 놀랍도록 영리했어요!"], ["One cannot help admiring the fellow.", "그 작자를 감탄하지 않을 수가 없습니다."], ["Must have been such a master of humbug.", "사기의 대가였음에 틀림없어요."], ["Knew people so well--got over them so completely--did so much with them!’", "사람들을 어찌나 잘 알았던지—어찌나 완벽하게 구워삶았던지—그들을 가지고 얼마나 많은 일을 해냈던지!\""]], [["In his easy way, he was really moved to genuine admiration.", "그 느긋한 투에서, 그는 정말로 진심 어린 감탄에 사로잡혀 있었다."]], [["‘I hope,’ said Arthur, ‘that he and his dupes may be a warning to people not to have so much done with them again.’", "\"바라건대,\" 아서가 말했다, \"그와 그에게 속은 자들이, 사람들이 다시는 그렇게 농락당하지 않도록 하는 경계가 되었으면 합니다.\""]], [["‘My dear Mr Clennam,’ returned Ferdinand, laughing, ‘have you really such a verdant hope?", "\"이보십시오, 클레넘 씨,\" 퍼디낸드가 웃으며 응수했다, \"정말 그런 풋내 나는 희망을 품고 계십니까?"], ["The next man who has as large a capacity and as genuine a taste for swindling, will succeed as well.", "사기에 그만한 역량과 그만한 진정한 취향을 지닌 다음 사람도 똑같이 성공할 것입니다. 죄송합니다만, 인간이라는 벌 떼가 낡은 양철 주전자 두드리는 소리에 얼마나 몰려드는지 선생께선 정말 짐작도 못 하실 겁니다."], ["Pardon me, but I think you really have no idea how the human bees will swarm to the beating of any old tin kettle; in that fact lies the complete manual of governing them.", "바로 그 사실 속에 그들을 다스리는 완벽한 지침서가 들어 있지요. 그 주전자가 귀금속으로 만들어졌다고 그들이 믿게 될 때, 바로 그 사실 속에 우리의 애석한 고인 같은 자들의 온갖 권능이 들어 있는 겁니다."], ["When they can be got to believe that the kettle is made of the precious metals, in that fact lies the whole power of men like our late lamented.", "물론 여기저기,\" 퍼디낸드가 정중하게 말했다, \"예외적인 경우도 있지요."], ["No doubt there are here and there,’ said Ferdinand politely, ‘exceptional cases, where people have been taken in for what appeared to them to be much better reasons; and I need not go far to find such a case; but they don’t invalidate the rule.", "사람들이 보기에 훨씬 더 그럴듯한 이유로 속아 넘어간 경우 말입니다. 그런 경우를 찾자고 멀리 갈 것도 없습니다만, 그렇다고 그 법칙이 무효가 되지는 않습니다."], ["Good day!", "그럼 안녕히 계십시오!"], ["I hope that when I have the pleasure of seeing you, next, this passing cloud will have given place to sunshine.", "다음번에 뵙는 기쁨을 누릴 때엔 이 지나가는 먹구름이 햇살에 자리를 내주었기를 바랍니다. 문 밖으로는 한 발짝도 나오지 마십시오."], ["Don’t come a step beyond the door.", "나가는 길은 제가 완벽하게 압니다."], ["I know the way out perfectly. Good day!’", "안녕히 계십시오!\""]], [["With those words, the best and brightest of the Barnacles went down-stairs, hummed his way through the Lodge, mounted his horse in the front court-yard, and rode off to keep an appointment with his noble kinsman, who wanted a little coaching before he could triumphantly answer certain infidel Snobs who were going to question the Nobs about their statesmanship.", "이 말과 함께, 배너클 일가 가운데 가장 훌륭하고 가장 빛나는 그는 아래층으로 내려가, 콧노래를 흥얼거리며 수위실을 지나, 앞마당에서 말에 올라타고는, 자신의 고귀한 친척과의 약속을 지키러 말을 몰아 떠났다. 그 친척은, 노브들의 치국 능력을 따져 물으려는 어떤 불경한 속물들에게 의기양양하게 답할 수 있으려면, 약간의 사전 지도가 필요했던 것이다."]], [["He must have passed Mr Rugg on his way out, for, a minute or two afterwards, that ruddy-headed gentleman shone in at the door, like an elderly Phoebus.", "그는 나가는 길에 러그 씨와 마주쳤음이 분명했다. 일이 분쯤 지나자, 그 불그스름한 머리의 신사가 늙은 포이보스처럼 문간에서 환히 빛났으니 말이다."]], [["‘How do you do to-day, sir?’ said Mr Rugg.", "\"오늘은 어떠십니까, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Is there any little thing I can do for you to-day, sir?’", "\"오늘 제가 해 드릴 자잘한 일이라도 있을는지요, 선생?\""]], [["‘No, I thank you.’", "\"아니요, 고맙습니다.\""]], [["Mr Rugg’s enjoyment of embarrassed affairs was like a housekeeper’s enjoyment in pickling and preserving, or a washerwoman’s enjoyment of a heavy wash, or a dustman’s enjoyment of an overflowing dust-bin, or any other professional enjoyment of a mess in the way of business.", "곤란해진 사건을 두고 러그 씨가 누리는 즐거움이란, 절이고 저장하는 일에서 가정부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 산더미 같은 빨랫감에서 세탁부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 넘쳐 나는 쓰레기통에서 청소부가 누리는 즐거움, 또는 그 어떤 직업이든 일거리로서의 난장판에서 누리는 직업적 즐거움과도 같은 것이었다."]], [["‘I still look round, from time to time, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, cheerfully, ‘to see whether any lingering Detainers are accumulating at the gate.", "\"저는 여전히 이따금씩 둘러봅니다, 선생.\" 러그 씨가 쾌활하게 말했다, \"혹시 남은 채권자들이 문간에 쌓이고 있지나 않은지 보려고요."], ["They have fallen in pretty thick, sir; as thick as we could have expected.’", "꽤나 두껍게 몰려들었습니다, 선생. 저희가 예상했던 만큼이나 두껍게요.\""]], [["He remarked upon the circumstance as if it were matter of congratulation: rubbing his hands briskly, and rolling his head a little.", "그는 이 사정을 마치 축하할 일인 양 언급하며, 두 손을 활기차게 비비고 고개를 약간 갸웃거렸다."]], [["‘As thick,’ repeated Mr Rugg, ‘as we could reasonably have expected.", "\"예상했던 만큼이나 두껍게요.\" 러그 씨가 되풀이했다."], ["Quite a shower-bath of ‘em.", "\"마치 소나기처럼 쏟아져 내렸지요."], ["I don’t often intrude upon you now, when I look round, because I know you are not inclined for company, and that if you wished to see me, you would leave word in the Lodge.", "둘러볼 때 요즘은 자주 폐를 끼치지 않습니다. 선생께서 사람을 반기지 않으시는 걸 알고 있고, 또 저를 보고 싶으시면 수위실에 말씀을 남기시리란 걸 아니까요."], ["But I am here pretty well every day, sir.", "하지만 저는 거의 매일 여기 와 있습니다, 선생."], ["Would this be an unseasonable time, sir,’ asked Mr Rugg, coaxingly, ‘for me to offer an observation?’", "한 가지 소견을 말씀드리기에 지금이 때 아닌 때는 아닐는지요, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 구슬리듯 물었다."]], [["‘As seasonable a time as any other.’", "\"여느 때만큼이나 적당한 때입니다.\""]], [["‘Hum!", "\"흠!"], ["Public opinion, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘has been busy with you.’", "여론이, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 말했다, \"선생을 두고 분주합니다.\""]], [["‘I don’t doubt it.’", "\"의심치 않습니다.\""]], [["‘Might it not be advisable, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, more coaxingly yet, ‘now to make, at last and after all, a trifling concession to public opinion?", "\"이제, 마침내, 모든 걸 따져 본 끝에, 여론에 자그마한 양보를 하시는 게 좋지 않겠습니까, 선생?\" 러그 씨가 한층 더 구슬리듯 말했다."], ["We all do it in one way or another.", "\"우리 모두 이런저런 식으로 그렇게 하지요."], ["The fact is, we must do it.’", "사실, 우리는 그래야만 합니다.\""]], [["‘I cannot set myself right with it, Mr Rugg, and have no business to expect that I ever shall.’", "\"저는 여론과 화해할 수가 없습니다, 러그 씨. 그리고 언젠가 그러리라 기대할 자격도 없지요.\""]], [["‘Don’t say that, sir, don’t say that.", "\"그런 말씀 마십시오, 선생, 그런 말씀 마세요."], ["The cost of being moved to the Bench is almost insignificant, and if the general feeling is strong that you ought to be there, why--really--’", "상급 감옥으로 옮기는 비용은 거의 보잘것없고, 선생께서 거기 계셔야 한다는 전반적인 정서가 강하다면야, 그야—정말이지—\""]], [["‘I thought you had settled, Mr Rugg,’ said Arthur, ‘that my determination to remain here was a matter of taste.’", "\"러그 씨, 제가 여기 남겠다고 결심한 것은 취향의 문제라고 결론지으신 줄 알았는데요.\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["‘Well, sir, well!", "\"글쎄요, 선생, 글쎄요!"], ["But is it good taste, is it good taste?", "하지만 그게 좋은 취향입니까, 좋은 취향이냐고요?"], ["That’s the Question.’ Mr Rugg was so soothingly persuasive as to be quite pathetic.", "그게 문제입니다.\" 러그 씨는 어찌나 달래듯 설득하던지 거의 측은해 보일 지경이었다."], ["‘I was almost going to say, is it good feeling?", "\"거의 좋은 정서냐고 말할 뻔했습니다."], ["This is an extensive affair of yours; and your remaining here where a man can come for a pound or two, is remarked upon as not in keeping.", "이것은 선생의 큰 사건입니다. 그런데 일 이 파운드 때문에 사람이 들어올 수 있는 이런 곳에 선생께서 남아 계신 것을, 격에 맞지 않는다고들 합니다."], ["It is not in keeping.", "격에 맞지 않지요."], ["I can’t tell you, sir, in how many quarters I heard it mentioned.", "선생, 제가 얼마나 여러 곳에서 그 말이 오가는 걸 들었는지 이루 말씀드릴 수가 없습니다."], ["I heard comments made upon it last night in a Parlour frequented by what I should call, if I did not look in there now and then myself, the best legal company--I heard, there, comments on it that I was sorry to hear.", "어젯밤에도, 제가 가끔 들여다보지 않는다면 최고의 법조계 인사들이라 부를 만한 이들이 드나드는 어느 응접실에서 그에 관한 논평을 들었지요—거기서, 듣기 안타까운 논평을 들었습니다. 선생을 생각하니 마음이 아프더군요."], ["They hurt me on your account.", "또, 바로 오늘 아침 식사 자리에서도요."], ["Again, only this morning at breakfast.", "제 딸이(선생께선 한낱 여자일 뿐이라 하시겠지요. 하지만 이런 일에 대한 감각이 있고, 게다가 러그 대 보킨스 소송의 원고로서 약간의 개인적 경험까지 있는 아이입니다) 큰 놀라움을 표하더군요."], ["My daughter (but a woman, you’ll say: yet still with a feeling for these things, and even with some little personal experience, as the plaintiff in Rugg and Bawkins) was expressing her great surprise; her great surprise.", "큰 놀라움을요. 자, 이런 사정에서, 또 우리 가운데 누구도 여론 위에 완전히 군림할 수는 없음을 생각하면, 그 여론에 자그마한 양보를 하시는 게—자, 선생,\""], ["Now under these circumstances, and considering that none of us can quite set ourselves above public opinion, wouldn’t a trifling concession to that opinion be--Come, sir,’ said Rugg, ‘I will put it on the lowest ground of argument, and say, Amiable?’", "러그가 말했다, \"논의의 가장 낮은 근거에 놓고 말씀드리지요. 호감 가는 일이 아니겠습니까?\""]], [["Arthur’s thoughts had once more wandered away to Little Dorrit, and the question remained unanswered.", "아서의 생각은 또다시 리틀 도릿에게로 흘러가 버렸고, 그 물음은 답을 얻지 못한 채 남았다."]], [["‘As to myself, sir,’ said Mr Rugg, hoping that his eloquence had reduced him to a state of indecision, ‘it is a principle of mine not to consider myself when a client’s inclinations are in the scale.", "\"저로 말씀드리면, 선생,\" 러그 씨가 자신의 웅변이 그를 망설임의 상태로 몰아넣었기를 바라며 말했다, \"의뢰인의 의향이 저울 위에 놓여 있을 때는 제 자신을 고려하지 않는 것이 제 신조입니다."], ["But, knowing your considerate character and general wish to oblige, I will repeat that I should prefer your being in the Bench.", "하지만 선생의 사려 깊은 성품과 남을 기쁘게 하려는 전반적인 마음씨를 알기에, 다시 말씀드리거니와 저는 선생께서 상급 감옥에 계시는 편을 선호합니다. 선생의 사건은 떠들썩한 화제가 되었지요."], ["Your case has made a noise; it is a creditable case to be professionally concerned in; I should feel on a better standing with my connection, if you went to the Bench.", "직업적으로 관여하기에 명예로운 사건입니다. 선생께서 상급 감옥으로 가신다면, 저는 제 인맥 사이에서 더 나은 입지를 얻을 것입니다."], ["Don’t let that influence you, sir.", "그러나 그것에 휘둘리지는 마십시오, 선생."], ["I merely state the fact.’", "저는 그저 사실을 말씀드릴 뿐입니다.\""]], [["So errant had the prisoner’s attention already grown in solitude and dejection, and so accustomed had it become to commune with only one silent figure within the ever-frowning walls, that Clennam had to shake off a kind of stupor before he could look at Mr Rugg, recall the thread of his talk, and hurriedly say, ‘I am unchanged, and unchangeable, in my decision. Pray, let it be; let it be!’ Mr Rugg, without concealing that he was nettled and mortified, replied:", "고독과 낙담 속에서 죄수의 주의는 이미 어찌나 산만해졌던지, 또 늘 찌푸린 벽 안에서 단 하나의 말 없는 형상하고만 마음을 나누는 데 어찌나 익숙해졌던지, 클레넘은 일종의 멍한 상태를 떨쳐 낸 뒤에야 러그 씨를 바라보고, 그가 한 말의 실마리를 되살리며, 황급히 말할 수 있었다. \"제 결심은 변함이 없고, 변할 수도 없습니다. 부디, 그냥 두십시오. 그냥 두세요!\" 러그 씨는, 자신이 약이 오르고 분했음을 감추지도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["Beyond a doubt, sir.", "그야 의심할 여지가 없지요, 선생."], ["I have travelled out of the record, sir, I am aware, in putting the point to you.", "그 점을 선생께 들이댄 것은, 제가 기록을 벗어난 일임을 압니다."], ["But really, when I hear it remarked in several companies, and in very good company, that however worthy of a foreigner, it is not worthy of the spirit of an Englishman to remain in the Marshalsea when the glorious liberties of his island home admit of his removal to the Bench, I thought I would depart from the narrow professional line marked out to me, and mention it.", "하지만 정말이지, 여러 모임에서, 그것도 매우 훌륭한 모임에서, 마셜시에 남는 것은 외국인에게야 어울릴지언정, 이 섬나라 고향의 영광스러운 자유가 상급 감옥으로의 이감을 허락하는 마당에 영국인의 기개에는 어울리지 않는다는 말을 들었을 때, 저는 제게 그어진 좁은 직업적 선을 벗어나서라도 이를 언급해야겠다고 생각한 것입니다. 개인적으로는,\""], ["Personally,’ said Mr Rugg, ‘I have no opinion on the topic.’", "러그 씨가 말했다, \"이 화제에 대해 아무 의견이 없습니다.\""]], [["‘That’s well,’ returned Arthur.", "\"그거 다행이군요.\" 아서가 응수했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["None at all, sir!’ said Mr Rugg.", "전혀 없습니다, 선생!\""], ["‘If I had, I should have been unwilling, some minutes ago, to see a client of mine visited in this place by a gentleman of a high family riding a saddle-horse.", "러그 씨가 말했다. \"만약 있었다면, 저는 조금 전 제 의뢰인이 안장 말을 탄 명문가의 신사로부터 이런 곳에서 방문을 받는 것을 보기를 꺼렸을 것입니다."], ["But it was not my business.", "하지만 그건 제 일이 아니었지요."], ["If I had, I might have wished to be now empowered to mention to another gentleman, a gentleman of military exterior at present waiting in the Lodge, that my client had never intended to remain here, and was on the eve of removal to a superior abode.", "만약 있었다면, 저는 지금 수위실에서 기다리는 또 다른 신사, 군인 같은 풍채의 신사에게, 제 의뢰인은 결코 여기 남을 의도가 없었으며 더 나은 거처로 이감되기 직전이라고 말할 권한이 부여되기를 바랐을지도 모릅니다."], ["But my course as a professional machine is clear; I have nothing to do with it.", "하지만 직업적 기계로서 제가 갈 길은 명확하니, 저는 그 일과 아무 상관이 없습니다."], ["Is it your good pleasure to see the gentleman, sir?’", "그 신사를 만나 보시겠습니까, 선생?\""]], [["‘Who is waiting to see me, did you say?’", "\"누가 저를 만나려고 기다린다고 하셨지요?\""]], [["‘I did take that unprofessional liberty, sir.", "\"그 직업적이지 못한 결례를 제가 저지르긴 했습니다, 선생."], ["Hearing that I was your professional adviser, he declined to interpose before my very limited function was performed.", "제가 선생의 직업적 자문가임을 듣고서, 그 신사는 제 매우 제한된 역할이 수행되기 전에 끼어들기를 사양하더군요."], ["Happily,’ said Mr Rugg, with sarcasm, ‘I did not so far travel out of the record as to ask the gentleman for his name.’", "다행히도,\" 러그 씨가 빈정대며 말했다, \"저는 그 신사에게 이름을 묻는 데까지 기록을 벗어나지는 않았습니다.\""]], [["‘I suppose I have no resource but to see him,’ sighed Clennam, wearily.", "\"만나는 것밖에 달리 도리가 없겠지요.\" 클레넘이 지친 듯 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["‘Then it _is_ your good pleasure, sir?’ retorted Rugg.", "\"그럼 만나시겠다는 거지요, 선생?\" 러그가 되받았다."], ["‘Am I honoured by your instructions to mention as much to the gentleman, as I pass out?", "\"제가 나가는 길에 그 신사에게 그렇게 전하라는 지시를 받는 영광을 누리는 것입니까? 그렇습니까?"], ["I am? Thank you, sir.", "감사합니다, 선생."], ["I take my leave.’ His leave he took accordingly, in dudgeon.", "이만 물러가겠습니다.\" 그리하여 그는 부아가 난 채로 물러갔다."]], [["The gentleman of military exterior had so imperfectly awakened Clennam’s curiosity, in the existing state of his mind, that a half-forgetfulness of such a visitor’s having been referred to, was already creeping over it as a part of the sombre veil which almost always dimmed it now, when a heavy footstep on the stairs aroused him.", "군인 같은 풍채의 그 신사는, 지금의 마음 상태에서 클레넘의 호기심을 너무도 어렴풋이만 일깨웠던 터라, 그런 방문객이 언급되었다는 사실에 대한 절반쯤의 망각이, 이제 거의 늘 그의 마음을 흐리게 하던 그 음울한 장막의 일부로서 이미 그 위로 슬며시 기어들고 있었는데, 그때 계단을 밟는 무거운 발소리가 그를 일깨웠다."], ["It appeared to ascend them, not very promptly or spontaneously, yet with a display of stride and clatter meant to be insulting.", "그 발소리는 계단을 오르는 듯했는데, 그리 신속하지도 자발적이지도 않으면서도, 모욕하려는 의도가 담긴 성큼성큼한 보폭과 덜그럭거림을 과시하고 있었다."], ["As it paused for a moment on the landing outside his door, he could not recall his association with the peculiarity of its sound, though he thought he had one.", "그것이 그의 문밖 층계참에서 잠시 멈추었을 때, 그는 그 소리의 특이함과 자신의 연상을 떠올리지 못했으나, 무언가 연상되는 게 있다고는 생각했다."], ["Only a moment was given him for consideration.", "그에게 곱씹을 시간은 한순간밖에 주어지지 않았다."], ["His door was immediately swung open by a thump, and in the doorway stood the missing Blandois, the cause of many anxieties.", "그의 문은 곧장 쿵 하고 활짝 열렸고, 그 문간에는 수많은 근심의 원인인, 행방불명이었던 블랑두아가 서 있었다."]], [["‘Salve, fellow jail-bird!’ said he.", "\"안녕하신가, 동료 감방새여!\""], ["‘You want me, it seems.", "그가 말했다. \"나를 원했다지."], ["Here I am!’", "자, 여기 왔네!\""]], [["Before Arthur could speak to him in his indignant wonder, Cavalletto followed him into the room.", "아서가 분노 어린 경악 속에 그에게 말을 건네기도 전에, 카발레토가 그를 뒤따라 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Mr Pancks followed Cavalletto.", "팽크스 씨가 카발레토의 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Neither of the two had been there since its present occupant had had possession of it.", "두 사람 모두 현재의 거주자가 이 방을 차지한 이래로 이곳에 온 적이 없었다."], ["Mr Pancks, breathing hard, sidled near the window, put his hat on the ground, stirred his hair up with both hands, and folded his arms, like a man who had come to a pause in a hard day’s work.", "팽크스 씨는 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 창가로 비스듬히 다가가 모자를 바닥에 내려놓고는, 두 손으로 머리카락을 헝클어뜨린 다음 팔짱을 꼈으니, 고된 하루 일과 중 한숨 돌리러 멈춘 사람 같았다."], ["Mr Baptist, never taking his eyes from his dreaded chum of old, softly sat down on the floor with his back against the door and one of his ankles in each hand: resuming the attitude (except that it was now expressive of unwinking watchfulness) in which he had sat before the same man in the deeper shade of another prison, one hot morning at Marseilles.", "밥티스트 씨는 옛날의 두려운 동무에게서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 문에 등을 대고 양손으로 제 발목을 하나씩 쥐고 바닥에 가만히 주저앉았으니, 마르세유의 어느 무더운 아침에 또 다른 감옥의 더 깊은 그늘 속에서 바로 그 사람 앞에 앉아 있던 그 자세를(이제는 깜박이지 않는 경계심을 드러낸다는 점만 빼면) 그대로 되풀이한 것이었다."]], [["‘I have it on the witnessing of these two madmen,’ said Monsieur Blandois, otherwise Lagnier, otherwise Rigaud, ‘that you want me, brother-bird.", "\"이 두 미치광이의 증언에 따르면,\" 블랑두아 씨가, 다른 이름으로는 라니에, 또 다른 이름으로는 리고가 말했다, \"자네가 나를 원한다더군, 동료 새여."], ["Here I am!’\nGlancing round contemptuously at the bedstead, which was turned up by day, he leaned his back against it as a resting-place, without removing his hat from his head, and stood defiantly lounging with his hands in his pockets.", "자, 여기 왔네!\" 낮에는 접어 올려져 있는 침대 틀을 경멸하듯 둘러본 그는, 모자를 머리에서 벗지도 않은 채 거기에 등을 기대고 쉬더니, 주머니에 두 손을 찌른 채 도전적으로 어슬렁거리며 섰다."]], [["‘You villain of ill-omen!’ said Arthur.", "\"이 흉조 같은 악당아!\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘You have purposely cast a dreadful suspicion upon my mother’s house.", "\"당신은 일부러 우리 어머니 댁에 끔찍한 의혹을 씌웠소."], ["Why have you done it?", "왜 그런 짓을 했소?"], ["What prompted you to the devilish invention?’", "무엇이 당신을 그 악마 같은 꾸밈수로 부추긴 거요?\""]], [["Monsieur Rigaud, after frowning at him for a moment, laughed.", "리고 씨는 잠시 그를 노려보다가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["‘Hear this noble gentleman!", "\"이 고귀하신 신사 좀 들어 보게!"], ["Listen, all the world, to this creature of Virtue!", "온 세상이여, 이 미덕의 화신 좀 들어 보게!"], ["But take care, take care.", "하지만 조심하게, 조심해."], ["It is possible, my friend, that your ardour is a little compromising.", "이보게, 친구, 자네의 열정이 좀 자네 입장을 곤란하게 만들지도 모르거든."], ["Holy Blue!", "맙소사!"], ["It is possible.’", "그럴 수도 있다네.\""]], [["‘Signore!’ interposed Cavalletto, also addressing Arthur: ‘for to commence, hear me! I received your instructions to find him, Rigaud; is it not?’", "\"시뇨레!\" 카발레토가 끼어들며, 그 역시 아서에게 말했다. \"우선, 제 말을 들어 보십시오! 제가 그를, 리고를 찾으라는 지시를 받았지요. 그렇지 않습니까?\""]], [["‘It is the truth.’", "\"사실이오.\""]], [["‘I go, consequentementally,’--it would have given Mrs Plornish great concern if she could have been persuaded that his occasional lengthening of an adverb in this way, was the chief fault of his English,--‘first among my countrymen.", "\"그래서 저는, 결과적으롭니다,\"—만일 그가 이따금 이런 식으로 부사를 늘여 빼는 것이 그의 영어에서 으뜸가는 흠이라고 플로니시 부인을 설득할 수 있었더라면, 그녀는 무척 마음을 놓았을 것이다,—\"먼저 제 동포들 사이로 갔습니다. 런던에 도착한 외국인들 소식이 뭐가 있는지 그들에게 물었지요."], ["I ask them what news in Londra, of foreigners arrived.", "그다음 프랑스 사람들 사이로 갔습니다. 그다음 독일 사람들 사이로 갔지요."], ["Then I go among the French.", "다들 제게 말해 줍니다."], ["Then I go among the Germans.", "우리 대부분은 서로를 잘 알거든요."], ["They all tell me.", "그래서 다들 제게 말해 줍니다."], ["The great part of us know well the other, and they all tell me.", "하지만!—그 누구도 그에 대해, 리고에 대해 아무것도 말해 주지 못했습니다. 열다섯 번이나,\""], ["But!--no person can tell me nothing of him, Rigaud.", "카발레토는 손가락을 모두 펼친 왼손을 세 차례나 내뻗었는데, 어찌나 빨리 했던지 눈으로는 그 동작을 좇기 힘들 정도였다, \"외국인들이 가는 곳마다 그에 대해 물었습니다."], ["Fifteen times,’ said Cavalletto, thrice throwing out his left hand with all its fingers spread, and doing it so rapidly that the sense of sight could hardly follow the action, ‘I ask of him in every place where go the foreigners; and fifteen times,’ repeating the same swift performance, ‘they know nothing.", "그런데 열다섯 번이나,\" 같은 날쌘 동작을 되풀이하며, \"그들은 아무것도 몰랐지요."], ["But!--’", "하지만!—\""]], [["At this significant Italian rest on the word ‘But,’ his backhanded shake of his right forefinger came into play; a very little, and very cautiously.", "이 의미심장한 이탈리아식 '하지만'에서의 쉼과 함께, 그의 오른쪽 집게손가락을 손등 방향으로 까딱이는 동작이 등장했으니, 아주 조금, 아주 조심스럽게였다."]], [["‘But!--After a long time when I have not been able to find that he is here in Londra, some one tells me of a soldier with white hair--hey?--not hair like this that he carries--white--who lives retired secrettementally, in a certain place.", "\"하지만!—오랜 시간이 지나도 그가 여기 런던에 있다는 걸 찾아내지 못하고 있을 때, 누군가 제게 흰 머리의 군인 하나 이야기를 해 주더군요—그렇지?—이렇게 그가 기르고 있는 그런 머리가 아니라—흰색—어느 일정한 곳에서 은밀하게 은거하며 사는 사람이라고요."], ["But!--’ with another rest upon the word, ‘who sometimes in the after-dinner, walks, and smokes.", "하지만!—\" 그 단어에서 또 한 번 쉼을 두며, \"이따금 식사 후에 산책을 하고 담배를 피운다고요."], ["It is necessary, as they say in Italy (and as they know, poor people), to have patience.", "이탈리아에서들 하는 말처럼(그리고 그 가엾은 사람들이 아는 대로), 인내심을 가질 필요가 있지요."], ["I have patience.", "저는 인내심이 있습니다."], ["I ask where is this certain place. One.", "저는 그 일정한 곳이 어디냐고 물었지요."], ["believes it is here, one believes it is there.", "어떤 이는 여기라 하고, 어떤 이는 저기라 합니다."], ["Eh well!", "그래!"], ["It is not here, it is not there.", "여기도 아니고, 저기도 아니었지요."], ["I wait patientissamentally.", "저는 더없이 끈기 있게 기다렸습니다."], ["At last I find it.", "마침내 그곳을 찾아냈지요."], ["Then I watch; then I hide, until he walks and smokes.", "그러고는 지켜보았고, 그가 산책하며 담배 피울 때까지 숨어 있었습니다."], ["He is a soldier with grey hair--But!--’ a very decided rest indeed, and a very vigorous play from side to side of the back-handed forefinger--‘he is also this man that you see.’", "그는 잿빛 머리의 군인이었지요—하지만!—\" 정말로 매우 단호한 쉼과 함께, 손등 방향의 집게손가락을 좌우로 아주 힘차게 놀리며—\"그가 또한 여러분이 보시는 이 사람입니다.\""]], [["It was noticeable, that, in his old habit of submission to one who had been at the trouble of asserting superiority over him, he even then bestowed upon Rigaud a confused bend of his head, after thus pointing him out.", "자신에게 우월함을 내세우느라 애써 온 자에게 복종하던 옛 습관 때문에, 그가 이렇게 리고를 가리킨 뒤에조차, 어수선하게 고개를 한 번 그에게 숙였다는 점이 눈에 띄었다."]], [["‘Eh well, Signore!’ he cried in conclusion, addressing Arthur again.", "\"그래, 시뇨레!\" 그가 결론짓듯 외치며 다시 아서에게 말했다."], ["‘I waited for a good opportunity.", "\"저는 좋은 기회를 기다렸습니다."], ["I writed some words to Signor Panco,’ an air of novelty came over Mr Pancks with this designation, ‘to come and help.", "시뇨르 판코께,\"—이 호칭과 함께 팽크스 씨에게 신기한 기색이 어렸다,—\"몇 자 적어 보내, 와서 도와 달라고 했지요."], ["I showed him, Rigaud, at his window, to Signor Panco, who was often the spy in the day.", "낮에 종종 정탐을 맡았던 시뇨르 판코께, 창가에 있는 리고를 보여 드렸습니다."], ["I slept at night near the door of the house.", "밤에는 그 집 문 가까이서 잤지요."], ["At last we entered, only this to-day, and now you see him!", "마침내 우리는, 바로 오늘에야 들어왔고, 이제 그를 보시는 겁니다!"], ["As he would not come up in presence of the illustrious Advocate,’ such was Mr Baptist’s honourable mention of Mr Rugg, ‘we waited down below there, together, and Signor Panco guarded the street.’", "그가 그 저명한 변호사님 앞에서는 올라오려 하지 않아서,\"—이것이 러그 씨에 대한 밥티스트 씨의 영예로운 언급이었다,—\"우리는 저 아래에서 함께 기다렸고, 시뇨르 판코께서 거리를 지키셨습니다.\""]], [["At the close of this recital, Arthur turned his eyes upon the impudent and wicked face.", "이 이야기가 끝나자, 아서는 그 뻔뻔하고 사악한 얼굴로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["As it met his, the nose came down over the moustache and the moustache went up under the nose.", "그 얼굴이 그의 눈길과 마주치자, 코는 콧수염 위로 내려오고 콧수염은 코 밑으로 올라갔다."], ["When nose and moustache had settled into their places again, Monsieur Rigaud loudly snapped his fingers half-a-dozen times; bending forward to jerk the snaps at Arthur, as if they were palpable missiles which he jerked into his face.", "코와 콧수염이 다시 제자리에 자리 잡자, 리고 씨는 여섯 번쯤 손가락을 요란하게 튕겼는데, 몸을 앞으로 기울여 아서에게 그 튕김을 휙휙 던졌으니, 마치 그것이 그의 얼굴에 던지는 손에 잡히는 발사체라도 되는 양했다."]], [["‘Now, Philosopher!’ said Rigaud.", "\"자, 철학자 양반!\" 리고가 말했다."], ["‘What do you want with me?’", "\"나한테 뭘 원하나?\""]], [["‘I want to know,’ returned Arthur, without disguising his abhorrence, ‘how you dare direct a suspicion of murder against my mother’s house?’", "\"알고 싶소,\" 아서가 자신의 혐오감을 감추지 않은 채 응수했다, \"당신이 어찌 감히 우리 어머니 댁에 살인 의혹을 들이댄 거요?\""]], [["‘Dare!’ cried Rigaud.", "\"감히라!\" 리고가 외쳤다."], ["‘Ho, ho!", "\"호, 호!"], ["Hear him! Dare?", "이 친구 좀 보게!"], ["Is it dare?", "감히라?"], ["By Heaven, my small boy, but you are a little imprudent!’", "감히라고? 맙소사, 이 애송이야, 자네 좀 경솔하군!\""]], [["‘I want that suspicion to be cleared away,’ said Arthur.", "\"나는 그 의혹이 말끔히 씻기기를 원하오.\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘You shall be taken there, and be publicly seen.", "\"당신을 거기로 데려가, 사람들 앞에 공공연히 보이도록 할 거요."], ["I want to know, moreover, what business you had there when I had a burning desire to fling you down-stairs.", "그리고 또 알고 싶소. 내가 당신을 계단 아래로 내던지고 싶은 불타는 욕망에 사로잡혔던 그때, 당신이 거기서 무슨 볼일이 있었던 거요."], ["Don’t frown at me, man!", "나한테 인상 쓰지 마시오!"], ["I have seen enough of you to know that you are a bully and coward.", "나는 당신이 약자에게 강한 척하는 겁쟁이임을 알 만큼 충분히 봐 왔소."], ["I need no revival of my spirits from the effects of this wretched place to tell you so plain a fact, and one that you know so well.’", "이렇게 명백한 사실을, 게다가 당신 스스로도 익히 아는 사실을 말해 주는 데에, 이 비참한 곳의 영향에서 기운을 되살릴 필요 따윈 없소.\""]], [["White to the lips, Rigaud stroked his moustache, muttering, ‘By Heaven, my small boy, but you are a little compromising of my lady, your respectable mother’--and seemed for a minute undecided how to act.", "리고는 입술까지 새하얘져서 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 중얼거렸다. \"맙소사, 이 애송이야, 자네 좀 우리 마님, 자네의 존경스러운 어머니를 곤란하게 만드는군\"—그러고는 잠시 어찌할지 결정하지 못한 듯 보였다."], ["His indecision was soon gone.", "그러나 그의 망설임은 곧 사라졌다."], ["He sat himself down with a threatening swagger, and said:", "그는 위협하듯 거들먹거리며 자리에 앉아 말했다."]], [["‘Give me a bottle of wine.", "\"포도주 한 병 다오."], ["You can buy wine here.", "여기서 포도주를 살 수 있지."], ["Send one of your madmen to get me a bottle of wine.", "저 미치광이 하나를 보내 포도주 한 병 가져오게 해."], ["I won’t talk to you without wine.", "포도주 없이는 자네와 이야기하지 않겠네. 자!"], ["Come!", "어떤가?"], ["Yes or no?’", "좋은가, 싫은가?\""]], [["‘Fetch him what he wants, Cavalletto,’ said Arthur, scornfully, producing the money.", "\"카발레토, 그가 원하는 걸 가져다주게.\" 아서가 경멸하듯 말하며 돈을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘Contraband beast,’ added Rigaud, ‘bring Port wine!", "\"밀수꾼 짐승아,\" 리고가 덧붙였다, \"포트와인을 가져와!"], ["I’ll drink nothing but Porto-Porto.’", "나는 포르토-포르토밖에 안 마시겠다.\""]], [["The contraband beast, however, assuring all present, with his significant finger, that he peremptorily declined to leave his post at the door, Signor Panco offered his services.", "그러나 그 밀수꾼 짐승은, 의미심장한 손가락으로 모두에게, 문간의 제 자리를 떠나기를 단호히 거부한다고 확언했고, 시뇨르 판코가 자청해 나섰다."], ["He soon returned with the bottle of wine: which, according to the custom of the place, originating in a scarcity of corkscrews among the Collegians (in common with a scarcity of much else), was already opened for use.", "그는 곧 포도주 병을 들고 돌아왔는데, 그 병은 (코르크 따개가 부족했던—이는 그 밖의 많은 것이 부족했던 것과 마찬가지였다—재소자들 사이에서 비롯된) 그곳의 관례에 따라, 이미 따져서 쓸 수 있게 되어 있었다."]], [["‘Madman!", "\"미치광이!"], ["A large glass,’ said Rigaud.", "큰 잔으로.\" 리고가 말했다."]], [["Signor Panco put a tumbler before him; not without a visible conflict of feeling on the question of throwing it at his head.", "시뇨르 판코는 그의 앞에 큰 잔을 놓았는데, 그것을 그의 머리에 내던질까 하는 문제로 눈에 보이는 감정의 갈등이 없지 않았다."]], [["‘Haha!’ boasted Rigaud.", "\"하하!\" 리고가 으스댔다."], ["‘Once a gentleman, and always a gentleman.", "\"한번 신사면 영원히 신사지."], ["A gentleman from the beginning, and a gentleman to the end.", "처음부터 신사요, 끝까지 신사라네. 빌어먹을!"], ["What the Devil! A gentleman must be waited on, I hope?", "신사라면 시중을 받아야지, 안 그런가?"], ["It’s a part of my character to be waited on!’", "시중받는 것이 내 성품의 일부거든!\""]], [["He half filled the tumbler as he said it, and drank off the contents when he had done saying it.", "그는 그 말을 하면서 잔을 절반쯤 채웠고, 말을 마치자 그 내용물을 단숨에 들이켰다."]], [["‘Hah!’ smacking his lips.", "\"하!\" 입맛을 다시며."], ["‘Not a very old prisoner _that_!", "\"그리 오래된 죄수는 아니구먼, 이건!"], ["I judge by your looks, brave sir, that imprisonment will subdue your blood much sooner than it softens this hot wine.", "자네 안색을 보니, 용감하신 양반, 투옥은 이 독한 포도주를 부드럽게 하는 것보다 훨씬 빨리 자네 피를 길들이겠어."], ["You are mellowing--losing body and colour already.", "자네는 익어 가고 있군—벌써 몸피와 혈색을 잃어 가고 있어."], ["I salute you!’", "자네에게 경배를!\""]], [["He tossed off another half glass: holding it up both before and afterwards, so as to display his small, white hand.", "그는 또 반 잔을 들이켰는데, 마시기 전후로 잔을 치켜들어 자신의 작고 흰 손을 드러내 보였다."]], [["‘To business,’ he then continued.", "\"본론으로.\" 그가 이어 말했다."], ["‘To conversation.", "\"대화로 들어가지."], ["You have shown yourself more free of speech than body, sir.’", "자네는 몸보다 입이 더 자유로운 걸 보여 줬어, 양반.\""]], [["‘I have used the freedom of telling you what you know yourself to be.", "\"나는 당신이 스스로 어떤 인간인 줄 아는 그대로 말해 준 자유를 누렸을 뿐이오."], ["You know yourself, as we all know you, to be far worse than that.’", "당신은, 우리 모두가 당신을 아는 대로, 그보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 인간임을 스스로 알고 있소.\""]], [["‘Add, always a gentleman, and it’s no matter.", "\"거기에 '항상 신사'를 덧붙이면, 그런 건 아무래도 좋아."], ["Except in that regard, we are all alike.", "그 한 가지만 빼면 우리는 다 똑같지."], ["For example: you couldn’t for your life be a gentleman; I couldn’t for my life be otherwise.", "예를 들어, 자네는 죽었다 깨어나도 신사가 될 수 없고, 나는 죽었다 깨어나도 신사 아닌 존재가 될 수 없네."], ["How great the difference!", "그 차이가 얼마나 큰가!"], ["Let us go on.", "계속하지."], ["Words, sir, never influence the course of the cards, or the course of the dice.", "양반, 말이라는 건 결코 카드의 흐름이나 주사위의 흐름에 영향을 주지 못해."], ["Do you know that?", "그걸 아나? 알아?"], ["You do? I also play a game, and words are without power over it.’", "나 또한 한판의 도박을 벌이는데, 말 따위는 그 위에 아무 힘도 없지.\""]], [["Now that he was confronted with Cavalletto, and knew that his story was known--whatever thin disguise he had worn, he dropped; and faced it out, with a bare face, as the infamous wretch he was.", "이제 카발레토와 맞닥뜨려, 자신의 내력이 알려졌음을 알게 된 그는—그동안 걸치고 있던 얄팍한 가면이 무엇이었든 그것을 벗어 던지고는, 본래의 파렴치한 악당답게 맨얼굴로 버텨 냈다."]], [["‘No, my son,’ he resumed, with a snap of his fingers.", "\"아니, 이 사람아,\" 그가 손가락을 한 번 튕기며 말을 이었다."], ["‘I play my game to the end in spite of words; and Death of my Body and Death of my Soul!", "\"나는 말 따위에 아랑곳없이 내 판을 끝까지 밀어붙일 것이고—내 몸의 죽음과 내 영혼의 죽음을 걸고!"], ["I’ll win it.", "기어이 이길 거야."], ["You want to know why I played this little trick that you have interrupted?", "자네가 방해한 이 자그마한 속임수를 내가 왜 벌였는지 알고 싶나? 그럼 알아 두게."], ["Know then that I had, and that I have--do you understand me?", "나에겐 팔 물건이 있었고, 또 있다네—내 말 알아듣겠나?"], ["have--a commodity to sell to my lady your respectable mother.", "있단 말이야—자네의 존경스러운 어머니, 우리 마님께 팔 물건이 말이지."], ["I described my precious commodity, and fixed my price.", "나는 내 귀한 물건을 설명하고 값을 매겼어."], ["Touching the bargain, your admirable mother was a little too calm, too stolid, too immovable and statue-like.", "그 거래에 관한 한, 자네의 훌륭하신 어머니는 좀 지나치게 침착하고, 지나치게 무덤덤하고, 지나치게 요지부동이고 석상 같았지."], ["In fine, your admirable mother vexed me.", "요컨대, 자네의 훌륭하신 어머니가 나를 짜증 나게 했단 말이야."], ["To make variety in my position, and to amuse myself--what!", "내 처지에 변화를 주고 나도 좀 즐기려고—뭐!"], ["a gentleman must be amused at somebody’s expense!--I conceived the happy idea of disappearing.", "신사라면 누군가의 희생으로 즐겨야 하지 않겠나!—나는 사라진다는 멋진 착상을 떠올렸지."], ["An idea, see you, that your characteristic mother and my Flintwinch would have been well enough pleased to execute.", "보다시피, 자네의 그 별난 어머니와 우리 플린트윈치라면 기꺼이 실행에 옮겼을 만한 착상이거든."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Bah, bah, bah, don’t look as from high to low at me!", "바, 바, 바, 나를 위에서 아래로 깔보듯 보지 말게!"], ["I repeat it.", "다시 말하네."], ["Well enough pleased, excessively enchanted, and with all their hearts ravished.", "기꺼이도 정도가 아니라, 지나치도록 황홀해하며, 진심으로 넋이 나가도록 좋아했을 거야."], ["How strongly will you have it?’", "얼마나 강하게 말해 줘야 믿겠나?\""]], [["He threw out the lees of his glass on the ground, so that they nearly spattered Cavalletto.", "그는 잔에 남은 찌꺼기를 바닥에 내던졌는데, 하마터면 카발레토에게 튈 뻔했다."], ["This seemed to draw his attention to him anew.", "이것이 그의 주의를 새삼 그에게 돌린 모양이었다."], ["He set down his glass and said:", "그는 잔을 내려놓고 말했다."]], [["‘I’ll not fill it.", "\"내가 직접 채우진 않겠다."], ["What!", "뭐라고!"], ["I am born to be served.", "나는 시중받으려고 태어난 사람이야."], ["Come then, you Cavalletto, and fill!’", "자, 그럼, 너 카발레토, 와서 채워라!\""]], [["The little man looked at Clennam, whose eyes were occupied with Rigaud, and, seeing no prohibition, got up from the ground, and poured out from the bottle into the glass.", "그 작은 사내는 클레넘을 바라보았는데, 그의 눈은 리고에게 쏠려 있었고, 제지하는 기색이 없자, 그는 바닥에서 일어나 병의 술을 잔에 따랐다."], ["The blending, as he did so, of his old submission with a sense of something humorous; the striving of that with a certain smouldering ferocity, which might have flashed fire in an instant (as the born gentleman seemed to think, for he had a wary eye upon him); and the easy yielding of all to a good-natured, careless, predominant propensity to sit down on the ground again: formed a very remarkable combination of character.", "그렇게 하는 동안, 그의 옛 복종심과 무언가 우스꽝스럽다는 느낌이 뒤섞이고, 또 그것이 한순간에 불꽃을 튀길 수도 있었던 어떤 들끓는 사나움과 다투며(타고난 신사는 그렇게 여기는 듯, 그를 경계하는 눈으로 지켜보았다), 그러면서도 그 모든 것이 다시 바닥에 주저앉으려는 선량하고 무심하며 우세한 성향에 손쉽게 굴복하는 모습은, 매우 주목할 만한 성격의 조합을 이루었다."]], [["‘This happy idea, brave sir,’ Rigaud resumed after drinking, ‘was a happy idea for several reasons.", "\"이 멋진 착상은, 용감하신 양반,\" 술을 마신 뒤 리고가 다시 말을 이었다, \"여러 이유로 멋진 착상이었지."], ["It amused me, it worried your dear mama and my Flintwinch, it caused you agonies (my terms for a lesson in politeness towards a gentleman), and it suggested to all the amiable persons interested that your entirely devoted is a man to fear.", "나를 즐겁게 했고, 자네의 사랑하는 어머니와 우리 플린트윈치를 애태웠고, 자네에게 고통을 안겨 주었으며(신사에 대한 예절을 가르친 수업의 대가지), 또 관심 있는 그 모든 상냥한 분들에게, 자네에게 전적으로 헌신하는 이 몸이 두려워해야 할 사람임을 일깨워 주었지."], ["By Heaven, he is a man to fear!", "맙소사, 그는 두려워해야 할 사람이라네!"], ["Beyond this; it might have restored her wit to my lady your mother--might, under the pressing little suspicion your wisdom has recognised, have persuaded her at last to announce, covertly, in the journals, that the difficulties of a certain contract would be removed by the appearance of a certain important party to it.", "그뿐인가. 그 일로 자네 어머니, 우리 마님이 정신을 되찾았을지도 모르지—자네의 지혜가 알아챈 그 자그마하고도 절박한 의혹의 압박 아래, 마침내 신문에다 넌지시, 어떤 계약의 난점들이 그 계약의 어떤 중요한 당사자가 나타나면 해소될 것이라고 공표하도록 설득했을지도 몰라."], ["Perhaps yes, perhaps no.", "어쩌면 그랬을 수도, 어쩌면 아닐 수도."], ["But that, you have interrupted.", "하지만 그걸 자네가 방해했지."], ["Now, what is it you say?", "자, 이제 뭐라고 할 텐가?"], ["What is it you want?’", "뭘 원하나?\""]], [["Never had Clennam felt more acutely that he was a prisoner in bonds, than when he saw this man before him, and could not accompany him to his mother’s house.", "자신 앞에 이 사내를 두고도 그를 어머니의 집으로 데려갈 수 없을 때만큼, 클레넘이 자신이 사슬에 묶인 죄수임을 절절히 느낀 적은 없었다."], ["All the undiscernible difficulties and dangers he had ever feared were closing in, when he could not stir hand or foot.", "그가 여태껏 두려워해 온 온갖 분간할 수 없는 난점과 위험들이, 손발 하나 까딱할 수 없는 바로 그때 죄어들고 있었다."]], [["‘Perhaps, my friend, philosopher, man of virtue, Imbecile, what you will; perhaps,’ said Rigaud, pausing in his drink to look out of his glass with his horrible smile, ‘you would have done better to leave me alone?’", "\"어쩌면, 내 친구, 철학자, 미덕의 인간, 멍청이, 뭐라 부르든 말이지. 어쩌면,\" 리고가 술을 마시다 멈추고 그 끔찍한 미소를 띤 채 잔 너머로 내다보며 말했다, \"자네는 나를 가만 내버려 두는 편이 나았을지도 몰라?\""]], [["‘No!", "\"아니오!"], ["At least,’ said Clennam, ‘you are known to be alive and unharmed.", "적어도,\" 클레넘이 말했다, \"당신이 살아 있고 무사하다는 게 알려졌소."], ["At least you cannot escape from these two witnesses; and they can produce you before any public authorities, or before hundreds of people!’", "적어도 당신은 이 두 증인에게서 달아날 수 없고, 그들은 어떤 공적 권위 앞에서든, 또는 수백 명의 사람들 앞에서든 당신을 내세울 수 있소!\""]], [["‘But will not produce me before one,’ said Rigaud, snapping his fingers again with an air of triumphant menace.", "\"하지만 단 한 명 앞에서도 나를 내세우지는 못할걸.\" 리고가 의기양양한 위협의 기색으로 다시 손가락을 튕기며 말했다."], ["‘To the Devil with your witnesses!", "\"자네의 증인들 따위는 악마에게나 줘 버려!"], ["To the Devil with your produced!", "자네가 내세운다는 것도 악마에게나 줘 버려!"], ["To the Devil with yourself!", "자네 자신도 악마에게나 줘 버리고!"], ["What!", "뭐라고!"], ["Do I know what I know, for that?", "그렇다고 내가 아는 것을 내가 모르나?"], ["Have I my commodity on sale, for that?", "그렇다고 내 물건이 팔려고 내놓아져 있지 않나?"], ["Bah, poor debtor!", "바, 가엾은 채무자여!"], ["You have interrupted my little project.", "자네는 내 자그마한 계획을 방해했어."], ["Let it pass.", "그건 됐고."], ["How then?", "그래서?"], ["What remains?", "무엇이 남았나?"], ["To you, nothing; to me, all.", "자네에겐, 아무것도. 나에겐, 전부."], ["Produce _me_!", "나를 내세우라고!"], ["Is that what you want?", "그게 자네가 원하는 건가?"], ["I will produce myself, only too quickly.", "내가 직접 나를 내세우지, 너무도 빠르게 말이야."], ["Contrabandist!", "밀수꾼!"], ["Give me pen, ink, and paper.’", "펜과 잉크와 종이를 다오.\""]], [["Cavalletto got up again as before, and laid them before him in his former manner.", "카발레토는 다시 아까처럼 일어나, 아까의 그 태도로 그것들을 그의 앞에 놓아 주었다."], ["Rigaud, after some villainous thinking and smiling, wrote, and read aloud, as follows:", "리고는 무언가 악랄한 생각에 잠겨 미소 짓더니, 다음과 같이 쓰고 소리 내어 읽었다. \"클레넘 부인께."]], [["‘Wait answer.", "답장을 기다림."]], [["‘Prison of the Marshalsea.", "마셜시 감옥에서."], ["‘At the apartment of your son.", "귀하의 아드님의 거처에서."]], [["‘Dear Madam,", "친애하는 부인,"]], [["‘I am in despair to be informed to-day by our prisoner here (who has had the goodness to employ spies to seek me, living for politic reasons in retirement), that you have had fears for my safety.", "오늘 이곳의 우리 죄수(정략상의 이유로 은거하며 사는 저를 찾아내고자 친히 정탐꾼들을 부린 분이지요)에게서, 부인께서 제 안위를 염려하셨다는 소식을 전해 듣고 저는 절망에 빠졌습니다."]], [["‘Reassure yourself, dear madam.", "안심하소서, 친애하는 부인."], ["I am well, I am strong and constant.", "저는 잘 있으며, 강건하고 한결같습니다."]], [["‘With the greatest impatience I should fly to your house, but that I foresee it to be possible, under the circumstances, that you will not yet have quite definitively arranged the little proposition I have had the honour to submit to you.", "저는 더없이 조바심을 내며 부인의 댁으로 날아가고 싶으나, 사정상 부인께서 제가 영광스럽게도 부인께 제출한 그 자그마한 제안을 아직 완전히 확정적으로 정리하지 못하셨을 수도 있음을 예견합니다."], ["I name one week from this day, for a last final visit on my part; when you will unconditionally accept it or reject it, with its train of consequences.", "이날로부터 일주일 뒤를, 저로서는 마지막 최종 방문일로 정하겠습니다. 그때 부인께서는 그 제안을, 그에 따른 일련의 결과와 함께, 조건 없이 받아들이시거나 거절하시면 됩니다."]], [["‘I suppress my ardour to embrace you and achieve this interesting business, in order that you may have leisure to adjust its details to our perfect mutual satisfaction.", "부인을 포옹하고 이 흥미로운 일을 마무리하고픈 제 열정을 억누르는 것은, 부인께서 그 세부 사항을 우리 양쪽의 완벽한 만족에 맞도록 조정하실 여유를 가지시도록 하기 위함입니다."]], [["‘In the meanwhile, it is not too much to propose (our prisoner having deranged my housekeeping), that my expenses of lodging and nourishment at an hotel shall be paid by you.", "그동안, (우리 죄수가 제 살림을 어지럽혀 놓았으니) 호텔에서의 제 숙박비와 식대를 부인께서 지불하시기를 제안하는 것은 지나친 일이 아닐 것입니다."]], [["‘Receive, dear madam, the assurance of my highest and most distinguished consideration,", "받아 주소서, 친애하는 부인, 저의 가장 높고 가장 각별한 경의의 표시를. 리고 블랑두아."]], [["‘A thousand friendships to that dear Flintwinch.", "저 사랑하는 플린트윈치에게 천 번의 우정을."]], [["‘I kiss the hands of Madame F.’", "F 부인의 두 손에 입맞춤을.\""]], [["When he had finished this epistle, Rigaud folded it and tossed it with a flourish at Clennam’s feet.", "이 서한을 다 쓴 뒤, 리고는 그것을 접어 과장된 몸짓으로 클레넘의 발치에 던졌다. \"이봐, 자네!"], ["‘Hola you! Apropos of producing, let somebody produce that at its address, and produce the answer here.’", "내세운다는 이야기가 나온 김에, 누군가 저것을 받는 이의 주소로 내세우고, 그 답장을 여기로 가져오게 하게.\""]], [["‘Cavalletto,’ said Arthur.", "\"카발레토,\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘Will you take this fellow’s letter?’", "\"이자의 편지를 가져가 주겠나?\""]], [["But, Cavalletto’s significant finger again expressing that his post was at the door to keep watch over Rigaud, now he had found him with so much trouble, and that the duty of his post was to sit on the floor backed up by the door, looking at Rigaud and holding his own ankles,--Signor Panco once more volunteered.", "그러나 카발레토의 의미심장한 손가락은, 이토록 애써서 리고를 찾아낸 마당에 자신의 자리는 그를 감시하는 문간이며, 또 그 자리의 임무란 문에 등을 대고 바닥에 앉아 리고를 바라보며 제 발목을 쥐고 있는 것이라고 다시금 표현했다. 그리하여 시뇨르 판코가 또 한 번 자청해 나섰다."], ["His services being accepted, Cavalletto suffered the door to open barely wide enough to admit of his squeezing himself out, and immediately shut it on him.", "그의 봉사가 받아들여지자, 카발레토는 문을 자신이 비집고 나갈 만큼만 빠끔히 열렸다가, 곧장 그의 뒤에서 닫히도록 했다."]], [["‘Touch me with a finger, touch me with an epithet, question my superiority as I sit here drinking my wine at my pleasure,’ said Rigaud, ‘and I follow the letter and cancel my week’s grace.", "\"내가 여기 앉아 마음껏 포도주를 마시는데, 손가락 하나라도 나를 건드리거나, 욕설 한마디라도 나를 건드리거나, 내 우월함에 의문을 제기해 봐,\" 리고가 말했다, \"그러면 나는 편지대로 따라 일주일의 유예를 취소해 버리겠어."], ["_You_ wanted me?", "자네가 나를 원했지?"], ["You have got me!", "자네는 나를 손에 넣었어!"], ["How do you like me?’", "내가 마음에 드나?\""]], [["‘You know,’ returned Clennam, with a bitter sense of his helplessness, ‘that when I sought you, I was not a prisoner.’", "\"당신도 알겠지만,\" 클레넘이 자신의 무력함에 쓰라림을 느끼며 응수했다, \"내가 당신을 찾으려 했을 때, 나는 죄수가 아니었소.\""]], [["‘To the Devil with you and your prison,’ retorted Rigaud, leisurely, as he took from his pocket a case containing the materials for making cigarettes, and employed his facile hands in folding a few for present use; ‘I care for neither of you.", "\"자네도 자네 감옥도 악마에게나 줘 버려.\" 리고가 느긋하게 되받으며, 주머니에서 궐련 마는 재료가 든 갑을 꺼내, 그 날렵한 손으로 당장 쓸 몇 개를 마는 데 몰두했다."], ["Contrabandist!", "\"나는 자네도 자네 감옥도 아랑곳없어. 밀수꾼!"], ["A light.’", "불을.\""]], [["Again Cavalletto got up, and gave him what he wanted.", "다시 카발레토가 일어나 그가 원하는 것을 주었다."], ["There had been something dreadful in the noiseless skill of his cold, white hands, with the fingers lithely twisting about and twining one over another like serpents.", "그 차갑고 흰 손이 소리 없이 놀리는 솜씨에는 무언가 끔찍한 것이 있었으니, 손가락들이 유연하게 비틀거리며 서로 휘감기는 모습이 마치 뱀들 같았다."], ["Clennam could not prevent himself from shuddering inwardly, as if he had been looking on at a nest of those creatures.", "클레넘은 마치 그 생물들의 둥지를 들여다보고 있기라도 한 듯, 속으로 진저리치는 것을 막을 수가 없었다."]], [["‘Hola, Pig!’ cried Rigaud, with a noisy stimulating cry, as if Cavalletto were an Italian horse or mule.", "\"이봐, 돼지!\" 리고가, 카발레토가 마치 이탈리아 말이나 노새이기라도 한 듯, 요란하게 부추기는 소리로 외쳤다."], ["‘What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["The infernal old jail was a respectable one to this.", "그 지옥 같은 옛 감옥이 이곳에 비하면 점잖은 곳이었어."], ["There was dignity in the bars and stones of that place.", "그곳의 쇠창살과 돌에는 위엄이 있었지."], ["It was a prison for men.", "그건 사내들을 위한 감옥이었어."], ["But this?", "그런데 이건?"], ["Bah!", "바!"], ["A hospital for imbeciles!’", "멍청이들을 위한 병원이로군!\""]], [["He smoked his cigarette out, with his ugly smile so fixed upon his face that he looked as though he were smoking with his drooping beak of a nose, rather than with his mouth; like a fancy in a weird picture.", "그는 궐련을 끝까지 피웠는데, 그 추악한 미소가 얼굴에 어찌나 고정되어 있던지, 마치 입이 아니라 축 늘어진 부리 같은 코로 담배를 피우는 것처럼 보였으니, 기이한 그림 속의 환상 같았다."], ["When he had lighted a second cigarette at the still burning end of the first, he said to Clennam:", "첫 번째 궐련의 아직 타고 있는 끝에 두 번째 궐련을 붙여 불을 댕긴 그는 클레넘에게 말했다."]], [["‘One must pass the time in the madman’s absence.", "\"미치광이가 없는 동안 시간을 보내야지."], ["One must talk.", "이야기를 해야겠어."], ["One can’t drink strong wine all day long, or I would have another bottle. She’s handsome, sir.", "독한 포도주를 하루 종일 마실 수는 없는 노릇이거든, 안 그러면 한 병 더 청했을 텐데."], ["Though not exactly to my taste, still, by the Thunder and the Lightning!", "그 여자는 미인이야, 양반. 딱 내 취향은 아니지만, 그래도, 천둥과 번개를 걸고!"], ["handsome.", "미인이지."], ["I felicitate you on your admiration.’", "자네의 그 감탄에 축하를 보내네.\""]], [["‘I neither know nor ask,’ said Clennam, ‘of whom you speak.’", "\"누구를 말하는지 나는 알지도 못하고, 묻지도 않겠소.\" 클레넘이 말했다."]], [["‘Della bella Gowana, sir, as they say in Italy.", "\"이탈리아에서들 말하듯, 델라 벨라 가우아나 말이야, 양반."], ["Of the Gowan, the fair Gowan.’", "가우언, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인.\""]], [["‘Of whose husband you were the--follower, I think?’", "\"내 생각엔, 당신이 그 부인 남편의—추종자였던 모양인데?\""]], [["‘Sir? Follower?", "\"양반?"], ["You are insolent.", "추종자라? 무례하군."], ["The friend.’", "친구였지.\""]], [["‘Do you sell all your friends?’", "\"당신은 친구를 죄다 팔아 치우오?\""]], [["Rigaud took his cigarette from his mouth, and eyed him with a momentary revelation of surprise.", "리고는 입에서 궐련을 떼고, 순간 놀라움을 드러내며 그를 쳐다보았다."], ["But he put it between his lips again, as he answered with coolness:", "그러나 그는 다시 그것을 입술 사이에 물고, 냉정하게 대답했다."]], [["‘I sell anything that commands a price.", "\"나는 값이 나가는 것이라면 무엇이든 팔지."], ["How do your lawyers live, your politicians, your intriguers, your men of the Exchange?", "자네네 변호사들은, 정치가들은, 모사꾼들은, 증권가의 인간들은 어떻게 먹고사나?"], ["How do you live?", "자네는 어떻게 먹고사나?"], ["How do you come here?", "자네는 어떻게 여기 왔나?"], ["Have you sold no friend?", "자네는 친구를 한 번도 팔지 않았나?"], ["Lady of mine!", "내가 모실 마님이여!"], ["I rather think, yes!’", "내 생각엔, 팔았을걸!\""]], [["Clennam turned away from him towards the window, and sat looking out at the wall.", "클레넘은 그에게서 등을 돌려 창문 쪽으로, 벽을 내다보며 앉았다."]], [["‘Effectively, sir,’ said Rigaud, ‘Society sells itself and sells me: and I sell Society.", "\"실로, 양반,\" 리고가 말했다, \"사회는 자신을 팔고 또 나를 파네."], ["I perceive you have acquaintance with another lady.", "그리고 나는 사회를 팔지. 자네가 또 다른 부인과 아는 사이임을 알겠군."], ["Also handsome.", "그 여자도 미인이야."], ["A strong spirit.", "강한 정신의 소유자고."], ["Let us see.", "어디 보자."], ["How do they call her?", "그 이름을 뭐라 부르더라?"], ["Wade.’", "웨이드.\""]], [["He received no answer, but could easily discern that he had hit the mark.", "그는 아무 대답도 듣지 못했지만, 자신이 정곡을 찔렀음을 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["‘Yes,’ he went on, ‘that handsome lady and strong spirit addresses me in the street, and I am not insensible.", "\"그래,\" 그가 말을 이었다, \"그 아름답고 강한 정신의 부인이 길거리에서 내게 말을 걸더군, 그리고 나는 무심하지 않았지."], ["I respond.", "나는 응답했어."], ["That handsome lady and strong spirit does me the favour to remark, in full confidence, “I have my curiosity, and I have my chagrins.", "그 아름답고 강한 정신의 부인이 호의를 베풀어, 아주 신뢰하는 투로 이렇게 말하더군. '나는 호기심이 있고, 또 분한 일들이 있어요."], ["You are not more than ordinarily honourable, perhaps?”", "당신은 어쩌면 보통 사람들보다 더 명예롭지는 않은 분이겠지요?'"], ["I announce myself, “Madame, a gentleman from the birth, and a gentleman to the death; but _not_ more than ordinarily honourable.", "나는 나를 이렇게 밝혔지. '부인, 저는 나면서부터 신사요, 죽을 때까지 신사이나, 보통 사람들보다 더 명예롭지는 않습니다."], ["I despise such a weak fantasy.”", "저는 그런 나약한 환상을 경멸하지요.'"], ["Thereupon she is pleased to compliment.", "그러자 그 부인이 기꺼이 칭찬을 하더군."], ["“The difference between you and the rest is,”", "'당신과 나머지 사람들의 차이는,'"], ["she answers, “that you say so.” For she knows Society.", "그녀가 대답하길, '당신은 그렇다고 말한다는 점이에요.'"], ["I accept her congratulations with gallantry and politeness.", "그녀는 사회를 아니까. 나는 그녀의 찬사를 정중하고 예의 바르게 받아들였지."], ["Politeness and little gallantries are inseparable from my character.", "예의와 자그마한 정중함은 내 성품과 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 것이거든. 그러더니 그녀가 한 가지 제안을 하더군."], ["She then makes a proposition, which is, in effect, that she has seen us much together; that it appears to her that I am for the passing time the cat of the house, the friend of the family; that her curiosity and her chagrins awaken the fancy to be acquainted with their movements, to know the manner of their life, how the fair Gowana is beloved, how the fair Gowana is cherished, and so on.", "요컨대, 그녀가 우리를 자주 함께 있는 걸 보았고, 자기 보기에 내가 그 무렵 그 집의 고양이, 그 가족의 친구인 것 같다는 거였어. 자기의 호기심과 분한 일들이, 그들의 동정을 알고 싶고, 그들의 생활 방식을, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인이 어떻게 사랑받는지, 그 아름다운 가우언 부인이 어떻게 아낌받는지, 등등을 알고 싶은 마음을 일으킨다는 거였지."], ["She is not rich, but offers such and such little recompenses for the little cares and derangements of such services; and I graciously--to do everything graciously is a part of my character--consent to accept them.", "그녀는 부유하지는 않지만, 그런 수고와 번거로움의 자그마한 봉사에 이러이러한 자그마한 보답을 하겠노라 제안했어. 그래서 나는 너그럽게—모든 일을 너그럽게 하는 것이 내 성품의 일부거든—그것을 받아들이기로 했지."], ["O yes!", "오, 그래!"], ["So goes the world.", "세상이란 그렇게 돌아가는 거야."], ["It is the mode.’", "그게 유행이지.\""]], [["Though Clennam’s back was turned while he spoke, and thenceforth to the end of the interview, he kept those glittering eyes of his that were too near together, upon him, and evidently saw in the very carriage of the head, as he passed with his braggart recklessness from clause to clause of what he said, that he was saying nothing which Clennam did not already know.", "그가 말하는 동안 클레넘은 등을 돌리고 있었고, 그 후로도 면담이 끝날 때까지 그러했지만, 리고는 너무 가까이 붙어 있는 그 번득이는 두 눈으로 그를 계속 지켜보았으며, 자신이 허풍스러운 무모함으로 한 구절 한 구절 늘어놓는 동안, 바로 그 고개를 든 자세에서, 자신이 클레넘이 이미 알지 못하는 것이라곤 아무것도 말하지 않고 있음을 분명히 알아챘다."]], [["‘Whoof!", "\"훅!"], ["The fair Gowana!’ he said, lighting a third cigarette with a sound as if his lightest breath could blow her away.", "아름다운 가우언 부인!\" 그는 마치 자신의 가장 가벼운 숨결로도 그녀를 날려 버릴 수 있다는 듯한 소리를 내며 세 번째 궐련에 불을 붙이고 말했다."], ["‘Charming, but imprudent!", "\"매혹적이지만, 경솔해!"], ["For it was not well of the fair Gowana to make mysteries of letters from old lovers, in her bedchamber on the mountain, that her husband might not see them.", "그 아름다운 가우언 부인이, 산 위의 침실에서, 남편이 못 보도록 옛 연인들에게서 온 편지들을 비밀로 한 것은 잘한 일이 아니었거든. 아니, 아니."], ["No, no. That was not well.", "그건 잘한 일이 아니었어."], ["Whoof!", "훅!"], ["The Gowana was mistaken there.’", "가우언 부인이 그 점에서 실수한 거지.\""]], [["‘I earnestly hope,’ cried Arthur aloud, ‘that Pancks may not be long gone, for this man’s presence pollutes the room.’", "\"진심으로 바라건대,\" 아서가 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"팽크스가 오래 걸리지 않았으면 좋겠소. 이자의 존재가 이 방을 더럽히고 있으니.\""]], [["‘Ah!", "\"아!"], ["But he’ll flourish here, and everywhere,’ said Rigaud, with an exulting look and snap of his fingers.", "하지만 나는 여기서도, 어디서든 번성할 거야.\" 리고가 의기양양한 표정으로 손가락을 튕기며 말했다."], ["‘He always has; he always will!’ Stretching his body out on the only three chairs in the room besides that on which Clennam sat, he sang, smiting himself on the breast as the gallant personage of the song.", "\"나는 늘 그래 왔고, 늘 그럴 거야!\" 클레넘이 앉은 의자 말고 방에 있는 단 세 개의 의자에 몸을 쭉 뻗고 누운 그는, 노래 속 늠름한 인물처럼 제 가슴을 치며 노래했다."]], [["‘Who passes by this road so late?", "\"이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Compagnon de la Majolaine!", "마졸렌의 동무여!"], ["Who passes by this road so late?", "이토록 늦은 시각에 이 길을 지나는 이는 누구인가?"], ["Always gay!", "언제나 즐겁다네!"]], [["‘Sing the Refrain, pig!", "후렴을 불러라, 돼지야!"], ["You could sing it once, in another jail.", "너는 다른 감옥에서 이걸 한 번 부른 적이 있지."], ["Sing it!", "불러!"], ["Or, by every Saint who was stoned to death, I’ll be affronted and compromising; and then some people who are not dead yet, had better have been stoned along with them!’", "안 그러면, 돌에 맞아 죽은 모든 성인을 걸고, 나는 모욕을 느끼고 곤란하게 굴 테다. 그러면 아직 죽지 않은 어떤 사람들은, 차라리 그 성인들과 함께 돌에 맞아 죽는 편이 나았을 거야!\""]], [["‘Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Compagnon de la Majolaine!", "\"임금의 모든 기사 중 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 마졸렌의 동무여!"], ["Of all the king’s knights ‘tis the flower, Always gay!’", "임금의 모든 기사 중 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 언제나 즐겁다네!\""]], [["Partly in his old habit of submission, partly because his not doing it might injure his benefactor, and partly because he would as soon do it as anything else, Cavalletto took up the Refrain this time.", "한편으로는 옛 복종의 습관 때문에, 한편으로는 그러지 않으면 자신의 은인에게 해가 될까 봐, 또 한편으로는 다른 무엇을 하느니 차라리 그러는 게 낫겠다는 마음에서, 카발레토는 이번에는 후렴을 받아 불렀다."], ["Rigaud laughed, and fell to smoking with his eyes shut.", "리고는 웃더니, 눈을 감고 담배를 피우기 시작했다."]], [["Possibly another quarter of an hour elapsed before Mr Pancks’s step was heard upon the stairs, but the interval seemed to Clennam insupportably long.", "팽크스 씨의 발소리가 계단에서 들리기까지 아마도 또 한 십오 분쯤 흘렀을 테지만, 그 시간은 클레넘에게 견딜 수 없이 길게 느껴졌다."], ["His step was attended by another step; and when Cavalletto opened the door, he admitted Mr Pancks and Mr Flintwinch.", "그의 발소리에는 또 다른 발소리가 함께했고, 카발레토가 문을 열자, 그는 팽크스 씨와 플린트윈치 씨를 들여보냈다."], ["The latter was no sooner visible, than Rigaud rushed at him and embraced him boisterously.", "후자가 모습을 보이기 무섭게, 리고가 그에게 달려들어 요란하게 그를 끌어안았다."]], [["‘How do you find yourself, sir?’ said Mr Flintwinch, as soon as he could disengage himself, which he struggled to do with very little ceremony.", "\"좀 어떠신가, 선생?\""], ["‘Thank you, no; I don’t want any more.’ This was in reference to another menace of attention from his recovered friend.", "플린트윈치 씨가, 거의 격식이라곤 없이 안간힘을 써서 몸을 떼어 낼 수 있게 되자마자 말했다. \"고맙지만, 됐네."], ["‘Well, Arthur.", "더는 필요 없어.\""], ["You remember what I said to you about sleeping dogs and missing ones.", "이는 되찾은 친구가 또 한 번 애정 공세를 위협한 데 대한 말이었다. \"그래, 아서."], ["It’s come true, you see.’", "잠자는 개와 행방불명된 개에 대해 자네에게 했던 말 기억하나? 보다시피, 그게 사실로 드러났네.\""]], [["He was as imperturbable as ever, to all appearance, and nodded his head in a moralising way as he looked round the room.", "그는 겉보기엔 여전히 평소처럼 태연했으며, 방 안을 둘러보면서 훈계조로 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["‘And this is the Marshalsea prison for debt!’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"그리고 이것이 채무자를 가두는 마셜시 감옥이라는 거지!\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Hah!", "\"허!"], ["you have brought your pigs to a very indifferent market, Arthur.’", "자네는 돼지들을 영 시원찮은 시장에 끌고 왔구먼, 아서.\""]], [["If Arthur had patience, Rigaud had not.", "아서에게는 인내심이 있었으나, 리고에게는 없었다."], ["He took his little Flintwinch, with fierce playfulness, by the two lapels of his coat, and cried:", "그는 사납고 장난스럽게 자신의 작은 플린트윈치를 외투의 두 옷깃을 잡고 외쳤다."]], [["‘To the Devil with the Market, to the Devil with the Pigs, and to the Devil with the Pig-Driver!", "\"시장 따위는 악마에게나 줘 버리고, 돼지들도 악마에게나 줘 버리고, 돼지치기도 악마에게나 줘 버려라!"], ["Now!", "자!"], ["Give me the answer to my letter.’", "내 편지에 대한 답을 다오.\""]], [["‘If you can make it convenient to let go a moment, sir,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, ‘I’ll first hand Mr Arthur a little note that I have for him.’", "\"잠깐만 놓아주시는 게 편하시다면, 선생,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 응수했다, \"먼저 아서 씨에게 전할 짧은 쪽지를 건네야겠습니다.\""]], [["He did so.", "그는 그렇게 했다."], ["It was in his mother’s maimed writing, on a slip of paper, and contained only these words:", "그것은 그의 어머니의 불구가 된 필체로, 종잇조각에 적혀 있었고, 다음의 말만이 담겨 있었다."]], [["‘I hope it is enough that you have ruined yourself.", "\"네가 네 자신을 파멸시킨 것으로 족하기를 바란다."], ["Rest contented without more ruin.", "더 이상의 파멸 없이 만족하며 지내라."], ["Jeremiah Flintwinch is my messenger and representative.", "제레마이어 플린트윈치가 나의 전령이자 대리인이다."], ["Your affectionate M.", "너를 사랑하는 어미, M."], ["C.’", "C.\""]], [["Clennam read this twice, in silence, and then tore it to pieces.", "클레넘은 이것을 잠자코 두 번 읽고는, 갈가리 찢어 버렸다."], ["Rigaud in the meanwhile stepped into a chair, and sat himself on the back with his feet upon the seat.", "그동안 리고는 의자 위로 올라서서, 발을 좌석에 올린 채 등받이에 걸터앉았다."]], [["‘Now, Beau Flintwinch,’ he said, when he had closely watched the note to its destruction, ‘the answer to my letter?’", "\"자, 멋쟁이 플린트윈치,\" 그가 그 쪽지가 결딴나는 것까지 유심히 지켜본 뒤 말했다, \"내 편지에 대한 답은?\""]], [["‘Mrs Clennam did not write, Mr Blandois, her hands being cramped, and she thinking it as well to send it verbally by me.’ Mr Flintwinch screwed this out of himself, unwillingly and rustily.", "\"클레넘 부인께서는 손이 경련을 일으켜 쓰지 못하셨고, 또 저를 통해 구두로 전하는 편이 낫겠다 여기셨습니다, 블랑두아 씨.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 마지못해, 녹슨 듯 뻑뻑하게 이 말을 쥐어짜 냈다."], ["‘She sends her compliments, and says she doesn’t on the whole wish to term you unreasonable, and that she agrees.", "\"부인께서 안부를 전하시며, 대체로 당신을 분별없는 자라 칭하고 싶지는 않으며, 동의하신다고 하셨습니다."], ["But without prejudicing the appointment that stands for this day week.’", "다만 이날로부터 일주일 뒤로 잡힌 약속에는 손상이 없도록 하면서요.\""]], [["Monsieur Rigaud, after indulging in a fit of laughter, descended from his throne, saying, ‘Good!", "리고 씨는 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨린 뒤, 자신의 옥좌에서 내려오며 말했다. \"좋아!"], ["I go to seek an hotel!’ But, there his eyes encountered Cavalletto, who was still at his post.", "호텔을 찾으러 가야겠군!\" 그런데 그때 그의 눈이 여전히 제 자리를 지키고 있는 카발레토와 마주쳤다."]], [["‘Come, Pig,’ he added, ‘I have had you for a follower against my will; now, I’ll have you against yours.", "\"이리 와, 돼지.\" 그가 덧붙였다."], ["I tell you, my little reptiles, I am born to be served.", "\"나는 내 뜻과 다르게 너를 추종자로 두었었지. 이번엔 네 뜻과 다르게 너를 두겠다."], ["I demand the service of this contrabandist as my domestic until this day week.’", "내 작은 파충류들아, 일러두는데, 나는 시중받으려고 태어난 사람이야. 나는 이 밀수꾼이 이날로부터 일주일 뒤까지 내 하인 노릇을 할 것을 요구한다.\""]], [["In answer to Cavalletto’s look of inquiry, Clennam made him a sign to go; but he added aloud, ‘unless you are afraid of him.’ Cavalletto replied with a very emphatic finger-negative.’No, master, I am not afraid of him, when I no more keep it secrettementally that he was once my comrade.’ Rigaud took no notice of either remark until he had lighted his last cigarette and was quite ready for walking.", "카발레토의 묻는 듯한 눈길에 답하여, 클레넘은 그에게 가라는 신호를 보냈다. 그러나 그는 소리 내어 덧붙였다. \"자네가 그를 두려워하는 게 아니라면 말일세.\" 카발레토는 손가락으로 매우 단호하게 아니라는 뜻을 표하며 대답했다. \"아니요, 주인님, 저는 그가 두렵지 않습니다. 그가 한때 제 동료였음을 더 이상 비밀로 간직하지 않게 되었으니까요.\" 리고는 마지막 궐련에 불을 붙이고 걸을 채비를 완전히 갖출 때까지는 어느 쪽의 말에도 아랑곳하지 않았다."]], [["‘Afraid of him,’ he said then, looking round upon them all.", "\"그가 두렵다고,\" 그가 그제야 그들 모두를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["‘Whoof!", "\"훅!"], ["My children, my babies, my little dolls, you are all afraid of him.", "내 아이들아, 내 갓난쟁이들아, 내 작은 인형들아, 너희는 모두 그를 두려워하지."], ["You give him his bottle of wine here; you give him meat, drink, and lodging there; you dare not touch him with a finger or an epithet. No.", "너희는 여기서 그에게 포도주 한 병을 주고, 저기서 그에게 먹을 것과 마실 것과 잠자리를 주고, 손가락 하나로도 욕설 한마디로도 감히 그를 건드리지 못해."], ["It is his character to triumph!", "그래. 승리하는 것이 그의 성품이거든!"], ["Whoof!", "훅!"]], [["‘Of all the king’s knights he’s the flower, And he’s always gay!’", "\"임금의 모든 기사 중 그가 으뜸가는 꽃이라네, 그리고 그는 언제나 즐겁다네!\""]], [["With this adaptation of the Refrain to himself, he stalked out of the room closely followed by Cavalletto, whom perhaps he had pressed into his service because he tolerably well knew it would not be easy to get rid of him.", "이렇게 그 후렴을 자신에게 맞추어 고쳐 부르며, 그는 카발레토를 바짝 뒤에 거느리고 거들먹거리며 방을 나섰으니, 어쩌면 그를 떼어 내기가 쉽지 않으리란 걸 제법 잘 알았기에 그를 자기 시중에 끌어들인 것이었다."], ["Mr Flintwinch, after scraping his chin, and looking about with caustic disparagement of the Pig-Market, nodded to Arthur, and followed.", "플린트윈치 씨는 턱을 긁적이고는, 돼지 시장에 대한 신랄한 멸시를 담아 주위를 둘러본 뒤, 아서에게 고개를 끄덕이고 따라 나섰다."], ["Mr Pancks, still penitent and depressed, followed too; after receiving with great attention a secret word or two of instructions from Arthur, and whispering back that he would see this affair out, and stand by it to the end.", "여전히 뉘우치며 풀이 죽어 있던 팽크스 씨도, 아서에게서 은밀한 지시 한두 마디를 매우 주의 깊게 받고, 이 일을 끝까지 지켜보며 끝까지 함께하겠노라 속삭여 답한 뒤, 따라 나섰다."], ["The prisoner, with the feeling that he was more despised, more scorned and repudiated, more helpless, altogether more miserable and fallen than before, was left alone again.", "죄수는, 전보다 더 멸시받고, 더 경멸받고 배척당하며, 더 무력하고, 통틀어 더 비참하고 추락한 신세가 되었다는 느낌과 함께, 또다시 홀로 남겨졌다."]], [["# CHAPTER 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea", "# CHAPTER 29. A Plea in the Marshalsea"]], [["Haggard anxiety and remorse are bad companions to be barred up with.", "초췌한 근심과 회한은 함께 갇혀 지내기에 나쁜 동무다."], ["Brooding all day, and resting very little indeed at night, will not arm a man against misery.", "온종일 골똘히 시름에 잠기고 밤에는 거의 쉬지 못하는 것으로는 사람을 비참함에 맞서도록 무장시켜 주지 못한다."], ["Next morning, Clennam felt that his health was sinking, as his spirits had already sunk and that the weight under which he bent was bearing him down.", "이튿날 아침, 클레넘은 자신의 건강이 가라앉고 있음을, 이미 기력이 가라앉아 버린 것처럼, 그리고 그가 짓눌려 굽어 있는 그 무게가 자신을 짓이겨 내리누르고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["Night after night he had risen from his bed of wretchedness at twelve or one o’clock, and had sat at his window watching the sickly lamps in the yard, and looking upward for the first wan trace of day, hours before it was possible that the sky could show it to him.", "밤마다 그는 비참한 잠자리에서 열두 시나 한 시쯤에 일어나, 창가에 앉아 마당의 병약한 등불을 지켜보며, 하늘이 그에게 그것을 보여 줄 수 있기까지 아직 여러 시간이나 남았는데도 위를 올려다보며 동트는 첫 창백한 자취를 찾았다."], ["Now when the night came, he could not even persuade himself to undress.", "그런데 이제 밤이 오자, 그는 옷을 벗으라고 스스로를 설득할 수조차 없었다."]], [["For a burning restlessness set in, an agonised impatience of the prison, and a conviction that he was going to break his heart and die there, which caused him indescribable suffering.", "타는 듯한 안절부절못함이 찾아왔기 때문이다. 감옥에 대한 고통스러운 초조, 그리고 자신이 이곳에서 가슴이 미어져 죽고 말리라는 확신이 그에게 형언할 수 없는 괴로움을 안겼다."], ["His dread and hatred of the place became so intense that he felt it a labour to draw his breath in it.", "이곳에 대한 그의 두려움과 증오가 너무도 격렬해져서, 그는 이 안에서 숨을 들이쉬는 것조차 노역처럼 느껴졌다."], ["The sensation of being stifled sometimes so overpowered him, that he would stand at the window holding his throat and gasping.", "질식하는 듯한 감각이 때로는 그를 너무도 압도하여, 그는 목을 움켜쥐고 헐떡이며 창가에 서 있곤 했다."], ["At the same time a longing for other air, and a yearning to be beyond the blind blank wall, made him feel as if he must go mad with the ardour of the desire.", "동시에 다른 공기를 향한 갈망, 저 눈먼 텅 빈 담장 너머로 나가고 싶은 간절함이, 그 욕망의 격렬함에 자신이 미쳐 버릴 것만 같은 느낌이 들게 했다."]], [["Many other prisoners had had experience of this condition before him, and its violence and continuity had worn themselves out in their cases, as they did in his.", "그보다 앞서 다른 많은 죄수들도 이런 상태를 겪은 바 있었고, 그 격렬함과 끈질김은 그의 경우에서처럼 그들의 경우에서도 제풀에 닳아 없어졌다."], ["Two nights and a day exhausted it.", "이틀 밤과 하루가 그것을 소진시켰다."], ["It came back by fits, but those grew fainter and returned at lengthening intervals.", "발작처럼 되돌아오곤 했으나, 그 발작은 점점 희미해졌고 점점 긴 간격을 두고 돌아왔다."], ["A desolate calm succeeded; and the middle of the week found him settled down in the despondency of low, slow fever.", "황량한 고요가 뒤를 이었고, 그 주의 중반에 이르러서는 낮고 더딘 미열의 의기소침 속에 그가 가라앉아 있는 것이 보였다."]], [["With Cavalletto and Pancks away, he had no visitors to fear but Mr and Mrs Plornish.", "카발레토와 팽크스가 자리를 비웠으니, 그가 두려워할 방문객이라곤 플로니시 부부밖에 없었다."], ["His anxiety, in reference to that worthy pair, was that they should not come near him; for, in the morbid state of his nerves, he sought to be left alone, and spared the being seen so subdued and weak.", "그 선량한 부부에 관한 그의 근심은, 그들이 그에게 가까이 오지 않기를 바라는 것이었다. 병적인 신경 상태에서 그는 홀로 남겨지기를 바랐고, 이토록 풀이 죽고 약해진 모습을 보이는 일을 면하고 싶었던 것이다."], ["He wrote a note to Mrs Plornish representing himself as occupied with his affairs, and bound by the necessity of devoting himself to them, to remain for a time even without the pleasant interruption of a sight of her kind face.", "그는 플로니시 부인에게 쪽지를 써서, 자신이 일에 매여 있으며 거기에 몰두해야 할 필요에 묶여 있어, 당분간은 부인의 다정한 얼굴을 보는 그 즐거운 방해조차 없이 지내야 한다고 둘러댔다."], ["As to Young John, who looked in daily at a certain hour, when the turnkeys were relieved, to ask if he could do anything for him; he always made a pretence of being engaged in writing, and to answer cheerfully in the negative.", "매일 일정한 시각에, 간수들이 교대할 때면 들여다보며 무엇이든 해 드릴 일이 없느냐고 묻는 젊은 존에게는, 그는 늘 글을 쓰는 척하며 명랑하게 없다고 대답했다."], ["The subject of their only long conversation had never been revived between them.", "그들 사이에 단 한 번 길게 나눈 대화의 주제는 두 사람 사이에서 다시 들춰진 적이 없었다."], ["Through all these changes of unhappiness, however, it had never lost its hold on Clennam’s mind.", "그러나 이 모든 불행의 변천을 거치는 동안에도, 그 주제는 클레넘의 마음을 결코 놓아주지 않았다."]], [["The sixth day of the appointed week was a moist, hot, misty day.", "정해진 한 주의 엿새째 날은 축축하고 무덥고 안개 낀 날이었다."], ["It seemed as though the prison’s poverty, and shabbiness, and dirt, were growing in the sultry atmosphere.", "마치 감옥의 가난과 초라함과 더러움이 후텁지근한 대기 속에서 자라나는 것만 같았다."], ["With an aching head and a weary heart, Clennam had watched the miserable night out, listening to the fall of rain on the yard pavement, thinking of its softer fall upon the country earth.", "머리가 지끈거리고 마음이 지친 채로, 클레넘은 그 비참한 밤을 지새우며, 마당 포석 위로 떨어지는 빗소리를 듣고, 그것이 시골 땅 위로 더 부드럽게 내리는 모습을 생각했다."], ["A blurred circle of yellow haze had risen up in the sky in lieu of sun, and he had watched the patch it put upon his wall, like a bit of the prison’s raggedness.", "해 대신 누런 안개의 흐릿한 둥근 테두리가 하늘에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것이 자기 벽에 던지는 얼룩을, 마치 감옥의 누더기 한 조각 같은 그 얼룩을 지켜보았다."], ["He had heard the gates open; and the badly shod feet that waited outside shuffle in; and the sweeping, and pumping, and moving about, begin, which commenced the prison morning.", "그는 문이 열리는 소리를, 밖에서 기다리던 신발 시원찮게 신은 발들이 끌리며 들어오는 소리를, 그리고 쓸고 물 푸고 이리저리 움직이는 소리가 시작되는 것을 들었으니, 그것이 감옥의 아침을 여는 일이었다."], ["So ill and faint that he was obliged to rest many times in the process of getting himself washed, he had at length crept to his chair by the open window.", "너무도 아프고 기운이 없어 몸을 씻는 동안에도 여러 번 쉬어야 했던 그는, 마침내 열린 창가의 의자로 기어가듯 다가갔다."], ["In it he sat dozing, while the old woman who arranged his room went through her morning’s work.", "그 의자에 앉아 그는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸았고, 그의 방을 정돈하는 노파는 아침 일과를 해 나갔다."]], [["Light of head with want of sleep and want of food (his appetite, and even his sense of taste, having forsaken him), he had been two or three times conscious, in the night, of going astray.", "잠이 모자라고 음식이 모자라(식욕도, 미각조차도 그를 떠나 버렸다) 머리가 어질했던 그는, 밤중에 두세 번 정신이 흐려지는 것을 의식한 적이 있었다."], ["He had heard fragments of tunes and songs in the warm wind, which he knew had no existence.", "그는 따뜻한 바람 속에서 가락과 노래의 조각들을 들었는데, 그것이 실재하지 않는 것임을 그도 알고 있었다."], ["Now that he began to doze in exhaustion, he heard them again; and voices seemed to address him, and he answered, and started.", "이제 그가 기진맥진하여 졸기 시작하자, 그는 그것들을 다시 들었고, 여러 목소리가 그에게 말을 거는 듯하여, 그는 대답하다가 화들짝 깨곤 했다."]], [["Dozing and dreaming, without the power of reckoning time, so that a minute might have been an hour and an hour a minute, some abiding impression of a garden stole over him--a garden of flowers, with a damp warm wind gently stirring their scents.", "졸고 꿈꾸며, 시간을 가늠할 힘도 없이—그래서 일 분이 한 시간 같고 한 시간이 일 분 같았는데—어떤 정원의 머무는 인상이 그에게 슬며시 스며들었다. 축축하고 따뜻한 바람이 향기를 부드럽게 흔드는, 꽃들이 핀 정원이었다."], ["It required such a painful effort to lift his head for the purpose of inquiring into this, or inquiring into anything, that the impression appeared to have become quite an old and importunate one when he looked round.", "이것을 살펴보려, 혹은 무엇이든 살펴보려 머리를 드는 데에 너무나 고통스러운 노력이 필요해서, 그가 주위를 둘러볼 즈음에는 그 인상이 아주 오래되고 끈질긴 것이 되어 버린 듯했다."], ["Beside the tea-cup on his table he saw, then, a blooming nosegay: a wonderful handful of the choicest and most lovely flowers.", "그러자 탁자 위의 찻잔 옆에, 그는 활짝 핀 작은 꽃다발을 보았다. 가장 고르고 가장 어여쁜 꽃들을 놀랍도록 한 아름 모은 것이었다."]], [["Nothing had ever appeared so beautiful in his sight.", "그의 눈에 일찍이 그토록 아름다워 보인 것은 없었다."], ["He took them up and inhaled their fragrance, and he lifted them to his hot head, and he put them down and opened his parched hands to them, as cold hands are opened to receive the cheering of a fire.", "그는 그것을 집어 들어 향기를 들이마셨고, 그것을 자신의 뜨거운 머리로 들어 올렸으며, 그것을 내려놓고는 메마른 두 손을 그쪽으로 펼쳤으니, 마치 차가운 손을 불의 온기를 받으려 펼치는 것 같았다."], ["It was not until he had delighted in them for some time, that he wondered who had sent them; and opened his door to ask the woman who must have put them there, how they had come into her hands.", "한참을 그것들로 기뻐한 뒤에야 비로소 그는 누가 보냈을까 궁금해졌고, 분명 그것을 거기 두었을 그 노파에게 어떻게 그 꽃이 그녀의 손에 들어왔는지 물으려 문을 열었다."], ["But she was gone, and seemed to have been long gone; for the tea she had left for him on the table was cold.", "그러나 노파는 가고 없었고, 떠난 지 오래된 듯했다. 탁자 위에 그를 위해 남겨 둔 차가 식어 있었기 때문이다."], ["He tried to drink some, but could not bear the odour of it: so he crept back to his chair by the open window, and put the flowers on the little round table of old.", "그는 좀 마셔 보려 했으나 그 냄새를 견딜 수 없었다. 그래서 그는 다시 열린 창가의 의자로 기어가 돌아왔고, 꽃들을 예의 그 작고 둥근 탁자 위에 놓았다."]], [["When the first faintness consequent on having moved about had left him, he subsided into his former state.", "이리저리 움직인 데서 비롯된 처음의 어지러움이 가시자, 그는 다시 이전 상태로 가라앉았다."], ["One of the night-tunes was playing in the wind, when the door of his room seemed to open to a light touch, and, after a moment’s pause, a quiet figure seemed to stand there, with a black mantle on it.", "밤의 가락 중 하나가 바람 속에서 흐르고 있을 때, 그의 방문이 가벼운 손길에 열리는 듯했고, 한순간 멈칫한 뒤, 검은 망토를 두른 조용한 형체가 거기 서 있는 듯했다."], ["It seemed to draw the mantle off and drop it on the ground, and then it seemed to be his Little Dorrit in her old, worn dress.", "그 형체가 망토를 벗어 바닥에 떨어뜨리는 듯하더니, 그러자 그것은 낡고 해진 옷을 입은 그의 리틀 도릿인 듯했다."], ["It seemed to tremble, and to clasp its hands, and to smile, and to burst into tears.", "그 형체는 떨고 있는 듯, 두 손을 맞잡는 듯, 미소 짓는 듯, 그리고 와락 눈물을 터뜨리는 듯했다."]], [["He roused himself, and cried out.", "그는 정신을 차리고 소리쳤다. 그러자 그는 그 사랑스럽고, 가엾어하고, 슬퍼하는, 정다운 얼굴 속에서, 거울에서처럼, 자신이 얼마나 변해 버렸는지를 보았다."], ["And then he saw, in the loving, pitying, sorrowing, dear face, as in a mirror, how changed he was; and she came towards him; and with her hands laid on his breast to keep him in his chair, and with her knees upon the floor at his feet, and with her lips raised up to kiss him, and with her tears dropping on him as the rain from Heaven had dropped upon the flowers, Little Dorrit, a living presence, called him by his name.", "그리고 그녀가 그에게로 다가왔다. 그를 의자에 붙잡아 두려고 두 손을 그의 가슴에 얹은 채, 그의 발치 바닥에 무릎을 꿇은 채, 그에게 입맞추려 입술을 들어 올린 채, 그리고 마치 하늘에서 내린 비가 꽃들 위로 떨어졌듯 그녀의 눈물을 그에게 떨어뜨린 채, 리틀 도릿이, 살아 있는 실재가, 그의 이름을 불렀다."]], [["‘O, my best friend!", "\"오, 나의 가장 좋은 벗이여!"], ["Dear Mr Clennam, don’t let me see you weep!", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, 제가 당신이 우는 것을 보게 하지 마세요!"], ["Unless you weep with pleasure to see me.", "저를 보고 기뻐서 우시는 게 아니라면요."], ["I hope you do.", "그러시기를 바라요."], ["Your own poor child come back!’", "당신의 가엾은 아이가 돌아왔어요!\""]], [["So faithful, tender, and unspoiled by Fortune.", "그토록 한결같고, 다정하며, 운명에 의해 조금도 더럽혀지지 않았다."], ["In the sound of her voice, in the light of her eyes, in the touch of her hands, so Angelically comforting and true!", "그녀의 목소리에서, 그녀의 눈빛에서, 그녀의 손길에서, 그토록 천사처럼 위로가 되고 참되었다!"]], [["As he embraced her, she said to him, ‘They never told me you were ill,’ and drawing an arm softly round his neck, laid his head upon her bosom, put a hand upon his head, and resting her cheek upon that hand, nursed him as lovingly, and GOD knows as innocently, as she had nursed her father in that room when she had been but a baby, needing all the care from others that she took of them.", "그가 그녀를 끌어안자, 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"그분들이 당신이 아프시다는 걸 제게 전혀 말해 주지 않았어요.\" 그러고는 부드럽게 한 팔을 그의 목에 두르고, 그의 머리를 자기 가슴에 누이며, 한 손을 그의 머리에 얹고, 그 손에 자기 뺨을 기댄 채, 그녀가 아직 갓난아기에 불과해, 자신이 남들에게 베푸는 그 모든 보살핌을 도리어 남들로부터 받아야 했던 시절에 바로 그 방에서 자기 아버지를 돌보았던 것처럼, 그렇게 사랑스럽게, 그리고 하느님은 아시거니와 그렇게 천진하게, 그를 간호했다."]], [["When he could speak, he said, ‘Is it possible that you have come to me?", "말을 할 수 있게 되자, 그가 말했다. \"당신이 내게로 오다니, 이게 가능한 일이오?"], ["And in this dress?’", "게다가 이 옷차림으로?\""]], [["‘I hoped you would like me better in this dress than any other.", "\"당신이 이 옷을 입은 저를 다른 어떤 차림보다 더 좋아하시길 바랐어요."], ["I have always kept it by me, to remind me: though I wanted no reminding.", "저는 늘 이 옷을 곁에 두어 저를 일깨우게 했어요. 일깨워질 필요는 없었지만요."], ["I am not alone, you see.", "보세요, 저는 혼자가 아니에요."], ["I have brought an old friend with me.’", "옛 벗을 한 사람 데려왔답니다.\""]], [["Looking round, he saw Maggy in her big cap which had been long abandoned, with a basket on her arm as in the bygone days, chuckling rapturously.", "둘러보니, 그는 오래전에 버려졌던 그 큰 모자를 쓰고, 지난날처럼 팔에 바구니를 건 매기가 황홀하게 키득거리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["‘It was only yesterday evening that I came to London with my brother.", "\"바로 어제저녁에야 오빠와 함께 런던에 왔어요."], ["I sent round to Mrs Plornish almost as soon as we arrived, that I might hear of you and let you know I had come.", "도착하자마자 거의 곧장 플로니시 부인에게 사람을 보내, 당신 소식을 듣고 제가 왔다는 것을 알리려 했어요."], ["Then I heard that you were here.", "그래서 당신이 여기 계시다는 걸 들었어요."], ["Did you happen to think of me in the night?", "혹시 밤에 저를 생각하셨나요?"], ["I almost believe you must have thought of me a little.", "당신이 저를 조금은 생각하셨으리라 거의 믿어요."], ["I thought of you so anxiously, and it appeared so long to morning.’", "저는 당신을 너무도 애타게 생각했고, 아침이 오기까지가 어찌나 길게 느껴지던지요.\""]], [["‘I have thought of you--’ he hesitated what to call her.", "\"나는 당신을 생각했소—\" 그는 그녀를 무어라 불러야 할지 망설였다."], ["She perceived it in an instant.", "그녀는 그것을 단번에 알아챘다."]], [["‘You have not spoken to me by my right name yet.", "\"당신은 아직 저를 제 본래 이름으로 부르지 않으셨어요."], ["You know what my right name always is with you.’", "당신에게 제 본래 이름이 늘 무엇인지 아시잖아요.\""]], [["‘I have thought of you, Little Dorrit, every day, every hour, every minute, since I have been here.’", "\"나는 당신을 생각했소, 리틀 도릿, 여기 온 뒤로 매일, 매시간, 매분.\""]], [["‘Have you?", "\"정말요?"], ["Have you?’", "정말이세요?\""]], [["He saw the bright delight of her face, and the flush that kindled in it, with a feeling of shame.", "그는 그녀 얼굴의 환한 기쁨을, 거기 타오르는 홍조를, 수치심을 느끼며 보았다."], ["He, a broken, bankrupt, sick, dishonoured prisoner.", "그는 망가지고, 파산하고, 병들고, 명예를 잃은 죄수였다."]], [["‘I was here before the gates were opened, but I was afraid to come straight to you.", "\"문이 열리기 전부터 여기 와 있었지만, 곧장 당신께 오기가 두려웠어요."], ["I should have done you more harm than good, at first; for the prison was so familiar and yet so strange, and it brought back so many remembrances of my poor father, and of you too, that at first it overpowered me.", "처음엔 제가 당신께 득보다 해를 끼쳤을 거예요. 이 감옥이 너무나 익숙하면서도 너무나 낯설었고, 가엾은 제 아버지에 대한, 그리고 당신에 대한 수많은 기억을 되살려서, 처음엔 그것이 저를 압도했거든요."], ["But we went to Mr Chivery before we came to the gate, and he brought us in, and got John’s room for us--my poor old room, you know--and we waited there a little.", "그래서 우리는 문에 오기 전에 치버리 씨께 갔고, 그분이 우리를 안으로 들여 존의 방을 내주셨어요—저의 그 가엾은 옛 방 말이에요, 아시잖아요—그래서 거기서 잠시 기다렸지요."], ["I brought the flowers to the door, but you didn’t hear me.’", "제가 꽃을 문 앞에 가져다 두었지만, 당신은 제 소리를 못 들으셨어요.\""]], [["She looked something more womanly than when she had gone away, and the ripening touch of the Italian sun was visible upon her face.", "그녀는 떠났을 때보다 다소 더 여성스러워 보였고, 이탈리아 햇볕에 무르익은 손길이 그녀의 얼굴에 보였다."], ["But, otherwise, she was quite unchanged.", "그러나 그 밖에는 그녀는 조금도 변하지 않았다."], ["The same deep, timid earnestness that he had always seen in her, and never without emotion, he saw still.", "그가 그녀에게서 늘 보아 왔던, 그리고 한 번도 감동 없이는 본 적이 없는 그 깊고 수줍은 진지함을, 그는 여전히 보았다."], ["If it had a new meaning that smote him to the heart, the change was in his perception, not in her.", "만약 그것이 그의 가슴을 후벼 파는 새로운 의미를 띠었다면, 그 변화는 그의 인식에 있는 것이지 그녀에게 있는 것이 아니었다."]], [["She took off her old bonnet, hung it in the old place, and noiselessly began, with Maggy’s help, to make his room as fresh and neat as it could be made, and to sprinkle it with a pleasant-smelling water.", "그녀는 낡은 보닛을 벗어 예전 자리에 걸고, 매기의 도움을 받아, 그의 방을 할 수 있는 한 산뜻하고 깔끔하게 꾸미고, 기분 좋은 향이 나는 물을 방 안에 뿌리는 일을 소리 없이 시작했다."], ["When that was done, the basket, which was filled with grapes and other fruit, was unpacked, and all its contents were quietly put away.", "그 일이 끝나자, 포도와 다른 과일이 가득 담긴 바구니를 풀어, 그 안의 것들을 모두 조용히 정리해 두었다."], ["When that was done, a moment’s whisper despatched Maggy to despatch somebody else to fill the basket again; which soon came back replenished with new stores, from which a present provision of cooling drink and jelly, and a prospective supply of roast chicken and wine and water, were the first extracts.", "그 일이 끝나자, 한순간의 속삭임으로 매기를 보내어 또 다른 누군가를 시켜 바구니를 다시 채우게 했고, 바구니는 이내 새 물건들로 가득 채워져 돌아왔는데, 그중에서 우선 식힌 음료와 젤리가, 그리고 앞으로 마련될 통닭구이와 포도주와 물이 첫 번째로 꺼내졌다."], ["These various arrangements completed, she took out her old needle-case to make him a curtain for his window; and thus, with a quiet reigning in the room, that seemed to diffuse itself through the else noisy prison, he found himself composed in his chair, with Little Dorrit working at his side.", "이런 여러 채비가 끝나자, 그녀는 그의 창에 걸 커튼을 만들어 주려고 낡은 반짇고리를 꺼냈다. 그리하여, 다른 데서는 시끄러운 감옥에까지 두루 퍼져 나가는 듯한 고요가 방 안을 다스리는 가운데, 그는 의자에 마음이 가라앉은 자신을 발견했고, 리틀 도릿은 그의 곁에서 바느질을 했다."]], [["To see the modest head again bent down over its task, and the nimble fingers busy at their old work--though she was not so absorbed in it, but that her compassionate eyes were often raised to his face, and, when they drooped again had tears in them--to be so consoled and comforted, and to believe that all the devotion of this great nature was turned to him in his adversity to pour out its inexhaustible wealth of goodness upon him, did not steady Clennam’s trembling voice or hand, or strengthen him in his weakness.", "그 겸손한 머리가 다시금 일감 위로 숙여지고, 날렵한 손가락들이 예전의 그 일에 분주한 것을 보는 일—비록 그녀는 거기 그렇게 몰두하지는 않아서, 가엾어하는 눈길을 자주 그의 얼굴로 들어 올렸고, 그 눈이 다시 떨궈질 때면 그 안에 눈물이 고여 있었지만—그렇게 위로받고 위안받으며, 이 위대한 천성의 모든 헌신이 역경에 처한 그에게로 돌려져 그 마르지 않는 선의의 풍요를 그에게 쏟아붓고 있다고 믿는 일은, 클레넘의 떨리는 목소리나 손을 진정시키지 못했고, 그의 쇠약함 속에서 그를 강하게 해 주지도 못했다."], ["Yet it inspired him with an inward fortitude, that rose with his love.", "그러나 그것은 그의 사랑과 함께 솟아오르는 내면의 꿋꿋함을 그에게 불어넣었다."], ["And how dearly he loved her now, what words can tell!", "그리고 그가 이제 그녀를 얼마나 애틋이 사랑하는지, 무슨 말로 다 할 수 있으랴!"]], [["As they sat side by side in the shadow of the wall, the shadow fell like light upon him.", "그들이 담장의 그늘 속에 나란히 앉아 있을 때, 그 그늘은 그에게 빛처럼 내렸다."], ["She would not let him speak much, and he lay back in his chair, looking at her.", "그녀는 그가 많은 말을 하도록 두지 않았고, 그는 의자에 등을 기댄 채 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Now and again she would rise and give him the glass that he might drink, or would smooth the resting-place of his head; then she would gently resume her seat by him, and bend over her work again.", "이따금 그녀는 일어나 그가 마시도록 잔을 건네주거나, 그의 머리가 기댄 자리를 매만져 주었다. 그러고는 다시 그의 곁 제자리에 가만히 앉아, 일감 위로 다시 몸을 굽혔다."]], [["The shadow moved with the sun, but she never moved from his side, except to wait upon him.", "그늘은 해를 따라 움직였으나, 그녀는 그를 시중들 때 외에는 그의 곁을 결코 떠나지 않았다."], ["The sun went down and she was still there.", "해가 졌어도 그녀는 여전히 거기 있었다."], ["She had done her work now, and her hand, faltering on the arm of his chair since its last tending of him, was hesitating there yet.", "그녀는 이제 일감을 마쳤고, 마지막으로 그를 돌본 이래 그의 의자 팔걸이에서 머뭇거리던 그녀의 손은 아직도 거기서 망설이고 있었다."], ["He laid his hand upon it, and it clasped him with a trembling supplication.", "그가 그 위에 자기 손을 얹자, 그 손이 떨리는 간청으로 그를 그러쥐었다."]], [["‘Dear Mr Clennam, I must say something to you before I go.", "\"친애하는 클레넘 씨, 가기 전에 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있어요."], ["I have put it off from hour to hour, but I must say it.’", "시간마다 미루어 왔지만, 꼭 말씀드려야 해요.\""]], [["‘I too, dear Little Dorrit.", "\"나도 그렇소, 친애하는 리틀 도릿."], ["I have put off what I must say.’", "나도 꼭 해야 할 말을 미뤄 왔소.\""]], [["She nervously moved her hand towards his lips as if to stop him; then it dropped, trembling, into its former place.", "그녀는 그의 말을 막으려는 듯 초조하게 손을 그의 입술 쪽으로 옮겼다. 그러더니 그 손은 떨면서 이전의 자리로 떨어졌다."]], [["‘I am not going abroad again.", "\"저는 다시 외국으로 가지 않아요."], ["My brother is, but I am not.", "오빠는 가지만, 저는 가지 않아요."], ["He was always attached to me, and he is so grateful to me now--so much too grateful, for it is only because I happened to be with him in his illness--that he says I shall be free to stay where I like best, and to do what I like best.", "오빠는 늘 제게 정이 깊었고, 지금은 제게 너무도 고마워해요—너무도 과하게 고마워하지요, 그저 제가 우연히 오빠의 병중에 곁에 있었던 것뿐인데도요—그래서 오빠는 제가 가장 좋아하는 곳에 머물고, 가장 좋아하는 일을 할 자유가 있다고 말해요."], ["He only wishes me to be happy, he says.’", "그저 제가 행복하기만을 바란다고 해요.\""]], [["There was one bright star shining in the sky.", "하늘에 환한 별 하나가 빛나고 있었다."], ["She looked up at it while she spoke, as if it were the fervent purpose of her own heart shining above her.", "그녀는 말하면서 그것을 올려다보았는데, 마치 그것이 자기 머리 위에서 빛나는, 그녀 자신의 마음속 간절한 뜻인 듯했다."]], [["‘You will understand, I dare say, without my telling you, that my brother has come home to find my dear father’s will, and to take possession of his property.", "\"오빠가 제 사랑하는 아버지의 유언장을 찾아 그 재산을 차지하려고 고향에 돌아왔다는 걸, 제가 말씀드리지 않아도 당신은 아마 아실 거예요."], ["He says, if there is a will, he is sure I shall be left rich; and if there is none, that he will make me so.’", "오빠는 말하길, 유언장이 있다면 제게 분명 많은 재산이 남겨졌을 테고, 없다면 자기가 저를 그렇게 만들어 주겠다고 해요.\""]], [["He would have spoken; but she put up her trembling hand again, and he stopped.", "그가 말하려 했으나, 그녀가 다시 떨리는 손을 들어 올리자 그는 멈추었다."]], [["‘I have no use for money, I have no wish for it.", "\"저는 돈이 필요 없고, 돈을 바라지도 않아요."], ["It would be of no value at all to me but for your sake.", "그것은 당신을 위해서가 아니면 제게 아무 가치도 없어요."], ["I could not be rich, and you here.", "당신이 여기 계신데 제가 부유할 수는 없어요."], ["I must always be much worse than poor, with you distressed.", "당신이 곤경에 처해 있는데, 저는 늘 가난한 것보다 훨씬 더 비참할 수밖에 없어요."], ["Will you let me lend you all I have?", "제가 가진 모든 것을 당신께 빌려드리게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["Will you let me give it you?", "그것을 당신께 드리게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["Will you let me show you that I have never forgotten, that I never can forget, your protection of me when this was my home?", "이곳이 제 집이었을 때 당신이 저를 보호해 주신 일을 제가 결코 잊지 않았으며, 결코 잊을 수 없다는 것을 보여드리게 해 주시겠어요?"], ["Dear Mr Clennam, make me of all the world the happiest, by saying Yes?", "친애하는 클레넘 씨, '그래'라고 말씀하셔서 저를 온 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람으로 만들어 주시겠어요?"], ["Make me as happy as I can be in leaving you here, by saying nothing to-night, and letting me go away with the hope that you will think of it kindly; and that for my sake--not for yours, for mine, for nobody’s but mine!--you will give me the greatest joy I can experience on earth, the joy of knowing that I have been serviceable to you, and that I have paid some little of the great debt of my affection and gratitude.", "오늘 밤엔 아무 말씀도 하지 않으시고, 당신이 그것을 너그러이 생각해 주시리라는 희망을 안고 제가 떠나게 해 주셔서, 당신을 여기 두고 떠나는 제가 누릴 수 있는 한 가장 행복하게 만들어 주세요. 그리고 저를 위해서—당신을 위해서가 아니라, 저를 위해서, 그 누구도 아닌 오직 저만을 위해서!—제가 이 세상에서 누릴 수 있는 가장 큰 기쁨을, 곧 제가 당신께 보탬이 되었다는, 그리고 제 애정과 감사의 그 큰 빚을 조금이나마 갚았다는 것을 아는 기쁨을 제게 주세요."], ["I can’t say what I wish to say.", "제가 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 수가 없어요."], ["I can’t visit you here where I have lived so long, I can’t think of you here where I have seen so much, and be as calm and comforting as I ought.", "제가 그토록 오래 살았던 이곳에서 당신을 찾아뵐 수도 없고, 제가 그토록 많은 것을 보았던 이곳에서 당신을 생각하면서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 차분하고 다독여드릴 수가 없어요."], ["My tears will make their way.", "제 눈물이 자꾸 흘러나오네요."], ["I cannot keep them back.", "도저히 참을 수가 없어요."], ["But pray, pray, pray, do not turn from your Little Dorrit, now, in your affliction!", "하지만 부디, 부디, 부디, 지금 당신의 괴로움 속에서 당신의 리틀 도릿에게서 등 돌리지 마세요!"], ["Pray, pray, pray, I beg you and implore you with all my grieving heart, my friend--my dear!--take all I have, and make it a Blessing to me!’", "부디, 부디, 부디, 슬퍼하는 제 온 마음으로 당신께 빌고 애원해요, 나의 벗이여—나의 그리운 이여!—제가 가진 모든 것을 받으셔서, 그것을 제게 축복으로 만들어 주세요!\""]], [["The star had shone on her face until now, when her face sank upon his hand and her own.", "별은 지금껏 그녀의 얼굴을 비추고 있었는데, 이제 그녀의 얼굴이 그의 손과 자신의 손 위로 떨궈졌다."]], [["It had grown darker when he raised her in his encircling arm, and softly answered her.", "그가 두 팔로 감싸안아 그녀를 일으키고 부드럽게 대답했을 때에는 더 어두워져 있었다."]], [["‘No, darling Little Dorrit.", "\"안 되오, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿."], ["No, my child.", "안 되오, 내 아이여."], ["I must not hear of such a sacrifice.", "나는 그런 희생에 대해 들을 수 없소."], ["Liberty and hope would be so dear, bought at such a price, that I could never support their weight, never bear the reproach of possessing them.", "자유와 희망이 그토록 비싼 값으로 산 것이라면 너무나 귀해서, 나는 결코 그 무게를 견디지 못할 것이고, 그것을 지녔다는 책망을 결코 감당하지 못할 것이오."], ["But with what ardent thankfulness and love I say this, I may call Heaven to witness!’", "그러나 내가 이 말을 얼마나 뜨거운 감사와 사랑으로 하는지는, 하늘을 증인으로 부를 수 있소!\""]], [["‘And yet you will not let me be faithful to you in your affliction?’", "\"그런데도 당신은 당신의 괴로움 속에서 제가 당신께 충실하도록 두지 않으시겠다는 건가요?\""]], [["‘Say, dearest Little Dorrit, and yet I will try to be faithful to you. If, in the bygone days when this was your home and when this was your dress, I had understood myself (I speak only of myself) better, and had read the secrets of my own breast more distinctly; if, through my reserve and self-mistrust, I had discerned a light that I see brightly now when it has passed far away, and my weak footsteps can never overtake it; if I had then known, and told you that I loved and honoured you, not as the poor child I used to call you, but as a woman whose true hand would raise me high above myself and make me a far happier and better man; if I had so used the opportunity there is no recalling--as I wish I had, O I wish I had!--and if something had kept us apart then, when I was moderately thriving, and when you were poor; I might have met your noble offer of your fortune, dearest girl, with other words than these, and still have blushed to touch it. But, as it is, I must never touch it, never!’", "\"이렇게 말하구려, 가장 사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 그래도 나는 당신에게 충실하려 애쓰겠소. 만약, 이곳이 당신의 집이고 이 옷이 당신의 옷이던 지난날에, 내가 나 자신을(나는 오직 나 자신에 대해서만 말하는 거요) 더 잘 이해하고, 내 가슴속 비밀을 더 또렷이 읽어 냈더라면. 만약 나의 조심성과 자기 불신을 뚫고서, 이제 그것이 멀리 지나가 버려 나의 약한 발걸음이 결코 따라잡을 수 없게 된 지금에야 환히 보이는 그 빛을 내가 그때 알아보았더라면. 만약 내가 그때 깨닫고, 당신을 사랑하고 존경한다고—내가 부르곤 했던 그 가엾은 아이로서가 아니라, 그 진실한 손이 나를 나 자신보다 훨씬 높이 끌어올려 나를 훨씬 더 행복하고 나은 사람으로 만들어 줄 한 여인으로서—당신에게 말했더라면. 만약 내가 그 다시 돌이킬 수 없는 기회를 그렇게 썼더라면—내가 그랬더라면 얼마나 좋았을까, 오 그랬더라면 좋았을 것을!—그리고 만약 내가 그런대로 형편이 나았고 당신이 가난했던 그때에 무언가가 우리를 갈라놓았더라면. 나는 당신 재산을 내놓는 그 고귀한 제안을, 가장 사랑하는 이여, 이런 말이 아닌 다른 말로 맞이했을지도 모르오. 그러고도 그것에 손을 대기를 부끄러워하며 얼굴을 붉혔을 거요. 그러나 지금 이대로는, 나는 결코 그것에 손을 대서는 안 되오, 결코!\""]], [["She besought him, more pathetically and earnestly, with her little supplicatory hand, than she could have done in any words.", "그녀는 어떤 말로도 할 수 있었을 것보다 더 애처롭고 간절하게, 그 작은 애원하는 손으로 그에게 빌었다."]], [["‘I am disgraced enough, my Little Dorrit.", "\"나는 이미 충분히 치욕을 당했소, 나의 리틀 도릿."], ["I must not descend so low as that, and carry you--so dear, so generous, so good--down with me.", "나는 그토록 낮게 떨어져서, 당신을—그토록 그립고, 그토록 너그럽고, 그토록 선한 당신을—나와 함께 끌어내려서는 안 되오."], ["GOD bless you, GOD reward you!", "하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를, 하느님이 당신에게 보답하시기를!"], ["It is past.’", "다 지난 일이오.\""]], [["He took her in his arms, as if she had been his daughter.", "그는 마치 그녀가 자기 딸이라도 되는 양 그녀를 두 팔로 안았다."]], [["‘Always so much older, so much rougher, and so much less worthy, even what I was must be dismissed by both of us, and you must see me only as I am.", "\"나는 늘 훨씬 더 나이가 많고, 훨씬 더 거칠고, 훨씬 덜 자격 없는 사람이오. 내가 한때 어떤 사람이었든 그조차도 우리 둘 다 떨쳐 버려야 하고, 당신은 지금의 나만을 보아야 하오."], ["I put this parting kiss upon your cheek, my child--who might have been more near to me, who never could have been more dear--a ruined man far removed from you, for ever separated from you, whose course is run while yours is but beginning.", "나는 이 작별의 입맞춤을 당신의 뺨에 남기오, 내 아이여—내게 더 가까운 사람이 될 수도 있었던, 그러나 이보다 더 그리운 사람일 수는 없었던—당신에게서 멀리 떨어진, 영원히 당신과 갈라진, 그 인생행로가 이미 다한 한 망가진 사내가 말이오. 당신의 행로는 이제 막 시작될 뿐인데."], ["I have not the courage to ask to be forgotten by you in my humiliation; but I ask to be remembered only as I am.’", "나는 내 굴욕 속에서 당신에게 잊어 달라고 청할 용기는 없소. 그러나 오직 지금의 나로서만 기억되기를 청하오.\""]], [["The bell began to ring, warning visitors to depart.", "종이 울리기 시작하여, 방문객들에게 떠나라고 알렸다."], ["He took her mantle from the wall, and tenderly wrapped it round her.", "그는 벽에서 그녀의 망토를 가져와 다정하게 그녀를 감싸 주었다."]], [["‘One other word, my Little Dorrit.", "\"한 마디만 더 하겠소, 나의 리틀 도릿."], ["A hard one to me, but it is a necessary one.", "내게는 힘겨운 말이지만, 꼭 해야 하는 말이오."], ["The time when you and this prison had anything in common has long gone by.", "당신과 이 감옥이 무언가 공통점을 가졌던 시절은 오래전에 지나갔소."], ["Do you understand?’", "알아듣겠소?\""]], [["‘O!", "\"오!"], ["you will never say to me,’ she cried, weeping bitterly, and holding up her clasped hands in entreaty, ‘that I am not to come back any more!", "당신은 결코 제게,\" 그녀가 비통하게 울며, 맞잡은 두 손을 들어 올려 애원하며 외쳤다."], ["You will surely not desert me so!’", "\"다시는 돌아오지 말라고 말씀하지 않으시겠죠! 설마 저를 그렇게 저버리지는 않으시겠죠!\""]], [["‘I would say it, if I could; but I have not the courage quite to shut out this dear face, and abandon all hope of its return.", "\"할 수만 있다면 그렇게 말하고 싶소. 그러나 나는 이 그리운 얼굴을 아주 닫아 버리고, 그것이 다시 돌아오리라는 모든 희망을 단념할 용기가 도무지 없소."], ["But do not come soon, do not come often!", "그러나 곧 오지는 마오, 자주 오지는 마오!"], ["This is now a tainted place, and I well know the taint of it clings to me.", "이곳은 이제 오염된 곳이고, 나는 그 오염이 내게 달라붙어 있음을 잘 알고 있소."], ["You belong to much brighter and better scenes.", "당신은 훨씬 더 밝고 나은 무대에 속한 사람이오."], ["You are not to look back here, my Little Dorrit; you are to look away to very different and much happier paths.", "당신은 여기를 돌아보아서는 안 되오, 나의 리틀 도릿. 당신은 아주 다르고 훨씬 더 행복한 길들을 향해 눈을 돌려야 하오."], ["Again, GOD bless you in them!", "거듭, 그 길들 위에서 하느님이 당신을 축복하시기를!"], ["GOD reward you!’", "하느님이 당신에게 보답하시기를!\""]], [["Maggy, who had fallen into very low spirits, here cried, ‘Oh get him into a hospital; do get him into a hospital, Mother!", "몹시 기운이 풀죽어 있던 매기가 여기서 외쳤다. \"오, 저분을 병원에 넣어 드려요."], ["He’ll never look like hisself again, if he an’t got into a hospital.", "어머니, 저분을 병원에 꼭 넣어 드려요! 병원에 들어가지 못하면 저분은 다시는 본래 모습으로 돌아오지 못할 거예요."], ["And then the little woman as was always a spinning at her wheel, she can go to the cupboard with the Princess, and say, what do you keep the Chicking there for? and then they can take it out and give it to him, and then all be happy!’", "그러면 늘 물레질을 하던 그 작은 여인이 공주님이랑 같이 찬장으로 가서, 닭을 거기 왜 두고 있느냐고 물으면, 그걸 꺼내서 저분에게 줄 수 있고, 그러면 다들 행복해질 거예요!\""]], [["The interruption was seasonable, for the bell had nearly rung itself out.", "그 방해는 때마침 알맞았으니, 종이 거의 다 울려 끝나 가고 있었던 것이다."], ["Again tenderly wrapping her mantle about her, and taking her on his arm (though, but for her visit, he was almost too weak to walk), Arthur led Little Dorrit down-stairs.", "다시 다정하게 그녀의 망토를 감싸 주고, 그녀를 자기 팔에 기대게 한 채(비록 그녀의 방문이 없었더라면 그는 거의 걸을 수도 없을 만큼 약했지만), 아서는 리틀 도릿을 데리고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["She was the last visitor to pass out at the Lodge, and the gate jarred heavily and hopelessly upon her.", "그녀는 수위실을 빠져나가는 마지막 방문객이었고, 문이 그녀의 등 뒤에서 묵직하고 가망 없게 삐걱거리며 닫혔다."]], [["With the funeral clang that it sounded into Arthur’s heart, his sense of weakness returned.", "그 문이 아서의 가슴속으로 울려 보낸 장례식 같은 쇳소리와 더불어, 그의 쇠약한 느낌이 되돌아왔다."], ["It was a toilsome journey up-stairs to his room, and he re-entered its dark solitary precincts in unutterable misery.", "자기 방으로 올라가는 길은 고된 여정이었고, 그는 형언할 수 없는 비참함 속에서 그 어둡고 외로운 영역으로 다시 들어섰다."]], [["When it was almost midnight, and the prison had long been quiet, a cautious creak came up the stairs, and a cautious tap of a key was given at his door.", "거의 자정이 되어, 감옥이 오래도록 조용해졌을 때, 조심스러운 삐걱 소리가 계단을 올라왔고, 그의 문에 조심스럽게 열쇠를 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["It was Young John.", "젊은 존이었다."], ["He glided in, in his stockings, and held the door closed, while he spoke in a whisper.", "그는 양말 바람으로 미끄러지듯 들어와, 문을 닫아 잡은 채 속삭이며 말했다."]], [["‘It’s against all rules, but I don’t mind.", "\"규칙에 다 어긋나는 일이지만, 저는 개의치 않아요."], ["I was determined to come through, and come to you.’", "꼭 들어와서, 당신께 오기로 마음먹었거든요.\""]], [["‘What is the matter?’", "\"무슨 일인가?\""]], [["‘Nothing’s the matter, sir.", "\"아무 일도 없어요, 선생님."], ["I was waiting in the court-yard for Miss Dorrit when she came out.", "도릿 양이 나오실 때 안마당에서 기다리고 있었어요."], ["I thought you’d like some one to see that she was safe.’", "그분이 무사한지 누군가 지켜보길 당신이 바라실 것 같아서요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, thank you!", "\"고맙네, 고마워!"], ["You took her home, John?’", "그녀를 집까지 데려다주었나, 존?\""]], [["‘I saw her to her hotel.", "\"호텔까지 모셨어요."], ["The same that Mr Dorrit was at.", "도릿 씨가 묵으셨던 바로 그 호텔이요."], ["Miss Dorrit walked all the way, and talked to me so kind, it quite knocked me over.", "도릿 양은 내내 걸으셨고, 어찌나 다정하게 제게 말씀하시던지, 저는 완전히 무너져 내렸어요."], ["Why do you think she walked instead of riding?’", "그분이 왜 마차를 타지 않고 걸으셨다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["‘I don’t know, John.’", "\"모르겠네, 존.\""]], [["‘To talk about you.", "\"당신에 관해 이야기하시려고요. 그분이 제게 말씀하셨어요."], ["She said to me, “John, you was always honourable, and if you’ll promise me that you will take care of him, and never let him want for help and comfort when I am not there, my mind will be at rest so far.”", "'존, 너는 늘 명예로운 사람이었으니, 내가 곁에 없을 때 그분을 보살피고, 그분이 도움과 위안이 부족하지 않도록 하겠다고 내게 약속해 준다면, 그만큼은 내 마음이 놓일 거야.' 저는 그분께 약속했어요."], ["I promised her.", "그리고 저는 당신 곁을 지킬 거예요,\""], ["And I’ll stand by you,’ said John Chivery, ‘for ever!’", "존 치버리가 말했다. \"영원히요!\""]], [["Clennam, much affected, stretched out his hand to this honest spirit.", "몹시 감동한 클레넘은 이 정직한 영혼에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["‘Before I take it,’ said John, looking at it, without coming from the door, ‘guess what message Miss Dorrit gave me.’", "\"그 손을 잡기 전에,\" 문에서 떨어지지 않은 채 그 손을 바라보며 존이 말했다. \"도릿 양이 제게 무슨 전갈을 주셨는지 맞혀 보세요.\""]], [["Clennam shook his head.", "클레넘은 고개를 저었다."]], [["‘“Tell him,”’ repeated John, in a distinct, though quavering voice, ‘“that his Little Dorrit sent him her undying love.”", "\"'그분께 전해 줘,'\" 또렷하지만 떨리는 목소리로 존이 되풀이했다."], ["Now it’s delivered.", "\"'그분의 리틀 도릿이 그분께 변치 않는 사랑을 보낸다고.'"], ["Have I been honourable, sir?’", "자, 이제 전해 드렸어요. 제가 명예로웠나요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘Very, very!’", "\"아주, 아주 그랬네!\""]], [["‘Will you tell Miss Dorrit I’ve been honourable, sir?’", "\"도릿 양께 제가 명예로웠다고 말씀해 주시겠어요, 선생님?\""]], [["‘I will indeed.’", "\"꼭 그러겠네.\""]], [["‘There’s my hand, sir,’ said John, ‘and I’ll stand by you forever!’", "\"여기 제 손이 있어요, 선생님,\" 존이 말했다. \"그리고 저는 영원히 당신 곁을 지킬게요!\""]], [["After a hearty squeeze, he disappeared with the same cautious creak upon the stair, crept shoeless over the pavement of the yard, and, locking the gates behind him, passed out into the front where he had left his shoes.", "진심 어린 악수를 나눈 뒤, 그는 똑같이 조심스러운 삐걱 소리를 내며 계단으로 사라졌고, 신발 없이 마당 포석 위를 살금살금 건너가, 등 뒤로 문을 잠그고는, 신발을 벗어 둔 앞쪽으로 나갔다."], ["If the same way had been paved with burning ploughshares, it is not at all improbable that John would have traversed it with the same devotion, for the same purpose.", "만약 같은 길이 불타는 쟁기날로 깔려 있었다 해도, 존은 같은 목적을 위해 같은 헌신으로 그 길을 건넜으리라는 것이 전혀 있음 직하지 않은 일은 아니다."]], [["# CHAPTER 30. Closing in", "# CHAPTER 30. Closing in"]], [["The last day of the appointed week touched the bars of the Marshalsea gate.", "마셜시 정문의 쇠창살에 정해진 한 주의 마지막 날이 닿았다."], ["Black, all night, since the gate had clashed upon Little Dorrit, its iron stripes were turned by the early-glowing sun into stripes of gold.", "리틀 도릿을 안에 가둔 채 철문이 쾅 닫힌 그날부터 밤새 까맣던 그 쇠줄무늬가, 일찍 떠오른 햇살에 황금빛 줄무늬로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["Far aslant across the city, over its jumbled roofs, and through the open tracery of its church towers, struck the long bright rays, bars of the prison of this lower world.", "도시를 가로질러 비스듬히, 뒤죽박죽 얽힌 지붕들 위로, 교회 첨탑들의 뚫린 격자무늬 사이로, 길고 환한 빛줄기가 내리꽂혔다 — 이 하계(下界)라는 감옥의 창살들이."]], [["Throughout the day the old house within the gateway remained untroubled by any visitors.", "하루 내내 정문 안쪽의 그 낡은 집은 어떤 방문객의 발길에도 흔들리지 않은 채 남아 있었다."], ["But, when the sun was low, three men turned in at the gateway and made for the dilapidated house.", "그러나 해가 낮게 기울었을 때, 세 사내가 정문으로 꺾어 들어와 그 허물어져 가는 집으로 향했다."]], [["Rigaud was the first, and walked by himself smoking.", "리고가 맨 앞이었고, 혼자서 담배를 피우며 걸었다."], ["Mr Baptist was the second, and jogged close after him, looking at no other object.", "바티스트 씨가 둘째로, 다른 데는 눈길도 주지 않은 채 그의 바로 뒤를 종종거리며 따라왔다."], ["Mr Pancks was the third, and carried his hat under his arm for the liberation of his restive hair; the weather being extremely hot.", "팽크스 씨가 셋째였는데, 날이 몹시 더운지라 제멋대로 뻗치는 머리카락을 풀어 주려고 모자를 겨드랑이에 끼고 있었다."], ["They all came together at the door-steps.", "그들은 다 함께 문간 계단에 이르렀다."]], [["‘You pair of madmen!’ said Rigaud, facing about.", "\"이 미치광이 한 쌍 같으니!\" 리고가 홱 돌아서며 말했다."], ["‘Don’t go yet!’", "\"아직 가지 말게!\""]], [["‘We don’t mean to,’ said Mr Pancks.", "\"그럴 생각 없소.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."]], [["Giving him a dark glance in acknowledgment of his answer, Rigaud knocked loudly.", "그 대답에 대한 답으로 음험한 눈길을 한 번 던지고는, 리고가 문을 요란하게 두드렸다."], ["He had charged himself with drink, for the playing out of his game, and was impatient to begin.", "그는 제 놀음을 끝까지 펼쳐 보이기 위해 술을 잔뜩 채워 둔 터라, 어서 시작하고 싶어 안달이 나 있었다."], ["He had hardly finished one long resounding knock, when he turned to the knocker again and began another.", "길게 울려 퍼지는 노크 한 번을 채 끝내기도 전에, 그는 다시 문고리로 돌아가 또 한 번을 시작했다."], ["That was not yet finished when Jeremiah Flintwinch opened the door, and they all clanked into the stone hall.", "그것이 채 끝나기도 전에 제리마이아 플린트윈치가 문을 열었고, 그들 모두 절그럭거리며 돌바닥 현관으로 들어섰다."], ["Rigaud, thrusting Mr Flintwinch aside, proceeded straight up-stairs.", "리고는 플린트윈치 씨를 옆으로 밀치며 곧장 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["His two attendants followed him, Mr Flintwinch followed them, and they all came trooping into Mrs Clennam’s quiet room.", "그의 두 수행원이 그를 따랐고, 플린트윈치 씨가 그들을 따랐으며, 그들 모두 우르르 클레넘 부인의 고요한 방으로 몰려 들어갔다."], ["It was in its usual state; except that one of the windows was wide open, and Affery sat on its old-fashioned window-seat, mending a stocking.", "방은 평소 그대로였다. 다만 창문 하나가 활짝 열려 있고, 애프리가 그 구식 창가 자리에 앉아 양말을 깁고 있는 것만 달랐다."], ["The usual articles were on the little table; the usual deadened fire was in the grate; the bed had its usual pall upon it; and the mistress of all sat on her black bier-like sofa, propped up by her black angular bolster that was like the headsman’s block.", "작은 탁자 위에는 늘 놓이던 물건들이 있었고, 화로 안에는 늘 그렇듯 죽어 가는 불이 있었으며, 침대에는 늘 덮여 있던 천이 덮여 있었다. 그리고 이 모든 것의 안주인은 검은 상여 같은 소파에 앉아, 형 집행인의 단두대 받침처럼 생긴 검고 각진 등받이 베개에 몸을 기대고 있었다."]], [["Yet there was a nameless air of preparation in the room, as if it were strung up for an occasion.", "그럼에도 방 안에는 무언가 채비를 갖춘 듯한, 어떤 사태를 위해 팽팽히 당겨진 듯한 이름 없는 기운이 감돌았다."], ["From what the room derived it--every one of its small variety of objects being in the fixed spot it had occupied for years--no one could have said without looking attentively at its mistress, and that, too, with a previous knowledge of her face.", "방의 그 자잘한 물건 하나하나가 수년간 차지해 온 제자리에 그대로 박혀 있었으므로, 그 기운이 어디서 비롯되는지는, 안주인을 주의 깊게 살피지 않고서는, 그것도 그녀의 얼굴을 미리 알고 있지 않고서는 아무도 말할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Although her unchanging black dress was in every plait precisely as of old, and her unchanging attitude was rigidly preserved, a very slight additional setting of her features and contraction of her gloomy forehead was so powerfully marked, that it marked everything about her.", "변함없는 검은 옷이 주름 하나하나까지 예전 그대로였고, 변함없는 자세도 빳빳하게 유지되고 있었지만, 그녀의 이목구비에 아주 미미하게 더해진 굳음과 음울한 이마의 수축이 어찌나 강렬하게 새겨져 있던지, 그것이 그녀를 둘러싼 모든 것에 새겨져 있었다."]], [["‘Who are these?’ she said, wonderingly, as the two attendants entered.", "\"이 사람들은 누구죠?\" 두 수행원이 들어서자 그녀가 의아한 듯 말했다."], ["‘What do these people want here?’", "\"이 사람들이 여기서 뭘 원하는 거예요?\""]], [["‘Who are these, dear madame, is it?’ returned Rigaud.", "\"이 사람들이 누구냐, 그 말씀이신지요, 친애하는 부인?\" 리고가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Faith, they are friends of your son the prisoner.", "\"맹세코, 이들은 죄수가 된 부인의 아드님 친구들입니다."], ["And what do they want here, is it?", "그리고 이들이 여기서 뭘 원하느냐, 그 말씀이시지요?"], ["Death, madame, I don’t know.", "원, 부인, 저야 모르지요."], ["You will do well to ask them.’", "직접 물어보시는 게 좋겠습니다.\""]], [["‘You know you told us at the door, not to go yet,’ said Pancks.", "\"문간에서 아직 가지 말라고 한 건 당신이잖소.\" 팽크스가 말했다."]], [["‘And you know you told me at the door, you didn’t mean to go,’ retorted Rigaud.", "\"문간에서 갈 생각 없다고 한 건 당신이잖나.\" 리고가 되받았다."], ["‘In a word, madame, permit me to present two spies of the prisoner’s--madmen, but spies.", "\"한마디로, 부인, 죄수의 첩자 두 명을 소개하게 해 주십시오 — 미치광이들이긴 하나, 첩자들입니다."], ["If you wish them to remain here during our little conversation, say the word.", "우리의 짧은 대화가 진행되는 동안 이들이 여기 남아 있기를 바라신다면, 한마디만 하십시오."], ["It is nothing to me.’", "저로서는 아무래도 좋습니다.\""]], [["‘Why should I wish them to remain here?’ said Mrs Clennam.", "\"내가 왜 이들이 여기 남아 있기를 바라겠어요?\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."], ["‘What have I to do with them?’", "\"내가 이들과 무슨 상관이 있어서?\""]], [["‘Then, dearest madame,’ said Rigaud, throwing himself into an arm-chair so heavily that the old room trembled, ‘you will do well to dismiss them.", "\"그렇다면, 더없이 친애하는 부인,\" 리고가 안락의자에 어찌나 묵직하게 몸을 던지던지 낡은 방이 흔들렸다, \"이들을 내보내시는 게 좋겠습니다."], ["It is your affair.", "부인의 일이지요."], ["They are not my spies, not my rascals.’", "이들은 제 첩자도, 제 부하들도 아니니까요.\""]], [["‘Hark!", "\"이봐요!"], ["You Pancks,’ said Mrs Clennam, bending her brows upon him angrily, ‘you Casby’s clerk!", "거기 팽크스,\" 클레넘 부인이 그에게 화난 듯 눈썹을 찌푸리며 말했다, \"거기 캐스비 사무원!"], ["Attend to your employer’s business and your own.", "당신 고용주의 일과 당신 자신의 일이나 챙겨요. 가요."], ["Go. And take that other man with you.’", "그리고 저 다른 사람도 데려가요.\""]], [["‘Thank you, ma’am,’ returned Mr Pancks, ‘I am glad to say I see no objection to our both retiring.", "\"고맙습니다, 부인.\" 팽크스 씨가 받아쳤다, \"우리 둘 다 물러가는 데 아무 이의 없음을 말씀드릴 수 있어 기쁩니다."], ["We have done all we undertook to do for Mr Clennam.", "우리는 클레넘 씨를 위해 떠맡은 일을 다 했습니다."], ["His constant anxiety has been (and it grew worse upon him when he became a prisoner), that this agreeable gentleman should be brought back here to the place from which he slipped away.", "그분의 한결같은 근심은(죄수가 된 뒤로 더 심해졌지요), 이 유쾌하신 신사분이 슬그머니 빠져나간 바로 이 자리로 다시 끌려오는 것이었습니다. 자, 여기 있군요 — 끌려왔습니다."], ["Here he is--brought back.", "그리고 한마디 하겠는데,\""], ["And I will say,’ added Mr Pancks, ‘to his ill-looking face, that in my opinion the world would be no worse for his slipping out of it altogether.’", "팽크스 씨가 덧붙였다, \"그 흉한 낯짝에다 대고 말하건대, 내 생각으론 그가 이 세상에서 아주 슬그머니 빠져나간다 해도 세상은 조금도 나빠지지 않을 거요.\""]], [["‘Your opinion is not asked,’ answered Mrs Clennam.", "\"당신 생각은 묻지 않았어요.\" 클레넘 부인이 대답했다."], ["‘Go.’", "\"가요.\""]], [["‘I am sorry not to leave you in better company, ma’am,’ said Pancks; ‘and sorry, too, that Mr Clennam can’t be present.", "\"더 나은 사람들 곁에 부인을 두고 가지 못해 죄송합니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["It’s my fault, that is.’", "\"그리고 클레넘 씨가 함께하지 못해 또한 죄송하고요. 그건 제 잘못입니다, 그건요.\""]], [["‘You mean his own,’ she returned.", "\"그 사람 자신의 잘못이겠죠.\" 그녀가 받아쳤다."]], [["‘No, I mean mine, ma’am,’ said Pancks, ‘for it was my misfortune to lead him into a ruinous investment.’ (Mr Pancks still clung to that word, and never said speculation.) ‘Though I can prove by figures,’ added Mr Pancks, with an anxious countenance, ‘that it ought to have been a good investment. I have gone over it since it failed, every day of my life, and it comes out--regarded as a question of figures--triumphant. The present is not a time or place,’ Mr Pancks pursued, with a longing glance into his hat, where he kept his calculations, ‘for entering upon the figures; but the figures are not to be disputed. Mr Clennam ought to have been at this moment in his carriage and pair, and I ought to have been worth from three to five thousand pound.’", "\"아니요, 제 잘못입니다, 부인.\" 팽크스가 말했다. \"그분을 파멸을 부르는 투자로 이끈 게 제 불운이었으니까요.\" (팽크스 씨는 여전히 그 말에 매달렸고, 결코 투기라고는 말하지 않았다.) \"물론 숫자로 증명할 수 있습니다만,\" 팽크스 씨가 근심스러운 낯빛으로 덧붙였다, \"그건 마땅히 좋은 투자가 됐어야 했습니다. 실패한 이래로 제 평생 하루도 빠짐없이 다시 검토해 봤는데, 결과는 — 숫자의 문제로 보자면 — 의기양양하게 나옵니다. 지금은 그 숫자를 따져 볼 때도 자리도 아닙니다만,\" 팽크스 씨는 자기 계산을 넣어 두는 모자 속을 그리운 눈길로 흘긋 보며 말을 이었다, \"숫자에는 이론의 여지가 없습니다. 클레넘 씨는 지금 이 순간 자기 마차에 쌍두마를 매고 있어야 마땅하고, 저는 삼천에서 오천 파운드의 재산을 가지고 있어야 마땅합니다.\""]], [["Mr Pancks put his hair erect with a general aspect of confidence that could hardly have been surpassed, if he had had the amount in his pocket.", "팽크스 씨는 그 금액이 제 주머니에 있었더라도 더할 나위 없었을 만큼의 자신감 어린 전반적인 태도로 머리카락을 곧추세웠다."], ["These incontrovertible figures had been the occupation of every moment of his leisure since he had lost his money, and were destined to afford him consolation to the end of his days.", "이 반박할 수 없는 숫자들은 돈을 잃은 이래로 그의 모든 한가한 순간을 차지해 온 일거리였고, 그의 여생이 다할 때까지 그에게 위안을 안겨 줄 운명이었다."]], [["‘However,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘enough of that.", "\"하지만,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다, \"그 얘긴 이쯤 하지요."], ["Altro, old boy, you have seen the figures, and you know how they come out.’ Mr Baptist, who had not the slightest arithmetical power of compensating himself in this way, nodded, with a fine display of bright teeth.", "알트로, 친구, 자네도 그 숫자를 봤으니 결과가 어떻게 나오는지 알 거야.\" 이런 식으로 자기를 위로할 산술적 능력이라곤 조금도 없는 바티스트 씨가, 하얀 이를 멋지게 드러내 보이며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["At whom Mr Flintwinch had been looking, and to whom he then said:", "플린트윈치 씨가 줄곧 그를 바라보고 있다가, 그제야 그에게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["it’s you, is it?", "자네였군, 그렇지?"], ["I thought I remembered your face, but I wasn’t certain till I saw your teeth.", "자네 얼굴을 기억하는 것 같긴 했는데, 자네 이를 보기 전까진 확신할 수 없었어."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["yes, to be sure.", "그래, 그렇고말고."], ["It was this officious refugee,’ said Jeremiah to Mrs Clennam, ‘who came knocking at the door on the night when Arthur and Chatterbox were here, and who asked me a whole Catechism of questions about Mr Blandois.’", "바로 이 주제넘은 망명자가,\" 제리마이아가 클레넘 부인에게 말했다, \"아서와 그 수다쟁이가 여기 있던 날 밤 문을 두드리고는, 블랑두아 씨에 관해 교리문답 한 통만큼이나 많은 질문을 내게 퍼부었지요.\""]], [["‘It is true,’ Mr Baptist cheerfully admitted.", "\"맞습니다.\" 바티스트 씨가 쾌활하게 인정했다."], ["‘And behold him, padrone!", "\"그리고 보십시오, 주인님!"], ["I have found him consequentementally.’", "제가 그 결과로(consequentementally) 그를 찾아냈습니다.\""]], [["‘I shouldn’t have objected,’ returned Mr Flintwinch, ‘to your having broken your neck consequentementally.’", "\"자네가 그 결과로 목이 부러졌더라도,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 받아쳤다, \"나는 반대하지 않았을 텐데.\""]], [["‘And now,’ said Mr Pancks, whose eye had often stealthily wandered to the window-seat and the stocking that was being mended there, ‘I’ve only one other word to say before I go. If Mr Clennam was here--but unfortunately, though he has so far got the better of this fine gentleman as to return him to this place against his will, he is ill and in prison--ill and in prison, poor fellow--if he was here,’ said Mr Pancks, taking one step aside towards the window-seat, and laying his right hand upon the stocking; ‘he would say, “Affery, tell your dreams!”’", "\"그럼 이제,\" 그동안 그의 눈길이 자주 창가 자리와 거기서 깁고 있는 양말로 슬그머니 흘러갔던 팽크스 씨가 말했다, \"가기 전에 딱 한마디만 더 하겠습니다. 클레넘 씨가 여기 계셨다면 — 하지만 안타깝게도, 이 훌륭하신 신사를 그 의사에 반하여 이곳으로 되돌려 보낼 만큼은 이겼다 해도, 그분은 병들어 감옥에 갇혀 있지요 — 병들어 감옥에, 가엾은 분이 — 그분이 여기 계셨다면,\" 팽크스 씨가 창가 자리 쪽으로 한 걸음 옮겨 오른손을 양말 위에 얹으며 말했다, \"이렇게 말씀하셨을 겁니다. '애프리, 당신의 꿈을 이야기해요!'\""]], [["Mr Pancks held up his right forefinger between his nose and the stocking with a ghostly air of warning, turned, steamed out and towed Mr Baptist after him.", "팽크스 씨는 으스스하게 경고하는 분위기로 오른쪽 집게손가락을 코와 양말 사이에 치켜들고는, 돌아서서 칙칙폭폭 나가며 바티스트 씨를 뒤로 끌고 갔다."], ["The house-door was heard to close upon them, their steps were heard passing over the dull pavement of the echoing court-yard, and still nobody had added a word.", "집 대문이 그들 뒤로 닫히는 소리가 들렸고, 메아리치는 안마당의 둔탁한 포석 위로 그들의 발소리가 지나가는 게 들렸지만, 여전히 아무도 한마디도 보태지 않았다."], ["Mrs Clennam and Jeremiah had exchanged a look; and had then looked, and looked still, at Affery, who sat mending the stocking with great assiduity.", "클레넘 부인과 제리마이아는 눈빛을 주고받았다. 그러고는 양말을 부지런히 깁고 앉아 있는 애프리를 바라보았고, 또 바라보았다."]], [["‘Come!’ said Mr Flintwinch at length, screwing himself a curve or two in the direction of the window-seat, and rubbing the palms of his hands on his coat-tail as if he were preparing them to do something: ‘Whatever has to be said among us had better be begun to be said without more loss of time.--So, Affery, my woman, take yourself away!’", "\"자!\" 마침내 플린트윈치 씨가 창가 자리 쪽으로 몸을 한두 번 비틀어 돌리며, 무언가를 할 채비라도 하는 듯 두 손바닥을 자기 외투 자락에 문지르며 말했다. \"우리 사이에서 해야 할 말이 무엇이든, 더 이상 시간 낭비 말고 시작하는 게 좋겠어. — 그러니, 애프리, 여보, 자네는 물러가게!\""]], [["In a moment Affery had thrown the stocking down, started up, caught hold of the windowsill with her right hand, lodged herself upon the window-seat with her right knee, and was flourishing her left hand, beating expected assailants off.", "순식간에 애프리는 양말을 내던지고 벌떡 일어나, 오른손으로 창턱을 붙잡고, 오른쪽 무릎을 창가 자리에 얹은 채, 예상되는 공격자들을 물리치듯 왼손을 휘둘러 댔다."]], [["‘No, I won’t, Jeremiah--no, I won’t--no, I won’t!", "\"안 돼요, 안 가요, 제리마이아 — 안 가, 안 가요 — 안 가요!"], ["I won’t go!", "안 갈 거예요!"], ["I’ll stay here.", "여기 있을 거예요."], ["I’ll hear all I don’t know, and say all I know.", "내가 모르는 건 다 듣고, 내가 아는 건 다 말할 거예요."], ["I will, at last, if I die for it.", "그러고 말겠어요, 마침내, 그러다 죽는 한이 있어도."], ["I will, I will, I will, I will!’", "할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요!\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, stiffening with indignation and amazement, moistened the fingers of one hand at his lips, softly described a circle with them in the palm of the other hand, and continued with a menacing grin to screw himself in the direction of his wife; gasping some remark as he advanced, of which, in his choking anger, only the words, ‘Such a dose!’ were audible.", "플린트윈치 씨는 분노와 경악으로 뻣뻣하게 굳은 채, 한 손가락들을 입술에 적셔, 다른 손바닥에 살그머니 동그라미를 그리고는, 위협적인 히죽임을 지으며 계속해서 아내 쪽으로 몸을 비틀어 다가갔다. 다가가면서 무언가 한마디를 헐떡이며 내뱉었는데, 목이 막힐 듯한 분노 속에서 알아들을 수 있는 것이라곤 \"이런 한 방을!\" 하는 말뿐이었다."]], [["‘Not a bit nearer, Jeremiah!’ cried Affery, never ceasing to beat the air.", "\"한 발짝도 더 가까이 오지 마요, 제리마이아!\" 애프리가 허공을 향한 손짓을 한순간도 멈추지 않으며 외쳤다."], ["‘Don’t come a bit nearer to me, or I’ll rouse the neighbourhood!", "\"나한테 한 발짝도 더 가까이 오면, 동네방네 사람들을 불러낼 거예요!"], ["I’ll throw myself out of window.", "창밖으로 몸을 던질 거예요."], ["I’ll scream Fire and Murder!", "불이야, 사람 죽인다, 소리칠 거예요!"], ["I’ll wake the dead!", "죽은 사람도 깨울 거예요!"], ["Stop where you are, or I’ll make shrieks enough to wake the dead!’", "거기 그대로 있어요, 안 그러면 죽은 사람도 깨울 만큼 비명을 질러 댈 테니까!\""]], [["The determined voice of Mrs Clennam echoed ‘Stop!’ Jeremiah had stopped already.", "클레넘 부인의 단호한 목소리가 \"멈춰요!\" 하고 울렸다. 제리마이아는 이미 멈춰 있었다."]], [["‘It is closing in, Flintwinch.", "\"좁혀 들어오고 있어요, 플린트윈치."], ["Let her alone.", "저 사람은 내버려 둬요."], ["Affery, do you turn against me after these many years?’", "애프리, 이 여러 해가 지난 뒤에 자네가 나한테 등을 돌리는 건가?\""]], [["‘I do, if it’s turning against you to hear what I don’t know, and say what I know.", "\"그래요, 내가 모르는 걸 듣고 내가 아는 걸 말하는 게 마님께 등을 돌리는 거라면요."], ["I have broke out now, and I can’t go back.", "난 이제 터뜨려 버렸고, 돌이킬 수 없어요."], ["I am determined to do it.", "그러기로 마음먹었어요."], ["I will do it, I will, I will, I will!", "할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요, 할 거예요!"], ["If that’s turning against you, yes, I turn against both of you two clever ones.", "그게 마님께 등을 돌리는 거라면, 그래요, 난 영리한 두 분 다한테 등을 돌릴 거예요."], ["I told Arthur when he first come home to stand up against you.", "아서 도련님이 처음 집에 돌아왔을 때 두 분께 맞서라고 일러 줬어요."], ["I told him it was no reason, because I was afeard of my life of you, that he should be.", "내가 목숨이 두려워 벌벌 떤다고 해서 도련님까지 그래야 할 이유는 없다고 일러 줬다고요."], ["All manner of things have been a-going on since then, and I won’t be run up by Jeremiah, nor yet I won’t be dazed and scared, nor made a party to I don’t know what, no more.", "그때 이후로 온갖 일이 벌어졌고, 난 더 이상 제리마이아한테 휘둘리지도, 정신이 멍해지고 겁먹지도, 내가 알지도 못하는 일에 한패가 되지도 않을 거예요. 안 그래요, 안 그래, 안 그럴 거예요!"], ["I won’t, I won’t, I won’t! I’ll up for Arthur when he has nothing left, and is ill, and in prison, and can’t up for himself.", "아서 도련님이 아무것도 남지 않고, 병들고, 감옥에 갇혀, 스스로 일어설 수 없을 때, 내가 도련님을 위해 일어설 거예요."], ["I will, I will, I will, I will!’", "그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요, 그럴 거예요!\""]], [["‘How do you know, you heap of confusion,’ asked Mrs Clennam sternly, ‘that in doing what you are doing now, you are even serving Arthur?’", "\"네가 어떻게 아니, 이 뒤죽박죽 덩어리야,\" 클레넘 부인이 엄하게 물었다, \"지금 네가 하는 짓이 아서를 돕기라도 하는 거라고?\""]], [["‘I don’t know nothing rightly about anything,’ said Affery; ‘and if ever you said a true word in your life, it’s when you call me a heap of confusion, for you two clever ones have done your most to make me such. You married me whether I liked it or not, and you’ve led me, pretty well ever since, such a life of dreaming and frightening as never was known, and what do you expect me to be but a heap of confusion? You wanted to make me such, and I am such; but I won’t submit no longer; no, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t, I won’t!’ She was still beating the air against all comers.", "\"전 뭐든 제대로 아는 게 하나도 없어요.\" 애프리가 말했다. \"그리고 마님이 평생 단 한 번이라도 옳은 말을 하셨다면, 그건 절 뒤죽박죽 덩어리라고 부르실 때예요. 왜냐하면 영리한 두 분이 절 그렇게 만들려고 갖은 짓을 다 하셨으니까요. 제가 좋든 싫든 절 결혼시키셨고, 그 이후로 줄곧 꿈과 겁박으로 점철된, 일찍이 들어 본 적 없는 그런 삶을 살게 하셨는데, 제가 뒤죽박죽 덩어리 말고 뭐가 되길 바라셨겠어요? 절 그렇게 만들고 싶어 하셨고, 전 그렇게 됐어요. 하지만 더는 굴복하지 않을 거예요. 안 해요, 안 해, 안 해, 안 할 거예요!\" 그녀는 여전히 다가오는 누구든 막으려 허공에 손을 휘둘러 댔다."]], [["After gazing at her in silence, Mrs Clennam turned to Rigaud.", "말없이 그녀를 응시하던 클레넘 부인이 리고에게로 돌아섰다."], ["‘You see and hear this foolish creature.", "\"이 어리석은 것이 보이고 들리시지요."], ["Do you object to such a piece of distraction remaining where she is?’", "저런 정신 나간 것이 저 자리에 그대로 있는 게 거슬리세요?\""]], [["‘I, madame,’ he replied, ‘do I?", "\"제가, 부인,\" 그가 대답했다, \"거슬리느냐고요?"], ["That’s a question for you.’", "그건 부인께 드릴 질문이지요.\""]], [["‘I do not,’ she said, gloomily.", "\"난 안 거슬려요.\" 그녀가 음울하게 말했다."], ["‘There is little left to choose now.", "\"이제 고를 여지가 거의 없으니까요."], ["Flintwinch, it is closing in.’", "플린트윈치, 좁혀 들어오고 있어요.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch replied by directing a look of red vengeance at his wife, and then, as if to pinion himself from falling upon her, screwed his crossed arms into the breast of his waistcoat, and with his chin very near one of his elbows stood in a corner, watching Rigaud in the oddest attitude.", "플린트윈치 씨는 아내에게 시뻘건 복수의 눈길을 던지는 것으로 대답하고는, 마치 그녀에게 달려들지 못하도록 제 몸을 붙들어 매려는 듯, 팔짱 낀 두 팔을 조끼 가슴팍에 비틀어 넣고, 턱을 한쪽 팔꿈치에 바싹 댄 채 한쪽 구석에 서서, 더없이 괴상한 자세로 리고를 지켜보았다."], ["Rigaud, for his part, arose from his chair, and seated himself on the table with his legs dangling.", "리고는 리고대로 의자에서 일어나 다리를 늘어뜨린 채 탁자 위에 걸터앉았다."], ["In this easy attitude, he met Mrs Clennam’s set face, with his moustache going up and his nose coming down.", "이 편안한 자세로, 그는 콧수염을 치켜올리고 코를 내리깐 채 클레넘 부인의 굳은 얼굴을 마주 보았다."]], [["‘Madame, I am a gentleman--’", "\"부인, 저는 신사입니다 —\""]], [["‘Of whom,’ she interrupted in her steady tones, ‘I have heard disparagement, in connection with a French jail and an accusation of murder.’", "\"그에 관해서라면,\" 그녀가 흔들림 없는 어조로 말을 끊었다, \"프랑스 감옥과 살인 혐의에 관련하여 헐뜯는 말을 들은 바 있어요.\""]], [["He kissed his hand to her with his exaggerated gallantry.", "그는 과장된 정중함으로 그녀에게 손에 입을 맞추는 시늉을 했다."]], [["‘Perfectly.", "\"완벽하십니다."], ["Exactly.", "정확합니다."], ["Of a lady too!", "게다가 한 부인을요!"], ["What absurdity!", "얼마나 터무니없는지!"], ["How incredible!", "얼마나 믿기 힘든지!"], ["I had the honour of making a great success then; I hope to have the honour of making a great success now.", "그때 저는 큰 성공을 거두는 영광을 누렸습니다. 지금도 큰 성공을 거두는 영광을 누리기를 바랍니다."], ["I kiss your hands.", "손에 입맞춤을 드립니다."], ["Madame, I am a gentleman (I was going to observe), who when he says, “I will definitely finish this or that affair at the present sitting,”", "부인, 저는 신사입니다(이렇게 말씀드리려던 참이었지요). '이번 자리에서 이러저러한 일을 반드시 끝내겠다'고 말하면, 반드시 끝내는 신사 말입니다."], ["does definitely finish it. I announce to you that we are arrived at our last sitting on our little business.", "우리의 자그마한 용무에 관해 우리가 마지막 자리에 이르렀음을 선언합니다."], ["You do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’", "잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시겠지요?\""]], [["She kept her eyes fixed upon him with a frown.", "그녀는 눈을 그에게 고정한 채 눈살을 찌푸리고 있었다."], ["‘Yes.’", "\"그래요.\""]], [["‘Further, I am a gentleman to whom mere mercenary trade-bargains are unknown, but to whom money is always acceptable as the means of pursuing his pleasures.", "\"또한, 저는 한낱 돈벌이를 위한 거래 따위는 모르는 신사입니다만, 제 쾌락을 좇는 수단으로서의 돈은 언제나 환영하는 신사입니다."], ["You do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’", "잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시지요?\""]], [["‘Scarcely necessary to ask, one would say.", "\"물어볼 필요도 거의 없겠다고 할 만하군요."], ["Yes.’", "그래요.\""]], [["‘Further, I am a gentleman of the softest and sweetest disposition, but who, if trifled with, becomes enraged.", "\"또한, 저는 더없이 부드럽고 더없이 다정한 기질의 신사입니다만, 농락당하면 격분하는 신사이기도 합니다."], ["Noble natures under such circumstances become enraged.", "고귀한 천성을 지닌 자는 그런 상황에서 격분하는 법이지요."], ["I possess a noble nature.", "저는 고귀한 천성을 지녔습니다."], ["When the lion is awakened--that is to say, when I enrage--the satisfaction of my animosity is as acceptable to me as money.", "사자가 깨어나면 — 즉, 제가 격분하면 — 제 적의를 채우는 것이 저에겐 돈만큼이나 환영할 만한 일입니다."], ["You always do me the favour to follow, and to comprehend?’", "늘 잘 따라오시고, 이해하시는 영광을 제게 베풀어 주시지요?\""]], [["‘Yes,’ she answered, somewhat louder than before.", "\"그래요.\" 그녀가 아까보다 다소 큰 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["‘Do not let me derange you; pray be tranquil.", "\"제가 부인을 어지럽히게 하지 마십시오. 부디 평온하시길."], ["I have said we are now arrived at our last sitting.", "우리가 이제 마지막 자리에 이르렀다고 제가 말씀드렸지요."], ["Allow me to recall the two sittings we have held.’", "우리가 가졌던 두 차례의 자리를 되짚어 보게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["‘It is not necessary.’", "\"그럴 필요 없어요.\""]], [["‘Death, madame,’ he burst out, ‘it’s my fancy!", "\"원, 부인,\" 그가 불쑥 내뱉었다, \"그게 제 취향입니다!"], ["Besides, it clears the way.", "게다가, 그래야 길이 트입니다. 첫 번째 자리는 제한적이었습니다."], ["The first sitting was limited.", "저는 부인과 알게 되는 영광을 — 제 소개장을 내미는 영광을 누렸지요. 저는 협잡의 기사입니다, 부인, 분부대로 하겠습니다만, 제 세련된 매너 덕분에 어학 선생으로서 큰 성공을 거두었더랬습니다."], ["I had the honour of making your acquaintance--of presenting my letter; I am a Knight of Industry, at your service, madame, but my polished manners had won me so much of success, as a master of languages, among your compatriots who are as stiff as their own starch is to one another, but are ready to relax to a foreign gentleman of polished manners--and of observing one or two little things,’ he glanced around the room and smiled, ‘about this honourable house, to know which was necessary to assure me, and to convince me that I had the distinguished pleasure of making the acquaintance of the lady I sought.", "서로에게는 제 풀 먹인 옷깃만큼이나 빳빳하면서도, 세련된 매너를 갖춘 외국 신사에게는 기꺼이 풀어지는 부인의 동포들 사이에서 말이지요 — 그리고 이 명망 높은 댁에 관한 한두 가지 자그마한 것들을 살피는 영광도요,\" 그는 방을 둘러보며 미소 지었다, \"그것들을 알아야 제가 찾던 부인과 알게 되는 출중한 기쁨을 누렸음을 확신하고 납득할 수 있었으니까요."], ["I achieved this.", "저는 이를 이루었습니다."], ["I gave my word of honour to our dear Flintwinch that I would return.", "우리의 친애하는 플린트윈치에게 다시 돌아오겠노라 명예를 걸고 약속드렸지요."], ["I gracefully departed.’", "저는 우아하게 떠났습니다.\""]], [["Her face neither acquiesced nor demurred.", "그녀의 얼굴은 수긍하지도 이의를 달지도 않았다."], ["The same when he paused, and when he spoke, it as yet showed him always the one attentive frown, and the dark revelation before mentioned of her being nerved for the occasion.", "그가 말을 멈출 때나 말할 때나 한결같이, 그 얼굴은 아직껏 그에게 늘 그 하나의 골똘한 찌푸림과, 앞서 언급한바 이 사태를 위해 단단히 마음먹은 그녀의 어두운 표정을 내보였다."]], [["‘I say, gracefully departed, because it was graceful to retire without alarming a lady.", "\"우아하게 떠났다고 말씀드리는 것은, 부인을 놀래지 않고 물러가는 것이 우아한 일이었기 때문입니다."], ["To be morally graceful, not less than physically, is a part of the character of Rigaud Blandois.", "육체적으로뿐 아니라 도덕적으로도 우아한 것, 그것이 리고 블랑두아라는 인물의 됨됨이의 일부입니다."], ["It was also politic, as leaving you with something overhanging you, to expect me again with a little anxiety on a day not named.", "그것은 또한 영리한 처사였습니다. 부인께 무언가가 머리 위에 드리워진 채로 두어, 정해지지 않은 어느 날 약간의 불안 속에서 저를 다시 기다리시게 했으니까요."], ["But your slave is politic.", "그러나 부인의 종은 영리합니다."], ["By Heaven, madame, politic!", "맹세코, 부인, 영리하지요!"], ["Let us return.", "다시 이어 갑시다."], ["On the day not named, I have again the honour to render myself at your house.", "정해지지 않은 그날, 저는 다시 부인 댁에 저 자신을 들이는 영광을 누렸습니다."], ["I intimate that I have something to sell, which, if not bought, will compromise madame whom I highly esteem.", "저는 팔 것이 있는데, 사지 않으시면 제가 높이 존경하는 부인을 곤란하게 할 것이라고 넌지시 비쳤지요."], ["I explain myself generally.", "대략적으로 제 뜻을 밝혔습니다."], ["I demand--I think it was a thousand pounds.", "저는 요구했습니다 — 천 파운드였던 것 같습니다."], ["Will you correct me?’", "바로잡아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Thus forced to speak, she replied with constraint, ‘You demanded as much as a thousand pounds.’", "이렇게 말하지 않을 수 없게 되자, 그녀는 마지못해 대답했다. \"당신은 천 파운드나 되는 돈을 요구했어요.\""]], [["‘I demand at present, Two.", "\"지금은 이천을 요구합니다."], ["Such are the evils of delay.", "지체의 폐해가 그런 것이지요."], ["But to return once more.", "그러나 다시 한 번 이어 갑시다."], ["We are not accordant; we differ on that occasion.", "우리는 합의에 이르지 못했습니다. 그때 우리는 의견이 갈렸지요."], ["I am playful; playfulness is a part of my amiable character.", "저는 장난기가 있습니다. 장난기는 제 사랑스러운 됨됨이의 일부니까요."], ["Playfully, I become as one slain and hidden.", "장난삼아, 저는 살해되어 숨겨진 사람처럼 되었습니다."], ["For, it may alone be worth half the sum to madame, to be freed from the suspicions that my droll idea awakens.", "제 우스꽝스러운 착상이 불러일으키는 의심에서 벗어나는 것만으로도 부인께는 그 금액의 절반은 족히 값어치가 있을 테니 말이지요."], ["Accident and spies intermix themselves against my playfulness, and spoil the fruit, perhaps--who knows?", "우연과 첩자들이 제 장난기에 끼어들어, 어쩌면 — 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["only you and Flintwinch--when it is just ripe.", "부인과 플린트윈치만 알겠지요 — 막 익었을 때 그 열매를 망쳐 버립니다."], ["Thus, madame, I am here for the last time.", "그리하여, 부인, 저는 마지막으로 여기 와 있습니다."], ["Listen!", "들으십시오!"], ["Definitely the last.’", "분명코 마지막입니다.\""]], [["As he struck his straggling boot-heels against the flap of the table, meeting her frown with an insolent gaze, he began to change his tone for a fierce one.", "그가 제멋대로 뻗은 부츠 뒤축을 탁자의 늘어진 덮개에 부딪치며, 그녀의 찌푸림을 거만한 응시로 맞받는 동안, 그는 어조를 사나운 쪽으로 바꾸기 시작했다."]], [["‘Bah!", "\"바!"], ["Stop an instant!", "잠깐 멈추시지요!"], ["Let us advance by steps.", "단계를 밟아 나갑시다."], ["Here is my Hotel-note to be paid, according to contract.", "여기 계약에 따라 지불되어야 할 제 호텔 청구서가 있습니다."], ["Five minutes hence we may be at daggers’ points.", "오 분 뒤면 우리는 서로 칼끝을 겨누게 될지도 모르지요."], ["I’ll not leave it till then, or you’ll cheat me.", "그때까지 이걸 미루지 않겠습니다, 안 그러면 부인이 저를 속일 테니까요."], ["Pay it!", "지불하십시오!"], ["Count me the money!’", "제게 돈을 세어 주십시오!\""]], [["‘Take it from his hand and pay it, Flintwinch,’ said Mrs Clennam.", "\"그 손에서 받아 지불해 줘요, 플린트윈치.\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."]], [["He spirted it into Mr Flintwinch’s face when the old man advanced to take it, and held forth his hand, repeating noisily, ‘Pay it!", "노인이 그것을 받으러 다가오자 그는 그것을 플린트윈치 씨의 얼굴에 휙 내던지고는, 손을 내밀며 시끄럽게 되풀이했다. \"지불하시오!"], ["Count it out!", "세어 내놓으시오!"], ["Good money!’ Jeremiah picked the bill up, looked at the total with a bloodshot eye, took a small canvas bag from his pocket, and told the amount into his hand.", "좋은 돈으로!\" 제리마이아는 청구서를 집어 들고 충혈된 눈으로 총액을 보고는, 주머니에서 작은 천 주머니를 꺼내 그 액수를 그의 손에 세어 주었다."]], [["Rigaud chinked the money, weighed it in his hand, threw it up a little way and caught it, chinked it again.", "리고는 돈을 짤랑거리고, 손으로 무게를 가늠하고, 조금 위로 던졌다 받고, 다시 짤랑거렸다."]], [["‘The sound of it, to the bold Rigaud Blandois, is like the taste of fresh meat to the tiger.", "\"대담한 리고 블랑두아에게 이 소리는, 호랑이에게 갓 잡은 고기 맛과도 같지요."], ["Say, then, madame.", "자, 그럼, 부인."], ["How much?’", "얼마입니까?\""]], [["He turned upon her suddenly with a menacing gesture of the weighted hand that clenched the money, as if he were going to strike her with it.", "그는 돈을 움켜쥔 묵직한 손으로 위협하는 몸짓을 하며, 마치 그것으로 그녀를 내려치기라도 할 듯 갑자기 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["‘I tell you again, as I told you before, that we are not rich here, as you suppose us to be, and that your demand is excessive.", "\"전에도 말했고 다시 말하지만, 우리는 당신이 짐작하는 만큼 여기서 부유하지 않으며, 당신의 요구는 과도해요."], ["I have not the present means of complying with such a demand, if I had ever so great an inclination.’", "그럴 마음이 제아무리 크다 한들, 그런 요구에 응할 만한 수단이 지금 내겐 없어요.\""]], [["‘If!’ cried Rigaud.", "\"한들이라니!\" 리고가 외쳤다."], ["‘Hear this lady with her If!", "\"한들이라니, 이 부인의 말 좀 들어 보시오!"], ["Will you say that you have not the inclination?’", "마음이 없다고는 말하지 못하시겠지요?\""]], [["‘I will say what presents itself to me, and not what presents itself to you.’", "\"나는 내게 떠오르는 대로 말하지, 당신에게 떠오르는 대로 말하지 않아요.\""]], [["‘Say it then.", "\"그럼 말하시지요."], ["As to the inclination.", "그 마음에 관해서."], ["Quick!", "어서!"], ["Come to the inclination, and I know what to do.’", "마음 얘기로 와 보시오, 그럼 제가 어떻게 할지 압니다.\""]], [["She was no quicker, and no slower, in her reply.", "그녀의 대답은 더 빠르지도 더 느리지도 않았다."], ["‘It would seem that you have obtained possession of a paper--or of papers--which I assuredly have the inclination to recover.’", "\"당신이 어떤 서류 — 혹은 여러 서류 — 를 손에 넣은 모양인데, 그건 확실히 내가 되찾고 싶은 마음이 있는 것이군요.\""]], [["Rigaud, with a loud laugh, drummed his heels against the table, and chinked his money.", "리고는 큰 소리로 웃으며 뒤축으로 탁자를 두드리고 돈을 짤랑거렸다."], ["‘I think so!", "\"그럴 줄 알았지!"], ["I believe you there!’", "그 점은 믿어요!\""]], [["‘The paper might be worth, to me, a sum of money.", "\"그 서류는 내게 어느 정도의 돈만큼 값어치가 있을지도 모르죠."], ["I cannot say how much, or how little.’", "얼마나 많을지, 얼마나 적을지는 말할 수 없어요.\""]], [["‘What the Devil!’ he asked savagely.", "\"빌어먹을!\" 그가 사납게 물었다."], ["‘Not after a week’s grace to consider?’", "\"일주일이나 유예를 두고 궁리하고도 그 모양이오?\""]], [["‘No!", "\"그래요!"], ["I will not out of my scanty means--for I tell you again, we are poor here, and not rich--I will not offer any price for a power that I do not know the worst and the fullest extent of.", "그 빈약한 수단으로는 — 다시 말하지만 우리는 여기서 가난하지 부유하지 않으니까요 — 그 최악과 최대치를 알지 못하는 위력에 무슨 값도 치르지 않겠어요."], ["This is the third time of your hinting and threatening.", "이번이 당신이 넌지시 비추며 위협하는 세 번째예요."], ["You must speak explicitly, or you may go where you will, and do what you will.", "분명하게 말해야 해요, 안 그러면 마음대로 가서 마음대로 해요."], ["It is better to be torn to pieces at a spring, than to be a mouse at the caprice of such a cat.’", "저런 고양이의 변덕에 놀아나는 생쥐가 되느니, 단번에 갈가리 찢기는 편이 나아요.\""]], [["He looked at her so hard with those eyes too near together that the sinister sight of each, crossing that of the other, seemed to make the bridge of his hooked nose crooked.", "그는 너무 가까이 붙은 그 두 눈으로 그녀를 어찌나 빤히 노려보던지, 한쪽 눈의 불길한 시선이 다른 쪽 시선과 엇갈려, 그 갈고리진 코의 콧대를 비뚤어지게 만드는 듯했다."], ["After a long survey, he said, with the further setting off of his internal smile:", "한참을 살피더니, 그는 안에서 새어 나오는 미소를 한층 더 도드라지게 하며 말했다."]], [["‘You are a bold woman!’", "\"부인은 대담한 여인이군요!\""]], [["‘I am a resolved woman.’", "\"나는 결심한 여인이에요.\""]], [["‘You always were.", "\"늘 그러셨지요."], ["What?", "뭐라고요?"], ["She always was; is it not so, my little Flintwinch?’", "부인은 늘 그러셨지요. 그렇지 않나, 나의 작은 플린트윈치?\""]], [["‘Flintwinch, say nothing to him.", "\"플린트윈치, 저 사람한테 아무 말도 하지 말아요."], ["It is for him to say, here and now, all he can; or to go hence, and do all he can.", "지금 여기서 그가 할 수 있는 말을 다 하든가, 아니면 여기서 나가서 할 수 있는 일을 다 하든가, 그건 저 사람이 정할 일이에요."], ["You know this to be our determination.", "이게 우리의 결정인 걸 당신도 알잖아요."], ["Leave him to his action on it.’", "저 사람이 그에 따라 어떻게 하든 내버려 둬요.\""]], [["She did not shrink under his evil leer, or avoid it.", "그녀는 그의 사악한 곁눈질에 움츠러들지도, 피하지도 않았다."], ["He turned it upon her again, but she remained steady at the point to which she had fixed herself.", "그가 다시 그 눈길을 그녀에게로 돌렸지만, 그녀는 스스로 정해 놓은 지점에 흔들림 없이 머물러 있었다."], ["He got off the table, placed a chair near the sofa, sat down in it, and leaned an arm upon the sofa close to her own, which he touched with his hand.", "그는 탁자에서 내려와 소파 가까이에 의자를 놓고 거기 앉아, 한쪽 팔을 그녀의 팔에 바싹 붙여 소파에 기대고, 그 손으로 그녀의 팔을 건드렸다."], ["Her face was ever frowning, attentive, and settled.", "그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 찌푸린 채, 골똘하고, 굳어 있었다."]], [["‘It is your pleasure then, madame, that I shall relate a morsel of family history in this little family society,’ said Rigaud, with a warning play of his lithe fingers on her arm.", "\"그렇다면 부인, 이 자그마한 집안 모임에서 제가 집안 내력 한 조각을 들려드리는 게 부인의 뜻이군요.\" 리고가 그녀의 팔 위에서 유연한 손가락을 경고하듯 놀리며 말했다."], ["‘I am something of a doctor.", "\"저는 제법 의사 노릇도 합니다."], ["Let me touch your pulse.’", "부인의 맥을 좀 짚어 보지요.\""]], [["She suffered him to take her wrist in his hand.", "그녀는 그가 자기 손목을 손에 쥐도록 내버려 두었다."], ["Holding it, he proceeded to say:", "그것을 쥔 채 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["‘A history of a strange marriage, and a strange mother, and a revenge, and a suppression.--Aye, aye, aye?", "\"기이한 결혼, 기이한 어머니, 복수, 그리고 은폐에 관한 내력입니다. — 어허, 어허, 어허?"], ["this pulse is beating curiously!", "이 맥박이 묘하게 뛰는군요!"], ["It appears to me that it doubles while I touch it.", "제가 짚고 있는 동안 두 배로 빨라지는 것 같습니다."], ["Are these the usual changes of your malady, madame?’", "이것이 부인의 지병에서 흔히 일어나는 변화입니까?\""]], [["There was a struggle in her maimed arm as she twisted it away, but there was none in her face.", "그녀가 자신의 불구가 된 팔을 비틀어 빼내는 동안 거기엔 안간힘이 있었으나, 그녀의 얼굴에는 아무것도 없었다."], ["On his face there was his own smile.", "그의 얼굴에는 그만의 그 미소가 있었다."]], [["‘I have lived an adventurous life.", "\"저는 모험으로 가득한 삶을 살아왔습니다."], ["I am an adventurous character.", "저는 모험적인 인물이지요."], ["I have known many adventurers; interesting spirits--amiable society!", "많은 모험가를 알아 왔습니다. 흥미로운 영혼들 — 사랑스러운 무리지요!"], ["To one of them I owe my knowledge and my proofs--I repeat it, estimable lady--proofs--of the ravishing little family history I go to commence.", "그중 한 사람에게서 저는 제 지식과 제 증거를 얻었습니다 — 다시 말하지만, 존경할 만한 부인, 증거를요 — 이제 시작하려는 이 매혹적인 자그마한 집안 내력의 증거 말입니다."], ["You will be charmed with it.", "부인도 매료되실 겁니다."], ["But, bah!", "한데, 바!"], ["I forget.", "깜빡했군요."], ["One should name a history.", "내력에는 이름을 붙여야지요."], ["Shall I name it the history of a house?", "어느 집의 내력이라 부를까요?"], ["But, bah, again.", "한데, 또 바."], ["There are so many houses.", "집은 너무 많으니까요."], ["Shall I name it the history of this house?’", "이 집의 내력이라 부를까요?\""]], [["Leaning over the sofa, poised on two legs of his chair and his left elbow; that hand often tapping her arm to beat his words home; his legs crossed; his right hand sometimes arranging his hair, sometimes smoothing his moustache, sometimes striking his nose, always threatening her whatever it did; coarse, insolent, rapacious, cruel, and powerful, he pursued his narrative at his ease.", "소파 위로 몸을 기울이고, 의자의 두 다리와 자기 왼쪽 팔꿈치로 몸을 떠받친 채, 그 손은 자기 말을 못 박듯 그녀의 팔을 자주 톡톡 치고, 다리는 꼰 채, 오른손은 때로 머리를 매만지고, 때로 콧수염을 쓰다듬고, 때로 코를 치면서, 무엇을 하든 늘 그녀를 위협하며, 거칠고, 거만하고, 탐욕스럽고, 잔인하고, 위세 등등한 그는 느긋하게 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["‘In fine, then, I name it the history of this house. I commence it.", "\"하면, 그러니까, 저는 이것을 이 집의 내력이라 부르겠습니다."], ["There live here, let us suppose, an uncle and nephew.", "시작하지요. 여기 어느 삼촌과 조카가 산다고 칩시다."], ["The uncle, a rigid old gentleman of strong force of character; the nephew, habitually timid, repressed, and under constraint.’", "삼촌은 강인한 성품을 지닌 완고한 노신사이고, 조카는 천성적으로 소심하고, 억눌리고, 제약 아래 있는 자입니다.\""]], [["Mistress Affery, fixedly attentive in the window-seat, biting the rolled up end of her apron, and trembling from head to foot, here cried out, ‘Jeremiah, keep off from me!", "창가 자리에서 골똘히 주의를 기울인 채, 둘둘 만 앞치마 끝자락을 깨물며, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 떨고 있던 애프리 부인이 여기서 외쳤다. \"제리마이아, 나한테서 떨어져요!"], ["I’ve heerd, in my dreams, of Arthur’s father and his uncle.", "난 내 꿈속에서, 아서 도련님 아버지와 그분 삼촌에 관해 들었어요."], ["He’s a talking of them.", "저 사람이 그분들 얘기를 하고 있어요."], ["It was before my time here; but I’ve heerd in my dreams that Arthur’s father was a poor, irresolute, frightened chap, who had had everything but his orphan life scared out of him when he was young, and that he had no voice in the choice of his wife even, but his uncle chose her.", "그건 내가 여기 오기 전 일이지만, 내 꿈속에서 들었다고요. 아서 도련님 아버지는 가엾고, 우유부단하고, 겁에 질린 사람이라, 젊었을 때 고아 신세인 목숨만 빼고 모든 게 겁박당해 빠져나가 버려서, 아내를 고르는 데조차 한마디 거들 권리도 없이 삼촌이 골라 줬다고요."], ["There she sits!", "저기 그 여자가 앉아 있어요!"], ["I heerd it in my dreams, and you said it to her own self.’", "난 그걸 꿈속에서 들었고, 당신이 그 여자한테 직접 그렇게 말했어요.\""]], [["As Mr Flintwinch shook his fist at her, and as Mrs Clennam gazed upon her, Rigaud kissed his hand to her.", "플린트윈치 씨가 그녀에게 주먹을 흔들고, 클레넘 부인이 그녀를 응시하는 동안, 리고는 그녀에게 손에 입맞춤을 보냈다."]], [["‘Perfectly right, dear Madame Flintwinch.", "\"완벽하게 옳습니다, 친애하는 플린트윈치 부인."], ["You have a genius for dreaming.’", "부인께는 꿈꾸는 재주가 있으시군요.\""]], [["‘I don’t want none of your praises,’ returned Affery.", "\"당신 칭찬 따위 필요 없어요.\" 애프리가 받아쳤다."], ["‘I don’t want to have nothing at all to say to you.", "\"당신하곤 할 말이라곤 하나도 없어요."], ["But Jeremiah said they was dreams, and I’ll tell ‘em as such!’ Here she put her apron in her mouth again, as if she were stopping somebody else’s mouth--perhaps Jeremiah’s, which was chattering with threats as if he were grimly cold.", "하지만 제리마이아가 그게 꿈이라고 했으니, 난 그걸 꿈으로 말할 거예요!\" 여기서 그녀는 마치 다른 누군가의 입을 — 어쩌면 제리마이아의 입을, 사납게 추위라도 타듯 위협의 말로 덜덜 떨리는 그 입을 — 틀어막기라도 하듯 다시 앞치마를 입에 물었다."]], [["‘Our beloved Madame Flintwinch,’ said Rigaud, ‘developing all of a sudden a fine susceptibility and spirituality, is right to a marvel.", "\"우리의 사랑스러운 플린트윈치 부인은,\" 리고가 말했다, \"느닷없이 고운 감수성과 영성을 펼쳐 보이시며, 놀랍도록 정확하십니다."], ["Yes. So runs the history.", "그렇습니다."], ["Monsieur, the uncle, commands the nephew to marry.", "내력은 그렇게 흘러갑니다. 삼촌 되시는 분이 조카에게 결혼하라 명합니다."], ["Monsieur says to him in effect, “My nephew, I introduce to you a lady of strong force of character, like myself--a resolved lady, a stern lady, a lady who has a will that can break the weak to powder: a lady without pity, without love, implacable, revengeful, cold as the stone, but raging as the fire.”", "삼촌이 실상 그에게 이렇게 말하지요. '조카야, 너에게 나처럼 강인한 성품을 지닌 부인을 소개하마 — 결심이 굳은 부인, 엄격한 부인, 약한 자를 가루로 부숴 버릴 의지를 지닌 부인, 연민도 없고, 사랑도 없고, 가차 없고, 복수심 강하고, 돌처럼 차갑되 불처럼 타오르는 부인을.'"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["what fortitude!", "얼마나 굳센지!"], ["Ah, what superiority of intellectual strength!", "아, 얼마나 빼어난 지력의 강인함인지!"], ["Truly, a proud and noble character that I describe in the supposed words of Monsieur, the uncle.", "참으로, 삼촌 되시는 분의 짐작 속 말로 제가 묘사하는 것은 자랑스럽고 고귀한 성품이지요. 하, 하, 하!"], ["Ha, ha, ha! Death of my soul, I love the sweet lady!’", "내 영혼이 죽을 노릇이지만, 이 사랑스러운 부인이 마음에 드는군요!\""]], [["Mrs Clennam’s face had changed.", "클레넘 부인의 얼굴이 달라져 있었다."], ["There was a remarkable darkness of colour on it, and the brow was more contracted.", "거기엔 두드러지게 짙은 빛이 어렸고, 이마는 더 깊이 찌푸려져 있었다."], ["‘Madame, madame,’ said Rigaud, tapping her on the arm, as if his cruel hand were sounding a musical instrument, ‘I perceive I interest you.", "\"부인, 부인,\" 리고가 마치 잔인한 손으로 악기라도 울리듯 그녀의 팔을 톡톡 치며 말했다, \"제가 부인의 흥미를 돋우는 걸 알겠습니다."], ["I perceive I awaken your sympathy.", "제가 부인의 공감을 일깨우는 걸 알겠습니다."], ["Let us go on.’", "계속해 봅시다.\""]], [["The drooping nose and the ascending moustache had, however, to be hidden for a moment with the white hand, before he could go on; he enjoyed the effect he made so much.", "그러나 늘어진 코와 치켜올라간 콧수염은, 그가 계속하기 전에, 잠시 그 하얀 손으로 가려져야 했다. 그는 자기가 만들어 낸 효과를 무척이나 즐기고 있었던 것이다."]], [["‘The nephew, being, as the lucid Madame Flintwinch has remarked, a poor devil who has had everything but his orphan life frightened and famished out of him--the nephew abases his head, and makes response: “My uncle, it is to you to command.", "\"조카는, 그 명민하신 플린트윈치 부인이 말씀하신 대로, 고아 신세인 목숨만 빼고 모든 게 겁박당하고 굶주려 빠져나가 버린 가엾은 자라서, 머리를 조아리며 대답합니다. '삼촌, 분부대로 하겠습니다."], ["Do as you will!”", "뜻대로 하소서!'"], ["Monsieur, the uncle, does as he will.", "삼촌 되시는 분은 뜻대로 하지요."], ["It is what he always does.", "그분이 늘 하는 그대로."], ["The auspicious nuptials take place; the newly married come home to this charming mansion; the lady is received, let us suppose, by Flintwinch.", "상서로운 혼례가 거행되고, 갓 결혼한 부부가 이 매혹적인 저택으로 돌아옵니다. 부인이 맞아들여지지요, 가령, 플린트윈치에게 말입니다."], ["Hey, old intriguer?’", "어이, 늙은 음모꾼?\""]], [["Jeremiah, with his eyes upon his mistress, made no reply.", "제리마이아는 안주인에게 눈을 둔 채 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["Rigaud looked from one to the other, struck his ugly nose, and made a clucking with his tongue.", "리고는 이쪽저쪽을 보더니, 흉한 코를 치고, 혀를 차 쩝쩝 소리를 냈다."]], [["‘Soon the lady makes a singular and exciting discovery.", "\"머지않아 부인은 기이하고 충격적인 사실을 발견합니다."], ["Thereupon, full of anger, full of jealousy, full of vengeance, she forms--see you, madame!--a scheme of retribution, the weight of which she ingeniously forces her crushed husband to bear himself, as well as execute upon her enemy.", "그리하여, 분노로 가득 차고, 질투로 가득 차고, 복수심으로 가득 차서, 그녀는 짜냅니다 — 보십시오, 부인! — 응징의 계략을, 그 무게를, 짓밟힌 자기 남편으로 하여금 직접 짊어지게 하는 동시에 자기 원수에게 집행하도록 교묘하게 강요하지요."], ["What superior intelligence!’", "얼마나 빼어난 지력인지!\""]], [["‘Keep off, Jeremiah!’ cried the palpitating Affery, taking her apron from her mouth again.", "\"떨어져요, 제리마이아!\" 가슴 두근거리는 애프리가 다시 앞치마를 입에서 빼며 외쳤다."], ["‘But it was one of my dreams, that you told her, when you quarrelled with her one winter evening at dusk--there she sits and you looking at her--that she oughtn’t to have let Arthur when he come home, suspect his father only; that she had always had the strength and the power; and that she ought to have stood up more to Arthur, for his father.", "\"하지만 그것도 내 꿈 중 하나였어요. 어느 겨울 저녁 땅거미 질 무렵 당신이 그 여자와 다툴 때, 당신이 그 여자한테 말했죠 — 저기 그 여자가 앉아 있고 당신이 그 여자를 보고 있네요 — 아서 도련님이 돌아왔을 때 아버지만을 의심하도록 내버려 둬선 안 됐다고, 힘과 권세는 늘 그 여자한테 있었다고, 아버지를 위해 아서 도련님께 더 맞섰어야 했다고요."], ["It was in the same dream where you said to her that she was not--not something, but I don’t know what, for she burst out tremendous and stopped you.", "같은 꿈속에서 당신이 그 여자한테, 그 여자가 — 뭐라더라, 뭔지는 모르겠지만 — 아니라고 말했어요. 그 여자가 어마어마하게 발끈하며 당신 말을 막았으니까요."], ["You know the dream as well as I do.", "당신도 나만큼 그 꿈을 잘 알잖아요."], ["When you come down-stairs into the kitchen with the candle in your hand, and hitched my apron off my head.", "당신이 촛불을 손에 들고 부엌으로 내려와 내 머리에서 앞치마를 홱 벗겨 냈을 때 말이에요. 내가 꿈을 꾼 거라고 당신이 말했을 때요."], ["When you told me I had been dreaming.", "그 소리들을 당신이 믿지 않으려 했을 때요.\""], ["When you wouldn’t believe the noises.’ After this explosion Affery put her apron into her mouth again; always keeping her hand on the window-sill and her knee on the window-seat, ready to cry out or jump out if her lord and master approached.", "이렇게 터뜨린 뒤 애프리는 다시 앞치마를 입에 물었다. 자기 주인이자 남편이 다가오면 소리치거나 뛰어내릴 수 있도록, 늘 한 손은 창턱에, 한 무릎은 창가 자리에 둔 채로."]], [["Rigaud had not lost a word of this.", "리고는 이 말을 한마디도 놓치지 않았다."]], [["‘Haha!’ he cried, lifting his eyebrows, folding his arms, and leaning back in his chair.", "\"하하!\" 그가 눈썹을 치켜올리고, 팔짱을 끼고, 의자에 등을 기대며 외쳤다."], ["‘Assuredly, Madame Flintwinch is an oracle!", "\"분명코, 플린트윈치 부인은 신탁이로군요!"], ["How shall we interpret the oracle, you and I and the old intriguer?", "이 신탁을 어떻게 풀이할까요, 부인과 저와 늙은 음모꾼이?"], ["He said that you were not--?", "그는 부인이 — 아니라고 말했지요?"], ["And you burst out and stopped him!", "그러자 부인이 발끈하며 그를 막았다!"], ["What was it you were not?", "부인이 무엇이 아니었습니까?"], ["What is it you are not?", "부인이 무엇이 아닙니까?"], ["Say then, madame!’", "자, 말씀하시지요, 부인!\""]], [["Under this ferocious banter, she sat breathing harder, and her mouth was disturbed.", "이 사나운 조롱 아래, 그녀는 숨을 더 거칠게 몰아쉬며 앉아 있었고, 입가가 흐트러졌다."], ["Her lips quivered and opened, in spite of her utmost efforts to keep them still.", "가만히 두려는 그녀의 안간힘에도 불구하고 입술이 떨리며 벌어졌다."]], [["‘Come then, madame!", "\"자 그럼, 부인!"], ["Speak, then!", "말씀하시지요!"], ["Our old intriguer said that you were not--and you stopped him.", "우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 부인이 — 아니라고 말했고, 부인이 그를 막았습니다."], ["He was going to say that you were not--what?", "그는 부인이 — 무엇이 아니라고 말하려던 참이었지요?"], ["I know already, but I want a little confidence from you.", "저는 이미 알고 있습니다만, 부인에게서 약간의 확언을 듣고 싶습니다."], ["How, then?", "어떻습니까?"], ["You are not what?’", "부인은 무엇이 아닙니까?\""]], [["She tried again to repress herself, but broke out vehemently, ‘Not Arthur’s mother!’", "그녀는 다시 자신을 억누르려 했으나, 격렬하게 터뜨렸다. \"아서의 어머니가 아니야!\""]], [["‘Good,’ said Rigaud.", "\"좋습니다.\" 리고가 말했다."], ["‘You are amenable.’", "\"부인은 말을 들으시는군요.\""]], [["With the set expression of her face all torn away by the explosion of her passion, and with a bursting, from every rent feature, of the smouldering fire so long pent up, she cried out: ‘I will tell it myself!", "격정의 폭발로 그녀의 얼굴에 굳어 있던 표정이 죄다 뜯겨 나가고, 그토록 오래 가둬 둔 채 속으로 타오르던 불길이 갈가리 찢긴 이목구비 하나하나에서 터져 나오며, 그녀가 외쳤다. \"내가 직접 말하겠어!"], ["I will not hear it from your lips, and with the taint of your wickedness upon it.", "당신 입에서, 당신의 사악함의 오점이 묻은 채로 그걸 듣지는 않겠어."], ["Since it must be seen, I will have it seen by the light I stood in.", "기어이 드러나야 한다면, 내가 섰던 그 빛으로 드러나게 하겠어. 한마디도 더 하지 마."], ["Not another word. Hear me!’", "내 말을 들어요!\""]], [["‘Unless you are a more obstinate and more persisting woman than even I know you to be,’ Mr Flintwinch interposed, ‘you had better leave Mr Rigaud, Mr Blandois, Mr Beelzebub, to tell it in his own way.", "\"당신이 내가 아는 것보다도 더 고집 세고 더 끈질긴 여자가 아니라면,\" 플린트윈치 씨가 끼어들었다, \"리고 씨든, 블랑두아 씨든, 베엘제붑 씨든, 저 사람이 자기 방식대로 말하도록 내버려 두는 게 나을 거야."], ["What does it signify when he knows all about it?’", "저 사람이 다 알고 있는데 그게 무슨 상관이람?\""]], [["‘He does not know all about it.’", "\"저 사람은 다 아는 게 아니야.\""]], [["‘He knows all he cares about it,’ Mr Flintwinch testily urged.", "\"신경 쓰는 건 다 알지.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 짜증스레 다그쳤다."]], [["‘He does not know _me_.’", "\"저 사람은 나를 몰라.\""]], [["‘What do you suppose he cares for you, you conceited woman?’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"저 사람이 당신을 두고 뭘 신경이나 쓴다고 그래, 이 자만에 찬 여자야?\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘I tell you, Flintwinch, I will speak.", "\"플린트윈치, 분명히 말하는데, 내가 말하겠어."], ["I tell you when it has come to this, I will tell it with my own lips, and will express myself throughout it.", "분명히 말하지만, 일이 이 지경에 이른 이상, 내 입으로 직접 말하고, 처음부터 끝까지 내 식대로 표현하겠어."], ["What!", "뭐라고!"], ["Have I suffered nothing in this room, no deprivation, no imprisonment, that I should condescend at last to contemplate myself in such a glass as _that_.", "내가 이 방에서 아무것도 겪지 않았던가, 아무런 결핍도, 아무런 유폐도? 그래서 마침내 저 따위 거울에 비친 나를 들여다보는 데까지 몸을 낮춰야 한다고?"], ["Can you see him?", "저 사람이 보여요?"], ["Can you hear him?", "저 사람 말이 들려요?"], ["If your wife were a hundred times the ingrate that she is, and if I were a thousand times more hopeless than I am of inducing her to be silent if this man is silenced, I would tell it myself, before I would bear the torment of the hearing it from him.’", "당신 아내가 지금의 백 배로 배은망덕하다 해도, 그리고 저 사람을 입막음하면 그녀를 잠자코 있게 할 가망이 지금의 천 배로 없다 해도, 저 사람 입에서 그걸 듣는 고문을 견디느니 내가 직접 말하겠어.\""]], [["Rigaud pushed his chair a little back; pushed his legs out straight before him; and sat with his arms folded over against her.", "리고는 의자를 조금 뒤로 밀고, 다리를 자기 앞으로 곧게 뻗고, 팔짱을 끼고 그녀를 마주한 채 앉았다."]], [["‘You do not know what it is,’ she went on addressing him, ‘to be brought up strictly and straitly.", "\"당신은 엄격하고 빡빡하게 길러진다는 게 어떤 건지 몰라요.\" 그녀가 그에게 말을 이어 갔다."], ["I was so brought up.", "\"나는 그렇게 길러졌어요."], ["Mine was no light youth of sinful gaiety and pleasure.", "내 젊음은 죄 많은 유흥과 쾌락의 가벼운 젊음이 아니었어요."], ["Mine were days of wholesome repression, punishment, and fear.", "내 나날은 유익한 억압과 처벌과 두려움의 나날이었어요."], ["The corruption of our hearts, the evil of our ways, the curse that is upon us, the terrors that surround us--these were the themes of my childhood.", "우리 마음의 부패, 우리 행실의 악함, 우리에게 내린 저주, 우리를 둘러싼 공포 — 이런 것들이 내 유년의 주제였어요."], ["They formed my character, and filled me with an abhorrence of evil-doers.", "그것들이 내 성품을 빚었고, 악행자에 대한 혐오로 나를 가득 채웠어요."], ["When old Mr Gilbert Clennam proposed his orphan nephew to my father for my husband, my father impressed upon me that his bringing-up had been, like mine, one of severe restraint.", "늙은 길버트 클레넘 씨가 자기 고아 조카를 내 남편 삼으라고 우리 아버지께 제안했을 때, 아버지는 그의 양육 또한 나와 마찬가지로 엄혹한 억제의 양육이었음을 내게 각인시켰어요."], ["He told me, that besides the discipline his spirit had undergone, he had lived in a starved house, where rioting and gaiety were unknown, and where every day was a day of toil and trial like the last.", "아버지는 내게 말했어요, 그가 받은 정신의 훈육 외에도, 그는 굶주린 집에서 살아왔는데, 거기선 흥청거림이나 유흥이라곤 알려진 바 없고, 하루하루가 지난날과 다름없는 수고와 시련의 날이었다고요."], ["He told me that he had been a man in years long before his uncle had acknowledged him as one; and that from his school-days to that hour, his uncle’s roof has been a sanctuary to him from the contagion of the irreligious and dissolute.", "아버지는 내게 말했어요, 그는 삼촌이 그를 어른으로 인정해 주기 한참 전부터 나이로는 이미 어른이었고, 학창 시절부터 그 순간까지 삼촌의 지붕이 그에게 불경하고 방탕한 자들의 전염으로부터의 성소가 되어 주었다고요."], ["When, within a twelvemonth of our marriage, I found my husband, at that time when my father spoke of him, to have sinned against the Lord and outraged me by holding a guilty creature in my place, was I to doubt that it had been appointed to me to make the discovery, and that it was appointed to me to lay the hand of punishment upon that creature of perdition?", "우리가 결혼한 지 일 년 안에, 내 아버지가 그를 두고 말했던 바로 그 사람이, 내 자리에 죄 많은 피조물을 두어 주님께 죄짓고 나를 능멸했음을 알게 되었을 때, 그 발견을 하도록 내가 정해진 자였음을, 그리고 그 멸망의 피조물에게 처벌의 손을 얹도록 내가 정해진 자였음을 내가 의심할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["Was I to dismiss in a moment--not my own wrongs--what was I!", "한순간에 떨쳐 버릴 수 있었겠어요 — 내 자신의 억울함이 아니라 — 내가 뭐라고!"], ["but all the rejection of sin, and all the war against it, in which I had been bred?’", "— 내가 길러지며 배운 죄에 대한 그 모든 배척과, 그것에 맞선 그 모든 싸움을 말이에요?\""]], [["She laid her wrathful hand upon the watch on the table.", "그녀는 분노에 찬 손을 탁자 위의 회중시계에 얹었다."]], [["‘No! “Do not forget.”", "\"안 돼!"], ["The initials of those words are within here now, and were within here then.", "'잊지 말라.' 그 말의 머리글자들이 지금도 이 안에 있고, 그때도 이 안에 있었어요."], ["I was appointed to find the old letter that referred to them, and that told me what they meant, and whose work they were, and why they were worked, lying with this watch in his secret drawer.", "그 글자들이 가리키는 옛 편지를 찾도록 내가 정해진 자였어요. 그것들이 무슨 뜻인지, 누가 새긴 것인지, 왜 새긴 것인지를 내게 일러 준 그 편지가, 이 시계와 함께 그의 비밀 서랍에 놓여 있었지요."], ["But for that appointment there would have been no discovery.", "그 정함이 아니었다면 발견도 없었을 거예요."], ["“Do not forget.”", "'잊지 말라.'"], ["It spoke to me like a voice from an angry cloud.", "그것은 성난 구름에서 들려오는 목소리처럼 내게 말했어요."], ["Do not forget the deadly sin, do not forget the appointed discovery, do not forget the appointed suffering.", "그 죽음에 이르는 죄를 잊지 말라, 정해진 발견을 잊지 말라, 정해진 고난을 잊지 말라."], ["I did not forget.", "나는 잊지 않았어요."], ["Was it my own wrong I remembered? Mine!", "내가 기억한 게 나 자신의 억울함이었던가요?"], ["I was but a servant and a minister.", "내 것이라니! 나는 한낱 종이자 일꾼이었을 뿐."], ["What power could I have over them, but that they were bound in the bonds of their sin, and delivered to me!’", "그들이 자기 죄의 결박에 묶여 내게 넘겨졌다는 것 말고, 내가 그들에게 무슨 권세를 부릴 수 있었겠어요!\""]], [["More than forty years had passed over the grey head of this determined woman, since the time she recalled.", "이 결연한 여인이 회상하는 그때 이후로 그녀의 희끗한 머리 위로 사십 년이 넘는 세월이 지나갔다."], ["More than forty years of strife and struggle with the whisper that, by whatever name she called her vindictive pride and rage, nothing through all eternity could change their nature.", "자기가 앙심 어린 자존심과 분노를 어떤 이름으로 부르든, 영원토록 그 본질은 결코 바뀌지 않으리라는 속삭임과 다투고 씨름해 온 사십 년이 넘는 세월이."], ["Yet, gone those more than forty years, and come this Nemesis now looking her in the face, she still abided by her old impiety--still reversed the order of Creation, and breathed her own breath into a clay image of her Creator.", "그러나 그 사십 년이 넘는 세월이 흘러가고, 이제 이 응보의 여신이 그녀의 얼굴을 마주 보고 있건만, 그녀는 여전히 자신의 그 오랜 불경에 머물러 있었다 — 여전히 창조의 질서를 뒤집어, 자기 창조주의 진흙 형상에 자기 자신의 숨결을 불어넣고 있었다."], ["Verily, verily, travellers have seen many monstrous idols in many countries; but no human eyes have ever seen more daring, gross, and shocking images of the Divine nature than we creatures of the dust make in our own likenesses, of our own bad passions.", "참으로, 참으로, 여행자들은 여러 나라에서 숱한 괴물 같은 우상을 보아 왔다. 그러나 우리 티끌 같은 피조물이 우리 자신의 못된 정념으로, 우리 자신을 본떠 빚어내는 신성(神性)의 형상보다 더 대담하고, 더 야비하고, 더 충격적인 형상을 본 인간의 눈은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["‘When I forced him to give her up to me, by her name and place of abode,’ she went on in her torrent of indignation and defence; ‘when I accused her, and she fell hiding her face at my feet, was it my injury that I asserted, were they my reproaches that I poured upon her?", "\"내가 그에게서 그녀의 이름과 거처를 대게 하여 그녀를 넘겨받았을 때,\" 그녀가 분노와 항변의 격류 속에서 말을 이어 갔다, \"내가 그녀를 단죄하고, 그녀가 얼굴을 가린 채 내 발치에 쓰러졌을 때, 내가 주장한 게 내 상처였던가요?"], ["Those who were appointed of old to go to wicked kings and accuse them--were they not ministers and servants?", "내가 그녀에게 퍼부은 게 내 비난이었던가요? 옛적에 악한 왕들에게 가서 그들을 단죄하도록 정해진 이들 — 그들은 일꾼이자 종이 아니었던가요?"], ["And had not I, unworthy and far-removed from them, sin to denounce?", "그러니 그들보다 훨씬 못하고 동떨어진 나에게도, 규탄해야 할 죄가 있지 않았던가요?"], ["When she pleaded to me her youth, and his wretched and hard life (that was her phrase for the virtuous training he had belied), and the desecrated ceremony of marriage there had secretly been between them, and the terrors of want and shame that had overwhelmed them both when I was first appointed to be the instrument of their punishment, and the love (for she said the word to me, down at my feet) in which she had abandoned him and left him to me, was it _my_ enemy that became my footstool, were they the words of my wrath that made her shrink and quiver!", "그녀가 내게 자기 젊음과, 그의 비참하고 고된 삶(그것은 그가 저버린 그 덕성스러운 양육을 두고 그녀가 쓴 표현이었지요)과, 둘 사이에 몰래 있었던 모독당한 혼인 예식과, 내가 그들의 처벌의 도구로 처음 정해졌을 때 둘 다를 짓눌렀던 궁핍과 수치의 공포와, 그리고 사랑을(그녀가 내 발치에 엎드려 내게 그 말을 썼으니까요) — 그녀가 그를 버리고 내게 넘겼던 그 사랑을 호소했을 때, 내 발받침이 된 게 내 원수였던가요? 그녀를 움츠리고 떨게 만든 게 내 분노의 말이었던가요!"], ["Not unto me the strength be ascribed; not unto me the wringing of the expiation!’", "그 힘을 내게 돌리지 말지니, 그 속죄의 쥐어짜냄을 내게 돌리지 말지어다!\""]], [["Many years had come and gone since she had had the free use even of her fingers; but it was noticeable that she had already more than once struck her clenched hand vigorously upon the table, and that when she said these words she raised her whole arm in the air, as though it had been a common action with her.", "그녀가 손가락조차 자유로이 쓰지 못한 지 여러 해가 가고 또 왔건만, 주목할 만하게도 그녀는 이미 한 번 이상 움켜쥔 손으로 탁자를 힘차게 내리쳤고, 이 말을 할 때는 마치 그것이 자기에게 예사로운 동작이기라도 한 듯 팔 전체를 허공에 치켜들었다."]], [["‘And what was the repentance that was extorted from the hardness of her heart and the blackness of her depravity?", "\"그러면 그녀의 굳은 마음과 시커먼 타락에서 짜낸 그 회개란 무엇이었나요?"], ["I, vindictive and implacable?", "내가, 앙심에 차고 가차 없다고요?"], ["It may be so, to such as you who know no righteousness, and no appointment except Satan’s.", "의로움이라곤 모르고, 사탄의 정함 말고는 아무 정함도 모르는 당신 같은 자에게는 그렇게 보일지도 모르죠. 비웃어요."], ["Laugh; but I will be known as I know myself, and as Flintwinch knows me, though it is only to you and this half-witted woman.’", "하지만 나는 내가 나 자신을 아는 대로, 플린트윈치가 나를 아는 대로 알려질 거예요. 비록 당신과 이 얼간이 여자한테뿐일지라도.\""]], [["‘Add, to yourself, madame,’ said Rigaud.", "\"부인 자신에게도, 라고 덧붙이시지요.\" 리고가 말했다."], ["‘I have my little suspicions that madame is rather solicitous to be justified to herself.’", "\"부인이 오히려 자기 자신에게 정당함을 입증하려 애쓰시는 게 아닌가 하는 자그마한 의심이 제겐 있습니다.\""]], [["‘It is false.", "\"거짓말이에요."], ["It is not so.", "그렇지 않아요."], ["I have no need to be,’ she said, with great energy and anger.", "그럴 필요가 내겐 없어요.\" 그녀가 굉장한 기세와 분노로 말했다."]], [["‘Truly?’ retorted Rigaud.", "\"정말입니까?\" 리고가 받아쳤다."], ["‘Hah!’", "\"하!\""]], [["‘I ask, what was the penitence, in works, that was demanded of her?", "\"내가 묻겠어요, 그녀에게 요구된 행실로서의 참회란 무엇이었나요?"], ["“You have a child; I have none.", "'너에게는 아이가 있고, 내게는 없다."], ["You love that child.", "너는 그 아이를 사랑한다."], ["Give him to me.", "그를 내게 다오."], ["He shall believe himself to be my son, and he shall be believed by every one to be my son.", "그는 자신이 내 아들이라고 믿게 될 것이며, 모든 이가 그를 내 아들로 믿게 될 것이다."], ["To save you from exposure, his father shall swear never to see or communicate with you more; equally to save him from being stripped by his uncle, and to save your child from being a beggar, you shall swear never to see or communicate with either of them more.", "너의 들통남을 막기 위해, 그의 아버지는 다시는 너를 보거나 너와 연락하지 않겠다고 맹세할 것이다. 마찬가지로 그가 삼촌에게 모든 걸 빼앗기는 것을 막고, 너의 아이가 거지가 되는 것을 막기 위해, 너는 다시는 둘 중 누구도 보거나 연락하지 않겠다고 맹세할 것이다."], ["That done, and your present means, derived from my husband, renounced, I charge myself with your support.", "그렇게 하고, 내 남편에게서 나오는 너의 현재 생계 수단을 단념하면, 나는 너의 부양을 떠맡겠다."], ["You may, with your place of retreat unknown, then leave, if you please, uncontradicted by me, the lie that when you passed out of all knowledge but mine, you merited a good name.”", "그러면 너는, 네 은신처를 아무도 모르게 한 채, 원한다면, 네가 나 말고는 누구의 기억에서도 사라졌을 때 좋은 평판을 얻을 만했다는 그 거짓을 내가 반박하지 않은 채로 남겨 두고 떠나도 좋다.'"], ["That was all.", "그게 다였어요."], ["She had to sacrifice her sinful and shameful affections; no more.", "그녀는 자기 죄 많고 수치스러운 정을 희생하기만 하면 됐어요. 그뿐."], ["She was then free to bear her load of guilt in secret, and to break her heart in secret; and through such present misery (light enough for her, I think!) to purchase her redemption from endless misery, if she could.", "그러면 그녀는 자유로이 자기 죄의 짐을 남몰래 짊어지고, 남몰래 가슴이 찢어지면 됐어요. 그리고 그런 현재의 비참함을(내 생각엔 그녀에게 충분히 가벼운 것이었지요!) 통하여, 할 수만 있다면 끝없는 비참함으로부터의 구원을 사들이면 됐어요."], ["If, in this, I punished her here, did I not open to her a way hereafter?", "이 일로 내가 그녀를 이승에서 처벌했다 한들, 저승으로 가는 길을 그녀에게 열어 주지 않았던가요?"], ["If she knew herself to be surrounded by insatiable vengeance and unquenchable fires, were they mine?", "그녀가 자신이 채울 길 없는 복수와 꺼지지 않는 불길에 둘러싸여 있음을 알았다 한들, 그것이 내 것이었던가요?"], ["If I threatened her, then and afterwards, with the terrors that encompassed her, did I hold them in my right hand?’", "내가 그때나 그 후에나, 그녀를 에워싼 그 공포로 그녀를 위협했다 한들, 내가 그것들을 내 오른손에 쥐고 있었던가요?\""]], [["She turned the watch upon the table, and opened it, and, with an unsoftening face, looked at the worked letters within.", "그녀는 탁자 위의 시계를 돌려 열고는, 누그러짐 없는 얼굴로 그 안에 새겨진 글자들을 바라보았다."]], [["‘They did _not_ forget.", "\"그들은 잊지 않았어요."], ["It is appointed against such offences that the offenders shall not be able to forget.", "이런 잘못에 대해서는 잘못한 자들이 잊을 수 없도록 정해진 거예요."], ["If the presence of Arthur was a daily reproach to his father, and if the absence of Arthur was a daily agony to his mother, that was the just dispensation of Jehovah.", "아서의 존재가 그의 아버지에게 날마다의 책망이었고, 아서의 부재가 그의 어머니에게 날마다의 고통이었다면, 그것은 여호와의 공의로운 처분이었어요."], ["As well might it be charged upon me, that the stings of an awakened conscience drove her mad, and that it was the will of the Disposer of all things that she should live so, many years.", "깨어난 양심의 가시가 그녀를 미치게 만들었다는 것도, 그녀가 그렇게 여러 해를 살도록 한 것이 만물을 주관하시는 분의 뜻이었다는 것도, 내 탓으로 돌릴 수 없기는 마찬가지예요."], ["I devoted myself to reclaim the otherwise predestined and lost boy; to give him the reputation of an honest origin; to bring him up in fear and trembling, and in a life of practical contrition for the sins that were heavy on his head before his entrance into this condemned world.", "나는 달리 예정되어 잃어버린 그 아이를 되찾는 데, 그에게 떳떳한 혈통의 평판을 주는 데, 그를 두려움과 떨림 속에서, 그리고 이 단죄받은 세상에 들어오기도 전에 그 머리 위에 무겁게 얹혀 있던 죄들에 대한 실천적 통회의 삶으로 길러 내는 데 나를 바쳤어요."], ["Was that a cruelty?", "그게 잔인한 일이었나요?"], ["Was I, too, not visited with consequences of the original offence in which I had no complicity?", "나 또한, 내가 가담하지도 않은 그 원죄의 결과를 겪지 않았던가요?"], ["Arthur’s father and I lived no further apart, with half the globe between us, than when we were together in this house.", "아서의 아버지와 나는, 지구 반 바퀴를 사이에 두고 떨어져 있던 때나, 이 집에서 함께 있던 때나, 조금도 더 멀리 떨어져 산 게 아니었어요."], ["He died, and sent this watch back to me, with its Do not forget.", "그는 죽으며 이 시계를 그 '잊지 말라'와 함께 내게 돌려보냈어요."], ["I do NOT forget, though I do not read it as he did.", "나는 잊지 않아요, 비록 그가 읽은 것과 똑같이 읽지는 않지만요."], ["I read in it, that I was appointed to do these things.", "나는 그 안에서, 이 일들을 행하도록 내가 정해진 자였음을 읽어요."], ["I have so read these three letters since I have had them lying on this table, and I did so read them, with equal distinctness, when they were thousands of miles away.’", "이 글자들이 이 탁자 위에 놓여 있게 된 이래로 나는 그렇게 읽어 왔고, 그것들이 수천 마일 떨어져 있을 때도 똑같이 또렷하게 그렇게 읽었어요.\""]], [["As she took the watch-case in her hand, with that new freedom in the use of her hand of which she showed no consciousness whatever, bending her eyes upon it as if she were defying it to move her, Rigaud cried with a loud and contemptuous snapping of his fingers.", "그녀가 손에 자유가 생긴 데 대한 자각이라곤 전혀 내보이지 않은 채, 그 새로운 자유로움으로 시계갑을 손에 쥐고, 마치 그것이 자기를 움직일 테면 움직여 보라고 도전하듯 그 위에 눈을 박는 동안, 리고가 손가락을 요란하고 경멸스럽게 탁 튕기며 외쳤다."], ["‘Come, madame!", "\"자, 부인!"], ["Time runs out.", "시간이 다 됐습니다."], ["Come, lady of piety, it must be!", "자, 경건하신 부인, 어쩔 수 없는 일이지요!"], ["You can tell nothing I don’t know.", "부인은 제가 모르는 건 아무것도 말씀하지 못합니다."], ["Come to the money stolen, or I will!", "도둑맞은 돈 얘기로 오시지요, 아니면 제가 하겠습니다!"], ["Death of my soul, I have had enough of your other jargon.", "내 영혼이 죽을 노릇이지, 부인의 그 다른 횡설수설은 이제 충분합니다."], ["Come straight to the stolen money!’", "곧장 도둑맞은 돈 얘기로 오시지요!\""]], [["‘Wretch that you are,’ she answered, and now her hands clasped her head: ‘through what fatal error of Flintwinch’s, through what incompleteness on his part, who was the only other person helping in these things and trusted with them, through whose and what bringing together of the ashes of a burnt paper, you have become possessed of that codicil, I know no more than how you acquired the rest of your power here--’", "\"이 흉악한 자야,\" 그녀가 대답했고, 이제 두 손은 자기 머리를 움켜쥐고 있었다. \"플린트윈치의 어떤 치명적인 실수로, 이 일들을 거든 유일한 다른 사람이자 그것들을 맡았던 그의 어떤 미진함으로, 누가, 무엇으로 태운 서류의 재를 그러모은 일로, 당신이 그 추가 유언을 손에 넣게 되었는지, 나는 당신이 여기서 가진 나머지 위력을 어떻게 얻었는지 아는 것 이상으로는 모르지만 —\""]], [["‘And yet,’ interrupted Rigaud, ‘it is my odd fortune to have by me, in a convenient place that I know of, that same short little addition to the will of Monsieur Gilbert Clennam, written by a lady and witnessed by the same lady and our old intriguer!", "\"그런데도,\" 리고가 말을 끊었다, \"제가 아는 어느 편리한 곳에 그것을, 길버트 클레넘 씨의 유언에 더해진 바로 그 짧은 부록을 가지고 있다는 게 제 묘한 운수지요."], ["Ah, bah, old intriguer, crooked little puppet!", "한 부인이 쓰고, 같은 부인과 우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 증인을 선 그것을요! 아, 바, 늙은 음모꾼, 비뚜름한 작은 꼭두각시!"], ["Madame, let us go on.", "부인, 계속합시다. 시간이 촉박합니다."], ["Time presses. You or I to finish?’", "부인이 끝내시겠습니까, 제가 끝내겠습니까?\""]], [["‘I!’ she answered, with increased determination, if it were possible.", "\"내가!\" 그녀가, 가능하다면 한층 더해진 결의로 대답했다."], ["‘I, because I will not endure to be shown myself, and have myself shown to any one, with your horrible distortion upon me.", "\"내가 하겠어, 당신의 그 끔찍한 왜곡을 뒤집어쓴 채 나 자신을 보이고, 누구에게든 나 자신이 보여지는 걸 견딜 수 없으니까."], ["You, with your practices of infamous foreign prisons and galleys would make it the money that impelled me.", "당신은, 악명 높은 외국 감옥과 갤리선에서 익힌 그 짓거리들로, 마치 돈이 나를 움직인 것처럼 만들겠지."], ["It was not the money.’", "돈이 아니었어.\""]], [["‘Bah, bah, bah!", "\"바, 바, 바!"], ["I repudiate, for the moment, my politeness, and say, Lies, lies, lies.", "잠시 제 정중함은 거두고 말하지요, 거짓말, 거짓말, 거짓말입니다."], ["You know you suppressed the deed and kept the money.’", "부인이 그 증서를 숨기고 돈을 챙긴 걸 부인 자신이 알지 않습니까.\""]], [["‘Not for the money’s sake, wretch!’ She made a struggle as if she were starting up; even as if, in her vehemence, she had almost risen on her disabled feet.", "\"돈을 위해서가 아니었어, 흉악한 자야!\" 그녀가 마치 벌떡 일어서기라도 할 듯, 심지어 그 격렬함 속에서 못 쓰는 발을 딛고 거의 일어선 듯 안간힘을 썼다."], ["‘If Gilbert Clennam, reduced to imbecility, at the point of death, and labouring under the delusion of some imaginary relenting towards a girl of whom he had heard that his nephew had once had a fancy for her which he had crushed out of him, and that she afterwards drooped away into melancholy and withdrawal from all who knew her--if, in that state of weakness, he dictated to me, whose life she had darkened with her sin, and who had been appointed to know her wickedness from her own hand and her own lips, a bequest meant as a recompense to her for supposed unmerited suffering; was there no difference between my spurning that injustice, and coveting mere money--a thing which you, and your comrades in the prisons, may steal from anyone?’", "\"길버트 클레넘이 치매에 이르러 죽음의 문턱에서, 자기 조카가 한때 어떤 처녀를 마음에 두었다가 그 마음을 짓눌러 없앴고, 그 처녀가 그 뒤 우울 속으로 시들어 그녀를 알던 모든 이에게서 물러나 버렸다는 말을 듣고는, 그 처녀를 향한 어떤 상상 속 마음의 누그러짐이라는 망상에 사로잡혀서, 그 무력한 상태에서, 그녀의 죄로 내 삶을 어둡게 한, 그리고 그녀의 사악함을 그녀 자신의 손과 입으로 알도록 정해졌던 나에게, 부당하다고 여겨진 고통에 대한 보상으로 의도된 유증을 받아쓰게 했다면, 그 불의를 내가 물리친 것과, 한낱 돈을 — 당신과 당신의 감옥 동료들이 아무에게서나 훔쳐 낼 수 있는 그 따위 것을 탐낸 것 사이에 아무 차이도 없었단 말이야?\""]], [["‘Time presses, madame.", "\"시간이 촉박합니다, 부인."], ["Take care!’", "조심하시지요!\""]], [["‘If this house was blazing from the roof to the ground,’ she returned, ‘I would stay in it to justify myself against my righteous motives being classed with those of stabbers and thieves.’", "\"이 집이 지붕에서 바닥까지 활활 타오른다 해도,\" 그녀가 받아쳤다, \"나는 내 의로운 동기가 칼잡이며 도둑들의 동기와 한데 묶이는 데 맞서 나 자신을 변호하기 위해 이 안에 머물겠어.\""]], [["Rigaud snapped his fingers tauntingly in her face.", "리고는 그녀의 얼굴에 대고 조롱하듯 손가락을 탁 튕겼다."], ["‘One thousand guineas to the little beauty you slowly hunted to death.", "\"부인이 서서히 죽음으로 몰아간 그 작은 미인에게 천 기니."], ["One thousand guineas to the youngest daughter her patron might have at fifty, or (if he had none) brother’s youngest daughter, on her coming of age, “as the remembrance his disinterestedness may like best, of his protection of a friendless young orphan girl.”", "그 후원자가 쉰에 두었을 가장 어린 딸에게, 혹은(딸이 없다면) 동생의 가장 어린 딸에게, 그녀가 성년이 될 때 천 기니. '의지가지없는 어린 고아 처녀를 보호해 준 데 대한, 그의 사심 없음의 가장 마음에 드는 기념으로서'."], ["Two thousand guineas.", "이천 기니. 뭐라고요!"], ["What! You will never come to the money?’", "부인은 도무지 돈 얘기로 오지 않으시는군요?\""]], [["‘That patron,’ she was vehemently proceeding, when he checked her.", "\"그 후원자가,\" 그녀가 격렬하게 이어 가려는데 그가 가로막았다."]], [["‘Names!", "\"이름을!"], ["Call him Mr Frederick Dorrit.", "그를 프레더릭 도릿 씨라고 부르시지요."], ["No more evasions.’", "더는 둘러대지 마시고.\""]], [["‘That Frederick Dorrit was the beginning of it all.", "\"그 프레더릭 도릿이 이 모든 일의 시작이었어."], ["If he had not been a player of music, and had not kept, in those days of his youth and prosperity, an idle house where singers, and players, and such-like children of Evil turned their backs on the Light and their faces to the Darkness, she might have remained in her lowly station, and might not have been raised out of it to be cast down. But, no.", "그가 음악 연주자만 아니었더라면, 그리고 그 젊고 번창하던 시절에, 가수며 악사며 그런 따위의 악의 자식들이 빛에 등을 돌리고 어둠에 얼굴을 향하는 그 게으른 집을 두지만 않았더라면, 그녀는 자기 비천한 처지에 머물러 있었을 테고, 거기서 끌어올려졌다가 다시 내동댕이쳐지지도 않았을 거야."], ["Satan entered into that Frederick Dorrit, and counselled him that he was a man of innocent and laudable tastes who did kind actions, and that here was a poor girl with a voice for singing music with.", "하지만, 아니지. 사탄이 그 프레더릭 도릿 속으로 들어가, 그가 선행을 베푸는, 순결하고 칭송할 만한 취향의 사내라고, 여기 노래할 목소리를 지닌 가엾은 처녀가 있다고 부추겼어."], ["Then he is to have her taught.", "그러더니 그는 그녀를 가르치게 하지."], ["Then Arthur’s father, who has all along been secretly pining in the ways of virtuous ruggedness for those accursed snares which are called the Arts, becomes acquainted with her.", "그러더니, 줄곧 덕성스러운 험난함의 길에서 그 예술이라 불리는 저주받은 올무들을 남몰래 갈망해 온 아서의 아버지가 그녀와 알게 돼. 그렇게, 가수가 되려고 수련하던 한 추레한 고아가, 그 프레더릭 도릿의 주선으로 나를 이기고, 나는 낮춰지고 기만당했어!"], ["And so, a graceless orphan, training to be a singing girl, carries it, by that Frederick Dorrit’s agency, against me, and I am humbled and deceived!--Not I, that is to say,’ she added quickly, as colour flushed into her face; ‘a greater than I.", "— 그러니까, 내가 아니라,\" 얼굴에 핏기가 솟구치자 그녀가 얼른 덧붙였다, \"나보다 더 크신 분이."], ["What am I?’", "내가 뭐라고?\""]], [["Jeremiah Flintwinch, who had been gradually screwing himself towards her, and who was now very near her elbow without her knowing it, made a specially wry face of objection when she said these words, and moreover twitched his gaiters, as if such pretensions were equivalent to little barbs in his legs.", "그동안 차츰차츰 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 비틀어 다가와, 그녀도 모르는 사이에 이제 그녀의 팔꿈치 바로 곁까지 와 있던 제리마이아 플린트윈치는, 그녀가 이 말을 하자 유난히 일그러진 못마땅한 표정을 지었고, 게다가 마치 그런 주장이 자기 다리에 박히는 작은 가시라도 되는 양 각반을 씰룩거렸다."]], [["‘Lastly,’ she continued, ‘for I am at the end of these things, and I will say no more of them, and you shall say no more of them, and all that remains will be to determine whether the knowledge of them can be kept among us who are here present; lastly, when I suppressed that paper, with the knowledge of Arthur’s father--’", "\"마지막으로,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다, \"이 일들도 이제 끝이고, 나는 이에 대해 더는 말하지 않겠고, 당신도 더는 말하지 않을 것이며, 남은 건 오직 이 일들에 대한 앎이 여기 모인 우리끼리만 간직될 수 있을지를 정하는 것뿐이니까. 마지막으로, 내가 아서의 아버지가 아는 가운데 그 서류를 숨겼을 때 —\""]], [["‘But not with his consent, you know,’ said Mr Flintwinch.", "\"하지만 그의 동의를 받고는 아니었지, 알다시피.\" 플린트윈치 씨가 말했다."]], [["‘Who said with his consent?’ She started to find Jeremiah so near her, and drew back her head, looking at him with some rising distrust.", "\"누가 그의 동의를 받았다고 했어?\" 제리마이아가 그렇게 가까이 있는 것에 그녀는 흠칫 놀라, 고개를 뒤로 빼고는 솟아나는 불신의 빛으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["‘You were often enough between us when he would have had me produce it and I would not, to have contradicted me if I had said, with his consent.", "\"그가 그걸 내보이라 했으나 내가 그러지 않으려 했을 때, 우리 사이에 당신이 끼어 있던 적이 충분히 많았으니, 내가 '그의 동의를 받고'라고 말했다면 당신이 반박했겠지."], ["I say, when I suppressed that paper, I made no effort to destroy it, but kept it by me, here in this house, many years.", "분명히 말하는데, 내가 그 서류를 숨겼을 때, 나는 그것을 없애려는 어떤 시도도 하지 않고, 여러 해 동안 여기 이 집에서 곁에 두었어."], ["The rest of the Gilbert property being left to Arthur’s father, I could at any time, without unsettling more than the two sums, have made a pretence of finding it.", "길버트 재산의 나머지가 아서의 아버지에게 남겨졌으니, 나는 언제든, 그 두 액수 말고는 더 흔들지 않으면서, 그것을 찾아낸 척할 수 있었어."], ["But, besides that I must have supported such pretence by a direct falsehood (a great responsibility), I have seen no new reason, in all the time I have been tried here, to bring it to light.", "하지만 그런 척을 직접적인 거짓으로(큰 책임이지) 떠받쳐야 했을 거라는 점 말고도, 내가 여기서 시련을 겪어 온 그 모든 세월 동안, 그것을 밝힐 어떤 새로운 이유도 보지 못했어. 그것은 죄에 상을 주는 일이었고, 망상의 그릇된 결과였어."], ["It was a rewarding of sin; the wrong result of a delusion.", "나는 내가 정해진 일을 행했고, 이 네 벽 안에서, 내가 겪도록 정해진 것을 겪어 왔어."], ["I did what I was appointed to do, and I have undergone, within these four walls, what I was appointed to undergo.", "마침내 그 서류가 — 내 생각엔 — 내 앞에서 없애졌을 때, 그녀는 이미 오래전에 죽었고, 그녀의 후원자 프레더릭 도릿은 오래전에 마땅하게도 파산하고 치매에 이르렀어. 그에게는 딸이 없었어."], ["When the paper was at last destroyed--as I thought--in my presence, she had long been dead, and her patron, Frederick Dorrit, had long been deservedly ruined and imbecile.", "나는 그 전에 조카딸을 찾아냈고, 내가 그녀를 위해 한 일은, 그녀에게 아무 득도 되지 않았을 그 돈보다 훨씬 더 그녀에게 나은 것이었어.\" 그녀는 잠시 후, 마치 시계에 대고 말하듯 덧붙였다."], ["He had no daughter.", "\"그녀 자신은 죄가 없었지."], ["I had found the niece before then; and what I did for her, was better for her far than the money of which she would have had no good.’ She added, after a moment, as though she addressed the watch: ‘She herself was innocent, and I might not have forgotten to relinquish it to her at my death:’ and sat looking at it.", "어쩌면 내 죽음에 이르러 그것을 그녀에게 넘겨주는 걸 잊지 않았을지도 모르지.\" 그러고는 그것을 바라보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["‘Shall I recall something to you, worthy madame?’ said Rigaud.", "\"제가 부인께 무언가를 상기시켜 드릴까요, 존경할 만한 부인?\""], ["‘The little paper was in this house on the night when our friend the prisoner--jail-comrade of my soul--came home from foreign countries.", "리고가 말했다. \"그 작은 서류는, 우리의 벗인 죄수가 — 내 영혼의 옥살이 동무가 — 외국에서 집으로 돌아오던 그날 밤, 이 집에 있었습니다."], ["Shall I recall yet something more to you?", "무언가 더 상기시켜 드릴까요?"], ["The little singing-bird that never was fledged, was long kept in a cage by a guardian of your appointing, well enough known to our old intriguer here.", "미처 깃털도 나지 않은 그 작은 노래하는 새는, 부인이 지정한 어느 보호자에 의해 오랫동안 새장에 갇혀 있었지요. 여기 우리의 늙은 음모꾼이 익히 잘 아는 자에게요."], ["Shall we coax our old intriguer to tell us when he saw him last?’", "우리의 늙은 음모꾼을 구슬려, 그가 그자를 마지막으로 본 게 언제인지 말하게 해 볼까요?\""]], [["‘I’ll tell you!’ cried Affery, unstopping her mouth.", "\"내가 말하겠어요!\" 애프리가 입을 떼며 외쳤다."], ["‘I dreamed it, first of all my dreams.", "\"그게 내 모든 꿈 중에 맨 처음 꾼 꿈이었어요."], ["Jeremiah, if you come a-nigh me now, I’ll scream to be heard at St Paul’s!", "제리마이아, 지금 나한테 가까이 오면, 세인트폴 성당까지 들리게 비명을 지를 거예요!"], ["The person as this man has spoken of, was Jeremiah’s own twin brother; and he was here in the dead of the night, on the night when Arthur come home, and Jeremiah with his own hands give him this paper, along with I don’t know what more, and he took it away in an iron box--Help!", "이 사람이 말한 그 사람은 제리마이아 자신의 쌍둥이 형제였어요. 그리고 그는 아서 도련님이 집에 돌아오던 날 밤, 한밤중에 여기 있었고, 제리마이아가 제 손으로 그에게 이 서류를, 또 뭔지 모를 다른 것들과 함께 건넸고, 그는 그걸 쇠 상자에 담아 가져갔어요 — 사람 살려!"], ["Murder!", "사람 죽인다!"], ["Save me from Jere-_mi_-ah!’", "제리—마이—아한테서 날 구해 줘요!\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch had made a run at her, but Rigaud had caught him in his arms midway.", "플린트윈치 씨가 그녀에게 달려들었으나, 리고가 중간에서 그를 팔로 붙잡았다."], ["After a moment’s wrestle with him, Flintwinch gave up, and put his hands in his pockets.", "잠시 그와 몸싸움을 벌인 끝에, 플린트윈치는 단념하고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣었다."]], [["‘What!’ cried Rigaud, rallying him as he poked and jerked him back with his elbows, ‘assault a lady with such a genius for dreaming!", "\"뭐라고!\" 리고가 팔꿈치로 그를 쿡쿡 찌르고 홱홱 밀어내며 그를 놀려 댔다."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "\"꿈꾸는 데 저런 재주를 지닌 부인께 손찌검을 하다니!"], ["Why, she’ll be a fortune to you as an exhibition.", "하, 하, 하! 원, 이 부인은 구경거리로 내세우면 당신에게 한밑천이 되겠소."], ["All that she dreams comes true.", "부인이 꿈꾸는 건 죄다 사실로 드러나니."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["You’re so like him, Little Flintwinch.", "당신은 그 사람을 어찌나 닮았는지, 작은 플린트윈치."], ["So like him, as I knew him (when I first spoke English for him to the host) in the Cabaret of the Three Billiard Tables, in the little street of the high roofs, by the wharf at Antwerp!", "내가 알던 그 사람을 어찌나 닮았는지(주인에게 그를 위해 처음 영어로 통역해 줬을 때 말이오), 앤트워프 부두 곁, 높은 지붕들의 그 작은 골목에 있는 '당구대 셋'이라는 술집에서요!"], ["Ah, but he was a brave boy to drink. Ah, but he was a brave boy to smoke!", "아, 정말이지 그는 술을 들이켜는 데는 용감한 친구였지."], ["Ah, but he lived in a sweet bachelor-apartment--furnished, on the fifth floor, above the wood and charcoal merchant’s, and the dress-maker’s, and the chair-maker’s, and the maker of tubs--where I knew him too, and wherewith his cognac and tobacco, he had twelve sleeps a day and one fit, until he had a fit too much, and ascended to the skies.", "아, 정말이지 그는 담배를 피우는 데도 용감한 친구였고! 아, 정말이지 그는 어느 아늑한 독신자 거처에서 살았어 — 가구 딸린, 오 층에, 장작과 숯 장수와, 양재사와, 의자 장수와, 통 만드는 자 위층에 말이지 — 거기서 나도 그를 알았고, 그곳에서 그는 코냑과 담배로 하루에 열두 번 잠을 자고 발작을 한 번 일으켰는데, 마침내 발작을 한 번 너무 많이 일으켜 하늘로 올라가 버렸지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["What does it matter how I took possession of the papers in his iron box?", "내가 그의 쇠 상자에 든 서류들을 어떻게 손에 넣었는지가 무슨 상관이오?"], ["Perhaps he confided it to my hands for you, perhaps it was locked and my curiosity was piqued, perhaps I suppressed it.", "어쩌면 그가 부인을 위해 그것을 내 손에 맡겼을 수도, 어쩌면 그게 잠겨 있어 내 호기심이 동했을 수도, 어쩌면 내가 그걸 가로챘을 수도 있지."], ["Ha, ha, ha!", "하, 하, 하!"], ["What does it matter, so that I have it safe?", "내가 그걸 안전하게 가지고 있으니, 무슨 상관이오?"], ["We are not particular here; hey, Flintwinch?", "우리는 여기서 까다롭게 굴지 않잖나, 어이, 플린트윈치?"], ["We are not particular here; is it not so, madame?’", "우리는 여기서 까다롭게 굴지 않지요, 안 그렇습니까, 부인?\""]], [["Retiring before him with vicious counter-jerks of his own elbows, Mr Flintwinch had got back into his corner, where he now stood with his hands in his pockets, taking breath, and returning Mrs Clennam’s stare.", "플린트윈치 씨는 자기 팔꿈치로 사납게 맞받아 홱홱 밀어내며 그의 앞에서 물러나, 다시 자기 구석으로 돌아갔다. 거기서 그는 이제 두 손을 주머니에 넣고 서서 숨을 고르며, 클레넘 부인의 응시를 되받아 보았다."], ["‘Ha, ha, ha!", "\"하, 하, 하!"], ["But what’s this?’ cried Rigaud.", "한데 이게 어찌 된 일이오?\""], ["‘It appears as if you don’t know, one the other.", "리고가 외쳤다. \"두 분이 서로를 모르는 듯하군요."], ["Permit me, Madame Clennam who suppresses, to present Monsieur Flintwinch who intrigues.’", "은폐하시는 클레넘 부인께, 음모를 꾸미시는 플린트윈치 씨를 소개하게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["Mr Flintwinch, unpocketing one of his hands to scrape his jaw, advanced a step or so in that attitude, still returning Mrs Clennam’s look, and thus addressed her:", "플린트윈치 씨는 턱을 긁으려고 한 손을 주머니에서 꺼내, 그 자세로 한두 걸음 나아오며, 여전히 클레넘 부인의 시선을 되받은 채 그녀에게 이렇게 말을 건넸다."]], [["‘Now, I know what you mean by opening your eyes so wide at me, but you needn’t take the trouble, because I don’t care for it.", "\"자, 당신이 나한테 그렇게 눈을 크게 뜨는 게 무슨 뜻인지 나도 알지만, 수고할 것 없어, 나는 그딴 거 신경 안 쓰니까."], ["I’ve been telling you for how many years that you’re one of the most opinionated and obstinate of women.", "내가 당신한테 몇 해 동안 말해 왔잖아, 당신이 여자들 중에 가장 고집 세고 가장 완고한 축에 든다고."], ["That’s what _you_ are.", "그게 바로 당신이야."], ["You call yourself humble and sinful, but you are the most Bumptious of your sex.", "당신은 스스로 겸손하고 죄 많은 사람이라 부르지만, 당신은 당신 성별 중에 가장 거만한 사람이야."], ["That’s what _you_ are.", "그게 바로 당신이라고."], ["I have told you, over and over again when we have had a tiff, that you wanted to make everything go down before you, but I wouldn’t go down before you--that you wanted to swallow up everybody alive, but I wouldn’t be swallowed up alive.", "우리가 다툴 때마다 내가 당신한테 거듭거듭 말했지, 당신은 모든 걸 당신 앞에 굴복시키고 싶어 하지만 나는 당신 앞에 굴복하지 않겠다고 — 당신은 모두를 산 채로 삼켜 버리고 싶어 하지만 나는 산 채로 삼켜지지 않겠다고."], ["Why didn’t you destroy the paper when you first laid hands upon it?", "왜 처음 그 서류를 손에 넣었을 때 없애지 않았어? 내가 그러라고 권했잖아."], ["I advised you to; but no, it’s not your way to take advice.", "하지만 안 되지, 충고를 받아들이는 건 당신 방식이 아니니까."], ["You must keep it forsooth.", "굳이 그걸 간직해야 했지."], ["Perhaps you may carry it out at some other time, forsooth.", "어쩌면 언젠가 그걸 실행에 옮길지도 모른다, 굳이 그러면서."], ["As if I didn’t know better than that!", "마치 내가 그보다 더 잘 모르기라도 하는 것처럼!"], ["I think I see your pride carrying it out, with a chance of being suspected of having kept it by you.", "당신의 그 자존심이, 그걸 곁에 간직해 온 게 의심받을 위험을 무릅쓰면서 그걸 실행에 옮기는 꼴이 눈에 선해."], ["But that’s the way you cheat yourself.", "하지만 그게 당신이 자기 자신을 속이는 방식이지."], ["Just as you cheat yourself into making out that you didn’t do all this business because you were a rigorous woman, all slight, and spite, and power, and unforgiveness, but because you were a servant and a minister, and were appointed to do it.", "꼭 당신이, 이 모든 일을 한 게 당신이 엄혹한 여자라서, 죄다 멸시와 앙심과 권세와 용서 없음 때문이 아니라, 당신이 종이자 일꾼이며 그걸 하도록 정해진 자라서라고 자기를 속여 우겨 대듯이 말이야. 당신이 뭐라고, 그걸 하도록 정해졌다는 거야?"], ["Who are you, that you should be appointed to do it?", "그게 당신 신앙일지는 몰라도, 나한텐 헛소리야."], ["That may be your religion, but it’s my gammon.", "그리고 이왕 말 나온 김에 진실을 다 말해 주자면,\""], ["And to tell you all the truth while I am about it,’ said Mr Flintwinch, crossing his arms, and becoming the express image of irascible doggedness, ‘I have been rasped--rasped these forty years--by your taking such high ground even with me, who knows better; the effect of it being coolly to put me on low ground.", "플린트윈치 씨가 팔짱을 끼며 성마른 외고집 그 자체의 형상이 되어 말했다, \"나는 짜증이 나 왔어 — 이 사십 년 동안 짜증이 나 왔다고 — 더 잘 아는 나한테까지 당신이 그렇게 고상한 자리를 차지하는 통에 말이야. 그 결과 나는 보란 듯이 낮은 자리에 놓이게 됐지."], ["I admire you very much; you are a woman of strong head and great talent; but the strongest head, and the greatest talent, can’t rasp a man for forty years without making him sore.", "나는 당신을 무척 우러러봐. 당신은 머리가 좋고 재주가 대단한 여자야."], ["So I don’t care for your present eyes. Now, I am coming to the paper, and mark what I say.", "하지만 제아무리 좋은 머리에 제아무리 대단한 재주라도, 사십 년 동안 사람을 짜증 나게 하면서 그를 쓰라리게 하지 않을 수는 없는 법이야."], ["You put it away somewhere, and you kept your own counsel where.", "그러니 지금 당신 눈초리 따위 신경 안 써. 자, 이제 그 서류 얘기로 들어가니, 내 말을 새겨들어."], ["You’re an active woman at that time, and if you want to get that paper, you can get it.", "당신은 그걸 어딘가에 치워 두고, 어디인지는 혼자만 알았지. 그때 당신은 활동적인 여자라, 그 서류가 갖고 싶으면 가질 수 있었어."], ["But, mark.", "하지만 새겨들어."], ["There comes a time when you are struck into what you are now, and then if you want to get that paper, you can’t get it.", "당신이 지금 같은 꼴이 되는 때가 오고, 그러면 그 서류가 갖고 싶어도 가질 수가 없게 되지."], ["So it lies, long years, in its hiding-place.", "그래서 그건 그 은닉처에서 오랜 세월 누워 있었어."], ["At last, when we are expecting Arthur home every day, and when any day may bring him home, and it’s impossible to say what rummaging he may make about the house, I recommend you five thousand times, if you can’t get at it, to let me get at it, that it may be put in the fire.", "마침내, 우리가 아서가 날마다 돌아오기를 기다리고, 어느 날이라도 그가 돌아올 수 있고, 그가 집 안을 뒤지며 무슨 일을 벌일지 알 수 없게 됐을 때, 나는 당신한테 오천 번도 더 권했어, 당신이 그걸 손에 넣을 수 없거든 내가 손에 넣게 해 달라고, 불에 던져 버리게 말이야."], ["But no--no one but you knows where it is, and that’s power; and, call yourself whatever humble names you will, I call you a female Lucifer in appetite for power!", "하지만 안 되지 — 그게 어디 있는지는 당신 말고는 아무도 모르고, 그게 권세니까. 그리고 당신이 자기를 무슨 겸손한 이름으로 부르든, 나는 당신을 권세에 굶주린 여자 루시퍼라 부른다!"], ["On a Sunday night, Arthur comes home.", "어느 일요일 밤, 아서가 집에 돌아오지."], ["He has not been in this room ten minutes, when he speaks of his father’s watch.", "그가 이 방에 들어온 지 십 분도 안 돼서, 자기 아버지의 시계 얘기를 꺼내."], ["You know very well that the Do Not Forget, at the time when his father sent that watch to you, could only mean, the rest of the story being then all dead and over, Do Not Forget the suppression.", "당신도 잘 알다시피, 그의 아버지가 그 시계를 당신한테 보냈을 때의 그 '잊지 말라'는, 나머지 이야기는 그때 이미 다 죽어 끝나 버렸으니, 오직 그 은폐를 잊지 말라는 뜻일 수밖에 없었어."], ["Make restitution!", "돌려놓으라는!"], ["Arthur’s ways have frightened you a bit, and the paper shall be burnt after all.", "아서의 태도에 당신은 좀 겁을 먹고, 그래서 그 서류를 결국 태워 버리기로 하지. 그래서, 저 깡충대는 화냥년 이세벨이,\""], ["So, before that jumping jade and Jezebel,’ Mr Flintwinch grinned at his wife, ‘has got you into bed, you at last tell me where you have put the paper, among the old ledgers in the cellars, where Arthur himself went prowling the very next morning.", "플린트윈치 씨가 자기 아내를 향해 히죽 웃었다, \"당신을 침대에 누이기 전에, 당신은 마침내 나한테 말하지, 그 서류를 어디에 뒀는지를, 지하실의 낡은 장부들 사이에 말이야. 바로 그 이튿날 아침 아서가 직접 어슬렁거리며 들어갔던 그곳에."], ["But it’s not to be burnt on a Sunday night.", "하지만 그건 일요일 밤에 태워서는 안 되지. 안 돼."], ["No; you are strict, you are; we must wait over twelve o’clock, and get into Monday.", "당신은 엄격하니까, 당신은 그러니까. 우리는 열두 시를 넘겨 월요일로 들어설 때까지 기다려야 해."], ["Now, all this is a swallowing of me up alive that rasps me; so, feeling a little out of temper, and not being as strict as yourself, I take a look at the document before twelve o’clock to refresh my memory as to its appearance--fold up one of the many yellow old papers in the cellars like it--and afterwards, when we have got into Monday morning, and I have, by the light of your lamp, to walk from you, lying on that bed, to this grate, make a little exchange like the conjuror, and burn accordingly.", "자, 이 모든 게 나를 산 채로 삼키는 짓이라 짜증이 나니까, 좀 부아가 치밀고, 당신만큼 엄격하지도 않은 나는, 열두 시 전에 그 문서를 한번 들여다봐, 그 생김새를 다시 기억해 두려고 — 지하실에 있는 그것과 비슷한, 누렇게 바랜 숱한 낡은 종이 중 하나를 그것처럼 접어 두지 — 그러고 나서, 우리가 월요일 아침으로 들어서고, 내가 당신 등잔불에 의지해, 저 침대에 누운 당신한테서 이 화로까지 걸어가야 할 때, 마술사처럼 살짝 바꿔치기를 해서 그에 맞게 태우는 거야."], ["My brother Ephraim, the lunatic-keeper (I wish he had had himself to keep in a strait-waistcoat), had had many jobs since the close of the long job he got from you, but had not done well.", "내 형 에프라임은, 정신병자 간수였는데(차라리 자기가 구속복을 입고 갇혔어야 했지), 당신한테서 받은 그 긴 일거리가 끝난 뒤로 여러 일을 했지만, 잘 풀리지 못했어."], ["His wife died (not that that was much; mine might have died instead, and welcome), he speculated unsuccessfully in lunatics, he got into difficulty about over-roasting a patient to bring him to reason, and he got into debt.", "그의 아내가 죽었고(그건 대수도 아니지. 차라리 내 아내가 죽었더라면 좋았을걸), 정신병자들로 투기를 했다가 실패했고, 환자를 정신 차리게 한답시고 너무 달군 일로 곤경에 빠졌고, 빚을 졌어."], ["He was going out of the way, on what he had been able to scrape up, and a trifle from me.", "그는 긁어모을 수 있었던 돈과 나한테서 받은 약간으로 자취를 감추려던 참이었어."], ["He was here that early Monday morning, waiting for the tide; in short, he was going to Antwerp, where (I am afraid you’ll be shocked at my saying, And be damned to him!) he made the acquaintance of this gentleman.", "그 월요일 이른 아침 그는 여기서 물때를 기다리고 있었지. 한마디로, 그는 앤트워프로 가려던 참이었고, 거기서(이런 말 하면 당신이 충격받을까 두렵네만, 그놈 지옥에나 떨어져라!) 이 신사 양반과 알게 된 거야."], ["He had come a long way, and, I thought then, was only sleepy; but, I suppose now, was drunk.", "그는 먼 길을 와서, 그때 나는 그저 졸린 거라고 생각했지만, 지금 생각하니 술에 취해 있었던 거지."], ["When Arthur’s mother had been under the care of him and his wife, she had been always writing, incessantly writing,--mostly letters of confession to you, and Prayers for forgiveness.", "아서의 어머니가 그와 그의 아내의 보살핌을 받고 있을 때, 그녀는 늘 편지를 썼어, 쉴 새 없이 써 댔지 — 대개는 당신한테 보내는 고백 편지와 용서를 비는 기도였어."], ["My brother had handed, from time to time, lots of these sheets to me.", "내 형은 그 종이들 뭉치를 이따금 나한테 건네줬어."], ["I thought I might as well keep them to myself as have them swallowed up alive too; so I kept them in a box, looking over them when I felt in the humour.", "나는 그것들마저 산 채로 삼켜지게 하느니 차라리 내가 갖고 있는 게 낫겠다 싶어서, 그것들을 상자에 넣어 두고, 기분이 동할 때 들여다봤어."], ["Convinced that it was advisable to get the paper out of the place, with Arthur coming about it, I put it into this same box, and I locked the whole up with two locks, and I trusted it to my brother to take away and keep, till I should write about it.", "아서가 그 일로 어슬렁거리니, 그 서류를 그곳에서 빼내 두는 게 상책이라는 확신이 들어, 나는 그것을 바로 그 같은 상자에 넣고, 자물쇠 두 개로 전부 잠가, 형한테 맡겨 가져가서 간직하라고 했어, 내가 그에 관해 편지를 쓸 때까지."], ["I did write about it, and never got an answer.", "나는 그에 관해 편지를 썼지만, 답을 받지 못했어."], ["I didn’t know what to make of it, till this gentleman favoured us with his first visit.", "나는 영문을 알 수 없었지, 이 신사 양반이 우리한테 첫 방문을 베풀어 주실 때까지는."], ["Of course, I began to suspect how it was, then; and I don’t want his word for it now to understand how he gets his knowledge from my papers, and your paper, and my brother’s cognac and tobacco talk (I wish he’d had to gag himself).", "물론 그때부터 어찌 된 일인지 의심하기 시작했지. 그리고 그가 내 종이들과, 당신 종이와, 내 형의 코냑과 담배에 절은 입놀림(그놈 입에 재갈이라도 물렸어야 했지)에서 어떻게 자기 앎을 얻어 냈는지를 이해하는 데, 지금 그자의 말 따위는 필요 없어."], ["Now, I have only one thing more to say, you hammer-headed woman, and that is, that I haven’t altogether made up my mind whether I might, or might not, have ever given you any trouble about the codicil.", "자, 이제 딱 한 가지만 더 말하겠어, 이 멍청한 여자야, 그건, 내가 그 추가 유언 일로 당신을 골치 아프게 했을지 안 했을지, 도무지 마음을 정하지 못했다는 거야."], ["I think not; and that I should have been quite satisfied with knowing I had got the better of you, and that I held the power over you.", "안 그랬을 것 같아. 당신을 이겼다는 것, 당신에 대한 권세를 쥐고 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로 나는 아주 흡족했을 거야."], ["In the present state of circumstances, I have no more explanation to give you till this time to-morrow night.", "지금 형편으로는, 내일 이맘때 밤까지 당신한테 더 해 줄 설명은 없어. 그러니,\""], ["So you may as well,’ said Mr Flintwinch, terminating his oration with a screw, ‘keep your eyes open at somebody else, for it’s no use keeping ‘em open at me.’", "플린트윈치 씨가 몸을 한번 비틀어 돌리며 연설을 마무리했다, \"다른 누구한테나 눈을 부릅뜨고 있으라고, 나한테 부릅떠 봐야 소용없으니까.\""]], [["She slowly withdrew them when he had ceased, and dropped her forehead on her hand.", "그가 말을 그치자 그녀는 천천히 눈을 거두고, 이마를 손에 떨구었다."], ["Her other hand pressed hard upon the table, and again the curious stir was observable in her, as if she were going to rise.", "다른 손은 탁자를 세게 누르고 있었고, 다시 그녀에게서 그 기이한 움직임이 — 마치 일어서려는 듯한 움직임이 — 보였다."]], [["‘This box can never bring, elsewhere, the price it will bring here.", "\"이 상자는 다른 어디서도, 여기서 받을 값을 결코 받지 못해."], ["This knowledge can never be of the same profit to you, sold to any other person, as sold to me.", "이 앎은, 다른 누구에게 팔든, 나에게 파는 것과 똑같은 이득을 결코 당신에게 가져다주지 못해."], ["But I have not the present means of raising the sum you have demanded.", "하지만 나는 당신이 요구한 액수를 마련할 수단이 지금 없어."], ["I have not prospered.", "나는 잘되지 못했어."], ["What will you take now, and what at another time, and how am I to be assured of your silence?’", "지금은 얼마를 받겠어, 그리고 나중에는 얼마를, 그리고 당신의 침묵을 내가 어떻게 보장받지?\""]], [["‘My angel,’ said Rigaud, ‘I have said what I will take, and time presses.", "\"나의 천사여,\" 리고가 말했다, \"제가 무엇을 받겠는지는 말씀드렸고, 시간이 촉박합니다."], ["Before coming here, I placed copies of the most important of these papers in another hand.", "여기 오기 전에, 저는 이 서류들 중 가장 중요한 것들의 사본을 다른 사람 손에 맡겨 두었습니다."], ["Put off the time till the Marshalsea gate shall be shut for the night, and it will be too late to treat.", "마셜시 정문이 밤을 위해 닫힐 때까지 시간을 끄시면, 흥정하기엔 너무 늦어질 겁니다."], ["The prisoner will have read them.’", "죄수가 그것들을 읽었을 테니까요.\""]], [["She put her two hands to her head again, uttered a loud exclamation, and started to her feet.", "그녀는 다시 두 손을 머리에 갖다 대고, 큰 소리로 외치며, 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["She staggered for a moment, as if she would have fallen; then stood firm.", "잠시, 마치 쓰러질 듯 비틀거리더니, 이내 꼿꼿이 섰다."]], [["‘Say what you mean.", "\"무슨 뜻인지 말해."], ["Say what you mean, man!’", "무슨 뜻인지 말하라고, 이 자야!\""]], [["Before her ghostly figure, so long unused to its erect attitude, and so stiffened in it, Rigaud fell back and dropped his voice.", "그토록 오래 곧추선 자세에 익숙하지 않아 그 자세로 굳어 버린 그녀의 유령 같은 형상 앞에서, 리고는 뒤로 물러서며 목소리를 낮췄다."], ["It was, to all the three, almost as if a dead woman had risen.", "세 사람 모두에게, 그것은 거의 죽은 여인이 일어선 듯했다."]], [["‘Miss Dorrit,’ answered Rigaud, ‘the little niece of Monsieur Frederick, whom I have known across the water, is attached to the prisoner.", "\"도릿 양은,\" 리고가 대답했다, \"제가 바다 건너에서 알았던 프레더릭 씨의 어린 조카딸 말입니다만, 그 죄수에게 정이 들어 있습니다."], ["Miss Dorrit, little niece of Monsieur Frederick, watches at this moment over the prisoner, who is ill.", "프레더릭 씨의 어린 조카딸인 도릿 양은, 이 순간 병든 그 죄수를 지키고 있지요. 저는 여기 오는 길에, 그녀를 위해 제 손으로 감옥에 꾸러미를 하나 남겨 두었습니다."], ["For her I with my own hands left a packet at the prison, on my way here, with a letter of instructions, “_for his sake_”--she will do anything for his sake--to keep it without breaking the seal, in case of its being reclaimed before the hour of shutting up to-night--if it should not be reclaimed before the ringing of the prison bell, to give it to him; and it encloses a second copy for herself, which he must give to her.", "'그를 위하여'라는 지시 편지와 함께요 — 그녀는 그를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 할 테니까요 — 오늘 밤 폐쇄 시각 전에 그것을 되찾아 가지 않을 경우에 대비해, 봉인을 뜯지 말고 간직하라고요 — 감옥의 종이 울리기 전에 그것을 되찾아 가지 않을 경우에는\n그것을 그에게 주라고. 그리고 거기에는 그녀 자신을 위한 두 번째 사본이 동봉되어 있으니, 그것은 그가 그녀에게 주어야 하오."], ["What!", "무슨 소리!"], ["I don’t trust myself among you, now we have got so far, without giving my secret a second life.", "이만큼 일이 진척된 마당에, 내 비밀에 두 번째 생명을 주어 두지 않고서야 당신들 틈에 내 한 몸을 맡길 수는 없지."], ["And as to its not bringing me, elsewhere, the price it will bring here, say then, madame, have you limited and settled the price the little niece will give--for his sake--to hush it up?", "그리고 그것이 다른 곳에서는 여기서 받을 값을 받아 주지 못한다는 점에 대해서는, 그러면 말해 보시오, 마담, 그 어린 조카가 — 그자를 위하여 — 이 일을 덮으려고 치를 값을 당신은 정하고 매겨 두었소?"], ["Once more I say, time presses.", "다시 한번 말하지만, 시간이 촉박하오."], ["The packet not reclaimed before the ringing of the bell to-night, you cannot buy.", "오늘 밤 종이 울리기 전에 그 봉투를 되찾아 가지 않으면, 당신은 살 수 없소."], ["I sell, then, to the little girl!’", "그렇다면 나는 그 어린 아가씨에게 팔겠소!”"]], [["Once more the stir and struggle in her, and she ran to a closet, tore the door open, took down a hood or shawl, and wrapped it over her head.", "다시 한번 그녀의 안에서 동요와 갈등이 일었고, 그녀는 벽장으로 달려가 문을 홱 열어젖히고 두건인지 숄인지를 끌어내려 머리에 둘러썼다."], ["Affery, who had watched her in terror, darted to her in the middle of the room, caught hold of her dress, and went on her knees to her.", "공포에 질려 그녀를 지켜보던 애프리가 방 한가운데로 달려들어 그녀의 옷자락을 붙잡고 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["‘Don’t, don’t, don’t! What are you doing?", "“그러지 마세요, 그러지 마세요, 그러지 마세요!"], ["Where are you going?", "무얼 하시는 거예요?"], ["You’re a fearful woman, but I don’t bear you no ill-will.", "어디로 가시려는 거예요? 마님은 무서운 분이지만, 저는 마님께 아무런 원한도 없어요."], ["I can do poor Arthur no good now, that I see; and you needn’t be afraid of me.", "이제 보니 저는 가엾은 아서를 위해서 해 드릴 수 있는 게 없어요. 그러니 저를 두려워하실 필요 없어요."], ["I’ll keep your secret.", "마님의 비밀을 지켜 드릴게요."], ["Don’t go out, you’ll fall dead in the street.", "나가지 마세요, 거리에서 쓰러져 돌아가실 거예요."], ["Only promise me, that, if it’s the poor thing that’s kept here secretly, you’ll let me take charge of her and be her nurse.", "다만 한 가지만 약속해 주세요. 여기 몰래 갇혀 있는 게 그 가엾은 사람이라면, 제가 그 사람을 맡아 돌볼 수 있게, 제가 그 사람의 간병인이 되게 해 주시겠다고요."], ["Only promise me that, and never be afraid of me.’", "그 한 가지만 약속해 주세요, 그러면 절대 저를 두려워하지 마세요.”"]], [["Mrs Clennam stood still for an instant, at the height of her rapid haste, saying in stern amazement:", "클레넘 부인은 그 황급한 다급함의 절정에서 한순간 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 준엄한 놀라움으로 말했다."]], [["‘Kept here?", "“여기 갇혀 있다고?"], ["She has been dead a score of years or more.", "그 여자는 죽은 지 스무 해도 넘었어."], ["Ask Flintwinch--ask _him_.", "플린트윈치에게 물어봐 — 그에게 물어보라고."], ["They can both tell you that she died when Arthur went abroad.’", "두 사람 다 말해 줄 수 있을 거다, 아서가 외국으로 떠났을 때 그 여자가 죽었다고.”"]], [["‘So much the worse,’ said Affery, with a shiver, ‘for she haunts the house, then.", "“그렇다면 더더욱 나빠요.” 애프리가 몸서리치며 말했다."], ["Who else rustles about it, making signals by dropping dust so softly?", "“그러면 그 여자가 이 집을 떠도는 거니까요. 그렇지 않다면 누가 그토록 살며시 먼지를 떨어뜨려 신호를 보내며 집 안을 바스락거리고 다니겠어요?"], ["Who else comes and goes, and marks the walls with long crooked touches when we are all a-bed?", "우리가 다 잠자리에 들었을 때 누가 오가며 벽에 길고 구불구불한 자국을 남기겠어요?"], ["Who else holds the door sometimes?", "또 누가 가끔 문을 붙들고 있겠어요?"], ["But don’t go out--don’t go out!", "하지만 나가지 마세요 — 나가지 마세요!"], ["Mistress, you’ll die in the street!’", "마님, 거리에서 돌아가실 거예요!”"]], [["Her mistress only disengaged her dress from the beseeching hands, said to Rigaud, ‘Wait here till I come back!’ and ran out of the room.", "그녀의 마님은 애원하는 손에서 옷자락을 떼어 낼 뿐, 리고에게 “내가 돌아올 때까지 여기서 기다려!”라고 말하고는 방 밖으로 달려 나갔다."], ["They saw her, from the window, run wildly through the court-yard and out at the gateway.", "두 사람은 창문 너머로, 그녀가 안마당을 미친 듯이 가로질러 정문 밖으로 달려 나가는 것을 보았다."]], [["For a few moments they stood motionless.", "잠시 동안 그들은 꼼짝 않고 서 있었다."], ["Affery was the first to move, and she, wringing her hands, pursued her mistress.", "먼저 움직인 것은 애프리였고, 그녀는 두 손을 비틀며 마님의 뒤를 쫓았다."], ["Next, Jeremiah Flintwinch, slowly backing to the door, with one hand in a pocket, and the other rubbing his chin, twisted himself out in his reticent way, speechlessly.", "다음으로 제러마이아 플린트윈치가 한 손은 주머니에 넣고 다른 손으로 턱을 문지르며 천천히 문 쪽으로 뒷걸음질하더니, 한마디 말도 없이 그 특유의 입 다문 태도로 몸을 비틀어 빠져나갔다."], ["Rigaud, left alone, composed himself upon the window-seat of the open window, in the old Marseilles-jail attitude.", "홀로 남은 리고는 열린 창문의 창턱에 옛 마르세유 감옥에서의 그 자세 그대로 자리를 잡았다."], ["He laid his cigarettes and fire-box ready to his hand, and fell to smoking.", "그는 담배와 부싯깃통을 손이 닿는 곳에 놓아두고 담배를 피우기 시작했다."]], [["‘Whoof!", "“흥!"], ["Almost as dull as the infernal old jail.", "그 지긋지긋한 옛 감옥만큼이나 따분하군."], ["Warmer, but almost as dismal.", "더 따뜻하긴 해도, 거의 그만큼 음울해."], ["Wait till she comes back?", "그녀가 돌아올 때까지 기다린다?"], ["Yes, certainly; but where is she gone, and how long will she be gone?", "그래, 물론이지. 하지만 그녀는 어디로 갔고, 얼마나 오래 가 있을까?"], ["No matter!", "상관없다!"], ["Rigaud Lagnier Blandois, my amiable subject, you will get your money.", "리고 라니에 블랑두아, 나의 사랑스러운 양반, 너는 네 돈을 받게 될 거야."], ["You will enrich yourself.", "너는 부자가 될 거야."], ["You have lived a gentleman; you will die a gentleman.", "너는 신사로 살아왔으니, 신사로 죽을 거야."], ["You triumph, my little boy; but it is your character to triumph.", "너는 승리한다, 나의 어린 사내야. 하지만 승리하는 것이야말로 네 본성이지."], ["Whoof!’", "흥!”"]], [["In the hour of his triumph, his moustache went up and his nose came down, as he ogled a great beam over his head with particular satisfaction.", "승리의 순간에, 그가 각별한 만족감으로 머리 위의 커다란 들보를 곁눈질하자, 그의 콧수염은 위로 올라가고 코는 아래로 내려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER 31. Closed", "# CHAPTER 31. Closed"]], [["The sun had set, and the streets were dim in the dusty twilight, when the figure so long unused to them hurried on its way.", "해가 지고, 그 거리에 그토록 오랫동안 익숙지 않던 형체가 길을 서둘러 나아갈 무렵, 거리는 먼지 낀 황혼 속에서 어둑했다."], ["In the immediate neighbourhood of the old house it attracted little attention, for there were only a few straggling people to notice it; but, ascending from the river by the crooked ways that led to London Bridge, and passing into the great main road, it became surrounded by astonishment.", "옛집 바로 근처에서는 그것이 거의 눈길을 끌지 못했으니, 그것을 알아챌 사람이라곤 드문드문 흩어진 몇뿐이었기 때문이다. 그러나 강가에서 런던 다리로 통하는 구불구불한 길을 따라 올라가 큰 한길로 들어서자, 그것은 경악에 에워싸이게 되었다."]], [["Resolute and wild of look, rapid of foot and yet weak and uncertain, conspicuously dressed in its black garments and with its hurried head-covering, gaunt and of an unearthly paleness, it pressed forward, taking no more heed of the throng than a sleep-walker.", "단호하고 사나운 표정에, 발걸음은 빠르면서도 미약하고 불안정했으며, 검은 의복과 황급히 둘러쓴 머릿수건으로 눈에 띄었고, 수척하고 이 세상 것 같지 않은 창백함을 띤 채, 그것은 군중을 몽유병자만큼도 개의치 않고 앞으로 밀고 나아갔다."], ["More remarkable by being so removed from the crowd it was among than if it had been lifted on a pedestal to be seen, the figure attracted all eyes.", "단상 위에 올려져 보이게 된 것보다도, 자신이 섞여 있는 군중에서 그토록 동떨어져 있음으로써 더욱 두드러진 그 형체는 모든 눈길을 끌어모았다."], ["Saunterers pricked up their attention to observe it; busy people, crossing it, slackened their pace and turned their heads; companions pausing and standing aside, whispered one another to look at this spectral woman who was coming by; and the sweep of the figure as it passed seemed to create a vortex, drawing the most idle and most curious after it.", "어슬렁대던 자들은 그것을 살피느라 주의를 곤두세웠고, 바쁜 사람들은 그것과 엇갈리며 걸음을 늦추고 고개를 돌렸으며, 길동무들은 멈춰 서서 비켜선 채 다가오는 이 유령 같은 여인을 보라며 서로 속삭였다. 그리고 지나가는 그 형체의 휩쓸림은 소용돌이를 일으켜, 가장 한가하고 가장 호기심 많은 자들을 그 뒤로 끌어들이는 듯했다."]], [["Made giddy by the turbulent irruption of this multitude of staring faces into her cell of years, by the confusing sensation of being in the air, and the yet more confusing sensation of being afoot, by the unexpected changes in half-remembered objects, and the want of likeness between the controllable pictures her imagination had often drawn of the life from which she was secluded and the overwhelming rush of the reality, she held her way as if she were environed by distracting thoughts, rather than by external humanity and observation.", "여러 해를 갇혀 있던 자신의 독방으로 이 수많은 응시하는 얼굴들이 어지러이 쳐들어온 탓에, 바깥 공기 속에 있다는 혼란스러운 감각과 두 발로 걷고 있다는 더욱 혼란스러운 감각 탓에, 어렴풋이 기억하던 사물들의 뜻밖의 변화 탓에, 그리고 그녀가 격리되어 있던 삶에 대해 상상이 곧잘 그려 내던 통제 가능한 그림들과 현실의 압도적인 쇄도 사이의 닮음 없음 탓에, 그녀는 어질어질해졌고, 외부의 인간 무리와 그 관찰에 둘러싸였다기보다 정신을 흩뜨리는 상념들에 에워싸인 듯이 길을 갔다."], ["But, having crossed the bridge and gone some distance straight onward, she remembered that she must ask for a direction; and it was only then, when she stopped and turned to look about her for a promising place of inquiry, that she found herself surrounded by an eager glare of faces.", "그러나 다리를 건너 곧장 얼마쯤 나아간 뒤, 그녀는 길을 물어야 한다는 것을 떠올렸다. 그리고 그제야, 멈춰 서서 물어볼 만한 곳을 찾으려 주위를 둘러보려 돌아섰을 때, 그녀는 자신이 열띠게 번득이는 얼굴들에 둘러싸여 있음을 알았다."]], [["‘Why are you encircling me?’ she asked, trembling.", "\"왜 나를 에워싸는 거죠?\" 그녀가 떨면서 물었다."]], [["None of those who were nearest answered; but from the outer ring there arose a shrill cry of ‘’Cause you’re mad!’", "가장 가까이 있던 자들 가운데 아무도 대답하지 않았으나, 바깥쪽 둘레에서 날카로운 외침이 솟아올랐다. \"당신이 미쳤으니까!\""]], [["‘I am sure as sane as any one here.", "\"나는 여기 있는 누구 못지않게 멀쩡해요."], ["I want to find the Marshalsea prison.’", "마셜시 감옥을 찾고 싶습니다.\""]], [["The shrill outer circle again retorted, ‘Then that ‘ud show you was mad if nothing else did, ‘cause it’s right opposite!’", "날카로운 바깥쪽 무리가 다시 받아쳤다. \"그것만으로도 당신이 미쳤다는 게 드러나죠, 바로 맞은편이니까!\""]], [["A short, mild, quiet-looking young man made his way through to her, as a whooping ensued on this reply, and said: ‘Was it the Marshalsea you wanted?", "이 대답에 함성이 뒤따르는 가운데, 키 작고 온순하며 조용해 보이는 젊은이 하나가 그녀에게로 헤치고 들어와 말했다. \"마셜시를 찾으셨습니까?"], ["I’m going on duty there.", "저도 거기 근무하러 가는 길입니다."], ["Come across with me.’", "저와 함께 건너가시죠.\""]], [["She laid her hand upon his arm, and he took her over the way; the crowd, rather injured by the near prospect of losing her, pressing before and behind and on either side, and recommending an adjournment to Bedlam.", "그녀가 그의 팔에 손을 얹자, 그는 그녀를 데리고 길을 건넜다. 군중은 그녀를 곧 놓치게 된다는 전망에 다소 분개하여, 앞뒤로 또 양옆으로 밀려들며 베들럼으로 옮겨 가기를 권했다."], ["After a momentary whirl in the outer court-yard, the prison-door opened, and shut upon them.", "바깥 마당에서 한순간 소용돌이가 인 뒤, 감옥 문이 열렸다가 그들을 안에 들이고 닫혔다."], ["In the Lodge, which seemed by contrast with the outer noise a place of refuge and peace, a yellow lamp was already striving with the prison shadows.", "바깥의 소음에 견주어 피난처이자 평온의 장소처럼 보이는 수위실에서는 노란 등불이 벌써 감옥의 그늘과 다투고 있었다."]], [["‘Why, John!’ said the turnkey who admitted them.", "\"아니, 존!\" 그들을 들인 간수가 말했다."], ["‘What is it?’", "\"무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["‘Nothing, father; only this lady not knowing her way, and being badgered by the boys.", "\"아무 일도 아니에요, 아버지. 그저 이 부인이 길을 모르셔서 애들한테 시달리고 계셨어요."], ["Who did you want, ma’am?’", "누구를 찾으십니까, 부인?\""]], [["‘Miss Dorrit.", "\"도릿 양이요."], ["Is she here?’", "여기 있나요?\""]], [["The young man became more interested.", "젊은이는 더욱 흥미를 보였다."], ["‘Yes, she is here.", "\"예, 여기 있습니다."], ["What might your name be?’", "성함이 어떻게 되십니까?\""]], [["‘Mrs Clennam.’", "\"클레넘 부인이에요.\""]], [["‘Mr Clennam’s mother?’ asked the young man.", "\"클레넘 씨의 모친이십니까?\" 젊은이가 물었다."]], [["She pressed her lips together, and hesitated.", "그녀는 입술을 꾹 다물고 망설였다. \"그래요."], ["‘Yes. She had better be told it is his mother.’", "그분의 어머니라고 전해 주는 편이 낫겠군요.\""]], [["‘You see,’ said the young man, ‘the Marshal’s family living in the country at present, the Marshal has given Miss Dorrit one of the rooms in his house to use when she likes.", "\"보십시오,\" 젊은이가 말했다."], ["Don’t you think you had better come up there, and let me bring Miss Dorrit?’", "\"지금 교도소장 가족이 시골에 살고 있어서, 소장이 도릿 양에게 자기 집 방 하나를 마음대로 쓰라고 내주었습니다. 그리로 올라가시고, 제가 도릿 양을 데려오는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["She signified her assent, and he unlocked a door and conducted her up a side staircase into a dwelling-house above.", "그녀가 동의를 표하자, 그는 문 하나를 열고 옆 계단을 따라 그녀를 위층의 살림집으로 안내했다."], ["He showed her into a darkening room, and left her.", "그는 그녀를 어둑해지는 방으로 들여보내고 떠났다."], ["The room looked down into the darkening prison-yard, with its inmates strolling here and there, leaning out of windows communing as much apart as they could with friends who were going away, and generally wearing out their imprisonment as they best might that summer evening.", "그 방은 어둑해지는 감옥 마당을 내려다보고 있었는데, 그 안의 수감자들은 여기저기 거닐기도 하고, 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 떠나가는 친구들과 되도록 떨어진 채 이야기를 나누기도 하며, 대체로 그 여름 저녁을 저마다 형편껏 갇힌 시간을 견뎌 내고 있었다."], ["The air was heavy and hot; the closeness of the place, oppressive; and from without there arose a rush of free sounds, like the jarring memory of such things in a headache and heartache.", "공기는 무겁고 더웠으며, 그곳의 답답함은 짓누르듯 했다. 그리고 바깥에서는 자유로운 소리들의 쇄도가 솟아올랐는데, 마치 두통과 가슴앓이 속에서 떠오르는 그런 것들에 대한 거슬리는 기억 같았다."], ["She stood at the window, bewildered, looking down into this prison as it were out of her own different prison, when a soft word or two of surprise made her start, and Little Dorrit stood before her.", "그녀가 어리둥절한 채 창가에 서서, 마치 자신만의 또 다른 감옥에서 내다보듯 이 감옥을 내려다보고 있을 때, 놀라움을 담은 부드러운 한두 마디 말에 그녀는 흠칫했고, 리틀 도릿이 그녀 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["‘Is it possible, Mrs Clennam, that you are so happily recovered as--’", "\"클레넘 부인, 정말로 이렇게 다행히도 회복하셔서—\""]], [["Little Dorrit stopped, for there was neither happiness nor health in the face that turned to her.", "리틀 도릿은 말을 멈췄다. 그녀를 향한 그 얼굴에는 다행스러움도 건강함도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["‘This is not recovery; it is not strength; I don’t know what it is.’ With an agitated wave of her hand, she put all that aside. ‘You have a packet left with you which you were to give to Arthur, if it was not reclaimed before this place closed to-night.’", "\"이건 회복이 아니에요. 기력도 아니고요. 나도 이게 무엇인지 모르겠어요.\" 그녀는 동요한 듯 손을 내저으며 그 모든 것을 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다. \"당신에게 맡겨진 꾸러미가 있죠, 오늘 밤 이곳이 문을 닫기 전에 되찾아 가지 않으면 아서에게 건네주기로 한 것 말이에요.\""]], [["‘Yes.’", "\"네.\""]], [["‘I reclaim it.’", "\"내가 그것을 되찾겠어요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit took it from her bosom, and gave it into her hand, which remained stretched out after receiving it.", "리틀 도릿은 그것을 품에서 꺼내 그녀의 손에 건넸다. 그녀의 손은 그것을 받은 뒤에도 내민 채로 있었다."]], [["‘Have you any idea of its contents?’", "\"그 안에 든 게 무엇인지 짐작하나요?\""]], [["Frightened by her being there with that new power Of Movement in her, which, as she said herself, was not strength, and which was unreal to look upon, as though a picture or statue had been animated, Little Dorrit answered ‘No.’", "그녀 자신이 말했듯 기력이라 할 수 없는, 그리고 마치 그림이나 조각상이 살아 움직이는 듯 보기에 비현실적인 그 새로운 운신의 힘을 지닌 채 그녀가 거기 있다는 데 겁을 먹은 리틀 도릿은 \"아니요\"라고 대답했다."]], [["‘Read them.’", "\"읽어 봐요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit took the packet from the still outstretched hand, and broke the seal.", "리틀 도릿은 여전히 내밀려 있는 손에서 꾸러미를 받아 봉인을 뜯었다."], ["Mrs Clennam then gave her the inner packet that was addressed to herself, and held the other.", "그러자 클레넘 부인은 자기 앞으로 되어 있는 안쪽 꾸러미를 그녀에게 주고, 다른 하나는 쥐고 있었다."], ["The shadow of the wall and of the prison buildings, which made the room sombre at noon, made it too dark to read there, with the dusk deepening apace, save in the window.", "정오에도 방을 어둑하게 만드는 담과 감옥 건물들의 그림자는, 황혼이 빠르게 짙어지는 가운데, 창가를 빼고는 거기서 글을 읽기에 너무 어둡게 만들었다."], ["In the window, where a little of the bright summer evening sky could shine upon her, Little Dorrit stood, and read.", "환한 여름 저녁 하늘이 조금이나마 그녀에게 비쳐 드는 창가에 리틀 도릿은 서서 읽었다."], ["After a broken exclamation or so of wonder and of terror, she read in silence.", "놀라움과 공포의 끊긴 탄성을 한두 번 내뱉은 뒤로, 그녀는 말없이 읽었다."], ["When she had finished, she looked round, and her old mistress bowed herself before her.", "다 읽고 나서 그녀가 주위를 둘러보자, 옛 안주인은 그녀 앞에 몸을 숙였다."]], [["‘You know, now, what I have done.’", "\"이제, 내가 무슨 짓을 했는지 알겠죠.\""]], [["‘I think so.", "\"그런 것 같아요. 그런 것 같아 두려워요."], ["I am afraid so; though my mind is so hurried, and so sorry, and has so much to pity that it has not been able to follow all I have read,’ said Little Dorrit tremulously.", "다만 제 마음이 너무도 다급하고, 너무도 슬프고, 가엾이 여길 것이 너무 많아서, 제가 읽은 모든 것을 다 따라가지는 못했어요.\" 리틀 도릿이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["‘I will restore to you what I have withheld from you.", "\"당신에게서 감춰 온 것을 당신에게 되돌려 주겠어요."], ["Forgive me.", "나를 용서해 줘요."], ["Can you forgive me?’", "용서할 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["‘I can, and Heaven knows I do!", "\"용서할 수 있어요, 그리고 하느님께서 아시듯이 용서해요! 제 옷에 입 맞추고 제게 무릎 꿇지 마세요."], ["Do not kiss my dress and kneel to me; you are too old to kneel to me; I forgive you freely without that.’", "부인은 제게 무릎 꿇기엔 너무 연로하세요. 그러지 않으셔도 저는 기꺼이 용서합니다.\""]], [["‘I have more yet to ask.’", "\"아직 더 청할 것이 있어요.\""]], [["‘Not in that posture,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"그런 자세로는 안 돼요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘It is unnatural to see your grey hair lower than mine.", "\"부인의 백발이 제 머리보다 낮게 있는 걸 보는 건 자연스럽지 않아요. 부디 일어나세요."], ["Pray rise; let me help you.’ With that she raised her up, and stood rather shrinking from her, but looking at her earnestly.", "제가 도와드릴게요.\" 그러면서 그녀는 그녀를 일으켜 세웠고, 그녀에게서 다소 움츠러든 채 서 있으면서도 그녀를 간절히 바라보았다."]], [["‘The great petition that I make to you (there is another which grows out of it), the great supplication that I address to your merciful and gentle heart, is, that you will not disclose this to Arthur until I am dead.", "\"내가 당신에게 드리는 큰 청은 (거기서 비롯되는 또 하나가 있어요), 당신의 자비롭고 부드러운 마음에 올리는 큰 간구는, 내가 죽을 때까지 이것을 아서에게 밝히지 말아 달라는 거예요."], ["If you think, when you have had time for consideration, that it can do him any good to know it while I am yet alive, then tell him.", "곰곰이 생각할 시간을 가진 뒤에, 내가 아직 살아 있는 동안 그가 이를 아는 것이 그에게 조금이라도 이로우리라 여겨지면, 그때는 그에게 말해요."], ["But you will not think that; and in such case, will you promise me to spare me until I am dead?’", "하지만 당신은 그렇게 여기지 않을 거예요. 그렇다면, 내가 죽을 때까지 나를 봐주겠다고 약속해 주겠어요?\""]], [["‘I am so sorry, and what I have read has so confused my thoughts,’ returned Little Dorrit, ‘that I can scarcely give you a steady answer.", "\"제가 너무도 안타깝고, 읽은 것이 제 생각을 너무도 어지럽혀 놔서,\" 리틀 도릿이 대답했다."], ["If I should be quite sure that to be acquainted with it will do Mr Clennam no good--’", "\"확고한 답을 드리기가 어려워요. 만약 이를 아는 것이 클레넘 씨에게 아무런 이로움도 되지 않으리라 제가 확신하게 된다면—\""]], [["‘I know you are attached to him, and will make him the first consideration.", "\"당신이 그를 애틋이 여기고 있다는 걸, 그를 가장 먼저 헤아리리라는 걸 나도 알아요."], ["It is right that he should be the first consideration.", "그가 가장 먼저 고려되는 게 마땅하지요. 그걸 청하는 거예요."], ["I ask that. But, having regarded him, and still finding that you may spare me for the little time I shall remain on earth, will you do it?’", "다만 그를 헤아린 뒤에도, 내가 이 땅에 머물 짧은 시간 동안 나를 봐줄 수 있다고 여전히 여긴다면, 그렇게 해 주겠어요?\""]], [["‘I will.’\n‘GOD bless you!’", "\"그렇게 하겠습니다.\""]], [["She stood in the shadow so that she was only a veiled form to Little Dorrit in the light; but the sound of her voice, in saying those three grateful words, was at once fervent and broken--broken by emotion as unfamiliar to her frozen eyes as action to her frozen limbs.", "\"하느님께서 당신을 축복하시기를!\" 그녀는 그늘 속에 서 있어서, 빛 속에 있는 리틀 도릿에게는 베일에 가린 형체일 뿐이었다. 그러나 그 고마움 어린 세 마디 말을 할 때 그녀의 목소리는 간절하면서도 끊겨 있었으니—그 얼어붙은 눈에 행동이 낯설듯, 그 얼어붙은 사지에 동작이 낯설듯, 감정에 끊긴 것이었다."]], [["‘You will wonder, perhaps,’ she said in a stronger tone, ‘that I can better bear to be known to you whom I have wronged, than to the son of my enemy who wronged me.--For she did wrong me!", "\"아마 의아하겠지요,\" 그녀가 더 힘찬 어조로 말했다."], ["She not only sinned grievously against the Lord, but she wronged me.", "\"내가 해를 끼친 당신에게 알려지는 것이, 나를 해한 내 원수의 아들에게 알려지는 것보다 더 견디기 쉽다는 게요.—그래요, 그 여자는 정말로 나를 해쳤어요! 그 여자는 주님께 끔찍한 죄를 지었을 뿐 아니라, 나를 해쳤어요."], ["What Arthur’s father was to me, she made him.", "아서의 아버지가 내게 어떤 사람이 되었는지, 그건 그 여자가 만든 거예요."], ["From our marriage day I was his dread, and that she made me.", "우리의 혼인날부터 나는 그의 두려움이었고, 그건 그 여자가 만든 일이에요."], ["I was the scourge of both, and that is referable to her.", "나는 두 사람 모두의 채찍이었고, 그것은 그 여자의 탓이에요."], ["You love Arthur (I can see the blush upon your face; may it be the dawn of happier days to both of you!), and you will have thought already that he is as merciful and kind as you, and why do I not trust myself to him as soon as to you.", "당신은 아서를 사랑하지요 (당신 얼굴에 떠오르는 홍조가 보여요. 그것이 두 사람 모두에게 더 행복한 날들의 새벽이기를!), 그리고 당신은 이미 그가 당신만큼이나 자비롭고 다정하다고 생각했겠지요, 그런데 어째서 나는 당신에게 그러듯 그에게는 선뜻 나를 맡기지 못하느냐고요."], ["Have you not thought so?’", "그렇게 생각하지 않았나요?\""]], [["‘No thought,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘can be quite a stranger to my heart, that springs out of the knowledge that Mr Clennam is always to be relied upon for being kind and generous and good.’", "\"클레넘 씨가 다정하고 너그럽고 선하다는 점에서 언제나 믿을 만한 분이라는 앎에서 솟아나는 어떤 생각도,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다. \"제 마음에 영 낯설 수는 없어요.\""]], [["‘I do not doubt it.", "\"나도 의심하지 않아요."], ["Yet Arthur is, of the whole world, the one person from whom I would conceal this, while I am in it.", "그렇지만 아서는, 온 세상에서, 내가 이 세상에 있는 동안 이것을 숨기고 싶은 단 한 사람이에요."], ["I kept over him as a child, in the days of his first remembrance, my restraining and correcting hand.", "그가 처음 기억하던 시절, 어린아이였던 그에게 나는 절제하고 바로잡는 손을 내내 얹어 두었어요."], ["I was stern with him, knowing that the transgressions of the parents are visited on their offspring, and that there was an angry mark upon him at his birth.", "부모의 죄과가 그 자손에게 미친다는 것을 알았기에, 그리고 그가 태어날 때부터 노여움의 표지가 그에게 새겨져 있었기에, 나는 그에게 엄했어요."], ["I have sat with him and his father, seeing the weakness of his father yearning to unbend to him; and forcing it back, that the child might work out his release in bondage and hardship.", "나는 그와 그의 아버지와 함께 앉아, 그의 아버지의 나약함이 그에게 마음을 풀어 놓기를 갈망하는 것을 보면서, 그것을 도로 억눌렀어요. 그 아이가 속박과 고난 속에서 자신의 구원을 이뤄 내도록 말이에요."], ["I have seen him, with his mother’s face, looking up at me in awe from his little books, and trying to soften me with his mother’s ways that hardened me.’", "나는 그가, 제 어미의 얼굴을 한 채, 작은 책에서 눈을 들어 두려움에 차 나를 올려다보며, 나를 굳어지게 만든 제 어미의 방식으로 나를 누그러뜨리려 애쓰는 것을 보았어요.\""]], [["The shrinking of her auditress stopped her for a moment in her flow of words, delivered in a retrospective gloomy voice.", "회상에 잠긴 음울한 목소리로 쏟아 내던 그녀의 말은, 듣는 이가 움츠러드는 바람에 한순간 멈췄다."]], [["‘For his good.", "\"그의 선을 위해서였어요."], ["Not for the satisfaction of my injury.", "내가 받은 상처에 대한 만족을 위해서가 아니라."], ["What was I, and what was the worth of that, before the curse of Heaven!", "하늘의 저주 앞에서 나는 무엇이며, 그것에 무슨 값어치가 있겠어요!"], ["I have seen that child grow up; not to be pious in a chosen way (his mother’s influence lay too heavy on him for that), but still to be just and upright, and to be submissive to me.", "나는 그 아이가 자라는 것을 보았어요. 정해진 방식으로 경건해지지는 못했지만 (그러기엔 제 어미의 영향이 그에게 너무 무겁게 드리워 있었어요), 그래도 정직하고 올곧게, 그리고 내게 순종하며 자라는 것을요."], ["He never loved me, as I once half-hoped he might--so frail we are, and so do the corrupt affections of the flesh war with our trusts and tasks; but he always respected me and ordered himself dutifully to me.", "그는, 내가 한때 반쯤 바랐던 만큼 나를 사랑한 적이 없어요—우리는 그토록 연약하고, 육신의 타락한 정은 우리의 본분과 소임과 그토록 다투지요. 그러나 그는 언제나 나를 존경했고, 나에게 본분을 다해 자신을 다스렸어요."], ["He does to this hour.", "지금 이 순간까지도 그래요."], ["With an empty place in his heart that he has never known the meaning of, he has turned away from me and gone his separate road; but even that he has done considerately and with deference.", "그는 자신이 그 뜻을 한 번도 알지 못한 텅 빈 자리를 가슴에 품은 채, 나에게서 등을 돌려 제 길을 떠나갔지만, 그것조차 사려 깊게, 또 공손하게 했어요."], ["These have been his relations towards me.", "이것이 나에 대한 그의 처신이었어요."], ["Yours have been of a much slighter kind, spread over a much shorter time.", "당신의 처신은 훨씬 가벼운 것이었고, 훨씬 짧은 시간에 걸친 것이었지요."], ["When you have sat at your needle in my room, you have been in fear of me, but you have supposed me to have been doing you a kindness; you are better informed now, and know me to have done you an injury.", "당신이 내 방에서 바느질을 하며 앉아 있을 때, 당신은 나를 두려워했지만, 내가 당신에게 친절을 베풀고 있다고 여겼어요. 이제 당신은 더 잘 알게 되었고, 내가 당신에게 해를 끼쳤다는 것을 알아요."], ["Your misconstruction and misunderstanding of the cause in which, and the motives with which, I have worked out this work, is lighter to endure than his would be.", "내가 이 일을 이뤄 낸 그 명분과 동기에 대한 당신의 오해와 그릇된 짐작은, 그의 것이 될 오해보다 견디기 한결 가벼워요."], ["I would not, for any worldly recompense I can imagine, have him in a moment, however blindly, throw me down from the station I have held before him all his life, and change me altogether into something he would cast out of his respect, and think detected and exposed.", "내가 상상할 수 있는 그 어떤 세속의 보상을 준다 해도, 그가 한순간에, 아무리 무턱대고라도, 평생 그의 앞에서 내가 지켜 온 자리에서 나를 끌어내려, 나를 자기 존경에서 내쳐 버릴 무언가로, 들통나고 폭로된 것으로 여길 무언가로 송두리째 바꿔 버리게 두지는 않을 거예요."], ["Let him do it, if it must be done, when I am not here to see it.", "정 그래야 한다면, 내가 그것을 볼 자리에 없을 때 그가 그렇게 하게 하세요."], ["Let me never feel, while I am still alive, that I die before his face, and utterly perish away from him, like one consumed by lightning and swallowed by an earthquake.’", "내가 아직 살아 있는 동안, 그의 면전에서 죽어 가는 것을, 벼락에 타 버리고 지진에 삼켜진 자처럼 그에게서 송두리째 사라져 버리는 것을 결코 느끼지 않게 해 주세요.\""]], [["Her pride was very strong in her, the pain of it and of her old passions was very sharp with her, when she thus expressed herself.", "이렇게 자신을 표현할 때, 그녀 안의 자존심은 매우 강했고, 그 자존심의 고통과 그녀의 오랜 격정의 고통은 그녀에게 매우 날카로웠다."], ["Not less so, when she added:", "그녀가 다음 말을 덧붙일 때도 그에 못지않았다."]], [["‘Even now, I see _you_ shrink from me, as if I had been cruel.’", "\"지금조차, 마치 내가 잔인하기라도 했던 것처럼, 당신이 나에게서 움츠러드는 게 보여요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit could not gainsay it.", "리틀 도릿은 그것을 부인할 수 없었다."], ["She tried not to show it, but she recoiled with dread from the state of mind that had burnt so fiercely and lasted so long.", "그녀는 드러내지 않으려 했으나, 그토록 맹렬히 타오르고 그토록 오래 지속된 그 마음의 상태에서 두려움에 차 물러섰다."], ["It presented itself to her, with no sophistry upon it, in its own plain nature.", "그것은 아무런 궤변도 덧입지 않은, 제 그대로의 적나라한 본성으로 그녀에게 다가왔다."]], [["‘I have done,’ said Mrs Clennam, ‘what it was given to me to do.", "\"나는,\" 클레넘 부인이 말했다."], ["I have set myself against evil; not against good.", "\"내게 주어진 일을 했어요. 나는 악에 맞섰지, 선에 맞선 게 아니에요."], ["I have been an instrument of severity against sin.", "나는 죄악에 대한 준엄함의 도구였어요."], ["Have not mere sinners like myself been commissioned to lay it low in all time?’", "나 같은 한낱 죄인들이 모든 시대에 걸쳐 죄악을 무너뜨리도록 위임받지 않았던가요?\""]], [["‘In all time?’ repeated Little Dorrit.", "\"모든 시대에 걸쳐서요?\" 리틀 도릿이 되뇌었다."]], [["‘Even if my own wrong had prevailed with me, and my own vengeance had moved me, could I have found no justification?", "\"설령 내 자신의 상처가 내 안에서 우세했고 내 자신의 복수심이 나를 움직였다 한들, 내가 아무런 정당함도 찾을 수 없었을까요?"], ["None in the old days when the innocent perished with the guilty, a thousand to one?", "무고한 자가 죄 있는 자와 함께, 천 대 하나로 멸하던 옛 시절에는 그런 게 없었을까요?"], ["When the wrath of the hater of the unrighteous was not slaked even in blood, and yet found favour?’", "의롭지 못한 자를 미워하는 이의 분노가 피로도 가시지 않았으면서도 도리어 가납받던 그 시절에는요?\""]], [["‘O, Mrs Clennam, Mrs Clennam,’ said Little Dorrit, ‘angry feelings and unforgiving deeds are no comfort and no guide to you and me.", "\"오, 클레넘 부인, 클레넘 부인,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["My life has been passed in this poor prison, and my teaching has been very defective; but let me implore you to remember later and better days.", "\"노여운 감정과 용서 없는 행위는 부인께도 저에게도 위안이 못 되고 인도가 못 돼요. 제 삶은 이 가련한 감옥에서 보내졌고, 제가 받은 가르침은 매우 부족했어요."], ["Be guided only by the healer of the sick, the raiser of the dead, the friend of all who were afflicted and forlorn, the patient Master who shed tears of compassion for our infirmities.", "그러나 부디 더 나중의, 더 나은 날들을 기억해 주시기를 간청해요. 병든 자를 고치시고, 죽은 자를 일으키시고, 고통받고 버림받은 모든 이의 벗이 되시고, 우리의 연약함을 가엾이 여겨 눈물을 흘리신 인내의 주님, 오직 그분께만 인도받으세요."], ["We cannot but be right if we put all the rest away, and do everything in remembrance of Him.", "나머지를 모두 치우고, 그분을 기억하며 모든 일을 행한다면, 우리는 옳을 수밖에 없어요."], ["There is no vengeance and no infliction of suffering in His life, I am sure.", "그분의 삶에는 복수도, 고통을 가하는 일도 없다는 걸 저는 확신해요."], ["There can be no confusion in following Him, and seeking for no other footsteps, I am certain.’", "그분을 따르며 다른 어떤 발자취도 구하지 않는다면 거기에 어떤 혼란도 있을 수 없다는 걸 저는 확신해요.\""]], [["In the softened light of the window, looking from the scene of her early trials to the shining sky, she was not in stronger opposition to the black figure in the shade than the life and doctrine on which she rested were to that figure’s history.", "창가의 부드러워진 빛 속에서, 자신의 어린 시절 시련의 무대로부터 빛나는 하늘로 눈길을 돌리며 서 있는 그녀는, 그녀가 의지하는 삶과 가르침이 그 형체의 내력과 대립하는 것보다 결코 덜하지 않게, 그늘 속의 검은 형체와 강하게 대립하고 있었다."], ["It bent its head low again, and said not a word.", "그 형체는 다시 고개를 낮게 숙이고 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["It remained thus, until the first warning bell began to ring.", "그것은 첫 경고 종이 울리기 시작할 때까지 그렇게 있었다."]], [["‘Hark!’ cried Mrs Clennam starting, ‘I said I had another petition.", "\"들어 봐요!\" 클레넘 부인이 흠칫 놀라며 외쳤다."], ["It is one that does not admit of delay.", "\"내게 또 다른 청이 있다고 했지요."], ["The man who brought you this packet and possesses these proofs, is now waiting at my house to be bought off.", "그건 지체를 용납하지 않는 거예요. 당신에게 이 꾸러미를 가져다주었고 이 증거들을 쥐고 있는 그 사내가, 지금 내 집에서 매수당하기를 기다리고 있어요."], ["I can keep this from Arthur, only by buying him off.", "그를 매수해야만 이것을 아서에게 숨길 수 있어요."], ["He asks a large sum; more than I can get together to pay him without having time.", "그는 거액을 요구하는데, 시간을 두지 않고는 그에게 지불할 만큼 마련할 수 없는 액수예요."], ["He refuses to make any abatement, because his threat is, that if he fails with me, he will come to you.", "그는 한 푼도 깎아 주려 하지 않아요. 나와 거래가 안 되면 당신에게 오겠다고 으름장을 놓으니까요."], ["Will you return with me and show him that you already know it?", "나와 함께 돌아가서 당신이 이미 이를 알고 있다는 걸 그에게 보여 주겠어요?"], ["Will you return with me and try to prevail with him?", "나와 함께 돌아가서 그를 설득해 보겠어요?"], ["Will you come and help me with him?", "가서 그를 상대하는 일을 나와 함께 도와주겠어요?"], ["Do not refuse what I ask in Arthur’s name, though I dare not ask it for Arthur’s sake!’", "비록 아서를 위해서라고는 감히 청하지 못하지만, 아서의 이름으로 내가 청하는 것을 거절하지 말아 줘요!\""]], [["Little Dorrit yielded willingly.", "리틀 도릿은 기꺼이 응했다."], ["She glided away into the prison for a few moments, returned, and said she was ready to go.", "그녀는 잠시 감옥 안으로 미끄러져 들어갔다가 돌아와, 갈 채비가 되었다고 말했다."], ["They went out by another staircase, avoiding the lodge; and coming into the front court-yard, now all quiet and deserted, gained the street.", "그들은 수위실을 피해 다른 계단으로 빠져나왔고, 이제는 온통 고요하고 인적 없는 앞마당으로 나와 거리로 들어섰다."]], [["It was one of those summer evenings when there is no greater darkness than a long twilight.", "긴 황혼보다 더 깊은 어둠이 없는, 그런 여름 저녁의 하나였다. 거리와 다리의 전경이 또렷이 보였고, 하늘은 잔잔하고 아름다웠다."], ["The vista of street and bridge was plain to see, and the sky was serene and beautiful.", "사람들은 제집 문간에 서거나 앉아, 아이들과 놀고 저녁을 즐기고 있었다."], ["People stood and sat at their doors, playing with children and enjoying the evening; numbers were walking for air; the worry of the day had almost worried itself out, and few but themselves were hurried.", "바람을 쐬러 거니는 이들이 무수히 많았다. 하루의 분주함은 거의 제풀에 잦아들었고, 그들 자신을 빼면 서두르는 이가 드물었다."], ["As they crossed the bridge, the clear steeples of the many churches looked as if they had advanced out of the murk that usually enshrouded them, and come much nearer.", "그들이 다리를 건널 때, 숱한 교회의 또렷한 첨탑들은 마치 평소 그것들을 감싸던 음울함에서 벗어나 한결 가까이 다가온 듯 보였다."], ["The smoke that rose into the sky had lost its dingy hue and taken a brightness upon it.", "하늘로 피어오르는 연기는 그 칙칙한 빛을 잃고 밝음을 띠었다."], ["The beauties of the sunset had not faded from the long light films of cloud that lay at peace in the horizon.", "지평선에 평화로이 누운 길고 가느다란 구름 막에는 석양의 아름다움이 아직 바래지 않았다."], ["From a radiant centre, over the whole length and breadth of the tranquil firmament, great shoots of light streamed among the early stars, like signs of the blessed later covenant of peace and hope that changed the crown of thorns into a glory.", "빛나는 한 중심에서, 고요한 창공의 전 길이와 너비에 걸쳐, 커다란 빛줄기들이 일찍 뜬 별들 사이로 뻗어 나왔으니, 가시 면류관을 영광으로 바꾸어 놓은, 평화와 희망의 그 복된 새 언약의 표지 같았다."]], [["Less remarkable, now that she was not alone and it was darker, Mrs Clennam hurried on at Little Dorrit’s side, unmolested.", "이제는 혼자가 아니고 더 어두워진 까닭에 덜 눈에 띈 채, 클레넘 부인은 리틀 도릿 곁에서 시달림 없이 서둘러 나아갔다."], ["They left the great thoroughfare at the turning by which she had entered it, and wound their way down among the silent, empty, cross-streets.", "그들은 그녀가 들어섰던 길모퉁이에서 큰 한길을 벗어나, 고요하고 텅 빈 골목길들 사이로 굽이굽이 내려갔다."], ["Their feet were at the gateway, when there was a sudden noise like thunder.", "그들의 발이 대문께에 이르렀을 때, 천둥 같은 갑작스러운 굉음이 났다."]], [["‘What was that!", "\"저게 뭐죠!"], ["Let us make haste in,’ cried Mrs Clennam.", "어서 안으로 들어가요,\" 클레넘 부인이 외쳤다."]], [["They were in the gateway.", "그들은 대문 안에 있었다."], ["Little Dorrit, with a piercing cry, held her back.", "리틀 도릿이 찢어지는 듯한 비명을 지르며 그녀를 붙들어 막았다."]], [["In one swift instant the old house was before them, with the man lying smoking in the window; another thundering sound, and it heaved, surged outward, opened asunder in fifty places, collapsed, and fell.", "순식간의 한 찰나, 그 옛집이 그들 앞에 있었고, 한 사내가 창가에 누워 담배를 피우고 있었다. 또 한 번 천둥 같은 소리가 나더니, 집이 들썩이며 바깥으로 부풀어 오르듯 솟구치고, 쉰 군데에서 갈라져 무너지며 쓰러졌다."], ["Deafened by the noise, stifled, choked, and blinded by the dust, they hid their faces and stood rooted to the spot.", "굉음에 귀가 먹먹해지고, 먼지에 숨이 막히고 목이 메고 눈이 멀어, 그들은 얼굴을 가린 채 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 서 있었다."], ["The dust storm, driving between them and the placid sky, parted for a moment and showed them the stars.", "그들과 잔잔한 하늘 사이를 휘몰아치던 먼지 폭풍이 한순간 갈라지며 별들을 보여 주었다."], ["As they looked up, wildly crying for help, the great pile of chimneys, which was then alone left standing like a tower in a whirlwind, rocked, broke, and hailed itself down upon the heap of ruin, as if every tumbling fragment were intent on burying the crushed wretch deeper.", "그들이 미친 듯 도움을 청하며 위를 올려다보았을 때, 회오리바람 속의 탑처럼 그때 홀로 남아 서 있던 그 거대한 굴뚝 무더기가 흔들리다 부서지더니, 떨어져 내리는 모든 조각이 짓눌린 가련한 자를 더 깊이 묻어 버리려 작정한 듯, 폐허 더미 위로 우박처럼 쏟아져 내렸다."]], [["So blackened by the flying particles of rubbish as to be unrecognisable, they ran back from the gateway into the street, crying and shrieking.", "날아드는 잔해 부스러기에 새카맣게 뒤덮여 알아볼 수 없게 된 채, 그들은 대문에서 거리로 도로 달려 나오며 울부짖고 비명을 질렀다."], ["There, Mrs Clennam dropped upon the stones; and she never from that hour moved so much as a finger again, or had the power to speak one word.", "거기서 클레넘 부인이 돌바닥 위에 쓰러졌다. 그리고 그 시각 이후로 그녀는 다시는 손가락 하나조차 움직이지 못했고, 한마디도 말할 힘을 잃었다."], ["For upwards of three years she reclined in a wheeled chair, looking attentively at those about her and appearing to understand what they said; but the rigid silence she had so long held was evermore enforced upon her, and except that she could move her eyes and faintly express a negative and affirmative with her head, she lived and died a statue.", "삼 년 남짓 그녀는 바퀴 달린 의자에 기대앉아, 주위 사람들을 유심히 바라보며 그들이 하는 말을 알아듣는 듯 보였다. 그러나 그토록 오래 지켜 온 그 굳은 침묵이 이제는 영영 그녀에게 강제되었고, 눈을 움직이고 머리로 희미하게 부정과 긍정을 표할 수 있는 것 말고는, 그녀는 조각상으로 살다 조각상으로 죽었다."]], [["Affery had been looking for them at the prison, and had caught sight of them at a distance on the bridge.", "애프리는 감옥에서 그들을 찾고 있다가, 다리 위 멀리서 그들을 발견했다."], ["She came up to receive her old mistress in her arms, to help to carry her into a neighbouring house, and to be faithful to her.", "그녀는 옛 안주인을 두 팔로 받아 안으려, 인근의 집으로 옮기는 것을 도우려, 그리고 그녀에게 충실하려 다가왔다."], ["The mystery of the noises was out now; Affery, like greater people, had always been right in her facts, and always wrong in the theories she deduced from them.", "그 소리들의 수수께끼는 이제 풀렸다. 애프리는, 더 대단한 사람들이 그러하듯, 사실에 관해서는 늘 옳았으나, 그 사실에서 끌어낸 이론에 관해서는 늘 그릇되어 있었다."]], [["When the storm of dust had cleared away and the summer night was calm again, numbers of people choked up every avenue of access, and parties of diggers were formed to relieve one another in digging among the ruins.", "먼지 폭풍이 걷히고 여름밤이 다시 잠잠해지자, 무수한 사람들이 접근로란 접근로를 모조리 메웠고, 폐허 사이를 파헤치며 교대로 일할 발굴 패들이 꾸려졌다."], ["There had been a hundred people in the house at the time of its fall, there had been fifty, there had been fifteen, there had been two.", "집이 무너질 때 그 안에 백 명이 있었다고도, 쉰 명이 있었다고도, 열다섯 명이 있었다고도, 둘이 있었다고도 했다."], ["Rumour finally settled the number at two; the foreigner and Mr Flintwinch.", "소문은 마침내 그 수를 둘로 정착시켰다. 그 외국인과 플린트윈치 씨였다."]], [["The diggers dug all through the short night by flaring pipes of gas, and on a level with the early sun, and deeper and deeper below it as it rose into its zenith, and aslant of it as it declined, and on a level with it again as it departed.", "발굴자들은 짧은 밤 내내 활활 타오르는 가스관 불빛에 의지해 파 내려갔고, 일찍 떠오른 해와 나란한 높이에서도, 해가 중천으로 솟아오를수록 그보다 더 깊고 깊은 곳에서도, 해가 기울 때는 그에 비스듬히, 해가 저물 때는 다시 그와 나란한 높이에서 파 내려갔다."], ["Sturdy digging, and shovelling, and carrying away, in carts, barrows, and baskets, went on without intermission, by night and by day; but it was night for the second time when they found the dirty heap of rubbish that had been the foreigner before his head had been shivered to atoms, like so much glass, by the great beam that lay upon him, crushing him.", "억센 파헤침과 삽질, 그리고 수레와 손수레와 바구니로 실어 나르는 일이 밤낮없이 쉼 없이 이어졌다. 그러나 그들이, 자기를 짓누르며 깔린 큰 들보에 머리가 마치 유리 조각처럼 산산이 부서지기 전의 그 외국인이었던 더러운 잔해 더미를 찾아냈을 때는, 다시 두 번째로 맞이한 밤이었다."]], [["Still, they had not come upon Flintwinch yet; so the sturdy digging and shovelling and carrying away went on without intermission by night and by day.", "그래도 그들은 아직 플린트윈치는 찾아내지 못했다. 그래서 억센 파헤침과 삽질과 실어 나르기가 밤낮없이 쉼 없이 이어졌다."], ["It got about that the old house had had famous cellarage (which indeed was true), and that Flintwinch had been in a cellar at the moment, or had had time to escape into one, and that he was safe under its strong arch, and even that he had been heard to cry, in hollow, subterranean, suffocated notes, ‘Here I am!’ At the opposite extremity of the town it was even known that the excavators had been able to open a communication with him through a pipe, and that he had received both soup and brandy by that channel, and that he had said with admirable fortitude that he was All right, my lads, with the exception of his collar-bone.", "그 옛집에 훌륭한 지하실이 있었다는 소문이 퍼졌고 (실제로 사실이었다), 플린트윈치가 그 순간 지하실에 있었거나 지하실로 피할 틈이 있었으리라는, 그래서 그 튼튼한 아치 밑에 무사히 있으리라는, 심지어 그가 \"나 여기 있소!\"라고 공허하고 지하 깊은 곳에서 나오는 듯 숨 막힌 소리로 외치는 것을 들었다는 소문까지 돌았다. 도시 반대편 끝에서는, 발굴자들이 관을 통해 그와 연락을 틀 수 있었고, 그가 그 통로로 수프와 브랜디를 받았으며, 그가 감탄스러운 의연함으로 쇄골만 빼면 멀쩡하다고, 다들 괜찮네, 친구들 하고 말했다는 소문까지 퍼졌다."], ["But the digging and shovelling and carrying away went on without intermission, until the ruins were all dug out, and the cellars opened to the light; and still no Flintwinch, living or dead, all right or all wrong, had been turned up by pick or spade.", "그러나 파헤침과 삽질과 실어 나르기는 폐허가 다 파헤쳐지고 지하실들이 빛에 드러날 때까지 쉼 없이 이어졌다. 그래도 플린트윈치는, 산 채로든 죽은 채로든, 멀쩡하든 엉망이든, 곡괭이나 삽에 들춰지지 않았다."]], [["It began then to be perceived that Flintwinch had not been there at the time of the fall; and it began then to be perceived that he had been rather busy elsewhere, converting securities into as much money as could be got for them on the shortest notice, and turning to his own exclusive account his authority to act for the Firm.", "그제야 사람들은 플린트윈치가 무너질 당시 거기에 없었음을 알아채기 시작했다. 그리고 그가 다른 곳에서 꽤나 바삐 움직이고 있었음을, 곧 증권들을 가장 짧은 통고로 받아낼 수 있는 한 많은 돈으로 바꾸고, 회사를 대리할 자기 권한을 자기 한 사람의 잇속으로 돌리고 있었음을 알아채기 시작했다."], ["Affery, remembering that the clever one had said he would explain himself further in four-and-twenty hours’ time, determined for her part that his taking himself off within that period with all he could get, was the final satisfactory sum and substance of his promised explanation; but she held her peace, devoutly thankful to be quit of him.", "애프리는, 그 영리한 자가 스물네 시간 안에 자신을 더 자세히 설명해 주겠다고 말했던 것을 떠올리며, 그자가 그 기간 안에 챙길 수 있는 모든 것을 가지고 사라져 버린 것이야말로 그가 약속한 설명의 최종적이고 흡족한 총괄이자 알맹이라고 나름대로 단정했다. 그러나 그녀는 그자에게서 벗어난 것을 진심으로 감사하며 잠자코 있었다."], ["As it seemed reasonable to conclude that a man who had never been buried could not be unburied, the diggers gave him up when their task was done, and did not dig down for him into the depths of the earth.", "한 번도 묻힌 적 없는 사람이 파묻혀 나올 수는 없다고 결론짓는 것이 이치에 맞는 듯하여, 발굴자들은 일을 마치자 그를 단념했고, 땅속 깊은 곳까지 그를 찾아 파 내려가지는 않았다."]], [["This was taken in ill part by a great many people, who persisted in believing that Flintwinch was lying somewhere among the London geological formation.", "이것을 많은 사람들이 못마땅하게 받아들였으니, 그들은 플린트윈치가 런던의 지질층 어딘가에 누워 있다고 끝끝내 믿었다."], ["Nor was their belief much shaken by repeated intelligence which came over in course of time, that an old man who wore the tie of his neckcloth under one ear, and who was very well known to be an Englishman, consorted with the Dutchmen on the quaint banks of the canals of the Hague and in the drinking-shops of Amsterdam, under the style and designation of Mynheer von Flyntevynge.", "그들의 믿음은, 한쪽 귀 밑에 목도리 매듭을 묶고 다니는, 영국인임이 매우 잘 알려진 어느 노인이, 헤이그의 운치 있는 운하 둑과 암스테르담의 술집들에서, 미인헤이르 폰 플린테빈게라는 이름과 호칭으로 네덜란드인들과 어울려 다닌다는 소식이 세월이 흐르며 거듭 전해져 와도, 그다지 흔들리지 않았다."]], [["# CHAPTER 32. Going", "# CHAPTER 32. Going"]], [["Arthur continuing to lie very ill in the Marshalsea, and Mr Rugg descrying no break in the legal sky affording a hope of his enlargement, Mr Pancks suffered desperately from self-reproaches.", "아서가 마셜시에서 여전히 위중하게 누워 있었고, 러그 씨로서는 그의 석방에 희망을 줄 법적 하늘의 틈새가 도무지 보이지 않았으므로, 팽크스 씨는 자책에 처참하게 시달렸다."], ["If it had not been for those infallible figures which proved that Arthur, instead of pining in imprisonment, ought to be promenading in a carriage and pair, and that Mr Pancks, instead of being restricted to his clerkly wages, ought to have from three to five thousand pounds of his own at his immediate disposal, that unhappy arithmetician would probably have taken to his bed, and there have made one of the many obscure persons who turned their faces to the wall and died, as a last sacrifice to the late Mr Merdle’s greatness.", "아서가 옥에 갇혀 야위어 갈 것이 아니라 쌍두마차를 타고 산책을 다녀야 마땅하며, 팽크스 씨도 서기 봉급에 묶일 것이 아니라 삼천에서 오천 파운드쯤은 제 손으로 당장 굴릴 수 있어야 한다는 그 틀림없는 숫자가 아니었더라면, 이 불행한 산술가는 아마 자리보전을 하고 누워, 고인이 된 머들 씨의 위대함에 바치는 마지막 제물로서 얼굴을 벽 쪽으로 돌리고 죽어 간 수많은 이름 없는 이들 가운데 하나가 되었을 것이다."], ["Solely supported by his unimpugnable calculations, Mr Pancks led an unhappy and restless life; constantly carrying his figures about with him in his hat, and not only going over them himself on every possible occasion, but entreating every human being he could lay hold of to go over them with him, and observe what a clear case it was.", "오직 그 반박할 수 없는 계산에만 떠받쳐진 채, 팽크스 씨는 불행하고 안절부절못하는 삶을 이어 갔다. 그는 늘 그 숫자들을 모자 속에 넣고 다니며 틈만 나면 스스로 되짚어 볼 뿐 아니라, 손에 잡히는 사람이라면 누구에게나 함께 따져 보고 이것이 얼마나 명백한 사건인지 보아 달라고 간청했다."], ["Down in Bleeding Heart Yard there was scarcely an inhabitant of note to whom Mr Pancks had not imparted his demonstration, and, as figures are catching, a kind of cyphering measles broke out in that locality, under the influence of which the whole Yard was light-headed.", "블리딩 하트 야드에는 팽크스 씨가 그 증명을 들려주지 않은 변변한 주민이 거의 없었고, 숫자란 옮는 법이라, 그 일대에 일종의 셈하기 홍역이 번져 야드 전체가 들떠서 정신을 못 차렸다."]], [["The more restless Mr Pancks grew in his mind, the more impatient he became of the Patriarch.", "팽크스 씨는 마음속이 안절부절못할수록 그 가장에게 더욱 참을성을 잃어 갔다."], ["In their later conferences his snorting assumed an irritable sound which boded the Patriarch no good; likewise, Mr Pancks had on several occasions looked harder at the Patriarchal bumps than was quite reconcilable with the fact of his not being a painter, or a peruke-maker in search of the living model.", "나중의 회담에서 그의 씩씩거리는 콧소리는 가장에게 결코 좋을 것 없는 짜증스러운 기색을 띠었다. 또한 팽크스 씨는 여러 차례, 자기가 화가도 아니고 생모델을 찾는 가발장이도 아니라는 사실과는 도무지 어울리지 않을 만큼 그 가장의 머리 혹들을 뚫어지게 쳐다보곤 했다."]], [["However, he steamed in and out of his little back Dock according as he was wanted or not wanted in the Patriarchal presence, and business had gone on in its customary course.", "그러나 그는 가장 앞에 불려 가느냐 마느냐에 따라 자기의 작은 뒤편 선거를 들락날락 증기를 뿜으며 드나들었고, 일은 늘 하던 대로 굴러갔다. 블리딩 하트 야드는 정해진 철마다 팽크스 씨에게 갈아엎어지고 캐스비 씨에게 거두어졌다."], ["Bleeding Heart Yard had been harrowed by Mr Pancks, and cropped by Mr Casby, at the regular seasons; Mr Pancks had taken all the drudgery and all the dirt of the business as _his_ share; Mr Casby had taken all the profits, all the ethereal vapour, and all the moonshine, as his share; and, in the form of words which that benevolent beamer generally employed on Saturday evenings, when he twirled his fat thumbs after striking the week’s balance, ‘everything had been satisfactory to all parties--all parties--satisfactory, sir, to all parties.’", "팽크스 씨는 그 일의 온갖 고역과 온갖 더러움을 자기 몫으로 떠맡았고, 캐스비 씨는 온갖 이문과 온갖 영묘한 김과 온갖 헛소리를 제 몫으로 가져갔다. 그리고 그 자애로운 빛을 뿜는 사람이 한 주의 결산을 맞춰 놓고 통통한 엄지를 빙빙 돌리며 토요일 저녁마다 으레 쓰던 말로 하자면, \"모든 일이 모두에게 만족스러웠습니다 — 모두에게 — 만족스러웠지요, 선생, 모두에게 말입니다.\""]], [["The Dock of the Steam-Tug, Pancks, had a leaden roof, which, frying in the very hot sunshine, may have heated the vessel.", "증기 예인선 팽크스의 선거에는 납지붕이 덮여 있었는데, 무척 더운 햇볕에 지글지글 달구어진 그것이 어쩌면 그 배까지 데웠는지도 몰랐다."], ["Be that as it may, one glowing Saturday evening, on being hailed by the lumbering bottle-green ship, the Tug instantly came working out of the Dock in a highly heated condition.", "어쨌든 어느 이글거리는 토요일 저녁, 그 묵직한 병녹색 배에게 부름을 받자 예인선은 곧장 잔뜩 달아오른 상태로 선거에서 빠져나왔다."]], [["‘Mr Pancks,’ was the Patriarchal remark, ‘you have been remiss, you have been remiss, sir.’", "\"팽크스 씨,\" 가장의 발언이었다. \"자네 게을렀어, 게을렀단 말이야, 선생.\""]], [["‘What do you mean by that?’ was the short rejoinder.", "\"그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\" 짤막한 응수였다."]], [["The Patriarchal state, always a state of calmness and composure, was so particularly serene that evening as to be provoking.", "가장의 상태는 늘 침착하고 차분한 상태였지만, 그날 저녁만은 유난히 평온하여 사람을 약 올릴 지경이었다."], ["Everybody else within the bills of mortality was hot; but the Patriarch was perfectly cool.", "사망 통계표 안의 다른 모든 이가 더위에 시달렸으나, 가장은 더할 나위 없이 시원했다."], ["Everybody was thirsty, and the Patriarch was drinking.", "모두가 목이 말랐으나, 가장은 마시고 있었다."], ["There was a fragrance of limes or lemons about him; and he made a drink of golden sherry, which shone in a large tumbler as if he were drinking the evening sunshine.", "그에게서는 라임이나 레몬 향이 감돌았고, 그는 황금빛 셰리주를 큼직한 잔에 따라 마셨는데, 마치 저녁 햇살을 마시는 것처럼 잔 속에서 그것이 빛났다."], ["This was bad, but not the worst.", "이것만으로도 고약했으나, 가장 고약한 것은 따로 있었다."], ["The worst was, that with his big blue eyes, and his polished head, and his long white hair, and his bottle-green legs stretched out before him, terminating in his easy shoes easily crossed at the instep, he had a radiant appearance of having in his extensive benevolence made the drink for the human species, while he himself wanted nothing but his own milk of human kindness.", "가장 고약한 것은, 그 큼직한 푸른 눈과 윤기 도는 머리통과 길고 흰 머리카락, 그리고 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 병녹색 다리가 발등께에서 느긋이 포개진 편안한 신발로 끝나는 그 모습으로, 마치 그가 자신의 드넓은 자비심으로 인류를 위해 그 음료를 만들어 두고서, 정작 자기 자신은 제 안의 인정이라는 젖 말고는 아무것도 원하지 않는다는 듯 환히 빛나는 자태였다는 점이다."]], [["Wherefore, Mr Pancks said, ‘What do you mean by that?’ and put his hair up with both hands, in a highly portentous manner.", "그리하여 팽크스 씨는 \"그게 무슨 말씀입니까?\" 하고 말하며 두 손으로 머리카락을 자못 불길한 모양새로 곤두세웠다."]], [["‘I mean, Mr Pancks, that you must be sharper with the people, sharper with the people, much sharper with the people, sir.", "\"내 말은, 팽크스 씨, 자네가 사람들한테 더 매섭게 굴어야 한다는 거야, 더 매섭게, 훨씬 더 매섭게 굴어야 해, 선생."], ["You don’t squeeze them.", "자네는 그들을 쥐어짜지 않아."], ["You don’t squeeze them.", "쥐어짜지 않는단 말이야."], ["Your receipts are not up to the mark.", "자네 수금액이 기준에 못 미쳐."], ["You must squeeze them, sir, or our connection will not continue to be as satisfactory as I could wish it to be to all parties.", "쥐어짜야 해, 선생, 안 그러면 우리 거래가 내가 바라는 만큼 모두에게 만족스럽게 이어지지 않을 거야."], ["All parties.’", "모두에게 말이지.\""]], [["‘_Don’t_ I squeeze ‘em?’ retorted Mr Pancks.", "\"제가 쥐어짜지 않는다고요?\" 팽크스 씨가 되받았다."], ["‘What else am I made for?’", "\"그럼 제가 달리 무엇에 쓰이려고 만들어졌습니까?\""]], [["‘You are made for nothing else, Mr Pancks.", "\"자네는 달리 아무것에도 쓰이려고 만들어진 게 아니야, 팽크스 씨. 자네는 자기 본분을 다하라고 만들어졌는데, 본분을 다하지 않아."], ["You are made to do your duty, but you don’t do your duty.", "자네는 쥐어짜라고 삯을 받는 거고, 삯을 받자면 쥐어짜야 하는 거야.\" 가장은 전혀 예상하지도 의도하지도 않았던 이 존슨 박사풍의 빛나는 표현에 스스로도 어찌나 놀랐던지 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["You are paid to squeeze, and you must squeeze to pay.’ The Patriarch so much surprised himself by this brilliant turn, after Dr Johnson, which he had not in the least expected or intended, that he laughed aloud; and repeated with great satisfaction, as he twirled his thumbs and nodded at his youthful portrait, ‘Paid to squeeze, sir, and must squeeze to pay.’", "그러고는 엄지를 빙빙 돌리며 제 젊은 시절 초상화를 향해 고개를 끄덕이고는, 자못 흡족하게 되풀이했다. \"쥐어짜라고 삯을 받고, 삯을 받자면 쥐어짜야 하는 거야, 선생.\""]], [["‘Oh,’ said Pancks.", "\"오,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Anything more?’", "\"또 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir, yes, sir. Something more.", "\"있지, 선생, 있고말고."], ["You will please, Mr Pancks, to squeeze the Yard again, the first thing on Monday morning.’", "더 있어. 팽크스 씨, 월요일 아침에 제일 먼저 그 야드를 다시 쥐어짜 주게나.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Pancks.", "\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Ain’t that too soon?", "\"그건 너무 이르지 않습니까?"], ["I squeezed it dry to-day.’", "오늘 바싹 말라붙도록 쥐어짰는데요.\""]], [["‘Nonsense, sir.", "\"무슨 소리야, 선생."], ["Not near the mark, not near the mark.’", "기준 근처에도 못 갔어, 근처에도.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Pancks, watching him as he benevolently gulped down a good draught of his mixture.", "\"오!\" 팽크스가, 그가 그 혼합 음료를 한 모금 자애롭게 꿀꺽 들이켜는 것을 지켜보며 말했다."], ["‘Anything more?’", "\"또 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir, yes, sir, something more.", "\"있지, 선생, 있어, 더 있어."], ["I am not at all pleased, Mr Pancks, with my daughter; not at all pleased.", "팽크스 씨, 나는 내 딸이 도무지 마음에 들지 않아. 도무지 마음에 안 들어."], ["Besides calling much too often to inquire for Mrs Clennam, Mrs Clennam, who is not just now in circumstances that are by any means calculated to--to be satisfactory to all parties, she goes, Mr Pancks, unless I am much deceived, to inquire for Mr Clennam in jail.", "클레넘 부인의 안부를 너무 자주 물으러 다니는 것도 모자라 — 클레넘 부인은 지금 무슨 일이든 모두에게 만족스러울 만한 그런 처지가 결코 못 되는데 말이야 — 그 애가, 팽크스 씨, 내가 크게 잘못 안 게 아니라면, 옥에 갇힌 클레넘 씨의 안부까지 물으러 다닌단 말이지."], ["In jail.’", "옥에 말이야.\""]], [["‘He’s laid up, you know,’ said Pancks.", "\"그가 앓아누웠으니까요.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Perhaps it’s kind.’", "\"아마 친절을 베푸는 걸 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Pooh, pooh, Mr Pancks.", "\"쳇, 쳇, 팽크스 씨."], ["She has nothing to do with that, nothing to do with that.", "그 애가 거기에 상관할 바 아니야, 상관할 바 아니라고."], ["I can’t allow it.", "나는 그걸 용납할 수 없어."], ["Let him pay his debts and come out, come out; pay his debts, and come out.’", "빚을 갚고 나오라고 해, 나오라고 해. 빚을 갚고 나오란 말이야.\""]], [["Although Mr Pancks’s hair was standing up like strong wire, he gave it another double-handed impulse in the perpendicular direction, and smiled at his proprietor in a most hideous manner.", "팽크스 씨의 머리카락은 빳빳한 철사처럼 곤두서 있었지만, 그는 두 손으로 그것을 다시 한 번 수직으로 곧추세우고는, 더없이 흉측한 모양새로 자기 고용주를 향해 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["‘You will please to mention to my daughter, Mr Pancks, that I can’t allow it, can’t allow it,’ said the Patriarch blandly.", "\"내 딸한테 전해 주게나, 팽크스 씨, 나는 그걸 용납할 수 없다고, 용납할 수 없다고 말이야.\" 가장이 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["‘Oh!’ said Pancks.", "\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘You couldn’t mention it yourself?’", "\"직접 말씀하실 수는 없습니까?\""]], [["‘No, sir, no; you are paid to mention it,’ the blundering old booby could not resist the temptation of trying it again, ‘and you must mention it to pay, mention it to pay.’", "\"없지, 선생, 없어. 자네가 그걸 전하라고 삯을 받는 거야.\" 그 얼간이 늙은이는 다시 한 번 써먹어 보고 싶은 유혹을 떨치지 못했다. \"그러니 삯을 받자면 그걸 전해야 하는 거지, 전해야 삯을 받는 거고.\""]], [["‘Oh!’ said Pancks.", "\"오!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘Anything more?’", "\"또 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.", "\"있지, 선생."], ["It appears to me, Mr Pancks, that you yourself are too often and too much in that direction, that direction.", "내가 보기엔 말이야, 팽크스 씨, 자네 자신이 그쪽으로 너무 자주, 너무 많이 드나드는 것 같아, 그쪽으로 말이야."], ["I recommend you, Mr Pancks, to dismiss from your attention both your own losses and other people’s losses, and to mind your business, mind your business.’", "팽크스 씨, 나는 자네한테 권하건대, 자네 자신의 손실이든 남의 손실이든 다 머릿속에서 떨쳐 버리고, 자네 일에나 신경을 쓰게, 자네 일에나.\""]], [["Mr Pancks acknowledged this recommendation with such an extraordinarily abrupt, short, and loud utterance of the monosyllable ‘Oh!’ that even the unwieldy Patriarch moved his blue eyes in something of a hurry, to look at him.", "팽크스 씨는 이 권고를 어찌나 유별나게 불쑥, 짧게, 그리고 큰 소리로 \"오!\"라는 한 음절로 받았던지, 그 둔중한 가장조차 푸른 눈을 다소 황급히 움직여 그를 쳐다볼 정도였다."], ["Mr Pancks, with a sniff of corresponding intensity, then added, ‘Anything more?’", "그러자 팽크스 씨는 그에 걸맞은 강도로 한 번 콧방귀를 뀌고는 덧붙였다. \"또 있습니까?\""]], [["‘Not at present, sir, not at present.", "\"지금은 없어, 선생, 지금은 없어. 나는 나가 봐야겠네.\""], ["I am going,’ said the Patriarch, finishing his mixture, and rising with an amiable air, ‘to take a little stroll, a little stroll.", "가장이 혼합 음료를 다 비우고 상냥한 기색으로 일어서며 말했다. \"잠깐 산책이나 하려고, 잠깐 거닐려고 말이야."], ["Perhaps I shall find you here when I come back.", "돌아왔을 때 자네가 여기 있을지도 모르겠군. 만약 없다면, 선생, 본분이야, 본분."], ["If not, sir, duty, duty; squeeze, squeeze, squeeze, on Monday; squeeze on Monday!’", "월요일엔 쥐어짜, 쥐어짜, 쥐어짜라고. 월요일엔 쥐어짜!\""]], [["Mr Pancks, after another stiffening of his hair, looked on at the Patriarchal assumption of the broad-brimmed hat, with a momentary appearance of indecision contending with a sense of injury.", "팽크스 씨는 머리카락을 한 번 더 빳빳이 세운 뒤, 가장이 챙 넓은 모자를 집어 쓰는 모습을, 한순간 망설임과 모욕감이 서로 다투는 듯한 표정으로 지켜보았다."], ["He was also hotter than at first, and breathed harder.", "그는 또한 처음보다 더 달아올라 더 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬었다."], ["But he suffered Mr Casby to go out, without offering any further remark, and then took a peep at him over the little green window-blinds.", "그러나 그는 더는 한마디도 건네지 않고 캐스비 씨가 나가도록 내버려 두었다가, 작은 초록색 차양 너머로 그를 슬쩍 엿보았다."], ["‘I thought so,’ he observed.", "\"그럴 줄 알았어.\" 그가 중얼거렸다."], ["‘I knew where you were bound to.", "\"당신이 어디로 향할지 다 알고 있었지. 좋아!\""], ["Good!’ He then steamed back to his Dock, put it carefully in order, took down his hat, looked round the Dock, said ‘Good-bye!’ and puffed away on his own account.", "그러고는 다시 증기를 뿜으며 자기 선거로 돌아가 그곳을 꼼꼼히 정돈하고, 모자를 내려 쓰고, 선거를 한 바퀴 둘러보더니, \"잘 있어라!\" 하고는 제 볼일을 보러 푹푹거리며 떠났다."], ["He steered straight for Mrs Plornish’s end of Bleeding Heart Yard, and arrived there, at the top of the steps, hotter than ever.", "그는 블리딩 하트 야드의 플로니시 부인 쪽 끝을 향해 곧장 키를 잡았고, 그 계단 꼭대기에 도착했을 때는 어느 때보다도 더 달아올라 있었다."]], [["At the top of the steps, resisting Mrs Plornish’s invitations to come and sit along with father in Happy Cottage--which to his relief were not so numerous as they would have been on any other night than Saturday, when the connection who so gallantly supported the business with everything but money gave their orders freely--at the top of the steps Mr Pancks remained until he beheld the Patriarch, who always entered the Yard at the other end, slowly advancing, beaming, and surrounded by suitors.", "계단 꼭대기에서, 들어와 해피 코티지에서 아버님과 함께 앉아 있으라는 플로니시 부인의 권유를 — 토요일이라 그 권유가 다른 어느 밤보다는 적어서 그로서는 다행이었는데, 돈만 빼고 온갖 것으로 그토록 늠름하게 가게를 받쳐 주는 단골들이 토요일이면 거리낌 없이 주문을 넣었기 때문이다 — 뿌리치며, 팽크스 씨는 계단 꼭대기에 그대로 머물러, 늘 반대편 끝으로 야드에 들어서는 가장이 천천히 다가오는 모습을, 빛을 뿜으며 청원자들에게 둘러싸여 오는 모습을 보게 되었다."], ["Then Mr Pancks descended and bore down upon him, with his utmost pressure of steam on.", "그러자 팽크스 씨는 계단을 내려가, 증기를 한껏 압축해 그를 향해 들이닥쳤다."]], [["The Patriarch, approaching with his usual benignity, was surprised to see Mr Pancks, but supposed him to have been stimulated to an immediate squeeze instead of postponing that operation until Monday.", "가장은 여느 때의 인자함으로 다가오다가 팽크스 씨를 보고 놀랐으나, 그가 그 쥐어짜는 작업을 월요일로 미루지 않고 당장 해치우고 싶어 자극받았으려니 짐작했다."], ["The population of the Yard were astonished at the meeting, for the two powers had never been seen there together, within the memory of the oldest Bleeding Heart.", "야드의 주민들은 그 만남에 깜짝 놀랐는데, 가장 나이 많은 블리딩 하트의 기억으로도 그 두 권력이 이곳에서 함께 있는 모습은 본 적이 없었기 때문이다."], ["But they were overcome by unutterable amazement when Mr Pancks, going close up to the most venerable of men and halting in front of the bottle-green waistcoat, made a trigger of his right thumb and forefinger, applied the same to the brim of the broad-brimmed hat, and, with singular smartness and precision, shot it off the polished head as if it had been a large marble.", "그러나 그들은 말로 다 할 수 없는 경악에 휩싸였으니, 팽크스 씨가 그 더없이 존경스러운 사람에게 바짝 다가가 병녹색 조끼 앞에 멈춰 서더니, 오른손 엄지와 검지로 방아쇠 모양을 만들어 챙 넓은 모자의 챙에 갖다 대고는, 유별나게 날렵하고 정확하게, 마치 커다란 구슬을 튕기듯 그 윤기 도는 머리통에서 모자를 튕겨 날려 버렸던 것이다."]], [["Having taken this little liberty with the Patriarchal person, Mr Pancks further astounded and attracted the Bleeding Hearts by saying in an audible voice, ‘Now, you sugary swindler, I mean to have it out with you!’", "가장이라는 인물에게 이런 작은 무례를 범한 뒤, 팽크스 씨는 또렷이 들리는 목소리로 이렇게 말하여 블리딩 하트들을 한층 더 놀라게 하고 끌어들였다. \"자, 이 달콤한 사기꾼아, 내 너하고 끝장을 봐야겠다!\""]], [["Mr Pancks and the Patriarch were instantly the centre of a press, all eyes and ears; windows were thrown open, and door-steps were thronged.", "팽크스 씨와 가장은 순식간에, 온통 눈과 귀뿐인 군중의 한복판이 되었다. 창문이 활짝 열리고 문간 계단이 사람들로 북적였다."]], [["‘What do you pretend to be?’ said Mr Pancks.", "\"당신은 자기를 뭐라고 내세웁니까?\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘What’s your moral game?", "\"당신의 도덕 놀음은 뭡니까?"], ["What do you go in for?", "당신은 뭘 표방합니까?"], ["Benevolence, an’t it?", "자비, 그렇지요?"], ["You benevolent!’ Here Mr Pancks, apparently without the intention of hitting him, but merely to relieve his mind and expend his superfluous power in wholesome exercise, aimed a blow at the bumpy head, which the bumpy head ducked to avoid.", "당신이 자비롭다고요!\" 여기서 팽크스 씨는, 그를 때릴 의도는 딱히 없이 그저 마음을 풀고 남아도는 힘을 건전한 운동으로 써 버리려는 양, 그 혹투성이 머리통을 향해 한 방 겨누었고, 혹투성이 머리통은 그것을 피하려 몸을 숙였다."], ["This singular performance was repeated, to the ever-increasing admiration of the spectators, at the end of every succeeding article of Mr Pancks’s oration.", "이 기묘한 동작은 팽크스 씨의 연설이 한 대목씩 끝날 때마다 되풀이되어, 구경꾼들의 감탄을 점점 더 키웠다."]], [["‘I have discharged myself from your service,’ said Pancks, ‘that I may tell you what you are.", "\"내가 당신 밑에서 스스로 그만두었으니,\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["You’re one of a lot of impostors that are the worst lot of all the lots to be met with.", "\"당신이 어떤 자인지 일러 주겠소. 당신은 세상에서 만날 수 있는 온갖 무리 가운데 가장 못된 무리에 속하는 사기꾼이오."], ["Speaking as a sufferer by both, I don’t know that I wouldn’t as soon have the Merdle lot as your lot.", "양쪽 다에 당한 사람으로서 말하건대, 나는 당신 무리보다는 차라리 머들 무리를 택하지 않겠다고 장담 못 하겠소."], ["You’re a driver in disguise, a screwer by deputy, a wringer, and squeezer, and shaver by substitute.", "당신은 변장한 몰이꾼이고, 대리로 죄어 대는 자고, 대신 시켜 짜내고 쥐어짜고 벗겨 먹는 자요."], ["You’re a philanthropic sneak.", "당신은 자선가인 척하는 비열한 놈이오."], ["You’re a shabby deceiver!’", "당신은 추레한 사기꾼이오!\""]], [["(The repetition of the performance at this point was received with a burst of laughter.)", "(이 대목에서 그 동작이 되풀이되자 웃음이 와르르 터졌다.)"]], [["‘Ask these good people who’s the hard man here.", "\"여기 이 선량한 사람들한테 누가 모진 사람이냐고 물어보시오."], ["They’ll tell you Pancks, I believe.’", "다들 팽크스라고 할 거요, 내가 보기엔.\""]], [["This was confirmed with cries of ‘Certainly,’ and ‘Hear!’", "이 말은 \"그렇고말고,\" \"옳소!\" 하는 외침으로 확인되었다."]], [["‘But I tell you, good people--Casby!", "\"하지만 내가 일러 주리다, 선량한 양반들 — 캐스비요!"], ["This mound of meekness, this lump of love, this bottle-green smiler, this is your driver!’ said Pancks.", "이 온순함의 더미, 이 사랑의 덩어리, 이 병녹색 미소꾼, 이자가 바로 당신들의 몰이꾼이오!\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘If you want to see the man who would flay you alive--here he is!", "\"당신들을 산 채로 가죽 벗길 자를 보고 싶거든 — 바로 여기 있소!"], ["Don’t look for him in me, at thirty shillings a week, but look for him in Casby, at I don’t know how much a year!’", "주급 삼십 실링짜리인 나한테서 그자를 찾지 말고, 일 년에 얼마인지도 모를 캐스비한테서 그자를 찾으시오!\""]], [["‘Good!’ cried several voices.", "\"옳소!\" 여러 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["‘Hear Mr Pancks!’", "\"팽크스 씨 말을 들어 봅시다!\""]], [["‘Hear Mr Pancks?’ cried that gentleman (after repeating the popular performance).", "\"팽크스 씨 말을 들어 보자고요?\" 그 신사가 (그 인기 있는 동작을 한 번 더 되풀이한 뒤) 외쳤다."], ["‘Yes, I should think so!", "\"그래요, 그래야 하고말고요!"], ["It’s almost time to hear Mr Pancks.", "이제 팽크스 씨 말을 들을 때도 됐지요."], ["Mr Pancks has come down into the Yard to-night on purpose that you should hear him.", "팽크스 씨는 바로 당신들이 자기 말을 듣게 하려고 오늘 밤 이 야드로 내려온 거요."], ["Pancks is only the Works; but here’s the Winder!’", "팽크스는 그저 기계 장치일 뿐이오. 하지만 여기 태엽 감는 자가 있소!\""]], [["The audience would have gone over to Mr Pancks, as one man, woman, and child, but for the long, grey, silken locks, and the broad-brimmed hat.", "청중은 남자고 여자고 아이고 할 것 없이 한 몸이 되어 팽크스 씨 편으로 넘어갔을 테지만, 저 길고 잿빛인 비단 같은 머리타래와 챙 넓은 모자 때문에 망설였다."]], [["‘Here’s the Stop,’ said Pancks, ‘that sets the tune to be ground.", "\"여기 멈춤쇠가 있소.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["And there is but one tune, and its name is Grind, Grind, Grind!", "\"갈아 낼 가락을 정해 주는 멈춤쇠 말이오. 그런데 그 가락은 단 하나뿐이고, 그 이름은 갈아라, 갈아라, 갈아라요!"], ["Here’s the Proprietor, and here’s his Grubber.", "여기 소유주가 있고, 여기 그자의 막일꾼이 있소."], ["Why, good people, when he comes smoothly spinning through the Yard to-night, like a slow-going benevolent Humming-Top, and when you come about him with your complaints of the Grubber, you don’t know what a cheat the Proprietor is!", "자, 선량한 양반들, 이자가 오늘 밤 굼뜨고 자애로운 팽이처럼 야드를 매끄럽게 빙빙 돌며 다가올 때, 그리고 당신들이 막일꾼에 대한 불평을 들고 그를 에워쌀 때, 당신들은 그 소유주가 얼마나 사기꾼인지 알지 못하오!"], ["What do you think of his showing himself to-night, that I may have all the blame on Monday?", "월요일에 모든 비난을 내가 다 뒤집어쓰도록 이자가 오늘 밤 제 모습을 드러낸 것을 어떻게 생각하시오?"], ["What do you think of his having had me over the coals this very evening, because I don’t squeeze you enough?", "내가 당신들을 충분히 쥐어짜지 않는다고 바로 오늘 저녁에 이자가 나를 호되게 닦달한 것을 어떻게 생각하시오?"], ["What do you think of my being, at the present moment, under special orders to squeeze you dry on Monday?’", "내가 지금 이 순간, 월요일에 당신들을 바싹 말라붙도록 쥐어짜라는 특별 명령을 받은 처지인 것을 어떻게 생각하시오?\""]], [["The reply was given in a murmur of ‘Shame!’ and ‘Shabby!’", "그 대답은 \"창피한 줄 알아라!\" \"추레하다!\" 하는 웅성거림으로 나왔다."]], [["‘Shabby?’ snorted Pancks.", "\"추레하다고요?\""], ["‘Yes, I should think so!", "팽크스가 콧방귀를 뀌었다."], ["The lot that your Casby belongs to, is the shabbiest of all the lots.", "\"그래요, 그렇고말고요! 당신네 캐스비가 속한 무리는 온갖 무리 가운데서도 가장 추레한 무리요."], ["Setting their Grubbers on, at a wretched pittance, to do what they’re ashamed and afraid to do and pretend not to do, but what they will have done, or give a man no rest!", "비참할 만큼 쥐꼬리만 한 삯을 주고 제 막일꾼들을 풀어, 자기들은 부끄럽고 두려워 못 하는 짓, 안 하는 척하면서도 기어이 시키고야 마는 짓, 안 하면 사람을 가만두지 않는 그런 짓을 하게 하지요!"], ["Imposing on you to give their Grubbers nothing but blame, and to give them nothing but credit!", "당신들을 속여서, 그 막일꾼들한테는 비난만 안기고, 자기들한테는 공치사만 돌리게 하고 말이오!"], ["Why, the worst-looking cheat in all this town who gets the value of eighteenpence under false pretences, an’t half such a cheat as this sign-post of The Casby’s Head here!’", "아니, 이 온 도시에서 거짓 핑계로 일 실링 육 펜스어치를 우려내는 가장 흉악해 보이는 사기꾼도, 여기 이 '캐스비 머리' 간판만큼 사기꾼은 절반도 못 되오!\""]], [["Cries of ‘That’s true!’ and ‘No more he an’t!’", "\"맞는 말이오!\" \"그렇고말고요!\" 하는 외침이 터졌다."]], [["‘And see what you get of these fellows, besides,’ said Pancks.", "\"게다가 이런 자들한테서 당신들이 또 무엇을 얻는지 보시오.\" 팽크스가 말했다."], ["‘See what more you get of these precious Humming-Tops, revolving among you with such smoothness that you’ve no idea of the pattern painted on ‘em, or the little window in ‘em.", "\"이 잘난 팽이들한테서 당신들이 또 무엇을 얻는지 보란 말이오. 어찌나 매끄럽게 당신들 사이를 빙빙 도는지, 그 위에 그려진 무늬도, 그 안의 작은 창문도 당신들은 짐작조차 못 하오."], ["I wish to call your attention to myself for a moment.", "잠깐 내게 주목해 주시오."], ["I an’t an agreeable style of chap, I know that very well.’", "나는 호감 가는 종류의 사내가 아니오, 그건 나도 아주 잘 아오.\""]], [["The auditory were divided on this point; its more uncompromising members crying, ‘No, you are not,’ and its politer materials, ‘Yes, you are.’", "청중은 이 점에서 의견이 갈렸으니, 더 타협 없는 축들은 \"아니, 당신은 그렇지 않소,\" 하고 외쳤고, 더 점잖은 부류는 \"맞소, 당신은 그렇소,\" 하고 외쳤다."]], [["‘I am, in general,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘a dry, uncomfortable, dreary Plodder and Grubber.", "\"나는 대체로,\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["That’s your humble servant.", "\"메마르고 거북하고 음울한, 터덜터덜 일하는 막일꾼이오."], ["There’s his full-length portrait, painted by himself and presented to you, warranted a likeness!", "그게 바로 당신들의 미천한 종이오. 여기 그자가 손수 그려 당신들에게 바치는, 똑 닮았다고 보증하는 전신 초상화가 있소!"], ["But what’s a man to be, with such a man as this for his Proprietor?", "그러나 이런 자를 소유주로 둔 사람이 무엇이 될 수 있겠소?"], ["What can be expected of him?", "그에게서 무엇을 기대할 수 있겠소?"], ["Did anybody ever find boiled mutton and caper-sauce growing in a cocoa-nut?’", "누가 코코넛 속에서 삶은 양고기에 케이퍼 소스가 자라는 걸 본 적이 있소?\""]], [["None of the Bleeding Hearts ever had, it was clear from the alacrity of their response.", "블리딩 하트 가운데 그런 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었으니, 그들의 잽싼 호응으로 보아 분명했다."]], [["‘Well,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘and neither will you find in Grubbers like myself, under Proprietors like this, pleasant qualities.", "\"그렇소.\" 팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["I’ve been a Grubber from a boy. What has my life been?", "\"그러니 나 같은 막일꾼한테서, 이런 자를 소유주로 둔 막일꾼한테서 당신들은 유쾌한 자질을 찾지 못할 거요."], ["Fag and grind, fag and grind, turn the wheel, turn the wheel!", "나는 어릴 적부터 막일꾼이었소. 내 인생이 뭐였소?"], ["I haven’t been agreeable to myself, and I haven’t been likely to be agreeable to anybody else.", "죽도록 일하고 갈아 대고, 죽도록 일하고 갈아 대고, 바퀴를 돌리고 또 돌리고! 나는 나 자신한테도 유쾌하지 못했고, 남한테 유쾌할 리도 없었소."], ["If I was a shilling a week less useful in ten years’ time, this impostor would give me a shilling a week less; if as useful a man could be got at sixpence cheaper, he would be taken in my place at sixpence cheaper.", "십 년 뒤에 내가 주급 일 실링어치만큼 덜 쓸모 있어지면, 이 사기꾼은 내 주급을 일 실링 깎을 거요. 나만큼 쓸모 있는 사람을 육 펜스 더 싸게 구할 수 있으면, 내 자리에 그자를 육 펜스 더 싸게 들일 거요."], ["Bargain and sale, bless you!", "흥정하고 사고팔기지, 원 참! 확고한 원칙이라나!"], ["Fixed principles!", "참 훌륭한 간판이오, 이 '캐스비 머리'라는 건.\""], ["It’s a mighty fine sign-post, is The Casby’s Head,’ said Mr Pancks, surveying it with anything rather than admiration; ‘but the real name of the House is the Sham’s Arms.", "팽크스 씨가 감탄과는 전혀 거리가 먼 눈길로 그것을 훑어보며 말했다. \"하지만 이 집의 진짜 이름은 '가짜의 문장'이오."], ["Its motto is, Keep the Grubber always at it.", "그 좌우명은, 막일꾼을 늘 부려 먹어라요. 여기 계신 분 가운데,\""], ["Is any gentleman present,’ said Mr Pancks, breaking off and looking round, ‘acquainted with the English Grammar?’", "팽크스 씨가 말을 끊고 둘러보며 말했다. \"영문법에 밝은 신사분이 있소?\""]], [["Bleeding Heart Yard was shy of claiming that acquaintance.", "블리딩 하트 야드는 그런 데 밝다고 나서기를 꺼렸다."]], [["‘It’s no matter,’ said Mr Pancks, ‘I merely wish to remark that the task this Proprietor has set me, has been never to leave off conjugating the Imperative Mood Present Tense of the verb To keep always at it.", "\"상관없소.\""], ["Keep thou always at it.", "팽크스 씨가 말했다."], ["Let him keep always at it. Keep we or do we keep always at it.", "\"그저 한마디 하고 싶은 건, 이 소유주가 내게 맡긴 과업이 '늘 부려 먹다'라는 동사의 명령법 현재 시제를 끊임없이 활용하는 일이었다는 거요."], ["Keep ye or do ye or you keep always at it.", "너는 늘 부려 먹어라. 그로 하여금 늘 부려 먹게 하라."], ["Let them keep always at it.", "우리는 늘 부려 먹자."], ["Here is your benevolent Patriarch of a Casby, and there is his golden rule.", "너희는 늘 부려 먹어라. 그들로 하여금 늘 부려 먹게 하라."], ["He is uncommonly improving to look at, and I am not at all so.", "여기 캐스비라는 자애로운 가장이 있고, 저기 그자의 황금률이 있소."], ["He is as sweet as honey, and I am as dull as ditch-water.", "그는 보기에 더없이 교화적인데, 나는 전혀 그렇지가 않소. 그는 꿀처럼 달고, 나는 도랑물처럼 따분하오."], ["He provides the pitch, and I handle it, and it sticks to me.", "그는 끈끈한 역청을 대 주고, 나는 그걸 만지고, 그게 내게 들러붙소. 자,\""], ["Now,’ said Mr Pancks, closing upon his late Proprietor again, from whom he had withdrawn a little for the better display of him to the Yard; ‘as I am not accustomed to speak in public, and as I have made a rather lengthy speech, all circumstances considered, I shall bring my observations to a close by requesting you to get out of this.’", "팽크스 씨가, 야드에 그를 더 잘 보여 주려고 잠시 물러나 있다가 다시 그 전직 소유주에게 바싹 다가서며 말했다. \"나는 사람들 앞에서 말하는 데 익숙지 않은 데다, 이런저런 사정을 다 따져도 꽤나 긴 연설을 했으니, 당신더러 여기서 꺼지라고 청하면서 내 소견을 마무리하겠소.\""]], [["The Last of the Patriarchs had been so seized by assault, and required so much room to catch an idea in, an so much more room to turn it in, that he had not a word to offer in reply.", "가장들의 마지막 후예는 워낙 기습을 당한 데다, 한 가지 생각을 붙잡는 데도 그토록 많은 여유가 필요하고 그것을 굴리는 데는 더더욱 많은 여유가 필요한 위인이라, 대꾸할 말이 한마디도 없었다."], ["He appeared to be meditating some Patriarchal way out of his delicate position, when Mr Pancks, once more suddenly applying the trigger to his hat, shot it off again with his former dexterity.", "그가 이 난처한 처지에서 빠져나갈 무슨 가장다운 방도를 궁리하는 듯 보일 때, 팽크스 씨가 다시 한 번 불쑥 그의 모자에 방아쇠를 갖다 대어 예의 그 솜씨로 또 튕겨 날려 버렸다."], ["On the preceding occasion, one or two of the Bleeding Heart Yarders had obsequiously picked it up and handed it to its owner; but Mr Pancks had now so far impressed his audience, that the Patriarch had to turn and stoop for it himself.", "앞서 한 번은 블리딩 하트 야드 사람 한둘이 굽실거리며 그것을 주워 주인에게 건네주었으나, 팽크스 씨가 이제 청중을 어찌나 사로잡아 놓았던지 가장은 손수 돌아서서 몸을 굽혀 그것을 주워야 했다."]], [["Quick as lightning, Mr Pancks, who, for some moments, had had his right hand in his coat pocket, whipped out a pair of shears, swooped upon the Patriarch behind, and snipped off short the sacred locks that flowed upon his shoulders.", "번개처럼 빠르게, 한동안 오른손을 외투 주머니에 넣고 있던 팽크스 씨가 가위 한 자루를 휙 꺼내 가장의 등 뒤로 덮쳐들더니, 그의 어깨 위로 흘러내리던 그 신성한 머리타래를 싹둑 짧게 잘라 버렸다."], ["In a paroxysm of animosity and rapidity, Mr Pancks then caught the broad-brimmed hat out of the astounded Patriarch’s hand, cut it down into a mere stewpan, and fixed it on the Patriarch’s head.", "그러고는 적의와 신속함이 발작하듯 치솟아, 팽크스 씨는 어안이 벙벙해진 가장의 손에서 챙 넓은 모자를 낚아채 그것을 한낱 스튜 냄비 꼴로 잘라 내고는, 가장의 머리에 눌러 씌웠다."]], [["Before the frightful results of this desperate action, Mr Pancks himself recoiled in consternation.", "이 무모한 행동이 빚어낸 끔찍한 결과 앞에서, 팽크스 씨 자신도 경악하여 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["A bare-polled, goggle-eyed, big-headed lumbering personage stood staring at him, not in the least impressive, not in the least venerable, who seemed to have started out of the earth to ask what was become of Casby.", "민머리에 눈이 휘둥그렇고 머리통이 큼직한 둔중한 인물이, 조금도 인상적이지도 조금도 위엄 있지도 않은 모습으로 그를 멀거니 쳐다보고 서 있었는데, 마치 땅속에서 불쑥 솟아나 캐스비가 어찌 되었느냐고 묻는 것만 같았다."], ["After staring at this phantom in return, in silent awe, Mr Pancks threw down his shears, and fled for a place of hiding, where he might lie sheltered from the consequences of his crime.", "이 유령을 마주 멀거니 쳐다보며 말없이 두려움에 잠겼다가, 팽크스 씨는 가위를 내던지고 제 죄의 결과로부터 몸을 피해 숨을 곳을 찾아 달아났다."], ["Mr Pancks deemed it prudent to use all possible despatch in making off, though he was pursued by nothing but the sound of laughter in Bleeding Heart Yard, rippling through the air and making it ring again.", "블리딩 하트 야드에 울려 퍼지며 공중에 잔물결처럼 번져 다시금 메아리치는 웃음소리 말고는 아무것도 그를 뒤쫓지 않았으나, 팽크스 씨는 있는 힘껏 서둘러 달아나는 것이 현명하다고 여겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER 33. Going!", "# CHAPTER 33. Going!"]], [["The changes of a fevered room are slow and fluctuating; but the changes of the fevered world are rapid and irrevocable.", "열병에 시달리는 방 안의 변화는 더디고 출렁이지만, 열병에 시달리는 세상의 변화는 빠르고 돌이킬 수 없다."]], [["It was Little Dorrit’s lot to wait upon both kinds of change.", "두 종류의 변화를 모두 보살피는 것이 리틀 도릿의 몫이었다."], ["The Marshalsea walls, during a portion of every day, again embraced her in their shadows as their child, while she thought for Clennam, worked for him, watched him, and only left him, still to devote her utmost love and care to him.", "하루의 일부 동안, 마셜시의 담장은 다시금 그 그림자로 그녀를 제 자식처럼 품었고, 그동안 그녀는 클레넘을 위해 궁리하고, 그를 위해 일하고, 그를 지켜보았으며, 오직 그에게 자신의 온 사랑과 정성을 쏟기 위해서만 그의 곁을 떠났다."], ["Her part in the life outside the gate urged its pressing claims upon her too, and her patience untiringly responded to them.", "문 밖의 삶에서 맡은 그녀의 몫 또한 절박하게 권리를 주장하며 그녀를 다그쳤고, 그녀의 인내는 지치지 않고 거기에 응했다. 거기엔 패니가 있었다."], ["Here was Fanny, proud, fitful, whimsical, further advanced in that disqualified state for going into society which had so much fretted her on the evening of the tortoise-shell knife, resolved always to want comfort, resolved not to be comforted, resolved to be deeply wronged, and resolved that nobody should have the audacity to think her so.", "거만하고 변덕스럽고 종잡을 수 없는 그녀는, 거북 등딱지 손잡이 칼 사건이 있던 그 저녁 그토록 그녀를 안달하게 만들었던 그 사교계 부적격 상태로 한층 더 깊이 나아가, 늘 위안을 갈구하기로 작정하고, 결코 위안받지 않기로 작정하고, 깊이 부당한 대접을 받았다고 여기기로 작정하고, 그러면서도 그 누구도 감히 자신을 그런 처지로 여겨서는 안 된다고 작정한 상태였다. 거기엔 그녀의 오라비도 있었다."], ["Here was her brother, a weak, proud, tipsy, young old man, shaking from head to foot, talking as indistinctly as if some of the money he plumed himself upon had got into his mouth and couldn’t be got out, unable to walk alone in any act of his life, and patronising the sister whom he selfishly loved (he always had that negative merit, ill-starred and ill-launched Tip!) because he suffered her to lead him.", "약하고 거만하며 술에 절은, 젊은 늙은이로, 머리부터 발끝까지 떨면서, 스스로 그토록 으스대던 그 돈의 일부가 입속에 들어가 빠져나오지 못하기라도 한 듯 발음이 불분명했고, 삶의 그 어떤 행동도 혼자서는 해내지 못했으며, 자기를 이끌어 주는 것을 허락한다는 이유로(불운하게 태어나 불운하게 출발한 팁! 그에게는 늘 그 소극적인 미덕만은 있었다) 이기적으로나마 사랑하는 누이를 짐짓 거두는 시늉을 했다."], ["Here was Mrs Merdle in gauzy mourning--the original cap whereof had possibly been rent to pieces in a fit of grief, but had certainly yielded to a highly becoming article from the Parisian market--warring with Fanny foot to foot, and breasting her with her desolate bosom every hour in the day.", "거기엔 머들 부인이 얇은 상복 차림으로 있었다. 그 원래의 모자는 아마 슬픔의 발작 속에 갈가리 찢겼을지 모르나, 분명 파리 시장에서 온 무척 잘 어울리는 물건에 자리를 내준 상태였고, 그녀는 패니와 한 치도 물러섬 없이 다투며 하루 종일 시시각각 그 황량한 가슴을 들이대며 맞섰다."], ["Here was poor Mr Sparkler, not knowing how to keep the peace between them, but humbly inclining to the opinion that they could do no better than agree that they were both remarkably fine women, and that there was no nonsense about either of them--for which gentle recommendation they united in falling upon him frightfully.", "거기엔 가엾은 스파클러 씨도 있었다. 그는 두 사람 사이의 평화를 어떻게 지켜야 할지 몰라, 두 분 다 대단히 훌륭한 여성이며 어느 쪽에도 허튼 구석이라곤 없다는 데에 의견을 모으는 것보다 더 나은 길은 없으리라는 의견으로 겸손히 기울었으나, 그 점잖은 권유에 대해 두 사람은 한데 뭉쳐 그에게 무섭게 달려들었다."], ["Then, too, here was Mrs General, got home from foreign parts, sending a Prune and a Prism by post every other day, demanding a new Testimonial by way of recommendation to some vacant appointment or other.", "그리고 또한 거기엔 제너럴 부인이 있었다. 외국에서 돌아와, 하루걸러 자두와 프리즘을 우편으로 보내며, 이러저러한 빈자리의 일자리에 추천받겠다는 명목으로 새 추천장을 요구했다."], ["Of which remarkable gentlewoman it may be finally observed, that there surely never was a gentlewoman of whose transcendent fitness for any vacant appointment on the face of this earth, so many people were (as the warmth of her Testimonials evinced) so perfectly satisfied--or who was so very unfortunate in having a large circle of ardent and distinguished admirers, who never themselves happened to want her in any capacity.", "이 주목할 만한 귀부인에 관해 마지막으로 일러둘 만한 것은, 이 지상의 그 어떤 빈자리에든 그녀가 더없이 어울린다는 데 대해 그토록 많은 사람이(그녀의 추천장들의 그 열렬함이 증명하듯) 그토록 완벽하게 만족한 귀부인은 일찍이 없었으리라는 점이며, 또한 열정적이고 저명한 찬미자들을 큰 무리로 거느렸으되 그들 가운데 누구도 정작 그녀를 어떤 직책으로든 필요로 한 적이 없었으니, 그토록 운이 없는 귀부인 또한 일찍이 없었으리라는 점이다."]], [["On the first crash of the eminent Mr Merdle’s decease, many important persons had been unable to determine whether they should cut Mrs Merdle, or comfort her.", "저명한 머들 씨의 별세라는 첫 충격이 닥쳤을 때, 많은 중요 인사들은 머들 부인과 절교해야 할지 위로해야 할지 결정을 내리지 못했다."], ["As it seemed, however, essential to the strength of their own case that they should admit her to have been cruelly deceived, they graciously made the admission, and continued to know her.", "그러나 그녀가 잔인하게 속았다고 인정하는 것이 자기들 입장의 근거를 굳히는 데 긴요해 보였으므로, 그들은 너그러이 그 사실을 인정하고는 계속 그녀와 교제했다."], ["It followed that Mrs Merdle, as a woman of fashion and good breeding who had been sacrificed to the wiles of a vulgar barbarian (for Mr Merdle was found out from the crown of his head to the sole of his foot, the moment he was found out in his pocket), must be actively championed by her order for her order’s sake.", "그리하여 머들 부인은, 천박한 야만인의 간계에 희생된 사교계의 교양 있는 숙녀로서(머들 씨는 주머니 사정이 들통난 그 순간, 머리 꼭대기부터 발바닥까지 죄다 들통이 났던 것이다) 자기 계급을 위해 그 계급에 의해 적극적으로 옹호받아야 마땅한 처지가 되었다."], ["She returned this fealty by causing it to be understood that she was even more incensed against the felonious shade of the deceased than anybody else was; thus, on the whole, she came out of her furnace like a wise woman, and did exceedingly well.", "그녀는 이 충성에 보답하고자, 고인의 그 흉악한 망령에 대해 자신이 그 누구보다도 더 분개하고 있음을 사람들이 알아차리게끔 만들었다. 그리하여 결국 그녀는 현명한 여인답게 그 용광로에서 무사히 빠져나왔고, 더없이 잘 처신했다."]], [["Mr Sparkler’s lordship was fortunately one of those shelves on which a gentleman is considered to be put away for life, unless there should be reasons for hoisting him up with the Barnacle crane to a more lucrative height.", "스파클러 씨의 관직은 다행히도, 더 짭짤한 높이로 끌어올릴 만한 배너클 기중기를 쓸 이유가 생기지 않는 한 신사가 평생 처박혀 두어지는 그런 선반 중 하나였다."], ["That patriotic servant accordingly stuck to his colours (the Standard of four Quarterings), and was a perfect Nelson in respect of nailing them to the mast.", "그리하여 그 애국적인 공복은 자기 깃발(네 가지 문장을 박은 가문기)을 고수했고, 그 깃발을 돛대에 못 박아 두는 일에서는 완벽한 넬슨이었다."], ["On the profits of his intrepidity, Mrs Sparkler and Mrs Merdle, inhabiting different floors of the genteel little temple of inconvenience to which the smell of the day before yesterday’s soup and coach-horses was as constant as Death to man, arrayed themselves to fight it out in the lists of Society, sworn rivals.", "그 대담함의 수익으로, 스파클러 부인과 머들 부인은 — 그제 끓인 수프와 마차용 말의 냄새가 인간에게 죽음만큼이나 끈질기게 따라붙는, 그 점잖은 불편함의 자그마한 신전, 그 서로 다른 층에 살면서 — 사교계의 결투장에서 끝까지 겨루기 위해 단단히 차려입은, 맹세코 둘도 없는 맞수였다."], ["And Little Dorrit, seeing all these things as they developed themselves, could not but wonder, anxiously, into what back corner of the genteel establishment Fanny’s children would be poked by-and-by, and who would take care of those unborn little victims.", "그리고 리틀 도릿은 이 모든 일이 전개되는 것을 지켜보면서, 머지않아 패니의 아이들이 그 점잖은 살림의 어느 뒷구석으로 처박힐지, 그리고 아직 태어나지 않은 그 가엾은 희생자들을 누가 돌볼지 불안하게 의아해하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Arthur being far too ill to be spoken with on subjects of emotion or anxiety, and his recovery greatly depending on the repose into which his weakness could be hushed, Little Dorrit’s sole reliance during this heavy period was on Mr Meagles.", "아서는 감정이나 근심에 관한 화제를 입에 올리기에는 너무도 위중했고, 그의 회복은 그 쇠약함이 가라앉아 안식에 이르는 데 크게 달려 있었으므로, 이 힘겨운 시기에 리틀 도릿이 의지할 수 있는 유일한 상대는 미글스 씨였다."], ["He was still abroad; but she had written to him through his daughter, immediately after first seeing Arthur in the Marshalsea and since, confiding her uneasiness to him on the points on which she was most anxious, but especially on one.", "그는 여전히 외국에 있었으나, 그녀는 마셜시에서 아서를 처음 본 직후부터, 그리고 그 후로도 줄곧, 그의 딸을 통해 그에게 편지를 보내, 자신이 가장 불안해하는 여러 사항, 특히 한 가지 사항에 관한 불안을 그에게 털어놓았다."], ["To that one, the continued absence of Mr Meagles abroad, instead of his comforting presence in the Marshalsea, was referable.", "미글스 씨가 마셜시에서 위안이 되어 주는 대신 외국에 계속 머무르는 것은 바로 그 한 가지 사항에 기인한 것이었다."]], [["Without disclosing the precise nature of the documents that had fallen into Rigaud’s hands, Little Dorrit had confided the general outline of that story to Mr Meagles, to whom she had also recounted his fate.", "리고의 손에 들어간 그 문서들의 정확한 성격은 밝히지 않은 채, 리틀 도릿은 그 사연의 대략적인 윤곽을 미글스 씨에게 털어놓았고, 또한 그에게 그자의 최후도 들려주었다. 저울과 됫박을 다루던 옛 신중한 습관 덕분에 미글스 씨는 그 원본 서류들을 되찾는 일의 중요성을 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["The old cautious habits of the scales and scoop at once showed Mr Meagles the importance of recovering the original papers; wherefore he wrote back to Little Dorrit, strongly confirming her in the solicitude she expressed on that head, and adding that he would not come over to England ‘without making some attempt to trace them out.’", "그리하여 그는 리틀 도릿에게 답장을 보내, 그 점에 관해 그녀가 드러낸 우려를 강력히 뒷받침하면서, \"그것들의 행방을 찾으려는 어떤 시도라도 해보지 않고는\" 영국으로 건너오지 않겠다고 덧붙였다."]], [["By this time Mr Henry Gowan had made up his mind that it would be agreeable to him not to know the Meagleses.", "이 무렵 헨리 가우언 씨는 미글스 일가와 알고 지내지 않는 편이 자기에게 더 유쾌하리라고 마음을 정한 터였다."], ["He was so considerate as to lay no injunctions on his wife in that particular; but he mentioned to Mr Meagles that personally they did not appear to him to get on together, and that he thought it would be a good thing if--politely, and without any scene, or anything of that sort--they agreed that they were the best fellows in the world, but were best apart.", "그는 그 점에 관해 아내에게 아무런 명령도 내리지 않을 만큼은 사려 깊었으나, 미글스 씨에게는 자기들이 개인적으로 서로 잘 맞지 않는 듯하니, 정중하게, 아무런 소동도 그런 따위의 일도 없이, 서로 세상에 둘도 없는 좋은 사람들이지만 떨어져 지내는 편이 가장 낫다는 데 합의하면 좋은 일이리라고 말했다. 딸이 보는 앞에서 끊임없이 무시당함으로써 딸의 행복을 더하지 못하고 있음을 이미 절감하던 가엾은 미글스 씨는 이렇게 말했다."], ["Poor Mr Meagles, who was already sensible that he did not advance his daughter’s happiness by being constantly slighted in her presence, said ‘Good, Henry!", "\"좋네, 헨리! 자네는 내 펫의 남편이고, 자연의 이치대로 나를 밀어냈으니, 자네가 원한다면 좋네!\""], ["You are my Pet’s husband; you have displaced me, in the course of nature; if you wish it, good!’ This arrangement involved the contingent advantage, which perhaps Henry Gowan had not foreseen, that both Mr and Mrs Meagles were more liberal than before to their daughter, when their communication was only with her and her young child: and that his high spirit found itself better provided with money, without being under the degrading necessity of knowing whence it came.", "이 합의에는, 아마 헨리 가우언이 미처 내다보지 못한 부수적 이점이 딸려 있었다. 즉 미글스 부부는, 오직 딸과 그 어린 아이만 상대하게 되자 전보다 딸에게 더 후해졌고, 그의 그 도도한 기개는 그 돈이 어디서 나온 것인지 알아야 하는 굴욕적인 처지에 놓이지 않으면서도 더 넉넉히 돈을 갖추게 되었다는 점이었다."]], [["Mr Meagles, at such a period, naturally seized an occupation with great ardour.", "이런 시기에 미글스 씨는 자연스레 어떤 일거리를 큰 열의로 붙들었다."], ["He knew from his daughter the various towns which Rigaud had been haunting, and the various hotels at which he had been living for some time back.", "그는 딸을 통해, 리고가 출몰하던 여러 도시며, 얼마 전부터 그가 묵어 온 여러 호텔을 알고 있었다."], ["The occupation he set himself was to visit these with all discretion and speed, and, in the event of finding anywhere that he had left a bill unpaid, and a box or parcel behind, to pay such bill, and bring away such box or parcel.", "그가 스스로에게 부과한 일거리는, 온갖 신중함과 신속함으로 이곳들을 찾아가, 어디든 그가 계산하지 않은 청구서와 남겨 둔 상자나 꾸러미가 있을 경우, 그 청구서를 갚고 그 상자나 꾸러미를 가져오는 것이었다."]], [["With no other attendant than Mother, Mr Meagles went upon his pilgrimage, and encountered a number of adventures.", "어머니 외에 다른 수행원 없이, 미글스 씨는 순례에 나섰고 숱한 모험과 마주쳤다."], ["Not the least of his difficulties was, that he never knew what was said to him, and that he pursued his inquiries among people who never knew what he said to them.", "그가 겪은 곤란 가운데 적지 않은 것은, 그가 남이 자기에게 하는 말을 도무지 알아듣지 못한다는 것, 그리고 자기가 하는 말을 도무지 알아듣지 못하는 사람들 사이에서 탐문을 이어 갔다는 것이었다."], ["Still, with an unshaken confidence that the English tongue was somehow the mother tongue of the whole world, only the people were too stupid to know it, Mr Meagles harangued innkeepers in the most voluble manner, entered into loud explanations of the most complicated sort, and utterly renounced replies in the native language of the respondents, on the ground that they were ‘all bosh.’ Sometimes interpreters were called in; whom Mr Meagles addressed in such idiomatic terms of speech, as instantly to extinguish and shut up--which made the matter worse.", "그래도, 영어란 어쩐지 온 세상의 모국어이며 단지 사람들이 너무 어리석어 그것을 모를 뿐이라는 흔들림 없는 확신을 품은 채, 미글스 씨는 더없이 청산유수로 여관 주인들을 향해 일장 연설을 늘어놓고, 더없이 복잡한 종류의 설명을 큰 소리로 펼쳤으며, 응답자들의 모국어로 된 대답은 \"죄다 헛소리\"라는 이유로 깡그리 무시해 버렸다. 때로는 통역이 불려 왔는데, 미글스 씨는 그들에게 어찌나 관용구로 가득 찬 말투로 말을 거는지 그들은 즉각 입을 다물고 말문이 막혀 버렸으니 — 이는 사태를 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이었다."], ["On a balance of the account, however, it may be doubted whether he lost much; for, although he found no property, he found so many debts and various associations of discredit with the proper name, which was the only word he made intelligible, that he was almost everywhere overwhelmed with injurious accusations.", "그러나 손익을 따져 보면, 그가 크게 손해를 보았는지는 의문이다. 왜냐하면 그는 아무런 재산도 찾아내지 못한 대신, 그가 알아들을 수 있게 만든 유일한 단어인 그 고유명사와 결부된 숱한 빚과 갖가지 불명예의 연루를 찾아냈으므로, 그는 거의 어디서나 모욕적인 비난에 파묻혔던 것이다."], ["On no fewer than four occasions the police were called in to receive denunciations of Mr Meagles as a Knight of Industry, a good-for-nothing, and a thief, all of which opprobrious language he bore with the best temper (having no idea what it meant), and was in the most ignominious manner escorted to steam-boats and public carriages, to be got rid of, talking all the while, like a cheerful and fluent Briton as he was, with Mother under his arm.", "무려 네 차례나 경찰이 불려 와 미글스 씨를 사기꾼 기사이자, 쓸모없는 자이며, 도둑이라 고발하는 소리를 접수했는데, 그는 그 모든 욕설을 더없이 좋은 기분으로 견뎌 냈고(그게 무슨 뜻인지 도통 알지 못했으므로), 가장 치욕적인 방식으로 증기선과 합승마차까지 호송되어 쫓겨났으니, 그러는 내내, 어머니를 팔짱에 낀 채, 명랑하고 거침없는 영국인답게 끊임없이 떠들어 댔다."]], [["But, in his own tongue, and in his own head, Mr Meagles was a clear, shrewd, persevering man.", "그러나 자기 모국어로, 그리고 자기 머릿속에서, 미글스 씨는 명료하고 영리하며 끈질긴 사람이었다."], ["When he had ‘worked round,’ as he called it, to Paris in his pilgrimage, and had wholly failed in it so far, he was not disheartened.", "그가 그 순례 도중, 자기 표현대로 \"빙 돌아\" 파리에 이르렀을 때, 그리고 거기까지 완전히 실패한 채였을 때도, 그는 낙심하지 않았다."], ["‘The nearer to England I follow him, you see, Mother,’ argued Mr Meagles, ‘the nearer I am likely to come to the papers, whether they turn up or no.", "\"내가 그자를 따라 영국에 가까워질수록, 알겠소, 어머니,\" 미글스 씨가 논했다."], ["Because it is only reasonable to conclude that he would deposit them somewhere where they would be safe from people over in England, and where they would yet be accessible to himself, don’t you see?’", "\"그 서류들이 나오든 안 나오든, 나는 그만큼 그 서류에 더 가까워질 공산이 크다는 거요. 왜냐하면 그자가 그것들을, 영국 쪽 사람들 손에는 안전하면서도 자기 자신은 손쉽게 닿을 수 있는 어딘가에 맡겨 두었으리라고 결론짓는 게 이치에 맞으니까, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["At Paris Mr Meagles found a letter from Little Dorrit, lying waiting for him; in which she mentioned that she had been able to talk for a minute or two with Mr Clennam about this man who was no more; and that when she told Mr Clennam that his friend Mr Meagles, who was on his way to see him, had an interest in ascertaining something about the man if he could, he had asked her to tell Mr Meagles that he had been known to Miss Wade, then living in such a street at Calais. ‘Oho!’ said Mr Meagles.", "파리에서 미글스 씨는 리틀 도릿이 보낸 편지가 자기를 기다리며 놓여 있는 것을 발견했다. 거기서 그녀는, 이제는 세상에 없는 그 사내에 관하여 클레넘 씨와 잠깐 일이 분 정도 이야기를 나눌 수 있었다고 했고, 그를 만나러 오는 길인 친구 미글스 씨가 가능하다면 그 사내에 관해 무언가 알아내는 데 관심이 있다고 클레넘 씨에게 전하자, 그가 미글스 씨에게 이렇게 전해 달라더라고 했다. 즉 그자가 와이드 양에게 알려져 있었으며, 그녀는 당시 칼레의 어느 거리에 살고 있다는 것이었다. \"오호!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."]], [["As soon afterwards as might be in those Diligence days, Mr Meagles rang the cracked bell at the cracked gate, and it jarred open, and the peasant-woman stood in the dark doorway, saying, ‘Ice-say! Seer! Who?’ In acknowledgment of whose address, Mr Meagles murmured to himself that there was some sense about these Calais people, who really did know something of what you and themselves were up to; and returned, ‘Miss Wade, my dear.’ He was then shown into the presence of Miss Wade.", "그 역마차 시절에 가능한 한 빠른 시일 안에, 미글스 씨는 그 금이 간 대문의 금이 간 종을 울렸고, 문은 삐걱대며 열렸으며, 그 시골 아낙이 어두운 문간에 서서 말했다. \"글-쎄-요! 선생님! 누구를?\" 그 말에 응답하여, 미글스 씨는 혼잣말로, 이 칼레 사람들에게는 그래도 분별이라는 게 좀 있어서 당신과 자기들이 무슨 볼일로 만나는지를 정말로 어느 정도는 알아듣는다고 중얼거리고는, 이렇게 대답했다. \"와이드 양 말이오, 아주머니.\" 그리하여 그는 와이드 양 앞으로 안내되었다."]], [["‘It’s some time since we met,’ said Mr Meagles, clearing his throat; ‘I hope you have been pretty well, Miss Wade?’", "\"우리가 만난 지도 꽤 됐구려.\" 미글스 씨가 목청을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"그동안 잘 지내셨기를 바라오, 와이드 양?\""]], [["Without hoping that he or anybody else had been pretty well, Miss Wade asked him to what she was indebted for the honour of seeing him again?", "그나 다른 누군가가 잘 지냈기를 바라지도 않으면서, 와이드 양은 그를 다시 보는 영광을 자기가 무엇 덕분에 누리게 되었느냐고 물었다."], ["Mr Meagles, in the meanwhile, glanced all round the room without observing anything in the shape of a box.", "그러는 동안 미글스 씨는 방 안을 죽 둘러보았으나 상자 비슷한 것은 아무것도 눈에 띄지 않았다."]], [["‘Why, the truth is, Miss Wade,’ said Mr Meagles, in a comfortable, managing, not to say coaxing voice, ‘it is possible that you may be able to throw a light upon a little something that is at present dark.", "\"실은 말이오, 와이드 양,\" 미글스 씨가 편안하게, 능숙하게, 구슬리는 듯하다고까지는 못 해도, 그런 목소리로 말했다."], ["Any unpleasant bygones between us are bygones, I hope.", "\"지금으로선 어두운 작은 일 하나에 당신이 어쩌면 빛을 비춰 줄 수 있을지도 모르겠소. 우리 사이의 어떤 불쾌한 지난 일들은 다 지난 일이오, 바라건대."], ["Can’t be helped now.", "이제 와 어쩔 수 없는 일이지."], ["You recollect my daughter?", "내 딸을 기억하시오? 세월이 이렇게도 변하다니!"], ["Time changes so! A mother!’", "한 아이의 어머니라오!\""]], [["In his innocence, Mr Meagles could not have struck a worse key-note.", "그 순진함 속에서, 미글스 씨는 이보다 더 못한 첫음을 짚을 수가 없었을 것이다."], ["He paused for any expression of interest, but paused in vain.", "그는 어떤 관심의 표현이라도 나오기를 기다리며 잠시 멈췄으나, 헛되이 멈춘 것이었다."]], [["‘That is not the subject you wished to enter on?’ she said, after a cold silence.", "\"그건 당신이 꺼내고자 하던 화제가 아니지요?\" 차가운 침묵 끝에 그녀가 말했다."]], [["‘No, no,’ returned Mr Meagles.", "\"아니오, 아니오.\" 미글스 씨가 대답했다."], ["‘No.", "\"아니오."], ["I thought your good nature might--’", "나는 당신의 그 선한 천성이라면 혹시—\""]], [["‘I thought you knew,’ she interrupted, with a smile, ‘that my good nature is not to be calculated upon?’", "\"내 선한 천성이란 건 기대할 만한 게 못 된다는 걸 당신은 알고 있는 줄 알았는데요?\" 그녀가 미소를 띠며 말을 가로챘다."]], [["‘Don’t say so,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘you do yourself an injustice.", "\"그런 말 마시오.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["However, to come to the point.’ For he was sensible of having gained nothing by approaching it in a roundabout way.", "\"스스로를 부당하게 깎아내리는 거요. 어쨌든, 본론으로 들어갑시다.\""], ["‘I have heard from my friend Clennam, who, you will be sorry to hear, has been and still is very ill--’", "빙 돌아 다가가서는 아무것도 얻지 못했음을 그는 깨달았던 것이다. \"내 친구 클레넘에게서 들었는데, 그가, 당신이 들으면 안타까워하겠지만, 아주 심하게 앓았고 지금도 앓고 있소—\""]], [["He paused again, and again she was silent.", "그는 다시 말을 멈췄고, 그녀는 다시 잠자코 있었다."]], [["‘--that you had some knowledge of one Blandois, lately killed in London by a violent accident.", "\"—당신이 블랑두아라는 자를 어느 정도 알고 있었다더군. 얼마 전 런던에서 격렬한 사고로 죽은 그자 말이오."], ["Now, don’t mistake me!", "자, 오해는 마시오!"], ["I know it was a slight knowledge,’ said Mr Meagles, dexterously forestalling an angry interruption which he saw about to break.", "그게 가벼운 안면이었다는 건 나도 아오.\" 미글스 씨가, 막 터지려는 성난 끼어듦을 능숙하게 미리 막으며 말했다."], ["‘I am fully aware of that.", "\"그 점은 충분히 알고 있소. 가벼운 안면이었다는 것, 나도 아오."], ["It was a slight knowledge, I know.", "한데 문제는 말이오,\" 여기서 미글스 씨의 목소리가 다시 편안해졌다."], ["But the question is,’ Mr Meagles’s voice here became comfortable again, ‘did he, on his way to England last time, leave a box of papers, or a bundle of papers, or some papers or other in some receptacle or other--any papers--with you: begging you to allow him to leave them here for a short time, until he wanted them?’", "\"그자가 지난번 영국으로 가는 길에, 서류 상자나, 서류 꾸러미나, 무슨 그릇에 담긴 무슨 서류 따위를—어떤 서류든—당신에게 맡기지 않았소? 잠시 동안, 그것들이 필요해질 때까지만 여기 두게 해 달라고 청하면서 말이오.\""]], [["‘The question is?’ she repeated.", "\"문제라뇨?\" 그녀가 되풀이했다."], ["‘Whose question is?’", "\"누구의 문제죠?\""]], [["‘Mine,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"내 문제요.\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘And not only mine but Clennam’s question, and other people’s question.", "\"그리고 내 문제일 뿐 아니라 클레넘의 문제이고, 다른 사람들의 문제이기도 하오. 자, 나는 확신하오,\""], ["Now, I am sure,’ continued Mr Meagles, whose heart was overflowing with Pet, ‘that you can’t have any unkind feeling towards my daughter; it’s impossible.", "가슴이 펫에 대한 마음으로 넘쳐흐르던 미글스 씨가 말을 이었다. \"당신이 내 딸에게 어떤 모진 감정을 품고 있을 리는 없다고 말이오."], ["Well!", "그건 있을 수 없는 일이지. 자!"], ["It’s her question, too; being one in which a particular friend of hers is nearly interested.", "이건 그 애의 문제이기도 하오. 그 애의 어느 각별한 친구가 깊이 얽혀 있는 문제이니까."], ["So here I am, frankly to say that is the question, and to ask, Now, did he?’", "그러니 이렇게 와서, 솔직히 그게 문제라고 말하고, 묻는 것이오. 자, 그자가 그랬소?\""]], [["‘Upon my word,’ she returned, ‘I seem to be a mark for everybody who knew anything of a man I once in my life hired, and paid, and dismissed, to aim their questions at!’", "\"맹세코,\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"나는 내 평생 단 한 번 고용해서 삯을 주고 내보낸 사내에 관해 조금이라도 아는 사람이라면 누구나 와서 질문을 겨누는 과녁이 된 것 같군요!\""]], [["‘Now, don’t,’ remonstrated Mr Meagles, ‘don’t!", "\"자, 그러지 마시오,\" 미글스 씨가 타일렀다."], ["Don’t take offence, because it’s the plainest question in the world, and might be asked of any one.", "\"그러지 마시오! 기분 상해하지 마시오."], ["The documents I refer to were not his own, were wrongfully obtained, might at some time or other be troublesome to an innocent person to have in keeping, and are sought by the people to whom they really belong.", "이건 세상에서 가장 간단한 질문이고, 누구에게나 물을 법한 것이니까. 내가 말하는 그 문서들은 그자의 것이 아니었고, 부당하게 손에 넣은 것이며, 언젠가는 죄 없는 사람이 그걸 가지고 있으면 곤란해질 수도 있는 것이고, 그게 정말로 속한 사람들이 찾고 있는 것이오."], ["He passed through Calais going to London, and there were reasons why he should not take them with him then, why he should wish to be able to put his hand upon them readily, and why he should distrust leaving them with people of his own sort.", "그자는 런던으로 가는 길에 칼레를 거쳐 갔고, 그때 그것들을 가지고 가서는 안 될 이유들이, 손쉽게 그걸 다시 손에 넣을 수 있기를 바랄 이유들이, 자기와 같은 부류의 사람들에게 맡기는 걸 못 미더워할 이유들이 있었소. 그자가 그것들을 여기 두고 갔소?"], ["Did he leave them here? I declare if I knew how to avoid giving you offence, I would take any pains to do it.", "분명히 말하건대, 당신 기분을 상하게 하지 않을 방법을 안다면, 어떤 수고라도 마다하지 않고 그렇게 하겠소."], ["I put the question personally, but there’s nothing personal in it.", "내가 사사로이 묻기는 하지만, 여기엔 사사로운 게 하나도 없소."], ["I might put it to any one; I have put it already to many people.", "누구에게라도 물을 법한 것이고, 이미 많은 사람에게 물어 본 것이오."], ["Did he leave them here?", "그자가 그것들을 여기 두고 갔소?"], ["Did he leave anything here?’", "그자가 여기에 뭐라도 두고 갔소?\" \"아니요.\""]], [["‘No.’\n‘Then unfortunately, Miss Wade, you know nothing about them?’", "\"그럼 유감스럽게도, 와이드 양, 당신은 그것들에 관해 아무것도 모른다는 거요?\""]], [["‘I know nothing about them.", "\"그것들에 관해 나는 아무것도 몰라요."], ["I have now answered your unaccountable question.", "이제 당신의 그 영문 모를 질문에 답했어요."], ["He did not leave them here, and I know nothing about them.’", "그자는 그것들을 여기 두고 가지 않았고, 나는 그것들에 관해 아무것도 몰라요.\""]], [["‘There!’ said Mr Meagles rising. ‘I am sorry for it; that’s over; and I hope there is not much harm done.--Tattycoram well, Miss Wade?’", "\"그렇군!\" 미글스 씨가 일어서며 말했다. \"그것 참 안타깝소. 그럼 이걸로 끝이오. 그리고 너무 큰 피해는 없었기를 바라오.—태티코럼은 잘 있소, 와이드 양?\""]], [["‘Harriet well?", "\"해리엇이 잘 있느냐고요?"], ["O yes!’", "오, 그럼요!\""]], [["‘I have put my foot in it again,’ said Mr Meagles, thus corrected.", "\"내가 또 실수를 했군.\" 그렇게 정정당하자 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘I can’t keep my foot out of it here, it seems.", "\"여기선 도무지 실수를 면할 수가 없는 모양이오."], ["Perhaps, if I had thought twice about it, I might never have given her the jingling name.", "어쩌면 두 번 생각했더라면, 나는 그 애한테 그런 딸랑이는 이름은 결코 붙이지 않았을지도 모르오."], ["But, when one means to be good-natured and sportive with young people, one doesn’t think twice.", "한데 젊은 사람들에게 다정하고 장난스럽게 굴려고 마음먹으면, 두 번 생각하지를 않게 되거든."], ["Her old friend leaves a kind word for her, Miss Wade, if you should think proper to deliver it.’", "그 옛 친구가 그 애에게 다정한 말 한마디를 남기오, 와이드 양, 혹시 전해 주는 게 좋겠다 싶으면 말이오.\""]], [["She said nothing as to that; and Mr Meagles, taking his honest face out of the dull room, where it shone like a sun, took it to the Hotel where he had left Mrs Meagles, and where he made the Report: ‘Beaten, Mother; no effects!’ He took it next to the London Steam Packet, which sailed in the night; and next to the Marshalsea.", "그녀는 그에 관해 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그리하여 미글스 씨는, 태양처럼 빛나던 그 정직한 얼굴을 그 음울한 방에서 거두어 내, 미글스 부인을 남겨 둔 호텔로 가져갔고, 거기서 보고를 올렸다. \"패배요, 어머니. 빈손이오!\" 그는 그 얼굴을, 밤에 출항하는 런던행 증기 우편선으로 가져갔고, 그다음에는 마셜시로 가져갔다."]], [["The faithful John was on duty when Father and Mother Meagles presented themselves at the wicket towards nightfall.", "해 질 녘 미글스 부부가 쪽문에 나타났을 때, 충직한 존이 당직을 서고 있었다."], ["Miss Dorrit was not there then, he said; but she had been there in the morning, and invariably came in the evening.", "도릿 양은 지금은 여기 없다고 그가 말했다. 하지만 아침에 왔었고, 저녁에는 어김없이 온다고 했다."], ["Mr Clennam was slowly mending; and Maggy and Mrs Plornish and Mr Baptist took care of him by turns.", "클레넘 씨는 더디게 차도를 보이고 있으며, 매기와 플로니시 부인과 뱁티스트 씨가 번갈아 그를 돌본다고 했다."], ["Miss Dorrit was sure to come back that evening before the bell rang.", "도릿 양은 그날 저녁 종이 울리기 전에 틀림없이 돌아올 것이었다."], ["There was the room the Marshal had lent her, up-stairs, in which they could wait for her, if they pleased.", "위층에는 간수장이 그녀에게 빌려준 방이 있으니, 원하시면 거기서 그녀를 기다릴 수 있다고 했다."], ["Mistrustful that it might be hazardous to Arthur to see him without preparation, Mr Meagles accepted the offer; and they were left shut up in the room, looking down through its barred window into the jail.", "준비 없이 자기를 보는 것이 아서에게 해로울지도 모른다고 못 미더워하며, 미글스 씨는 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그리하여 그들은 그 방에 들어가 갇힌 채, 창살 박힌 창문 너머로 감옥을 내려다보게 되었다."]], [["The cramped area of the prison had such an effect on Mrs Meagles that she began to weep, and such an effect on Mr Meagles that he began to gasp for air.", "비좁은 감옥 마당은 미글스 부인에게 어찌나 영향을 미쳤던지 그녀는 울기 시작했고, 미글스 씨에게는 어찌나 영향을 미쳤던지 그는 숨을 헐떡이기 시작했다."], ["He was walking up and down the room, panting, and making himself worse by laboriously fanning himself with her handkerchief, when he turned towards the opening door.", "그가 헐떡이며 방 안을 오르락내리락하면서, 아내의 손수건으로 애써 부채질을 하는 통에 도리어 사태를 더 악화시키고 있을 때, 그는 열리는 문 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["‘Eh?", "\"어?"], ["Good gracious!’ said Mr Meagles, ‘this is not Miss Dorrit!", "이런 세상에!\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Why, Mother, look!", "\"이건 도릿 양이 아니잖아! 아니, 어머니, 보시오!"], ["Tattycoram!’", "태티코럼이오!\""]], [["No other.", "다름 아닌 바로 그녀였다. 그리고 태티코럼의 팔에는 사방 두 자쯤 되는 쇠 상자가 안겨 있었다."], ["And in Tattycoram’s arms was an iron box some two feet square.", "그런 상자를, 애프리 플린트윈치는 자기 첫 꿈속에서 한밤중에 더블의 팔에 끼인 채 그 낡은 집을 빠져나가는 것을 본 적이 있었다. 이것을, 태티코럼은 옛 주인의 발치 땅바닥에 내려놓았다."], ["Such a box had Affery Flintwinch seen, in the first of her dreams, going out of the old house in the dead of the night under Double’s arm.", "이것 곁에, 태티코럼은 무릎을 꿇고 그 위를 두 손으로 두드리며, 반은 환희에 반은 절망에, 반은 웃음에 반은 눈물에 젖어 외쳤다. \"용서해 주세요, 친애하는 주인님."], ["This, Tattycoram put on the ground at her old master’s feet: this, Tattycoram fell on her knees by, and beat her hands upon, crying half in exultation and half in despair, half in laughter and half in tears, ‘Pardon, dear Master; take me back, dear Mistress; here it is!’", "저를 다시 받아 주세요, 친애하는 마님. 여기 있어요!\""]], [["‘Tatty!’ exclaimed Mr Meagles.", "\"태티!\" 미글스 씨가 외쳤다."]], [["‘What you wanted!’ said Tattycoram.", "\"이게 바로 어르신이 찾으시던 거예요!\""], ["‘Here it is!", "태티코럼이 말했다."], ["I was put in the next room not to see you.", "\"여기 있어요! 어르신을 보지 못하도록 저는 옆방에 들어가 있었어요."], ["I heard you ask her about it, I heard her say she hadn’t got it, I was there when he left it, and I took it at bedtime and brought it away.", "어르신이 그분에게 그걸 묻는 것도 들었고, 그분이 그걸 갖고 있지 않다고 말하는 것도 들었어요. 그가 그걸 두고 갈 때 저는 거기 있었고, 잠자리에 들 시간에 제가 그걸 챙겨 가지고 빠져나왔어요."], ["Here it is!’", "여기 있어요!\""]], [["‘Why, my girl,’ cried Mr Meagles, more breathless than before, ‘how did you come over?’", "\"아니, 얘야,\" 미글스 씨가 전보다 더 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다. \"어떻게 건너왔니?\""]], [["‘I came in the boat with you.", "\"어르신과 같은 배를 타고 왔어요."], ["I was sitting wrapped up at the other end.", "저는 반대편 끝에 몸을 싸매고 앉아 있었어요."], ["When you took a coach at the wharf, I took another coach and followed you here.", "어르신이 부두에서 마차를 잡으셨을 때, 저는 다른 마차를 잡아 어르신을 따라 여기까지 왔어요."], ["She never would have given it up after what you had said to her about its being wanted; she would sooner have sunk it in the sea, or burnt it.", "어르신이 그게 필요하다고 그분에게 말씀하신 뒤로, 그분은 결코 그걸 내놓지 않았을 거예요. 차라리 바다에 가라앉히거나 불태워 버렸을 거예요."], ["But, here it is!’", "하지만, 여기 있어요!\""]], [["The glow and rapture that the girl was in, with her ‘Here it is!’", "그 \"여기 있어요!\"를 외치며 소녀가 빠져 있던 그 환희와 황홀이라니."]], [["‘She never wanted it to be left, I must say that for her; but he left it, and I knew well that after what you said, and after her denying it, she never would have given it up.", "\"그분은 그게 맡겨지는 걸 결코 원치 않았어요."], ["But here it is!", "그건 그분을 위해 꼭 말해 둬야겠어요."], ["Dear Master, dear Mistress, take me back again, and give me back the dear old name!", "하지만 그가 그걸 두고 갔고, 어르신이 그렇게 말씀하신 뒤로, 또 그분이 그걸 부인한 뒤로, 그분이 결코 그걸 내놓지 않으리라는 걸 저는 잘 알고 있었어요. 하지만 여기 있어요!"], ["Let this intercede for me.", "친애하는 주인님, 친애하는 마님, 저를 다시 받아 주세요. 그리고 그 정다운 옛 이름을 제게 돌려주세요!"], ["Here it is!’", "이게 저를 위해 변론해 주게 해 주세요. 여기 있어요!\""]], [["Father and Mother Meagles never deserved their names better than when they took the headstrong foundling-girl into their protection again.", "미글스 부부가 그 고집 센 고아 소녀를 다시 자기들의 보호 아래 거두어들였을 때만큼, 그들이 자기들 이름에 합당했던 적은 일찍이 없었다."]], [["‘Oh!", "\"오!"], ["I have been so wretched,’ cried Tattycoram, weeping much more, ‘always so unhappy, and so repentant!", "저는 너무나 비참했어요,\" 태티코럼이 한결 더 많이 울며 외쳤다."], ["I was afraid of her from the first time I saw her.", "\"늘 그렇게 불행했고, 그렇게 후회스러웠어요! 저는 그분을 처음 본 순간부터 두려웠어요."], ["I knew she had got a power over me through understanding what was bad in me so well.", "그분은 제 안의 나쁜 것을 너무도 잘 꿰뚫어 봄으로써 저를 휘어잡는 힘을 가졌다는 걸 알았어요."], ["It was a madness in me, and she could raise it whenever she liked.", "그건 제 안의 광기였고, 그분은 마음만 먹으면 언제든 그걸 불러일으킬 수 있었어요."], ["I used to think, when I got into that state, that people were all against me because of my first beginning; and the kinder they were to me, the worse fault I found in them.", "그런 상태에 빠지면 저는, 제 출신이 그래서 사람들이 다 저를 거스른다고 여기곤 했고, 그들이 제게 친절할수록 저는 그들에게서 더 못된 점을 찾아냈어요."], ["I made it out that they triumphed above me, and that they wanted to make me envy them, when I know--when I even knew then--that they never thought of such a thing.", "저는 그들이 저를 짓밟고 우쭐댄다고, 그들이 저로 하여금 자기들을 부러워하게 만들려 한다고 멋대로 단정했는데, 사실은—그때조차도 사실 저는 알고 있었는데—그들은 그런 건 꿈에도 생각지 않았다는 걸요."], ["And my beautiful young mistress not so happy as she ought to have been, and I gone away from her!", "게다가 제 아름다운 젊은 마님은 마땅히 누려야 할 만큼 행복하지도 못한데, 저마저 곁을 떠나 버렸으니!"], ["Such a brute and a wretch as she must think me!", "그분이 저를 얼마나 짐승 같고 형편없는 것으로 여기실까요!"], ["But you’ll say a word to her for me, and ask her to be as forgiving as you two are?", "하지만 어르신이 그분에게 저를 위해 한마디 해 주시고, 두 분처럼 너그러이 용서해 달라고 청해 주시겠지요? 저는 예전만큼 나쁘진 않아요,\""], ["For I am not so bad as I was,’ pleaded Tattycoram; ‘I am bad enough, but not so bad as I was, indeed.", "태티코럼이 애원했다. \"저는 충분히 나쁘긴 하지만, 정말이지 예전만큼 나쁘진 않아요."], ["I have had Miss Wade before me all this time, as if it was my own self grown ripe--turning everything the wrong way, and twisting all good into evil.", "줄곧 제 눈앞에 와이드 양을 두고 봐 왔어요. 마치 무르익은 제 자신을 보는 것처럼요—모든 걸 잘못된 쪽으로 틀고, 온갖 선한 것을 악으로 비틀어 버리는 사람을요."], ["I have had her before me all this time, finding no pleasure in anything but keeping me as miserable, suspicious, and tormenting as herself.", "줄곧 제 눈앞에 그분을 두고 봐 왔어요. 저를 자기처럼 비참하고 의심 많고 괴로운 상태로 붙들어 두는 것 말고는 아무 데서도 즐거움을 찾지 못하는 사람을요."], ["Not that she had much to do, to do that,’ cried Tattycoram, in a closing great burst of distress, ‘for I was as bad as bad could be.", "하기야 그렇게 만드는 데 그분이 애쓸 것도 별로 없었지만요,\" 태티코럼이 마지막으로 크나큰 비탄을 터뜨리며 외쳤다."], ["I only mean to say, that, after what I have gone through, I hope I shall never be quite so bad again, and that I shall get better by very slow degrees.", "\"왜냐하면 저는 더없이 나쁠 대로 나빴으니까요. 제가 그저 하고 싶은 말은, 제가 겪어 온 일들을 겪고 나니, 다시는 결코 그렇게까지 나빠지지 않기를 바라며, 아주 더디나마 차츰 나아지리라는 거예요."], ["I’ll try very hard.", "정말 열심히 노력할게요."], ["I won’t stop at five-and-twenty, sir, I’ll count five-and-twenty hundred, five-and-twenty thousand!’", "스물다섯에서 멈추지 않겠어요, 어르신. 이천오백까지, 이만 오천까지 세겠어요!\""]], [["Another opening of the door, and Tattycoram subsided, and Little Dorrit came in, and Mr Meagles with pride and joy produced the box, and her gentle face was lighted up with grateful happiness and joy.", "또다시 문이 열렸고, 태티코럼은 잠잠해졌으며, 리틀 도릿이 들어왔고, 미글스 씨는 자랑스럽고 기쁜 마음으로 그 상자를 꺼내 보였으니, 그녀의 부드러운 얼굴이 감사의 행복과 기쁨으로 환히 밝아졌다. 이제 비밀은 안전했다!"], ["The secret was safe now!", "그녀는 그 비밀에서 자기 몫만은 그에게 감출 수 있었다."], ["She could keep her own part of it from him; he should never know of her loss; in time to come he should know all that was of import to himself; but he should never know what concerned her only.", "그는 결코 그녀의 손실을 알지 못할 것이었다. 때가 되면 그는 자기 자신에게 중요한 모든 것은 알게 되겠지만, 오직 그녀에게만 관계된 것은 결코 알지 못할 것이었다."], ["That was all passed, all forgiven, all forgotten.", "그것은 모두 지나갔고, 모두 용서되었으며, 모두 잊혔다."]], [["‘Now, my dear Miss Dorrit,’ said Mr Meagles; ‘I am a man of business--or at least was--and I am going to take my measures promptly, in that character.", "\"자, 도릿 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Had I better see Arthur to-night?’", "\"나는 사업가요—적어도 한때는 그랬소—그러니 그 자격으로, 신속하게 조치를 취할 작정이오. 오늘 밤 아서를 보는 게 좋겠소?\""]], [["‘I think not to-night.", "\"오늘 밤은 안 될 것 같아요."], ["I will go to his room and ascertain how he is.", "제가 그분 방에 가서 어떤 상태인지 확인해 볼게요."], ["But I think it will be better not to see him to-night.’", "하지만 오늘 밤은 안 보시는 편이 좋을 것 같아요.\""]], [["‘I am much of your opinion, my dear,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘and therefore I have not been any nearer to him than this dismal room.", "\"나도 자네 의견에 크게 동의하오, 도릿 양,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["Then I shall probably not see him for some little time to come.", "\"그래서 이 음울한 방보다 그에게 더 가까이 가지 않은 거요. 그렇다면 아마 앞으로 얼마간은 그를 보지 못하겠지."], ["But I’ll explain what I mean when you come back.’", "하지만 자네가 돌아오면 내가 뜻하는 바를 설명하겠소.\""]], [["She left the room.", "그녀는 방을 나갔다."], ["Mr Meagles, looking through the bars of the window, saw her pass out of the Lodge below him into the prison-yard.", "미글스 씨는 창살 너머로 내다보다가, 그녀가 아래쪽 수위실에서 나와 감옥 마당으로 들어서는 것을 보았다."], ["He said gently, ‘Tattycoram, come to me a moment, my good girl.’", "그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"태티코럼, 잠깐 이리 와 보렴, 착한 아이야.\""]], [["She went up to the window.", "그녀가 창가로 다가갔다."]], [["‘You see that young lady who was here just now--that little, quiet, fragile figure passing along there, Tatty?", "\"방금 여기 있었던 저 젊은 아가씨가 보이지—저기 지나가는, 저 작고 조용하고 가냘픈 모습 말이다, 태티?"], ["Look.", "보렴."], ["The people stand out of the way to let her go by.", "사람들이 길을 비켜 그녀를 지나가게 해 주는구나."], ["The men--see the poor, shabby fellows--pull off their hats to her quite politely, and now she glides in at that doorway.", "저 사내들이—저 가엾고 추레한 친구들을 보렴—그녀에게 자못 정중하게 모자를 벗는구나. 그리고 이제 그녀가 저 문간으로 스르르 들어가는구나."], ["See her, Tattycoram?’", "보이느냐, 태티코럼?\""]], [["‘Yes, sir.’", "\"네, 어르신.\""]], [["‘I have heard tell, Tatty, that she was once regularly called the child of this place.", "\"내가 들은 바로는, 태티, 그녀는 한때 이 장소의 자식이라고 정식으로 불렸다는구나."], ["She was born here, and lived here many years.", "여기서 태어났고, 여기서 여러 해를 살았지."], ["I can’t breathe here.", "나는 여기선 숨을 쉴 수가 없구나."], ["A doleful place to be born and bred in, Tattycoram?’", "태어나고 자라기에 참 처량한 곳이로구나, 태티코럼?\""]], [["‘Yes indeed, sir!’", "\"정말 그래요, 어르신!\""]], [["‘If she had constantly thought of herself, and settled with herself that everybody visited this place upon her, turned it against her, and cast it at her, she would have led an irritable and probably an useless existence.", "\"만일 그녀가 끊임없이 자기 자신만 생각하고, 모두가 이 장소를 두고 자기를 탓하며, 그걸 자기에게 불리하게 돌리고, 자기에게 들이댄다고 마음속으로 결론지었더라면, 그녀는 늘 짜증나고 아마 쓸모없는 삶을 살았을 게다."], ["Yet I have heard tell, Tattycoram, that her young life has been one of active resignation, goodness, and noble service.", "그러나 내가 들은 바로는, 태티코럼, 그녀의 젊은 삶은 능동적인 체념과 선함과 고귀한 헌신의 삶이었다는구나."], ["Shall I tell you what I consider those eyes of hers, that were here just now, to have always looked at, to get that expression?’", "방금 여기 있었던 그녀의 그 두 눈이 무엇을 늘 바라봄으로써 그런 표정을 얻게 되었다고 내가 생각하는지, 말해 줄까?\""]], [["‘Yes, if you please, sir.’", "\"네, 괜찮으시다면요, 어르신.\""]], [["‘Duty, Tattycoram.", "\"의무란다, 태티코럼."], ["Begin it early, and do it well; and there is no antecedent to it, in any origin or station, that will tell against us with the Almighty, or with ourselves.’", "일찍 시작하고, 잘 해 나가거라. 그러면 그 어떤 출신이나 신분에도, 전능하신 분 앞에서나 우리 자신 앞에서나 우리에게 불리하게 작용할 그 무엇도 그 의무에 앞서지 못한단다.\""]], [["They remained at the window, Mother joining them and pitying the prisoners, until she was seen coming back.", "그들은 창가에 머물렀고, 어머니도 그들 곁에 와서 죄수들을 가엾어했으니, 마침내 그녀가 돌아오는 것이 보일 때까지 그러했다."], ["She was soon in the room, and recommended that Arthur, whom she had left calm and composed, should not be visited that night.", "그녀는 이내 방에 들어와, 차분하고 안정된 상태로 두고 온 아서를 그날 밤에는 찾아가지 않는 것이 좋겠다고 권했다."]], [["‘Good!’ said Mr Meagles, cheerily.", "\"좋소!\" 미글스 씨가 쾌활하게 말했다."], ["‘I have not a doubt that’s best.", "\"그게 가장 좋다는 걸 나는 조금도 의심치 않소."], ["I shall trust my remembrances then, my sweet nurse, in your hands, and I well know they couldn’t be in better.", "그렇다면 내 추억거리들을, 다정한 간호사 양반, 자네 손에 맡기겠소. 그것들이 그보다 더 나은 손에 맡겨질 수 없다는 걸 나는 잘 아오."], ["I am off again to-morrow morning.’", "나는 내일 아침 또 떠나오.\""]], [["Little Dorrit, surprised, asked him where?", "리틀 도릿이 놀라서, 어디로 가시느냐고 그에게 물었다."]], [["‘My dear,’ said Mr Meagles, ‘I can’t live without breathing.", "\"이보게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["This place has taken my breath away, and I shall never get it back again until Arthur is out of this place.’", "\"나는 숨을 쉬지 않고는 살 수 없소. 이곳이 내 숨을 앗아가 버렸고, 아서가 이곳에서 나오기 전까지는 나는 결코 그걸 되찾지 못할 거요.\""]], [["‘How is that a reason for going off again to-morrow morning?’", "\"그게 어째서 내일 아침 또 떠나실 이유가 되나요?\""]], [["‘You shall understand,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"설명해 주겠소,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘To-night we three will put up at a City Hotel.", "\"오늘 밤 우리 셋은 시내의 한 호텔에 묵을 거요."], ["To-morrow morning, Mother and Tattycoram will go down to Twickenham, where Mrs Tickit, sitting attended by Dr Buchan in the parlour-window, will think them a couple of ghosts; and I shall go abroad again for Doyce.", "내일 아침, 어머니와 태티코럼은 트위크넘으로 내려갈 거고, 거기서 응접실 창가에 부캔 박사를 모시고 앉아 있던 티킷 부인은 그 둘을 한 쌍의 유령으로 여길 거요. 그리고 나는 도이스를 데리러 다시 외국으로 가겠소."], ["We must have Dan here.", "우리는 댄을 이리로 데려와야 하오."], ["Now, I tell you, my love, it’s of no use writing and planning and conditionally speculating upon this and that and the other, at uncertain intervals and distances; we must have Doyce here.", "자, 내 말 들어보오, 이보게, 이러저러한 일들을 두고 띄엄띄엄 불확실한 간격과 거리를 두고 편지를 쓰고 궁리하고 이러쿵저러쿵 가정해 봐야 아무 소용이 없소. 우리는 도이스를 이리로 데려와야 하오."], ["I devote myself at daybreak to-morrow morning, to bringing Doyce here.", "나는 내일 동틀 녘에 도이스를 이리로 데려오는 일에 전념하겠소."], ["It’s nothing to me to go and find him.", "그를 찾아가는 건 내게 아무것도 아니오."], ["I’m an old traveller, and all foreign languages and customs are alike to me--I never understand anything about any of ‘em.", "나는 노련한 여행자고, 모든 외국어와 외국 풍습은 내게 다 매한가지요—나는 그중 어느 것도 도무지 알아듣지 못하니까."], ["Therefore I can’t be put to any inconvenience.", "그러니 나는 어떤 불편도 겪을 게 없소."], ["Go at once I must, it stands to reason; because I can’t live without breathing freely; and I can’t breathe freely until Arthur is out of this Marshalsea.", "가야 하는 건 당연하오, 이치가 그렇소. 나는 자유로이 숨 쉬지 않고는 살 수 없고, 아서가 이 마셜시에서 나오기 전까지는 자유로이 숨 쉴 수 없으니까."], ["I am stifled at the present moment, and have scarcely breath enough to say this much, and to carry this precious box down-stairs for you.’", "지금 이 순간 나는 숨이 막혀, 이만큼 말할 숨조차, 그리고 자네를 위해 이 소중한 상자를 아래층으로 들고 내려갈 숨조차 간신히 있을 뿐이오.\""]], [["They got into the street as the bell began to ring, Mr Meagles carrying the box.", "종이 울리기 시작할 무렵 그들은 거리로 나섰고, 미글스 씨가 상자를 들고 있었다."], ["Little Dorrit had no conveyance there: which rather surprised him.", "리틀 도릿은 거기에 탈 것이 없었는데, 이것이 그를 다소 놀라게 했다."], ["He called a coach for her and she got into it, and he placed the box beside her when she was seated.", "그는 그녀를 위해 마차를 불렀고, 그녀가 그 안에 올라타자, 그녀가 자리에 앉았을 때 그는 상자를 그녀 곁에 놓아 주었다."], ["In her joy and gratitude she kissed his hand.", "기쁨과 감사에 겨워 그녀는 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘I don’t like that, my dear,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"그건 싫소, 이보게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘It goes against my feeling of what’s right, that _you_ should do homage to _me_--at the Marshalsea Gate.’", "\"자네가 나에게 경의를 표하다니—그것도 마셜시 정문에서 말이오—내가 옳다고 여기는 바에 어긋나는 일이오.\""]], [["She bent forward, and kissed his cheek.", "그녀는 몸을 앞으로 숙여, 그의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["‘You remind me of the days,’ said Mr Meagles, suddenly drooping--‘but she’s very fond of him, and hides his faults, and thinks that no one sees them--and he certainly is well connected and of a very good family!’", "\"자네는 그 시절을 떠올리게 하는구려,\" 미글스 씨가 갑자기 풀이 죽으며 말했다. \"—하지만 그 애는 그를 무척 사랑하고, 그의 흠을 감싸며, 아무도 그걸 못 본다고 여기지—게다가 그는 분명 인맥도 좋고 아주 좋은 집안 출신이고 말이지!\""]], [["It was the only comfort he had in the loss of his daughter, and if he made the most of it, who could blame him?", "그것은 딸을 잃은 슬픔 속에서 그가 가진 유일한 위안이었으니, 그가 그것을 한껏 우려낸들, 누가 그를 탓할 수 있으랴?"]], [["# CHAPTER 34. Gone", "# CHAPTER 34. Gone"]], [["On a healthy autumn day, the Marshalsea prisoner, weak but otherwise restored, sat listening to a voice that read to him.", "건강한 가을날, 마셜시 죄수는 쇠약하지만 그밖에는 회복된 몸으로 앉아, 자신에게 책을 읽어 주는 목소리에 귀 기울이고 있었다."], ["On a healthy autumn day; when the golden fields had been reaped and ploughed again, when the summer fruits had ripened and waned, when the green perspectives of hops had been laid low by the busy pickers, when the apples clustering in the orchards were russet, and the berries of the mountain ash were crimson among the yellowing foliage.", "건강한 가을날, 황금빛 들판이 추수되고 다시 갈아엎어진 때, 여름 과일이 익었다가 시든 때, 푸르게 늘어선 홉밭이 바쁜 손길의 따는 이들에게 베여 쓰러진 때, 과수원에 송이송이 맺힌 사과가 적갈색으로 물들고 마가목 열매가 노래져 가는 잎새 사이에서 진홍으로 빛나던 때였다."], ["Already in the woods, glimpses of the hardy winter that was coming were to be caught through unaccustomed openings among the boughs where the prospect shone defined and clear, free from the bloom of the drowsy summer weather, which had rested on it as the bloom lies on the plum.", "숲속에서는 어느덧, 졸음 어린 여름 날씨가 자두에 분이 내려앉듯 풍경 위에 내려앉아 있던 그 뽀얀 기운이 가신 채, 정경이 또렷하고 맑게 빛나는, 평소 보지 못하던 가지 사이의 틈을 통해 다가오는 모진 겨울의 모습을 언뜻언뜻 엿볼 수 있었다."], ["So, from the seashore the ocean was no longer to be seen lying asleep in the heat, but its thousand sparkling eyes were open, and its whole breadth was in joyful animation, from the cool sand on the beach to the little sails on the horizon, drifting away like autumn-tinted leaves that had drifted from the trees.", "그리하여 바닷가에서도 바다는 더 이상 열기 속에 잠들어 누운 모습이 아니라, 그 수천 개의 반짝이는 눈을 뜬 채, 해변의 서늘한 모래에서부터 지평선 위의 작은 돛들에 이르기까지 — 나무에서 흩날려 떨어진 가을빛 나뭇잎처럼 멀어져 가는 그 돛들에 이르기까지 — 그 너른 폭 전체가 기쁨에 찬 생기로 가득했다."]], [["Changeless and barren, looking ignorantly at all the seasons with its fixed, pinched face of poverty and care, the prison had not a touch of any of these beauties on it.", "변함없고 메마른 채, 가난과 근심에 찌든 굳은 얼굴로 모든 계절을 무지하게 바라보던 그 감옥에는 이런 아름다움이라곤 한 점도 닿아 있지 않았다."], ["Blossom what would, its bricks and bars bore uniformly the same dead crop.", "무엇이 피어나든, 그 벽돌과 쇠창살은 한결같이 똑같은 죽은 작물을 맺을 뿐이었다."], ["Yet Clennam, listening to the voice as it read to him, heard in it all that great Nature was doing, heard in it all the soothing songs she sings to man.", "그러나 자신에게 책을 읽어 주는 그 목소리에 귀 기울이며, 클레넘은 그 안에서 저 위대한 자연이 하고 있는 모든 것을 들었고, 자연이 인간에게 불러 주는 온갖 어루만지는 노래를 들었다."], ["At no Mother’s knee but hers had he ever dwelt in his youth on hopeful promises, on playful fancies, on the harvests of tenderness and humility that lie hidden in the early-fostered seeds of the imagination; on the oaks of retreat from blighting winds, that have the germs of their strong roots in nursery acorns.", "그 어머니의 무릎이 아니고서는, 그는 젊은 시절 단 한 번도 희망에 찬 약속에, 장난스러운 공상에, 일찍 길러진 상상의 씨앗 속에 숨어 있는 다정함과 겸손함의 수확에, 어린 도토리 속에 강한 뿌리의 싹을 품은 채 매서운 바람을 피해 자라난 은신의 떡갈나무에 마음을 기울여 본 적이 없었다."], ["But, in the tones of the voice that read to him, there were memories of an old feeling of such things, and echoes of every merciful and loving whisper that had ever stolen to him in his life.", "그러나 그에게 책을 읽어 주는 그 목소리의 가락 속에는, 그런 것들에 대한 어떤 오래된 느낌의 기억이, 그리고 평생 그에게 살며시 스며든 적 있는 온갖 자비롭고 사랑 어린 속삭임의 메아리가 깃들어 있었다."]], [["When the voice stopped, he put his hand over his eyes, murmuring that the light was strong upon them.", "목소리가 멈추자, 그는 손으로 눈을 가리며, 빛이 너무 강하다고 중얼거렸다."]], [["Little Dorrit put the book by, and presently arose quietly to shade the window.", "리틀 도릿은 책을 옆에 내려놓고, 곧 조용히 일어나 창에 그늘을 드리웠다."], ["Maggy sat at her needlework in her old place.", "매기는 늘 앉던 자리에서 바느질을 하고 있었다."], ["The light softened, Little Dorrit brought her chair closer to his side.", "빛이 부드러워지자, 리틀 도릿은 의자를 그의 곁으로 더 가까이 끌어당겼다."]], [["‘This will soon be over now, dear Mr Clennam.", "\"이제 곧 다 끝날 거예요, 클레넘 씨."], ["Not only are Mr Doyce’s letters to you so full of friendship and encouragement, but Mr Rugg says his letters to him are so full of help, and that everybody (now a little anger is past) is so considerate, and speaks so well of you, that it will soon be over now.’", "도이스 씨가 당신께 보내 온 편지들이 우정과 격려로 가득할 뿐 아니라, 러그 씨 말로는 그분이 자기에게 보낸 편지들도 도움이 될 만한 이야기로 가득하다고 하고, (이제 조금 분이 가신 지금은) 모두가 당신을 그토록 배려하고 그토록 좋게 말한다고 하니, 이제 곧 다 끝날 거예요.\""]], [["‘Dear girl.", "\"사랑스러운 아가씨."], ["Dear heart.", "사랑스러운 마음."], ["Good angel!’", "착한 천사 같으니!\""]], [["‘You praise me far too much.", "\"당신은 저를 너무 지나치게 칭찬하세요."], ["And yet it is such an exquisite pleasure to me to hear you speak so feelingly, and to--and to see,’ said Little Dorrit, raising her eyes to his, ‘how deeply you mean it, that I cannot say Don’t.’", "그래도 당신이 그토록 마음 깊이 말씀하시는 걸 듣는 게, 그리고—또 당신이 얼마나 진심으로 그러시는지 보는 게,\" 리틀 도릿은 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 말했다, \"제게는 너무나 더없이 큰 기쁨이라서, 그만두시라는 말을 할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["He lifted her hand to his lips.", "그는 그녀의 손을 들어 입술에 갖다 댔다."]], [["‘You have been here many, many times, when I have not seen you, Little Dorrit?’", "\"당신은 여기 여러 번, 아주 여러 번 왔었지요, 내가 당신을 보지 못한 때에도, 리틀 도릿?\""]], [["‘Yes, I have been here sometimes when I have not come into the room.’", "\"네, 방에 들어오지 않은 채로 여기 와 있던 적이 더러 있었어요.\""]], [["‘Very often?’", "\"아주 자주?\""]], [["‘Rather often,’ said Little Dorrit, timidly.", "\"꽤 자주요,\" 리틀 도릿이 수줍게 말했다."]], [["‘Every day?’", "\"날마다?\""]], [["‘I think,’ said Little Dorrit, after hesitating, ‘that I have been here at least twice every day.’", "\"제 생각엔,\" 리틀 도릿은 머뭇거리다가 말했다, \"적어도 하루에 두 번씩은 여기 와 있었던 것 같아요.\""]], [["He might have released the little light hand after fervently kissing it again; but that, with a very gentle lingering where it was, it seemed to court being retained.", "그는 그 가볍고 작은 손에 다시 한 번 뜨겁게 입맞춘 뒤 놓아주었을 법도 했다. 그러나 그 손은 제자리에 아주 부드럽게 머무르며, 붙잡혀 있기를 청하는 듯했다."], ["He took it in both of his, and it lay softly on his breast.", "그는 두 손으로 그 손을 감싸 쥐었고, 그 손은 그의 가슴 위에 부드럽게 놓였다."]], [["‘Dear Little Dorrit, it is not my imprisonment only that will soon be over.", "\"사랑하는 리틀 도릿, 이제 곧 끝날 것은 내 감금만이 아니오."], ["This sacrifice of you must be ended.", "당신의 이 희생도 끝나야만 하오."], ["We must learn to part again, and to take our different ways so wide asunder.", "우리는 다시 헤어지는 법을, 그리고 그토록 멀리 갈라진 서로 다른 길을 가는 법을 배워야만 하오."], ["You have not forgotten what we said together, when you came back?’", "당신이 돌아왔을 때 우리가 함께 나눈 이야기를 잊지 않았겠지요?\""]], [["‘O no, I have not forgotten it.", "\"오, 아뇨, 잊지 않았어요."], ["But something has been--You feel quite strong to-day, don’t you?’", "하지만 무슨 일이 있었어요—오늘은 기운이 아주 좋으시죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["‘Quite strong.’", "\"아주 좋소.\""]], [["The hand he held crept up a little nearer his face.", "그가 쥐고 있던 손이 그의 얼굴 쪽으로 조금 더 가까이 기어 올라왔다."]], [["‘Do you feel quite strong enough to know what a great fortune I have got?’", "\"제가 얼마나 큰 재산을 얻었는지 들으실 만큼 기운이 충분히 좋으세요?\""]], [["‘I shall be very glad to be told.", "\"들으면 무척 기쁘겠소."], ["No fortune can be too great or good for Little Dorrit.’", "어떤 재산도 리틀 도릿에게는 지나치게 크거나 좋을 수 없으니.\""]], [["‘I have been anxiously waiting to tell you.", "\"저는 당신께 말씀드리려고 애타게 기다려 왔어요."], ["I have been longing and longing to tell you.", "말씀드리고 싶어 정말 정말 안달이 났었어요."], ["You are sure you will not take it?’", "정말 받지 않으실 거죠?\""]], [["‘Never!’", "\"절대로!\""]], [["‘You are quite sure you will not take half of it?’", "\"정말 그 절반도 받지 않으실 거죠?\""]], [["‘Never, dear Little Dorrit!’", "\"절대로, 사랑하는 리틀 도릿!\""]], [["As she looked at him silently, there was something in her affectionate face that he did not quite comprehend: something that could have broken into tears in a moment, and yet that was happy and proud.", "그녀가 말없이 그를 바라볼 때, 그 다정한 얼굴에는 그가 잘 이해할 수 없는 무언가가 있었다. 금방이라도 눈물로 무너질 듯하면서도, 행복하고 자랑스러운 무언가가."]], [["‘You will be sorry to hear what I have to tell you about Fanny.", "\"제가 패니에 대해 말씀드릴 이야기를 들으시면 마음이 아프실 거예요."], ["Poor Fanny has lost everything.", "가엾은 패니는 모든 걸 잃었어요."], ["She has nothing left but her husband’s income.", "이제 남은 거라곤 남편의 수입뿐이에요."], ["All that papa gave her when she married was lost as your money was lost.", "패니가 결혼할 때 아버지가 주신 것이 모두 당신의 돈처럼 사라져 버렸어요."], ["It was in the same hands, and it is all gone.’", "같은 사람 손에 맡겨져 있었고, 그래서 전부 없어졌어요.\""]], [["Arthur was more shocked than surprised to hear it.", "아서는 그 말을 듣고 놀랐다기보다 충격을 받았다. \"그렇게까지 나쁘진 않기를 바랐는데,\""], ["‘I had hoped it might not be so bad,’ he said: ‘but I had feared a heavy loss there, knowing the connection between her husband and the defaulter.’", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 그녀의 남편과 그 파산자 사이의 관계를 아는 터라, 그쪽에서 큰 손실이 있을까 봐 두려워하고는 있었소.\""]], [["‘Yes.", "\"네."], ["It is all gone.", "전부 사라졌어요."], ["I am very sorry for Fanny; very, very, very sorry for poor Fanny.", "패니가 정말 안됐어요. 가엾은 패니가 정말, 정말, 정말 안됐어요."], ["My poor brother too!’", "가엾은 오라버니도요!\""]], [["‘Had _he_ property in the same hands?’", "\"그 사람도 같은 사람 손에 재산을 맡겼소?\""]], [["‘Yes!", "\"네!"], ["And it’s all gone.--How much do you think my own great fortune is?’", "그것도 전부 사라졌어요.—제 큰 재산이 얼마나 된다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["As Arthur looked at her inquiringly, with a new apprehension on him, she withdrew her hand, and laid her face down on the spot where it had rested.", "아서가 새로운 불안에 사로잡힌 채 캐묻듯 그녀를 바라보자, 그녀는 손을 거두어, 조금 전까지 그 손이 놓여 있던 자리에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["‘I have nothing in the world.", "\"저는 세상에 아무것도 없어요."], ["I am as poor as when I lived here.", "여기 살던 때만큼이나 가난해요."], ["When papa came over to England, he confided everything he had to the same hands, and it is all swept away.", "아버지가 영국으로 건너오셨을 때, 가진 것을 모두 그 같은 사람 손에 맡기셨고, 그래서 전부 쓸려가 버렸어요."], ["O my dearest and best, are you quite sure you will not share my fortune with me now?’", "오, 가장 사랑하고 가장 소중한 분, 이제 정말로 저와 제 재산을 함께 나누지 않으실 거예요?\""]], [["Locked in his arms, held to his heart, with his manly tears upon her own cheek, she drew the slight hand round his neck, and clasped it in its fellow-hand.", "그의 팔에 안긴 채, 그의 가슴에 꼭 끌어안긴 채, 사내다운 그의 눈물이 자기 뺨 위로 흐르는 가운데, 그녀는 그 가녀린 손을 그의 목에 둘러 다른 손으로 맞잡았다."]], [["‘Never to part, my dearest Arthur; never any more, until the last!", "\"절대로 헤어지지 말아요, 가장 사랑하는 아서. 마지막 순간까지 다시는 헤어지지 말아요!"], ["I never was rich before, I never was proud before, I never was happy before, I am rich in being taken by you, I am proud in having been resigned by you, I am happy in being with you in this prison, as I should be happy in coming back to it with you, if it should be the will of GOD, and comforting and serving you with all my love and truth.", "저는 한 번도 부유했던 적이 없고, 한 번도 자랑스러웠던 적이 없고, 한 번도 행복했던 적이 없어요. 저는 당신에게 받아들여져 부유하고, 당신에게 단념받았기에 자랑스럽고, 이 감옥에서 당신과 함께 있어 행복해요."], ["I am yours anywhere, everywhere!", "하느님의 뜻이라면 당신과 함께 이곳으로 다시 돌아와 제 모든 사랑과 진심을 다해 당신을 위로하고 섬기는 것도 똑같이 행복할 거예요."], ["I love you dearly!", "저는 어디서든, 어디에서나 당신의 것이에요!"], ["I would rather pass my life here with you, and go out daily, working for our bread, than I would have the greatest fortune that ever was told, and be the greatest lady that ever was honoured.", "저는 당신을 진심으로 사랑해요! 여태껏 들어 본 가장 큰 재산을 가지고 여태껏 떠받들린 가장 위대한 귀부인이 되느니, 차라리 여기서 당신과 함께 살며 날마다 나가서 우리 먹을 빵을 위해 일하는 편이 더 좋아요."], ["O, if poor papa may only know how blest at last my heart is, in this room where he suffered for so many years!’", "오, 가엾은 아버지가, 그토록 여러 해 고통받으신 이 방에서 마침내 제 마음이 얼마나 큰 축복을 받았는지 알아만 주신다면!\""]], [["Maggy had of course been staring from the first, and had of course been crying her eyes out long before this.", "매기는 물론 처음부터 줄곧 빤히 쳐다보고 있었고, 물론 이미 한참 전부터 눈이 빠지도록 울고 있었다."], ["Maggy was now so overjoyed that, after hugging her little mother with all her might, she went down-stairs like a clog-hornpipe to find somebody or other to whom to impart her gladness.", "매기는 이제 너무도 기뻐서, 자기 작은 어머니를 있는 힘껏 끌어안은 뒤, 자기 기쁨을 전할 누군가를 찾으러 나막신 호른파이프 춤이라도 추듯 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Whom should Maggy meet but Flora and Mr F.’s Aunt opportunely coming in?", "그런데 매기가 마주친 사람이 누구였겠는가, 마침 들어오던 플로라와 에프 씨의 고모가 아닌가?"], ["And whom else, as a consequence of that meeting, should Little Dorrit find waiting for herself, when, a good two or three hours afterwards, she went out?", "그리고 그 만남의 결과로, 두세 시간이 족히 지난 뒤 리틀 도릿이 밖으로 나갔을 때 자신을 기다리고 있는 것을 발견한 사람은 또 누구였겠는가?"]], [["Flora’s eyes were a little red, and she seemed rather out of spirits.", "플로라의 눈은 조금 붉었고, 기운이 다소 없어 보였다."], ["Mr F.’s Aunt was so stiffened that she had the appearance of being past bending by any means short of powerful mechanical pressure.", "에프 씨의 고모는 어찌나 뻣뻣하게 굳어 있던지, 강력한 기계적 압력이 아니고서는 도무지 구부러지지 않을 것 같은 모습이었다."], ["Her bonnet was cocked up behind in a terrific manner; and her stony reticule was as rigid as if it had been petrified by the Gorgon’s head, and had got it at that moment inside.", "그녀의 보닛은 뒤쪽이 무시무시하게 치켜올려져 있었고, 돌처럼 단단한 손가방은 마치 고르곤의 머리에 닿아 돌로 굳어 버린 듯, 그리고 바로 그 순간 그 머리를 안에 담고 있기라도 한 듯 빳빳했다."], ["With these imposing attributes, Mr F.’s Aunt, publicly seated on the steps of the Marshal’s official residence, had been for the two or three hours in question a great boon to the younger inhabitants of the Borough, whose sallies of humour she had considerably flushed herself by resenting at the point of her umbrella, from time to time.", "이렇게 위압적인 모습을 갖춘 채, 에프 씨의 고모는 마셜시 간수장 관사 계단에 떡하니 앉아, 문제의 두세 시간 동안 자치구의 어린것들에게 큰 구경거리가 되어 주었는데, 그 아이들의 우스갯소리에 이따금 우산 끝을 들이대며 분개하는 통에 그녀 자신이 상당히 얼굴을 붉히곤 했다."]], [["‘Painfully aware, Miss Dorrit, I am sure,’ said Flora, ‘that to propose an adjournment to any place to one so far removed by fortune and so courted and caressed by the best society must ever appear intruding even if not a pie-shop far below your present sphere and a back-parlour though a civil man but if for the sake of Arthur--cannot overcome it more improper now than ever late Doyce and Clennam--one last remark I might wish to make one last explanation I might wish to offer perhaps your good nature might excuse under pretence of three kidney ones the humble place of conversation.’", "\"도릿 양, 운명에 의해 그토록 높이 멀어진 데다 최상류 사교계의 그토록 떠받들림과 어루만짐을 받는 분께 어딘가로 자리를 옮기자 청한다는 것이 늘 주제넘게 비치리란 걸 저도 정말 가슴 아프게 잘 알지만, 비록 지금의 신분보다야 한참 아래인 파이 가게라 한들 그리고 안쪽 거실이라 한들 그래도 점잖은 사람이라서, 하지만 아서를 봐서라도—도무지 그걸 떨쳐 낼 수가 없네요 이제 와선 그 어느 때보다 부적절하지만 고인이 된 도이스 앤드 클레넘—마지막으로 한마디 드리고 싶은 게 있고 마지막으로 설명 하나 올리고 싶은 게 있으니 어쩌면 콩팥 파이 세 개를 핑계로 삼아 그 소박한 대화의 자리를 당신의 너그러움으로 너그러이 봐주실지도,\" 플로라가 말했다."]], [["Rightly interpreting this rather obscure speech, Little Dorrit returned that she was quite at Flora’s disposition.", "이 다소 모호한 말을 제대로 알아들은 리틀 도릿은, 자신은 전적으로 플로라가 하자는 대로 하겠다고 답했다."], ["Flora accordingly led the way across the road to the pie-shop in question: Mr F.’s Aunt stalking across in the rear, and putting herself in the way of being run over, with a perseverance worthy of a better cause.", "그러자 플로라는 길 건너 문제의 파이 가게로 앞장서 갔다. 에프 씨의 고모는 그 뒤에서 성큼성큼 걸으며, 차에 치이는 길에 제 몸을 들이미는 짓을 더 나은 명분에나 어울릴 끈기로 거듭했다."]], [["When the ‘three kidney ones,’ which were to be a blind to the conversation, were set before them on three little tin platters, each kidney one ornamented with a hole at the top, into which the civil man poured hot gravy out of a spouted can as if he were feeding three lamps, Flora took out her pocket-handkerchief.", "대화의 눈가림이 될 그 \"콩팥 파이 세 개\"가 작은 양철 접시 셋에 담겨 그들 앞에 놓이고, 콩팥 파이마다 위쪽에 구멍이 하나씩 장식처럼 뚫려 있어 점잖은 사내가 마치 등잔 세 개에 기름을 채우듯 주둥이 달린 깡통에서 뜨거운 그레이비를 그 구멍에 부어 넣자, 플로라는 손수건을 꺼냈다."]], [["‘If Fancy’s fair dreams,’ she began, ‘have ever pictured that when Arthur--cannot overcome it pray excuse me--was restored to freedom even a pie as far from flaky as the present and so deficient in kidney as to be in that respect like a minced nutmeg might not prove unacceptable if offered by the hand of true regard such visions have for ever fled and all is cancelled but being aware that tender relations are in contemplation beg to state that I heartily wish well to both and find no fault with either not the least, it may be withering to know that ere the hand of Time had made me much less slim than formerly and dreadfully red on the slightest exertion particularly after eating I well know when it takes the form of a rash, it might have been and was not through the interruption of parents and mental torpor succeeded until the mysterious clue was held by Mr F.", "\"공상의 고운 꿈이,\""], ["still I would not be ungenerous to either and I heartily wish well to both.’", "그녀가 입을 열었다, \"혹시라도 아서가—도무지 떨쳐 낼 수가 없네요 부디 용서하세요—자유의 몸이 되었을 때, 지금 이것처럼 도무지 바삭하지도 않고 콩팥이 모자라 그 점에선 다진 육두구나 다름없는 파이라 한들 참된 정성의 손길로 내민다면 받아 주지 않으리란 법도 없으리라 그려 본 적이 있다 해도, 그런 환상은 이제 영영 달아나 버렸고 모든 게 무효가 되었지만, 다정한 인연이 무르익고 있다는 걸 아는 터라 두 분 모두 진심으로 잘되시길 바라며 어느 쪽에도 아무런 흠을 잡지 않으니 조금도 흠잡지 않으니, 세월의 손길이 저를 예전보다 훨씬 덜 날씬하게 만들고 조금만 움직여도 끔찍하게 붉어지게 만들기 전에 특히 뭘 먹고 나면 더 그렇고 그게 발진의 모양으로 나타날 땐 저도 잘 알지만, 그럴 수도 있었으나 부모의 훼방으로 그러지 못했고 그 신비로운 실마리를 에프 씨가 쥐기 전까지 마음의 마비가 이어졌다는 걸 안다는 게 가슴이 시들 만큼 쓰라리겠지만, 그래도 저는 어느 쪽에도 인색하게 굴고 싶지 않으며 두 분 모두 진심으로 잘되시길 바라요.\""]], [["Little Dorrit took her hand, and thanked her for all her old kindness.", "리틀 도릿은 그녀의 손을 잡고, 예전에 베풀어 준 온갖 친절에 감사했다."]], [["‘Call it not kindness,’ returned Flora, giving her an honest kiss, ‘for you always were the best and dearest little thing that ever was if I may take the liberty and even in a money point of view a saving being Conscience itself though I must add much more agreeable than mine ever was to me for though not I hope more burdened than other people’s yet I have always found it far readier to make one uncomfortable than comfortable and evidently taking a greater pleasure in doing it but I am wandering, one hope I wish to express ere yet the closing scene draws in and it is that I do trust for the sake of old times and old sincerity that Arthur will know that I didn’t desert him in his misfortunes but that I came backwards and forwards constantly to ask if I could do anything for him and that I sat in the pie-shop where they very civilly fetched something warm in a tumbler from the hotel and really very nice hours after hours to keep him company over the way without his knowing it.’", "\"친절이라 부르지 마세요,\" 플로라는 그녀에게 진심 어린 입맞춤을 건네며 말했다, \"당신은 외람되이 말하자면 여태껏 있은 가장 착하고 가장 사랑스러운 작은 사람이었고 돈의 관점에서 보더라도 그야말로 양심 그 자체였으니까요 다만 한마디 보태자면 제 양심이 제게 그랬던 것보다야 훨씬 더 상냥한 양심이었지만 제 양심은 남들 것보다 더 무겁진 않기를 바라지만 그래도 늘 사람을 편하게 해 주기보단 불편하게 만드는 데 훨씬 더 빠르고 그러는 걸 더 즐기는 게 분명했으니까요 하지만 또 옆길로 새네요, 막이 내리기 전에 한 가지 바람을 말씀드리고 싶은데 그것은 옛 시절과 옛 진심을 봐서 아서가 꼭 알아 주기를 바란다는 거예요 제가 그분의 불행 속에서 그분을 저버리지 않았다는 걸 그분이 알아 주기를, 그리고 제가 끊임없이 오가며 그분을 위해 제가 할 수 있는 일이 없는지 물었다는 걸, 그리고 길 건너편에서 그분 모르게 곁을 지켜 주려고, 호텔에서 텀블러에 따뜻한 걸 아주 정중히 가져다 주고 정말 아주 좋았던 그 파이 가게에서 몇 시간이고 몇 시간이고 앉아 있었다는 걸 알아 주기를 바란다는 거예요.\""]], [["Flora really had tears in her eyes now, and they showed her to great advantage.", "이제 플로라의 눈에는 정말로 눈물이 고여 있었고, 그 눈물은 그녀를 무척 돋보이게 했다."]], [["‘Over and above which,’ said Flora, ‘I earnestly beg you as the dearest thing that ever was if you’ll still excuse the familiarity from one who moves in very different circles to let Arthur understand that I don’t know after all whether it wasn’t all nonsense between us though pleasant at the time and trying too and certainly Mr F.", "\"그뿐만 아니라,\""], ["did work a change and the spell being broken nothing could be expected to take place without weaving it afresh which various circumstances have combined to prevent of which perhaps not the least powerful was that it was not to be, I am not prepared to say that if it had been agreeable to Arthur and had brought itself about naturally in the first instance I should not have been very glad being of a lively disposition and moped at home where papa undoubtedly is the most aggravating of his sex and not improved since having been cut down by the hand of the Incendiary into something of which I never saw the counterpart in all my life but jealousy is not my character nor ill-will though many faults.’", "플로라가 말했다, \"여태껏 있은 가장 사랑스러운 사람인 당신께 간곡히 부탁드리는데 아주 다른 세계에서 노니는 사람의 이 격의 없음을 그래도 용서해 주신다면, 아서가 이걸 알게 해 주세요 결국 우리 사이의 그 모든 게 부질없는 일이 아니었는지 저도 잘 모르겠지만 그때는 즐거웠고 또 가슴 아프기도 했지만 분명 에프 씨가 변화를 일으켰고 그 주문이 깨진 뒤로는 새로 엮어 내지 않고서야 아무 일도 일어날 수 없었는데 여러 사정이 겹쳐 그것을 막았고 그중에서도 가장 강력했던 건 아마 그게 이루어질 운명이 아니었다는 것이며, 그게 아서에게도 흡족했고 애초에 자연스럽게 그렇게 되었더라면 제가 무척 기뻐하지 않았으리라고는 말 못 하겠으니 제가 본래 활달한 성미인 데다 집에선 시무룩하게 지냈고 거기선 아버지가 분명 남자 중에 가장 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓는 양반이고 방화범의 손에 잘려 나가 제 평생 본 적 없는 무언가가 되어 버린 뒤로는 조금도 나아지지 않았으니까요 하지만 질투는 제 성품이 아니고 악의도 아니에요 비록 흠이야 많지만.\""]], [["Without having been able closely to follow Mrs Finching through this labyrinth, Little Dorrit understood its purpose, and cordially accepted the trust.", "리틀 도릿은 핀칭 부인의 이 미로를 빠짐없이 따라가지는 못했으나, 그 취지만은 알아들었고, 그 부탁을 진심으로 받아들였다."]], [["‘The withered chaplet my dear,’ said Flora, with great enjoyment, ‘is then perished the column is crumbled and the pyramid is standing upside down upon its what’s-his-name call it not giddiness call it not weakness call it not folly I must now retire into privacy and look upon the ashes of departed joys no more but taking a further liberty of paying for the pastry which has formed the humble pretext of our interview will for ever say Adieu!’", "\"시든 화관은요 아가씨,\" 플로라가 무척 즐거운 듯 말했다, \"이제 다 말라 버렸고 기둥은 무너졌으며 피라미드는 그 뭐라더라 위에 거꾸로 서 있는데 그걸 어지럼증이라 부르지도 말고 나약함이라 부르지도 말고 어리석음이라 부르지도 마세요 저는 이제 홀로 물러나 사라진 기쁨의 잿더미를 더는 바라보지 않으렵니다만 우리 만남의 소박한 핑계가 되어 준 이 파이 값을 치르는 한 가지 격의 없음을 또 한 번 부려, 영영 작별을 고하겠어요!\""]], [["Mr F.’s Aunt, who had eaten her pie with great solemnity, and who had been elaborating some grievous scheme of injury in her mind since her first assumption of that public position on the Marshal’s steps, took the present opportunity of addressing the following Sibyllic apostrophe to the relict of her late nephew.", "에프 씨의 고모는 자기 파이를 무척 엄숙하게 먹어 치웠고, 간수장 계단에 그 공공연한 자리를 처음 차지한 이래로 마음속에 어떤 통탄할 보복의 계략을 골똘히 꾸며 오던 차였는데, 이 기회를 틈타 고인이 된 조카의 미망인을 향해 다음과 같은 무녀 같은 호령을 내질렀다."]], [["‘Bring him for’ard, and I’ll chuck him out o’ winder!’", "\"그자를 앞으로 끌어내, 그럼 내 창밖으로 내던질 테니!\""]], [["Flora tried in vain to soothe the excellent woman by explaining that they were going home to dinner. Mr F.’s Aunt persisted in replying, ‘Bring him for’ard and I’ll chuck him out o’ winder!’ Having reiterated this demand an immense number of times, with a sustained glare of defiance at Little Dorrit, Mr F.’s Aunt folded her arms, and sat down in the corner of the pie-shop parlour; steadfastly refusing to budge until such time as ‘he’ should have been ‘brought for’ard,’ and the chucking portion of his destiny accomplished.", "플로라는 우리는 이제 저녁을 먹으러 집에 가는 거라고 설명하며 이 훌륭한 부인을 달래려 했으나 헛수고였다. 에프 씨의 고모는 한사코 \"그자를 앞으로 끌어내, 그럼 내 창밖으로 내던질 테니!\"라고만 되받았다. 리틀 도릿을 향해 도전적인 노려봄을 줄곧 거두지 않은 채 이 요구를 엄청난 횟수로 거듭한 뒤, 에프 씨의 고모는 팔짱을 끼고 파이 가게 거실 구석에 주저앉아, \"그자가\" \"앞으로 끌려 나오고\" 그자의 운명 가운데 내던지는 대목이 완수되기 전에는 꿈쩍도 하지 않겠다고 단호히 버텼다."]], [["In this condition of things, Flora confided to Little Dorrit that she had not seen Mr F.’s Aunt so full of life and character for weeks; that she would find it necessary to remain there ‘hours perhaps,’ until the inexorable old lady could be softened; and that she could manage her best alone.", "이런 상황에서, 플로라는 리틀 도릿에게 에프 씨의 고모가 이렇게 활기와 기개로 가득 찬 모습을 몇 주 만에 처음 본다고, 저 완고한 노부인이 누그러질 때까지 자기가 \"어쩌면 몇 시간이고\" 여기 머물러야 할 것 같다고, 그리고 자기 혼자서 가장 잘 다룰 수 있을 거라고 털어놓았다."], ["They parted, therefore, in the friendliest manner, and with the kindest feeling on both sides.", "그리하여 두 사람은 더없이 다정하게, 그리고 양쪽 모두 더없이 따뜻한 마음으로 헤어졌다."]], [["Mr F.’s Aunt holding out like a grim fortress, and Flora becoming in need of refreshment, a messenger was despatched to the hotel for the tumbler already glanced at, which was afterwards replenished.", "에프 씨의 고모가 음산한 요새처럼 버티고, 플로라는 원기를 돋울 것이 필요해지자, 앞서 언급한 그 텀블러를 가지러 호텔로 심부름꾼을 보냈고, 그것은 나중에 다시 채워졌다."], ["With the aid of its content, a newspaper, and some skimming of the cream of the pie-stock, Flora got through the remainder of the day in perfect good humour; though occasionally embarrassed by the consequences of an idle rumour which circulated among the credulous infants of the neighbourhood, to the effect that an old lady had sold herself to the pie-shop to be made up, and was then sitting in the pie-shop parlour, declining to complete her contract.", "그 내용물과 신문 한 부, 그리고 파이 재고에서 떠낸 크림 약간의 도움으로, 플로라는 그날의 나머지를 더없이 좋은 기분으로 보냈다. 다만 동네의 잘 속는 어린것들 사이에 떠도는 한가한 소문 때문에 이따금 곤란을 겪었는데, 그 소문인즉 어떤 노부인이 자기 몸을 재료로 만들어지라고 파이 가게에 팔아넘겼고, 지금 파이 가게 거실에 앉아 그 계약을 마저 이행하기를 거부하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["This attracted so many young persons of both sexes, and, when the shades of evening began to fall, occasioned so much interruption to the business, that the merchant became very pressing in his proposals that Mr F.’s Aunt should be removed.", "이 소문이 남녀 가릴 것 없이 어찌나 많은 젊은이를 불러 모았는지, 그리고 저녁 어스름이 깔리기 시작할 무렵엔 장사에 어찌나 큰 지장을 주었는지, 가게 주인은 에프 씨의 고모를 데려가 달라고 무척 다급하게 청해 왔다."], ["A conveyance was accordingly brought to the door, which, by the joint efforts of the merchant and Flora, this remarkable woman was at last induced to enter; though not without even then putting her head out of the window, and demanding to have him ‘brought for’ard’ for the purpose originally mentioned.", "그래서 탈것 하나가 문 앞에 대령되었고, 주인과 플로라의 합심한 노력으로 이 놀라운 여인은 마침내 그 안에 들어가도록 구슬려졌으나, 그러면서도 창밖으로 머리를 내밀고 애초에 말한 그 목적으로 그자를 \"앞으로 끌어내라\"고 요구하는 것만은 빼놓지 않았다."], ["As she was observed at this time to direct baleful glances towards the Marshalsea, it has been supposed that this admirably consistent female intended by ‘him,’ Arthur Clennam.", "이때 그녀가 마셜시 쪽으로 사악한 눈길을 던지는 것이 목격된 까닭에, 이 감탄스러우리만치 일관된 여인이 \"그자\"로 가리킨 것은 아서 클레넘이었으리라고 추측되어 왔다."], ["This, however, is mere speculation; who the person was, who, for the satisfaction of Mr F.’s Aunt’s mind, ought to have been brought forward and never was brought forward, will never be positively known.", "그러나 이는 한낱 억측일 뿐이다. 에프 씨의 고모의 마음을 흡족하게 하기 위해 응당 앞으로 끌려 나왔어야 했으나 끝내 끌려 나오지 않은 그 사람이 누구였는지는 영영 확실히 알려지지 않을 것이다."]], [["The autumn days went on, and Little Dorrit never came to the Marshalsea now and went away without seeing him.", "가을날이 흘러가는 동안, 리틀 도릿은 이제 마셜시에 와서 그를 보지 않은 채 돌아가는 일이 결코 없었다."], ["No, no, no.", "결코, 결코, 결코."]], [["One morning, as Arthur listened for the light feet that every morning ascended winged to his heart, bringing the heavenly brightness of a new love into the room where the old love had wrought so hard and been so true; one morning, as he listened, he heard her coming, not alone.", "어느 날 아침, 아서가 매일 아침 날개 돋친 듯 그의 가슴으로 올라오던, 옛 사랑이 그토록 애써 일하고 그토록 진실했던 그 방으로 새로운 사랑의 천상의 광휘를 실어 오던 그 가벼운 발소리에 귀 기울이고 있을 때, 어느 날 아침 귀 기울이고 있을 때, 그는 그녀가 혼자가 아니게 다가오는 소리를 들었다."]], [["‘Dear Arthur,’ said her delighted voice outside the door, ‘I have some one here.", "\"아서 씨,\" 문밖에서 그녀의 들뜬 목소리가 들려왔다, \"여기 누구를 한 분 모셔 왔어요."], ["May I bring some one in?’", "들어가도 될까요?\""]], [["He had thought from the tread there were two with her.", "발소리로 보아 그녀와 함께 둘이 있다고 그는 생각했다."], ["He answered ‘Yes,’ and she came in with Mr Meagles.", "그가 \"네\"라고 답하자, 그녀는 미글스 씨와 함께 들어왔다."], ["Sun-browned and jolly Mr Meagles looked, and he opened his arms and folded Arthur in them, like a sun-browned and jolly father.", "햇볕에 그을리고 유쾌해 보이는 미글스 씨는 두 팔을 벌려, 햇볕에 그을리고 유쾌한 아버지처럼 아서를 감싸 안았다."]], [["‘Now I am all right,’ said Mr Meagles, after a minute or so.", "\"자, 이제 됐네,\" 잠시 후 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘Now it’s over.", "\"이제 끝났어."], ["Arthur, my dear fellow, confess at once that you expected me before.’", "아서, 이 친구야, 자네 진작 나를 기다렸다고 당장 실토하게.\""]], [["‘I did,’ said Arthur; ‘but Amy told me--’", "\"그랬습니다,\" 아서가 말했다. \"하지만 에이미가 제게 말하길—\""]], [["‘Little Dorrit.", "\"리틀 도릿이에요."], ["Never any other name.’ (It was she who whispered it.)", "다른 이름은 절대 안 돼요.\" (속삭여 그렇게 말한 것은 그녀였다.)"]], [["‘--But my Little Dorrit told me that, without asking for any further explanation, I was not to expect you until I saw you.’", "\"—하지만 제 리틀 도릿이 말하길, 더 캐묻지 말고, 직접 뵙기 전까지는 어르신을 기다리지 말라더군요.\""]], [["‘And now you see me, my boy,’ said Mr Meagles, shaking him by the hand stoutly; ‘and now you shall have any explanation and every explanation. The fact is, I _was_ here--came straight to you from the Allongers and Marshongers, or I should be ashamed to look you in the face this day,--but you were not in company trim at the moment, and I had to start off again to catch Doyce.’", "\"그리고 이제 자네가 나를 보는 게로군, 이 사람아,\" 미글스 씨가 그의 손을 힘차게 흔들며 말했다. \"그러니 이제 어떤 설명이든 죄다 들려줌세. 사실은 말일세, 내가 여기 와 있었어—'알롱제'와 '마르송제'들 한복판에서 곧장 자네에게 달려왔지, 안 그랬다면 내가 오늘 자네 얼굴을 볼 낯이 없었을 게야—하지만 그때 자넨 사람 만날 형편이 아니었고, 나는 도이스를 붙잡으러 다시 떠나야 했네.\" * 알롱제·마르송제: 미글스 씨가 프랑스어 \"allons(가자)\", \"marchons(걷자)\"를 멋대로 비틀어 만든, 외국인을 가리키는 우스개 말."]], [["‘Poor Doyce!’ sighed Arthur.", "\"가엾은 도이스!\" 아서가 한숨지었다."]], [["‘Don’t call him names that he don’t deserve,’ said Mr Meagles.", "\"그 사람이 받지도 않을 이름으로 부르지 말게,\" 미글스 씨가 말했다."], ["‘_He’s_ not poor; _he’s_ doing well enough.", "\"그 사람은 가엾지 않아. 아주 잘 지내고 있어."], ["Doyce is a wonderful fellow over there.", "도이스는 저쪽에선 대단한 인물이야."], ["I assure you he is making out his case like a house a-fire.", "정말이지 일을 불붙듯 척척 풀어내고 있다네."], ["He has fallen on his legs, has Dan.", "댄, 그 친구 제대로 자리를 잡았어."], ["Where they don’t want things done and find a man to do ‘em, that man’s off his legs; but where they do want things done and find a man to do ‘em, that man’s on his legs.", "일이 되는 걸 바라지 않으면서 그 일을 할 사람을 만나면, 그 사람은 발붙일 데가 없어지지. 하지만 일이 되기를 바라면서 그 일을 할 사람을 만나면, 그 사람은 든든히 발을 딛고 서게 되는 법이야."], ["You won’t have occasion to trouble the Circumlocution Office any more.", "자넨 이제 에두름국을 더 성가시게 할 일이 없을 걸세."], ["Let me tell you, Dan has done without ‘em!’", "내 말 들어 보게, 댄은 그자들 없이도 해냈다네!\""]], [["‘What a load you take from my mind!’ cried Arthur.", "\"이렇게 제 마음의 짐을 덜어 주시다니요!\" 아서가 외쳤다."], ["‘What happiness you give me!’", "\"이렇게 큰 행복을 주시다니요!\""]], [["‘Happiness?’ retorted Mr Meagles.", "\"행복이라?\" 미글스 씨가 되받았다."], ["‘Don’t talk about happiness till you see Dan.", "\"댄을 보기 전까진 행복 얘긴 꺼내지도 말게."], ["I assure you Dan is directing works and executing labours over yonder, that it would make your hair stand on end to look at.", "정말이지 댄은 저쪽에서 공사를 지휘하고 역사를 척척 해내고 있어서, 그걸 보면 자네 머리카락이 쭈뼛 설 정도라네."], ["He’s no public offender, bless you, now!", "이제 그 사람은 절대 공공의 범죄자가 아니야, 천만에!"], ["He’s medalled and ribboned, and starred and crossed, and I don’t-know-what all’d, like a born nobleman.", "메달에 리본에 별 훈장에 십자 훈장에, 그 밖에 뭐가 뭔지 모를 것까지 줄줄이 받아서, 타고난 귀족이나 다름없다네."], ["But we mustn’t talk about that over here.’", "하지만 그 얘긴 여기 이쪽에선 해선 안 되네.\""]], [["‘Why not?’", "\"왜 안 됩니까?\""]], [["‘Oh, egad!’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head very seriously, ‘he must hide all those things under lock and key when he comes over here.", "\"오, 이런!\" 미글스 씨가 아주 진지하게 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["They won’t do over here.", "\"그 사람은 여기 이쪽으로 건너오면 그런 것들을 죄다 자물쇠 채워 감춰 둬야 해."], ["In that particular, Britannia is a Britannia in the Manger--won’t give her children such distinctions herself, and won’t allow them to be seen when they are given by other countries.", "여기선 그게 통하지 않거든. 바로 그 점에서, 브리타니아는 여물통 위의 개 같은 브리타니아야—제 자식들에게 그런 영예를 스스로 주지도 않으면서, 다른 나라들이 줬을 땐 그걸 드러내는 것조차 허락하지 않으니 말일세."], ["No, no, Dan!’ said Mr Meagles, shaking his head again.", "안 되지, 안 돼, 댄!\" 미글스 씨가 다시 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["‘That won’t do here!’", "\"여기선 그게 통하지 않아!\""]], [["‘If you had brought me (except for Doyce’s sake) twice what I have lost,’ cried Arthur, ‘you would not have given me the pleasure that you give me in this news.’", "\"제가 잃은 것의 곱절을 (도이스를 위해서가 아니라면) 가져다주셨더라도,\" 아서가 외쳤다. \"이 소식으로 제게 주시는 이 기쁨만큼은 주지 못하셨을 겁니다.\""]], [["‘Why, of course, of course,’ assented Mr Meagles.", "\"암, 그렇고말고, 그렇고말고,\" 미글스 씨가 맞장구쳤다."], ["‘Of course I know that, my good fellow, and therefore I come out with it in the first burst.", "\"물론 나도 그걸 알지, 이 사람아, 그래서 맨 먼저 이 얘기부터 꺼낸 걸세."], ["Now, to go back, about catching Doyce.", "자, 다시 도이스를 붙잡은 얘기로 돌아가서."], ["I caught Doyce.", "내가 도이스를 붙잡았네."], ["Ran against him among a lot of those dirty brown dogs in women’s nightcaps a great deal too big for ‘em, calling themselves Arabs and all sorts of incoherent races.", "자기들한텐 너무 큰 여자용 나이트캡을 쓴 그 지저분한 갈색 개 같은 작자들, 스스로를 아랍인이네 뭐네 별 두서없는 인종이라 일컫는 작자들 한복판에서 그를 딱 맞닥뜨렸지."], ["_You_ know ‘em!", "자네도 그치들 알지!"], ["Well!", "그래!"], ["He was coming straight to me, and I was going to him, and so we came back together.’", "그 사람은 곧장 내게로 오던 참이었고, 나도 그에게 가던 참이라, 그렇게 우리 둘이 함께 돌아왔다네.\""]], [["‘Doyce in England!’ exclaimed Arthur.", "\"도이스가 영국에 와 있다고요!\" 아서가 외쳤다."]], [["‘There!’ said Mr Meagles, throwing open his arms.", "\"바로 그거야!\" 미글스 씨가 두 팔을 활짝 벌리며 말했다."], ["‘I am the worst man in the world to manage a thing of this sort.", "\"이런 일을 처리하는 데는 내가 세상에서 가장 서투른 사람이라네."], ["I don’t know what I should have done if I had been in the diplomatic line--right, perhaps!", "내가 외교 쪽 일을 했더라면 어쩔 뻔했는지 모르겠어—하긴 잘했을지도 모르지!"], ["The long and short of it is, Arthur, we have both been in England this fortnight.", "요컨대 말일세, 아서, 우리 둘 다 이 주째 영국에 와 있었네."], ["And if you go on to ask where Doyce is at the present moment, why, my plain answer is--here he is!", "그리고 도이스가 지금 이 순간 어디 있느냐고 자네가 묻겠다면, 뭐, 내 솔직한 대답은—여기 있다는 걸세!"], ["And now I can breathe again at last!’", "그러니 이제야 나도 마침내 한숨 돌리는구먼!\""]], [["Doyce darted in from behind the door, caught Arthur by both hands, and said the rest for himself.", "도이스가 문 뒤에서 불쑥 나와, 두 손으로 아서를 붙잡고는, 나머지 이야기를 직접 했다."]], [["‘There are only three branches of my subject, my dear Clennam,’ said Doyce, proceeding to mould them severally, with his plastic thumb, on the palm of his hand, ‘and they’re soon disposed of.", "\"내 이야기엔 가지가 셋뿐이라네, 친애하는 클레넘,\" 도이스가 자기 손바닥 위에 그 가지들을 하나하나 빚어내듯 능숙한 엄지로 매만지며 말했다."], ["First, not a word more from you about the past.", "\"그리고 금방 끝날 거야. 첫째, 지난 일에 대해선 자네 입에서 더는 한마디도 듣지 않겠네."], ["There was an error in your calculations.", "자네 계산에 오류가 있었지."], ["I know what that is.", "나도 그게 뭔지 안다네."], ["It affects the whole machine, and failure is the consequence.", "그 오류가 기계 전체에 영향을 미치고, 그 결과로 실패가 따르지. 자넨 그 실패에서 배울 것이고, 다음번엔 그걸 피할 걸세."], ["You will profit by the failure, and will avoid it another time.", "나 자신도 설계하다가 그런 일을 숱하게 겪었어. 실패는 배우려는 사람에게 무언가를 가르쳐 주는 법이고, 자넨 이 실패에서 배우지 못할 만큼 분별없는 사람이 아니야."], ["I have done a similar thing myself, in construction, often.", "첫째는 그쯤 해 두지. 둘째."], ["Every failure teaches a man something, if he will learn; and you are too sensible a man not to learn from this failure.", "자네가 그 일을 그토록 무겁게 가슴에 담고 그토록 모질게 자책한 게 안타까웠어. 나는 우리 친구의 도움을 받아 일을 바로잡으려고 밤낮없이 귀국길에 오르던 차에, 그가 자네에게 알려 준 대로, 우리 친구와 마주쳤네."], ["So much for firstly.", "셋째."], ["Secondly. I was sorry you should have taken it so heavily to heart, and reproached yourself so severely; I was travelling home night and day to put matters right, with the assistance of our friend, when I fell in with our friend as he has informed you.", "우리 둘은 이렇게 합의했네, 자네가 그토록 고초를 겪고, 그토록 마음고생을 하고, 또 병까지 앓은 뒤이니, 자네 모르게 일을 완벽히 정리해 두었다가 찾아와서, 모든 사정이 순조롭다고, 만사가 잘되었다고, 사업이 그 어느 때보다 자네를 절실히 필요로 한다고, 그리고 동업자인 자네와 나 앞에 새롭고 번창하는 앞날이 열렸다고 말해 줄 수 있다면 그것이 유쾌한 깜짝 선물이 되리라고 말이야."], ["Thirdly.", "그게 셋째일세."], ["We two agreed, that, after what you had undergone, after your distress of mind, and after your illness, it would be a pleasant surprise if we could so far keep quiet as to get things perfectly arranged without your knowledge, and then come and say that all the affairs were smooth, that everything was right, that the business stood in greater want of you than ever it did, and that a new and prosperous career was opened before you and me as partners.", "하지만 자네도 알다시피 우리는 늘 마찰을 감안해 여유를 두는 법이니, 마무리를 위해 자리를 좀 남겨 두었네. 친애하는 클레넘, 나는 자네를 온전히 믿네."], ["That’s thirdly. But you know we always make an allowance for friction, and so I have reserved space to close in.", "자넨 내게, 내가 자네에게 도움이 될 수 있었거나 도움이 되어 온 만큼이나, 똑같이 도움이 될 힘을 지니고 있어."], ["My dear Clennam, I thoroughly confide in you; you have it in your power to be quite as useful to me as I have, or have had, it in my power to be useful to you; your old place awaits you, and wants you very much; there is nothing to detain you here one half-hour longer.’", "자네의 옛 자리가 자네를 기다리고 있고, 자네를 몹시 필요로 한다네. 자네가 여기 단 반 시간이라도 더 머물러 있을 이유가 전혀 없어.\""]], [["There was silence, which was not broken until Arthur had stood for some time at the window with his back towards them, and until his little wife that was to be had gone to him and stayed by him.", "침묵이 흘렀고, 그 침묵은 아서가 그들에게 등을 돌린 채 한동안 창가에 서 있은 뒤, 그리고 장차 그의 작은 아내가 될 이가 그에게로 가 곁에 머무를 때까지 깨어지지 않았다."]], [["‘I made a remark a little while ago,’ said Daniel Doyce then, ‘which I am inclined to think was an incorrect one.", "\"내가 좀 전에 한마디 했는데,\" 그때 대니얼 도이스가 말했다, \"아무래도 그게 잘못된 말이었던 듯싶네."], ["I said there was nothing to detain you here, Clennam, half an hour longer.", "나는 자네가 여기 반 시간이라도 더 머물러 있을 이유가 없다고 했지, 클레넘."], ["Am I mistaken in supposing that you would rather not leave here till to-morrow morning?", "그런데 자네가 내일 아침까지는 여길 떠나고 싶지 않으리라고 짐작한다면, 내가 틀린 걸까?"], ["Do I know, without being very wise, where you would like to go, direct from these walls and from this room?’", "그리 대단한 현자가 아니라도, 자네가 이 담장에서, 이 방에서 곧장 어디로 가고 싶어 하는지 내가 알지 않겠나?\""]], [["‘You do,’ returned Arthur.", "\"아시는군요,\" 아서가 답했다."], ["‘It has been our cherished purpose.’", "\"그건 우리가 소중히 품어 온 뜻이었습니다.\""]], [["‘Very well!’ said Doyce.", "\"좋아!\" 도이스가 말했다."], ["‘Then, if this young lady will do me the honour of regarding me for four-and-twenty hours in the light of a father, and will take a ride with me now towards Saint Paul’s Churchyard, I dare say I know what we want to get there.’", "\"그렇다면, 이 젊은 아가씨가 스물네 시간 동안 나를 아버지로 여겨 주는 영광을 베풀어, 지금 나와 함께 세인트폴 교회 묘지 쪽으로 마차를 타고 가 준다면, 우리가 거기서 무엇을 마련해야 하는지 내가 안다고 감히 말하겠네.\""]], [["Little Dorrit and he went out together soon afterwards, and Mr Meagles lingered behind to say a word to his friend.", "리틀 도릿과 도이스는 곧 함께 나갔고, 미글스 씨는 친구에게 한마디 하려고 뒤에 남았다."]], [["‘I think, Arthur, you will not want Mother and me in the morning and we will keep away.", "\"내 생각엔 말일세, 아서, 자넨 아침에 어머니와 나를 필요로 하지 않을 테니, 우린 자리를 비켜 줌세."], ["It might set Mother thinking about Pet; she’s a soft-hearted woman.", "어머니가 펫 생각을 하게 될지도 모르거든. 마음 여린 사람이라서."], ["She’s best at the Cottage, and I’ll stay there and keep her company.’", "어머닌 별장에 있는 게 제일 좋고, 나도 거기 남아 곁을 지켜 줄 참이네.\""]], [["With that they parted for the time.", "그러고서 두 사람은 그때를 위해 헤어졌다."], ["And the day ended, and the night ended, and the morning came, and Little Dorrit, simply dressed as usual and having no one with her but Maggy, came into the prison with the sunshine.", "그리고 낮이 저물고, 밤이 저물고, 아침이 왔다. 그리고 리틀 도릿은 여느 때처럼 수수하게 차려입고, 매기 외에는 아무도 데리지 않은 채, 햇살과 함께 감옥으로 들어왔다."], ["The poor room was a happy room that morning.", "그 초라한 방은 그날 아침 행복한 방이었다."], ["Where in the world was there a room so full of quiet joy!", "세상 어디에 이토록 고요한 기쁨으로 가득 찬 방이 있었으랴!"]], [["‘My dear love,’ said Arthur.", "\"내 사랑,\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘Why does Maggy light the fire?", "\"왜 매기가 불을 피우는 거요?"], ["We shall be gone directly.’", "우린 곧 떠날 텐데.\""]], [["‘I asked her to do it.", "\"제가 시킨 거예요."], ["I have taken such an odd fancy.", "좀 이상한 변덕이 들어서요."], ["I want you to burn something for me.’", "당신이 저를 위해 뭔가 하나 태워 주셨으면 해요.\""]], [["‘What?’", "\"무엇을?\""]], [["‘Only this folded paper.", "\"이 접은 종이뿐이에요."], ["If you will put it in the fire with your own hand, just as it is, my fancy will be gratified.’", "당신이 손수, 이대로, 불 속에 넣어 주시면 제 변덕이 흡족해질 거예요.\""]], [["‘Superstitious, darling Little Dorrit?", "\"미신을 믿는 거요, 사랑스러운 리틀 도릿?"], ["Is it a charm?’", "부적인가요?\""]], [["‘It is anything you like best, my own,’ she answered, laughing with glistening eyes and standing on tiptoe to kiss him, ‘if you will only humour me when the fire burns up.’", "\"당신이 가장 좋아하는 그 무엇이든이에요, 내 사람,\" 그녀는 눈을 반짝이며 웃고, 발끝으로 서서 그에게 입맞추며 답했다, \"불이 활활 타오를 때 제 변덕을 받아 주시기만 한다면요.\""]], [["So they stood before the fire, waiting: Clennam with his arm about her waist, and the fire shining, as fire in that same place had often shone, in Little Dorrit’s eyes.", "그리하여 두 사람은 불 앞에 서서 기다렸다. 클레넘은 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고, 불은 바로 그 자리에서 종종 그러했듯, 리틀 도릿의 눈 속에서 빛나고 있었다."], ["‘Is it bright enough now?’ said Arthur.", "\"이제 충분히 활활 타오르오?\" 아서가 말했다."], ["‘Quite bright enough now,’ said Little Dorrit.", "\"이제 아주 충분히 활활 타올라요,\" 리틀 도릿이 말했다."], ["‘Does the charm want any words to be said?’ asked Arthur, as he held the paper over the flame.", "\"그 부적엔 무슨 말을 해야 하는 거요?\" 아서가 종이를 불꽃 위에 들고서 물었다."], ["‘You can say (if you don’t mind) “I love you!”’ answered Little Dorrit.", "\"(괜찮으시다면) '사랑해요!'라고 하시면 돼요,\" 리틀 도릿이 답했다."], ["So he said it, and the paper burned away.", "그래서 그는 그 말을 했고, 종이는 타서 사라졌다."]], [["They passed very quietly along the yard; for no one was there, though many heads were stealthily peeping from the windows.", "두 사람은 마당을 아주 조용히 지나갔다. 거기엔 아무도 없었으나, 많은 머리들이 창문에서 슬며시 엿보고 있었다."], ["Only one face, familiar of old, was in the Lodge.", "오래전부터 낯익은 단 하나의 얼굴만이 수위실에 있었다."], ["When they had both accosted it, and spoken many kind words, Little Dorrit turned back one last time with her hand stretched out, saying, ‘Good-bye, good John!", "두 사람이 그에게 인사를 건네고 다정한 말을 많이 나눈 뒤, 리틀 도릿은 마지막으로 한 번 더 돌아서서 손을 내밀며 말했다, \"잘 있어요, 착한 존!"], ["I hope you will live very happy, dear!’", "부디 아주 행복하게 살기를 바라요, 그대!\""]], [["Then they went up the steps of the neighbouring Saint George’s Church, and went up to the altar, where Daniel Doyce was waiting in his paternal character.", "그러고서 두 사람은 이웃한 세인트조지 교회의 계단을 올라, 대니얼 도이스가 아버지의 역할로 기다리고 있는 제단 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["And there was Little Dorrit’s old friend who had given her the Burial Register for a pillow; full of admiration that she should come back to them to be married, after all.", "그리고 그곳에는, 그녀에게 매장 등록부를 베개 삼아 내주었던 리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 있었으니, 그녀가 결국 결혼하러 자기들에게 돌아왔다는 사실에 감탄을 금치 못했다."]], [["And they were married with the sun shining on them through the painted figure of Our Saviour on the window.", "그리고 두 사람은 창문에 그려진 우리 구주의 형상을 통해 햇살이 그들 위로 비쳐 드는 가운데 혼인을 맺었다."], ["And they went into the very room where Little Dorrit had slumbered after her party, to sign the Marriage Register.", "그리고 두 사람은 결혼 등록부에 서명하러, 리틀 도릿이 그 파티가 끝난 뒤 잠들었던 바로 그 방으로 들어갔다."], ["And there, Mr Pancks, (destined to be chief clerk to Doyce and Clennam, and afterwards partner in the house), sinking the Incendiary in the peaceful friend, looked in at the door to see it done, with Flora gallantly supported on one arm and Maggy on the other, and a back-ground of John Chivery and father and other turnkeys who had run round for the moment, deserting the parent Marshalsea for its happy child.", "그리고 그곳에서, 팽크스 씨가 (장차 도이스 앤드 클레넘의 수석 서기가 되고, 나중엔 그 회사의 동업자가 될 운명의 인물이) 그 방화범의 모습을 평화로운 벗의 모습 속에 가라앉힌 채, 한쪽 팔엔 플로라를, 다른 쪽 팔엔 매기를 늠름하게 부축하고서 문안을 들여다보며 그 광경을 지켜보았는데, 그 뒤편으로는 존 치버리와 그의 아버지, 그리고 잠시 어버이 마셜시를 버리고 그 행복한 자식을 위해 한걸음에 달려온 다른 간수들이 배경을 이루고 있었다."], ["Nor had Flora the least signs of seclusion upon her, notwithstanding her recent declaration; but, on the contrary, was wonderfully smart, and enjoyed the ceremonies mightily, though in a fluttered way.", "그리고 플로라에게는 얼마 전의 선언에도 불구하고 은둔의 기색이라곤 조금도 없었으며, 오히려 놀랍도록 말쑥하게 차려입고, 비록 들뜬 모양새이긴 했으나 그 의식을 더할 나위 없이 즐겼다."]], [["Little Dorrit’s old friend held the inkstand as she signed her name, and the clerk paused in taking off the good clergyman’s surplice, and all the witnesses looked on with special interest.", "리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 그녀가 이름을 적는 동안 잉크병을 들어 주었고, 서기는 선량한 목사의 제의를 벗기다 말고 손을 멈추었으며, 모든 증인이 각별한 관심으로 지켜보았다."], ["‘For, you see,’ said Little Dorrit’s old friend, ‘this young lady is one of our curiosities, and has come now to the third volume of our Registers.", "\"보세요,\" 리틀 도릿의 옛 친구가 말했다, \"이 젊은 아가씨는 우리 명물 가운데 하나로, 이제 우리 등록부의 셋째 권에까지 이르렀답니다."], ["Her birth is in what I call the first volume; she lay asleep, on this very floor, with her pretty head on what I call the second volume; and she’s now a-writing her little name as a bride in what I call the third volume.’", "이 아가씨의 출생은 내가 첫째 권이라 부르는 데 적혀 있고, 바로 이 마룻바닥 위에서 그 예쁜 머리를 내가 둘째 권이라 부르는 것에 베고 잠들어 있었으며, 이제는 내가 셋째 권이라 부르는 데에 신부로서 그 자그마한 이름을 적고 있으니 말입니다.\""]], [["They all gave place when the signing was done, and Little Dorrit and her husband walked out of the church alone.", "서명이 끝나자 모두가 자리를 비켜 주었고, 리틀 도릿과 그녀의 남편은 둘이서만 교회 밖으로 걸어 나갔다."], ["They paused for a moment on the steps of the portico, looking at the fresh perspective of the street in the autumn morning sun’s bright rays, and then went down.", "두 사람은 현관 계단에서 잠시 멈추어, 가을 아침 햇살의 환한 빛줄기 속에 새로이 펼쳐진 거리의 정경을 바라보다가, 이윽고 내려갔다."]], [["Went down into a modest life of usefulness and happiness.", "쓸모 있고 행복한 소박한 삶 속으로 내려갔다."], ["Went down to give a mother’s care, in the fulness of time, to Fanny’s neglected children no less than to their own, and to leave that lady going into Society for ever and a day.", "때가 무르익으면 자기들 자식 못지않게 패니의 방치된 아이들에게도 어머니의 보살핌을 베풀어 주고, 그 부인은 영영 사교계로 드나들게 내버려 두고자 내려갔다."], ["Went down to give a tender nurse and friend to Tip for some few years, who was never vexed by the great exactions he made of her in return for the riches he might have given her if he had ever had them, and who lovingly closed his eyes upon the Marshalsea and all its blighted fruits.", "몇 해 동안 팁에게 다정한 간병인이자 벗이 되어 주고자 내려갔으니, 팁은 만약 자기에게 재물이 있었더라면 그녀에게 주었을지도 모를 그 재물에 대한 보답으로 그녀에게 큰 요구를 해 댔어도 그녀는 결코 언짢아하는 법이 없었고, 사랑으로 그의 두 눈을 감겨 마셜시와 그 시들어 버린 온갖 열매에 작별을 고하게 해 주었다."], ["They went quietly down into the roaring streets, inseparable and blessed; and as they passed along in sunshine and shade, the noisy and the eager, and the arrogant and the froward and the vain, fretted and chafed, and made their usual uproar.", "두 사람은 떼려야 뗄 수 없이, 또 축복받은 채로, 떠들썩한 거리 속으로 조용히 내려갔다. 그리고 두 사람이 햇빛과 그늘 속을 지나는 동안, 시끄러운 자들과 안달하는 자들, 거만한 자들과 고집스러운 자들과 허영에 찬 자들은 안달복달 부대끼며 늘 그러하듯 소란을 피워 댔다."]]];
